{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro: Nashim & Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nashim Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin {\i Seder Nashim}. The word {\i nashim} is generally translated as \u8220"women,\u8221" but a very reasonable suggestion has been made that the more proper translation is actually \u8220"wives.\u8221" The word {\i isha} in the plural yields {\i nashim}, and when we say, for instance, that {\i Shlomo} had many {\i nashim}, it does not mean women in general \u8212- it means wives. Indeed, so much of the tractates in this {\i seder} concern issues relating to wives: {\i Yevamot}, {\i Gittin}, {\i Kiddushin}, and even {\i Nedarim}, where, though the {\i Torah} also addresses young daughters and the rights of fathers to annul vows, the primary subject is husbands and fathers exercising those powers. It is worth pausing to reflect on what it means that women are treated largely as subject matter rather than as subjects in their own right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Masechet Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yevamot} is a very challenging {\i masechta} with which to open the {\i seder}. For {\i Daf Yomi} purposes, the ordering \u8212- largest {\i masechtos} first, as judged by number of chapters \u8212- is not easy on the learner. One begins with {\i Shabbat} and {\i Eruvin}, enormous tractates, and now with {\i Yevamot}, which will occupy us for many months. It would have been more pedagogically gentle to build up gradually. Nevertheless, that is the order, and we proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevamot Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While a good part of this {\i masechta} deals with the actual {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, an equally important section addresses general issues of personal status. Questions of {\i mamzer}, of who may marry whom, of {\i patzu daka} and {\i krus shepcha} (those with certain physical disqualifications from the congregation), questions about individuals of uncertain Jewish status or uncertain paternity \u8212- all of these appear here. So too do matters concerning the women whom {\i kohanim} are forbidden to marry, extensive discussion of conversion ({\i gerus}), and even questions of {\i agunah}, which one might expect to find in {\i Gittin}. The question of whether a husband has died and whether a single witness suffices to establish that fact is treated at length in {\i Yevamot}. It is therefore more accurate to approach this tractate as the comprehensive treatment of personal status law, with the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} as its central organizing theme rather than its only concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yabam Terminology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The literal meaning of {\i yabam} and {\i yevama} deserves mention: these terms mean brother-in-law and sister-in-law respectively. The word {\i yibum} is rendered in English as \u8220"levirate marriage\u8221" \u8212- and it turns out that \u8220"levirate\u8221" derives from the Latin {\i levir}, meaning a husband\u8217's brother. The Hebrew terms, however, carry a broader meaning beyond the technical {\i mitzvah} context; {\i yevama} simply means sister-in-law and {\i yabam} simply means brother-in-law, though both obviously also carry their specific technical legal senses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum History\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practice of {\i yibum} is attested before {\i matan Torah}: we find it in the story of {\i Yehuda} and {\i Tamar}, and there are echoes of something like it in the book of {\i Rut}, though that case does not involve brothers. The {\i Ramban} draws on the {\i Rut} story to argue that {\i yibum} existed as an ancient cultural practice predating {\i matan Torah}, and that it originally applied not only to brothers but to any male relative. The {\i Torah} then came and, because of the prohibitions of {\i arayot}, restricted this practice generally \u8212- but preserved it in the one specific case of brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lehakim Shem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a profound question: what does it truly mean {\i lehakim shem achiv hamet} \u8212- to establish the name of the dead brother? It certainly cannot mean merely giving the child the dead brother\u8217's literal name, since we find no such practice in the {\i Torah} and none in the story of {\i Yehuda} and {\i Tamar} \u8212- indeed, {\i Yehuda} himself named the children. So why does the act of {\i yibum} specifically accomplish this purpose?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ramban} hints at the idea of {\i gilgul neshamot} \u8212- some form of reincarnation of the dead brother\u8217's soul. The suggestion is that when conception occurs through a family member, it may allow for some continuity of the dead brother\u8217's soul in the child born from that union, even if one does not adopt a full doctrine of {\i gilgul neshamot}. There clearly seems to be something deeper operating on a metaphysical level: when this happens through a family member, the child represents a more genuine continuity of the dead father than would otherwise be possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i halacha} purposes, the implications of {\i yibum} touch heavily on questions of inheritance \u8212- who inherits the dead brother\u8217's property \u8212- though we will see that this is not a matter of complete consensus. The entire complex of laws surrounding {\i yibum} is animated by this underlying tension between perpetuating the dead brother\u8217's name and line, and the network of legal relationships the act creates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asei/Lo Taase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The central legal challenge of {\i yibum} is striking: the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} requires a man to marry his dead brother\u8217's wife \u8212- yet the prohibition of {\i eshet ach} (a brother\u8217's wife) is among the most serious {\i arayot}, carrying the penalty of {\i kares}. How is this resolved? The non-simple answer is that this may be one of those cases where the {\i Torah} states two things that are essentially contradictory {\i be-dibbur echad} \u8212- as a single unified declaration. We find a parallel in {\i shamor ve-zachor}, though those two are not contradictory, merely two dimensions of {\i Shabbat}. More pertinently, there are cases where the {\i Torah} states two things that genuinely contradict one another and yet both are true \u8212- and {\i yevama yavo aleha} alongside the prohibition of {\i eshet ach} is one of the most powerful examples of this. Whether {\i yibum} fits the standard pattern of {\i asei doche lo ta\u8217'asei} \u8212- a positive commandment overriding a prohibition \u8212- or represents a more radical {\i chiddush} where the {\i Torah} itself holds the contradiction in simultaneous validity, is one of the foundational conceptual questions this {\i masechta} will address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karaite Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Non-rabbinic groups found their own solutions to this apparent contradiction. The {\i Karaites} offered a fascinating approach: they argued that the {\i Torah} only permits {\i yibum} precisely in the case where the man is {\i not} the actual brother \u8212- that when the {\i Torah} uses the word {\i achim}, it uses it loosely, as in {\i Avraham}\u8217's words to {\i Lot}, {\i halo anashim achim anachnu}, where it does not mean literal brothers. On their reading, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} would apply only in a case like {\i Rut} \u8212- where the parties are related but not in a relationship of {\i issur erva}. That is how they resolve the apparent collision with the prohibition of a brother\u8217's wife. The {\i Essenes} (or another such group) proposed yet another solution \u8212- that {\i yibum} applied only to {\i eirusin} (betrothal) rather than {\i nissu\u8217'in} (full marriage) \u8212- another way of defusing the conflict with the {\i issur erva} of {\i eshet ach}. For {\i Chazal}, of course, that collision is precisely the point and the {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Sugya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the beginning of this {\i masechta} we encounter what we find at the opening of many {\i masechtot}: an initial {\i stam} passage that is technically oriented and hyper-attentive to questions of language and grammatical precision in the {\i mishna}, rather than engaging with conceptual substance. Just as {\i Kiddushin} opens by asking why the {\i mishna} uses the formulation {\i ha-isha nikneit} rather than {\i ha-ish koneh}, and why the language of {\i kinyan} is employed, so too here. This type of opening is generally understood to represent one of the latest layers of the {\i Gemara}, perhaps added even after the main {\i stam} editorial work. {\i Rav Sherira Gaon}, in his famous letter, notes that these opening passages frequently serve as an opportunity to quote material that will appear later or to introduce ideas relevant to future discussions \u8212- they do not develop new ideas themselves. That is precisely what we will find here, as this {\i sugya} opens a window onto some of the complex material we will encounter later in the tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the {\i Gemara} takes up is: what is the organizing principle behind the {\i mishna}\u8217's list of the fifteen women whose status as {\i arayot} exempts them \u8212- and their {\i tzarot} (co-wives) \u8212- from {\i yibum} and {\i chalitza}? The {\i mishna} begins with {\i bito} (his daughter) and proceeds through granddaughter, then to wife\u8217's daughter and her descendants, then mother-in-law and grandmother-in-law, then sisters, and finally the various categories of wives. What determines this sequence?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bito Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} at the bottom of 2b says: {\i ella bito, keivan de\u8217'ati midrasha chaviva lei} \u8212- the reason the {\i mishna} begins with a daughter is that this prohibition is learned from {\i derasha} rather than being explicit in the verse, and it is therefore dear to the {\i tanna}. Tannaim show greater interest in foregrounding material they have themselves uncovered or derived through interpretation, rather than what is explicit in the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bito vs Bat Ishto\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does this mean \u8212- that the prohibition of a man\u8217's daughter is not explicit? {\i Rashi} quotes the relevant {\i pasuk}: {\i ervat isha u\u8217'vitah lo tegaleh, et bat bnah v\u8217'et bat bitah lo tikach legalot ervatah} \u8212- you shall not uncover the nakedness of a woman and her daughter, nor take her son\u8217's daughter or her daughter\u8217's daughter. Every time the {\i Torah} addresses this category, it speaks about a man\u8217's wife and {\i her} daughter, or {\i her} descendants. The most natural reading of {\i isha u\u8217'vitah} \u8212- a woman and her daughter \u8212- is: your wife and her daughter. The {\i rabbis} interpret this as: you are forbidden from your wife\u8217's daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what about his biological daughter who is not his wife\u8217's daughter \u8212- born, for instance, from a non-marital union? That prohibition nowhere appears explicit in the {\i Torah}. The {\i Gemara} therefore designates it a {\i milta de\u8217'ita bidrasha} \u8212- something learned through Rabbinic interpretation \u8212- and that is why the {\i tanna} opens with it. The {\i Gemara} refers to this case specifically as {\i bito me\u8217'anusato} (his daughter from a woman he violated), a formulation that is, it must be said, disturbing. Why the {\i Gemara} chose this example rather than {\i bito mi\u8217'mefutato} (his daughter from a woman he seduced) \u8212- itself already a painful category, but at least one that implies some measure of consent \u8212- is not entirely clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i kulu nami midrasha atu} \u8212- but isn\u8217't everything in the {\i mishna} learned from {\i derasha}? The entire principle that {\i arayot} are exempt from {\i yibum} is derived through Rabbinic interpretation, not stated explicitly. So why should {\i bito} be privileged for this reason?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i nehi de\u8217'inyan yibum atyan midrasha} \u8212- true, the {\i yibum} exemption of all these {\i arayot} is learned from {\i derasha}, but {\i ikkar issuraihu bahedaya ketiv v\u8217'hu} \u8212- the fundamental fact that they are {\i arayot} at all is explicit in the text. Except for {\i bito}: {\i ikkar issura midrasha} \u8212- the very foundation of the prohibition of his biological daughter is itself a {\i derasha}. {\i De\u8217'amar Rava}, as {\i Rav Yitzchak bar Avdimi} taught him: {\i atya heina heina, atya zima zima} \u8212- we derive it through a {\i gezeira shava} from the words {\i heina-heina} and {\i zima-zima}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does this work? The {\i Torah} states concerning biological granddaughter: {\i ervat bat bincha o bat bitecha lo tegaleh ervatan ki ervat-cha heina} \u8212- the nakedness of your son\u8217's daughter or your daughter\u8217's daughter you shall not uncover, for their nakedness is your own. Notably, this verse addresses biological grandchildren \u8212- there is no mention of wife. The word {\i heina} appears here. Separately, in the passage about a wife\u8217's daughter and granddaughter, the {\i Torah} states: {\i isha u\u8217'vito lo tegaleh, sh\u8217'eira heina zima hi} \u8212- a woman and her daughter, their nakedness is {\i heina}, and it is {\i zima}. And in yet another verse: {\i zima hi, ba-esh yisrefu oto v\u8217'et-hen} \u8212- it is {\i zima}, they shall be burned in fire. And: {\i v\u8217'lo tihye zima betochechem} \u8212- there shall be no {\i zima} among you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gezeira shava} operates as follows: the word {\i heina} in the verse about biological granddaughter links it to the verse about wife\u8217's daughter and granddaughter, where {\i heina} also appears. In the latter context, daughter is explicitly prohibited and the punishment of {\i sreifa} (burning) is stated. Through the {\i heina-heina} link, we establish that just as the wife\u8217's granddaughter is forbidden, so too is the biological granddaughter \u8212- and, by daisy-chaining, since the biological is linked via {\i heina} to a context where daughter is mentioned, daughter is also forbidden. Furthermore, since that context contains the word {\i zima}, and {\i zima} is linked to {\i sreifa}, the biological daughter is also subject to the same punishment. The prohibition of biological daughter is thus learned entirely from this {\i gezeira shava} \u8212- hence {\i ikkar issura midrasha}, and hence its pride of place at the opening of the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then observes: {\i hashta de\u8217'amarta kol milta de\u8217'atya midrasha chaviva lei} \u8212- now that we have said the {\i tanna} favors things learned from {\i derasha}, {\i lisni la\u8217'achot ishto libasof} \u8212- the {\i mishna} should then have placed {\i achot ishto} (wife\u8217's sister) at the very end of the list. The reason is that {\i achot ishto} is the one {\i erva} in the list whose exemption from {\i yibum} is most explicit in the {\i pasuk} \u8212- as we will see when we turn the page. If the organizing principle is to lead with {\i derasha}-derived categories and defer what is explicit, wife\u8217's sister should come last.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i aidei de\u8217'ayei be\u8217'issur achvaso, tana achot ishto} \u8212- since we had already moved into discussing the category of siblings, the {\i mishna} included wife\u8217's sister there naturally. The organization shifted from being governed by {\i derasha} to being governed by logical grouping. Fine. But the {\i Gemara} pushes further: {\i lisni lehai bava libasof} \u8212- even if we accept that wife\u8217's sister belongs in the sibling section, why doesn\u8217't the entire sibling section come after the wife-category section, given that {\i drashot} should dominate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korvei Ordering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} abandons the {\i derasha} theory entirely and offers what is clearly the straightforward explanation: {\i tana korvei korvei nakat} \u8212- the {\i mishna} lists the categories in order of closeness of relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tana bito u\u8217'bat bito u\u8217'bat bno de\u8217'korvei atzmo} \u8212- first come his own descendants: daughter, granddaughter. These are the people most closely related to him. {\i Ve\u8217'aidei de\u8217'tana shlosha dorot lemata didei, tana nami shlosha dorot lemata didah} \u8212- once having dealt with three generations downward on his own side, the {\i mishna} naturally turns to three generations downward on his wife\u8217's side \u8212- wife\u8217's daughter and granddaughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'aidei de\u8217'tana shlosha dorot lemata didah, tana shlosha dorot lemala didah} \u8212- and having gone three generations downward on her side, the {\i mishna} goes three generations upward on her side: mother-in-law and grandmother-in-law. The vertical dimension is thus exhausted \u8212- down three generations on his side, down three on hers, and up three on hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then: {\i ve\u8217'tana achota} \u8212- we move horizontally to siblings. {\i Ve\u8217'achot ima, shekrovei atzma} \u8212- mother\u8217's sister, who is a blood relative of his wife. {\i Ve\u8217'achot ishto, achot ishto} \u8212- and then wife\u8217's sister, who is not his blood relative but fits within the horizontal relational category. The ordering is thus: vertical (downward, his side), vertical (downward, her side), vertical (upward, her side), horizontal (blood relatives \u8212- sisters), horizontal (his wife\u8217's sister by association with the sibling category).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wives Ordering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having exhausted the sibling category, the {\i mishna} turns to wives. Here a further wrinkle emerges: {\i badin hu dilekadmei lekallato mikamei eshet achiv shelo haya beolamo, delav mishum korva hu de-assira} \u8212- it would have been logical to list {\i kallato} (daughter-in-law, i.e., his son\u8217's wife) before {\i eshet achiv shelo haya beolamo} (the wife of a brother who was not yet born when the dead brother married her). The reason {\i kallato} should come first is that her prohibition is rooted in {\i korva} \u8212- closeness of relationship, the core organizing principle. {\i Eshet achiv shelo haya beolamo}, by contrast, is not primarily an {\i issur} of kinship; it arises from the absence of any {\i mitzat yibum} (obligation of {\i yibum}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To clarify: if {\i Shimon} was married and died, {\i Reuven} performed {\i yibum}, and then {\i Levi} was subsequently born \u8212- when {\i Reuven} later dies, {\i Levi} has no obligation of {\i yibum} toward {\i Reuven}\u8217's first wife (who was originally {\i Shimon}\u8217's wife), because {\i Levi} was never in the world at the time of that original marriage. There was never a potential {\i mitzat yibum} between them. This is what {\i eshet achiv shelo haya beolamo} means: the wife of a brother toward whom he never had any obligation of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Logically, then, {\i kallato} should have preceded this case. But the {\i Gemara} explains: {\i ela aidei de\u8217'ayrei be\u8217'issur achva, tani eshet achiv shelo haya beolamo} \u8212- since we were already dealing with wives of siblings in the sibling section, the {\i mishna} listed the wives of siblings first: {\i eshet achiv me\u8217'imo} (wife of a maternal half-brother) and {\i eshet achiv shelo haya beolamo} both belong in the sibling-wives category. {\i Ve\u8217'hadar tani kallato} \u8212- only then does the {\i mishna} reach daughter-in-law. And this, the {\i Gemara} concludes, is entirely cogent: the organization proceeds by vertical relations, then horizontal relations, leading naturally into wives of those horizontal relatives, and finally arriving at {\i kallato}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Language: Potrot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Potrot Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having resolved the questions of ordering, the {\i Gemara} turns to a different linguistic issue: {\i u-mai irya detani potrot} \u8212- why does the {\i mishna} use the language \u8220"they exempt\u8221" ({\i potrot}) their {\i tzarot}, rather than \u8220"they forbid\u8221" ({\i osrot}) their {\i tzarot} from {\i yibum}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Assur vs Patur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ei tana osrot} \u8212- had the {\i mishna} said \u8220"they forbid,\u8221" {\i hava amina assur leyabbem aval michlatz chaltza} \u8212- one might have thought it is forbidden to perform {\i yibum} but perhaps {\i chalitza} is still required. This is not an idle concern: there are indeed categories of women for whom, due to {\i safek} or rabbinic considerations, one does not perform {\i yibum} but does perform {\i chalitza}. If the {\i mishna} merely said \u8220"forbidden,\u8221" a reader might conclude that a biblical obligation still lurks and requires {\i chalitza} to discharge it. The word \u8220"exempt\u8221" ({\i potrot}) forecloses that misunderstanding: these women are entirely exempt, from both {\i yibum} and {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges: {\i u-litni assura lachlotz} \u8212- be explicit then, say it is forbidden to perform {\i chalitza}, which conveys the same total exemption. The {\i Gemara} replies: {\i mai ka avid?} \u8212- but how could it be forbidden to perform {\i chalitza}? {\i Yibum} is forbidden because it is an act of sexual relations with {\i arayot}, carrying {\i kares} absent the {\i mitzvah}. But what {\i issur} attaches to removing a shoe and spitting in a court proceeding? {\i Alma lo? Im ata omer choletzes, misyabbemes} \u8212- because if we say she may perform {\i chalitza}, the next person will come along and perform {\i yibum}, reasoning that if there is a {\i chalitza} option there must be a {\i yibum} option. So while we cannot properly say it is {\i assur} to perform {\i chalitza} (since {\i chalitza} itself is a neutral act), we also cannot allow it, lest it signal that {\i yibum} is on the table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzara Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So why not simply say {\i assur lachlotz} to communicate this? The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i kevan debimakom mitzvah hu de-assira tzara, ve-shelo bimakom mitzvah sharya, mishum hachi tana potrot} \u8212- because the {\i tzara} becomes forbidden specifically in the context of {\i mitzva}, that is, in the context of a brother\u8217's wife. If there is no {\i mitzat yibum}, then the {\i tzara} of an {\i erva} is perfectly permitted to the man \u8212- as long as they were not married to his brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is crucial. The mechanism is not that an {\i erva} renders her co-wife forbidden to the man as such. Rather, the {\i erva} removes the {\i mitzat yibum} entirely. Once the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} is removed, the other woman defaults back to being prohibited as a brother\u8217's wife \u8212- because there is no {\i mitzva} to override that prohibition. But in a case where the same woman is an {\i erva} to someone she is not related to through a brother \u8212- say, her father is married to two women and one of them is his daughter \u8212- the daughter does not make the other wife forbidden to the father, because there was never any {\i mitzat yibum} to remove in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why the {\i mishna} had to say {\i potrot} \u8212- \u8220"exempt\u8221" \u8212- rather than anything conveying a direct prohibition on the {\i tzara}. The mechanism is the removal of the {\i mitzva}, not the imposition of a new {\i issur}. {\i Mishum hachi tana potrot}: that is precisely the conceptual reality being described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-mai arya detani min hachalitzah u-min hayibbum} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} next asks: why does the {\i mishna} list {\i chalitza} before {\i yibum}? Since {\i yibum} is the primary {\i mitzvah}, should it not have been mentioned first? {\i Ei tani min hayibbum, hava amina michlatz chaltza yibumi lo mivama} \u8212- if the {\i mishna} said only \u8220"exempt from {\i yibum},\u8221" one might have thought: do not perform {\i yibum} but do perform {\i chalitza}, since perhaps the {\i yibum} exemption is only rabbinic while a biblical {\i chalitza} obligation remains. {\i Ka mashma lan} that this is not so: {\i kol ha\u8217'oleh leyibum oleh lachalitza, ve-chol she\u8217'eino oleh leyibum eino oleh lachalitza} \u8212- whoever is subject to {\i yibum} is subject to {\i chalitza}, and whoever is not subject to {\i yibum} is not subject to {\i chalitza}. At the biblical level they are a package: one implies the other. Hence the {\i mishna} must mention both, to prevent any inference that exemption from one leaves the other in place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i ve-litni min hayibum umin hachalitza} \u8212- fine, say both, but why list {\i chalitza} first? {\i Abba Shaul hi} \u8212- the {\i mishna} follows the position of {\i Abba Shaul}, {\i de\u8217'amar mitzvat chalitza kodemet lemitzvat yibum} \u8212- who held that {\i chalitza} is the preferred mode, taking precedence over {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leshem Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} at this juncture quotes {\i Abba Shaul} from a later chapter: {\i Abba Shaul omer hachones yevamto leshum noy leshum ishut ke\u8217'ilu pogeah be\u8217'erva} \u8212- one who takes his {\i yevama} in marriage for reasons of attraction or desire for marriage is as one who transgresses the {\i issur erva}. The concern is that if a man actually wishes to marry this woman \u8212- because he finds her attractive or genuinely desires to wed her \u8212- the act of {\i yibum} is no longer {\i leshem mitzvah}, no longer performed as a religious obligation, but as an ordinary marital pursuit. {\i Ve-karov be\u8217'einai lihyot ha-vlad mamzer} \u8212- and it is close in his view to the child being a {\i mamzer}, because it approaches the quality of {\i issur eshet ach} when the act is not performed entirely for the sake of the {\i mitzvah}. This is a striking position: one might assume that genuine mutual interest between the parties would be a positive factor, yet {\i Abba Shaul} regards motivation by attraction as what transforms the act from {\i mitzvah} to something approaching transgression. Following {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's view, the {\i mishna} places {\i chalitza} first to underscore that it is the preferred approach.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fifteen Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Disputes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the number itself: {\i minyana derisha lema\u8217'utei mai, minyana deseifa lema\u8217'utei mai} \u8212- why does the {\i mishna} need to specify fifteen? And why does it reiterate at the end \u8220"these women are exempt\u8221"? These specifications serve to exclude certain positions: {\i lema\u8217'utei derav vederav Assi} \u8212- to exclude the views of {\i Rav} and {\i Rav Assi}, who wished to add additional categories of women exempt from {\i yibum} to the list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suta/Aylonit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} proposed the case of {\i tzarat sotah} \u8212- the co-wife of a woman who has committed adultery (or is at minimum suspected of it). A {\i sotah} is forbidden to her husband; their relationship is, in terms of conjugal life and the possibility of conceiving a child together, broken. The principle of {\i yibum} exists because the surviving brother steps into the dead brother\u8217's place to continue the possibility of children and continuity. A dead brother who could not have had licit relations with his wife \u8212- because she was a {\i sotah} \u8212- never had that viable conjugal relationship. Therefore, {\i Rav} argued, the surviving brother has no {\i mitzat yibum} with her. Fine. But {\i Rav} went further: if there is no {\i yibum} obligation with respect to the {\i sotah} herself, her {\i tzara} (co-wife) should likewise be exempt. This is the case of {\i tzarat sotah}. Crucially, the {\i sotah} is not an {\i erva} \u8212- the {\i issur} does not arise from kinship but from the state of the marital relationship. {\i Rav} nonetheless maintained that the {\i tzara} principle applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Assi}\u8217's case is {\i tzarat aylonit} \u8212- the co-wife of a woman who is incapable of bearing children. An {\i aylonit} presents a different kind of problem: not a marital breakdown through prohibited conduct, but a physical impossibility of conception. {\i Rav Assi} held that since there is no viable prospect of progeny from the union with the {\i aylonit}, there is no {\i mitzat yibum} to her, and therefore her {\i tzara} is also exempt. Again, the {\i aylonit} is not an {\i erva}; the exemption arises from the nature of the relationship\u8217's inability to fulfill the purpose of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both {\i Rav} and {\i Rav Assi} thus held that the {\i tzara} principle \u8212- which the {\i mishna} applies only to cases of {\i arayot} \u8212- extends to cases where there is no {\i yibum} obligation for a different reason. The {\i mishna}\u8217's specification of precisely fifteen women, the {\i Gemara} argues, rejects this expansion: the co-wife of a {\i sotah} and the co-wife of an {\i aylonit} are not exempt; {\i yibum} would still apply to them because the {\i tzara} principle operates only where the primary woman is an {\i erva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzara Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then poses the question from {\i Rav} and {\i Rav Assi}\u8217's perspective: {\i u\u8217'l\u8217'Rav u\u8217'l\u8217'Rav Assi l\u8217'mautei mai?} If they believe their cases are correct and not excluded by the {\i mishna}, why does the {\i mishna} specify only fifteen? What do {\i they} say the number is excluding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i ei sevira lehu d\u8217'hadadei} \u8212- if {\i Rav} holds of {\i Rav Assi}\u8217's case and {\i Rav Assi} holds of {\i Rav}\u8217's case, so that they agree with one another on both, then the two instances of numerical specification in the {\i mishna} \u8212- \u8220"fifteen\u8221" at the beginning and \u8220"these women\u8221" at the close \u8212- each exclude one of two other cases: {\i chada l\u8217'mautei tzarat m\u8217'ma\u8217'enet v\u8217'chada l\u8217'mautei tzarat machzir gerushato} \u8212- one excludes the {\i tzara} of a {\i mema\u8217'enet} and the other excludes the {\i tzara} of a {\i machzir gerushato}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And if they do not hold of one another \u8212- if {\i Rav} does not accept {\i Rav Assi}\u8217's case and vice versa \u8212- then {\i chada l\u8217'mautei d\u8217'chavreih} \u8212- one specification excludes the other\u8217's case, and {\i v\u8217'chada l\u8217'mautei idach m\u8217'ma\u8217'enet o tzarat machzir gerushato} \u8212- the other specification excludes one of the remaining two cases: either {\i mema\u8217'enet} or {\i machzir gerushato}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mema\u8217'enes Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What are these two cases? {\i Mema\u8217'enet} refers to an underage girl who was married off by her brothers or mother (rather than by her father) after her father\u8217's death. Such a marriage is only rabbinic in character, and the girl retains the right of {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- refusal \u8212- to dissolve the marriage. The connection between {\i Reuven} and this underage wife is, at every level, a creation of rabbinic law. When {\i Reuven} dies, a {\i zika} \u8212- a quasi-marital attachment \u8212- transfers in some sense to {\i Shimon}, the surviving brother. But just as the girl could have performed {\i mi\u8217'un} to dissolve her marriage to {\i Reuven}, she can perform {\i mi\u8217'un} against {\i Shimon} and walk away from the {\i zika}. Once she does {\i mi\u8217'un}, {\i Shimon} can no longer perform {\i yibum} with her. The case of {\i tzarat mema\u8217'enet} would be: {\i Reuven} had two wives, and one was this underage girl who did {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- can {\i Shimon} still perform {\i yibum} with the other wife? The answer is yes. The entire structure here is rabbinic: the girl\u8217's marriage was rabbinic, her {\i mi\u8217'un} is rabbinic, the effect on the {\i zika} is rabbinic. It is therefore not at all surprising that her {\i mi\u8217'un} does not exempt the {\i tzara}, and the {\i mishna}\u8217's fifteen cases necessarily exclude this scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machzir Grushaso\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i machzir gerushato} \u8212- remarrying one\u8217's divorced wife \u8212- is more straightforward. The {\i Torah} prohibits a man from remarrying a woman he divorced if in the interim she was married to another man. In this scenario, {\i Yaakov} was married to {\i Leah}, divorced her, and then remarried her after she had been with another man (he was not permitted to do so, but he did). He was also always married to {\i Rachel}, whom he never divorced. When {\i Yaakov} dies, {\i Leah} cannot be subject to {\i yibum} because of the prohibition of {\i machzir gerushato}. But would {\i Rachel}\u8217's obligation of {\i yibum} be affected? No.\u160 ?The {\i tzara} principle does not apply here. The {\i issur} of {\i machzir gerushato} differs in quality from that of {\i sotah}: the {\i sotah} prohibition bears primarily on sexual relations, whereas {\i machzir gerushato} is more precisely an {\i issur} on the act of remarriage itself. For that reason \u8212- which the {\i Gemara} leaves for later elaboration \u8212- the {\i issur} does not create a {\i tzara} effect that would exempt {\i Rachel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarat Tzara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes by asking why {\i Rav} and {\i Rav Assi} did not simply include their cases in the list in the {\i mishna} if they believed the {\i tzara} principle applies there. The answer is: {\i lefi she\u8217'eino betzaras tzara} \u8212- because even according to {\i Rav} and {\i Rav Assi}, the {\i tzara} in their cases (whether {\i sotah} or {\i aylonit}) would not go further. In the {\i mishna}\u8217's fifteen cases, the {\i erva} exempts not only her direct {\i tzara} but also the {\i tzarat tzara} \u8212- the co-wife of the co-wife \u8212- and this extends indefinitely. In the cases of {\i sotah} and {\i aylonit}, even if one accepts that the immediate {\i tzara} is exempt, that exemption does not propagate to subsequent levels. Since the {\i mishna}\u8217's principle encompasses {\i tzarat tzara} and theirs does not, their cases are categorically different and were therefore deliberately excluded from the list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi}, in his comment on the {\i mishna}, notes on the phrase {\i tzarat tzarata ad sof ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- the co-wife of the co-wife to the end of the world \u8212- that this is explained {\i b\u8217'masnisin meforesh}: the {\i mishna} itself, further along, will spell out what this means. {\i Rashi} normally writes {\i b\u8217'gemara meforesh} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} will explain \u8212- but here he notes that it is the {\i mishna} itself that provides the explanation, in a remarkably self-referential way, with the {\i mishna} later formulating its own question and then answering it by spelling out the scenario in detail.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 4\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arayot Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mana Hani Milei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} on 3b opens with a foundational question: {\i Mana hani milei?} \u8212- where do all these laws come from? Specifically, the question targets two distinct principles established in the {\i mishna}: first, that there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} when one of the women is an {\i ervah} to the {\i yavam}; and second, that the {\i tzara} \u8212- the co-wife \u8212- of such a woman is equally exempt. The {\i Gemara} signals that it will now provide the scriptural and exegetical basis for everything discussed thus far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isha El Achota\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Detanu rabanan} \u8212- the Rabbis taught in a {\i baraita}: {\i Isha el achota lo tikach} \u8212- do not take a woman and her sister, {\i litzror} \u8212- to create friction or agitation between them \u8212- {\i legalot ervata aleha bechayeha} \u8212- to uncover her nakedness upon her, in her lifetime. The qualification {\i bechayeha} \u8212- in her lifetime \u8212- is significant: the prohibition of a woman and her sister applies specifically while the original wife is still alive. The verse thus establishes the general prohibition. The {\i baraita} then asks: {\i Ma talmud lomar?} \u8212- what does the verse come to teach? {\i Lefi shene\u8217'emar} \u8212- because the verse says {\i yevama yavo aleha} \u8212- the brother-in-law shall come upon her \u8212- one might have thought even in the case of {\i yibbum}, the {\i mitzvah} could override the prohibition of {\i arayot}. That is the {\i hava amina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aleha Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} resolves this through a {\i gezeirah shavah}-like verbal link on the word {\i aleha}. {\i Ne\u8217'emar kan aleha} \u8212- it says here, in the case of the wife\u8217's sister: {\i legalot ervata aleha} \u8212- to uncover her nakedness {\i aleha}. {\i Vene\u8217'emar lehalan aleha} \u8212- and it says there, in the case of {\i yibbum}: {\i yevama yavo aleha}. The word {\i aleha} appears in both contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The method of inference works as follows: since in the context of {\i yibbum} the word {\i aleha} refers to a case in which there is a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}, so too when the verse forbidding a woman and her sister uses the word {\i aleha}, it means: even in a case of {\i aleha} \u8212- even in a case of {\i yibbum}. {\i Ma lehalan bemakom mitzvah} \u8212- just as {\i aleha} there denotes the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}, {\i af kan bemakom mitzvah} \u8212- so too here it means even in a context of {\i yibbum}. The Torah then says {\i ve\u8217'amar rachmana lo tikach} \u8212- even in that case, you may not proceed. The verse thus teaches unambiguously that the prohibition of a wife\u8217's sister is not displaced by the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Sister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i Ve\u8217'ein li ela hi} \u8212- I know only that there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} in the case of the wife\u8217's sister herself. {\i Tzaratah minayin?} \u8212- from where do I know that the {\i tzara} \u8212- the co-wife of the wife\u8217's sister \u8212- is also exempt from {\i yibbum}? {\i Talmud lomar litzror} \u8212- the verse teaches through the word {\i litzror}. The word {\i litzror}, meaning to create friction or agitation, is read as encoding the word {\i tzara} \u8212- co-wife \u8212- just as we find by Hannah and Penina: {\i ve\u8217'ki\u8217'asata tzaratah gam ka\u8217'as}. This is a {\i drasha} on the word, not a verse from the Torah itself. Thus {\i litzror} signals that the exemption extends to the co-wife as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then continues: {\i Ve\u8217'ein li ela tzaratah} \u8212- I know only the direct co-wife. {\i Tzarat tzaratah minayin?} \u8212- what about the co-wife\u8217's co-wife? If this {\i tzara} subsequently marries another brother who then dies, does her co-wife also become exempt? {\i Talmud lomar litzror velo litzor} \u8212- the verse says {\i litzror} with a doubled {\i resh} rather than {\i litzor} with a single one. The doubled form indicates: even the {\i tzara} of a {\i tzara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates an interesting conceptual question that the {\i Gemara} will return to: is the {\i tzarat tzarah} really a distinct category, or does the mechanism work differently? Once there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}, the woman becomes an {\i eshet ach} \u8212- a forbidden relation in her own right. So when the {\i tzara} subsequently enters a {\i yibbum} situation with another brother, perhaps her co-wife is exempt not because of the category of {\i tzarat tzarah} per se, but simply because the {\i tzara} has herself become an {\i ervah} through her status as {\i eshet ach}. Whether these are truly distinct conceptual categories or simply generate overlapping cases of {\i eshet ach} remains an open question, though the {\i Gemara}\u8217's textual derivation from {\i litzror} stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mah Matzinu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'ein li ela achot ishah} \u8212- I know only that the exemption and the exemption of the {\i tzara} apply in the case of the wife\u8217's sister. {\i She\u8217'ar arayot minayin?} \u8212- from where do I know the same applies to all other {\i arayot}? The {\i baraita} employs a {\i mah matzinu} \u8212- a paradigm inference: {\i mah achot ishah miyuchedet shehi ervah vechayavin al zedonah kareit ve\u8217'al shigegatah chatat ve\u8217'asurah leyavam} \u8212- just as the wife\u8217's sister is an {\i ervah} for which intentional transgression carries {\i kareit} and unintentional transgression requires a {\i chatat} offering, and she is forbidden to the {\i yavam} \u8212- {\i af kol shehi ervah vechayavin al zedonah kareit ve\u8217'al shigegatah chatat ve\u8217'asurah leyavam} \u8212- so too any other woman who is an {\i ervah} of comparable severity is likewise forbidden to the {\i yavam}. There is nothing distinctive about the wife\u8217's sister that should make her unique. The relevant criterion is simply: {\i ervah} with a {\i kareit} penalty. Any such case falls under the same rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarot Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the exemption is extended to all {\i arayot}, the {\i baraita} asks: {\i Tzaroteihen minayin?} \u8212- from where do I know that the co-wives of all such {\i arayot} are also exempt? The same paradigmatic reasoning applies: {\i mah achot ishah miyuchedet shehi ervah vechayavin al zedonah kareit ve\u8217'al shigegatah chatat ve\u8217'asurah leyavam vetzaratah asurah} \u8212- just as the wife\u8217's sister, being an {\i ervah} of that severity, is forbidden to the {\i yavam} and her {\i tzara} is also forbidden \u8212- {\i af kol} \u8212- so too any similarly situated {\i ervah} exempts her co-wife as well. This establishes the general principle: {\i ha\u8217'arayot pturot vepoterot tzaroteihen} \u8212- {\i arayot} are themselves exempt and they exempt their co-wives. The entire principle is derived from the wife\u8217's sister as paradigm, and then generalized to all fifteen {\i arayot} enumerated in the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chamesh Esrei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then articulates this conclusion formally: {\i Mikan amru chachamim} \u8212- from here the Sages said: {\i chamesh esrei nashim poterot tzaroteihen vetzarat tzaroteihen min hachalitzah umin hayibbum ad sof kol ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- fifteen women exempt their co-wives and co-co-wives from {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibbum} indefinitely. The {\i baraita} then raises a further question: {\i yachol she\u8217'ani marbeh af sheish arayot hachamurot me\u8217'elu sheyihiyu tzaroteihen asurot} \u8212- perhaps I should include even the six more weighty {\i arayot}, those more severe than the fifteen, and say that their co-wives are also forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheish Chamurot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A brief look at {\i Daf} 13 clarifies what the \u8220"six more weighty\u8221" cases are. The {\i mishna} there states: {\i sheish arayot chamurot me\u8217'elu mipnei shenesuot l\u8217'acherim} \u8212- six {\i arayot} are more weighty than those fifteen because they are married to other people \u8212- specifically because they could never be married to one\u8217's brother. Examples include one\u8217's mother and one\u8217's sister. In these cases, since the brother could never have been married to such a woman, there is never a context of {\i yibbum} to begin with. Since there is no {\i yibbum} context, the co-wife does not become an {\i eshet ach}, and therefore she remains permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzara Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} now answers the question it raised: {\i amrat} \u8212- you respond. {\i Mah achot ishah miyuchedet shehi ervah vechayavin al zedonah kareit ve\u8217'al shigegatah chatat ve\u8217'efshar l\u8217'hinasay l\u8217'ach} \u8212- what is distinctive about the wife\u8217's sister is that she is an {\i ervah}, carries a {\i kareit} penalty, and \u8212- crucially \u8212- she {\i could} have been married to a brother. {\i Im ken asurah leyavam vetzaratah asurah} \u8212- in such a case she is forbidden to the {\i yavam} and her co-wife is forbidden. {\i Af kol shehi ervah vechayavin al zedonah kareit ve\u8217'al shigegatah chatat ve\u8217'efshar l\u8217'hinasay l\u8217'ach} \u8212- similarly, any {\i ervah} that could have been married to a brother: {\i asurah leyavam vetzaratah asurah} \u8212- she is forbidden and her {\i tzara} is forbidden. {\i Yatz\u8217'u sheish arayot hachamurot me\u8217'elu sheharei ee efshar l\u8217'hinasay l\u8217'ach} \u8212- this excludes the six more severe cases, since they cannot be married to a brother. {\i Tzaroteihen mutarot} \u8212- their co-wives are therefore permitted. {\i She\u8217'ein tzara ela mei\u8217'ach} \u8212- the entire concept of a {\i tzara} creating a {\i yibbum} exemption exists only in the context of a co-wife of one\u8217's brother.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Onesh Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onesh Minayin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} now transitions from prohibition to punishment. {\i Azharah shamanu} \u8212- we have heard that there is a prohibition: from the verse {\i isha el achota lo tikach litzror} read in light of the word {\i aleha}, we learn that a wife\u8217's sister remains forbidden even in a {\i yibbum} context. But how do we know what punishment applies? {\i Onesh minayin?} \u8212- from where do we derive that if one nonetheless goes ahead and performs {\i yibbum} with an {\i ervah}, the full punishment is in force? One might have thought that since a {\i mitzvah} is involved, the punishment is somehow attenuated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kareit Yibbum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse provides the answer: {\i amar kra ki kol asher ya\u8217'aseh mikol hato\u8217'evot ha\u8217'eleh venichretu} \u8212- the verse says, whoever does any of these abominations shall be cut off. This {\i kareit} passage comes at the conclusion of the entire enumeration of {\i arayot}, and that enumeration includes {\i isha el achota lo tikach litzror}. Since the prohibition of the wife\u8217's sister includes the {\i yibbum} context \u8212- as we established through the word {\i aleha} \u8212- and the punishment of {\i kareit} is attached to the entire list of {\i arayot}, the {\i kareit} applies equally in the {\i yibbum} context. The word {\i litzror} further teaches that the prohibition and its punishment extend to the {\i tzara} as well. And once we know that having relations with the {\i tzara} in a {\i yibbum} context is forbidden and carries {\i kareit}, it follows that the {\i tzara} herself acquires the status of {\i eshet ach} \u8212- a forbidden relation \u8212- once there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} attaching to her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hava Amina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meichdi Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i baraita} concluded, the {\i Gemara} steps back to analyze the logical foundations of the entire exercise. {\i Meichdi} \u8212- let us think about this: {\i ta\u8217'ama dechtav rachmana aleha} \u8212- it is because the Torah wrote {\i aleha} that we know {\i yibbum} does not apply in the case of an {\i ervah}. {\i Halav hacha hava amina achot isha meyabemet} \u8212- were it not for that verse, I would have thought that you could indeed perform {\i yibbum} with a wife\u8217's sister. But why would I have thought such a thing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asei Dochei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama?} \u8212- what was the reasoning behind that {\i hava amina}? {\i Amrinan ati asei vedachi lo ta\u8217'asei} \u8212- I might have said: a positive {\i mitzvah} comes and overrides a negative prohibition. The {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} is a positive commandment; the prohibition of a wife\u8217's sister is a negative prohibition. Hence the {\i hava amina} that the {\i mitzvah} might override the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kareit Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} immediately pushes back on this {\i hava amina}: {\i eima de\u8217'amrinan asei vidachei lo ta\u8217'asei} \u8212- when do we say a positive {\i mitzvah} overrides a negative? {\i Lo ta\u8217'asei greida} \u8212- only when it is a straightforward negative prohibition, not a particularly weighty one. {\i Lo ta\u8217'asei she\u8217'yesh bo kareit} \u8212- a negative prohibition that carries {\i kareit} \u8212- {\i mi dachi?} \u8212- does a positive {\i mitzvah} ever override that? The principle of {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'asei} does not apply to prohibitions of such severity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might respond: but the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} already overrides one prohibition that carries {\i kareit} \u8212- namely {\i eshet ach}. If it overrides that, perhaps it could override other {\i arayot} as well? That is indeed the most natural formulation of the {\i hava amina}. However, the {\i Gemara} has not yet reached that point. First it wants to establish the more fundamental question: where do we know the principle of {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'asei} at all? Understanding that principle is essential to understanding why anyone might have thought {\i yibbum} could override {\i arayot} \u8212- and why ultimately it cannot when {\i kareit} is involved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shatnez-Tzitzit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Velo Litzor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now opens a broader inquiry: {\i lo ta\u8217'asei greida minalan de\u8217'dachi?} \u8212- from where do we even know that a positive {\i mitzvah} overrides a straightforward negative prohibition? This takes the discussion temporarily away from {\i yibbum}, but the purpose is clear: to establish the principle of {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'asei} so that we can understand the {\i hava amina} that {\i yibbum} might have overridden the prohibition of {\i arayot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Tilbash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i de\u8217'ksiv lo tilbash shatnez, gedilim ta\u8217'aseh lach} \u8212- because the verse says do not wear {\i shatnez}, and the very next verse says make yourself fringes. {\i Shatnez} is the prohibition of mixing wool and linen; {\i tzitzit} may require a wool string on a linen garment, which would constitute {\i shatnez}. The juxtaposition teaches that the positive {\i mitzvah} of {\i tzitzit} overrides the negative prohibition of {\i shatnez}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smuchim Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But how do we know that juxtaposition in the Torah is a legitimate hermeneutic tool? {\i Ve\u8217'amar Rabbi Elazar: smuchin min hatorah minayin?} \u8212- from where is the principle of {\i smuchin} \u8212- learning from textual juxtapositions \u8212- itself derived? {\i Shene\u8217'emar smuchim la\u8217'ad le\u8217'olam asuyim be\u8217'emes veyashar} \u8212- as the verse in {\i Tehillim} says, they are joined forever, made in truth and uprightness \u8212- which the {\i Gemara} reads as endorsing the method of {\i smuchin}. Thus the juxtaposition of {\i shatnez} and {\i gedilim ta\u8217'aseh lach} is a valid source for the principle that {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'aseh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} introduces a related teaching. {\i Ve\u8217'amar Rav Sheshes amar Rabbi Elazar mishum Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah: minayin leyevama she\u8217'nafla lifnei Mukeh Shechin she\u8217'ein chosmin oto} \u8212- from where do we know that if a {\i yevama} falls before a {\i yavam} who is a repugnant person, such as one afflicted with severe boils, you do not silence her? {\i Shene\u8217'emar lo tachsom shor be\u8217'disho} \u8212- do not muzzle an ox while it threshes \u8212- {\i vesamich lei ki yeshvu achim yachdav} \u8212- and immediately juxtaposed to it is the passage establishing the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}. This {\i smuchin} teaches that we attend to the woman\u8217's objections and ensure that {\i chalitzah} is performed rather than {\i yibbum}. This is a significant point: although so much of this entire {\i tractate} is framed from the man\u8217's perspective \u8212- who is permitted to whom, where a {\i mitzvah} applies \u8212- this {\i drasha} establishes at the very outset that the woman has a voice and that her protest is legally cognizable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Smuchim General\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reb Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then notes: {\i afilu lema\u8217'an delo darash smuchin be\u8217'alma, be\u8217'Mishneh Torah darash} \u8212- even one who does not generally make {\i drashot} from {\i smuchin} will do so in {\i Mishneh Torah} \u8212- that is, in {\i Devarim}, where {\i Moshe Rabbeinu} recaps the earlier laws. This is directly relevant because the juxtaposition of {\i shatnez} and {\i tzitzit} appears in {\i Devarim}, which means that even those who reject {\i smuchin} as a general hermeneutic accept this particular {\i drasha} and therefore accept the principle of {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'aseh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Who is it that generally rejects {\i smuchin}? {\i Detanya: Rabbi Yehuda be\u8217'alma lo darash} \u8212- we know from a {\i baraita} that {\i Rabbi Yehuda} in general does not derive {\i halacha} from juxtaposition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai vs Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} spells this out: {\i Ben Azzai omer: ne\u8217'emar machasheifa lo techayeh} \u8212- it says, do not suffer a sorceress to live \u8212- {\i vene\u8217'emar kol shochev im behemah mot yumat} \u8212- and it is juxtaposed to: whoever lies with an animal shall be put to death. {\i Samchu inyan lo} \u8212- they juxtaposed the two. {\i Mah shochev im behemah be\u8217'skilah} \u8212- just as lying with an animal is punishable by stoning \u8212- {\i af machasheifa be\u8217'skilah} \u8212- so too a sorceress is executed by stoning. This {\i smuchin} derivation appears in {\i Shemot} in {\i Parshat Mishpatim}, not in {\i Devarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Rabbi Yehuda: ve\u8217'chi mipnei she\u8217'samchu inyan lo notzi zeh le\u8217'skilah?} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yehuda} objects: just because they are juxtaposed we are going to stone someone? Juxtaposition alone is not a sufficient basis for a ruling of such gravity. Many things are juxtaposed in the Torah \u8212- in {\i Parshat Kedoshim} alone, the most disparate subjects appear side by side \u8212- and that cannot automatically serve as the basis for a {\i drasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela} \u8212- rather, says {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, I agree the sorceress is stoned, but not because of {\i smuchin}. {\i Ov veyidoni be\u8217'chlal machasheifim hayu} \u8212- the {\i ov} and {\i yidoni}, two forms of necromancy, were in the general category of sorcery. {\i Velama yatz\u8217'u} \u8212- why were they singled out? {\i Ba\u8217'even yirgemu otam dmeihem bam} \u8212- because the Torah states explicitly that they are stoned. {\i Lehakish lahem} \u8212- they were extracted from the general category in order to serve as a comparison: {\i mah ov veyidoni be\u8217'skilah af machasheifa be\u8217'skilah} \u8212- just as {\i ov} and {\i yidoni} are stoned, so too the sorceress. This is not {\i smuchin} but rather the standard hermeneutic of a {\i davar shehaya be\u8217'chlal veyatza min hakhlal lelamed} \u8212- a matter included in a general rule that was singled out to teach about the whole category \u8212- which is one of the standard thirteen hermeneutic principles. {\i Smuchin}, by contrast, is not among those thirteen principles, which is why {\i Rabbi Yehuda} refuses to employ it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishneh Torah Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217've\u8217'Mishneh Torah minalan de\u8217'darash?} \u8212- but how do we know that {\i Rabbi Yehuda} is willing to use {\i smuchin} in {\i Devarim}? {\i De\u8217'tanan} \u8212- because we learned in the {\i mishna} what follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Anusas Aviw\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mefutat Aviv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i noseh adam anusat aviv umefutat aviv, anusat beno umefutat beno} \u8212- a man may marry a woman his father raped or seduced, and a woman his son raped or seduced. The prohibition of {\i eshet av} \u8212- father\u8217's wife \u8212- and {\i eshet beno} \u8212- daughter-in-law \u8212- does not extend to women with whom the father or son had non-marital sexual relations. These are simply not included in those prohibitions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reb Yehuda Assur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda oser be\u8217'anusat aviv umefutat aviv} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yehuda} disagrees in the case of a woman the father had sexual relations with, whether by rape or seduction, and holds she is forbidden. He agrees, however, that a woman the son had relations with does not become forbidden to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knaf Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'amar Rav Gidal amar Rav: mai ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'Rebbe Yehuda?} \u8212- what is the basis for {\i Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's} position? The verse says: {\i lo yikach ish et eshet aviv velo yigaleh knaf aviv} \u8212- a man shall not marry his father\u8217's wife and shall not uncover the hem of his father. The phrase {\i velo yigaleh knaf aviv} \u8212- shall not uncover his father\u8217's hem \u8212- is initially puzzling. On one reading, it describes the wrongness of the act: this is the woman under whose garment-hem the father was; she is, as it were, {\i knaf aviv} \u8212- the covering of his father. On {\i Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's} reading, the phrase {\i velo yigaleh knaf aviv} \u8212- he shall not uncover any nakedness that was his father\u8217's \u8212- extends the prohibition beyond the formal category of {\i eshet av} to any woman the father had sexual relations with. The logic follows from the very meaning of {\i knaf}: what matters is not the formal marital bond but the fact of the father\u8217's intimacy with this woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smuchim Anusa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i smuchin} dimension enters. {\i U\u8217'mimai de\u8217'be\u8217'anusa ktiv?} \u8212- how do we know that the verse is referring specifically to a woman the father had relations with outside of marriage? {\i Me\u8217'iluya de\u8217'kraya} \u8212- because of what is written immediately before the verse in question. The {\i parasha} preceding {\i lo yikach ish et eshet aviv} is the passage about a man who rapes an unmarried woman: {\i venatan ha\u8217'ish hashochev imah la\u8217'avi hana\u8217'arah chamishim kasef} \u8212- and the man who lay with her shall pay fifty pieces of silver to the girl\u8217's father. It is the case of {\i anusa} \u8212- a raped woman \u8212- that is discussed just before {\i lo yikach ish et eshet aviv}. {\i Vesamich lei} \u8212- and then this prohibition is juxtaposed to it. Thus, {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, reading {\i velo yigaleh knaf aviv} in light of the immediately preceding context of {\i anusa}, understands the phrase as extending the prohibition to women the father had extra-marital relations with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, in fact, a use of {\i smuchin}. But it is {\i smuchin} in {\i Devarim} \u8212- this passage appears in {\i Parshat Ki Tetzei} in {\i Devarim} \u8212- which is exactly the point the {\i Gemara} is making: {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, who rejects {\i smuchin} in general, accepts it in {\i Mishneh Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- and what would the Rabbis respond? They would say: {\i ee hava samich lei ke\u8217'de\u8217'ka\u8217'amrat} \u8212- if it were truly juxtaposed immediately, as you say, you would be right. {\i Hashata de\u8217'lo samich lei} \u8212- but now that it is not directly juxtaposed, as the gloss makes clear \u8212- {\i de\u8217'chtiv lo yikach ish et eshet aviv beintayim} \u8212- since the verse {\i lo yikach ish et eshet aviv} intervenes between the {\i anusa} passage and {\i velo yigaleh knaf aviv} \u8212- it is not sufficiently proximate to derive the teaching through {\i smuchin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomeret Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, what does the Rabbis\u8217' reading of {\i velo yigaleh knaf aviv} refer to? {\i Beshomeret yavam hakatuv medaber} \u8212- the verse is speaking of a {\i shomeret yavam}, a woman who is quasi-married to the father by virtue of the bond of {\i zikah}. That is: if the father\u8217's brother died childless, the father now has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} toward his brother\u8217's widow. That widow is in a quasi-marital relationship with the father, since {\i zikah} \u8212- the {\i yibbum} bond \u8212- is like a form of inchoate marriage. {\i Velo yigaleh knaf aviv} then teaches that the son may not have relations with that woman either \u8212- she is effectively in the domain of the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then notes: but you do not really need a verse to prohibit this, because that woman is the son\u8217's aunt. The son is already forbidden to his aunt by a separate {\i issur}. The verse must therefore be coming to add a second prohibition \u8212- {\i lav aleiha bishnei lavin} \u8212- so that there are two distinct negative prohibitions applying to the act, not merely to spell out a novel {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Muchach Mufneh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Muchach Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the question of why {\i smuchin} is valid in {\i Mishneh Torah} in the first place. {\i U\u8217've\u8217'Mishneh Torah mai ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'darash?} \u8212- why does everybody agree to {\i smuchin} in {\i Devarim}? {\i Ee ba\u8217'it eima mishum de\u8217'muchach} \u8212- one answer: because it is evident from context. What does that mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De\u8217'im ken lichtov rachmana gabei arayot} \u8212- if the Torah wished to introduce the prohibition of {\i velo yigaleh knaf aviv} as a free-standing {\i ervah}, it should have written it back where it lists the {\i arayot} in {\i Acharei Mot} and {\i Kedoshim}. Why is it appearing here for the first time, in a different context, in {\i Devarim}? The very fact that this prohibition is introduced here \u8212- and not in its more natural location among the {\i arayot} \u8212- signals that it is here to communicate something specific to this context. The juxtaposition itself is communicating. When {\i Moshe Rabbeinu} recaps the laws of the Torah in {\i Devarim}, he sometimes frames things differently, places them in new sequences, and introduces them in new contexts. The {\i Gemara} treats this as hermeneutically significant: if something appears in {\i Devarim} that was not present in its natural earlier context, it is appearing here because this context is intended to teach us something that the earlier context could not. The new placement is itself the message.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mufneh Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'ee ba\u8217'it eima mishum de\u8217'mufni} \u8212- alternatively, because it is superfluous. {\i De\u8217'im ken lichtov rachmana lo yikach ish et eshet aviv velo yigaleh knaf} \u8212- the Torah could have simply said do not uncover the hem, without the word {\i aviv} \u8212- of his father. That is already obvious from the surrounding context. So the word {\i aviv} is extra, and something extra invites {\i drasha}. By being extra, it licenses us to read it in light of the adjacent {\i parasha} about {\i anusa} and derive a law from the juxtaposition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} acknowledges, however, that this second argument \u8212- {\i mufneh} \u8212- is less distinctive to {\i Mishneh Torah}. Extra words appear throughout the Torah, and learning from {\i smuchin} on the basis of a superfluous word is not something unique to {\i Devarim}. By contrast, the argument from {\i muchach} \u8212- the idea that something newly appearing in {\i Devarim} without having been in its original context must be communicating something specific to its new surroundings \u8212- is genuinely distinctive to {\i Mishneh Torah}. That is what makes {\i Devarim} a uniquely rich context for {\i smuchin}-based {\i drashot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzemer Upishtim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now applies the same two arguments \u8212- {\i muchach} and {\i mufneh} \u8212- to the case of {\i shatnez} and {\i tzitzit} in {\i Devarim}. {\i Ee ba\u8217'it eima mishum de\u8217'muchach: de\u8217'im ken lichtov rachmana gabei parashat tzitzit} \u8212- if there was nothing to derive from context, why is the prohibition of {\i shatnez} appearing here in {\i Devarim} alongside {\i tzitzit}? {\i Gedilim ta\u8217'aseh lach} was already written back in {\i Parshat Shelach} in {\i Bamidbar}. Why repeat it here? The new context itself communicates: the juxtaposition is intentional, and it teaches that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i tzitzit} overrides the prohibition of {\i shatnez}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'ee ba\u8217'it eima mishum de\u8217'mufni} \u8212- or alternatively, something in the verse is superfluous. The Torah already wrote in {\i Vayikra: uveged kilayim shatnez lo ya\u8217'aleh alecha}. The prohibition of {\i shatnez} is already there. {\i Lo tilbash shatnez lama li?} \u8212- why does it need to repeat the prohibition here in {\i Devarim}? The repetition is superfluous, and that superfluity licenses the {\i drasha} from {\i smuchin}. {\i Shema mina le\u8217'afnuyei} \u8212- conclude from this that it was made extra for the purpose of enabling the {\i drasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tilbash Elevation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges this: {\i hani mitzterechei} \u8212- you actually need both verses, and neither is superfluous. Here is why. {\i Dichasav rachmana lo ya\u8217'aleh alecha} \u8212- the verse in {\i Vayikra} says do not put [{\i shatnez}] upon you. {\i Hava amina kol derech ha\u8217'ala\u8217'ah asrah rachmana} \u8212- I would have thought: any form of placing {\i shatnez} on oneself is forbidden, even if it is not in a mode of wearing. {\i Va\u8217'afilu mochrei chsut} \u8212- even the fabric merchants who carry many garments stacked on their bodies in order to transport them. {\i Kasav rachmana lo tilbash shatnez} \u8212- therefore the Torah says in {\i Devarim}: do not {\i wear} {\i shatnez}. The verb {\i tilbash} \u8212- to wear \u8212- tells me it must be something comparable to genuine wearing: {\i dumya de\u8217'levisha de\u8217'it bah hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- similar to wearing in that there is tangible benefit and pleasure, not merely the act of placing it on oneself for transport purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hana\u8217'ah Levels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely: {\i de\u8217'ai khasav rachmana lo tilbash} \u8212- had the Torah only said do not wear, {\i hava amina davka levisha de\u8217'nafish hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- I would have said only actual wearing, where the benefit is substantial, is forbidden. {\i Aval ha\u8217'ala\u8217'ah} \u8212- but a blanket placed over oneself, {\i de\u8217'lo nafish hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- where the benefit is lesser \u8212- {\i la} \u8212- perhaps that would not be forbidden under {\i shatnez}. So {\i kasav rachmana lo ya\u8217'aleh alecha} \u8212- the verse in {\i Vayikra} comes to extend the prohibition to cases of placing on oneself that are not technically {\i levisha} but still involve a recognizable benefit. Each verse therefore carries independent work: {\i lo ya\u8217'aleh alecha} extends the prohibition beyond wearing; {\i lo tilbash} restricts it to acts that involve genuine personal benefit, excluding mere transport.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} then identifies what truly is superfluous. {\i Im kein lichtov rachmana lo tilbash shatnez \u8212- tzemer ufishtim lama li?} \u8212- if that is so, why does the verse in {\i Devarim} need to say {\i tzemer ufishtim yachdav} \u8212- wool and linen together? Why specify the composition of {\i shatnez}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One would think this is the least superfluous element \u8212- after all, without {\i tzemer ufishtim yachdav} we would not know what {\i shatnez} is. But the {\i Gemara} argues otherwise: {\i michdi kasav uveged kilayim shatnez lo ya\u8217'aleh alecha} \u8212- since the verse in {\i Vayikra} already says {\i uveged kilayim shatnez}, and {\i de\u8217'tana de\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- the school of {\i Rabbi Yishmael} teaches \u8212- {\i kol bi nimru baTorah stam begadim uferat lecha hakatuv be\u8217'echad meihem tzemer ufishtim} \u8212- wherever the Torah speaks of garments without specification, it means wool or linen garments, as established by the one instance where the Torah explicitly mentions {\i veged tzemer o veged pishtim} in the context of {\i tzara\u8217'at}. Thus, when {\i Vayikra} says {\i uveged kilayim shatnez} \u8212- a mixed garment \u8212- by the rule of {\i Rabbi Yishmael}, that means a garment mixing wool and linen. I would already have known {\i shatnez} means wool and linen. {\i Af kol tzemer ufishtim} \u8212- every garment is either wool or linen, and the mixing can only be wool with linen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then does {\i Devarim} repeat {\i tzemer ufishtim yachdav}? The {\i Gemara} says it is still needed: {\i salka da\u8217'atach amina ha\u8217'ala\u8217'ah hi de\u8217'lo nafish hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- I might have thought that the act of {\i ha\u8217'ala\u8217'ah} \u8212- placing on oneself \u8212- involves lesser benefit and pleasure, and in that context the derivation from {\i Rabbi Yishmael} about the composition of garments applies, so {\i shatnez} there means only wool and linen. {\i Aval levisha de\u8217'nafish hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- but wearing, where there is greater pleasure, {\i kol terei minei asar rachmana} \u8212- perhaps the Torah prohibited any two different types of fiber mixed together, not just wool and linen. The word {\i tzemer ufishtim yachdav} is therefore needed even in {\i Devarim}, to explicitly confirm that the definition of {\i shatnez} as wool-and-linen specifically applies to the more pleasurable act of {\i levisha} just as much as to {\i ha\u8217'ala\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So {\i tzemer ufishtim} is still not superfluous? The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i im kein lishtok kera minei veteisei shatnez shatnez mehala} \u8212- if so, let the verse in {\i Devarim} be silent about {\i tzemer ufishtim} and I would have derived it from a {\i gezeirah shavah}: the word {\i shatnez} appears in {\i Vayikra} and the word {\i shatnez} appears in {\i Devarim}; from the verbal analogy I would have learned that the definition of {\i shatnez} is the same in both verses \u8212- wool and linen \u8212- even for the act of {\i levisha}. Therefore, the explicit mention of {\i tzemer ufishtim yachdav} in the verse about {\i levisha} in {\i Devarim} is in fact superfluous. {\i Shema mina le\u8217'afnuyei} \u8212- it must be that it was made extra, in order to enable the {\i drasha} from the juxtaposition with {\i tzitzit}. And that {\i drasha} is what establishes the principle of {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'aseh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Summary Asei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Established Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We can now draw together the threads of this entire {\i sugya}. The {\i Gemara} set out to explain why a {\i pasuk} was needed to prohibit {\i yibbum} in the case of {\i arayot} \u8212- that is, why the {\i hava amina} existed at all that {\i yibbum} might override those prohibitions. The answer begins with the principle of {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'aseh}: a positive {\i mitzvah} overrides a negative prohibition. We derive this principle from the juxtaposition in {\i Devarim} of the prohibition of {\i shatnez} and the {\i mitzvah} of {\i tzitzit}, which teaches that one may attach a wool string to a linen garment even though that constitutes {\i shatnez}, because the positive {\i mitzvah} of {\i tzitzit} overrides the negative prohibition of {\i shatnez}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is accepted by everyone, including those who generally reject {\i smuchin} as a hermeneutic, because the juxtaposition appears in {\i Mishneh Torah} \u8212- in {\i Devarim} \u8212- where {\i smuchin} is universally valid. It is valid there either because something newly appearing in {\i Devarim} in a different context from its original setting is evidently communicating something by means of that new context ({\i muchach}), or because the repetition of material already stated elsewhere renders the repeated passage superfluous ({\i mufneh}), specifically the phrase {\i tzemer ufishtim yachdav}, which could have been derived from a {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i shatnez}\u8211-{\i shatnez} and therefore need not have been written out explicitly here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the principle of {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'aseh} thus firmly established and universally accepted, we have the foundation for the {\i hava amina}: one could have thought that the positive {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} would override the negative prohibition of {\i arayot} in the {\i yibbum} context. The reason that {\i hava amina} does not hold in practice \u8212- because the prohibition of {\i arayot} carries {\i kareit}, and {\i asei dochei lo ta\u8217'aseh} does not apply to prohibitions of such severity \u8212- is precisely the point the opening {\i baraita} of the {\i sugya} established through its derivation from the word {\i aleha}. The full picture is now in place, and the discussion will continue from here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum & Arayot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Review: Yibum Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins approximately fifteen lines down, at the words {\i amar lei Rav Acha mi-Difti}. To recall where we left off: after many dappin establishing why one might have thought that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} would apply even in a case of {\i arayot}, the Gemara worked through the entire framework of {\i asei docheh lo ta\u8217'asei}, ultimately concluding that this principle could not serve as a valid basis, since it does not apply where {\i karet} is at stake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then arrived at a different {\i hava amina} entirely. The very case of {\i yibum} is itself a case of {\i arayot} \u8212- namely, {\i eshet ach}, one\u8217's brother\u8217's wife \u8212- and this fact might have provided a basis for assuming that {\i yibum} could apply even in the presence of other {\i arayot}. The reasoning: either through the principle of {\i keivan de-ishtari ishtari} \u8212- once something is permitted to enable a particular outcome, other related obstacles should similarly be set aside \u8212- or through the logic that since a verse links all the {\i arayot} to one another, what is true of one may be true of the rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is on this basis that we require the verse containing the word {\i aleha}. That verse is in the prohibition of the wife\u8217's sister: {\i ve-ishah el achotah lo tikach litzror legalot ervatah aleha be-chayeha} \u8212- do not take a woman alongside her sister as a rival wife, to uncover her nakedness upon her, during her lifetime. The word {\i aleha} links this verse to {\i yevamah yavo aleha}, the operative phrase of {\i yibum}, thereby establishing that the prohibition of {\i achos ishah} applies even in the context of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha mi-Difti raised a pointed question to Ravina: {\i mikdi kol arayot itka\u8217'u le-eshet ach ve-itka\u8217'u le-achos ishah} \u8212- the verse links all {\i arayot} to one another, and we now have two {\i arayot} in play: {\i eshet ach}, which tells us that {\i yibum} is operative, and {\i achos ishah}, which tells us, via {\i aleha}, that {\i yibum} does not apply. Given this equipoise, why do we assume that {\i achos ishah} sets the rule? Why not link all other {\i arayot} to {\i eshet ach} and conclude that {\i yibum} applies in all such cases, with {\i achos ishah} standing as the sole exception?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Prohibition Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers two responses. The first is simply a matter of interpretive default: {\i le-kula u-le-chumra, le-chumra makshinan} \u8212- when one has a choice between linking toward leniency or stringency, one defaults to stringency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second answer is conceptually richer: {\i hacha terei issurei ve-hacha chad issura, trei mi-trei yalfanan}. Any other {\i ervah} arising in a {\i yibum} context carries two distinct prohibitions \u8212- the standing prohibition of {\i eshet ach} that always applies to a brother\u8217's wife, plus the additional prohibition specific to that {\i ervah} \u8212- daughter-in-law, mother-in-law, or whichever it might be. {\i Achos ishah} in a {\i yibum} context is exactly analogous: it too carries the prohibition of {\i eshet ach} combined with the prohibition of {\i achos ishah}. The simple case of {\i yibum} \u8212- {\i eshet ach} alone \u8212- carries only one prohibition. It is therefore far more apt to derive the rule for other {\i arayot} in a {\i yibum} context from {\i achos ishah}, which like them carries two prohibitions, than from the isolated case of {\i eshet ach} with its single prohibition. One does not derive a two-prohibition case from a one-prohibition case: {\i terei mi-terei yalfanan, aval me-chad lo yalfanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: No Verse Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now stakes out a more radical position: {\i erva gufah lo tzericha kra de-ein asei docheh lo ta\u8217'asei she-yesh bo karet}. The prohibition of {\i ervah} requires no verse at all, because it would never have occurred to anyone that {\i yibum} \u8212- a positive commandment \u8212- could override a {\i lo ta\u8217'asei} that carries {\i karet}. The principle of {\i asei docheh lo ta\u8217'asei} simply does not operate in the presence of {\i karet}, and so no {\i hava amina} exists in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might immediately object: but we just established a stronger {\i hava amina}, the argument of {\i keivan de-ishtari ishtari} \u8212- once one prohibition has been set aside to enable {\i yibum}, perhaps another should be as well. Tosafot addresses this directly. First, Rava may simply not hold of Ulla\u8217's principle of {\i keivan de-ishtari ishtari} at all. But Tosafot goes further and offers a more precise conceptual distinction: the case where {\i keivan de-ishtari ishtari} operates \u8212- the classic example involving a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} and a {\i metzora} \u8212- involves a single shared {\i issur}, namely a {\i tamei} person entering the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. Regardless of the source of one\u8217's {\i tumah}, the prohibition being waived is one and the same. By contrast, in our case, the Torah waives the prohibition of {\i eshet ach} to enable {\i yibum}, but the prohibition of {\i achos ishah} is an entirely separate {\i issur} with its own name and status. Allowing one {\i issur ervah} does not entail allowing a different {\i issur ervah}. The two prohibitions are not merely different degrees of intensity of one thing; they are categorically distinct. Even if one accepts {\i keivan de-ishtari ishtari} in principle, it would not apply across categorically distinct {\i issurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's conclusion, then, is that one never had a genuine {\i hava amina} that {\i yibum} could override {\i ervah}. What, then, does the verse accomplish? {\i Ki itztarich kra le-maseir tzarah} \u8212- the verse was needed not to exclude {\i ervah}, which we knew, but to establish the law of the {\i tzarah}, the co-wife. This is precisely the {\i chiddush} that our Mishnah opens with: {\i chamesh esrei nashim potrot tzaroteihen} \u8212- fifteen women exempt their co-wives. The Mishnah highlights the exemption of the {\i tzarah}, not the exemption of the {\i ervah} herself, because the latter is taken for granted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism works as follows: the word {\i aleha} links the verse to {\i yibum}, placing the verse in the context of {\i yibum}. Once the verse is speaking about a {\i yibum} context, it is positioned to address the {\i litzror} clause \u8212- that not only does the {\i ervah} herself not undergo {\i yibum}, but her co-wife does not either.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava: Tzarah is Key\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection to Rava\u8217's reading. If {\i ervah} requires no verse, why does the {\i braisa} itself appear to be proving precisely that point? The {\i braisa} uses the {\i aleha} linkage to conclude that {\i yibum} does not apply in the case of {\i achos ishah}, and only then proceeds to ask: {\i ein li ela hi \u8212- tzarasa minayin?} The {\i braisa}\u8217's very structure seems to treat the {\i ervah} exclusion as the primary {\i chiddush} requiring derivation, with the {\i tzarah} exclusion following as a secondary consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Responds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's answer is that the {\i braisa} is not trying to prove the {\i ervah} case on its merits. It establishes the {\i ervah} context only as a necessary step toward proving the case of {\i tzarah}. The {\i braisa} must first get the verse talking about {\i yibum} and {\i ervah} \u8212- not because we needed proof of the {\i ervah} rule, which we could have derived ourselves \u8212- but in order to introduce the {\i litzror} clause and derive from it the law of the {\i tzarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The objection is then extended: the {\i braisa} does not stop with {\i achos ishah}. It then asks how we know the rule applies to all other {\i arayot}, and derives this through a {\i mah matzinu} \u8212- reasoning by analogy from {\i achos ishah} to other {\i arayot}. And similarly, it asks how we know that the {\i tzarot} of those other {\i arayot} are also excluded, and again uses analogy. The Gemara suggests that this expanded structure also seems to prove the {\i arayot} exclusion as a primary {\i chiddush}. Rava\u8217's response is the same: the entire progression through {\i achos ishah} and the other {\i arayot} serves only to set up the corresponding {\i tzarah} cases. At each stage, the {\i braisa} is moving toward the law of {\i tzarah}; the {\i ervah} cases are vehicles, not destinations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Braisa Cited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To ground this discussion, it is worth recalling the {\i braisa} as it appears on 3b. The text reads: {\i isha el achosah lo tikach litzror} \u8212- the verse against marrying a wife\u8217's sister. Then: {\i aleha} \u8212- {\i mah talmud lomar?} What does this teach? The verse is juxtaposed with {\i yevamah yavo aleha} \u8212- the operative language of {\i yibum} \u8212- through a {\i gezeirah shavah} on the shared word {\i aleha}. The {\i braisa} concludes that this prohibition applies {\i bimkom mitzvah}, in a context of {\i yibum}, and the Torah says: {\i lo sikach}. It then asks: {\i ein li ela hi, tzaratah minayin?} \u8212- I have derived only her; from where do I derive the co-wife? The answer: {\i talmud lomar litzror}. And then: {\i ein li ela tzaratah, tzaras tzaratah minayin?} \u8212- from where do I derive the co-wife of the co-wife? The answer: the doubled form {\i litzror} rather than the simple {\i latzor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} then extends to all other {\i arayot} through a {\i mah matzinu}: just as {\i achos ishah} is a {\i ervah} for which one is liable, so too all other {\i arayot} are excluded from {\i yibum}, and so too their co-wives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava reads the entire structure as oriented toward {\i tzarah}. The {\i braisa} needed to get {\i achos ishah} into a {\i yibum} context in order to generate the {\i litzror} derivation; it needed to extend the {\i arayot} framework in order to cover the {\i tzarot} of those other {\i arayot}. None of it was proving something we didn\u8217't already know about {\i ervah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a competing tradition: {\i Rabbi omer} \u8212- Rabbi derives the same laws from different verses. The relevant verses in the {\i yibum} passage read: {\i velakachah lo leishah veyibmah} \u8212- and he shall take her to be his wife and perform {\i yibum} with her. Rabbi notes that the Torah could have written {\i velakach} and {\i veyibam} \u8212- and he shall take, and he shall perform {\i yibum} \u8212- without the pronominal suffix. Instead it writes {\i velakachah} and {\i veyibmah} \u8212- her specifically. The feminine suffix, Rabbi argues, is exclusionary: it is {\i her} he takes and performs {\i yibum} with, not others in certain related situations. Specifically: one {\i heh} excludes the {\i ervah} herself from {\i yibum}, and the other excludes the {\i tzaras ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reading thus yields: {\i velakachah} \u8212- her but not an {\i ervah}; {\i veyibmah} \u8212- her but not the co-wife of an {\i ervah}. This is an entirely different {\i drasha} from the {\i achos ishah} \u8212- {\i aleha} derivation. The weakness of Rabbi\u8217's {\i drasha} is readily apparent: there is nothing in {\i velakachah} or {\i veyibmah} that hints specifically at {\i ervah} or {\i tzaras ervah}; one must import those concepts entirely from without.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Drashas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava reads Rabbi\u8217's {\i drasha} through his own lens. Since Rava holds that {\i ervah} needs no verse, he reinterprets the two suffixed forms accordingly. {\i Velakachah} means: her, and not the {\i tzarah} \u8212- there is no {\i yibum} for the co-wife of an {\i ervah}. {\i Veyibmah} specifies that this exclusion operates {\i bimkom yibum}, in a {\i yibum} context. The practical upshot of this second specification is critical: it tells us that the concept of {\i tzarah} as a problem is limited to the {\i yibum} context. Outside of {\i yibum}, the co-wife of a woman forbidden to me as an {\i ervah} is not forbidden to me by virtue of being her {\i tzarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus, according to Rava, Rabbi\u8217's two words together teach: no {\i yibum} for a {\i tzarah} in a {\i yibum} context \u8212- and the implication that outside a {\i yibum} context, the {\i tzarah} relationship creates no prohibition. The {\i ervah} herself, meanwhile, we already knew was excluded without any verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Observes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi adds that Rava\u8217's reading is implicit in the Mishnah itself. The Mishnah opens: {\i chamesh esrei nashim potrot tzaroteihen} \u8212- fifteen women exempt their {\i tzarot}. Crucially, the Mishnah does not say \u8220"fifteen women are themselves exempt {\i and} exempt their {\i tzarot}.\u8221" It simply states that they exempt their {\i tzarot}, taking their own exemption entirely for granted. {\i Shema mina} \u8212- this confirms that the {\i chiddush} of the Mishnah is the law of {\i tzarah}, not the law of {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzarah Logic Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the verse is needed for {\i tzarah} rather than {\i ervah}, the Gemara poses a symmetrical challenge: {\i tzarah nami lo tiba\u8217'i kra} \u8212- perhaps {\i tzarah} likewise needs no verse. Just as {\i ervah} needs no verse because {\i asei} cannot override {\i lo ta\u8217'asei she-yesh bo karet}, perhaps the same is true of {\i tzarah}. If {\i tzarah} is itself an {\i issur} carrying {\i karet}, one should have been able to derive the same conclusion without a special derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Explains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi clarifies the Gemara\u8217's reasoning. Without linking the verse to a {\i yibum} context, one would have read the verse straightforwardly: the prohibition of {\i achos ishah} and her {\i tzarah} apply as a general {\i issur ervah} in all cases. And since {\i karet} is stated at the end of the {\i arayot} section \u8212- {\i ve-nichresu} \u8212- one would have concluded that {\i tzaras ervah} is independently forbidden with {\i karet} in all circumstances, not only in a {\i yibum} context. Therefore, just as {\i ervah} itself needs no verse since {\i asei} cannot override a {\i karet}-bearing prohibition, so too {\i tzarah} should need no verse on the same grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's question, then, is: what work does the {\i drasha} actually do?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarah Contexts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the answer, it is essential to distinguish two scenarios. In one scenario \u8212- {\i bimkom mitzvah} \u8212- the {\i tzarah} arises within a {\i yibum} context. For example: Reuven is married to Rachel and Dvorah, and Shimon is married to Leah. Reuven and Shimon are brothers, and Rachel and Leah are sisters. When Shimon dies childless, Reuven cannot perform {\i yibum} with Leah, his wife\u8217's sister. Since there is no {\i mitzvat yibum} with Leah, Dvorah \u8212- who is Reuven\u8217's co-wife to Leah, his brother\u8217's wife \u8212- also has no {\i mitzvat yibum}. Once the {\i mitzvah} falls away, Dvorah reverts to the default status of {\i eshet ach} with no operative {\i mitzvah}, and she becomes forbidden. This is the law of {\i tzaras ervah bimkom mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second scenario \u8212- {\i shelo bimkom mitzvah} \u8212- a different man, Gideon, is married to Rachel and Dvorah, with no brother-sister relationship to Reuven\u8217's wife. When Gideon dies, Reuven cannot marry Rachel because she is his wife\u8217's sister. But Dvorah? She is permitted. She was a co-wife to someone who was an {\i ervah} to Reuven, but the context of {\i yibum} is entirely absent. The concept of {\i tzaras ervah} as an exclusion from {\i yibum} simply does not apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami bar Chama Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama now poses a sharp question to Rava: {\i eima ha-ervah gufah shelo bimkom mitzvah tishteri} \u8212- if {\i tzarah} is permitted outside the {\i yibum} context, why not say the same about {\i ervah} itself? If the verse\u8217's role is to limit the {\i tzarah} prohibition to a {\i yibum} context, perhaps all {\i arayot} prohibitions are similarly limited to {\i yibum} contexts. Outside of {\i yibum}, perhaps the wife\u8217's sister and all other {\i arayot} should be permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately rejects the suggestion regarding {\i ervah} itself: {\i lav kal va-chomer hu?} \u8212- is this not a {\i kal va-chomer}? If {\i ervah} is forbidden even {\i bimkom mitzvah}, all the more so is she forbidden {\i shelo bimkom mitzvah}. Rava responds to the parallel question about {\i tzarah}: {\i tatim alecha kra} \u8212- the verse bears you down. The verse says {\i be-chayeha} \u8212- during her lifetime. This phrase establishes a sweeping, time-bound prohibition of {\i achos ishah} in all contexts: as long as one\u8217's wife is alive, the sister is categorically forbidden regardless of circumstance. The word {\i aleha} in the verse then specifically links this prohibition to {\i yibum}, limiting the {\i tzarah} exclusion to that context. {\i Bimkom aleha hu de-asira} \u8212- it is in the {\i yibum} context that the {\i tzarah} is also forbidden; {\i shelo bimkom aleha sharya} \u8212- outside that context, the {\i tzarah} is permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Verse Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna: Trei Krai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara identifies a deeper textual problem. Rav Huna bar Tachlifa, citing Rava, observes: {\i trei krai ktivi} \u8212- the verse of {\i achos ishah} contains, in effect, two distinct textual sections that appear to contradict one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining the contradiction, the Gemara notes that {\i be-chayeha} is needed for a more fundamental {\i halacha}: to tell us that the prohibition of {\i achos ishah} applies even after the first wife has been divorced, not only during marriage. One might have inferred from the phrase {\i ishah el achosah} \u8212- a woman alongside her sister \u8212- that the prohibition depends on the co-wife relationship being active, and that upon divorce the sister becomes permissible. Therefore, {\i be-chayeha} teaches {\i kol she-be-chayeha}: as long as the first wife is alive, the prohibition stands, even after divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciling Verse Halves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first half of the verse reads: {\i ve-ishah el achotah lo tikach litzror} \u8212- and a woman alongside her sister you shall not take as a rival. The word {\i litzror} implies {\i both} the sister and her {\i tzarah} are forbidden, since it references the co-wife relationship. The second half reads: {\i legalot ervatah aleha be-chayeha} \u8212- to uncover her nakedness; {\i ervatah} is singular, implying only {\i she} is forbidden, with no mention of a {\i tzarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two halves appear in tension. {\i Trei krai ktivi} \u8212- there are two messages embedded in one verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Cases Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is: {\i bimkom mitzvah shteihem asurot} \u8212- in the {\i yibum} context, both she and the {\i tzarah} are forbidden. {\i Shelo bimkom mitzvah, hi asurah ve-tzaratah muteret} \u8212- outside the {\i yibum} context, she alone is forbidden and the {\i tzarah} is permitted. The first half of the verse \u8212- she and her {\i tzarah} are forbidden \u8212- speaks to the {\i yibum} context; the second half \u8212- singular {\i ervatah}, only she \u8212- speaks to the non-{\i yibum} context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversing the Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges: {\i ipcha ana} \u8212- why not reverse the assignment? Why not say that {\i bimkom mitzvah} only she is forbidden while the {\i tzarah} is permitted, and {\i shelo bimkom mitzvah} both are forbidden? This alternative is not entirely unreasonable: one could argue that {\i tzaras ervah} is a general {\i ervah} status applying broadly, but within a {\i yibum} context the {\i tzarah} prohibition is overridden to enable {\i yibum} \u8212- just as the {\i eshet ach} prohibition is overridden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversal Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects the reversal: {\i im kein lo yomar aleha} \u8212- if that were correct, the verse would not need the word {\i aleha} at all. Without {\i aleha}, one would naturally read the two halves of the verse as follows: the first prohibits both her and the {\i tzarah}, the second prohibits only her. To reconcile them, the logical inference would be to assign the more permissive reading to the {\i yibum} context \u8212- that is, only she is forbidden in {\i yibum} and the {\i tzarah} is not \u8212- in order to enable {\i yibum}. Without {\i aleha}, one would have arrived at the reversed rule as the most natural reading. The Torah therefore writes {\i aleha}, explicitly linking the verse to {\i yibum}, to signal that the assignment is the {\i opposite} of what we would have inferred: in the {\i yibum} context, both are forbidden. The {\i legalot ervatah} \u8212- singular \u8212- refers to the non-{\i yibum} case, where only she is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi: Permit?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi poses a final alternative reading to Rav Kahana: {\i mi-mai de-hai aleha le-issura, dilma le-hetera} \u8212- how do we know that {\i aleha} comes to restrict, i.e., to forbid the {\i tzarah} in the {\i yibum} context? Perhaps {\i aleha} comes to permit. On this reading, {\i ve-ishah el achotah lo tikach litzror} establishes the general prohibition against both the sister and her {\i tzarah}, while {\i legalot ervatah aleha be-chayeha} comes to say: but {\i bimkom aleha} \u8212- in the context of {\i yibum} \u8212- perhaps both are permitted, with {\i yibum} overriding both prohibitions. {\i Aval bimkom aleha shtehen mutarot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara dispatches this reading: {\i im ken legalot ervah de-chada heichi mashkachat lah?} If {\i aleha} permits everything in the {\i yibum} context, there would never be a case where only the singular {\i ervatah} is at issue. On Rav Ashi\u8217's proposed reading, the possibilities are: outside {\i yibum}, both are forbidden; inside {\i yibum}, both are permitted. But {\i legalot ervatah} \u8212- the singular \u8212- requires a case where only she, and not the {\i tzarah}, is forbidden. That case is the non-{\i yibum} scenario, and it must remain: {\i hi asurah ve-tzaratah muteret}. The singular {\i ervatah} cannot find its referent if both cases are symmetric. The reversal therefore fails, and the original resolution stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize Rava\u8217's ultimate position: {\i ervah} is excluded from {\i yibum} without requiring any verse, since {\i asei} cannot override {\i lo ta\u8217'asei she-yesh bo karet}, and no serious {\i hava amina} ever existed. The real {\i chiddush} introduced by the verse \u8212- through {\i aleha} linking to {\i yibum}, and through {\i litzror} \u8212- is the law of {\i tzarah}. And the verse must do double duty: it must both establish that {\i tzaras ervah} is excluded from {\i yibum bimkom mitzvah}, and clarify that {\i tzaras ervah} is {\i not} a general {\i issur ervah} extending to non-{\i yibum} contexts. That limiting function \u8212- {\i shelo bimkom mitzvah sharya tzarah} \u8212- is what the word {\i aleha} accomplishes. For Rabbi, the same twin conclusions are derived from {\i velakachah} and {\i veyibmah}, with Rava reading those suffixed forms as pointing exclusively to {\i tzarah}, and the {\i veyibmah} specifying that the exclusion operates within a {\i yibum} context.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 9\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Ervah Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Drashos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the bottom of 8b, at the {\i gufa} \u8212- the main body of the {\i sugya}. At this point we have already worked through the question of why a {\i pasuk} is necessary to establish that there is no {\i yibum} in the case of {\i ervah}. The difficulty was that one might have tried to derive the exclusion from the case of {\i achos isha} \u8212- wife\u8217's sister \u8212- on the grounds that {\i yibum} already overrides that aspect of the {\i ervah} prohibition, and precisely for that reason a special {\i pasuk} is needed to place the case of wife\u8217's sister back into a {\i yibum} context. That was the first {\i drasha}, based on the word {\i aleha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i drasha} was Rabbi\u8217's \u8212- more was being read into the {\i pasuk} of {\i velakach veyibmah} \u8212- and it was less immediately transparent. Rabbi reads the phrase as indicating that only she, and not another, is subject to the obligation of {\i yibum}, implying that there are cases where the {\i mitzvah} cannot be performed: specifically, the case of {\i ervah} and the case of {\i tzarat ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further critical point emerged in this context: once the Gemara introduces the concept of {\i tzarat ervah}, one might reasonably have assumed that the {\i tzara} prohibition applies even outside the context of {\i yibum}. If a man\u8217's brother dies and one of the brother\u8217's wives is an {\i ervah} to the surviving brother, perhaps the co-wife is also forbidden to him as a {\i tzarat ervah} even in ordinary circumstances \u8212- not merely at the moment of {\i yibum}. The {\i pasuk} therefore had to make emphatically clear that {\i tzarat ervah} is only a category in the context of {\i yibum}. The logic is that since there is no {\i mitzvas yibum} with the co-wife of an {\i ervah}, she falls back under the prohibition of {\i eshet ach} \u8212- at least, that is the most straightforward explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi\u8217's Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Velakach Veyibmah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that background in place, the {\i gufa} turns to examine Rabbi\u8217's {\i drasha} from {\i velakach veyibmah} directly. Rabbi argues: the {\i pasuk} could have written {\i velakach} alone, and it could have written {\i veyibem} alone, yet it says {\i velakcha veyibmah} \u8212- with the extra {\i hei} in each word \u8212- {\i le-asur tzaros ervayos}: to exclude the co-wives of {\i arayos} from the obligation of {\i yibum}. As Rava noted in the preceding discussion, the exclusion of {\i ervah} herself could be taken for granted; it is specifically {\i tzarat ervah} that requires a textual source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzara Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately presses: where exactly is the concept of {\i tzara} to be found in this {\i pasuk}? And further, is not {\i tzara} already derived from the word {\i litzror} in the {\i pasuk} concerning {\i achos isha} \u8212- where the very root connoting {\i tzara} appears in the context of a {\i ervah}? Why abandon so elegant a source? The Gemara answers: Rabbi needs {\i litzror} for an entirely different {\i drasha} \u8212- one that will be explained presently \u8212- and therefore {\i litzror} is not available to Rabbi for the ordinary case of {\i tzarat ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Ervah Review (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika & Achos Zekuka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i drasha} Rabbi applies to {\i litzror} concerns the concept of {\i zika} \u8212- the quasi-connection that exists between a surviving brother and his deceased brother\u8217's widows prior to {\i yibum}. The understanding of {\i zika} is that some remnant of the deceased brother\u8217's marriage continues and attaches itself to the living brother; when {\i yibum} is actually performed, that potential is actualized into a full marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i case} Rabbi derives from {\i litzror} is the following: two brothers \u8212- call them Reuven and Shimon \u8212- are each married to one of two sisters. Both brothers die. A third brother, Levi, now has a {\i mitzvas yibum} to both women. One might have thought: since Levi is not personally forbidden to either Rachel or Leah, let him choose one of them and perform {\i yibum} with her. But Rabbi Shimon rules that he cannot perform {\i yibum} at all. Here is why: because Levi has a {\i zika} \u8212- a quasi-{\i kiddushin} \u8212- to both women simultaneously. If he performs {\i yibum} with Rachel, he thereby becomes fully married to the sister of Leah, to whom he still has an active {\i zika}. Since {\i zika} functions as a form of quasi-{\i kiddushin}, completing the marriage with one renders him the husband of the {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- the sister of the woman to whom he remains {\i zakuk}. That is a genuine {\i ervah} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's language is precise on this point: {\i v\u8217'zika havei k\u8217'ktzat kiddushin} \u8212- {\i zika} is like a partial {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and therefore {\i v\u8217'zekukaso havei ke-ishto} \u8212- the woman to whom he has a {\i zika} is treated as his wife. He cannot do {\i yibum} with either one, though he must perform {\i chalitza}. The source for this ruling is {\i litzror}: when two {\i yevamos} become competitors \u8212- {\i tzaros} \u8212- to one another through the {\i zika}, as when they are sisters, the {\i pasuk} teaches {\i lo yihyeh lecha likuchin afilu be-achas mehen} \u8212- you cannot even execute a {\i likuchin} with either of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the case of {\i achos zekuka} \u8212- the sister of the woman to whom there is {\i zika} \u8212- and it is a categorically different situation from the ordinary {\i tzarat ervah}. This explains why Rabbi cannot use {\i litzror} for the standard case of {\i tzarat ervah}: he needs it for this more specialized {\i drasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi\u8217's Drasha (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Wives & Choice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established what Rabbi does with {\i litzror}, the Gemara returns to the original question: how does Rabbi actually read the concept of {\i tzara} into the {\i pasuk} of {\i velakcha veyibmah}? The answer proceeds as follows. The {\i pasuk} uses the form {\i velakcha} \u8212- with the extra {\i hei} \u8212- rather than the simpler {\i velakach}. The significance of {\i velakcha} is that it implies {\i hi v\u8217'lo acheres} \u8212- this one and not another \u8212- which only makes sense if there are two women to choose between. The {\i pasuk} is therefore speaking about a situation with two potential {\i likuchin}, where the brother could take this one or that one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation is: {\i kol heicha d\u8217'ika terei likuchin d\u8217'ee ba\u8217'i nasiv hai v\u8217'ee ba\u8217'i nasiv hai, sharei} \u8212- wherever there are two marriages potentially available, and the brother is free to choose either, then there is a {\i mitzvas yibum}. But {\i v\u8217'ee lo, trevaihu asirin} \u8212- if he cannot exercise a free choice with either of them, both are forbidden. The implicit premise \u8212- already established \u8212- is that there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with an {\i ervah}. So if one of the two wives is an {\i ervah}, the brother cannot freely choose her; and since he cannot choose either freely, neither one is subject to {\i yibum}. That is how {\i tzara} is read into {\i velakcha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Viyibmah & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then does {\i veyibmah} contribute? {\i B\u8217'makom yibum hu d\u8217'asira tzara, shelo b\u8217'makom yibum sharya tzara} \u8212- the prohibition of {\i tzara} applies only in the context of {\i yibum}, not outside it. As reviewed earlier, the concern might have arisen that a {\i tzarat ervah} should be forbidden as a general matter. {\i Veyibmah} makes clear that this prohibition is confined strictly to the moment of {\i yibum} obligation. Outside that context, the co-wife of an {\i ervah} carries no such disability. This completes Rabbi\u8217's reading of {\i velakcha veyibmah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cross-Exam Drashos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Velakcha & Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With both {\i drashos} now mapped out, the Gemara proceeds to its characteristic cross-examination: what does each opinion do with the textual sources relied upon by the other? The Rabbanan use {\i velakcha} and {\i veyibmah} differently. The extra {\i hei} in {\i velakcha}, according to the statement of Rabbi Yose berabbi Chanina, teaches that {\i yibum} creates a normal marriage status: {\i she-megarshah be-get u-machzirah} \u8212- the {\i yavam} may divorce the {\i yevama} with a {\i get}, and if he does, he may subsequently remarry her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two important {\i chiddushim} are embedded in this ruling. First, after {\i yibum} is performed, there can be a divorce \u8212- one might have thought the {\i mitzvah} created a permanent bond. Second, and more striking, even after he divorces her, she does not revert to being {\i achos ishto}. One might have supposed that the dispensation that enabled the {\i yibum} was exhausted by the first act, or at minimum by the marriage as a whole, such that upon divorce she would reassume her former status as a forbidden relative. The {\i pasuk} teaches otherwise: {\i velakcha} tells us that {\i yibum} confers a completely normal {\i kiddushin} status, with all the attendant consequences \u8212- divorce and remarriage alike.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Viyibmah & Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The extra {\i hei} of {\i veyibmah}, according to the Rabbanan, teaches that {\i yibum} may be effectuated even {\i al korchah} \u8212- even against the woman\u8217's will. This is a conceptually significant distinction from ordinary {\i kiddushin}, which requires the woman\u8217's consent. The explanation is that since the {\i zika} already transfers to the living brother \u8212- constituting a quasi-{\i kiddushin} already in place \u8212- the subsequent act of intercourse merely actualizes a potential that preexists. Therefore neither explicit consent nor even conscious intention is required. The Gemara extends this principle: if the {\i yavam} did not realize the woman was his {\i yevama} \u8212- or even, in the Gemara\u8217's more extreme formulation, if he fell from a roof and accidentally had intercourse with her \u8212- the {\i yibum} is nonetheless valid. The requirement of {\i kavanah} that applies in other halachic contexts does not apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: since Rabbi uses {\i velakcha veyibmah} to derive the laws of {\i tzarat ervah}, where does he obtain the rulings that the Rabbanan derive from those words \u8212- the normal marriage status enabling divorce and remarriage, and the {\i al korchah} principle? Rabbi answers: both are derived from other places in the same passage. The rule that {\i yibum} creates a marriage with full wife status \u8212- enabling divorce and remarriage \u8212- he derives from the words {\i velakachah lo le-ishah}, \u8220"and he shall take her to be his wife,\u8221" which establishes the normal {\i ishah} status. The {\i al korchah} principle \u8212- that the act is valid even without consent \u8212- he derives from {\i yevamah yavo aleha}, \u8220"the brother-in-law shall come upon her,\u8221" which by its phrasing implies that the act takes effect regardless of circumstance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aleha & Kares\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the Gemara to a pivotal question: what does Rabbi do with the word {\i aleha} \u8212- the word the Rabbanan use to situate the prohibition of {\i tzarat ervah} within the context of {\i yibum}? The answer is that Rabbi uses {\i aleha} for an entirely different, and far-reaching, legal principle derived from a {\i braisa}: the category of sin for which {\i beis din} and private individuals bring a {\i chatat} offering is limited exclusively to sins whose deliberate violation is punishable by {\i kares} and whose inadvertent commission by a private individual incurs a {\i chatat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} in {\i Vayikra} concerning the {\i chatat} of the congregation contains the phrase {\i asher chatu aleha}; the {\i pasuk} in the {\i ervah} section concerning {\i achos isha} likewise contains the word {\i aleha}. By means of a {\i gezeirah shavah}, Rabbi links these two instances: just as {\i aleha} by {\i achos isha} pertains to something punishable by {\i kares}, so too {\i aleha} in the context of the congregational {\i chatat} indicates that the relevant category of sin is specifically one punishable by {\i kares}. According to Rabbi, then, {\i aleha} by {\i achos isha} teaches not about {\i tzarat ervah} \u8212- which he derives elsewhere \u8212- but about the threshold of {\i kares} that defines the entire domain of {\i chatat} liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatat Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} cited here articulates the principle with precision: {\i ein chayavin beis din ela al davar shezdonoh kares v\u8217'shigagato chatat} \u8212- {\i beis din} incurs liability for a ruling error only when the sin in question is one that, if committed intentionally, would render the individual liable to {\i kares}, and if committed inadvertently, would require a private individual to bring a {\i chatat}. The same limitation applies to the {\i korban} of the {\i Kohen Gadol} ({\i mashiach}) when he sins, and similarly to the congregational {\i korban} for {\i avodah zarah} \u8212- only the categories of {\i avodah zarah} that carry {\i kares} for intentional violation fall within this framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction within {\i avodah zarah} may seem surprising. Not all forms of {\i avodah zarah} carry the same weight. Rashi specifies the classic {\i avodah zarah} acts that do incur {\i kares}: slaughtering to an idol ({\i zivuach}), burning incense ({\i ketores}), libation ({\i nissukh}), and prostration ({\i hishtachavayah}). But more expressive acts of devotion \u8212- embracing an idol or kissing it \u8212- fall under a {\i lav} rather than {\i kares} and do not trigger the congregational {\i korban} if performed inadvertently en masse. The halachic world of {\i chatat} is thus consistently bounded by the {\i kares} threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Congregation General\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} that anchors this principle for the congregation is {\i v\u8217'noda hachatat asher chatu aleha} (Vayikra 4), in the section discussing the inadvertent communal sin. The word {\i aleha} links, by {\i gezeirah shavah}, to the word {\i aleha} in the {\i ervah} passage, where {\i achos isha} carries {\i kares}. The result is that the congregational {\i chatat} obligation is established as pertaining only to sins of the {\i kares} category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i Kohen Gadol} \u8212- referred to in the {\i pasuk} as the {\i mashiach} \u8212- the derivation takes a different route. The {\i pasuk} states {\i im hakohen hamashiach yecheta le-ashmat ha-am} \u8212- if the anointed Kohen sins to the guilt of the people. The Gemara reads {\i le-ashmat ha-am} not merely in its plain sense (that the nation bears the consequences of the Kohen Gadol\u8217's error) but halachically: {\i harei mashiach k\u8217'tzibbur} \u8212- the Kohen Gadol\u8217's sin is treated as equivalent to the sin of the people. Since the congregational {\i chatat} is limited to {\i kares}-category sins (established above), the {\i Kohen Gadol}\u8217's {\i chatat} is likewise so limited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nasi & Yachid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i nasi} and the private individual, the derivation proceeds via {\i gezeirah shavah} using the word {\i mitzvos}. The {\i pasuk} concerning the {\i nasi} reads {\i asher nasi yecheta v\u8217'asa achas mikol mitzvos Hashem}, and the {\i pasuk} for the individual reads {\i v\u8217'nefesh achas techeta v\u8217'asata achas mikol mitzvos}. Both contain {\i mikol mitzvos}. The same phrase {\i v\u8217'asu achas mikol mitzvos} appears in the congregational {\i chatat} section in {\i Vayikra}. Through the verbal parallel of {\i mitzvos} with {\i mitzvos}, the {\i kares} requirement established for the congregation (via {\i aleha}) is extended to the {\i nasi} and the {\i yachid}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A whiteboard layout helps clarify the chain: (1) the congregational {\i chatat} in {\i Vayikra} contains {\i aleha}, linking through the {\i ervah} passage to {\i kares}; (2) the phrase {\i le-ashmat ha-am} equates the {\i Kohen Gadol} with the congregation, extending the {\i kares} requirement upward to him; (3) the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i mitzvos}/{\i mitzvos} brings the {\i nasi} and the {\i yachid} into the same orbit. Then, for the congregational {\i korban} of {\i avodah zarah} in {\i Bamidbar} 15, the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i me\u8217'einei}/{\i me\u8217'einei} \u8212- the phrase {\i v\u8217'hayah im me\u8217'einei ha-eidah} in {\i Bamidbar} connects with {\i v\u8217'ne\u8217'elam davar me\u8217'einei ha-kahal} in {\i Vayikra} \u8212- links the {\i avodah zarah} case to the same {\i kares} standard. For the case of a private individual committing {\i avodah zarah} inadvertently, the {\i pasuk} of {\i v\u8217'im nefesh achat} with its linking {\i vav} is read as tying that individual to the congregational {\i avodah zarah} standard, allowing the derivation to flow downward from the congregation to individuals, or upward from the individual to the congregation, depending on the direction of reasoning required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overarching rule is thus established: whether the sinner is a private individual, a king, a {\i Kohen Gadol}, or the collective \u8212- and whether the sin involves the general corpus of {\i mitzvos} or the specific category of {\i avodah zarah} \u8212- the {\i chatat} obligation is uniformly limited to transgressions for which intentional violation incurs {\i kares}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan\u8217's Torah Achas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the Gemara asks: the Rabbanan use {\i aleha} for {\i tzarat ervah} in the {\i yibum} context \u8212- so where do they derive the principle that the {\i chatat} is limited to {\i kares}-category sins? Their source is a {\i pasuk} that Rav Yehoshua read to his son, from {\i Bamidbar} 15: {\i torah achas yihyeh lachem l\u8217'oseh bishgagah} \u8212- one law shall apply to all who sin inadvertently. What immediately follows in the {\i pasuk} is {\i v\u8217'hanefesh asher ta\u8217'aseh b\u8217'yad ramah\u8230 ? venikratah} \u8212- the soul who acts with a raised hand is cut off. Some of {\i Chazal} read {\i megadef} in that verse as a reference to {\i avodah zarah}, making the sequence: one law for inadvertent sin, followed by {\i kares} for intentional {\i avodah zarah}. The Gemara draws the inference: {\i hukshah kol hatorah kulah l\u8217'avodat kochavim} \u8212- the entire Torah is compared to {\i avodah zarah}. Just as {\i avodah zarah} is a sin for which intentional violation incurs {\i kares} and inadvertent violation requires a {\i chatat}, so too all the sins for which a {\i chatat} is required must share that characteristic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The limitation of {\i torah achas}, however, is that it is written {\i l\u8217'oseh bishgagah} \u8212- for the individual sinner. It therefore establishes the rule for all individual categories: {\i yachid}, {\i nasi}, and {\i mashiach}. It does not, however, address the congregational {\i chatat} cases. For the congregation\u8217's {\i chatat} in general {\i mitzvos}, the Rabbanan still require the {\i me\u8217'einei}/{\i me\u8217'einei} link; and the direction of derivation between the {\i avodah zarah} congregation and the general congregation can run in both directions using the {\i vav} connecting the cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi\u8217's Torah Achas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same Korban Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the Rabbanan use {\i torah achas} to establish the {\i kares} threshold for individuals, what does Rabbi do with {\i torah achas} \u8212- since he uses {\i aleha} for the {\i kares} principle and has no need for {\i torah achas} for that purpose? Rabbi applies {\i torah achas} to a different {\i halacha} taught in a {\i braisa}: since we find in the Torah itself that individuals and a community are treated differently for {\i avodah zarah} \u8212- individuals receive {\i skilah} and their property is not destroyed, while a community that collectively worships {\i avodah zarah} is treated like an {\i ir hanidachat} and sentenced to death by sword with their property destroyed \u8212- one might have thought that this distinction should carry over into their respective {\i korbanot} as well, such that a city that performed {\i avodah zarah bishogeg} would bring a different {\i korban} than an individual who did so. {\i Torah achas yihyeh lachem} comes to teach that in this respect there is no distinction \u8212- one law governs all, the same {\i korban} applies regardless of whether the transgressor was a single person or an entire city acting collectively without a {\i beis din} ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chilkiya Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi Chilkiya of Hagronya pressed this reading: if not for {\i torah achas}, what {\i korban} did you think such a collective would bring that differs from what an individual brings? The question is pointed \u8212- every available animal was already assigned to a specific {\i korban} category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Impossible Combinations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's response is instructive. Going through the possibilities: a bull ({\i par}) is already what the congregation brings for a general {\i chatat}; a female sheep ({\i kisbah}) is what a private individual brings; a male goat ({\i sa\u8217'ir}) is the {\i nasi}\u8217's {\i korban}; a bull for {\i olah} and male goat for {\i chatat} together is the congregational {\i korban} for {\i avodah zarah}; a female goat ({\i se\u8217'irah}) coincides with what an individual brings for {\i avodah zarah}. Every available {\i korban} combination is already occupied. {\i Tosafos} note that the Gemara did not even propose a male sheep or ram \u8212- and the question of why not is itself left unresolved. The upshot is: without {\i torah achas}, one would have been forced to conclude either that the city brings an unprecedented {\i korban} combination \u8212- such as the reverse of the congregational {\i avodah zarah} offering, a {\i se\u8217'ir} for {\i olah} and {\i par} for {\i chatat} \u8212- or that the obligation exists in principle but has no possible fulfillment. {\i Torah achas} resolves this by teaching that the {\i korban} of a city sinning collectively mirrors that of an individual exactly, with no distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Missing Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the derivations of {\i tzarat ervah} and the {\i chatat} threshold fully worked out \u8212- a discussion that has spanned considerable ground \u8212- the Gemara returns to the {\i mishnah} and raises a fresh question. Levi said to Rabbi: {\i mai irya d\u8217'tani chamesh esreh} \u8212- why does the {\i mishnah} list specifically fifteen women? It should list sixteen \u8212- {\i lisni shesh esreh}. Someone is conspicuously absent from the list: {\i imo}, his mother. How could a brother be married to the other brother\u8217's mother? Through a half-brother on the father\u8217's side, whose mother was not the deceased brother\u8217's wife but merely a woman with whom the father had relations. That scenario does not fall under {\i eshet av} \u8212- the father\u8217's wife \u8212- and might therefore seem to be a case worth listing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anusat Aviv Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi\u8217's response was pointed: {\i kimdumeh li she-ein lo moach b\u8217'kadkodo} \u8212- it seems to me you have no brain in your head. The bluntness of the rebuke is notable, particularly since the question is not unreasonable. The substantive answer is this: the case in question \u8212- a mother who is {\i anusat aviv}, a woman the father had sex with outside of marriage \u8212- is a matter of {\i machlokes} between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i Rabbanan}. Rabbi Yehuda holds that a man is forbidden not only to marry his father\u8217's wife but also to marry any woman his father had sexual relations with, even without marriage. According to Rabbi Yehuda, the scenario where a brother could be married to the other brother\u8217's mother through an {\i anusat av} relationship simply never arises \u8212- she would always be forbidden to that son under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling. Since the question is contested, it does not belong in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's list of unambiguous cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An aside on the formulation {\i anusat aviv}: it is worth noting the discomfort in using the language of rape to describe a scenario that could equally be described as {\i mefutah} \u8212- a seduced or consenting woman. The apparent reason the {\i mishnah} prefers {\i anusat aviv} may be precisely to absolve the woman of any transgression, specifically the possible {\i lav} of {\i lo tihyeh kedeshah}. If the father raped her, she bears no legal responsibility. Whether that {\i lav} would even apply to a consenting relationship is itself a major {\i machlokes rishonim} \u8212- the {\i Rambam} considers such a prohibition to apply \u8212- but by framing the case as {\i anusat}, only the father\u8217's sin is in play.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unanimous Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara formalizes the principle underlying Rabbi\u8217's response: the {\i mishnah} in this first {\i perek} deals exclusively with positions that are unanimous \u8212- {\i lo ka-mairi ela be-pirkin}, we are speaking within our chapter. The later chapters of {\i Yevamos} do contain {\i mishnayos} that record debates, such as the dispute between Rabbi Akiva and the {\i Rabbanan} concerning whether women forbidden by a {\i lav} are treated the same as those forbidden by {\i kares} for purposes of {\i yibum}. Rabbi Akiva takes the stringent view that an {\i issur lav} has the same exempting force as an {\i issur kares}, and further that offspring of an {\i issur lav} union are {\i mamzerim} \u8212- a position that is not the accepted ruling. The cases of {\i issur kedushah} \u8212- such as where the deceased brother was married to a {\i mamzeres}, where {\i kiddushin} is technically binding but the marriage was transgressive \u8212- similarly generate their own category: one must perform {\i chalitza} but not {\i yibum}, since the {\i yibum} cannot be halachically executed despite the underlying {\i zika} still creating an obligation. These cases involve genuine dispute and therefore fall outside the scope of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's opening list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai Noted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises one final issue: the {\i mishnah} framed its entire list as women who exempt their {\i tzaros} from {\i yibum} and {\i chalitza}. But this presupposes that the category of {\i tzarat ervah} is halachically operative \u8212- precisely what {\i Beis Shammai} denies. {\i Beis Shammai} holds that {\i tzaros} remain fully subject to {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}; they recognize no category that exempts a {\i tzara} by virtue of her co-wife being an {\i ervah}. If so, the entire framework of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- which presupposes the {\i halacha} of {\i tzarat ervah} \u8212- is not a universally held position. How then can the {\i mishnah} claim to be dealing only with unanimous rulings?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i Beis Shammai bi-makom Beis Hillel einah mishnah} \u8212- where {\i Beis Hillel} rules, {\i Beis Shammai} does not count as an independent position for purposes of establishing the {\i mishnah}\u8217's baseline. The list is a {\i Beis Hillel} position, and {\i Beis Hillel}\u8217's rulings are treated as the operative consensus. With that, the {\i mishnah} is vindicated as a statement of unanimous {\i halacha} \u8212- within the bounds of {\i Beis Hillel} \u8212- and the {\i machlokes} of {\i anusat aviv} explains why mother is absent from the list. The {\i sugya} will continue in the following session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 10\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mother Omission Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up at the bottom of 9a, and the subject is the question that was posed to {\i Rabbi}: why does the {\i mishnah} enumerate fifteen women and not sixteen, having omitted the case of {\i imo} \u8212- one\u8217's mother? The scenario that makes this question possible is as follows. Reuven is the son of Yaakov\u8217's marriage to Devorah, while Yosef is the son of Yaakov and Rachel, who had relations with Yaakov outside of marriage. Since Rachel was never married to Yaakov, she is not technically {\i eshet aviv} \u8212- the father\u8217's wife \u8212- from Reuven\u8217's perspective. Reuven could therefore marry Rachel, and if he did, Yosef would find himself in a {\i yibum} situation with his own mother upon Reuven\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anusat Aviv Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entire force of this question hinges on the case of {\i anusat aviv} \u8212- a woman with whom one\u8217's father had sexual relations but whom he never married. Is she forbidden to the son as well? This is precisely the subject of a dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i Rabbanan}. Rabbi Yehuda forbids {\i anusat aviv} to the son, while the {\i Rabbanan} permit her. Accordingly, whether Reuven could ever have married Rachel in the first place is itself a matter of debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah & Disputes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi}\u8217's response to Levi was sharp: it seems to me you have no brain in your head. The basic explanation offered for why the {\i mishnah} does not list {\i imo} is that the {\i mishnah} confines itself to cases that are not subject to dispute \u8212- {\i plugta lo ka-mairi} \u8212- and the permissibility of marrying {\i anusat aviv} is precisely such a dispute. With that, the {\i gemara} moves on to probe this claim more deeply.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eshet Achiv Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the premise that the {\i mishnah} avoids matters of dispute by pointing to the last line of 9a: {\i v\u8217'harei eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- what about the case of a brother\u8217's wife when the surviving brother was not yet born at the time of the marriage? This case does appear in the {\i mishnah} as one of the fifteen, yet it is, as the {\i gemara} notes, itself the subject of a dispute between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth Order Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the case: Reuven marries Rivka. Reuven then dies. Shimon \u8212- who was alive contemporaneously with Reuven \u8212- performs {\i yibum} with Rivka and marries her. Subsequently, Levi is born. Now Levi has a potential {\i mitzvat yibum} with Rivka if Shimon were to die, because she is {\i eshet achiv} \u8212- Shimon\u8217's wife. But Rivka was also Reuven\u8217's wife, and with respect to Reuven\u8217's marriage, Levi never had a {\i mitzvat yibum} at all, since he was not yet alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister-in-Law Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule of {\i ki yeshvu achim yachdav} \u8212- the brothers must have been alive simultaneously \u8212- means that if a brother was not yet born when the deceased brother was alive, there is no {\i mitzvat yibum} with respect to that first marriage. So when Levi is born, how does he relate to Rivka? Since {\i yibum} had not yet occurred, he relates to her solely as the wife of his dead brother Reuven \u8212- {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- with no {\i mitzvat yibum} and with a full status of {\i ervah}. At that moment she is forbidden to him as a dead brother\u8217's wife. Then, when Shimon subsequently performs {\i yibum}, she remains an {\i ervah} to Levi \u8212- because that status had already crystallized before the {\i yibum} occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees in a specific scenario: if Levi was born {\i after} Shimon had already performed {\i yibum} with Rivka. In that case, when Levi comes into the world, Rivka\u8217's status in his eyes is that of a living brother\u8217's wife \u8212- Shimon\u8217's wife \u8212- with whom he has a {\i mitzvat yibum}. He has no basis to relate to her as Reuven\u8217's wife with respect to whom he was never obligated. In that scenario, Rabbi Shimon holds that Rivka is not an {\i ervah} to Levi, and Levi would actually be obligated to perform {\i yibum} with her upon Shimon\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: the {\i mishnah}\u8217's inclusion of {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} is not problematic even given Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position, because there exists a scenario upon which all agree \u8212- namely, the case where Levi was born {\i before} Shimon performed {\i yibum}. In that scenario, even Rabbi Shimon concedes that Rivka is an {\i ervah} to Levi. Thus the {\i mishnah} can list this case and remain on universally agreed ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites a {\i baraita}: {\i be-nolad u-lesof yibem lo pligi Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- where Levi was born first and Shimon performed {\i yibum} afterward, Rabbi Shimon does not disagree that she is an {\i ervah}. However, Rabbi Oshia is then cited as saying that Rabbi Shimon in fact disagrees even in that scenario, maintaining she is never an {\i ervah}. The {\i gemara} dismisses this: {\i hi tiyufta d\u8217'Rabbi Oshia} \u8212- Rabbi Oshia was contradicted. That dispute will be taken up in a later {\i sugya}. For now, the operative conclusion is that even according to Rabbi Shimon, there is always a scenario of {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} upon which everyone agrees she is an {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bekulan Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fifteen Apply Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now arrive at a striking and intricate passage. Rabbi Yehuda said in the name of Rav, and Rabbi Chiyya taught similarly: {\i be-khulan ani kore bahem ha-asura la-zeh muteret la-zeh ve-asura la-zeh muteret la-zeh} \u8212- in all fifteen of the forbidden relationships, I can apply the rule that she is forbidden to this one and permitted to that one, and forbidden to that one and permitted to this one. And further, the woman who is {\i achoto she-hi yevimto} \u8212- his sister who is also his sister-in-law \u8212- may perform {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand what this means, we must look ahead to the {\i mishnah} in {\i perek arbaah achim}, which deals with four brothers. Suppose Reuven and Shimon are married to Rachel and Leah, who are sisters. Levi and Yehuda are the other two brothers. Now Reuven and Shimon both die. Why can\u8217't Levi simply perform {\i yibum} with Rachel and Yehuda with Leah? The problem is {\i zikah} \u8212- a quasi-spousal bond that attaches before {\i yibum} occurs. Levi has a {\i zikah} to both Rachel and Leah, as does Yehuda. If Levi were to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel, he would be actualizing a marital bond with {\i achot zikukato} \u8212- the sister of his quasi-wife Leah \u8212- which is equivalent to marrying one\u8217's wife\u8217's sister. The same problem applies in reverse. Accordingly, the {\i halachah} is that both women require {\i chalitzah}, and neither can have {\i yibum} performed with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asymmetry Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i mishnah} introduces a crucial qualification. If Rachel happens to be an {\i ervah} to Levi \u8212- say, his aunt, his daughter, or some other forbidden relationship \u8212- but is not an {\i ervah} to Yehuda, then the dynamic changes entirely. Levi has no {\i zikah} to Rachel, since she is his {\i ervah}. That means Leah is not {\i achot zikukato} for Levi \u8212- he has no quasi-spousal bond to her sister. Levi is therefore free to perform {\i yibum} with Leah. Symmetrically, since Levi will perform {\i yibum} with Leah, Yehuda has no {\i zikah} problem with Rachel, and Yehuda may perform {\i yibum} with Rachel. This is the rule of {\i ha-asurah la-zeh muteret la-zeh}: she who is forbidden to one is permitted to the other. This is the {\i bekulan} principle \u8212- the claim that for each of the fifteen forbidden relationships, one can construct a scenario in which that forbidden relationship creates precisely this asymmetry, freeing the other brother to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bekulan Explored\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The elegance of {\i bekulan} is that it must work for all fifteen cases. For each {\i ervah} on the list, one must find a scenario where that {\i ervah} is forbidden to one brother but not the other, and moreover, where she and her co-yevamah are sisters \u8212- since that is the structural requirement for the case to generate the asymmetry described above. The straightforward part of the exercise is establishing that the woman is an {\i ervah} to one brother and not the other. The complicating factor is the additional requirement that the two women must be sisters to one another, and yet the brother who is forbidden to one sister must still be permitted to perform {\i yibum} with the other. Working through all fifteen cases is precisely what the extended Rashi on this passage accomplishes. Rather than exhaustively reproducing that analysis, we will trace through one illustrative example.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bito Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Take the case of {\i bito} \u8212- one\u8217's daughter. Can Rachel be Levi\u8217's daughter? Certainly. Suppose Levi and his wife Devorah have a daughter named Rachel. Can Rachel marry Reuven? Yes \u8212- a man may marry his niece. And is Rachel an {\i ervah} to Yehuda? No \u8212- she is his niece, permitted to him. So this case works at the first level: Rachel is forbidden to her own father Levi but permitted to Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter Complication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, there is a further requirement: Leah must be Rachel\u8217's sister, and Levi must still be permitted to perform {\i yibum} with Leah. This is where the complications arise. If Leah is also a daughter of Devorah, then Devorah\u8217's daughter is his wife\u8217's daughter \u8212- {\i bat ishto} \u8212- which triggers the prohibition of {\i isha u-vitah}, a woman and her daughter. So Levi cannot do {\i yibum} with Leah if Devorah was his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only way to have Rachel and Leah be sisters through Devorah while preserving Levi\u8217's ability to do {\i yibum} with Leah is if Levi was never married to Devorah. If their relationship was non-marital \u8212- {\i onnes} in the broad sense \u8212- then Leah, as Devorah\u8217's daughter, is not Levi\u8217's {\i bat ishto} in the halachically operative sense. The prohibition of {\i isha u-vitah} applies to a woman one was {\i married} to; since Levi was never married to Devorah, Leah is not his wife\u8217's daughter. Thus Rachel, born of Levi and Devorah, is his daughter and therefore his {\i ervah}, while Leah, born of some other father and Devorah, is his niece\u8217's half-sister \u8212- with no forbidden relationship between them \u8212- and he may perform {\i yibum} with her. This scenario works, but only through a non-marital union.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yehuda Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chamoto Onward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda {\i metargem} \u8212- he interpreted the scope of {\i bekulan} \u8212- and limited it: {\i me-chamato va-eilach}, from {\i chamato} \u8212- mother-in-law \u8212- downward. He holds that only the latter categories of the fifteen should be included in the {\i bekulan} claim. The first six variations involving daughter and her descendants should not be included.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onnes vs.\u160 ?Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason, as the previous analysis of {\i bito} illustrated, is that the case of a daughter can only be constructed through a non-marital union \u8212- {\i onnes} \u8212- not through {\i nisuin}, lawful marriage. As demonstrated, the only way to have Rachel and Leah be sisters while Levi can still do {\i yibum} with Leah is if Levi was never married to their mother. Since the case requires extra-marital relations to even be possible, and since the {\i bekulan} principle should speak to ordinary marital scenarios, Rav Yehuda holds that {\i b\u8217'nisuin ka-mairi, b\u8217'onnes lo ka-mairi} \u8212- we are discussing cases arising from marriage, not from non-marital union. Tosafot notes that the very phrase {\i be-khulan ani kore bahem} \u8212- \u8220"in all of them I can apply\u8221" \u8212- implies cases that commonly occur. On that basis, the first six categories are excluded from {\i bekulan} by Rav Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Disagrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye {\i metargem} differently. He holds that {\i af bito me-anusato} \u8212- even the case of a daughter arising from a non-marital union \u8212- should be included in {\i bekulan}. His reasoning is that the scenario is possible regardless: {\i ei ba\u8217'it teima mi-nisuin tehei, ei ba\u8217'it teima me-anusin tehei} \u8212- whether we construct it through marriage or through extra-marital union, the principle can be applied. Since the case is achievable, it belongs in the list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eshet Achiv Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Abaye draws his own exclusion: {\i aval eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- the case of a brother\u8217's wife when the surviving brother was not yet born \u8212- cannot be included in {\i bekulan}. This case, as we noted earlier, requires two additional brothers in the scenario to operate in parallel. Specifically, for the required asymmetry to work, one would need Rachel to be an {\i ervah} to Levi as {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo}, while simultaneously, the parallel case \u8212- Leah being {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} to Yehuda \u8212- would need to hold as well, and each would need to be permitted to the other brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem, Abaye argues, is structural. {\i Ki-van de-l\u8217'Rabbi Shimon hu de-mishkachat lah, le-Rabbanan lo mishkachat lah, be-plugta lo ka-mairi} \u8212- the scenario can only be constructed according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position, not according to the {\i Rabbanan}. According to the {\i Rabbanan}, {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} is simply an {\i ervah} in all cases. So for the scenario to work in parallel \u8212- Rachel forbidden to Levi and permitted to Yehuda, Leah forbidden to Yehuda and permitted to Levi \u8212- one cannot have both operate symmetrically. The reason is age: if Levi is older, one can construct the case where Leah is {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} to Yehuda \u8212- who was born after Yissachar died \u8212- while Levi, being older, was alive contemporaneously with Yissachar and thus has a {\i mitzvat yibum} with Leah. But then one cannot run the parallel scenario with Rachel, because if Levi is older, Rachel cannot be {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} to Levi while simultaneously not being the same to Yehuda who is younger. The asymmetry cannot be made to work in both directions simultaneously according to the {\i Rabbanan}. Since including this case in {\i bekulan} would require relying on Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position \u8212- which is a disputed view \u8212- and the {\i mishnah} should not be read as depending on matters of {\i plugta}, Abaye excludes {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} from {\i bekulan}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Safra Includes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safra\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Safra {\i metargem} still differently. He holds that even {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} can be included in {\i bekulan} \u8212- {\i af eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo meshkachat lah} \u8212- though only {\i ve-aliba de-Rabbi Shimon}, according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. He is willing to include a case that works only within one opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mnemonic Sequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To demonstrate how this works, the {\i gemara} offers a mnemonic: {\i met, noseh, ve-yibem, met, noseh, ve-yibem} \u8212- died, married, and performed {\i yibum}, died, married, and performed {\i yibum}. This sequence of events is the key to constructing the scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Brothers Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario requires six brothers: Reuven, Shimon, Levi, Yehuda, Yissachar, and Zevulun. Reuven and Shimon are married to Rachel and Leah respectively. Levi and Yehuda are the brothers who will ultimately perform {\i yibum}. Yissachar and Zevulun will be born into the picture at critical junctures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth-Death Sequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here is the sequence: Reuven dies. At this point, Yissachar is born. Then Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel. Now, according to Rabbi Shimon, what is Rachel\u8217's status to Yissachar at the moment he comes into the world, {\i before} Levi performs {\i yibum}? She is {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- Reuven\u8217's wife, with whom Yissachar had no {\i mitzvat yibum} since he was not alive. Once Levi then performs {\i yibum}, she remains an {\i ervah} to Yissachar, because that status had already crystallized when he was born.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then Shimon dies. Now Zevulun is born. Levi performs {\i yibum} with Leah. When Zevulun comes into the world, what is Leah\u8217's status? At the moment of his birth, before {\i yibum}, Leah is {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} to him \u8212- she is Shimon\u8217's wife, with whom Zevulun had no {\i mitzvat yibum} since he was not alive. She is therefore an {\i ervah} to Zevulun, just as Rachel is an {\i ervah} to Yissachar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now what is Rachel\u8217's status to Zevulun? By the time Zevulun is born, Levi has already performed {\i yibum} with Rachel. According to Rabbi Shimon, when a brother is born {\i after yibum} has already been completed, he relates to the woman as a living brother\u8217's actual wife \u8212- and therefore has a {\i mitzvat yibum} obligation to her. Rachel is therefore {\i mutar} to Zevulun.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the crucial asymmetry: Rachel is {\i assur} to Yissachar \u8212- because he was born before {\i yibum} occurred \u8212- and {\i mutar} to Zevulun \u8212- because he was born after {\i yibum} occurred. Leah, correspondingly, is {\i assur} to Zevulun for the same structural reason and {\i mutar} to Yissachar. According to the {\i Rabbanan}, this cannot be made to work in parallel, because the younger brother will always find that any {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} is {\i assur} to him by virtue of being younger \u8212- he cannot simultaneously have a case where it is {\i mutar} to him. But according to Rabbi Shimon, the timing of {\i yibum} relative to birth \u8212- not merely birth order \u8212- determines the status, and this allows the asymmetry to be constructed symmetrically on both sides. Thus Rav Safra maintains that {\i bekulan} can include {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo}, according to Rabbi Shimon.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mother Q Resolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now return to the original question with which we began. If {\i bekulan} can include matters of dispute \u8212- specifically the {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} scenario, which works only according to Rabbi Shimon \u8212- then why did {\i Rabbi} exclude {\i imo} from the fifteen? If the {\i mishnah} is willing to deal with {\i plugta}, why not include the case of mother, which is also a {\i plugta} (between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i Rabbanan} regarding {\i anusat aviv})?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ada\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ada Karchina, in the presence of Rav Kahana, said in the name of Rava: {\i le-olam itei de-Rabbi hani klali} \u8212- in truth, {\i Rabbi} can hold that the entire {\i mishnah} applies to the {\i bekulan} scenario, and that {\i bekulan} includes matters of dispute. And nevertheless, {\i imo} could not be listed. Why? Because {\i imo anusat aviv be-chada mishkachat lah, be-tarti lo ka-mishkachat lah} \u8212- the case of mother who is {\i anusat aviv} satisfies only one of the two structural requirements of the {\i bekulan} scenario, but not both simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother Impossibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Recall the requirements. For {\i bekulan} to work, we need: (1) that the woman is an {\i ervah} to one brother but not the other \u8212- {\i ha-asurah la-zeh muteret la-zeh} \u8212- and (2) that the two women involved are sisters \u8212- {\i achoto she-hi yevimto}. The case of mother can satisfy (1) alone or (2) alone, but not both at once. Here is why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rachel to be Levi\u8217's mother in the required scenario, Yaakov and Rachel must have had extramarital relations, producing Levi. Reuven, from Yaakov\u8217's marriage to Leah, then marries Rachel \u8212- since she is neither his mother nor his father\u8217's lawful wife, only {\i anusat aviv}, which is the disputed case. This makes Levi liable for {\i yibum} with his own mother should Reuven die. That is the {\i imo} scenario. Now, for {\i bekulan} to work, Leah must be Rachel\u8217's sister, and Yehuda \u8212- Levi\u8217's brother \u8212- must be permitted to do {\i yibum} with Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sisters Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But here is the fatal complication. Rachel and Leah, in this scenario, are sisters. And Yaakov had extramarital relations with Rachel. But Leah is Rachel\u8217's sister \u8212- she is Levi\u8217's mother\u8217's sister, {\i achot imo}. The prohibition of {\i achot imo} \u8212- one\u8217's maternal aunt \u8212- applies universally, without dispute. Levi cannot do {\i yibum} with Leah. So the moment you have Rachel and Leah be sisters, Leah becomes forbidden to Levi as {\i achot imo}, and the {\i bekulan} structure collapses: you cannot have Levi do {\i yibum} with one and Yehuda do {\i yibum} with the other in the required symmetric fashion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What if Rachel and Leah are not sisters \u8212- if Yaakov had relations with two unrelated women? Then you have (1): Rachel is an {\i ervah} to Levi as his mother, while Leah is not. But you have lost (2): the two women are no longer sisters, and {\i achoto she-hi yevimto} does not apply. {\i Ve-hayu shtei nochriyot anusos} \u8212- if they are unrelated women, then {\i ha-asurah la-zeh u-muteret la-zeh mishkachat lah} \u8212- the forbidden-to-one / permitted-to-the-other condition holds. But {\i achot she-hi yevimto lo mishkachat lah} \u8212- you do not have them as sisters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In short: if the two women are sisters, Levi is forbidden to Leah as {\i achot imo}, and the structure fails. If they are not sisters, the {\i bekulan} scenario does not apply. Therefore, the case of {\i imo} as {\i anusat aviv} cannot be incorporated into {\i bekulan} at all, not because it involves {\i plugta}, but because it structurally cannot satisfy both conditions of {\i bekulan} simultaneously. This is why {\i Rabbi} did not list {\i imo} in the fifteen \u8212- not to avoid disputes, but because {\i imo} simply does not fit the {\i bekulan} framework that the list is designed to support.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Next Mishnah Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Severe Arayot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, Rav Ashi offers a different and \u8212- in the {\i gemara}\u8217's presentation \u8212- strikingly simpler explanation. The next {\i mishnah} states: {\i shesh arayot chamurot me-eilu} \u8212- there are six forbidden relationships more severe than those of the fifteen. The severity consists in the fact that these six women {\i cannot} be married to a brother \u8212- {\i mipnei she-hen nesuot la-acherim} \u8212- because they are ineligible for {\i yibum} altogether, being permanently forbidden to any brother. As a result, {\i tzaroseihen mutarot} \u8212- their co-wives are exempt from {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} as well, since there is never a valid {\i yibum} bond to begin with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother in Next List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Who are these six? The first is {\i imo} \u8212- one\u8217's mother. This is the key to Rav Ashi\u8217's argument. The very fact that the next {\i mishnah} lists {\i imo} as a woman who can {\i never} be married to a brother tells us something critical about the {\i mishnah}\u8217's position. If the {\i mishnah} were following the {\i Rabbanan}, who permit {\i anusat aviv}, then a mother who was never married to the father {\i could} potentially be married to a brother \u8212- since {\i anusat aviv} is {\i mutar} according to them. There would be no basis to place {\i imo} on the list of those who can never be married to a brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mother Severe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i mishnah} does place {\i imo} on that list. This implies that the {\i mishnah} holds like Rabbi Yehuda, who forbids {\i anusat aviv} \u8212- and therefore a mother who was {\i anusat aviv} can never be married to a brother, since she too is forbidden as {\i anusat aviv}. Thus {\i imo} does not appear in the first list of fifteen because she is on the next list: she belongs among those who can never be married to a brother, not among those who \u8212- as the {\i yevamot} scenario requires \u8212- sometimes can be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Rebuke\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the explanation underlying {\i Rabbi}\u8217's rebuke of Levi. The question of why {\i imo} is absent from the fifteen should have been answered by simply reading the next {\i mishnah}. It is apparent from there that the {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Yehuda, and that {\i imo} \u8212- as {\i anusat aviv} \u8212- is categorically forbidden to any brother. Levi\u8217's failure to notice this is what prompted the pointed remark that he appears to lack a brain in his head. And indeed, Rav Ashi observes, whatever complexity was generated by the earlier discussion \u8212- about {\i plugta}, {\i bekulan}, and the structural impossibility of {\i imo} satisfying both conditions \u8212- all of that could have been set aside. The simple answer was always available from the next {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ravina Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Be-di-avad Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina, however, presses Rav Ashi. He argues: {\i Rabbi Yehuda nami mishkachat lah} \u8212- even according to Rabbi Yehuda, one can find a scenario in which a mother is married to a brother, making {\i imo} a potential candidate for the first list of fifteen after all. The first case Ravina proposes is {\i be-di-avad}. Even Rabbi Yehuda, who forbids {\i anusat aviv}, does not classify it as {\i issur karet}. And when a prohibited marriage does not rise to the level of {\i karet}, the {\i kiddushin} is nonetheless binding {\i be-di-avad}. Therefore, if a brother went ahead and married {\i anusat aviv} \u8212- transgressing Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling \u8212- the marriage would be valid after the fact. In such a case, {\i imo} would technically be a brother\u8217's wife, and upon his death there would be a {\i yibum} scenario involving one\u8217's own mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Le-chatchila Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is immediately dismissed. {\i De-i lo katani} \u8212- we do not include cases of transgression in the list. The {\i mishnah} speaks {\i le-chatchila}, of situations that arise through proper, permitted conduct. A scenario that can only occur because someone violated the {\i halachah} is not the kind of case the {\i mishnah} enumerates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adultery Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina then offers a more sophisticated scenario \u8212- one that does not require any transgression on the part of the brother. The case: Yaakov commits adultery with Reuven\u8217's wife, Rachel, and she bears a son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Specifically: Reuven marries Rachel. They have no children together. Yaakov \u8212- Reuven\u8217's father \u8212- sleeps with Rachel, and she conceives a son, Yosef. Yosef is therefore Reuven\u8217's paternal brother (they share the same father, Yaakov). Reuven did nothing wrong; he married Rachel properly. Yaakov\u8217's conduct is another matter entirely, but that is not what concerns us here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum with Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now Reuven dies, leaving Rachel childless. Rachel is Reuven\u8217's wife, and Yosef is Reuven\u8217's brother. Therefore Rachel falls before Yosef for {\i yibum}. But Rachel is also Yosef\u8217's mother. This is a {\i yibum} scenario involving one\u8217's own mother \u8212- and it arose not from any transgression by the brother, but from an adulterous act by the father. The original marriage \u8212- Reuven to Rachel \u8212- was entirely proper. So {\i imo} could in theory be listed in the fifteen even according to Rabbi Yehuda, without requiring any {\i be-di-avad} transgression by the brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heter vs.\u160 ?Isur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana responds to this: {\i be-achvaysa de-hetera ka-mairi, be-achvaysa de-isura lo ka-mairi} \u8212- we speak of relationships that arose through a permitted union, not through a forbidden one. Yosef is Reuven\u8217's brother only because Yaakov committed adultery \u8212- an act of {\i isur}. The brotherly relationship itself is a product of a forbidden union. Furthermore, Yosef would be a {\i mamzer} \u8212- the offspring of an adulterous relationship \u8212- which raises its own complications with respect to {\i yibum}. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's framework presupposes that the family relationships under discussion arose through ordinary, permitted means. A case of {\i imo} that can only be constructed through an adulterous act is not the type of case the {\i mishnah} addresses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Levi\u8217's List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi\u8217's Insertion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite {\i Rabbi}\u8217's dismissal \u8212- and the multiple layers of explanation we have now traversed \u8212- Levi was not content to leave the question unanswered by simply noting that {\i imo} belongs to the next {\i mishnah}. Instead, {\i badka Levi be-matnisin} \u8212- Levi inserted the case of {\i imo} directly into his own version of the {\i mishnah}, expanding the list from fifteen to sixteen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Married Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Levi\u8217's formulation states: {\i imo pe\u8217'amim poteret tzaratah, pe\u8217'amim einah poteret tzaratah} \u8212- one\u8217's mother sometimes exempts her co-wife and sometimes does not. He then distinguishes two scenarios. First: {\i hayetah imo nesuah aviv} \u8212- if his mother was his father\u8217's lawful wife. In that case, the brother can never validly marry her \u8212- it is {\i eshet aviv}, a category of {\i issur karet} \u8212- and any purported marriage would be halachically void. Therefore if a brother contracted such a marriage and died, there is no {\i yibum} bond and no effect on the co-wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anusat Aviv Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: {\i hayetah imo anusat aviv} \u8212- if his mother was never married to his father. A brother could validly marry her according to the {\i Rabbanan} who permit {\i anusat aviv}. If that brother then married her and died, there would be a genuine {\i yibum} obligation \u8212- including all the consequences for the co-wife. This is the case where {\i imo} does belong on the list, at least according to the {\i Rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sixteenth Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Levi therefore concludes that while the {\i Rabbanan} established fifteen cases, {\i yesh lanu le-hosif shesh esrei ke-gon zu} \u8212- there is a sixteenth case possible, of this type. Levi\u8217's underlying point is that rather than accepting the explanation that the {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Yehuda and therefore categorically excludes {\i imo}, he preferred to flag the case of {\i imo as anusat aviv} as a potential addition to the list. The list of fifteen was ancient and authoritative \u8212- the very fact that the {\i Amoraim} debated why it contains fifteen and not sixteen confirms that this number had long been established. Levi\u8217's response was not to dispute that established count but to annotate it: the number is fifteen, the ancient tradition is clear, and yet there exists a sixteenth case of this kind worth noting. It is that annotation which Levi incorporated into his own recension of the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 10b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine Dafim Survey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing at this juncture to take stock of where we are in the tractate. We began on daf 4, and from that point until now, the first genuinely new amoraic statement bearing directly on the {\i mishnah} was the exchange between Levi and Rebbi regarding why the {\i mishnah} enumerates fifteen co-wives rather than sixteen. Everything preceding that was essentially {\i stam} \u8212- anonymous Talmudic discussion \u8212- with no amoraic voices meeting one another head-to-head. The {\i Gemara} worked through an extended opening {\i sugya}: why one might have entertained the {\i hava amina} that {\i yibbum bemakom ervah} could be permitted; the principle of {\i aseh doche lo ta\u8217'aseh} and what might have been derived from {\i eshet ach}; and then the whole question of what source each Tanna uses for the exemption of {\i ervah vetzarat ervah}, and finally the question of fifteen versus sixteen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transition to Standard Format\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this point forward, the {\i sugya} moves into a more recognizable Talmudic structure. Rather than the extended, largely anonymous introductory material that characterized the preceding nine dapim, we now encounter direct amoraic disputes, back-and-forth argumentation, and proofs marshaled for competing positions. The {\i sugyot} in Yevamot remain anything but simple, but they take on a more familiar shape \u8212- statements of {\i amoraim}, countervailing opinions, and attempts at resolution. That is precisely where we now begin, at 11b.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reish Lakish vs.\u160 ?Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Then Remarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion opens with Reish Lakish addressing Rabbi Yochanan, framing his question around the position of Levi. Levi, recall, wished to expand the {\i mishnah}\u8217's list to sixteen by including {\i anusat aviv} \u8212- a woman whom a father violated but did not marry. Levi was apparently willing for the {\i mishnah} to list cases that apply only {\i bedi\u8217'eved}, meaning situations where marriage, though prohibited, would nonetheless be binding after the fact. The {\i Gemara} then asks: if Levi is willing to include {\i bedi\u8217'eved} cases, why not include yet another such case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the case in question, set up the following scenario: Reuven is married to Rachel; Shimon is married to an unrelated woman, Devorah. Reuven dies. Shimon now stands before a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, and similarly with Devorah (Reuven\u8217's wife). He performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel. At that point, the {\i mitzvah} is discharged \u8212- one brother performing one act, whether {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibbum}, releases all the wives and exempts all the remaining brothers. Everyone is free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the question: what happens if Shimon, having performed {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, goes ahead and remarries her? Everyone agrees that this is prohibited. The source is the verse {\i asher lo yivneh es beis achiv} \u8212- \u8220"who did not build his brother\u8217's house\u8221" \u8212- from which we derive that once he has declined to build, {\i keivan shelo vana shuv eino voneh}, he may not reverse course and marry her afterward. This constitutes a {\i lav} \u8212- a prohibition rooted in the {\i yibbum} framework that now governs his relationship with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet suppose he does so anyway. The {\i kiddushin} is binding {\i bedi\u8217'eved} because it is only an {\i issur lav}, not an {\i issur kares}. He has now in effect remarried Rachel. Then Shimon dies. What is Levi\u8217's relationship to Rachel, whom Shimon had remarried? This is precisely the kind of {\i bedi\u8217'eved} situation Levi was willing to include in the {\i mishnah}. Rachel could be considered an {\i ervah} to Levi \u8212- a {\i chalutzah} who was subsequently remarried \u8212- and if Shimon also had a co-wife (for instance, Devorah had also become bound to the same estate through {\i yibbum}), then that co-wife would become a {\i tzarat ervah}. This is a new case of exemption from {\i yibbum} that perhaps should have appeared in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers on behalf of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i lefi she-einah betzoras tzora} \u8212- the reason this case was not included is that it does not generate a {\i tzarat tzara}. Recall that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's list included {\i tzarat tzara} \u8212- a co-wife\u8217's co-wife \u8212- as a further extension of the exemption. For {\i tzarat tzara} to exist, there must be a co-wife who is prohibited only to some brothers and not others, so that when she marries another brother her own co-wife becomes a {\i tzarat ervah} exempt from {\i yibbum} with yet another brother. In the case of the {\i chalutzah} who was remarried, however, Rachel is equally prohibited to all of Reuven\u8217's brothers \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} had fallen equally on all of them \u8212- and therefore no configuration arises in which one brother\u8217's wife becomes a {\i tzarat tzara} for another brother. Since the {\i tzarat tzara} extension does not apply here, the case was excluded from the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: why didn\u8217't Rabbi Yochanan simply respond to Reish Lakish by saying that according to him, Rachel and Devorah are only an {\i issur lav} to the remaining brothers, not an {\i issur kares}, and {\i chayavei lavin} retain their {\i yibbum} and {\i chalitzah} obligations and therefore do not belong in the list of fifteen at all? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i ledivarav ka\u8217'amar lei} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan was arguing within Reish Lakish\u8217's own framework, showing that even granting Reish Lakish\u8217's premise, the case still does not fit the {\i mishnah} for the reason of {\i tzarat tzara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This roundabout framing, however, was simply the {\i Gemara}\u8217's vehicle for arriving at the core dispute. The direct statement of the debate now follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Itmar} \u8212- it was stated. The case: {\i ha-choleitz li-yevamto ve-chazar ve-kiddashah} \u8212- a man performs {\i chalitzah} with his {\i yevamah} and then goes and betroths her again. Reish Lakish holds: {\i hu eino chayav al ha-chalutzah kares} \u8212- he himself, Shimon, who remarries the woman he performed {\i chalitzah} with, is not liable for {\i kares} with respect to her. His ongoing relationship with her has been restructured within the {\i yibbum} framework: the prohibition on his remarrying her is the specific {\i issur lav} of {\i keivan shelo vana shuv eino voneh}. That is a new category of {\i issur}, not a reversion to {\i eshet ach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But for the other brothers \u8212- Levi, Yehuda, and so forth \u8212- {\i ha-achim chayavim al ha-chalutzah kares}: their relationship with Rachel reverts to {\i eshet ach}, which carries {\i kares}. The logic is that Shimon had a {\i yibbum} relationship with Rachel, and so his prohibition is defined within that {\i yibbum} world. Levi, however, never actualized any {\i yibbum} relationship with Rachel. The potential was there, but it was not actualized, and so the general prohibition of {\i eshet ach} with its {\i kares} consequence remains in full force for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the same token, Shimon\u8217's relationship with Devorah \u8212- the other wife \u8212- also reverts to {\i eshet ach} with {\i kares}, because he never had a {\i yibbum} relationship with Devorah at all. And both he and the other brothers are equally liable for {\i kares} with respect to the {\i tzarah}. The only exception carved out from full {\i kares} status is Shimon\u8217's specific relationship with Rachel, to whom he performed {\i chalitzah} \u8212- that and only that relationship is redefined in {\i yibbum} terms and carries merely an {\i issur lav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan rejects this framework entirely. {\i Mi ika midi de-me\u8217'ikara ei ba\u8217'ei hai chalitz ve\u8217'ei ba\u8217'ei hai chalitz} \u8212- is there anything here where, from the outset, only one specific party was designated? Before anyone did anything, Shimon could have performed {\i chalitzah} with Rachel or with Devorah; Levi equally could have performed {\i chalitzah} with Rachel or with Devorah. Any combination was possible. All the brothers stood equally before the {\i mitzvah}; all the wives stood equally subject to it. To say that one act \u8212- Shimon performing {\i chalitzah} with Rachel \u8212- changes the status of Rachel vis-\u224 ?-vis Shimon but leaves everyone else in the pre-existing {\i eshet ach} category is, in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view, incoherent. They all had an equal relationship to this {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, Rabbi Yochanan presses the point: {\i ve-hashta de-kayala bekarei} \u8212- now that she has performed the act of {\i chalitzah}, is it reasonable that all these relationships which could equally have been the ones to undergo {\i chalitzah} should suddenly revert to {\i issur kares}? That makes no sense given that any of them could have been the participants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a striking conceptual formulation: {\i ihu shlichusa de-achin ka-avid, ihi shlichusa de-tzarah ka-avda} \u8212- he, Shimon, performs {\i chalitzah} as the agent of all the brothers; she, Rachel, receives the {\i chalitzah} as the agent of all the co-wives. Shimon is, in effect, a {\i shliach tzibur} \u8212- representing Levi, Yehuda, and all the rest. Rachel is representing Devorah and any other co-wife in the same estate. The implication is radical: the {\i chalitzah} performed by Shimon with Rachel is considered as though all the brothers performed {\i chalitzah} with all the wives. Therefore, everyone now stands in the same relationship \u8212- not {\i eshet ach} with {\i kares}, but a new {\i issur} defined within the {\i yibbum} world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yochanan, nobody \u8212- not Shimon, not Levi, not Yehuda, with respect to Rachel or Devorah \u8212- is liable for {\i kares}. They all have the same status: a new {\i issur lav} that emerged from the {\i chalitzah} act, applicable universally because the act was performed representatively for all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agency & Representation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism articulated by Rabbi Yochanan deserves emphasis because it is one of the conceptually foundational ideas of tractate Yevamot. The claim is not merely that the brothers and wives had a theoretical possibility of participating \u8212- it is that the one who actually participates does so as a representative of all the others. This is what {\i shlichut} means in this context: Shimon\u8217's {\i chalitzah} is treated as legally equivalent to every brother performing {\i chalitzah}, and Rachel\u8217's receipt of it is treated as every co-wife\u8217's receipt. The act is, in a sense, collective even when physically performed by individuals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is what differentiates Rabbi Yochanan from Reish Lakish at the deepest level. Reish Lakish sees the act as individual: Shimon and Rachel entered a particular legal relationship, and everything outside that bilateral relationship remains governed by the prior law of {\i eshet ach}. Rabbi Yochanan sees the act as representative: through the mechanism of agency, all parties are equally drawn into the new legal reality created by that single act of {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the two positions established, the {\i Gemara} turns to evidence. Rabbi Yochanan challenges Reish Lakish from a {\i baraita}: {\i ha-choleitz li-yevamto ve-chazar ve-kiddashah u-meis, tzrichah chalitzah min ha-achin} \u8212- if a man performs {\i chalitzah} with his {\i yevamah}, then remarries her, and then dies, the remaining brothers are required to perform {\i chalitzah} with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yochanan, this is entirely consistent: their status is an {\i issur lav}, not an {\i issur kares}, and women in an {\i issur lav} relationship with the brothers still require {\i chalitzah} \u8212- they cannot simply walk free, but neither can {\i yibbum} be performed. {\i Bishlama lidi, amina chayavei lavin ninhu, hainu de-tzrichah chalitzah min ha-achin} \u8212- this is exactly what my position predicts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But according to Reish Lakish, the other brothers stand in an {\i issur kares} relationship with this woman. Women who are {\i arayot} \u8212- full {\i ervah} \u8212- are entirely exempt from both {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibbum}. If her status is {\i issur kares} for the brothers, why does the {\i baraita} require {\i chalitzah} at all? This seems to be a clear proof for Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lakish Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish counters: {\i u-letamach, eima seifa} \u8212- you cited only the first half of the {\i baraita}; read the second half. {\i Amad echad min ha-achin ve-kiddshah, ein lah alav klum} \u8212- if one of the brothers went ahead and betrothed her, the {\i kiddushin} is entirely null and void, as though nothing happened. But if their status were only an {\i issur lav}, {\i kiddushin} would be binding {\i bedi\u8217'eved}, as it is in all cases of {\i chayavei lavin}. The fact that the {\i kiddushin} takes no hold whatsoever \u8212- {\i ein lah alav klum} \u8212- indicates {\i issur kares}, where {\i kiddushin} never takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i baraita} is internally contradictory. Its first clause requires {\i chalitzah}, implying {\i issur lav}. Its second clause declares {\i kiddushin} null, implying {\i issur kares}. Neither Rabbi Yochanan nor Reish Lakish can claim the {\i baraita} as consistent support; it speaks with two irreconcilable voices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proposes an initial resolution attributed to Rav Sheshet: {\i seifa asya ke-Rabbi Akiva de-amar ein kiddushin tofsin be-chayavei lavin} \u8212- the second clause follows Rabbi Akiva, who holds that {\i chayavei lavin} are treated like {\i chayavei kares} and {\i kiddushin} does not take effect with them either. The first clause follows the normative view that {\i lav} differs from {\i kares} and {\i chalitzah} is still required. The {\i Gemara} immediately objects: if the second clause represents only Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view, it should have been introduced with explicit attribution \u8212- {\i le-divrei Rabbi Akiva}. Since it was not, this resolution is unsatisfying. {\i Kashya.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now records two amoraic positions on how to reconcile the {\i baraita}: {\i Rav Ashi savar lah ke-Reish Lakish u-metaretz lah ke-Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- Rav Ashi holds like Reish Lakish and reconciles the {\i baraita} through Rabbi Shimon\u8217's principle. {\i Ravina savar lah ke-Rabbi Yochanan u-metaretz ke-rabbanan} \u8212- Ravina holds like Rabbi Yochanan and reconciles the {\i baraita} according to the rabbis who disagree with Rabbi Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi\u8217's resolution: The {\i baraita} addresses {\i achin ha-noladim} \u8212- brothers who were born after the relevant events. Recall Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling that a brother born after the fact relates to a woman only in the status she occupied when he entered the world, without the prior history affecting him. Under this principle, the two halves of the {\i baraita} refer to two different groups of brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate: suppose Shimon performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel and then remarries her \u8212- stage one. At that point, say, Yehuda had already been born, while Levi is born only after Shimon has remarried Rachel. For Levi, who enters the world when Rachel is already Shimon\u8217's wife (a binding marriage, even if {\i bedi\u8217'eved}), all the prior history \u8212- the {\i chalitzah}, the prohibition \u8212- is irrelevant. He finds Rachel as his brother Shimon\u8217's wife, governed by the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}. When Shimon subsequently dies, Levi can and must perform either {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibbum} \u8212- that is the first clause of the {\i baraita}: {\i tzrichah chalitzah min ha-achin}, referring to newly born brothers like Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Yehuda, who was alive throughout the entire sequence, Rachel is an {\i issur kares} \u8212- she was always in the {\i yibbum} orbit and he was always present to it, so Reish Lakish\u8217's principle applies fully. If Yehuda goes ahead and performs {\i kiddushin} with Rachel, {\i ein lah alav klum} \u8212- that is the second clause of the {\i baraita}, referring to brothers who were already present ({\i ha-yeludim}) when all this transpired. Thus the two halves of the {\i baraita} are perfectly consistent according to Reish Lakish, parsed through Rabbi Shimon\u8217's distinction of {\i noladim} versus {\i yeludim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina, holding like Rabbi Yochanan and reconciling according to the {\i rabbanan} who disagree with Rabbi Shimon, reaches the opposite parsing. For Rabbi Yochanan, all brothers who were present when the {\i chalitzah} took place have the status of {\i issur lav} \u8212- they participated representatively in the {\i chalitzah} process, and so they still require {\i chalitzah} but cannot perform {\i yibbum}. These are the brothers referred to in the first clause: {\i tzrichah chalitzah min ha-achin} \u8212- namely, the brothers who were there, like Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what of a brother born afterward \u8212- Yehuda, who enters the world after the {\i chalitzah} and the subsequent remarriage, when Rachel is already Shimon\u8217's wife? For Yehuda, Rachel is {\i eshet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} \u8212- the wife of a brother who predated him. According to the {\i rabbanan}, unlike Rabbi Shimon, the temporal sequence of birth does not retroactively adjust the prohibition. Yehuda never had a {\i mitzvat yibbum} at all with respect to Rachel. Without any {\i yibbum} relationship having ever attached to him, there is no {\i yibbum}-framework {\i issur lav} \u8212- Rachel simply remains an {\i eshet ach} and carries {\i issur kares} for him. Therefore, if Yehuda attempts {\i kiddushin}, {\i ein lah alav klum} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} does not take hold because it is {\i issur kares}. That is the second clause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two resolutions thus mirror each other structurally while reflecting the underlying dispute: for Rav Ashi following Reish Lakish, it is the {\i previously born} brothers who are {\i issur kares} and the {\i newly born} who still have {\i chalitzah} requirements; for Ravina following Rabbi Yochanan, it is precisely the reverse \u8212- the previously present brothers are {\i issur lav} with {\i chalitzah} obligations, while the newly born brother, who never had a {\i yibbum} connection, is {\i issur kares}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibbum Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibbum Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a parallel dispute involving {\i yibbum} rather than {\i chalitzah}. The case: Reuven is married to Rachel and Devorah. He dies. Levi performs {\i yibbum} with Rachel \u8212- that is, he has marital relations with her in fulfillment of the {\i mitzvah}. Subsequently, Yehuda comes and has relations with Devorah. What is Devorah\u8217's status with respect to Yehuda?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate of Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish applied to {\i chalitzah}; one can now pose the same question for {\i yibbum}. Once one brother has performed {\i yibbum} with one wife, what is every other brother\u8217's relationship to the remaining co-wife? Reish Lakish\u8217's logic would say: Levi actualized a {\i yibbum} relationship with Rachel specifically, and everything else reverts to {\i eshet ach} with {\i kares}. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's logic of representation would say: everyone participated representatively, so all relationships are now reconfigured within the {\i yibbum} framework, and no one reverts to full {\i eshet ach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha vs.\u160 ?Ravina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Peligi bah Rav Acha ve-Ravina} \u8212- Rav Acha and Ravina disagree. {\i Chad amar be-kares, ve-chad amar be-asei} \u8212- one holds the prohibition is {\i issur kares}, the other holds it is an {\i issur asei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why {\i issur asei} rather than {\i issur lav} as in the {\i chalitzah} case? The source differs. In the {\i chalitzah} case, the {\i lav} came from {\i asher lo yivneh es beis achiv} \u8212- the negative formulation of not building. In the {\i yibbum} case, the relevant verse yields a positive commandment: {\i bayit echad hu voneh ve-eino voneh shenei batim} \u8212- he builds one household, not two. Levi performed {\i yibbum} with Rachel; the verse teaches that he may not perform a second {\i yibbum} with Devorah. This is understood as an {\i issur asei}. By Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's representational logic, the same {\i issur asei} applies equally to all brothers and all wives, since all participated representatively in the {\i yibbum} act. Hence one who holds like Rabbi Yochanan says {\i be-asei}; one who holds like Reish Lakish says {\i be-kares}, since for all parties other than the specific Levi-Rachel {\i yibbum} pair, the relationship reverts to {\i eshet ach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We know from the earlier discussion that Ravina holds like Rabbi Yochanan; it follows that Ravina is the one who says {\i be-asei}, and Rav Acha takes the Reish Lakish position of {\i be-kares}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sotah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav on Sotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having completed the central {\i sugya} of the Reish Lakish\u8211-Rabbi Yochanan dispute and its application, the {\i Gemara} pivots to a different question: are there women who perhaps should have been added to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's list of fifteen but were not? One such case is now introduced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav: tzarat sotah asurah} \u8212- the co-wife of a {\i sotah} is prohibited from {\i yibbum}. The reasoning: {\i tumah ketiv bah} \u8212- the {\i Torah} describes the {\i sotah} who committed adultery using the language of {\i tumah}. The verse states {\i ve-nistara ve-hi nitma\u8217'ah}, and separately, {\i ve-nistara ve-hi lo nitma\u8217'ah}. When she has in fact committed adultery, the act is described as {\i tumah}. Since the {\i issur arayot} is likewise described in the {\i Torah} in terms of {\i tumah} \u8212- {\i lo titam\u8217'u be-chol eleh} \u8212- a {\i sotah} who definitively committed adultery is treated as though she carries the status of {\i arayot}. There is therefore no {\i mitzvat yibbum} with respect to her, and by extension, no {\i mitzvat yibbum} with respect to her {\i tzarah} either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note the irony: the status of {\i tumah} that makes her forbidden was, in the first instance, her prohibition to her own husband, Reuven. It was not a prohibition extending to anyone else. Yet when Reuven dies and Shimon faces the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}, we say that because she is termed {\i temeiah} \u8212- carrying the character of {\i arayot} \u8212- Shimon is exempt from {\i yibbum} entirely. The {\i Gemara} itself does not make this observation, but it sharpens what is at stake in Rav\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chisda Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda challenges this from a {\i baraita}: {\i Rav Shimon omer, biah o chalitzatah me-achiv ha-rishon poteres tzaratah} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon rules that {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} performed with her by the first husband\u8217's brother exempts her co-wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case behind this citation: a woman\u8217's husband travels overseas and is reported dead by a single witness. Relying on that testimony, she remarries. Then the first husband returns alive. She is now in the anomalous position of being bound to two men, and the law imposes severe consequences \u8212- {\i asurah la-zeh ula-zeh, tetze mi-zeh u-mi-zeh}. Yet the {\i baraita} indicates that if the first husband subsequently dies before these matters are resolved, the levir still performs {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibbum} \u8212- and that act exempts her co-wife. This implies that even a woman in the quasi-{\i sotah} category, having effectively cohabited with two men, still generates {\i yibbum} obligations. How, then, can Rav say a {\i sotah}\u8217's co-wife is entirely exempt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is immediate: {\i amar lach Rav, amina lach ana sotah de-oraita} \u8212- Rav was speaking of a {\i sotah de-oraita}, a woman who willfully and definitively committed adultery, a true biblical {\i sotah}. The case in the {\i baraita} is a {\i sotah de-rabbanan} \u8212- the woman acted entirely in good faith, relying on valid testimony; her second marriage was entered unwittingly. She is not a {\i sotah} in the biblical sense at all. The various stringencies imposed upon her \u8212- that she cannot return to either husband \u8212- are rabbinic measures. At the biblical level she is an {\i ones}, her act was entirely unwitting, and Rav\u8217's ruling about true {\i sotah} does not apply to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashi\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then raises a challenge from another {\i baraita}: {\i nichnesah immo le-seter ve-shafah immo ke-dei tumah, asurah le-beitah ve-asurah le-echol bi-trumah} \u8212- a woman who secluded herself with the suspected man for a duration sufficient for intercourse to have occurred is forbidden to resume marital relations with her husband and, if she is married to a {\i kohen}, is forbidden to eat {\i terumah}. {\i Im meis \u8212- choletzet ve-lo mityabemet} \u8212- if the husband then dies, the {\i yevamah} performs {\i chalitzah} but not {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} thus shows that even a woman suspected of adultery \u8212- not one who definitively committed it \u8212- still requires {\i chalitzah}. This seems to undercut Rav\u8217's position: if even the {\i sotah safek} requires {\i chalitzah}, how can Rav hold that a {\i sotah vaday} is treated as {\i arayot} with no {\i chalitzah} requirement?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definite vs.\u160 ?Suspected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, again, is Rav\u8217's own distinction: {\i amina lach ana sotah vaday} \u8212- I was speaking of a woman who definitely committed adultery. A {\i sotah safek} is precisely that \u8212- uncertain. Perhaps she is entirely innocent. Because of that doubt, the law cannot treat her as a full {\i ervah}, and so {\i chalitzah} remains required. But a {\i sotah vaday}, whose adultery is established, is described in the {\i Torah} as {\i temeiah}, and that {\i tumah} language assimilates her to the category of {\i arayot}, where there is no {\i yibbum} and no {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then probes the source of the {\i lav} that applies to a {\i sotah vaday}. The word {\i tumah} appears in the {\i sotah} passage ({\i ve-hi nitma\u8217'ah}), which establishes her status. But what is the source of the prohibition itself \u8212- the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- that makes it forbidden for her husband to continue with her? The {\i Gemara} traces this to the verse in {\i Devarim} dealing with {\i machzir gerushato}: {\i lo yuchal ba\u8217'alah ha-rishon asher shilcheha lashuv lekachtah acharei asher hutama\u8217'ah}. The word {\i hutama\u8217'ah} at the end of that verse, which would seem redundant given that the entire passage describes a woman who legitimately remarried, is interpreted as extending the prohibition to a {\i sotah} who committed adultery \u8212- teaching that a woman declared {\i temeiah} may not return to her husband. And since the verse imposes a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, this establishes the {\i lav} for {\i sotah vaday}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi ben Kippur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi ben Kipar, citing Rabbi Eliezer, reads the verse entirely differently. He holds that {\i ha-machzir gerushato min ha-nisuin asurah, min ha-eirusin muteret} \u8212- a man who divorced his wife and she subsequently married another man may not remarry her if she had full {\i nisuin} with the second husband, but if she had only {\i eirusin} \u8212- betrothal without cohabitation \u8212- she is permitted to return to him. The word {\i hutama\u8217'ah} in the verse is therefore not extending the prohibition to {\i sotah} cases; it is limiting the prohibition of {\i machzir gerushato} to cases where the second marriage involved {\i nisuin}, meaning there was an opportunity for actual relations, so that the {\i tumah} language is meaningful. Without {\i nisuin}, there was no realistic {\i tumah} and the {\i lav} does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yosi ben Kipar, then, the verse is entirely occupied with {\i machzir gerushato} and does not extend to {\i sotah} at all. As a result, {\i lav be-sotah leisa lei} \u8212- he has no {\i lav} for the {\i sotah} who committed adultery. The {\i Gemara} notes the striking implication: {\i ve-afilu zenai nami} \u8212- even a woman who definitively committed adultery does not carry a biblical {\i lav} prohibiting her from returning to her husband, according to Rabbi Yosi ben Kipar. The {\i lav} of {\i machzir gerushato} is generated by {\i havayah ve-ishus} \u8212- the legitimate marriage and marital bond with a second man, formalized through {\i nisuin}. A case of adultery, however severe, does not fulfill that description.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony is unmistakable, and the {\i Gemara} plainly intends it to register: the very prohibition that would seem most obviously to apply in the case of adultery in fact, according to this view, applies only in the fully legitimate case \u8212- where a woman was properly divorced, properly remarried, and the second marriage was consummated. The worse violation carries no {\i lav}; the entirely proper sequence does. This is where the {\i sugya} rests for now, and the discussion of {\i machzir gerushato} as a potential addition to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's list will continue in the {\i daf} that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 14\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Uniformity vs.\u160 ?Pluralism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Titgodedu Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion picks up from a broader question that was raised by Rav Yochanan and Reish Lakish concerning consistency of practice \u8212- specifically, the concern of {\i lo titgodedu agudot agudot}, the prohibition against creating factions through divergent communal practice. This prohibition has a particular relevance to situations where {\i halacha} itself seems to produce differing outcomes for different people. The Gemara\u8217's inquiry, however, has evolved from the narrow question of {\i lo titgodedu} into something considerably wider: whether we demand uniformity of {\i pesak} across the community, or whether we accommodate \u8212- and perhaps even welcome \u8212- a pluralism of halachic practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's working answer has been that {\i lo titgodedu} applies only when different people within the same geographic location are acting inconsistently. If they belong to distinct communities in distinct places, that is permitted. The irony, noted previously, is that geographic separation may actually {\i reinforce} factionalism by entrenching the identity of each locale around its particular practice \u8212- yet at least within any single place, there is coherence. Nowadays, when communities are no longer defined by geography but rather by affiliation and tradition, the concept of a \u8220"virtual community\u8221" has taken on increasing relevance, though the Gemara\u8217's framework remains primarily geographic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Radical Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava went considerably further, asserting that even within a single geographic location, differences in {\i pesak} do not constitute a violation of {\i lo titgodedu}. In his view, the prohibition applies only when a {\i beit din} has failed to arrive at a ruling and the result is a kind of halachic anarchy \u8212- everyone doing as they please without any principled basis. But where there are genuinely different {\i psakim} held by different authorities, and different people follow different {\i psakim}, that does not violate {\i lo titgodedu}. This is, admittedly, a fairly radical position, and in practice something between Abaye and Rava \u8212- allowing for community-based distinctions even without strict geographic separation \u8212- seems more workable. Nevertheless, the Gemara has effectively reduced {\i lo titgodedu} to a quite narrow concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remaining question \u8212- the one that drives the discussion forward \u8212- is what actually happened {\i lema\u8217'aseh} with Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel. Did Beit Shammai ultimately follow Beit Hillel\u8217's {\i psak}, or did they maintain their own? And beyond the question of {\i lo titgodedu}, the issue is sharpened by the challenge posed by the Mishna itself: the Mishna states that despite their conflicting positions, Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel intermarried with one another. This is a startling statement. If a woman from Beit Hillel is, according to Beit Shammai\u8217's {\i psak}, forbidden \u8212- or if her children would be problematic \u8212- how could they intermarry freely? If one answers that Beit Shammai simply adopted Beit Hillel\u8217's practice, then the Mishna\u8217's statement is almost trivially true and loses its force as a {\i chiddush}. The tension between the straightforward meaning of the Mishna and the halachic implications of genuine pluralism of practice is precisely what the Gemara must now resolve.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geographic Locations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a {\i ta shema} from a source dealing with the place of Rabbi Eliezer. In the place of Rabbi Eliezer, they would actually cut down trees on Shabbat in order to make charcoal, in order to forge iron implements, in order to fashion a knife for the {\i milah} \u8212- because Rabbi Eliezer held that not only the act of {\i milah} itself overrides Shabbat on the eighth day, but so do all of its preparatory implements, {\i machshirei milah}. Tosafot explains that in the place of Rabbi Eliezer, they would intentionally perform all these preparatory acts on Shabbat itself, out of a desire to demonstrate the supreme importance of {\i bris milah} \u8212- its capacity to override even the full range of Shabbat restrictions. This, of course, is not how we rule; the accepted {\i pesak} is that only the act of {\i milah} itself overrides Shabbat, not its preparations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi HaGelili\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, in the place of Rabbi Yosi HaGelili, they would eat chicken cooked in milk. Rabbi Yosi HaGelili maintained that the prohibition of {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} applies only to the flesh of an animal in its mother\u8217's milk, and since fowl has no mother\u8217's milk, the prohibition does not apply even rabbinically. The accepted ruling disagrees \u8212- we hold that poultry with milk is rabbinically forbidden \u8212- but Rabbi Yosi HaGelili maintained his position, and the people of his community maintained it with him. Here, then, is a clear instance of at least two distinct geographic locations following different halachic positions, and indeed the connection between a locale and its rabbi\u8217's {\i psak} was not random \u8212- it was a {\i mesorah}, a tradition following the ruling of the {\i rav} of that place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer vs Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes a further distinction: in the place of Rabbi Eliezer, they would cut trees on Shabbat for the purpose of {\i milah} preparations, but in the place of Rabbi Akiva they would not. The reason is a general principle articulated by Rabbi Akiva: any {\i melachah} that could have been performed before Shabbat does not override Shabbat. The preparations could have been done on {\i erev Shabbos}, and therefore they cannot be done on Shabbat itself. This is in fact the ruling we follow. The upshot is that even in this area \u8212- where Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is the normative {\i psak} \u8212- in the specific locale of Rabbi Eliezer, they followed Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Gravity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks why this case is even posed as a challenge. The answer was already given: {\i mikomos mikomos shani} \u8212- different locations can follow different {\i psakim}, and that is not a problem. The Gemara responds that one might have thought that because of the particular gravity of Shabbat, even geographically distinct communities should maintain a uniform practice. The concern is that {\i mishum chumrah d\u8217'Shabbos k\u8217'chad makom dami} \u8212- the weight of Shabbat is so great that perhaps all communities should be treated as a single place for purposes of Shabbat practice. The Gemara rejects this: even in matters as weighty as Shabbat, different communities in different places may follow different practices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One area where the Gemara does insist on absolute uniformity is the calendar \u8212- when {\i Rosh Chodesh} falls and consequently when the holidays are observed. The calendar must be shared. Divergence on when Shabbat or Yom Tov is observed is precisely the kind of break that sunders a community into fundamentally different peoples, as was the case with the sect of Jubilees and the {\i Tzadukim}. Indeed, the dispute between the {\i Geonim} of Bavel and the {\i Geonim} of Eretz Yisrael over calendar reckoning was ultimately resolved in favor of Bavel, and that resolution effectively marked the transfer of halachic authority to the Babylonian academies. Beyond calendar, however, the Gemara is prepared to allow divergent practice even in the most serious areas of Shabbat law.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Abba Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lamp at Levi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings another {\i ta shema}. When Rabbi Abba would come to the place of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, he would carry an extinguished lamp \u8212- that is, a lamp that had been burning on Shabbat and was then extinguished. The question is whether such a lamp is {\i muktzeh} or not. In the place of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, Rabbi Abba would carry it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lamp at Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But when Rabbi Abba came to the place of Rabbi Yochanan, he would not carry the lamp. Rabbi Yochanan did not permit carrying such objects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deference Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks how this is to be understood. Different places may follow different {\i psakim} \u8212- that much has been established \u8212- but Rabbi Abba is a single individual. How could one person practice differently in two different places? The answer is that Rabbi Abba personally ruled like Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, the lenient position. But when he was in the place of Rabbi Yochanan, he refrained from carrying the lamp out of deference \u8212- {\i mishum kvod de-Rabbi Yochanan}. He was not changing his {\i psak}; he simply did not act on it in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's presence, as a gesture of respect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: but what about his attendant? His attendant had seen Rabbi Abba carry the lamp in one place, and might assume the practice applies in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's place as well. The answer: Rabbi Abba informed his attendant ahead of time. He would tell him: when we are in the place of Rabbi Yochanan, be careful not to carry the lamp.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This episode \u8212- the fact that Rabbi Abba took care to inform his attendant of the change in practice \u8212- actually anticipates the Gemara\u8217's resolution of the broader Beit Shammai/Beit Hillel question, as will become clear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Shammai Questio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the central question, the Gemara cites the statement of the Mishna: {\i af al pi she\u8217'elu ossrin ve\u8217'elu mattirin} \u8212- even though these forbid and these permit \u8212- {\i lo nimne\u8217'u Beit Shammai milisa nashim miBeit Hillel vilo Beit Hillel miBeit Shammai} \u8212- Beit Shammai did not refrain from marrying women of Beit Hillel, nor did Beit Hillel from women of Beit Shammai.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now revisits the two possible ways of reading this Mishna. If one says that Beit Shammai did not keep to their own practices \u8212- that is, that they followed Beit Hillel \u8212- then of course there would be no impediment to intermarriage: {\i mishum hachi lo nimne\u8217'u}, that is precisely why they could intermarry. But if one says that Beit Shammai maintained their own practices, then the Mishna\u8217's statement becomes deeply puzzling: {\i amai lo nimne\u8217'u} \u8212- how could they not hold themselves back from intermarrying?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzarot Practices (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asymmetric Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an asymmetry in the concern here that requires examination. As the Gemara will work out, the concern is not equal in both directions. Beit Shammai would have less reason to fear marrying into Beit Hillel, while Beit Hillel would have very strong reason to fear marrying into Beit Shammai. Understanding why requires working through the specifics of the {\i tzara} dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Shammai Questio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lav vs.\u160 ?Kares\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara spells out why Beit Shammai would have relatively little to fear in marrying into Beit Hillel. According to Beit Hillel\u8217's position, the {\i tzara} is exempt from {\i yibbum} and goes free. According to Beit Shammai, she is obligated in either {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah}. If she proceeds to marry another man without {\i chalitzah}, that is a violation of a {\i lav} from Beit Shammai\u8217's perspective \u8212- but the child of such a union is not a {\i mamzer}, because a child born of a violation of a {\i lav} alone is not rendered a {\i mamzer}. So the worst outcome for Beit Shammai when it comes to children from Beit Hillel women is that there has been a {\i lav} violation \u8212- significant, but not producing {\i mamzeirus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzerim Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The situation is entirely different for Beit Hillel in relation to Beit Shammai. According to Beit Hillel, the {\i tzara} is completely exempt from {\i yibbum} \u8212- she is the {\i tzaras ervah}, and the prohibition of {\i eishet ach} applies. If Beit Shammai performs {\i yibbum} with the {\i tzara} \u8212- as they maintain is not only permitted but obligatory \u8212- then from Beit Hillel\u8217's perspective, this is an act of {\i issur kares}. The children of such a union are {\i mamzerim}. Beit Hillel could not countenance marrying into families where they believed {\i mamzeirim} had been produced. The asymmetry is therefore stark: Beit Shammai has relatively little to lose, while Beit Hillel has everything to lose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore presses: one might answer that Beit Hillel holds that {\i mamzeirus} does not result from {\i chayvei kerisot}, only from {\i chayvei misos}. But this is foreclosed by Rabbi Elazar\u8217's ruling, which we will examine shortly, that both houses agree there is no {\i mamzer} except from one whose prohibition carries a penalty of {\i kares}. Since Beit Hillel holds the {\i yibbum} done by Beit Shammai on the {\i tzara} is an {\i issur kares}, the child is indeed a {\i mamzer} according to Beit Hillel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Informing Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Kept Stance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes that Beit Shammai did in fact maintain their own practices \u8212- {\i lo le\u8217'olam assu} is rejected; {\i le\u8217'olam assu} is affirmed. They did not abandon their {\i pesak}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Informing Solution (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demodiim Lehu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How then did they intermarry? The Gemara answers: {\i demodei\u8217'ei lehu ufarshi} \u8212- they were understanding and sympathetic enough to one another, such that they would proactively inform each other of relevant family status. If someone from Beit Hillel sought to marry into a Beit Shammai family, Beit Shammai would say: look, you should know that this woman\u8217's status involves a {\i tzara} situation \u8212- here is the background. They shared the information because they genuinely respected each other\u8217's halachic position and were concerned for one another\u8217's well-being. This did not mean there was no concern \u8212- of course there was \u8212- but the {\i ahava} and {\i rei\u8217'ut}, the love and friendship between the houses, motivated proactive disclosure. The concern about {\i mamzeirus} would then be neutralized not by ignoring it but by supplying the information needed to navigate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dynamic is not unfamiliar. Sometimes a host will say, when inviting guests of different halachic standards: here is exactly what I use, here is my practice, so you can make an informed decision. The {\i chiddush} here is that Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel operated with that degree of mutual transparency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumat Tahara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers a corroborating proof from the {\i seipha} of the same source: {\i kol ha-teharos ve-kol ha-temeios she-hayu eileh metaharim ve-eileh metameim lo nimnu osim teharos elu al gabei elu} \u8212- all the matters of purity and impurity, where one house declared pure and the other declared impure, they did not refrain from using each other\u8217's vessels for {\i taharos}. The Gemara asks: how was this possible? If one holds the other\u8217's vessel is {\i tamei}, how can the {\i machmir} borrow from the {\i meikel}? What the {\i meikel} considers {\i tahor}, the {\i machmir} considers {\i tamei}. The only way to explain this is that they informed each other \u8212- {\i ela lav demodei lehu}. They would disclose the status of their vessels so the other house could make an appropriate judgment. The Gemara accepts this as {\i shema mina} \u8212- a valid proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks why we need both proofs \u8212- the intermarriage case and the {\i tumah ve-tahara} case. The answer: one might have thought that in the case of the {\i tzara}, the matter was well-known \u8212- {\i tzara kala it lei} \u8212- and they did not need to actively inform each other because the information was public. But from the case of {\i tumah ve-tahara}, where there was no particular reason the status would be publicly known, we learn that the houses went out of their way to inform one another even in cases that were not self-evident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Love and Friendship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} draws its conclusion with a verse: {\i ha\u8217'emet ve\u8217'hashalom ahavu} \u8212- love both truth and peace. The Maharsha observes beautifully that this is not {\i emes le\u8217'amito} \u8212- not the claim that there is one absolute exclusive truth \u8212- but rather the {\i emes} of {\i eilu ve\u8217'eilu divrei Elokim chayim}, a pluralistic truth. The houses were able to maintain both truth and peace precisely because they could embrace the notion that the other\u8217's position, though not their own, was a legitimate expression of the living word of God. That is what made the combination of {\i emet ve\u8217'shalom} possible: not compromise, but genuine respect for legitimate pluralism.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzarot Practices (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Information Exchange\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's conclusion regarding the {\i tzarot} case is therefore that Beit Shammai gave Beit Hillel the relevant information about family status. They did not abandon their practices, and they did not pretend the differences did not exist \u8212- but they cared enough about one another to ensure that no one was unwittingly walking into a halachic problem according to the other\u8217's {\i psak}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yerushalmi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Bavli\u8217's resolution \u8212- that the houses informed each other and thus intermarriage was possible without compromising either {\i pesak} \u8212- is notably not the approach of the Yerushalmi. The Yerushalmi in Yevamot poses the same question: {\i mamzeirus beintayim ve\u8217'at amarta hachin?} \u8212- there are {\i mamzeirim} in this situation and you are saying they married one another? How could that have happened?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One answer in the Yerushalmi, attributed to Rabbi Yaakov bar Acha in the name of Rabbi Yochanan, is that Beit Shammai conceded to Beit Hillel on the stringent side \u8212- a position that parallels certain Bavli discussions. Then Rabbi Tanchum in the name of Rabbi Yochanan adds: {\i elu ve\u8217'elu k\u8217'halacha hayu osim} \u8212- each house followed their own {\i halacha} properly. And this leads again to the question: if each followed their own {\i halacha}, how did intermarriage work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Providence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Yerushalmi\u8217's ultimate answer is: {\i hamakom mishamer ve\u8217'lo ira maaseh le\u8217'olam} \u8212- God watched over them and it never actually came to pass. That is to say, it never actually happened that someone from Beit Hillel married a person who according to their own {\i psak} was a {\i mamzer} from a Beit Shammai union. Divine Providence ensured it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legal Fictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer is striking in that it actually accepts the Mishna\u8217's literal meaning at face value \u8212- yes, they simply intermarried, each following their own {\i halacha} \u8212- and resolves the problem not by harmonizing the practices but by invoking a divine guarantee that the collision of {\i psakim} would never actually produce harm. The Bavli is unwilling to rest on that, and instead constructs the answer of mutual notification. But the Yerushalmi is apparently more comfortable with the notion that the system will be protected from its own consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to a broader characteristic of the Yerushalmi: a greater readiness to sustain legal fictions even in the face of countervailing evidence. A case in point, which will arise much later in this tractate, involves a woman whose husband was testified to be dead, who then remarried, and whose original husband subsequently reappears. The Yerushalmi\u8217's approach in such cases is to insist that the legal conclusion stands \u8212- clearly he is not her husband, because the witnesses testified he died \u8212- even when the facts on the ground seem to say otherwise. The capacity to maintain a halachic determination against empirical counter-evidence is something the Yerushalmi is more prepared to deploy than the Bavli.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further parallel from the Yerushalmi, striking in its own right: the Yerushalmi rules that if a girl below age three was violated, the hymen regenerates. But above age three, it does not. The Yerushalmi then says that if such an event occurred when she had just turned three \u8212- but the court subsequently declared a leap year, inserting a second Adar \u8212- then her birthday technically had not yet occurred, and the hymen {\i will} regenerate. This is not a physical claim so much as an assertion that {\i beit din}\u8217's legal determinations carry metaphysical force, or perhaps that halachic categorization can be sustained even in tension with what the physical facts seem to show. The Yerushalmi\u8217's answer of {\i hamakom mishamer} fits within this same orientation: we are prepared to say that the system works, that divine providence undergirds the halachic world, rather than that we must preemptively restructure practice to ensure no collision occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Bavli, by contrast, wants to work within what is knowable and manageable \u8212- and so it gives us the answer of mutual disclosure.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzer Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elazar Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now takes up the specific legal statement of Rabbi Elazar: {\i af al pi shenechleku Beit Shammai uBeit Hillel betzaros, modin she\u8217'ein mamzer ela mimi she\u8217'issuro issur erva ve\u8217'anush kares} \u8212- even though Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel disagreed about the status of {\i tzaros}, they agree that there is no {\i mamzer} except from one whose prohibition is {\i issur ervah} with a penalty of {\i kares}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concession Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: who is conceding to whom in this statement of {\i modin}? Is it Beit Shammai conceding to Beit Hillel, or Beit Hillel conceding to Beit Shammai?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Concedes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara first considers: if it is Beit Shammai conceding to Beit Hillel, then the concession would be that children born in Beit Hillel\u8217's world \u8212- where a {\i tzara} marries another man without {\i chalitzah}, which Beit Shammai regards as a violation of a {\i lav} \u8212- are not {\i mamzerim}. But this is obvious: {\i pshita bnei chayavei lavin ninhu} \u8212- they are children of a mere {\i lav} violation, and nobody has ever suggested that a {\i lav} violation alone produces {\i mamzeirus}. There is no {\i chiddush} there for Beit Shammai to make.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the Gemara concludes: {\i ella Beit Hillel le\u8217'Beit Shammai} \u8212- it is Beit Hillel conceding to Beit Shammai. Beit Hillel acknowledges that where Beit Shammai performs {\i yibbum} with the {\i tzara}, even though Beit Hillel regards this as an {\i issur kares} and the child would be a {\i mamzer}, the {\i mamzer} status flows from {\i issur kares} \u8212- and that is exactly what {\i mamzeirus} requires according to both houses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then clarifies what the {\i chiddush} is: {\i le\u8217'afukei mid\u8217'Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- to exclude the position of Rabbi Akiva, who holds that there is a {\i mamzer} even from {\i chayvei lavin}. The statement that both houses agree there is no {\i mamzer} except from {\i chayvei kerisot} is directed against Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position, establishing that neither Beit Shammai nor Beit Hillel follows him in this regard. As Tosafot notes, the question of whether even Rabbi Akiva would apply this rule to the specific case of {\i yevamah le\u8217'shuk} is debated, but the principal point stands: both houses align against the broader application of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzarot Practices (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now poses a dilemma from another {\i braita} that discusses how Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel did or did not avoid intermarriage. The {\i braita} lists a range of disputes between the houses \u8212- {\i tzaros}, sisters, {\i get yashan}, {\i safek eishet ish}, a case of a woman divorced and then spending the night with her former husband at an inn, and the question of the value of the {\i kesef} for {\i kiddushin}. In the {\i kiddushin} case, the asymmetry is particularly sharp: Beit Shammai requires a {\i dinar} and Beit Hillel requires only a {\i prutah}. If Reuven betrothed a woman with a {\i prutah} and Shimon subsequently betrothed her with a {\i dinar}, then according to Beit Hillel she is married to Reuven and according to Beit Shammai she is married to Shimon. Whichever man sleeps with her, he has committed adultery according to the other house, and the child is a {\i mamzer}. This is not a case of {\i meikel} and {\i machmir} \u8212- it is a case where each {\i psak} creates a genuine prohibition for the other, and there is no hierarchy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite all of this, the {\i braita} states: {\i lo nimne\u8217'u Beit Shammai milisa nashim miBeit Hillel vilo Beit Hillel miBeit Shammai}. And the reason given is {\i le\u8217'kayem mah shene\u8217'emar ha\u8217'emet ve\u8217'hashalom ahavu} \u8212- to fulfill the verse, \u8220"love truth and peace\u8221" \u8212- and {\i she\u8217'chibah ve\u8217're\u8217'ut nohagim zeh bazeh}, that love and friendship governed their relationship with one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Beit Shammai maintained their own practices throughout, one understands why Rabbi Shimon later says they did hold themselves back in cases of {\i vadai} \u8212- but then the first part of the {\i braita} needs explanation. And if Beit Shammai followed Beit Hillel\u8217's practices, why does any of this controversy matter at all?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Definite vs.\u160 ?Uncertain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon enters with a clarification: {\i nimne\u8217'u hen min ha\u8217'vadai ve\u8217'lo nimne\u8217'u min ha\u8217'safek} \u8212- they held themselves back from intermarrying in cases of {\i vadai} prohibition, but they did not hold themselves back in cases of {\i safek}. The idea is that where a woman was definitely forbidden according to one house\u8217's {\i psak}, that house would not marry her. But where the issue was one of doubt \u8212- as in the {\i safek eishet ish} case where a husband and wife spent a night together at an inn after divorce, and it is uncertain whether they effectively remarried \u8212- then one could be more accommodating of the other house\u8217's lenient ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asymmetry Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: accepting that Beit Shammai maintained their own practices, one understands why Beit Hillel would hold themselves back from Beit Shammai \u8212- the children are {\i mamzerim} according to Beit Hillel. But why would Beit Shammai hold themselves back from Beit Hillel, even in a case of {\i vadai}? The children from Beit Hillel women are {\i bnei chayvei lavin} \u8212- they are not {\i mamzerim} at all. What is the concern that would cause Beit Shammai to refuse marriage in a clear case? The Gemara answers by invoking the principle stated later by Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak: {\i lo nitzrecha ela le\u8217'tzara atzma} \u8212- the issue is not the children but the {\i tzara} herself. Marrying the {\i tzara} directly would be a violation of a {\i lav} according to Beit Shammai, and Beit Shammai would not want to be {\i over} on that {\i issur lav}. So Beit Shammai would hold themselves back not because of the children\u8217's status but because of the woman\u8217's own status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then catches a problem: Rabbi Shimon said they would not hold themselves back in cases of {\i safek}. But {\i safek issur d\u8217'Oraita le\u8217'chumra} \u8212- a doubtful Torah-level prohibition should be treated stringently. Why should a {\i safek} be more permissive? The Gemara answers: {\i lo teima min ha\u8217'safek ela eima min ha\u8217'stam} \u8212- do not say the category is {\i safek} (doubt); rather say the category is {\i stam} \u8212- cases where the status of the woman was simply not known, not cases of active uncertainty. The resolution is that in a {\i stam} case, one could rely on the assumption that if there were a problem, one would have been informed. {\i Demodeu lehu parshi} \u8212- the proactive disclosure between the houses was reliable enough that a person could marry any woman whose status was simply unknown, trusting that had there been an issue, someone would have said something. The {\i stam} intermarriage was thus not a gamble on a {\i safek} but rather an act of confidence in the system of mutual transparency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Trust\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant {\i chiddush}. The beginning of the {\i braita} says that they intermarried freely \u8212- {\i lo nimne\u8217'u}. According to this reading, that refers to {\i stam} cases: any woman about whom nothing particular was known. The confidence to marry her rested not on ignoring the other house\u8217's concerns but on the assurance that the mutual love and friendship between the houses guaranteed proactive notification when necessary. As Rashi frames it: I know that in your community there are women who according to my {\i psak} are {\i mamzerot}. But I can go ahead and seek a wife there because I know that if there is a problem, you will tell me. And you can assume the same from me. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i braita} \u8212- {\i she\u8217'chibah ve\u8217're\u8217'ut nohagim zeh bazeh} \u8212- is precisely this: that the love and friendship between the houses was reliable enough to serve as a halachic foundation for trust.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Authorship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes: {\i ha ka\u8217'mashma lan d\u8217'kula Rabbi Shimon hi} \u8212- the entire {\i braita} is to be attributed to Rabbi Shimon. This is a significant move: the beginning of the {\i braita}, which appeared to say simply that they did not hold themselves back \u8212- suggesting perhaps a radical tolerance of the other house\u8217's {\i psak} even in cases of {\i vadai} \u8212- is now read through the lens of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's clarification. Rabbi Shimon is not simply disagreeing with the beginning of the {\i braita}; he is its author and is explaining what it means. They held back in {\i vadai} cases, they did not hold back in {\i stam} cases \u8212- and the stated reason of love and friendship is the explanation of how {\i stam} cases could be navigated with confidence: through the reliable practice of mutual disclosure.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nuri\u8217's Proposal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nuri\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces the proposal of Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri: {\i heicha halacha revacha be\u8217'Yisrael?} \u8212- how should the {\i halacha} be propagated broadly in Israel regarding the dispute of {\i tzaras ervah}? Should we follow Beit Shammai or Beit Hillel? This framing suggests an attempt to establish a consensus {\i pesak} \u8212- a single ruling that would apply throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Problems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is that each position creates a problem from the other\u8217's perspective. If we rule like Beit Shammai \u8212- that {\i yibbum} may be performed with the {\i tzara} \u8212- then according to Beit Hillel, the children of that {\i yibbum} are {\i mamzerim}. If we rule like Beit Hillel \u8212- that the {\i tzara} is completely free and requires neither {\i yibbum} nor {\i chalitzah} \u8212- then according to Beit Shammai the child born from her subsequent marriage is {\i pagum}, flawed in status, as the child of a {\i lav} violation. And leaving aside the children, the woman herself is in a problematic status according to one house or the other. There is no single ruling that satisfies both houses\u8217' concerns simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri\u8217's proposed solution is elegant: {\i bo\u8217'u ve\u8217'nesaken lahem le\u8217'tzaros she\u8217'choltzot ve\u8217'lo misyabmot} \u8212- establish as the requirement that every {\i tzara} perform {\i chalitzah} and not {\i yibbum}. Beit Hillel is satisfied because the woman is freed without {\i yibbum} having been performed. Beit Shammai is satisfied because {\i chalitzah} was performed \u8212- even if they believe {\i yibbum} would have been preferable, their requirement has been met. The cost is that {\i chalitzah} renders a woman forbidden to marry a {\i kohen}, but at least it resolves the question in a way that neither house can object to in principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to contemporary discussions about {\i geirus} standards is noteworthy: some have proposed that everyone simply undergo a new conversion {\i le\u8217'chumra} to satisfy all positions \u8212- a {\i yotzei kol hadeos} approach. But as the Gemara\u8217's subsequent discussion makes clear, such an approach is not without serious costs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Failed Completion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ultimately, this proposal was never brought to completion: {\i lo hispiku ligmor et ha\u8217'davar} \u8212- they did not manage to conclude the matter before the opportunity was lost. Something disrupted the process, and the consensus was never formalized.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gamliel\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earlier Tzarot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel raised an objection to the proposal: {\i mah na\u8217'aseh le\u8217'tzara rishona mei\u8217'ata?} \u8212- what are we to do about the earlier {\i tzaros}, the women who were already in this situation? If we now establish that {\i chalitzah} is required, what does that imply about all the previous cases?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Problem If Followed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara considers: if Beit Shammai had been following Beit Hillel all along \u8212- that is, if the resolution were that {\i lo assu}, they did not maintain their own practices \u8212- then this objection would not apply. Everyone had been following Beit Hillel, no {\i yibbum} had been done, and there are no prior cases to be concerned about. But since the Gemara has concluded that {\i le\u8217'olam assu} \u8212- Beit Shammai maintained its own practices \u8212- there are indeed earlier {\i tzaros} who may have done {\i yibbum} or whose status may be affected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzara Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak sharpens the concern: {\i lo nitzrecha ela le\u8217'tzara atzma} \u8212- the real issue is not the children but the {\i tzara} herself. The scenario is this: under the working arrangement where everyone has been following Beit Hillel, the {\i tzara} has been treated as completely free \u8212- she has married another man and built her life. Now, if we formally require {\i chalitzah} going forward, we implicitly say that there {\i was} a {\i zika}, an obligation of {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah}, hanging over her. That retroactively casts a shadow over her status \u8212- she has been living with a man without {\i chalitzah} having been performed, and now we are suggesting that perhaps she should have had {\i chalitzah} first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Human Cost\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific human cost is captured in the phrase: {\i mi masya gavreihu} \u8212- her husband will find her disgusting. The meaning is that he will feel retroactively troubled, knowing that he had married a woman who, according to at least one major {\i psak}, still had an unresolved {\i zika} to her deceased husband\u8217's brother when she came to him. Even if one says the Beit Hillel {\i psak} was correct and there was no actual prohibition, the retroactive implication is damaging. The suggestion that she now do {\i chalitzah} sends a message about her prior status that is destructive to the marriage and to her dignity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pleasantness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara invokes the principle: {\i darcheha darchei noam ve\u8217'chol nesivoseha shalom} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's ways are ways of pleasantness and all its paths are peace. Playing it safe by imposing {\i chalitzah} on everyone sounds responsible, but it carries enormous human costs \u8212- costs that the Torah does not countenance. This principle is equally relevant to modern analogies involving {\i geirus}: when rabbinic authorities suggest that a convert simply do a {\i mikvah} again {\i le\u8217'chumra}, or that a couple perform a new {\i kiddushin} to satisfy all opinions, they often underestimate the profound message that sends about the person\u8217's prior status, their family\u8217's standing, and their own sense of religious authenticity. The Gemara here is explicit that those costs are real halachic considerations \u8212- not sentimentalities to be overridden in the name of safety. {\i Pasken} a position and follow it. The attempt to cover all bases at human expense is not a {\i chumra}; it is a violation of {\i darchei noam}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 15\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Shammai Hold Positi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on this amud continues to investigate a question that animated much of the preceding discussion: did {\i beit shammai} ultimately adopt the rulings of {\i beit hillel}, or did they persist in maintaining their own positions? This question matters enormously in practical terms \u8212- particularly regarding {\i tzarat ha-bat} and {\i tzarat ervah}, where the stakes involve the permissibility of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}, the status of children, and the eligibility of women to marry {\i kohanim}. As was noted in the prior discussion, Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri had suggested a safe-harbor approach of always requiring {\i chalitzah}, but the Gemara pointed out the considerable costs of that position: it would render women forbidden to {\i kohanim}, and would impose severe emotional and relational burdens on couples already bound together in marriage. Those concerns \u8212- the human cost of reflexive stringency \u8212- remain very much in the background as the Gemara now marshals a series of proofs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with a {\i tashma}: \u8220"Come and hear, for Rabbi Tarfon said, {\i te-avni} \u8212- how I desire that I should have a case of {\i tzarat ha-bat} come before me, so that I may act upon it.\u8221" In context, this means that Rabbi Tarfon was eager for a scenario in which his brother died childless while having two wives, one of whom was Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's daughter \u8212- and Rabbi Tarfon wished to perform {\i yibum} with the co-wife, following the position of {\i beit shammai} that she is permitted for {\i yibum}. Since Rabbi Tarfon was from the school of {\i beit shammai}, this seems to demonstrate that {\i beit shammai} adherents maintained their own {\i psak}. The Gemara, however, pushes back: perhaps {\i va-asena} should be read causatively \u8212- not \u8220"I will marry her\u8221" but \u8220"I will have her marry out,\u8221" meaning he wished to demonstrate his allegiance to {\i beit hillel}, who holds the {\i tzarat ha-bat} is entirely free from {\i chalitzah} and may simply marry without it. He would be eager to show he goes with {\i beit hillel}, rejecting Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri\u8217's cautious middle position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: but the language {\i te-avni} expresses personal longing \u8212- why would he be so emotionally invested in this if the point was merely to reject Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri\u8217's position? The answer given is that it was precisely that \u8212- {\i le-apuki me-Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri} \u8212- he wanted to repudiate Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri\u8217's suggestion that we simply always require {\i chalitzah} as a safeguard. He wished to demonstrate a clear ruling like {\i beit hillel}, without any such hedging. Of course, it remains somewhat awkward that a figure of {\i beit shammai}\u8217's school would be so animated about ruling purely like {\i beit hillel}. The reading is possible but strained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel Illanit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then brings another {\i tashma}: there was a story involving the daughter of Rabban Gamliel, who had been married to his brother Abba, and that brother died childless. Rabban Gamliel then performed {\i yibum} with the co-wife of his daughter rather than with his daughter herself. On its face, this looks like Rabban Gamliel acting according to {\i beit shammai} \u8212- that the {\i tzarat ha-bat} is permitted for {\i yibum}. The Gemara immediately objects: {\i ve-tisbarah} \u8212- does this make sense? Rabban Gamliel was a {\i nasi}, a descendant of Hillel himself. He would not be ruling like {\i beit shammai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore explains: {\i ela shani bito shel Rabban Gamliel de-illanit havai} \u8212- the reason Rabban Gamliel could do {\i yibum} with the co-wife was that his daughter was an {\i illanit}, a woman constitutionally incapable of bearing children. Because of her status as an {\i illanit}, the case never presented a genuine {\i tzarat ervah}, and therefore there was no prohibition on performing {\i yibum} with the co-wife. The {\i chiddush} of the story was not that Rabban Gamliel was ruling like {\i beit shammai}, but that the {\i illanit} status dissolved any potential problem of {\i tzarat ervah} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Illanit Status Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara presses further. The {\i baraita} ends with the statement: {\i acherim omrim bito shel Rabban Gamliel illanit hayeta} \u8212- \u8220"Others say his daughter was an {\i illanit}.\u8221" The fact that {\i acherim} add this detail implies that the {\i tanna kamma} told the story without emphasizing the {\i illanit} status \u8212- meaning the original {\i tanna} apparently did not regard the {\i illanit} as the operative factor. So what is the dispute between {\i tanna kamma} and {\i acherim}? The Gemara proposes that they agree the daughter was an {\i illanit}, but disagree about whether the husband knew this at the outset. If he did not know, the marriage was a {\i mekach ta\u8217'os} \u8212- a transaction entered into under a false premise \u8212- and the marriage never took effect. That would be an obvious case in which there is no {\i tzarat ervah}. If he did know and married her anyway, then the marriage is valid, yet arguably the {\i mitzvat yibum} never applied because an {\i illanit} cannot fulfill the purpose underlying {\i yibum}. The {\i tanna kamma} may be speaking of the case where the {\i illanit} status was discovered afterward \u8212- rendering the marriage void \u8212- while {\i acherim} are addressing the case where the {\i illanit} status was known from the outset and the valid marriage simply did not create a {\i tzarat ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tenai Bikiddushin Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then entertains two further possibilities of what might be at stake between {\i tanna kamma} and {\i acherim}. One is whether the case involved a {\i t\u8217'nai bikiddushin} \u8212- a conditional stipulation in the marriage \u8212- and whether such a condition remains operative even after the couple has lived together and consummated the marriage. The {\i tanna kamma} might hold that the condition persists and invalidated the marriage retroactively, such that there was never a {\i tzarat ervah}. {\i Acherim} might hold that the condition was nullified by their subsequent life together, and therefore the only way to resolve the case is through the {\i illanit} factor itself. This question has obvious contemporary relevance: proposals to address the {\i agunah} crisis through conditional {\i kiddushin} often founder on precisely this issue \u8212- can a stipulation survive cohabitation and physical union?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Ma\u8217'aserot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara shifts away from {\i tzarat ervah} and examines whether {\i beit shammai} maintained their own rulings more broadly. Rav Mesharshiya raises a {\i tashma} from the case of Rabbi Akiva, who harvested an {\i esrog} on the first of {\i Shevat} \u8212- a date that falls between the competing positions of {\i beit shammai} and {\i beit hillel} regarding the new year for {\i ma\u8217'aser}. {\i Beit shammai} holds the new year is the first of {\i Shevat}; {\i beit hillel} holds it is the fifteenth. If the {\i esrog} was harvested in the second year of the {\i ma\u8217'aser} cycle and the new year for {\i ma\u8217'aser} had just turned, then whether it requires {\i maaser sheni} or {\i maaser ani} depends on which position one follows. The story relates that Rabban Gamliel \u8212- it appears the original text means Rabban Gamliel, though the attribution appears via Rabbi Akiva in the tradition \u8212- performed {\i shnei isurin}, two separate tithings: one according to {\i beit shammai} and one according to {\i beit hillel}. This involved a creative series of stipulations: he took {\i maaser sheni}, redeemed it on money, purchased food with it in Jerusalem, and then, in case the {\i esrog} was actually from year three, he gave the food he had redeemed to the poor \u8212- thereby satisfying {\i maaser ani} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adduces this as proof that {\i beit shammai} held their position, since the double tithing practice was evidently still a live necessity in Rabbi Akiva\u8217's time. {\i Shema mina asu} \u8212- you see that they maintained their position. However, the Gemara rejects this inference: {\i gamrei istapka lei} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel had genuine uncertainty about the tradition, not knowing which position was actually {\i beit hillel}\u8217's. He was not trying to hedge between {\i beit shammai} and {\i beit hillel}; he simply did not know which date {\i beit hillel} had endorsed. He doubled the tithing out of uncertainty about {\i beit hillel}\u8217's view, not as an endorsement of {\i beit shammai}\u8217's continued authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel\u8217's Uncertainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This rebuttal \u8212- that the double {\i ma\u8217'aser} practice reflected Rabban Gamliel\u8217's uncertainty about {\i beit hillel}\u8217's position rather than any concession to {\i beit shammai} \u8212- is characteristic of the Gemara\u8217's pattern throughout this discussion. Every apparent proof that {\i beit shammai} adhered to their own rulings is deflected by an alternative explanation. The Gemara has not yet conceded the point, though the evidence is accumulating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Sukkah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra then raises a proof from a striking story: Shammai the Elder\u8217's daughter-in-law gave birth, and Shammai himself removed part of the ceiling directly above the crib and placed {\i sechach} over that opening, so that the newborn infant would be dwelling, at least notionally, under a valid {\i sukkah}. The {\i mishnah} in {\i Sukkah} states that minors who still require their mothers \u8212- certainly newborns \u8212- are exempt from the obligation of {\i sukkah}. Shammai, however, was stringent even for infants. The Gemara asks: {\i shema mina asu} \u8212- does this not prove that {\i beit shammai} held their position? The Gemara deflects: {\i ha-ro\u8217'eh omer le-afukei avira avad} \u8212- an observer would simply assume he was ventilating the space, not making a {\i sukkah}. This is an acknowledged thin response; there is no violation of {\i agudot agudot} \u8212- the prohibition on creating factional displays \u8212- because it looks like a home improvement, not a public legal statement. But as the {\i mesivot} in the room observe, the rejoinder is hardly satisfying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehu\u8217's Trough Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra continues with another {\i tashma}: the story of {\i shokat Yehu}, a large trough in Jerusalem that collected water and had a small hole connecting it to a valid {\i mikvah}. All the purifications performed in Jerusalem were done in this {\i shokat Yehu}, whose validity depended on that connecting hole. The relevance is that {\i beit shammai} requires a majority of the dividing wall to be removed for two {\i mikvaot} to be considered joined, while {\i beit hillel} requires only an opening the width and hollow of a tube inserted into a flask \u8212- approximately two finger-widths in diameter. The story relates that {\i beit shammai} sent word and had the hole in the {\i shokat Yehu} widened to satisfy their own standard. This was not merely an internal community practice but an intervention into a public {\i mikvah} used by all of Jerusalem. The Gemara again attempts to deflect \u8212- people who watched would assume they were simply trying to let more water in \u8212- but again this answer is weak, and the pattern is clear: {\i beit shammai} actively enforced their positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikvaot Connection Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} records the operative {\i halacha}: the connection between {\i mikvaot} requires an opening of {\i shfoferet ha-nod} \u8212- the width of the tube of a flask \u8212- measured both in terms of the tube\u8217's outer width and its hollow interior, amounting to roughly two finger-widths. This is the position of {\i beit hillel}, and it is the ruling that has been followed in practice. This contextualizes the significance of the {\i beit shammai} intervention in the {\i shokat Yehu} story: not only were they holding to their own position, they were acting upon it in communal space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Bar Tzadok\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now brings a {\i tashma} that carries more weight and begins to shift the discussion toward a concession. Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok testifies: \u8220"When I was learning Torah by Rabbi Yochanan Hachorni, there were times when he ate only dry bread with salt\u8221" \u8212- this during years of famine, when even that meager sustenance was all he had. Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok\u8217's father sent olives for the teacher. When Rabbi Yochanan Hachorni saw the olives were wet, he declined to eat them \u8212- he was scrupulous about {\i chullin betuma}, avoiding food that might have been made susceptible to {\i tumah} through contact with liquid. Wetness from the olives\u8217' own juice would constitute {\i hechsher} \u8212- making the food receptive to {\i tumah} \u8212- if the liquid had been desired by the owner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok\u8217's father then sent word explaining that the barrel had a hole in it \u8212- {\i chavis nekuva hayita} \u8212- but leaves and sediment had stopped up the hole. Since the owner had clearly not wanted the liquid (having put the olives in a barrel with a drainage hole), the liquid that collected was not considered {\i machshir} and the olives remained {\i tahor}. The {\i mishnah} confirms: if a barrel of pressed olives had a hole and it was stopped up by sediment, the olives are {\i tehora}. More significantly, the Gemara adds: {\i ve-af al pi she-talmid Shammai haya, kol ma\u8217'asav lo asa ela ke-divrei Beit Hillel} \u8212- even though Rabbi Yochanan Hachorni was a student of Shammai, all of his practices followed {\i beit hillel}. The fact that he was concerned about the wetness of the olives \u8212- a concern only meaningful on {\i beit hillel}\u8217's view that the liquid of olives is {\i machshir} \u8212- demonstrates that in practice he ruled like {\i beit hillel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Novelty Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This testimony is different in character from the earlier proofs. Here, the Gemara finds the argument genuinely compelling: {\i i amart bishlama asu} \u8212- if in general the students of {\i beit shammai} maintained their own positions, then there is a genuine {\i chiddush} in reporting that Rabbi Yochanan Hachorni was an exception who practiced like {\i beit hillel}. But {\i i amart lo asu} \u8212- if {\i beit shammai} never maintained their positions and everyone followed {\i beit hillel} universally, then there is no novelty whatsoever in reporting that Rabbi Yochanan Hachorni did the same. The testimony would be utterly unremarkable. The Gemara does not answer this challenge, and while it does not formally say {\i shema mina}, it is clearly a powerful inference. The evidence that {\i beit shammai} held their rulings is now very strong.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehoshua Refuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standard Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a series of inquiries directed at Rabbi Yehoshua regarding the specific case of {\i tzarat ha-bat}. Someone asks: {\i tzarat ha-bat mahu} \u8212- what is the {\i halacha} about the co-wife of a daughter? Rabbi Yehoshua gives the standard, safe response: {\i machloket beit shammai u-veit hillel} \u8212- it is a dispute between {\i beit shammai} and {\i beit hillel}. This is, as the discussion notes with a certain wryness, almost always a correct answer for any difficult question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasken Like Whom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The questioner is not satisfied with the identification of the dispute, however, and presses: {\i halacha ke-divrei mi} \u8212- whose position do we rule like? This second question is the harder one, and it is to this that Rabbi Yehoshua refuses to respond directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head Between Mountains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's reply is one of the most memorable in this sugya: {\i mipnei mah atem machnisin roshi bein shenei harim gedolim} \u8212- \u8220"Why are you forcing me to put my head between two great mountains?\u8221" He elaborates: {\i beit shammai} and {\i beit hillel} are two enormous factions \u8212- {\i shenei machloket gedolot} \u8212- and {\i misyarei ani shema yarutzu gulgelet roshi}, he fears that his head will be crushed between them. The image is vivid: if he were to rule definitively like {\i beit hillel}, he would be declaring retroactively that all those who followed {\i beit shammai} on this question committed a {\i issur karet} by engaging in {\i yibum} with a {\i tzarat ervah} \u8212- and that all children born of such unions are {\i mamzerim} or at minimum {\i pesulin} for {\i kehuna}. The consequences of a definitive ruling are devastating for real people, real families, real lives. This is why he refuses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase has resonated throughout the {\i teshuva} literature: {\i poskim} who are asked to adjudicate a matter already debated by great earlier authorities often write {\i mi ani le-hachnis et roshi bein harim gedolim} \u8212- who am I to thrust my head between these great mountains \u8212- though they sometimes do so anyway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Great Families\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite refusing to rule on the underlying question, Rabbi Yehoshua does offer testimony: {\i ani meid lachem al shtei mishpachot gedolot she-hayu bi-Yerushalayim} \u8212- \u8220"I testify regarding two great families in Jerusalem.\u8221" These were {\i mishpachat beit tzvu\u8217'im mi-beit Achmai} and {\i mishpachat beit Kufa\u8217'i mi-beit Mkoshesh}. Both families were {\i b\u8217'nei tzarot} \u8212- descended from women who had been in the position of {\i tzarat ervah} and had married out without {\i chalitzah}. Despite this, members of these families became {\i kohanim gedolim} and served at the altar. Rabbi Yehoshua presents this not as a direct {\i psak} but as a factual testimony: here are families, here is their provenance, here is their standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explains the Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now uses this testimony to reinforce the inference that {\i beit shammai} had indeed been maintaining their position. {\i I amart bishlama asu} \u8212- if {\i beit shammai} held by their rulings, then Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's fear is entirely intelligible. He would be saying that all those {\i beit shammai} families who did {\i yibum} in {\i tzarat ha-bat} cases were committing {\i issur karet} and their children might be {\i pesulin}. {\i Ella i amart lo asu} \u8212- but if {\i beit shammai} was already following {\i beit hillel}, what was there to fear? Everyone agreed. Once again the Gemara does not formally concede {\i shema mina}, but the inference is nearly irresistible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequence Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even granting that {\i beit shammai} held its position, the Gemara asks: but {\i ani meid lachem ein mamzer ella mi-chayavei misos beit din} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua himself holds that a {\i mamzer} results only from a union that carries a penalty of death by {\i beit din}! Relations with a {\i tzarat ervah} carry {\i karet}, not court-administered death. So even if {\i beit shammai} was wrong and those unions were {\i issur karet}, the children would not be {\i mamzerim} by Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's own ruling. Why then is he afraid to give a {\i psak}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pagum Not Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i venei nami de-mamzer lo havi, pagum meha havi} \u8212- true, the child would not be a {\i mamzer}, but the child would at minimum be {\i pagum} \u8212- blemished and forbidden to {\i kehuna} \u8212- and this is established through a {\i kal vachomer} from the case of an {\i almana} and a {\i kohen gadol}. The marriage of a widow to the {\i kohen gadol} is forbidden only to a {\i kohen gadol}, not to all people \u8212- it is a limited prohibition \u8212- and yet the child of such a union is {\i pagum} and forbidden to all of {\i kehuna}. How much more so in the case of a {\i tzarat ervah}, whose prohibition is {\i shaveh ba-kol} \u8212- applying equally to all people \u8212- should the child be {\i pagum}? Even without a {\i mamzer} ruling, the families of {\i beit shammai} who performed {\i yibum} in {\i tzarat ha-bat} cases would have children who are {\i pesulin le-kehuna} \u8212- and those children\u8217's children, and so on through generations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} is structured as follows: in the case of an {\i almana} and a {\i kohen gadol}, the prohibition does not apply universally \u8212- she may marry ordinary {\i kohanim} \u8212- yet the child is {\i pagum}. The mechanism appears tied to the specific {\i issurei kehuna} context: the forbidden union makes the woman a {\i chalala} and the child a {\i chalal} or {\i chalala}, since that category of prohibition uniquely creates the status of {\i chalalah}. The question then arises whether the same logic extends to {\i yibum} violations or to {\i machzir gerushato} \u8212- a man who remarries a woman he divorced after she had married someone else \u8212- unions that are {\i issur lav} but are not {\i issurei kehuna} and do not carry the mechanism of {\i chalalah}. Perhaps in those cases the child is not {\i pagum}. This is what the Gemara is exploring when it discusses the consequences for {\i benei tzarat} specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evasion Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains the structure of Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's testimony more precisely. They asked him two questions: first, what is the {\i halacha} about the {\i tzarot} themselves \u8212- do they need {\i chalitzah} or may they simply marry? Second, according to {\i beit shammai}\u8217's view, what would be the status of children born from those who followed {\i beit hillel} and married without {\i chalitzah}? The answer to the second question is relevant not only to {\i tzarat ervah} cases but also to understanding whether children of {\i issur lav} unions \u8212- like {\i machzir gerushato} \u8212- are {\i pagum} to {\i kohanim}. Rabbi Yehoshua refuses to rule on the first question \u8212- {\i tzarot mitzaer ani}, I will not touch the question of the co-wives themselves \u8212- but on the second he offers testimony: the {\i benei tzarot} from those two great families served as {\i kohanim gedolim}, so their status was evidently not {\i pagum}. This testimony is carefully calibrated: he is not ruling that the {\i tzarot} were permitted, but that their children were not considered disqualified, which is itself the lesser of the two explosive questions. By testifying about the children without ruling on the wives, Rabbi Yehoshua threads the needle \u8212- or, as the Gemara reads it, strategically evades the most dangerous part of the inquiry.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dosa & Yonatan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tashma Dosa Time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings its most conclusive piece of evidence yet: {\i bimei Rabbi Dosa ben Horkinas, hutru tzarat ha-bat la-achim} \u8212- in the time of Rabbi Dosa ben Horkinas, they had permitted the {\i tzarat ha-bat} to the brothers for {\i yibum}, following the position of {\i beit shammai}. This is a clear, unambiguous statement. The Gemara responds with {\i shema mina} \u8212- hear from this \u8212- and concedes the point: yes, {\i beit shammai} held their position and acted upon it. The long series of attempted refutations has finally been resolved. The question of how {\i beit hillel} and {\i beit shammai} could have intermarried despite their dispute must therefore be explained on other grounds \u8212- such as the practice of informing the other side about relevant status issues so that the absence of such notice could be relied upon as an indication that no problem existed, or other such solutions discussed earlier in the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' Distress\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the Gemara turns to the story itself. {\i Va-haya ha-davar kasha la-chachamim} \u8212- the matter was deeply distressing to the sages, for they understood that someone had issued a {\i psak} permitting {\i tzarat ha-bat} for {\i yibum} under the authority of Rabbi Dosa ben Horkinas, and Rabbi Dosa ben Horkinas was {\i chacham gadol haya} \u8212- a great sage, elderly and venerable, to whom deference was owed. Compounding matters, {\i einav kamu mi-lavo le-veit ha-midrash} \u8212- his eyes had grown dim, he had become blind, and he no longer came to the {\i beit ha-midrash}. The sages could not easily engage him in the normal collegial setting. There is something pointed in this detail: this great elder had been, in a sense, cut off from the community of scholars, and now a ruling had emerged under his name that alarmed his colleagues. If he had been more present, more visited, perhaps the situation would have developed differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delegation Visits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sages deliberated: {\i mi yelech ve-yodi\u8217'o} \u8212- who will go to inform him of their opposition? Rabbi Yehoshua volunteered: {\i ani eilech}. Then Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah joined as the second, and Rabbi Akiva as the third. The delegation was thus composed of three of the leading figures of the generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Honors Distributed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They went and stood at the entrance of his home. His maidservant entered and informed him: {\i chachmei Yisrael ba\u8217'im etzlecha} \u8212- the sages of Israel are coming to you. He instructed her: {\i yikansu} \u8212- let them enter. The text then describes a curious detail: they {\i tafsu} \u8212- seized or pressed \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua and seated him on a golden couch. Then Rabbi Dosa asked Rabbi Yehoshua to invite his other colleague to sit as well. This prompts the question: who is the next visitor? \u8212- and the answer reveals a remarkable generational gap in Rabbi Dosa\u8217's awareness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dosa Realizes Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When told the second sage is Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah, Rabbi Dosa responds with astonishment: {\i veyesh lo ben le-Azaryah chaveireinu} \u8212- does our friend Azaryah have a son? He had been so long removed from communal life that he did not even know of Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah\u8217's existence, let alone his stature. He recites the verse: {\i naar hayiti gam zakanti} \u8212- \u8220"I was young and now I am old\u8221" \u8212- and then the verse {\i ve-lo ra\u8217'iti tzaddik ne\u8217'ezav ve-zaro mevakesh lachem} \u8212- \u8220"I have never seen a righteous man abandoned, with his children seeking bread.\u8221" The {\i Maharsha} notes that Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah was in fact very wealthy and from the {\i nasi}\u8217's family, and this is the fulfillment of that verse: Azaryah\u8217's son is not seeking bread. There is also, the {\i Maharsha} implies, an ironic double meaning: {\i lo ra\u8217'iti} \u8212- I have not seen him, literally, both because he cannot see and because he was unaware of him. They had Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah seated on the golden couch as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Indirect Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua then mentioned the third member of the party. Asked who the next visitor was, he said: {\i Akiva ben Yosef}. Rabbi Dosa responded with genuine warmth and wonder: {\i ata hu Akiva ben Yosef she-shimcha holech mi-sof ha-olam ve-ad sofo} \u8212- \u8220"You are Akiva ben Yosef, whose name goes from one end of the world to the other!\u8221" He invited him to sit: {\i shev beni shev, ke-motecha yirbu be-Yisrael} \u8212- sit, my son, sit; may your like multiply in Israel. The {\i Maharsha} notes that Rabbi Akiva was not placed on a golden couch, as he lacked the lineage or official position of the other two, yet he was warmly welcomed. The delegation then did not immediately raise the pressing question but instead began engaging Rabbi Dosa in broad halachic discussion \u8212- {\i hitchilu mesavvevim oto be-halachot} \u8212- surrounding him with topics of {\i halachot}, drawing him gently into conversation before broaching the sensitive matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Main Question Asked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After a period of {\i halachic} discussion, they eventually arrived at the heart of the matter: {\i tzarat ha-bat mahu} \u8212- what is the {\i halacha} regarding the co-wife of a daughter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dosa\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Dosa\u8217's response was unambiguous: {\i machloket beit shammai u-veit hillel. Halacha ke-veit hillel} \u8212- it is a dispute between {\i beit shammai} and {\i beit hillel}, and we rule like {\i beit hillel}. There was no hesitation, no hedging. His {\i psak} was clearly with {\i beit hillel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sages then pressed him: {\i halo mi-shimcha amru halacha ke-veit shammai} \u8212- but we heard that under your name they ruled like {\i beit shammai}, permitting {\i tzarat ha-bat} for {\i yibum}. The confrontation was now direct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother Yonatan Revealed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Dosa responded with a clarifying question: in the report you heard, did it say {\i Dosa} permitted this, or did it say {\i ben Horkinas}? The sages admitted: {\i stam shamanu} \u8212- we heard it attributed simply to {\i ben Horkinas}, without the first name. Rabbi Dosa then explained: {\i ach katan yesh li} \u8212- I have a younger brother. This brother is {\i bechor satan hu} \u8212- {\i Rashi} explains the phrase as describing someone utterly headstrong, indifferent to others\u8217' opinions, a provocateur of the first order \u8212- and his name is {\i Yonatan}. And it is {\i Yonatan}, not {\i Dosa}, who issued the permissive ruling. {\i Ve-hu mi-talmidei Shammai} \u8212- and he is a student of Shammai, committed to the {\i beit shammai} position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Hundred Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Dosa issued a serious caution: {\i ve-hizzaharu bo she-lo yikapach etchem be-halachot} \u8212- be careful when you engage with him, because he will overwhelm you with {\i halachot}. {\i Lefi she-immo shalosh me\u8217'ot teshuvot be-tzarat ha-bat she-hi mutteret} \u8212- he has three hundred proofs that the {\i tzarat ha-bat} is permitted for {\i yibum}. Do not underestimate what you are walking into.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chagai\u8217's Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, Rabbi Dosa affirmed: {\i aval me\u8217'id ani alai shamayim} \u8212- I testify, calling heaven and earth upon me \u8212- {\i she-al maducha zo yashav Chagai ha-Navi} \u8212- that on this very grinding stone, this seat, sat Chagai the prophet \u8212- {\i ve-eid al shelosha devarim} \u8212- and he testified to three things. The first: {\i tzarat ha-bat asurah} \u8212- the co-wife of a daughter is forbidden, she may not be taken in {\i yibum}. Whatever proofs {\i Yonatan} might marshal through logical argumentation, there is a {\i mesorah} \u8212- a received tradition \u8212- going back to {\i Chagai} the prophet himself that {\i tzarat ha-bat} is forbidden. This is the crux of the matter: the debate between tradition and logical derivation. {\i Yonatan} can construct three hundred arguments; the tradition that supersedes them goes back through {\i Chagai} to the chain of transmission that connects the prophets to Sinai. The prophets in this context are not transmitting {\i nevuah} \u8212- they are transmitting {\i mesorah}, the chain of practice and ruling that passed through them as links: {\i Moshe kibel Torah mi-Sinai u-Mesarah le-Yehoshua, ve-Yehoshua le-Zkenim, ve-Zkenim li-Neviim} \u8212- and it is in this capacity, as transmitters of tradition rather than receivers of prophecy, that {\i Chagai} testifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chagai\u8217's Three Points\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second point of {\i Chagai}\u8217's testimony: {\i Ammoni u-Mo\u8217'avi measrin ma\u8217'aser ani bi-shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- Ammon and Moab tithe {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} in the {\i shmita} year. Ammon and Moab, situated across the Jordan, were not part of {\i Eretz Yisrael} proper, but had a quasi-sanctified status rabbinically. The ordinance was that during {\i shmita}, when agricultural production in {\i Eretz Yisrael} ceased and the poor had nothing to live from, Ammon and Moab \u8212- which continued to farm \u8212- would designate their second tithe not as {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} (which would be taken to Jerusalem) but as {\i ma\u8217'aser ani}, so that the poor would have sustenance. The irony is acute: {\i shmita} is meant to be liberating, yet in practice it could devastate the poor; the rabbinic solution was to leverage the produce of neighboring territories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third point: {\i u-mekablim geirim min ha-Karduyim u-min ha-Tarmudim} \u8212- converts are accepted from the Karduyim and the Tarmudim. These nations were sometimes suspected of having intermixed Jewish lineage compromised by {\i mamzerut}, but Chagai\u8217's testimony affirmed that they are in fact simply non-Jews, and their {\i geirim} are accepted without concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leaving Separately\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} adds: when the three sages entered Rabbi Dosa\u8217's home, they came through a single door, but when they left, {\i yatzu bi-shlosha petachim} \u8212- they departed through three different doors. {\i Tosafot} explains this was deliberate strategy: they feared encountering {\i Yonatan} all together. If {\i Yonatan} were to defeat all three of them in {\i halachic} argument simultaneously, there would be enormous pressure to concede the {\i psak} in his direction. But if he could only engage one at a time, the other two could each say they had not been present and would not feel bound by the outcome of that particular exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Stumped\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The plan did not fully succeed. {\i Yonatan} encountered Rabbi Akiva \u8212- {\i akshi lei} \u8212- and challenged him with his proofs. {\i Ve-ukmei} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva was, in the expression of the Gemara, left standing; he had no response. He was not persuaded, but he could not refute the arguments. This is a remarkable moment: Rabbi Akiva, who routinely emerges victorious in dialectical combat throughout the Talmud, was intellectually outmaneuvered. {\i Yonatan}\u8217's three hundred proofs were not easily dismissed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yonatan\u8217's Insult\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yonatan}\u8217's response to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's silence was withering: {\i ata hu Akiva she-shimcha holech mi-sof ha-olam ve-ad sofo} \u8212- you are the great Akiva whose name goes from one end of the world to the other? {\i Ashreikha she-zachita le-shem ve-adayin lo higata le-ro\u8217'eh} \u8212- you should be blessed that you earned such a name, yet you have not even attained the level of a simple shepherd. This is a deliberate echo \u8212- and inversion \u8212- of his brother Rabbi Dosa\u8217's greeting. Rabbi Dosa had said {\i ke-motecha yirbu be-Yisrael} and {\i shev beni shev}; {\i Yonatan} greeted the same man\u8217's reputation with contempt and told him he was not worthy even of a shepherd\u8217's work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva replied: {\i va-afilu ro\u8217'eh tzon} \u8212- yes, I am not even worthy of a shepherd. The {\i Maharsha} attempts to read this as a subtle {\i vort}: the {\i ro\u8217'eh tzon} \u8212- shepherd \u8212- alludes to Moshe Rabbeinu, who was also a shepherd, and Rabbi Akiva is saying that this {\i halacha} rests on a {\i mesorah} going back to Moshe. Whether or not that reading is in the text, it is certainly true that the Gemara\u8217's resolution of this confrontation is not through Rabbi Akiva\u8217's logical refutation of {\i Yonatan}\u8217's proofs \u8212- it is through the invocation of {\i Chagai}\u8217's tradition. The three hundred proofs may stand logically, but the tradition supersedes them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chagai\u8217's Traditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ammon Moav Ma\u8217'aser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to examine the second and third of {\i Chagai}\u8217's three traditions in greater detail. Regarding {\i Ammoni u-Mo\u8217'avi measrin ma\u8217'aser ani bi-shevi\u8217'it}: the practice of tithing {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} during the {\i shmita} year by Ammon and Moab is explained by the principle of {\i harbeh krachim kibshu olei Mitzrayim ve-lo kibshu olei Bavel} \u8212- many towns that were conquered in the time of the exodus from Egypt and the original entry into the land under Yehoshua were not reconquered by the returnees from Babylon in the time of Ezra. The {\i kedusha} of the first conquest \u8212- the {\i kedusha rishona} \u8212- did not endure: {\i kidsha le-sha\u8217'tah ve-lo kidsha le-atid lavo}, it sanctified for its time but not for the future. The operative sanctity for {\i halachic} purposes follows the second entry and the second sanctification, and those towns that were not resanctified in the time of Ezra do not carry the full {\i kedushah} of {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cities for Poor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, some of those towns were deliberately left unsanctified. {\i Ve-henichum k\u8217'dei she-yismachu aleihem aniyim bi-shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- they were intentionally left so that the poor could rely on them during the {\i shmita} year. In those towns, farming continued during {\i shmita}, and {\i Rashi} notes that the poor could also be hired to work those fields, providing livelihood. The same logic applied to Ammon and Moab: they had a quasi-{\i eretz Yisrael} status rabbinically and therefore incurred {\i ma\u8217'aser} obligations, but the {\i ma\u8217'aser} designated there in {\i shmita} was {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} rather than {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, channeling resources to the poor precisely when they were most needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accepting Converts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third tradition of {\i Chagai} \u8212- that {\i geirim} are accepted from the {\i Karduyim} and the {\i Tarmudim} \u8212- prompts the Gemara\u8217's extended investigation into which nations are meant and what the precise concern was regarding each.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kartuyim vs Karduyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} of Rami bar Yechezkel seems to contradict the tradition: {\i ein mekablim geirim min ha-Karduyim} \u8212- we do not accept {\i geirim} from the {\i Karduyim}. Rav Ashi resolves this by distinguishing between {\i Kartuyim} and {\i Karduyim}: these are different nations. The prohibition applies to {\i Kartuyim}, not {\i Karduyim}. A popular folk saying apparently supported this distinction: {\i Kartuyim psulin} \u8212- \u8220"the {\i Kartuyim} are {\i pasul}\u8221" \u8212- and this was a recognized phrase that rhymed and circulated widely, indicating that the problematic group was specifically the {\i Kartuyim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarmodim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question about the {\i Tarmudim} is more involved. Both {\i Chagai}\u8217's tradition and certain other sources seem to accept {\i geirim} from {\i Tarmod}, while other traditions attributed to the {\i amora} Rabbi Yochanan reject it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rekem Stains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a {\i mishnah} from the laws of {\i niddah}-related stains: {\i kol ha-ktamim ha-ba\u8217'im min ha-Rekem tehorim} \u8212- all stains coming from {\i Rekem} are {\i tahor}, because {\i Rekem} is a non-Jewish locality and stains that originate with non-Jewish women do not carry the {\i tumah} of {\i niddah} stains. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees, declaring such stains {\i tamei} \u8212- because {\i Rekem}, in his view, is inhabited by converts who observe some Jewish law but err in their practice, and therefore their stains are treated as those of Jews. The general ruling of the {\i tanna kamma}, however, is that stains from non-Jews \u8212- explicitly {\i bein ovdei kochavim} \u8212- are {\i tahor}, with no exception carved out for {\i Tarmod}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then points out that the {\i mishnah} is categorical in declaring stains from non-Jews universally {\i tahor} \u8212- {\i ka pasik ve-tanei} \u8212- without distinguishing {\i Tarmod} from any other non-Jewish locale. This implies that even {\i Tarmod} is treated as straightforwardly non-Jewish, and therefore {\i geirim} from there should be accepted. Rabbi Yochanan himself is cited as having drawn this conclusion explicitly: {\i zot omeret mekablim geirim mi-Tarmod} \u8212- this {\i mishnah} implies that we accept {\i geirim} from {\i Tarmod}. Yet elsewhere, Rabbi Yochanan and {\i Savya} are both reported as saying the opposite: {\i ein mekablim geirim min ha-Tarmudim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution Amoraim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this apparent contradiction: {\i amorai ninhu ve-aliba de-Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- these are {\i amoraim} who disagree about what Rabbi Yochanan actually held. One {\i amora} understood Rabbi Yochanan to be inferring from the {\i mishnah} that {\i Tarmod} is simply non-Jewish and its {\i geirim} are acceptable; another {\i amora} understood Rabbi Yochanan as holding that the {\i Tarmudim} are suspect and their {\i geirim} are not accepted. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's categorical statement \u8212- treating all non-Jewish localities as {\i tahor} without exception \u8212- seems to favor the view that {\i Tarmod} is genuinely non-Jewish. The full resolution of what distinguishes the {\i Tarmudim} from other nations, and why the question of their lineage was debated at all, will be taken up in the continuation of the discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 16\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tarmod & Converts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Masechet Yevamot is not exclusively about {\i yibum}; it is equally concerned with questions of personal status in the broadest sense. Daf 16 plunges directly into some of the most central and consequential of those questions \u8212- who is Jewish, what is the status of children born of mixed unions, and what communities carry with them a taint of {\i mamzerut} or compromised lineage. These themes, introduced here in a relatively compressed passage, serve as a genuine precursor to major discussions that will recur throughout the tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haggai\u8217's Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara had already, in the preceding amud, introduced the testimony of Haggai haNavi on two matters: first, that {\i tzarat habat} is {\i muteret}, in accordance with the position of Beit Hillel; and second, that one may accept {\i geirim} from the peoples of Tarmod and Karduyim. This second ruling implies that the residents of those places are presumed to be non-Jews \u8212- which is itself significant, because the alternative fear was that they might be Jews who are {\i mamzerim}. If they were {\i mamzerim}, the problem would be irresolvable: they would not need {\i geirus}, yet they could not marry into the community. The testimony of Haggai thus served to relieve that concern and permit the acceptance of converts from those locales.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara had noted an apparent contradiction in the statements of Rabbi Yochanan regarding whether one may or may not accept converts from Tarmod. That tension sets the stage for the present analysis, which now investigates what the underlying problems with Tarmod actually were.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzeirut Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarmod Problems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i MiTarmod mai ta\u8217'ama lo?} \u8212- what is the concern that would prevent accepting converts from Tarmod? Rabbi Yochanan and Savya, a rare {\i amora}, dispute the matter. One says the problem stems from {\i avdei Shlomo} \u8212- the servants of Solomon \u8212- who apparently intermarried with women from Tarmod. The other says the issue is {\i benot Yerushalayim} \u8212- the young women of Jerusalem, whose fate during the destruction of the Beit HaMikdash we will examine shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's initial framing already seems puzzling: if these women had Jewish mothers, why should there be any problem? The children of a Jewish mother are Jewish regardless of the father\u8217's lineage \u8212- or so we would ordinarily say. The nature of the problem only becomes clear once the Gemara introduces a startlingly radical opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew = Mamzer?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains the approach of those troubled by {\i avdei Shlomo} as follows: {\i kasavar oved kochavim ve\u8217'eved haba al bat Yisrael havlad mamzer} \u8212- the child born of a non-Jew or a slave who has relations with a Jewish woman is a {\i mamzer}. This is a profoundly counterintuitive position. We are accustomed to saying that matrilineal descent settles the matter: if the mother is Jewish, the child is Jewish, full stop. Yet here is an opinion that this child is not merely Jewish, but Jewish and a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be emphasized at the outset that we do not rule this way. But the opinion exists in the Gemara, and its implications are striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, in the {\i dibur hamatchil} {\i kasavar}, takes up the challenge of explaining this opinion and raises a pointed difficulty. The first possible explanation is that this position follows Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view that {\i mamzerut} results from any violation of a negative prohibition \u8212- {\i mamzer mechayavei lavin}. A union with a slave carries a biblical {\i lav}, and thus the child would be a {\i mamzer} according to Rabbi Akiva. But the case of a non-Jewish man is more complicated. On the one hand, such a union is treated with the utmost gravity \u8212- communities would sit {\i shiva} for a child who intermarried \u8212- yet the technical biblical prohibition of sexual relations with a non-Jew may in fact be only rabbinic, at least when the non-Jew is not from one of the {\i shiva amamim}, the seven Canaanite nations. Relations in public invoke {\i kana\u8217'im pog\u8217'in bo}, as in the story of Pinchas \u8212- but that applies specifically to the public act. In private, if there is no biblical {\i lav}, why would the child be a {\i mamzer}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers one answer: perhaps the {\i mamzerut} is only {\i miderabbanan}. But he then develops a second, more penetrating answer, which reframes the entire question. Moving to the passage beginning {\i ve\u8217'im tomar}, Tosafot writes: granted that for the case of a slave there is a {\i lav}, making sense of {\i mamzerut} along Rabbi Akiva\u8217's lines; but for a non-Jew, where relations may be {\i bias heter} from a biblical standpoint in private \u8212- {\i mikol makom}, since {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect with a non-Jew ({\i keivan delo tafsi bah kiddushin havay mamzer, kemo be\u8217'eved}) \u8212- the child is a {\i mamzer} in a sense that has nothing to do with the weight of the transgression at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a fundamental reconceptualization of {\i mamzerut}. The standard understanding is that {\i mamzerut} results from a prohibited union \u8212- a {\i lav} or a {\i karet} relationship. But Tosafot here articulates an alternative framework: {\i mamzerut} may be a function not of the severity of the transgression but of the impossibility of {\i kiddushin} taking effect. In all cases of {\i issurei karet}, {\i kiddushin} does not take effect. Here too, with a non-Jew, {\i kiddushin} does not take effect \u8212- not because of a weighty transgression, but because the Torah does not recognize such a {\i kiddushin}. And in this sense, the union with a non-Jew resembles the {\i karet} category structurally, even if not in terms of the gravity of the sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framework also resolves an apparent paradox: a union that is {\i issur karet}, namely {\i nidda}, does not produce a {\i mamzer} \u8212- because {\i kiddushin} could take effect in that relationship and indeed may even be present. Conversely, a union with no biblical prohibition whatsoever can, according to this view, produce a {\i mamzer} \u8212- because {\i kiddushin} is categorically impossible. The determining factor is not transgression but the impossibility of marital bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again, we do not rule this way. The {\i halakhah} is that the child of a Jewish mother and a non-Jewish father is simply Jewish, without any taint of {\i mamzerut}. The practical stakes are significant: {\i mamzerut} cannot be solved, whereas questions of Jewish identity \u8212- say, where one parent was not Jewish \u8212- can be resolved through conversion. That said, it is worth noting that even according to our normative position, the question of {\i pagum le\u8217'kehunah} \u8212- whether such a child is {\i pasul} to marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- is not entirely without discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Patrilineal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An even more shocking possibility now emerges. The Gemara shifts to discussing the case of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav Assi: {\i oved kochavim shekidesh b\u8217'zman hazeh chosheshin l\u8217'kiddushin shema me\u8217'aseret shevatim hu} \u8212- a non-Jew who betroths a Jewish woman nowadays, we are concerned that the {\i kiddushin} may be binding, lest this person turns out to be a descendant of the ten tribes and is therefore Jewish. The Gemara asks: surely we follow the principle that anyone who separates from a group is assumed to have come from the majority \u8212- and the majority of non-Jews are genuinely non-Jews? The Gemara answers that the case is specifically in places where we know the ten tribes were exiled, citing Rabbi Abba bar Kahana\u8217's identification of the locations \u8212- {\i Chalach} is {\i Chalazon}, {\i Chavor} is {\i Chadyav}, {\i Nehar Gozan} is {\i Ginzak}, and {\i arei Madai} is {\i Chemdan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might assume that the concern about these people being descendants of the ten tribes is a concern about matrilineal descent \u8212- perhaps a great-great-great-grandmother all the way back through the maternal line was Jewish, and therefore the concern is that there is an unbroken chain of Jewish mothers. That is indeed one way of reading the Gemara, and it is ultimately what Tosafot concludes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rashi reads the Gemara differently, and his reading is genuinely startling. In the passage beginning {\i measeret shevatim hu} (toward the bottom of 17b), Rashi explains: {\i shenasu ovdot kochavim} \u8212- the Jewish men of the ten tribes married non-Jewish women, and Rashi then states: {\i dekasavar ovedet kochavim sheyalda mi\u8217'Yisrael havlad mamzer vechoshshin lekiddushei mamzer} \u8212- there is a view that the child born of a non-Jewish woman from a Jewish father is a {\i mamzer}, and we are concerned about the {\i kiddushin} of a {\i mamzer}. That is: Rashi understands the Gemara to be entertaining the possibility that patrilineal descent \u8212- Jewish father, non-Jewish mother \u8212- produces not a non-Jew, but a Jew who is a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot is understandably troubled. Multiple {\i mishnayot} establish clearly that Jewishness follows the mother, not the father. How can Rashi be reading this Gemara as suggesting patrilineal descent with {\i mamzerut}? Tosafot offers two resolutions. The first is that if one accepts the earlier principle \u8212- that a Jewish mother and non-Jewish father produce a {\i mamzer} because {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect \u8212- then perhaps by the same logic the mirror image holds: a Jewish father and non-Jewish mother would also produce a {\i mamzer}, because that union too admits no possibility of {\i kiddushin}. In that case, Rashi\u8217's reading is coherent, but only within the framework of that radical earlier opinion. Since we do not rule that way in the first case \u8212- we say the child is simply Jewish \u8212- we certainly do not say so in this direction either; we say the child is simply not Jewish. Tosafot\u8217's second resolution, which he treats as more straightforward, is that the concern is simply about matrilineal descent: perhaps these people are descendants of Jewish women from the ten tribes, and therefore the concern is an unbroken maternal line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result of this discussion is that two radical positions have now been articulated, both rejected in normative {\i halakhah}: first, that any union between a Jew and a non-Jew \u8212- regardless of which parent is Jewish \u8212- produces a child who is both Jewish and a {\i mamzer}; and second, that there is something akin to patrilineal descent, at least in the sense that the child retains Jewish identity from the father. We do not rule either way. We rule that a Jewish mother produces a Jewish child without any {\i mamzerut} issue, and a Jewish father with a non-Jewish mother produces a non-Jewish child \u8212- also without any {\i mamzerut} issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benot Yerushalayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the second explanation for the concern about Tarmod \u8212- {\i benot Yerushalayim}. The Gemara asks: {\i mai hi}? What is this about? Rav Yosef and the Rabbis debate, both in the name of Rabbah bar bar Chana. One says 12,000 men and 6,000 archers; the other says 18,000. The substance: when the non-Jews entered the Beit HaMikdash to destroy it at the time of the {\i churban}, everyone else went for the gold and silver, but this group \u8212- whether 12,000 or 18,000 men \u8212- {\i nitnu al benot Yerushalayim}, they went to seize the young women of Jerusalem. As the verse in Eichah says: {\i nashim be-Tziyon inu, betulot be-arei Yehudah} \u8212- the women of Jerusalem were subjected to assault, which is understood as referring to rape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halakhic} question is thus the same one we have already been examining: what is the status of children born from these unions of non-Jewish men and Jewish women? According to one view, entirely unproblematic \u8212- the children are Jewish. According to the radical opinion we have discussed, the children would be {\i mamzerim}. The concern about the descendants of these events thus explains why there was a question about accepting {\i geirim} from Tarmod, or indeed about the status of people from that region generally.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Enemy Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metatron Passage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara interpolates a {\i derasha} from Rav Shmuel bar Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yonatan: the verse {\i naar hayiti gam zakanti} \u8212- \u8220"I was a youth and now have grown old\u8221" \u8212- was said by the {\i sar ha\u8217'olam}, the steward or prince of the world. The strong inclination among commentators is to identify this figure with Metatron, understood as a supreme angelic being or perhaps something beyond that. The Gemara\u8217's reasoning is: if it were God who said {\i gam zakanti}, that is impossible \u8212- God would not say He has grown old. And if it were David who said it, David was not so old that the phrase {\i gam zakanti} would be appropriate. The conclusion is therefore {\i shema mina sar ha\u8217'olam amro}. The precise theological implications of this passage are a matter for deeper exploration; the Gemara itself moves quickly past it to the next and more legally significant {\i derasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefer Torah Burned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then states, again from Rav Shmuel bar Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yonatan: what is meant by the verse {\i yado paras tzar al kol machmadeha} \u8212- the hand of the enemy stretched out over all its precious things? {\i Zeh Amon u\u8217'Moav} \u8212- this refers to Ammon and Moab. When the non-Jews entered the {\i heichal} to destroy it, everyone else went for the gold and silver, but {\i heim nitnu al sefer Torah} \u8212- they, Ammon and Moab, turned to the Sefer Torah, for the Sefer Torah is {\i machmadeha}, the most precious and desirous thing of all. Why did they attack the Sefer Torah specifically? {\i Amru}: because it is written there, {\i lo yavo Amoni u\u8217'Moavi b\u8217'kehal Hashem} \u8212- Ammon and Moab may not enter the congregation of God. So {\i Amon u\u8217'Moav yisrofu ba\u8217'eish} \u8212- they burned it. The Maharsha points out a play on words: the word {\i paras} read backwards spells {\i saraf} \u8212- burned. There is also a verse in Eichah that reads {\i asher tzivita lo yavo\u8217'u b\u8217'kahalecha} \u8212- those whom You commanded shall not enter Your congregation \u8212- which in its plain sense means that enemies came into the Temple, the very ones excluded from the community. The {\i drasha} sharpens this: the specific enemy who burned the Sefer Torah was Ammon and Moab, precisely those excluded by the text they destroyed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ammon & Moab\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse in Eichah, {\i tzivah Hashem l\u8217'Yaakov sevivav tzarav} \u8212- God commanded for Jacob that enemies would surround it \u8212- is connected by Rav to cities such as Humanya and Pum Nahara. Rashi notes that Humanya was populated by Greeks and Pum Nahara by Jews, and the Greeks of that city were oppressive toward the Jewish residents. The broader theme is the same: the enemies of Israel and the problem of mixed ancestry and the intertwining of peoples at the time of the {\i churban} \u8212- Ammon and Moab being paradigmatic cases of peoples whose lineage was itself the product of illicit unions, and who were therefore excluded from the {\i kahal}, and who then sought to destroy the very text that excluded them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matrilineal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Tribes Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns explicitly to the issue of descendants of the ten tribes. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav Assi: {\i oved kochavim shekidesh b\u8217'zman hazeh chosheshin l\u8217'kiddushin shema me\u8217'aseret shevatim hu} \u8212- if a non-Jew betroths a Jewish woman in our time, we are concerned that his {\i kiddushin} might be binding, lest he is in fact a descendant of the ten tribes and therefore Jewish. The Gemara objects: do we not ordinarily say that anyone who separates from a group separates from the majority, and since most non-Jews are genuinely non-Jewish, there is no concern? The Gemara answers: {\i b\u8217'duchta d\u8217'kviei} \u8212- the concern applies specifically in places where the ten tribes are known to have been settled. The locations are identified by Rabbi Abba bar Kahana through a verse: {\i vayanaichem b\u8217'Chalach u\u8217'vChavor nehar Gozan v\u8217'arei Madai} \u8212- {\i Chalach} is {\i Chalazon}, {\i Chavor} is {\i Chadyav}, {\i Nehar Gozan} is {\i Ginzak}, and {\i arei Madai} is {\i Chemdan}. An alternative tradition identifies the area as Nehar and its surrounding cities, which Shmuel specifies as Moshchei, Chidkei, and Dumkaya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the question becomes more nuanced. If we are worried that a person might be a descendant of the ten tribes, what exactly are we picturing? Are we assuming that men and women of the ten tribes married only amongst themselves and descended in a pure line? Or are we allowing for intermarriage, in which case the concern would be about someone whose maternal great-great-great-grandmother was Jewish?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot ultimately explains that the concern tracks matrilineal descent: perhaps there is an unbroken chain of Jewish mothers, generation after generation, from the exiled Jewish women of the ten tribes through to the present day. That is the straightforward reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But as noted above, Rashi reads the Gemara differently \u8212- understanding the case as involving Jewish fathers with non-Jewish mothers, with the concern that such a child is Jewish and a {\i mamzer}. That reading is, as Tosafot observes, deeply difficult to reconcile with the standard {\i halakhah} established in numerous {\i mishnayot} that Jewish identity follows the mother. The resolution in Tosafot\u8217's first approach is that if one accepts the framework in which any Jew-plus-non-Jew union produces {\i mamzerut} (in either direction), then Rashi\u8217's reading is internally coherent \u8212- but since we reject that framework entirely, we are left with the simpler reading that the concern is about matrilineal descent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tradition continues: {\i ki amritah kamei d\u8217'Shmuel amar li} \u8212- when Rav Yehuda reported Rav Assi\u8217's ruling before Shmuel, Shmuel responded in the clearest possible terms. {\i Bincha haba min hayisraelit karuy bincha} \u8212- your son who comes from a Jewish woman is called your son. {\i Ve\u8217'ein bincha haba min ha\u8217'ovedet kochavim karuy bincha ella b\u8217'nah} \u8212- but your son who comes from a non-Jewish woman is not called your son; he is called her son. This is the definitive articulation of matrilineal descent. The child of a Jewish man and a non-Jewish woman is not Jewish \u8212- he is her son, not yours, in the sense that Jewish identity does not pass through you in that case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This response also clarifies why the earlier Gemara was concerned about non-Jews in those places: if the concern had been about Jewish fathers (as Rashi read), Shmuel now rules that the children of those Jewish fathers are simply not Jewish, and there is therefore no concern at all. If the non-Jews there are descended from Jewish men who married non-Jewish women, their children are not Jewish, period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scripture & Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to identify the scriptural basis for this principle. The relevant verse is in Devarim 7: {\i lo titchaten bam, bitcha lo titen livno u\u8217'vito lo tikach livnecha} \u8212- do not intermarry with them; do not give your daughter to his son, and do not take his daughter for your son. The structure of the verse is telling: the daughter always follows the man she is with. The reason given is {\i ki yasir et bincha me\u8217'achai} \u8212- because he will turn your son away from Me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The grammatical problem here is significant. {\i Ki yasir} is in the masculine \u8212- {\i he} will turn away. Who is the male who will turn your son away? If it were the non-Jewish daughter who leads your son astray, the verse should read {\i ki tasir et bincha}, using the feminine. If it were the non-Jewish man leading your daughter astray, it should read {\i ki yasir et bitcha}. But {\i ki yasir et bincha} \u8212- a male turning away your {\i son} \u8212- what does that mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis read it as follows: {\i bincha} here does not mean your son but your grandson. When a non-Jewish man marries a Jewish woman, that non-Jew will lead your grandson \u8212- who will be Jewish through the Jewish mother \u8212- away from God. That grandson is called {\i bincha} because he is indeed Jewish through his mother. By contrast, when a Jewish man\u8217's daughter marries a non-Jewish man\u8217's son, there is no such concern \u8212- because the child of that union (Jewish father, non-Jewish mother) follows the mother and is not Jewish, so there is no Jewish grandson to be led astray. The Torah therefore does not say {\i ki yasir et bincha} in that direction, because there is no {\i bincha} \u8212- no Jewish grandson \u8212- at stake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i pasuk} is the textual peg upon which the principle of matrilineal descent is hung. As the Gemara notes, that is a great deal to derive from a single {\i yud} \u8212- the difference between the masculine {\i yasir} and the feminine {\i tasir} \u8212- but that is indeed what the {\i drashah} is built upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina adds: {\i shema mina ben bitcha haba min oved kochavim karuy bincha} \u8212- we learn from this that the son of your daughter who was born from a non-Jewish man is still called your son. In other words, a Jewish daughter who has a child with a non-Jewish man produces a child who is Jewish \u8212- and that child is considered your descendant ({\i bincha}) through the maternal line. This is implicit in the Shmuel formula already stated, but Ravina makes it explicit: matrilineal descent means that your daughter\u8217's child is Jewish regardless of the father, and that child is in a real sense your son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Infertile Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the concern about the ten tribes could still be answered by saying: well, perhaps these people are descendants through an unbroken maternal line from Jewish women of the ten tribes \u8212- the Gemara closes that avenue with a tradition. {\i Gemiri} \u8212- we have received a tradition \u8212- {\i debanshei dehahu dara itztaruy itztaru} \u8212- the women of the generation exiled with the ten tribes became infertile; their wombs were somehow rendered incapable of bearing children. If that tradition is correct, then no descendants trace back to the Jewish women of the ten tribes. Any descendants of those exiled communities trace only through Jewish men, and since Jewish men with non-Jewish women produce non-Jewish children, all of those descendants are simply non-Jews. According to this Gemara, there are no remaining Jewish descendants of the ten tribes at all \u8212- a remarkable and sobering conclusion that stands in some tension with both popular imagination and certain {\i responsa} literature on communities like the Ethiopian Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Assimilation View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative version of the tradition is also recorded. Some say that when Rav Yehuda reported Rav Assi\u8217's ruling before Shmuel, Shmuel responded differently: {\i lo zazu misham ad she-asa\u8217'um ovdei kochavim gemurim} \u8212- they did not leave those places until they had been made into complete non-Jews. The verse cited is from Hoshea: {\i ba-Hashem bagadu ki banim zarim yaldu} \u8212- they betrayed God, for they bore foreign, non-Jewish children. Whether this verse refers to deliberate assimilation that caused the ten tribes to lose their Jewish status as a matter of communal or rabbinic ruling, or whether it is simply another way of expressing that all children born of those communities were non-Jewish by matrilineal reckoning, is not entirely clear. The listener who noted that the verse in Hoshea is prophetic rather than descriptive \u8212- that it depicts intermarriage before the exile rather than after \u8212- raises an interesting point about the original context of the verse, though its application here in the Gemara is as described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Either way, the upshot is the same: there is no viable concern that a non-Jew from those regions today is actually a Jewish descendant of the ten tribes. The door is closed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichus Cities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hamnuna Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara transitions to a series of vivid vignettes about cities of questionable lineage. Rav Hamnuna was sitting before Ulla, and Ulla was posing challenging questions in the course of their learning. Rav Hamnuna, impressed, exclaimed: {\i ma gavra} \u8212- what a man! And then, in what he presumably intended as a compliment with an unfortunate addendum: {\i u\u8217'ma gavra ilav deHarfanya ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- and how much greater this man would be if he were not from Harfanya. Apparently, Harfanya was a city known for its questionable {\i yichus} \u8212- not necessarily outright {\i mamzerut}, but lineage that was questioned enough that others would not want to intermarry with its residents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remark caused Ulla to be {\i ichsif} \u8212- embarrassed. Ulla then asked, with a play on the words {\i ichsif} and {\i kesef}: {\i kesef gulgulta le\u8217'heika yahavas} \u8212- where do you pay your poll tax? Rav Hamnuna replied that he paid it to Pum Nahara. Ulla immediately said: {\i im ken mi-Pum Nahara at} \u8212- in that case, you\u8217're from Pum Nahara. Since Pum Nahara was a Jewish city of unquestioned lineage \u8212- indeed, we encountered it earlier as a Jewish city oppressed by the neighboring Greek city of Humanya \u8212- Rav Hamnuna\u8217's payment of taxes there indicated that he was affiliated with Pum Nahara, not truly of Harfanya. The embarrassment was thereby mitigated: Rav Hamnuna was not casting aspersions on a resident of Harfanya; he was speaking about a city without reference to his interlocutor\u8217's actual community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harfanya City\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai Harfanya?} What does this city\u8217's name mean? Rav Zeira offers a {\i drash}: {\i har she-kol ponim bo} \u8212- a mountain, or city, that everyone turns toward, in the sense that all {\i psulim}, all those with questionable parentage, migrate there. When no one else will marry you, when your lineage is in doubt, you gravitate toward a community of similarly situated people. A {\i Beraisa} reinforces this: {\i kol she-ein makir mishpachto ve-shivto nifneh le-sham} \u8212- anyone who does not know his family or his tribe ends up there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking social portrait. Even outside the specific legal category of {\i mamzerut}, the intense focus on {\i yichus} and known ancestry meant that people with uncertain lineage were effectively shut out of normal communal life and marriage. They congregated in cities like Harfanya, creating communities defined by their exclusion from others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hierarchy of Defect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds a grim metaphor: {\i ve-hi amuka mi-she\u8217'ol} \u8212- Harfanya is deeper than the netherworld. For, as the verse says, {\i mi-yad she\u8217'ol efdem mi-mavet eg\u8217'alem} \u8212- from the hand of {\i she\u8217'ol}, from death itself, God can redeem. But the invalidity of {\i Harfanya} \u8212- meaning the {\i mamzerut} that ultimately characterizes its residents \u8212- has no remedy. The Gemara then traces the genealogy of the problem. The {\i psulim} of Harfanya came from those of Meshang; the {\i psulim} of Meshang came from those of Tarmod; and the {\i psulim} of Tarmod came from the servants of Solomon who had intermarried with Jewish women. And as the folk saying goes: {\i kava raba vekava zuta, minda ve\u8217'azla lish\u8217'ol} \u8212- a large measure and a small measure, rolling and going down to {\i she\u8217'ol}. From {\i she\u8217'ol} to Tarmod, from Tarmod to Meshang, from Meshang to Harfanya \u8212- everything rolls downhill, and each stop is further from any possibility of redemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The image is deliberately bleak, and yet one should perhaps hear in it something more than mere legal taxonomy. These are communities of human beings whose condition the Gemara regards with the gravity it deserves \u8212- a gravity that comes precisely from the irreversibility of their situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzeirut Irredeemable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara makes this explicit: {\i leit lei takanta} \u8212- there is no remedy. Unlike someone who is merely from a city of unknown {\i yichus}, or even someone caught in {\i she\u8217'ol}, the {\i mamzer} has no path out. The law of {\i mamzerut} is absolute and hereditary \u8212- a {\i mamzer} cannot marry into the congregation, and the status passes to subsequent generations. The Gemara acknowledges what the earlier discussion made clear: the Rabbis were deeply aware of this irreversibility and worked to construct frameworks \u8212- such as the rule that {\i safek mamzerim mutar} (those of doubtful {\i mamzer} status are permitted) \u8212- that could offer some mitigation. But those efforts did not change the fundamental reality that genuine {\i mamzerut} carried with it a permanent exclusion that no remedy could undo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage, taken together with everything that has preceded it on this {\i daf}, represents what the Gemara presents as a distillation of the major concerns of Masechet Yevamot beyond the specific laws of {\i yibum}: the definition of Jewish identity, the catastrophic consequences of {\i mamzerut}, the fierce communal concern with {\i yichus}, and the question of what happens to communities that have lived at the intersection of Jewish and non-Jewish worlds for generations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eshet Achivu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now opens the next {\i perek} with the {\i mishna} that asks: {\i keitzad eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo?} \u8212- what is the case of the wife of a brother who was not yet alive at the same time? The scenario requires careful tracking. Reuven and Shimon are brothers. Reuven marries Rachel. Reuven then dies. Subsequently, Levi is born. At this point, Shimon \u8212- who is still alive \u8212- performs {\i yibum} with Rachel: that is stage three. Then Shimon dies. Now Levi is the surviving brother. What is his relationship to Rachel?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rachel is {\i erva} to Levi \u8212- specifically, {\i eshet ach shelo haya be\u8217'olamo}, the wife of a brother who was not in existence at the same time as that brother. Because Levi was born after Reuven died, Levi and Reuven were never alive simultaneously, which means Levi has no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with Rachel; she is simply a brother\u8217's wife, and therefore {\i erva}. Shimon was married to Sarah, making Sarah {\i tzarat erva} \u8212- the co-wife of a woman who is {\i erva} \u8212- and therefore Sarah is also exempt from {\i yibum} and forbidden to Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rachel & Sarah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} therefore rules that Rachel \u8212- the first wife in this scenario \u8212- is excluded from {\i yibum} on the grounds of {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo}, and Sarah is excluded as her {\i tzarat erva}. Rachel stands as {\i erva} and Sarah as {\i tzarat erva}. Both are forbidden to Levi: Rachel by direct prohibition, and Sarah derivatively by virtue of being the co-wife of a woman who is {\i erva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} adds a further variation: what if Shimon performed {\i maamar} with Rachel \u8212- a quasi-betrothal, a rabbinic {\i kiddushin} intended as a precursor to full {\i yibum} \u8212- and then Shimon died before actually completing {\i yibum}? In this case, Rachel is not fully his wife even by rabbinic standard, and certainly not biblically. Biblically, Shimon was married only to Sarah, and the full obligation of {\i yibum} rests upon Sarah alone. Yet because Shimon performed {\i maamar} with Rachel, there is a quasi-marital bond to Rachel at a rabbinic level. Rachel therefore stands as something like a quasi-{\i erva} in the context of {\i yibum}, and Sarah becomes something like a quasi-{\i tzarat erva}. The practical consequence: Levi may not perform {\i yibum} with Sarah because of the quasi-{\i tzarat erva} concern, but he also cannot simply walk away, because his real biblical obligation is to Sarah. The resolution is {\i chalitza}: Sarah requires {\i chalitza} from Levi, but not {\i yibum}. Levi discharges his obligation through {\i chalitza} rather than marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Variants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes a textual discrepancy. Some versions of the {\i mishna} refer to Rachel as {\i rishona} \u8212- the first \u8212- while others refer to her as {\i shniya} \u8212- the second. Rav Nachman rules that neither version is an error: {\i man de\u8217'tani rishona lo mishtabesh, man de\u8217'tani shniya lo mishtabesh}. What does {\i rishona} mean? {\i Rishona l\u8217'nefilah} \u8212- she was the first to fall to {\i yibum}, in the sense that from the very beginning of this chain of events, the first person involved in the {\i yibum} process was Rachel (when Reuven died). And what does {\i shniya} mean? {\i Shniya b\u8217'nisuim} \u8212- not \u8220"second\u8221" in the ordinal sense, but the one who has been married twice: Rachel was married first to Reuven and subsequently taken into the household of Shimon through {\i yibum}. Sarah, by contrast, was married only once. So while {\i shniya} seems counterintuitive as a label for Rachel \u8212- she was involved from the beginning \u8212- it refers to the fact that she is the doubly-married woman in this scenario.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ki Yeshvu Achim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Living\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: {\i heicha ketiva?} \u8212- where in the Torah is the principle of {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} derived? Rav Yehuda answers: from the verse {\i ki yeshvu achim yachdav} \u8212- \u8220"if brothers dwell together.\u8221" The phrase {\i yeshvu yachdav} \u8212- dwell together \u8212- requires {\i shehayta lahem yeshiva achat ba\u8217'olam}: they must have had a period of simultaneous coexistence in this world. {\i Prat le\u8217'eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- this excludes the case where one brother was born only after the other had already died. The word {\i yachdav} is doing real halakhic work: it mandates contemporaneous existence as a precondition for the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} to apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternal Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same verse also teaches that only paternal brothers \u8212- not maternal brothers \u8212- are subject to {\i yibum}. The Gemara derives this from {\i yachdav} as well: {\i meyuchadim benachala} \u8212- they are unified by inheritance. Brothers who share a father inherit from one another; maternal half-brothers do not share inheritance. Therefore, {\i yachdav} excludes {\i achiv min ha\u8217'em} \u8212- a maternal half-brother \u8212- from the obligation of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba adds a parallel derivation: we learn the meaning of {\i achim} \u8212- brothers \u8212- in the context of {\i yibum} from the sons of Jacob, not from the case of Lot. Joseph\u8217's brothers say {\i shneim asar achim anachnu} \u8212- we are twelve brothers \u8212- and those brothers are all paternal brothers. Therefore, {\i achim} in {\i ki yeshvu achim yachdav} means paternal brothers. This derivation tracks: {\i ma lehalan min ha\u8217'av velo min ha\u8217'em, af kan min ha\u8217'av velo min ha\u8217'em} \u8212- just as there (by Jacob\u8217's sons) it means paternal brothers only, so here too.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yachdav Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: why not learn {\i achim} from the case of {\i arayot}, where the Torah uses the word {\i achicha} \u8212- your brother \u8212- and where that term is understood to include even a maternal half-brother? If {\i achicha} in the context of {\i arayot} includes maternal brothers, perhaps {\i achim} in the context of {\i yibum} should also include them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds: {\i danin achim me\u8217'achim ve\u8217'ein danin achim me\u8217'achicha} \u8212- we derive a rule using {\i achim} from another context that also uses {\i achim}, not from a context that uses {\i achicha}. The exact same word is a closer linguistic match.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pushes back: what difference does it make whether the word is {\i achim} or {\i achicha}? They are close enough. The Gemara answers with the principle of Rabbi Yishmael regarding {\i tzara\u8217'at}: where one verse says {\i veshav hakohen} and another says {\i uva hakohen}, the Rabbis link them despite the different words \u8212- {\i zo hi shiva, zo hi via} \u8212- because essentially the same act is being described. So too here, {\i achim} and {\i achicha} might be close enough to learn from one another. The Gemara\u8217's reply is: that principle applies only where there is no equally close match available. Here, we have {\i achim} appearing both in the context of Jacob\u8217's sons and in the context of {\i yibum}, which is a closer and more direct match. {\i Midi dedami lei yalpinan} \u8212- we derive from the closer analogue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Words Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: given all this, why was it necessary for the verse to write both {\i achim} and {\i yachdav}? Could not one word have done the work of both? The Gemara responds that each is necessary to prevent a misreading. If the Torah had written only {\i achim}, we might have derived the meaning of {\i achim} from the case of Lot \u8212- where {\i ki anashim achim anachnu} refers not to brothers but to relatives or even a nephew-uncle relationship. Perhaps {\i yibum} therefore applies even to a nephew? In that case, the problem of {\i erva} could theoretically be \u8220"solved\u8221" by limiting {\i yibum} to cases where there is no {\i erva} prohibition \u8212- i.e., only to a nephew, not a brother. The Gemara notes that this was not merely a hypothetical concern; certain non-rabbinic sects of the Second Temple period actually interpreted the Torah\u8217's law of {\i yibum} this way, holding that {\i achim} means relatives-but-not-brothers, and that {\i yibum} applies specifically where there is no {\i issur erva}. The Gemara therefore requires {\i yachdav} \u8212- which indicates shared inheritance \u8212- to clarify that {\i achim} here means brothers in the strict paternal sense, not relatives more broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And if the Torah had written only {\i yachdav}, we might have thought that the {\i mitzvah} requires brothers who share {\i both} a father and a mother \u8212- i.e., full brothers only. The word {\i achim} (interpreted in light of Jacob\u8217's sons, all of whom were paternal brothers but had different mothers) establishes that maternal relationship is irrelevant; paternal brothers suffice even without a shared mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum & Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's explanation for why {\i yachdav} signals inheritance \u8212- {\i yibum banachala tala Rachmana} \u8212- is genuinely striking. The ordinarily cited rationale for {\i yibum} is {\i yakum al shem achiv hamet} \u8212- to establish the name of the deceased brother. Yet the Gemara here understands that part of the essential structure of {\i yibum} is tied to inheritance: the {\i yavam} takes over the brother\u8217's {\i nachala} and, by implication, is responsible for perpetuating the brother\u8217's line through inheritance. Since inheritance flows through the father, the relevant fraternal relationship for {\i yibum} is similarly paternal. Maternal brothers have no shared {\i nachala} and therefore fall outside the {\i yibum} framework. This is not a peripheral point; it reveals something about the Gemara\u8217's underlying understanding of what {\i yibum} is for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Closest Relationship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final {\i hava amina} rounds out the discussion. One might have thought to limit the application of {\i yibum} \u8212- which does override the ordinary {\i erva} prohibition of a brother\u8217's wife \u8212- to only the very closest possible fraternal relationship: brothers who share both a father and a mother. The logic would be: {\i yibum} is a {\i chiddush}, an exceptional override of an {\i erva} prohibition. Since it is exceptional, perhaps we should require the absolute closest relationship \u8212- full brothers \u8212- before we invoke this exceptional permission. Therefore, we might have required {\i yachdav} to mean sharing both father and mother. The verse\u8217's use of {\i achim} (from Jacob\u8217's sons, who were paternal but not all maternal brothers) teaches us that this is not required \u8212- paternal alone is sufficient for {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ironically, the Gemara also notes that one sect resolved the tension between {\i yibum} and {\i erva} by going in the opposite direction \u8212- applying {\i yibum} only to a nephew rather than a brother, thereby eliminating the {\i erva} issue entirely. The Gemara rejects this reading entirely, but it is worth registering that the tension the Gemara is grappling with \u8212- the apparent collision between the obligation of {\i yibum} and the prohibition of {\i eshet ach} \u8212- was recognized as a genuine puzzle that attracted competing solutions in Jewish antiquity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 16b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixed Marriages & Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chaggai & Tarmod\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 16b continues from where the previous daf left off. Two testimonies were attributed to {\i Chaggai HaNavi}: first, that {\i tzarat habat} is {\i muteret}, in accordance with the position of {\i Beit Hillel}; and second, that {\i geirim} may be accepted from the inhabitants of Tarmod and Karduyim. That second point implies that those inhabitants are presumed to be non-Jews \u8212- as distinct from a concern that they might be Jews afflicted with {\i mamzerus}. The significance of that distinction is considerable: {\i mamzerus} cannot be resolved, whereas non-Jewish identity can be addressed through conversion. The Gemara had already noted a contradiction in the statements of Rabbi Yochanan regarding whether {\i geirim} could be accepted from Tarmod, and it is precisely that tension the Gemara now moves to resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- what is the concern with Tarmod in the first place? {\i Pligi beih Rabbi Yochanan v\u8217'Savia} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan and Savia, an exceedingly rare {\i Amora}, debate the matter. One holds the concern is {\i mishum avdei Shlomo} \u8212- because the non-Jewish servants of King Solomon had intermarried with Jewish women from that region. The other holds the concern is {\i mishum bnos Yerushalayim} \u8212- because of what was done to the young women of Jerusalem at the time of the {\i Churban Beit HaMikdash}. In both cases, the immediate question is: if there is a Jewish mother, what is the problem? If {\i yichus} follows the mother, these descendants should simply be Jewish. Why should {\i mamzerus} even enter the picture?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avdei Shlomo Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara itself acknowledges the tension: {\i bishlama l\u8217'man d\u8217'amar mishum avdei Shlomo} \u8212- the concern about the servants of Solomon is intelligible if one holds the view that {\i kasavar oveid kochavim v\u8217'eved haba al bat Yisrael havlad mamzer} \u8212- that a non-Jew or a slave who has relations with a Jewish woman produces a {\i mamzer}. This is, to put it plainly, a striking position. The conventional understanding, which is also the normative ruling, is that {\i matrilineal descent} governs: the child of a Jewish mother is Jewish, full stop. Yet here is a view that the child is not merely Jewish, but specifically a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logical mechanism driving this view requires explanation, and {\i Tosafot} provides it in a careful and layered analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} in the {\i dibur hamatchil} of {\i kasavar} first proposes that this position follows Rabbi Akiva\u8217's opinion that a child born from a union prohibited by a {\i lav} \u8212- a simple negative commandment \u8212- carries the status of {\i mamzer}. This is the principle of {\i mamzer m\u8217'chiyuvei lavin}. That reasoning works more naturally for the case of an {\i eved}, where at least some {\i pasuk} can be read to establish a biblical {\i lav} against relations with a slave. But the case of a non-Jew is more complicated. Counterintuitive as it may seem given the profound sociological weight attached to intermarriage \u8212- parents historically sitting {\i shiva}, communities ostracizing such unions \u8212- the technical prohibition of relations with a non-Jew may in fact be only {\i d\u8217'rabanan}, at least where the non-Jew is not from the seven Canaanite nations. The famous principle of {\i kana\u8217'im pog\u8217'im bo}, applied in the episode of Pinchas, operates only when the act is committed publicly; in private, the biblical prohibition may not exist. So {\i Tosafot} asks: if the act itself is not biblically forbidden, why would the child be a {\i mamzer}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} offers one resolution: perhaps the {\i mamzer} status here is only {\i mid\u8217'rabanan}. But then {\i Tosafot} advances a second and more conceptually significant answer. The relevant passage begins approximately a third of the way into the line starting with {\i al bat Yisrael havlad mamzer v\u8217'al korchacha Rabbi Akiva hi}, at the words {\i v\u8217'im tomar}. There, {\i Tosafot} writes: {\i bishlema me-eved havai mamzer d\u8217'v\u8217'eved ika lav ela me-oved kochavim amai havai mamzer} \u8212- granted that a slave produces a {\i mamzer} because there is a {\i lav}, but a non-Jew, why should that produce a {\i mamzer}? One answer the Gemara suggests is a rabbinic enactment \u8212- {\i Beis Din shel Shem gazru}. And then: {\i u\u8217'me-d\u8217'oraisa bias heter hi} \u8212- biblically, the act may actually be entirely permitted in private. Yet despite that, {\i Tosafot} continues: {\i v\u8217'yesh lomar}, one can say, even though the act is technically permitted {\i me-d\u8217'oraisa} in private, {\i keivan de-lo tafsi bah kiddushin havai mamzer k\u8217'mo b\u8217'eved} \u8212- since {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect in such a union, the child is a {\i mamzer}, just as in the case of a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the crucial insight. The standard framework for {\i mamzerus} links it to the weight of transgression \u8212- {\i issurei kares} or {\i issurei lavin} according to Rabbi Akiva. What {\i Tosafot} is pointing to here is an entirely different axis: not the weight of the prohibition, but the impossibility of {\i kiddushin}. In the case of {\i issurei kares}, {\i kiddushin ein tofsin} \u8212- the betrothal does not take effect. The same is true, {\i Tosafot} argues, with a non-Jew, even absent any biblical prohibition: there is simply no capacity for the {\i Torah} to recognize {\i kiddushin} in such a union. The {\i mamzer} status, on this reading, is not a consequence of having violated a prohibition; it is a consequence of having produced a child from a union in which the marital bond is structurally impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reframing has a striking implication: a child born from relations with a {\i niddah} \u8212- which is unquestionably an {\i issur kares} \u8212- is not a {\i mamzer}, precisely because {\i kiddushin} is possible with a {\i niddah} and may even be present simultaneously. Conversely, a child born from a union with a non-Jew \u8212- where there may be no biblical prohibition at all \u8212- is a {\i mamzer} on this view, because {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect. The defining characteristic of {\i mamzerus}, on this approach, may have less to do with the severity of transgression and more to do with the structural incapacity for a recognized marital bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It bears emphasis that we do not rule in accordance with this position. The normative {\i halakha} is that the child of a Jewish mother and a non-Jewish father is simply Jewish, without any taint of {\i mamzerus}. At most, there may be questions regarding {\i pagum l\u8217'kehuna} \u8212- the fitness of such a child to marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- and that is a genuinely contested issue that arises in contemporary cases involving children of Jewish mothers and non-Jewish fathers. But the underlying concern of {\i mamzerus} itself is not applied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnos Yerushalayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then turns to the second opinion \u8212- that the problem with Tarmod stems from {\i bnos Yerushalayim}. The {\i meimra} of Rav Yosef and the {\i rabbanan}, both in the name of Rabbah bar bar Channah, describes what occurred when the nations entered the {\i Heichal}. The debate between Rav Yosef and {\i rabbanan} is relatively minor \u8212- one says twelve thousand men and six thousand archers, the other says twelve thousand men among whom were six thousand archers, amounting to either twelve thousand or eighteen thousand total. What matters is the act they committed: {\i hakol nifnu al kesef vezahav} \u8212- everyone else rushed for the gold and the silver, {\i vehem nifnu al bnos Yerushalayim} \u8212- this group turned specifically to the young women of Jerusalem. The verse invoked is {\i nashim beTzion inu, betulot b\u8217'arei Yehuda} \u8212- the women of Zion were violated, the young women of Judah\u8217's cities. The plain meaning of {\i inu} here is rape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The halachic consequence is structurally identical to the {\i avdei Shlomo} scenario: in both cases we are dealing with offspring of Jewish women and non-Jewish men. According to the view that such unions produce {\i mamzerim} \u8212- whether on the basis of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's principle regarding {\i chiyuvei lavin} or on the basis of {\i kiddushin ein tofsin} \u8212- the descendants in Tarmod would be {\i mamzerim} and could not simply convert. According to the normative view, which does not recognize {\i mamzerus} in such cases, the descendants would simply be Jewish and there would be no bar to their accepting {\i geirim} from there, since there would be no one who requires conversion at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefer Torah Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to a related {\i drasha} on the verse {\i yado parash tzar al kol machmadeha} \u8212- the enemy stretched out his hand over all her precious things. Rav Shmuel bar Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yonatan identifies {\i tzar} here as Amon and Moav. When the non-Jews entered the {\i Heichal}, everyone else went for the gold and silver; Amon and Moav turned to the {\i Sefer Torah}. They declared: this is what it is written in \u8212- {\i lo yavo Amoni Moavi bi-kehal Hashem}, that we may not enter the congregation of God \u8212- and so they burned it. The {\i Maharsha} notes that the word {\i paras} read backwards yields {\i saras}, which is part of the wordplay operative in this passage. Additionally, the verse in {\i Eicha} states {\i asher tzivita lo yavo\u8217'u bi-kehalekha} \u8212- the enemies entered the Temple, the very enemies whom You commanded shall not enter Your congregation \u8212- and the {\i drasha} picks up on that resonance: those excluded from the {\i kahal} attacked the {\i Sefer Torah} that excluded them. The {\i machmada}, the most precious thing, was the {\i Sefer Torah} itself, and that is what Amon and Moav destroyed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matrilineal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Patrilineal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a statement that, if anything, is even more striking. Rav Yehuda reports in the name of Rav Assi: {\i oveid kochavim she-kideish bi-zman ha-zeh, chosheshin le-kiddushin} \u8212- a non-Jew who betroths a Jewish woman nowadays, we are concerned that the betrothal may be binding. Why? {\i Shema me-aseret ha-shevatim hu} \u8212- perhaps this non-Jew is actually a descendant of the ten tribes, and therefore may in fact be Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately objects: {\i kol de-parish me-rubah parish} \u8212- the principle is that anyone who separates from a group is assumed to come from the majority. The majority of non-Jews are genuinely non-Jews. Why should we entertain this concern? The Gemara responds: {\i be-duchta de-kavia} \u8212- this applies specifically in a place where the ten tribes are known to have been settled. In such a location, one cannot rely on {\i rov}, because the very composition of the population is in question. The biblical source for the locations of the exile is cited: Rav Abba bar Kahana identifies the places of the Assyrian exile \u8212- {\i Chalach, Chavor, nehar Gozan, and arei Madai} \u8212- with the identifiable cities of {\i Chalazon, Chadyav, Ginzak, and Chemdan} respectively. A second tradition, cited as {\i ve-ika d\u8217'amri}, gives an alternative set of locations centered around {\i Nihar} and its surrounding cities, which Shmuel identifies as {\i Mushkhei, Chidki, and Dumkaya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now one must pause and ask: in what way might the descendants of the ten tribes still be Jewish? The ten tribes were exiled centuries earlier, and obviously intermarriage with the surrounding non-Jewish populations would have occurred extensively. If we are concerned that some of these apparent non-Jews carry Jewish ancestry, we are implicitly concerned about a matrilineal chain \u8212- some great-great-grandmother, several generations back, who was Jewish. That is the straightforward reading, and it is the reading that {\i Tosafot} ultimately endorses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i Rashi} reads it differently, and his reading is remarkable. On the words {\i shema me-aseret ha-shevatim hu}, approximately six lines from the bottom of 16b, {\i Rashi} explains: {\i shenasu ovdot kochavim} \u8212- the Jewish men there married non-Jewish women. That is, the concern is not about a matrilineal Jewish grandmother but about a patrilineal Jewish grandfather. And {\i Rashi} continues: {\i ve-kasvar de-ovedet kochavim she-yaldah mi-Yisrael ha-velad mamzer ve-chosheshin le-kiddushei mamzer} \u8212- the opinion being operated with here is that a child born of a non-Jewish mother and a Jewish father is a {\i mamzer}, and therefore we are concerned that the {\i kiddushin} with this apparent non-Jewish man might be {\i kiddushei mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an astonishing move. {\i Rashi} is reading the Gemara as operating with an assumption of patrilineal Jewish identity \u8212- the child of a Jewish father and a non-Jewish mother is, on this view, still Jewish, and indeed a {\i mamzer}. One might think this opens some door: there is a Talmudic opinion, apparently, that patrilineal descent confers Jewish identity. But as will become immediately clear, that is precisely the wrong conclusion to draw. If this view says the child is a {\i mamzer}, then patrilineal descent, on this reading, is worse than no descent at all \u8212- a {\i mamzer} cannot be remedied, whereas a non-Jew can convert.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Rejects Patrilin\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} reacts with considerable force. In the {\i dibur hamatchil} beginning {\i oved kochav she-kidesh be-zman ha-zeh}, {\i Tosafot} writes: {\i peirush be-kuntres de-kasvar ovedet kochavim she-yaldah mi-Yisrael ha-velad mamzer ve-chosheshin le-kidushei mamzer} \u8212- so {\i Rashi} has explained it. But {\i Tosafot} objects: {\i u-teimah de-ve-kama mishnayot muchach de-ein ha-yachas ela achareha} \u8212- it is demonstrated from numerous {\i Mishnayot} that lineage follows the mother, not the father. When the father is Jewish and the mother is not Jewish, the child is not Jewish. The evidence from across {\i Shas} is overwhelming, and {\i Tosafot} marshals it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then attempts to defuse the difficulty: {\i ve-nireh de-lo kashya kol kach de-ha nami mishnah hi savira lei de-oved kochav ve-eved ha-ba al bat Yisrael ha-velad kasher} \u8212- the same position that says a non-Jewish man with a Jewish woman produces a {\i kasher} Jew would also say, in the mirror image, that a Jewish man with a non-Jewish woman produces a {\i kasher} child \u8212- just not Jewish. That is, if you accept the normative view that the Jewish-mother case produces a straightforward Jew with no {\i mamzer} issue, then logically you accept that the Jewish-father case produces a straightforward non-Jew with no {\i mamzer} issue either. {\i Ve-hu ha-din ipcha} \u8212- the same logic applies in reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i Tosafot} also explains what {\i Rashi\u8217's} reading would entail if followed through: {\i de-Rav Assi savar ke-Rabbi Akiva de-amar ein kiddushin tofsim be-chayvei lavin ve-ha-velad mamzer} \u8212- Rav Assi operates with Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect with {\i chiyuvei lavin} and the child is a {\i mamzer}. If that were true in the case of a Jewish mother, {\i ve-hu ha-din} it would be true in the mirror image \u8212- the child of a Jewish father and non-Jewish mother would also be a {\i mamzer}. The two cases rise and fall together. Since we do not rule like this in the Jewish-mother case \u8212- the child is simply Jewish \u8212- we correspondingly do not apply it in the Jewish-father case \u8212- the child is simply non-Jewish. There is no {\i mamzer} in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot\u8217's} final word on the matter is contained in the passage beginning with the word {\i Yehuda}: {\i ve-nireh de-tameih de-Rabbi Yehuda amar Rav Assi mishum banot} \u8212- the real reason for Rav Assi\u8217's concern is {\i mishum banot}, because of the Jewish women, that is, the concern is about a matrilineal chain through the Jewish mothers of the ten tribes, not through the Jewish fathers. This is the reading {\i Tosafot} ultimately endorses, and it is the reading consistent with the universal {\i halakhic} principle of matrilineal descent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the two radical possibilities that have now emerged: one is that a Jewish mother combined with a non-Jewish father produces a {\i mamzer} \u8212- not on account of a biblical transgression, but because {\i kiddushin ein tofsin}. The other, following {\i Rashi\u8217's} reading, is that a Jewish father combined with a non-Jewish mother also produces a {\i mamzer} rather than a non-Jew. {\i Tosafot} shows that these two positions are interdependent: they stand or fall together, since they rest on the same underlying logic about the impossibility of {\i kiddushin}. The normative ruling rejects both: Jewish mother means Jewish child, Jewish father and non-Jewish mother means non-Jewish child, and {\i mamzerus} is not in play in either scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is delivered by Shmuel himself. Rav Yehuda reports: when I stated this ruling before Shmuel, he said to me \u8212- {\i bincha haba min ha-Yisraelis kari bincha, v\u8217'ein bincha haba min ha-ovedet kochavim kari bincha ela bino} \u8212- your son who comes from a Jewish woman is called your son; the child who comes from a non-Jewish woman is not called your son, but her son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the classical formulation of matrilineal descent in its most direct and unambiguous form. The child of a Jewish woman, regardless of who the father is, follows the mother and is Jewish. The child of a non-Jewish woman, regardless of who the father is, follows the mother and is not Jewish. The asymmetry is total and admits no qualification. This statement also retroactively explains why {\i Rashi} read the earlier Gemara the way he did: the Gemara was operating with an assumption that would be flatly denied by Shmuel, and Shmuel\u8217's ruling is precisely the rejection of that assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One observation raised in discussion is worth noting here. It was pointed out that Shmuel\u8217's own daughters were reportedly kidnapped and violated, with the question of the status of their children being one that Shmuel himself would have faced with personal acuity. That biographical context lends an additional layer of significance to Shmuel\u8217's authoritative ruling on precisely this issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Yasir Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the ruling, the Gemara provides its biblical anchor. The relevant verse is in {\i Devarim} 7, in the passage beginning {\i v\u8217'lo titchaten bam} \u8212- do not intermarry with them. The {\i pasuk} reads: {\i bitcha lo titein livno u-vito lo tikach l\u8217'vanecha} \u8212- do not give your daughter to his son, and do not take his daughter for your son. Note the structure: the daughter always follows the man she goes to. Now the reason given is {\i ki yasir et bincha me\u8217'acharai} \u8212- for he will turn your son away from Me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a grammatical puzzle here. {\i Ki yasir} is masculine \u8212- \u8220"he will turn.\u8221" But who is the \u8220"he\u8221" who will turn \u8220"your son\u8221"? If the concern is about your daughter marrying a non-Jewish man, then {\i he} \u8212- the non-Jewish man \u8212- will take your daughter away. But it says {\i yasir et bincha}, \u8220"will turn your son.\u8221" Why would the non-Jewish man turn your son away?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbinic reading resolves this by understanding {\i bincha} not as your immediate son but as your grandson. The structure is: do not give your daughter to his son \u8212- because the non-Jewish husband ({\i yasir}, masculine) will turn your grandson ({\i bincha}, meaning your daughter\u8217's child) away from God. And that child is {\i bincha}, is called {\i your} (grand)son \u8212- meaning he is Jewish \u8212- because the mother is Jewish. By contrast, the case of taking a non-Jewish woman for your son carries no such warning, because in that case your grandchild through your son is not Jewish to begin with. There is no grandson to lead astray.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This derivation hangs on the word {\i yasir} \u8212- masculine, referring to the non-Jewish man taking your daughter \u8212- and the object {\i bincha} \u8212- the product of that union who is nonetheless still your Jewish (grand)son. The child of a Jewish daughter and a non-Jewish man is Jewish; the child of a Jewish son and a non-Jewish woman is not Jewish. That is the halakha, and this is the verse from which it is derived. The whole edifice of matrilineal descent rests, as the Gemara understands it, on this single grammatical turn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After Shmuel\u8217's ruling has established that descent from a non-Jewish mother renders a child non-Jewish regardless of paternal Jewish lineage, {\i Ravina} raises the obvious follow-up question. The Gemara\u8217's argument for dismissing concern about the inhabitants of the exiled-tribes\u8217' cities was that if Jewish fathers intermarried with non-Jewish women, the children would simply be non-Jewish \u8212- no cause for concern. But what about the reverse? What about the Jewish women of the ten tribes? Surely some of them also remained and had children. Those children, born of Jewish mothers, would be Jewish by matrilineal descent. And their descendants, several generations later, might still carry a matrilineal Jewish identity even while appearing fully assimilated into the surrounding non-Jewish population. Should we not be concerned that the apparent non-Jew in one of those cities is actually the matrilineal descendant of a Jewish woman from the ten tribes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina} makes this point explicit: {\i shma mina, ben bitcha haba min ha-oved kochavim kari bincha} \u8212- we derive from the previous ruling that the son of your daughter, who had relations with a non-Jewish man, is called your son. That child is Jewish. So the concern about matrilineal descent from the women of the ten tribes is real and pressing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Infertility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara provides a definitive answer: {\i Gemiri, de-b\u8217'nesha de-hadora ishtaruei ishtaru} \u8212- we have a received tradition that the women of that generation \u8212- the women of the ten tribes who were exiled \u8212- experienced some form of rupture or infertility. {\i Rashi} understands this to mean that their wombs were somehow burst, rendering them unable to bear children. As a result, there are no matrilineal descendants of the ten tribes whatsoever. Any descendants that exist come only through the Jewish men, not the Jewish women \u8212- and since the Jewish men intermarried with non-Jewish women, those descendants are all non-Jewish. There is, therefore, no living person with a matrilineal claim to Jewish identity through the women of the ten tribes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking and theologically loaded {\i gemara}. If taken at face value, it means that the ten tribes are genuinely and irrevocably lost \u8212- not merely assimilated, but without any surviving matrilineal line. Various eschatological traditions posit the return of the ten tribes at the end of days; this {\i gemara} would seem to complicate such a vision considerably. The identification of, for example, the Ethiopian Jewish community as descendants of {\i shevet Dan} by the {\i Radbaz} raises the question of how he navigated this tradition \u8212- whether he understood it narrowly, as applying only to those specific exiled populations, or in some other way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative version of Shmuel\u8217's response to the same question is also cited: {\i lo zazu misham ad she-asam ovdei kochavim gemurim} \u8212- they did not leave there until they had been rendered completely non-Jewish. Whether this means a {\i halakhic} ruling was issued declaring them non-Jews, or whether it reflects the factual claim that all remaining descendants were patrilineal only, is not entirely clear. The verse cited in support is from {\i Hoshea}: {\i ba-Hashem bagadu ki banim zarim yaldu} \u8212- they betrayed God, for they bore foreign children. This could be read as describing deep assimilation destroying Jewish identity in a more diffuse sense, or as a restatement of the factual claim that all children born were non-Jewish because all were born of non-Jewish mothers.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cities of Defect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarmod Destruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now shifts to a vivid scene of {\i amora\u8217'ic} discourse. Rav Yosef sat behind Rav Kahana, who sat in front of Rav Yehuda in the {\i beis midrash}. Rav Yehuda was teaching: {\i asidin Yisrael de-avdi yoma tava ki charvi Tarmod} \u8212- the Jewish people will celebrate a holiday when Tarmod is destroyed. The basis for this sentiment is the very material we have been studying: there was a persistent concern about the population of Tarmod, whether they were {\i mamzerim} or non-Jews, and according to {\i Rashi} they were regarded as {\i mamzerim} who were seeping into the Jewish community through intermarriage. The prospect of that city\u8217's elimination would be cause for celebration \u8212- though one might charitably understand \u8220"destroyed\u8221" to mean dispersed rather than anything more severe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarmod Persistent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds with a practical challenge: {\i ha-charuv?} \u8212- but it has already been destroyed! Rav Yehuda distinguishes: the city that was destroyed was {\i Tamod}, not {\i Tarmod}; they are different cities. The Gemara, however, objects that we have said elsewhere these are in fact the same city. The answer is that the city was rebuilt: {\i ichpulei d\u8217'ichpal} \u8212- it redoubled. {\i Chariv m\u8217'hai gisa, yativ m\u8217'hai gisa} \u8212- if one side of the city is destroyed, the other side is built up, and vice versa. Tarmod is persistent in the way the {\i tzinim u-pachim} of the {\i Torah} are persistent \u8212- a recurring affliction that does not disappear. And so Rav Yehuda\u8217's hope for its eventual destruction remains very much aspirational.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harfanya Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna sat before Ulla, engaged in {\i halacha}. Ulla was posing difficult questions, and Rav Hamnuna was struggling with them. Rav Hamnuna then remarked admiringly \u8212- though the remark was actually made about him, by Ulla: {\i mah gavra} \u8212- what a great person this is! {\i Mah gavra ila d\u8217'maharfanya mah avvad} \u8212- imagine how great he would be if he were not from Harfanya, if his lineage were not pulled down by his connection to that problematic city. Harfanya, like Tarmod, was a city with questions of {\i yichus}, though the concern there appears to be not {\i mamzerus} specifically but rather genealogical uncertainty more broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Upon hearing this, Rav Hamnuna was embarrassed. Ulla sought to reassure him. He asked: {\i kesaf gulgalta l\u8217'heicha yahavat} \u8212- where do you pay your poll tax? Rav Hamnuna replied: to {\i Pum Nahara}. Just as an American living abroad might still file taxes with the United States, Rav Hamnuna, though residing in Harfanya, was registered as a subject of {\i Pum Nahara} \u8212- and we noted earlier that {\i Pum Nahara} was identified as a Jewish city. Ulla therefore concluded: {\i im ken mi-Pum Nahara at} \u8212- if so, you are really from {\i Pum Nahara}, not from Harfanya. His lineage is intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harfanya Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then explains the name {\i Harfanya} through a form of {\i drash}. Rav Zeira says: {\i har she-hakol ponim bo} \u8212- a mountain toward which everyone turns, meaning everyone who has been excluded elsewhere, anyone with questionable parentage, converges there. The {\i Beraita} reinforces this: {\i kol she-ein makir mishpachto v\u8217'shivto nifne l\u8217'sham} \u8212- whoever cannot identify his family and tribal lineage goes to that city, because no one else will have him. This is a striking sociological observation embedded in the Talmudic text. Even setting aside the formal legal category of {\i mamzerus}, the community\u8217's insistence on {\i yichus} \u8212- knowing with certainty one\u8217's genealogical origins \u8212- was so acute that those who lacked such clarity were socially marginalized and effectively driven to settle among others in the same situation. This heightened concern with {\i yichus} will prove to be one of the central recurring themes of {\i Masechet Yevamot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Worse Than Sheol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then delivers a sharp epigram: {\i v\u8217'hi amuka mi-she\u8217'ol} \u8212- Harfanya is deeper than {\i Sheol}, the netherworld. His source is the verse: {\i mi-yad she\u8217'ol efdem mi-mavet eg\u8217'alem} \u8212- from the hand of {\i Sheol} I will redeem them, from death I will ransom them. The logic is painful in its clarity: those in {\i Sheol} can be redeemed. Those afflicted with the {\i psulot} of Harfanya cannot be redeemed; their disqualification is more permanent than death itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defect Chain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then traces a cascade of genealogical defect that flows from one city to the next. The {\i psulim} of Harfanya came from the {\i psulim} of Meshan. The {\i psulim} of Meshan came from the {\i psulim} of Tarmod. The {\i psulim} of Tarmod came from the servants of Solomon \u8212- {\i mishum avdei Shlomo} \u8212- the non-Jewish slaves who had intermarried with Jewish women at the beginning of this entire discussion. And the folk saying captures this cascading descent: {\i kava rabba v\u8217'kava zuta, minder ve-azil li-she\u8217'ol, u-mi-she\u8217'ol le-Tarmod, u-mi-Tarmod le-Meshan, u-mi-Meshan le-Harfanya} \u8212- a large measure and a small measure rolling downhill, from {\i Sheol} to Tarmod to Meshan to Harfanya \u8212- each stage more irredeemable than the last.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One cannot help but sense, beneath this imagery, the human reality it describes: communities of people, generation after generation, for whom there was no return, no remedy, no reintegration into the broader Jewish community. {\i Chazal} were deeply aware of this, and their efforts to introduce leniencies \u8212- the principle of {\i safek mamzer mutar}, and the many {\i halakhic} mechanisms for dissolving {\i mamzerus} concerns \u8212- reflect their discomfort with this severity. But those leniencies did not erase the underlying social reality in which {\i yichus} functioned as a form of communal gatekeeping, and those without it were effectively exiled to cities of the rejected.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brother Not Alive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four-Stage Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the words {\i hadran alach chamisha asar nashim}, the {\i perek} concludes and the next one begins. The opening Mishnah asks: {\i keitzad eshet achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo} \u8212- how does the case of a brother\u8217's wife who was not alive at the same time as the deceased brother work? The scenario is built in four stages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stage one: Reuven and Shimon are alive. Reuven marries Rachel. Reuven then dies. Stage two: Levi is born. Because Levi was not alive when Reuven was alive \u8212- they never shared existence in this world \u8212- Levi has no {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum} with Rachel. For him, Rachel is simply his brother\u8217's wife, which is an {\i erva}. Stage three: Shimon, who was alive at the same time as Reuven, does {\i yibum} with Rachel. He is already married to Sarah. So now Shimon is married to both Sarah and Rachel. Stage four: Shimon dies. Now the question of Levi\u8217's obligations comes into full focus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ervah & Tzarat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's ruling is as follows. {\i Ha-rishona} \u8212- Rachel, the first woman \u8212- {\i yotzeis mishum eshet achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo} \u8212- is exempt from {\i yibum} with Levi because she is an {\i erva} to him, being the wife of a brother he never coexisted with. {\i Ve-ha-shniya mishum tzarasah} \u8212- Sarah, the second woman, is exempt because she is the {\i tzarat erva}: the co-wife of an {\i erva} is herself exempt from {\i yibum} just as the {\i erva} herself is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah continues with a further complication. Suppose Shimon did not actually perform {\i yibum} with Rachel but instead performed {\i maamar} \u8212- the rabbinic act of betrothal intended as a preliminary step toward {\i yibum}, which creates a quasi-{\i kiddushin} status for the {\i yevama} \u8212- and then he died. The halachic picture shifts. {\i De\u8217'oraita}, Shimon was fully and biblically married only to Sarah. Rachel was not biblically his wife; she was simply the {\i yevama} with whom he had performed a rabbinic betrothal. Biblically, the {\i yibum} obligation rests entirely with Sarah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet because of the {\i maamar}, Rachel has a quasi-marital status vis-\u224 ?-vis Shimon. That means that from Levi\u8217's perspective, Rachel is something of a quasi-{\i erva} \u8212- not a full biblical {\i erva}, since Shimon\u8217's marriage to her was only rabbinic, but sufficiently complicated to generate a concern resembling {\i tzarat erva}. Sarah, in turn, becomes a quasi-{\i tzarat erva}. The result: Levi cannot perform {\i yibum} with Sarah because it looks like a {\i tzarat erva} situation, but he also cannot simply walk away from his obligation to Sarah, because biblically Sarah is his true {\i yevama}. The resolution is {\i chalitza}: Levi must perform {\i chalitza} with Sarah, releasing her, without performing {\i yibum}. She is neither available for {\i yibum} nor free without {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question was raised here: would not the {\i zika} \u8212- the quasi-betrothal bond that exists between a {\i yavam} and his {\i yevamot} even before any {\i maamar} is performed \u8212- create a similar concern even without the {\i maamar}? The answer is that in this case {\i zika} alone does not generate the same status as {\i maamar}, though the question of {\i zika} as quasi-{\i kiddushin} is a broader sugya that will be addressed as the {\i masechet} continues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rishona/Shniya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman then addresses a textual question about the Mishnah\u8217's terminology. {\i Man d\u8217'sani rishona v\u8217'lo mishtabesh} \u8212- one who has the text reading {\i rishona} (the first one) to describe Rachel is not in error. {\i Man d\u8217'sani shniya v\u8217'lo mishtabesh} \u8212- one who has the text reading {\i shniya} (the second one) to describe Rachel is also not in error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why might Rachel be called {\i rishona}? {\i Rishona l\u8217'nefila} \u8212- she was the first one to enter the picture when Reuven died, and when describing the situation to Levi, she is the first woman discussed. Why might Rachel be called {\i shniya}? One might think this means she was Shimon\u8217's second wife, but the Gemara objects: who says Shimon married her after Sarah? Perhaps he performed {\i yibum} with Rachel first and then married Sarah. The Gemara therefore resolves that {\i shniya} here means {\i shniya b\u8217'nisuim} \u8212- not the second in sequence but the one who has been married twice: Rachel was first married to Reuven and then, through {\i yibum}, to Shimon. That double-married status is what {\i shniya} refers to.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Paternal Brotherhood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yachdov Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now seeks the biblical source for the rule that a brother who was not alive at the same time as the deceased has no {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum} and his sister-in-law is an {\i erva} to him. Rav Yehuda identifies the verse: {\i ki yeshvu achim yachdov} \u8212- \u8220"when brothers dwell together.\u8221" The word {\i yachdov} \u8212- \u8220"together\u8221" \u8212- teaches {\i she-hayta lahem yeshiva achat ba-olam} \u8212- that they must have shared simultaneous existence in the world. {\i Prat l\u8217'eshet achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo} \u8212- this excludes the case where a brother is born only after the other has already died. The word {\i yachdov} insists on contemporaneity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternal Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established what {\i yachdov} teaches about temporal coexistence, the Gemara derives a second principle from the same word: {\i meyuchadim b\u8217'nachala} \u8212- they must be united in inheritance. {\i Prat l\u8217'achiv min ha-em} \u8212- this excludes a maternal brother. A maternal brother does not inherit together with his half-siblings through the father; inheritance flows through the paternal line. {\i Yachdov}, the togetherness required, is specifically the togetherness of those who share an inheritance \u8212- and that is a paternal, not a maternal, connection. Therefore, {\i yibum} applies only to paternal brothers, not to maternal ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejecting Lot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah offers an alternative derivation of paternal brotherhood: {\i achi min ha-av yalif achva achva mi-bnei Yaakov} \u8212- the principle that \u8220"brother\u8221" in this context means paternal brother is learned through a {\i gezerah shava} of {\i achva-achva} from the sons of Yaakov. When the sons of Yaakov say {\i shneim asar achim anachnu} \u8212- we are twelve brothers \u8212- they are referring exclusively to paternal brothers. From that usage, the meaning of {\i achim} in {\i ki yeshvu achim yachdov} is also restricted to paternal brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: why not derive the meaning of {\i achim} from {\i arayot}, where the verse says {\i eshet achicha} \u8212- your brother\u8217's wife \u8212- and where {\i achicha} refers even to a maternal brother? If we learn the scope of {\i yibum} from {\i arayot}, we might include maternal brothers as well. The Gemara responds: {\i danim achim me-achim v\u8217'ein danim achim me-achicha} \u8212- we prefer to derive {\i achim} from {\i achim}, the same word, rather than from the related but distinct {\i achicha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presses further: what is the difference? One might argue that these are close enough to be linked through the principle of {\i Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's school}, which in the context of {\i tzara\u8217'at} links {\i veshav ha-kohen} and {\i u-va ha-kohen} \u8212- \u8220"the kohen shall return\u8221" and \u8220"the kohen shall come\u8221" \u8212- as referring to the same act even though the words differ. The Gemara answers: {\i hani mili heicha de-laika de-dami lei} \u8212- that principle applies only when there is no closer match. {\i Aval heicha de-ika de-dami lei, mi-de-dami lei yalfinan} \u8212- when a closer verbal parallel is available, we learn from the closer match. Since {\i achim} is available in the context of Yaakov\u8217's sons, we derive from there rather than from {\i arayot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why not derive from Lot, where the word {\i achim} appears to refer to relatives more broadly? When Lot says to Abraham {\i ki anashim achim anachnu}, the word {\i achim} there seems to mean simply kinsmen or relatives \u8212- not brothers in the strict sense, and certainly not paternal brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jacob\u8217's Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that the word {\i achim} in the declaration of Yaakov\u8217's sons is a demonstrably superfluous word, and therefore it must be there to teach something. {\i Michdi hava lei le-michtav shneim asar avdecha bnei avinu} \u8212- they could have said simply \u8220"we are twelve, servants, sons of our father.\u8221" Why add {\i achim} \u8212- brothers \u8212- at all? {\i V\u8217'ichtiv achim, shma mina le-afnuyei} \u8212- the extra word {\i achim} is there to serve as the source-word for the {\i gezerah shava}, teaching that {\i achim} means specifically paternal brothers. The word by Lot, by contrast, may or may not be superfluous \u8212- the Gemara acknowledges it might be \u8212- and indeed {\i le-afnuyei mifnei}: even the word by Lot can be shown to be extra, since Lot could have said \u8220"we are friends\u8221" ({\i reim}) rather than brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Words Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes that both {\i achim} and {\i yachdov} are necessary and neither could serve alone. Had the verse said only {\i achim}, we might have learned from Lot that {\i achim} means any relative, including a nephew. Had the verse said only {\i yachdov}, it might have implied that we require brothers who share both mother and father \u8212- an even stricter requirement than paternal brotherhood alone. {\i Tzaricha} \u8212- each word is required to do its own work. {\i Achim} confirms, through the {\i gezerah shava} from Yaakov\u8217's sons, that paternal brotherhood is sufficient and that {\i yachdov} does not demand a shared mother. And {\i yachdov} confirms that the brothers must share an inheritance \u8212- ruling out the derivation from Lot that {\i achim} might mean any relative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But one might still ask: why would I have thought {\i yachdov} requires shared parentage on both sides? The Gemara\u8217's answer is fundamental:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum & Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yibum b\u8217'nachala talya rachamana} \u8212- the Torah has tied {\i yibum} to inheritance. This is a striking claim. The surface rationale for {\i yibum} given by the Torah is {\i yakum al shem achiv ha-met} \u8212- the child born of the {\i yibum} will perpetuate the name of the deceased brother. But the Gemara understands that embedded within this rationale is the idea of inheritance: the {\i yavam} takes the deceased brother\u8217's {\i nachala} and passes it on through the child. {\i Yibum} is therefore structurally connected to inheritance law, and since inheritance flows through the father \u8212- {\i nachala min ha-av v\u8217'lo min ha-em} \u8212- maternal brothers, who do not share in the paternal inheritance, fall outside the scope of {\i yibum} altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought to push in the opposite direction: since {\i yibum} involves overriding what would otherwise be an {\i erva} \u8212- a normally forbidden relationship \u8212- perhaps we should limit its application to only the closest possible fraternal bond, which would be brothers who share both mother and father. {\i Yibum} is a {\i chiddush}, an exception to the ordinary rules of {\i ervah}; perhaps we should minimize the exception and apply it only in the most intimate brotherly relationship. The Gemara rejects this through the dual derivation: {\i achim} from Yaakov\u8217's sons establishes that paternal brotherhood alone suffices, and {\i yachdov} rules out the extension to mere relatives. The result is the ruling as we know it: {\i yibum} applies to paternal brothers, not maternal ones, and only when both brothers were alive simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 18\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zika & Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum & Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous discussion concluded with a remarkable line in the {\i gemara}: {\i yibum b\u8217'nachala tala rachmana} \u8212- the obligation of {\i yibum} is somehow directly connected to the concept of inheritance. That connection is worth keeping in mind as we proceed, because it will circle back in subsequent discussions. We now turn to a new topic \u8212- or rather, one that is not entirely new \u8212- which brings us back to the end of the {\i mishna} that dealt with the concepts of {\i zika} and {\i maamar}. Before that, however, we had revisited the case of {\i eshes achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo}, derived from the word {\i yachdav}, which teaches us that {\i yibum} applies only when both brothers were alive at the same time, and that the patrilineal relationship alone \u8212- even without shared maternity \u8212- suffices to create the {\i yibum} bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with the statement of Rav Huna in the name of Rav. The concept at the center of this discussion is {\i zika} \u8212- a quasi-marital connection that the surviving brother inherits from his deceased brother with respect to the {\i yevama}. This is not a concept that appears explicitly anywhere in the {\i psukim}; the {\i psukim} simply legislate the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}. Yet {\i Chazal} understand {\i zika} to be the underlying biblical mechanism \u8212- the metaphysical or legal reality that makes {\i yibum} possible. It is what allows the {\i yibum} act to be a continuation of the previous relationship rather than a new {\i issur} of {\i eshes achiv}. Because of this {\i zika}, the {\i yevama}\u8217's status is transferred in some quasi-marital sense to the surviving brother, and this transfer then requires actualization through either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}. Importantly, {\i zika} is understood as a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} concept, not {\i derabbanan} \u8212- though, as we shall see, the question of exactly which implications flow from it at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level is precisely what is being debated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomeres Yavam Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this backdrop, Rav Huna states in the name of Rav: {\i shomeres yavam shemeisa muteres b\u8217'ima} \u8212- if a {\i shomeres yavam} dies, the {\i yavam} is permitted to marry her mother. The case is straightforward: Reuven is married to Rachel, Reuven dies, and Shimon now stands obligated in {\i yibum} to Rachel. Rachel, in her waiting status, is called a {\i shomeres yavam}. She then dies. The question is whether Shimon may now marry Rachel\u8217's mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think this is prohibited, since if Shimon had actually been married to Rachel and she died, he could not marry her mother \u8212- the prohibition of {\i isha v\u8217'ima} applies even to a deceased wife\u8217's mother. The question, then, is whether Rachel\u8217's status as Shimon\u8217's {\i zekuka} \u8212- as the woman to whom he has a {\i zika}, a quasi-marital connection inherited from Reuven \u8212- is sufficient to render her mother forbidden as the mother of his {\i zekukaso}, presumably at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's ruling is that this is permitted. The ruling is interpreted as reflecting the position of {\i ein zika}: either the concept of {\i zika} is denied altogether, or \u8212- more precisely \u8212- whatever {\i zika} exists is not substantial enough to carry the implication of rendering the woman\u8217's relatives forbidden as {\i erva}. There is also a distinct possible reading: even if one accepts {\i yesh zika} while the {\i shomeres yavam} is alive, one might argue that once she dies, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} no longer exists and the {\i zika} dissolves with it. After all, {\i zika} was tied to an obligation that needed to be actualized; once she is dead, there is no longer anything to actualize. However, as we will see, the {\i gemara} here is not limiting its statement in that way. It understands Rav\u8217's ruling as reflecting {\i ein zika} categorically \u8212- or at least, {\i ein zika} in any sense meaningful enough to create prohibited relationships.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein vs.\u160 ?Yesh Zika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: given that this ruling reflects {\i ein zika}, why not simply state the general principle \u8212- {\i halacha k\u8217'divrei ha\u8217'omer ein zika} \u8212- and be done with it? Why construct a specific case about marrying the mother?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers with a two-step narrowing argument. First, had we only ruled {\i ein zika} in the context of the case involving two brothers \u8212- a case where Levi is engaged to Leah (Rachel\u8217's sister) but has not yet consummated the marriage, while Shimon also stands obligated to Rachel \u8212- one might have said: {\i ein zika} applies there because with two brothers, the {\i zika} is indeterminate. It is not clear that Rachel is specifically bound to Levi, since Shimon might be the one to perform {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}. Perhaps with only one surviving brother, the {\i zika} is fully concentrated in him, and we would say {\i yesh zika} in that case. Therefore, Rav had to state a case of a single surviving {\i yavam} to demonstrate {\i ein zika} even there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, had Rav simply ruled {\i ein zika} even in the case of one brother \u8212- without limiting his ruling to the scenario after her death \u8212- one might conclude that Shimon may marry Rachel\u8217's mother even while Rachel is still alive. So Rav deliberately chose the case of Rachel having died, teaching us: after her death, yes; while she is alive, no. But why not? If we hold {\i ein zika} even when she is alive, what prevents it? The answer is {\i mishum de\u8217'assur levatel mitzvos yevamin} \u8212- one may not nullify the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}. In principle, there is no {\i issur} of {\i zika} even while Rachel is alive, but if Shimon were to marry Rachel\u8217's mother while Rachel still lives, he would have rendered himself unable to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel (since she would now be his {\i eshet ima}), and the {\i mitzvah} would simply vanish. The {\i gemara}, following Rashi, holds that if the {\i yevama} becomes an {\i erva} after the {\i yibum} obligation begins, she is treated like one who was an {\i erva} from the outset \u8212- neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitza} applies. Therefore, marrying the mother would entirely eliminate both options. This is considered {\i assur lechatchila} as a negation of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, though {\i bediavad} the marriage would take effect, since there is no actual {\i issur} of {\i ein zekukaso} according to this position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The precise rationale for the prohibition of {\i bitul mitzvat yevamin} remains somewhat difficult to define sharply. It is something like the principle that where a {\i mitzvah} stands to be performed, one should not actively cause it to disappear \u8212- though whether it rises to more than a {\i lechatchila} concern is unclear from the {\i gemara} here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika vs.\u160 ?Maamar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point it is worth clarifying the distinction between {\i zika} and {\i maamar}. {\i Maamar} is a rabbinic institution \u8212- an act of {\i kiddushin} performed by the {\i yavam} with the {\i yevama} prior to {\i yibum}, giving the relationship a character more closely resembling conventional marriage before the consummating act. It is purely {\i derabbanan}. {\i Zika}, by contrast, is {\i Chazal}\u8217's understanding of the underlying biblical mechanism operative in {\i yibum} law: at the moment the husband dies, his surviving brothers inherit a quasi-marital connection to his wife, and this connection \u8212- this {\i zika} \u8212- is what must be resolved through either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}. The question of whether {\i zika} has significant legal implications \u8212- rendering the woman\u8217's relatives {\i erva}, or making her sister forbidden as {\i achot zekukaso} \u8212- is precisely the question the {\i gemara} is now exploring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents a potential proof from a {\i mishna} that states: {\i yevamto shemeisa mutar b\u8217'achosah} \u8212- if the {\i yevama} dies, he may marry her sister. The {\i mishna} specifies only the sister, implying he may not marry the mother, which would suggest {\i yesh zika}. The {\i gemara} dismisses this: {\i hu hadin de\u8217'afilu b\u8217'imah} \u8212- in truth he could even marry the mother. The {\i mishna} only mentioned the sister in parallel with the opening clause, which addressed the case of one\u8217's actual wife dying \u8212- where only the sister is permitted and the mother remains forbidden {\i d\u8217'oraisa} regardless. The {\i seifa} follows that language pattern without implying the same restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this, Rav Yehuda rules: {\i shomeres yavam shemeisa assur b\u8217'imah} \u8212- even after she dies, it is forbidden to marry her mother \u8212- reflecting the position of {\i yesh zika}. The {\i gemara} again asks why Rav Yehuda could not simply state {\i yesh zika} as a general principle with reference to the case of Levi and Leah. The answer mirrors the logic above: had he stated {\i yesh zika} only for that case (which involves two brothers), one might have thought {\i yesh zika} applies only with one brother. But had he said {\i yesh zika} even with one brother, one might have thought it applies only while the woman is alive \u8212- {\i mechayim} \u8212- but that once she dies, {\i paka la zika}, the {\i zika} dissolves. Therefore Rav Yehuda had to specify explicitly the case after death: {\i kamashma lan d\u8217'zika b\u8217'chadi lo paka} \u8212- {\i zika} does not simply disappear on its own. Even after the woman has died, the {\i zika} persists, and the mother remains forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the scenario involving two brothers. Reuven is married to Rachel. Levi has performed {\i kiddushin} \u8212- but not yet {\i nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- with Leah, Rachel\u8217's sister. Reuven then dies. The question facing Levi is stark: may he proceed to consummate his marriage with Leah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achot Zekukato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem is that Rachel, as {\i shomeres yavam}, now stands in a {\i zika} relationship with Levi. Leah, Levi\u8217's fianc\u233 ?e, is Leah\u8217's sister. If {\i zika} creates a quasi-marital bond, then Leah is the sister of Levi\u8217's {\i zekuka}, making her {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- analogous to {\i achot ishto}. Consummating the marriage with Leah while Rachel remains in a {\i zika} relationship with Levi would therefore be problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The position discussed \u8212- attributed ultimately to {\i mishum Rabbi Yehuda ben Beseira} \u8212- is that Levi must wait: {\i hamtein ad she-ya\u8217'aseh achicha ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- wait until your brother Shimon performs {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} with Rachel. Once Rachel is removed from the picture \u8212- either through {\i yibum} with Shimon or through {\i chalitza} \u8212- Levi is free to consummate his marriage with Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note that this case differs from the mother case in two respects, both of which the {\i gemara} will address. First, here Rachel is still alive, which gives the concept of {\i zika} more intuitive force \u8212- there is still a live obligation that must be actualized. Second, however, there is somewhat less reason to apply {\i zika} specifically to Levi in this case, since there are two surviving brothers, Shimon and Levi, and the {\i zika} is not concentrated in Levi alone. It is Shimon who bears the primary obligation: {\i mitzvah begadol leyabem}, the older brother has precedence. To the extent that the {\i mitzvah} lies primarily with Shimon, the {\i zika} binding Rachel to Levi specifically is more attenuated, which gives additional grounds for arguing {\i ein zika} in this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Specificity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks why the {\i mishna} in the case of {\i eshes achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo} specifies the scenario where Shimon performed {\i maamar} with Rachel. If {\i yesh zika}, then even without {\i maamar}, Shimon\u8217's mere {\i zika} to Rachel should have been sufficient to render Sarah ({\i tzarat zekukaso}) problematic for Levi after Shimon\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds through Rava: {\i hu hadin de\u8217'af al gav delo avad ba maamar, shniya mechlatz chaltza yibumi lo meyabma} \u8212- even without {\i maamar}, {\i zika} alone is sufficient to create the quasi-marital relationship, and therefore Levi may only perform {\i chalitza} with Sarah, not {\i yibum}. Why, then, did the {\i mishna} specify {\i maamar}? {\i Le\u8217'apokei mi-Beis Shammai de\u8217'amar maamar koneh kinyan gamur} \u8212- to exclude the position of Beis Shammai, who holds that {\i maamar} effects a complete acquisition equivalent to {\i yibum} itself. According to Beis Shammai, {\i maamar} with Rachel would make Sarah a full {\i tzarat erva} for Levi, precluding even {\i chalitza}. The {\i mishna} therefore specifies {\i maamar} to teach that even in that case, Levi must perform {\i chalitza} \u8212- contra Beis Shammai, who would say it is an outright {\i tzarat erva} requiring nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Alone Sufficient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical conclusion is thus that {\i zika} alone \u8212- without any formal act of {\i maamar} \u8212- is sufficient to create a quasi-marital relationship with significant halachic implications. Even if Shimon did absolutely nothing after Reuven died, the {\i zika} he acquired to Rachel means that after Shimon\u8217's death, Rachel functions as a quasi-{\i erva} for Levi, rendering Sarah a {\i tzarat erva al yedei zika}. Levi may therefore perform only {\i chalitza} with Sarah, not {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion emerges from the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis of the {\i mishna}. The {\i braisa} introduced by Abaye, which describes the exact scenario \u8212- Shimon had a {\i zika} to Rachel but died before performing {\i maamar}, and Levi was born only afterward \u8212- confirms this: regarding Rachel, Levi is exempt ({\i achot zekukaso shelo haya b\u8217'olamo}), while regarding Sarah, {\i o choletzes o misyabemes} \u8212- Levi may perform either {\i chalitza} or {\i yibum}. This seems to prove the contrary \u8212- that without {\i maamar}, Sarah is not a problem and Levi may even do {\i yibum} with her. The {\i gemara} acknowledges this directly: {\i ve-i amarta yesh zika, havya la tzoras eshes achiv shelo haya baolamo b\u8217'zika} \u8212- if {\i zika} were operative, Sarah should be the {\i tzara} of Shimon\u8217's quasi-wife, and Levi should be unable to perform {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Not Born Yet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stage Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i eshes achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo} now takes center stage in a more complex form. The setup is as follows: Reuven is married to Rachel; Shimon is alive and married to Sarah; Reuven dies with no children. At that moment, Rachel becomes a {\i shomeres yavam} \u8212- obligated in {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} with Shimon. Subsequently, Levi is born. Since Levi was not yet alive when Reuven died, Rachel is {\i eshes achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo} with respect to Levi \u8212- she carries no {\i mitzvat yibum} in relation to him. Shimon then dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Without Maamar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question now is: what is Levi\u8217's status regarding Sarah, Shimon\u8217's wife? Regarding Rachel, it is clear \u8212- she is an {\i erva} to him as {\i eshes achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo}. But what about Sarah? After Shimon\u8217's death, Sarah falls into {\i yibum} with Levi as the surviving brother. The complication is that Shimon had a {\i zika} to Rachel \u8212- a quasi-marital bond. If {\i yesh zika}, then Rachel functions quasi as Shimon\u8217's wife; Levi is then obligated in {\i yibum} to Rachel, but she is an {\i erva} to him. Sarah would then be {\i tzarat erva al yedei zika} \u8212- the co-wife of Shimon\u8217's quasi-wife \u8212- and Levi could not do {\i yibum} with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, in this scenario, Shimon performed no {\i maamar} with Rachel. The {\i zika} existed but was never concretized by any formal act. The question is whether {\i zika} alone \u8212- without {\i maamar} \u8212- suffices to render Sarah problematic for Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially assumes that based on our analysis ({\i zika} alone is sufficient), Levi should indeed be prohibited from {\i yibum} with Sarah, since Rachel\u8217's {\i zika} to Shimon casts her in a quasi-{\i erva} role. Yet the {\i mishna} in question specifies the scenario of {\i maamar}, implying that without {\i maamar} there is no such restriction. This tension is what drives the inquiry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braisa: Levi Yibum OK\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye raises a challenge from a {\i braisa}: {\i shnei achim b\u8217'olam echad, u-meis echad mehem b\u8217'lo valad} \u8212- two brothers exist in the world, one dies without children. Shimon was about to perform {\i maamar} with Rachel but did not manage to do so before Levi was born, and then Shimon died. The {\i braisa} rules: {\i ha-rishona yotza mishum eshes achiv shelo haya b\u8217'olamo} \u8212- Rachel is exempt from {\i yibum} with Levi; {\i v\u8217'ha-shniya o choletzes o misyabemes} \u8212- regarding Sarah, Levi may perform either {\i chalitza} or {\i yibum}. Sarah is not rendered problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a direct challenge: if {\i yesh zika} and {\i zika} alone suffices even after death, then Sarah should be {\i tzarat erva al yedei zika} and Levi should be unable to do {\i yibum} with her. Yet the {\i braisa} permits it. Evidently, in this case \u8212- where Shimon had only a {\i zika} and no {\i maamar} \u8212- Sarah is not a problem for Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Zika After Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes that this {\i braisa} reflects the position of {\i ein zika} \u8212- at minimum, {\i ein zika l\u8217'achar mita}, that {\i zika} does not persist after the death of the {\i yavam} who held it. The {\i braisa} is attributed to Rabbi Meir, who holds {\i ein zika}. However, this attribution is immediately complicated: the {\i gemara} will show that a {\i stam mishna} \u8212- presumed to reflect Rabbi Meir\u8217's view \u8212- appears to operate on the assumption of {\i yesh zika}. The {\i braisa}\u8217's position, then, is not necessarily the final ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Brothers Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To test this further, the {\i gemara} turns to a {\i mishna} about four brothers. Reuven and Levi were married to Rachel and Leah, who are sisters. Both Reuven and Levi die. The two surviving brothers \u8212- let us call them Yissachar and Zevulun \u8212- now face obligations of {\i yibum} to both women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achot Ishah Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem is immediate. Yissachar has a {\i zika} to both Rachel and Leah \u8212- each came from a different deceased brother, but both obligations now fall on the surviving brothers. Similarly for Zevulun. If Yissachar were to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel, he would thereby be having relations with {\i achot zekukaso} \u8212- Leah is his {\i zekuka}, and Rachel is her sister. Since {\i zika} creates a quasi-marital bond, consummating {\i yibum} with Rachel would be like having relations with the sister of his quasi-wife. The same logic applies in reverse if he were to go to Leah first. It seems as though {\i yibum} cannot be performed at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} rules: {\i harei elu chotzot v\u8217'lo mityabmot} \u8212- both women perform {\i chalitza} and may not undergo {\i yibum}. Why {\i chalitza} rather than each brother doing {\i yibum} with a different sister \u8212- Yissachar with Rachel and Zevulun with Leah? The {\i gemara} explains: {\i dilma ad-meyabbem chad u-met idach} \u8212- perhaps after Yissachar performs {\i yibum} with Rachel, Zevulun dies before performing {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} with Leah. Now Yissachar has done {\i yibum} with Rachel, so Leah is {\i achot ishto} \u8212- forbidden to him as his wife\u8217's sister. No one can perform {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} with Leah. She exits without any resolution. That is {\i bitul mitzvat yevamim} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} obligation with respect to Leah is entirely negated. To prevent even the possibility of that outcome, both women must do {\i chalitza}. That way, if one {\i chalitza} has been performed, the surviving brother can still perform {\i chalitza} for the other woman, since {\i chalitza} does not create the {\i achot ishto} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: is it not also a case of {\i bitul mitzvat yevamim} if someone dies in the middle of the {\i chalitza} proceedings? The answer is that if Yissachar performs {\i chalitza} with Rachel, the prohibition of {\i achot ishto} does not apply \u8212- he has merely released her, not married her \u8212- and he can still perform {\i chalitza} with Leah as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Ein Zika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now confronts a tension: if Rabbi Meir holds {\i ein zika}, as we concluded from the {\i braisa} above, why does this {\i stam mishna} \u8212- presumed to be Rabbi Meir \u8212- operate on the assumption that {\i yesh zika}? If {\i ein zika}, why can\u8217't Yissachar simply do {\i yibum} with Rachel and Zevulun with Leah? They come from two different households ({\i mitrei batei ka\u8217'atyan}) \u8212- there is no {\i tzarat erva} issue. Each brother could do {\i yibum} with a different woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i le\u8217'olam ein zika}. Rabbi Meir does indeed hold {\i ein zika}. The prohibition against doing {\i yibum} in this case is not because of {\i achot zekukato} but because of {\i bitul mitzvat yevamin} \u8212- the concern that if one brother does {\i yibum} and the other then dies, the second woman loses her {\i mitzvah} entirely. Rabbi Meir is so sensitive to this concern that even in a merely possible (not certain) scenario of {\i bitul mitzvat yevamim}, he requires {\i chalitza} over {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Underage Wife Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To support the proposed connection between the positions of {\i ein zika} and permissibility of {\i bitul mitzvat yevamin}, the {\i gemara} cites Rabban Gamliel from a {\i mishna}: Reuven is married to Rachel; Levi is married to Leah, who is underage and whose marriage has only {\i derabbanan} validity (having been arranged by her mother and brothers, such that {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- retroactive rejection \u8212- remains an option). Reuven dies. The question is what Levi should do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel rules: {\i im miana miana} \u8212- if Leah performs {\i mi\u8217'un} and retroactively annuls the marriage, that is ideal; Levi is then free to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel. {\i Ve-im lav tamtin ad she-tagdil} \u8212- if she does not perform {\i mi\u8217'un}, wait until she grows up. Levi continues living with Leah; when Leah reaches adulthood, their relations constitute {\i kiddushin d\u8217'oraisa}, and at that point Rachel \u8212- as Leah\u8217's sister and Levi\u8217's {\i achot ishto} \u8212- is released from any {\i yibum} obligation without {\i chalitza}, as she has become an outright {\i erva} to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel Proves Ein Zika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof that Rabban Gamliel holds {\i ein zika} lies in this ruling: during the period of waiting for Leah to grow up, Levi continues having relations with Leah despite the fact that Rachel is his {\i zekuka}. If {\i zika} were significant, Leah \u8212- as {\i achot zekukaso} \u8212- would be forbidden to him during this period. Yet Rabban Gamliel raises no objection. This demonstrates that Rabban Gamliel holds {\i ein zika}: the {\i zika} to Rachel carries no implications that would render Leah problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel\u8217's Two Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling effectively ensures that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with Rachel will disappear entirely. Once Leah grows up and {\i kiddushin d\u8217'oraisa} is in force, Rachel becomes {\i achot ishto} \u8212- an outright {\i erva} \u8212- and neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitza} applies. Rabban Gamliel is entirely untroubled by this outcome. He holds both {\i ein zika} and that {\i bitul mitzvat yevamin} is not a concern, even when the disappearance of the {\i mitzvah} is certain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus presents Rabban Gamliel as holding both positions together, in contrast to Rabbi Meir, who was said to hold {\i ein zika} but still to be concerned about {\i bitul mitzvat yevamin} even in cases of doubt. The {\i gemara} suggests that these two positions may be logically linked: if {\i ein zika}, perhaps there is no conceptual basis for worrying about {\i bitul mitzvat yevamin} either, since the whole idea of something that must be \u8220"actualized\u8221" or \u8220"transformed\u8221" \u8212- the underpinning of the {\i bitul} concern \u8212- derives from the concept of {\i zika} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Necessarily Connected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back on this proposed connection: {\i Rabban Gamliel ad Rabbi Meir ka ramit?} \u8212- what justification is there for comparing Rabban Gamliel\u8217's two positions to Rabbi Meir\u8217's? Perhaps they are not logically connected after all. Perhaps Rabbi Meir holds {\i ein zika} and yet still holds {\i assur levatel mitzvat yevamin}. The {\i gemara} concedes that these positions need not be linked. What it offers instead is a characterological distinction: {\i Rabbi Meir chayish afilu l\u8217'sfeika} \u8212- Rabbi Meir is concerned about {\i bitul mitzvat yevamin} even in a merely doubtful case (e.g., the possibility that one brother might die mid-process in the four-brothers scenario). {\i Rabban Gamliel afilu l\u8217'vadai lo chayish} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel is unconcerned even where the {\i bitul} is certain (as in the underage-wife case, where Leah\u8217's eventual maturation will definitively eliminate the obligation toward Rachel). The difference between them, then, is one of degree in how much weight they assign to {\i bitul mitzvat yevamin}, not necessarily a matter of logical entailment from {\i ein zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the original ruling with which the discussion opened. Abaye says to Rav Yosef: {\i ha de-Rav Yehuda de-Shmuel hi} \u8212- the ruling that the {\i shomeres yavam} who died renders her mother forbidden (i.e., {\i yesh zika}) is properly attributed to Shmuel, not to Rav. The initial ruling permitting the marriage of the mother was stated as {\i Rav Huna amar Rav}, reflecting {\i ein zika}. The opposing ruling \u8212- forbidding it \u8212- was attributed to Rav Yehuda. Since Rav Yehuda was a student of both Rav and Shmuel, the attribution was ambiguous. If Rav Yehuda was transmitting in the name of Rav, we would have two conflicting traditions about Rav\u8217's position. Abaye clarifies: the ruling of Rav Yehuda that it is {\i assur} is actually Shmuel\u8217's position, not Rav\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Consistent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This attribution is consistent with another known ruling of Shmuel: {\i amar Shmuel halacha ke-Rabbi Yehuda ben Beseira} \u8212- Shmuel rules in accordance with Rabbi Yehuda ben Beseira that we instruct Levi to wait before consummating his marriage with Leah until his brother Shimon resolves the {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} with Rachel. Both positions \u8212- the one about the mother and the one about the sister \u8212- reflect the same underlying view: {\i yesh zika}, and the {\i zika} creates genuine restrictions on relationships with the {\i zekuka}\u8217's relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana reports that when he related this discussion before Rav Zvid of Nehardea, Rav Zvid responded that in their tradition there was an explicit formulation: {\i amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel, shomeres yavam shemeisa assur b\u8217'imah} \u8212- Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel that if the {\i shomeres yavam} died it is forbidden to marry her mother. There is thus no need to infer the attribution from Shmuel\u8217's other rulings; it was transmitted explicitly. {\i Alma kasavar yesh zika} \u8212- he holds {\i yesh zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why both statements of Shmuel were necessary. Had Shmuel only ruled about the mother case, one might have thought that {\i yesh zika} applies only where there is a single {\i yavam}, since in that scenario the {\i zika} is fully concentrated in him. Perhaps in the case of two brothers (the Levi-Leah-Shimon case), the indeterminate nature of the {\i zika} would render it insufficient \u8212- {\i ein zika} in that case, and Levi could consummate his marriage with Leah without waiting for Shimon. Therefore Shmuel also had to rule like Rabbi Yehuda ben Beseira, applying {\i yesh zika} even with two brothers. Conversely, had Shmuel only ruled about the two-brothers case, one might have concluded that {\i yesh zika} applies only while the {\i zekuka} is alive \u8212- {\i mechayim} \u8212- but that after her death {\i paka la zika}, the {\i zika} dissolves. Shmuel therefore also had to rule in the mother case, which arises specifically {\i after} her death, teaching us {\i kamashma lan d\u8217'zika b\u8217'chadi lo paka} \u8212- {\i zika} does not simply dissolve on its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is thus broad and sweeping. {\i Yesh zika} \u8212- the {\i gemara} adopts this position comprehensively. {\i Zika} persists even after the {\i zekuka} has died. It applies even in cases where there are multiple brothers and the {\i zika} is not exclusively concentrated in one of them. And it carries genuine halachic implications \u8212- rendering the {\i zekuka}\u8217's mother forbidden, and requiring Levi to wait before consummating his marriage with {\i achot zekukato} until the {\i zika} has been resolved through {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}. This is the position of Shmuel, affirmed by explicit tradition, and it appears to be the {\i gemara}\u8217's accepted ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 19\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eishes Achiv Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Zika Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous discussion centered on the concept of {\i zika} \u8212- whether it creates a genuine prohibition extending to a woman\u8217's relatives, and whether it persists even after {\i misa}. With that analysis as backdrop, we now turn to the next {\i mishna}, which brings us back to the central theme of {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}. It will come as no surprise that the question of {\i zika} will resurface prominently here as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Brothers Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} takes up the case of three brothers: Reuven, Shimon, and Levi. Reuven is married to Rachel. Reuven dies, and then Levi is born. The critical variable the {\i mishna} wishes to examine is the precise timing of Shimon\u8217's {\i yibum} with Rachel: did it occur before Levi was born, or after?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Timing Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The significance of this question is as follows. In all that preceded, we assumed that Shimon performed {\i yibum} with Rachel after Levi was born. Under those circumstances, when Levi entered the world, he encountered Rachel already in the status of {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- an {\i erva} to him. But if Shimon performed {\i yibum} with Rachel before Levi was ever born, then by the time Levi came into existence, perhaps her prior identity as Reuven\u8217's wife is simply irrelevant. Perhaps Rachel relates to Levi only as Shimon\u8217's wife, her earlier history having been entirely superseded. If so, when Shimon dies, it might be a full {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum} for Levi with Rachel. That is precisely the question our {\i mishna} addresses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Two Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reisha: Birth Before\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens: {\i shnei achim shemet echad meihem} \u8212- two brothers, Reuven and Shimon. Reuven dies. Then Levi is born. At that point, Rachel becomes {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} in relation to Levi. And then, in the {\i reisha}, Shimon performs {\i yibum} with Rachel \u8212- the wedding occurring after Levi\u8217's birth. When Shimon subsequently dies, the {\i mishna} rules: {\i harishona yotzeis mishum eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}. It makes no difference that Rachel was already married to Shimon when Levi was born. She carries with her the {\i erva} status she held before the marriage, and that does not change for Levi. If Shimon also had a second wife, {\i hashniya hareizo tzaras eishes ach}, she would be exempt from {\i yibum} as a {\i tzaras erva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seifa: Yibum Before\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i seifa} reverses the sequence. {\i Veyibam hasheini es eishes achiv ve\u8217'achar kach nolad lahem ach} \u8212- Shimon performs {\i yibum} with Rachel, and only afterward is Levi born. Now when Levi comes into the world, Rachel is already Shimon\u8217's wife. Her prior connection to Reuven is, as it were, past history. The {\i tanna kamma} nonetheless holds that this does not matter \u8212- she remains {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} even in this case, and Levi may not perform {\i yibum} with her. The {\i mishna} also addresses the scenario of {\i maamar} without {\i yibum}: if Shimon performed {\i maamar} with Rachel but did not complete {\i yibum} before dying, then Rachel is not truly Shimon\u8217's wife {\i de\u8217'oraisa}, though she is treated as such {\i derabbanan}. In that case, Levi certainly does not perform {\i yibum} with Rachel \u8212- she is {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- but the {\i co-wife}, who is the only genuine wife {\i de\u8217'oraisa}, neither falls away without anything nor is permitted for {\i yibum}: {\i hashniya choletzes velo misyabemes}, she performs {\i chalitza} but does not enter {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma v Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon, however, disputes the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's position in the first case \u8212- the {\i reisha} without {\i maamar}. {\i Rabbi Shimon omer miyabem le\u8217'eizo meihen sheyirtzeh} \u8212- he may perform {\i yibum} with whichever of the women he wishes, whether with Rachel or with the co-wife. Alternatively, {\i o cholets le\u8217'eizo meihen sheyirtzeh} \u8212- he performs {\i chalitza} with whichever he chooses. According to Rabbi Shimon, the relevant fact is that by the time Levi was born, Rachel was already Shimon\u8217's wife. Her status as {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} has been transformed; it no longer obtains, and when Shimon dies she is simply available for {\i yibum} like any other {\i yevama}. The dispute between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Shimon, then, turns on precisely this question: does the prior {\i erva} status persist through {\i yibum}, or does it dissolve once Levi enters a world in which Rachel is already married to his brother?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Oshaya Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Superfluous Reisha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Oshaya: cholek alav Rabbi Shimon af le-reisha.} Rav Oshaya now advances a striking claim: Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position that the {\i erva} status dissolves holds even in the {\i reisha} \u8212- even when Levi was born before Shimon performed {\i yibum} with Rachel. The structure of the {\i mishna} itself, Rav Oshaya argues, compels this reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His argument runs as follows. {\i Mimai? Mide\u8217'katani mishna yeseirah bava de-reisha} \u8212- the {\i reisha} is an entirely superfluous {\i mishna}. For whom was it taught? {\i Eleima le-rabbanan} \u8212- if it was taught for the {\i rabbanan}, then consider: in the {\i seifa}, the case where {\i yibum} had already occurred and Levi was born afterward \u8212- {\i deki ashkach bah be-heiteira ashkach} \u8212- Levi found Rachel already in a state of permissibility, already Shimon\u8217's wife. If even in that case the {\i rabbanan} hold that Rachel is forbidden, {\i lo choletzes velo mityabemes}, then in the {\i reisha} where Levi was born first and {\i yibum} came afterward \u8212- {\i mibaya?} \u8212- is there even a question? Of course she is forbidden to him. The {\i chiddush} for the {\i rabbanan} lies entirely in the {\i seifa}. The {\i reisha} adds nothing according to the {\i rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reisha Showcases Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It follows, Rav Oshaya argues, that the {\i reisha} must have been included exclusively to showcase Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. {\i Ela natru le-rabbanan ad demesaymi le-milsaihu ve-hadar palig aleihu} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon allowed the {\i rabbanan} to complete their statement and then voiced his opposition. But his opposition is relevant even to the {\i reisha}. {\i Ve-tana reisha lehodeia kocho de-Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- the {\i reisha} reveals the full force of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view, that even when Levi was born before {\i yibum}, Rachel becomes permitted once Shimon has married her. {\i Ve-tana seifa lehodeia kocho de-rabbanan} \u8212- the {\i seifa} reveals the full force of the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's view, that even when Levi was born after {\i yibum}, Rachel remains forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Board Diagram\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make this visually clear, consider the two scenarios side by side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i reisha} (scenario one): First, Reuven is married to Rachel, with Shimon present. Second, Reuven dies. Third, Levi is born \u8212- at this point, Rachel is an {\i erva} in relation to Levi. Fourth, Shimon marries Rachel. Even at step four, Levi has attended the wedding, as it were, but Rachel\u8217's {\i erva} status vis-\u224 ?-vis Levi has already been established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i seifa} (scenario two): The same opening, but the order reverses. After Reuven dies, Shimon performs {\i yibum} with Rachel. Only then is Levi born. When Levi enters the world, Rachel is already Shimon\u8217's wife. The question is whether Levi perceives Rachel as {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} or simply as his brother\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rachel\u8217's Status Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question now becomes: what is the mechanism by which Rachel\u8217's status could be said to change? The intuitive answer \u8212- and the one most naturally read into the {\i mishna} \u8212- is that Shimon\u8217's {\i yibum} with Rachel transforms her status. She begins as {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}, but once {\i yibum} occurs, that status is superseded. Levi subsequently enters a world in which Rachel is straightforwardly Shimon\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, what Rav Oshaya is actually claiming \u8212- as the {\i gemara} will develop \u8212- is subtler. It is not the act of {\i yibum} that transforms Rachel\u8217's status for Levi. Rather, it is the {\i zika} that already exists between Rachel and Shimon from the moment of Reuven\u8217's death. Even before {\i yibum} is completed, and even before Levi is born, Rachel stands in a relationship with Shimon by virtue of {\i zika}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's position will therefore be: when Levi is born, Rachel is not encountered as {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} because she is already, at the moment of Levi\u8217's birth, in a relationship with Shimon through {\i zika}. She was, in a sense, never unattached from a brother\u8217's household when Levi arrived. That is the mechanism behind Rav Oshaya\u8217's reading of Rabbi Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to note, however, that Levi is born before {\i yibum} has taken place and before Rachel is formally Shimon\u8217's wife. So you might have said: yes, at stage three Rachel is technically {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}, but the {\i yibum} that follows transforms that status. But the {\i gemara} resists that formulation. Instead, it insists that no transformation occurs \u8212- because from the moment of Reuven\u8217's death, {\i zika} already binds Rachel to Shimon in a meaningful way, and so when Levi is born, the {\i zika} is already operative, and Rachel is already, in that sense, in Shimon\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zika Kkenussa Damya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ella eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo le-Rabbi Shimon heichi mashkachas lah?} The {\i gemara} now presses: if Rav Oshaya\u8217's reading of Rabbi Shimon is correct, when would one ever encounter a genuine case of {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} according to Rabbi Shimon? Even if Levi is born before {\i yibum}, and even if Rachel appears to be {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} at that moment, Rabbi Shimon says she is permitted once Shimon\u8217's {\i zika} has taken hold. So in any scenario with three brothers \u8212- Reuven, Shimon, and Levi \u8212- where Reuven dies and his {\i zika} passes to Shimon, Rachel is already bound to Shimon by {\i zika} from the moment of Reuven\u8217's death. When Levi is then born, Rachel encounters him as effectively Shimon\u8217's wife, not as Reuven\u8217's widow. There would seem to be no room for {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies: {\i bishlama yibem ve\u8217'achar kach nolad} \u8212- the {\i seifa} is intelligible, because {\i ki asya be-heitera asya}, Levi found Rachel in a state of permissibility, already in a recognized relationship with Shimon. But in the {\i reisha}, if Levi was born first and {\i yibum} afterward, {\i mai ta\u8217'ama?} \u8212- how is Rachel not an {\i erva} to Levi? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i kasavar yesh zika ve-zika ke-knusa damya}. Rabbi Shimon holds two propositions: first, {\i yesh zika} \u8212- {\i zika} is a real and operative phenomenon. Second, and crucially, {\i zika ke-knusa damya} \u8212- the {\i zika} is treated as though the {\i yibum} has already been consummated. {\i Knusa} evokes the language of formal entry into marriage, of {\i nisuin}. So not only is there {\i zika}, but the {\i zika} is powerful enough that Rachel is regarded as already fully in Shimon\u8217's household. Therefore, when Levi is born, she is not {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- she is {\i eishes Shimon}, and when Shimon subsequently dies, the {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum} applies in full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers No Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} provides two scenarios in which {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} could still arise according to Rabbi Shimon. First: {\i be-chad acha u-meis ve-nolad lo ach} \u8212- there is only one brother. Reuven dies and then Levi is born. There is no Shimon in the picture, no {\i zika} to anyone, and Rachel is simply {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} to Levi. Second: {\i inami be-trei ve-lo yibem ve-lo meis} \u8212- Shimon is present but has neither performed {\i yibum} nor died. As long as Shimon has not acted \u8212- has not completed {\i yibum} \u8212- his {\i zika} to Rachel has not yet transformed her status in the decisive way, and were Levi to be intimate with Rachel, it would constitute {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}. It is specifically Shimon\u8217's {\i yibum} \u8212- or, under Rav Oshaya\u8217's expanded reading, the operative {\i zika} itself \u8212- that achieves the transformation. Without Shimon acting at all, the transformation does not occur.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zika Kkenussa Damya (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now articulates the underlying principle with precision. {\i Kasavar yesh zika ve-zika ke-knusa damya} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds that {\i zika} exists as a genuine halachic phenomenon and, further, that {\i zika ke-knusa damya}, the {\i zika} renders the woman as though she has already entered into marriage with the prospective {\i yavam}. This is not a rabbinic enactment but a characterization of the fundamental nature of {\i zika}: it is not merely a legal obligation pending action, but a quasi-marital bond powerful enough to redefine the woman\u8217's status. When Levi is born, Rachel is therefore already, by virtue of the {\i zika}, in Shimon\u8217's domain \u8212- and there is no basis for the {\i issur} of {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} to take hold.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yosef Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Masiv lei Rav Yosef.} Rav Yosef challenges the claim that Rabbi Shimon holds {\i zika ke-knusa damya} by demonstrating that Rabbi Shimon himself is uncertain about this. {\i Hashta zika u-ma\u8217'amar mispeka lei le-Rabbi Shimon ei ke-knusa damya ei lo ke-knusa damya} \u8212- Rav Yosef shows that Rabbi Shimon has a genuine {\i safek} whether the combination of {\i zika} and {\i maamar} is sufficient to render the woman as {\i ke-knusa}. If even {\i zika plus maamar} leaves Rabbi Shimon in doubt, then {\i zika le-chuda mibaya?} \u8212- {\i zika} alone is certainly not something Rabbi Shimon would declare definitively equivalent to full {\i knusa}. Rav Yosef then presents the case that demonstrates this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario: {\i shlosha achim nesu\u8217'im shalosh nashim nochrivot} \u8212- three brothers married to three unrelated women. In our terms: Reuven is married to Rachel, Shimon is married to Sarah, and Levi is present. Reuven dies. Shimon now has a {\i zika} to Rachel. Shimon then performs {\i maamar} with Rachel but does not complete {\i yibum}. Shimon then dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, Levi faces a dual situation with respect to Rachel. On one level, there is a {\i de\u8217'oraisa} {\i zika} to Rachel as Reuven\u8217's brother. On another level, there is a {\i derabbanan} quasi-marital bond to Rachel as a result of Shimon\u8217's {\i maamar}. Rachel therefore stands in relation to Levi with what appears to be a double {\i zika} \u8212- connected to him via two separate brothers simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Yibum Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Can Levi perform {\i yibum} with both Rachel and Sarah? He cannot. {\i Dilma yesh zika ve-havei shtei yevamos ha-ba\u8217'os mi-beis echad} \u8212- perhaps the {\i zika} and {\i maamar} together render Rachel fully Shimon\u8217's wife, in which case Rachel and Sarah are both coming from the same brother\u8217's household, and one cannot perform {\i yibum} with two wives of a single brother. Therefore he cannot do {\i yibum} with both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Zika Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Can he perform {\i yibum} with Rachel alone and release Sarah? He cannot do that either. {\i Dilma ein zika ke-knusa} \u8212- perhaps the {\i maamar} and {\i zika} do not render Rachel fully Shimon\u8217's wife, and she remains primarily Reuven\u8217's wife. In that case, Rachel and Sarah come from two separate brothers, and the act of {\i yibum} with one does not release the other. Sarah still requires her own {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}. Therefore Levi must perform {\i chalitza} with both women. Though {\i de\u8217'oraisa} he has an independent obligation toward each woman separately, the dual-status problem with Rachel prevents him from acting on either obligation through {\i yibum}, and the result is {\i chalitza} with both. The {\i pasuk} is invoked: {\i mi she-yesh lah zikas yavam echad} \u8212- a woman may be taken in {\i yibum} when she is bound to only one {\i yavam}, {\i ve-lo she-yesh lah zikas shnei yevamim} \u8212- not when she is bound to two {\i yavamin} simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Denies Dual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon, however, does not accept the premise of a {\i double zika} problem. {\i Meyabem le-eizo meihen she-yirtzeh ve-cholets la-shniya} \u8212- he performs {\i yibum} with whichever of the two women he chooses and {\i chalitza} with the other. Rabbi Shimon denies that Rachel possesses a genuinely dual status. But he does not therefore say one may perform {\i yibum} with both women, for a different reason: {\i dilma yesh zika}, and the combination of {\i zika} and {\i maamar} may render Rachel fully Shimon\u8217's wife, in which case she and Sarah are both from the same household. Therefore he cannot do {\i yibum} with both. But he can do {\i yibum} with one and {\i chalitza} with the other, because in his view Rachel does not have a double status \u8212- she has a {\i doubtful} status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Status Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: One vs Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Abaye: ve-lo shani lach bein zikas yavam echad le-zikas shnei yevamim?} Abaye attempts to preserve Rav Oshaya\u8217's framework by drawing a distinction. Perhaps Rabbi Shimon\u8217's {\i safek} about whether {\i zika ke-knusa damya} applies only when two {\i yevamin} are simultaneously in the picture \u8212- when Rachel has a {\i zika} to both Shimon and Levi at once, making it unclear to whom the {\i zika} primarily attaches. In that scenario of competing {\i zikot}, one cannot say with certainty that the {\i zika} has rendered her any particular man\u8217's wife. But {\i ki amar Rabbi Shimon zika ke-knusa damya}, Abaye suggests, that is in the case {\i be-yavam echad} \u8212- when there is only one {\i yavam}, and the {\i zika} is clear and unambiguous. In that case, the {\i zika} alone suffices to render her {\i ke-knusa}, fully as though she has entered the {\i yavam}\u8217's household.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This would preserve Rav Oshaya\u8217's position: in our {\i mishna}, when Levi has not yet been born, the {\i zika} is exclusively between Rachel and Shimon. There is only one {\i yavam} in the picture. In that context, {\i zika ke-knusa damya} operates cleanly, and when Levi is then born, Rachel is already fully regarded as Shimon\u8217's wife. The {\i safek} that Rabbi Shimon evinces in the three-brother case is a product of the dual {\i zika} problem, not of any inherent weakness in {\i zika} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Maamar Strength\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further. Perhaps one might argue that Rabbi Shimon indeed holds {\i zika ke-knusa damya} in a thoroughgoing {\i de\u8217'oraisa} sense \u8212- that Rachel is fully considered Shimon\u8217's wife {\i de\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- and the reason Levi must perform {\i chalitza} with Sarah is only a {\i derabbanan} enactment. {\i Mide\u8217'oraisa}, Levi could simply do {\i yibum} with Rachel and Sarah could be released without anything. But {\i miderabbanan hu de\u8217'asur}, the rabbis required {\i chalitza} with Sarah as well \u8212- {\i gezeira mishum she-yomru shtei yevamos ha-ba\u8217'os mi-shnei batim chada miyabema ve-idach mifatra be-la klum} \u8212- out of concern that people will say: if you have two {\i yevamot} from two different brothers, you need only perform {\i yibum} with one and may simply ignore the other. To avoid this appearance, the {\i rabbanan} imposed the requirement of {\i chalitza} with Sarah. Under this reading, Rabbi Shimon does hold {\i zika ke-knusa damya de\u8217'oraisa}, and the {\i chalitza} with Sarah is only a rabbinic safeguard against a misleading impression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek vs Dual Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this construction and establishes the correct understanding of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. {\i Ta\u8217'amei de-Rabbi Shimon mishum ma\u8217'amar ve-lav ma\u8217'amar hu} \u8212- the mechanism at work for Rabbi Shimon is genuinely whether the {\i maamar} constitutes a real {\i maamar} or not. It is a {\i safek}, a true legal doubt, not a {\i gezeira}. The proof comes from a {\i baraita} in which Rabbi Shimon articulates his reasoning directly: {\i amar lahem Rabbi Shimon la-chachamim, im ma\u8217'amaro shel sheini ma\u8217'amar, eishet sheini hu bo\u8217'el} \u8212- if Shimon\u8217's {\i maamar} with Rachel is legally operative, then Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel as Shimon\u8217's wife. {\i Ve-im ma\u8217'amaro shel sheini eino ma\u8217'amar, eishet rishon hu bo\u8217'el} \u8212- if the {\i maamar} is not operative, then Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel as Reuven\u8217's wife. Either way, Levi may perform {\i yibum} with Rachel. The {\i rabbanan}, by contrast, hold that Rachel\u8217's status is genuinely dual \u8212- she carries both relationships simultaneously \u8212- and therefore no {\i yibum} is possible with her at all. Rabbi Shimon denies the dual status: for him, Rachel is either Reuven\u8217's wife or Shimon\u8217's wife, and the {\i safek} is which of the two she is. It is therefore clear from Rabbi Shimon\u8217's own formulation that he harbors a genuine {\i safek} about Rachel\u8217's status, even when {\i zika} and {\i maamar} are both present. This confirms Rav Yosef\u8217's challenge: if {\i zika plus maamar} leaves Rabbi Shimon in doubt, how could {\i zika alone} definitively make Rachel {\i ke-knusa} and resolve the problem for Levi?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth Before Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kulah amar Rabbi Shimon: kol she-ha-leida kodemes le-nisuin, lo choletzes ve-lo mityabemes.} Rabbi Shimon articulates a general principle: whenever the birth of Levi precedes the {\i nisuin} \u8212- here, the {\i yibum} \u8212- Rachel requires neither {\i chalitza} nor {\i yibum}. She is forbidden to Levi as {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} and simply exits without any act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin Before Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i nisuin kodmin le-leida, o choletzes o mityabemes} \u8212- if first there was {\i yibum} with Rachel and only then Levi was born, then Levi has a full obligation toward Rachel: either {\i chalitza} or {\i yibum}, as in a standard case. This {\i baraita} appears to state Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position with crystalline clarity: the only variable that matters is whether Levi was born before or after {\i yibum}. If before, Rachel is forbidden; if after, she is fully permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: {\i mai lav be-yavam echad?} \u8212- presumably this {\i baraita} is speaking of a case with only one surviving brother (Shimon) at the time of Reuven\u8217's death, and Levi is then born afterward. If so, and Rabbi Shimon declares Rachel forbidden when Levi is born before {\i yibum}, this directly contradicts Rav Oshaya\u8217's claim that Rabbi Shimon would permit Rachel even in that {\i reisha} scenario on the basis of {\i zika ke-knusa damya}. One might have thought that {\i zika} alone \u8212- with only one {\i yavam} in the picture \u8212- suffices to render Rachel as Shimon\u8217's wife. Yet this {\i baraita} suggests Rabbi Shimon makes no such ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to salvage Rav Oshaya\u8217's framework: {\i lo, bi-shnei yevamin} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is not speaking of a scenario with only Shimon present before Levi\u8217's birth. Rather, it assumes two brothers (say, Shimon and Issachar) were both present when Reuven died. Because two {\i yevamin} simultaneously hold a {\i zika} to Rachel, the {\i zika} is ambiguous and cannot render Rachel clearly bound to either one. That is why Rachel remains a problem even according to Rabbi Shimon. But if only Shimon were present, his {\i zika} would operate unambiguously and Rachel would be {\i ke-knusa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth Timing Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ultimately finds this reading untenable. If the {\i baraita}\u8217's point were really about the number of {\i yevamin}, it would have said so explicitly. Moreover, the {\i baraita} opens {\i klal amar Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon stated a general principle. A {\i klal} should encompass all cases cleanly. The {\i klal} Rabbi Shimon announces is: birth before {\i nisuin} means forbidden, {\i nisuin} before birth means obligated. That is the only variable. Had Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling depended on whether one or two brothers were present, the {\i baraita} would have specified this condition. Its silence strongly indicates that the sole determinant is the timing of Levi\u8217's birth relative to {\i yibum}, regardless of how many brothers were in the picture. The {\i gemara} therefore concludes that the attempt to read Rav Oshaya\u8217's expanded position into Rabbi Shimon through this {\i baraita} has failed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Competing Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup Two Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i baraita} presents a distinct but related complication. {\i Shlosha achim: shnayim meihem nesu\u8217'im shtei achayot, o isha u-vita, o isha u-vat bita, o isha u-vat bina} \u8212- three brothers, two of whom are each married to one of two women who stand in a relationship of {\i ervah} to each other: sisters, a mother and her daughter, a woman and her granddaughter, and so on. These are women who cannot themselves be married to the same man simultaneously. The two brothers then die.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Yibum Either\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Levi now faces both women as {\i yevamot}. The difficulty is that he cannot simply perform {\i yibum} with one and release the other. If he has a {\i zika} to both, and then consummates with one of them, he has thereby become intimate with {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- the sister of a woman to whom he is legally bound \u8212- or with {\i em zekukato} or {\i bat zekukato}, depending on the specific relationship. This renders the consummation itself an act with a relative, even though he has not yet performed {\i yibum} with the second woman. The {\i zika} to the second creates the problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} therefore rules: {\i harei eilu choltzos ve-lo mityabemos} \u8212- both women perform {\i chalitza} and neither enters {\i yibum}. Both are released through {\i chalitza}, one from each brother\u8217's household.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon: Both Exempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon poter} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon exempts. His position is that when the two women are in a situation of mutual competition \u8212- {\i tzarot zo la-zo} \u8212- neither may be taken at all, and both are exempt from both {\i chalitza} and {\i yibum}. The {\i baraita} that later explains this derives it from the verse {\i ve-isha el achotah lo tikach li-tzror} \u8212- do not take a woman alongside her sister to create rivalry. {\i Be-sha\u8217'ah she-na\u8217'asu tzarot zo la-zo, lo yihiyu lekuchin afilu be-achat meihen} \u8212- at the moment when the two women stand as competing claimants, none may be taken, even one of them. This is Rabbi Shimon\u8217's principle: when the situation itself is one of competing {\i yevamot} who cannot both be taken, the entire {\i yibum} structure falls away and both women exit without any act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Amram Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Amram: mai poter nami, poter bi-shniya} \u8212- Rav Amram proposes a narrower reading of {\i Rabbi Shimon poter}. Rather than exempting both women entirely, Rabbi Shimon exempts only the second wife. Under this reading, Levi performs {\i yibum} with the first wife \u8212- the wife of whichever brother died first \u8212- and the second wife is simply released.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava Reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Multiple Pairs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava: shniya she-be-zug zeh ve-ha-shniya she-be-zug zeh} \u8212- Rava offers a strikingly different reading of the {\i baraita}\u8217's phrase {\i Rabbi Shimon poter bi-shteihen}. He proposes that the two brothers are not each married to one of a single problematic pair, but rather each is married to multiple pairs of wives. So Reuven and Shimon are simultaneously married to two sisters {\i and} to a mother and daughter {\i and} to further problematic combinations enumerated in the {\i baraita}. The phrase {\i be-zug zeh} refers to the second member of each pair, and Rabbi Shimon\u8217's exemption applies to the second woman from each pairing. Under this reading, {\i shteihen} \u8212- both \u8212- refers to both of the second wives, one from each of the two problematic pairs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Or Not And\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Katai Rava} \u8212- the {\i gemara} immediately rejects this. {\i Chada, de\u8217'o o katani} \u8212- the {\i baraita} uses \u8220"or, or, or\u8221": {\i sisters, or mother and daughter, or mother and granddaughter}. It is plainly a list of alternatives, not a cumulative conjunction. The two brothers are married to one of these problematic pairs, not all of them simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Should Say Four\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further objection reinforces this rejection: if Rava were correct that the brothers are married to multiple pairs \u8212- say, to two sisters {\i and} to a mother and daughter \u8212- then when Rabbi Shimon exempts {\i both}, he should have said all four women are exempt, not merely {\i shteihen}, two. The {\i baraita}\u8217's language of exempting {\i two} women only makes sense if there are two women in the scenario, not four.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzarot Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarot Cannot Be Taken\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-od tanya: Rabbi Shimon poter bi-shteihen min ha-chalitza u-min ha-yibum, she-ne\u8217'emar, ve-isha el achotah lo tikach li-tzror.} The additional {\i baraita} makes explicit that Rabbi Shimon exempts both women from both {\i chalitza} and {\i yibum}, deriving this from the verse prohibiting taking a woman alongside her sister to create rivalry. {\i Be-sha\u8217'ah she-na\u8217'asu tzarot zo la-zo, lo yihiyu lekuchin afilu be-achat meihen} \u8212- at the moment when the women become competitors, the entire framework of acquisition \u8212- whether through {\i yibum} or even {\i chalitza} \u8212- does not apply to either of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi: Simultaneous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ella amar Rav Ashi: i de-naful be-zeh achar zeh, hachi nami} \u8212- if Reuven died and then Shimon died sequentially, Rav Ashi concedes, one could indeed argue that Levi should follow the logic of {\i zika ke-knusa damya} and simply perform {\i yibum} with the first wife, releasing the second. {\i Hacha be-mai askinan de-naful be-vat achat} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is dealing with the case where both brothers died simultaneously, at exactly the same moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Like Yossi Glili\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon savar lah ke-Rabbi Yossi ha-Galili de-amar efshar le-tzamtzem} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds in accordance with the position of Rabbi Yossi ha-Galili, who maintains that truly simultaneous events are halachically possible and must be reckoned with. When both brothers die at the exact same moment, the question of which {\i zika} \u8220"came first\u8221" is literally unanswerable, and the competing status of the two women crystallizes instantaneously. It is in that uniquely simultaneous scenario that Rabbi Shimon applies the {\i tzarot} principle and exempts both women entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequential Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the deaths are sequential \u8212- one brother dying before the other \u8212- Rabbi Shimon would follow the logic of the first wife. The {\i zika} to the first wife is established first; if {\i zika ke-knusa damya}, she is already as though bound to Levi from that moment. When the second brother then dies, his wife encounters Levi in a status in which the first wife\u8217's {\i zika} already effectively occupies his household, rendering the second wife an {\i achot zekukato} or its equivalent. The {\i tzarot} exemption, with its simultaneous-death premise, is therefore not in tension with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's holding of {\i zika ke-knusa damya} in the sequential case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Papa Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Then Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Papa amar: be-yibum ve-achar kach nolad, palig Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- Rav Papa states clearly where Rabbi Shimon\u8217's dispute is actually located. Rabbi Shimon disagrees with the {\i tanna kamma} only in the case where {\i yibum} occurred first and Levi was born afterward \u8212- the {\i seifa}. In that case, Rabbi Shimon holds that Rachel is no longer {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} by the time Levi is born, and {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} applies normally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth Then Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Be-nolad ve-achar kach yibem, lo palig} \u8212- in the case where Levi was born first and {\i yibum} occurred afterward, Rabbi Shimon does not disagree. He concedes that Rachel is forbidden to Levi as {\i eishes achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}. This is precisely what the {\i baraita}\u8217's {\i klal} stated, and it is the straightforward {\i peshat} of the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejecting Rav Oshaya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rav Papa\u8217's formulation, the {\i gemara} definitively sets aside Rav Oshaya\u8217's elaborate reading. Rav Oshaya had argued that the {\i reisha} was taught solely to showcase Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position, implying that Rabbi Shimon would permit Rachel even when Levi was born before {\i yibum} \u8212- on the basis of {\i zika ke-knusa damya}. This argument, while initially clever, cannot survive the {\i baraita}\u8217's explicit statement that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's own {\i klal} turns entirely on the timing of birth relative to {\i nisuin}, with no special claim about {\i zika} overriding the {\i erva} status when Levi is born first. Rav Papa\u8217's {\i peshat} is the accepted reading: Rabbi Shimon\u8217's singular {\i chiddush} is the {\i seifa}, where {\i yibum} predates Levi\u8217's birth, and in that case alone does he say Rachel is permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 19b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Pappa\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens in the wake of a sustained engagement with Rabbi Oshaya\u8217's interpretation of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. Rabbi Oshaya had attempted to argue that Rabbi Shimon was unbothered by the concern of {\i eishet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} across all cases \u8212- a reading that proved conceptually difficult and straining against the {\i mishna}. We turn now to Rav Pappa, who offers the more measured and logically coherent position: Rabbi Shimon disagrees with the {\i rabbanim} only in a single, specific scenario \u8212- where Shimon performed {\i yibbum} with Rachel and only thereafter was Levi born. In that case, Rabbi Shimon maintains that once Rachel was already married to Shimon at the moment Levi entered the world, the connection to Reuven falls away and there is no problem. The {\i braita} confirms this reading, and the {\i gemara} notes that it simultaneously constitutes a refutation of Rabbi Oshaya\u8217's broader interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} begins: two brothers are alive at the same time, and one of them \u8212- Reuven \u8212- dies without a child. Shimon prepares to perform {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel, his {\i yevama}, but does not complete it before another brother, Levi, is born \u8212- and then Shimon dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ma\u8217'amar Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is Case A: Shimon did absolutely nothing with Rachel. He may have intended to perform {\i ma\u8217'amar}, but intention is legally irrelevant \u8212- what matters is whether the act was done. Rachel therefore remains in her original status as Reuven\u8217's widow, an {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} relative to Levi, who was born after Reuven\u8217's death. Since Shimon had no meaningful legal relationship with Rachel, her status does not impinge on Sarah\u8217's situation at all. The {\i braita}\u8217's ruling is therefore: Rachel is exempt \u8212- she exits under the category of {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- and Sarah, Shimon\u8217's actual wife, falls before Levi for either {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah}. The two women are entirely unrelated in their legal status, and Levi may proceed with Sarah without restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar Before Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Case B introduces the scenario where Shimon performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel at some point before his death \u8212- whether before or after Levi\u8217's birth is, for this analysis, immaterial; what matters is that {\i ma\u8217'amar} was done while Shimon was still alive. In this case, Rachel still exits on the grounds of {\i eishet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- Shimon never consummated the marriage, so she remains an {\i erva}. But the {\i ma\u8217'amar} creates a quasi-relationship: Rachel now has some form of attachment to Shimon, which generates a quasi-{\i tzarat ervah} status for Sarah. This quasi-relationship is not equivalent to full {\i yibbum} \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is a rabbinic institution, a form of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- but it is sufficient to prevent Levi from performing {\i yibbum} with Sarah. Since on the level of Torah obligation the {\i zikah} remains and {\i chalitzah} is still required, Levi must perform {\i chalitzah} with Sarah but may not perform {\i yibbum}. Rachel, meanwhile, simply walks away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the {\i gemara} notes the curious language of the {\i braita}: {\i amad lo hispik} \u8212- \u8220"he stood to do and did not have the chance.\u8221" If he did {\i ma\u8217'amar}, he did it; if not, he did not. Why introduce the narrative of intention and missed opportunity? The {\i gemara} explains: {\i amad} indicates that she had initially agreed, {\i lo hispik mida\u8217'atah} means that in the end she withdrew her consent \u8212- and then he performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} against her will, {\i be\u8217'al karchah}. This raises the fundamental question of whether {\i ma\u8217'amar} is to be understood through the rules of {\i kiddushin}, in which case consent is required, or through the rules of {\i yibbum}, in which case \u8212- since {\i yibbum} itself takes effect even {\i be\u8217'al karchah} \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'amar} might likewise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibbum Before Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Case C \u8212- actual {\i yibbum}, not merely {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- is the case that drives the central dispute. Here Shimon married Rachel and Sarah both: he performed {\i yibbum} with Rachel and was married to Sarah. The {\i braita} presents two sub-scenarios: {\i yibbum} occurred, then Shimon died, then Levi was born; or Levi was born, then {\i yibbum} occurred, then Shimon died. In both of those orderings, the ruling of the {\i tanna kamma} is that both women are exempt \u8212- from both {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibbum}. In the first sub-ordering, Rachel is {\i eshet Shimon shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} to Levi; in the second, she is {\i eshet Reuven shelo haya be\u8217'olamo}. In either case she is an {\i erva} to Levi, and Sarah, as her {\i tzarah}, is likewise exempt. Rabbi Shimon, however, dissents in precisely the third scenario \u8212- introduced separately \u8212- where {\i yibbum} occurred, then Levi was born, and then Shimon died. In that case, when Levi entered the world, Rachel was already Shimon\u8217's wife. She never stood before Levi for even a moment in a prohibited status. Therefore, Rabbi Shimon rules that Levi may perform {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} with whichever of the two women he chooses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Yibbum Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibbum \u8594 ? Birth \u8594 ? Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first of the three {\i yibbum} orderings, Shimon performs {\i yibbum} with Rachel, then dies, and only then is Levi born. When Levi enters the world, Rachel is {\i eshet Shimon shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- the wife of a brother, Shimon, who predated his existence. Everyone agrees this is a problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth \u8594 ? Yibbum \u8594 ? Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second ordering, Levi is born first, and only then does Shimon perform {\i yibbum} with Rachel, and then dies. When Levi was born, Shimon had not yet altered Rachel\u8217's status; she still bore the identity of Reuven\u8217's widow, as {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} with respect to Reuven. Shimon\u8217's subsequent {\i yibbum} does not retroactively change the status that was already fixed at the moment of Levi\u8217's birth. Everyone also agrees that this is a problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibbum \u8594 ? Birth \u8594 ? Death (\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third scenario is the exception: Shimon performs {\i yibbum} with Rachel, then Levi is born, and then Shimon dies. When Levi enters the world, Rachel is Shimon\u8217's wife. She has never stood before him in any prohibited status \u8212- {\i lo amda alav sha\u8217'ah achat be\u8217'issur}. Rabbi Shimon therefore rules that Levi may perform {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} with either woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks a pointed structural question: why does the {\i braita} treat the third scenario as a separate clause? If Rabbi Shimon disagreed with all three scenarios equally, as Rabbi Oshaya had maintained, the {\i braita} should have grouped all three together. The fact that the first two scenarios are presented jointly \u8212- and only the third is introduced as a distinct case over which Rabbi Shimon dissents \u8212- proves that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's disagreement is confined to that third scenario alone. {\i Shema mina}: Rabbi Shimon disputes the ruling only when {\i yibbum} preceded Levi\u8217's birth, not when Levi was born before {\i yibbum} was performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rachel\u8217's Status Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Continuity Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises: if Rachel was already married to Shimon through {\i yibbum}, why should she continue to carry any legal identity as Reuven\u8217's widow in relation to Levi? Is she not now fully Shimon\u8217's wife?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual crux is this: at the moment of Levi\u8217's birth, a legal status attaches to him regarding Rachel. If {\i yibbum} occurred before he was born, then when he arrived in the world, Rachel was Shimon\u8217's wife \u8212- and that is how he encounters her. But if he was born before {\i yibbum} occurred, then at the moment of his birth, Rachel was Reuven\u8217's childless widow \u8212- and that status, {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo}, is fixed with respect to him from that moment forward. The subsequent act of Shimon\u8217's {\i yibbum} does not undo what was already legally established. The status attaches to the woman relative to Levi at the time of his birth, and it does not dissolve simply because circumstances later change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Retention Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle receives further elaboration in the {\i gemara}. The {\i mishna} elsewhere states that after {\i yibbum}, {\i harei hi k\u8217'ishto l\u8217'chol davar} \u8212- she is fully like his wife in every respect. Rabbi Yose the son of Rabbi Chanina explains: this means he may divorce her with a {\i get} and remarry her. Yet if she is still somehow to be considered Reuven\u8217's widow, {\i chalitzah} \u8212- not {\i get} \u8212- would have been required to dissolve any such bond. The {\i gemara} resolves the tension through the principle of {\i shadi heiteira a\u8217'heiteira v\u8217'shadi issura a\u8217'issura}: where the legal context is entirely permissible \u8212- as in the case of divorce and remarriage after {\i yibbum} \u8212- we apply the more lenient framing and treat her fully as Shimon\u8217's wife, without importing the complication of her prior status as Reuven\u8217's widow. Where, however, the question involves a potential {\i issur ervah} \u8212- namely, her status relative to Levi \u8212- that prior status continues to obtain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: does this not mean that a woman carries two statuses simultaneously? The answer, as the {\i gemara} makes clear, is precisely yes \u8212- at least for the purposes of the relevant {\i issur}. Rachel never loses her identity as {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} relative to Levi, regardless of her subsequent marriage to Shimon. In the domain of {\i issur}, that earlier status persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar Institution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to Case A of the {\i braita}, the {\i gemara} questions the unusual language: {\i amad hasheni la\u8217'asot ma\u8217'amar\u8230 ? velo hispik}. If he did {\i ma\u8217'amar}, he did it; if not, he did not \u8212- why describe an intention that was not fulfilled? The {\i gemara} explains that the language conveys a specific scenario: initially, Rachel agreed to the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and then, before it could be executed with her consent, she withdrew her agreement. At that point, Shimon performed the {\i ma\u8217'amar} {\i be\u8217'al karchah} \u8212- without her acquiescence. The question then becomes whether such a {\i ma\u8217'amar} is legally effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar = Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ma\u8217'amar} is a rabbinic institution \u8212- Chazal required the {\i yavam} to perform an act of {\i kiddushin} before proceeding to {\i yibbum}. As the Rambam formulates it: by rabbinic enactment, the {\i yavam} may not consummate the {\i yibbum} until he has consecrated her in front of two witnesses with a {\i perutah} or its equivalent in value \u8212- and this is called {\i ma\u8217'amar}. It is thus structurally equivalent to a standard {\i kiddushin}: money, witnesses, and \u8212- at least according to the {\i chachamim} \u8212- her consent. The dispute between Rebbi and the {\i chachamim} turns on precisely this last requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbi\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi holds that {\i ma\u8217'amar} performed without her consent is nonetheless legally effective. His reasoning: {\i gamar mi-bi\u8217'ah de-yevamah} \u8212- he derives the rule by analogy to the act of {\i yibbum} itself. Just as {\i yibbum} takes halakhic effect even against her will \u8212- since the {\i zikah} already constitutes a kind of pre-existing quasi-bond \u8212- so too the {\i ma\u8217'amar} that precedes it takes effect even without her agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Disagree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} disagree and rule that a {\i ma\u8217'amar} performed without her consent is legally null: {\i lo asah klum}. Their reasoning: {\i gamri mi-kiddushin de-alma} \u8212- since Chazal instituted {\i ma\u8217'amar} as a form of {\i kiddushin}, it must follow the rules of ordinary {\i kiddushin}, which requires her consent. A woman cannot be consecrated against her will: {\i ein ishah miskadeshes ela l\u8217'rtzonah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar Classification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying dispute, the {\i gemara} frames explicitly: one view holds that since {\i ma\u8217'amar} is a {\i yevamah} institution, it should be governed by {\i yevamah} rules \u8212- and {\i yibbum} works even {\i be\u8217'al karchah}. The other view holds that since what Chazal created is a form of {\i kiddushin}, it must be governed by {\i kiddushin} rules \u8212- and {\i kiddushin} requires her consent. Our {\i braita}, by treating a {\i ma\u8217'amar} performed without consent as ineffective, follows the position of the {\i chachamim} and not Rebbi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar + Birth Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Scenario B involves {\i ma\u8217'amar} performed by Shimon with Rachel \u8212- whether before or after Levi\u8217's birth is immaterial \u8212- followed by Shimon\u8217's death. Rachel exits under the banner of {\i eishet achiv shelo haya be\u8217'olamo}; she was never fully married to Shimon and remains an {\i erva}. But because of the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, a quasi-relationship exists between Rachel and Shimon, which creates a quasi-{\i tzarat ervah} with respect to Sarah. The {\i rabbanim} rule: Sarah may only perform {\i chalitzah}, not {\i yibbum}, with Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All or Nothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position here is distinctive. He holds that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is not a middle category that creates a quasi-relationship \u8212- it is either fully equivalent to {\i yibbum} or it is nothing at all. If {\i ma\u8217'amar} counts as {\i yibbum} and it was performed before Levi\u8217's birth, then when Levi arrives, Rachel is already Shimon\u8217's wife, and Levi may perform {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} with either woman. If {\i ma\u8217'amar} counts as nothing, then Rachel is entirely out of the picture \u8212- she is {\i eshet Reuven shelo haya be\u8217'olamo} and completely irrelevant \u8212- and Sarah is fully available to Levi without any complication from Rachel. Either way, the focus should be on Sarah. Rabbi Shimon therefore rules: {\i bi\u8217'aso o chalitzaso shel achad meihen poteres tzarasah} \u8212- the {\i bi\u8217'ah} or {\i chalitzah} performed with one of them \u8212- specifically Sarah \u8212- exempts the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what if Levi were to direct his attention to Rachel \u8212- performing {\i chalitzah} with the {\i ba\u8217'alas ma\u8217'amar} rather than with Sarah? The {\i gemara} rules: {\i chalatz l\u8217'va\u8217'alas ma\u8217'amar lo nifterah tzarah} \u8212- {\i chalitzah} to the {\i ma\u8217'amar} woman does not free the {\i tzarah}. The reasoning: Sarah\u8217's status as Shimon\u8217's wife and her obligation to Levi is {\i vadai} \u8212- certain. Rachel\u8217's status, by contrast, is a {\i safek} \u8212- she is either completely Shimon\u8217's wife or completely irrelevant. {\i V\u8217'ein safeik motzi miyedei vaday} \u8212- a matter of doubt cannot override a certainty. Directing the act of {\i chalitzah} toward Rachel, whose very eligibility is uncertain, accomplishes nothing for Sarah, whose standing is clear. Therefore, whatever is done must be done with Sarah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzaras Ailonit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klal\u8217's Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's concluding formulation \u8212- {\i klal amru} \u8212- is taken by the {\i gemara} as signaling that something additional is being included beyond the cases explicitly enumerated. The question is: what?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Excludes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa offers one reading: the {\i klal} comes to include the case of {\i tzaras ailonit} \u8212- the {\i tzarah} of a woman who is an {\i ailonit}, a woman physiologically incapable of bearing children. Since there is no {\i mitzvas yibbum} with an {\i ailonit} \u8212- the entire purpose of {\i yibbum} being to perpetuate the deceased brother\u8217's name through offspring \u8212- her {\i tzarah} is likewise excluded from {\i yibbum} and {\i chalitzah}. This follows the position of Rav Assi. Rav Pappa acknowledges, however, that this is not the accepted ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Interpretati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second reading of the {\i klal} goes in the opposite direction. According to this view, the {\i mishna} is specifying that the prohibition on the {\i tzarah} applies only where the woman herself is an {\i issur ervah} \u8212- {\i kol she-issurah issur ervah hi d\u8217'assira tzarasah}. The implication: if the woman is excluded not because she is an {\i erva} but only because there is no {\i mitzvas yibbum} \u8212- as in the case of an {\i ailonit} \u8212- then her {\i tzarah} is not forbidden. This is the operative ruling: an {\i ailonit} is excluded from {\i yibbum} not on account of {\i ervah} but because there is no {\i mitzvah} to perform, and therefore her {\i tzarah} remains entirely permitted. This reading of the {\i klal} thus excludes {\i tzaras ailonit} from the prohibition \u8212- the inverse of Rav Assi\u8217's position \u8212- and this is indeed how we rule in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status Permanence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Erasure Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to examine the broader principle at stake in the dispute between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i rabbanim} regarding Rachel\u8217's status after {\i yibbum}. Once Shimon has married Rachel through {\i yibbum}, does her prior identity as Reuven\u8217's widow simply disappear? The {\i rabbanim} maintain that it does not \u8212- at least for the purpose of determining Levi\u8217's relationship to her \u8212- and they derive this from a careful reading of the relevant {\i pasuk}: {\i v\u8217'lekachah lo l\u8217'isha v\u8217'yibmah} \u8212- the Torah states both that he takes her as a wife and that he performs {\i yibbum}. The double formulation indicates that even after she becomes Shimon\u8217's wife, her identity as a {\i yevamah} \u8212- connected to Reuven \u8212- continues to persist. {\i Adayin yibbum me\u8217'harishonim aleha.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Not Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a difficulty: if Rachel perpetually retains some status as Reuven\u8217's widow, then when Shimon wishes to divorce her after {\i yibbum}, should he not be required to perform {\i chalitzah} rather than give a {\i get}? The {\i mishna} states explicitly that after {\i yibbum} she is fully like his wife in every sense \u8212- {\i k\u8217'ishto l\u8217'chol davar} \u8212- and Rabbi Yose the son of Rabbi Chanina explains that this means he may divorce her with a {\i get} and subsequently remarry her. But if she remains Reuven\u8217's widow in any meaningful sense, {\i chalitzah} would seem to be the appropriate mechanism. The tension is palpable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shadi Heiteira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty through the principle of {\i shadi heiteira a\u8217'heiteira v\u8217'shadi issura a\u8217'issura}: apply permissive rulings to permissive contexts, and apply restrictive rulings to restrictive contexts. When the question involves an entirely permissible situation \u8212- Shimon married Rachel lawfully through {\i yibbum} and now wishes to divorce her in an equally permissible context \u8212- we apply the lenient framing and treat her fully as his wife, subject only to {\i get}. We do not introduce the additional complication of Reuven\u8217's prior claim into a context where no {\i issur} is at stake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'ah M\u8217'Tzad Heiteira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when Levi was born after {\i yibbum} and the question is whether Rachel constitutes an {\i issur} to him, we apply the restrictive framing: her status as {\i eshet achiv} \u8212- connected to Reuven \u8212- remains operative, and she is an {\i erva} to Levi. This is the principle of {\i ba\u8217'ah m\u8217'tzad heiteira} that Rabbi Shimon invokes from the other direction: since when Levi was born she came to him entirely from a position of permission \u8212- she was already Shimon\u8217's wife \u8212- she never stood before him even for a moment in a prohibited status, and therefore there is no {\i issur} to sustain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: if it is true that once Rachel married Shimon her prior status as Reuven\u8217's widow is dissolved relative to Levi, then by the same logic, a sister who is also a {\i yevamah} should likewise lose her forbidden status as a sister once she marries Shimon through {\i yibbum} \u8212- and then when Levi is born, she should be fully permitted to him. Why not apply the same reasoning? The {\i gemara} answers decisively: {\i hai issur delait lei heiteira} \u8212- the prohibition of a sister is absolute and permanent; it has no mechanism for dissolution. {\i Hai issur de\u8217'it lei heiteira} \u8212- the prohibition of a dead brother\u8217's wife, by contrast, is inherently dissolvable; the entire {\i masechta Yevamot} is premised on the fact that under certain circumstances that {\i erva} status is overridden by {\i yibbum}. Because the {\i issur} of {\i eshet achiv} admits of a {\i heter}, once she marries Shimon we no longer look at her through the lens of that prior {\i issur}. But sisterhood, carrying no such {\i heter}, never dissolves \u8212- and the sister analogy therefore does not hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Women Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rule Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} formulates a comprehensive principle: {\i kol shehi issur ervah lo choletzet velo mityabemet} \u8212- any woman who is a genuine {\i erva} neither performs {\i chalitzah} nor {\i yibbum}, and her {\i tzarah} likewise exits entirely. Three categories of lesser {\i issurim} are then distinguished: {\i issur mitzvah}, {\i issur kedushah}, and the case of a sister who is simultaneously a {\i yevamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isur Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Issur mitzvah} refers to {\i shniyot mi-divrei soferim} \u8212- secondary {\i arayos} that are forbidden by rabbinic enactment. Because such a prohibition is {\i de-rabbanan} in character, and since {\i kiddushin} with a woman forbidden by a rabbinic prohibition is binding {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, there remains a {\i zikah} in a {\i yibbum} context and therefore an obligation of {\i chalitzah}. The woman thus performs {\i chalitzah} but not {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isur Kedushah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Issur kedushah} refers to prohibitions arising from the laws of {\i kehunah}: an {\i almanah l\u8217'Kohen Gadol}, a {\i gerushah} or {\i chalutzah} to an ordinary {\i kohen}, or a {\i mamzeret} married to a {\i yisrael} \u8212- and vice versa, a daughter of Israel married to a {\i mamzer}. Since these are {\i lavin} rather than {\i arayot}, the {\i kiddushin} is binding {\i bedi\u8217'avad} and a {\i zikah} exists; {\i chalitzah} is therefore required, but {\i yibbum} is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister-Yevama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i achotah shehi yevamtah} \u8212- a sister who is also a sister-in-law \u8212- arises when two brothers married two sisters. For example, Reuven married Rachel, Levi married Leah, and both Reuven and Levi died. Now Rachel and Leah stand together before a third brother, Zevulun. Since he has a quasi-{\i kiddushin} \u8212- a {\i zikah} \u8212- with both women simultaneously, consummating a relationship with one would constitute intimacy with {\i achos zikukaso}, the sister of his quasi-wife. He therefore cannot perform {\i yibbum} with either. However, if one of the two women is an {\i erva} to Zevulun \u8212- say, she is his daughter \u8212- then she exits the picture entirely, and he may perform {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibbum} with the other. The {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling of {\i choletzet o mityabemet} thus applies where the one who is the {\i erva} frees the other from the problem of {\i achos zikukaso}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 22\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Great-Aunts & Sixteen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Great-Aunt Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens with a query sent by Rav Mesharshiya of Tusanya to Rav Pappi: {\i eishet achi avi ha\u8217'av v\u8217'achot avi ha\u8217'av mahu} \u8212- what is the status of one\u8217's great-aunt, whether she be the sister of one\u8217's grandfather or the wife of one\u8217's grandfather\u8217's brother? Having already worked through the standard list of {\i shniyot l\u8217'arayot} and touched on the question of {\i yesh lahem hefsek} or {\i ein lahem hefsek} \u8212- whether the secondary prohibitions continue across multiple generations \u8212- the {\i gemara} now turns to flesh out the list further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To situate the question: the {\i Torah} itself forbids a man\u8217's aunt who is his father\u8217's sister ({\i erva de\u8217'oraita}), and equally the wife of his father\u8217's paternal brother. These are {\i arayot} on the level of biblical law. What the {\i gemara} is now asking concerns the exact same structural relationship, but shifted one generation upward \u8212- not one\u8217's father\u8217's sister but one\u8217's grandfather\u8217's sister, not one\u8217's father\u8217's paternal brother\u8217's wife but one\u8217's grandfather\u8217's brother\u8217's wife. In other words, the great-aunt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira for Great-Aunts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is framed as follows: {\i midelemata ervah, lemala nami gazru bah} \u8212- since at the generation directly below this scenario the relationship constitutes a biblical {\i ervah}, do we say that the Rabbis likewise extended the prohibition one generation upward to the grandfather\u8217's generation? Or do we say, {\i hai palig dara} \u8212- the generations have grown too distant, and therefore no such {\i gezeira} was enacted?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is somewhat striking, given that the {\i gemara} elsewhere does not seem particularly troubled by adding additional generations, particularly in the context of {\i ein lahem hefsek}. Nevertheless, the {\i gemara} here treats the question as genuinely open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Omission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first attempts to resolve the question from the {\i mishnah} or {\i baraita} that enumerated the {\i shniyot}: since great-aunts were not listed there, perhaps they are not forbidden. The {\i gemara} rejects this reasoning: {\i tana v\u8217'shayer} \u8212- the list was incomplete, and one cannot draw inferences from omissions. The question then becomes: {\i mai shayer d\u8217'hai shayer} \u8212- what else was left off? And the answer is: {\i shayer shniyot d\u8217'bei Rabbi Chiya} \u8212- the additional secondary prohibitions of Rabbi Chiya\u8217's school, which we will encounter momentarily. Since we know the list is incomplete, the absence of great-aunts proves nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimar & Sixteen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar, in practice, permitted great-aunts \u8212- {\i achshar b\u8217'eishet achi avi aviv u\u8217'bachot avi aviv}. However, Rav Hillel then reported to Rav Ashi that he had personally seen a list of secondary {\i arayot} from the house of Mar brei de-Ravna, and that list recorded precisely sixteen prohibitions as forbidden \u8212- {\i u\u8217'chtivan shitser l\u8217'issura}. This becomes the basis for an attempt to establish that great-aunts are indeed included in the count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sixteen Count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The counting proceeds as follows. The {\i baraita} discussed the previous day enumerated eight. Rabbi Chiya\u8217's school, as we are about to see, adds six more. That yields fourteen. For the total to reach sixteen, the remaining two must be precisely the two forms of great-aunt: one\u8217's grandfather\u8217's sister and one\u8217's grandfather\u8217's brother\u8217's wife. This would account for the full number of sixteen, and would mean Ameimar\u8217's lenient ruling is incorrect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seventeen vs Sixteen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects: {\i l\u8217'ta\u8217'amecha, shivsar havyan} \u8212- by your reasoning, the count should reach seventeen, not sixteen. The previous day\u8217's discussion had concluded that the wife of one\u8217's maternal uncle on the purely maternal side \u8212- {\i eishet achi ha\u8217'em min ha\u8217'em} \u8212- is also forbidden, an addition beyond the original eight. If that addition stands, then even without great-aunts the list exceeds fourteen, and adding two forms of great-aunt would yield seventeen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i hanach tarti d\u8217'damyan l\u8217'hadadi k\u8217'chada chashiv lehu} \u8212- the two that resemble one another are counted as one. Rashi explains that the two versions of great-aunt \u8212- grandfather\u8217's sister and grandfather\u8217's brother\u8217's wife \u8212- are both paternal-side great-aunts and are sufficiently similar to be grouped as a single entry. With that adjustment, the arithmetic works out to sixteen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then deflates the entire line of reasoning. Rav Ashi responds to Rav Hillel: even if one saw such a list written, if it had stated the opposite \u8212- that great-aunts are {\i permitted} \u8212- would one have relied upon it? After all, the document was not signed by Mar brei de-Ravna himself; its authority is unknown. {\i De-Mar brei de-Ravna mi chatim aleiyhu} \u8212- Mar brei de-Ravna did not affix his signature to it. Just as one would not rely on an unsigned document to be lenient, one cannot rely on it to be strict. The attempt to establish a {\i halakha} about great-aunts from this source therefore fails, and the question remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Chiya\u8217's List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Two-Generation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the list of Rabbi Chiya\u8217's school, which had been invoked in the preceding discussion. To understand what Rabbi Chiya adds, one must first appreciate the baseline. The {\i Torah} itself already prohibits relationships spanning two generations: a man may not marry a woman and also her granddaughter, and he may not marry his own granddaughter. Similarly, a man may not marry his wife\u8217's grandmother. These two-generation prohibitions are {\i arayot de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Great-Granddaughters Down\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya\u8217's innovation begins with the ruling {\i shlishi she-bivno ve-she-bivito} \u8212- the third generation downward through one\u8217's son or through one\u8217's daughter. The key {\i chiddush} is this: when the {\i Torah} prohibits one\u8217's granddaughter, it forbids the second generation going straight down through daughters \u8212- {\i bat bito} and {\i bat beno} are both explicitly biblical. If one continued downward through daughters alone, that would simply be an application of {\i ein lahem hefsek}. But Rabbi Chiya addresses a different case: where the second generation is a son \u8212- a grandson \u8212- and then the third generation produces a granddaughter. At the second generation, there is no biblical prohibition, since a grandson\u8217's relationship to his grandfather does not generate a restriction. Yet Rabbi Chiya teaches that the great-granddaughter \u8212- the third generation, even where the intervening link is a grandson \u8212- is nevertheless forbidden as a {\i shnia}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi makes this precise: {\i shlishi she-bivno} refers to {\i bat ben beno} \u8212- one\u8217's son\u8217's son\u8217's daughter; and {\i shlishi she-bivito} refers to {\i bat ben bito} \u8212- one\u8217's daughter\u8217's son\u8217's daughter. In both cases, the second generation is a son ({\i ben}), breaking the direct daughter-to-daughter chain, yet the third generation great-granddaughter is still rabbinically prohibited. This accounts for two of Rabbi Chiya\u8217's six additions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Great-Granddaught\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next two entries are parallel, but applied to one\u8217's wife. The {\i Torah} prohibits a man from marrying a woman along with her granddaughter \u8212- which means, structurally, one may not marry one\u8217's wife\u8217's granddaughter, whether through the wife\u8217's son or through the wife\u8217's daughter. Those are {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibitions. But again, where the second generation is a grandson \u8212- the wife\u8217's grandson \u8212- the biblical prohibition does not attach at that level. Rabbi Chiya extends the prohibition to the wife\u8217's great-granddaughter in such a case: {\i she-be-ven ishto ve-she-be-bat ishto} \u8212- whether the great-granddaughter descends through the wife\u8217's son or through the wife\u8217's daughter, she is rabbinically forbidden. This yields two more entries, for a running total of four.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Great-Grandmother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final two entries of Rabbi Chiya\u8217's list extend the prohibitions upward rather than downward: {\i revi\u8217'i she-be-chamiv ve-she-be-chamoto} \u8212- the fourth generation ascending through one\u8217's father-in-law or through one\u8217's mother-in-law. Here too the {\i Torah} already forbids a man from marrying a woman\u8217's grandmother, meaning one\u8217's wife\u8217's grandmother is biblically forbidden. Going one generation further upward yields one\u8217's wife\u8217's great-grandmother. Rabbi Chiya teaches that this too is rabbinically prohibited, whether the great-grandmother descends through the wife\u8217's paternal line or her maternal line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} is most significant when the great-grandmother is reached through a grandfather \u8212- that is, where the intervening generation is a grandfather rather than a grandmother, at which level there is no independent biblical prohibition \u8212- yet even then, the great-grandmother is a {\i shnia}. Rashi describes these cases specifically in terms of the mother of the mother of the father-in-law ({\i em em chamiv}) and the mother of the mother of the mother-in-law ({\i em em chamoto}), though one might equally apply the reasoning to the paternal grandfather\u8217's mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These final two entries complete Rabbi Chiya\u8217's list of six additions: two forms of one\u8217's own great-granddaughters, two forms of one\u8217's wife\u8217's great-granddaughters, and two forms of one\u8217's wife\u8217's great-grandmothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generation Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina posed a question to Rav Ashi about the terminology: {\i mai shna lemala de-chashiv lei ishto, mai shna lemata de-lo kachashiv lei ishto} \u8212- why when counting upward is one\u8217's wife counted as generation one (making her great-grandmother \u8220"generation four\u8221"), while when counting downward one is not counted as generation zero (making one\u8217's great-granddaughter \u8220"generation three\u8221")?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi explains: {\i lemala, de-issura mikoch ishto ka\u8217'ati chashiv lah} \u8212- going upward, the prohibition derives entirely from one\u8217's relationship to one\u8217's wife; she is the anchor of the relationship, and so she is counted as generation one, her mother as two, her grandmother as three, her great-grandmother as four. {\i Lemata, de-issura lav mikoch ishto ka\u8217'ati, lo kachashiv lah} \u8212- going downward, one\u8217's own great-granddaughter, the prohibition does not stem from the wife; one counts beginning with the first generation after oneself, so son or daughter is generation one, grandchild generation two, great-grandchild generation three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a difficulty: what of one\u8217's wife\u8217's great-granddaughters? That prohibition also derives from one\u8217's relationship to the wife \u8212- {\i de-issura mikoch ishto ka\u8217'ati} \u8212- so by the same logic, the wife should be counted as generation one, making the great-granddaughter generation four, not three. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i aidei de-tani tlat daragei lemata didei} \u8212- since when we counted generations below oneself we called it \u8220"three\u8221" and did not count ourselves, we likewise do not count the wife when going below her, and so her great-granddaughter is called \u8220"three\u8221" as well, for consistency of formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefsek Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi poses the question to Avimi of Hagronya: {\i shniyot de-Rabbi Chiya yesh lahen hefsek o ein lahen hefsek} \u8212- do Rabbi Chiya\u8217's additional prohibitions have a cutoff, or do they continue indefinitely? The {\i gemara} considers a proof from Rav\u8217's statement that there are four women whose {\i shniyot} prohibitions stop \u8212- {\i arba nashim yesh lahen hefsek} \u8212- and since Rabbi Chiya\u8217's categories were not among those four, perhaps they continue without limit. The {\i gemara} deflects: perhaps Rav was only speaking about the original {\i baraita} of eight, and Rabbi Chiya\u8217's list may have its own cutoff.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An attempt is then made from Rabbi Chiya\u8217's own language: he specified {\i shlishi} \u8212- the third generation below \u8212- and {\i revi\u8217'i} \u8212- the fourth generation above. These are specific numbers, suggesting the prohibition stops there. The {\i gemara} again is unconvinced: {\i dilma mi-shlishi va-eilakh u-me-revi\u8217'i va-eilakh} \u8212- perhaps \u8220"third\u8221" means starting from the third generation onward, not limited to it. The question of {\i hefsek} for Rabbi Chiya\u8217's list is thus left without resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Converts & Shniyot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Converts Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A rabbi who came from the west \u8212- from {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- reported to Rava, who conveyed the matter to Rav Nachman: {\i be\u8217'ei be-ma\u8217'arva} \u8212- they posed the following question in {\i Eretz Yisrael}: {\i gazru shniyot be-gerim} \u8212- did the Rabbis extend the secondary {\i arayot} prohibitions to converts? The question arises from the principle of {\i kegar she-nitgayer ke-katan she-nolad dami} \u8212- a convert is legally as a newborn child, severed from prior family relationships. But does this principle interact with the rabbinic secondary prohibitions, and if so, how?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eretz Yisrael Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling that emerges from {\i Eretz Yisrael} is that {\i shniyot} do not apply to converts. To explain why, the {\i gemara} first addresses the more basic question of biblical {\i arayot} for converts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holiness Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even at the level of biblical {\i arayot}, a convert is technically no longer related to his pre-conversion family members. A convert and his sister who converted together could, strictly speaking, marry one another. Yet the Rabbis prohibited this. The {\i gemara} explains: {\i ee lav d\u8217'lo yomru banu mi-kdusha chamura li-kdusha kala} \u8212- were it not for the concern that people would say \u8220"we moved from a stricter religion to a more lenient one,\u8221" the Rabbis would not have enacted the prohibition at all. The prohibition of biblical-level {\i arayot} for converts was thus a targeted decree, motivated entirely by the concern that converts not perceive Judaism as less holy than their religion of origin, in which such unions were also forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shniyot Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this reasoning, the {\i gemara} concludes that {\i shniyot} present no problem: {\i shniyot miba\u8217'ya} \u8212- is there even a question? A non-Jew is not bound by the rabbinic {\i shniyot}. If a convert\u8217's great-great-grandmother were to convert along with him, she was never forbidden to him even as a non-Jew. There is therefore no appearance of leniency \u8212- no one will say Judaism permits something that his prior religion forbade. Accordingly, {\i shniyot} are entirely permitted to converts. The concern of {\i kedusha kala mi-kedusha chamura} does not arise, and the technical principle of {\i ke-katan she-nolad dami} operates without restriction in this domain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Convert Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maternal Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the discussion has turned to converts, Rav Nachman adds a related ruling: {\i achi min ha\u8217'em lo ya\u8217'idu} \u8212- brothers on the maternal side who have both converted may not testify together {\i lechatchila}. However, {\i v\u8217'im he\u8217'idu, edutan edut} \u8212- if they did testify together {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, their testimony is valid, since {\i ke-katan she-nolad dami} renders them legally unrelated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternal Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, {\i achi min ha\u8217'av me\u8217'idin lechatchila} \u8212- paternal brothers who have both converted may testify together from the outset, without any {\i lechatchila} restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bio Relationship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is somewhat counterintuitive. Throughout the laws of {\i arayot}, paternal relationships carry greater legal weight than maternal ones. Why, then, is the maternal bond treated more stringently here for purposes of testimony? The answer lies not in legal weight but in biological visibility. When two people share the same mother, the biological relationship is plain and apparent to all \u8212- there is a clear, enduring physical bond. Even after conversion, it is obvious to observers that these two individuals are biological brothers. The concern is therefore that it will appear as though related parties are testifying together, even though {\i halakhically} they are not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Paternal brotherhood, by contrast, rests not merely on biology but substantially on the social construct of family membership \u8212- being recognized as part of the same father\u8217's household. That social construct dissolves upon conversion in a more complete and perceptible way. The {\i halakhic} severance is therefore more evident to onlookers, and there is less concern that allowing paternal brothers to testify will create a false appearance of impropriety. Rashi notes in this context that among non-Jews, the maternal bond carried particular significance even prior to conversion, which further explains why the {\i lechatchila} restriction attaches specifically to maternal brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes further: {\i afilu hachi min em nami me\u8217'idim lechatchila} \u8212- even maternal brothers who have converted may testify together from the outset. This is because testimony is a court-controlled matter, not a private one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayot Concerns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a formal distinction: {\i erva lechol mesura} \u8212- marital decisions are made by individuals and are not subject to court supervision. If a convert were permitted to marry his sister, other people might follow suit and believe they could do the same. The prohibition must therefore be maintained as an absolute rule, regardless of whether those particular individuals know they are permitted. Testimony, by contrast, is {\i edut levei dina mesura} \u8212- entirely under the jurisdiction of the {\i beit din}. The court knows that {\i kegar she-nitgayer ke-katan she-nolad dami} and that converts are not {\i halakhically} related. There is therefore no risk of error spreading, and even maternal brothers may testify together {\i lechatchila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This represents a shift from the earlier concern \u8212- where the issue was the convert\u8217's perception of relative religious stringency \u8212- to a new concern about the potential for the public to draw incorrect inferences. The two rationales operate in different domains, which is why the {\i gemara}\u8217's application differs between {\i arayot} and testimony.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brother & Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Any Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i mi she-yesh lo ach mikol makom, zokek et eshet achiv l\u8217'yibum} \u8212- whoever has any type of brother, that brother creates a {\i yibum} obligation upon the widow in the event of death. The {\i mishnah} adds that the brother is considered a full brother {\i l\u8217'chol davar} \u8212- for all purposes \u8212- with one exception: a half-brother born of a non-Jewish woman or a slave-woman is not considered a legal brother and does not create the {\i yibum} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mikol makom la\u8217'atuyei mai} \u8212- what does the phrase \u8220"any type\u8221" come to include? Rav Yehuda answers: {\i la\u8217'atuyei mamzer} \u8212- it includes even a brother who is a {\i mamzer}. That is, if one\u8217's father had a child through a forbidden union, producing a {\i mamzer}, that {\i mamzer} half-brother still creates a {\i yibum} obligation upon his brother\u8217's widow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought to exclude the {\i mamzer} by applying a {\i gezeira shava} from the brothers of Yaakov \u8212- {\i shneim asar achim anachnu} \u8212- reasoning that just as those brothers were all legally fit ({\i kesharim}), the term \u8220"brother\u8221" in the context of {\i yibum} should likewise be restricted to fit ({\i kasher}) individuals, excluding a {\i mamzer}. The {\i gemara} rejects this: since a {\i mamzer} son exempts his father\u8217's wife from {\i yibum} \u8212- as we are about to see \u8212- it follows that {\i mamzer} status has full relevance within the laws of {\i yibum}, and therefore a {\i mamzer} brother likewise creates the {\i yibum} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Full Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further states that the {\i mamzer} brother is considered a full brother {\i l\u8217'chol davar} \u8212- for all purposes. The {\i gemara} identifies two concrete applications: {\i l\u8217'yarsho} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} brother inherits from his brother just as any other brother would \u8212- and {\i l\u8217'tamei lo} \u8212- a {\i kohen} whose brother is a {\i mamzer} is still obligated to render himself {\i tamei} for the burial of that brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second application requires elaboration. One might have thought to exclude it based on the verse {\i ki im l\u8217'she\u8217'ero ha-karov eilav} \u8212- a {\i kohen} renders himself {\i tamei} only for close flesh \u8212- and the teaching that {\i she\u8217'ero} refers to one\u8217's wife. Since a {\i kohen} does not render himself {\i tamei} for a wife who is a {\i grusha}, one might analogize: just as the {\i kohen} is not {\i metamei} for a wife of disqualified status, so too he should not be {\i metamei} for a brother of disqualified status such as a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kashering Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this analogy: {\i hatam l\u8217'afukei kaima} \u8212- in the case of the {\i grusha}, the reason the {\i kohen} is not {\i metamei} for her is not that she is a person of diminished status, but that the relationship itself is one he should be dissolving. She should not be his wife at all; the marital bond is therefore weakened and does not support the obligation of {\i tumah}. The {\i mamzer} brother, however, is not a relationship that one should be ending or severing. He is simply always one\u8217's brother. The quality of the relationship \u8212- as distinct from the personal status of the relative \u8212- determines the {\i tumah} obligation. Since the fraternal relationship is intact and permanent, the {\i kohen} is fully obligated to render himself {\i tamei} for his {\i mamzer} brother.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Son Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Any Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i mi she-yesh lo ben mikol makom poter eshet aviv min ha-yibum} \u8212- whoever has any type of son, even a {\i mamzer}, that son exempts his father\u8217's wife from {\i yibum}. The biblical law of {\i yibum} applies only when a man dies {\i without a son}; if any legally recognized son exists, the {\i yibum} obligation does not arise. The {\i mishnah} specifies that the exception is again a son born of a non-Jewish woman or slave-woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again the {\i gemara} asks what \u8220"any type\u8221" comes to include, and again Rav Yehuda answers: {\i la\u8217'atuyei mamzer}. The derivation comes from the phrase {\i u-ven ein lo} \u8212- \u8220"and he has no son\u8221" \u8212- which the {\i gemara} reads as {\i ayen alav}, inviting a broader investigation into what constitutes a son for this purpose. By this expansive reading, any person who falls within the category of son exempts the father\u8217's wife from {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters & Grandchildren\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same {\i drashah} of {\i ayen alav} yields further expansions. Not only a son but even a daughter exempts the father\u8217's wife from {\i yibum}. Moreover, a grandson or granddaughter \u8212- indeed any descendant \u8212- also exempts. The {\i Torah}\u8217's language of \u8220"son\u8221" is thus read broadly to encompass the full range of descendants, such that so long as any descendant of the deceased exists, the {\i yibum} obligation is displaced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Honor & Teshuva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further states that if the {\i mamzer} son were to strike or curse his father, he would be liable ({\i chayav mita} for striking, and the equivalent prohibition for cursing). The {\i gemara} questions this. The verse {\i v\u8217'nasi be\u8217'amcha lo ta\u8217'or} is interpreted to mean that the prohibition against cursing \u8212- and by extension, striking \u8212- applies only to one who acts as part of the community: {\i be\u8217'oseh ma\u8217'aseh amcha}. A sinner who has placed himself outside the community would not be protected by this prohibition. Since the {\i mamzer}\u8217's father committed a grave transgression to produce the {\i mamzer}, perhaps the father should not qualify as {\i oseh ma\u8217'aseh amcha} and should therefore not be protected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites Rav Pinchas in the name of Rav Pappa: {\i be\u8217'oseh teshuvah} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} speaks of a father who has done {\i teshuvah}. Once the father has repented, he is again within the community, and the son\u8217's obligation of honor is restored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oseh Ma\u8217'aseh Amcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then questions whether {\i teshuvah} is even possible in such a case. A {\i baraita} cited by Shimon ben Menasya identifies the verse {\i me\u8217'uvat lo yuchal litkon} \u8212- that which is crooked cannot be made straight \u8212- as referring specifically to one who had forbidden relations and produced a {\i mamzer}. The effects of the sin persist permanently in the world in the form of the {\i mamzer} child; full rectification seems impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i hashta miha oseh ma\u8217'aseh amcha hu} \u8212- the question of {\i oseh ma\u8217'aseh amcha} is not about whether the father has achieved complete metaphysical atonement or whether God has fully erased the sin from his account. The question is simply whether, at present, the father is living an observant life and conducting himself within the norms of the community. As long as he is presently acting within the framework of the Jewish people, the prohibition against striking and cursing him applies in full, regardless of whether complete atonement for the original transgression has been or can be achieved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Double Transgression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i baraita}: {\i ha-ba al achoto ve-hi bat eshet aviv} \u8212- one who engages in relations with his sister who is also his father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter transgresses a double prohibition, {\i chayav mishum achoto u-mishum bat eshet aviv} \u8212- liable for both \u8220"his sister\u8221" and \u8220"his father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} holds that in such a case, two distinct prohibitions are violated. Each prohibition \u8212- \u8220"sister\u8221" and \u8220"father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter\u8221" \u8212- is an independent biblical {\i issur}, and when a single act violates both simultaneously, the transgressions are counted separately. This would mean, for instance, that if the act were done {\i be-shogeg}, two separate {\i korbanos} would be required, or if warnings were given, two sets of lashes would be administered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case requires clarification. A sister who is not the daughter of one\u8217's father\u8217's wife is possible: she could be the daughter of a woman one\u8217's father had a child with but never married. Conversely, a father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter who is not one\u8217's sister would simply be a stepsister, and stepsister alone \u8212- that is, one\u8217's father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter who is not also one\u8217's sister \u8212- is not biblically prohibited. The prohibition of \u8220"father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter\u8221" only attaches when she is also one\u8217's sister. The question, then, is whether the fact that the father was married to the mother creates a new and additional prohibition on top of the existing prohibition of \u8220"sister.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda disagrees. He holds that there is only one prohibition: {\i mishum achoto ata mechayvo ve\u8217'ei ata mechayvo mishum bat eshet aviv} \u8212- the liability is only for \u8220"his sister,\u8221" and the fact that the father was married to the mother does not create any additional {\i issur}. The father\u8217's marriage to the mother does not transform the sister into a more prohibited relative; she is prohibited only as a sister, full stop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Redundancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's reasoning proceeds as follows. The {\i Torah} writes in one verse: {\i ervat achotecha bat avicha o bat imecha} \u8212- the prohibition of one\u8217's sister, whether paternal or maternal. In a separate verse, the {\i Torah} writes: {\i bat eshet avicha moledes avicha, achotecha hi} \u8212- your father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter, born of your father, she is your sister. If the sister prohibition already covers all sisters whether the father was married to the mother or not, why did the {\i Torah} need to write a separate verse about the father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter? The redundancy teaches that in the case where the father {\i is} married to the mother, a second and distinct prohibition applies \u8212- the prohibition of \u8220"father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter\u8221" \u8212- in addition to the prohibition of \u8220"sister.\u8221" Thus: {\i shema mina, le-chayvo mishum achoto u-mishum bat eshet aviv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achosecha Hi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda reads the second verse differently. The verse states {\i achotecha hi} \u8212- she is your sister. He understands this phrase as restrictive: it tells you that in this case, the liability is as for {\i your sister}, and no more. The father\u8217's marriage to the mother adds nothing to the prohibition; it remains solely the prohibition of sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Sister Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} then accounts for the phrase {\i achotecha hi} differently: it is needed to teach that a full sister \u8212- one who shares both the same father and same mother \u8212- is also prohibited. One might have thought that since the {\i Torah} specifies the paternal sister in one verse and the maternal sister in another, a sister who is both \u8212- a full sister \u8212- might somehow fall between the categories and require separate derivation. The phrase {\i achotecha hi} serves to establish that a full sister is also prohibited, a fact one could not derive by {\i kal va-chomer} alone, and therefore requires explicit scriptural statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Placement Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda responds: if the purpose of {\i achotecha hi} were simply to establish the prohibition of a full sister, why would the {\i Torah} place it in the verse dealing specifically with one\u8217's father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter? If the point were only about full sisters, the phrase should appear in the verse about sisters generally \u8212- the verse that begins {\i ervat achotecha bat avicha o bat imecha}. The fact that {\i achotecha hi} appears specifically in the verse about the father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter indicates that its function is restrictive: it is telling you that even in that case \u8212- even where the father is married to the mother \u8212- the prohibition remains only that of \u8220"sister,\u8221" and no new prohibition is generated by the marital relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} responds in turn: {\i af al gav de-chtiv achotecha, itzterich le-michtav hi} \u8212- even granting that the word {\i achotecha} on its own could teach the prohibition of a full sister, the additional word {\i hi} was still necessary. {\i Shelo tomar be\u8217'alma mazhir min hadin} \u8212- one might have argued in principle that since the prohibition of full sister could have been derived by {\i kal va-chomer} from the partial-sister prohibitions, no explicit verse was needed, and that the verse could instead function restrictively. The {\i Torah} therefore writes {\i hi} to make clear that the verse does require an explicit statement \u8212- and is not merely restrictive \u8212- leaving it available to teach the double prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda closes: {\i im kein, lichtov Rachmana achotecha hi be\u8217'idach kra} \u8212- if all the {\i Torah} intended with {\i achotecha hi} was to establish the prohibition of a full sister, it should have written that phrase in the other verse, the general verse about sisters. The fact that it placed {\i achotecha hi} specifically in the verse about the father\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter signals that its message is directed at that very case \u8212- and the message is that in that case, the liability is {\i only} for sister, not for any additional prohibition. The debate is left standing, with both positions textually anchored, and the {\i gemara} will continue from this point in the following session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 23\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yossi: Marriageability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Daughter Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 27b returns to the position of Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, addressing a pointed question: given that Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda holds there is only a single prohibition of {\i achot} (sister) regardless of whether the father was married to the sister\u8217's mother, what does he do with the verse that specifically mentions {\i bat eshet avicha} \u8212- the daughter of your father\u8217's wife? If the prohibition of {\i achot} applies in all cases, the additional specification seems superfluous. What work, then, does that verse perform?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ishus Le\u8217'Avicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i miba\u8217'ei lei mi sheyesh lo ishus le\u8217'avicha} \u8212- the verse is needed to establish that the only woman who qualifies as your sister in a halachic sense is one whose mother your father could have married. The operative concept is {\i ishus} \u8212- the theoretical availability of a marital bond between the woman\u8217's mother and the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclude Shifcha, Akum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence is stated explicitly: {\i perat l\u8217'achoiso mi\u8217'shifcha ve\u8217'akum she\u8217'ein ishus le\u8217'avicha bah} \u8212- this excludes a half-sibling whose mother is a {\i shifcha} (female slave) or a non-Jewish woman, since marriage with such a woman was not legally available to your father. Such a child is therefore not considered one\u8217's legal sibling \u8212- and, by extension, not one\u8217's legal child in the full halachic sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wedlock vs.\u160 ?Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anusa Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately presses: could one not have said that the verse excludes a child born from an {\i anusa} \u8212- a woman who was not the father\u8217's wife at all, without any formal prohibition, simply outside the context of marriage? In broader usage, of course, a child born outside of marriage is considered illegitimate. Perhaps halacha follows the same logic, treating the child of any non-wife as not the father\u8217's legal child?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayos Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this: {\i hahi lo matzit amrit} \u8212- you cannot say this. The proof comes from Rava, who identifies an apparent contradiction between two verses. One verse states {\i ervat bat bincha o bat bitecha lo tegaleh} \u8212- do not uncover the nakedness of your son\u8217's daughter or your daughter\u8217's daughter \u8212- with an emphasis on {\i your} granddaughter. This might imply that only your own granddaughter is forbidden, not your wife\u8217's granddaughter from another man. Yet a second verse states {\i ervat isha u\u8217'vitah lo tegaleh}, including {\i bat b\u8217'nah ve\u8217'at bat bitah} \u8212- forbidding a woman and her daughter and her granddaughter. How are these reconciled? {\i Kan be\u8217'onsin, kan benissuin} \u8212- one verse refers to a context outside of marriage, the other to a marital context. The upshot is that in a non-marital union, the resulting grandchild is still {\i your} granddaughter for purposes of the {\i arayos} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the essential point: it is not the {\i mamzer} question being addressed here, nor the question of a child\u8217's moral legitimacy in the colloquial sense. The question is whether the child is halachically reckoned as a legal child of the father. English intuition about \u8220"bastard\u8221" conflates the question of legal status with birth outside of wedlock, but halacha treats them entirely separately. As our {\i mishnah} makes clear, {\i kol makom} \u8212- every child, including a {\i mamzer}, is the father\u8217's legal child. The only exceptions are children born of a {\i shifcha} or a non-Jewish mother. The Gemara\u8217's concern here is thus: if even a child born out of wedlock in the {\i arayos} context remains a legal grandchild, one cannot simply say that birth outside of marriage strips a child of legal status. The non-marital origin does not change the child\u8217's legal standing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lavin Don\u8217't Exclude\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lavin Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara advances to a second objection: perhaps the exclusion from legal sibling status applies not only to non-marital unions but to any union prohibited even by a simple negative prohibition \u8212- {\i chayvei lavin}. For example, a {\i kohen} who marries a {\i grusha}, or a case involving a {\i mamzer} or {\i mamzeres}. Should the child of such a prohibited union not be considered a legal child?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Binding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa responds: {\i chayvei lavin tafsei behu kiddushin} \u8212- in cases where the union is prohibited only by a {\i lav}, marriage is nonetheless fully binding. Since marriage takes effect in such cases, the child\u8217's status as a legal child is not in question. The child is no different from any other \u8212- the prohibition affected the act of marriage, but the legal reality of marriage, and thus of parenthood, stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof text: {\i ki tiheina le\u8217'ish shtei nashim} \u8212- if a man has two wives, one beloved and one hated. The Gemara raises a curious question: is there really a beloved and a hated before God? What is the verse actually teaching? The answer is that {\i ahuvah b\u8217'nisueha, snuah b\u8217'nisueha} \u8212- one is beloved in her marriage, meaning the marriage was entirely legitimate; the other is hated in her marriage, meaning the marriage should not have taken place due to a {\i lav}. Nevertheless, the Torah uses the word {\i tiheina} \u8212- they {\i are} his wives \u8212- indicating through the language of {\i havayah} that even a marriage entered into in violation of a negative prohibition is a binding marriage. The verse thus teaches a {\i chiddush}: even in such a case, where there is an objective reason the marriage was problematic, the child retains full inheritance rights and legal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beloved/Hated Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is now clarified. The progression of categories is as follows. First, a union that is simply extra-marital \u8212- birth outside of wedlock \u8212- clearly does not strip a child of legal status, as shown from the {\i arayos} granddaughter case. Second, a union that is prohibited by a {\i lav} \u8212- marriage was forbidden but would have been binding \u8212- likewise does not affect the child\u8217's legal standing, as shown from the beloved-and-hated verse. The next category to examine is therefore the one where not only is the union prohibited, but marriage would not even be binding \u8212- the {\i issurei karet}, the {\i arayos} prohibitions themselves, which produce {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kares Unions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kares Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises the third and most serious objection: {\i ve\u8217'eima perat l\u8217'chayvei kerisos} \u8212- perhaps the exclusion applies to children born from unions where the prohibition carries {\i karet}, where marriage is not merely forbidden but is entirely non-binding. In these cases, the man had relations with a woman he could never have legally married. Should such a child not be considered a legal child?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Sister Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds with a verse: {\i ervat achotecha bat avicha o bat imecha moledes bayis o moledes chutz} \u8212- the nakedness of your sister, the daughter of your father or your mother, whether born in the house or born outside. The phrase {\i moledes bayis o moledes chutz} \u8212- born in the house or born outside \u8212- is interpreted as follows: {\i bein she\u8217'omrim lo le\u8217'avicha kayem, bein she\u8217'omrim lo le\u8217'avicha hotze} \u8212- whether they told your father to keep his wife, or whether they told him to send her away. Even in a case where the father was told to divorce the woman \u8212- because the marriage was forbidden \u8212- the child resulting from that union is still called {\i achosechi hi}, your sister. The Torah thus explicitly designates the offspring of even a prohibited union as one\u8217's legal sibling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moledes Chutz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i moledes chutz} \u8212- born outside \u8212- refers to a child born from a union in which marriage was not binding at all, as in cases of {\i issur karet}. Even such a child is Torah-designated as one\u8217's sister. One might object that {\i omrim lo le\u8217'avicha hotze} could refer to a case of a {\i lav}, where the marriage is binding but should be dissolved \u8212- that might not be a case of {\i karet} at all. But since the previous objection regarding {\i lavin} was already addressed, the Gemara reads this verse as moving to the next level: even in {\i karet} cases, the child is a legal sibling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to an important observation. A {\i mamzer} \u8212- a child produced from an {\i issur karet} such as incest or adultery \u8212- is still the father\u8217's legal child in every halachic dimension other than the single restriction on whom the {\i mamzer} may marry. The {\i mamzer} inherits, fulfills {\i pru u\u8217'revu} for the father, and is counted as a sibling for purposes of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}. If a man\u8217's only brother was a {\i mamzer} and that man died, his widow would be obligated in {\i yibum} with that {\i mamzer} brother, or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- though of course {\i yibum} in such a case would be prohibited, it would still be binding. The category of {\i mamzer} is thus, perhaps counterintuitively, not a category of \u8220"non-legal child\u8221" \u8212- it is a single halachic restriction on marriage. One helpful comparison is the category of {\i p\u8217'tzu\u8217'a daka}, which similarly carries a specific marital restriction without affecting the person\u8217's fundamental legal status. The only category of non-legal child, halachically, is the child of a {\i shifcha} or a non-Jewish mother.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the offspring of {\i karet} unions are still legal children and siblings, the Gemara now confronts the obvious question: both {\i arayos} unions and unions with a non-Jewish woman or a slave share the feature that marriage is not binding \u8212- indeed, not even possible. Why should the child of an {\i arayos} union be a legal sibling while the child of a non-Jewish mother is not? The Gemara formulates the question sharply: the verse {\i moledes chutz} seems to include the offspring of a woman who could not be in the house \u8212- but how do we know this refers to the {\i arayos} case rather than the {\i shifcha} or {\i akum} case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayos Marriageable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer: {\i mistabra} \u8212- it stands to reason \u8212- that {\i chayvei kerisos} should be included as legal children. Why? {\i Sheken tofsin bah kiddushin l\u8217'achreini} \u8212- because such a woman is still marriageable within the Jewish world. Your sister, forbidden to you by {\i karet}, can nonetheless legally marry another Jewish man. She is not excluded from the category of those capable of {\i kiddushin}. She remains, in principle, a member of the marriageable world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Completely Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The non-Jewish woman and the slave, by contrast, cannot marry any Jew at all. They are entirely outside the category of those with whom {\i kiddushin} can take effect. The Gemara anticipates the objection: could one not argue that a non-Jewish woman or slave is {\i more} theoretically marriageable to you personally, since conversion or manumission is at least conceivable \u8212- whereas your own sister you could never marry under any circumstances? The Gemara dismisses this: {\i lichi migayra gufa achrina hi} \u8212- when she converts, she becomes legally a different person. The principle of {\i kekatan shenolad} applies: a convert is as one newly born, legally a distinct individual. Therefore the theoretical possibility of conversion does not render the non-Jewish woman marriageable as {\i this} person. As this person, in her current state, she is entirely and irrevocably outside the category of those to whom any Jew could be married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The organizing principle is thus: legal sibling status requires that the mother belong to the category of the potentially marriageable \u8212- not necessarily to {\i you}, but to some Jew. A woman forbidden to you by {\i karet}, such as your sister, remains marriageable to others and is therefore within the broader category. A non-Jewish woman or a slave is marriageable to no Jew in her current state, and her post-conversion identity would be legally a different person. She is therefore categorically excluded, and the child born of such a union is not your legal child.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbis\u8217' Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave from Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: those who disagree with Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- who use the verse {\i bat eshet avicha} for a different purpose entirely \u8212- where do {\i they} derive the principle that the child of a {\i shifcha} or non-Jewish woman is not a legal child?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer regarding the {\i shifcha} is straightforward: {\i nafka lehu me\u8217'ha\u8217'ishah ve\u8217'yeladeha tihyeh la\u8217'adoneha} \u8212- from the verse in the context of a slave woman who has borne children, the Torah states that the children belong to the master, not to the father. This establishes explicitly that the offspring of a slave woman are not legally reckoned as the father\u8217's children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Verses Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, this verse covers only the {\i shifcha}. The non-Jewish woman requires a separate source, and one cannot simply learn the non-Jewish case from the slave case. The Gemara explains why both are needed: {\i de\u8217'ei ashmeinan shifcha, mishum de\u8217'leit lehu chayis} \u8212- had the Torah told us only about the slave, one might have said the reason is because slave families lack recognized familial relationships; slave society was characterized by broken families and the legal non-recognition of kinship. But a non-Jewish woman does have recognized familial relationships, and her children know their parents and siblings. One might therefore have thought the offspring with a non-Jewish woman is a legal child. Conversely, {\i de\u8217'ei ashmeinan avodat kochavim, mishum de\u8217'lo shaycha be\u8217'mitzvot} \u8212- had the Torah told us only about the non-Jewish woman, one might have attributed the exclusion to her being entirely outside the sphere of Jewish obligation. But a {\i shifcha}, an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani}, is obligated in negative commandments and positive non-time-bound commandments, placing her in a quasi-Jewish status. One might have thought her child with a Jewish man retains full legal child status. Therefore both verses are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Verse Covers Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, derives both exclusions from the single verse of {\i bat eshet avicha} and the principle of {\i ishus le\u8217'avicha}. Since neither a {\i shifcha} nor a non-Jewish woman can enter into a marriage with a Jewish man that has any legal effect, both are excluded by the same principle, and no separate verse for each is required. This elegant economy is the hallmark of his reading: one verse, one principle, both cases covered.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matrilineal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Intermarriage Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the rabbis who disagree with Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda: having established from the verse about the slave woman that {\i shifcha} offspring are not a man\u8217's legal children, where do they derive that the offspring of a non-Jewish mother likewise are not his legal children \u8212- and by extension, are not Jewish? This is the foundational question of matrilineal descent as treated in the Gemara.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai, cites the verse governing intermarriage with the seven Canaanite nations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Yasir Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse in question contains the prohibition {\i lo titchaten bam} \u8212- do not intermarry with them \u8212- including the directive {\i uvito lo tikach le\u8217'vincha}, do not take their daughter for your son. The reason given is {\i ki yasir et bincha me\u8217'acharai} \u8212- for he will turn your son away from Me. Here lies the crux of the {\i drasha}: the subject of {\i yasir} is masculine \u8212- {\i he} will lead astray \u8212- yet the subject of the clause is the non-Jewish daughter taken for your son. Grammatically, a non-Jewish daughter-in-law would be {\i she} who leads astray. The masculine {\i yasir} redirects the reading: it refers to the non-Jewish husband, pointing us back to the earlier clause about your daughter being given to their son. The non-Jewish son-in-law will lead your son \u8212- meaning your grandson \u8212- away from Me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion: {\i bincha mi\u8217'Yisraelit itkarei bincha} \u8212- your grandson born of a Jewish daughter and a non-Jewish husband is still called your son. The fact that {\i yasir} refers to this man leading your grandson astray presupposes that the grandson is indeed yours. And therefore, by reversal: {\i ve\u8217'ein bincha haba min ha\u8217'ovedet kochavim itkarei bincha ela bna} \u8212- the child born of your son and a non-Jewish woman is not called your son but her son. Since she is not Jewish, the child follows the mother and is not your son, not Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matrilineal Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the Talmudic basis for matrilineal descent, derived from a single {\i yud} \u8212- the {\i yasir} versus the expected {\i tasir}. The principle establishes two things simultaneously: that the child of a Jewish mother and non-Jewish father remains legally your grandchild, and that the child of a Jewish father and non-Jewish mother is not legally your child \u8212- and is therefore not Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Psul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina raises a sharp question: {\i shma mina ben bitcha haba min oved kochavim itkarei bincha} \u8212- we learn from this that your daughter\u8217's son from a non-Jewish father is still called your son. Does this mean Ravina holds that a child born of a non-Jewish father and Jewish mother is entirely {\i kasher}, with no halachic blemish whatsoever?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yisrael Pasul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds: the child is not a {\i mamzer} \u8212- that position was considered and rejected \u8212- but neither is the child entirely {\i kasher} in every sense. {\i Yisrael pasul mikrei} \u8212- the child is designated an invalid Israelite, carrying some form of {\i psul}. The Gemara does not elaborate here on the nature of that {\i psul}, but this becomes a substantial discussion elsewhere \u8212- in other {\i gemarot}, among the {\i rishonim}, and in practical {\i halacha}. The major issue that flows from this is whether a woman born of a Jewish mother and non-Jewish father is permitted to marry a {\i kohen}, given that some form of {\i psul} is hinted to here. This is a significant and debated question that is only alluded to in this {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scope of Matrilineal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Nations Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an important objection: the verse {\i lo titchaten bam} is written in the context of the seven Canaanite nations. Perhaps the entire derivation \u8212- including the rule of matrilineal descent \u8212- applies only to intermarriage with the {\i sheva umot}, not with non-Jews generally. If so, a child born of a Jewish father and, say, a non-Canaanite non-Jewish mother might retain legal child status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution invokes the methodological principle of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai: {\i darash ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'kra} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon derives halacha by identifying the reason embedded in the verse and applying the law wherever that reason applies. The classic illustration of this method is the prohibition against taking a widow\u8217's garment as collateral: Rabbi Shimon holds that if the widow is wealthy, the reason for the prohibition does not apply, and therefore the prohibition itself does not apply. The {\i rabbanan} disagree, holding that the verse\u8217's language defines its scope categorically, without reference to the underlying reason. Rabbi Shimon, by contrast, reads the reason as integral to the law\u8217's application.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Yasir Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied here: the verse states {\i ki yasir} \u8212- for he will lead astray. Rabbi Shimon identifies this as the reason for the prohibition on intermarriage. And the reason \u8212- the potential to lead your offspring away from God \u8212- applies to {\i kol hamessirim}, anyone capable of leading your son astray. This is not limited to the seven Canaanite nations; any non-Jewish spouse can lead your children from the path. Therefore, according to Rabbi Shimon, the verse and its derivation extend to all non-Jews, and matrilineal descent applies universally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leaves a significant question for those who do not share Rabbi Shimon\u8217's methodology: the {\i rabbanan} who do not {\i darash ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'kra} cannot extend the verse beyond the {\i sheva umot} on the basis of its rationale. Where, then, do they derive the universal rule of matrilineal descent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer: the opinion that argues with Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda is precisely Rabbi Shimon. Those who do not adopt Rabbi Shimon\u8217's methodology and do not agree with Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's reading of {\i bat eshet avicha} will, in fact, use the verse of {\i bat eshet avicha} as Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda does \u8212- that is, they will hold like Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda for this derivation. The question of where {\i rabbanan} who reject both approaches find their source is thus resolved: the {\i tanna} who argues with Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda is Rabbi Shimon, and Rabbi Shimon has his own source \u8212- {\i ki yasir} extended via {\i ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'kra}. Those who do not hold like either will simply learn it from the verse of {\i ishus le\u8217'avicha} as Rabbi Yossi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda does.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Sisters Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: One Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on 23b opens a new section, returning to the practical territory of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}. The foundational case: a man performed {\i kiddushin} with one of two sisters but does not know which one he married. He cannot continue to live with either woman, because whichever one he approaches might be his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get to Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To free the women to marry other men, the husband must give a {\i get} to each of them. Since either one might be his wife, each requires a {\i get} from him. He gives two {\i gittin} and releases them both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Bro: Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this man then dies and leaves only one brother, that brother must perform {\i chalitzah} with both sisters. He cannot perform {\i yibum} with either one, because whichever one he approaches might be {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- the sister of the woman to whom he stands in a {\i zika} relationship from {\i yibum}. {\i Achas zekukato} is forbidden to him like a married woman\u8217's sister; having sex with her would be a serious prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza & Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the man dies leaving two brothers, one performs {\i chalitzah} and the other performs {\i yibum}. The mechanism works as follows. Say the first brother performs {\i chalitzah} with one sister. This act either fulfilled his obligation \u8212- in which case she was the actual {\i yevamah} \u8212- or was performed with the wrong woman, in which case it was an inconsequential act. Either way, the second brother can now perform {\i yibum} with the other sister: if she was the real {\i yevamah}, he is fulfilling the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}; if she was not the {\i yevamah}, the first {\i chalitzah} was real, and this is simply an ordinary marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical point is that the second brother\u8217's {\i yibum} cannot precede the first brother\u8217's {\i chalitzah}. If the second brother performs {\i yibum} first, he risks having relations with {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- the sister of the woman who may actually be the real {\i yevamah} \u8212- which is forbidden. Only after the first brother has performed {\i chalitzah}, removing one woman from the state of {\i zika}, can the second brother safely perform {\i yibum} with the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Men, Two Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Two Men\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now complicates the scenario: two men \u8212- say, two unrelated individuals \u8212- each performed {\i kiddushin} with one of the two sisters, and neither man knows which sister he married. Now each man faces the same dilemma as before: he cannot consummate the marriage. Each man must therefore give a {\i get} to both sisters \u8212- two {\i gittin} from each man, four {\i gittin} in total.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If both men die, each man\u8217's surviving brother must perform {\i chalitzah} with both sisters. The presence of the second possible husband compounds the uncertainty: each surviving brother must address the possibility that his deceased brother was married to either of the sisters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Bros\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If each deceased man left a single brother, each of those brothers performs {\i chalitzah} with both sisters. If one of the deceased men left a single brother and the other left two brothers, the single brother performs {\i chalitzah} with both sisters to neutralize his family\u8217's involvement \u8212- and once he has done so, we return in effect to the prior scenario. The two brothers of the other deceased man can then arrange one {\i chalitzah} and one {\i yibum} among themselves, as in the earlier case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Both Sides: Two Bros\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual-Family Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds yet another layer: each of the two deceased men left two brothers. On one side are two brothers \u8212- call them Shimon and Levi \u8212- and on the other side are two other brothers. Each pair of brothers represents the heirs of one of the deceased husbands, each of whom was married to one (unknown) sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cross-Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The solution is a cross-{\i chalitzah} strategy. One brother from the first family performs {\i chalitzah} with one sister, and one brother from the second family performs {\i chalitzah} with the other sister. Then the remaining brother from each family performs {\i yibum} with the respective sister freed by the other family\u8217's {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why It Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic: consider Shimon\u8217's performing {\i chalitzah} with one sister and Levi\u8217's subsequently performing {\i yibum} with the other. This is precisely the arrangement established in the earlier case \u8212- one {\i chalitzah}, one {\i yibum} \u8212- and it works for all the reasons already explained. The complication introduced by the second family is that the sister Levi wishes to perform {\i yibum} with may have been married to the second deceased man, in which case she stands in {\i zika} to his brothers and cannot simply marry Levi. But once one brother from the second family has performed {\i chalitzah} with her, that {\i zika} is dissolved, and Levi can perform {\i yibum} without any concern of {\i achot zekukato}. The same logic operates symmetrically for the other pair. Each side\u8217's {\i chalitzah} clears the path for the other side\u8217's {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Both Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Risk: Achat Zekuka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} addresses a final scenario: what if, rather than following the prescribed cross-{\i chalitzah} strategy, one brother went ahead and performed {\i yibum} directly \u8212- before any {\i chalitzah} had taken place? This is problematic because at the moment he performs {\i yibum}, the woman he approaches might be {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- the sister of the woman genuinely obligated to him through {\i yibum}. He has thus potentially transgressed a serious prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nonetheless, once the second brother from the other family then goes ahead and performs {\i yibum} with the other sister, the situation is retroactively resolved. The first brother\u8217's woman is no longer his {\i achat zekukato} \u8212- she has no remaining relationship of {\i zika} to him \u8212- because the second {\i yibum} completed the discharge of the other obligation. Since the {\i zika} no longer exists, the first brother may remain married to the woman he took. The principle is the same one articulated earlier: {\i ein motzi\u8217'in miyadam} \u8212- once {\i yibum} has been performed by both, even if the order was wrong and the first act was technically problematic, the parties are not required to separate. The second {\i yibum} retroactively eliminates the basis of the prohibition that attached to the first.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 23b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unknown Marriage Cas (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins after the {\i mishnah} on 23b, with the {\i gemara} picking up approximately two lines before the text widens on the page. The {\i mishnah} addressed the case of a man who married two women without knowing which one he had married: before his death he must give a {\i get} to each, and after his death {\i chalitzah} is performed with each. The {\i mishnah} then extends the discussion to scenarios involving two brothers or two separate men in parallel situations. The {\i gemara} now proceeds to analyze the underlying halachic assumptions of this {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Sisters Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opening scenario of the {\i mishnah} concerns a man who married one of two sisters without knowing which one he had married. The {\i gemara} initially infers from this case that {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam re\u8217'uyim le\u8217'biah} \u8212- an act of betrothal that can never lead to marital relations \u8212- is nonetheless halachically binding. This inference follows because the scenario assumes that from the very moment of {\i kiddushin}, it was impossible to determine which woman was his wife, and therefore it was never possible to be intimate with either sister, since one might be one\u8217's wife\u8217's sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Unsuitable (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Binding Nature Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws the inference that {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam re\u8217'uyim le\u8217'biah havei kiddushin} \u8212- betrothal that is inherently incapable of leading to intimacy is still a valid act of {\i kiddushin}. This question touches on the centrality of marital relations within the institution of marriage itself. One may recall that earlier in the tractate, the {\i gemara} discussed at length cases involving women for whom pregnancy posed a danger, or cases of underage girls and pregnant women, and the discussion there concerned the use of contraception \u8212- but never the option of complete abstention from relations. That itself reflects how fundamental intimacy is to the definition of marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if {\i kiddushin} that cannot lead to relations is still binding, why is {\i kiddushin} with an {\i ervah} not binding? After all, relations with an {\i ervah} are also forbidden. The answer \u8212- and this is indeed the question that Tosafot addresses \u8212- is that in the case of standard {\i issurei lavin}, the prohibition exists independently of the {\i kiddushin} itself. By contrast, in the two-sisters case, it is the very act of {\i kiddushin} that generates the impossibility: by creating an act of betrothal to one of the two sisters, one immediately renders the other a potential {\i achot ishto}, making relations with either woman impossible. As Tosafot states: {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam re\u8217'uyim le\u8217'biah peirush she\u8217'al yedei kiddushin kamafsida biviah} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} itself is what precludes the possibility of relations. By contrast, with {\i chayavei lavin} or {\i chayavei aseh}, the {\i kiddushin} does not generate the prohibition; the prohibition exists prior to and independent of the act of betrothal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unknown Marriage Cas (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's explanation of how the {\i kiddushin} could be indeterminate from the very outset is as follows. He writes: {\i she\u8217'ein sofam lavo lidei biah} \u8212- at the moment of {\i kiddushin} it was never possible for relations to follow. The scenario Rashi envisions is one in which the man declared {\i harei achas mikem mekudeshet li} \u8212- \u8220"one of you is betrothed to me\u8221" \u8212- without specifying which sister. Both sisters had appointed a single agent to receive the {\i kiddushin} on their behalf, and the man gave the money to that agent without designating which sister he intended. As a result, the betrothal was indeterminate from its very inception, and since neither woman could be identified as his wife, relations with either would always have been forbidden as potentially intimacy with one\u8217's wife\u8217's sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that this scenario is somewhat distinct from a case where the identity was knowable in principle but happened to be unknown. One can imagine an alternative scenario \u8212- for example, where the parties agreed that the outcome would be determined by a coin flip, but the coin rolled away and its result was never observed. In that case, there was at least a definite intended recipient under each possible outcome, even if the result was unobserved. In Rashi\u8217's case, however, no specific woman was ever designated at all. Either way, the conceptual question of whether {\i kiddushin} that cannot lead to relations is binding remains significant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Unsuitable (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayos vs.\u160 ?Lo Ta\u8217'aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might press further: in the case of an {\i ervah} \u8212- a woman with whom relations are biblically forbidden as {\i arayot} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is likewise not binding. Why, then, should the two-sisters case, where relations are also forbidden, lead to a different conclusion? The distinction, as noted above and addressed explicitly by Tosafot, is that the {\i kiddushin} in the two-sisters case is itself the source of the problem. The act of betrothal is what creates the {\i issur}, not a pre-existing prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot formulates this clearly: {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam re\u8217'uyim le\u8217'biah peirush she\u8217'al yedei kiddushin kamafsida biviah} \u8212- the category of {\i kiddushin} that cannot lead to relations refers specifically to a case where the {\i kiddushin} itself destroys the possibility of relations. By contrast, in cases of {\i chayavei lavin} or {\i chayavei aseh} \u8212- such as a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i gerushah}, or a {\i mamzer}, or a {\i petzu\u8217'a daka} \u8212- the prohibition on relations does not arise from the {\i kiddushin}. Indeed, in a somewhat ironic framing, those are considered {\i kiddushin ha-re\u8217'uyim le\u8217'biah} \u8212- betrothal that is in some sense oriented toward relations \u8212- because the {\i kiddushin} itself does not generate the impossibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves its initial inference by reframing the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case: {\i hacha bemai askinan keshe-hukru basof nit\u8217'arvu} \u8212- we are dealing here with a situation where the identities were originally known and then subsequently became confused. The man did intend a specific woman at the moment of betrothal, the {\i kiddushin} was therefore unambiguously valid, and only afterwards did confusion arise as to which sister he had married. Accordingly, no proof can be derived from this {\i mishnah} regarding {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam re\u8217'uyim le\u8217'biah}, since the {\i mishnah} is not addressing that case at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Language & Seifa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that this reading is also supported by the precise language of the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} uses the formulation {\i ve\u8217'eino yodeia} \u8212- \u8220"and he does not know\u8221" \u8212- rather than {\i ve\u8217'eino yadu\u8217'a}, which would mean \u8220"and it is not known.\u8221" The active construction indicates that {\i he} does not know, but the information exists and was once known. This linguistic precision confirms that the scenario is one of subsequent confusion, not original indeterminacy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seifa\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that the {\i kiddushin} is clearly valid and this point requires no special derivation, the {\i gemara} asks: what is the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah}? The answer is {\i seifa itztricha lei} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} is needed for the practical rulings found in its concluding portion, which spell out how {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} are to be conducted once the husband dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Brother Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the husband died and there is one surviving brother, he performs {\i chalitzah} with both women, since it is unknown which one creates the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If there were two brothers, one performs {\i chalitzah} and the other performs {\i yibum}. However, this must be done in a specific order: {\i davka mechaleitz ve\u8217'hadar yabumei} \u8212- the {\i chalitzah} must precede the {\i yibum}. The reason is that if the brother were to perform {\i yibum} first \u8212- say, with one of the sisters \u8212- and that sister turns out not to be the one to whom he is {\i zakuk}, then he would have engaged in relations with the sister of the woman to whom he is {\i zakuk}, violating the prohibition of {\i achot zikukato}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate: suppose the deceased was married to either Rachel or Leah, and the surviving brothers are Shimon and Levi. If Shimon performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, and it turns out Rachel was indeed the obligated {\i yevamah}, Levi can then perform {\i yibum} with Leah without any concern. If, on the other hand, Leah was the obligated {\i yevamah}, then Shimon\u8217's {\i chalitzah} was technically superfluous but harmless, and Shimon himself has fulfilled the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with Leah. In either scenario, proceeding in the order of {\i chalitzah} first ensures that no violation occurs. But if Levi were to begin with {\i yibum} to Leah, and Rachel was in fact the obligated woman, then Levi would have had relations with {\i achot zikukato} \u8212- the sister of the woman to whom he bears a {\i zikah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Men & Two Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents a more complex scenario involving two unrelated men \u8212- each of whom married one of two sisters without knowing which \u8212- who both die. One man has a single surviving brother and the other has two surviving brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Brother Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When one of the husbands dies leaving a single brother, that brother performs {\i chalitzah} with both women and thereby removes himself entirely from the picture, since he cannot perform {\i yibum} in any case: even if he were to perform {\i chalitzah} with one woman and then {\i yibum} with the other, the second would be {\i achot chalutzato} \u8212- the sister of the woman with whom he performed {\i chalitzah} \u8212- rendering the {\i yibum} forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Men Die\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When both husbands die \u8212- one leaving one brother and one leaving two brothers \u8212- the single brother performs {\i chalitzah} with both women and steps aside. This allows the two brothers of the other husband to proceed: one performs {\i chalitzah} and the other performs {\i yibum}, just as in the earlier case. The {\i gemara} then asks whether there is any novelty in stating this, given that the earlier portion of the {\i mishnah} already established the same principle. The answer is that without this ruling one might have feared a precedent \u8212- perhaps one of the parties would reason that since two brothers in the other family can do {\i chalitzah} and then {\i yibum}, perhaps the single brother could also do {\i chalitzah} with one woman and {\i yibum} with the other. The {\i mishnah} therefore states explicitly that this is not permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why No Yibum First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the reason the single brother cannot perform {\i yibum} at all \u8212- even after {\i chalitzah} with one of the women \u8212- is that if he were to do so, it could constitute relations with {\i achot chalutzato}. And before he has performed {\i chalitzah} and exited the picture, if one of the two brothers of the other husband were to perform {\i yibum}, that brother might be performing {\i yibum} with a {\i yevamah lashuk} \u8212- a woman who is awaiting resolution with the single brother \u8212- since the {\i zikah} of the single brother has not yet been dissolved. Thus, the single brother must perform {\i chalitzah} with both women first, clearing the way for the remaining two brothers to proceed with one performing {\i chalitzah} and the other performing {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Brothers & Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complex Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final scenario of this section involves two unrelated men, each with two surviving brothers, both of whom had married two sisters without knowing which sister each had married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solution Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} prescribes a specific solution: each pair of brothers coordinates so that one member of each pair performs {\i chalitzah} with one woman while the other performs {\i yibum} with the other. Specifically, the first brother performs {\i chalitzah} with Leah and the second brother of the other pair performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel; then the respective partners in each pair perform {\i yibum} with the other woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Removing the Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This arrangement systematically removes the problem of {\i achot zikukato}. By performing {\i chalitzah} with Leah, the first brother dissolves whatever {\i zikah} he might have had to Leah from the first husband\u8217's marriage, so that Leah is no longer in a state of {\i zikukato} with respect to Levi. By performing {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, Shimon removes Rachel from the state of {\i zikukato} with respect to Levi, so that Levi may now perform {\i yibum} with Leah. The same logic operates symmetrically for the other pair and Rachel. The {\i gemara} asks why this ruling was necessary given that earlier rulings covered similar ground, and answers that one might otherwise have thought a concern exists that, since {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} are being performed in each pair, participants might bypass the {\i chalitzah} and rush directly to {\i yibum}. The {\i mishnah} therefore teaches explicitly that the ordered sequence is mandatory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Language & Seifa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's language \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'eino yodeia} rather than {\i ve\u8217'eino yadu\u8217'a} \u8212- was already shown to confirm that the {\i mishnah} deals with a case of subsequent confusion, not original indeterminacy. This precision permeates the entire structure of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rulings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Certain Case Contrast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Certain Marriage Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now compares the foregoing uncertain scenarios to a distinct case taught elsewhere in the {\i mishnah}: four brothers, two of whom are married to two sisters. This is not a case of doubt \u8212- it is known that Reuven is married to Rachel and Shimon is married to Leah. Both Reuven and Shimon die. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i harei elu choltzot velo mityabmot} \u8212- the surviving brothers Levi and Yehuda must perform {\i chalitzah} with both women; neither may perform {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Both Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling requires explanation. One might have supposed that the same solution applied in the uncertain case would apply here: Levi performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, thereby releasing Rachel from the state of {\i zikukato}, and then proceeds to perform {\i yibum} with Leah. Why is that not permitted here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers two distinct rationales, each tied to a different position in an underlying dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the position that {\i yesh zikah} \u8212- that the {\i zikah} creates a quasi-marital bond \u8212- Rashi explains the distinction as follows. At the very moment both Reuven and Shimon die, Rachel becomes forbidden to Levi as {\i achot zikukato}: she is the sister of Leah, to whom he is {\i zakuk} via the {\i zikah} from Shimon\u8217's marriage. Now, a woman who is {\i achot zikukato} is not merely a woman he may not currently have relations with \u8212- she is a {\i yevamah} to whom there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, and therefore she takes on the status of {\i eshet ach} without the redemptive possibility of {\i yibum}. That makes her an {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Levi then performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, the {\i zikah} to Rachel is dissolved, and she is no longer {\i zikukato}. But this dissolution does not undo the original {\i issur}: at the moment that Rachel became {\i achot zikukato} she simultaneously became {\i eshet ach} without {\i mitzvat yibum}, and that status of {\i ervah} does not disappear merely because the {\i zikah} has now been resolved. Rachel remains forbidden. By the same logic, Leah is also {\i achot zikukato} from the moment of Reuven\u8217's death \u8212- she is forbidden as the sister of the woman to whom Levi is {\i zakuk} via Reuven\u8217's {\i zikah}. Even after {\i chalitzah} is performed with Rachel, Leah retains her status as {\i ervah} because she too, at the moment of death, was a {\i yevamah} with no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, making her an {\i eshet ach} with {\i ervah} status. That status endures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the critical difference from the uncertain case. In the uncertain case, only one of the two sisters was actually Reuven\u8217's wife. The other sister bore no marital connection to Reuven whatsoever. If the {\i zikah} ran to Rachel, then Leah was {\i achot zikukato} \u8212- but she was not an {\i eshet ach} of anyone. She had no marital tie to either Reuven or Shimon. Once the {\i zikah} to Rachel is dissolved, Leah has no residual forbidden status. In the certain case, by contrast, both sisters are simultaneously {\i yevamot} \u8212- both were wives of a deceased brother \u8212- and both became simultaneously {\i asurot} as {\i ervah} the moment the {\i zikah} created a conflict. That simultaneous {\i issur} remains even after {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bitul Mitzvas Yevamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also cites a second rationale, tied to the position of {\i ein zikah}. According to that position, there is no quasi-marital bond created by the {\i zikah}, and therefore there is no concept of {\i achot zikukato} as an {\i ervah}. Under that view, why couldn\u8217't Levi perform {\i yibum} with Rachel? If there is no {\i zikah}, there is no prohibition. Yet the {\i mishnah} still prohibits it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern in this case is {\i bitul mitzvas yevamim} \u8212- the nullification of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}. If Levi were to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel and then Yehuda were to die before performing {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} with Leah, Leah would be left as {\i eshet ach} of Levi \u8212- his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- and neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitzah} could be performed with her. The {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} would be entirely nullified for her, which is prohibited. To prevent this outcome, the {\i mishnah} mandates that both surviving brothers perform {\i chalitzah}, ensuring that no one proceeds with {\i yibum} in a way that could lead to the complete frustration of the other woman\u8217's {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncertain Case Tolerated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then ask: why does the uncertain case tolerate both brothers proceeding with {\i chalitzah} and then {\i yibum}, given the same theoretical concern about {\i bitul mitzvas yevamim}? The answer is that in the uncertain case, only one of the two women was ever genuinely obligated in {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. The other woman had no actual obligation at all. Since each brother can say {\i didi kamei ramyah} \u8212- \u8220"the obligated woman falls to me\u8221" \u8212- and since we do not know who the true {\i yevamah} is, there is no certainty that both women are genuinely owed {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. The concern of {\i bitul mitzvas yevamim} therefore applies with far less force in a case built on {\i safek}, and accordingly the {\i mishnah} permits the more flexible approach in the uncertain case while forbidding it in the certain case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Both Yibum & Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further states: {\i kidmu ve-khnasu ein motzi\u8217'in} \u8212- if both brothers went ahead and performed {\i yibum}, they are permitted to remain married. Although the proper procedure required one to perform {\i chalitzah} before the other performed {\i yibum}, once both have acted, the {\i yibum} takes effect. The reasoning is that while the first brother to act may have initially violated the prohibition of {\i achot zikukato}, once his brother performed {\i yibum} with the other sister, the {\i zikah} to that sister was dissolved, removing retroactively whatever problem had existed. The marriages may therefore stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} taught by Sheshila adds: {\i ve\u8217'afilu sheneihem kohanim} \u8212- this is true even if both brothers are {\i kohanim}. This requires explanation. In the scenario under discussion, both brothers have each received a {\i chalitzah} \u8212- since the resolution involved each pair performing a {\i chalitzah} with one woman and a {\i yibum} with the other. If both brothers are {\i kohanim}, the concern arises that one of the women they have now married may be a {\i chalutza}, and a {\i kohen} is forbidden to marry a {\i chalutza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalutza Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} rules that they may remain married. The {\i gemara} asks: is the prohibition of {\i chalutza} to a {\i kohen} only rabbinic? The {\i gemara} affirms: {\i chalutza d\u8217'rabanan hi} \u8212- yes, the prohibition is rabbinic in nature, and the biblical verse that includes {\i chalutza} alongside {\i gerushah} in the prohibition for {\i kohanim} is treated as a mere {\i asmakhta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Chalutza Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the prohibition is only rabbinic, a {\i safek chalutza} \u8212- a woman who may or may not be a {\i chalutza} \u8212- is treated leniently. In the present case, {\i chalitzah} was performed with both women, but only one of them actually required it. An act of {\i chalitzah} performed upon a woman who did not require it is halachically meaningless. Therefore, one of the two women is a genuine {\i chalutza} and the other is not; it is simply unknown which is which. Since each brother is married to only a {\i safek chalutza}, and {\i safek chalutza lo gazru bah rabanan} \u8212- the Rabbis did not extend their decree of {\i issur} to a {\i safek chalutza} \u8212- both brothers may remain married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalutza Source Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges whether {\i chalutza} is truly only rabbinic, citing a {\i baraita} that derives the prohibition from a biblical verse. The {\i gemara} resolves that the derivation is an {\i asmakhta} alone. It should be noted that a significant body of Tannaitic material appears to treat {\i chalutza} as {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, and indeed this may be the position of the Yerushalmi. Nevertheless, the Bavli consistently maintains that {\i chalutza} is {\i d\u8217'rabanan}, which allows for the leniency in the {\i safek} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader question of {\i chalutza}\u8217's status is also illuminating conceptually. A {\i chalutza} occupies a status analogous to a {\i gerushah} \u8212- a divorcee \u8212- in that the {\i zikah} was a quasi-marriage and the {\i chalitzah} was its dissolution. Since widowhood is not forbidden to an ordinary {\i kohen} \u8212- only to a {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- it is the quasi-divorce aspect of {\i chalutza} that is halachically relevant for {\i kohanim}, and it is that dimension which the Rabbis addressed with their decree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum Mitzvah Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gadol Preference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} states: {\i mitzvah be-gadol le-yabbem} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} falls first upon the eldest surviving brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Younger Brother Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, {\i ve\u8217'im kadam ha-katan zakha} \u8212- if the younger brother rushed ahead and performed {\i yibum}, his act is fully valid. The preference for the eldest brother does not render {\i yibum} by a younger brother ineffective; it establishes a proper order, but does not create an exclusive entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bechor Verse Drashah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna\u8217'im} teach: {\i ve-haya ha-bechor asher teleid yakum al shem achiv ha-met} \u8212- the plain reading of this verse would seem to say that the firstborn son born from the {\i yibum} union will be named after his dead father. The {\i gemara}, however, reads the verse entirely differently. {\i Ve-haya ha-bechor} is read not as a reference to the firstborn child who will be born, but as a directive that the {\i bechor} \u8212- the oldest brother \u8212- is the one who performs the {\i yibum}. The phrase {\i ve-yibma} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall perform {\i yibum}\u8221" \u8212- is followed by {\i ve-haya ha-bechor}, meaning: it is the {\i bechor} among the brothers who performs the {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fertile Woman Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Asher teleid} \u8212- \u8220"who will bear\u8221" \u8212- is read as a qualification on the {\i yevamah}: the {\i bechor} performs {\i yibum} specifically with a woman who is capable of bearing children. This excludes an {\i ilonit} \u8212- a woman who is constitutionally infertile \u8212- from the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}: {\i prat le\u8217'ilonit she\u8217'eina yoledet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance Aspect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yakum al shem achiv} \u8212- the verse continues \u8212- is read as {\i lenachala}: inheritance. A {\i gezeirah shavah} connects this phrase to a parallel usage elsewhere \u8212- {\i al shem acheihem yikare\u8217'u be-nachalatam}, referring to Ephraim and Menashe and the inheritance of tribal portions \u8212- establishing that {\i al shem} in both contexts means assuming an inheritance. Thus {\i yakum al shem achiv} means that the brother who performs {\i yibum} assumes his dead brother\u8217's portion of the estate. None of this is about naming a child; it is all about the brother performing the act of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Continuation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-lo yimache shemo mi-Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"and his name shall not be blotted out from Israel\u8221" \u8212- is similarly read as a qualification on the brother, not as a reference to perpetuating the dead brother\u8217's name through a child. It is read as excluding a {\i saris} \u8212- a sterile man \u8212- from the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}: {\i prat le-saris she-shemo machui}, for a {\i saris} cannot have children, his name is already as if blotted out, and the rationale underlying the {\i mitzvah} does not apply to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Peshat Observation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Peshat Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava makes a striking observation: {\i af al gav de-ve-chol ha-Torah kullah ein mikra yotze mi-yedey peshuto} \u8212- in the entire Torah, even when a {\i derashah} is made from a verse, the {\i peshat} of the verse retains some meaningful value. One may derive a halacha that overrides the plain reading, yet the plain reading continues to express something of the underlying values or spirit of the text. Rava\u8217's point is that this principle does not hold here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Shavah Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gezeirah shavah} connecting {\i yakum al shem achiv} to {\i lenachala} \u8212- inheritance \u8212- completely removes the verse from its apparent meaning. There is no residual {\i peshat} that remains operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peshat Eradication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Rashi explains: {\i she-ein tzarich likro shemo be-shem achiv ha-met} \u8212- there is no requirement, nor even a meaningful expression of an idea, that the child born of the {\i yibum} be named after the deceased father. Under ordinary circumstances, even when a {\i derashah} re-reads a verse technically, one might still say: the spirit of the verse suggests naming the child after the dead father, and while this is not a halacha, it is a praiseworthy practice, analogous to the way some authorities treat {\i ve-re\u8217'item oto} by the {\i tzitzit} as suggesting they should be worn visibly, or the way some understand {\i ayin tachat ayin} as conveying a conceptual value even when the operative halacha is monetary compensation. Here, Rava teaches, no such residual spirit of the {\i peshat} applies. The {\i peshat} has been entirely eradicated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Verse Audience Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Address Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} probes the matter further. If the {\i peshat} of the verse were that the child should be named after the dead father, who exactly would the Torah be addressing? If it is speaking to the {\i yavam} \u8212- the brother-in-law \u8212- then the verse should have said {\i yakum al shem achicha}, \u8220"you shall name him after your brother,\u8221" speaking in the second person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General Address\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, the verse may be addressing the {\i beit din} or {\i klal Yisrael} generally, prescribing what shall be done in this situation. Under that reading, the verse is not a directive to the {\i yavam} personally, but a general statement of how things are to be ordered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother vs.\u160 ?Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But even under that reading, a difficulty remains: the verse says {\i al shem achiv} \u8212- after his brother \u8212- rather than {\i al shem aviv} \u8212- after his father. If the verse is about a child being named after the deceased, the deceased is the child\u8217's father, and the verse should have said {\i al shem aviv ha-met}, or alternatively, from the perspective of the {\i beit din} addressing the yavam\u8217's child, {\i al shem achi aviv} \u8212- \u8220"after his father\u8217's brother.\u8221" The use of {\i achiv} \u8212- his brother \u8212- is inexplicable on the {\i peshat} reading, which further supports Rava\u8217's conclusion that the {\i peshat} has been entirely displaced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brother Focus Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Address\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the matter: the verse speaks to the {\i beit din}, directing them to ensure that the {\i yavam} \u8212- referred to as the {\i bechor} because he is the eldest \u8212- performs {\i yibum} and assumes his dead brother\u8217's portion of the inheritance. {\i Yakum al shem achiv} refers not to naming a child but to the {\i yavam} rising to take his brother\u8217's place with respect to {\i nachala}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All About Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Every element of the verse \u8212- {\i ve-haya ha-bechor}, {\i asher teleid}, {\i yakum al shem achiv}, {\i ve-lo yimache shemo} \u8212- refers to the brother performing {\i yibum}, not to any child who may be born. The verse is entirely about the {\i yavam} and his obligations and entitlements, with no residual reference to a child\u8217's name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bechor Exclusivity T (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Exclusivity T (II)heory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now that the verse is established as speaking about the eldest brother, the {\i gemara} raises a series of further questions. Perhaps the verse intends that {\i only} a {\i bechor} \u8212- a halachic firstborn \u8212- may perform {\i yibum}, and non-firstborn brothers may not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof from Shelo Haya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is disproved from the concept of {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be-olamo} \u8212- the wife of a brother who was born after the deceased brother had already died. The Torah established a special exclusion for this case, teaching that there is no {\i mitzvat yibum} in such a situation. But consider: a brother born after his brother\u8217's death cannot be a {\i bechor} by any reckoning, since his older brother died before he was born. The fact that the Torah needed to explicitly exclude this brother from the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} demonstrates that in principle such a brother \u8212- a non-firstborn \u8212- is subject to {\i yibum}. Otherwise, no exclusion would have been necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 No Bechor Scenario (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Presence Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question: perhaps {\i yibum} is valid when performed by a younger brother, but only when a {\i bechor} is still alive. Perhaps the presence of a {\i bechor} is a prerequisite for the {\i mitzvah} to apply at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} refutes this from the verse {\i ki yeshvu achim yachdav} \u8212- \u8220"when brothers dwell together.\u8221" This verse links all the brothers together as an undifferentiated group, implying that the obligation falls upon the surviving brothers as a collective, regardless of whether the {\i bechor} is among them. Furthermore, since the verse {\i u-meit echad mehem} \u8212- \u8220"and one of them died\u8221" \u8212- encompasses the scenario where the {\i bechor} himself died, leaving only a younger brother, and the Torah still mandates {\i yibum}, it is clear that the presence of a {\i bechor} is not a precondition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bechor Exclusivity T (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Younger Brother Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i eshet achiv shelo haya be-olamo} reinforces this conclusion: the brother born after the deceased cannot be a {\i bechor}, yet {\i yibum} applies to him in principle. This confirms that {\i yibum} is not conditioned on the performing brother being a {\i bechor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gadol Hierarchy (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbaye\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abbaye the elder ({\i Abbaye kishisha}) teaches: {\i mitzvah be-gadol le-yabeim} \u8212- it is a {\i mitzvah} for the {\i gadol}, the eldest surviving brother, to perform {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cascade to Younger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i gadol} refuses or is unwilling, the obligation passes to the next younger brother \u8212- {\i achiv ha-katan} \u8212- and proceeds down in order of seniority among the surviving brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Preference vs.\u160 ?Exclusivi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Preference Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the principle of {\i gadol} preference established, the {\i gemara} asks whether that preference negates the capacity of younger brothers to act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Block Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Specifically: perhaps when a {\i bechor} or {\i gadol} is present, the younger brother is entirely blocked from performing {\i yibum}, and his act, if performed, is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gadol Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's use of the word {\i gadol} \u8212- oldest, not {\i bechor}, firstborn \u8212- already suggests that the preference is relative and contextual, not absolute. The eldest surviving brother has priority, but his presence does not invalidate {\i yibum} performed by a younger brother who acts first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gadol Hierarchy (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gadol Default\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If all the brothers refuse \u8212- if each in turn declines to perform either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- the obligation returns to the {\i gadol}, and {\i beit din} demands that he perform one or the other. The {\i gadol} bears ultimate responsibility that the {\i mitzvah} is not left entirely unfulfilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ke-Bechor Derash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse uses the term {\i bechor} \u8212- firstborn \u8212- but the {\i gemara} understands it to mean {\i ke-bechor}: in the manner of a firstborn. Just as firstborn status places one at the head of the line in matters of inheritance, so too does seniority in age place a brother at the head of the line for the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}. The principle of priority is abstracted from {\i bechor} status and applied to the concept of {\i gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Inheritance Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance Exclusivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises yet another possible limitation: perhaps the {\i yavam}\u8217's inheritance of the dead brother\u8217's portion \u8212- {\i yakum al shem achiv le-nachala} \u8212- applies only when the performing {\i yavam} is the {\i bechor}. The {\i bechor} already has enhanced inheritance rights; perhaps the entitlement to the dead brother\u8217's portion through {\i yibum} is his alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universal Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this. {\i Yakum al shem achiv} refers to whoever performs {\i yibum} \u8212- he assumes the dead brother\u8217's place and thereby inherits his portion. There is no limitation to the {\i bechor}. Whoever steps forward and fulfills the {\i mitzvah} receives the dead brother\u8217's estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bechor Status Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terminology Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the verse\u8217's mention of {\i bechor} does not limit the {\i mitzvah} to firstborns, does not restrict the inheritance to firstborns, and does not require the presence of a firstborn \u8212- what purpose does the term {\i bechor} serve in this context?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Privilege Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i le-garei-uta} \u8212- to limit privileges, specifically to impose on the {\i yavam} the same limitation that applies to a {\i bechor} in matters of inheritance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possession vs.\u160 ?Prospecti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i bechor} receives a double portion in his father\u8217's estate, but only in property that was {\i muchzak} \u8212- actually in his father\u8217's possession at the time of death \u8212- and not in property that was {\i ra\u8217'uy} \u8212- prospective or expected but not yet realized. The verse, by calling the {\i yavam} a {\i bechor}, applies this same limitation to him: he inherits the dead brother\u8217's actual possessions, but not the dead brother\u8217's prospective entitlements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam\u8217's Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Concretely, the {\i yavam} who performs {\i yibum} acquires what the dead brother already owned, but does not acquire what was merely coming to the dead brother at some future point \u8212- including, according to some authorities, the dead brother\u8217's prospective share of the father\u8217's estate if the father has not yet died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Inheritance Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A concrete illustration: suppose the father has one million in actual assets and a further hundred thousand in outstanding loans owed to him. Reuven, one of his sons, dies, and Shimon performs {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Actual Possession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shimon, by virtue of {\i yibum}, steps into Reuven\u8217's position with respect to the father\u8217's estate. When the father dies, the one million in actual assets is divided as if there are three sons \u8212- Reuven, Shimon, and Levi \u8212- with Shimon receiving Reuven\u8217's share of approximately 333,000, in addition to his own share. This reflects the inheritance of {\i muchzak} property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prospective Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The hundred thousand in outstanding loans, however, is {\i ra\u8217'uy} \u8212- it was not yet in the father\u8217's possession. When that money is eventually collected, it is divided equally among the living heirs, with Shimon having no enhanced claim to Reuven\u8217's portion thereof. Reuven\u8217's prospective entitlement to a share of the recovered loans does not pass to Shimon through {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gra\u8217's Girsa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gra, citing Rabbeinu Tam, understands the passage differently. According to Rabbeinu Tam, the operative case is not the father\u8217's outstanding loans but rather Reuven\u8217's own existing property at the time of his death. If Reuven already owned a house worth five hundred thousand, and Shimon performs {\i yibum}, Shimon acquires Reuven\u8217's house outright \u8212- that is {\i muchzak}. What Shimon does {\i not} acquire is Reuven\u8217's prospective share of the father\u8217's inheritance if the father is still alive at the time of {\i yibum}. That prospective inheritance is {\i ra\u8217'uy}, and the verse\u8217's equation of the {\i yavam} with a {\i bechor} means that he, like a {\i bechor}, does not receive {\i ra\u8217'uy ke-muchzak}. When the father eventually dies, the estate is divided equally among all living heirs, without Shimon receiving any enhanced claim based on his {\i yibum} of Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five Brothers Debt Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on 24a presents a related case: five brothers who collect a debt that was owed to their father. The question is whether the {\i bechor} among them receives a double portion of the recovered debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Double Portion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules that the {\i bechor} does not receive a double portion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rau\u8217'i Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is that the debt is {\i ra\u8217'uy} \u8212- prospective \u8212- rather than {\i muchzak} \u8212- actually in the father\u8217's possession. The money was in the possession of the borrower during the father\u8217's lifetime, not in the father\u8217's possession. It therefore falls into the category of property to which the {\i bechor}\u8217's double portion does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question arises: if the brothers collected land from the debtor in lieu of the monetary debt, does the status change? The {\i mishnah} addresses this as well, with the position that even land received in payment for the debt retains its {\i ra\u8217'uy} character, since it stands in the place of the debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machloket on Land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is however a {\i machloket} in the {\i gemara} on this point. Some hold that once land has been physically collected and taken into possession, it assumes the status of {\i muchzak}, and the {\i bechor} would then receive a double portion in the land. The definitive ruling on this question is to be examined in the {\i gemara} on the following {\i amud}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 24\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Theme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf under discussion is 24b, and it represents something of a thematic pivot. The immediately preceding {\i mishnah} had dealt with the complex case of two brothers who each married two women in a state of {\i safek}, and its conclusion established a general principle: {\i im kadmu v\u8217'kansu ein motzin mi-yadam} \u8212- if the brothers went ahead and performed {\i yibbum} in a manner that was technically impermissible, they are nonetheless allowed to remain married. The present {\i mishnah} picks up on that very theme: marriages that ought not to have occurred but that are permitted to stand after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theme Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This thematic thread \u8212- prohibited marriages that remain valid {\i bedi\u8217'avad} \u8212- will run through the entirety of the daf. The cases that follow explore what happens when a marriage has been contracted in violation of a rabbinic prohibition. In most such cases, the marriage stands; in one notable case it does not. Understanding what distinguishes the exceptional case from the general rule is the central analytical task of this daf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibbum Primacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this theme was somewhat interrupted by an intervening {\i mishnah} and its accompanying {\i gemara}, which addressed the question of which brother bears the primary obligation of {\i yibbum} \u8212- a discussion anchored in the {\i pasuk} {\i v\u8217'hayah habechor asher teled}, which led the {\i gemara} into a broader exploration of the {\i pesukim} governing {\i yibbum}: whether the verse concerns naming a son after the deceased brother, or whether it speaks to the {\i yavam}\u8217's right to inherit his brother\u8217's estate. That extended discussion, however important in its own right, was something of an interruption. We now return to the theme of marriages contracted improperly and the question of whether they may stand.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah: Affairs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave/Non-Jew Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on 24b presents two distinct scenarios. The first: {\i ha-nit\u8217'an al ha-shifchah v\u8217'nishtachrerah} \u8212- a man who is suspected of having had a sexual relationship with a non-Jewish slave, who was subsequently freed, or {\i al ha-nachrit v\u8217'nitgayerah} \u8212- with a non-Jewish woman who subsequently converted. In both instances, the {\i mishnah} rules {\i harei zeh lo yikanes} \u8212- he should not go ahead and marry this woman. Even granting that the rumor may be entirely false, the act of marrying her would amount to a retroactive endorsement of the rumor. People will say: evidently it was true all along \u8212- she converted and married him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i v\u8217'im kanas, ein motzin mi-yado} \u8212- if he nevertheless went ahead and married her, we do not force a divorce. The prohibition is thus {\i lechatchila} only; once the marriage has occurred, it stands. The concern animating the {\i lechatchila} prohibition is entirely sociological: the marriage reinforces a damaging narrative. But once the marriage has been contracted, that concern does not rise to the level of requiring dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Married Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario is categorically different. {\i Ha-nit\u8217'an al eshet ish v\u8217'hotzi\u8217'uha} \u8212- a man who was suspected of having an adulterous affair with a married woman who was subsequently divorced from her husband. Here, the ruling is {\i af al pi she-kanas yotzi} \u8212- even if he went ahead and married her {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, he must divorce her. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's language {\i hotzi\u8217'uha} \u8212- literally, they removed her \u8212- suggests that the divorce from her first husband was compelled rather than voluntary, a point the {\i gemara} will revisit at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry between the two cases invites explanation. One might initially think that forcing a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} divorce in the married woman case is counterproductive, since that forced divorce itself reinforces the very rumor it is meant to address: if the marriage has to be dissolved, people will say the affair must have been real. Yet the {\i mishnah} insists on the forced divorce all the same. Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in the severity of the underlying concern. In the case of the non-Jewish woman or the slave, even if the rumor were true, there is no {\i halacha} that would render her forbidden to him. A man who was involved with a non-Jewish woman before her conversion incurs no biblical prohibition against marrying her thereafter. The only objection is the reinforcement of the rumor. Accordingly, {\i bedi\u8217'avad} the marriage stands. In the adultery case, by contrast, if the rumor is true, there is a biblical prohibition at stake: a woman who commits adultery is forbidden {\i de\u8217'oraita} not only to her husband but to the man with whom she transgressed. Were the rumor true, he could not be married to her even biblically. This is the operative distinction \u8212- and it is why even {\i bedi\u8217'avad} we cannot permit them to remain married in the second case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geirus Motivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conversion Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks a sharper question about the {\i giyoret} in the first case. The {\i mishnah} implies that the conversion is valid \u8212- the objection is only to the marriage. But is this obvious? One might argue that a woman who converted precisely in order to marry a specific Jewish man \u8212- especially when there was already a rumor of a relationship between them \u8212- has converted with an ulterior motive. Does such a conversion qualify as genuine {\i geirus}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulterior Motives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a {\i braita} as a challenge: {\i echad ish shenitgayer leshum isha, ve\u8217'echad isha shenitgayerah leshum ish} \u8212- a man who converted for the sake of a woman, or a woman who converted for the sake of a man. The {\i braita} continues: {\i vechen mi shenitgayer leshum shulchan melachim} \u8212- someone who converted for the sake of, literally, the royal table; that is, because being Jewish offered social or economic advantage. An historical example would be the era of David and Solomon, when attaching oneself to the Jewish nation carried clear benefits. The {\i braita} also mentions {\i leshum avdei Shlomo} \u8212- converting to marry one of Solomon\u8217's servants, who were of non-Jewish origin. All of these are cases of conversion driven by calculation rather than sincere religious motivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Nechemia Invalidity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Einam gerim divrei Rabbi Nechemia} \u8212- none of these conversions are valid, according to Rabbi Nechemia. Rabbi Nechemia elaborates: whether one converted because of {\i girei arayot} \u8212- as the Samaritans did, out of fear of the lions sent against them, as recounted in {\i Tanach} at the end of the period of the First Temple \u8212- or because of a dream ({\i girei chalomot}), or because of the mass conversions inspired by fear during the Purim period, {\i girei Mordechai ve\u8217'Esther}, as the verse says {\i verabbim me\u8217'ammei ha\u8217'aretz mityahadim ki nafal pachad ha\u8217'yehudim aleihem}: in all these cases, fear rather than religious conviction was the motivating force. Rabbi Nechemia\u8217's ruling is sweeping: unless the conversion occurs {\i bazman hazeh} \u8212- at a time when there is neither great advantage nor great danger in being Jewish, and thus the only plausible motive is genuine religious commitment \u8212- the {\i geirus} is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yitzchak Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction with the {\i mishnah} by citing a ruling: {\i amar Rav Yitzchak bar Shmuel bar Marta mishmei deRav: halacha kedivrei ha\u8217'omer kulam gerim heim} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows the view that says they are all valid {\i gerim}. We do not rule like Rabbi Nechemia. What is notable is that no alternative {\i tanna} is even explicitly quoted; the {\i gemara} simply asserts the existence of an opposing voice \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'omer kulam gerim} \u8212- and rules accordingly. This is itself a striking feature of the passage. {\i Bedi\u8217'avad}, conversions with ulterior motives are valid, and the {\i giyoret} in our {\i mishnah} is fully Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Allowance Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately raises a new question: {\i ei hachi lechatchila nami} \u8212- if the conversion is entirely valid, why should the man not be permitted to marry her outright? The {\i gemara} had initially assumed that the prohibition against marriage might be grounded in a doubt about the quality of the conversion itself; once that doubt is removed, what justification remains for prohibiting the marriage even {\i lechatchila}? Note that this question applies most naturally to the {\i giyoret} case, not to the freed slave, since the freeing of a slave involves no question of motivation \u8212- once freed, she is simply free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers by invoking the principle articulated by Rav Ashi: {\i haser mimcha ikshus peh v\u8217'lazus sefasayim} \u8212- remove from yourself the perversion of the mouth, avoid becoming the subject of rumor and disrepute. Even though the {\i giyoret} is fully Jewish and the marriage would be entirely valid, going ahead and marrying the woman about whom one\u8217's affair was the subject of public rumor reinforces that rumor, normalizes the behavior, and contributes to a corrosive social dynamic. The concern is not merely personal but societal: allowing the marriage to proceed {\i lechatchila} creates a visible pattern in which affairs are conducted, a conversion arranged, and a marriage follows \u8212- all apparently validated. {\i Chazal} sought to resist that pattern, even at the cost of restricting an otherwise valid marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Pnuyot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unmarried Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a natural objection, precisely the question a careful reader would ask: what about a case where the suspected relationship was between two single people, a {\i pnuya}? If the prohibition is driven by the concern of {\i lazus sefasayim} \u8212- creating or reinforcing damaging rumor \u8212- should that concern not equally apply when an unmarried woman converts and then marries a Jewish man with whom she had been involved?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Proper\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} answers that in the case of two unmarried people, marriage is in fact the {\i proper} response. The Torah itself, in the context of {\i anusa}, prescribes marriage as the appropriate resolution to such a situation. When a man has been involved with an unmarried woman, the act of marrying her is not a concession to impropriety but rather the halakhically correct response \u8212- {\i adaraba mitzvah l\u8217'chonsah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Behavioral Outcome\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction {\i Tosafot} draws is therefore one of behavioral outcome. The objection of {\i lazus sefasayim} applies only when marriage to the woman in question is itself improper \u8212- when the appropriate halakhic response to the prior relationship would have been to end it, not to formalize it. In the case of an unmarried woman, the prior relationship, while problematic, calls for marriage as its resolution. The rumor concern therefore falls away, and the marriage is not only permitted but indicated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geirus Centrality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geirus Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} is one of the most central in all of rabbinic literature for the contemporary questions surrounding {\i geirus}. There are two major issues that dominate the modern discussion: first, what constitutes adequate {\i kabbalat mitzvot} \u8212- the requisite acceptance of the commandments; and second, what role motivation plays in the validity of a conversion. These questions are often intertwined in practice, since someone who converts primarily for social or marital reasons may not be genuinely committed to {\i mitzvah} observance. But the {\i gemara} itself does not address them together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motivation Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} deals exclusively with the question of motivation, and it does so without any explicit reference to the depth of halakhic commitment. One might reasonably infer that Rabbi Nechemia\u8217's concern about ulterior motives is ultimately a concern about sincerity of commitment \u8212- someone who converts for the sake of marriage may not intend to observe {\i mitzvot} seriously. But Rabbi Nechemia himself does not say this. His stated objection is to the motivation itself, as if the act of conversion must be intrinsically and religiously motivated to be valid, regardless of what commitment it produces.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motivation Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} follows the opposing view: motivation is a {\i lechatchila} concern, not a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} invalidation. A conversion performed with an ulterior motive is not ideal, but once performed, it stands. This is the clear ruling against Rabbi Nechemia.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the contemporary world, conversions for the sake of marriage occur routinely \u8212- including in contexts that touch on the broadly Orthodox community, particularly in cases where a couple has already intermarried and one partner subsequently wishes to convert. The classical halakhic position \u8212- that such conversions are {\i lechatchila} prohibited \u8212- has been subjected to significant reconsideration in modern {\i teshuvah} literature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kluger Teshuvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One important early treatment of this question is a {\i teshuvah} by Rav Shlomo Kluger. The argument runs as follows: historically, conversion for the sake of marriage served as the mechanism that made the marriage possible in the first place \u8212- without the conversion, the Jewish-non-Jewish marriage simply could not occur. In that context, the conversion was purely instrumental, a means to an otherwise unavailable end. But in modern societies where civil marriage across religious lines is entirely legal and socially accepted, a conversion no longer functions as a prerequisite for marriage. The couple could simply marry without any conversion. When a non-Jewish partner nonetheless chooses to convert, the motivation is therefore likely to be something more than merely removing an obstacle \u8212- it may involve a genuine desire to identify with the Jewish people, to be part of the spouse\u8217's family and community, or to raise Jewish children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bedi\u8217'avad Reality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a broader {\i bedi\u8217'avad} argument that has gained traction in contemporary {\i poskim}: we already inhabit a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} reality. The practical choice is not between a {\i lechatchila} conversion and no conversion, but between a conversion undertaken with mixed motives and the alternative of the couple marrying without any conversion whatsoever. Given that framing, a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} conversion represents a more halakhically constructive outcome than no conversion at all. The {\i cheshbon} has thus shifted significantly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extreme Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A more extreme position inverts this logic. Some authorities argue that in the specific case of our {\i mishnah} \u8212- where a woman converts and then intends to marry the man with whom she is suspected of having had an affair \u8212- the {\i geirus} should be considered invalid not merely {\i lechatchila} but even {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. The reasoning: conversion requires {\i kabbalat mitzvot}, and if the woman is planning from the outset to violate the halakhic prohibition against marrying this particular man, then she is actively excluding at least one {\i halacha} from her acceptance of {\i mitzvot} at the very moment of her conversion. A conversion that proceeds with the explicit intention of transgressing a rabbinic prohibition cannot be said to involve genuine {\i kabbalat mitzvot}. This is a demanding position, but it illustrates how the {\i gemara}\u8217's ruling \u8212- that {\i bedi\u8217'avad} all these conversions are valid \u8212- does not necessarily resolve every case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerim David/Solomon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashiach Era\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now cites a {\i braita} that broadens the discussion of motivation-based restrictions on {\i geirus}: {\i ein mekabelim gerim lemot HaMashiach} \u8212- we do not accept converts in the era of {\i Mashiach}. The reasoning is plain: when the Jewish people are triumphant and the messianic reality is manifest, conversion would be driven by the obvious advantages of association with the victorious nation, not by genuine religious conviction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} extends this principle historically: {\i lo kibelu gerim lo bimei David velo bimei Shlomo} \u8212- converts were not accepted in the era of David and in the era of Solomon. These were periods of extraordinary Jewish flourishing, when conversion to Judaism carried real social and material benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites the scriptural basis from a verse read homiletically: {\i hen gor yagor efes me\u8217'oti mi gar itach alayich yipol}. The phrase {\i efes me\u8217'oti} \u8212- without me, or apart from my benefit \u8212- is read as a condition: a {\i ger} who converts must do so without material or social benefit accruing to him. Only {\i mi gar itach} \u8212- one who joins when the Jewish people are in a less advantageous position \u8212- will have a genuine portion with them in the future.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises an obvious difficulty: we know from the biblical record that conversions did occur in the time of David and Solomon. What of the wife of Uriah the Hittite, of {\i Ittai HaGitti}, or of Pharaoh\u8217's daughter whom Solomon married? {\i Tosafot} offers two answers. Regarding some \u8212- such as those wealthy individuals who attached themselves to Solomon\u8217's court \u8212- the economic motive argument does not apply since they were already prosperous. But the harder cases \u8212- the large-scale conversions recorded during David\u8217's reign \u8212- {\i Tosafot} explains as having occurred either spontaneously, {\i mei\u8217'aleihen}, without formal rabbinic reception, or as having been processed by a {\i beit din shel hedyotot}, a court of non-expert judges, or alternatively as cases of {\i gerim gerurim}, conversions that were driven along rather than genuinely embraced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam addresses this directly. He explains that even when conversions were not supposed to be accepted \u8212- because of the historical context that rendered all motivation suspect \u8212- any conversion actually performed by a functioning {\i beit din}, even an irregular one, is valid {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. This is consistent with the ruling against Rabbi Nechemia: {\i lechatchila} the courts should not have accepted these {\i gerim}, but having done so, the {\i geirus} stands. History has in fact produced many such {\i bedi\u8217'avad} realities, including perhaps the mass conversions of the {\i Chashmonaim} period and others.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Adultery Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the second case of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the suspected affair with a married woman \u8212- and investigates what type of evidence is required to trigger the {\i bedi\u8217'avad} compelled divorce described there. If a woman whose husband divorced her on account of a suspected affair then goes ahead and marries the man she was suspected of having the affair with, must she divorce him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's position is stringent in one sense and lenient in another: {\i ub\u8217'edim} \u8212- only if there are witnesses who actually observed the act of adultery will we compel a divorce from the second husband. A rumor, no matter how persistent or widespread, does not suffice. In the absence of formal testimony, even a woman who marries the man she was suspected of having an affair with need not be compelled to divorce him {\i bedi\u8217'avad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheshet Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet objects forcefully. He says that Rav must have been falling asleep when he said that \u8212- {\i d\u8217'ki nayim v\u8217'shachiv Rav amrah l\u8217'shma\u8217'ata} \u8212- because a {\i braita} directly contradicts him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} presents the following case: a woman is suspected of having an affair with Reuven. She is divorced from her husband \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hotziuha} \u8212- and subsequently marries a different man, whom we will call Shimon, not the man she was suspected of having the affair with. She then divorces Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Interrupt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} rules: {\i im kanas lo yotzi} \u8212- if Reuven then marries her, he need not divorce her. The reasoning is intuitive: the fact that she went and married a completely different man after the divorce effectively undermines any lingering suspicion that she and Reuven were involved. Had the affair been real, she presumably would have married Reuven immediately. Having married someone else first, the rumor has been interrupted and substantially discredited, and by the time she and Reuven marry, the narrative has sufficiently shifted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita}\u8217's logic, as the {\i gemara} reads it, cuts against Rav. If there were witnesses to the adultery, the {\i braita}\u8217's scenario makes no sense \u8212- the interruption by a second marriage would be irrelevant, since we have objective proof she committed adultery and she is therefore forbidden to Reuven regardless. The scenario must therefore be one without witnesses. And the {\i braita}\u8217's implication is clear: {\i ha lav hachi mafkinan} \u8212- had there been no marriage to a second man to interrupt the rumor, Reuven would have been compelled to divorce her even in the absence of witnesses. This appears to be a direct refutation of Rav.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav/Braita Reconcile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav responds to this challenge by proposing a reading of the {\i mishnah} and {\i braita} that preserves his position. Rav argues that the scenario involves two conditions simultaneously: the woman has children from her first husband, {\i and} there are witnesses to the adultery. On this reading, the case where Reuven is permitted to remain married to her after the second marriage interrupted the rumor is specifically because of the combination of these two factors, and the forced divorce in the other case is driven by the witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's actual halakhic position is thus: without witnesses, a divorce is never compelled, regardless of circumstances. With witnesses, a divorce is compelled even if the woman has children from her first husband. The reference to a second marriage interrupting the rumor, on this reading, is not about eliminating the need for witnesses but about limiting the {\i lechatchila} prohibition even in cases where witnesses are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further. The {\i braita} elsewhere seems to speak plainly: {\i keshe\u8217'ein lah banim} \u8212- when she has no children from her first husband \u8212- we do force a divorce, and this without any mention of witnesses. {\i Aval yesh lah banim, lo tetze} \u8212- if she has children from the first husband, we do not force a divorce, because to do so would reinforce the suspicion and potentially cast doubt on the legitimacy of those children, making people suspect they are {\i mamzerim}. And {\i ve\u8217'im ba\u8217'u edei tumah, afilu yesh lah kama banim tetze} \u8212- only if there are actual witnesses to the adultery do we force a divorce even when there are children. The implication is unambiguous: the baseline case of {\i ein lah banim} involves forcing a divorce even without witnesses. This is a compelling proof against Rav.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Intervention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves the difficulty by focusing on the {\i mishnah}\u8217's precise language. {\i Mah irya detani hotziuha} \u8212- why does the {\i mishnah} use the term {\i hotzi\u8217'uha}, meaning they removed her, rather than simply saying her husband divorced her? The only scenario in which a {\i beit din} would have compelled the divorce from the first husband is one in which there were witnesses. Courts do not force women to divorce their husbands on the basis of rumor alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Rava\u8217's reading, {\i hotzi\u8217'uha} \u8212- {\i k\u8217'vo hotzi\u8217'uha d\u8217'beit din} \u8212- is the linguistic marker that the first divorce was court-compelled, which in turn requires witnesses. Rav is thus not making a novel halakhic claim; he is reading the {\i mishnah}\u8217's specific language as presupposing witnesses. The underlying {\i halacha} \u8212- that without witnesses and without children a divorce would be forced, while without witnesses but with children it would not \u8212- is not disputed. What Rav maintains is only that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation implies witnesses were present. This removes any substantive {\i halakhic chiddush} from Rav\u8217's position, but the {\i gemara} subsequently makes clear that Rav\u8217's position is in fact halakhically serious in its own right, and that we will never force a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} divorce from the second husband without witnesses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Refined Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Compromise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now articulates a compromise between the positions of Rav and Rabbi. With witnesses, a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} divorce is forced, in accordance with Rav. But what about a {\i kala delo pasik} \u8212- a rumor that simply does not cease? In such a case, the {\i gemara} rules in accordance with Rabbi that a divorce may be forced even without direct testimony. This is a middle position: Rav himself would not have conceded this even for an unceasing rumor, and Rabbi might have gone further still in the direction of circumstantial evidence. But the compromise position adopted by the {\i gemara} is that the persistence of a rumor, by itself and without witnesses, creates a sufficient basis for compelling a divorce \u8212- provided other conditions are met.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Stringent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suspicious Conduct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces the {\i braita} that establishes Rabbi\u8217's broader position on circumstantial evidence. {\i Rochel yotze v\u8217'ishah chogeres besinar} \u8212- the spice-seller, the traveling merchant, is leaving the house, and the husband\u8217's wife is fastening her girdle. The scene is, to put it delicately, strongly suggestive. There are no witnesses to any actual act, no testimony, no direct evidence. {\i Amar Rabbi hu mechurar hadavar, tetzei} \u8212- Rabbi says, because this is an unseemly thing, she must be divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i hu mechurar hadavar} is itself ambiguous, and the ambiguity is significant. It could be read as an evidentiary claim: the circumstantial evidence is strong enough that we can reasonably assume something happened, and therefore she must divorce. Alternatively, it might be read as a propriety-based claim: regardless of whether anything actually happened, the situation is sufficiently improper that continuing the marriage in the shadow of such a scene is halakhically intolerable. The difference between {\i yesh raglayim ladavar} \u8212- there is good basis for assuming something occurred \u8212- and {\i mechurar hadavar} \u8212- the matter is unseemly \u8212- is meaningful. The latter formulation does not require that we conclude the act took place; it requires only that the appearance of impropriety has crossed a threshold we cannot ignore.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Additional Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi applies the same standard to other suspicious circumstances. {\i Rok lemalah min hakilah} \u8212- if there is spit on the canopy above the bed, which {\i Rashi} takes literally. {\i Amar Rabbi hen l\u8217'mechurar davar tetzei}. {\i Minalayim hafuchim tachat hamitah} \u8212- shoes overturned beneath the bed, interpreted by the {\i gemara} as indicating that shoes had been placed there and the situation rearranged. In each of these cases, Rabbi rules {\i tetzei} \u8212- she must be divorced \u8212- on grounds of seemliness and the appearance of impropriety, without requiring direct testimony to the act of adultery itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records two apparently contradictory rulings. {\i V\u8217'hilchata kevatei d\u8217'Rav} \u8212- we rule like Rav, requiring witnesses. {\i V\u8217'hilchata kevatei d\u8217'Rabbi} \u8212- we also rule like Rabbi, forcing a divorce based on circumstantial evidence. These two rulings seem irreconcilable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is clean. {\i Lo kashya ha bekala depasik ha bekala delo pasik} \u8212- there is no difficulty. The two rulings apply to two different situations. Rav\u8217's requirement of witnesses applies to a {\i kala depasik} \u8212- a rumor that has ceased. Where the rumor has died down, we will not force a divorce without formal testimony. Rabbi\u8217's circumstantial evidence standard applies to a {\i kala delo pasik} \u8212- a rumor that has not ceased, that continues to circulate without interruption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot: where the rumor is continuous and uninterrupted, it carries sufficient weight to force a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} divorce even in the absence of formal witnesses, in accordance with Rabbi. Where the rumor has stopped, only witnesses will suffice, in accordance with Rav. It is worth noting that Rav and Rabbi themselves would likely not have agreed to this precise compromise \u8212- Rav would not have conceded even a persistent rumor as grounds for compelled divorce, and Rabbi may have gone further in both directions. But this middle position is what the {\i gemara} adopts as the operative {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Refined Halakha (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Duration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that a {\i kala delo pasik} creates grounds for a compelled divorce, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i v\u8217'kala delo pasik ad kamma} \u8212- how long must a rumor persist to qualify? {\i Amar Abaye amrah li em} \u8212- Abaye said, my mother \u8212- who was in fact his foster mother \u8212- told me: a day and a half. This is a remarkably low threshold. A rumor sustained for a day and a half without interruption is sufficient to constitute a {\i kala delo pasik} and to create the legal basis for compelled divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meaningful Interrupt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately qualifies this. {\i V\u8217'lo amrin ela d\u8217'lo pasik beinei u\u8217'beinei} \u8212- this applies only if the rumor was genuinely continuous without pause during that day and a half. If there was an interruption, the rumor is {\i pasik} and does not qualify. But the {\i gemara} adds a further qualification: {\i v\u8217'lo amrin ela d\u8217'lo pasik machmat yira} \u8212- even an apparent pause does not count as a genuine interruption if the people spreading the rumor stopped only because they were afraid of consequences. {\i Aval pasik machmat yira, machmat yira hu} \u8212- if the cessation was driven by fear rather than by the rumor having run its natural course, the interruption carries no weight. The rumor is still considered continuous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enemies Caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Perhaps the most practically significant qualification is the last: {\i v\u8217'lo amrin ela d\u8217'leika oyvim} \u8212- all of these provisions apply only if the people involved have no enemies. {\i Aval ika oyvim, oyvim hu d\u8217'afki leih l\u8217'kala} \u8212- if there are enemies, they are the ones who generated and sustained the rumor, and a rumor driven by hostile parties carries no probative weight. This caveat provides a real avenue of defense: in a world where virtually anyone may have adversaries willing to spread damaging stories, the grounds for establishing a legally actionable {\i kala} are substantially narrowed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} now raises a fundamental question about the scope of Rabbi\u8217's rulings in the suspicious-circumstance cases \u8212- the milkman leaving, the spit on the canopy, the shoes beneath the bed. Reading the {\i gemara} straightforwardly, one might conclude that Rabbi\u8217's standard for compelled divorce applies equally to the woman\u8217's divorce from her original husband: that these kinds of suspicious circumstances would be sufficient to compel her husband to divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} emphatically limits Rabbi\u8217's rulings. {\i Kol ha\u8217'sugya airya b\u8217'nit\u8217'an} \u8212- the entire {\i sugya} is discussing whether to force a divorce from the man suspected of having had the affair, the second husband, not about compelling the dissolution of the original marriage. When Rabbi says {\i tetzei} in the milkman case, he means that if this woman subsequently marries the traveling merchant, she can be compelled to divorce him. He is not ruling that her original husband must divorce her on the basis of these suspicious circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the divorce from the original husband, {\i Tosafot} insists on a far higher evidentiary standard. {\i De\u8217'ein isha nis\u8217'eseret al ba\u8217'alah ela al yedei kinui u\u8217'setira} \u8212- a woman is not rendered forbidden to her husband except through the formal {\i sotah} process: {\i edim} testifying to the husband\u8217's formal warning ({\i kinui}) followed by {\i edim} testifying that she secluded herself with the warned man ({\i setira}). Alternatively, direct observation of the sexual act \u8212- {\i o she\u8217'yiru k\u8217'derech hamenafim} \u8212- is required. These are the standards of Masechet Sotah, and they are the only grounds for compelling a woman\u8217's divorce from her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 High Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reasoning is clear. The stakes of compelling a woman to divorce her husband are of an entirely different order than the question of whether she may remain married to a second man she was not supposed to marry in the first place. The {\i gemara}\u8217's discussion of persistent rumors and circumstantial evidence, which might have seemed to create a broadly applicable standard for compelled divorce in adultery cases, is therefore limited by {\i Tosafot} to the specific question of the second husband. For the original marriage, the evidentiary bar remains extremely high. Even a case where a private investigator produces significant circumstantial evidence \u8212- short of actual testimony to the sexual act \u8212- would not suffice, according to Rav\u8217's reading, to compel divorce from the original husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bad Rep/Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i mishnah}, cited from elsewhere, addresses two additional cases. {\i Hamotzi et ishto mishum shem ra, lo yachazir} \u8212- a man who divorces his wife because of a bad reputation, because of rumors of adultery, may not remarry her. {\i Mishum neder, lo yachazir} \u8212- a man who divorces his wife because she made a vow he found intolerable may likewise not remarry her. In both cases, the prohibition on remarriage is rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale for the {\i neder} case is worth examining carefully. Suppose a man divorces his wife because of a vow she made. Later, she has the vow annulled \u8212- there are halakhic mechanisms for annulling vows through a {\i posek} who finds a {\i petach}, an opening, some basis on which the vow was made in error or can be retroactively undone. Or suppose the husband later comes to believe that the suspicion of adultery underlying {\i shem ra} was false. In either case, the husband might be tempted to argue: had I known that the vow could be annulled, or had I known the rumor was false, I never would have divorced her. The {\i get} was given on the assumption that these facts were as I believed them to be. Now that I discover they were otherwise, the {\i get} was given under a mistaken assumption and should be {\i batel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concern is devastating: it means that the woman\u8217's status \u8212- and the status of any children she may have borne in a subsequent marriage \u8212- would always be in doubt, hostage to the first husband\u8217's later claims. The rabbinic response is to eliminate the possibility of remarriage entirely. If the man knows from the outset that there is no path back \u8212- that divorcing for {\i shem ra} or {\i neder} forecloses remarriage absolutely \u8212- then the very act of giving the {\i get} demonstrates that his reasons were not contingent. He would not have divorced her knowing he could never remarry her unless the reasons were real and final. The absence of any opportunity for remarriage thus guarantees the unconditional nature of the divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unconditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the logic: by foreclosing remarriage, the rabbis ensure that the {\i get} cannot subsequently be characterized as conditional or as given under a mistaken assumption. The man who divorces knowing he can never remarry is making a definitive statement. He cannot later plausibly claim he only intended to divorce on the condition that the facts were as he believed them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post Facto Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads naturally to the question that anchors the subsequent {\i gemara} discussion: if a man divorces his wife for {\i shem ra} or {\i neder} and then \u8212- in violation of the rabbinic prohibition \u8212- remarries her anyway, what is the ruling {\i bedi\u8217'avad}? Must he divorce her again? This question fits squarely within the daf\u8217's overarching theme: when a marriage that should not have occurred has nonetheless taken place, does it stand or must it be dissolved?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Case Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explores whether the {\i bedi\u8217'avad} rule from the adultery case \u8212- {\i af al pi she-kanas yotzi} \u8212- can be applied to the {\i shem ra} and {\i neder} remarriage cases. An objection is raised: the two cases are not parallel. In the adultery case, the {\i gemara} noted that {\i hotzi\u8217'uha} implies the court forced the first divorce, which requires witnesses. The divorce from the original husband was externally compelled on the basis of objective evidence. In the {\i shem ra} and {\i neder} cases, the husband chose to divorce voluntarily. {\i Hasam hotziuha, hacha hotziah} \u8212- there the court removed her; here he removed her on his own initiative. The grounds for applying the same strict {\i bedi\u8217'avad} rule are therefore weaker.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The counterargument is structural: in both cases, there is a rabbinic prohibition that was violated by the marriage. In both cases, the principle should be the same: {\i lo yikanes, v\u8217'im kanas, yotzi} \u8212- do not marry, and if you did, divorce. The violation of a rabbinic restriction should carry the same {\i bedi\u8217'avad} consequence regardless of the specific content of that restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Severity Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back. The adultery case is categorically more severe, precisely because of the {\i kala} dynamic. When the suspected adulterer marries the woman he was suspected of having an affair with, the marriage itself reinforces the damaging rumor \u8212- it appears to confirm that the affair was real. This active reinforcement of a socially destructive narrative makes the {\i bedi\u8217'avad} marriage intolerable in a way that has no parallel in the {\i shem ra} and {\i neder} remarriage cases. In those cases, by contrast, a remarriage serves the opposite function: if a man divorces his wife claiming there was an affair and then remarries her, observers will conclude that the rumor was false \u8212- why else would he remarry her? The remarriage undermines rather than reinforces the rumor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i v\u8217'lo hi} \u8212- the comparison fails. {\i Hasam alumi alma l\u8217'kala} \u8212- in the adultery case, the marriage reinforces the rumor and cannot be permitted to stand. {\i Hacha amina kam bei b\u8217'kala u\u8217'leisa} \u8212- in the remarriage case, the act of remarrying demonstrates that the rumor must have been groundless. The {\i gemara} thus rules that in the {\i shem ra} and {\i neder} remarriage cases, {\i bedi\u8217'avad} the marriage stands and he need not divorce her. This ruling affirms the broader pattern of the {\i mishnah}: in the overwhelming majority of cases where a prohibited marriage has occurred, the marriage is permitted to endure. Only where the marriage itself compounds an ongoing {\i halachic} problem \u8212- as in the adultery case \u8212- does the {\i bedi\u8217'avad} dissolution become mandatory.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 24b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous Mishnah Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 25, but we begin at the top of 24b. The preceding {\i mishnah} addressed a situation of two brothers who each married two women under conditions of {\i safek} \u8212- uncertainty \u8212- regarding the obligations of {\i yibum}. That {\i mishnah} concluded with the ruling: {\i im kadmu ve-kanasu ein motzi\u8217'in miyadam} \u8212- if the brothers went ahead and performed {\i yibum} with both women, we do not compel them to dissolve the marriages. The present {\i mishnah} picks up on that theme: marriages that ought not to have taken place in the first instance, but which, once contracted, may be permitted to remain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Insertion Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this thematic thread was interrupted by a {\i mishnah} that, in a sense, could have appeared at the very opening of the tractate \u8212- or whose {\i gemara} at least could have been the first {\i gemara} of the {\i masechet}. That discussion concerned the principle that the primary obligation of {\i yibum} devolves upon the eldest surviving brother. This led the {\i gemara} to engage the relevant {\i pesukim}, including {\i ve-hayah ha-bechor asher teled}, and to probe whether the verse speaks of naming the deceased brother\u8217's son or of inheritance rights \u8212- ultimately arriving at core questions about the nature and consequences of {\i yibum}. Important as that discussion was, it constituted something of an interruption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theme Transition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now return to the governing theme: what is the {\i halakhah} when a marriage that should not have been contracted has in fact taken place? Do we compel dissolution, or do we permit the parties to remain together {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Suspected Liaison: Non-J\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freed Slave Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on 24b opens: {\i ha-nichshad al ha-shifcha ve-nishtachrerah} \u8212- a man who is suspected of having had a sexual relationship with a non-Jewish slave woman who is subsequently freed by her master. Even if the suspicion is nothing more than a rumor \u8212- even if it may be entirely false \u8212- the {\i mishnah} rules {\i harei zeh lo yichnos}: this man should not go ahead and marry her. The reason is sociological: by marrying the woman about whom there was a rumor of an affair, he effectively reinforces that rumor. People will say, \u8220"Clearly it was true \u8212- she was freed, and now he married her.\u8221" Nonetheless, {\i ve-im kanas ein motzi\u8217'in mi-yado} \u8212- if he did go ahead and marry her, we do not force them to divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Converting Non-Jew Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with a parallel case: {\i o al ovedet kokhavim ve-nitgayerah} \u8212- a man suspected of having a relationship with a non-Jewish woman who subsequently converts to Judaism. The same ruling applies: he should not marry her {\i le-khatkhila}, but if he did, the marriage stands {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rationale for Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i mishnah} permits these marriages {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} \u8212- even while discouraging them {\i le-khatkhila} \u8212- is that in both of these cases, even if the rumor were entirely true, the relationship itself would not have constituted a biblical prohibition between the two of them that would carry forward. There is no {\i halakhah} that renders a man permanently forbidden to a woman with whom he had an illicit relationship prior to her becoming fully Jewish or free. The entire concern is therefore one of social perception \u8212- of reinforcing a {\i kala}, a rumor \u8212- and for that reason, {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad}, we tolerate the marriage and do not compel divorce.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Suspected Liaison: Marri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Married Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents a sharply different case: {\i ha-nichshad al eshet ish} \u8212- a man suspected of having had an adulterous affair with a married woman who is subsequently divorced from her husband. The {\i mishnah} uses the striking phrase {\i hotzi\u8217'uha mitachat yado}, which carries the connotation of forced removal \u8212- we will return to that language in the {\i gemara}. In this case, {\i af al pi she-kanas yotzi}: even if {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} he went ahead and married this woman, he must divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why More Stringent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is the {\i eshet ish} case categorically more stringent? The answer lies in a critical distinction. In the cases of the {\i shifcha} and the {\i nochrit}, the concern is purely one of rumor \u8212- of {\i lashon ha-ra} and its social consequences. But in the case of a married woman, if the rumor were true, there would be a biblical prohibition: a woman who commits adultery becomes forbidden both to her husband and to the man with whom she had the affair. The potential harm is therefore not merely sociological but {\i d\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: but perhaps the rumor is false? Perhaps no affair took place at all \u8212- in which case, why should we force a divorce based on unverified suspicion? That question drives the {\i gemara\u8217's} extended discussion. But the basic distinction the {\i mishnah} draws is this: in the first two cases, even granting the truth of the rumor, there would be no inherent {\i issur} going forward; in the third case, were the rumor true, the union would be biblically prohibited. This asymmetry justifies the asymmetric {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further complication deserves mention: forcing a divorce from the second man might itself seem to reinforce the rumor. If the rabbis compel divorce, people will say, \u8220"You see \u8212- it must have been true; that is why the court would not let them remain married.\u8221" That concern weighs against compulsion. Yet the {\i mishnah} rules that we compel divorce anyway, because we cannot tolerate a potentially forbidden union \u8212- particularly one that carries the risk that children born to the first husband might be suspected of being {\i mamzeirim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conversion Intent & Moti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conversion Validity Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a challenge. Granted that the man should not marry the {\i giyoret b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} \u8212- but if she has genuinely converted and is now a full Jewess, why should the marriage be forbidden even {\i le-khatkhila}? After all, there is nothing technically impermissible about a Jewish man marrying a Jewish woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulterior Motive Converts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a {\i baraita} that complicates the picture: {\i echad ish she-nitgayer l\u8217'shum isha} \u8212- a man who converted in order to marry a Jewish woman \u8212- {\i ve-echad isha she-nitgayerah l\u8217'shum ish} \u8212- or a woman who converted in order to marry a Jewish man \u8212- {\i ve-khen mi she-nitgayer l\u8217'shum shulchan melachim} \u8212- or one who converted because of the material benefits of being Jewish, as in the time of Solomon\u8217's servants \u8212- {\i einam gerim, divrei Rabi Nechemia}: none of these are valid converts, according to Rabbi Nechemia.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Nehemiah\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nechemia\u8217's principle is articulated through a list of paradigm cases: {\i gerei arayot} \u8212- those who converted out of fear of lions, as in the story of the Samaritans in {\i Tanakh}; {\i gerei chalomot} \u8212- those who converted because of dreams or visions, driven again by fear of consequences rather than genuine religious motivation; and {\i gerei Mordechai ve-Esther} \u8212- those who converted during the events described in {\i Megillat Esther}, when {\i rabbim me-ammei ha-aretz mityahadim ki nafal pachad ha-Yehudim aleihem}, many peoples converted out of fear. All of these, according to Rabbi Nechemia, are invalid conversions, because the motivation was external pressure or anticipated benefit rather than authentic religious commitment. The standard, he says, is conversion {\i ki-vzman ha-zeh} \u8212- like conditions today, meaning times when being Jewish carries neither obvious advantage nor obvious danger, so that conversion is driven solely by religious sincerity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records that it was stated regarding this dispute: {\i halacha k\u8217'divrei ha-omer kulam gerim hem} \u8212- we rule like the position that holds all of these are valid converts. We do not rule like Rabbi Nechemia. This is noteworthy: the {\i gemara} asserts the existence of an opposing position \u8212- {\i ha-omer kulam gerim hem} \u8212- without having explicitly quoted a named {\i tanna} to that effect. {\i B\u8217'di\u8217'avad}, a conversion undertaken with ulterior motives is nonetheless binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the conversion is valid, the {\i gemara} asks, why should she not be permitted to marry this man even {\i le-khatkhila}? The answer is the principle articulated by Rav Ashi: {\i haser mimecha ikshus peh} \u8212- \u8220"remove from yourself the perversity of the mouth\u8221" \u8212- and {\i ve-lizus sefatayim}, avoid giving occasion for rumors and unseemly speech. By marrying the woman about whom there was a rumor of an affair, one reinforces that rumor, and this {\i chazal} found socially intolerable. The concern is not merely personal \u8212- it is not simply that the individual suffers reputationally. There is a broader societal interest in not normalizing or publicly validating behavior that society has deemed unacceptable. When such marriages are allowed to proceed openly, it implicitly communicates that affairs followed by marriage are an acceptable pattern. {\i B\u8217'di\u8217'avad}, however, the couple may remain married.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geirus Deep Dive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechatilla vs B\u8217'dieavad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} is one of the most central texts in the entire halakhic literature bearing on {\i geirus}, specifically on the question of motivation. Two distinct questions arise in contemporary discussions of conversion: first, what constitutes sufficient {\i kabbalat mitzvot} \u8212- acceptance of the commandments; and second, what role does the convert\u8217's motivation play. These questions are frequently intertwined, since a person converting solely for the purpose of marriage may not be genuinely committed to observing {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} does not address the depth-of-commitment question directly. One might read Rabbi Nechemia\u8217's concern about ulterior motives as implicitly rooted in the assumption that someone converting for extrinsic reasons will not be genuinely observant. But Rabbi Nechemia himself makes no such argument \u8212- he states that extrinsic motivation itself invalidates the process, regardless of subsequent behavior. And we rule against him: {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad}, such a conversion is valid. What remains firm is that {\i le-khatkhila}, conversion for the purpose of marriage is not permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Teshuvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i le-khatkhila} / {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} distinction has generated a substantial body of {\i teshuvot} in the modern period. Rabbi Shlomo Kluger, among others, wrote an important {\i teshuva} addressing the changed circumstances of contemporary life. The argument runs as follows: in earlier times, civil marriage across religious lines was either legally impossible or socially untenable. Under those conditions, a non-Jew who converted in order to marry a Jew was doing so purely to remove a legal barrier to marriage \u8212- the motivation was entirely instrumental. In contemporary Western societies, by contrast, intermarriage is legally and socially unimpeded. When a non-Jew converts today, the conversion is not strictly necessary to enable the marriage; in many cases the couple could simply marry without conversion. The decision to convert therefore often reflects something beyond the bare desire to be legally eligible for marriage \u8212- it reflects, at minimum, some degree of identification with the Jewish people or responsiveness to the spouse\u8217's wishes in a way that goes beyond pure obstacle-removal. This consideration substantially changes the {\i le-khatkhila} calculus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lesser-of-Evils Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a practical {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} dimension that demands attention. If a {\i beit din} refuses to convert a prospective convert who is motivated by marriage, the likely outcome is not that the couple will separate. The likely outcome is that they will marry without conversion \u8212- an intermarriage. The question therefore becomes: between a conversion motivated by marriage ({\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} valid, as we have ruled) and an unconverted intermarriage, which represents the lesser harm? We are, in many cases, already operating within a {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} reality. The choice is not between a {\i le-khatkhila} conversion and a {\i le-khatkhila} marriage \u8212- it is between one {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} outcome and another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kabbalat Mitzvot Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A more stringent argument has been raised in some quarters, pushing back against any willingness to convert for marriage even {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad}. The argument is this: conversion requires {\i kabbalat mitzvot} \u8212- an unqualified acceptance of all the commandments. According to some authorities, even the rejection of a single rabbinic precept renders the {\i kabbalat mitzvot} invalid. Now, if a woman converts with the full intention of marrying the Jewish man about whom there was a rumor of an affair \u8212- knowing that this marriage is rabbinically prohibited \u8212- she is, at the moment of conversion, already planning to violate a rabbinic {\i halakhah}. This, the argument goes, constitutes a defective {\i kabbalat mitzvot} and should invalidate the conversion even {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad}. This is an extreme position, but it illustrates how tightly the motivational and commitment questions can become intertwined.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Historical Conversion Ac\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejection in Prosperous\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now extends the discussion beyond individual motivation to the question of historical periods. {\i Ein mekabbelim gerim li-ymot ha-Mashiach} \u8212- we will not accept converts in the messianic era, because at that moment everyone will wish to convert, and the motivation will be purely self-interested. {\i Lo kiblu gerim lo bi-ymei David ve-lo bi-ymei Shlomo} \u8212- similarly, converts were not accepted in the time of David or in the time of Solomon, periods when Jewish prosperity made conversion materially advantageous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar\u8217's Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar derives this from a {\i pasuk}: {\i hein gor yagur efes me-oti mi gar itach alayikh yipol}. The word {\i gor yagur} is read as: if a {\i ger} comes to convert, {\i efes me-oti} \u8212- it must be without the benefit, without the obvious reward that I provide. Only {\i mi gar itach} \u8212- one who converts when things are not particularly good for the Jewish people \u8212- {\i alayikh yipol}, will share in the ultimate portion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises the obvious difficulty: we know that conversions did in fact occur during the time of Solomon \u8212- most notably {\i Bat Paroh} and others whom Solomon married. The answer {\i Tosafot} proposes is that these individuals were already wealthy and powerful in their own right; they were not converting to gain material advantage from association with Solomon\u8217's court. {\i Demidei hu-da\u8217'imu ela mishum shulchan melachim \u8212- va-anu lo tzerichi}: since they had no need of the \u8220"king\u8217's table,\u8221" the concern of ulterior motive did not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerim Grurim Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A more challenging question is raised regarding the Gibeonites in David\u8217's time, and the 150,000 converts who joined Israel at various periods described in {\i Sefer Arakhim}. {\i Tosafot} suggests that some of these may have converted {\i me-atzmam} \u8212- of their own accord \u8212- while others were {\i gerim grurim}, converts who were, as it were, dragged along, swept up in collective conversion movements rather than individually motivated. The Rambam\u8217's position, cited in this context, is that such converts were processed by a {\i beit din shel hedyotot} \u8212- a court of lay judges acting without proper authority \u8212- and {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} the conversions held. This maps precisely onto the broader {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} framework: {\i le-khatkhila} such conversions should not have occurred; historically, they did occur with some frequency; and {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} we accept that they are valid. The current reality of mass conversions motivated by marriage or social pressure follows the same pattern.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Adultery Evidence Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the foundational question the {\i mishnah} raised regarding the {\i eshet ish} case: what standard of evidence is required before we compel the suspected man to divorce the woman he has married? Rav rules: {\i u-ve-edim} \u8212- only if there are actual witnesses to the act of adultery do we force a divorce. A mere rumor, however persistent, is insufficient to compel dissolution of the marriage if they have already wed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet responds with characteristic force: {\i ki nayim ve-shakhiv Rav amrah le-hai shema\u8217'teta} \u8212- Rav must have been asleep when he made that ruling. He then cites a {\i baraita} that appears to directly contradict Rav\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interruption Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} presents the following case: {\i ha-nichshad al eshet ish ve-hotzi\u8217'uha al yado} \u8212- a man suspected of an adulterous affair with a married woman, who is subsequently divorced. She then marries a different man \u8212- not the man with whom she was suspected of the affair \u8212- and is later divorced from that second husband. At that point, {\i im kanas lo yotzi}: if the suspected man (call him Reuven) then marries her, he need not divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Interruption Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is this: the fact that she married an entirely different man after her first divorce undermines the rumor that she was having an affair with Reuven. Had there been a genuine affair, presumably she would have married Reuven first. Her having instead married a different man effectively interrupts and discredits the {\i kala}. By the time she divorces that second husband and eventually marries Reuven, the original rumor has been so thoroughly disrupted that allowing the marriage to stand does not meaningfully reinforce it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then reasons: in what case does this scenario make sense? If there were witnesses to the affair, then the fact that she married an intermediate man should be irrelevant \u8212- we should always compel divorce from Reuven, regardless of whether a rumor was interrupted. The only way the intermediate marriage matters is if there were {\i no witnesses} \u8212- and in that case, the {\i baraita} implies that the rumor itself is operative. If the rumor persists uninterrupted, she cannot marry Reuven; if it is interrupted by her marriage to another man, she can. This, Rav Sheshet argues, proves that rumors alone \u8212- without witnesses \u8212- suffice to compel divorce, directly contradicting Rav.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav\u8217's Clarification (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Maintains Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav does not abandon his position. He argues that even if the {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} reading of the {\i baraita} appears to point toward rumors being operative without witnesses, the halakhic conclusion is that no witnesses means no forced divorce \u8212- in either direction. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i baraita}, Rav maintains, is not that rumors without witnesses suffice. Rather, the {\i baraita} is addressing a different question: even in the scenario where the rumor was interrupted by an intermediate marriage, she should not {\i le-khatkhila} go and marry Reuven. The intermediate marriage removes the {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} obligation to divorce, but does not make the marriage {\i le-khatkhila} advisable. The {\i baraita} is being {\i machmir} at the {\i le-khatkhila} level, not {\i meikel} at the {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} level.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forced Divorce Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Children Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i baraita} clarifies the conditions under which divorce is compelled without witnesses. {\i Ba-meh devarim amurim} \u8212- under what circumstances do we compel divorce? {\i Ke-shein lah banim} \u8212- when the woman had no children from her first husband. If there are no children whose {\i yichus} is implicated, the sociological cost of forcing a divorce is more manageable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children Present Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval yesh lah banim, lo tetzei} \u8212- if she had children from her first husband, we do not force her to divorce Reuven. The reasoning is important: compelling a divorce from the suspected man is itself a powerful reinforcement of the rumor. It signals to all observers that the suspicion was well-founded \u8212- why else would the {\i beit din} intervene? If she has children from her first marriage, forcing divorce from Reuven will cause people to begin questioning the paternity of those children, casting suspicion on their status and potentially treating them as {\i mamzeirim}. The cost of compulsion is therefore too high: we decline to force the divorce in order to protect the children from this reputational harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eidim Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i ve-im ba\u8217'u edei tumah} \u8212- if actual witnesses to adultery come forward \u8212- {\i afilu yesh lah kama banim tetzei}: even if she has many children, she must divorce. When we have objective, direct evidence of adultery, we know that she is biblically forbidden to the man she had the affair with. We cannot tolerate that union. The concern for the children\u8217's perceived status is real, but it cannot override a known and proven biblical prohibition. The {\i eidei tumah} thus function as an absolute override of the children-present leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} poses a powerful challenge to Rav. The implication is that without witnesses but without children, we {\i do} force divorce \u8212- meaning that a rumor alone, in the absence of children, is sufficient. That directly contradicts Rav\u8217's position that only {\i edim} trigger forced divorce.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav\u8217's Clarification (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Force Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav responds by drawing attention to the precise language of the {\i mishnah}: {\i hotzi\u8217'uha} \u8212- \u8220"they removed her\u8221" \u8212- language that implies compulsory court action. A {\i beit din} would only compel a woman to divorce her husband under such dramatic terms if witnesses to actual adultery were present. Rav therefore maintains that the {\i mishnah} presupposes a case in which there are both witnesses {\i and} children \u8212- witnesses, because the court is acting coercively against the first husband; children, because otherwise the case would not need to specify this degree of force. Rav is not disagreeing with the underlying {\i halakhah} as stated in the {\i baraita} \u8212- he acknowledges that without children and without witnesses, the {\i baraita\u8217's} ruling would apply. His point is purely interpretive: the {\i mishnah\u8217's} use of {\i hotzi\u8217'uha} signals that it is speaking to the case where witnesses exist.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Reinterpretat (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava presses this point further. Why does the {\i baraita} itself employ the language of {\i hotzi\u8217'uha} \u8212- forced removal from the first husband? If the question before us is about forcing divorce from the second husband (Reuven), why does the text speak of coercive action against the first? This language suggests that we are dealing with a case in which {\i beit din} compelled the first divorce \u8212- which in turn implies that witnesses were present. Rava\u8217's interpretive observation therefore lends support to Rav\u8217's reading: the {\i baraita} describes a scenario in which the court acted coercively at the outset, which presupposes witnesses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav\u8217's Clarification (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Disagreement Resolut\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution that emerges is that Rav is not engaged in a substantive dispute about the {\i halakhah} itself. Everyone agrees: without witnesses but without children, rumors alone suffice to compel divorce from the second husband; with children, only witnesses trigger the forced divorce. Rav\u8217's contribution is not a competing {\i halakhic} position but an interpretive reading of the {\i mishnah\u8217's} language. The phrase {\i hotzi\u8217'uha} led Rav to understand the {\i mishnah} as speaking to the case where witnesses are present and children too \u8212- and under those conditions, of course the divorce is compelled. There is, in other words, no real {\i machloket} between Rav and the {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Reinterpretat (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final resolution of this cluster of discussions is as follows: the {\i baraita} presupposes a case with witnesses and with children. Rav accepts the underlying {\i halakhah} as stated \u8212- without children, rumors alone may force divorce; with children, only witnesses will do. His interpretive reading of the {\i mishnah} and {\i baraita} is simply a different way of accounting for the language used, not a substantive disagreement about which cases trigger compulsion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rumor Cessation Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Positions Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now harmonizes the positions of Rav and Rebbi in a different dimension of the dispute. The resolution is that both are correct, but in different scenarios. {\i Kala d\u8217'lo pasik} \u8212- a rumor that does not cease \u8212- follows the view of Rebbi: even without direct witnesses, a persistent, uninterrupted rumor is sufficient to compel divorce. {\i Kala d\u8217'pasik} \u8212- a rumor that has ceased \u8212- follows the view of Rav: only actual {\i edim} will compel divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Duration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What constitutes a {\i kala d\u8217'lo pasik}? Abbaye transmits a teaching he received from his nursemaid \u8212- the woman who raised him after he was orphaned, whom he called his \u8220"mother\u8221" \u8212- that the rumor must persist for {\i yoma u-palga}, a day and a half, continuously. This is a remarkably low threshold: a day and a half of sustained gossip is sufficient to be treated as a {\i kala d\u8217'lo pasik}. Rashi explains {\i dumi d\u8217'masa} as either referring to the gossips of the town, or as indicating that people are speculating and speaking {\i be-dumiya} \u8212- in hushed tones, not with certainty, but persistently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an important qualification: even if the rumor appears to cease within the day and a half, that interruption only counts as a genuine break if it was not caused by fear. If the people who were spreading the rumor fell silent because they feared retaliation from the parties being discussed \u8212- if they would have continued talking but for fear of consequences \u8212- that interruption is not halakhically meaningful. The rumor is treated as if it never stopped.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enemies Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The strongest practical limitation on the entire framework of {\i kala} as evidence is the enemies exception: {\i v\u8217'lo amrinan ela de-leka oyvim} \u8212- this entire analysis applies only where the parties have no known enemies. {\i Oyvim hu d\u8217'apki lei l\u8217'kala} \u8212- if they have enemies, those enemies are the likely source of the rumor, and a rumor generated by hostile parties carries no evidentiary weight. This exception provides a significant practical off-ramp: in many real-world cases, parties to a suspected affair are likely to have adversaries with motives to spread damaging rumors. When that is demonstrably the case, the {\i kala} is discounted entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope of Sugya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, a crucial clarification by {\i Tosafot} \u8212- specifically attributed to Rabbeinu Tam \u8212- fundamentally reframes the scope of the entire {\i sugya}. Rabbeinu Tam insists: {\i d\u8217'chula sugya ayri b\u8217'nitan} \u8212- the entire discussion in this {\i sugya}, from Rebbi\u8217's cases of suspicious circumstances through the {\i kala d\u8217'lo pasik} analysis, is exclusively concerned with whether we compel the suspected man to divorce the woman if she has married him. It is not, and never was, about whether we compel a woman to divorce her own husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Husband Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De-ein isha ne\u8217'eseret al ba\u8217'alah ela al yedei kinui ve-stira} \u8212- the only mechanism by which a woman becomes forbidden to her husband and would be compelled to divorce him is through the formal {\i sotah} process: a prior {\i kinui}, a warning by her husband before witnesses, followed by {\i stira}, established seclusion with the suspected man. Alternatively, direct witnesses to the act of intercourse itself would suffice. Short of these very specific and demanding evidentiary thresholds, no rumor, no pattern of suspicious circumstantial evidence, and no {\i kala d\u8217'lo pasik} would ever compel a woman to divorce her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suspicious Evidence Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means that all of Rebbi\u8217's cases \u8212- the milkman leaving as the husband arrives and his wife is readjusting her garments; the spit on the canopy above the bed; the overturned shoes beneath the bed \u8212- are relevant only to the question of whether we compel divorce from the {\i second} husband, the man with whom she is suspected of having had the affair. These suspicious circumstances are insufficient, according to {\i Tosafot}, to force divorce from the woman\u8217's {\i original} husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant and clarifying intervention. Without Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's qualification, the {\i gemara} could be read to establish a standard under which suspicious but non-conclusive evidence would compel a woman to leave her husband \u8212- a reading that would have vast and destabilizing halakhic consequences. Rabbeinu Tam closes that reading off entirely: for the original husband, only {\i edei tumah} \u8212- witnesses to the act of intercourse itself \u8212- or the completed {\i sotah} process can sever that marriage against her will. The {\i kala} and the suspicious-circumstances analysis apply solely to the second-husband scenario.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Remarriage Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shem Ra Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now cites a {\i mishnah} from elsewhere: {\i ha-motzi et ishto mishum shem ra} \u8212- a man who divorces his wife on account of a {\i shem ra}, a rumor that she had an affair \u8212- {\i lo yachzir}: he may not remarry her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel case: {\i mishum neder} \u8212- a man who divorces his wife because she took a vow that he found intolerable to live with, and which could not be annulled \u8212- {\i lo yachzir}: he likewise may not remarry her after the divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale is not self-evident, and the {\i gemara} works to explain it. The concern is that the husband, having divorced for a specific and stated reason, may subsequently discover that the reason was baseless: evidence may emerge that clears his wife\u8217's name, or a {\i hakham} may find a {\i petach} and annul the vow. At that point, the husband may wish to claim retroactively that the divorce was conditional \u8212- that had he known the vow would be annulled or the rumor disproved, he never would have divorced her, and therefore the {\i get} was never truly intended and is {\i batel}. If the {\i get} were {\i batel}, the wife\u8217's subsequent marriage to another man would be {\i mamash} adulterous, and any children of that second union would be {\i mamzeirim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To prevent this scenario, {\i chazal} rule that there is no possibility of remarriage. The reasoning is as follows: if a husband knows that divorcing his wife on account of the {\i shem ra} or the {\i neder} will permanently bar him from ever remarrying her \u8212- even one day later, even if she never remarries anyone else \u8212- then if he goes ahead and divorces her {\i anyway}, he cannot claim the divorce was conditional or would have been withheld had circumstances been different. A man who divorces knowing there is no possibility of return is thereby shown to have intended the divorce absolutely, not conditionally. The removal of the remarriage option thus inoculates the {\i get} against later retroactive nullification claims.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 B\u8217'dieavad Remarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is then posed to us directly: if the prohibition on remarriage is rabbinic, and the man goes ahead and remarries his divorced wife {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} \u8212- must they then divorce?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An initial argument is advanced by analogy to the adultery case. Since in the {\i eshet ish} scenario, where a woman marries the man she was suspected of having an affair with, we require divorce even {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} \u8212- perhaps the same should apply here. If {\i chazal} said not to remarry, and the remarriage happened, force dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counter-Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy is resisted. The adultery case is different in a critical respect: when the suspected adulterer actually marries the woman, the marriage {\i itself} is the act that reinforces the rumor and validates the suspicion in the public eye. That sociological harm is precisely what renders {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} intolerable in that case. The remarriage case is structurally different: there, the remarriage does not reinforce anything problematic in the same way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 B\u8217'dieavad Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad}, the remarriage is permitted. Once the remarriage has taken place, we do not compel dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is not merely that the rabbinic prohibition is light. It is that the remarriage actually works in the {\i opposite} direction sociologically. If a man divorces his wife because of rumors of an affair and then subsequently {\i remarries} her, his act of remarriage demonstrates to the world that the rumor was false. Why would a man remarry a woman whose affair he genuinely believed in? The remarriage thus {\i counters} the {\i shem ra} rather than reinforcing it. It carries no inherent social harm equivalent to the harm of the suspected adulterer marrying his alleged partner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pattern Observation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stepping back, it is worth noting the overall pattern that emerges from the {\i mishnayot} on this {\i daf}. Of the several cases examined \u8212- the {\i safek} marriages of two brothers, the {\i shifcha} and {\i nochrit} who converted or were freed, the remarriage after {\i shem ra} or {\i neder} \u8212- virtually all are permitted {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad}. There is only one case in the entire sequence where {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} dissolution is required: the case of the man who marries the woman he was suspected of having an adulterous affair with. That singular stringency reflects the unique character of adultery as an act that generates an actual biblical prohibition between the parties \u8212- not merely a rabbinic discouragement or a concern about social perception \u8212- and it is that alone which the {\i gemara} treats as categorically intolerable even after the fact.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 25\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get from Overseas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Overseas Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens on 25a with a {\i mishna} that, at first glance, seems more at home in {\i Masechet Gittin} than in {\i Yevamos}. {\i Ha-mevi get mi-medinat ha-yam} \u8212- the one who brings a {\i get} from overseas. As is familiar from the opening {\i mishna} of {\i Gittin}, a messenger who delivers a {\i get} from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} must declare {\i bifnei nechtav bifnei nechtam} \u8212- that the document was written and signed in his presence, in order to validate its authenticity. The {\i mishna} here rules that such a messenger, {\i lo yisa ishto} \u8212- may not subsequently marry the newly divorced woman. The reason is evident: his interest in marrying her would cast retroactive suspicion on the validity of his testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Murder Confession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} extends this principle in a striking direction. If the witness declares {\i haragti} \u8212- I killed the husband \u8212- or {\i haragnu} \u8212- we killed the husband \u8212- he likewise cannot marry the wife. What is remarkable is that the {\i mishna} makes no move to prosecute him for murder; the entire concern is the integrity of the testimony that permits the woman to remarry. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} draws a distinction: if he says {\i haragti}, I killed him, she may not remarry from his testimony; but if he says {\i haragnu}, we killed him, she may. The precise basis of this distinction will emerge in the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reason for Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} articulates the logic underlying the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling about the overseas {\i get}: {\i ta\u8217'ama de-medinat ha-yam} \u8212- the reason he cannot remarry applies specifically when he brings the {\i get} from overseas, {\i de-alei kasamchina}, because we are relying on his testimony. But {\i aval me-eretz yisrael}, if he delivers the {\i get} within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where no such declaration is required, {\i de-lav alei kasamchina} \u8212- since we are not relying on his testimony \u8212- {\i yisa ishto}, he may marry the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Death Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a challenge: in the case where the witness testifies that the husband has died, {\i de-lav alei kasamchina} \u8212- we do not ultimately rely on him in the same direct sense, because the principle established elsewhere is {\i isha dayka u-minsava} \u8212- the woman herself is trusted to investigate. The {\i gemara} elsewhere grapples with the conceptual difficulty of permitting remarriage on the basis of a single witness; the resolution it offers is that since the woman is fully aware of the severe consequences should the testimony prove false \u8212- should her husband turn out to be alive \u8212- we rely entirely on her own careful investigation following the testimony. On this reading, the operative reliability is hers, not the witness\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, one might ask, why can he not marry her? If we are essentially relying on the woman rather than on him, why should his interest in the outcome disqualify him? The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer brings us to the next distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death vs Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hatam leka ksava hacha ikka ksava} \u8212- there is still a critical difference between the case of testifying to the husband\u8217's death and the case of delivering a {\i get} within {\i Eretz Yisrael}. Even when the testimony about death is ultimately bolstered or even superseded by the woman\u8217's investigation, the witness is still testifying to facts; there is still some testimonial act that requires integrity. By contrast, when delivering a {\i get} within {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- where no declaration is required \u8212- the document effectively validates itself. {\i Kidtnan, mah bein get l\u8217'misa \u8212- shehaksav mochiach} \u8212- what is the difference between divorce and death? The written document proves its own legitimacy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is the well-known rule that {\i eidim shehachatumim al hashtar k\u8217'mi shenechkra edusan b\u8217'veis din} \u8212- witnesses whose signatures appear on a document are treated as if their testimony has already been examined before a court. Within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, the signatures are presumed reliable without external validation; the need for {\i bifnei nechtav uvifnei nechtam} arises only when the document originates from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, where the reliability of signatures is less certain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Splitting Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape Witness Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hu nihu d\u8217'lo yisav iteseih, ha l\u8217'acher tinasei} \u8212- he himself cannot marry her, but she may marry another man. With this the {\i gemara} transitions to a celebrated discussion, introduced by Rav Yosef. If a man testifies {\i ploni revani le\u8217'onsi} \u8212- a certain man had intercourse with me against my will \u8212- then {\i hu ve\u8217'acher mitztarfim le\u8217'hargo}: he may join with another witness to have that man executed by the court. Since he himself was a victim, he bears no culpability and retains his standing as a witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if he says {\i birtzoni} \u8212- it was done willingly, I was a consenting participant \u8212- then {\i rasha hu}. He has admitted to a transgression. The Torah states {\i al tashet yadcha im rasha lihyot ed chamas} \u8212- do not place your hand with a wicked person to be a corrupt witness \u8212- which excludes a {\i rasha} from giving testimony. By his own admission he has rendered himself invalid, and his testimony implicating the other party cannot stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This introduces the concept of {\i palginan dibura} \u8212- the question of whether we may split a witness\u8217's statement, accepting the portion that relates to another person while discarding the self-incriminating portion. Here, Rav Yosef rules that we cannot: the moment he admits to willing participation, he has disqualified himself entirely, and we cannot bracket that admission and salvage the remainder of his testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to the {\i mishna}\u8217's case of murder is apparent. There, too, the witness is confessing to a serious crime. If we cannot split the testimony in the case of the rape witness, we might expect to be equally unable to split it in the murder case \u8212- and thus unable to use the testimony to permit the woman to remarry at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might suggest a distinction: in the testimony about the murdered husband, we are operating in the domain of {\i edus isha}, about which the Rabbis were especially lenient. Perhaps in that domain, a more flexible standard applies, including the possibility of {\i palginan dibura}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef vs Menashe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents the positions of Rav Yosef and Rav Menashe in structured opposition, and this is a striking {\i gemara} to follow carefully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Menashe states: {\i gazlan de-divreihem kasher le-edus isha} \u8212- a person who is rabbinically invalid as a witness, such as a gambler, is nonetheless valid for testimony concerning a woman\u8217's ability to remarry. But {\i gazlan de-divrei Torah pasul le-edus isha} \u8212- one who is biblically invalid is excluded even in the more lenient domain of {\i edus isha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that {\i Tosafos} raises the question of whether the invalidity of a witness due to monetary corruption \u8212- where the logic is that such a person will shade testimony for financial gain \u8212- necessarily applies to one invalidated merely as a habitual sinner who poses no such temptation to lie about the death of a husband. The {\i gemara} does not distinguish along these lines.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: should we say that Rav Menashe holds like {\i Rabbi Yehuda} in the {\i mishna} \u8212- who refuses to accept the testimony of one who says he is the murderer \u8212- while the {\i Tanna Kamma} permits it? The {\i gemara} rejects this forced identification. {\i Amar lach Rav Menashe} \u8212- Rav Menashe would say: {\i ve-ein adam mesim atzmo rasha}. A person cannot make himself into a {\i rasha} by his own testimony against himself. This is the principle of {\i palginan dibura} applied in the affirmative: because one cannot legally self-incriminate in matters of {\i misa} or {\i malkos} \u8212- confession of a criminal offense has no halachic standing \u8212- the self-incriminating portion of the statement is simply discarded. We retain only the relevant claim that the husband is dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if Rav Menashe holds that we can do {\i palginan dibura} \u8212- split the testimony \u8212- why does he still rule that a biblically invalid witness is excluded from {\i edus isha}? And conversely, if Rav Yosef holds that {\i palginan dibura} is rejected, how can the {\i mishna\u8217's Tanna Kamma} accept the murderer\u8217's testimony at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by assigning each position a different hinge. Rav Menashe, who does {\i palginan dibura}, would therefore disagree with Rav Yosef in the rape case \u8212- he would split the testimony there as well and not disqualify the witness for the self-incriminating part. Conversely, Rav Yosef, who refuses {\i palginan dibura} in the rape case, can still accept the testimony in the {\i edus isha} context by holding that even a biblically invalid witness is acceptable there \u8212- and therefore the self-incriminating admission becomes irrelevant in any event.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also notes a possible synthesis: one might hold that neither {\i palginan dibura} alone nor the leniency of {\i edus isha} alone would suffice, but that the combination of both considerations together permits the testimony. Thus the {\i Tanna Kamma}, who accepts the murderer\u8217's testimony, could be understood as relying on the convergence of the two grounds \u8212- the unique latitude of {\i edus isha} and the doctrine of {\i palginan dibura} \u8212- each of which independently might not cross the threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bandit Confession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} is then introduced in which the Rabbis challenge the position of {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. A bandit, {\i listis}, was being led out to execution in a place called Kaputkia. Before his death he instructed those present to go and tell the wife of Shimon ben Kohen: {\i ani haragti et ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- I murdered her husband. The story records that the woman\u8217's husband was subsequently declared dead and she was permitted to remarry. Here, then, is a clear case of accepting the testimony of someone who confesses to murder \u8212- precisely what {\i Rabbi Yehuda} would reject.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Responsibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} deflects the challenge: {\i be\u8217'omer ani hayiti im horgav} \u8212- I was with those who killed him. The statement attributed to the bandit, he maintains, did not mean that he personally committed the murder but rather that he was present, part of the group, a participant of some degree \u8212- but not the one who physically carried it out. Thus {\i Rabbi Yehuda} does not necessarily hold that one who directly admits to the killing can be used as a witness even for {\i edus isha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects: but the {\i baraisa} explicitly identifies the man as a {\i listis}, a bandit, and says he was going out to execution \u8212- surely that implies he was convicted of murder? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i beit din shel ovdei kochavim delo dayki vekatli} \u8212- the non-Jewish courts would execute on the basis of suspicion without the rigorous evidentiary standards of a Jewish court. His being led to execution by a non-Jewish court does not halachically establish him as a murderer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is that both the {\i Tanna Kamma} and {\i Rabbi Yehuda} agree that if a witness is clearly established as a murderer by proper {\i beit din} standards, the testimony cannot be relied upon \u8212- at least according to Rav Menashe\u8217's position. The disagreement between them is narrower: does the witness\u8217's own self-incriminating statement suffice to disqualify him? The {\i Tanna Kamma} says no, because we invoke {\i palginan dibura}, or because we are lenient in {\i edus isha}, or both. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} says yes \u8212- a self-incriminating admission of personal murder does disqualify, though not an admission of mere presence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vow Annulment Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} turns to yet another scenario in the running theme of persons who are prohibited from marrying a woman they have in some way facilitated into being divorced. {\i He\u8217'chacham she\u8217'asar et ha\u8217'ishah b\u8217'neder al ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a {\i chacham} who was asked to annul a vow and was unable to do so, with the result that the vow rendered the woman forbidden to her husband. This is actually a more extreme scenario than it might initially appear: it is not merely that the husband threatened divorce over the vow, but that the vow itself made cohabitation impossible. Such a {\i chacham} may not subsequently marry the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i mishna} adds important qualifications. If the {\i chacham} was involved in a {\i mi\u8217'un} proceeding \u8212- a minor girl who was married through the consent of her brothers or mother exercising her right of verbal refusal \u8212- or supervised a {\i chalitzah} ceremony, and he did so as part of a {\i beit din}, then {\i yisa\u8217'enah}, he may marry the woman afterward. The reason is {\i mipnei shehu beit din} \u8212- because he acted as part of a court. When he acts as one member of a three-person panel, the decision cannot be attributed to him personally, and there is far less reason to suspect that his judgment was distorted by personal interest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Expert Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now interrogates the precise configuration assumed by the {\i mishna}. If the {\i chacham} annulled the vow as a single authority, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i chad mi matzi matir} \u8212- can a single person annul a vow? After all, Rav, citing Rabbi Chiya or Rabbi Chiya bar Avin, taught: {\i tana hataras nedarim b\u8217'shlosha} \u8212- the release of vows requires three. Alternatively, if it was done by three, why is any one of them suspect at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by invoking a ruling of Rav Chisda in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i b\u8217'yachid mumcheh} \u8212- even where three laypeople are normally required, a single recognized expert, a {\i mumcheh}, may annul vows. The {\i mishna} is therefore discussing the case of such an individual expert. And the concluding clause \u8212- that if he acted as part of a {\i beit din}, he may marry the woman \u8212- is distinguished precisely because in the {\i beit din} context he was not acting unilaterally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} extends the analysis. {\i Ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'beit din, ha bitrei lo} \u8212- if the exemption from suspicion applies specifically when there is a full {\i beit din}, then perhaps two witnesses acting jointly should still fall under suspicion? A {\i baraita} is cited: witnesses who signed a field-sale document or a woman\u8217's {\i get} \u8212- {\i lo chashu chachamim l\u8217'davar zeh} \u8212- the Sages were not concerned that a witness might have forged the document in order to profit from the transaction or marry the woman. Any time one is not acting entirely alone, the concern dissipates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes that the {\i mishna}\u8217's use of {\i beit din} language is incidentally conveying an additional ruling: do not think that {\i mi\u8217'un} may be performed before two \u8212- {\i mi\u8217'un b\u8217'gimmel}, it requires three. But for the purpose of permitting remarriage, even two witnesses acting together suffice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Occurred\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ibaya lehu: knas mahu sheyotzi?} \u8212- the question is finally posed that has been lurking beneath all the preceding cases: if one of these men actually went ahead and married the woman despite the prohibition, must he now divorce her? This is the {\i knas} question \u8212- whether the Rabbis imposed a punitive requirement of divorce in cases where the initial marriage should not have taken place. Notably, {\i Rashi} understands this question as applying both to the case of the {\i chacham} and the {\i neder} and to the case of the one who brought the {\i get}. The {\i Rashba}, however, holds that the question applies only to the case of the {\i chacham} in the current {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kahana vs Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Kahana amar kanas motzi} \u8212- Rav Kahana rules that he must divorce. {\i Rav Ashi amar kanas eino motzi} \u8212- Rav Ashi rules that he need not. A tradition was taught in the school of Rav Papa supporting the position of Rav Ashi. When the Rabbis pressed Rav Ashi \u8212- is this a received tradition or a logical inference? \u8212- he replied: {\i matnitin hi} \u8212- I derive it from the {\i mishna} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} cited as support is the ruling concerning one suspected of an affair who subsequently converted or whose circumstances changed: {\i harei zeh lo yichnos v\u8217'im kanas ein motzi} \u8212- he should not have married, but if he did, he need not divorce. From there the {\i gemara} draws the inference: {\i b\u8217'rinana lo mafkinan} \u8212- based on rumor and communal suspicion alone, we do not compel divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hacha nami b\u8217'rinana lo mafkinan} \u8212- here too, the concern that the community might draw adverse conclusions does not rise to the level of requiring a divorce after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing on the disanalogy. In the case of the suspected affair, even if the rumor were true, the woman is still halachically permitted \u8212- the act was wrong but the marriage can stand. In the cases under discussion here, however, if the underlying concern is correct \u8212- if the {\i get} was forged or the husband is actually alive \u8212- the woman may be an {\i eishet ish}. The {\i Rashba}\u8217's position that the question applies only to the {\i neder} case and not to the {\i get}-delivery case becomes understandable: in the {\i neder} case, even if the {\i chacham} manipulated matters, the husband still gave the woman a {\i get} of his own accord, and her divorce is fully valid. But in the {\i get}-delivery case, doubts about the legitimacy of the document or the testimony about death carry potentially severe consequences, making the {\i b\u8217'rinana} framework less clearly applicable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Remarriage Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Already Married\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'kulam shehayu lahem nashim umetu mutarot l\u8217'hinasay lahem} \u8212- all these individuals, if they were already married at the time they delivered the {\i get} or testified to the husband\u8217's death, and their own wives subsequently died, the woman is permitted to marry them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable {\i mishna}. Although {\i halacha} technically permits polygamy, the {\i mishna} operates on the sociological assumption that a man who already has a wife will not, as a practical matter, seek to take a second wife. This assumption is stated casually and taken for granted, which is itself striking \u8212- {\i halacha} permits something but the {\i mishna} treats it as effectively non-occurring for most men. Therefore, if a man was already married when he facilitated the divorce or testified about the husband\u8217's death, we do not suspect that he was manipulating events in order to free this woman for himself. He had a wife; he was not in the market for another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might counter: even if we assume he was not planning to take a second wife, he might have been planning to divorce his first wife unilaterally, which he is halachically entitled to do. This precise objection is taken up by the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Metu in, nitgarshu lo} \u8212- the {\i gemara} infers from the {\i mishna} that if his wife died, the woman may marry him; but if he divorced his wife, she may not. A {\i baraita}, however, states {\i afilu nitgarshu} \u8212- even if they divorced their wives, the woman may marry them. The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya, ha d\u8217'havi ktata, ha d\u8217'la havi ktata} \u8212- there is no difficulty; the cases differ depending on whether there was preexisting marital strife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-conflict Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} explains the rule in a direction that may at first seem counterintuitive. Where there was preexisting strife \u8212- {\i d\u8217'havi ktata} \u8212- and he subsequently divorced his wife, the woman may marry him. Where there was no preexisting strife, she may not. The reasoning, as {\i Rashi} explains, is {\i d\u8217'achti lo natan da\u8217'ato}: if the couple was already in conflict before this woman came onto the scene, the decision to divorce was not generated by interest in the new woman. He would have divorced his wife regardless. The delivery of the {\i get} or the testimony about the husband\u8217's death was therefore not motivated by desire for this woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If there was no prior conflict, however, the divorce appears to have originated precisely when this woman became available \u8212- which creates the inference that the entire chain of events was engineered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Initiated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second resolution is offered: {\i ha deargil hu, ha deargila hi} \u8212- in the case where he was the one pushing for divorce, she may not marry him; in the case where she was the one pushing for divorce, she may. If the wife initiated the desire to separate, then the husband was evidently committed to the marriage and his role in facilitating the other woman\u8217's divorce was not self-interested. If he was the one pressing for divorce, the suspicion is stronger.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} elsewhere acknowledges that one can subtly maneuver a spouse into seeking divorce \u8212- but that level of psychological complexity is not developed further here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intervening Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'chulan shenisu l\u8217'acherim v\u8217'nitgarshu o shenit\u8217'almenu mutarot l\u8217'hinnase lahem} \u8212- if all these women went on to marry another man, and that second man then died or divorced them, the original messenger or witness may now marry the woman. Since she married an entirely different person in the interim, it is inconceivable that the witness\u8217's original act was designed to enable this eventual marriage to himself. No one fabricates an elaborate scheme to free a woman for another man\u8217's marriage, and then waits to see if that marriage will end, hoping eventually to marry her himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes a further detail: the pairing of outcomes \u8212- husband testified dead, second husband divorces; or first husband gave {\i get}, second husband dies \u8212- should not necessarily be read as parallel. The {\i mishna} may intend a cross-matched reading, which also avoids any entanglement with the ruling of {\i Rabbi} that two deceased husbands may establish a {\i chazaka} of fatal ill fortune \u8212- a discussion reserved for later in {\i Yevamos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relatives Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'chulan mutarot livneihem o l\u8217'acheihem} \u8212- all these women are permitted to marry the brother or the son of the person who brought the {\i get} or testified to the husband\u8217's death. The witness is not assumed to be lying and falsifying testimony so that a relative of his can benefit. The concern is confined to the witness\u8217's own potential interest; we do not project the suspicion outward to his family.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Relative Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Affair Suspicion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this ruling by comparing it to the law of one who is suspected of an illicit affair. The {\i gemara} cites: {\i ha\u8217'nitan min ha\u8217'isha asur be\u8217'ima uvevita} \u8212- one who is suspected of an affair with a woman is forbidden to marry her mother or her daughter \u8212- {\i uv\u8217'achotah} \u8212- and her sister. Once suspicion attaches, it is extended to close relatives. Why, then, are we not similarly concerned that the witness in our {\i mishna} might have delivered a false {\i get} or false testimony precisely in order that a brother or son could marry this woman, with the witness himself intending to continue an affair with her through access provided by the relative\u8217's household?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to understand what the {\i issur} in the affair case actually is. {\i Rashi} clarifies: the Torah\u8217's prohibitions of a woman and her daughter, or of a wife\u8217's sister, are triggered only by marriage. One who had illicit relations with a woman before marrying her daughter has not technically violated {\i arayot} \u8212- that violation requires a marriage to be in place first. The concern in the affair case is therefore prospective: {\i shema le\u8217'achar sheyissa et imma o et bitta tizneh zo immo} \u8212- perhaps after marrying the mother or daughter, he will continue the affair with the original woman, and now it will constitute a full biblical violation of {\i issur arayot}. The family context creates opportunity and proximity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the question is: does the same logic apply here? If the witness\u8217's brother marries the woman, will the witness have opportunity \u8212- and motivation \u8212- to pursue an illicit relationship with her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Visit Frequency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers a first answer: {\i nashi legabi nashi shechichan de\u8217'azlan, gavrei legabi gavrei lo shechichan} \u8212- women visit one another\u8217's homes frequently; mothers, sisters, and daughters spend time together and create natural opportunity for continued encounters. Men, however, do not visit their brothers\u8217' homes with comparable frequency. The access that the family-marriage dynamic creates is substantially less in the male-relative scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ee nami} \u8212- alternatively, the {\i gemara} proposes: {\i nashi delo asran shechivotan ahadadei lo kapdi ahadadei; gavrei de\u8217'asran shechivotan ahadadei kapdi ahadadei} \u8212- women are not rendered halachically forbidden to one another by what one does with another woman\u8217's relative; therefore a wife is not especially vigilant about her husband\u8217's access to her own relatives. But a man whose brother\u8217's wife becomes sexually involved with his brother is in a serious situation: {\i she} becomes forbidden to {\i his brother}, potentially causing a forced divorce. Therefore brothers are acutely vigilant about one another\u8217's wives, which itself curtails opportunity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking argument. The {\i gemara} is suggesting that the protective jealousy of the brother-in-law himself serves as a deterrent, in a way that does not operate in the case of a woman\u8217's female relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ee hachi, aviv nami} \u8212- if these are the operative distinctions, why does the {\i mishna} specify brothers and sons but not fathers? Should a father similarly be permitted to marry this woman? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i lo mibaya ka\u8217'amar, lo mibaya aviv} \u8212- of course the father may marry her, and the {\i mishna} need not even state it, because {\i de\u8217'bayit bereih mineih} \u8212- a son is embarrassed before his father and would not dare pursue an affair with his father\u8217's wife. {\i Aval beno delo bayit aviv mineih} \u8212- but a son\u8217's wife, regarding whom a father might feel he can take liberties with his own child \u8212- {\i eima lo}, one might have thought not to permit; {\i ka mashma lan} \u8212- the {\i mishna} teaches us that even so, the father is permitted to marry this woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The organizing concern throughout this entire section is thus not that someone would sin purely for the benefit of another person. We assume people sin for themselves. The question is whether marrying a relative would create opportunity and occasion for the witness to pursue an ongoing relationship with this woman. Given the distinctions in proximity, access, and marital stakes, the {\i gemara} finds sufficient grounds to confine the suspicion to the witness himself and not extend it to his male relatives.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Achot Zekuka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf concludes with a return to the {\i mishna} that has, in various forms, punctuated the entire discussion of {\i Yevamos}. {\i Arba\u8217'ah achim, shenayim mehem nesu\u8217'im shtei achayot} \u8212- four brothers, two of whom, Reuven and Shimon, are married to two sisters, Rachel and Leah. Reuven and Shimon both die.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Levi and Yehuda now face the {\i yibum} obligation. The {\i mishna} rules: {\i harei elu cholsot velo mityabmot} \u8212- these women must perform {\i chalitzah} and may not be taken in {\i yibum}. The reason is the configuration of {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- a {\i zika}, a quasi-marital bond, attaches each surviving brother to each of the widows. Levi cannot perform {\i yibum} with Rachel, because Rachel is connected through {\i zika} to Yehuda as well, making her {\i achot zekukato}. The same applies symmetrically to Yehuda with Leah. Even if Levi were to perform {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, he could not then proceed to {\i yibum} with Leah, because having performed {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, Leah would become {\i achot chalutzato} \u8212- the sister of his {\i chalutza} \u8212- which is itself rabbinically forbidden. The only resolution is {\i chalitzah} by both brothers with both women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one of them performed {\i yibum} in violation of this ruling, the question of whether the marriage stands becomes a matter of dispute between {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel}. {\i Beit Shammai} permits the marriage to remain, holding that the prohibition may have been only rabbinic and therefore does not require dissolution {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. {\i Beit Hillel} rules {\i yotsiu} \u8212- they must divorce. And indeed, the severity of performing {\i yibum} improperly here may rise to the level of a {\i d\u8217'oraita} violation \u8212- the {\i zika} may constitute a quasi-marriage, making the completed {\i yibum} an act involving a woman who remains in some sense {\i eshet ach} to the other brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Ervah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hayeta achat mehen asura alav al ha\u8217'echad issur ervah} \u8212- if Rachel is actually Levi\u8217's daughter, Rachel is categorically an {\i ervah} to him, not merely a {\i zekukah}. In that case, the {\i ervah} removes the {\i zika}: Levi has no {\i yibum} bond to Rachel at all, and Rachel therefore does not function as {\i achot zekukato} in relation to Leah. Levi may perform {\i yibum} with Leah directly. Yehuda, however, still has {\i zika} to both and cannot do {\i yibum} with either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Eruvin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hayeta achat mehen asura al zeh issur ervah vehashniya asura al zeh issur ervah} \u8212- if Rachel is Levi\u8217's daughter and Leah is Yehuda\u8217's daughter, each woman is a full {\i ervah} to one brother, eliminating his {\i zika} to her. Rachel is out of the picture for Levi; he may do {\i yibum} with Leah. Leah is out of the picture for Yehuda; he may do {\i yibum} with Rachel. {\i Ha\u8217'asura lazeh muteret lazeh veha\u8217'asura lazeh muteret lazeh} \u8212- the one forbidden to this one is permitted to the other, and vice versa. This is the case the {\i mishna} describes with the phrase {\i achotah keshe\u8217'hi yevamtah} \u8212- her sister when she is also her sister-in-law: where one is an {\i ervah}, the other may do {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum} freely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shma mina yesh zika} \u8212- the {\i gemara} infers from all of this that {\i zika} must be a real halachic bond, because the entire structure of the {\i mishna} depends upon it. If there were no {\i zika}, {\i hanei mitrei batei ka\u8217'atu} \u8212- these women come from two separate households, there is no {\i tzarah} relationship between them. {\i Hai liyabbem chad, vehai liyabbem chad} \u8212- Levi could simply take Rachel and Yehuda could simply take Leah, with no conflict at all. The fact that the {\i mishna} prohibits this entire arrangement must be because {\i zika} creates a cross-linkage that generates the problem of {\i achot zekukato}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} anticipates a counter-position: {\i le\u8217'olam eima lach ein zika} \u8212- perhaps there is in fact no {\i zika}, and yet {\i yibum} is still forbidden here. The reason would be {\i mishum dekasavar asur levatel mitzvat yevamin} \u8212- because performing {\i yibum} with one woman might lead to the {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum} for the other being lost entirely. {\i De\u8217'ad meyabbem chad meis idach} \u8212- while Levi is in the process of completing {\i yibum} with Rachel, Yehuda might die, and then Leah would have no one to perform either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} for her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if both brothers perform {\i chalitzah} with both women, that possibility is foreclosed: once {\i chalitzah} has been performed with Rachel, even if Yehuda dies, {\i chalitzah} can still be completed with Leah. The prohibition on {\i yibum} is thus not rooted in {\i zika} per se, but in the protection of the {\i mitzva} from being rendered impossible through circumstance. The {\i gemara} notes that this is an exceedingly far-fetched explanation, and the discussion will continue on the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 25b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get from Overseas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Overseas Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the bottom of 21a opens with a case that initially seems to belong in {\i Masechet Gittin}: {\i hamavee get mimedinat hayam} \u8212- someone who brings a {\i get} from overseas. As any student of {\i Gittin} knows, when delivering a {\i get} from overseas the messenger must declare {\i bifnay nechtav u\u8217'bifnay nechtam} \u8212- that the document was written and signed in his presence, thus validating its execution. The {\i mishnah} here establishes that this messenger, having delivered the testimony required to render the {\i get} valid, may not subsequently marry the newly divorced woman. Allowing him to do so would cast retrospective suspicion on the reliability and sincerity of his testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death/Murder Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with related cases. If the witness says {\i mes hu} \u8212- the husband died \u8212- or {\i haragtee} \u8212- I killed him \u8212- or {\i haragnu} \u8212- we killed him \u8212- he likewise may not marry the woman, because in each instance he has testified to the fact of the husband\u8217's death and thereby enabled the woman to remarry. Permitting him to marry her would undermine the credibility of his testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking, of course, is that the {\i mishnah} raises no concern about the confession of murder itself. The legal focus remains entirely on the question of whether his subsequent marriage to the woman would taint the testimony that allowed her to remarry. We will return to the confession below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} draws a distinction: if the witness says {\i haragtee} \u8212- I killed him \u8212- the woman may not remarry this particular man; but if he says {\i haragnu} \u8212- we killed him \u8212- she may. The precise meaning of this distinction is clarified by the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Investigation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} begins by probing the logic of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's opening case. It observes: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'mimedinat hayam} \u8212- the reason the witness cannot marry the woman is that it is specifically because he comes from overseas, meaning we are relying on his testimony ({\i de-alav kasamchinan}). But if he delivers a {\i get} from within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where no such declaration is required, and we are not formally relying on him ({\i d\u8217'lav alav kasamchinan}), then he may marry the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the case of testifying to the husband\u8217's death, and here a sharp difficulty arises. When the {\i gemara} says {\i d\u8217'lav alav kasamchinan} \u8212- we are not relying on him \u8212- that seems plainly wrong. Of course we are relying on him; he is the one who said the husband is dead! The {\i gemara} resolves this by invoking a well-known principle: {\i isha dayka u\u8217'minsava} \u8212- the woman herself investigates before she remarries. Because the consequences of remarrying while a husband is still alive are so severe, we trust the woman to undertake her own thorough investigation following the witness\u8217's testimony. In this framing, it is ultimately her investigation, not his testimony alone, that bears the weight of the permission to remarry. This is quite a radical {\i gemara}: the entire operative mechanism is relocated to the woman\u8217's own diligence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get vs.\u160 ?Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so, one might ask: if we are not really relying on the witness\u8217's testimony in the death case \u8212- since the woman\u8217's investigation is doing the real work \u8212- why can\u8217't he marry her? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hatam leka ksava, hacha ika ksava} \u8212- there is still a meaningful difference between the two cases. When someone testifies to the husband\u8217's death, he is still presenting factual testimony, even if it is subsequently bolstered by the woman\u8217's investigation. Delivering a {\i get} within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, by contrast, requires no testimonial declaration whatsoever; the messenger is doing nothing more than physically transmitting a document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the {\i mishnah} itself puts it, {\i mah bein get lemita} \u8212- what is the difference between divorce and death? {\i She\u8217'haksav mochiach} \u8212- the written document proves itself. A signed document from {\i Eretz Yisrael} carries inherent presumptive validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Validation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for this presumption is the {\i gemara}\u8217's principle that {\i edim she\u8217'bishtar kemi she\u8217'nechkerah edusam b\u8217'veis din} \u8212- witnesses whose signatures appear on a document are treated as though their testimony has already been fully examined in court. Even without what we might today call notarization, two signatures on a document establish its validity as a matter of law. It is only when a document arrives from outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- that questions may arise about the reliability of the signatures, and external verification in the form of {\i bifnay nechtav u\u8217'bifnay nechtam} becomes necessary.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Murder & Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Murder Confession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now draws out a crucial implication from the {\i mishnah}. It states: {\i hu nihu d\u8217'lo yisa et ishto} \u8212- it is specifically he who may not marry the woman \u8212- {\i ha l\u8217'acher tinase} \u8212- but she may marry a different man. This makes clear that the testimony is in fact legally acceptable. The {\i mishnah} never says that because the man confessed to murder his testimony is disqualified. On the contrary, the testimony stands; the only concern is preventing {\i him} from marrying {\i her}, lest it appear that the testimony was self-serving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is consistent with the broader halachic principle \u8212- extensively developed throughout {\i Yevamot} \u8212- that for the purpose of {\i edut isha}, testimony enabling a woman to remarry, the usual standards of testimony are relaxed considerably. A single witness suffices, and the {\i gemara} elsewhere extends this to women, slaves, and even hearsay testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, a difficulty arises from a ruling of {\i Rav Yosef}. {\i Rav Yosef} addresses a case where a man testifies that another man committed a sexual act with him {\i le\u8217'onsi} \u8212- against his will, meaning he was violated. In that case, he and a second witness may combine ({\i hu ve\u8217'acher mitztarfim lehargo}) to testify against the perpetrator and have him executed. Since the victim was not a willing participant, he bears no guilt and retains his status as a valid witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the man says the act was {\i birtzoni} \u8212- performed willingly \u8212- then he has admitted to being a willing participant in a grave transgression. The {\i Torah} declares {\i al tashet yadcha im rasha liheyot ed chamas} \u8212- do not join a wicked person to be a corrupt witness. He has effectively disqualified himself as a witness, and his testimony cannot be combined with that of another to prosecute anyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Splitting Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question at the heart of the {\i sugya}: the principle of {\i palginan dibura} \u8212- can we split his words? Can we take the portion of his testimony that concerns the other person and set aside the portion that incriminates himself? In {\i Rav Yosef}\u8217's case, the answer is no. The man\u8217's declaration of willing participation renders him a {\i rasha} and invalidates him as a witness entirely; we cannot bracket his self-incrimination and retain the rest of his testimony. This would seem to apply equally to our case: if a man confesses to murder, he has declared himself a {\i rasha}, and should be disqualified for {\i edut isha} just as for any other testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalid Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Menashya} offers a position that adds a further layer. He holds that a {\i gazlan d\u8217'divreihem} \u8212- someone rabbinically disqualified as a witness, such as a gambler \u8212- is nonetheless valid for {\i edut isha}, because the rabbis were lenient in matters of testimony concerning a woman\u8217's ability to remarry. However, a {\i gazlan d\u8217'divrei Torah} \u8212- someone biblically disqualified as a witness \u8212- is invalid even for {\i edut isha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder, as {\i Tosafot} does, whether there is a distinction between different categories of biblical disqualification. One type of invalid witness is someone disqualified because he has sinned for financial gain \u8212- his testimony is suspect because it may be purchased. That concern is intelligible in the context of {\i edut isha}. But another type of disqualified witness is simply a habitual sinner ({\i neveila leteiavon}), someone who sins out of appetite rather than greed. Why should that person necessarily be invalid for {\i edut isha}? The {\i gemara} here does not draw that distinction, and the difficulty remains, at least implicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In our case, if the witness confessed to murder, he would appear \u8212- by his own account \u8212- to be biblically disqualified, and according to {\i Rav Menashya} he should be invalid even for {\i edut isha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adam Karov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises the possibility that {\i Rav Menashya} must be following {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position in the {\i mishnah}, which disqualifies the witness who says {\i haragtee}. The {\i gemara} rejects this. {\i Rav Menashya} himself says: {\i ana de\u8217'amri afilu Rabbanon} \u8212- I can maintain my position even according to the Rabbis who accept the testimony. The reason the Rabbis accept it is based on {\i Rava}\u8217's principle: {\i adam karov etzel atzmo, ve\u8217'ein adam mesim atzmo rasha} \u8212- a person is as close to himself as his nearest relative, and just as a relative\u8217's testimony about himself is inadmissible, a person cannot render himself a {\i rasha} through his own declaration. This is the mechanism of {\i palginan dibura}: we split his words, discard the self-incriminating portion as legally inert, and retain only the testimony concerning the other person \u8212- the fact that the husband is dead. Self-incrimination in matters of {\i mita} or {\i malkot} is halachically irrelevant; it requires external testimony to establish criminal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef/Menashya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers whether {\i Rav Yosef} \u8212- who refuses to split the testimony in his case \u8212- must be following {\i Rabbi Yehuda} in the {\i mishnah}. Again the answer is no. {\i Rav Yosef} says {\i ana de\u8217'amri afilu Rabbanon}: I too can work with the position of the Rabbis. But my case is different: {\i shani edut isha de\u8217'akilu bah Rabbanon} \u8212- testimony concerning a woman\u8217's remarriage is a domain where the Rabbis were lenient, and it is specifically in that domain that we permit {\i palginan dibura}, splitting the testimony to retain only what is relevant. In the case of prosecuting someone for a capital offense, {\i Rav Yosef} holds, we do not split the words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus arrives at a nuanced picture. {\i Rav Menashya} holds that we do apply {\i palginan dibura} \u8212- he disagrees with {\i Rav Yosef} \u8212- and therefore even a witness who has testified against himself can have the relevant portion of his testimony accepted. But {\i Rav Menashya} also holds that a biblically disqualified witness cannot testify for {\i edut isha}; what saves the murderer-confessor is that his self-incrimination is discarded and he is therefore not treated as biblically disqualified. {\i Rav Yosef} holds that we do not apply {\i palginan dibura} \u8212- the testimony cannot be split \u8212- but is nonetheless lenient because even a biblically disqualified witness may testify for {\i edut isha}. The {\i gemara} suggests that one could in principle combine both leniencies, and in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case according to the {\i Tanna Kamma}, it is plausible that both factors work together: the {\i edut isha} context permits us to apply {\i palginan dibura}, thereby accepting the relevant portion of the testimony while discarding the self-incriminating portion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tanna/R. Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haragi vs.\u160 ?Haragnu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's distinction in the {\i mishnah}: why does it matter whether he says {\i haragtee} \u8212- I killed him \u8212- or {\i haragnu} \u8212- we killed him? Even in the latter case, he was part of a murder. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i amar Rav Yehuda be\u8217'omer ani hayisi im horgav} \u8212- it does not mean \u8220"I murdered him.\u8221" It means \u8220"I was there, I was among those who killed him\u8221" \u8212- present, perhaps involved in the planning, but not the one who literally committed the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Part of Group\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position is entirely coherent. When a man says \u8220"I was part of the group that killed him,\u8221" he has not rendered himself a {\i rasha} in the biblical sense; he has not confessed to being the direct killer. His testimony about the husband\u8217's death therefore remains valid for {\i edut isha}, and the woman can remarry anyone except him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bandit Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then challenges {\i Rav Yehuda} \u8212- the {\i amora} who articulated this interpretation \u8212- with a {\i baraisa}. A certain bandit ({\i listis}) was being taken out for execution in {\i Kapotkia}, and he said to those present: go and tell the wife of {\i Shimon ben Kohen} that I killed her husband when I was entering {\i Lud}. They went and told her, and she was subsequently remarried on the basis of this testimony. Here, apparently, a man who was plainly being executed for murder \u8212- and who explicitly said {\i ani haragti}, I killed him \u8212- had his testimony accepted, and the woman remarried. This seems to contradict {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's interpretation that {\i haragnu} is only acceptable because it means \u8220"I was part of the group, not the actual killer.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} responds: {\i misham raya?} \u8212- do you bring a proof from that case? There too, the man was saying {\i ani hayisi im horgav} \u8212- I was among the killers \u8212- not that he personally committed the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Courts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} presses further: the text says he was being taken out to be executed ({\i hayotzei lehayeireig}). Does this not prove that he was a convicted murderer? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i bedina de\u8217'umot ha\u8217'olam dlo dayki vekatli} \u8212- non-Jewish courts do not apply the rigorous standards of evidence required by Jewish law; they may execute on the basis of suspicion or insufficient evidence. His execution by a non-Jewish court does not establish him as halachically defined as a {\i rasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes, however, that this caveat is instructive: according to {\i Rav Menashya}\u8217's position that a biblically disqualified witness is invalid even for {\i edut isha}, everyone agrees that a witness definitively established as a murderer through proper {\i beit din} proceedings could not testify for {\i edut isha}. It is only because his status was established by a non-Jewish court \u8212- which is halachically insufficient \u8212- that his testimony remains acceptable. What divides the {\i Tanna Kamma} and {\i Rabbi Yehuda} is therefore the question of self-incriminating confession: the {\i Tanna Kamma} disregards it via {\i palginan dibura} and {\i adam karov etzel atzmo}, while {\i Rabbi Yehuda} treats the self-declaration of \u8220"I killed him\u8221" as effective self-disqualification, though he accepts \u8220"I was among the killers.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sage & Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} introduces another category of prohibited marriage. {\i Chacham she\u8217'asar et ha\u8217'ishah bineder al ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a sage who, when asked to annul a woman\u8217's vow, ruled that the vow could not be annulled, with the consequence that the vow itself forbids her to her husband. The case is more extreme than simply a husband threatening divorce; the {\i neder} itself renders her halachically unavailable to her husband, making continued cohabitation impossible. Once the husband then divorces her, the sage who rendered that ruling may not marry her: {\i harei zeh lo yisaena}. His ruling directly brought about the divorce, and his subsequent marriage to the woman would cast retroactive doubt on the sincerity and integrity of his ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then carves out an exception. If the same sage was part of a {\i beit din} before which a minor performed {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- verbal refusal of a marriage contracted by her mother or brothers \u8212- or before which a {\i yevama} performed {\i chalitza}, in those cases he may subsequently marry the woman: {\i yisaena mipnei she\u8217'hu beis din}. The decisive factor is that he did not act alone; he was one member of a {\i beit din} and therefore cannot be said to have unilaterally engineered the woman\u8217's release. Whether the reasoning is that appearances are less problematic when a group acted together, or that collective deliberation is less susceptible to manipulation, the {\i mishnah} draws a clear line: independent action raises suspicion; action as part of a {\i beit din} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mumcheh Expert\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} probes the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case more carefully. If the {\i chacham} acted alone in ruling on the {\i neder}, how is that valid at all? Does not {\i hataras nedarim} require a {\i beit din} of three? The {\i gemara} cites the ruling: {\i tana hataras nedarim b\u8217'shlosha} \u8212- the annulment of vows requires three. And if we assume it was done by a {\i beit din} of three, why would any single member be suspect? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i le\u8217'olam b\u8217'chad} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} deals with a single individual. The basis for this is the ruling of {\i Rav Chisda} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: {\i b\u8217'yachid mumcheh} \u8212- a single recognized expert ({\i mumcheh}) can annul vows independently, just as three laypeople can. The {\i mishnah} is thus dealing with the case of a solitary {\i mumcheh} who ruled that the vow could not be annulled; since he acted alone, he is subject to suspicion and may not marry the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Self-Interest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a case to test how broadly we extend the concern for group composition. A {\i baraita} states: {\i ein hem me\u8217'idim} \u8212- when two witnesses have signed a sale document for a field, or signed a woman\u8217's {\i get}, the sages were not concerned ({\i lo chashu chachamim l\u8217'davar zeh}) that a witness might subsequently purchase the field or marry the woman. Even when only two are involved \u8212- which is less than a {\i beit din} of three \u8212- since the individual is not acting alone, there is no disqualifying suspicion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by noting that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's reference to {\i beit din} is not primarily about requiring three rather than two; rather, it is clarifying in passing that {\i mi\u8217'un} requires a {\i beit din} of three, against those who hold that two suffice. The underlying principle for permitting remarriage, however, is simply that the individual did not act independently \u8212- and two suffices for that purpose.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Knas Yotzi Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kahana vs.\u160 ?Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to what appears to be the implicit question underlying all these {\i mishnayot}: {\i kanas mah she\u8217'yotsi} \u8212- if one of these men went ahead and married the woman despite the prohibition, must he divorce her? {\i Rashi} notes that the question applies to both the case of the {\i chacham} and the {\i neder}, and the case of the one who brought the {\i get} or testified to the husband\u8217's death, though the {\i Rashba} limits it to the final {\i mishnah} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Kahana} says {\i kanas motzi} \u8212- he must divorce. {\i Rav Ashi} says {\i kanas eino motzi} \u8212- he need not divorce. A {\i baraisa} taught by {\i Rav Zuti} in the academy of {\i Rav Papa} supports {\i Rav Ashi}\u8217's position. When the rabbis asked {\i Rav Ashi} whether his ruling was a received tradition ({\i gemara}) or a logical derivation ({\i sevara}), he replied that it can be inferred from the {\i mishnah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof is from an earlier {\i mishnah} dealing with cases of suspected relationships \u8212- conversion, suspected adultery, and the like \u8212- where the {\i mishnah} rules: {\i im kanas eino motzi} \u8212- if he married her after the fact, he need not divorce. The principle derived is {\i birnana lo mafkinan} \u8212- we do not force divorce on the basis of rumor and communal suspicion alone. That same principle, the {\i gemara} concludes, applies here: {\i hacha nami birnana lo mafkinan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow vs.\u160 ?Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might object, however, that the cases are not truly parallel. In the earlier cases of suspected adultery and conversion, even if the suspicion were true, the woman was still legally divorced or widowed; the only concern was reputational. Here, in the case of the {\i get} or the death testimony, if the underlying concern were true \u8212- if the {\i get} were invalid or the husband were still alive \u8212- the woman would be {\i eishes ish}, a married woman, which is a far more serious problem than mere appearances. This explains why the {\i Rashba} restricts the {\i gemara}\u8217's ruling to the case of the {\i neder}: there, even if the {\i chacham} manipulated his ruling, the husband still gave a {\i get}, and the woman\u8217's divorce is halachically valid. The only issue is one of appearances and suspicion, to which {\i birnana lo mafkinan} properly applies. In the {\i get} and death-testimony cases, by contrast, there may be a genuine doubt about the woman\u8217's legal status \u8212- and that is a substantially weightier concern.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The following {\i mishnah} addresses when these same individuals \u8212- the {\i chacham} who could not annul the vow, the one who delivered the {\i get}, the one who testified to the husband\u8217's death \u8212- may in fact marry the woman. {\i Ve\u8217'kulam she\u8217'hoyu lahem nashim u\u8217'mesu mutarot le\u8217'hinasay lahem} \u8212- if all of these men were already married at the time they performed the relevant act, and their wives subsequently died, then the women they enabled to remarry are permitted to them. The logic is sociological: even in a society where polygamy was technically permitted, it was exceedingly rare in practice, and was essentially limited to men wealthy enough to support multiple households. The {\i mishnah} therefore takes it as a practical given that a married man would not seek to marry an additional woman. If he was already married when he delivered the {\i get} or testified to the husband\u8217's death, we do not suspect that he engineered the whole situation in order to marry her. One might ask: couldn\u8217't he simply have planned to divorce his own wife? The {\i mishnah} here says nothing about that scenario \u8212- and the {\i gemara} addresses it directly below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marital Strife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a pointed inference: {\i mesu in, nisgarshu lo} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} permits marriage only when his wife died, not when he divorced her. If he divorced his wife, we can suspect that it was all part of a scheme: he manipulated the {\i get} or the death testimony, and then divorced his own wife so that he could marry this other woman, while the sequence created the appearance of propriety.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Hillel} then challenges this from a {\i baraisa} which states {\i afilu nisgarshu} \u8212- even if they divorced their wives, it is permitted. The {\i gemara} resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya, ha de-havei ketata, ha de-la havei ketata} \u8212- it depends on whether there was prior marital strife ({\i ketata}) or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading here is counterintuitive and worth examining carefully. One might assume that if the couple was already fighting before the {\i get} was delivered, the suspicion would be heightened \u8212- the man was already contemplating divorce and could therefore have planned to marry this other woman all along. {\i Rashi} says the opposite: if they were already fighting beforehand, then his divorce of his wife was not precipitated by this woman. He would have divorced his wife regardless. The plan to leave his wife predated the woman\u8217's {\i get}, and therefore we do not say it was all engineered for her sake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then offers a second answer: {\i iba\u8217'it eima, ha de\u8217'argil hu, ve\u8217'ha de\u8217'argilah hee} \u8212- even without any pre-existing fighting, it matters who was the one pressing for divorce. If he was the one pushing for the divorce of his own wife, he cannot then marry the woman, because it looks as though he engineered everything. If she \u8212- his own wife \u8212- was the one pushing for the divorce, we say he was not the architect of the situation, and he may marry the other woman. The {\i gemara} elsewhere acknowledges that interpersonal dynamics can be manipulated to induce one\u8217's spouse to seek a divorce, but the {\i mishnah} apparently does not press suspicion that far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intervening Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further states: {\i ve\u8217'khulan she-nisu le\u8217'acherim ve\u8217'nisgarshu o she\u8217'nisalmenu mutarot le\u8217'hinase lahem} \u8212- if these women went ahead and married other men, and those second husbands then divorced them or died, the original witnesses may now marry them. Since the woman first married someone else, no one could reasonably conclude that the witness manipulated the original {\i get} or death testimony in order to marry her himself \u8212- for if he truly had wanted to marry her, he would not have wanted her to marry another man in the interim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relatives Allow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally: {\i ve\u8217'khulan mutarot li\u8217'vneihem o le\u8217'acheihem} \u8212- all of these women may marry the sons or brothers of the witnesses who enabled their remarriage. We do not extend suspicion so far as to imagine that a man would falsify testimony or manipulate a vow ruling so that a {\i relative} of his could marry the woman. The concern we have is self-interested scheming; we do not carry it over to third parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Visit; Men Don\u8217't\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a challenge from a {\i baraisa} that seems to go further in its prohibitions. Someone suspected of an affair with a woman is forbidden to marry her mother, daughter, or sister \u8212- {\i asur be\u8217'imah u\u8217've\u8217'vitah u\u8217've\u8217'achotah}. If we extend concern so broadly in that case, why do we not similarly prohibit the brother or son of the witness from marrying the woman?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, as {\i Rashi} clarifies, is important. The prohibition against marrying the mother, daughter, or sister in the suspicion case is not because the man has already sinned by sleeping with the woman before marriage. Rather, the concern is prospective: once he is married to the mother or daughter, the woman herself will visit \u8212- {\i nashi l\u8217'gabei nashi shekichan de\u8217'azlan} \u8212- because women in a family regularly visit one another. He will then have opportunity to continue the affair, and at that point it will constitute the biblical prohibition of {\i eishes ish} or the forbidden union of a wife and her close female relative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic does not apply when it is a brother or son who marries the woman. {\i Gavri l\u8217'gabei gavri lo shekichan} \u8212- men do not visit each other\u8217's households with the same frequency and intimacy. The opportunity for an illicit encounter is therefore substantially less.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers an additional distinction: {\i nashi d\u8217'lo asran shechivatan ahadadi lo kapdi ahadadi} \u8212- women are not halachically barred from their husbands simply because the husband slept with their relative, and therefore the wife is not vigilantly guarding against this. {\i Gavri d\u8217'asran shechivatan ahadadi kapdi ahadadi} \u8212- men, by contrast, are very much on guard, because if a brother-in-law were to sleep with his wife, she would become forbidden to her husband and the marriage would have to end. That vigilance itself diminishes the opportunity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question: if so, why does the {\i mishnah} permit the {\i father} of the witness to marry the woman? Surely the father is also a male relative. The {\i gemara} answers: the {\i mishnah} uses the formulation {\i lo tzricha} \u8212- it need not even be stated. It is obvious that the father may marry the woman, because a son is embarrassed to initiate an affair with his own father\u8217's wife. The {\i mishnah} finds it necessary to state only the case of the son, where one might have thought that the father \u8212- feeling a parental sense of authority or access \u8212- might take advantage, and therefore comes to teach that even the son\u8217's case is permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sisters & Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the {\i amud} returns to territory that has been covered multiple times in {\i Yevamot}, though it bears revisiting. {\i Arba\u8217'ah achim} \u8212- four brothers. Two of them, let us call them Reuven and Shimon, are married to two sisters, Rachel and Leah. Both Reuven and Shimon die. {\i Harei elu choltzot ve\u8217'lo mityabmot} \u8212- the surviving brothers, Levi and Yehuda, may not perform {\i yibum} with either sister; they may only perform {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achat Zekukato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is the prohibition of {\i achot zekukato}. {\i Zika} \u8212- the quasi-marital bond that the {\i Torah} creates between a {\i yevama} and her {\i yavam} \u8212- connects Levi to both Rachel and Leah simultaneously. Rachel is the sister of the woman to whom Levi stands in a {\i zika} relationship, and {\i vice versa}. Therefore neither may be taken in {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Ervah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then refines the analysis. {\i Haysa achat me\u8217'hen asurah alav issur ervah} \u8212- if one of the women was an {\i ervah} to one of the brothers \u8212- his own daughter, for example \u8212- then that {\i yavam} is free to perform {\i yibum} with the other sister. An {\i issur ervah} removes the {\i zika} connection entirely, releasing him to marry the remaining {\i yevama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Options\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In that case: {\i achota k\u8217'she\u8217'hi yevimtah, o choletzet o mityabmenet} \u8212- when the sister stands in the relationship of both sister and {\i yevama}, she has full options: either {\i chalitza} or {\i yibum}. With one woman an {\i ervah} and thus out of the picture, the other is a straightforward {\i yevama} with no competing {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also addresses the case where the prohibition is merely {\i issur mitzvah} or {\i issur kedusha} \u8212- a rabbinic prohibition or a lesser {\i lav} \u8212- rather than a full {\i issur ervah}. In that case, the weaker prohibition is insufficient to sever the {\i zika}, and the woman remains {\i zekukato}. Only a genuine {\i ervah} can remove the {\i zika} bond entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might naturally ask: even granting all of the above, why not simply have Levi perform {\i yibum} with Rachel and Yehuda perform {\i yibum} with Leah? They come from two separate marriages ({\i mi\u8217'trei batei ka\u8217'atu}), and there is no {\i tzara} issue. Let each {\i yavam} take one {\i yevama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises this question directly: {\i shma mina yesh zika} \u8212- the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling implies that the concept of {\i zika} is operative, for otherwise, with no {\i zika}, there would be no obstacle to each {\i yavam} performing {\i yibum} with one of the sisters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} then considers the opposite position: {\i le\u8217'olam eima lach ein zika} \u8212- perhaps there is no {\i zika}. If so, what prevents each {\i yavam} from performing {\i yibum} with one woman? {\i Rava} offers an explanation: {\i mishum d\u8217'kasavar asur levatel mitzvas yavam} \u8212- it is prohibited to bring about a situation in which the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} might be nullified. If, for example, Levi proceeds to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel, Leah now must wait for Yehuda. If Yehuda then dies in the interim, Leah is left without a {\i yavam} to perform either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}, and the {\i mitzvah} has been effectively nullified. If, on the other hand, Levi performs {\i chalitza} with Rachel, then even if Yehuda dies, Levi remains available and can perform {\i chalitza} with Leah as well, ensuring that neither woman is left in a state of unresolved {\i zika}. It is to prevent the potential nullification of the {\i mitzvah} that we require {\i chalitza} in this case \u8212- a far-reaching and admittedly somewhat attenuated rationale, but one that the {\i gemara} presents as Rava\u8217's resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Four Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the very bottom of 26a, where the Gemara is discussing the Mishnah concerning four brothers \u8212- two of whom die, leaving their widows to fall before the two surviving brothers \u8212- in a case where each surviving brother cannot perform {\i yibum} because each widow is {\i achot zekukato}, the sister of a woman to whom he stands in a {\i zikah} bond. The ruling of the Mishnah is that each surviving brother performs {\i chalitzah} with both women. We had already encountered an alternative explanation of this Mishnah: the real concern is {\i assur levatel mitzvat yabam} \u8212- it is forbidden to nullify the opportunity for {\i yibum}. According to this reading, if Levi were to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel, we fear that Yehuda might die, at which point Leah would become Levi\u8217's {\i achot ishto}, rendering her ineligible for either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. Therefore, Levi must perform {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, so that even if Yehuda dies, Levi retains the ability to perform {\i chalitzah} with Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bittul Mitzvat Yabam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This alternative explanation \u8212- grounded in the concern of {\i assur levatel mitzvat yabam} rather than in the prohibition of {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- shifts the focus of the Mishnah\u8217's ruling from a structural {\i issur} to a prophylactic concern. The worry is not that the act of {\i yibum} is itself forbidden, but that performing it carelessly could eliminate the possibility of fulfilling the {\i mitzvah} for the other widow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Four Not Three?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this reading: {\i i hachi, telata nami} \u8212- if the concern is {\i bittul mitzvat yabam}, why does the Mishnah require four brothers? Three would suffice to illustrate the point. If Levi alone survives and both women fall before him, he obviously cannot perform {\i yibum} with Rachel, since Leah would then be left with no recourse whatsoever. The problem is manifest even with three brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zikah & Four Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds that the Mishnah employs the principle of {\i lo miba\u8217'aya}: the case of three brothers is so obvious it goes without saying. The {\i chiddush} lies precisely in the case of four brothers. One might have thought that with two surviving brothers, the concern of {\i bittul mitzvat yabam} is attenuated \u8212- after all, even if Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel, Yehuda is still present and will likely be available to perform {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} with Leah. The Mishnah therefore teaches that even in this case, where there is a second brother standing by, we remain concerned that Yehuda might die before fulfilling his obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i achot zekukato} reading, the {\i chiddush} of four brothers is different: it is a more novel ruling that {\i achot zekukato} applies even when the {\i zikah} is distributed between two brothers, since one might have thought that a shared {\i zikah} is too weak to generate the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear of One Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presses further: if we are worried that one brother might die, why not extend the case to five brothers \u8212- and fear that both Yehuda and a third brother might die, leaving Leah without {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}? The Gemara responds: {\i lemita de-trei lo chayshinan} \u8212- we do not entertain the fear that two people will die simultaneously before any act can be performed. The concern for one death is realistic enough to legislate around; the concern for two simultaneous deaths is too remote. With this, the Gemara closes the discussion of the four-brothers case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Sisters Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Three Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a new case, cited in the name of Rav: three sisters who are {\i yevamot} fall before two surviving brothers \u8212- Yissachar and Zebulun. Let us call the three sisters Rachel, Leah, and Sarah, all sisters of one another, each married to a different brother who has died, leaving the two survivors with a {\i zikah} obligation to all three women. Since all three women are sisters to one another, any act of {\i yibum} by either surviving brother would render the other two women {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- a rabbinic quasi-prohibition analogous to {\i achot ishto}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling of Rav is: {\i zeh choletz l\u8217'achas v\u8217'zeh choletz l\u8217'achas v\u8217'emtza\u8217'it tzericha chalitzah mishneihem} \u8212- one brother performs {\i chalitzah} with one sister, the other brother performs {\i chalitzah} with another, and the third woman \u8212- called {\i emtza\u8217'it}, \u8220"the middle one,\u8221" though she is really the last to become available \u8212- requires {\i chalitzah} from both brothers. Tosafot notes that she is called \u8220"middle\u8221" because she stands, as it were, between Yissachar and Zebulun.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case unfolds sequentially: the first husband dies, Yissachar performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel; then the second husband dies, Zebulun performs {\i chalitzah} with Leah; then the third husband dies, and Sarah requires {\i chalitzah} from both of them. The question is why Sarah requires {\i chalitzah} from both brothers, and why there is a preference that Zebulun perform {\i chalitzah} with Leah rather than Yissachar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Psula Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava frames the logic as follows: {\i mideka\u8217'amret emtza\u8217'it tzericha chalitzah mishneihem, kasavar yesh zikah} \u8212- since Rav rules that the last sister requires {\i chalitzah} from both brothers, this ruling presupposes the position of {\i yesh zikah}, and moreover invokes a distinct principle: {\i chalitzah pesulah tzarich lachzor al kol ha\u8217'achim} \u8212- a defective, second-rate {\i chalitzah} must be obtained from all surviving brothers, not just one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a governing principle that requires careful understanding. Ordinarily, when a man dies leaving multiple wives, {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} with one wife suffices to release all the others. But this rule applies only when the {\i zikah} is full and unimpaired. In cases where, for any reason, the {\i zikah} to a particular woman is weaker or altered in character, the resulting {\i chalitzah} is considered {\i pesulah} \u8212- not invalid in the sense of being null, but second-rate, a {\i chalitzah gru\u8217'ah}. Rashi explains it as a {\i chalitzah} performed in a situation where {\i yibum} is impossible. Tosafot refines this: the definition is not merely that {\i yibum} is impossible, but specifically that the reason {\i yibum} is impossible is because something has weakened or complicated the {\i zikah} itself. When the {\i zikah} is weakened, the {\i chalitzah} is correspondingly weakened, and a weakened {\i chalitzah} must be obtained from all brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot enumerates the main categories that produce a {\i chalitzah pesulah}: (1) the woman is {\i achot zikukato} \u8212- for one who holds {\i yesh zikah}, this creates a quasi-{\i ervah} status that weakens the {\i zikah}; (2) the woman is {\i achot chalutzato} \u8212- her sister has already received {\i chalitzah} from this brother, making her a rabbinic quasi-{\i ervah}; (3) she is a {\i ba\u8217'alat haget} \u8212- the surviving brother gave her a {\i get}, which, though not halachically required and without full {\i d\u8217'oraita} effect in this context, nonetheless prevents {\i yibum} and shifts the character of the {\i zikah}; and (4) she is a {\i ba\u8217'alat ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- the surviving brother performed {\i maamar} with her, a rabbinic act of quasi-betrothal that strengthens the bond in one sense but simultaneously removes her from the simple center of the {\i zikah} relationship, making {\i chalitzah} less straightforwardly applicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In all these cases, the {\i zikah} has been displaced from its normal configuration \u8212- whether weakened by distance or complicated by prior action \u8212- and the {\i chalitzah} performed in such circumstances carries less force, requiring that it be obtained from each of the surviving brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequential Deaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the three-sisters case, Rava asks: if every woman is {\i achot zekukato} and therefore in a weakened {\i zikah} relationship, why don\u8217't Rachel and Leah also require {\i chalitzah} from both brothers? The Gemara responds: {\i lo tzricha d\u8217'naphal zo achar zo} \u8212- the case is specifically one where the husbands died sequentially, not simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the first husband dies, only one woman \u8212- Rachel \u8212- falls before the two brothers. At that moment she is not {\i achot zekukato} to anyone, since no other woman has yet fallen. Her {\i zikah} is full and unimpaired. The {\i chalitzah} Yissachar performs with Rachel is therefore a complete, first-rate {\i chalitzah}. Then the second husband dies and Leah falls. What is Leah\u8217's status vis-\u224 ?-vis Yissachar? She is {\i achot chalutzato} \u8212- her sister Rachel has just received {\i chalitzah} from Yissachar. But vis-\u224 ?-vis Zebulun, she bears no such disability: the {\i zikah} was fully severed with respect to Yissachar by Rachel\u8217's {\i chalitzah}, and Leah is neither {\i achot chalutzato} nor {\i achot zekukato} relative to Zebulun. Therefore Zebulun performs a complete, first-rate {\i chalitzah} with Leah. By the time the third husband dies and Sarah becomes available, she is {\i achot chalutzato} of both Yissachar and Zebulun. Any {\i chalitzah} performed with her by either brother is therefore {\i pesulah}, and she requires the {\i chalitzah} to be performed by both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, all three men had died simultaneously, every woman would stand from the outset as {\i achot zekukato} to both brothers, and all three would require {\i chalitzah} from both \u8212- the Gemara concedes this point explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rachel & Sarah\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks why Rachel is specifically called {\i achot zikukato} in Rav\u8217's formulation, since the application of that term is what sets the case in motion. The answer follows from the sequential structure of the case: before any death, Rachel stands in a full {\i zikah} with both brothers. Her status as {\i achot zikukato} exists only insofar as the {\i zikah} bond \u8212- which is a collective bond \u8212- implies that her sisters share in it. Once the deaths become sequential and {\i chalitzah} begins to be performed, Rachel\u8217's full {\i chalitzah} from Yissachar is the event that establishes Sarah as {\i achot chalutzato} of Yissachar, and Leah\u8217's {\i chalitzah} from Zebulun establishes Sarah as {\i achot chalutzato} of Zebulun as well. Sarah\u8217's need for {\i chalitzah} from both brothers thus flows from the sequential accumulation of {\i chalutzah} relationships as each act of {\i chalitzah} transforms the status of the remaining women.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the framework of {\i chalitzah pesulah} and its requirement of {\i chalitzah} from all brothers, the Gemara turns to the formal debate between Rav and Shmuel. The Gemara notes a potential tension: the ruling about the middle sister requiring {\i chalitzah} from both brothers was cited in Rav\u8217's name, yet we know from elsewhere that Rav holds {\i ein zikah} \u8212- he does not recognize the rabbinic category of {\i achot zekukato} as generating a true quasi-{\i ervah} relationship. The Gemara resolves this by clarifying that Rav articulated this ruling according to the position of {\i yesh zikah}, not necessarily as his own personal ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's position is stated simply: {\i echad choletz lekulan} \u8212- one brother performs {\i chalitzah} with all of the women. Shmuel apparently rejects both the requirement of first-rate {\i chalitzah} in every case and, more strikingly, the principle that {\i chalitzah pesulah} requires {\i chalitzah} from all brothers. According to Shmuel, if Reuven has already performed {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, making him unable to perform first-rate {\i chalitzah} with Leah \u8212- since she is now {\i achot chalutzato} \u8212- he may nonetheless go ahead and perform second-rate {\i chalitzah} with Leah, and then with Sarah as well. He need not defer to Shimon; one brother suffices throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Me\u8217'alya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses Shmuel: do we not know from elsewhere that Shmuel himself holds {\i chalitzah ma\u8217'alya beinun} \u8212- that a superior, first-rate {\i chalitzah} is required? The source for this is a ruling of Shmuel cited elsewhere (122a): {\i chalatz l\u8217'achayot lo nifteru tzarot} \u8212- if a man performs {\i chalitzah} with the sisters, the co-wives are not thereby released; but {\i chalatz litzarot nifteru achayot} \u8212- if he performs {\i chalitzah} with the co-wives, the sisters are released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling clearly presupposes that a first-rate {\i chalitzah} carries greater power than a second-rate one: a {\i chalitzah} performed with a woman whose status is {\i achot zekukato} (a second-rate {\i chalitzah}, grade B) does not release the co-wife, whereas a {\i chalitzah} performed with the co-wife \u8212- who, though herself subject to a one-degree-removed quasi-{\i ervah} status, stands in a fuller relationship with the {\i yavam} \u8212- does release the sister. If Shmuel values first-rate {\i chalitzah} to the point that only it can release a co-wife, why does he say that one brother may perform second-rate {\i chalitzah} with all the women when a first-rate option is available?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Co-Wives & Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weaker Chalitzah Insuffi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the stakes, the case that grounds Shmuel\u8217's ruling about co-wives must be spelled out carefully. Reuven dies leaving Rachel and her co-wife Devorah; Shimon dies leaving Leah and her co-wife Naomi. Yissachar alone survives. Of these four women, Rachel and Leah are sisters to one another, making each of them {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- a quasi-{\i ervah} \u8212- to the surviving brother. Devorah and Naomi are their respective co-wives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Can Yissachar perform {\i yibum} with Rachel? No \u8212- she is {\i achot zekukato}. Can he perform {\i yibum} with Devorah? Devorah is a degree further removed: she is not {\i achot zekukato} herself, but {\i tzaras achot zekukato} \u8212- the co-wife of a quasi-{\i ervah}. This is a weaker prohibition, but it is still a prohibition. However, because Devorah\u8217's relationship to Yissachar is less attenuated by the quasi-{\i ervah} structure, his {\i chalitzah} with Devorah is of a higher grade than his {\i chalitzah} with Rachel. And it is this gradation that determines the outcome: a second-rate {\i chalitzah} with Rachel does not release Devorah, but a relatively better {\i chalitzah} with Devorah does release Rachel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stronger Chalitzah Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question returns: if Shimon is available and could perform a full, first-rate {\i chalitzah} with Leah \u8212- since Leah is not {\i achot chalutzato} or {\i achot zekukato} relative to Shimon \u8212- why should we allow Reuven to perform a second-rate {\i chalitzah} with Leah? The {\i chalitzah} from Shimon would be {\i kesherah} in the fullest sense; {\i chalitzah} from Reuven would be {\i pesulah}. If Shmuel holds {\i chalitzah ma\u8217'alya beinun} \u8212- that a superior {\i chalitzah} is preferred \u8212- why does he permit the inferior option when the superior is available?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers a first reread of Shmuel: when Shmuel said {\i echad choletz lekulan}, perhaps he was not saying that one brother literally does {\i chalitzah} with all three women. Perhaps he meant only to dispute the requirement of obtaining {\i chalitzah} from all brothers in the case of the last woman, Sarah. He agrees that Reuven performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel and Shimon performs {\i chalitzah} with Leah; his innovation is only that Sarah needs {\i chalitzah} from just one brother, not both. And since Shimon handled two of the three women, the Gemara permits calling him the one who did {\i chalitzah} \u8220"with all of them\u8221" \u8212- {\i keivan deruba gabeih, kari leh kulan}, since the majority was with him. This is acknowledged as a somewhat forced reading but at least allows Shmuel to maintain his preference for {\i chalitzah ma\u8217'alya} while disputing the {\i tzarich lachzor al kol ha\u8217'achim} principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achot Zikukato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then attempts to defend the original, simpler reading of Shmuel, distinguishing when {\i chalitzah ma\u8217'alya} is required. The resolution offered is: {\i ki ka\u8217'amar Shmuel chalitzah ma\u8217'alya beinun, hanei mili limftar tzartah} \u8212- Shmuel\u8217's principle that a superior {\i chalitzah} is needed applies specifically when the goal is to release a co-wife. Only a first-rate {\i chalitzah} carries enough force to release another woman. But {\i aval limftar nafshah patrah} \u8212- to release the woman herself, a second-rate {\i chalitzah} is entirely sufficient. In the three-sisters case, Sarah has no co-wives. Therefore Shmuel sees no reason to insist on first-rate {\i chalitzah} for her; second-rate suffices, and the dispute with Rav reduces to whether {\i chalitzah pesulah} requires {\i chalitzah} from all brothers (Rav: yes; Shmuel: no) and whether a second-rate {\i chalitzah} is permissible when no co-wife is at stake (Shmuel: yes).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sequential Deaths Matter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Deaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies a fundamental structural point: the distinction between first-rate and second-rate {\i chalitzah}, and which woman receives {\i chalitzah} from which brother, depends entirely on whether the deaths occurred simultaneously or sequentially. If all three husbands had died at once, every woman would stand from the outset as {\i achot zekukato} to both surviving brothers. No one would have a privileged {\i chalitzah} relationship with either brother. All three women would occupy equal \u8212- and equally weakened \u8212- positions, and the question of ordering would carry no special significance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Changes Sequentia\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once deaths are sequential, however, each act of {\i chalitzah} transforms the status of the remaining women for each of the surviving brothers. This is the key insight underlying Rav\u8217's ruling. When only Rachel has fallen \u8212- because only the first husband has died \u8212- she bears no {\i achot zekukato} disqualification, since no other sister has yet entered the {\i zikah}. Her {\i chalitzah} from Yissachar is complete. The moment that {\i chalitzah} is performed, however, the remaining sisters become {\i achot chalutzato} of Yissachar. They are not yet {\i achot chalutzato} of Zebulun \u8212- that status accrues only when he performs {\i chalitzah} with one of them. Thus the sequential nature of events generates a layered accumulation of statuses that varies between brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rachel Becomes Achot Cha\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the time Sarah requires {\i chalitzah}, she is {\i achot chalutzato} of Yissachar \u8212- because Yissachar performed {\i chalitzah} with Rachel \u8212- and simultaneously {\i achot chalutzato} of Zebulun \u8212- because Zebulun performed {\i chalitzah} with Leah. Any {\i chalitzah} performed with Sarah by either brother is therefore {\i pesulah}. Since both brothers generate only a {\i chalitzah pesulah} with her, the principle of {\i chalitzah pesulah tzarich lachzor al kol ha\u8217'achim} applies, and she must receive {\i chalitzah} from both. This is the complete logic underlying Rav\u8217's ruling: it is the sequential accumulation of {\i achot chalutzato} relationships across both brothers that locks Sarah into needing {\i chalitzah} from each of them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beginning vs.\u160 ?Finishing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzaras Krovat Chalitzato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara introduces an entirely different reread of Shmuel\u8217's ruling \u8212- not based on the graduated strength of {\i chalitzah}, but on a distinct {\i issur}. The Mishnah elsewhere teaches: {\i assur adam bitzaras krovat chalitzato} \u8212- a man is forbidden to the co-wife of the relative of his {\i chalutza}. Rashi explains the case: if Reuven performed {\i chalitzah} with Leah and then a certain Barak \u8212- an entirely unrelated man \u8212- was married both to Leah\u8217's mother Rivkah and to Devorah, Reuven is forbidden to Devorah after Barak\u8217's death, because Devorah is the co-wife ({\i tzara}) of Rivkah, who is a relative of Reuven\u8217's {\i chalutza} Leah ({\i krovat chalitzato}). The reasoning, though complex, establishes that a {\i tzaras krovat chalitzato} is independently prohibited, not merely as a subset of the {\i chalitzah} relationship itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prohibition has direct implications for the case at hand. After Yissachar performs {\i chalitzah} with Leah, Rachel becomes {\i achot chalutzato} of Yissachar \u8212- Yissachar\u8217's {\i krovat chalitzato}. Naomi, who was Reuven\u8217's co-wife alongside Rachel, now stands as {\i tzaras krovat chalitzato} of Yissachar. This is an independent {\i issur}, on equal footing with the {\i issur} of {\i achot chalutzato}, not a derivative one degree removed. Accordingly, both Rachel and Naomi are now problematic for Yissachar in their own independent ways, and performing {\i chalitzah} with one of them cannot release the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Finish with Tzaros\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's ruling \u8212- on this reread \u8212- is not about the gradations of {\i chalitzah} quality at all. Rather, it is about the order in which one begins. {\i Hitchil ba\u8217'achayot, lo yigmor bitzarot} \u8212- if he began with the sisters (performing {\i chalitzah} with Leah first), he should not finish with the co-wives (attempting to use the {\i chalitzah} with Naomi to release Rachel). The reason is the {\i issur} of {\i tzaras krovat chalitzato}: once he has performed {\i chalitzah} with Leah, Naomi is forbidden to him as {\i tzaras krovat chalitzato}. A {\i chalitzah} with Naomi will not release Rachel, because both women are now independently prohibited. Rashi clarifies that {\i lo yigmor bitzarot} does not mean he cannot perform {\i chalitzah} with Naomi at all, but rather that doing so will not exempt Rachel; he must do both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Can Finish with Achayos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i hitchil bitzarot, yigmor af ba\u8217'achayot} \u8212- if he is prudent and begins with Devorah (the co-wife), before any act of {\i chalitzah} with the sisters has been performed, then no {\i achot chalutzato} or {\i tzaras krovat chalitzato} relationship has yet been created. Both Rachel and Naomi remain fully available to him. In this case, a {\i chalitzah} with either woman will release the other, because {\i mutar adam bikrovas tzaras chalitzato} \u8212- a man is permitted to the relative of his {\i chalitzah}-co-wife. This is the mirror image of the earlier prohibition and explicitly not a concern. By beginning with the co-wives before any {\i achot chalutzato} relationship is established, he preserves the full effectiveness of each subsequent {\i chalitzah}. The lesson Shmuel is teaching, on this radical reread, is entirely about sequencing: the order in which one begins determines whether the remaining women can be released by a single subsequent {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zikah Not Strong Enough\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having traversed Shmuel\u8217's radical reread, the Gemara finally offers what it presents as the straightforward explanation, attributed to Rav Ashi: {\i le\u8217'olam ked\u8217'kamris} \u8212- revert to the plain reading of Shmuel. Shmuel indeed holds that performing {\i chalitzah} with the co-wife (Devorah) is preferable to performing it with the sister (Leah), and that {\i chalitzah} with the co-wife releases the sister. The reason is not the elaborate sequencing analysis, but rather a principle about the strength of {\i zikah}: {\i umishum d\u8217'lo alima zika l\u8217'shavyei l\u8217'tzara k\u8217'erva} \u8212- the {\i zikah} is not strong enough to render the co-wife as significant an {\i ervah} as the sister herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Leah is {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- a quasi-{\i ervah} by rabbinic decree, one degree removed from {\i achot ishto}. Devorah is {\i tzaras achot zekukato} \u8212- the co-wife of a quasi-{\i ervah}. Ordinarily, the rule of {\i tzarat ervah} extends the {\i ervah} prohibition to co-wives, but that rule assumes a genuine, {\i d\u8217'oraita} {\i ervah}. Here, even the underlying {\i ervah} \u8212- {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- is only rabbinic. There is already a {\i chiddush} in declaring that {\i achot zekukato} generates any {\i ervah}-like status at all; extending that derivative status one further degree to the co-wife is too attenuated to carry full force. Devorah may therefore not be a {\i sug alef} (grade A) in the absolute sense, but she is a grade B relative to Leah\u8217's grade C. She stands in a fuller, less impaired relationship with the {\i yavam}, and {\i chalitzah} with her is therefore the stronger act \u8212- strong enough to release Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah with Tzara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical application is thus: performing {\i chalitzah} with Devorah releases Leah, because the {\i zikah} between Devorah and the {\i yavam} is sufficiently unimpaired that the {\i chalitzah} carries full releasing force vis-\u224 ?-vis a woman who, though {\i achot zekukato}, is not a true {\i ervah}. The graduated hierarchy \u8212- sisters ({\i achot zekukato}, grade B), co-wives of sisters ({\i tzaras achot zekukato}, grade B-plus relative to the sisters) \u8212- means that acting on the relatively stronger relationship releases the weaker one, but not vice versa.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Permits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba bar Mammel proposes an entirely different approach to Shmuel\u8217's ruling. Rather than resolving the case through the logic of {\i zikah} strength and graduated {\i chalitzah} quality, he suggests: {\i ha mani Beis Shammai hi} \u8212- perhaps Shmuel is operating according to the position of Beit Shammai. The Mishnah records that Beit Shammai permits the co-wives ({\i tzaros}) to marry into the family of the surviving brothers \u8212- that is, Beit Shammai does not recognize the category of {\i tzarat ervah} at all in the context of {\i yibum}. On this view, Devorah is not subject to any prohibition whatsoever, and there is no tension between performing {\i chalitzah} with Devorah and subsequently releasing Leah. The preference for beginning with Devorah is simply that, according to Beit Shammai, there is a full {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} available with Devorah, making her the primary obligee.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Covering All Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises the obvious objection: if Shmuel follows Beit Shammai and Devorah is fully permitted, why not perform {\i yibum} with her rather than {\i chalitzah}? The Gemara responds by citing Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri, who ruled: {\i bo\u8217'u venisaken lahem l\u8217'tzaros sheyehu choltzot v\u8217'lo misyabmot} \u8212- let us enact a {\i takanah} that co-wives must receive {\i chalitzah} and may not undergo {\i yibum}. This ruling is designed to cover both positions: according to Beit Hillel, the co-wife is prohibited and can receive only {\i chalitzah}; according to Beit Shammai, she is permitted but we impose {\i chalitzah} rather than {\i yibum} as a compromise. By requiring {\i chalitzah}, we satisfy Beit Shammai\u8217's concern that the {\i mitzvah} be fulfilled in some form, without permitting the {\i yibum} that Beit Hillel would forbid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, notes a complication: the tradition records that the sages did not complete ({\i lo hispiku ligmor}) the enactment of Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri before the moment passed. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak responds that a later session revisited the matter and the {\i takanah} was ultimately enacted \u8212- {\i achar kach chazru v\u8217'tiknu}. The force of stringency ({\i chumra}) ultimately prevailed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara leaves this as a possible reading of Shmuel, but the explanation of Rav Ashi \u8212- that {\i zikah} is simply not strong enough to render the co-wife a full {\i ervah}, so {\i chalitzah} with the co-wife remains the superior act and releases the sister \u8212- is upheld by a {\i baraita} and stands as the primary resolution. The {\i baraita} confirms: {\i chalatz l\u8217'achayot lo nifteru tzarot, chalatz l\u8217'tzarot nifteru achayot}, and explains the reason as {\i d\u8217'kasavar yesh zikah v\u8217'lo almi zika l\u8217'shavyei l\u8217'tzara k\u8217'erva} \u8212- exactly as Rav Ashi formulated it. This closes the discussion on 26a.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 26b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion opens at the very bottom of 26a, returning to a {\i mishnah} that has appeared several times: four brothers married to four women, two of whom are sisters. Because of the principle of {\i achos zekukaso} \u8212- the sister of one\u8217's {\i zekukah} \u8212- neither surviving brother may perform {\i yibum}, and both women require {\i chalitzah}. The {\i mishnah} thus far has been explained in two distinct ways. The first explanation focuses on {\i achos zekukaso} itself. The second explanation holds that the real concern is {\i assur levatel mitzvas yabmin}: if, for example, Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel, and Yehudah subsequently dies, Leah becomes {\i achos ishto} \u8212- forbidden to both Levi (as his wife\u8217's sister) and now unavailable for {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum} from anyone. To prevent this, Levi is required to perform {\i chalitzah}, so that even in the event of Yehudah\u8217's death, Levi would still be able to perform {\i chalitzah} with Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Four Brothers?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presses a sharp question against the second explanation: {\i i hachi, tlosa nami} \u8212- if the concern is {\i assur levatel mitzvas yabmin}, why does the {\i mishnah} require four brothers? Three would suffice. With only three brothers and two sisters, if Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel, Leah is immediately left without any option \u8212- she becomes {\i achos ishto} to Levi and has no other brother to turn to. The prohibition is obvious even in the three-brother case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Strength vs Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by invoking the principle of {\i lo mibaya ka\u8217'mar}: the three-brother case is the more obvious case and therefore need not be stated. The {\i chidush} of the four-brother case operates at a different level. When two brothers remain behind, there is reason to argue that the concern of {\i achos zekukaso} is attenuated \u8212- the {\i zika} is weaker when two brothers share it, since it is not exclusively Levi\u8217's {\i zika} nor exclusively Yehudah\u8217's. The {\i mishnah} therefore teaches that even with two remaining brothers, the {\i achos zekukaso} prohibition is operative. The alternative concern \u8212- {\i assur levatel mitzvas yabmin} \u8212- introduces a further {\i chidush} in the four-brother case: even though Yehudah is alive and presumably available, we nonetheless fear that he might die before performing {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}, leaving Leah stranded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Death Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: if we fear Yehudah\u8217's death in the four-brother case, why not fear the deaths of both Yehudah and Yissachar in a five-brother case? The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i lemisa detrei lo chashinan} \u8212- we do not entertain the fear that two brothers will die simultaneously before any act of {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} can be performed. That possibility is too remote. We allow for the possibility of one death; two is beyond the scope of our concern. Thus in the four-brother scenario, where Leah depends on Yehudah alone, we do fear his death \u8212- but once two brothers stand between a woman and her potential {\i yibum}-less fate, we no longer worry.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Sisters Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Sisters Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a new case: {\i shalosh achayos yevamos shenaplu lifnei shenei achim yevamim} \u8212- three sisters, all {\i yevamos}, who have fallen before two surviving brothers. To cast this concretely: three brothers \u8212- Reuven, Shimon, and Levi \u8212- each married one of three sisters, and all three have died. The two surviving brothers, Yissachar and Zevulun, now face three sisters \u8212- Rachel, Leah, and a third (call her Sarah) \u8212- all of whom stand in a relationship of {\i zika} to both of them. No woman is a genuine {\i ervah} to either brother, since each was married to a different man. But under the principle of {\i yesh zika}, any act of {\i yibum} would implicate {\i achos zekukaso}, since each woman is a sister of the others to whom the brother is also {\i zakuk}. The case is therefore a larger variant of {\i achos zekukaso}, and the question is how to distribute {\i chalitzah} between the two brothers and three women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why not simply allow Yissachar to perform {\i chalitzah} with all three women? As the {\i Gemara} will develop, the answer turns on a new principle \u8212- that not all {\i chalitzah} acts are of equal weight \u8212- and the distribution matters precisely because of differential strength in the {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distribution Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling, stated in the name of Rav via Rava bar Rav Huna, is as follows: one brother performs {\i chalitzah} with the first sister, the other brother performs {\i chalitzah} with the second sister, and the third sister \u8212- called the {\i emtza\u8217'it}, the \u8220"middle\u8221" one \u8212- requires {\i chalitzah} from both brothers. The term {\i emtza\u8217'it} is somewhat misleading: she is called \u8220"middle\u8221" not because she is the second to fall to {\i yibum}, but because she stands between both brothers in terms of their obligations. She has a claim on each of them and, as will be explained, requires {\i chalitzah} from each.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequential Deaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} refines the scenario. If all three brothers had died simultaneously, every sister would be {\i achos zekukaso} from the outset, and each woman would indeed require two {\i chalitzos}. But the case being discussed is one of sequential deaths \u8212- {\i denafelu zu achar zu}. When the first brother dies, one sister becomes subject to {\i yibum} with no complicating factor: there is no co-zekukah, the {\i zika} is complete, and the {\i chalitzah} performed by, say, Yissachar is fully valid. When the second brother dies, the next sister becomes available. At this point, from Yissachar\u8217's perspective, she is {\i achos chalutzaso} \u8212- the sister of the woman he just performed {\i chalitzah} with \u8212- making his {\i chalitzah} weaker. Zevulun, however, has no such problem: the first {\i chalitzah} was Yissachar\u8217's, not his. Zevulun\u8217's {\i chalitzah} with the second sister is therefore first-rate. By the time the third brother dies and Sarah becomes available, both brothers have each performed {\i chalitzah} with one of her sisters. She is {\i achos chalutzaso} to both Yissachar and to Zevulun. Any {\i chalitzah} she receives will be second-rate from either one of them. And this is precisely why she needs it from both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Pesulah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to a new and central principle that governs the remainder of the {\i daf}: the concept of {\i chalitzah pesulah}. The observation is this \u8212- when the {\i zika} between a brother and his {\i yevamah} is weakened, the {\i chalitzah} performed in that context is correspondingly weakened. A full, first-rate {\i chalitzah} by one brother frees a {\i yevamah} completely; all other brothers are discharged and she may go free. But a {\i chalitzah pesulah} \u8212- a second-rate {\i chalitzah} \u8212- does not accomplish this. It requires, instead, {\i chazor al kol ha\u8217'achim}: the woman must receive {\i chalitzah} from every surviving brother, each of whom must separately discharge his own {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Pesulah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defining Pesulah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i chalitzah pesulah} thus emerges as the organizing concept for the cases that follow. When is a {\i chalitzah} considered {\i pesulah}? Rashi offers a general formulation: {\i chalitzah pesulah} is {\i chalitzah gerua}, a second-rate {\i chalitzah}, in any case where {\i yibum} was not possible \u8212- {\i she\u8217'im ratza leyabbem eino yachol}. If the brother could not have performed {\i yibum} with this woman, then the {\i chalitzah} he performs with her is by definition second-rate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Refinement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos, however, sharpens this definition. Rashi\u8217's criterion is accurate as far as it goes, but it is somewhat overbroad. There are cases where {\i yibum} is impossible for reasons unrelated to any weakening of the {\i zika} \u8212- and Tosafos holds that such cases would not qualify as {\i chalitzah pesulah} in the operative sense. For {\i chalitzah pesulah} to apply, the specific reason {\i yibum} is impossible must be that the {\i zika} itself has been problematized or attenuated. It is not merely the impossibility of {\i yibum} that creates the category, but the particular mechanism: a {\i zika} that has been weakened, distorted, or displaced from its normative form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Examples of Weakening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos identifies three paradigmatic cases in which the {\i zika} is weakened in this operative sense, collected at the end of the relevant {\i dibur hamatchil}. The first is {\i achos zekukaso}: when among all the women to whom a brother is {\i zakuk}, one is the sister of another. She is not a genuine {\i ervah} \u8212- that would make her {\i achos ishto} and would generate full exemption \u8212- but she is a quasi-{\i ervah} through rabbinic extension. The {\i zika} to her is real, but it operates under a cloud, and any {\i chalitzah} performed with her is therefore {\i pesulah}. The second is {\i ba\u8217'alas haget}: if a brother gave the {\i yevamah} a {\i get} before performing {\i chalitzah}, the {\i get} has no Torah-level effect in this context \u8212- the woman is not his wife, and a {\i get} cannot dissolve the {\i zika} \u8212- but rabbinic law grants it enough weight to preclude {\i yibum}. Having now made {\i yibum} impossible, the subsequent {\i chalitzah} is second-rate. The third case is the inverse and is in some ways more striking: {\i ba\u8217'alas ma\u8217'amar}. Here the brother performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- a rabbinic-level {\i kiddushin} with the {\i yevamah}, a formal act of partial acquisition that is the precursor to full {\i yibum} \u8212- thereby strengthening rather than weakening the apparent connection. Yet this too renders {\i chalitzah} second-rate. The dynamic is instructive: {\i chalitzah} is relevant to the middle zone of the {\i zika} relationship. Move the woman too far away \u8212- as with {\i achos zekukaso} or {\i ba\u8217'alas haget} \u8212- and {\i chalitzah} is less relevant because she is quasi-forbidden. Move her too close \u8212- as with {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- and {\i chalitzah} is less relevant because she is quasi-his-wife. In either direction, the displacement from the normative {\i zika} weakens the {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that when {\i ma\u8217'amar} has been performed, the full procedure requires both a {\i get} (to undo the {\i ma\u8217'amar}) and {\i chalitzah} (to undo the underlying {\i zika}). But the {\i chalitzah} in that context is still regarded as second-rate because the woman\u8217's status has shifted from the classic {\i yevamah} who stands ready for {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} without prior complication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Chalitzos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot of {\i chalitzah pesulah} is the rule of {\i chazor al kol ha\u8217'achim}: she must go around to all the brothers. Why? Because when a {\i chalitzah} is first-rate and complete, one brother\u8217's act fully extinguishes the entire nexus of {\i zika} \u8212- all brothers are discharged simultaneously, since the {\i zika} was a unified bond. But when the {\i chalitzah} is second-rate, it is only sufficient to remove the particular {\i zika} of the one brother who performed it. Each brother retains his own residual {\i zika}, and each must therefore act separately to discharge it. This explains the case of Sarah in the three-sisters scenario: since she is {\i achos chalutzaso} to both Yissachar and Zevulun, any {\i chalitzah} she receives is {\i pesulah}, and she must therefore obtain {\i chalitzah} from each of them individually.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises an immediate difficulty: this entire analysis \u8212- {\i achos zekukaso}, {\i chalitzah pesulah}, {\i chazor al kol ha\u8217'achim} \u8212- is predicated on the position of {\i yesh zika}. But the ruling in question was stated by Rav, and Rav is on record elsewhere as holding {\i ein zika}. The {\i Gemara} resolves this: Rav was speaking {\i ledivrei ha\u8217'omer yesh zika} \u8212- he was articulating the {\i halacha} according to the position that holds {\i yesh zika}, not necessarily expressing his own view. Within that framework, the ruling stands: {\i chalitzah pesulah} requires {\i chalitzah} from all the brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees. His position is stated concisely: {\i echad choletz lekullan} \u8212- one brother may perform {\i chalitzah} for all of them. Shmuel apparently does not accept the {\i chazor al kol ha\u8217'achim} requirement. Even where a {\i chalitzah} would be second-rate \u8212- because the woman is {\i achos chalutzaso} \u8212- Shmuel maintains that one brother\u8217's {\i chalitzah} suffices, and the woman need not seek {\i chalitzah} from the remaining brothers.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs Shmuel (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achos vs Tzaros\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presses Shmuel\u8217's position from a different direction. We know from elsewhere on 26a that Shmuel himself holds {\i chalitzah ma\u8217'alya ba\u8217'inan} \u8212- that a first-rate {\i chalitzah} is required. Given this, there is a question about Shmuel\u8217's permitting one brother to perform {\i chalitzah} with all three sisters: even setting aside the question of {\i how many} brothers must participate, shouldn\u8217't we at least insist that the {\i better} {\i chalitzah} be used when available?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To see why this matters, consider the following case \u8212- stated by Shmuel on 26a \u8212- involving two sets of women. Yissachar is married to Rachel, and Rachel has a co-wife, Devorah. Zevulun is married to Leah, and Leah has a co-wife, Naomi. Both brothers die. Reuven is the sole surviving brother. All four women stand before him. He cannot perform {\i yibum} with Rachel, because Rachel is {\i achos zekukaso} \u8212- she is the sister of Leah, to whom he is also {\i zakuk}. He cannot perform {\i yibum} with Leah for the same reason. What about Devorah? Devorah is a co-wife of Rachel, who is herself a quasi-{\i ervah} by virtue of {\i achos zekukaso}. Devorah is therefore, in the language of the {\i Gemara}, {\i tzaras achos zekukaso} \u8212- a co-wife of a quasi-{\i ervah}. This is one degree removed from the problem, but Reuven still cannot perform {\i yibum} with her. Nevertheless, Reuven\u8217's relationship with Devorah is of a different character than his relationship with Rachel: his connection with Devorah is less problematized, and therefore a {\i chalitzah} with Devorah is stronger than a {\i chalitzah} with Rachel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's ruling: {\i chalatz l\u8217'achayos lo nifteru tzaros} \u8212- if Reuven performs {\i chalitzah} with the sisters (Rachel or Leah), the co-wives (Devorah, Naomi) are not freed. But {\i chalatz letzaros nipteru achayos} \u8212- if he performs {\i chalitzah} with the co-wives, the sisters are freed. This demonstrates that Shmuel does recognize a hierarchy of {\i chalitzah} quality, and that the stronger {\i chalitzah} \u8212- with the co-wife \u8212- is more powerful than the weaker one. The same principle applies to {\i ba\u8217'alas haget} and {\i ba\u8217'alas ma\u8217'amar}: in each case, {\i chalitz leba\u8217'alas haget} (or {\i ma\u8217'amar}) does not free her co-wife, but {\i chalatz letzarah} frees the {\i ba\u8217'alas haget} (or {\i ma\u8217'amar}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this, the {\i Gemara} objects: if Shmuel holds that a first-rate {\i chalitzah} is always preferable \u8212- indeed, necessary to free a co-wife \u8212- why does he permit one brother to perform all three {\i chalitzos} in the three-sisters case, including the weaker ones with the sisters who are {\i achos chalutzaso}? When Shimon\u8217's {\i chalitzah} with the second sister would be fully first-rate, why allow Reuven to do a second-rate {\i chalitzah} instead?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Co-wives Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-wives Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To clarify the stakes of this debate, the {\i Gemara} works through the case involving co-wives directly. Here the scenario is as described above: two brothers married to two pairs of women (each wife having a co-wife), with only one brother surviving. All four women are now before Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesulah Doesn\u8217't Free\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's ruling establishes a clear principle: a second-rate {\i chalitzah} \u8212- one performed in a context where the {\i zika} is weakened \u8212- lacks the force to free a co-wife. If Reuven performs {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, who is {\i achos zekukaso}, Devorah remains bound. The {\i chalitzah pesulah} is effective for Rachel herself \u8212- she is freed from the obligation \u8212- but it cannot discharge the obligation of a co-wife, because the co-wife\u8217's release depends on the {\i chalitzah} being fully valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First-Rate Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inverse, however, holds: a first-rate {\i chalitzah} with Devorah \u8212- whose relationship to Reuven, while still not permitting {\i yibum}, is less burdened \u8212- does free Rachel. The logic is that Reuven\u8217's {\i zika} to Devorah, although precluded from {\i yibum} by virtue of her being {\i tzaras achos zekukaso}, is not as deeply problematized as his {\i zika} to Rachel. It is therefore a stronger act of {\i chalitzah}, and a stronger {\i chalitzah} has the power to discharge co-wives as well. This establishes that when co-wives are present, a first-rate {\i chalitzah} is not merely preferable but necessary to achieve their release.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Pesulah (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strength Weakens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a striking irony embedded in the case of {\i ba\u8217'alas ma\u8217'amar} that is worth pausing on. When a brother performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with his {\i yevamah}, he is strengthening the {\i zika} \u8212- he is moving closer to full {\i yibum}, not away from it. Yet this very strengthening renders the subsequent {\i chalitzah} weaker. The explanation, as noted above, is that {\i chalitzah} is calibrated to the specific relational zone of the {\i zika}: the woman stands in a liminal status, available for {\i yibum} but not yet his wife, available for {\i chalitzah} but not an {\i ervah}. Any movement from that liminal zone \u8212- whether toward greater distance (as in {\i achos zekukaso} or {\i ba\u8217'alas haget}) or toward greater closeness (as in {\i ma\u8217'amar}) \u8212- displaces the act of {\i chalitzah} from its normative context and weakens it. The {\i chalitzah} in a {\i ma\u8217'amar} case is therefore {\i pesulah}, even though the {\i zika} was, in one sense, intensified. The result is that both weakening and strengthening the {\i zika} produce the same outcome: a {\i chalitzah pesulah} that requires {\i chazor al kol ha\u8217'achim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel Reread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the case of Reuven and the four women \u8212- Rachel, Leah, Devorah, and Naomi \u8212- the {\i Gemara} presses a deeper question. The {\i Gemara} had accepted that performing {\i chalitzah} with Devorah is preferable to doing so with Rachel, because Devorah is {\i tzaras achos zekukaso} (one degree removed) rather than {\i achos zekukaso} directly. But the {\i Gemara} now asks: if one holds {\i yesh zika}, doesn\u8217't this make Devorah {\i tzaras} a quasi-{\i ervah}? And if so, isn\u8217't she just as problematic in her own way as Rachel? Why is the {\i chalitzah} with Devorah considered stronger?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This challenge threatens the entire framework of Shmuel\u8217's ruling. If both Rachel and Devorah are problematic \u8212- one directly as {\i achos zekukaso} and the other as {\i tzaras achos zekukaso} \u8212- on what basis do we say that the {\i chalitzah} with Devorah is first-rate and gets Rachel off the hook, while the {\i chalitzah} with Rachel is second-rate and does not free Devorah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially responds by suggesting that Shmuel holds {\i ein zika}. But this immediately generates a new difficulty: if there is no {\i zika}, then Rachel is not {\i achos zekukaso} at all, so why can\u8217't Reuven simply perform {\i yibum} with her? And if all four women stand on equal footing before him, what is the basis for distinguishing the {\i chalitzah} performed with Rachel from the {\i chalitzah} performed with Devorah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beginning vs Ending\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore undertakes a radical re-reading of Shmuel\u8217's ruling. Rather than comparing Rachel and Devorah as two fixed alternatives and asking which is better, the {\i Gemara} proposes that Shmuel is addressing a sequential question: {\i hitchil velo hitchil} \u8212- the question of how one begins the process and how one ends it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Begin Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Reuven begins with the sisters \u8212- that is, he performs {\i chalitzah} first with Leah (one of the sisters) \u8212- then Leah becomes his {\i chalutzah}. Rachel is now {\i achos chalutzaso}. At this point, Naomi (Leah\u8217's co-wife) is {\i tzaras krovat chalutzaso} \u8212- the co-wife of a relative of his {\i chalutzah}. The {\i mishnah} teaches that a man is {\i assur} in {\i tzaras krovat chalutzaso}. Consequently, if Reuven started with Leah, he may not finish with Naomi, because doing so would implicate {\i tzaras krovat chalutzaso}. Doing {\i chalitzah} with Naomi would not free Rachel either, because she is independently problematic. He is therefore stuck and must still do {\i chalitzah} with Rachel \u8212- but that {\i chalitzah} will not get Naomi off the hook.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Begin Co-wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if Reuven begins with the co-wives \u8212- performing {\i chalitzah} first with Devorah \u8212- then the situation is entirely different. Devorah is now his {\i chalutzah}, and Rachel is not {\i achos chalutzaso} because no {\i chalitzah} has yet been performed with any sister. At this stage, the {\i mishnah} teaches {\i mutar adam bekrovat tzaras chalitzaso} \u8212- a man is permitted to the relative of his {\i chalutzah}\u8217's co-wife. This is the mirror image of the previous case and is not considered problematic. With Devorah as his {\i chalutzah}, he may proceed with either Rachel or Naomi. If he does {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, it will free Naomi; if he does it with Naomi, it will free Rachel. By beginning with the co-wife, both options remain open, and first-rate {\i chalitzah} is available for each.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel Reread (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} ultimately abandons the radical re-reading and returns to the simpler original interpretation of Shmuel, through the intervention of Rav Ashi.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Insufficient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi states: {\i leolam kidekamris} \u8212- Shmuel means exactly what we originally thought he meant. The {\i chalitzah} with Devorah is better than the {\i chalitzah} with Rachel, even within the framework of {\i yesh zika}. The key principle is: {\i umishum delo alima zika lishveiye litzarah ke\u8217'erva} \u8212- the {\i zika} is not strong enough to render the co-wife fully equivalent to an {\i ervah}. The reasoning proceeds as follows: Rachel, through {\i achos zekukaso}, is a quasi-{\i ervah} \u8212- a rabbinic extension of the prohibition of {\i achos isha}. She is not a genuine Torah-level {\i ervah}, but she is treated as one rabbinically. Normally, in a case of full {\i ervah}, the principle of {\i tzaras ervah} would apply \u8212- the co-wife of an {\i ervah} is also exempt from {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}. But in this case, since Rachel herself is only a quasi-{\i ervah} through rabbinic extension, the derivative principle \u8212- that her co-wife Devorah is {\i tzaras ervah} \u8212- does not carry the same weight. The {\i zika} generates the {\i achos zekukaso} prohibition, but it is not strong enough to then activate, in full, the {\i tzaras ervah} mechanism for Devorah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-wife Better\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence is that Devorah, although she cannot be taken in {\i yibum}, stands in a better position than Rachel for purposes of {\i chalitzah}. Rachel\u8217's {\i zika} is directly burdened by {\i achos zekukaso}, making her {\i chalitzah} second-rate. Devorah\u8217's {\i zika}, while indirectly affected, is not burdened to the same degree \u8212- because the chain of derivation (from {\i achos zekukaso} to {\i tzaras achos zekukaso}) attenuates the problem. She is, in the terms introduced earlier, perhaps a grade B rather than grade A, while Rachel is grade C. The {\i chalitzah} with Devorah is therefore relatively first-rate \u8212- stronger than the {\i chalitzah} with Rachel \u8212- and suffices to free Rachel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Alignment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} confirms that this understanding is in line with a {\i baraita}: {\i tanya kevasei deRav Ashi} \u8212- it was taught in accordance with Rav Ashi. The {\i baraita} states: {\i chalatz l\u8217'achayos lo nifteru tzaros, chalatz letzaros nifteru achayos} \u8212- performing {\i chalitzah} with the sisters does not free the co-wives, but performing {\i chalitzah} with the co-wives does free the sisters. And the reason given is precisely: {\i mishum dekasavar yesh zika velo alumi zika lishveiye litzara ke\u8217'erva} \u8212- because the {\i zika} exists but is insufficient to make the co-wife a full {\i ervah}. This is exactly what Rav Ashi said. The {\i baraita} resolves the debate in Rav Ashi\u8217's favor.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beis Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba bar Mamul offers an alternative account of why performing {\i chalitzah} with the co-wife is preferable. Rather than invoking the subtlety of {\i zika} strength, he proposes that Shmuel\u8217's ruling reflects the position of Beis Shammai. The {\i mishnah} elsewhere records: {\i Beis Shammai matirin hatzaros leachim} \u8212- Beis Shammai permit the co-wives to the brothers. According to Beis Shammai, the principle of {\i tzaras ervah} does not apply at all. Co-wives of an {\i ervah} are not exempt from {\i yibum}; they are fully obligated. On this view, Devorah is not problematic in any sense \u8212- she is simply another {\i yevamah} who may be taken in {\i yibum}. There is therefore no question of first-rate versus second-rate {\i chalitzah} with her; she stands in a fully normal {\i chalitzah} relationship with Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately objects: if Beis Shammai permits {\i yibum} with co-wives, and Shmuel is following Beis Shammai, then why is Shmuel requiring {\i chalitzah} with Devorah at all? Let Reuven perform {\i yibum} with her outright.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Compromise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds by invoking the position of Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri, who proposed: {\i bo\u8217'u unetaken lahem latzaros sheyihyu choltzos velo mityabmos} \u8212- let us enact for the co-wives that they perform {\i chalitzah} and not {\i yibum}. This ruling is a compromise designed to honor both Beis Shammai and Beis Hillel. Beis Hillel holds that co-wives of an {\i ervah} are exempt even from {\i chalitzah}; Beis Shammai holds that they are fully obligated and may even perform {\i yibum}. The enactment of Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri requires {\i chalitzah} but forbids {\i yibum} \u8212- satisfying the concern of Beis Hillel (who would object to {\i yibum}) while acknowledging the Beis Shammai position that some obligation exists. The {\i Gemara} notes that the full ratification of this ruling was not completed in its time \u8212- {\i lo hispiku ligmor et hadavar} \u8212- but Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak replies that it was subsequently enacted. The position of Rabbi Abba bar Mamul is thus that Shmuel\u8217's ruling reflects this compromise: operating within a Beis Shammai framework modified by the later enactment requiring {\i chalitzah} rather than {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 28\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issur Mitzvah/Ervah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the very last line of 28a, with the conclusion of the {\i mishna} dealing with four brothers, two of whom died having been married to two sisters. The case under discussion is one in which the surviving brothers face the problem of {\i achos zekukaso} \u8212- the sister of a {\i zekuka}, a quasi-wife. Ordinarily, because each surviving brother has a {\i zika} (a quasi-marital bond) to each of the deceased brothers\u8217' widows, and those widows are sisters to one another, neither brother can perform {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}, since each woman is the sister of the other\u8217's {\i zekuka}. The {\i mishna} teaches, however, that if one of the women is an {\i ervah} to one of the brothers \u8212- forbidden to him by a biblical incest prohibition \u8212- that {\i zika} is dissolved, and the other brother may then perform {\i yibum} with the remaining woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The canonical illustration: if Rachel is Levi\u8217's daughter, there is no {\i zika} between Levi and Rachel \u8212- she is an {\i ervah} to him outright \u8212- and therefore Leah is not Levi\u8217's {\i achos zekukaso}. He may perform {\i yibum} with Leah. One might wonder how Rachel and Leah can be sisters if one is Levi\u8217's daughter and the other is not. The answer, as the {\i gemara} will clarify, is that they share the same mother but have different fathers \u8212- Rachel\u8217's father being Levi himself, and Leah\u8217's father being another man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Mitzvah vs.\u160 ?Ervah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 28b opens by noting that we have already been taught the case of {\i issur mitzvah} and {\i issur kedushah} in a simpler context: {\i issur mitzvah choletzet v\u8217'lo misyabbemet} \u8212- a woman who is forbidden only by rabbinic prohibition, or by a {\i lav} such as the prohibition against a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i grushah}, does {\i chalitza} and does not do {\i yibum}. That earlier teaching addressed the straightforward scenario of a single deceased brother; the present teaching introduces the more complex hybrid case where two sisters are {\i zekukos} to two surviving brothers, and one of those sisters is an {\i issur mitzvah} \u8212- a rabbinic-level prohibition \u8212- to one of the brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hava Amina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a compelling {\i hava amina}: since the entire problem of {\i achos zekukaso} is itself only a {\i derabbanan} prohibition \u8212- Rachel was never fully his wife, only a {\i zekuka}, a quasi-wife \u8212- perhaps if Rachel is an {\i issur derabbanan} to Levi, that should be sufficient to remove her from the picture. After all, we are operating entirely within the rabbinic domain: the {\i zika} is a rabbinic construct, and Rachel\u8217's forbidden status is also rabbinic. Let one {\i derabbanan} solve another {\i derabbanan} problem, and allow Levi to perform {\i yibum} with Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejection of Hava Amina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i keivan demide\u8217'oraita ramya kamei} \u8212- since, on the biblical level, Levi still has a {\i zika} to Rachel, she remains his {\i zekuka de\u8217'oraita}, and Leah is therefore still the sister of his {\i zekuka} even if the {\i zika} is only actualized rabbinically. Rachel does not disappear from the scene merely because she is an {\i issur derabbanan} to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishum Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further suggest: since the entire problem is rabbinic, and the {\i rabbanan} instituted {\i achos zekukaso} in service of the {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum}, perhaps they would relax it in order to facilitate {\i yibum} in a case where the woman is already an {\i issur derabbanan}. The {\i gemara} dismisses this as well \u8212- {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- we are taught that the {\i rabbanan} would not override the {\i zika} problem in this fashion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Variations & Case Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pedagogical Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks why the {\i mishna} needed to state both the case where one of the two women is an {\i ervah} and the case where both women are each an {\i ervah} to the respective other brother. Is not the second case simply a doubled version of the first? {\i Hainu hach} \u8212- is it not the same point? The {\i gemara} answers that both cases are pedagogically necessary, and proceeds to explain the distinctive logical function each one serves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Ervah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i mishna} had taught only the case where one woman is an {\i ervah} \u8212- say, Rachel is Levi\u8217's daughter, and Levi therefore performs {\i yibum} with Leah \u8212- one might fail to recognize that this is only permitted because of the special circumstance of {\i ervah}. Without the second case to illuminate the point, an observer might conclude that in any such configuration, the surviving brother is simply permitted to perform {\i yibum} with the other woman, regardless of whether an {\i ervah} relation is actually present. The singular case, standing alone, might be mistakenly read as establishing a general rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Ervos Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of two {\i ervos}, where each woman is an {\i ervah} to the respective other brother, allows each brother to perform {\i yibum} with the woman who is not his {\i ervah}. The presence of the second brother here is itself probative: because Yehuda performs {\i yibum} with Rachel and Levi performs {\i yibum} with Leah \u8212- each with a different woman \u8212- the mutual arrangement makes evident that something special is operative. This cross-pattern demonstrates that only because of the {\i ervah} relationship is each brother permitted to act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheni\u8217's Evidentiary Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the principle of {\i sheni demuchach} \u8212- the second one proves. In the case of two {\i ervos}, the second brother\u8217's restricted behavior demonstrates that a special circumstance accounts for why the first brother is permitted. Each brother\u8217's situation illuminates the other\u8217's. Conversely, if the {\i mishna} had taught only the two-{\i ervah} case, one might think the reason both brothers can perform {\i yibum} is self-evident from the symmetry alone, without realizing that each {\i yibum} is contingent on the {\i ervah} relation operating in that particular direction. The case of one {\i ervah} is then needed to establish that the permission is not structural but derives from the specific {\i ervah} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis extends further: the {\i gemara} asks about the final line of the {\i mishna} \u8212- {\i zu hi she\u8217'amru}, \u8220"this is what they said\u8221" \u8212- and concludes that it comes to exclude the case where both women are an {\i issur mitzvah} to the respective brothers, rather than a true {\i ervah}. One might have thought that if each brother has a {\i derabbanan ervah} to one woman, and each can therefore perform {\i yibum} with the other, the symmetry of the situation should allow it \u8212- since neither brother is being disadvantaged and there is no concern about the {\i sheni}. The {\i mishna} teaches that even in this symmetric case, the {\i issur derabbanan} does not suffice to overcome the {\i zika}, and {\i yibum} remains forbidden.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fifteen Arayos Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Arayos Apply\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a broader principle, one that may be recalled from early in the {\i masechet}. A {\i braita} teaches in the name of Rav Yehuda citing Rav, and likewise taught by Rabbi Hiyya: {\i v\u8217'chulan ani korei bahen ha\u8217'asurah lazeh muteret lazeh v\u8217'achosah k\u8217'shehi yevimtah o choletzes o misyabbemet} \u8212- in all of the fifteen forbidden relationships enumerated in the opening {\i mishna} of the {\i masechet}, one can construct the scenario in which the forbidden woman is forbidden to one brother but permitted to the other, and her sister who is simultaneously a sister-in-law may either do {\i chalitza} or {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The puzzle being posed is essentially a combinatorial one: for each of the fifteen {\i arayos}, can one construct a configuration in which two sisters are each married to two brothers, such that one sister is an {\i ervah} to one brother but not the other, while the other sister is an {\i ervah} to the second brother but not the first? The claim is that this can be worked out for all fifteen cases. A simple illustration: if Leah is the mother-in-law of Yehuda (i.e., the mother of Yehuda\u8217's wife), she is an {\i ervah} to Yehuda but has no relationship to Levi. Similarly, Rachel is Levi\u8217's mother-in-law but not Yehuda\u8217's. Since Rachel and Leah are sisters through their father, the entire configuration is achieved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Excludes Six\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rav Yehuda himself restricts the application of this principle. He will only apply it beginning from the case of {\i chamot} \u8212- mother-in-law \u8212- and onward through the list. The first six cases of the {\i mishna} \u8212- which all derive from the category of {\i bat}, daughter \u8212- he excludes: {\i aval sheis bavi d\u8217'reisha lo}. He does not wish to teach that these six cases can be applied to the scenario of {\i achos zekukaso}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter Cases Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with the daughter cases is as follows. For Rachel to be Levi\u8217's daughter but not Yehuda\u8217's daughter, while Rachel and Leah are sisters, they must share the same mother. But if they share the same mother, and Levi fathered Rachel through that woman, then Leah \u8212- as the daughter of the same woman \u8212- would be Levi\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter, which is itself a forbidden relationship. The only way to avoid this is if Levi was never married to the mother. In other words, the case of daughter within this framework can only be constructed if the daughter was born out of wedlock. The {\i gemara} formulates this as: {\i keivan de\u8217'vita ba\u8217'onsin hu d\u8217'mishkachat lah, benisuin lo mishkachat lah} \u8212- the case of daughter is only workable when the child was born through an illicit union, not through marriage. Rav Yehuda, who prefers to speak only of cases that arise through legitimate marriage, excludes all six daughter-derived categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abayei Includes Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye disagrees and extends the principle to all fifteen cases, including the daughter categories. His reasoning is pragmatic: {\i keivan de\u8217'ashkachei mishkachat lah, ei ba\u8217'onsin teihavei ei benisuin teihavei} \u8212- since the scenario is achievable, whether through an out-of-wedlock birth or otherwise, it is legitimate to include it. Abaye is willing to speak even about cases that require positing an illicit origin for the daughter, provided the legal structure works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eishet Achiv Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, carves out one exception of his own: the case of {\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} \u8212- the wife of a brother who was not yet born when his brother was alive and married. Abaye says this case cannot be applied to the {\i achos zekukaso} scenario, because the only way to construct such a configuration is according to the position of Rabbi Shimon, and not according to the {\i rabbanan}. Since this is a matter of {\i machloket}, Abaye does not wish to build the scenario on a disputed foundation: {\i biflugeta lo ka\u8217'mira} \u8212- I do not speak of a case that is subject to dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meis Nolad Yibem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records that Rav Safra disagrees with Abaye on this last point, insisting that the case of {\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} can indeed be applied to the {\i achos zekukaso} scenario \u8212- but only according to Rabbi Shimon, and it requires six brothers. The mnemonic given is {\i meis, nolad, v\u8217'yibem; meis, nolad, v\u8217'yibem} \u8212- died, born, did {\i yibum}; died, born, did {\i yibum}. The {\i gemara} then elaborates the case fully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin with four brothers: Reuven and Shimon, who are married to two sisters \u8212- Rachel and Leah respectively. A second pair of brothers, Levi and Yehuda, are married to two unrelated women, Devorah and Channah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stage 1: Reuven Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first stage, Reuven dies. In the second stage, a new brother, Yissachar, is born. Because Yissachar was born after Reuven had already died, Rachel \u8212- Reuven\u8217's widow \u8212- is an {\i ervah} to Yissachar as {\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}: the wife of a brother who was never alive at the same time as him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stage 2: Levi Yibums\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the third stage, Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel. He was already married to Devorah; now he takes Rachel as well through {\i yibum}. Rachel remains an {\i ervah} to Yissachar even after Levi\u8217's {\i yibum}, since the {\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} prohibition is rooted in the relationship between Rachel and Reuven, not in Rachel\u8217's current marital status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stages 3-4: Shimon Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the fourth stage, Shimon dies. In the fifth stage, another new brother, Zevulun, is born. By the time Zevulun is born, Rachel is already married to Levi \u8212- she is not available as a {\i zekuka} to Zevulun, and therefore she is not an {\i ervah} to him. Leah, however, was Shimon\u8217's wife, and since Zevulun was born after Shimon died, Leah is {\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} to Zevulun. Leah, by contrast, is not an {\i ervah} to Yissachar: Shimon and Yissachar were contemporaries, so there is a standard {\i zika} relationship \u8212- a {\i mitzva} obligation \u8212- not an {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stage 5: Yehuda Yibums\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the sixth stage, Yehuda performs {\i yibum} with Leah. Now, when Levi and Yehuda die childless, their widows \u8212- Rachel and Leah \u8212- fall before Yissachar and Zevulun for {\i yibum}. The result is the desired cross-pattern: Rachel is an {\i ervah} to Yissachar ({\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}) but permitted to Zevulun; Leah is an {\i ervah} to Zevulun ({\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo}) but permitted to Yissachar. Thus, {\i asurah lazeh muteret lazeh v\u8217'asurah lazeh muteret lazeh} \u8212- each woman is forbidden to one brother and permitted to the other. The full structure of the {\i achos zekukaso} ruling with {\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} is thereby demonstrated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This construction, as was anticipated, works only according to Rabbi Shimon. The {\i rabbanan} would hold that even though Rachel was already married to Levi by the time Zevulun was born, Rachel\u8217's status as {\i eishet achiv shelo hayah be\u8217'olamo} vis-\u224 ?-vis Reuven remains intact, and therefore she would be an {\i ervah} to Zevulun as well. Rabbi Shimon, by contrast, holds that once Rachel entered a fully consummated {\i yibum} with Levi \u8212- who was alive when Reuven was alive \u8212- her status with respect to Zevulun is redefined by her current marriage, not by her original connection to Reuven. Hence the scenario only functions according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why Yehuda\u8217's {\i yibum} with Leah was necessary to the construction, since it seems one could arrive at the same result without him. The answer is that Yehuda\u8217's presence is needed to generate the case of {\i tzarat ervah} \u8212- the co-wife of an {\i ervah} \u8212- as well. Without Yehuda in the picture, both women would be in an {\i ervah} relationship with the same brother, and there would be no way to introduce the co-wife scenario. The {\i gemara} further notes that with the addition of two more brothers, Gad and Asher, one can extend the case to include {\i tzarat tzarah} \u8212- the co-wife of a co-wife of an {\i ervah} \u8212- as well, though the details are left to the reader.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Brothers Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i mishna} opens: {\i sheloshah achim, shenayim meihem nesuim shtei achayos} \u8212- three brothers, two of them married to two sisters. The case is structurally similar to the four-brother case discussed previously, now simplified to three brothers. The third brother is {\i mufneh} \u8212- unattached, single.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Arayos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} extends the ruling to apply not only to cases where the two women are sisters but also to any analogous {\i ervah} relationship between them: {\i o ishah u-vittah, o ishah u-vas bittah, o ishah u-vas binnah} \u8212- or a woman and her daughter, or a woman and her granddaughter (through the daughter or through the son). Any pair of women who could not both be married to the same man may stand in this configuration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensus Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The baseline ruling of the {\i mishna} \u8212- consistent with what we have established \u8212- is that when such a configuration exists, {\i harei eilu choltzos v\u8217'lo misyabbemet}: these women do {\i chalitza} and do not do {\i yibum}. Since neither surviving brother can perform {\i yibum} with either woman without encountering the problem of {\i zika} to the other, they must each do {\i chalitza}. This is the standard position of the {\i rabbanan}, grounded in the rabbinic prohibition of {\i achos zekukaso}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon, however, takes a strikingly different position: {\i Rabbi Shimon poter} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon exempts entirely. In his view, both women are completely exempt from both {\i chalitza} and {\i yibum}. They simply walk away. This is a remarkable ruling, standing in sharp contrast to the majority position. Where the {\i rabbanan} treat {\i achos zekukaso} as a rabbinic prohibition requiring {\i chalitza} as a safeguard, Rabbi Shimon regards the entire configuration as generating a {\i de\u8217'oraita} exemption from the {\i yibum} obligation altogether. The {\i gemara} will proceed to examine his reasoning.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prooftext Citation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites the {\i braita}: Rabbi Shimon derives his position from the verse {\i v\u8217'ishah el achota lo tikach litzror} \u8212- \u8220"you shall not take a woman alongside her sister to create rivalry between them.\u8221" This verse, which is the source of the prohibition against marrying two sisters simultaneously, is applied by Rabbi Shimon to the {\i yibum} context as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarot Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon reasons: {\i besha\u8217'ah shena\u8217'asu tzarot zu l\u8217'zu} \u8212- at the moment when these two women become {\i tzarot} to one another, co-sisters-in-law, each bound by the {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum} to different brothers who have now both died, they are as though they are co-wives competing for the same household. In such circumstances, {\i lo yihyeh lachem afilu b\u8217'achat meihen} \u8212- there is no {\i mitzva} relationship with even one of them. The prohibition of taking two sisters simultaneously extends, for Rabbi Shimon, to the {\i yibum} context, where the {\i zika} of each brother to each woman creates a functional co-wife relationship between the two sisters. Therefore, rather than a mere rabbinic enactment, Rabbi Shimon holds that there is a {\i de\u8217'oraita} exemption: the {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum} simply does not apply, and consequently neither does the obligation of {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 De\u8217'Oraita Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a critical theological distinction. For the {\i rabbanan}, {\i achos zekukaso} is a {\i derabbanan} problem: biblically, one might argue that {\i yibum} should be performed with one of the women, and the rabbinic enactment merely prevents it for fear of complication. The {\i rabbanan} therefore require {\i chalitza} to discharge the residual {\i zika}. For Rabbi Shimon, the exemption is {\i de\u8217'oraita}: the {\i yibum} obligation does not arise at all in this configuration, and without a {\i mitzva} of {\i yibum}, there is no {\i yibum} obligation to discharge and therefore no need for {\i chalitza} either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hava Amina Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given the severity of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position \u8212- that this is a {\i de\u8217'oraita} exemption, and that performing {\i yibum} in such a case would constitute an actual {\i issur} of {\i eishet achiv} \u8212- one might have thought that even in the case where one of the women is a genuine {\i ervah} to one of the brothers, Rabbi Shimon would prohibit {\i yibum} with the other woman as well. The concern would be a {\i gezeirah}: if we allow {\i yibum} in this case, people might come to permit {\i yibum} in the ordinary case of {\i achos zekukaso}, which for Rabbi Shimon is a {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejection Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this {\i hava amina}: {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- we are taught that even Rabbi Shimon agrees that when one woman is a genuine {\i ervah} to one brother, the other brother may perform {\i yibum} with the remaining woman. There is no {\i gezeirah} in that case. When one woman is an {\i ervah} and is entirely removed from the picture, the remaining woman is simply a standard {\i yevama} to the other brother, and there is no circumstance that could generate a misunderstanding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issur Mitzvah Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Perception Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: when one of the women is an {\i issur mitzvah} \u8212- a rabbinic-level prohibition, or a {\i lav} such as the prohibition against a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i grushah} \u8212- {\i choltzos v\u8217'lo misyabbemet} \u8212- both women do {\i chalitza} and neither does {\i yibum}. This ruling applies even according to Rabbi Shimon. The {\i gemara} notes that this seems surprising: in a plain case of two sisters with no other {\i issur} present, Rabbi Shimon says they walk away without {\i chalitza} at all. Why, when one of them is an {\i issur mitzvah}, should Rabbi Shimon require {\i chalitza}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grusha/Kohen Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: it is a {\i gezeirah} on account of {\i issur mitzvah d\u8217'alma} \u8212- a protective measure for the general category of {\i issur mitzvah} cases. Consider the following scenario: one of the women happens to be a {\i grushah} who was married to a {\i kohen}. Now, if both women are simply allowed to walk away without {\i chalitza} \u8212- as Rabbi Shimon would normally permit in a case of two sisters \u8212- observers will not focus on the sisters-and-brothers dimension of the case. They will focus on the specific fact that was salient to them: the woman was a {\i grushah} and the surviving brother was a {\i kohen}. They will conclude: \u8220"Apparently, when a woman is forbidden as an {\i issur mitzvah} to a {\i kohen}, no {\i chalitza} is required.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Get Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This would be a catastrophically wrong inference, since the {\i halacha} requires {\i chalitza} whenever a woman is an {\i issur mitzvah} to the {\i yavam} in a standard {\i yibum} situation. To prevent this misunderstanding, the {\i rabbanan} require {\i chalitza} from both women even in the case of {\i achos zekukaso} where one is an {\i issur mitzvah} \u8212- specifically so that no one will infer that {\i issur mitzvah} cases are generally exempt from {\i chalitza}. The woman who is the {\i issur mitzvah} must do {\i chalitza} to remove the fear of a wrong public inference. And since we are requiring {\i chalitza} from her, the {\i gezeirah} extends to require {\i chalitza} from her sister as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ervah Clarity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: in the case of a real {\i ervah} \u8212- say, the woman was Levi\u8217's daughter \u8212- Rabbi Shimon does not make a {\i gezeirah} and does not require {\i chalitza} from the sister. Why is a {\i gezeirah} not similarly needed there? The answer: {\i shani ervah d\u8217'mifarsma l\u8217'milsa} \u8212- the case of {\i ervah} is publicly known. When the woman is someone\u8217's daughter, or another clear {\i ervah} relationship, the nature of the prohibition is evident to all, and observers will correctly understand why the parties departed without {\i chalitza}. The confusion that would arise in the {\i issur mitzvah} case \u8212- where the reason is subtle and easily misattributed \u8212- does not arise when the {\i ervah} status is obvious. Therefore the {\i gezeirah} applies to {\i issur mitzvah} but not to genuine {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar Before Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup Ma\u8217'amar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i mishna} introduces a fresh scenario. Three brothers again: two married to two sisters \u8212- call them Reuven married to Rachel, and Shimon married to Leah \u8212- and a third brother, Levi, who is {\i mufneh}, unattached. Reuven dies. Now Levi is the {\i yavam} for Rachel. At this point, before Shimon has died, there is no problem of {\i achos zekukaso}: Levi can straightforwardly perform {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} with Rachel. Levi, instead of consummating {\i yibum}, performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel \u8212- the rabbinic form of {\i kiddushin} that is a partial fulfillment of the {\i yibum} obligation. After this, Shimon dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar Strength\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question now before us is whether the {\i ma\u8217'amar} that Levi performed with Rachel is strong enough to alter the legal reality. Specifically: does the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, combined with the original {\i zika}, create a bond sufficiently robust to treat Rachel as Levi\u8217's wife for {\i de\u8217'oraita} purposes? If so, then once Shimon dies and Leah falls before Levi, Leah is the sister of Levi\u8217's wife \u8212- {\i achos isha} \u8212- and would simply depart without {\i chalitza}. If not, then Leah remains Levi\u8217's {\i zekuka} and there remains a {\i yibum} obligation to her, which cannot be discharged without {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are really two separate questions embedded here. First, at the rabbinic level: does the {\i ma\u8217'amar} suffice to overcome the {\i derabbanan} problem of {\i achos zekukaso}, so that Levi may proceed to consummate {\i yibum} with Rachel? Second, at the {\i de\u8217'oraita} level: has the {\i ma\u8217'amar} transformed Rachel\u8217's status into that of a full wife, such that Leah \u8212- as the sister of Levi\u8217's wife \u8212- requires no {\i chalitza} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai rules: {\i ishto imo v\u8217'halazu taitzei mishum achos isha} \u8212- Rachel is his wife, and Leah departs on account of being the sister of a wife. Beit Shammai grants the {\i ma\u8217'amar} full operative weight: it is sufficient to definitively establish Rachel as Levi\u8217's wife at the {\i de\u8217'oraita} level, such that Leah \u8212- now the sister of a married woman \u8212- is simply exempt from both {\i yibum} and {\i chalitza}. There is no {\i mitzva} and no need to discharge any bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel rules otherwise: {\i motzi et ishto be-get u-ve-chalitza, v\u8217'eshet achiv be-chalitza} \u8212- Levi must give Rachel a {\i get} on account of the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and also {\i chalitza} on account of the residual {\i zika}; and he must give Leah {\i chalitza} as well. Beit Hillel refuses to grant the {\i ma\u8217'amar} the weight that Beit Shammai does. At the {\i de\u8217'oraita} level, Rachel is not yet Levi\u8217's wife in any full sense; the {\i zika} has not been converted into actual marriage. Therefore Leah still has a {\i zika} to Levi, which requires {\i chalitza}. And Rachel, whose relationship with Levi was deepened by the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, requires a {\i get} to dissolve the rabbinic {\i kiddushin}, plus {\i chalitza} to dissolve the {\i de\u8217'oraita} {\i zika}. Now, moreover, since Shimon has died and Leah is in the picture, the {\i achos zekukaso} problem is fully active: Levi cannot consummate with Rachel because Leah is her sister and Leah is his {\i zekuka}. Both women require {\i chalitza}; neither can be taken in {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences Harm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the poignant statement of the {\i mishna}: {\i oy li al ishto v\u8217'oy li al eshet achiv} \u8212- woe to his wife and woe to his brother\u8217's wife. According to Beit Hillel, both Rachel and Leah are harmed. Rachel, who had the {\i ma\u8217'amar} and was on her way to being fully married, cannot be taken in {\i yibum} because of {\i achos zekukaso}; she must take a {\i get} and {\i chalitza} and goes away with nothing. Leah, who did nothing, is trapped and must also receive {\i chalitza} before she can remarry. According to Beit Shammai, by contrast, Levi consummates his marriage with Rachel and Leah departs freely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask \u8212- as indeed arises in the {\i gemara} \u8212- how, according to Beit Hillel, the {\i ma\u8217'amar} can be strong enough to require a {\i get} but not strong enough to solve the {\i achos zekukaso} problem. The answer, as the {\i gemara} makes clear, is that the {\i ma\u8217'amar} operates only at the rabbinic level, generating the rabbinic parallel to {\i kiddushin} that requires a {\i get} to dissolve \u8212- but the {\i de\u8217'oraita} {\i zika} to Leah persists and is not resolved by any amount of rabbinic {\i kiddushin} with Rachel. The {\i gemara} had already established this principle earlier: a {\i derabbanan ervah} does not resolve a {\i derabbanan} problem of {\i achos zekukaso} if the {\i de\u8217'oraita} {\i zika} remains intact.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar now clarifies the position of Beit Shammai more precisely. One should not say that {\i ma\u8217'amar}, according to Beit Shammai, creates a {\i kinyan gamur} \u8212- a complete, total acquisition \u8212- in the full sense of {\i yibum} consummation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The test case is divorce. When a man consummated {\i yibum} with his {\i yevama} and subsequently wishes to divorce her, a {\i get} alone suffices \u8212- {\i chalitza} is not needed once the marriage has been consummated. If {\i ma\u8217'amar}, according to Beit Shammai, were a complete {\i kinyan gamur}, then dissolving it should similarly require only a {\i get}. But that is not the case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, the {\i ma\u8217'amar} according to Beit Shammai is sufficient to push Leah away and to permit consummation with Rachel \u8212- but it does not fully transform Rachel\u8217's legal status in every dimension. The relationship, while operative enough to displace the co-sister-in-law, has not fully converted from {\i yibum}-track to ordinary-marriage-track in every respect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar Dachaya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar articulates the principle: {\i ma\u8217'amar le-Beit Shammai eino koneh ella lidchot be-tzarah bilvad} \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, according to Beit Shammai, acquires only to the extent of pushing off the co-woman. This is {\i ma\u8217'amar dachaya}: the {\i ma\u8217'amar} operates by displacing Leah\u8217's claim rather than by fully constituting Rachel as Levi\u8217's wife in the ordinary sense. The relationship with Rachel has been meaningfully strengthened by the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, but it has not crossed the threshold of a {\i kinyan gamur} in the fullest sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar Solution Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Ma\u8217'amar?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A sharp question now arises. If {\i ma\u8217'amar}, according to Beit Shammai, is strong enough to push Leah off the scene and allow Levi to consummate with Rachel \u8212- then why, in a standard case of {\i achos zekukaso} where no {\i ma\u8217'amar} has yet been done, should Levi not simply perform {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel first, thereby displacing Leah, and then consummate {\i yibum} with Rachel? This would solve the entire {\i achos zekukaso} problem from the outset, without the need to suffer through the situation at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deheitera vs.\u160 ?Issura\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers by drawing a fundamental distinction: {\i ma\u8217'amar deheitera dachi} \u8212- only a {\i ma\u8217'amar} that was performed permissibly, without a competing {\i zika} problem at the time it was done, can have the effect of displacing the co-woman. In the case in our {\i mishna}, the {\i ma\u8217'amar} was performed with Rachel while Shimon was still alive. At that moment, there was no problem: Levi had a {\i zika} only to Rachel, Shimon was still married to Leah, and there was no {\i achos zekukaso} difficulty. The {\i ma\u8217'amar} was therefore a {\i ma\u8217'amar deheitera} \u8212- a permissible {\i ma\u8217'amar} done in legitimate circumstances. It is this {\i ma\u8217'amar} that, according to Beit Shammai, retains its effect even after Shimon dies and Leah enters the picture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if Levi were to perform {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel after both brothers have already died \u8212- when there is already a {\i zika} to Leah and a {\i zika} to Rachel simultaneously, and {\i yibum} with Rachel is prohibited because of {\i achos zekukaso} \u8212- that {\i ma\u8217'amar} would be a {\i ma\u8217'amar de\u8217'issura}, performed in circumstances where the underlying {\i yibum} is forbidden. Such a {\i ma\u8217'amar} cannot have operative legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar Works Cleanly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle is that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is only meaningful when it is performed in a context where {\i yibum} itself would be permitted. The {\i ma\u8217'amar} is the rabbinic anticipation of {\i yibum} \u8212- a partial enactment of the relationship. If the relationship itself cannot be consummated because of a competing {\i zika} and the {\i achos zekukaso} prohibition, the {\i ma\u8217'amar} cannot bootstrap itself into creating legal reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Interference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once Leah\u8217's {\i zika} is on the scene and {\i yibum} with Rachel is prohibited by reason of {\i achos zekukaso}, performing {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel is itself problematic \u8212- it is a {\i ma\u8217'amar de\u8217'issura}. Such a {\i ma\u8217'amar} cannot create full marital status and cannot displace Leah\u8217's claim. The effect of {\i ma\u8217'amar} depends entirely on the legitimacy of the context in which it was done. This is why the {\i mishna}\u8217's case is one where the {\i ma\u8217'amar} was done before the second brother died \u8212- that timing is not incidental but is the entire legal basis on which Beit Shammai\u8217's ruling operates.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar Status TBD\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Degree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the {\i ma\u8217'amar} in the {\i mishna} was a {\i ma\u8217'amar deheitera} and that this is why it has operative effect according to Beit Shammai, the question remains: what precisely is the weight of that {\i ma\u8217'amar}? Does it create full marital status \u8212- a {\i kinyan gamur} \u8212- such that if Levi and Rachel subsequently wished to part ways, only a {\i get} would be needed? Or is it a partial status, sufficient to displace Leah, but insufficient to fully transform the relationship \u8212- so that {\i chalitza} would still be required in addition to a {\i get} if dissolution were sought?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Implications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question is not merely theoretical. It determines the practical consequences for the couple. If the {\i ma\u8217'amar deheitera} creates a {\i kinyan gamur}, then Levi and Rachel are fully married once he consummates, Leah walks away, and any future divorce would require only a {\i get}. If the {\i ma\u8217'amar deheitera} creates only a partial {\i kinyan} \u8212- enough to displace Leah but not enough to fully convert the relationship \u8212- then even after consummation, {\i chalitza} would remain a requirement should they ever divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ongoing Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar\u8217's initial formulation \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'amar le-Beit Shammai eino koneh ella lidchot be-tzarah bilvad} \u8212- suggests it is not a full {\i kinyan}. However, the full weight of that ruling, and whether Beit Shammai could be read as granting a complete {\i kinyan gamur} under some interpretation, remains a point of ongoing discussion in the {\i gemara}. The daf concludes with Rav Ashi\u8217's version of Rabbi Elazar\u8217's statement beginning to be introduced, and the full analysis of the {\i ma\u8217'amar}\u8217's degree of acquisition according to Beit Shammai will continue in the next shiur.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 28b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Achot Zekukato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Brothers Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf we take up begins at the very last line of 28a, with the word {\i issur mitzvah}. We are completing a {\i mishnah} that presented the case of four brothers, two of whom died having been married to two sisters. The question was whether the surviving brothers face the problem of {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- the sister of one\u8217's {\i zekukah}, a woman to whom one stands in a quasi-marital bond through the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}. The {\i mishnah} established that if one of the sisters is an {\i ervah} to one of the surviving brothers, that resolves the problem: since he has no {\i zikah} to her, she is entirely outside the picture, and his fellow brother may perform {\i yibbum} with the other sister without triggering the prohibition of {\i achot zekukato}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Ervah Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make the case concrete: suppose Reuven and Shimon died, having been married to Rachel and Leah, who are sisters. The surviving brothers are Levi and Yehudah. If Rachel is Levi\u8217's daughter \u8212- from a different mother than Leah \u8212- then Levi has no {\i zikah} to Rachel at all, since she is a forbidden relative. With no {\i zikah} connecting Levi to Rachel, Leah is not the sister of Levi\u8217's {\i zekukah}, and Yehudah may therefore perform {\i yibbum} with Rachel. This is the logic of the {\i ervah} solution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Mitzvah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then arises: what if Rachel is not a biblical {\i ervah} to Levi, but is instead forbidden to him through a rabbinic prohibition \u8212- an {\i issur mitzvah}, such as being a {\i grushah} to a {\i kohen}, or some other {\i issur derabbanan}? The entire problem of {\i achot zekukato} is itself rabbinic in origin, since the {\i zikah} that creates the quasi-marital bond is a {\i derabbanan} construct. One might therefore reason that since we are operating in a {\i derabbanan} world \u8212- the {\i zikah} is {\i derabbanan}, the problem of {\i achot zekukato} is {\i derabbanan} \u8212- perhaps a {\i derabbanan ervah} should suffice to dissolve a {\i derabbanan} problem. Let the rabbinic prohibition against Rachel push her out of Levi\u8217's picture, thereby permitting Yehudah to perform {\i yibbum} with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Mitzvah Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the {\i hava amina} the {\i gemara} entertains at the top of 28b. The {\i gemara} cites the teaching: {\i issur mitzvah ve-issur kedushah \u8212- choletzet velo mityabemet} \u8212- a woman who is rabbinically forbidden performs {\i chalitzah} but not {\i yibbum}. The {\i gemara} distinguishes between that simpler case, where a single brother dies and the {\i yevamah} is forbidden to the surviving brother through a rabbinic prohibition alone, and the more complex case here, where we have both the {\i issur mitzvah} and the additional complication of {\i achot zekukato}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i kevan de-de\u8217'oraita ramya kammei} \u8212- since at the biblical level there still is a {\i zikah} connecting Levi to Rachel, the problem of {\i achot zekukato} persists. The reasoning that a {\i derabbanan ervah} might dissolve a {\i derabbanan} problem is rejected. Even though {\i achot zekukato} is rabbinic in character, the {\i zikah} that underlies it is a real {\i de\u8217'oraita} relationship, and no rabbinic-level prohibition on Rachel is sufficient to sever that connection. The {\i mishnah} thus teaches us \u8212- {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- that an {\i issur mitzvah} does not solve the {\i achot zekukato} problem, and {\i chalitzah} is still required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This was a genuine and reasonable {\i hava amina}: if everything is {\i derabbanan}, let a {\i derabbanan ervah} resolve a {\i derabbanan} difficulty. The {\i gemara} rejects it cleanly, but it was not an unreasonable idea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Ervas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents a further case: {\i haysah achas meihen zo ervah v\u8217'zu ervah} \u8212- if each sister is a forbidden relative to one of the surviving brothers. For instance, Rachel is Levi\u8217's daughter and Leah is Yehudah\u8217's daughter. In this scenario, each brother may perform {\i yibbum} with the sister who is not forbidden to him: Levi does {\i yibbum} with Leah, and Yehudah does {\i yibbum} with Rachel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i ha tu lama li? hainu hach} \u8212- why is this case necessary? It is essentially the same principle stated twice. What is added by doubling it? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i tzricha} \u8212- both cases are needed. If we only knew the case of one {\i ervah}, we might not appreciate that it is truly a case of {\i ervah} at work, because there is only one brother performing {\i yibbum} and the asymmetry is not fully evident. But because Yehudah is present in that scenario and {\i does not} perform {\i yibbum}, his restriction makes it clear that Levi\u8217's {\i yibbum} is a special case enabled by the {\i ervah} status. Conversely, if we only knew the case of two {\i ervas}, where both brothers each perform {\i yibbum} with the opposite sister, one might think this is simply the general rule and not a consequence of {\i ervah} status at all. Each case is therefore necessary to make the full picture clear to the reader.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Mishnah Line\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final line of the {\i mishnah} reads: {\i zu hi she\u8217'amru} \u8212- \u8220"this is what the Sages meant when they said\u8221" that if a sister is also one\u8217's {\i yevamah}, and one of the sisters is an {\i ervah}, then the other may either perform {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibbum}. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i zu hi l\u8217'me\u8217'utei mai?} \u8212- what does this phrase come to exclude?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i l\u8217'me\u8217'utei issur mitzvah l\u8217'zeh v\u8217'issur mitzvah l\u8217'zeh} \u8212- to exclude the case where each sister is a {\i derabbanan ervah} to one of the brothers. The {\i gemara} already established that a single {\i derabbanan ervah} does not dissolve the {\i achot zekukato} problem. But might one think that if {\i both} sisters carry a {\i derabbanan} prohibition \u8212- each to one of the brothers \u8212- then perhaps together they are sufficient? Perhaps when each brother faces a {\i derabbanan} obstacle to one sister, we treat it as though both are effectively {\i ervas} to their respective brothers, and allow {\i yibbum} with the alternate sisters?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again asks: {\i mah li chad mah li l\u8217'trei} \u8212- what difference does doubling it make? And again the {\i gemara} provides a genuine {\i hava amina}: in the single case, a concern arises that we might confuse others if we allow {\i yibbum} based on a {\i derabbanan ervah}, since only one brother benefits and the second brother will not understand why. But in the symmetric case, where each brother does {\i yibbum} with one sister, there is no concern about the second brother \u8212- perhaps here we should allow the {\i derabbanan ervah} to function. The {\i mishnah} teaches us that even in this symmetric case we do not permit it. The {\i gezeirah} concern \u8212- that people will come to think a {\i derabbanan ervah} generally dissolves the {\i achot zekukato} problem \u8212- remains in force regardless.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fifteen Arayot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a famous teaching that readers may recall from much earlier in the {\i masechet}: applying all fifteen {\i arayot} of the opening {\i mishnah} to the scenario of sisters married to brothers. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav, and so too taught Rabbi Chiya: {\i v\u8217'chulan ani korei bahen} \u8212- in all fifteen cases of the {\i arayot} listed in the first {\i mishnah}, one can find a scenario where the rule applies: {\i asurah l\u8217'ze muteret l\u8217'ze v\u8217'achotah k\u8217'she-hi yevimtah o choletzet o mityabemet} \u8212- she is forbidden to this one and permitted to that one, and her sister, who is also her {\i yevamah}, either performs {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sisters Asymmetry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The puzzle embedded in this teaching is like a mind-bender: one needs to construct a case where two women are sisters, both share the same type of {\i ervah} relationship, yet each is forbidden only to one of the surviving brothers and permitted to the other. The women must be sisters, they must both carry the same category of {\i ervah}, and the {\i ervah} must fall asymmetrically \u8212- this one to that brother, that one to this brother. The claim is that for all fifteen categories listed in the opening {\i mishnah}, one can engineer such a scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother-in-Law Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The easy case to begin with is {\i chamot} \u8212- mother-in-law. Leah is Yehudah\u8217's wife\u8217's mother, which obviously has no bearing on Levi. Rachel is Levi\u8217's wife\u8217's mother, equally irrelevant to Yehudah. Both women are sisters \u8212- through their father in such a scenario \u8212- and each carries the {\i ervah} of mother-in-law, but only to one brother. The {\i ervah} falls asymmetrically, and the sisters remain sisters. The teaching thus works straightforwardly for the mother-in-law case, and the claim is that one can similarly construct working cases for all fifteen categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Excludes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rav Yehuda restricts his own teaching: {\i metargem me-chamoso v\u8217'eilech} \u8212- he will only apply it starting from {\i chamot}, mother-in-law, the ninth entry in the list. {\i Aval shita bavei de-reisha} \u8212- the first six cases, which are all derived from the category of daughter, he does not wish to apply. The first six {\i arayot} of the opening {\i mishnah} are: one\u8217's daughter, one\u8217's granddaughter through one\u8217's daughter, one\u8217's granddaughter through one\u8217's son, one\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter, one\u8217's wife\u8217's granddaughter through her son, and one\u8217's wife\u8217's granddaughter through her daughter. All six are rooted in the daughter relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does Rav Yehuda exclude the first six? The difficulty becomes clear when one tries to construct the case of daughter. Suppose Rachel is Levi\u8217's daughter. For Rachel and Leah to be sisters, they must share the same mother \u8212- call her Chana. Since they have different fathers, Levi is Rachel\u8217's father and another man is Leah\u8217's father, while Chana is mother to both. So far so good: Leah is not Levi\u8217's daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But here is the problem: if Chana was ever Levi\u8217's wife \u8212- even if he later divorced her \u8212- then Leah, as Chana\u8217's daughter, becomes Levi\u8217's wife\u8217's daughter, which is itself an {\i ervah}. Both sisters would then be {\i ervas} to Levi, not just one, and the asymmetry collapses. For the case to work \u8212- where Rachel is Levi\u8217's daughter but Leah is not any kind of {\i ervah} to him \u8212- Chana could never have been Levi\u8217's wife. Rachel must be a daughter born outside of marriage altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} states this explicitly: {\i kevan de-bita ba-onsin hu de-mishkachas lah} \u8212- the case of daughter only works when the daughter was born through an out-of-wedlock union. {\i Bi-nisuyin lo mishkachas lah} \u8212- through marriage one cannot find this case. And since Rav Yehuda says {\i bi-nisuyin kamairi, ba-onsin lo kamairi} \u8212- I want to speak of scenarios that arise through marriage, not through out-of-wedlock birth \u8212- he excludes the entire first six cases from his teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Includes All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye disagrees: {\i metargem af bita be-anusato} \u8212- Abaye extends the teaching even to the case of daughter, even though it only arises through an out-of-wedlock scenario. {\i Kevan de-ashkochei mishkachas lah} \u8212- since one {\i can} find the case, it does not matter whether it arises through marriage or not: {\i ei ba-onsin tehvi, ei bi-nisuyin tehvi} \u8212- whether through an out-of-wedlock union or through marriage, either way the case is findable, and Abaye is willing to include it. Working out all fifteen cases is a genuine intellectual puzzle, and according to Abaye all fifteen yield valid scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother\u8217's Wife Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Abaye himself draws a line at one case: {\i aval eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} \u8212- the {\i ervah} of one\u8217's brother\u8217's wife in the case where the brother was not yet alive when she was married. This is the principle that a woman who was married to a brother who subsequently died, and then a new brother was born after the first brother\u8217's death \u8212- that new brother never coexisted with his deceased brother, and therefore the widow has a special {\i ervah} status toward him. Abaye says one cannot apply this category to the puzzle of the fifteen {\i arayot}, because {\i kevan de-aliba de-Rabbi Shimon hu de-mishkachas lah} \u8212- the case only works according to Rabbi Shimon. {\i Aliba de-rabbanan lo mishkachas lah} \u8212- according to the Rabbanan the case cannot be constructed. And since this is a matter of {\i machlokes}, {\i be-pilugta lo kamairi} \u8212- Abaye does not want to build his teaching on a position disputed among the authorities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Safra Includes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Safra goes further still: {\i metargem af eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} \u8212- even this final case can be made to apply, and one can find it with six brothers according to Rabbi Shimon. The mnemonic for the construction is: {\i mes, nolad, ve-yibem, mes, nolad, ve-yibem} \u8212- died, was born, performed {\i yibbum}, died, was born, performed {\i yibbum}. The {\i gemara} will now spell out this elaborate scenario in full.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Six-Brother Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Brother Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} sets the stage: Reuven and Shimon are married to the two sisters, Rachel and Leah. Levi and Yehudah are also brothers, each married to unrelated women \u8212- Devorah and Chana respectively. These are four of the six brothers. Two more brothers, Yissachar and Zevulun, do not yet exist; they will be born in the course of the scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Death-Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Step one: Reuven dies. Step two: Yissachar is born. Since Yissachar was born only after Reuven had already died, Reuven and Yissachar never coexisted. Rachel, who was Reuven\u8217's wife and is now his widow, was never married to a man who was alive at the same time as Yissachar. She is therefore {\i eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} with respect to Yissachar \u8212- the wife of a brother who was not in the world when he was born \u8212- and she stands as an {\i ervah} to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Step three: Levi performs {\i yibbum} with Rachel. Levi was already married to Devorah, and now he takes Rachel in {\i yibbum}. At this moment Rachel becomes Levi\u8217's wife. Crucially, Rachel had already become an {\i ervah} to Yissachar at step two \u8212- she was {\i eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} to him \u8212- and this status remains even after Levi performs {\i yibbum} with her. An {\i ervah} relationship, once established, persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, looking ahead: when Zevulun will later be born, Levi will already be married to Rachel. Rachel will therefore not be {\i eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} with respect to Zevulun, because by the time Zevulun enters the world she is already a married woman \u8212- Levi\u8217's wife \u8212- and not a free widow. Rachel is thus an {\i ervah} to Yissachar but not to Zevulun.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Death-Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Step four: Shimon dies. Step five: Zevulun is born. Since Zevulun was born only after Shimon had already died, Leah \u8212- Shimon\u8217's widow \u8212- is {\i eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} with respect to Zevulun. Leah is therefore an {\i ervah} to Zevulun.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crossed Ervah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Step six: Yehudah performs {\i yibbum} with Leah. Now consider the position of Yissachar and Zevulun. Rachel is an {\i ervah} to Yissachar but permitted to Zevulun; Leah is an {\i ervah} to Zevulun but permitted to Yissachar. The two sisters are crossed {\i ervas} \u8212- each forbidden to one of the brothers and permitted to the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Levi and Yehudah subsequently die without children and their wives \u8212- Rachel and Leah \u8212- fall for {\i yibbum} before Yissachar and Zevulun, the situation is precisely: {\i asurah lazo muteret lazo va\u8217'asurah lazo muteret lazo achotah k\u8217'she-hi yevamtah} \u8212- each is forbidden to one and permitted to the other, and they are sisters who are each other\u8217's {\i yevamah}. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i mes Levi vi-Yehudah belo banim venaflu lifnei Yissachar u-Zevulun}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Yehuda Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i lama li yibem Yehudah?} \u8212- why was it necessary to include Yehudah performing {\i yibbum} with Leah? Could one not achieve the same scenario with fewer brothers \u8212- simply having Shimon die, Zevulun be born, and let the two surviving original brothers both die, leaving Yissachar and Zevulun to face both widows? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i mishum tzarah} \u8212- because the purpose is also to generate a case of {\i tzarat ervah}, the co-wife of a forbidden relative. If Yehudah had not performed {\i yibbum} with Leah, and Shimon had had a co-wife, that co-wife too would have been {\i eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} to Zevulun, making her also an {\i ervah} rather than merely a {\i tzarat ervah}. By including Yehudah\u8217's {\i yibbum}, Chana \u8212- Yehudah\u8217's original wife \u8212- becomes only a {\i tzarat ervah} rather than an {\i ervah} herself, and the scenario captures that additional case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This entire construction, the {\i gemara} notes, works only according to Rabbi Shimon. The dispute is this: once Rachel became Levi\u8217's wife, does Yissachar\u8217's {\i ervah} status toward her persist? According to Rabbi Shimon, it does: the {\i ervah} of {\i eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} was established when Yissachar was born and Rachel was a free widow, and that status is not dissolved when Levi later takes her in {\i yibbum}. According to the Rabbanan, however, even they would say that once Rachel is Levi\u8217's wife when Zevulun is born, she stands as {\i eishet ach} \u8212- a brother\u8217's wife \u8212- to Zevulun through a current, ordinary marriage, and the {\i ervah} relationship of {\i eishet achiv she-lo haya be-olamo} that Yissachar has to her persists in their view as well. The precise contours of the dispute run through the whole elaborate scenario, and the conclusion is that this six-brother construction operates {\i aliba de-Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Brother Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now presents a new scenario: three brothers, two of whom are married to two sisters, and one who is unattached \u8212- {\i mufneh}, literally empty, unoccupied. This is the {\i mishnah} of {\i sheloshah achim, shenayim mehem nesuim shetei achayot, ve-echad mufneh}. The case builds on everything established before but introduces a new complication: {\i maamar}, the rabbinic form of {\i kiddushin} performed in the context of {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader point of this {\i mishnah} is largely to establish the ruling of Rabbi Shimon. The {\i mishnah} teaches that in all related cases \u8212- two sisters, or a woman and her daughter, or a woman and her granddaughter, or a woman and the daughter of her son \u8212- {\i harei elu choltzot velo mityabmot}, they all perform {\i chalitzah} and not {\i yibbum}. But Rabbi Shimon goes further: {\i aval Rabbi Shimon poter} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon exempts them even from {\i chalitzah}. Because when a {\i zikah} would, if consummated, create a forbidden union of two sisters or mother and daughter, Rabbi Shimon treats it as a complete {\i ervah} situation, deriving from the verse {\i ve-isha el achotah lo tikach litzror} \u8212- do not take a woman together with her sister to create friction \u8212- that when the sisters become co-{\i yevamot}, there is no bond of {\i yibbum} at all, not even one requiring {\i chalitzah}. This is a striking position: what the Rabbanan treat as a rabbinic prohibition, Rabbi Shimon regards as a complete biblical exemption from the {\i mitzvah} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death and Maamar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's specific case: Reuven and Shimon are married to Rachel and Leah; Levi is unattached. Reuven dies. At that point, with Shimon still alive, there is no problem of {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- Levi could perform {\i yibbum} with Rachel without difficulty. Instead, Levi performs {\i maamar} with Rachel \u8212- the rabbinic act of {\i kiddushin} that a {\i yavam} may perform with his {\i yevamah} in lieu of or as a prelude to {\i yibbum}. Then, after the {\i maamar}, Shimon dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Marriage Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the question crystallizes. If Levi had performed full {\i yibbum} \u8212- actual consummation \u8212- with Rachel, then when Shimon dies and Leah falls before him, Leah would simply go free as {\i achot ishto}, his wife\u8217's sister. There would be no issue. But Levi performed only {\i maamar}, not {\i yibbum}. Is {\i maamar} sufficient to transform Rachel into his wife at the biblical level, such that Leah is truly considered {\i achot ishto} and may leave without {\i chalitzah}? Or does the {\i maamar} leave his relationship with Rachel in a kind of intermediate state \u8212- enough to matter rabbinically, but insufficient to achieve the full biblical transformation that would fully exempt Leah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two questions arise: first, may Levi now consummate the relationship with Rachel despite the new {\i zikah} to Leah \u8212- that is, does the {\i maamar} dissolve the {\i achot zekukato} concern even at the rabbinic level? Second, and more strikingly, may Leah simply walk away without {\i chalitzah} at all \u8212- does the {\i maamar} transform Rachel into Levi\u8217's wife sufficiently at the biblical level to make Leah {\i achot ishto} in the full sense?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Allow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai rule: {\i ishto imo, ve-halazu teitzei mishum achot isha} \u8212- his wife is with him, meaning he may consummate the relationship with Rachel, and Leah goes free on account of being his wife\u8217's sister. Beit Shammai treats the combination of {\i zikah} plus {\i maamar} as sufficient to establish Rachel as his wife even at the biblical level. Leah does not require even {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Stringent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel take the opposing stringent position: {\i motzi et ishto b\u8217'get u-v\u8217'chalitzah ve-et eshet achiv b\u8217'chalitzah}. Beit Hillel hold that {\i maamar} alone is not sufficient to dissolve the {\i achot zekukato} problem. Once Shimon dies, Leah falls into {\i zikah} with Levi, and since Levi has not fully consummated with Rachel through {\i yibbum}, his relationship with Rachel has not fully transformed. Leah is still enough of a {\i zekukah} to make Rachel {\i achot zekukato}, and therefore Levi may not consummate with Rachel either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result for Beit Hillel is that Levi must: first, give Rachel a {\i get} on account of the {\i maamar} \u8212- since {\i maamar} is a rabbinic {\i kiddushin} and the rabbinic dissolution of {\i maamar} requires a {\i get}; second, perform {\i chalitzah} with Rachel on account of the original {\i zikah} that was never resolved through {\i yibbum}; and third, perform {\i chalitzah} with Leah for her {\i zikah}. Everything requires formal dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences Stated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Zu hi she\u8217'amru: oy li al ishto ve-oy li al eshet achiv} \u8212- this is what the Sages meant when they said, woe for my wife and woe for my brother\u8217's wife. According to Beit Shammai everyone benefits: Rachel and Levi consummate their relationship, and Leah goes free without {\i chalitzah}. According to Beit Hillel, both women \u8212- Rachel and Leah \u8212- lose out: neither gains a proper resolution, and both require formal proceedings to extricate themselves. Notably, even in this lament, Rachel is already called {\i ishto}, his wife \u8212- Beit Shammai calls her that, reflecting that {\i kiddushin} through {\i maamar} did take effect at the rabbinic level.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maamar Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Not Complete\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar clarifies the precise position of Beit Shammai regarding {\i maamar}: {\i lo teima maamar l\u8217'Beit Shammai koneh kinyan gamur} \u8212- do not say that {\i maamar} for Beit Shammai is a complete {\i kinyan} that fully replaces {\i yibbum} in every respect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The test case: if after performing {\i maamar} Levi wished to dissolve the relationship with Rachel, would a {\i get} alone suffice, or would he also need to perform {\i chalitzah}? If {\i maamar} were a complete {\i kinyan} equivalent to {\i yibbum}, then since consummated {\i yibbum} would require only a {\i get} upon divorce, {\i maamar} treated as complete {\i yibbum} should likewise require only a {\i get}. But Rabbi Elazar implies that even Beit Shammai would require {\i chalitzah} in addition to the {\i get} if the relationship were to be dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Transformation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The formulation is: {\i maamar l\u8217'Beit Shammai eino koneh ella lidchot b\u8217'tzarah bilvad} \u8212- {\i maamar} for Beit Shammai only achieves the effect of pushing away the co-wife. It is sufficient to treat Leah as {\i achot ishto} so that she need not perform {\i chalitzah}, and it is sufficient for Levi to consummate with Rachel without the obstacle of {\i achot zekukato}. But it does not fully transform the relationship with Rachel such that {\i chalitzah} is no longer relevant if they were to separate. The relationship stands in an intermediate state: enough to push Leah away, but not a complete {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Avin Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Avin adds: {\i af anan nami tanina} \u8212- we too have taught this from an earlier {\i mishnah}. In the previous case involving four brothers and two sisters, if the brothers went ahead and performed {\i yibbum} despite the {\i achot zekukah} situation \u8212- that is, in violation of the rabbinic prohibition \u8212- Beit Shammai says {\i yekayemu}, they may remain married. This is {\i be-di\u8217'avad} \u8212- after the fact, they did something impermissible but the marriage holds. The {\i achot zekukah} prohibition is rabbinic, and once {\i yibbum} was performed, the marriage stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Maamar First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question: given that Beit Shammai holds {\i maamar} pushes away the co-wife and lets the remaining {\i yibbum} proceed, why not do the following? In the original four-brother case where {\i achot zekukah} applies, have Levi perform {\i maamar} with Rachel first \u8212- not {\i yibbum}, just {\i maamar}, which nobody prohibited \u8212- and once {\i maamar} transforms Rachel into his wife (pushing Leah off the scene), then perform {\i yibbum} with Rachel legitimately. If {\i maamar} is enough to push Leah off entirely, why can one not do {\i maamar} first and then {\i yibbum}, thereby achieving a clean and fully permissible outcome?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fact that the {\i mishnah} does not permit this, and instead says that doing {\i yibbum} in such a case is only permissible {\i be-di\u8217'avad}, must mean that this trick does not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i maamar de-heiteira dachi; maamar de-issura lo dachi} \u8212- {\i maamar} that is performed permissibly works; {\i maamar} performed in the presence of a competing {\i zikah} does not work. In the case of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the three-brother case \u8212- the {\i maamar} was done while Shimon was still alive and before any problem of {\i achot zekukato} arose. At the moment of the {\i maamar}, it was entirely permissible, and when Shimon subsequently dies, Leah falls before a man who already has a legitimate {\i maamar} with Rachel. The {\i maamar} was {\i de-heiteira} \u8212- done in a state of full permissibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in the four-brother case, attempting to do {\i maamar} after both brothers have died and the {\i zikah} to both sisters already exists \u8212- that is a {\i maamar de-issura}. Since Levi is not permitted to perform {\i yibbum} with Rachel in the face of the {\i achot zekukato} problem, the {\i maamar} with Rachel cannot carry any legal weight. If {\i yibbum} itself is unavailable, {\i maamar} \u8212- which is a rabbinic shadow of {\i yibbum} \u8212- loses its significance. The trick is therefore unavailable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Makes Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having resolved that question, the analysis returns to whether {\i maamar} is a full {\i kinyan} or only a partial one. Whichever position one takes \u8212- whether {\i maamar} for Beit Shammai is a hundred percent or only ninety-five percent \u8212- the {\i maamar de-heiteira / maamar de-issura} distinction already disposes of the question of why the trick was not available. What remains is the separate, unresolved question: when {\i maamar} does work, how strong is it? Is it complete enough that if Levi and Rachel then separate, only a {\i get} is required \u8212- or does {\i chalitzah} remain necessary as well? That is the question Rabbi Elazar raised, and it continues to drive the analysis forward.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi presents a further version of this discussion, citing Rav Elazar\u8217's clarification directly: {\i lo teima maamar l\u8217'Beit Shammai dachi dechiyah gemurah ve-tzaratah chalitzah nami lo ba\u8217'aya} \u8212- do not say that {\i maamar} for Beit Shammai pushes the co-wife away completely such that she does not even require {\i chalitzah}. Rav Elazar\u8217's formulation in Rav Ashi\u8217's presentation refines the analysis of how much weight {\i maamar} carries for Beit Shammai, and the precise nature of its effect on both the woman herself and her co-wife. This discussion will be taken up and continued in the following session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 29\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maamar Status (Beit Sham\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar as Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens by returning to the bottom of 29a in order to reframe the central question and correct a slightly imprecise reading from the previous session. The {\i mishna} had described a case involving three brothers \u8212- Reuven, Shimon, and Levi \u8212- where Reuven and Shimon are each married to one of two sisters, Rachel and Leah. Reuven dies, and before Shimon dies, Levi performs {\i maamar} with Rachel. Then Shimon dies. The dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel turns on the legal weight of that {\i maamar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai holds that Leah simply walks away \u8212- she is free without {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- while Beit Hillel requires both a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} with Leah (to counteract the {\i maamar}) and a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} with Rachel. Beit Shammai\u8217's position implies that {\i maamar} carries a real {\i de-Oraita} status: it not only removes the problem of {\i achot zikukato}, which is itself only {\i derabbanan}, but it frees Leah to walk away entirely, as though she were an {\i erva} in the full sense. The {\i gemara} now wants to determine exactly how powerful that {\i maamar} is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pushes Off Co-wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces this question through Rabbi Elazar: {\i lo teima maamar l\u8217'Beit Shammai koneh kinyan gamur} \u8212- do not say that {\i maamar} according to Beit Shammai is a complete {\i kinyan}, equivalent in every respect to the consummation of {\i yibum} itself. The proof of a complete {\i kinyan gamur} would be that if Levi now wished to divorce Rachel, he would need only a {\i get}, just as in an ordinary marriage after {\i yibum}, where the {\i yibum} dimension is fully spent and no {\i chalitzah} is required for dissolution. Rabbi Elazar rejects this. Even if {\i maamar} frees Leah entirely, it does not complete the relationship with Rachel to that degree. Should Levi wish to divorce Rachel, he would require both a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah}, because there remains a residual dimension of the {\i yibum} bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The formulation is precise: {\i maamar l\u8217'Beit Shammai eino koneh ela lidechot bi-tzarah bilvad} \u8212- what the {\i maamar} accomplishes is to push off Leah, to give her a sufficiently strong {\i erva} status that she can walk away, while the relationship with Rachel remains not yet fully concluded. It is strong enough to free Leah but not strong enough to fully discharge the {\i yibum} obligation with Rachel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar de-Heitera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avin then attempts to find support for Rabbi Elazar\u8217's position from the {\i mishna} itself. The earlier {\i mishna} had discussed a case where both brothers died more or less simultaneously, or where no {\i maamar} had been performed before either death, creating a situation in which Levi and Yehuda cannot perform {\i yibum} with Rachel and Leah because each is {\i achot zikukato} of the other. Beit Shammai says that if they went ahead and consummated the {\i yibum} anyway ({\i yekayemu}), they may remain married \u8212- {\i bidi\u8217'avad} yes, {\i l\u8217'chatchilah} no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avin\u8217's argument is this: if {\i maamar} according to Beit Shammai were a {\i kinyan gamur}, there would be a ready solution in that lower case even {\i l\u8217'chatchilah}. Since nobody prohibits performing {\i maamar} \u8212- the prohibition is only on the act of intercourse with {\i achot zikukato} \u8212- one could simply do {\i maamar} with Rachel, thereby fully acquiring her and removing Leah from the {\i zika} picture entirely, and then consummate with Rachel. Then the other brother would do the same with Leah. If {\i maamar} were truly 100%, this should resolve the problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses back: this objection applies equally whether you say {\i maamar} is {\i kinyan gamur} or whether you say it is merely {\i dechiya gemura} \u8212- that it fully pushes Leah off without constituting a complete acquisition of Rachel. Either way, the same question stands: why not use {\i maamar} to push the co-wife out of the picture and then consummate? The {\i gemara} therefore concludes that neither formulation can draw a proof from the structure of the case below, and offers a different resolution entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is {\i maamar de-heteira dachi, de-issura lo dachi} \u8212- or in the parallel formulation, {\i maamar de-heteira kanei, de-issura lo kanei}. The {\i maamar} that Levi performed with Rachel in the {\i mishna} was done before Shimon died, at a point when {\i yibum} with Rachel was fully permissible. A {\i maamar} introduced into an uncomplicated, permitted situation carries legal force. In the case below, however, there is already a prohibition in place \u8212- the {\i achot zikukato} problem means {\i yibum} is already forbidden \u8212- and a {\i maamar} performed in that context of {\i issura} does not carry the same weight and cannot resolve the entanglement. This is what distinguishes the workable case from the unworkable one, regardless of whether {\i maamar} is {\i kinyan gamur} or merely {\i dechiya gemura}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of the entire discussion to this point is that the core question remains open within Beit Shammai: is {\i maamar} a {\i kinyan gamur}, such that only a {\i get} would be needed from Rachel; or is it {\i dechiya gemura}, fully pushing Leah off but leaving the {\i yibum} dimension with Rachel incomplete, such that both a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} would still be required?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshayer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi now introduces a third, yet more restricted reading of Beit Shammai\u8217's position. Where the previous discussion debated whether {\i maamar} achieves {\i kinyan gamur} or merely {\i dechiya gemura} vis-\u224 ?-vis Rachel, Rav Ashi teaches that the question is not about Rachel at all but rather about Leah. {\i Lo teimar maamar l\u8217'Beit Shammai dachi dechiya gemura} \u8212- do not say that {\i maamar} pushes Leah off completely, freeing her without any remaining need for {\i chalitzah}. Rather, {\i doche u-meshayyer hu} \u8212- the {\i maamar} pushes Leah off enough that Levi is not guilty of {\i achot zikukato} when he consummates with Rachel, but it leaves a residual connection to Leah such that she would still require {\i chalitzah} to be fully freed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading is significantly more restrictive than what the {\i mishna} appears to say, and the {\i gemara} will challenge it on precisely those grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshayer Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avin responds by pointing directly to the {\i mishna}\u8217's language. The {\i mishna} states explicitly: {\i ishto imo v\u8217'halazu teitzei mishum achot isha} \u8212- his wife Rachel remains with him, and Leah walks away as his wife\u8217's sister. The {\i mishna} says she walks away. There is no mention of any {\i chalitzah} required of Leah. The language of {\i teitzei} is unambiguous, and it undermines the {\i meshayer} position entirely. According to Beit Shammai as read in the {\i mishna}, Leah simply goes free; the {\i maamar} has effected a real {\i de-Oraita} change in her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevama Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does not abandon the {\i maamar de-heteira / maamar de-issura} framework but instead uses it to explain why the {\i meshayer} position fails in the lower case. The principle articulated is: {\i yevama de-chazya le-kulhu chazya le-miktzata} \u8212- a {\i yevama} who was eligible for {\i yibum} (from this {\i yavam}) can be affected by even a partial {\i maamar}. {\i Yevama de-lo chazya le-kulhu lo chazya le-miktzata} \u8212- a {\i yevama} who was ineligible for {\i yibum}, because the prohibition of {\i achot zikukato} was already in place, is not affected even by a partial {\i maamar}. This is simply another formulation of the {\i maamar de-heteira} principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's final accounting on the {\i meshayer} question is that it clearly runs against the {\i mishna}. The {\i mishna} shows Leah walking away entirely. Of the remaining positions, the {\i gemara} has left open whether Beit Shammai holds {\i kinyan gamur} \u8212- in which case only a {\i get} is needed from Rachel \u8212- or {\i dechiya gemura} \u8212- in which case Leah is fully freed but a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} are still needed from Rachel because the {\i yibum} dimension has not been fully discharged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask how a {\i maamar}, widely understood to be {\i derabbanan}, could produce the {\i de-Oraita} effect of freeing Leah without {\i chalitzah}. The {\i Yerushalmi} addresses this directly. It derives a biblical basis for Beit Shammai\u8217's position from the verse {\i yevamah yavo aleha ve-lakacha lo le-isha ve-yibbmah} \u8212- the {\i Yerushalmi} reads {\i ve-lakacha lo le-isha} as a reference to {\i maamar}-style acquisition, and {\i ve-yibbmah} as the act of {\i yibum} itself, identifying {\i lekicha l\u8217'ishah} as a {\i de-Oraita} component of the {\i yibum} process according to Beit Shammai.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maamar Completeness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now pushes the inquiry in an entirely different direction. Assuming Beit Shammai holds {\i maamar} to be a real {\i kinyan} \u8212- perhaps even {\i kinyan gamur} \u8212- the question becomes not merely whether it is stronger than standard {\i kiddushin} but whether it might accomplish something even beyond {\i kiddushin}: does {\i maamar} according to Beit Shammai achieve {\i nisuin} status, or only {\i eirusin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking question, given that {\i maamar} consists of a verbal declaration analogous to {\i kiddushin} and involves no act of consummation. But Rava is probing whether the unique {\i de-Oraita} framework of Beit Shammai might assign it a higher categorization.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye asks: {\i le-mai hilchata} \u8212- what practical difference are you asking about? If the question is whether the rights associated with {\i nisuin} \u8212- inheritance, the husband\u8217's right to become {\i tamei} for her as a {\i kohen}, and annulment of vows without the father\u8217's participation \u8212- if that is what you mean, Abaye says those cannot apply to {\i maamar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eirusin Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof lies in the status of ordinary {\i eirusin}. A {\i baraita} taught in the school of Rabbi Chiyya specifies the rights that do {\i not} yet apply after {\i kiddushin} alone: the husband does not have {\i onen} status upon her death, does not become {\i tamei} for her even if he is a {\i kohen}, does not inherit her, and she does not inherit him (though she may collect her {\i ketuvah} if one was written). All of these belong to the {\i nisuin} stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's argument follows a {\i kal va-chomer}: if standard {\i eirusin}, which is {\i de-Oraita}, does not confer these rights, how could {\i maamar} \u8212- which is at most a {\i de-Oraita} institution of a different and arguably lesser character \u8212- do so? The {\i Ritva} presses on this, noting that within the {\i sugya} we are operating according to Beit Shammai who considers {\i maamar} to have biblical force, and therefore the question is not about its rabbinic versus biblical status but about its intrinsic weight. The {\i Ritva} points to another {\i gemara} indicating that a woman who has relations with another man after {\i maamar} but before the act of {\i yibum} does not incur the full penalty of {\i eishet ish}, even according to Beit Shammai \u8212- which shows that even where {\i maamar} may be {\i de-Oraita}, it does not carry the full gravity of an ordinary marital relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuppah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava refines the question. If {\i nisuin} rights are off the table regardless \u8212- since even {\i eirusin de-Oraita} does not produce them \u8212- then what is the actual question? The question, Rava clarifies, is about {\i mesirah la-chuppah}: does {\i maamar} itself constitute something equivalent to {\i chuppah}, so that no subsequent act of {\i nisuin} is required? Or does it function only as {\i eirusin}, meaning that after {\i maamar}, before proceeding to {\i yibum}, a formal act of {\i nisuin} \u8212- entrance under the {\i chuppah} \u8212- would also be required?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question is not merely abstract. If, having performed {\i maamar}, the {\i yavam} is understood to have stepped onto the {\i kiddushin} track rather than the {\i yibum} track, then perhaps the rabbinic structure of {\i yibum} demands that {\i nisuin} follow {\i maamar} before consummation, just as {\i chuppah} follows {\i kiddushin} in ordinary marriage. Rava is asking: having done {\i maamar}, must one also perform an act of {\i nisuin} before proceeding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bi\u8217'ah as Chuppah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds by observing that in the ordinary case of {\i yibum} \u8212- where no {\i maamar} is performed \u8212- the {\i Torah} states {\i yevamah yavo aleha}, and this can take place {\i be\u8217'al korchah}, even against her will. The act of intercourse itself constitutes the completion of the {\i yibum} and brings with it whatever the equivalent of {\i nisuin} would be. This is because any consensual cohabitation naturally serves as {\i chuppah}, and even non-consensual {\i yibum} is halachically effective \u8212- a point the {\i gemara} acknowledges is deeply difficult but which it does not soften. The implication is that the act of intercourse itself, in any context of {\i yibum}, serves as {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, even if {\i maamar} places the parties on the {\i kiddushin} track, the subsequent act of {\i yibum} \u8212- the bi\u8217'ah \u8212- would itself constitute {\i chuppah}. The question of whether one would separately need an act of {\i mesirah la-chuppah} appears to dissolve, at least in the case without {\i maamar}, since bi\u8217'ah inherently is {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zikat Yevamim Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper answer emerges from Rava\u8217's own statement, introduced with {\i sh\u8217'ani omer}: {\i kol ha-oseh maamar b\u8217'yevamto, parcha mimenu zikat yevamin} \u8212- whoever performs {\i maamar} with his {\i yevama}, the bond of {\i zikat yevamin} actually departs from him entirely, {\i v\u8217'chal alav zikat eirusin} \u8212- and is replaced entirely by the bond of {\i eirusin}. According to this view, once {\i maamar} is performed, the parties are no longer in the {\i yibum} track at all. The {\i zika} is gone and the relationship has been converted entirely into an {\i eirusin} relationship. This is a remarkable restructuring: the {\i maamar} does not supplement {\i yibum} but displaces it entirely and initiates a new marital trajectory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is true, then the question of {\i chuppah} is genuine: they are now in an {\i eirusin} state, not a {\i yibum} state, and the subsequent act of consummation is no longer the act of {\i yibum be\u8217'al korchah} but must be framed as a {\i nisuin} within the ordinary marital model. This remains among the most fascinating theoretical positions within Beit Shammai\u8217's account of {\i maamar} \u8212- the idea that {\i maamar} does not merely strengthen or initiate the {\i yibum} process but fundamentally redirects it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hafarat Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomeret Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a case involving {\i hafarat nedarim} \u8212- annulment of vows \u8212- as a means of probing the status of {\i maamar}. The case concerns a {\i shomeret yavam}, a woman waiting for her {\i yavam} to perform either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}, who makes a vow. The question is whether the {\i yavam} (or {\i yevamin}, if there are more than one) can annul her vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three {\i tanna\u8217'im} dispute this. Rabbi Eliezer says {\i yafer} \u8212- he can annul the vows. Rabbi Yehoshua says that only if there is a single {\i yavam} can he annul; if there are two {\i yevamin}, neither can annul. Rabbi Akiva says that not even a single {\i yavam} can annul \u8212- the {\i zika} alone is insufficient to confer the right of {\i hafarat nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is internally coherent: he holds {\i ein zika} \u8212- the bond of {\i zika} is legally insufficient, even with a single {\i yavam}. Rabbi Yehoshua is also coherent: with one {\i yavam} there is {\i zika} directed clearly at him and he may annul; with two the {\i zika} is diffuse and neither has a strong enough individual relationship to annul alone. The difficulty is with Rabbi Eliezer, who evidently allows annulment even when there are two {\i yevamin}. One can understand why a single {\i yavam} could annul, but if there are two, neither has an exclusive claim \u8212- how can either one annul?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami bar Abba resolves this by identifying the precise case under discussion: {\i kegon she-asah bah maamar} \u8212- it is a case where one of the two {\i yevamin} has already performed {\i maamar} with the woman, and the dispute reflects the position of Beit Shammai that {\i maamar koneh kinyan gamur}. The {\i maamar} singles out one of the {\i yevamin} as the one with the active relationship, which is why Rabbi Eliezer permits him to annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin vs Eirusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This identification has immediate implications. {\i Ei amart bishlama nisuin osah, mishum hachi mefer} \u8212- if {\i maamar} achieves {\i nisuin} status, one can readily explain why the {\i yavam} who performed {\i maamar} can annul her vows on his own, since a husband in the {\i nisuin} stage annuls alone. But if {\i maamar} only achieves {\i eirusin} status, there is a difficulty: the law ({\i mishna} in {\i Nedarim}) is that for a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}, vows are annulled jointly by the {\i arus} and the father. If the {\i maamar} puts her in an {\i eirusin} state, the {\i yavam} should need the father\u8217's participation and could not annul alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This appears to be strong evidence that {\i maamar} achieves {\i nisuin}, not merely {\i eirusin}: the ability to annul alone is the hallmark of {\i nisuin} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partnership Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak challenges the inference. {\i Mai mefer? Mefer b\u8217'shutfut} \u8212- when the {\i tanna} says {\i yafer}, perhaps it means the {\i yavam} annuls in partnership with the father. The word {\i yafer} does not necessarily mean he annuls alone; it could be read as his participation in a joint annulment. On this reading, {\i maamar} achieves only {\i eirusin}, and the {\i yavam} annuls together with the father, which fully accords with the law of {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshayer & Hafara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then returns to Rabbi Elazar\u8217's position \u8212- the earlier view that {\i maamar} only pushes the co-wife off and does not even constitute {\i kinyan gamur} with Rachel. If that is the framework, how does {\i hafarat nedarim} work at all, even in partnership with the father? The {\i kinyan gamur} assumption was the prerequisite for any {\i eirusin}-level analysis; if we are below even that, neither alone nor with the father should suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar responds: his statement \u8212- {\i eino koneh ela lidechot b\u8217'tzarah bilvad} \u8212- was only about the consequence of needing a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} rather than just a {\i get} from Rachel. It does not mean the {\i kiddushin} itself is incomplete or lacking. The {\i kiddushin} component is fully present; what has not yet been discharged is the {\i yevama} dimension. On this reading, one can say she is simultaneously a full {\i arusah} \u8212- in which case he can annul with the father \u8212- while also retaining a residual status as {\i yevama} that requires {\i chalitzah} to discharge. These are two parallel statuses rather than a partially completed single status. This is a nuanced position: not partial acquisition, but dual status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Support Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this reading still seems strained \u8212- since Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak\u8217's proposed answer requires him to annul in partnership, but the {\i baraita} uses the singular {\i yafer} \u8212- the {\i gemara} offers a different account of the entire case. {\i She\u8217'amda b\u8217'din u-fasku lah mezonot mishelo} \u8212- this is a case where the woman appeared before a court and the court ordered the {\i yavam} to support her financially until he performs either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. In that context, the {\i hafara} is not a formal act of {\i hafarat nedarim} at all but rather reflects the principle stated by Rav Pinchas in the name of Rava: {\i kol ha-noderet al da\u8217'at ba\u8217'alah hi noderet} \u8212- a woman who makes a vow does so implicitly with her husband\u8217's implicit consent in mind, and therefore vows made under conditions where she is financially dependent on him and seeking to preserve the relationship are understood as conditional on his agreement from the outset. This is not a technical {\i hafarat nedarim} within the {\i Torah}\u8217's framework but an implicit stipulation, and the reason it is confined to this unusual case rather than applied universally is precisely that it depends on the particular type of relationship and financial dependency involved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader takeaway from this entire section is a rich menu of possible standings for {\i maamar} according to Beit Shammai: full {\i kinyan gamur} requiring only a {\i get}; {\i kiddushin} with a residual {\i yevama} status requiring both {\i get} and {\i chalitzah}; and the radical option that {\i maamar} removes {\i zikat yevamin} entirely and places the couple on a pure {\i eirusin} track \u8212- potentially even requiring a formal {\i chuppah} before consummation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Complex Sibling Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 1: Rachel Then Leah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to a series of cases involving three brothers, two of whom are married to two sisters, and one of whom is married to an unrelated woman ({\i nochrit}). In the first case, Reuven is married to Rachel, Shimon is married to Leah, and Levi is married to Devorah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the sequence: Reuven dies; Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel (he is the only eligible {\i yavam}); then Levi dies. Now Shimon survives with two women before him: Rachel (Levi\u8217's now-wife, formerly Reuven\u8217's wife) and Devorah (Levi\u8217's original wife).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 1 Result\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha-rishona yotzei\u8217'a mishum achot isha} \u8212- Rachel walks away because she is now Shimon\u8217's wife\u8217's sister (the sister of Leah, who is Shimon\u8217's wife). {\i Ve-ha-shniya mishum tzaratah} \u8212- Devorah requires {\i chalitzah} and may not have {\i yibum} because she is a co-wife of a woman who is forbidden to Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then addresses the scenario where Levi performed {\i maamar} rather than {\i yibum} with Rachel before dying. In that case, since we are now operating within the framework of Beit Hillel (not Beit Shammai), the {\i maamar} creates only a quasi-marital relationship. Rachel, being the quasi-wife of Levi, is effectively an {\i erva} in the {\i yibum} context for Shimon. Devorah therefore becomes a quasi-{\i tzarat erva}. The ruling is {\i nochrit choletzet ve-lo mityabemet} \u8212- Devorah must perform {\i chalitzah} and cannot have {\i yibum}, because the {\i maamar} has implicated her as a co-wife of a quasi-forbidden woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Zikat Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} extracts a further inference. {\i Ta\u8217'ama de-avad bah maamar} \u8212- the reason Devorah is in a problematic status is specifically because Levi performed {\i maamar} with Rachel, creating that quasi-marital bond. {\i Ha lo avad bah maamar, nochrit yibumi nami mityabma} \u8212- had Levi not done {\i maamar}, the implication of the {\i mishna} is that Shimon could go ahead and perform full {\i yibum} with Devorah. But this requires attention: even without {\i maamar}, Levi had a {\i zika} with Rachel \u8212- a bond of the {\i yibum} relationship. After Levi dies with that {\i zika} intact, does that {\i zika} not create a quasi-marital connection to Rachel that would implicate Devorah as a quasi-{\i tzarat erva}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's implication that Shimon could do {\i yibum} with Devorah if no {\i maamar} was done answers this question. Rav Nachman draws the conclusion: {\i zot omeret ein zika va-afilu b\u8217'chad acha} \u8212- this demonstrates that there is no operative {\i zika}, even with a single {\i yavam}. The {\i zika} alone, without {\i maamar}, does not create a strong enough relationship to render Rachel a quasi-wife and Devorah a quasi-{\i tzarat erva}. This principle \u8212- {\i ein zika} \u8212- is one of the major {\i machlokot} in {\i masechet Yevamot}, and Rav Nachman here identifies this {\i mishna} as evidence for that position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 2: Devorah First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second configuration: Levi (married to Devorah) dies first. Reuven, who is married to Rachel, performs {\i yibum} with Devorah. Then Reuven dies. Now Shimon has before him: Leah (his own wife, who is an {\i erva} to Reuven \u8212- his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- but is not a {\i yibum} candidate for Shimon since she is his own wife), Rachel (his wife\u8217's sister, an {\i erva} in the {\i yibum} context), and Devorah (Reuven\u8217's {\i yibum}-wife).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 2 Result\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling: {\i ha-rishona yotzei\u8217'a mishum achot isha} \u8212- Rachel walks away as Shimon\u8217's wife\u8217's sister. {\i Ve-ha-shniya mishum tzaratah} \u8212- Devorah requires {\i chalitzah} as the co-wife of Rachel, who is an {\i erva}. As in case one, if Reuven had performed {\i maamar} with Devorah rather than {\i yibum} before dying, the result is that Devorah requires {\i chalitzah} and cannot have {\i yibum} \u8212- she is now the co-wife of a quasi-{\i erva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structural difference from case one: in case one, the {\i maamar} was performed with Rachel (who is the {\i erva} \u8212- Shimon\u8217's wife\u8217's sister), and Devorah was implicated as {\i tzarat erva}. In case two, the real wife \u8212- Rachel, who is genuinely Shimon\u8217's wife\u8217's sister \u8212- is the {\i erva}, and Devorah is implicated through the {\i maamar} as a quasi-{\i tzarat erva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Va-Chomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} questions why the {\i mishna} needed to teach both cases. {\i Hainu hach} \u8212- are they not essentially the same ruling? The {\i gemara} responds with a {\i kal va-chomer}. In case one, the {\i erva} is the quasi-wife \u8212- brought in through {\i maamar} \u8212- and yet that was sufficient to render Devorah (who is the genuine {\i yibum} candidate, the real wife) forbidden to {\i yibum}. If even a quasi-{\i erva} suffices to block {\i yibum} with the real wife, then certainly in case two \u8212- where the {\i erva} is the full, genuine wife and Devorah is only quasi-implicated through {\i maamar} \u8212- one would expect the prohibition to apply. Case two, then, is the more obvious of the two, derivable by {\i kal va-chomer} from case one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why, then, were both cases taught? The {\i gemara} explains: {\i tana hacha tana bereisha} \u8212- the second case (case two, where the {\i erva} is the real wife) was actually taught first. And the reason is that the author initially entertained the possibility that in case one, where the basic {\i de-Oraita} obligation of {\i yibum} runs toward Devorah and the {\i erva} is only quasi-connected through {\i maamar}, perhaps the genuine {\i yibum} obligation should override the {\i maamar}-generated complication and {\i yibum} should be permitted. The {\i tanna} thought about it and then ruled no \u8212- even in that case, {\i chalitzah} is required. Because that was the more novel ruling \u8212- the greater {\i chiddush} \u8212- it was placed first. And once it was placed first, the subsequent {\i mishna} was retained even though it is technically superfluous, since once you prohibit the harder case, the easier case follows automatically. {\i Tosafot} notes that one might normally expect the formula {\i zu ve-ein tzarich lomar zu} to handle such redundancy, but that usage applies to a brief additional phrase, not an entire separately formulated {\i mishna} restating a full scenario.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status Lock at Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequence of Deaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third scenario unfolds. Once again: three brothers, two married to sisters, one to an unrelated woman. Reuven dies. Before Levi acts \u8212- before any {\i yibum} or {\i maamar} is performed \u8212- Leah dies. Then Levi dies. Now Rachel and Devorah stand before Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question: Rachel was Shimon\u8217's wife\u8217's sister when Reuven died. But Leah has since died, and Leah\u8217's death dissolved the {\i achot isha} prohibition. By the time Levi dies and the {\i yibum} obligation crystallizes for Shimon, Rachel is no longer Shimon\u8217's wife\u8217's sister. May Shimon now perform {\i yibum} with Rachel?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Olamit Forbidding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} answers with striking finality: {\i harei zu asurah alav olamit ho\u8217'il ve-nesrah alav sha\u8217'ah achat} \u8212- she is permanently forbidden to him, because she was forbidden to him for even one moment. At the moment Reuven died \u8212- {\i sha\u8217'ah achat} \u8212- Rachel was Shimon\u8217's wife\u8217's sister and {\i yibum} was impossible. That original moment of prohibition is determinative. Even though the {\i achot isha} prohibition subsequently fell away with Leah\u8217's death, the status has been locked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda formulates a general principle in the name of Rav: {\i kol yevama she-eina nikraat bah bi-sha\u8217'at nefila yevama yavo aleha, harei hi ke-eshet ach she-yesh lah banim ve-asurah} \u8212- any {\i yevama} who cannot have the verse {\i yevama yavo aleha} applied to her at the moment she becomes a {\i yevama} (i.e., at the moment of the husband\u8217's death) is treated like a brother\u8217's wife who has children from her husband \u8212- an {\i eshet ach} for whom {\i yibum} is forbidden \u8212- and she remains permanently forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is precise. The moment Reuven died, two things happened to Rachel simultaneously: she became a candidate for {\i yibum} with any eligible {\i yavam}, and she was prohibited as {\i achot isha} to Shimon. Since at that moment of {\i nefila} the verse {\i yevama yavo aleha} could not be applied to her in relation to Shimon, she acquired the status of an {\i eshet ach} forbidden as such \u8212- not merely the contingent status of {\i achot isha} \u8212- and that status does not dissolve when the {\i achot isha} prohibition later falls away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai kamashma lan?} What is Rav Yehuda adding beyond what the {\i mishna} itself already states? The {\i mishna} explicitly says {\i harei zu asurah lo olamit ho\u8217'il ve-nesrah alav sha\u8217'ah achat}. That is precisely what Rav Yehuda said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Finality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that Rav Yehuda\u8217's {\i chiddush} addresses a case not covered by the {\i mishna}. The {\i mishna} describes a situation where Rachel was continuously {\i asurah} to Shimon throughout the entire period that she was a {\i yevama} \u8212- from Reuven\u8217's death through Levi\u8217's {\i yibum} \u8212- and only after Levi married her did Leah die. Her {\i asur} status was unbroken. One might say this unbroken status is what makes the {\i issur} permanent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what if the status changed while she was still in the {\i yibum} process \u8212- before any {\i yavam} acted? For instance: Reuven dies, and then \u8212- before anyone performs {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- Leah dies. Rachel\u8217's {\i achot isha} status dissolves. She is no longer {\i asurah} to Shimon. And she is still available for {\i yibum} from Levi. Perhaps now, since her status vis-\u224 ?-vis Shimon has changed while she is still in the open {\i yibum} period, she should be permitted to Shimon if Levi later dies. This is the case Rav Yehuda rules on: even here, the status at the moment of death controls, and she remains forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Status Lock\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this by citing yet another {\i mishna}: {\i shenei achim nesu\u8217'im shtei achayot} \u8212- two brothers married to two sisters. One dies; the other cannot perform {\i yibum} because his {\i yevama} is his wife\u8217's sister. Then his wife (the surviving sister) dies. Now the {\i yevama} is no longer his wife\u8217's sister. The {\i mishna} rules: {\i harei zu asurah lo olamit ho\u8217'il ve-nesrah alav sha\u8217'ah achat}. This is the same {\i halacha}, and it covers the very case Rav Yehuda is addressing \u8212- where the {\i achot isha} prohibition disappears before any {\i yibum} is done, and there is only one brother who could theoretically perform {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes: in the case of only one surviving brother, when the first husband dies and the sole surviving brother cannot act because of {\i achot isha}, we might say the entire {\i yibum} process is finished for her vis-\u224 ?-vis that household \u8212- she has been completely pushed away. The {\i mishna} tells us even then she remains forbidden. In Rav Yehuda\u8217's case, however \u8212- with multiple brothers \u8212- when Reuven dies, Rachel has not been completely rejected from the household. Levi remains and can perform {\i yibum}. She is still in process, still within the {\i yibum} framework. One might think that since she has not been closed off from the household entirely, a subsequent change in her {\i achot isha} status could permit her to Shimon. Rav Yehuda teaches that this reasoning fails: the status at the moment of {\i nefila} is final, and the process of being in flux does not reopen the question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divorce Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Permits Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishna} of this sequence introduces a variant: instead of death, {\i divorce}. The setup is the same \u8212- three brothers, Reuven married to Rachel, Shimon married to Leah, Levi married to Devorah. Now Shimon divorces Leah. Then Levi dies. Can Shimon perform {\i yibum} with Devorah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yes. Shimon divorced Leah; Devorah is not {\i achot ishto} in the relevant sense, and there is no obstacle to {\i yibum}. {\i Ve-chanas ha-migaresh} \u8212- Shimon performs {\i yibum} with Devorah. Then Shimon dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Life vs Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibition of {\i achot isha} applies only {\i bechayeha} \u8212- while the sister-wife lives. Divorce has the same structural effect as death in removing the {\i achot isha} prohibition: once Leah has been divorced, Rachel ceases to be {\i achot ishto} in the operative sense (for purposes of the {\i yibum} framework), just as she would cease to be {\i achot ishto} if Leah had died. The {\i mishna} here demonstrates that it is the ongoing existence of the marital bond \u8212- not merely the woman\u8217's physical life \u8212- that generates the prohibition for the co-wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Permit Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes: {\i kulan she-metu o she-nitgareshu tzaroteihen mutarot} \u8212- in all the cases, if the sisters died or were divorced, their co-wives are permitted. This is the general principle: the {\i tzara} stands forbidden only as long as the {\i achot isha} prohibition is active \u8212- whether because the sister-wife is alive and still married. Once death or divorce removes that prohibition, the co-wife is freed, and there is no doctrine of {\i sha\u8217'ah achat} applying here to lock in a prior status. The {\i sha\u8217'ah achat} principle is operative only where a {\i yibum} status crystallized at a moment of death; divorce, operating prospectively, does not retroactively implicate the {\i tzara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 29b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Reframing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin today with a brief return to the bottom of 29a, not because we have time to spare, but because yesterday\u8217's presentation of the {\i gemara} requires a small correction, and the core question needs to be reframed before we can proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} before us presents the following scenario: Reuven and Shimon are brothers, married respectively to Rachel and Leah, who are sisters. Reuven dies. Before Shimon dies, Levi performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel \u8212- a formal declaration of betrothal in the context of {\i yibum}, falling just short of the act of {\i yibum} itself. Then Shimon dies, leaving Leah as a second potential {\i yevamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai holds that the {\i ma\u8217'amar} Levi performed with Rachel is sufficient to effectively complete the {\i yibum} relationship with her, so that when Leah subsequently becomes a widow, she walks away entirely. This is a striking position: it implies that {\i ma\u8217'amar} carries real {\i de\u8217'oraita} weight, powerful enough to free Leah not merely from a rabbinic restriction but from any {\i yibum} obligation whatsoever \u8212- she is released as an {\i ervah}, his wife\u8217's sister. Beit Hillel, by contrast, maintains that both Rachel and Leah now share the status of {\i achot zekukato}, and Levi must perform {\i chalitzah} with Leah and {\i chalitzah} with Rachel (along with a {\i get} to undo the {\i ma\u8217'amar}). The question the {\i gemara} now pursues is: precisely how powerful is this {\i ma\u8217'amar} according to Beit Shammai?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar opens with a qualification. Do not say, he teaches \u8212- {\i lo teima ma\u8217'amar le-Veit Shammai koneh kinyan gamur} \u8212- that {\i ma\u8217'amar} for Beit Shammai constitutes a complete {\i kinyan}, the full equivalent of {\i yibum}. The evidence that it is not fully equivalent would be this: if it were a complete {\i kinyan}, then should Levi wish to separate from Rachel afterward, a {\i get} alone would suffice, just as in an ordinary marriage. But Rabbi Elazar holds that even after {\i ma\u8217'amar}, both a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} would be required. What {\i ma\u8217'amar} accomplishes, he says, is {\i lidchot batzara bilvad} \u8212- it pushes off the {\i tzarah}, it frees Leah from any obligation \u8212- but the {\i yibum} component with Rachel has not been fully discharged. There remains some residue of the {\i yibum} relationship even as Leah walks free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achot Zekukato Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avin then brings a proof from the {\i mishnah} itself. In a different case \u8212- one where two brothers each face a sister-in-law who is the {\i achot zekukato} of the other\u8217's potential {\i yevamah} \u8212- Beit Shammai rules that if the brothers went ahead and performed {\i yibum} in that complicated scenario, the marriages stand {\i bedieved}, but {\i lechatchila} they may not. Now, if {\i ma\u8217'amar} were a complete {\i kinyan gamur}, there would be an obvious {\i lechatchila} solution available: let one brother perform {\i ma\u8217'amar} with his {\i yevamah}, thereby fully acquiring her and removing the other woman from the {\i zika} entirely, and then let the second brother proceed likewise. The fact that Beit Shammai offers no such solution suggests that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is not a complete {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} pushes back: this same question arises equally whether {\i ma\u8217'amar} is a {\i kinyan gamur} or merely a {\i dichiya gemura} \u8212- a complete pushing-off without full acquisition. Either way, if the {\i ma\u8217'amar} frees Leah, why can\u8217't the brothers use {\i ma\u8217'amar} to resolve the problem in the simultaneous case? The question applies symmetrically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar DeHeteira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution emerges from a crucial principle: {\i ma\u8217'amar de-heteira dachi, de-issura lo dachi}. A {\i ma\u8217'amar} performed in a legally uncomplicated situation \u8212- where {\i yibum} itself would have been permissible \u8212- carries weight and produces its legal effect. A {\i ma\u8217'amar} attempted to cut through an existing prohibition \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'amar de-issura} \u8212- accomplishes nothing. In the original {\i mishnah} case, Levi performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel before Leah became a widow and before any problem of {\i achot zekukato} arose. That {\i ma\u8217'amar} came in at a moment of {\i heter} and therefore had the power to free Leah when she subsequently fell into the {\i yibum} pool. In the simultaneous case, the problem of {\i achot zekukato} already exists at the moment of the proposed {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and so the {\i ma\u8217'amar} cannot cut through it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle applies identically whether {\i ma\u8217'amar} is framed as {\i kinyan gamur} or {\i dichiya gemura}: {\i ma\u8217'amar de-heteira kani, de-issura lo kani}. The distinction is not about the strength of {\i ma\u8217'amar} but about the circumstances into which it enters. So the unresolved question remains: does {\i ma\u8217'amar} constitute a complete {\i kinyan} requiring only a {\i get}, or is it {\i dichiya gemura} \u8212- Leah is fully out, but Rachel\u8217's relationship with Levi is incomplete, requiring both a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi now proposes a third reading, one that reduces the power of {\i ma\u8217'amar} even further. Do not say, teaches Rav Ashi in the name of Rabbi Elazar, that {\i ma\u8217'amar} for Beit Shammai is a {\i dichiya gemura} \u8212- a complete pushing-off of Leah. Rather, it is {\i docheh u-meshayer}: it pushes Leah off but leaves some remnant of connection even with respect to Leah herself. On this reading, the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is strong enough to weaken the {\i zika} to Leah so that the prohibition of {\i achot zekukato} no longer blocks consummation with Rachel \u8212- that rabbinic prohibition is sufficiently addressed. But the connection to Leah has not been entirely severed, and therefore Leah would still require {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejection of Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately rejects this reading from the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} states explicitly: {\i ishto imo, ve-halezu tetzei mi-shum achot isha} \u8212- his wife Rachel is his, and Leah walks away entirely because she is his wife\u8217's sister. There is no {\i chalitzah} required with Leah. The {\i mishnah} makes clear that Beit Shammai\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'amar} completely frees Leah, with no residual obligation. Rav Ashi\u8217's reading of {\i docheh u-meshayer} is simply incompatible with the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore restates the operative principle \u8212- {\i yevamah de-chazya le-kulhu chazya le-miktzatah, yevamah de-lo chazya le-kulhu lo chazya le-miktzatah} \u8212- a {\i yevamah} in respect of whom full {\i yibum} was possible, even a partial {\i ma\u8217'amar} is meaningful; a {\i yevamah} in respect of whom {\i yibum} was impossible at the time, not even a partial {\i ma\u8217'amar} has effect. This is another formulation of the same {\i ma\u8217'amar de-heteira} principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We are thus left with two live positions within Beit Shammai\u8217's view: either {\i ma\u8217'amar} is a {\i kinyan gamur} requiring only a {\i get} from Rachel, or it is a {\i dichiya gemura} \u8212- Leah is fully free but the relationship with Rachel is not complete, and therefore both a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} are needed. The {\i gemara} will shortly revisit the first of these as a serious possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder how {\i ma\u8217'amar}, which has generally been treated as a rabbinic institution, could carry the {\i de\u8217'oraita} force that Beit Shammai\u8217's position seems to demand \u8212- freeing Leah without any {\i chalitzah}. The {\i Yerushalmi} supplies a biblical source for Beit Shammai\u8217's view, deriving it from the verse {\i ve-lakachah lo le-ishah ve-yivemah} (Deuteronomy 25:5). The {\i Yerushalmi} reads this as embedding two distinct acts within the {\i yibum} process: {\i ve-yivemah} refers to the act of {\i yibum} itself, and {\i ve-lakachah lo le-ishah} refers to {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- a formal act of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- as a {\i de\u8217'oraita} component of the {\i yibum} process. On this reading, {\i ma\u8217'amar} is not purely rabbinic; it has a biblical grounding that explains why Beit Shammai grants it sufficient force to free Leah without {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i de\u8217'oraita} grounding of {\i ma\u8217'amar} established according to Beit Shammai, Rava now presses the question further. If {\i ma\u8217'amar} has real biblical standing, and if it may even be a {\i kinyan gamur}, perhaps it does not merely replicate the status of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- perhaps it goes all the way to {\i nissu\u8217'in}. The question Rava poses is: {\i ma\u8217'amar le-Veit Shammai \u8212- nissu\u8217'in oseh o erusin oseh?} Does {\i ma\u8217'amar} effect the status of full marriage ({\i nissu\u8217'in}), or does it produce only betrothal ({\i erusin})?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds by pressing for practical consequences: {\i le-mai hilcheta?} What difference would {\i nissu\u8217'in} make over {\i erusin}? He identifies three specific legal rights that {\i nissu\u8217'in} adds beyond {\i kiddushin}: the husband inherits his wife upon her death, a {\i kohen} husband may become {\i tamei} for her, and the husband may annul her vows unilaterally, without the participation of her father. These rights do not attach at the stage of {\i erusin} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusah vs Nesuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiyya teaches explicitly: if his wife is an {\i arusah} \u8212- betrothed but not yet fully married \u8212- the husband is not an {\i onen} upon her death, a {\i kohen} husband may not become {\i tamei} for her, and she similarly lacks the status of {\i onen} for him. Furthermore, if she predeceases him, he does not inherit her. If he predeceases her, she does collect her {\i ketubbah}, since that obligation arose at the time of {\i kiddushin}. These distinctions make clear how much {\i nissu\u8217'in} adds to {\i erusin} in terms of rights and statuses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads Rava to a {\i kal va-chomer}: if standard {\i kiddushin} \u8212- which is unambiguously {\i de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- does not confer these rights of inheritance, {\i tum\u8217'ah}, and unilateral vow annulment without {\i nissu\u8217'in}, then {\i ma\u8217'amar}, which (as Rashi notes) is fundamentally a rabbinic institution \u8212- even if Beit Shammai grants it {\i de\u8217'oraita} significance, it remains not as weighty as ordinary {\i kiddushin} in all its dimensions \u8212- why would {\i ma\u8217'amar} leap past {\i erusin} and confer the rights of {\i nissu\u8217'in}? The {\i gemara} cites Rashi\u8217's formulation: {\i ma\u8217'amar yavmi mi-derabanan hu, de-mi-de\u8217'oraita yevamah yavo alehah ketiv rachmana} \u8212- rabbinically the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is the prescribed mode, but the {\i Torah} itself speaks of {\i yibum} as the act of entering her. The {\i Ritva} notes a difficulty with Rashi here: according to Beit Shammai, we have just established that {\i ma\u8217'amar} has {\i de\u8217'oraita} grounding from {\i ve-lakachah lo le-ishah}. The {\i Ritva} therefore prefers a different formulation: even if {\i ma\u8217'amar} is {\i de\u8217'oraita} according to Beit Shammai, it does not carry the full weight of ordinary {\i kiddushin}. Evidence for this appears elsewhere in the {\i gemara}, where it is stated that if the {\i yevamah} were to sleep with another man after {\i ma\u8217'amar} but before the completion of {\i yibum}, she would not incur the capital penalty of {\i eshet ish} \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'amar} does not impose the full weight of a married woman\u8217's status, even if it demands a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mesirah LeChuppah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} reframes Rava\u8217's question more precisely. The question is not about whether {\i ma\u8217'amar} confers the rights associated with {\i nissu\u8217'in} \u8212- those clearly do not apply. The real question is whether {\i ma\u8217'amar} itself constitutes an act of {\i nissu\u8217'in} such that no subsequent act of {\i mesirah le-chuppah} (formal conveyance to the {\i chuppah}) would be required before consummating the {\i yibum}. Or does {\i ma\u8217'amar} leave the couple at the level of {\i erusin}, such that the rabbinic framework would demand a formal act of {\i nissu\u8217'in} before they proceed? This is a subtle but important distinction: if one has put himself on the {\i ma\u8217'amar} track rather than going directly to {\i yibum}, has he accepted upon himself the full rabbinic structure of betrothal-followed-by-marriage, including the need for {\i chuppah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex as Chuppah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then explores what the act of {\i yibum} itself accomplishes in the context of {\i nissu\u8217'in}. In a standard {\i yibum} case where no {\i ma\u8217'amar} was performed, the Torah says {\i yevamah yavo alehah} \u8212- the brother-in-law shall enter her. If the {\i yibum} is performed with the woman\u8217's consent and the couple proceed together willingly, that consensual act of intimacy itself constitutes {\i chuppah} and {\i nissu\u8217'in}. This is not unusual: in ordinary marriage as well, once a couple who have completed {\i kiddushin} begin living together as husband and wife, that shared act counts as {\i nissu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coercive Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds the difficult point that {\i yibum} works even {\i al korcha} \u8212- even without the woman\u8217's full consent. This is a deeply uncomfortable {\i halacha}, and one cannot minimize its difficulty. The {\i gemara} seems to be working with a distinction: an act of {\i yibum} that is not fully consensual would not itself carry the character of {\i chuppah}, but it would still be legally effective as {\i yibum}. {\i Kiddushin} cannot be performed {\i al korcha} \u8212- the woman\u8217's consent is required \u8212- but the act of {\i yibum} through intimacy retains its legal force even without it. The practical point being made is that any act of {\i yibum}, however it occurs, automatically produces the status of {\i nissu\u8217'in} for the woman, with no separate {\i chuppah} required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Track Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba now offers what emerges as the most compelling resolution. He proposes that once a man performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with his {\i yevamah}, {\i parcha heimena zikas yivamin} \u8212- the entire {\i yibum} nexus flies away from her. She is no longer on the {\i yibum} track at all. According to Beit Shammai, the act of {\i ma\u8217'amar} does not merely supplement the {\i yibum} relationship \u8212- it displaces it entirely, {\i dechalah zikas erusin}, shifting the couple completely onto the {\i kiddushin} track. This is a remarkable structural point: the two possible paths \u8212- {\i yibum} via intimacy, or the {\i ve-lakachah} path via {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- are genuinely alternative tracks. Once {\i ma\u8217'amar} is performed, the logic of {\i yibum} (including its special properties around {\i chuppah} and even {\i baal korcha}) no longer applies. The couple are now, in essence, in the position of a man and his betrothed, and if they are to proceed to full marriage, the regular apparatus of {\i nissu\u8217'in} through {\i mesirah le-chuppah} would apply. This model also reinforces the conclusion we reached earlier: since the {\i yibum} track has been abandoned in favor of the {\i kiddushin} track, presumably only a {\i get} would be needed for a separation \u8212- not a {\i get} plus {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibamah Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomer Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i mishnah} to test the question of {\i ma\u8217'amar}\u8217's legal strength. The {\i mishnah} discusses a woman who is a {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- waiting for either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- and asks whether the {\i yavam} can annul her vows. The scenario specifies that the woman may be in a {\i zika} relationship with one {\i yavam} or with multiple brothers-in-law simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Annulment Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three Tannaitic opinions are presented. Rabbi Eliezer says the {\i yavam} may annul her vows ({\i yafer}). Rabbi Yehoshua says he may annul if there is only one {\i yavam}, but not if there are two \u8212- in the case of two, neither individual has a sufficiently defined or exclusive relationship with her. Rabbi Akiva says neither one nor two can annul, because the {\i zika} itself is not strong enough to grant the {\i yavam} the legal standing to annul vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The positions of Rabbi Akiva and Rabbi Yehoshua are each internally coherent. Rabbi Akiva holds there is no operative {\i zika} at all for purposes of vow annulment \u8212- {\i ein zika} even with one. Rabbi Yehoshua holds there is {\i zika} with one \u8212- {\i yesh zika le-echad} \u8212- but the {\i zika} becomes diluted when two brothers share it \u8212- {\i ein zika le-trei}. Both positions follow naturally from their respective understandings of {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Annulment Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, however, is puzzling. Granting that he holds {\i yesh zika} even with two brothers, two difficulties arise. First, if the relationship is genuinely shared between two brothers, which of them has the standing to annul alone? Should both not be required? Second \u8212- and this is the deeper issue \u8212- even if one grants the {\i yavam} the standing of a {\i baal} for vow annulment purposes, the rule for a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} is that her vows are annulled jointly by her father and her {\i arus}. If the {\i yevamah} stands at the level of an {\i arusah}, the {\i yavam} alone cannot annul \u8212- her father must participate. How then does Rabbi Eliezer permit the {\i yavam} to annul unilaterally?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami resolves the difficulty by reframing the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case. The {\i mishnah} is not discussing a simple {\i shomeret yavam} waiting with only a {\i zika} in place. Rather, it is discussing a case {\i ke-gon de-avid bah ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- where {\i ma\u8217'amar} has already been performed, and moreover, the {\i mishnah} follows Beit Shammai\u8217's view that {\i ma\u8217'amar koneh kinyan gamur}. Once {\i ma\u8217'amar} has been performed and Beit Shammai treats it as a full {\i kinyan}, the {\i yevamah} is no longer merely betrothed \u8212- she has the status of a {\i nesu\u8217'ah}, a fully married woman. And a fully married woman\u8217's vows may be annulled by her husband alone, without the father\u8217's participation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolves Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position neatly: he permits vow annulment because one of the brothers performed {\i ma\u8217'amar}, giving her {\i nissu\u8217'in} status, and the other brother is no longer in the picture. The {\i gemara} adds that this reading feeds back into our earlier question: {\i i amarta bishlama nissu\u8217'in osah, mishum hachi meifer} \u8212- if {\i ma\u8217'amar} creates {\i nissu\u8217'in} status, that is precisely why the {\i yavam} can annul the vows. But {\i i amarta erusin osah \u8212- heichi mati meifer?} If {\i ma\u8217'amar} produces only {\i erusin}, vow annulment requires the father, and the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling is unexplained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak pushes back. Perhaps we need not concede that {\i ma\u8217'amar} creates {\i nissu\u8217'in}. One might re-read Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i yafer} as referring not to solo annulment but to annulment performed together with the father \u8212- {\i meifer bi-reshus ha-av}. The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this reading on textual grounds: the {\i mishnah} says {\i yafer}, in the singular, not {\i yaferu} \u8212- \u8220"he annuls,\u8221" not \u8220"they annul.\u8221" Solo annulment is what is being described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a further complication. Given that we have been working within Beit Shammai\u8217's framework and considering the view of Rabbi Eliezer (the earlier amora cited at the start) that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is not a {\i kinyan gamur} but only {\i lidchos batzara bilvad}, the question returns: even if we grant the {\i yevamah arusah} status, the {\i yavam} still cannot annul alone \u8212- and the {\i mishnah} attributes full annulment power to him. The {\i gemara} poses this difficulty directly: how does any of this work if we do not accept {\i kinyan gamur}? One response offered is that perhaps there is a dual status at play \u8212- she is both an {\i arusah} on the {\i kiddushin} track and still retains a {\i yevamah} dimension \u8212- and the {\i erusin} component, together with the father, would allow {\i hafarat nedarim}. This is a strained answer, and the {\i gemara} acknowledges it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Stipulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore offers an alternative account of the {\i mishnah} that dispenses with {\i ma\u8217'amar} and {\i nissu\u8217'in} altogether. The case is one where the {\i yevamah} went to court ({\i amda be-din}), and the {\i beit din} ruled that since the {\i yavam} was refusing to perform either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}, he must nonetheless support her financially \u8212- {\i pasku lah mezonot mi-shelo}. Once he is obligated to support her as a court-mandated dependent, we apply the principle stated by Rav Pinchas in the name of Rava: {\i kol ha-noderet al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah hi noderet} \u8212- any woman who makes a vow does so implicitly against the background of her husband\u8217's or quasi-husband\u8217's consent. Her vows are therefore understood as already implicitly conditional on his acceptance. This is not a technical {\i hafarat nedarim} in the formal sense \u8212- it is a presumption built into the structure of the relationship. The {\i gemara} recognizes the tension this creates: if such an implicit presumption always operates, why does the {\i Torah} need a formal mechanism for {\i hafarat nedarim} at all? The answer is that this implicit-stipulation framework applies specifically in the unusual relational context created by the court-ordered support arrangement \u8212- it is not a general rule displacing the {\i Torah}\u8217's formal structure of vow annulment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 1: Reuven Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now works through a series of cases involving three brothers \u8212- two married to two sisters, one married to an unrelated woman. Take the names: Reuven married to Rachel, Shimon married to Leah, Levi married to Dvorah. The cases will vary by who dies first, who performs {\i yibum}, and whether {\i ma\u8217'amar} is substituted for {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Case 1:} Reuven dies. Levi performs {\i yibum} with Rachel. Then Levi dies. Shimon now has before him both Rachel and Dvorah. Rachel is {\i achot ishto} \u8212- his wife\u8217's sister. Dvorah is {\i tzarat ervah}. The ruling: Rachel walks away as {\i achot isha}, and Dvorah is exempt because she is {\i tzaratah} \u8212- the co-wife of a forbidden relative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 1 Ma\u8217'amar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the sub-case: instead of performing {\i yibum}, Levi performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel and then died. Since this is now a case involving Beit Hillel\u8217's framework (not Beit Shammai\u8217's), Rachel is not actually fully married to Levi \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'amar} creates a quasi-marital connection, not a complete {\i kinyan}. Rachel is thus a quasi-{\i ervah} in the {\i yibum} category. Dvorah, as the {\i tzarah} of a quasi-{\i ervah}, cannot be taken in {\i yibum} \u8212- but she does require {\i chalitzah}. Rachel walks away without obligation. The ruling: {\i nochrit choletzet ve-lo mityabemet} \u8212- Dvorah performs {\i chalitzah} but not {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Zika Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a significant inference from this. The {\i mishnah} implies that if Levi had done neither {\i yibum} nor {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- if he died without having acted at all \u8212- Dvorah would be entirely free for Shimon to perform {\i yibum} with her. The reason: Levi did have a {\i zika} to Rachel, but since he did nothing with it and Rachel is, in any case, {\i achot ishto} and off limits to Shimon, that {\i zika} carries no weight. Rav Nachman draws the explicit conclusion: {\i zot omeret ein zika, va-afilu be-chad acha} \u8212- this {\i mishnah} holds that there is no operative {\i zika}, even with a single brother-in-law. The {\i zika} alone \u8212- without any {\i ma\u8217'amar} and without {\i yibum} \u8212- does not bring Rachel into a problematic relationship with Shimon, and Dvorah is therefore free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 2: Levi Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Case 2:} Now the order changes. Levi dies first. Reuven \u8212- married to Rachel \u8212- performs {\i yibum} with Dvorah and thus holds both Rachel (his wife) and Dvorah (his new {\i yevamah} via {\i yibum}). Then Reuven dies. Now Shimon faces Rachel and Dvorah. Rachel is {\i achot ishto}; Dvorah is {\i tzarat ervah}. The ruling is the same: Rachel walks away as {\i achot isha}, Dvorah is exempt as {\i tzaratah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 2 Ma\u8217'amar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Sub-case: instead of {\i yibum}, Reuven performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Dvorah and then died. Shimon\u8217's situation mirrors Case 1: Rachel is the real {\i ervah} (his wife\u8217's sister and his own wife), Dvorah is the quasi {\i tzarat ervah} through the {\i ma\u8217'amar}. Dvorah requires {\i chalitzah} but cannot be taken in {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redundancy Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why does the {\i mishnah} need to state this second case at all? Once we know the ruling in Case 1, Case 2 follows {\i a fortiori}. In Case 1, the {\i ervah} \u8212- Rachel \u8212- was only quasi-connected to Levi through {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and yet that was enough to make Dvorah a {\i tzarat ervah} requiring {\i chalitzah}. In Case 2, the {\i ervah} \u8212- Rachel \u8212- is Shimon\u8217's actual wife, and the quasi-connection runs only through Dvorah. If the weaker case (quasi-{\i ervah}) creates enough of a problem to block {\i yibum}, then the stronger case (real {\i ervah}) certainly does. The second {\i mishnah} appears to be a {\i kal va-chomer} from the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pedagogical Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by explaining the actual compositional history. The second {\i mishnah} was in fact taught first \u8212- it is the case where the {\i ervah} is the real wife and Dvorah is brought in only quasi, through {\i ma\u8217'amar}. This case may have initially seemed permissible: after all, the core obligation was with Dvorah, and the {\i ervah} is only tangentially implicated through {\i ma\u8217'amar}. One might have thought to allow {\i yibum} with Dvorah in that scenario. The author therefore ruled that even this case is forbidden and requires only {\i chalitzah}. This was the {\i chiddush}. The first {\i mishnah} \u8212- where the quasi-wife is the {\i ervah} \u8212- followed later as an extension, and the ruling there is far more obvious. {\i Vahadur chazi le\u8217'issura} \u8212- the author came to see that even the easier-seeming case must be forbidden. Because that ruling was the dear {\i chiddush}, the author placed it first in the text. And {\i mishneh lo zaz mi-mekoma} \u8212- the second {\i mishnah} was not removed even after it became seemingly redundant, because it had an independent pedagogical history. {\i Tosafot} notes that this explanation is necessary: had the second case been just a brief addendum, one might have attributed it to an incidental {\i zu ve-ein tzarich lomar zu} formulation. But since it is a full, independently described {\i mishnah}, a fuller explanation of why both were retained is required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Death Moment Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 3: Changed Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Case 3} introduces a critical new variable. Reuven dies. At this moment, Rachel \u8212- Shimon\u8217's wife \u8212- is still alive, so Rachel stands before Shimon as {\i achot ishto}. Levi is also present, and he could in principle perform {\i yibum} with Rachel. But rather than anyone acting, Leah then dies. And then Levi dies. Now, when Levi dies, Rachel falls before Shimon. At this point Leah is dead \u8212- Rachel is no longer {\i achot ishto}. Should Shimon now be permitted to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i harei zu asurah alav olamit, ho\u8217'il ve-ne\u8217'esrah alav sha\u8217'at achat} \u8212- she remains forbidden to him permanently, because there was a moment at which she was forbidden. That moment was the instant Reuven died: at that point, Rachel became {\i achot ishto} \u8212- forbidden to Shimon. That initial prohibition fixes her status permanently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav articulates the governing principle: {\i kol yevamah she-einah nikre\u8217'et bah be-sha\u8217'at nefilah yevamah yavo alehah, harei hi ke-eshet ach she-yesh lah banim ve-asurah} \u8212- any {\i yevamah} who could not be designated at the moment of her husband\u8217's death for the verse \u8220"the {\i yavam} shall enter her\u8221" \u8212- meaning {\i yibum} was impossible at that very moment \u8212- takes on the permanent status of a brother\u8217's wife for whom there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, and she remains forbidden indefinitely. The reason is not merely that she was {\i achot ishto} at that moment. When Reuven died, Rachel simultaneously became two things: {\i achot ishto} (forbidden for that reason) and {\i eshet achiv} (dead brother\u8217's wife, ordinarily calling for {\i yibum}). But since {\i yibum} was not possible \u8212- {\i lo chazya} \u8212- the {\i eshet achiv} status does not generate a {\i mitzvah} but rather an {\i issur}: she is a brother\u8217's wife for whom there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, which is the forbidden category of {\i eshet ach she-yesh lah banim}. That {\i issur} is permanent, surviving even the subsequent death of Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Different\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why this case differs from the standard case of two brothers and two sisters, which was addressed in an earlier {\i mishnah}. There, when there was only one brother and one sister-in-law who was forbidden, the process ended entirely at the moment Reuven died \u8212- there was no one else to perform {\i yibum}, the {\i yibum} process was over, and the permanent {\i issur} of {\i eshet achiv} was unambiguously in place. Here, by contrast, when Reuven dies, Levi is still available \u8212- the {\i yibum} process with Rachel is not over. Rachel could still be taken by Levi. The process is ongoing. Perhaps, one might argue, the status that attaches to Shimon vis-\u224 ?-vis Rachel should be reassessed once the {\i yibum} process plays out and Leah dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} acknowledges this argument. When multiple brothers remain and the {\i yevamah} is still in process \u8212- {\i lo idchi lah me-hai beita le-gamrei} \u8212- one might have thought that a change in her status during the waiting period could open the door for another brother. Since she was not definitively excluded from the house \u8212- Levi could still act on her \u8212- perhaps Shimon\u8217's prohibition is not yet sealed. Rav\u8217's ruling establishes that this is not so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason lies in the dual status Rachel acquired at the moment of Reuven\u8217's death. She was {\i achot ishto} \u8212- that is why {\i yibum} was impossible for Shimon at that moment \u8212- and simultaneously she became {\i eshet achiv she-yesh lah banim}, a dead brother\u8217's wife for whom there is no {\i mitzvah of yibum}. The first status, {\i achot ishto}, can dissolve when Leah dies. The second status, {\i eshet achiv}, does not dissolve. It is fixed at the {\i sha\u8217'at nefilah}, the moment of death, and it persists. Even when Rachel stops being {\i achot ishto}, she remains permanently forbidden to Shimon as a brother\u8217's wife who was never amenable to {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divorce Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} introduces divorce in place of death. The same three brothers: Reuven married to Rachel, Shimon married to Leah, Levi married to Dvorah. Now: Shimon divorces Leah. Then Levi dies. Shimon performs {\i yibum} with Dvorah. Then Shimon dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leah Freed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key move is Shimon\u8217's divorce of Leah. Once Leah is divorced, she is no longer Shimon\u8217's wife. The prohibition of {\i achot ishto} does not apply to a divorced woman when the original wife is no longer in the picture \u8212- or more precisely, the {\i issur} of {\i achot isha} applies only during the life of the wife ({\i bi-chayeha}), and a divorced woman\u8217's sister is technically not subject to that prohibition while the divorce stands. What matters for our purposes is that Leah is entirely outside the {\i yibum} equation: there is no {\i zika} to Leah because she was divorced, not widowed. She has no {\i yibum} obligation and brings no complications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dvorah Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Levi dies, Dvorah falls into the {\i yibum} pool. Shimon \u8212- who has already divorced Leah \u8212- faces only Dvorah. There is no {\i ervah} in the picture. Dvorah is an unrelated woman whose husband (Levi) has died childless, and Shimon is the available {\i yavam}. There is nothing blocking {\i yibum} with Dvorah. The barrier of {\i achot ishto} that might have existed were Leah still his wife is absent. Shimon performs {\i yibum} with Dvorah. When Shimon subsequently dies, Reuven faces Dvorah. She is his brother Shimon\u8217's widow \u8212- an obligation of {\i yibum} \u8212- with no complicating {\i ervah} in the picture. {\i Yibum} with Dvorah is entirely permissible. As for Leah: she was divorced, not widowed, and so she never entered the {\i yibum} calculation at all. Reuven has no {\i yibum} obligation toward her, and the only prohibition that exists is the ordinary {\i ervah} of {\i achot isha} \u8212- he may not marry the sister of his wife Rachel, but that is an independent matter entirely unrelated to the {\i yibum} structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} closes with the general principle: {\i kulan she-meisu o nitgareshu, tzaroseihen mutarot} \u8212- in all cases where the forbidden wives have died or have been divorced, their {\i tzarot} \u8212- their co-wives who were blocked from {\i yibum} only because of their association with those forbidden women \u8212- become permitted. Once the {\i ervah} leaves the picture, whether through death or through divorce, the barrier that her presence created dissolves, and the remaining {\i yevamah} may be taken in {\i yibum} normally.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Brothers Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah with which we begin deals with a scenario involving three brothers and two sisters. The Hebrew runs: {\i shlosha achim shnayim mehem nesuim shtei achayot ve-echad nasui achrit} \u8212- three brothers, two of whom are married to two sisters, and one who is married to an unrelated woman. For purposes of clarity, we will name the brothers Reuven, Shimon, and Levi. Reuven is married to Rachel, Shimon is married to Leah, and Levi is married to Devorah. Rachel and Leah are sisters, while Devorah is entirely unrelated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Stage Sequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then traces a three-stage sequence of events. Stage one: {\i gerash echad mibaalei ha-achayot et ishto} \u8212- one of the husbands of the sisters divorces his wife. Specifically, Shimon divorces Leah; a {\i get} is given and Leah exits the picture. Stage two: {\i u-met nasui achrit} \u8212- Levi, the brother who was married to Devorah, dies. At this point, Devorah falls before the surviving brothers for {\i yibbum}. Stage three: {\i u-kinesa ha-migaresh} \u8212- the one who had divorced, meaning Shimon, goes ahead and performs {\i yibbum} with Devorah, marrying her. Thereafter, {\i u-met} \u8212- Shimon himself dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Devorah Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question now is what Reuven\u8217's status is with respect to Devorah. When Levi died, both Reuven and Shimon became potential {\i yevamim} for Devorah, and Shimon acted on that obligation and married her. Now that Shimon has died, Devorah once again falls before the remaining brother, Reuven. The Mishnah rules that Devorah is permitted to Reuven. The logic is straightforward: the potential complication would have been Leah, who is Rachel\u8217's sister and therefore an {\i ervah} to Reuven. Had Leah been present as Shimon\u8217's wife when Devorah entered the picture, Devorah could have become a {\i tzarat ervah}. But Leah was already gone \u8212- divorced by Shimon even before Levi died \u8212- so she was out of the picture from the very outset. With Leah removed, Devorah enters free of any taint, and when Shimon subsequently dies, Reuven may freely perform {\i yibbum} with Devorah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's closing formulation, {\i zu hi she-amru ve-khulan she-metu o nisgarshu tzaroteihen mutarot}, captures the principle: when the {\i ervot} have died or been divorced, their co-wives are permitted. This is presented as an extreme case \u8212- the easiest possible scenario \u8212- because Leah was removed from the picture entirely before any of the subsequent complications even arose.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zika Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extreme Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately focuses on the fact that the Mishnah chose to present this particular, most straightforward scenario. The Gemara says: {\i ta\u8217'ama de-geiraish ve-achar kakh met} \u8212- the reason this works is that Shimon first divorced Leah and only then did Levi die. Leah was out of the picture before Devorah even became relevant to the discussion. That is why everything proceeds smoothly. This is indeed an extreme case \u8212- Leah was removed at the first step before anything else had occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara now asks about a more complicated middle case that the Mishnah did not address: {\i aval met ve-achar kakh geiraish} \u8212- what if Levi died first, and only afterward was Leah divorced? In that scenario, when Levi died, Devorah fell before the brothers for {\i yibbum}. At that moment, Leah had not yet been divorced. What is the legal status of that situation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The issue is {\i zika}. Even before {\i yibbum} is performed, the death of Levi creates a bond \u8212- a {\i zika} \u8212- between Devorah and each of the surviving brothers. If {\i yesh zika}, if that bond has legal force, then at the moment Levi dies, Devorah becomes, in some sense, linked to both Reuven and Shimon. Since Leah is still present and is an {\i ervah} to Reuven, Devorah would immediately become a {\i tzarat ervah} by virtue of the {\i zika} alone. This would potentially problematize Devorah permanently for Reuven, even if Leah were subsequently divorced and Shimon later died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara thus reads the Mishnah\u8217's choice to present the extreme case as implying that had the order been reversed \u8212- Levi dying before Leah\u8217's divorce \u8212- there would indeed have been a problem. This implication favors {\i yesh zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi vs Rav Nachman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this reading of the Mishnah, Rav Ashi draws an explicit conclusion: {\i zot omeret, yesh zika afilu bitrei achai} \u8212- from here we infer that there is {\i zika} even in a case of two brothers. The significance of specifying \u8220"two brothers\u8221" is that the force of {\i zika} might be thought weaker when there are multiple potential {\i yevamim}, since no single brother is obviously designated to act. Yet Rav Ashi maintains that even so, the {\i zika} is operative the moment the {\i yavam} dies, and it is strong enough to render Devorah a {\i tzarat ervah} with respect to Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the Gemara immediately raises the difficulty from the contrary position: {\i u-le-Rav Ashi kashya de-Rav Nachman}. Rav Nachman had maintained {\i ein zika afilu be-chad} \u8212- that there is no operative {\i zika} even with a single brother. This was derived from a different Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To reconstruct Rav Nachman\u8217's source, the Gemara recalls an earlier Mishnah in which Reuven and Shimon are both married to sisters Rachel and Leah, while Levi is married to Devorah. Reuven dies, and Levi \u8212- now the relevant {\i yavam} for Rachel \u8212- does a {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel. Levi then dies. The Mishnah states that Shimon cannot perform {\i yibbum} with Devorah, because Rachel has been drawn into the picture through the {\i ma\u8217'amar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman\u8217's question is sharp: why does it take a {\i ma\u8217'amar} to create the problem? If {\i zika} alone were operative, the moment Reuven died and Levi had a {\i zika} to Rachel, that {\i zika} would already have made Rachel a kind of {\i ervah} \u8212- and Devorah, as her co-wife, would already have been problematized for Shimon. The fact that the Mishnah required a {\i ma\u8217'amar} to bring Rachel into the equation implies that {\i zika} alone accomplishes nothing. Hence: {\i ein zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashi\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi responds: {\i amar lekha Rav Ashi, hu ha-din de-af al gav de-lo avid ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- you are correct that even without {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the {\i zika} alone would have created a problem. Even without the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, Rachel was already pulled into the equation by the {\i zika}, and Shimon would therefore have been unable to perform {\i yibbum} with Devorah. The {\i ma\u8217'amar} was not mentioned because it was necessary to create the prohibition; it was mentioned for a different reason entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then did the Mishnah specify {\i ma\u8217'amar}? {\i Le-afukei mi-Beit Shammai de-amri ma\u8217'amar koneh kinyan gamur} \u8212- to exclude the view of Beit Shammai, who held that a {\i ma\u8217'amar} effects a complete acquisition of the woman, equivalent to full marriage. According to Beit Shammai, if Levi performed a {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel, Rachel might be considered fully his wife. In that case, Leah\u8217's status as a {\i tzarat ervah} might be considered complete and unambiguous, and she might even be able to walk free without {\i chalitza}. The Mishnah therefore emphasizes {\i ma\u8217'amar} to tell us not that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is necessary to create a problem \u8212- the {\i zika} alone already did that \u8212- but that {\i ma\u8217'amar} does not function as a full {\i kinyan} in the manner Beit Shammai would suggest. Even with a {\i ma\u8217'amar}, Devorah still requires {\i chalitza} rather than being freed automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, as one might note, a striking feature of this tractate: the opinions of Beit Shammai carry more weight in the context of {\i yibbum} than they tend to elsewhere, and the Mishnah explicitly legislates against their view in a way that presupposes their position as a real halakhic possibility.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman\u8217's Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the two opposing positions \u8212- Rav Ashi maintaining {\i yesh zika} and Rav Nachman maintaining {\i ein zika} \u8212- the Gemara now asks how each position reads the Mishnah. Specifically, the Mishnah\u8217's formulation sounds as though it is permitting a specific case while implicitly excluding other cases. The Gemara asks: {\i zu le-ma\u8217'utei mai} \u8212- what is the Mishnah coming to exclude?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are, upon analysis, three possible sequences of events to consider in this scenario. Case one is what the Mishnah actually presents: Leah is divorced first, then Levi dies, then Shimon performs {\i yibbum} with Devorah, then Shimon dies. Case two is: Levi dies first, creating a {\i zika}, and then Leah is divorced, and only after that Shimon performs {\i yibbum} with Devorah. Case three is: Levi dies, Shimon performs {\i yibbum} with Devorah so that he is now married simultaneously to both Leah and Devorah, and only then is Leah divorced \u8212- before Shimon himself dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rav Nachman, who holds {\i ein zika}, the {\i zika} of case two is legally meaningless. Whether Levi died before or after Leah\u8217's divorce makes no difference, because the {\i zika} does not draw Devorah into any problematic status. Therefore, both case one and case two are equally permissible. What the Mishnah must be coming to exclude is case three \u8212- the scenario in which Shimon was simultaneously married to both Devorah and Leah, and only divorced Leah thereafter. That case remains problematic. But why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nishuin/Mita Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to an important underlying debate: {\i nishuin rishonim mappilin} versus {\i mita mappelet}. The question is how we determine whether a {\i tzarat ervah} situation exists. Is it determined at the time of marriage \u8212- {\i nishuin rishonim mappilin} \u8212- meaning we look at who was married to whom simultaneously and that determines status? Or is it determined at the moment of death \u8212- {\i mita mappelet} \u8212- meaning we look at who is in the picture at the time the {\i yavam} dies, and that determines whether the co-wife is forbidden?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If we say {\i mita mappelet}, then what matters is only who is present at the moment Shimon dies. If Leah was divorced before Shimon died, she is out of the picture at the critical moment and Devorah is permitted. Under this view, case three would also be permissible, since Leah was gone before Shimon\u8217's death \u8212- and there would be nothing left for the Mishnah to exclude, since every scenario involving any divorce before death would be acceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other hand, if we say {\i nishuin rishonim mappilin}, then the fact that Shimon was married to both Devorah and Leah simultaneously \u8212- even briefly \u8212- is what counts. In case three, they were co-wives under the same husband at the same time, and that status, once established at the time of marriage, cannot be undone by a subsequent divorce. Case three thus remains problematic, and that is precisely what the Mishnah is coming to exclude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore concludes that Rav Nachman, who holds {\i ein zika} and thus cannot distinguish between cases one and two, must hold {\i nishuin rishonim mappilin} in order to have something left to exclude \u8212- namely, case three. As the Gemara puts it: {\i al korchach ke-Rabbi Yirmiyah sevira leh} \u8212- he must hold like Rabbi Yirmiyah, who maintained that the two Mishnayot reflect different opinions, one holding {\i nishuin rishonim mappilin} and one holding {\i mita mappelet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then turns to Rava, who maintained that it is all one {\i tanna} and that we always go by {\i mita mappelet}. Under Rava\u8217's position, any divorce before death resolves the situation \u8212- and under {\i mita mappelet}, cases one, two, and three are all potentially permissible. So the Gemara asks: {\i zu le-ma\u8217'utei mai} \u8212- according to Rava, what does the Mishnah exclude?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer depends on whether Rava aligns with Rav Ashi ({\i yesh zika}) or Rav Nachman ({\i ein zika}). If Rava holds {\i yesh zika} like Rav Ashi, there is one scenario that remains problematic even under {\i mita mappelet}: the case in which Levi dies, creating a {\i zika} between Devorah and the surviving brothers, and Shimon then dies without ever divorcing Leah at all. In that case \u8212- {\i meis belo geiraish} \u8212- the {\i zika} alone was present, and since Leah was never divorced at any point before Shimon\u8217's death, the {\i zika} made Devorah a {\i tzarat ervah} and she is forbidden. This is the case the Mishnah excludes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Paradoxically, this is the case where Shimon\u8217's connection to Devorah was at its weakest \u8212- a mere {\i zika}, not actual marriage \u8212- yet it is precisely this weak connection that creates the problem, because Leah was never removed from the picture at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara makes the conclusion explicit: {\i al korchach ke-Rav Ashi sevira lei} \u8212- Rava must hold like Rav Ashi, maintaining {\i yesh zika}. If Rava were to hold {\i ein zika} like Rav Nachman, there would be nothing left to exclude. Under {\i mita mappelet}, case three is permissible because the divorce occurred before death. Under {\i ein zika}, cases one and two are both permissible. There is literally no scenario involving a divorce at any point before death that would remain prohibited. The Mishnah would be excluding nothing, which is impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, Rava must hold both {\i yesh zika} and {\i nishuin rishonim} \u8212- wait, not {\i nishuin rishonim}, but {\i mita mappelet} with {\i yesh zika}. It is the combination of {\i mita mappelet} and {\i yesh zika} that leaves exactly one case to be excluded: {\i meis belo geiraish}, dying without having divorced Leah, where the {\i zika} alone was operative and Leah was never removed from the picture.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah now shifts to a different type of problem. Rather than cases where the {\i ervah} is definitively present or definitively absent, we encounter cases where her status is uncertain. The Mishnah introduces the category of {\i safek kiddushin} and {\i safek gerushin} \u8212- cases where we do not know whether the {\i ervah} was ever fully married or whether she was ever validly divorced. The Mishnah rules that in all such cases of uncertainty, the co-wife performs {\i chalitza} and does not do {\i yibbum}: {\i choltzot ve-lo misyabmot}. This is because perhaps the {\i ervah} is in the picture, in which case {\i yibbum} would involve an {\i issur ervah}, while if she is not in the picture, the co-wife still requires {\i chalitza} or {\i yibbum} to be released from the {\i zika}. Since we cannot know, we require {\i chalitza} without {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the {\i ervah} herself \u8212- Rachel in our scenario \u8212- she requires no action in any case. If she was never truly married, she has no obligation; if she is genuinely married and therefore an {\i ervah}, she is forbidden to the {\i yavam} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah illustrates {\i safek kiddushin} with the following case: {\i zaraka kiddushin, safek karov lo safek karov lah} \u8212- he threw the ring of {\i kiddushin}, and we do not know whether it landed closer to him or closer to her. The operative principle of acquisition here is the four-cubit radius of personal domain ({\i arba amot}). Rashi describes the scenario where the couple stands exactly eight {\i amot} apart, so the precise midpoint between them is where their respective four-cubit domains meet. The ring fell at or near that midpoint, and it is unknown whether it came to rest within her four {\i amot} \u8212- effecting a valid {\i kinyan} \u8212- or within his. This constitutes a genuine {\i safek kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Gerushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i safek gerushin}, the Mishnah takes a somewhat different approach. Rather than offering a parallel case of uncertainty about whether the {\i get} reached her \u8212- which would be the simple structural parallel to the {\i kiddushin} case \u8212- the Mishnah instead enumerates three types of {\i gittin} that are rabbinically invalid but Biblically valid. The Mishnah calls these {\i safek gerushin}, though in truth they represent a distinct category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three cases are: first, a {\i get} written in the husband\u8217's own handwriting but bearing no witnesses\u8217' signatures ({\i katav bi-khtav yado ve-ein alav eidim}); second, a {\i get} bearing witnesses but lacking a date ({\i yesh alav eidim ve-ein bo zman}); and third, a {\i get} bearing a date but signed by only one witness rather than two ({\i yesh bo zman ve-ein bo ella ed echad}). The Mishnah\u8217's formulation is notably crafted for mnemonic continuity: each clause picks up where the previous left off \u8212- \u8220"it has witnesses but no date,\u8221" \u8220"it has a date but only one witness\u8221" \u8212- carrying the reader from one case to the next in a fluid chain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each of these {\i gittin} is Biblically valid as a {\i get} but rabbinically disqualified. The woman is therefore in a quasi-divorced state, treated as half-and-half, which is why the Mishnah classifies this as {\i safek gerushin} and requires {\i chalitza} rather than {\i yibbum} for the co-wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes a conspicuous asymmetry: {\i ve-ilu be-gerushin safek karov lo safek karov lah lo katani} \u8212- the Mishnah did not include the parallel case for {\i gerushin} of a {\i get} thrown and of uncertain receipt, analogous to the thrown ring in {\i kiddushin}. The Gemara asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- what is the reason?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, developed in detail in the next section, relates to the principle of {\i chazakah} \u8212- status quo. A {\i safek get} of the thrown variety would not alter the woman\u8217's existing status. Since she was definitely married before the uncertain act occurred, her status quo as a married woman remains intact. The uncertainty about whether the {\i get} reached her does not disturb that status quo. The Mishnah therefore had no reason to include this case, because Rava holds that we simply leave her with her prior status. The cases the Mishnah does bring \u8212- the three rabbinically defective {\i gittin} \u8212- are categorically different and warranted inclusion for their own reasons.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chazakah/Status Quo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now articulates the general principle underlying the Mishnah\u8217's selectivity. The principle is {\i chazakah}: a person\u8217's legal status before the uncertain event occurred remains her status after it, absent proof of change. One does not alter legal status on the basis of doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Status Quo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied to the case at hand: if Shimon was definitely married to Rachel and threw her a {\i get} that may or may not have reached her, Rachel\u8217's {\i chazakah} is that of a married woman \u8212- {\i bechezkat eshet ish}. We do not change that status based on doubt. Rachel remains presumed married. Correspondingly, Devorah\u8217's {\i chazakah} before the uncertain {\i get} was that she was a {\i tzarat ervah} \u8212- forbidden to the {\i yavam}. We do not change that status either. Rava formulates it as: {\i ishah zo bechezkat heter lashuk omedet u-misafek attah ba le-osrah al te\u8217'asrennah misafek} \u8212- this woman stands in a status of being permitted, and you are coming to forbid her on the basis of doubt; do not forbid her from doubt. Everyone retains their prior status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately presses this reasoning: {\i i hachi be-kiddushin nami neima} \u8212- if so, apply the same logic to {\i safek kiddushin}. Before the ring was thrown, Devorah\u8217's status was that she was permitted to the {\i yavam} for {\i yibbum}. The question of whether Rachel was married is now a {\i safek}. Why not say: Devorah stands in her prior status of being permitted to do {\i yibbum}, and we should not disturb that status based on doubt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i hatam le-chumra} \u8212- there it is a case of being stringent. The possibility that {\i yibbum} would be a violation of {\i issur ervah} \u8212- a prohibition carrying {\i karet} \u8212- is sufficiently serious that we do not allow {\i yibbum} to proceed even though in principle the status quo would permit it. We require {\i chalitza} instead. This is a {\i chumra de-rabbanan}: go ahead and do {\i chalitza} and forgo {\i yibbum} as a matter of caution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chumra Costs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No-Cost Chumra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now engages in a sophisticated cost-benefit analysis of rabbinic stringencies, and the discussion is genuinely striking. The question is not only whether a {\i chumra} is warranted, but whether it comes at a cost \u8212- specifically, whether it sends a misleading message that will lead to real halakhic harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of {\i safek kiddushin}, requiring {\i chalitza} rather than {\i yibbum} is a no-cost {\i chumra}. Here is why. The true status, based on {\i chazakah}, is that Rachel was not married to Shimon \u8212- the {\i safek kiddushin} did not alter her prior status as an unmarried woman. Devorah\u8217's true status is therefore that she is permitted to do {\i yibbum}. We are being stringent and not allowing {\i yibbum}, but we do require {\i chalitza}. What message does {\i chalitza} send? It sends the message that Rachel is out of the picture \u8212- not married to Shimon. That is precisely the true state of affairs according to {\i chazakah}. The act of {\i chalitza} is fully consistent with the correct legal reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, if someone were to observe the {\i chalitza} and draw the conclusion that {\i yibbum} could have been performed \u8212- and were to go ahead and perform {\i yibbum} \u8212- even that mistake would not be a serious halakhic violation, because Rachel really was not married to Shimon in the first place. The {\i chumra} of requiring {\i chalitza} instead of permitting {\i yibbum} is thus entirely costless: it is consistent with the true status, it sends the right message, and even if the message is misread and leads to {\i yibbum}, the {\i yibbum} would in fact have been permissible. As the Gemara puts it: {\i v\u8217'keivan de-kamatzreikh chalitza, midei yada de-chumra be-alma hu} \u8212- since {\i chalitza} is demanded, it is understood to be a mere stringency, and the right message is communicated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the concern about the absence of {\i yibbum} sending a wrong message is misplaced, because people do not observe the absence of {\i yibbum} \u8212- they observe the presence of {\i chalitza}. And {\i chalitza} here says exactly the right thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Get Chumra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks the natural question: why not apply the same reasoning to {\i safek get}? If a {\i get} of uncertain delivery was given, why not require {\i chalitza} as a {\i chumra} there as well? After all, perhaps Rachel was divorced and Devorah therefore needs to be freed from her obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wrong Message\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that in the case of {\i safek get}, {\i chalitza} sends exactly the wrong message. The true status based on {\i chazakah} is that Rachel remains married to Shimon. The {\i safek get} did not change that. Devorah\u8217's status as a {\i tzarat ervah} therefore remains. Now, if {\i chalitza} is performed, observers will reason: why is {\i chalitza} being done for Devorah? The only reason {\i chalitza} would be performed is that Rachel is out of the picture. But Rachel is not out of the picture \u8212- she is presumed married. {\i Chalitza} here therefore communicates that Rachel was successfully divorced, which is false. And that false message has serious consequences: people may conclude that Rachel is now free to remarry, when in fact she remains a married woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Quo Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The solution, therefore, is to go by the status quo without requiring any {\i chalitza} at all. Devorah retains her status as a {\i tzarat ervah} \u8212- forbidden to the {\i yavam} \u8212- and no {\i chalitza} is performed. Not because we are being lenient about the {\i safek}, but because requiring {\i chalitza} here would actively mislead people into thinking Rachel was divorced when {\i chazakah} says otherwise. The cost of the {\i chumra} is too high: it would generate a false message that leads to precisely the wrong behavior. As Rava\u8217's logic is developed through the Gemara, the rule emerges: {\i chumrot} are appropriate only when they do not themselves produce a {\i kula} \u8212- a leniency in the wrong direction. A {\i chumra de-atya lidei kula} is not a genuine stringency; it is a counterproductive measure that halakha rejects.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Building Collapse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now challenges Rava\u8217's principle of {\i chazakah} with a case from a different Mishnah. The new case: {\i nafal ha-bayit alav ve-al bat achiv ve-ein yaduah eizeh meihem meis rishon} \u8212- a building fell on a man and on his niece, and we do not know which of them died first. Let us reconstruct the case. Shimon is married to Rachel, who is the daughter of Reuven \u8212- meaning Rachel is Reuven\u8217's daughter and therefore an {\i ervah} to Reuven. Shimon is also married to Devorah. The building then collapses on both Shimon and Rachel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncertainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The uncertainty about the order of death creates a halakhic dilemma. If Rachel died first, then when Shimon subsequently died, Devorah was the only wife in the picture \u8212- no {\i ervah} was present \u8212- and Devorah is therefore permitted to Reuven for {\i yibbum}. But if Shimon died first, then at the moment of his death Rachel was still alive and present as his wife, making Devorah a {\i tzarat ervah}, forbidden to Reuven. We have a genuine {\i safek} about the order of death and therefore about Devorah\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah rules: {\i tzarasah choletzet ve-lo misyabbemet} \u8212- in a case of {\i safek}, Devorah performs {\i chalitza} but does not do {\i yibbum}. This is the standard response to uncertainty about whether {\i tzarat ervah} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now applies Rava\u8217's own reasoning. Before the building collapsed, what was Devorah\u8217's status? She was {\i muteret la-shuk} \u8212- free of any {\i yibbum} obligation that would preclude her from the world. Her {\i chazakah} was that of a woman who had no issue of {\i tzarat ervah}. Why, asks Abaye, do we not apply Rava\u8217's principle? Let Devorah retain her prior status. The {\i safek} should not change it. She should be free to walk away without {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Wrong Message\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might try to answer Abaye\u8217's challenge the way the earlier cases were resolved: perhaps {\i chalitza} is required here because it is a no-cost {\i chumra}. After all, in the building-collapse case, Rachel is not visibly present in the way she would be in a divorce scenario. People are not walking around saying \u8220"but what about Rachel?\u8221" So {\i chalitza} does not send the same kind of wrong message here that it would in the {\i safek get} scenario. Performing {\i chalitza} in this case does not make people think Rachel was divorced; it merely suggests that the Rabbis took precautions about an uncertain situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Frequency Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution, however, operates on a different principle altogether: {\i gerushin de-shkhikhei gazru bohu rabbanan; mapoles de-lo shkhikhei lo gazru bohu rabbanan} \u8212- cases of divorce are common, and the Rabbis therefore legislated concerning them; cases of a building collapse are rare, and the Rabbis did not legislate for them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The import of this distinction is as follows. The reason the Rabbis were concerned about requiring {\i chalitza} in the {\i safek get} case was not merely the wrong message, but the cost that flows from that wrong message in a commonly recurring context. If the rule were that {\i chalitza} is required whenever a {\i get} might have been given, the message sent by {\i chalitza} \u8212- that Rachel is divorced and Devorah can potentially do {\i yibbum} \u8212- would propagate widely, generating real harm across many cases. The frequency of divorce as a social reality means that such a policy would lead to systematic violations: people would perform {\i yibbum} with genuine {\i ervot} based on the misleading implication of the {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In contrast, a building collapsing on a husband and his wife simultaneously is exceedingly rare. The scenario practically never arises. Therefore, even if requiring {\i chalitza} in this case sends a somewhat ambiguous message, the Rabbis did not need to create a general policy of leniency to address it. The risk of mass confusion and systematic violation simply does not exist. The Rabbis can afford to be stringent \u8212- requiring {\i chalitza} \u8212- in a case that is so unusual that it will generate no meaningful pattern of misleading behavior.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds a further nuance. In the {\i safek get} case, people seeing a {\i chalitza} being performed will ask: why is {\i chalitza} being done for Devorah? Rachel is still around. This actively prompts them to reason backward to the conclusion that Rachel must have been divorced \u8212- and that conclusion will then lead to harmful behavior. In the building-collapse case, by contrast, Rachel\u8217's absence from the scene (she is deceased, one way or another) means that no one is asking pointed questions that would lead them to draw dangerous conclusions. The Rabbis therefore determined that the calculus differs: {\i gerushin} is common and the wrong message spreads dangerously; {\i mapoles} is rare and the wrong message, to the extent it is even sent, causes no systemic harm.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 30b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Brothers & Zika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} under discussion presents a case involving three brothers: two are married to two sisters, and one is married to a third, unrelated woman. Concretely, Reuven and Shimon are married to Rachel and Leah respectively, while Levi is married to Devorah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequence Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then describes a sequence of events. First, Shimon divorces Leah \u8212- she is out of the picture from the very outset. Second, Levi dies, at which point either Reuven or Shimon could perform {\i yibum} with Devorah. Third, Shimon does in fact perform {\i yibum} with Devorah, bringing her into his household. Finally, Shimon dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is: what is Reuven\u8217's status vis-\u224 ?-vis Devorah? Since Leah was already divorced before Levi died \u8212- before Devorah became relevant to the conversation at all \u8212- she was fully out of the picture by the time Shimon died. Devorah is therefore permitted to Reuven. This is the principle the {\i mishna} establishes: {\i zo hi she\u8217'amru, v\u8217'chulan shemetu o nisgareshu} \u8212- when the {\i ervah} has died or been divorced, her {\i tzarot} are permitted. In this case, since Leah was removed from the equation at the very first stage, Devorah is permitted to Reuven. The {\i mishna} deliberately chose an extreme case where Leah was out of the picture well before anything else occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A more nuanced scenario \u8212- one the {\i mishna} does not directly address \u8212- would be if Shimon had first married Devorah and then divorced Leah afterward. The question in that scenario would be whether the period during which both women were simultaneously in Shimon\u8217's household created a permanent problem for Devorah vis-\u224 ?-vis Reuven. But the {\i mishna}\u8217's chosen case avoids that complication entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yesh Zika Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now probes the {\i mishna}\u8217's implicit logic. The {\i mishna} specifies that the reason the case works is {\i d\u8217'girash v\u8217'achar kach met} \u8212- Shimon first divorced Leah and only then did Levi die and {\i yibum} unfold. But what would have happened had Levi died first, creating a {\i zika} \u8212- a juridical connection \u8212- between Shimon and Devorah, before Leah was divorced? The {\i gemara} suggests that if Levi died first and Shimon thereby acquired a {\i zika} to Devorah, Devorah might immediately become a {\i tzarat ervah} relative to Reuven, even before any {\i yibum} had taken place. This would be problematic regardless of what followed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi draws an explicit inference: {\i amar Rav Ashi, zos omeret, yesh zikah afilu b\u8217'trei achei} \u8212- from this {\i mishna} we can infer that {\i zika} applies even when there are two brothers. When Levi died, Reuven also had a claim on Devorah, not just Shimon. Even so, Shimon\u8217's {\i zika} is strong enough to render Devorah a quasi-{\i tzarat ervah}. This is a robust statement of {\i yesh zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Zika Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a difficulty: Rav Nechman held {\i ein zika afilu b\u8217'chad} \u8212- that {\i zika} is not a legally operative connection, even when there is only a single surviving brother. He derived this from an earlier {\i mishna} in which Reuven and Shimon are married to Rachel and Leah, and Levi is married to Devorah. That {\i mishna} stated that if Reuven dies and Levi subsequently performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel and then dies, Shimon cannot perform {\i yibum} with Devorah because she has become a {\i tzarat ervah}. Rav Nechman\u8217's reading is that Rachel only became problematic because of the {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- the {\i zika} alone, without {\i ma\u8217'amar}, was insufficient to pull Rachel into the equation. Yet the {\i mishna} before us seems to imply that even {\i zika} alone \u8212- without {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- creates a problem with Devorah. How are these two {\i mishnayot} to be reconciled?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zika & Tzarat Erva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi responds to Rav Nechman\u8217's challenge directly. He concedes the point: even without {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the {\i zika} itself would have made Rachel a problem. {\i Af al gav d\u8217'lo avad bah ma\u8217'amar, nachis michlal chalitzah, yibumi lo miyabma} \u8212- even without {\i ma\u8217'amar}, Devorah would have required {\i chalitza} and {\i yibum} would have been forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, why did the {\i mishna} specifically mention that Levi performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel? The {\i ma\u8217'amar} did not create the problem \u8212- {\i zika} alone was sufficient to pull Rachel in and render Devorah a {\i tzarat ervah}. The {\i ma\u8217'amar} is mentioned not to establish the prohibition but to address a different question entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's mention of {\i ma\u8217'amar} is {\i l\u8217'apuki mi\u8217'Beit Shammai d\u8217'amri ma\u8217'amar koneh kinyan gamur} \u8212- to exclude the position of Beit Shammai, who hold that {\i ma\u8217'amar} constitutes a full acquisition, equivalent to {\i nisuin}. According to Beit Shammai, one might have thought that if Levi performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel, Rachel was now fully his wife. In that case, when Shimon subsequently became the surviving brother, Rachel would be a complete {\i tzarat ervah} \u8212- not merely a quasi-{\i tzarat ervah} on the strength of {\i zika} \u8212- and Devorah could theoretically walk free without {\i chalitza}, since she is the {\i tzarah} of a genuine {\i ervah}. The {\i mishna} teaches that we do not rule like Beit Shammai: even with {\i ma\u8217'amar}, Rachel is not fully acquired, {\i chalitza} remains necessary, and {\i yibum} with Devorah is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i mishna}\u8217's structure is this: {\i zika} itself pulls Rachel in and creates the problem; {\i ma\u8217'amar} does not solve it by making Rachel a complete {\i nisuin}-level wife. Doing {\i chalitza} with Devorah is required, and doing {\i yibum} with Devorah is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nechman\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having explained Rav Ashi\u8217's reading, the {\i gemara} now turns the tables. {\i U\u8217'l\u8217'Rav Nachman kashya d\u8217'Rav Ashi} \u8212- according to Rav Nechman, who holds {\i ein zika} and maintained that Rachel was only a problem because of {\i ma\u8217'amar}, how does our {\i mishna} work? If {\i zika} is legally inert, why does the {\i mishna} appear to exclude cases where Levi dies before Leah is divorced, implying that the {\i zika} at that moment creates a problem?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nechman\u8217's answer, as the {\i gemara} reconstructs it, is that the {\i mishna} does not necessarily exclude that scenario. One might say: {\i hu hadin d\u8217'afilu metat ach v\u8217'achar kach gerush, tzarata muteret} \u8212- even if Levi died first and only then Leah was divorced, the case would still be permissible, since {\i ein zika} and the {\i zika} creates no legal problem. If so, what is the {\i mishna} actually excluding?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario A\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The simplest scenario \u8212- Scenario A \u8212- is precisely the one the {\i mishna} describes: Leah is divorced before Levi dies, before anything else unfolds. Leah is out of the picture from the very beginning. This is the easiest case and clearly presents no problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario B\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Scenario B is that Levi dies first and then Leah is divorced. According to those who hold {\i yesh zika}, this might immediately create a problem at the moment Levi dies, since Shimon acquires a {\i zika} to Devorah while Leah is still in the picture. According to those who hold {\i ein zika}, however, Scenario B is just as permissible as Scenario A \u8212- the {\i zika} is meaningless, and as long as Leah is out of the picture before Shimon dies, there is no issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario C\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Scenario C is more complex: Levi dies, Shimon performs {\i yibum} with Devorah and thereby marries her, and then \u8212- while married to both Devorah and Leah \u8212- he divorces Leah before he himself dies. In this scenario, there was a period when Shimon was simultaneously married to both women. Even though Leah was out of the picture before Shimon died, the question is whether that matters. This scenario is what the {\i gemara} designates as {\i kanas v\u8217'achar kach geireish} \u8212- he married Devorah and only afterward divorced Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin vs.\u160 ?Mita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} to a fundamental dispute about when {\i tzarat ervah} is determined. One opinion holds {\i nisuin rishonim mappilin} \u8212- the relevant moment is the time of the original marriage; the question is whether the two women were ever simultaneously married to the same man. Another opinion holds {\i mita mapelet} \u8212- everything is determined at the moment of death; if the {\i ervah} is out of the picture when the husband dies, there is no problem regardless of the marital history. The {\i gemara} notes that this dispute was articulated by earlier {\i amoraim}: one position is associated with Rabbi Yirmiyah\u8217's reading ({\i mi sheshana zu lo shana zu}, that the different {\i mishnayot} reflect different {\i tannaim}), and the other with Rava\u8217's position that it is all one {\i tanna} and everything goes by the moment of death.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yirmiyah & Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yirmiyah View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one follows Rabbi Yirmiyah\u8217's approach \u8212- that {\i tzarat ervah} is determined by the time of marriage, i.e., {\i nisuin rishonim mappilin} \u8212- then the criterion is whether the two women were ever simultaneously married to the same man. Under this view, Scenario A and Scenario B are both permitted: in neither case was Shimon simultaneously married to both Leah and Devorah. Scenario C, however, is problematic, because Shimon was indeed married to both women at the same time, even though Leah was eventually divorced before he died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What Mishna Excludes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This gives the {\i gemara} a clear answer to the question of what the {\i mishna} is excluding. Since, under {\i ein zika}, Scenario B is permissible \u8212- the {\i zika} creates no problem \u8212- what remains to be excluded is Scenario C, the case of {\i kanas v\u8217'achar kach geireish}: Shimon married Devorah and only later divorced Leah. That case is forbidden because they were simultaneously married at some point, and {\i nisuin rishonim} determines the status. The {\i mishna}\u8217's emphasis that Leah was divorced first is not merely incidental \u8212- it is precisely excluding the scenario in which Shimon was ever married to both women at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} makes this explicit: {\i al kurchak k\u8217'Rabbi Yirmiyah s\u8217'vira lei} \u8212- if one holds {\i ein zika} and reads the {\i mishna} consistently, one is compelled to follow Rabbi Yirmiyah\u8217's view that the critical moment is the time of marriage, not the time of death. Only then is there a meaningful case to exclude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, holds that everything goes by the moment of death \u8212- {\i mita mapelet}. Under Rava\u8217's view, any divorce before the husband\u8217's death is sufficient, regardless of the marital history. If Leah was divorced at any point before Shimon died, Devorah is permitted. This means Scenario A, Scenario B, and even Scenario C are all permissible, because in all three cases Leah was out of the picture at the moment of Shimon\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Rava holds that all three scenarios of divorce work, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i zu hi l\u8217'me\u8217'utei mai?} \u8212- what does the {\i mishna}\u8217's formulation actually exclude? There must be some case that does not work when {\i geirushin} is in play. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i meis b\u8217'lo geireish} \u8212- the case being excluded is one where Levi died and Shimon acquired a {\i zika} to Devorah, and then Shimon himself died without ever divorcing Leah. In that situation, if one holds {\i yesh zika} as Rav Ashi does, the {\i zika} alone was sufficient to render Devorah a {\i tzarat ervah} at the moment of Levi\u8217's death, and since Leah was never divorced, the problem was never resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking conclusion. The one case excluded under Rava\u8217's view is precisely the case where Shimon\u8217's connection to Devorah was at its weakest \u8212- a mere {\i zika}, without {\i yibum}, without {\i ma\u8217'amar}. Yet that weak {\i zika}, on the view of {\i yesh zika}, is enough to create a lasting problem. All scenarios in which an actual divorce occurred at any point before Shimon\u8217's death are fine under Rava; the only failure case is the {\i zika} without any subsequent divorce whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i ella i savra leh k\u8217'Rav Nachman} \u8212- but if one holds like Rav Nechman that {\i ein zika}, then under Rava\u8217's framework there is nothing left to exclude, because every scenario of divorce before death works and the {\i zika} is meaningless. {\i Al kurchak k\u8217'Rav Ashi s\u8217'vira lei} \u8212- therefore Rava must hold like Rav Ashi, that {\i yesh zika}. Only then can Rava\u8217's framework exclude anything at all, and the excluded case is the {\i zika} without any divorce.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek & Status Quo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to a different category of cases. Up to this point, the discussion concerned {\i erva} who was definitively married or definitively divorced, and the question was about the sequence and timing of those events. The {\i mishna} now addresses situations where the {\i erva}\u8217's status is itself uncertain \u8212- she may be married, or she may not be. The two categories of uncertainty are {\i safek kiddushin} (uncertain betrothal) and {\i safek gerushin} (uncertain divorce).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In all such cases, the {\i mishna} rules: {\i tzarotehen choltzot v\u8217'lo misyabmot} \u8212- the co-wife performs {\i chalitza} and does not do {\i yibum}. She cannot perform {\i yibum} because perhaps the {\i erva} is in the picture and that would create a forbidden situation. But she cannot simply walk away either, because perhaps the {\i erva} is not in the picture and some form of resolution is required. The solution is {\i chalitza} without {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} defines {\i safek kiddushin} with the following scenario: a man declares betrothal to a woman and throws the ring between them, and it is unclear whether it fell closer to his domain or to hers \u8212- {\i safek karov lo safek karov lah}. As Rashi explains, the relevant {\i kinyan} here is that of {\i arba amos} \u8212- within a person\u8217's four cubits, objects can be acquired. If the couple were standing eight cubits apart, with their respective {\i arba amos} meeting at the midpoint, and the ring landed precisely at that midpoint, it is genuinely unclear whether the woman\u8217's {\i kinyan} of {\i arba amos} was effected. That is {\i safek kiddushin} \u8212- we do not know if she was ever betrothed to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Gerushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Safek gerushin} is defined differently. Rather than a parallel scenario of a {\i get} that fell ambiguously between the two parties, the {\i mishna} instead presents three cases of a {\i get} that is valid {\i d\u8217'oraisa} but invalidated {\i m\u8217'd\u8217'rabbanan}, and which is therefore treated as a quasi-{\i gerushin} of uncertain status. The three cases are: a {\i get} written in the husband\u8217's own handwriting but lacking witnesses ({\i kasav b\u8217'ksav yado v\u8217'ein alav edim}); a {\i get} bearing witnesses but lacking a date ({\i yesh alav edim v\u8217'ein bo zman}); and a {\i get} with a date but bearing only one witness rather than two ({\i yesh bo zman v\u8217'ein bo ela ed echad}). The {\i mishna}\u8217's phrasing is worth noting: the language flows from one case into the next, with each statement introducing an element that the next case then takes as its starting point \u8212- a mnemonic elegance in the oral formulation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerushin vs.\u160 ?Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} observes an asymmetry. By {\i kiddushin}, the {\i mishna} gave the parallel case of a ring thrown between them \u8212- a genuine {\i safek} about whether the {\i kinyan} was effected. By {\i gerushin}, however, the {\i mishna} did not give the parallel case of a {\i get} thrown toward the woman, where it is unclear whether it entered her domain or remained in his. Why not? {\i V\u8217'ilu b\u8217'gerushin safek karov lo safek karov lah lo katani} \u8212- this case is simply not mentioned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Status Quo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava explains the omission on the basis of {\i chazakah} \u8212- the principle of status quo. In the case of a {\i get} thrown ambiguously between them, the woman was definitively married before the act. We do not know if that status changed. Since her status quo is that she remains married, we do not treat her as a {\i safek gerusha} \u8212- she retains the {\i chazakah} of a married woman, and there is no {\i safek} to speak of. The {\i get} that may or may not have reached her does not displace that presumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might apply the same logic in reverse to {\i safek kiddushin}: before the ring was thrown, the woman was definitively unmarried. Her status quo is that she is single. If we keep everyone at their status quo, she should simply remain unmarried, and no {\i safek kiddushin} should be recognized at all. If that is so, her co-wife Devorah\u8217's status quo \u8212- that she was permitted to do {\i yibum} with the {\i yavam} \u8212- should equally be preserved, and there should be no reason to require {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses this logic: {\i i hachi, b\u8217'kiddushin nami neima, isha zo b\u8217'chezkat heter l\u8217'yavam omedes} \u8212- apply the same status quo principle to Devorah in the {\i safek kiddushin} case. Before the questionable {\i kiddushin} took place, Devorah was permitted for {\i yibum}. {\i Umisafek ata ba u\u8217'osra} \u8212- why should a doubt change that? {\i Al tosrena misafek} \u8212- do not impose a prohibition on account of doubt. Let Devorah retain her status quo and allow {\i yibum} without restriction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cost of Chumra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Message Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: even though the status quo argument is valid in principle, in the case of {\i safek kiddushin} we are nonetheless stringent and require {\i chalitza} rather than permitting {\i yibum}. The concern is the message conveyed to the public. If Reuven declares his intent to perform {\i yibum} with Devorah and is told he may not, this prohibition sends a signal: people will infer that Rachel must in fact be married to Shimon, since that is the only reason {\i yibum} would be denied. If people conclude that Rachel is married to Shimon, they will draw consequences from that: any subsequent {\i kiddushin} that Rachel or someone close to her entered into will be viewed as having failed. {\i Ud\u8217'batar lav kiddushin} \u8212- those subsequent acts are actually valid, but the public message will lead people to treat them as void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Specifically, the {\i gemara} identifies two problematic consequences: if Shimon subsequently performs a definite {\i kiddushin} to Leah ({\i kiddushei vadai l\u8217'achota}), people will assume it was ineffective because Rachel was already his wife; and if another man \u8212- let us say Barak \u8212- performs a definite {\i kiddushin} to Rachel herself after the ambiguous first {\i kiddushin}, people will assume that too was ineffective. Both of these real marriages will be undermined by the false inference that the first {\i safek kiddushin} was real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Consistency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here is the critical insight: the {\i gemara} notes that the relevant public signal is not the absence of {\i yibum} but the performance of {\i chalitza}. And {\i chalitza}, in the case of {\i safek kiddushin}, is entirely consistent with the true legal reality. What is the status quo? Rachel was not married to Shimon \u8212- that is the {\i chazakah}, since we go by the status quo of being unmarried. {\i Chalitza} with Devorah sends precisely that message: the reason {\i chalitza} was performed is that Rachel is not in the picture, that there was no operative {\i kiddushin}. People who see {\i chalitza} will understand that Rachel is out of the equation, and the subsequent {\i kiddushin} to Leah and to Rachel herself will be understood as valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is what makes the {\i chumra} here cost-free. The {\i halacha} is that Rachel, by status quo, is not married to Shimon after a {\i safek kiddushin}. Demanding {\i chalitza} from Devorah conveys the accurate message. The only restriction is that Reuven is not permitted to do {\i yibum} \u8212- but the public does not see the absence of {\i yibum}; they see the performance of {\i chalitza}, which accurately reflects the underlying legal reality. {\i Meida yedaya d\u8217'chumra b\u8217'alma hu} \u8212- people will understand that this is simply a stringency. The {\i chumra} costs nothing because it sends no misleading signal. {\i V\u8217'kevan d\u8217'kamatba\u8217'ei chalitzah} \u8212- since {\i chalitza} is demanded, the right message is broadcast.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wrong Message\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of {\i safek gerushin}, however, the situation is reversed. Here Rachel was definitively married to Shimon, and the question is whether she was divorced. Her status quo is that she remains married. If Devorah were required to perform {\i chalitza}, people would ask: why is {\i chalitza} being done? Isn\u8217't Rachel still in the picture as a {\i tzarat ervah}? The answer they would draw is: the Rabbis must have investigated and determined that Rachel was in fact divorced \u8212- otherwise, why would {\i chalitza} be occurring at all? {\i Kamu bah rabbanan b\u8217'gita d\u8217'gita maalya hu} \u8212- they will assume that the Rabbis validated the {\i get} and established that Rachel is genuinely divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Chalitza Demanded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That inference leads directly to harmful consequences. If people conclude that Rachel is divorced, they will then reason that {\i yibum} with Devorah is possible \u8212- since Rachel the {\i ervah} is out of the picture. Reuven or someone like him will come to perform {\i yibum} when Rachel is in fact not divorced and Devorah remains a genuine {\i tzarat ervah}. {\i V\u8217'asu l\u8217'yibumei l\u8217'tzarah} \u8212- this is a serious {\i issur}, potentially implicating {\i karet}. Performing {\i chalitza} in the case of {\i safek gerushin} is therefore not a no-cost {\i chumra} \u8212- it actively generates the most dangerous leniency imaginable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, the {\i gemara} concludes, the Rabbis did not impose {\i chalitza} as a {\i chumra} in the case of {\i safek gerushin}. When the status quo says Rachel is married, Devorah retains her prior status of {\i tzarat ervah} and is simply permitted to go free \u8212- {\i muteret lashuk} \u8212- without any requirement of {\i chalitza}. The logic of status quo is preserved, and the dangerous message that {\i chalitza} would send is avoided entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fallen House Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a challenge from Abaye based on the case of a fallen house. The scenario is this: Shimon is married to both Rachel and Devorah. Rachel is Reuven\u8217's daughter, making her an {\i ervah} to Reuven. Shimon and Rachel go together and a house falls upon them \u8212- {\i nafal habayit alav v\u8217'al bat achiv} \u8212- and it is unknown which of them died first. {\i V\u8217'ein yadua eizeh meihem meis rishon.} The {\i mishna} rules: {\i tzarata choletzet v\u8217'lo misyabemes} \u8212- Devorah performs {\i chalitza} and does not do {\i yibum} with Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i safek} is this: if Rachel died first, then when Shimon subsequently died, only Devorah was his wife and Reuven could perform {\i yibum} normally. But if Shimon died first, then at the moment of his death both Rachel and Devorah were his wives, making Devorah a {\i tzarat ervah} to Reuven and forbidding {\i yibum} entirely. Since we do not know the order of death, the {\i mishna} requires {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Devorah\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's challenge to Rava\u8217's status quo principle is immediate: before the house fell, what was Devorah\u8217's status quo? She was {\i muteret lashuk} \u8212- she was a {\i tzarat ervah} and therefore not subject to {\i yibum} at all, fully permitted to the outside world. That was her {\i chazakah}. {\i Umisafek ata ba l\u8217'osra} \u8212- why should the doubt created by the building collapse change that status? {\i Al tosrena misafek} \u8212- keep her at her status quo and let her go free without {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rare Event\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} acknowledges the force of this logic but answers on the basis of frequency. In this case, requiring {\i chalitza} is indeed a {\i chumra}, but it is a {\i chumra} whose cost is negligible because the underlying event is extraordinarily rare. How often does a house fall on a husband and his relative such that the order of death is unknown? Since the scenario almost never occurs, even if demanding {\i chalitza} sends a potentially misleading message, the real-world cost of that message is minimal. The Rabbis had no difficulty being stringent in a case that barely ever arises. {\i Mapoles d\u8217'lo shechiachi lo gazru behu rabbanan} \u8212- for a case of a building collapse, which is uncommon, the Rabbis did not issue the same decree as for a more common situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common vs.\u160 ?Rare\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Divorce, by contrast, is unfortunately common. Cases of {\i safek gerushin} \u8212- where the validity of a {\i get} is uncertain \u8212- arise with sufficient regularity that the Rabbis had to think carefully about what message demanding {\i chalitza} would send to the public at large. The rarity of the {\i mapoles} case and the frequency of divorce cases thus lead to opposite outcomes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Verification Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} makes this distinction explicit. In the case of {\i safek gerushin}, people understand that the Rabbis have the capacity and authority to investigate the validity of a {\i get}. If they see {\i chalitza} being performed in a case where Rachel is apparently still in the picture as a potential {\i ervah}, they will naturally assume that the Rabbis looked into it and determined that the {\i get} was in fact valid. {\i Kamu bah rabbanan b\u8217'gita} \u8212- they will conclude the Rabbis verified the divorce. And from that conclusion they will infer that {\i yibum} is permissible, when in truth Rachel remains married and Devorah is still a genuine {\i tzarat ervah}. The cost of demanding {\i chalitza} in the {\i safek gerushin} case is therefore real and substantial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the fallen house, no such inference is available. Determining the order in which two people died when a building collapsed is not something people associate with Rabbinic investigation and verification. Nobody will assume the Rabbis conducted an inquiry into which death came first. {\i Mi kamu bei Rabbanan b\u8217'mapoles?} \u8212- the Rabbis are not understood as having the tools to determine that. Therefore, if {\i chalitza} is performed, people will accept that it is simply a precautionary measure under uncertainty, and no harmful inference will be drawn. The {\i chumra} of demanding {\i chalitza} in the {\i mapoles} case carries no risk of leading to the forbidden {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 31\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Gerushin intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is officially 34, but we continue from where we left off, picking up at the bottom of 31b. The {\i mishna} deals with a case of {\i safek kiddushin} and {\i safek gerushin} \u8212- doubtful betrothal and doubtful divorce \u8212- and addresses what is done with the {\i tzaras ervah}, the co-wife of a forbidden relative, in such circumstances: whether she requires {\i chalitza} or {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek vs.\u160 ?Psul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} makes a pointed observation: the case of {\i safek gerushin} presented in the {\i mishna} is not, strictly speaking, a genuine {\i safek gerushin} at all. It is rather a case of a {\i gerushin} that is {\i pasul derabanan} \u8212- rabbinically invalid divorce. The {\i gemara} is therefore working from the assumption that a classic {\i safek gerushin} would carry different consequences entirely. In such a case, the woman would retain her {\i chazaka} \u8212- her presumed status \u8212- and remain {\i eshes ish}, a married woman, with the result that the {\i tzaras ervah} would retain her status as exempt from {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the tension the {\i gemara} is working through. Being {\i mechamir} \u8212- stringent \u8212- sometimes comes without cost, but in this case it would impose a serious burden: demanding {\i chalitza} where none is halachically required creates its own complications. Therefore the {\i gemara} leaves the matter on the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level, holding that such a woman retains her status as {\i eshes ish}, her co-wife retains her status as {\i tzaras ervah}, and the co-wife walks away without {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gittin case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get thrown case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now challenges this framework by invoking a parallel case. It asks: is there not a case of genuine {\i safek gerushin} in the {\i mishna} in {\i Gittin}, which would produce the same consequence \u8212- namely, that the co-wife requires {\i chalitza}? The {\i mishna} there discusses the case of {\i ha-omedes bereshus harabim} \u8212- a woman standing in the public domain \u8212- where her husband throws a {\i get} toward her. If the {\i get} lands closer to her, she is divorced; if closer to him, she is not divorced; and if it lands exactly at the midpoint \u8212- {\i mechtza al mechtza} \u8212- she is in a state of doubt: {\i megureshes ve-eino megureshes}, divorced and not divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} in {\i Gittin} then asks: what is the practical significance of this doubtful status? Two implications are given. First, if the husband is a {\i kohen}, he cannot simply decide retrospectively that perhaps no divorce occurred and remain married \u8212- since she may in fact be divorced, she would be a {\i grusha}, whom a {\i kohen} cannot remarry. Second \u8212- and this is the {\i nafka mina} directly relevant here \u8212- if she is an {\i ervah}, her co-wife is a questionable {\i tzaras ervah} and therefore cannot simply walk away. She might be {\i megureshes}, in which case the co-wife is obligated in {\i yibum}, yet {\i yibum} cannot be performed because she might equally be {\i eina megureshes} and thus a true {\i tzaras ervah}. The ruling is therefore {\i ba\u8217'ei chalitza} \u8212- {\i chalitza} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i gemara} asks: we see in {\i Gittin} that in a genuine case of {\i safek gerushin}, the {\i halacha} is {\i chalitza}, not that the woman simply retains her {\i chazaka} and walks away without anything. The {\i gemara} there does not say we fear that demanding {\i chalitza} will lead to {\i yibum} \u8212- it simply demands {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safeka D\u8217'Oraisa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To this the {\i gemara} responds: {\i haya itmara alah} \u8212- it was already said about that case. Both Rabba and Rav Yosef explain: {\i hacha bishtei kitei eidim askinan} \u8212- there we are actually dealing with two pairs of witnesses. One pair testifies that the {\i get} landed closer to her; another pair testifies that it landed closer to him. The reason the woman does not simply retain her {\i chazaka} of being married \u8212- and the co-wife her {\i chazaka} of being a {\i tzaras ervah} \u8212- is not because of an abstract doubt but because there is direct testimony opposing the {\i chazaka}. The testimony is contradicted, to be sure, but there is nevertheless direct testimony working against the presumed status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, the {\i gemara} says, creates a {\i sefika d\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- a genuine biblical-level doubt. The distinction is this: when there is merely an abstract doubt and nothing more, the principle is to rely on the {\i chazaka} and preserve the status quo. But when there is concrete testimony working against the {\i chazaka} \u8212- even if that testimony is itself contradicted \u8212- the {\i chazaka} is displaced, and one enters the domain of genuine {\i safek}. In such a case it is a real {\i d\u8217'oraisa} doubt, not a rabbinic stringency, and therefore {\i chalitza} is demanded. {\i Chalitza nibei bah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our {\i mishna}, by contrast, is not talking about two pairs of witnesses. As the {\i gemara} explains: {\i u\u8217'masnisin d\u8217'hacha b\u8217'kas achas} \u8212- our {\i mishna} is dealing with a single pair of split witnesses, or with an abstract doubt, not with two opposing pairs of witnesses directly testifying against the {\i chazaka}. That would at most be a rabbinic {\i safek}, meaning that at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level one would still follow the status quo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then anticipates the question: how do we know that the {\i mishna} in {\i Gittin} is discussing two pairs of witnesses, and how do we know that our {\i mishna}\u8217's case of {\i kiddushin} is similarly a case of abstract doubt rather than contradictory testimony? The answer is that the cases are parallel: just as the {\i kiddushin} case involves a single pair whose members disagree \u8212- not two full pairs testifying in opposition \u8212- so too the {\i gerushin} case follows the same structure. {\i Mah kiddushin b\u8217'kas achas af geirushin b\u8217'kas achas.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might press further: how do we actually know the {\i kiddushin} case is one of abstract doubt? Perhaps it too involves contradictory pairs of witnesses. The {\i gemara} responds: if the {\i kiddushin} case involved two full pairs of contradictory witnesses, then how could it have been said earlier that we would not fear the {\i chalitza} leading to {\i yibum}? If there are witnesses testifying directly that the ring was clearly closer to her \u8212- that is, that she is genuinely married and the co-wife genuinely a {\i tzaras ervah} \u8212- then how could one be unconcerned about the possibility of {\i yibum} following? Rashi and Tosafot note the somewhat self-referential character of this argument, but the essential logic stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses cancel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then makes a further and crucial point. Even granting the scenario of two contradictory pairs of witnesses, one might have thought that conflicting direct testimony displaces the {\i chazaka} and leaves a genuine blank-slate doubt. The {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i beshnei kitei eidim nami sefeika d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- even with two contradictory pairs of witnesses the result is only a rabbinic {\i safek}. The reason is that when witnesses contradict one another, one does not say \u8220"there is testimony against the {\i chazaka}, so the {\i chazaka} is undermined and we are left with a genuine doubt.\u8221" Rather, one says: the testimonies contradict one another, so we reject all the testimony entirely, and we are restored to the {\i chazaka}. This is a fundamental principle: {\i okei terei bahadei terei} \u8212- put two against two \u8212- and return to the presumed status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Shatya case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} illustrates this principle with the case of the property of Bar Shatya. Bar Shatya was a person who experienced episodes of mental incapacity, and the question arose concerning transactions he conducted: was he of sound mind at the time? He sold some property. Two witnesses came and testified that he sold it while he was of clear mind \u8212- {\i keshu chalim zavin}. Another two witnesses came and said he sold it while he was of unsound mind \u8212- {\i keshu shoteh zavin}. What does one do? Rav Ashi ruled: {\i okei terei lahadei terei} \u8212- place two against two, cancel all the testimony, and restore the property to Bar Shatya\u8217's presumed ownership \u8212- {\i ve-ara okma be-chezkas Bar Shatya}. That was the default position. We wipe the testimony from the slate and return to the status quo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This demonstrates that even in the face of contradictory witnesses, the {\i halacha} is to restore the {\i chazaka}, not to treat the situation as an open {\i safek}. This brings us back to the central difficulty: if the {\i halacha} in every case of {\i safek gerushin} \u8212- whether abstract doubt or contradictory witnesses \u8212- is to rely on the {\i chazaka}, then why does the {\i mishna} in {\i Gittin} rule that {\i chalitza} is required? And if the answer is that we are nevertheless {\i mechamir} and demand {\i chalitza} in such a case even though the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} principle would be to rely on the {\i chazaka}, why did our {\i mishna} not present a classic {\i safek get} to illustrate this rule?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds by invoking the principle {\i yagid alav rei\u8217'o} \u8212- let context and juxtaposition illuminate the matter. He argues: {\i tana be-kiddushin ve-hu hadin le-geirushin} \u8212- the {\i mishna} taught the rule by means of the {\i kiddushin} case, and the same applies equally to {\i geirushin}. The reason the {\i mishna} gave a different type of scenario by {\i get} was not to exclude the {\i kiddushin} example from applying to {\i gerushin}, but simply to add another scenario. Similarly, {\i tana be-geirushin ve-hu hadin le-kiddushin} \u8212- the {\i mishna} gave an example by {\i get} of a rabbinically invalid document, and the same would be true if one had a {\i kiddushin} that was rabbinically defective in a parallel way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The essential point: do not read the scenarios in the {\i mishna} as mutually exclusive. The {\i mishna} gives one set of examples by {\i kiddushin} and another set by {\i geirushin}, but both sets of cases are halachically valid in both domains. And in terms of the underlying rule, even though the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} principle is to follow the {\i chazaka}, at the rabbinic level {\i chalitza} is demanded \u8212- and this is true for a classic {\i safek get} just as it is for a rabbinically defective one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, pushes back. If cases can simply be learned from one context to the other, what is the force of the word {\i zehu} \u8212- \u8220"this is the case\u8221" \u8212- which the {\i mishna} uses? That word implies precision: {\i these} are the cases, as though to exclude others. {\i Mai zehu d\u8217'katani?} \u8212- why does the {\i mishna} say \u8220"this is the case of {\i safek kiddushin}\u8221" and \u8220"this is the case of {\i safek geirushin}\u8221" if all cases apply in both directions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore offers a more nuanced formulation: {\i kol she\u8217'yesh be-kiddushin yesh be-geirushin} \u8212- every case of {\i safek} that applies to {\i kiddushin} applies equally to {\i geirushin}, but {\i yesh be-geirushin mah she-ein be-kiddushin} \u8212- there are cases in {\i geirushin} that do not apply to {\i kiddushin}. The {\i zehu} by {\i geirushin} is therefore {\i lav davka} \u8212- it is not exclusive; {\i geirushin} is a broader category and includes the classic {\i safek} as well. But the {\i zehu} by {\i kiddushin} is precise \u8212- it excludes the particular {\i psulim} of {\i get} that are not relevant to {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeman issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What specifically is excluded? The {\i psul} of lacking a date \u8212- {\i ein bo zman} \u8212- which invalidates a {\i get} at a rabbinic level, does not apply to a {\i shtar kiddushin}. The other defects mentioned in the {\i mishna} \u8212- such as the document being written in the husband\u8217's handwriting without witnesses, or having only one witness \u8212- would equally apply to {\i kiddushei shtar}, betrothal effected by document. But the absence of a date: that {\i psul} is unique to {\i geirushin} and does not render a {\i shtar kiddushin} invalid. That is why the {\i mishna} uses {\i zehu} by {\i kiddushin} \u8212- not to exclude the classic {\i safek}, but to distinguish the cases that are genuinely unique to {\i get} from those that apply to both.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zeman reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeman reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the absence of a {\i zman} is a {\i psul} in {\i get} but not in {\i shtar kiddushin}, the {\i gemara} now asks directly: {\i u\u8217'mipnei mah lo tiknu zman be-kiddushin?} Why did the Rabbis not require a date in a {\i shtar kiddushin}, and correspondingly declare one invalid without it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To answer this, the {\i gemara} first identifies the reasons for requiring a date in a {\i get}. Two reasons are given. The first is {\i mishum peirot}: from the moment a woman is divorced, the husband forfeits his right to benefit from the fruits of her {\i nichsei milug} \u8212- the property she brought into the marriage whose usufruct belonged to him during the marriage. Rashi cites the {\i gemara} in {\i Gittin}, in the chapter {\i Kol ha-Get}, which explains: the date is required so that it can be determined from when she may claim from her husband the fruits he consumed from her property. Since from the moment the husband wrote the {\i get} \u8212- the moment he set his mind to divorce her \u8212- he forfeits the right to the fruits, the woman needs to know precisely when that moment was. Without a date, the husband could always claim he had only just now given her the {\i get}, and dispute her claim to recover what he consumed over the years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second reason is {\i mishum bas achoso} \u8212- so that a husband cannot protect his wife from consequences. The scenario is this: a man divorces his wife, perhaps because she committed adultery and faces severe consequences, but he wishes to shield her. He might therefore give her an undated {\i get}, which she could then present and claim pre-dates the act. Once a date is required, this manipulation is impossible \u8212- the witnesses who signed the {\i get} ensure the date is accurate, and predating is prevented.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusah no Peirot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i gemara} asks: the first reason \u8212- {\i mishum peirot} \u8212- clearly does not apply to a {\i shtar kiddushin}, and this is straightforward. An {\i arusah} \u8212- a woman who is betrothed but not yet fully married \u8212- has no {\i peirot} rights. The husband\u8217's entitlement to the fruits of her property begins only from {\i nissu\u8217'in}, not from {\i kiddushin}. So there is no question of when to date the transition of those rights, and therefore no reason to require a date in the {\i shtar kiddushin} for this purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef majority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the second reason \u8212- {\i mishum bas achoso}, the concern about covering up adultery \u8212- would seem to apply equally to {\i kiddushin}. If a woman receives an undated {\i shtar kiddushin}, she could commit adultery and then claim she was only married afterward. Why then do the Rabbis not require a date in a {\i shtar kiddushin} to prevent this? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i mishum de-ika de-mikadesh be-chaspa ve-ika de-mikadesh be-shtara lo tiknu rabbanan zman} \u8212- because people perform {\i kiddushin} both with money and with a document, and since {\i kiddushei kesef} is far more common, in those cases there is no document and therefore no precise record of the date of {\i kiddushin} at all. Since we are already not worried about knowing the exact date when {\i kiddushin} is performed with money, it would be inconsistent to suddenly invalidate a {\i shtar kiddushin} for lacking a date. We are not going to impose a requirement of {\i zman} on the document simply because the parties chose to use a {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Aha the son of Rav Yosef challenges this reasoning by pointing to the case of slaves: {\i ve-ha avadim} \u8212- slaves can also be acquired either with money or with a document, yet the Rabbis did require a date in a slave acquisition document. Why does the prevalence of non-document acquisition not similarly exempt the slave document from the {\i zman} requirement? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i hasam rova be-shtara} \u8212- there, the majority of slave acquisitions are in fact done with a document, since owners naturally want documented proof of property. Given that the majority use a {\i shtar}, the requirement of {\i zman} applies, and the minority who use {\i kesef} are simply not the reference point. {\i Hacha rova be-chesef} \u8212- here, by contrast, the vast majority of {\i kiddushin} are performed with money, and the document is the exception. The exception does not drive the requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second answer is offered: {\i ibayis eima mishum d\u8217'lo efshar} \u8212- or alternatively, it is simply not practically workable to require a date in a {\i shtar kiddushin}, because it would serve no purpose. {\i Heichi ne\u8217'avid?} \u8212- what would you do with such a document? If the {\i shtar kiddushin} is held by her \u8212- {\i nanchayei gabei didah} \u8212- she can simply erase it or claim to have lost it. She has every incentive to do so, since the whole purpose of the date would be to establish when she became married and thereby limit her ability to claim that a subsequent act of adultery predated the marriage. So she is not the right party to hold it. {\i Lihvei gabei didei} \u8212- perhaps the husband should hold it? But the same problem arises in reverse: {\i zimnin d\u8217'basar chosno mechapei lah} \u8212- perhaps he will want to protect her, and he too will lose or suppress the document. {\i Linhvei gabei eidim} \u8212- perhaps the witnesses should hold it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} to a fundamental principle: if the witnesses remember the event, {\i leisavu v\u8217'lisahadu} \u8212- let them come and testify from memory. And if they do not remember, allowing them to look at the document creates a different problem: {\i zimnin d\u8217'chazu me\u8217'hai ksav} \u8212- they may see what is written and testify based on it, not on their actual recollection. And the {\i Torah} says {\i mipichem v\u8217'lo mipi ksavam} \u8212- testimony must come from your mouths, from your memory, not from what you read in a document. So having the witnesses hold the document does not help.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then challenges its own reasoning: {\i ei hachi be-geirushin nami} \u8212- if this is the problem, should the same not apply to a {\i get}? There too, people could lose or erase the document. The answer is obvious: {\i hasam le-hatzala didah ka\u8217'asi} \u8212- by a {\i get}, the document serves to protect her. She needs to produce the {\i get} to establish her status as a divorced woman eligible to remarry. She therefore has every incentive to preserve it. {\i Hacha le-chovsa didah ka\u8217'asi} \u8212- by {\i kiddushin}, the document is a liability for her, not a protection. So she will dispose of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raise a pointed question about the {\i mipichem v\u8217'lo mipi ksavam} principle. They note: the entire purpose of a signed {\i shtar} is that it can be produced as evidence. Why would witnesses need to testify from memory at all? They could simply present the signed document to the court without saying anything, and the document itself would serve as the testimony. Tosafot answer: that would indeed work perfectly well, and if witnesses actually produced the document and refrained from testifying, the signed document would be accepted. The concern is rather that witnesses might not do this \u8212- they might read the document and then come to court and testify as though from memory when they do not in fact remember, essentially laundering their testimony through what they read. {\i Aval chaishinan shema lo ya\u8217'idu shekach ra\u8217'u bashtar ela shema ya\u8217'idu al hama\u8217'aseh atzmo k\u8217'ilu heim zochrim v\u8217'ein heim zochrim ela al pi haksav.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot further clarify: if a witness wrote down notes and then re-reads them, such that reading them actually causes him genuinely to recall the event, and he testifies from that authentic recollection, that is permissible. The {\i gemara} in {\i Kesubos} (chapter 2) says a person may write down his testimony in a document and testify from it even many years later \u8212- {\i ve-hu shezochra me\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- provided that he genuinely remembers from himself. But {\i ein zochra me\u8217'atzmo lo} \u8212- if he does not actually remember and is merely relaying what he read, that testimony is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the problematic case is narrow: testifying about an event one does not remember, not by producing the signed document, but by claiming to remember based on having read the document. Producing the document itself is fine. Testifying from genuine memory prompted by the document is also fine. Only the middle case \u8212- purporting to testify from memory when the \u8220"memory\u8221" is really just what one read \u8212- is invalid. Rabbeinu Tam adds that the contemporary practice of witnesses sending their written testimony to court by letter is similarly valid, since there they genuinely do remember \u8212- {\i keivan she-heim zochrim edusam} \u8212- and the written form is merely the medium of communication, not a substitute for memory.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maamar case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now presents a new scenario. Three brothers are married to three unrelated women \u8212- {\i gimmel achin nesuin gimmel nochriyos} \u8212- and one of them dies. The second brother then performs {\i maamar} with the first brother\u8217's widow, and then he too dies. To illustrate concretely: Reuven, Shimon, and Levi. Reuven is married to Rachel, Shimon to Devorah, Levi to another woman. Reuven dies. Shimon performs {\i maamar} \u8212- a quasi-betrothal in the context of {\i yibum}, effected like {\i kiddushin} with money, but not possessing full {\i d\u8217'oraisa} force \u8212- with Rachel. Then Shimon dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Levi now has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} to Devorah, coming from Shimon. He also has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} to Rachel, coming from Reuven. But since Shimon performed {\i maamar} with Rachel, Shimon has also created a quasi-connection to Rachel. Levi therefore has a dual connection to Rachel: one from Reuven and one from Shimon. He is connected to one woman through two separate deceased brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} rules: {\i harei elu choltzos v\u8217'lo mityabmos} \u8212- both women, Devorah and Rachel, do {\i chalitza} and not {\i yibum}. The {\i mishna} provides scriptural grounding: the verse states that when one brother dies, {\i yevamah yavo aleha} \u8212- the surviving brother comes upon his {\i yevama}. This is interpreted to mean {\i mi she\u8217'aleha zikas yavam echad} \u8212- only a woman connected to him through one brother, {\i v\u8217'lo she\u8217'aleha zikas shnei yevamin} \u8212- not a woman connected through two brothers-in-law. Rachel, with her dual connection, falls in the latter category and is therefore excluded from {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that most authorities hold {\i maamar} to be entirely rabbinic in status, and accordingly this entire {\i derasha} is understood as a rabbinic enactment. But for those who hold, like Beis Shammai, that {\i maamar} carries some {\i d\u8217'oraisa} weight, Rachel\u8217's dual status might indeed have genuine biblical significance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for why Devorah cannot receive {\i yibum} \u8212- that will be addressed by the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi Shimon takes a different position: {\i miyabem le-eizo she-yirtzeh v\u8217'choleitz la-shniya} \u8212- he may perform {\i yibum} with whichever one he wishes and {\i chalitza} with the other. Rebbi Shimon does not accept the intermediate position that {\i maamar} is \u8220"fifty percent.\u8221" He holds that {\i maamar} is either fully valid \u8212- one hundred percent \u8212- or completely void. If {\i maamar} is fully valid, then Rachel was fully married to Shimon, Reuven is out of the picture, and Rachel\u8217's connection to Levi comes only from Shimon; Levi may then do {\i yibum} with either Rachel or Devorah. If {\i maamar} is void, then Rachel\u8217's only connection is to Reuven, she and Devorah come from different brothers, and Levi may do {\i yibum} with both. The problematic case only arises if {\i maamar} is exactly \u8220"fifty percent\u8221" \u8212- creating a dual status \u8212- which Rebbi Shimon rejects. Since it is either all or nothing, Levi can do {\i yibum} with one and must do {\i chalitza} with the other \u8212- because if {\i maamar} worked, both come from Shimon, and if it did not, only Rachel comes from Reuven. In either scenario one {\i yibum} and one {\i chalitza} suffices.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezeira logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doraita exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now reframes the {\i mishna}\u8217's reasoning. It observes: {\i ei zikas beit yevamin de\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- if the problem of Rachel having a dual connection were a genuine {\i d\u8217'oraisa} issue, then {\i chalitza nami lo tiba\u8217'ei} \u8212- not only would {\i yibum} be forbidden, but {\i chalitza} too would not be required. If the {\i Torah} is simply excluding a woman with this double status from the household of the surviving brother, she should simply be able to walk away without any ceremony. The very fact that {\i chalitza} is demanded indicates that the problem is not {\i d\u8217'oraisa}. {\i Ela miderabbanan} \u8212- the entire framework of the double {\i zikah} as a {\i psul} is rabbinic, which follows naturally from the fact that {\i maamar} itself is rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Shema\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the problem is rabbinic, why is {\i chalitza} demanded from Rachel? {\i Gezeira shema yomru shtei yevamos haba\u8217'os mibeit echad mityabmos} \u8212- it is a preventive rabbinic decree, lest people say that two {\i yevamos} from the same household may both be taken in {\i yibum}. The scenario is this: Reuven may have died years ago, and people have forgotten. Everyone sees Shimon as Rachel\u8217's husband \u8212- he performed {\i maamar} with her, a public act of quasi-betrothal. Now Shimon dies, and Levi performs {\i yibum} with both Devorah and Rachel. Observers will think that a surviving brother performed {\i yibum} with both of his deceased brother\u8217's wives \u8212- which is incorrect. The proper rule is that {\i yibum} is performed with one, and the others receive {\i chalitza}. To prevent this misunderstanding, the Rabbis enacted that Levi should not perform {\i yibum} with Rachel. She is the apparent source of the confusion, and so she is designated for {\i chalitza}. The {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level permits {\i yibum} with both; it is this {\i gezeira} that prevents it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if that is so, why not permit Levi to do {\i yibum} with Devorah and {\i chalitza} with Rachel? That would be accurate {\i halacha} with no false impression created.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wrong order fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i gezeira shema yomru beit echad miktzato banuy umiktzato chalutz} \u8212- there is a concern that people will observe Levi doing {\i yibum} with one woman and {\i chalitza} with the other, and will conclude that whenever a man dies leaving multiple wives, the surviving brother may perform {\i yibum} with one and {\i chalitza} with the others. This is a misunderstanding, but it is not without halachic cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} probes this: why should we care if people draw that mistaken conclusion? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ei de-miyabeim ve-hadar chalitz} \u8212- the problem is not the misunderstanding per se, but its practical consequence. If people believe that one performs {\i yibum} with one wife and {\i chalitza} with another, they may do them in the wrong order: {\i dilma chalitz bereisha ve-hadar miyabeim} \u8212- perhaps they will first perform {\i chalitza} with the woman they are less interested in, and only then perform {\i yibum} with the other. This sequence is halachically fatal. Once {\i chalitza} has been performed, the surviving brother enters the category of one who has {\i not} built up his brother\u8217's house \u8212- {\i ba\u8217'asher lo yivneh} \u8212- and {\i rachmana amar keivan shelo bana shuv lo yivneh}: once he has not built, he may no longer build. The {\i chalitza} disqualifies him from subsequently performing {\i yibum} with any of the remaining {\i yevamos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore the Rabbis enacted the full stringency: both women must do {\i chalitza}, and neither may receive {\i yibum}. This comes at no halachic cost \u8212- an extra {\i chalitza} performed when not strictly required is far preferable to the risk that someone performs {\i chalitza} before {\i yibum} and thereby permanently forecloses the {\i yibum} option.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get negation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now introduces a further question. Suppose that after performing {\i maamar} with Rachel, Shimon \u8212- before he died \u8212- decided he no longer wished to proceed, and gave Rachel a {\i get}. Does this {\i get}, which was intended to negate the {\i maamar}, free Levi to perform {\i yibum} with Devorah? And might Levi even be free to perform {\i yibum} with Rachel herself, who would revert to her status as a {\i yevama} awaiting {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: {\i nosan get le-maamaro, hutra tzorasa, aval hi asurah} \u8212- if the second brother gave a {\i get} to negate his {\i maamar}, the co-wife Devorah is freed and becomes available for {\i yibum}, but Rachel herself remains forbidden to {\i yibum}. She requires {\i chalitza}, but not {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confusion risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why? {\i Demichlifa be-baalas get} \u8212- because she is liable to be confused with a woman who received a {\i get} addressed to the {\i zika} itself. The relevant background: if no {\i maamar} was performed and the surviving brother wishes to decline {\i yibum}, he performs {\i chalitza}. But there also exists the option of giving the {\i yevama} a {\i get} addressed to the {\i zika} \u8212- a {\i get le-zika}. Such a {\i get} is a rabbinic mechanism: it does not dissolve the {\i zika} at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level (which only {\i chalitza} can do), but it rabbinically precludes the possibility of {\i yibum}, after which {\i chalitza} is still required. So there are two distinct uses of a {\i get} in this context: a {\i get le-maamar}, which only negates the quasi-betrothal, and a {\i get le-zika}, which precludes {\i yibum} for anyone. Since Rachel has now received a {\i get} from one of the brothers, observers cannot know which type of {\i get} it was. It looks like she received a {\i get} on the {\i zika} \u8212- and a woman who received such a {\i get} may not receive {\i yibum} from any of the surviving brothers. Therefore, even though Shimon\u8217's {\i get} was only intended to negate the {\i maamar}, Levi cannot perform {\i yibum} with Rachel, because she appears to be a woman for whom {\i yibum} has been foreclosed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative view\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ika d\u8217'amri} \u8212- some say Rava rules differently in this case: {\i nosan get le-maamaro, hutra, afilu hi} \u8212- even Rachel herself is permitted for {\i yibum}. Why? {\i Mah d\u8217'avad bah shaklei} \u8212- what he did to her, the {\i maamar}, he fully removed. The {\i get} is seen as precisely canceling the {\i maamar} and nothing more \u8212- it does not alter her status beyond that, and she is not treated as a {\i baalas ha-get} in any broader sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical question underlying the dispute is: how does one distinguish between a {\i get le-maamar} and a {\i get le-zika}? Rashi explains that it depends on what is specified at the time the {\i get} is given. A {\i get} given without specification \u8212- {\i stama} \u8212- is presumed to apply to the {\i zika} as well and to prevent {\i yibum} by any of the brothers. Only a {\i get} explicitly given solely against the {\i maamar} is treated as limited in its effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ambiguity, as the {\i gemara} itself makes evident, actually strengthens Rava\u8217's first position considerably. Given how unclear it typically is whether a {\i get} was given {\i le-maamar} or {\i le-zika}, it makes abundant sense to treat any {\i get} received by such a woman as potentially precluding {\i yibum} \u8212- and therefore to forbid Levi from performing {\i yibum} with Rachel even when the original intent was merely to cancel the {\i maamar}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sisters case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} moves to a further case: {\i shnei achim nesuim shtei achayos u-meis echad mehen} \u8212- two brothers married to two sisters, and one of them dies. {\i V\u8217'achar kach meitah ishto shel sheni} \u8212- and afterward, the wife of the second brother dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permanent status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The situation is this: Reuven married Rachel, Shimon married Leah. Reuven dies. Rachel\u8217's status relative to Shimon is immediately that of {\i achos isha} \u8212- the sister of his wife \u8212- which is an {\i ervah}, a forbidden relation. She walks away without any need for {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}. Then Leah, Shimon\u8217's wife, dies. One might wonder: perhaps now that Leah is no longer alive, Rachel is no longer {\i achos isha} and Shimon could perform {\i yibum} with her. The {\i mishna} rules otherwise: {\i v\u8217'achar kach meitah ishto shel sheni harei zo asurah alav olamit} \u8212- Rachel remains eternally forbidden to Shimon. {\i Ho\u8217'il v\u8217'neasrah alav sha\u8217'ah achas} \u8212- because at that critical initial moment, when {\i yibum} first became relevant upon Reuven\u8217's death, she was forbidden to Shimon. That status does not dissolve when Leah subsequently dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obvious case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} observes: {\i pshita} \u8212- this is obvious. If anything, the case just discussed in the previous {\i mishna} \u8212- where Rachel was not completely excluded from the household, because she was still relevant to Levi even if forbidden to Shimon \u8212- presents a harder question, and yet there too she remained forbidden to Shimon. Here, where Rachel is completely pushed away from the family \u8212- {\i d\u8217'ka midcheya mei-hai beisa l\u8217'gamrei} \u8212- there are only two brothers and she is forbidden to both, so she has no connection to the family whatsoever \u8212- {\i lo kol sheken} \u8212- is it not all the more obvious that she remains forbidden?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teaching order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why the {\i mishna} nevertheless teaches this case. {\i Tana hai tana b\u8217'reisha} \u8212- the {\i tana} taught this case first. And initially, when the {\i tana} was arranging the {\i mishna}, he considered the case of the more complex three-brother scenario and perhaps thought that after Levi married Rachel, her status relative to Shimon might have changed. {\i V\u8217'sharya} \u8212- he thought it might be permitted. {\i Ve-hadar chaza le-isura} \u8212- and then he concluded that no, even there, she was forbidden to Shimon from the moment of Reuven\u8217's death, and that initial forbidden status endures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once having resolved the complex case, the simpler two-brother case was clearly also forbidden. {\i V\u8217'aydi d\u8217'chaviva lei akdama} \u8212- but because this simpler case had seemed like a significant {\i chiddush} worth exploring, it had already been placed first in the arrangement of the {\i mishna}. {\i U-mishna lo zaza mimkomah} \u8212- and a {\i mishna} does not move from its place. Once it was included, it remained, even though by the time the full teaching was in its final form the case had become obvious given the other {\i mishna} that preceded it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 31b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Gierushin Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is officially 32, though we continue picking up from the bottom of 31b. The {\i mishnah} under discussion deals with cases of {\i safek kiddushin} and {\i safek gierushin}, examining what happens when there is a question about whether an {\i erva} is in the picture and what must be done with her {\i tzarat erva}. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling is {\i chalitza}, not {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Gierushin Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pointedly observes that the case of {\i safek gierushin} discussed in the {\i mishnah} is not really a classic {\i safek gierushin} at all. Rather, it involves a {\i gierushin} that is {\i pasul d\u8217'rabbanan}. This distinction is consequential: the {\i gemara} operates on the assumption that a genuine {\i safek gierushin} would not be legally meaningful, because the woman would retain her {\i chazaka}\u8212-her status quo presumption\u8212-as an {\i eshet ish}. Correspondingly, the co-wife, the {\i tzarat erva}, would retain her status as exempt from {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cost of Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through the implications of being {\i machmir} in such a case. In some situations, one can demand {\i chalitza} without incurring any cost. Here, however, if we were to be {\i machmir} and demand {\i chalitza}, it would come with a serious cost. Therefore, we leave the matter on its {\i d\u8217'oraita} footing: such a woman is considered still an {\i eshet ish}, her co-wife is a {\i tzarat erva}, and the co-wife walks away without {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gittin Safek Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gittin Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises an apparent challenge. When it comes to {\i get}, does the {\i gemara} not have a real case of {\i safek gierushin} with the same consequence\u8212-that the co-wife requires {\i chalitza}? The challenge is introduced with the formula {\i v\u8217'ha tnan}: we taught in the {\i mishnah} in Gittin the following classic case of {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A woman was standing in the public domain\u8212-{\i hayso omedet birshus harabbim}\u8212-and her husband threw a {\i get} toward her. If the {\i get} landed closer to her, {\i megureshet}; if closer to him, {\i eina megureshet}. And if it landed exactly at the midpoint\u8212-{\i mechtza al mechtza}\u8212-then she is in a state of doubt: {\i megureshet v\u8217'eina megureshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nafka Minos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What are the practical consequences of this doubtful divorce? The {\i gemara} in Gittin identifies two {\i nafka minos}. First, if her husband is a {\i kohen}, he cannot simply decide that perhaps the divorce was invalid and remain married to her\u8212-she might be divorced, and remarrying a {\i gerusha} would be forbidden to him. Second, and directly relevant to our discussion, if she is an {\i erva}, her co-wife is a questionable {\i tzarat erva}. The co-wife cannot simply walk away, because the woman might indeed be divorced and the co-wife therefore obligated in {\i yibum}; yet {\i yibum} cannot be performed because the woman might not be divorced, making the co-wife a genuine {\i tzarat erva}. The resolution: {\i ba\u8217'ee chalitza}, she requires {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus observes that in another tractate we rule that a genuine {\i safek gierushin} calls for {\i chalitza}, not that she simply retains her {\i chazaka} and walks off. The concern about being {\i machmir} leading to {\i yibum} does not deter us there\u8212-we demand {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Pairs Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by invoking a teaching of Rabbah and Rav Yosef, who both said: {\i hacha b\u8217'shtei kitei edim askinan}\u8212-the case in Gittin is specifically one involving two pairs of witnesses. {\i Achas omeret karov la v\u8217'achas omeret karov lo}: one group of witnesses says definitively that it landed closer to her, and the other group says definitively it landed closer to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i chazaka} does not simply hold in that case\u8212-the woman\u8217's {\i chazaka} as an {\i eshet ish} and the co-wife\u8217's {\i chazaka} as a {\i tzarat erva}\u8212-is that there is something directly opposing the {\i chazaka}. It is not merely an abstract doubt: we don\u8217't know where the {\i get} fell, so we default to the status quo. Here there is direct testimony against the {\i chazaka}. The only complication is that the testimony is contradicted. Nevertheless, because {\i dahavei leh sefeika d\u8217'oraisa}\u8212-this creates a biblical-level doubt\u8212-it is sufficient to displace the {\i chazaka} and place us genuinely in the realm of uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, our {\i mishnah} in Yevamot\u8212-{\i u\u8217'masnisin d\u8217'haka be\u8217'chas akhas}\u8212-is dealing with a single pair of witnesses, split between themselves. There is no two-against-two confrontation with the {\i chazaka}. At most this would generate {\i sefeika d\u8217'rabbanan}, a rabbinic-level doubt. At the {\i d\u8217'oraita} level, one simply follows the status quo, and in such a case {\i chalitza} would not even be required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chazaka Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 D\u8217'Oraisa Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction\u8212-between an abstract doubt and direct contradictory testimony\u8212-raises a deeper question about how {\i chazaka} functions. If you have a {\i chazaka} and a {\i safek}, you follow the {\i chazaka}. If there are witnesses testifying against the {\i chazaka}, you follow the witnesses. But what if there is a {\i chazaka}, and witnesses against it, and those witnesses are themselves contradicted by other witnesses? Do you say: once there is direct testimony, even if ultimately contradicted, that is sufficient to undermine the {\i chazaka} and leave you with a blank slate\u8212-a genuine {\i safek}? Or do you say: throw out both pairs of witnesses, since they contradict one another, and return to the {\i chazaka}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially stated that contradictory witnesses displace the {\i chazaka} and create a real {\i d\u8217'oraita} doubt. It now revisits that claim and says: {\i v\u8217'su}\u8212-moreover, that is not actually correct. When two pairs of witnesses contradict each other\u8212-{\i okei trei l\u8217'bahadi trei}\u8212-we set them against one another, cancel them out, and restore the woman to her {\i chazaka}. This is the true {\i halacha}: contradictory witnesses are rejected entirely, and one returns to the status quo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Shatya Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} supports this from a concrete legal story: {\i meidiy de-hava a-nichsei d\u8217'bar shatya}\u8212-like the case of the property of Bar Shatya. Bar Shatya was a person who apparently experienced intermittent episodes of mental incapacity\u8212-a {\i shoteh}\u8212-and the legal question concerned business transactions he had conducted: were they carried out during a period of lucidity or not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Shatya sold some property. Two witnesses came and testified: {\i k\u8217'she-hu chalim zavin}\u8212-he was of sound mind when he sold it. Then another two witnesses came and testified: {\i k\u8217'she-hu shoteh zavin}\u8212-he was not of sound mind when he sold it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi ruled: {\i okei trei l\u8217'bahadi trei v\u8217'ara okmah b\u8217'chazakas Bar Shatya}\u8212-set the two pairs against each other, cancel them out, and have the property remain under Bar Shatya\u8217's presumptive ownership. This is the default position: when testimony is irreconcilably contradicted, we reject all of it and revert to the {\i chazaka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One could observe that there is something to discuss about the difference between a {\i chazaka} rooted in property ownership and one rooted in a person\u8217's legal status, but the operative principle here is clear. If the doubt were genuine, one might expect a different outcome\u8212-perhaps dividing the property, as in the famous opening {\i mishnah} of Bava Metzia. Instead, we reject the witnesses and default to the status quo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} back to the original difficulty. If even contradictory witnesses against a {\i chazaka} are thrown out and the {\i chazaka} is restored, how can the {\i mishnah} in Gittin say that in a case of {\i safek get} one requires {\i chalitza}? Whether the doubt arises from contradictory witnesses or from an abstract uncertainty, the {\i d\u8217'oraita} result should always be to follow the status quo and not require {\i chalitza}. If {\i chalitza} is nonetheless required, it must be a {\i chumra} even in the face of a {\i chazaka}. And if that is so, why does our {\i mishnah} in Yevamot not present a classic case of {\i safek get}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds with the principle of {\i yagid alav rei\u8217'o}\u8212-quoting the verse in Iyov, let his companion reveal it\u8212-meaning: learn one case from its context and juxtaposition with the other. His answer is: {\i tana b\u8217'kiddushin v\u8217'hu ha-din l\u8217'gerushin}. The {\i mishnah} taught a classic {\i safek} scenario in the context of {\i kiddushin}\u8212-a case where one did not know whether the object of {\i kiddushin} landed closer to the woman or closer to him\u8212-and the same {\i halacha} of {\i chalitza} without {\i yibum} applies to {\i gierushin} as well. Conversely, the {\i mishnah} taught certain cases in the context of {\i gierushin}\u8212-specifically, a {\i get} that was {\i pasul d\u8217'rabbanan}\u8212-and the same would be true if one had {\i kiddushin} that were {\i pasul d\u8217'rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point is: do not read the two sets of scenarios as mutually exclusive. The {\i mishnah} gave one cluster of examples under {\i kiddushin} and another cluster under {\i gierushin}, but both sets of {\i halachot} apply in both directions. The different examples were given to add scenarios, not to exclude the applicability of the other category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava pushes back. If one can simply learn one case from the other in both directions, why does the {\i mishnah} use the language {\i zehu}\u8212-\u8220"this is the case\u8221"\u8212-for both {\i safek kiddushin} and {\i safek gierushin}? That formulation sounds precise and exclusionary. When the {\i mishnah} says {\i zehu}, it implies: this and no more, not a broader category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore offers his own resolution: {\i kol she-yesh b\u8217'kiddushin yesh b\u8217'gerushin}. Whatever applies to {\i kiddushin} also applies to {\i gierushin}. Any classic {\i safek} scenario\u8212-one did not know whether the item landed closer or farther\u8212-would generate the same {\i halacha} in both cases. In that sense, the cases of {\i kiddushin} are not limited to the specific scenarios mentioned; a classic {\i safek} by {\i gierushin} would equally call for {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i v\u8217'yesh b\u8217'gerushin ma she-ein b\u8217'kiddushin}: there are defects specific to a {\i get}\u8212-the absence of a {\i zman}, the absence of two {\i eidim}, and so on\u8212-that do not apply to a {\i shtar kiddushin}. When the {\i mishnah} says {\i zehu safek gerushin}, the word {\i zehu} is {\i lav davka}\u8212-it is not meant to limit the category. Even a classic {\i safek} would apply. But when the {\i mishnah} says {\i zehu} regarding {\i kiddushin}, that {\i zehu} is {\i davka}, because the defects of a {\i get} that render it {\i pasul} do not carry over to {\i shtar kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Specifically, {\i le-ma\u8217'utei zman de-leita b\u8217'kiddushin}: to exclude the case of the missing {\i zman}, which invalidates a {\i get} but does not invalidate a {\i shtar kiddushin}. This explains why the {\i mishnah} uses exclusive language regarding {\i kiddushin}: it is not limiting {\i kiddushin} to only those scenarios, but rather signaling that the problems of {\i gierushin}\u8212-particularly the {\i zman} requirement\u8212-do not apply to {\i kiddushin}. That prompts the next question: why?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zman (Date) Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did the Rabbis not require a {\i zman} in a {\i shtar kiddushin}? To answer this, the {\i gemara} first identifies why a {\i zman} is required in a {\i get} at all. Two reasons are offered. {\i Hani\u8217'cha l\u8217'man d\u8217'amar mishum peiri}: according to the view that the purpose of the date is to protect the woman\u8217's right to recover {\i peiros}\u8212-the usufruct\u8212-from her husband. And the second reason, which the {\i gemara} calls {\i mishum bas achoso}: to prevent a husband from backdating a {\i get} in order to protect his wife\u8212-perhaps his niece\u8212-from a charge of adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the {\i peiros} reason as follows: from the moment a woman is divorced, her husband loses his entitlement to consume the fruit from her {\i nichsei melog}\u8212-her personal property. She is entitled to recover from him the fruit he consumed after the date of divorce. Since the husband may be tempted not to date the {\i get}, or to date it as late as possible, the Rabbis required a date to protect her. The question of why the husband would not be motivated to include an early date\u8212-since that would reduce his liability\u8212-is resolved by the observation that {\i mi-sha\u8217'ah she-nasan einav bah l\u8217'garshah shuv ein l\u8217'ba\u8217'al peiros}: from the moment he began writing the {\i get} with the intention of divorcing her, he forfeits his right to the fruit. So it is actually against his interest to have any date at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, once the Rabbis required a date, the concern about predating is neutralized, because witnesses who sign the {\i get} will not sign a false date. The date is therefore an effective protection: it establishes the precise moment from which the woman can recover her fruit, and it prevents false claims about timing in cases touching on adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peiros Inapplicable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given these two reasons, the {\i gemara} now asks why the {\i zman} was not required for a {\i shtar kiddushin} as well. Regarding the {\i peiros} reason, the answer is straightforward: {\i arusa leit lah peiros}. A husband\u8217's right to eat the fruit of his wife\u8217's property begins only at {\i nissu\u8217'in}, not at {\i kiddushin}. No transfer of property rights occurs at the moment of {\i kiddushin}, and therefore a date serves no function with respect to {\i peiros}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adultery Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what of the second reason\u8212-{\i mishum bas achoso}, the concern about covering up adultery? If we require a date in a {\i get} so that one can know precisely when the divorce took place and prevent someone from claiming that a woman was already divorced before an act of adultery, the same logic would seem to apply to {\i kiddushin}. Without a date on the {\i shtar kiddushin}, a woman could claim she was only married after a particular event, thereby retroactively altering the legal picture. Should we not therefore require a date in a {\i shtar kiddushin} for the same reason?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money vs.\u160 ?Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer: {\i mishum d\u8217'ika d\u8217'mikadesh b\u8217'chaspa v\u8217'ika d\u8217'mikadesh b\u8217'shtara lo tiknu rabanan zeman}. Since people perform {\i kiddushin} with money far more often than with a document\u8212-and when {\i kiddushin} is performed with money there is no dated document at all\u8212-we would not have precise knowledge of the date of {\i kiddushin} in most cases anyway. It would therefore be inconsistent and practically ineffective to suddenly require a date when the minority of people who perform {\i kiddushin} with a {\i shtar} do so. The absence of a date would be the normal state of affairs, and the Rabbis did not impose a {\i zman} requirement for this reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As a practical observation, it is worth noting that among all the evidence recovered from the Cairo Geniza\u8212-the vast corpus of marriage documents and {\i ketubot} analyzed by scholars such as Mordechai Friedman\u8212-there appears to be not a single documented instance of {\i kiddushei shtara} in practice. The {\i gemara} acknowledges that some do {\i kiddushin} with money and some with a document, but the historical record suggests overwhelmingly that {\i kiddushei kesef} was the universal practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha the son of Rav Yosef raised a challenge to Rav Ashi: {\i v\u8217'ha avda}\u8212-what about a slave? A slave can be acquired either with money or with a document, just as {\i kiddushin} can be performed either way. Yet the Rabbis did require a {\i zman} in the document of slave acquisition. Why, then, does the same logic not apply to require a {\i zman} in a {\i shtar kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Majority Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hatam ruba b\u8217'shtara}\u8212-there, the majority of slave acquisitions are done with a document, since owners naturally want documented proof of ownership of their property. Therefore, the Rabbis require a {\i zman} in that document, and we are not concerned about the minority cases done with money. {\i Hacha ruba b\u8217'chesef}\u8212-here, the majority of {\i kiddushin} are performed with money. We do not restructure the entire institution for the rare case of {\i kiddushei shtara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers an alternative reason why a {\i zman} in a {\i shtar kiddushin} would be impractical: {\i ibba\u8217'it eima mishum d\u8217'lo efshar}\u8212-because it would simply not be effective. The question is: who would hold onto the {\i shtar kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she holds onto it\u8212-{\i lenacheihu gabi didah}\u8212-she could erase the date and deny when the {\i kiddushin} took place: {\i machkah lei}. If he holds onto it\u8212-the same problem arises in reverse: {\i zimnin de-bat achoto hee, mechaplei lah}, perhaps she is his niece and he will want to protect her by making the document disappear. And if the witnesses hold onto it as a neutral third party\u8212-{\i lincha gabei edim}\u8212-that also will not help. If they still remember the event, {\i leisu lisan}, they can testify from their own memory without needing the document. But if they do not remember, they might look at the document and testify based on what they read, not based on genuine recollection. And the Torah says {\i mi-pihem velo miksavam}: testimony must come from their mouths, from what they actually remember, and not from what they read in a document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Memory vs.\u160 ?Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle\u8212-{\i mi-pihem velo miksavam}\u8212-opens a genuinely striking discussion. Tosafot raises an obvious objection: the entire institution of a signed {\i shtar} is premised on the document serving as evidence. Why would witnesses need to testify at all? They should simply produce the signed document and let it speak for itself. Tosafot concedes the point entirely: if the witnesses actually produce the document and present it to the court without claiming independent memory, that is perfectly valid\u8212-{\i shapir havu avdi}. The signed witnesses on a {\i shtar} are treated as if their testimony has already been examined by a {\i beit din}\u8212-{\i de-edim ha-chasumim a-shetar na-asu ke-mi she-nechkerah edutan be-Veit Din}. The concern is specifically that they might not produce the document but instead claim to testify from memory when in fact they are relying on what they read\u8212-{\i ela shema ya-idu al ha-ma\u8217'aseh atzmo ke-ilu hem zochrim ve-einam zochrim, ela al pi ha-ksav}. That is the invalid case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a further refinement: if reading a document actually prompts genuine recollection\u8212-not simply reading and asserting the event happened, but actually remembering it\u8212-then testifying based on that recalled memory is valid. The problematic case is the one in the middle: the witnesses do not genuinely remember, they are not producing the document, but they are testifying as if they remember because they read it. What is permitted, then, is either presenting the signed document directly, or using a written note to jog one\u8217's memory so that one genuinely recalls the event and testifies from actual recollection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot also notes the practice cited by Rabbeinu Tam of witnesses writing out their testimony and sending it in writing to the {\i beit din}. This is different from the problematic case: since the witnesses do genuinely remember, {\i d\u8217'lo karinan bei mipihem velo mipi kesavam}, the mode of communication through writing does not render the testimony invalid\u8212-{\i keivan d\u8217'heim zochrim edusam}. The problem arises only when the written record is substituting for, rather than expressing, actual memory.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now presents: {\i gimel achin nesuin gimel nochriyos}\u8212-three brothers married to three unrelated women. One of them dies\u8212-{\i v\u8217'meis echad meihem}\u8212-and the second one performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with the available {\i yevamah}\u8212-{\i v\u8217'asah bah hasheini ma\u8217'amar}\u8212-and then the second one dies as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To set out the case concretely: Reuven is married to Rochel, Shimon is married to Devorah, and Levi is married to Chuldah. Reuven dies. Shimon does {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rochel. Shimon then dies. Levi now has an obligation of {\i yibum} toward Devorah, coming from Shimon, and an obligation of {\i yibum} toward Rochel, coming from Reuven\u8212-and additionally a quasi-obligation toward Rochel from Shimon, since {\i ma\u8217'amar} establishes a semi-marital bond. Rochel thus comes to him through two separate brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i harei elu choltzos v\u8217'lo misyabmos}\u8212-both women, Devorah and Rochel, receive {\i chalitza} and may not undergo {\i yibum}. Why is Rochel problematic is clear from the {\i drashah} on the verse: {\i mi she\u8217'aleha zikas yavam echad}, the {\i yibum} obligation applies only to a woman connected through one deceased brother, {\i v\u8217'lo she\u8217'aleha zikas shnei yevamin}\u8212-not to a woman connected through two brothers. Since Shimon\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'amar} created a quasi-bond with Rochel while Reuven\u8217's original marriage also binds her to Levi, she arrives at Levi through two separate brothers, and that dual {\i zika} is excluded. If {\i ma\u8217'amar} carries some {\i d\u8217'oraita} weight\u8212-as the school of Beit Shammai maintains\u8212-then Rochel could genuinely stand in a quasi-status that creates a real double {\i zika}; even according to those who hold {\i ma\u8217'amar} is purely {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, the {\i drashah} would be applied at the {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is less obvious is why Devorah cannot undergo {\i yibum}. Devorah is connected to Levi through Shimon, straightforwardly, without any complication. Why does the {\i mishnah} require that she also receive only {\i chalitza}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Decree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} addresses this. If the prohibition on Rochel were a genuine {\i d\u8217'oraita} problem\u8212-a real biblical disqualification\u8212-then why does she require {\i chalitza} at all? If the Torah excludes a woman in such a double-{\i zika} situation, she should simply walk away without any {\i chalitza}. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ela miderabbanan}\u8212-this entire prohibition is only rabbinic in origin, most likely because {\i ma\u8217'amar} itself is a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} institution. Since it is a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} matter, Rochel still requires {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason Devorah also requires only {\i chalitza}\u8212-and cannot undergo {\i yibum}\u8212-is a {\i gezeira}: {\i shema yomru shtei yevamot habaot mibayit echad mityabmot}. People will think that since Rochel was quasi-married to Shimon through {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and Devorah was actually married to Shimon, both are Shimon\u8217's wives. If Levi does {\i yibum} with one and {\i chalitza} with the other, observers will conclude that when a brother dies leaving two wives, the surviving brother may do {\i yibum} with both. That conclusion is of course wrong: one does {\i yibum} with one wife and {\i chalitza} with the others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers: why not simply allow {\i yibum} with Devorah and {\i chalitza} with Rochel? The {\i gemara} answers with a further {\i gezeira}: {\i shema yomru bayit echad miktzato banuy umiktzato chalutz}. People will see both women in the picture, and they will conclude that when a brother dies with multiple wives, one can do {\i yibum} with one and {\i chalitza} with another interchangeably.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might respond: so what if people think that? What is the actual harm? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ee d\u8217'miabem v\u8217'hadar chalitz}\u8212-if the normal practice were {\i yibum} first and then {\i chalitza} for the remaining wives, the error would carry no intrinsic cost. Perhaps people would perform unnecessary {\i chalitza}, and the women would be disqualified from {\i kehuna}, but the {\i beit din} would know better, and the act of {\i chalitza} itself would not be inherently harmful. But here the concern is different: {\i dilma chalitz be\u8217'reisha v\u8217'hadar miyabem}\u8212-perhaps, based on the mistaken understanding, someone will first perform {\i chalitza} with one and then attempt {\i yibum} with the other. That reversal is catastrophic. Once he has performed {\i chalitza} he enters the category of one who has not built up his brother\u8217's house\u8212-{\i v\u8217'kam lei b\u8217'asher lo yivneh}\u8212-and the Torah says {\i shuv lo yivneh}: once he has failed to build, he can no longer do so. The {\i chalitza} would render the subsequent {\i yibum} a violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is therefore to prevent this catastrophic wrong-order scenario that the {\i gemara} requires both women to receive {\i chalitza} and permits neither to undergo {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Get Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now introduces a new variant: {\i nasan get l\u8217'maamaro}. What if, after performing {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rochel, Shimon changed his mind and gave her a {\i get} to undo the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and then died? Is Rochel now out of the picture, freeing Levi to perform {\i yibum} with Devorah? And might Levi even be free to perform {\i yibum} with Rochel herself, since she reverts to being a simple {\i yevamah} awaiting {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: {\i nasan get l\u8217'maamaro, hutra tzarasah}\u8212-if he gave a {\i get} to counteract his {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the co-wife Devorah is released and may undergo {\i yibum}. {\i Aval hi asura}\u8212-but Rochel herself remains forbidden to {\i yibum}, and Levi can perform only {\i chalitza} with her. Why? {\i D\u8217'michalfa b\u8217'baalas get}\u8212-because people will confuse her situation with that of a woman who received a {\i get} on the {\i zika} itself, not on the {\i ma\u8217'amar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To clarify the relevant structure of {\i halacha}: if a man performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} and wishes to separate, he requires both a {\i get} for the {\i ma\u8217'amar} and {\i chalitza} for the {\i zika}. If he did no {\i ma\u8217'amar} and wishes to release the {\i yevamah}, only {\i chalitza} is required. There is also the concept of a {\i get l\u8217'zika}\u8212-a {\i get} addressed not to a {\i ma\u8217'amar} but to the {\i zika} itself\u8212-which is a rabbinic act that prevents {\i yibum} going forward, while still leaving the woman requiring {\i chalitza}. The critical distinction in Rava\u8217's ruling is that the {\i get} in this case was given {\i l\u8217'maamaro}\u8212-specifically to counteract the {\i ma\u8217'amar}\u8212-and not to the {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second opinion is cited: {\i icha d\u8217'amri}, some say Rava\u8217's ruling is that {\i nasan get l\u8217'maamaro, hutra afilu hi}\u8212-even Rochel herself is permitted, and Levi may perform {\i yibum} even with her. The reasoning: {\i ma d\u8217'avad bah shaklei}\u8212-what he did to her, the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, he completely removed. The {\i get} neutralizes the {\i ma\u8217'amar} entirely, and she is treated as if no {\i ma\u8217'amar} was ever performed. We do not view her as a {\i baalas get} who received a {\i get} on the {\i zika}; we see her simply as a {\i yevamah} who no longer has any {\i ma\u8217'amar} in the picture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi clarifies the distinction between the two opinions: the {\i get} must explicitly specify that it is directed at the {\i ma\u8217'amar} and not at the {\i zika}\u8212-{\i she-peresh l\u8217'maamaro v\u8217'lo l\u8217'zikato}. A {\i stam get}, one without explicit specification, is presumed to be directed at the {\i zika} as well, thereby preventing all the brothers from performing {\i yibum}: {\i aval stama kaima v\u8217'lo yivne kulu achim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ambiguity actually lends force to Rava\u8217's first position. Since it is quite unclear in practice whether a given {\i get} is directed at the {\i ma\u8217'amar} or at the {\i zika}, the concern about confusion\u8212-about people thinking this is a case of receiving a {\i get} on the {\i zika}\u8212-is not at all far-fetched. The {\i gezeira} is well-grounded precisely because the distinction is subtle and practically difficult to maintain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Sisters Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents: {\i shnei achim nesuim shtei achayot}\u8212-two brothers married to two sisters\u8212-and one of them dies. Reuven is married to Rochel and Shimon is married to Leah. Reuven dies. What is Rochel\u8217's status with respect to Shimon? She is {\i achot isha}\u8212-his wife\u8217's sister\u8212-an {\i erva}, and she is forbidden. She walks away without {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permanent Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then adds: {\i v\u8217'achar kach meta ishto shel sheni}\u8212-and afterwards the wife of the second brother, Leah, dies. Could it now be argued that Rochel is no longer {\i achot isha}, since the sister is no longer alive, and Shimon might now be permitted to perform {\i yibum} with Rochel? The {\i mishnah} rules emphatically: {\i harei zu asura alav l\u8217'olam}\u8212-Rochel remains forbidden to Shimon forever. {\i Ho\u8217'il v\u8217'nasra alav sha\u8217'a achas}\u8212-since she was forbidden to him at that critical moment when the {\i yibum} obligation first arose, that prohibition is permanent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Brother Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i p\u8217'shita}\u8212-this is obvious! The {\i gemara} supports this with a comparison to a case discussed just prior, involving three brothers. Reuven, Shimon, and Levi. Reuven dies. Levi then marries Rochel. Levi dies. Shimon is now confronted with the question: may he perform {\i yibum} with Rochel? Even though by the time of Levi\u8217's death Rochel is no longer Shimon\u8217's wife\u8217's sister in any direct sense\u8212-Leah may be dead, or Rochel is no longer in that relationship\u8212-Shimon remains forbidden. The reasoning: at the initial critical moment when Reuven died, Rochel was {\i achot isha} to Shimon. That status was fixed at that moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obviousness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that case\u8212-where Rochel was not completely excluded from the family because Levi had married her and she was still {\i mutar} to Levi\u8212-already established that the initial forbidden status is permanent, how much more so in the case of our {\i mishnah}, where Rochel was completely pushed away from the family the moment Reuven died. {\i Hacha d\u8217'ka midchaya mehai beita legamrei}\u8212-here she was entirely excluded from the entire fraternal household at that moment. If even a partial connection was insufficient to undo the forbidden status, surely a complete exclusion from the household makes the permanence of the prohibition all the more obvious. {\i Lo kol sheken}\u8212-this is an {\i a fortiori} case that requires no independent teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order of Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why this {\i mishnah} was taught nonetheless: {\i tana hai tana be\u8217'reisha}\u8212-this case was actually taught first, before the more complex three-brother case. At the time it was initially taught, the {\i tana} thought the three-brother case might be {\i mutar}, because Rochel was not completely out of the family circle\u8212-Levi had married her and she remained relevant to that household. The {\i tana} therefore initially contemplated that her status might change once Levi married her. Only afterward did the {\i tana} reconsider and conclude that even in that case she remains forbidden to Shimon: once she was {\i asura} to him at that initial moment, she remains {\i asura}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the three-brother case was decided, the present {\i mishnah} became superfluous\u8212-it is now an {\i a fortiori} case that teaches nothing new. But since the {\i mishnah} had already been taught and fixed in the tradition as an important ruling in its own right, it was not removed: {\i u-mishna lo zaza mi-mekoma}. The {\i mishnah} remains in place, even though its independent necessity has been superseded by the more complex case that was ultimately resolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 32\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eshet Ach & Achot Isha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 32a, approximately six lines below where the daf begins, introduces a {\i braita} that shifts the discussion into entirely new terrain \u8212- away from the specific case of two brothers and two sisters, and into the broader question of {\i issur chal al issur}, whether one prohibition can take effect on top of another. The {\i braita} presents the following scenario: a man\u8217's brother married his wife\u8217's sister. The woman is thus, to this man, simultaneously {\i eshet ach} (his brother\u8217's wife) and {\i achot isha} (his wife\u8217's sister) \u8212- two independent prohibitions apply to the very same person. He then has relations with her. What is his liability?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose vs.\u160 ?R. Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} records a dispute between Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon. Rabbi Yose takes the straightforward position: he has transgressed two prohibitions, {\i eshet ach} and {\i achot isha}, and is liable for both. Rabbi Shimon disagrees, holding that he is liable for only one. Before even analyzing the reasoning behind this dispute, however, the Gemara immediately confronts a contradiction: a second version of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling states the exact opposite \u8212- that he is liable only for {\i achot isha}, not for {\i eshet ach}. So Rabbi Shimon consistently holds that only one prohibition applies, but the two versions disagree about which one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya}. The two versions reflect two different factual sequences. In one case, the brother who ultimately dies married her first \u8212- making her {\i eshet ach} before she became {\i achot isha}. In the other case, the surviving brother married first, making her {\i achot isha} before she became {\i eshet ach}. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position is that you follow whichever status was established first. This is the key principle: the first prohibition defines her status, and the second cannot displace it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make this concrete: Reuven married Rachel, and Shimon married Leah. Reuven then dies. From Shimon\u8217's perspective, Rachel is both {\i eshet ach} and {\i achot isha}. If Reuven married Rachel before Shimon married Leah, then Rachel\u8217's first status relative to Shimon was {\i eshet ach}; the subsequent marriage to Leah that created the {\i achot isha} status came later and, according to Rabbi Shimon, cannot take effect. Conversely, if Shimon married Leah first, Rachel was already {\i achot isha} before Reuven ever married her, and the subsequent {\i eshet ach} status cannot displace the earlier one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Issur Chal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative principle behind Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position is {\i ein issur chal al issur}: one prohibition does not take effect on top of a prohibition that is already in force. The second status, whatever it may be, cannot replace or supplement the first. Rabbi Shimon applies this categorically \u8212- whichever status came first is the one that counts, and only its attendant consequences apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note what is practically at stake. Both prohibitions carry {\i karet}. The difference is not about going to hell once versus twice, as it were, but rather about whether one who acted {\i beshogeg} is obligated to bring one {\i korban} or two, and potentially about other related consequences. These are real {\i halakhic} distinctions that make the debate substantive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also raises an important side point regarding {\i yibum}. According to Rabbi Shimon, if Reuven died without children, one might think Shimon could perform {\i yibum} with Rachel \u8212- after all, in the scenario where Rachel\u8217's first status was {\i eshet ach}, the {\i achot isha} prohibition never took effect. But Rav Ashi explains that the {\i issur} of {\i achot isha} is, as it were, hovering above, latent but present. Were the {\i issur eshet ach} to fall away \u8212- precisely what {\i yibum} would accomplish \u8212- the {\i issur achot isha} would immediately descend and take hold. Because performing {\i yibum} would only usher in the next prohibition, the first prohibition does not dissolve, and Rachel remains forbidden. This is a striking mechanism: the very act that would remove one prohibition would instantaneously activate another, so the system locks in place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yose\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to Rabbi Yose, who says that both prohibitions apply simultaneously. Does Rabbi Yose truly hold that {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- that one prohibition can take effect on top of another? This seems to be a striking claim, and the Gemara immediately raises a contradiction from Rabbi Yose\u8217's own rulings elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Deaths Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} teaches: if one commits a sin that carries two different death penalties, the court imposes only the more severe one \u8212- {\i nidon b\u8217'chamura}. Rabbi Yose disagrees: {\i nidon b\u8217'zika harishona haba\u8217'ah alav}, one is judged according to the first relationship that defined his liability. The term {\i zika} \u8212- connection or relationship \u8212- is significant here. Rabbi Yose says that the first relationship is what defines the punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother-in-Law Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then illustrates Rabbi Yose\u8217's view with a concrete case. A mother-in-law is subject to the penalty of {\i sreifa} (burning), while an {\i eshet ish} (married woman) is punishable by {\i chenek} (strangulation), the less severe death. Now consider a woman who is both a man\u8217's mother-in-law and a married woman \u8212- a natural scenario, since she is presumably married to his father-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika Rishona\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} would say he is punished by {\i sreifa}, the more severe penalty. Rabbi Yose says no \u8212- go by the first relationship. If she was his mother-in-law first and subsequently became a married woman (for instance, she was widowed or divorced and then remarried), then the first {\i zika} is mother-in-law, and he is punished by {\i sreifa}. But if she was a married woman first \u8212- that is, she was already married before he ever married her daughter \u8212- then the first {\i zika} is {\i eshet ish}, and he is punished only by {\i chenek}, not by {\i sreifa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the critical insight. The only way Rabbi Yose can arrive at the lighter penalty of {\i chenek} is if he genuinely holds that the mother-in-law status, though technically applicable, does not create a separate legal reality. He is not saying merely that we pick the lighter punishment while acknowledging both \u8212- he is saying the later status never took effect. This is structurally identical to {\i ein issur chal al issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose\u8217's Stance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then we have the contradiction. The same Rabbi Yose who says in the mother-in-law case that you go by the first relationship \u8212- which implies {\i ein issur chal al issur} \u8212- says in the {\i eshet ach}/{\i achot isha} case that you are liable for both. Does Rabbi Yose hold {\i issur chal al issur} or not?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mosif vs.\u160 ?Kolel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Mosif Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer, articulated by Rabbi Abahu, is: {\i modeh Rabbi Yosi be-issur mosif} \u8212- Rabbi Yose concedes that a second prohibition {\i can} take effect when it constitutes an {\i issur mosif}, a prohibition that broadens the scope of persons to whom the forbidden object or person is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept requires careful exposition. The general rule is {\i ein issur chal al issur}: a second prohibition cannot descend onto an object already carrying a prohibition. But there is an exception: if the second prohibition extends the reach of that object\u8217's forbidden status to a wider class of people, it {\i can} take effect even on top of the existing one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider the {\i eshet ach}/{\i achot isha} scenario. If Shimon married Leah first, Rachel became {\i achot isha} to Shimon. At that point, she was forbidden only to Shimon. Then Reuven married Rachel. By becoming {\i eshet ach}, Rachel\u8217's forbidden status now extends to all of Reuven\u8217's brothers \u8212- not just to Shimon, but to any other brothers as well. The scope of her prohibition has expanded: she was forbidden to one person, and now she is forbidden to a broader class of people. This is an {\i issur mosif}, and because it genuinely adds something \u8212- it reaches people who were not previously affected \u8212- it can take effect even on top of the existing {\i achot isha} prohibition. So in this sequence, Shimon is liable for both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does it matter to Shimon that she is now forbidden to other brothers? The conceptual logic is that the new status has real operative content regarding other parties; since it takes effect vis-\u224 ?-vis those others, it takes effect as a status altogether, including with respect to Shimon, even though she was already forbidden to him personally. The {\i issur mosif} creates an objectively new prohibition, not merely redundant with the one already in place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, on the other hand, Reuven married Rachel first, she was {\i eshet ach} from the outset, forbidden to all the brothers. Then Shimon married Leah. Now Rachel is {\i achot isha} \u8212- but the {\i achot isha} prohibition adds no new persons to whom Rachel is forbidden. She was already forbidden to all the brothers as {\i eshet ach}; the {\i achot isha} prohibition merely covers Shimon again, who was already covered. This is not an {\i issur mosif}, and thus the second prohibition cannot take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara confirms: in the sequence where the surviving brother married first (wife\u8217's sister established first) and then the deceased brother married her (brother\u8217's wife established second), {\i migo de-itosif issur legabei achim} \u8212- since the new status broadens her prohibition to encompass the other brothers, {\i to-isif issur legabei dideih} \u8212- it also reinforces the prohibition with respect to Shimon himself. Both apply. In the reverse sequence, no such broadening occurs, and only the first prohibition counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Kolel Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the Gemara turns to a second category: {\i issur kolel}. This is a subtler and more contested concept. Whereas {\i issur mosif} means the forbidden object itself becomes prohibited to a wider class of people, {\i issur kolel} means the forbidden person finds themselves included within a larger population of people who are all now subject to the same prohibition \u8212- without the scope of the object\u8217's prohibition having changed at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction can be illustrated as follows. With {\i issur mosif}: Rachel begins as {\i achot isha} \u8212- forbidden to Shimon. She then becomes {\i eshet ach} \u8212- now forbidden to all the brothers. Rachel herself has become prohibited to more people. Her own status has expanded outward. That is {\i issur mosif}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i issur kolel}: Rachel begins as {\i eshet ach} \u8212- forbidden to all the brothers. Shimon then marries Leah. Shimon is now forbidden not just to Rachel but to all of Leah\u8217's sisters. Rachel, as one of Leah\u8217's sisters, now finds herself among a larger class of women who are all forbidden to Shimon. But Rachel\u8217's own status has not changed \u8212- she was forbidden to all the brothers before, and she remains forbidden to all the brothers after. What changed is not Rachel\u8217's reach but Shimon\u8217's prohibitions: he is now subject to a broader class of women he cannot marry, and Rachel happens to be one of them. She becomes part of a {\i kolel}, a broader collective category, without her own status having expanded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To crystallize the difference: {\i issur mosif} \u8212- the forbidden object is now prohibited to more people. {\i Issur kolel} \u8212- the person subject to the prohibition is now prohibited regarding a broader class of objects. The Gemara articulates this explicitly: {\i hai issur kolel hu} \u8212- this is an {\i issur kolel}, not an {\i issur mosif}. The object\u8217's scope has not changed; the subject\u8217's prohibited field has widened, sweeping the original object in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mosif Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does {\i issur mosif} allow a second prohibition to take effect but {\i issur kolel} does not? The Gemara\u8217's logic is that for the second prohibition to take effect on something already prohibited, it must bring something genuinely new that attaches to the object itself. With {\i issur mosif}, the object \u8212- Rachel \u8212- genuinely acquires a new dimension: she is now forbidden to people who were not previously forbidden from her. The new prohibition is a real, operative status change in the object. With {\i issur kolel}, the object itself has not changed; Rachel is still forbidden to exactly the same people as before. The change is in the subject\u8217's broader landscape of prohibitions, and that is not enough to generate a second, independent {\i issur} on the object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore concludes that Rabbi Yose holds of {\i issur mosif} but not {\i issur kolel}. This resolves the apparent contradiction: in the {\i eshet ach}/{\i achot isha} case, if the sequence creates an {\i issur mosif} \u8212- wife\u8217's sister first, then brother\u8217's wife \u8212- Rabbi Yose says both apply. In the mother-in-law and {\i eshet ish} case, when {\i eshet ish} comes first, mother-in-law is a narrower scope prohibition that cannot take effect on top of the broader one, so only the first applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a remaining difficulty: what does Rabbi Yose mean when he says both apply in the {\i eshet ach}/{\i achot isha} case in the opposite sequence, where no {\i issur mosif} exists? Rava answers: {\i ma\u8217'aleh ani alav ke\u8217'ilu asah shta\u8217'im bein chayav ela achas} \u8212- we consider it as if he committed two transgressions, even though he is formally liable for only one. This means that while the second prohibition does not create a second technical liability in terms of punishment, it still speaks to who this person is. The same formulation is confirmed by Rabbi Yochanan when Ravin transmitted his teaching: {\i ma\u8217'aleh ani alav ke\u8217'ilu asah shta\u8217'im ve\u8217'eino chayav ela achas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai nafka mina} \u8212- what is the practical consequence of this statement, which seems to say nothing more than \u8220"it\u8217's as if\u8221"? The answer is {\i likvoro bein resha\u8217'im gemurim} \u8212- it affects where he is buried. When the court executes a person, the severity of the sentence affects the section of the cemetery in which they are interred. The \u8220"as if\u8221" formulation means that in terms of his personal moral identity \u8212- what Briskers might call the {\i gavra} dimension \u8212- he is treated as one who committed two {\i aveiros}, even though the formal consequences of the act are determined by only one. This is how Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon are in fact arguing: Rabbi Shimon says it is purely one, even in this moral-identity sense; Rabbi Yose says it carries the character of two.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Chiya vs.\u160 ?Bar Kappara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zar on Shabbos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now connects the {\i issur chal al issur} discussion to a parallel series of debates between Rabbi Chiya and Bar Kappara. {\i Uviflukta} \u8212- this controversy, the Gemara says, is linked to another dispute. The first case: {\i zar she-shimesh be-Shabbos} \u8212- a non-{\i kohen} who performs the Temple service on {\i Shabbos}. The {\i halakha} is that a {\i zar} who performs the Temple service is liable with death {\i bidei shamayim}, and that performing {\i melacha} on {\i Shabbos} also incurs the death penalty, {\i bidei adam}. This person has done both: he has performed the Temple service as a non-{\i kohen}, and he has done so on {\i Shabbos}. Is he liable twice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya says {\i chayav shta\u8217'im} \u8212- twice. Bar Kappara says {\i eino chayav ella achas} \u8212- only once. What follows is one of the more colorful passages in this section of the Gemara. Rabbi Chiya jumps up and takes an oath \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'avodah}, by the divine Temple service \u8212- that he heard from Rebbi (Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi) that the liability is twice. Bar Kappara immediately jumps up and takes his own oath \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'avodah} \u8212- that he heard from Rebbi exactly the opposite, that the liability is only once. Two Tannaim, two oaths, two contradictory reports. Rather than simply deferring to one or the other, both proceed to offer logical arguments in support of their positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Chiya\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya argues: {\i Shabbos} was forbidden to everyone. When it was permitted in the Temple \u8212- that is, when the Temple service overrides {\i Shabbos} \u8212- that dispensation was granted {\i etzel kohanim}, specifically to {\i kohanim}. For non-{\i kohanim}, no such dispensation was ever extended. Therefore, {\i yesh kan mishum zarus ve-yesh kan mishum Shabbos} \u8212- he has violated both the prohibition against a {\i zar} performing the Temple service and the prohibition of {\i Shabbos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Kappara Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara counters: {\i Shabbos} was forbidden to everyone; when it was permitted in the Temple, it was permitted {\i in the Temple} \u8212- that is, within the context of the Temple service, {\i melacha} on {\i Shabbos} does not apply at all. The premise of the {\i Shabbos} prohibition is simply set aside entirely in this context, not merely waived for {\i kohanim} specifically. Therefore, {\i ein kan ella zarus} \u8212- there is only the prohibition of {\i zar} performing the service; the {\i Shabbos} violation simply does not exist here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a strange position on its face. One might ask: does this mean anyone can walk into the Temple and light a match on {\i Shabbos}? Bar Kappara\u8217's logic seems to push in that direction, and the Gemara is aware of the difficulty. The debate is ultimately about how to conceptualize the nature of the {\i heter}: is it a targeted dispensation for {\i kohanim} doing the {\i avodah}, leaving the {\i Shabbos} prohibition intact for everyone else? Or does the Temple context itself dissolve the {\i Shabbos} prohibition as an operative category, such that there is simply no {\i Shabbos} issue for any action performed there?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baal Mum & Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second parallel case: {\i baal mum she-shimesh be-tumah} \u8212- a {\i kohen} with a physical blemish who performs the Temple service in a state of {\i tumah} (ritual impurity), specifically in the context of a {\i korban tzibur} (communal offering). As background: a {\i baal mum} is normally forbidden from performing the {\i avodah}, and serving in a state of {\i tumah} is also normally forbidden. However, {\i tumah} is overridden for a {\i korban tzibur} \u8212- when the entire community is {\i tamei}, the communal sacrifice may still be brought. So were this {\i kohen} unblemished, the {\i tumah} would not be a problem. But he is a {\i baal mum}, so he cannot perform the {\i avodah} in any case. Has he violated only the prohibition of {\i baal mum}, or has he also violated the prohibition of {\i tumah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya says twice; Bar Kappara says once. They take the same oaths as before. Rabbi Chiya\u8217's argument: {\i tumah} was forbidden to all; when it was permitted in the Temple for a {\i korban tzibur}, that dispensation was granted {\i lekohanim tmimim} \u8212- to unblemished {\i kohanim} capable of performing the {\i avodah}, not to a {\i baal mum}. Since this {\i baal mum} was not the intended beneficiary of the dispensation, the {\i tumah} prohibition still applies to him in full. He therefore violates both. Bar Kappara\u8217's argument: when the Torah permits {\i tumah} in the Temple for a {\i korban tzibur}, it permits it for all Temple service in that context \u8212- the {\i tumah} prohibition is simply not operative in this situation, regardless of who is performing the {\i avodah}. Therefore only the {\i baal mum} prohibition applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that Bar Kappara\u8217's position here is somewhat more coherent than in the {\i Shabbos} case. The principle of {\i tumah hutra be-tzibur} \u8212- that {\i tumah} is broadly set aside when communal sacrifices are required \u8212- is a recognized {\i halakhic} category with genuine {\i sugyot} behind it, and one can plausibly argue that in such a context the prohibition of {\i tumah} is not merely waived for eligible {\i kohanim} but is categorically suspended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melikah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case: {\i zeh she-achal melikah} \u8212- a non-{\i kohen} who ate from a bird {\i korban} that had been slaughtered by the {\i kohen} through {\i melikah} (the method of severing the bird\u8217's spine with the fingernail from behind the neck). {\i Melikah} is the valid method for bringing a bird sacrifice; when performed by a {\i kohen} on a qualifying {\i korban}, it renders the bird a legitimate offering, edible by {\i kohanim}. But this particular bird offering \u8212- a {\i chatas} \u8212- may only be eaten by {\i kohanim}. A non-{\i kohen} who eats it has certainly violated the prohibition against {\i zarim} eating from a {\i korban chatas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But has he also violated the prohibition of eating {\i neveilah}? Because {\i melikah}, unlike {\i shechita}, does not involve cutting the neck with a knife in a way that would render the bird a properly slaughtered animal for ordinary consumption. A bird slaughtered in this fashion outside the Temple context would indeed be a {\i neveilah}. Is this bird, then, simultaneously a kosher {\i korban} (permitted to {\i kohanim}) and a {\i neveilah} (with respect to {\i zarim})? Rabbi Chiya says twice; Bar Kappara says once. The same oaths, the same structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya\u8217's argument: {\i neveilah} was forbidden to all; when it was permitted in the Temple through the vehicle of {\i melikah}, that permission was granted to {\i kohanim} specifically \u8212- {\i lekohanim hutra, velo le-zarim}. For this non-{\i kohen}, it remains a {\i neveilah}. He therefore violates both. Bar Kappara\u8217's argument: when the Torah established {\i melikah} as the mode of bringing a bird sacrifice, it was declaring that an animal prepared this way {\i is not a neveilah} \u8212- not that it is a {\i neveilah} for which there happens to be a special permit. The Torah redefined the category: done this way, in this context, it simply is not a {\i neveilah} at all. Therefore there is no {\i neveilah} issue, and only the {\i zarus} prohibition applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara\u8217's position in this third case is arguably the most compelling of the three. There is a coherent reading of {\i melikah} as a mode of preparation that is {\i sui generis} \u8212- the Torah, by prescribing it, defined the result as {\i not neveilah} rather than as {\i neveilah with a dispensation}. This is a meaningful conceptual distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite the local coherence of each of Bar Kappara\u8217's arguments, the Gemara is going to reframe all three cases as expressing a single, unified {\i halakhic} debate rather than three separate local questions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simultaneous Prohibi (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Three Are Kolel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: {\i be-mai kamiflagi} \u8212- what is the underlying dispute between Rabbi Chiya and Bar Kappara across all three cases? The answer proposed: {\i be-issur kolel ve-aliba de-Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- they are debating {\i issur kolel} according to Rabbi Yose. Recall that we established Rabbi Yose holds of {\i issur mosif} but the question remained whether he holds of {\i issur kolel}. All three cases, the Gemara now suggests, are structured as instances of {\i issur kolel}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simultaneous Prohibi (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Chiya on Kolel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya holds: {\i Rabbi Yosi be-issur kolel mechayev tartei} \u8212- Rabbi Yose says that even an {\i issur kolel} can take effect, generating liability for two prohibitions. This is why Rabbi Chiya rules twice across all three cases \u8212- he understands them as {\i issur kolel} scenarios in which Rabbi Yose would impose dual liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Kappara on Kolel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara holds: {\i lo mechayev ella chada} \u8212- even Rabbi Yose does not permit the second prohibition to take effect when it is only {\i issur kolel}. The second prohibition must genuinely expand the scope of the object itself ({\i issur mosif}); merely sweeping the object into a broader class of the subject\u8217's prohibitions ({\i issur kolel}) is insufficient. Hence only one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then demonstrates how each of the three cases can be understood as {\i issur kolel}. In the case of the {\i zar} on {\i Shabbos}: initially, the {\i zar} could perform {\i melacha} freely on weekdays \u8212- his only prohibition was the {\i avodah} in the Temple. When {\i Shabbos} arrives, he becomes forbidden in {\i melacha} generally; the class of things he cannot do expands, sweeping the Temple {\i avodah} into that broader class. His own forbidden status \u8212- the {\i avodah} prohibition \u8212- has not changed; he was forbidden from the {\i avodah} all along. But the range of activities he cannot engage in has broadened, and because the {\i avodah} falls within the same category of activity as {\i melacha}, it is now doubly prohibited, so to speak \u8212- once from {\i zarus} and once because it is a form of {\i melacha} that he cannot perform on {\i Shabbos}. This is {\i issur kolel}: the subject\u8217's prohibited activities have expanded, sweeping the already-forbidden act into a broader category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the {\i baal mum} who became {\i tamei}: a {\i baal mum} cannot perform the {\i avodah} but may eat {\i korbanot}. Once he becomes {\i tamei}, he loses the ability to eat {\i korbanot} as well. Because he is now restricted in eating \u8212- and being restricted in eating {\i korbanot} is linked to being restricted in performing the {\i avodah} \u8212- the {\i tumah} status adds an additional dimension to his prohibition against performing the {\i avodah}. More of his activities are now forbidden; the {\i avodah} prohibition gets swept into this broader set of restrictions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Achat Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But here a fundamental problem emerges with the {\i melikah} case. The Gemara states: {\i ella melikah be-vat achat hi de-mishkachat lah \u8212- be-issur kolel lo mishkachat lah}. In the {\i melikah} case, you cannot construct any sequence at all. The moment the {\i kohen} performs {\i melikah}, two things happen simultaneously: the bird becomes a {\i neveilah} (were it not a {\i korban}) and it becomes a {\i korban} prohibited to {\i zarim}. There is no prior moment when one prohibition existed without the other. You cannot say the {\i zarus} prohibition came first and the {\i neveilah} prohibition was added later, or vice versa \u8212- they arise in the same instant. The very act of {\i melikah} introduces both {\i issurin} in {\i bat achat}, at one and the same moment. {\i Issur kolel} requires at minimum a conceptual sequence; if both prohibitions arise simultaneously, {\i issur kolel} cannot be the operative framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Real Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore proposes a reframing: {\i kamiflagi be-issur bat achat} \u8212- the debate between Rabbi Chiya and Bar Kappara is actually about what happens when two prohibitions arise simultaneously. And the debate is {\i aliba de-Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- according to Rabbi Yose, who is already known to permit {\i issur chal al issur} in the case of {\i issur mosif}. Would Rabbi Yose also say that when two prohibitions arise at exactly the same moment \u8212- {\i bat achat} \u8212- both take effect? Rabbi Chiya says yes: {\i Rabbi Yosi be-issur bat achat mechayev tartei}. Bar Kappara says no: {\i lo mechayev ella chada} \u8212- even simultaneous prohibitions yield only one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mosif vs.\u160 ?Kolel (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Application to Yevamot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To close the loop back to our original case, we must now apply the framework of {\i issur mosif} and {\i issur kolel} to the question of {\i eshet ach} and {\i achot isha} that opened the discussion. We established that {\i issur mosif} works when the second prohibition broadens the scope of persons to whom the object is forbidden. In the sequence where {\i achot isha} comes first \u8212- she is forbidden to Shimon alone \u8212- and then she becomes {\i eshet ach} by marrying Reuven, she is now forbidden to all of the brothers. That expansion of scope constitutes {\i issur mosif}, and Rabbi Yose says both prohibitions apply. In the reverse sequence, where {\i eshet ach} comes first \u8212- she is already forbidden to all the brothers \u8212- the subsequent {\i achot isha} status, which merely sweeps her into the broader class of sisters forbidden to Shimon, constitutes {\i issur kolel}. Since Rabbi Yose does not hold of {\i issur kolel}, only the first prohibition applies, and the {\i ke\u8217'ilu asah shta\u8217'im} formulation of Rava addresses the residual moral dimension in that sequence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reconciling Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the most pressing structural puzzle: how can both Rabbi Chiya and Bar Kappara have taken oaths that they heard opposite rulings from the same teacher, Rebbi? They are not simply disagreeing about the law; each claims a direct personal tradition. Somebody heard incorrectly, but the Gemara resists attributing this to dishonesty \u8212- hence the need to reconstruct a scenario in which both could have genuinely misunderstood. And crucially, the Gemara is not satisfied with an explanation that works in only one direction; it needs an account that explains how each side could have innocently reached the wrong conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theory One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first proposed resolution: {\i ki asnei l\u8217'dideih aliba de-Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- when Rebbi taught Rabbi Chiya, he was teaching according to Rabbi Yose, and said twice. {\i Ki asnei le-Bar Kappara aliba de-Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- when he taught Bar Kappara, he was presenting the view of Rabbi Shimon, and said once. Each student heard Rebbi\u8217's statement correctly but took it as Rebbi\u8217's definitive ruling rather than as a presentation of a particular Tanna\u8217's position. Rabbi Chiya heard twice and remembered twice; Bar Kappara heard once and remembered once. The mistake was one of attribution \u8212- each attached the ruling to Rebbi\u8217's own authority rather than to the Tanna whose position Rebbi was presenting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theory Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara is not fully satisfied with this explanation, however, and proposes a second framework. {\i Ela kamifligi be-issur bat achat aliba de-Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- perhaps the real debate is about {\i bat achat}, simultaneous prohibitions, according to Rabbi Shimon, not Rabbi Yose. The logic: Rabbi Yose is already known to permit {\i issur chal al issur} in certain cases. Rabbi Shimon categorically says {\i ein issur chal al issur}. Perhaps, however, even Rabbi Shimon would permit dual liability in the case of {\i bat achat} \u8212- when the prohibitions arise simultaneously, there is no moment where one preceded the other, and the logic of \u8220"one cannot take effect on top of the other\u8221" simply does not apply. They took hold together. Rabbi Chiya swears twice, maintaining that even Rabbi Shimon holds of liability in a {\i bat achat} scenario. Bar Kappara swears once, holding that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position is consistent: even {\i bat achat} yields only one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the Gemara identifies a persistent difficulty with both frameworks. In either formulation \u8212- whether the debate is {\i bat achat aliba de-Rabbi Yosi} or {\i bat achat aliba de-Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- one can explain how one of the two scholars might have misunderstood. Under theory one, Rabbi Chiya could understand Bar Kappara\u8217's mistake: Rebbi said once according to Rabbi Shimon, and Bar Kappara mistakenly took it as Rebbi\u8217's general ruling; Rabbi Chiya knows the twice ruling was according to Rabbi Yose. But under the same theory, it is harder to explain from Bar Kappara\u8217's vantage point how Rabbi Chiya could have innocently arrived at twice if, according to Bar Kappara, even Rabbi Yose only yields once for {\i bat achat} \u8212- what would twice even be based on?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under theory two, the problem is similarly asymmetric. If the debate is whether Rabbi Shimon allows {\i bat achat} to generate dual liability, then Rabbi Chiya is swearing that Rabbi Shimon permits twice in such a case, a position that contradicts the well-established understanding of Rabbi Shimon. One can understand why Bar Kappara would say once \u8212- that is simply Rabbi Shimon\u8217's known position. But {\i why} would Bar Kappara have needed to swear to that? It is the obvious, unremarkable reading of Rabbi Shimon. The oath adds nothing. And conversely, it becomes difficult to construct a plausible innocent mistake in the other direction. The Gemara leaves this {\i kashya} unresolved \u8212- {\i aliba de-Bar Kappara Rabbi Chiya shikurei ka-mishaker?} is asked but not answered to the Gemara\u8217's own satisfaction, and the discussion will continue.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 32b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Achos Isha & Eshes Ach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 32b picks up towards the bottom of the page, approximately six lines after the {\i gemara} begins, with {\i v\u8217'hashtah tanu rabbanan} \u8212- and now the Sages taught in a {\i baraita}. The case under discussion involves two brothers who have married two sisters, and the {\i gemara} has already established that even after one brother dies, his widow remains forbidden as {\i achos isha} \u8212- the surviving brother\u8217's wife\u8217's sister \u8212- to the surviving brother. Nothing here is dramatically new from what the {\i mishnah} has already introduced. But now the {\i gemara} presents a {\i baraita} that will open into a major conceptual discussion, taking us into very different territory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} describes a woman who is simultaneously forbidden in two distinct ways: she is both {\i eshes ach}, a brother\u8217's wife, and {\i achos isha}, a wife\u8217's sister. The scenario is not complicated to construct. One brother married a woman who happens to be the other brother\u8217's wife\u8217's sister. The brother dies \u8212- assume, for now, that there are children, so {\i yibbum} is not in play. This woman stands in two prohibited relationships to the surviving brother at once. If the surviving brother has relations with her, what is he liable for?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yossi: Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba aleha chayav aleha mishum eshes ach umishum achos isha divrei Rabbi Yossi} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yossi} says that he is liable under two headings: she is his brother\u8217's wife and she is his wife\u8217's sister. This seems entirely straightforward. She is in fact both, and the prohibition of each applies independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon: One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon omer eino chayav ela mishum eshes ach bilvad} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon says no: he is liable only for {\i eshes ach}, brother\u8217's wife, and not for {\i achos isha}, wife\u8217's sister. But this immediately generates a contradiction, because a second version of the {\i baraita} records Rabbi Shimon as saying precisely the reverse \u8212- {\i eino chayav ela mishum achos isha bilvad} \u8212- only wife\u8217's sister, not brother\u8217's wife. How can Rabbi Shimon say it is only one, and how can one version say it is {\i eshes ach} while the other says it is {\i achos isha}, if she is undeniably both?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Kashya Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction with {\i lo kashya}: there is no difficulty. The two versions of the {\i baraita} reflect two different factual sequences. {\i Kan shenasa chai v\u8217'achar kach nasa mes} \u8212- in one case, the surviving brother married first, and then the brother who would eventually die married her afterward. {\i Kan shenasa mes v\u8217'achar kach nasa chai} \u8212- in the other case, it was the reverse: the one who died married her first, and then the surviving brother married his own wife later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution flows from Rabbi Shimon\u8217's principle of {\i ein issur chal al issur} \u8212- one prohibited status does not take effect on top of a pre-existing one. The first prohibited relationship that was established is the operative one; the second cannot displace or supplement it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Sequence Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make this concrete: suppose Reuven is married to Rachel and Shimon is married to Leah, and Reuven and Shimon are brothers. Reuven dies. Rachel is now both {\i eshes ach} and {\i achos isha} to Shimon. Which status controls?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Reuven married Rachel before Shimon married Leah \u8212- {\i nasa mes v\u8217'achar kach nasa chai} \u8212- then when Reuven married Rachel, she first became {\i eshes ach}, a brother\u8217's wife, to Shimon. Only later, when Shimon married Leah, did she become {\i achos isha}. Rabbi Shimon says the first status, {\i eshes ach}, is the operative one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the order was reversed \u8212- Shimon first married Leah, making Rachel his {\i achos isha}, and only afterward did Reuven marry Rachel \u8212- then when Reuven\u8217's marriage came about, she was already his {\i achos isha}, and the status of {\i eshes ach} was the second one to arrive. Rabbi Shimon again says go by the first status: she is {\i achos isha}. That explains why one version of the {\i baraita} records {\i eshes ach} as the operative prohibition and the other records {\i achos isha} \u8212- the difference is entirely a matter of which relationship was established first.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ein Issur Chal Issur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concept Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the central concept that will drive the {\i gemara} for the balance of today\u8217's material: {\i ein issur chal al issur}, one prohibited status does not take effect on top of another pre-existing one. It is the foundational principle behind Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious question is: what practical difference does it make? Both {\i eshes ach} and {\i achos isha} carry the penalty of {\i kares}. The person is going to the same fate regardless. But the difference is not trivial. If the act was performed {\i beshogeg} \u8212- without full awareness of the prohibition \u8212- the question of whether there is one prohibition or two determines whether one brings one {\i korban} or two. That is a concrete and significant {\i nafka minah}, as one of the students correctly observed, and it sets the table for why the disagreement matters at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also asks whether {\i ein issur chal al issur} operates in other familiar contexts \u8212- such as a person who eats {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} on Yom Kippur, stacking multiple prohibitions on a single act. The answer, as the {\i gemara} will clarify, depends on the precise nature of the second prohibition and its relationship to the first. But before reaching those distinctions, the {\i gemara} introduces another dimension to the problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi: Hovering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: issur achos isha miktala tali vekay.} Rav Ashi explains that the prohibition of {\i achos isha} does not simply disappear or become legally nonexistent merely because {\i eshes ach} was there first. Rather, it hovers. It is a real and applicable status \u8212- it simply cannot take effect because there is a pre-existing status occupying that space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Replacement Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of this hovering is striking. {\i Ee paka issur eshes ach} \u8212- if the prohibition of {\i eshes ach} were somehow removed, {\i asi issur achos isha vechayeil} \u8212- the hovering prohibition of {\i achos isha} would immediately descend and take effect. The moment the first status is vacated, the second rushes in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibbum Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a paradox for {\i yibbum}. Assume the order was: Reuven married Rachel first, making her {\i eshes ach} to Shimon; then Shimon married Leah, making Rachel {\i achos isha} as well, but that second status merely hovers. Now Reuven dies childless. Ordinarily, {\i eshes ach} would dissolve through the fulfillment of {\i yibbum}. But Rav Ashi explains: the very act of performing {\i yibbum} would vacate the {\i eshes ach} status, which would immediately invite the {\i achos isha} prohibition to land. Rachel would become Shimon\u8217's {\i achos isha} the moment {\i yibbum} is attempted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehilchach lo paka} \u8212- therefore the {\i eshes ach} status does not dissolve. Because {\i yibbum} would only trigger the hovering prohibition, it cannot be performed. Rachel remains forbidden under the operative prohibition of {\i eshes ach} even when Reuven dies childless, and the other wives in the {\i yibbum} structure are consequently treated as {\i tzaras erva} as well. The mechanism is self-sealing: {\i yibbum} is impossible precisely because it would activate the very {\i issur} it would otherwise seem to avoid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yossi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a deep tension in Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position. Rabbi Yossi said in the {\i baraita} that the person who has relations with a woman who is both {\i eshes ach} and {\i achos isha} is liable for both. This implies that Rabbi Yossi holds {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- that a second prohibition can indeed take effect on top of a first. But can that really be his view consistently?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother-in-law Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehatanya} \u8212- and we have a {\i baraita} teaching that if someone committed a transgression that carries two different death penalties \u8212- {\i avar aveira sheyeish bah shtei misos} \u8212- the {\i halacha} is {\i nidon bachamura}, he is judged by the more severe punishment. A mother-in-law, {\i chamoso}, is punishable by {\i sreifa}, which is a weightier form of execution. A married woman, {\i eishet ish}, is punishable by {\i chenek}, which is considered the less severe form. Now consider a woman who is simultaneously a man\u8217's mother-in-law and a married woman \u8212- the most natural case being that his mother-in-law is married to his father-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} would say he is judged by {\i sreifa}, the heavier penalty, since that is the weightier of the two death sentences. But Rabbi Yossi says no: {\i nidon bezika harishona habaa alav} \u8212- he is judged according to the first relationship that defined their connection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Relationship Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} specifies how this plays out. If she was first his mother-in-law \u8212- she was widowed or divorced and then remarried \u8212- so that the {\i chamoso} status preceded the {\i eishet ish} status, then he is judged by {\i sreifa}. If, however, she was first a married woman to another man and then he married her daughter \u8212- so {\i eishet ish} came first and {\i chamoso} came later \u8212- then he is judged only by {\i chenek}, the penalty for {\i eishet ish}. The {\i chamoso} prohibition, though weightier, arrived second and does not displace the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only coherent way to explain why Rabbi Yossi would ever impose the {\i lighter} penalty \u8212- {\i chenek} rather than {\i sreifa} \u8212- is to say that he holds the second prohibition simply does not attach when a first one is already in place. He is not punished by {\i sreifa} because, in his view, the {\i chamoso} status never legally took effect. That is precisely {\i ein issur chal al issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequence Determines Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So we have a contradiction. In the case of {\i achos isha} and {\i eshes ach}, Rabbi Yossi seems to hold {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- you can get both statuses. In the case of mother-in-law and married woman, he clearly holds {\i ein issur chal al issur} \u8212- the first status controls and the second does not take effect. Which is it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Abbahu Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Abbahu: modeh Rabbi Yose be\u8217'issur mosif} \u8212- Rabbi Abbahu resolves the contradiction. Rabbi Yossi concedes, as a category, to {\i issur mosif}. This introduces the crucial distinction that will occupy the {\i gemara} from this point forward, and it opens the door to a broader taxonomy of how second prohibitions relate to first ones.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mosif vs.\u160 ?Kolel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Mosif Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are, as the {\i gemara} now elaborates, multiple ways in which a second {\i issur} can relate to a first. The {\i gemara} identifies two principal categories: {\i issur mosif} and {\i issur kolel}. A third \u8212- {\i issur be\u8217'vas achas}, simultaneous prohibitions \u8212- will be addressed later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Issur mosif} means a prohibition that broadens the scope of {\i people} to whom the forbidden object or person is prohibited. The focus is on the {\i cheftza} \u8212- the object or person who is forbidden \u8212- and the question is whether that object or person has become forbidden to a wider circle of people under the second {\i issur} than it was under the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mosif Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Return to the case of {\i achos isha} and {\i eshes ach}. Consider the sequence: Shimon first marries Leah, making Rachel forbidden to Shimon as {\i achos isha} \u8212- wife\u8217's sister. Subsequently, Reuven marries Rachel. At the moment Reuven marries Rachel, Rachel becomes {\i eshes ach} \u8212- brother\u8217's wife \u8212- not only to Shimon but to all of Reuven\u8217's brothers. Her status has expanded. Before Reuven\u8217's marriage, Rachel was forbidden to Shimon alone under the {\i achos isha} prohibition. After Reuven\u8217's marriage, she is forbidden to all of Reuven\u8217's brothers \u8212- a broader scope of people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is {\i issur mosif}: the second {\i issur}, {\i eshes ach}, is broader in scope than the first, {\i achos isha}, because it applies to a wider population. The logic is that since this new status, {\i eshes ach}, has genuine relevance to the other brothers \u8212- {\i migo d\u8217'itosaf issur legabei achim}, since an additional prohibition accrues with respect to the brothers \u8212- {\i itosaf issur legabei deidei} \u8212- it also takes effect with respect to Shimon, even though she was already forbidden to him under the first status. The new status has enough traction to be real because it matters to other people; and because it is real, it attaches even for Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why Rabbi Yossi can say, in one sequence, that the person is liable for both {\i eshes ach} and {\i achos isha}: when {\i achos isha} comes first and {\i eshes ach} comes second, the second {\i issur} is an {\i issur mosif} and can take effect despite the prior prohibition. Rabbi Yossi concedes to {\i issur mosif} \u8212- he agrees that a second {\i issur} of broader scope can impose itself on a first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a question immediately arises: what about the reverse sequence? If she first became {\i eshes ach} \u8212- Reuven married her first, making her the brother\u8217's wife, forbidden to all brothers \u8212- and then Shimon married Leah, now making Rachel his {\i achos isha} as well, why would Rabbi Yossi say both in that case? {\i Mai issur mosif ika?} \u8212- what broadening of scope is there here? She was already forbidden to all the brothers as {\i eshes ach}. {\i Achos isha} is actually a narrower {\i issur} \u8212- it applies only to Shimon, not to the other brothers. It adds no additional people. So there is no {\i issur mosif} in this sequence, and it seems Rabbi Yossi should agree that only the first status, {\i eshes ach}, applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Perhaps one might argue: {\i migu d\u8217'issar becholhu achvasa} \u8212- since when Shimon married Leah, all of her sisters became forbidden to Shimon, maybe Rachel now finds herself within a larger class of forbidden women, and that is a kind of broadening. But the {\i gemara} identifies this as something categorically different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Kolel Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to {\i issur kolel}. While {\i issur mosif} focuses on the {\i cheftza} \u8212- the forbidden person\u8217's scope has broadened to include more people \u8212- {\i issur kolel} focuses on the {\i gavra}, the person who is prohibited. In {\i issur kolel}, the forbidden object has not become forbidden to more people. Rather, the person who is subject to the prohibition now finds himself prohibited in a broader range of activities or in a broader class of forbidden relationships.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The clearest way to distinguish them: in {\i issur mosif}, Rachel herself becomes forbidden to more people. In {\i issur kolel}, Shimon finds himself forbidden to more women \u8212- Rachel is now part of a larger category of women that Shimon cannot marry. She has not become prohibited to a wider circle; rather, she has been swept into a wider {\i klal} of things forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kolel Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The example is exactly the scenario of the reverse sequence: Reuven married Rachel first, making her {\i eshes ach} to all the brothers. Then Shimon married Leah, making all of Leah\u8217's sisters \u8212- including Rachel \u8212- forbidden to Shimon as {\i achos isha}. Rachel\u8217's status vis-\u224 ?-vis the brothers has not changed; she was forbidden to them all before and remains so. What has changed is that Shimon, by marrying Leah, now finds himself forbidden to a broader range of women: not only Rachel, but all of Leah\u8217's sisters. Rachel finds herself included in that broader class.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question {\i issur kolel} asks is: since Rachel\u8217's sisters are now forbidden to Shimon, and Rachel is one of those sisters, does the fact that she has been drawn into this larger {\i klal} of forbidden women allow the {\i achos isha} prohibition to attach \u8212- even though she was already forbidden to Shimon under {\i eshes ach}? The argument would be: since the new {\i issur} covers a broader category of activities or relationships for the {\i gavra}, perhaps it can take hold even where a prior {\i issur} already exists, because it is operating at a different level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i mosif} and {\i kolel} can be summarized this way: {\i issur mosif} changes the status of the forbidden {\i cheftza} by expanding the scope of people to whom it is prohibited. {\i Issur kolel} changes the status of the {\i gavra} by expanding the range of things he is prohibited from. In the first case, the forbidden thing is now forbidden more broadly. In the second case, the forbidden person now has a broader set of prohibitions, of which this one is part.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kolel Application Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks whether Rabbi Yossi, who concedes to {\i issur mosif}, would also hold of {\i issur kolel} \u8212- whether a second {\i issur} that broadens the {\i gavra}\u8217's prohibited activities can also take effect on a pre-existing first {\i issur}. This question is what sets up Rava\u8217's intervention.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava: As If Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Formulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ella amar Rava} \u8212- Rava steps in with a formulation that is at once elegant and puzzling: {\i mah lani alav k\u8217'ilu asah shtayim v\u8217'eino chayav ela achas} \u8212- it is as if he transgressed two, but he is only liable for one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava is acknowledging the force of the preceding analysis. Rabbi Yossi holds {\i issur mosif}, not {\i issur kolel}. In the sequence where {\i eshes ach} came first and {\i achos isha} came second, there is no {\i issur mosif} \u8212- the second {\i issur} does not broaden the scope of who is forbidden. And since Rabbi Yossi does not hold of {\i issur kolel} \u8212- that is clear from the mother-in-law case \u8212- technically, Rabbi Yossi in that reverse sequence ought to agree that only one prohibition applies. So why does he say you transgress both?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's answer: in the reverse sequence, Rabbi Yossi does not mean that literally, technically, in terms of punishment and consequence, two prohibitions have taken effect. He means rather that {\i k\u8217'ilu} \u8212- it is {\i as if} \u8212- two prohibitions were transgressed. The same formulation is cited in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ki ata Ravin amar Reb Yochanan mah lani leh k\u8217'ilu asah shtayim v\u8217'eino chayav ela achas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mah Nafka Mina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately presses the obvious question: {\i mah nafka mina?} If he is only liable for one, what difference does the {\i k\u8217'ilu} make? What is the practical meaning of saying it is {\i as if} he transgressed two, if in terms of punishment and legal consequence there is only one? If we strip away punishment entirely \u8212- imagine the {\i Torah} had imposed no penalty \u8212- would we say this person violated one prohibition or two? {\i Ein issur chal al issur}, as Rabbi Shimon frames it, might not mean the second prohibition literally does not exist; it might mean only that its consequences are subsumed by the first. The act may still be a genuine transgression of both, even if the practical consequences are assessed through the first status alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Burial Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Likavro bein reshaim gemurim} \u8212- the {\i nafka minah} is where the person is buried after {\i beis din} executes him. The Sanhedrin would bury those it executed in designated sections of the cemetery, calibrated to the severity of their crime. This determination is not about punishment per se \u8212- it is about personal status and identity, about what this person\u8217's actions say about {\i who he is}. If he is treated {\i b\u8217'gavra}, in terms of personal standing, as someone who transgressed two prohibitions, he is buried among the fully wicked. This is the tangible consequence of the {\i k\u8217'ilu} \u8212- not an additional penalty imposed on the act, but a statement about the person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a strikingly thin {\i nafka minah}, and the precise nature of the dispute with Rabbi Shimon remains somewhat elusive. But what the {\i gemara} has now established is a full conceptual map: Rabbi Shimon holds categorically {\i ein issur chal al issur}; Rabbi Yossi concedes to {\i issur mosif} \u8212- when the second {\i issur} broadens the scope of people who are forbidden \u8212- but does not hold of {\i issur kolel}; and in cases that fall into neither category, Rava explains Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position as a {\i k\u8217'ilu} \u8212- a matter of identity and status, not of additional punishment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Parallel Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zar BeShabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now connects the principle of {\i issur chal al issur} to a famous set of three parallel debates, each involving a situation where two prohibitions seem to converge on a single act. The first case: {\i zar sheshimmesh b\u8217'shabbos} \u8212- a non-{\i kohen} who performs the {\i avodah} in the {\i Beis Hamikdash} on {\i Shabbos}. A non-{\i kohen} who performs the Temple service is liable with the death penalty. Someone who performs {\i melacha} on {\i Shabbos} is also liable. Here both seem to apply simultaneously. Is he liable twice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chiyya vs.\u160 ?Bar Kappara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Chiyya omer chayav shtayim} \u8212- Rabbi Chiyya says he is liable for two. {\i Bar Kappara omer eino chayav ela achas} \u8212- Bar Kappara says only one. Both then take dramatic oaths. {\i Kafatz Rabbi Chiyya v\u8217'nishba: ha\u8217'avodah, kach shamati meRebbi shtayim} \u8212- Rabbi Chiyya jumped up and swore, by the Temple service itself: this is what I heard from Rebbe, that he is liable twice. {\i Kafatz Bar Kappara v\u8217'nishba: ha\u8217'avodah, kach shamati meRebbi achas} \u8212- Bar Kappara jumped up and swore the same oath in the opposite direction: this is what I heard from Rebbe, only once. The oaths make it impossible to dismiss either report as simple error of transmission \u8212- both are insisting with the utmost solemnity on having heard from the same source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiyya\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiyya then offers his reasoning. {\i Shabbos l\u8217'chol nitra} \u8212- {\i Shabbos} was forbidden to everyone. {\i K\u8217'shehutra b\u8217'Mikdash} \u8212- when it was permitted in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, {\i etzel kohanim hutra} \u8212- it was permitted specifically for {\i kohanim}. {\i L\u8217'kohanim hutra v\u8217'lo l\u8217'zarim} \u8212- the dispensation applies to {\i kohanim}, not to non-{\i kohanim}. Therefore this person, as a {\i zar}, never received any dispensation from the {\i Shabbos} prohibition. {\i Yesh kan mishum zarus v\u8217'yesh kan mishum Shabbos} \u8212- there is a violation of performing unauthorized {\i avodah} as a non-{\i kohen}, and there is also a violation of {\i Shabbos}. Both apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Kappara Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara\u8217's counter-argument is structurally similar but arrives at an opposite conclusion. {\i Shabbos l\u8217'chola nasra} \u8212- {\i Shabbos} was forbidden to all. {\i K\u8217'shehutra, b\u8217'Mikdash hutra} \u8212- when a dispensation was given, it was given for the {\i Mikdash} as such, not specifically for {\i kohanim}. The {\i Mikdash} context itself waives the {\i Shabbos} concern. Therefore, anyone performing any activity in the {\i Mikdash} on {\i Shabbos} \u8212- even a {\i zar} performing unauthorized {\i avodah} \u8212- is operating in a context where {\i Shabbos} has been waived. {\i Ein kan ela zarus} \u8212- the only violation is the prohibition of a non-{\i kohen} performing the {\i avodah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position is, as one might note, genuinely surprising. It seems to imply that anyone who walks into the {\i Beis Hamikdash} on {\i Shabbos} and strikes a match incurs no {\i Shabbos} violation \u8212- which is bizarre. But Bar Kappara is apparently conceptualizing the {\i Mikdash} as a space where the {\i Shabbos} prohibition does not apply, full stop, rather than as a space where {\i kohanim} receive a specific dispensation to perform the {\i avodah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'al Mum BeTemeiut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second parallel case: {\i ba\u8217'al mum sheshimmesh b\u8217'tuma} \u8212- a {\i kohen} with a physical blemish who performs the {\i avodah} while {\i tamei}. A {\i ba\u8217'al mum} is ordinarily forbidden to perform the {\i avodah}, though he may eat from the {\i korbanot}. A {\i tamei} person is also forbidden to perform the {\i avodah}. Here the relevant background is that {\i tumah} can be overridden for a {\i korban tzibbur} \u8212- a communal sacrifice. Were this a normal {\i kohen}, he would have a dispensation to perform the {\i avodah} despite being {\i tamei} when there is a {\i korban tzibbur}. But this {\i kohen} has a blemish, which bars him from performing the {\i avodah} regardless. When he nonetheless performs the {\i avodah} while {\i tamei} in the context of a {\i korban tzibbur}, is he liable for both prohibitions \u8212- {\i ba\u8217'al mum} and {\i tumah} \u8212- or only for {\i ba\u8217'al mum}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again Rabbi Chiyya says twice and Bar Kappara says once, and both take the same solemn oath attributing their position to Rebbe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's reasoning: {\i tumah l\u8217'chola nasra} \u8212- {\i tumah} was forbidden to all. {\i K\u8217'shehutra b\u8217'Mikdash l\u8217'kohanim temimim hutra} \u8212- when a dispensation was given, it was given to unblemished {\i kohanim} who are actually eligible to perform the {\i avodah}. A {\i ba\u8217'al mum} is not among those for whom the dispensation was granted. Therefore the {\i tumah} prohibition still applies to him. {\i Yesh kan mishum ba\u8217'al mum v\u8217'yesh kan mishum tumah} \u8212- both prohibitions are in force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara counters: {\i k\u8217'shehutra b\u8217'Mikdash, b\u8217'Mikdash hutra} \u8212- when the dispensation was given, it was given for anything performed in the {\i Mikdash} in the context of {\i korban tzibbur}. The waiver is contextual, not person-specific. In this context, {\i tumah} as such is not a violation. {\i Ein kan ela mishum ba\u8217'al mum} \u8212- the only operative prohibition is that of the blemished {\i kohen} performing the {\i avodah}. This argument, notably, carries more intuitive weight than the {\i Shabbos} case. The principle of {\i tumah hutra b\u8217'tzibbur} is itself a known categorical rule; one can plausibly argue that in the context of communal sacrifice, {\i tumah} concerns are simply set aside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zar Melikah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case: {\i zar she\u8217'achal melikah} \u8212- a non-{\i kohen} who ate from a bird offering that was slaughtered by {\i melikah}. The {\i kohen} pierces the back of the bird\u8217's neck with his fingernail. This is the prescribed method for a bird {\i chatas}. If this were done outside the context of a {\i korban}, the bird would be a {\i neveilah} \u8212- an animal that did not die through a valid {\i shechitah}. In the context of the {\i korban}, it is kosher and may be eaten by {\i kohanim}. A non-{\i kohen} ate this bird. He has certainly violated the prohibition against a {\i zar} eating a {\i korban chatas}. Has he also violated the prohibition against eating {\i neveilah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again Rabbi Chiyya says twice and Bar Kappara says once; again both swear by {\i ha\u8217'avodah} that Rebbe said their version.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's reasoning: {\i neveilah lechol ne\u8217'esra} \u8212- {\i neveilah} was forbidden to all. {\i K\u8217'shehutra b\u8217'Mikdash, etzel kohanim hutra} \u8212- when it was permitted in the {\i Mikdash}, by virtue of {\i melikah} making it a valid {\i korban}, that permission was granted specifically to {\i kohanim}. For a {\i zar}, it remains {\i neveilah}. {\i Yesh kan mishum zarus v\u8217'yesh kan mishum melikah} \u8212- two violations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara responds: {\i k\u8217'shehutra b\u8217'Mikdash hutrah} \u8212- the Torah itself, in prescribing {\i melikah} as the method for bird sacrifices, effectively declared that a bird killed this way is not a {\i neveilah}. It is simply not in the category of {\i neveilah}. {\i Ein kan ela mishum zarus} \u8212- there is only the violation of a non-{\i kohen} eating from a {\i korban}. This argument, as noted, carries real force: one can reasonably read the Torah\u8217's prescription of {\i melikah} as a wholesale redefinition of that act, such that the {\i neveilah} category simply does not apply.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issur Chal Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reframing Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having presented all three parallel debates, the {\i gemara} steps back and asks: {\i bemai kamifligi?} \u8212- what is the underlying disagreement? On the surface, each case looks like a local question about how to conceptualize a specific halakhic dispensation. But the {\i gemara} suggests that these three parallel disputes reflect a unified deeper question, and it proposes that the real debate is: {\i be\u8217'issur kolel ve\u8217'aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Yosei} \u8212- it concerns the principle of {\i issur kolel}, and specifically whether Rabbi Yossi would apply it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We have already established that Rabbi Yossi holds {\i issur mosif} \u8212- he allows a second {\i issur} to take effect when it broadens the scope of people to whom the {\i cheftza} is forbidden. The question now is whether Rabbi Yossi would also hold {\i issur kolel} \u8212- whether a second {\i issur} that broadens the class of {\i activities} forbidden to the person can take effect on a pre-existing prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiyya: Kolel Applies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Chiyya savar Rabbi Yosei be\u8217'issur kolel mechayev} \u8212- Rabbi Chiyya holds that Rabbi Yossi would say {\i issur kolel} does allow a second prohibition to take effect, generating liability twice. All three of the parallel cases are instances of {\i issur kolel}, and Rabbi Chiyya is arguing that Rabbi Yossi\u8217's logic extends to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Kappara: Kolel Doesn\u8217't Apply\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bar Kappara savar lo mechayev ela chada} \u8212- Bar Kappara holds that even Rabbi Yossi would not apply {\i issur kolel} to create double liability. Only one prohibition counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zar BeShabbat Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through each case to demonstrate where {\i issur kolel} appears. For the {\i zar} who performs {\i avodah} on {\i Shabbos}: {\i zar me\u8217'ikara shari bemelacha v\u8217'assur ba\u8217'avodah} \u8212- initially, on any weekday, the {\i zar} is permitted to perform {\i melacha} but is forbidden to perform the {\i avodah} in the {\i Mikdash}. That is his one prohibition. Then {\i Shabbos} arrives. {\i Asya leShabbos: migu d\u8217'kamitra bemelacha, itsar nami ba\u8217'avodah} \u8212- since he is now prohibited from {\i melacha} in general, and the {\i avodah} falls within the category of {\i melacha}, the broader prohibition of {\i Shabbos melacha} now encompasses the {\i avodah} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is {\i issur kolel}: the {\i zar}\u8217's prohibited activities have broadened. Before {\i Shabbos}, he could do most things but not the {\i avodah}. On {\i Shabbos}, he cannot do a wide class of activities \u8212- {\i melacha} generally \u8212- and the {\i avodah} is included within that broader class. The {\i avodah} has not become forbidden to more people ({\i mosif}); rather, the {\i zar} now has a broader set of prohibited activities ({\i kolel}), into which the {\i avodah} is swept.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'al Mum Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the {\i ba\u8217'al mum}: initially, he could eat from {\i korbanot} but could not perform the {\i avodah}. Then he became {\i tamei}. {\i Migu d\u8217'itassar ba\u8217'achila, itassar nami ba\u8217'avodah} \u8212- since he is now prohibited from eating {\i korbanot} due to {\i tumah}, and his relationship to {\i korbanot} has broadly changed, that broader prohibition also extends to performing the {\i avodah}. The class of activities related to {\i korbanot} that are now forbidden to him has widened, and the {\i avodah} is within that class. Again, {\i issur kolel}: his prohibited activities have broadened.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issur Chal Analysis (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melikah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But when the {\i gemara} tries to apply the same {\i issur kolel} framework to the third case \u8212- the {\i zar} who ate {\i melikah} \u8212- it runs into a problem. {\i Ela: melikah be\u8217'vas achas hi} \u8212- the {\i melikah} case is one where everything happens at once. The moment the {\i kohen} performs {\i melikah} on the bird, two things occur simultaneously: the bird becomes a valid {\i korban}, triggering the prohibition of a {\i zar} eating it; and it also has the status of {\i neveilah} for those not eligible to eat it. There is no sequence \u8212- no prior state in which one prohibition existed before the other. {\i Be\u8217'issur kolel lo mishkachas lah} \u8212- you simply cannot construct an {\i issur kolel} scenario in this case, because {\i issur kolel} requires a first {\i issur} already in place before a second, broader one arrives. Here both arrive at the same instant. The {\i issur kolel} analysis, therefore, cannot be what is at stake in the {\i melikah} case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bat Achat Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the {\i gemara} to a fresh formulation. {\i Kamipalgi be\u8217'issur be\u8217'vas achas} \u8212- the real debate underlying all three cases is not {\i issur kolel} but rather {\i issur be\u8217'vas achas}: what happens when two prohibitions arise simultaneously, at the exact same moment? According to Rabbi Yossi, who is prepared under some conditions to hold that a second {\i issur} takes effect despite a first, would he say the same when the two {\i issurim} arrive together, with no sequencing at all? {\i Rabbi Chiyya savar Rabbi Yosei be\u8217'issur be\u8217'vas achat mechayev tarti} \u8212- Rabbi Chiyya holds that Rabbi Yossi would impose double liability even when both prohibitions arise at once. {\i Bar Kappara savar lo mechayev ela chada} \u8212- Bar Kappara holds that even Rabbi Yossi would impose only one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenarios Constructed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then addresses an obvious challenge: if this is about simultaneous prohibitions, where does the simultaneity appear in the {\i Shabbos} and {\i ba\u8217'al mum} cases? Those seem to involve a prior condition ({\i zar}; {\i ba\u8217'al mum}) already in place before {\i Shabbos} arrives or before {\i tumah} supervenes. The {\i gemara} constructs specific scenarios to generate genuine {\i be\u8217'vas achas} situations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zar Bar Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kegon d\u8217'aysi shtei sa\u8217'aros b\u8217'Shabbos} \u8212- imagine a young man who reaches adulthood \u8212- acquiring his two pubic hairs, the halachic sign of majority \u8212- at the very moment {\i Shabbos} begins. Before that moment, he was a minor and was not subject to these prohibitions in the same way. At the instant he becomes an adult, it is simultaneously {\i Shabbos}, and simultaneously he is a {\i zar}. Both the prohibition of a non-{\i kohen} performing the {\i avodah} and the prohibition of {\i Shabbos} {\i melacha} descend upon him at the exact same moment. {\i D\u8217'havya leh zarus v\u8217'shabbos bahadi hadadi} \u8212- {\i zarus} and {\i Shabbos} come to him together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohein Bar Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} case: {\i kegon d\u8217'afik shtei sa\u8217'aros b\u8217'mum} \u8212- imagine a young man who had a blemish and who was also {\i tamei}, and who acquired his two pubic hairs \u8212- reached adulthood \u8212- at this moment. Before majority, neither prohibition was operative on him in the full sense. At the moment of majority, both the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} prohibition and the {\i tumah} prohibition descended simultaneously. {\i D\u8217'havya leh ba\u8217'al mum v\u8217'tumah bahadi hadadi} \u8212- both came at the same time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knife Injury Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively \u8212- and this is presented as a somewhat more interesting construction \u8212- {\i shechatach etzba\u8217'o b\u8217'sakin tme\u8217'iah} \u8212- he cut his finger with a {\i tamei} knife. At the very moment he was cut, he simultaneously acquired a {\i mum} \u8212- the blemish disqualifying him from the {\i avodah} \u8212- and became {\i tamei} through contact with the {\i tamei} knife. Both statuses arrived at the same instant, without any possibility of sequencing one before the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Disagreement Reconstruct\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Heard Rebbe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the theoretical framework now constructed, the {\i gemara} returns to the immediate puzzle with which it began: how can Rabbi Chiyya and Bar Kappara take opposite oaths about what Rebbe said? Rebbe said one thing. The {\i gemara} is unwilling to say Rebbe changed his mind, and it is unwilling to say either rabbi is lying \u8212- particularly given the oaths. So it seeks to reconstruct how both could have heard accurately and still arrived at opposite conclusions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Tannaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bishlama l\u8217'Rabbi Chiyya} \u8212- it works easily according to Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's perspective. {\i Ki asnei l\u8217'didei aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Yosei} \u8212- when Rebbe taught Rabbi Chiyya, saying you get double liability, he was presenting the position according to Rabbi Yossi, who is the one prepared to hold {\i issur chal al issur} and who, per Rabbi Chiyya, extends it even to {\i be\u8217'vas achas} cases. {\i Ki asnei l\u8217'Bar Kappara aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- and when Rebbe taught Bar Kappara, saying only once, he was presenting the view of Rabbi Shimon, who categorically holds {\i ein issur chal al issur}. From Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's vantage point, Bar Kappara\u8217's mistake is entirely understandable: he heard the teaching according to Rabbi Shimon and mistook it for Rebbe\u8217's definitive ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the symmetrical problem arises immediately. According to Bar Kappara\u8217's understanding \u8212- that even Rabbi Yossi would hold only once in a {\i be\u8217'vas achas} case \u8212- how can he explain what Rabbi Chiyya heard? {\i Ela l\u8217'Bar Kappara Rabbi Chiyya shikurei kameshaker?} \u8212- is Rabbi Chiyya simply lying? He swore. The {\i gemara} cannot accept that either sworn testimony is fabricated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Truthfulness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} grapples with the fact that both rabbis are insisting with equal conviction on having heard from the same master. Any reconstruction must be capable of accounting for both directions \u8212- explaining how each could have heard accurately, and how each could charitably understand where the other went wrong. Simply asserting one framework or the other always leaves one of the two swearing truthfully and the other irreconcilably mistaken in a way that is hard to explain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore shifts the frame of the entire debate. {\i Ela k\u8217'mipalgi b\u8217'issur bas achas aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- rather than asking whether Rabbi Yossi would extend {\i issur} to {\i be\u8217'vas achas} cases, the real dispute may concern Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position on simultaneous prohibitions. Rabbi Shimon ordinarily and categorically holds {\i ein issur chal al issur}. The question is whether {\i be\u8217'vas achas} might be an exception even for Rabbi Shimon \u8212- since when both prohibitions arrive at the exact same instant, neither can be said to pre-exist the other. There is no first {\i issur} for the second to overlay. Perhaps even Rabbi Shimon would concede that both take effect simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading: Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's oath that Rebbe said twice reflects Rebbe teaching that even Rabbi Shimon holds double liability in a {\i be\u8217'vas achas} case. Bar Kappara\u8217's oath that Rebbe said once reflects Rebbe teaching that Rabbi Shimon holds his usual position \u8212- {\i ein issur chal al issur} \u8212- even when they arrive together. Both could have heard Rebbe accurately, each in a different teaching session or context, each presenting Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. Rabbi Chiyya is not saying Rabbi Yossi; both are debating what Rabbi Shimon holds about the {\i be\u8217'vas achas} scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} then presses the same question in reverse: {\i bishlama l\u8217'Bar Kappara kamishhtava l\u8217'afuki l\u8217'Rabbi Shimon me\u8217'Chizkiya} \u8212- according to Bar Kappara, Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's oath makes sense as arguing that even Rabbi Shimon holds twice in a {\i be\u8217'vas achas} case, that being a non-trivial claim worth swearing about. {\i Ela l\u8217'Rabbi Chiyya lamma lei l\u8217'ishtabuei?} \u8212- but according to Rabbi Chiyya, why would Bar Kappara need to swear? Bar Kappara is simply saying Rabbi Shimon holds his normal position, one violation. That is not a controversial claim requiring an oath. {\i Kashya} \u8212- the {\i gemara} records this as a difficulty, unresolved at this point in the discussion. The full resolution of how both sworn testimonies can be vindicated simultaneously will be addressed as the {\i gemara} continues.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 33\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chiya/Bar Kappara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 33b picks up a dispute between Rabbi Chiya and Bar Kappara concerning three scenarios involving transgressions performed simultaneously or in sequence in the Temple context: a {\i zar} (non-kohen) who performs the {\i avodah} on {\i Shabbat}, a {\i baal mum} (physically blemished kohen) who performs the {\i avodah} in a state of {\i tumah} at a time when {\i tumah hutra betzibbur} (communal impurity is permitted), and a {\i zar} who eats from a bird upon which {\i melikah} (the nape-pinching slaughter unique to bird-offerings) was performed. The question was whether, in each scenario, the individual transgresses one prohibition or two. The Gemara sought to frame this disagreement as a debate about {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- whether a second prohibition can take effect upon a preexisting one \u8212- and explored several explanatory models.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Kolel/Bevas Achas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The model of {\i issur kolel} \u8212- where a later prohibition encompasses a broader category and thus takes effect despite a prior prohibition already being in place \u8212- could account for the first two scenarios, which admit of a sequential reading: first one becomes a {\i zar}, and only thereafter does {\i Shabbat} arrive; first one is a {\i baal mum}, and only thereafter does {\i tumah} set in. The {\i melikah} case, however, cannot be sequential. The moment the bird\u8217's neck is pinched, it simultaneously becomes a {\i korban} forbidden to non-kohanim and becomes non-kosher for ordinary consumption. These two prohibitions arise at the exact same instant \u8212- {\i bevas achas} \u8212- making it impossible to say that one preceded the other. The Gemara therefore concluded that the underlying debate is whether {\i issur chal al issur} can operate {\i bevas achas}, and specifically whether Rabbi Shimon \u8212- who ordinarily rejects {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- would concede that when two prohibitions arise simultaneously, both take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Kappara\u8217's Error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion, however, creates a difficulty. Both Rabbi Chiya and Bar Kappara swore oaths that they had heard Rebbi\u8217's ruling directly: Rabbi Chiya maintained that Rebbi said two transgressions, Bar Kappara that Rebbi said one. If the {\i bevas achas} case genuinely yields two transgressions even according to the most lenient opinion \u8212- even according to Rabbi Shimon \u8212- then from Rabbi Chiya\u8217's perspective, how could Bar Kappara ever have heard from Rebbi that one only transgresses once? The Gemara poses this starkly: {\i ela l\u8217'Rabbi Chiya, Bar Kappara shikra ka meshaker?} \u8212- is Bar Kappara simply lying?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Misremembering Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya\u8217's answer is that Bar Kappara is not lying \u8212- he genuinely misremembered. The Gemara explains: {\i ki asniyah Rebbi lidi-di tarti l\u8217'patur asniyah} \u8212- when Rebbi taught the ruling of \u8220"one transgression\u8221" to Bar Kappara, he was teaching only the first two cases, those of the {\i zar} on {\i Shabbat} and the {\i baal mum} in {\i tumah}. In those two cases, the sequence is that first one already has the status of {\i zar} or {\i baal mum}, and only afterward the additional prohibition of {\i Shabbat} or {\i tumah} arrives. This is a case of {\i issur kolel} \u8212- the later prohibition is broader in scope. According to Rabbi Shimon, who rejects {\i issur chal al issur}, if he even rejects {\i issur mosif} (a prohibition that extends the class of persons to whom the object is forbidden), he certainly rejects {\i issur kolel}. So Rebbi taught Bar Kappara that according to Rabbi Shimon, in these two sequential cases you transgress only once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then Bar Kappara encountered the third case \u8212- {\i vezar she\u8217'achal melicha}, the {\i zar} who ate from the {\i melikah} bird. Because it was structurally similar \u8212- again a non-kohen doing something ordinarily permitted when done correctly \u8212- he mixed it together with the first two in his mind: {\i ve\u8217'aydi d\u8217'damya lei arbav bahadeihu}. Now, treating all three as a single teaching, he noticed that the only common denominator across the newly combined set was {\i bevas achas}. He therefore concluded that the entire teaching was about {\i bevas achas}, and since Rebbi had exempted from a second transgression in those cases, he assumed Rebbi meant that {\i bevas achas} also yields only one transgression. In short, Bar Kappara misremembered \u8212- he inadvertently inserted the {\i melikah} case, which changed the character of the set, and drew a conclusion that Rebbi had never actually taught.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Forms Summarized\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis occasions a useful summary of the three forms by which {\i issur chal al issur} can operate. {\i Issur mosif} is perhaps the most intuitively accessible: a second prohibition takes effect because it extends the forbidden status to a wider audience \u8212- more people, or more types of activity, are now prohibited with respect to this object. {\i Issur kolel} is harder: the object was already forbidden to everyone, but now more items within the same class are also forbidden, and by virtue of that expansion, the original prohibition is deemed to have been re-imposed in its fuller form. {\i Bevas achas} \u8212- when both prohibitions arise simultaneously \u8212- is in some respects the most conceptually straightforward, since there is no prior prohibition whose \u8220"territorial claim\u8221" would be challenged; both take effect together, and it is argued that even Rabbi Shimon would concede to this.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beraita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zar/Baal Mum Beraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the debate between Rabbi Chiya and Bar Kappara concerns whether {\i bevas achas} would cause even Rabbi Shimon to concede that two transgressions are incurred, the Gemara turns to a {\i beraita} that addresses almost precisely the same cases. The {\i beraita} states: {\i zar sheshimash b\u8217'Shabbat u\u8217'vaal mum sheshimash b\u8217'tumah} \u8212- a {\i zar} who performed the {\i avodah} on {\i Shabbat}, and a {\i baal mum} who performed the {\i avodah} in {\i tumah} even in a situation of {\i tumah hutra betzibbur}. In the first case, says the {\i beraita}, one transgresses both for being a {\i zar} and for {\i Shabbat}; in the second, for being a {\i baal mum} and for {\i tumah}. This is the position of Rabbi Yose. Rabbi Shimon disagrees: {\i ein kan ela mishum zar u\u8217'vaal mum bilvad} \u8212- there is only the first, original transgression of being a {\i zar} or {\i baal mum}; the subsequently arriving prohibition of {\i Shabbat} or {\i tumah} does not take further effect. This confirms that the debate is indeed between Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon, the very Tannaim known to disagree about {\i issur chal al issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melikah Omitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, the {\i beraita} discusses only two of the three scenarios \u8212- and conspicuously omits the {\i melikah} case. The Gemara asks: for whom was it omitted, and why? If the omission is from Rabbi Yose\u8217's perspective, that makes no sense. Rabbi Yose is prepared to say two transgressions even when the prohibitions arrive sequentially \u8212- how much more so would he say so when they arrive {\i bevas achas}, where there is no question of one preceding the other. {\i Hashta Rabbi Yose be\u8217'issur kolel mechayev shtayim, be\u8217'issur bevas achas mibaya?} \u8212- if Rabbi Yose obligates twice even in a sequential {\i kolel} case, would he ever hesitate regarding a {\i bevas achas} case? Clearly not. The {\i melikah} case must therefore have been omitted because it would yield a {\i different} ruling for the other disputant \u8212- Rabbi Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion on Bevas Achas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes: {\i ela lav Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- it is precisely because Rabbi Shimon would rule differently in the {\i melikah} case that it was left out. The {\i beraita} records Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency only for the sequential cases, where {\i issur kolel} is the operative mechanism. {\i Be\u8217'issur kolel hu d\u8217'patar} \u8212- it is specifically in the {\i kolel} scenario that Rabbi Shimon rules exempt. But {\i aval be\u8217'issur bevas achas mechayev} \u8212- in the {\i bevas achas} case, Rabbi Shimon would concede and hold the person liable for two transgressions. The Gemara therefore declares {\i tiyuvta d\u8217'Bar Kappara tiyuvta} \u8212- this is a refutation of Bar Kappara\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this proof is fully decisive. The {\i beraita} clearly treats three cases in the prior discussion, yet mentions only two. That something distinctive about the third case caused it to be omitted is a reasonable inference, and if one accepts {\i bevas achas} as the distinguishing feature, this constitutes a solid proof that Rabbi Shimon concedes in the {\i melikah} scenario. Whether the {\i beraita} also establishes precisely what the original dispute between Rabbi Chiya and Bar Kappara was about is a more open question \u8212- but the core ruling, that Rabbi Shimon holds {\i bevas achas chayav shtayim}, emerges clearly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zar Shechita Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechita Permitted?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the Gemara moves to the next {\i Mishnah}, it pauses on a technical question that the {\i beraita}\u8217's case raises: {\i bemai} \u8212- in what act exactly did the {\i zar} violate {\i Shabbat}? The problem is sharper than it first appears. If the {\i zar} performed {\i shechita} (slaughter of the {\i korban}), there is no {\i Shabbat} violation, because {\i shechita} of a {\i korban} by a non-kohen is actually {\i kasher}. This is a striking and perhaps unfamiliar {\i halacha}: even on {\i Shabbat}, when a communal {\i korban} overrides the day\u8217's restrictions, a non-kohen may perform the {\i shechita}, and that act is entirely valid. So there would be no secondary transgression there to speak of.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If instead the {\i zar} performed {\i kabbalah} (catching the blood) or {\i holachah} (carrying the blood to the {\i mizbeach}), this is essentially a matter of carrying within an enclosed area \u8212- a form of {\i tiltul b\u8217'alma}, simple moving of an object \u8212- and hardly the substantive {\i Shabbat} violation the {\i beraita} has in mind. If the act in question was {\i haktarah} \u8212- burning the limbs on the {\i mizbeach} \u8212- then, as Rav Yosef taught, {\i hav\u8217'ara l\u8217'lav yatza}: making a fire on {\i Shabbat} is, according to Rabbi Yose himself (one of the two disputants in the {\i beraita}), only a {\i lav} rather than a capital offense, because the Torah singled it out explicitly, and Rabbi Yose interprets that singling-out as a demotion in severity. This creates an inconsistency: are we seriously saying the two-transgression debate hinges on the lighter {\i lav} of {\i hav\u8217'ara}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yom Kippur Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha bar Yaakov resolves the difficulty by re-framing the scenario entirely: the case is not {\i Shabbat} in the ordinary sense but rather {\i Yom Kippur} \u8212- specifically the {\i shechita} of the High Priest\u8217's ox ({\i paro shel Kohen Gadol}). On {\i Yom Kippur}, the {\i avodah} is exclusive to the Kohen Gadol, and according to the opinion that the {\i shechita} of the Kohen Gadol\u8217's ox, when performed by a non-Kohen Gadol, is {\i pasul} \u8212- invalid \u8212- the act of {\i shechita} itself becomes a prohibited labor that the {\i zar} has no right to perform. Now we have a genuine {\i Shabbat}-category transgression through {\i shechita}, and it is a case where that {\i shechita} was specifically not {\i kasher b\u8217'zar}. The Gemara then pushes back: if that is the case, why specify a {\i zar} at all? Even a regular {\i kohen hedyot} would be disqualified from performing this particular {\i shechita} on {\i Yom Kippur} according to that opinion. The Gemara answers: {\i shezar etzlo ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- we mean someone who is like a {\i zar} with respect to this particular {\i korban}, i.e., anyone other than the Kohen Gadol. The Gemara itself acknowledges this is a stretch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Point\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi cuts through the difficulty with a fundamentally clarifying observation. {\i Midi chatos katani? O lavin katani?} \u8212- did the {\i beraita} ever say anything about how many {\i chataot} one must bring, or how many sets of lashes? It did not. {\i Ela issurei be\u8217'alma ka chashiv} \u8212- the {\i beraita} was simply counting how many {\i issurim} (prohibitions) were violated, without specifying or implying anything about the legal consequences. The question of one or two is a question of how many wrong things the person did \u8212- {\i likro rabbeinu rasha gamur}, to assess how fully blameworthy a person is \u8212- not a question about capital liability or sin-offerings. Once that reframing is in place, there is no difficulty with {\i haktarah} and its {\i lav}-only status: we were never asked to equalize the penalties of the two transgressions, only to count them. This recalibration is not without irony: a sustained Tannaic dispute about {\i issur kolel}, {\i issur mosif}, and {\i bevas achas} ultimately resolves into a question about moral characterization rather than concrete legal obligation. But the Gemara had already flagged this dimension earlier, and Rav Ashi\u8217's point is that the earlier difficulty never should have arisen in the first place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixed-Up Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now arrives at the {\i Mishnah} that actually motivates the entire preceding discussion of {\i issur chal al issur}. The {\i Mishnah} opens: {\i shnayim shekidshu shtei nashim} \u8212- two men betrothed two women. The men were unrelated to each other and the women were unrelated to each other. When the time came for the {\i chupah} \u8212- the consummation of the marriages \u8212- {\i hechlifu es shel zeh lazeh} \u8212- they mixed up the wives, each man going in to the other\u8217's wife. This is, in a sense, a worse situation than the {\i Rachel and Leah} episode, because there each man knows whose wife is whose \u8212- here, each man is unknowingly sleeping with the other man\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Transgressions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then systematically enumerates the potentially compounding transgressions. {\i Harei elu chayavim mishum eishet ish} \u8212- each man has transgressed the prohibition of adultery with a married woman. {\i Ve\u8217'im hayu achim, mishum eishet ach} \u8212- if the two men happened to be brothers, there is an additional transgression of {\i eishet ach}, the prohibition of a brother\u8217's wife. {\i Ve\u8217'im hayu achayot} \u8212- if the two women happened to be sisters, then each man is also in violation of {\i isha el achosah}, the prohibition of his wife\u8217's sister ({\i achat u\u8217'niddah} in the shorthand). {\i Ve\u8217'im hayu niddot, mishum niddah} \u8212- and if the women happen to be in a state of {\i niddah} at the time, that is yet a fourth transgression. In the worst configuration \u8212- brothers who have married sisters who are currently {\i niddot} \u8212- each man potentially incurs four separate prohibitions in a single act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises about the woman\u8217's role in this accounting. The answer is that the prohibitions of {\i arayot} are formulated from the man\u8217's perspective throughout the Torah, but the woman is a full participant in the act. She is therefore liable not as one who facilitates another\u8217's sin ({\i lifnei iver}) but as a direct party to the same transgression. Curiously, this is so even though the substantive definitions \u8212- married woman, brother\u8217's wife, wife\u8217's sister \u8212- are all defined from the male vantage point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separation/Pregnancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the error is discovered, the {\i Mishnah} requires a period of enforced separation of three months \u8212- {\i shema me\u8217'ubarot hen} \u8212- lest the women be pregnant, which would create questions of {\i mamzerut}. There were no pregnancy tests; one waited three months to determine by external signs. If the women were minors ({\i ketanot}) who could not conceive, they are returned to their husbands immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara establishes that the scenario described is one of inadvertent error, not deliberate wife-swapping. One proof: Rabbi Chiya teaches in a {\i beraita} that there would be sixteen {\i chataot} (sin-offerings) \u8212- four transgressions times four persons (each of the two men and each of the two women), all acting as {\i shogegin} (unwitting sinners). If the act had been intentional, there would be no {\i chata} obligation at all. Additionally, the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's allowance for the women to return to their husbands after three months would be impossible if the act had been willful, since a woman who commits adultery voluntarily becomes forbidden to her husband. The Gemara therefore accepts the reading {\i huchlafu} \u8212- \u8220"they were exchanged\u8221" (passively, accidentally) \u8212- rather than {\i hechlifu}, \u8220"they exchanged.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One apparent exception: the Gemara notes that a minor\u8217's consent does not constitute legal consent \u8212- {\i pituy ketanah ones hu} \u8212- so even if a minor were to act willfully in such a scenario, she would be considered legally coerced and would not be forbidden to return to her husband. But that consideration does not apply here, where the entire case is one of unintentional error.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Which Tanna?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's scenario established, the Gemara asks: {\i uman hai tanna d\u8217'is lei issur kolel ve\u8217'issur mosif ve\u8217'issur bas achas?} \u8212- which Tanna is it who accepts all three forms of {\i issur chal al issur}? The scenario involves {\i eishet ish} (married woman) and {\i eishet ach} (brother\u8217's wife) arising together \u8212- potentially {\i bevas achas} \u8212- and then {\i achos isha} (wife\u8217's sister) added through {\i issur kolel}, and {\i niddah} added through {\i issur mosif}. All three mechanisms must be operative for the {\i Mishnah} to yield four distinct transgressions. The Gemara, citing Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav, identifies this as the position of Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source is a {\i beraita}: {\i yesh ochel achilah achas ve\u8217'chayav aleha arba chataot ve\u8217'asham echad} \u8212- one can perform a single act of eating and thereby incur four {\i chataot} and one {\i asham}. The case: a person who is {\i tamei} eats the {\i cheilev} (forbidden fat) of a sanctified animal that has become {\i notar} (leftover sacrifice, which may no longer be consumed) on {\i Yom Kippur}. The four {\i chataot} correspond to: eating {\i cheilev} (forbidden fat in general), being {\i tamei} while eating consecrated food, eating {\i notar}, and eating on {\i Yom Kippur}. The {\i asham} corresponds to {\i me\u8217'ilah} \u8212- deriving benefit from consecrated property. Rabbi Meir adds: if it was {\i Shabbat} and the person carried this food in his mouth, he would be liable for that as well. The other Sages object \u8212- {\i eino min hashem} \u8212- carrying the food in one\u8217's mouth is not the same act as eating it; the additional {\i Shabbat} violation arises from a conceptually distinct action and should not be counted together. Tosafot, however, notes that Rabbi Meir may have held that the act of swallowing constitutes the {\i hanachah} (placement) required for the {\i melacha} of carrying, making it technically part of the same act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Forms Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rashi} on this passage assigns each of the three {\i issur chal al issur} mechanisms to a specific stage in this sequence. When the animal was plain {\i chullin} (unsanctified), its {\i cheilev} was forbidden to eat but permitted for other benefit. When it was sanctified \u8212- {\i hukdesha} \u8212- the {\i cheilev} acquired an additional dimension of prohibition: now benefit ({\i hana\u8217'ah}) from it is also forbidden. Rashi terms this {\i issur mosif}, since the sanctification extended the nature of the prohibition. It must be noted, however, that this is a somewhat unusual application of {\i issur mosif}: the standard understanding is that {\i issur mosif} means the forbidden object becomes forbidden to a wider circle of persons, whereas here it is the same persons who now face a stricter prohibition. Rashi\u8217's extension of the term to cover this case is worth flagging as genuinely questionable, even if it is the framework the Gemara works with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the animal became {\i notar}, the prohibition expanded to include the {\i mizbeach} itself \u8212- it can no longer be offered on the altar \u8212- and so the class of \u8220"entities\u8221" forbidden from it has grown. This is a more conventional {\i issur mosif}. When the person himself became {\i tamei}, he became forbidden from eating any consecrated food, not just this particular piece of {\i cheilev}; the expansion here is in the range of objects forbidden to him, not a change in the status of the object. This is {\i issur kolel} \u8212- the broader prohibition sweeps in this particular item along with everything else in its class. The {\i bevas achas} dimension, according to Tosafot, is illustrated by Rabbi Meir\u8217's addition of {\i Shabbat} to the case: when it is simultaneously {\i Yom Kippur} and {\i Shabbat}, both the {\i Yom Kippur} prohibition on eating and the {\i Shabbat} prohibition on carrying arise together, instantiating the {\i bevas achas} mechanism.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eliezer/Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alignment Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the {\i Mishnah} can be attributed to Rabbi Meir as the Tanna who accepts all three forms of {\i issur chal al issur}, the Gemara raises a further complication. There is a separate, well-known dispute between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua about {\i to\u8217'eh bidvar mitzvah} \u8212- one who errs in the course of performing a {\i mitzvah}. The men in the {\i Mishnah} were attempting to sleep with their own wives \u8212- an act of marital relations, which is itself a {\i mitzvah}. If Rabbi Yehoshua holds that such a person is exempt from a {\i chata}, then the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's assumption that {\i chataot} are owed becomes problematic. {\i Le-Rabbi Meir aliba de-man?} \u8212- according to which opinion of this second debate is Rabbi Meir\u8217's position in the {\i Mishnah} tenable?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara initially suggests it must align with Rabbi Eliezer, who does not grant an exemption for {\i mitzvah} errors. But the Gemara immediately offers an alternative: {\i ibba\u8217'it eima afilu aliba de-Rabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- one can maintain that even Rabbi Yehoshua would not exempt here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bohul/Time Pressure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key to reconciling Rabbi Meir with Rabbi Yehoshua lies in the precise conditions under which Rabbi Yehoshua grants the {\i mitzvah}-error exemption. The Gemara explains with a paradigmatic case: a {\i mohel} who has two children to circumcise on consecutive days \u8212- one whose eighth day falls on {\i Shabbat}, one whose eighth day falls on Sunday \u8212- accidentally performs the circumcision on {\i Shabbat} for the Sunday child. He has violated {\i Shabbat} through an act of {\i melacha}, yet he was attempting to perform a {\i mitzvah}. Rabbi Yehoshua exempts him from a {\i chata}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attributes this exemption to {\i bohul} \u8212- a sense of urgency or time pressure. The {\i mohel} operates under the pressure that today\u8217's circumcision must be done today; that ambient pressure, combined with the fact that he was acting in the service of a {\i mitzvah}, constitutes sufficient mitigation. The crucial point is that {\i bohul} and {\i mitzvah} together produce the exemption \u8212- neither alone is sufficient. In the case of the mixed-up wives, there is no {\i bohul}. A man does not face a deadline by which he must sleep with his wife on a particular night. Without time pressure, the combination that gives Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's exemption its force is absent, and even Rabbi Yehoshua would say the man owes a {\i chata}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara is careful here about what exactly is doing the exculpatory work. One reading is that {\i bohul} is a form of {\i ones} \u8212- unavoidable compulsion \u8212- and that the {\i mitzvah} context amplifies that {\i ones} to the point of exculpation. Another reading is that the {\i mitzvah} context is itself the primary exempting factor, but a high degree of negligence can override it; {\i bohul} functions to lower the threshold of apparent negligence. On either reading, the absence of {\i bohul} in the wife-mixing case is fatal to the exemption claim, and the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's liability for a {\i chata} is consistent with Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trumah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges this framework by pointing to another case in which Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua debate, one that also lacks time pressure: a kohen eating {\i trumah} who is informed mid-meal that he is actually a {\i chalal} (a disqualified kohen, in this instance the son of a divorced woman or a {\i chalutzah}), and therefore unauthorized to eat {\i trumah} \u8212- he is, with respect to {\i trumah}, a {\i zar}. Rabbi Eliezer holds him liable for principal payment plus the additional {\i chomesh} penalty; Rabbi Yehoshua exempts him. Yet eating {\i trumah} is not a {\i mitzvah} under time pressure. Why then does Rabbi Yehoshua exempt him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erev Pesach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by the comment of Rabbi Abba bar Chiya: {\i hacha bitrumah be\u8217'erev pesach askinan} \u8212- the {\i trumah} case in question is specifically one that occurred on {\i erev Pesach}, when the kohen needed to eat his {\i trumah} (which was {\i chametz}) before the prohibition on {\i chametz} took effect. That creates a genuine {\i bohul} \u8212- a time-pressured context \u8212- and Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's exemption fits the same framework as before. Alternatively, the Gemara suggests {\i ve\u8217'ibba\u8217'it eima} \u8212- even without that specific setting, one could resolve the {\i Mishnah} differently, by attributing it to Rabbi Shimon operating under a {\i bevas achas} framework, which would be a separate line of analysis altogether.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bevas Achas Reconcile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Transgressi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final alternative the Gemara entertains is that the {\i Mishnah} need not be attributed to Rabbi Meir at all. Instead, one could read all four transgressions as arising literally simultaneously \u8212- {\i bevas achas} \u8212- in which case even Rabbi Shimon, who otherwise rejects {\i issur chal al issur}, would concede that all four apply. The challenge is constructing a scenario in which {\i eishet ish}, {\i eishet ach}, {\i achos isha}, and {\i niddah} can all genuinely take effect at the identical moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlichim Synchronization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The synchronization of {\i eishet ish}, {\i eishet ach}, and {\i achos isha} is achievable through the mechanism of {\i shlichim} (agents). Suppose the two brothers \u8212- Reuven and Shimon \u8212- appoint a single agent, Michael, to perform two {\i kiddushin} simultaneously. Reuven\u8217's {\i kiddushin} is to Rachel, Shimon\u8217's is to Leah. Simultaneously, Rachel and Leah appoint their own agent, Devorah, to receive their {\i kiddushin}. Michael presents two {\i perutot} (coins) to Devorah in a single act \u8212- one for Rachel, one for Leah. In that exact instant, Rachel becomes Reuven\u8217's {\i eishet ish}, and since Shimon is Reuven\u8217's brother, she is also Shimon\u8217's {\i eishet ach}; Leah simultaneously becomes Shimon\u8217's wife and Rachel\u8217's sister, rendering her Reuven\u8217's {\i achos isha}. All three statuses crystallize in the same moment. This is the scenario the Gemara constructs: {\i d\u8217'shavyuhu shaliach ve\u8217'shavu inhu shaliach, u-faga shaliach b\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- the brothers made one agent, the women made another, and the two agents met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Age Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i niddah} prohibition, however, resists this synchronization. A woman is either already a {\i niddah} before the {\i kiddushin} moment \u8212- in which case the {\i niddah} prohibition predates {\i eishet ish} and is not simultaneous with it \u8212- or she becomes a {\i niddah} afterward, in which case it arrives later. Neither option gives us {\i bevas achas} for all four.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Three Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this with a striking construction. {\i B\u8217'shofaat mitoch g\u8217' shanim} \u8212- we are dealing with women who began experiencing {\i niddah} bleeding before their third birthdays and who remained in {\i niddah} status continuously through their third birthday. The key is that halachic liability for acts of {\i biah} (intercourse) with a girl under three does not take effect \u8212- the act is not formally reckoned as {\i biah} under three years of age. So: the fathers of these girls (who are minors, and whose {\i kiddushin} may be effected by their fathers) arranged the {\i kiddushin} before the girls reached three. The girls were already in a state of {\i niddah} from before age three. For the brothers, all four prohibitions \u8212- {\i eishet ish}, {\i eishet ach}, {\i achos isha}, and {\i niddah} \u8212- crystallize at the single moment when the girls turn three, because only at that moment does the {\i biah} become formally actionable, and at that moment the {\i niddah} status is already present alongside the others. This is {\i bevas achas} for the brothers\u8217' liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the women\u8217's own {\i chata} obligations, the relevant moment is when they themselves reach the age of {\i bat mitzvah} (twelve years). The Gemara\u8217's formulation \u8212- {\i v\u8217'chayiveinu mitoch shteim esrei l\u8217'achar shteim esrei l\u8217'chiyuvey inhei} \u8212- indicates that to create the women\u8217's liability, the {\i niddah} status established before age three (and never interrupted by {\i tevilah} in a {\i mikveh}) must persist continuously through their becoming {\i bat mitzvah}. At the moment they become obligated in {\i mitzvot}, the same set of simultaneous prohibitions is already in place, and the {\i niddah} component is the same continuous status that was operative from before age three. If they had immersed in the interim, the {\i niddah} status would have lapsed and the simultaneity would be broken. The scenario thus requires that neither woman immersed from before age three until after becoming {\i bat mitzvah} \u8212- a span of roughly nine years.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pregnancy/Tamar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Act Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} requires a three-month separation to ensure the women are not pregnant \u8212- {\i shema me\u8217'ubarot hen}. But a challenge arises: the Gemara operates on the assumption, widely held in the ancient world, that a woman cannot become pregnant from the first act of intercourse. If the men slept with the wrong wives only once, there is no risk of pregnancy. Rav Nachman, citing Rabba bar Avuha, resolves this: {\i shebau ve\u8217'shanu} \u8212- they had intercourse more than once. The second act is when pregnancy becomes possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further objection presents itself: if we count each act separately, Rabbi Chiya\u8217's accounting of sixteen {\i chataot} would rapidly become incoherent, since Rabbi Eliezer holds that every distinct act of thrusting within a prolonged sexual encounter constitutes a separate act of {\i biah} \u8212- potentially multiplying the {\i chataot} enormously. The Gemara resolves this by explaining that the count of sixteen is based on the first act of {\i biah} alone \u8212- {\i b\u8217'via rishona kachashiv} \u8212- and that the three-month separation presupposes a second act having occurred. These are simply two separate framings of the scenario operating in parallel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hymen Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises the biblical case of Tamar, who became pregnant by Yehuda apparently from a single encounter \u8212- {\i d\u8217'b\u8217'via rishona iabra}. How does this square with the assumption that pregnancy cannot result from a first act? The answer offered is that Tamar had previously removed her hymen manually: {\i Tamar b\u8217'etzba miacha}. Rav Yitzchak adds that this practice was known from the household of Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi \u8212- girls there would do so and were therefore given the name Tamar as a kind of epithet, reflecting what the original Tamar had done. The Gemara presupposes that the hymen, when intact, physically prevents conception on the first act; once it is removed, the obstacle is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to a broader halachic context: there is a rabbinic (post-Talmudic in its full development) treatment of {\i dam besulim} (hymenal blood) as resembling {\i niddah} blood, which historically gave rise to practices of pre-emptive hymen removal before the wedding night so as to avoid the resulting {\i niddah} status on the first night. The Gemara\u8217's assumptions about physiology reflect the medical understanding of the Talmudic period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Er/Onan Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a new difficulty arises: Tamar had been married to Er and Onan before Yehuda. Was she not therefore no longer a virgin? The Gemara\u8217's answer is that {\i Er v\u8217'Onan shimshu shelo kedarkan} \u8212- Er and Onan had intercourse with her in a non-vaginal manner ({\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan}), which left her hymen intact. The Gemara then cites a {\i beraita}: {\i kol esrim ve\u8217'arba\u8217'ah chodesh dash mibifnim ve\u8217'zoreh mibachutz divrei Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- for the twenty-four months of nursing, Rabbi Eliezer permits a form of early withdrawal (threshing on the inside, winnowing on the outside, to borrow the agricultural metaphor) to avoid impregnating the nursing mother. The Sages respond: {\i hallalu eino ela k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan} \u8212- this is nothing other than what Er and Onan did, and is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent tension between the {\i beraita} (which equates early withdrawal with the sin of Er and Onan) and its own characterization of Er and Onan\u8217's sin as {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan} (non-vaginal sex, not early withdrawal): {\i k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan ve\u8217'lo k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan} \u8212- the nursing practice is {\i like} what they did in one respect (the semen is expelled outside the body, {\i ve\u8217'shichet artzah}) but {\i not} exactly what they did, because theirs was {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan} while the nursing practice involves normal vaginal intercourse ({\i kedarkan}) with withdrawal at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Early Withdrawal Forbidd\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: we understand from the verse {\i ve\u8217'shichet artzah} that Onan practiced this form of emission, but what is the source that it was {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan} rather than early withdrawal? And separately, the verse only explicitly describes Onan\u8217's act \u8212- what is the basis for assuming Er sinned in the same way? Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak answers from the word {\i gam} in {\i vayamot gam oto} \u8212- \u8220"he too died.\u8221" The word {\i gam} implies \u8220"also,\u8221" meaning Er died by the same cause and in the same manner as Onan. The reason given for Onan\u8217's act is explicit: {\i lo lo yihyeh hazera} \u8212- he knew the child would be reckoned under his deceased brother\u8217's name, not his own, and he resisted that. Er\u8217's motive, by contrast, is inferred by the Gemara as having been aesthetic: {\i kedei shelo titaber ve\u8217'yachahish yofyah} \u8212- he did not want Tamar to become pregnant and lose her beauty. In both cases, the act was {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zera Levatala Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot First View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan} and the condemnation of Er and Onan\u8217's conduct opens onto a significant halakhic question that the Rishonim debate at length: in the context of marital relations, is a husband ever permitted to have a seminal emission outside of his wife? Two distinct {\i Gemara} passages appear to point in different directions. Here, early withdrawal is condemned as {\i k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan}; elsewhere (in tractate {\i Nedarim}), the Gemara appears to permit {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan} in the marital setting without qualification. Tosafot addresses this tension directly. The first approach offered by Tosafot reconciles the sources by limiting the {\i Nedarim} permission to cases where {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan} does not involve seminal emission: {\i mekivan d\u8217'leis hashchasas zera lo havei k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan} \u8212- only because there is no destruction of seed does it not constitute the sin of Er and Onan. On this reading, {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan} is permissible in marriage only so long as it does not result in the semen being expelled outside the wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ri\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ri (Rabbi Yitzchak, cited within the same Tosafot) offers a second, more permissive approach. {\i Velo chashiv k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan ela keshemitkavein lehashchis hazera ve\u8217'ragil la\u8217'asos kein tamid} \u8212- an act is only considered to be in the category of Er and Onan\u8217's sin when the person\u8217's {\i intention} is the destruction of seed as such, and when this is his habitual practice. If, on the other hand, the couple engages in {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkan} simply as part of their marital intimacy \u8212- {\i aval be\u8217'akrai be\u8217'alma umis\u8217'aveh lavo le\u8217'ishto} \u8212- without this being a calculated strategy to avoid procreation or destroy seed systematically, then {\i shelo kedarka shary}: it is permitted. The Ri grounds this in the {\i Nedarim} principle that {\i kol mah she\u8217'adam rotzeh la\u8217'asos be\u8217'ishto ya\u8217'aseh} \u8212- within the marital relationship a man may conduct himself with his wife as he wishes. On this view, the marital context transforms the character of the act: what would otherwise be {\i zera levatala} (wasted seed) is not so considered when it arises naturally from the couple\u8217's intimacy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rema\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical {\i halakha} is shaped decisively by the {\i Rema} (Rabbi Moshe Isserles), who rules in accordance with the Ri\u8217's second approach. The implication is far-reaching: according to the {\i Rema}, the prohibition of {\i zera levatala} does not apply within the marital relationship regardless of whether seminal emission occurs during vaginal intercourse or otherwise, provided it is not a deliberate and habitual practice of seed destruction. This ruling has significant relevance for how marital relations are understood within {\i halakha}, and its basis in this passage of the Gemara \u8212- via the Ri in Tosafot \u8212- reflects the broader principle that contextual intention and relational setting are determinative in this domain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 33b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Chiyya vs.\u160 ?Bar Kap\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Prohibition Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya on 33b picks up in the middle of a discussion concerning {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- the question of whether a second prohibition can take effect upon an object or person already subject to a prior prohibition. Three specific cases had been presented: a {\i zar} (non-{\i kohen}) who performs the {\i avodah} in the {\i Beit HaMikdash} on {\i Shabbat}; a {\i kohen} who is a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} and performs the {\i avodah} in a state of {\i tumah} during a circumstance of {\i tumah hutrah b\u8217'tzibbur}; and a {\i zar} who eats a bird upon which {\i melikah} had been performed \u8212- an act of slaughter that is valid for {\i kohanim} but renders the bird forbidden to non-{\i kohanim} at the very same moment it takes effect. The question in each case was whether the individual transgresses two distinct prohibitions or only one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Misattribution Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate was attributed to Rabbi Chiyya and Bar Kappara, both of whom swore that Rebbe had taught them his ruling \u8212- Rabbi Chiyya reporting that Rebbe said two transgressions, Bar Kappara reporting that Rebbe said one. The {\i Gemara} had concluded that their dispute was really about {\i issur b\u8217'vas achas} \u8212- prohibitions that take effect simultaneously \u8212- and whether Rabbi Shimon, who generally holds {\i ein issur chal al issur}, would concede that when two prohibitions arrive at exactly the same moment, both take effect. That explanation seems coherent. But it raises a sharp difficulty: if Rabbi Chiyya is correct that even Rabbi Shimon would agree to liability in a case of {\i b\u8217'vas achas}, from where did Bar Kappara ever hear that Rebbe taught only one transgression? The {\i Gemara} asks directly: {\i l\u8217'Rabbi Chiyya, Bar Kappara shikora kameshakeir} \u8212- is Bar Kappara simply lying?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kolel vs.\u160 ?Bas Achas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by having Rabbi Chiyya explain that Bar Kappara did not misrepresent Rebbe deliberately \u8212- he misremembered. Specifically: {\i ki asnaya Rebbe l\u8217'didi, tarti l\u8217'ftor asnaya} \u8212- when Rebbe taught Bar Kappara, and said one is exempt (i.e., only one transgression), he was teaching only two of the three cases, not all three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first two cases \u8212- the {\i zar} performing {\i avodah} on {\i Shabbat}, and the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} performing {\i avodah} in a state of {\i tumah} \u8212- can each be understood as sequential. In the first, a person performs {\i avodah} and {\i Shabbat} then arrives; in the second, a person is a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} and then becomes {\i tamei}. In both cases, a first prohibition is already in place and a second prohibition descends upon it afterward. This is the domain of {\i issur kolel} \u8212- where the arriving prohibition does not render the object forbidden to a wider set of people, but rather expands the category of forbidden acts while something already forbidden to the whole world becomes forbidden in a broader sense. In cases of {\i issur kolel}, even opinions that are more permissive regarding {\i issur chal al issur} may not grant double liability. Rebbe taught Bar Kappara these two sequential cases according to the view of Rabbi Shimon, who says that even {\i issur mosif} does not take effect \u8212- and certainly {\i issur kolel}, which is a weaker category, would not \u8212- and therefore in these two cases one is liable only once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical distinction is that {\i issur kolel} is a concept that applies only in sequential settings: first prohibition A, then prohibition B descends and encompasses a broader class of forbidden acts. It is incoherent to speak of {\i issur kolel} when both prohibitions arrive simultaneously \u8212- in a {\i b\u8217'vas achas} scenario, there is no \u8220"first\u8221" and \u8220"second,\u8221" no earlier prohibition that could block a later one. Bar Kappara heard Rebbe\u8217's teaching about the two sequential cases, understood that in those cases (as {\i issur kolel}) one is liable only once according to Rabbi Shimon, and then \u8212- as the {\i Gemara} explains \u8212- inadvertently mixed in the third case of the {\i zar} who ate the bird on which {\i melikah} was performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Case Confusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That third case \u8212- {\i melikah} by a non-{\i kohen} \u8212- is categorically different. The moment {\i melikah} is performed, the bird simultaneously becomes a valid {\i korban} (and thus forbidden to non-{\i kohanim}) and becomes forbidden in the sense relevant to the act of {\i melikah} itself. This case cannot be sequential; it is inherently {\i b\u8217'vas achas}. When Bar Kappara added it to the set in his memory, the combined set of three cases could no longer be characterized as {\i issur kolel} operating sequentially. The only common denominator he could identify across all three was {\i b\u8217'vas achas}. He then reasoned: since Rebbe taught that in these cases one is liable only once, and these cases are all {\i b\u8217'vas achas}, Rebbe must have ruled that even {\i b\u8217'vas achas} yields only one transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} states this explicitly: {\i v\u8217'aydi d\u8217'damya lei arvibihu} \u8212- because the third case was similar to the other two (all involving acts normally permitted when done correctly, all involving non-{\i kohanim} or disqualified {\i kohanim}), Bar Kappara mixed it in. {\i V\u8217'lo ashkach ela b\u8217'vas achas} \u8212- examining the merged set, the only explanatory framework he found was {\i b\u8217'vas achas}. {\i V\u8217'savar mid\u8217'ha b\u8217'vas achas, ha nami b\u8217'vas achas} \u8212- he concluded that since the {\i melikah} case is {\i b\u8217'vas achas}, the whole teaching must concern {\i b\u8217'vas achas}. {\i U\u8217'mid\u8217'ha l\u8217'pattur, ha nami l\u8217'pattur} \u8212- and since Rebbe had taught exemption, he concluded that {\i b\u8217'vas achas} generally yields only one transgression. He had honestly misremembered \u8212- not lied \u8212- and that misremembering is what the {\i Gemara} sets out to explain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Issur Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mosif, Kolel, Achas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through the Bar Kappara episode, the {\i Gemara} pauses to present the systematic framework governing {\i issur chal al issur} in its three recognized forms. {\i Issur mosif} is when the newly arriving prohibition expands the range of persons to whom the object is forbidden \u8212- it \u8220"adds\u8221" to the audience of the prohibition. {\i Issur kolel} is when the new prohibition does not change who is forbidden the object, but broadens the category of acts forbidden in connection with it, encompassing it within a wider class. And {\i issur b\u8217'vas achas} refers to the case where two prohibitions descend simultaneously, with no temporal priority between them \u8212- the scenario where the very question of one prohibition \u8220"blocking\u8221" another loses its footing, because neither arrived first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the debate of Rabbi Chiyya and Bar Kappara concerns whether Rabbi Shimon would concede double liability in a case of {\i b\u8217'vas achas}, the {\i Gemara} attempts to test this with a {\i baraita}. The {\i baraita} states: a {\i zar} who performs {\i avodah} on {\i Shabbat}, or a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} who performs {\i avodah} in a state of {\i tumah} even when there is a {\i korban tzibbur} \u8212- in each case there are two transgressions: transgressing as a {\i zar} and transgressing {\i Shabbat}, or transgressing as a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} and transgressing {\i tumah}. This is the position of Rabbi Yose. Rabbi Shimon, however, says one is liable only for the first prohibition \u8212- the {\i zar} status or the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} status \u8212- not for the prohibition that arrived afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, the {\i baraita} discusses only two of the three cases. The {\i melikah} case \u8212- the {\i zar} who ate the bird \u8212- is absent. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i l\u8217'man shiyarah} \u8212- for whom\u8217's sake was it left out? It cannot have been omitted on account of Rabbi Yose, since Rabbi Yose, who holds {\i issur chal al issur} even sequentially and even in {\i issur kolel}, would certainly hold it in a case of {\i b\u8217'vas achas} as well \u8212- {\i hashta Rabbi Yose b\u8217'issur kolel mechayev shtayim, b\u8217'vas achas mibaya}. If he permits double liability when prohibitions arrive sequentially, he would surely permit it when they arrive simultaneously. There is thus no reason the {\i baraita} would have omitted the {\i melikah} case on Rabbi Yose\u8217's account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It must therefore be Rabbi Shimon for whose sake the case was left out \u8212- meaning that Rabbi Shimon would not have the same ruling in the {\i melikah} case as in the other two. Since Rabbi Shimon says only one transgression in the sequential cases, the {\i baraita}\u8217's omission of the {\i melikah} case implies that Rabbi Shimon would say something different there \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} concludes that he would say {\i two} transgressions when prohibitions arrive simultaneously. {\i U\u8217'b\u8217'issur kolel hu d\u8217'pattar} \u8212- it is specifically in the {\i issur kolel} sequential scenario that Rabbi Shimon exempts from double liability. {\i Aval b\u8217'issur b\u8217'vas achas mechayev} \u8212- but in {\i b\u8217'vas achas}, he concedes double liability. This yields the conclusion: {\i tyufta d\u8217'Bar Kappara tyufta} \u8212- a refutation of Bar Kappara\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth flagging that the force of this proof depends on accepting the {\i Gemara}\u8217's prior conclusion that the distinction between the {\i melikah} case and the other two cases is precisely the {\i b\u8217'vas achas} character of the former. Given that, the omission of {\i melikah} from the {\i baraita} is indeed significant evidence that Rabbi Shimon treats {\i b\u8217'vas achas} differently \u8212- and would hold double liability there. Whether this cleanly refutes Bar Kappara\u8217's specific position (as opposed to simply proving that Rabbi Shimon agrees {\i b\u8217'vas achas} yields two transgressions) is a fair question, but the {\i Gemara} presents it as a refutation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zar on Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Which Act Violates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to a focused question that arises from one of the cases in the {\i baraita}: the {\i zar} who performs {\i avodah} on {\i Shabbat}. The ruling is that he transgresses twice \u8212- once for acting as a {\i zar} and once for {\i chillul Shabbat}. But this prompts a precise question: {\i b\u8217'mai} \u8212- in what act exactly did he violate {\i Shabbat}? Every prohibited {\i melachah} he might have performed in the context of {\i avodah} is subject to scrutiny.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he performed {\i shechitah} \u8212- the answer is immediate: {\i shechitah b\u8217'zar kesherah} \u8212- a non-{\i kohen} may perform {\i shechitah} of a {\i korban}. This is actually a striking {\i halachah}: even in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, even on {\i Shabbat} when the {\i avodah} is permitted, even a woman may perform {\i shechitah} of a {\i korban tzibbur}. So {\i shechitah} by a {\i zar} is not prohibited, and therefore cannot constitute a {\i Shabbat} violation in this context. If it was {\i kabalah} or {\i holachah} \u8212- catching the blood or carrying it to the {\i mizbeach} \u8212- that is merely {\i tiltul b\u8217'alma}, movement within an enclosed space, which does not rise to the level of a {\i Shabbat} violation in the usual sense. And if it was {\i haktarah} \u8212- burning the limbs on the {\i mizbeach} \u8212- one faces the position of Rabbi Yose himself, that {\i hava\u8217'arah l\u8217'lav nitna}: making a fire on {\i Shabbat}, according to Rabbi Yose, is only a {\i lav} and not a {\i chiyuv misah}, since the Torah singles it out explicitly \u8212- {\i lo tivaru eish b\u8217'chol moshvoseichem b\u8217'yom ha-Shabbat} \u8212- and Rabbi Yose derives from that singling-out that it was separated from the general category of {\i melachot} to teach that it is less severe. Since the discussion concerns equivalent-weight transgressions (carrying {\i chiyuv} for {\i korban} purposes), {\i haktarah} would not serve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Par on Yom Kippur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha bar Yaakov resolves the difficulty: the case is not {\i Shabbat} in the ordinary sense but {\i Yom Kippur}, and the specific act is {\i shechitah} of the {\i par} \u8212- the ox of the {\i Kohen Gadol} on {\i Yom Kippur}. The {\i avodah} of {\i Yom Kippur} is performed exclusively by the {\i Kohen Gadol}, and according to the opinion that even {\i shechitah} of the {\i Kohen Gadol}\u8217's {\i par} requires the {\i Kohen Gadol} himself, a {\i zar} performing that {\i shechitah} commits a prohibited act \u8212- and {\i shechitah} is universally agreed to constitute a {\i melachah} on {\i Shabbat} (or {\i Yom Kippur}). So the case involves a {\i zar} who performed the {\i shechitah} of the {\i par} on {\i Yom Kippur}: one transgression for acting as a {\i zar}, one for violating {\i Yom Kippur} through {\i shechitah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a further difficulty: {\i mai irya zar} \u8212- why is the case specifically a {\i zar}? If the ruling follows the opinion that any non-{\i Kohen Gadol} cannot perform this {\i shechitah}, then even an ordinary {\i kohen} ({\i kohen hedyot}) would be disqualified. The answer given is {\i she-zar etzlo ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- when the case says \u8220"{\i zar},\u8221" it means someone who is a {\i zar} relative to this particular {\i korban}, i.e., anyone who is not the {\i Kohen Gadol}. The {\i Gemara} itself notes that this is a significant stretch in the language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi cuts through the difficulty with a sharper observation. He asks: {\i midi chataos katani} \u8212- did the {\i baraita} say anything about how many {\i chataot} one must bring? {\i O lavei katani} \u8212- or about how many {\i lavin} were transgressed? {\i Ella issurei b\u8217'alma ka chashiv} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is simply counting how many prohibited acts were committed, not calibrating {\i chiyuvim} of lashes or {\i korbanos}. The {\i nafka mina}, Rav Ashi implies, is not the practical consequence of multiple sin-offerings or floggings \u8212- it is the more abstract question of how grievous a {\i rasha} one is for having committed one wrong or two. This observation, while somewhat reductionist in its immediate context, preserves the force of the debate: when the stakes are higher \u8212- multiple {\i chataot}, multiple sets of lashes \u8212- the same logic would apply with weightier consequences. Here, the practical implication is simply the moral accounting of the transgression.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife Exchange Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuppah Exchange\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i Mishnah}, whose subject matter is closely related to all that preceded it. The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i shenayim she-kiddeshu shtei nashim} \u8212- two men married two women; the men were unrelated to each other, the women were unrelated to each other. {\i Uvi-sh\u8217'at knisatan la-chuppah} \u8212- when the time came to enter the {\i chuppah} (a euphemism for the consummation of the marriage), {\i hechlifu et shel zeh la-zeh} \u8212- they accidentally exchanged wives. This is reminiscent of the exchange of Rachel and Leah, except that here it is more serious: each man has now had relations with the other man\u8217's wife. The critical point is that the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the formal betrothal \u8212- had preceded the {\i chuppah}: each man was already fully married to his own wife when the exchange occurred. Whether the {\i chuppah} was a literal canopy under which faces were veiled or a euphemism for the bedroom, the legal reality is identical: each man had relations with another man\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Transgressions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then enumerates the transgressions. {\i Harei elu chayavim mishum eshet ish} \u8212- each man transgresses the prohibition of adultery with a married woman. {\i V\u8217'im hayu achim, mishum eshet ach} \u8212- if the two men happened to be brothers, there is an additional transgression of relations with a brother\u8217's wife. {\i V\u8217'im hayu achayot, mishum isha el achotah} \u8212- if the two women happened to be sisters, there is a further transgression, since each man\u8217's wife is the other woman\u8217's sister, making the illicit partner his wife\u8217's sister. {\i V\u8217'im hayu nidot, mishum niddah} \u8212- and if the women were {\i nidot} at the time, there is a fourth transgression. These four can combine in a single case: four transgressions arising from a single act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minors Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} adds: {\i ve-im hayu ketanot she-ein re-uyot le-yeled, machzirin otan mi-yad} \u8212- if the women were minors who were incapable of bearing children, they are returned immediately to their lawful husbands. This exception follows from the principle that {\i pituy ketanah ones ninhu} \u8212- the seduction of a minor is treated legally as coercion, regardless of any apparent consent. A minor\u8217's consent is legally meaningless; it constitutes statutory rape in {\i halachic} terms. Accordingly, even if the act were intentional on the man\u8217's part, the woman \u8212- being a minor \u8212- would not be rendered forbidden to her husband, since her status is that of one who was violated against her will.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accident Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} also specifies: {\i u-mafrishin otan sheloshah chodashim, shema me-ubarot hen} \u8212- the women are kept separate from their husbands for three months lest they be pregnant, since a pregnancy by the wrong man would create serious questions of lineage. The {\i Gemara} engages with the word {\i hechlifu} \u8212- they exchanged \u8212- and asks whether this implies intentional wife-swapping, which would be incongruent with the rest of the {\i Mishnah}. Rav Yehudah rules that the correct reading is {\i huchlefu} \u8212- they were exchanged, passively, accidentally. This reading is confirmed by the {\i Gemara} from the clause requiring three months of separation: {\i ve-ei be-meizid, mi sharya} \u8212- if it were intentional, how could the women go back to their husbands at all? An intentional act of adultery renders a woman forbidden to her husband. The fact that the {\i Mishnah} contemplates their return \u8212- conditional only on confirming the absence of pregnancy \u8212- demonstrates conclusively that the act was unintentional, and the participants are liable to a {\i korban chatat} rather than subject to the severer consequences of deliberate transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} also notes, from the {\i baraita} of Rabbi Chiyya, that if there are four prohibitions and four parties (two men, two women, each potentially liable), and each brings four {\i chataot}, the total is sixteen sin-offerings \u8212- {\i sheish esrei chataot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four from One Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i Mishnah} established as a case of {\i shogegin} (inadvertent transgressors), the {\i Gemara} raises a conceptual question. How can a single act give rise to four distinct transgressions? The case in the {\i Mishnah} involves prohibitions that did not all exist simultaneously at the moment of betrothal; they accrued at different times. The woman was first an {\i eshet ish} \u8212- a married woman \u8212- from the moment of {\i kiddushin}. She then became an {\i achot isha} \u8212- a sister to someone\u8217's wife \u8212- only when her sister was also married. She became a {\i niddah} at a different moment entirely. Each of these prohibitions arrived at a different time. This is precisely the domain of {\i issur chal al issur}: can a later prohibition take effect upon a person or object already subject to a prior prohibition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-man hai tanna d\u8217'it leh issur kolel v\u8217'issur mosif v\u8217'issur bas achas} \u8212- which {\i tanna} holds that all three categories of {\i issur chal al issur} are operative? Rav Yehudah said in the name of Rav: it is Rabbi Meir \u8212- {\i Rebbe Meir hi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chelev Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof comes from a {\i baraita} in Rabbi Meir\u8217's name: {\i yesh ochel achilah achas v\u8217'chayav aleha arba chataot v\u8217'asham echad} \u8212- there is a case where one can eat a single item in a single act and incur four {\i chataot} and one {\i asham}. The scenario: one is {\i tamei} and eats {\i chelev} that comes from a {\i korban}, and this {\i chelev} is {\i notar} (left over beyond its permitted time), and it is from the sanctified {\i mukdashim}, and one does this on {\i Yom Kippur}. Each of these elements \u8212- eating {\i chelev}, eating as a {\i tamei} person from {\i kodshim}, eating {\i notar}, eating on {\i Yom Kippur} \u8212- independently carries {\i chiyuv karet} and obligates a {\i chatat}. The {\i asham} comes from {\i me\u8217'ilah}, the additional prohibition of deriving benefit from {\i hekdesh}. That is five in total \u8212- four {\i chataot} and one {\i asham}. Rabbi Meir adds: if it was {\i Shabbat} and the person was carrying the food in his mouth while walking through a public domain, there is a sixth. The Sages respond: {\i eino min ha-shem} \u8212- that is not of the same type, since the act of carrying is categorically distinct from the act of eating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} engages seriously with the Sages\u8217' objection, suggesting that perhaps Rabbi Meir\u8217's point was that the act of swallowing constitutes a kind of {\i hanachah} \u8212- the placing-down that completes the {\i melachah} of carrying \u8212- making it more integrally connected to the act of eating than it might appear. This remains a somewhat difficult argument, and the {\i Gemara} does not fully resolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chelev Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} involving Rabbi Meir\u8217's {\i chelev} case contains all three types of {\i issur chal al issur}. Rashi explains the structure as follows. The {\i chelev} begins as a non-sanctified animal ({\i chullin}), and at that stage the {\i chelev} is prohibited in eating but permitted in benefit. When the animal is sanctified ({\i hukdeshah}), an additional prohibition of {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- benefit \u8212- takes effect on the {\i chelev}. Rashi identifies this as {\i issur mosif}, since the sanctification adds the prohibition of {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} on top of the existing {\i issur achilah}. It must be noted that this is a somewhat unusual application of {\i issur mosif}: the classic formulation of {\i issur mosif} involves the object becoming forbidden to a wider group of people, whereas here the object was already forbidden to everyone \u8212- the new prohibition simply adds a more stringent dimension ({\i issur hana\u8217'ah}) to the same object for the same people. Rashi\u8217's categorization should be noted as contested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chelev} then becomes {\i notar}, left over past its permitted time. At that moment, as Rashi explains, the object becomes forbidden to the {\i mizbeach} as well \u8212- it can no longer be offered on the altar \u8212- {\i migo d\u8217'itosif issura la-mizbeach}. Since it is now forbidden to an additional party (the altar itself), this too constitutes {\i issur mosif} in the sense of expanding the domain of what is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tamei} person then arrives and eats. Rashi identifies this as {\i issur kolel}: the {\i tamei} person was previously permitted to eat non-sanctified {\i chelev}, and now, having become {\i tamei}, he is forbidden from eating all {\i kodshim} \u8212- not just this piece of {\i chelev} but the entire category. The prohibition does not change the status of the {\i chelev} but expands the category of things forbidden to this person, which in turn includes this piece. That is the hallmark of {\i issur kolel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chelev Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What, then, is the {\i issur bas achas} in the {\i chelev} case? Rashi\u8217's explanation is acknowledged to be complex. {\i Tosafot} proposes that Rabbi Meir\u8217's addition of {\i Shabbat} alongside {\i Yom Kippur} provides the {\i bas achas} element: on a day that is simultaneously {\i Shabbat} and {\i Yom Kippur}, the two prohibitions of {\i Yom Kippur} and {\i Shabbat} arrive at exactly the same moment and are not sequential. This is the case of {\i bas achas} \u8212- both {\i Shabbat} and {\i Yom Kippur} are in effect simultaneously, so there is no question of one blocking the other, and both are independently operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Following Whom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i l\u8217'Rabbi Meir aliba d\u8217'man} \u8212- Rabbi Meir\u8217's ruling that all four transgressions in the {\i Mishnah} are operative: does it align with Rabbi Eliezer or with Rabbi Yehoshua on the separate question that must now be introduced?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mitzvah Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an independent debate between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua: {\i to\u8217'eh bid\u8217'var mitzvah patur} \u8212- one who makes an error in the course of performing a {\i mitzvah} is exempt from bringing a {\i korban chatat}. Rabbi Yehoshua holds this exemption; Rabbi Eliezer does not. In the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's case of the wife exchange, the man who inadvertently had relations with the wrong woman was attempting to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of marital relations with his wife \u8212- {\i onah}, the conjugal obligation. If Rabbi Yehoshua exempts one who errs in the course of performing a {\i mitzvah}, why is the man in the {\i Mishnah} liable to a {\i chatat} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: if the {\i Mishnah} follows Rabbi Yehoshua, the four {\i chataot} should be inapplicable \u8212- since the man erred while attempting a {\i mitzvah}. It must therefore be that the {\i Mishnah} follows Rabbi Eliezer, who does not grant this exemption. Alternatively \u8212- {\i i ba\u8217'is eima} \u8212- even if it follows Rabbi Yehoshua, Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's exemption is not absolute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brit Milah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's exemption is illustrated by the case of a {\i mohel} who performs {\i brit milah} on {\i Shabbat} and accidentally circumcises the wrong infant \u8212- the baby born on Sunday rather than the one born on Saturday, whose {\i brit milah} is duly scheduled for that {\i Shabbat}. The {\i mohel} was attempting to perform a {\i mitzvah} and erred in its execution, and for this error he is exempt from a {\i chatat} according to Rabbi Yehoshua. Why? Because {\i d\u8217'zmano bahul} \u8212- there is a time pressure involved. The {\i Shabbat milah} must be performed on {\i Shabbat} specifically, and that urgency creates a mitigating context. Combined with the fact that the person was genuinely engaged in performing a {\i mitzvah}, the result is that the error falls into the category of an exempted inadvertence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two factors thus converge: the attempt to perform a {\i mitzvah}, and the urgency of a time-bound obligation. One may understand these as jointly producing a kind of {\i ones} \u8212- an externally imposed compulsion that reduces culpability \u8212- or alternatively as the {\i mitzvah}-attempt providing a moral dispensation that can only operate when the degree of negligence is sufficiently low. The time pressure reduces the fault level to the point where the {\i mitzvah} exemption can take hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Time Pressure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the wife exchange, neither mitigating factor is present in full. The man was indeed attempting to fulfill a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- marital relations \u8212- but there was no {\i zman u\u8217'vahul}, no time pressure compelling him to act at that moment. Marital relations are not a time-bound obligation in the same way as a {\i brit milah} scheduled for a specific {\i Shabbat}. Without the time pressure, the fault level is not mitigated enough for Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's exemption to apply. Accordingly, even according to Rabbi Yehoshua, the man in the {\i Mishnah} would be liable to bring a {\i chatat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises what appears to be a challenge: the case of {\i terumah}. A {\i kohen} eating {\i terumah} \u8212- which is a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- subsequently discovers that he is actually a {\i chalal} (the son of a divorced woman or a {\i chalutzah}), and therefore disqualified from {\i kehunah} and prohibited from eating {\i terumah}. Rabbi Yehoshua exempts him from having to pay the principal plus the additional fifth ({\i keren v\u8217'chomesh}) that a {\i zar} who inadvertently eats {\i terumah} would normally owe. Yet there is no particular time pressure in eating {\i terumah} \u8212- one can eat it at any time. Does this not contradict the claim that Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's exemption requires {\i zman u\u8217'vahul}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Bibi bar Abaye resolves this by positing that the {\i terumah} case in question was specifically on {\i Erev Pesach}: the {\i kohen} was eating {\i chametz terumah} before the deadline and was under time pressure to consume it before {\i Pesach} began, creating exactly the {\i zman u\u8217'vahul} needed. Alternatively \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ibayis eima} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's case of the wife exchange may be read as {\i issur bas achas}, as will now be explored, and the entire preceding analysis may be reconfigured accordingly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bas Achas Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now offers an alternative reading of the entire {\i Mishnah}: perhaps all four prohibitions \u8212- {\i eshet ish}, {\i eshet ach}, {\i achot isha}, and {\i niddah} \u8212- applied simultaneously, making this a pure case of {\i issur bas achas}. If so, the {\i Mishnah} could hold even according to Rabbi Shimon, who normally denies {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- since Rabbi Shimon concedes double liability in {\i bas achas} cases, as the {\i baraita} proved earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge is to construct a scenario in which all statuses applied at literally the same moment. {\i Eshet ish} and {\i eshet ach} are not difficult to synchronize in principle \u8212- if two brothers betroth two women simultaneously, both statuses ({\i married woman} and {\i brother\u8217's wife}) arise at the same instant. Simultaneity of {\i kiddushin} can be achieved through a {\i shaliach}: the two brothers appoint a single representative, and the two women appoint a single representative, and the two {\i shaliachs} execute the {\i kiddushin} in a single transaction \u8212- {\i paga shliach b\u8217'shliach}. In that moment, each woman becomes simultaneously an {\i eshet ish} and an {\i eshet ach}, and additionally an {\i achot isha} (the wife\u8217's sister to her brother-in-law).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Simultaneity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i niddah} status, however, is more difficult to synchronize. Either the woman was already a {\i niddah} before the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- in which case the {\i niddah} prohibition preceded the others and the question of {\i issur chal al issur} applies in reverse \u8212- or she became a {\i niddah} afterward, in which case it is again sequential. A stopwatch-precise coincidence of menstruation and {\i kiddushin} at the same moment seems impossible to engineer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Under Three Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this with a striking answer attributed to Rabram in the name of Rav: {\i b\u8217'shofet mitoch gimel l\u8217'achar gimel} \u8212- the girl was a {\i niddah} from before her third birthday, and that {\i niddah} status continued past her third birthday. In practical terms: the girl experienced her first menstrual flow before turning three, and it continued through that birthday. This means that at the moment she turned three years old, she was simultaneously a {\i niddah} and, for the first time, subject to the full legal consequences of the prohibitions that had already been formally created.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This solution exploits a fundamental {\i halachah}: sexual relations with a girl under the age of three are not considered {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- a legally cognizable act of intercourse \u8212- from the Torah\u8217's perspective. Consequently, even though the brothers had formally married these girls (their father acting as {\i shaliach} to accept the {\i kiddushin} on their behalf), the prohibitions of {\i eshet ish}, {\i eshet ach}, and {\i achot isha} were technically in place but had no practical operative force \u8212- because any physical act with them would not constitute {\i bi\u8217'ah}. The {\i niddah} prohibition similarly was in place but not yet operative as a practical matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The moment the girls turned three, all of these prohibitions became simultaneously operative: the {\i bi\u8217'ah} prohibition of {\i eshet ish}, {\i eshet ach}, and {\i achot isha} took effect at that same instant, and the {\i niddah} prohibition \u8212- which had been in effect since before the third birthday and had never been removed by {\i tevilah} \u8212- was equally operative at that moment. Thus, from the standpoint of practical {\i chiyuv}, all four prohibitions descended simultaneously, constituting a genuine {\i issur bas achas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} further notes that this analysis applies to the men\u8217's liability \u8212- {\i l\u8217'chiyuvinuhu} \u8212- which takes effect when the girls turn three. For the women\u8217's own liability, the relevant moment is when they reach {\i bat mitzvah} (twelve years old). For the {\i bas achas} analysis to hold for the women as well, the {\i niddah} status must have been continuously in effect from before age three through age twelve without any intervening {\i tevilah} \u8212- an extended period of nine years. The {\i Gemara} accepts this as the structure of the case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pregnancy Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the three-month separation requirement, the {\i Gemara} confronts what might seem a logical difficulty. If the wife exchange was an isolated incident \u8212- a single mistaken act \u8212- why should there be a concern about pregnancy requiring three months of separation? The {\i Gemara} invokes a widespread belief of the ancient world: {\i ein ishah misaberet bi-vi\u8217'ah rishonah} \u8212- a woman does not conceive from the first act of intercourse. If this is so, the one-time error should not produce pregnancy, and no waiting period should be necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this with the answer of Rav Nachman in the name of Rabbah bar Avuha: {\i sheba\u8217'u v\u8217'shanu} \u8212- they did not err only once; they had relations a second time as well. That second act creates the possibility of pregnancy and justifies the three-month separation. The {\i Gemara} then notes that if they had relations twice, Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's count of sixteen {\i chataot} might seem too low \u8212- shouldn\u8217't there be thirty-two? The answer is that the count of sixteen is reckoned from the first act alone, and the question of how many subsequent acts there were (and indeed, according to Rabbi Eliezer, how many distinct {\i kochos} \u8212- thrusts \u8212- occurred within a single act) is left aside. The {\i baraita} counts from the initial {\i bi\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamar Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a challenge to the principle that a woman cannot conceive from the first act of intercourse: {\i veha Tamar d\u8217'vi\u8217'ah rishonah i\u8217'abra} \u8212- Tamar became pregnant with Judah from what was apparently a single act, since the verse says {\i v\u8217'lo yasaf od lada\u8217'atah} \u8212- he did not know her again. If the first act cannot produce pregnancy, how did Tamar conceive?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question presupposes that Tamar was a virgin when she had relations with Judah \u8212- otherwise she might have had prior acts of intercourse and this was not technically her \u8220"first.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} will now address this presupposition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hymen Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav answers: {\i Tamar b\u8217'etzba mi\u8217'acha} \u8212- Tamar had removed her own hymen with her finger before the encounter with Judah. The {\i Gemara} then cites Rav Yitzchak\u8217's tradition: {\i kol mu\u8217'achos shel beit Rebbi, Tamar sheman} \u8212- all the girls in the household of Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi who engaged in this practice were given the name Tamar, or were associated with this nickname, because this was understood to be what Tamar herself had done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i Gemara} is remarkable in its assumptions about ancient practice and touches on the {\i halachic} category of {\i dam besulim} \u8212- hymenal blood. The {\i halachah} (developed toward the end of the Talmudic period and formalized post-Talmudicaly) treats hymenal blood with {\i niddah}-like stringency rabbinically. The discussion here implicitly engages with the reality that hymenal blood would not be present if the hymen had been previously removed, which has {\i halachic} ramifications for the wedding night. The Gemara\u8217's concern is not the {\i halachah} of {\i dam besulim} per se but rather the question of whether Tamar could truly have been a virgin \u8212- and it answers that she was technically a virgin (in terms of never having had vaginal intercourse) while not having an intact hymen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Er Onan Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if Tamar was a virgin in the relevant sense, what then were Er and Onan doing? The {\i Gemara} states: {\i Er v\u8217'Onan shimeshu shelo k\u8217'darkah} \u8212- they practiced non-vaginal intercourse ({\i bi\u8217'ah shelo k\u8217'darkah}, anal intercourse), which is why the hymen remained intact and why no conception occurred. This reading of the verse {\i va-yishchet artzah} \u8212- Onan \u8220"spoiled [his seed] to the ground\u8221" \u8212- differs from the {\i peshat}, which most naturally reads as a description of withdrawal and external ejaculation. The {\i Gemara} here reads the verse as describing the inherent nature of the act (non-vaginal intercourse necessarily results in the seed going to the ground), not as a separate act of withdrawal mid-intercourse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zera Le-Vatalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now cites the teaching of Rabbi Eliezer regarding a nursing woman: {\i kol esrim v\u8217'arba chodesh dash m\u8217'bifnim v\u8217'zoreh m\u8217'bachutz} \u8212- for the full twenty-four months of nursing, a husband should thresh on the inside and winnow on the outside. The agricultural metaphor \u8212- {\i dash} refers to the threshing that loosens grain from chaff, {\i zoreh} to the winnowing that separates them through the wind \u8212- describes intercourse with external ejaculation (early withdrawal). Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's rationale is that pregnancy would endanger the nursing infant by potentially disrupting the milk supply, and he prescribes this method as a form of birth control for the nursing period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Withdrawal Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages respond sharply: {\i halalu eino ela k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan} \u8212- this practice is like the sin of Er and Onan. The objection is categorical: early withdrawal, which results in seminal emission outside the wife, constitutes {\i zera l\u8217'vatalah} \u8212- the destruction of seed \u8212- and is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamar Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then connects the Onan case to the earlier discussion of Tamar. Since Onan\u8217's sin is described as {\i va-yishchet artzah}, and we now know this meant {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo k\u8217'darkah} rather than simple withdrawal, the Sages\u8217' equation of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's practice with {\i ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan} requires clarification: in what sense is early withdrawal \u8220"like\u8221" what Er and Onan did, if Er and Onan did something categorically different (non-vaginal intercourse)?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this with a careful formulation: {\i k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan v\u8217'lo k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan} \u8212- similar to but not identical with what Er and Onan did. The similarity is that in both cases the semen is emitted outside the body of the wife \u8212- {\i va-yishchet artzah}, the seed is destroyed. The difference is that Er and Onan engaged in {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo k\u8217'darkah} (non-vaginal intercourse), while Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's practice involves normal vaginal intercourse ({\i k\u8217'darkah}) with external ejaculation. The common thread is the destruction of seed; the manner differs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for Er\u8217's sin specifically \u8212- the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i Onan} we understand, since the verse says {\i va-yishchet artzah} of him explicitly. {\i Er minalan} \u8212- how do we know Er sinned in the same way? Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak derives it from the word {\i gam} \u8212- {\i va-yamet gam oto}, \u8220"he also died\u8221": the {\i gam} indicates that Er died for the same sin as Onan. And why did Er do it? {\i K\u8217'dei shelo tisaber v\u8217'yachish yofyah} \u8212- so that Tamar would not become pregnant and her beauty would not be diminished by pregnancy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} engages directly with the apparent tension in this {\i Gemara}. On the one hand, the {\i Gemara} here condemns external ejaculation as akin to {\i ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan}. On the other hand, a {\i Gemara} in Nedarim appears to permit {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo k\u8217'darkah} as a legitimate mode of marital intercourse, which necessarily involves seminal emission outside the wife. {\i Tosafot} presents two answers in the name of the {\i Ri}. The first: the {\i Gemara} in Nedarim speaks only of {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo k\u8217'darkah} performed without seminal emission \u8212- {\i b\u8217'lo hotza\u8217'at zera d\u8217'shari} \u8212- and the prohibition of {\i ma\u8217'aseh Er v\u8217'Onan} applies only when there is actual destruction of seed. Without emission, the act is not {\i zera l\u8217'vatalah} regardless of the manner of intercourse. The second answer of the {\i Ri} is broader and more permissive: the prohibition of {\i zera l\u8217'vatalah} applies only when the husband specifically intends to destroy the seed \u8212- {\i k\u8217'she-mitkaven l\u8217'hashchit ha-zera} \u8212- and when this is a habitual practice \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ragil la\u8217'asot ken tamid}. But if the act arises spontaneously from desire for one\u8217's wife, and is not motivated by the purpose of avoiding procreation or destroying seed as such, then it is permitted \u8212- {\i d\u8217'amar b\u8217'Nedarim kol mah she-adam rotzeh la\u8217'asot b\u8217'ishto ya\u8217'aseh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rama\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This second position of the {\i Ri} is of considerable practical {\i halachic} significance. The {\i Rama} rules in accordance with this second interpretation of {\i Tosafot}: within the context of marital relations, the prohibition of {\i zera l\u8217'vatalah} is not triggered by the place or manner of seminal emission but only by the intent and habitual purpose of destroying seed to avoid procreation. Any form of sexual activity between husband and wife, without that specific intent, is not categorized as {\i zera l\u8217'vatalah}. This ruling is fundamental to understanding the {\i halachic} parameters of marital intimacy, and represents the authoritative {\i Ashkenazic} position on the question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 36\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pregnant Woman Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens on 35b, four lines before the wide lines begin, with a dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish concerning the validity of {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} performed while the {\i yevama} is pregnant. The {\i mishnah} establishes that the outcome of such acts depends on whether the child born is viable ({\i ben kayama}) or not. The critical question, however, is whether Reish Lakish goes further than the {\i mishnah} and holds that even if the child turns out not to be viable \u8212- meaning, retroactively, that {\i chalitzah} was necessary \u8212- a {\i chalitzah} performed while the woman was pregnant is nonetheless not a valid {\i chalitzah} and must be repeated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan opens by challenging Reish Lakish from the {\i mishnah}: {\i ein ha-vlad shel kayama hu assur bi-krovoteha ve-hi assura bi-krovav u-psula min ha-kehuna} \u8212- if the child is not viable, the man who performed {\i chalitzah} is forbidden to her relatives, she is forbidden to his relatives, and she is disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}. In other words, the {\i mishnah} treats the {\i chalitzah} as having rendered her a {\i chalutza}, with all its attendant consequences. Rabbi Yochanan presses: {\i bishlama ledidi} \u8212- this works out well according to me, who holds that {\i chalitzat me\u8217'uberet shema chalitzah}, that {\i chalitzah} performed on a pregnant woman is a valid {\i chalitzah} if the child subsequently proves non-viable. {\i Mishum hachi pasla} \u8212- that is why the {\i mishnah} disqualifies her to a {\i kohen}, because she has genuinely become a {\i chalutza}. {\i Ela ledidach} \u8212- but according to you, Reish Lakish, who holds that {\i chalitzat me\u8217'uberet lo shema chalitzah}, why does the {\i mishnah} impose the {\i chumra} of {\i psula min ha-kehunah}? By your account, the {\i chalitzah} was never meaningful to begin with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish responds: {\i miderabbanan u-lechumra}. The Rabbis can treat a {\i chalitzah} performed on a pregnant woman as a {\i chalitzah} for purposes of stringency \u8212- rendering her a {\i chalutza} with all the relevant prohibitions \u8212- without conceding that it is a valid {\i chalitzah} at the biblical level. Hence the {\i mishnah} imposes all the {\i chumrot} associated with {\i chalitzah} without affirming that the act actually freed her. She will still require a proper {\i chalitzah} from the brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alt. Version of Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presents the alternate version: {\i ika de-amri}, some say the exchange ran in the opposite direction, with Reish Lakish initiating the challenge to Rabbi Yochanan. Reish Lakish cites the same {\i mishnah} language and argues: {\i bishlama ledidi} \u8212- according to me, who holds that {\i chalitzat me\u8217'uberet lo shema chalitzah}, it makes sense that the {\i mishnah} teaches only the {\i chumrot}: she is forbidden to a {\i kohen}, she is forbidden to his relatives, and so on. {\i V\u8217'lo katni eina tzricha chalitzah min ha-achin} \u8212- but it does not tell you the major {\i chiddush} that she is now fully free and requires no further {\i chalitzah} from the brothers. That larger leniency is absent because it would be false according to my view. {\i Ela ledidach} \u8212- but according to you, Rabbi Yochanan, the {\i mishnah} should have led with the primary novelty: that {\i chalitzah} performed while she was pregnant, if the child proves non-viable, fully exempts her and she needs no further {\i chalitzah} from the brothers. Why did the {\i mishnah} omit that {\i kula}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan answers: {\i ein hachi nami}, you are correct that it could have said so. {\i Aidi detani reisha lo pasla} \u8212- but since the opening clause of the {\i mishnah} established that when the child turns out to be viable, the {\i chalitzah} carries no weight even {\i lechumra} \u8212- there are no {\i chumrot} at all, we treat the act as though it never occurred \u8212- the {\i mishnah} set up the parallel in the concluding clause by teaching the {\i chumra} of {\i psula min ha-kehunah} for the non-viable scenario. But you are correct, Rabbi Yochanan concedes: she would in fact need no further {\i chalitzah}, and that {\i kula} is implicit even if unstated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan now presses Reish Lakish from a different angle. If a man performed {\i yibum} with his {\i yevama} while she was pregnant, and she subsequently miscarried, the {\i mishnah} says he may remain married to her \u8212- {\i yikayem}. {\i Bishlama ledidi} \u8212- according to me, who holds that both {\i chalitzat me\u8217'uberet shema chalitzah} and {\i biat me\u8217'uberet shema biah}, the act of {\i yibum} is retrospectively valid if the child proves non-viable. Hence {\i mishum hachi tani yikayem} \u8212- that is why it says he may remain married. {\i Ela ledidach} \u8212- but according to you, Reish Lakish, who holds the act was not meaningful, the {\i mishnah} should not merely say {\i yikayem}; it should say {\i yachzor v\u8217'yibom} \u8212- let him go back and perform a proper act of {\i yibum}, and only then remain married. Why does it simply say {\i yikayem}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish responds: {\i mai yikayem} \u8212- what do you think {\i yikayem} means? It means precisely {\i yachzor v\u8217'yibom v\u8217'yikayem} \u8212- let him perform another act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} to make the {\i yibum} binding, and then he may remain married. And there is no other option here: {\i d\u8217'lo sagi b\u8217'lav hachi} \u8212- there is no way forward without this step.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the reasoning: {\i d\u8217'lo mistra belo biah l\u8217'achar she\u8217'hipilah} \u8212- after she has miscarried, if he now wishes to either stay married or separate from her, he cannot simply give a {\i get}, because {\i biat me\u8217'uberet lav shema biah} \u8212- the earlier act did not constitute a valid act of {\i yibum}. Nor can a {\i chalitzah} follow, since he is in any case considered to have had relations with her and performing {\i chalitzah} after such contact would be entirely inappropriate at least at the rabbinic level. Therefore, his only available step is to have another act of {\i bi\u8217'ah}, which will then constitute binding {\i yibum}. After that, he may either remain married or give a {\i get}. Thus, {\i yikayem} is not merely permission to remain married \u8212- it is instruction that this is the only path forward regardless of what he ultimately chooses to do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presents the alternative version of this exchange as well \u8212- {\i ika d\u8217'amri} \u8212- in which Reish Lakish uses the {\i yibum} case proactively to argue against Rabbi Yochanan, and Rabbi Yochanan responds {\i ein hachi nami}, that {\i ratza yotzi ratza yikayem} could have been stated, but the {\i mishnah} chose the parallel construction because the opening clause in the viable-child case required the language of {\i yotzi} \u8212- since there the man must divorce, as the woman is his brother\u8217's wife \u8212- and so the concluding clause correspondingly used {\i yikayem}, meaning he is permitted to remain married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Reframes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a {\i baraita}: {\i hakones et yevamto v\u8217'nimtzeit me\u8217'uberet harei zo lo tinasei tzaratah} \u8212- a man who performed {\i yibum} with his {\i yevama} and it turns out she was pregnant: her co-wife ({\i tzarah}) may not go ahead and remarry, lest the child be viable. This poses a question: if the child is viable, then {\i yibum} was unnecessary; if not viable, the {\i yibum} was valid. Either way, the {\i tzarah} seems to be released. Why then is she barred from remarrying?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara emends: the concern is {\i shema lo yehei havelad ben kayama} \u8212- perhaps the child will not be viable, meaning the {\i yibum} was necessary but invalid because performed during pregnancy. This seems to be strong support for Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's opponents. Abaye steps in with a reframing: {\i bebiat kulei alma lo pligi delo patra} \u8212- regarding {\i bi\u8217'ah} performed as {\i yibum} with a pregnant woman, everyone agrees it does not take effect. The logic is clear: since pregnancy creates a presumption that the child may be viable, the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} would risk transgressing the prohibition of {\i eishet ach}, and therefore no retroactive validity can be granted. {\i Ki pligi bachalitzah} \u8212- the dispute is limited to {\i chalitzah}. Since {\i chalitzah} separates the man and woman rather than drawing them together, there is at least an argument that it could be valid even if performed during pregnancy. On this Rabbi Yochanan holds {\i chalitzat me\u8217'uberet shema chalitzah}, conceding that {\i biat me\u8217'uberet lo shema biah}; Reish Lakish holds both are invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Focus on Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Abaye\u8217's reframing, the {\i tzarah} cannot remarry in the {\i yibum} case because everyone agrees the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} did not work, and therefore if the child proves non-viable she remains bound and requires {\i chalitzah} or valid {\i yibum}. The dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish thus concerns only whether {\i chalitzah} performed during pregnancy is retrospectively valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Package Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects to Abaye\u8217's reframing: {\i mamma nafshach} \u8212- make up your mind. {\i I biah me\u8217'uberet shema biah, chalitzat me\u8217'uberet shema chalitzah} \u8212- if the {\i bi\u8217'ah} is valid, then the {\i chalitzah} is valid; {\i v\u8217'i biah me\u8217'uberet lo shema biah, chalitzat me\u8217'uberet nami lo shema chalitzah} \u8212- if the {\i bi\u8217'ah} is not valid, the {\i chalitzah} is not valid either. The principle is that {\i kol ha\u8217'oleh l\u8217'yibum oleh l\u8217'chalitzah, v\u8217'chol she\u8217'eino oleh l\u8217'yibum eino oleh l\u8217'chalitzah} \u8212- whatever is eligible for {\i yibum} is eligible for {\i chalitzah}, and whatever is ineligible for {\i yibum} is ineligible for {\i chalitzah}. They are a package deal. Abaye\u8217's bifurcation \u8212- that {\i yibum} fails but {\i chalitzah} succeeds \u8212- cannot be sustained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child\u8217's Birth Factor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having rejected Abaye\u8217's reframing, Rava now explains the {\i baraita} differently. {\i Hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- here is what the {\i baraita} is actually saying: the {\i tzarah} cannot remarry {\i shema yihiyeh velad ben kayama} \u8212- lest the child be viable. Now, if the child is viable, why does it matter whether the {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} was valid? Because an entirely new principle now enters the discussion: {\i ha-velad eino poter ad sheyeitzei l\u8217'avir ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- the child does not release the wives from the {\i zikah} until he is actually born. Even if we know the child will be viable, the co-wives are not free until the moment of birth. Therefore, the {\i baraita}\u8217's concern is precisely this: if the child turns out to be viable, neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitzah} was meaningful \u8212- everyone agrees on that \u8212- but furthermore, even knowing a viable child is coming, the co-wives remain bound until the birth actually occurs. Hence the {\i tzarah} cannot go ahead and remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Supporting Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited in direct support of Rava\u8217's framework: {\i tanya kevateih d\u8217'Rava} \u8212- we taught a {\i baraita} consistent with Rava. {\i Hakones yevamto v\u8217'nimtzeit me\u8217'uberet harei zo lo tinasei tzaratah shema yihiyeh velad ben kayama} \u8212- and now the {\i baraita} spells out why that is a problem: {\i v\u8217'ein biah v\u8217'chalitzah poteret} \u8212- neither {\i bi\u8217'ah} nor {\i chalitzah} exempts in the case of a viable child. {\i Ela velad poter} \u8212- it is the child that exempts. {\i She-ha-velad eino poter ad sheyeitzei l\u8217'avir ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- and the child does not exempt until he has actually entered the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Co-Wife\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof for Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now draws an inference from the {\i baraita}: {\i taama d\u8217'shama yihiyeh velad ben kayama} \u8212- the reason the {\i tzarah} cannot remarry is specifically because the child might be viable and the birth has not yet occurred. {\i Ha lo havi velad ben kayama, miftar tzaratah} \u8212- but if the child were not viable, the {\i tzarah} would be permitted to remarry. This seems to be a proof in favor of Rabbi Yochanan: the {\i yibum} performed during pregnancy would be retroactively valid if the child proves non-viable, and the {\i tzarah} would then be released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore asks whether this constitutes a conclusive refutation of Reish Lakish. Reish Lakish responds: {\i hachi katani} \u8212- here is how the {\i baraita} is truly to be read. The {\i tzarah} cannot remarry for two independent reasons. First, {\i shema lo yehei havelad ben kayama} \u8212- maybe the child will not be viable, in which case {\i chalitzat me\u8217'uberet lo shema chalitzah u-viat me\u8217'uberet lo shema bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- neither the {\i chalitzah} nor the {\i yibum} was meaningful, and the {\i tzarah} remains bound. Second, even if you invoke {\i rov nashim velad malya yalden} \u8212- most pregnancies produce viable children, and one might assume the {\i tzarah} is effectively free \u8212- the answer is {\i velad eino poter ad sheyetzei l\u8217'avir ha\u8217'olam}: she is still not released until the child is born. Thus the {\i baraita} focused on the viable-child scenario because that is the one people might think resolves the problem, but in truth the {\i tzarah} is stuck either way \u8212- if non-viable, the {\i yibum} failed; if viable, she must wait for the birth. Nothing effective happens while the woman is pregnant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Dies Away\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar then remarks that Reish Lakish\u8217's position must have support in a {\i mishnah} somewhere, and upon investigation he finds it. The {\i mishnah} teaches: {\i ha-ishah she-halach ba\u8217'alah v\u8217'tzaratah limdinat ha-yam} \u8212- a woman whose husband went overseas together with her co-wife, and word came back that the husband died. {\i Harei zo lo tinasei v\u8217'lo tityabem} \u8212- she may neither remarry nor perform {\i yibum} with the brother, {\i ad shetedah shema me\u8217'uberet hi tzaratah} \u8212- until she knows whether the co-wife is pregnant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to analyze this. Why can she not perform {\i yibum} with the brother? {\i Shema yehei havelad ben kayama v\u8217'yifga b\u8217'issur eishet ach d\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- lest the co-wife carry a viable child, making the act of {\i yibum} a biblical transgression of {\i eishet ach}, since if a viable child exists there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} at all. That is clear. But why can she not perform {\i chalitzah} and then remarry? That question requires careful examination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine-Month Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i ela nechzei} \u8212- let us think about this. I understand that she cannot perform {\i chalitzah} and remarry {\i betoch tishah} \u8212- within nine months \u8212- since during that period we are uncertain whether the co-wife is pregnant with a viable child. {\i Ela tachlotz betoch tishah v\u8217'tinasei l\u8217'achar tishah} \u8212- but why not perform {\i chalitzah} right away and then remarry only after nine months have passed? At that point, either the {\i chalitzah} was valid because the co-wife was not pregnant, or if she was pregnant the child would by then have been born and the co-wife released in any event. This combined approach should satisfy all possibilities. Since the {\i mishnah} does not offer this option, it appears to prove like Reish Lakish \u8212- that {\i chalitzah} performed while the co-wife is pregnant is not valid, and therefore performing {\i chalitzah} now would be pointless if the co-wife turns out to be pregnant with a viable child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, questions whether this is a strong proof. Even if the {\i chalitzah} is valid, one might still object. The Gemara responds: {\i dilma ika inish d\u8217'havi b\u8217'chalitzah v\u8217'lo havi b\u8217'achraza} \u8212- the concern is that someone present at the {\i chalitzah} might later not hear the retraction. As stated by Abaye bar Avin and Rav Chanana bar Abaye: the reason we avoid performing {\i chalitzah} prematurely is {\i shema yehei velad ben kayama v\u8217'nimtza ata matricha kruz li-khehunah} \u8212- lest the child prove viable, in which case the {\i chalitzah} was meaningless, yet we would have to make a public announcement retracting it. The danger is that someone who was present at the {\i chalitzah} but missed the retraction might later see this woman marrying a {\i kohen} and conclude that a {\i chalutza} may marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- a grave error. To avoid this confusion, the Rabbis prefer not to perform {\i chalitzah} at all while there is uncertainty about the co-wife\u8217's pregnancy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye intervenes with a simpler resolution. He points out that the {\i mishnah} never in fact said she cannot perform {\i chalitzah}. {\i Lo tinasei v\u8217'lo tityabem tani} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} said only that she may not remarry and may not perform {\i yibum}, but it did not say she may not obtain {\i chalitzah}. Therefore the {\i mishnah} carries no implication about the validity of {\i chalitzah} during the co-wife\u8217's pregnancy, and thus provides no support for Reish Lakish. {\i Aval ee chalitz lah hachi nami desharya} \u8212- if she does obtain {\i chalitzah} and then waits nine months to remarry, that is perfectly permissible. The {\i mishnah} simply rules out {\i yibum} (due to the risk of {\i eishet ach}) and remarrying without any prior {\i chalitzah} \u8212- not {\i chalitzah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result of Abaye\u8217's reading is that the {\i mishnah} offers no proof for Reish Lakish, and we are returned to the unresolved debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At last, a {\i baraita} is cited that supports Reish Lakish explicitly. {\i Ha-choletz l\u8217'me\u8217'uberet v\u8217'hipila} \u8212- one who performed {\i chalitzah} with a pregnant woman, and she subsequently miscarried: {\i tzricha chalitzah min ha-achim} \u8212- she still requires {\i chalitzah} from the brothers. The {\i chalitzah} performed during the pregnancy was not binding, even though it turned out retroactively that it would have been necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Paskins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: {\i hilchesa k\u8217'vasei d\u8217'Reish Lakish} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Reish Lakish. This is a striking ruling: the weight of the textual arguments throughout the {\i sugya} seemed to lean toward Rabbi Yochanan, yet in the end we have a {\i baraita} explicitly teaching Reish Lakish\u8217's position, and Rava rules accordingly. {\i Chalitzah} performed while the woman is pregnant is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling creates a clean and elegant symmetry. While the woman is pregnant, nothing binding or effective can occur \u8212- regardless of which way the pregnancy resolves. If the child will prove viable, then {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} are simply inapplicable, and moreover the co-wives are not released until the moment of birth. If the child will prove non-viable, then by Reish Lakish\u8217's ruling {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum} during the pregnancy still do not take effect. In both scenarios, while she is pregnant, everything is in abeyance. Nothing binding happens until after the birth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then notes that this is one of three cases \u8212- {\i b\u8217'hani telat} \u8212- in which we rule like Reish Lakish against Rabbi Yochanan, notwithstanding the general principle that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yochanan in his disputes with Reish Lakish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case involves a deathbed bequest. The {\i mishnah} rules that a dying man who distributes his property, enlarging one heir\u8217's share and diminishing another\u8217's, or equalizing the firstborn\u8217's portion with those of other sons, his words stand \u8212- provided the language of gift ({\i matana}) appears somewhere in the document, whether at the beginning, middle, or end. The dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish arises when a father uses the language of inheritance ({\i yerusha}) for one son and the language of gift ({\i matana}) for another. Rabbi Yochanan holds that the presence of gift language anywhere in the transaction validates the entire bequest, even the portion stated in the language of inheritance. Reish Lakish holds that it will not work unless the gift and inheritance language are bundled together \u8212- {\i ploni u-ploni yirshu sadeh ploni shenatatim lahem b\u8217'matana li-yerusha} \u8212- naming both heirs jointly. If he separated them, saying let Reuven inherit and let Shimon receive a gift, then Shimon\u8217's share is valid as a gift but Reuven\u8217's is not, since his portion was stated exclusively in the language of {\i yerusha}, which cannot override the Torah\u8217's inheritance law. We rule like Reish Lakish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is the major debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish that runs throughout {\i Shas}: {\i ha-kotev kol nechasav livno le-achar moto} \u8212- a father writes that he is giving his son a field \u8220"from now, effective after my death.\u8221" The rabbis interpret this as: title ({\i kinyan ha-guf}) transfers to the son immediately, while the right to usufruct ({\i kinyan peirot}) \u8212- the ongoing benefit and produce of the field \u8212- remains with the father until death. If the son sells his interest in the field during the father\u8217's lifetime and the son subsequently predeceases the father, the question is whether the purchaser acquires anything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan holds {\i kinyan peirot ke-kinyan ha-guf dami} \u8212- the father\u8217's right to the produce is tantamount to full title, and therefore the son\u8217's sale of the bare legal title during the father\u8217's lifetime, when the father\u8217's {\i kinyan peirot} gave him effective ownership, was meaningless. Since the son is no longer alive when the father dies, the sale yields nothing to the purchaser. Reish Lakish holds {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan ha-guf dami} \u8212- the right to usufruct does not constitute title, the son was genuinely the title-holder and could validly sell his title even while the father retained {\i kinyan peirot}, and therefore the purchaser acquires the field upon the father\u8217's death. We rule like Reish Lakish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pregnant/Nursing Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the second section of the {\i mishnah}: {\i ein ha-vlad shelo kayama} \u8212- if the child proved non-viable and the {\i yibum} was therefore retrospectively valid, the man may remain married. A {\i baraita} then adds: {\i tana mishum Rabbi Eliezer amru, yotzi b\u8217'get} \u8212- they said in the name of Rabbi Eliezer that he must nonetheless divorce her with a {\i get}. Even though retroactively the {\i yibum} was entirely legitimate, the fact that he had relations with his {\i yevama} while she was pregnant \u8212- when in all likelihood it would have constituted a violation of {\i eishet ach}, a prohibition subject to {\i kares} \u8212- cannot be tolerated even {\i bedi\u8217'eved}. We demand a divorce regardless of how it resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava & R. Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava observes: {\i Rabbi Meir v\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer amru davar echad} \u8212- Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Eliezer share a common principle, namely that when someone enters a forbidden or problematic union, even if it resolves in a technically permissible direction, we demand that he divorce and separate, and we do not passively accept the fait accompli.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel from Rabbi Meir is sourced from a {\i baraita}: {\i lo yisa adam uberet chaveiro u-meineket chaveiro} \u8212- a man may not marry a woman who is pregnant or nursing from a previous marriage, whether the previous husband has died or divorced her. The concern \u8212- developed elsewhere in the Gemara \u8212- is that a new pregnancy while nursing poses a danger to the nursing infant. {\i V\u8217'im nasa yotzi v\u8217'lo yachazir olamit divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- if he went ahead and married her, he must divorce her and may never remarry her. Rabbi Meir is extraordinarily strict: even though the prohibition is violated, he will not permit the marriage to stand even after the problematic period has passed. The {\i Chachamim} are more lenient: {\i yotzi}, he must separate, but {\i u-che-sheyagia zmano lichnos, yichnos} \u8212- once the nursing period ends and the concern is resolved, he may remarry her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Distinguishes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rava\u8217's equation of Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Meir: {\i dilma lo hi} \u8212- perhaps the cases are not parallel and the two {\i amora\u8217'im} do not in fact share the same principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One could argue in either direction. Perhaps Rabbi Eliezer is strict here specifically because the act in question \u8212- having relations with a pregnant {\i yevama} \u8212- risked a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} prohibition of {\i eishet ach} with its attendant {\i karet}. We will not tolerate even a retroactively valid {\i yibum} that was attended by such risk. But Rabbi Meir\u8217's case involves only a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} prohibition \u8212- marrying a nursing woman \u8212- and perhaps Rabbi Eliezer would be lenient there and agree with the {\i Chachamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, perhaps Rabbi Meir\u8217's stringency is confined to the {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} case, where the Rabbis applied {\i chizuk} \u8212- extra reinforcement of their own enactments \u8212- beyond even what would apply to a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} prohibition, since people tend to treat rabbinic rules lightly. But in the {\i d\u8217'Oraita} case of Rabbi Eliezer, where the prohibition is {\i eishet ach}, people naturally keep far away from it, and Rabbi Meir might well be lenient {\i bedi\u8217'eved} were it all resolved. The two cases thus cannot be assumed to express a single shared position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divorce Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get vs.\u160 ?Parshan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara addresses the precise meaning of {\i yotzi} in the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's ruling regarding one who marries a pregnant or nursing woman. Rava clarifies: {\i ul\u8217'divrei Chachamim yotzi b\u8217'get} \u8212- the {\i Chachamim} require an actual {\i get}, a formal bill of divorce, not merely physical separation ({\i perisha}). This is not a trivial distinction: a {\i get} renders the woman a {\i gerusha}, with all consequent legal ramifications \u8212- most significantly, that if her husband is a {\i kohen}, he cannot divorce her with a {\i get} and subsequently remarry her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra adds that this reading is supported by a close examination of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's own language: {\i dika nami d\u8217'katani yotzi} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} uses the word {\i yotzi} (literally: he shall send her out / divorce her), {\i v\u8217'lo katani yafrish} \u8212- and not the word {\i yafrish} (he shall separate from her). {\i Yotzi} is the standard technical term for divorce, not mere separation. {\i Shema minah} \u8212- this close reading of the text confirms the ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Marriage Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate difficulty. Rav Ashi poses the question to Rav Hoshaya son of Rav Idi: demanding a {\i get} is well and good when the husband is an ordinary {\i Yisrael}, who may always remarry the same woman. But would one actually demand a {\i get} when the husband is a {\i kohen}, thereby permanently severing the marriage? A {\i kohen} may not remarry a {\i gerusha}, and his wife \u8212- once divorced \u8212- acquires the disqualification of {\i gerusha} which follows her even with respect to other {\i kohanim}. The demand for a {\i get} thus carries far graver consequences for a {\i kohen} than for a {\i Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As a prelude to this question, the Gemara cites a {\i baraita} from Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel: {\i kol she-shaha b\u8217'adam shloshim yom, einu nefel} \u8212- if a newborn survives thirty days, he is no longer considered a {\i nefel} (non-viable infant). Rashi qualifies: this thirty-day rule applies specifically when it is uncertain whether the pregnancy was full-term. If it is known to be full-term, then {\i tinok ben yomo poter min ha-yibum} \u8212- a one-day-old infant, as soon as he is born, releases his mother from the obligation of {\i yibum}, as taught in Tractate Niddah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then develops the following scenario: a child was born, it was uncertain whether the pregnancy was full-term, and thus uncertain whether the child was viable. The child died within thirty days \u8212- without resolving the uncertainty \u8212- and the mother, assuming she was free because she had a child, went ahead and remarried. But in fact her status is uncertain: if the child was not viable, she was never released from {\i yibbum} and should have obtained {\i chalitzah} from the brothers-in-law first. She is thus a {\i safek} in need of {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina in the name of Rava rules: {\i im eshet Yisrael hi, choletzet} \u8212- if she is now married to a {\i Yisrael}, she must obtain {\i chalitzah} now to resolve the doubt. {\i V\u8217'im eshet kohen hi, eina choletzet} \u8212- but if she is married to a {\i kohen}, we do not demand that she obtain {\i chalitzah}, because doing so would make her a {\i chalutza} \u8212- or at least a {\i safek chalutza} \u8212- and render her permanently forbidden to her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demand Chalitza?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharshiya, also in the name of Rava, disagrees: {\i achat zo v\u8217'achat zo choletzet} \u8212- in both cases she must obtain {\i chalitzah}, even if this means she must separate from her {\i kohen} husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina then informs Rav Mesharshiya that he may have heard Rava say this at night, but {\i u-vetzafra hadar bei} \u8212- the next morning Rava reversed himself. Rav Mesharshiya responds with sharp skepticism \u8212- {\i sharyuta? yehei ra\u8217'ava d\u8217'tishrei afilu tarba} \u8212- you\u8217've found a leniency? Let it be God\u8217's will that you can permit even forbidden fat \u8212- expressing his refusal to credit the claimed retraction. He heard Rava\u8217's ruling from Rava himself, and will not accept a secondhand report of a reversal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Kohen Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This debate sets the stage for the governing question with which the daf concludes: {\i hacha gabi me\u8217'uberet chaveiro u-meineket chaveiro ha-nesu\u8217'ah l\u8217'kohen, mah \u8212- mi avadu rabbanan takanta l\u8217'kohen o lo?} In our case, where a man married a pregnant or nursing woman from a previous marriage and the Rabbis require him to issue a {\i get} \u8212- did the Rabbis create an exemption ({\i takana}) for a {\i kohen}, allowing him to remain married rather than be required to give a {\i get} that would forever prohibit remarriage? Or does the requirement of {\i get} apply equally to a {\i kohen}, at whatever cost? This question remains unresolved, and the Gemara will return to it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 37\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forced Divorce: Nursing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child Viability & 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at 37a with a continuation of the discussion concerning forced divorce in cases where someone married against a rabbinic concern \u8212- specifically, the case of a woman who was pregnant or nursing a child from a previous relationship and subsequently remarried. The Gemara had established that such a woman must separate from her new husband, and if she was widowed or divorced she must wait until the child is no longer nursing before remarrying. The question now is what happens when the man who married her in violation of this concern is a {\i kohen}, for whom a divorce creates a permanent bar to remarriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion overlaps with a parallel case involving a {\i yevamah} whose infant died within thirty days of birth, leaving uncertain whether the child was viable. The relevant background: if the child was born at full term, we assume viability without requiring the thirty-day period. If it was not a full-term pregnancy, we require thirty days to establish viability. Furthermore, the context assumes the child died from an external cause \u8212- some accident or tragedy \u8212- not a natural death, since a natural death within thirty days would itself be evidence of non-viability. According to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, in the non-full-term case, a death within thirty days leaves the child\u8217's status uncertain, and consequently, the mother\u8217's obligation of {\i chalitza} or {\i yibbum} remains unclear. If she then remarried, the question of whether to demand {\i chalitza} from her current situation is live \u8212- with the added complication that if she married a {\i kohen}, demanding {\i chalitza} at this point would permanently separate them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen & Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i hacha gabei me\u8217'uberet chaveiro umineket chaveiro deniseit lekohen mai?} \u8212- what is the ruling when the woman who was pregnant or nursing has married a {\i kohen}? {\i Mi avdi Rabbanan takanta lekohen o lo?} \u8212- did the Rabbis make some accommodation to avoid forcing a divorce where remarriage would become permanently impossible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds by sharply distinguishing the two cases: {\i amar lei hachi hashta?} \u8212- \u8220"How can you compare them?\u8221" In the {\i yevamah} case, where we refrain from demanding {\i chalitza}, there is a genuine halakhic position to rely upon. {\i Keivan de\u8217'ika Rabbanan dephaligi aleih deRabban Shimon ben Gamliel} \u8212- since there are Rabbis who disagree with Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel and hold that even a child who did not survive thirty days was a viable child ({\i velad malya hu}), we are not simply waiving the requirement because of the {\i kohen}\u8217's predicament. Rather, we are relying on the existing rabbinic opinion \u8212- the principle that {\i kedai hu ploni lismokh alav bish\u8217'at hadechak}, a given opinion is worthy of reliance in a time of pressing need. Since demanding {\i chalitza} would force a permanent separation from her husband, we apply this minority \u8212- or in this case, majority \u8212- opinion when we need it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why No Exception for Koh\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the nursing or pregnant woman, however, this logic does not apply. {\i Aval hacha, keman abdinnan?} \u8212- according to which opinion would we refrain from demanding a divorce? If we follow Rabbi Meir, he says {\i yotzi velo yachzir olamit} \u8212- he must divorce and may never remarry. If we follow the {\i Rabbanan}, they too require a {\i get}, even if remarriage is later permitted. There is simply no opinion in the debate over the nursing and pregnant case that permits the man to remain married without any divorce at all. We cannot invoke the \u8220"time of need\u8221" principle where no halakhic opinion actually supports the result we are trying to reach. Accordingly, even when the husband is a {\i kohen}, the divorce is required \u8212- and since the divorce creates a permanent bar to remarriage, the couple is permanently separated. This is a striking result: a {\i kohen} who technically entered a legitimate marriage, whose only violation was the timing with respect to the nursing period, loses his wife permanently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin in 3 Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3-Month Wait & Forced Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a related case: {\i kiddesh b\u8217'toch shlosha u\u8217'varach} \u8212- a man performed {\i kiddushin} with a woman within the three-month waiting period required after the death or divorce of her prior husband, and then ran away. The rationale for the three-month waiting period is to establish clarity of paternity: if the woman remarries immediately, it will be unclear whose child she is carrying. The question is what happens when someone violated this by performing {\i kiddushin} within the three months, but did not yet proceed to {\i nisu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rafram\u8217's Running Away\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Pligi bah Rav Acha v\u8217'Rafram} \u8212- Rav Acha and Rafram disagree. One opinion holds that we place him in {\i cherem} until he gives a {\i get}. The assumption is that even {\i kiddushin} alone within the three-month period is serious enough to compel a forced divorce. The other opinion holds that {\i arukei mistayei} \u8212- his running away suffices. Since the fundamental concern is {\i nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- actual cohabitation which could result in a pregnancy and confusion about paternity \u8212- and since by running away he has made clear that {\i nisu\u8217'in} will not be taking place anytime soon, we need not pursue him and force a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that a practical case came before Rafram, {\i havei uvda ramia l\u8217'Rafram}, and he ruled in accordance with his own position: {\i arukei mistayei}, the fact that he ran away is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the practical question \u8212- especially relevant for a {\i kohen} who performed {\i kiddushin} within the three-month period \u8212- of whether running away solves the problem. For a {\i kohen}, running away would be far preferable to being compelled to give a {\i get}, since a {\i get} would permanently bar him from remarrying this woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Kiddushin Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The earlier discussion about the nursing mother gave no indication that {\i kiddushin} alone, without {\i nisu\u8217'in}, would be any different \u8212- the Gemara seemed to demand a {\i get} regardless, even when the husband was a {\i kohen}. Tosafot, however, on 36b, cite an interesting leniency. Rabbi Yosef of Orl\u233 ?ans ({\i v\u8217'haRav Rabbi Yosef m\u8217'Orlinesh}) ruled that a man who performed {\i kiddushin} with a nursing woman ({\i b\u8217'echad shekiddesh meineket chaveiro}) need not be required to give a {\i get} \u8212- merely separation suffices ({\i ela hafrasha b\u8217'alma}). His reasoning: {\i keivan d\u8217'belav hachi assura lei} \u8212- since in any event they are prohibited from cohabiting, because {\i kallah belo bracha assura l\u8217'ba\u8217'alah k\u8217'niddah} \u8212- a bride without the recitation of {\i sheva brachot} and the consummation of {\i nisu\u8217'in} is forbidden to her husband like a {\i niddah} \u8212- there is no concern that they will live together as husband and wife during the nursing period. Without {\i nisu\u8217'in}, the fundamental problem does not arise, and therefore a formal {\i get} is not required. This constitutes a significant {\i kula}, though it does require reconciliation with the later Gemara that implies a {\i get} is necessary even in the {\i kohen} scenario.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unknown Paternity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case: 7 vs 9 Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a fascinating and detailed discussion about a woman who performed {\i yibbum} with her brother-in-law and subsequently gave birth at a time that left uncertain whether the child was fathered by the first husband or the {\i yavam}. The scenario: the first husband died, and the woman did {\i yibbum} before the three-month waiting period had elapsed. She then gave birth at a point in time that was consistent with either a seven-month pregnancy from the {\i yavam} or a nine-month pregnancy from the first husband. The child is not a {\i mamzer} in either case \u8212- if from the first husband, the child is legitimately the first husband\u8217's son; if from the {\i yavam}, then the {\i yibbum} was valid and the child is the {\i yavam}\u8217's legitimate son. But the uncertainty of paternity has important practical consequences for inheritance and for determining whether the {\i yavam} is currently violating an {\i issur kares} by continuing to cohabit with his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Rashi notes, another implication concerns the {\i chatas} obligation. The Mishnah rules they bring an {\i asham talui} \u8212- the conditional guilt offering brought when one is unsure whether a sin was committed. But if we could establish that the child is indeed from the first husband, then the {\i yibbum} was clearly invalid, there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}, and the {\i yavam} is definitely cohabiting with an {\i arayos} \u8212- he would be required to bring a definite {\i chatas kavu\u8217'a} rather than a mere {\i asham talui}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Rava l\u8217'Rav Nachman} \u8212- Rava challenged Rav Nachman: {\i leima halach achar rov nashim} \u8212- why not simply go by the majority? Most women give birth after nine months. If we apply the principle of {\i rov}, the majority, we should assume this child is a nine-month baby and therefore the son of the first husband. Why is there any doubt at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Visibly Pregnant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman\u8217's initial response \u8212- {\i nashei didan beshiva yaldan}, our women give birth after seven months \u8212- provoked a sharp rejoinder from Rava: {\i nashei didichu havu ruba d\u8217'alma?} \u8212- are the women of your town the majority of the world? Even if a non-trivial minority of women give birth in the seventh month, the majority of women worldwide still give birth after nine months, so why not apply {\i rov}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman then refined his answer. He said: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amina} \u8212- this is what I mean to say. {\i Rov nashim yaldan l\u8217'tisha} \u8212- yes, most women give birth after nine months, {\i u\u8217'miyuta l\u8217'shiva} \u8212- but a minority give birth in the seventh month. However, there is an additional critical factor: {\i v\u8217'chol hayoledes l\u8217'tisha ubra nikar l\u8217'shlish yameha} \u8212- any woman who will give birth after nine months will show visible signs of pregnancy by the end of the third month.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now consider the case before us. If this woman had become visibly pregnant three months after the death of her first husband \u8212- that is, one month after the {\i yavam} married her \u8212- it would have been immediately apparent that she was pregnant from the first husband, and there would have been no uncertainty. The very existence of the doubt in this case means \u8212- implicitly \u8212- that she was {\i not} visibly pregnant three months after the first husband\u8217's death. And that fact undermines the {\i rov}. On the one hand, the majority suggests a nine-month pregnancy from the first husband; on the other hand, if it were a nine-month pregnancy, she would (in most cases) have been visibly pregnant by three months, which she was not. {\i V\u8217'zu ho\u8217'il v\u8217'lo hukeir ubra l\u8217'shlish yameha, itrei lah ruba} \u8212- since she was not visibly pregnant after three months, the majority is thrown into question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Visible Pregnancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses the logic further. If every woman carrying a nine-month baby is visibly pregnant after three months ({\i kol hayoledes l\u8217'tisha ubra nikar l\u8217'shisha meha}), then the absence of visible pregnancy after three months would be conclusive proof in the {\i opposite} direction \u8212- we should know {\i for certain} that this is a seven-month baby from the {\i yavam}. {\i Ha me\u8217'delo u\u8217'kar l\u8217'shlisha meha, vadai bar sheva l\u8217'vastra hu} \u8212- if she was not visibly pregnant after three months, certainly the child is from the second husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore amends the formulation: {\i ela eima rov} \u8212- say rather that {\i most} women carrying nine-month babies show visible pregnancy after three months, but not all. This yields the correct result: the absence of visible pregnancy at three months undermines but does not eliminate the {\i rov} that most babies are nine-month babies. We thus have two countervailing considerations \u8212- the {\i rov} pointing toward a nine-month pregnancy from the first husband, and the absence of visible pregnancy at three months creating serious doubt about that. The result is a genuine {\i safek}, and that is why the Mishnah treats the child\u8217's paternity as unknown.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First & Second Child\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First: Kohen Gadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the legal consequences for the children born into this uncertain situation. The {\i Baraita} states: {\i rishon ra\u8217'uy lihyot kohen gadol} \u8212- the first child born in this ambiguous situation is fit to be a {\i kohen gadol}. Since both brothers are {\i kohanim} \u8212- if one is a {\i kohen} the other necessarily is as well \u8212- the child is certainly a {\i kohen} regardless of which man is his father. Moreover, the child was born within a legitimate marriage in any case: either he is the first husband\u8217's child, entirely legitimate; or he is the {\i yavam}\u8217's child from a valid {\i yibbum}, equally legitimate. Tosafot raises the question of whether a {\i kohen} must be {\i miyuchas} \u8212- with fully known paternity \u8212- to serve as {\i kohen gadol}, but concludes that the requirement of {\i miyuchas} applies only where there is a question of illegitimate birth, not where the child was unquestionably born within marriage and the only uncertainty is which of two legitimate marriages he derives from.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second: Safek Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Baraita} continues: {\i v\u8217'sheni mamzer misafek} \u8212- but a second child, born after the first, is a doubtful {\i mamzer}. The reasoning: if the first child was indeed the first husband\u8217's son, then there was no obligation of {\i yibbum} and the {\i yavam} was living with a woman who is {\i arayos} to him. Any subsequent child born of that relationship would be a {\i mamzer}. If the first child was the {\i yavam}\u8217's own child from a valid {\i yibbum}, then a subsequent child would also be legitimate. Since we do not know which is the case, the second child is a {\i safek mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The striking contrast is thus established: the first child could be a {\i kohen gadol}, and yet his sibling is a {\i safek mamzer} \u8212- perhaps the most legally disadvantaged status in Jewish law.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Mamzer Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's position is that the second child is a {\i safek mamzer}, and as such is {\i asur b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- he cannot marry even a {\i mamzeret}. The logic of this is painful but straightforward: a {\i safek mamzer} cannot marry a regular {\i Yisrael} because he might be a {\i mamzer}; he cannot marry a {\i mamzeret} because he might not be a {\i mamzer}, and a {\i mamzeret} is forbidden to one who is not a {\i mamzer}. He is caught on both sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Treat as Vad\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov states: {\i ein mamzer misafek} \u8212- there is no such thing, halakhically, as a {\i mamzer from doubt}. This is not a factual dispute \u8212- he agrees the child\u8217's status is uncertain. It is a halakhic dispute about how to categorize and treat a person of doubtful {\i mamzer} status. His position, as elaborated by the Gemara, is {\i vadai mamzer u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- we treat him as a definite {\i mamzer}, and therefore he is permitted to marry a {\i mamzeret}. The reasoning: the verse {\i lo yavo mamzer b\u8217'kehal Hashem} is understood to forbid entry into the \u8220"unblemished community.\u8221" A person about whom there is a question mark \u8212- even if he may in truth be completely untainted \u8212- is by definition not part of the category of the \u8220"unblemished.\u8221" The doubt itself removes him from the pristine category and places him in the category of the {\i mamzer}, so he may marry within that category. Thus, saying {\i vadai mamzer} is not a factual claim but a halakhic assignment: given the doubt, we treat him as a full {\i mamzer}, which is paradoxically a {\i leniency}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs Rava Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is then raised: who in this dispute is saying what? The Gemara notes that the debate between Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov and the {\i Tanna Kamma} is parallel to a debate recorded in the name of Rabbi Elazar, who states: {\i vadan be\u8217'vadan muttar} \u8212- a definite {\i mamzer} may marry a definite {\i mamzer}; but {\i vadan bi\u8217'sfekan, sfekan be\u8217'vadan, sfekan be\u8217'sfekan asur} \u8212- a definite {\i mamzer} may not marry a {\i safek mamzer}, and two {\i safek mamzerim} may not marry each other. The categories of {\i safek} mentioned by Rabbi Elazar are {\i shtuki} (a child whose mother is known but whose father is not \u8212- she deflects the question), {\i asufi} (a foundling whose parents are unknown), and {\i kuti} (a Samaritan, since Samaritan practice regarding {\i kiddushin} differs from halakha and their children may unknowingly be {\i mamzerim}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda quoted Rav ruling {\i halacha ke\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- that a {\i safek mamzer} is {\i asur}, cannot marry either a regular {\i Yisrael} or a definite {\i mamzer}. But when Rav Yehuda reported this ruling to Shmuel, Shmuel responded: {\i Hillel shana asara yuchasin} \u8212- Hillel taught that there are ten genealogical categories that came up from Babylonia, and all of them can intermarry in some combination ({\i v\u8217'chulan mutarin lavo zeh ba\u8217'zeh}). If a {\i safek mamzer} could marry no one at all, Hillel\u8217's teaching would be internally incoherent, for there would be no valid marriage partner for the {\i safek} categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sets the stage for the central interpretive dispute. {\i Abaye savar lah k\u8217'Shmuel, d\u8217'amar halacha k\u8217'Hillel} \u8212- Abaye holds like Shmuel that we rule like Hillel, meaning a {\i safek mamzer} is {\i mutar} to marry another {\i mamzer}, and therefore he reads Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov as the one who is {\i lenient} \u8212- the one who says {\i vadai mamzer u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- in order that the halakhic ruling in favor of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov not conflict with the ruling like Hillel. {\i Rava savar lah k\u8217'Rav, d\u8217'amar halacha ke\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- Rava holds like Rav that the {\i safek mamzer} is {\i asur}, and he therefore reads Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov as the one who is {\i stringent} \u8212- the one who says {\i safek mamzer ve\u8217'asur b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- so that the ruling in favor of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov aligns with the ruling like Rabbi Elazar. Each Amora interprets the ambiguous dispute of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov so that it coincides with the halakhic ruling he holds, in order to avoid an internal contradiction between rulings.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ten Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda, citing Rav, rules: {\i halacha ke\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- the halakha follows Rabbi Elazar, meaning a {\i safek mamzer} is {\i asur} and cannot marry a definite {\i mamzer} or a {\i Yisrael}. This is the operative ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel\u8217's Ten\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Hillel\u8217's teaching lists ten genealogical categories that ascended from Babylonia: {\i kohanim, levi\u8217'im, yisraelim, chalalim} (a {\i kohen} who lost his priestly sanctity through prohibited union, such as the son of a {\i kohen} and a {\i grusha}), {\i geirim} (converts), {\i meshucharim} (freed slaves), {\i mamzerim, nesinim} (a group reduced to servitude in the time of Yehoshua or David, whose status as Jews is uncertain), {\i shtuki}, and {\i asufi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Groups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Hillel\u8217's statement that all these groups can intermarry ({\i v\u8217'chulan mutarin lavo zeh ba\u8217'zeh}) does not mean that every group may marry every other group \u8212- it means that one can identify overlapping sub-groupings within which marriage is permitted. A male {\i kohen} cannot marry a convert; but {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, and {\i yisrael} may marry within their own group, and {\i chalal} may marry within that group as well. {\i Yisrael}, {\i chalal}, {\i ger}, and {\i meshuchrar} may marry one another. {\i Mamzer}, {\i ger}, and {\i meshuchrar} may marry one another. And the question is where the {\i safek} categories of {\i shtuki} and {\i asufi} fit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a {\i safek mamzer} is {\i mutar} to marry a definite {\i mamzer}, then the {\i shtuki} and {\i asufi} can marry {\i mamzer}, {\i ger}, and {\i meshuchrar} \u8212- an expansive sub-group. If, however, a {\i safek mamzer} is {\i asur} even to a definite {\i mamzer}, then the only option is {\i geirim}, but to reach {\i geirim} on the list, one would have to jump over {\i mamzer} and {\i ner}, which the Gemara does not contemplate. The {\i Rambam} (Hilchot Issurei Bi\u8217'ah 15:21) clarifies the underlying structure: {\i d\u8217'oraita}, a {\i safek mamzer} may actually marry into the general Jewish community, since the verse {\i lo yavo mamzer b\u8217'kehal Hashem} excludes only a {\i vadai mamzer} ({\i mamzer vaday assur lavo b\u8217'kahal v\u8217'lo safek}). Rabbinically, however, the sages enacted a higher standard for {\i yuchsin} and prohibited {\i safek mamzerim} from marrying {\i Yisrael} as well. A {\i vadai mamzer} may marry a {\i vadai mamzeret}. But a {\i safek mamzer, shtuki}, or {\i asufi} is forbidden to marry a {\i bas Yisrael} and, the {\i Rambam} rules, {\i v\u8217'assur lisa mamzeres, v\u8217'afilu mamzeres mi-safek assura lo} \u8212- even a {\i mamzeret misafek} is forbidden, lest one be a {\i mamzer vaday} and the other not. {\i V\u8217'ein li-sfekos elu takanah} \u8212- there is no remedy for such persons, except marriage to {\i geirim}. And even then, {\i vehavlad holech achar hapgam} \u8212- the child carries the same compromised status as the parent, so the problem is never fully resolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Eliezer\u8217's Teachings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 World Filled Mamzerim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye introduces a proof for his reading of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov. He cites a {\i baraita}: {\i mina amina lei dechol safek le-Rebbi Elazar ben Yaakov kevadai mashvei lei} \u8212- from where do I derive that Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov treats every {\i safek} as a {\i vadai}? From the following teaching: {\i harey sheba\u8217'al nashim harbeh, ve-eino yode\u8217'a eyzeh mehen ba\u8217'al} \u8212- a man had relations with many women and does not know which one he had relations with; and similarly, a woman had relations with many men and does not know from whom she conceived. {\i Nimtsa av nosei et bito ve-ach nosei et achoto} \u8212- the result is that a father might unknowingly marry his daughter, or a brother his sister. {\i Nimleh kol ha-olam kulo mamzerim} \u8212- the world would be filled with {\i mamzerim}. {\i Ve-al zeh ne\u8217'emar, umalah ha-arets zimah} \u8212- and concerning this the verse says, \u8220"the land will be filled with {\i zimah}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching is important on its own terms. It touches on the question of whether casual or promiscuous sexual relations outside marriage \u8212- even where neither party is formally married and thus no {\i mamzer} status would technically result from adultery \u8212- are nonetheless forbidden. The {\i Rambam} holds that any sexual union outside the context of marriage falls under the prohibition of {\i kedeshah}. Even for those who permit a {\i pilegesh} relationship in the context of commitment, it is clear from this passage that casual, indiscriminate sexual activity is the source of the prohibition {\i umalah ha-arets zimah} \u8212- the concern that the world will be filled with prohibited relationships, since no one knows who is related to whom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But for our purposes, Abaye draws his proof from the phrasing: Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov calls these children {\i mamzerim} even though technically they are {\i safek mamzerim}. This, says Abaye, demonstrates that Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov treats {\i safek} status as {\i vadai} for halakhic purposes \u8212- which in context means a {\i leniency}, since a {\i vadai mamzer} may marry a {\i mamzeret}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disputes this reading. He says: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- when Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov says the world will be filled with {\i mamzerim}, he does not mean they will be halakhically categorized as {\i vadai mamzerim}. Rather, {\i zimah} in that verse is to be read as a {\i roshei teivos} ({\i ro\u8217'shei teivos}): {\i zo ma hi} \u8212- \u8220"who is this person?\u8221" \u8212- meaning the world will be filled with people of unknown parentage, genuine {\i safek mamzerim} whose identity is uncertain. There is no halakhic assignment of {\i vadai} status; there is only a description of a world in which doubt and uncertainty pervade.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical halakhic ruling is stringent: we rule that a {\i safek mamzer} is {\i assur bisafek, assur b\u8217'mamzeret, ve-assur b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- prohibited on all sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Countries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues with additional teachings of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov. {\i Lo yisa adam isha mi-medinah zu v\u8217'yelech v\u8217'yisa isha mi-medinah acheres} \u8212- a man should not marry a woman in one country and then go and marry a woman in another country. {\i Shema yizdavgu zeh l\u8217'zeh} \u8212- in an era without telecommunications or systematic record-keeping, the children of the two wives may one day meet and, with no awareness that they share a father, a brother may marry a sister. {\i Ve-nimtza ach nosei et achoto} \u8212- the result would be incest. The problem is not limited to illegitimate unions; it arises even from legitimate marriages when children are raised in ignorance of their full family structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Temp Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might object that the great Amoraim themselves seem to have practiced something like this. When Rav would travel to Darshier, he would announce: {\i man havya lei leyoma} \u8212- who wishes to be my wife for the day? Similarly, Rav Nachman, when he came to Shnichziv, would make the same announcement. These were not permanent unions but temporary marriages contracted for the duration of a visit. Did they not risk exactly the kind of confusion Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov warned against?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i shani rabbanan de-faki\u8217'a shemayhu} \u8212- great Torah scholars are so famous that their travels and temporary arrangements are publicly known. A child born of such a union would know: my mother was Rav\u8217's wife during his visit to this town. There would be no mystery about paternity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question arises from Rava\u8217's ruling that {\i tevu\u8217'a linasei} \u8212- if a woman\u8217's {\i chatan} suddenly advanced the date of {\i nisu\u8217'in} without her prior expectation \u8212- {\i ve-nifaysa}, and she agreed \u8212- {\i tzrikha leishev shiva neki\u8217'im} \u8212- she must count seven clean days and go to the {\i mikvah}. The Gemara\u8217's assumption is that the sudden prospect of imminent intimacy may cause a woman to experience discharge sufficient to render her a {\i niddah}. If that is the halakha, how could Rav and Rav Nachman arrange same-day marriages?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers two answers. First, {\i rabbanan shluchei havu meshaderu u-modu lehu} \u8212- they would send advance notice, giving the woman adequate time to prepare and immerse, so that there would be a seven-day lead time. Second, {\i ve-iba\u8217'is eima, le-rabbanan yichuda be-alma hu de-meyachadi lehu} \u8212- alternatively, these arrangements involved {\i yichud} alone, not actual cohabitation. The Gemara then cites the principle {\i eino domeh mi she-yesh lo pas be-salo le-mi she-ein lo pas be-salo} \u8212- it is not the same to have bread in one\u8217's basket as to have none; the mere availability and closeness of a woman addresses the scholar\u8217's impulse even without consummation. The Rishonim offer various explanations for how {\i yichud} in this context could be permitted and how it might differ from the {\i tevu\u8217'a} scenario \u8212- some suggesting that a gradual, non-sudden transition to intimacy (as in {\i yichud} without an explicit announcement of imminent relations) would not cause the physiological response that triggers the concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Don\u8217't Plan Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final teaching of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov: {\i lo yisa adam et isha v\u8217'daato l\u8217'garsha} \u8212- a man should not marry a woman \u8212- or remain married to and cohabit with a woman \u8212- while planning to divorce her. {\i Mishum shene\u8217'emar: al tachrosh al re\u8217'acha ra\u8217'a v\u8217'hu yoshev lavetach itach} \u8212- as it is said, \u8220"Do not plot evil against your neighbor while he dwells in security with you.\u8221" The treachery of a man who continues to enjoy the benefits of marriage \u8212- the companionship, the intimacy, the shared life \u8212- while privately planning to end it, without any transparency toward his wife, is a fundamental betrayal of trust. The verse addresses the corruption of such deceptive planning and demands that one not weaponize another person\u8217's confidence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Inheritance Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek vs Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the property implications of the uncertain paternity case. The child of unknown paternity \u8212- let us call him Gershon \u8212- and the {\i yavam} (let us call him Shimon) come to divide the estate of the deceased first husband (Reuven). Gershon claims: {\i ana bar mitna hu, v\u8217'nichsei didi hu} \u8212- I am Reuven\u8217's son, and the entire estate is mine. Shimon the {\i yavam} claims: {\i at beri didi at, v\u8217'leit lach v\u8217'lo midi b\u8217'nichsei} \u8212- you are my son, and you have no share in Reuven\u8217's estate. Both claims are entirely dependent on the unresolvable question of paternity; neither party has {\i chazakah} \u8212- prior possession \u8212- of the property, since Reuven held it and both Gershon and Shimon are claiming from Reuven\u8217's estate equally. This is {\i mamon hamutal b\u8217'safek} \u8212- money in doubtful ownership \u8212- and the rule is {\i cholkin}, they divide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek vs Sons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second scenario: suppose Shimon himself has since died, leaving his own sons \u8212- Kehat and Merari. Now Gershon comes to divide Reuven\u8217's estate not with Shimon but with Shimon\u8217's sons. Gershon claims the entire estate as Reuven\u8217's son; Shimon\u8217's sons claim that Gershon is their brother, Shimon inherited Reuven\u8217's estate through valid {\i yibbum}, Shimon subsequently died, and therefore the three of them \u8212- Gershon, Kehat, and Merari \u8212- should divide it equally, with each getting a third.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis before Rav Mesharsheya thought to apply the principle of a different Mishnah: {\i hu eino yoresh otam v\u8217'hem yorshim oto} \u8212- he does not inherit them but they inherit him. That Mishnah involves a child of unknown paternity \u8212- say, the son of either Yehuda or Barak \u8212- who comes to claim a portion of Yehuda\u8217's estate. Yehuda\u8217's children say: you are Barak\u8217's son, go claim from him. He goes to Barak\u8217's children and they say: you are Yehuda\u8217's son, go claim from him. Each party deflects him to the other, {\i ayti ra\u8217'aya u\u8217'shkol} \u8212- bring proof and collect. Since he cannot prove his case, he collects nothing from either side. But when he dies, both families inherit from him, since each is a possible sibling. By analogy, the Rabbis suggested, Gershon should likewise get nothing \u8212- they will each tell him to prove his case, and he cannot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Parties Doubtful\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharsheya rejects this comparison: {\i mi dami?} \u8212- the cases are not comparable. {\i Hatam inhu vaday v\u8217'ihu safek} \u8212- in the Mishnah case, each family he approaches has a definite, unquestionable claim as legitimate children of their father, and he alone has the uncertain claim. They are {\i vaday}; he is {\i safek}. They can therefore say: {\i ayti ra\u8217'aya} \u8212- you want from us, prove you belong. {\i Hacha idi v\u8217'idi safek} \u8212- here, however, all parties are in some sense doubtful. Gershon\u8217's claim is uncertain, but so is the claim of Shimon\u8217's sons, since they can only inherit Reuven\u8217's estate through Shimon\u8217's {\i yibbum}, and that {\i yibbum} is only valid if Gershon is not Reuven\u8217's son. Rav Mesharsheya adds \u8212- and Rashi amplifies this \u8212- that Gershon\u8217's claim, even if he is certain to have some stake in the property, rests on an uncertain basis: he cannot say whether he holds it as Reuven\u8217's son or as Shimon\u8217's son. The entirety of his claim is without a definite legal foundation, unlike the {\i vaday} claimants in the Mishnah case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharsheya then notes what the present scenario actually does resemble: {\i l\u8217'safek u\u8217'vnei yavam sheba\u8217'u lachloke b\u8217'nichsei yavam gufei} \u8212- it resembles a case where Gershon and Shimon\u8217's sons come to divide {\i Shimon}\u8217's estate, not Reuven\u8217's. There, Gershon claims to be their brother and demands a portion of their father Shimon\u8217's estate. They respond: {\i ayti ra\u8217'aya de\u8217'achuna at u\u8217'shkol} \u8212- prove you are our brother and collect. Since Gershon cannot prove paternity, he gets nothing from Shimon\u8217's estate, exactly parallel to the Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the original question \u8212- Gershon and Shimon\u8217's sons dividing Reuven\u8217's estate \u8212- Rashi proposes the following resolution. {\i Venireh be\u8217'einai, dehu mantsa dekamodu leh shakil} \u8212- the portion that all parties concede Gershon is entitled to, he takes outright. Since both Gershon and Shimon\u8217's sons would concede that Gershon holds at least a one-third share (Shimon\u8217's sons\u8217' own claim is that Gershon gets a third as a co-heir; Gershon claims the whole), that one-third is undisputed and goes directly to Gershon. {\i Ve\u8217'idach havei mamon hamutal besafek vecholkin} \u8212- the remaining two-thirds are in genuine dispute: Gershon claims all of it, Shimon\u8217's sons claim all of it as their father\u8217's inheritance. That disputed portion is divided equally, with Gershon receiving half and Shimon\u8217's sons receiving half between them. The practical result: Gershon receives the undisputed third plus half of the disputed two-thirds, giving him two-thirds of the total estate, while Shimon\u8217's sons together receive the remaining third.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 38\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Veyavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gershon vs.\u160 ?Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens at the top of 38a with the case of {\i safek veyavam} \u8212- a doubtful son and a {\i yavam} \u8212- who come to divide the inheritance of their grandfather Yaakov. The scenario is as follows. Yaakov owns a field. Gershon claims to be the son of Reuven, who has died, and on that basis demands half the field. Shimon, the {\i yavam}, is still alive, and his position is straightforward: Gershon is my son, not Reuven\u8217's, and therefore you have no claim to anything while I am living \u8212- the entire field is mine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames the competing claims in its own terms. Gershon declares, {\i hai gavra bar mitna hu u-palga didi hu} \u8212- I am the son of the deceased and half the field is mine. Shimon responds, {\i at bera didi at v-lo shayech lach v-lo midi} \u8212- you are my son and you have no portion in that field whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Motzi Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with a clean application of the principle {\i ein safek motzi mi-yedei vadai} \u8212- a doubtful claim cannot displace a certain one. Shimon has a definite claim: he is unquestionably an heir to this field, whether he takes half or the whole, his status as heir is certain. Gershon\u8217's claim, whatever he may assert with confidence, is in reality a {\i safek} \u8212- he is possibly an heir. Since one party has a definite standing and the other only a doubtful one, we apply {\i ha-motzi me-chaveiro alav ha-re\u8217'aya} and Shimon takes the entire field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Way Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then proceeds to {\i safek u-vnei yavam sheba\u8217'u lachlok bi-nichsei sabba} \u8212- the case where Shimon has died, and now Gershon is disputing with Shimon\u8217's sons Kehat and Merari over Yaakov\u8217's field. Gershon again claims to be Reuven\u8217's son and demands half. Kehat and Merari counter: you are our brother, {\i achuna}, and you take an equal share alongside us.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concessions Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the analysis turns on mutual concessions. Gershon concedes that the other half belongs to Kehat and Merari \u8212- his claim is only to his half. They in turn concede a third of the entire field to him \u8212- they acknowledge he is at least a brother entitled to a third. So each side has conceded something. Half and a third together account for five sixths of the field, leaving one sixth in dispute. About this remaining sixth, no one has a stronger claim than the other, and the rule is {\i havei mamon hamutal b\u8217'safek v-cholkin} \u8212- when money rests in genuine doubt, we divide it. So the contested sixth is split, giving Kehat and Merari together a half plus a twelfth \u8212- that is, seven twelfths \u8212- while Gershon receives a third plus a twelfth, which is five twelfths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yaakov & Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers the inverse scenario: {\i sabba v-yavam b-nichsei safek} \u8212- what happens when Gershon himself dies. Now Yaakov the grandfather and Shimon the {\i yavam} are both alive and both want to inherit Gershon\u8217's estate. Recall that Gershon had received half of Reuven\u8217's property. Shimon argues that Gershon was like his son and he therefore inherits from him. Yaakov argues that Gershon was Reuven\u8217's son, and since Reuven is gone, the inheritance flows back up to him. Neither claim is stronger than the other. And the parallel case \u8212- {\i sabba v-safek b-nichsei yavam} \u8212- arises when Shimon dies without Kehat and Merari yet in the picture: Gershon claims Shimon was his father, while Yaakov claims Reuven was Gershon\u8217's father and that Shimon\u8217's estate should come to him. In both configurations the {\i gemara} concludes: {\i havei mamon hamutal b\u8217'safek v-cholkin} \u8212- each side presents a claim without decisive advantage, and the property is divided.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shomeret Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nafal Nechasim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the {\i mishna} on {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- a woman waiting for {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} after her husband Reuven has died, while Shimon stands as the {\i yavam}. The question is: if she inherits property during this period of waiting \u8212- {\i naflo lah nechasim} \u8212- what is the {\i halacha} governing that property?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nechsei Milog\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the stakes, one must recall the framework of {\i nechsei milog}: property to which a wife holds title, while the husband enjoys the {\i peirot}, the usufruct. The principle of {\i kinyan peirot k\u8217'kinyan haguf} \u8212- that the right to the fruit is treated like ownership of the body of the asset itself \u8212- means that a husband\u8217's right to {\i peirot} is substantial. The question for the {\i shomeret yavam} is whether the {\i zika}, the bond tying her to Shimon, is strong enough to confer upon him the rights of a husband over her property. If the {\i zika} effectively makes her like his wife, he would be entitled to the {\i peirot}. If it does not, the property remains fully in her domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buying & Selling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens with a ruling on which Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel agree: {\i modim Beit Shammai u-Veit Hillel she-mocheres u-nosenet v-kayam} \u8212- she may sell, give gifts, and transact with the property, and it all stands. This is itself a {\i chiddush}: not only does Shimon have no claim to the {\i peirot}, he lacks even the standing to block her sale of the principal. In this state, Shimon is entirely out of the picture \u8212- she holds the property freely, and the {\i zika} confers upon him no effective right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Upon Her Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then asks: {\i meisa \u8212- ma ya\u8217'aseh bi-kesuvatah u-vi-nechasim ha-nichnasim v-yotzim imah} \u8212- if she dies, what becomes of her {\i kesubah} and the property she carried in and out? Two competing claimants emerge. Lavan, her father, argues that the property was hers, she died without heirs in the relevant sense, and it therefore reverts to him. Shimon argues that she was in some sense his wife, and though he may not have had rights to the property while she was alive, upon her death he ought to inherit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kesubah} requires a word of clarification. When Reuven died, Rachel became entitled to collect her two hundred {\i zuz}. However, if Shimon performs {\i yibbum}, the marriage to Reuven is treated as continuing \u8212- it is not a new marriage \u8212- and she does not collect. The {\i kesubah} becomes collectable only if Shimon performs {\i chalitzah}, releasing her from the bond. So the question of who controls the uncollected {\i kesubah} is bound up with Shimon\u8217's ambiguous standing: if Shimon stands in Reuven\u8217's place, the {\i kesubah} has not yet been released and remains within Shimon\u8217's estate; if the {\i zika} relationship is not considered a genuine marriage, her claim against it is doubtful. One might also observe \u8212- and this point was raised in the course of study \u8212- that since this is a continuation of her {\i kesubah} against Reuven rather than a fresh obligation from Shimon, the amount remains two hundred {\i zuz} rather than one hundred, reflecting that this is not treated as the {\i kesubah} of a widow marrying anew, but as the original obligation carrying forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Divide\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai rules {\i yachloku yoresh ha-ba\u8217'al v-yoresh ha-av} \u8212- the husband\u8217's heirs and the father\u8217's heirs divide. Beit Hillel rules {\i nechasim b\u8217'chezkasan} \u8212- the property remains in its {\i chazakah}, its presumptive status. The underlying conceptual question is whether we apply {\i mamon hamutal b\u8217'safek cholkin} \u8212- disputed assets are split \u8212- or whether we follow {\i chazakah} and leave things where they stood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel elaborates: the {\i kesubah} is {\i b\u8217'chezkat yorshei ha-ba\u8217'al} \u8212- it was never collected, it remained under Shimon\u8217's control, so it stays there. The {\i nechasim ha-nichnasim v-yotzim imah} \u8212- the property she carried \u8212- is {\i b\u8217'chezkat yorshei ha-av}, for it never entered Shimon\u8217's domain. She received it, held it, and controlled it throughout; it rightfully passes to her heirs. Beit Hillel\u8217's position is thus internally coherent: follow the {\i chazakah} in each case, and each category of property flows to the party who definitively held it. Beit Shammai, by contrast, holds that since Shimon\u8217's status is genuinely uncertain \u8212- he may or may not be treated as a husband \u8212- we treat this as a {\i safek} and divide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusa vs.\u160 ?Nesua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: {\i mai shna reisha de-lo paligei u-mai shna seifa de-paligei} \u8212- why does Beit Shammai agree completely in the first case, where she is alive and in full control, yet contest the property in the second case after she dies? If Shimon has a doubtful claim as quasi-husband, why does that claim not surface while she is alive?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla answers by introducing a distinction not stated explicitly in the {\i mishna}: {\i reisha d\u8217'nafla k\u8217'shehi arusa} \u8212- the first case is where she fell to {\i yibbum} having been an {\i arusa} to Reuven; {\i seifa d\u8217'nafla k\u8217'shehi nesua} \u8212- the second case is where she had been a {\i nesua}. Ulla holds {\i zikas arusa osa safek arusa} \u8212- the {\i zika} that derives from an {\i eirusin} relationship produces a doubtful {\i arusa} status for Shimon \u8212- and {\i zikas nesua osa safek nesua} \u8212- the {\i zika} that derives from a {\i nisuin} relationship produces a doubtful {\i nesua} status. Since a husband in an {\i eirusin} has no right to {\i peirot} in the first place, even a certain {\i eirusin} status would give Shimon no claim to the property. Hence in the {\i arusa} case, Beit Shammai fully concedes that she controls the property. But in the {\i nesua} case, the transferred {\i zika} creates at least a doubtful {\i nesua} bond, and upon her death Shimon can press a claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} supplies the proofs. How do we know {\i zikas arusa osa safek arusa} and not {\i vadai arusa}? Because a {\i mishna} elsewhere teaches that if a woman received property {\i after} she became a regular (non-{\i yibbum}) {\i arusa}, Beit Hillel holds she may not sell it {\i l\u8217'chatchila}, even though {\i b\u8217'dieved} the sale stands. Yet here, the {\i shomeret yavam} may sell {\i l\u8217'chatchila}. This can only be because her {\i arusa} status through {\i zika} is merely a {\i safek}, and the weaker doubtful status does not even prohibit her from selling outright. Similarly, how do we know {\i zikas nesua osa safek nesua} and not {\i vadai nesua}? Because a {\i mishna} elsewhere teaches that if a woman received property after she became a {\i nesua}, both Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel agree that if she sold it the husband can reclaim it from the buyers \u8212- the husband is fully in control. Yet here, when she dies, Beit Shammai says only to split the property, not to award it entirely to Shimon. This confirms his status is only a {\i safek nesua}, not {\i vadai}: a {\i vadai} husband would take everything; since he takes only half, the {\i zika} must have produced only {\i safek nesua} standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges Ulla\u8217's construction directly: {\i atu mafalgi b\u8217'gufa v-le\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- is the {\i mishna} really discussing the body of the property and what happens after her death merely as a vehicle for distinguishing {\i arusa} from {\i nesua}? If that were the operative distinction, {\i liflug b\u8217'chayeha u-l\u8217'peirot} \u8212- the {\i mishna} should simply have discussed what happens to the {\i peirot} while she is alive, and distinguished between the two relationships there. Why does the {\i mishna} shift entirely to a case where she has died? Ulla\u8217's reading requires us to say that the difference between case one and case two has nothing to do with whether she is alive or dead \u8212- but the {\i mishna} plainly presents that as the pivotal distinction. Rava\u8217's objection is that you cannot explain away the {\i mishna}\u8217's own structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Title vs.\u160 ?Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore offers his own resolution: {\i eidi v\u8217'eidi d\u8217'nafla k\u8217'shehi nesua} \u8212- both cases of the {\i mishna} concern a woman who had been a {\i nesua} to Reuven. In both cases, {\i zikas nesua osa safek nesua}, and Shimon has a doubtful {\i nesua} claim throughout. So why does Beit Shammai concede that she controls the property while alive?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is elegant and turns on a precise analysis of what is being claimed in each scenario. When she is alive: {\i reisha d\u8217'hee kayma, havya lah hee vadai v\u8217'ihu safek} \u8212- she is a {\i vadai} and he is a {\i safek}. She is the definite holder of {\i title} to the property \u8212- that is unquestioned even in {\i nechsei milog}. Shimon\u8217's claim is only to the {\i peirot}, the usufruct, and even that claim is doubtful because it depends on whether the {\i zika} is treated as a genuine {\i nisuin}. She says: I definitively own the land; you have a doubtful claim to the fruit. {\i V\u8217'ein safek motzi mi-yedei vadai} \u8212- a doubtful claim cannot displace a certain one. Therefore, Beit Shammai fully concedes while she is alive: her {\i vadai} title overrides his {\i safek} claim to {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When she dies, the situation fundamentally changes. Now the debate is no longer about {\i peirot} but about the {\i title} itself \u8212- who inherits the principal. At that point, both Shimon and Lavan are coming to claim ownership. She is no longer present to assert her {\i vadai} title. Both claimants arrive with uncertain standing: Shimon may have been her husband, Lavan is her father. This is {\i mamon hamutal b\u8217'safek}, and Beit Shammai says {\i yachloku} \u8212- divide it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye presses a challenge: {\i u-l\u8217'Veit Shammai ein safek motzi mi-yedei vadai}? Does Beit Shammai truly hold that a {\i safek} cannot displace a {\i vadai}? The following {\i mishna} seems to present a counterexample.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Collapse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nafal ha-bayit alav v\u8217'al aviv} \u8212- a house collapsed on Reuven and his father Yaakov simultaneously, and it is unknown who died first. Reuven had creditors \u8212- a wife\u8217's {\i kesubah} or other debts. The {\i yorshim}, the heirs, argue that Reuven died first and then Yaakov, so the property never passed through Reuven and the creditors have no lien. The {\i ba\u8217'alei chov}, the creditors, argue that Yaakov died first, Reuven inherited from Yaakov, and the creditors acquired a lien on that inherited property before Reuven himself died. Beit Shammai rules {\i yachloku} \u8212- divide the contested property. Beit Hillel rules {\i nechasim b\u8217'chezkasam} \u8212- maintain the {\i chazakah}, which in this case favors the heirs, since the property was definitively under Yaakov\u8217's control and the creditors are outside claimants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes the apparent contradiction: {\i v\u8217'hacha yorshim vadai u-ba\u8217'alei chov safek} \u8212- the heirs have definite standing and the creditors have a doubtful claim. Yet Beit Shammai says divide! If Beit Shammai holds {\i ein safek motzi mi-yedei vadai}, why does a {\i safek} creditor get to press a claim against the {\i vadai} heirs?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with an important principle: {\i kasvari Veit Shammai shtar ha\u8217'omed ligvos k\u8217'gavuy dami} \u8212- Beit Shammai holds that an outstanding {\i shtar}, a written debt instrument, is treated as though it has already been collected. This is what distinguishes the creditor case from the {\i shomeret yavam} case. Normally, one might conceptualize the creditor\u8217's situation as follows: the heirs inherit, and then the creditors come as outsiders attempting to take the property away \u8212- in which case the creditors would indeed be a {\i safek} pressing against the heirs\u8217' {\i vadai}, and {\i ein safek motzi mi-yedei vadai} would apply. But Beit Shammai does not conceptualize it that way. Because the creditors hold a {\i shtar}, their claim is treated as already realized \u8212- {\i k\u8217'gavuy dami}. When the heirs and the creditors present themselves before {\i beit din}, neither party is an outsider pressing against the other\u8217's established {\i chazakah}. Both stand at the same level, and since the facts are in doubt, you divide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, in the {\i shomeret yavam} case while she is alive, Shimon holds no {\i shtar}. He has a doubtful claim to {\i peirot}, nothing more. She holds definite title. There is no instrument that elevates his standing to equal hers, and so {\i ein safek motzi mi-yedei vadai} applies in full force \u8212- he cannot press his doubtful claim against her certain ownership.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 39\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shomeret Yavam Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rachel\u8217's Property Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 39, and we pick up in the middle of 38b. The {\i gemara} is engaged with the cases presented in the {\i mishnah}, where a woman \u8212- call her Rachel \u8212- is a {\i shomeret yavam}, waiting for her brother-in-law Shimon to either perform {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. During this period, Rachel inherits property, which functions in the manner of {\i nichsei milug}: she holds title, and were she fully married, her husband would be entitled to the {\i peirot}. The {\i mishnah} establishes that while Rachel is alive, she has full control \u8212- she may buy and sell freely \u8212- and both Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel agree to this. The dispute arises only when she dies, at which point there is a question of who inherits: Lavan, her father, or Shimon, the {\i yavam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek vs.\u160 ?Vadai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the {\i gemara} presses is this: if Beit Shammai holds that upon Rachel\u8217's death the estate is split between Shimon and Lavan \u8212- a ruling that appears to reflect Shimon\u8217's {\i safek} claim of quasi-marital status through {\i zika}, a doubtful {\i nisuin} \u8212- why does Shimon receive nothing while Rachel is alive? His potential entitlement to {\i peirot} exists regardless of whether she is alive or dead, yet during her lifetime Beit Shammai concedes entirely to her position. Why does his {\i safek} claim not entitle him to at least something?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Safek Motzi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by invoking the principle of {\i ein safek motzi midei vadai}: an uncertain claim cannot displace a certain one. While Rachel lives, she unquestionably holds title. Shimon\u8217's claim \u8212- whether or not {\i zika} gives him a quasi-marital status that would entitle him to {\i peirot} \u8212- is at most a {\i safek}. Since she is a {\i vadai} possessor of the property, even Beit Shammai concedes that his {\i safek} cannot dislodge her. When she dies, however, the question of who holds title to the estate becomes genuinely open, and that is where Beit Shammai says to divide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Collapse Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A difficulty arises, however, from another ruling associated with Beit Shammai: the case of a house collapsing on a minor and his father, where the question is whether creditors may claim a portion of the estate alongside the heirs. There, Beit Shammai appears to say that we divide despite someone being in presumptive possession \u8212- which would seem to contradict the principle just established, that a {\i safek} cannot displace a {\i vadai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Ligvot Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by explaining that Beit Shammai holds {\i shtar ha-omed ligvot k\u8217'gavuy dami} \u8212- a document whose time for collection has arrived is treated as though collection has already taken place. In that case, therefore, it is not a matter of the creditors\u8217' {\i safek} claim attempting to extract money from heirs who are {\i vadai} possessors. Because the debt instrument was ripe for collection, both parties stand in an equal posture of possession. Neither party is more {\i muchzak} than the other. The two doctrines thus operate in tandem: Beit Shammai accepts {\i ein safek motzi midei vadai} as a general principle, but carves out an exception when the {\i safek} claimant holds a mature {\i shtar}, which grants them a co-equal standing rather than the position of an outsider.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sotah & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: from where do we know that Beit Shammai holds {\i shtar ha-omed ligvot k\u8217'gavuy dami}? It cites the {\i mishnah} in the laws of {\i sotah}: a woman who was warned by her husband and secluded with another man, and who therefore must drink the {\i sotah} waters \u8212- if her husband dies before she is able to drink, what is the status of her {\i ketubah}? The {\i mishnah} records a dispute. Beit Shammai says: {\i notlot ketuvatam ve-ein shosot} \u8212- they collect their {\i ketubah} in full. Beit Hillel says: {\i o shosot o lo notlot ketuvatam} \u8212- either they drink, or they do not collect. The {\i gemara} clarifies that Beit Hillel cannot literally mean they should drink, since the verse states the husband must bring his wife to drink \u8212- and if the husband is dead, that is impossible. Rather, Beit Hillel means: {\i mitoch she-lo shosot, lo notlot ketuvatam} \u8212- since they cannot drink, they cannot collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Safek Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sotah}\u8217's status is genuinely doubtful. She may have committed adultery, or she may not have \u8212- that is precisely why the entire {\i sotah} procedure exists. Her {\i ketubah} entitlement is therefore a {\i safek}. The heirs of the deceased husband, meanwhile, are in presumptive possession of the estate. A woman coming to collect her {\i ketubah} is, in the ordinary framing, a creditor seeking to extract money from people who hold title. Beit Hillel\u8217's position is therefore perfectly consistent with {\i ein safek motzi midei vadai}: her doubtful right cannot override the heirs\u8217' certain possession, so she collects nothing. Beit Shammai\u8217's position, however, requires explanation. Why does he allow collection despite her {\i safek} status?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Proves Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shma mina shtar ha-omed ligvot k\u8217'gavuy dami}. A {\i ketubah} is a {\i shtar} \u8212- a legal instrument \u8212- and since it was ripe for collection at the moment of the husband\u8217's death, it is treated as though collection has already occurred. The {\i sotah} is not to be understood as a {\i safek} claimant reaching into the heirs\u8217' certain possession. Rather, both she and the heirs stand in an equal posture: her {\i ketubah} instrument grants her standing co-equal to the heirs, not subordinate to them. This is why Beit Shammai allows collection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further subtlety demands attention here. In the house collapse case, Beit Shammai splits the disputed property between the heirs and the creditors. But in the {\i sotah} case, Beit Shammai allows the woman to collect the {\i entire} {\i ketubah}, not merely half. Why the asymmetry?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several distinctions apply. In the house collapse case, there is a pure epistemic vacuum: no one knows in which order the deaths occurred, and neither party has any prior claim to certainty. In the {\i sotah} case, the heirs are asserting that she committed adultery, but they have no actual knowledge of this \u8212- she, by contrast, is in a position to know whether she was faithful and is asserting her entitlement from a position of personal knowledge. Beyond that, the {\i ketubah} debt pre-existed in a fully established form: at an earlier point she was unquestionably entitled to it. That certain, prior entitlement distinguishes her claim from that of a creditor in the collapse case, where one cannot say the debt obligation ever attached beyond doubt. These distinctions must be worked through on a case-by-case basis, but the governing principle is clear: when both parties hold equal standing by virtue of the {\i shtar} doctrine, it is not a case of {\i safek motzi midei vadai}, and the particulars of each situation determine whether one party collects entirely or the two divide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why House Case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: given that the {\i sotah} case also demonstrates Beit Shammai\u8217's position on {\i shtar ha-omed ligvot k\u8217'gavuy dami}, why did the {\i gemara} not derive the principle from there in the first place? Since we are already in the legal domain of women and {\i ketubot}, why was it necessary to invoke the case of a collapsing house?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chinah Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i dilma kesuvas isha shani mishum china}. One cannot necessarily generalize from the {\i ketubah} to all {\i shtarot}, because the Rabbis may have accorded special legal strength to a woman\u8217's {\i ketubah} that they would not extend to an ordinary debt instrument. The reason is {\i china} \u8212- a concept of attractiveness or desirability, reflecting the Rabbis\u8217' interest in strengthening the {\i ketubah}\u8217's enforceability in order to make marriage an attractive and secure institution for women. Since the {\i ketubah} might occupy a uniquely privileged category, one cannot draw a general rule from it about {\i shtarot} as a whole. The case of the collapsing house, by contrast, involves ordinary creditors and ordinary debt instruments, making it the proper vehicle for establishing the general principle. And there, the answer is that a ripe {\i shtar} of any kind grants co-equal standing \u8212- not merely a {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Split Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the framework of Beit Shammai\u8217's positions now established, the {\i gemara} turns to a tension internal to the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} addresses the scenario in which Rachel dies as a {\i shomeret yavam}, and it raises the question: {\i mah ya\u8217'aseh biktuvatah u-va-nekhasim ha-nikhnasim ve-ha-yotzim imah} \u8212- what should be done with her {\i ketubah} and with her property? The focus of the preceding discussion has been on the {\i nekhasim} \u8212- the {\i nichsei milug} \u8212- and the dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel over how to divide that estate. But what about the {\i ketubah} \u8212- the 200 {\i zuz} Rachel held against Reuven, which by operation of {\i yibum} now effectively rests against Shimon\u8217's estate? The {\i mishnah} appears to say that Beit Shammai divides everything down the middle. Yet if Shimon is clearly {\i muchzak} in the estate he inherited from Reuven, why would Beit Shammai say that a {\i safek} claim to the {\i ketubah} can extract any portion from him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves this by offering a bold re-reading of the {\i mishnah}: {\i lo palig} \u8212- Beit Shammai does not in fact disagree regarding the {\i ketubah}. Beit Shammai concedes that Shimon retains the {\i ketubah} money in full. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's question \u8212- {\i mah ya\u8217'aseh biktuvatah} \u8212- is not answered by the subsequent ruling; it stands as an unanswered question, followed by a period. What follows \u8212- {\i nekhasim ha-nikhnasim ve-ha-yotzim imah} \u8212- is a separate statement about the property alone, and it is only regarding the property that Beit Shammai says {\i yachloku}. The {\i mishnah}, on this reading, rules explicitly only on the {\i nekhasim}, leaving the {\i ketubah} without a stated ruling from Beit Shammai \u8212- because Beit Shammai concedes the {\i ketubah} to Shimon entirely. This is admittedly a strained reading of the {\i mishnah}, and the {\i gemara} acknowledges as much, but it then attempts to find support from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's own language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi observes that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language can itself be read as supporting this interpretation. The {\i mishnah} says {\i yachloku yorshei ha-ba\u8217'al im yorshei ha-av} \u8212- the heirs of the husband come and divide with the heirs of the father. The framing is that the husband\u8217's heirs \u8212- Shimon\u8217's side \u8212- are the ones who come to divide {\i with} the father\u8217's heirs. This implies that the father\u8217's family is already in possession, and Shimon is entitled to come and take his portion from them. That formulation naturally describes the {\i nekhasim}, where the father\u8217's family holds the default position. Had the {\i mishnah} also intended to address the {\i ketubah}, the language would have been reversed: the father\u8217's heirs come and take their portion from the husband\u8217's estate. Since the language is oriented in only one direction, it appears that only the {\i nekhasim} are being discussed, and the {\i ketubah} was never part of Beit Shammai\u8217's ruling of {\i yachloku} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion Admits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of Rava\u8217's analysis is that Beit Shammai is in full agreement with {\i ein safek motzi midei vadai} across the board. Beit Shammai concedes that when Rachel is alive, her certain possession of the {\i nichsei milug} cannot be overridden by Shimon\u8217's {\i safek} claim to {\i peirot}. And Beit Shammai likewise concedes that when Rachel dies, Shimon\u8217's certain possession of the estate cannot be disturbed by a {\i safek} claim to the {\i ketubah} on behalf of her heirs. The dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel concerns only the {\i nichsei milug} upon Rachel\u8217's death, where the question of title itself becomes genuinely uncertain \u8212- and there Beit Shammai says to divide, because at that point neither side is more {\i vadai} than the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Inherited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers an alternative explanation of the {\i mishnah} that shifts the axis of analysis entirely. Rather than locating the critical variable in whether Rachel is alive or dead, Abaye proposes that the real distinction is {\i when} she came to own the property. The {\i reisha} of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the case in which Beit Shammai agrees that Rachel retains full control \u8212- concerns property that fell to Rachel {\i after} Reuven had already died, that is, while she was already a {\i shomeret yavam}. The {\i seifa} \u8212- the case in which Beit Shammai says to divide \u8212- concerns property that fell to Rachel {\i while she was still married to Reuven}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Strength\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction carries significant legal weight. When Rachel inherits property after Reuven\u8217's death, there is no real husband in the picture. Her {\i zika} to Shimon is at most a quasi-relationship. In this posture, Rachel establishes herself as the undisputed, uncompromised owner of the property. Her title is clean. Shimon\u8217's quasi-claim through {\i zika} is not strong enough to cloud what is, in effect, sole ownership. Beit Shammai therefore concedes she retains full control \u8212- while she lives, and presumably also in terms of her family\u8217's inheritance claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When, however, Rachel acquired the property while she was still married to Reuven, the situation is fundamentally different. At that moment, Reuven \u8212- as her real, full husband \u8212- had a recognized and substantial position in the property by virtue of his entitlement to {\i peirot}, which carries a quasi-proprietary character ({\i k\u8217'kinyan haguf}). Her title was never clean in the way it is in the first case; from the moment of acquisition, she and Reuven stood in a kind of co-ownership. When Reuven dies and Shimon steps in through {\i zika}, Shimon steps into a relationship with property that never belonged exclusively to Rachel. Since she never fully established herself as sole owner, Shimon\u8217's position \u8212- though weaker than Reuven\u8217's had been \u8212- is sufficient to generate a genuine dispute, and Beit Shammai therefore says to divide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-Equal Partner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A key question in Abaye\u8217's framework is precisely how to characterize the shift from Reuven\u8217's position to Shimon\u8217's. If Reuven had a strong position in the property \u8212- and his entitlement to {\i peirot} gives him at least a quasi-proprietary claim \u8212- and Shimon\u8217's position through {\i zika} is admittedly weaker, then why does Shimon\u8217's weaker position suffice to generate a {\i yachloku}? Abaye\u8217's answer is that while Shimon\u8217's claim is indeed weaker than Reuven\u8217's was, the key point is that Rachel\u8217's title had already been compromised before Reuven\u8217's death. She never fully owned the property in an unencumbered sense. When Reuven leaves the picture and Shimon enters at a somewhat lower level of entitlement, the result is that Shimon\u8217's claim and Rachel\u8217's claim are now roughly co-equal \u8212- neither clearly superior \u8212- which is precisely the condition under which Beit Shammai says {\i yachloku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel disagrees with this analysis. Beit Hillel holds that Reuven\u8217's position in the property, though significant, was co-equal to Rachel\u8217's rather than superior. Under that view, when Reuven dies and Shimon steps in at the lower level of {\i zika}, Rachel\u8217's title effectively reasserts itself as the dominant one, and it is her heirs \u8212- Lavan\u8217's family \u8212- who receive the property. The dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel, on Abaye\u8217's reading, thus turns on whether a husband\u8217's position in his wife\u8217's {\i nichsei milug} is stronger than, or merely equal to, hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yadav Keyidah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's formulation of Beit Hillel\u8217's position invokes the phrase {\i yado k\u8217'yidah} \u8212- his hand is as her hand, meaning the husband and wife occupy equal positions in relation to the property. It is precisely because Beit Hillel holds {\i yado k\u8217'yidah} that they do not say {\i yachloku} in the {\i seifa}. When Reuven \u8212- who was merely co-equal \u8212- exits the picture, and Shimon \u8212- who is at the still-lower level of {\i zika} \u8212- enters, Rachel\u8217's position is now unambiguously superior, and Beit Hillel therefore awards the property to her family.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Rejoinder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Stronger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now engages with Abaye\u8217's framework, accepting the basic structural insight \u8212- that the timing of Rachel\u8217's acquisition is the relevant variable \u8212- but disagreeing sharply on the characterization of the husband\u8217's position. Rava holds that there is no debate: {\i dechulei alma lo pligi deyado adifa miyidah} \u8212- everyone agrees that the husband\u8217's position in {\i nichsei milug} property is stronger than the wife\u8217's, not merely equal. The husband\u8217's entitlement to {\i peirot}, understood at the level of {\i kinyan ha-guf} or reinforced by rabbinic enactment, makes him the senior property partner. This is not a disputed premise; it is a baseline legal reality that even Beit Hillel would accept.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this is true, then Abaye\u8217's proposed distinction between the two cases \u8212- one where she inherited while a {\i shomeret yavam} versus one where she inherited while fully married \u8212- would actually yield the wrong result for Beit Hillel. If the husband was the senior partner and Shimon steps in at a co-equal level, even Beit Hillel ought to say {\i yachloku}. There would be no case in which Beit Hillel grants Rachel\u8217's family the full property under Abaye\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore proposes a different variable to distinguish the {\i reisha} from the {\i seifa}: whether or not Shimon performed {\i maamar} with Rachel. The {\i reisha} \u8212- where Beit Shammai concedes that Rachel retains full control, and by extension that Lavan retains the estate upon her death \u8212- is the case in which no {\i maamar} was performed. In this scenario, Shimon\u8217's connection to Rachel remains at the level of {\i zika} alone \u8212- a purely halakhic, quasi-marital bond that is real but limited. Beit Shammai concedes that this is insufficient to generate a competing claim to the property while Rachel lives, and that upon her death her family takes the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i seifa} \u8212- where Beit Shammai says {\i yachloku} \u8212- is the case in which Shimon did perform {\i maamar}. The {\i maamar} introduces a new and significant factor, elevating Shimon\u8217's relationship with Rachel beyond the baseline {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rava, {\i maamar} for Beit Shammai accomplishes the following: {\i oseh vaday arusah ve-safek nisu\u8217'ah} \u8212- it renders Rachel a definite {\i arusah} and a doubtful {\i nisu\u8217'ah} vis-\u224 ?-vis Shimon. The status of definite {\i arusah} has implications for {\i erva} and {\i tzarat erva} cases \u8212- it is enough to push away co-wives in the context of {\i yibum} eligibility. But the status of {\i safek nisu\u8217'ah} has implications for property: as a doubtful {\i nisu\u8217'ah}, Shimon acquires a {\i safek} claim to the {\i nichsei milug}. When Rachel then dies, her family\u8217's claim to the property is a {\i vadai} in one sense, but Shimon\u8217's {\i safek nisu\u8217'ah} status generates a competing claim that is not simply a {\i safek} coming to displace a {\i vadai} from the outside. Rather, both claims are now of a character that justifies division \u8212- and Beit Shammai accordingly says {\i yachloku}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maamar Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the two {\i amoraim} of the earlier generation map onto the positions of Rava and Abaye: the tradition in the name of Rabbi Elazar follows Rava\u8217's position, while the tradition in the name of Rabbi Yose bar Rabbi Chanina follows Abaye\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A challenge is immediately raised against the attribution to Rabbi Elazar: did Rabbi Elazar not himself say that {\i maamar} for Beit Shammai only operates {\i lidchos b\u8217'tzarah bilvad} \u8212- that its sole halakhic effect is to push away co-wives in the context of {\i erva} and {\i tzarat erva}, implying that it has no relevance to property claims? If so, how can Rabbi Elazar be aligned with Rava, who holds that {\i maamar} generates a {\i safek nisu\u8217'ah} status with direct property implications?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this apparent contradiction by clarifying what Rabbi Elazar\u8217's statement actually meant. Rabbi Elazar can maintain that {\i maamar} creates both an {\i arusa} status and a {\i safek nisu\u8217'ah} status \u8212- including for property purposes \u8212- consistent with Rava\u8217's position. What his earlier statement was saying is something different: that {\i maamar} does not fully replace the woman\u8217's status as a {\i yevamah}. Even after {\i maamar}, she remains in some sense a {\i shomeret yavam}, and therefore if Shimon subsequently wishes to separate from her, a {\i get} alone is insufficient \u8212- {\i chalitzah} is also required. The {\i chiddush} of Beit Shammai on Rabbi Elazar\u8217's account is not that {\i maamar} is legally weak, but rather that it adds a layer of status without eliminating the pre-existing {\i yevamah} relationship. Shimon has done {\i kiddushin} through {\i maamar}, and she may even acquire a quasi-{\i nisu\u8217'ah} status for monetary purposes \u8212- but since {\i yibum} was never performed, the {\i yevamah} relationship remains simultaneously in force, and {\i chalitzah} is still needed to sever it. Both statuses coexist; {\i maamar} adds without displacing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa now offers linguistic support from the {\i mishnah} itself for Abaye\u8217's reading. The {\i mishnah} refers to the disputed property as {\i nekhasim ha-nikhnasim ve-ha-yotzim imah} \u8212- property that \u8220"comes in and goes out with her.\u8221" This phrase demands explanation: where is this property going? Rav Papa argues that it must mean: property that entered into the domain of the husband \u8212- {\i nikhnasim le-reshut ha-ba\u8217'al} \u8212- and went out with her from the husband\u8217's domain \u8212- {\i yotzim mi-reshut ha-ba\u8217'al le-reshut ha-av} \u8212- at his death. In other words, the {\i mishnah} is describing property that Rachel acquired {\i while she was married to Reuven}, such that it entered into the marital estate and then reverted when Reuven died. This phrasing appears only in the {\i seifa} of the {\i mishnah}, and Rav Papa argues that it therefore indicates that the {\i seifa} is specifically discussing property acquired during the marriage to Reuven \u8212- precisely the scenario Abaye identified as the relevant distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Emphasis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This support is real, but a difficulty remains. The plain reading of the {\i mishnah} structures its two cases around whether Rachel is alive or dead: in the {\i reisha} she is alive, in the {\i seifa} she is dead and they are dividing the estate. If, as Abaye holds, the real distinction is when she acquired the property rather than whether she is alive or dead, why does the {\i mishnah} frame the {\i seifa} around her death at all? One could have discussed the implications of having acquired property during the marriage even while Rachel is still alive \u8212- for instance, in terms of {\i peirot} rights. The fact that the {\i mishnah} introduces the case by telling us she died and asks what to do with the estate suggests, on its face, that the critical variable is her death, not the timing of acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Support Despite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite this problem, the {\i gemara} concludes that the linguistic evidence from {\i nikhnasim ve-ha-yotzim imah} lends meaningful support to Abaye\u8217's reading. Rashi\u8217's comment at this point is precise: {\i ein lahashiv al kach, devadai dika kavatei, ve-kashya le-meiti} \u8212- there is nothing to refute the linguistic support for Abaye, yet the emphasis on death in the {\i seifa} remains a genuine difficulty against him. The {\i mishnah} thus offers simultaneous support and challenge to Abaye\u8217's framework, and the {\i gemara} leaves the matter with a {\i ve-tulo midai}, noting that there is nothing more to resolve the tension definitively.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 After Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now addresses what happens after Shimon performs {\i yibum} with Rachel. The ruling is straightforward in principle: once {\i yibum} is accomplished, Rachel becomes Shimon\u8217's full wife in every respect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first practical consequence is that if Shimon subsequently wishes to separate from Rachel, he must give her a {\i get}. The mechanism of {\i chalitzah} \u8212- which was the operative mode of separation while Rachel was a {\i yevamah} \u8212- is no longer available or relevant. She is no longer a {\i yevamah}; she is simply a wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second consequence is that if Shimon divorces Rachel and she subsequently marries another man and is widowed or divorced from him, Shimon may remarry Rachel through ordinary {\i kiddushin}. The relationship has been entirely normalized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the apparent obviousness of the first ruling. Surely it goes without saying that after {\i yibum} one gives a {\i get} rather than performs {\i chalitzah}? The {\i gemara} responds that this needed to be stated. One might have thought \u8212- {\i salka de-atach amina} \u8212- that since the Torah says \u8220"take her for a wife and perform {\i yibum},\u8221" the act of {\i yibum} initiates the relationship but does not fully extinguish her {\i yevamah} status. Perhaps she retains the status of sister-in-law throughout the marriage, such that separating from her would require both a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} therefore teaches that this is not so. Once {\i yibum} is performed, {\i amar kra u-lakachah lo l\u8217'ishah} \u8212- the Torah describes the act as taking her as a wife. {\i Keivan she-lakachah, harei hi k\u8217'ishto le-chol davar} \u8212- once he has taken her, she is fully his wife in every respect. All prior statuses \u8212- sister-in-law, {\i yevamah}, {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- are entirely dissolved. The act of {\i yibum} is not merely a marital act that coexists with her former status; it transforms her status completely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle resolves the concern about remarriage. One might have thought that if Shimon divorces Rachel and she marries another man, she cannot return to Shimon \u8212- not because of any general prohibition, but because the act of {\i yibum} functioned as a divinely mandated override of the normal prohibition of a brother\u8217's wife, and that override applied only within the specific context of the {\i mitzvah}. Once divorced, a remarriage would not be a continuation of {\i yibum} but a new marriage \u8212- and a new marriage to a brother\u8217's wife would reinstantiate the original prohibition. The {\i mishnah} teaches that this concern is unfounded. The {\i yibum} act comprehensively changes her status, and she is simply his wife; the prohibition of {\i eishet ach} has no further purchase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One qualification attaches to this full-wife status in the domain of {\i ketubah}. If Shimon dies after {\i yibum}, Rachel collects her {\i ketubah} primarily from Reuven\u8217's estate \u8212- because the {\i ketubah} obligation originated with Reuven, and Shimon effectively stepped into Reuven\u8217's position along with Reuven\u8217's estate. The {\i gemara} notes: {\i ishah hiknu lo min ha-shamayim} \u8212- a wife was given to him by Heaven, meaning that the {\i ketubah} obligation tracks the estate that was transferred to Shimon from Reuven, and thus remains anchored there. However, if Reuven\u8217's estate is insufficient to satisfy the {\i ketubah}, Rachel may collect from Shimon\u8217's own estate \u8212- {\i tiknu lah mi-sheini} \u8212- because without recourse to Shimon\u8217's estate she would be left without protection, and the Rabbis were concerned that absent such protection a husband might be too quick to divorce his wife: {\i kedei she-lo tehei kalah be-einav le-hotzi\u8217'ah}. As Tosafot observes, since Shimon in any event now possesses Reuven\u8217's property, the practical question of which estate she collects from involves careful analysis of what the distinction amounts to \u8212- but the {\i halakha} establishes a clear ordering of primary and secondary sources of collection.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mitzvah Begadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oldest Obligated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to a topic that, the {\i gemara} suggests, probably should have appeared at the very opening of the {\i masechet}: the primary obligation to perform {\i yibum} falls on the oldest brother. {\i Mitzvah begadol leyabem} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} is incumbent first and foremost upon the eldest brother. This principle carries two implications: first, that {\i yibum} is preferred over {\i chalitzah}; and second, that among the brothers, the eldest bears the primary obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Go to Others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the oldest brother is unwilling to perform {\i yibum}, the {\i mishnah} directs that one should go to the other brothers \u8212- not necessarily in order of age \u8212- to see whether any of them is prepared to perform {\i yibum}. The preference for {\i yibum} over {\i chalitzah} means that a younger brother\u8217's willingness to do {\i yibum} is worth pursuing even at the cost of bypassing the older brother\u8217's readiness to do {\i chalitzah}. Notably, the {\i mishnah} frames this entire process from the perspective of the brothers; the woman\u8217's preference is not foregrounded, a point to which the discussion will return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Force Oldest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If none of the brothers wish to perform {\i yibum}, one returns to the oldest brother and tells him directly: {\i alecha mitzvah} \u8212- the obligation is yours. He must either perform {\i yibum} or perform {\i chalitzah}; the obligation cannot remain unresolved. He is the one to be pressured and, if necessary, compelled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Waiting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further specifies that there are cases in which one might be tempted to delay: if there is a younger brother who is a minor and might grow up and wish to perform {\i yibum}; if the oldest brother is overseas and one might wait for his return; or if one of the brothers is a {\i cheresh} or {\i shoteh} who might conceivably recover. In all these cases, {\i ein shom\u8217'in lo} \u8212- we do not listen to arguments for delay. The response is: {\i alecha mitzvah, o chalotz o yabem} \u8212- you are the one obligated, act now. The {\i mishnah} emphasizes that the present situation, with the brothers who are present and capable, is what determines the obligation. One does not suspend action in hopes that a better outcome might materialize.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum vs.\u160 ?Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a specific case that tests the priorities established by the {\i mishnah}: a younger brother wishes to perform {\i yibum}, while the older brother is prepared to perform {\i chalitzah}. The question is which takes precedence. Based on the {\i mishnah}\u8217's plain reading \u8212- which sends us to the younger brothers only when the oldest refuses to do anything \u8212- one might think the answer is obvious. But the {\i gemara} shows that the matter is in fact disputed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan holds {\i biat katan adifa} \u8212- the act of {\i yibum} by the younger brother is preferable. His reasoning: {\i yibum} is a greater {\i mitzvah} than {\i chalitzah}, and therefore if {\i yibum} can be achieved through any brother, that outcome is to be preferred over {\i chalitzah} even by the oldest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligation Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi holds {\i chalitzat gadol adifa} \u8212- the action of the oldest brother takes precedence. His reasoning does not deny that {\i yibum} is the more significant {\i mitzvah}. Rather, he argues that {\i b\u8217'makom gadol, biat katan lav klum hu} \u8212- when the oldest brother is present and prepared to act, the younger brother\u8217's act is of no legal significance. The oldest brother is the one upon whom the obligation devolves; his willingness to do {\i something} means that he is the relevant actor, and one does not look past him to the other brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying disagreement, then, is between two frameworks. Rabbi Yochanan focuses on the quality of the {\i mitzvah} being performed: if {\i yibum} is better, find the best person to do it. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi focuses on the identity of the obligated party: once the oldest brother is ready to fulfill his obligation in any manner, he is the man, and the question of which {\i mitzvah} is greater is secondary.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to read the {\i mishnah} as settling this dispute. The {\i mishnah} says that if the oldest brother {\i lo ratza} \u8212- did not want \u8212- one goes to the other brothers. A natural reading of {\i lo ratza} is that he refused to perform {\i yibum} but was prepared to perform {\i chalitzah}. If so, the {\i mishnah} is teaching that even though the oldest brother is willing to do {\i chalitzah}, one still goes to the other brothers to seek {\i yibum} \u8212- proving that {\i yibum} of a younger brother takes precedence over {\i chalitzah} of the oldest, in accordance with Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Ratza Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this reading. {\i Lo ratza} must mean that the oldest brother refused to do anything \u8212- neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitzah}. It is this total refusal that prompts the {\i mishnah} to send us to the other brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Force Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This corrected reading creates its own difficulty. If all the brothers ultimately refuse to do anything \u8212- neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitzah} \u8212- and compulsion becomes necessary, why does the {\i mishnah} direct us to compel the oldest brother specifically? Once we are in the realm of compulsion, the question of who bears the primary obligation seems less relevant. We simply need someone to act \u8212- so why not compel a younger brother?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvah Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers without hesitation: {\i keivan de-mitzvah le-didei ramya, le-didei kafnan} \u8212- since the {\i mitzvah} is primarily incumbent upon the oldest brother, he is the one we compel. Even when force is necessary, the legal structure of obligation has not changed. The oldest bears the primary responsibility, and that responsibility means he is the one subjected to compulsion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Waiting Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wait for Young\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i lo ratza} means refusing both {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}, the {\i gemara} presses a further question. According to Rabbi Yochanan, who holds that {\i yibum} is always preferable even when performed by a younger brother \u8212- why does the {\i mishnah} say we do not wait for a minor brother to grow up? If we could obtain {\i yibum} by waiting for the younger brother to come of age, should we not do so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wait for Old\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same challenge can be posed in the opposite direction, now aimed at Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, who holds that the oldest brother\u8217's action takes precedence. If so, why does the {\i mishnah} say we do not wait for the oldest brother to return from overseas? If the oldest brother is the obligated party and his action is what we prefer, we should be willing to wait for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Delay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that neither challenge lands, because both rest on the same false premise \u8212- that our hierarchy of preferences justifies delay. The {\i gemara} states: {\i ella kol she-hu mitzvah lo mashinan} \u8212- wherever there is a {\i mitzvah} to be done, we do not postpone it. No matter what one\u8217's priorities are regarding who should perform the {\i mitzvah} or in what form, the overriding imperative is that the {\i mitzvah} be discharged promptly with the people who are present and capable right now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Act Now\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two questions thus cancel each other out, and the answer is the same in both directions: it is not a question of which preference takes precedence, but of the principle that a {\i mitzvah} cannot be held in abeyance while we hope for a more optimal actor to become available. Whatever our series of preferences \u8212- {\i yibum} over {\i chalitzah}, oldest over youngest \u8212- those preferences operate among present options, not as grounds for indefinite delay.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Preference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second version of the dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi now emerges, one that sharpens the question. In the first version, the debate concerned {\i yibum} by a younger brother versus {\i chalitzah} by the older brother. Now the question is narrower: if {\i yibum} is entirely off the table and the only available outcome is {\i chalitzah}, does the principle of {\i mitzvah begadol} still apply? That is, when choosing between {\i chalitzat katan} and {\i chalitzat gadol}, is there a preference for the older brother to perform {\i chalitzah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvah Applies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One opinion holds {\i chalitzat gadol adifa} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah begadol} principle extends even to {\i chalitzah}, and therefore the older brother should be the one to perform it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Equivalent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other opinion holds {\i ke-hadadi ninhu} \u8212- once we are operating in the {\i chalitzah} domain, the brothers are equivalent. The reasoning behind the {\i mitzvah begadol} principle was connected to the role of {\i yibum} in perpetuating the dead brother\u8217's name and legacy, an act for which the oldest brother\u8217's position is especially meaningful. {\i Chalitzah}, however, is simply an act of severing the levirate bond, with no such resonance \u8212- and for a mere act of severance, age confers no special significance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts again to resolve the dispute from the {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} says {\i lo ratzu, chozrin eitzel gadol} \u8212- if the brothers did not want to act, one returns to the oldest brother. The {\i gemara} reads {\i lo ratzu} as meaning the younger brothers refused to do {\i yibum} but were prepared to do {\i chalitzah}. If so, we now have a situation where multiple brothers are prepared to do {\i chalitzah}, and the {\i mishnah} directs us to return to the oldest \u8212- proving that even in the {\i chalitzah} domain the older brother\u8217's action is preferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Proof Yet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once again, however, the {\i gemara} corrects this reading: {\i lo ratzu} means the brothers refused to do anything at all \u8212- neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitzah}. The same logic as before applies: we go to the oldest because the {\i mitzvah} obligation remains on him, and we compel him because he is the obligated party. But this tells us nothing about whether, when {\i chalitzah} is the only option and the brothers are cooperating, there is a preference for the oldest to perform it. The {\i mishnah} provides no resolution to this question, and the dispute remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 40\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum vs Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 39b, picking up from a {\i mishna} cited from tractate {\i Kesubos} that establishes the basic halakhic ordering: {\i mitzvas yibum kodemes lemitzvas chalitzah} \u8212- the commandment of {\i yibum} takes precedence over the commandment of {\i chalitzah}. This was the original arrangement, operating under the assumption that men performed {\i yibum} for the right reasons: {\i barishona shehayu miskavnim leshem mitzvah} \u8212- originally, they had proper intent, acting for the sake of the {\i mitzvah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Changes Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces a striking reversal. Once the situation changed \u8212- {\i achshav she\u8217'ein miskavnim leshem mitzvah}, now that men no longer perform {\i yibum} for the sake of the {\i mitzvah} \u8212- the ruling shifts: {\i mitzvas chalitzah kodemes lemitzvas yibum}, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i chalitzah} now comes first. The most obvious ulterior motive, as the very next {\i mishna} will make clear, is the inheritance: a man who performs {\i yibum} acquires his deceased brother\u8217's entire estate. Once financial gain rather than religious purpose drives the act, the Sages reversed the priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a profound question: what exactly constitutes acting {\i shelo leshem mitzvah}, without proper intent? And what is the practical consequence of that reversal \u8212- does it mean {\i chalitzah} is now strictly mandated, or merely preferred? These questions animate the entire subsequent {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} addresses the question with the ruling {\i ein kofin} \u8212- we do not compel. {\i Rashi} explains this to mean that we do not force a couple to perform {\i chalitzah} when both parties wish to perform {\i yibum}. On {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading, while we may now prefer {\i chalitzah}, we do not prevent {\i yibum} when both sides genuinely want it. The preference for {\i chalitzah} is real, but it does not translate into outright prohibition of {\i yibum} when there is mutual consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point {\i Rashi} introduces a critically important dimension that the {\i sugya} had until now entirely ignored: the woman\u8217's voice. {\i Rashi} writes that if the {\i yabam} wishes to perform {\i yibum} but the woman says {\i lo ba\u8217'ina lei} \u8212- she does not want him \u8212- and she offers {\i devarim nikarim}, a reasonable explanation for her refusal, then the court intervenes. The first step is essentially a form of financial inducement: the court encourages the {\i yabam} to perform {\i chalitzah} by offering him compensation on her behalf, up to two hundred {\i zuz}. If that fails, {\i kafnan lei v\u8217'chalatz} \u8212- the court compels him to perform {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} goes further, clarifying that the standard is not limited to extreme cases of physical repulsion \u8212- the example given is a {\i yabam} afflicted with {\i mukei shechin}, a severe skin disease \u8212- but extends to {\i kol amasa shetitan lidevareha}, any reasonable grounds she articulates. This is a remarkable insertion, because the entire preceding discussion had been framed entirely around the brothers and their preferences. {\i Rashi} here establishes that the woman\u8217's reasoned refusal is legally determinative: if she does not consent, {\i yibum} will not take place, whether through persuasion or coercion of the {\i yabam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When couples actually came before {\i Rav}, his practice illustrates the tension vividly. He would say: {\i iba\u8217'is chalot iba\u8217'is yabem} \u8212- do you wish to perform {\i chalitzah}, or do you wish to perform {\i yibum}? He presented both as genuine options. True, he listed {\i chalitzah} first, and that ordering reflects the preference \u8212- but the framing otherwise treats them as equivalent choices. He then cited the verse: {\i ve\u8217'im lo yachpotz ha\u8217'ish}, if the man does not wish to do {\i yibum} he performs {\i chalitzah}, but {\i ha im chafetz} \u8212- if he does wish to \u8212- the choice is his, {\i bedidach tala rachmana}, the Torah leaves it in his hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This practice goes even further than merely not preventing {\i yibum}: {\i Rav} is actively presenting it as a legitimate option. The only concession to the preference for {\i chalitzah} is the order in which he raises the possibilities. The Torah\u8217's own prioritization of {\i yibum} makes it difficult to do more than that: one can acknowledge the problems with {\i yibum} performed for ulterior motives and still recognize that the Torah itself frames {\i yibum} as the primary act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} likewise held {\i ein kofin}, as demonstrated by the document he established for {\i chalitzah} proceedings \u8212- the {\i get} of {\i chalitzah}. In that document, the court offers the {\i yabam} the option of performing {\i yibum} even as he stands before the court to perform {\i chalitzah}. This detail will be explored in full in the next section, but its relevance here is clear: even in the formal procedure for {\i chalitzah}, the option of {\i yibum} is explicitly preserved. And {\i Rav Yehuda} explicitly grounded his position in the verse {\i ve\u8217'im lo yachpotz ha\u8217'ish} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's own framing, which treats the man\u8217's willingness as the deciding factor and implies that if he does wish to perform {\i yibum}, that remains a legitimate path.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get of Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} established the precise language for the formal court record of a {\i chalitzah} proceeding \u8212- called the {\i get} of {\i chalitzah}, a usage worth pausing on. The term {\i get} does not mean divorce specifically; it means a writ or document. This document is the {\i beis din}\u8217's official record that the {\i chalitzah} took place, and the woman could produce it subsequently to establish that she was free to remarry. {\i Rav Yehuda} fixed the language of this document, and it opens with the words {\i anan sahadei} \u8212- we testify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother Identification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The document begins by identifying the woman: {\i plonis bas ploni}, so-and-so daughter of so-and-so. It then records that she, the {\i yevamah}, brought her {\i yabam} before the court \u8212- the Torah frames the woman as the active party who brings the {\i yabam} to the {\i beis din}. The court then attests: {\i ve\u8217'ishtemod\u8217'einu hu de-achua de-misna me\u8217'abba nihu} \u8212- we recognized him to be a paternal brother of the deceased.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoe Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The document continues: the court said to him, if you wish to perform {\i yibum} do so, and if not, extend your right leg toward her. He extended his right leg, and she removed the shoe from his leg.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spitting & Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She then spat before him \u8212- {\i roka d\u8217'miskhazi leBeis Dina}, a spitting that was visible to the {\i beis din}, directed toward the ground in front of him rather than literally at his face. {\i Rav Chiya bar Avya} adds, in the name of {\i Rav Yehuda}, an additional element: the court had him recite the relevant verse from the Torah, {\i lo chafatzti lekachtah}, \u8220"I do not wish to take her,\u8221" with the communal response {\i kacha ye\u8217'aseh la\u8217'ish asher lo yivneh es beis achiv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note what is conspicuous about the first version of the document: it omits those verses. This is likely deliberate. Since we now wish to hold out {\i chalitzah} as a dignified option \u8212- and since we are trying to make {\i chalitzah} function without the stigma of the {\i yabam} being publicly shamed \u8212- emphasizing the {\i pesukim} that frame the {\i yabam} as shirking his familial responsibility would be counterproductive. The {\i gemara} has effectively transformed {\i chalitzah} into a technical legal procedure, stripped of the element of social reproach that the Torah seems to have built into it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting the structural point that throughout this entire procedure, the woman is the initiating actor: she brings the {\i yabam} to court, she removes the shoe, she spits. When {\i yibum} is the act, the man is the primary agent; in {\i chalitzah}, the woman is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Achai/Ravina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One phrase in the document requires further analysis: {\i ve\u8217'ishtemod\u8217'einu hu} \u8212- \u8220"we recognized him\u8221" to be a paternal brother. This raises a practical question: how does the court establish that identification? {\i Rav Achai} and {\i Ravina} debate the matter. One holds that formal witnesses are required to testify that he is a paternal brother. The other holds that even a relative or a woman \u8212- people ordinarily ineligible to serve as formal witnesses \u8212- suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} follows the latter view: {\i giluy milsa b\u8217'alma hu}, this is merely a matter of publicly known information, not formal testimony. Since the fact of someone being a brother is typically known to the community, the ordinary rules of testimony do not apply, and we may rely on whatever evidence establishes the fact \u8212- even a single relative or woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical reason this identification matters is that without it, there could be abuse of the procedure. If a woman\u8217's {\i yabam} is refusing to act, or is threatening to perform {\i yibum} against her wishes, she might conceivably bring a stranger to {\i beis din} and claim he is the {\i yabam}, thereby fraudulently obtaining a {\i chalitzah}. The identification requirement prevents this. But since the fact is generally in the realm of common knowledge rather than disputed legal fact, the standard of proof is correspondingly relaxed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} notes that this detail also reinforces the earlier point: even in the formal document for {\i chalitzah}, the man is told he may still perform {\i yibum} if he chooses. This is the ongoing demonstration that even as we say {\i mitzvas chalitzah kodemes}, we are not eliminating {\i yibum} as an option.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intent Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Reversion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now reports a further development. {\i Rami bar Chama} says in the name of {\i Rav Yitzchak}: {\i chazru lomar mitzvas yibum kodemes lemitzvas chalitzah} \u8212- they went back to saying that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} comes first. This appears to undo the entire premise of the preceding discussion, reverting to the original formulation of the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spiritual Capacity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak} challenges this immediately: {\i achshar daray}? Have the generations suddenly improved? If the reason for reversing the priority was that people were no longer performing {\i yibum leshem mitzvah}, and {\i yeridat hadorot} \u8212- the general decline across generations \u8212- is a familiar assumption, on what basis could a later generation simply revert to the original practice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shift in Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is not that the generations improved morally or spiritually. Rather, the shift reflects a change in which {\i tannaitic} position is being followed. {\i Me\u8217'ikara savri lah ke\u8217'Abba Shaul} \u8212- originally they held in accordance with {\i Abba Shaul}, who says that {\i kavana}, intent, is determinative. {\i Ulevasof savri lah keRabbanan} \u8212- later they reverted to holding like the {\i Rabbanan}, who say that intent is legally irrelevant to the validity of {\i yibum}. The reversal was not demographic; it was doctrinal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents the fundamental dispute directly. {\i Abba Shaul} says: one who takes his {\i yevamah leshem noi}, for her physical attractiveness, {\i leshum ishut}, for the social status of marriage \u8212- {\i Tosafos} explains this as a desire for the standing that comes with being a married man \u8212- or {\i leshum davar acher}, for any other ulterior motive, most obviously the inheritance of the brother\u8217's estate: {\i ke\u8217'ilu pogea be\u8217'ervah}, it is as if he is engaging in a forbidden sexual relationship. And {\i Abba Shaul} adds: {\i vekarov ani be\u8217'einai lihyot havlad mamzer} \u8212- I would consider the child close to being a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking position, and notably non-technical in character. {\i Abba Shaul} is not arguing that the formal categories of {\i yibum} were not met. He is arguing from the logic of what makes {\i yibum} permissible in the first place. The relationship between a man and his brother\u8217's wife is ordinarily an {\i ervah}, one of the gravest sexual prohibitions. What overrides that prohibition is the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} \u8212- specifically the imperative {\i lehakim shem achiv}, to perpetuate his brother\u8217's name and legacy. When a man performs {\i yibum} not for that purpose but for his own desires or financial gain, {\i Abba Shaul} holds that the overriding consideration is effectively absent, and therefore the underlying prohibition reasserts itself. The technical category of {\i yibum} is present, but its validating rationale is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe that even on {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's view, one could argue that regardless of the man\u8217's subjective intent, if a child results, {\i lehakim shem achiv} is objectively achieved. {\i Abba Shaul} seems to respond that when the act is not subjectively framed in terms of religious purpose, it cannot function at the societal level as an act of perpetuating the brother\u8217's name \u8212- whatever the metaphysical outcome. The reasoning is admittedly elusive, but the moral seriousness of {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's position is unmistakable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vachachamim omrim} \u8212- the {\i Rabbanan} disagree entirely. {\i Yevamah yavo aleiha mikol makom} \u8212- the Torah says he shall have relations with her, full stop. The halakhic permission established by the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} does not depend on the actor\u8217's internal religious motivation. The Torah established an act; when that act is performed, the permission applies, regardless of whether the man\u8217's {\i kavana} was {\i leshem shamayim} or otherwise. Intent may be a religious desideratum, but it is not a halakhic condition for the validity of {\i yibum} according to the {\i Rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: {\i man tanna leha detanu Rabbanan}? Which {\i tanna} is the source of the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position? It then cites a {\i braita} that formulates the issue in structural terms: the {\i yevamah} was originally {\i mutar} to the {\i yabam} before she married his brother; then she became {\i assur}; then upon the brother\u8217's death she became {\i mutar} again. The {\i braita} asks: does she revert to her original status of full {\i heter}? The answer is: {\i talmud lomar yevamah yavo aleiha mitzvah} \u8212- the Torah says the {\i yabam} shall come to her as a {\i mitzvah}, indicating that the current {\i heter} is not identical to the original one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yitzchak bar Avdimi} reads this {\i braita} as representing {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's position. The {\i braita} is saying that returning to {\i heter} after the brother\u8217's death does not restore the original unconditioned permission: now the act must be done {\i lemitzvah}, for the sake of the {\i mitzvah}, as {\i Abba Shaul} requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava}, however, reads the {\i braita} as consistent with the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position. His reading reframes the {\i hava amina} entirely. Originally, before she married the brother, the man had no obligation toward this woman: {\i ratza konsah, ratza eino konsah} \u8212- if he wished to marry her he could, if not he need not. After the brother\u8217's death, {\i zika} \u8212- the bond of {\i yibum} \u8212- attaches. One cannot imagine that he might simply walk away; she is {\i agida bei}, bound to him. So the {\i hava amina} is not that he can walk away altogether, but rather that perhaps {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} are fully equivalent options, as unconditioned as his original relationship to her. The Torah answers: no, {\i yibum} is the primary {\i mitzvah}. This is {\i Rava}\u8217's reading for the {\i Rabbanan} \u8212- the verse prioritizes {\i yibum} over {\i chalitzah}, not as a condition of intent, but as a statement of halakhic hierarchy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parallel Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established {\i Rava}\u8217's reading of the {\i braita} in favor of the {\i Rabbanan}, the {\i gemara} tests this reading against the parallel case the {\i braita} employs: the {\i mincha} offering eaten by the {\i kohanim} as {\i matzot} in a sacred place. The {\i braita} applies the identical logical structure \u8212- original {\i heter}, then {\i issur}, then return to {\i heter} \u8212- asking whether the flour reverts to its original unconditioned status. The verse answers: {\i matzot teiachel b\u8217'makom kadosh mitzvah} \u8212- it shall be eaten as {\i matzot} in a sacred place, as a {\i mitzvah}. On {\i Rava}\u8217's reading, this is a structural parallel: just as {\i yibum} is the prioritized option over {\i chalitzah}, here there is a prioritized manner of eating the {\i mincha}, with a second-grade alternative that is still valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matza Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does this parallel work in practice? The {\i gemara} works through several possibilities for what the two \u8220"grades\u8221" of eating might be. The initial {\i heter} was that the flour, before being sanctified, could be eaten by anyone, in any manner, for any reason \u8212- {\i ratza ochla ratza eino ochla}, one could eat it or not eat it. After sanctification it becomes {\i assur}. After the {\i kometz} is brought, it becomes {\i mutar} again for the {\i kohanim}. The {\i hava amina} that it might revert to the original equivalence of options is dismissed by the {\i gemara}: how could one imagine that the {\i kohen} might simply choose not to eat it? The verse {\i ve\u8217'achlu otam asher kuppar bahem} \u8212- \u8220"they shall eat those through which atonement was achieved\u8221" \u8212- establishes that the {\i kohanim}\u8217's eating is obligatory, as it is through their eating that the owner\u8217's atonement is effected, {\i hakohanim ochlim ube\u8217'alim mitchaprin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i hava amina} is reframed: perhaps the specific {\i kohen} who performed the {\i avoda} and brought the {\i kometz} could choose to have a different {\i kohen} eat the {\i mincha} instead. The verse answers that there is a {\i mitzvah davka} with him \u8212- he should eat it himself, in priority over another {\i kohen}. This parallels the {\i yibum} case: just as {\i yibum} is preferred over {\i chalitzah}, this {\i kohen} eating is preferred over another {\i kohen} eating. Both options are valid; one is prioritized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Alternatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the structural shape of the parallel, the {\i gemara} must now identify the actual two grades in the {\i matza} case that correspond to {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} in {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's reading \u8212- since {\i Rava} reads it for the {\i Rabbanan}, but the {\i braita} itself needs to make sense on {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's account as well. On {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's reading, the two variations are {\i yibum leshem mitzvah} (the higher grade) and {\i yibum shelo leshem mitzvah} (the lower but still technically valid grade). What is the parallel in the {\i mincha} case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first considers: perhaps eating {\i achilah gassah} \u8212- eating when completely sated and forcing oneself \u8212- is the lower grade. This is rejected: {\i achilah gassah mi shma achilah}? Forced eating is not eating at all. {\i Resh Lakish} rules that one who eats {\i achilah gassah} on {\i Yom Kippur} is {\i patur}, since the Torah prohibits {\i inui}, affliction, which presupposes that normal eating would provide genuine satisfaction. {\i Tosafos} notes that there are degrees of {\i achilah gassah}: mild overeating (\u8220"oh, perhaps just a bit of dessert\u8221") does not reach the level that is halakhically exempt on {\i Yom Kippur}; only eating when truly unable to experience any satisfaction does. But either way, the most extreme form of {\i achilah gassah} cannot serve as a lower grade of eating, because it is not eating at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers: perhaps the two grades are eating it as {\i matza} versus eating it as {\i chametz}. This too is dismissed: eating the {\i mincha} as {\i chametz} is simply forbidden. {\i Resh Lakish} derives from the verse {\i lo te\u8217'afeh chametz chelkam} that even the priestly portion of a {\i mincha} may not be baked as {\i chametz}. Since {\i chametz} is not a permitted option at all, it cannot be the lower-grade alternative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scalded Matza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} arrives at the correct answer: the two grades are {\i matza} baked in the ordinary fashion versus {\i chalut} \u8212- flour that has been scalded prior to baking. {\i Chalita} is a process that alters the texture of the dough without necessarily rendering it {\i chametz}. If after scalding the dough is baked, does the result still qualify as {\i matza}? The {\i gemara} raises the question: {\i im matzah hi hamatzah hi} \u8212- if it still counts as {\i matza}, then why is it a second-grade option? And if it does not count as {\i matza}, then one is not {\i yotzei} the obligation and the Torah would not permit it at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is: {\i lo, le\u8217'olam eima lach matzah hi} \u8212- it is indeed {\i matza}, but of a lesser grade. The verse {\i matzot ye\u8217'achel} specifies this to teach that there are two tiers of {\i matza}: ideal {\i matza} and {\i matza} that is technically valid but halakhically inferior. This is structurally parallel to {\i yibum leshem mitzvah} and {\i yibum shelo leshem mitzvah} on {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that scalded {\i matza} is valid but second-grade for the {\i mincha}, the {\i gemara} asks {\i l\u8217'mai hilchata}? \u8212- beyond the structural parallel, what is the practical halakhic implication of declaring {\i chalut matza}? The answer: {\i lomar she\u8217'yotzei bo yedei chovato bePesach} \u8212- one may use such scalded {\i matza} to fulfill the obligation of eating {\i matza} on Pesach. Even though it is a second-grade option for the {\i mincha}, for other contexts requiring {\i matza} it is fully adequate: {\i af al pi d\u8217'chalitah mei\u8217'ikara}, even if the grain was scalded at the outset, {\i keivan d\u8217'hadar afyeih batenur}, since it was subsequently baked in an oven, {\i lechem oni karina bei}, we call it \u8220"poor person\u8217's bread,\u8221" and one fulfills one\u8217's Pesach obligation with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In sum, the {\i braita} operates on two levels simultaneously. On {\i Rava}\u8217's reading it speaks for the {\i Rabbanan}, establishing that {\i yibum} is the prioritized option over {\i chalitzah}, parallel to the specific {\i kohen} eating being prioritized over another {\i kohen} eating. On the reading of {\i Yitzchak bar Avdimi} it speaks for {\i Abba Shaul}, establishing that {\i yibum leshem mitzvah} is the higher grade and {\i yibum shelo leshem mitzvah} is a lower but technically valid grade, parallel to ideal {\i matza} and scalded {\i matza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is significant: after introducing the reversal \u8212- that {\i mitzvas chalitzah kodemes} \u8212- and then showing how extensively even the Amoraim preserved {\i yibum} as a live option, the discussion ends by reporting that later authorities reverted to saying {\i mitzvas yibum kodemes}, because they followed the {\i Rabbanan} over {\i Abba Shaul}. This appears to be the conclusion of the {\i Bavli}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geography Factor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Babylonian Polygamy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Bavli}\u8217's conclusion \u8212- reverting to {\i mitzvas yibum kodemes} \u8212- stands in contrast to the {\i Yerushalmi}, which maintains the earlier position that {\i mitzvas chalitzah kodemes}. This divergence is not arbitrary; it reflects the radically different social realities in which each Talmud developed. {\i Bavel} was a polygamous society, situated within a Zoroastrian and later Islamic cultural world in which a man having multiple wives was entirely normative. In such a context, performing {\i yibum} even when already married posed no legal or social difficulty. Prioritizing {\i yibum} as the ideal was therefore practically feasible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eretz Yisrael Monogamy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eretz Yisrael}, by contrast, came under the influence of Rome, which was moving toward monogamy \u8212- and Rome\u8217's cultural trajectory was followed by Christianity, under which monogamy became a religious requirement. In a monogamous society, {\i yibum} is frequently not a practical option: if the {\i yabam} is already married, he cannot simply take a second wife. To hold {\i yibum} out as the halakhic ideal in such a society would create systematic problems. The {\i Yerushalmi} therefore maintained that {\i mitzvas chalitzah kodemes}, which functions as the workable norm across a wide range of circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefardic Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This geographic and cultural divide maps directly onto the division among the {\i Rishonim}. The {\i Sefardic} tradition, as the inheritors of the Babylonian halakhic culture and living within Islamic societies that permitted polygamy, continued to hold that {\i mitzvas yibum kodemes}. Even in more recent generations, as formal polygamy became less common even in {\i Sefardic} communities, the tradition of treating {\i yibum} as the preferred option persisted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashkenazi Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ashkenazic} tradition, living in Christian lands under conditions of strict monogamy and the {\i Cherem de-Rabbeinu Gershom} \u8212- the rabbinic prohibition of polygamy accepted across Ashkenaz \u8212- reached the opposite conclusion: {\i mitzvas chalitzah kodemes}. In a society where a married man absolutely cannot take a second wife, consistently prioritizing {\i yibum} would be untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reinterpret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi}, writing within the {\i Ashkenazic} world, reads the {\i gemara} straightforwardly without signaling any divergence from what the {\i gemara} seems to conclude. This is perhaps because {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading of {\i ein kofin} \u8212- that we allow {\i yibum} when both parties consent \u8212- is already somewhat of a mitigation, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's final position as read by {\i Rashi} is not entirely irreconcilable with practical {\i Ashkenazic} preference for {\i chalitzah}. {\i Tosafos}, however, goes further and explicitly reinterprets the {\i Bavli} to align with {\i Ashkenazic} practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} offers a sophisticated reading of the passage where {\i Rav} offered both options to the couple and {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's document invited the {\i yabam} to perform {\i yibum}. {\i Tosafos} argues that these cases were exceptional: {\i ve-chi havru atu l\u8217'kamei de-Rav} \u8212- those were people who came to {\i Rav} already intending to perform {\i yibum leshem mitzvah}, and it was precisely because their intent was pure that {\i Rav} validated {\i yibum} as an option. Similarly, the person in {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's document who was offered the choice was someone who had originally come to perform {\i chalitzah}: since he had come for {\i chalitzah}, he clearly had no ulterior motives for {\i yibum}, and therefore, if he changed his mind and chose {\i yibum}, his intent would by definition be {\i leshem mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is a near-perfect logical catch: a man who wants to perform {\i yibum} may be suspected of ulterior motives and is therefore not permitted to do so. A man who wants to perform {\i chalitzah}, and is therefore free of suspicion, is then offered the option of {\i yibum} \u8212- but if he takes it and appears eager, the suspicion reattaches. {\i Tosafos} concludes that {\i Rabbeinu Tam} ruled in accordance with {\i Abba Shaul}, as reflected in an apparently uncontested {\i mishna} in {\i Bechoros}, and that the {\i halacha} therefore follows {\i mitzvas chalitzah kodemes}. {\i Rabbeinu Chananel} initially ruled that {\i mitzvas yibum kodemes}, but subsequently retracted and ruled {\i mitzvas chalitzah kodemes}. The {\i machloket} between the {\i Bavli} and the {\i Yerushalmi}, and between {\i Ashkenazim} and {\i Sefardim}, is thus a principled disagreement rooted in divergent social and legal contexts as much as in divergent readings of the Talmudic text.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Share\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} addresses the property implications of {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}. When a brother performs {\i chalitzah} with his {\i yevamah}, {\i harei hu ke\u8217'echad min ha\u8217'achim linachal} \u8212- he retains an equal share of the deceased brother\u8217's estate, the same as any other surviving brother. One might have thought otherwise: having rejected the {\i yevamah}, perhaps he also forfeits any claim to his brother\u8217's property, analogous to the episode in {\i Megillas Rus} where the {\i goel} who initially wished to redeem the land withdrew when he learned that Ruth came with the transaction. Here, the {\i mishna} rules that rejecting the woman does not mean rejecting the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'im yesh sham av} \u8212- however, if the father is still alive, {\i nichasim shel av}, the estate goes to the father. This is simply the ordinary rule of inheritance: when a son dies, the father precedes the brothers in the line of heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Estate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i yibum} the situation is dramatically different: {\i hakones es yevamto zacha benichasim shel achiv} \u8212- one who performs {\i yibum} acquires the entirety of his deceased brother\u8217's estate. This is the financial incentive {\i Abba Shaul} found so troubling. The verse {\i yakum al shem achiv} \u8212- he shall stand up on his brother\u8217's name \u8212- is understood to link the act of {\i yibum} to the acquisition of the brother\u8217's name and, with it, his property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} disagrees: {\i bein kach uvein kach im yesh sham av nichasim shel av} \u8212- in both cases, whether {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}, if the father is alive the property goes to the father. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} reads {\i yakum al shem achiv} as establishing no independent property right; it speaks to something metaphysical or relational \u8212- the {\i yabam} perpetuates the brother\u8217's legacy in some sense that does not necessarily translate into estate acquisition, at least not when a prior heir such as the father is present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Precedence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} establishes {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's reasoning through an analogy to the {\i bechor}, the firstborn son. The verse {\i vehayah habechor} is read earlier in the {\i masechta} as a reference to the {\i yabam} performing the act of {\i yibum}, with {\i k\u8217'bechor} implying that he is treated like a firstborn. A firstborn son receives a double portion \u8212- but crucially, {\i mah bechor ein lo bechayei av}, a firstborn has no portion while the father is still alive. His enhanced share materializes only after the father\u8217's death. {\i Af hai nami ein lo bechayei av} \u8212- similarly, the {\i yabam}\u8217's claim to the brother\u8217's estate does not materialize if the father is alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the possibility that this analogy implies even more: just as the {\i bechor} eventually receives his double portion after the father dies, so too perhaps the {\i yabam} should eventually receive the estate after the father dies. The {\i gemara} rejects this: the verse says {\i yakum al shem achiv}, he stands on his brother\u8217's name, not his father\u8217's. If at the moment of the brother\u8217's death he did not step into the brother\u8217's estate \u8212- because the father was alive and took it \u8212- then his connection to that estate is severed permanently. {\i Yakum al shem achiv velo al shem aviv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then ask: if the father is alive and takes the property, in what sense does the {\i yabam} perpetuate his brother\u8217's name at all? The {\i gemara} does not pursue a financial answer here. The {\i Ramban} suggests something more metaphysical \u8212- an allusion to {\i gilgul neshamot}, transmigration of souls \u8212- but beyond that, caring for the widow, providing her with a home and continuity, is itself the fulfillment of {\i yakum al shem achiv} regardless of property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum vs Estate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a further implication of {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position. If the {\i yabam} and the estate are conditionally linked \u8212- he gets the estate only when there is no father \u8212- one might argue that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} itself is similarly conditional: perhaps where there is a father who will take the estate, there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} at all, since {\i yibum} and {\i nachalah} go together as a package. The {\i gemara} dismisses this emphatically: {\i midi yibum benachalah talah rachmana}? Does {\i yibum} depend on the {\i nachalah}? The causality runs in the opposite direction. {\i Yibumei miyatney} \u8212- {\i yibum} comes first; it is the act of {\i yibum} that, when performed, carries the {\i nachalah} with it as a consequence. If there is no {\i nachalah} \u8212- either because the brother had no property or because the father takes it \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} is unaffected. The {\i nachalah} follows {\i yibum}; {\i yibum} does not follow {\i nachalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Text Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ulla} and {\i Rabbi Yitzchak Napcha} both rule {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}. This is then complicated by a fascinating episode of textual transmission. {\i Rav Chanina Kara}, a Torah reader who sat before {\i Rabbi Yannai}, stated {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}. {\i Rabbi Yannai} told him: {\i puk kari karich l\u8217'bara} \u8212- go teach your readings outside. He rejected the ruling, saying the correct formulation is {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, a {\i tanna} \u8212- a reciter of {\i baraisos} rather than a reader of {\i pesukim} \u8212- sat before {\i Rav Nachman} and recited {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}. {\i Rav Nachman} challenged him: if that is the {\i halacha}, that we do not rule like {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, why is a {\i braisa} needed to say so? The principle {\i yachid verabim halacha kerabim} \u8212- the majority position prevails over the individual \u8212- should make it obvious without any formal teaching that we reject {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's minority view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} then reconstructs what must have happened. The original tradition was {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- we do rule like {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. This was a case where we follow the minority, an unusual but not unprecedented ruling. The {\i tanna} who transmitted this was troubled by it: how could we rule like a {\i daas yachid}, a solitary opinion? His discomfort led him unconsciously to invert the text, remembering it as {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}. {\i Rav Nachman} says: your fix, though born of misunderstanding, happens to be correct \u8212- {\i shappir afachit}, you corrected it correctly \u8212- because in fact the {\i halacha} does not follow {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. The original {\i braisa} was corrupted, but the corruption produced the right result. We therefore preserve {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} as the authoritative formulation, even though the path by which it arrived was through an unconscious scribal correction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalutzah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man\u8217's Relatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now addresses the web of prohibited relationships that flows from {\i chalitzah}. The act of {\i chalitzah} creates a relationship that is treated, for purposes of prohibited relatives, as analogous to a dissolved marriage. The woman who underwent {\i chalitzah} \u8212- the {\i chalutzah} \u8212- is treated in certain respects as the man\u8217's divorced wife. As a consequence, {\i hu assur bikrovoteha vehi assurah bikrovov} \u8212- he is forbidden to her relatives and she is forbidden to his relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Specifically, the man who performed {\i chalitzah} is forbidden to: her mother, her maternal grandmother, her paternal grandmother \u8212- since these fall under the prohibition of a woman and her daughter\u8217's line, the virtual \u8220"wife\u8217's grandmother\u8221"; her daughter; her granddaughter through a daughter; her granddaughter through a son. He is also forbidden to her sister, {\i bizman shehi kayyamet} \u8212- as long as the {\i chalutzah} herself is still alive. This mirrors exactly the prohibition of wife\u8217's sister, which lapses only upon the wife\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehachim mutarin} \u8212- the other brothers, who did not perform {\i chalitzah}, are permitted to all these women. The restrictions attach only to the specific brother who performed the act, not to the entire family.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Relatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chalutzah} is in turn forbidden to certain relatives of the man who performed {\i chalitzah}: his father and his paternal grandfather; his son and his grandson; his brother\u8217's son. The reasoning for each runs as follows. His father: from the father\u8217's perspective, this woman is his daughter-in-law \u8212- a biblical prohibition. His grandfather: from the grandfather\u8217's perspective, this woman is his son\u8217's daughter-in-law \u8212- a rabbinic prohibition, since the biblical categories extend only one generation in that direction. Nonetheless, the {\i mishna} includes rabbinic prohibitions within this framework. His son: from the son\u8217's perspective, this woman is his father\u8217's wife or ex-wife \u8212- a biblical prohibition. His grandson: she would be the grandfather\u8217's wife, again a rabbinic prohibition. His brother\u8217's son: from the nephew\u8217's perspective, this woman is his father\u8217's brother\u8217's wife, i.e., his aunt by marriage \u8212- a biblical prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzara Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} closes with a more complex case involving the {\i tzara}, the co-wife. {\i Mutar adam bikrovat tzarat chalutzato} \u8212- a man is permitted to marry a relative of the {\i tzara} of his {\i chalutzah}. {\i Ve\u8217'assur betzarat krovat chalutzato} \u8212- but he is forbidden to the {\i tzara} of a relative of his {\i chalutzah}. This distinction requires illustration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Take {\i Shimon}, who performs {\i chalitzah} with {\i Rachel}. {\i Rachel} is his {\i chalutzah}. {\i Rachel} had a co-wife, {\i Leah}, who was simultaneously married to the same deceased {\i Reuven}. {\i Leah} goes free as a result of the {\i chalitzah} \u8212- she no longer has any {\i yibum} obligation \u8212- but she did not herself undergo {\i chalitzah}, so she is not a {\i chalutzah} and does not bear the status of {\i Shimon}\u8217's virtual divorced wife. Therefore, {\i Shimon} is permitted to marry {\i Leah}\u8217's relatives \u8212- for instance, {\i Leah}\u8217's daughter {\i Dinah} \u8212- because {\i Leah} carries no prohibited status vis-\u224 ?-vis {\i Shimon}. That is the case of {\i krovos tzarat chalutzato}: relative of the co-wife is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse case is more puzzling. Suppose {\i Baruch} was married to both {\i Leah} and {\i Devorah}. {\i Leah} is {\i Rachel}\u8217's sister \u8212- a relative of {\i Shimon}\u8217's {\i chalutzah}. {\i Baruch} then dies or divorces {\i Devorah}, leaving {\i Devorah} free. Why would {\i Shimon} be forbidden to {\i Devorah}? {\i Devorah} is the {\i tzara} of {\i Leah}, and {\i Leah} is a relative of {\i Rachel}, {\i Shimon}\u8217's {\i chalutzah}. The case of {\i tzarat krovat chalutzato} \u8212- the co-wife of a relative of the {\i chalutzah} \u8212- is apparently {\i assur}. The reason {\i Shimon} cannot marry {\i Leah} is clear: {\i Leah} is his {\i chalutzah}\u8217's sister, and a man is forbidden to his virtual divorced wife\u8217's sister while she lives. But why {\i Devorah} is also {\i assur} is less obvious \u8212- and the {\i gemara} will address that question in the following {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 41\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shniyot B\u8217'Chalutza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shniyot Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 41b opens with a focused question: {\i gazru shniyot b\u8217'chalutza o lo?} When the {\i chalutza} is treated like a {\i gerusha} and the relatives of the woman with whom {\i chalitza} was performed become forbidden to him, does that prohibition extend beyond the biblical {\i arayot} to include the rabbinic secondary prohibitions \u8212- the {\i shniyot l\u8217'arayot} \u8212- as well? The {\i mishna} sounds as though it does, but the Gemara subjects that assumption to careful scrutiny.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 D\u8217'Oraisa vs.\u160 ?D\u8217'Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's initial answer draws a principled distinction: {\i d\u8217'oraisa gazru rabbanan shniyot; b\u8217'chalutza lo gazru rabbanan shniyot.} The secondary prohibitions are applied where the man was actually married to the woman \u8212- a biblical relationship \u8212- but not in the case of the {\i chalutza}, whose status as a quasi-wife is itself only rabbinic. Before proceeding, it is worth noting a broader point: the {\i chalutza} is treated as a {\i gerusha} for the purposes of a {\i kohen}, and there is some evidence in tannaic literature that this status was understood as {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, though the Gemara insists it is rabbinic. In any event, the prohibition on a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i chalutza} is a distinct question from whether the {\i arayot} framework \u8212- with all its secondary extensions \u8212- applies to the {\i zika} relationship created by {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ima v\u8217'Eim Ima\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To test this principle, the Gemara turns to the {\i mishna}\u8217's specific examples. The {\i mishna} states that the man who performed {\i chalitza} is forbidden in his {\i chalutza}\u8217's mother and grandmother \u8212- {\i ima u\u8217'veim ima} \u8212- but does not mention the great-grandmother. Since the great-grandmother would represent a rabbinic {\i shniya}, the omission seems to prove that {\i shniyot} do not apply to the {\i chalutza}. The Gemara deflects: perhaps the reason the great-grandmother is not mentioned is simply that the {\i mishna} then goes on to teach the leniency that the brothers are permitted with all these relatives, and had the {\i mishna} mentioned the great-grandmother, a reader might mistakenly think the brothers are permitted only with the great-grandmother but not with the grandmother and mother. By stopping at grandmother, the {\i mishna} prevents that misreading. The Gemara counters: but why not simply state explicitly that both the man is forbidden in the great-grandmother {\i and} the brothers are permitted with all of them? {\i Kashya} \u8212- this is difficult. So there remains a strong suggestion that the {\i mishna} stops at grandmother because {\i shniyot} are not applied to the {\i chalutza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aviv v\u8217'Avi Aviv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara tries a different case: the {\i mishna} states the {\i chalutza} is forbidden with his father and his grandfather \u8212- {\i aviv u\u8217'vavi aviv}. This becomes more interesting when analyzed carefully. Why cannot she marry the father? Because from the man\u8217's perspective, she is his daughter-in-law \u8212- an explicit biblical {\i erva}. But the grandfather? She would be his son\u8217's daughter-in-law \u8212- a {\i shniya l\u8217'arayot}, a second-degree rabbinic prohibition. So it seems the {\i mishna} applies {\i shniyot} to the {\i chalutza} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara replies: {\i lo mishum mishna} \u8212- this is not because of the {\i chalitz} himself (the man who performed {\i chalitza}, here called {\i Shimon}), but rather {\i mishum mes} \u8212- because of the deceased brother ({\i Reuven}). In other words, the grandfather is also Reuven\u8217's grandfather, and she is his son\u8217's daughter-in-law through the biblical relationship with Reuven, not through the rabbinic {\i chalutza} relationship with Shimon. It is a {\i shniya l\u8217'arayot}, yes, but generated by the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} relationship with Reuven, not by the {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} relationship with Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reuven vs.\u160 ?Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This logic drives the entire analysis of the subsequent cases in the {\i mishna}, and it is worth pausing to appreciate its structure. Throughout these cases, what looks like {\i shniyot} applied to the {\i chalutza} relationship turns out, on examination, to be {\i shniyot} applied to the underlying biblical relationship with the deceased husband. The prohibition attaches because of Reuven, not because of Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate: if Shimon performed {\i chalitza} with Rachel, and the question is whether she may marry Shimon\u8217's grandfather Yitzchak, the answer is no \u8212- but the reason is not that Yitzchak\u8217's relationship to Rachel through Shimon\u8217's {\i chalutza} status generates a {\i shniya}. The reason is that Yitzchak is also Reuven\u8217's grandfather, and Rachel is Reuven\u8217's daughter-in-law through the biblical marriage, making this a {\i shniya} derived from the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} relationship, not the {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing \u8212- that all these prohibitions trace to Reuven and not to Shimon \u8212- is how the Gemara defends the position that {\i shniyot} do not apply to the {\i chalutza} relationship per se.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabi Chiya Arba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis receives its most explicit formulation in a {\i beraita} of Rabbi Chiyya: {\i d\u8217'tani Rabbi Chiyya arba midivrei Torah v\u8217'arba midivrei Soferim.} Among those forbidden to the {\i chalutza}, four categories are forbidden based on biblical relationships and four based on rabbinic ones. The four biblical categories are his father, his son, his brother, and his brother\u8217's son \u8212- all of whom, when analyzed from the perspective of the relative in question, stand in a relationship of biblical {\i erva} to Rachel through their connection to Reuven. For example, why is Shimon\u8217's brother\u8217's son forbidden? Because from that son\u8217's perspective, Rachel is his father\u8217's brother\u8217's wife \u8212- aunt \u8212- which is biblical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The four rabbinic categories \u8212- grandfather ({\i avi aviv}), maternal grandfather ({\i avi imo}), grandson ({\i ben bno}), and daughter\u8217's son ({\i ben bito}) \u8212- are {\i midivrei Soferim}, and it is explicit that they represent {\i shniyot}. The Gemara then goes through each of these, attempting to demonstrate that even these four could be explained through the biblical Reuven relationship rather than the rabbinic Shimon relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avi Imo Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Take the case of {\i avi imo}, the maternal grandfather \u8212- in our terms, Lavan, the father of Rachel\u8217's mother Leah. The Gemara asks: is this forbidden because of Shimon\u8217's relationship to Rachel ({\i mishum choletz}), making this his daughter\u8217's daughter-in-law, a {\i shniya l\u8217'arayot}? Or is it because of Reuven ({\i mishum migareish}), making it Reuven\u8217's daughter\u8217's daughter-in-law? The Gemara gives the same answer \u8212- it is because of Reuven. But this requires a slight digression. The natural extension of {\i shniyot} follows within the same gender \u8212- a son-in-law is biblical, so a son\u8217's son-in-law is rabbinic. The extension to daughter\u8217's son-in-law, crossing genders, is less obvious but nevertheless how the system operates. The answer remains the same in structure: yes, it is a {\i shniya}, but it derives from the biblical relationship with Reuven, not from the rabbinic {\i chalutza} relationship with Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Beno Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then turns to the case of {\i ben bno} \u8212- Shimon\u8217's grandson. From the grandson\u8217's perspective, Rachel would be his grandfather\u8217's wife \u8212- {\i eshes avi aviv} \u8212- a rabbinic {\i shniya}. The Gemara asks whether this prohibition flows from the {\i chalutza} status ({\i mishum choletz}). The Gemara replies: {\i lo mishum mifna} \u8212- no, because through Reuven\u8217's relationship, Rachel is the great-uncle\u8217's wife ({\i eshes achi avi aviv}), which is itself a {\i shniya l\u8217'arayot} derived from the biblical concept of uncle\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimar Machshir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara introduces a critical complication. {\i Ameimar machshir b\u8217'eshes achi avi aviv} \u8212- Ameimar holds that a great-uncle\u8217's wife is not subject to {\i shniyot} at all, and therefore the Reuven-based explanation is unavailable for the {\i ben bno} case. The Gemara acknowledges that Ameimar\u8217's position cannot be rescued by pushing things up a generation, as was done in earlier cases, when working within Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's {\i beraita}. Therefore the Gemara concludes: {\i Ameimar mokim lah mishum choletz} \u8212- Ameimar is forced to ground the prohibition in Shimon\u8217's {\i chalutza} relationship to Rachel, {\i v\u8217'kasavar gazru shniyot b\u8217'chalutza} \u8212- and Ameimar would have to concede that {\i shniyot} do apply to the {\i chalutza} relationship. This proves the point for Ameimar\u8217's position, at least.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses further with the case of {\i ben bito} \u8212- the daughter\u8217's son. From the perspective of this descendant, Rachel would be his mother\u8217's father\u8217's wife. The Gemara attempts the same Reuven-based explanation: she is the wife of his mother\u8217's father\u8217's brother ({\i eshes achi avi imo}). But the Gemara then recognizes that a mother\u8217's father\u8217's brother\u8217's wife is simply not recognized as a {\i shniya l\u8217'arayot} \u8212- {\i shniyot de\u8217'erva lo gazru} for that relationship. Therefore the Reuven-based explanation fails entirely, and the prohibition can only be grounded in the {\i chalutza} relationship with Shimon. {\i Shema mina gazru shniyot b\u8217'chalutza shema mina} \u8212- this establishes definitively that {\i shniyot} do apply to the {\i chalutza} relationship.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzaras Krovat Chalutza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutar vs.\u160 ?Assur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes with the rule: {\i mutar adam b\u8217'krovos tzaras chalutzaso} \u8212- a man is permitted to marry the relatives of the co-wife ({\i tzara}) of his {\i chalutza}. To understand this, consider the setup: Reuven was married to both Rachel and Devorah. Reuven dies. Shimon performs {\i chalitza} with Rachel. Rachel thereby acquires {\i chalutza} status with respect to Shimon, making her relatives forbidden to him. But Devorah, the co-wife, never underwent {\i chalitza} \u8212- the act of {\i chalitza} was performed only with Rachel. Since the {\i chalutza} status attaches only to the woman with whom the act was performed, Devorah is not Shimon\u8217's {\i chalutza}, and her relatives remain permissible to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haba al Tzarat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this background, Rav Tuvi bar Kisna citing Shmuel teaches: {\i haba al tzarat chalutza, havlad mamzer} \u8212- if Shimon goes ahead and has sexual relations with Devorah, the child is a {\i mamzer}. The reason: {\i be\u8217'issura kaima} \u8212- she retains her original forbidden status. Devorah was Reuven\u8217's wife. She would have been permitted to Shimon only through the act of {\i yibbum}. Since no act of {\i yibbum} was performed \u8212- and {\i chalitza} was performed to Rachel, not to Devorah \u8212- Devorah remains forbidden as a brother\u8217's wife, and relations with her produce a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef infers from the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that the {\i chalutza} status applies exclusively to the woman who was party to the act. The {\i mishna} says he is permitted to marry Devorah\u8217's relatives. If Devorah herself were considered a {\i chalutza}, one could not explain why her relatives are permissible \u8212- they would be the relatives of a {\i chalutza} and therefore forbidden. It must be, then, that Devorah has no {\i chalutza} status whatsoever. This explains simultaneously two things: why he may marry Devorah\u8217's relatives (she is not his {\i chalutza}), and why relations with Devorah produce a {\i mamzer} (her status as brother\u8217's wife was never altered by the {\i chalitza} performed to Rachel).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A tension arises when the Gemara considers the position of Rabbi Yochanan. Rabbi Yochanan holds that neither the man nor his brothers incur {\i karet} for relations with either the {\i chalutza} herself or with her co-wife \u8212- {\i ein chayavin lo al hachalutza karet v\u8217'lo al tzarata karet}. This implies that Devorah\u8217's status {\i does} change as a consequence of the {\i chalitza} performed to Rachel \u8212- she is no longer considered a biblical {\i eshet ach} \u8212- yet the {\i mishna} still permits the man to marry Devorah\u8217's relatives. This seems contradictory: if her status changes enough to remove {\i karet}, why does she not acquire {\i chalutza} status sufficient to forbid her relatives?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 D\u8217'Oraisa vs.\u160 ?D\u8217'Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's response is instructive: {\i v\u8217'chi tisbara? achot chalutza d\u8217'oraita?} Does anyone think the relative of a {\i chalutza} is a biblical prohibition? Reish Lakish already stated \u8212- {\i kan shana Rabbi: achot gerusha midivrei Torah, achot chalutza midivrei Soferim} \u8212- the sister of a {\i gerusha} is a biblical prohibition, whereas the sister of a {\i chalutza} is only rabbinic. This resolution hinges on distinguishing the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} plane from the {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} plane. On the biblical level, the {\i chalitza} performed to Rachel transforms the status of both Rachel and Devorah \u8212- neither is any longer {\i eshet ach} in the full biblical sense, and {\i karet} no longer applies. But the rabbinic enactment that forbids the relatives of a {\i chalutza} is a separate layer, and that rabbinic enactment is applied only to the woman who was actually party to the act, not to Devorah who was affected incidentally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mai Shna Hai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses the final line of the {\i mishna}: not only is the man permitted with the relatives of his {\i chalutza}\u8217's co-wife ({\i krovos tzaras chalutzaso}), but he is also forbidden with the co-wife of his {\i chalutza}\u8217's relative ({\i tzara} of {\i krovat chalutzato}). The question is: {\i mai shna hai u\u8217'mai shna hai?} \u8212- why the asymmetry? One case is permitted, the other forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where Tosafot offers a fundamentally different reading of the {\i mishna}\u8217's scenario. According to Rashi, the {\i mishna} is discussing a situation where Barak \u8212- a completely external party \u8212- was married to both Leah (the {\i chalutza}\u8217's relative) and some second wife Devorah. But Tosafot finds this logically unsatisfying and the Gemara\u8217's answer strained under Rashi\u8217's reading. Tosafot instead reads the {\i mishna} as dealing with a case of two brothers married to two sisters \u8212- Levi, another brother of Shimon and Reuven, who was married to Leah. Under this reading, the scenario makes immediate sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarat Ervah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once Shimon performed {\i chalitza} with Rachel, Rachel becomes like his ex-wife \u8212- {\i achos chalutza}, which carries the status of a quasi-{\i erva}. Leah, as Rachel\u8217's sister, is therefore a quasi-{\i erva} to Shimon as well. If Levi then dies, Leah falls before Shimon for {\i yibbum}. But Leah is a {\i tzarat erva} \u8212- the co-wife of a quasi-{\i erva} \u8212- and just as {\i tzarat erva} is exempt from {\i yibbum} on the biblical level, this rabbinic analog creates the same exemption. The answer to {\i mai shna hai u\u8217'mai shna hai} is therefore self-evident under Tosafot\u8217's reading: the woman who underwent the act of {\i chalitza} is the source of the {\i chalutza} status; {\i hach d\u8217'azla behada l\u8217'vei dina} \u8212- she who was brought to court and had the act performed with her acquires the full {\i chalutza} status, from which all subsequent prohibitions derive. The co-wife who never came to court, {\i d\u8217'lo azla behada l\u8217'vei dina}, acquires no {\i chalutza} status, so her relatives remain permissible \u8212- and yet, on the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} plane, her own status changes such that relations with her are no longer an {\i issur karet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shomeres Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} opens a new scenario that follows naturally from the Tosafot reading just discussed. {\i Hacholeitz l\u8217'yevimto v\u8217'nasa achiv et achoto} \u8212- a man performed {\i chalitza} with his {\i yevama}, and his brother then married her sister. So Shimon performed {\i chalitza} with Rachel, and Levi comes along and marries Leah, Rachel\u8217's sister. This is precisely the scenario that Tosafot identified in the previous {\i mishna}\u8217's final case. Leah is forbidden to Shimon because she is his {\i chalutza}\u8217's sister \u8212- a rabbinic {\i erva}. But Leah is permissible to Levi, because the {\i chalutza} status created by the {\i chalitza} act applies only between Shimon and Rachel, not between Levi and Rachel. Levi\u8217's ability to marry Leah is thus unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamten\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The complication arises when the {\i mishna} introduces the scenario of {\i shomeres yavam} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibbum}. Here, Rachel was not yet a {\i chalutza} but a {\i shomeres yavam}, waiting for one of the brothers to act, while Levi went ahead and performed {\i kiddushin} with Leah. The problem: if Levi consummates with Leah, he will be having relations with {\i achot zikukato} \u8212- the sister of the woman to whom he has a {\i zika} (levirate bond). The ruling, in the name of Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira, is {\i hamten} \u8212- wait. Levi must wait until Shimon performs either {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitza} with Rachel. Only once Shimon has done something to sever Levi\u8217's {\i zika} to Rachel may Levi proceed to consummate with Leah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once Shimon performs {\i chalitza} or {\i yibbum} with Rachel, or if Rachel herself dies, Levi\u8217's relationship to Rachel is terminated and he may consummate with Leah. These are the uncomplicated resolutions. {\i Chalatz l\u8217'achiv o kinsah \u8212- yichnos et ishto; metah hayevama \u8212- yichnos et ishto.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Met Hayavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tragic scenario is when Shimon himself dies. Now Levi is stuck. Rachel falls before him as a second {\i yevama} through the now-deceased Shimon. What can Levi do? He cannot perform {\i yibbum}, because that would mean having relations with his wife\u8217's sister. He cannot do {\i chalitza} freely and then marry Rachel, because Rachel would then be his {\i chalutza} and Leah is Rachel\u8217's sister \u8212- he would be in the same trap. The ruling: {\i yotzi et ishto b\u8217'get v\u8217'eshet achiv b\u8217'chalitza} \u8212- he must divorce Leah and perform {\i chalitza} with Rachel. Both relationships must be dissolved; he cannot be with either woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wording\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes a textual issue with the {\i mishna}\u8217's transition. When the {\i mishna} moves from the {\i chalutza} case to the case of a genuine {\i gerusha}, it uses the language {\i v\u8217'chen} \u8212- \u8220"and similarly.\u8221" But the cases are not similar at all: if Reuven genuinely divorced Rachel (making her a {\i gerusha}), and Levi then married Leah, Leah would be a full biblical {\i erva} to Levi \u8212- a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} prohibition \u8212- and she would need no {\i chalitza} at all; she is entirely exempt. So {\i v\u8217'chen} is misleading. The Gemara says: read it as {\i aval} \u8212- \u8220"but\u8221" \u8212- indicating a contrast rather than a similarity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorcee vs.\u160 ?Chalutza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the occasion for Reish Lakish\u8217's statement, already cited in an earlier context: {\i kan shana Rabbi: achas gerusha midivrei Torah, achas chalutza midivrei Soferim.} The sister of a {\i gerusha} is forbidden on a biblical level, and therefore Leah in the {\i gerusha} case requires no {\i chalitza} and is entirely exempt. The sister of a {\i chalutza} is forbidden only rabbinically, and therefore even though she may not do {\i yibbum}, she must nonetheless perform {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mesa Ishto Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mahu B\u8217'Yevimto\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now poses the key question arising from the {\i shomeres yavam} scenario: {\i mesa ishto mahu b\u8217'yevimto?} If, after Levi married Leah and created the forbidden status for Rachel, Leah then dies \u8212- what is the law regarding Rachel? We know that in the {\i mishna}\u8217's case, Rachel was initially a {\i shomeres yavam} and therefore permissible to Levi before his marriage to Leah. Marriage to Leah then made Rachel forbidden. But now that Leah is dead, does Rachel revert to her originally permitted status, or does she remain permanently forbidden?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case involves a sequence of three statuses: first permitted (as {\i shomeres yavam}), then forbidden (when Levi married Leah), then potentially permitted again (when Leah dies). The question is whether the intermediate period of prohibition permanently locks in her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav and Rabbi Chanina both hold: {\i mesa ishto, mutar b\u8217'yevimto} \u8212- if his wife dies, he may once again consummate with his {\i yevama}. Rava explains the reasoning: {\i mishum d\u8217'hava yevama she\u8217'hutrah, v\u8217'ne\u8217'esra, v\u8217'chazra v\u8217'hutrah} \u8212- she was a {\i yevama} who was permitted, became forbidden, and then became permitted again; {\i tachzor l\u8217'heiteira harishon} \u8212- she returns to her original status. The foundational identity of a {\i yevama} is determined at the moment of the husband\u8217's death. When Reuven died, Rachel was in no way an {\i erva} to Levi, and that basic, original status can reassert itself once the intervening obstacle is removed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel and Rav Assi disagree: {\i mesa ishto, assur b\u8217'yevimto.} Once forbidden, she remains forbidden \u8212- even though she was initially permitted and only became forbidden subsequently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gimmel Achim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna challenges Rav\u8217's position with the following case: three brothers \u8212- Reuven, Shimon, and Levi \u8212- where Reuven and Shimon are married to two sisters, Rachel and Leah respectively, and Levi is single. Reuven dies. Levi performs a {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel (a quasi-betrothal in the {\i yibbum} context). Then Shimon dies. Now Levi has a {\i zika} to Leah as well, while being quasi-married to Rachel. He cannot consummate with either, as one is his wife\u8217's sister and the other creates the symmetric problem. Then \u8212- and here is the crucial question \u8212- Leah dies. May Levi now consummate {\i yibbum} with Rachel?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomeres Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue, using Rav\u8217's logic, that Rachel was initially permitted when she became a {\i yevama} through Reuven\u8217's death, that she became forbidden only because of Leah, and that now that Leah is gone, she should revert to her permitted status. But closer examination reveals the cases are not parallel. In the original case, Rachel was a {\i shomeres yavam} and thus permissible to Levi {\i before} he married Leah. It was only Levi\u8217's act of marrying Leah that then made Rachel forbidden. There was a genuine prior permitted state from Levi\u8217's perspective. In Rav Hamnuna\u8217's case, by contrast, when Leah became a {\i yevama} through Shimon\u8217's death, Levi had already performed a {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel. At the very moment Leah entered {\i yevama} status, she was already the sister of someone who was quasi-his-wife. She was {\i never} permissible from the moment she became a {\i yevama}. Therefore \u8212- {\i otah yevamah choletzet v\u8217'lo mityabemet} \u8212- she may only perform {\i chalitza}. This seems to prove that even Rav would agree: a {\i yevama} who was never permitted cannot revert to a permitted state, and here there is nothing to revert {\i to}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After this exchange, Rav was silenced \u8212- {\i ishtik} \u8212- apparently conceding the point. But after leaving, he reflected and said: why didn\u8217't I invoke Rabbi Eliezer? {\i Amai lo amart lei, Rabbi Elazar hi} \u8212- that case in the {\i beraita} is the position of Rabbi Eliezer, who holds {\i keivan she-amdah alav sha\u8217'ah achat be-issur, ne\u8217'esrah alav le-olam} \u8212- once a woman stood before him in a forbidden status for even one moment, she is forbidden to him forever. He could have said: I do not rule like Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- I rule that she may revert to being permitted \u8212- and you have cited a {\i beraita} that follows Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's stringent position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerusha Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then he reconsidered further: could I actually have said it was Rabbi Eliezer? When does Rabbi Eliezer say once forbidden, always forbidden? The case of Rabbi Eliezer is as follows: Reuven married Rachel, then divorced her. At that point, Rachel became a biblical {\i erva} to Shimon \u8212- a divorced brother\u8217's wife, without any context of {\i yibbum} whatsoever. Reuven then remarried Rachel. Reuven dies. Does Rachel now come before Shimon as a {\i yevama}, or does her earlier status as his brother\u8217's {\i gerusha} \u8212- which occurred outside any {\i yibbum} framework \u8212- permanently bar her? Rabbi Eliezer says yes, once she was forbidden to him, she remains forever forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Chazya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical limiting principle is: {\i heicha d\u8217'lo chazya bi-she\u8217'at nefilah} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's stringency applies where the woman was never in a permissible {\i yevama} status at the moment she became a {\i yevama}. In the {\i gerusha} case, when Reuven died and she became a {\i yevama}, her prior history of having been a divorced brother\u8217's wife outside the {\i yibbum} context meant she entered {\i yevama} status already tainted. But in Rav\u8217's case \u8212- the case of the {\i mishna} \u8212- when Reuven died and Rachel became a {\i shomeres yavam}, she was entirely permissible. The question is only whether a subsequent intervening prohibition (Levi\u8217's marriage to Leah) can permanently bar her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Status Locks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So Rav Hamnuna\u8217's challenge ultimately resolves differently from what it first appeared. In the three-brothers case, Leah was never permissible {\i from the moment of her entry into yevama status} \u8212- and even Rav might agree she remains forbidden. In the original case, Rachel {\i was} permissible at the moment of entry into {\i yevama} status, and once Leah dies the question is whether she can revert. Rav says yes; Shmuel and Rav Assi say no. The {\i beraita} of Rabbi Eliezer cannot resolve this debate because Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's principle is limited to women who never had a permissible {\i yevama} status to begin with.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlosh Chodashim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} introduces an entirely new topic: {\i ha-yevama lo tachlot v\u8217'lo tityabem ad she-yeish la shlosha chodashim} \u8212- the {\i yevama} may not perform {\i chalitza} nor may she consummate {\i yibbum} for three months after the death of the husband. The concern, in a world without pregnancy testing, is to establish who the father of any child will be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Hanashim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This rule is extended to all women whose marriages have ended: {\i v\u8217'chen kol sh\u8217'ar nashim} \u8212- widows and divorcees alike must wait three months before entering any new marital relationship. {\i Lo ye\u8217'arsu v\u8217'lo yinasu ad she-yeish lahem shlosha chodashim} \u8212- they may neither perform {\i kiddushin} nor proceed to {\i nisuin} during this period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Ye\u8217'arsu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The extension to {\i kiddushin} is notable, since {\i kiddushin} without {\i nisuin} involves no sexual relations and would seem to pose no risk of paternity confusion. This prohibition on {\i kiddushin} is a kind of {\i lo plug} \u8212- a blanket rule that does not differentiate \u8212- because once you permit {\i kiddushin} for some women immediately, you risk confusion about which women may and may not proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Plug\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i tanna} draws no distinctions among women: {\i echad betulot v\u8217'echad be\u8217'ulot, echad gerushos v\u8217'echad almanos, echad nesuas v\u8217'echad arusos} \u8212- whether a virgin or a non-virgin, whether divorced or widowed, whether the first marriage involved full {\i nisuin} or only {\i eirusin}, all must wait three months. Even a woman who was only an {\i arusa} to her first husband \u8212- with no sexual contact possible \u8212- and who is now only about to be an {\i arusa} to her second husband \u8212- still involving no sexual contact \u8212- must wait. This double extension to cases where the concern is entirely theoretical represents a deliberate {\i lo plug}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda takes a more calibrated position. Women who had been {\i nesuous} to their first husbands \u8212- where sex was possible and the pregnancy concern is real \u8212- must wait before proceeding to {\i nisuin} with their second husbands, but they may at least enter {\i kiddushin} in the interim, since {\i kiddushin} alone does not permit sexual relations. Women who had only been {\i arusot} to their first husbands \u8212- where sex was not permitted \u8212- may proceed directly to {\i nisuin} with their second husbands, since there is no possibility they are pregnant. He does not accept the {\i lo plug} reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusa B\u8217'Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's single exception is the {\i arusa} from Yehuda \u8212- {\i mipnei shelibo gas bah} \u8212- because in Yehuda the practice was for the {\i arus} and {\i arusa} to spend private time together before {\i nisuin}, becoming comfortable with one another. This private time occasionally led to sexual relations, meaning {\i arusot} in Yehuda could in fact be pregnant. Therefore they must also wait, and may not even enter {\i kiddushin} with their next partner during the three-month period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi agrees with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's general framework \u8212- that {\i kiddushin} is permissible for all women during the three months \u8212- but adds one exception for a different reason entirely: {\i chutz min ha\u8217'almana mipnei ha\u8217'eivul} \u8212- a widow must wait because of the {\i aveilus} mourning period. Even setting aside the pregnancy concern, Rabbi Yosi holds that propriety requires a widow to complete her mourning before taking a new partner, and therefore she may not even perform {\i kiddushin} until the {\i aveilus} has concluded.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shma Yehei Velad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Kayama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now analyzes the {\i mishna}\u8217's reasoning. The most obvious question is: why may the {\i yevama} not do either {\i chalitza} or {\i yibbum} for three months? The concern is {\i shema yehei velad ben kayama} \u8212- perhaps there is a viable child in utero. If there is, no action is necessary or appropriate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eshet Ach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i yibbum}, the concern is particularly weighty. If the woman is pregnant and the child is viable, there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum}, and performing {\i yibbum} would constitute a violation of the biblical prohibition of {\i eshet ach} \u8212- {\i vekapaga be\u8217'issur eshet ach de\u8217'oraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karoz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why should {\i chalitza} be delayed? Surely {\i chalitza} cannot violate a biblical prohibition even if a child exists. The {\i mishna} must be implying that {\i chalitzas me\u8217'uberes} \u8212- {\i chalitza} with a pregnant woman \u8212- does not take effect. This, indeed, was the subject of an earlier dispute, where Rabbi Yochanan held that {\i chalitzo me\u8217'uberes shema chalitza} \u8212- it does take effect. Our {\i mishna}\u8217's prohibition on {\i chalitza} within three months would then seem to contradict Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position. The Gemara notes that Rabbi Yochanan has already been challenged on this point before, and piles on this {\i mishna} as an additional proof against him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers Rabbi Yochanan a defense. Even if {\i chalitza} with a pregnant woman is technically valid, we still prohibit it {\i lechatchila} for a practical reason: {\i shema yehei velad ben kayama} \u8212- perhaps the child is viable, meaning {\i chalitza} was not needed at all \u8212- {\i venimtzesa ata matzricha karoz l\u8217'kehuna} \u8212- and then a retraction must be announced, correcting the public impression that she has become a {\i chalutza} (which would disqualify her from marrying a {\i kohen}). The Gemara proposes: just make the announcement. The Gemara responds: {\i dilma ika d\u8217'havai bachalitza v\u8217'lo havai bahachraza} \u8212- perhaps some who were present at the {\i chalitza} ceremony were not present at the retraction, and they will go about saying she is disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen} without having heard the correction. Like a retraction printed in small type the following day, the announcement does not reach everyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question: even granting that concern, why does the three-month waiting period apply to a {\i grusha}? If the woman had already been divorced from a previous marriage before marrying Reuven, she is already disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen} regardless of any {\i chalitza} status. The concern about an uncorrected {\i karoz} therefore does not apply. So why cannot Shimon proceed to {\i chalitza} with her immediately?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezonos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer reframes the entire rationale: {\i mishum d\u8217'mapslu lah mimezonos} \u8212- because performing {\i chalitza} prematurely would deprive the woman of her {\i mezonos}, the financial support she is entitled to receive from the deceased husband\u8217's estate during the waiting period. Before {\i chalitza} is performed, she is supported by the estate. Once {\i chalitza} is performed, that entitlement ends. The Gemara is therefore saying: regardless of the {\i kohen} concern, there is an independent reason not to perform {\i chalitza} too early \u8212- it removes her from the estate\u8217's support before the appropriate time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses further: this works for a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} \u8212- a woman who was in full {\i nisuin} and therefore entitled to {\i mezonos} from the estate. But what about an {\i arusa} who had only {\i kiddushin} and is therefore not entitled to {\i mezonos}, and who was also a {\i gerusha} from a previous marriage? The {\i kohen} concern does not apply (already disqualified), and the {\i mezonos} concern does not apply (she has no entitlement). Why must she wait?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oleh Leyibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's final answer invokes the principle of Rabbi Yosi: {\i ella mishum d\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- because of Rabbi Yosi\u8217's teaching. A {\i ma\u8217'aseh} is cited in which someone came before Rabbi Yosi and asked whether he could perform {\i chalitza} within the three months. Rabbi Yosi said no. The petitioner pressed: {\i v\u8217'sachloz umah bekach?} \u8212- what would be the harm? Rabbi Yosi responded by citing the verse \u8212- {\i im lo yachpotz}, \u8220"if he does not wish\u8221" \u8212- implying that the alternative of {\i yibbum} must be a genuine option: {\i ha im chafetz, yabem}. This leads to the principle: {\i kol ha\u8217'oleh leyibum oleh lechaltiza v\u8217'kol she\u8217'eino oleh leyibum eino oleh lechaltiza} \u8212- whoever is eligible for {\i yibbum} is eligible for {\i chalitza}, and whoever is ineligible for {\i yibbum} is ineligible for {\i chalitza}. We perform {\i chalitza} only where {\i yibbum} would also have been a valid option. Since during the first three months, {\i yibbum} is not a valid option \u8212- the child may be viable \u8212- {\i chalitza} is likewise not performed. Rabbi Yosi\u8217's principle thus provides a conceptual ground for the three-month restriction that applies even where the {\i mezonos} and {\i kohen} concerns are absent. As Rashi observes, this principle holds even in cases where {\i yibbum} is ultimately forbidden rabbinically, so long as {\i chalitza} is performed only when {\i yibbum} could theoretically be an option \u8212- that is, {\i lechatchila}, we confine {\i chalitza} to contexts where the {\i yibbum} option genuinely exists.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 42\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three-Month Wait\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Month Wait Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens on 42b, picking up approximately five lines from the top, with the Mishnah\u8217's statement {\i achas besulos v\u8217'achas beulos} \u8212- whether the woman is a virgin or a non-virgin, whether her first marriage was {\i eirusin} or {\i nissuin}, she must wait three months before remarrying. The rationale is the familiar principle of {\i lo plug}: even in cases where logic would seem to confirm that pregnancy is impossible, we do not make distinctions. The Gemara then opens by asking why the Mishnah needed to list both categories of virgin/{\i arusa} and non-virgin/{\i nesuah} separately, since they appear to overlap entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Virgin/Non-Virgin Distin\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- what the Mishnah is saying is that the categories of virgin and non-virgin simply map onto the categories of {\i eirusin} and {\i nissuin}, respectively. They are not independent variables but rather explanatory: women widowed or divorced from {\i eirusin} and women widowed or divorced from {\i nissuin}. The two terms elaborate the same two categories rather than introducing additional ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following this, we are told that {\i Rabbi Eliezer} did not come to the {\i beis medrash} on a particular occasion. He later encountered {\i Rabbi Assi} and inquired what had been taught. Rabbi Assi reported: {\i hachi amar Rabbi Yochanan, halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yossi} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan ruled that we follow Rabbi Yossi, who holds that we do {\i not} apply {\i lo plug}. Both Rabbi Yossi and Rabbi Yehuda agree that the three-month wait is only necessary when the first marriage involved {\i nissuin} and the new marriage will also be {\i nissuin} \u8212- i.e., when there was an opportunity for marital relations at both ends. If either stage is merely {\i eirusin}, there is no concern, and Rabbi Yossi adds a separate point regarding {\i aveilus} that we will address later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Plug as Daas Yachid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling prompts the Gemara to observe: {\i mikhlal d\u8217'yechida pligi alei} \u8212- does this imply that the {\i stam} voice in the Mishnah, which demands the three-month wait universally and thus represents the position of {\i lo plug}, is in fact not the consensus but a {\i daas yachid}, a single minority opinion? The Gemara answers: {\i in} \u8212- yes, and it can be demonstrated from a {\i braisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pregnancy-Impossible Cas\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} enumerates a long series of cases in which we can effectively presume that the woman is not pregnant, yet the requirement to wait three months nonetheless applies. Among these: a woman who was always running off to her father\u8217's house during the marriage ({\i redufa l\u8217'beis aviha}), a woman who was in open conflict with her husband ({\i katata im ba\u8217'ala}), a woman whose husband was imprisoned, or was elderly, or was sick, or who was herself ill.\u160 ?Additionally: a woman who suffered a miscarriage after her husband\u8217's death, a woman who was infertile ({\i akara}), a woman who was post-menopausal ({\i zkeina}), a woman who was too young, a woman born incapable of bearing children ({\i ailonis}), or any woman for whatever reason incapable of giving birth. In all of these cases, {\i Rabbi Meir} says she must wait three months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- and some have the variant reading {\i Rabbi Yossi}, since both make a similar distinction \u8212- says: {\i matir l\u8217'areis v\u8217'linasei miyad}, she may perform {\i eirusin} immediately, and in these specific cases where pregnancy is impossible, she may even proceed to {\i nissuin} immediately. The {\i lo plug} position is thus identified as the view of {\i Rabbi Meir} alone \u8212- a {\i daas yachid} \u8212- which is why Rabbi Yochanan was able to rule against it, following Rabbi Yehuda or Rabbi Yossi, who permit immediate {\i nissuin} in circumstances where pregnancy is demonstrably impossible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yochanan Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazar Bo Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Abba then reports: {\i chazar bo Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan reversed himself. He now reverts to {\i lo plug} and holds that the three-month wait is always required. Rav Yosef explains the reason for this reversal: {\i mi-misneisa d\u8217'karma hadar bei} \u8212- he reversed himself on account of the {\i braisa} from {\i Kerem B\u8217'Yavne}. As the {\i braisa} records, Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka testified: I heard from the sages gathered in {\i Kerem B\u8217'Yavne} \u8212- the seat of rabbinic authority established after the destruction of the Temple, so called because the sages sat in rows like a vineyard \u8212- {\i kulam tzrichin l\u8217'hamtin shlosha chodashim}, all women, without exception, must wait three months. No distinction is made based on the possibility or impossibility of pregnancy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Mishna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya then instructs Rabbi Zeira: when you appear before {\i Rabbi Abbahu}, present him with the following contradiction. On one hand, Rabbi Yochanan ruled {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yossi}, following the opinion that we do not apply {\i lo plug}. On the other hand, Rabbi Yochanan is the one who established the principle that {\i halacha k\u8217'stam mishna} \u8212- we rule in accordance with the anonymous, unattributed Mishnah. And our Mishnah states {\i stam}, without attribution, that all women must wait three months regardless of whether they are virgins or not. How could Rabbi Yochanan have originally ruled against that {\i stam}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu\u8217's response is sharp: {\i lo chash l\u8217'kimchei} \u8212- the one who raised that question has not ground his flour properly; it is an unworthy question. The case before us is not a simple {\i stam mishna} \u8212- it is a case of {\i stam v\u8217'acharav machlokes}: the Mishnah first presents an anonymous ruling and then immediately records a dispute. Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yossi disagree right there in the very same Mishnah. When Rabbi Yochanan says we rule according to the {\i stam mishna}, that principle applies only when the {\i stam} stands uncontested in its own context, while dissenting opinions are found elsewhere \u8212- in other {\i mishnayos} or in a {\i braisa}. But when the Mishnah itself voices the disagreement immediately after the {\i stam}, that is a case of {\i stam v\u8217'acharav machlokes}, and that configuration carries different weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam V\u8217'Achar Machloket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The governing rule is stated explicitly: {\i machlokes v\u8217'acharakav stam, halacha k\u8217'stam} \u8212- if a dispute appears first and is then followed by a {\i stam}, we rule according to the {\i stam}. But {\i stam v\u8217'acharakav machlokes, ein halacha k\u8217'stam} \u8212- if the {\i stam} comes first and is then followed by a dispute, we do not necessarily rule according to the {\i stam}. This does not necessarily mean we rule against the {\i stam}, but it does mean we are not obligated to follow it in the way we would follow an unchallenged {\i stam}. This is why Rabbi Yochanan was initially free to rule like Rabbi Yossi, notwithstanding the {\i stam} in the Mishnah. When he subsequently reversed himself, it was due to the independent authority of the {\i braisa} from {\i Kerem B\u8217'Yavne}, not because of the {\i stam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle \u8212- that {\i stam} followed by {\i machlokes} does not carry binding force \u8212- has broad significance well beyond our {\i sugya}. The same hermeneutic logic applies to the interpretation of the {\i Shulchan Aruch}: when the {\i Shulchan Aruch} states a ruling anonymously and then introduces {\i v\u8217'yesh omrim}, there is a developed jurisprudence around which opinion is operative, parallel to the Talmudic questions of {\i stam v\u8217'yesh omrim} and {\i yesh omrim v\u8217'stam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the Gemara had earlier identified the {\i stam} voice in this Mishnah as representing {\i Rabbi Meir}. This observation is appropriate, since {\i stam mishna aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Meir} \u8212- the anonymous Mishnah generally reflects Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. The Gemara\u8217's point that the {\i stam} is really a {\i daas yachid} carries extra weight here, since every {\i stam} is in some sense traceable to Rabbi Meir rather than to a genuine consensus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam/Machloket Configs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu\u8217's attendant ({\i shammash}) uses their time together \u8212- as Rabbi Abbahu was being supported on the attendant\u8217's shoulders and the attendant was gathering {\i halachos} from him \u8212- to work through several configurations of {\i stam} and {\i machlokes} across different sources. The attendant asks: {\i machlokes v\u8217'achar kach stam, mai?} \u8212- if the dispute appears first and the {\i stam} follows, what do we say? Rabbi Abbahu answers: {\i halacha k\u8217'stam}, we rule according to the {\i stam}. And if the {\i stam} appears first and the {\i machlokes} follows? {\i Ein halacha k\u8217'stam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then the question of cross-source configurations: {\i stam} in the Mishnah, {\i machlokes} in a {\i braisa} \u8212- what is the rule? {\i Halacha k\u8217'stam}, still follow the {\i stam}. If anything, this is even clearer than the case where the debate is embedded within the Mishnah itself. What about the reverse \u8212- {\i machlokes} in the Mishnah and {\i stam} in a {\i braisa}? Rabbi Abbahu answers: {\i v\u8217'chi Rabbi lo shna\u8217'ah, Rabbi Chiya m\u8217'na lei} \u8212- if Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi chose not to codify that position as a {\i stam} in the Mishnah, on what basis would Rabbi Chiya\u8217's transmission of it as a {\i stam} in the {\i braisa} override that? The Mishnah carries supreme authority; the compiler\u8217's editorial choices are themselves normative signals. The fact that Rabbi chose not to present a given opinion as an uncontested ruling in the Mishnah cannot simply be overridden by Rabbi Chiya\u8217's different editorial decision in the {\i braisa}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Combs Digression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flax/Wool Combs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The attendant now challenges Rabbi Abbahu\u8217's principle by producing a {\i stam mishna} that everyone knows is not ruled upon in practice. The Mishnah in question concerns combs \u8212- specifically, flax combs and wool combs \u8212- and whether they remain susceptible to {\i tumah} when their teeth have been broken off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevant Mishnah teaches: regarding a flax comb ({\i masreika shel pishtan}) whose teeth have been removed \u8212- if two teeth remain together ({\i nishtayaru vo shtayim}), the comb is {\i tamei}, meaning it retains its status as a functional vessel and can become {\i tamei}. If only one tooth remains ({\i achas}), it is {\i tehorah}, since a comb with a single tooth is no longer functionally a comb and loses its {\i kli} status. As for the individual teeth themselves that break off one by one, they are {\i temeios} \u8212- susceptible to {\i tumah} \u8212- because a single tooth can be repurposed as something like a toothpick.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then turns to a wool comb ({\i shel tzemer}): if teeth are missing such that there is a gap of one between every two remaining teeth ({\i achas mibein shtayim}), it is {\i tehorah}. If three teeth remain in one place ({\i nishtayaru vo gimel bemakom echad}), it is {\i tamei}. If the outermost tooth is one of those three, it is {\i tehorah}, since the outer teeth are less functionally significant than the inner ones. If two teeth broke off together and can serve as a tweezer-like implement, they are {\i temeios}. If a single tooth was fashioned into a lamp cleaner or thread-stretcher ({\i tikkena l\u8217'ner o l\u8217'misuach}), it is {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point of invoking this Mishnah is this: {\i dekayma lan de\u8217'ein halacha k\u8217'zos hamishna} \u8212- we know that the {\i halacha} is not like this Mishnah. Here is an apparently uncontested {\i stam mishna} that is nonetheless not followed. How then can one maintain that we always rule according to a {\i stam mishna}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara does not immediately accept the attendant\u8217's challenge at face value, however, and instead probes whether the Mishnah is internally consistent at all. The section on the wool comb appears contradictory: one clause implies that two teeth together render the comb {\i tamei}, while the concluding clause states that only three together do so. The Gemara asks: {\i umai kashya} \u8212- is this truly a contradiction? Perhaps the two clauses refer to different types of teeth \u8212- inner teeth versus outer teeth ({\i gavyaysa} vs.\u160 ?{\i varaysa}), as Rashi explains in terms of a two-tiered comb structure where the outer teeth scrape and the inner teeth collect. The outer teeth require three in one place to constitute a functional configuration; the inner teeth require only two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then pursues a different internal contradiction between the flax and wool sections. By the flax comb, individual broken teeth are {\i temeios} without any modification \u8212- it is assumed they can naturally serve as toothpicks. By the wool comb, a single tooth is {\i tamei} only {\i if} it was modified and fashioned into a usable instrument. That appears contradictory. The Gemara proposes: perhaps one speaks of a tooth that broke off with a piece of the base still attached ({\i b\u8217'kataya}) and one of a tooth that broke off cleanly without the base ({\i b\u8217'lo kataya}) \u8212- the tooth with a base-piece has a natural handle and needs no modification, while the bare tooth requires fashioning. Rav Papa offers yet another resolution: perhaps the difference is between thin teeth and thick teeth ({\i katinta} vs.\u160 ?{\i alimta}), where the thinner ones naturally lend themselves to reuse while the thicker ones do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Comb Mishna Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After exhausting these resolutions, the Gemara concedes that the internal contradictions can in fact be resolved \u8212- the Mishnah may not be genuinely problematic at all. But then the Gemara discloses the true reason we do not rule like this Mishnah: {\i mishum demasaymi bei dokanay, zu divrei Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- those who are precise about the text ({\i dokanai}) conclude the Mishnah with the words {\i zu divrei Rabbi Shimon}: \u8220"these are the words of Rabbi Shimon.\u8221" It is therefore not a true {\i stam mishna} in the first place. Other textual versions attribute the ruling explicitly to Rabbi Shimon, and once the opinion carries an attribution, the rule of following the {\i stam} does not apply. The attendant\u8217's challenge thus collapses: this is not a case where a genuine {\i stam mishna} is overridden \u8212- it is a case where the {\i stam} is more apparent than real. Certain manuscripts and precise transmitters record it with explicit attribution, and that suffices to classify it as a named opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This entire digression \u8212- remarkable in how many distinct sub-questions it generates about combs and tumah \u8212- is in the end a vehicle for clarifying the limits and the reliability of the principle of {\i halacha k\u8217'stam mishna}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Day 90 vs.\u160 ?Day 91\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eirusin Within 3 Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the main topic of the three-month wait, the Gemara records a ruling transmitted by {\i Rabbi Yitzchak bar Avin}: {\i ma\u8217'arsin toch gimel} \u8212- one may perform {\i eirusin} within the three-month period. This reflects the approach of Rabbi Yehuda, who holds that {\i eirusin} does not place the woman in a context where she could become pregnant, and therefore does not trigger the waiting requirement. Furthermore, {\i v\u8217'chein osin ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- this is the operative practice. Rabbi Elazar is also cited as having taught similarly in the name of {\i Rabbi Chanina hagadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Associated with this position is the teaching that {\i rubo shel rishon, v\u8217'emtza\u8217'i mishalem, v\u8217'ruvo shel shelishi} \u8212- the majority of the first month, the entirety of the middle month, and the majority of the third month would suffice as the requisite waiting period. This formulation is striking, and neither Rashi nor Tosafot engage with it directly in the context of the Gemara\u8217's discussion. One possible reading is that this opinion does not accept the Gemara\u8217's earlier principle that a viable birth can occur after six months and two days of gestation \u8212- perhaps this view requires a full seven months. If so, then waiting two months plus a portion of a third would be sufficient to rule out the earliest possible birth being attributed to the deceased husband. This remains somewhat unclear from the Gemara\u8217's framing. Tosafot suggests this lenient calculation applies only to the case of an {\i arusah} \u8212- it represents not a complete waiver of the three-month period but a more flexible definition of how those three months are measured.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amimar vs.\u160 ?Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ameimar} went further and ruled that one may even perform {\i eirusin} on day ninety itself \u8212- the ninetieth day counting from the husband\u8217's death. This reflects a measure of leniency in defining the conclusion of the three-month period, stopping short of requiring a full ninety-one days. Rav Ashi challenged this position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav/Shmuel Exclusion Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi invoked the joint ruling of {\i Rav and Shmuel}, who both stated: {\i tzricha l\u8217'hamtin gimel chodashim, chutz mi\u8217'yom she\u8217'meis bo v\u8217'chutz mi\u8217'yom she\u8217'nis\u8217'arsah bo} \u8212- the woman must wait three months, not including the day of the husband\u8217's death and not including the day of the {\i eirusin}. The practical implication is that the {\i eirusin} must be performed on day ninety-one, not day ninety. Ameimar attempted to deflect this: he argued that the citation from Rav and Shmuel was not about the three-month wait at all, but about the twenty-four-month nursing period \u8212- the rule that a nursing mother must wait twenty-four months before remarrying, {\i chutz mi\u8217'yom she\u8217'nolad bo v\u8217'chutz mi\u8217'yom she\u8217'nis\u8217'arsah bo}, not counting the day of the birth and not counting the day of the {\i eirusin}, so that the wedding takes place on the twenty-fifth month. Rav Ashi rejected this reading: the formulation applies to both contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final: 3 Months Always\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then records a case involving someone who made a {\i seudat erusin} \u8212- a betrothal feast \u8212- on the ninetieth day; {\i Rava} voided the event and required them to wait until the following day. The Gemara notes that this case was actually a {\i seudat nissuin}, a full marriage feast, not merely {\i eirusin} \u8212- and naturally, for {\i nissuin}, day ninety-one is unambiguously required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i hilchata} \u8212- the authoritative conclusion \u8212- is stated: for the nursing mother\u8217's twenty-four-month wait, not counting the day of the birth and not counting the day of {\i kiddushin}, so the wedding falls on the twenty-fifth month. For the three-month remarriage wait, not counting the day of death and not counting the day of {\i eirusin}, so {\i eirusin} falls on day ninety-one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most significant point of the conclusion, beyond the day-ninety versus day-ninety-one question, is that {\i lo plug} is fully in force: even {\i eirusin} requires a complete three-month wait. The discussions of leniency \u8212- whether to permit {\i eirusin} within three months, or to count day ninety as sufficient \u8212- are consistently ruled against. The final position is unequivocal: three months are required across the board, including for {\i eirusin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pregnancy Testing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pregnancy Test Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might conclude from the {\i lo plug} ruling that modern pregnancy tests cannot satisfy the requirement \u8212- that we always demand three months regardless. But this may not follow. The Gemara itself, in earlier portions of the {\i sugya}, entertained the possibility of physical tests to establish the woman\u8217's status. That suggests the underlying concern is not three months for its own sake, but rather a reliable determination of whether she is pregnant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Understood this way, there are two distinct questions. The first is whether we require {\i some} means of determination in all cases. The second is whether the only valid means is the three-month wait, or whether an alternative means \u8212- such as a modern pregnancy test \u8212- would be equally acceptable. There is a coherent argument that {\i lo plug} operates on the first question, not necessarily on the second. The rationale of {\i lo plug} is not that we genuinely believe a post-menopausal woman might be pregnant; rather, we do not wish to create distinctions between cases, lest some genuine cases of potential pregnancy slip through undetected. If that is the concern, then {\i lo plug} demands that we require {\i a} determination in all cases \u8212- but the determination itself need not be limited to the passage of three months. A reliable pregnancy test, even for a woman where pregnancy is biologically impossible, might satisfy the requirement under this logic: we require the test universally, but we allow the test to substitute for the three-month wait. Whether {\i poskim} in fact permit this is a {\i machlokes haposkim}, and the question is live.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eirusin/Aveilus Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yossi Widow Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the second dimension of Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position: his exception for widows. As the Mishnah records, even if {\i eirusin} is otherwise permissible within the three-month period \u8212- because {\i eirusin} does not place the woman in a context of marital relations \u8212- a widow ({\i almanah}) may not enter into {\i eirusin} during the first month, because she is still in {\i aveilus} for her deceased husband. The paradigmatic case involves a woman who was {\i nesuah} to her first husband, who died; she is in {\i aveilus} for him. She is now going to perform {\i eirusin} with a second man. Since the new relationship is only {\i eirusin} \u8212- not yet {\i nissuin} \u8212- there is no concern of pregnancy attribution. But the first month of {\i aveilus} for the deceased husband still applies, and during that period, the {\i eirusin} cannot take place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The complication that the Gemara will work through is this: the assumption that there is no {\i aveilus} for a relationship that was merely {\i eirusin} is universal and undisputed. If the deceased husband and this woman were only {\i arusim} \u8212- never {\i nesuim} \u8212- she would not be in {\i aveilus} at all. So Rabbi Yossi\u8217's exception must be addressing a case where the first marriage was {\i nissuin}, meaning she genuinely is in {\i aveilus}, and the new relationship is going to be {\i eirusin}, which is why there is no pregnancy concern \u8212- but the {\i aveilus} concern remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal V\u8217'Chomer Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} challenges this by means of a {\i kal vachomer}: if during a period when laundering is forbidden \u8212- such as the first week of {\i shiva} \u8212- {\i eirusin} is nonetheless permitted, then certainly during the rest of the month of {\i aveilus}, when laundering {\i is} permitted, {\i eirusin} should be permitted. The {\i kal vachomer} rests on the premise that {\i eirusin} does not constitute the kind of {\i simcha} that is prohibited during {\i aveilus} \u8212- only {\i nissuin} does. It is well established, after all, that one may perform {\i kiddushin} even on {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} itself, as {\i Shmuel} rules based on the concern {\i shema yekadmena acher} \u8212- lest someone else preempt him in betrothing the woman. If {\i kiddushin} is permitted on {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}, how can it be restricted during the less stringent later period of personal {\i aveilus}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tisha B\u8217'Av Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara probes the sources underlying this {\i kal vachomer}. A Mishnah teaches that during the week in which {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} falls ({\i shavua she\u8217'chal bo Tisha B\u8217'Av}), one may not get a haircut or do laundry. A {\i braisa} then states that beginning from {\i Rosh Chodesh Av}, the public limits its commerce, building, and planting \u8212- but {\i ma\u8217'arsin}, {\i kiddushin} may be performed. One may not, however, bring the woman into the home ({\i lo konsin}) \u8212- {\i nissuin} is prohibited. Nor may one hold a {\i seudat erusin}, a betrothal feast, though the act of {\i kiddushin} itself is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then complicates the {\i kal vachomer}: perhaps the {\i braisa}\u8217's phrase \u8220"before this time\u8221" ({\i kodem hazman hazeh}) does not mean during {\i Rosh Chodesh Av} in anticipation of {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}, but rather before the week of {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} itself \u8212- and during the actual week of {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}, even {\i kiddushin} is forbidden. Rava responds: {\i v\u8217'kodem d\u8217'kodem nami kal v\u8217'chomer} \u8212- even if {\i kiddushin} is forbidden during the week of {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}, I can reconstruct the {\i kal vachomer} using the earlier period. During the nine days when business is restricted ({\i mishenikhnas Av mema\u8217'atin b\u8217'simcha}), {\i kiddushin} is still permitted. Certainly then, during the month of personal {\i aveilus} when business is {\i not} restricted, {\i kiddushin} should be permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Flip Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara shifts strategy. The {\i kal vachomer} appears compelling \u8212- {\i eirusin} seems like it should always be permitted during {\i aveilus}. The Gemara therefore concludes that it must reread Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position entirely. Rather than accepting the plain reading of the Mishnah \u8212- that Rabbi Yossi says {\i all women may do eirusin} except for the {\i almanah} who is in {\i aveilus} \u8212- the Gemara proposes: {\i lo teima d\u8217'Rabbi Yossi omer kol hanashim ye\u8217'arsu; ella eima kol hanashim yinashu} \u8212- do not read it as saying all women may do {\i eirusin}; read it instead as saying all women may do {\i nissuin}, except for the {\i almanah} due to her {\i aveilus}. On this reading, {\i eirusin} is entirely unmentioned and unproblematic; it is {\i nissuin} that Rabbi Yossi is discussing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately asks: but {\i nissuin} is a problem for all women during the three-month waiting period \u8212- why would the {\i almanah} be singled out? The answer: {\i leit lei l\u8217'Rabbi Yossi l\u8217'havchana} \u8212- Rabbi Yossi is not concerned with establishing paternity. He does not require the three-month wait in principle. So for Rabbi Yossi, {\i nissuin} is generally permitted immediately after widowhood or divorce; the only constraint is {\i aveilus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative formulation is then proposed: perhaps Rabbi Yossi is saying that {\i all arusot grushot} \u8212- women who were {\i arusot} and are now {\i grushot} (divorcees), not {\i almanos} \u8212- may proceed to {\i nissuin}. The case is inverted: the first marriage was {\i eirusin} (hence no pregnancy concern), and the question is whether the woman may proceed to {\i nissuin} in the new marriage. The {\i almanah} from {\i eirusin} cannot, because \u8212- but now the Gemara will question even that, since it is precisely the case of an {\i almanah from eirusin} that raises the problem: does she have {\i aveilus} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues to work through this reinterpretation in a {\i braisa}. The {\i braisa} states: {\i Rabbi Yossi omer kol hanashim yit-arsu chutz m\u8217'almanah min ha-eibol} \u8212- all women may perform {\i eirusin} except for the {\i almanah} due to {\i aveilus}, and the period of her {\i aveilus} is thirty days. Furthermore, all women may not do {\i nissuin} until three months have passed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The plain reading of this {\i braisa} corresponds to the plain reading of the Mishnah: {\i neshuot} may do {\i eirusin}, with the only exception being the {\i almanah} during her month of {\i aveilus}. The Gemara had earlier resolved the Mishnah by inverting its terms; it now attempts the same with the {\i braisa}. {\i Okmina b\u8217'arusot} \u8212- we interpret the Mishnah\u8217's references not to {\i neshuot} doing {\i eirusin}, but to {\i arusot grushot} doing {\i nissuin}. And {\i hachi nami kol arusot grushot yinase\u8217'u} \u8212- the {\i braisa} similarly is reread: all {\i arusot grushot} may proceed to {\i nissuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The end of the {\i braisa} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'kulan lo yinase\u8217'u ad sheyihiye lahem gimel chodashim} \u8212- yields a further inference: {\i ha arusah shapir dami} \u8212- if the woman started as an {\i arusah}, she may presumably perform {\i eirusin} and even proceed to {\i nissuin} immediately. This would imply that Rabbi Yossi permits a {\i nesuah} to perform {\i eirusin} freely. Rava intervenes: {\i tartzei v\u8217'eima hachi} \u8212- reread the end of the {\i braisa} as well. Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling is now to be read as: all {\i arusot grushot} may do {\i nissuin}, except for the {\i almanah} who is in {\i aveilus} for thirty days. And in the reverse case: {\i nesuos lo yisarsu ad she\u8217'yehu lahem shlosha chodashim} \u8212- women who were {\i nesuot} may not do {\i eirusin} until three months have passed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This rewriting is acknowledged as extremely complex. What is not fully explained in the Gemara is why Rabbi Yossi, once his case is flipped to be about an {\i arusah} doing {\i nissuin}, should thereby be committed to prohibiting a {\i nesuah} from doing {\i eirusin} in all circumstances. Why could he not hold, like Rabbi Yehuda, that as long as one of the two stages is {\i eirusin}, there is no pregnancy concern? The Gemara does not fully resolve this, but the result of the entire textual reworking is to demonstrate that the Gemara is bothered by any reading of Rabbi Yossi under which {\i eirusin} would be restricted during {\i aveilus}, and it therefore inverts his case so that only {\i nissuin} is at stake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Aveilus Eirusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fundamental premise underlying all of this is now made explicit: does a woman who was only {\i arusa} \u8212- never {\i nesua} \u8212- to a man have {\i aveilus} if he dies? A {\i braisa} transmitted by {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Ami} is unequivocal: if a man\u8217's wife was only his {\i arusa}, he is not an {\i onen} for her if she dies, and if he is a {\i kohen}, he does not become {\i tamei} for her. Similarly, if the husband dies, she is not an {\i onenet} and has no burial obligations toward him. If she dies, he does not inherit her. If he dies, she is entitled to collect her {\i kesubah} \u8212- the basic sum of two hundred {\i zuz} is owed from the time of {\i eirusin} \u8212- but she has no {\i aveilus} obligations. There is thus no {\i aveilus} whatsoever in an {\i eirusin} relationship, neither for the man nor the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the Gemara back to where it began. No one disputes that there is no {\i aveilus} for an {\i eirusin} relationship. Therefore, if the case involves a woman who was only {\i arusa} to her first husband and he died, she is in no {\i aveilus} at all and could in principle do {\i kiddushin} immediately. If the case involves a woman who was {\i nesuah} to her first husband, she is in {\i aveilus}, but in order to remove the three-month pregnancy concern, the new relationship must be {\i eirusin} \u8212- and that raises the question of why {\i eirusin} would be problematic during {\i aveilus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension: {\i ella tanai hi} \u8212- the question of whether {\i eirusin} is prohibited during personal {\i aveilus} is itself a {\i machlokes tannaim}. A {\i braisa} records: from {\i Rosh Chodesh Av} until the fast, one limits business and also refrains from {\i me\u8217'ares u\u8217'milisa} \u8212- from {\i eirusin} and {\i nissuin}. Then, during the week in which {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} falls, haircuts and laundry are also prohibited. Some say the haircut and laundry restrictions extend through the entire month.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to one opinion in this {\i braisa}, then, even {\i eirusin} is prohibited during the nine days preceding {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}. It is that position which Rabbi Yossi follows when he prohibits {\i eirusin} during personal {\i aveilus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then raises a careful question: when the {\i braisa} prohibits {\i eirusin} before {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}, does it really mean the formal act of {\i kiddushin}, or does it mean only the {\i seudat erusin} \u8212- the betrothal feast? Perhaps the act of {\i kiddushin} itself is always permitted, and only the feast is restricted. The Gemara pushes back: by the same token, when the {\i braisa} prohibits {\i nissuin}, would we say only the {\i seudat nissuin} is forbidden but the actual bringing-in of the bride is permitted? Obviously not \u8212- {\i nissuin} without a feast still constitutes the full {\i simcha} of marriage. But {\i eirusin} without a feast is a different matter: {\i erusin belo seuda mi ika simcha?} \u8212- is there any {\i simcha} at all in {\i eirusin} without a feast? The answer is that there is not. Therefore, the {\i braisa}\u8217's prohibition of {\i eirusin} must refer only to the feast, and the act of {\i kiddushin} itself is always permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We are thus back to the original question: if {\i kiddushin} itself is always permitted even during the {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} period, what is Rabbi Yossi\u8217's basis for restricting it during personal {\i aveilus}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aveilus Chadasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} resolves this definitively: {\i shani aveilus chadasha m\u8217'aveilus yeshana} \u8212- a fresh, new {\i aveilus} differs from an old, commemorative one. {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} is an {\i aveilus yeshana}, an ancient collective mourning; its demands are real but not of the same intimate, personal character as fresh personal mourning. Additionally: {\i shani aveilus d\u8217'rabbim m\u8217'aveilus d\u8217'yachid} \u8212- communal {\i aveilus} differs from individual {\i aveilus}. While one might argue that communal {\i aveilus} is more weighty in some respects \u8212- it affects the entire people \u8212- it is also less personally intimate than the acute grief of an individual mourner. Any evidence drawn from the {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} laws is therefore inapplicable to the case of personal, fresh {\i aveilus}. Rabbi Yossi is entitled to hold \u8212- and Rabbi Yehuda may disagree \u8212- that in a personal, newly occurring {\i aveilus}, there is an {\i issur} of {\i kiddushin}. And with that resolution, we return to the simple reading of the Mishnah: Rabbi Yossi says {\i eirusin} is restricted during the first month of personal {\i aveilus}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Brothers Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: 4 Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf concludes with the opening of a new Mishnah. {\i Arba\u8217'ah achim nesuim arba nashim u\u8217'metu} \u8212- four brothers were married to four women, and all four brothers died. The Mishnah does not specify that the four wives are sisters; it is simply presenting a scenario in which a single surviving fifth brother is faced with multiple {\i yibum} obligations simultaneously, from the wives of four of his brothers. The question is how he may and should proceed. {\i Im ratza hagadol shebahem l\u8217'yavem et kullan, hareshut b\u8217'yado} \u8212- if the oldest brother wishes to perform {\i yibum} with all four of them, the prerogative is his. The responsibility devolves upon the eldest surviving brother, and he may choose to perform {\i yibum} with any or all of the {\i yevamos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Disqualifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related rule is introduced: {\i mi she-hayah nasui l\u8217'shtei nashim u-meit} \u8212- if a man was married to two women and died, and {\i chalitzah} was performed with one of them, or marital relations were established with one of them, the co-wife ({\i tzarah}) is automatically released. Either {\i chalitzah} or {\i biah} with one of the {\i yevamos} exempts all the others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Preference Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical principle follows: {\i hayetah achas k\u8217'sheirah v\u8217'achas p\u8217'sulah} \u8212- if one of the two wives was {\i k\u8217'sheirah} and the other was already {\i p\u8217'sulah l\u8217'kehunah} \u8212- for instance, she was a {\i grushah} from a prior marriage, and performing {\i chalitzah} with her would not add to her {\i pesulah} status since she already bears it \u8212- then if the {\i yavam} intends to perform {\i chalitzah}, he should perform {\i chalitzah} with the {\i p\u8217'sulah}, thereby not rendering the {\i k\u8217'sheirah} wife {\i p\u8217'sulah} to a {\i kohen} by virtue of {\i chalitzah}. Conversely, if the {\i yavam} intends to perform {\i yibum}, he should perform {\i yibum} with the {\i k\u8217'sheirah} \u8212- the more strategically and halachically preferable match. Whether this means he should exclusively do {\i yibum} with the {\i k\u8217'sheirah}, or merely that it is preferable, will be clarified by the Gemara in subsequent discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 42b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lo Pelug Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Virgins vs Non-Virgins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} begins in the middle of a discussion about the three-month waiting period that a woman must observe before remarrying, so that it can be determined whether she is pregnant from her previous husband. The {\i mishna} states that this requirement applies equally whether the woman is a {\i betula} \u8212- a virgin whose first marriage was her only marriage \u8212- or a {\i be\u8217'ula}, a non-virgin, and regardless of whether either the prior or the forthcoming relationship is {\i erusin} or {\i nissuin}. The logic behind applying the wait even where pregnancy is clearly impossible is {\i lo pelug}: the Rabbis did not wish to introduce distinctions that might lead to confusion or abuse. However, the {\i mishna} then records that both Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yossi disagree, holding that if either the prior marriage or the new relationship is merely {\i erusin} \u8212- a stage that has not yet involved marital relations \u8212- there is no need to impose the three-month wait.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a textual question: since virgins are by definition women whose only prior relationship was {\i erusin}, why does the {\i mishna} list \u8220"virgins\u8221" and \u8220"those who were only {\i arusa}\u8221" as if they were two separate categories? Similarly, being a {\i be\u8217'ula} is equivalent to having been {\i nesua}. The {\i Gemara} answers through {\i Rav Yehuda}, who explains that the {\i mishna} is not presenting two independent categories but rather is clarifying a single one: women who were either divorced or widowed whether from {\i erusin} or {\i nissuin}. The mention of {\i erusin} and {\i nissuin} is therefore explanatory of the earlier distinction between {\i betulot} and {\i be\u8217'ulot}, not an additional case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorced/Widowed Categor\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this reading established, the discussion moves to a practical ruling. Rabbi Eliezer had missed the session in the {\i beis medrash} and sought out Rabbi Assi to learn what had been said. Rabbi Assi reported that Rabbi Yochanan had ruled: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yossi} \u8212- the law follows Rabbi Yossi. Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position, shared in substance with Rabbi Yehuda, is that the three-month wait is necessary only when the woman was {\i nesua} to her prior husband and is entering {\i nissuin} with the new one, meaning there was opportunity for marital relations at both ends. If either the prior or the new relationship is only {\i erusin}, no such wait is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling immediately raises a methodological problem. Rabbi Yochanan is known for the principle that {\i halacha k\u8217'stam mishna} \u8212- the law follows the anonymous, unattributed position in the {\i Mishna}. But our {\i mishna} opens with an anonymous {\i stam} voice demanding the three-month wait in all cases. How, then, can Rabbi Yochanan rule against that {\i stam}? The {\i Gemara} responds that the {\i stam} voice in this case should not be treated as the standard authoritative {\i stam}, because it is {\i yachid} \u8212- it reflects only a single opinion \u8212- as the {\i Gemara} is about to demonstrate from a {\i braisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Pregnant Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} presents a series of circumstances in which the woman is presumptively not pregnant: she had been habitually at her father\u8217's house away from her husband ({\i redufa levei aviha}); there had been ongoing conflict ({\i ka\u8217'as}) between the spouses, making relations unlikely; the husband had been imprisoned; he was elderly to the point of near-infertility; he was ill; she herself was ill; she miscarried after the husband\u8217's death; she was infertile ({\i akara}); she was postmenopausal ({\i zekeina}); she was a minor ({\i ketana}); she was an {\i ailonis}, a woman constitutionally incapable of bearing children; or for any other reason she could not carry a pregnancy. In all of these cases, Rabbi Meir rules that she still must wait three months. The {\i lo pelug} of our {\i mishna} is therefore the position of Rabbi Meir alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- or in some versions, Rabbi Yossi, as both draw distinctions based on the circumstances \u8212- permits {\i erusin} immediately even following a prior {\i nissuin}, and in these demonstrably non-pregnant cases permits even {\i nissuin} immediately. This confirms that the anonymous {\i stam} voice is effectively {\i Rabbi Meir\u8217's} individual opinion, and therefore Rabbi Yochanan was entitled to rule against it in favor of Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yossi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba then reports that Rabbi Yochanan subsequently reversed himself \u8212- {\i chazru bo Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- and returned to demanding the three-month wait in all cases without distinction. Rav Yosef clarifies that the reversal was prompted by the {\i braisa} of the {\i Kerem b\u8217'Yavneh}, the tradition preserved from the sages who gathered at Yavneh following the destruction of the Temple. Rabbi Yishmael, the son of Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka, testified: {\i shamati mipi chachamim b\u8217'Kerem b\u8217'Yavneh} \u8212- \u8220"I heard from the sages in the {\i Kerem b\u8217'Yavneh} that all women must wait three months.\u8221" No distinctions are made, regardless of whether pregnancy was possible. This {\i Kerem b\u8217'Yavneh} tradition evidently carried sufficient weight to cause Rabbi Yochanan to reverse his earlier ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stam vs Machloket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Raised\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya then sends Rabbi Zerika to raise the following contradiction before Rabbi Abbahu: on the one hand, Rabbi Yochanan ruled {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yossi}; on the other, Rabbi Yochanan is the very authority behind the principle of {\i halacha k\u8217'stam mishna}. And our {\i mishna} states explicitly, in anonymous voice, that {\i kol hanashim lo yinaseu v\u8217'lo yisarsu} \u8212- all women may neither marry nor become betrothed \u8212- until the three-month waiting period has elapsed, whether they are virgins or non-virgins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu\u8217's response is pointed: the one who posed this question {\i lo chash l\u8217'kimchei} \u8212- is not careful about the quality of his thinking. This is not a standard {\i stam mishna}. A {\i stam mishna} that obligates us is one where the anonymous position stands uncontested within the {\i mishna} itself. Here, the {\i mishna} immediately follows the {\i stam} with a recorded debate \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yossi disagree. This configuration is called {\i stam v\u8217'achar kach machlokes}, and in such a case, {\i ein halacha k\u8217'stam} \u8212- we are not obligated to rule according to the {\i stam}. One may note that this does not necessarily mean we rule {\i against} the {\i stam}, only that we are not bound by it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Then Machloket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then articulates the general principle through the name of Rav Papa, with some attributing it to Rabbi Yochanan himself: {\i machlokes v\u8217'achar kach stam \u8212- halacha k\u8217'stam}. If the debate appears first in the {\i Mishna} and the anonymous position follows it, we rule according to the {\i stam}. But {\i stam v\u8217'achar kach machlokes \u8212- ein halacha k\u8217'stam}. When the {\i stam} comes first and the debate follows, we do not rule according to the {\i stam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This hermeneutical framework has broad applications. It is worth noting that comparable interpretive questions arise in the {\i Shulchan Aruch} as well \u8212- when the {\i Shulchan Aruch} states a ruling and then records {\i v\u8217'yeish omrim}, there is an entire developed methodology for determining which view carries practical weight depending on the order and structure of the presentation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe that, as the {\i Gemara} already noted, the {\i stam} of our {\i mishna} represents the opinion of Rabbi Meir \u8212- since the anonymous voice in the {\i Mishna} is generally attributed to Rabbi Meir. This makes it no surprise that it stands in tension with the views of other {\i tanna\u8217'im}. That said, even the principle of {\i stam mishna} does not typically concern itself with who authored the {\i stam} but with whether it is formally presented as uncontested \u8212- and here it clearly is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Abbahu\u8217's Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu expands the discussion to enumerate four configurations. His attendant Rabbi Nachum posed a series of questions while accompanying him, and Rabbi Abbahu addressed each in turn. First: {\i machlokes v\u8217'achar stam} \u8212- {\i halacha k\u8217'stam}, we follow the {\i stam}. Second: {\i stam v\u8217'achar machlokes} \u8212- {\i ein halacha k\u8217'stam}, we do not follow the {\i stam}. Third: {\i stam b\u8217'masnisin, machlokes b\u8217'braisa} \u8212- the {\i Mishna} presents it anonymously but a {\i braisa} records it as a debate. Rabbi Abbahu rules {\i halacha k\u8217'stam}: since the anonymous position stands uncontested in the {\i Mishna}, the {\i braisa\u8217's} recording of a debate does not dislodge it. Fourth: {\i machlokes b\u8217'masnisin, stam b\u8217'braisa} \u8212- the {\i Mishna} records a debate, but a {\i braisa} presents the matter anonymously. Here Rabbi Abbahu replies: {\i v\u8217'chi Rebbi lo shna\u8217'ah, Rebbi Chiyya minalo} \u8212- if Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi did not include this as a {\i stam} in the {\i Mishna}, on what authority does Rabbi Chiyya present it as one? The {\i Mishna} carries ultimate authority, and the {\i braisa\u8217's} anonymous formulation does not override the {\i Mishna\u8217's} explicit recording of a dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall logic of these cases is intuitive: the {\i stam} carries authority when it represents the formal, uncontested position in the highest-level source. Any configuration that diminishes either the formality or the source level weakens its claim to be the definitive ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combs Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point Rabbi Abbahu\u8217's questioner challenges the principle with a {\i mishna} that is taught as {\i stam} yet, as everyone knows, is not followed in practice. The {\i mishna} in question concerns combs used for processing fiber. A flax comb ({\i masreik shel pishtan}) whose teeth have been removed: if two teeth remain adjacent to one another, the implement is {\i tamei} because it retains functional utility; if only one tooth remains, it is {\i tahor} since a single tooth is useless for combing. As for the individual teeth that break off one by one, they are themselves {\i tamei} because a single tooth can serve as a toothpick. Now regarding a wool comb ({\i shel tzemer}): if every other tooth has been removed such that no two remain adjacent, the comb is {\i tahor}. If three teeth remain together in one location, it is {\i tamei}. Further, if the outer tooth is one of a cluster of three, it is {\i tahor} \u8212- the outer teeth being less functionally critical. If two adjacent teeth broke off still connected at their base, they can serve as tweezers and are {\i tamei}. If one tooth broke off and was modified to serve as a lamp-wick cleaner or for stretching material, it is {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This final ruling \u8212- that a single modified tooth is {\i tamei} \u8212- is presented in the {\i Mishna} as a {\i stam}. Yet Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish are known to both declare {\i zu einah mishna} \u8212- this ruling is not authoritative and we do not decide the law accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is pressing: here is a {\i stam mishna} that established authorities explicitly set aside. How can one maintain that {\i halacha k\u8217'stam mishna} as a binding principle?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combs Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu answers: {\i bar minei d\u8217'hi} \u8212- put that case aside entirely. It is a known exception, and both Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish explicitly identified it as one. The {\i Gemara} then proceeds to explain {\i why} this {\i mishna} is treated as an exception, and the answer turns on internal problems within the {\i mishna} itself. Rav Huna bar Minyumi in the name of Rav Idi reports that the {\i reisha} contradicts the {\i seifa}: the opening of the {\i mishna} dealing with the wool comb states that when teeth are missing with a gap between remaining ones ({\i achas mi-beino}), the comb is {\i tahor}, implying that two adjacent teeth ({\i shtayim b\u8217'makom echad}) would render it {\i tamei} \u8212- yet the later clause specifies {\i three} together ({\i shalosh tamei}), implying that two alone would not suffice. The {\i Gemara} raises several possible resolutions of this apparent internal contradiction \u8212- perhaps the cases involve outer versus inner teeth, or teeth that break off with the base of the comb attached versus those that break off cleanly, or thick teeth versus thin teeth \u8212- and concludes that none of these distinctions are entirely forced. The {\i mishna} is not demonstrably self-contradictory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final answer is therefore textual rather than logical: those who are precise about the {\i girsa} \u8212- the exact text \u8212- note that their manuscripts conclude the {\i mishna} with the phrase {\i zu divrei Rabbi Shimon}, identifying the final ruling as specifically the opinion of Rabbi Shimon rather than an anonymous {\i stam}. There is therefore no {\i stam} ruling here to begin with, and the challenge to the principle dissolves. As the {\i Gemara} acknowledges, this was a rather extraordinary digression \u8212- the example chosen to challenge the principle of {\i stam mishna} turns out to involve the textual apparatus of combs, a matter of limited practical consequence. Whether the ruling about the modified tooth affects any of the other cases in the {\i mishna} is left unexplored.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Months & Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day Ninety Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the three-month waiting period and records a series of practical rulings. Rabbi Yoshiyah bar Avin sent a ruling that {\i me\u8217'arsin toch gimel} \u8212- one may perform {\i erusin} within the three months \u8212- following the lenient view of Rabbi Yehuda that {\i erusin} is not problematic because it does not yet place the woman in a context of marital relations. He adds that this was also the practice and teaching of Rabbi Elazar. Additionally, a tradition in the name of Rabbi Chanina HaGadol holds that {\i rubo shel rishon, v\u8217'rubo shel shlishi, v\u8217'emtza\u8217'i v\u8217'hashalem} \u8212- the majority of the first month, the majority of the third month, and the entirety of the middle month would be sufficient. This formulation is striking, as it implies that something less than three complete months may suffice \u8212- perhaps reflecting a view that seven full months are required to establish paternity, in which case slightly more than two months of waiting would be adequate. Neither {\i Rashi} nor {\i Tosafot} address this point directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} suggests that this leniency in counting applies only when the waiting woman is an {\i arusa}, not a {\i nesua}. On that reading, the ruling is less radical: it is not that such a woman need not wait at all, only that the three months may be calculated with some flexibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ameimar} went further and permitted performing {\i erusin} on day ninety itself, without requiring the couple to wait until day ninety-one. Rav Ashi challenges this from a ruling attributed to both Rava and Shmuel, who state that the three-month wait requires a full three months, exclusive of the day of the husband\u8217's death and exclusive of the day of the {\i erusin} itself \u8212- meaning the {\i erusin} must take place on day ninety-one at the earliest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing Women Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this challenge by clarifying that the ruling of Rava and Shmuel was not stated in the context of the three-month wait at all. It was stated regarding the waiting period before remarriage for a woman who is nursing her infant \u8212- {\i l\u8217'inyan meneket}. Rava and Shmuel had said that a nursing mother must wait twenty-four months before remarrying, exclusive of the day the child was born and exclusive of the day of the {\i erusin}, placing the {\i erusin} on the day after the twenty-four months conclude. The version cited by Rav Ashi had confused the two contexts: it was not three months and day ninety-one, but twenty-four months and the day following.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A subsequent case reinforces this: there is a report that a man arranged a {\i seudas erusin} on day ninety and Rava compelled him to cancel the feast and postpone. The {\i Gemara} clarifies that this too involved {\i seudas nissuin}, not {\i seudas erusin} \u8212- and for {\i nissuin}, the stricter count indeed applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Pelug Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final ruling ({\i hilchasa}) is that a nursing woman must wait twenty-four months, exclusive of the day of birth and the day of {\i erusin}. For the three-month wait applicable to all other remarrying women, it is likewise exclusive of the day of death and exclusive of the day of {\i erusin}. The more significant conclusion, however, is substantive: the {\i halacha} follows {\i lo pelug}, and even {\i erusin} requires the full three-month wait. This represents Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's final position following his reversal based on the {\i Kerem b\u8217'Yavneh} tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion raises a broader question about the applicability of {\i lo pelug} in the contemporary context. The {\i lo pelug} rationale does not express a genuine belief that a postmenopausal woman or a minor might be pregnant. Rather, it reflects a concern that if we begin introducing distinctions, borderline cases will escape the necessary scrutiny. But if that is the logic, then the underlying concern \u8212- which is determining pregnancy status \u8212- could in principle be satisfied by a modern pregnancy test. On this analysis, {\i lo pelug} requires {\i some} form of determination in all cases; it does not necessarily require that the determination take the form of waiting three months. That said, whether pregnancy tests may substitute for the three-month wait is a {\i machlokes haposkim}, and on the strict reading, one would require even an elderly woman to demonstrate non-pregnancy before proceeding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow\u8217's Aveilus Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yossi Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} records that Rabbi Yossi permits women to perform {\i erusin} immediately \u8212- {\i chutz min ha\u8217'almanah mipnei ha\u8217'eibul} \u8212- except for a widow who is in {\i aveilus}. The logic is straightforward: if the woman was {\i nesua} to her first husband and he died, she is in {\i aveilus} for the first month. Even though the new relationship involves only {\i erusin}, and therefore poses no concern about determining paternity, she is still mourning the prior husband and may not yet enter even a betrothal relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The background assumption is that a woman who was {\i nesua} to her first husband and is now entering {\i erusin} with a new partner does not need the three-month wait \u8212- because the new relationship is only {\i erusin}, which involves no marital relations. Yet she cannot proceed even with this limited step during the first month, because she is in personal {\i aveilus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda challenges this on {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} grounds. He argues: during the period when it is forbidden to launder one\u8217's garments \u8212- that is, during the first week of personal {\i aveilus}, when the laws of mourning are most severe \u8212- it is still permitted to perform {\i erusin}. How much more so during the subsequent weeks of the month, when laundering is again permitted, should {\i erusin} certainly be allowed? If the severity of the prohibition does not extend to blocking {\i erusin} even at its most stringent point, how can Rabbi Yossi claim there is any prohibition at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tisha B\u8217'Av Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} draws on a {\i Mishna} and {\i braisa} relating to the nine-day period preceding {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}. The {\i Mishna} states that during the week in which {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} falls ({\i shavua she\u8217'chal bo Tisha B\u8217'Av}), it is forbidden to take a haircut or launder clothing. A {\i braisa} records that during the period before this week, the people limit business dealings, building, and planting \u8212- yet they may still perform {\i kiddushin}, though not {\i nissuin}, and may not hold a {\i seudas erusin}. The {\i Gemara} understands {\i Shmuel\u8217's} principle \u8212- that one may perform {\i kiddushin} even close to {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} lest another man get there first ({\i shema yekadmenu acher}) \u8212- as the basis for this leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preliminary {\i braisa} seems to speak of the period before the {\i shavua she\u8217'chal bo}, meaning even before the most restricted week. The {\i Gemara} considers whether the permission to perform {\i kiddushin} extends into the {\i shavua she\u8217'chal bo} itself, when laundering is forbidden. Rava constructs a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}: if during the nine days when business dealings are restricted one may still perform {\i kiddushin}, how much more so during the less restricted days of personal {\i aveilus} when business dealings are permitted should {\i kiddushin} be allowed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reinterpreting R. Yossi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In order to accommodate the evident logic that {\i erusin} should not be prohibited during personal {\i aveilus}, the {\i Gemara} undertakes a significant reinterpretation of Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling. Rather than reading him as saying {\i kol hanashim yisarsu chutz min ha\u8217'almanah} \u8212- all women may perform {\i erusin} except for the widow \u8212- the {\i Gemara} proposes reading: {\i kol hanashim yinaseu} \u8212- all women may perform {\i nissuin}. What Rabbi Yossi objects to is not {\i erusin} during {\i aveilus}, but {\i nissuin} during {\i aveilus}. {\i Erusin}, he agrees, is unproblematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this revised reading, the structure of the case changes entirely. The prior relationship was {\i erusin} \u8212- which is why there is no concern about determining pregnancy \u8212- and the new step being contemplated is {\i nissuin}. Rabbi Yossi says: ordinarily this {\i nissuin} is fine (since the prior stage was only {\i erusin}, there is no issue of confusing lineage), but if the woman is an {\i almanah}, her first month of {\i aveilus} blocks even {\i nissuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: but if Rabbi Yossi is talking about a woman whose prior relationship was {\i erusin}, and she is now proceeding to {\i nissuin} \u8212- why should there be any concern about determining pregnancy at all? Since the prior stage was {\i erusin}, there were no marital relations, and she cannot be pregnant. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i lais lei} \u8212- Rabbi Yossi is simply not concerned about determining pregnancy in this case. He does not hold the position of {\i lo pelug} in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, the {\i Gemara} considers: perhaps Rabbi Yossi does accept the concern about pregnancy but is dealing with a different configuration \u8212- specifically, women who were previously {\i arusot} and are now divorced ({\i arusot gerushot}) and wish to proceed to {\i nissuin}. In this reading, there is no {\i aveilus} concern because a {\i gerusha} is not a widow; the exception is only an {\i almanah} \u8212- a woman who was previously {\i arusa} and whose {\i arus} died. In that case, one might ask: does such a woman have {\i aveilus} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: if Rabbi Yossi is now only talking about an {\i arusa} who became an {\i almanah} proceeding to {\i nissuin} \u8212- what is the difference between his position and Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's? The {\i Gemara} answers: the distinction lies in the case of a {\i nesua} seeking to perform {\i erusin}. Rabbi Yehuda permits {\i erusin} as well \u8212- as long as {\i one} of the two stages is {\i erusin}, the waiting period is unnecessary. Rabbi Yossi, on the revised reading, addresses only the case where the {\i first} stage is {\i erusin} and the {\i second} step is {\i nissuin}; he does not explicitly permit {\i erusin} for a woman who was already {\i nesua}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almanah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates the next question. According to the revised reading of Rabbi Yossi \u8212- where he is discussing an {\i arusa} who is an {\i almanah} proceeding to {\i nissuin} \u8212- does such a woman even have {\i aveilus}? The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i braisa} taught by Rabbi Chiyya bar Ami: a man\u8217's wife who is only his {\i arusa} \u8212- they have not yet begun living together \u8212- {\i lo onen}, he is not an {\i onen} for her if she dies; {\i v\u8217'lo mitame lah}, if he is a {\i kohen} he does not become {\i tamei} on her account; {\i v\u8217'chen hi}, and similarly she is not an {\i onenes} for him and does not defile herself for him; {\i mes hu \u8212- govah kesuvashah}, if he dies she is still entitled to collect her {\i kesuvah}, since the basic obligation of the {\i kesuvah} attaches from the time of {\i erusin} even though the ongoing marital obligations do not yet apply; {\i meitah \u8212- eino yorshah}, but if she dies he does not inherit her. The clear implication of this {\i braisa} is that there is no {\i aveilus} obligation for an {\i arusa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the discussion full circle. Everyone agrees there is no {\i aveilus} for an {\i arusa} relationship. If the deceased was the woman\u8217's husband in a full {\i nissuin} relationship, then there is {\i aveilus}, but then in order to remove the three-month concern, the {\i new} relationship must be only {\i erusin} \u8212- and we are back to the original question: why should {\i erusin} be prohibited during {\i aveilus}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes that this question reflects a genuine {\i machlokes tanna\u8217'im}. A {\i braisa} addressing the nine-day period states: from {\i Rosh Chodesh Av} until {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}, people limit business dealings, building, planting, {\i erusin}, and {\i nissuin}. This represents a {\i tanna} who holds that {\i erusin} is indeed restricted during mourning-adjacent periods. The same {\i braisa} then states that during the {\i shavua she\u8217'chal bo Tisha B\u8217'Av} it is forbidden to launder and to take a haircut, with one opinion extending these restrictions to the entire month. Rabbi Yossi may be following this stricter {\i tanna} who restricts {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New vs Old Aveilus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises one final challenge. How do we know that the {\i braisa}\u8217's prohibition of {\i le\u8217'ares} during the nine days refers to the actual act of {\i kiddushin} rather than merely to holding a {\i seudas erusin}? The {\i Gemara} answers: the same question could be raised regarding {\i nissuin} \u8212- and we know that the prohibition on {\i nissuin} during this period refers to the act itself, not merely to the feast. The distinction is: {\i nissuin} without a feast still constitutes a full marriage and carries its own inherent {\i simcha}; but {\i erusin} without a feast carries no {\i simcha} at all. Therefore, if there is any prohibition of {\i erusin} in such a context, it must apply to the act itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us back to the starting point without resolution through the {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} material alone. The final answer given by Rav Ashi is conceptual: {\i shani aveilus chadasha me\u8217'aveilus yeshana} \u8212- a fresh personal {\i aveilus} is categorically different from the ancient, commemorative mourning of {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}. And {\i shani aveilus derabbim me\u8217'aveilus deyachid} \u8212- the communal mourning of {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} is different from the individual\u8217's personal {\i aveilus}. Whatever evidence one brings from the {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} period \u8212- whether permissive or restrictive \u8212- is not directly transferable to personal {\i aveilus}. Rabbi Yossi is therefore entirely entitled to hold that in a fresh, personal {\i aveilus}, even the act of {\i kiddushin} is prohibited, regardless of what one rules about the analogous period before {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}. And if Rabbi Yehuda disagrees, the dispute is rooted in precisely this question: whether the communal, commemorative mourning of {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} is strict enough to serve as a relevant precedent for individual personal {\i aveilus}. On the simple reading, the {\i mishna} is restored: Rabbi Yossi prohibits {\i erusin} for a widow during the month of {\i aveilus}, and the permissive cases discussed throughout the {\i sugya} do not override this.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Next Mishna Preview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} concludes with a preview of the next {\i mishna}. Four brothers are married to four women, and all four brothers die, leaving a fifth surviving brother. The {\i mishna} states that if the eldest brother wishes to perform {\i yibum} with all four widows, the prerogative is his \u8212- the responsibility for {\i yibum} devolves primarily upon the eldest, and he may choose to marry all four.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then turns to a man who was married to two women and dies. Performing either {\i bi\u8217'ah} or {\i chalitzah} with one of them automatically exempts the other \u8212- as has been established from the beginning of the tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kasher/Pasul Mix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the {\i mishna} addresses the case where of the two wives, one is {\i kashera} \u8212- eligible for marriage to a {\i kohen} \u8212- and one is {\i pesulah}, already disqualified by virtue of a prior divorce. If the surviving brother intends to perform {\i chalitzah}, he should do so with the {\i pesulah}, so as not to add a further disqualification to the {\i kashera}. If he intends to perform {\i yibum}, he should do {\i yibum} with the {\i kashera}. Whether this means he performs {\i yibum} exclusively with the {\i kashera}, or whether after performing {\i yibum} with the {\i kashera} he may also marry the {\i pesulah}, will be taken up in the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 44\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum Rights & Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a corrective gloss on the {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} speaks of four brothers, and the {\i gemara} immediately notes that the formulation \u8220"four brothers\u8221" raises an obvious difficulty: if they all died, who remains to perform {\i yibum}? The {\i gemara} therefore emends the reading \u8212- {\i ela eima arba me-achim}, say rather that four of the brothers died, leaving four widows, while other brothers survive. This is, as the lecturer observes, one of those {\i mishnayot} in this {\i masechet} that would have made an excellent opening for the tractate had it appeared earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oldest Brother Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the corrected reading in place, the {\i mishnah} establishes that the oldest surviving brother holds the right \u8212- {\i ha-reshut beyado} \u8212- to perform {\i yibum} with all four widows before any other brother may act. He is not limited to one; if he wishes, he may marry all of them before the younger brothers have any opportunity to intervene.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Exempts All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses the exemption principle that has underlain the entire tractate from its opening: {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum} performed with any one wife exempts all the others from further obligation. Though this has been assumed throughout, the {\i gemara} now derives it explicitly from scriptural sources, which are examined in detail in the following section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Wives Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elders\u8217' Advice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a challenge: would we simply allow this brother to marry four women simultaneously? Surely that is an extraordinary domestic situation. The {\i gemara} invokes a {\i baraita} on the verse {\i ve-karu lo ziknei iro} \u8212- \u8220"and the elders of his city shall call to him.\u8221" The {\i gemara} understands {\i ve-dibru eilav}, \u8220"and they shall speak to him,\u8221" as mandating substantive guidance, not mere ritual formality. {\i Melamed she-masiin lo eitzah hogenes lo} \u8212- they give him advice that is appropriate and fitting for him specifically. Crucially, the elders must address him directly, {\i hen ve-lo sheluchan}, not through an intermediary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Matching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The content of that guidance is elaborated: if he is young and the {\i yevamah} is elderly, or he is elderly and she is young, the elders say to him \u8212- {\i mah lecha etzel yaldah}, what do you want with such a young woman, or {\i mah lecha etzel zekeinah}, what do you want with such an old woman? {\i Kelech etzel she-kamotecha}, go and find someone of your own age, {\i ve-al tasim ketatah be-veitecha} \u8212- do not bring strife into your house. The advice is non-binding; he is free to reject it. And notably, the woman\u8217's own voice is not heard in this passage, a theme that recurs throughout the tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Math of Conjugal Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The immediate question is why the {\i mishnah} limits the brother to four wives. If he is a man of means who can support multiple wives \u8212- the {\i gemara} answers {\i lo tzericha de-efshar lei}, this is indeed a case where he has the resources \u8212- why not permit ten or twenty? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i eitzah tovah ka mashma lan}, this is good advice even for a wealthy man. {\i Arba in, tfeih lo} \u8212- four wives yes, more no. The reasoning is mathematical: {\i ki heichi de-timtei onah be-chodesh}, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i onah} for a {\i talmid chacham} is once per week. With four wives rotating, each wife receives conjugal attention once a month. Any more than four wives and a woman would receive her {\i onah} less than once a month, which is an unacceptable diminishment of her rights. Four, therefore, is both the maximum permitted and the outer limit of practical wisdom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Senegal Digression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is an interesting coincidence that Islamic law similarly limits a man to four wives, a number said to reflect the personal practice of Muhammad. The parallel with the Talmud\u8217's reasoning is worth noting, though the justifications differ. By way of illustration: on a rabbinic mission to Senegal \u8212- a society in which the overwhelming majority of the population practices polygamy, with only a small Christian minority living monogamously \u8212- the lecturer had occasion to observe a kind of living parallel to the world of {\i Avraham, Yitzchak, ve-Yaakov}: village compounds in which co-wives occupied separate quarters, maintained their own domestic rotations, and shared child-rearing responsibilities. A question from one of the visiting rabbinical students \u8212- \u8220"what is it like living in a polygamous society?\u8221" \u8212- met with understandable puzzlement from the Senegalese women, who might equally have asked what it is like to be monogamous. The tension was dissolved with a joke: asked what the penalty for polygamy is, the answer was \u8212- two mothers-in-law. The laughter that followed confirmed that mother-in-law humor requires no translation across cultures.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum vs.\u160 ?Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bayis Echad Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having addressed the practical question of how many wives a {\i yavam} may take in {\i yibum}, the {\i gemara} now establishes the scriptural basis for the principle that {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} with one wife exempts all the others \u8212- and that it must be one or the other, not both. {\i Amar Rabbi Chama berabi Chanina amar Rabbi Yochanan amar kra}: the verse reads {\i asher lo yivneh et beit achiv}, describing the man who will not build his brother\u8217's house. The {\i gemara} derives: {\i bayis echad hu boneh, v\u8217'ein boneh shenei batim} \u8212- he builds one house, not two. And since {\i beito zu ishto} \u8212- \u8220"his house\u8221" is his wife \u8212- this limits {\i yibum} to a single wife. The equation of {\i bayis} and {\i ishah} is a well-established trope in the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Exclusivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle is derived for {\i chalitzah} from a different verse. {\i Amar Mar Zutra bar Tuvia amar kra}: the verse {\i beit chalutz ha-na\u8217'al}, \u8220"the house of the withdrawn shoe,\u8221" similarly uses the singular \u8212- {\i bayis echad hu choletz, v\u8217'ein choletz shenei batim} \u8212- he performs {\i chalitzah} with one house, not two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Option Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a question that it acknowledges is somewhat unusual: if one cannot perform {\i yibum} with two wives and cannot perform {\i chalitzah} with two wives, perhaps he should do {\i yibum} with one and {\i chalitzah} with the other? After all, neither verse individually prohibits this combination. Why assume the obligation is purely either/or?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Either/Or Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this from a third verse: {\i im lo yachpotz} \u8212- if he does not wish to perform {\i yibum}, he does {\i chalitzah}. The implication is: {\i ha im chafetz, yavam} \u8212- if he wants to, he performs {\i yibum} and does not perform {\i chalitzah}. The governing principle follows: {\i kol ha-oleh l\u8217'yibbum oleh l\u8217'chalitzah, v\u8217'chol she-eino oleh l\u8217'yibbum eino oleh l\u8217'chalitzah} \u8212- whoever is eligible for {\i yibum} is eligible for {\i chalitzah}, and whoever is ineligible for {\i yibum} is equally ineligible for {\i chalitzah}. Once {\i yibum} has been performed with one wife, the others are no longer candidates for {\i yibum}; they are therefore also not candidates for {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then adds a rabbinic rationale as well: {\i she-lo yomru bayis miktzaso banuy u-miktzaso chalutz} \u8212- lest people say that one may build half a house through {\i yibum} and release the other half through {\i chalitzah}. This seems circular at first \u8212- why should we care if people say that? \u8212- but the concern is practical: if it were permitted to do {\i yibum} first and then {\i chalitzah}, people might attempt the reverse, performing {\i chalitzah} first and then {\i yibum}. That sequence is forbidden, because {\i keivan shelo banah shuv lo yivneh} \u8212- once he has declined to build, he may not build again. The prohibition of {\i asher lo yivneh} applies retroactively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarat Ervah Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} entertains a further {\i hava amina}: perhaps the entire {\i yibum} obligation applies only when there is a single {\i yevamah}, since all the relevant verses are phrased in the singular \u8212- {\i bayis echad}, one house. When there are two {\i yevamot}, perhaps there is no {\i yibum} obligation at all. The {\i gemara} dismisses this forcefully: {\i im kein tzaras ervah d\u8217'asar rachmana lamah li}? If the obligation simply dissolves whenever there are two {\i yevamot}, the entire first chapter\u8217's discussion of {\i tzaras ervah} \u8212- the co-wife of a forbidden relative \u8212- would be superfluous. If two wives already eliminate the {\i yibum} obligation, there is no need to specify that it is eliminated when one of the two is an {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back: perhaps {\i tzaras ervah} is still necessary. One might have thought that since the {\i ervah} herself is outside the {\i yibum} framework \u8212- {\i ervah abara\u8217'i kaima} \u8212- her co-wife should still be subject to {\i yibum}. The verse therefore tells us that even the co-wife of an {\i ervah} is forbidden. This remains a meaningful ruling even within the framework of the {\i hava amina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Wives Apply\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the question of how {\i yibum} can apply at all when there are multiple {\i yevamot} by invoking the doubled formulation of {\i yevimto yevimto} \u8212- the word {\i yevimto} appears more than once in the Torah, teaching by way of expansion ({\i ribuy}) that the {\i yibum} framework applies even when there are multiple {\i yevamot}. Only one wife need perform {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}, but the obligation is real and the exemption is comprehensive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Preferential Choice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Valid vs.\u160 ?Invalid Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} addresses a refinement: what if among the {\i yevamot} one woman is {\i kesheirah} \u8212- of full valid status \u8212- and another is {\i pesulah}, disqualified in some way (not an {\i ervah}, but perhaps already a {\i grushah} from a previous marriage)? The {\i halacha} differentiates based on whether {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum} is being performed. If the {\i yavam} intends to perform {\i chalitzah}, he should do so with the woman who is already {\i pesulah}. If he intends {\i yibum}, he should do so with the {\i kesheirah} \u8212- or at minimum, the {\i Rambam} understands, he may do {\i yibum} with whichever he wishes, but the preference is for the {\i kesheirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Don\u8217't Waste Wells\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale for performing {\i chalitzah} specifically with the {\i pesulah} is articulated by {\i Rav Yosef}: {\i amar Rav Yosef kan shanah Rebbi} \u8212- here the {\i Rebbi} taught the principle of {\i lo yishpoch adam mei boro v\u8217'acherim tzrichim lahem} \u8212- a person should not pour out the water of his well when others need it. In other words, do not casually disqualify someone when the act of doing so serves no purpose. If {\i chalitzah} is to be performed anyway, and it will disqualify a woman from marrying a {\i kohen} by conferring upon her the status of {\i chalutzah}, perform it with the woman who already cannot marry a {\i kohen}. Do not needlessly foreclose the options of the {\i almanah} who retains her full status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Personal Status Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following the conclusion of the {\i yibum} discussions, the {\i mishnah} pivots to questions of personal status and {\i mamzer}. These topics \u8212- {\i geirusin}, {\i mamzer}, and {\i psul l\u8217'kehunah} \u8212- are deeply connected to {\i Masechet Yevamot}, which serves as a natural home for these fundamental questions of Jewish identity and status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machzir Gerushah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case is {\i hamachzir grushaso} \u8212- a man who remarries a woman he previously divorced, after she had in the interim married another man. This is a violation of a negative prohibition in the Torah, but it is not categorized as one of the {\i arayot} \u8212- the cardinal sexual prohibitions. It is an {\i issur lav}, a simple negative commandment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalutzah Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case is {\i ve-honosei chalutzaso} \u8212- a man who marries a woman with whom he performed {\i chalitzah}. This too is a simple negative prohibition, grounded in the principle of {\i keivan she-banah shuv lo yivneh}: once the bond of {\i yibum} has been formally dissolved, it cannot be rebuilt. This prohibition applies even if the woman subsequently married and divorced another man entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relative of Chalutzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is {\i ve-honosei kerovas chalutzaso} \u8212- a man who marries the relative of his {\i chalutzah}, for example her sister or her mother. This prohibition appears on its face to be purely rabbinic in character: just as a man may not marry his divorced wife\u8217's sister while that prohibition persists, so too the rabbis extended that restriction to the relative of a woman with whom he performed {\i chalitzah}, treating the {\i chalutzah} as if she were his divorced wife. The {\i gemara} will subject this assumption to considerable scrutiny.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzer Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} records the dispute regarding the offspring of these unions. {\i Divrei Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva holds that the child is a {\i mamzer}. His position is striking in its severity: any offspring of any prohibited relationship, even a violation of a simple {\i lav}, even a case as remote from incest as a man remarrying his own ex-wife, renders the child a {\i mamzer}. Equally, the child of a man who married his {\i chalutzah} or the relative of his {\i chalutzah} is a {\i mamzer} according to Rabbi Akiva.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} disagree. {\i Chachamim omrim ein ha-vlad mamzer} \u8212- the child is not a {\i mamzer}. In the Sages\u8217' view, the {\i mamzer} status arises only from the offspring of a relationship that carries {\i issur kares}, not from a mere {\i issur lav}. This is the authoritative ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorc\u233 ?e Relatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the {\i chachamim} concede one case: {\i u-modim be-nosei kerovas gerushaso she-ha-vlad mamzer} \u8212- they agree that if a man marries the relative of his actual {\i grushah}, his divorced wife \u8212- her sister, daughter, or mother \u8212- the child is a {\i mamzer}. The prohibitions that attach to relatives of one\u8217's wife are not dissolved by divorce (with the single exception of the wife\u8217's sister, whose prohibition lapses upon the wife\u8217's death). All other prohibited relatives remain forbidden even after divorce, and those prohibitions carry the weight of {\i ervah} and {\i kares}. It is therefore a genuine {\i ervah} violation, and the Sages accordingly agree that a {\i mamzer} results.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Text Emendation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalutzah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A sharp textual question now emerges: does Rabbi Akiva really hold that marrying the {\i relative} of a {\i chalutzah} produces a {\i mamzer}? That prohibition, as established in an earlier {\i mishnah}, is only rabbinic. {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish} had already stated explicitly: {\i achot gerushah midivrei Torah, achot chalutzah midivrei sofrim} \u8212- the sister of a {\i grushah} is a biblical prohibition, while the sister of a {\i chalutzah} is only rabbinic. Surely Rabbi Akiva would not generate a biblical {\i mamzer} status from a rabbinic prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RSBL\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish\u8217's formulation thus creates a difficulty for reading the {\i mishnah} at face value. If the prohibition of marrying a {\i chalutzah}\u8217's relative is only rabbinic, Rabbi Akiva cannot have intended to declare such a child a {\i mamzer} in the full biblical sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Text Amendment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's response is to propose an emendation: {\i tani kerovas gerushaso} \u8212- the text of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling should be corrected to read \u8220"relative of his {\i grushah},\u8221" not \u8220"relative of his {\i chalutzah}.\u8221" On this reading, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's third case is the relative of a woman he genuinely divorced \u8212- a biblical prohibition \u8212- and the Sages, while disagreeing with his first two cases, concede this third one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} marshals an argument in favor of this emendation. The {\i mishnah} closes with the concession of the {\i chachamim}: {\i u-modim be-nosei kerovas gerushaso she-ha-vlad mamzer}. One asks: why do the Sages frame their concession specifically around the case of the relative of a {\i grushah}, rather than simply stating in the abstract that {\i mamzer} status applies wherever there is {\i issur kares}? That latter formulation would appear in a later {\i mishnah} in any case. If all the Sages wished to convey was the principle that {\i kares} produces a {\i mamzer}, there was no need to single out this particular case. {\i Ela shema mina airy bah} \u8212- it must be that Rabbi Akiva himself mentioned this case, and the Sages responded to it specifically, conceding it while rejecting his other claims. This strongly implies that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's three cases were: remarrying a {\i grushah}, marrying a {\i chalutzah}, and marrying the relative of a {\i grushah} \u8212- not the relative of a {\i chalutzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counter Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i gemara} acknowledges that this inference is not airtight. The Sages might have chosen the case of the relative of a {\i grushah} not because Rabbi Akiva mentioned it, but because Rabbi Akiva {\i did} mention the relative of a {\i chalutzah}, and the Sages wished to contrast that case \u8212- which they reject \u8212- with the stronger case of the relative of a {\i grushah}, which they accept. On this reading, the {\i mishnah} text stands as written: Rabbi Akiva genuinely spoke of {\i kerovas chalutzaso}, and the Sages replied by conceding only {\i kerovas gerushaso}. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i ve-dilma le-olam lo airy bah} \u8212- perhaps Rabbi Akiva was never discussing the case of the {\i grushah}\u8217's relative at all, and the plain reading of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's three cases include the relative of a {\i chalutzah} \u8212- is in fact the correct one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalutzah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Chalutz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i mishnah} is taken at face value \u8212- that Rabbi Akiva genuinely holds the relative of a {\i chalutzah} produces a {\i mamzer} \u8212- one must explain the basis for treating a {\i chalutzah}\u8217's relatives as biblically forbidden. {\i Amar Rabbi Chiya bar Avin amar Rabbi Yochanan hayinu ta\u8217'ama de-Rabbi Akiva}: the reason for Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is derived from the very verse that describes the {\i chalitzah} ceremony. The Torah calls the result {\i beit chalutz ha-na\u8217'al} \u8212- \u8220"the house of the withdrawn shoe.\u8221" {\i Ha-kasuv kera\u8217'o bayis} \u8212- the verse itself designates the {\i chalutzah}\u8217's household as his {\i bayis}, his house. This is the same word used to denote his wife. Rabbi Akiva therefore understands the {\i chalutzah} as possessing a biblically grounded equivalence to a divorced wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Equivalence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is significant and marks a genuine departure from the assumed framework: although {\i Rebbi} (Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi) taught that the prohibition of marrying a {\i chalutzah}\u8217's relative is rabbinic \u8212- and this has been the operating assumption throughout \u8212- Rabbi Akiva holds it to be biblical. In Rabbi Akiva\u8217's reading, {\i beit chalutz ha-na\u8217'al} creates a full scriptural equivalence between a {\i chalutzah} and a {\i grushah}, making the relatives of a {\i chalutzah} as forbidden as those of an ex-wife at the level of {\i de-Oraisa}. As the {\i gemara} notes: {\i ein hachi nami kerovas chalutzaso de-Oraisa} \u8212- and that is indeed Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lav vs.\u160 ?Kares\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machzir Consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the specific question of what status attaches to the {\i offspring} of these forbidden unions. {\i Amar Rav Shimon bar Rebbe: ha-kol modim be-machzir grushaso she-ha-vlad pagum li-kehunah} \u8212- everyone agrees that in the case of {\i machzir grushaso}, even though the child is not a {\i mamzer}, the child is {\i pagum li-kehunah}, disqualified for priestly purposes. For a female child, this means she may not marry a {\i kohen}. The question, then, is whether the offspring of a simple {\i issur lav} \u8212- a union that produces no {\i mamzer} \u8212- nonetheless carries a taint of {\i psul l\u8217'kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not an abstract question. Its most practically significant application, as the {\i gemara} will make clear, is the case of a non-Jewish father and a Jewish mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almana Counterpoint\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To establish that the offspring of an {\i issur lav} carries {\i pagum} status for {\i kehunah}, the {\i gemara} constructs a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} from the case of {\i almana l\u8217'kohen gadol} \u8212- a widow who marries a {\i kohen gadol}. That union is a violation of a narrowly scoped {\i lav}, applying only to the {\i kohen gadol}, not to all priests. Yet the offspring of such a union is {\i pagum}. If so narrow a prohibition produces {\i pagum} offspring, how much more so should a universal {\i issur lav} \u8212- like {\i machzir grushaso}, which applies to all Jewish men \u8212- produce {\i pagum} offspring?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almana Unique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}: {\i mah le-almana she-kein hi atzmah mischaleles} \u8212- the {\i almana} case is unique in that the woman herself becomes a {\i chalalah} through that act of union. It is specifically because the act of sex constitutes a violation of a {\i kohen}-specific prohibition that the woman acquires a changed status, and it is that changed status which transmits {\i psul} to the offspring. This dynamic does not apply in the case of {\i machzir grushaso}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Status Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} elaborates on this distinction. A woman\u8217's status is affected by an act of prohibited sex only when that act is a prohibition specific to {\i kehunah} \u8212- for example, a {\i kohen gadol} marrying a widow, or an ordinary {\i kohen} marrying a {\i grushah}. In such cases, the woman becomes a {\i chalalah} and is barred from {\i kehunah} benefits even if the {\i kohen} subsequently dies. By contrast, {\i machzir grushaso} is not a prohibition rooted in {\i kehunah}. The woman in that case is already a {\i grushah} and already disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}; the act of remarriage does not further diminish her status \u8212- she does not, for example, lose her entitlement to eat {\i terumah} if she is a {\i bat kohen} (as {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} note). Since the woman\u8217's status is unchanged by the act, there is no basis for arguing that the child\u8217's status is affected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 To\u8217'evah Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An additional proof had been raised from the verse {\i hi to\u8217'eivah} \u8212- the Torah describes the act of remarriage as \u8220"an abomination.\u8221" A {\i derashah} reads: {\i hi to\u8217'evah, v\u8217'ein baneha to\u8217'evim} \u8212- she is the abomination, but her children bear no problematic status. This verse seems to explicitly exempt the children of this prohibition from any stigma. The {\i gemara}, however, notes that this {\i derashah} can be redirected: {\i hi to\u8217'evah, v\u8217'ein tzaratah to\u8217'evah} \u8212- she is the abomination but not her co-wife. The verse excludes the co-wife from the taint of the prohibition, but says nothing about the children. The {\i phat} in the verse thus does not definitively resolve the question of the offspring\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} revisits all three cases from the {\i mishnah} in light of this framework. Rabbi Akiva holds that in each of these {\i issur lav} cases \u8212- {\i machzir grushaso}, {\i nosei chalutzaso}, and {\i nosei kerovas chalutzaso} \u8212- {\i ein lo bakiddushin}, the {\i kiddushin} is not binding, no {\i get} is required, the woman becomes invalid, and the child is {\i pasul}. The {\i chachamim} take the opposite position on every point: {\i kiddushin} is binding, a {\i get} is required if she wishes to leave, {\i hi kesheirah} \u8212- the woman\u8217's status is unchanged \u8212- and {\i vlada kasher} \u8212- the child is {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Context Specificity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva vs.\u160 ?Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between Rabbi Akiva and the Sages on these three points \u8212- the validity of {\i kiddushin}, the woman\u8217's status, and the child\u8217's status \u8212- is now examined carefully. When the Sages say {\i vlada kasher}, how comprehensive is that {\i kashrut}? Does it mean the child is fully valid, including for {\i kehunah} purposes? Or does it mean only that the child is not a {\i mamzer}, while still being {\i pagum li-kehunah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grammatical Parsing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} parses the language precisely. According to Rabbi Akiva, the baraita states {\i hi pesulah} \u8212- she is disqualified. To whom is she disqualified? It cannot mean disqualified from marrying other Jews, since no act of forbidden sex can render the woman herself a {\i mamzer} \u8212- that category applies only to offspring. The only meaningful disqualification the woman could acquire is disqualification from {\i kehunah} \u8212- either from marrying a {\i kohen} or from eating {\i terumah} as a {\i bat kohen}. So when Rabbi Akiva says {\i hi pesulah}, he means: this {\i issur lav} is so weighty that it retroactively makes the woman invalid for {\i kehunah} purposes. When he then says {\i vlada pasul}, the {\i gemara} notes that we know from elsewhere that Rabbi Akiva declares such a child a {\i mamzer}; therefore {\i pesul} here means {\i mamzer} status, disqualified from the congregation of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to the Sages\u8217' parallel formulation \u8212- {\i hi kesheirah, vlada kasher} \u8212- the {\i gemara} applies the same logic. {\i Hi kesheirah} cannot mean that the woman is {\i kasher} to marry other Jews in the general sense, since that would never have been in doubt; forbidden sex does not create {\i mamzer} status in the woman herself. Therefore {\i hi kesheirah} must mean she remains {\i kesheirah li-kehunah} \u8212- the act of sex with an {\i issur lav} did not alter her {\i kehunah} eligibility. And since the woman\u8217's status remains unchanged by the act, the parallel reading of {\i vlada kasher} must mean the child too is {\i kasher li-kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i gemara} resists this inference. {\i Midi irya? Ha kide\u8217'ita, ve-ha kide\u8217'ita} \u8212- each statement exists in its own context. When the Sages say {\i hi kesheirah}, they address her particular vulnerability: could she have become a {\i chalalah}? No, she remains {\i kesheirah}. When they say {\i vlada kasher}, they address the child\u8217's particular vulnerability: could the child be a {\i mamzer} as Rabbi Akiva claims? No, the child is {\i kasher} \u8212- meaning, not a {\i mamzer}. It does not follow that the child is also {\i kasher li-kehunah}, since that question was never the focus of the Sages\u8217' statement about the child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Almana Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus concludes that the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} from {\i almana li-kohen gadol} provides no valid proof, and the linguistic analysis of the {\i baraita} also provides no proof. The underlying question \u8212- whether the offspring of a simple {\i issur lav} is {\i pagum li-kehunah} \u8212- remains genuinely unresolved on the basis of these sources. The {\i gemara} acknowledges: {\i almanah kashya} \u8212- the attempted proof from {\i almanah} is problematic \u8212- and the entire structure built upon it collapses. In the case of {\i machzir grushaso}, a simple {\i issur lav} in which the woman\u8217's {\i kehunah} status is not altered by the act, there is no demonstrated basis for saying the child is {\i pagum li-kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kares Only Pagum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kares Pagum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now shifts the framework. {\i Amar Rav Yosef bar Shimon bar Rebbe: ha-kol modim be-ba al chayavei kerisos she-ha-vlad pagum} \u8212- everyone agrees that if a man had relations with a woman whose prohibition carries {\i issur kares}, the child is {\i pagum}. Having abandoned the claim that an {\i issur lav} makes a child {\i pagum}, the {\i gemara} asks instead about {\i issur kares}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teimani Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Who is the \u8220"everyone\u8221" who concedes this point? The {\i gemara} identifies the concession as belonging to Rabbi Yehoshua. Rabbi Yehoshua holds an even narrower definition of {\i mamzer} than the {\i chachamim} \u8212- he requires not merely {\i kares} but {\i chayavei misos beis din} (capital offenses by a court, such as adultery) to produce a {\i mamzer}. Even he concedes that the offspring of an {\i issur kares} is at minimum {\i pagum}, even if not a full {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal V\u8217'Chomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concession is again derived via {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} from {\i almana l\u8217'kohen gadol}: if the offspring of that narrow, {\i kohen}-specific {\i lav} is {\i pagum}, then certainly the offspring of a universal {\i issur kares} should be {\i pagum}. This time, the {\i gemara} does not dismiss the argument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zonah Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} succeeds here, where it failed for {\i issur lav}, is that a woman who engages in a sexually prohibited act carrying {\i issur kares} is rendered a {\i zonah} \u8212- a category explicitly disqualified from {\i kehunah} marriage. {\i Keivan she-ba\u8217'alah, asa\u8217'ah zonah} \u8212- the act of union itself confers {\i zonah} status upon the woman. Since the woman herself becomes disqualified, that disqualification flows to the child as well. The mechanism thus mirrors that of the {\i almana}-{\i kohen gadol} case: it is always the woman\u8217's changed status that serves as the conduit for the child\u8217's {\i psul}. When the woman\u8217's status does not change \u8212- as in a simple {\i issur lav} \u8212- the child is unaffected. When the woman becomes a {\i zonah} through {\i issur kares}, that status transmits to the offspring.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jew Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensus Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbah bar bar Chana amar Rabbi Yochanan: ha-kol modim she-eved ve-oved kochavim ha-ba al bas Yisrael she-ha-vlad mamzer} \u8212- everyone agrees that a slave or a non-Jew who has relations with a Jewish woman produces a {\i mamzer}. This is a striking claim, and the {\i gemara} immediately asks: who is the \u8220"everyone\u8221" here? The answer is {\i Shimon Ha-Teimani} \u8212- even he who insists that {\i mamzer} status requires {\i issur kares} concedes this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lav But No Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concession is puzzling, because relations with a non-Jew are only an {\i issur lav}, not an {\i issur kares}. On Shimon Ha-Teimani\u8217's own framework, there should be no {\i mamzer}. The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent inconsistency: {\i hani mili} \u8212- the rule that {\i issur lav} does not produce a {\i mamzer} applies specifically to {\i lav}-prohibitions where {\i kiddushin} is nonetheless valid and binding. In ordinary {\i issur lav} cases, the man could in principle marry the woman through {\i kiddushin}, and if he does, a {\i get} is required to dissolve the marriage. It is within that framework of potential marital bindingness that we say the child is not a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in the case of a non-Jew or a slave, {\i kiddushin} is categorically impossible \u8212- {\i keivan d\u8217'lo tafsi bu kiddushin, k\u8217'chayavei krisos dami} \u8212- since marriage cannot be binding, the case is treated as if it were {\i issur kares}. This raises a broader theoretical question: perhaps the operative criterion for {\i mamzer} is not {\i lav} versus {\i kares} at all, but rather whether {\i kiddushin} could be binding. When {\i kiddushin} is binding, the union carries a marital framework, and even a severe prohibition like {\i kares} does not produce a {\i mamzer} (for this we shall see the {\i niddah} counterexample shortly). When {\i kiddushin} cannot be binding, the offspring lacks any legitimate familial framework, and {\i mamzer} status results.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i niddah} case presents itself as a powerful illustration. Relations with a {\i niddah} carry {\i issur kares}, yet the offspring of a husband and wife during a period of {\i niddah} is universally held not to be a {\i mamzer}. Why? Because {\i kiddushin} is fully binding in that marriage \u8212- the couple is validly married. The {\i kares}-level prohibition is severe, but it occurs within the context of a legally recognized marriage, and therefore the {\i mamzer} criterion is not triggered. This inverts the logic: it is not the {\i kares} per se that creates a {\i mamzer}, it may be the absence of bindable {\i kiddushin}. And if so, the non-Jew case \u8212- where {\i kiddushin} can never bind \u8212- would produce a {\i mamzer} even though it is only a {\i lav}. This is, the {\i gemara} acknowledges, a coherent and reasonable argument \u8212- though we shall see that the {\i halacha} does not follow it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jew Non-Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RSBY Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a challenge from a {\i baraita}: {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda omer ein mamzer ela mi-she-issuro issur ervah ve-onush kares} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} results only from a union that is both a sexual prohibition ({\i issur ervah}) and punishable by {\i kares}. Intercourse with a non-Jew, being only an {\i issur lav}, would not produce a {\i mamzer} on this view. The {\i baraita} thus directly contradicts the claim that \u8220"everyone\u8221" agrees a non-Jew-fathered child is a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Possible Excepti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} responds: {\i Ella amar Rav Yosef man d\u8217'chula modei Rabbi} \u8212- the \u8220"everyone\u8221" who concedes this point is {\i Rebbi}, Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi himself. Although {\i Rebbi} in another context says the lenient positions apply only according to Rabbi Akiva, and his own ruling aligns with the view that {\i mamzer} requires {\i issur kares} \u8212- nevertheless, in the specific case of a non-Jewish or slave father, {\i Rebbi} may concede that the child is a {\i mamzer}. The rationale is that even one who normally reserves {\i mamzer} status for {\i issur kares} might make an exception when {\i kiddushin} is categorically impossible, treating the non-Jew case as functionally equivalent to an {\i issur kares} situation in terms of its legal consequences. This remains the position being tested.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Captive Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ami\u8217's Consultation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A narrative is now introduced. {\i Rabbi Acha Sar Ha-Birah} and {\i Rabbi Tanchum} the son of {\i Rabbi Chiya} of {\i Kfar Akko} redeemed a group of captive women traveling from Armon to Tiberias. One of these captive women had become pregnant through a non-Jew. The practical question of the child\u8217's status was brought before {\i Rabbi Ami}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Say Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Ami} reported the opinions he had received: {\i Rabbi Yochanan, Rabbi Elazar}, and {\i Rabbi Chanina} all held that a non-Jewish or slave father together with a Jewish mother produces a {\i mamzer}. These are three of the most authoritative voices in the generation of Palestinian {\i amoraim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Say Kasher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} is unimpressed with the weight of three authorities and responds in kind: {\i ravusa le-mechashev gavrei} \u8212- is it such an achievement to count up names? He provides a counter-list of four: {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} in Bavel, and {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} and {\i Bar Kappara} in Eretz Yisrael \u8212- or, in an alternative formulation, {\i Bar Kappara} is replaced by the {\i ziknei darom}, the elders of the south. All four (or this variant group of four) hold that the child of a non-Jewish father and Jewish mother is {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does not resolve the standoff by simple head-counting. Instead, {\i Rav Yosef} himself declares: {\i ella amar Rav Yosef: Rebbe hi} \u8212- it is {\i Rebbi}\u8217's position that is operative. The basis is the tradition brought by {\i Rav Dimi} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yitzchak bar Avdimi} in the name of {\i Rabbeinu} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi} \u8212- that {\i oved kochavim ve-eved ha-ba al bas Yisrael, ha-vlad mamzer}. So we have an explicit ruling attributed to {\i Rebbi} himself: the child born of a non-Jewish father and a Jewish mother is a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kehunah Status TBD\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf closes with this contested ruling standing unresolved in terms of final {\i pesak}. The {\i gemara} notes, and leaves for the following daf, the second question that flows from this discussion: even granting that we ultimately rule the child is {\i not} a {\i mamzer} \u8212- as the majority of {\i poskim} conclude \u8212- what is the child\u8217's status for {\i kehunah}? May a daughter born of a non-Jewish father marry a {\i kohen}? That question, deeply relevant in practice and tightly connected to everything that has been established in today\u8217's analysis, will be taken up on the next {\i amud}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 51\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Framework: Acts After Ac\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna on daf 51 works through an intricate series of permutations involving four distinct acts in the context of {\i yibum}: {\i maimar}, {\i get}, {\i biah}, and {\i chalitzah}. The governing question is what happens when one act follows another \u8212- whether performed by the same {\i yavam} with the same {\i yevama}, or across different brothers-in-law and different co-wives. Before entering the {\i Gemara}\u8217's analysis, it is worth fixing the basic framework in mind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic vs.\u160 ?Torah Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The four acts divide into two categories based on their legal standing. {\i Maimar} and {\i get} are rabbinic in this context. {\i Maimar} is an act of {\i kidushin} \u8212- literally {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- performed in the proper form, and {\i get} is a standard bill of divorce, executed properly in every technical respect. The critical point is that because these acts take place within the framework of {\i yibum}, their operative force is only {\i derabbanan}, not {\i d\u8217'oraisa}. {\i Biah} and {\i chalitzah}, by contrast, are {\i d\u8217'oraisa}: they constitute the Torah-mandated mechanisms for either completing or releasing the {\i yibum} bond. This distinction between rabbinic and Torah-level acts is the fulcrum on which the entire Mishnaic debate turns.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that framework in place, the dispute between Rabban Gamliel and the {\i chachamim} can be stated precisely. Rabban Gamliel holds: {\i ein get achar get v\u8217'lo maimar achar maimar} \u8212- once a {\i get} or {\i maimar} has been given, no subsequent act of the same kind carries any consequence, even though those acts operate only rabbinically. And of course, {\i biah} after {\i biah} and {\i chalitzah} after {\i chalitzah} are similarly without effect, since the Torah-level act has already been completed. The {\i chachamim} agree regarding {\i biah} and {\i chalitzah}, but disagree regarding {\i get} and {\i maimar}: {\i yesh get achar get v\u8217'yesh maimar achar maimar} \u8212- because these acts are only rabbinic, they do not fully terminate the situation, and a second act of the same or a different type may still carry halakhic import. The debate thus focuses on a single axis: how absolute is a first rabbinic act against anything that comes afterwards?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Foundational Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mehani Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before entering the dispute itself, the {\i Gemara} at the bottom of 51b steps back to establish the conceptual foundations. The opening question is: {\i mahani mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- on what basis did the Rabbis institute the concept that a {\i get} or {\i maimar} has any effect at all in the {\i yibum} context? The term {\i mehani} here does not mean that these acts accomplish everything. A {\i get} given to a {\i yevama} does not function as a {\i chalitzah}, which is required {\i d\u8217'oraisa}; a {\i maimar} does not constitute {\i yibum}, which requires actual {\i biah}. What {\i mehani} means is that these acts carry halakhic implications. A {\i get} given to a {\i yevama}, for instance, closes off the possibility of {\i yibum} \u8212- {\i pasla kol ha\u8217'achim}; the other brothers can no longer perform {\i yibum} with her, and she takes on a status analogous to a divorced wife, prohibiting marriage to the {\i yavam}\u8217's relatives. A {\i maimar} similarly creates a quasi-marital relationship, implicating the prohibitions involving relatives of the {\i yevama} and affecting the ability to perform {\i yibum} with another co-wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Confusion Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then explains why the Rabbis could not simply declare these acts meaningless. The argument is one of public confusion: {\i get l\u8217'hotzi v\u8217'chalitzah l\u8217'hotzi} \u8212- a {\i get} normally functions to release a full wife, and {\i chalitzah} functions to release a {\i yevama}. Were the Rabbis to say that a {\i get} given to a {\i yevama} is entirely meaningless, people would observe such a {\i get} being given and then watch as the {\i yavam} proceeds to perform {\i yibum} nonetheless. They would conclude that {\i chalitzah} is similarly meaningless \u8212- {\i chalitzah nami lo mahani} \u8212- and would come to believe that only {\i yibum} itself counts. The result would be that after a {\i chalitzah} they might still permit {\i biah} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'atei l\u8217'miba\u8217'al achar chalitzah} \u8212- which would be a severe violation. The same logic applies to {\i maimar}: were it declared null, people would conclude from parallel reasoning that {\i biah} in this context is also ineffective, leading to the possibility of {\i biah} after {\i biah} \u8212- violating the principle of {\i bayis echad hu boneh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is that the Rabbis needed these institutions to carry some weight in order to prevent the entire halakhic structure of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} from becoming unintelligible to the public. Whether this represents a purely reactive rabbinic response to existing popular practice or a more proactive rabbinic design is a nuanced question \u8212- the {\i Gemara}\u8217's framing here sounds reactive, as though people were already performing these acts and the Rabbis could not simply negate them. Other sources may present a more deliberately constructive picture of the institution of {\i maimar} in particular. In any case, the conclusion is firm: {\i get} and {\i maimar} in the {\i yibum} context must carry {\i some} halakhic meaning \u8212- {\i mehani} \u8212- even if not the full force of the Torah-level acts they parallel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biah Pesulah Doctrin (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biah After Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: {\i u\u8217'mai ta\u8217'ama amru rabanan bi\u8217'ah pesulah yesh achareiha klum} \u8212- why did the Rabbis say that after an invalid {\i biah} ({\i biah pesulah}), subsequent acts remain meaningful? To answer this, one must understand what {\i biah pesulah} actually is. The Mishna at the close of the preceding discussion states: {\i bizman she\u8217'hi b\u8217'techilah ein achareiha klum} \u8212- when {\i biah} comes at the outset of the {\i yibum} process, the act of {\i yibum} is complete and nothing afterward counts. But {\i ba\u8217'emtza uv\u8217'sof} \u8212- when {\i biah} comes after something else has already transpired \u8212- things that follow it can still be meaningful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paradigmatic case of {\i biah pesulah} involves a scenario where the {\i yavam} gave a {\i get} to one {\i yevama} and then performed {\i biah} with another. The {\i Gemara} reasons: {\i gezeirah bi\u8217'ah achar get mishum bi\u8217'ah achar chalitzah} \u8212- the Rabbis treat {\i biah} following a {\i get} as problematic by analogy to {\i biah} following {\i chalitzah}. The logic is as follows. Suppose, instead of giving a {\i get} to the first {\i yevama}, the {\i yavam} had performed {\i chalitzah} with her \u8212- then {\i biah} with the second would be entirely forbidden, as {\i chalitzah} definitively closes the process. Since {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} are easily confused, and since permitting {\i biah} after {\i get} risks being confused with the forbidden {\i biah} after {\i chalitzah}, the Rabbis characterize {\i biah} after {\i get} as {\i biah pesulah} as well. The consequence is that although {\i d\u8217'oraisa} the {\i biah} constitutes valid {\i yibum} with the second {\i yevama}, the Rabbis do not allow the {\i yavam} to remain in that relationship; he must give her a {\i get} and she requires {\i chalitzah} to be fully released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case of {\i biah pesulah} arises when {\i biah} follows a {\i maimar}. If the {\i yavam} performed {\i maimar} with one {\i yevama} and then {\i biah} with another, the principle {\i bayis echad hu boneh v\u8217'eino boneh shnei batim} \u8212- that a man builds one household, not two \u8212- means that had the first act been full {\i yibum} rather than {\i maimar}, the {\i biah} with the second would be impermissible. Since {\i maimar} is a quasi-{\i yibum}, the {\i Gemara} treats {\i biah} after {\i maimar} as {\i biah pesulah} by a {\i gezeirah}: {\i gezeirah bi\u8217'ah achar ma\u8217'amar mishum bi\u8217'ah achar biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The crucial consequence of {\i biah pesulah} is that the relationship is not considered fully consummated. Something remains unresolved, and therefore subsequent acts \u8212- giving a {\i get}, performing {\i maimar}, or doing {\i chalitzah} \u8212- can still carry halakhic meaning. The {\i Gemara} designates this with the phrase {\i yesh achareiha klum} \u8212- there is still something after it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah vs.\u160 ?Biah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point one might ask: if {\i biah} after {\i get} is rendered {\i pesulah} because of its similarity to the forbidden {\i biah} after {\i chalitzah}, why does {\i chalitzah} after {\i get} not similarly become {\i chalitzah pesulah}? The {\i Gemara} addresses this directly: {\i mai nigzar} \u8212- what decree should we make? If we were to treat {\i chalitzah} after {\i get} as {\i pesulah} because it resembles {\i chalitzah} after {\i chalitzah}, the consequence would only be that we demand further {\i chalitzahs} \u8212- {\i kol ki hani tachlots v\u8217'teitzei} \u8212- and since {\i chalitzah} is not a forbidden act, requiring additional {\i chalitzahs} carries no grave consequence. It does affect the woman\u8217's status as a {\i chalutzah} and her prohibition to a {\i kohen}, but it does not generate the kind of severe {\i arayot} violation that motivates the {\i gezeirah} regarding {\i biah}. {\i Biah} with a woman one is forbidden to is a fundamentally different order of problem from performing an extra {\i chalitzah}. Therefore the {\i gezeirah} applies to {\i biah pesulah} but not to {\i chalitzah}: a {\i chalitzah} that follows a {\i get} or a {\i maimar} remains fully valid and ends the process \u8212- {\i ein achar chalitzah klum}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All-or-Nothing Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to explain Rabban Gamliel\u8217's position in depth. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Rabban Gamliel} \u8212- what is Rabban Gamliel\u8217's reasoning that even {\i get} after {\i get} and {\i maimar} after {\i maimar} are without effect? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i demispeka lei get ee dachi ee lo dachi, maimar ee kani ee lo kani} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel is uncertain whether a {\i get} in the {\i yibum} context fully \u8220"pushes off\u8221" ({\i dachi}) all the {\i yevamot}, or does nothing at all; and similarly whether {\i maimar} fully \u8220"acquires\u8221" ({\i kani}) the {\i yevama} in a quasi-marital bond, or accomplishes nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The binary nature of this uncertainty is what drives his ruling. If the {\i get} fully pushes off \u8212- {\i ee ka madchi} \u8212- then the first {\i get} has already resolved the situation with respect to all the {\i yevamot}, and the second {\i get} finds nothing left to accomplish: {\i basar mai ka avid}. If the {\i get} accomplishes nothing \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ee kama lo dachi} \u8212- then even the first {\i get} did nothing, and the second certainly does not: {\i basar nami lo dachi}. Either the first fully worked or neither works. The same analysis applies to {\i maimar}: if the first {\i maimar} fully acquired the {\i yevama}, the second is superfluous; if the first acquired nothing, the second is no better. The only meaningful act is therefore always the first one \u8212- which {\i might} be fully effective \u8212- while every subsequent act of the same kind is certainly without consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consistency Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This binary model has a striking implication that Tosafot and Rashi both highlight. Rabban Gamliel says {\i ein get achar get v\u8217'ein maimar achar maimar} \u8212- but he appears to concede that a {\i get} after a {\i maimar}, or a {\i maimar} after a {\i get}, {\i can} be meaningful. If he truly held that these rabbinic acts function at full force, why would mixing them matter? The binary model explains this elegantly. The uncertainty about {\i get} and the uncertainty about {\i maimar} are independent of one another: {\i get} might be effective or might not; {\i maimar} might acquire or might not. They need not share the same outcome. Therefore, if {\i maimar} was effective and {\i get} was not, the {\i get} after {\i maimar} does something previously unaddressed, and vice versa. Two acts of the same type, however, necessarily target the same outcome \u8212- either the first worked and the second is redundant, or the first failed and the second will likewise fail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye raises a sharp difficulty against Rabban Gamliel\u8217's binary model. The Mishna acknowledges that Rabban Gamliel concedes the existence of {\i biah pesulah} \u8212- that {\i biah} following {\i get} or {\i maimar} remains problematic and subsequent acts carry meaning. Abaye asks: {\i v\u8217'ee salka da\u8217'atach d\u8217'Rabban Gamliel havyah k\u8217'viah d\u8217'lechatchilah v\u8217'tikni} \u8212- if the {\i maimar} is either fully effective or fully zero, as Rabban Gamliel maintains, how does {\i biah pesulah} arise at all? If the {\i maimar} with the first {\i yevama} was fully effective, then the subsequent {\i biah} with the second is like {\i biah} that comes {\i b\u8217'techilah} \u8212- at the outset \u8212- and nothing afterward counts: {\i ein achareiha klum}. If the {\i maimar} was zero, then the subsequent {\i biah} is a clean {\i biah} not preceded by anything, and again nothing afterward would count. On either prong of the binary, there is no room for {\i biah pesulah}. Yet Rabban Gamliel concedes the category. The binary model, taken strictly, cannot account for it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fifty-Percent Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Halves Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's challenge dissolves Rabban Gamliel\u8217's pure binary framework and compels a different account of how {\i get} and {\i maimar} function. {\i Ella amar Abaye} \u8212- rather, Abaye says, the correct understanding is that the Rabbis established {\i get} and {\i maimar} in the {\i yibum} context not as all-or-nothing propositions, but as acts that each address exactly one half of the {\i zikah} bond. The {\i zikah} \u8212- the quasi-spousal connection that ties the {\i yevama} to the {\i yavam} \u8212- is not a single undifferentiated legal relationship but is conceptually composed of two distinct components.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slot Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One half of the {\i zikah} is the {\i kinyan} component \u8212- the aspect of acquisition and quasi-marital attachment. This is the half that {\i maimar} addresses. The other half is the {\i dechiyah} component \u8212- the aspect of release and severance. This is the half that {\i get} addresses. {\i Maimar} fills the {\i kinyan} slot; {\i get} fills the {\i dechiyah} slot. Each act operates on its own distinct portion of the {\i zikah}: {\i bechad tzad mahani bah get uvechad tzad mahani bah maimar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same vs.\u160 ?Different Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This fifty-percent model explains the full pattern of what does and does not carry effect. {\i Get achar get lo dachi} \u8212- a second {\i get} cannot do anything because the first {\i get} already addressed the {\i dechiyah} half; there is nothing remaining in that slot for the second to work on. {\i Maimar achar maimar lo kani} \u8212- a second {\i maimar} similarly finds the {\i kinyan} half already addressed. But {\i get achar maimar} and {\i maimar achar get} do carry meaning, because each act addresses the half that the other left untouched. The cross-combination works precisely because the two acts target distinct halves of the {\i zikah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Connection Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fifty-percent model also explains {\i biah pesulah} in a way the binary model could not. When a {\i maimar} was performed with one {\i yevama}, the {\i yavam} has forged a half-connection to her \u8212- the {\i kinyan} half of the {\i zikah} has been engaged. When he then performs {\i biah} with a second {\i yevama}, this is not a {\i biah} that comes against the backdrop of either zero or a full prior relationship. There is a genuine, if partial, prior bond to the first {\i yevama} \u8212- the released half of the {\i zikah} remains unreleased. This unresolved partial connection makes the {\i biah} with the second {\i yevama} a {\i biah pesulah}: it is not the clean, first-in-time {\i biah} of a complete {\i yibum} situation. The relationship is therefore not treated as concluded, and subsequent acts retain halakhic significance. The intermediate state is not a conceptual fiction born of doubt \u8212- it is a real halakhic condition arising from the structure of the {\i zikah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} elaborates further: {\i biah pesulah} occupies an intermediate position \u8212- stronger than {\i maimar} in some respects, weaker in others. {\i Adifa mi\u8217'maimar} \u8212- stronger than {\i maimar} \u8212- in that if a second {\i maimar} follows the first, it is meaningless because it tries to fill an already-occupied slot; but {\i biah} after {\i maimar} does carry force because the {\i biah}, being {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, effectively dominates and causes us to view the situation as {\i biah} followed by {\i maimar} \u8212- a different pairing that can still be operative. {\i Gri\u8217'ah mi\u8217'maimar} \u8212- weaker than {\i maimar} \u8212- in that if {\i maimar} had filled its half of the {\i zikah}, a second {\i maimar} that follows would find nothing left; but because {\i biah} fills that slot in a weaker mode, the {\i maimar} that follows it remains operative rather than being treated as a redundant overlay.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biah Pesulah Doctrin (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Efficacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fifty-percent model also clarifies why {\i chalitzah} that follows {\i maimar} or {\i get} remains fully valid and terminates the process. {\i Chalitzah} is not an act that targets one half of the {\i zikah} to the exclusion of the other; it is a Torah-level act of release that cuts through the entire bond. Whether the {\i kinyan} half or the {\i dechiyah} half has or has not been previously addressed at the rabbinic level, {\i chalitzah d\u8217'oraisa} dissolves whatever remains. There is therefore no basis for treating it as {\i chalitzah pesulah} \u8212- it accomplishes its full work regardless of what preceded it. The consequence is that once {\i chalitzah} has been performed, the process is genuinely complete: {\i ein achar chalitzah klum}. The {\i yavam} may need to address whatever rabbinic acts were done beforehand \u8212- for example, giving a {\i get} to the {\i yevama} with whom {\i maimar} was performed \u8212- but the {\i chalitzah} itself is unimpaired and conclusive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chachamim\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Individual Slots\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} accept the basic architecture of the fifty-percent model \u8212- that every relationship between a {\i yavam} and a {\i yevama} contains a {\i kinyan} slot addressable by {\i maimar} and a {\i dechiyah} slot addressable by {\i get} \u8212- but they contest Rabban Gamliel\u8217's premise that these slots are shared across the entire class of {\i yevamot} and {\i yevamim}. The {\i chachamim} hold: {\i kol chad v\u8217'chad tikinu lei Rabbanan get u\u8217'maimar b\u8217'yevama} \u8212- the Rabbis established a {\i get} and a {\i maimar} for each individual brother-in-law with each individual {\i yevama}. Every {\i yavam}\u8211-{\i yevama} pairing has its own independent set of slots.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problematic Efficacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This individualization means that even when one {\i yavam} has performed {\i maimar} with a particular {\i yevama} \u8212- filling the {\i kinyan} slot in their relationship \u8212- a second {\i yavam} can come and perform {\i maimar} with that same {\i yevama}, addressing {\i his} {\i kinyan} slot in {\i their} pairing, which is distinct. Similarly, a second {\i maimar} with a different {\i yevama} addresses that pairing\u8217's independent slot. The result is that what Rabban Gamliel treats as exhausted \u8212- because the class-wide slot has been filled \u8212- the {\i chachamim} treat as potentially still operative, because each pairing maintains its own capacity. This does not mean that subsequent acts confer rights or privileges upon the second {\i yavam}; rather, it means they generate halakhic complications, obligations, and prohibitions \u8212- the need for additional {\i gitin}, {\i chalitzot}, and so forth. Once these entanglements accumulate, the practical result is a complex web of relationships that must each be individually resolved. The {\i chachamim}\u8217's disagreement with Rabban Gamliel is therefore not about whether the fifty-percent model is correct, but about whether the slots are pooled or individuated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Sisters, One Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RG: Two Gets Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents the practical cases from the {\i baraita} that illustrate the dispute. The case of two {\i yevamot} and one {\i yavam}: he gave a {\i get} to one and a {\i get} to the other \u8212- {\i natan get l\u8217'zo v\u8217'get l\u8217'zo}. Rabban Gamliel rules: {\i choletz l\u8217'rishonah v\u8217'asur b\u8217'krovoteha u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'krovot shniyah} \u8212- he does {\i chalitzah} with the first {\i yevama}, since the second {\i get} was entirely without effect, and he is forbidden to her relatives. With the second {\i yevama}, the {\i get} was meaningless and she carries no {\i grusha} status; he is therefore permitted to her relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim: Both Divorced\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} rule differently: {\i natan get l\u8217'zo v\u8217'get l\u8217'zo asur b\u8217'krovot shteihen} \u8212- the second {\i get} also carries meaning, rendering her a quasi-{\i gerushah}, and he is forbidden to the relatives of both. Moreover, since both carry {\i grusha} status, {\i chalitzah l\u8217'achat mehen} \u8212- a {\i chalitzah} to either one suffices, as neither has a halakhic advantage over the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel: Two Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} notes the symmetric case: {\i v\u8217'chen ata omer b\u8217'shnei yevamim v\u8217'yevamah achat} \u8212- the same principles apply when two brothers-in-law each give a {\i get} to the same {\i yevama}. According to Rabban Gamliel, only the first {\i yavam}\u8217's {\i get} carries effect; he is forbidden to her relatives. The second {\i yavam}\u8217's {\i get} is without consequence; he remains permitted to her relatives. The {\i chachamim} hold that the second {\i yavam}\u8217's {\i get} also carries effect, and he too is forbidden to her relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RG: Two Maimars Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to the {\i maimar} cases: two {\i yevamot} and one {\i yavam}, and he performed {\i maimar} with both \u8212- {\i asa ma\u8217'amar b\u8217'zo u\u8217'ma\u8217'amar b\u8217'zo}. Rabban Gamliel rules: {\i noten get l\u8217'rishonah v\u8217'choletz lah v\u8217'asur b\u8217'krovoteha u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'krovot shniyah} \u8212- only the first {\i maimar} was operative, so he must give a {\i get} for the {\i maimar} and perform {\i chalitzah} with the first {\i yevama}, and {\i chalitzah} should not be done with the second so as not to impose {\i chalutzah} status upon her unnecessarily, since the second {\i maimar} was meaningless. He is forbidden to the first\u8217's relatives and permitted to the second\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim: Both Need Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} rule: {\i noten get l\u8217'shteihen} \u8212- the second {\i maimar} was also operative, so both {\i yevamot} require a {\i get}. Because both carry quasi-{\i gerushah} status, he is forbidden to the relatives of both \u8212- {\i asur b\u8217'krovot shteihen} \u8212- and {\i chalitzah l\u8217'achat mehen} is sufficient. Again, the symmetric case of two brothers-in-law performing {\i maimar} with a single {\i yevama} follows the same logic in both directions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah After Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a challenge from Shmuel\u8217's earlier ruling. Rabban Gamliel has said that in the case of two {\i gets} and two {\i yevamot}, one need only do {\i chalitzah} with the first {\i yevama} \u8212- {\i choletz l\u8217'rishonah} \u8212- and that is sufficient. But Shmuel had stated: {\i chalatz l\u8217'ba\u8217'alas haget lo nifterah tzarah} \u8212- if the {\i yavam} did {\i chalitzah} with the {\i yevama} who had received a {\i get}, the co-wife is {\i not} thereby released. Shmuel\u8217's position is that {\i chalitzah} following a {\i get} is a weakened {\i chalitzah} \u8212- something like {\i chalitzah pesulah} \u8212- because the {\i get} already diminished the {\i zikah} bond, and therefore this {\i chalitzah} does not exempt the co-wife; all co-wives must receive individual {\i chalitzahs}. This appears to flatly contradict Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling that one {\i chalitzah} to the first {\i yevama} suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by placing Shmuel\u8217's ruling in its proper doctrinal context. Shmuel himself explains: {\i ki amina ana aliba d\u8217'man d\u8217'amar yeish zikah} \u8212- when I said that {\i chalitzah} after {\i get} is weakened and requires multiple {\i chalitzahs}, I was speaking according to the view that there is a {\i zikah}, a genuine quasi-spousal bond connecting the {\i yevama} to the {\i yavam}. On that view, the {\i get} has already eroded the {\i zikah}, and the {\i chalitzah} that follows it is correspondingly diminished in force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RG: No Zikah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel, however, holds {\i ein zikah} \u8212- there is no {\i zikah}. While there is a {\i mitzvah} of {\i chalitzah} binding the {\i yevama} to the process, Rabban Gamliel does not recognize the category of a quasi-marital bond called {\i zikah} as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequence: One Chalitz\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If there is no {\i zikah} to be weakened, then a {\i get} given to a {\i yevama} cannot diminish what does not exist. The {\i chalitzah} that follows the {\i get} is therefore not a weakened {\i chalitzah} \u8212- it is as full and effective as any other {\i chalitzah}. Consequently, Rabban Gamliel can consistently hold that one {\i chalitzah} with the first {\i yevama} suffices, with no need to repeat the process for the second. Shmuel\u8217's ruling of multiple {\i chalitzahs} and Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling of a single {\i chalitzah} reflect not a contradiction but the application of each position to its own foundational premise \u8212- {\i yeish zikah} versus {\i ein zikah} \u8212- and the two rulings are entirely coherent with one another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 52\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar/Get debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 52. We pick up on 51b, in the middle of the extended discussion concerning {\i ma\u8217'amar} after {\i ma\u8217'amar} and {\i get} after {\i get}. The most technically demanding portions of that analysis are largely behind us. What the {\i gemara} is doing now is clarifying how these principles interact with related questions raised earlier in the sugya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabban Gamliel says {\i ein ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar v\u8217'ein get achar get}, the {\i gemara} considers a specific scenario: the {\i yavam} gave a {\i get} to two {\i yevamot}, and according to Rabban Gamliel he may ignore the second co-wife and proceed to perform {\i chalitzah} with the first. The {\i gemara}\u8217's question is how this squares with Shmuel, who holds that a {\i chalitzah} following a {\i get} is a weakened {\i chalitzah pesulah} \u8212- because the {\i get} has already attenuated the {\i zika} \u8212- and in such a case the {\i chalitzah} must be extended to the other co-wife as well. Yet Rabban Gamliel rules that even in the case of {\i chalitzah} after {\i get}, the other woman is completely released. The {\i gemara} resolves this by attributing to Shmuel the position of {\i yesh zika}: the {\i get} weakens the {\i zika}, thereby weakening the {\i chalitzah} that follows. Rabban Gamliel, by contrast, holds {\i ein zika}, so the {\i get} has no such attenuating effect on the {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zika disagreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then suggests that the {\i rabbanan} who disagree with Rabban Gamliel hold {\i yesh zika}, and examines the end of the relevant {\i mishnah} \u8212- the case of two {\i yevamin} and one {\i yevamah}, where both brothers gave her a {\i get} and the {\i chalitzah} is performed by only one. If the {\i rabbanan} hold {\i yesh zika}, the {\i chalitzah} following the {\i get} would be a {\i chalitzah pesulah}, and by the principle stated in the name of Rav Abba bar Avina in the name of Rav \u8212- that a {\i chalitzah pesulah} requires going back to all the brothers \u8212- each brother should have to perform {\i chalitzah} separately. Yet the {\i mishnah} indicates that one {\i chalitzah} suffices, which would seem to contradict Rav Abba bar Avina\u8217's ruling if the {\i rabbanan} hold {\i yesh zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Zika consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba bar Avina resolves this by stating that both Rabban Gamliel and the {\i rabbanan} in fact hold {\i ein zika}. The entire framework of a weakened {\i chalitzah} requiring repetition across all brothers and co-wives depends on the position of {\i yesh zika}, whereby the {\i get} drains strength from the {\i zika} and consequently from the {\i chalitzah} that follows. Since both Rabban Gamliel and the {\i rabbanan} reject this \u8212- they hold {\i ein zika} \u8212- the {\i get} has no impact on the {\i chalitzah}, the {\i chalitzah} is not weakened, and it is therefore not a challenge to Rav Abba bar Avina\u8217's principle. Their dispute is confined to the question of {\i ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar} and {\i get achar get}, not to the consequences of {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezeirah concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amar Mar setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to examine a previously cited statement more closely. The case: a {\i yavam} performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with each of two co-wives. Rabban Gamliel rules: ignore the second {\i ma\u8217'amar} as meaningless, and treat only the first as operative. To extricate himself from the first, he gives her a {\i get} to dissolve the {\i ma\u8217'amar} and then performs {\i chalitzah}. He is then permitted to marry the relatives of the second woman, since the second {\i ma\u8217'amar} was legally void and created no relationship. He does not perform {\i chalitzah} with the second woman, since doing so would damage her status unnecessarily; the relevant legal relationship exists only with the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why break first?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates an obvious difficulty. If the second {\i ma\u8217'amar} was entirely meaningless \u8212- as Rabban Gamliel explicitly holds \u8212- then the first {\i ma\u8217'amar} remains uncompromised. Why must he break off from the first wife with a {\i get} and {\i chalitzah} at all? He should simply be permitted to proceed directly to {\i yibum} with her, since the second {\i ma\u8217'amar} never affected their relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i gezeira}. Even though the {\i yibum} with the first wife would be entirely permitted in this case, we are concerned that observers will draw an incorrect inference. They will see that a {\i ma\u8217'amar} was followed by {\i yibum} and reason that the same logic applies to the second {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- thus permitting {\i yibum} after the second {\i ma\u8217'amar} in some future case, which would be forbidden because one cannot establish two households simultaneously ({\i bayis echad hu boneh v\u8217'eino boneh shenei batim}). Once two co-wives are implicated, we require complete dissolution even according to Rabban Gamliel \u8212- not because the first relationship is legally compromised, but to preserve clarity and prevent confusion in analogous cases. The {\i gezeira} is admittedly somewhat far-fetched, since the present case itself presents no problem once the {\i yibum} is performed with one. Nevertheless, the rabbis preferred to keep the rules clean rather than risk someone drawing the wrong conclusion in a structurally similar situation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar opinions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan states that Rabban Gamliel, Beit Shammai, Rabbi Shimon ben Azzai, and Rabbi Nechemiah all hold, in varying formulations, that {\i ma\u8217'amar koneh kinyan gamur} \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'amar} effects a complete {\i kinyan}. It is important to note, as Tosafot emphasizes repeatedly, that saying all these {\i tannaim} hold the same position means they share a family resemblance of approach, not an identical ruling. Beginning with Rabban Gamliel: his position, as already established, is {\i ein ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar}. Once a {\i ma\u8217'amar} has been performed, no second {\i ma\u8217'amar} can take hold. Within the {\i ma\u8217'amar} slot, the first act fills it completely and leaves no room for a subsequent one \u8212- demonstrating that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is a full {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case for Beit Shammai involves three brothers: two married to two sisters and one {\i mufneh} (unmarried). One of the married brothers dies. The single brother performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with that brother\u8217's widow, Rachel. Then the second married brother dies, leaving his widow Leah. At this point the {\i yavam} has a {\i zika} to both women \u8212- who are sisters. Normally, since he is bound to both, he would need to perform {\i chalitzah} with each, since consummating with one would render the other {\i achos zikukaso}, a prohibited relation under {\i arayot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai rules: since he has already performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Rachel, it is as if he has already consummated the {\i yibum}. The {\i ma\u8217'amar} operates at a {\i d\u8217'oraita} level for Beit Shammai, reframing the relationship as though Rachel is already his wife. Leah therefore walks away entirely as an {\i ervah} \u8212- she requires no {\i chalitzah}. This is the paradigmatic expression of {\i ma\u8217'amar koneh kinyan gamur}: the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is treated as equivalent to consummation itself, with full {\i d\u8217'oraita} consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case for Rabbi Shimon involves two brothers who are both under the age of Bar Mitzvah \u8212- nine years old and a day \u8212- who each had relations with a {\i yevamah}. The {\i halacha} establishes that {\i bias katan} (intercourse by a minor) is treated by the rabbis as equivalent to {\i ma\u8217'amar}: it is not a full act of {\i yibum}, since the boy has not reached adulthood, but it carries sufficient legal significance to be treated as a rabbinic {\i ma\u8217'amar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If both underage brothers had relations with the same {\i yevamah}, this constitutes the analog of {\i ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar}. The {\i chachamim} hold that both must give a {\i get}. Rabbi Shimon disagrees: {\i im bias rishon bia, bias sheni einah bia} \u8212- if the first act was legally meaningful, the second is not. And {\i v\u8217'im bias rishon einah bia, bias sheni nami einah bia} \u8212- if the first is not meaningful at all, neither is the second. Either way, there is no need to address the second brother\u8217's act. This is the direct parallel to Rabban Gamliel\u8217's {\i ein ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar}, applied to the domain of {\i bias katan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Azzai\u8217's position is more nuanced. He holds {\i yesh ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar bishnei yevamin v\u8217'yevamah achas} \u8212- if there are two brothers-in-law and one {\i yevamah}, the second {\i ma\u8217'amar} (from the second brother) is meaningful. However, {\i ein ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar bishtei yevamot v\u8217'yavam echad} \u8212- if there is one {\i yavam} and two {\i yevamot}, once he has used his {\i ma\u8217'amar} on one woman, he cannot perform a valid {\i ma\u8217'amar} on the other. There is a dispute between Rashi and Tosafot over whether the {\i girsa} should be read in the opposite direction. The underlying question is which configuration better fits the {\i kiddushin} model: intuitively, it makes more sense that a woman who is already {\i mekudeshes} cannot be the object of a second {\i ma\u8217'amar} from another man, but the {\i girsa} and its reversal are debated. Whichever reading one accepts, Ben Azzai agrees with Rabban Gamliel in at least one configuration of {\i ein ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Nechemiah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nechemiah states: {\i achas bia v\u8217'achas chalitza bein batchila bein ba-emtza bein basof, ein achareiha klum} \u8212- whether it is {\i bia} or {\i chalitza}, whether performed at the beginning, middle, or end of the process, nothing counts afterward. This is more sweeping than what we have established elsewhere. We have generally said that {\i bia pesulah} (defective intercourse) is treated rabinically as equivalent to {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and that something may still be done afterward. Rabbi Nechemiah disagrees: even a {\i bia pesulah} in the middle of the process terminates it entirely. The {\i gemara} notes that this position presupposes {\i ein ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar}, since {\i bia pesulah} has been assimilated to {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and Rabbi Nechemiah holds that once that has occurred, nothing further can follow. It should be noted that Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's view is not identical to Rabban Gamliel\u8217's \u8212- these {\i tannaim} share the general orientation that {\i ma\u8217'amar} or its equivalent forecloses what follows, but the specific applications differ.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Keitzad meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to the question {\i keitzad asa ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- how is {\i ma\u8217'amar} performed? But before the {\i gemara} can address the mechanics, it must clarify what the {\i mishnah} is doing. The section proceeds to discuss hybrid cases \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'amar} followed by {\i get}, {\i get} followed by {\i chalitzah}, and so forth \u8212- rather than the pairings of {\i ma\u8217'amar} after {\i ma\u8217'amar} or {\i get} after {\i get} that dominated the preceding discussion. The {\i gemara} notes the apparent incongruity: the {\i mishnah} says {\i keitzad} as though elaborating on {\i get achar get}, yet it goes on to discuss different acts combined with each other. Rav Yehuda clarifies: the debate between Rabban Gamliel and the {\i chachamim} regarding {\i get achar get} and {\i ma\u8217'amar achar ma\u8217'amar} is what was already stated. {\i Keitzad} introduces the hybrid cases \u8212- one {\i yavam} and one {\i yevamah} \u8212- where two qualitatively different acts are combined, and asks what the implications of each sequence are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic sequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In all hybrid cases, both Rabban Gamliel and the {\i chachamim} agree that the second act remains meaningful, since it is of a different type than the first. The {\i mishnah} then proceeds through the various combinations. {\i Asa ma\u8217'amar v\u8217'natan lah get, tzricha heimenu chalitzah} \u8212- if he began with {\i ma\u8217'amar} and then gave a {\i get}, he has not concluded the process and she still requires {\i chalitzah} from him. This is the standard incomplete-release scenario. The {\i mishnah} also addresses {\i ma\u8217'amar} followed by {\i yibum}: {\i asa ma\u8217'amar u\u8217'va\u8217'al harei zu kemitzvatah} \u8212- if he performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} and then consummated the {\i yibum}, this is the proper procedure. Just as {\i kiddushin} precedes {\i nisuin} in ordinary marriage, here the {\i ma\u8217'amar} precedes and frames the act of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether the phrase {\i harei zu kemitzvatah} supports Rav Huna\u8217's ruling. Rav Huna states: {\i mitzvat yavam mekadesh v\u8217'achar kach bo\u8217'el} \u8212- the ideal manner of performing {\i yibum} is first to perform {\i ma\u8217'amar} (the rabbinic analog of {\i kiddushin}) and only then to have intercourse, mirroring the structure of normal marriage in which {\i kiddushin} precedes {\i nisuin}. The logic is compelling not only formally but substantively: one should not begin the relationship with the physical act. The relationship ought to be framed legally and relationally before the intimacy follows \u8212- even where the {\i d\u8217'oraita} act is the intercourse itself and the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is purely rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reframing question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a concern: when {\i ma\u8217'amar} precedes {\i yibum}, does this not reframe the entire relationship such that it no longer looks like {\i yibum} at all? Once the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is performed, the {\i gemara} reasons, {\i parchah himenu zikat yavam v\u8217'chalah aleha zikat eirusin} \u8212- the {\i yibum} bond falls away and is replaced by an {\i erusin} bond. The woman is no longer treated as a {\i yevamah} but as an {\i arusah} in the ordinary sense. If that is so, perhaps the ideal procedure should avoid {\i ma\u8217'amar} before {\i yibum}, lest it transform the relationship into something that loses its character as {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acceptable answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} dismisses this concern: {\i af zo kemitzvatah} \u8212- even this is an acceptable, indeed praiseworthy, manner of performing the {\i mitzvah}. The reframing is not a problem; it is in fact the preferred result. Remarkably, the Rambam rules ({\i Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Yibum}) that when a {\i yavam} does {\i ma\u8217'amar} with his {\i yevamah}, he recites {\i birkat nisuin} \u8212- {\i sheva brachot} \u8212- with ten men present, and writes a {\i ketubah}. The act is treated in every external respect exactly like a normal marriage. The underlying {\i d\u8217'oraita} category remains {\i yibum}, but the rabbinic overlay of {\i ma\u8217'amar} causes the relationship to present itself in precisely the form of ordinary {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin}, and according to the Rambam this is entirely the point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav\u8217's lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites Rav Huna\u8217's full statement: {\i mekadesh v\u8217'achar kach bo\u8217'el} is the ideal; however, {\i sheim ba v\u8217'lo asah ma\u8217'amar kanah} \u8212- if he had intercourse without having performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} at all, the {\i yibum} is nonetheless valid. The {\i gemara} immediately questions this: {\i peshita}, of course the {\i yibum} takes effect \u8212- {\i kaniah be\u8217'via}. The question is therefore refined: the point is that even if he reversed the order entirely, having intercourse without ever performing {\i ma\u8217'amar}, it still works. But the {\i gemara} counters with a {\i baraita}: such a person receives {\i makat mardut} \u8212- rabbinic lashes for defying the rabbinic norm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Makat Mardut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The lashes are not because the {\i yibum} is invalid. The {\i yibum} is entirely valid {\i d\u8217'oraita}. Rather, the lashes are {\i makat mardut midrabbanan} \u8212- punishment for contravening the rabbinic preference for {\i ma\u8217'amar} to precede intercourse. The institution of {\i ma\u8217'amar} was created precisely so that {\i yibum} not be inaugurated purely by the physical act, mirroring the rabbinic insistence in ordinary marriage that {\i kiddushin} precede {\i nisuin}. Bypassing this framework violates the rabbinic expectation, even though it does not invalidate the underlying transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Broader principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to a broader principle of Rav\u8217's, who would administer rabbinic lashes in several analogous situations. Rav would give {\i malkut} to one who performed {\i kiddushin} by means of {\i bia} \u8212- using intercourse as the act of {\i kiddushin} rather than {\i kesef} or {\i shtar}. This is striking, since {\i kiddushei bia} is itself a biblical institution \u8212- the first {\i mishnah} of {\i Kiddushin} lists {\i kesef, shtar, v\u8217'via} as the three modes of {\i kiddushin}. Yet Rav held this to be morally and socially unacceptable, regardless of its legal validity {\i d\u8217'oraita}. His principle: sex should not inaugurate the relationship; the legal commitment should precede the physical one. Similarly, Rav would give lashes for one who performed {\i kiddushin be-shuka} \u8212- giving {\i kesef kiddushin} in a public marketplace. Whatever the precise objection (whether it is the lack of intimacy in the setting, the impulsiveness it suggests, or the objectifying nature of a public transaction), the act is deemed improper. And likewise for one who performed {\i kiddushin belo shidukhei} \u8212- without any prior courtship or engagement process. Just as the physical relationship should be preceded by the legal commitment, the legal commitment ({\i kiddushin}) should itself be preceded by a considered process of {\i shidukhim}. One should not leap directly into {\i kiddushin} without having built a relationship first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav extends these lashes further: to one who effects {\i bittul} of a {\i get} when the {\i shaliach} is not present (causing the woman to believe she is divorced when she is not); to one who submits a {\i moda\u8217'a} before signing a {\i get} (a prior declaration that the document he is about to sign is given under duress and therefore invalid \u8212- Tosafot notes that even where these acts may technically fail on legal grounds, the mere doing of them casts doubt on the woman\u8217's status and constitutes {\i motzi la\u8217'az} on the {\i get}); to one who behaves contemptuously toward a rabbinic messenger; and to one who has had a {\i niduy} placed on him for thirty days without appearing before the court to resolve it. Finally, Rav addresses one who lives in his father-in-law\u8217's house, where the concern is proximity to his mother-in-law in her husband\u8217's absence. The {\i gemara} qualifies: {\i dedayer in, dechalif lau} \u8212- only one who actually resides there, not one who merely passes by. The case of Rav Sheshet, who administered lashes to someone who was merely passing the door of his father-in-law\u8217's house, is explained differently: that individual was already under suspicion of an improper relationship, and the frequent appearances made the concern concrete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nahardai limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nahardeai} qualify Rav\u8217's practice significantly: in none of these cases would Rav actually administer lashes except when two factors converged \u8212- {\i kiddushin} performed via {\i bia} and the complete absence of {\i shidukhim}. Only when the intercourse was the very first act, with no prior courtship whatsoever, did Rav act. A dissenting view among the {\i nahardeai} holds that even when {\i shidukhim} did precede, if the {\i kiddushin} were effected via {\i bia} it is still {\i mishum pritzuta} \u8212- licentious \u8212- and lashes are appropriate. This latter view coheres with the general orientation noted earlier: the insistence that {\i ma\u8217'amar} precede {\i yibum}, the parallel insistence that {\i kiddushin} precede marital intercourse, and the further insistence that {\i shidukhim} precede {\i kiddushin} \u8212- all reflect a consistent rabbinic concern that relationships be built in ordered stages, each stage providing the framework within which the next properly occurs.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar wording\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cash/Shtar method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} now addresses the mechanics of {\i ma\u8217'amar}: {\i natan lah kesef o shaveh kesef} \u8212- he gives her money or something worth money, precisely as in regular {\i kiddushin}. Rashi understands that accompanying words are required \u8212- something like {\i hitkadshi li} or {\i hitkadshi li b\u8217'ma\u8217'amar yevamin} \u8212- though the {\i gemara} itself focuses on the act of giving rather than specifying the formula. The {\i gemara} proceeds to ask about {\i ma\u8217'amar} by {\i shtar}: if a {\i get} of a {\i yevamah} is rabbinic, is the {\i shtar} of {\i ma\u8217'amar} analogous to a normal {\i shtar kiddushin}? The answer given is {\i ki-de-amran} \u8212- yes, like a standard {\i shtar kiddushin}, written on paper or pottery, worth less than a {\i perutah}, bearing the formula {\i harei at mekudeshet li}. The {\i gemara} does not posit different language for {\i ma\u8217'amar} in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar wording\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye reframes the question: the {\i mishnah}\u8217's inquiry about {\i shtar} is actually asking how one writes a {\i ketubah} for {\i yibum} \u8212- {\i shtar ketuvas yevamin keitzad}? The {\i ketubah} obligation has now transferred to the {\i yavam}, and a new document must be written. The formula: {\i ana ploni bar ploni kabbaleit yat plonit yevamti alai la-zun u-le-farnesah ke-ra\u8217'uy} \u8212- I, so-and-so son of so-and-so, accept so-and-so, my {\i yevamah}, upon me, to sustain and support her as is fitting. This constitutes the ongoing {\i tenai ketubah}. The {\i gemara} adds: the lien for collecting the {\i ketubah} falls on the estate of the first husband \u8212- the {\i ketubah} obligation follows the original matrimonial bond. If the first estate lacks sufficient assets, the {\i rabbanan} established that she may collect from the second husband, {\i kedei she-lo tehei kallah b\u8217'einav le-hotzi\u8217'ah} \u8212- so that it should not be easy for him to dismiss her without financial consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get wording test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye poses a question to Rabba: if the {\i yavam} said to the {\i yevamah}, {\i harei at megureshet mimmeni v\u8217'i at muteret le-chol adam} \u8212- \u8220"you are divorced from me, but not permitted to anyone else\u8221" \u8212- does this constitute a valid {\i get} for the purposes of {\i yibum}? This formulation would be meaningless in an ordinary marital {\i get}, since a {\i get} that fails to render the woman {\i muteret le-chol adam} does not fully dissolve the marriage for all purposes. But the {\i get} of a {\i yevamah} is rabbinic in nature, and its function is specific: it operates to prevent {\i yibum}, to {\i pasul} her from the other brothers, and to implicate the co-wives. The question is whether this half-formulated expression carries enough {\i get}-like character to trigger those consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reiach HaGet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Chanan objects: if we say that anything resembling a {\i get} carries its consequences, one could argue that handing over a blank piece of paper while saying {\i harei zeh gittech} would equally {\i pasul} her. Surely there must be some minimum standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes: {\i hatam lo pasil bekhuna, hacha ka pasil bekhuna}. The relevant criterion is whether the act is sufficient to render the woman a {\i gerusha} with respect to {\i kehuna}. The {\i gemara} cites the {\i baraita}: {\i v\u8217'isha gerusha meisha, lo yikachu} \u8212- the prohibition on a {\i kohen} marrying a divorced woman applies to one who is {\i gerusha me\u8217'isha}, divorced from her husband, even if not {\i muteret le-chol adam}. The Talmudic derivation \u8212- {\i afilu lo nitgareshah ela meisha} \u8212- indicates that the one-sided formula {\i harei at megureshet mimmeni} is sufficient to confer the status of {\i gerusha} at a rabbinic level for purposes of {\i kehuna}, even though {\i d\u8217'oraita} the marriage remains intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the concept of {\i reiach ha-get} \u8212- the \u8220"smell of a {\i get}.\u8221" A document or act that shares enough of the characteristics of a {\i get}, even if technically invalid, may carry sufficient {\i get}-like quality to produce certain rabbinic consequences: rendering a woman a {\i gerusha} for {\i kehuna} purposes, and \u8212- as the present discussion shows \u8212- having real legal effect in the domain of {\i hilchos yibum}. Not all {\i gittin psulim} possess {\i reiach ha-get}; determining which defective {\i getin} have sufficient formal integrity to be treated as carrying this \u8220"smell\u8221" is a question that will recur throughout {\i masechet Gittin}. What this passage establishes is that {\i reiach ha-get} operates in two distinct areas simultaneously: {\i issur kehuna} and the laws of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Get questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get for Arusah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama introduces a series of questions by first laying out established rules of {\i hilchos gittin}. If a man told a scribe to write a {\i get} for his {\i arusah} \u8212- a woman to whom he had already performed {\i kiddushin} \u8212- with the intention of divorcing her after {\i nisuin}, the {\i get} is valid even though written before {\i nisuin}. The reasoning: {\i mipnei she-beyado le-garshah} \u8212- he could have given it to her immediately, since one may divorce an {\i arusah}. The fact that he intended to wait does not affect the validity of the writing. By contrast, if he instructed a scribe to write a {\i get} for a woman with whom he had no current marital relationship \u8212- merely intending to betroth her in the future \u8212- the {\i get} is invalid: {\i mipnei she-ein beyado le-garshah} \u8212- he has no current power to divorce her, and a {\i get} requires a present relationship to which it is addressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get for stranger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two cases establish the principle: the validity of a {\i get} written before the marital bond it is intended to dissolve depends on whether the relationship exists at the time of writing. A {\i get} written for an {\i arusah} is valid because the bond of {\i kiddushin} is already in place; a {\i get} written for a woman with whom no relationship yet exists is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevama Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama now poses his question: {\i le-yevamto mahu}? A man\u8217's brother has died; he has a {\i yevamah} bound to him by {\i zika}. May he instruct a scribe to write a {\i get} now, planning to give it after performing {\i ma\u8217'amar}? Is the {\i zika} sufficient to treat her as analogous to an {\i arusah}, such that a {\i get} written at the stage of {\i zika} is valid for use after {\i ma\u8217'amar}? Or is the {\i zika} alone insufficient \u8212- no {\i get} can be written because she is not yet truly his in any legally actionable sense?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi suggests the question may alternatively concern whether a {\i get} can be written at the stage of {\i zika} for use after {\i yibum} itself has been performed, but the more straightforward reading keeps the discussion within the rabbinic plane of {\i zika}, {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevama status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two positions emerge. On one view: {\i keivan de-agidah bei ka-arusato damya} \u8212- since she is bound and knotted to him via {\i zika}, she is comparable to an {\i arusah} and the {\i get} written now would be valid. On the other view: {\i keivan de-lo avad bah ma\u8217'amar}, since he has not yet performed {\i ma\u8217'amar}, there is no real relationship yet, and the {\i get} is meaningless. The {\i gemara} leaves this unresolved: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divided Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chanina raises a further question: {\i katav get le-zikato v\u8217'lo le-ma\u8217'amaro, le-ma\u8217'amaro v\u8217'lo le-zikato} \u8212- after a {\i ma\u8217'amar} has been performed, the {\i yavam} writes a {\i get} and declares that it is intended to sever only the {\i ma\u8217'amar} component of his relationship, not the {\i zika}. Or alternatively, only the {\i zika} but not the {\i ma\u8217'amar}. Is such a targeted {\i get} legally effective?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the stakes: if the {\i get} attacks only the {\i zika}, it would in principle prevent {\i yibum} entirely (since {\i yibum} depends on {\i zika}) and perhaps affect the co-wives, even though the {\i ma\u8217'amar} remains nominally in place. If the {\i get} attacks only the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, it restores the situation to pre-{\i ma\u8217'amar} {\i zika} \u8212- the woman remains a {\i yevamah} but the rabbinic {\i ma\u8217'amar} layer is peeled away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The initial analysis frames this as analogous to divorcing half a woman: {\i v\u8217'havay lei ke-migaresh chatzi isha, ha-migaresh chatzi isha lo asa v\u8217'lo klum} \u8212- the woman carries a double-layered status ({\i zika} and {\i ma\u8217'amar}), and trying to dissolve only one stratum while leaving the other intact is like divorcing half a wife \u8212- a legal nullity. The {\i get} would have no effect whatsoever, leaving intact both the {\i zika} and the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and theoretically leaving open the possibility of {\i yibum} afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Independence theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The counterposition: {\i o dilma hai lachud v\u8217'hai lachud} \u8212- perhaps {\i zika} and {\i ma\u8217'amar} are independent legal statuses. They were created by distinct acts and occupy distinct legal registers. If so, a {\i get} directed at one could validly sever that one, even without addressing the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} suggests that Rava\u8217's ruling offers support. Rava states: {\i natan get le-ma\u8217'amaro hutra tzarata} \u8212- if after performing {\i ma\u8217'amar} with one co-wife, the {\i yavam} gives a {\i get} addressed to the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, it frees the co-wife and returns the situation to its prior state. This shows that a {\i get} can operate exclusively against the {\i ma\u8217'amar} layer, without simultaneously addressing the {\i zika}. That the {\i ma\u8217'amar}-targeted {\i get} is effective is thus established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rava, the question of whether a {\i get} can target only the {\i ma\u8217'amar} ({\i peshita lei}) \u8212- obviously yes. What remains genuinely uncertain is whether a {\i get} can be directed exclusively at the {\i zika} layer without addressing the {\i ma\u8217'amar} built upon it \u8212- attacking the foundation while leaving the superstructure. Rav Chanina\u8217's question precisely concerns this more difficult case. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Ma\u8217'amar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the case of {\i chalatz v\u8217'achar kach ma\u8217'amar}. The {\i yavam}\u8217's brother has died, leaving two {\i yevamot}, call them Sarah and Rivkah. He performs {\i chalitzah} with Sarah. He then attempts to perform {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- either with Sarah or with Rivkah. What is the status of this {\i ma\u8217'amar}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the {\i yavam} performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} after {\i chalitzah}, he is attempting to effect {\i kiddushin} with a woman who is now his {\i chalutza}. This raises an immediate question about the nature of his prohibition toward her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Lav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibition on a {\i yavam} remarrying his {\i chalutza} is not {\i arayot} \u8212- it is not {\i kareit}-level. Rather, it derives from the {\i lav} of {\i keivan she-lo bana, shuv lo yivneh} \u8212- since he failed to \u8220"build\u8221" with her via {\i yibum} and instead performed {\i chalitzah}, he may not build with her again. It is a simple {\i issur lav}. The question is therefore: does {\i kiddushin} \u8212- or its rabbinic analog {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- take hold on a woman who is subject to an {\i issur lav}? This is the general principle of whether {\i kiddushin tossin b\u8217'chayei lavin} \u8212- whether {\i kiddushin} is binding when the woman is prohibited to the man via a {\i lav} rather than an {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rav Yehuda citing Rav, the ruling that {\i ma\u8217'amar} after {\i chalitzah} is entirely void ({\i ein achar chalitzah klum}) follows Rabbi Akiva. Rabbi Akiva holds {\i ein kiddushin tossin b\u8217'chayei lavin} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} does not bind when the woman is prohibited to the man by a {\i lav}. Since the {\i chalutza} is forbidden to him by {\i lav} ({\i shuv lo yivneh}), neither {\i ma\u8217'amar} nor regular {\i kiddushin} can take hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} disagree: {\i yesh achar chalitzah} \u8212- even after {\i chalitzah}, a subsequent {\i ma\u8217'amar} creates binding {\i kiddushin}. The {\i chachamim} do not subscribe to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's blanket rule that {\i kiddushin} cannot attach when the woman is subject to a {\i lav}. In their view, {\i kiddushin} is a sufficiently robust legal act that it binds even in the presence of a {\i lav} prohibition, leaving the man in a position where he has violated a {\i lav} but the {\i kiddushin} is nonetheless valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a difficulty: the {\i mishnah} states that if the {\i yavam} gave a {\i get} to the {\i yevamah} and then performed {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is effective and she requires a {\i get} to dissolve it. But if Rabbi Akiva holds {\i ein kiddushin tossin b\u8217'chayei lavin}, and if a {\i get} of a {\i yevamah} creates the same prohibition as {\i chalitzah} \u8212- namely that she becomes forbidden to him \u8212- then the {\i ma\u8217'amar} after {\i get} should equally fail. Why does the {\i mishnah} treat {\i ma\u8217'amar} after {\i get} as effective? Rabbi Akiva even derives from the verse {\i lo yuchal ba\u8217'alah ha-rishon asher shilchah} \u8212- {\i achar shiluach} alone is sufficient to create a permanent prohibition \u8212- suggesting the {\i get} of a {\i yevamah} is {\i d\u8217'oraita}. If so, the prohibition created by the {\i get} should be at least as strong as that created by {\i chalitzah}, and {\i ma\u8217'amar} should equally fail afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abiyi resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves this: {\i get yavni mi-de\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- the {\i get} of a {\i yevamah} is rabbinic, not {\i d\u8217'oraita}. The verse ({\i lo yuchal ba\u8217'alah ha-rishon asher shilchah}) is merely an {\i asmachta} \u8212- a rabbinic support text, not the source of a biblical prohibition. After a {\i get}, therefore, she is forbidden to him only at the rabbinic level, not the {\i d\u8217'oraita} level. Since Rabbi Akiva\u8217's principle ({\i ein kiddushin tossin b\u8217'chayei lavin}) operates only with respect to {\i d\u8217'oraita} prohibitions, a rabbinic-level prohibition does not block {\i kiddushin} from taking hold. {\i Ma\u8217'amar} after {\i get} therefore works. By contrast, after {\i chalitzah} the prohibition is {\i d\u8217'oraita}, and under Rabbi Akiva\u8217's rule, {\i kiddushin} \u8212- including {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- cannot bind.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Framing matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum framing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} introduces a further distinction, attributed to Rabbi (Rebbi). Rabbi holds that the effectiveness of {\i kiddushin} performed after {\i chalitzah} depends on how the {\i kiddushin} was framed. If the {\i yavam} said {\i hitkadshi li b\u8217'ma\u8217'amar yevamin} \u8212- framing the act explicitly as a {\i ma\u8217'amar} of {\i yibum} \u8212- then it does not take effect. The reasoning is that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is structurally built upon {\i zika}: it is the rabbinic {\i kiddushin} that operates within and atop the {\i yibum} framework. Once {\i chalitzah} has severed the {\i zika}, the foundation upon which {\i ma\u8217'amar} rests no longer exists. An act framed as {\i ma\u8217'amar yevamin} thus reaches for a foundation that has been dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regular framing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if after {\i chalitzah} the {\i yavam} said simply {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- the ordinary formula of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- without invoking the {\i yibum} context, Rabbi holds this should in principle bind as regular {\i kiddushin}. Regular {\i kiddushin} does not depend on {\i zika} as its foundation; it stands on its own. The prohibition she carries toward him is a {\i lav}, and the question of whether {\i kiddushin tossin b\u8217'chayei lavin} is debated \u8212- but Rabbi does not necessarily accept Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position on that question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} formalizes Rabbi\u8217's distinction: {\i im kidshah le-shum ishus tzricha heimenu get} \u8212- if he betrothed her framed as ordinary marriage ({\i ishus}), she requires a {\i get} to be released; {\i le-shum yevamus ein tzricha heimenu get} \u8212- if framed as {\i yevamus} (as an act of {\i yibum}), she does not require a {\i get}, because the {\i kiddushin} did not take hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} reject this distinction entirely: {\i bein she-kidshah le-shum ishus bein she-kidshah le-shum yevamus tzricha heimenu get} \u8212- regardless of how the {\i kiddushin} was framed, if he said {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, she requires a {\i get}. The {\i chachamim} hold that the words {\i harei at mekudeshet li} are operative regardless of the speaker\u8217's framing intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef defends Rabbi\u8217's position with an analogy: one who plows in the field of a {\i ger} (a convert whose estate is ownerless upon death, claimable by the first person who works it) without knowing it belongs to a {\i ger} \u8212- thinking it is his own field \u8212- does not acquire it. {\i Lo kavan le-mikni} \u8212- he had no intent to perform a {\i kinyan}. The acquisition fails not because the act was wrong but because the specific {\i kinyan}-intent was absent. Similarly, one who says {\i harei at mekudeshet li b\u8217'ma\u8217'amar yevamin} did not intend to perform regular {\i kiddushin}; his intent was directed at a {\i yibum}-framed act, and since that frame is no longer available (the {\i zika} having been severed by {\i chalitzah}), the {\i kiddushin} fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counter analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye counters: the analogy does not hold. {\i Hatam lo kavan le-mikni} \u8212- in the {\i ger} case, the person truly had no {\i kinyan}-intent at all; he thought he was working his own property. {\i Hacha kavan le-mikni} \u8212- in the {\i kiddushin} case, the {\i yavam} fully intends to acquire this woman; he is simply operating under a mistaken classification of the type of acquisition he is effecting. A better analogy: one who plows in the field of one {\i ger}, thinking it belongs to a different {\i ger} who died \u8212- both fields being ownerless, both eligible for acquisition by {\i chazakah}. In that case, the {\i kinyan} succeeds: the person has full {\i kinyan}-intent directed at this field, even if his identification of the deceased owner is mistaken. Similarly, here the {\i yavam} has full intent to acquire this woman via {\i kiddushin}; his characterization of the {\i kiddushin} as {\i yibum}-framed is immaterial to the operative intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the difficulty by clarifying the precise scope of Rabbi\u8217's ruling. Rabbi does not hold that background intent \u8212- thinking one is doing {\i kiddushin yevamin} rather than regular {\i kiddushin} \u8212- suffices to defeat the {\i kiddushin}. Rather, Rabbi\u8217's ruling applies only where the actual words spoken framed the act as {\i ma\u8217'amar yevamin}. When the formula itself invokes {\i yevamin}, the act is constituted as a {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- an act that by definition builds on the {\i zika}. Since the {\i chalitzah} severed the {\i zika}, there is no foundation left for the {\i ma\u8217'amar} to rest on, and the act is void. The {\i rabbanan}, by contrast, hold {\i hai lachud v\u8217'hai lachud} \u8212- {\i zika} and {\i ma\u8217'amar} are independent, and even without {\i zika}, the words {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- even with the appended {\i b\u8217'ma\u8217'amar yevamin} \u8212- carry their own {\i kiddushin} force and bind the woman. The dispute thus turns on whether {\i ma\u8217'amar} is so intrinsically constituted by its dependence on {\i zika} that an explicit invocation of {\i ma\u8217'amar yevamin} renders it a structural nullity once {\i chalitzah} has dissolved the {\i zika}, or whether the {\i kiddushin} language stands independently regardless of the framing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 57\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Marriage S (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present {\i daf} takes up from the bottom of 56b, at the {\i mishnah} there. The preceding {\i gemaras} addressed significant questions: the halachic definition of the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah}, and the status of a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife who is raped and thereby forbidden to her husband \u8212- a {\i halacha} with profound and tragic consequences. The sugya now turns to the next {\i mishnah}, which continues the theme of personal status as it intersects with {\i kehuna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens by enumerating cases of forbidden relationships involving {\i kohanim}: an {\i almanah} with a {\i kohen gadol}, or a {\i gerushah} or {\i chalutzah} with a {\i kohen hedyot} \u8212- all situations we know are forbidden at the stage of {\i nisuin}, when the couple has actually gone under the {\i chuppah} and consummated the marriage. Through that forbidden act of {\i bi\u8217'ah}, the woman not only transgresses a negative prohibition but also becomes a {\i chalalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does {\i chalalah} status matter if she is already forbidden to a {\i kohen}? For an {\i almanah} forbidden to a {\i kohen gadol}, the consequence is that she becomes lost even to a {\i kohen hedyot}. And for a {\i gerushah} or {\i chalutzah} with a {\i kohen hedyot}, it matters in a different way: if she was a {\i bat kohen}, she would ordinarily be entitled to return to her father\u8217's household and continue eating {\i terumah} after her husband\u8217's death \u8212- provided she has no children and meets the other conditions. But if she has become a {\i chalalah}, she is disqualified even from eating {\i terumah} from her father\u8217's household, just as a male {\i kohen} who is a {\i chalal} loses his {\i kedushas kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Erusin vs Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of the above consequences attach after the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah}. But the {\i mishnah} raises the case of {\i erusin} alone \u8212- {\i erusin} with a {\i kohen} in circumstances where the eventual {\i nisuin} would constitute an {\i issur}. The {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i mishnah}, whom the {\i gemara} will identify as Rebbi Meir, rules: {\i min ha-erusin lo yokhelu bi-trumah} \u8212- even during {\i erusin}, she may not eat {\i terumah}. Since {\i erusin} is but one step removed from {\i nisuin}, and there is no purpose to {\i erusin} unless one intends to proceed to {\i chuppah} and {\i nisuin}, Rebbi Meir treats the situation as though {\i bi\u8217'ah} is already imminent. She is therefore regarded as quasi-{\i chalalah} \u8212- at least rabbinically \u8212- and she cannot eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Marriage S (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalalah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding to the opposing positions, the case requires clarification. There is a debate among the {\i rishonim} \u8212- reflecting a disagreement between Rashi and Tosafot \u8212- as to whether the {\i mishnah} speaks of a {\i yisraelit} married to a {\i kohen}, or of a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i kohen}. If the subject is a {\i yisraelit}, one confronts a double difficulty: normally, a {\i yisraelit} cannot eat {\i terumah} until {\i nisuin}, not merely {\i erusin}, and furthermore, this is an {\i erusin} leading toward an {\i issur bi\u8217'ah}. It is difficult to see how anyone could permit her to eat {\i terumah} in such circumstances. Rashi nevertheless defends that reading, though we will not develop it here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more straightforward approach \u8212- that of Tosafot \u8212- reads the case as one involving a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i kohen}. She is already entitled to eat {\i terumah} by virtue of her father\u8217's household. The question, therefore, is not what entitles her to eat, but whether something now disqualifies her. Does the {\i erusin} to a {\i kohen} \u8212- when it would eventually ripen into a forbidden {\i nisuin} \u8212- strip her of her existing entitlement to eat {\i terumah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Erusin vs Nisuin (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer & Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi Eliezer and Rebbi Shimon disagree with Rebbi Meir and rule {\i makhshirin} \u8212- they permit her to eat. Their reasoning is that until {\i nisuin} actually occurs, until {\i bi\u8217'ah} actually takes place, she has not become a {\i chalalah}. She was eating {\i terumah} by virtue of her father, and that entitlement continues. The {\i kiddushin} is not {\i nisuin}; there has been no {\i chuppah}, no {\i bi\u8217'ah}, and therefore no disqualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Kohen Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then addresses the transitions in this woman\u8217's status across the different stages of the relationship. The positions of Rebbi Eliezer and Rebbi Shimon hold that she may continue eating by virtue of her father. Rebbi Meir holds she cannot. The husband\u8217's {\i kohen} status, it bears noting, is not at issue \u8212- {\i terumah} entitlements are not a punishment; the question concerns how her own status has been affected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Position Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Proceeding from {\i erusin} to {\i nisuin} clarifies matters further. {\i Nis\u8217'almanah o nisgareshah min hanisuin pesulah min haterumah} \u8212- if she becomes an {\i almanah} or {\i gerushah} after {\i nisuin}, she is fully disqualified. Once they have actually gone under the {\i chuppah} and consummated the marriage, she truly becomes a {\i chalalah}. Even if the husband dies the next moment, she remains a {\i chalalah} and cannot eat {\i terumah}. This is the universal position; everyone agrees to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gittin Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Nisuin Chalalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the relationship has reached the stage of {\i nisuin} \u8212- actual {\i chuppah} and {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the woman\u8217's disqualification as a {\i chalalah} is complete and permanent. The {\i gemara} will later discuss what happens in the case of {\i chuppah} without {\i bi\u8217'ah}, but in the standard case of {\i nisuin} with consummation, the {\i chalalah} status is irrevocable. Even subsequent death or divorce of the {\i kohen} does not restore her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erusin Only Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i min ha-erusin kesherot} \u8212- if the {\i kohen} dies during {\i erusin} alone, before {\i nisuin} has taken place, the women are {\i kesherot}, their status is restored. Presumably this holds even according to Rebbi Meir, who disqualifies her during {\i erusin}. His reasoning was that {\i kiddushin} places her on the cusp of forbidden {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- but if that {\i bi\u8217'ah} never materializes, the anticipated disqualification never crystallizes, and she reverts to her prior acceptable status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kal Vachomer Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir\u8217's Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records Rebbi Meir\u8217's {\i kal vachomer} argument. He reasons: {\i u\u8217'mah kiddushei reshut ein ma\u8217'achilin, kiddushei averah lo kol sheken?} \u8212- if {\i kiddushei reshut}, the case of a {\i bat kohen} who marries a {\i yisrael}, removes her from her father\u8217's household and disqualifies her from eating {\i terumah}, then {\i kiddushei averah} \u8212- a betrothal leading toward a forbidden {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- should certainly do so. When a {\i bat kohen} marries a {\i yisrael}, she is immediately pulled out of her father\u8217's category; she cannot eat {\i terumah} as a result of those {\i kiddushin}. How much more so, argues Rebbi Meir, should {\i kiddushin} to a {\i kohen} in a context of {\i issur} pull her out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refutation Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response to Rebbi Meir\u8217's {\i kal vachomer} is striking. The refutation is: {\i im amarta b\u8217'kiddushei reshut sheken ein lo lehachil b\u8217'makom acher} \u8212- in the case of {\i kiddushei reshut}, the {\i yisrael} cannot enable anyone to eat {\i terumah} on his account under any circumstances. {\i Tomar b\u8217'kiddushei averah sheken yesh lo lehachil b\u8217'makom acher?} \u8212- but in the case of {\i kiddushei averah}, this {\i kohen}, if he were married to another woman permissibly, she would eat {\i terumah} on his account. Being married to a {\i kohen} \u8212- even in a context of {\i issur} \u8212- is categorically different from being married to a {\i yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Zone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of the refutation reflects a subtle and counterintuitive principle: although this marriage, if consummated, would produce a full {\i issur} and make her a {\i chalalah}, the fact that she is betrothed to a {\i kohen} rather than a {\i yisrael} keeps her within the {\i kehuna} realm during {\i erusin}. She has not yet had {\i bi\u8217'ah}, and so has not yet become a {\i chalalah}; and she has not married a {\i yisrael}, so she has not been pulled out of her father\u8217's household. She exists in a middle zone \u8212- within the {\i kohen} realm but not yet disqualified by {\i bi\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying structure is this: normally, {\i erusin} \u8212- even to a {\i yisrael} \u8212- is enough to pull a {\i bat kohen} away from her father\u8217's household, even though {\i nisuin} has not yet occurred. She does not yet eat on her husband\u8217's account, but she can no longer eat on her father\u8217's account either. And yet, Rebbi Eliezer and Rebbi Shimon maintain that being betrothed to a {\i kohen} \u8212- however forbidden the ultimate {\i nisuin} would be \u8212- keeps her within the {\i terumah} realm. Marriage to a {\i kohen}, even a forbidden marriage, is better than marriage to a {\i yisrael} for purposes of eating {\i terumah}. The {\i chalalah} status has not occurred, and she remains, during this middle zone, able to eat {\i terumah} by virtue of her father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ptzua Daka Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oshaya\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then extends this framework. Rebbi Elazar reports in the name of Rebbi Oshaya: if a {\i kohen} who is a {\i ptzua daka} \u8212- one with crushed testicles, forbidden to marry \u8212- betroths a {\i bat kohen} (reading the {\i girsa} with Tosafot, not {\i bat yisrael} as Rashi had it), the same three-way {\i machloket} between Rebbi Meir, Rebbi Eliezer, and Rebbi Shimon applies. The {\i ptzua daka kohen}, if the relationship were to become {\i nisuin} and were consummated, would make her a {\i chalalah}. The parallel to our {\i mishnah}\u8217's case is exact: the problem is not her status but his, yet the consequences of consummation are identical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problem Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately interrogates this parallelism. Perhaps Rebbi Eliezer and Rebbi Shimon said what they said in the original case only because {\i yesh lo lehachil b\u8217'makom acher} \u8212- in that case, the {\i kohen} is the sort of person whose wife, in a permissible marriage, would eat {\i terumah} on his account. That quality \u8212- the capacity of this type of {\i kohen} to cause someone to eat {\i terumah} through marriage \u8212- is what keeps her within the {\i terumah} realm even during the forbidden {\i erusin}. But a {\i ptzua daka} is one who, by definition, cannot legally have a wife who eats {\i terumah} on his account. Is he not therefore equivalent to a {\i yisrael} from this perspective? And if marriage to a {\i yisrael} removes her from her father\u8217's {\i terumah} category, should not {\i erusin} to a {\i ptzua daka kohen} do the same?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Support Criterion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: but can we not say {\i yesh lo lehachil} even for a {\i ptzua daka kohen}? Perhaps there exists a woman he could marry such that she would eat {\i terumah} on his account \u8212- specifically, a {\i bat gerim}. The question of whether a {\i ptzua daka kohen} marrying a {\i bat gerim} can enable her to eat {\i terumah} is, as the {\i gemara} records, precisely the question that Rebbi Yochanan posed to Rebbi Oshaya \u8212- and to which Rebbi Oshaya gave no answer. Therefore, Rebbi Oshaya\u8217's own assumption that the case of a {\i ptzua daka kohen} maps cleanly onto the three-way {\i machloket} is itself unresolved: we do not know whether this {\i kohen} is {\i yesh lo lehachil b\u8217'makom acher}, because we do not know the answer to Rebbi Yochanan\u8217's unanswered question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye vs Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now records a dispute between Abaye and Rava. The specific scenario, as Rashi explains it, is a {\i kohen} who married a woman while he was still fully fit, and subsequently became a {\i ptzua daka}. He has not had {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her since becoming a {\i ptzua daka}. The question is whether she continues to eat {\i terumah} on his account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's explanation of the case is that the {\i nisuin} was entirely valid at the time it was performed \u8212- the {\i kohen} was not yet a {\i ptzua daka} \u8212- and she therefore became a full {\i eshet kohen} and was eating {\i terumah}. Now that he has become a {\i ptzua daka}, if he were to have {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her he would make her a {\i chalalah}. But as long as he has not done so, she has not become a {\i chalalah}, and the marriage remains valid because it was contracted when he was still permitted. Abaye therefore rules: {\i ho\u8217'il u\u8217'machilah belo bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- he can cause her to eat even without having now had {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her. The relationship is legitimate and she continues to eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Continuity Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's proof: one cannot simply say that what happened earlier dictates ongoing status. The {\i gemara} puts it this way \u8212- {\i v\u8217'i lo tema hachi}, if you say that an earlier state alone justifies the continuation, then a {\i bat yisrael} who was married to a {\i kohen} and the {\i kohen} died should continue to eat {\i terumah} on the logic that she was already eating it. Obviously we do not say that. This demonstrates that ongoing eating is not justified merely by prior status. There must therefore be something currently active in the relationship between a {\i bat kohen} and her {\i ptzua daka} husband that supports her eating \u8212- and Abaye identifies that as the fact that the {\i kinyan} of {\i ishut} between them remains intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds by distinguishing the two cases. When the {\i kohen} dies, the relationship is severed entirely \u8212- {\i paka kinyano}, his bond lapses \u8212- and that is why she stops eating. Here, by contrast, the {\i kohen} has become a {\i ptzua daka} but has not died; the relationship, the {\i kinyan}, has not been severed \u8212- {\i lo paka kinyano}. She therefore continues to eat not merely on the basis of the past but because the ongoing {\i kinyan} remains in force. Abaye\u8217's rejoinder to Rava is that one should derive the law about the {\i kinyan} of {\i ishut} from another case of {\i kinyan} of {\i ishut}, not from the {\i kinyan} of slaves \u8212- {\i kinyan d\u8217'ishut mikinyan d\u8217'ishut yalfinan v\u8217'lo yalfinan kinyan d\u8217'ishut mikinyan d\u8217'avadim} \u8212- since Rava had proposed that the {\i ptzua daka kohen}\u8217's {\i avadim} eating {\i terumah} demonstrates that he is {\i yesh lo lehachil b\u8217'makom acher}. Rava\u8217's response is that in the case where the {\i kohen} married before becoming a {\i ptzua daka}, she is eating on the basis of the continuation of the earlier relationship, and this does not demonstrate that a {\i ptzua daka} can newly enable his wife to eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bat Gerim Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this background the {\i gemara} returns to the question Rebbi Yochanan posed: {\i ptzua daka kohen she-nasa bat gerim mahu she-yachilenah bi-trumah?} \u8212- a {\i kohen} who is a {\i ptzua daka} and who married a {\i bat gerim} in a straightforward way (she is not herself a {\i giyoret}, and there is no {\i issur} from the {\i ptzua daka} side if she is indeed permitted to him) \u8212- can he enable her to eat {\i terumah}? This question remained unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oshaya\u8217's Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi Oshaya gave Rebbi Yochanan no answer. Sometime afterward, another great scholar \u8212- the {\i gemara} suggests this was Resh Lakish \u8212- came and posed a different question, and Rebbi Oshaya resolved it. This contrast prompted a rebuke from Rebbi Yehuda Nesia.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebuke & Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi Yehuda Nesia \u8212- the grandson of Rebbi Yehuda HaNasi, not Rebbi Yehuda HaNasi himself \u8212- said to Rebbi Oshaya: is Rebbi Yochanan not also a great person? Why did you answer Resh Lakish and not Rebbi Yochanan? Rebbi Oshaya responded: {\i de-ka bei minai milta de-lav pshita li} \u8212- Rebbi Yochanan asked me something I did not know the answer to. My silence was not a slight on his stature; it was because the question has no clear resolution. The {\i gemara} now examines why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerim Status Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty lies in what position one takes on several intersecting questions. The {\i gemara} frames it by examining the two major opinions \u8212- those of Rebbi Yehuda and Rebbi Yossi \u8212- and then a third, that of Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} begins: {\i ei Rebbi Yehuda bein bi-kdushateih kaei bein be-lav bi-kdushateih kaei lo achlah} \u8212- according to Rebbi Yehuda, whether the {\i ptzua daka kohen} retains his {\i kedushas kehunah} (meaning, whether the marriage restrictions of {\i kehunah} still apply to him) or not, the woman cannot eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two sub-questions embedded in the phrase {\i bi-kdushateih kaei}: first, whether a {\i kohen} who is a {\i ptzua daka} retains the {\i issurim} that govern whom a {\i kohen} may marry; and second, the {\i machloket} between Rebbi Yehuda and Rebbi Yossi regarding whether descendants of {\i gerim} \u8212- {\i kehal gerim} \u8212- are themselves still considered {\i kahal} for purposes of the restriction of {\i ptzua daka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i ptzua daka kohen} retains his {\i kedushas kehunah} in full, then one cannot solve the problem by having him marry a {\i bat ger}, because Rebbi Yehuda rules {\i bat ger ke-vat chalal zachar} \u8212- the daughter of a male {\i ger} is like the daughter of a {\i chalal}, and a {\i kohen} may not marry her, just as he may not marry a {\i gerushah} or {\i chalalah}. This is not the {\i halacha} as we rule, and one can only note what enormous implications such a ruling would have \u8212- a {\i kohen} would have to investigate any trace of a {\i ger} in a woman\u8217's lineage, through the father\u8217's line as well as the mother\u8217's, for all generations. But according to Rebbi Yehuda, it is so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Ger Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus, if the {\i kohen}\u8217's {\i kedushas kehunah} remains operative, there is no one this {\i ptzua daka kohen} can marry who would be permitted by the {\i kohen} rules and yet would be permitted by the {\i ptzua daka} rules \u8212- at least not among the natural candidates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yossi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi Yossi takes the opposite position: {\i bein bi-kdushateih kaei bein be-lav bi-kdushateih kaei achla} \u8212- either way she can eat. His reasoning on both flanks is the mirror image of Rebbi Yehuda\u8217's. As to the {\i kehuna} restriction: {\i ger she-nitgayer bito keshera li-kehuna} \u8212- a convert\u8217's daughter is fully {\i keshera} to marry a {\i kohen}. A {\i kohen} is forbidden to a {\i giyoret} herself, but her children, born after conversion, have no such restriction. And as to the {\i ptzua daka} restriction: {\i kehal gerim lo ikrei kahal} \u8212- the descendants of {\i gerim} are not considered {\i kahal Hashem} for purposes of the {\i ptzua daka} prohibition. A {\i ptzua daka} may marry into the second generation of converts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paradox Rebbi Yossi\u8217's position generates is notable. He holds that a {\i bat giyoret} is sufficiently a full Jew that a {\i kohen} may marry her \u8212- no residual {\i ger} status clings to her in that context. Yet simultaneously, she retains enough of a non-native status that she falls outside {\i kehal Hashem} and may marry a {\i ptzua daka}. Rebbi Yehuda\u8217's position produces the mirror-image paradox: a {\i bat ger} is still enough of a {\i ger} that a {\i kohen} cannot marry her \u8212- but she is enough of a {\i yisrael} that a {\i ptzua daka} cannot marry her either. Both {\i tanna\u8217'im} end up in an internally asymmetric position, though each in the opposite direction. According to Rebbi Yossi, the question posed by Rebbi Yochanan poses no difficulty \u8212- either way she can eat. According to Rebbi Yehuda, either way she cannot. The question is therefore answerable according to both \u8212- which only deepens the puzzle of why Rebbi Oshaya said he had no answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eliezer b. Yaakov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution lies with the position of Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov. He occupies a middle position: {\i isha bat gerim lo tinase li-kehuna ad she-tei imah mi-Yisrael} \u8212- a woman of {\i gerim} descent may not marry a {\i kohen} unless her mother is a {\i yisraelit}. In other words, Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov distinguishes between the {\i bat ger} \u8212- whose father is a {\i ger} but mother is a {\i yisraelit} \u8212- and the {\i bat giyoret}, whose mother is a convert. The former may marry a {\i kohen}; the latter may not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implication Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, if Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's rule is that a {\i yisraelit} mother enables the daughter to marry a {\i kohen}, the question becomes: what does that {\i yisraelit} mother add? Does it add {\i kashrut} in the sense of {\i kashrus li-kehunah} \u8212- fitness to marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- but nothing else? Or does it add {\i kedushah} \u8212- a higher level of {\i kehal Hashem} status \u8212- which would simultaneously make her {\i assur} to a {\i ptzua daka}? According to Rebbi Yehuda and Rebbi Yossi, the two categories (permitted to {\i kohen} / forbidden to {\i ptzua daka}) always move in tandem: what is forbidden to one is permitted to the other. But perhaps Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov contemplates a third category: a woman who, by virtue of her {\i yisraelit} mother, has been elevated to {\i kashrus} for {\i kehunah} \u8212- but not so elevated in {\i kedushah} as to become {\i assur} to a {\i ptzua daka}. This is the question Rebbi Oshaya could not resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then adduces a {\i baraita} brought by Rav Aha bar Katinah from the south. The {\i baraita} asks: {\i minayin le-ptzua daka kohen she-nasa bat gerim she-ma\u8217'achilah bi-trumah?} \u8212- how do we know that a {\i ptzua daka kohen} who married a {\i bat gerim} may enable her to eat {\i terumah}? It derives this from the verse {\i ve-kohen ki yikneh nefesh kinyan kaspo hi yokhal bo}. The {\i gemara} immediately asks: according to whose opinion is this {\i baraita} needed? According to Rebbi Yehuda \u8212- it contradicts his position, since he holds she cannot eat. According to Rebbi Yossi \u8212- no verse is needed, since either way she may eat. It must therefore be according to Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, whose position left open the question of the {\i ptzua daka}\u8217's case. And from the {\i baraita} we learn: {\i kashira hi ve-tosaf bah acher} \u8212- she is {\i kashira}, more fit to marry a {\i kohen}, but no additional {\i kedushah} is added to place her in the {\i kehal Hashem} category. Therefore the {\i ptzua daka kohen} can marry her and enable her to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eliezer b. Yaakov (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kashrah vs Kedushah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot, then, is a three-way taxonomy. According to Rebbi Yehuda and Rebbi Yossi, the two axes \u8212- permitted to {\i kohen} / forbidden to {\i ptzua daka} \u8212- are perfectly correlated. What is {\i assur} on one side is {\i muttar} on the other. But Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov introduces a conceptual split: {\i kashrut} for {\i kehunah} and {\i kedushah} for the {\i ptzua daka} restriction are not identical measures. The {\i yisraelit} mother adds {\i kashrut} to the daughter \u8212- making her eligible to marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- without adding enough {\i kedushah} to remove her from the set of women a {\i ptzua daka} may marry. The {\i baraita} confirms this: the {\i ptzua daka kohen} who married a {\i bat gerim} (with {\i yisraelit} mother) enables her to eat {\i terumah}. {\i Shema mina.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chuppah Pesulot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new dispute. Rav holds {\i yesh chuppah le-pesulot} \u8212- there is a meaningful act of {\i chuppah} even for {\i pesulot}, women in forbidden marriages. Shmuel holds {\i ein chuppah le-pesulot} \u8212- the act of {\i chuppah} has no halachic significance in a forbidden relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question being asked requires some framing. There is a well-known debate among the {\i rishonim} about {\i chuppas niddah} \u8212- whether a {\i chuppah} performed when the woman is a {\i niddah} is halachically valid for purposes of {\i nisuin}, {\i sheva berachot}, and so on. Sefardim, following the Rambam, hold one may not; Ashkenazim hold one may. But that debate concerns whether {\i chuppah} in such circumstances is meaningful enough to constitute {\i nisuin}. The present dispute is effectively the reverse: given a situation where the parties are forbidden to ever consummate the marriage \u8212- an {\i almanah} to a {\i kohen gadol}, a {\i gerushah} to a {\i kohen hedyot}, a {\i ptzua daka} with a woman, and so on \u8212- does the act of {\i chuppah} without {\i bi\u8217'ah} carry any significance? Specifically: does it operate as though they have had {\i bi\u8217'ah}, so as to make her a {\i chalalah} and disqualify her from eating {\i terumah} from her father?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a ratcheted version of Rebbi Meir\u8217's {\i mishnah} position: Rebbi Meir held that mere {\i kiddushin} operates quasi-like {\i bi\u8217'ah}. Now we ask \u8212- even according to those who rejected Rebbi Meir, what about {\i chuppah} itself, which is more advanced than {\i erusin} but still without actual {\i bi\u8217'ah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Agrees Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel notes that Rav \u8212- whom Shmuel refers to as {\i Abba} (Tosafot notes, against Rashi\u8217's reading of this as an honorific, that {\i Abba} was simply Rav\u8217's actual name, as the {\i Aruch} explains) \u8212- agrees with him in one specific case: {\i betinoket p\u8217'chuta mi-bas shalosh shanim ve-yom echad} \u8212- a girl under three years old and one day. {\i Ho\u8217'il ve-ein lah bi\u8217'ah ein lah chuppah} \u8212- since an act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} with such a girl has no halachic standing, an act of {\i chuppah} similarly has no standing. Even Rav concedes this exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Possible Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The corollary is also spelled out: once a girl is three years old and one day \u8212- the threshold at which {\i bi\u8217'ah} has halachic effect \u8212- the act of {\i chuppah} does carry significance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava confirms this from the {\i mishnah}: {\i bat shalosh shanim ve-yom echad mitkadeshet be-vi\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} takes effect from this age. {\i Ve-im ba aleha yavam kana\u8217'ah} \u8212- an act of {\i yibbum} is effective at this age. {\i Ve-chayavin aleha mishum eshet ish} \u8212- if she was betrothed and someone has {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her, the laws of {\i eshet ish} apply. {\i U-metamah et bo\u8217'alah} \u8212- if she is a {\i niddah}, her {\i tumah} transfers to the man who has {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her, rendering the bedding beneath and above subject to {\i mishkav} and {\i moshav tumah}. {\i Niset le-kohen ochelet bi-trumah} \u8212- if she is married to a {\i kohen} in the context of valid {\i nisuin}, she eats {\i terumah}, because {\i nisuin} is only meaningful in a context where {\i bi\u8217'ah} is halachically possible. {\i Ba aleha echad mi-kol ha-pesulim paslah} \u8212- if one of the {\i pesulim} has {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her, he disqualifies her, rendering her a {\i chalalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} draws the conclusion: {\i bat shalosh shanim ve-yom echad hu de-mipsala be-vi\u8217'ah mipsala be-chuppah} \u8212- it is at the age of three years and one day that {\i bi\u8217'ah} disqualifies her, and {\i chuppah} likewise disqualifies her at that age. {\i Ha-p\u8217'chuta mi-bas shalosh shanim ve-yom echad de-la mipsala be-vi\u8217'ah lo mipsala nami be-chuppah} \u8212- below that threshold, since {\i bi\u8217'ah} itself would not disqualify her, {\i chuppah} similarly does not disqualify her. {\i Shema mina.} The principle is clear: {\i chuppah} for {\i pesulot} carries halachic weight \u8212- it operates as {\i bi\u8217'ah} for purposes of {\i chalalah} status \u8212- but only where {\i bi\u8217'ah} itself would be halachically significant, i.e., where the girl is at least three years and one day old.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 58\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chuppah for Psulot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Chuppah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf under discussion opens with a debate introduced by Rami bar Chama concerning whether {\i chuppah} is halakhically meaningful for women who are {\i psulot} \u8212- women disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}, such as a {\i grushah}. The question of {\i yesh chuppah li-psulot} has been framed as whether the act of {\i chuppah} carries legal weight when the woman is forbidden to the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before engaging the Gemara\u8217's argument directly, it is worth clarifying what \u8220"meaningful\u8221" means in this context. The question is not whether one fulfills the {\i mitzvah} of {\i chuppah} for the purposes of {\i sheva brachot} \u8212- a separate issue that arises, for instance, in the case of a {\i niddah}, who is a woman in a temporary forbidden status that will eventually be permitted. Here we are speaking of a {\i psulah} in a substantive sense: a {\i grushah} to a {\i kohen}, for example, a woman who is categorically disqualified. The halakhic question is whether the act of {\i chuppah} carries any legal consequence \u8212- specifically, whether it is treated as equivalent to {\i biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevance of this question is not primarily whether such a woman may now marry the {\i kohen}, since she is already disqualified. Rather, the concern is whether she has been invalidated from eating {\i terumah}. If she is the daughter of a {\i kohen}, she eats {\i terumah} by virtue of her father. An act of forbidden intercourse of the kind that concerns {\i kehunah} would render her a {\i chalalah}, permanently stripped of that right. The prior discussion in the Gemara established that we speak only of a {\i bat shalosh shanim v\u8217'yom echad} \u8212- the halachically significant threshold age \u8212- and the question is whether {\i chuppah} functions like {\i biah} in that regard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Chuppah Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reading of the Gemara is that the entire debate concerns {\i chuppah} that occurred without any prior {\i kiddushin}. The couple proceeded directly to {\i chuppah} without having performed {\i kiddushin}. This creates two opposing arguments simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On one hand, one might be more lenient than in a case of {\i kiddushin} alone: even if Rabbi Meir holds that {\i kiddushin} by a {\i kohen} to a {\i psulah} disqualifies her from {\i terumah}, he might agree that {\i chuppah} without {\i kiddushin} has no halakhic standing whatsoever and therefore creates no consequence. {\i Chuppah} that arrives without {\i kiddushin} perhaps means nothing at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other hand, one might be stricter than in a case of mere {\i kiddushin}: even if Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon hold that {\i kiddushin} alone does not disqualify her \u8212- since {\i kiddushin} is still distant from actual {\i biah} \u8212- they might agree that {\i chuppah} is different. {\i Chuppah} is fundamentally about {\i nisuin} and cohabitation; it exists in the very context of sexual union. Therefore, even those who are lenient about {\i kiddushin} might concede that {\i chuppah} disqualifies her, treating it as functionally equivalent to {\i biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Chuppah Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot finds Rashi\u8217's framing difficult. It seems strange, Tosafot argues, to treat {\i chuppah} completely devoid of {\i kiddushin} as though it carries any independent halakhic significance. If the act has no halakhic grounding whatsoever, why would it create legal consequences? A couple who entered a room together without any prior {\i kiddushin} \u8212- why should that be treated as tantamount to forbidden {\i biah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot therefore turns to a Talmudic debate in tractate Kiddushin that considers whether {\i chuppah} can itself constitute an act of {\i kiddushin}, parallel to the standard modes of {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i biah}. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, the Gemara here presupposes this possibility: the {\i chuppah} is being discussed as itself an act of {\i kiddushin}, and the question is whether that kind of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- effected through {\i chuppah} \u8212- disqualifies the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot acknowledges that this reading creates its own circularity. If {\i chuppah} functions as {\i kiddushin}, then one is back to the question of {\i kiddushin} itself, and it is not immediately clear how this advances the debate beyond where Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Eliezer already stand. Tosafot also offers an alternative reading according to which the Gemara is asking about a case where there was {\i kiddushin} followed by {\i chuppah} but no actual {\i biah}, and then the husband died or divorced her. The question becomes whether the {\i chuppah}, having occurred without {\i biah}, has permanently altered her status \u8212- rendering her a {\i chalalah} \u8212- or whether it is only the act of {\i biah} that causes permanent invalidation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: No Sex Yet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a {\i baraita} in support of the discussion. The {\i tanna kamma} teaches that women \u8212- whether {\i kesherot} or {\i psulot} \u8212- who married {\i kohanim}, or who entered {\i chuppah} without having had sexual relations, eat from the husband\u8217's table and eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara questions this reading. When the {\i baraita} says \u8220"{\i nisu}\u8221" \u8212- they married \u8212- surely that implies actual marriage including consummation. How, then, could these {\i psulot} be eating {\i terumah}? If they have had actual {\i biah} that is forbidden from a {\i kehunah} perspective, they should be disqualified as {\i chalalot} and permanently barred from {\i terumah} even from the father\u8217's house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by reading the two phrases \u8212- {\i nisu} and {\i nichnesu l\u8217'chuppah v\u8217'lo niv\u8217'alu} \u8212- as parallel descriptions of the same scenario rather than two distinct categories. The {\i baraita} must be speaking of women who entered {\i chuppah} but did not have intercourse. In that case, {\i psulot} who had {\i chuppah} without {\i biah} are still permitted to eat {\i terumah}. The {\i tanna kamma} holds {\i ein chuppah li-psulot}: {\i chuppah} without {\i biah} is not treated as equivalent to {\i biah}, and her status remains unchanged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael: Parallel Ac\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael, son of Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka, takes the opposing position and articulates it by drawing a direct parallel between {\i chuppah} and {\i biah}: whatever {\i biah} would entitle a woman to eat \u8212- {\i chuppah} also entitles her to eat. Whatever {\i biah} would not entitle her to eat \u8212- {\i chuppah} also does not. In a case where the woman is a {\i psulah} and the {\i biah} would not only fail to entitle her to eat {\i terumah} but would affirmatively disqualify her by making her a {\i chalalah}, Rashi understands Rabbi Yishmael as saying that {\i chuppah} operates the same way: it is not merely that she is not entitled to eat in this state, but that the {\i chuppah} itself has rendered her permanently a {\i chalalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This represents {\i yesh chuppah li-psulot} at its most sweeping: even without {\i biah}, the act of {\i chuppah} with a {\i psulah} creates the same permanent disqualification that actual forbidden intercourse would.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks whether this dispute between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Yishmael simply maps onto the existing debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon. It concludes that the mapping is not straightforward. Rabbi Meir might be lenient specifically about {\i chuppah} without {\i kiddushin} \u8212- since without {\i kiddushin} there is no halakhic {\i kinyan} at all \u8212- even though he is strict about {\i kiddushin} alone. And Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon might be stricter about {\i chuppah} than about {\i kiddushin} alone \u8212- since {\i chuppah} is, by its very nature, oriented toward {\i biah} in a way that {\i kiddushin} is not. The {\i baraita}\u8217's emphasis on {\i chuppah} rather than {\i kiddushin} or {\i kesef} points toward the {\i chuppah} itself as the operative legal act, which supports reading the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Yishmael as staking out independent positions rather than merely echoing the tannaitic dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa then reports that Rav Sheshet illuminated this very question from a {\i mishnah}, declaring {\i yesh chuppah li-psulot}, and promising to demonstrate that this position is already embedded in an existing {\i mishnah}. This brings the discussion to the {\i mishnah} in tractate {\i Sotah}, which will reframe the question in far broader terms.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sotah Investigation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Prerequisites\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the {\i mishnah} in {\i Sotah} to support the position of {\i yesh chuppah li-psulot}. That {\i mishnah} explains the double {\i amen} spoken by the suspected woman: {\i amen she-lo satisi arusa v\u8217'nesuah, shomeret yavam u-knusah} \u8212- I did not stray when I was an {\i arusah}, when I was a {\i nesuah}, when I was a {\i shomeret yavam}, or after {\i yibum} was performed. The {\i mishnah} thus indicates that the {\i kinui} \u8212- the formal jealousy warning \u8212- and the {\i stirah} \u8212- the seclusion \u8212- can operate even in the context of {\i arusah} and {\i shomeret yavam}, not only in the standard case of full {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately asks: what exactly is the case of {\i arusah} here? If the {\i kinui} was issued while she was an {\i arusah} and the {\i hashkayah} \u8212- making her drink the {\i sotah} water \u8212- also occurred while she was an {\i arusah}, this cannot work. The {\i mishnah} in {\i Sotah} itself establishes that an {\i arusah} and a {\i shomeret yavam} do not drink and do not collect their {\i ketubah}. The {\i sotah} water is administered only when the verse {\i tachat ishech} \u8212- under your husband \u8212- applies, meaning the woman must be a full {\i nesuah}. An {\i arusah} has not yet reached that status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuppah No Consummation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore reconstructs the case: the {\i kinui} was issued when she was an {\i arusah}, the {\i stirah} occurred, and then they proceeded to {\i chuppah} without having had intercourse \u8212- {\i nichnesah l\u8217'chuppah v\u8217'lo niv\u8217'alah}. The husband can now administer the {\i sotah} water for what she did during the {\i erusin} period, because she is now considered a {\i nesuah} by virtue of the {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But \u8212- and here lies the broader legal significance \u8212- this is only possible if the {\i chuppah} itself is meaningful. She was a {\i sotah} during {\i erusin}, forbidden to her husband after the {\i stirah}. The {\i chuppah} therefore occurred while she was a {\i psulah} \u8212- forbidden to him. If {\i chuppah} for a {\i psulah} has no halakhic standing, then she never became a {\i nesuah} at all, and the {\i sotah} procedure cannot be performed. The very structure of the {\i mishnah} thus implies {\i yesh chuppah li-psulot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking broadening of the question. Earlier the discussion was confined to a narrow question: does {\i chuppah} create the equivalent of {\i biah} to invalidate a woman for {\i terumah}? Now the question has become general: is {\i chuppah} with a woman who is forbidden to the husband a meaningful act of {\i nisuin} at all? The {\i sotah} case demonstrates that {\i chuppah} in the presence of {\i issur} can be halakhically operative in the full sense \u8212- not merely creating an analogue to {\i biah}, but conferring the complete status of {\i nesuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Requirement Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a further difficulty from a {\i baraita} brought by Rav Acha bar Chanina, who came from the south bearing a teaching: {\i mibal\u8217'adei ishech} \u8212- the verse \u8220"aside from your husband\u8221" \u8212- applies only when {\i shechivat ba\u8217'al} preceded {\i shechivat bo\u8217'el}: the husband must have had intercourse with the woman before the suspected adulterer. If the adulterer had intercourse with her first, the {\i sotah} water does not apply. This is derived from the plain sense of the verse \u8212- your husband has already been with you, and now another man has been with you as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a serious problem for our constructed case. We said the {\i stirah} and suspected sin occurred when she was an {\i arusah}. How, then, did they ever proceed to {\i chuppah}? After {\i stirah}, the husband is forbidden to have intercourse with a suspected {\i sotah}. If they had {\i chuppah} without {\i biah}, then the condition of {\i shechivat ba\u8217'al} preceding {\i shechivat bo\u8217'el} is never satisfied, and the {\i sotah} water would not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama proposes: the {\i arus} had intercourse with her while she was still in her father\u8217's house \u8212- an act of intimacy during the {\i erusin} period, before the {\i kinui} and {\i stirah} took place. He then performed {\i kinui}, she had the {\i stirah}, and now \u8212- after {\i chuppah} but without {\i biah} \u8212- he can make her drink, because the prior {\i biah} satisfies the condition of {\i shechivat ba\u8217'al}, and his current abstinence ensures he is {\i menukah me\u8217'avon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolves the case of {\i arusah}. But the {\i sotah mishnah} also includes {\i shomeret yavam}. The Gemara asks: how can one construct a case of {\i shomeret yavam} in which there has already been {\i shechivat ba\u8217'al}? Once the brother-in-law has intercourse with the {\i yevamah}, she is no longer a {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- she is fully his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Intent Irrelevant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara recalls the earlier debate between Rav and Shmuel. Rav holds {\i kanah l\u8217'chol mili} \u8212- an act of {\i biah} with a {\i yevamah}, even if performed with no intent to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, with no intent to create a marital bond, but purely for the sake of gratification, nevertheless acquires her completely and makes her his full wife in every respect. Shmuel disagrees: without the proper intent to effect {\i yibum}, such intercourse acquires her only for those matters explicitly enumerated in the {\i parshah} of {\i yibum} \u8212- exempting co-wives, exempting other brothers from {\i yibum}, creating inheritance rights \u8212- but does not confer the full status of {\i ishto} that we speak of in general {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, one might think: according to Shmuel, the {\i yavam} could have had intercourse with the {\i yevamah} purely for gratification, and since that intercourse does not confer full wife-status, she could still be called a {\i shomeret yavam} in a meaningful sense. But then the {\i sotah} water would not work on her either, since for {\i sotah} purposes one needs the full status of {\i nesuah}. The circle cannot be squared using Shmuel\u8217's position alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore notes that this entire line of reasoning is being advanced in support of Rav \u8212- who is the one who holds {\i yesh chuppah li-psulot}. It is Rav\u8217's position we are trying to demonstrate from the {\i mishnah}. But the {\i shomeret yavam} case cannot be explained satisfactorily according to Rav, because Rav holds that any act of {\i biah} with a {\i yevamah} makes her completely his wife, leaving no room for the category of {\i shomeret yavam who has already had relations with the yavam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Changes Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces an answer of remarkable conceptual audacity. According to Beit Shammai, {\i maamar} \u8212- the formal levitical declaration analogous to {\i kiddushin} \u8212- acquires the {\i yevamah} with the force of a complete {\i kinyan} and effects a full {\i arusah} status. More than that, it completely displaces ({\i hefriach}) the {\i zikat yibum}. Once {\i maamar} is performed, she is no longer in the category of {\i shomeret yavam}; she is now in the category of a standard {\i arusah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence is striking: subsequent intercourse \u8212- if performed not for the purpose of {\i nisuin} or {\i yibum} but simply as an act of intimacy \u8212- does not have the normal halakhic effect that {\i biah} with a {\i yevamah} would have. In a standard {\i yibum} case, any {\i biah} automatically elevates the woman to {\i nesuah} status. But according to Beit Shammai, once {\i maamar} was performed, the {\i zika} is removed, and the subsequent intercourse is more analogous to {\i biah} with an {\i arusah} \u8212- meaningful but insufficient to create {\i nisuin} without the proper intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means the {\i yavam} can have had intercourse with her while she was still classified as an {\i arusah}, satisfying the condition of {\i shechivat ba\u8217'al}, and yet she remains in an {\i arusah}-like status \u8212- parallel to the category of {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- without having been elevated to {\i nesuah} through that act. Rashi makes this explicit: the {\i maamar} rendered her a complete {\i arusah gemura} and severed the {\i zikat yibum}, such that {\i biah mezid} \u8212- intercourse without the intent of {\i yibum} \u8212- no longer functions as a {\i yibum} act. She therefore continues to be called {\i shomeret yavam}, even after intercourse, because the relationship is now defined by the {\i maamar}-{\i kiddushin} rather than by the {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Parallels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks why the {\i mishnah} lists {\i shomeret yavam} and {\i knusah} as separate categories when \u8212- if {\i yibum} is performed \u8212- {\i knusah} is legally indistinguishable from {\i nesuah}. The answer is that the {\i mishnah} is drawing parallel categories: {\i nesuah} refers to a woman the man himself chose to marry through the ordinary process, while {\i knusah} refers to a woman who came to him through his brother\u8217's death. Similarly, {\i arusah} and {\i shomeret yavam} are parallel constructs \u8212- {\i arusah} is the {\i erusin} he himself initiated, while {\i shomeret yavam} is the {\i arusah}-like state created through {\i maamar} vis-\u224 ?-vis a woman originally bound to him through {\i zika}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel structure of the {\i mishnah} thus accommodates both pairs without requiring that they be substantively different in every respect, only that they represent distinct relational pathways to an equivalent halakhic status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately offers two resolutions to the complex case of {\i sotah} in the context of {\i arusah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi cites Rav Pappa who invokes a {\i baraita}: one does not perform {\i kinui} on an {\i arusah} for the purposes of making her drink while she is still an {\i arusah}, but one may perform {\i kinui} while she is an {\i arusah} for the purpose of making her drink once she has become a {\i nesuah}. On this reading, {\i ein l\u8217'hashkotah k\u8217'shehi arusah} \u8212- she may not drink while an {\i arusah} \u8212- but {\i aval mekana lah l\u8217'hashkotah k\u8217'shehi nesuah} \u8212- the warning issued during {\i erusin} can serve as the basis for administering the water after {\i nisuin}. This resolves the problem by clarifying that the {\i stirah} occurred when she was already a {\i nesuah}; it was only the {\i kinui} that was issued during {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak offers a different solution: {\i al yedei gilgul}. The principle of {\i gilgul shvua} holds that once a person is properly before the court and obligated to take an oath on some matter, additional sworn statements on other matters can be \u8220"rolled into\u8221" that oath. This principle \u8212- by no means limited to {\i sotah} \u8212- is derived from this very context. Here, it means that once the woman is validly in the {\i sotah} procedure based on a proper qualifying case, the court can compel her to swear regarding suspected sins that occurred even during the {\i erusin} period, even if those incidents would not independently have triggered the full {\i sotah} process. The double {\i amen} \u8212- {\i amen she-lo satisi arusa} \u8212- reflects precisely this: her oath about the {\i erusin} period is folded into the main {\i sotah} oath through {\i gilgul}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maamar & Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now moves to a new case transmitted by Rav Chanina in the name of Rav Yochanan. A {\i kohen} performs {\i maamar} with his {\i yevamah} \u8212- his sister-in-law awaiting {\i yibum} \u8212- and there remains another brother who has not yet performed any act. Even where the {\i maamar}-performing brother is a {\i kohen} and the {\i yevamah} is a {\i bat kohen}, and the marriage is entirely unproblematic on its face, the {\i maamar} has nonetheless disqualified her from eating {\i terumah}. She cannot eat {\i terumah} until the {\i yibum} is fully consummated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zikat Yibum Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is subtle and reveals the layered nature of the {\i zikat yibum}. On the surface, everything appears in order. She is a {\i bat kohen}, entitled to eat {\i terumah} from her father\u8217's house. The {\i kohen} who performed {\i maamar} is a perfectly kosher husband-in-waiting; she is not a {\i grushah} or {\i almanah} forbidden to him. One would expect that either by virtue of her father, or by virtue of the pending {\i nisuin} with this {\i kohen}, she should be permitted to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the other brother \u8212- the one who did not perform {\i maamar} \u8212- still retains a {\i zikat yibum} toward her. He is forbidden, rabbinically, to act on that {\i zika} once {\i maamar} has been performed by the first brother. Yet the {\i zika} itself has not been dissolved. That residual {\i zika}, the Gemara explains, functions as a quasi-{\i kiddushin}. It is as if there is a second claim on her, and were that claim ever to be acted upon, it would constitute a {\i biah asurah} \u8212- since the {\i maamar} has defined her as bound to the first brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara frames this through the lens of Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle: {\i shomeret l\u8217'viah psulah lo achlah} \u8212- a woman who is \u8220"awaiting a forbidden {\i biah}\u8221" does not eat {\i terumah}. The other brother\u8217's {\i zika}, if consummated, would be forbidden; therefore she is in a state of {\i shomeret l\u8217'viah psulah} and cannot eat. The question then becomes: does this principle apply only when the awaited forbidden {\i biah} is a biblical prohibition, or also when it is merely rabbinic?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Brother Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara refines the case further. When Ravin came from the Land of Israel, he transmitted: if the {\i kohen} performed {\i maamar} with his {\i yevamah}, and there is no complicating factor with the other brother \u8212- even if the first brother is a {\i kohen} and she is a {\i bat kohen} \u8212- she may eat {\i terumah}. The {\i divrei hakol} position in the simple case is lenient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the other brother \u8212- the one who did not perform {\i maamar} \u8212- is a {\i chalal}, the situation changes entirely. A {\i chalal} who has intercourse with a woman causes her to become a {\i chalalah}, permanently disqualified. The {\i zika} of this {\i chalal} brother toward the {\i yevamah} thus represents a potential {\i biah psulah d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- a biblically forbidden act of intercourse that would create a permanent and irremediable disqualification. In this case, even Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon \u8212- who are generally lenient \u8212- agree that she cannot eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structural reason explains why. Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon are normally lenient even in cases where the eventual {\i biah} would be problematic, because: the {\i kohen} involved could, in another situation, have a woman who eats {\i terumah} on his behalf \u8212- {\i yesh lo l\u8217'haachil b\u8217'makom acher}. That conceptual permission attaches to him and flows through to her. But a {\i chalal} can never have any woman eat {\i terumah} by virtue of her relationship with him; there is no scenario in which a {\i chalal}\u8217's wife eats {\i terumah} on account of him. Therefore the {\i zika} of the {\i chalal} brother cannot be \u8220"covered\u8221" by that principle, and even Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon concede the disqualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Get Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then shifts to what happens after the {\i maamar}-performing brother gives her a {\i get}. The {\i get} in a {\i yibum} context dissolves the {\i maamar}, but because a {\i get} has now been given, it is rabbinically prohibited for either brother to consummate {\i yibum} \u8212- the act of {\i yibum} after a {\i get} is rabbinically forbidden. Biblically, the {\i zika} still exists. So she is now in a state where, if the brother were to act on his {\i zika}, it would be a {\i biah} that is rabbinically but not biblically prohibited. Is she permitted to eat {\i terumah} at this stage?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan v. Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says she may eat. Even Rabbi Meir, the strict opinion, applies his principle only where the awaited forbidden {\i biah} is a biblical prohibition. In this case, the potential {\i biah} \u8212- {\i yibum} after a {\i get} \u8212- is forbidden only rabbinically. Therefore even Rabbi Meir would agree she may eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish says she may not eat. Even Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon, the lenient opinion, apply their leniency only where the {\i kohen} involved could, in some analogous scenario, have a woman eat {\i terumah} on his behalf \u8212- {\i yesh lo l\u8217'haachil b\u8217'makom acher}. Here, this brother has given a {\i get} to this woman. The very nature of his relationship with any woman he might stand in such a relation to is defined by the {\i get}, and no woman in that category can ever eat {\i terumah} on account of him. Even if he were married to another woman with whom this same {\i get}-defined dynamic held, that woman could not eat {\i terumah} by virtue of him. Therefore even Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon would not permit her to eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara addresses a potential objection: might a woman who receives a {\i get} from a {\i kohen} subsequently return to her father\u8217's house and eat {\i terumah} by virtue of her father \u8212- thus demonstrating that women connected to a {\i kohen} through a {\i get} can eat {\i terumah} in some fashion? The Gemara rejects this: a woman who returns to her father\u8217's house ({\i chozeret}) has fully separated from the {\i kohen}\u8217's sphere \u8212- {\i psika minei u\u8217'krova l\u8217'vei nasha}, the relationship is severed and she reverts to her family. But in the present case the woman is still {\i agidah beih} \u8212- bound to him by the residual {\i zika} and the {\i get} \u8212- and cannot be treated as analogous to the {\i chozeret}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Gadol Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah-Bogeret Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara transitions to the laws of the {\i kohen gadol} and his marital restrictions, in preparation for the {\i mishnah} that follows. Rav Chiyya bar Yosef poses a question to Shmuel: a {\i kohen gadol} performs {\i kiddushin} with a {\i ketanah} \u8212- a minor girl \u8212- and she matures into a {\i bogeret} while still under his authority, before {\i chuppah} has occurred. The {\i kohen gadol} is restricted to marrying a woman who meets specific criteria \u8212- she must be a virgin, and according to one interpretation she must not yet be a {\i bogeret}. The question is: does this restriction apply at the moment of {\i kiddushin}, or at the moment of {\i nisuin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel responds by pointing to the {\i mishnah}: women forbidden to a {\i kohen} who were divorced or widowed after {\i nisuin} remain disqualified \u8212- {\i pasul} \u8212- while those divorced or widowed after only {\i erusin} are {\i kesheirah}. He argues that this demonstrates what matters for the {\i kohen}\u8217's special disqualifications: it is the {\i nisuin}, not the {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiyya bar Yosef pushes back: the {\i mishnah} speaks of disqualification through {\i biah}, and it is {\i biah} that creates the disqualification of {\i chalalah}. What makes a woman a {\i chalalah} is the act of forbidden intercourse, which occurs at {\i nisuin}, not at {\i kiddushin}. The {\i mishnah} tells us only which act creates that specific disqualification; it says nothing about the threshold criteria for the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces another case that directly tests the question: a man performs {\i kiddushin} with an {\i almanah} \u8212- a widow \u8212- while he is a regular {\i kohen}, and then he is subsequently appointed {\i kohen gadol}. May he go through with the {\i chuppah}? The {\i mishnah} here appears to say yes \u8212- {\i yichnos}, he may marry her \u8212- implying that since the {\i kiddushin} was permissible at the time it was performed, the {\i nisuin} may proceed even though he is now a {\i kohen gadol} for whom an {\i almanah} would normally be forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel supports his position from this ruling: since the {\i kohen gadol} may complete the marriage to an {\i almanah} when the {\i kiddushin} preceded his appointment, it seems that the operative moment is {\i kiddushin}, not {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Word Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges this inference. The case of {\i almanah} is derived from an extra word in the {\i pasuk}: {\i yikach isha} \u8212- he shall take a woman. From this seemingly superfluous phrase, the Gemara derives that even a {\i kohen gadol} may complete {\i nisuin} with an {\i almanah} when the {\i kiddushin} predated his appointment. This is a special {\i limud} for the {\i almanah} case. By the same token, one could propose that the same {\i limud} should apply to the {\i bogeret} case \u8212- that the {\i pasuk}\u8217's word {\i isha} likewise permits the {\i kohen gadol} to complete {\i nisuin} with a {\i bogeret} when the {\i kiddushin} was performed while she was still a {\i naarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this: {\i isha achas v\u8217'lo shtayim} \u8212- the word {\i isha} appears once, and from one word only one special case can be derived. The {\i limud} was already used for {\i almanah}; it cannot be simultaneously applied to {\i bogeret}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this creates the obvious question: given that both cases seem to be covered by the same verse, why does the {\i almanah} case receive the benefit of the {\i limud} while the {\i bogeret} case does not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i ha ishtani gufa v\u8217'ha lo ishtani gufa} \u8212- in one case her body changed, and in the other it did not. Tosafot offers a clearer account of the underlying logic. The decisive factor is not the physical change itself, but rather: {\i who} underwent the change in status?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i almanah} case, the woman\u8217's own status did not change between {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin}. She was an {\i almanah} at the time of {\i kiddushin} and remains an {\i almanah} now. It was the man whose status changed \u8212- he was a regular {\i kohen} at {\i kiddushin} and became a {\i kohen gadol} afterward. Since her status was permissible when the relationship was established, the {\i kiddushin} defines the framework, and the {\i nisuin} proceeds accordingly. The special {\i limud} from {\i yikach isha} confirms this reading explicitly for the {\i almanah} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, in the {\i bogeret} case, it is the woman whose status changed between {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin}. She was a {\i ketanah} or {\i naarah} at the time of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- fully permissible to the {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- but she became a {\i bogeret} before {\i nisuin}. Since the relevant change occurred in her status, the {\i nisuin} must be evaluated based on who she is now, not who she was at {\i kiddushin}. The {\i limud} from {\i isha} is therefore reserved for the {\i almanah} case where the woman\u8217's status was constant, and the {\i bogeret} case \u8212- where the woman\u8217's own status changed \u8212- cannot benefit from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction shapes a broader principle: when assessing whether the special marital requirements of a {\i kohen gadol} are satisfied, one evaluates the woman\u8217's status at {\i kiddushin} when the change was on his side, and at {\i nisuin} when the change was on hers. The discussion of the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's special restrictions will continue in the material that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 59\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Gadol Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin/Nissu\u8217'in Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion on this daf flows directly from the {\i sugya} that concluded the previous day\u8217's learning \u8212- namely, the case of a {\i kohen gadol} who betroths a woman when she is still a {\i ketannah} and the question of what happens if she becomes a {\i bogeret} before the marriage is consummated. The core issue was whether the governing moment is the {\i kiddushin} or the {\i nissu\u8217'in}. The {\i gemara} had attempted to resolve this from {\i pesukim} but ultimately concluded that she would need to be a {\i ketannah} at the time of {\i nissu\u8217'in} as well \u8212- though a different rule may apply in the parallel case where it is the man who changed status, having performed {\i kiddushin} as an ordinary {\i kohen} and subsequently becoming a {\i kohen gadol}. In that scenario, she might be assessed according to her state at the time of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What this earlier discussion established is the existence of special {\i halachot} governing the marriage of a {\i kohen gadol} that go beyond those applying to an ordinary {\i kohen}. One of them is clear from the text of the {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i ishah bi-vsuteha yikkach}, he shall take a woman in her virginity \u8212- namely, the {\i mitzvah} to marry a {\i besulah}. Less transparent from the {\i pesukim}, yet taken for granted by the {\i gemara}, is the requirement that she be a {\i ketannah} or at least a {\i na\u8217'arah} at the time of marriage, and not a {\i bogeret} \u8212- a woman who has reached full physical maturity, presumed to occur from the age of twelve and a half. Whether this requirement applies at the moment of {\i kiddushin} or {\i nissu\u8217'in} is itself a question the {\i gemara} has been wrestling with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow/Mature Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that background in place, the {\i mishnah} addresses these issues directly. {\i Kohen gadol lo yissa almanah} \u8212- the {\i kohen gadol} may not marry a widow. This is explicit in the {\i Torah}. And the {\i mishnah} clarifies: {\i bein almanah min ha-erusin bein almanah min ha-nissu\u8217'in} \u8212- it makes no difference whether she is a widow from {\i erusin}, and therefore presumably still a virgin, or a widow from {\i nissu\u8217'in}. Either way, the {\i Torah}\u8217's prohibition applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i ve-lo yissa bogeret} \u8212- he may not marry a {\i bogeret}. The three stages of female maturity bear repeating: a {\i ketannah} is under twelve years and one day; from twelve years and one day to twelve and a half she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}; from twelve and a half onward she is a {\i bogeret}, fully physically mature, and a {\i kohen gadol} may not marry her \u8212- though this is nowhere made explicit in the {\i pesukim}. To this, {\i Rabbi Elazar ve-Rabbi Shimon makhshirin bi-vogheret} \u8212- they declare a {\i bogeret} permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also records, in brackets whose precise {\i girsa} is uncertain, the further restriction of {\i ve-lo yissa mukat etz} \u8212- he may not marry a woman who has been, literally, smitten by wood. This refers to a woman whose hymen has been ruptured not through any act of intercourse but through some other physical activity \u8212- something having been inserted vaginally, or more commonly in practice, rupture resulting from general physical activity. Whether this clause is stated {\i divrei ha-kol} or attributed specifically to Rabbi Elazar and Rabbi Shimon is itself a textual question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What this {\i mishnah} immediately sets in motion is a fundamental inquiry: what is the definition of virginity for these purposes? Is it about the state of the hymen, or is it about sexual experience? The two are not always identical. One can imagine an act of intercourse that does not rupture the hymen, and conversely, a rupturing of the hymen that has nothing to do with intercourse. The {\i gemara} turns itself to this question at once.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Virginity Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Maturity Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before entering the debate between the {\i tanna\u8217'im}, the {\i gemara} first dispenses with the question of the {\i almanah}. The {\i baraita} cites the {\i pasuk almanah lo yikkach}, and one might have thought to limit this prohibition to a widow from {\i nissu\u8217'in} \u8212- on the model of Tamar, {\i shevi almanah beit avikh}, who had been in a full {\i nissu\u8217'in} relationship with the brothers and had therefore had marital relations. Perhaps all {\i almanot} should be read through that lens? No, the {\i gemara} responds. Because the {\i Torah} juxtaposes {\i almanah} with {\i gerushah} \u8212- {\i et eleh lo yikach} \u8212- and a {\i gerushah} has no such limiting precedent. A {\i gerushah} applies whether from {\i erusin} or {\i nissu\u8217'in}, and so the same is true of an {\i almanah}. The prohibition on marrying an {\i almanah} or a {\i gerushah} concerns their status, not the question of virginity; that concern is addressed by the separate {\i pasuk}, {\i ishah bi-vstuleha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to the {\i bogeret}: the {\i gemara} cites the {\i baraita} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hu ishah bi-vstuleha yikkach}. Why does the {\i Torah} use the unusual phrase {\i bi-vstuleha} rather than simply {\i besulah yikkach}? The extra formulation, according to {\i Rabbi Meir}, comes to exclude the {\i bogeret} \u8212- {\i prat le-bogeret she-kalu lah betuleha} \u8212- she whose {\i betulim} have dissipated. {\i Tosafot} explains, and the {\i gemara} itself makes clear, that this does not mean the hymen has entirely disappeared, but that it has somehow thinned out or is no longer in its original state. The anatomical question of whether the hymen actually changes during puberty is genuinely complex \u8212- contemporary medical professionals tend to resist the binary framing of \u8220"there or not there,\u8221" since perforation is a matter of degree even in younger women. Nevertheless, the {\i gemara}\u8217's assumption is that by the completion of puberty, the hymen is in a meaningfully different state than it was before.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Hymen View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is anchored entirely in the physical reality of the hymen. Had the {\i Torah} simply said {\i besulah yikkach}, {\i besulah} would be an inclusive term \u8212- {\i afilu miktzat betulim mashma} \u8212- even if there is only some portion of the hymen present, she would qualify. The word {\i bi-vstuleha}, with its intensifying formulation, demands something more \u8212- {\i ad de-ika kol betulim} \u8212- the hymen must be in its complete, unchanged state, as it exists prior to puberty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And then there is the second dimension of the word {\i bi-vstuleha}. If the {\i bet} prefix comes to narrow the category \u8212- demanding that the hymen be entirely intact \u8212- it also simultaneously comes to broaden it in another direction. Specifically, {\i kedarkah ve-lo shelo kedarkah}: the extra {\i bet} of {\i bi-vstuleha} teaches that the concern applies only to vaginal intercourse. Anal intercourse is not a problem. This is quite striking, but within Rabbi Meir\u8217's framework it is perfectly coherent. His entire focus is the anatomical state of the hymen \u8212- not the question of sexual experience. If a woman has had anal sex, that act has no impact on the hymen, and she remains, in his view, a {\i besulah}. Conversely, she could be a {\i bogeret} whose hymen has changed simply through the process of maturation, and she would be disqualified. The criterion is purely physical: the state of the hymen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implications follow inexorably. {\i Mukat etz} is a problem \u8212- something has physically ruptured the hymen, even without intercourse. Anal sex is not a problem \u8212- there is no impact on the hymen. And since the only criterion is anatomical, the restriction is extremely narrow in one direction and extremely demanding in another: she must be a girl who has not yet matured, whose hymen is entirely intact, and in whom nothing whatsoever has caused any physical rupture. But she could, in principle, have had sexual relations \u8212- so long as they had no physical impact on the hymen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer/Shimon Sex View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon reason in precisely the opposite direction. Had the {\i Torah} said simply {\i besulah}, that alone would imply \u8212- {\i besulah sheleimah mashma} \u8212- a complete, intact hymen. The word {\i bi-vstuleha}, with its expanded formulation, moves in the opposite direction: {\i afilu miktzat betulim} \u8212- even if the hymen is not in its original state, even if she is a {\i bogeret}, she still qualifies. Their concern is not with the precise anatomical condition of the hymen, but with whether the woman has had sexual intercourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then {\i bi-vstuleha} must be doing something in the restrictive direction as well. If it is broadening the definition of {\i besulah} to include even a {\i bogeret}, it simultaneously narrows it by establishing that {\i bein kedarkah bein shelo kedarkah} \u8212- both vaginal and anal intercourse disqualify her. This is equally logical from their perspective: since the criterion is about whether she has had sex, not about the hymen, the act of anal intercourse renders her a non-virgin, even if it has had no impact on the hymen whatsoever. The question of {\i mukat etz} then becomes more nuanced. If the {\i mishnah}\u8217's {\i mukat etz} prohibition is attributed to Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon, they would be operating with two distinct criteria simultaneously: one relating to the hymen having been physically compromised, and one relating to whether she has had sex. Since both criteria exist, neither needs to be taken to an extreme. The hymen criterion does not need to demand pristine anatomical perfection \u8212- that would be Rabbi Meir\u8217's extreme. And there is still a category in which a woman is not a {\i besulah} even without sexual intercourse, namely {\i mukat etz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure of the debate can be stated simply: Rabbi Meir holds it is entirely about the hymen, not about sex. Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon hold it is about sex \u8212- and either entirely so, or about sex combined with a more moderate standard regarding the hymen \u8212- but in any event not about the hymen alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Anal Sex Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehudah Anal Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehudah amar Rav: niv\u8217'alah shelo kedarkah pesulah li-kehunah}. Rav Yehudah in the name of Rav rules that a woman who has had anal intercourse is disqualified for marriage to a {\i kohen} \u8212- which in context means specifically a {\i kohen gadol}. This is precisely the position one would intuit: of course a woman who has had intercourse, even anal, is no longer a virgin in any meaningful sense. What the {\i gemara} has already shown is that according to Rabbi Meir, the opposite is true \u8212- since Rabbi Meir focuses exclusively on the hymen. Rav\u8217's ruling, then, reflects the position of Rabbi Eliezer, and the {\i gemara} will shortly say as much: {\i ve-Rav de-amar ke-Rabbi Eliezer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mativ Rava}. Rava raises a challenge from the {\i baraita} dealing with the rapist\u8217's obligation to marry his victim: {\i ve-lo tihyeh le-isha} \u8212- she shall be his wife \u8212- applies only to a woman who is {\i ra\u8217'uyah lo}, halachically fitting for him. {\i Prat le-almanah le-kohen gadol, u-gerushah ve-chalutzah le-kohen hedyot} \u8212- if a {\i kohen gadol} rapes an {\i almanah}, or an ordinary {\i kohen} rapes a {\i gerushah} or {\i chalutzah}, the obligation to marry does not apply, since these women are forbidden to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: how exactly did this {\i kohen gadol} rape this {\i almanah}? {\i Ileima kedarkah} \u8212- if it was vaginal intercourse \u8212- then why must the {\i baraita} invoke the fact that she is an {\i almanah} at all? {\i Teipokei lei mishum de-hava lei be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- before any question of her status as a widow, she is already a {\i be\u8217'ulah} by virtue of his own act. The act of rape itself, even though it was {\i be\u8217'ulat atzmo}, has made her a non-virgin, and therefore she is disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen gadol} on that ground alone. Why does the {\i baraita} need to say she is an {\i almanah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela shelo kedarkah} \u8212- it must be that the {\i kohen gadol} raped her {\i shelo kedarkah}, anally. And in that case, according to Rav\u8217's ruling, she would still be a {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- yet the {\i baraita} needs to find a separate ground for her disqualification, namely that she is an {\i almanah}. This demonstrates that {\i shelo kedarkah} does not make her a {\i be\u8217'ulah}. {\i Ha mani Rabbi Meir} \u8212- this {\i baraita} must be reflecting Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. {\i Ve-Rav de-amar ke-Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- and Rav is ruling in accordance with Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But now the {\i gemara} presses further. If we are operating within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework, {\i mai irya mishum be\u8217'ulah}? Why need we discuss the category of {\i be\u8217'ulah} and the specific restrictions of a {\i kohen gadol} at all? {\i Teipokei de-hava lei zonah} \u8212- there is a far more sweeping issue: a woman who has had intercourse outside of marriage is, according to Rabbi Eliezer, a {\i zonah}, and is forbidden even to an ordinary {\i kohen}. Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i penui ha-ba al ha-penuya asah zona} \u8212- a single man who has intercourse with a single woman outside the context of marriage renders her a {\i zonah}. So within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's world, if the woman has had anal intercourse with some man outside of marriage, she is not only a {\i be\u8217'ulah} for purposes of the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's restriction; she is a {\i zonah} forbidden to every {\i kohen}. The question of {\i kohen gadol} and {\i be\u8217'ulah} is then superfluous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bestiality Non-Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve the difficulty, {\i Rav Yosef} offers a striking suggestion: {\i kegon she-niv\u8217'alah li-vehemah} \u8212- the case involves a woman who was penetrated by an animal. Through this, the {\i gemara} seeks to identify a scenario in which a woman has had an act of penetration \u8212- making her a {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- but has not engaged in {\i zenut} in the ordinary sense, and is therefore not a {\i zonah} according to Rabbi Eliezer. The reasoning is that {\i zenut} is a category defined by human fornication. An act with an animal falls outside this category entirely \u8212- it is not considered sexual in the relevant halachic sense for purposes of {\i issur zona}, even as it might still bear on her status as a {\i be\u8217'ulah} by virtue of the physical act itself. Of course, this itself raises a question: if we say the act is not considered \u8220"sex\u8221" for purposes of {\i zona}, on what basis do we say it is considered sex for purposes of {\i be\u8217'ulah}? The two seem to rest on the same question of whether the act constitutes a sexual act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye: mimman nafshach} \u8212- make up your mind. {\i Ee be\u8217'ulah havya, zonah nami havya} \u8212- if you consider an act of intercourse with an animal to make her a {\i be\u8217'ulah}, on the grounds that it is an act of sex, then it also makes her a {\i zonah}. {\i Ve-ee zonah lo havya, be\u8217'ulah nami lo havya} \u8212- and if it is not considered an act of sex for purposes of {\i zona} because the act with an animal is categorically different, then for the same reason it does not make her a {\i be\u8217'ulah} either. Since we are no longer discussing the hymen \u8212- the {\i gemara} is now firmly in the territory of anal intercourse, where the hymen is not the issue \u8212- the only question is whether this constitutes \u8220"sex.\u8221" One cannot answer that question differently depending on which {\i issur} is under discussion. Abaye\u8217's objection is accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mukat Etz Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds with the case of {\i mukat etz}: here is a woman who is not a {\i besulah} \u8212- the hymen is ruptured \u8212- yet she is certainly not a {\i zonah}. One might have thought the same logic could be applied to the case of {\i shelo kedarkah} \u8212- perhaps the act of anal intercourse or penetration by an animal is considered to have done something to the hymen, not to have constituted \u8220"sex,\u8221" and on that physical basis she is disqualified, while remaining free of the {\i zona} status. But the {\i gemara} immediately forecloses this. {\i Im ken ein lach ishah she-kesheirah li-kehunah she-lo na\u8217'asis mukat etz al yedei tzeror} \u8212- if every physical intrusion into the body, even one that bears no relationship to sexual intercourse, were to disqualify, then wiping oneself with a stone \u8212- the ancient practice \u8212- would render every woman a {\i mukat etz}. This is clearly impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, when the {\i gemara} discusses {\i shelo kedarkah}, it is not discussing something that physically ruptures the hymen. It is discussing an act of sex. And once it is an act of sex, Abaye\u8217's objection stands: you cannot say it is sex for purposes of {\i be\u8217'ulah} and not sex for purposes of {\i zona}. The two are the same question. The {\i gemara} accepts this conclusion, and the challenge of how to construct a case in which a woman is a {\i be\u8217'ulah} according to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework without also being a {\i zonah} according to that same framework remains unresolved \u8212- until Rabbi Zeira offers his solution, which begins the next section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'ona Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Zeira Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ella amar Rabbi Zeira: bi-mema\u8217'enet} \u8212- Rabbi Zeira\u8217's answer is that the case involves a woman who exercises {\i mi\u8217'un}, the right of refusal. Here is the scenario: a {\i ketannah}, a minor girl, was married off by her mother and brother through a rabbinic {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i kiddushei ketanah}. She lived with her husband and they had intercourse. However, the intercourse was {\i shelo kedarkah} only. Now she turns twelve, reaches majority, and exercises {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- she declares that she does not wish this marriage and dissolves it. Since the {\i kiddushin} was only rabbinic in the first place, and she exercises her right of refusal, the marriage is retroactively undone. She is not an {\i almanah} and not a {\i gerushah} \u8212- there is no legal aftermath of the marriage. She is, retroactively, as though she was never married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now examine her status. On the one hand, since she was in a marriage \u8212- even a rabbinic one \u8212- when the intercourse took place, it was not {\i bi\u8217'at zenut}. Even Rabbi Eliezer concedes that a legally and socially recognized marriage, even a rabbinic one, removes the act from the category of fornication. She is therefore not a {\i zonah}. On the other hand, since the marriage has been retroactively dissolved, she is regarded as never having been married, and she may marry a regular {\i kohen}. Yet she has had intercourse {\i shelo kedarkah}, and according to Rav\u8217's ruling in the name of Rav Yehudah, that makes her a {\i be\u8217'ulah}. She therefore cannot marry a {\i kohen gadol}, even though she is not a {\i zonah}. This is precisely the case needed: a {\i be\u8217'ulah} who is not a {\i zonah}, within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a celebrated observation of Rav Chaim regarding this case of {\i mi\u8217'un} that deserves careful attention, because it reveals a conceptual tension at the heart of the solution. The {\i mah nafshach} presses itself: if the marriage is retroactively undone, then at the time of intercourse she was retroactively not married \u8212- in which case the sex was non-marital, and Rabbi Eliezer should declare her a {\i zonah} after all. Conversely, if we say the sex was marital, should she not be a {\i gerushah} of some rabbinic variety, rather than being fully available to an ordinary {\i kohen}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chaim resolves this by articulating the principle of {\i mikan u-le-haba le-mafrei\u8217'a}: moving forward, retroactively she was never married; for things that already occurred, we treat them as having happened within the context of marriage. In other words, the retroactivity operates in different directions depending on the question being asked. For any new question that arises \u8212- her status going forward \u8212- we say she was never married. For events that already occurred \u8212- the sexual acts that took place during the relationship \u8212- we say those happened within a marriage. The marriage exists yesterday but not tomorrow. This allows the {\i be\u8217'ulah} status and the absence of {\i zona} status to coexist without contradiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} has considerable relevance to contemporary discussions about solutions to the {\i agunah} problem, particularly proposals involving {\i hafka\u8217'at kiddushin} or {\i kiddushin al tenai} \u8212- conditional marriage. One argument raised against such proposals is the principle {\i ein adam oseh be\u8217'ilato bi\u8217'at zenut} \u8212- no one would want the {\i kiddushin} to be conditional, because if the condition retroactively dissolves the marriage, all the intercourse that occurred within it is retroactively rendered non-marital fornication. This argument draws on the idea that in the {\i gemara}\u8217's world, the only institution defining the relationship was the religious institution of marriage. If that is undone, nothing remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A counter-argument, however, emerges from the case of {\i mema\u8217'enet}. Even a rabbinic {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i kiddushei ketanah}, arranged by the mother and brother \u8212- is sufficient to define the relationship as non-fornication, {\i de-oraita}, for purposes of the {\i zona} prohibition. In the contemporary context, even where a {\i halachic} marriage is retroactively dissolved, a civil marriage recognized legally and communally remains in place. One might argue, building on the logic of {\i mema\u8217'enet}, that this civil recognition is sufficient to prevent the retroactive dissolution from rendering past intercourse as {\i bi\u8217'at zenut} \u8212- since the relationship was embedded in a recognized legal and social structure, even if not a {\i halachic} one. This is, to be clear, a complex and contested question, but the {\i sugya} of {\i mema\u8217'enet} is a genuine and relevant data point in that conversation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bestiality Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bestiality Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Shimi bar Chiya: niv\u8217'alah li-vehemah kesheirah li-kehunah}. Having established that {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo kedarkah} \u8212- notwithstanding its non-impact on the hymen \u8212- nonetheless renders a woman a {\i be\u8217'ulah} and disqualifies her from marrying a {\i kohen gadol}, the {\i gemara} now returns to the case of bestiality. Rav Shimi bar Chiya declares that a woman who has been penetrated by an animal is {\i kesheirah li-kehunah} \u8212- fit to marry a {\i kohen gadol}. This is the ruling that was anticipated in the earlier exchange: since an act with an animal is categorically different from a sexual act in the halachic sense, it neither renders her a {\i be\u8217'ulah} nor a {\i zonah}. Rashi notes that the discussion here presumes {\i kedarkah}, vaginal penetration, which would normally rupture the hymen \u8212- and this ruling therefore implies that the position regarding {\i mukat etz} is lenient, or that the case is {\i shelo kedarkah}. Either way, the act is not considered an act of sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Non-Human\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanya nami hachi} \u8212- a {\i baraita} teaches similarly: {\i niv\u8217'alah le-mi she-eino ish} \u8212- if a woman was penetrated by one who is not a man, {\i af al pi she-bi-sekilah} \u8212- even though the offense carries the death penalty of stoning, {\i kesheirah li-kehunah} \u8212- she is fit for {\i kehunah}. The severity of the prohibition does not transform the act into an act of sex for these purposes. It remains categorically sui generis, and therefore does not affect her status with regard to virginity or the {\i zona} prohibition. The question is raised whether the phrase {\i she-eino ish} is a euphemism for an animal, or could it encompass other scenarios \u8212- such as an act with a minor, whose sexual act may or may not be considered a full {\i bi\u8217'ah} depending on age. The {\i gemara} understands the {\i baraita} to be speaking by way of euphemism, with an animal as the intended referent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heitlu Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Dimi came from {\i Eretz Yisrael} he brought the following incident: {\i ma\u8217'aseh be-na\u8217'arah achat be-Heitlu} \u8212- a story about a young woman in a place called Heitlu, who was cleaning herself, {\i she-hayetah mekabedet et ha-bayit} \u8212- this is understood as a euphemism for wiping herself \u8212- and a wild Cyprian dog attacked her from behind and penetrated her, {\i va-hikhshirah Rebbe li-kehunah} \u8212- and Rebbe ruled that she was fit to marry a {\i kohen gadol}. Since the act was committed by an animal, it does not constitute an act of intercourse in the halachic sense, and she retains her eligibility. Whether the penetration was vaginal or anal is a question the {\i gemara} leaves open; in either case, Rebbe\u8217's ruling stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i le-kohen gadol mi hava} \u8212- was there actually a {\i kohen gadol} in Rebbe\u8217's time to whom this would be practically relevant? {\i Ela re\u8217'uyah le-kohen gadol} \u8212- the meaning is that she is theoretically fit for a {\i kohen gadol}, even if the ruling had no immediate practical application.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Esnan Zonah Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Rava mi-Parquin le-Rav Ashi: heicha amur Rabbanan ein zenut li-vehemah} \u8212- where do the Rabbis derive that bestiality does not constitute {\i zenut}? The source is the {\i pasuk}: {\i lo tavi esnan zonah u-mechir kelev beit Hashem elokecha} \u8212- do not bring a gift of a prostitute or the purchase price of a dog into the House of God. The {\i halacha} derived from the first clause is that a sheep given to a prostitute as payment for her services cannot be brought as a {\i korban}. And {\i mechir kelev} \u8212- whether read in its plain sense as the price paid for a dog, or understood by the Sages quite literally as the price paid to engage in an act with a dog \u8212- teaches that if a sheep is exchanged for an act of bestiality, that sheep {\i is} permitted for use as a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is established in the {\i mishnah}: {\i esnan kelev u-mechir zonah mutarin} \u8212- the payment given for a dog and the payment received for a {\i zonah} are permissible. {\i She-ne\u8217'emar gam sheneihem} \u8212- the {\i Torah} says \u8220"the two of them,\u8221" meaning these two specific things, {\i shenayim ve-lo arba} \u8212- two, not four. The exclusion of the price paid for bestiality demonstrates that the act is not categorized as {\i zenut} at all. Were it considered {\i zenut}, the sheep would be forbidden as {\i esnan zonah}. Since it is not forbidden, the {\i Torah} is implicitly declaring that the act falls entirely outside the category of sexual fornication. The {\i gemara} acknowledges one could make other distinctions \u8212- perhaps the exclusion is for some different reason \u8212- but the {\i gemara} reads the exclusion as reflecting the categorical distinction: bestiality is simply not the same type of act as human intercourse. Calling it something else may be unavoidable, but placing it within the same legal category as {\i zenut} cannot be done.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aseh Violation Chalal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape/Seduction Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} and the {\i gemara} now turn to the consequences of a {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's violations of the {\i mitzvat aseh} of {\i ishah bi-vstuleha yikkach}. The {\i baraita} states: {\i anusas atzmo u-mefutas atzmo} \u8212- if a {\i kohen gadol} raped or seduced a woman, she has now become a {\i be\u8217'ulat atzmo}, a woman whose loss of virgin status is owing to his own prior act. He is forbidden to marry her \u8212- he violates the {\i mitzvat aseh} of marrying a virgin \u8212- but {\i im nasa nasa}: if he did marry her, the marriage stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then distinguishes this case from that of {\i anusas chaveiro u-mefutas chaveiro} \u8212- a woman who was raped or seduced by another man. Here too the prohibition applies, since she is no longer a {\i besulah}, and the {\i mitzvat aseh} is violated if he marries her. But the legal consequence is sharper: {\i ve-im nasa}, if he did marry her, {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov omer ha-vlad chalal} \u8212- the child is a {\i chalal}, stripped of {\i kedushat kehunah}. {\i Ve-chachamim omrim ha-vlad kasher} \u8212- the Sages say the child is entirely valid. This is a debate about whether a child conceived through a violation of an {\i aseh} \u8212- as opposed to a {\i lav} \u8212- in the laws of {\i kehunah} has the status of a {\i chalal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding to the {\i chalal} debate, the {\i gemara} addresses the meaning of {\i im nasa nasa} in the case of {\i be\u8217'ulat atzmo}. {\i Amar Rav Huna amar Rav: u-motzi be-get} \u8212- yes, the marriage stands, but Rav adds: and he must give her a {\i get}. The {\i gemara} notes the apparent redundancy: what {\i chiddush} is there in saying the marriage is legally binding? Even when a {\i kohen} violates a {\i lav} \u8212- marrying a {\i gerushah}, for instance \u8212- the marriage is binding {\i bedi\u8217'avad} and requires a {\i get}. Why single this case out?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Acha bar Yaakov}: the {\i chiddush} lies in the law of {\i knas}. When a man seduces a woman, he either marries her or pays the penalty fine. If he chooses to marry her, the marriage exempts him from paying. In a case involving a {\i lav} violation, such a marriage would not be considered a licit marriage for this purpose, and would not exempt from the {\i knas}. Here, since the violation is only of an {\i aseh}, the marriage does exempt from the {\i knas}. That is the substance of {\i im nasa nasa} \u8212- the marriage is legally operative and counts. Nevertheless, rabbinically, the situation cannot be permitted to continue. The {\i get} must be given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Toleration Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites a tradition in the name of Rav and Rabbi Yochanan: {\i bogeret u-mukat etz lo yissa, ve-im nasa nasa} \u8212- a {\i kohen gadol} should not marry a {\i bogeret} or a {\i mukat etz}, but if he did, the marriage stands. The {\i gemara} asks: if the principle is {\i im nasa nasa} and the marriage continues, why not say the same about {\i be\u8217'ulat atzmo}? Why not tolerate the situation {\i bedi\u8217'avad} in that case too?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer given is {\i sofa lihyot bogeret tachtav} and {\i sofa lihyot mukat etz tachtav}: in the cases of {\i bogeret} and {\i mukat etz}, the point of prohibition is the moment of marriage, not the ongoing state of being married to a non-{\i besulah}. After all, every woman a {\i kohen gadol} marries will eventually not be a {\i besulah}, and the {\i Torah} does not prohibit that ongoing state. Therefore, {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, we can tolerate the situation. But in the case of {\i be\u8217'ulat atzmo}, the problem traces back to general inappropriate behavior \u8212- a {\i choteh niskar} concern \u8212- and therefore the situation cannot be rabinically sanctioned even after the fact. The ongoing presence of the {\i issur} is a factor at the societal level, even if the question of whether he transgresses {\i biblically} on an ongoing basis is more complex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: does the {\i kohen gadol} transgress the {\i aseh} only at the initial moment of marriage, or does he continue to transgress throughout the marriage? The {\i gemara}\u8217's framing suggests the transgression is concentrated in the initial act, since any woman becomes a {\i be\u8217'ulah} after that first act \u8212- yet Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's position, discussed next, implies the violation continues. This remains an open and significant question in the {\i issurei kehunah} framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal from Aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states the dispute: Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov says the child of such a union is a {\i chalal}; the Sages say the child is valid. The {\i gemara} notes that this applies to the case of {\i anusas chaveiro u-mefutas chaveiro} \u8212- the woman made a {\i be\u8217'ulah} by another man \u8212- and the child could be conceived well into the marriage, long after the first act. Since Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov holds the child is a {\i chalal} even then, it implies he views the {\i issur} as continuous throughout the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Chalal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One approach to explaining Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's ruling was proposed earlier: perhaps his position aligns with Rabbi Eliezer, who holds that premarital sex renders a woman a {\i zonah}, forbidden even to an ordinary {\i kohen}. If she is a {\i zonah}, the child\u8217's {\i chalal} status derives from the {\i zona} prohibition, not from the {\i aseh} of marrying a {\i besulah}. The {\i gemara} rejects this because Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's rulings are described as {\i kav ve-naki} \u8212- few but reliable \u8212- such that we rule by them. But Rabbah bar bar Chana in the name of Rav explicitly rules that {\i halacha} does not follow Rabbi Eliezer on the {\i zona} issue. If Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's ruling depended on Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, we would be forced to accept Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling as well. Since we do not, this explanation is rejected: {\i kashya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi}: the simpler explanation of the debate is that it concerns {\i yesh chalal mi-chayvei aseh} \u8212- whether a {\i chalal} can be produced by a violation of a {\i mitzvat aseh}, as opposed to a {\i lav}. {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov savar yesh chalal mi-chayvei aseh} \u8212- he holds that violating even an {\i aseh} produces a {\i chalal} child. {\i Ve-Rabbanan savri ein chalal mi-chayvei aseh} \u8212- the Sages hold that {\i chalal} status requires a {\i lav} violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The textual support for Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov is the {\i pasuk}: {\i almanah u-gerushah ve-chalala zona et eleh lo yikach ki im besulah me-amav yikach ishah, ve-lo yechalel zaro be-amav} \u8212- and he shall not desecrate his seed. Reading the two {\i pesukim} together, any violation \u8212- including the {\i aseh} of {\i ki im besulah yikach} \u8212- produces a {\i chalal}. The Sages counter that {\i eleh} \u8212- \u8220"these\u8221" \u8212- is a limiting term: the word {\i eleh} interrupts the series and marks off only the {\i lavin} as generating {\i chalal} status; the {\i aseh} of {\i ki im besulah} is in a separate category and does not produce {\i chalal}. Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov, in response, reads {\i eleh} as doing something different: excluding a woman who is a {\i niddah} from generating {\i chalal} status. Even though violating the {\i issur karet} of {\i niddah} is a far weightier offense, since it is not a marital {\i issur kehunah} about status \u8212- it is temporary and situational \u8212- it does not produce a {\i chalal}. The word {\i eleh} carves out that exclusion, not the exclusion of {\i aseh} violations. The {\i gemara} notes that this position of Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov leaves a structural difficulty \u8212- {\i kashya} \u8212- that is left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Priestly Tumah Sister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betrothed Sister Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now opens a new but closely related topic: the obligation of a {\i kohen} to become {\i tamei} for his virgin sister. The {\i Torah} states: {\i le-achoto ha-besulah ha-kerova elav asher lo hayetah le-ish} \u8212- for his sister the {\i besulah}, close to him, who has not been to a man. The {\i baraita} records a dispute: {\i Rabbi Meir ve-Rabbi Yehudah omrim metamei lah} \u8212- a {\i kohen} does become {\i tamei} for his sister even if she is {\i arusah}; {\i Rabbi Yosei ve-Rabbi Shimon omrim ein metamei lah} \u8212- he does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Excludes Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the question of {\i anusah u-mifutah} \u8212- a sister who has been raped or seduced and has had sexual intercourse \u8212- {\i divrei ha-kol ein metamei lah}: all authorities agree the {\i kohen} may not become {\i tamei} for her. The {\i pasuk}\u8217's language is plain: {\i besulah asher lo hayetah le-ish}. Intercourse has occurred; she is no longer {\i besulah} in the relevant sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mukat Etz Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of {\i mukat etz} in this context is whether a sister whose hymen has been ruptured without intercourse is still within the category of {\i besulah} for purposes of {\i tumah}. {\i U-mukat etz ein metamei lah divrei Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds that the {\i kohen} does not become {\i tamei} for a {\i mukat etz} sister. {\i She-hayah Rabbi Shimon omer: re\u8217'uyah le-kohen gadol metamei lah; she-einah re\u8217'uyah le-kohen gadol ein metamei lah} \u8212- he links the criterion directly to the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's marriage eligibility standard. Since a {\i mukat etz} is unfit for a {\i kohen gadol}, she is also not in the category for {\i tumah} by an ordinary {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bogeret Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-vogeret metamei lah divrei kol adam} \u8212- a {\i bogeret} sister: the {\i kohen} does become {\i tamei} for her, and this is the unanimous position. This is striking, because Rabbi Meir himself \u8212- who ordinarily holds that a {\i bogeret} is no longer a {\i besulah} for purposes of the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's marriage \u8212- agrees here that the {\i kohen} becomes {\i tamei} for a {\i bogeret} sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standard Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How can Rabbi Meir hold two different standards simultaneously \u8212- {\i bogeret} is not a {\i besulah} for marriage to a {\i kohen gadol}, yet she is a {\i besulah} for {\i tumah} by a {\i kohen} for his sister? The {\i gemara} addresses this directly. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama de-Rabbi Meir ve-Rabbi Yehudah} \u8212- why do Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehudah say the {\i kohen} remains {\i metamei} even for an {\i arusah}? They interpret the {\i pesukim} as follows: {\i le-achoto ha-besulah \u8212- prat le-anusah u-mifutah}. {\i Besulah} excludes a woman who has had intercourse. One might then have thought to exclude {\i mukat etz} as well, since anatomically the hymen is compromised. But {\i talmud lomar asher lo hayetah le-ish} \u8212- the additional phrase {\i who has not been to a man} defines what {\i besulah} means in this context: {\i mi she-havayatah al yedei ish} \u8212- one whose rupture was caused by a man. Only that case is excluded. A {\i mukat etz}, whose rupture has no human sexual cause, is not excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha-kerovah elav \u8212- le-rabbos arusah}: the word {\i kerovah}, close to him, is interpreted as including even an {\i arusah}, since she is still within his familial orbit. {\i Eilav \u8212- le-rabbos et ha-bogeret}: the word {\i eilav}, to him, includes even a {\i bogeret}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks a pointed question: why does the {\i baraita} need a separate {\i derasha} to include a {\i bogeret}, given that Rabbi Meir himself holds {\i besulah afilu miktzat betulim mashma} \u8212- {\i besulah} includes even one with a partial {\i hymen}? The answer is that without this {\i derasha}, one might have applied a {\i gezerah shavah} \u8212- learning {\i besulah besulah} from the {\i kohen gadol} context, where a {\i bogeret} is excluded \u8212- and imposed the same restriction here. Therefore the {\i derasha} is necessary to establish that the two contexts operate by different standards. The same Rabbi Meir who demands anatomical perfection of the hymen for the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's marriage, and for whom {\i mukat etz} is disqualifying in that context, rules here \u8212- in the context of a {\i kohen}\u8217's {\i tumah} for his sister \u8212- entirely by the criterion of sex, not by the state of the hymen. In this context, {\i bogeret} poses no problem, {\i mukat etz} poses no problem, and {\i arusah} poses no problem: the only disqualifying factor is intercourse. The two domains, while sharing terminology, operate by entirely distinct principles.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of Rabbi Yosei and Rabbi Shimon\u8217's opposing position \u8212- that an {\i arusah} is excluded from the {\i tumah} obligation \u8212- is introduced at the close of the {\i shiur}, where the {\i baraita}\u8217's {\i derasha} of {\i le-achoto ha-besulah prat la-arusah} begins to be analyzed. That analysis will be taken up in the following day\u8217's learning.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 59b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Betulah Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hermeneutics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya at the bottom of 60a presents the position of Rabbi Yosi and Rabbi Shimon regarding the {\i kohen}\u8217's obligation to become {\i tamei} for a sister who is a {\i betulah}. The relevant {\i pasuk} reads: {\i l\u8217'achoso habetulah hakerovah eilav asher lo haysah l\u8217'ish} \u8212- \u8220"for his sister the virgin who is close to him who has not been with a man.\u8221" The question is how to read the dual characterization of {\i betulah} and {\i asher lo haysah l\u8217'ish}, and what each phrase independently demands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi and Rabbi Shimon read the {\i pasuk} as follows: {\i l\u8217'achoso habetulah} \u8212- {\i prat l\u8217'anusa u\u8217'meputah u\u8217'mukat eitz} \u8212- the word {\i betulah} excludes a woman who was raped, seduced, or who suffered a {\i mukat eitz} (a rupture of the hymen through non-sexual means). This establishes both a physical and behavioral criterion. Then {\i asher lo haysah l\u8217'ish} \u8212- {\i prat l\u8217'arusa} \u8212- the phrase \u8220"who has not been with a man\u8221" is read not as a reference to sexual intercourse but as a reference to {\i kiddushin}: a woman who has been {\i mekudeshet} to a man has in a sense \u8220"been his,\u8221" even without physical relations. Therefore an {\i arusa} is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusha Divorced\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i hakerovah} \u8212- \u8220"who is close to him\u8221" \u8212- is read inclusively: {\i l\u8217'rabos arusa shenitgareshah}, to include a woman who was an {\i arusa} and was subsequently divorced. She is now close to him in the sense of being eligible, and since her prior {\i erusin} was not a physical act \u8212- {\i lo is\u8217'avid bah ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- she can be restored to the category. However, since {\i hakerovah} can only include one case \u8212- {\i krova achas v\u8217'lo shtayim} \u8212- the question arises: why include the divorced {\i arusa} rather than the {\i mukat eitz}? The Gemara answers that the {\i mukat eitz} involves something that physically happened to her \u8212- {\i is\u8217'avid bah ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- whereas the exclusion of the {\i arusa} was based entirely on her legal status, which can be reversed. The {\i mukat eitz}, by contrast, touches the definition of {\i betulah} itself, a category learned from the broader context of the {\i Kohen Gadol}, and one cannot extend the inclusion to encompass her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin vs Virgin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara raises a sharp observation: Rabbi Yosi has, by his silence on the {\i mukat eitz} question, parted ways with Rabbi Shimon. The {\i brayta} listed Rabbi Yosi and Rabbi Shimon together in holding that a {\i kohen} cannot become {\i tamei} for an {\i arusa}; but when Rabbi Shimon ruled that a {\i mukat eitz} is also excluded, Rabbi Yosi is nowhere to be found. The Gemara infers \u8212- {\i miklal d\u8217'mukat eitz Rabbi Meir sharei lei} \u8212- that Rabbi Yosi permits {\i tum\u8217'ah} for a sister who is a {\i mukat eitz}, aligned with Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. His textual basis is a split reading of the phrase {\i lo haysah l\u8217'ish}: {\i lo haysah} addresses the {\i kiddushin} dimension, excluding an {\i arusa}; {\i l\u8217'ish} addresses the sexual dimension, meaning that the definition of {\i betulah} here tracks whether she has had intercourse, not whether the hymen is anatomically intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alav Bogeres\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses the word {\i eilav}, which is read {\i l\u8217'rabos es habogeres} \u8212- to include a woman who has reached adulthood ({\i bogeret}) as one to whom the {\i kohen} may still become {\i tamei}. This is striking, since the word {\i betulah} on its own would seem to exclude a {\i bogeret}. The Gemara explains, in the name of Rabbi Shimon: {\i besulah shleima mashma} \u8212- the word {\i betulah} by itself connotes a fully intact state of {\i besulim}, even prior to any physical maturation. The very need for {\i eilav} to include a {\i bogeret} proves that without that inclusion, the word {\i betulah} alone would have excluded her. This, the Gemara notes, is also the source for Rabbi Shimon\u8217's general principle that {\i betulah} implies {\i besulah shleimah} \u8212- a completely intact state \u8212- which he applied in the context of the {\i Kohen Gadol} as well: {\i taamei didei nami hasam mehacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking in all of this is Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. In the context of the {\i Kohen Gadol}, Rabbi Meir defined {\i betulah} entirely anatomically \u8212- even a {\i mukat eitz} is a problem, even a {\i bogeret} is a problem, but {\i bi\u8217'ah shelo k\u8217'darkah} is not. Yet here, with respect to the {\i kohen}\u8217's obligation of {\i tum\u8217'ah} for a sister, Rabbi Meir reverses course entirely and defines {\i betulah} in terms of whether she has had intercourse, not in anatomical terms. The two definitions of {\i betulah} \u8212- anatomical integrity and never having had intercourse \u8212- coexist, and Rabbi Meir applies them selectively: anatomical by the {\i Kohen Gadol}, behavioral here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Giyoret Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RS Giyoret Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara transitions to a {\i brayta} presenting another ruling of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai, this one no longer about {\i tum\u8217'ah} or {\i Kohen Gadol} but about the permissibility of a female convert marrying a {\i kohen}. The baseline assumption the Gemara operates with, made explicit in the final {\i Mishnah} on 61a, is that a {\i giyoret} is categorized as a {\i zonah} and therefore prohibited to a {\i kohen hedyot}. The grounds for this categorization are a matter of significant debate among the {\i Rishonim} and {\i Tosafot}: one approach holds that we suspect a female convert of having had intercourse with a non-Jew prior to conversion, and since there can be no {\i kiddushin} with a non-Jew, such intercourse is defined as {\i bi\u8217'at znut}; a second approach holds that the legal category of {\i zonah} attaches to a non-Jewish woman by virtue of her identity, irrespective of actual conduct, on the assumption that non-Jews are not governed by the same sexual norms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this background, Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai rules: {\i giyoret pechutah mibat shalosh shanim v\u8217'yom echad kesheira l\u8217'kehunah} \u8212- a convert who converted when she was less than three years and one day old is permitted to marry a {\i kohen}. His source is the {\i pasuk} from {\i Midyan}: {\i v\u8217'chol hataf banashim asher lo yadu mishkav zachar hachayu lachem} \u8212- all the young girls who had not known lying with a male, keep alive for yourselves. The Gemara notes that Pinchas, a {\i kohen}, was present, and the formulation {\i lachem} implies eligibility to marry {\i kohanim} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yada Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna raises a difficulty. If the criterion is {\i asher lo yadu mishkav zachar} \u8212- not having known intercourse \u8212- then why should three years matter at all? If an older woman demonstrably had not had intercourse, she should be equally permitted. Conversely, the {\i pasuk} seems to draw a distinction between {\i ishah} (adult women) and {\i taf} (minors), suggesting age is the operative distinction. Rav Huna shows that the two {\i pesukim} in the {\i parsha} of Midyan create a contradiction: one implies the distinction is based on having had sex, the other implies it is based on age.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Capacity Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by re-reading {\i asher lo yadu mishkav zachar} not as a description of actual experience but of capacity: {\i b\u8217're\u8217'uya l\u8217'hibba\u8217'el hakatuv medabber} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} speaks of a woman who is fit for intercourse, who is capable of it. The {\i taf} are excluded not because they demonstrably have not had sex, but because they are not yet capable of it in the {\i halachic} sense \u8212- which is operationalized as being under three years of age. A {\i brayta} confirms: {\i v\u8217'chol ishah yoda\u8217'at ish b\u8217're\u8217'uya l\u8217'hibba\u8217'el hakatuv medabber} \u8212- the phrase \u8220"knowing a man\u8221" refers to capacity, not experience.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testing Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a practical question: how did the Israelites at {\i Midyan} actually determine which captive women were under three? Rav Huna bar Bizna citing Rabbi Shimon Chasida explains that they passed the women before the {\i tzitz} \u8212- the golden frontplate of the {\i Kohen Gadol}. Any woman whose face turned a sallow or bloated color was identified as fit for intercourse, i.e., over three years old. Rashi characterizes this as a {\i nes}. The Gemara also cites Rav Kahana\u8217's teaching about the women of {\i Yavesh Gilad}: there a different test was used, having each woman sit atop the opening of a wine barrel. A woman who had had intercourse \u8212- {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- would emit a discernible scent from the wine vapors; a {\i betulah} would not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavesh Gilad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question: why was the {\i tzitz} not used for the women of {\i Yavesh Gilad} as it was for Midyan? The answer offered by Rav Kahana son of Rav Natan is that the {\i tzitz} functions for {\i leratzon} \u8212- for favorable purposes \u8212- but not for {\i pur\u8217'anut}, punitive ones. In the case of {\i Yavesh Gilad}, those who failed the test would be killed, and using the {\i tzitz} to facilitate their deaths would be using a sacred instrument for a punitive purpose. Rav Ashi then draws a distinction: {\i lahem leratzon v\u8217'lo lepur\u8217'anut} \u8212- for {\i Bnei Yisrael} the {\i tzitz} operates only for {\i leratzon}; {\i ule\u8217'ovdei kokhavim afilu lepur\u8217'anut} \u8212- for non-Jews it can operate even for {\i pur\u8217'anut}. That is why it could be used at Midyan, where the non-Jewish women who failed the test were the ones subject to the consequence, but not at {\i Yavesh Gilad}, where the issue concerned the {\i Bnei Yisrael} women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yaakov bar Idi citing Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi rules: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai} \u8212- we rule like Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai that a {\i giyoret pechutah mibat shalosh shanim} is permitted to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira asked Rav Yaakov bar Idi whether he heard this ruling explicitly from Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi or whether he inferred it. The relevance of the question: Rav Zeira points to a case where Rebbe sent Rabbi Romanos to investigate a certain woman\u8217's status in a town in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. Rabbi Romanos found that the concern was that she was the daughter of a {\i giyoret} who had converted as a young child under three years. Rebbe ruled her permitted to remain married to her {\i kohen} husband. One might argue this was a leniency rooted solely in the fact that she was already married \u8212- analogous to the rule elsewhere ({\i bogeres u\u8217'mukat eitz lo yisa, im nasa nasa}) that though one should not marry certain women, if already married one need not divorce. If so, it would not constitute a ruling permitting marriage {\i lechatchila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this inference. Unlike the case of a {\i bogeres} or {\i mukat eitz}, whose status shifts over time, a {\i giyoret} would perpetually be in the category of {\i zonah} if her status were genuinely problematic \u8212- and we would never countenance a {\i kohen} remaining married to a {\i zonah}. Since Rebbe did permit it, the ruling must reflect a position that she is genuinely permitted, not merely tolerated after the fact. Rav Safra, who had learned the ruling as inferred rather than explicit, resolved the same difficulty in the same way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jewish Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kevrei Ovdei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues with additional rulings of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai concerning the legal status of non-Jews. The first: {\i kivrei ovdei kokhavim einam mitamim be\u8217'ohel} \u8212- the graves of non-Jews do not convey {\i tum\u8217'ah} in an {\i ohel}. The source: {\i ve\u8217'atem tzoni tzon mar\u8217'iti adam atem} \u8212- \u8220"you are my flock, you are {\i adam}\u8221" \u8212- which is read {\i atem keruyim adam}, you are called {\i adam}, implying that non-Jews are not called {\i adam} in the relevant {\i halachic} sense and are therefore not subject to the {\i pasuk} that creates {\i tum\u8217'at ohel} from a human corpse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metamei Bimaga\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina adds an important qualification: while the graves of non-Jews do not convey {\i tum\u8217'ah be\u8217'ohel}, non-Jews are nonetheless {\i metamei be\u8217'maga u\u8217've\u8217'masa} \u8212- through direct contact and carrying. This distinction has significant practical {\i halachic} implications: a {\i kohen} cannot dismiss {\i tum\u8217'ah} concerns entirely in contexts involving non-Jewish bodies, even if the {\i ohel} dimension is removed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adam Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling that non-Jews are not called {\i adam} in this context has generated considerable discussion, particularly given its juxtaposition with the preceding ruling about {\i giyoret pechutah mibat shalosh shanim}, where Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai was if anything more lenient toward non-Jews. The irony of the pairing is striking.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Giyoret Status (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RN Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this backdrop \u8212- and after the Gemara had recorded the ruling of Rav Yaakov bar Idi in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi that we rule like Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai \u8212- comes a sharp reversal. A certain {\i kohen} had married a {\i giyoret} who had converted when she was less than three years and one day old, citing that ruling. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak told him: {\i zil afik} \u8212- go and divorce her \u8212- and added pointedly that if he continued to invoke Rabbi Yaakov bar Idi, he would pull Rabbi Yaakov bar Idi out of his ear. The force with which Rav Nachman rejected the ruling suggests strongly that he did not regard it as settled {\i halacha}. The Gemara leaves the matter unresolved, but the {\i poskim} have understood this dramatic dismissal as a strong indication that the {\i halacha} does not follow Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai on this point, and there is accordingly a {\i machlokes Rishonim} as to whether a {\i giyoret pechutah mibat shalosh shanim} is in fact permitted to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jewish Status (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesukim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises two challenges to the principle that non-Jews are not called {\i adam}. First, in the narrative of {\i Midyan}, the {\i pasuk} refers to {\i nefesh adam} \u8212- sixteen thousand persons \u8212- and those are non-Jews. Second, the {\i pasuk} in Jonah regarding Nineveh states {\i adam asher lo yada bein yemino lismolo} \u8212- people who do not know their right from their left \u8212- and those too are non-Jews. The Gemara addresses the Midyan {\i pasuk} by arguing that {\i adam} there is used in contrast to {\i behemah}, the animals \u8212- the word is employed to distinguish humans from livestock, not to confer the {\i halachic} status of {\i adam}. The Nineveh {\i pasuk} is similarly addressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Nuance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} attempts to go beyond the case-by-case resolution offered by the Gemara and provide a more systematic account. One approach in {\i Tosafot} distinguishes between {\i adam} and {\i ha\u8217'adam} \u8212- with and without the definite article \u8212- suggesting that the form of the word carries different implications in different contexts, such that Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai\u8217's exclusion of non-Jews from the category of {\i adam} need not be read as a sweeping theological statement but as a technical, context-sensitive rule. This softens the categorical quality of his position and prevents his ruling from being read as a blanket denial of humanity to non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Meshullam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} records a further answer in the name of Rav Meshullam that is not widely known: {\i d\u8217'gabei pur\u8217'anut davka ka\u8217'amar atem keruyim adam} \u8212- the term {\i adam} is used specifically in contexts dealing with death and {\i pur\u8217'anut}, and in those contexts, when the {\i Torah} could have said {\i Yisrael} explicitly, it instead said {\i adam}, using {\i adam} as a code for {\i Yisrael}. The reason is literary decorum: the {\i Torah} did not want to write {\i Yisrael ki yamut b\u8217'ohel} \u8212- it avoided coupling the name {\i Yisrael} directly with death. Therefore it used {\i adam} in its place. But {\i aval gabei ha\u8217'adam v\u8217'chai} \u8212- in contexts of life and observance, such as {\i asher ya\u8217'aseh osam ha\u8217'adam vachai bahem}, the word {\i adam} is used in its ordinary sense and includes everyone. On this reading, Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai is not making any categorical statement about the ontological status of non-Jews; he is making a narrowly technical observation about the contexts in which the word {\i adam} serves as a term for {\i Yisrael} in particular.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 KG Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yikach Isha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the {\i Mishnah} addressing the case where a {\i kohen} performed {\i erusin} with an {\i almana} and was subsequently appointed {\i Kohen Gadol}: the ruling is {\i yichnos} \u8212- he may proceed to {\i nisuin}. The question addressed in the relevant {\i brayta} is the textual basis for this ruling. The {\i drasha} proceeds from the {\i pasuk} {\i uv\u8217'tulah me\u8217'amav yikach ishah} \u8212- the word {\i yikach} here is read as referring to {\i nisuin}, meaning that once she is already {\i ishto}, already his wife by virtue of the prior {\i erusin}, he may complete the marriage through {\i nisuin}. The case of Yehoshua ben Gamla is cited as a {\i ma\u8217'aseh} corroborating this principle: he had performed {\i kiddushin} with Marta bat Baitos and was subsequently appointed {\i Kohen Gadol} by the king, whereupon he completed the marriage with {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevama Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then distinguishes the case of a {\i shomeret yavam} who falls before a {\i kohen hedyot} who is subsequently appointed {\i Kohen Gadol}. Even if he performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- the rabbinic form of {\i kiddushin} available in {\i yibbum} \u8212- the ruling is {\i lo yichnos}. The reason: {\i ma\u8217'amar} is only rabbinic {\i kiddushin}, not a full {\i halachic} bond. The word {\i isha} in the {\i pasuk} \u8212- once she is already {\i his wife} \u8212- applies only when there is a genuine prior {\i erusin}; it does not apply to a {\i shomeret yavam} where the {\i zika} and {\i ma\u8217'amar} have not created a true {\i ishut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes a nuance in the language of the {\i Mishnah}. In the case of Yehoshua ben Gamla it says {\i uminahu hamelech} \u8212- the king appointed him. In the case of {\i shomeret yavam} it uses the passive {\i nitmane}. The Gemara reads this as significant: {\i mineihu in, nitmane lo} \u8212- where the king appointed him actively, there is a {\i ma\u8217'aseh}, a demonstrable appointment; where the language suggests something more like a general consensus or selection process, it may not suffice. Rav Yosef perceives something troubling in the Yehoshua ben Gamla story \u8212- {\i kesher}, a kind of arrangement \u8212- and Rav Assi confirms: Marta bat Baitos, the wealthy {\i almana} whom Yehoshua had married by {\i kiddushin}, sent a basket full of {\i dinarim} to Yannai the king until the king installed Yehoshua ben Gamla among the senior {\i kohanim} as {\i Kohen Gadol}. The royal appointment was purchased through her wealth, which is why the {\i Mishnah} says {\i uminahu hamelech} \u8212- it was a top-down, bribed appointment \u8212- not the result of the normal selection process.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 KG Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Choletz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i Kohen Gadol she\u8217'mes achiv choletz v\u8217'lo meyavem} \u8212- if a {\i Kohen Gadol}\u8217's brother dies, leaving a {\i yevama}, the {\i Kohen Gadol} performs {\i chalitzah} but does not perform {\i yibbum}. The Gemara notes: {\i pasik v\u8217'sanei lo shna min ha\u8217'erusin v\u8217'lo shna min ha\u8217'nisuim} \u8212- no distinction is drawn between whether the brother had contracted only {\i erusin} or full {\i nisuim} with the {\i yevama}. Both cases require {\i chalitzah} and prohibit {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aseh Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises the obvious question. In the case of {\i nisuim} \u8212- where the brother had completed the marriage \u8212- there is both a {\i lo sa\u8217'aseh} (the prohibition against an {\i almana} for the {\i Kohen Gadol}) and an {\i aseh} (the obligation of {\i betulah me\u8217'amav yikach ishah}). A {\i mitzvas aseh} cannot override a combined {\i lo sa\u8217'aseh} and {\i aseh}, so {\i yibbum} is clearly blocked. But in the case where there was only {\i erusin}, there is only a {\i lo sa\u8217'aseh} of {\i almana}; and ordinarily, a {\i mitzvas aseh} of {\i yibbum} can override a {\i lo sa\u8217'aseh}. Why then does the {\i Mishnah} prohibit {\i yibbum} even in that case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i gezeirah bi\u8217'ah rishonah atu bi\u8217'ah shniyah}. The {\i mitzvas aseh} of {\i yibbum} technically applies only to the first act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the act that accomplishes the {\i yibbum} itself. After that first act, there is no longer an {\i aseh} at play. The {\i Kohen Gadol} would then be required to divorce her, since the ongoing marriage would be forbidden. But we are concerned that once the first act occurs, they will not separate, and the subsequent acts of intercourse would take place without any {\i mitzvas aseh} to legitimize the {\i lo sa\u8217'aseh}. Therefore {\i chazal} decreed that even the first act \u8212- which technically could override the {\i lo sa\u8217'aseh} \u8212- may not be performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Bizayon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a further consideration. Given that the {\i Mishnah} states the {\i Kohen Gadol} must perform {\i chalitzah}, one might have thought that the honor of the {\i Kohen Gadol} would preclude such a proceeding \u8212- since {\i chalitzah} involves the woman spitting before him, which is a {\i bizayon}, a degradation. {\i Tosafot} notes that nonetheless the {\i Mishnah} requires it, since without {\i chalitzah} the {\i yevama} would remain permanently bound and unable to remarry.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ailonis Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 TK Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i kohen hedyot lo yisa ailonis ella im kein yesh lo ishah u\u8217'vanim} \u8212- a {\i kohen hedyot} may not marry an {\i ailonis} \u8212- a woman who is physically incapable of bearing children, characterized by certain masculine physical traits \u8212- unless he already has a wife or children, thereby having already fulfilled or having the means to fulfill {\i peru u\u8217'revu}. The {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's position is that the sole concern here is the {\i mitzva} of {\i peru u\u8217'revu}: there is nothing specifically about the {\i kehunah} that makes an {\i ailonis} prohibited; the problem is simply that a {\i kohen} should not foreclose his ability to fulfill the universal obligation of {\i peru u\u8217'revu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RY Zona\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda takes a fundamentally different position: {\i af al pi she\u8217'yesh lo ishah u\u8217'vanim lo yisa ailonis she\u8217'hi zonah ha\u8217'amurah ba\u8217'Torah} \u8212- even if he already has a wife and children, a {\i kohen} may not marry an {\i ailonis}, because she is a {\i zonah} in the sense employed by the {\i Torah}. On this view, the prohibition is not about {\i peru u\u8217'revu} at all; it is a {\i kehunah} prohibition, rooted in the definition of {\i zonah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achlu Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna derives Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's reasoning from the {\i pasuk}: {\i achlu v\u8217'lo yisba\u8217'u, hiznu v\u8217'lo yifrotzu} \u8212- they ate and were not satisfied, they fornicated and did not spread forth. The Gemara reads {\i hiznu v\u8217'lo yifrotzu} as teaching: any act of intercourse that does not carry with it the {\i pirtzah} \u8212- the potential to spread forth progeny \u8212- is in its essence an act of {\i bi\u8217'at znut}. Since an {\i ailonis} is categorically incapable of childbearing, intercourse with her is permanently severed from the procreative context that would otherwise legitimize it, and therefore it constitutes {\i bi\u8217'lat znut}, making her a {\i zonah} in the {\i Torah}\u8217's sense. This reflects a broader position that marital relations are legitimated not as an intrinsic value in themselves but because of the procreative framework that surrounds them \u8212- a position that stands in tension with the {\i Ramban}\u8217's reading of {\i v\u8217'hayu l\u8217'vasar echad}, which sees the union of husband and wife as possessing intrinsic value independent of procreation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chakhamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chakhamim} reject Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's expansive definition of {\i zonah} and restrict the category to three cases: {\i ein zonah ela giyoret u\u8217'meshuchreret v\u8217'sheniv\u8217'alah be\u8217'vi\u8217'at znut} \u8212- a {\i zonah} is only a female convert, a freed slave-woman, or a woman who has had a specific act of forbidden intercourse. Forbidden intercourse for this purpose means either an {\i issur kares} or a category of {\i bi\u8217'ah} that is prohibited specifically in the context of {\i kehunah} \u8212- such as that of a {\i gerushah} with a {\i kohen}, which may also render her a {\i challalah}. Critically, on the {\i Chakhamim}\u8217's view, an {\i ailonis} is not a {\i zonah}; she is neither a convert nor a freed slave nor one who has had forbidden intercourse. The prohibition against marrying her is therefore purely a {\i peru u\u8217'revu} concern, and once that is satisfied, there is no further impediment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RE Ketana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i brayta} teaches: {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer kohen lo yissa et hakketana} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer rules that a {\i kohen} may not marry a {\i ketana}, a minor girl. This appears puzzling at first: we have seen that the {\i Mishnah} contemplates a {\i Kohen Gadol} marrying a {\i ketana} in certain contexts. The question is what drives Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's prohibition for a {\i kohen hedyot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer, worked out by Rabba in response to Rav Chisda\u8217's directive to investigate before Rav Huna\u8217's inquiry: {\i Rabbi Eliezer savar lah k\u8217'Rabbi Meir v\u8217'savar lah k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer combines two distinct positions. He holds like Rabbi Meir, who is {\i chayeish l\u8217'miyuta} \u8212- who takes seriously even minority-case possibilities \u8212- and he holds like Rabbi Yehuda, who rules that an {\i ailonis} is a {\i zonah} and therefore prohibited to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonis Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Putting these two positions together: a small percentage of women who appear to be normal will turn out, upon reaching maturity, to be an {\i ailonis}. Rabbi Eliezer, following Rabbi Meir\u8217's general principle of {\i chiyush l\u8217'miyuta}, is concerned even about that small minority possibility. And following Rabbi Yehuda, he holds that an {\i ailonis} is prohibited to a {\i kohen} as a {\i zonah}, not merely because of {\i peru u\u8217'revu}. Therefore a {\i kohen} may not marry a {\i ketana} \u8212- because she might, in a small percentage of cases, prove to be an {\i ailonis}, which would render the marriage a prohibited union entirely. Notably, the Gemara does not suggest that the problem with a {\i ketana} has anything to do with {\i bi\u8217'at znut} on account of her being non-procreative; that framing is reserved for the {\i ailonis} herself. The concern here is purely the risk of a future {\i ailonis} status materializing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 61\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen & Ketana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit & Zonah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up at the top of 61b, having already introduced the position of Rabbi Yehuda that an {\i ailonit} \u8212- a woman who is not merely infertile but whose very physical development places her categorically outside the realm of childbearing \u8212- is forbidden to a {\i kohen} because she falls under the category of {\i zonah}. This flows from a particular perspective in which sexual relations entirely outside the theoretical framework of procreation constitute an act of {\i zenut}. That view is certainly not the only rabbinic attitude toward marital intimacy, but it is consequential for the discussions that follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then quoted the position of Rabbi Eliezer that a {\i kohen} cannot marry a {\i ketana} \u8212- a minor girl \u8212- and it now attempts to determine why. Significantly, the {\i gemara} does not suggest that the category of {\i zonah} is relevant here, nor that marrying a minor would somehow constitute {\i bi\u8217'at zenut} in any meaningful sense. Even within the broader theoretical context of procreation, the {\i gemara} does not even raise that possibility. So what, then, is the prohibition about?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Combination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first suggestion comes from Rabba, who proposes a combination of the positions of Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Yehuda holds that an {\i ailonit} is forbidden to a {\i kohen}; Rabbi Meir operates on the principle of {\i chaishinan lemiut} \u8212- we are concerned about minority likelihoods. Combining these two views, Rabba suggests: do not marry a {\i ketana} because she might grow up to become an {\i ailonit}. That is Rabba\u8217's proposal; now the {\i gemara} examines whether it holds up.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Rabba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this reconstruction: does Rabbi Eliezer really hold like Rabbi Meir? The challenge draws from a {\i baraita} in which Rabbi Meir himself rules that a minor boy and a minor girl \u8212- a {\i katan} and a {\i ketana} \u8212- cannot perform {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibbum}. When the Sages noted to Rabbi Meir that his position against {\i chalitzah} is well-founded \u8212- since the {\i parasha} uses the word {\i ish}, a man, and we derive that the woman must be an adult as well \u8212- they pressed him on why {\i yibbum} should also be prohibited. After all, {\i yibbum} does not require intent; it functions as a halakhic act of intercourse once performed, and since a girl above the age of three and a boy above the age of nine are considered to have performed such an act in a legally cognizable sense, why should the {\i mitzvah} not be fulfilled?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's answer is that a minor boy might later turn out to be a {\i saris} \u8212- a man with a fundamental physical condition that places him outside the normative category of procreation \u8212- and a minor girl might turn out to be an {\i ailonit}. If that were the case, there would be no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} to begin with, and what would have occurred retroactively would be a forbidden sexual act, since without the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} the woman remains forbidden as a brother\u8217's wife. This is precisely why Rabbi Meir\u8217's concern is formulated as {\i shema yimtza saris} and {\i shema timtza ailonit} rather than merely {\i shema yimtza akar} \u8212- there is something categorically significant about the {\i ailonit} and the {\i saris} beyond simple infertility, and that categorical status is what renders them outside the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now demonstrates that Rabbi Eliezer does not hold like Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Eliezer explicitly rules that a {\i ketana} may perform {\i yibbum}, even though she cannot perform {\i chalitzah} \u8212- since {\i chalitzah} requires adulthood as derived from the word {\i ish}. Rabbi Eliezer is manifestly unconcerned with the minority possibility that she might become an {\i ailonit}. This eliminates the first leg of Rabba\u8217's combination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then proceeds to show that Rabbi Eliezer also does not hold like Rabbi Yehuda, by quoting a {\i baraita} that presents a full range of {\i tanna\u8217'ic} positions on the definition of {\i zonah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zonah Definitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} records a fascinating and consequential dispute among the {\i Tannaim} about the biblical category of {\i zonah} that renders a woman forbidden to a {\i kohen}, conducted with almost no subsequent {\i gemara} analysis despite its weighty halakhic implications. The positions are as follows:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first opinion says simply: {\i zona kishmah} \u8212- a {\i zonah} as her name implies. This is the position of Rabbi Eliezer. Rashi explains that {\i zona kishmah} in Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's usage refers to a woman who committed adultery \u8212- she strayed from beneath her husband to others \u8212- and that for Rabbi Eliezer, the category of {\i zonah} is limited to a married woman who engaged in such an act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva says: {\i zona} refers to a {\i mufkeret} \u8212- a promiscuous woman who has indiscriminate relations, even if not for payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Matya ben Charash says: even a woman whose husband went to administer the {\i sotah} waters to her and a man had relations with her along the way \u8212- even that forbidden act within the very context of marriage renders her a {\i zonah}. This is a striking position: an isolated act of forbidden intercourse occurring within an otherwise completely legitimate marriage suffices to confer the status of {\i zonah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i zonah} refers to an {\i ailonit} \u8212- which is precisely the view we encountered in the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} say: {\i zonah} applies only to a female convert or a freed slave woman who had relations in the manner of {\i zenut}. This position is more limiting than that of Rabbi Matya ben Charash, since the {\i beilat zenut} in question would need to be an act forbidden under penalty of {\i kares} or an {\i issur kehunah}, and the concern extends either to having had relations with a non-Jew or to a more foundational view of how to regard the non-Jewish world. The implications for {\i kohanim} today \u8212- particularly regarding the question of a woman who has had relations with a non-Jew \u8212- are real and not infrequent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further opinion deserves attention: Rabbi Elazar says that any act of extramarital sex \u8212- even between two unmarried, unattached individuals, even in a committed relationship \u8212- renders the woman a {\i zonah}. He goes even further than Rabbi Akiva, who required promiscuity. Notably, a brief {\i Tosafot} on {\i panuy ha-ba al penuyah} adds that the prohibition applies {\i a fortiori} if the man is himself married, since then he is being {\i mezanneh al ishto} \u8212- unfaithful to his wife. What is fascinating here is that {\i Tosafot} is invoking a concept of marital fidelity as incumbent on both partners, even within a halakhic framework that historically permitted polygamy. This likely reflects the normative reality of Ashkenazic life after {\i Rabbeinu Gershom}, where monogamy had become the communal standard, and {\i Tosafot} appears to incorporate that sensibility into its reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the purposes of the {\i gemara}\u8217's question, the critical point is that Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- the one who prohibits a {\i kohen} from marrying a {\i ketana} \u8212- holds {\i zona kishmah}, which in Rashi\u8217's reading means a woman who committed adultery. This is emphatically not the category of {\i ailonit}. The combination Rabba attempted \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view that an {\i ailonit} is a {\i zonah} together with Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle of {\i chaishinan lemiut} \u8212- cannot be attributed to Rabbi Eliezer, since Rabbi Eliezer holds neither position. Both legs of the combination collapse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Alternative Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} must now find an alternative explanation for why Rabbi Eliezer prohibits a {\i kohen} from marrying a {\i ketana}. Rava proposes: perhaps the case concerns a {\i kohen gadol}. A {\i kohen gadol}, it is widely held, may not marry a {\i bogeret} \u8212- a fully mature woman past {\i na\u8217'arus}. Now consider the situation: if a {\i ketana} betroths herself to a {\i kohen gadol}, when will the marriage take legal effect? Only when she reaches adulthood. By that time, she will already be a {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- a non-virgin \u8212- because intercourse will have occurred between betrothal and the moment the {\i kiddushin} is legally binding. On this reasoning, the prohibition would not be unique to Rabbi Eliezer at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seduction Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava himself immediately dismisses this suggestion \u8212- calling it {\i mi-blei lev}, a thought offered without sufficient consideration. He notes: if the father betrothed her, the marriage takes effect immediately and there is no problem. If she betrothed herself, the difficulty that would arise \u8212- she would be a {\i be\u8217'ulah} at the moment the {\i kiddushin} becomes binding \u8212- would be a problem for every {\i posek}, not only Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore returns to the case of a {\i kohen hedyot} and offers a different rationale: we are concerned that she might be seduced by another man, rendering her forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband. One might object that this concern should apply equally to a non-{\i kohen} \u8212- why should any man marry a {\i ketana} if she is vulnerable to seduction? The answer is that {\i pittuy ketana ones hu} \u8212- seduction of a minor is legally equivalent to rape. Therefore, if a man has relations with a {\i ketana}, it is considered {\i ones}, non-volitional on her part, and she remains permitted to a non-{\i kohen} husband. But if she is married to a {\i kohen}, it makes no difference whether the act was volitional or not \u8212- she is forbidden to him regardless. This asymmetry is what makes the {\i ketana} specifically problematic for a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Naara Age Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa returns to the case of a {\i kohen gadol} and offers a different resolution, citing a specific {\i tanna}. The {\i baraita} reads: one might think a {\i kohen gadol} may marry a {\i ketana} \u8212- the {\i talmud lomar} teaches {\i isha}, a woman. And one might think he may marry a {\i bogeret} \u8212- the {\i talmud lomar} teaches {\i betula}. Since {\i betula} in this context means one whose {\i betulim} are fully intact \u8212- which is not the case with a {\i bogeret} \u8212- and {\i isha} excludes a {\i ketana}, the {\i kohen gadol} is restricted to the narrow window between the two: a girl who is {\i yatz\u8217'ah mi-klal ketana u-le-klal bagrut lo ba\u8217'at} \u8212- who has left the category of {\i ketana} but has not yet entered full maturity. That is precisely a {\i na\u8217'ara}, a girl between twelve and twelve-and-a-half years of age.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betula Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak adds another version of the same conclusion, citing a {\i tanna} who derives it differently: {\i ein betula ela na\u8217'ara} \u8212- {\i betula} means only a {\i na\u8217'ara}. The proof text is the verse describing Rivkah: {\i ve-ha-na\u8217'ara tovat mareh me\u8217'od betula ve-ish lo yeda\u8217'ah} \u8212- the two terms are presented together, indicating that they describe the same status. The conclusion is identical: a {\i kohen gadol} may only marry a {\i na\u8217'ara}, the intermediate stage. All of these are distinct answers to a single question, and the {\i sugya} presents them as alternative approaches rather than a single resolved position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halachic Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Amram Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the close of this {\i sugya}, the {\i gemara} records: {\i amar Rav Amram ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- we do not rule like Rabbi Elazar\u8217's position that any act of extramarital sex between two unmarried individuals renders the woman a {\i zonah} forbidden to a {\i kohen}. This ruling carries significant practical weight. Nowadays, when questions arise about whether a woman\u8217's prior sexual history disqualifies her from marrying a {\i kohen}, it is the position of the {\i Chachamim} \u8212- limiting {\i zonah} to a convert or freed slave who had relations in the manner of {\i zenut} \u8212- that governs, not Rabbi Elazar\u8217's sweeping definition. The ruling {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} ensures that we do not extend the prohibition to every case of premarital relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for whether an {\i ailonit} is forbidden to a {\i kohen}, since we rule like the {\i Chachamim} who restrict {\i zonah} to specific categories, the {\i ailonit} would not be included. The broader and more fraught questions \u8212- such as the {\i kohen} whose prospective wife has had relations with a non-Jew \u8212- belong to the category the {\i Chachamim} address, and those situations arise with some regularity in contemporary {\i psak}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pru U\u8217'Revu Basics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preceding discussion of an {\i ailonit} forbidden to a {\i kohen}, and the broader question of whether non-procreative marital sex constitutes {\i zenut}, forms a natural bridge to the {\i mishnah} that now opens on the primary {\i gemara} discussion of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. It is worth noting that the central {\i sugyot} on {\i pru u\u8217'revu} are found here in Yevamot rather than in Kiddushin or Kesuvos \u8212- which is fitting, since Yevamot addresses questions of personal status and the legal dimensions of sexual relations, and {\i pru u\u8217'revu} belongs naturally in that conversation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} frames the obligation not as \u8220"when have you fulfilled {\i pru u\u8217'revu}?\u8221" but rather as \u8220"do not cease from {\i pru u\u8217'revu} until you have children.\u8221" This framing is deliberate. Unlike taking a {\i lulav} and {\i esrog}, {\i pru u\u8217'revu} is not a single, discrete act of fulfillment. People may try for many years without success. Moreover, in cases such as a man whose wife of twenty years has died \u8212- the very scenario at the heart of {\i yibbum} \u8212- the framing of \u8220"do not stop\u8221" is apt: the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} remains incumbent upon him and should motivate remarriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai/Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents the dispute between {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} on the minimum required to fulfill {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. {\i Beit Shammai} says: two males. {\i Beit Hillel} says: a male and a female, as the verse states {\i zachar u-nekevah bera\u8217'am} \u8212- male and female He created them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel\u8217's Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i Beit Hillel} cites \u8212- {\i zachar u-nekevah bera\u8217'am} \u8212- is not itself the verse of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. Its invocation requires explanation. {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's reasoning appears to be twofold. First, there is the practical concern of continuing to populate the world: a son and a daughter leave behind both male and female contributors to the next generation. Second, and perhaps more profoundly, the verse gestures toward {\i imitatio Dei} \u8212- replicating the original divine act of creation by bringing forth male and female into the world. The {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} is thus not merely instrumental but participates in the ongoing work of creation inaugurated when God fashioned {\i adam} and {\i chavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical dimension of {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's position also deserves attention. Because the requirement is a boy {\i and} a girl, couples who have two children of the same sex must continue. This means that the actual average number of children produced under {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's standard will exceed two: roughly half of couples will stop at two \u8212- those who happen to have a boy and a girl \u8212- while the other half will continue until they achieve the required combination. The expected birth rate under {\i Beit Hillel} is therefore something in the range of two-and-a-half children per couple, not exactly two, which has its own demographic interest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also raises the question of what it means for a man who already has children to be {\i batel} \u8212- released \u8212- from {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, but who is still not released from the obligation to be married. This is derived from the statement of Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel: {\i af al pi she-yesh lo le-adam kama banim, assur la\u8217'amod belo isha} \u8212- even if a man has several children, he may not remain without a wife. The proof text is {\i lo tov heyot ha-adam levado} \u8212- it is not good for a person to be alone. Marriage is thus an intrinsic good, not merely an instrument for procreation. This is an important clarification: elsewhere the value of marriage is framed instrumentally, as a safeguard against transgression, but this {\i gemara} grounds it in the inherent worth of human companionship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers how the {\i mishnah} might be read in the opposite direction \u8212- that once {\i pru u\u8217'revu} is fulfilled, one is also released from any obligation to marry. The {\i gemara} resolves the tension: the {\i mishnah} means only that after fulfilling {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, one need not specifically marry a fertile woman, nor need one go so far as to sell a {\i Sefer Torah} to fund a marriage. But the intrinsic obligation to be married remains. The statement {\i batel mi-pru u\u8217'revu} does not mean {\i batel mi-isha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i mishnah}, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama de-Beit Shammai?} What is the reasoning of {\i Beit Shammai} for requiring two males? The answer is: {\i yalfina mi-Moshe} \u8212- we learn from Moshe Rabbeinu, who had two sons: Gershom and Eliezer. This is presented not as a logical argument about the purpose of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} but as a proof of its minimum threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately asks why {\i Beit Shammai} does not instead learn from the creation of the world \u8212- {\i beri\u8217'ato shel olam} \u8212- as {\i Beit Hillel} does, which would point toward a son and a daughter. And conversely, why does {\i Beit Hillel} not learn from Moshe? The {\i gemara} answers both questions together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} responds to the argument from creation: {\i ein danin mi-efshar mi-she\u8217'i efshar} \u8212- one cannot derive a rule from a case of necessity. The world could not have been populated unless Adam and Chava had both male and female children; that was a structural requirement of the beginning of creation, not a model for individual obligation. The fact that God commanded {\i pru u\u8217'revu} for the first pair necessarily implied both sexes, but that tells us nothing about what is required today when the world is already populated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel} responds to the argument from Moshe: {\i Moshe mi-da\u8217'atei hu de\u8217'avid} \u8212- Moshe acted on his own understanding, and his behavior is not a normative model. His case belongs to the three things Moshe decided on his own initiative and in which his judgment was confirmed by God \u8212- {\i ve-hiskima da\u8217'ato le-da\u8217'at ha-Makom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe\u8217's Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first of the three decisions attributed to Moshe\u8217's independent judgment is {\i perash min ha-isha} \u8212- he separated from his wife after the revelation at Sinai. The {\i gemara} reconstructs Moshe\u8217's reasoning: if the entire Jewish people, who received the divine communication only for a brief, defined moment \u8212- and were told not to approach a woman in preparation for it \u8212- how much more so does Moshe, who communes with God at any time without advance notice, need to remain in a constant state of readiness? Moshe could never know when God would speak to him, and therefore he could not maintain a marital relationship. The confirmation that God approved this decision comes from the verse: {\i lech emor lahem shuvu lachem le-oholeichem} \u8212- go tell them to return to their tents \u8212- followed immediately by {\i ve-ata po amod imadi} \u8212- but you, stand here with Me. The instruction for Israel to return to their wives is paired with an instruction for Moshe to remain permanently in divine attendance. This is read not as a temporary arrangement but as an ongoing existential state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Broken Tablets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second decision is {\i shiber ha-luchot} \u8212- Moshe broke the tablets. The {\i gemara} reconstructs his reasoning: if {\i Pesach}, which is only one of 613 {\i mitzvot}, excludes one who has become {\i mechuyyav} \u8212- estranged from God through his deeds \u8212- from participation, then how much more so should a people who have become {\i mumarim} in some sense be excluded from receiving the entire Torah in its totality? On this reasoning, Moshe concluded that presenting the tablets to Israel in its current state would be wrong, and he broke them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The divine confirmation is the verse: {\i asher shibarta} \u8212- which you broke \u8212- and Reish Lakish reads this with a characteristic wordplay: {\i yishar kochacha asher shibarta} \u8212- well done, {\i shkoyach}, that you broke them. The phrase {\i asher}, understood here almost in the sense of {\i ashrei}, becomes an expression of divine endorsement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further point arises: the underlying {\i kal va-homer} from {\i Pesach} is itself noted as imperfect \u8212- {\i Tosafot} observes that {\i ein zeh kal va-chomer gamur}, since {\i Pesach} involves a {\i korban kodashim} and the {\i korban} dimension may make it more exclusive, whereas the entire Torah has a dimension of outreach and {\i teshuvah}. Nevertheless, this was Moshe\u8217's reasoning, and it was confirmed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extra Day\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third decision is {\i hosif yom echad mi-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- Moshe added one extra day of separation before the revelation at Sinai. The Torah instructed: {\i ve-kidashtam ha-yom u-machar} \u8212- prepare them today and tomorrow. Moshe calculated that {\i machar leilo imo} \u8212- tomorrow includes the night before it. If the command was issued at some point during the day, then \u8220"today\u8221" had already partially elapsed; to count two full days starting from the night, Moshe had to push the beginning of the period back. The math therefore required beginning the count from the previous evening, adding what amounted to a third day. The divine confirmation is derived from the same structural reading: God\u8217's intent corresponded to Moshe\u8217's conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's point in presenting all three cases is to establish that Moshe\u8217's behavior \u8212- including his having had only two sons and apparently not having more children \u8212- cannot serve as a normative halakhic model. {\i Moshe mi-da\u8217'atei hu de\u8217'avid ve-hiskima da\u8217'ato le-da\u8217'at ha-Makom}: he acted uniquely, under unique circumstances, and his judgment was uniquely confirmed. {\i Beit Hillel} is therefore correct not to derive the minimum of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} from Moshe\u8217's family.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Variants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Males/Females\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After establishing the basic positions of {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel}, the {\i gemara} presents alternative versions of their dispute recorded by Rabbi Natan. The first {\i baraita} has Rabbi Natan reporting that {\i Beit Shammai} requires two males and two females. This version essentially combines both the {\i imitatio dei} logic \u8212- one of each sex \u8212- and the {\i pru/revu} structure, perhaps reading {\i pru} as generating one pair and {\i revu} as doubling it to two pairs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina asks: what is the reasoning of Rabbi Natan according to {\i Beit Shammai}? The proof is from the verse describing Chava\u8217's births: {\i va-tosef laledet et achiv et Hevel} \u8212- and she continued to give birth to his brother Hevel. The word {\i et} is understood as indicating a sister who was born alongside each male child \u8212- Kayin was born with a twin sister, and Hevel with a twin sister. Subsequently, when Kayin killed Hevel, Chava had Shet \u8212- with the verse explaining: {\i ki shat li Elohim zera acher tachat Hevel ki harago Kayin} \u8212- God has given me another seed in place of Hevel, whom Kayin slew. This implies that by Hevel\u8217's death, the fulfillment of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} was incomplete, requiring replacement. The {\i Hagahot ha-Bach} reads this as indicating that {\i pru u\u8217'revu} requires two males and two females, since Hevel\u8217's death left a deficit that Shet came to fill. The Rabbis, however, reject this derivation: Chava\u8217's words were simply an expression of gratitude \u8212- {\i hoda\u8217'aya de-ka moda} \u8212- not a statement about the requirements of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male & Female\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i baraita} attributed to Rabbi Natan presents an even more striking version: here, {\i Beit Shammai} requires a male and a female \u8212- what we had previously understood to be the {\i Beit Hillel} position \u8212- while {\i Beit Hillel} requires only one child of either sex. Rava explains {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's position in this version from the verse in Yeshayahu: {\i lo tohu bera\u8217'ah la-shevet yetzarah} \u8212- God did not create the world to be desolate but to settle it. Even contributing one additional person to the settled world is a fulfillment of {\i la-shevet}. This is a notably minimalist reading of the {\i mitzvah}, and it places the entire weight of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} on the concept of {\i yishuv ha-olam} \u8212- settling the world \u8212- rather than on any particular numerical threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is striking that across these {\i baraitot}, the rationale of {\i la-shevet yetzarah} plays a decisive role not only in determining the minimum number of children required, but also, as we will see, in shaping the question of what happens when those children predecease the parent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jews & Converts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Convert\u8217's Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} next addresses a case that sits at the intersection of the legal question of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} and the broader question of a convert\u8217's legal continuity: what of a person who had children as a non-Jew and then converted? He has biological children who were legally recognized as his children under the laws governing non-Jews. Does this count toward the fulfillment of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two considerations are in tension. On one hand, as a non-Jew he did not bear the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} in the same sense as a Jew. On the other hand, as is well established, {\i ger she-nitgayer ke-katan she-nolad dami} \u8212- a convert is like a newborn child \u8212- which means his prior legal relationships may no longer be recognized after conversion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conversion Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says: he has fulfilled {\i pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- {\i kiyam pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- because he has children. Reish Lakish says: he has not \u8212- {\i lo kiyam pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- because as a convert he is like a newborn, and those children are no longer legally considered his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that this dispute tracks a parallel one: the same two {\i amora\u8217'im} dispute whether the first child born to this man after his conversion has the status of {\i bechor} for purposes of inheritance. Rabbi Yochanan says the first post-conversion child is not a {\i bechor le-nachala}, because the man\u8217's biological firstborn preceded the conversion and is considered {\i reishit ono} \u8212- the first of his strength \u8212- in a biological sense. Reish Lakish says the post-conversion child is a {\i bechor}, because the convert starts fresh. The {\i gemara} notes that both disputes are necessary, as the reasoning in each case might not entail the conclusion in the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance/Bechor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} spells out why both cases must be stated explicitly. With respect to {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, one might have argued that Rabbi Yochanan acknowledges the biological children because even as non-Jews, those individuals were {\i bnei pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- they were born within a normative framework where {\i pru u\u8217'revu} applies to all of humanity. The verse supports this: God commanded {\i pru u\u8217'revu} first to Adam and Chava, and then again to Noach, making it a universal obligation. This is not a major {\i chiddush} \u8212- it is the plain sense of the Torah\u8217's universal address. Nevertheless, the question of whether non-Jews are formally {\i metzuveh} in {\i pru u\u8217'revu} is debated among {\i rishonim}, and its practical implications are real: is it a {\i mitzvah} to arrange a {\i shidduch} between two non-Jewish individuals as part of {\i milu et ha-aretz}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the inheritance case, by contrast, one might have thought that Rabbi Yochanan would concede to Reish Lakish, since inheritance operates according to strictly halakhic categories of legal parentage rather than the broad biological framework that underlies {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. And correspondingly, one might have thought that in the {\i pru u\u8217'revu} case, Reish Lakish might concede to Rabbi Yochanan. Since each case has a distinct internal logic, both disputes must be stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi confirms: {\i bnei Noach bnei pru u\u8217'revu ninhu}, derived from the verse {\i va-yomer lahem pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- the universal command establishes that all of humanity is included in the {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records a direct exchange between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish. Rabbi Yochanan cites the verse describing the Babylonian king as {\i Ben Hadad ben Tavrimon ben Hezyon} \u8212- a chain of paternal lineage across generations \u8212- demonstrating that non-Jews legally recognize paternal descent. Reish Lakish concedes this point for non-Jews who remain non-Jews, but maintains that upon conversion, {\i leit lehu chayyus} \u8212- the legal parentage is dissolved. One might ask why the establishment of legal parentage in a non-Jewish framework should dissolve upon conversion if it was genuine legal recognition while it existed. Reish Lakish\u8217's position is that the convert\u8217's new legal identity as a Jew is so thoroughgoing that prior legal relationships are entirely superseded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves vs.\u160 ?Non-Jews\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ami states a point on which all agree: with respect to a slave, even the initial legal recognition of parentage was absent. A slave\u8217's children are not legally his children in any halakhically recognized sense, and therefore even Rabbi Yochanan would concede that a freed slave who had children during his enslavement cannot fulfill {\i pru u\u8217'revu} through those children. The verse {\i shvu lachem po im ha-chamor} \u8212- remain here with the donkey \u8212- is read as {\i im ha-domeh la-chamor}, those who are similar to donkeys, understood as a reference to a slave among those accompanying Avraham to the {\i akeida}, illustrating that a slave lacks the legal standing of a full person in this regard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if non-Jewish parentage is merely biological, why do we assume it is legal? The answer is that the biblical text explicitly traces paternal descent \u8212- {\i ben so-and-so ben so-and-so} \u8212- in the case of non-Jews, while no such genealogical recognition appears in contexts involving slaves. Moreover, in some cases non-Jewish parentage is traced across multiple generations \u8212- grandfather and great-grandfather \u8212- indicating not merely biological reproduction but a recognized family structure. This distinguishes the non-Jewish case from the slave case and supports the initial recognition of legal parentage for non-Jews, even if that recognition dissolves upon conversion according to Reish Lakish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Children\u8217's Longevity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death of Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final question the {\i gemara} raises on this daf is whether {\i pru u\u8217'revu} is fulfilled if a person\u8217's children predecease him. This question connects to the earlier material about Chava\u8217's having Shet in place of Hevel \u8212- which the {\i gemara} initially read as suggesting that the death of a child can undo the fulfillment of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- and it also connects to the purpose-driven analysis of {\i la-shevet yetzarah}. The case is stated clearly: {\i hayu lo banim u-mesu} \u8212- he had children and they died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules: {\i m\u8217'kayem pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- he has fulfilled {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. The children were born; the biological event of birth suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Messiah Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna grounds his position in the view of Rabbi Assi, who states: {\i ein ben David ba ad she-yichlu kol ha-neshamot she-ba-guf} \u8212- the son of David, the {\i Mashiach}, will not come until all the souls stored in the heavenly repository known as {\i guf} have been brought into the world. On this understanding, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} functions as the mechanism for drawing down souls and thereby hastening the coming of {\i Mashiach}. Each birth depletes the store; each soul that enters the world brings redemption closer. If this is the purpose of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, then even if a child dies, the soul has been brought into the world and the goal has been advanced. The birth itself is the {\i kiyum}, regardless of how long the child lives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Living Settlement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disagrees: {\i lo kiyam pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- he has not fulfilled the {\i mitzvah}. His reasoning draws again on {\i la-shevet yetzarah}: the purpose of procreation is to settle the world, and settling the world requires living people who actually contribute to it. A child who has died is no longer settling the world. The fulfillment of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} thus requires not only that the children be born, but that they survive and inhabit the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a further question about the practical scope of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position. If a man has children and they live for decades \u8212- and then, at the age of, say, seventy, predecease their ninety-year-old father \u8212- does he retroactively lose his {\i kiyum}? The {\i gemara} does not fully resolve this, but the underlying principle of {\i la-shevet} suggests that there is a threshold of lived contribution to the world, not merely the fact of birth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raising Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position, that {\i la-shevet} requires living children who actually inhabit and contribute to the world, opens a broader conceptual window. If {\i pru u\u8217'revu} is not simply about the biological event of birth but about bringing people into the world who then settle and sustain it, then the {\i mitzvah} encompasses not just the having of children but their raising. This understanding carries significant halakhic implications for couples who face infertility. If part of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} is raising children rather than merely birthing them, adoption may constitute at least a partial {\i kiyum} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. Some {\i poskim} have indeed understood it this way \u8212- that bringing children to adulthood and contributing to their formation as people who will settle the world is itself a dimension of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, not merely a worthy act adjacent to it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 61b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aylonit & Zonah (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aylonit Zonah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We open at the top of 61b with the position of Rabbi Yehuda, who rules that an {\i aylonit} \u8212- a woman who is not merely infertile but whose very physical development places her categorically outside the realm of childbearing \u8212- is forbidden to a {\i kohen} on the grounds that she falls into the category of {\i zonah}. The underlying principle is that sexual relations in a context entirely removed from procreation, even in the broadest theoretical sense, constitutes an act of {\i zenut}. This is a significant and particular rabbinic attitude; it is certainly not the only such attitude toward marital sex, and it will bear heavily on the discussions that follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling that a {\i kohen} may not marry a {\i ketanah}. Notably, the {\i gemara} does not suggest that a {\i ketanah} is herself a {\i zonah}, nor does it characterize relations with her as {\i bi\u8217'as zenut} \u8212- presumably because the larger context of procreation remains theoretically within the picture. So the prohibition cannot rest on the same logic Rabbi Yehuda applied to an {\i aylonit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketanah Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba: Minority Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba offers the first explanation: the prohibition is constructed from a combination of two Tannaitic positions \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling that an {\i aylonit} is forbidden to a {\i kohen}, and Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle of {\i chayesh l\u8217'miyuta}, concern for minority likelihoods. Put together: a {\i kohen} should not marry a {\i ketanah} because she might grow up to become an {\i aylonit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aylonit & Zonah (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer & Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this construction. Does Rabbi Eliezer actually hold like Rabbi Meir? The challenge comes from a {\i baraita}: {\i katan v\u8217'ketanah lo choltzin v\u8217'lo meyabmin divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- Rabbi Meir rules that a minor, whether boy or girl, cannot perform {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}. When challenged on the {\i yibum} ruling \u8212- since {\i yibum} does not require intent and the {\i gemara} has established that relations with a girl over three years old and a boy over nine years old constitute a halachic act of intercourse \u8212- Rabbi Meir responds: {\i katan shema yimtza saris}, perhaps the minor will turn out to be a {\i saris}, and {\i ketanah shema timtza aylonit}, perhaps the girl will turn out to be an {\i aylonit}. In such a case, if either party is categorically excluded from the realm of procreation, there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} \u8212- and without a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, the act retroactively becomes a forbidden relation with a sister-in-law, an {\i isur} of {\i arayos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing here to note what Rabbi Meir does and does not say. He does not invoke the worry {\i shema timtza akarah} \u8212- perhaps she will turn out to be simply infertile. The concern is specifically the categorical status of {\i aylonit} and {\i saris}, which implies something more fundamental than merely practical inability to conceive. This connects directly to why, in the earlier discussion, an {\i aylonit} is considered a {\i zonah}: the exclusion is categorical and essential, not merely contingent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now adduces a further {\i baraita} to show that Rabbi Elazar (the authority whose prohibition of a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i ketanah} we are trying to explain) does not hold like Rabbi Meir: {\i ketanah me\u8217'ana bo be-emet ve-eina choletzet divrei Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar holds that a {\i ketanah} can perform {\i yibum} but not {\i chalitzah}, the latter being excluded because the Torah specifies {\i ish}, an adult male, and the woman is {\i hukshah} to him. Rabbi Elazar\u8217's willingness to allow {\i yibum} by a {\i ketanah} demonstrates that he is not concerned with the minority possibility of her becoming an {\i aylonit}. This disposes of Rabba\u8217's construction at the first step: Rabbi Elazar clearly does not hold like Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then continues to show that Rabbi Elazar also does not hold like Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- which brings us to the {\i baraita} cataloguing the debate over the definition of {\i zonah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definitions of Zonah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} presents a striking range of Tannaitic opinions on the biblical category of {\i zonah} as it applies to a {\i kohen}, with almost no {\i gemara} analysis to sort through them \u8212- and yet with enormous practical implications for {\i kohanim} today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first opinion: {\i zona zona k\u8217'shma} \u8212- \u8220"a {\i zonah} is as her name implies\u8221" \u8212- stated by Rabbi Eliezer. The second: {\i zona zu mufkeret}, said by Rabbi Akiva \u8212- a {\i zonah} is a woman who has sex indiscriminately, not necessarily for pay but promiscuously. Rabbi Matya ben Charash says: even if a husband is on his way to administer the {\i sotah} waters to his wife and another man has relations with her along the way \u8212- even this act of forbidden sex, occurring within the marital context and technically prohibited only on account of {\i safek} (suspicion), renders her a {\i zonah}. Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i zona zu aylonit} \u8212- as we saw in the {\i mishnah}. And the Sages say: {\i ein zona ela giyoret u\u8217'meshuchreret v\u8217'shenib\u8217'ala beilat zenut} \u8212- a {\i zonah} is only a convert, a freed slave, or a woman who has had a forbidden sexual encounter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Zonah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's comment on the phrase {\i zona zona k\u8217'shma} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position \u8212- is quite unexpected. Rather than reading it as a reference to a prostitute, Rashi explains: {\i lashon to\u8217'eh she-to\u8217'eh mi-tachat ba\u8217'alah la-acherim de-le-Rabbi Eliezer ein zona ela eshet ish} \u8212- a {\i zonah} is a woman who has strayed from her husband to other men; according to Rabbi Eliezer, {\i zona} means specifically a married woman who committed adultery. This is a strikingly narrow definition and not the reading one would naturally assume from the phrase {\i zona k\u8217'shma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages\u8217' category of {\i beilat zenut} is narrower than Rabbi Matya ben Charash\u8217's formulation in one respect: while Rabbi Matya includes violations that are prohibited only rabbinicly or by reason of doubt, the Sages appear to require either an {\i isur kares} or an {\i isur kehunah}-level violation. (Though one might note that if she has already violated an {\i isur kehunah}, the {\i kohen} question is moot, so that prong may better be understood as pertaining to {\i chalalah}.) The {\i giyoret u\u8217'meshuchreret} category reflects concerns either about sexual history with non-Jews or a more fundamental perspective on the non-Jewish world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer: Non-Marital Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning now to Rabbi Elazar\u8217's own position in the {\i baraita}: {\i panui haba al pnuyah shelo leshem ishut as\u8217'ah zona} \u8212- any act of extra-marital intercourse, even between two unattached individuals and even in the context of a committed relationship, renders the woman a {\i zonah} forbidden to a {\i kohen}. This is in one sense even more expansive than Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i mufkeret}, which requires promiscuity; Rabbi Elazar requires only that the sex occur outside of marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is important for resolving the {\i gemara}\u8217's investigation. Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- whose position we are trying to explain \u8212- states {\i zona zona k\u8217'shma}, which, as Rashi reads it, means a woman who committed adultery. That definition does not encompass an {\i aylonit} at all. We have now established both prongs: Rabbi Elazar does not hold like Rabbi Meir (from the {\i yibum} case), and Rabbi Eliezer does not hold like Rabbi Yehuda (from the definition of {\i zonah}). Rabba\u8217's combined construction is therefore doubly disproven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Cheating Znut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} adds a pointed observation on Rabbi Elazar\u8217's formulation of {\i panui haba al pnuyah}. The {\i Tosafot} notes: {\i lav davka nakat panui v\u8217'chol shekein nasui} \u8212- the word {\i panui} (single man) is not precise; the ruling applies all the more forcefully if the man is married. The reason: {\i shemizanneh al ishto d\u8217'chashuva zona} \u8212- because he is being unfaithful to his wife, which makes the act of {\i zenut} even more severe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking here is that {\i Tosafot} introduces the moral concept of marital fidelity as a category that intensifies the character of the forbidden act \u8212- and this in a halachic framework that, in principle, did not prohibit polygamy and in which the prohibitions on sexual relations were asymmetrical between husband and wife. The notion that a man is {\i mizanneh al ishto} by having sex with another woman almost certainly reflects the reality of {\i Ashkenaz} after {\i Cherem de-Rabbeinu Gershom} and the internalization of monogamous norms, such that extramarital sex by a married man is characterized as a betrayal of his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketanah Prohibition (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having exhausted Rabba\u8217's construction, the {\i gemara} turns to a second explanation. Rav Adda bar Ahava suggests: {\i hacha b\u8217'kohen gadol askinan} \u8212- we are dealing with a {\i kohen gadol}. A {\i kohen gadol} may not marry a {\i bogeret} (a fully mature woman past {\i na\u8217'arut}). The concern is the timing of {\i kiddushin}: if a {\i ketanah} were to arrange her own marriage to a {\i kohen gadol} (without her father\u8217's involvement), {\i kiddushin} would not take legal effect until she reaches adulthood \u8212- at which point she is already a {\i be\u8217'ulah} (no longer a virgin), disqualifying her for a {\i kohen gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fituy & Ones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejects this immediately: {\i michlei lev} \u8212- this was said without thinking. If her father arranged the marriage, there is no issue at all; and if she arranged it herself, that would be a universal problem, not specific to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling. Rava then offers his own explanation: {\i l\u8217'olam b\u8217'kohen hedyot askinan} \u8212- we return to an ordinary {\i kohen}. The concern is {\i de-chayshinan shema tispateh alav} \u8212- we worry that she will be seduced by another man, thus rendering her an {\i eshet ish} who committed adultery, making her a {\i zonah} and therefore forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects: if so, why shouldn\u8217't even a non-{\i kohen} avoid marrying a {\i ketanah}? The answer is: {\i pituyei ketanah ones hu} \u8212- seduction of a minor is legally equivalent to rape ({\i ones}), non-volitional. And {\i ones b\u8217'Yisrael mishra shari} \u8212- if the wife of a non-{\i kohen} is raped, she remains permitted to her husband. But if she is the wife of a {\i kohen}, it makes no difference whether the intercourse was voluntary or coerced; she becomes forbidden to him. Hence the prohibition applies specifically to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Na\u8217'arah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa returns to the {\i kohen gadol} explanation but with a different source. He cites a {\i baraita} that derives from the verse {\i ve-hu ishah be-visuleha} that a {\i kohen gadol} must marry a {\i be-sulah} who is an {\i ishah} \u8212- an adult. {\i Yachol ketanah?} Could she be a minor? No \u8212- {\i talmud lomar ishah}. {\i Yachol bogeret?} Could she be fully mature? No \u8212- {\i talmud lomar be-sulah}, which implies intact {\i betulim}, characteristic of a {\i na\u8217'arah} rather than a {\i bogeret}. The result: {\i ha ketzad? yatza mikhlal katnut ve-lo ba le-khlal bagrus} \u8212- she must be in the intermediate stage, neither {\i ketanah} nor {\i bogeret}, which is a {\i na\u8217'arah} between twelve and twelve and a half years of age. According to this Tanna, the {\i kohen gadol} may only marry a woman during that narrow window.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aylonit & Zonah (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruling Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugiyah} concludes with Rav Amram\u8217's ruling: {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- we do not rule like Rabbi Elazar\u8217's position that a woman who has had any non-marital sexual encounter is a {\i zonah} forbidden to a {\i kohen}. This is practically significant: questions about whether a {\i kohen} may marry a woman who has had premarital relations do arise, and Rabbi Elazar\u8217's formulation would forbid such a marriage categorically. We do not rule that way. We rule, rather, like the {\i chachamim} that {\i zona} means only a {\i giyoret}, a freed slave, or a woman who has engaged in {\i beilat zenut} in the more restricted sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether an {\i aylonit} remains forbidden to a {\i kohen} as a {\i zona} is left somewhat open: since we rule like the {\i chachamim} and not like Rabbi Yehuda, and an {\i aylonit} does not appear in the {\i chachamim}\u8217's definition, she would presumably not be prohibited on grounds of {\i zona}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This entire discussion \u8212- the definition of {\i zenut}, sex in a non-procreative context, and the {\i aylonit} \u8212- serves as a natural bridge into the {\i mishnah} before us, which takes up the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru urvu} in earnest. It is worth noting that the primary {\i gemara} on {\i pru urvu} is found here in Yevamot rather than in Kiddushin or Ketubot, which makes a certain sense: Yevamot deals extensively with personal status and with the legal dimensions of sexual relations, and it is within that frame that the {\i mitzvah} of having children is most fully analyzed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pru Urvu Basics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligation & Cessation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i lo yivatel adam mi-piryah ve-rivyah} \u8212- a man must not cease from the {\i mitzvah} of having children {\i ela im kein yesh lo banim} \u8212- unless he already has children. The phrasing is notable. Rather than stating the threshold at which one has {\i yatza} the {\i mitzvah}, the {\i mishnah} frames it as a prohibition on stopping \u8212- which captures something important. {\i Pru urvu} is not a discrete act like taking a {\i lulav}; it is an ongoing obligation that some people pursue for years without success. The {\i mishnah} may therefore be addressing the phase of continued effort: do not abandon the endeavor until you have fulfilled the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How many children constitute fulfillment? {\i Beit Shammai omrim shnei zecharim} \u8212- two males. {\i Beit Hillel omrim zachar u-nekeva} \u8212- a male and a female, {\i she-ne\u8217'emar zachar u-nekeva bera\u8217'am} \u8212- as it is said, \u8220"male and female He created them.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel: Imitatio Dei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel\u8217's reasoning, grounded in the verse {\i zachar u-nekeva bera\u8217'am}, points to two distinct rationales simultaneously. On a straightforward level, having one son and one daughter ensures that the world continues to be populated \u8212- each child represents a gender necessary for future generations. But the citation of the verse from creation suggests something deeper: the fulfillment of {\i pru urvu} is not only about achieving a demographic goal but about replicating the original divine act of creation. To bring a male and a female into the world is, in some sense, an act of {\i imitatio dei}, a participation in the creative act by which God populated the world with {\i zachar u-nekeva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai, by requiring two males, clearly has a different conception of the {\i mitzvah}\u8217's purpose. One might ask: doesn\u8217't requiring only sons skew the population? In practice, the birth rate implied by Beit Hillel\u8217's ruling is also above two per couple, since those who have two daughters or two sons must continue. But Beit Shammai appears unconcerned with population balance, suggesting their conception of the {\i mitzvah} is not fundamentally about the continued settlement of the world in the way Beit Hillel understands it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Intrinsic Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Adda: Must Stay Marr\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} infers from the {\i mishnah} that once a man has children \u8212- {\i batel mi-pru urvu} \u8212- his obligation regarding {\i pru urvu} is fulfilled; however, {\i mi-ishah lo batel} \u8212- he does not cease his obligation to remain married. This inference is said to support the position of Rav Nachman bar Shmuel: {\i af al pi she-yesh lo le-adam kama banim, assur la\u8217'amod belo ishah} \u8212- even a man who already has several children is forbidden to remain without a wife if his wife dies or they divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Tov Levado\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this obligation is the verse {\i lo tov heyot ha-adam levado} \u8212- \u8220"it is not good for man to be alone.\u8221" This framing is important. One might argue that a man should remarry simply to manage his own drives and avoid transgression. But Rav Adda\u8217's formulation grounds the obligation to be married not in an instrumental concern but in an intrinsic one: the state of marriage is a human good in itself, independent of its procreative function. This is a significant {\i gemara} for thinking about marriage and companionship as values in their own right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then offers an alternative reading of the {\i mishnah}: {\i ika de-amri}, some say the {\i mishnah} implies both that the obligation regarding {\i pru urvu} ceases {\i and} that the obligation to remain married ceases \u8212- {\i batel mi-pru urvu u-vatel min ha-ishah}. Under this reading, the {\i mishnah} would be a challenge to Rav Nachman bar Shmuel\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic underlying this alternative reading rests on the practical reality of a society without effective contraception. How does a married man stop having children? The only plausible mechanism is by not being married. So the {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement that one may stop once the {\i mitzvah} is fulfilled is implicitly a statement about not having to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Infertile Partner OK\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this challenge: {\i lo} \u8212- it is not a contradiction. The distinction is as follows. A man who has not yet fulfilled {\i pru urvu} must marry a woman capable of bearing children. Once he has fulfilled {\i pru urvu}, the {\i mishnah} is telling him that he need not restrict his choice of wife to a fertile woman \u8212- {\i nosei ishah delav bat banim} \u8212- he may marry a woman without fertility potential. He must still marry, but the fertility requirement no longer constrains his choice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} adds a further {\i nafka mina}: the {\i gemara} elsewhere teaches that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru urvu} is so weighty that one would even sell a {\i sefer Torah} to obtain the funds necessary to marry and fulfill it. That extreme measure applies only before one has fulfilled the {\i mitzvah}. After {\i yatza hamitzvah}, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's declaration that one is {\i batel mi-piryah ve-rivyah} means he need not take such measures \u8212- but it does not mean he is exempt from marriage altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pru Urvu Basics (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the debate between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama de-Beit Shammai?} \u8212- What is Beit Shammai\u8217's reasoning? {\i Yalfina mi-Moshe} \u8212- they derive it from Moshe, who had two sons, Gershom and Eliezer. Presumably Moshe would not have stopped had he not been {\i yatza} the {\i mitzvah}, and since he stopped with two boys, the fulfillment requires two males.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why does Beit Shammai not learn from creation \u8212- {\i beri\u8217'ato shel olam} \u8212- as Beit Hillel does? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ein danin efshar mi-she-iy efshar} \u8212- one cannot derive a ruling for the possible from the impossible. God\u8217's creation required both male and female for the world to begin; there was no alternative. That extraordinary situation cannot serve as a normative model for individual obligation in an ongoing populated world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel & Moshe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, why does Beit Hillel not derive the requirement from Moshe\u8217's two sons? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i Moshe mi-da\u8217'atei hu de-avad} \u8212- Moshe acted on his own judgment in ways that were exceptional and unrepeatable. He may not have been {\i mekayem pru urvu} in the conventional sense at all; {\i ve-hiskim da\u8217'ato le-da\u8217'at ha-Makom} \u8212- his reasoning aligned with God\u8217's will, but it was not a model for others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's 3 Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} elaborates on three things Moshe did independently \u8212- {\i shlosha devarim asa Moshe mi-da\u8217'ato ve-hiskim da\u8217'ato le-da\u8217'at ha-Makom}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is {\i pirash min ha-isha} \u8212- he separated permanently from his wife after {\i Matan Torah}. His reasoning: the Jewish people were told to abstain from relations only for the brief period before God spoke to them at Sinai, and the Torah says {\i al tikrevu el isha} for that duration. Moshe, however, was always potentially available for divine communication \u8212- God could speak to him at any moment, without advance notice. Therefore he concluded he must remain in a constant state of readiness, which required permanent separation from his wife. How do we know God ratified this? From the verse {\i lechu imru lahem shuvu lachem le-oholeichem} \u8212- go tell them to return to their tents (i.e., resume marital relations) \u8212- {\i ve-attah poh amod imadi} \u8212- but you, stand here with Me, implying an ongoing, not merely temporary, condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second is {\i ve-shiber ha-luchot} \u8212- he broke the {\i luchot}. His reasoning: {\i u-mah Pesach she-hu echad mi-shesh me\u8217'ot u-shlosh esreh mitzvos} \u8212- the Pesach offering, which is merely one of 613 commandments, is restricted so that {\i kol ben nechar lo yochal bo} \u8212- a foreigner may not eat from it, and this is understood halachically as referring to one who has distanced himself from God. How much more so, he reasoned, should the entire Torah \u8212- represented by the {\i luchot} \u8212- not be given to a people who have become {\i mumarim}? God\u8217's ratification is indicated by the verse {\i asher shibarta} \u8212- Reish Lakish reads this as {\i yishar kochacha she-shibarta}, an expression of divine approval for the breaking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third is {\i ve-hosif yom echad mi-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- he added one day of separation before {\i Matan Torah}. The verse says {\i ve-kiddashtam hayom u-machar} \u8212- prepare today and tomorrow. Moshe reasoned: just as tomorrow is a full day beginning from its preceding night, so too must today be a full day from its preceding night \u8212- but that night had already passed. Therefore two complete days were required from that point forward. God\u8217's approval is evident from the fact that the schedule proceeded without correction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Variant Baraitas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai: 2M 2F\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} attributed to Rabbi Natan presents a variant reading of Beit Shammai: {\i Beit Shammai omrim shenei zecharim u-shtei nekevot} \u8212- two males and two females. In this version, Beit Shammai agrees that gender parity matters for the purpose of {\i pru urvu}, but doubles the requirement: {\i peru} generates one boy and one girl; {\i revu} doubles it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai/Hillel Variants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraita} attributed to Rabbi Natan presents yet another version: {\i Beit Shammai omrim zachar u-nekeva} (what we had known as the Beit Hillel position), and {\i Beit Hillel omrim o zachar o nekeva} \u8212- even one child of either sex suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hevel & Sister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna explains Rabbi Natan\u8217's version of Beit Shammai \u8212- two males and two females \u8212- from the verse {\i va-tosef laledet et achiv et Hevel}. The word {\i et} implies an accompanying sister; so Kayin had a sister and Hevel had a sister. When the {\i gemara} then reads {\i ki shat li Elohim zera acher tachat Hevel ki harago Kayin} \u8212- that God gave Chava another child to replace Hevel \u8212- it understands this as indicating she needed to restore the full complement: if {\i pru urvu} had been fulfilled with Hevel and his sister and Kayin and his sister, then losing Hevel meant the {\i mitzvah} was no longer fulfilled, and she needed to replace him. This implies the minimum is two boys and two girls.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects: perhaps Chava simply wanted to mourn and replace Hevel as a human being she loved, not because she was no longer {\i mekayem} the {\i mitzvah}. The {\i rabbanan} respond: {\i oduyei hu de-kamodei} \u8212- she was simply thanking God for a new child. The verse need not be read as evidence about the parameters of {\i pru urvu} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Settlement World\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava explains Rabbi Natan\u8217's version of Beit Hillel \u8212- even one child suffices \u8212- from the verse {\i lo tohu vera\u8217'ah lashevet yetzarah}: God did not create the world to be desolate but to be settled. The {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru urvu} is oriented toward {\i yishuv ha-olam} \u8212- the settling of the world \u8212- and even one child constitutes a contribution to that end. {\i Ve-ha avdei lah shevet} \u8212- having even one child is an act of settling the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about this {\i gemara} is that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru urvu} is being analyzed almost entirely through the lens of its purpose rather than through the technical requirements one might expect. And the verse from {\i Yeshayahu} \u8212- {\i lo tohu vera\u8217'ah lashevet yetzarah} \u8212- appears to carry more weight in shaping these parameters than one might have anticipated. This approach of deriving halachic parameters from the purpose of a {\i mitzvah} is relatively uncommon; here it is the dominant mode of analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-Conversion Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now takes up a new case: {\i hayu lo banim biyoto oveid kochavim ve-nitgayer} \u8212- a man who had children while he was a non-Jew and subsequently converted. The question is twofold: first, is he {\i mekayem pru urvu} through those children? Second \u8212- which turns out to require a separate analysis \u8212- does his first child born after conversion carry the status of {\i bechor} for purposes of inheritance?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Yes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan rules: {\i kiyam pru urvu} \u8212- yes, he has fulfilled the {\i mitzvah}. His reasoning encompasses two components: the children are biologically his, and even as a non-Jew he was within the framework of {\i pru urvu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish: No\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish disagrees: {\i lo kiyam pru urvu de-keivan she-nitgayer k\u8217'katan she-nolad dami} \u8212- upon conversion a person is like a newborn child; his prior legal relationships \u8212- including parenthood \u8212- are severed, and his pre-conversion children do not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} establishes that both cases \u8212- {\i pru urvu} and {\i bechor} for inheritance \u8212- are necessary to state the dispute. If only the {\i pru urvu} case were stated, one might have thought Rabbi Yochanan rules as he does because non-Jews are themselves within the {\i parshah} of {\i pru urvu}, so the act of having children was already a fulfillment of the {\i mitzvah} even before conversion, and that fulfillment carries over. But in matters of inheritance \u8212- where the pre-conversion children have no halachic standing as legal heirs at all \u8212- one might have thought Rabbi Yochanan would concede to Reish Lakish. Conversely, if only the inheritance case were stated, one might have thought Reish Lakish concedes on {\i pru urvu} since it is ultimately about biological children. Stating both cases makes clear that they maintain their respective positions across both contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews Obligated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion carries an important implication: non-Jews are themselves obligated in {\i pru urvu}. This should not be surprising \u8212- God commands {\i pru urvu} first to Adam and Chava, then to Noach, making it a universal command. And yet there is a genuine {\i machlokes rishonim} on this question, partly because {\i pru urvu} does not appear explicitly among the {\i sheva mitzvos benei Noach}. Rashi here affirms the position clearly: {\i bnei Noach bnei pru urvu ninhu de-chtiv vayomer lahem pru urvu} \u8212- the sons of Noach are in the category of {\i pru urvu}, as it is written that God commanded them to be fruitful and multiply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parentage Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} establishes a further distinction: non-Jews have legally recognized parentage \u8212- the example being {\i Berodach Baladan ben Baladan melech Bavel}, where a non-Jewish king is identified by his father\u8217's name, indicating halachic recognition of the parent-child relationship. Slaves, however, have no legally recognized parentage at all \u8212- only biological children, with no halachic {\i chiyus}. The source cited is {\i shvu lachem po im ha-chamor}, and the famous {\i drasha}: {\i am ha-domeh la-chamor} \u8212- those who are like donkeys. The point is not about all non-Jews but about the slave Avraham brought with him, indicating that a slave\u8217's children carry no legal relationship to him whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An additional proof is drawn from the fact that non-Jewish parentage is traced not only one generation back but several: {\i vayishlach ha-melech Asa ben Hadad ben Tavrimon ben Chezyon melech Aram}, where three generations of ancestry are recorded for a non-Jewish king. This multi-generational tracing indicates a genuine, legally recognized family structure \u8212- distinct from the purely biological description that applies to a slave\u8217's offspring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot: when it comes to the question of whether a converted man\u8217's pre-conversion children count for {\i pru urvu}, they did at least exist within a halachically recognized legal family relationship as non-Jews. The dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish is therefore not about whether there was any prior status at all, but about whether conversion severs that prior status for purposes of the Jewish legal framework going forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Children Predecease\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna: Fulfilled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to a final question: {\i hayu lo banim u-metu} \u8212- a man had children who subsequently died. Is he {\i mekayem pru urvu}? Rav Huna says yes \u8212- {\i kiyem piryah u-rivyah} \u8212- because he had the children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Assi: Mashiach Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna\u8217's position rests on the view of Rabbi Assi: {\i ein Ben David ba ad she-yichlu kol ha-neshamot she-ba-guf} \u8212- the Mashiach will not come until all the souls stored in the heavenly {\i guf} (the divine repository of souls) have been used up, as the verse says {\i ki ruach mi-lefanai ya\u8217'atof}. Under this understanding, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru urvu} is oriented toward bringing about the conditions for {\i mashiach} \u8212- each child born depletes the heavenly reservoir of souls. From this perspective, the child need not survive; the act of birth already accomplishes the purpose of the {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Settlement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disagrees: {\i lo kiyem piryah u-rivyah}. He grounds his position in the verse {\i lashevet yetzarah} \u8212- the purpose of {\i pru urvu} is to settle the world, and settling the world requires that the children actually be present to do so. If they have died, the goal of {\i yishuv ha-olam} has not been achieved, and the {\i mitzvah} remains unfulfilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raising Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking position with significant implications. If the purpose of {\i pru urvu} is {\i lashevet} \u8212- settlement \u8212- then not only must children be born, they must live and contribute to the world\u8217's settlement. The {\i mitzvah} is not merely about birth but about the entire arc of bringing children into productive existence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adoption & Pru Urvu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This understanding opens the door to a discussion that has very real relevance for couples challenged by infertility. If part of {\i pru urvu} is the raising of children \u8212- not just their biological production \u8212- then adoption can be understood as at least a partial {\i kiyum} of the {\i mitzvah}. Indeed, a number of {\i poskim} take exactly this view: that raising children, even if not one\u8217's biological offspring, fulfills a genuine dimension of {\i piryah u-rivyah}. This remains an active area of halachic discussion, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's grounding of {\i pru urvu} in {\i lashevet yetzarah} provides the conceptual foundation for such a position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 63\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage & Wife\u8217's Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context & Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 63, and we pick up at the very bottom of 62b. Before doing so, a brief recap is in order. Yesterday\u8217's discussion covered the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} and specifically Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position about not stopping at two children \u8212- along with the broader question of how much of that represents a formal obligation versus {\i minhag derech eretz}. The {\i gemara} is now in the middle of a discussion about the importance of marriage, presented from a man\u8217's perspective. We have already seen two schools attributed to Bavel and Eretz Yisrael respectively: the Babylonian position frames marriage as an intrinsic good \u8212- a source of {\i simcha}, wholeness, and blessing \u8212- while what is attributed to Eretz Yisrael is more instrumental, focusing on the prevention of sin and the husband\u8217's ability to learn Torah. It is worth noting the irony that most other sources suggest the actual schools were reversed, with the differing attitudes relating to leaving home to study Torah in Bavel versus Eretz Yisrael, and to questions of polygamy and monogamy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanu Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a {\i tanu rabbanan}: one who loves his wife as himself \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'ohev et ishto k\u8217'gufo} \u8212- and respects her even more than himself \u8212- {\i u\u8217'mechabda yoter m\u8217'gufo} \u8212- where \u8220"respect\u8221" here is understood similarly to how {\i kibbud em} is understood: attending to the person\u8217's physical needs such as clothing, feeding, and care, though it appears to carry a broader meaning as well. The {\i baraita} continues: one who instructs his sons and daughters to walk on the right path \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'hamadrich banav u\u8217'venotav b\u8217'derech yeshara} \u8212- and who marries them off close to their age of maturity \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'hamassian samuch l\u8217'pirkan} \u8212- regarding him the verse says: {\i v\u8217'yadata ki shalom ohalecha u\u8217'fakadta navecha v\u8217'lo techeta}, \u8220"you will know that your tent is at peace, and you will see that your dwelling is without sin.\u8221" What is added here beyond the earlier discussion is the dimension of children: educating them on the right moral path and marrying them off while young, so that once they are sexually mature they are already married and protected from sin. This includes even the notion of marrying off a young son while he is quite young.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neighbors & Charity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then continues with further obligations: one who loves his neighbors \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'ohev et shechenav} \u8212- one who draws his relatives close \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'hamakarev et kerovav}, a nicely phrased formulation acknowledging that relatives are not always actually close \u8212- one who marries his niece ({\i ve\u8217'hanosei et bat achoto}, though there is some discussion as to whether this refers specifically to his sister\u8217's daughter or also his brother\u8217's daughter), and one who lends money to a poor person in a moment of particular distress \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'hamalveh sela l\u8217'ani b\u8217'shat dochko} \u8212- regarding all of these the verse says: {\i az tikra v\u8217'Hashem ya\u8217'aneh tishva v\u8217'yomar hineni}, \u8220"then you will call out and God will answer, you will cry out and He will say: here I am.\u8221" As Rashi points out, this verse follows the passage {\i ki tireh arom v\u8217'chisito u\u8217'mibesarcha lo titalam} \u8212- seeing a naked person and dressing him \u8212- where covering the naked corresponds to lending to the poor in their distress, and \u8220"not hiding from your own flesh\u8221" corresponds to taking care of relatives, marrying one\u8217's niece, and so forth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Essential\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar offers a striking teaching: {\i kol adam she\u8217'ein lo isha eino adam} \u8212- any man without a wife is not a man \u8212- as it is written, {\i zachar u\u8217'nekeva bera\u8217'am vayikra shemam Adam}: only when they were both male and female together was the name \u8220"Adam\u8221" applied to them. This clearly reflects the message of Bereishit \u8212- the sense of wholeness and oneness that is created through marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar then adds: {\i kol adam she\u8217'ein lo karka eino adam} \u8212- any person without land is likewise not a real man. The introduction of land into the discussion is surprising, and one is prompted to reconsider what he means with the earlier statement about a wife. The scholar Satlow, in his study of marriage in Jewish antiquity, argues that Rabbi Elazar\u8217's point is primarily about a person\u8217's social standing \u8212- that having a home and being married is what it takes to be recognized as a person within society. Seen this way, the two statements complement each other: they are not existential claims about human completeness in a philosophical sense, but rather a description of what constitutes full membership in the community. This reading is supported by the sociological reality that in many Jewish communities, even now, unmarried individuals are sometimes not viewed as fully integrated members \u8212- a reality that may not be ideal but is nonetheless real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Helper Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues with Rabbi Elazar: {\i mai d\u8217'chtiv e\u8217'eseh lo ezer k\u8217'negdo} \u8212- what is the meaning of the verse \u8220"I will make him a helpmate opposite him\u8221"? {\i Zacha} \u8212- if he merits \u8212- {\i ozarto}, she will be his help. {\i Lo zacha} \u8212- if he does not merit \u8212- {\i k\u8217'negdo}, she will be against him. A second tradition presents the same idea through a textual discrepancy: the word is written {\i k\u8217'negdo} but read {\i k\u8217'nigdo}. The homiletical reading derives: if he merits \u8212- {\i k\u8217'negdo}, meet for him, appropriate for him; if he does not merit \u8212- {\i minagdato}, from the Aramaic for \u8220"lashing\u8221" \u8212- she lashes him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yossi found Elijah and asked him: the verse says \u8220"I will make him a helpmate\u8221" \u8212- in what way does a woman help a man? Elijah responded: when a man brings wheat home from the field, does he chew wheat directly? When he brings home flax, does he wear raw flax? Rather, the wife grinds the wheat into flour and bakes bread from it; she beats the flax and fashions it into linen and clothing. Thus: {\i nimtzeit me\u8217'ira einav u\u8217'ma\u8217'amidato al raglav} \u8212- she lights up his eyes and makes him stand on his legs. This is, admittedly, a very concrete, material understanding of the wife\u8217's contribution \u8212- placing her in what we might call the kitchen or the workroom \u8212- and it must be acknowledged that not all of these framings reflect modern sensibilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bone & Flesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar then addresses the verse {\i zot hapa\u8217'am etzem me\u8217'atzami u\u8217'vasar mibsari} \u8212- \u8220"this time, bone of my bone, flesh of my flesh.\u8221" He reads it as teaching that Adam had relations with every animal and creature \u8212- {\i melamed sheba adam al kol behema v\u8217'chaya} \u8212- but {\i lo nitkarera da\u8217'ato}, his mind was not put at ease, until he came to Chava. Only then did he recognize, \u8220"this is my own species.\u8221" The reading is motivated by the interpretive question: what exactly prompted Adam\u8217's declaration? Merely seeing her did not seem sufficient to explain the force of the words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abraham\u8217's Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar continues: what is the meaning of {\i v\u8217'nivrechu becha kol mishpachot ha\u8217'adamah} \u8212- \u8220"and through you all the families of the earth will be blessed\u8221"? God said to Avraham: I have two graftings to graft onto you \u8212- {\i shtei brachot tovot yesh li l\u8217'havrich becha}. The word {\i l\u8217'havrich} carries a double meaning: not only \u8220"blessing\u8221" ({\i berachah}) but also {\i havrachah}, the grafting of a branch from one tree onto another. The two graftings are Ruth the Moabite and Na\u8217'amah the Ammonite. From Ruth will come David; Na\u8217'amah, too, produces children who enter the royal lineage. This is a particularly significant statement within the context of Yevamot, where questions of lineage and the status of converts have been and will continue to be central themes. Here the {\i gemara} is emphasizing, rather pointedly, the wonderful blessing that comes specifically from outside one\u8217's own gene pool.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Israel\u8217's Centrality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues: {\i kol mishpachot ha\u8217'adamah} \u8212- all the families of the earth \u8212- {\i afilu mishpachot hadarot ba\u8217'adamah}, even the families dwelling out in the countryside, far from cities \u8212- {\i ein berachah ela bishvil Yisrael}, all their blessing derives from Israel. {\i Kol goyei ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- all the nations of the earth \u8212- even the ships sailing from Gaul to Spain, entirely remote from the land of Israel and not even on solid ground but on the sea \u8212- {\i einan mitbarechot ela bishvil Yisrael}, all their blessing is by virtue of Israel. Interestingly, there is actually a rabbi from Gaul mentioned in the Yerushalmi, suggesting that the Jewish world extended even to that distant region.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Labor Professions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Agriculture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar continues with a series of statements. He teaches: {\i atidin kol ba\u8217'alei umanut sheya\u8217'amdu al hakarka} \u8212- in the future, all craftspeople will stand upon the land, meaning they will ultimately turn to farming. The verse cited is {\i veyardu me\u8217'oniyotayhem kol tofsei mashot malachim kol chovlei hayam al ha\u8217'aretz ya\u8217'amdu} \u8212- all those who work the sails, all the mariners, will descend to the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agriculture Lowly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar then immediately declares: {\i ein lecha umanut pechutah min hakarka} \u8212- there is no craft more lowly than working the land. This is striking: if in the future all craftspeople will leave their trades and turn to the land, one might expect that to be presented as a positive development. Yet here Rabbi Elazar seems to be saying the opposite. One possible resolution is that the vision of the future involves the non-Jews becoming the farmers, freeing others \u8212- perhaps, to learn Torah \u8212- but the tension remains worth noting. The verse cited is {\i veyardu} \u8212- they will descend \u8212- understood as a downward movement, not merely from their boats but from their station.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Against Farming\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then offers two anecdotes illustrating this attitude. Rabbi Elazar saw a particular plot of land in which a furrow \u8212- or, in another reading, a head of cabbage \u8212- was being planted widthwise. He said to it: even if you were planted lengthwise, it would be better to be involved in business than to worry about working you. Rav walked between stalks of grain and saw them swaying in the wind. He said to them: wave all you like \u8212- it is still far better to be involved in business than to farm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Business Economics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers an economic analysis. If one invests a hundred {\i zuz} in business \u8212- {\i me\u8217'ah zuzei be\u8217'iska} \u8212- every day one eats meat and drinks wine. If one invests the same hundred {\i zuz} in farming \u8212- {\i me\u8217'ah zuzei be\u8217'ara} \u8212- one eats vegetables with salt. Moreover, one has to sleep on the land to guard the crops \u8212- {\i maganya leh a\u8217'ara} \u8212- and gets into disputes with neighbors \u8212- {\i uramya leh tigrei}. The conclusion is that it is better to be a businessman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plant vs.\u160 ?Purchase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa, however, offers a counter-position: {\i zara velo tazbin} \u8212- plant rather than purchase. Even if the cost works out the same, the work of one\u8217's own hands is blessed, so one should produce one\u8217's own food rather than go buy it in the market. Then he adds: {\i zabin velo tazor} \u8212- purchase rather than weave. The precise interpretation of this second clause is disputed. Rashi reads it as: sell your property before you become impoverished, though with the caveat that this applies to certain objects like mats; for a cloak, one would not sell it \u8212- {\i lo mistramlei} \u8212- out of concern that one would not be able to find another that fits as well. Tosafot, working from a variant text, reads the contrast differently: while one should grow one\u8217's own food, one should purchase one\u8217's own clothes rather than weave them \u8212- except that for a garment like a cloak, custom tailoring may be preferable. This raises the natural question of how Rav Papa\u8217's endorsement of personally working the land sits alongside the earlier denigration of farming \u8212- a tension the {\i gemara} leaves in place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Suffering & Justice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Punishment Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar bar Avina teaches: {\i ein puraniyot ba\u8217'ot la\u8217'olam ella bishvil Yisrael} \u8212- bad things happen to the world for the sake of Israel. The verse cited is {\i hikratti goyim nashammu pinnotam, hecherevti chutzotam} \u8212- I have cut off nations, their corners are desolate, I have laid waste their courtyards \u8212- followed by: {\i amarti ach tir\u8217'i oti tikchi musar}, \u8220"I said: perhaps you will fear Me and take heed.\u8221" The implication is that all the devastation visited upon the entire world is ultimately so that Israel should take heed and return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a transition. Rav was departing from Rabbi Chiyya, and Rabbi Chiyya said to him: {\i rachamana litzlan mimmidi de-kashi mimota} \u8212- may God save us from something that is worse than death. Rav wondered to himself: is there anything worse than death? He went and searched and found the verse: {\i u-motze ani mar mimmavet et ha-ishah} \u8212- I find more bitter than death the woman. We now enter a section of the {\i gemara} that is, it must be acknowledged, written entirely by men discussing wives. What came before was also from the male perspective \u8212- the value of a wife for the husband, whether intrinsically or instrumentally, the husband\u8217's obligations \u8212- but now the {\i gemara} turns to a frank and mixed assessment of marriage from the man\u8217's vantage point. The question of when we get to hear how wives feel about their husbands is a pointed one, and we will return to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} reports: {\i Rav hava ka metzatra leih devitehu} \u8212- Rav\u8217's wife was causing him a great deal of anguish. When he would ask her to make {\i tluphi} \u8212- lentils \u8212- she would make {\i chimtzi} \u8212- a type of pickled or brined dish, not radically different but not what he asked for. When he asked for {\i chimtzi}, she would make {\i tluphi}. She would invert his requests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Food Requests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When his son Chiyya grew up, he began relaying his father\u8217's requests to his mother \u8212- but inverting them, so that Rav ultimately received what he actually wanted. When Rav noticed that his meals were now matching his requests, he said to his son: your mother has improved! The son replied: no \u8212- {\i ana hu de-ka-afichna lah} \u8212- I was the one inverting her responses so that you got what you wanted. Rav then said to him: that is exactly what people mean when they say {\i de-nafik minach ta\u8217'ama malfach} \u8212- the one who comes from you will teach you. You learn wisdom from your children. Nevertheless, Rav continued, {\i at lo ta\u8217'avid hachi} \u8212- do not do this \u8212- citing the verse: {\i limdu leshonam daber sheker} \u8212- \u8220"they have taught their tongues to speak falsehood.\u8221" Do not teach deception, even for a worthy end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Perspective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point it is worth pausing to read a contemporary {\i midrash} by Rivkah Lubitch, who wrote the wife\u8217's perspective on this story. Her account asks: why did Rav\u8217's wife do this? Not, she writes, because Rav would sometimes tell her he was coming home early from the {\i beit midrash} and would arrive only after dark \u8212- leaving her sitting in distress, waiting, until anger and resentment finally overtook her. Not because he would sometimes say he was coming home late and would arrive early, leaving her scrambling to prepare the meal. Not because he would sometimes signal that they would dine together privately \u8212- only for him to arrive from the {\i beit midrash} with many students, all of whom would sit, eat, and enjoy themselves in Torah discussion while she labored to serve them all. Not because he would sometimes say he was bringing guests and then come home alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, Lubitch writes, it was because when Rav knew she had cooked {\i chimtzi}, he would ask for {\i tluphi}; and when she had cooked {\i tluphi}, he would ask for {\i chimtzi}. And she \u8212- she was silent. She said nothing. She simply served him what she had already prepared in the pot. And he never knew why she was doing this. And she, this poor woman, did not know why her husband was treating her this way \u8212- he who was her entire world and everything she worked for. And so she was bitter all her days, and nearly longed for death. And it is for this that the verse says: {\i u-motze ani mar mimmavet et ha-ishah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This rendering gives a profound sense of what the same story looks like from a very different vantage point, and it is with this in mind that we turn to the rest of the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Complexity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chiyya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to Rabbi Chiyya. His wife, too, caused him much anguish \u8212- {\i Rabbi Chiya havah ka metzaara lei devitehu}. Yet when he found a nice thing in the marketplace, he would wrap it in his handkerchief and bring it to her as a small gift. Rav asked him: but she causes you such suffering! Rabbi Chiyya responded: {\i dayeinu} \u8212- it is enough for us \u8212- {\i she-megadlot baneinu u-matziylot otanu min ha-chet}: that they raise our children and save us from sexual sin. Even through all the difficulty, one must remain appreciative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents what appears to be a contradiction. Rav Yehuda was teaching his son Rav Yitzchak the verse {\i u-motze ani mar mimmavet et ha-ishah} \u8212- I find bitterer than death the woman. Rav Yitzchak asked: who is an example of such a woman? Rav Yehuda replied: your mother. But elsewhere Rav Yehuda taught his son the verse {\i yehi mekorecha baruch u\u8217'smach me-eshet neurecha} \u8212- \u8220"may your source be blessed and rejoice with the wife of your youth\u8221" \u8212- with the interpretation {\i ein adam motze korat ruach ela me-ishto rishona}, a man finds true satisfaction of spirit only with his first wife. And when asked for an example of this, he again said: your mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One wonders about the wisdom of these conversations with one\u8217's children about their other parent. But the {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i matkif takifa ve-avurei mi-avurei be-milei} \u8212- she would get very angry but could also be calmed down and pacified with words. In other words, the marriage was genuinely mixed \u8212- at times very difficult, at times a source of deep satisfaction. This is, of course, true of many marriages. It is neither all one thing nor all another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Good vs.\u160 ?Bad Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i heichi dami isha ra\u8217'ah} \u8212- what would be an example of a bad wife? Abaye says: {\i mekashta lei taka u-mekashta lei puma} \u8212- she prepares the dish and at the same time prepares her mouth \u8212- she makes his meals while simultaneously getting ready to berate him during the meal. Rava says: {\i mekashta lei taka u-mahadra lei gava} \u8212- she prepares the dish and turns her back to him. She feeds him while shunning him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chama bar Chanina then offers a more positive teaching: {\i keivan she-nasa adam isha, avonotav mitpakkekim} \u8212- when a man marries a woman, his sins are sealed up or covered over, as derived from the verse {\i matza isha matza tov ve-yafek ratzon me-Hashem} \u8212- \u8220"one who finds a wife finds good and draws favor from God,\u8221" where {\i vayafek} is understood as related to {\i pitpuk}, closing or sealing. And in Eretz Yisrael, when a man would marry a woman, they would ask him: {\i matza o motze?} \u8212- is your wife a {\i matza} or a {\i motze}? A {\i matza}, as in {\i matza isha matza tov} \u8212- one who finds good \u8212- or a {\i motze}, as in {\i u-motze ani mar mimmavet et ha-ishah} \u8212- one who is bitterer than death? This was an ingenious way of asking how the marriage was working out, while simultaneously acknowledging that marriages exist on a wide spectrum \u8212- sometimes good, sometimes mixed, sometimes genuinely difficult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A beautiful {\i drasha} on this exchange suggests that the question was not about the woman at all but about the man: {\i matza o motze} \u8212- what kind of person are you? Are you a {\i matza}, someone who, having found something, is content \u8212- who has a {\i metzia} and stops searching? Then {\i matza isha matza tov}. Or are you a {\i motze} \u8212- someone who, even after finding, keeps looking around, wondering if something better is out there? Then {\i u-motze ani mar mimmavet et ha-ishah} \u8212- you will never be satisfied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava teaches: {\i isha ra\u8217'ah mitzvah le-garsha} \u8212- if a man has a bad wife, it is a {\i mitzvah} to divorce her, as derived from the verse {\i garesh letz ve-yeitzei madon ve-yishbot din ve-kalon} \u8212- \u8220"drive out the mocker and strife departs, quarreling and disgrace will cease.\u8221" This is a significant ruling in light of a tendency in some {\i batei din} to resist any action toward divorce under the blanket assumption \u8212- invoked under the name of {\i shalom bayit} \u8212- that it is always better to remain married. Rava\u8217's position pushes back clearly against that assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bad Marriage Remedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds: if the wife is bad but her {\i ketubah} is large and the husband cannot afford to divorce her, the remedy is to marry another wife \u8212- the presence of a competitor will change the dynamic. The popular saying is invoked: {\i be-chavarta v\u8217'lo b\u8217'silta} \u8212- better to change someone\u8217's behavior through a friend (i.e., a rival) than with a thorn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Comparisons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then elaborates: {\i kasheh isha ra\u8217'ah k\u8217'yom sagrir} \u8212- a bad wife is as terrible as a stormy, relentless day, as the verse equates: {\i delef tored b\u8217'yom sagrir v\u8217'eshes midyanim nishtaveh} \u8212- a dripping leak on a stormy day and a contentious woman are the same.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then offers a comparative hermeneutic: {\i bo u\u8217're\u8217'ei kamah tovah isha tovah v\u8217'kamah ra\u8217'ah isha ra\u8217'ah} \u8212- come and see how good is a good wife and how bad is a bad wife. If the verse {\i matza isha matza tov} is read literally, then the simple meaning testifies to the goodness of a good wife. If it is read metaphorically \u8212- as {\i isha} referring to Torah or wisdom, as is common in Mishlei \u8212- then how good is a good wife that the Torah itself is likened to her. Either way, the goodness of a good wife is established. Conversely, if {\i u-motze ani mar mimmavet et ha-ishah} is read literally, it testifies to how terrible a bad wife is. If it is read metaphorically \u8212- {\i isha} as a reference to {\i Gehinnom} \u8212- then how bad is a bad wife that {\i Gehinnom} itself is compared to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Escape Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues with several verses interpreted through the lens of the bad wife with a large {\i ketubah}. {\i Hineni meivi ra\u8217'ah she-lo yuchlu latzes mimena} \u8212- \u8220"behold I bring an evil from which they cannot escape\u8221" \u8212- Rav Nachman quoting Rabbah bar Avuha says: this refers to a bad wife with a large {\i ketubah}. {\i Nesannani Hashem b\u8217'yadei lo uchal kum} \u8212- \u8220"God has delivered me into the hands of those before whom I cannot stand\u8221" \u8212- this too, say Rav Chisda quoting Mar Ukva bar Chiyya, refers to the same situation. In Eretz Yisrael, the reading is: {\i zo she-mezonoseha teluyim b\u8217'chaspa} \u8212- one whose food supply depends entirely on purchasing, who cannot grow her own food, always uncertain whether what she needs will be available in the market. This also echoes the earlier passage about the wife who makes her husband {\i stand on his feet} and {\i lights up his eyes} \u8212- {\i me\u8217'iras eineinu u\u8217'mekimas ragleinu} \u8212- suggesting that the counterimage is the wife whose household cannot function without her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then reads {\i banecha u\u8217'venotav nesunim l\u8217'am acher} \u8212- \u8220"your sons and daughters shall be given to another people\u8221" \u8212- as referring to a stepmother: one\u8217's children are under the authority of someone who is not their parent. And {\i b\u8217'goy naval ach\u8217'isaim} \u8212- \u8220"I will anger them with a degenerate nation\u8221" \u8212- is interpreted by Rav Chanan bar Rava in the name of Rav as again referring to a bad wife with a large {\i ketubah}, though the precise connection to {\i naval} is not spelled out. Rabbi Eliezer (with the correct reading being {\i eilu ha-minim} rather than {\i Tzedukim}, as found in the Steinsaltz edition) says: these are the {\i minim}, the heretics. The connection may relate to the pattern of verbal argumentation and debate associated with the {\i minim} \u8212- and thus the verse {\i amar naval belibo, ein Elokim} \u8212- \u8220"the fool has said in his heart: there is no God.\u8221" A {\i beraita} adds: {\i elu anshei Barbarya ve-anshei Martanai} \u8212- these are the Barbarians and the people of Mauritania \u8212- {\i she-mehalchin arumim ba-shuk}, who walk mostly unclothed in the marketplace \u8212- {\i she-ein lecha meshukatz u-metoav lifnei ha-Makom yoter mimi she-mahalech ba-shuk arum} \u8212- for there is none so disgusting and abhorrent before God as one who walks naked in the marketplace. Here {\i naval} points not to speech but to degenerate conduct.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chabarim Persecution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Denying God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i eilu Chaverin} \u8212- these are the {\i Chabarim}. Now {\i chaver} can refer to the class of people in the Second Temple period who ate {\i chulin be-tahara}, but here it refers to a specific group in Babylonia \u8212- Zoroastrian priests who came to Bavel in the time of Rav and later and were notably oppressive, showing no deference to {\i talmidei chachamim} as other Babylonian authorities had.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Decrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabbi Yochanan was told that {\i Chabarai le-Bavel} \u8212- the {\i Chabarim} had come to Babylonia \u8212- {\i shaga ve-nafal}, he collapsed in distress, recognizing what this would mean for the rabbis there. When told that they could be bribed, {\i taritz ve-yativ} \u8212- he straightened up and recovered, understanding that as long as they were susceptible to bribery, matters were not hopeless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chabarim} issued three decrees against the Jews of Bavel corresponding to three sins. First: {\i al bisra mipnei ha-matanot} \u8212- they prohibited eating kosher meat, understood as punishment for the Jews\u8217' failure to give the priestly gifts \u8212- {\i matanot} \u8212- from slaughtered animals, a discussion with implications for whether those obligations apply outside Eretz Yisrael. Second: {\i gazru al ha-merchatza\u8217'ot mipnei ha-tevila} \u8212- they shut down the bathhouses, understood as punishment for laxity in attending the {\i mikveh}. Third: {\i ve-ka-machtati shichvei} \u8212- they exhumed the dead. The Zoroastrian theological position held that burying bodies in the earth defiles the ground; their practice was to expose the body in a covered outdoor structure where birds of prey would strip the flesh from the bones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Body Exhumation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The penalty of exhumation brings with it a theological difficulty. The verse says {\i vahayta yad Hashem bachem uv\u8217'avoteichem} \u8212- \u8220"the hand of God will be against you and against your fathers.\u8221" How can punishment reach one\u8217's fathers? The {\i gemara} answers: only in the case of exhumation of the bodies \u8212- {\i davar ze, chitutei shichvi}. Rabbah bar Shmuel explains: {\i ba\u8217'avon chayim metim mitchatetin} \u8212- because of the sins of the living, the dead are exhumed. The {\i gemara} continues with an exchange between Rabbah and Rav Mari about two apparently contradictory verses on the subject of burial: one says the dead {\i lo ye\u8217'asfu velo yikaveru, ledomen al pnei ha\u8217'adamah yihiyu} \u8212- will not be gathered or buried, left like dung on the earth \u8212- while the other says {\i venivchar mavet mechayim} \u8212- death will be preferred to life. The resolution offered is that the second verse applies to the {\i resha\u8217'im}: for the wicked, it is better that they die than that they continue to live in this world, sin further, and fall into {\i Gehinnom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Sira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the book of Ben Sira, citing it with the word {\i katuv} \u8212- \u8220"it is written\u8221" \u8212- as though it carries scriptural weight, despite Ben Sira being classified among the {\i sefarim chitzoniyim}. A selection of passages is quoted. {\i Isha tova matana tova leva\u8217'alah} \u8212- a good wife is a good gift to her husband \u8212- {\i becheik yerei Elokim tinaten} \u8212- she will be given into the bosom of one who fears God. {\i Isha ra\u8217'ah tzara\u8217'at leva\u8217'alah} \u8212- a bad wife is like leprosy to her husband \u8212- and what is the remedy? {\i Yegareshena miyad vetitraper mitzara\u8217'ato} \u8212- divorce her immediately and be healed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Isha yafa ashrei ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a beautiful woman: happy is her husband \u8212- {\i mispar yamav kiflayim}, the number of his days will be doubled. But: {\i he\u8217'alem eineicha me\u8217'ishet chen} \u8212- hide your eyes from a charming woman who belongs to another \u8212- {\i pen tilached bimtzudatah}, lest you be caught in her net. Do not incline toward her husband \u8212- {\i al tetei etzel ba\u8217'alah limsoch imo yayin veshichar} \u8212- do not join him in drinking wine and mead. For in the beauty of another man\u8217's wife, {\i rabbim hushchatu} \u8212- many have been destroyed \u8212- {\i va\u8217'atzumim kol harugeiha} \u8212- and great are the numbers of those she has killed. {\i Rabbim hayu pitzei rochel} \u8212- great are the wounds of the spice merchant, the seller of fragrances \u8212- {\i ha-margilim lidvar aveira} \u8212- who habituate people to transgression, since the fragrances lead people to sin \u8212- {\i kenitzotz mav\u8217'ir gachelet} \u8212- like a spark upon a coal. {\i Kechluv malei of ken bateihem mele\u8217'im mirma} \u8212- as a bird trap full of birds, so their houses are filled with deceit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The selection then pivots away from the subject of women: {\i al tetzar tzarat machar} \u8212- do not grieve over tomorrow\u8217's troubles \u8212- {\i ki lo teda mah yeled yom} \u8212- for you do not know what the day will bring \u8212- {\i shema machar ba ve\u8217'einenu} \u8212- perhaps tomorrow will come and none of your fears will have materialized \u8212- {\i nimtza mitzta\u8217'er al olam she\u8217'eino shelo} \u8212- and you will have been suffering over a world that was never yours to begin with. Finally: {\i mine\u8217'a rabbim mitoch beitecha} \u8212- keep many people out of your home \u8212- {\i velo hakol tavi leveitecha} \u8212- do not bring everyone in \u8212- {\i rabbim yihiyu dorshei shelomecha} \u8212- many will present themselves as seeking your welfare \u8212- {\i vegale sod leachad me\u8217'alef} \u8212- but reveal your secrets to only one in a thousand.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Procreation Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashiach Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Assi teaches: {\i ein Ben David ba ad sheyichlu kol neshamot shebaguf} \u8212- Ben David does not come until all the souls stored in the heavenly repository have been sent into the world \u8212- as the verse says: {\i ki ruach milifnai ya\u8217'atof uneshamot ani asiti} \u8212- \u8220"for the spirit that passes before Me, and the souls that I have made.\u8221" The coming of {\i Mashiach} is thus linked to the exhaustion of the reservoir of souls, which in turn depends on the fulfillment of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tanna} teaches: Rabbi Eliezer says: whoever does not engage in {\i firya verivya} \u8212- procreation \u8212- it is as though he has spilled blood \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'ilu shofech damim} \u8212- as derived from the juxtaposition of {\i shofech dam ha\u8217'adam ba\u8217'adam damo yishafach} with {\i ve\u8217'atem pru urevu}. The one who takes life and the one who fails to create life are placed side by side. Rabbi Yaakov says: it is as though he has diminished the divine image \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'ilu mema\u8217'et hadmut} \u8212- since {\i ki betzelem Elokim asa et ha\u8217'adam}, humans are made in the image of God; not bringing human beings into the world therefore diminishes God\u8217's presence in the world, not merely in biological terms but in terms of the person as a bearer of {\i tzelem Elokim}. Ben Azzai combines both: {\i ke\u8217'ilu shofech damim umema\u8217'et hadmut} \u8212- as though he has both spilled blood and diminished the divine image.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They said to Ben Azzai: {\i yesh na\u8217'eh doresh vena\u8217'eh mekayem} \u8212- some expound well and fulfill well; {\i na\u8217'eh mekayem ve\u8217'ein na\u8217'eh doresh} \u8212- some fulfill even without eloquence. But you \u8212- {\i ata na\u8217'eh doresh ve\u8217'ein na\u8217'eh mekayem} \u8212- you speak beautifully but do not practice what you preach. You are not married. Ben Azzai replied: {\i uma e\u8217'ese shenafshi chashka baTorah} \u8212- what can I do? My soul is bound up in Torah. {\i Efshar la\u8217'olam sheyitkayem al yedei acherim} \u8212- let others fulfill the obligation. This raises a fascinating question that generates much subsequent discussion: can one who devotes himself entirely to Torah study be exempt from {\i pru u\u8217'revu}? And is there a sense in which Torah study serves as some kind of analog or substitute?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records a second {\i beraita} with the same essential structure \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer: {\i shofech damim}; Rabbi Elazar ben Azarya: {\i mema\u8217'et hadmut}; Ben Azzai: both \u8212- followed again by the challenge to Ben Azzai and his reply. The repetition prompts a closer look at what is really at stake in the debate between {\i shofech damim} and {\i mema\u8217'et hadmut}. Are these not both ultimately saying the same thing \u8212- that failing to bring life into the world is a grave failure?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A possible reading is that the distinction tracks the debate between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel on the minimum requirement for {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. Beit Shammai requires two boys; Beit Hillel requires a boy and a girl. The logic behind Beit Shammai\u8217's position \u8212- why boys specifically? \u8212- has never been fully explained, and the usual answer that boys were simply more valued in rabbinic culture, while sociologically accurate, does not resolve the internal halakhic logic. But if the emphasis falls on {\i betzelem Elokim} \u8212- on bringing into the world beings who will bear and enact the divine image through Torah and {\i mitzvot} \u8212- and if in the halakhic framework of the {\i gemara} men are the primary bearers of Torah obligation, then one could argue that Beit Shammai\u8217's preference for boys flows directly from the {\i mema\u8217'et hadmut} framework. The point of procreation, on that view, is not merely biological perpetuation of life \u8212- that would be captured by {\i shofech damim} \u8212- but the amplification of God\u8217's presence in the world through Torah-observant persons. That framing might genuinely favor boys over girls in Beit Shammai\u8217's understanding. Whether or not one accepts this reasoning normatively, it offers a coherent internal logic for an otherwise puzzling position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tanu rabbanan} is cited: {\i uvenucho yomar shuva Hashem rivvot alfei Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"and when it rested, he said: return, O God, to the myriads of thousands of Israel.\u8221" This teaches that the {\i Shechina} does not rest on fewer than two thousand and two myriads \u8212- twenty-two thousand \u8212- of Israel. Suppose Israel numbered exactly one fewer than that \u8212- 21,999. And suppose one person among them had not engaged in {\i pru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- {\i nimtza ze gorem la-Shechina shetistalek mi-Yisrael}: that one person\u8217's failure to bring the missing child into the world is what caused the {\i Shechina} to depart from Israel. This is the fullest expression of {\i mema\u8217'et hadmut}: not an abstract diminishment but a direct causal link between one individual\u8217's failure to procreate and the withdrawal of divine presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lineage Continuity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Others say: {\i gorem la-Shechina shetistalek mi-Yisrael}, derived from the verse {\i lihyot lecha l\u8217'Elokim u\u8217'l\u8217'zaracha acharecha} \u8212- \u8220"to be your God and the God of your offspring after you.\u8221" {\i Bi-zman she-zaracha acharecha, ha-Shechina shorah} \u8212- when you have offspring after you, the {\i Shechina} rests. {\i Ein zaracha acharecha} \u8212- if there is no offspring \u8212- {\i al mi shorah? al ha-eitzim v\u8217'al ha-avanim} \u8212- upon whom will it rest? Upon the sticks and stones?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the phrase {\i al ha-eitzim v\u8217'al ha-avanim} likely carries an additional resonance. There is a {\i gemara} elsewhere that speaks of wasting seed {\i al ha-eitzim v\u8217'al ha-avanim} \u8212- upon the sticks and the stones \u8212- in the context of improper emission of seed. The echo here seems intentional: if one\u8217's seed does not produce children and thereby provide a vessel for the {\i Shechina}, the implicit question is where exactly that seed was going, and where the divine presence could possibly be expected to reside. The emphasis in both passages on {\i Yisrael} \u8212- that the {\i Shechina} requires Israel specifically, not humanity in general \u8212- also connects to the earlier point about {\i betzelem Elokim} and the patrilineal continuity of {\i zaracha acharecha}, which in a patriarchal framework focuses on the male line: Avraham, Yitzchak, Yaakov. This in turn further illuminates the Beit Shammai position: the continuity of {\i Yisrael} as the bearer of God\u8217's presence in the world depends on sons.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 64\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ten-Year Rule & Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a {\i mishnah} addressing a man who has been married for ten years without his wife giving birth: {\i nasa isha ve\u8217'shaha imah eser shanim velo yalda} \u8212- he married a woman and dwelt with her ten years and she did not give birth. The immediate question the {\i mishnah} raises, though without resolving it explicitly, is whether the absence of children is attributable to her or to him. What is clear from the outset is that the {\i mishnah} is building on the language of the previous {\i mishnah}, which discussed when a man may cease pursuing the obligation of {\i pru u\u8217'rvu} once he has a son and a daughter. Here the {\i mishnah} rules: {\i eino rashai levatel} \u8212- he cannot simply nullify his obligation. Even after ten childless years, he cannot declare the matter closed and allow the situation to remain as it is. He must do something.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Nullify Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What, precisely, must he do? The {\i mishnah} contemplates two possibilities: he may marry a second wife while remaining married to the first \u8212- for in a society where multiple wives were permitted, this was legally available \u8212- or he may divorce her. The {\i mishnah} proceeds to note that if he did divorce her, {\i muteret le\u8217'hinasay le\u8217'acher} \u8212- she is permitted to marry another man. The implication is significant: the other man need not have yet fulfilled {\i pru u\u8217'rvu} himself, because we do not presume that the inability to have children was rooted in her. She may therefore marry another man who hopes to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} through this marriage. And that second husband, the {\i mishnah} adds, is similarly entitled to a waiting period: {\i ve\u8217'rashai ha\u8217'sheni lishhos imah eser shanim} \u8212- the second husband may remain with her for up to ten years before he too must act. The language {\i rashai} again implies that after ten years the man has no further discretion; he will be required either to divorce her or to take an additional wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further provision: if she miscarried, the count of ten years restarts from the moment of the miscarriage, {\i moneh mi\u8217'sha\u8217'ah she\u8217'hipila}. A miscarriage is not treated as confirmation of infertility \u8212- quite the contrary, it is taken as evidence of potential fertility, of an ability to conceive even if not to carry to term, and so the clock begins anew from that point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} cited immediately afterward makes the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling more explicit: {\i nasa isha ve\u8217'shaha imah eser shanim velo yalda yotzi ve\u8217'yitein kesubah} \u8212- he must divorce her and pay her {\i kesubah}. The requirement to pay the {\i kesubah} carries its own legal weight. This is a society in which divorce has a fault structure: whether the woman receives her {\i kesubah} depends in part on whether the fault for the dissolution lies with her or not. By requiring payment of the {\i kesubah}, the {\i baraita} is declaring that we do not presume the infertility to be her fault. As the {\i gemara} phrases it: {\i shema lo zachah le\u8217'hibanot mimenah} \u8212- perhaps it is he who has not merited to have children from her. The fault, if any, is assigned to him, and she collects her {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads directly to the question of whether the language {\i yotzi} \u8212- he must divorce her \u8212- implies actual compulsion. The {\i Tosafot} addresses this immediately, noting that at the end of tractate {\i Hamadir} there is a dispute among {\i amoraim} about whether a husband in this situation is actually forced to divorce or not, and concludes that even verbal compulsion may not apply. The word {\i yotzi} should be understood as expressing what he ought to do, not necessarily what {\i beit din} physically compels. This ties into a ruling of the {\i Rema} in {\i Shulchan Aruch} that in contemporary circumstances we do not in general compel divorce in cases of this nature, particularly given that Jewish communities no longer wield the autonomous coercive power they once did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abraham & Ten Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} derives the ten-year principle from the episode of {\i Avraham} and {\i Hagar}: {\i mi\u8217'ketz eser shanim le\u8217'sheves Avram be\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an} \u8212- after ten years of Avram\u8217's dwelling in the land of Canaan, Sarai gave him Hagar. The plain reading is that ten years had passed and something had to be done. This is taken as the textual basis \u8212- {\i zecher la\u8217'davar} rather than a full {\i ra\u8217'aya} \u8212- for the ten-year rule of our {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusions from Count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further inference is drawn from this source: since the {\i pasuk} specifies {\i Avram\u8217's dwelling in the land of Canaan}, time spent outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} does not count toward the ten years. {\i Rashi} explains that dwelling in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} itself may have been a cause preventing them from having children, and so years spent there cannot be used to measure a couple\u8217's fertility. The {\i gemara} generalizes this principle: {\i lefikach chalah hu o she\u8217'chalsa hi} \u8212- therefore, if he was ill during some period, or she was ill, or if both were imprisoned \u8212- {\i chavushim be\u8217'veis ha\u8217'asurim} \u8212- those periods do not count toward the ten years, since the absence of children can be attributed to an independent cause. This principle of excluding periods where external factors may explain the childlessness considerably narrows the practical application of the rule. One might extend the principle to other circumstances, such as periods of significant stress, though the {\i gemara} specifies only illness and imprisonment explicitly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Patriarchs\u8217' Sterility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isaac vs Abraham\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a pointed question: why derive the ten-year rule specifically from {\i Avraham}? Could we not equally derive it from {\i Yitzchak}? As the {\i pasuk} records, {\i Yitzchak} was forty years old when he married {\i Rivka}, and sixty years old when she gave birth \u8212- a gap of twenty years. Since he appears to have prayed for children only shortly before the birth, it seems he waited twenty years before taking any action. Why not learn from {\i Yitzchak} that twenty years is the relevant threshold?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes that according to the {\i midrash}, {\i Rivka} was only three years old at the time of the marriage, which would mean that {\i Yitzchak} naturally had to wait until she was of childbearing age \u8212- perhaps around twelve \u8212- and from that point the twenty years reduce to something closer to eight or nine, which fits comfortably within the ten-year framework. However, the {\i gemara} itself does not rely on that {\i midrash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Instead, the {\i gemara} offers a different answer: {\i Yitzchak akar hayah} \u8212- {\i Yitzchak} himself was sterile. Since the problem was with him, there was no remedial action he could take \u8212- he could not simply marry another woman and hope for children from her. His situation was categorically different from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case, in which we are uncertain whose fertility is compromised. This raises an important question that the {\i gemara} will address later: if a husband is himself the {\i akar}, does that relieve him of all obligation? The answer will involve the question of whether women are themselves obligated in {\i pru u\u8217'rvu} and whether a wife has an independent right to demand children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} continues: if that objection applies to {\i Yitzchak}, does it not equally apply to {\i Avraham}? {\i Avraham nami akar hayah} \u8212- {\i Avraham} too was sterile, as the {\i gemara} will demonstrate. Why then can we learn the ten-year principle from {\i Avraham}? The answer is that the twenty years associated with {\i Yitzchak} is mentioned in the {\i Torah} not to teach a halachic principle at all, but to provide chronological data needed for an entirely different purpose. {\i Chizkiyah bar Abba} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan} taught: why does the {\i Torah} enumerate {\i Yishmael}\u8217's years? In order to establish the chronology of {\i Yaakov}\u8217's life \u8212- to calculate how old {\i Yaakov} was when he fled from home and when he returned, and to track the broader family history. The {\i psukim} that list {\i Yitzchak}\u8217's age at marriage and at the birth of his children exist to enable those calculations, not to teach anything about a waiting period. By {\i Avraham}, however, there is no parallel chronological need; the only reason to highlight that {\i Hagar} was introduced specifically after ten years of dwelling in {\i Eretz Yisrael} is to teach the halachic principle itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isaac\u8217's Sterility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} establishes {\i Yitzchak}\u8217's sterility from the language of the {\i pasuk}: {\i vaye\u8217'etar Yitzchak l\u8217'Hashem lenochach ishto} \u8212- {\i Yitzchak} importuned God {\i lenochach} his wife. Why does the {\i pasuk} say {\i lenochach} rather than simply {\i al ishto}, for his wife? The {\i Maharsha} explains this beautifully. The word {\i lenochach} implies a parallel or facing position \u8212- suggesting that both {\i Yitzchak} and {\i Rivka} were sterile, {\i melamed she\u8217'shneihem akurim hayu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if both were sterile, why does it say {\i vaye\u8217'etar lo Hashem} \u8212- God responded to him \u8212- rather than {\i vaye\u8217'etar lahem}, to them? If {\i Yitzchak} too was sterile, then {\i Rivka} was also praying for him, and God should have responded to both of them. The {\i Maharsha} resolves this with an elegant reading. The {\i gemara} invokes the principle that {\i kol hamitpallel al chavero hu ne\u8217'eneh techilah} \u8212- one who prays for another is answered first. {\i Yitzchak} was praying for {\i Rivka}, and so he would be answered. But if {\i Yitzchak} is also sterile, then {\i Rivka} must also be praying for him \u8212- and then the response should address both. The fact that it says only {\i lo}, to him, requires explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i lefi she\u8217'eino domeh tefilat tzaddik ben tzaddik litefilat tzaddik ben rasha} \u8212- the prayer of a {\i tzaddik} who is the son of a {\i tzaddik} is not equivalent to the prayer of a {\i tzaddik} who is the son of a {\i rasha}. {\i Yitzchak} was the son of {\i Avraham}; {\i Rivka} was the daughter of {\i Betuel}. Both prayed, but it was {\i Yitzchak}\u8217's prayer that was primarily answered, because of the greater potency of his lineage. This gives rise to a curious stylistic observation: the standard Aramaic idiom {\i eino domeh X le-Y} typically presents the second term as the more important or potent \u8212- as in the famous teaching that in the place where {\i ba\u8217'alei teshuvah} stand, {\i tzaddikim gemurim} cannot stand, where the {\i ba\u8217'al teshuvah} is the second term. Here, however, the more potent prayer is clearly that of the {\i tzaddik ben tzaddik}, who is mentioned first. This is a striking inversion of the usual rhetorical structure, and it remains difficult to resolve satisfactorily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Sterility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yitzchak} asks: {\i mipnei mah hayu avoteinu akurim} \u8212- why were our forefathers sterile? The answer: {\i mipnei she\u8217'Hakadosh Baruch Hu mit\u8217'aveh l\u8217'tefilatan shel tzadikim} \u8212- because God desires the prayers of the righteous. The pattern is strikingly consistent throughout the narratives of the {\i avot} and {\i imahot}: in each generation, those whose fertility flows easily \u8212- {\i Nachor} with his twelve sons, {\i Yishmael} with his twelve princes, {\i Esav} with his multitude of descendants \u8212- are peripheral figures. Those at the center of the covenant struggle intensely. The {\i gemara} frames this as God\u8217's deliberate desire to draw forth prayer, though the theme clearly invites deeper reflection: the covenantal path is not one of effortless blessing but of hard-won closeness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prayer as Pitchfork\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yitzchak} extends this idea with a {\i mashal}: {\i lamah nimshelah tefilatan shel tzadikim ke\u8217'atar} \u8212- why is the prayer of the righteous compared to a pitchfork? Because {\i Yitzchak} importuned God using the root {\i vaye\u8217'etar}, which shares its root with {\i atar}, a pitchfork. Just as a pitchfork turns grain from one place to another, so the prayers of the righteous turn God\u8217's attributes from {\i middat rogez} \u8212- the attribute of anger \u8212- to {\i middat rachamim} \u8212- the attribute of compassion. Prayer is not a plea into a fixed order; it is a force that reorients the divine stance entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abraham & Sarah Tumtumim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now substantiates {\i Avraham}\u8217's sterility. {\i Rabbi Ami} teaches: {\i Avraham ve\u8217'Sarah tumtumim hayu} \u8212- {\i Avraham} and {\i Sarah} were originally {\i tumtumim}, individuals whose genitalia were initially undifferentiated or concealed beneath a membrane, rendering their sex anatomically indeterminate at birth. Even after the condition is resolved, such individuals are presumed to face ongoing challenges with fertility. This is derived from the {\i psukim} in {\i Yeshayahu}: {\i habitu el tzur chutzavtem ve\u8217'el makevet bor nukartem} \u8212- look to the rock from which you were hewn and the hollow from which you were chiseled \u8212- followed immediately by {\i habitu el Avraham avichem ve\u8217'el Sarah techolelchem} \u8212- look to {\i Avraham} your father and {\i Sarah} who gave birth to you. The language of chiseling and hollowing is read as describing a process of physical completion, of forms not yet fully expressed requiring further formation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava bar Avuha} adds a further dimension: {\i Sarah Imeinu ayalonit hayata} \u8212- our matriarch {\i Sarah} was an {\i ayalonit}, a category of woman distinguished from a simple {\i akara} (infertile woman) by specific physical characteristics indicating a congenitally different physical constitution. The {\i pasuk} states: {\i vatehi Sarai akara ein lah valad} \u8212- {\i Sarai} was barren, she had no child. The apparent redundancy \u8212- if she was barren, obviously she had no child \u8212- is read as adding something: {\i afilu beit valad ein lah} \u8212- she did not even have a uterus. This goes beyond ordinary infertility and places her in the category of {\i ayalonit}. And all of this, of course, magnifies the {\i nes}: that from two individuals with such profound physical challenges, a child was ultimately born.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Generational Adjustments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earlier Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having anchored the ten-year rule in {\i Avraham}\u8217's experience, the {\i gemara} immediately confronts an obvious tension: {\i Avraham} lived 175 years; the fertility window of his generation was proportionally vast. Does the ten-year standard derived from him apply without modification to generations with dramatically shorter lifespans? {\i Rav Yehuda} in the name of {\i Rav} rules: {\i lo shanu ela bedorot harishonim}, the ten-year standard was stated only for earlier generations whose years were many \u8212- {\i sheshnoteihem merubot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For later generations, with shorter lifespans and narrower windows of fertility, the obligation comes much sooner: {\i shtei shanim u\u8217'mechtza} \u8212- two and a half years. The rationale is {\i keneged shelosha iburim} \u8212- corresponding to three pregnancy cycles. Three pregnancies of nine months each yields twenty-seven months, plus time between pregnancies, approximating thirty months or two and a half years. The underlying logic is that three cycles without a successful pregnancy is enough to trigger concern and action. The number of years is contextually determined by the lifespan and fertility window of the generation; the structure of the obligation \u8212- that after a sufficient period without children, something must be done \u8212- remains constant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Year View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} in the name of {\i Rav Nachman} dissents: the threshold for later generations is {\i shalosh shanim} \u8212- three full years \u8212- {\i keneged shalosh pekidot}, corresponding to three divine visitations. The {\i gemara} invokes the teaching that {\i berosh Hashanah nifkedu Sarah Rachel ve\u8217'Channah} \u8212- on Rosh Hashanah, {\i Sarah}, {\i Rachel}, and {\i Channah} were remembered by God and their fertility was granted. The three-year figure gives a couple three Rosh Hashanahs \u8212- three full cycles of divine remembrance and potential intervention \u8212- before concluding that conception is not forthcoming.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Years Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} then dismisses both of these modifications with a decisive argument: {\i leis lehu lehani klalei} \u8212- neither of those principles applies. Who authored our {\i mishnah}? {\i Rabbi}. And {\i Rabbi} himself lived at a time when people\u8217's years were already few \u8212- as the {\i pasuk} says, {\i yemei shnoteinu bahem shiv\u8217'im shanah}, our years are seventy. Yet {\i Rabbi} codified ten years in the {\i mishnah}, not two and a half and not three. Therefore, {\i Rabbi}\u8217's ruling in our {\i mishnah} is authoritative and stands as the standard for all subsequent generations: ten years is the threshold. The attempted revision based on generational lifespan is rejected, because {\i Rabbi} himself bridged the gap by writing ten years into the {\i mishnah} for people of shorter lifespan.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fault & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncertainty of Cause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the question of why the husband must pay the {\i kesubah} upon divorcing his wife after ten childless years. One might argue that since at least one of them is clearly not fertile \u8212- the absence of children proves that \u8212- the woman should bear the burden of proof that it was his problem rather than hers, in accordance with the principle {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'ayah}, the one seeking to collect from another must prove the claim. Why do we default to protecting her {\i kesubah} without requiring her to establish that the fault lies with him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Pru U\u8217'Rvu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i kivan d\u8217'lo amifkada a pru u\u8217'rvu lo m\u8217'ancha} \u8212- since she is not commanded in {\i pru u\u8217'rvu}, she is not punished through it. If we view the absence of children as a form of divine punishment \u8212- an inability to fulfill one\u8217's obligation \u8212- and she bears no such obligation, then we look to see who is being penalized by this absence. That must be the man, who does bear the obligation. Therefore we assign the inability to have children to him rather than to her, and she collects her {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning is admittedly difficult to receive simply, since women are the ones who conceive, carry, and give birth \u8212- the entire physical experience of fertility and its absence falls on them in the most immediate way. To say they are not suffering through it because it was not their {\i mitzvah} seems jarring. One possible reading, offered in the course of discussion, is not that we are making an objective metaphysical claim that she suffers no consequence \u8212- but that {\i halachically} we choose to assign the fault to him, in a way that works entirely in her favor: she can remarry, the new husband can hope to fulfill {\i pru u\u8217'rvu}, and she retains her {\i kesubah}. Whether or not that is precisely the {\i gemara}\u8217's intent, it reflects a legal structure that consistently protects her interests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Abba Bar Zavda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a challenge to this framework. The rabbis had said to {\i Rabbi Abba bar Zavda}: you have had one child, but now your wife is presumably past the point of bearing children \u8212- why not remarry and attempt to have more? He replied: {\i zakai havah li mikamaita} \u8212- had I merited it, I would have had children with my first wife. Since I did not, I will not have children with a second wife either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does this prove? If he is saying that the infertility lies with him definitively, that seems to undermine the {\i gemara}\u8217's framework, which holds that perhaps a different pairing will work. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i hasam de-chovah ka medachi lehu de-rabbanan} \u8212- there he was merely deflecting the rabbis. His case was in fact unique: {\i Rav Abba bar Zavdi} had become sterile as a direct result of remaining in {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's {\i shiur} without excusing himself to relieve himself, a situation that caused him physical damage. The sterility was therefore clearly traceable to him as the cause, unlike the ordinary case of a couple who have not had children and where attribution remains uncertain. In that unusual case there was no sense in remarrying; the problem was entirely and unambiguously his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Ground Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's full framework is thus revealed as occupying a carefully constructed middle space. On one side: she is entitled to her {\i kesubah}, because we cannot say with certainty that it was her problem. On the other side: he is still obligated to try to have children, so he must divorce her (or marry another woman), because we cannot say with certainty that it was his problem. The two inferences run simultaneously and in tension: {\i lo zachah l\u8217'hibanot mimenah} \u8212- it did not work out between them \u8212- a formulation that assigns no blame to either party individually, but maintains the imperative of {\i pru u\u8217'rvu} on the man while protecting the woman financially.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is a strikingly sophisticated legal position. It protects her right to remarry without the stigma of presumed infertility; it protects her financial entitlement; and simultaneously it preserves his religious obligation and requires him to continue pursuing it. The situation is not that she is at fault, and it is not that he is definitively at fault \u8212- it is that together they have not succeeded, and he must try again.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two vs Three Times\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds that while she may marry a second husband after the first marriage ends childlessly, a third man who has not yet fulfilled {\i pru u\u8217'rvu} should not marry her: {\i sheni in, shelishi lo} \u8212- the second is permitted, the third is not. After two marriages without children, we begin to presume that the infertility may lie with her. The {\i gemara} notes that this position belongs to {\i Rabbi} \u8212- {\i matnitin mani Rabbi hi} \u8212- and a dispute between {\i Rabbi} and {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} about precisely this question is embedded in the broader {\i sugya} to follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brit Milah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} introduces the dispute in the context of {\i brit milah}: if a family\u8217's first son died following circumcision, and the second son also died following circumcision, {\i Rabbi} rules that the third son should not be circumcised \u8212- {\i shelishi lo simol}. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} rules that the third son should still be circumcised, and it is only if that child too dies that the fourth \u8212- {\i revi\u8217'i lo simol} \u8212- should not be. A complicating {\i baraita} appears to reverse the attributions, and the {\i gemara} cannot readily resolve which position belongs to whom from the text alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting at the outset why this is not simply a debate about what constitutes a {\i chazakah}. A {\i chazakah} means something is definitively established as a pattern. Here the question is more nuanced: at what point must one act out of concern \u8212- not certainty \u8212- that a pattern exists? After one death from circumcision, no one is suggesting we abandon the {\i mitzvah} entirely, even though the evidence of danger is already present. The question is when the threshold of concern becomes actionable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Sisters Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To help resolve the dispute over attribution, the {\i gemara} cites a {\i ma\u8217'aseh} reported by {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Abba} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}. In the city of {\i Tzipori} there were four sisters. The first gave birth to a son, circumcised him, and the boy died. The second and third did likewise, with the same fatal result. When the fourth sister came before {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}, he told her: {\i al tamoli} \u8212- do not circumcise your son. This establishes that {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} required three occurrences before ruling against the {\i brit milah} \u8212- and accordingly, he holds three is the operative number.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects: perhaps {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} was only responding to the situation as presented. Maybe if the third sister had come to him after only two deaths, he would have ruled against circumcision even then. The {\i gemara} responds that if so, there would be nothing at all to learn from {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Abba}\u8217's story \u8212- it would communicate no principle. Therefore, the story must be teaching something \u8212- and that teaching is the content of the next segment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Genetic Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Abba}\u8217's story does teach is this: {\i achayos machazkos} \u8212- sisters establish a {\i chazakah} for one another. Information about what happened in one sister\u8217's family is halachically relevant when evaluating risk for another sister. The reason is immediately evident: hemophilia \u8212- which is transmitted through females and expressed in males \u8212- runs through the maternal line exactly in this fashion. The sisters are carriers; their sons are at risk. The {\i gemara}\u8217's teaching that one draws inferences from sister to sister maps precisely onto the genetics of hemophilia, and it is almost universally understood that this is the medical reality underpinning these {\i gemarot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is no small {\i chiddush}. It means that evidence need not be derived from the individual alone; family medical patterns are legally cognizable in halachic decision-making.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Family Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} extends the principle: {\i lo yissa adam isha lo mi-mishpachas nichpin ve-lo mi-mishpachas metzora\u8217'in} \u8212- a man should not marry a woman from a family with epilepsy or from a family with leprosy \u8212- {\i ve-hu de-itchazek tlata zimni}, provided the condition has occurred three times in that family. Even if the woman herself has not manifested the condition, what has occurred in her relatives is relevant to the assessment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching opens onto a painful contemporary reality. The stigmatization of families in certain communities because of known or rumored health conditions \u8212- epilepsy in particular \u8212- creates enormous social pressure and suffering. The fact that people in these communities sometimes travel to distant hospitals so that no one from their own community will recognize them in the same ward reflects both the weight the community places on this information and the devastation that exposure can cause. The institution of {\i Dor Yeshorim} \u8212- genetic testing for compatibility \u8212- was designed precisely to address this: results are never reported to individuals directly; couples are only told whether they are or are not compatible with each other, so that no individual or family is labeled or stigmatized. The {\i gemara}\u8217's framework provides the halachic foundation, even as its contemporary application requires enormous sensitivity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye & Choma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yom Kippur Incident\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a dramatic episode. A story occurred before {\i Rabbi Yochanan} in the synagogue of {\i Ma\u8217'on} \u8212- and it was Yom Kippur that fell on Shabbat. In that setting, circumcisions were being performed, and one after another, children died following their {\i brit milah}. {\i Be-yoma di-kippura she-chal lihyos be-Shabbos} \u8212- the stakes could hardly have been higher. The first child died, the second died, and the third case was brought before {\i Rabbi Yochanan}. He ruled: {\i lechi u\u8217'moli} \u8212- go ahead and circumcise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} challenged the reporter of this ruling, {\i Rav Yitzchak bar Yosef}: {\i chazi de\u8217'kashris issura ve\u8217'sakanta} \u8212- notice that on your testimony you are permitting both {\i issur} and {\i sakana}. The {\i issur} is performing {\i brit milah} on Shabbat and Yom Kippur when, if the circumcision is medically contraindicated due to risk to life, it constitutes {\i chilul Shabbat}. The {\i sakana} is exposing the infant himself to a potentially life-threatening procedure. This is not a trivial ruling; it carries enormous consequences either way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} nevertheless noted that this ruling follows {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}\u8217's position \u8212- that three cases are required before stopping, not two. He then added, {\i samach alei Abaye} \u8212- he relied on this ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Marries Choma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reliance takes a personal and startling turn. {\i Abaye} went and married a woman named {\i Choma}, described as the daughter of {\i Issi} the son of {\i Rav Yitzchak} the son of {\i Rav Yehuda}. What was {\i Choma}\u8217's history? {\i Rechava of Pumbedisa} had married her and died. {\i Rav Yitzchak} the son of {\i Rabbah bar bar Chana} had married her and also died. Two previous husbands had died after marrying her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} proceeded to marry her anyway, and he too died \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'shachiv}. {\i Rava} responded with the observation: {\i umi ika de\u8217'avid uvda be\u8217'nafshei ki hai} \u8212- where do you find someone who experiments on himself like this? One does not use oneself as the test case for a ruling of this nature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The episode thus raises the question of the credibility of the evidence {\i Abaye} relied upon. The {\i gemara} points out that {\i Abaye} had based himself on a report from {\i Yitzchak Sumcha} about what {\i Rabbi Yochanan} had ruled \u8212- and {\i Abaye} himself had said that {\i Avin} is reliable when reporting in {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's name because {\i Avin yeshno be\u8217'chazarah} \u8212- he reviews his learning or goes back to check whether {\i Rabbi Yochanan} has revised his view. {\i Yitzchak Sumcha eino be\u8217'chazarah} \u8212- {\i Yitzchak Sumcha} does not do this, and so his reports of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's rulings are unreliable. {\i Abaye}\u8217's reliance on {\i Yitzchak Sumcha}\u8217's version of this ruling was therefore questionable from the start.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, the {\i gemara} asks: even granting the authenticity of the ruling, {\i Rabbi} and {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} disagree specifically {\i be\u8217'inyan milah} \u8212- in the context of circumcision. Does that debate extend to {\i nisuin}, to marriage? Presumably the causal mechanism in the two cases differs entirely. The {\i gemara} resolves this: yes, they do in fact debate the marriage case as well. A {\i baraita} states: if a woman\u8217's first husband died, and her second husband died, {\i Rabbi} rules she should not be married to a third man \u8212- {\i lo sinasei}. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} rules the third may marry her; it is only the fourth who should not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Causation Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks what makes sense as the causal mechanism in each case. By {\i brit milah}, the explanation is clear: {\i ika mishpacha de\u8217'rafi dama ve\u8217'ika mishpacha de\u8217'kamit dama} \u8212- some families have loose, free-flowing blood, others have more viscous blood. A bleeding disorder running in the family \u8212- precisely hemophilia \u8212- explains why circumcision proves fatal for the children of this family. The cause-and-effect relationship is medically coherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what explains the case of the woman whose husbands die? {\i Rav Mordechai} in the name of {\i Bibi of Agronya} in the name of {\i Rav Huna} offers one answer: {\i mayan gorem} \u8212- the sexual union itself is somehow the cause, suggesting perhaps some form of transmissible disease through intimacy. {\i Rav Ashi} offers a different answer: {\i mazal gorem} \u8212- it is simply her {\i mazal}, her fate, that causes harm to those who are bound to her, with no medical mechanism required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference between these two positions: if a fianc\u233 ? died during {\i erusin}, before any physical union occurred, the {\i mazal} theory would treat that death as equally significant \u8212- being betrothed to her is enough to be subject to her {\i mazal}. But if the cause is the sexual union itself, a death before consummation would be irrelevant. Similarly, if a husband fell from a tree and died \u8212- an obvious accident \u8212- that would count toward the pattern under the {\i mazal} theory, since bad fate could manifest in any form of death, but would not count under the {\i mayan} theory, since the death had no connection to the physical relationship. The example of the man who fell from a tree is a vivid illustration that two occurrences of something do not by themselves establish causation \u8212- the cause may be entirely independent of what they appear to have in common.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Differentiated Rulings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} has been circling the question of what the operative {\i halacha} is. {\i Rava} reports having asked {\i Rav Yosef}: do we rule like {\i Rabbi}? {\i Rav Yosef} said yes. Do we rule like {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}? {\i Rav Yosef} again said yes. {\i Rava} thought he was being mocked \u8212- {\i achyuchei achyuch bi} \u8212- but {\i Rav Yosef} clarified: {\i stamei hi} \u8212- it is a {\i stam} ruling, and the correct answer is that we rule like each of them in different domains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage & Lashing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i nisuin} \u8212- marriage \u8212- and for {\i malkiyos} \u8212- lashes \u8212- we rule like {\i Rabbi}: two occurrences are sufficient to require action. By marriage, this means that if a woman has been married twice and both husbands died, a third man who has not yet fulfilled {\i pru u\u8217'rvu} should not marry her. By lashes, the {\i mishnah} rules that if someone received {\i malkos} for a transgression and repeated the same transgression a second time, {\i beit din} does not simply administer another round of lashes and release him \u8212- it places him in the {\i kippah} and feeds him barley until he dies. This is already triggered after the second offense. The language there \u8212- {\i beit din konasin oso la-kippah u-ma\u8217'achilin oso seorin ad she-tibaka kreiso ve-yumas} \u8212- is arresting. {\i Tosafot} suggests that the phrase about the stomach bursting is hyperbolic, meaning merely that they confine him and give him coarse barley rations; but in either reading, drastic action follows the second offense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Menstrual & Ox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i vestos} \u8212- menstrual patterns \u8212- and for {\i shor ha-muad} \u8212- the habitually goring ox \u8212- we rule like {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}: three occurrences are required. Regarding {\i vestos}, the {\i mishnah} rules that {\i ein ishah kova\u8217'as lah veses ad she-tikba\u8217'enah shalosh pe\u8217'amim} \u8212- a woman does not establish a fixed menstrual cycle until it has occurred three times. Similarly, {\i einah mitaheres min ha-veses ad she-ta\u8217'akrenah shalosh pe\u8217'amim} \u8212- once a regular cycle is established, it is not considered changed until three consecutive cycles have diverged from it. The halachic implications are significant: if a woman has a fixed {\i veset} and the time of her expected period arrives, she is treated as if she has become a {\i niddah} even without a physical check. That presumption requires a three-time foundation. Similarly, a {\i shor ha-muad} \u8212- an ox whose owner is liable for full damages rather than half \u8212- attains that status only after {\i sheyeidu bo shalosh pe\u8217'amim}, three witnessed gorings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs Three Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The best resolution of this apparent contradiction \u8212- ruling like {\i Rabbi} in some areas and {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} in others \u8212- is that the two thresholds address two categorically different questions. Two occurrences are enough to generate concern and require a response. They establish that we cannot dismiss what has happened as a pure coincidence, and they obligate action. Three occurrences, however, establish something stronger: that the pattern is fixed, regular, and essentially certain. This is the difference between {\i choshesh} \u8212- being apprehensive \u8212- and {\i chazakah} \u8212- a legally established presumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Pnei Yehoshua} and other {\i achronim} articulate this distinction clearly. For {\i vestos}, we are not merely saying a woman should be concerned about bleeding on a certain day \u8212- we presume she has bled. That is a full legal presumption, requiring three-time establishment. For the {\i shor ha-muad}, the ox is not merely one to watch carefully \u8212- his legal identity changes; he becomes a definitively dangerous animal whose owner bears full liability. That too requires three. But for marriage and for punishing a repeat offender, two times is enough to act: the evidence of danger or habitual wrongdoing is sufficient to respond, even before the pattern is definitively established. This is analogous to the {\i Chofetz Chaim}\u8217's distinction in {\i hilchos lashon hara} between believing a report and being {\i choshesh} for it \u8212- between accepting information as true and treating it as warranting concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case: a woman who has gone through two marriages of ten years each without children may not be married by a third man who has not yet fulfilled {\i pru u\u8217'rvu}. Yet the second husband who divorced her must still pay her {\i kesubah}, because we have not definitively established that the infertility lies with her. These two rulings, taken together, define precisely the space between two-time concern and three-time certainty: there is enough evidence to prohibit the third marriage, but not enough to deprive her of her {\i kesubah}. That is exactly the distinction between ruling like {\i Rabbi} in matters of marriage \u8212- where two times suffices to require action \u8212- and what it would mean to have established her infertility with the certainty of a {\i chazakah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of how these principles apply today \u8212- particularly in the case of a woman whose multiple husbands have died, where the causal theories of {\i mayan gorem} and {\i mazal gorem} are both medically elusive \u8212- remains open and, as the {\i gemara} itself demonstrates through the case of {\i Abaye}, requires considerable care.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 65\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Marriages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf under discussion is 65, and we pick up where the previous discussion left off. The sugya concerns a woman who has been married to a man for ten years without bearing children and is subsequently divorced. The situation carries an interesting internal tension: on one hand, there is a presumption that the infertility is not necessarily her fault, such that she may remarry a man who still needs to fulfill {\i peru u\u8217'revu}; on the other hand, there is a countervailing assumption that she may be a contributing factor, which is why the first husband is permitted \u8212- indeed encouraged \u8212- to take another wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanu Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita} beginning {\i tanu rabbanan}, found four lines from the top of 65a. The case runs as follows: she married the first man and had no children, married the second man and still had no children \u8212- that is two marriages without children, which, as we have seen, is the subject of the dispute between {\i Rebbe} and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel regarding how many instances are required to establish a {\i chazakah}. The {\i baraita} then rules: {\i vishlishi lo tinasei ela lemi sheyeish lo banim} \u8212- she should not marry a third man unless that man has already fulfilled the {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru u\u8217'revu}. Following {\i Rebbe}\u8217's position, after two such marriages we must at minimum be concerned that the problem lies with her. Therefore, if she does marry a man who has no children, {\i taitzei belo kesubah} \u8212- she is divorced without receiving her {\i kesubah}, since it has effectively been established that the infertility is her problem, and that husband must now pursue having children elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Marriage Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\i ibaya} is then posed: suppose she proceeds to marry the third man, waits the requisite ten years, and still has no children. At this point, after three successive childless marriages, the case that the infertility lies with her seems firmly established. The question is whether the first two husbands \u8212- who did pay her {\i kesubah} at the time of their divorces, since the fault had not yet been conclusively identified as hers \u8212- may now return to her and demand the {\i kesubah} money back: {\i mahu delitve\u8217'u hanach kamaei, mi matzu amru lah, iglai milta de\u8217'at hu digramat} \u8212- it has now been revealed that you were the cause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Return Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The thrust of this question is really about the burden of proof. Throughout these cases, the woman appears to be presumed entitled to her {\i kesubah}, placing the burden on the husband to establish otherwise. The question is whether newly available evidence \u8212- the failure of the third marriage \u8212- retroactively shifts that burden and allows the first two husbands to recover what they paid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Body Weakened Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i mistavra matzi amrah lehu hashta hu de\u8217'achash} \u8212- the more reasonable conclusion is that she can respond to them, \u8220"It is only now that my body has grown weaker.\u8221" Perhaps twenty years have passed since the first marriage, ten since the second. Even if she was a partial cause earlier, or no cause at all, the passage of time and the weakening of her constitution may explain the current pattern. The {\i Gemara} accepts this defense: {\i mistavra matzi amrah lehu hashta hu de\u8217'achash}. She may say this to them, and they cannot recover the {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fourth Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fertile Fourth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i ibaya} follows: suppose she marries a fourth man and now, with him, she does have children. So she was fertile all along. The question is now the mirror image of the previous one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demand Third\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 May she go back to the third husband \u8212- who, recall, did not pay her {\i kesubah} precisely because by that point there was sufficient grounds to presume the infertility was hers \u8212- and demand her {\i kesubah} now that her fertility has been demonstrated?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtikosayich Yafah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers with a striking formulation: {\i shtikosayich yafah midibura\u8217'yich} \u8212- your silence is better than your speech. The reason is that if she presses her claim against husband number three, asserting that she was always fertile and that the failure of that marriage was his fault, he can turn around and say: {\i ana ada\u8217'ata de\u8217'hachi lo garashtich} \u8212- had I known you were fertile, I never would have divorced you. If that argument succeeds, it would mean the {\i get} was given in error, invalidating it retroactively. And if the {\i get} is invalid, then the child she bore with husband number four is a {\i mamzer}. The stakes of pressing this claim are therefore catastrophic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Raises\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa objects: {\i ee ishtaka ishtakin? anan mi shatkinan? nimtza get batel u\u8217'vanim mamzerim} \u8212- if the concern about the {\i get}\u8217's validity is real, how can we simply stay silent? We ourselves \u8212- the {\i beit din}, the {\i Gemara}, everyone \u8212- cannot simply keep quiet if there is a genuine question about whether the divorce was valid and the children are legitimate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Changed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i ella amrinan hashta hu d\u8217'avraya} \u8212- we say that it is only now that her condition has improved. That is, we adopt the position that her fertility is a new development, not a revealed truth about her condition at the time of the third marriage. This is consistent with the earlier principle that she could claim her body weakened over time; the converse applies here as well \u8212- we will say her body strengthened or her circumstances changed, and we will not revisit the factual premise of the original divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Retroactive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is that we do not retroactively revise legal determinations based on later-emerging evidence. The divorce was valid on the facts as they were understood at the time, and we judge the case as it stood then. Subsequent developments are treated as changes in status, not revelations of hidden prior truth. This principle operates in everyone\u8217's best interest: the woman avoids the catastrophic outcome of her children being declared {\i mamzerim}, and the legal system avoids the destabilizing effect of constantly re-litigating settled matters whenever new evidence surfaces.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this principle extends to business or damages contexts \u8212- whether claims could be revisited whenever a counter-assumption cannot be articulated. The broader answer is grounded in the concept of {\i dvarim she-balev einam dvarim} and the general rule that legal determinations are made on the basis of the best available assumptions at the time, without regularly revising them based on facts that emerge only later.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Infertility Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Blame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the classic scenario: {\i hu amar minei, v\u8217'hi amra mineh} \u8212- he says the infertility problem is hers, and she says it is his. This is presumably the standard case underlying the entire discussion, and the {\i halacha} would seem to be that she receives her {\i kesubah} since the fault is not established. Tosafot raises a number of complicated questions about what precisely distinguishes this scenario from earlier cases, but the {\i Gemara} here takes a specific direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yora K\u8217'chetz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami rules: {\i dvarim she-beino l\u8217'veina ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- in matters that are between the two of them privately, she is believed. The {\i Gemara} then explains the mechanism: {\i hi kama leh b\u8217'yora k\u8217'chetz, hu lo kama leh b\u8217'yora k\u8217'chetz} \u8212- she is in a better position to know whether he ejaculates with the force of an arrow, {\i yora k\u8217'chetz}, than he is during the act itself. If she is claiming that the cause of the infertility is a weakness in his ejaculation, we credit her testimony because she has better access to that information than he does. This raises a natural question: why must this be the specific claim? Why isn\u8217't a general assertion that it may be his problem sufficient? We have already said that since it may be his problem, she receives her {\i kesubah} \u8212- the language {\i lo zachah l\u8217'hibanos mimenah} suggests the possibility is always open. This is a question that Tosafot grapples with, though the discussion there is complicated enough not to pursue here in full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fertility Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The husband then proposes: {\i eizal insiv iteta v\u8217'ivdok nafshai} \u8212- I will go ahead and marry another woman and see if I have children with her. If I do, that will prove the problem is hers and she should not receive her {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami responds: {\i be-zo yotzi ve-yitein ketubah} \u8212- if he wishes to pursue that course, he must first divorce his wife and give her the {\i kesubah} before marrying another woman. The {\i Gemara} frames this in terms of a broader principle: {\i she\u8217'ani omer kol hanosei isha al ishto yotzi v\u8217'yiten ketubah} \u8212- anyone who marries another woman while still married to his first wife, at least if the first wife protests, must give the first wife a {\i get} and pay her {\i kesubah}. The first wife has the right to object to a second marriage, and if the husband insists on proceeding, that objection entitles her to a compelled divorce with {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Polygamy Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami Monogamy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami\u8217's position thus treats the first wife\u8217's protest as legally effective: if she does not want him to take another wife, he cannot do so while remaining married to her. If he insists, he must divorce her with her {\i kesubah} first. This is not merely a moral preference but a legal entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Polygamy OK\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees: {\i nosei adam kama nashim al ishto ve-hu d\u8217'is lei l\u8217'mizan ninhu} \u8212- a man is entitled to marry multiple women even alongside his existing wife, provided he has the means to support them all. On Rava\u8217's view, the first wife\u8217's protest does not carry legal force sufficient to prevent a second marriage; the only operative constraint is economic capacity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bavli-Yerushalmi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This disagreement is not merely a technical {\i halachic} dispute but reflects a deeper question about whether polygamy is regarded as fundamentally incompatible with the first wife\u8217's rights or as a permissible institution subject only to practical constraints. As noted, this maps quite neatly onto the broader divide between {\i Bavel} and {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and historically between Sephardic and Ashkenazic practice, which in turn tracks whether these communities were embedded in polygamous or monogamous surrounding cultures. It is worth noting that even in polygamous cultures, multiple wives were practically possible only for wealthy men who could support them \u8212- which is precisely the limiting condition Rava articulates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i aguna} question hovers in the background here as well: in cases where the husband refuses to give a {\i get} while pursuing or maintaining another relationship, the question of whether {\i beit din} has the authority and will to actually compel the divorce and {\i kesubah} payment is distinct from what {\i halacha} says ought to happen. It is also worth noting \u8212- and this was confirmed in the course of discussion \u8212- that Rabbi Ami was from {\i Eretz Yisrael}, which tracks perfectly: Rabbi Ami\u8217's more restrictive view of polygamy aligns with the {\i Eretz Yisrael} tradition, while Rava\u8217's permissive view aligns with the Babylonian tradition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women Sue Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miscarriage Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to a new set of cases in which it is the woman who is suing for divorce. {\i Hu amar apalta b\u8217'go eser ve\u8217'hi amra lo apaliti} \u8212- he claims that she had a miscarriage during the ten-year period, such that he is not yet obligated to divorce her, since the ten-year clock has not run uninterrupted by any pregnancy. She denies having had a miscarriage and demands her divorce and {\i kesubah}. Note the curious posture here: she is the one pressing for divorce, presumably because she wishes to have the opportunity to remarry and have children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami rules: {\i af b\u8217'zu hi ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- here too she is believed. The reason: {\i d\u8217'ei issa d\u8217'hapila nafshah b\u8217'akarta lo machzaka} \u8212- if it were true that she had not miscarried, she would not be claiming that she went the full ten years without even a miscarriage. By claiming there was no miscarriage, she is effectively establishing herself as infertile, which is {\i halachically} not a definitive ruling \u8212- she can still remarry \u8212- but practically is damaging to her prospects. As one participant noted with some wry understatement, it is not good for a {\i shidduch}. She would not fabricate a claim that stigmatizes her in this way if it were not true, and therefore we believe her even though the claim serves her interest in the immediate legal context by entitling her to a divorce with {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Miscarry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hapila v\u8217'chazra v\u8217'hapila v\u8217'chazra v\u8217'hapila} \u8212- what if she miscarried three times? In that case she does not have a {\i chazakah} of being unable to conceive, but she may well have a {\i chazakah} of being unable to carry to term. The {\i Gemara} rules: {\i huchzaka l\u8217'nefalim} \u8212- she has established a {\i chazakah} of miscarrying. Rashi explains: {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten ketubah shema lo zachah libanos mimena} \u8212- he divorces her and gives the {\i kesubah}, because perhaps he has not been granted the merit of having children through her. The {\i kesubah} is still paid, because even with this established pattern it is not conclusively proven that the problem lies exclusively with her \u8212- it might be some combination \u8212- and so the {\i lo zachah l\u8217'hibanos mimena} framing keeps the possibility open that it is not solely her deficiency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Count Miscarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A dispute then arises: {\i hu amar hapila terei ve\u8217'hi amra telasa} \u8212- he claims she miscarried twice, not yet reaching the threshold for a {\i chazakah}, while she claims it was three times. This matters because three miscarriages triggers the {\i chazakah} and with it the obligation to divorce her and pay the {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Negates Self\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan bar Elazar reports: {\i uvda hava b\u8217'bei midrasha de\u8217'amru hi mehemana} \u8212- a case came before the {\i beit midrash} and they ruled she is believed. The reasoning is the same as before: {\i d\u8217'ee issa d\u8217'lo aplah nafshah b\u8217'nefalim lo machzeka} \u8212- if it were false, she would not be willing to establish herself as a woman who regularly miscarries. The {\i beit midrash} credits her precisely because the claim, while advantageous to her in the immediate dispute, carries a broader self-stigmatizing dimension that she would not invoke falsely. Underlying all of these cases is a consistent pattern: she is believed over him across the full range of disputes, in each instance because her claim contains an element that works against her broader interests. This functions as something like a {\i migo} or an analogous credibility-enhancing consideration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is particularly striking is the uniformity of this pattern across such varied scenarios \u8212- the source of the infertility, the occurrence of a miscarriage, the count of miscarriages \u8212- and the interesting question this raises about what distinguishes these cases from earlier {\i mishnayot} in which the husband\u8217's claim was credited. Tosafot engages this question, though the details are sufficiently complicated as to require separate treatment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Peru u\u8217'Revu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now transitions to the {\i mishnah} that concludes this {\i perek}: {\i haish metzuveh al peru u\u8217'revu aval lo haisha} \u8212- the man is commanded on {\i peru u\u8217'revu} and not the woman. This ruling is striking on its face, given that it is the woman who bears children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Commanded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka dissents, arguing that both are commanded, deriving this from the verse: {\i vayivarech osam Elokim vayomer lahem Elokim peru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- God blessed them and said to them, to both of them, be fruitful and multiply. This is a reasonable textual argument, and one that carries additional intuitive force given that it is the woman who carries and births children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Conquers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Illa, in the name of Rabbi Elazar the son of Rabbi Shimon, derives the limitation to the man from the continuation of the same verse: {\i u\u8217'milu et ha\u8217'aretz v\u8217'chivshuha} \u8212- fill the land and conquer it. {\i Ish darko lichvosh v\u8217'ein isha darkah lichvosh} \u8212- it is the way of the man to conquer and not the way of the woman. Since the verse concludes with an act \u8212- conquest \u8212- that characterizes the man, the rabbis conclude that even the earlier command was addressed to the man alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plural Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An objection is raised: {\i adderaba, v\u8217'chivshuha tarti mashma} \u8212- on the contrary, the word {\i v\u8217'chivshuha} is in the plural form, implying {\i atem tichvishu osah} \u8212- you, in the plural, shall conquer it. How then can the verse be read as addressing only the man?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kri u\u8217'Kesiv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak resolves this: {\i v\u8217'chivshah ksiv} \u8212- the word is actually written in the text without the {\i vav}, making it singular rather than plural. The {\i kri} \u8212- the traditional reading \u8212- has it in the plural, but the {\i kesiv} \u8212- the written spelling \u8212- renders it singular. On the basis of this {\i kri u\u8217'chesiv}, the verse is read as addressing the man alone. It is quite striking that a verse which in its plain reading appears to address both husband and wife is restricted to the man alone on the basis of a defective spelling \u8212- but that is how the {\i Gemara} proceeds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Exempt Why\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashevet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} having established the textual derivation, the question remains: what is the theological or conceptual rationale? One approach, grounded in the {\i Gemara} itself, connects {\i peru u\u8217'revu} to the broader principle of {\i lashevet} \u8212- settling and populating the world \u8212- derived from the verse {\i lo tohu b\u8217'ra\u8217'ah lashevet yetzarah}, God did not create the world to be void but to be inhabited. On this reading, {\i peru u\u8217'revu} is not merely about biological reproduction but about the larger project of {\i yishuv ha\u8217'olam}, building and sustaining civilization. That project, on a traditional division of roles, falls to the man as the one who goes out into the world to build society, while the woman remains in the domestic sphere. If this framing is correct, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru u\u8217'revu} as an aspect of settling the world naturally devolves upon the man. This is probably the most straightforward reading of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's own logic in context, even if it carries its own set of assumptions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Patriarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second explanation, noted by several {\i acharonim}, focuses on the verb {\i v\u8217'chivshuha} and reads it not as conquering the land but as the man\u8217's conquest, so to speak, in the act of sexual intercourse \u8212- connecting it to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's broader framing of the man as the initiator and active party in relations, with the woman cast in a more passive role ({\i karka olam}). On this reading, since children come through the man\u8217's active role in the sexual act, the {\i mitzvah} is assigned to him as the agent. More broadly, the patriarchal structure of the family as understood throughout {\i Yevamot} \u8212- where children belong to the father\u8217's household, not the mother\u8217's \u8212- renders the father the primary party in the act of having children. This is reflected in the very language of {\i Tanach}: {\i Avraham holid et Yitzchak} \u8212- Abraham sired Isaac \u8212- where the woman, Sarah, is invisible in the formulation even though she is the one who gave birth. Within a social structure where children are born into and named for the father\u8217's household, it follows that the {\i mitzvah} of having them is primarily attributed to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Feminist View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dimension of the sugya raises what one might call the feminist critique: the woman is simultaneously burdened with the physical reality of pregnancy, labor, and childbirth, and excluded from receiving credit \u8212- {\i mitzvah} credit \u8212- for the very act she alone performs. She is assigned the traditional role of child-bearer by societal expectation, and then rendered invisible in the legal framework that assigns the corresponding {\i mitzvah} to the man. It is, as one might put it, the worst of both worlds: socially defined by her child-bearing role but not recognized {\i halachically} as the one obligated \u8212- or credited \u8212- in that act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Berman Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Saul Berman, in a well-known essay written approximately thirty years ago, proposed a reading that inverts this critique. On his account, the {\i halacha}\u8217's exemption of the woman from {\i peru u\u8217'revu} is not a denial of her role but a liberation from a compelled one. By not obligating her in {\i peru u\u8217'revu}, the {\i halacha} leaves open the possibility that a woman may choose not to marry, or choose not to have children, and pursue a more independent path without violating a Torah obligation. The exemption thus functions as a grant of freedom rather than an erasure. Readers may evaluate which of these readings they find more persuasive \u8212- or reject both \u8212- but the question itself is one the sugya genuinely invites.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rule Like Broka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i amoraic} dispute over the {\i halacha}: {\i itmar, Rabbi Yochanan v\u8217'Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} \u8212- there is a recorded dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, one of whom says we rule like Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka that the woman is also commanded, and one of whom says we do not rule like him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Don\u8217't Rule Broka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} seeks to determine which {\i amora} held which position: {\i tistayayeim d\u8217'Rabbi Yochanan hu d\u8217'amar ein halacha} \u8212- let us conclude that it is Rabbi Yochanan who says we do not rule like Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka. The basis for this conclusion is discussed in the next segment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Practical Diff\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stay Single\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining the evidence for how Rabbi Yochanan ruled, it is worth pausing to consider what practical difference this dispute makes \u8212- because beyond the abstract question of who receives {\i mitzvah} credit, the {\i halacha} has substantial real-world implications. If a woman is not commanded on {\i peru u\u8217'revu}, can she choose to remain unmarried without violating any Torah obligation? On the view that she is not commanded, the answer would appear to be yes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demand Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second practical difference: suppose a man has already had children from a previous wife but his current wife has not. If the woman is commanded, she has the basis to demand a divorce so that she may remarry and attempt to have children with another man. If she is not commanded, it is less clear what legal standing she has to compel such a divorce, as we will see in the stories below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third and particularly significant contemporary implication: if a woman is not commanded on {\i peru u\u8217'revu}, may she use contraception freely? Rav Moshe Feinstein raised precisely this question \u8212- if a woman is not commanded, perhaps she may use hormonal contraception without {\i halachic} objection, since she bears no obligation regarding {\i peru u\u8217'revu}. Of course, the residual question then becomes whether she is permitted to do so if it prevents her husband from fulfilling {\i his} obligation, since the context in which he can fulfill it is dependent on her. But the question is genuinely opened by the exemption, and the story of Yehudit later in this sugya is one of the primary {\i Talmudic} sources engaged in that discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Peru u\u8217'Revu (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 U\u8217'Milu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a further source for the derivation: {\i u\u8217'milu et ha\u8217'aretz v\u8217'chivshuha} \u8212- the verse from which the gendered reading of {\i peru u\u8217'revu} is established, as discussed above.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rule Like Broka (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbahu/Chiya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of how Rabbi Yochanan ruled: {\i d\u8217'yativ Rabbi Abbahu v\u8217'ka\u8217'amar mishmeih d\u8217'Rabbi Yochanan: halacha} \u8212- Rabbi Abbahu sat and declared in the name of Rabbi Yochanan that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka, meaning the woman is also commanded. There is then a textual variant: some say it was not Rabbi Abbahu but Rabbi Chiya bar Abba who transmitted this ruling in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Face Away\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In either version: {\i hadreinh Rabbi Ami v\u8217'Rav Assi l\u8217'apeihu} \u8212- Rabbi Ami and Rav Assi turned their faces away from the speaker. They did not openly contradict him, but their gesture of disapproval made clear that they believed this ruling was incorrect and did not represent Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's actual position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Why\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa raises a question about the textual variant: {\i bishlama} if it was Rabbi Abbahu who said it, then one can understand why Rabbi Ami and Rav Assi merely turned away rather than explicitly contradicting him \u8212- {\i mishum kevod beit Keisar}, because Rabbi Abbahu had a close relationship with the imperial court and they did not want to publicly contradict someone of his standing; hence their silent expression of displeasure rather than open refutation. But {\i ella l\u8217'man d\u8217'amar} that it was Rabbi Chiya bar Abba who said it \u8212- who is Rabbi Chiya bar Abba that they could not simply tell him directly that Rabbi Yochanan did not rule this way? Why would they resort to turning their faces rather than saying {\i leimru lei lama amar Rabbi Yochanan hachi} \u8212- just tell him explicitly that Rabbi Yochanan did not say this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion that emerges is that Rabbi Ami and Rav Assi clearly believed Rabbi Yochanan held that we do {\i not} rule like Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka, and that the {\i halacha} is therefore that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru u\u8217'revu} falls on the man alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Not Commanded Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents narrative episodes illustrating the practical stakes. {\i Ki ha de\u8217'asa l\u8217'kamei d\u8217'Rabbi Ami} \u8212- a woman came before Rabbi Ami. From the context, the man in question apparently already had children from another woman, so the case is not one where he is being compelled to divorce in order to fulfill {\i peru u\u8217'revu} himself. She, on the other hand, has not had children and wishes to be divorced so she can remarry. He wants to remain married. She demands a divorce with {\i kesubah}. Rabbi Ami says to her: {\i zil lo mifkadat} \u8212- go, you are not commanded on {\i peru u\u8217'revu}, and therefore you have no legal basis for compelling a divorce with {\i kesubah}. Without a commanded obligation, there is no cause of action, and a divorce without cause would not entitle her to the {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Old Age Need\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The woman responds: {\i l\u8217'siva d\u8217'la mai tehvei alah} \u8212- but what about her old age? Who will take care of her? The implicit claim is that regardless of any formal {\i mitzvah}, she has a fundamental human need and desire to have children \u8212- people who will support her in old age \u8212- and that this constitutes sufficient cause to compel a divorce with {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami accepts the argument: {\i hi vadai kafinan} \u8212- in that case, we will certainly compel it. The woman\u8217's basic human interest in having children, quite apart from any {\i mitzvah} obligation, is recognized as legitimate cause. The practical import is significant: even without a formal {\i mitzvah}, the desire to have children \u8212- grounded in the very human need for support and continuity \u8212- gives her standing to sue for divorce with {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same argument plays out again before Rav Nachman. He too initially tells the woman she is not commanded and therefore cannot demand a {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grave Need\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She responds: {\i lo ba\u8217'ya hach iteta chutra l\u8217'yadah u\u8217'mara l\u8217'kvurah} \u8212- does this woman not need a staff for her hand and a shovel for her grave? She needs someone to support her in old age and to bury her when she dies. Rav Nachman accepts this: {\i ki ha vadai kafinan} \u8212- in that case we will certainly compel the divorce. The story carries a wry edge: one might wonder why {\i beit din} should have to wait for the woman to articulate this claim herself, rather than assuming it from the outset. The fact that the judges do wait reveals something about how automatic or invisible these basic human needs were in the context of the legal discourse of the time.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twin Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces the story of Yehudit, the wife of Rabbi Chiya. {\i Yehudah v\u8217'Chizkiyah te\u8217'omei havu} \u8212- Yehudah and Chizkiyah were twins, one whose formation was completed after nine months and one after seven. This unusual pregnancy \u8212- two children forming at different intervals within the womb \u8212- caused their mother, Yehudit, {\i tza\u8217'ara d\u8217'leidah}, severe pain in childbirth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disguise Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shinatah manah} \u8212- she disguised herself, changing her clothes, and came before her husband Rabbi Chiya as if she were a different woman. {\i Amrah lei: iteta mifkeda apirya v\u8217'rivya?} \u8212- she asked him: is a woman commanded on {\i peru u\u8217'revu}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya Answers No\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lah: lo} \u8212- he answered: no, she is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sterilize\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Azlah ashtaya sama d\u8217'akrta} \u8212- she went and drank a sterilizing potion, a medical preparation that rendered her infertile, because she could not endure the prospect of more pregnancies like the last one, and her {\i posek} had told her she was not obligated to do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matter Revealed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'l\u8217'sof igli milta} \u8212- eventually the matter came to light. Rabbi Chiya discovered what she had done. He said to her: {\i eichu yaladt li chada karsa achriti} \u8212- I wish you had waited to give us one more brood of children. He was, apparently, already {\i yotzei peru u\u8217'revu} twice over: {\i d\u8217'amar mar, Yehudah v\u8217'Chizkiyah achi} \u8212- Yehudah and Chizkiyah, his sons, were his; and {\i Pazi v\u8217'Tavi achvata} \u8212- Pazi and Tavi, his daughters, were also his, apparently another pair of twins. So he had already fulfilled his obligation with two boys and two girls, even according to Beit Shammai\u8217's standard. His lament was not that she had deprived him of his {\i mitzvah} but that they could have had one more cohort of children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halachic} question this story raises is whether it constitutes evidence that a woman who is not commanded on {\i peru u\u8217'revu} may use sterilizing contraception without restriction, leaving the consequences for the husband\u8217's fulfillment of his {\i mitzvah} as his concern alone. The complicating factor here is that the husband was already {\i yotzei}, so the story cannot straightforwardly be extended to cases where he has not yet fulfilled his obligation. Nevertheless, it is one of the central {\i Talmudic} sources in discussions of women and birth control.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half Free Freed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'amar Rav Acha bar Katina amar Rabbi Yitzchak: ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'ishah achat she-chatziyah shifchah v\u8217'chatziyah bat chorin v\u8217'kafu et rabah v\u8217'asa\u8217'ah bat chorin} \u8212- a case is cited in which a woman who was half slave and half free was compelled by {\i beit din} to be fully freed. The parallel case involving a man in this status has appeared earlier in {\i Yevamot}. One might infer from this ruling that the woman must be freed in order to be able to have children, which would in turn suggest she bears some form of obligation regarding {\i peru u\u8217'revu} \u8212- otherwise why compel her master to free her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak rejects this inference: {\i minhag hefker nahagu bah} \u8212- the compulsion was not because she had an obligation to have children, but because her intermediate status exposed her to sexual exploitation. A woman who can neither marry a free man nor a slave is left with no legitimate marital option, and as a result was vulnerable to being taken advantage of sexually. {\i Beit din} intervened to protect her from that situation, not to vindicate a {\i mitzvah} obligation. One cannot therefore derive from this case that the woman is commanded on {\i peru u\u8217'revu}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bavli View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consistent weight of the {\i Bavli} is that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru u\u8217'revu} is not incumbent upon the woman. Case after case \u8212- the ruling of Rabbi Ami, the turning away of Rabbi Ami and Rav Assi from the claim that Rabbi Yochanan ruled otherwise, the resolution of the half-slave case by Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak \u8212- all point in the same direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted, however, that the {\i Yerushalmi} treats this question in a more open way, and at least creates the impression that there is a genuine ongoing debate over the woman\u8217's obligation, or that the ruling may actually go in the direction of obligating her. The {\i Bavli} arrives at a firm conclusion; the {\i Yerushalmi} does not appear to foreclose the question in the same way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Exempt Why (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Childbirth Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One final explanatory approach to the woman\u8217's exemption is worth noting, even though it cannot be found in the {\i Gemara} itself: the {\i Meshech Chochma}, writing approximately 150 years ago, proposed that since childbirth involves genuine physical risk to the woman\u8217's life, the {\i Torah} could not impose it as a {\i mitzvah} obligation upon her. A {\i mitzvah} that mandates a person to undertake life-threatening danger as a positive obligation would be problematic, and therefore the {\i Torah} leaves {\i peru u\u8217'revu} as a free choice for the woman rather than a commanded act. This explanation resonates with many people, and one hears it cited frequently as though it were a traditional {\i Talmudic} position; in fact, as best one can determine, it originates with the {\i Meshech Chochma} and has no earlier source. Whether it captures the {\i Gemara}\u8217's reasoning is another question entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an interesting position that also deserves mention in this context: even if a woman is not technically commanded in {\i peru u\u8217'revu} in its formal sense, she may nonetheless be included in the broader mandate of {\i lashevet} \u8212- settling and populating the world, derived from {\i lo tohu b\u8217'ra\u8217'ah lashevet yetzarah}. This is one opinion in one Tosafot, and it is not codified in the {\i Shulchan Aruch}, but it receives serious attention in {\i halachic} discussion. The implication is that even without the technical form of the {\i mitzvah}, the woman partakes in its underlying value \u8212- not through strict obligation but through a kind of mandated orientation toward the project of {\i yishuv ha\u8217'olam}. Whether this constitutes {\i psak halacha} is genuinely debated, but it is an important position to be aware of.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 New Topic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Start\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the conclusion of this {\i perek} \u8212- {\i hadran alach HaBa Yevimto} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} opens the following chapter, which returns to the domain of {\i kehunah} and forbidden marriages. The opening {\i mishnah} establishes the cases: {\i almana l\u8217'Kohen Gadol, gerusha v\u8217'chalutza l\u8217'Kohen Hedyot} \u8212- a widow married to a {\i Kohen Gadol}, or a divorc\u233 ?e or {\i chalutza} married to a regular {\i kohen}. These are marriages that are {\i halachically} forbidden yet legally binding \u8212- the child is not a {\i mamzer}, but the woman, through the forbidden act of sexual intercourse with a {\i kohen}, becomes a {\i chalala}, disqualified from the benefits she would otherwise enjoy by virtue of being a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Servants Brought\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} addresses a specific scenario within these forbidden marriages: {\i hechnisa lo avdei melog v\u8217'avdei tzon barzel} \u8212- the woman brings into the marriage servants who belong to two different legal categories, {\i nichsei melog} or {\i nichsei tzon barzel}. The status of those servants with respect to eating {\i terumah} will turn on which category they belong to, and that in turn depends on how ownership and title are structured in each type.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Property Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzon Barzel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nichsei tzon barzel} \u8212- literally \u8220"iron sheep property\u8221" \u8212- refers to property she brings into the marriage where title transfers entirely to the husband. Upon bringing in, say, a parcel of real estate, the deed is effectively signed over to him; he becomes the full legal owner. However, the value of that property is assessed at the time of entry into the marriage, and that assessed value constitutes a debt he owes her: if they divorce or if he predeceases her, she is entitled to recover that sum. This is precisely what is reflected in the standard {\i ketubah} text, where the groom accepts responsibility for all the property she brings in \u8212- {\i bein b\u8217'manei d\u8217'levusha bein b\u8217'manei d\u8217'arsa} \u8212- at an assessed value. The groom acquires full title but is liable for the value. With respect to slaves classified as {\i nichsei tzon barzel}, they thus become his slaves entirely during the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melog\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nichsei melog} operates differently. The wife retains title to the property \u8212- it remains hers \u8212- but the husband enjoys the {\i kinyan peiros}, the right to benefit from the property\u8217's fruits, its {\i usufruct}, during the marriage. Rental income from her land, produce from her fields, the labor of her slaves \u8212- all of this goes to him during the marriage. Correspondingly, he bears the obligation to maintain those assets: {\i af al pi she-hu chayav b\u8217'mzonoson} \u8212- even though he is responsible for feeding her {\i melog} slaves. But the title remains hers. If the slaves die, the loss is hers; if they increase in value or produce children, the gain is hers. He has rights over the benefit but not the ownership.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Normal Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In a normal, permitted marriage between a {\i kohen} and a woman he may marry \u8212- say, a {\i bat Yisrael} or {\i bat kohen} \u8212- both categories of servants eat {\i terumah}. The servants classified as {\i tzon barzel} eat by virtue of him, since title has passed to him and they are his slaves. The servants classified as {\i melog} eat by virtue of her, since she, as the wife of a {\i kohen}, is herself entitled to eat {\i terumah}, and her slaves eat through her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalala Block\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the complication: when the marriage is a forbidden one and the woman thereby becomes a {\i chalala}, she loses her entitlement to eat {\i terumah}. She cannot eat it because she is now disqualified from the priestly benefits she would otherwise enjoy as a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife. This disqualification flows from the forbidden union itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is what happens to her {\i melog} slaves. The {\i tzon barzel} slaves are straightforward: title passed to him, they are his slaves, they eat {\i terumah} through him, and his priestly status is unaffected \u8212- a {\i kohen} never loses his own priestly standing through a forbidden marriage, only the woman is degraded. So the {\i tzon barzel} slaves eat. But the {\i melog} slaves are still {\i her} slaves. Their right to eat {\i terumah} would have to flow through her, and since she herself cannot eat, the question arises whether they can.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A diagram may help clarify the issue. In a normal case, the {\i kohen} is at the top, his wife \u8212- whether {\i bat Yisrael} or {\i bat kohen} \u8212- eats {\i terumah} by virtue of the marriage, the {\i tzon barzel} slaves eat by virtue of him directly, and the {\i melog} slaves eat by virtue of her, which is legitimate because she herself is entitled. But when she becomes a {\i chalala}, she is cut off from {\i terumah}: she cannot eat it, and therefore the {\i melog} slaves, whose eating must flow through her, cannot eat either. The {\i tzon barzel} slaves, eating directly through him, are unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states the rule explicitly: {\i v\u8217'avdei milug lo yochlu bitrumah v\u8217'avdei tzon barzel yochlu bitrumah} \u8212- the {\i melog} servants do not eat {\i terumah} and the {\i tzon barzel} servants do eat. The {\i mishnah} then defines each category: {\i melog} slaves are those for whom {\i im meisu meisu lah} \u8212- if they die, the loss is hers \u8212- and {\i im hosiru hosiru lah} \u8212- if they increase in value, the gain is hers \u8212- even though {\i af al pi she-hu chayav b\u8217'mzonoson}, he must feed them. {\i Tzon barzel} slaves are those for whom {\i im meisu meisu lo} \u8212- if they die, the loss is his \u8212- and {\i im hosiru hosiru lo} \u8212- if they appreciate, the gain is his \u8212- {\i ho\u8217'il v\u8217'hu chayav b\u8217'achrayusan}, since he bears full responsibility for them. In the normal case of a {\i bat Yisrael} married to a {\i kohen}, both types eat {\i terumah}; in the case of a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i Yisrael}, neither type eats, since she has lost her entitlement upon marrying a non-{\i kohen} and he never had it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Not Eat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Indirect Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: why should the {\i melog} slaves not eat {\i terumah}? The {\i kohen} does own something in relation to those slaves \u8212- he owns the {\i kinyan peiros}, the right of usufruct. Let the wife be regarded as {\i his} acquisition in some sense, and the slaves as her acquisition, so that we view the {\i melog} slaves as an indirect acquisition of his own: {\i lahavei kekinyano shekana kinyan} \u8212- let it be treated as his acquired property that itself has acquired additional property. If the {\i Gemara} elsewhere extends {\i terumah} eating rights through indirect ownership chains, why not here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scripture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers from the verse: {\i v\u8217'Kohen ki yikneh nefesh kinyan kaspo hu yochal bo} \u8212- when a {\i kohen} acquires a soul through his money, that soul shall eat of it. From this the {\i Gemara} derives the right of a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife and slaves to eat {\i terumah}, and extends it: {\i kinyano shekana kinyan} \u8212- if the {\i kohen}\u8217's acquisition itself acquires another being, that being may also eat. So a wife can eat, her slaves can eat, his slaves\u8217' slaves can eat. The chain of indirect ownership can support {\i terumah} rights.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ochel Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} then formulates a limiting principle: {\i kol ha\u8217'ochel ma\u8217'achil, kol she\u8217'eino ochel eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- whoever can eat herself enables her dependents to eat; whoever cannot eat herself cannot enable her dependents to eat. The {\i melog} slaves eat by virtue of the wife. If she can eat \u8212- if she is a legitimate {\i eishes kohen} entitled to {\i terumah} \u8212- then she enables them. If she cannot eat because she has become a {\i chalala}, the enabling link is broken, and they cannot eat through her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Areil Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An apparent exception is raised: what about a {\i kohen} who is an {\i areil} \u8212- uncircumcised, whether due to medical necessity or other reason \u8212- or who is {\i tamei}, ritually impure? Such a {\i kohen} may not eat {\i terumah} himself, yet his wife and slaves may still eat. If the principle is that only one who can eat enables others to eat, this seems to be a counterexample: here is one who cannot eat, yet he enables others to eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temporary vs Permanent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by drawing a distinction based on the nature of the disqualification: {\i hasam pumayu ka\u8217'iv lehu} \u8212- there, their mouth is hurting them, meaning the impediment is a personal, temporary incapacity, not a fundamental change in status. An {\i areil} could in principle become circumcised \u8212- whatever the practical obstacles \u8212- and a {\i tamei} person can always become {\i tahor}. The disqualification is situational and reversible. A {\i chalala}, by contrast, is in a permanent state: the forbidden sexual union has categorically changed her status, and there is no mechanism by which she reverts. A permanent status-change severs the enabling link entirely; a temporary personal incapacity does not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 66\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slaves of Chalalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the opening of our discussion addresses the status of slaves belonging to a woman married to a {\i kohen}. Two categories of property are relevant here. {\i Nichsei milug} refers to property over which the wife retains title, though the husband enjoys its use; {\i nichsei tzon barzel} refers to property that has been assessed and formally transferred to the husband\u8217's ownership, leaving the wife with only a monetary claim. The {\i mishnah} focuses on the more interesting case \u8212- the slaves she owns through {\i nichsei milug} \u8212- and introduces a complicating factor: if she is an {\i almanah}, a {\i gerushah}, a {\i chalutzah}, or an {\i almanah l\u8217'kohen gadol}, she becomes a {\i chalalah} through the marriage. As a {\i chalalah}, she cannot eat {\i terumah} even by virtue of being married to the {\i kohen}. The question then becomes: what is the status of her slaves? Can they eat {\i terumah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules that they cannot. The {\i gemara} explains that this follows from the principle governing {\i kinyano she-kanah kinyan} \u8212- that is, the wife herself, who is his {\i kinyan} of a kind and who owns these slaves, is the conduit through which they eat. If she herself cannot eat {\i terumah}, the question is whether she can nonetheless enable her slaves to eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating Through Whom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an apparent challenge to the principle that those who eat do so through her. The case involves a {\i mamzer} \u8212- and the word {\i mamzer} is used here to make the example as extreme as possible, though the core case could involve any non-{\i kohen}. Suppose a {\i kohen} has a daughter who marries a {\i mamzer} or a {\i yisrael}, and they have a child. That child is not a {\i kohen} \u8212- the father being a non-{\i kohen} \u8212- and would never himself eat {\i terumah}. Yet if the {\i kohen} dies and there remains such a grandchild descended from the marriage, the widow may continue eating {\i terumah} for as long as that grandchild is alive, even after she has collected her {\i ketubah}. The grandchild enables her to eat even though the grandchild itself cannot eat and is, in an extreme case, even a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This seems to challenge the rule that only one who eats can enable others to eat. The {\i gemara} cites this case with the phrase {\i va-harei mamzer she-ein ochel u-maachil} \u8212- \u8220"behold, the {\i mamzer} who does not eat yet enables.\u8221" Should this not demonstrate that someone who cannot eat can still enable others to?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Ochel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina responds sharply: {\i kinyan ochel ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the relevant principle concerns specifically a {\i kinyan} that eats. The case of the grandchild enabling the grandmother to eat is categorically different: the grandmother eats not because the grandchild \u8220"enables\u8221" her in the technical sense relevant to property ownership, but because the grandchild\u8217's existence keeps her connected to the {\i kohen}\u8217's family. That is a distinct mechanism entirely. The operative principle for our {\i mishnah} remains: {\i kinyan ochel ma\u8217'achil, kinyan she\u8217'eino ochel eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- only a {\i kinyan} that itself eats can feed others. Since the wife is called a {\i kinyan} in this context, and since she cannot eat {\i terumah} as a {\i chalalah}, her slaves cannot eat through her. This is the first explanation of the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Gezerah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a second explanation. He acknowledges that in principle, the slaves of the {\i chalalah} ought to be entitled to eat {\i terumah}, because indirectly they are connected to the {\i kohen} and their eating flows through him rather than purely through her. {\i Mide\u8217'oraisa me\u8217'achlei} \u8212- by Torah law they would be permitted to eat. However, {\i verabbanan hu de-gazru behu} \u8212- the Rabbis enacted a prohibition against their eating in this case, as an incentive to dissolution of the forbidden marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is as follows: because the {\i kohen} is married to a {\i gerushah}, {\i chalutza}, or the {\i kohen gadol} to an {\i almanah}, a situation the Rabbis find deeply objectionable, they sought to create pressure toward divorce. The wife can be expected to say: {\i ani eini ochelet} \u8212- I myself cannot eat {\i terumah}; {\i avadai einam ochlim} \u8212- my slaves cannot eat {\i terumah}. She is, as it were, like a {\i zonah} to him, {\i zonah hi etzlo} \u8212- not fully a wife in the sense of enjoying the privileges of wifehood. This sense of exclusion and non-belonging, says Rava, was designed by the Rabbis \u8212- {\i va\u8217'ati lefuka} \u8212- to bring about a divorce. The slaves could in principle eat; the Rabbis withheld that entitlement as a form of social and legal pressure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi agrees with the premise that the slaves could in principle eat, but offers a different reason for the rabbinic prohibition: {\i gezerah shema ta\u8217'achilah achar misah} \u8212- a decree lest she continue feeding them {\i terumah} even after the {\i kohen} dies. Her entitlement to feed them {\i terumah} is entirely contingent on her husband\u8217's status; once he dies, that entitlement ceases. If we permit her to feed them during the marriage, we fear she will continue the practice after his death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: that concern should apply universally. Every Jewish woman married to a {\i kohen} might continue feeding her slaves {\i terumah} after his death \u8212- should we therefore prohibit all such wives from feeding their slaves? Rav Ashi withdraws that formulation and offers a refined version.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela amar Rav Ashi: be-almanah kohenes} \u8212- the particular case that generates a real risk is when the woman married to the {\i kohen gadol} is herself a {\i bas kohen}, a daughter of a {\i kohen}. Here is the chain of reasoning she might follow after her husband dies: {\i me\u8217'ikara achli bi-terumah debei nasha} \u8212- originally, my slaves ate {\i terumah} because of my father\u8217's house, by virtue of the fact that I was a {\i bas kohen}. Then {\i inseiva le-hai kohen} \u8212- I married this {\i kohen}, {\i achli bi-terumah de-gavrai} \u8212- and they continued eating {\i terumah} by virtue of my husband. Now that my husband has died, she may reason, {\i hadri leh milsa ke-me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- things return to their original state and my slaves may continue eating {\i terumah} as they did before.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The flaw in this reasoning is that she may be unaware \u8212- {\i delo yade\u8217'a} \u8212- that in the course of this marriage she became a {\i chalalah}. {\i De-me\u8217'ikara lo shavisah le-nafshah chalalah} \u8212- originally, when the slaves ate by virtue of her father\u8217's house, she was not yet a {\i chalalah}. {\i Hashta shavisah nafshah chalalah} \u8212- but now, by virtue of this forbidden marriage, she is a {\i chalalah}, and therefore her slaves cannot eat even after her husband dies. The concern, then, is not general but specific: she will assume she may revert to her original entitlement without recognizing that the marriage itself permanently altered her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almanah Kohenes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: this reasoning accounts for the case of the {\i almanah kohenes}, the {\i bas kohen} who married the {\i kohen gadol}. But what of the {\i almanah bas yisrael} \u8212- the woman who is not a {\i bas kohen}? She has no prior entitlement from her father\u8217's house; why should the same prohibition apply to her? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i be-manusa lo pligi rabbanan} \u8212- where there is a specific case that genuinely warrants the decree, the Rabbis did not distinguish among cases. {\i O lo plug} \u8212- they enacted a uniform prohibition across all these situations rather than creating distinctions that might be confused in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is that we have two general approaches. The first, associated with Ravina, is that the slaves eat only through the wife, and since she cannot eat, they cannot either \u8212- this is the {\i de\u8217'oraisa} position. The second, associated with Rava and Rav Ashi, acknowledges that in principle the slaves eat through the {\i kohen}, but a rabbinic {\i gezerah} was imposed for the reasons described. In either case the practical ruling is that the slaves of a {\i chalalah} do not eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzon Barzel Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Assessment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a new topic with the word {\i itmar} \u8212- \u8220"it was stated\u8221" \u8212- signaling an {\i amoraic} dispute. The subject is {\i nichsei tzon barzel}: property a woman brings into the marriage whose value is formally assessed \u8212- {\i shum} \u8212- and recorded in the {\i ketubah}. The {\i ketubah} lists the items and assigns them a total monetary value, and the husband accepts full financial responsibility for that amount. If the property appreciates, he benefits; if it depreciates or is lost, he bears the loss. What the wife is guaranteed is the return of the assessed monetary value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical question arises when the husband dies or the marriage is dissolved and the actual property is still available \u8212- the original field, the original slaves, the original vessels. Can the wife demand the return of the specific items? Or has the husband taken absolute title, leaving her only a monetary claim against the estate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Title vs.\u160 ?Debt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely the dispute between Rav Yehuda and Rav Ami. Rav Yehuda rules {\i hadin immah} \u8212- the law is with her; she may demand the return of the specific property. Rav Ami rules {\i hadin immo} \u8212- the law is with him; she holds only a monetary debt and cannot compel return of the items themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda\u8217's reasoning is formulated as {\i mishum shevach beis aviha} \u8212- on account of the benefit of her father\u8217's house. This is the property that came with her from her father as a dowry; she ought to be entitled to reclaim it. Rav Ami reasons from the {\i mishnah} itself, which states explicitly regarding {\i nichsei tzon barzel}: {\i im mesu mesu lo, ve-im hokiru hokiru lo} \u8212- if they die, it is his loss; if they appreciate, it is his gain. This language, he argues, demonstrates that the property entered his possession absolutely, {\i bi-rshusei kayami} \u8212- it stands in his domain, {\i u-ch\u8217'didei damu} \u8212- and is treated as fully his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rental Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to test whether formal liability ({\i chiyuv achrayus}) alone is sufficient to give a person such a relationship to property that the property\u8217's slaves could eat {\i terumah} on his account \u8212- without necessarily establishing outright ownership. If we take seriously the claim that {\i chayav be-achrayusan} establishes this relationship without full title, we should be able to find a parallel. The {\i gemara} brings a {\i mishnah}: a {\i yisrael} who rents a cow from a {\i kohen} may feed it {\i terumah} fodder, because the cow remains the {\i kohen}\u8217's and he has the relevant interest in it. But a {\i kohen} who rents a cow from a {\i yisrael} \u8212- even though {\i mezonoseha alav}, he is responsible for feeding it and bears liability for certain losses \u8212- nevertheless {\i lo ya\u8217'achilennah karshinei terumah}, he may not feed it {\i terumah} fodder. The cow is still the {\i yisrael}\u8217's. Rental liability is simply insufficient to confer the kind of interest that enables feeding {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Zone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds that the husband\u8217's relationship to the {\i tzon barzel} property is not comparable to a renter\u8217's. A renter bears liability only for theft and loss; he is not responsible for depreciation due to market changes, normal wear, or reduction in market value. The {\i tzon barzel} relationship is categorically different: no matter what happens \u8212- whether the market changes, whether the property deteriorates through ordinary use, whether the decrease is anyone\u8217's fault or not \u8212- the husband must restore the full assessed value. Conversely, any increase in value belongs entirely to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sliding scale of liability raises the question acutely. The {\i gemara} is exploring whether there exists a legal middle zone: the wife retains formal title, but the husband has such comprehensive rights and obligations with respect to the property \u8212- all gains his, all losses his regardless of fault \u8212- that the slaves can eat {\i terumah} through his relationship to them, even without his having acquired outright ownership. The {\i gemara} presses: {\i v\u8217'chol heicha de-chayav be-achrayusan achli bi-terumah?} \u8212- does any degree of comprehensive liability trigger the right to eat? The rental cases show low-level liability is insufficient. The question is whether this uniquely intensive relationship suffices to permit {\i terumah} eating without constituting full ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Braitot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava and Rav Yosef were sitting together at the conclusion of Rav Nachman\u8217's {\i drasha}, and in their discussion they noted that the sources appear to point in both directions. One {\i braita} supports Rav Ami: {\i avdei tzon barzel yotzin be-shen va-ayin la-ish, aval lo la-ishah} \u8212- slaves brought in as {\i tzon barzel} go free if the husband knocks out their eye or tooth, but not if the wife does the same. This strongly implies the husband is considered their master \u8212- he is the one whose act of violence triggers the law of {\i shen va-ayin}. If she retained genuine title, why would her striking not also trigger their release?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i braita} supports Rav Yehuda: {\i ha-machneses shum le-ba\u8217'alah im ratza ba\u8217'al limkor lo yimkor} \u8212- if a wife brings in assessed property and the husband wishes to sell it, he may not do so. If he held absolute title, he would plainly be entitled to sell his own property. The fact that he cannot sell it \u8212- and that the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} before Rabban Gamliel affirmed that any such sale is void and the husband must retrieve the property from the purchasers \u8212- demonstrates that she retains rights in the objects themselves, not merely a monetary claim.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She Retrieves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, citing Rav Nachman, rules: {\i halacha ke-Rav Yehuda} \u8212- the law follows Rav Yehuda. The wife is entitled to retrieve the specific property she brought in. The {\i braita} showing that the husband cannot sell the property without her consent was evidently persuasive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shen V\u8217'Ayin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava acknowledges the counter-{\i braita}, which seemed to prove Rav Ami\u8217's position by stating that only the husband\u8217's violence triggers the slave\u8217's freedom. He addresses this directly: {\i af al gav de-tanya ke-vassa de-Rabbi Ami, mistabra ta\u8217'ama de-Rav Yehuda mishum shevach beis aviha} \u8212- even though the {\i braita} appears to favor Rav Ami, the underlying rationale of Rav Yehuda\u8217's position is more compelling, because this property came from her father\u8217's house and she ought to be entitled to reclaim it. Tosafot, for their part, find the {\i shen va-ayin braita} less than a decisive proof for Rav Ami in any case: just as the slaves eat {\i terumah} by virtue of the husband\u8217's comprehensive relationship to them, so too the {\i shen va-ayin} law might operate through him without that implying full title.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cloak Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i ma\u8217'aseh} illustrates the practical limits of Rav Yehuda\u8217's ruling. A woman brought into her marriage a fine wool cloak as part of her {\i nichsei tzon barzel}, recorded in her {\i ketubah}. Her husband died. Before she had the opportunity to claim the cloak, the orphans seized it and spread it over the dead body, using it as a shroud. Once an object is designated and used as a shroud for a deceased, it becomes {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- prohibited from benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava ruled: {\i kanya misna} \u8212- the dead body acquires it. The cloak has changed its identity and she loses any right to that specific object, though she remains entitled to its monetary value from the estate. A grandson of Rava challenged: do we not rule like Rav Yehuda, that the property is hers? If so, the orphans had no right to use her property as a shroud, and their doing so should not affect its status. The response is decisive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Requires Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reply: {\i mi lo modeh Rabbi Yehuda de-mechusar gubyanya?} \u8212- even Rav Yehuda concedes that the property requires active collection before it reverts to her control. Rav Yehuda\u8217's ruling does not mean that the property is automatically, at every moment, fully under her title and control. He holds that she has a right to reclaim it \u8212- but that right must be exercised; it must be collected. {\i De-kivun de-mechusar gubyanya bi-rshusei kai} \u8212- since it still requires collection, it remains under the control of the husband\u8217's heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual picture that emerges is subtle: she may hold some form of title, or she may hold only a superior claim of retrieval, but in neither case does the property automatically revert to her domain upon the husband\u8217's death. Until she acts to collect it, it remains under the heirs\u8217' effective control. And because of that, the heirs were in a position to use it as a shroud, triggering {\i asur be-hana\u8217'ah} and extinguishing her specific rights in that object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's reasoning invokes his broader principle: {\i hekdesh, chametz, u-shichrur motzi\u8217'in mi-yedei shibud} \u8212- that sanctification of property ({\i hekdesh}), the transformation of {\i chametz} during Passover into something {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah}, and the emancipation of a slave all operate to dissolve existing liens or encumbrances upon property by fundamentally transforming the identity of the object. Here, using the cloak as a shroud similarly transforms its identity \u8212- just as {\i chametz} that becomes forbidden during Passover loses its character as encumbered property, so too this cloak, once assigned to the dead, loses whatever lien or claim she held over it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Appreciation Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda presents the following scenario at the top of 67a: a wife brings into the marriage two vessels assessed at one thousand {\i zuz}. By the time of divorce or the husband\u8217's death, they have appreciated and are now worth two thousand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevach Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is: {\i echad notalah bi-chtuvtasah} \u8212- she takes one vessel back as part of her {\i ketubah} entitlement. {\i Ve-echad nosenet damim ve-notalah mipnei shevach beis aviha} \u8212- and the second vessel she is also entitled to demand, paying the difference in value, on account of {\i shevach beis aviha}, the benefit of her father\u8217's house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what is the novelty here? Didn\u8217't Rav Yehuda already establish that she can retrieve her property? The answer: one might have thought that her right to retrieve extends only up to the original assessed value \u8212- that is, she can demand the vessels back insofar as they satisfy her monetary entitlement, but not insofar as they have appreciated beyond that value. Perhaps she should be entitled to only one of the two vessels, since one is now worth the full original thousand. The ruling teaches that {\i mipnei shevach beis aviha} she is entitled to retrieve both the objects she brought in and to pay the appreciated difference \u8212- not merely to receive the monetary equivalent. The same logic applies in reverse: if the property has lost value, she retrieves all of it and the estate compensates her for the shortfall.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fetus & Eating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} turns to a related but distinct problem. A {\i bas yisrael} \u8212- a non-{\i kohen} woman \u8212- was married to a {\i kohen}, and the {\i kohen} dies leaving her pregnant. When a {\i kohen} dies, the general rule is that if he leaves behind a child from that marriage, the widow continues to eat {\i terumah} through the child. But here the child is not yet born. What is the status of the slaves?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules {\i lo yokhlu avadeha bi-terumah mipnei chelko shel ubar} \u8212- the slaves may not eat {\i terumah} on account of the portion of the {\i ubar} (the fetus). The reasoning requires unpacking. The {\i kohen} died leaving his estate to his heirs. If he left sons, those sons are the heirs and are co-owners of the slaves; the slaves eat {\i terumah} by virtue of those sons. But here, the {\i ubar} is among the heirs \u8212- indeed, if the child is born and is male, he will inherit a portion. The {\i ubar} is thus a partial owner of the slaves. Since the {\i ubar} cannot yet cause the slaves to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- he is unborn \u8212- his partial ownership prevents the slaves from eating, because that portion of the slaves that is owned by him is not entitled to eat. Ten percent owned by the {\i ubar} is not entitled; and since the slaves are partially owned by one who cannot enable their eating, none of them may eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the {\i mishnah} notes that the {\i ubar} also does not enable his mother to eat: she too cannot eat {\i terumah} on account of an unborn child. This produces the striking result that the {\i ubar} {\i posel} \u8212- he disqualifies \u8212- and yet {\i eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- he does not feed. He is the worst of both legal worlds: his existence as a partial owner creates a disqualification, but his unborn status prevents him from conferring any entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Divrei Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- this is the ruling of Rabbi Yosi. Rabbi Yosi formulates the principle: {\i ha-ubar posel ve-eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- the fetus invalidates and does not feed. He prevents the slaves from eating by virtue of his partial ownership, while himself being unable to enable anyone to eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohenes Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages pose a challenge to Rabbi Yosi: {\i me\u8217'achar she-eidasta lanu al bas yisrael le-kohen, af bas kohen le-kohen} \u8212- if this is true when the mother is a {\i bas yisrael} married to a {\i kohen}, the same logic should apply equally when the mother is a {\i bas kohen} married to a {\i kohen}. Why would the mother\u8217's lineage matter? The fetus still holds a portion of the estate and is still unborn. The Sages appear to be making the point that according to Rabbi Yosi\u8217's reasoning, the same ruling should hold in both cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Theories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a fundamental question about what Rabbi Yosi\u8217's reasoning actually is. Two possible explanations are offered. The first \u8212- associated with Rabba \u8212- is: {\i ta\u8217'ama de-Rabbi Yosi mishum de-kasavar ubar be-mei zarah zar hu} \u8212- Rabbi Yosi\u8217's reason is that the fetus, while still in the womb of a non-{\i kohen} mother, is considered a {\i zar}, a non-{\i kohen}. He has a portion of the estate, but because his mother is not a {\i kohen} and he has not yet achieved independent identity, he is classified as a {\i zar}. The slaves thus have a partial {\i zar}-owner, and therefore cannot eat. On this reading, the critical variable is the mother\u8217's status, not the fact of being unborn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second explanation \u8212- associated with Rav Yosef \u8212- is: {\i yalud ma\u8217'achil, she-eino yalud eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- one who is born enables feeding; one who is not yet born does not enable feeding. On this reading, it has nothing to do with the mother being a non-{\i kohen}. Even if the mother were a {\i bas kohen}, the result would be identical: being unborn is itself the disqualifying factor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} between these two theories is precisely the case of an {\i ubar} whose mother is a {\i bas kohen}. On Rabba\u8217's reading, since the mother is a {\i kohen}, the {\i ubar} is not a {\i zar}, and therefore his partial ownership creates no problem \u8212- the slaves could eat. On Rav Yosef\u8217's reading, being unborn is categorically disqualifying regardless of the mother\u8217's identity, so the result would be identical: the slaves cannot eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} presses Rabbi Yosi directly on this: {\i zo shamati ve-zo lo shamati} \u8212- this I have received as a tradition, and that I have not. He has a tradition that a {\i bas yisrael}\u8217's slaves cannot eat on account of the {\i ubar}; whether the same applies when the mother is a {\i bas kohen}, he declines to rule. If his reasoning were simply that an unborn person cannot enable feeding regardless of the mother\u8217's identity, then the Sages\u8217' challenge would be unanswerable and he would have had to respond in kind. His silence on the {\i bas kohen} case suggests that his actual reasoning was the first \u8212- that the relevant factor is the {\i ubar}\u8217's status as a {\i zar} by virtue of his mother being a {\i bas yisrael}. But this is not resolved definitively, and the {\i gemara} leaves the ambiguity in place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fetus Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fetus Title\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the deeper theoretical issue beneath the surface of the {\i mishnah}: does a fetus acquire title to an inheritance before birth? Rabbi Yosi\u8217's ruling in the {\i mishnah} rests on the premise that the {\i ubar} already holds a portion of the estate \u8212- that he is, in some sense, already a partial heir from the moment of his father\u8217's death. This is assumption number one. Assumption number two is that the {\i ubar}, despite holding this partial title, cannot cause slaves to eat {\i terumah} because he is unborn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} who challenge Rabbi Yosi appear to reject assumption number one entirely. Their position is that {\i yesh lo banim, okhlin mishum banim} \u8212- if the deceased left sons, the slaves eat through those sons; {\i ein lo banim, okhlin mishum achim} \u8212- if there are no sons, the slaves eat through the brothers. In other words, the {\i ubar} is simply not counted until he is born. Before birth, the existing relatives \u8212- sons, brothers, or any {\i mishpachah} \u8212- are treated as the heirs for purposes of determining who can enable {\i terumah} eating. The {\i ubar} creates no disruption because he has, in the view of the {\i chachamim}, no present title to the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This produces a sharp disagreement: Rabbi Yosi holds that the {\i ubar} acquires a portion of the inheritance immediately upon his father\u8217's death, even before birth. The {\i chachamim} hold that no such acquisition occurs until birth. The {\i gemara} asks whether Shmuel, who reports Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position, personally agrees with it or merely reports it neutrally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer emerges from Shmuel\u8217's dramatic declaration to Rav Chana Bagdata \u8212- identified by Rashi as either a master of {\i aggadah} or, more likely given the name, a resident of Baghdad in Babylonia: {\i puk ayti li bei asara} \u8212- go and bring me a group of ten people \u8212- {\i ve-eima lach be-anpaihu} \u8212- and I will declare publicly before them: {\i ha-mezakeh le-ubar kanah} \u8212- one who transfers title to a fetus, the fetus acquires it. This is not a quiet, tentative ruling; Shmuel wants to make a public declaration of principle. A fetus can receive a transfer of title \u8212- {\i da\u8217'at acheret makneh oto}, through the mechanism of another person acting on its behalf \u8212- and presumably, if it can receive a transfer, it also inherits by operation of law while still unborn. This aligns Shmuel clearly with Rabbi Yosi on assumption number one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel vs.\u160 ?Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then revisits the phrase {\i zu divrei Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- \u8220"this is the position of Rabbi Yosi\u8221" \u8212- which might have implied that Shmuel was merely reporting a minority view without endorsing it. In light of Shmuel\u8217's public declaration, the {\i gemara} corrects course: {\i ela zu u-sevira lei} \u8212- it means both \u8220"this is Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position\u8221" and \u8220"this is how Shmuel himself holds.\u8221" Shmuel endorses Rabbi Yosi\u8217's first principle that the {\i ubar} acquires a portion of the inheritance before birth. He notes, however, that {\i chachamim} disagree with this, so students of the tradition should be aware that the matter is not uncontested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shemaya Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a report: Rabbi Zakai testified that Rabbi Yosi transmitted this ruling {\i mipi Shemaya ve-Avtalyon} \u8212- in the name of Shemaya and Avtalyon. This clarifies an apparent anachronism: Rabbi Yosi did not testify {\i before} the {\i tanna\u8217'im} of earlier generations but rather passed on a tradition {\i in their name}. The question then becomes whether this transmission constitutes a definitive ruling. {\i Ve-hodu lo} \u8212- and they conceded to him, or agreed that his reasoning made sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi clarifies the force of that concession: {\i mi katani de-kiblu?} \u8212- the text does not say they {\i accepted} his position. {\i Ve-hodu lo katani} \u8212- it says they {\i conceded} or acknowledged that the reasoning was compelling. {\i De-mistabra ta\u8217'ameih} \u8212- it made great sense to them. But acknowledging that an argument is compelling is not the same as issuing a formal ruling in its favor. The {\i chachamim} were impressed but did not formally commit to the position. This leaves the debate in a nuanced state: Shmuel holds like Rabbi Yosi; the {\i chachamim} of the {\i mishnah} appear to disagree; and even the great authorities of earlier generations, while finding the argument persuasive, stopped short of a definitive acceptance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eating Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a systematic {\i braita} working through the various scenarios. The first case: {\i heniah banim} \u8212- the {\i kohen} dies leaving sons. {\i Elu va-elu okhlin} \u8212- both these slaves and those slaves eat {\i terumah}. The {\i nichsei tzon barzel} slaves eat because they are part of the estate now inherited by the sons. The {\i nichsei milug} slaves eat because the wife herself is entitled to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- there are living children from the marriage who keep her connected to the {\i kohen}\u8217's family, and through her the slaves eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Only Pregnancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: {\i heniah meuberet} \u8212- he leaves her pregnant, with no born children. {\i Elu va-elu einan okhlin} \u8212- neither set of slaves may eat. This is the teaching of the {\i mishnah} itself, following Rabbi Yosi. The wife cannot eat {\i terumah} through an unborn child, so her {\i milug} slaves cannot eat through her. And the {\i tzon barzel} slaves are now partially owned by the {\i ubar}, who cannot enable them to eat. {\i Ubar posel ve-eino ma\u8217'achil}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons & Pregnancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third and most complex case: {\i heniah banim ve-heniah meuberet} \u8212- the {\i kohen} leaves both born sons and a pregnant wife. {\i Avdei melog okhlin ke-derekh she-hi okhelet} \u8212- the {\i milug} slaves eat, just as the wife herself eats. Since there are born children from the marriage, she is entitled to eat {\i terumah}, and they eat through her. {\i Avdei tzon barzel lo yokhlu mipnei chelko shel ubar} \u8212- but the {\i tzon barzel} slaves may not eat, because the {\i ubar}\u8217's partial ownership of the estate prevents them. The {\i ubar} thus {\i posel} \u8212- he disqualifies the {\i tzon barzel} slaves \u8212- while the born sons enable the wife and through her the {\i milug} slaves. The {\i ubar} does not enable anything; he only disqualifies. {\i Divrei Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- this is Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter vs.\u160 ?Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yosi reports a position {\i mishum aviv} \u8212- in his father\u8217's name: {\i ha-bat ma\u8217'achelet, ha-ben eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- the daughter enables feeding but the son does not. The {\i gemara} leaves this to be unpacked further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai offers a further position: {\i zekharim yokhlu kullan, nekeivot lo yokhlu, shema yimatzei ubar zeh} \u8212- males eat, all of them, but females do not, out of concern that this {\i ubar} may turn out to be male. The discussion of these positions and their underlying reasoning extends into the following {\i daf}, where we will continue the analysis tomorrow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 66b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slaves & Chalalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Property Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on this {\i daf} addresses the question of what happens to the slaves of a woman married to a {\i kohen} with respect to the eating of {\i terumah}. Two categories of property require distinction from the outset. The first is {\i nichsei milug}: property that the wife retains legal title to, though the husband enjoys its use. The second is {\i nichsei tzon barzel}: property whose title transfers to the husband, in exchange for which he bears all losses and retains all gains, while remaining obligated to return the assessed value upon divorce or death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} focuses on the more conceptually interesting category, {\i nichsei milug}, the slaves she owns. The case involves a woman who, by virtue of her marriage to a {\i kohen} \u8212- whether as a {\i gerusha} or {\i chalutzah} married to an ordinary {\i kohen}, or as an {\i almanah} married to a {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- becomes a {\i chalalah}. The very act of marital relations in a forbidden union renders her a {\i chalalah}, disqualified from eating {\i terumah}. The question is: what about her slaves? They are, in some sense, connected to the {\i kohen} household. May they eat {\i terumah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules that they may not. The {\i gemara} explains by invoking the principle {\i kinyano shekanah kinyan} \u8212- a formulation that, while uncomfortable in its description of the wife as a {\i kinyan}, refers to the husband\u8217's legal relationship to her and through her to what she owns. The slaves, in turn, are eating by virtue of her, and if she cannot eat \u8212- being a {\i chalalah} \u8212- then she cannot, as it were, confer that right upon her slaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Ochel Maachil\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this straightforward explanation. If the reason the slaves can eat {\i terumah} is traced back through the wife, consider the case of a {\i mamzer} who cannot himself eat {\i terumah} yet can enable his grandmother to eat. The scenario is this: a {\i kohen} dies leaving a daughter, who has since married a {\i mamzer} or even an ordinary {\i Yisrael}. Their child, not being a {\i kohen}, cannot eat {\i terumah}. Yet as long as that grandchild is alive, the grandmother \u8212- the original wife of the {\i kohen} \u8212- may continue eating {\i terumah} by virtue of the connection maintained through her child or grandchild, even if that descendant could never eat {\i terumah} himself. Here, then, is someone who cannot eat yet enables another to eat. Does the same logic not apply to the wife\u8217's slaves?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina replies that these two cases are categorically different. The grandmother eats by virtue of a descendant who traces back to the {\i kohen} lineage \u8212- a separate and independent principle. The question in the {\i mishnah} concerns eating by virtue of a {\i kinyan}, specifically the rule {\i kinyan ochel maachil}: only a {\i kinyan} that eats can feed. A {\i kinyan} that cannot itself eat \u8212- here, the wife who is a {\i chalalah} \u8212- cannot enable those she owns to eat. That is the first explanation of the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a second approach. In truth, {\i mid\u8217'raisa} \u8212- on the level of Torah law \u8212- the slaves could eat. They are connected to the {\i kohen} and, independent of the wife\u8217's personal disqualification, they retain a basis for eating {\i terumah} through him. However, the rabbis instituted a {\i gezeira} preventing the slaves from eating in this case. The reasoning is social and corrective: the {\i kohen} finds himself in a forbidden union, married to a woman who should not have entered into this marriage with him. By disallowing the slaves from eating, the rabbis create pressure. The wife says: I cannot eat {\i terumah}, my slaves cannot eat {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i zona hi etzlo}, I am like a stranger to him, excluded from the very benefits of his priestly status. This discontent, the rabbis reasoned, would motivate the couple to dissolve the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi: Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi also agrees in principle that the slaves could eat, but offers a different rationale for the rabbinic prohibition. The {\i gezeira} is {\i shema ta\u8217'achil achar misa} \u8212- lest she continue feeding the slaves {\i terumah} even after the {\i kohen} dies. Having become accustomed to feeding them during the marriage, she might simply continue the practice after his death, when she is no longer entitled to do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: by that logic, why should any {\i bat Yisrael} married to a {\i kohen} be permitted to feed her slaves {\i terumah}? She, too, might continue after his death. Should we not decree against her as well? This objection effectively forces Rav Ashi to refine his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almanah Kohenet Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern, says Rav Ashi, is specific to the case of an {\i almanah} married to a {\i kohen gadol} who is herself a {\i bat kohen}. Here is where the {\i gezeira} finds its most compelling foothold. Such a woman, upon the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's death, could reason as follows: {\i me\u8217'ikara achli bitruma devei nasha} \u8212- originally, my slaves ate {\i terumah} by virtue of my father\u8217's household, because I was a {\i bat kohen}. Then, when I married this {\i kohen gadol}, they continued eating by virtue of my husband. Now that he has died, they should revert to their original status and continue eating on account of my being a {\i bat kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What she fails to realize is that by entering into the forbidden union \u8212- as an {\i almanah} who had relations with a {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- she became a {\i chalalah}. She is no longer a {\i bat kohen} in the relevant legal sense. Her slaves cannot eat by virtue of her father\u8217's household anymore, because that status has been forfeited. The fear is that she will not appreciate this transformation and will permit the slaves to eat {\i terumah} erroneously after his death. The {\i gezeira} addresses precisely this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uniform Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: very well, the concern makes sense when the wife is an {\i almanah kohenet}, but why does the {\i mishnah} apply this ruling even when she is a {\i bat Yisrael} \u8212- an ordinary Jewess who has no such parallel status to return to? The {\i gemara} answers with the principle {\i lo plug rabanan} \u8212- the rabbis did not differentiate within a single category. Since the {\i gezeira} was necessary for the case of the {\i almanah kohenet}, the rabbis applied it uniformly to all cases of {\i almanah}, without distinguishing between those who are daughters of {\i kohanim} and those who are not. This avoids confusion and ensures consistent application of the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize: two explanatory frameworks emerge. The first, Ravina\u8217's, holds that the slaves eat only by virtue of the wife, and since she is a {\i chalalah} and cannot eat, she cannot extend that right \u8212- {\i kinyan ochel maachil}. The second, shared by Rava and Rav Ashi, holds that in principle the slaves could eat through the {\i kohen}, but a rabbinic {\i gezeira} overrides this, whether to discourage the forbidden marriage or to prevent erroneous feeding after the husband\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzon Barzel Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now takes up a distinct but related question about {\i nichsei tzon barzel}. When a woman brings assessed property into the marriage \u8212- the husband enters the value into the {\i ketubah}, accepting full financial responsibility \u8212- and the marriage subsequently ends through divorce or death, what exactly is she entitled to recover? She assessed, say, a set of vessels at one thousand {\i zuz}. Those vessels are still extant. Does she retrieve the vessels themselves, or only their assessed monetary value?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The wife says: {\i keli ani notelet} \u8212- I take my vessels. The husband says: {\i damim ani noten} \u8212- I owe you money, not objects. The dispute is whether title fundamentally transferred to the husband upon the bringing in of the dowry, leaving her only a creditor\u8217's claim, or whether she retained some form of ownership such that she may demand the objects back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda rules {\i hadin imah} \u8212- the law favors the wife. She is entitled to retrieve the actual objects. His reasoning is {\i mishum shevach beit aviha} \u8212- on account of the benefit, or honor, of her father\u8217's house. This is the property she received from her family, and she is entitled to reclaim it. It is admittedly not a purely technical legal argument \u8212- it is an appeal to equitable principle \u8212- but it is the position Rav Yehuda maintains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ami rules {\i hadin imo} \u8212- the law favors the husband. His reasoning flows from the language of the {\i mishnah} itself: {\i im mesu mesu lo v\u8217'im hosiru hosiru lo} \u8212- if the property dies or is lost, the loss is his; if it increases in value, the gain is his. This, says Rav Ami, demonstrates conclusively that title has transferred. He bears all risk and receives all benefit. The property is therefore his, and she is merely a creditor for its assessed value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Renting Analogy Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to probe the logic by noting the {\i mishnah}\u8217's further language: {\i v\u8217'hoil v\u8217'chayav b\u8217'achrayusan yochalu} \u8212- and since he is responsible for them, the slaves eat {\i terumah}. This seems to confirm both that the slaves belong to him and that his responsibility is characteristic of ownership. But does mere responsibility, {\i achrayus}, suffice to allow slaves to eat {\i terumah} even if they are not technically owned by the {\i kohen}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is introduced: a {\i Yisrael} who rents a cow from a {\i kohen} \u8212- the cow remains the {\i kohen}\u8217's, and the renter may feed it animal fodder of {\i terumah}. Conversely, a {\i kohen} who rents a cow from a {\i Yisrael}, even though he is responsible for feeding it and bears the liability of a renter, may not feed it {\i terumah}, because it remains the {\i Yisrael}\u8217's cow. Mere {\i achrayus} of the renter\u8217's level does not confer the right to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: there are degrees of {\i achrayus}, and rental is a low degree. A renter is liable for theft and loss, but not for unavoidable accident, not for the animal becoming thinner through natural attrition, and not for a decline in market value. By contrast, the husband who takes in {\i nichsei tzon barzel} is responsible for the full assessed value regardless of fault, market changes, or any other cause. If the slaves were worth one thousand when they entered the marriage and the market drops so they are now worth five hundred at divorce, he must nonetheless restore the full thousand. This degree of {\i achrayus} is qualitatively different \u8212- it resembles ownership far more than rental does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Responsibility vs.\u160 ?Owner\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is therefore pressed: perhaps this extraordinary level of responsibility is tantamount to ownership after all, which would support Rav Ami. But it resists this conclusion. The {\i baraita} itself offers a parallel case that illuminates the middle ground. If a {\i Yisrael} assesses a cow from a {\i kohen} and takes full responsibility to return its assessed value regardless of circumstance, he may not feed it {\i terumah} \u8212- the cow remains the {\i kohen}\u8217's. Conversely, if a {\i kohen} does the same from a {\i Yisrael} \u8212- undertakes full assessed-value responsibility \u8212- he may feed it {\i terumah}, even though technically speaking the cow has not been transferred in title. This suggests that a sufficiently high level of {\i achrayus} can ground the right to eat {\i terumah} without constituting full legal ownership. Applying this to the dowry slaves: the husband\u8217's extraordinary responsibility may suffice for the slaves to eat {\i terumah}, while the wife technically retains her entitlement to reclaim the objects themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah and Rav Yosef were present at the end of one of Rav Nachman\u8217's public lectures and remarked that there exists {\i baraita} support for both positions. In favor of Rav Ami: {\i avdei tzon barzel yotzin b\u8217'shen v\u8217'ayin la\u8217'ish aval lo l\u8217'isha} \u8212- if the husband knocks out the eye or tooth of a {\i tzon barzel} slave, the slave goes free, because the husband is treated as the slave\u8217's master; but if the wife does the same, the slave does not go free, suggesting the husband holds full title. In favor of Rav Yehuda: if the husband wishes to sell the dowry property, he may not. This is a powerful indication that she retains rights over the property that transcend a mere creditor\u8217's claim. Indeed, even property that the husband himself contributed to her dowry cannot be sold by him unilaterally. This case came before Rabban Gamliel, who voided the sale and ordered restitution from the buyers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: No Sale Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The force of this {\i baraita} \u8212- that neither party may sell the dowry property without mutual consent, and that Rabban Gamliel enforced this rule against buyers \u8212- strongly supports Rav Yehuda\u8217's position. If he held full title and merely owed a monetary debt, there would be no legal basis to void his sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules in accordance with Rav Yehuda: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rav Yehuda} \u8212- she is entitled to retrieve the actual property she brought in. The {\i baraita} concerning the prohibition on sale was evidently persuasive, and Rava adds that the reasoning {\i mishum shevach beit aviha} \u8212- the property of her father\u8217's house \u8212- supports giving her the right to reclaim what she brought in, even though the {\i baraita} supporting Rav Ami regarding {\i shen v\u8217'ayin} presented a counterweight. As Tosafot notes, even if the slaves technically belong to her, it is conceivable that the {\i kohen}\u8217's extensive rights over them \u8212- rights sufficient for them to eat {\i terumah} on his account \u8212- could also ground the {\i shen v\u8217'ayin} rule without establishing full title in him. Rava thus dismisses that {\i baraita} as a conclusive proof for Rav Ami and rules for Rav Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shroud Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical story follows. A woman brought a fine wool cloak into her marriage as part of her {\i tzon barzel} dowry. The husband died. Before she had an opportunity to act, the orphans \u8212- the husband\u8217's heirs \u8212- took the cloak and used it as a shroud for the deceased. Something designated and used as a shroud for a corpse is {\i asur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- forbidden for any benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: {\i kanye misna} \u8212- the dead body has, as it were, acquired it. Its status has been permanently transformed. While the wife retains her monetary claim against the estate for the assessed value of the cloak, she loses any right to reclaim this particular object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collection Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling prompted a sharp question from Nemchi, the grandson of Rav Yosef the son of Rava, to Rav Kahana: but do we not rule like Rav Yehuda that she is entitled to retrieve the objects? If so, the cloak was hers. The orphans had no right to use her property as a shroud, and their doing so should not affect her claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana responded: {\i mi lo modei Rabbi Yehuda demchusar guvaina} \u8212- even Rav Yehuda concedes that her entitlement requires actual collection. The right to reclaim exists, but it has not yet been exercised. Until she actively retrieves the object, it remains under the husband\u8217's \u8212- or now the heirs\u8217' \u8212- control. {\i Dekeivan demchusar guvaina birshuseih kai} \u8212- since it has not yet been collected, it stands in his domain. The heirs are therefore in a position to use it, and their doing so \u8212- transforming it into a shroud, rendering it {\i asur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- removes her practical rights in this object, even if she is technically owed its value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to a broader principle that Rava articulates: {\i hekdesh chametz veshichrur motzi\u8217'in miyedei shibbud} \u8212- sanctification, {\i chametz} on Passover, and manumission of a slave are three situations where a change in the object\u8217's essential identity dissolves an existing lien or claim. Here, the transformation of the cloak into a {\i tachrichim} \u8212- a burial shroud that is {\i asur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- operates analogously to {\i chametz} becoming prohibited on Passover, severing the wife\u8217's rights in the object itself. This illustrates that even where Rav Yehuda\u8217's ruling grants her the right to retrieve, that right must be actively exercised; it does not automatically transfer control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Vessels Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda then presents a concrete scenario that bears on the practical implications of his own ruling. A woman brings two vessels worth a total of one thousand {\i zuz} into the marriage; by the time of divorce or the husband\u8217's death, they are worth two thousand {\i zuz}. What does she retrieve?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is: she takes one vessel as part of her {\i ketubah} entitlement \u8212- that is, within the original assessed value \u8212- and for the second vessel, she pays the difference in value and retrieves it as well, {\i mipnei shevach beit aviha}. Even though the vessels have doubled in value, she is entitled to take both back and simply compensate for the increase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: is this ruling necessary? Has Rav Yehuda not already stated that she is entitled to retrieve the property? The answer is that one might have thought her entitlement to retrieve extends only up to the original assessed value \u8212- meaning she could take back property worth up to a thousand, but not beyond that threshold. The ruling teaches that even when the property has appreciated beyond its original value, she may still retrieve all of it by paying the increment. The principle {\i shevach beit aviha} \u8212- the special consideration given to property that she brought from her father\u8217's house \u8212- extends to allow her to reclaim the full quantity of objects she originally brought in, with the increase in value paid out to the estate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fetus & Slave Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ubar Posel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns from the question of forbidden marriage and its effect on the wife\u8217's status to a different dynamic entirely: the role of an unborn fetus in determining whether the slaves of a deceased {\i kohen} may eat {\i terumah}. The case is as follows. A {\i bat Yisrael} marries a {\i kohen}, the {\i kohen} dies, and she is left pregnant. What is the status of the slaves?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As a general rule, when a {\i kohen} dies and leaves a child through his wife, that wife may continue eating {\i terumah} on account of her child\u8217's connection to the priestly lineage. But that applies to a born child. Here, only an unborn fetus remains. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i lo yochlu avadeha biterumah mipnei chelko shel ubar} \u8212- the slaves may not eat {\i terumah} on account of the fetus\u8217's portion. That is, we assume the fetus will be a son and will therefore inherit a portion of the estate, including a portion of the slaves. Because he is a partial owner, the slaves cannot eat {\i terumah} by virtue of him \u8212- not yet, since he is unborn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yossi\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} encapsulates the rule: {\i she\u8217'ha\u8217'ubar posel ve\u8217'eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- the fetus invalidates and does not feed. He is in the worst of both positions simultaneously. On the one hand, his partial ownership of the estate \u8212- specifically, of the slaves \u8212- invalidates them from eating {\i terumah} through that portion he owns. On the other hand, he is not yet sufficient of a person to enable his mother to eat {\i terumah}, and cannot cause his own portion of the slaves to eat through him. Were he fully nonexistent in legal terms, the other heirs\u8217' portions of the slaves would eat unimpeded. Were he fully born, his mother would eat and all the slaves would eat. In his intermediate state, he creates problems without providing any benefit. This is the position of Rabbi Yossi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sages in the {\i mishnah} respond by pointing out that this logic should apply equally when the mother is a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i kohen}. If the {\i kohen} dies and she is pregnant, by the same reasoning the slaves should not eat on account of the fetus\u8217's portion. Rabbi Yossi\u8217's response, and whether the sages are genuinely disagreeing or merely drawing out the implications, remains to be developed in the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reason A: Non-Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the question of what exactly underlies Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling. Two possible reasons are entertained, and the difference between them has a practical {\i nafka mina}. The first possibility: {\i ubar b\u8217'mei zara zar hu} \u8212- an unborn fetus takes on the legal identity of his mother. Before birth, the fetus is considered an extension of his mother \u8212- {\i ubar yerech imo}. Since his mother in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case is a {\i bat Yisrael}, a non-{\i kohen}, the fetus is also considered a non-{\i kohen}. If a partial owner of the slaves is a non-{\i kohen}, those slaves cannot eat {\i terumah} through that portion, because a {\i zar} \u8212- a non-priest \u8212- cannot confer the right to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reason B: Yalud Maachil\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possibility: {\i yalud machil she\u8217'ein yalud eino machil} \u8212- only a born person can enable others to eat {\i terumah} through him; an unborn person cannot. On this reading, the fetus\u8217's {\i kohen} status is not in question \u8212- once born, he will be a {\i kohen} \u8212- but his being unborn disqualifies him from serving as the basis for others\u8217' eating of {\i terumah}. The incapacity is not about identity but about actualized legal personhood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} between these two reasons is stark: what if the mother is a {\i bat kohen}? If the reason is {\i ubar b\u8217'mei zara zar hu} \u8212- that the fetus takes on his mother\u8217's non-{\i kohen} identity \u8212- then when his mother is a {\i bat kohen}, that concern disappears. The fetus\u8217's mother is a {\i kohen}, and therefore the fetus is not a {\i zar}. In that case, the slaves would eat. But if the reason is {\i yalud machil she\u8217'ein yalud eino machil}, it makes no difference whether his mother is a {\i bat kohen} or a {\i bat Yisrael} \u8212- the problem is solely that he has not yet been born.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages: No Fetus Share\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah identifies reason A as Rabbi Yossi\u8217's actual position: {\i ubar b\u8217'mei zara zar hu} \u8212- the fetus, while still in a non-priestly mother, takes her identity and is therefore a {\i zar} whose portion of the slaves cannot eat {\i terumah}. Rav Yosef identifies reason B: {\i yalud machil she\u8217'ein yalud eino machil}, independent of the mother\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents the disagreement of the sages and unpacks it. The sages of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} who respond to Rabbi Yossi \u8212- hold the following position. If there are born children of the deceased {\i kohen}, they eat {\i terumah} by virtue of those children. If there are no children, others eat {\i terumah} by virtue of the {\i kohen}\u8217's brothers or by virtue of any other living member of the priestly family. The presence of the unborn fetus does not alter these arrangements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Assumptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What drives the sages\u8217' disagreement? Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling rests on two assumptions. Assumption one: the fetus already acquires a portion of the estate, even before birth. Assumption two: since he is unborn, no one may eat {\i terumah} by virtue of that portion. The {\i chachamim} reject assumption one outright. Until the fetus is born, he has no title to the estate whatsoever. He is not a legal heir in the present moment. The other relatives \u8212- sons, brothers, or extended family \u8212- are the current heirs, and the slaves eat {\i terumah} through them without any interference from an unborn fetus who holds no current legal claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the important implication: even the {\i chachamim} do not necessarily deny that once born the child will inherit. Their claim is merely temporal \u8212- until birth, he holds nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Stance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda reports in the name of Shmuel: {\i zo divrei Rabbi Yossi} \u8212- this, the ruling of the {\i mishnah}, is the position of Rabbi Yossi. The phrasing suggests that Shmuel is presenting Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling as a particular opinion, implying he himself may not hold it. This leads the {\i gemara} to question whether Shmuel actually rejects Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this impression: did Shmuel not say to Rav Chana Bagdatna \u8212- {\i puk aytei li bei asara ve-eima lachu be-anpaihu ha-mezakeh le-ubar kanah} \u8212- go gather me ten people, and I will declare publicly before them that one who transfers an asset to a fetus acquires it on the fetus\u8217's behalf? If Shmuel holds that a fetus can receive a transfer of title through someone acting on his behalf, then presumably he also holds that a fetus inherits from his father \u8212- which is precisely assumption one of Rabbi Yossi. How then can Shmuel be understood as rejecting Rabbi Yossi?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fetus Can Inherit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i ela zu ve-sevira leh} \u8212- Shmuel is not dismissing Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling. He agrees with it, and by saying \u8220"this is the position of Rabbi Yossi,\u8221" he is not distancing himself but rather identifying and affirming it. He is informing his audience that there is a competing opinion \u8212- that of the {\i chachamim} who deny assumption one \u8212- of which they should be aware. But Shmuel himself sides with Rabbi Yossi, consistent with his broader principle that a fetus can acquire title.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i mahu mashma lan} \u8212- what is the novelty in Shmuel\u8217's statement? The answer is that Shmuel is letting us know that the {\i chachamim} do in fact disagree with Rabbi Yossi, even though their formulation in the {\i mishnah} may have sounded like they were merely drawing out his logic rather than disputing him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further complication is introduced. Rabbi Zakai reports that Rabbi Yossi gave this testimony in the name of Shemaya and Avtalyon, and the sages conceded to him \u8212- {\i ve-hodu lo}. Does this mean the ruling achieved consensus? Rav Ashi responds: {\i mi katani ve-kibbelu} \u8212- it does not say they accepted it. {\i Ve-hodu lo katani} \u8212- they conceded, meaning {\i de-mistabar tameh} \u8212- they acknowledged the reasoning was cogent, but this falls short of a formal ruling that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yossi. The tradition from Shemaya and Avtalyon confers weight to Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position, but does not settle the dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} now plays out the full range of scenarios according to Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position. If the {\i kohen} dies leaving sons \u8212- and no pregnancy \u8212- then {\i elu va\u8217'elu ochlin}: all the slaves eat. The {\i nichsei tzon barzel} slaves eat because they form part of the estate now in the sons\u8217' possession. The {\i nichsei milug} slaves eat because, with living children from the marriage, the wife continues to eat {\i terumah} and therefore her slaves eat through her. If the {\i kohen} dies leaving his wife pregnant and no other children \u8212- {\i elu va\u8217'elu ein ochlin}: none of the slaves eat. She cannot eat on account of an unborn child, and both categories of slaves are blocked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he leaves both sons and a pregnant wife \u8212- a mixed case \u8212- then {\i avdei melog ochlin kederech shehi ochelet}: the {\i milug} slaves eat, because the presence of born children entitles the wife to eat, and therefore her slaves eat through her. But {\i avdei tzon barzel lo yochlu mipnei chelko shel ubar}: the estate\u8217's slaves cannot eat because the fetus holds a partial ownership in them, and that partial ownership \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ha\u8217'ubar posel ve\u8217'eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- invalidates their eating without itself enabling them to eat through the fetus. The born sons\u8217' portions would normally suffice, but the fetus\u8217's portion creates an insoluble impediment for that share of the slaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yossi adds a ruling in his father\u8217's name: {\i ha\u8217'bat ma\u8217'achelet, ha\u8217'ben eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- a daughter enables the mother to eat, but a son does not. The precise meaning of this statement is not immediately clear and the {\i gemara} will unpack it in subsequent discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai adds yet another nuance: {\i zecharim yochlu kulam, nekevot lo yochlu} \u8212- all males eat, but females do not. His reasoning appears to hinge on a concern about the possibility of finding a male fetus \u8212- {\i shema yimtza ubar zachar} \u8212- but the full elaboration of his position, along with the full analysis of Rabbi Yishmael bar Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling, will be taken up in the continuation of the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 67\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pregnant Woman & Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the bottom of 67a, where the Gemara presents a {\i baraita} that elaborates on the positions already introduced in the {\i Mishnah}. The {\i baraita} examines a series of scenarios involving a woman married to a {\i kohen} whose husband has died, asking in each case whether her slaves may eat {\i terumah}. The three basic cases are: born children only, a fetus only, and a combination of born children alongside a current pregnancy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Born Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first case \u8212- {\i hiniach banim}, where the husband left behind born sons \u8212- the ruling is {\i elu v\u8217'elu okhlim}: both the woman and all the slaves may eat {\i terumah}. The born child entitles the woman to eat, and the slaves, now fully owned by the {\i kohen}\u8217's heirs, eat by virtue of that ownership. There is no complication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fetus Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second case \u8212- {\i hiniach ubar}, where the only surviving connection is a fetus \u8212- the ruling reverses: {\i elu v\u8217'elu ein okhlim}, neither the woman nor the slaves may eat. The woman cannot eat on behalf of the fetus, since only a born child confers that right. And the slaves cannot eat because they are owned, at least in part, by the fetus \u8212- and the fetus cannot cause them to eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Born + Pregnant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case \u8212- {\i hiniach banim v\u8217'hiniach uberet}, where there are born children and the woman is also pregnant \u8212- produces a split ruling. Her own slaves, the {\i avdei milug}, may eat, because she herself eats by virtue of the born children, and her slaves follow her status. However, his slaves \u8212- the {\i avdei tzon barzel}, which remain part of the estate \u8212- may not eat, {\i mipnei chelko shel ubar}, because the fetus holds a portion of what the born sons have inherited, thereby creating a problem. The principle articulated is {\i she\u8217'ubar posel v\u8217'eino ma\u8217'achil}: the fetus invalidates \u8212- enough to prevent the estate slaves from eating \u8212- but does not itself feed, meaning the woman cannot eat on its account. This is identified as the position of Rabbi Yose, consistent with what the {\i Mishnah} already established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon Yochai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai offers a different position. He rules that if the living children are male \u8212- {\i zekharim yokhlu kulam} \u8212- then all the slaves may eat despite the pregnancy. If the living children are female \u8212- {\i nekevot lo yokhlu} \u8212- the slaves may not eat, {\i shema yimatze ubar zakhar}, lest the fetus turn out to be male and thereby inherit the entire estate, leaving the daughters with nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minority Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious question on Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai\u8217's position regarding male children is: why aren\u8217't we concerned that the fetus might be a boy and thereby hold a portion in the estate? The answer the Gemara provides is that Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai holds {\i ein choshashin l\u8217'miuta} \u8212- one need not be concerned with a minority. Since there is only a 50% chance the fetus will be a boy, and since not every pregnancy results in a live birth, the probability that a live baby boy will emerge is less than 50% \u8212- call it something like 48%. That minority possibility does not generate enough concern to prevent the slaves from eating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, it must be said, a striking position. In the realm of {\i issurim}, we generally are concerned even with much smaller minorities when there is a {\i miut hamatzuy} \u8212- a commonly occurring minority. {\i Tosafot} wrestles with precisely this tension. One possible distinction is the unique status of the {\i ubar}, something that is not yet {\i lefanenu}, not yet present before us \u8212- but the oddness of disregarding a near-50% possibility is not lost on the commentators.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the case of daughters only: the Gemara notes that there the problem is actually two-fold. If the fetus is a girl, she splits the estate with the daughters already alive. If the fetus is a boy, he displaces them entirely. So the {\i baraita}\u8217's formulation \u8212- {\i shema yimatze ubar zakhar} \u8212- is not the exclusive reason. The Gemara explains: {\i chada v\u8217'od ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the {\i baraita} states one reason and then an additional, stronger one. First, even a female fetus would create a problem for the existing daughters\u8217' portion. And furthermore, there is the additional concern that the fetus may be male and take everything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the case of male children, the Gemara now asks: granted that Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai holds we need not concern ourselves with the minority \u8212- but perhaps there is an alternative explanation that does not depend on the {\i miuta} principle at all. Perhaps Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai actually does hold {\i choshashin l\u8217'miuta}, that one is generally concerned with minorities, but holds that there is an independent mechanism that resolves the problem even if the fetus would be a boy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apotropos Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That mechanism is the ruling of Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel: {\i yetomim sheba\u8217'u lachalok benichsei avihem} \u8212- when orphaned heirs, some adults and some minors, come to divide their father\u8217's estate, {\i beit din} appoints an {\i apotropos}, a guardian or steward, who selects a favorable portion on behalf of the minors. The question of whether the minors, upon reaching adulthood, can subsequently challenge and reverse that assignment is itself disputed: the ruling in the name of Shmuel suggests they can, while Rav Nachman himself holds {\i higdilu ein yekholim limchot} \u8212- once they come of age, the apportionment stands, because to allow reversal would undermine the authority and effectiveness of {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application to the {\i ubar} is as follows: rather than allowing the fetus to hold an undivided fractional share in every slave in the estate \u8212- which would prevent all of them from eating \u8212- {\i beit din} could appoint an {\i apotropos} for the fetus and carve out a specific portion of the estate on his behalf. If there are ten slaves, the {\i apotropos} might assign two of them to the fetus\u8217's account. Those two slaves cannot eat, since the fetus owns them and a {\i ubar} cannot cause slaves to eat. But the remaining eight, now wholly owned by the born brothers, can eat freely. The {\i apotropos} effectively transforms a fractional co-ownership of every slave into an exclusive ownership of specific slaves \u8212- freeing the majority for {\i terumah} consumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what happens if the fetus is ultimately a girl? Then the entire premise of the assignment would need to be revisited. This solution was constructed on the assumption the fetus would be a boy. If a girl is born, the allocation would need to be redistributed. And in the case where the existing children are daughters, the {\i apotropos} solution is more complicated, since a male fetus would be entitled to everything, not just a fractional share.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apotropos Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then considers whether the underlying dispute between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai might itself reflect a dispute about the validity of the {\i apotropos} mechanism \u8212- i.e., whether {\i beit din} can in fact make such an assignment for an {\i ubar}. But the Gemara dismisses this: {\i lo}. Everyone can accept the ruling of Rav Nachman. The {\i apotropos} idea is set aside entirely. The dispute returns to its simpler formulation: the question is simply whether we are concerned with the minority possibility that the {\i ubar} will be born as a live male. The {\i tanna kamma} holds {\i choshashin l\u8217'miuta} and is therefore concerned; Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai holds {\i ein choshashin l\u8217'miuta} and is therefore not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yishmael\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Puzzle Stated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} also records a teaching of Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yose, transmitted in his father\u8217's name: {\i ha-bat ma\u8217'akhila, ha-ben eino ma\u8217'akhil} \u8212- the daughter feeds, the son does not feed. This statement, on its face, is deeply puzzling. Everything we have seen until now suggested the opposite: a son in the estate is the mechanism that allows eating, while a daughter does not carry the same inheritance rights and therefore creates different dynamics. How can it be that daughters allow the slaves to eat but sons do not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limited Estate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves this by positing that the case concerns a {\i nechasim mu\u8217'atim} \u8212- a small or limited estate. The relevant {\i halachah} is that when an estate is small, the daughters\u8217' claim to be supported takes priority over the sons\u8217' right to inherit. The daughters are entitled by rabbinic enactment to be maintained from the estate until they marry, and if the estate is small enough that fully supporting them leaves nothing for the sons, so be it \u8212- the sons may have to go without, while the daughters\u8217' support rights are fully honored. In such a case, the daughters\u8217' claim effectively controls the estate, even though technical title may still rest with the sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters\u8217' Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case Rabbi Yishmael is describing, then, involves a living son, a living daughter, and a pregnant woman. The {\i baraita}\u8217's teaching can be parsed as follows. {\i Ha-ben eino ma\u8217'akhil} \u8212- if there is only a son, the slaves cannot eat, because the fetus might also be a boy and would then hold an equal portion in the estate alongside the living son. {\i Ha-bat ma\u8217'akhila} \u8212- but if there is also a daughter, the slaves can eat. Why? Because in a {\i nechasim mu\u8217'atim} situation, the daughter\u8217's support rights consume the entire estate. The slaves are effectively at her disposal, and her entitlement is what governs whether they eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fetus & Daughters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This then raises a further question: even granting the daughter\u8217's priority, why doesn\u8217't the fetus disrupt her control of the estate? Consider the two possibilities. If the fetus is a boy, does he get a portion of this small estate alongside the daughter? In a {\i nechasim mu\u8217'atim} case, no \u8212- the daughter\u8217's support rights trump even a male heir\u8217's claim. If the fetus is a girl, should she not be entitled to a share of the daughters\u8217' support allocation? Here the {\i halachah} distinguishes sharply: the daughters\u8217' support rights established by rabbinic enactment apply only to born daughters. {\i Kama de-lo nafka la\u8217'avir ha\u8217'olam, lo takinu rabbanan} \u8212- until she emerges into the world as a born person, the Rabbis did not extend their enactment to cover her. The fetus, therefore, cannot diminish the living daughter\u8217's claim, whether the fetus would be male or female.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estate Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, challenges Abaye\u8217's reading. The end of the {\i baraita} \u8212- in Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai\u8217's statement \u8212- says that if the fetus turns out to be male, the daughters get nothing, {\i v\u8217'ein la-banot bimkom ha-ben klum}. This implies that in the scenario Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai is discussing, the son takes everything and the daughters have no claim. But that is precisely the opposite of a {\i nechasim mu\u8217'atim} case, where the daughters\u8217' rights would prevail. So the {\i baraita} appears to be addressing a {\i nechasim merubim} \u8212- a large estate \u8212- not a small one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's response is that the two halves of the {\i baraita} need not address the same scenario: the statement of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai concerns a large estate, while Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's statement concerns a small one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then pushes further. Even granting that the daughters control a small estate in terms of support, the {\i halachah} established by Rabbi Assi in the name of Rabbi Yochanan is that if the sons of such an estate go ahead and sell it \u8212- {\i yetomim shekadmu u\u8217'makhru be-nechasim mu\u8217'atim, mah she-makhru makhru} \u8212- the sale is binding. This implies that the legal title to the property remains with the sons, not the daughters. The daughters have a {\i right} against the estate, something like a debt or lien, but title is the sons\u8217'. If so, the slaves are the sons\u8217' slaves, and the question of whether the daughters \u8220"control\u8221" the estate in a way that determines eating rights becomes considerably more complicated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother & Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Faced with this difficulty, the Gemara proposes an entirely different reading of the {\i baraita}. The words {\i bat} and {\i ben} in Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's statement do not mean daughter and son at all \u8212- they mean {\i em} and {\i av}, mother and father. {\i Ha-bat ma\u8217'akhila} means the mother\u8217's property enables eating; {\i ha-ben eino ma\u8217'akhil} means the father\u8217's property does not. In other words, the statement is simply recapitulating what Rabbi Yose already established: when there are born children and a pregnant woman, her {\i avdei milug} eat because she eats, while his {\i avdei tzon barzel} do not eat because of the fetus\u8217's portion in the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading is admittedly forced \u8212- {\i bat} and {\i ben} as stand-ins for mother and father is an unusual usage \u8212- but the Gemara accepts it: {\i kula Rabbi Yishmael beno shel Rabbi Yosi katani}. The entire teaching of Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yose was simply an elaboration and explanation of the ruling already stated in his father\u8217's name. It adds nothing substantively new.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gray Zone Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gray Zone Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} now turns to a broader framework that extends well beyond the specific question of slaves and {\i terumah}. The underlying issue is a woman who stands in an intermediate relationship \u8212- partially connected to her husband\u8217's household, partially not. The practical consequence of this ambiguity plays out differently depending on the woman\u8217's lineage and her husband\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the framework, consider two mirror-image scenarios. First: a {\i bat kohen} who married a {\i Yisrael}. If her husband dies and she has no children, she reverts to her father\u8217's household and may once again eat {\i terumah}. But if she has children, she remains connected to her deceased husband\u8217's household and does not revert; she cannot eat {\i terumah}. Second: a {\i bat Yisrael} who married a {\i kohen}. If her husband dies and she has children, she remains connected to his household and may continue eating {\i terumah}. But if there are no children, she reverts to her father\u8217's household and loses the right to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gray zone arises when the connection to the husband is real but incomplete. Such a partial connection is strong enough to pull the woman out of her father\u8217's household \u8212- and thereby prevent a {\i bat kohen} who married a {\i Yisrael} from reverting and eating {\i terumah} \u8212- yet it is not strong enough to fully integrate her into the husband\u8217's household, and therefore does not entitle a {\i bat Yisrael} who married a {\i kohen} to eat {\i terumah} on the basis of that connection. In the language of the {\i Mishnah}: these cases {\i poslim v\u8217'lo ma\u8217'akhilim} \u8212- they invalidate but do not feed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} lists several such cases, each presenting its own variety of partial or ambiguous connection: the {\i ubar} (fetus), the {\i yavam} (brother-in-law awaiting {\i yibum}), {\i erusin} (betrothal without consummation), a {\i cheresh} (deaf-mute husband), and a nine-year-old in a specific context. Before unpacking each case, the {\i Mishnah} also mentions a {\i safek} variant: situations where it is uncertain whether the man is nine years old, or uncertain whether he has reached halachic adulthood through the appearance of two pubic hairs. Even these doubts are sufficient to invalidate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} includes a digression about a collapsed building that killed both a man and his niece \u8212- his wife \u8212- leaving unresolved which died first. This bears on whether the surviving co-wife undergoes {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. The connection to the present context is the shared theme of {\i yavam} and {\i safek} cases; the Gemara notes it is a digression, explained by the fact that {\i yavam} was just mentioned and {\i safek} cases were at hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fetus Gray Zone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now provides the sourcing for each case. Regarding the {\i ubar}: if the woman is a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i Yisrael}, the fetus invalidates her from reverting to eat {\i terumah}, because the verse says {\i ki-neureiha} \u8212- she returns to her father\u8217's house as in her youth \u8212- and {\i perat l\u8217'muberet}, the case of pregnancy is excluded, as she is still too connected to be considered fully reverted. If she is a {\i bat Yisrael} married to a {\i kohen}, the fetus does not allow her to eat because of the principle {\i yalud ma\u8217'akhil she\u8217'ein yalud ma\u8217'akhil} \u8212- only a born child, not an unborn one, generates the right to eat. No verse is quoted for this latter point; it is treated as an established axiom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam Gray Zone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i yavam} \u8212- the brother-in-law awaiting {\i yibum} \u8212- presents the same dual dynamic. If the woman is a {\i bat kohen} and the brother-in-law is a {\i Yisrael}, he invalidates her from returning to eat {\i terumah}, because {\i v\u8217'shava el beit aviha} \u8212- she can only return to her father\u8217's house \u8212- and {\i perat l\u8217'shomeres yavam}, she cannot return while she is still bound to the {\i yavam} and requires {\i chalitzah} to free herself. If she is a {\i bat Yisrael} and the {\i yavam} is a {\i kohen}, the {\i zikah} relationship is not strong enough to entitle her to eat, because {\i kinyan kaspa amar Rachmana} \u8212- the Torah requires that she be his acquisition of money, i.e., properly betrothed \u8212- and at this stage she remains a woman his brother married, not yet his own acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erusin Gray Zone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i erusin} \u8212- betrothal without consummation \u8212- is almost a {\i kal va-chomer} from the {\i yavam}: here there has been genuine {\i kiddushin}, yet the relationship remains in a gray zone. If the woman is a {\i bat kohen} and her betrothed is a {\i Yisrael}, the betrothal invalidates her, because {\i d\u8217'hakanya bahavaya} \u8212- the verse \u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1514 ? \u1499 ?\u1492 ?\u1503 ? \u1499 ?\u1497 ? \u1514 ?\u1492 ?\u1497 ?\u1492 ? \u1500 ?\u1488 ?\u1497 ?\u1513 ? \u1494 ?\u1512 ?, \u8220"and a daughter of a {\i kohen} when she belongs to a foreign man,\u8221" establishes that once there is any connection to another man, she is no longer entirely in her father\u8217's household. Even though conceptually the period of {\i erusin} is still the time when she lives in her father\u8217's house before moving to her husband\u8217's house at {\i nissu\u8217'in}, the connection to the other man is real and sufficient to disqualify her from eating on the basis of her father\u8217's household.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she is a {\i bat Yisrael} and her betrothed is a {\i kohen}, the betrothal does not entitle her to eat. Here the Gemara invokes the ruling of Ulla. There is actually a dispute between the Bavli and the Yerushalmi on this point: the Bavli\u8217's logic would suggest that {\i kiddushin}, which is effected through money and constitutes a form of {\i kinyan}, should in principle entitle her to eat already at the stage of {\i erusin}. The Yerushalmi, by contrast, holds that the mechanism for a wife\u8217's eating is {\i beiso hem yokhlu b\u8217'lachmo} \u8212- being part of his household \u8212- which applies only after {\i nissu\u8217'in}. According to the Yerushalmi, it is actually a biblical prohibition for her to eat {\i terumah} before {\i nissu\u8217'in}. According to the Bavli, Ulla\u8217's rationale is practical and rabbinic: during {\i erusin} the woman still lives in her father\u8217's house, and if the {\i kohen} sends her {\i terumah}, she will end up sharing it with her father and brothers, who are not permitted to eat it. This concern provides the rabbinic basis for not allowing her to eat during this period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf-Mute Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A marriage to a {\i cheresh} \u8212- a deaf-mute \u8212- presents yet another gradient. The status of the {\i cheresh} in halachic discourse is significant: treated in the Talmudic period as lacking the legal competence of a fully cognizant adult, though recognized as occupying a considerably higher level than a {\i shoteh}, the Rabbis nonetheless instituted a form of rabbinic marriage for a {\i cheresh}. That rabbinic institution is sufficient to remove the woman from her father\u8217's household in the relevant halachic sense. Therefore, if she is a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i Yisrael cheresh}, the rabbinic marriage invalidates her from eating {\i terumah}, {\i d\u8217'hakanya b\u8217'takanata d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- through rabbinic enactment she has been acquired. If she is a {\i bat Yisrael} married to a {\i kohen cheresh}, she cannot eat, because {\i kinyan kaspo amar Rachmana v\u8217'hai lav bar kinyanei} \u8212- the Torah requires a genuine biblical {\i kiddushin}, and the {\i cheresh} is not capable of effecting one biblically. The rabbinic institution of marriage, powerful enough to invalidate, is not powerful enough to entitle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine-Year-Old\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the nine-year-old is the most complex in this section. Initially, it might seem to refer to a {\i yibum} scenario: a woman waiting for {\i yibum} from a nine-year-old brother-in-law who consummates the relationship. The Gemara, however, works through the logic and finds this reading inadequate. If the concern is that the {\i yavam} prevents her return to her father\u8217's house \u8212- the {\i poslim} side \u8212- even a one-day-old infant would create that bond, since she is a {\i shomeres yavam} regardless of the {\i yavam}\u8217's age. And if the concern is whether he entitles her to eat \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'akhilim} side \u8212- then even an adult {\i yavam} does not entitle her to eat until consummation. So a nine-year-old {\i yavam} who has actually had relations would be an interesting case: Abaye initially suggests that the novelty is that although the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} is biblically valid as an act of {\i yibum} \u8212- {\i de-mid\u8217'oraita kani lei} \u8212- the Rabbis treated the sexual act of a nine-year-old as equivalent only to {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the rabbinic act of betrothal, rather than full consummation. She might think she is entitled to eat since biblically the {\i yibum} is complete; the teaching comes to say she is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejects this reading, however, and points out that the subsequent clause of the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- {\i safek ben tesha shanim, safek eino} \u8212- becomes unintelligible under Abaye\u8217's interpretation. If a definite nine-year-old who has had relations does not entitle her to eat, it would be obvious that an uncertain nine-year-old also does not. But the {\i Mishnah} appears to teach it as a separate point, implying that in the case of certainty the answer would be different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore reframes the entire case. The nine-year-old here does not belong to the gray-zone section at all. Rather, it belongs to the following category: people whose sexual act {\i invalidates} the woman completely. The {\i Mishnah} is transitioning to a new list \u8212- people whose forbidden sexual contact renders the woman a {\i zonah} or {\i chalalah} and disqualifies her from marrying a {\i kohen} or eating {\i terumah}. Even a nine-year-old from such a category has this effect. And if it is uncertain whether he is nine years old, the doubt itself is sufficient to invalidate \u8212- which now makes perfect sense, because even certainty that he is younger would not help her if it is a {\i safek} whether the disqualifying act occurred.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalidation Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rava\u8217's reframing established, the Gemara turns directly to the new topic: the category of sexual acts that completely invalidate a woman, removing her from the rights of {\i kehunah} \u8212- either the right to eat {\i terumah} if she is a {\i bat kohen}, or the right to marry a {\i kohen} if she is a {\i bat Yisrael} or {\i Leviyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Partners\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: a nine-year-old and a day who is a {\i ger}, a convert of Ammonite or Moabite origin \u8212- forbidden by the verse {\i lo yavo Ammoni bikhal Hashem} \u8212- or an Egyptian, an Edomite (before the third generation of conversion), a {\i Kuti} (Samaritan, treated as not fully Jewish in this context), a {\i nasin} (of Gibeonite descent), a {\i chalal} (a {\i kohen} who has lost his sanctity through a disqualifying union), or a {\i mamzer} \u8212- any of these who engages in sexual relations with a {\i kohenet}, a {\i Leviyah}, or a {\i Yisraelit}, {\i pasluha}: they invalidate her. If she is a {\i bat kohen}, she is disqualified from eating {\i terumah}. If she is a {\i Yisraelit} or {\i Leviyah}, she is disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the structural diversity of this list. Some of these figures are forbidden to marry {\i any} Jew \u8212- a {\i mamzer} cannot marry into the community of Israel, nor can an Ammonite or Moabite convert. Others are specifically forbidden to {\i kohanim} \u8212- a {\i chalal}, for example, does not create a problem for marriage to a {\i Yisrael} but does affect the woman\u8217's {\i kehunah} status. The Gemara will distinguish between these two sub-categories, identifying the first group as {\i psulei kahal} \u8212- those barred from the general community \u8212- and the second group as {\i psulei kehunah} \u8212- those specifically barred in the context of priestly marriage. The {\i chalal} in particular occupies an unusual position: his act of sex is not itself a forbidden one in the ordinary sense, but it renders the woman a {\i chalalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yossi View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose qualifies the general rule: {\i kol she-zaro pasul, posel} \u8212- only one whose offspring would be disqualified invalidates the woman. {\i Kol she-ein zaro pasul, eino posel} \u8212- one whose offspring would not be disqualified does not invalidate her. The implication is that there are cases of forbidden sexual contact where the resulting child would not be halachically problematic. One such scenario might involve a man who is at the boundary of permissibility \u8212- for example, the third generation of Egyptian or Edomite converts, who are just crossing the threshold into full eligibility. In such edge cases, Rabbi Yose would hold that the act does not invalidate the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel articulates his position as a memorable rule: {\i kol she-ata nosei bito, ata nosei almanato} \u8212- whoever\u8217's daughter you may marry, you may marry his widow (in the sense of the woman with whom he had relations). {\i V\u8217'kol she-ei ata nosei bito, ei ata nosei almanato} \u8212- whoever\u8217's daughter you may not marry, you may not marry the woman he has rendered a widow. The import is that the eligibility of the man\u8217's daughter to marry a {\i kohen} determines whether his sexual act invalidates the woman. At first glance this seems very close to Rabbi Yose\u8217's position \u8212- in both cases the determining factor is the status of offspring \u8212- and the Gemara will address the relationship between these views.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Source & Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having laid out the rules, the Gemara asks: {\i mena hani mili}? Where do we derive the foundational principle that forbidden sexual intercourse invalidates a woman \u8212- specifically, that a {\i bat kohen} who has such relations loses her right to eat {\i terumah}? Rav Yehudah in the name of Rav cites the verse: {\i u-vat kohen ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar} \u8212- \u8220"and a daughter of a {\i kohen} when she belongs to a foreign man.\u8221" In its simple reading, this refers to a {\i bat kohen} who marries a {\i Yisrael} and can no longer eat {\i terumah} during the marriage. But the Gemara derives from it a more far-reaching principle: {\i keivan she-niv\u8217'alah l\u8217'pasul lah} \u8212- once she has had relations with a {\i zar} in the sense of someone wholly forbidden to her, she is permanently invalidated from eating {\i terumah}, not merely during the marriage. The word {\i zar} is read not simply as \u8220"non-{\i kohen}\u8221" but as \u8220"someone completely foreign to her\u8221" \u8212- that is, halachically prohibited to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then carefully accounts for every component of the verse\u8217's derivation. One might object that this verse is needed to teach the simple {\i pshat} \u8212- that a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i Yisrael} cannot eat {\i terumah}. But that, the Gemara replies, can be derived from the continuation of the passage: {\i v\u8217'shava el beit aviha mi-lechem aviha tochel} \u8212- \u8220"she shall return to her father\u8217's house and eat of her father\u8217's bread\u8221" \u8212- implying that while married she does not eat. So the {\i ki tihyeh} clause is available for the new {\i derasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then object that if we derive the prohibition of eating during marriage inferentially, it carries only the weight of a {\i mitzvat asei} \u8212- a positive commandment\u8217's implicit negative. We need the Torah to state a direct {\i lav}. But that too is addressed: the prohibition of a {\i Yisrael} eating {\i terumah} \u8212- and by extension, a woman connected to a {\i Yisrael} \u8212- is derived from {\i v\u8217'chol zar lo yochal kodesh}, \u8220"no stranger shall eat of sacred things,\u8221" which appears twice in the relevant passages. One instance teaches that a non-{\i kohen} cannot eat {\i terumah}; the other teaches that a woman married to a non-{\i kohen} cannot eat. The word {\i v\u8217'chol} in the second instance further teaches, following Rabbi Yose the son of Rabbi Chanina, that it is specifically the status of {\i zarus} \u8212- foreignness \u8212- that disqualifies, and not {\i aninus} (the state of mourning before burial). An {\i onen} is forbidden to eat {\i korbanos} but not forbidden to eat {\i terumah}. All of this is accounted for, leaving {\i ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar} free to teach the invalidating effect of forbidden sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara also notes another teaching derived from this passage. A {\i baraita} teaches that when a woman reverts to her father\u8217's household after her husband\u8217's death, {\i chozeret l\u8217'terumah v\u8217'einah chozeret la-chazeh v\u8217'shok} \u8212- she returns to eating {\i terumah} but does not return to eating the {\i chazeh v\u8217'shok}, the breast and thigh portions of certain offerings. The phrase {\i bi-trumat ha-kodashim lo tochal} is read as {\i bi-murum min ha-kodashim lo tochal} \u8212- she no longer partakes of what is lifted up from the {\i korbanos}. Once she has been married to a non-{\i kohen}, that permanent exclusion from {\i kodashim kalim} remains, even after she reverts and resumes {\i terumah}. The phrasing {\i bi-trumat ha-kodashim} rather than simply {\i ba-kodashim} signals that both {\i terumah} and {\i kodashim} are being addressed in a single formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extra Vav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the invalidation of a {\i bat kohen} from {\i terumah}, the Gemara asks how this extends to all women \u8212- {\i Leviyah} and {\i Yisraelit} as well. The answer, following Rabbi Abba in the name of Rav, is derived from the prefix \u1493 ? in \u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1514 ? \u1499 ?\u1492 ?\u1503 ?: {\i bat u-vat} \u8212- the extra \u1493 ? extends the ruling beyond the {\i bat kohen} to include all women. Since the law of invalidation now applies to a {\i Leviyah} or {\i Yisraelit}, the relevant consequence cannot be the loss of {\i terumah} eating rights \u8212- those women never ate {\i terumah} to begin with. The implication must therefore be the prohibition of marriage to a {\i kohen}. The Gemara notes that this derivation follows even the {\i Rabbanan} who generally do not learn from extra letter forms, since here the entire phrase {\i u-vat kohen} is superfluous \u8212- the verse could have begun simply with {\i ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar}. The full prefix phrase is thus available as an extension to all women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point in the derivation, the Gemara has established from the verse that forbidden sexual relations invalidate a woman \u8212- specifically that a {\i bat kohen} is disqualified from eating {\i terumah}. This much follows from the direct reading of the verse about a {\i bat kohen} and {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Ban Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains to be established is that the same act disqualifies all women from marriage to a {\i kohen}. As noted above, this is the implication of extending the ruling to a {\i Leviyah} or {\i Yisraelit} through the extra \u1493 ?. But the derivation merits closer attention: once we say {\i bat u-vat} includes all women, and for those women the only relevant consequence is marriage prohibition, the {\i terumah} dimension and the marriage dimension are brought together under a single biblical source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal VeChomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Could one not derive the marriage prohibition through a {\i kal va-chomer}? If forbidden sex invalidates a {\i bat kohen} \u8212- who has her own intrinsic sanctity, {\i kedushat ha-guf} \u8212- surely it should all the more so disqualify a {\i bat Yisrael} from marriage to a {\i kohen}. The Gemara rejects this argument with a sharp reversal: {\i hee ha-notenet} \u8212- it is precisely the opposite. A {\i bat kohen} eats {\i terumah} by virtue of her own personal sanctity, {\i kedushat gufah}, and forbidden sex impinges directly on that sanctity. But a {\i bat Yisrael} who eats {\i terumah} does so not by virtue of her own status but by virtue of her child \u8212- {\i bishvil benah}. If her eating is not based on her own sacred status but on an external connection, perhaps forbidden sex, which affects personal status, is simply irrelevant to her ability to eat. {\i Lo kadish gufah} \u8212- her body carries no inherent {\i kedushah} that the forbidden act can desecrate. The {\i kal va-chomer} thus fails: far from being more stringent for a {\i bat Yisrael}, the logic of personal sanctity would suggest she should be {\i less} affected. A verse is therefore necessary, and the verse provides it through the extension to all women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} thus concludes this daf with the question of invalidation from {\i terumah} fully resolved, and sets the stage for the following discussion: the derivation of the prohibition on marrying a {\i kohen} that applies to all women who have had forbidden sexual relations \u8212- which will be taken up on the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 67b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Uber & Slave Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins with a {\i braita} that elaborates upon the position of Rabbi Yose set out in the {\i mishna}, spelling out the various scenarios in which a pregnant woman married to a {\i kohen} has died and the question turns on which household members may eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu rabbanan}: if the {\i kohen} left behind born sons and his wife was not pregnant, then both she and all the slaves may eat \u8212- the born child entitles the wife to remain connected to the {\i kohen}\u8217's household, and all the slaves are fully owned by the heirs. If he left the wife pregnant but with no born children, then neither she nor the slaves may eat: she cannot eat on the basis of an unborn fetus, and the slaves cannot eat because they are owned, wholly or in part, by the fetus, who cannot confer upon them the right to eat {\i terumah}. If he left both born children and a pregnant wife, then her {\i nichsei melog} slaves \u8212- those she brought as her personal dowry property \u8212- may eat, because she herself eats by virtue of the born children, and {\i kederech she-hi ochelet} her slaves follow her status. The {\i nichsei tzon barzel} slaves, however \u8212- those she brought as the husband\u8217's property and which remain part of his estate \u8212- do not eat, {\i mipnei chelko shel uber}, because the fetus holds a partial share in what the born sons have inherited, and that share creates the problem. This is summarized as: the fetus {\i posel ve-eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- he invalidates but cannot feed. This is the ruling of Rabbi Yose, and the {\i braita} is simply an expansion of the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yose then states in his father\u8217's name: {\i ha-bat ma\u8217'achelet, ha-ben eino ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- the daughter feeds, the son does not feed. The {\i gemara} notes that this statement is initially opaque and proceeds to unpack it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai adds: {\i zecharim yochlu kulam, nekevot lo yochlu} \u8212- if there are only male children and the wife is pregnant, all the slaves may eat; if there are only daughters and the wife is pregnant, the slaves may not eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male Minority Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses on Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai\u8217's ruling: if there are born boys and a pregnant wife, why are the slaves permitted to eat? The fetus might itself be a boy and thereby hold a share in the estate, which should prevent the slaves from eating. The answer given is that Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai holds {\i ein choshishin le-mi\u8217'uta} \u8212- one need not be concerned about a minority possibility. The chain of logic is this: there is a fifty percent chance the fetus will be female, in which case it holds no share in the estate (daughters do not inherit when sons are present). Even if the fetus is male, not every pregnancy results in a live birth. The combined probability that a live baby boy will be born from this pregnancy falls below fifty percent \u8212- a {\i mi\u8217'ut} \u8212- and one need not worry about a {\i mi\u8217'ut} in the majority of cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why, when there are only daughters and the wife is pregnant, the slaves cannot eat. The answer, {\i chada ve-od}, is twofold: first, if the fetus is female, she too will share in the estate with the other daughters; second, even if the fetus is female and girls generally share in the estate only by rabbinic enactment, there remains the possibility the fetus is male and takes the entire estate \u8212- {\i ve-ein la-banot bi-makom ha-ben klum}, the daughters have no share in the presence of a son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a second possible reading of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai\u8217's ruling. Perhaps he does hold {\i choshishin le-mi\u8217'uta} \u8212- he is indeed concerned about a forty-eight percent probability \u8212- but holds that there is a practical solution available via the ruling of Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel. Rav Nachman amar Shmuel teaches that when orphans come to divide their father\u8217's estate and some are minors, {\i beit din} appoints an {\i apotropus} \u8212- a guardian or steward \u8212- to choose a good portion on behalf of the minor heir. The {\i apotropus} can then carve out specific property for the fetus: instead of the fetus holding an undivided fractional interest in every slave, he holds full ownership of two specific slaves (for example), and the remaining eight slaves are fully owned by the born brothers and may eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further nuance of Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling concerns what happens when the minors come of age: in the name of Shmuel, the ruling states that they may reverse the division if it did not serve their interests, since the {\i apotropus} only acted on their behalf. Rav Nachman himself, however, rules that once they reach adulthood they {\i cannot} reverse it \u8212- {\i mah koach beit din yafeh?} For otherwise, what power does {\i beit din} have?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then steps back and rejects this entire approach, concluding {\i lo} \u8212- everyone can hold by Rav Nachman\u8217's principle, the {\i apotropus} idea is not the point at all, and the debate among the {\i tanna\u8217'im} is simply whether one is concerned about the {\i mi\u8217'ut} that a live boy will be born.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Small Estate Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the cryptic ruling of Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yose: {\i ha-bat ma\u8217'achelet, ha-ben eino ma\u8217'achil}. Abaye explains that the entire case concerns {\i nichsei mu\u8217'atim} \u8212- a small estate. When the estate is small, a well-known {\i halacha} applies: the daughters\u8217' right to be supported from the estate until marriage takes full precedence over the sons\u8217' inheritance. Even if meeting the daughters\u8217' maintenance costs consumes the entire estate and leaves the sons nothing, the daughters\u8217' rights come first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case, therefore, involves the presence of both a born son and a born daughter. {\i Mima nafshach} \u8212- either way: if the pregnant woman\u8217's fetus is a boy, he is no better positioned than the born boy who is already present. In a small estate, the daughter already owns the whole of the estate for purposes of support, so the fetus acquires nothing from it even if male. If the fetus is a girl, why do girls eat in this case at all? Because it is by virtue of a {\i takana d\u8217'rabbanan}, a rabbinic enactment \u8212- and the rabbis enacted that right only for a born girl, not for an unborn fetus. So when there is a daughter present, the slaves may eat \u8212- the daughter holds the estate by rabbinic enactment, and the fetus, whether male or female, does not disturb her hold on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then tests this reading: the end of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai\u8217's statement spoke of boys getting everything if the fetus is male, which presupposes a large estate where sons inherit everything and daughters are left with nothing. The {\i gemara} concedes that Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai\u8217's statement applies to a large estate, while Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's teaching concerns the special small-estate case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a further challenge arises: if the estate is a {\i nichsei mu\u8217'atim}, can one truly say it belongs to the daughters? Rabbi Assi amar Rabbi Yochanan teaches that if orphans in a small estate go ahead and sell property, the sale is legally binding \u8212- which demonstrates that ultimate {\i title} remains with the sons, even if the daughters have a right to be fed. The daughters hold a right akin to a debt against the estate, not outright ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore abandons the reading that {\i bat} and {\i ben} refer to literal daughter and son of the deceased, and proposes instead that the text uses {\i bat} to mean the mother \u8212- the wife \u8212- and {\i ben} to mean the father \u8212- the deceased husband. {\i Ha-bat ma\u8217'achelet} means: the mother\u8217's own property ({\i nichsei melog}) feeds her slaves; {\i ha-ben eino ma\u8217'achil} means: the father\u8217's property ({\i nichsei tzon barzel}) does not feed those slaves, because of the fetus\u8217's partial share. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i hainu Rabbi Yose} \u8212- this is simply what Rabbi Yose already taught, and Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yose is merely restating his father\u8217's ruling with different terminology, which is admittedly an unusual reading of the {\i braita}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gray Zone Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gray Zone Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion shifts to a broader framework: a list of cases in which a woman finds herself in a legal gray zone with respect to {\i terumah} consumption. The governing principle is as follows. When a {\i bat kohen} marries a {\i yisrael} and he dies, the presence or absence of children determines whether she remains connected to the {\i yisrael}\u8217's household or reverts to her father\u8217's. If she has children, she remains connected to her dead husband\u8217's household and may not return to eating {\i terumah}. If she has no children, she reverts to her father\u8217's house \u8212- conceptually, not physically \u8212- and resumes eating {\i terumah}. The mirror image applies to a {\i bat yisrael} married to a {\i kohen}: with children she remains in his household and eats {\i terumah}; without children she reverts to her father\u8217's household and does not eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i uber} \u8212- fetus \u8212- creates precisely the gray zone the {\i gemara} now catalogs. The fetus is enough of a connection to the dead husband that it prevents the woman from being considered fully reverted to her father\u8217's house, thereby {\i poseling} a {\i bat kohen} from eating {\i terumah}. But the fetus is not enough of a connection to the husband\u8217's house to entitle a {\i bat yisrael} married to a {\i kohen} to eat {\i terumah} in the first place. The fetus thus occupies both the {\i posel} function and the non-{\i ma\u8217'achil} function simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents a list of relationships that share this dual gray-zone character: the {\i uber}, the {\i yavam}, {\i erusin}, a {\i cheresh}, and a {\i ben tesha shanim ve-yom echad}. In all these cases: {\i poslin ve-lo ma\u8217'achilin} \u8212- they invalidate but they do not feed. And even a {\i safek} of such a relationship, whether it is uncertain if the man is nine years old and a day, or uncertain whether he has reached halachic maturity through two pubic hairs, is sufficient to invalidate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} also mentions, as an aside, a {\i nafal ha-bayit} case: a house collapsed upon a man and his brother\u8217's daughter (his wife), with no known order of death, and he had a co-wife. Since the {\i tzarah}\u8217's obligation of {\i yibbum} depends on the order of death \u8212- if the niece-wife died first, the co-wife requires {\i yibbum}; if the husband died first, the co-wife is exempt because a niece cannot create the {\i yibbum} bond \u8212- the {\i halacha} is that the {\i tzarah} performs {\i chalitzah} and not {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fetus Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} unpacks the {\i uber} case. If the woman is a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i yisrael}, the fetus {\i paslah} \u8212- invalidates her from returning to {\i terumah}. The derivation: the verse speaks of her returning to her father\u8217's house {\i ke-ne\u8217'ureha} \u8212- as in her youth \u8212- implying a full reversion; a pregnant woman has not fully reverted. If she is a {\i bat yisrael} married to a {\i kohen}, the fetus does not entitle her to eat, because {\i yalud ma\u8217'achil she-ein yalud ma\u8217'achil} \u8212- only a born child confers that right; an unborn fetus does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i yavam}, the {\i gemara} reasons as follows. If she is a {\i bat kohen} and the {\i yavam} is a {\i yisrael}, he {\i paslah}, because the verse says {\i ve-shavah el beit aviha} \u8212- she can return to her father\u8217's house \u8212- which implicitly excludes a {\i shomeret yavam}, who remains bound by the {\i zikah} bond and cannot return until {\i chalitzah} is performed. If she is a {\i bat yisrael} and the {\i yavam} is a {\i kohen}, she does not eat, because {\i kinyan kaspo amar rachamana} \u8212- the Torah requires that she be his acquisition through money (the paradigm of {\i kiddushin}), and this woman was acquired by his {\i brother}, not by him. Once {\i yibbum} is consummated the relationship is complete, but at the stage of {\i shomeret yavam} it is not sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erusin Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i erusin}, the case involves actual {\i kiddushin} without {\i nisuin}. If she is a {\i bat kohen} and the {\i arus} is a {\i yisrael}, he invalidates her: once there is some {\i kinyan} to another man, the verse {\i u-vat kohen ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar} applies and she is no longer considered fully within her father\u8217's house \u8212- or, more precisely, even if she remains physically in her father\u8217's house, her connection to another man is sufficient to sever her {\i terumah} entitlement based on her father. If she is a {\i bat yisrael} and the {\i arus} is a {\i kohen}, she does not eat \u8212- and here the {\i gemara} invokes the ruling of Ulla. Biblically, it would seem that {\i kiddushin} should suffice to allow her to eat {\i terumah}, since the verse speaks of {\i kinyan kaspo} \u8212- his monetary acquisition, encompassing both slaves and wife acquired through {\i kiddushin}. However, Ulla explains that the rabbis were concerned lest she share the {\i terumah} with her father and brothers while still residing in her father\u8217's house during the period between {\i erusin} and {\i nisuin}. Accordingly, the rabbis enacted that she does not eat until {\i nisuin}. The {\i gemara} notes that the Yerushalmi actually takes a stronger position: {\i terumah} entitlement is derived not from {\i kinyan kaspo} but from {\i beito hem yochlu belachmo} \u8212- the idea of his {\i bayit}, his household \u8212- which is only established at {\i nisuin}, making this a biblical, not merely rabbinic, exclusion during {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i cheresh} \u8212- a deaf-mute \u8212- the {\i gemara} notes that while a {\i cheresh} is not considered legally competent in the biblical sense and thus cannot effect a biblical {\i kiddushin}, the rabbis nonetheless instituted a form of rabbinic marriage for a {\i cheresh}, recognizing that he functions at a higher level than, for example, a {\i shoteh}. If she is a {\i bat kohen} and the {\i cheresh} is a {\i yisrael}, this rabbinic institution is sufficient to remove her from her father\u8217's house and {\i posel} her. If she is a {\i bat yisrael} and the {\i cheresh} is a {\i kohen}, she does not eat, because {\i kinyan kaspo amar rachamana}, the Torah requires a real biblical acquisition, and a {\i cheresh lav bar kinyan hu} \u8212- a deaf-mute is not capable of effecting a biblical acquisition. So the rabbinic marriage invalidates but does not feed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine Years Old Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ben tesha shanim ve-yom echad} case requires further clarification. One might initially think this refers to a {\i yavam} who is nine years old and a day performing {\i bi\u8217'ah} for {\i yibbum} purposes. The {\i gemara} considers whether the intent is to show that the {\i yavam}\u8217's minority status alone \u8212- even without any act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- invalidates. But the {\i gemara} notes that even an infant {\i yavam} invalidates her as a {\i shomeret yavam}; and conversely, even an adult {\i yavam} does not {\i feed} her until {\i yibbum} is consummated. Abaye therefore proposes that the case involves a nine-year-old {\i yavam} who has actually had {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i yevamah}. Since biblically this act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} is a valid act of {\i yibbum}, one might think she should be entitled to eat {\i terumah} if he is a {\i kohen}. The {\i chiddush} is that the rabbis treated the {\i bi\u8217'ah} of a nine-year-old not as a complete {\i yibbum} but as equivalent to {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- a stronger but still incomplete form of the {\i yibbum} bond \u8212- and thus she remains in the gray zone and does not eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects to Abaye\u8217's reading: the {\i mishna} states that even a {\i safek} of nine years old invalidates. If a definite nine-year-old having consummated does not {\i feed} her, of course a doubtful nine-year-old does not feed her \u8212- there is no {\i chiddush} in the {\i safek} formulation. Rava therefore reinterprets: the {\i ben tesha shanim ve-yom echad} in this {\i mishna} is not about {\i yibbum} at all. It belongs to a different category \u8212- the category of forbidden sexual relations. Starting at age nine years and a day, an act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} with one of the persons listed in the following {\i braita} is considered a legally significant act of sex that invalidates the woman. That is the {\i chiddush}, and the {\i safek} formulation then makes perfect sense: even if it is uncertain whether he was nine years old and a day, the woman is still invalidated by the act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i braita} it has been previewing: a nine-year-old boy and a day who is a {\i ger Amoni o Mo\u8217'avi}, an Ammonite or Moabite convert, or a {\i Mitzri}, {\i Edomi} (within the first three generations), {\i Kuti}, {\i natin}, {\i chalal}, or {\i mamzer}, who has relations with a {\i kohenet}, a {\i Leviyah}, or a {\i Yisraelit} \u8212- all are {\i pasluha}, they invalidate her. If she is a {\i bat kohen}, she is prevented from eating {\i terumah}. If she is a {\i Yisraelit}, she is prevented from marrying a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes a structural distinction in the {\i mishna}: the first section of the {\i mishna} addressed the {\i pesulei kahal} \u8212- those forbidden from entering the congregation of Israel altogether, not merely from {\i kehuna} \u8212- including Ammonites, Moabites, {\i mamzerim}, and the like. The subsequent {\i mishna} will address {\i pesulei kehuna} \u8212- those specifically forbidden to {\i kohanim}, such as a divorc\u233 ?e or widow married to a {\i kohen}. Though different in kind, both categories of forbidden relations invalidate the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose states: {\i kol she-zar\u8217'o pasul posel} \u8212- anyone whose offspring from this woman would be {\i pasul} (invalid) invalidates the woman through this act of sex. {\i Ve-kol she-ein zar\u8217'o pasul eino posel} \u8212- anyone whose offspring would not be {\i pasul} does not invalidate her. This formulation raises the question of what kind of relationship might be forbidden yet produce valid offspring \u8212- for instance, a man who is forbidden to marry into the congregation only for a limited number of generations, where the offspring of the union would fall on the permitted side of that generational threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel offers a parallel formulation: {\i kol she-ata nosei bito ata nosei almanato} \u8212- anyone whose daughter you (the {\i kohen}) could marry, you could also marry his widow (the woman he had relations with). {\i Ve-kol she-ei ata nosei bito ei ata nosei almanato} \u8212- anyone whose daughter you could not marry, you cannot marry his widow either. This sounds closely parallel to Rabbi Yose\u8217's position, and the {\i gemara} will explore the relationship between the two formulations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parashat Emor Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mena hanei milei} \u8212- what is the biblical source for the principle that forbidden sexual relations invalidate a woman from eating {\i terumah} or from marrying a {\i kohen}? Rav Yehuda amar Rav derives it from the verse {\i u-vat kohen ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar} (Parashat Emor). While the {\i peshat} of the verse is that a {\i bat kohen} married to a non-{\i kohen} ({\i zar}) may not eat {\i terumah} of her father\u8217's house, Rav reads it as follows: {\i zar} means someone totally foreign, completely forbidden to her. Once she has been {\i niv\u8217'ala} \u8212- had relations \u8212- with such a man, {\i paslah} \u8212- she is permanently invalidated from eating {\i terumah}, not merely suspended during the marriage. This reading converts a temporary status rule into a permanent invalidation based on an act of forbidden sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges: we need this verse simply for its {\i peshat} \u8212- to teach that a {\i bat kohen} married to a non-{\i kohen} cannot eat {\i terumah} while married. The {\i gemara} replies that we could derive that rule from elsewhere: from the verse {\i ve-shavah el beit aviha} \u8212- when she returns to her father\u8217's house she may eat \u8212- we can infer by implication that while she is married she cannot eat. The {\i gemara} pushes back: that inference produces only a {\i lav ha-ba mi-klal aseh} \u8212- a negative prohibition inferred from a positive commandment, which carries only the weight of a positive {\i mitzvah}. We need the verse {\i ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar} to establish this as a full, black-letter negative prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} counters that the negative prohibition can be derived from the verse {\i ve-chol zar lo yochal kodesh} \u8212- no non-{\i kohen} may eat {\i terumah}. By extension, a woman connected to a non-{\i kohen} may not eat. Then the {\i gemara} notes that {\i ve-chol zar lo yochal kodesh} appears twice in the Torah. One instance teaches that a non-{\i kohen} cannot eat {\i terumah}; the other, appearing in the section of a {\i bat kohen}, teaches that a woman married to a non-{\i kohen} cannot eat. And the word {\i ve-chol} in that context comes specifically to teach, as Rabbi Yose birabbi Chanina states: {\i zarut amarti lach ve-lo aninut} \u8212- only the disqualification of being a {\i zar} prevents eating {\i terumah}; being an {\i onen} (mourner on the day of burial) does not. An {\i onen} is forbidden to eat {\i korbanot} but not {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then layers in yet another {\i halacha} that requires a verse: {\i Rami bar Avin amar Rav Ashi} teaches from the phrase {\i u-vat kohen ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar hi bi-trumat ha-kodashim lo tochel} \u8212- the specific phrase {\i trumat ha-kodashim} teaches that she cannot eat {\i be-muram min ha-kodashim}, the portions lifted from {\i korbanot} (i.e., the {\i chazeh ve-shok}, the breast and thigh of peace-offerings given to {\i kohanim}). Once a {\i bat kohen} has been married to a non-{\i kohen}, even after that husband\u8217's death and her return to her father\u8217's house, she can resume eating {\i terumah} but she does not resume eating the portions of {\i kodashim}. The {\i gemara} then asks: if the verse only wanted to prohibit {\i kodashim}, it should have simply said {\i lo tochel ba-kodashim} \u8212- why does it also mention {\i terumah}? The answer is {\i shma mina tarti} \u8212- the verse is doing double duty, teaching both rules. The full accounting of available verses thus yields: one {\i ve-chol zar} for the prohibition on a non-{\i kohen} eating {\i terumah}, one {\i ve-chol zar} (with {\i ve-chol}) for the prohibition on a {\i bat kohen} married to a non-{\i kohen}, the {\i ve-chol} particle specifically excluding {\i aninut}, and the phrase {\i ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar} for the permanent invalidation through forbidden sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Kohen Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analysis has established that forbidden sexual relations invalidate a {\i bat kohen} from eating {\i terumah}. But how do we know the rule extends to a {\i Leviyah} and a {\i Yisraelit} \u8212- for them, the relevant consequence is not {\i terumah} (which they do not eat in their own right) but prohibition from marrying a {\i kohen}? The {\i gemara} answers by invoking the ruling of Rabbi Abba in the name of Rav: {\i bat u-vat} \u8212- the extra {\i vav} in {\i u-vat kohen} is textually superfluous, since the verse could simply have stated {\i ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar} without specifying {\i bat kohen} at all. The additional {\i u-vat kohen} comes to include all women \u8212- {\i Leviyot} and {\i Yisraeliyot} as well. And once the rule applies to a {\i Leviyah} and {\i Yisraelit}, who have no connection to {\i terumah} in the first place, the only operative consequence must be the prohibition from marrying a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether this derivation follows only the view of Rabbi Akiva, who characteristically derives law from extra {\i vavim}. It answers that even the {\i rabbanan} concede the point here, because the entire phrase {\i u-vat kohen} is textually excess \u8212- not just a single letter. The verse could have been written simply as {\i ki tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar}; the addition of {\i u-vat kohen} is therefore available to teach the extension to all women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leviyah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now that the rule has been extended to all women, the {\i gemara} asks: we have established the rule for {\i terumah} \u8212- but how do we know that forbidden sex prohibits a {\i Leviyah} or {\i Yisraelit} from marrying a {\i kohen}? At first glance, once we extend the verse to a {\i Leviyah} and {\i Yisraelit}, who do not eat {\i terumah}, the only possible implication is marriage \u8212- so it is self-evident. But the {\i gemara} entertains a counterargument: one could construct a {\i terumah} case for a {\i Yisraelit}. If she had previously been married to a {\i kohen} and bore him a son, she would be eating {\i terumah} by virtue of that son. Now she has forbidden sex with one of the listed men \u8212- she is invalidated and may no longer eat. Perhaps, then, even the extension to {\i Yisraelit} is still only about {\i terumah}, not about marriage to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} dismisses this with a {\i kal va-chomer}: if a {\i bat kohen}, whose personal status entitles her to eat {\i terumah}, is invalidated by forbidden sex \u8212- then a {\i Yisraelit}, who eats only on account of her son\u8217's status and not her own, would surely be invalidated as well. The {\i gemara} pushes back: {\i mah la-kohenet}, one can distinguish \u8212- a {\i bat kohen} has inherent {\i kedusha}, and her personal status is what is impaired by the forbidden act. A {\i Yisraelit} has no such inherent {\i kedusha}; her eating {\i terumah} is entirely derivative of her child, and perhaps her own status is irrelevant to the act. The {\i kal va-chomer} therefore fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} leaves us at the threshold: we have now established, through the extension via {\i u-vat}, that forbidden sexual relations invalidate all women \u8212- {\i bat kohen}, {\i Leviyah}, and {\i Yisraelit} alike \u8212- and that for {\i Leviyot} and {\i Yisraeliyot} the operative consequence must be prohibition from marrying a {\i kohen}. The further analysis of exactly how that prohibition is derived for purposes of marriage, as distinct from {\i terumah}, will be taken up in the following session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 72\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Northern Wind & Chatzot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wind Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} of {\i he\u8217'arel} \u8212- the laws governing what an {\i arel} may and may not eat, and particularly the prohibition against eating {\i terumah} derived from the {\i Korban Pesach} \u8212- continues on 72a. The discussion has been anchored in the narrative from Yehoshua establishing that {\i Bnei Yisrael} were {\i areilim} throughout their years in the {\i midbar}, and the {\i Gemara} has attributed this to the danger of performing {\i brit milah} when the northern wind did not blow. Before returning to the {\i halacha}, the {\i Gemara} lingers on an {\i agadata} that creates an apparent tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tanu rabanan} teaches: {\i kol osan arba\u8217'im shana shehayu Yisrael bamidbar, lo haya yom shelo nashva bo ruach tzephonit} \u8212- throughout all forty years that Israel was in the wilderness, there was no day on which the northern wind did not blow. This seems to directly contradict the earlier teaching that the north wind was absent during those years. The resolution is that the wind did blow, but only {\i bachatzot halayla} \u8212- precisely at midnight. It did not blow during the day with sufficient force to make {\i brit milah} safe, yet it blew at midnight nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatzot Eis Ratzon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} derives this from the verse {\i vayehi bachatzot halayla v\u8217'Hashem hika kol bechor b\u8217'eretz Mitzrayim} \u8212- \u8220"And it was at midnight, and God smote every firstborn in the land of Egypt.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai talmuda} \u8212- what is the lesson drawn from this verse? {\i De\u8217'eis ratzon milta hi} \u8212- we learn that {\i chatzot} is a time God regards with special favor, an {\i eis ratzon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking observation. One might have expected that the midnight plague of the firstborn \u8212- a catastrophic act of divine judgment \u8212- would mark {\i chatzot} as an {\i eis din}, a time of judgment. Yet it is interpreted as an {\i eis ratzon}, a time of divine favor. The logic is that even that fearsome event was ultimately an expression of redemption for Israel. And so even when the north wind could not blow during the day, the corresponding {\i eis ratzon} on high found its earthly expression in the wind blowing at midnight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tikkun Chatzot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects directly to the practice of {\i Tikkun Chatzot}. The understanding that night is generally an {\i eis din} while {\i chatzot} itself is an {\i eis ratzon} provides its theological foundation. Rashi references the {\i Gemara} in Berachot, where the northern wind blowing at midnight caused David Hamelech\u8217's harp to play, awakening him at {\i chatzot} for his {\i slichot} \u8212- which is precisely the wind described in this passage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of how people before the modern era managed to rise at midnight without alarm clocks is itself illuminating. One answer may simply be that without artificial lighting, {\i chatzot} was understood more loosely as the deep of night. A more compelling answer, however, emerges from recent studies on natural human sleep patterns. When subjects are removed from artificial light and modern time structures, they tend to fall asleep at nightfall, sleep approximately four hours, then experience a natural period of wakefulness lasting roughly two hours, before returning to sleep. The suggestion is that {\i Tikkun Chatzot} was organically aligned with this natural awakening \u8212- a period of wakefulness in the middle of the night that was, in pre-modern life, entirely normal and expected.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mashoch Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to {\i halacha}. Rav Huna states: {\i devar Torah, mashoch ochel biterumah} \u8212- biblically speaking, a {\i mashoch} \u8212- someone who underwent {\i brit milah} but subsequently reversed it through a procedure that stretches the remaining skin to recreate the appearance of a foreskin \u8212- is permitted to eat {\i terumah}. The foreskin was removed; whatever it looks like, he has had a {\i brit milah}. {\i Midivreihem gazru alav mipnei shenireh ke\u8217'arel} \u8212- the Rabbis, however, enacted a decree forbidding him, because he appears to be an {\i arel}. The prohibition is therefore {\i derabbanan}, not {\i deoraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A challenge is immediately raised: {\i meisiv} \u8212- a {\i braita} states that a {\i mashoch tzarich sheyimal}, a {\i mashoch} must have a {\i brit milah} performed. The {\i Gemara} notes that this says no more than {\i derabbanan} \u8212- it does not prove a biblical prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De-ka-remi mai ka-remi lei} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} expresses surprise: why was this even presented as a challenge? The {\i braita} does not say the {\i mashoch} is {\i assur} in {\i terumah}; it merely says {\i tzarich}, he must. {\i Tzarich} is notably soft language, far from the categorical formulation one would expect for a biblical prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains that the challenge arose not from the opening clause but from the {\i seifa} \u8212- the end of the {\i braita} \u8212- which had made it sound as though the obligation was biblical. Rabbi Yehuda says in the {\i seifa}: {\i lo yimal mipnei shesacanah hi lo} \u8212- such a person should not have a {\i brit milah} because it is dangerous. Rashi explains that attempting to circumcise skin that has been stretched risks turning the person into a {\i kerus shofcha}, causing damage to his genitalia and rendering him unable to have children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i amru lo} \u8212- the response to Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- is that many men underwent this procedure during the days of Ben Koziva and subsequently had children, demonstrating it was not categorically dangerous. The {\i braita} then invokes the verse {\i himol yimol} \u8212- the doubled language \u8212- which is taken to mean the obligation repeats even if the {\i brit milah} must be performed again, up to {\i afilu me\u8217'ah pe\u8217'amim}, even one hundred times. A second verse, {\i et briti heifar} \u8212- \u8220"he has violated my covenant\u8221" \u8212- is cited {\i lerabbot es hamashoch}, to include someone who has reversed his circumcision as having violated the covenant and being obligated to correct it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai ve-omer} \u8212- why was the second verse necessary? Because one might say that {\i himol yimol} addresses only {\i tzitzin sheme\u8217'akvin}, fragments of foreskin that prevent the {\i milah} from being complete \u8212- meaning the doubled language teaches that one must go back and finish the job, not that a reversed {\i brit milah} requires repetition. The verse {\i et briti heifar} is therefore needed separately to include the {\i mashoch}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes that the Tannaic author ({\i hashas}) \u8212- an unusual term here applying to the author of the {\i braita} rather than to the {\i Mishnah} or {\i Gemara} \u8212- quoted these verses, and this gave the impression of a biblical obligation. In reality, {\i ve-lav hi mi-derabbanan ve-kra asmachta be-alma} \u8212- the prohibition is Rabbinic and the verse serves only as an {\i asmachta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges incidentally from this passage is a window into historical reality. The reference to {\i bimei Ben Koziva} \u8212- the period of Bar Kokhba \u8212- establishes that reversal of circumcision was practiced in two distinct historical contexts: the Hellenistic period and the Bar Kokhba period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the Hellenistic period, Jewish men who wished to assimilate into Greek culture sometimes underwent this procedure because the Greeks regarded circumcision as a disfigurement of the body. The {\i gymnasia}, central to Hellenistic civic and religious life, were venues of athletic competition conducted in the nude, making circumcision visible and socially stigmatizing for those seeking to integrate. In the Bar Kokhba period, the motivations may have been different \u8212- sometimes external pressures rather than voluntary assimilation. The fact that the {\i braita} mentions that many who underwent the re-circumcision during that period subsequently had children suggests that the {\i braita} addresses both those who reversed the reversal willingly and possibly those for whom even the restoration of circumcision may not have been entirely an act of free choice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumtum Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumtum Eating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a challenge against Rav Huna\u8217's ruling from a {\i braita}: {\i tumtum eino ochel biterumah, nashav va\u8217'avadav ochlin} \u8212- a {\i tumtum}, someone whose genitalia are covered and whose sex is therefore indeterminate, cannot eat {\i terumah}, but his wives and slaves can eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i tumtum} himself cannot eat {\i terumah} requires unpacking. If he is the child of a {\i kohen}, he should presumably be permitted to eat {\i terumah} regardless of sex. The answer is that if he is male, he is necessarily an {\i arel}, since his genitalia are covered and he cannot have had a {\i brit milah}. Therefore, because he might be male \u8212- and thus an {\i arel} \u8212- he cannot eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i mashoch}, a {\i mashoch}, {\i venolad keshu maul}, or someone born already circumcised \u8212- {\i harei eilu ochlin}, these may eat {\i terumah}. This appears to be a direct challenge to Rav Huna, since a {\i mashoch} is declared simply permitted, not merely Rabbinically restricted. Rashi, apparently working with a variant text, suggests reading this not as a {\i tiyuvta} \u8212- a refutation \u8212- of Rav Huna, but as a support: the {\i braita} establishes that a {\i mashoch} is at least {\i biblically} permitted, which is precisely what Rav Huna said. In the text as standardly printed, however, the {\i Gemara} reads it as {\i tiyuvta deRav Huna tiyuvta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} also addresses: {\i androgynos ochel biterumah} \u8212- an {\i androgynos}, one who has both male and female genitalia, may eat {\i terumah}. Since he has had a {\i brit milah} performed on his male anatomy and is the child of a {\i kohen}, he is not an {\i arel} and may eat {\i terumah}. However, {\i ve\u8217'eino ochel bekodshim} \u8212- he may not eat {\i kodshei kodashim}, which are restricted to male {\i kohanim}, since he might be female or a third category. The {\i tumtum}, by contrast, cannot eat {\i terumah} or {\i kodshim} \u8212- he cannot eat {\i terumah} because he might be a male {\i arel}, and he cannot eat {\i kodshim} because he might be female.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then focuses on the line that a {\i tumtum}\u8217's wives eat. This raises an immediate difficulty: {\i nashav detumtum mena leh} \u8212- how does a {\i tumtum} have wives? If he performed an act of {\i kiddushin} with a woman, this leads to the question: {\i eileima dekideish itata} \u8212- if he married a woman. A {\i braita} teaches: {\i tumtum shekideish, kidushav kidushin} \u8212- if a {\i tumtum} performs {\i kiddushin}, it takes effect, because out of doubt we must be concerned that he is male. Similarly, {\i nitkadesh} \u8212- if a man gave a ring to a {\i tumtum}, it too has binding implications, since the {\i tumtum} might be female.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i Gemara} objects: that stringency applies {\i lechumra}, to be strict about the consequences \u8212- implications for relatives, the need for a {\i get}, {\i mamzerut} and so forth. But {\i lekula mi amrinan} \u8212- can we rule leniently and assume the {\i tumtum} is male such that his \u8220"wife\u8221" is the wife of a {\i kohen} and therefore permitted to eat {\i terumah}? {\i Safek isha hi} \u8212- she might not actually be his wife if he is female, and {\i ein isha mekaddeshet isha} \u8212- a woman cannot marry a woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the problem by proposing: {\i kiveitzav mibachutz} \u8212- the case in the {\i braita} involves a {\i tumtum} whose testicles have descended visibly. This is not a total {\i tumtum} in the classic sense of complete indeterminacy; his maleness is established through visible anatomy even though a {\i brit milah} cannot be performed. He is thus a definite male, a definite {\i arel}, and his wife\u8217's status is certain. According to Abaye, the {\i braita} thus presents two distinct cases: the {\i reisha} describes this unusual {\i tumtum} who is a {\i vadai arel}, while the {\i seifa} \u8212- which repeats that a {\i tumtum} cannot eat {\i terumah} or {\i kodshim} \u8212- refers to the ordinary {\i tumtum} who is a {\i safek arel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees and offers a different reading of {\i nashav}. {\i Mai nashav} \u8212- what does \u8220"his women\u8221" mean? {\i Imo} \u8212- his mother. The word {\i isha} can mean wife or woman, and Rava reads {\i nashav} as referring not to wives but to the mother of the {\i tumtum}, who is married to a {\i kohen} and may eat {\i terumah} as long as there is a surviving child, regardless of that child\u8217's sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} objects that this is obvious \u8212- {\i pshita} \u8212- and asks what novel point Rava would be making. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i mahu deteima, ei ma hazera molid umachil, she\u8217'ein molid eino machil} \u8212- one might have thought that only a child capable of procreation enables the mother to eat {\i terumah}, whereas a child who cannot reproduce does not. Since a {\i tumtum} was presumed incapable of having children, one might have concluded the mother cannot eat. The {\i Gemara} teaches otherwise \u8212- {\i kamashma lan}, she may eat regardless. Rava\u8217's explanation, the {\i Gemara} acknowledges, is a stretch, and so is Abaye\u8217's; both are reconstructions that do not settle comfortably as {\i peshat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses the later passage of the {\i braita} \u8212- {\i tumtum eino ochel lo biterumah velo bekodshim} \u8212- which repeats that a {\i tumtum} cannot eat {\i terumah}. According to Abaye this is elegantly coherent: the {\i reisha} addressed an unusual {\i tumtum} who is a {\i vadai arel}, while the {\i seifa} addresses the ordinary {\i tumtum} who is a {\i safek arel}, demonstrating that even a doubtful case disqualifies. According to Rava, the repetition of {\i tumtum} serves to explain the {\i reisha}: why did it originally say a {\i tumtum} cannot eat {\i terumah}? Because he might be a {\i safek arel}, and {\i arel eino ochel lo biterumah velo bekodshim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumtum Safek Arel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's final resolution of the {\i tumtum} discussion is that the {\i tumtum} is excluded from {\i terumah} because he is a {\i safek arel}. This also explains the relationship between the two parts of the {\i braita}: the opening statement that a {\i tumtum} cannot eat {\i terumah} and the closing statement that underscores this \u8212- both are expressions of the same concern that he might be male and therefore an {\i arel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The outstanding question about whether a {\i mashoch} is Biblically or Rabbinically forbidden remains: the {\i braita} either wholly contradicts Rav Huna or \u8212- per Rashi\u8217's reading \u8212- supports him. Either way, the {\i braita} treats {\i mashoch} as a distinct category, and the language of {\i tzarich} rather than {\i assur} continues to suggest Rabbinic rather than Biblical status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transgender Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether the {\i Gemara}\u8217's discussion of {\i tumtum} \u8212- and especially the ruling that {\i tumtum shekideish kidushav kidushin, nitkadesh kidushav kidushin} \u8212- has any bearing on contemporary questions of transgender and intersex identity in {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i androgynos} tends to generate more significant discussion in this context than the {\i tumtum}. The {\i androgynos} raises the possibility, contemplated in Tannaic literature, that the {\i androgynos} is a {\i biryah bifnei atzmah} \u8212- a category unto itself, neither male nor female \u8212- which speaks directly to intersex questions and the possibility of recognizing a third halakhic sex category. The {\i tumtum}, by contrast, is fundamentally a case of {\i safek} \u8212- the sex is one thing or the other, we simply do not know which.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the transgender question: the {\i Gemara}\u8217's framework rests on the assumption that sex is determined by genitalia. Most contemporary {\i poskim} hold that {\i halachic} status is determined by birth sex. The more complex question arises when a child is born with ambiguous genitalia requiring surgical determination of sex in infancy \u8212- that is, where there is a genuine indeterminate status at birth. In that case there may be more basis to argue that a subsequent determination, through medical intervention, affects {\i halachic} status. When an individual has a clearly defined birth sex and later transitions, the overwhelming {\i halachic} consensus is that status is determined by what one is at birth.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brit Milah Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now explores whether the dispute about a {\i mashoch}\u8217's status \u8212- biblical or Rabbinic \u8212- corresponds to a known Tannaic debate. A {\i braita} lists: a {\i mashoch}, a convert who was already circumcised at the time of conversion, an infant whose {\i brit milah} was not performed on day 8, and all other non-standard cases of {\i milah} \u8212- {\i einam nimolin ela bayom}, all of these must be circumcised specifically during the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar bar Shimon dissents: {\i bizmano eno nimol ela bayom, shelo bizmano nimol bayom ubalayla} \u8212- the requirement of daytime applies only to the eighth-day {\i brit milah}; if it is performed later than the prescribed time, it may be done either by day or by night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daytime Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proposes that the underlying dispute concerns whether a {\i mashoch}\u8217's obligation is {\i deoraita} \u8212- in which case it follows the rules of the classic {\i brit milah} and must be performed during the day \u8212- or {\i derabbanan}, in which case it might be permitted at night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately rejects this framing. {\i Ve-tisbara} \u8212- does this make sense? An infant whose {\i brit milah} is performed after day 8 \u8212- {\i katan she\u8217'avar zamano} \u8212- is surely a biblical obligation. No one would say {\i derabbanan}. So the question of day versus night clearly does not track the biblical/Rabbinic distinction, since the list contains both clearly biblical cases and clearly Rabbinic ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vav Drashah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, {\i dechulei alma mashoch derabbanan} \u8212- let us accept that the obligation to re-circumcise a {\i mashoch} is Rabbinic. And {\i katan she\u8217'avar zamano deoraita}, that the infant after day 8 is biblical. The list contains both, and nevertheless the same question applies to all: does the Torah\u8217's requirement of {\i bayom} apply only to day 8 itself, or does it extend to any later performance?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate is resolved to be specifically about a textual {\i drashah}. The verse states {\i bayom hashmini yimol basar orlato} \u8212- on the eighth day the flesh of his foreskin shall be circumcised. The word {\i uvayom} \u8212- with the extra {\i vav} \u8212- is read by one opinion as teaching that even if it is not the eighth day, it must still be {\i yom}, daytime. The other opinion does not read the {\i vav} of {\i uvayom} as bearing this extended meaning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Notar Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan was sitting and expounding a parallel from the laws of {\i notar}. The verse {\i vehanotar bayom hashlishi baesh yisaref} \u8212- \u8220"and the remainder on the third day shall be burnt in fire\u8221" \u8212- establishes that the burning of {\i notar} must begin by day, specifically day 3. Rabbi Yochanan taught: {\i nosar bizmano eno nisraf ela bayom, shelo bizmano nisraf bayom ubalayla} \u8212- when the time first arrives it must be by day, but once the obligation has begun and extends beyond its initial moment, it continues into the night and is not limited to daytime thereafter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Exchange\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This parallel was taken to support the position that {\i brit milah}, too, begins with a daytime requirement on day 8 but may be performed at night if delayed \u8212- like the view of Rabbi Elazar bar Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar then challenged Rabbi Yochanan directly: {\i ein li ela nimol lishmini she\u8217'eno nimol ela bayom} \u8212- I know only that the eighth-day {\i brit milah} must be during the day. {\i Minayin lerabot letisha le\u8217'asara leachad asar velishnem asar} \u8212- from where do I know that even if performed on day 9, 10, 11, or 12, it must still be during the day? {\i Talmud lomar uvayom} \u8212- the extra {\i vav} teaches that even on a later date, it is exclusively daytime.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The days 9 through 12 correspond to real cases. A baby born on {\i erev Shabbat bein hashmashot} presents a safek, and one must assume the later possibility, pushing the {\i brit milah} to day 9. If Shabbat itself was {\i bein hashmashot}, it becomes day 10. A Shabbat followed by {\i Yom Tov} produces day 11, and two days of {\i Rosh Hashanah} yield day 12.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar added that even the opinion that does not read {\i uvayom} as extending the daytime requirement must acknowledge the {\i vav-heh} of {\i vehanotar} \u8212- the longer prefix \u8212- which teaches that even when the obligation extends beyond its starting point, it remains tied to daytime. Rabbi Yochanan, confronted with this argument, fell silent. Afterward, when Rabbi Elazar had left, Rabbi Yochanan turned to Resh Lakish and said: {\i ra\u8217'iti le-Ben Pedat she-yoshev ve-doresh ke-Moshe mi-pi ha-Gevurah} \u8212- I saw Ben Pedat sitting and expounding like Moshe from the mouth of the Almighty. Whether this was admiration or mild criticism \u8212- he was doing what I was doing, but perhaps with greater authority \u8212- is not entirely clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish replied: {\i dideih?} \u8212- Do you think Rabbi Elazar composed that {\i drashah} on his own authority? {\i Matnitin hi} \u8212- it is a {\i braita}; he was quoting established teaching, not originating it. Rabbi Yochanan asked: {\i heicha tana leih} \u8212- where is this {\i braita} found? Resh Lakish answered: {\i be-Toras Kohanim} \u8212- in the Sifra.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brit Milah Timing (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Toras Kohanim Study\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Upon hearing this, Rabbi Yochanan went and immersed himself in {\i Toras Kohanim}: {\i tanyah be-tlata yomei} \u8212- he mastered the text in three days, {\i ve-savrah be-tlata yarchei} \u8212- and spent three months working through its analysis. This raises a remarkable question: had Rabbi Yochanan truly not previously learned {\i Toras Kohanim} with sufficient depth? Or had he not appreciated it as he should have? The implication seems to be that until this moment he had not engaged with the Sifra adequately, and the encounter with Rabbi Elazar\u8217's precise citation of a {\i braita} he did not recognize prompted him to undertake that study.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This naturally raises the question of when {\i Toras Kohanim} was committed to writing. The general assumption has been that any written form of {\i Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh} belongs to the Geonic period or later. However, Shlomo Naeh published a study examining the various and overlapping division systems of {\i Toras Kohanim} \u8212- its dual organization by {\i prakim} and {\i parshiyyot}, whose boundaries frequently do not align and seem to have been laid atop one another \u8212- and proposed that the length of each unit, across all divisions, falls within a specific range. That range corresponds to the accepted dimensions of scrolls in the Greco-Roman world, where scroll length was governed by practical considerations: too short meant too many scrolls; too long meant too much winding. Naeh argued that these divisions reflect the original written organization of the text, and that {\i Toras Kohanim} may be the earliest component of {\i Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh} to have been set down in writing \u8212- plausibly because the dominance of {\i Beit HaMikdash} culture preceded the Tannaic era, and the practical requirements of {\i avodah} demanded written precision. Scholarly opinion on his conclusions is divided, but the argument is considered compelling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arel & Parah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to a new ruling: {\i amar Rabbi Elazar, arel shehizah hazaso kesherah} \u8212- an {\i arel} who performed the {\i hazaah}, the sprinkling of the {\i parah adumah} waters, his sprinkling is valid. The question is whether there is any reason to think an {\i arel} should be disqualified from performing this service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tvul Yom Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar offers a comparison: {\i mi havei mi-tvul yom} \u8212- is an {\i arel} any worse than a {\i tvul yom}? The practice of using a {\i tvul yom} to perform the {\i parah adumah} service was deliberately instituted by the {\i chachamim} to refute the {\i tzedukim}, who insisted the {\i kohen} performing the {\i parah} service must have waited until nightfall after immersion. The {\i chachamim} ensured the service was performed by one who had immersed that day but nightfall had not yet come \u8212- demonstrating the {\i tzedukim} were wrong. A {\i tvul yom}, who retains some residual {\i tumah} until nightfall, is {\i asur biterumah} yet {\i kasher beparah} \u8212- forbidden from eating {\i terumah} yet valid for the {\i parah adumah} service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges this comparison: {\i mah litvul yom sheken mutar bemaaser} \u8212- a {\i tvul yom} is permitted to eat {\i maaser}, whereas an {\i arel} may not be. This will be discussed in subsequent {\i sugyot}. But the {\i Gemara} immediately deflects this objection: {\i atu anachilah kamina} \u8212- who said we are talking about eating? {\i Ana lenegiah kamina} \u8212- the relevant category for performing the service is the ability to handle and touch, not the rules of eating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proper comparison, then, is a {\i kal vachomer}: a {\i tvul yom} who is {\i asur binegiah biterumah} \u8212- forbidden from touching {\i terumah} \u8212- is {\i mutar beparah}, valid for the {\i parah adumah} service. An {\i arel}, who is {\i mutar binegiah} \u8212- permitted to handle {\i terumah} \u8212- surely should be permitted for the {\i parah adumah} service, {\i ain din shemutar beparah}. This argument is confirmed by a {\i braita}: {\i arel shehizah hazaso kesherah, umaaseh hayah vehichshiru chachamim hazaso} \u8212- an {\i arel} who performed the {\i hazaah} is valid, and this was actually practiced and the {\i chachamim} ruled it acceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arel Cannot Mix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A challenge is immediately raised: {\i tumtum shekidesh kiddushav pasul} \u8212- here {\i kiddush} refers not to marriage but to the {\i kiddush} of the {\i parah adumah}, the mixing of the ashes with water \u8212- a {\i tumtum} who performed this mixing has rendered it invalid, {\i mipnei shesafek arel}, because he might be an {\i arel}, and {\i arel pasul lekadesh} \u8212- an {\i arel} is invalid for the {\i kiddush}. This {\i braita} explicitly states that an {\i arel} cannot perform the {\i kiddush}, directly contradicting the ruling that he may perform the {\i hazaah}. The {\i Gemara} accepts for the moment that these two acts \u8212- {\i kiddush} and {\i hazaah} \u8212- are treated equivalently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i androgynos shekidesh kiddushav kasher} \u8212- an {\i androgynos} who performed the {\i kiddush} is valid, because the concern about an {\i androgynos} is that he might be female, not that he might be an {\i arel}. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: {\i af androgynos shekidesh kiddushav psulin, mipnei shesafek isha hi, ve\u8217'isha psulah melekadesh} \u8212- an {\i androgynos} is also invalid, because he might be a woman and a woman is invalid for this service. This frames the debate between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i Tanna Kamma} about whether women may perform the {\i parah adumah} service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i braita} states explicitly: {\i arel vesafek arel pasul melekadesh} \u8212- both a definite and a doubtful {\i arel} are invalid for the {\i kiddush} of the {\i parah adumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef offers a resolution: {\i hai tanna tanna devei Rabbi Akiva hu, demarbei leh le\u8217'arel ketamei} \u8212- this {\i tanna} who invalidates the {\i arel} belongs to the school of Rabbi Akiva, who treats the {\i arel} as equivalent to a {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef\u8217's reference is to the beginning of the {\i perek}, where the source of the prohibition against an {\i arel} eating {\i terumah} was disputed. Rabbi Akiva derived it from the double expression {\i ish ish} in a verse about {\i tmei\u8217'im} \u8212- {\i lerabbot es ha\u8217'arel}, to include the {\i arel} in that verse\u8217's prohibition. From this, Rav Yosef draws the stronger conclusion: Rabbi Akiva treated the {\i arel} not merely as analogous to a {\i tamei} for purposes of {\i terumah}, but as equivalent to a {\i tamei} in terms of personal status across the board. Wherever a {\i tamei} is personally excluded \u8212- not in the sense of transferring {\i tumah}, but in terms of his own disqualification \u8212- an {\i arel} would be similarly disqualified. That is why Rabbi Akiva\u8217's school would invalidate an {\i arel} from performing the {\i parah adumah} service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects strongly: {\i hevei yasivina kamei d\u8217'Rav Yosef ve-kashyei li} \u8212- I sat before Rav Yosef and this troubled me. If there were truly a position of Rabbi Akiva treating the {\i arel} as a {\i tamei} in all areas, we should have found some statement in {\i Shas} that said {\i ha\u8217'arel ve-hatamei} cannot do such-and-such, accompanied by the attribution to Rabbi Akiva. The absence of any such formulation across the entirety of {\i Shas} argues strongly against this being Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position. {\i Lo ishtami tanna ve-lisni ha\u8217'arel ve-hatamei ve-leima Rabbi Akiva hi} \u8212- it would not have escaped notice; some {\i tanna} would have formulated the rule in that way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arel Tamei Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i ve-haktani ha\u8217'arel ve-hatamei paturim min hare\u8217'iyah} \u8212- there is a {\i Mishna} stating that an {\i arel} and a {\i tamei} are both exempt from {\i olat re\u8217'iyah}, the pilgrimage offering. Here we have exactly the formulation Rava said was missing \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'arel ve-hatamei} \u8212- grouped together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} counters: {\i chutz mishum d\u8217'mais} \u8212- an {\i arel} is excluded from the {\i Beit HaMikdash} not because he has the status of a {\i tamei}, but simply because it is unseemly for someone who is an {\i arel} to present himself in the Temple. The grouping with {\i tamei} in that {\i Mishna} is incidental and does not reflect a principled equation of the two categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} acknowledges that this response somewhat undercuts Rava\u8217's objection \u8212- after all, the {\i Mishna} does say {\i ha\u8217'arel ve-hatamei} together, and the reason given for excluding the {\i arel} could itself reflect an assumption of equivalence. What remains is the fascinating Tannaic possibility \u8212- preserved in the school of Rabbi Akiva \u8212- that in terms of personal status, wherever a {\i tamei} is excluded, an {\i arel} is excluded as well. Rava\u8217's objection does not fully resolve the matter, and the question of whether this was Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position continues to resonate through the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parah Service Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disqualified List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with the broader dispute about who may perform the various functions of the {\i parah adumah} service, following the principle {\i ve-azdu le-taimeihu} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i Rabbanan} proceed according to their respective reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} states: {\i hakol keshairin lekadesh} \u8212- anyone is eligible to perform the {\i kiddush} of the {\i parah adumah}, mixing the ashes with water, {\i chutz mi-cheresh shoteh ve-katan} \u8212- with the exception of a deaf-mute, an imbecile, and a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Androgynos Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda differs: {\i machshir be-katan u-fosel be-ishah} \u8212- he validates the {\i katan} but invalidates the {\i ishah}, and by extension an {\i androgynos} who might be female. The {\i Rabbanan}, conversely, invalidate the {\i katan} but validate the {\i ishah}. An {\i androgynos} according to the {\i Rabbanan} is valid \u8212- he is excluded only if he might be female, and if his male nature is established his participation is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verses Asifah Kiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between the {\i Rabbanan} and Rabbi Yehuda is rooted in the interpretation of two adjacent verses. One verse states {\i ve-asaf ish tahor} \u8212- \u8220"and a pure man shall gather\u8221" the ashes. The next verse states {\i ve-lakhu latamei me\u8217'afar sereifat hachatat} \u8212- \u8220"and they shall take for the {\i tamei} person from the ashes\u8221" \u8212- and place it with water. The principle derived is: {\i hanakh de-pasli be-asifah psulin be-kiddush} \u8212- those who are invalid for the gathering of the ashes are invalid for the mixing of the water; {\i hanakh de-keshairin be-asifah keshairin be-kiddush} \u8212- those valid for gathering are valid for mixing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Included\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan} argue as follows. The word {\i tahor} in {\i ve-asaf ish tahor} is understood to include women \u8212- there is a {\i drashah} elsewhere establishing this. Therefore, the {\i asifah} can be performed by any adult of sound mind, male or female, but not by a {\i katan} who lacks {\i da\u8217'at}. Since the same persons valid for {\i asifah} are valid for {\i kiddush}, a woman is valid for {\i kiddush} as well. A {\i katan} is excluded from both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda counters: {\i amar lach} \u8212- if the intent of {\i ve-lakhu} were simply to preserve the same eligibility as {\i ve-asaf}, the verse should have said {\i ve-lakach} in the singular, parallel to {\i ve-asaf}. {\i Mai ve-laku} \u8212- why does the verse shift to the plural? This teaches that the category for {\i kiddush} is actually {\i broader} than for {\i asifah}: {\i de-afilu hanakh de-psulin hatam keshairin hacha} \u8212- even those invalid for {\i asifah}, such as a {\i katan}, are valid for {\i kiddush}. Rabbi Yehuda therefore holds that a {\i katan} may perform {\i kiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ei hachi, ishah nami?} \u8212- But if the plural {\i ve-laku} expands eligibility to include even those excluded from {\i asifah}, why is a woman still invalid? The answer comes from the verse continuing: {\i ve-nasan alav mayim chayim el keli} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall place living water into a vessel.\u8221" The verse says {\i ve-nasan} in the masculine singular, not {\i ve-nasnah}, implicitly excluding a woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plural Laku Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan} respond to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's grammatical argument. Had the verse said {\i ve-lakach ve-nasan} in the singular throughout, one might have concluded that the identical person who gathers must be the one who mixes, and that only one person may perform the entire act. The plural {\i ve-laku} teaches that multiple people may be involved in the gathering \u8212- or that different people may perform different stages. Had it continued {\i ve-lakhu ve-nasnu}, one might have required two people for the mixing as well. {\i Ve-lakhu ve-nasan} \u8212- the shift from plural to singular \u8212- teaches that even if multiple people gathered, a single person may perform the mixing. The grammatical movement from plural to singular is not meant to exclude women but to clarify the flexibility of personnel for each stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hizah Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes with the verse that grounds the initial argument about the {\i tvul yom} and the {\i hazaah}: {\i ve-hizah ha-tahor al ha-tamei} \u8212- \u8220"and the pure one shall sprinkle upon the impure one.\u8221" {\i Tahor miklal she-hu tamei} \u8212- this is a remarkable {\i drashah}: the word {\i tahor}, pure, implies by contrast that this person had previously been {\i tamei} and went through a process of {\i taharah}. Yet the verse\u8217's qualification of {\i tahor} \u8212- rather than calling him simply pure \u8212- suggests that his {\i taharah} is not entirely complete. From here we derive that a {\i tvul yom}, who has immersed but whose {\i taharah} is not yet concluded until nightfall, is valid for performing the {\i hazaah} of the {\i parah adumah}. And from this teaching about the {\i tvul yom}, the {\i Gemara} earlier sought to establish that an {\i arel} could likewise perform the {\i hazaah} \u8212- though that proposal encountered the challenge of the {\i braita} invalidating an {\i arel}, attributed to the school of Rabbi Akiva, who equates the personal status of an {\i arel} with that of a {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 73\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arel & Maaser Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Posed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens with a new question posed to Rav Sheshet: {\i arel mahu bema\u8217'aser} \u8212- can an {\i arel}, an uncircumcised person, eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}? This question arises in the midst of the chapter of {\i Ha\u8217'Arel}, which deals broadly with the legal disabilities of an uncircumcised man. Just before this, the {\i gemara} had been discussing whether an {\i arel} can perform the sprinkling of the ashes of the {\i parah adumah}, a question in which two opinions were recorded. One view, attributed to Rabbi Akiva, held that wherever a {\i tamei} person is disqualified on account of personal status rather than the transmission of {\i tuma}, an {\i arel} is likewise disqualified. Rava disagreed, but the position remained significant. Now the discussion shifts to a new and somewhat more obscure question: can an {\i arel} eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Sheni Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ma\u8217'aser} at issue is specifically {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, the second tithe, for a straightforward reason. {\i Ma\u8217'aser rishon} goes to the {\i Levi}, and {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} is a gift distributed to the poor \u8212- neither of these carries any inherent {\i kedusha}. They are simply agricultural gifts, and the produce itself has no sanctified status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Maaser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ma\u8217'aser rishon} is given to the {\i Levi} and {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} to the {\i ani}; neither imposes restrictions on the produce itself. The only {\i ma\u8217'aser} that possesses a quality of {\i kedusha} is {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, and it is therefore the only one that could plausibly be subject to the same restrictions as {\i terumah}. We will also encounter, later in the discussion, the anomalous position of Rabbi Meir regarding {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}, but the default assumption is that {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} alone carries sanctity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Sheni Laws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the nature of {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}\u8217's {\i kedusha}? It must be eaten in Yerushalayim. If one does not wish to bring the produce itself to Yerushalayim, it may be redeemed \u8212- its sanctity is transferred onto coins, which are then brought to Yerushalayim and spent on food, drink, or anointing, that is, on things that are directly consumed or used by the body. According to Rashi, {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} may only be used for these direct bodily benefits. It cannot be used in commercial transactions; it is not at the owner\u8217's disposal in the way ordinary property is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this {\i kedusha}, the question takes shape. We already know that an {\i arel} cannot eat {\i terumah} \u8212- that is the very subject of this chapter, derived from a {\i pasuk}. The questioners now ask: do we extend this to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}? The {\i gemara} frames the question around a potential inference. We know that an {\i onen} \u8212- a mourner in the immediate period following a relative\u8217's death \u8212- cannot eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}, derived from the phrase {\i lo achalti be\u8217'oni mimenu} in the {\i viduy ma\u8217'aser}. From that, the {\i gemara} elsewhere learned that an {\i onen} cannot eat the {\i korban Pesach}. The logic moves from the lighter ({\i ma\u8217'aser}) to the weightier ({\i Pesach}): if even {\i ma\u8217'aser} bars an {\i onen}, certainly the {\i korban Pesach} does. Can we run this logic in reverse and say: since an {\i arel} cannot eat {\i Pesach}, therefore an {\i arel} cannot eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the objection: {\i chamir mikal yalif, kal michamir lo yalif} \u8212- an inference from the lighter to the weightier works, but you cannot necessarily reverse it and infer from the weightier to the lighter. The {\i korban Pesach} is {\i kodshim}; it is far more weighty than {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. The fact that an {\i arel} is forbidden from {\i Pesach} does not by itself establish that he is forbidden from the lighter {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. Tosafot notes that the question may turn on whether the link between {\i Pesach} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} is a {\i kal vachomer} or a {\i hekesh} \u8212- a formal textual equation \u8212- since a {\i hekesh} is not directional in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet Answers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet responds to those who asked him: {\i tenituha} \u8212- we have already learned the answer. He directs them to a {\i mishna} in {\i Bikkurim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} in {\i Bikkurim} lists the ways in which {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} share laws that do not apply to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. Everything enumerated in this first list is a stringency or a property that {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} share and that {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bikkurim Hybridity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding, it is worth noting the somewhat surprising structure of this {\i mishna}. Intuitively, one might have expected {\i bikkurim} to be grouped with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} rather than with {\i terumah}. Both {\i bikkurim} and {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} involve bringing produce to a central location \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} to Yerushalayim, {\i bikkurim} to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- and both involve a formal declaration. Yet the first list groups {\i bikkurim} with {\i terumah}. The reason is that {\i bikkurim}, like {\i terumah}, is given to the {\i kohen} and may only be eaten by {\i kohanim}; a non-{\i kohen} who eats it is liable. So {\i bikkurim} is genuinely a hybrid: it shares properties with {\i terumah} by virtue of its priestly ownership and restriction, and it shares properties with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} by virtue of being brought to Yerushalayim and requiring a declaration. This duality explains why both lists exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mita & Chomesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Haterumah vehabikkurim chayavim aleihem mita vechomesh.} Both {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} carry the penalty of {\i mita} \u8212- death at divine hands \u8212- for a non-{\i kohen} who eats them, as derived from {\i umetu vo ki yechalelehu}. {\i Bikkurim} derives this law from the linkage to {\i terumah} through the phrase {\i terumat yadecha}. Alongside {\i mita}, there is the {\i chomesh}: if a non-{\i kohen} eats {\i terumah} or {\i bikkurim} unintentionally, he must return the principal to the {\i kohen} plus an additional fifth. These penalties obviously have no application to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, since non-{\i kohanim} eat {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} routinely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i shehen nichsei kohen} \u8212- they are the property of the {\i kohen}. This may seem self-evident given that one gives them to the {\i kohen}, but it carries a meaningful legal consequence that is actually a leniency of {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} relative to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. Because {\i terumah} is the {\i kohen}\u8217's property, he may dispose of it as he sees fit within the constraints of its sanctity. He can, for example, use it as a medium of exchange \u8212- bringing it to a shopkeeper and purchasing goods with it. The shopkeeper, a non-{\i kohen}, may not eat the {\i terumah}, but he can trade it onward to another {\i kohen} or otherwise realize its value. The {\i terumah} has a diminished market value because of its restrictions \u8212- it can only be consumed by {\i kohanim}, must be kept {\i tahor}, and so forth \u8212- but it is nonetheless treated as genuine transferable property. A {\i kohen} could even use {\i terumah} to effect {\i kiddushin} with a woman, telling her {\i harei at mekudeshet li b\u8217'terumah zo}, and once she is fully married and eats {\i terumah} as a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife, that is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ma\u8217'aser sheni} is fundamentally different. It cannot be used in {\i sechorah} \u8212- commercial transactions \u8212- at all. It may only be directly consumed: eaten, drunk, or used for anointing. The owner has no freedom to trade it or use it as a medium of exchange. There is a further, deeper question about {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}\u8217's legal status: Rabbi Meir holds that {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} is {\i mammon gavoha} \u8212- it belongs to the divine realm \u8212- while the {\i Chachamim} hold it is {\i mammon hedyot} \u8212- the owner\u8217's property, albeit with {\i kedusha} and attendant restrictions. This debate has practical consequences for questions of ownership such as liability when an animal purchased with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money causes damage. The point for our purposes is that whatever one holds about ultimate ownership, the prohibition against using {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} in transactions is clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bittul Ratios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further distinction: {\i v\u8217'erev echad bemeah} \u8212- {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} are only {\i batel}, nullified, when mixed with other produce if the other produce constitutes at least one hundred times their volume. This is a special stringency the Rabbis applied to {\i terumah} \u8212- and extended to {\i bikkurim} \u8212- as well as to {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim}. The rationale that Rashi implies is that {\i terumah} is so potent in concept, since even a tiny amount of {\i terumah} can exempt an enormous pile of grain from the obligation, that it does not become {\i batel} at normal proportions. One needs a hundred-to-one ratio. {\i Ma\u8217'aser sheni}, by contrast, follows the ordinary rule, which according to Rashi is {\i rov} \u8212- a simple majority \u8212- though perhaps {\i shishim} might apply as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand Washing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Teunim rechitzat yadayim} \u8212- eating {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} requires prior washing of hands. This is because the Rabbis declared hands to be at the level of {\i sheni letumah} \u8212- a secondary source of impurity \u8212- and since {\i terumah} can be made {\i tamei} by a {\i sheni}, one must wash before handling it. {\i Ma\u8217'aser sheni} does not carry this requirement, reflecting its lower level of {\i kedusha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Levels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-ha\u8217'arev shemesh} \u8212- the requirement of waiting until nightfall after immersion. A person who was {\i tamei} and immersed in a {\i mikveh} is not fully {\i tahor} until sunset. In the interval \u8212- after immersion but before nightfall \u8212- he has the status of a {\i sheni letumah}. He may therefore not touch {\i terumah} or {\i bikkurim}, but he may touch {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader {\i halakhic} picture here is worth sketching. A primary source of {\i tuma} \u8212- an {\i av hatuma}, such as a dead animal \u8212- can make a person, food, or a vessel {\i tamei}. A person so made {\i tamei} becomes a {\i rishon letumah} and can in turn make food {\i tamei}. That food becomes a {\i sheni}, and a {\i sheni} can make {\i terumah tamei}, rendering it a {\i shlishi}. A {\i shlishi} in {\i terumah} can then make {\i kodshim tamei}. This chain of {\i tuma} transmission operates precisely because {\i terumah} has a higher level of {\i kedusha} \u8212- it is restricted to {\i kohanim}, and violating it carries {\i mita}. {\i Ma\u8217'aser sheni} does not have this elevated {\i kedusha}, and therefore a {\i sheni letumah} cannot make {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni tamei} in the same way. The hand-washing and {\i ha\u8217'arev shemesh} requirements follow from this: since one\u8217's hands have the status of {\i sheni}, and since a {\i sheni} can make {\i terumah} into a {\i shlishi}, one washes before touching {\i terumah}. This does not apply to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Burning When Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final distinction in this first list, and one that will receive extensive analysis later: when {\i terumah} becomes {\i tamei}, the correct procedure is to burn it and derive benefit from it as fuel \u8212- the classic case discussed in the {\i mishna} of {\i Bameh Madlikin} involving {\i shemen sereifa}. By contrast, when {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} or {\i bikkurim} becomes {\i tamei}, one may not burn it for fuel and benefit from it. Similarly, eating {\i tamei} {\i ma\u8217'aser} incurs {\i malkot}, lashes, since it violates a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, whereas eating {\i tamei} {\i terumah} does not incur lashes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Exhaustive Lists\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arel Absence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now attempts to draw a proof from this {\i mishna}. If an {\i arel} is permitted to eat {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, then the prohibition against an {\i arel} eating {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} \u8212- which is the subject of this entire chapter \u8212- would constitute another point of distinction between {\i terumah}/{\i bikkurim} on the one hand and {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} on the other. The {\i mishna} could have said: {\i arel assur bahem}, an {\i arel} is forbidden in {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim}, {\i mah she\u8217'ein kein bema\u8217'aser}, but not in {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. Since the {\i mishna} did not include this as a point of difference, the implication seems to be that an {\i arel} cannot eat {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} either \u8212- otherwise it would have been mentioned as a distinction. This appears to be a strong proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's response: {\i tana veshayar} \u8212- the list is not exhaustive; things were left off. One cannot infer anything from an absence in an incomplete list. But this requires substantiation. {\i Uma\u8217'i shayar de-hai shayar} \u8212- what else was left off, such that we can say this too was left off? The {\i gemara} needs to identify at least two omissions to establish that the list is genuinely non-exhaustive, since if only one item were missing, one should simply have included it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} points to the {\i seifa} \u8212- the second half of the {\i mishna}, which lists the ways in which {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} and {\i bikkurim} are similar to each other and different from {\i terumah}. This second list includes: the requirement to bring them to a specific place \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} to Yerushalayim, {\i bikkurim} to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}; the requirement of {\i viduy}, a formal declaration \u8212- the {\i bikkurim} declaration of {\i Arami oved avi}, and the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} declaration including {\i lo achalti be\u8217'oni mimenu}; the prohibition against an {\i onen} eating them, derived from {\i lo achalti be\u8217'oni mimenu}; and the requirement of {\i biur} \u8212- clearing them out at the end of the three-year cycle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the last two items, the {\i mishna} records a dispute. The {\i tanna kamma} holds that the {\i onen} and {\i biur} restrictions, which clearly apply to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, also apply to {\i bikkurim}, since the {\i pasuk lo tuchal le\u8217'echol bish\u8217'arecha} links {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} and {\i bikkurim} together. Rabbi Shimon disagrees and exempts {\i bikkurim} from both, holding that {\i bikkurim}\u8217's fundamental identity is that of {\i terumah}, not {\i ma\u8217'aser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Omitted Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} observes that this second list also left things off \u8212- and not just one thing. Two items that belong on this list, in that they apply to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} (and arguably to {\i bikkurim}) but not to {\i terumah}, are missing. First: {\i assur leva\u8217'er mehem betumah} \u8212- when {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} becomes {\i tamei}, one may not burn it for fuel, whereas {\i terumah} that becomes {\i tamei} is burned. Second: {\i ve\u8217'ochlan betumat atzman lokeh} \u8212- if one eats {\i tamei} {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, one receives {\i malkot}, lashes, since the prohibition derives from {\i lo tuchal le\u8217'echol bish\u8217'arecha}, a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, whereas the prohibition on eating {\i tamei} {\i terumah} is derived from a positive commandment and therefore does not carry lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both of these omissions are rooted in the same {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i lo bi\u8217'arti mimennu betamei}, from the {\i viduy ma\u8217'aser}. That verse teaches: do not consume it in a state of {\i tuma}, whether you were {\i tamei} and the {\i ma\u8217'aser} was {\i tahor}, or you were {\i tahor} and the {\i ma\u8217'aser} was {\i tamei}. The word {\i bi\u8217'arti} encompasses both burning for fuel and eating. Neither applies to {\i tamei} {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, in stark contrast to {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inference Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The demonstration that the second list is also non-exhaustive establishes the general point: these are not complete enumerations. Both the first list ({\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} against {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}) and the second list ({\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} and {\i bikkurim} against {\i terumah}) leave things off. One may therefore not infer from the absence of a distinction that no such distinction exists. The failure to mention the {\i arel} as a point of difference between {\i terumah}/{\i bikkurim} and {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} proves nothing. Perhaps an {\i arel} can eat {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, and the {\i mishna} simply left that distinction off an incomplete list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make this vivid: the entire thrust of the attempted proof was that since the {\i mishna} did not say \u8220"an {\i arel} cannot eat {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} but can eat {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni},\u8221" this must mean the {\i arel} is treated the same way by {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. The {\i gemara} dismantles this inference by showing that both lists are admittedly truncated, and therefore silences cannot bear the weight of legal conclusions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Onen & Biur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Linkage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to examine the debates embedded in the {\i mishna}\u8217's second list. It asks: what is the source for the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's view that the {\i onen} and {\i biur} rules apply to {\i bikkurim} as well as to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}? The answer is the {\i pasuk}: {\i lo tuchal le\u8217'echol bish\u8217'arecha ma\u8217'aser deganecha tiroshcha veyitzharecha u\u8217'terumat yadecha}. This verse prohibits eating certain things \u8220"in your gates\u8221" \u8212- meaning outside Yerushalayim. Taken at face value the verse is puzzling, since {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} is given to the {\i Levi} in one\u8217's own city and {\i terumah} is eaten by {\i kohanim} throughout the land. The {\i gemara} therefore clarifies: the {\i ma\u8217'aser} that cannot be eaten \u8220"in your gates\u8221" is {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, which must be eaten in Yerushalayim. And the {\i terumat yadecha} refers to {\i bikkurim}, as the tradition has it: {\i terumat yadecha elu bikkurim}. The verse thus links {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} and {\i bikkurim} together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Onen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this linkage, the {\i tanna kamma} derives: just as an {\i onen} cannot eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- as stated explicitly in the {\i viduy} declaration {\i lo achalti be\u8217'oni mimennu} \u8212- so too an {\i onen} cannot eat {\i bikkurim}, since they are joined in the same {\i pasuk}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Onen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon rejects this extension. His argument is that {\i bikkurim}\u8217's identity is fundamentally that of {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i terumah karinah Rachmana}. Although {\i bikkurim} appears in the same verse as {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, that linkage reflects the coincidence of both being brought to Yerushalayim, not a substantive equation of their {\i kedusha}. The basic character of {\i bikkurim} \u8212- given to the {\i kohen}, eaten only by {\i kohanim}, carrying {\i mita} and {\i chomesh} \u8212- aligns it with {\i terumah}. And {\i terumah} is permitted to an {\i onen}. Therefore {\i bikkurim} is also permitted to an {\i onen}. It is worth noting that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position is the more intuitively compelling one: since every substantive legal property of {\i bikkurim} tracks {\i terumah}, it is difficult to uproot it from that identity on the basis of a single verse that places it in proximity to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biur Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logical structure governs the {\i biur} dispute. The {\i tanna kamma} holds that just as {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} must be cleared from the house in the third year of the {\i shemita} cycle \u8212- derived from {\i bi\u8217'arti hakodesh min habayit} \u8212- so too must {\i bikkurim}, on the basis of the same {\i pasuk} linkage. Rabbi Shimon exempts {\i bikkurim} from {\i biur}, for the same reason: {\i bikkurim}\u8217's identity is {\i terumah}, and {\i terumah} has no such {\i biur} requirement. Rabbi Shimon adopts a minimalist stance throughout \u8212- he will link {\i bikkurim} to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} only where the connection is entirely obvious, and in all other respects he insists on {\i bikkurim}\u8217's identity as {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lo Ba\u8217'er Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Ba\u8217'arti Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the source for the two items omitted from the second list \u8212- the prohibition on burning {\i tamei} {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} and the prohibition (with attendant {\i malkot}) on eating it when {\i tamei}. A {\i baraita} is cited in which Rabbi Shimon expounds: {\i lo bi\u8217'arti mimennu betamei} \u8212- I did not consume it in a state of {\i tuma}. This covers two scenarios: {\i bein she-ani tamei v\u8217'hu tahor}, whether I was {\i tamei} and the {\i ma\u8217'aser} was {\i tahor}, or {\i bein she-ani tahor v\u8217'hu tamei}, whether I was {\i tahor} and the {\i ma\u8217'aser} itself had become {\i tamei}. The word {\i bi\u8217'arti} is understood to include both burning for fuel and eating. Therefore: if the {\i ma\u8217'aser} became {\i tamei}, one may not burn it for fuel and may not eat it. One must simply set it aside and allow it to decompose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then proceeds to address the prohibition on eating {\i ma\u8217'aser} when {\i tamei}, and at this point the {\i gemara} interrupts. The {\i baraita} poses the question {\i va\u8217'hiecha muzhar al achilato} \u8212- how do we know eating {\i tamei} {\i ma\u8217'aser} is prohibited? \u8212- and answers {\i eini yodea}, I do not know, before citing the {\i pasuk} as the source. The {\i gemara} interjects: what do you mean you don\u8217't know? The case where a person is {\i tamei} and the {\i ma\u8217'aser} is {\i tahor} is written explicitly in the Torah \u8212- {\i nefesh asher tiga bo vetame\u8217'ah ad ha\u8217'erev velo yokhal min hakodashim} \u8212- a {\i tamei} person may not eat {\i kodashim}, and {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} is clearly included in {\i kodashim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then clarifies the {\i baraita}\u8217's actual question: {\i tumat atzmo minalan} \u8212- what the {\i baraita} was asking about is not the case where the person is {\i tamei} but the {\i ma\u8217'aser} is {\i tahor}; that is well established. The question is: how do we know that one may not eat {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} that is itself {\i tamei}, when the person is {\i tahor}? This is a different and subtler question, since when an object becomes {\i tamei} it has lost its pristine state and one might have thought the prohibition is less severe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer comes from the {\i pasuk} {\i lo tuchal le\u8217'echol bish\u8217'arecha ma\u8217'aser deganecha} \u8212- you may not eat your {\i ma\u8217'aser} in your gates. This is then read in light of another verse: {\i bish\u8217'arecha tochelenu} \u8212- you shall eat it in your gates \u8212- which appears in the context of {\i kodashim} that became {\i pasul} and were redeemed. The Torah says that once redeemed, such {\i kodashim} may be eaten in your gates, {\i hatamei vehatahur yachdav}, by the {\i tamei} person and the {\i tahor} person together, just as one eats a deer or an ibex \u8212- animals that can never be {\i korbanot} and hence carry none of the restrictions of {\i kodashim}. The school of Rabbi Yishmael taught this explicitly: even a {\i tamei} and {\i tahor} person can eat this redeemed {\i kodshim} from the same plate without concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of the derivation is elegant. Eating \u8220"in your gates\u8221" in the Torah represents a mode of unrestricted consumption: outside Yerushalayim, without the limitations of {\i kedusha}, including the ability to eat even in a state of {\i tuma}. When the Torah says about {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} {\i lo tuchal le\u8217'echol bish\u8217'arecha}, it is not merely saying \u8220"don\u8217't eat it outside Yerushalayim.\u8221" It is also saying: do not eat your {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} in the manner of eating \u8220"in your gates\u8221" \u8212- do not eat it as you eat things that carry no {\i kedusha}, do not eat it {\i betumah}. Therefore {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} that has itself become {\i tamei} may not be eaten by a {\i tahor} person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Contrast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This rule \u8212- do not burn {\i tamei} {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} for fuel, do not eat it when it is {\i tamei} \u8212- stands in contrast to {\i terumah}. When {\i terumah} becomes {\i tamei}, the established practice is precisely to burn it and benefit from it as fuel. How do we know this? The {\i gemara} cites Rabbi Abahu citing Rabbi Yochanan, who derives it from {\i mimennu} in the verse {\i lo bi\u8217'arti mimennu betamei}: the word {\i mimennu} \u8212- \u8220"from it\u8221" \u8212- implies a limitation, indicating that while you cannot burn {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} when it is {\i tamei}, you can burn oil of {\i terumah} that has become {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 KV Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling raises an immediate difficulty. {\i Ma\u8217'aser sheni} is the lighter item, and yet one cannot burn it when it is {\i tamei}. {\i Terumah} is weightier \u8212- restricted to {\i kohanim}, carrying {\i mita} and {\i chomesh} \u8212- and yet one may burn it. Is this not backwards? Someone raises the {\i kal vachomer}: if {\i ma\u8217'aser}, which is the lighter item ({\i ma\u8217'aser hakal}), may not be burned when {\i tamei} because the Torah says {\i lo bi\u8217'arti mimennu betamei}, how much more so should {\i kodshim} \u8212- which is weightier \u8212- not be burnable when {\i tamei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counter KV\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if {\i kodshim} cannot be burned, then {\i terumah} should also be caught in the same {\i kal vachomer}: if {\i ma\u8217'aser} cannot be burned, {\i terumah} cannot either, since {\i terumah} is more weighty than {\i ma\u8217'aser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mimenu Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response: {\i haktiv mimennu} \u8212- the Torah writes {\i mimennu}, \u8220"from it,\u8221" which must be limiting something. The word implies that while you cannot burn {\i ma\u8217'aser} when {\i tamei}, something else can be burned. The question is then {\i uma ra\u8217'ita} \u8212- given that the {\i kal vachomer} would extend to everything more weighty than {\i ma\u8217'aser}, and given that {\i mimennu} must exclude something, what justifies excluding {\i terumah} and permitting its burning while leaving {\i kodshim} subject to the prohibition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weighting Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: it makes sense not to exclude {\i kodshim} from the prohibition because {\i kodshim} is the weightier item overall, and we do not extend leniencies to weightier items. {\i Terumah}, though weightier than {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, is lighter than {\i kodshim}, so it is {\i terumah} that receives the leniency of being burnable when {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodashim Weights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know {\i kodshim} is weightier than {\i terumah}? The {\i gemara} uses the mnemonic {\i pinkhas} \u8212- spelling out the stringencies of {\i kodshim}: {\i pigul} (improper intent during the {\i avodah} renders it forbidden), {\i nosar} (leaving it past its time), the fact that it is brought within the {\i Beit HaMikdash} itself, {\i meila} (misuse of {\i kodshim} is a distinct prohibition), {\i kares} (eating {\i tamei} {\i kodshim} incurs {\i kares}), and it is {\i assur le\u8217'onein} \u8212- forbidden to an {\i onen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Weights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then entertains the counterargument: {\i terumah} has its own stringencies. The mnemonic {\i machpaz}: {\i mita} \u8212- a non-{\i kohen} who eats {\i terumah} is liable to death at divine hands; {\i chomesh} \u8212- he must add a fifth if he ate it unintentionally; {\i ein lah pidyon} \u8212- {\i terumah} cannot be redeemed, whereas consecrated animals that acquire a {\i mum} can be redeemed; {\i asurah lezarim} \u8212- while some {\i korbanot} such as {\i shelamim} may be eaten by non-{\i kohanim}, {\i terumah} may never be eaten by a non-{\i kohen}. These are genuine stringencies of {\i terumah} that {\i kodshim} does not share.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kares Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension in two ways. First, quantitatively: {\i hanech nefishan} \u8212- the stringencies of {\i kodshim} are more numerous, six to four. Second, and more incisively: {\i kares adifa} \u8212- {\i kares} is a more severe punishment than {\i mita biyedei shamayim}. The fact that eating {\i tamei} {\i kodshim} incurs {\i kares}, while eating {\i terumah} incurs only {\i mita biyedei shamayim}, decisively tips the scales in favor of {\i kodshim} being weightier overall. Therefore, the leniency to burn when {\i tamei} is given to {\i terumah} \u8212- which is the intermediate item, less weighty than {\i kodshim} but more weighty than {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- and not to {\i kodshim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Exhaustive Lists (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi now offers a more economical solution to the earlier proof attempt. Recall that the entire discussion of the second list and its omissions was mobilized to show that the first list is non-exhaustive \u8212- that the absence of {\i arel} from the list of distinctions between {\i terumah}/{\i bikkurim} and {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} cannot be taken as proof that an {\i arel} may eat {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. Rav Ashi points out that one need not look to the second list at all. Even from the beginning of the first list \u8212- {\i mereisha} \u8212- one can see that things were left off. {\i Midelo katani venohagin bish\u8217'ar shenei shavua} \u8212- the {\i mishna} did not say that {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} apply in all years of the {\i shemita} cycle while {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} applies only in the third and sixth years. That is a major point of difference and it was not listed. {\i Ve\u8217'ein lahem pidyon} \u8212- nor did the {\i mishna} say that {\i terumah} and {\i bikkurim} cannot be redeemed, while {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} can be redeemed by transferring its {\i kedusha} to coins. Both of these are prominent distinctions between {\i terumah}/{\i bikkurim} and {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, and neither appears on the list. {\i Shema mina} \u8212- one learns from this, already from the beginning of the {\i mishna}, that the list is non-exhaustive. The entire detour through the second list, while illuminating, was unnecessary for this specific point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita on Arel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now brings a stronger challenge. {\i Ta shema} \u8212- come and hear. A {\i baraita} teaches that in the case of {\i nishtayeru tzitzin hame\u8217'akvin et hamilah} \u8212- pieces of flesh that should have been removed in the {\i brit mila} were left in place \u8212- the person is still considered an {\i arel}. And the {\i baraita} then enumerates what this person may not eat: {\i harei zeh eino ochel lo bitrumah velo bePesach velo bekodshim velo bema\u8217'aser}. He may not eat {\i terumah}, he may not eat {\i Pesach}, he may not eat {\i kodshim}, and he may not eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}. This is an apparently explicit source that an {\i arel} cannot eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}. The {\i gemara} asks: does this not mean {\i ma\u8217'aser} of grain, i.e., {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Behema\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers a first deflection: {\i ma\u8217'aser behemah} \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'aser} referred to is the animal tithe, which is a {\i korban}. When an animal is designated as {\i ma\u8217'aser behemah}, it has the status of {\i kodshim}. So the {\i baraita} is not adding a new category at all; it is simply another way of saying the {\i arel} cannot eat {\i kodshim}. The {\i gemara} makes this explicit: {\i ma\u8217'aser behemah hainu kodshim} \u8212- the animal tithe is {\i kodshim} and was already covered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Rishon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this explanation is strained. If {\i ma\u8217'aser behemah} is already subsumed under {\i kodshim}, why mention it separately? The {\i gemara} points out that this problem mirrors an analogous one in the {\i baraita} itself: {\i mi lo tenan Pesach vetani kodshim} \u8212- the {\i baraita} already mentions {\i Pesach} and also mentions {\i kodshim}. Isn\u8217't {\i Pesach} already subsumed under {\i kodshim}? The {\i gemara} answers that both are necessary: had the {\i baraita} mentioned only {\i Pesach}, one might have thought the prohibition on an {\i arel} applies only to {\i Pesach} specifically, where the {\i pasuk} states {\i kol arel lo yokhal bo}; had it mentioned only {\i kodshim}, one might have thought the word {\i kodshim} refers specifically to {\i Pesach}. Mentioning both clarifies that {\i kodshim} means all {\i korbanot} and that {\i Pesach} is merely one instance. But this analysis makes the redundancy of {\i ma\u8217'aser behemah} all the more glaring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore offers a second explanation: the {\i ma\u8217'aser} in the {\i baraita} refers to {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} according to the position of Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Meir holds the anomalous view that just as {\i terumah} is forbidden to non-{\i kohanim}, {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} is forbidden to non-{\i Leviyim}. Under this view, {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} carries a restriction analogous to that of {\i terumah}, confining its consumption to the {\i Levi} just as {\i terumah} is confined to the {\i kohen}. The {\i gemara} suggests that the {\i baraita} may be invoking this position: an {\i arel} cannot eat {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} according to Rabbi Meir, for the same reason he cannot eat {\i terumah} \u8212- these are items restricted to a particular class of people and the {\i arel}\u8217's status creates an additional disqualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inconclusive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is notably tentative. Even with the {\i baraita} in hand \u8212- which states explicitly that an {\i arel} may not eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- it has not been definitively established that an {\i arel} cannot eat {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. The {\i baraita} can be explained as referring either to {\i ma\u8217'aser behemah} (a form of {\i kodshim}) or to {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} according to Rabbi Meir\u8217's distinctive position that treats it similarly to {\i terumah}. Neither of these is {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. The original question \u8212- {\i arel mahu bema\u8217'aser} \u8212- thus remains unresolved, and the {\i gemara} leaves it so.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 74\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arel & Maaser Sheni\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Maasrot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question before the {\i gemara} is whether an {\i arel} \u8212- an uncircumcised man \u8212- is permitted to eat {\i maaser sheni}. A {\i braita} had already been cited stating that an {\i arel} is forbidden in {\i maaser}, and the {\i gemara} attempted to reinterpret this as referring to {\i maaser behema} \u8212- the tithe of animals \u8212- rather than the grain tithe. That reading was clearly strained. The {\i gemara} then fell back on a second escape: the {\i braita} might be referring to {\i maaser rishon} according to the position of Rabbi Meir, who holds that {\i maaser rishon} is forbidden to {\i zarim} and is thus treated analogously to {\i teruma}. This is admittedly a radical position of Rabbi Meir, but it is the vehicle the {\i gemara} uses to avoid conceding that an {\i arel} is forbidden in {\i maaser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya Bar Rav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: come and hear. Rabbi Chiya bar Rav of Difti teaches a {\i braita}: an {\i arel} is forbidden in two {\i maasrot} \u8212- {\i shtei maasrot}. The natural reading is that one of these is {\i maaser dagan}, the grain tithe, and the other is {\i maaser behema}, the animal tithe \u8212- the latter being {\i kodshim} and thus clearly forbidden. If so, the prohibition on the grain tithe establishes that an {\i arel} is forbidden in {\i maaser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Rishon Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects once more: here too, the reference is to {\i maaser rishon} and {\i maaser behema}, invoking Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. The {\i braita} still does not compel the conclusion that {\i maaser sheni} is forbidden to an {\i arel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Rishon Again\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same move is made a second time: whenever a {\i braita} appears to establish the prohibition of an {\i arel} in the grain tithe, the {\i gemara} reasserts that the reference is to {\i maaser rishon} according to Rabbi Meir. Each iteration of this defense holds off the proof but is clearly makeshift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onen Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a structurally elegant {\i braita} that compares three categories of persons \u8212- {\i onen}, {\i tvul yom}, and {\i mechusar kippurim} \u8212- against three categories of sanctity: {\i maaser} (which, as will become clear from the cases, can only mean {\i maaser sheni}), {\i teruma}, and {\i para aduma}. The pattern is a rotating one: each of the three persons is forbidden in exactly one of the three categories and permitted in the other two, and the forbidden category differs for each. This creates a kind of three-way matrix in which every combination is covered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onen Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\i onen} \u8212- a mourner before the burial of his close relative \u8212- is forbidden in {\i maaser}, as the verse states {\i lo achalti be\u8217'oni mimenu}, and permitted in {\i teruma} and {\i para aduma}. The permission to eat {\i teruma} was established earlier in the {\i sugya}. The permission to perform the {\i avoda} of the {\i para aduma} \u8212- including the burning and the sprinkling \u8212- follows from the fact that, although an {\i onen} cannot eat {\i kodshim}, the {\i para aduma} is not technically a {\i korban}; it belongs to the category of {\i bedek habayit} and is not treated as a sacrificial offering in the usual sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Onen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi notes parenthetically that the permission of an {\i onen} to perform the {\i para aduma} follows by {\i kal vachomer}: if a {\i tvul yom} can do so, certainly an {\i onen} can. Tosafot pushes back on the logic of this inference: it does not always follow that leniency in one area implies leniency in another, given that the {\i onen} is actually more restricted than the {\i tvul yom} regarding {\i maaser}, while the {\i tvul yom} is more restricted than the {\i onen} regarding {\i teruma}. Tosafot therefore clarifies that the only prohibition on an {\i onen} in the context of {\i maaser sheni} is eating it \u8212- {\i le\u8217'achilah} \u8212- whereas touching {\i maaser sheni} is permitted for both; the {\i onen}\u8217's permission to perform the {\i para aduma} speaks to physical involvement, not consumption, and the two categories therefore cannot be simply compared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onen Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why should an {\i onen} be more restricted regarding {\i maaser sheni} than {\i teruma}, given that {\i teruma} is generally the more stringent category? The answer lies in the character of {\i maaser sheni}: it must be brought to Jerusalem and consumed there, making it structurally analogous to a {\i korban}. Just as an {\i onen} is forbidden in a {\i korban}, he is forbidden in {\i maaser sheni}. {\i Teruma}, by contrast \u8212- though possessing greater {\i kedusha} \u8212- is eaten anywhere, and thus lacks the {\i korban}-like quality that triggers the {\i onen}\u8217's restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tvul Yom Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tvul yom} \u8212- one who has immersed in a {\i mikvah} but for whom nightfall has not yet arrived \u8212- is forbidden in {\i teruma}, because a {\i tvul yom} has the status of a {\i sheni} and would render {\i teruma} a {\i shlishi}, disqualifying it. He is, however, permitted to perform the {\i avoda} of the {\i para aduma} \u8212- this is in fact the famous {\i halakha} that the {\i para aduma} was specifically performed by a {\i tvul yom}, demonstrating that {\i tahor min hakhlal de\u8217'ika tamei}. He may also eat {\i maaser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechusar Kippurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i mechusar kippurim} \u8212- one who has immersed and for whom nightfall has passed, but who has yet to bring the required {\i korban} \u8212- represents a weaker degree of residual impurity than the {\i tvul yom}. He is forbidden in {\i para aduma} but permitted in {\i teruma} and {\i maaser sheni}. This requires explanation: if the {\i tvul yom} \u8212- who is more restricted than the {\i mechusar kippurim} in most respects \u8212- can perform the {\i para aduma}, why can the {\i mechusar kippurim} not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tvul Yom Para\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is that the {\i tvul yom} who may perform the {\i para aduma} is one whose remaining purification process consists entirely of waiting \u8212- no additional action, no {\i korban}, is pending. For instance, someone who touched a carcass, immersed, and need only wait for nightfall: that person may perform the {\i para aduma}. But a {\i tvul yom} who still has a {\i korban} to bring \u8212- someone in a longer purification process such as a {\i zavah} \u8212- cannot perform the {\i para aduma} even after immersion, and cannot do so even after nightfall until the {\i korban} is brought. When we say the {\i tvul yom} can perform the {\i para aduma}, we mean specifically the person whose entire remaining process will resolve on its own within a matter of hours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i gemara} turns to use this {\i braita} as a proof regarding the {\i arel}. If it were true that an {\i arel} can eat {\i maaser sheni}, the {\i braita}\u8217's rotating pattern would have included a fourth configuration: an {\i arel} is forbidden in {\i teruma} \u8212- which we know \u8212- permitted in {\i para aduma} \u8212- since there is no reason to exclude him \u8212- and permitted in {\i maaser sheni}. This configuration would have fit perfectly into the pattern being elaborated, yet it is conspicuously absent. The omission implies that an {\i arel} cannot be described as forbidden in {\i teruma} but permitted in {\i maaser sheni}, because in fact he is forbidden in {\i maaser sheni} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects once more: this {\i braita} might reflect the position of Rabbi Akiva, who \u8212- as will be elaborated \u8212- treats an {\i arel} as equivalent to a {\i tamei} person in all matters where personal status creates a prohibition. According to Rabbi Akiva, an {\i arel} would also be forbidden in {\i para aduma}, since the verse states {\i ve\u8217'asaf ish tahor}, and the {\i arel} is equated to one who is not {\i tahor}. If so, the configuration of {\i arel} being permitted in {\i para aduma} and {\i maaser sheni} but forbidden only in {\i teruma} was never available to the {\i braita} \u8212- and its absence therefore proves nothing about {\i maaser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva Arel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ish Ish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position now brought into focus, the {\i gemara} cites his derivation explicitly: {\i Rabbi Akiva omer, ish ish le\u8217'rabos es ha\u8217'arel} \u8212- the doubled language of {\i ish ish} in the verse about the {\i tamei} person who may not eat {\i teruma} is used by Rabbi Akiva to include the {\i arel} in that prohibition. The broader implication drawn from this is that wherever a {\i tamei} person is forbidden due to his personal status \u8212- not merely because of the risk of contaminating food, but because of who he is \u8212- an {\i arel} is likewise forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef HaBavli\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: who is the {\i tanna} who disagrees with Rabbi Akiva and holds that an {\i arel} is not equivalent to a {\i tamei}? The answer is the {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i braita} of Rabbi Yosef haBavli.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Para Srifa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevant {\i braita} states: {\i sreifat onen u\u8217'mechusar kippurim kesheira} \u8212- the {\i tanna kamma} holds that both the {\i onen} and the {\i mechusar kippurim} are valid performers of the burning of the {\i para aduma}. Rabbi Yosef haBavli, however, distinguishes: an {\i onen} is valid ({\i kesheira}), but a {\i mechusar kippurim} is invalid ({\i pesulah}). Both positions concern which residual-impurity categories are excluded from the {\i para aduma} by the verse\u8217's requirement of {\i ish tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi infers from this debate a parallel controversy about the {\i arel}: just as the {\i tannaim} disagree about whether a {\i mechusar kippurim} may perform the {\i para aduma}, so too they would disagree about whether an {\i arel} \u8212- given his contested equivalence to a {\i tamei} \u8212- may perform it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot objects to Rashi\u8217's inference on logical grounds: the dispute over {\i mechusar kippurim} concerns the extent of his residual impurity, while the dispute over the {\i arel} concerns a categorically different question \u8212- whether he is classified as {\i tamei} at all. These are independent considerations, and one does not follow from the other. Indeed, Tosafot appears to have had a different text of the {\i braita} that explicitly mentioned {\i arel} rather than leaving it to inference by analogy. Our standard text does not mention {\i arel} directly, and Rashi\u8217's inference by analogy is what fills the gap \u8212- but it is an inference Tosafot explicitly rejects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezeira Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mimenu Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the series of {\i braitot}, Rabbi Yitzchak independently establishes that an {\i arel} is forbidden in {\i maaser}. He derives this through a {\i gezeirah shavah} on the word {\i mimenu}: in {\i maaser} the verse states {\i lo achalti be\u8217'oni mimenu}, and in {\i pesach} the Torah states {\i al tochlu mimenu na} and {\i lo totiru mimenu ad boker}. Just as {\i mimenu} at {\i pesach} implies an {\i arel} is forbidden \u8212- since he may not eat the {\i korban pesach} \u8212- so too {\i mimenu} at {\i maaser} implies an {\i arel} is forbidden there as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weight Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a challenge: if the word is not free \u8212- {\i mufneh} \u8212- at both ends of the {\i gezeirah shavah}, the analogy is vulnerable to a logical rebuttal. In this case, the rebuttal is compelling: {\i pesach} is weightier than {\i maaser}, since one is liable for {\i pesach} meat on account of {\i pigul}, {\i notar}, and {\i tumah} \u8212- none of which apply to {\i maaser sheni}. If the {\i gezeirah shavah} is not ironclad \u8212- because the word is not free \u8212- one could argue that the severity of {\i pesach} accounts for the restriction and it cannot be extended to the lighter {\i maaser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Free Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution lies in establishing that the word {\i mimenu} appears three times at {\i pesach}, and at least one of those appearances is free \u8212- not required by the grammatical or legal demands of the verse. It is the {\i mufneh} \u8212- free \u8212- word that enables a valid {\i gezeirah shavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tochlu Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first occurrence of {\i mimenu} at {\i pesach} is in the phrase {\i al tochlu mimenu na} \u8212- do not eat of it raw. This is grammatically required; the verse cannot function without {\i mimenu} and it therefore does no independent legal work beyond what the verse demands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Free for Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second occurrence is the word free to anchor the {\i gezeirah shavah}, available precisely because the first was required by plain grammar and the third is dealt with separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Notar Clause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third occurrence of {\i mimenu} at {\i pesach} appears in the clause {\i ve\u8217'hanotar mimenu ad boker ba\u8217'esh tisrofu} \u8212- what remains of it until morning you shall burn with fire. The question is whether this {\i mimenu} is itself doing independent legal work, or whether it is merely incidental to the larger phrase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Notar Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a dispute about what this third clause accomplishes. One view holds that the phrase {\i ba\u8217'esh tisrofu} following {\i lo totiru mimenu} creates a {\i mitzvat asei} linked to the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} of not leaving the {\i pesach} overnight \u8212- making the prohibition {\i nitak la\u8217'asei} \u8212- and thus the word {\i mimenu} was simply carried along for linguistic flow, doing no independent work. Another view holds that the purpose of this verse is to establish that if {\i notar} remains, one does not burn it on the morning of the 15th of Nisan \u8212- which is {\i yom tov} \u8212- but waits for the morning of the 16th. Either way, the {\i mimenu} in this third clause is not doing independent legal work; it was written along with the phrase that was needed, whether for {\i nitak la\u8217'asei} or for the teaching about a second morning of burning. The conclusion is the same: the {\i mimenu} at the end is written only because the larger phrase was needed, and is therefore genuinely free, available to serve as one node of the {\i gezeirah shavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Mimenu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the three appearances of {\i mimenu} in the {\i maaser} context: {\i lo vi\u8217'arti mimenu be\u8217'oni}, {\i velo vi\u8217'arti mimenu be\u8217'tamei}, and {\i velo natati mimenu le\u8217'met}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Grammar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i mimenu} in the {\i maaser vidui} passage is grammatically required \u8212- it is the natural mode of expression and does no more than what the text demands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbahu Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i mimenu} \u8212- {\i velo vi\u8217'arti mimenu be\u8217'tamei} \u8212- is the source for the teaching of Rabbi Abbahu in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: this prohibition of burning {\i tamei maaser sheni} applies specifically to {\i maaser sheni}, whereas {\i teruma} that has become {\i tamei} may indeed be burned. The {\i mimenu} at {\i maaser} thus derives a specific law about {\i maaser sheni}, and is not free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anointing Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i mimenu} \u8212- {\i velo natati mimenu le\u8217'met} \u8212- is the source for Reish Lakish\u8217's teaching, cited in the name of Rabbi Samya: how do we know that {\i maaser sheni} which has become {\i tamei} may be used for {\i sichah} \u8212- anointing the body \u8212- even though it may not be eaten? From the word {\i mimenu}: I did not give {\i of it} to the dead. The inference is that what I did not do for the dead \u8212- anoint them \u8212- I may do for the living. What is something equally relevant to both the living and the dead? Anointing. The living person may therefore use {\i tamei maaser sheni} for {\i sichah}. This is a striking {\i halakha}: even though you cannot burn {\i tamei maaser sheni} for fuel or light, you may anoint your body with it, because {\i sichah} is a form of bodily consumption that does not constitute eating or drinking and therefore remains legitimate even in a state of {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sichah Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this derivation. Mar Zutra asks: perhaps the verse means that one should not use the proceeds of selling the {\i maaser sheni} to buy a coffin or shrouds for the dead \u8212- implying that one may sell it and buy clothes or other items for the living? Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua responds: {\i mimenu migufo} \u8212- {\i of it} means direct use of the item itself. You do not make shrouds from produce; therefore, the verse must be speaking of direct bodily application, not indirect commercial use. Rav Ashi adds a parallel argument: {\i lo natati} is analogous to {\i lo achalti} \u8212- just as eating is a direct consumption of the object itself, so too the giving spoken of here must be a direct use of the {\i maaser sheni} itself on the body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Side Free\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analysis reveals an asymmetry: at {\i pesach}, one of the three {\i mimenu} words is genuinely free. At {\i maaser}, however, all three words are occupied \u8212- none is free. The {\i gezeirah shavah} therefore has only one free node, at {\i pesach}, and is technically a {\i gezeirah shavah mitzad echad} \u8212- free on only one side. According to the view that such a one-sided {\i gezeirah shavah} can be challenged, the weight-of-{\i pesach} argument remains a valid rebuttal and the proof is not ironclad.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Free Sides\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the problem by drawing on a teaching of Rav Nachman citing Rabbah bar Avuha. If the teaching that {\i tamei teruma} may be burned \u8212- which Rabbi Abbahu derived from the second {\i maaser mimenu} \u8212- can actually be derived from a different source entirely, then that second {\i mimenu} is genuinely free after all, and we have {\i mimenu} free on both sides of the {\i gezeirah shavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teruma Burning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative source is Rav Nachman\u8217's teaching in the name of Rabbah bar Avuha: the verse states {\i va\u8217'ani hineh natati lecha et mishmeret terumotai} \u8212- I have given you the guardianship of my {\i terumot}, in the plural. The Torah speaks of two {\i terumot} \u8212- one {\i tehorah} and one {\i teme\u8217'ah}. Even {\i tamei teruma} has a use: {\i lehasikah tachat tavshilcha} \u8212- you may burn it to fuel your cooking fire. Since the permission to burn {\i tamei teruma} can be derived from this verse about {\i teruma} directly, the second {\i mimenu} at {\i maaser} \u8212- which Rabbah bar Avuha used for the same teaching \u8212- is freed from that obligation. With the second {\i maaser mimenu} now available as a free word, both sides of the {\i gezeirah shavah} are free, and the analogy between {\i pesach} and {\i maaser sheni} regarding the {\i arel} is established on firm ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion & Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary Arel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's sustained treatment of the {\i arel} and {\i maaser sheni} draws to a close. Multiple {\i braitot} \u8212- each of which the {\i gemara} attempted to neutralize through various reinterpretations \u8212- consistently pointed toward the same conclusion: an {\i arel} is forbidden in {\i maaser sheni}. Rabbi Yitzchak then provided an independent derivation via {\i gezeirah shavah} linking {\i pesach} and {\i maaser} through the word {\i mimenu}, and Abaye secured both sides of that {\i gezeirah shavah} by showing that the relevant {\i maaser} word is genuinely free. The overall conclusion is that an {\i arel} is forbidden in {\i pesach}, forbidden in {\i teruma}, and likewise forbidden in {\i maaser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i arel} question resolved, the {\i mishnah} moves on to what would seem to be the more self-evident clause: {\i ve\u8217'chol ha\u8217'tmei\u8217'im} \u8212- all {\i tamei} persons are likewise forbidden in {\i teruma}. This transition shifts the discussion from a category defined by a physical status ({\i arel}) to the broader world of ritual impurity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tamei Teruma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: where is the source that all {\i tamei} persons are forbidden in {\i teruma}? Rabbi Yochanan, citing Rabbi Yishmael, identifies the verse: {\i ish ish mi\u8217'zera aharon ve\u8217'hu tzaru\u8217'a o zav ba\u8217'kodashim lo yochal ad asher yithar} \u8212- any man of the seed of Aharon who is afflicted with {\i tzara\u8217'at} or is a {\i zav} shall not eat of the holy things until he becomes {\i tahor}. The verse continues with additional categories of {\i tamei} persons. The operative question is: what do {\i kodashim} \u8212- holy things \u8212- refer to here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zera Aharon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key to the identification lies in the phrase {\i mi\u8217'zera aharon} \u8212- of the seed of Aharon. The {\i gemara} asks: what holy thing is shared equally by all members of the seed of Aharon? {\i Kodshei kodashim} cannot be the answer, because those may only be eaten by male {\i kohanim} \u8212- women of priestly lineage cannot eat them. {\i Teruma}, on the other hand, may be eaten by both male and female members of a {\i kohen}\u8217's household. The {\i gemara} therefore identifies {\i kodashim} in this verse as {\i teruma}: {\i havei omer zo terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A possible alternative is raised: perhaps the reference is to {\i chazeh ve\u8217'shok} \u8212- the breast and thigh of the {\i shelamim} offering, which are {\i kodshim kalim} that women of priestly families may eat. The {\i gemara} rejects this: a {\i bat kohen} who marries an {\i Yisrael} and is subsequently widowed without children returns to eating {\i teruma}, but cannot return to eating {\i kodshim kalim} of her father\u8217's house \u8212- this is derived from a separate verse. So {\i kodshim kalim} is not equally accessible to all {\i zera aharon} once they enter various marital situations. {\i Teruma} remains the most universally shared form of {\i kodesh} for all of {\i zera aharon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further argue that even {\i teruma} is not truly universal: a {\i chalalah} \u8212- a woman born of a disqualified priestly union \u8212- cannot eat {\i teruma}. The {\i gemara} resolves this: a {\i chalalah} is not considered {\i zera aharon} in the relevant halachic sense. Although she is obviously his biological descendant and carries all obligations of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}, her lineage is no longer considered {\i meyuchas} in the priestly sense; a male in her position would not be a {\i kohen}. The phrase {\i zera aharon} therefore points precisely to {\i teruma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ad Asher Yithar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse states these {\i tamei} persons may not eat {\i kodashim} \u8212- now established as {\i teruma} \u8212- {\i ad asher yithar}, until they become {\i tahor}. A question arises: how do we know this phrase means specifically until nightfall following immersion \u8212- which would exclude a {\i tvul yom} but permit a {\i mechusar kippurim}? Perhaps {\i ad asher yithar} means until one has completed the entire purification process, including bringing a {\i korban}? If so, even a {\i mechusar kippurim} would be forbidden in {\i teruma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites the school of Rabbi Yishmael: the verse is speaking specifically of a {\i zav ba\u8217'al shtei re\u8217'iyot} \u8212- a {\i zav} who experienced only two sightings \u8212- and a {\i metzora musgar} \u8212- a leper in the confined stage but not the declared stage. These two cases are cited as parallel to the case of one who becomes {\i tamei} through contact with a corpse ({\i tmei nefesh}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sunset Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is that one who is {\i tamei} through corpse contact does not bring a {\i korban} at all. Since the verse draws these cases together, when we read {\i ad asher yithar} in this context, we read it as applicable to persons who likewise are not in a {\i korban}-bearing process. A {\i zav} with only two sightings does not bring a {\i korban}; a {\i metzora musgar} does not bring a {\i korban}. For such people, {\i tahor} means: go to the {\i mikvah} and wait for nightfall. Hence {\i ad asher yithar} signifies sunset alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resists the inference: perhaps this means only that those without a {\i korban} obligation need wait only for nightfall \u8212- but those who are obligated to bring a {\i korban} must wait until they have done so, even for {\i teruma}. This is a reasonable position and is not automatically refuted by the reading of the verse. The {\i gemara} presses: what then is the source that permits a {\i mechusar kippurim} to eat {\i teruma} the night before bringing the {\i korban}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Hierarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} brings the {\i mishna}: one who immerses and emerges may eat {\i maaser}; once the sun sets ({\i he\u8217'erev shemesh}), one may eat {\i teruma}; once the {\i korban} is brought, one may eat {\i kodshim}. This neatly establishes three ascending levels of purity required for three ascending levels of sanctity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechusar Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: where is the proof that specifically the {\i mechusar kippurim} \u8212- one who still needs to bring a {\i korban} \u8212- is permitted in {\i teruma} the night before? The source for this precise ruling requires its own derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Psukim (IV)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, citing Rav Chisda, explains that three verses underlie this three-tier hierarchy, each corresponding to one level of sanctity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikvah Pesach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first verse states {\i lo yochal min ha\u8217'kodashim ki im rachatz besaro ba\u8217'mayim} \u8212- he shall not eat of the holy things unless he has bathed his flesh in water. This establishes that immersion in a {\i mikvah} alone is sufficient to permit eating something. This is the lightest level, corresponding to {\i maaser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Kodshim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third verse states {\i ve\u8217'chipper aleiha ha\u8217'kohen ve\u8217'tahera} \u8212- the {\i kohen} shall atone for her and she shall be {\i tahor}. This establishes that bringing a {\i korban} is required to achieve the fullest {\i tahara}, corresponding to {\i kodshim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sunset Teruma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second verse \u8212- {\i uva ha\u8217'shemesh ve\u8217'taher ve\u8217'achar yochal min ha\u8217'kodashim} \u8212- establishes that sunset marks the purity threshold for the intermediate level, corresponding to {\i teruma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teruma Weight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know {\i teruma} is heavier than {\i maaser} and lighter than {\i kodshim}? A mnemonic is employed: {\i mechpaz}. {\i Mem} stands for {\i mita bidei shamayim} \u8212- one who eats {\i teruma} in a state of impurity incurs death at the hands of Heaven; {\i chet} is {\i chomesh} \u8212- one who eats {\i teruma} accidentally must add a fifth; {\i peh} is the absence of {\i pidyon} \u8212- {\i teruma} has no redemption, whereas {\i maaser sheni} does; {\i zayin} is {\i zar} \u8212- {\i teruma} is forbidden to non-{\i kohanim}. Against this, one might argue that {\i maaser} is weightier, represented by the mnemonic {\i hadastav}: {\i hei} is {\i hava\u8217'as makom} \u8212- {\i maaser sheni} must be brought to Jerusalem; {\i dalet} is {\i vidui} \u8212- a declaration is required; {\i samech} represents that an {\i onen} is forbidden in it; {\i tet} is {\i tumah} \u8212- {\i tamei maaser sheni} may not be burned for benefit; and {\i bet} is {\i biur} \u8212- one must eliminate {\i maaser} from the house every three years. The {\i gemara} resolves the question: {\i mita bidei shamayim} tips the balance \u8212- the consequence of death at the hands of Heaven makes {\i teruma} demonstrably weightier. Rava adds that even without invoking {\i mita}, the first verse \u8212- which speaks of {\i nefesh} \u8212- itself points to {\i maaser} as the lightest category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nefesh Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse that requires only immersion reads: {\i nefesh asher tiga bo ve\u8217'tame\u8217'ah, ki im rachatz besaro ba\u8217'mayim}. The word {\i nefesh} \u8212- person \u8212- implies something available to every person. {\i Teruma} is restricted to {\i kohanim} and their households; it is not {\i shaveh bechol nefesh} \u8212- equal for all people. The only form of {\i kodesh} that all people may eat is {\i maaser sheni}. Therefore the verse permitting eating after immersion alone must be speaking of {\i maaser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yoledet Case (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yoledet Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remaining question \u8212- whether a {\i mechusar kippurim} who is obligated to bring a {\i korban} may nevertheless eat {\i teruma} before bringing it \u8212- is answered through the case of a {\i yoledet}, a woman who has given birth. Her purification process spans forty days for a boy (and eighty for a girl). At the end of seven days she immerses in the {\i mikvah}. From that point, any blood she produces is {\i dam tohar} and does not render her {\i tamei}. However, she is not fully {\i tehorah} until she brings her {\i korban} on day forty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Long Tvul Yom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After her immersion on day seven, she enters a prolonged {\i tvul yom} status \u8212- she has immersed but her effective nightfall does not arrive until the night before day forty. During these thirty-three intermediate days she is called a {\i tvulas yom aroch} \u8212- a {\i tvul yom} of extended duration. In that period she may eat {\i maaser} but not {\i teruma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Night Before\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the night before day forty \u8212- the evening preceding the morning on which she brings her {\i korban} \u8212- she transitions to {\i mechusar kippurim} status. She is now permitted to eat {\i teruma}, even though she has not yet brought her {\i korban}. The following morning, when she brings the {\i korban}, she may eat {\i kodshim} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Psukim (IV) (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodshim Weight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kodshim} is weightier than {\i teruma}. The mnemonic is {\i pinkakas}: {\i peh} is {\i pigul}, {\i nun} is {\i notar}, {\i kaf} is {\i karet}, {\i ayin} is {\i meila}, and {\i samech} is the general prohibition of {\i assur}. None of these \u8212- with the exception of some overlap \u8212- apply to {\i teruma}, which instead carries {\i mita bidei shamayim} rather than {\i karet}. While one might object that {\i teruma}\u8217's features from {\i mechpaz} are more numerous, the {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is that the {\i kodshim} characteristics as a collection outweigh those of {\i teruma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yoledet Case (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milos Yamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye demonstrates the two-stage purity of the {\i yoledet} from two verses. The first verse states {\i ad milos yemei tahara} \u8212- until the completion of the days of purity. This verse establishes that once the days of purity are complete \u8212- that is, when nightfall arrives on the night before day forty \u8212- she is in some sense {\i tehorah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Psukim (IV) (III)\u8230 ?\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba offers an alternative derivation: even without counting features, the language of the verse {\i ve\u8217'chipper aleiha ha\u8217'kohen ve\u8217'tahera} itself establishes that {\i kodshim} is weightier. The language of {\i kapparah} \u8212- atonement \u8212- is uniquely associated with {\i korban}-bearing, and the fact that the verse uses this formulation to mark the point of {\i tahara} for {\i kodshim} demonstrates that {\i kodshim} occupies a categorically higher level of sanctity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yoledet Case (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vechiper\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second verse states {\i ve\u8217'chipper aleiha ha\u8217'kohen ve\u8217'tahera} \u8212- after the {\i kohen} brings the atonement-{\i korban} for her, she becomes {\i tehorah} for {\i kodshim}. This verse marks the moment of full {\i tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two verses therefore govern the {\i yoledet}\u8217's purity: the first \u8212- {\i ad milos yemei tahara} \u8212- applies to {\i teruma}, marking the night before the {\i korban} as her point of {\i tahara} for purposes of eating {\i teruma}; the second \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'chipper aleiha} \u8212- applies to {\i kodshim}, marking the bringing of the {\i korban} as her point of {\i tahara} for eating sanctified offerings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this we draw the critical inference: a woman who is {\i mechusar kippurim} \u8212- who still has a pending {\i korban} obligation \u8212- is nonetheless {\i tahor} for {\i teruma} from the night before the {\i korban} is brought. This is the halachic proof that a {\i mechusar kippurim} is not forbidden in {\i teruma} even when a {\i korban} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Psukim (IV) (III)\u8230 ?\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second verse\u8217's formulation \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'chipper aleiha ha\u8217'kohen ve\u8217'tahera}, signaling that she becomes {\i tehorah} only through the {\i korban} \u8212- implies that before the {\i korban} is brought, she retains a personal status of {\i teme\u8217'ah} with respect to {\i kodshim}. The verse {\i ve\u8217'habasar asher yiga bechol tamei lo ye\u8217'achel} is explicit: one with a personal {\i tamei} status may not eat the meat of {\i kodshim}. Therefore, even after nightfall and even after becoming permitted in {\i teruma}, she remains forbidden in {\i kodshim} until the {\i korban} is brought. It would be untenable to say that one still considered {\i teme\u8217'ah} in any degree could eat {\i kodshim} \u8212- the prohibition is categorical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sheshet Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Priestly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet, son of Rav Idi, raises a powerful challenge against the entire framework. He notes that a {\i braita} on the laws of {\i yoledet} cites the verse {\i daber el bnei Yisrael}, and asks: I know only that Jewish women by birth are subject to {\i tumas leida} \u8212- how do I know a {\i giueres} and a {\i shifcha meshucheres} are also subject to it? The answer given in the {\i braita} is that the verse {\i isha ki tazria} \u8212- when a woman conceives \u8212- comes to include any woman. The {\i braita} therefore explicitly establishes that the {\i tahara} laws of the {\i yoledet} apply even to a {\i giueres} and a freed slave woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet presses: if the verses we have been using to derive the hierarchy of {\i maaser}, {\i teruma}, and {\i kodshim} are speaking about {\i teruma} \u8212- as the {\i gemara} has concluded \u8212- how can they apply to a {\i giueres} or a {\i shifcha meshucheres}? These women cannot eat {\i teruma}. Paradoxically, a {\i giueres} may eat {\i shelamim} \u8212- she can eat {\i kodshim kalim} if she wishes, as a full member of the Jewish people \u8212- but she has no access to {\i teruma}, which is restricted to priestly households. If the verses regarding her purity are about {\i teruma}, the {\i braita}\u8217's inclusion of a {\i giueres} is incoherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Segments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds that the verse operates in distinct segments \u8212- {\i kra milei milei kachashiv} \u8212- different parts of the verse address different matters and different audiences. It is not the case that every clause of the passage speaks to every person mentioned. The {\i braita}\u8217's inclusion of a {\i giueres} establishes only that she is subject to {\i tumas leida} as such, not that every specific law derived from the passage applies to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inclusion Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tahara} laws regarding {\i teruma} \u8212- derived from the same verses \u8212- continue to apply to {\i kohen} women who are the primary subjects of the passage. The fact that the {\i braita} also applies {\i tumas leida} to a {\i giueres} does not transform the purity thresholds for {\i teruma} into laws for non-priestly women. The two teachings coexist within the same passage without conflict: one clause teaches universality of {\i tumas leida}, while another teaches the specific {\i teruma}-based purity thresholds applicable to those who can eat {\i teruma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teruma Purity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks why three separate verses are required to establish the {\i teruma} hierarchy. Could we not have derived everything from one or two?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Process Req\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first verse \u8212- {\i ad asher yithar} \u8212- establishes that there is some purification process required before eating {\i teruma}. Without it, we would not know that any formal {\i tahara} step is necessary at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sunset Spec\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second verse \u8212- {\i uva ha\u8217'shemesh ve\u8217'taher} \u8212- specifies that the relevant moment of {\i tahara} is sunset. Without this verse, knowing only that {\i tahara} is required, we would not know when that threshold is crossed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even with both of these verses, an ambiguity would remain: perhaps the sunset rule applies only when no {\i korban} is pending, but for a {\i bar kapparah} \u8212- one who must bring a {\i korban} \u8212- perhaps the requirement extends until the {\i korban} is brought, even for {\i teruma}. Without a third verse, this question would remain unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yoledet Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third verse \u8212- the {\i yoledet} passage with {\i ad milos yemei tahara} \u8212- resolves this ambiguity. It demonstrates that even a woman in the {\i korban}-bearing process becomes {\i tehorah} for {\i teruma} once nightfall arrives before the day of her offering. The {\i mechusar kippurim} status, even when a {\i korban} is required, does not extend the {\i teruma} restriction beyond nightfall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Yoledet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had we had only the {\i yoledet} verse \u8212- {\i ad milos} \u8212- without the intermediate sunset verse, we might have concluded that the completion of the purification {\i days} is the determinative factor, and that immersion in a {\i mikvah} alone, without waiting for nightfall, would suffice. It is the second verse ({\i uva ha\u8217'shemesh ve\u8217'taher}) that corrects this, insisting that nightfall is necessary. The {\i yoledet} verse alone would have been insufficient to teach that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Together, the three verses establish the complete framework: {\i ad asher yithar} teaches that a purification process is required; {\i uva ha\u8217'shemesh ve\u8217'taher} specifies that nightfall is the critical moment; and the {\i yoledet} verses demonstrate that even in the presence of a pending {\i korban} obligation, nightfall suffices to permit {\i teruma}, while full {\i tahara} for {\i kodshim} awaits the bringing of the {\i korban} itself. This three-verse architecture corresponds precisely to the three-tier hierarchy of {\i maaser}, {\i teruma}, and {\i kodshim} \u8212- each requiring a progressively more complete form of {\i tahara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 75\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mechussar Kipurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Mechussar Kipurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at approximately ten lines down on 75a, at the words {\i asher yithar}. We are completing the discussion of how we know that a {\i mechussar kipurim} \u8212- one who has undergone immersion and waited until nightfall but has not yet brought the required {\i korban} \u8212- may eat {\i terumah} but may not eat {\i kodshim}. The preceding analysis established three progressive stages of purification, derived primarily from the case of the {\i yoledes}: after immersion in the {\i mikvah} one may eat {\i maaser}; after nightfall one may eat {\i terumah}; and only after bringing one\u8217's {\i korban} does one attain full {\i tahara} permitting the eating of {\i kodshim}. A key point in learning from the {\i yoledes} was demonstrating that even a person still in the process of completing the {\i korban} obligation is already permitted to eat {\i terumah} once nightfall has passed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dissenting Tana View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses an alternative reading. The {\i tana de-bei Rabbi Yishmael} had read the verse {\i ad asher yithar} as referring to a {\i zav} who had seen a flow only twice and a {\i metzora} who was at the final stage of purification \u8212- individuals who do not require a {\i korban} \u8212- so that the phrase means only \u8220"until nightfall.\u8221" But the dissenting {\i tana} reads the verse as referring to a classic {\i zav} with three episodes of flux ({\i zav ba\u8217'al gimel re\u8217'iyot}) and a fully quarantined {\i metzora muchlat}, who do require a {\i korban}. According to this view, {\i ad asher yithar} means \u8220"until he brings his {\i kapparah},\u8221" and the prohibition against eating {\i kodshim} ({\i u-vakodshim lo yokhal}) refers to actual {\i korbanot} \u8212- meaning one may not eat one\u8217's own sacrificial portions until one has completed the atonement process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Pesukim Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question: according to the dissenting {\i tana}, why do we need two verses \u8212- the verse in Leviticus governing the {\i zav} and {\i metzora}, and the verse by the {\i yoledes} ({\i ve-chiper alav ha-kohen ve-taherah}) \u8212- both teaching that one may eat {\i korbanot} only after bringing the {\i korban}? The {\i gemara} answers that both are necessary. Had the Torah written only the verse regarding the {\i yoledes}, one might have thought the requirement is specific to her case because her {\i tum\u8217'a} is so extensive \u8212- spanning forty days for a boy and eighty for a girl \u8212- and therefore one might reason more leniently about the {\i zav} whose process is shorter. Conversely, had the Torah written only the verse regarding the {\i zav}, one might have said that his {\i tum\u8217'a} is strict precisely because it admits of no exceptions ({\i lo hutchal mikelalo}), whereas the {\i yoledes}, whose flow during the thirty-three or sixty-six days of {\i dam tohar} does not increase her {\i tum\u8217'a}, might be treated more leniently even before bringing her {\i korban}. Both verses are therefore needed, and in either case the conclusion is the same: a {\i mechussar kipurim} may eat {\i terumah} but not {\i kodshim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tvul Yom & Contact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels & Tvul Yom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now cites the verse {\i bamayim yuva ve-tamei ad ha-erev ve-taher}, which appears in the context of non-kosher animals and their carcasses, and which addresses the case of a vessel that has become {\i tamei} through contact with such a carcass. The verse prescribes that the vessel be immersed in water and remains {\i tamei} until nightfall \u8212- the same principle of {\i tvul yom} applies to vessels. One might ask: what is the {\i chidush} of this verse if the principle of {\i tvul yom} is already known? The answer points to something deeper than the application to vessels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Zeira\u8217's Insight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira explains: the verse comes to teach us the law of {\i negi\u8217'ah} \u8212- contact. Until now the entire discussion has been framed in terms of eating: after immersion, when may one eat {\i maaser}? After nightfall, when may one eat {\i terumah}? But the question of what a {\i tvul yom} may touch is a separate matter entirely. A {\i tvul yom} holds the status of a {\i sheini le-tum\u8217'a}. A {\i sheini} does not transmit {\i tum\u8217'a} to {\i chullin} or {\i maaser}, but does render {\i terumah tamei} by contact. The verse, by speaking of the vessel \u8212- which cannot eat anything \u8212- teaches that the category of {\i tvul yom} is a problem not only for eating {\i terumah} but for coming into contact with {\i terumah}. These are not the same prohibition, and one cannot automatically derive the latter from the former.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Baraita} elaborates: the phrase {\i ve-tamei ad ha-erev ve-taher} creates a tension between {\i ve-tamei} (\u8220"it is still {\i tamei}\u8221") and {\i ve-taher} (\u8220"it becomes {\i tahor}\u8221"). The {\i drasha} notes that {\i ve-taher} is written in a form that implies an already-present degree of {\i tahara}, not purely a future state. The {\i gemara} resolves the tension: {\i kan le-ma\u8217'aser, kan le-terumah} \u8212- relative to {\i maaser} it is already {\i tahor}, but relative to {\i terumah} it remains {\i tamei}. This establishes a two-tiered status for the {\i tvul yom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Could one reverse the hierarchy \u8212- treating {\i maaser} as the more stringent category? The {\i gemara} rejects this: it is straightforward that eating {\i terumah} is more stringent than eating {\i maaser} \u8212- this we have already established \u8212- and therefore the parallel holds that touching {\i terumah} is more stringent than touching {\i maaser}. The argument proceeds with its own internal logic: since a {\i tvul yom} is prohibited from eating {\i terumah}, it follows naturally that this same status makes contact with {\i terumah} problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yoledes & Tamei Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then offers an alternative source: {\i bechol kodesh lo tiga ve-el ha-mikdash lo tavo}, the verse describing the {\i yoledes} during her thirty-three or sixty-six days of {\i dam tohar}. Her status during this period is that of a {\i tvul yom shel yom aroch} \u8212- a long-day {\i tvul yom} \u8212- since she has immersed but is still awaiting the night before her {\i korban}. The verse says she may not \u8220"touch\u8221" anything holy ({\i kodesh}). One might suppose this directly prohibits contact. However, the {\i gemara} demonstrates that {\i bechol kodesh lo tiga} means \u8220"do not eat {\i terumah},\u8221" not \u8220"do not physically touch.\u8221" The analogy is drawn between {\i kodesh} and {\i mikdash}: just as entering the {\i mikdash} while {\i tamei} carries the penalty of {\i karet} \u8212- a punishment involving {\i netilat neshamah} \u8212- so too the prohibition of {\i kodesh} must refer to something that carries such a penalty. Since one is never liable to {\i karet} for merely touching {\i terumah}, only for eating it while {\i tamei}, the verse must be speaking of eating, not touching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the verse uses the language of {\i tiga} \u8212- touching \u8212- rather than {\i achila} \u8212- eating. The {\i gemara} explains: {\i negi\u8217'ah ke-achilah} \u8212- touching is being compared to eating, teaching that the same prohibition applies to contact as applies to eating. Just as the {\i tvul yom} cannot eat {\i terumah}, she cannot touch it either. The same parallel hierarchy applies: a {\i tvul yom} may eat {\i maaser} but not {\i terumah}, and similarly may touch {\i maaser} but may not touch {\i terumah}; a {\i mechussar kipurim} may eat {\i terumah} but not {\i kodshim}, and the same follows for contact.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ptzua Daka Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Ptzua Daka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now shifts to a new section of the {\i mishnah}, turning to the laws of {\i ptzua daka} and {\i krus shofcha} \u8212- those whose reproductive injury prohibits them from marrying a naturally born Jewish woman, as derived from the verse {\i lo yavo bi-khal Hashem}. The {\i mishnah} notes that a {\i ptzua daka} who is a {\i kohen} retains his ability to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- his own status is not compromised \u8212- but his wife loses the ability to eat {\i terumah} because by having relations with a man forbidden to her she becomes a {\i chalalah}, and a {\i chalalah} is excluded from eating {\i terumah}. This asymmetry is striking: the verse speaks primarily to him, yet his {\i terumah} status is unaffected while hers is transformed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Status Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An interesting case arises at the end of the {\i mishnah}: what if the man\u8217's condition developed after they were already married but before they had consummated the marriage? The woman has not yet had relations with him, has not yet become a {\i chalalah}, and therefore has not yet been invalidated. The question is whether she should nonetheless be prohibited from eating {\i terumah} on the grounds that consummation \u8212- and thus her invalidation \u8212- is inevitable. The {\i mishnah} rules that she may continue eating {\i terumah} until she has actually had relations with him under these circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: whose position does this {\i mishnah} reflect? {\i Mishtameret le-vi\u8217'ah pesulah de-Oraita achla?} Who holds that a woman \u8220"watching\u8221" for a {\i bi\u8217'ah} that is forbidden and that will surely come \u8212- yet has not happened \u8212- may still eat {\i terumah}? Rabbi Elazar identifies this as the position of Rabbi Elazar and Rabbi Shimon in an earlier dispute, which addressed the analogous case of a widow ({\i almanah}) betrothed to a {\i Kohen Gadol}, or a divorc\u233 ?e betrothed to a regular {\i kohen}, where {\i erusin} has taken place but the marriage has not yet been consummated. In those cases as well, the question is whether the anticipated \u8212- but not yet actualized \u8212- {\i bi\u8217'ah psulab} disqualifies her from {\i terumah}. Rabbi Elazar and Rabbi Shimon said she may still eat, and our {\i mishnah} follows that ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Reply\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan offers a broader reading: even according to Rabbi Meir \u8212- who in the {\i erusin} case prohibits eating {\i terumah} because the anticipated invalidation is so clear \u8212- here the {\i mishnah} would be different. Why? {\i She-kvar achlah} \u8212- she has already been eating {\i terumah} throughout the marriage. Under these circumstances, one does not force her to stop eating based on something that has not yet happened. The ruling is not to initiate eating when invalidation is on the horizon, but where she has already been eating validly, she is not told to stop until her status actually changes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar pushes back: {\i she-kvar achlah lo amrinan} \u8212- the fact that one has been eating is not in itself a halakhically relevant consideration. He offers a proof: a {\i bat Yisrael} married to a {\i kohen} who was eating {\i terumah} during the marriage certainly cannot continue eating after her husband dies, even though {\i she-kvar achlah}. Rabbi Yochanan responds: {\i hasam pasak kinyana} \u8212- there the marriage bond has ceased entirely. But here the relationship has not ended; she is still married. The only reason to stop her would be anticipation of a future event that has not yet occurred, and so long as her actual status has not changed, and she has been eating legitimately, she may continue.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ptzua Daka Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testicle Injuries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a detailed and practically significant discussion of what physically constitutes {\i ptzua daka}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i eizu p\u8217'suah dakah \u8212- kol she-niftzu ha-beitzim shelo}, any case in which the testicles have been {\i niftzu}. Rashi understands {\i niftzu} as deriving from the word {\i petza} \u8212- a wound, specifically a cutting-type injury, such as one made with a knife. Rashi\u8217's reading should be kept in mind when other translations are considered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Injury Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Baraita} elaborates further: the injuries include {\i niftzu} (wounded), {\i nikbu} (punctured, i.e., a hole has been made), and {\i nimoku} (withered or shrunken). Rashi explains {\i nimoku} as {\i machamas makkah nimoku ufachtu lahem mei\u8217'aleihem} \u8212- they shrank as a result of an injury. This is significant: Rashi specifies that it was {\i machamas makkah}, due to a blow, because the entire discussion \u8212- as becomes explicit shortly \u8212- pertains only to injuries caused through human agency ({\i bidei adam}). Rabbi Yishmael son of Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka adds: {\i ve-afillu chasru} \u8212- even if a small portion is simply cut away. He reports this ruling in the name of the sages of {\i Kerem Beyavneh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also derives from the word {\i dakah} a third injury type \u8212- crushing \u8212- alongside cutting and severing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Body Parts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then teaches that all three injury types are interchangeable across all three relevant body parts: the {\i gid} (penis), the {\i beitzim} (testicles), and the {\i chutei beitzim} (the ducts, analogous to the vas deferens, that connect the testicles to the penis). The full formulation is: {\i ptzua} (wounded/punctured) in any of them, {\i dach} (crushed) in any of them, {\i karus} (severed) in any of them \u8212- whether the injury occurred to the {\i gid}, the {\i beitzim}, or the {\i chutei beitzim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One Testicle Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael son of Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka reported from the sages of {\i Kerem Beyavneh}: {\i kol she-ein lo ella beitzah achas eino ella saris chamah} \u8212- whoever has only one testicle is like a {\i saris chamah}, and is {\i kasher}. This teaching is immediately surprising. We have just established that even a wound to a single testicle is disqualifying, so surely the complete absence of one testicle should be even more severe. And the comparison to a {\i saris chamah} \u8212- a man rendered sterile naturally, typically through illness or fever \u8212- raises its own complications, since we are not necessarily speaking of sterility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saris Chama Clarified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses: is he literally a {\i saris chamah}? That cannot be, since a {\i saris chamah} is someone rendered sterile through a natural process such as illness, whereas missing one testicle may be a birth defect with no such connection. The {\i gemara} clarifies: {\i ela harei hu ke-saris chamah} \u8212- he is not a {\i saris chamah}, but he is {\i like} a {\i saris chamah}, and just as a {\i saris chamah} is {\i kasher}, so too is he.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bidei Shamayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's explanation is that the case involves a birth defect \u8212- the person was born with only one testicle \u8212- and the ruling follows from the principle that {\i bidei shamayim} conditions do not disqualify. The comparison to {\i saris chamah} fits perfectly on Rashi\u8217's reading: just as a {\i saris chamah} is naturally sterile and yet {\i kasher} because it is {\i bidei shamayim}, so too someone born with one testicle is {\i kasher} by the same principle. Tosafot, however, reads the case differently and says we are dealing with {\i bidei adam} \u8212- a case where one testicle was removed through human action. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, the ruling would represent a disagreement with the earlier position, or alternatively, an ironic inversion: a complete removal of a testicle may be {\i less} problematic than an injury to it, because an injury to the testicle may cause infertility whereas removal of a complete testicle \u8212- as Tosafot observes from practical experience \u8212- need not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Surgical Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's evidence is remarkable. At the bottom of 75a, and continuing onto 75b, Tosafot writes: {\i ve-od de-bidei adam nami hachazinan kama bnei adam she-gomemim mehen beitza achat u-molidin} \u8212- and furthermore, even when done through human agency, we see a number of people from whom one testicle has been removed who nonetheless father children. Tosafot adds that we cannot simply respond by saying {\i d\u8217'lo mistavra lomar tzei ve-chakur bneihem me\u8217'eimat} \u8212- that all these men\u8217's children are suspected of being {\i mamzerim} and their wives have been unfaithful. That would be absurd given the scale of the phenomenon. So Tosafot concludes that removal of a single testicle does not render a person infertile and therefore does not create the status of {\i ptzua daka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a further question about what precisely Tosafot is observing historically. He appears to be referring to surgical removal of a testicle as a common practice in his time. He even notes, toward the end of that {\i tosafot}, that since there is a {\i Yerushalmi} opinion that the right testicle may be treated differently from the left, {\i ve-tov le-hazhir la-refuim la-chtoch shel smol im efshar} \u8212- it is advisable to instruct physicians, if they are removing one, to remove the left one if possible. The medical and halachic engagement here is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Moshe Reassessment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a significant responsum by Rav Moshe Feinstein. A man had undergone a testicular biopsy \u8212- a small portion of tissue was removed from a testicle \u8212- in order to assist with fertility treatments. The earlier {\i Baraita} had explicitly stated {\i ve-afillu chasru} \u8212- even if a small portion is cut away, it is disqualifying. On a straightforward reading, a biopsy would seem to fall under this category, since it is {\i bidei adam} and involves the removal of even a small portion. Rav Moshe argues, however, that the {\i gemara\u8217's} entire framework ties the disqualification to infertility. Since modern medicine demonstrates that such a biopsy does not cause infertility \u8212- indeed, the very purpose of the procedure was to enable fertility \u8212- the {\i gemara\u8217's} criterion of {\i chasru} must be understood as referring only to cases where infertility results. When it does not, the {\i din} of {\i ptzua daka} does not apply. This is a striking example of the interplay between halachic categories and empirical medical knowledge.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fertility & Causation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case: Man in Palm Tree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks directly: {\i ve-nekev lo molid?} \u8212- is it truly the case that a person with a puncture cannot father children? A case is cited: a man climbed a palm tree ({\i salikal\u8217'dikla}) and a thorn punctured his testicle ({\i charzeia silu be-beitzim}). A viscous stream then emerged from his penis ({\i ve-nafak minei ke-chut de-mugla}), and he subsequently fathered children. This would seem to prove that {\i nekev} \u8212- puncture \u8212- does not necessarily cause infertility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Skeptical Reply\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel transmitted this story to Rav. Rav\u8217's response was dismissive: {\i tzei u-vtzor bivnav mei\u8217'ayin heim} \u8212- go and investigate the origins of his children. Rav was not prepared to accept the empirical evidence at face value and instead suggested that the children might not actually be his. This response represents one pole in the ongoing tension between empirical observation and established {\i halachic} categories: rather than revising the {\i halakha}, Rav preferred to question the apparent facts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natural Causes Kosher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: {\i ptzua daka bidei shamayim kasher} \u8212- if the condition arose through natural causes, the person is {\i kasher}. The category of {\i bidei shamayim} includes birth defects, illness, and conditions arising from the natural functioning of the body. The question of whether a physical trauma caused by an external but non-human event \u8212- such as a falling tree branch struck by lightning \u8212- would be considered {\i bidei shamayim} is left open, but standard {\i bidei shamayim} cases such as illness and congenital conditions are clearly excluded from the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ptzua vs.\u160 ?Haptzua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava derives this distinction from the language of the verse itself: the Torah writes {\i ptzua} and not {\i ha-ptzua}. {\i Ha-ptzua} would suggest a permanent, pre-existing state \u8212- how one was born. {\i Ptzua} indicates that this occurred to the person as an event, implying that the disqualification requires an act of injury, not a natural condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Baraita} reinforces this: {\i ne\u8217'emar lo yavo ptzua daka ve-ne\u8217'emar lo yavo mamzer} \u8212- the Torah employs the same language of {\i lo yavo} for both {\i ptzua daka} and {\i mamzer}. Just as {\i mamzeirus} arises only through a human act \u8212- a forbidden sexual union \u8212- {\i ma le-halan bidei adam, af kan bidei adam} \u8212- so too {\i ptzua daka} applies only when the injury resulted from human agency. Where the condition is entirely natural, there is no disqualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Herzog Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further and quite radical extension of the {\i bidei shamayim} category is proposed in a responsum by Rav Herzog. He addresses a case where a person had a natural condition \u8212- such as cancer \u8212- which required surgery, and the surgery itself constituted one of the physical injuries that would ordinarily create the status of {\i ptzua daka}. One would initially classify this as {\i bidei adam}, since a surgeon performed the procedure. Rav Herzog argues otherwise by analogy to the {\i mamzer} case. With {\i mamzeirus}, the human act ({\i bidei adam}) occurs at the beginning of the process \u8212- the forbidden union \u8212- while the rest of the process, the gestation and birth of the child, is {\i bidei shamayim}. What makes something {\i bidei adam} is that the initiating cause was a human act. By parallel reasoning, where the initiating cause \u8212- the underlying condition \u8212- was {\i bidei shamayim}, the fact that a surgeon then treated it does not transform the entire sequence into {\i bidei adam}. The initial cause governs the classification. Therefore, surgery performed to treat a naturally occurring condition remains in the {\i bidei shamayim} category, and the resulting state does not disqualify the person.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Injuries Interchangeable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's teaching, noted above, is that all three injury types ({\i ptzua}, {\i dach}, {\i karus}) apply to all three body parts ({\i gid}, {\i beitzim}, {\i chutei beitzim}). The {\i gemara} spells this out fully: whether the {\i gid} is wounded, whether the {\i beitzim} are wounded, whether the {\i chutei beitzim} are wounded; whether the {\i gid} is crushed, whether the {\i beitzim} are crushed, whether the {\i chutei beitzim} are crushed; whether the {\i gid} is severed, whether the {\i beitzim} are severed, whether the {\i chutei beitzim} are severed \u8212- in all cases the {\i din} of {\i ptzua daka} or {\i krus shofcha} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Hamduri Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Hamduri challenged Rava: {\i mimai de-hai ptzua daka be-oto makom?} \u8212- how do we know that {\i ptzua daka} refers specifically to the genitals? Perhaps {\i ptzua} (wounded) and {\i dakah} (crushed) describe any kind of bodily wound, such as a head injury?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Generations Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: from the fact that the verse does not enumerate {\i dorot} \u8212- future generations \u8212- as it does by {\i Ammoni u-Mitzri} ({\i dor shlishi yavo lahem}) or {\i mamzer} ({\i lo yavo ad dor asiri}). The absence of any generational qualifier implies that we are discussing an injury the very nature of which precludes future generations \u8212- i.e., an injury affecting fertility, which necessarily points to the genitals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Hamduri counters: perhaps the verse mentions no future generations simply because only the man himself is forbidden \u8212- {\i ihu hu de-assur, brei u-bar brei kashrei} \u8212- only he is disqualified, not his descendants. On this reading the absence of generational language proves nothing about the nature or location of the injury.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Krus Shofcha Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore turns to the companion term: {\i ptzua daka} is juxtaposed with {\i krus shofcha}. The word {\i shofcha} \u8212- from {\i lishpoch}, to pour out \u8212- evokes a pouring or spilling of fluid. {\i Krus shofcha} thus means a severing that causes a flow, rather than a stream. This makes plain that we are speaking of the genitals, since the {\i shofcha} terminology describes what ordinarily emerges as a stream now emerging as a flood because of the injury. Thus by analogy, {\i ptzua daka} must also be speaking of the same bodily location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stream vs.\u160 ?Flow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} then asks: how do we know that {\i krus shofcha} itself refers to the genitals? Perhaps {\i shofcha} refers to a wound on the lip, causing profuse bleeding? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i shafcha ketiv bi-mkom she-shofech} \u8212- the word {\i shafcha} indicates a place where fluid ordinarily flows, but now, as a result of the cutting ({\i krus}), it pours out as a flood rather than a stream. {\i Mi she-al yedei krita shofech} \u8212- it must be a part of the body that, because of the injury, now pours where it formerly streamed. {\i She-lo al yedei krita eino shofech ela mekale\u8217'ach} \u8212- without the injury, the fluid from that location would emerge only as a stream, not a flow. This excludes the nose and mouth, where fluids flow freely regardless of injury. The combination of \u8220"a place where fluid ordinarily exits as a stream\u8221" and \u8220"only as a result of the cutting does it now pour\u8221" identifies the genitals unambiguously.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Corona Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Through-Piercing Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} had stated: {\i ve-im nishtayer me-ha\u8217'atara afillu ke-chut ha-sa\u8217'arah kasher} \u8212- if even a hair-thin strip of the {\i atara} (corona) remains toward the body, the man is {\i kasher}. The implication is that the disqualifying injury to the penis ({\i krus}) must extend from the corona toward the body; an injury affecting only the portion from the corona toward the tip is not disqualifying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A specific case is now posed: {\i nikav le-mata me-atara she-kenegdo le-mala me-atara} \u8212- what if a puncture begins below the {\i atara} (i.e., on the side of the tip) but passes entirely through the thickness of the penis and emerges on the other side above the {\i atara} (i.e., on the side toward the body)? Does the point of entry \u8212- which would not be disqualifying \u8212- determine the status, or does the point of exit \u8212- which is toward the body \u8212- disqualify?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Corona Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba was inclined to rule {\i le-achshurei} \u8212- that such a case is {\i kasher}, since the puncture originates from below the {\i atara}. Rabbi Assi corrected him, citing Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i atara kol she-hi me\u8217'akevet} \u8212- even a minimal amount of the {\i atara} is decisive. As long as any portion of the puncture emerges from the side of the corona toward the body, it is disqualifying. The fact that part of the puncture originates from the other side does not save the case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair-Thin Strip Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the ruling about the severed penis: if even a hair-thin strip of the corona remains toward the body, the person is {\i kasher}. This rule requires clarification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Circumference Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina raised the following question: when the Sages say that a hair-thin strip ({\i ke-chut ha-sa\u8217'arah}) of the {\i atara} suffices \u8212- must this strip extend across the full circumference of the {\i atara}, or does a {\i rov} (majority) of the circumference suffice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Majority Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba Tospa\u8217'a answered Ravina: a hair-thin strip across the majority ({\i rov}) of the circumference, toward the body ({\i ve-chelapi reisha}), is sufficient. The {\i Gra} notes that there is an {\i alef} indicating a variant reading of {\i kula} (the entire circumference) rather than {\i ruba} (majority), and so the precise textual tradition here is a matter of some question. But on the version of the text as cited, Rabba Tospa\u8217'a\u8217's answer is that majority suffices.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Thickness Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quill Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules on two cases involving partial removal of the penis\u8217's thickness rather than complete severance. The first is {\i ke-kulmus} \u8212- like a quill. When scribes prepared quills, they would shave down the thickness of the shaft to create a tapered tip. Similarly, if the thickness of the penis was cut away on one side, creating a tapered appearance without puncturing all the way through, Rav Huna rules this is {\i kesherah} \u8212- valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drain Pipe Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case is {\i ke-marzav} \u8212- like a drain pipe. Here the exterior remains intact but the interior flesh has been hollowed out, leaving the internal piping ({\i gid}) in place but removing the surrounding tissue. Rav Huna rules this is {\i pesulah} \u8212- disqualifying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Air Exposure Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is Rav Huna\u8217's reasoning? {\i Hai sholet ba-avira, ve-hai lo sholet ba-avira} \u8212- in the case of the pipe, the interior workings of the penis are exposed to air, which somehow leads to infertility. In the case of the quill, only the external thickness is affected; the interior remains protected and unexposed to air. This is Rav Huna\u8217's determinative criterion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Opposite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda rules precisely the opposite: {\i ke-marzav kesherah, ke-kulmus pesulah} \u8212- the pipe is valid, the quill is disqualifying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Friction Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda\u8217's reasoning is different: {\i hai garid ve-hai lo garid} \u8212- one case draws out semen effectively during intercourse and the other does not. Rashi explains: if the outer thickness of the penis is cut away, friction during intercourse is reduced, and this impairs the ejaculation of semen, thus affecting fertility. In the case where the interior is hollowed out but the thickness remains, friction is preserved and ejaculation is normal. The criterion for Rav Chisda is not air exposure but the mechanics of fertilization.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Agrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava sides with Rav Huna: {\i ke-vatei de-Rav Huna mistavra} \u8212- Rav Huna\u8217's reasoning is persuasive. The air-exposure criterion is the more compelling one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Faucet Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava addresses Rav Chisda\u8217's friction argument directly: even if the outer thickness is reduced, this need not impair fertility, because {\i midi de-havah a-barza de-chavita} \u8212- it is like the faucet inserted into a barrel. Rashi explains that such a spout, though it may be wide at one end, narrows as it enters the barrel; similarly, even if the exterior of the penis is thinned, during deeper penetration the surrounding tissue narrows and provides friction at that point. Therefore, reduction in outer thickness does not necessarily impair the drawing out of semen, and Rav Chisda\u8217's concern about {\i gridusa} \u8212- the friction mechanism \u8212- is answered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina reported to Mareimar: {\i hachi amar Mar Zutra mishemei de-Rav Pappa, hilchasa bein ke-kulmus bein ke-marzav kesherah} \u8212- the {\i halakha} is that in both the quill case and the drain pipe case, the ruling is {\i kasher}. The {\i gemara} then notes a further question \u8212- {\i mihu miba\u8217'ei lei le-mata me-heter le-mala} \u8212- regarding the precise location of the injury relative to the permissible and prohibited zones, which carries over into the continuation of the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 76\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Penile injuries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prior interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 76, and we continue the important discussion surrounding the halachic definition of {\i patzua daka u\u8217'krut shofcha}. Yesterday we noted how some of these definitions were changed radically \u8212- or interpreted in a radically narrow way \u8212- after the time of the Gemara, in order to permit men with these conditions to marry. Tosafot addresses the distinction between injury and complete removal, suggesting that the latter may not fall under the prohibition. Rav Moshe takes the position that even conditions explicitly mentioned in the Gemara are not disqualifying if we now know that they do not lead to infertility, since the Gemara\u8217's physical descriptions assumed infertility as their operative concern. Rav Herzog goes further, arguing that if a medical procedure was performed to treat a pre-existing medical condition, the entire situation is considered {\i b\u8217'yedei shamayim} and therefore excluded from the prohibition. With that context established, we continue in the Gemara at the bottom of 75b.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Komus and Mardik cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra, in the name of Rav Papa, states the {\i halacha}: whether the penis was thinned and tapered like a quill ({\i kekomus}) or hollowed out like a drainpipe ({\i kemardik}), either way it is {\i kashera} \u8212- valid. Presumably, neither condition constitutes a sufficiently serious wound, likely because neither necessarily leads to infertility, which is the Gemara\u8217's central concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina then transmitted a question to Mareimar in the name of Mar Zutra: when we say these injuries are {\i kashera}, are we referring to injuries above the corona \u8212- meaning toward the body \u8212- or below the corona, toward the tip? The Gemara immediately answers: obviously we are discussing injuries above the corona, toward the body. Injuries below the corona, toward the tip, are not a problem even if the organ is completely severed at that point. As the Gemara remarks, {\i pshita d\u8217'l\u8217'mata me\u8217'hatara} \u8212- it is self-evident that such injuries would not be a concern. The Gemara then adds that Ravina\u8217's purpose in posing the question to Mareimar was not truly to resolve a doubt, but to test him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There follows a case from Masa M\u8217'chasya: Mar Bar Rav Ashi performed a procedure on a man whose condition resembled the {\i mardik} case, and surgically converted it into what resembled the {\i komus} case, thereby rendering it valid. Rashi explains that this was the nature of the intervention \u8212- transforming one configuration into the other \u8212- though the Gemara moves forward without elaborating further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blocked semen passage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a case from Pumbedita. The pathway of the semen had become blocked ({\i istatam}), and the semen was emerging from the urinary tract rather than through its natural channel. Rav Bibi bar Abaye initially ruled that the man was still valid \u8212- after all, the semen was still emerging, so what difference did the pathway make? Rav Papa rebuked him, and Rashi notes that his formulation alludes to Abaye\u8217's lineage from the house of Eli, which carried a divine curse \u8212- the implication being that Rav Bibi spoke carelessly. Rav Papa\u8217's substantive point is: {\i bimkomo mevashelah shelo bimkomo lo mevashelah} \u8212- when semen travels through its natural passage, it is properly warmed by the body and is capable of causing conception; when it travels through the urinary tract, this does not occur, and the man is therefore infertile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about this case is that there is no obvious physical injury of the kind the Gemara elsewhere describes \u8212- no severing, no crushing. The passage was simply {\i istatam}, blocked or clogged. One might ask: since when does a blocked duct fall into the category of {\i kerus shofcha} or {\i patzua daka}? The Gemara seems to imply that any physical condition causing infertility in this manner is disqualifying, even without a classic wound. One possibility, raised in discussion, is that the blockage may itself have resulted from a physical injury rather than occurring {\i b\u8217'yedei shamayim} \u8212- which would explain both why it is disqualifying and why the {\i b\u8217'yedei shamayim} exemption does not apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Corona hole test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: if a hole existed in the penis \u8212- the classic case of {\i nikaiv} \u8212- and it subsequently became sealed ({\i nistam}), the determining criterion is {\i kol she\u8217'ilu nikri v\u8217'nikra pasul} \u8212- if, at the moment of seminal emission ({\i keri}), the hole would reopen, then the man is invalid. The reasoning is presumably that a hole that reopens upon ejaculation demonstrates that the channel has not truly healed, with direct implications for fertility. If it does not reopen, he is {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then raised the question: where exactly is this hole located? If it is below the corona \u8212- toward the tip \u8212- even complete severance would be permissible, so that cannot be the scenario. It must therefore be speaking of a hole in the corona itself. A parallel statement is then cited in the name of Shmuel through Rav Mari bar Mar citing Mar Ukva, specifying explicitly that we are discussing a hole {\i in the corona itself} ({\i nikaiv ba\u8217'atarah atzmah}), with the same rule applying: if it would reopen upon ejaculation, {\i pasul}; if not, {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar Brei d\u8217'Rabba then sent an inquiry to Rav Yosef: {\i heichi avdinan} \u8212- how do we actually determine this in practice? The problem is circular: one cannot simply instruct the man to examine himself after relations with his wife, because the entire question is whether he is permitted to have relations with his wife in the first place. Some procedure for inducing ejaculation in a controlled way is needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ejaculation methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef replied: bring hot barley bread, place it near the anus, and this will induce ejaculation ({\i mikrei}), after which the organ can be inspected. Abaye objected: does one assume that everyone is as righteous as Yaakov Avinu? The Gemara understands {\i kochi v\u8217'reishit oni} \u8212- Reuven is described as the first of his father\u8217's strength \u8212- to mean that the conception of Reuven was Yaakov\u8217's first seminal emission ever; he was so scrupulous that he had never experienced one prior to his first conjugal act with Leah. Most men, Abaye observed, require far less inducement. Instead, Abaye suggested: pass brightly colored women\u8217's garments before him ({\i bigdei tzvonin}), and for most men that will be sufficient stimulation to cause ejaculation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava countered in the opposite direction: does one assume that everyone is as licentious as Barzillai the Gileadite, who the Gemara elsewhere describes as {\i shatuf b\u8217'zimah} \u8212- immersed in licentiousness? Colored garments may not be sufficient for everyone; many men require more. Since no satisfactory middle ground could be identified between the colored garments and the more invasive medical procedure, the Gemara concludes {\i mechvarta k\u8217'dishninan me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- the original method, the barley bread procedure, stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is noteworthy here is that the Gemara has no apparent concern about {\i zera l\u8217'vatala} or {\i hirhurim} in this context. The barley procedure, being purely physiological, may not involve {\i hirhurim} at all. But the {\i bigdei tzvonin} presumably do. The Gemara\u8217's silence suggests that when ejaculation is induced for a legitimate medical or halachic purpose, neither concern applies. This has obvious relevance to the contemporary responsa literature on procuring semen from a husband for fertility treatment involving that same couple \u8212- a topic for which this {\i sugya} serves as a foundational model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Healing defects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hole closure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a {\i Baraita}: {\i nikaiv pasul mipnei shehu shotet} \u8212- if there is a hole, it is invalid because the semen dribbles rather than flowing in a proper stream. This is a restatement and formalization of what was discussed previously. {\i Nistam kasher mipnei shemolid} \u8212- if it becomes sealed, it is valid because the man can now father children. {\i Ve-zehu pasul she-chozer l\u8217'hechsheiro} \u8212- and this is a case of invalidity that can revert to validity through healing. The man was in the category of {\i kerus shofchah}, but if the hole closes, he is restored to fitness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scab coverage issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i zehu l\u8217'mautei mai} \u8212- what does the word {\i zehu} (\u8220"this is\u8221") come to exclude? The answer: it excludes a case where what appears to be closure is in fact only a {\i kerum she\u8217'alah machamas makah} \u8212- a membrane or scab that has grown over a wound. In the laws of {\i treifot} pertaining to the lung, such a covering is not considered a genuine seal; the lung is still regarded as perforated, and the animal remains {\i treifah}. The analogy here is that a mere scab over a hole in the relevant organ does not constitute true closure. The hole remains, and the man\u8217's status is unchanged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to the broader halachic question of {\i glatt} \u8212- literally \u8220"smooth.\u8221" The entire debate around the smoothness of the lung turns on whether abrasions, {\i sirchos}, or membranous growths are indicative of an underlying perforation or are independently problematic. A {\i kerum}, properly understood, is not a scab but a membrane \u8212- the {\i sircha} is closer to what one would call a scab in common usage. In any case, the principle is clear: genuine physical closure restores validity; superficial coverage does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wound healing methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Idi bar Avin sent an inquiry to Abaye: {\i heichi avdinan} \u8212- if there is a hole and we want the body to close it, what do we do to facilitate that healing? Abaye replied with the following protocol. Take a barley kernel and scratch the area around the hole, drawing some blood and stimulating the body\u8217's healing response. Then apply fat to the area, rubbing it in, to promote scabbing and filling. Finally, obtain a large ant \u8212- {\i gamla}, camel-sized \u8212- place it at the wound site, allow it to bite the area, and then sever its head, leaving the head embedded. As the head decomposes, it may serve as raw material for the body to seal the wound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important clarification: one must scratch specifically with barley and not with iron, because {\i de-mizraf zarif} \u8212- iron causes inflammation rather than the beneficial scabbing one wants. This reflects a practical awareness that metal contact with a wound produces a different physiological response than a gentler abrasion. And all of this applies only to a small hole ({\i katana}); for a large one, any such closure will ultimately peel away and prove ineffective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual urination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava bar Rav Huna ruled: if a man urinates from two places \u8212- apparently from some opening in the penis in addition to its normal tip, possibly indicating that the urine is passing through two internal channels \u8212- he is {\i pasul}. The same underlying puzzle applies here as in the case of the blocked semen passage: why does a condition that is not obviously a classic physical wound fall under these categories? Unless, again, the presumption is that such a condition results from some form of physical injury rather than arising {\i b\u8217'yedei shamayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then ruled: {\i leit hilcheta} \u8212- the halacha does not follow Rava bar Rav Huna in either of his rulings. Neither regarding the son\u8217's statement about urination from two places, nor regarding the father Rav Huna\u8217's statement, which held that women who engage in sexual contact with one another ({\i nashim hamisolelot zo b\u8217'zo}) are {\i psulot l\u8217'kehuna}, disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}. Rashi understands the disqualification to affect eligibility for a {\i kohen gadol}, implicating the requirement that he marry a {\i betulah}. Tosafot suggests it relates to the category of {\i zona} for an ordinary {\i kohen}. The Gemara rules that neither position is accepted as {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion here opens onto a range of fascinating contemporary questions. The Gemara\u8217's treatment of female same-sex contact as {\i pritzuta b\u8217'alma} \u8212- licentiousness but not halachically constituted sexual intercourse \u8212- reflects the principle that {\i halacha} defines sexual intercourse as requiring penile penetration, whether vaginal or anal. Without that element, there can be no {\i halachic} act of sex, and consequently no {\i zona}, no loss of {\i betulah} status in the operative halachic sense, and arguably no {\i zera l\u8217'vatala} and no {\i hirhurim} in the same way that male sexual activity generates those concerns. The Gemara confirms this by invoking even Rabbi Elazar\u8217's stringent position \u8212- that extramarital relations between an unmarried man and an unmarried woman constitute {\i znut} \u8212- and limiting it explicitly: {\i hatam mili ish}, that applies when there is a man, meaning an act of halachically defined intercourse. {\i Aval isha} \u8212- when only women are involved, it cannot rise to that level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ptzua marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through the physical definitions, the Gemara turns to the halachic consequences for marriage. A {\i patzua daka} or {\i krut shofcha} is {\i mutar b\u8217'giyoret u\u8217'meshuchreret} \u8212- permitted to marry a convert or a freed slave, women who were not born into the Jewish people. The basis is {\i de\u8217'eino assur ella m\u8217'lavo b\u8217'kahal} \u8212- the prohibition is specifically against entering the {\i kahal}, meaning against marrying those who are natural-born members of the community. A {\i giyoret} is not included in that category. The verse is: {\i lo yavo patzua daka u\u8217'krut shofcha b\u8217'kehal Hashem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leniency, while welcome, does prompt discomfort. The Torah\u8217's principle of {\i k\u8217'ger k\u8217'ezrach} implies that a convert stands fully equal to a born Jew, yet in several halachic contexts \u8212- including the law of {\i tum\u8217'at yoledet} which required a separate derivation to include a {\i giyoret}, and here \u8212- converts are treated as outside the primary {\i kahal}. This tension between the ideal of full conversion and the practical halachic categorizations is a recurring theme.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priestly status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is then posed to Rav Sheshet: a {\i patzua daka} who is also a {\i kohen} \u8212- what is his status regarding marriage to a {\i giyoret} or {\i meshuchreret}? We already know from an earlier {\i mishnah} that a {\i patzua daka kohen} retains his priestly status and may eat {\i terumah}. His {\i kedushat kehuna} is intact. Given that, should the ordinary restrictions on whom a {\i kohen} may marry still apply to him? Or, since he is now excluded from the {\i kahal} with respect to marriage, does that exclusion open up categories of women who would ordinarily be forbidden to a {\i kohen}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuine tension: on one side, his {\i kedushah} remains in every domain where a physical injury is irrelevant; on the other, the very fact that he is now categorized as outside the {\i kahal} for marriage purposes may simultaneously remove certain barriers. As was clarified in discussion: the question is not about a {\i gerushah}, whom a {\i patzua daka kohen} still cannot marry regardless, but specifically about a {\i giyoret} \u8212- a woman who is excluded from the {\i kahal} but would otherwise be permissible to a {\i yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Excluded status benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet responded: {\i tanituha} \u8212- the answer has already been taught. The parallel case involves a {\i patzua daka yisrael} and a {\i netina} \u8212- a woman descended from the Gibeonites, members of the seven Canaanite nations who converted in the time of Yehoshua by subterfuge. Ordinarily a {\i yisrael} may not marry someone from the {\i shivat amamim}, just as a {\i kohen} may not marry a {\i giyoret}. Yet a {\i patzua daka yisrael} is permitted to marry a {\i netina}. If he still retained his full {\i kedushah} \u8212- including the prohibition {\i lo titchaten bam} \u8212- this should be forbidden. Since it is permitted, it must be that exclusion from the {\i kahal} opens up marriage to those who are themselves outside that category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, raised a complication. The prohibition of {\i lo titchaten bam} for the {\i shivat amamim} is not straightforwardly biblical once they convert. When they are still non-Jews, the verse {\i lo titchaten bam} cannot mean prohibiting marriage in the halachic sense, since their {\i kiddushin} would not take effect. The prohibition must therefore be speaking of after conversion. But Rava then suggests that the rabbinic extension of this prohibition \u8212- covering converts from the {\i shivat amamim} \u8212- was enacted only where children are in the equation, since the Torah\u8217's concern ({\i ki yasir et bincha me\u8217'acharai}) is about children being led astray. A {\i patzua daka} who cannot have children was never included in that rabbinic decree. So the leniency regarding the {\i netina} may be rabbinic in character and cannot be used to prove the case of a {\i kohen} and a {\i giyoret}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzer and Netin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Excluded analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this argument is then challenged by Rava himself. If the {\i netina} case is merely rabbinic, consider: a {\i mamzer} can certainly have children, and yet the {\i mishnah} teaches {\i mamzerim u\u8217'nesinim mutarim lavo zeh b\u8217'zeh} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} and a {\i netin} may marry one another. If the criterion were about fertility, a {\i mamzer} should be forbidden to a {\i netina}. Evidently the principle is different: {\i ki gazru Rabbanan b\u8217'ksherim, b\u8217'psulim lo gazru} \u8212- when the Rabbis enacted the prohibition against marrying {\i nesinim}, they only applied it to those who are halachically {\i kasher}. Those who are themselves in a category of {\i pesul} \u8212- like {\i mamzer} and {\i patzua daka} \u8212- were not included. So the leniency has a different basis than fertility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical vs rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then reconsidered his own earlier position entirely: {\i lav milsa hi d\u8217'amri} \u8212- what I said was not correct. He had claimed that the biblical prohibition of {\i lo titchaten bam} applies only to the {\i shivat amamim} while they are non-Jews, and that the prohibition post-conversion is rabbinic. But in fact, when the nations are non-Jews, their {\i kiddushin} has no binding effect \u8212- {\i leis lehu chasnus}. The very concept of marriage ({\i chasnus}) only becomes relevant after conversion. Therefore, the Torah\u8217's {\i lo titchaten bam} must be speaking of the prohibition even after conversion \u8212- which means it is a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} prohibition. And yet, despite this being biblical, a {\i patzua daka} and a {\i mamzer} are permitted to marry {\i nesinim}. This demonstrates the principle clearly: once someone is excluded from {\i kahal Hashem}, they are freed from certain restrictions vis-\u224 ?-vis others who are similarly outside that category. And this principle applies equally to a {\i kohen} who is a {\i patzua daka} \u8212- despite his {\i kedushat kehuna}, his exclusion from {\i kahal Hashem} for marriage purposes opens the door to marry a {\i giyoret}. This is indeed the ruling recorded in the {\i Shulchan Aruch}, Even HaEzer, siman 5, se\u8217'if 1, which states explicitly that even a {\i kohen} who is a {\i patzua daka} is permitted to marry a {\i giyoret} or {\i meshuchreret}, since he is no longer within his [full marital] {\i kedushah} in that domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlomo and Egypt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenged the position that the biblical {\i lo titchaten bam} applies only after conversion. If that were so, he asked, how could the verse say {\i vayitchaten Shlomo es bat Paroh melech Mitzrayim} \u8212- that Shlomo married the daughter of Pharaoh? The Gemara responds that she converted ({\i digayara}). But one might raise an obvious objection: was it not a rule that converts were not accepted in the times of David and Shlomo due to suspicion of ulterior motives \u8212- joining the king\u8217's household for material benefit? The Gemara answers: the concern about {\i shulchan melachim} does not apply to bat Paroh herself, since she already came from a royal table; she had nothing to gain materially from conversion. So she was accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara presses further: even if she converted, she was a {\i mitzrit} of the first generation. The verse states {\i lo tita\u8217'ev mitzri ki ger hayita b\u8217'artzo}, and then {\i banim asher yivaldu lahem dor shelishi yavo lahem bikhal Hashem} \u8212- only the third generation of a Mitzri convert may enter the {\i kahal}. A first-generation {\i mitzrit} converting would not be eligible to marry into the {\i kahal} for two more generations. So how was Shlomo permitted to marry her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate that the Mitzri prohibition was still operative in that era, the Gemara cites a {\i Baraita} in which Rabbi Yehuda reports: \u8220"I had a colleague, Minyamin the Mitzri convert, from among the students of Rabbi Akiva. He told me: I am a first-generation Mitzri convert, and I married a first-generation Mitzrit. I intend to have my son marry a second-generation Mitzrit, so that my grandson will be a third-generation [convert of Mitzri descent] and permitted to enter the {\i kahal}.\u8221" This shows that even in the time of Rabbi Akiva \u8212- well after Sancheriv had displaced populations \u8212- the {\i halacha} still treated the Mitzri prohibition as operative. The governing principle is {\i holchin achar hapesul}: if a first-generation marries a second-generation, the child\u8217's status follows the more disqualified line and is counted as second-generation; only when a second-generation marries a second-generation does the child achieve third-generation status and full eligibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the Mitzri prohibition was still in effect in the era of the Talmudic Sages, it must have been in effect in Shlomo\u8217's time as well, making bat Paroh\u8217's status highly problematic. How then did Shlomo marry her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa answered: {\i anan miShlomo leika d\u8217'lisof} \u8212- are we really going to ask questions from Shlomo? The implication is that Shlomo transgressed. But the Gemara then revisits this and suggests: {\i Shlomo lo nasiv midi} \u8212- Shlomo did not actually marry them. The verse says: {\i min hagoyim asher amar Hashem el bnei Yisrael lo tavo\u8217'u bahem\u8230 ? bahem davak Shlomo l\u8217'ahava} \u8212- unto them Shlomo cleaved for love. The word {\i davak}, cleaved, indicates passionate attachment, not formal marriage. He was bound to them deeply, but did not legally marry them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then acknowledges the residual difficulty: but the verse explicitly says {\i vayitchaten} \u8212- he married. The answer: {\i mitoch ahava yeseirah she\u8217'ahava, ma\u8217'alah alav ke\u8217'ilu yitchaten bah} \u8212- because of the exceptional love he bore her, it was as though he had married her, but technically he had not. This is, the Gemara acknowledges, a somewhat forced reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an important related point: after Sancheriv displaced the nations, shouldn\u8217't we simply say that the people living in Egypt are no longer the halachic {\i mitzrim} of the Torah? Tosafot quotes a {\i Tosefta} in which Rabbi Akiva told Minyamin directly: {\i ta\u8217'isa} \u8212- you made a mistake. You think you are a {\i mitzri}, but Sancheriv already came and scrambled all the nations ({\i bilbel kol ha\u8217'olam}), and therefore no one living now can be considered a halachic {\i mitzri}. The Gemara itself, however, maintains that the identification persisted \u8212- not just in Shlomo\u8217's time but even in the era of Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Akiva.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One broader halachic point emerges from this entire discussion. The prohibitions we have been examining \u8212- {\i patzua daka}, {\i mamzer}, {\i mitzri}, {\i amoni} \u8212- concern the formal institution of {\i nisu\u8217'in}, marriage. The question of whether the prohibition is violated by sexual relations outside the framework of marriage is a distinct and nuanced question. As the Gemara implies here, the {\i lo titchaten} formulation presupposes a formal act of marriage. By contrast, prohibitions that involve {\i kares} \u8212- the severe biblical penalties for incestuous or adulterous relations \u8212- are entirely about the act of intercourse, and marriage is not even a relevant concept in those contexts since {\i kiddushin} would not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intermarriage rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoni/Moavi rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to the generational restrictions on various nations joining the {\i kahal}. {\i Amoni u\u8217'Moavi asurin v\u8217'isuran issur olam} \u8212- an Ammonite and a Moabite are forbidden forever. Even {\i gam dor asiri} \u8212- a tenth generation \u8212- may not enter the {\i kahal} of Hashem. Their females, however, are {\i mutarot miyad} \u8212- immediately permissible upon conversion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzri/Edom rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Mitzri or an Edomite is forbidden only {\i ad shelosha dorot} \u8212- for three generations, after which {\i dor shelishi yavo lahem bikhal Hashem}. Here the {\i mishnah} specifies {\i echad zecharim v\u8217'echad nekivot} \u8212- both males and females must wait three generations. This is notable: the more severe prohibition of {\i amoni u\u8217'moavi}, which is permanent for males, is actually more lenient for females. The less severe prohibition of {\i mitzri} and {\i edomi} applies equally to both genders.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon rules that females of Egyptian and Edomite descent should be {\i mutarot miyad}, just as female Ammonites and Moabites are. He advances a {\i kal vachomer}: if in the case where males are permanently forbidden \u8212- {\i amoni u\u8217'moavi} \u8212- the females are immediately permitted, then certainly in the case where males are forbidden only for three generations \u8212- {\i mitzri v\u8217'edomi} \u8212- the females should be permitted immediately. The Sages responded: if you have a tradition ({\i halacha}) we will accept it, but if you are arguing by logic ({\i ledin}), we have a refutation of your {\i kal vachomer}. Rabbi Shimon replied: I am not relying solely on logic; I am stating this as a received {\i halacha}. Whether that tradition was ultimately accepted will emerge from the Gemara\u8217's continued discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 David and Ruth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saul\u8217's inquiry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now grounds the rule that a female Ammonite or Moabite is {\i muteret miyad} in a narrative from {\i Navi}. Rabbi Yochanan cites the verse: {\i v\u8217'chirot Shaul et David yotzei likrat haplishti} \u8212- when Shaul saw David going out to confront the Philistine, Goliath, {\i amar el Avner sar hatzava} \u8212- he said to Avner, his general \u8212- {\i ben mi zeh hana\u8217'ar} \u8212- whose son is this young man?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This seems puzzling. Surely Shaul knew who David was \u8212- David had been his personal armor-bearer, and the verse itself records {\i vayeehavehu me\u8217'od vayehi lo nosei keilim} \u8212- Shaul loved him greatly and David bore his weapons. Furthermore, Yishai was not an obscure figure: the verse describes him as {\i zaken ba b\u8217'anashim}, a man of great stature in the time of Shaul, and the Gemara cites Rav (and some say Rabbi Abba): {\i zeh Yishai avi David shenichnas b\u8217'uchlusa v\u8217'yatza b\u8217'uchlusa} \u8212- whenever Yishai entered or departed, he was accompanied by tens of thousands. Rashi notes that Yishai commanded 600,000. How could Avner not have known him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves: Shaul was not asking about David\u8217's identity or about Yishai. {\i Ei mi\u8217'Peretz asi ei mi\u8217'Zerach asi} \u8212- what he wanted to know was: does David descend from Perez or from Zerach, the two lineages of Yehuda? {\i Ei mi\u8217'Peretz asi, malka havei} \u8212- if from Perez, Shaul had reason for concern. The king is said to {\i poretz}, to burst through boundaries without restraint \u8212- that is the nature of royal power. Descent from Perez would indicate David was destined for kingship. {\i Ei mi\u8217'Zerach asi, chashiva b\u8217'alma havei} \u8212- if from Zerach, the shining one, David would have prestige but not threaten the throne.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What provoked Shaul\u8217's anxiety at precisely this moment? The verse records that Shaul dressed David in his own garments \u8212- {\i madav kemidoto} \u8212- and David wore them to measure, fitting him as perfectly as they fit Shaul, who was {\i mishichmo vamala gavoah mikol ha\u8217'am}, a head taller than all the people. David matched Shaul physically, and Shaul, seeing before him a young man of equal stature and evident charisma, felt genuinely threatened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doeg\u8217's objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, {\i Doeg ha\u8217'Edomi} intervened. He said to Shaul: {\i ad she\u8217'ata mash\u8217'il alav im hagun hu l\u8217'malchus im lav} \u8212- before you worry whether he is of royal lineage, ask a more fundamental question: {\i sha\u8217'al alav im ra\u8217'uy lavo b\u8217'kahal im lav} \u8212- is he even eligible to be part of the {\i kahal}? {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama deka\u8217'asi me\u8217'Rus haMoaviyah} \u8212- because he descends from Ruth the Moabitess.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Avner responded: {\i tanina, Amoni velo Amonit, Moavi velo Moavit} \u8212- we have a tradition: the prohibition is {\i Amoni}, not {\i Amonit}; {\i Moavi}, not {\i Moavit}. A female Moabitess is immediately permitted. Doeg challenged Avner\u8217's silence with a counter-argument: very well, but then say {\i Mitzri velo Mitzrit} \u8212- why don\u8217't we apply the same logic to Egypt and permit female Egyptian converts immediately?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intermarriage rules (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Greeting logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains the textual basis for distinguishing between male and female Ammonites and Moabites, and thereby answering Doeg\u8217's challenge about Egypt. The verse gives a specific reason for the prohibition: {\i al asher lo kidmu etchem balechem uvamayim} \u8212- because they did not come out to greet you with bread and water. This reason generates the limited application: {\i darko shel ish lekadem velo darko shel isha lekadem} \u8212- it is the way of a man to go out and greet, not the way of a woman. Therefore, only the men can be held culpable for the failure to extend hospitality; the women cannot. This is the basis for reading {\i Amoni} and not {\i Amonit}, {\i Moavi} and not {\i Moavit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 David and Ruth (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis HaMedrash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Doeg was not satisfied. He pressed: if men should have gone out to greet the men and women should have gone out to greet the women \u8212- {\i anashim likrat anashim venashim likrat nashim} \u8212- then the women of Moab were equally remiss for not greeting the women of Israel. Why should they be exempt? Avner fell silent. He had no ready answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shaul then directed: {\i tze ushal b\u8217'veis hamedrash} \u8212- go and pose this question in the {\i Beis HaMedrash}. The Gemara notes the wordplay: Shaul called David an {\i elem} in this verse rather than a {\i na\u8217'ar} as he did earlier, and the Gemara reads this as Shaul saying {\i halacha nit\u8217'almah mimcha} \u8212- the {\i halacha} has been hidden from you; Avner does not know the answer. Go to the {\i Beis HaMedrash} and find out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When they arrived at the {\i Beis HaMedrash}, the ruling was the same as Avner had stated: {\i Amoni velo Amonit, Moavi velo Moavit}. But Doeg was there in the {\i Beis HaMedrash} and challenged everyone with all the same objections. {\i Ba\u8217'i le\u8217'achruzei alei} \u8212- he sought to make a public proclamation disqualifying David. The entire {\i Beis HaMedrash} fell silent before his challenge. How David\u8217's lineage was ultimately vindicated and his legitimacy as king of Israel established \u8212- that we will take up tomorrow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 78\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon vs.\u160 ?Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up toward the very bottom of 77b, in the midst of the debate concerning Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position in the Mishnah. Rabbi Shimon maintains that {\i Mitzri} and {\i Adomi} women should be permitted immediately \u8212- that is, from the first generation \u8212- reasoning by way of a {\i kal vachomer}: just as {\i Ammoni} and {\i Moavi}, where the prohibition is far stricter (applying to all generations), is limited to males \u8212- {\i Ammoni v\u8217'lo Ammonit} \u8212- then certainly {\i Mitzri} and {\i Adomi}, whose prohibition extends only two generations, should similarly not apply to the women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan\u8217's Challal Prece\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis respond by adducing the cases of {\i mamzer} and {\i arayot} as precedent: in those contexts, the prohibition falls upon both men and women, demonstrating that a prohibition need not be limited to males even where other asymmetries exist. Rabbi Shimon would rejoin that {\i arayot} and {\i mamzer} are different in a crucial respect: {\i mamzer} is conceived through an act carrying {\i karet}, and {\i arayot} themselves are {\i karet} offenses. By contrast, the prohibition against marrying {\i Mitzri} or {\i Adomi} does not even rise to the level of a {\i lav} \u8212- it is merely an {\i issur asei}. Given that lighter status, the {\i kal vachomer} in favor of permitting the women should hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's Kareis Distinct\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: what would the Rabbis answer to this? Seven lines from the bottom of the page, we read {\i rabbanan} \u8212- the Rabbis would cite the case of the {\i challal} who is produced through a violation of an {\i issur asei}, in accordance with Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov. For example, a {\i Kohen Gadol} who marries a non-{\i besulah} violates only an {\i issur asei}, yet according to Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov the child of that union is a {\i challal}, and {\i challal} status applies to both males and females immediately. This parallel, the Rabbis argue, shows that even an {\i issur asei} can yield a prohibition that applies equally to both sexes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan\u8217's Asei Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Of course, the analogy is imperfect in one respect \u8212- the {\i challal} case is a {\i issur kehunah}, not an {\i issur kahal} \u8212- but the point stands nonetheless: there do exist cases where a person\u8217's status, derived from an {\i issur asei}, applies equally and immediately to both men and women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakha Ani Omer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Six lines from the bottom of 77b, the Gemara asks what Rabbi Shimon means when he concludes the Mishnah debate with the formulation {\i halacha ani omer} \u8212- \u8220"I have a tradition.\u8221" The Gemara clarifies: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar lehu} \u8212- here is what he is actually saying to them. {\i Ledidi lo svira li de\u8217'Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} \u8212- I personally do not hold like Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov; I maintain that an {\i issur asei} does not create {\i challal} status. Therefore, {\i ledidchu de\u8217'svira lechu ke\u8217'Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} \u8212- you, who do hold like Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov, think you can draw the parallel to {\i Mitzri} and {\i Adomi}. But even so, {\i halacha ani omer} \u8212- I still have a received tradition, and therefore even if my logical argument cannot be pressed to a definitive conclusion, the tradition itself is determinative. It is worth noting, as an aside, that the logical argument is in fact on Rabbi Shimon\u8217's side as well, even if the Rabbis have a technical basis for pushing back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An interesting point emerges from {\i Tosafot} here. {\i Tosafot} notes: {\i lav halacha le\u8217'Moshe mi\u8217'Sinai ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon is not claiming a {\i halacha le\u8217'Moshe mi\u8217'Sinai} in the technical sense. For if he were, why would the Rabbis continue to debate him? Rather, {\i ella hachi ka\u8217'amar halacha ani omer kach kibalti mei\u8217'rabosai} \u8212- he is saying, \u8220"This is my tradition: I received it from my teachers.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point resonates with the broader position of the Rambam, who argued that genuine {\i halachot le\u8217'Moshe mi\u8217'Sinai} are never subject to rabbinic debate. This was the Rambam\u8217's response to the Karaite critique that the very existence of Talmudic disputes undermines the claim to an authentic {\i mesorah}. The Rambam\u8217's answer is that all rabbinic debates concern human minds engaging with the sacred text, reaching different conclusions through reasoning \u8212- not a broken chain of tradition. Where there was an actual transmitted tradition, there is no dispute. While the Rambam\u8217's position is itself subject to challenge \u8212- there are in fact disputes recorded about matters described as {\i halacha le\u8217'Moshe mi\u8217'Sinai} \u8212- {\i Tosafot}\u8217's point here is more specific: in this Mishnah, the Rabbis do not appear to be advancing a competing tradition of their own. They are simply arguing from logic. That being the case, why do they not simply concede to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's tradition? The answer, says {\i Tosafot}, is that he does not have a {\i halacha le\u8217'Moshe mi\u8217'Sinai} \u8212- he has something more limited, a ruling or understanding received from his {\i Rebbi}, perhaps based on earlier {\i pesak} or on argument, and thus the Rabbis feel entitled to contest it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita on Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Barim Pasuk Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} now addresses the same dispute. {\i Tanu rabbanan}: Rabbi Shimon said to them, {\i halacha ani omer} \u8212- I have a received tradition \u8212- {\i v\u8217'od mikra mesay\u8217'ani} \u8212- and moreover the verse itself supports me. And this support is arguably even stronger than the inference from {\i Ammoni v\u8217'lo Ammonit}. The verse reads: {\i banim asher yivaldu lahem dor shelishi yavo\u8217'u lahem b\u8217'kahal Hashem} \u8212- \u8220"the children that will be born to them, the third generation, shall enter the congregation of Hashem.\u8221" Rabbi Shimon reads {\i banim} \u8212- \u8220"sons\u8221" \u8212- as excluding daughters: the sons will be permitted in the third generation, implying the daughters were never restricted in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Yuladu Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda offers an alternative reading of the same verse. The verse says {\i banim asher yivaldu lahem dor shelishi} \u8212- \u8220"the children that will be born to them, the third generation.\u8221" Rabbi Yehuda observes: {\i hakatuv telayan b\u8217'leida} \u8212- the verse has made the matter dependent upon birth, upon the act of giving birth. The one who gives birth is the mother, and the mother is a woman. This suggests that the mothers themselves \u8212- that is, women of the first and second generation {\i Mitzri} or {\i Adomi} \u8212- are also forbidden. The verse is concerned with when they will give birth, implying those women are themselves subject to the prohibition until the third generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan\u8217's Evaluation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's assessment of this exchange is striking. He says: {\i ilmalei d\u8217'amar Rabbi Yehuda hakatuv telayan b\u8217'leida} \u8212- if Rabbi Yehuda had not made his point about the verse focusing on birthing and thereby demonstrating that women too are forbidden, {\i lo matza yadav v\u8217'raglav b\u8217'beit hamidrash} \u8212- he would have had no standing in the study hall, meaning his overall position would have been logically incoherent. The reason will now become clear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Possibility Iss\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerim Are Kahal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's remark points to a fundamental problem with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position that had not yet been articulated. The issue stems from another ruling associated with Rabbi Yehuda: {\i kahal gerim ikrei kahal} \u8212- converts are considered part of {\i kahal}, that is, the term {\i kahal Hashem} encompasses converts as well. This means that those who are forbidden from marrying into {\i kahal Hashem} may not marry {\i gerim} either. The standard assumption in this entire sugya had been that {\i Ammoni}, {\i Moavi}, {\i Mitzri}, and {\i Adomi} converts could at least marry other {\i gerim}. But Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling on {\i kahal gerim} closes that door.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marital Impasse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a severe problem: {\i Mitzri sheni bameh yit-har?} \u8212- how does a second-generation {\i Mitzri} ever come to have a third-generation child who is permitted? He cannot marry a {\i Yisraelit} \u8212- she is in the {\i kahal}. He cannot marry a {\i giyores} \u8212- she too is now {\i kahal} according to Rabbi Yehuda. Who remains for him to marry?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might suggest he could marry a {\i mamzeret} \u8212- but then the children would themselves be {\i mamzerim} and could never enter {\i kahal Hashem} regardless. Another suggestion: perhaps he could marry a {\i Mitzrit} convert. But if, following Rabbi Shimon, {\i Mitzrit} women are permitted to all of Israel \u8212- that is, they are included in {\i kahal Hashem} like ordinary {\i gerim} \u8212- then they too would be forbidden to him. So if one holds both that {\i gerim} are {\i kahal} and that {\i Mitzrit} women are unrestricted, there is simply no permissible path by which a male {\i Mitzri} convert can ever produce a third generation that enters {\i kahal Hashem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutar Pathway\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution must be, then, that {\i Mitzrit} women are themselves forbidden \u8212- that is, they are not included in {\i kahal Hashem}, their status is restricted just like the men, and therefore a male {\i Mitzri} can marry a female {\i Mitzrit}. That union provides the {\i mutar} pathway through which the third generation can ultimately come about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Doesn\u8217't Speak B\u8217'di\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara suggests an alternative: perhaps we are talking about {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} \u8212- a second-generation {\i Mitzri} who transgresses and marries someone he should not, and whose child similarly transgresses, thereby producing a third generation through prohibited unions. But the Gemara rejects this: {\i d\u8217'i lo katav kra} \u8212- the Torah does not write a verse addressing what happens when people have been sinning for two generations. The Torah would not frame a {\i hetter} \u8212- a permission to enter {\i kahal Hashem} \u8212- around the premise that transgressions were committed to get there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges this principle with the case of {\i mamzer}: is not {\i mamzer} status itself created only through a transgression, and yet the Torah writes about it? So the Torah does speak of {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Negative vs.\u160 ?Positive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i d\u8217'ei l\u8217'issura ktiv} \u8212- that verse addresses {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'avad} in order to impose negative consequences. If someone transgresses and a child is born from a forbidden union, the Torah tells you the negative outcome: the child is a {\i mamzer}. That is fundamentally different from a verse that would, {\i de\u8217'ei lehateira}, come to teach a {\i hetter} \u8212- a positive permission \u8212- that results from transgression. The Torah will tell you the negative consequences of sin; it will not frame a {\i hetter} as something achieved through sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechzir Grushato Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But is that really true? The Gemara raises the case of {\i machzir grushato} \u8212- the prohibition against a man remarrying a woman he divorced after she had married another man. From the verse dealing with that prohibition, a {\i drasha} teaches {\i hi toeivah ve\u8217'ein baneha toeivim} \u8212- she is forbidden but her children from the second marriage do not acquire a degraded status. That is a {\i hetter} derived from a context of prohibition. So the Torah does teach {\i heteirim} arising from problematic situations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Primary vs.\u160 ?Secondary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the difficulty: {\i hasam mishum ikar issura hu dekatveih} \u8212- the primary purpose of that verse is to teach the {\i issur}, to say that this man may not remarry her. The {\i hetter} regarding the children is incidental, secondary to the main point. A verse whose primary purpose is to establish a prohibition may include, in passing, a {\i hetter}. But a verse would not be written {\i primarily} to teach a {\i hetter} that is only accessible through transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Forbidden Necessary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion stands: for the Torah to have written that the third generation {\i Mitzri} may enter {\i kahal Hashem}, there must be a {\i mutar} path to reach that third generation. Given Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling that {\i kahal gerim ikrei kahal}, the only available {\i mutar} path is for {\i Mitzri} men to marry {\i Mitzrit} women. And for that to be permissible, the {\i Mitzrit} women must themselves be in a restricted status \u8212- forbidden to ordinary Israel, and therefore available to marry {\i Mitzri} men. This is Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's point: without Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's observation that the verse focuses on birthing \u8212- thereby establishing that women too are subject to the prohibition \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda would have been unable to defend any coherent position. His ruling about {\i kahal gerim} would render the entire mechanism of the three-generation process impossible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Textual Parsing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Banim vs.\u160 ?Dorot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to close textual analysis of the verse {\i banim asher yivaldu lahem dor shelishi yavo\u8217'u lahem b\u8217'kahal Hashem}. The verse contains apparent redundancy: it uses both {\i banim} (children) and {\i dor} (generation). Why are both necessary? If the Torah wished to say \u8220"the third generation may enter,\u8221" why not simply say {\i ben shelishi} or {\i dor shelishi}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Banim Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Im ne\u8217'emar banim lama ne\u8217'emar dorot} \u8212- if it had only said {\i banim} (sons or children), without specifying {\i dorot} (generations), one might have thought the count is based on children, not generations. That is, the third child born to the convert \u8212- even if that child is a sibling of the first and second child \u8212- would already be permitted. This would mean you need only a third child, not a third generation. Therefore {\i lekach ne\u8217'emar dorot} \u8212- the term \u8220"generations\u8221" is necessary to establish that it is the third generational descent that is required, not merely the third child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dorot Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i im ne\u8217'emar dorot lama ne\u8217'emar banim} \u8212- had it said only {\i dorot} without {\i banim}, one might have read the three generations as counting from Har Sinai, or from the time the Torah was given, rather than from the moment of conversion. This would be a reasonable reading: these were the nations that wronged Israel, and so three generations from the revelation \u8212- from a fixed historical point \u8212- they will be permitted. Therefore {\i lekach ne\u8217'emar banim} \u8212- the word {\i banim} is needed to anchor the count to when they begin having children, that is, from the time of conversion and the onset of their progeny. Both terms are thus indispensable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Lahem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse also contains the word {\i lahem} twice: {\i banim asher yivaldu lahem dor shelishi yavo\u8217'u lahem b\u8217'kahal Hashem}. Each appearance teaches something distinct. The first {\i lahem} \u8212- {\i mehem meneh}, count from them \u8212- establishes that the generational count begins with the converts themselves, not from Har Sinai, and not from the children. The {\i lahem} fixes the starting point as the converts themselves. Without this, one might have read {\i asher yivaldu} (that will be born) as making the children generation one, so that the \u8220"third generation\u8221" would in fact be the great-grandchildren of the convert. {\i Lahem} corrects this: the convert is generation one, the children are generation two, and the grandchildren are the third generation permitted to enter {\i kahal Hashem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is confirmed by the following consideration: one might have thought that a pregnant {\i Mitzrit} who converted, she and her fetus count as one \u8212- the fetus, having been converted as a fetus, remains generation one along with the mother. Therefore {\i katav rachamana asher yivaldu} \u8212- \u8220"that will be born\u8221" \u8212- to teach that once the child is actually born, that birth already advances the count to the next generation, even though the fetus was converted in utero.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Lahem - Psul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i lahem} teaches {\i halech achar psulan} \u8212- go after their disqualification, whichever parent bears the {\i Mitzri} or {\i Adomi} status, the child inherits that status, regardless of whether it is the father or the mother. One might have assumed that status follows only the father \u8212- given the general patrilineal principle in matters of tribal and national identity \u8212- or conversely only the mother. The second {\i lahem} establishes that whenever a {\i Mitzri} or {\i Adomi} is in the parentage, the {\i psul} follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical example: if a first-generation {\i Mitzri} marries a second-generation {\i Mitzrit}, or vice versa, the child is counted as second generation rather than third \u8212- because the child goes after the more severe of the two statuses. Similarly, if a {\i Mitzri} marries a {\i Yisraelit}, the child does not simply become a full {\i Yisrael} by following the father; the {\i lahem} ensures the {\i Mitzri psul} continues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel derivation exists by {\i mamzer}. The verse regarding {\i mamzer} says {\i lo yavo mamzer b\u8217'kehal Hashem}. From the word {\i lo} \u8212- corresponding to the {\i lahem} by {\i Mitzri} \u8212- the Gemara derives that wherever the {\i mamzer} is in the parentage, the {\i psul} follows that parent, whether the {\i mamzer} is the father or the mother. The same principle applies: the child of a {\i mamzer} father or a {\i mamzeret} mother is a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why state this principle separately for both {\i mamzer} and {\i Mitzri}? State it once and derive the other by analogy. The Gemara explains that each is necessary. Had the principle been written only by {\i Mitzri}, one might have said it is so because the {\i Mitzri} comes from a {\i tipah pesulah} \u8212- he was born outside the Jewish people, and therefore there is a profound invalidity to either parental line. But a {\i mamzer}, who was born Jewish albeit through a forbidden union, might be different \u8212- perhaps the {\i psul} would not follow from either parent. Therefore {\i lo} is written by {\i mamzer} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And conversely, had it been written only by {\i mamzer} \u8212- whose prohibition is absolute and eternal \u8212- one might have concluded that this severe, permanent {\i psul} is what generates the rule that either parent transmits it. But by {\i Mitzri}, whose prohibition is only two generations and lighter in character, perhaps the {\i psul} follows only one parent. Therefore {\i lahem} is needed by {\i Mitzri} as well. Both formulations are indispensable: {\i tzericha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Paternity vs.\u160 ?Maternity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan - Follow Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now addresses whether the {\i psul} in practice follows the father, the mother, or simply the weightier of the two statuses. Rabba bar bar Chana cites Rabbi Yochanan: {\i Mitzri sheni she\u8217'natal Mitzrit rishona bnah shelishi havei} \u8212- a second-generation {\i Mitzri} man who marries a first-generation {\i Mitzrit} woman, their child is a third generation and therefore permitted. This is a significant statement: it means that what matters is the father\u8217's status, not the mother\u8217's. Since the father is second generation, the child is third, regardless of the mother being first generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Patrilineal Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication, as the Gemara draws it: {\i alma kasavar basar didei shadina lei} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan holds that we follow the father. This is, after all, the intuitively natural position in a patrilineal system. Yet the Gemara does not simply accept this inference and moves on to interrogate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenges this with a {\i baraita}. Rabbi Tarfon says: {\i yecholim mamzerim litaher} \u8212- it is possible to purify {\i mamzerim} from their status. How? If a {\i mamzer} marries a {\i shifcha} (a Canaanite slave woman), the child is an {\i eved} \u8212- a slave \u8212- and the {\i mamzer} status is overridden or displaced by the slave status. If that child is subsequently freed, the person is a free Jew with no {\i mamzer} status. This practice was in fact suggested as a practical ruling by Rav Moshe Feinstein.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then notes that from the {\i shifcha} case it might appear that the child follows the mother exclusively \u8212- the child of a slave woman is a slave, which seems to say you go by the mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minymin Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef\u8217's challenge is further developed by a {\i baraita} about Minymin, a first-generation {\i Mitzri} convert who was a student of Rabbi Akiva. Minymin said: \u8220"I am a first-generation {\i Mitzri}, and I married a first-generation {\i Mitzrit}. I intend to have my son marry a second-generation {\i Mitzrit}, so that my grandson will be fit to enter {\i kahal}.\u8221" The critical element here is that Minymin planned for his son \u8212- who as the child of two first-generation {\i Mitzrim} is himself second generation \u8212- to marry a second-generation {\i Mitzrit}. The assumption is that the child of a second-generation father and a second-generation mother would be third generation, and therefore permitted. But this only works if both parents\u8217' status matters. If you simply followed the father, Minymin\u8217's son could marry even a first-generation {\i Mitzrit} \u8212- the child would still be third generation by the father\u8217's reckoning. The fact that Minymin insists on a second-generation mother demonstrates that you do not simply follow the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Emends\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan responds to this challenge by amending the text: {\i l\u8217'tana tani rishona} \u8212- emend the {\i baraita} to read that Minymin said his son could marry even a first-generation {\i Mitzrit}, consistent with the view that you always follow the father. Rabbi Yochanan is revising the wording of the {\i baraita} to conform to his own ruling, rather than taking the {\i baraita} as evidence against his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dimi - Follow Mother?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Dimi arrived from Eretz Yisrael, he transmitted a different version of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling: {\i Mitzri sheni shenassa Mitzrit rishona beno sheni} \u8212- a second-generation {\i Mitzri} man who married a first-generation {\i Mitzrit} woman, the son is second generation. This is the opposite conclusion from what Rabba bar bar Chana cited in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's name. In this version, the child is second generation \u8212- apparently because the mother is first generation, which is the more severe status. The Gemara reads this as implying that you follow the mother: {\i alma basar imeh shadinan lei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rav Dimi: if Rabbi Yochanan truly holds that you go by the mother, this contradicts another ruling of Rabbi Yochanan concerning a {\i chatas} that was pregnant when sanctified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is this: if one set aside a female animal as a {\i chatas} while it was pregnant, and it subsequently gave birth, one may use either the mother or the offspring as the {\i chatas}. Rabbi Yochanan holds that this works because {\i ubar lav yerech imo hu} \u8212- the fetus is not considered a limb of the mother; it has an independent identity even while in the womb. Therefore when the pregnant animal was sanctified, there were effectively two animals sanctified: the mother and the fetus as an independent entity. This is like sanctifying two animals {\i le\u8217'achrayut} \u8212- as mutual backups \u8212- in which case one may use either one, and the other grazes until it acquires a blemish and is redeemed. Rabbi Oshaya rules that if two {\i chataot} are designated as backups for each other, one may atone with one of them and the other grazes until it is redeemed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ubar Yerech Imo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle {\i ubar lav yerech imo hu} \u8212- the fetus is not a limb or extension of the mother but has its own independent identity \u8212- is what enables this ruling. If, by contrast, {\i ubar yerech imo hu} \u8212- the fetus were merely part of the mother\u8217's body \u8212- then the fetus would be in the status of {\i velad chatas}, a child born to a sanctified animal, and {\i velad chatas lemissa azil} \u8212- such an offspring must be left to die and may not be used as a {\i korban}. The fact that Rabbi Yochanan permits using the offspring shows he holds {\i ubar lav yerech imo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's argument is as follows: if the fetus has an independent identity \u8212- separate from the mother even while in the womb \u8212- then its personal status should not follow the mother. The independent entity of the fetus ought to follow the father, consistent with the general patrilineal principle. How, then, can Rabbi Yochanan simultaneously hold {\i ubar lav yerech imo} and also that the {\i psul} follows the mother?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It must be said that this is a striking association. The principle of {\i ubar yerech imo} concerns whether a fetus is viewed as a separate legal entity for purposes of sanctification, ritual status of {\i kodshim}, and similar matters. Why would the same concept govern whether a child\u8217's national or ethnic {\i psul} follows the father or the mother? The application seems genuinely difficult to understand. Yet the Gemara draws precisely this connection \u8212- and it is worth flagging that the Gemara itself, in debates about abortion and other {\i peshat} applications of {\i ubar yerech imo}, does not typically invoke this principle in the same framework. Here, the Gemara is forcing an association that, as the Gemara itself seems aware, requires explanation. Nevertheless, that is the argument Abaye presents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dimi\u8217's Pasuk Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the challenge, Abaye himself suggests the resolution: {\i dilma shani hasam dichtiv asher yivaldu} \u8212- perhaps by {\i Mitzri} it is different, because the verse says {\i asher yivaldu}, \u8220"that will be born,\u8221" and {\i hakatuv talao b\u8217'leida} \u8212- the Torah has made the matter dependent on the act of birth. Since the verse focuses on the birthing, perhaps the Torah is specifically indicating that here, uniquely, the status follows the mother \u8212- she is the one who gives birth \u8212- even though in general {\i ubar lav yerech imo} would seem to point the other way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Normally Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Dimi responds with warm acknowledgment: {\i karkafna chaziti lreishach beini amudei} \u8212- I saw your head among the pillars of the study hall, meaning: you were there with Rabbi Yochanan when this was taught. And indeed, {\i ki amar Rav Yochanan lha shmata} \u8212- when Rabbi Yochanan taught this ruling, {\i taama dichtiv asher yivaldu} \u8212- the reason he gave was precisely the verse {\i asher yivaldu}, the focus on birthing. And he explicitly added: {\i ha be\u8217'alma basar avuh shadinan lei} \u8212- in all other cases, you follow the father. The rule here is a local exception derived from the specific formulation of the verse, not a general principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the Gemara\u8217's entire discussion oscillates between three apparent positions: always following the father, always following the mother, and following the more severe of the two {\i psulim}. The resolution, as the Gemara will ultimately frame it, is that in general you do indeed follow the {\i psul} \u8212- the more severe of the two parental statuses \u8212- but by {\i Mitzri} and {\i Adomi} there is the additional local rule derived from {\i asher yivaldu} that anchors the status particularly in the birth, and thus the mother. The discussion of the {\i chatas} and {\i ubar yerech imo} in this connection, though logically puzzling, is the Gemara\u8217's vehicle for arriving at this conclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jews Follow Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Males Determine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Ravin arrived from Eretz Yisrael, he cited a third version of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's teaching, one that synthesizes the various strands: {\i ba\u8217'umot halech achar hazachar} \u8212- among non-Jews, before conversion, you follow the male. When two individuals of different national backgrounds \u8212- say, a {\i Mitzri} and an {\i Ammonit}, or any two nationalities \u8212- have a child, and neither parent is Jewish, the child\u8217's national identity follows the father. This is the straightforward expression of the patrilineal principle in a context of non-Jewish nationality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Canaanite Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is derived from a {\i baraita}: {\i minayin le\u8217'achad min ha\u8217'umot sheba al hakena\u8217'anit veholid ben} \u8212- from where do we know that a man of another nation who had relations with a Canaanite woman and produced a child \u8212- that child is not Canaanite and you may purchase him as a slave? The verse states: {\i vegam mibnei hatoshavim hagerim imachem mehem tiknu} \u8212- from the settlers and sojourners who dwell among you, from them you may purchase. These are men of other nations settling among the Canaanites and having children with Canaanite women. Those children are not Canaanite, and may be acquired as slaves \u8212- whereas a Canaanite would have to be killed. This demonstrates that among the nations, identity follows the father: the child of a non-Canaanite father is not Canaanite even if born of a Canaanite mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, one might have thought the reverse \u8212- that a Canaanite man who had children with a woman of another nation would produce Canaanite children. The verse {\i asher holidu b\u8217'artzechem} \u8212- that were born in your land \u8212- implies that the children are born where the mothers are from, and those born in the land of another nation (through a Canaanite father with a foreign mother) are not of the same national status as Canaanites born locally. This reinforces: among non-Jews, identity follows the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Worse Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once they have converted, however, the rule changes: {\i nitgeiru halech achar pagum she\u8217'bishneihem} \u8212- after conversion, you follow the worse status of the two parents. Whatever {\i psul} is weightier between the two parents is the one that determines the child\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender-Dependent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies this with a concrete example. Suppose it is an {\i Amoni} man who married a {\i Mitzrit} woman. What is the worse status? The answer depends on the sex of the child, and this is where the analysis becomes nuanced. If the child is male, then being {\i Amoni} is the worse outcome \u8212- an {\i Amoni} male is forbidden to {\i kahal Hashem} for all generations. Being {\i Mitzri} is better since it is only two generations. So for a male child, the worse status is {\i Amoni}, and the child is classified as {\i Amoni} and forbidden entirely. If the child is female, however, then being {\i Amoni} is in a sense not a {\i psul} at all \u8212- {\i Ammonit} women are entirely permitted. Being {\i Mitzrit} is actually worse for a female, because she carries the two-generation restriction. So a female child of this union would be classified as a second-generation {\i Mitzrit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In other words, both parental identities are present in the child, and the one that generates the greater actual disability in that particular case \u8212- given the child\u8217's sex \u8212- is the one that governs. The conclusion, {\i halach achar ha\u8217'psul}, is thus not a simple mechanical rule but must be applied with attention to the specific configuration of both parents and the sex of the child.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzer Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the final sugya of the daf. The Mishnah states: {\i mamzerim u-netinim asurim be-issur olam echad zecharim ve-echad nekevot} \u8212- {\i mamzerim} and {\i netinim} are forbidden forever, and this applies equally to males and females. The {\i netinim} are the Gibeonites who converted; their precise status \u8212- whether the prohibition against them is biblical or rabbinic \u8212- is a matter for subsequent discussion. The {\i mamzer} prohibition, of course, is well established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resh Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this Mishnah, Resh Lakish advances a startling position: {\i mamzeret le-achar asarah dorot muteret} \u8212- a female {\i mamzer} becomes permitted after ten generations. This is undeniably a bold claim given the explicit language of the Mishnah, and it demands careful examination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish learns this through a {\i gezeira shavah} connecting {\i mamzer} to {\i Ammoni} and {\i Moavi}. The verse concerning {\i Ammoni} and {\i Moavi} reads: {\i lo yavo Ammoni u-Moavi b\u8217'kahal Hashem gam dor asiri lo yavo lahem b\u8217'kahal Hashem ad olam} \u8212- they shall not enter the congregation of Hashem; even the tenth generation shall not enter the congregation of Hashem forever. Here, {\i dor asiri} (tenth generation) functions as a gloss on {\i ad olam} (forever): what \u8220"ten generations\u8221" means is \u8220"forever.\u8221" By {\i mamzer}, the verse says: {\i lo yavo mamzer b\u8217'kahal Hashem gam dor asiri lo yavo lo b\u8217'kahal Hashem} \u8212- the same formulation of {\i dor asiri} appears, but without {\i ad olam}. Resh Lakish proposes to borrow the {\i ad olam} from {\i Ammoni} and {\i Moavi} and apply it to {\i mamzer} via this {\i gezeira shavah}. But \u8212- and this is the crux \u8212- the {\i ad olam} of {\i Ammoni} and {\i Moavi} applies only to the males of those nations: {\i Ammoni v\u8217'lo Ammonit}. Women of {\i Ammon} and {\i Moav} are permitted. Therefore, when we extend {\i mamzer} to {\i ad olam} via this {\i gezeira shavah}, we can only extend the male {\i mamzer}. Female {\i mamzerim}, after the tenth generation, are no longer covered by the {\i ad olam} and become permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Shavah Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reconciles Resh Lakish\u8217's position with the Mishnah, which states that males and females are equally forbidden {\i ad olam}, by reference to a classical dispute about the methodology of {\i gezeira shavah}. There are two approaches: {\i don minah u-minah} \u8212- you take the learned principle together with its original context \u8212- and {\i don minah ve-okei be-atrah} \u8212- you derive the principle from the {\i gezeira shavah} but then anchor it in the new context. If you follow {\i don minah u-minah}, you take the {\i ad olam} of {\i Ammoni} and {\i Moavi} along with its original restriction to males, and apply that to {\i mamzer}: males are forbidden forever, females are not. If you follow {\i don minah ve-okei be-atrah}, you take the {\i ad olam} from {\i Ammoni} and {\i Moavi}, but plant it in the soil of {\i mamzer} \u8212- where the context is males and females equally \u8212- and then {\i ad olam} applies equally to both sexes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Compromise Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah, then, follows {\i don minah ve-okei be-atrah}, and Resh Lakish follows {\i don minah u-minah}. The {\i gezeira shavah} itself operates on the word {\i asiri}: both verses contain {\i dor asiri}, and from this shared term the {\i ad olam} is extended to {\i mamzer}. The practical upshot of Resh Lakish\u8217's position is that the first ten generations of both male and female {\i mamzerim} are explicitly forbidden by the verse; beyond ten generations, the {\i ad olam} extension applies only to males.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer Cryptic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites an enigmatic exchange: {\i sha\u8217'alu et Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- they asked Rabbi Eliezer, what is the status of a {\i mamzer} after ten generations? He answered: {\i mi yiten li dor shelishi va-ataharenu} \u8212- if I had a third generation in hand, I would purify them, make them permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzerim Don\u8217't Live\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains: {\i amar kasavar mamzera lo chaya} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i mamzerim} simply do not live long enough to reach many generations. This is a metaphysical proposition: God, as it were, would never allow a situation in which unwitting Jews might transgress by marrying {\i mamzerim} whose status has been forgotten. Therefore divine providence ensures that {\i mamzerim} do not survive beyond a small number of generations. When Rabbi Eliezer says \u8220"if I had a third generation I would purify them,\u8221" he means: you are asking me about ten generations \u8212- but they won\u8217't even reach three. And Rav Huna similarly holds: {\i mamzera lo chaya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if so, why does the Mishnah speak of {\i mamzerim} being forbidden {\i ad olam}? Presumably the Mishnah implies they do in fact persist. The Mishnah\u8217's language of eternal prohibition must be addressing the normative rule, even if in practice the situation is mitigated by providence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeira\u8217's Nuance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira offers a more precise formulation of this principle, transmitted from Rav Yehuda. The rule is not absolute: {\i d\u8217'yedya chaya} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} who is publicly known does live, because there is no danger of unwitting transgression. The community is aware of his status and will not intermarry with him. {\i D\u8217'lo yedya lo chaya} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} who is unknown to the community dies, because the danger of inadvertent transgression is immediate. {\i D\u8217'yedya v\u8217'd\u8217'lo yedya} \u8212- one who is somewhat known, where there is uncertainty, {\i ad telata dara chaya} \u8212- lives up to three generations, because for one or two generations the memory of his status will keep people wary, but by the third generation the memory will have faded entirely, and at that point he will die.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes this section with a striking story. There was a {\i mamzer} who lived in the neighborhood of Rav Ami. Rav Ami investigated and determined that this person was indeed a {\i mamzer}, and he made a public announcement to that effect. The man wept: what have you done to me by announcing my status to all? Rav Ami said to him: {\i chayim natati lach} \u8212- I have given you life. Had your status remained unknown, you would have died \u8212- God\u8217's providence would have ensured it, to prevent the community from inadvertently transgressing. Now that everyone knows you are a {\i mamzer}, you will live. The announcement of his {\i mamzer} status was itself an act of kindness, preserving his life by transforming him from an unknown {\i mamzer} into a known one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We will pick up tomorrow with the discussion of {\i netinim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 79\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Givonim & David\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Netinim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 78b, following a relatively brief discussion in the Gemara concerning {\i mamzer} status. The Mishnah now turns to the {\i netinim} \u8212- the designation applied to the {\i givonim}, the Gibeonites, who are forbidden in marriage. The {\i givonim} were members of one of the {\i shivat ha-amamim}, the seven Canaanite nations, who had deceived Yehoshua into making a pact with them. They are assumed to have converted at some point \u8212- otherwise they would simply be non-Jews \u8212- and so the question becomes one of their status as a distinct group even after conversion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Titchaten Bam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might suggest that the source of the prohibition is the biblical verse {\i lo titchaten bam} \u8212- the prohibition against intermarrying with the seven Canaanite nations. There is an earlier discussion in the tractate regarding whether this prohibition applies even after the {\i shivat ha-amamim} have converted. That debate bears on the {\i givonim} as well, but the Gemara now proceeds to explore an additional basis for the prohibition, rooted in a later episode in the narrative of Tanakh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David\u8217's Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces the following teaching: {\i Amar Rabbi Chana bar Adda: netinim David gazar aleihem} \u8212- it was David who issued a decree concerning the {\i netinim}, presumably prohibiting intermarriage with them. The proof-text is drawn from Shmuel, where the narrative of a famine in David\u8217's time opens: {\i vayikra hamelech lagivonim vayomer aleihem} \u8212- the king called the Gibeonites and spoke to them. The verse also notes, {\i veha-givonim lo mibnei Yisrael heim} \u8212- they were not of the Jewish people. The question the Gemara then pursues is: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama gazar aleihem} \u8212- why did David enact this decree?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Year Famine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for the decree emerges from the account of a three-year famine during David\u8217's reign: {\i vayehi ra\u8217'av bimei David shalosh shanim shana acharei shana} \u8212- there was a famine, year after year, for three consecutive years. The Gemara understands that David actively investigated the cause each of those years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first year, David told the people: {\i shema ovdei avodah zarah yesh bachem} \u8212- perhaps there are idolaters among you. The scriptural basis is the verse warning that serving other gods will cause God to close the heavens and withhold rain. They investigated \u8212- {\i badku velo matzu} \u8212- and found nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second year, he said: {\i shema ovrei aveirah yesh bachem} \u8212- perhaps there are sexual sinners among you. The verse cited describes God withholding the rains and invokes the imagery of the brazen forehead of a wayward woman \u8212- a punishment associated with sexual sin. Again they investigated and found nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the third year, he said: {\i shema porskei tzedakah b\u8217'rabim yesh bachem ve\u8217'einam notnim} \u8212- perhaps there are those who make public pledges at an appeal and do not fulfill them. The verse speaks of clouds and wind that promise rain but deliver none, analogous to the person who praises himself with a false donation. They investigated and again found nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Inquiry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point David declared, {\i ein hadavar taluy ela bi} \u8212- it must be up to me to determine the cause \u8212- and immediately sought God directly: {\i vayevakesh David et pnei Hashem}. The Gemara asks how David sought God in this manner, and Resh Lakish answers: {\i she-sha\u8217'al ba-Urim veTummim} \u8212- he inquired through the Urim and Thummim. Rabbi Elazar confirms this through a {\i gezerah shavah} on the word {\i pnei}: the verse here says {\i vayevakesh David et pnei Hashem}, and elsewhere the Torah says regarding the Urim and Thummim, {\i vesha\u8217'alu lo b\u8217'mishpat ha-Urim lifnei Hashem} \u8212- the shared word {\i pnei} / {\i lifnei} connects the two passages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God\u8217's response was: {\i Vayomer Hashem sha\u8217'ul v\u8217'el beit hadamim} \u8212- it is on account of Shaul and the house of blood, {\i al asher hemit hagivonim} \u8212- because he killed the Gibeonites. The Gemara then identifies two causes implicit in this divine response: (1) {\i al Shaul shelo nispas ke-halacha} \u8212- Shaul was not properly eulogized, and (2) the killing of the Gibeonites. The motivation to read a secondary theme of the eulogy into this passage comes from the end of the narrative in Shmuel, where David retrieves and reinters the bones of Shaul and Yehonatan alongside those of the executed sons \u8212- indicating that the proper treatment of Shaul\u8217's remains was part of what this entire episode addressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saul\u8217's Culpability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i ve-chi heichan matzinu b\u8217'Shaul shehemit et hagivonim} \u8212- where do we ever find explicitly that Shaul killed the Gibeonites? The verse in this story is the first such mention. The Gemara answers: {\i ela mitoch sheharag Nov ir Kohanim} \u8212- because Shaul destroyed Nov, the city of priests. The Gibeonites, who served the priests as hewers of wood and drawers of water \u8212- a role assigned to them by Yehoshua and later connected to the altar \u8212- were thereby deprived of their livelihood and sustenance. {\i Ma\u8217'aleh alav ha-katuv k\u8217'ilu haragam} \u8212- Scripture accounts it to Shaul as though he had killed them outright. This reading notably softens what the plain reading of the Shmuel narrative implies \u8212- that Shaul had actually attempted to decimate the Gibeonites \u8212- reducing it to an indirect harm caused by the destruction of those they depended upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an apparent difficulty: the text assigns punishment for two things simultaneously \u8212- Shaul\u8217's lack of proper eulogy, and the harm done to the Gibeonites. How can both be true together? Is Shaul a victim in one sense and a perpetrator in another? Resh Lakish resolves this through a verse: {\i bikshu et Hashem kol anvei ha-aretz asher mishpato sham pa\u8217'alu} \u8212- seek God, all the humble of the earth, where God\u8217's justice is, there too are God\u8217's righteous deeds. The same point where justice is exacted is also where the person\u8217's due is rendered. David reflected that Shaul had gone twelve months \u8212- indeed thirty years \u8212- without a proper {\i hesped}, and that while the formal mourning period had long passed and it was no longer the ideal time for a eulogy, {\i velo dacha le-mispedei} \u8212- he had not pressed to make it happen. His more immediate task was to address the Gibeonites: {\i nikrina u-nepaysinu} \u8212- let us call them and attempt to appease them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Givonim Demand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The king called the Gibeonites and asked: {\i mah e\u8217'eseh lachem u-bamah achaper} \u8212- what shall I do for you and how can atonement be made? The Gibeonites responded that they wanted neither silver nor gold, nor any claim against the broader Jewish people \u8212- only seven of Shaul\u8217's sons: {\i yutan lanu shiv\u8217'ah anashim mibanav ve-hokanum le-Hashem} \u8212- give us seven of his sons, and we will impale them before God. David attempted to negotiate some other form of appeasement \u8212- {\i mifas lo faysinu} \u8212- but they would not be moved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewish Virtues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Confronted with this display of implacable cruelty, David reflected on the defining characteristics of the Jewish people. He declared: {\i shloshah simanim yesh b\u8217'umah zu} \u8212- the Jewish nation has three identifying marks: {\i ha-rachmanim} \u8212- they are compassionate, {\i ha-bayshanim} \u8212- they are capable of shame and embarrassment, and {\i gomlei chasadim} \u8212- they perform acts of loving-kindness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each characteristic is grounded in a scriptural source. Compassion: {\i ve-nasan lecha rachamim ve-richamcha} \u8212- God will instill compassion in you. Shame: {\i ba-avur tihyeh yir\u8217'aso al pneichem le-vilti techetau} \u8212- God\u8217's awe should be upon your faces so that you do not sin, implying a non-brazen, humble bearing. Kindness: {\i le-ma\u8217'an asher yetzaveh et banav ve-et beiso acharav ve-shamru derech Hashem la\u8217'asos tzedakah u-mishpat} \u8212- to walk in God\u8217's ways and do righteousness and justice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle then follows: {\i kol she-yesh bo shloshah simanim elalu ra\u8217'uy le-hidabek b\u8217'umah zu} \u8212- whoever possesses these three traits is fit to attach themselves to the Jewish people. For a nation that lacks them, intermarriage is not appropriate even if they have converted and are technically Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion: Unfit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation \u8212- made in the context of the Gibeonites\u8217' heartless demand \u8212- is what leads David to his conclusion. Having seen their cruelty firsthand, David determined that they were unfit to intermarry with Israel, and {\i gazar aleihem} \u8212- he issued his decree. Some derive the prohibition from the very verse introducing the Gibeonites in this narrative, {\i ve-ha-givonim lo mibnei Yisrael heim} \u8212- not merely that they were not originally of Israel, but that they were never to be integrated into the people through marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos identifies three possible understandings of what David\u8217's {\i gezeirah} actually entailed. One view holds that the biblical prohibition of {\i lo titchaten bam} ceases to apply after the {\i shivat ha-amamim} convert, and David\u8217's {\i gezeirah} was a rabbinic or extra-legal decree filling that gap \u8212- prohibiting intermarriage even post-conversion. A second view suggests that David\u8217's contribution was to extend Yehoshua\u8217's designation of the Gibeonites as servants beyond the specific conditions Yehoshua had set, and since slaves cannot be married into the congregation, the marriage prohibition follows indirectly from their servitude. A third view, that the biblical prohibition applies even after conversion, would leave David\u8217's decree focused purely on the question of servitude rather than intermarriage directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The plain sense of the Gemara here, it should be noted, concerns only marriage \u8212- there is no hint in this passage of the servitude question. The discussion is entirely embedded in a tractate about who may or may not marry whom. The servitude dimension will enter later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Execution & Kiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Selection Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The seven descendants of Shaul were identified through a specific selection process. The verse specifies that David took the two sons of Ritzpah daughter of Ayah \u8212- Armoni and Mephiboshes \u8212- as well as five sons of Michal daughter of Shaul whom she bore to Adriel ben Barzillai ha-Mecholasi. This Mephiboshes is not the son of Yehonasan, as the verse immediately prior notes that David had compassion on Yehonasan\u8217's son Mephiboshes on account of the oath he had sworn to Yehonasan \u8212- there were two individuals of that name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna teaches that the selection of which seven to hand over was determined by passing them before the {\i aron}: {\i kol she-ha-aron kolto le-misah} \u8212- whoever was drawn in by the Ark was designated for death; {\i kol she-ein ha-aron kolto le-chayim} \u8212- whoever was not drawn in was spared. This parallels a similar methodology used in another episode. The Gemara then raises a difficulty from the verse {\i vayachmol ha-melech al Mephiboshes ben Yehonasan} \u8212- the king had compassion on Mephiboshes son of Yehonasan \u8212- which implies that it was David\u8217's personal discretion rather than an objective test that determined his fate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this in stages. First it is suggested that the compassion was simply that David did not submit Yehonasan\u8217's Mephiboshes to the test at all \u8212- that was the act of mercy. But then it is objected: {\i ve-chi maso panim yesh badavar} \u8212- is it permissible to show favoritism in capital matters? One ought to let God decide. The Gemara then revises: Mephiboshes was in fact submitted to the test, but David prayed that he not be drawn in \u8212- {\i bikaesh alav rachamim shelo yikletenu ha-aron} \u8212- and he was spared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the objection resurfaces: {\i ve-achti maso panim yesh badavar} \u8212- even that involves favoritism, since sparing one through prayer effectively sends another in his place. Rashi articulates this point with striking force: by praying that Mephiboshes be released, David was implicitly assigning someone else to death in his stead. The Gemara ultimately resolves this by clarifying that David\u8217's prayer preceded the test entirely \u8212- he prayed from the outset that Mephiboshes would not be drawn in, rather than praying for his release after the Ark had already claimed him, meaning no other individual was displaced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children Punished\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks the obvious question: {\i ve-haktiv lo yumtu avot al banim} \u8212- does the Torah not prohibit putting children to death for the sins of their fathers? These seven sons were being executed for what Shaul had done. Rabbi Chiya bar Abba said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i mutav she-te\u8217'aker ot achat min ha-Torah u-val yitchalel Shem Shamayim be-farhesya} \u8212- it is better that one letter of the Torah be uprooted than that the divine name be publicly desecrated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have supposed that executing innocent people is itself a desecration of the divine name \u8212- but Rashi explains the calculus differently. The concern was what the nations would say: {\i she-yehu ha-umot omrim ein umah zo re-uyah le-hidabek bah} \u8212- this is not a nation worthy of joining, because they allowed these converts to be killed without their food being provided and without any vengeance being taken on their behalf. The perception of Israel as a nation that abandoned vulnerable converts in their midst would constitute a {\i chilul Hashem}. To prevent that, even a violation of the prohibition against punishing children for their fathers\u8217' sins was permitted \u8212- a genuinely radical application of the concern for how Israel presents itself to the outside world, and one where the concern extends specifically to non-Jewish perception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ritzpah\u8217's Vigil\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the execution, Ritzpah daughter of Ayah, the mother of two of the executed sons, took sackcloth and spread it by the rock, keeping vigil from the beginning of the harvest season until rain finally fell: {\i lo natnah of ha-shamayim lanoach aleihem yomam ve-chayat ha-sadeh laylah} \u8212- she did not let the birds of the heaven rest on the bodies by day, nor the beasts of the field approach them by night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises another halakhic problem: {\i ve-haktiv lo talin nivlato al ha-etz} \u8212- does the Torah not prohibit leaving a body hanging on a tree overnight? Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yehotzadak answers with the same formulation: {\i mutav she-te\u8217'aker ot achat min ha-Torah ve-yitkadesh Shem Shamayim be-farhesya} \u8212- it is better that one letter of the Torah be uprooted than that the divine name be desecrated in public. Here the argument inverts: what is gained by leaving the bodies exposed is not the avoidance of {\i chilul Hashem} but the active achievement of {\i kiddush Hashem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddush Hashem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism of {\i kiddush Hashem} is spelled out explicitly. Passersby would stop and ask: {\i mah tivan shel elu} \u8212- who are these people? Why are they hanging here? And they would be told: {\i hallalu b\u8217'nei melachim hem} \u8212- these are the sons of kings, princes. {\i U-mah asu} \u8212- and what did they do? {\i Pashtu yadeihem be-geirim gerurim} \u8212- they acted against lowly converts. The word {\i gerurim} here refers to their status as converts who had entered under deception \u8212- not the most sincere or prestigious of converts, yet still the objects of such punitive concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion drawn by the onlookers: {\i ein le-cha umah she-re-uyah le-hidabek bah ke-zo} \u8212- there is no nation as worthy of joining as this one. {\i U-mah b\u8217'nei melachim kach} \u8212- if that is what they do to princes for harming such people, {\i b\u8217'nei hedyotot al achat kamah ve-chamah} \u8212- how much more would they demand of ordinary citizens who harm the vulnerable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The narrative is striking in how it inverts the moral positions established earlier. Shaul had committed a terrible act against the Gibeonites. Yet ultimately, it is the Gibeonites themselves who are shown to be heartless \u8212- demanding the lives of seven men \u8212- while Israel emerges as the nation of exceptional ethical standards. Tosafos notes a beautiful {\i midrash} on the phrase {\i va-tatehu lah el ha-tzur}: the word {\i ha-tzur} alludes to the {\i tzidduk ha-din}, the acceptance of divine judgment \u8212- {\i Ha-Tzur Tamim Pe\u8217'alo} \u8212- suggesting that Ritzpah, in her grief, affirmed the justice of what had occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Converts Arrive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As a direct result of this demonstration of Israel\u8217's moral standards, 150,000 people converted: {\i nisvasfu Yisrael me\u8217'ah va-chamishim elef}. The scriptural basis is drawn from the account of Shlomo\u8217's workforce: {\i vayihi li-Shlomo shiv\u8217'im elef nosei saval u-shmonim elef chotzeiv ba-har} \u8212- 70,000 burden-bearers and 80,000 hewers in the mountain, for the construction of the Beit HaMikdash.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara questions whether these might simply have been native-born Israelites. The objection is raised: {\i u-mibnei Yisrael lo nasan Shlomo eved} \u8212- Shlomo did not impose servitude on the children of Israel \u8212- yet this workforce was engaged in labor that resembles servitude, so they must have come from the gentile nations. A further verse makes this explicit: {\i vayispor Shlomo kol ha-anashim ha-geirim asher be-eretz Yisrael vayimmatze\u8217'u me\u8217'ah va-chamishim elef} \u8212- Shlomo counted all the resident aliens in the land of Israel and found 150,000, placing 70,000 as burden-bearers and 80,000 as hewers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Netinim Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua\u8217's Decree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the legal status of the {\i netinim} in terms of servitude, tracing its origins back to Yehoshua. The verse records: {\i vayitneim Yehoshua ba-yom ha-hu chotevei eitzim ve-sho\u8217'avei mayim la-eidah u-le-mizbeach Hashem} \u8212- Yehoshua designated them on that day as hewers of wood and drawers of water for the congregation and for the altar of God. This is the foundational act that established the Gibeonites as servants in relation to the Beit HaMikdash.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe vs.\u160 ?Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks whether Moshe might have preceded Yehoshua in this designation, citing the verse {\i mi-chotev eitzecha ad sho\u8217'ev meimecha} \u8212- from the hewer of your wood to the drawer of your water \u8212- which appears in the Torah itself. The Gemara thus suggests that Moshe had already in some sense identified a people for this role, even before the conquest of Canaan and before the episode with the Gibeonites. The question of how this works precisely \u8212- since the Gibeonites had not yet made their pact with Yehoshua in Moshe\u8217's time \u8212- is left somewhat open. The conclusion is: {\i Moshe gazar le-hu dara} \u8212- Moshe\u8217's decree was for that generation only; {\i David gazar le-chulei dara} \u8212- David extended it to all future generations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David\u8217's Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yehoshua\u8217's decree, the Gemara explains, was conditioned on the existence of the Temple: {\i Yehoshua gazar bi-zman she-Beit HaMikdash kayam} \u8212- the verse says {\i le-mizbeach Hashem}, \u8220"for the altar of God,\u8221" tying the servitude to the Temple\u8217's operation. Since there was no Temple in David\u8217's time \u8212- only David extended their status to periods when there is no Temple: {\i David gazar bi-zman she-ein Beit HaMikdash kayam}. This extension is what David added beyond Yehoshua\u8217's designation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what exactly David was extending \u8212- the servitude status, or the prohibition of intermarriage \u8212- becomes complicated here. The Gemara seems to intertwine the two, and Tosafos proposes that the intermarriage prohibition may follow naturally from slave status, since one cannot marry a slave. The simpler reading of the earlier Gemara, however, would suggest that David\u8217's decree was directly about intermarriage, with no dependence on the slave question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repeal Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the time of Rabbi, an attempt was made to rescind the {\i netinim} status: {\i bikshu le-hatir nesinim} \u8212- they sought to permit the {\i netinim}, at least by releasing them from servitude. Rabbi responded: {\i chelkeinu natia} \u8212- we can release our portion, the claim the Jewish people hold over them, through the principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker}. But {\i chelek mizbe\u8217'ach mi yatish} \u8212- who are we to release the Temple\u8217's portion? The Temple\u8217's share in their servitude is not ours to annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shares Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position is in tension with a view of Rabbi Chiya bar Abba in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i chelek adam le-olam assur} \u8212- the people\u8217's portion is forever forbidden unless released; {\i chelek mizbe\u8217'ach bi-zman she-Beit HaMikdash kayam assur} \u8212- the Temple\u8217's portion is forbidden when the Temple stands, {\i ein Beit HaMikdash kayam shari} \u8212- but when there is no Temple, the Temple\u8217's portion is no longer binding. According to this view, the people could in theory release their portion, and the Temple\u8217's portion would lapse in the absence of the Beit HaMikdash, potentially opening the door for the {\i netinim} to be freed from their status entirely \u8212- and perhaps, by extension, permitted for intermarriage. The Gemara remains somewhat ambiguous about whether the ultimate focus is on servitude or on the marriage prohibition, with the two questions running through each other throughout this passage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Saris & Ailonit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saris Traditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah now shifts to a discussion of {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} as they apply to a {\i saris} \u8212- a eunuch \u8212- and to an {\i ailonit}, a woman who cannot bear children. Rabbi Yehoshua opens with a declaration: {\i shamati she-saris choletz} \u8212- I have heard that a {\i saris} performs {\i chalitzah}, and {\i choltzin le-ishto} \u8212- that one performs {\i chalitzah} with the wife of a {\i saris} who dies. This runs against intuition: if a man cannot have children, why should he or his wife be relevant to {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}, whose entire purpose is {\i le-hakim shem achiv} \u8212- to perpetuate the name of the deceased brother?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua then adds: {\i ve-saris lo choletz ve-lo choltzin le-ishto} \u8212- I also heard the opposite, that a {\i saris} neither performs {\i chalitzah} nor is {\i chalitzah} performed with his wife. He acknowledges he cannot reconcile the two traditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva, Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's student, offers to resolve the contradiction: {\i ani afareish} \u8212- I will explain. {\i Saris adam choletz ve-choltzin le-ishto} \u8212- a man who became a {\i saris} through human agency performs {\i chalitzah} and one performs {\i chalitzah} with his wife. {\i Saris chamah lo choletz ve-lo choltzin le-ishto} \u8212- a man who is a {\i saris} by nature does not perform {\i chalitzah} and one does not perform {\i chalitzah} with his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sha\u8217'at Kosher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for Rabbi Akiva\u8217's distinction is the concept of {\i sha\u8217'at kosher} \u8212- a prior moment of valid capacity. A {\i saris adam} \u8212- one whose condition was caused by a later physical event, analogous to {\i ptzua daka} or {\i khrut shofcha} \u8212- at least began life with the capacity to father children. Even if that capacity has been entirely and permanently eliminated, his personal identity is not intrinsically that of someone excluded from the category of procreation. He had {\i sha\u8217'at kosher}, and so in terms of his halakhic {\i gavra} status he remains within the {\i parshah} of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} \u8212- even though fathering children is currently and permanently impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i saris chamah}, by contrast \u8212- one born with the condition, whose entire life has been that of someone incapable of reproduction \u8212- never had {\i sha\u8217'at kosher}. His personal identity is intrinsically that of someone excluded from the category, and therefore {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} do not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer, Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's colleague and a fellow teacher of Rabbi Akiva, disagrees and inverts the ruling. {\i Saris chamah choletz ve-choltzin le-ishto} \u8212- a natural {\i saris} performs {\i chalitzah} and one performs {\i chalitzah} with his wife, {\i mipnei she-yesh lo refua} \u8212- because his condition may be curable. If the incapacity arose from natural circumstances \u8212- a condition one was born into \u8212- perhaps it is something that could yet resolve itself. {\i Saris adam lo choletz ve-lo choltzin le-ishto mipnei she-ein lo refua} \u8212- a {\i saris adam} cannot perform {\i chalitzah} and one does not perform it with his wife, because when something anatomically decisive has been done through human agency \u8212- severed, as in {\i khrut shofcha}, or damaged, as in {\i ptzua daka} \u8212- it is permanent and admits of no remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic of the two positions is thus symmetrical but opposite. Rabbi Akiva focuses on prior personal identity: was there ever a moment when this person stood in the category of one who can have children? Rabbi Eliezer focuses on present and future reality: is there any possibility, however remote, that this condition is reversible? Both agree that the immediate practical impossibility of conception is not the operative question \u8212- the {\i saris} who cannot have children right now and never will again can nonetheless, under the right analytical framework, remain within the halakhic category. What differs is what places one inside or outside that category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Magusas Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Betira testified regarding a certain Ben Magusas who lived in Jerusalem: he was a {\i saris adam}, and when he died, {\i yibum} was performed with his wife \u8212- {\i veyibemu et ishto le-kayem divrei Rabbi Akiva}. The testimony was not issued in order to affirm Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position, but it functions as corroboration. Here was an actual case of a {\i saris adam} where {\i yibum} \u8212- not merely {\i chalitzah} \u8212- was performed with the deceased\u8217's wife, consistent with Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling that {\i saris adam} retains full standing in the laws of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then presents what appears to be a later, independent formulation: {\i ha-saris lo choletz ve-lo meyabem} \u8212- the {\i saris} does not perform {\i chalitzah} and does not perform {\i yibum}. And similarly: {\i ve-chen ailonit lo choletzet ve-lo mityabemet} \u8212- an {\i ailonit} does not perform {\i chalitzah} and is not subject to {\i yibum}. This categorical statement makes no distinction between types of {\i saris} and seems to ignore the entire preceding debate among Rabbi Yehoshua, Rabbi Akiva, and Rabbi Eliezer. The most natural reading is that this was an earlier, undifferentiated {\i stam} teaching, upon which the later {\i tannaim} built their distinctions \u8212- similar to other cases in {\i Shas} where an early undifferentiated Mishnah is followed by a debate that constitutes the various {\i tannaim}\u8217's interpretations of its terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex vs.\u160 ?Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i saris} who nevertheless goes ahead and performs {\i chalitzah} with his {\i yevamah}: {\i lo pasla} \u8212- it does not invalidate her for a {\i kohen}. The act is halakhically meaningless; it accomplished nothing. But if he had sexual relations with her \u8212- {\i pasla} \u8212- he has invalidated her. Since there is no {\i mitzvat yibum} with a {\i saris}, the act is one of {\i arayot} \u8212- it constitutes {\i eshet ach} in a context where the {\i mitzvah} does not apply \u8212- and she is therefore rendered a {\i chalalah}, {\i mipnei she-hi be\u8217'ilat zenut}. It is more precise to say {\i be\u8217'ilat arayot} \u8212- an act of forbidden relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is thus clear: meaningless {\i chalitzah} leaves her status unchanged, while sexual relations in the absence of a valid {\i mitzvat yibum} renders the act prohibited and invalidates her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same framework applies to an {\i ailonit}. If one of the brothers performed {\i chalitzah} with her: {\i lo pasluha} \u8212- they did not invalidate her for a {\i kohen}, since the act was halakhically meaningless in the absence of any {\i mitzvat yibum} or {\i mitzvat chalitzah}. If they had sexual relations with her \u8212- {\i pasluha} \u8212- they invalidated her, since without {\i mitzvat yibum} the act is one of {\i arayot}, {\i mipnei she-be\u8217'ilatah be\u8217'ilat zenut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\i ailonit} is not simply a woman who is currently infertile or who has not yet conceived. The parallel to the {\i saris} is instructive: just as the relevant question for the {\i saris} is not whether conception is practically possible at this moment but whether his personal identity places him within the category, so too the {\i ailonit} must be someone whose identity is intrinsically that of a woman outside the category \u8212- not an {\i akara}, a woman who happens not to be bearing children, but an {\i ailonit}, defined by specific physical characteristics that place her categorically elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prohibition Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a striking analytical problem with Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position. We know that Rabbi Akiva holds {\i chayavei lavin ke-chayavei keritot dami} \u8212- those who violate a {\i lav} are treated as those who violate an {\i issur karet}. This means, among other consequences, that the offspring of such unions is a {\i mamzer}, that {\i kiddushin} is ineffective, and \u8212- crucially for our context \u8212- that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} is entirely excluded, just as it is excluded in cases of {\i arayot}. The very first Mishnah in the tractate established that if a woman is forbidden to her {\i yavam} by an {\i issur karet}, she is exempt from {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a {\i saris adam} \u8212- the category in which Rabbi Akiva says {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} apply \u8212- is presumably someone in the category of {\i ptzua daka u-khrut shofcha}, who is forbidden by a {\i lav} to marry a Jewish woman. If Rabbi Akiva treats a {\i lav} like an {\i issur karet}, then the {\i saris adam}\u8217's wife should be entirely excluded from {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}, not included. The contradiction is direct: {\i ve-chayvei keritot lav benei chalitzah ve-yibum ninhu} \u8212- those subject to {\i issur karet} are not within the category of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Convert Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ammi resolves the problem: {\i hakha be-mai askinan ke-gon she-nasa achiv giyoret} \u8212- we are discussing a case where the deceased brother\u8217's wife is a convert. There is no prohibition of {\i ptzua daka} applying to a {\i giyoret} \u8212- the {\i lav} of {\i lo yavo ptzua daka be-kehal Hashem} does not prohibit marriage with a convert.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kahal Gerim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva follows the position of Rabbi Yossi, who holds {\i kehal gerim lo ikrei kehal} \u8212- the congregation of converts is not considered {\i kehal} for this purpose. Therefore, the prohibition does not apply to the {\i saris adam} who married a convert, there is no {\i lav}, and consequently the relationship is not treated as {\i chayvei keritot} by Rabbi Akiva \u8212- leaving {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} applicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A follow-up question arises: if the prohibition is entirely absent in the case of a {\i giyoret}, why does the Mishnah speak only of {\i chalitzah} rather than {\i yibum} as well? If there is no {\i lav}, why not simply perform {\i yibum}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restriction Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: Rabbi Yehoshua, who reported the two traditions in the first place, would hold that the prohibition of {\i ptzua daka} applies even to {\i geirim} \u8212- he does not accept Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position that {\i kehal gerim} is not {\i kehal}. Therefore, Rabbi Yehoshua had to frame the teaching as {\i chalitzah} only, since in his view there remains a prohibition even vis-\u224 ?-vis a convert. Rabbi Akiva, by contrast, would have no objection in principle to {\i yibum} as well in this case. When Rabbi Akiva stated {\i choletz} \u8212- he used the language of Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's formulation, but in principle according to his own view, {\i yibum} could also be performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testimony Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is confirmed by the testimony regarding Ben Magusas: when Ben Magusas, a {\i saris adam}, died, {\i yibum} was performed with his wife \u8212- not merely {\i chalitzah}. The Gemara infers: {\i shema mina} \u8212- this is evidence that even {\i yibum} was considered possible for a {\i saris adam}, supporting the Gemara\u8217's conclusion that the case involves a {\i giyoret} and that there is no operative prohibition preventing {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Saris Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permission Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a {\i baraita} that lays out the full application of the laws of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} for those with physical defects or advanced age. {\i Ptzua daka u-khrut shofcha}, as well as an elderly man who is no longer capable of reproduction \u8212- whether such a person died leaving a wife, or whether he is the surviving brother obligated in {\i yibum} \u8212- the ruling is: {\i o chaltzin o meyabmin} \u8212- either {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum} may be performed. These men are not categorically excluded from the framework of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Valid Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} elaborates: whether it is the {\i saris adam} or elderly man who died and left a {\i yevamah} behind for his brothers, or whether he is the living brother, all legal acts are fully valid. {\i Maamar}, {\i get}, {\i chalitzah} \u8212- and {\i yibum} itself \u8212- are all binding and effective: {\i mah she-asu asui}. The Gemara stresses this applies regardless of whether the deceased was in this condition or the surviving brother is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prohibition Remains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i baraita} adds: {\i ve-assur le-kayanam} \u8212- they are nevertheless forbidden to remain married. Even though {\i yibum} is valid and effective, the husband cannot maintain the marriage, because the prohibition of {\i lo yavo ptzua daka u-khrut shofcha be-kehal Hashem} still applies. The Gemara notes that here we are dealing with a case where the wives are not converts \u8212- the prohibition does apply \u8212- which raises anew the question of how Rabbi Akiva can permit {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} in the presence of a {\i lav} that he treats as equivalent to an {\i issur karet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Later Defects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava proposes a resolution. The scenario described in the {\i baraita} \u8212- where {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} apply despite the {\i lav} of {\i ptzua daka} \u8212- is one where the physical condition arose after the husband\u8217's death: {\i ke-gon she-naflo lo be-sof niftzu} \u8212- the condition occurred at the end, meaning after the original obligation of {\i yibum} was already established. At the moment the brother died and the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} attached, everything was normal. The {\i saris} condition of the surviving brother arose only afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Proposal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's reasoning is that if the {\i issur} came after the {\i mitzvah} had already taken hold, it does not retroactively dissolve the obligation. The {\i yevamah} was already fully obligated in {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} at the moment of the brother\u8217's death; a condition that subsequently afflicts the surviving brother should not undo what had already been established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges this reasoning: {\i ve-dachi issur ptzua ve-nidchei issur asei de-yibum} \u8212- why should the newly arising prohibition of {\i ptzua daka} not override and displace the positive obligation of {\i yibum}, even if it came later? He brings proof from an established teaching: {\i min lo tanan Rabban Gamliel omer im mianah mianah} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel ruled regarding a man married to an underage girl whose sister fell before him for {\i yibum}. Rabban Gamliel said that if the underage wife performs {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- refusal \u8212- the {\i yibum} obligation with her sister can proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Underage Wife Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanics of Abaye\u8217's proof: when the brother died, his wife\u8217's sister was only rabbinically married to the surviving brother, not yet a full halakhic {\i ishah}. The prohibition of {\i achot isha} \u8212- a man\u8217's wife\u8217's sister \u8212- had not yet fully crystallized. At that moment, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} applied to the deceased\u8217's wife. But if the surviving brother waits until the underage girl matures, she becomes his full halakhic wife, and her sister \u8212- the {\i yevamah} \u8212- becomes his {\i achot isha}, canceling the obligation of {\i yibum}. Rabban Gamliel rules: {\i tamtin ad she-tagdil ve-tetzei halazu mishum achot isha} \u8212- wait until she grows up, and the {\i yevamah} must leave on account of the prohibition of a wife\u8217's sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prohibition Cancellation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case demonstrates that even when the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} was established at the moment of death, a prohibition that arises afterward \u8212- here {\i achot isha} \u8212- can come along and override the obligation. {\i Alema ati issur achot isha ve-dachi} \u8212- we see that a later {\i issur} can displace an earlier {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}. Therefore, says Abaye, the same should be true here: even if the {\i saris} condition arose after the brother\u8217's death, the resulting prohibition of {\i ptzua daka} should be sufficient to cancel the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}. Rava\u8217's proposed resolution is therefore inadequate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simple Lav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef provides the definitive answer: {\i hai tanna d\u8217'vei Rabbi Akiva hi de-amar me-chayavei lavin d\u8217'she\u8217'er havei mamzer, me-chayavei lavin geridei lo havei mamzer} \u8212- this teaching follows the position of the school of Rabbi Akiva, which distinguishes between two types of {\i lavin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simple Lav Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabbi Akiva says that {\i chayavei lavin} are treated like {\i chayavei keritot} \u8212- producing {\i mamzer} status and being excluded from {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} \u8212- he does not apply this to every {\i lav} universally. The prohibition of {\i ptzua daka} is a {\i lav gerei} \u8212- a simple, freestanding prohibition \u8212- that has nothing to do with kinship or relational categories. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's equation of {\i lavin} with {\i keritot} applies only in a more limited domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinship Lavim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The category of {\i lavin} that Rabbi Akiva treats as equivalent to {\i issur karet} \u8212- producing {\i mamzer} status and excluding the relationship from {\i yibum} \u8212- is specifically {\i chayavei lavin d\u8217'she\u8217'er}: prohibitions rooted in the relational category of {\i she\u8217'er}, kinship. These are the prohibitions that neighbor the domain of {\i arayot} \u8212- unions forbidden because of who the parties are to one another, not merely because of some personal defect or disability. {\i Ptzua daka} is not in this category. It is a prohibition about the man\u8217's physical status, entirely unrelated to kinship between the parties. Therefore, even Rabbi Akiva does not treat it as producing {\i mamzer} status or as excluding the couple from the framework of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}. The {\i lav} remains \u8212- they may not stay married \u8212- but it does not rise to the level of an {\i issur karet} analog that would dissolve the {\i yibum} obligation entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 80\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Saris Adam Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva: Saris Adam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling regarding the {\i saris adam} \u8212- a man who was born fertile and subsequently became infertile through some human act or accident. Rabbi Akiva holds that such a person remains within the halakhic category of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}, whether as the deceased husband or as the surviving brother, because his having been born fertile places him fundamentally in that category even if he can no longer produce children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Lav & Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a difficulty with this position. A {\i saris adam} who is a {\i ptzua daka u\u8217'krot shofcha} is subject to an {\i issur lav}. Rabbi Akiva famously treats an {\i issur lav} similarly to an {\i issur kares} with regard to the offspring being a {\i mamzer} \u8212- which would seemingly exclude such a person from the {\i parsha} of {\i yibum} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef: School Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several answers are offered, and the last of them is introduced at the bottom of 79b: {\i ela amar Rav Yosef \u8212- hai tana, tana de-bei Rabbi Akiva hi}. The {\i tanna} of our {\i mishnah}, who holds that a {\i ptzua daka} is nonetheless included in the category of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} according to Rabbi Akiva, belongs to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's own school.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Kinship Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key to understanding this answer lies in a distinction within Rabbi Akiva\u8217's own position. When Rabbi Akiva says an {\i issur lav} produces a {\i mamzer}, he means specifically an {\i issur lav} that falls under the category of {\i she\u8217'er} \u8212- a kinship-based prohibition, one rooted in a familial or quasi-familial relationship. The school of Rabbi Akiva holds: {\i mi-chayvei lavin de-she\u8217'er havei mamzer, mi-chayvei lavin geridei lo havei mamzer} \u8212- a plain {\i issur lav} unrelated to kinship does not produce a {\i mamzer}. Rashi on an earlier daf cites two examples of {\i issur lav} prohibitions that would qualify as {\i she\u8217'er}: a woman with whom one\u8217's father had relations ({\i lo yigaleh knaf aviv}), and a woman with whom one performed {\i chalitzah} ({\i keivan she-lo banah shuv lo yivneh}). By contrast, a {\i kohen} with a {\i gerushah} or our case of a {\i ptzua daka} is a plain {\i issur lav} with no kinship dimension \u8212- and therefore would not produce a {\i mamzer} and would not exclude the person from the {\i parsha} of {\i yibum}. This is a clean and straightforward resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Children Possible\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i saris adam} question settled, the Gemara turns to a broader and more fundamental challenge. Whatever the {\i saris adam}\u8217's formal status, practically speaking he cannot have children. The verse speaks of {\i le-hakim l\u8217'achiv shem} \u8212- establishing a name for his brother \u8212- and this person is not capable of doing so. The Gemara thus asks: {\i ikri kan le-hakim l\u8217'achiv shem v\u8217'ha lav bar hachi hu} \u8212- how can there be a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} when producing children is simply not possible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Moment Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds with a striking argument: {\i im ken, ein lecha isha she-keshera le-yabem} \u8212- if practical fertility at the moment is the determining criterion, then there will never be a woman who is subject to {\i yibum}. Why? {\i She-lo na\u8217'aseh ba\u8217'alah saris chamah sha\u8217'ah achat kodem le-mitato} \u8212- because a man who dies naturally, whether from old age or illness, is in all likelihood not fertile in the final moments before his death. If we required actual capacity to produce children, {\i yibum} would never apply to any normal death. Rava\u8217's proof thus establishes that we do not measure eligibility by the last moment of incapacity; we look at the person\u8217's status more broadly. A man who was once fertile and capable of having children remains categorially within the {\i parsha}, even if that capacity has since ceased.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer vs Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that for Rabbi Eliezer, Rava\u8217's argument is actually a question rather than a proof: {\i peruka de-Rava pircha hi}. Rabbi Eliezer does not rely on the principle of {\i sha\u8217'at ha-kosher} \u8212- the fact that earlier in life the person was capable. Rabbi Eliezer grounds his position in the theoretical possibility of recovery. A {\i saris chama} may potentially heal; it is that reversibility, not prior fertility, that places him in the category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temporary vs Permanent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework, one might ask: how does he respond to Rava\u8217's scenario of a man dying \u8212- since by Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's logic, a dying man is not in a theoretical state of recovery either? The Gemara answers: {\i hatam kichishuta de\u8217'atchala bei} \u8212- in a dying man, it is not that the reproductive capacity is truly broken; it is a weakening that has overtaken him. The equipment, as it were, is not itself defective, even if the person cannot in practice exercise it. The moment the physical apparatus actually breaks down, however \u8212- as with a {\i saris adam} \u8212- that is a different matter entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges is a highly formalistic ruling: as long as people fall within the category of those who were at some stage capable of producing children \u8212- or, in Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view, theoretically capable of recovery \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} applies, even if children are practically impossible at the time, even if the marriage itself produced no children, and even if the surviving brother is currently incapable. Conceptual membership in the category is what drives the obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Saris Chama Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Never Fertile Once\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the theoretical framework, the Gemara turns to defining {\i saris chama} in practical terms. Rabbi Yitzchak bar Rav Yosef in the name of Rabbi Yochanan defines it precisely: {\i kol shelo ra\u8217'ah sha\u8217'ah achat be-chashruto} \u8212- someone who never had even one moment of fertility. This definition is the hinge of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position: such a person was never in the category, and therefore Rabbi Akiva excludes him from {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Urination Arc Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: how does one identify someone who has never had that moment of fertility? Abaye offers a diagnostic: {\i kol ha-matil mayim ve-ein oseh kipa} \u8212- anyone whose urination does not form an arc. If this condition was observable even in early childhood, it indicates a permanent, congenital state rather than something acquired later in life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth Defect Causes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks what causes this condition to develop in utero. Two maternal factors during pregnancy are identified: {\i de-afya imei be-tihara} \u8212- the mother baked bread in the noonday sun, so that both the heat of the oven and the heat of the midday sun acted upon her while pregnant \u8212- and {\i ve-shashtya shikra} \u8212- she drank strong or heavily mixed intoxicating drink. These are the causes of this particular birth defect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smitten from Womb\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef adds: this is what I had heard Rav Ami teaching when he said {\i kol she-mi-me\u8217'ei imo laki} \u8212- whoever was smitten from his mother\u8217's womb \u8212- and I did not know what he was referring to. Now I understand: he was identifying the {\i saris chama}, someone whose condition traces back to the womb itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Recovery Presumed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a further concern: even if we observed this urination pattern when the child was six years old, perhaps at some point between that early observation and the present he recovered \u8212- even briefly \u8212- which would, according to Rabbi Akiva, place him in the category. The Gemara resolves this: {\i keivan de-tichilaso ve-sofo laki, lo chashinan} \u8212- since we see the condition at the beginning and at the end, we do not concern ourselves with the possibility of an intermediate recovery. A consistent beginning and end permit a reasonable presumption of continuity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Limb Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mari challenges this reasoning from a {\i braita} concerning a {\i bechor} animal with a certain eye condition. Rabbi Chanina ben Antignos rules there that one must examine the animal three times within eighty days \u8212- beginning, middle, and end \u8212- before concluding that the condition has persisted continuously. Why is a two-endpoint check sufficient here but not there? The Gemara answers: {\i le-chad evar chashinan, le-chula gufa lo chashinan} \u8212- for a condition affecting a single limb, we worry about possible intermediate recovery; for a systemic condition affecting the whole body, we do not. The {\i saris chama} condition, even though it manifests in a particular way, is understood as something systemic rather than a localized physical defect, and therefore the two-endpoint check suffices.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Saris Adam Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age 20 Indicator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a {\i braita} that addresses how one identifies a {\i saris} and when that status becomes operative. The case: {\i ben esrim shana ve-lo heivi shtei se\u8217'aros} \u8212- a man who has reached the age of twenty without having grown two pubic hairs. Such a person must bring proof that he is indeed twenty years old, and he is then classified as a {\i saris}. The underlying reasoning is that a {\i saris chama} \u8212- someone with this systemic condition \u8212- never reaches sexual maturity, never develops secondary sex characteristics, and therefore will not grow pubic hair. Ordinarily, halakhic adulthood requires reaching thirteen years of age and having two pubic hairs. When someone reaches twenty without pubic hair, it is not a case of delayed puberty but of an absence of natural maturation altogether, and the person is classified as a {\i saris} \u8212- an adult by virtue of age alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Chalitzah/Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then states the {\i nafka mina}: {\i lo choletz ve-lo meyabem} \u8212- such a person does not perform {\i chalitzah} and does not perform {\i yibum}. The parallel ruling applies to a woman: {\i bas esrim ve-lo hevia shtei se\u8217'aros} \u8212- a woman of twenty without two pubic hairs is classified as an {\i ailonit}, the female counterpart, {\i lo choletzet ve-lo misyabemes} \u8212- she neither performs nor receives {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}. This is {\i divrei Beis Hillel}, who set the threshold at twenty. Beis Shammai says eighteen for both. Rabbi Eliezer says: follow Beis Hillel for males (twenty) and Beis Shammai for females (eighteen), since women mature earlier and their status can therefore be determined at a younger age.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting what the {\i saris} category actually represents here. This is not merely a person who is infertile \u8212- an {\i akara} (a barren woman) would still be subject to {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}. The {\i saris chama} and {\i ailonit} are people whose entire physical development has not followed the normal trajectory; it is a systemic condition, not simply the absence of offspring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an apparent contradiction. Rabbi Eliezer, in participating in the {\i braita}\u8217's debate about whether the threshold is eighteen or twenty, seems to accept its {\i nafka mina} \u8212- namely, that a {\i saris} does not perform {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}. Yet in our {\i mishnah}, Rabbi Eliezer explicitly says that a {\i saris chama} does perform {\i chalitzah}, because people with this condition can be healed in Alexandria in Egypt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retraction Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Dikuli in the name of Shmuel says: {\i chazar bo Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer retracted one of these positions. The question then becomes: which one did he abandon? A further {\i braita} is cited in which Rabbi Eliezer explicitly states that a {\i saris chama} performs {\i chalitzah} and {\i chalitzah} is performed with his wife, because {\i she-ken be-minan misrapin be-Alexandria shel Mitzrayim} \u8212- those with this condition are healed in Alexandria in Egypt. This {\i braita} thus aligns with the {\i mishnah}. Rashi explains that the concreteness of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's knowledge \u8212- that healing is actually available in Alexandria \u8212- indicates that this was his considered and final position, and that it was the statement in the {\i braita} excluding the {\i saris} from {\i chalitzah} that he abandoned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not About Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar (not to be confused with Rabbi Eliezer) offers a different resolution: {\i le-olam lo hadar bei} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer never retracted at all. {\i Ve-chi tnan ha-hi le-onshin itmar} \u8212- when Rabbi Eliezer participated in the discussion about eighteen versus twenty, he was not endorsing the {\i nafka mina} of exclusion from {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}. That {\i braita} is concerned with a different question entirely: the {\i onshin} \u8212- the question of when a {\i saris} or {\i ailonit} becomes halakhically adult for purposes of liability and punishment. Rabbi Eliezer disagrees with the other positions about the {\i nafka mina} of {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}, but agrees that there is a distinct question of when the person is considered a {\i gadol} for other legal purposes, and it is only on that question that he offers the eighteen-versus-twenty ruling. In Rabbi Elazar\u8217's reading, Rabbi Eliezer consistently holds throughout that a {\i saris chama} does perform {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}; both the {\i mishnah} and the {\i braita} about Alexandria reflect his unretracted view.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Adult Status at 20\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Fat Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that Rabbi Eliezer did not retract, the Gemara now explores the practical implications of the age-twenty determination for {\i onshin}. The case: a person ate {\i chelev} (forbidden fat) from the age of twelve years and one day \u8212- the age of halakhic maturity for a girl, had she had pubic hair \u8212- through the age of eighteen, without ever developing pubic hair. At eighteen (or twenty, depending on the authority), the {\i simanei saris} or {\i ailonit} manifest, and the person is categorized accordingly. Then, afterward \u8212- {\i le-achar mikan} \u8212- pubic hair does appear. The question is when, exactly, this person became a halakhic adult, and whether the sins committed between twelve and eighteen carry liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Retroactive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav amar na\u8217'aseh saris le-mafreia} \u8212- Rav holds that once we identify a person as a {\i saris} at the age of eighteen or twenty, that status applies retroactively to age twelve or thirteen. The person was, in hindsight, a {\i saris} from the point of {\i bar} or {\i bas mitzvah} \u8212- they are therefore liable for the {\i aveiros} committed during that period, since retroactively they were an adult at the time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Forward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees: {\i katan haya be-osa sha\u8217'a} \u8212- at the time of those transgressions the person was still a minor. Adult status is conferred only from the moment the {\i saris} classification is established \u8212- at eighteen or twenty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Hair Appears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i le-achar mikan} clause \u8212- the appearance of pubic hair after the {\i saris} classification \u8212- adds a further complication. One might think that subsequent pubic hair growth is evidence of delayed rather than absent puberty, suggesting that adult status should be assigned even later. Yet Rav maintains his retroactive position even in this case: once classified as a {\i saris} at eighteen or twenty, the person is retroactively an adult from twelve or thirteen, regardless of later pubic hair. The {\i nafka mina} here extends beyond questions of sin and punishment; it applies equally to financial transactions undertaken during that period, which would be binding retroactively according to Rav but not according to Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit Knas Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenges Rav from the laws of {\i knas} in {\i Kesubos}. Rabbi Meir holds that {\i knas} \u8212- the fifty-shekel payment for rape or seduction \u8212- applies only to a {\i na\u8217'arah}, not to a {\i ketanah}. An {\i ailonit}, having no pubic hair, remains a {\i ketanah} and therefore has no {\i na\u8217'arus} period. According to Rav\u8217's retroactive logic, however, since an {\i ailonit} is retroactively an adult from age twelve, she should have a {\i na\u8217'arus} period from twelve to twelve-and-a-half \u8212- and should therefore be entitled to {\i knas} during that window.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Na\u8217'arus Period\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the difficulty: {\i mi-katnusa nafka le-voger} \u8212- she goes directly from {\i ketanah} to {\i bogeres}. The period of {\i na\u8217'arus} \u8212- the transitional stage of physical maturation between twelve and twelve-and-a-half \u8212- presupposes a naturally maturing body. {\i Na\u8217'arus} is constituted by the process of puberty itself; it is the stage during which the body is actively developing the secondary sex characteristics. An {\i ailonit} undergoes no such process. Even according to Rav, who says that retroactively she became an adult at twelve, she became a {\i bogeres} directly \u8212- there is no {\i na\u8217'arus} interlude. This is equally true according to Shmuel: whether the transition to adulthood occurred at twelve or at eighteen, in neither case does the {\i ailonit} pass through a stage of {\i na\u8217'arus}. A supporting {\i braita} confirms: {\i ein saris nidon ke-ven sorer u-moreh} \u8212- a {\i saris} cannot be a {\i ben sorer u-moreh}, because a {\i ben sorer u-moreh} requires the presence of lower pubic hair, which a {\i saris} will never have. {\i Ve-ein ailonit nidonis ke-na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} \u8212- an {\i ailonit} is not in the category of {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}, with its associated capital punishment for adultery, {\i she-mi-katnusah yatz\u8217'ah le-darga} \u8212- she leapfrogs directly from {\i ketanah} to {\i gedolah}, bypassing the {\i na\u8217'arah} status entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Physical Simanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waiting Until 20\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu states: {\i simanei saris ve-ailonit u-ven shemonah ein osim bahem ma\u8217'aseh ad she-yihyu ben esrim} \u8212- for someone who displays the indicators of {\i saris} or {\i ailonit}, and for someone born in the eighth month of pregnancy, no halakhic determination is made until they reach the age of twenty. Even if at nineteen a person presents all the indicators of {\i saris} or {\i ailonit}, the formal classification cannot be finalized until twenty. The eighth-month baby ({\i ben shemonah}) is grouped here because the Gemara assumes such a child cannot survive, and therefore the child has no presumption of viability ({\i chazakas chayah}) until the twentieth year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eighth Month Baby\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts an immediate question: {\i u-ven shemonah mi chayei?} \u8212- does an eighth-month baby actually survive? A {\i braita} is cited: {\i ben shemonah harei hu ke-even} \u8212- an eighth-month baby is like a stone; it is {\i muktzah} on {\i Shabbos} and one may not handle it. The implication is stark: such a child is presumed to be non-viable, essentially already dead. How, then, is it possible to speak of waiting until this child reaches twenty?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Held-Over Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: {\i kesha-gamru simanav} \u8212- the case is one where the physical characteristics at birth are those of a complete child. Hair, fingernails, and other markers are developed. In such a case, the Gemara concludes, {\i amina hai bar sheva hu ve-ishtahui de-ishtahi} \u8212- this is really a seven-month baby that was held over in the womb beyond its term. The standard view is that a seventh-month baby is viable while an eighth-month baby is not. A baby born in what appears to be the eighth month, but whose physical markers are those of a full-term child, is presumed to be a seven-month baby born late. The {\i chiddush} of Rabbi Abbahu is that even such a child \u8212- whose physical development looks normal \u8212- requires waiting until twenty before the full {\i chazakas chayah} is established. Conversely, even a person with all the {\i simanei saris} must wait until twenty before that status is formally confirmed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twelve Month Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This idea of in-utero delay gives rise to a striking practical ruling: {\i ha de-avad Rava Tosfaah} \u8212- Rava Tosfaah issued a ruling in a case of a woman whose husband had traveled overseas and who gave birth twelve months after his departure. Rava Tosfaah declared the child {\i kasher} \u8212- not a {\i mamzer}. The reasoning: following Rabbi\u8217's view that babies can be held over in the womb, a nine-month fetus that was retained for three additional months would still be the husband\u8217's child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 30 Day Survival\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds that Rava Tosfaah\u8217's ruling was not merely a lone opinion: {\i keivan de-ika Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel de-amar mishtahai} \u8212- since Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel also holds that babies can be held over in the womb, the ruling reflects a broader consensus. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's relevant teaching: {\i kol she-shaha sheloshim yom ba-adam eino nefel} \u8212- any infant who survives thirty days is no longer classified as a {\i nefel}. A child born in the eighth month who survives a full month is no longer treated as a stillborn; the thirty-day survival is itself evidence that the child was a viable holdover. This supports the broader framework: even an apparent eighth-month birth may represent a held-over seven-month fetus, and survival confirms that presumption.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Saris Signs Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Beard/Hair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents the full enumeration of the physical signs of {\i saris chama}. A {\i baraisa} teaches: {\i eizehu saris chama? Kol she-ben esrim ve-lo heivi shtei se\u8217'aros} \u8212- anyone who reaches twenty without two pubic hairs. {\i Ve-afilu heivi le-achar mikan, harei hu ke-saris le-chol devarav} \u8212- even if pubic hair appears afterward, this does not lead to a reassessment of status. Once classified as a {\i saris} at twenty, that classification is final.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smooth Skin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additional signs follow: {\i kol she-ein lo zakan} \u8212- anyone without a beard; {\i ve-sa\u8217'aro lakui} \u8212- whose hair is affected (seemingly sparse, soft, or insufficiently developed); {\i u-vesaro machlik} \u8212- and whose skin is smooth. These are characteristics that reflect the absence of normal male physical development and are understood as more feminine-type traits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Urination & Heat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several further traditions are cited. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel in the name of Rabbi Yehuda ben Yair: {\i kol she-ein meimav ma\u8217'alin retichu} \u8212- anyone whose urine does not produce foam (or, according to another reading, steam) upon urination. Another view: {\i kol ha-matil mayim ve-ein oseh kipah} \u8212- one whose urine does not form an arc (as noted earlier by Abaye). Another: {\i kol she-shichvat zaro dacha} \u8212- one whose semen is watery rather than viscous. Another: {\i kol she-mei raglav machmitzim} \u8212- one whose urine does not become malodorous. And yet another: {\i kol she-rocheitz bi-ymot ha-geshamim ve-ein besaro ma\u8217'aleh hevel} \u8212- one who bathes in winter and whose body produces no steam. All of these indicators point in the same direction: a systemic absence of the bodily heat and physical characteristics associated with normal male development.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voice Quality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar adds: {\i kol she-kolo lakui, ve-ein nikar bein ish le-isha} \u8212- anyone whose voice is affected such that one cannot distinguish whether it is a man\u8217's or a woman\u8217's voice. This is the male counterpart of the corresponding sign for an {\i ailonit}, where Rabban Shimon ben Elazar notes that a deep voice indistinguishable from a man\u8217's is a sign of that condition. The parallel list of {\i simanei ailonit} includes: absence of breasts, difficulty during intercourse, absence of the Mons pubis ({\i shipulei me\u8217'ayim}), and a low voice indistinguishable from a man\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 All vs One Sign\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna: All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the full list of {\i simanim} before us, the Gemara takes up the question of how many are required. {\i Simanei saris} \u8212- {\i Rav Huna amar: ad she-yihyu kulam} \u8212- Rav Huna holds that a person is classified as a {\i saris} only if all the {\i simanim} are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan: One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disagrees: {\i afilu be-echad mehen} \u8212- even one of these signs suffices to establish {\i saris} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Present: All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies the scope of the dispute. {\i Heicha de-heivi shtei se\u8217'aros ba-zakan} \u8212- where a person has two hairs of the beard (or, per the {\i Gra}\u8217's reading, two pubic hairs), everyone agrees that all the {\i simanim} together would be required to overcome the evidence of that hair growth. {\i Ki pligi be-shelo heivi} \u8212- the dispute between Rav Huna and Rabbi Yochanan applies specifically where the person has neither pubic hair nor facial hair (depending on the reading). In that case: does the presence of even one of the listed signs establish {\i saris} status (Rabbi Yochanan), or must all signs be present (Rav Huna)?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a remarkable incident. Rava bar Rav Huna instructed the rabbis to observe Rav Nachman: {\i inu bei Rav Nachman} \u8212- if when bathing his body produced steam, {\i i besaro ma\u8217'aleh havel}, then {\i asiv lei brat} \u8212- you could go ahead and give your daughter in marriage to him, knowing that he was not a {\i saris}. The implication is that Rava bar Rav Huna was looking to rule out {\i saris} status on the basis of a single {\i siman} \u8212- consistent with Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Likely Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, offers a different reading of this incident. {\i Rav Nachman sichei dikna havu leh} \u8212- Rav Nachman in fact had some stubble of beard, or some small amount of pubic hair. In that case \u8212- where some hair is present \u8212- everyone agrees that all the {\i simanim} are required for {\i saris} classification. Since Rav Nachman had that hair, checking for even one counter-indicator was sufficient to rule out {\i saris} status: if he had steam from his body, there was at least one {\i siman} working against {\i saris} classification, and in a case where all {\i simanim} are required, failing even one test means he is not a {\i saris}. The incident therefore does not compel the conclusion that Rava bar Rav Huna held like Rabbi Yochanan; it is compatible with Rav Huna\u8217's position as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 No Chalitzah/Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the concluding ruling of the {\i mishnah}: {\i ha-saris she-chalatz le-yivamto} \u8212- the {\i saris} who performs {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}, and similarly the {\i ailonit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saris Chama Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara observes: {\i katani saris dumya de-ailonit} \u8212- since the {\i mishnah} groups {\i saris} and {\i ailonit} together, they must be parallel categories. An {\i ailonit} is unambiguously a natural condition \u8212- {\i bidei Shamayim}. Therefore the {\i saris} referred to in the {\i mishnah} is also {\i bidei Shamayim} \u8212- a {\i saris chama}, not a {\i saris adam} who acquired his condition through human action or injury.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-stama ke-Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- the {\i stam mishnah} ruling here reflects Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position. Rabbi Akiva holds: {\i bidei adam} \u8212- a {\i saris adam} \u8212- is included in the category of {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}; {\i bidei Shamayim} \u8212- a {\i saris chama} \u8212- is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Invalidates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i saris} nonetheless performs {\i yibum} with his {\i yevamah}, he invalidates her \u8212- {\i pesulah le-kehunah}. The reason: he has engaged in a forbidden sexual union with his sister-in-law outside the context of {\i mitzvah}, an {\i issur arayot}, and this makes her prohibited for marriage to a {\i kohen}. If, on the other hand, he performed {\i chalitzah}, she is not invalidated, since a non-meaningful {\i chalitzah} carries no such consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought that any man\u8217's sexual relations with the {\i yevamah} \u8212- not just the {\i saris} brother-in-law \u8212- would invalidate her, given that she is a {\i shomeret yavam} and her {\i zikah} bond constitutes a quasi-marital relationship. The Gemara indeed confirms this: {\i hu ha-din afilu le-acher nami} \u8212- even if another man had relations with her while she was a {\i shomeret yavam}, she would likewise be invalidated for the remaining brothers. The reason the {\i mishnah} frames the ruling in terms of the {\i saris} himself is literary: since the opening of the clause discusses what happens when {\i he} performs {\i chalitzah}, the {\i mishnah} naturally continues by discussing what happens when {\i he} performs {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same rules apply to an {\i ailonit}: if the brothers perform {\i chalitzah} with her, she is not invalidated; if they perform {\i yibum} \u8212- sexual relations with her outside of {\i mitzvah} \u8212- she is invalidated, because it constitutes relations with an {\i eishet ach} in the absence of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes the implicit assumption: {\i ta\u8217'ama de-ba\u8217'aluha} \u8212- it is specifically the sexual act that creates the invalidation. If no such act occurred, she would not be invalidated merely by virtue of being an {\i ailonit}. This ruling stands in contrast to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, which holds that an {\i ailonit} is inherently a {\i zonah} \u8212- since she can never conceive, every sexual act she engages in is considered fornication outside the context of procreation. The {\i mishnah} here does not follow Rabbi Yehuda. According to the {\i mishnah}, an {\i ailonit} is not a {\i zonah} by nature, remains permitted to a {\i kohen}, and is only invalidated by the specific prohibited sexual act of {\i yibum} performed without the force of {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 81\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Saris & Androgynos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saris Wife Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah on this daf returns us to the question of the {\i saris} \u8212- one who is incapable of procreation \u8212- and its implications for {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}. Having established in earlier discussion that a {\i saris chama} (one whose condition is congenital, from the sun, as it were) cannot perform {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} according to Rabbi Akiva \u8212- whereas a {\i saris adam} (one castrated by human intervention) had at one point been capable and is therefore not categorically excluded \u8212- the mishnah now turns to a distinct question: the {\i saris chama}\u8217's wife and her eligibility to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah states: a {\i kohen} who is a {\i saris chama} and who married a {\i bat Yisrael} \u8212- she may eat {\i terumah} on his behalf. The distinction is critical. A {\i saris adam} is a {\i patzua daka}, whose marriage is forbidden, rendering the woman a {\i chalalah}. But a {\i saris chama} has committed no {\i issur} in marrying her; the marriage is fully valid, and she accordingly enjoys the rights of a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife, including eating {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Androgynos Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon introduce the case of an {\i androgynos} \u8212- a person with both male and female genitalia \u8212- who is a {\i kohen} and has married a {\i bat Yisrael}. They rule that she may eat {\i terumah} on his behalf. Before examining the ruling itself, it is worth considering the three possible halakhic frameworks for understanding an {\i androgynos}. The intuitive assumption might be {\i safek} \u8212- perhaps we simply do not know whether to categorize this person as male or female. But there are two other possibilities as well. One is that the {\i androgynos} is a {\i berya bifnei atzmo}, a creature of a third category entirely, neither male nor female but something unto itself \u8212- a recognition that the binary division of the world into male and female may not be exhaustive. The third possibility is that the {\i androgynos} is definitively male, on the reasoning that maleness is defined by the presence of male genitalia, and since this person possesses male genitalia, he is male regardless of any other characteristics present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Androgynos Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling of Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon \u8212- that an {\i androgynos kohen}\u8217's wife may eat {\i terumah} \u8212- presupposes that the {\i androgynos} is halakhically male. For the marriage to confer {\i terumah} rights upon his wife, he must be definitively her husband, which requires him to be definitively male. Were he a {\i safek} or a {\i berya bifnei atzmo}, it is difficult to see how the marriage would unambiguously transfer {\i terumah} eligibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah further states that an {\i androgynos} can marry a woman but cannot be married by a man \u8212- again consistent with the position that he is definitively male. Rabbi Eliezer goes further, ruling that if an {\i androgynos} marries a man, the act is treated as an act of male homosexuality and carries the penalty of {\i sekilah}, again treating him as fully male. Whether the earlier positions in the mishnah require us to view the {\i androgynos} as definitively male, or whether they are compatible with a {\i safek} reading, remains to be examined in the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumtum Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda adds that a {\i tumtum} \u8212- a person whose genitalia were covered and whose sex was initially indeterminate \u8212- who was later found upon tearing of the skin covering to be male, may nonetheless not perform {\i chalitzah}, because he is considered like a {\i saris}. The presumption is that someone born in this condition will be unable to father children, rendering him a {\i saris chama} from birth and thus excluded from the entire framework of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yochanan vs Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saris Wife Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens by questioning whether the first ruling \u8212- that a {\i saris chama}\u8217's wife may eat {\i terumah} \u8212- is even in need of stating. {\i Peshita} \u8212- it seems obvious. He is a valid {\i kohen}, the marriage is valid, she is not {\i pesulah} in any way. Why would we think otherwise? The {\i gemara} answers: one might have thought that the verse {\i yelid beito hem yochlu belachmo} \u8212- members of his household eat of his bread \u8212- is tied specifically to the context of a marriage that produces progeny. One might reason that only {\i molid ma\u8217'achil}, one who can have children causes his wife to eat {\i terumah}, and one who cannot, {\i she\u8217'eino molid}, does not. The mishnah teaches us that we do not apply this reasoning; the wife of a {\i saris chama} is his wife in every sense, and she eats {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question arises as to whether the {\i saris chama} and the {\i tumtum she-nikra} \u8212- the {\i tumtum} who was found to be male \u8212- are treated identically. In both cases, the person is male but incapable of procreation; the mishnah simply addressed each in the context of a different {\i halakha} \u8212- {\i terumah} for the former, {\i chalitzah} for the latter. But these are not contradictory rulings; both {\i halakhot} apply to both cases. The distinction that does emerge from earlier discussions is between a {\i saris} and an ordinary {\i akar} \u8212- a man who is simply infertile. An {\i akar} does perform {\i chalitzah}, while a {\i saris} does not, and it is the {\i saris}\u8217's categorical status, not merely his infertility, that excludes him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lakish Safek View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the case of the {\i androgynos}. Reish Lakish states: the wife of an {\i androgynos kohen} may eat {\i terumah}, but she may not eat {\i chazeh v\u8217'shok} \u8212- the breast and thigh portions of {\i kodashim} that a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife is entitled to receive. Rabbi Yochanan disagrees and holds that she may even eat {\i chazeh v\u8217'shok}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of Reish Lakish\u8217's position flows from his view that an {\i androgynos} is a {\i safek} \u8212- possibly male, possibly not. If there is genuine doubt as to whether this woman is legally married to a {\i kohen}, then she cannot eat {\i chazeh v\u8217'shok}, which is biblical. Why then can she eat {\i terumah}? Because, the {\i gemara} explains, the {\i gemara} is speaking of {\i terumah bizman hazeh} \u8212- {\i terumah} in our times, which is only rabbinic in force, presumably because following the destruction of the {\i Beis HaMikdash} the land lost its sanctification for purposes of {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'asros}. In a case of {\i safek}, we are lenient in matters of rabbinic law, and so she may eat {\i terumah} even though the marriage is uncertain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Male View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, by contrast, holds that the {\i androgynos} is definitively male, the marriage is fully valid, and the woman may eat {\i terumah} and {\i kodashim} alike, with no restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} shifts from the primary dispute \u8212- the status of the {\i androgynos} \u8212- to challenge the secondary premise underlying Reish Lakish\u8217's position, namely that {\i terumah} today is rabbinic. Rabbi Yochanan presses: do you really hold that {\i terumah bizman hazeh} is {\i derabbanan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish confirms this and offers a proof: he teaches a {\i baraita} in which an {\i igul} \u8212- a round of pressed figs \u8212- of {\i terumah} that becomes mixed with ordinary rounds of pressed figs is {\i oleh b\u8217'echad u\u8217'me\u8217'ah}, nullified in a ratio of one to a hundred. Now, an {\i igul} is a {\i davar she-biminyan} \u8212- an item sold by count \u8212- and the general rule is that things counted individually are {\i chashuv} and cannot be subject to {\i bittul}. The fact that {\i bittul} works here, Reish Lakish argues, must be because {\i terumah} nowadays is only rabbinic, and in rabbinic matters we are lenient enough to allow {\i bittul} even for a {\i davar she-biminyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the obvious objection: {\i terumah} is itself a biblical prohibition. Reish Lakish acknowledges this but maintains the distinction between {\i terumah} in Temple times and {\i terumah} today, given the absence of the {\i Beis HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then points out: if Reish Lakish wishes to contrast {\i terumah} as rabbinic with {\i chazeh v\u8217'shok} as biblical, he need not reach for {\i chazeh v\u8217'shok} at all. He could simply distinguish within {\i terumah} itself \u8212- rabbinic {\i terumah} today versus biblical {\i terumah} at the time of the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. The {\i gemara} accepts that this is indeed Reish Lakish\u8217's intent. When he says she eats {\i terumah} but not {\i chazeh v\u8217'shok}, he means: she eats today\u8217's {\i terumah}, which is rabbinic. And at a time when the {\i Beis HaMikdash} stood and {\i terumah} was biblical, she would not eat even that. Moreover, even rabbinic {\i terumah} \u8212- such as {\i terumah} of fruit, which is rabbinic even in Temple times \u8212- would be prohibited to her at the time of the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, because {\i dilma asi l\u8217'ochla b\u8217'terumah d\u8217'oraisa}: one might come to eat from the biblical {\i terumah} as well, and we are strict across the board when biblical {\i terumah} coexists with rabbinic {\i terumah}. In our times, however, when there is no biblical {\i terumah} at all, there is no such concern, and she may eat rabbinic {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bittul Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Igul Batel Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish\u8217's proof that {\i terumah} today is rabbinic rests on the {\i igul} case: a round of pressed figs of {\i terumah} that becomes mixed with other rounds is {\i batel} in a hundred, despite being a {\i davar she-biminyan}. This requires some background on the principles of {\i bittul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism of {\i bittul} \u8212- halakhic nullification \u8212- operates on the logic that when a prohibited item is mixed into a much larger quantity of permitted items, the prohibited item loses its independent identity and becomes absorbed into the whole. This works when the items are undifferentiated and can be viewed collectively. When, however, items are individually significant \u8212- {\i chashuv} \u8212- each one retains its independent identity and cannot simply be dissolved into the collective. Such items are not subject to {\i bittul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One basis for this individual significance is the principle of {\i davar she-biminyan} \u8212- items sold by count. Things one counts by number, rather than by weight or volume, are treated as individually significant, and they are therefore generally not {\i batel}. The intuition is straightforward: a chair, a house, a barrel of wine \u8212- these are things one counts individually, each retaining its separate existence. They are not viewed as an undifferentiated mass.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, Reish Lakish argues that an {\i igul} \u8212- sold by count \u8212- should not be {\i batel} under this principle. Yet the {\i baraita} says it is {\i batel}. This can only be because, Reish Lakish reasons, {\i terumah} today is rabbinic, and in rabbinic domains we relax the strictures of {\i bittul} sufficiently to allow {\i bittul} even of a {\i davar she-biminyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Et vs Kol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan rejects this inference entirely. The proof fails, he says, because Reish Lakish has misread the {\i baraita} about {\i davar she-biminyan}. Reish Lakish understood the rule to be {\i kol she-darko limanot} \u8212- everything that is ever sold by count \u8212- is not {\i batel}. Rabbi Yochanan corrects him: the proper text of the {\i baraita} is {\i et she-darko limanot} \u8212- only that which is sold {\i exclusively} by count is not {\i batel}. The word {\i et} rather than {\i kol} narrows the category considerably.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is meaningful. Some items are {\i sometimes} sold by count and {\i sometimes} sold by weight or volume. Eggs, for instance, may be purchased individually or by the basket. Under Reish Lakish\u8217's reading, anything that is ever counted \u8212- even if it is also sometimes sold otherwise \u8212- would be {\i chashuv} and thus not {\i batel}. Under Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reading, only that which is {\i exclusively} counted \u8212- always and only sold by number, never by weight or volume \u8212- is considered sufficiently individuated to resist {\i bittul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\i igul} of pressed figs, Rabbi Yochanan explains, is sometimes sold by count and sometimes not. It therefore falls outside the narrow category of {\i et she-darko limanot}, and accordingly it {\i can} be {\i batel}. This has nothing whatsoever to do with {\i terumah} being rabbinic. The {\i igul} is {\i batel} simply because it is not a {\i davar she-biminyan} in the strict sense Rabbi Yochanan defines. Reish Lakish\u8217's inference collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kilaim Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish \u8212- {\i et she-darko limanot} versus {\i kol she-darko limanot} \u8212- finds its source in a {\i baraita} about {\i kilaim}. The {\i mishnah} teaches: one who possesses bundles of fenugreek ({\i tiltan}) that are {\i kilei hakerem} \u8212- produce that grew intermixed with a vineyard and is therefore forbidden \u8212- must burn them. If these bundles become mixed with other bundles of ordinary fenugreek, Rabbi Meir says all of them must be burned, because the forbidden bundles are not {\i batel}. The {\i chachamim}, however, say they are {\i batel} in one part in two hundred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis of Rabbi Meir\u8217's stringency is his rule that a {\i davar she-biminyan} is not {\i batel} \u8212- and the text attributed to Rabbi Meir is {\i kol she-darko limanot}, everything that is counted. The {\i chachamim} reject this broad category and instead limit the category of items that are not {\i batel} to a specific list of six things: nuts of Parach, pomegranates from Badan, sealed barrels of wine, shoots of beets, heads of cabbage, and Greek gourd. Rabbi Akiva adds a seventh: home-baked loaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These six or seven items, whatever their particular significance in their time and context, represent a much narrower definition of {\i chashuv} than Rabbi Meir\u8217's. The {\i chachamim} are not saying that these items are the only things ever counted; they are saying that the category of things that resist {\i bittul} by virtue of individual significance is far more restricted than anything-that-is-ever-counted. The {\i Rambam}, in codifying this {\i halakha}, frames the list in terms of what is {\i chashuv} in a given time and place, rather than as a fixed eternal enumeration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} assumes that the discussion between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish operates within the framework of Rabbi Meir\u8217's view, and their dispute concerns the correct reading of Rabbi Meir\u8217's own formulation \u8212- {\i et} versus {\i kol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatichah Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's decisive argument against Reish Lakish is a {\i baraita} about a {\i chatichah} of {\i kodashim}. A {\i chatichah} \u8212- a chunk of meat, a steak \u8212- of {\i chatat} that became {\i tamei} and became mixed with a hundred chunks of {\i tahor chatat} meat: the {\i baraita} says {\i ta\u8217'aleh} \u8212- it is {\i batel}, and the mixture may be distributed and eaten by {\i kohanim}. Similarly, a slice of {\i lechem hapanim} \u8212- the showbread \u8212- that became {\i tamei} and was mixed with a hundred {\i tahor} slices: again, {\i ta\u8217'aleh}, it is {\i batel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda, however, says {\i lo ta\u8217'aleh} \u8212- it is not {\i batel} in these cases. The force of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's proof comes from the view of the {\i chachamim}, who hold it is {\i batel}. A {\i chatichah} is something sold by count \u8212- you do not buy meat in an undifferentiated mass, you buy portions, individual pieces. Under Reish Lakish\u8217's reading of {\i kol she-darko limanot}, a {\i chatichah} would be a {\i davar she-biminyan} and not {\i batel}. Yet {\i kodashim} is unquestionably biblical. If {\i bittul} works for a {\i chatichah} of {\i kodashim}, it cannot be because we are lenient due to rabbinic status. It must be because a {\i chatichah}, while sometimes counted, is not {\i exclusively} counted \u8212- and therefore, under Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's rule of {\i et she-darko limanot}, it falls outside the category of things that resist {\i bittul}. Reish Lakish\u8217's inference from the {\i igul} case is therefore unfounded; the {\i bittul} of an {\i igul} proves nothing about {\i terumah} being rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then presents a contrasting case: a {\i tahor} chunk of {\i chatat} that became mixed with a hundred {\i tahor} chunks of ordinary {\i chulin} \u8212- and a {\i tahor} slice of {\i lechem hapanim} mixed with a hundred {\i tahor} slices of ordinary bread. In these cases, {\i divrei hakol lo ta\u8217'aleh} \u8212- by universal agreement it is not {\i batel}. Rashi explains this result by reference to a combination of factors: the items are somewhat {\i chashuv} \u8212- quasi-counted \u8212- and, critically, the consequences of non-{\i bittul} are not severe: one simply needs to arrange for a {\i kohen} to eat the mixture. Since {\i bittul} is not strictly necessary and the item has some individual weight, we do not apply it here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nimucha Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ground Meat Batel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does Reish Lakish respond to Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's proof from the {\i chatichah}? Reish Lakish, speaking through Rav Assi son of Rav Huna, answers: {\i binimucha} \u8212- we are speaking of a case where the {\i chatichah} has been dissolved, ground up into what one might describe as hamburger meat. The {\i baraita}\u8217's ruling that the {\i chatichah} is {\i batel} does not refer to a case where five distinct steaks sit before you. It refers to a case where the meat has been ground together into an indistinct mass. In that state, there is no longer an independent {\i chatichah} \u8212- each piece has lost its separate identity \u8212- and {\i bittul} accordingly operates without obstacle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is how Reish Lakish preserves his view. He agrees that a {\i chatichah} is something sometimes sold by count, and that it would normally be covered by his principle of {\i kol she-darko limanot} and thus not {\i batel}. But once it is ground, there is no longer any {\i chatichah} in existence \u8212- the pieces no longer retain their individual identity \u8212- and the factor that prevented {\i bittul} no longer applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One must consider whether this reading does justice to the {\i baraita}\u8217's language, which speaks of a {\i chatichah} becoming mixed with other {\i chatichot}. On its face, the text seems to describe distinct pieces mixed with distinct pieces, and the ruling that it is {\i batel} appears to be a ruling about that very situation. This is precisely what Rabbi Yochanan takes it to mean. Reish Lakish, however, insists that the underlying case must be one of ground meat, since only that would explain why {\i bittul} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question touches on the role of {\i min b\u8217'mino} \u8212- same-kind mixtures \u8212- in this discussion. When meat is mixed with meat of the same kind, the analysis can cut in two directions. On one view, because the items were never distinct from one another, they never lose their individual identity, and they are always present \u8212- and therefore, according to Rabbi Yehuda, {\i min b\u8217'mino lo batel}, a same-kind mixture is never {\i batel}. On the other view, because the items were never distinct, {\i bittul} is immediate, even without the need for {\i shishim}. The stringent outcome in certain cases \u8212- requiring one in a hundred or one in two hundred rather than the ordinary threshold \u8212- actually applies specifically to {\i min b\u8217'mino} mixtures, since it is only there that we say each item retains its presence and requires reduction to trivial quantity. For {\i aino mino} \u8212- different-kind mixtures \u8212- once the prohibited item can no longer be tasted it has lost its independent identity entirely and is {\i batel} on that basis alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is also why Rashi explains that Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling of {\i lo ta\u8217'aleh} in the {\i chatichah} case is attributable to his general position that {\i min b\u8217'mino lo batel}. Tosafot offers a different explanation, but the {\i min b\u8217'mino} framework is essential for understanding why {\i bittul} operates asymmetrically across these cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now challenges Reish Lakish\u8217's answer directly. The {\i baraita} presents both a {\i reisha} \u8212- where {\i bittul} does apply \u8212- and a {\i seifa} \u8212- where it does not. Reish Lakish reads the {\i reisha} as a case of ground meat, which is why {\i bittul} works. But if that is so, the {\i seifa} \u8212- where everything is {\i tahor} but {\i chatat} is mixed with {\i chulin} \u8212- must also be a case of ground meat (since the {\i baraita} deals with comparable scenarios throughout). Why then does {\i bittul} not work in the {\i seifa}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, the {\i gemara} notes a structural problem: if the way to show that {\i bittul} does not apply is to describe a non-ground-meat case, why did the {\i baraita} not make that distinction {\i within the same scenario} \u8212- ground meat versus non-ground meat \u8212- rather than switching to an entirely different scenario involving {\i chulin}? According to Reish Lakish, one could simply have said: when dissolved into ground meat, {\i batel}; when still in distinct pieces, not {\i batel}. The fact that the {\i baraita} instead switches cases requires explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i tahor b\u8217'tahor adif lei} \u8212- the {\i baraita} prefers to give you the scenario where everything is {\i tahor}, because that is a bigger {\i chiddush}. One might have thought that when everything is {\i tahor}, the only question being whether a {\i kohen} or a non-{\i kohen} may eat it, we would readily say it is {\i batel} and allow anyone to eat freely. The {\i baraita} teaches that even in this easier case, {\i bittul} does not apply \u8212- a more surprising ruling, and therefore a more instructive one to state.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumah & Severity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic vs Biblical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet son of Rav Idi offers a different resolution to the {\i reisha}/{\i seifa} asymmetry. The {\i reisha} \u8212- where {\i bittul} applies \u8212- involves a {\i chatichah} that became {\i tamei} through {\i tum\u8217'at mashkin derabbanan}: the piece of {\i chatat} became impure because it came into contact with a liquid that had itself been rendered {\i tamei} through a secondary chain of rabbinic upgrade. Because the {\i tumah} is only rabbinic in origin, the prohibition against eating this piece is sufficiently attenuated that we allow {\i bittul} to function.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i seifa}, by contrast \u8212- where {\i bittul} does not apply \u8212- involves the mixing of {\i tahor chatat} with {\i tahor chulin}. Here the concern is not {\i tumah} at all, but rather that if one declares the {\i chatat} {\i batel}, a {\i tamei} person or a non-{\i kohen} will eat it, and eating {\i tahor kodashim} while {\i tamei} is an {\i issur d\u8217'oraisa}. Because the consequence of applying {\i bittul} is a biblical violation, we do not allow {\i bittul} to function.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All-Pure Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As before, the {\i gemara} asks: if this is the distinction, why did the {\i baraita} not simply demonstrate the non-{\i batel} case by introducing a biblical {\i tumah} \u8212- say, contact with a {\i sheretz} \u8212- within the same framework, rather than switching to the {\i chulin} scenario? The answer is again {\i tahor b\u8217'tahor adif lei}: the {\i baraita} finds it more instructive to present the case where everything is {\i tahor}, because in that case one might most readily assume there is no reason not to apply {\i bittul} \u8212- nothing is actually impure, no one has done anything wrong \u8212- and the teaching that {\i bittul} nonetheless does not apply is the more striking and informative ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lav vs Karet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers yet another resolution. The distinction between {\i reisha} and {\i seifa} is not {\i derabbanan} versus {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, but severity: the {\i reisha} involves an {\i issur lav} \u8212- a negative prohibition \u8212- while the {\i seifa} involves an {\i issur karet} \u8212- a violation punishable by excision. In the {\i reisha}, where the {\i chatat} became {\i tamei}: if the piece itself is {\i tamei}, the {\i kodashim} is degraded, and eating {\i tamei kodashim} is severe but falls at the level of a {\i lav} in this context. In the {\i seifa}, where the {\i kodashim} is {\i tahor} but a {\i tamei} person eats it, that is an {\i issur karet}. Rava reasons that we allow {\i bittul} for an {\i issur lav} but not for an {\i issur karet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this on the basis of an apparently contradictory ruling elsewhere in which Rava himself states that when it comes to biblical matters, we do not differentiate between an {\i issur lav} and an {\i issur karet} for purposes of {\i bittul}. This creates a direct contradiction within Rava\u8217's own positions, and the {\i gemara} marks it as a {\i kashya} \u8212- a difficulty left without resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matirin Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers a final attempt. The {\i seifa}, he argues, involves a {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} \u8212- a matter that has a permissible pathway. The concept of {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} holds that when a prohibited item has an available route to becoming permitted \u8212- or, in a related formulation, when the need to eat it can be satisfied through a permitted pathway \u8212- we do not allow {\i bittul}, even if the quantity would technically be sufficient to {\i mevatel}. The classic case is an egg born on {\i Yom Tov}: it is prohibited today, but will become permitted tomorrow; we therefore do not apply {\i bittul}, since there is no pressing need to declare the egg nullified when one can simply wait.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi applies this reasoning to the {\i chatat}: one need not declare it {\i batel} and allow a non-{\i kohen} to eat the mixture, because there is a permissible route \u8212- give it to a {\i kohen}, who can eat it lawfully. This is analogous to waiting until tomorrow in the {\i Yom Tov} egg case. Since there is a {\i heter}, a permitted means of disposition, {\i bittul} is not invoked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejection Matirin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately dismisses this argument as {\i dedusay} \u8212- foolish, or at minimum untenable. The category of {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} refers to something that is {\i assur} for this person now but will become {\i muttar} for that same person later. The {\i Yom Tov} egg is forbidden to you today and will be permitted to you tomorrow \u8212- it is the same person, the same item, with a change in time. Here, the {\i chatat} is never {\i muttar} for a {\i Yisrael} \u8212- it is {\i le-olam assur}. And for a {\i kohen} it was never {\i assur} \u8212- {\i mi sharei}, it was always permitted. There is no entity for whom this item is currently {\i assur} and will later become {\i muttar}. The fact that a {\i kohen} can eat it is irrelevant to whether {\i bittul} should apply for the {\i Yisrael} who wishes to eat it. Thus the {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} framework simply does not fit, and Rav Ashi\u8217's suggestion is withdrawn.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unresolved Bittul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Answers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} ends in a state of genuine unresolution. We are left with three proposed answers to the question of why {\i bittul} does not work in the {\i seifa}: (1) Rav Sheshet\u8217's answer \u8212- the {\i reisha} is {\i derabbanan} tumah, the {\i seifa} is {\i d\u8217'oraisa}; (2) Rava\u8217's answer \u8212- the {\i reisha} is an {\i issur lav}, the {\i seifa} is an {\i issur karet}; and (3) Rav Ashi\u8217's answer \u8212- {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}. Of these, the third has been explicitly rejected by the {\i gemara} itself as untenable. The second is left as a {\i kashya}, contradicted by Rava\u8217's own ruling elsewhere. The first \u8212- the distinction between rabbinic and biblical {\i tumah} \u8212- stands as the most plausible of the three, though the underlying difficulty remains: even granting that the {\i seifa} is more severe, why should {\i bittul} not operate? {\i Bittul} routinely applies to biblical prohibitions \u8212- a drop of {\i chazir} mixed into a pot is {\i batel b\u8217'shishim} even though the prohibition is biblical. What makes this case different remains genuinely unclear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the {\i bittul} analysis, the {\i sugya} leaves open three intertwined debates. First: the status of the {\i androgynos} \u8212- is he definitively male, as Rabbi Yochanan holds, or a {\i safek}, as Reish Lakish maintains? Second: is {\i terumah} today biblical or rabbinic? And third: what are the precise parameters of {\i davar she-biminyan} \u8212- does {\i et she-darko limanot} (Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's narrow reading) or {\i kol she-darko limanot} (Reish Lakish\u8217's broader reading) correctly describe when an item\u8217's countability prevents {\i bittul}? These questions are interlocking: the dispute about the {\i androgynos}\u8217's status generates the question about {\i terumah}\u8217's status today, which in turn generates the dispute about {\i bittul} and what kinds of items can be nullified. The {\i daf} thus traverses a remarkable range of halakhic terrain \u8212- from the status of an {\i androgynos kohen}\u8217's wife to the fine structure of {\i hilkhot bittul} \u8212- without arriving at a clean resolution to any of them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 82\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Androgynus & Teruma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Androgynus feeds wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our discussion picks up near the end of a lengthy analysis of the {\i androgynus} \u8212- the hermaphrodite \u8212- and the question of whether such a person can cause his wife to eat {\i teruma}. As established in earlier sessions, the {\i mishna} records that according to Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon, an {\i androgynus} can indeed feed his wife {\i teruma}. Rabbi Yochanan extended this further, maintaining that the {\i androgynus} can even feed his wife {\i matnot kehuna} \u8212- those portions of {\i kodesh mamash}, the genuinely sacred priestly portions, including parts of the sacrificial offerings. Resh Lakish disagreed, holding that the {\i androgynus} cannot confer eligibility to those more elevated forms of {\i kodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teruma status debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now frames the underlying dispute: Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position implies that {\i teruma bizman hazeh} \u8212- {\i teruma} in the present era \u8212- retains the force of {\i de-oraita} law. For if {\i teruma} today were merely {\i de-rabbanan}, the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that the {\i androgynus} can feed his wife {\i teruma} would not necessarily tell us anything about his ability to confer eligibility to more elevated forms of sanctity. The {\i gemara} therefore asks pointedly: {\i ve-savar Rabbi Yochanan Teruma bizman hazeh de-oraita?} \u8212- does Rabbi Yochanan truly hold that {\i teruma} today is biblical in force?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed baskets case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In support of this challenge, the {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: there are two baskets \u8212- one of {\i chullin} and one of {\i teruma} \u8212- and above or adjacent to them are two {\i se\u8217'in}, one of {\i chullin} and one of {\i teruma}. The {\i se\u8217'in} tip over and fall into the baskets. We are now uncertain which {\i se\u8217'ah} fell into which basket. The {\i baraita} rules that everything is permitted, because we say: the {\i teruma} fell into the {\i teruma} basket and the {\i chullin} fell into the {\i chullin} basket \u8212- {\i teruma le-toch Teruma nafla, ve-chullin le-toch chullin nafla}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov requirement dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this {\i baraita}, Resh Lakish added a qualification: {\i ve-hu she-rabbu chullin al ha-Teruma} \u8212- this ruling applies only when the {\i chullin} already in the basket is greater in quantity than the {\i se\u8217'ah} of {\i teruma} above it. His reasoning is that even if the {\i teruma} had fallen into the {\i chullin} basket, it would have been {\i batel be-rov} \u8212- nullified by a majority \u8212- since for a prohibition that is {\i de-rabbanan} we do not require sixty parts; a simple majority suffices. This is clear evidence that Resh Lakish holds {\i teruma bizman hazeh} is {\i de-rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, by contrast, said: {\i af al pi she-lo rabbu chullin al ha-Teruma} \u8212- even if there is not a majority of {\i chullin} over {\i teruma}, we still permit everything, relying entirely on the possibility that each {\i se\u8217'ah} fell into its corresponding basket. Notably, this makes Rabbi Yochanan {\i more} lenient than Resh Lakish in this particular case \u8212- a striking result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kashe on Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses the difficulty: {\i bishlama} Resh Lakish\u8217's position is coherent \u8212- he holds {\i teruma} is {\i de-rabbanan}, and therefore a {\i rov} is sufficient to render the mixture permitted. But {\i ella le-Rabbi Yochanan kashya}: if Rabbi Yochanan holds {\i teruma bizman hazeh} is {\i de-oraita}, how can he permit the mixture without any {\i rov}? A {\i de-oraita} prohibition surely cannot be nullified by a bare majority, let alone without one. The {\i gemara} is stuck.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seder Olam proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita is Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty by asking: {\i ha mani} \u8212- whose position does this {\i baraita} represent? The answer: {\i Rabbanan hi} \u8212- it reflects the position of those who disagree with Rabbi Yose. And Rabbi Yochanan declares: {\i ve-ana de-amri ke-Rabbi Yose} \u8212- I am speaking in accordance with the view of Rabbi Yose. The {\i baraita} about the baskets is therefore irrelevant to Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position, as it does not accord with the opinion he is invoking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerusha & Kedusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now demonstrates that Rabbi Yose does indeed hold {\i teruma bizman hazeh} is {\i de-oraita}, citing a {\i baraita} from {\i Seder Olam}: {\i asher yarshu avotecha ve-irshtah} \u8212- \u8220"which your fathers inherited and you shall inherit it.\u8221" The {\i Seder Olam} derives from this verse that a first {\i yerusha} and a second {\i yerusha} carry enduring {\i kedusha}, but a third {\i yerusha} is unnecessary because the {\i kedusha} was never lost. As Rashi explains, the first {\i yerusha} refers to the conquest of Yehoshua bin Nun; the second to the return led by Ezra. The exile of {\i Galut Bavel} did eliminate the original {\i kedusha} of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, but Ezra\u8217's return re-consecrated the land. The subsequent Roman exile \u8212- {\i Galut Titus} \u8212- does not eliminate that second consecration, and therefore the {\i kedusha} of {\i Eretz Yisrael} remains intact, which in turn means that {\i teruma} retains its {\i de-oraita} force. This is the position of Rabbi Yose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seder Olam nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing on the nature of {\i Seder Olam} itself. It is not entirely clear whether this citation refers to the same {\i Seder Olam} commonly invoked as a work of historical chronology, though Chaim Milikowsky, who has written extensively on {\i Seder Olam}, argues that the text is extremely early \u8212- so the identification is at least plausible. The work attempts to fill in historical gaps not fully addressed in the {\i Chumash}, tracing events through the period of {\i Beit Sheni}. It is best understood as a kind of chronicle, organized something like a collection of {\i baraitot} \u8212- attributed statements and discussions \u8212- rather than flowing narrative prose. Whether the attribution of {\i Seder Olam} as a whole, or just this particular passage within it, belongs to Rabbi Yose is a question that admits no definitive answer here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seder Olam explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any case, the {\i gemara} identifies the {\i tanna} of {\i Seder Olam} as Rabbi Yose: {\i ve-amar Rabbi Yochanan man tana Seder Olam \u8212- Rabbi Yose}. This closes the loop: Rabbi Yochanan is following Rabbi Yose, who holds that the {\i kedusha} established by Ezra was never nullified by {\i Galut Titus}, and therefore {\i teruma} today carries {\i de-oraita} status. The {\i baraita} concerning the baskets, which assumes {\i teruma} is {\i de-rabbanan}, is the position of the {\i Rabbanan} who dispute Rabbi Yose \u8212- and Rabbi Yochanan accordingly dismisses it as inapplicable to his ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rov & Derabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov question again\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a follow-up question. Even granting that the {\i baraita} of the baskets reflects the {\i Rabbanan} rather than Rabbi Yose \u8212- fine. But Rabbi Yochanan still rules leniently in that {\i baraita}\u8217's case: he does not require a {\i rov} of {\i chullin} over {\i teruma} in order to permit the mixture. If we are operating on the assumption that {\i teruma} is {\i de-rabbanan}, should he not at minimum require a {\i rov}? Why does Rabbi Yochanan permit without one?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikveh drawn water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve this, the {\i gemara} invokes a {\i mishna} in {\i Mikvaot}: a {\i mikveh} containing exactly forty {\i se\u8217'ah} \u8212- the minimum for a valid {\i mikveh} \u8212- into which one pours a {\i se\u8217'ah} of something problematic, such as fruit juice, and then removes a {\i se\u8217'ah} of the now-mixed water. Despite the fact that this process dilutes the proportion of valid {\i mikveh} water, the {\i mishna} rules: {\i kosher}. On this {\i mishna}, Rabbi Yochanan said \u8212- via the transmission of Rabbi Yehuda bar Shila citing Rav Assi \u8212- {\i ad rubbo}: this process may continue until the majority of the {\i mikveh} is no longer valid water. The reason Rabbi Yochanan permits up to that point \u8212- but not beyond \u8212- is that {\i mayim she\u8217'uvim}, drawn water, is a {\i de-rabbanan} problem, and therefore a {\i rov} of valid water is sufficient to maintain the {\i mikveh}\u8217's status. This demonstrates that Rabbi Yochanan does hold that for {\i de-rabbanan} prohibitions, a {\i rov} suffices \u8212- but he does not require {\i more} than a {\i rov}, and in fact he permits up to but not exceeding that threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot kashya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a significant difficulty on this point. The {\i mishna} in {\i Mikvaot} is not necessarily speaking about {\i mayim she\u8217'uvim} \u8212- drawn water \u8212- at all. It may be referring to fruit juice or similar liquids that invalidate a {\i mikveh} on a {\i de-oraita} level. If so, how can Rabbi Yochanan permit the {\i mikveh} to continue functioning when up to half its content consists of something that is {\i de-oraita} problematic? Rov should not suffice in a case of biblical prohibition. {\i Tosafot} acknowledges this as a genuine difficulty and offers answers that are not entirely satisfying. The question itself, however, is an important one for understanding the scope of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's principle, and one should keep it in mind when applying his ruling more broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For practical purposes, this issue does not arise in contemporary {\i mikveh} construction, as one does not pour fruit juice into a {\i mikveh}. But analytically, {\i Tosafot}\u8217's challenge remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek status vs event\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers a second answer \u8212- {\i ve-iba\u8217'it eima} \u8212- which it presents as a fully acceptable resolution. In the case of the two baskets, Rabbi Yochanan is not relying on a {\i rov} at all. Rather, he is relying on the principle of {\i she\u8217'ani omer} \u8212- we can say that the {\i teruma} fell into the {\i teruma} basket and the {\i chullin} fell into the {\i chullin} basket. So long as this outcome is a genuine possibility, and there exist two matching pairs \u8212- two baskets, two {\i se\u8217'in} \u8212- we are entitled to invoke this reasoning without needing to establish a probabilistic majority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a natural question: why is this principle of {\i she\u8217'ani omer} not applied to the {\i androgynus} himself? If the {\i androgynus} is a {\i safek} as to his gender, could one not similarly say {\i she\u8217'ani omer} \u8212- perhaps he is male? The answer lies in a crucial distinction: in the case of the baskets, the {\i safek} concerns an {\i event} \u8212- which {\i se\u8217'ah} fell where \u8212- and it is a plausible and equal possibility that each fell into the right place. In the case of the {\i androgynus}, the {\i safek} is not about an event but about {\i status} \u8212- what kind of being he is. A {\i safek} about an ongoing ontological status is categorically different from a {\i safek} about a discrete occurrence, and the principle of {\i she\u8217'ani omer} does not extend from one to the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Androgynus status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Noseh vs Nisha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i mishna} itself to probe the precise status of the {\i androgynus}. The {\i mishna} states: {\i androgynus noseh} \u8212- an {\i androgynus} marries \u8212- {\i aval lo nisa} \u8212- but is not wedded. The {\i gemara} investigates whether {\i noseh} is intended {\i lechatchila} \u8212- as an initial, affirmative ruling \u8212- or only {\i be-di\u8217'avad} \u8212- retroactively valid after the fact. The language of {\i noseh} would seem to suggest an active, prospective permission, implying that the {\i androgynus} has a sufficiently clear male status to marry {\i lechatchila}. The {\i gemara} therefore tests whether {\i noseh} should be read as {\i im nasa} \u8212- if he married, it is valid \u8212- treating it as a {\i be-di\u8217'avad} formulation rather than a {\i lechatchila} one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechatchila vs Di\u8217'avad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: but the {\i mishna} says {\i noseh} \u8212- the active form. If you reinterpret that as {\i di\u8217'avad}, what then do you do with {\i lo nisa} \u8212- the passive, \u8220"is not wedded\u8221"? That formulation seems itself to be {\i be-di\u8217'avad}: even if someone married him in the feminine role, it is not valid. This would yield a reading where {\i noseh} is {\i lechatchila} and {\i lo nisa} is {\i di\u8217'avad}, or alternatively, both are {\i di\u8217'avad} \u8212- meaning {\i noseh be-di\u8217'avad} and even {\i di\u8217'avad} he cannot be wedded. Perhaps the {\i mishna} operates on a {\i lo zu af zu} principle: even the more lenient retroactive case is not valid. Ultimately the {\i gemara} leaves this reading under discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sekila & gender\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling: {\i chayavin alav sekila ke-zachar} \u8212- one who has relations with a married {\i androgynus} incurs {\i sekila}, stoning, just as with a male. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's formulation strongly implies that the {\i androgynus} is treated as fully male. The {\i gemara} infers from the fact that Rabbi Eliezer found it necessary to state this explicitly that the {\i tanna kamma} is apparently uncertain \u8212- {\i mispaka lei} \u8212- regarding the {\i androgynus}\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sekila mishnei mekomot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this inference. In fact, {\i bein le-mar bein le-mar mipshat pshita lei} \u8212- everyone agrees that the {\i androgynus} is definitively male; the dispute between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Eliezer is not about his fundamental status. Rather, the disagreement is {\i sekila mishnei mekomot} \u8212- whether {\i sekila} applies depending on the nature of the sexual act. The {\i tanna kamma} holds that regardless of which form of relations one has with the {\i androgynus}, {\i sekila} applies. Rabbi Eliezer holds {\i ke-zachar} \u8212- specifically in the masculine mode, i.e., {\i bimkom zachrut}. If one has relations through the feminine aspect of the {\i androgynus}, Rabbi Eliezer would not impose {\i sekila} for that act. This, and not any uncertainty about maleness, is the substance of their disagreement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Androgynus category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav now intervenes with a striking challenge. He declares: {\i leita matnita kemei baraita} \u8212- this {\i mishna} does not stand in light of a {\i baraita} I can bring. The {\i baraita} cites Rabbi Yose: {\i androgynus beriya bifnei atzma hu, lo hichri\u8217'u bo chachamim} \u8212- the {\i androgynus} is a creature unto itself, a third independent category; the {\i chachamim} have not determined it to be male or female. This directly contradicts the thrust of our {\i mishna}, which treats the {\i androgynus} as essentially male. After the extended discussion in which Rabbi Yose was portrayed as holding that the {\i androgynus} is male, Rav now produces a {\i baraita} in which Rabbi Yose himself declares the opposite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna vs Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back: {\i aderaba leita le-baraita dekamei matnitin} \u8212- on the contrary, why should the {\i baraita} take precedence over the {\i mishna}? This question reflects a broader principle that deserves attention: in many places in the {\i gemara}, it is entirely clear that a {\i mishna} and a {\i baraita} are considered {\i shkulim} \u8212- completely equal in authority. The fact that a ruling was included in the {\i mishna} rather than left as a {\i baraita} does not automatically grant it superiority. One cannot simply say \u8220"it is in the {\i mishna}\u8221" as a trump card.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oral canonization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to an important dimension of the {\i mishna}\u8217's character. The {\i mishna} was not, in the first instance, a written document. Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi canonized a certain collection of oral material as the authoritative code \u8212- a set text that was recited orally and transmitted accordingly. It remained in that form for centuries. The {\i Geonim} in their {\i yeshivot} still used oral {\i tannaim} to recite the {\i mishna}, even as manuscript fragments were beginning to circulate. The earliest manuscript evidence of the {\i mishna} as a written text dates to approximately the 10th century, with one fragment from {\i Chullin} traceable to the 8th. The nature of the {\i Seder Olam} and other early compilations similarly reflects a world of oral, canonized texts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} exists precisely to preserve the {\i baraitot} that were not incorporated into the {\i mishna} \u8212- another canonized but secondary collection that supplements and parallels the {\i mishna} without superseding it. Not every {\i baraita} appears in the {\i Tosefta}, but everything in the {\i Tosefta} is a {\i baraita}, and both carry the same fundamental standing as transmitted {\i tannaitic} teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Changed position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve the conflict between {\i mishna} and {\i baraita}, the {\i gemara} offers a decisive inference: {\i mideshavkei Rabbi Yose le-var zugya shma mina hadar bei} \u8212- from the fact that Rabbi Yose in the {\i mishna} formulated his ruling together with Rabbi Shimon, whereas in the {\i baraita} he speaks alone, we infer that he changed his mind. In the {\i mishna}, Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon together implied that the {\i androgynus} is male. In the {\i baraita}, Rabbi Yose appears independently and declares the {\i androgynus} to be a {\i beriya bifnei atzmah}. His separation from Rabbi Shimon suggests he revised his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a counterpoint: perhaps Rabbi Yose held his own view originally and then temporarily joined Rabbi Shimon, before reverting to his independent position. {\i Tosafot} acknowledges they have no clean answer to this and flag it as a genuine difficulty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel preference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now records Shmuel\u8217's general ruling on the relationship between {\i mishna} and {\i baraita}: {\i leita le-baraita dekamei matnitin} \u8212- the {\i baraita} does not stand before the {\i mishna}. Shmuel grants the {\i mishna} authority over a contradicting {\i baraita}. This is grounded in Shmuel\u8217's broader principle: he is generally {\i choshesh} \u8212- concerned \u8212- for a {\i da\u8217'at yachid}, a minority individual opinion, and is inclined to rule {\i le-chumra} in accordance with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yachida in Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} qualifies this principle. Shmuel\u8217's stringency toward a {\i da\u8217'at yachid} applies specifically when that individual opinion appears {\i in the mishna}. When the {\i da\u8217'at yachid} appears only in a {\i baraita} and contradicts a {\i mishna} that records the more general, lenient ruling, Shmuel does not follow the {\i yachid}. In such a case, the combined weight of the {\i mishna}\u8217's general position and its canonical status overcomes the stringent individual view preserved only in a {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna authority (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this framework flows the practical {\i halacha}: we follow Rabbi Yose on the question of the {\i androgynus} and on the question of {\i harkava} \u8212- grafting. The resolution of the conflict between {\i mishna} and {\i baraita} on the {\i androgynus}, and Shmuel\u8217's rule about the authority of the {\i mishna} over a contradicting {\i baraita}, lead to this conclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yose rulings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Androgynus & graft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The school of Rav ({\i bei Rav}) states in the name of Rav: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yose be-androgynus u-ve-harkava} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yose with regard to the {\i androgynus} and with regard to grafting. It should be noted that {\i bei Rav} \u8212- the school or {\i beit midrash} of Rav \u8212- does not always transmit Rav\u8217's own positions exclusively; there is considerable cross-pollination between the academies of Sura and Nehardea, and a transmission {\i mishmo de-Rav} in this context signals the chain of transmission rather than a rigid institutional allegiance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth & field\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel offers parallel rulings in two other domains: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yose be-koshi u-ve-kidush} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yose in the case of {\i koshi} (the woman experiencing difficulty in childbirth) and in the case of {\i kidush} (the mixed or hybrid field, referring to {\i kilei ha-kerem}, the prohibition of mixed plantings in a vineyard, {\i de-kadesh amra Torah}).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Grafting timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmitta restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concerning {\i harkava} establishes the following rule: one may not plant, clear land for agricultural use ({\i mavrichin} \u8212- understood here as bending and inserting a vine into the ground), or graft in the {\i Elul} immediately preceding the {\i shmitta} year if one is doing so fewer than thirty days before Rosh Hashanah. This period of thirty days constitutes {\i tosefet shvi\u8217'it} \u8212- the additional pre-{\i shmitta} period in which such activities are forbidden. If one did plant, clear, or graft during this prohibited window, one must uproot what was done: {\i ya\u8217'akor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Graft takeover time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} records a dispute about how long it takes for a graft to \u8220"take\u8221" \u8212- to bond with and draw sustenance from the host plant. Rabbi Yehuda holds that a graft takes only three days to take hold; if it does not take within three days, it will not take at all. Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon, by contrast, hold that a graft requires two weeks \u8212- {\i shtei shavuot} \u8212- to fully take hold. This same dispute appears in the tractate of Rosh Hashanah (10b).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman, citing Rabba bar Avuha, derives a practical ruling from this dispute. The thirty-day prohibition of {\i tosefet shvi\u8217'it} is measured against the point at which the grafting process is fully complete. Accordingly, one must count backward not merely thirty days but thirty days {\i plus} the time it takes for the graft to take hold. According to the view that a graft takes three days: one must graft no fewer than thirty-three days before Rosh Hashanah. According to the view of Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon that it takes two weeks: one must graft no fewer than thirty plus fourteen days \u8212- forty-four days \u8212- before Rosh Hashanah. The thirty-day figure in the {\i mishna} thus represents the fixed {\i tosefet shvi\u8217'it} window, to which the grafting period must be added.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Difficult birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zava status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yose in {\i koshi} directs us to a {\i mishna} that addresses the following situation. A woman who sees blood outside her normal menstrual cycle is subject to the laws of {\i zivah}. If she sees such blood on three consecutive days, she becomes a {\i zava} and must count seven clean days before immersing. These {\i halachot} are {\i de-oraita} in character, though less practically operative today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pregnant woman labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A pregnant woman in labor, however, presents a distinct case. Blood that appears as a result of contractions \u8212- {\i koshi}, the difficulty of labor \u8212- is not counted as {\i zivah} bleeding, even if it is continuous. The blood is attributed to the birthing process rather than to the condition of {\i zov}. Moreover, this exemption applies even if the blood is continuous from its onset until the moment of birth: a woman who bleeds continuously from the onset of labor contractions until delivery is not classified as a {\i yoledet be-zov} \u8212- one who gives birth while in a state of {\i zivah} \u8212- because the blood is subsumed under the birthing process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then becomes: how far back before the actual birth can this reasoning extend? If a pregnant woman in, say, her sixth month begins to bleed continuously all the way until delivery, does the entire period of bleeding count as {\i koshi}? This cannot extend indefinitely, because attributing months of continuous bleeding to labor contractions defies biological reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bleeding duration limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} records three positions on this question. Rabbi Meir holds that either forty or fifty days before the birth is the outer limit: if the bleeding began continuously that far back and continued until delivery, we attribute the entire period to {\i koshi} and she is not a {\i zava}. Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i daya chodsha} \u8212- a month suffices; Rashi understands this as measured from Rosh Chodesh of the month in which she gives birth, though the plain meaning could equally indicate a calendar month before delivery. Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon hold: {\i ein kishuy yoter mi-shtei shabbatot} \u8212- {\i koshi} can extend no more than two weeks. Beyond two weeks before the birth, bleeding is no longer attributed to labor contractions, and the woman would be subject to the ordinary {\i halachot} of {\i zivah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 83\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel & Rav Positio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dvei Rav Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion picks up where it left off. {\i Dvei Rav} \u8212- the school or circle of Rav \u8212- had established that Rav rules in accordance with Rabbi Yose in two distinct cases: the case of the {\i androgynos} (hermaphrodite), which has been the central subject of the chapter, and the case of {\i harkava} (grafting), which the Gemara illustrated with a concrete example. In addition, a statement in Shmuel\u8217's name was cited regarding a woman who bleeds as a result of contractions or some physiological event connected to pregnancy. The question there is whether such bleeding constitutes {\i zivah} (the type of abnormal discharge that triggers a more stringent impurity), and Shmuel rules in accordance with Rabbi Yose. Shmuel also rules like Rabbi Yose in the case of {\i kiddush} \u8212- the subject to which the Gemara now turns.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddush Hakerem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddush Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces the concept of {\i kiddush hakerem} through a {\i mishnah}: {\i hamsakech gafno al gabei tevuato shel chaveiro harei ze kideish vechayav be\u8217'achrayuto divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- one who drapes his grapevine so that it overhangs his fellow\u8217's grain crop has created a situation of {\i kil\u8217'ei hakerem}, a forbidden mixture of vine and other species. The result is that the grain beneath becomes {\i kodesh} in the sense of being forbidden for use and benefit; it must be burned. Rabbi Meir further holds that this person bears financial liability for his fellow\u8217's losses. Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon, however, take a different view: {\i ein adam oseir davar she\u8217'eino shelo} \u8212- a person cannot render {\i asur} that which does not belong to him. Although {\i kilayim} is fundamentally a state-of-being violation rooted in the physical proximity of two different species, Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon maintain that the prohibition has a proprietary dimension: one cannot go around making someone else\u8217's field {\i kodesh} by extending one\u8217's own vine over it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection of this case to the broader list is worth noting. Both Rashi and Tosafot observe that in every instance where Rav rules like Rabbi Yose, Rabbi Shimon is a co-signatory to that same position. These are not cases of Rabbi Yose alone; they are consistently cases where Rabbi Yose and Rabbi Shimon form a pair \u8212- a fact that draws these otherwise disparate topics more tightly into a single conceptual grouping.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel & Rav Positio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel & Androgynos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i androgynos ma li amar Shmuel?} \u8212- in the case of the {\i androgynos}, what is Shmuel\u8217's position? The answer: {\i de\u8217'amar Shmuel leRav Anan leita livraita mikamei matnitin} \u8212- Shmuel told Rav Anan that the {\i baraita} does not stand in the face of the {\i mishnah}. The {\i baraita} in question is the one in which Rabbi Yose says that an {\i androgynos} is {\i birya bifnei atzmah} \u8212- its own category \u8212- and that the {\i chachamim} never rendered a definitive judgment as to whether it is male or female. The {\i mishnah}, by contrast, presents Rabbi Yose as holding that an {\i androgynos} is male in status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a significant question that Tosafot develops at length: is the {\i androgynos} simply a case of {\i safek} (unresolved doubt) that the {\i chachamim} never adjudicated, or is it a genuine third category, a {\i birya bifnei atzmah mamash}, a distinct gender that is neither male nor female? Tosafot tend toward the view that in this {\i baraita} Rabbi Yose means a genuinely independent third category, while acknowledging that other {\i mishnaic} passages treat the {\i androgynos} as a {\i safek}. The tension remains unresolved. In any case, Shmuel\u8217's ruling that the {\i mishnah} prevails over the {\i baraita} signals that the authoritative statement of Rabbi Yose is the one found in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- which implies that Shmuel endorses that position, since there would be little reason to evaluate which formulation of Rabbi Yose is authoritative if Shmuel did not intend to rule accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grafting & Shemita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i harkavah mah li amar Shmuel?} \u8212- in the case of grafting, what is Shmuel\u8217's position? Again Shmuel speaks to Rav Anan: {\i tanei keman de\u8217'amar sheloshah ushloshim} \u8212- teach the {\i baraita} in accordance with the one who says thirty-three days. To appreciate this, one must recall the background. The question concerns how much time before the {\i shemita} year one must complete one\u8217's final grafting. One opinion holds that the cutoff is thirty days before the beginning of the {\i shemita} year; another holds that three days suffice. But Rav Nachman clarified that the real discussion concerns not the month of Elul \u8212- in which everyone agrees that such agricultural work is forbidden \u8212- but rather the month of Tammuz, when the question is how much lead time the graft needs before entering the month of Elul. Shmuel rules in accordance with the position that three days are sufficient for a graft to take root, which means one may graft as late as three days before the end of Tammuz.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why does grafting appear in a list otherwise dominated by issues of personal status and sacrificial law? The answer is organizational rather than thematic. In an oral culture, a useful mnemonic device is to group rulings together not by subject but by the authority who issued them. Remembering that \u8220"Rav rules like Rabbi Yose in the following cases\u8221" \u8212- and the same for Shmuel \u8212- provides a framework for retaining disparate {\i psakim} across entirely unrelated domains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to Rav\u8217's positions on the remaining items in the list. On {\i koshi} \u8212- the question of whether a woman\u8217's bleeding during labor is associated with her contractions or constitutes independent {\i zivah} \u8212- Rav\u8217's position is simply unknown. {\i Teku.} On {\i kiddush}, the vine overhanging another\u8217's field, the Gemara proceeds to a dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddush Hakerem (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef & Huna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the question of Rav\u8217's ruling in {\i kiddush hakerem}, two conflicting traditions emerge. Rav Yosef cites a tradition in the name of Rav Huna, who said in Rav\u8217's name: {\i ein halacha ke\u8217'Rabbi Yose} \u8212- the {\i halacha} does not follow Rabbi Yose. Abaye, Rav Yosef\u8217's student, challenges this: why privilege that tradition? There is an equally valid statement in the name of Rav Ada bar Ahava, who likewise transmitted in Rav\u8217's name: {\i halacha ke\u8217'Rabbi Yose} \u8212- the {\i halacha} does follow Rabbi Yose. The result is an apparent contradiction between two reliable transmitters of Rav\u8217's positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution draws on the identity of {\i Bei Rav} \u8212- the school that originally established the list of cases in which Rav rules like Rabbi Yose. The Gemara concludes that {\i Bei Rav} refers to Rav Huna. Since it is Rav Huna himself who is the source of the tradition that Rav rules like Rabbi Yose in those enumerated cases, and since that same Rav Huna states explicitly that Rav does {\i not} rule like Rabbi Yose in the case of {\i kiddush hakerem}, the implication is clear: {\i kiddush hakerem} was never part of that list to begin with. Rav Huna\u8217's tradition, which defines the scope of the list, takes precedence over Rav Ada\u8217's contrary report. Rav does not rule like Rabbi Yose in the case of the vine overhanging another\u8217's field.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumtum & Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda Tumtum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to the {\i mishnah} on 81a. Rabbi Yehuda states: {\i tumtum shenikra venimtza zachar lo yachlotz mipnei shehu kesaris} \u8212- a {\i tumtum}, a person whose genitalia are concealed by an undifferentiated membrane such that one cannot determine the sex, who undergoes a tearing or rupturing ({\i nikra}) of that membrane and is found to be male, should nonetheless not perform {\i chalitza}, because such a person is like a {\i saris} (eunuch). The logic: since the {\i tumtum} is presumed to be a {\i saris}, he is exempt from {\i yibum}, and consequently he cannot perform {\i chalitza} either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birei\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami challenges Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position with a concrete case: {\i mai avid leh Rabbi Yehuda letumtum devirei} \u8212- what would Rabbi Yehuda do with the famous {\i tumtum} from Birei, who was placed on a special chair for the procedure, underwent the rupturing, was found to be male, and then fathered seven children? How can one maintain that every {\i tumtum} who is found to be male is necessarily a {\i saris}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's response is striking: {\i chazor al banav me\u8217'ayin hem} \u8212- examine his children carefully; you will find they are not his. In Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, it is established medical fact that {\i tumtums} revealed to be male are sterile. The {\i chazaka} that children born to a married woman belong to her husband does not apply here, because once you are no longer dealing with a standard case \u8212- once you have a man known to be infertile \u8212- the ordinary presumption of paternity cannot be maintained. The {\i tumtum} who appears to have fathered children has not undermined the principle; rather, something else has occurred that the community was charitable enough not to investigate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose b\u8217'R. Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} introduces Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's own son, who follows broadly in his father\u8217's direction but with a distinct formulation. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i tumtum lo yachlotz shema yikara venimtza saris chama} \u8212- a {\i tumtum} should not perform {\i chalitza}, lest the {\i nikra} occur and he be found to be a {\i saris chama}, a natural eunuch whose sexual organs never fully developed. Since a {\i saris chama} is exempt from {\i yibum}, his {\i chalitza} would be retroactively invalid, and any woman who had proceeded on the basis of that {\i chalitza} would find herself still bound by {\i zika} to the remaining brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, presses on this formulation. Is the primary concern really that the {\i tumtum} will turn out to be a male {\i saris chama}? Surely the more immediate concern is that the {\i tumtum} will turn out to be female altogether. A {\i tumtum} who is {\i nikra} and found to be a woman creates no {\i zika} and no {\i yibum} obligation; but a {\i tumtum} who is {\i nikra} and found to be male but also a {\i saris chama} leaves the {\i yevama} in a complicated situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore reconstructs what Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda actually meant: {\i shema yikarei venimtzah nekeivah ve\u8217'afilu nimtza zachar shema yimatzei saris chama} \u8212- perhaps, upon rupturing, the {\i tumtum} will be found to be female; and even if found to be male, perhaps that male will turn out to be a {\i saris chama}. Both possibilities counsel against permitting {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i mai beinaihu} \u8212- where exactly do Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda disagree? Rabbi Yehuda holds that {\i every} male {\i tumtum} who is {\i nikra} is certainly a {\i saris}. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda says it is a matter of {\i safek}: perhaps he is a {\i saris chama}, perhaps not. This distinction has practical consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One must be clear about the underlying scenario. The case involves a {\i tumtum} together with at least one other living brother. One brother has died; his widow is now a {\i yevama}. The {\i tumtum} must not perform {\i chalitza} because: if the {\i nikra} reveals him to be female, then {\i yibum} never applied in the first place; and if the {\i nikra} reveals him to be male, he might still be a {\i saris chama}, rendering his {\i chalitza} null and the widow still bound by {\i zika} to the other brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava articulates the precise {\i machloket}: {\i lifsol bemakom achin velachalotz shelo bemakom achin ika beinaihu} \u8212- the dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda concerns two distinct scenarios: whether the {\i tumtum\u8217's} {\i chalitza} disqualifies the {\i yevama} to the surviving brothers when those brothers exist, and whether the {\i tumtum} may perform {\i chalitza} when there are no other brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rashi\u8217's exposition: if the {\i tumtum} has already performed {\i chalitza} in the presence of other brothers, Rabbi Yehuda would say his {\i chalitza} is null \u8212- he is certainly a {\i saris} \u8212- and therefore she is not {\i pasul} to the other brothers and may undergo {\i yibum}. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, holding that the question remains a {\i safek}, would say that if there is any chance the {\i chalitza} was valid, she is now {\i pasul} to the brothers and they cannot perform {\i yibum} until the matter is clarified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second scenario, where there are no other brothers, Rabbi Yehuda would say: there is no point in performing {\i chalitza} at all, since he is a {\i saris} and no obligation of {\i yibum} applies. Permitting {\i chalitza} would needlessly render her {\i asur} to a {\i kohen}, for instance. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, would permit the {\i chalitza} to proceed. If he turns out to be a fertile male, the {\i chalitza} is valid and resolves her situation. If not, nothing has been lost. In this reading, Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position is significantly more lenient \u8212- and more practically accommodating of the {\i yevama}\u8217's circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of no other brothers also bears on the possibility of {\i agunah}. If the {\i tumtum} is not {\i nikra} and there are surviving brothers, the woman remains in a state of uncertainty, bound by {\i zika} and unable to remarry. While this is less severe than a full {\i agunah} \u8212- since she is forbidden to the rest of the world only by a {\i lav} \u8212- it remains a pressing practical concern.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Androgynos Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records: {\i androgynos chayavin alav skila mishtei mekomot} \u8212- Rav states that one is liable to {\i skila} (stoning) for sexual intercourse with an {\i androgynos} in either of two modes. The reference, as established in the prior session, is to both anal and vaginal intercourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited that appears to challenge Rav\u8217's ruling. Rabbi Eliezer says: {\i androgynos chayavin alav skila k\u8217'vizachar} \u8212- one is liable for intercourse with an {\i androgynos} like one is liable for intercourse with a male \u8212- that is, only by way of his male aspect. {\i Bameh devarim amurim bizchrut shelo} \u8212- when are these words said? In the male mode. {\i Aval b\u8217'nikvut shelo patur} \u8212- but by way of the female aspect, one is exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Simai Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension by locating Rav within a different tannaitic tradition. {\i Hu de\u8217'amar ki hai tanna} \u8212- Rav speaks in accordance with this {\i tanna}: Rabbi Simai, who says that one is liable to {\i skila} for intercourse with an {\i androgynos} in both modes. Rav is thus not in conflict with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i baraita}; he simply follows Rabbi Simai\u8217's view. And since Rav is categorized as one who has the standing of a {\i tanna} \u8212- {\i Rav tanna hu u\u8217'palig} \u8212- a contradicting {\i baraita} would not have constituted a fatal challenge in any case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midrashic Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Rabbi Simai?} \u8212- what is the {\i drashah} that grounds Rabbi Simai\u8217's position? Rava bar Hamdurei explains: {\i v\u8217'et zachar lo tishkav mishkevei ishah} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} prohibiting homosexual intercourse speaks of \u8220"the lyings of a woman\u8221" ({\i mishkevei ishah}). The {\i derasha} proceeds: {\i eizehu zachar sheyesh bo shenei mishkavot?} \u8212- what kind of male can have two types of {\i mishkavot}? {\i Hevei omer zeh androgynos} \u8212- one must say this refers to an {\i androgynos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sage vs.\u160 ?Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i midrashic} derivation immediately raises a challenge. If the {\i pasuk} is interpreted to be specifically about an {\i androgynos}, from where do the Rabbis derive the prohibition of ordinary homosexual intercourse? {\i Minalhu?} The answer: {\i me\u8217'ishah} \u8212- from the word {\i ishah} at the end of the verse. The extra word {\i ishah} teaches that even straightforward homosexual intercourse is forbidden. But this generates a further difficulty. In many contexts {\i mishkevei ishah} is the source for the principle that anal intercourse with any woman one is forbidden to is itself a prohibited act ({\i be\u8217'isha shelo kedarka}). If that derivation is now redirected toward the {\i androgynos} case, from where is {\i be\u8217'isha shelo kedarka} derived?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer is that the source lies in the combination of the {\i vav} at the beginning of {\i ve\u8217'et zachar} and the word {\i ishah} at the end of the verse: {\i ve\u8217'et zachar lo tishkav mishkevei ishah}. This is admittedly a difficult piece of {\i midrash}. Some {\i nuscha\u8217'im} read {\i ve\u8217'et ishah} rather than the standard text, which would alter the derivation, and the {\i Bach} as well as the {\i Masoret HaShas} note these textual variants. The precise mechanics of the derivation remain elusive. One might indeed suggest, as a general principle, that in certain cases the Rabbis possessed a firm tradition that a given act was prohibited at the {\i d\u8217'oraita} level and were working to find an asmachta in the text \u8212- though one would hope for a more transparent {\i drasha} here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of the dispute is that Rabbi Eliezer and the Sages hold the same overall {\i pasuk} but derive its scope differently. According to Rabbi Eliezer, the {\i androgynos} is treated as male, and therefore only the male mode of intercourse with him generates liability. The Sages and Rabbi Simai derive from the plural {\i mishkevei} that both modes of intercourse with an {\i androgynos} render one liable to {\i skila}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Androgynos Sacrifices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shizbi in the name of Rav Chisda clarifies the scope of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position: {\i lo lekol amar Rabbi Eliezer androgynus zachar ma\u8217'alya} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer did not say that an {\i androgynos} is a full male for every purpose. Why not? {\i Hu she\u8217'im attah omer ken bemo kodeshim yikdash} \u8212- because if one were to say so, then a hermaphroditic animal offered as a sacrifice would have genuine {\i kedushat kodashim}, which Rabbi Eliezer plainly does not hold. The Gemara therefore asks: from where do we know that an {\i androgynos} animal does not acquire {\i kedusha} in that context?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bird Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited. Among the categories of animals that are {\i pasul} for the Temple \u8212- an animal used for bestiality, one set aside for {\i avodah zarah}, one that was actually worshipped, one purchased with the fee of a prostitute ({\i etnan zonah}), and one purchased with the price of a dog ({\i mchir kelev}, which in the biblical context may refer to male prostitutes, though the Gemara\u8217's own understanding may lean toward a literal dog) \u8212- the {\i baraita} includes a {\i tumtum} or {\i androgynos}. These animals, the Sages say, cause ritual impurity to garments upon being swallowed, {\i mitam\u8217'im begadim beit habli\u8217'ah} \u8212- which is the specific impurity that affects one who eats {\i nivlat of} (the carcass of a bird that died without valid {\i shechita}). Since birds are not slaughtered in the Temple but rather undergo {\i melika} (a snapping of the neckbone from behind), an invalid bird \u8212- one that cannot be brought as a {\i korban} \u8212- when subjected to {\i melika} does not achieve the status of valid {\i shechita}, and therefore what results is a {\i neveilah}, which triggers this form of impurity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer, however, says that a {\i tumtum} or {\i androgynos} does {\i not} cause this impurity \u8212- {\i ein metam\u8217'im begadim beit habli\u8217'ah}. This initially seems to prove the opposite of what is needed: if Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i melika} on a {\i tumtum} or {\i androgynos} bird does not produce {\i neveilah} impurity, does that not imply that he considers such animals valid for the Temple?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Principle Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The apparent contradiction is resolved by Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's own stated principle: {\i kol makom shene\u8217'emar zachar unekeva ata motzi tumtum ve\u8217'androgynus mibeineihem} \u8212- wherever the Torah specifies \u8220"male\u8221" or \u8220"female\u8221" in connection with sacrifices, a {\i tumtum} or {\i androgynos} is excluded. For four-legged animals, the Torah consistently designates the required gender of the offering \u8212- {\i seh zachar tamim}, or {\i seh nekeva} \u8212- and therefore a {\i tumtum} or {\i androgynos} animal cannot be brought as a {\i korban}. {\i Ve\u8217'of ho\u8217'il velo ne\u8217'emar bo zachar unekeva i ata motzi tumtum ve\u8217'androgynus mibeineihem} \u8212- but birds, since the Torah does not specify gender when discussing bird offerings, a {\i tumtum} or {\i androgynos} bird is not excluded from consideration. Hence Rabbi Eliezer holds that such a bird {\i can} be brought as a {\i korban}, the {\i melika} is valid, and the result is not a {\i neveilah} \u8212- which is precisely why it does not cause garment impurity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This clarifies Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's full position: an {\i androgynos} is not a {\i zachar lakol} \u8212- not treated as fully male for all purposes. Where the Torah specifies gender (as it does for most four-legged {\i korbanot}), the {\i androgynos} is excluded. Where the Torah does not specify gender (as with bird offerings), the {\i androgynos} may qualify. This is consistent with his view that the {\i androgynos} is in some respects male (hence {\i skila} for the male mode of intercourse with him) but not in all respects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temura Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak \u8212- who characteristically sees himself as an organizer and cross-referencer rather than an originator of novel rulings \u8212- adds: {\i af anan nami tanina} \u8212- I too can cite a {\i mishnah} to this effect, not merely a {\i baraita}. The {\i mishnah} is: {\i hakilayim utreifa veyotzei dofen tumtum ve\u8217'androgynus lo kedoshim velo makdishim} \u8212- a hybrid animal, a {\i treifa}, an animal born by cesarean, a {\i tumtum}, or an {\i androgynos} \u8212- none of these animals can receive {\i kedusha}, and none of them can transfer {\i kedusha} to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel explains that this {\i mishnah} refers specifically to {\i temura}, the act of substituting one animal for another. In {\i temura}, if one attempts to replace a dedicated {\i korban} by designating a different animal in its stead, the result is that both animals become {\i kodesh}. But if one tries to perform {\i temura} using an {\i androgynos} animal \u8212- either as the subject being swapped out or as the replacement \u8212- nothing happens. The {\i androgynos} animal neither acquires {\i kedusha} through the act of substitution nor transmits it. An {\i androgynos} animal that is already {\i kodesh} by virtue of being born from a {\i kodesh} mother is {\i kodesh} by birth; but it cannot be an active participant in the mechanics of {\i temura}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: is {\i androgynos} automatically a {\i mum} that would disqualify a {\i korban}? The answer is no \u8212- {\i androgynos} is not classified as a {\i mum}. An {\i androgynos} animal born to a {\i kodesh} mother remains {\i kodesh} but with no valid sacrificial use, and therefore it grazes ({\i yir\u8217'eh}) until it develops an actual {\i mum}, at which point its {\i kedusha} can be transferred and the animal redeemed. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling confirms that Rabbi Eliezer does not treat the {\i androgynos} as a {\i zachar lakol}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Personal Account\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a {\i baraita} in which Rabbi (Yehudah HaNasi) offers a personal account: {\i kshehalachti lilmod Torah etzel Rabbi Elazar ben Shamoa} \u8212- when I went to learn Torah from Rabbi Elazar ben Shamoa, the famous student of Rabbi Akiva. His disciples crowded around him so aggressively, {\i ketarnigolim shel beit bukya} \u8212- like the roosters of Beit Bukya, a place known for its particularly combative fowl \u8212- {\i velo hinichuni lilmod} \u8212- and they did not allow me to learn. The account has a certain pathos. Whether it reflects the disciples\u8217' protective jealousy of their time with Rabbi Elazar ben Shamoa, or an almost overwhelming excitement at having the future {\i Nasi} in their midst, we cannot say. The incident is cited in the {\i baraita} immediately following a discussion of {\i of} (birds), and the reference to roosters connects evocatively to the surrounding material \u8212- though presumably that juxtaposition is incidental.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi reports that despite this interference he was able to learn one thing: {\i ela davar echad bemishnatenu Rabbi Eliezer omer androgynus chayavin alav skila kezachar} \u8212- only one teaching from their {\i mishnah}: Rabbi Eliezer holds that one is liable to {\i skila} for intercourse with an {\i androgynos} as one is liable for intercourse with a male. This single transmitted teaching encapsulates the fundamental ruling of the chapter\u8217's concluding passage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 End He-Arel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach He-arel} \u8212- with this, the chapter {\i He-Arel} concludes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opening {\i mishnah} of the new chapter introduces a typology of women\u8217's permissibility and prohibition in the context of marriage and {\i yibum}. {\i Yesh mutarot l\u8217'va\u8217'aleihen va-asurot livyameihen} \u8212- there are women who are permitted to their husbands but, should the husband die, are forbidden to the {\i yavam} (the surviving brother obligated in {\i yibum}). Conversely, {\i yesh mutarot livyameihen va-asurot l\u8217'va\u8217'aleihen} \u8212- there are women who are permitted to the {\i yavam} but were, from the outset, forbidden to the husband himself. And of course the majority case: {\i mutarot l\u8217'elu ul\u8217'elu} \u8212- most women are permitted to both, in their respective situations. The {\i mishnah} will proceed to supply concrete examples of each category \u8212- a discussion that belongs to the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 86\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status of Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women & Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah on this amud opens with the question of who is entitled to eat {\i terumah}, and more specifically, which women may eat it by virtue of their spousal connection to a {\i kohen}. The mishnah establishes that a {\i bat Yisrael} who is merely betrothed ({\i me\u8217'ureset}) to a {\i kohen}, or who is pregnant by a {\i kohen} but not yet married, or who is a {\i shomeret yavam} awaiting {\i yibbum} with a {\i kohen} \u8212- in all these cases, she does not eat {\i terumah}. In each scenario, her connection to the {\i kohen} is real but still contingent: the marriage has not been fully concluded, the {\i yibbum} has not yet taken place, or she has conceived but not yet given birth to a living child. That uncertainty is sufficient to withhold the privilege of {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same rule, surprisingly, applies in the reverse direction: a {\i bat kohen} who is connected to a {\i Yisrael} \u8212- through betrothal, pregnancy, or {\i yibbum} \u8212- also may not eat {\i terumah}. This is somewhat counterintuitive. One might have assumed that since these tenuous connections are insufficient to grant a {\i bat Yisrael} access to {\i terumah}, they should equally be insufficient to strip a {\i bat kohen} of it. Yet the mishnah rules that any connection whatsoever to a {\i Yisrael} removes her from the category of those permitted to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic, it seems, is the principle of {\i safek d\u8217'orayta l\u8217'chumra} \u8212- when there is doubt in a matter of Torah law, we rule stringently. For the {\i bat Yisrael}, the uncertainty is whether she will ultimately complete her connection to the {\i kohen} and thereby become entitled to eat {\i terumah}; since we are not yet certain, she does not eat. For the {\i bat kohen}, the uncertainty runs in the opposite direction: perhaps her connection to the {\i Yisrael} will be fully realized, and it will emerge that she ought not to have been eating {\i terumah}. We therefore forbid her in advance. In both cases, the doubt drives the stringent outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women & Ma\u8217'aser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same rules that govern {\i terumah} are applied by the mishnah to {\i ma\u8217'aser} as well. A {\i bat Yisrael} who is betrothed to, pregnant by, or a {\i shomeret yavam} of a {\i Levi} does not eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}. The same logic prevails: parallel to the rules of {\i terumah} for {\i kohanim}, the rules of {\i ma\u8217'aser} apply to {\i Levi\u8217'im}, and the same range of incomplete connections to a {\i Levi} is insufficient to grant access.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Kohen/Levi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah then states that a {\i bat Levi} connected to a {\i kohen} \u8212- and a {\i bat kohen} connected to a {\i Levi} \u8212- eats neither {\i terumah} nor {\i ma\u8217'aser}. This is one of the more unusual {\i halachot} in the mishnah. One can readily understand why a {\i bat Levi} betrothed to a {\i kohen} should not eat {\i terumah} \u8212- she is not yet a full member of the {\i kohen}\u8217's household. But why should a {\i bat kohen} connected to a {\i Levi} be forbidden from eating {\i ma\u8217'aser}? As will emerge later in the {\i sugya}, a {\i kohen} is himself a species of {\i Levi}; if so, the {\i bat kohen} is already associated with the tribe that is entitled to {\i ma\u8217'aser}. The mishnah seems to be anticipating the possibility that she will fully become part of the {\i Levi}\u8217's household, and we therefore restrict her in the interim \u8212- just as we restrict the {\i bat Levi} who might become part of the {\i kohen}\u8217's household. The important underlying question of whether {\i zarim} \u8212- non-{\i Levi\u8217'im} \u8212- can eat {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} at all will occupy the {\i gemara}\u8217's attention in the next section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Meir & Zarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews & Ma\u8217'aser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises an immediate difficulty with the mishnah. When a {\i bat Yisrael} marries a {\i Levi}, why should she be forbidden to eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} while she is only betrothed? After all, even a {\i zarah} \u8212- a complete outsider \u8212- can eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}; why would someone partially connected to a {\i Levi} be more restricted? The {\i gemara} answers, in the name of Rav Nachman citing Shmuel, that the mishnah follows the opinion of Rabbi Meir, who holds that {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} is forbidden to {\i zarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position is grounded in a {\i baraita}: \u8220"{\i terumah} goes to the {\i kohen} and {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} goes to the {\i Levi} \u8212- these are the words of Rabbi Meir.\u8221" The question arises: in what sense does the {\i baraita} imply that {\i zarim} are forbidden from eating {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}? Rashi explains that there is a {\i hekesh} \u8212- a linguistic parallel \u8212- between {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser}, drawn from the verse in the context of {\i ma\u8217'aser}: {\i ki et ma\u8217'asar Bnei Yisrael asher yarimu la-Hashem terumah} \u8212- the Torah applies the term {\i terumah} to {\i ma\u8217'aser}. Just as {\i terumah} is forbidden to {\i zarim}, so too {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} is forbidden to {\i zarim}. This derivation is attributed in the {\i gemara} with the phrase {\i mishmeih d\u8217'Gemara} \u8212- transmitted in the name of the tradition rather than in the name of an identified individual. Rashi notes that this phrase indicates an unbroken chain of transmission whose links are not enumerated, and that it is used to assure us that the {\i amora} is not generating this interpretation himself but rather reporting a received tradition traced back to Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar Permits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: \u8220"Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah permits [it] to the {\i kohen}.\u8221" The {\i gemara} notes that the word {\i matirah} \u8212- he permits \u8212- implies that there is someone who forbids. The {\i gemara} initially finds this strange: is there really any authority who would say that a {\i kohen} cannot eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}? The {\i gemara} points out that this is indeed Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's position in contrast to Rabbi Meir, yet it is perplexing because universally {\i kohanim} are themselves {\i Levi\u8217'im} \u8212- so on what basis would anyone restrict them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Reworking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty by reinterpreting the {\i baraita}. Rather than reading {\i matirah l\u8217'kohen} as \u8220"he permits [the {\i kohen} to eat] {\i ma\u8217'aser},\u8221" the {\i gemara} proposes reading it as {\i notnah af l\u8217'kohen} \u8212- one may give {\i ma\u8217'aser} even to a {\i kohen}. In other words, the dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah is not about whether a {\i kohen} is permitted to consume {\i ma\u8217'aser}, but rather about to whom one may hand it over in the first instance: Rabbi Meir holds that {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} is given specifically to the {\i Levi}, while Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah holds that one may give it even to a {\i kohen} directly. This reinterpretation will also become significant later in the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'aser as Tevul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Linguistic Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, the {\i gemara} inquires further: what exactly is the basis for applying the laws of {\i terumah} to {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} via the {\i hekesh} in the verse? The verse {\i ki et ma\u8217'asar Bnei Yisrael asher yarimu la-Hashem terumah} uses the term {\i terumah} in connection with {\i ma\u8217'aser}, and from this Rabbi Meir derives that just as {\i terumah} is forbidden to {\i zarim}, so too {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} is forbidden to {\i zarim} \u8212- {\i mah terumah asurah l\u8217'zarim af ma\u8217'aser rishon asur l\u8217'zarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then push further: since {\i terumah} carries penalties of {\i mitah b\u8217'yedei shamayim} for intentional consumption by a {\i zar}, and {\i chomesh} \u8212- payment of principal plus a fifth \u8212- for inadvertent consumption, perhaps {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} similarly carries these penalties. If the {\i hekesh} is broad, perhaps the full penal structure of {\i terumah} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Penalties Applied\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises this possibility directly: just as one who eats {\i terumah} illicitly incurs {\i mitah} and pays {\i chomesh}, so perhaps one who eats {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} illicitly also incurs {\i mitah} and {\i chomesh}. The Torah, however, confines these penalties with the words {\i bo v\u8217'lo b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- \u8220"for {\i it},\u8221" meaning for {\i terumah}, but not for {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- and {\i alav v\u8217'lo al ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- the {\i chomesh} applies to {\i terumah} specifically, not to {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}. Thus, while {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} shares with {\i terumah} the quality of being forbidden to {\i zarim}, it does not carry the same severe penalties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers the position of the {\i rabanan} \u8212- those who disagree with Rabbi Meir and permit {\i zarim} to eat {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}. According to them, the {\i hekesh} between {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} does not teach that {\i zarim} are forbidden from eating {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}. Instead it teaches something different: just as {\i terumah} renders produce {\i tovel} \u8212- untithed produce that is forbidden to be eaten until the {\i terumah} has been properly separated \u8212- so too {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} renders produce {\i tovel} when it has not yet been separated. In other words, the {\i hekesh} from {\i terumah} teaches the {\i tovelet} status of {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}, not its prohibition upon {\i zarim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yossi\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yossi\u8217's Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} of Rabbi Yossi is introduced at this point. Rabbi Yossi addresses the question of whether the severe penalty of {\i mitah b\u8217'yedei shamayim} applies only to produce from which no tithes whatsoever have been taken, or whether it extends even to produce from which some tithes have been taken but others remain. He poses the problem as follows: one might have thought that the {\i mitah} penalty applies only to {\i tevel} in its purest form \u8212- produce from which no separation at all has been made. But what if {\i terumah gedolah} has been taken but {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} has not? Or if {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} has been taken but {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} has not? And further still, how do we know that even the failure to separate {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} leaves the produce with the status of {\i tevel} that triggers the death penalty?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Ani Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To extend the prohibition all the way down to {\i ma\u8217'aser ani}, Rabbi Yossi invokes the verse {\i lo tukhal le\u8217'ekhol bi-sh\u8217'arekha} \u8212- \u8220"you cannot eat it in your gates.\u8221" He then draws a {\i gezerah shavah} from a parallel usage of {\i sh\u8217'arekha} in a verse that speaks of {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'akhlu bi-sh\u8217'arekha v\u8217'sav\u8217'u}, \u8220"and they shall eat in your gates and be satisfied.\u8221" Since the word {\i sh\u8217'arekha} appears in both contexts, we learn that the prohibition of {\i lo tukhal le\u8217'ekhol bi-sh\u8217'arekha} extends to {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} as well. The produce remains {\i tevel} and is restricted so long as the {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} has not been properly set aside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that this appears in tension with the well-known rules of {\i masechet Demai}, which treats produce of uncertain tithing status leniently in most cases. The suggestion is that the obligations discussed in these {\i baraitot} operate at the biblical level for produce that is certainly {\i tevel}, while the rabbinic dispensations of {\i Demai} apply specifically where there is doubt whether the second and third tithes have been separated \u8212- a {\i chumra d\u8217'rabanan} that does not carry the biblical penalties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Penalty Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then clarifies the interaction between Rabbi Yossi\u8217's derivation and Rabbi Meir\u8217's. If we had heard only from Rabbi Yossi\u8217's {\i pasuk}, we might have thought that the prohibition is only a {\i lav} \u8212- a negative commandment \u8212- but not one that carries the {\i mitah} penalty. The {\i pasuk} of Rabbi Meir \u8212- which creates the {\i hekesh} between {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} and applies the term {\i terumah} to {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- is therefore necessary to establish that the prohibition of {\i tevel} in its various stages carries the severity of {\i mitah} and not merely a {\i lav}. A {\i lishna achrina} \u8212- an alternative manuscript formulation \u8212- reverses the role of the two sources while reaching the same conclusion: {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} acquires its {\i tovel} status from Rabbi Yossi\u8217's derivation, and from Rabbi Meir\u8217's {\i hekesh} we learn that the attendant prohibition reaches the level of {\i mitah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apparent Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the mishnah with a sharp challenge. Having explained that the mishnah follows Rabbi Meir \u8212- who holds that {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} is forbidden to {\i zarim} \u8212- the {\i gemara} invites us to examine the latter clause of the mishnah: a {\i bat Levi} betrothed to a {\i kohen} and a {\i bat kohen} betrothed to a {\i Levi} eat neither {\i terumah} nor {\i ma\u8217'aser}. The problem here is glaring. The explanation that the mishnah follows Rabbi Meir was invoked to explain why a {\i bat Yisrael} betrothed to a {\i Levi} does not eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- because Rabbi Meir forbids {\i zarim} from eating {\i ma\u8217'aser}, and she has the status of a {\i zarah}. But a {\i bat kohen} is herself a kind of {\i Levi\u8217'it}; why should she be forbidden from {\i ma\u8217'aser} when she is connected to a {\i Levi}? And symmetrically, a {\i bat Levi} connected to a {\i kohen} \u8212- why should she not eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} from the {\i Levi} side of her identity? The mishnah forbids both, and the explanation that we are following Rabbi Meir\u8217's view on {\i zarim} does not resolve this difficulty at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet proposes a creative reinterpretation of the mishnah. When the mishnah says she does not eat ({\i eina ochelet}), he argues, it does not mean she is forbidden from eating {\i ma\u8217'aser} at all. Rather, it means she cannot authorize others to separate or collect {\i ma\u8217'aser} on her behalf \u8212- {\i eina notenet reshut litrom}. Normally, a woman\u8217's {\i shaliach} \u8212- her appointed agent \u8212- can go to the {\i goren} and take {\i terumah} or {\i ma\u8217'aser} on her behalf. The mishnah\u8217's ruling, according to Rav Sheshet, is that in these intermediate states of connection to a new household, she cannot send a {\i shaliach} to collect from her prospective husband\u8217's tithes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Married Women\u8217's Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reinterpretation immediately raises a question: does a married woman actually have the right to send a {\i shaliach} to collect {\i terumah} on her behalf? If she does not have this right even when fully married, then Rav Sheshet\u8217's interpretation does not explain what she loses in the betrothed state. The {\i gemara} responds by citing a {\i baraita} that confirms she does indeed have this right when fully married. The verse states {\i va\u8217'achaltem oto bechol makom atem uveitchem} \u8212- \u8220"and you shall eat it in every place, you and your household\u8221" \u8212- and the {\i baraita} derives from {\i uveitchem} (your household) that a married {\i bat Yisrael} is empowered to authorize the taking of {\i terumah} on her husband\u8217's behalf. One might ask whether the verse is teaching that she is permitted to eat {\i terumah} rather than that she may authorize its collection. The {\i baraita} dismisses this reading: if the lenient {\i ma\u8217'aser} may be eaten by a married woman, surely {\i terumah} \u8212- which is more stringent \u8212- need not be taught anew. The verse must therefore be teaching the more novel point: that a married woman can serve as an authorizing agent for the taking of {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar brei de-Ravina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar brei de-Ravina offers an alternative explanation of the mishnah that differs from Rav Sheshet\u8217's. He reads the mishnah\u8217's {\i eina ochelet} to mean that she may not personally go to the {\i granot} \u8212- the threshing floors or storehouses \u8212- and collect {\i ma\u8217'aser} herself. Even if she is substantively entitled to eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}, she cannot personally appear at the place of distribution and take it. Her husband must go on her behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} probes the rationale for this restriction. One view is that it is {\i mishum yichud} \u8212- the concern about a woman being alone with men at the threshing floor is the reason she should not collect independently. Another view is that the concern is {\i mishum gerushah}: if she goes alone to collect, onlookers may not realize that she has been divorced and is no longer entitled to those tithes, leading to improper distribution. The {\i gemara} challenges this second rationale: if the concern is that a divorced {\i bat Levi} might show up and improperly collect, then a divorced {\i bat kohen} should equally be a concern when collecting {\i terumah} for her father. The {\i gemara} responds: this is indeed a {\i gezeirah mishum gerushah bat Yisrael} \u8212- a rabbinic precaution that we impose broadly so that people do not get into the habit of giving tithes to women who arrive at the {\i goren} alone, since sometimes that woman will turn out to be a {\i bat Yisrael} with no entitlement at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this {\i gezeirah} applies broadly, the {\i gemara} continues, why does the mishnah speak specifically of a {\i me\u8217'ureset} \u8212- a betrothed woman? Would not the same concern apply to a married woman? The {\i gemara} answers: since the opening cases of the mishnah \u8212- where the question of actual eating rights was at issue \u8212- employed the language of betrothal, the mishnah uses consistent terminology throughout, even in the closing case, even though the ruling would apply equally to a fully married woman. This resolution, while analytically necessary, does produce an unusual reading of the mishnah, in which {\i eina ochelet} does not straightforwardly mean \u8220"she does not eat\u8221" but rather refers to a more limited practical restriction on personal collection at the {\i goren}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen vs.\u160 ?Levi Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is introduced that parallels the earlier one but attributes the positions to different {\i tanna\u8217'im}: \u8220"{\i terumah} goes to the {\i kohen} and {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} goes to the {\i Levi} \u8212- these are the words of Rabbi Akiva.\u8221" This closely parallels the language previously attributed to Rabbi Meir, and the {\i gemara} will need to sort out the relationship between these two positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah states in this {\i baraita} that {\i ma\u8217'aser} goes to the {\i kohen} \u8212- or rather, as the {\i gemara} clarifies, even to the {\i kohen}. The {\i gemara} immediately challenges a literal reading: does {\i ma\u8217'aser} go to the {\i kohen} to the exclusion of the {\i Levi}? That cannot be. So the {\i gemara} corrects: read it as {\i af l\u8217'kohen} \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} may be given even to a {\i kohen}, not only to a {\i Levi}. This means that in Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view, {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} properly goes to the {\i Levi} and may not go to a {\i kohen}; Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah, by contrast, permits giving {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} even to a {\i kohen}. This is a sharper and more substantive disagreement than what was implied earlier in the {\i sugya}, and it carries significant consequences. It is also worth noting that Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah was himself a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 24 Places Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains that Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's position follows the principle articulated by Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i b\u8217'esrim v\u8217'arba\u8217'ah mekomos nikra\u8217'u kohanim Levi\u8217'im} \u8212- in twenty-four places in the Torah and {\i Nevi\u8217'im}, {\i kohanim} are called {\i Levi\u8217'im}. This is one of those places. The verse governing the distribution of {\i ma\u8217'aser} addresses {\i ha-Levi\u8217'im}, and since {\i kohanim} are themselves called {\i Levi\u8217'im} in Scripture, the {\i kohen} too falls within the category of those to whom {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} may be given. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi himself likely made this observation as a standalone {\i midrashic} point \u8212- that the {\i kohanim} are consistently referred to as {\i Levi\u8217'im} in twenty-four places \u8212- without necessarily knowing he was providing the conceptual basis for Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cemetery Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's counter-argument is based on a different verse. The Torah states {\i achaltem oto bechol makom} \u8212- \u8220"you shall eat it in every place.\u8221" Rabbi Akiva reads this to mean that only someone who can eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} in every place is entitled to receive it. A {\i kohen}, however, cannot eat in a cemetery \u8212- he is forbidden from contracting corpse-impurity in a graveyard. Therefore a {\i kohen} does not qualify as someone who can eat it {\i bechol makom}, and he is excluded from the class of recipients.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Garden Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a historical incident that illuminates this dispute. Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah \u8212- himself a {\i kohen} \u8212- was in the habit of collecting {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} from a certain garden. This was his practice in accordance with his own ruling that a {\i kohen} is entitled to receive {\i ma\u8217'aser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cemetery Blockade\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva responded to this practice. The {\i gemara} initially appears to describe him leading Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah to the entrance of a cemetery, as if to say: you think you can eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- go ahead and enter. But a more careful reading of the {\i gemara}\u8217's language suggests something more pointed: Rabbi Akiva arranged that the only entrance to the storage area of that garden ran through a cemetery, effectively blocking all {\i kohanim} from accessing it. When the {\i gemara} asks why Rabbi Akiva would do this if even according to one opinion the {\i ma\u8217'aser} was to be redistributed to {\i kohanim}, the answer is that Rabbi Akiva was sending a message to Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah: if you are taking this as a legal entitlement ({\i torat chalukah}), that is unacceptable; if you understand it only as an emergency measure flowing from the historical {\i knas} on the {\i Levi\u8217'im}, that is another matter. Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah reportedly responded: \u8220"Akiva with his shepherd\u8217's sack \u8212- and I shall live.\u8221" The wry remark reflects that Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah was wealthy, and he was speaking not for himself but for {\i kohanim} in general who have no fields of their own: where are we {\i kohanim} supposed to get our food?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Levites\u8217' Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Punishment Q.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks a foundational question: {\i mifnei mah kinsu Levi\u8217'im b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser?} \u8212- Why were the {\i Levi\u8217'im} punished by having {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} taken away from them? The historical premise is that at some point, the {\i Levi\u8217'im} lost their statutory right to {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}, and the {\i gemara} seeks to understand why this occurred and what became of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a dispute between Rabbi Yonatan and the {\i sav\u8217'ya} \u8212- the elders. One view holds that the {\i Levi\u8217'im} were punished because they did not make {\i aliyah} in the time of Ezra, when the Jewish people returned to the Land of Israel from Babylon. The other view holds that the redistribution of {\i ma\u8217'aser} was instituted so that {\i kohanim} would have something to rely upon during the periods when they were {\i teme\u8217'im} and therefore unable to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensus Reached\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the second explanation. If the only reason the {\i Levi\u8217'im} lost their {\i ma\u8217'aser} was in order to feed {\i kohanim} when they were {\i teme\u8217'im}, why should the {\i Levi\u8217'im} bear this burden? The {\i gemara} concludes that in fact {\i kulei alma} \u8212- all opinions agree \u8212- that the {\i knas} was imposed because the {\i Levi\u8217'im} did not make {\i aliyah} in the time of Ezra. That is the core punishment; the {\i Levi\u8217'im} chose not to return to the Land, and so they forfeited the privilege that was theirs within the Land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secondary Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the punishment was for failing to make {\i aliyah}, the {\i gemara} identifies the actual point of dispute more precisely: the two opinions disagree about where the confiscated {\i ma\u8217'aser} was redirected. One opinion holds that it was distributed to the {\i ani\u8217'im} \u8212- the poor. If the {\i Levi\u8217'im} forfeited their {\i ma\u8217'aser}, it should simply go to those most in need. The other opinion holds that it was redirected to {\i kohanim} \u8212- specifically because {\i kohanim bimei tum\u8217'atan ani\u8217'im ninhu}: when {\i kohanim} become {\i teme\u8217'im} and lose access to {\i terumah}, they suddenly find themselves with nothing to eat, since unlike ordinary {\i Yisrael\u8217'im} they have no fields of their own. In their state of impurity they are effectively destitute, and the former {\i ma\u8217'aser} of the {\i Levi\u8217'im} was redirected to address their need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This secondary dispute also illuminates Rabbi Akiva\u8217's behavior. According to the opinion that the {\i ma\u8217'aser} was redirected to the {\i ani\u8217'im}, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's blocking of Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's collection is entirely comprehensible \u8212- the {\i kohen} has no legitimate claim to that {\i ma\u8217'aser} at all. But according to the opinion that it was redirected to {\i kohanim}, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's message was more nuanced: you may collect it, but understand that you do so only as a consequence of the {\i knas} against the {\i Levi\u8217'im}, not as an expression of your inherent legal entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: how do we know that the {\i Levi\u8217'im} in fact failed to make {\i aliyah} in Ezra\u8217's time? The answer comes from a {\i pasuk meforash} in the book of Ezra: {\i va\u8217'akbetzem el ha-nahar ha-ba el Ahava va-nachaneh sham yamim sheloshah va-avinan ba-am u-va-kohanim u-mibnei Levi lo matzati sham} \u8212- \u8220"And I gathered them at the river that flows to Ahava, and we camped there for three days, and I looked among the people and the {\i kohanim}, and I found none of the sons of Levi there.\u8221" The verse is explicit: when Ezra assembled the returnees, no {\i Levi\u8217'im} were to be found among them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda\u8217's History\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda adds a historical observation that flows from this situation: {\i batchilah lo hayu ma\u8217'amidim shotrim ela min ha-Levi\u8217'im} \u8212- originally, communal officials ({\i shotrim}) were drawn exclusively from the {\i Levi\u8217'im}, as indicated by the verse {\i ve-shotrim ha-Levi\u8217'im lifneichem}. But now, following the {\i Levi\u8217'im}\u8217's failure to return in Ezra\u8217's time, officials are drawn from {\i Yisrael} instead. Rav Chisda cites a verse for this latter point, though the {\i Masoret ha-Shas} notes that the verse he cites does not appear verbatim in {\i Tanach}. The commentators suggest he may be loosely paraphrasing the verse in Devarim 1:13 \u8212- {\i havu lachem anashim chachamim u-nevonim vi-yedu\u8217'im le-shivteichem va-ashimem be-rasheichem} \u8212- though the correspondence is imprecise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Status Shifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Yisrael Path\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second mishnah of the {\i daf} sets out the full range of possible status shifts for a woman over the course of multiple marriages. A {\i bat Yisrael} who marries a {\i kohen} eats {\i terumah}. If her husband dies and she has a son from him, she continues to eat {\i terumah} by virtue of that son. If she then marries a {\i Levi}, she eats {\i ma\u8217'aser}. If he too dies and she has a son from him, she can eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} by virtue of that son \u8212- but she has lost her access to {\i terumah}, since the {\i levi} son does not restore that privilege. If she then marries a {\i Yisrael}, she eats neither {\i terumah} nor {\i ma\u8217'aser}. If he dies and she has a son from him, she remains excluded from both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons\u8217' Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the status conferred by a son persists even after the relevant marriage ends. If the son from the {\i Yisrael} dies, she regains her status from the {\i Levi} son \u8212- she may again eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}. If the son from the {\i Levi} dies, she regains her status from the {\i kohen} son \u8212- she may eat {\i terumah}. The sons function as status-anchors that outlast the marriages which produced them, and their deaths can restore her to progressively earlier entitlements. It is worth noting that this applies to a son of any age \u8212- even a minor child functions to determine the mother\u8217's eligibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Kohen Path\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah then traces the corresponding path for a {\i bat kohen}. A {\i bat kohen} who marries a {\i Yisrael} does not eat {\i terumah}. If he dies with no son from him, she still does not eat {\i terumah}. If she then marries a {\i Levi}, she eats {\i ma\u8217'aser}. If he dies with no son from him, she continues to eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}. If she then marries a {\i kohen}, she eats {\i terumah}. The mishnah implies that her final marital connection carries priority: if her last husband was a {\i kohen}, and she has a son from that {\i kohen} as well as sons from earlier marriages, she eats {\i terumah} \u8212- the highest status \u8212- even though she also has a {\i ben Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversion to Origins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As sons die, the woman\u8217's status reverts. If her {\i kohen} son dies, she can no longer eat {\i terumah}. If her {\i Levi} son dies, she can no longer eat {\i ma\u8217'aser}. If her {\i Yisrael} son dies, {\i chozeret le-veit aviha} \u8212- she returns to her father\u8217's house. {\i Beit aviha} here is understood as her tribal identity, not necessarily the physical home of her biological father; the term {\i beit av} refers broadly to her collective family and original tribal affiliation. Once all her sons from all her marriages have died, she reverts entirely to her original status as a {\i bat kohen}, and may again partake of the privileges of that station \u8212- eating as though she had never left her father\u8217's house. As was observed at the close of the shiur, this entire complex of shifting entitlements is the reason why the daughters of {\i kohanim} ideally marry {\i kohanim} \u8212- so that they never face these complications at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 88\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Single Witness Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The perek of {\i Ha-Ishah Rabbah} is one of the most perplexing and challenging {\i perakim} in all of Shas \u8212- not only analytically, but in terms of {\i rabbinic} justice. The central difficulty is understanding how a woman who appears to have done everything right ends up in such severe legal jeopardy: she must leave both husbands, and she loses her rights from each of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Talmud} begins by identifying the precise situation described in the {\i Mishnah}. A single witness came and told the woman that her husband was dead. She went to the {\i beit din}, received permission to remarry, and did so. Because the remarriage was predicated on the testimony of a single witness, all of these harsh consequences follow. Had two witnesses come and told her that her husband was dead, the picture would be entirely different \u8212- she could return to her first husband, the second marriage would be treated as a nullity, and so on. What the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's structure implies, then, is that a single witness is in fact {\i believed} in such a case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Chatas Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the {\i Gemara} to a further implication. It is established that if one witness comes and says {\i achalta chelev} \u8212- \u8220"you ate forbidden fat\u8221" \u8212- and the accused responds {\i lo achalti chelev}, he is {\i patur} from bringing a {\i korban}. But if he remains silent, he would be {\i chayav}. This means that on the basis of a single witness, one is obligated to bring a {\i korban chatas} \u8212- not merely an {\i asham talui}, the offering brought in cases of genuine doubt, but the {\i chatas} itself, which implies that the sin is treated as established. This in turn implies that the single witness has been believed {\i m\u8217'doraisa} \u8212- a striking conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i minalan} \u8212- from where do we derive that a single witness can be believed at the Torah level? The source is a {\i baraita} interpreting the verse {\i o hoda eilav chataso}: his sin must become known {\i to him}, and not through others informing him. The {\i baraita} raises the possibility that even if he fails to contradict someone who accuses him, he might still be {\i patur} \u8212- since another person informed him, perhaps that does not satisfy the requirement of {\i o hoda eilav}. The verse is therefore read broadly: {\i mikol makom}, from any situation, he can come to know of his sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ne\u8217'emanut vs.\u160 ?Shtika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is this case? If two witnesses came and the accused failed to contradict them, why would we need a verse at all? Two witnesses establish a fact; the {\i korban} obligation would be obvious. It must therefore be that the {\i baraita} is discussing a single witness \u8212- {\i chad} \u8212- and the inference is that if one fails to contradict a single witness, one is {\i chayav}. This would mean that the single witness is actually believed, {\i shema mina eid echad ne\u8217'eman}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately pushes back: {\i umimai demishum de\u8217'meiman}? Perhaps this has nothing to do with the reliability of the witness at all. Perhaps the reason for the {\i chiyuv} is {\i mishum de\u8217'kashtik} \u8212- because the accused was silent. And {\i shtika ke\u8217'hoda\u8217'ah damya} \u8212- silence functions as a form of admission. This is confirmed by the {\i seifa} of the {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Witnesses Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The end of the {\i baraita} states: if two witnesses say {\i achalta chelev} and the accused says {\i lo achalti}, he is {\i patur} \u8212- even against two witnesses. {\i Rabbi Meir} disagrees and holds him {\i chayav}. This is a striking passage. Our initial assumption was that two witnesses obviously should be believed \u8212- {\i lama li kra}, why would we need a verse to establish what is self-evident? But the {\i seifa} reveals that the {\i Rabbanan} do not convict even on the basis of two witnesses if the accused denies it. This suggests that the {\i Rabbanan} are not operating in the realm of {\i ne\u8217'emanut} \u8212- of witness reliability \u8212- at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtika K\u8217'Hoda\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} argues by way of a {\i kal vachomer}: if two witnesses can bring a person to the death penalty, surely they should be sufficient to obligate a {\i korban}, which is far less severe. The {\i Rabbanan} respond: the accused is not without recourse even against two witnesses, because he could always say {\i mezid hayiti} \u8212- I acted intentionally \u8212- in which case no {\i korban} would be due. This is a form of {\i migo}: since he has available to him a more extreme claim that would exempt him, we credit his denial as well. The deeper point is that a {\i korban chatas} depends not on whether a person sinned, but on whether they {\i know} they sinned. No external testimony can substitute for a person\u8217's own internal certainty about what occurred and what their intentions were.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the opening of the {\i baraita}, the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i reisha mai ta\u8217'ama kemachayvei Rabbanan} \u8212- why do the {\i Rabbanan} hold that silence in the face of a single witness creates {\i chiyuv}? If it were because the single witness is {\i ne\u8217'eman}, then we would be back to a contradiction \u8212- here are two witnesses who are {\i ne\u8217'eman} in all circumstances, yet the {\i Rabbanan} exempt the accused even against their testimony. The answer must be that the {\i Rabbanan} are not working within the framework of {\i ne\u8217'emanut} at all. Rather, {\i ela lav mishum de\u8217'ishtik} \u8212- it is entirely because the accused was silent. {\i Shtika ke\u8217'hoda\u8217'ah damya}: silence is treated as an admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Internal Knowledge Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore proposes to abandon the verse entirely and seek a {\i sevara} \u8212- a logical basis. It cites the known case of {\i safek chelev safek shuman}: a piece of meat that might be {\i chelev} or might be permitted fat, where a single witness comes and declares {\i bari li d\u8217'shuman hu} \u8212- I am certain it is permitted \u8212- and is believed. A brief aside is warranted here: it is striking that the {\i Talmud} reaches for the analogy of the piece of meat to describe the legal status of a wife. Feminist scholars of the {\i Talmud} have noted this kind of comparison, as the {\i din} of the woman in this case is treated as formally identical to the {\i din} of a cut of meat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chelev Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Can this analogy actually sustain the case of the woman? The {\i Gemara} objects: {\i hatam lo itchazek issura} \u8212- in the meat case, the prohibition was never established; we simply did not know what category the piece fell into. {\i Hacha itchazek issura d\u8217'eshet ish} \u8212- here we know for certain that this woman was married. Furthermore, {\i ein davar she-be\u8217'erva pachot mishnayim} \u8212- in cases that touch on sexual prohibitions, we require no fewer than two witnesses. These two objections are related: once we say {\i itchazek issura d\u8217'eshet ish}, we are in the domain of {\i arayot}, and therefore we cannot rely on a single witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The objection can be sharpened further. Our case does not resemble {\i safek chelev safek shuman} at all. It more closely resembles a case of {\i vadai chelev} \u8212- a piece of fat that is definitely forbidden \u8212- where a single witness comes and says it is actually permitted. In that case, {\i d\u8217'lo meheiman} \u8212- the single witness is not believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: is it not the case that even where {\i itchazek issura} applies, a single witness would be ineffective? Yes \u8212- and this is precisely the point. Even the case of {\i safek chelev} allows a single witness only because there is no established prohibition. The moment something is {\i vadai chelev} or {\i itchazek issura}, no number of witnesses can overturn the established fact through testimony alone. The only exception would be personal knowledge \u8212- if you yourself saw a piece of meat cut from the animal\u8217's kidney, no hundred witnesses can tell you otherwise, because it is your direct knowledge, not testimony at all, that establishes the {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a further observation from the {\i Gemara}: {\i hatam afilu bei mea lo meheimni} \u8212- in the case of {\i vadai chelev}, even a hundred witnesses cannot permit it. But {\i hacha keivan d\u8217'ki atu bei trei meheimni} \u8212- in the case of the woman, if two witnesses came and said her husband is dead, we would believe them. Therefore, {\i chad nami meheiman} \u8212- perhaps we should believe a single witness as well, just as we do in the analogous cases of {\i tevel}, {\i hekdesh}, and {\i konamot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevel, Hekdesh, Konamot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now examines each of these three parallel cases in detail to see if any of them can actually serve as the source or model for the woman\u8217's case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding {\i tevel} \u8212- grain from which tithes have not yet been separated \u8212- suppose we have a basket that may not have been tithed, and a single witness comes and says it has been properly separated. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i ei dideih} \u8212- if the witness is the owner himself, then we understand why he is believed: {\i mishum d\u8217'beyado l\u8217'takno} \u8212- it is within his power to fix it at any moment, and since he could always tithe it, we treat his assertion that he already did so as credible. But the case that actually resembles ours must involve a witness speaking about {\i someone else\u8217's} property. In that case, the analysis depends on a {\i machloket}: can one tithe on behalf of another person using one\u8217's own grain, without the owner\u8217's consent? If so, then {\i mishum d\u8217'beyado l\u8217'takno} \u8212- this witness himself could effect the remedy at any moment, and so he has a degree of standing analogous to ownership. But if the owner\u8217's knowledge is required, then the witness is simply making an assertion about a fact he did not himself create \u8212- and we are back to the question of where we get the authority to believe him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same difficulty recurs with {\i hekdesh}. If something has been consecrated to the Temple and a witness says it has been redeemed, we can explain the case of {\i kedushat damim} \u8212- monetary consecration \u8212- by saying {\i mishum deyadei liftoto}: the owner can always effect a redemption. And even for {\i kedushat haguf} \u8212- where the animal itself has been consecrated for a {\i korban} \u8212- if it is the owner\u8217's own animal, {\i mishum deyadei leishalei alei}, he can always go to a {\i talmid chacham} and have the {\i hekdesh} annulled. But once again, the case that resembles ours is where a third party comes and says the owner went to a {\i talmid chacham} and cleared the animal of its consecrated status. And {\i hi gufa minlan} \u8212- from where do we derive that such a witness is believed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same pattern plays out with {\i konamot}. A {\i konam} can operate in two ways: it can render something forbidden to oneself, or it can render one\u8217's own property forbidden to others. In either case, if it is one\u8217's own {\i konam}, the owner can seek annulment at any time, which explains why a single witness who happens to be the owner is credited. But the moment we move to a witness testifying about someone else\u8217's {\i konam} \u8212- that the owner has already sought annulment \u8212- we once again reach {\i hi gufa minlan}. Each time the analysis is brought to the point that actually resembles our case, the underlying logic collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is notable that all of this reasoning has been {\i stama d\u8217'Talmuda} \u8212- anonymous throughout. Every one of these exchanges represents the back-and-forth of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's own anonymous layer of analysis, and no solution has been found.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, for the first time, a named {\i amora} steps forward with a direct answer. {\i Amar Rabbi Zeira: mitoch chomer she\u8217'hichmarta aleha besofah hekaltah aleha betechilah.} Because the consequences at the end are so severe \u8212- because she must leave both husbands, forfeit her {\i ketubah}, and face all the attendant penalties \u8212- the rabbis did not need to make life so difficult for her at the beginning. Knowing full well that she stands to lose everything if she acts precipitously, she can be trusted to wait until she is genuinely certain that her husband is dead before proceeding to remarry. Therefore the rabbis permitted reliance on a single witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds tartly: {\i lo tachmir velo takal} \u8212- do not make it harder at the end and thereby justify making it easier at the beginning. Leave well enough alone; the cost is too high.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the final answer is: {\i mishum aguna ikilu bah rabanan}. Ultimately, the rabbis were lenient with her out of concern for the {\i agunah}. Without this leniency, a woman whose husband disappears and is presumed dead would have no legal recourse and would remain chained for the rest of her life. It is important to note that this type of {\i igun} \u8212- where a husband has actually died or disappeared \u8212- is categorically different from the {\i agunah} crisis of the modern era, which involves husbands who are known to be alive yet refuse to deliver a {\i get}. The halakhic solutions that have been developed for the former have far less application to the latter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Returns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} continues: if the husband returns after she has remarried, {\i tetze mizeh umizeh} \u8212- she must leave both the second husband and the first. At this point, {\i Rav} makes a striking statement: {\i lo shanu ela she\u8217'niset be\u8217'ed echad, aval niset al pi shenei edim lo tetze.} Rav holds that the rule of {\i tzet mizeh umizeh} applies only when she remarried on the basis of a single witness. But if two witnesses came and testified that her husband was dead, and she married on that basis \u8212- she does not have to leave her second husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In {\i Eretz Yisrael}, they laughed at this: {\i machku aleha bema\u8217'arava.} The man is standing right in front of you, and you are telling her she may remain with the second husband?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} formulates the objection directly: {\i ata gavra veka\u8217'i} \u8212- the man has come and is standing before you. {\i Ve\u8217'at amart lo tetze?} And you are saying she does not need to leave?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i lo tzericha} \u8212- Rav\u8217's ruling is still necessary. The case is one of {\i d\u8217'la yad\u8217'ina leh} \u8212- we do not recognize this person. Someone is claiming to be the long-lost husband, but there is no positive identification. The {\i Gemara} presses: if we do not recognize him at all, why would she even need to leave her second husband in the case where she married on the basis of a single witness? It answers: the ruling is needed for the case where two witnesses come and say, {\i anan havainan bahadei mikai nafak v\u8217'ad hashta} \u8212- \u8220"We have been with him continuously from the time he left until this very moment; it is you who fail to recognize him.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} supports this with the verse describing Yosef\u8217's reunion with his brothers: {\i vayaker Yosef et echav ve-hem lo hikiruhu} \u8212- Yosef recognized his brothers but they did not recognize him. {\i Rav Hisda} explains: he left before the seal of a beard had formed on his face, and he returned with it. The broader point is clear: it is entirely plausible that a person could return after a long absence and not be recognized, whether because of physical changes, the passage of time, or the disappearance of those who originally knew him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conflicting Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a further difficulty. Even granting all of this \u8212- even accepting that the two witnesses who accompany the returning husband are credible \u8212- we now have {\i trei v\u8217'trei ninhu}: two witnesses who said the husband was dead against two witnesses who say he is alive and present. This creates a {\i safek}, and anyone who now sleeps with this woman would be obligated in an {\i asham talui} \u8212- the conditional guilt offering for uncertain sin. How then can Rav say {\i lo tetze}, that she remains with the second husband, when every act of marital relations generates a {\i safek} requiring an {\i asham talui}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} offers a solution: {\i kegon she-nisset l\u8217'achad me-adeha} \u8212- the case is one where the woman married one of the original witnesses who testified to her husband\u8217's death. This is relevant because an {\i asham talui} is not an offering that comes from external legal pressure; it reflects a person\u8217's own internal doubt about whether they have sinned. If she married the very witness who established the fact of the husband\u8217's death, that witness himself has no doubt whatsoever \u8212- he was there. He does not feel any uncertainty, and therefore an {\i asham talui} simply does not apply to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pushes further: even if the man does not have a doubt, {\i hi gufa be\u8217'asham talui} \u8212- what about her? Does she not still feel uncertain? The answer is: {\i be\u8217'omeret bari li} \u8212- she herself says, \u8220"I am certain that he is dead.\u8221" Rashi explains that the witnesses produced convincing signs \u8212- an item that only the husband would have had, for example \u8212- that left her with no personal doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temporal Sequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises the question of {\i novelty}: if the case requires both that she married one of her witnesses and that she herself is certain the husband is dead, what is the {\i chiddush} of Rav\u8217's ruling? Even {\i Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi} \u8212- who takes the strictest position \u8212- confines his ruling to a specific scenario. {\i Afilu Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi lo ka\u8217'amar ela sheba\u8217'u eidim ve\u8217'achar kach niset} \u8212- even he only rules strictly when both sets of witnesses appeared {\i before} she married. But if she married first and only then did contradicting witnesses emerge, {\i Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi} would also not require her to leave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita} to clarify this. Two witnesses say the husband is dead; two witnesses say he is not. Two witnesses say she was divorced; two witnesses say she was not. {\i Harei zo lo tinasei} \u8212- she should not marry, since she is a {\i safek}. {\i Ve\u8217'im niset, lo tetze} \u8212- but if she did marry, she need not leave, say the {\i Rabbanan}. {\i Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi omer tetzei} \u8212- Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi says she must leave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi} clarifies his own position: {\i eimatai ani omer tetzei? Bizman sheba\u8217'u eidim ve\u8217'achar kach niset.} He requires her to leave only when both sets of contradicting witnesses appeared {\i before} she married. If she knew going in that the situation was a {\i safek}, and she married anyway, she cannot now claim the protection of {\i lo tetze}. But if she married first and only afterward did contradicting witnesses arrive, even he would agree she need not leave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's ruling is therefore not merely a restatement of what all {\i Tannaim} agree on. There are two ways to read Rav. According to the first reading ({\i yesh omrim}), Rav\u8217's case is one where she married {\i after} the contradicting witnesses appeared, and he rules {\i lo tetze} \u8212- unlike {\i Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi}. On this reading, Rav is announcing that the {\i halacha} does not follow {\i Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi}. According to the second reading, Rav\u8217's ruling applies to the case where she married {\i before} the contradicting witnesses appeared, meaning Rav actually holds {\i like} Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi \u8212- his {\i chiddush} being simply that the {\i halacha} follows that position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Case Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} raises an objection to Rav from a different {\i baraita}. The case is a {\i kohen} who has married a woman forbidden to him \u8212- a {\i gerushah}, for example. The {\i baraita} asks: from where do we know that if he refuses to divorce her, we compel him? The verse says {\i ve-kidashto} \u8212- \u8220"you shall sanctify him\u8221": he is made holy whether he wishes it or not. Now, {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what is the scenario of this {\i baraita}? If it is the simple case without the complications of contradicting witnesses and personal certainty, the {\i kohen}\u8217's obligation to divorce is obvious; no {\i baraita} is needed. It must therefore be referring to the more complex case \u8212- one where she did marry one of her witnesses and she herself declared certainty about her husband\u8217's death. And yet the {\i baraita} says we compel him. This seems to be a direct challenge to Rav, who said {\i lo tetze}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers several possible resolutions. First: {\i issur kehunah shani} \u8212- the {\i kohen} case is categorically different. A {\i kohen} faces additional prohibitions that do not apply to a regular {\i Yisrael}, and therefore even a doubt that would not require dissolution of an ordinary marriage is sufficient to require dissolution of a {\i kohen}\u8217's marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: {\i mai dafnu? Dafnu be-eidim.} What does it mean to \u8220"compel\u8221" him in the {\i baraita}? Rashi explains that this refers not to forcing him to give a {\i get} after the fact, but rather to preventing the marriage in the first place \u8212- by producing witnesses who will complicate the situation and demonstrate that she may in fact still be married, thereby deterring him from marrying her at all. But once the marriage has occurred, we would not necessarily dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third: {\i kasheba\u8217'u eidim ve\u8217'achar kach niset ve-Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi hi} \u8212- perhaps this {\i baraita} represents the position of {\i Rabbi Menachem Berabbi Yosi} specifically, in the scenario where the contradicting witnesses appeared before she married. Rav may simply hold differently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi & Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Reinterpretat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} now offers a fundamentally different reading of Rav\u8217's original ruling. {\i Mai lo tetze deka\u8217'amar Rav? Lo tetze meheterah harishon.} When Rav said {\i lo tetze}, he did not mean she stays with the second husband. He meant she does not lose her permitted status with respect to the {\i first} husband. In other words, when two witnesses testified that her husband was dead and she married on that basis, and the husband subsequently returns \u8212- she is not {\i asurah} to the first husband. She may go back to him. She is {\i asurah} to the second husband, whose marriage has been nullified, but her original marriage is very much intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: but Rav already said this once \u8212- why does he need to say it a second time? {\i Detnan: niset shelo birshut muteret lachzor lo} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} states that if she married {\i shelo bi-reshut} (understood here to mean on the basis of two witnesses, without needing the permission of {\i beit din}), she is permitted to return to the first husband. And {\i Rav Huna} reported that Rav ruled that the {\i halacha} follows this {\i Mishnah}. So Rav has already told us she can return to the first husband. Why restate it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is: {\i chada miklala de-chavreita itmar} \u8212- one ruling was derived from the other. Once we know the {\i halacha} follows the end of the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- that she can return to her first husband \u8212- it follows as a corollary that she does not forfeit her original {\i heter}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel} now enters with his own formulation: {\i lo shanu ela shelo machchishto, aval machchishto lo tetze.} What we have been discussing applies only when the woman does {\i not} contradict the returning man. If the husband returns and she contradicts him \u8212- she says, \u8220"You are not my husband; I do not know you\u8221" \u8212- then she does not have to leave her second husband and may remain with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i bemai askinan} \u8212- what is the scenario? If two witnesses come and say the husband is alive, then her denial is irrelevant; two witnesses cannot be contradicted by one person\u8217's claim. It must therefore be that a {\i single} witness comes and says the husband is alive. So the {\i Gemara} concludes: {\i ta\u8217'ama demachishatei} \u8212- the reason she stays with the second husband is that she contradicted the single witness. {\i Ha shetaka teitzei} \u8212- but if she had been silent, she would have had to leave, even on the basis of this single witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts the {\i Gemara} to invoke {\i Ulla}\u8217's principle: {\i kol makom she-he\u8217'emina Torah ed echad harei kan shnayim, ve-ein devarav shel echad bimkom shnayim.} Anywhere that the Torah has granted the reliability of a single witness, the Torah has not simply lowered the bar \u8212- it has elevated that witness to the status of two. So when the original single witness said the husband was dead and the woman was permitted to remarry on that basis, that witness now carries the weight of two. A single witness who subsequently says the husband is alive cannot override that, because one witness cannot stand against two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: the {\i Gemara} earlier concluded that it was the {\i rabbis} who permitted reliance on a single witness, out of concern for the {\i agunah} \u8212- not the Torah itself. How then can {\i Ulla} invoke the language of {\i he\u8217'emina Torah}? The force of the objection is real, and the {\i Gemara} does not resolve it fully here. What seems to be operating is the understanding that even the rabbinic permission was grounded in a Torah-level framework, and that the leniency \u8212- once granted \u8212- confers the full status that the Torah accords to a single witness in contexts where it is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disqualified Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: what exactly is the case where someone comes after the fact and says the husband is alive? If it is a {\i kosher} single witness against the original {\i kosher} single witness who now has the status of two \u8212- the later witness can obviously not override. So the {\i Gemara} answers: {\i bipsulei edut} \u8212- the case is where two disqualified witnesses, such as two women or two relatives, come and say the husband is alive. {\i Ucherabbi Nechemiah} \u8212- and this is in accordance with the view of {\i Rabbi Nechemia}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Nechemia\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Nechemia} holds: {\i kol makom she-he\u8217'emina Torah ed echad halach achar rov de\u8217'ot} \u8212- in all cases where the Torah accepts a single witness, one follows the majority of opinions. Since the original single witness has the status of two, and the two disqualified witnesses also count as two opinions, one follows the larger number. {\i Ve-asu shtei nashim ke-ish echad} \u8212- and by this principle, two women count like one man in terms of testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this rule applies symmetrically only when the original witness was also a woman. {\i Aval shtei nashim be-ish echad \u8212- ke-palga u-palga dami} \u8212- if two women testify against a single male witness (who has the Torah-granted status of two), the result is half against half \u8212- a standoff. On the alternative reading proposed by the {\i Gemara}, the case is that the original witness was a woman from the outset, and the principle of {\i Rabbi Nechemia} is applied accordingly: two women testifying against one woman is treated like two men against one man.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 89\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Returns Alive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Single Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens on 88b at the bottom, still discussing the case of a woman whose husband departed to a distant land, whereafter a single witness testified that he had died. Acting on this testimony \u8212- and with the permission of {\i beit din} \u8212- she remarried, only for her first husband to return alive. The ruling is that she must leave both husbands. The {\i gemara} now takes up the question of {\i get}: the {\i mishnah} states {\i tzericha get mizeh umizeh} \u8212- she requires a {\i get} from each husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} begins with a {\i bishlama}: it is straightforward that she needs a {\i get} from the first husband, since she was legally married to him and, if she ever wishes to remarry, she must be formally divorced. But why does she require a {\i get} from the second husband? Her union with him was {\i zenut b\u8217'alma} \u8212- mere adultery. She was still a married woman when she entered that relationship; no marriage was ever contracted. Why should a {\i get} be necessary?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna answers: it is a {\i gezeira}. The Rabbis were concerned that observers would say: the first husband divorced her, she then married the second, and now she is leaving the second \u8212- all without a {\i get}. This perception would create the impression that a married woman can exit a marriage without a {\i get}, which is intolerable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Only Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a challenge from the continuation of the {\i mishnah}: if she was told her husband died and she accepted only {\i kiddushin} \u8212- betrothal \u8212- but did not proceed to {\i nisuin}, she is permitted to return to her first husband. The {\i gemara} asks: should we not also be concerned in that case that people will say the first husband divorced her, she became betrothed to a second man, and now she is leaving him without a {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Perception Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If we apply the same logic, we would say that even in the {\i kiddushin}-only case she should need a {\i get} from the second man \u8212- {\i le\u8217'olam ba\u8217'ei get}, she always requires a {\i get}. But this leads to a further difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechzir Gerushato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she must obtain a {\i get} from the second man (her {\i mekudeshet}, her betrothed), then her first husband \u8212- having \u8220"divorced\u8221" her in the eyes of observers who believe she received a {\i get} \u8212- would be re-taking a woman who had been \u8220"divorced\u8221" and then betrothed to another. This implicates the prohibition of {\i mechzir gerushato}, the prohibition against a man remarrying his divorced wife after she has been married to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose ben Kipur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue this is only a problem according to Rabbi Yose ben Kipur, who holds that {\i mechzir gerushato} applies even from {\i eirusin} alone \u8212- {\i min hanisuin asurah, min ha\u8217'eirusin muteret}, that only after full {\i nisuin} is she forbidden to return, but from {\i eirusin} she may return. Since Rabbi Yose ben Kipur\u8217's view is a {\i da\u8217'at yachid} not accepted as {\i halacha}, one could argue that public confusion about his position is itself undesirable, which would make the entire scenario problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Doesn\u8217't Matter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now adduces evidence from the {\i mishnah} itself: {\i af al pi she-natan ha-acharon get, lo pasla min hakehunah} \u8212- even if the second husband gave her a {\i get}, she is not disqualified from {\i kehunah}. This tells us something important: a {\i get} from the second husband does not carry the halachic force of a real {\i get}. For if it were a genuine {\i get}, it would render her a {\i gerushah} and disqualify her from marrying a {\i kohen}. The fact that it does not disqualify her proves the {\i get} in this case is essentially meaningless \u8212- it is given only for the purpose of appearances, not as a real act of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushei Ta\u8217'ut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is {\i amrei kiddushei ta\u8217'ut havu}: in the {\i kiddushin}-only case, people will naturally understand that the betrothal was a mistake \u8212- she accepted {\i kiddushin} without knowing her first husband was still alive. Therefore, when she exits the situation without a {\i get}, no one concludes that a married woman can leave without a {\i get}; everyone understands it as a nullification of a betrothal entered under false pretenses. This is what people will think.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kensu Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so \u8212- that in the {\i kiddushin} case people will say {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'ut havu} \u8212- why don\u8217't we say the same in the first case, where she actually married and lived with the second man? There too, was it not a marriage entered in error?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is {\i kensu rabanan} \u8212- the Rabbis penalized her. In the first case, an actual act of prohibition took place: she cohabited with a man while still legally married. The Rabbis therefore imposed a penalty. In the second case, {\i delo avda issura} \u8212- she did not actually commit any prohibited act; she merely accepted some tokens of betrothal without knowing the full picture. Where no genuine transgression occurred, {\i lo kensu rabanan} \u8212- the Rabbis did not impose the penalty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states {\i ein lah ketubah} \u8212- she receives no {\i ketubah} from either husband. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama tiknu lah rabanan ketubah?} What was the entire purpose of the rabbinic enactment of the {\i ketubah}? {\i Kedei shelo tehei kalah be\u8217'einav lehotzi\u8217'ah} \u8212- so that divorcing her would not be a trivial matter in the husband\u8217's eyes; the financial burden of the {\i ketubah} payment was meant to deter hasty divorce. In the present case, however, we actively want the husband to divorce her. Creating any obstacle to that divorce by requiring a {\i ketubah} payment would be counterproductive. Therefore, the Rabbis do not require it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises here: is not at least some component of the {\i ketubah de\u8217'oraita}? The answer is that the {\i ketubah} \u8212- the obligation to pay a sum upon divorce \u8212- is entirely {\i takanat derabbanan}. What is {\i de\u8217'oraita} is the clause of {\i she\u8217'er, kesus, v\u8217'onah}: the husband\u8217's obligations to provide food, clothing, and conjugal rights. The monetary payment upon divorce is purely a rabbinic institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a more refined question: if the {\i ketubah} is a contractual document that has already been entered into, does one not need to honor the terms of a contract? Two approaches suggest themselves. One could argue that the {\i ketubah} is not a fully voluntary contract \u8212- it is a mandated condition of marriage \u8212- and therefore the assumption underlying the contract is that the rabbinic mandate for it remains in force. Once the reason for the {\i ketubah} is void \u8212- as in this case, where divorce is required \u8212- the {\i ketubah} itself is also void. Alternatively, and relatedly, the principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker} \u8212- which the {\i gemara} will develop at length later \u8212- gives {\i beit din} the authority to dissolve obligations of this kind. The final clause of the {\i ketubah} document supports this reading: {\i v\u8217'kibel alav chasan den kichomer kol shtarei ketubot v\u8217'toseftos de-nahagin bivnot Yisrael hatzni\u8217'ot ketikkun chachameinu zichronam livrachah} \u8212- the groom accepts the terms \u8220"according to the enactment of our Sages of blessed memory,\u8221" which means the contract operates within the rabbinic framework that established it, and that same framework can dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Support Benefits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the {\i ketubah} itself, the {\i mishnah} states {\i ein lah peiros, v\u8217'lo mezonot, v\u8217'lo blaot} \u8212- she is entitled to neither {\i peiros} nor {\i mezonot} nor {\i blaot}. To explain briefly: {\i nichsei milog} are properties a wife brings into the marriage that she retakes upon divorce, while the husband enjoys the profits \u8212- the {\i peiros} \u8212- during the marriage. In cases where the marriage is found to have been invalid (such as an {\i almanah l\u8217'kohen gadol}), the husband must retroactively return those profits. In our case, she does not even receive that restitution. {\i Mezonot} \u8212- her food support \u8212- likewise does not apply, nor do the {\i blaot} \u8212- the worn garments that ordinarily belong to the wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i tenai ketubah ke-ketubah dami} \u8212- the conditions and appurtenances of the {\i ketubah} share the same status as the {\i ketubah} itself. Since she forfeits the {\i ketubah}, she forfeits everything that comes along with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Competing Values\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling raises a genuine moral tension. The Rabbis were lenient with this woman at the outset \u8212- they permitted her to remarry on the basis of a single witness \u8212- precisely because they did not want her to become an {\i agunah}. As the {\i gemara} stated elsewhere, {\i mishum agunah akeilu bah rabanan}: to prevent the tragedy of a chained woman, the Rabbis eased the standard of evidence. And yet, when things go wrong through no real fault of her own, the full weight of the consequences falls on her alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue that at minimum she should receive her {\i ketubah}: it is not her fault this happened, and she now finds herself with nothing. The {\i gemara\u8217's} response to this, implicitly, is that the Rabbis view the {\i ketubah} instrumentally \u8212- as a mechanism for regulating the husband\u8217's motivation, not as a social welfare provision for the wife. Since the goal here is to ensure the husband divorces her promptly and without financial hesitation, the {\i ketubah} serves no purpose and is withheld. Compelling as this logic is from a purely formal standpoint, it leaves the woman in a precarious position that is difficult not to find troubling. The {\i gemara} itself, back on an earlier {\i daf} (around daf 8), acknowledged the tension: {\i shappir avdu Rabbanan de\u8217'avdu hachi} \u8212- the Rabbis structured it correctly \u8212- though it also acknowledged the ulterior motive of preventing {\i iggun}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Return Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Natlah mizeh umizeh} \u8212- if she actually took anything from either husband, she must return it. One might think this is {\i peshita} \u8212- obvious. The {\i gemara} clarifies: it is necessary to state this because one might have thought that {\i keivan detafsa, lo mafkinan minah} \u8212- since she has already taken possession, we do not wrest it from her. In many cases of disputed property, once someone has gotten hold of something, we do not remove it. This ruling teaches that in our case we do: she must return it. This makes the ruling even more stringent from the woman\u8217's perspective \u8212- not only does she receive nothing, but whatever she may have already received must be given back.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzerim Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzerim Both Sides\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further states: {\i v\u8217'havlad mamzer mizu umizu} \u8212- children she bears are {\i mamzerim} from either husband. The {\i gemara} will now examine exactly what the nature of this {\i mamzer} status is: when is it {\i de\u8217'oraita} and when {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}? Counterintuitively, as will become apparent, the one whose {\i mamzeirus} is {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} is actually in a worse position than the one whose status is {\i de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To address this, the {\i gemara} pivots entirely to {\i Seder Zeraim}, specifically {\i Masechet Terumot}, and stays there for a substantial discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tenan hatam} \u8212- a {\i mishnah} in {\i Masechet Terumot} states: {\i ein tormin min ha-tamei al ha-tahor} \u8212- one may not separate {\i terumah} from {\i tamei} produce on behalf of {\i tahor} produce. If one does so {\i b\u8217'shogeg} \u8212- accidentally \u8212- {\i terumato terumah}, it counts as {\i terumah}. If one does so {\i b\u8217'meizid} \u8212- intentionally \u8212- {\i lo asa v\u8217'lo klum}, one has accomplished nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bad Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before analyzing this {\i mishnah}, the {\i gemara} introduces a parallel case from {\i Masechet Terumot}: if one sets aside a cucumber ({\i kishut}) as {\i terumah} and it turns out to be bitter, or a melon and it turns out to be rotten \u8212- the ruling is {\i terumah v\u8217'yachzor v\u8217'yitrom}: it counts as {\i terumah} (and must be treated accordingly) but one must go back and separate {\i terumah} a second time from valid produce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chisda vs.\u160 ?Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a dispute between Rav Chisda and Rav Natan bar Rav Yehoshua regarding the precise meaning of {\i lo asa v\u8217'lo klum} when {\i tamei terumah} is separated on {\i tahor} produce intentionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda interprets it maximally: {\i lo asa v\u8217'lo klum kol ikar} \u8212- he has done absolutely nothing whatsoever. Even the {\i se\u8217'ah} of produce that was physically separated as {\i terumah} from the {\i tamei} pile reverts to {\i tevel} status; it is as if no {\i terumah} was ever separated from either the {\i tamei} pile or the {\i tahor} pile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Natan bar Rav Yehoshua takes a narrower view: {\i lo asa v\u8217'lo klum letaken et ha-shirayim} \u8212- he has accomplished nothing with respect to permitting the {\i tahor} remainder for consumption. The {\i tahor} produce still requires valid {\i terumah} to be separated from it. However, the produce that was physically taken as {\i terumah} from the {\i tamei} pile does have the status of {\i terumah} \u8212- it is {\i tamei terumah} that must be burned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason Rav Chisda rejects Rav Natan\u8217's position is a practical concern: {\i gezeira dilma pasha v\u8217'lo mafrish} \u8212- if we tell the person that the {\i tamei} stuff he separated really is {\i terumah}, he may become negligent and fail to separate valid {\i terumah} a second time. He will reason: \u8220"I already separated {\i terumah} \u8212- the fact that it must be burned is irrelevant, I fulfilled my obligation.\u8221" To prevent this negligence, Rav Chisda holds that nothing the person did has any {\i terumah} status whatsoever, and he must start from scratch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then challenges Rav Chisda from the {\i mishnah} about the bad cucumber: there, even though the {\i terumah} was invalid, we say {\i terumah v\u8217'yachzor v\u8217'yitrom} \u8212- it has {\i terumah} status and he goes back to separate more. Why does the same logic of negligence not apply there? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shogeg ameizid karamis} \u8212- you are comparing an accidental case to an intentional one. In the {\i shogeg} case, the person is fundamentally trustworthy and genuinely attempting to fulfill the obligation; we can rely on him to separate {\i terumah} again when told to do so. The {\i meizid} case involves someone who is already acting improperly, and we cannot trust him to do anything correctly thereafter. Therefore the concern about negligence applies only to the {\i meizid} case, justifying Rav Chisda\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question is raised: {\i v\u8217'rami shogeg ashogeg} \u8212- let us compare a {\i shogeg} case to another {\i shogeg} case. In our {\i mishnah}, {\i b\u8217'shogeg terumato terumah} \u8212- accidentally separating {\i tamei} on {\i tahor} results in {\i terumah} status without requiring a second separation. But in the case of the bad cucumber, it says {\i terumah v\u8217'yachzor v\u8217'yitrom} \u8212- it is {\i terumah} and one must separate again. Why the difference?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i shogeg karov lemeizid} \u8212- the {\i shogeg} in the case of the bad cucumber is closer to {\i meizid}. {\i Ibai lei l\u8217'mitamei} \u8212- he really should have tasted the produce before designating it as {\i terumah}. Since he failed to take that simple precaution, his negligence is borderline intentional. The {\i Tosfos} raise the obvious question: how is one supposed to taste {\i tevel} produce before the separation? Their answer is that one should first separate {\i terumah} from a small piece that one then tastes for quality; having confirmed it is fit, one gives the remainder to the {\i kohen} as {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further comparison is raised: {\i v\u8217'rami meizid ameizid} \u8212- comparing intentional cases to intentional cases. In our {\i mishnah}, intentional separation of {\i tamei} on {\i tahor} is {\i lo asa klum}. But another {\i mishnah} in {\i Masechet Terumot} discusses taking {\i terumah} from a flowerpot without drainage holes (which is not obligated in {\i terumah}, being treated as detached from the ground) on behalf of a flowerpot with holes (which is obligated). There the ruling is {\i terumah v\u8217'yachzor v\u8217'yitrom} \u8212- it counts as {\i terumah} and one separates again. Why is that not also {\i lo asa klum}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i bitrei mani tza\u8217'at, uvechad mana la tza\u8217'at} \u8212- when dealing with two different types of vessels (one with holes, one without), the distinction is visually apparent and the person will understand when told to separate again. When dealing with a single vessel (as in the {\i tamei/tahor} case \u8212- two piles of identical-looking produce), the person will protest: \u8220"What difference does it make? This is {\i tevel} and that is {\i tevel}, they look the same to me.\u8221" He will not comply with a second separation, and therefore we must tell him upfront that his action accomplished nothing at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to Rav Natan bar Rav Yehoshua\u8217's position, the {\i gemara} poses the parallel challenge: a {\i mishnah} states that if one separates {\i terumah} from a flowerpot {\i with} holes on a flowerpot {\i without} holes \u8212- taking from the obligated on behalf of the exempt \u8212- {\i terumato terumah}, it has {\i terumah} status, and {\i velo tochal ad she-yotzi aleha terumah u-ma\u8217'aser mi-makom acher}, she may not eat from it until additional {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} are taken from elsewhere. Rashi explains: the separation has removed a portion from the obligated pile and treated it as {\i tevel}, so one cannot eat it or give it to a {\i kohen} until {\i terumah} is properly taken for it from another source. The question to Rav Natan bar Rav Yehoshua is: why in {\i his} case do we say the separated portion retains {\i terumah} status (and must be burned), while in this case we treat it as {\i tevel}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shani hacha de-mi-d\u8217'oraita terumah me\u8217'alyata hi} \u8212- our case is different, because the {\i tamei} {\i terumah} that was intentionally separated is in fact genuine {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}. This is the position of Rabbi Ilai.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalid Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through several configurations of valid and invalid {\i terumah} separation to clarify the positions of Rav Chisda and Rav Natan bar Rav Yehoshua and where the respective concerns about negligence and {\i terumah} status apply. The critical variable throughout is whether we are dealing with a {\i shogeg} or {\i meizid}, and whether the two types of produce involved are visually distinguishable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ilai Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Natan bar Rav Yehoshua\u8217's position that {\i tamei terumah} intentionally separated still has genuine {\i terumah} status is grounded in Rabbi Ilai\u8217's teaching. Rabbi Ilai asks: {\i minayin li-trom min ha-ra\u8217'ah al ha-yafah she-terumato terumah} \u8212- how do we know that if one separates {\i terumah} from low-quality produce on behalf of high-quality produce, it nonetheless counts as {\i terumah}? He derives it from the {\i pasuk}: {\i v\u8217'lo tisu alav cheit ba-harimechem et chelbo mimenu} \u8212- \u8220"you shall not carry a sin upon it when you take the best of it.\u8221" The {\i gemara} reasons: {\i v\u8217'ee ein kodesh, nesi\u8217'at cheit lama} \u8212- if separating from low-quality produce were not {\i terumah} at all, there would be no legal implication to the act, and therefore no {\i cheit} to speak of. The very fact that the {\i pasuk} speaks of a {\i cheit} for failing to take the {\i chelev} \u8212- the best \u8212- proves that when one does separate from inferior produce, the separation is legally effective as {\i terumah}. Otherwise, the person has done nothing at all and there is nothing to have a {\i cheit} about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} itself, when read contextually, is an exhortation to remember to give {\i terumah} and to give it from the best. But Rabbi Ilai reads it to yield a specific {\i halachic} derivation: the obligation to give the best implies that giving the worst still constitutes a valid act of {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i mikan she\u8217'ha-toram min ha-ra\u8217'ah al ha-yafah, terumato terumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Din Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah now turns to Rav Chisda with a sharp challenge: according to Rav Chisda, who holds that intentionally separating {\i tamei terumah} on {\i tahor} produce accomplishes absolutely nothing \u8212- even the portion physically removed reverts to {\i tevel} \u8212- the reason is a {\i gezeira} against negligence: we worry that if we call it {\i terumah}, the person will not separate again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this is a remarkable claim. {\i Mi ika midi de-mide\u8217'oraita havi trumah, u-mishum dilma pasha, afkuha rabanan l\u8217'chulin?} \u8212- Can it be that something which is {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} is retroactively stripped of that status and converted back into {\i chulin} by the Rabbis, simply because they are worried the person might not act properly? {\i V\u8217'chi beit din matnin la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah?} \u8212- Can {\i beit din} make a ruling that uproots something from the Torah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before Rav Chisda responds, Rabbah himself presents the {\i mamzer} case as an instance of exactly this problem. {\i V\u8217'hatnan, havlad mamzer mizeh umizeh} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} declares the children {\i mamzerim} from both husbands. With respect to the second husband, this is straightforward: she was still legally married to the first husband when she cohabited with the second, and so the child is a genuine {\i mamzer de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what of a child from the first husband? {\i Ishto hi} \u8212- she is his legal wife. {\i V\u8217'Yisroel ma\u8217'alya hu} \u8212- and he is a perfectly kosher Jew. How can their child be a {\i mamzer}? And this is not a mere stringency we can treat lightly: {\i v\u8217'kashrina lei b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- by declaring him a {\i mamzer}, we are binding him to a status that restricts whom he may marry. He cannot marry a {\i bat Yisroel}, and he cannot marry a {\i mamzeret} either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, the {\i gemara} brings in the ruling of Shmuel \u8212- confirmed by Rabbi Yochanan when Ravin came from {\i Eretz Yisroel} \u8212- that this child is {\i assur b\u8217'mamzeret}, forbidden even to a {\i mamzeret}. This is a {\i mamzer l\u8217'chumra} in the most stringent sense: he cannot marry a {\i bat Yisroel} (because the Rabbis call him a {\i mamzer}) and cannot marry a {\i mamzeret} (because {\i de\u8217'oraita} he is a perfectly kosher Jew, and the Rabbis did not create a positive permission for him to marry a {\i mamzeret} \u8212- only the real prohibition). The {\i gemara} states explicitly: {\i v\u8217'amai karay lei mamzer? L\u8217'osro b\u8217'vat Yisroel} \u8212- why do we call him a {\i mamzer}? Specifically in order to prevent him from marrying a {\i bat Yisroel}. He can, however, apparently marry another {\i mamzer derabbanan}. This is the painful architecture of {\i mamzeirus derabbanan}: the child is the vehicle through which the mother is punished, and he is caught in a status that makes him, in one sense, worse off than a {\i mamzer de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chisda\u8217's Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda responds to Rabbah\u8217's challenge \u8212- {\i v\u8217'at lo tisbera?} You yourself don\u8217't think {\i beit din} has that power? \u8212- by marshaling examples of cases where Chazal undeniably do uproot Torah obligations. The {\i mamzer} case just discussed is itself such an example: a child of a perfectly valid marriage is declared a {\i mamzer} by rabbinic decree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Daughters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda sends Rabbah further proof via Rav Acha bar Rav Huna: the {\i baraita} dealing with the marriage of a minor girl ({\i ketanah}). When a father dies, the mother and brothers may arrange the marriage of a daughter who has not yet reached majority. Such a marriage is only {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}; the girl retains the right, upon reaching majority, to exercise {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- refusal \u8212- and leave without a {\i get}, with no blemish to her status. The {\i baraita} states that this husband can inherit her, can become {\i tamei} for her if he is a {\i kohen}, and she can eat {\i terumah} by virtue of his household. The dispute is about the precise timing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Inherit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} holds the husband can inherit her {\i mishe\u8217'ta\u8217'amod be-komatah} \u8212- from when she reaches physical maturity (in years and signs). {\i Beit Hillel} holds it is {\i mishetikanes le-chuppah} \u8212- from when she enters the {\i chuppah}. {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds it is {\i mishetiba\u8217'el} \u8212- from when the marriage is consummated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then clarifies {\i Beit Shammai\u8217's} position. One might have read them as requiring {\i both} maturity {\i and} the {\i chuppah}. The {\i gemara} explains that this is actually Beit Shammai\u8217's critique of Beit Hillel: Beit Shammai is saying to Beit Hillel \u8212- when does the {\i chuppah} actually take legal effect? Only if she has already reached majority. If she is too young, the {\i chuppah} is legally meaningless. Conversely, Beit Shammai holds that once she is of age, {\i chuppah} alone is sufficient \u8212- even without a second ceremony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes a tension with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's own position: {\i v\u8217'ha amar Rabbi Eliezer ein ma\u8217'aseh ketanah klum} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer himself holds that the acts of a {\i ketanah} are legally null. If so, how can consummation of the marriage while she is still a minor establish his right to inherit her? The resolution is {\i eima mishe-tagdil ve-tibaeil} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer must mean from when she has reached majority {\i and then} the marriage is consummated. This aligns him with the thrust of Beit Shammai\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the central question: the {\i baraita} states {\i yorshah} \u8212- he inherits her. But {\i d\u8217'midoraita} her family inherits her property, since the marriage is only {\i derabbanan}. The Rabbis are therefore taking property that belongs {\i de\u8217'oraita} to her family and transferring it to the husband. This is a direct application of {\i hefker beit din hefker} \u8212- the principle that {\i beit din} has the authority to declare property ownerless and redistribute it. Rav Chisda is using this as proof that rabbinic authorities can indeed override Torah-level property rights.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ezra Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i minayin she-hefker beit din hayah hefker} \u8212- what is the source for this principle? Rabbi Yitzchak cites the verse in Ezra: {\i kol asher lo yavo li-shloshet ha-yamim ba-atzat ha-sarim ve-ha-zekenim, yachoram kol rechusho} \u8212- whoever fails to appear within three days at the order of the princes and elders will have all his property confiscated and he will be separated from the community. The {\i gemara} notes that this proof is not quite a textual derivation in the classical sense \u8212- it is not using the verse to establish what God intended as law. Rather, Ezra\u8217's authoritative precedent demonstrates that {\i beit din} possesses this power; the assumption is that Ezra was acting within the framework of {\i halacha}, and therefore his action is evidence that {\i halacha} permits it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joshua Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar cites a different source, from Yehoshua: {\i eleh ha-nechalot asher nichalu Elazar ha-Kohen v\u8217'Yehoshua bin Nun v\u8217'rashei ha-avot l\u8217'matot Bnei Yisrael} \u8212- these are the inheritances that Elazar the Kohen, Yehoshua bin Nun, and the heads of the fathers of the tribes of Israel distributed. Rabbi Elazar asks: what is the connection between {\i rashim} (leaders) and {\i avot} (fathers)? He derives: just as fathers apportion inheritance to their children however they wish, so too the {\i rashim} \u8212- the {\i beit din} \u8212- can distribute the people\u8217's property however they choose. Here, unlike the Ezra source, there is a genuine textual derivation being offered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} also states {\i u-metame lah} \u8212- if the minor wife dies, her husband (if he is a {\i kohen}) may become {\i tamei} for her. But {\i de\u8217'oraita}, only her father may become {\i tamei} for her \u8212- the husband\u8217's right to do so comes from the rabbinic recognition of the marriage. The Rabbis are therefore overriding a {\i de\u8217'oraita} restriction on a {\i kohen\u8217's} becoming {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Met Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The justification offered is {\i mishum de-havya lah met mitzvah} \u8212- she is treated as a {\i met mitzvah}, a corpse with no one to bury it. The {\i baraita} defines {\i met mitzvah} as {\i kol she\u8217'ein lo kovrim} \u8212- one for whom there are no available mourners. {\i Korei v\u8217'achereim onim oto, ein zeh met mitzvah} \u8212- if one calls out and others respond that they will attend to the burial, that is not a {\i met mitzvah}. How does this girl become a {\i met mitzvah}? Because the Rabbis, through {\i hefker beit din hefker}, have transferred her property to the husband. Her family therefore says: since we receive no inheritance from her, we decline to bury her \u8212- let the one who inherits her take care of the burial. When the husband calls out for the family to bury their daughter and they refuse, she becomes a {\i met mitzvah} and he, as her husband and heir, must attend to her. This is admittedly a stark picture of the rabbinic reconstruction of incentives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah D\u8217'Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the {\i baraita} states {\i v\u8217'ochelet bigino terumah} \u8212- she eats {\i terumah} by virtue of being part of his household. The {\i gemara} specifies: {\i biterumah derabbanan} \u8212- she eats only {\i terumah derabbanan}, since the marriage itself is {\i derabbanan}. A marriage that exists only by rabbinic enactment confers only rabbinic-level rights, and therefore {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} is not available to her through this marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 90\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Remarries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 90 falls within the {\i perek} of {\i Ha\u8217'isha Raba}, one of the more challenging {\i perakim} in Yevamot \u8212- challenging both because the logic of the first {\i mishna} is genuinely difficult to follow, and because the {\i sugyot} themselves are dense and complicated. The {\i perek} introduces the sweeping idea of {\i yesh koach l\u8217'chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}, as well as the whole framework of believing an {\i ed echad}. It is also, notably, one of the {\i perakim} of the {\i masechet} most intensively studied in the {\i yeshivot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} in the {\i mishna} is as follows: a woman receives a report that her husband has died and she proceeds to remarry. If she does so {\i birshut beit din} \u8212- with the permission of the court \u8212- and the husband subsequently reappears alive, she suffers a cascade of negative consequences: she must receive a {\i get} from both husbands, any child born is a {\i mamzer}, and she forfeits all of her financial rights. However, if she remarried {\i shelo birshut beit din} \u8212- without court authorization, operating unilaterally \u8212- she simply returns to her first husband without suffering any of these consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This boggles the mind on two fronts. First, why the negative consequences at all? And second, why is her situation worse when she acted {\i birshut} \u8212- with official backing \u8212- than when she acted {\i shelo birshut}, which would seem to be the more reckless course?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The very first line of the {\i gemara} \u8212- spoken in the voice of the {\i stam}, not a specific {\i amora} \u8212- resolves the distinction by reinterpreting the terms. {\i Birshut beit din}, it proposes, refers to a case in which she remarried on the basis of a single witness, and it was the {\i beit din} that granted permission to do so on that thin evidence. {\i Shelo birshut beit din}, the case without negative consequences, refers to a situation in which there were actually two witnesses \u8212- no special {\i heter} was even necessary, and therefore there was no court involvement. So what initially appears to be a harsher case ({\i shelo birshut}) is actually grounded in stronger evidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences as Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reframing still leaves the underlying question unresolved: even granting that there was only a single witness, why do all these consequences follow? If {\i beit din} gave her permission, why should she bear such severe penalties?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i stam} of the {\i gemara} introduces the principle of {\i dayka u-mintsva}: we permit a woman to remarry on the strength of a single witness only because we rely on the assumption that she will thoroughly investigate the matter before proceeding. She knows, in other words, that if the husband returns, devastating consequences await her \u8212- and it is precisely this knowledge of those consequences that motivates her to investigate carefully. The negative consequences are thus framed not as punishments but as the mechanism that underwrites and legitimates the radical leniency of allowing remarriage on the basis of {\i ed echad} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explanation is difficult on its own terms. One wonders how a woman stranded in distant {\i Bavel} is supposed to conduct such an investigation, and indeed some {\i acharonim} argue that she should therefore be formally warned of the consequences before remarrying \u8212- something the {\i gemara} never actually requires. The explanation strains, but it is the {\i stam}\u8217's answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Friedman\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stripping Stam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scholarly reading offered by Shamma Friedman of Bar-Ilan University proposes a different approach. His method involves stripping away the {\i stam} layer of the {\i gemara} to recover what the {\i amoraim} themselves understood \u8212- a methodology associated with the academic study of Talmud. When one does this, he argues, there is no trace whatsoever among the {\i amoraim} of the radical reinterpretation that {\i birshut} means one witness and {\i shelo birshut} means two witnesses. That reading appears to be a {\i stam} innovation with no amoraic foundation. The {\i mishna} should instead be read {\i k\u8217'pshuto}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reinterpreting Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, {\i birshut} and {\i shelo birshut} mean precisely what they say: in both cases the woman may have acted on the same quality of evidence (whether one witness or two is not the operative distinction), but in the {\i birshut} case she brought the evidence before {\i beit din} and received an official ruling, while in the {\i shelo birshut} case she acted on her own without seeking a {\i psak}. The question then remains: why the vastly more severe consequences when she operated through the court?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natural Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Friedman\u8217's answer is that the consequences are not a {\i knas} \u8212- not a punishment \u8212- at all. The {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna} is that when she acts {\i birshut beit din}, the court officially recognizes the second marriage. That is the {\i chiddush} of {\i chachamim}: in the name of {\i mishum agunah akilu bah rabanan}, the rabbis validate a second marriage on grounds that would ordinarily be insufficient. Having done so, if the first husband reappears, the woman\u8217's {\i halachic} status is not that she has committed adultery \u8212- she acted with full {\i beit din} authorization \u8212- but rather that she is now, in an unprecedented situation, legally married to two men simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Married to Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of the consequences that follow are not punishments but the natural {\i halachic} fallout of this unprecedented status. She needs a {\i get} from each husband because she is married to both. She forfeits financial rights from each because the entitlements of one marriage are incompatible with a simultaneous second marriage. {\i Halacha} has simply never encountered this creature before, and the consequences are the necessary result of working out what it means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When she acted {\i shelo birshut beit din}, by contrast, the second marriage was never officially recognized. She slept with another man believing her husband to be dead, and in that sense she is something of an {\i anusah} \u8212- she did not willfully commit adultery. But since the court never validated the second marriage, it remains legally invisible; she returns to her first husband, and there is nothing to undo. The second man\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was never acknowledged as a marriage in the eyes of {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading preserves the plain meaning of {\i birshut} and {\i shelo birshut}, and renders the consequences comprehensible as logical rather than punitive. It also arguably reflects better on the institution: the rabbis were not designing a punitive apparatus to intimidate women into investigating carefully; they were simply confronting the {\i halachic} reality generated by their own leniency on behalf of {\i agunot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Audience Q&A\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several questions arise naturally from Friedman\u8217's reading. One might ask: the first marriage was also just two witnesses without court involvement \u8212- why should the court\u8217's validation of the second marriage create a categorically different status? The answer is that the second marriage requires court permission because a woman cannot simply acquire a second husband while still legally married to a first; it is the {\i beit din}\u8217's ruling that creates the recognition, not the act of {\i kiddushin} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A deeper question concerns incentive structures: does Friedman\u8217's reading not create a strong incentive to avoid the {\i beit din} altogether, since operating {\i shelo birshut} produces none of these consequences? The {\i mishna} itself addresses this, at least partially, by noting that the woman who acted {\i birshut beit din} does not bring a {\i korban} and is not considered to have sinned, whereas the woman who acted unilaterally would still owe a {\i korban}. And practically, social pressure to have marriages recognized by the court remains powerful \u8212- people do not typically wish to enter marriages that society will not acknowledge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the status of a child born in the {\i shelo birshut} scenario: a child born to the first husband would not be a {\i mamzer}, since she returns to that marriage; a child born from the unrecognized second union, however, would still be a {\i mamzer}, since that was an act of adultery, however unintentional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might finally ask why, if this is the better reading, the {\i stam} of the {\i gemara} did not adopt it. That question is difficult to answer definitively; at some point, reconstruction of editorial motivations becomes speculation. What can be said is that the {\i stam} consistently pushes toward a stricter, more punitive reading throughout this {\i perek}, treating the consequences as a {\i knas}, and reads both the {\i mishna} and the statements of the {\i amoraim} through that lens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Ed Echad?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Setting aside the question of how to read the {\i mishna}, the deeper issue lurking behind the entire discussion is: on what basis do we believe an {\i ed echad} in this context at all? Torah law requires two witnesses for matters of consequence, yet the entire architecture of this {\i perek} rests on permitting remarriage on the testimony of one. This is a major question, and the {\i rishonim} offer three distinct answers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is {\i yesh koach l\u8217'chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah} \u8212- the rabbis simply have the power to uproot a Torah rule. This is the position associated with Tosafot, and it helps explain why the {\i sugya} on {\i yesh koach} appears here: not merely to address the {\i mamzer} question raised by the {\i gemara}, but to justify the entire framework of {\i ed echad} in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second approach, associated with Rashi, is {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}: the rabbis have a particular power in the domain of marriage to uproot {\i kiddushin} retroactively. This is not a sweeping power to override Torah law in general, but a targeted capacity tied to the institution of marriage itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third approach, offered by the Ra\u8217'ah (and rejected by Tosafot), is that once there is sufficient circumstantial evidence, it rises to the level of {\i anan sa\u8217'adi} \u8212- something so self-evident that it operates on a {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} level without any rabbinic override at all. The combination of {\i ed echad} and the woman\u8217's theoretical capacity to investigate, the Ra\u8217'ah argues, creates that threshold. Tosafot rejects this: it is simply not a genuine {\i anan sa\u8217'adi}, and to claim otherwise is to stretch the category beyond recognition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a {\i Sifrei} that attempts to anchor {\i ed echad} directly in a biblical verse: {\i lo yakum ed echad b\u8217'ish l\u8217'chol avon u\u8217'l\u8217'chol chatas} \u8212- one witness shall not stand against a man for any sin or transgression. The {\i Sifrei} reads {\i l\u8217'chol avon u\u8217'l\u8217'chol chatas} as limiting: one witness does not suffice for those matters, but it does suffice for testimony about a woman\u8217's marital status. This would give a biblical basis for the {\i ed echad} leniency here. But the question of why we believe {\i ed echad} remains genuinely difficult, and {\i yesh koach la\u8217'akor davar} is one serious answer \u8212- all the more striking given that the very {\i sugya} we are in the middle of cannot definitively establish that principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Limiting the Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against the background of the {\i stam}\u8217's sweeping and stringent reading of the {\i mishna}, the {\i amoraim} themselves attempt to limit its scope. Rav\u8217's position is perhaps the most radical: where there were two witnesses who testified to the husband\u8217's death, even if the husband subsequently reappears alive, she may remain married to the second husband. The {\i gemara} cannot straightforwardly accept this, since the husband is standing there in front of everyone, so it qualifies Rav\u8217's statement by framing it as a case of competing testimonies \u8212- two witnesses say he is dead, others say this man before them is the husband \u8212- and adjudicates between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Yerushalmi, as Tosafot notes, reads Rav\u8217's statement more simply. It accepts the apparent legal fiction: two witnesses have testified he is dead, and even if the man appears before us and every person in town recognizes him, we create a legal fiction and say he is not the man. Two witnesses are as authoritative as a hundred, and patently obvious reality cannot simply override their testimony. The implication is stunning: once she has remarried on the basis of two witnesses, even if he returns, she stays with the second husband, and none of the {\i mishna}\u8217's negative consequences apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel offers a different limiting principle: when she herself contradicts the witnesses \u8212- when she denies that this man is her husband, or makes some claim against the testimony \u8212- she also does not suffer the full consequences of the {\i mishna}. The {\i gemara} reads this in a similar frame to Rav\u8217's position, involving competing testimonies about identity. But read straightforwardly, Shmuel may simply be saying that when the woman herself disputes the matter, whether by claiming the returnee is not her husband or in some broader sense denying the situation, her position carries weight sufficient to change the outcome. Together, Rav and Shmuel chart two paths for limiting the {\i mishna}\u8217's harsh sweep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Rejects Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i stam} of the {\i gemara}, however, consistently resists these limiting moves. Rather than accepting Rav\u8217's or Shmuel\u8217's positions as valid limitations on the {\i mishna}, the {\i stam} repeatedly narrows the scope of those amoraic statements themselves, so that the {\i mishna}\u8217's consequences remain applicable in nearly all cases. The dynamic is a recurring one throughout this {\i perek}: the {\i amoraim} try to limit the {\i mishna}, and the {\i stam} pushes back, insisting on a broader, more stringent application of the {\i mishna}\u8217's rule. The {\i stam} thus consistently presents itself as more {\i machmir} than the {\i amoraim}, reading the case as a {\i knas} and treating the sweep of the consequences as essentially unqualified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Ed Echad? (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of why {\i ed echad} is believed here, Tosafot\u8217's approach, noted above as {\i yesh koach la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}, carries an important nuance. Tosafot does not simply say the rabbis can uproot Torah law whenever they wish. Rather, Tosafot\u8217's position is that when an innovation has some logical support behind it \u8212- when it {\i appears} to operate within Torah categories even if technically it does not \u8212- then the rabbis may effectuate it without it constituting a genuine {\i akira d\u8217'Oraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paradigm case is the discussion elsewhere in this {\i perek} about a {\i kohen} being {\i metamei} to a minor wife ({\i ketana}) whose {\i kiddushin} is rabbinically dissoluble. The {\i gemara} justifies this by analogy to {\i meis mitzvah}. Tosafot objects: she is not really a {\i meis mitzvah} \u8212- a {\i kohen} cannot be {\i metamei} to every relative who happens to have no other heirs simply by invoking that category. But Tosafot explains that because it is {\i sort of like} a {\i meis mitzvah}, because there is a logical basis to the parallel, it does not constitute a clear {\i akira d\u8217'Oraisa}. The same logic applies to {\i ed echad}: because the woman is assumed to investigate ({\i dayka u-mintsva}), and because there is therefore some rational basis for treating the testimony as sufficient, the act of relying on it is not a naked uprooting of Torah law. There is enough of a fig leaf to place the innovation in a category short of outright {\i akira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's approach points in a similar direction but rests on a distinct mechanism. Rashi\u8217's explanation of why {\i ed echad} suffices here is {\i afkinu rabbanan l\u8217'kiddushin minei} \u8212- the rabbis have the power of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}, the uprooting of the marital bond. Critically, Rashi does not regard this as an unlimited power. The rabbis exercise {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} only when there is, in the phrase used to describe it, a fig leaf \u8212- some mechanism that makes it appear as though something legitimate is happening. They will not simply declare a marriage dissolved by fiat. But when there is a {\i get}, even a defective one, or when there is testimony that the husband has died, that serves as the fig leaf: behind the scenes the rabbis perform {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}, but it {\i looks} like the {\i get} is operating, or it {\i looks} like the husband is confirmed dead. The machinery of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} thus turns on the presence of some apparent legal basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'ah\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ra\u8217'ah\u8217's approach, as noted in the earlier discussion of the three {\i rishonim}, holds that once the combination of {\i ed echad} and the woman\u8217's investigative capacity creates sufficient circumstantial evidence, the matter rises to the level of {\i anan sa\u8217'adi} \u8212- as self-evident as direct testimony \u8212- and therefore operates on a full {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} level without any need for rabbinic override. The appeal of this approach is that it avoids invoking the controversial power of {\i yesh koach} entirely. Its weakness, which leads Tosafot to reject it, is that the evidentiary basis here is simply not strong enough to genuinely qualify as {\i anan sa\u8217'adi} in any rigorous sense. Calling it that understates the real legal risk being taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teruma Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repayment Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to an extended series of cases designed to test the principle of {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}. The first involves the laws of {\i teruma}. The baseline {\i halacha} is that a non-{\i kohen} who eats {\i teruma} unintentionally ({\i beshogeg}) must repay an equivalent amount of food to the {\i kohen}, whereupon that food acquires the status of {\i teruma} in its stead. The {\i braita} under discussion concerns the specific case where the {\i teruma} he ate was {\i tehora} \u8212- ritually pure \u8212- and he repays with {\i chullin temei\u8217'in} \u8212- impure non-{\i teruma} food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute is between {\i Sumchus} in the name of Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim}. {\i Sumchus} says: if the non-{\i kohen} repaid with {\i chullin temei\u8217'in beshogeg} \u8212- inadvertently \u8212- the repayment is valid and he is done; if he did so {\i bemezid} \u8212- deliberately \u8212- the repayment is invalid ({\i ein tashlumo tashlumin}). The {\i chachamim} say: in either case, {\i beshogeg} or {\i bemezid}, the repayment takes effect \u8212- the {\i chullin temei\u8217'in} becomes {\i teruma temei\u8217'ah} \u8212- but he must then go back and repay again with {\i chullin tehorim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an obvious problem: why should the {\i chachamim} require a second payment? The {\i chullin temei\u8217'in} became {\i teruma} in its stead \u8212- that works {\i d\u8217'Oraisa}. And why, according to Rabbi Meir, should the repayment be invalid {\i bemezid}? If {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} the food transferred ownership to the {\i kohen} the moment it was handed over, on what basis do the rabbis say it did not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the problem in a pointed way. If the {\i kohen} received the {\i chullin temei\u8217'in}, and they are now his property on a biblical level, and he then uses that money \u8212- say, to betroth a woman \u8212- that betrothal should take effect. For the rabbis to declare {\i ein tashlumo tashlumin} and strip the {\i kohen} of what is biblically his, retroactively making that betrothal invalid, appears to be a clear case of {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before taking up the {\i gemara}\u8217's response, it is worth noting that Tosafot raises a threshold question: why does the {\i gemara} need to use the case of betrothal to dramatize the problem? Why not simply point to the more straightforward {\i halachic} consequence \u8212- that a {\i kohen} who eats the supposedly-{\i teruma} food while {\i tamei} would be liable or exempt based on whether we treat it as {\i teruma} or not? Tosafot\u8217's discussion of this is instructive, though not pursued at length here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raba\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raba (or, some say, the anonymous {\i kadi}) proposes that the {\i braita} is missing a clause, and must be reconstructed as follows ({\i chasori mechasra ve-hacha katni}): the beginning of the {\i braita} is actually speaking of a different case \u8212- where the non-{\i kohen} ate {\i teruma temei\u8217'ah}. There, he can certainly repay with {\i chullin temei\u8217'in} of any kind, since he ate something that was already impure. The real case being debated \u8212- about valid and invalid repayment, {\i beshogeg} versus {\i bemezid} \u8212- concerns eating {\i teruma tehora}. When one eats {\i teruma tehora} and repays with {\i chullin temei\u8217'in}, the {\i kohen} is genuinely damaged: he ate something pure and usable, and he is getting back something that becomes {\i teruma temei\u8217'ah}, which he cannot eat and can only burn. That is where the dispute between Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim} lies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Negating Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's core question remains: even under Raba\u8217's reconstruction, how can the rabbis \u8212- whether by declaring {\i ein tashlumo tashlumin} (Rabbi Meir\u8217's position on {\i mezid}) or by requiring a second payment (the {\i chachamim}\u8217's position) \u8212- negate or complicate what works {\i d\u8217'Oraisa}? The biblical transfer of ownership happened. The betrothal with that money would be valid. How can rabbinic law undo that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where the {\i sugya} explicitly asks whether {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers with a statement from Rav Acha the son of Rav Ika: {\i kensu shogeg atu mezid ikka beinaiahu} \u8212- the real difference between Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim} is not about the {\i mezid} case at all; everyone agrees that in the {\i mezid} case, the repayment took effect and the {\i kohen} owns what he received. The dispute is about the {\i shogeg} case. The {\i chachamim} say that even {\i beshogeg}, the payment takes effect and you must give a second time. Rabbi Meir is lenient: {\i beshogeg}, the payment takes effect and you are done \u8212- no second payment required. Rabbi Meir imposes the additional burden of a second payment only in the {\i mezid} case, as a {\i knas}, but even there, the original transfer of ownership stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under this reading, no one is actually declaring that the {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} transfer of ownership was void. The question is only whether a rabbinic fine ({\i knas}) attaches \u8212- a second payment. No one claims that the {\i kohen} doesn\u8217't own what he received, so the betrothal concern dissolves. There is no {\i akira d\u8217'Oraisa} here at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution prompts a natural question: if the real issue is just about ownership of money, why didn\u8217't the {\i gemara} simply invoke the principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker}? The rabbis could declare the original payment ownerless and resolve the ownership problem that way, without needing to invoke {\i yesh koach la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this directly. In a formulation that cuts to the heart of {\i hefker beit din hefker}\u8217's proper scope, Tosafot says: {\i de\u8217'hacha ein shayach lomar kein} \u8212- that principle simply does not apply here, and the reason is telling. {\i Demah pasha kohen she\u8217'yesh lanu lehafkir mamono} \u8212- what did the {\i kohen} do wrong that we should strip him of his property? {\i Hefker beit din hefker} in its authentic application is a punitive mechanism: it is used to fine someone who did something wrong. It is not a free-floating power to redistribute ownership whenever convenient. The {\i kohen} here did nothing wrong; the person who paid him improperly is the one at fault, and yet we would be taking money from the {\i kohen} to solve the problem. Tosafot holds that {\i hefker beit din hefker} cannot be applied in that direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant point. The principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker}, rooted in texts from Yehoshua and in early amoraic usage, was originally conceived as a coercive or punitive tool \u8212- seizing the property of someone who flouted communal authority. Over time the {\i stam} of the {\i gemara} expanded it into a broader mechanism for manipulating property ownership. Tosafot here pulls it back toward its original context: you can use {\i hefker beit din hefker} against someone who did something wrong, not to reallocate assets from an innocent party.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blood Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Atonement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} moves to its second extended case bearing on {\i yesh koach}. The case involves {\i dam} \u8212- the blood of a {\i korban} \u8212- that became {\i tamei} and was then sprinkled on the {\i mizbeiach}. The general {\i halacha} is that the {\i tzitz} worn by the {\i kohen gadol} effects acceptance ({\i meratzeh}) for offerings that became {\i tamei}: {\i bedi\u8217'eved}, the sprinkling works and the owners receive {\i kappara}. However, if the {\i kohen} who sprinkled the blood did so {\i bemezid} \u8212- intentionally, knowing it was {\i tamei} and violating the rule \u8212- the rabbis declare that the owners do not receive {\i kappara} and presumably must bring a new offering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzitz Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This seems to be a direct case of {\i yesh koach la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}: the {\i braita} itself states that the {\i tzitz} effects acceptance {\i bein beshogeg bein bemezid bein be\u8217'ones bein beratzon bein beyachid bein betzibbur} \u8212- in every case, regardless of intent, willfulness, or whether it is an individual or communal offering. {\i D\u8217'Oraisa}, the sprinkling works and the owners are atoned. Yet the rabbis declare {\i bemezid lo urtza} \u8212- it does not work \u8212- and the owners bring a second {\i korban}, which would constitute {\i chullin ba\u8217'azarah} given that {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} they were already atoned. This appears to be a clear example of the rabbis uprooting a biblical law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Chanina resolves the contradiction. {\i Lo urtza}, he says, does not mean that the owners fail to receive {\i kappara}. They do receive {\i kappara}; the {\i tzitz} works on the {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} level and they need not bring another {\i korban}. What {\i lo urtza} means is that the {\i kohanim} are not permitted to eat the meat of the offering. The {\i tzitz} atones for the owners, but it does not grant permission for the {\i kohanim} to eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses this answer. Even if we accept that the owners are atoned and no second {\i korban} is required, we are still uprooting a {\i mitzvah}: the verse states {\i ve\u8217'achalu osam asher kuppar bahem} \u8212- the {\i kohanim} shall eat it, through which the owners are atoned. The eating of the meat by the {\i kohanim} is itself a biblical obligation, and by telling the {\i kohanim} not to eat, the rabbis are preventing the performance of a {\i mitzvas aseh d\u8217'Oraisa}. How is that permissible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shev Ve\u8217'al Ta\u8217'aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh shani}. There is a fundamental distinction between the rabbis actively uprooting a biblical law \u8212- causing something prohibited to be treated as permitted, or something unmarried to be treated as married \u8212- and the rabbis passively preventing a positive commandment from being fulfilled. In the latter case, they instruct: sit still and do not perform this act. The non-performance is the violation, but it is a passive non-performance, and the rabbis possess the authority to say {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh} even where a {\i mitzvas aseh d\u8217'Oraisa} thereby goes unperformed. That is the conclusion that emerges from this {\i sugya}: {\i yesh koach la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah be-shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should also be noted: the passive-active distinction here is not simply the distinction between a {\i lav} and an {\i aseh}. In this very case there is in fact a {\i lav} \u8212- the prohibition of {\i nosar} ({\i lo sosiru mimmenu ad boker}) \u8212- yet what the rabbis are doing is still classified as {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh}, because the rabbis are not making anyone actively transgress; they are simply preventing an act from being performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shev Ve\u8217'al Ta\u8217'aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a series of cases \u8212- the questioner had prepared them as proofs that {\i yesh koach la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah} extends beyond {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- each of which involves the rabbis preventing the performance of a biblical {\i mitzvah} through a rabbinic prohibition. There are seven such cases, and the questioner introduces them one by one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arel Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case involves an {\i arel} \u8212- more precisely, a convert ({\i ger}) who underwent {\i berit milah} on the eve of Pesach. The {\i halacha} is that such a person is treated rabbinically as though he has just separated from a corpse and requires a seven-day {\i taharah} process \u8212- even though {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} no such requirement exists. The consequence is that the rabbis, by imposing this seven-day waiting period, prevent him from bringing the {\i korban pesach} \u8212- an enormously significant positive commandment \u8212- that very night. This looks like a case of the rabbis uprooting a biblical {\i mitzvah} by means of a rabbinic rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haza\u8217'ah Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case involves {\i haza\u8217'ah}: sprinkling the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} on a person on the seventh day of his purification process. If that seventh day falls on {\i erev Pesach} which is {\i Shabbos}, and sprinkling on {\i Shabbos} is rabbinically prohibited, then the person cannot complete his purification, cannot become {\i tahor} in time, and therefore cannot bring the {\i korban pesach} that night. The rabbinically prohibited {\i haza\u8217'ah} has thus prevented a {\i d\u8217'Oraisa mitzvah}. Similar logic drives the other examples: carrying a knife ({\i izmel}) for {\i berit milah} through a {\i karmelis} is rabbinically forbidden, preventing performance of {\i milah} if the knife was not pre-positioned; wearing {\i tzitzit} on a linen garment is rabbinically prohibited out of concern one will wear it at night when the {\i mitzvah} does not apply and thereby violate {\i shatnez}, so the rabbis say don\u8217't put on {\i tzitzit} at all; the two sheep ({\i kivsei atzeret}) brought on {\i Shavuos} that fell on {\i Shabbos} require sprinkling of blood but the rabbis say not to sprinkle, preventing the eating of their meat; {\i shofar} on {\i Rosh Hashana} and {\i lulav} on {\i Sukkos} when those days fall on {\i Shabbos} are not taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of these cases, the questioner had assumed, would prove that the rabbis can actively override a {\i d\u8217'Oraisa mitzvah}. But once the {\i gemara} establishes the principle of {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh}, the questioner concedes: every single one of these cases is precisely {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- the rabbis issue a prohibition, and a {\i mitzvah} thereby goes unperformed, but no active uprooting of Torah law takes place. None of them, therefore, proves {\i yesh koach} in the stronger sense of actively overriding a biblical rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Navi Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elijah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} considers whether a {\i navi} can violate Torah law, citing the paradigmatic case of Elijah at Mount Carmel. Elijah had his followers build an altar outside Jerusalem during a period when the {\i beit hamikdash} was standing \u8212- an act that constitutes building a {\i bamah}, ordinarily prohibited. Yet the {\i navi} commanded it and God accepted it. This seems to demonstrate that a {\i navi} has the power to override a Torah prohibition. If so, one might ask: can we learn from a {\i navi}\u8217's authority to {\i chazal}\u8217's authority \u8212- if {\i nevuah} allows temporary override, does rabbinic authority include the same?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Mitzva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the inference. {\i Shani hatam dechtiv elav tishme\u8217'un} \u8212- that case is different, because there is a specific biblical commandment to listen to a {\i navi}. The {\i navi}\u8217's authority to issue temporary rulings is itself grounded in a {\i mitzvas aseh d\u8217'Oraisa} ({\i elav tishme\u8217'un}), not in any general principle of overriding Torah law. One cannot derive from this a general principle applicable to the rabbinic system.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migdar Milta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, even the {\i navi}\u8217's ability to override is categorically different in nature. What Elijah did was a {\i hora\u8217'at sha\u8217'ah} \u8212- a ruling for an exigent moment \u8212- not a systemic or permanent modification of the law. And the {\i gemara} characterizes it as {\i migdar milta}: a protective measure necessitated by exigent circumstances, designed to be limited in time and scope. {\i Migdar milta} is categorically distinct from {\i yesh koach} in the sense of a structural rabbinic power to uproot Torah law as part of the ongoing legal system.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shev Ve\u8217'al Ta\u8217'aseh (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzitzit Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the cases from the earlier list \u8212- {\i sadin b\u8217'tzitzit} \u8212- raises a conceptual question. If a person wears a linen garment and the rabbis tell him not to put {\i tzitzit} on it, is that really {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh}? After all, wearing a four-cornered garment without {\i tzitzit} is a failure to perform a {\i mitzvah} while actively wearing the garment \u8212- that sounds more like {\i kum ve\u8217'aseh} (active failure) than passive inaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this by rethinking the conceptual structure of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i tzitzit}. The obligation, Tosafot argues, does not attach at the moment of putting the garment on; the obligation is to have {\i tzitzit} on a garment one {\i is wearing}. The act of donning the garment does not yet trigger the {\i mitzvah} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} is a continuous one that takes effect once the garment is on. Therefore, the person who wears the linen garment without {\i tzitzit} is not actively committing a violation in the act of putting it on; he is passively failing to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} that runs throughout the time he wears it. That is {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh}: the rabbis, by prohibiting {\i tzitzit} on linen, cause him to passively not fulfill the ongoing obligation, rather than requiring him to actively do something wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents the case of a husband who annuls a {\i get} not in the presence of his wife or the {\i shaliach} carrying it. The Rabbis legislated that such annulment is forbidden ({\i lo yevatel}), in order to prevent a situation where the {\i shaliach} delivers the {\i get}, the wife remarries thinking she is divorced, and only later does it emerge that the {\i get} had been annulled beforehand \u8212- producing a situation of {\i mamzer} and chaos. Having enacted this prohibition, the question is what happens when the husband violates it and annuls anyway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Annulment Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a dispute. {\i Bitlo mevutal}, says {\i Rebbi} (Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi): even though he violated the rabbinic rule, the annulment takes effect. The {\i get} is void and the woman remains his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel disagrees: {\i eini yachol levatloh} \u8212- he cannot annul it; the annulment does not work. Nor can he add conditions to the {\i get}. His reasoning: {\i she\u8217'im kein mah koach beit din yafeh} \u8212- if we permit him to ignore the rabbinic rule and annul anyway, what force does the rabbinic enactment have? To protect the institution and the woman, the rabbis must back up their rule by refusing to recognize an annulment that was performed in violation of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sounds like a powerful case of {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}: {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} the annulment is effective, and the rabbis are declaring it void, which means a woman who is biblically still married (because the {\i get} is {\i batel}) is treated as divorced \u8212- a woman potentially free to remarry who is in fact an {\i eishet ish}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafka\u8217'as Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the inference. This is not {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah} in the general sense. Rather, it is a specific instance of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}: {\i man de\u8217'mekadesh ada\u8217'ata de\u8217'rabbanan mekadesh, afkinu rabbanan le\u8217'kiddushin minei}. Anyone who marries does so on the understanding ({\i ada\u8217'ata}) that the rabbis sanction the marriage, and if the rabbis retract that sanction, the marriage itself unravels retroactively. This is not a sweeping power to override Torah law; it is a targeted capacity within the domain of marriage, grounded in the understanding that {\i kiddushin} are always conditioned on rabbinic approval.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redefining Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises a further question. The mechanism of {\i hefker beit din hefker} can dissolve ownership of property \u8212- explaining why the {\i kohen gadol\u8217's} ring no longer belongs to him \u8212- but what about a marriage effected through {\i bi\u8217'ah} rather than money? If the {\i kiddushin} were performed through an act of intercourse, there is no property to retroactively un-own. The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer: {\i shavyuha rabbanan le\u8217'vi\u8217'aso bi\u8217'as znus} \u8212- the rabbis retroactively redefine the act of intercourse as {\i bi\u8217'as znus}, fornication, not {\i bi\u8217'as kiddushin}. It was never an act of marriage; therefore no marriage ever came into existence. This is not uprooting a marriage from the outside; it is retroactively redefining what the act was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fig Leaf Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rashba makes explicit a constraint that Rashi implies: {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} is not an unlimited power. The rabbis will perform it only when there is a fig leaf \u8212- some apparent mechanism that makes it look as though the marriage was legitimately dissolved. In the {\i get} case, that fig leaf is the existence of the {\i get} itself: even though {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} the {\i get} was annulled and therefore void, the rabbis can perform {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} behind the scenes while it {\i appears} that the {\i get} operated. In a case of pure recalcitrance, with no {\i get} at all, the fig leaf is absent, and {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} cannot be invoked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has significant contemporary resonance: some who advocate using {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} in cases of {\i get} refusal propose that a {\i get} be simultaneously tendered \u8212- precisely to create the fig leaf the Rashba requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot makes an important observation about the structure of the {\i gemara}\u8217's answer. The {\i gemara} says this case is resolved through {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}, not through {\i yesh koach la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}. But Tosafot argues that the two are not as separate as they appear: the power of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} itself is grounded in {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}. What makes {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} acceptable where a naked {\i yesh koach} would not be is that the presence of some apparent mechanism \u8212- the redefinition of the ring\u8217's ownership, the redefinition of the act of intercourse \u8212- makes it look as though no clear {\i akira d\u8217'Oraisa} is taking place. It is {\i yesh koach} operating through a fig leaf, and therefore not obviously a violation of Torah categories. The distinction, as Tosafot acknowledges, is subtle \u8212- perhaps uncomfortably so \u8212- but it is the line Tosafot draws between a permissible use of rabbinic power and a blatant uprooting of Torah law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maaseh Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's final set of cases involves the statement of Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov that {\i beit din} gives lashes and penalties not prescribed by Torah law \u8212- not in order to violate Torah, but {\i la\u8217'asot seyag la-Torah}, to build a protective fence. This is the idea of {\i migdar milta} in its most direct form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Horse Riding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i maaseh}: during the period of Greek dominance, a man was found riding a horse on {\i Shabbos} \u8212- a rabbinic violation, not a biblical one. He was brought before {\i beit din} and stoned. {\i Lo mipnei she-ra\u8217'uy lekach} \u8212- not because he legally deserved that punishment \u8212- {\i ela she-hasha\u8217'ah tzericha lekach} \u8212- but because the times demanded it. Assimilation and religious laxity were rampant, and a dramatic response was necessary to restore communal discipline.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i maaseh}: a man had relations with his wife under a fig tree in public view. He was brought before {\i beit din} and flogged \u8212- again, not because halakhic law prescribed that penalty for that act, but because the social circumstances ({\i hasha\u8217'ah tzericha lekach}) required a public demonstration that such behavior would not be tolerated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migdar Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's response is the same as before: {\i migdar milta shani}. These punishments are not evidence of a general structural power for {\i chachamim} to override Torah law. They are exigent-circumstance interventions, targeted responses to specific crises, not systematic changes to the {\i halachic} order. They belong to the category of {\i hora\u8217'at sha\u8217'ah}, not {\i yesh koach} as a permanent legal principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceded Uses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} ends without a decisive resolution on the broadest question of {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}, but it does establish certain clear conclusions. Two categories of rabbinic power over Torah law are definitively conceded: {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- the rabbis may issue prohibitions that result in a {\i mitzvas aseh d\u8217'Oraisa} going unperformed, without this constituting an active {\i akira} \u8212- and {\i migdar milta} \u8212- {\i beit din} may impose non-Torah punishments and take extraordinary measures in response to exigent circumstances, as a protective fence around communal observance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Tosafot, there is also a third category that falls short of outright {\i akira}: cases in which there is some apparent mechanism, some fig leaf, that makes the innovation look internally consistent with Torah categories rather than nakedly contrary to them. When such a mechanism is present, the rabbis may effectively uproot a Torah rule without it being characterized as {\i yesh koach} in the problematic sense. Both {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} and the leniency of {\i ed echad} in our {\i perek} are examples of this type, in Tosafot\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inconclusive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains unresolved is whether {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah} applies in a broader, undisguised sense \u8212- where there is no {\i shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh}, no {\i migdar milta}, and no fig leaf mechanism. The {\i gemara} cannot prove this, and it cannot definitively deny it. The {\i teku} is implicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rishonim} debate how to {\i pasken} on this question. Many cases are effectively conceded by all parties. The dispute turns on whether, in principle, the rabbis possess a structural power to uproot Torah law in a systematic rather than exigent way, and whether that power \u8212- if it exists \u8212- requires some covering mechanism or can operate openly. This {\i sugya} is among the most significant in all of {\i Shas} for understanding the scope and limits of rabbinic authority, and the answers one gives here have far-reaching implications for how to approach many of the most difficult problems in contemporary {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 92\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Remarriage & Fault\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Culpability Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf under discussion is 91b, and the broader sugya concerns the {\i kulan} in the {\i mishna} \u8212- the clause stating that if a woman remarries {\i shelo birshut}, without the authority of {\i beit din}, she may return to her first husband. The Gemara has been probing whether this leniency represents only the opinion of Rabbi Shimon, who holds generally that the child of such a union is not a {\i mamzer} and that she may not even need a {\i get} from the second man, or whether it is {\i divrei hakol} \u8212- a unanimous ruling. The practical stakes are high: Rav apparently ruled in practice according to this line, but if it reflects only Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view, serious questions arise about how we {\i pasken}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument for {\i divrei hakol} proceeds as follows: the Gemara understands {\i shelo birshut} to mean that the woman remarried on the basis of two witnesses \u8212- stronger evidence than the single witness ordinarily required \u8212- and therefore she is an {\i anusah}, entirely blameless. She did everything correctly; there was nothing more she could have done. How, then, could all the severe negative consequences of the {\i mishna} be imposed on her? The argument runs that everyone should agree: where there is zero fault, there are no consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now challenges precisely this assumption. Ulla raises a pointed objection: do we really reason that consequences apply only when a woman was culpable? The Gemara invokes a series of {\i mishnayot} from tractate Gittin, each of which carries the same full consequence formula \u8212- {\i teitzei mizeh umizeh, ve\u8217'havalad mamzer mizeh umizeh}, she must leave both the first and second husband, the children from either are {\i mamzerim}, she loses her {\i kesubah} and {\i mezonos}, and all the other penalties enumerated in our {\i mishna}. The question is whether in any of those cases the woman was in fact at fault.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gittin Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case from Gittin: the {\i get} was dated using an unrecognized or non-standard kingdom \u8212- {\i shem malchus she\u8217'einah hogenes} \u8212- such as the kingdom of the Medes or the Greeks, or it was dated by the years of the construction or destruction of the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, rather than by the conventional regnal dating. Or alternatively, the location written on the {\i get} was incorrect \u8212- the transaction took place in the east but the document read \u8220"the west,\u8221" or vice versa. Tosafos notes that the operative question is not precisely where the {\i sofer} sat but where the witnesses signed. In any event, none of these are obviously glaring defects, yet the {\i mishna} rules {\i teitzei mizeh umizeh} and all consequences follow if the woman relied on this {\i get} and remarried.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is immediate: what is this poor woman supposed to know about the technical details of {\i hilchos gittin}? Her husband handed her a {\i get} and she relied on it. The Gemara\u8217's answer is: {\i ibayei lah likro legittah} \u8212- she should have read the {\i get} and noticed the non-standard dating or the incorrect location. This is already a strained answer given that the vast majority of people were illiterate, and even a literate person would not necessarily identify a non-conventional date as technically disqualifying. But the Gemara nonetheless deploys this response.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum & Arayot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case, introduced by Rav Shimi bar Ashi: a man performed {\i yibum} with his sister-in-law, and thereupon the {\i tzarah}, the co-wife, considered herself free and went and remarried another man \u8212- which would seem entirely legitimate. Subsequently, however, it emerged that the woman with whom {\i yibum} had been performed was an {\i ailonit}, a woman incapable of childbearing, and therefore the act of {\i yibum} had no halachic validity. There is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with an {\i ailonit}, and thus the {\i tzarah} had in fact still required {\i chalitza} or a valid {\i yibum} before she could remarry. Yet she had gone ahead. The ruling: {\i teitzei mizeh umizeh}, all consequences apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is striking on two levels. First, as a matter of substantive {\i halacha}, we have spent the entirety of Masechet Yevamot learning that once one co-wife undergoes {\i yibum}, the others are free to remarry. Now we discover that if it retroactively emerges that the {\i yibum} was invalid \u8212- because the woman was an {\i ailonit} \u8212- the {\i tzarah} who had already remarried suffers all these dire consequences. Second, the Gemara\u8217's response to the question of fault is: {\i ibayei lah lamtonei} \u8212- she should have waited to see whether the co-wife would turn out to be an {\i ailonit}. How long should she have waited? Ten years? Twenty? The co-wife may already have been married for a decade. The answer seems almost absurd, yet the Gemara maintains it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case, brought by Abaye: all the {\i arayos} \u8212- relatives who exempt their co-wives from {\i yibum} \u8212- if those co-wives go and remarry and it subsequently emerges that the woman who was the {\i ervah} was herself an {\i ailonit}, then she was never truly a co-wife in the marriage, and her status as {\i ervah} cannot exempt the others. The {\i tzarot} who had remarried now face the same consequences. Again the Gemara asks what they should have done, and again the answer is: they should have waited to determine whether the {\i ervah} was an {\i ailonit}. The halachic shockwave here is the same: everything we have studied about the {\i ervah} exempting co-wives from {\i yibum} is now qualified by this latent uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scribe Mix-up\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next case, introduced by Rava: a {\i sofer} prepared two documents simultaneously \u8212- a {\i get} for the husband to give his wife, and a receipt ({\i shover}) for the wife to give the husband acknowledging payment of the {\i kesubah}. The {\i sofer} then mixed them up and handed each party the wrong document. The husband received what he believed was the receipt but was actually the {\i get}, and the wife received what she believed was the {\i get} but was actually the receipt. Each gave the other their respective document, so no valid {\i get} was ever transferred. Some time later, the husband produces the document he received and someone identifies it as a {\i get}; the wife produces hers and it turns out to be a receipt. The woman had in the meantime remarried, believing she possessed a valid {\i get}. The ruling: {\i teitzei mizeh umizeh} and all consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer: she should have read the {\i get} given to her and realized it was a receipt. The irony is manifest \u8212- the vast majority of people in that era were illiterate, and the demand that she read and identify the technical character of the document is in obvious tension with historical reality. Yet the Gemara asserts it was her responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bald Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final case in this series, introduced by Rav Ashi: a {\i get mekushar}, a folded-and-sewn {\i get} in which the witnesses sign on successive folds, had a {\i get kireiach} \u8212- a \u8220"bald {\i get}\u8221" \u8212- meaning one of the folds lacked a signature. The woman relied on this document, remarried, and then the defect was discovered. {\i Teitzei mizeh umizeh}, all consequences follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i get mekushar} functions as follows: the {\i get} is written, then folded, and a witness signs on the fold; the document is folded again and another witness signs on the next fold, and so on, with more witnesses than the standard two. The purpose, among other reasons, was to slow down the process of divorce for {\i kohanim}, requiring a more elaborate procedure so they might reconsider. A {\i get kireiach} has a fold missing its signature \u8212- a bald spot. The Gemara asks what this woman should have done, and answers: she should have read the {\i get} and noticed the missing signature. Of course, she did read the {\i get} and found it in order \u8212- the problem is whether she was obligated to know the technical {\i halacha} that every fold requires a signature. That is a very demanding standard, and yet the Gemara insists this was her responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natural Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara\u8217's argument crystallizes. We have a substantial body of evidence \u8212- multiple {\i mishnayot} in Gittin \u8212- demonstrating that all these consequences apply even in cases where the woman bears no genuine fault. The Gemara nominally provides answers in each case by identifying some theoretical negligence, but as has been noted, these answers are {\i dachuk} \u8212- strained. The natural reading of all these cases is that fault is not the operative criterion at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This points toward a fundamentally different understanding of why these consequences exist \u8212- an approach associated with Shammai Friedman\u8217's reading of the {\i mishna}. The consequences are not a {\i knas}, a penalty designed to incentivize more careful investigation. They are not punitive. Rather, they are the natural result of a particular halachic reality: in certain circumstances, a woman appears halachically as though she is married to two men simultaneously. Whether because witnesses testified she could remarry (as in our {\i mishna}), or because a {\i get} appeared valid but contained a technical defect (as in the Gittin cases), the legal situation creates what amounts to a dual-marriage reality. The consequences flow not from punishment but from that reality itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this is correct, then fault is simply irrelevant. The consequences arise whether or not the woman is blameworthy. And this has a direct implication for the present sugya: if fault is irrelevant, then the fact that a woman acted on two witnesses \u8212- and is therefore an {\i anusah} with no conceivable fault \u8212- does not change the analysis. She still faces all these consequences. The argument that the leniency of the {\i mishna} should be {\i divrei hakol} because she was blameless collapses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Witnesses Loophole\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pappa\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa sought to issue a practical ruling based on the principle of {\i mai hava lah leme\u8217'avad} \u8212- what could she have done? Specifically, he wished to hold that when a woman remarries on the basis of two witnesses and the husband subsequently reappears alive, she is free of consequences because she was entirely blameless. On his reading, the {\i mishna}\u8217's statement that she may return to her first husband when acting {\i shelo birshut} is {\i divrei hakol} precisely because with two witnesses there is absolutely no fault on her part, and therefore that specific leniency applies universally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua challenged Rav Pappa directly: what about all those {\i mishnayot} in Gittin? They demonstrate plainly that these consequences apply even when the woman is not at fault. Rav Pappa replied that we did in fact answer each of those cases \u8212- we identified ways in which, theoretically, the woman bore some responsibility. But Rav Huna pressed further: {\i veshinuyei leiku velismoch}? Are we really going to rely on those answers? The {\i pshat} of all those {\i mishnayot} points overwhelmingly in one direction \u8212- consequences apply regardless of fault. To rest a practical ruling on weak, forced {\i terutzim} that strain against the plain meaning of multiple {\i mishnayot} is not a sound halachic foundation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Witnesses Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's conclusion is therefore that even when a woman remarries on the basis of two witnesses \u8212- even when she is completely blameless, even when there was no need to invoke the special authority of {\i beit din} to credit weak testimony \u8212- if the husband subsequently appears alive, all the negative consequences apply. The statement in the {\i mishna} that she may return to her first husband when acting {\i shelo birshut} reflects only the position of Rabbi Shimon, not {\i divrei hakol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an unexpected and striking conclusion, particularly given that the entire sugya was framed around the weakness of {\i eid echad} and the role of {\i beit din} in authorizing remarriage on thin evidence. By the end, we learn that even with two witnesses and even in cases from Gittin that have nothing to do with testimony about a deceased husband, the same consequences apply. The logical corollary \u8212- which the earlier framing of the sugya had implied but which is now effectively withdrawn \u8212- was that these consequences existed to bolster the reliability of {\i eid echad} and incentivize careful investigation. That rationale has been dismantled. As has been noted, it is somewhat surprising that the {\i Rishonim} and {\i Acharonim} did not more explicitly revisit the opening framing of the {\i masechet} in light of where the sugya actually lands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumors Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi adds an important qualification: {\i ulekala lo chaishinan} \u8212- we are not concerned about mere rumors. The Gemara clarifies which rumor is at issue. If the rumor surfaces after the second marriage has already taken place, this is not a new principle \u8212- Rav Ashi himself already stated elsewhere that any {\i kala} that arises after {\i nisuin} is not acted upon. If you wish to disturb an existing marriage, you need concrete evidence; a rumor is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought that this case is different. Since the woman had been married to the first husband \u8212- that was the established prior status \u8212- and {\i beit din} had to exercise authority to permit her to remarry, perhaps this existing foundation means that a subsequent rumor of the husband\u8217's continued survival should be treated as a pre-marital rumor and create a problem for the second marriage. The Gemara rejects this: {\i kala} alone does not disturb the second marriage. Only concrete evidence \u8212- the husband\u8217's appearance or witness testimony that he was alive \u8212- triggers the consequences. Rumors do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical halachic conclusion that emerges from the entire sugya, as codified by the {\i Rif} and as reported by the {\i Rosh} in the name of all the {\i Geonim}, is that even when a woman remarries on the basis of two witnesses and is completely without fault, if the husband turns out to be alive, all the negative consequences of the {\i mishna} apply. The one exception \u8212- the sole narrowing of the {\i mishna}\u8217's severe reach \u8212- is that a bare rumor of the husband\u8217's survival does not trigger these consequences.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Din & Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues with a further distinction: if a woman remarries {\i al pi beit din} \u8212- on the authority of {\i beit din} \u8212- and the husband subsequently proves to have been alive, she does not bring a {\i korban}. If she remarried {\i shelo al pi beit din} \u8212- without the authority of {\i beit din}, which in the Gemara\u8217's reading means on the basis of two witnesses rather than on {\i beit din}\u8217's specific authorization \u8212- she does bring a {\i korban}. Here, paradoxically, the woman with better evidence (two witnesses) is subject to a stricter consequence in terms of the {\i korban}, because she was not acting under the special institutional authority of {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeiri\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Zeiri, however, challenges the {\i mishna}\u8217's entire premise. {\i Leisa lemasnisin} \u8212- we do not rule like our {\i mishna}. He invokes a teaching from the {\i beis medrash}: if {\i beit din} ruled that the sun had set \u8212- issuing their decision on a cloudy day, permitting people to perform {\i melacha} on what they believed to be {\i Motzaei Shabbos} \u8212- and it turned out the sun had not in fact set, that is not a {\i psak} in any meaningful sense. It is merely an error in fact. A {\i psak} is a mistaken legal judgment \u8212- an incorrect application of law to given facts. A mistake in fact, by contrast, is not a ruling at all; the facts simply were not what they appeared to be. The same applies here: {\i beit din}\u8217's permission for the woman to remarry was premised on the belief that her husband was dead. If he was alive, there was no {\i psak} \u8212- there was only a factual error. Since it was never a genuine {\i psak} of {\i beit din}, the woman cannot claim exemption from a {\i korban} on the grounds that she was following their ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman disagrees: it is a {\i hora\u8217'ah}, a genuine ruling. His proof is elegant. In virtually every area of {\i halacha}, {\i eid echad} is not credited as testimony. Yet here, {\i beit din} believes the single witness who says the husband is dead and permits the woman to remarry. What is the source of this authority? It must be that {\i beit din} is exercising its institutional power \u8212- its {\i hora\u8217'ah} \u8212- to credit that testimony. Crediting {\i eid echad} is not simply a passive assessment of the facts; it is an active halachic act requiring rabbinic authority. Therefore, whenever {\i beit din} permits remarriage on the basis of {\i eid echad}, this constitutes a genuine {\i psak}, and the woman acting under it is exempt from a {\i korban} if the husband later appears.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi understands this to mean that {\i beit din} is not merely assessing what happened \u8212- they are exercising judgment and authority in determining that {\i eid echad} is sufficient in this context. It is their authority, not just their assessment, that allows the woman to act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Fact Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava takes the opposing view: {\i teda detaus hi} \u8212- it is an error, not a {\i hora\u8217'ah}. His proof comes from the dynamics of sequential testimony. Suppose one witness says the husband died; {\i beit din} permits the woman to remarry. Then two witnesses say the husband was alive; {\i beit din} forbids her. Then another witness comes and says the husband subsequently died \u8212- and {\i beit din} permits her again. Why does this second single witness prevail over the earlier pair who testified he was alive? Because the second witness is not contradicting the first pair: he is saying that after they saw him alive, he then died. The most recent factual report governs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But consider the implication. If this were a question of legal judgment rather than fact, we would not simply defer to the most recent argument. When {\i beit din} reasons through a question of law, first permitting something, then finding a stronger reason to forbid it, then finding a weaker reason to permit it again \u8212- the stronger argument for the prohibition prevails; you do not simply adopt the last position because it came later. The fact that we do follow the most recent testimony here demonstrates that this is not a matter of legal judgment at all. It is a question of what actually happened \u8212- a purely factual question. And therefore, when {\i beit din} was wrong about the husband being dead, it was a factual error, not a mistaken {\i psak}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer similarly holds that it is an error and not a ruling, and accordingly that the woman must bring a {\i korban}. This emerges from the famous dictum attributed to him: {\i yikov hadin et hahar} \u8212- let the law pierce through the mountain. In this context, the saying means that the facts must govern, regardless of how {\i beit din} characterized the situation: the husband was alive, the woman committed what amounts to a transgression, and she must bring a {\i chatat shmeinah} \u8212- a substantial sin-offering. The use of this phrase signals that Rabbi Eliezer sees the matter as a straightforward question of legal fact, not a case involving institutional deference to a ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might suggest that Rabbi Eliezer holds a general principle that even acting under a {\i beit din psak} does not exempt one from a {\i korban}. The Gemara rejects this: if that were his general position, why phrase it as {\i yikov hadin et hahar}, as though this case demands special emphasis? The phrasing implies that something unique to this case requires emphasis \u8212- namely, that despite the involvement of {\i beit din}, she still brings a {\i korban} because it was never truly a {\i psak} to begin with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psak vs Fact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws out the logical structure: if it is an error ({\i taus}), then obviously she brings a {\i korban} \u8212- she was not operating under any genuine {\i psak}. But if it is a {\i hora\u8217'ah}, why does she bring a {\i korban}? Under normal principles, someone who acts in accordance with a {\i beit din} ruling is exempt. The Gemara entertains the possibility that Rabbi Eliezer simply holds that acting on {\i beit din}\u8217's authority never exempts an individual from a {\i korban}, but dismisses this in light of the {\i yikov hadin} phrasing, which signals a specific ruling in this case, not a general principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a broader irony here. The entire early portion of the sugya emphasized the extraordinary authority of {\i beit din} \u8212- {\i yesh koach b\u8217'yad chachamim}, {\i mishum agunah akilu bah rabbanan}, the whole framework of {\i beit din} wielding special institutional power to rely on {\i eid echad} and permit remarriage. Now, at the sugya\u8217's conclusion, multiple opinions converge in minimizing that framing: the permission was not a genuine {\i psak}, it was a factual assessment, and factual assessments can be wrong without attracting the legal protection afforded to authoritative rulings. This dovetails with the earlier conclusion that even two witnesses trigger all the same consequences \u8212- both developments, taken together, pull back from the strong institutional-authority reading that opened the {\i masechet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether acting under two witnesses triggers a {\i korban} is somewhat implicit. Since the {\i mishna} already states {\i lo niset al pi beit din chayevet b\u8217'korban} \u8212- and Rashi glosses this as meaning she acted on the basis of witnesses \u8212- it is clear that acting on two witnesses does require a {\i korban}. This invites reflection: if she is entirely blameless \u8212- an {\i anusah} who relied on two witnesses and could not have done more \u8212- why does she bring a {\i korban} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer illuminates the nature of the {\i korban} as an institution. A {\i korban} is not simply a punishment for culpable wrongdoing. Even a {\i tinok she\u8217'nishba} \u8212- a Jewish child raised entirely among non-Jews, with no knowledge of Torah, who desecrated {\i Shabbos} throughout his life without any fault whatsoever \u8212- must bring a {\i korban} for {\i chillul Shabbos} upon learning of his Jewish identity. The {\i korban} addresses a metaphysical rupture in the fabric of {\i halacha}, a transgression that occurred regardless of moral fault, and that rupture requires atonement. What exempts a person from a {\i korban} is not merely being blameless; it is when the act is legally not attributable to that person at all. When {\i beit din} issues an authoritative {\i psak} and a person acts on it, the act is in a meaningful sense {\i beit din}\u8217's act, not hers \u8212- the responsibility is shifted institutionally. That is why {\i psak beit din} exempts from a {\i korban}. But if what occurred was only a factual error, no such institutional transfer of responsibility took place, and the {\i korban} remains her obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Violating Permission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incorrect Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} introduces yet another case: {\i hora\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i beit din} gave her permission to remarry, but then she acted {\i shekilkela}, incorrectly. Even though she was operating under {\i beit din}\u8217's authority, if she exceeded the scope of what she was permitted to do, she must bring a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Znut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i kilkela} means she had sexual relations outside of marriage \u8212- {\i zenus}, relations with a man without {\i nisuin}, acting merely on the basis of {\i beit din}\u8217's determination of her {\i pnuyah} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan: Issur Lav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan holds that {\i kilkela} refers to a specific {\i issur lav} \u8212- she entered a marriage that carries a negative prohibition, such as an {\i almanah} marrying a {\i kohen gadol}, or a {\i gerushah} or {\i chalutzah} marrying a regular {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Znut Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara analyzes the dispute. According to Rabbi Yochanan, why does simple {\i zenus} not constitute acting outside {\i beit din}\u8217's authority? Because she can argue: {\i atun hu deshaviten pnuyah} \u8212- you yourselves declared me a {\i pnuyah}, an unmarried woman. By ruling that her husband is dead and she may remarry, {\i beit din} effectively established her status as an unattached woman. If she is a {\i pnuyah}, then having sexual relations outside of formal marriage, while not ideal, is consistent with the status they conferred upon her. She is not operating outside the scope of their permission \u8212- she is simply acting as a {\i pnuyah} acts. By contrast, if she enters a marriage that involves an {\i issur lav} \u8212- such as marrying a {\i kohen} despite being a {\i gerushah} \u8212- that specific marriage arrangement was never within the scope of {\i beit din}\u8217's permission. They permitted her to remarry in a halachically valid way; they did not authorize a prohibited union. There, she is acting outside their {\i psak} and must bring a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Korbanos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question: how many {\i korbanos} must she bring? Rabbi Eliezer holds {\i al kol biah u\u8217'viah} \u8212- a separate {\i korban} for each act of relations. The logic is that each act of sexual relations with the second man, while the first husband was alive, constitutes an independent act of adultery, each requiring its own atonement. Rabbi Yochanan holds that {\i chachamim} say {\i korban echad al kulam} \u8212- one {\i korban} covers all the acts, on the principle that multiple unknowing transgressions of the same kind constitute a single continuous {\i shogeg} requiring one {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} concede to Rabbi Eliezer, however, that if she married five different men sequentially and committed adultery with each, she brings a separate {\i korban} for each man. Relations with a different man constitutes a categorically different transgressive act, and each one requires its own {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Death Sequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Then Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to a series of cases involving reversed testimony about the sequence of deaths. A woman\u8217's husband and son both traveled to a distant country. Witnesses came and reported: your husband died, and then afterward your son died. Since the husband predeceased the son, the woman at the moment of the husband\u8217's death had a living son, and therefore fell into neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitza} \u8212- she was free to remarry without any levirate obligation, as the inheritance line was secured through her son. The son\u8217's subsequent death did not retroactively create a {\i yibum} obligation, since at the time she became a widow there was no occasion for {\i yibum}. She therefore remarried.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Afterward came testimony reversing the sequence: actually, the son died first and then the husband. This means that at the moment of the husband\u8217's death she had no living son, and the {\i yibum} obligation had attached. She had remarried another man while she was still bound by the levirate obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling: {\i teitzei} \u8212- she must leave the second husband \u8212- and {\i havalad rishon ve\u8217'acharon mamzer}. The children, the first and the last, are {\i mamzerim}. The phrasing \u8220"first and last\u8221" requires explanation (addressed below), but the substance is that all her children from the second union carry {\i mamzer} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a question: she married the second man while she still required {\i chalitza} \u8212- that is an {\i issur lav}, not an {\i issur kares}. Why would the children be {\i mamzerim}? The classification of {\i mamzer} status for a violation of an {\i issur lav} is the position of Rabbi Akiva, as the Gemara will establish. The stricter {\i halacha} here reflects his view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son Then Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: witnesses report first that the son died, then that the husband died. The woman, obligated in {\i yibum}, performs the levirate marriage with the brother-in-law. Subsequently, testimony reverses the sequence: the husband actually died before the son, meaning the woman was never obligated in {\i yibum}. She has now engaged in relations with her brother-in-law outside of any valid {\i yibum} context \u8212- an {\i issur arayos} carrying the severity of {\i issur kares}. The ruling: {\i teitzei}, and {\i havalad rishon ve\u8217'acharon mamzer}, all children are {\i mamzerim}. The severity here is clear \u8212- the violation involves {\i kares}-level transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is structurally similar to the core case of our first {\i mishna}: witnesses say the husband died, the woman remarries, and then subsequent testimony establishes that actually the husband was alive at the time of the second marriage \u8212- he has only now died. This case introduces the critical distinction between the \u8220"first\u8221" and \u8220"last\u8221" children that motivated the {\i mishna}\u8217's unusual phrasing. {\i Teitzei}, and: {\i havalad rishon mamzer ve\u8217'acharon eino mamzer} \u8212- the first children are {\i mamzerim}, the last are not. Children born while the first husband was still alive \u8212- children of the second union during that period \u8212- are {\i mamzerim}. But children born after the testimony that the first husband is now truly dead are not {\i mamzerim}, because at that point the second union is no longer adulterous \u8212- the first husband has actually died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos notes that in the original {\i mishna}, where the Talmud states that children from both the {\i rishon} and {\i acharon} are {\i mamzerim}, this could mean \u8212- even according to Tosafos\u8217's reading \u8212- that even if the first husband gives a {\i get} and the woman remains with the second husband, we impose a Rabbinic {\i knas} declaring those children {\i mamzerim}. But in this third case, Tosafos implies no such Rabbinic {\i knas} applies, and the ruling is purely at the {\i de\u8217'oraisa} level: once the husband is actually dead, children of the second union are straightforwardly not {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth case narrows the scenario further: witnesses reported the husband\u8217's death, and the woman accepted {\i kiddushin} from a second man \u8212- but had not yet proceeded to {\i nisuin} and marital cohabitation. Then the first husband returned alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Get Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In this case, the analysis changes substantially. The woman may return to her first husband, and notably, even if the second man gave her a {\i get} as a precaution, that {\i get} does not affect her status \u8212- she is not rendered a {\i gerushah}, and were her first husband now to die, she could marry a {\i kohen}. The principle behind this emerges from the {\i drashah} of Rabbi Eliezer ben Masya: {\i ishah gerushah me\u8217'ishah} \u8212- a woman is a {\i gerushah} only from her husband, not from a man who is not her husband. The {\i get} of a man who was never validly her husband is halachically inoperative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is this case so different? The key is that {\i nisuin} \u8212- actual marital cohabitation \u8212- had not occurred. All the severe consequences in the earlier cases derive from the reality that the woman appears as if she is actually living with two men. Where she accepted {\i kiddushin} only and never began living with the second man, that factual reality of apparent dual-marriage has not come into being. The transgression has not concretized in the same way, and therefore the natural consequences of being in that situation do not attach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First & Last Kids\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's use of the terms {\i rishon} and {\i acharon} \u8212- first and last \u8212- requires explanation. On its face, one might simply expect the {\i mishna} to say \u8220"the children are {\i mamzerim}\u8221" in each case. Why specify first and last? The answer is that the {\i mishna} adopts this formulation for the sake of the third case, where the distinction is halachically operative: children born before the second pair of witnesses testified that the first husband is now dead are {\i mamzerim}, while children born afterward are not. Because that distinction needs to be expressed, the {\i mishna} uses the \u8220"first and last\u8221" formulation throughout \u8212- even in the cases where all children have the same status \u8212- in order to frame the language uniformly across all cases and allow the third case to express itself naturally within the same grammatical structure.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzer by Lav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Baraita} clarifies: the ruling that children of a woman who remarried while still obligated in {\i yibum} \u8212- an {\i issur lav} \u8212- are {\i mamzerim} reflects the position of Rabbi Akiva. Rabbi Akiva holds that {\i kiddushin} do not take effect in a relationship that carries an {\i issur lav}: {\i ein kiddushin tofsin be\u8217'chayavei lavin}. When {\i kiddushin} have no validity, the union has no marital standing, and children born from it are {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Disagree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} disagree: {\i ein mamzer miyevamah} \u8212- there is no {\i mamzer} from a woman who required {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} and married another man instead. For the {\i chachamim}, the threshold for {\i mamzer} status requires an {\i issur kares}, not merely an {\i issur lav}. A relationship that would have required no more than {\i chalitza} to resolve does not produce {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relationship Lavin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara sharpens Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position further. One might ask: why does the {\i Baraita} specifically say there is no {\i mamzer miyevamah} rather than making the broader statement that there is no {\i mamzer} from {\i chayavei lavin} in general? This suggests that even within Rabbi Akiva\u8217's framework, there is a distinction. According to this {\i tanna}, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's rule that {\i mamzer} results from {\i issur lav} applies only to {\i lavin deshe\u8217'er} \u8212- prohibitions grounded in a family or quasi-family relationship ({\i she\u8217'er}). The examples Rashi adduces are: a woman with whom one\u8217's father had relations (but never married), whom some hold is forbidden as a {\i lav}; and a woman to whom one personally performed {\i chalitza}, who is thereafter forbidden to that man by a {\i lav} \u8212- since she was his brother\u8217's wife and thereby entered a relational orbit. These cases involve {\i she\u8217'er}, a dimension of kinship or quasi-kinship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, a simple {\i lav} with no such relational dimension \u8212- such as a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i gerushah}, or the case of a woman requiring {\i chalitza} who instead marries a stranger \u8212- is {\i lav greida}, a bare prohibition unconnected to family relation. Rabbi Akiva, according to this {\i tanna}, would not produce {\i mamzer} status even from such a {\i lav}. The ruling in our {\i mishna} that children are {\i mamzerim} in the {\i yibum} case thus represents the most stringent application of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view \u8212- the view that even a bare {\i issur lav} can generate {\i mamzer} status \u8212- which is only one position within the range of opinions attributed to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's school.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 92b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Yevamah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: No Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} with which we concluded the previous {\i daf} addresses the case of a woman whose husband travels abroad, contradictory testimony is received regarding the order of deaths \u8212- first that the husband died and then the sons, leading to remarriage, and subsequently it emerged that the reverse was true. The woman thus turned out to have been a {\i shomeret yavam} who married another man, and the {\i mishnah} declares the child a {\i mamzer}. As was established, this ruling follows Rabbi Akiva, who holds that a child born of a union in violation of an {\i issur lav} is a {\i mamzer}, and indeed the status of {\i shomeret yavam} involves such a prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now opens a new discussion, citing the teaching of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav. Rav asks: {\i minayin she\u8217'ein kiddushin tofsin b\u8217'yevamah} \u8212- from where do we know that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect at all with a {\i yevamah}? This question is significant because it steps back from the particular question of {\i mamzerut}, which may be limited to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position, and addresses the more fundamental question of whether {\i kiddushin} with a {\i yevamah} is legally effective. Rav argues, and the {\i rishonim} make this clear, that even those who would not follow Rabbi Akiva with respect to {\i mamzerut} from {\i chayavei lavin} would agree that {\i kiddushin} simply does not take hold with a woman who is {\i zekukah l\u8217'yibum}. The logic runs as follows: the relationship between the {\i yevamah} and the {\i yavam} is not merely a prohibition standing in the way of her marrying another; rather, the {\i zikah} \u8212- the ongoing quasi-marital bond to her deceased husband that transfers to the {\i yavam} \u8212- means she is already in some sense bound to the {\i yavam}. She cannot become bound to another man while that {\i zikah} remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i drashah} is from the verse {\i lo tihiyeh eishet ha-mes ha-chutzah l\u8217'ish zar} \u8212- the wife of the dead shall not be to the outside to a foreign man. Rav reads this not merely as a prohibition but as a statement that the {\i havayah}, the legal bond of marriage, will not take hold. The word {\i tihiyeh} is thus read as referring to the validity of the marriage itself: she cannot become legally bound to a stranger.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, however, responds {\i ba\u8217'aniyoseinu tzrichah get} \u8212- in our humility, she requires a {\i get}. The {\i gemara} reads this as an expression of doubt: {\i mesapkah lei l\u8217'Shmuel} \u8212- Shmuel is uncertain whether the verse is only establishing a prohibition, in which case the {\i kiddushin} might nonetheless take effect, or whether it is establishing that {\i kiddushin} will not take effect at all, as Rav maintains. Because of this doubt, Shmuel requires her to receive a {\i get} at least as a precaution, {\i mi-safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mari bar Rachel reports to Rav Ashi that Amemar rules the {\i halacha} follows Shmuel \u8212- she requires a {\i get}, at least as a matter of doubt. Rav Ashi then draws a practical distinction based on whether the {\i yavam} is a {\i kohen} or a {\i Yisrael}. If the {\i yavam} is a {\i kohen} and she subsequently accepted {\i kiddushin} from another man, then once that second man gives her a {\i get}, she becomes a {\i gerushah} and the {\i kohen} will no longer be permitted to perform {\i yibum}. Therefore, since {\i yibum} is in any event going to be impossible and only {\i chalitzah} can resolve the bond, Rav Ashi suggests: let the {\i yavam} perform {\i chalitzah} first, and she may then remain with the man she married. As the {\i gemara} puts it: {\i choletz lah v\u8217'sharya lei} \u8212- he performs {\i chalitzah} for her and she is permitted to the one she\u8217's with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sinner Rewarded Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i im kein matzinu choteh niskar} \u8212- if so, we find that the sinner is rewarded. The man who married the {\i yevamah} when he had no right to do so would benefit from his transgression, since it now forces the {\i yavam}\u8217's hand toward {\i chalitzah} \u8212- an outcome that might have been different had the {\i yavam} wished to perform {\i yibum}. The {\i gemara} therefore rejects the scenario of {\i chalitzah} first in the case of the {\i kohen} brother-in-law, lest the wrongdoer profit from his act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of a {\i Yisrael} brother-in-law, however, the rule is: {\i nosei la-sheni get v\u8217'hutra lo} \u8212- the second man gives a {\i get} and she becomes permitted to her {\i yavam} for {\i yibum}. The {\i chiddush} here, and the reason a lengthy {\i Tosafos} engages with it, lies in how different this resolution is from the treatment of the woman whose husband returned after being reported dead. In that earlier {\i mishnah}, there were sweeping negative consequences \u8212- she was forbidden to both men. Here, the {\i gemara} allows the situation to resolve itself cleanly: the second man gives a {\i get}, and she goes to her {\i yavam} for {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Kiddushin/Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos raises a significant qualification: this clean resolution may be available only because the couple had completed no more than {\i kiddushin} with each other, not {\i nisuin}. Tosafos draws the parallel to the case of the husband reported dead \u8212- where {\i kiddushin} without {\i nisuin} to the second man allowed her to return to her original husband without the broader negative consequences. Similarly, in this case, where only {\i kiddushin} was contracted, we can allow her to get a {\i get} and proceed to {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} without imposing all the severe consequences familiar from the beginning of the {\i perek}. But if {\i nisuin} had been completed, Tosafos suggests the more severe consequences might apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Tosafos text in {\i nosei la-sheni get} clarifies this with a ruling of Rabbeinu Tam in an actual case: a woman who was unaware that she had a {\i yavam} and was subsequently {\i mekudeshes} to another man. Rabbeinu Tam ruled that if the {\i yavam} was in any event inclined to perform {\i chalitzah} \u8212- meaning the second man had not forced his hand \u8212- then the {\i yavam} may perform {\i chalitzah} first, and she is permitted to remain with the man to whom she had been {\i mekudeshes}. The operative distinction is: we do not allow the second man to benefit from having compelled {\i chalitzah}. But if {\i chalitzah} would have been the {\i yavam}\u8217's choice regardless, then the sequence of {\i chalitzah} followed by remaining with the second man is permitted \u8212- and, crucially, this applies only where the couple had {\i kiddushin} and not {\i nisuin}. Rashi, by contrast, appears to permit it even after {\i nisuin}, and the question of whether {\i nisuin} would trigger the full array of consequences known from the opening {\i mishnah} remains unresolved between these two views.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nisuin Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Gidel Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now cites two parallel traditions, both transmitted in the name of Rav. Rav Gidel says in the name of Rav, and Rav Chiya bar Yosef says in the name of Rav: {\i yevamah kiddushin ein bah, nisuin yesh bah} \u8212- with respect to a {\i yevamah}, {\i kiddushin} has no effect, but there is {\i nisuin}. The {\i gemara} immediately registers the problem: if {\i kiddushin} does not take effect, how can {\i nisuin} take effect? {\i Ei kiddushin ein bah, nisuin nami ein bah} \u8212- if {\i kiddushin} has no validity, {\i nisuin} likewise has no validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore offers multiple interpretations of what {\i nisuin yesh bah} means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first possibility, which Tosafos develops: {\i nisuin ein bah} \u8212- even in the interpretation that says there is no {\i nisuin} \u8212- might actually be the intended teaching, meaning that even if the couple proceeds through {\i nisuin}, we do not impose the negative consequences. There is no {\i tetzei mi-zeh u-mi-zeh}, no {\i mamzerut}, no stripping away of the {\i ketubah}. The statement {\i nisuin ein bah} would then be a statement of legal leniency: even {\i nisuin} does not trigger those severe outcomes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possibility ({\i ve\u8217'ee ba\u8217'is teima}): {\i nisuin yesh bah} in the sense of {\i biznus} \u8212- following Rav Hamnuna, who taught that a {\i shomeret yavam} who engaged in sexual relations with another man is {\i asura} to her {\i yavam}. The act of {\i nisuin} is significant not as a valid marriage but as a sexual act that renders her forbidden to the {\i yavam}, precluding {\i yibum} and leaving only {\i chalitzah} as the means of release. There is an {\i issur lav}, but {\i yibum} is now foreclosed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third possibility ({\i ve\u8217'ee ba\u8217'is teima le\u8217'olam teima me\u8217'ikara}): {\i nisuin yesh bah} in the sense that it creates an appearance sufficiently resembling a real marriage to require a {\i get}. The analogy offered is to a woman whose husband has gone {\i limedinat ha-yam} and who then begins living with another man after a report of the husband\u8217's death. Even if the marriage is not legally valid, it looks like a marriage, and therefore consequences attach to it. Here too, the {\i yavam}-bond is not the most obvious social reality \u8212- many would not even know that {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect with a {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- and once she goes through with {\i nisuin}, it appears to onlookers as though she is married to the second man. That appearance is significant enough that she requires a {\i get}. This interpretation ties back to the analysis offered earlier in the {\i masechta}: some of the consequences in the opening {\i mishnah} stem not from punitive {\i knassos} but from the legal reality that the second marriage looks genuine and must be unwound as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yannai Vote\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Amar Rabbi Yannai: {\i bi-chavura nimnu ve-gamru} \u8212- in the rabbinic assembly they voted and concluded: {\i ein kiddushin tofsin b\u8217'yevamah} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} does not take effect with a {\i yevamah}. This is against Shmuel\u8217's position, which had left the matter in doubt. The {\i gemara} thus records a formal consensus rejecting the possibility that {\i kiddushin} takes hold. Whether a rabbinically-imposed requirement of a {\i get} remains applicable after {\i nisuin} is not resolved by this vote, but the fundamental legal nullity of {\i kiddushin} with a {\i yevamah} is here stated as the accepted position. It is striking that this ruling emerges so far into {\i Masechet Yevamot} \u8212- a masechta entirely devoted to {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} \u8212- and provides what is, in retrospect, one of the most foundational statements about the nature of {\i zikah}: the bond is strong enough to preclude any other man\u8217's {\i kiddushin} from taking effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Transactions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Responding to Rabbi Yannai\u8217's report of the vote, Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i lo mishnatenu hi zu} \u8212- is this not simply our {\i mishnah}? We need not rely on a rabbinic vote; the matter is already stated explicitly in a {\i mishnah}. Which {\i mishnah}? {\i Ha\u8217'omer le\u8217'isha harei at mekudeshet li le\u8217'achar she\u8217'etgayer} \u8212- a man says to a woman: you are betrothed to me after I convert, or after you convert, or after I am freed from slavery, or after you are freed, or after your husband dies, or after my wife your sister dies, or \u8212- {\i o le\u8217'achar she-yachalotz yevamayich} \u8212- after your {\i yavam} performs {\i chalitzah}: in all these cases, {\i einah mekudeshet} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why should the case of {\i chalitzah} be different from the others? In all the other cases, the man or woman is currently ineligible for {\i kiddushin} \u8212- one is not Jewish, one is enslaved, one\u8217's husband is alive, one\u8217's sister is alive. But the case of the {\i yevamah} presents a different profile: it is merely an {\i issur lav} that stands between her and another man. The fact that the {\i mishnah} places it in the same category as the others proves that {\i kiddushin} with a {\i yevamah} does not take effect \u8212- and this is the teaching of the {\i mishnah} itself, not merely a rabbinic vote.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish responds to Rabbi Yochanan: had this great man \u8212- Rabbi Yannai \u8212- not praised him for finding this proof, Reish Lakish would have argued that there is in fact no proof from that {\i mishnah} at all. For that {\i mishnah} may reflect only the position of Rabbi Akiva: {\i matnitin Rabbi Akiva hi, de\u8217'amar ein kiddushin tofsin be-chayavei lavin} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva holds that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect with those who are subject to a {\i lav}, and therefore the {\i mishnah} disqualifies {\i kiddushin} with a {\i yevamah}. But this does not establish the consensus view; it may simply be one expression of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's broader ruling about {\i chayavei lavin}, and not a universally agreed-upon position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} however rejects the reading that the {\i mishnah} reflects Rabbi Akiva. For if this were Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i mishnah}, the {\i kiddushin} should actually work \u8212- at least in the future, once {\i chalitzah} has been performed. This follows from the fact that Rabbi Akiva holds {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} \u8212- a person may transact with something not yet in existence, and the transaction takes effect when the necessary conditions are eventually met. If Rabbi Akiva were the {\i tanna} of that {\i mishnah}, then a man who says {\i harei at mekudeshet li le\u8217'achar she-yachalotz yevamayich} should be establishing a conditional {\i kiddushin} that takes effect once {\i chalitzah} is performed. Since the {\i mishnah} says it does not work, it cannot be expressing Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view. Therefore, the {\i mishnah} reflects the consensus, and the consensus is that {\i kiddushin} with a {\i yevamah} never takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This introduces the broader topic of {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam}, to which the {\i gemara} now devotes sustained attention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konamic Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To establish that Rabbi Akiva indeed holds {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam}, the {\i gemara} cites a {\i mishnah}: {\i konam she\u8217'ani osa leficha} \u8212- a woman says to her husband that the labor she will produce should be {\i assur} to his mouth, consecrated in the manner of a {\i korban}. Ordinarily a husband is entitled to his wife\u8217's earnings; she is not the owner of that future labor and cannot render it forbidden by a {\i neder}. The {\i tanna kamma} rules {\i lo tzarich lehafer} \u8212- the husband need not annul it, because she has no right to what she produces. But Rabbi Akiva says {\i yafer} \u8212- he must annul it, {\i shema ta\u8217'adif alav yoter min ha-ra\u8217'uy lo} \u8212- lest she produce in excess of what he is legally entitled to, in which case the surplus belongs to her and the {\i neder} on that portion would be valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, the underlying question: how can a {\i neder} work on something that does not yet exist? A {\i neder} is not an oath of future behavior ({\i shevua}); it changes the legal status of an object. How can she alter the status of labor that has not yet been produced? The answer is that Rabbi Akiva permits this \u8212- and this is the expression of his position that {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Hands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua qualifies the proof: the {\i mishnah} need not depend on the principle of {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} at all. The woman is not making a {\i neder} on the non-existent labor; she is making a {\i neder} in the formulation of {\i yikdeshu yadai la\u8217'oseihen} \u8212- my hands are consecrated to their Maker. She is sanctifying her hands themselves, and her hands do exist. Just as one may sanctify a tree for the fruit it will produce, she sanctifies her hands for the labor they will generate. {\i Ve\u8217'yadayim isnhu ba\u8217'olam} \u8212- the hands do exist in the world. Therefore, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling in that case does not necessarily prove that he accepts {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} in general.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretation creates a tension with Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak\u8217's subsequent claim, discussed in the following section, that all the authorities in a long chain hold of {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} \u8212- a claim that includes Rav Huna. The {\i gemara} therefore flags: {\i u-fliga d\u8217'Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak} \u8212- this reading by Rav Huna (that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's case is explicable without recourse to {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam}) conflicts with Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak\u8217's position, which assumes that Rabbi Akiva indeed affirms {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} and places him at the head of a chain of authorities who all share that view.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaim Chain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Lineage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak presents a remarkable chain of tradition: {\i Rav Huna ke-Rav, ve-Rav ke-Rabbi Yannai, ve-Rabbi Yannai ke-Rabbi Hiyya, ve-Rabbi Hiyya ke-Rebbe, ve-Rebbe ke-Rabbi Meir, ve-Rabbi Meir ke-Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov, ve-Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov ke-Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- each figure in order of {\i rav} and {\i talmid}, from {\i Amoraim} back through {\i Tannaim}, all sharing the same fundamental position: {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam}, that one may transact on something not yet in existence, with the transaction taking effect when the necessary conditions are fulfilled. It is worth noting that Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak is not claiming that every case in this chain is literally identical; rather, they are related positions grouped under this broad principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is Rav Huna\u8217's case? The {\i gemara} records a dispute regarding one who sells the fruit of his palm tree to another before those fruit have come into existence. Rav Huna holds: {\i ad shelo ba la\u8217'olam yachol lachzor bo} \u8212- until the fruit comes into existence, either party may retract. But {\i misheba la\u8217'olam eino yachol lachzor bo} \u8212- once the fruit exists, neither party may retract; the transaction takes effect at that moment. Rav Nachman disagrees, holding that even once the fruit exists, the seller may retract, since the transaction never took legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman adds a concession: {\i modina d\u8217'ee shemit ve\u8217'achil lo mafkinan minei} \u8212- if the purchaser actually seized and consumed the fruit, we do not force him to pay it back. The basis for this concession is not entirely clear \u8212- it may reflect a principle that we do not disturb an already-consummated state of affairs even if the underlying transaction was legally invalid, or it may reflect some residual social enforceability of the agreement. In any event, the clear expression of Rav Huna\u8217's view is that {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} and the transaction takes effect when the object comes into the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Field Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's position is transmitted by Rav Huna in Rav\u8217's name \u8212- itself an illustration of the chain \u8212- and concerns a more complex case: {\i ha\u8217'omer le-chavero sadeh zo she\u8217'ani lokeach, lich\u8217'she\u8217'ekachenah knuyah lach me\u8217'achshav} \u8212- one who says to his fellow: \u8220"This field that I intend to purchase, when I purchase it, it shall be yours as of now.\u8221" Rav rules {\i kana} \u8212- it works. When the seller subsequently acquires the field, it automatically transfers to the purchaser, even though at the time of the sale the seller did not own it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here several layers of complexity are present. This is not merely a case of an object that does not yet exist in the world; the object \u8212- the field \u8212- does exist. The seller simply does not own it. The {\i gemara} is placing these cases together under the umbrella of related positions, even while recognizing that they are not identical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'achshav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i me\u8217'achshav} \u8212- \u8220"as of now\u8221" \u8212- in the formulation {\i lich\u8217'she\u8217'ekachenah knuyah lach me\u8217'achshav} is given its own analysis. Tosafos explains that {\i me\u8217'achshav} serves a specific legal function: it ensures that the {\i maaseh kinyan} \u8212- the act of acquisition performed at the time of the sale \u8212- retains its legal force even if, by the time the seller actually acquires the field, the physical instrument of that {\i kinyan} (such as a {\i shtar}) has been destroyed. Ordinarily, one might argue that some remnant of the {\i maaseh kinyan} must still be present when the object comes into the buyer\u8217's control. If I gave you money and the field is acquired on Friday, you still hold the money (or owe it back), so the {\i kinyan} retains its foothold. But if I gave you a {\i shtar} and it was subsequently torn up, there may be nothing left of the act when the field is actually acquired. The word {\i me\u8217'achshav} \u8212- \u8220"as of now\u8221" \u8212- lends sufficient legal weight to the original act that the transaction takes effect upon acquisition even without a surviving instrument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, the transfer of title to the buyer occurs when the seller acquires the field, not retroactively at the time of the sale. The buyer does not own the field earlier than Friday and does not benefit from any fruit produced before that point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe the proximity of {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} to {\i breira} \u8212- the principle that a current act may be clarified retroactively. These are distinct concepts. {\i Breira} operates retroactively once facts clarify themselves. {\i Adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} is not retroactive at all: the transaction does not take effect earlier than the moment when the conditions are met. Until {\i chalitzah} is performed, the {\i kiddushin} has no effect; once {\i chalitzah} is performed, it takes effect at that future time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yannai Sharecrop\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yannai\u8217's case follows: {\i de-Rabbi Yannai hava lei arisa de-hava maytei lei kinta de-peirei kol ma\u8217'alei shabta} \u8212- Rabbi Yannai had a sharecropper who would bring him a basket of fruit every {\i erev Shabbos}. On one occasion the sharecropper was delayed and {\i Shabbos} was approaching. Rabbi Yannai wished to eat the fruit on {\i Shabbos} but could not separate {\i tithes} on {\i Shabbos} itself, and the fruit had not yet arrived. He therefore took {\i ma\u8217'aser} from fruit in his own house {\i on behalf of} the fruit that would arrive \u8212- fruit not yet in his possession. This is parallel to {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam}: he is designating {\i ma\u8217'aser} for produce that is not yet in his control and perhaps not even legally his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Hiyya Dream\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yannai was uncertain whether his action was valid and went to ask Rabbi Hiyya. Rabbi Hiyya said to him: {\i shapir avadt} \u8212- you did well. The {\i gemara} cites a {\i braita}: the verse {\i lema\u8217'an tilmad leyira es Hashem Elokecha kol ha-yamim} \u8212- \u8220"in order that you shall learn to fear Hashem your God all the days\u8221" \u8212- teaches that even on {\i Shabbatot} and {\i Yamim Tovim} one takes {\i ma\u8217'aser}. The question is what practical ruling this verse comes to allow on {\i Shabbos}, since separating {\i ma\u8217'aser} is in any case only a rabbinic prohibition and a scriptural verse would not be needed to override a rabbinic ruling. The {\i gemara} concludes that the verse comes to validate a case precisely like Rabbi Yannai\u8217's: one who takes {\i ma\u8217'aser she-lo min ha-mukaf} \u8212- not with all the produce physically present in one location \u8212- because circumstances compelled it. Despite the technical deficiency that the designated {\i ma\u8217'aser} and the produce it covers were not in the same place, the designation is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yannai then tells Rabbi Hiyya: but in a dream they showed me the verse {\i kaneh ratzutz} \u8212- a weak reed \u8212- suggesting that what I did was unreliable, like resting one\u8217's weight on a broken reed ({\i hinei vatachta lecha al mish\u8217'enet ha-kaneh ha-ratzutz ha-zeh}). Rabbi Hiyya responds: they were citing you a different verse with the same phrase \u8212- {\i kaneh ratzutz lo yishbor, u-fishtah kehah lo yichebenah} \u8212- a bent reed shall not be broken, and a dimly burning wick shall not be extinguished. In other words, the heavenly message was one of reassurance, not rebuke: something that appears fragile and questionable is in fact sound and will not break.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This little narrative carries a significant implicit message about {\i halachic} methodology. The image of the flexible reed that does not snap \u8212- as opposed to something rigid and brittle \u8212- points toward a posture of finding valid grounds for leniency rather than mechanically rejecting what is imperfect. One is reminded of the well-known principle of {\i hanach lahem le-Yisrael im einam nevi\u8217'im hem b\u8217'nei nevi\u8217'im hem} \u8212- if they are not prophets, they are the children of prophets \u8212- where the question among later {\i acharonim} is whether a certain {\i rishon} was invoking the principle of {\i mutav sheyihyu shogegim} (tolerated inadvertent transgression) or the principle that the Jewish people intuited correctly. The {\i kaneh ratzutz} image here functions similarly: it can be read in either direction, and the proper reading depends on where the verse ends.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe Slave Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbe\u8217's position is derived from a {\i braita} interpreting the verse {\i lo tasgir eved el adonav} \u8212- do not hand over a slave to his master. Rebbe reads this as referring to one who purchases a slave from another master on the condition of freeing him. Rebbe\u8217's reading implies that the act of manumission \u8212- the {\i shichrur} \u8212- takes effect at the moment of purchase. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak specifies the mechanism: {\i de-chtav lei lich\u8217'she\u8217'ekachecha harei atzmecha kanuy lach me\u8217'achshav} \u8212- the purchaser wrote a writ of manumission stating \u8220"when I purchase you, you own yourself as of now.\u8221" Here again the {\i me\u8217'achshav} formulation is used, and the act of freedom takes effect upon acquisition even though the document was written when the seller had no ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Conditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is drawn from the {\i mishnah} already cited above: {\i ha\u8217'omer le\u8217'isha harei at mekudeshet li le\u8217'achar she\u8217'agayer} etc. \u8212- where he rules {\i mekudeshet}, in contrast to the {\i tanna kamma} who rules {\i einah mekudeshet}. Rabbi Meir holds that where the condition ({\i le\u8217'achar she-yachalotz yevamayich}, for instance) is later fulfilled, the {\i kiddushin} that was performed before those conditions were satisfied retroactively or prospectively takes effect. This is the direct application of {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} to {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov is cited from a {\i braita}: {\i afilu amar peirot arugah zu tlushin yihyu terumah al peirot arugah mechubarim} \u8212- even if one says that the picked fruit of this row shall be {\i terumah} on the attached fruit of the row, or conversely that the attached fruit shall be {\i terumah} on the picked fruit \u8212- to take effect when the attached fruit has ripened to a third and been picked \u8212- {\i devarav kayamim}, his words stand. One performs an act of designation for produce that does not yet meet the conditions required for {\i terumah}, and when those conditions are met, the designation takes effect. This is another variant of the broad principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Earnings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's case returns us to the {\i mishnah} about {\i konam she\u8217'ani osa leficha} \u8212- the woman who makes her future earnings {\i assur} to her husband. Rabbi Akiva holds {\i yafer} \u8212- the husband must annul the {\i neder} \u8212- because she may produce in excess of what belongs to him, and a {\i neder} on non-existent future labor is valid. This is the {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} in the domain of {\i nedarim}. (Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua\u8217's interpretation \u8212- that she is actually consecrating her existing hands and not the non-existent fruit \u8212- was flagged earlier as creating tension with this chain, since Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak explicitly includes Rav Huna in the list of those who affirm {\i adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam}.)\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One Witness Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Believe Ed Echad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens a new inquiry: Rav Sheshet asks {\i ed echad b\u8217'yevamah mahu} \u8212- what is the {\i halacha} regarding one witness testifying for the purpose of {\i yibum}? That is, if a single witness testifies that a woman\u8217's husband has died, thereby allowing her to perform {\i yibum} with her {\i yavam}, do we accept that testimony? This question forces an explicit articulation of the foundations upon which we credit the testimony of a single witness ({\i ed echad}) in matters of marital status \u8212- a question central to the entire {\i perek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two theories are advanced. The first: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'ed echad mishum d\u8217'milta d\u8217'avida l\u8217'igluye lo meshaker} \u8212- the reason we believe a single witness is that such matters have a way of coming to light. Because the truth about a husband\u8217's death will eventually emerge, the witness will not risk the disgrace of being exposed as a liar. This rationale operates independently of the woman; it is a feature of the social and epistemic environment. If this is the {\i ta\u8217'am}, it should apply equally to testimony enabling {\i yibum}: what is known will come to light, and the witness will not lie.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Loves Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second theory: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'ed echad mishum d\u8217'hi daika u-minsva} \u8212- the reason we believe a single witness is that the woman herself will investigate the claim before proceeding to marry. Her personal stake in not unwittingly marrying a man whose first husband is alive motivates her to verify the testimony independently. If {\i this} is the {\i ta\u8217'am}, a question arises for the {\i yibum} scenario: {\i ve-hacha kivvan d\u8217'zimnin d\u8217'rachamah lei, lo daika u-minsva} \u8212- since she may have feelings for the {\i yavam}, her motivation to investigate might be compromised. She might not investigate as thoroughly as she ought to, precisely because she is eager to proceed with {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet answers his own question: {\i tanitu\u8217'a} \u8212- we have already learned this. He points to the {\i mishnah}: {\i amru lah mes banayich ve\u8217'achar kach mes ba\u8217'alayich ve-nisyabma} \u8212- they told her your sons died and then your husband died, and she performed {\i yibum}; and then {\i amru lah chiluf ha-devarim} \u8212- they said it was the reverse \u8212- and the {\i mishnah} rules that she must leave the {\i yavam} and the children are {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what is the scenario? If the first testimony was two witnesses and the second testimony was also two witnesses, why would the second override the first? And furthermore, the ruling of {\i mamzer} would then be problematic \u8212- since there are conflicting testimonies, the child should at most be a {\i safek mamzer}. Yet the {\i mishnah} rules {\i mamzer} definitively and is precise about which children are {\i mamzerim} and which are not. Therefore, the {\i mishnah} must be describing a case where the first testimony that permitted {\i yibum} was given by a {\i single} witness, which was then contradicted by two witnesses. {\i Ve-ta\u8217'ama de-asu betrei akachshuha} \u8212- the reason the second report prevailed was only that two witnesses contradicted the single one. {\i Ha lav hachi ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- had there been no such contradiction, the testimony of the single witness would have been sufficient to permit {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion Ed Echad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof is thus established: a single witness is believed to permit {\i yibum}, even in circumstances where the woman might have an incentive to remarry her {\i yavam} and therefore might not investigate with full diligence. Whether this follows because {\i milta d\u8217'avida l\u8217'igluye} operates independently of the woman\u8217's investigation, or whether we simply do not treat the possibility of emotional attachment to the {\i yavam} as a sufficient reason to distrust her investigation \u8212- the practical conclusion is unambiguous. An {\i ed echad} is believed for {\i yibum} just as he is believed for ordinary remarriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} will continue this discussion in the next {\i daf}, where it bears directly on the broader question \u8212- central to the entire {\i perek} \u8212- of why we credit a single witness at all in matters of marital status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 94\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eid Echad Foundation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Witness Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the midst of a question that has been developing across several folios: can the principles governing {\i eid echad} \u8212- the credibility extended to a single witness \u8212- be applied to the domain of {\i yibum}? The standard scenario for {\i eid echad} is familiar: a single witness testifies that a woman\u8217's husband has died, and on the basis of that testimony she is permitted to remarry. The question now is whether the same credibility operates when, instead of marrying a stranger, the woman is to perform {\i yibum} with her brother-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why might this case differ? Because the stranger is someone toward whom she has no particular prior relationship or incentive to cut corners, whereas the brother-in-law is a man who has been part of the family. She knows him. Perhaps she is even more fond of him than of her husband. The concern, then, is whether that familiarity might lead her to do inadequate investigation \u8212- to accept thin evidence of her husband\u8217's death because the result would be a marriage she actually desires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara frames the question by articulating two alternative explanations for why we believe an {\i eid echad} in the first place. The first is {\i milta d\u8217'avida l\u8217'igluyei} \u8212- the principle that matters of life and death naturally come to light. A man is either alive or dead; that fact will eventually be known. Because the truth will surface on its own, the single witness has no motive to lie, and that alone is sufficient to render his testimony credible. Under this reading, her own investigation is irrelevant, and whether she wants the outcome or not makes no difference to the reliability of the {\i eid}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second explanation is {\i dayka u\u8217'minsva} \u8212- that the woman herself investigates carefully before acting on the testimony. It is her own due diligence that bolsters the single witness\u8217's credibility, providing an independent check on his report. Under this reading, the reliability of the {\i eid echad} is not self-contained but is intertwined with the quality of her investigation. And if her investigation might be compromised by self-interest \u8212- whether through love of the brother-in-law or, as we will see, through hatred of him \u8212- then the credibility of the {\i eid echad} is correspondingly weakened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed difficulty. If the Rishonim had all along assumed that the credibility of {\i eid echad} rests on her investigation, how can the Gemara now present these two explanations as genuine alternatives? Tosafot resolves this by distinguishing between degrees of reliance on her investigation: one can say the earlier discussions assumed {\i some} role for her investigation without committing to its being the {\i primary} or {\i exclusive} basis. Tosafot therefore reads the Gemara as positing that her investigation contributes meaningfully but need not bear the full weight alone. This is an important resolution, though one must acknowledge that the Gemara\u8217's own framing presents the two explanations as a genuine opposition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband vs Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overwritten Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara had previously sought proof that {\i eid echad} is believed even for purposes of {\i yibum} from a case in the Mishnah where testimony about the order of deaths was subsequently reversed. The Mishnah discusses a scenario in which a woman is told that her husband died first and her son died afterward \u8212- an order in which she is {\i not} obligated in {\i yibum}, since her son was alive when her husband died and thus there is no surviving brother-in-law without offspring. She then remarries. Subsequently she is told {\i chiluf hayu ha-devarim} \u8212- the facts were reversed \u8212- that the son died first and then the husband, meaning she {\i was} obligated in {\i yibum}. The Mishnah rules that she must leave her new husband and that her children are {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the Gemara raises is: why does the second report overturn the first? If both consisted of two witnesses each, there is no obvious reason to prefer the second over the first. The Gemara therefore infers that the first report must have been a single witness and the second consisted of two, and the two outweighed the one. This would demonstrate that in principle an {\i eid echad} can be credited with testifying about the order of deaths \u8212- effectively permitting her to escape the obligation of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Critical Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now a further question is posed: the student body asks Rav Sheshet that the real question is not whether we believe an {\i eid echad} that the {\i husband} is dead and permits {\i yibum} \u8212- even the woman herself is believed to say her husband died, as a Mishnah teaches: {\i ha\u8217'isha she\u8217'amra met ba\u8217'ali tinasei}, a woman who says her husband died may remarry; and {\i met ba\u8217'ali tisyabem}, she may perform {\i yibum} on the strength of her own word. If she is believed in all this, certainly an {\i eid echad} is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The real question is {\i lemishra yevama le\u8217'alma} \u8212- whether one can believe an {\i eid echad} in order to permit the {\i yevama} to marry someone outside the family altogether, thereby releasing her entirely from the {\i yibum} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two obvious scenarios in which this question arises. One: the husband is already known to be dead, there is a {\i yavam} waiting, and a single witness reports that the {\i yavam} has died in a foreign country \u8212- may she now remarry freely? The second scenario emerges from the Mishnah itself: the testimony about the order of deaths, which, if believed, releases her from {\i yibum} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The woman\u8217's own testimony provides the baseline. She is believed to say {\i met ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- her husband died \u8212- and on that basis she may remarry or perform {\i yibum}. But she is {\i not} believed to say {\i met yivami she\u8217'inasei} \u8212- my {\i yavam} has died so that I may marry another man. Nor is she believed to say {\i meta achoti} \u8212- my sister died so that I may marry her husband. These cases differ from testimony about the husband\u8217's death, and the parallel question follows naturally: if she herself is not believed in these cases, does the same limitation apply to an {\i eid echad}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Incentive Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatred vs Love\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot provides a penetrating analysis of why the concern about improper investigation is more acute when the woman hates the {\i yavam} than when she loves him. The Gemara had already raised the possibility that if she loves the brother-in-law she might not investigate sufficiently because she wants to marry him. But that concern was the original question \u8212- and we had in some sense decided that her own credibility regarding the husband\u8217's death was not undermined even then. Why should hatred be worse?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains with a principle both psychologically astute and Talmudically grounded: {\i shegedola sinas hasone me\u8217'ahavas ha\u8217'ohev} \u8212- the hatred of an enemy is a more powerful force than the love of one who loves. Those motivated by strong negative feeling act with greater urgency and force than those motivated by positive attachment. The hater is more likely to neglect due diligence than the lover. Tosafot adds that it is even more common for a woman to hate her {\i yavam} than to love him, as is stated elsewhere in the tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trapped Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot presses further and articulates what is perhaps the more decisive point: the consequences are asymmetric. If she loves the {\i yavam} and yet fails to investigate properly, the worst outcome is that {\i yibum} takes place \u8212- she winds up married to a man she favored. But she might not have been particularly invested, because perhaps he will do {\i chalitzah} instead, or perhaps she would have been equally happy marrying any number of other men. The upside is modest. By contrast, if she hates the {\i yavam}, the worst-case scenario is catastrophic: she is trapped in a marriage to a man she despises. She has no legal recourse, and if {\i yibum} is performed she cannot unilaterally escape it. {\i Einah yecholah linatzel mimenu linasei le\u8217'acher} \u8212- she cannot escape him to marry another. Her only alternative is perpetual {\i iggun}, never receiving {\i chalitzah}, never being free to move on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry is stark. Not marrying the man you love still leaves you free. Being married to the man you hate leaves you with no options. That is the most powerful of all the negative forces we have catalogued, and it is precisely why the Gemara is more troubled by the case of hatred than by the case of love.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Sheshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Cannot Testify\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet responds to the students\u8217' formulation of the question by saying {\i tenisuah} \u8212- we have already learned this in the Mishnah. He cites the Mishnah: {\i amru lah meis ba\u8217'alaich ve\u8217'achar kach meis binaiach} \u8212- they said to her that her husband died and afterward her son died. She remarried. Then they said {\i chiluf hayu ha-devarim} \u8212- the order was reversed. The Mishnah rules {\i teitzei} \u8212- she must leave \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'havlad rishon ve\u8217'acharon mamzer} \u8212- and the children from her first and last relationships are {\i mamzerim}. This is the view of Rabbi Akiva, who holds that even a violation of an {\i issur lav} can produce a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The force of Rav Sheshet\u8217's point is the following: the Mishnah overturns the first report in favor of the second. But why? If both consisted of two witnesses, there is no obvious basis for preferring one over the other. Furthermore, if both had equal standing, the child\u8217's status should be {\i safeik mamzer} \u8212- a doubtful {\i mamzer} \u8212- not a definite one. But the Mishnah does not say {\i safeik mamzer}; a later clause of the same Mishnah carefully distinguishes which child is a {\i mamzer} and which is not, demonstrating that the Mishnah is being quite precise. A {\i safeik mamzer} would not be called simply {\i mamzer}. Therefore, the Mishnah must be working with the assumption that the second report definitively outweighs the first \u8212- which can only be because the first was an {\i eid echad} and the second was two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rebuts Rav Sheshet. It says {\i le\u8217'olam trei ve-trei} \u8212- perhaps the Mishnah is discussing two witnesses against two witnesses throughout, and that does not preclude it being a valid proof of anything about {\i eid echad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eidei Hazama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues: according to Rav Acha bar Minyumi, the reason we credit the second set of witnesses over the first is that the second set are not merely offering contrary factual testimony, but are {\i eidei hazama} \u8212- witnesses who testify that the first witnesses were not present at the events they claimed to have witnessed, which is the formal Talmudic mechanism for invalidating testimony entirely. On this reading, the second witnesses first disqualified the first witnesses as {\i eidei hazama} and then provided their own account of the reversed order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah, however, does not say anything about {\i eidei hazama}; it says {\i chiluf hayu ha-devarim} \u8212- the facts were reversed. The Gemara is reading the {\i eidei hazama} dynamic into the Mishnah. This reading is possible, and if it is adopted, then the Mishnah cannot serve as proof that we believe a single witness at all: the Mishnah might be describing a scenario where two witnesses trumped two others for the specific reason of {\i eidei hazama}, not because one of the original reports was an {\i eid echad}. The hoped-for proof therefore collapses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agunah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trapped Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now draws on a broader principle to frame when {\i eid echad} is believed at all: the rabbinic leniency of {\i eid echad} is specifically extended in cases where a person\u8217's status is trapped and in need of relief. The clearest such case is the {\i agunah} \u8212- a woman whose husband may or may not be alive and who cannot remarry until his death is established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Not Trapped\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this principle in view, the Gemara examines the parallel Mishnah\u8217's cases about what a man is not believed to testify. The Mishnah states: a man is not believed to say that his brother died so that he may perform {\i yibum} with his brother\u8217's wife, nor is he believed to say that his wife died so that he may marry her sister. The Gemara asks: should we infer from this that while the man is not believed, an {\i eid echad} would be believed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is no \u8212- because the principle of {\i mishum igguna akeilu bah rabbanan} applies only where someone is genuinely trapped. A man is never truly trapped in the way a woman is. Even before Rabbeinu Gershom\u8217's enactment, a man could marry additional wives; he is never locked into an inescapable marital limbo. The worst a man faces is that there is a particular woman he cannot marry \u8212- but he is free. A woman, by contrast, may be completely unable to remarry and has no recourse. Therefore, the entire framework of leniency for {\i eid echad} is targeted at her situation, not his. One cannot infer from a Mishnah dealing with both a man and a woman anything definitive about whether an {\i eid echad} would be believed in cases where no one is truly trapped. The Mishnah\u8217's purpose is simply to state that she \u8212- and he \u8212- are not believed; it carries no implication about a third-party {\i eid echad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds that the reason the Mishnah even needed to state this about her \u8212- given that according to Rabbi Akiva, if she were lying the child would be a {\i mamzer} and she herself would face all the severe consequences catalogued in earlier Mishnayos \u8212- is precisely that we might have thought: {\i chaysa al kilkula d\u8217'zar\u8217'ah u\u8217'dayka} \u8212- she would be scrupulously careful because she would never risk producing a {\i mamzer}. She would not even risk not doing due diligence. Therefore, the Mishnah teaches us that even so, we do not believe her, because the force of hatred toward the {\i yavam} is powerful enough to override even the fear of producing a {\i mamzer}-status child. That is how deeply the Gemara takes that psychological dynamic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava cuts through the accumulated complexity with a {\i kal vachomer}. If we believe an {\i eid echad} to permit an {\i issur karet} \u8212- the grave transgression of a married woman remarrying, or performing {\i yibum} with a {\i yavam} \u8212- how can we fail to believe an {\i eid echad} for the comparatively lighter {\i issur lav} of a {\i yevama} marrying someone outside the family before receiving {\i chalitzah}? Surely if we extend credibility for the heavier matter, the lighter follows automatically.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection to Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the rabbis responds to Rava\u8217's {\i kal vachomer} with a pointed counter-argument: look at the woman herself as a proof. She herself is believed regarding the {\i issur karet} \u8212- the testimony that her husband died, which permits her to remarry or perform {\i yibum}, both of which involve {\i issur karet}. Yet she is {\i not} believed to say that the {\i yavam} died, even though that involves only an {\i issur lav}. So it is demonstrably not the case that lighter stringency automatically follows from heavier \u8212- we see a case where she is believed for the heavier but not the lighter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Due Diligence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is she not believed about the {\i yavam}\u8217's death? {\i De-kivan de-zimnin de-sanya lei, lo daika u\u8217'minsva} \u8212- because there are cases in which she hates the {\i yavam} and will not perform due diligence. She may not consciously lie, but she will accept insufficient evidence, she will not push hard enough to confirm that he is truly dead, because she desperately wants to escape the prospect of marrying him. That motivational failure renders her testimony unreliable \u8212- not because she is dishonest but because she may be self-deceived or insufficiently rigorous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Bolstered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication for {\i eid echad} then follows directly. If the credibility of a single witness rests in part on {\i dayka u\u8217'minsva} \u8212- on her investigation bolstering his testimony \u8212- then in a case where we cannot trust her investigation, the {\i eid echad} loses that bolstering as well. We cannot believe the {\i eid echad} about the {\i yavam}\u8217's death for the same reason we cannot believe her: because her investigation, which props up his testimony, may have been compromised by the powerful force of hatred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara does not formally resolve whether we do or do not believe the {\i eid echad} in this case. The argument hangs on how deeply the credibility of the {\i eid} depends on her own reliability. What the {\i Shulchan Aruch} ({\i Even Ha-Ezer} 158, section 3) does rule is: {\i neeman eid echad lehahid leyevama shemet baalah umityabemet al piv, o shemet yevamah shetinasei lashuk} \u8212- a single witness is believed to testify that the {\i yevama}\u8217's husband died and she performs {\i yibum} on his word, or that the {\i yavam} died and she may marry another man. So the practical ruling follows Rava leniently \u8212- we do believe the {\i eid echad} even for releasing her from {\i yibum}. But the Gemara leaves the theoretical underpinning unresolved, and it touches on the core question that has run through this entire chapter: is the {\i eid echad} believed because of his own inherent reliability, or because of the investigation she performs?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Reappears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions to the Mishnah. The final case of the preceding Mishnah involved a woman who, based on testimony that her husband had died, performed {\i kiddushin} \u8212- betrothal \u8212- with another man, and then the husband reappeared alive. Since she had only done {\i kiddushin} and not yet {\i nisuin} \u8212- had not yet begun living with the second man \u8212- she simply returns to her husband. There is no harm and no foul. Furthermore, if the second man nonetheless gives her a {\i get}, that {\i get} does not render her a {\i grushah} \u8212- a divorcee \u8212- for purposes of marrying a {\i Kohen}, because he was never truly her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grushah Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah attributes a {\i drasha} on this point to Rabbi Elazar ben Mattia, who derived it from the verse {\i ve-ishah grushah me-ishah} \u8212- a woman divorced from {\i her husband}. Since this man never achieved the status of her husband, the {\i get} he gives her carries no force and does not make her a {\i grushah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav observes that Rabbi Elazar could have derived a much more illuminating ruling \u8212- a pearl \u8212- from that verse, and instead used it for something relatively obvious \u8212- a piece of pottery. The pearl would have been: {\i afilu lo nitgarsha ela me-ishah} \u8212- even if she was divorced only from her husband, meaning even a {\i get} that says \u8220"you are divorced from me but remain forbidden to everyone else,\u8221" which is not a valid {\i get} that permits remarriage \u8212- even such a document renders her {\i pesulah le-kehuna}, invalid for marriage to a {\i Kohen}. This is the concept of {\i reiach ha-get ha-posel bi-kehuna} \u8212- the \u8220"scent\u8221" of a {\i get} that disqualifies for {\i kehuna} even without fully releasing from the marriage. That is a genuine {\i chiddush}. By contrast, using the verse to teach that a {\i get} from someone who was never truly a husband has no effect is, to put it plainly, not much of a teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next Mishnah introduces a new scenario. A man\u8217's wife goes to a distant country. Others come and tell him she has died. He then marries her sister. Subsequently his wife returns, alive. The Mishnah rules that the wife is {\i muteret lachazor lo} \u8212- permitted to return to him. The marriage to the sister is treated as entirely without consequence: the sister walks away without needing a {\i get} from him, he is {\i mutar bi-krovot shniyah} \u8212- permitted to the sister\u8217's relatives \u8212- and {\i ha-shniyah muteret bi-krovav} \u8212- the sister is permitted to his relatives. It is as though the marriage to the sister never existed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is this so different from the parallel case where the woman remarries based on false testimony about her husband\u8217's death? The answer lies in how the situation appears to onlookers. When a woman marries a second man while her husband may still be alive, people might reasonably think: perhaps the husband gave her a {\i get}; perhaps the marriage was dissolved legitimately. The second marriage therefore appears plausible and carries real legal weight \u8212- it must be undone with a {\i get} and produces all the negative consequences. But when a man marries his wife\u8217's sister and it turns out his wife is alive, everyone understands immediately that even if he had divorced his wife, he could not have been validly married to the sister while his wife was alive. There is no scenario, however one arranges the facts, in which this marriage could have been legitimate. Therefore it is not treated as a marriage at all, requires no {\i get}, and carries no consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds that this holds {\i even} if he is {\i mutar} to remain with the wife \u8212- it is the woman whose legal status is affected by certain forbidden sexual relations, not the man. His sleeping with the sister does not alter his wife\u8217's status, because she was not a party to what occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Actually Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further case: suppose the man was told his wife had died, married the sister, and then \u8212- after this had already occurred \u8212- the wife actually does die. He is now {\i mutar bi-shniyah} \u8212- permitted to remain with the sister. Whatever occurred in the interim, the eventual death of the first wife removes all barriers. Furthermore, the Mishnah specifies: if it subsequently becomes clear that the wife was in fact still alive when he married the sister, {\i havlad rishon mamzer} \u8212- the child born during the period the first wife was alive is a {\i mamzer} \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'ha\u8217'acharon eino mamzer} \u8212- but the child born after the first wife genuinely died is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi states a general principle: {\i kol she\u8217'posel al yedei acherim posel al yedei atzmo} \u8212- whoever disqualifies through others disqualifies through himself; {\i ve\u8217'chol she\u8217'ein posel al yedei acherim eino posel al yedei atzmo} \u8212- and whoever does not disqualify through others does not disqualify through himself. This statement, as the Gemara will clarify, requires considerable unpacking.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife and Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Dead Reports\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara extends the discussion to an additional scenario. Suppose both the wife and the brother-in-law go to a distant country and witnesses report that both of them have died. To use the names introduced in the shiur: Reuven is married to Rachel, and Baruch is married to Leah. Rachel and Baruch go overseas. Reports come back that both have died. Now Reuven, believing Rachel is dead, is free to marry Leah \u8212- his wife\u8217's sister is no longer forbidden now that the wife has died, and Leah, believing Baruch is dead, is free to marry Reuven. They marry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then both Rachel and Baruch return alive. What are the consequences?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Irony Unveiled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From Leah\u8217's perspective, she received testimony that her husband Baruch was dead, remarried Reuven, and it turned out the testimony was false. All the devastating consequences of the earlier Mishnah fall upon her: she must leave both men \u8212- {\i tetzei mizeh umizeh} \u8212- she receives {\i gettin} from both, she is forbidden to both, her children\u8217's statuses are affected. She bears the full weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what of Rachel? One might think: if we say it {\i looked like} Reuven was married to Leah \u8212- seriously enough that Leah suffers all these consequences \u8212- then perhaps it {\i also} looks like Reuven was married to another woman, which should affect Rachel\u8217's ability to return to him. Yet the Gemara rules: {\i afilu hachi, eshet giso asira agiso, ishto sharya} \u8212- even so, the brother-in-law\u8217's wife is forbidden to the brother-in-law, but his own wife remains permitted to him. The two consequences do not run in parallel. {\i Ve\u8217'lo amrinan Rachel u\u8217'mitoch shenesra eshet giso agiso te\u8217'aser ishto alav} \u8212- we do not say that because Leah is now forbidden to Baruch, therefore Rachel should be forbidden to Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony is considerable. The Gemara acknowledges it directly: the very fact that the marriage looked real enough to heap consequences on Leah does not, by some form of symmetry, impose consequences on Rachel. The Gemara explains this partly on the grounds that it is the woman\u8217's status that is affected by forbidden relationships \u8212- Rachel was not involved \u8212- and partly on the grounds that with Rachel alive and known to have been alive the whole time, no observer could plausibly conclude that the marriage to Leah was ever legitimate. There is no scenario that makes it work, so it is not treated as a marriage with respect to Rachel, even as it is treated as consequential enough with respect to Leah to impose all the negative ramifications upon her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Reality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof that the marriage was real enough in some sense is that Leah requires a {\i get} from both Reuven and from Baruch \u8212- she is bound to divorce proceedings with two husbands. Why does she need a {\i get} from Reuven? Because it looked like they were married. Yet this same \u8220"it looked like they were married\u8221" reasoning does not extend to forbid Rachel to Reuven. This asymmetry \u8212- which the Gemara accepts without fully resolving the apparent tension \u8212- reflects the broader principle that a man\u8217's forbidden relationship does not alter his wife\u8217's personal status, which is determined by her own actions, not his.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a challenge from Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position. If the Mishnah says Leah needs a {\i get} from Reuven \u8212- and it does \u8212- then according to Rabbi Akiva, shouldn\u8217't that trigger consequences for Rachel as well? Once Leah needs a {\i get}, she is legally treated, at least nominally, as Reuven\u8217's divorcee. And if she is his divorcee, then Rachel \u8212- her sister \u8212- becomes his divorcee\u8217's sister, which is a category of {\i arayot} that a {\i Kohen} may not marry, and which, if treated seriously, could make Rachel forbidden to Reuven entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This presents a real problem for the Mishnah\u8217's ruling that Rachel remains permitted. If Rabbi Akiva is the author of the Mishnah \u8212- or if we are ruling according to Rabbi Akiva \u8212- how does the Mishnah conclude that Rachel is {\i muteret}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings a {\i baraita} to resolve this. The {\i baraita} states: {\i kol elu she-ba-Torah ein tzerichos mimennu get} \u8212- all the forbidden relationships enumerated in the Torah: if a man began living with his sister, his father\u8217's wife, or any of the {\i arayot}, no {\i get} is required to separate them, because the relationship is understood by all to be legally meaningless. The one exception is: {\i chutz me-eshet ish she-niset al pi beit din} \u8212- a married woman who remarried based on testimony accepted by a {\i beit din}. That case, as we have seen, looks enough like a legitimate marriage \u8212- because it is conceivable the husband had given her a {\i get} \u8212- that it requires a {\i get} to formally undo it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Adds Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva then adds two further cases where a {\i get} is required: {\i af eshet ach ve-achot isha} \u8212- even the brother\u8217's wife and the wife\u8217's sister. What makes these two different from blood-relation {\i arayot}, and why would Rabbi Akiva demand a {\i get} in these cases?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in two features unique to these two relationships. First, they are not blood relatives \u8212- there is no automatic universal recognition that such a relationship is forbidden by nature. Second \u8212- and more importantly \u8212- both of these relationships have circumstances under which they become entirely permitted: the brother\u8217's wife becomes permitted through {\i yibum} if the brother died childless; the wife\u8217's sister becomes permitted after the wife dies. The existence of a legitimate pathway to this marriage means that observers might actually believe the marriage was permissible, and therefore it looks real enough to require a formal {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All other {\i arayot} are forever forbidden without exception \u8212- there is no scenario in which marrying one\u8217's mother or one\u8217's sister becomes permitted. Therefore no observer will mistake such a union for a legitimate marriage. But with brother\u8217's wife and wife\u8217's sister, one can imagine a legitimate pathway, and therefore the illicit union takes on the appearance of a real marriage, requiring a {\i get} to dissolve.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Limiting Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Only Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately qualifies Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position significantly. Rav Yehuda, citing Rav Chiya bar Yosef citing Rav, asks: what exactly is the case in which Rabbi Akiva says the brother\u8217's wife needs a {\i get}? The answer: {\i kegon she-kiddesh achiv et ha-isha} \u8212- it is specifically where the brother had done only {\i kiddushin} with the woman, without {\i nisuin} \u8212- and then went to a distant country, and the other man heard he had died and married the brother\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tnai Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does the stage of the first marriage matter? Because {\i de-amri inshi} \u8212- people will say \u8212- {\i tenai hava lei be-kiddushin} \u8212- perhaps the brother\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was subject to a stipulation that was never fulfilled, meaning the {\i kiddushin} never actually took effect. And therefore, {\i shappir nasa} \u8212- the second man genuinely and legitimately married her. Once people believe the second marriage was valid, it looks real, and it requires a {\i get} to undo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This logic applies precisely because {\i kiddushin} without {\i nisuin} is subject to the possibility of conditions: it is known and understood that betrothal can be contingent on stipulations that may fail to be met. Therefore, the mere fact of {\i kiddushin} does not definitively settle the matter in the eyes of the public.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same reasoning governs the case of the wife\u8217's sister. If the man had done only {\i kiddushin} with his first wife Rachel and not {\i nisuin}, and she went abroad and he heard she had died and he married Leah, people might say: {\i tenai hava lei be-kiddushin} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} to Rachel was conditional and never took effect \u8212- {\i ve-shappir nasiv} \u8212- and therefore the marriage to Leah was perfectly valid. Because the marriage to Leah thus appears real, it requires a {\i get}, and once it requires a {\i get}, the sister of his apparent divorcee becomes forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But after {\i nisuin}, even Rabbi Akiva agrees that no {\i get} is required. The principle {\i ein tnai be-nisuin} \u8212- stipulations do not apply after {\i nisuin} has taken place \u8212- means that no one will entertain the possibility that the first marriage fell through. Once a couple has lived together as husband and wife, the marriage is treated as complete and unconditional. Therefore, no observer will think the second marriage was legitimate, it will not be treated as a real marriage, and no {\i get} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical outcome is significant. If a man had {\i nisuin} with his first wife \u8212- as is the standard case \u8212- and she goes overseas and is reported dead and he marries her sister, and then the first wife returns, no {\i get} is needed to separate him from the sister. The marriage to the sister is simply dissolved, without formal proceedings, without lingering consequences for status. Rachel remains permitted to Reuven, and Leah walks away as though the marriage never was \u8212- because, in the eyes of anyone who knows that {\i nisuin} had taken place, it effectively never was.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 95\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife Dead Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins toward the bottom of 94b. The case under discussion is not the more familiar scenario of a woman whose husband is reported dead and who then remarries \u8212- that case occupied much of the preceding material \u8212- but rather its mirror image: a husband whose wife is reported dead, who then marries her sister, and whose wife subsequently returns alive. The Gemara had already addressed certain preliminary complications arising from this scenario, particularly whether the marriage to the sister creates ongoing obligations. Specifically, it considered whether, according to Rabbi Akiva, the sister would require a {\i get}, which would in turn render the original wife forbidden to him as the sister of his divorc\u233 ?e ({\i achos grushaso}). That possibility was ultimately rejected, and even according to Rabbi Akiva, it is assumed that the husband may return to his first wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, Rav Ashi poses a question to Rav Kahana, beginning with the word {\i Akiva} approximately twelve lines from the bottom of 94b: if the {\i mishnah} can even be in accordance with Rabbi Akiva \u8212- since the man is not required to give a {\i get} to the sister \u8212- then why does the {\i mishnah} present only the case of the sister as the scenario in which the report of the wife\u8217's death would be relevant to his marrying someone new?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For most forbidden relationships ({\i arayot}), the prohibition does not dissolve upon the death of the relevant party. The sister, however, is the paradigmatic exception: the prohibition of {\i achos isha} is tied explicitly to the wife\u8217's lifetime, as the verse states that it is forbidden to take a woman alongside her sister {\i in her lifetime} ({\i bechayeha}). That is presumably why the sister is the only such case addressed in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- she is the only {\i ervah} who could become permitted once the wife dies. But one might ask: what about the mother-in-law ({\i chamuto})? The general assumption is that the prohibition of marrying a woman and her mother applies even after the wife has died. Yet according to Rabbi Akiva, perhaps it does not. If so, then the {\i mishnah} should also have presented the case where the wife is reported dead and the husband marries the mother-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand why this is relevant, the Gemara cites a {\i baraisa} on the verse {\i ki yikach ish es isha ve\u8217'imah} \u8212- \u8220"if a man takes a woman and her mother, you shall burn him and them ({\i oso ves-hen}).\u8221" The question arises: what did the wife do that she too should be executed? The debate between Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva turns on the reading of {\i ves-hen}. Rabbi Yishmael reads it as referring to only one of them \u8212- the mother \u8212- {\i oso ves achas meihen}. Rabbi Akiva reads it as both \u8212- {\i oso ves shteihen} \u8212- meaning both the mother and the grandmother, or some other combination involving multiple generations, not the original wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye and Rava disagree about how to interpret this tannaitic dispute. According to Abaye, Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva are not debating any practical {\i halakha} at all; they are debating only how to read the verse ({\i mashma\u8217'us dorshin ika beina\u8217'ihu}). Rabbi Yishmael understands the verse as describing one configuration of the prohibited relationship; Rabbi Akiva understands it as describing two distinct configurations. No one debates the underlying {\i din}. This reading, as Abaye acknowledges, is fine ({\i bishlama le-Abaye}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rava, however, there is a genuine halakhic dispute \u8212- namely, whether the prohibition of the mother-in-law persists after the wife\u8217's death ({\i chamosto le\u8217'achar misah ika beina\u8217'ihu}). On Rava\u8217's reading, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's phrase {\i oso ves shteihen} is interpreted to mean that the death penalty applies only when both women \u8212- the wife and the mother \u8212- are still alive. If the wife had already died, the man who then marries the mother would not be executed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading creates the logical opening for Rav Ashi\u8217's question: if the {\i mishnah} can accord with Rabbi Akiva, and if Rabbi Akiva holds that the mother-in-law prohibition dissolves after the wife\u8217's death, then the {\i mishnah} should include the case of the mother-in-law as a second scenario in which hearing of the wife\u8217's death opens up a new permitted marriage. The Gemara\u8217's response, formulated by Abaye, is that even if Rabbi Akiva exempts from the {\i sreifah} (burning), one cannot conclude that he removes the prohibition entirely. As Tosafos makes explicit: the phrase {\i me\u8217'issura mi mietei rachmana} \u8212- \u8220"did the Torah exempt it from prohibition?\u8221" \u8212- refers not to some minor rabbinic {\i issur} but to the full weight of a negative commandment and {\i kares}. The man simply would not be executed; the {\i issur} remains. Therefore, it would not be relevant to include this scenario in the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sleeping Sister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hava Amina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the case at hand \u8212- Reuven is married to Rachel, she travels to {\i medinat hayam}, word arrives that she has died, and Reuven marries her sister Leah \u8212- the Gemara now raises the following question: granted that we do not treat this as a genuine marriage, and Leah requires no {\i get}, should we not at least say that the very act of Reuven sleeping with Leah ought to render Rachel forbidden to him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The intuition behind this suggestion is a kind of symmetry with the laws of adultery: if a woman sleeps with another man, that act renders her forbidden to her husband, even if she acted entirely unknowingly ({\i shogeg}). The Rabbis extended the biblical prohibition to cases of {\i shogeg} as a rabbinic decree ({\i gazru bah rabanan}). Should the same not apply here in reverse?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects the comparison \u8212- {\i lo damya}. When a wife commits adultery, even inadvertently, the Rabbis decreed she becomes forbidden because the underlying biblical prohibition is severe: intentional adultery ({\i bemezid}) renders her forbidden {\i mid\u8217'Oraisa}. The rabbinic extension to {\i shogeg} is therefore grounded in a pre-existing biblical framework. But in the case of {\i achos isha}: sleeping with the wife\u8217's sister, even intentionally ({\i bemezid}), does not render the wife {\i Rachel} forbidden to Reuven under any biblical provision. There is no {\i issur d\u8217'Oraisa} establishing that intercourse with the sister causes the wife to become forbidden. Consequently, there is no basis for a rabbinic decree extending such a consequence to cases of {\i shogeg} either. ({\i Bishogeg lo gazru bah rabanan.})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Otah Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites a {\i baraisa} that grounds this principle in a textual source. From the verse in the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah} \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'shachav ish osah shichvas zera} \u8212- the Rabbis derive: {\i otah} \u8212- specifically {\i her} act of sleeping with another man renders her forbidden ({\i shichvasah osartah}), but the act of her sister sleeping with her husband does not render her forbidden ({\i ve\u8217'ein shichvas achosah osartah}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why did the Torah need to state this? Surely this is self-evident. The {\i baraisa} therefore presents the logical argument that might have led us astray \u8212- the {\i hava amina} requiring refutation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i hava amina} is a {\i kal vachomer}. In a case where a man has intercourse with a woman who is an {\i issur kal} \u8212- adultery is here called a \u8220"light prohibition\u8221" \u8212- the one who defines her status ({\i ha\u8217'oser}) becomes forbidden. Specifically: Reuven sleeps with Dvora while she is married to Barak. Reuven has committed adultery, the {\i issur kal}. The consequence: Barak \u8212- who is the {\i oser}, the one whose existence creates her forbidden status as a married woman \u8212- now finds his wife forbidden to him. That is the effect of {\i ba al issur kal ne\u8217'esar ha\u8217'oser}. By {\i kal vachomer}: if Reuven were instead to sleep with Leah, his own wife\u8217's sister, which is called {\i issur chamur}, then Rachel \u8212- the one who defines Leah\u8217's forbidden status as her sister \u8212- should certainly become forbidden as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning here is admittedly strained. The {\i baraisa} itself acknowledges that we need a verse to correct the {\i kal vachomer}, and the verse ({\i otah}) does so: only {\i her} act \u8212- the wife\u8217's own act of infidelity \u8212- can render her forbidden. Not the act of her sister.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Shammai Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda now introduces a statement clarifying what Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel actually debate. According to Rav Yehuda, Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel did not disagree about the case where a man has intercourse with his wife\u8217's mother: in that case, all agree that the wife becomes forbidden ({\i posel}) as a result. The source for this position is the very verse we handled earlier \u8212- {\i ves-hen} \u8212- which, even if not interpreted to mandate execution of the wife, establishes that the wife\u8217's status is affected when a man sleeps with her mother. The debate of Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel, according to Rav Yehuda, concerns only the case of {\i boel achos ishto} \u8212- intercourse with the wife\u8217's sister. There, Beit Shammai says that the wife does become forbidden ({\i posel}), while Beit Hillel says she does not ({\i lo posel}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi disagrees with this framing entirely. He states that Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel did not debate the case of the wife\u8217's sister at all \u8212- everyone agrees that sleeping with the sister does not render the wife forbidden. This follows directly from the {\i drasha} on {\i otah}: {\i otah} \u8212- {\i her} sleeping with another man makes her forbidden; the sleeping of her sister does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then is the debate of Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel? According to Rabbi Yosi, the debate is only in the case of {\i boel chamoso} \u8212- intercourse with the mother-in-law. Beit Shammai holds that this does render the wife forbidden ({\i posel}); Beit Hillel holds that it does not ({\i lo posel}). The ruling of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel is stated explicitly: {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- we do not rule like Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, which extended the prohibition to the mother-in-law even according to Beit Hillel. The law follows Beit Hillel, and thus sleeping with the mother-in-law does not render the wife forbidden. This ruling is illustrated with a practical case: a man who committed the transgression of sleeping with his mother-in-law was brought before Rav Yehuda and given lashes, but Rav Yehuda explicitly told him: had Shmuel not ruled otherwise, I would have declared you permanently forbidden to your wife \u8212- but since Shmuel ruled {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}, you may return to your wife.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Shammai Debate (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asymmetry Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraisa} cited in this context articulates the logical basis for the {\i hava amina} that had suggested the wife\u8217's sister ought to render the wife forbidden. The argument proceeds by observing a fundamental asymmetry between the husband\u8217's and wife\u8217's respective prohibitions upon marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before marriage, he was permitted to any woman in the world and she was permitted to any man in the world ({\i hu mutar bechol hanashim shebaolam vehi muteret bechol ha-anashim shebaolam}). Upon marriage, he makes her forbidden to all other men, and she makes him forbidden only to her relatives. The asymmetry is therefore striking: his prohibition upon her is total \u8212- every other man in the world is now off-limits \u8212- while her prohibition upon him is narrow \u8212- only her immediate relatives (her mother, her sister, etc.) are now forbidden.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sleeping Sister (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife vs.\u160 ?Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This asymmetry generates a {\i kal vachomer}. If we consider the wife\u8217's situation: he makes her forbidden to the entire world, and yet if she unintentionally ({\i shogeg}) sleeps with one of those men, that does not render her additionally forbidden to the one man she is permitted to, namely her husband \u8212- since as established, {\i shogeg} adultery renders her forbidden only by rabbinic decree in view of the underlying biblical prohibition. How much more so should the husband, whose prohibition created by her is far narrower, limited only to her relatives, not find that his act of {\i shogeg} intercourse with one of those relatives renders his wife forbidden to him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer}, properly formulated: the wife, whose prohibition is vastly more extensive and whose status is far more globally redefined by marriage, does not lose her permitted status vis-\u224 ?-vis her husband when she unintentionally transgresses one of the men she is forbidden to. Certainly then the husband, whose prohibitions are far more limited \u8212- only her relatives \u8212- should not find that his unintentional intercourse with one of those relatives renders his wife forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This disposes of the {\i shogeg} case. But what of {\i bemezid}? After all, intentional adultery does render the wife forbidden to her husband. Perhaps intentional intercourse with the sister should similarly render the wife forbidden. The {\i baraisa} then addresses this: {\i v\u8217'zo adim l\u8217'shogeg} \u8212- the above reasoning is effective for {\i shogeg}, but {\i lamezid minayin}? How do we know that even intentional intercourse with the sister does not render the wife forbidden? The answer: {\i talmud lomar otah} \u8212- the verse says {\i otah}. Her own sexual act renders her forbidden; the sexual act of her sister does not, under any circumstances, render her forbidden.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issur Kal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to examine the {\i kal vachomer} that drove the {\i hava amina} \u8212- the argument that sleeping with the wife\u8217's sister ({\i issur chamur}) should render the wife forbidden, since sleeping with a married woman ({\i issur kal}) renders her husband forbidden to her. The phrase {\i issur kal} in this context requires clarification. What precisely counts as an {\i issur kal}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda proposes: {\i machzir grushato misheniset} \u8212- the case of a man who remarries his divorced wife after she had married another man. The structure of the case runs as follows. Reuven marries Rachel; he divorces her; she marries Barak. Now Barak divorces Rachel and Reuven remarries her. Reuven has committed the prohibition of {\i machzir grushato misheniset}. But what has happened to Barak\u8217's status vis-\u224 ?-vis Rachel? Because Rachel remarried Reuven, she is now forbidden to Barak \u8212- he cannot take her back. So: Barak\u8217's prior marriage to Rachel is what made her forbidden to Reuven in stage two, making Barak the {\i oser}; and then Reuven\u8217's act of remarriage \u8212- the {\i issur kal} \u8212- renders the {\i oser} (Barak) forbidden to the woman. {\i Ba la-hai asra a-hai, ba la-idach asrah l\u8217'hai.} This, says Rav Chisda, is the paradigm of {\i bemakom she\u8217'ba issur kal ne\u8217'esar ha\u8217'oser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this as a basis for comparison, on two grounds. First: {\i nitma\u8217'ah haguf} \u8212- in that case the woman\u8217's own body was directly involved in the act that changed her status; she herself married Reuven. Second: {\i ve\u8217'issur olam} \u8212- the prohibition that results is permanent; she will always be forbidden to Barak. By contrast, the prohibition of {\i achos isha} dissolves if the wife dies. Therefore this is not a genuine {\i issur kal} \u8212- in the relevant respects, it is in fact more severe than the sister prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevama Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish proposes the case of a {\i yevama} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibum}. The {\i issur kal} would be intercourse with another man, violating the prohibition of {\i lo tihyeh eishes hameis hachutza l\u8217'ish zar}, a simple {\i lav}. Per the ruling of Rav Hamnuna, a {\i shomeres yavam} who commits {\i zenus} becomes forbidden to the brother-in-law ({\i yavam}), who is the one who made her status as a {\i yevama} defined. This appears to be a cleaner parallel: a simple {\i lav} is violated, and the result is that the {\i oser} \u8212- the {\i yavam} who defined her status \u8212- becomes forbidden to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara again objects: {\i nitma\u8217'ah haguf} \u8212- it was her own person, her own act, that generated the consequence. And additionally, {\i issura berov} \u8212- the prohibition she now carries extends broadly: she is forbidden to the entire world, not just to one person, as would be the case for the wife whose husband sleeps with her sister. The parallel therefore fails on the same grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then considers whether the {\i yevama} case can be reformulated as one involving the {\i brothers} rather than a stranger \u8212- specifically, a case where one brother performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with the {\i yevama} and a second brother then violated that by engaging in {\i yibum}, thereby rendering her forbidden to the first brother. The Gemara notes that it is not the sexual act per se that generates the prohibition; even a {\i get} or {\i chalitza} by the second brother would produce the same effect, making the act of intercourse irrelevant as the operative cause. This proposed parallel thus fails as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan proposes {\i sotah} as the {\i issur kal}. The idea requires clarification, because the {\i sotah} case has two possible readings. One reading: the husband has intercourse with the {\i sotah}, and this makes her forbidden to the adulterer \u8212- {\i ba al ha-baal asar lah le-boel}. But the Gemara objects: the wife becomes forbidden to the adulterer from the moment she commits adultery, regardless of whether the husband subsequently has intercourse with her; even a {\i get} from the husband would seal her prohibition from the adulterer. The husband\u8217's intercourse is therefore not the operative cause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative reading, therefore, must be: the {\i sotah} as a paradigm for the prohibition of a married woman ({\i eshet ish}) sleeping with any man other than her husband \u8212- the fundamental prohibition of adultery itself. That act renders her forbidden to her husband. This would be the {\i issur kal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But can adultery really be called an {\i issur kal}? The Gemara itself raises the objection: {\i issur chamur hu, dehainu eshet ish} \u8212- it is a grave prohibition, carrying the death penalty. Rava\u8217's response, and simultaneously that of Ravin citing Rabbi Yochanan, is to simply own the comparison directly: yes, the {\i issur kal} we are discussing is {\i eshet ish} \u8212- adultery. Why does it merit the label {\i issur kal}? Because the prohibition is not permanent. A married woman could in principle become permitted to any given man \u8212- including the adulterer \u8212- if she were to receive a {\i get}. By contrast, the wife\u8217's sister is forbidden so long as the wife is alive, with no mechanism for permissibility. {\i She-eina ossra kol yamav} \u8212- she does not always remain forbidden to him. That is the tenuous distinction that allows us to call adultery {\i issur kal} relative to the prohibition of {\i achos isha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraisa} cited by Abba Chanan in the name of Abba Elazar confirms this reading. {\i U-mah bimkom ha-baal issur kal she-eina ossra kol yamav ne\u8217'esar ha-oseh} \u8212- when a man comes upon a married woman ({\i issur kal}, since she could become permitted via divorce), the act makes her forbidden to the one who defined her status. {\i Ha-ba al issur chamur she-ossra kol yamav} \u8212- one who sleeps with the wife\u8217's sister, the more severe prohibition, applicable for the wife\u8217's entire lifetime \u8212- should render the one who defined her status ({\i ha-oseh}) forbidden as well, by a {\i kal vachomer}. The answer: {\i talmud lomar otah} \u8212- only her own act of forbidden intercourse renders her forbidden. {\i Otah shchivasah poseltah, ve-ein shchivat achotah poseltah.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos notes a residual difficulty. Even granting that adultery is called {\i issur kal}, the earlier objection remains: in the adultery case, the woman\u8217's own body was the locus of the violation ({\i nitma\u8217'ah haguf}). That objection still applies and was never fully answered. Tosafos remarks with understated reserve \u8212- {\i aval kzat kashya} \u8212- that it remains somewhat difficult. The honest assessment is that the {\i hava amina} that sleeping with the sister should render the wife forbidden is not a particularly strong one; the {\i otah} verse is still needed, but the logical underpinnings of the argument being refuted are acknowledged to be strained.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yosi Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} contains the following enigmatic ruling attributed to Rabbi Yosi: {\i kol sheposel al yedei acherim posel al yedei atzmo} \u8212- whoever invalidates others also invalidates himself; {\i v\u8217'chol she\u8217'eino posel al yedei acherim eino posel al yedei atzmo} \u8212- and whoever does not invalidate others does not invalidate himself. The Gemara asks: {\i mai ka\u8217'amar Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- what exactly is Rabbi Yosi saying?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara first considers whether Rabbi Yosi is responding to the case described by the {\i Tanna Kamma}, which the Gemara (following Tosafos) reads as the background scenario for this debate. That case is: both Reuven\u8217's wife Rachel and his brother-in-law Barak travel to a distant country ({\i medinat hayam}). Word arrives that both died. Reuven, hearing that Rachel is dead and Barak is dead, marries Leah (Barak\u8217's wife). It then turns out that both Rachel and Barak are alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lack of Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is Leah\u8217's status? She believed her husband Barak was dead, remarried Reuven, and now it turns out Barak is alive. She suffers all the negative consequences: {\i asura mizeh umizeh}, forbidden to both men and required to leave. What is Rachel\u8217's status? Rachel\u8217's husband Reuven married her sister Leah \u8212- as we have established, this does not constitute a genuine marriage, Leah requires no {\i get}, and Reuven may return to Rachel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The striking asymmetry: Leah, the one who triggered the question about whether she was \u8220"really\u8221" married to Reuven, bears all the consequences of that quasi-marriage. Yet Reuven, who was on the other side of that same quasi-marriage, bears none of them and may freely return to Rachel. The {\i Tanna Kamma} accepts this asymmetry. Rabbi Yosi apparently does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consistency Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explores how Rabbi Yosi would formulate his consistency principle. One possibility is that he argues {\i le-kula}: since Reuven is permitted to return to Rachel, showing that his marriage to Leah was not genuine, then by the same logic Leah should be permitted to return to Barak. If this is Rabbi Yosi\u8217's intent, then the formulation {\i kol she\u8217'eino posel al yedei acherim eino posel al yedei atzmo} \u8212- if you don\u8217't invalidate others, you don\u8217't invalidate yourself \u8212- fits this reading, starting with the {\i kula} and deriving leniency in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative reading is that Rabbi Yosi argues {\i le-chumra}: just as Leah is forbidden to Barak, so Rachel should be forbidden to Reuven. This fits the other half of the formulation: {\i kol sheposel al yedei acherim posel al yedei atzmo} \u8212- whoever invalidates others also invalidates himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To properly situate Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position, the Gemara introduces the distinction between cases involving {\i eid echad} (one witness) and {\i shenei eidim} (two witnesses). The rule established earlier in the tractate is that all the negative consequences of a mistaken remarriage \u8212- the requirement to leave both men, the loss of the {\i ketubah}, etc. \u8212- apply only when the remarriage was based on a single witness ({\i al pi eid echad}), because only then does the Rabbinic leniency ({\i mishum iguna hikilu bah rabanan}) apply and the woman is expected to act on insufficient testimony. When two witnesses testified to the husband\u8217's death, the remarriage was fully permissible, and if the husband returns alive, she may return to him with no negative consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Armed with this distinction, the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's position becomes: it does not matter whether the remarriage was based on one witness (such that Leah cannot return to Barak) or two witnesses (such that she can). Either way, Reuven may return to Rachel. Rabbi Yosi\u8217's response, as now understood, is: you cannot split the ruling this way. When it was {\i al pi beis din} (i.e., a single witness \u8212- {\i eid echad} \u8212- which is what relying on {\i beis din}\u8217's authorization means in this context), and Leah is therefore forbidden to Barak, Rachel is also forbidden to Reuven \u8212- {\i poseil al yedei acherim, posel al yedei atzmo}. When it was {\i al pi shenei eidim}, such that Leah is not forbidden to Barak, Rachel is also not forbidden to Reuven \u8212- {\i eino posel al yedei acherim, eino posel al yedei atzmo}. You must be consistent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question arises from this discussion: what standard of testimony does Reuven himself need in order to remarry in the first place \u8212- to marry Leah \u8212- upon hearing that Rachel has died? May he rely on a single witness? Tosafos on 94b ({\i af al gav}) addresses this directly, concluding that for Reuven to marry the sister specifically, two witnesses are required. The rationale for the general leniency of {\i eid echad} is {\i mishum iguna} \u8212- to spare a woman the suffering of remaining an {\i agunah}. A man does not face that predicament; he can always marry another woman. Therefore the same leniency does not apply, and he would need two witnesses to the wife\u8217's death before marrying her sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha offers an alternative framing of the scenario. Rather than the one-witness versus two-witness distinction, he focuses on whether Rachel was fully married to Reuven ({\i nisuin}) or only betrothed ({\i eirusin}/{\i arusin}) at the time she traveled away with Barak.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction matters for the following reason. The reason Reuven is generally permitted to return to Rachel \u8212- and the reason Leah is not viewed as his genuine wife \u8212- is that everyone understands that Leah\u8217's sister was alive and that Reuven was married to her. With {\i nisuin}, no one entertains the possibility that the marriage to Rachel somehow never materialized. With {\i eirusin} only, however, people might speculate that perhaps a condition ({\i tenai}) in the betrothal was never fulfilled, or that the {\i nisuin} simply never occurred, and therefore Reuven\u8217's marriage to Leah might be seen as more plausible. That perception matters enormously for how the community understands whether this was a genuine marriage or a complete mistake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef\u8217's reading of Rabbi Yosi takes this even further, introducing a scenario where Rabbi Yosi is actually {\i lenient} \u8212- going even further than the {\i Tanna Kamma} in one respect. The {\i Tanna Kamma} says that in the case of {\i ishto ve-giso} (Rachel and Barak both went away), {\i eshet giso asura ve-ishto sharya} \u8212- Leah is forbidden to Barak and Rachel is permitted to Reuven. Rav Yosef\u8217's reading of Rabbi Yosi is: when Rachel was fully married to Reuven ({\i nisuin}), everyone in hindsight knows that it was simply impossible for him to have been genuinely married to Leah. Since everyone will understand this retroactively, the marriage to Leah will not even carry the appearance of a marriage \u8212- it will be seen universally as a mistake, indeed as {\i shogeg} if at all, but certainly not a genuine {\i kinyan}. In that case, not only can Reuven return to Rachel \u8212- Leah can return to Barak as well. There is no semblance of a real marriage to Reuven, and therefore Leah\u8217's status is not compromised. Rabbi Yosi, in the case of {\i nisuin}, is {\i meikel} in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of {\i eirusin}, however \u8212- where Rachel was only betrothed to Reuven \u8212- people might genuinely wonder whether the first marriage ever fully took effect. In that case, the marriage to Leah does carry some semblance of a real marriage. Leah is forbidden to Barak ({\i posel al yedei acherim}), and therefore Rachel is also forbidden to Reuven ({\i posel al yedei atzmo}). Rabbi Yosi is {\i machmir} in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The elegance of Rav Yosef\u8217's reading is that Rabbi Yosi is entirely consistent: never splitting the ruling, going {\i le-kula} in both directions when {\i nisuin} makes the mistake transparent, and {\i le-chumra} in both directions when {\i eirusin} creates ambiguity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel rules: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- the {\i halakha} follows Rabbi Yosi, with his principle of consistency between the treatment of the two couples. The daf concludes here with Rav Yosef raising a challenge to this ruling \u8212- {\i metiv Rav Yosef, u-mi amar Shmuel hachi} \u8212- which will be taken up in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 96\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yossi & Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Rabbi Yossi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf picks up at the bottom of 95b. The {\i mishnah} there contains a somewhat cryptic statement attributed to Rabbi Yossi, to the effect that whoever causes a prohibition to fall upon others causes the same prohibition to fall upon himself, and conversely, if no prohibition falls upon others, none falls upon him either. The {\i gemara} has been explaining this in terms of a case of consistency: a man\u8217's wife and his brother-in-law both travel to a distant country ({\i medinat hayam}), both are reported dead, and it subsequently emerges that both are alive. The man had, in the interim, married his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- believing his wife to be dead \u8212- and his wife\u8217's sister had married the brother-in-law, believing her husband to be dead. Rabbi Yossi\u8217's principle demands consistency: the question of whether that intermediate marriage is perceived as having been binding or not must be applied uniformly to both parties. Either both will be {\i muttar} or both will be {\i assur}, depending on whether that marriage was understood by onlookers as having actually taken effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Continuing from this point, the {\i gemara} records: {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel, halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosei} \u8212- Rav Yehuda rules in the name of Shmuel that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yossi. However, as the {\i gemara} will immediately make clear, there are different versions and interpretations of Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position, so it is not immediately obvious which formulation Shmuel intends to validate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that Shmuel\u8217's statement is meant to apply to the entirety of Rabbi Yossi\u8217's ruling. The concern animating Rabbi Yossi\u8217's principle, according to Rashi, is whether people would perceive the intermediate marriage as having been binding \u8212- the same concern that underlies other restrictions, such as prohibiting a woman who remarried after a mistaken report of her husband\u8217's death from returning to her first husband, lest people conclude that her first husband had divorced her and she had legitimately remarried. The critical variable, Rashi notes, is whether the original marriage between Reuven and Rachel had been consummated through full {\i nissuin} or only {\i kiddushin}. If Rachel and Reuven had only reached the stage of {\i kiddushin}, it is conceivable that people might have perceived Reuven\u8217's subsequent marriage to Leah as valid \u8212- reasoning, perhaps, that the {\i kiddushin} with Rachel had fallen through. If, however, they had completed {\i nissuin}, no one could plausibly have viewed his marriage to Leah as binding, since a fully married man cannot take his wife\u8217's sister. Only if it had been {\i kiddushin} with Leah would there be no problem whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yosef Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef raises a sharp question on Shmuel\u8217's ruling: {\i Umi amar Shmuel hachi} \u8212- did Shmuel really say this? {\i D\u8217'Shmuel l\u8217'taamei} \u8212- for Shmuel himself is on record elsewhere with a seemingly contradictory position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a well-known dispute: {\i Yevamah \u8212- Rav amar harei hi k\u8217'eishesh ish}, Rav holds that a {\i yevamah} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} \u8212- is to be treated like a married woman. {\i U\u8217'Shmuel amar einah k\u8217'eishesh ish}, Shmuel holds she is not treated like a married woman. The bare formulation of this debate leaves its context obscure, and the {\i gemara} now turns to Rav Huna\u8217's interpretation to explain what exactly is at stake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna proposes the following as the context of the Rav-Shmuel dispute: a man\u8217's brother performed {\i kiddushin} with a woman and then traveled to a distant country ({\i medinat hayam}), whereupon it was reported that the brother had died. The surviving brother, believing he was performing {\i yibum}, proceeded to perform {\i kiddushin} with the widow \u8212- his {\i yevamah}. Then the brother turned out to be alive. This case differs from a standard case of a woman who remarries after a mistaken report of her husband\u8217's death. In that ordinary case, even in hindsight, observers might still believe the second marriage was valid \u8212- reasoning, for instance, that the first husband had sent a {\i get} from afar. Here, however, once the brother emerges alive, it becomes retroactively impossible for anyone to have regarded the second marriage as binding, since a man\u8217's sister-in-law is forbidden to him even after divorce so long as the brother lives. In hindsight, no one could have perceived that {\i kiddushin} as a genuine marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin & Perception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the complication, as Rav Huna\u8217's reading highlights, is that the original marriage between the brother and the woman was only {\i kiddushin} \u8212- not full {\i nissuin}. This creates a potential avenue for people to think that perhaps the first {\i kiddushin} had not taken full effect, and therefore the second {\i kiddushin} \u8212- performed by the surviving brother thinking he was doing {\i yibum} \u8212- might have been perceived as valid. Rav holds that this concern is live: since the original marriage was only {\i kiddushin}, people in hindsight might plausibly imagine it had been conditional or defective, making the brother\u8217's subsequent {\i kiddushin} appear binding. Hence the {\i yevamah} is treated like a married woman, with all the attendant stringencies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Resolves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, however, rules {\i einah k\u8217'eishesh ish} \u8212- even the fact that the first marriage was only {\i kiddushin} does not lead us to fear that people will perceive the second act as a genuine marriage. Once the first brother appears alive, it is obvious to all that the surviving brother could never have validly married her, regardless of what stage the first marriage had reached. The {\i gemara} now returns to Rav Yosef\u8217's challenge: if Shmuel is lenient even when the first marriage was only {\i kiddushin} in the case of the {\i yevamah} and her brother-in-law, how can Shmuel also rule like Rabbi Yossi, who appears to impose a stringency specifically when the first marriage was only {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye presses the question further: {\i Memai d\u8217'chi amar Shmuel halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosei, ad Rabbi Yitzchak Napcha ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- how do we know which version of Rabbi Yossi Shmuel intended to validate? Perhaps when Shmuel said {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosei}, he meant to endorse only the interpretation of Rabbi Ami, not that of Rabbi Yitzchak Napcha. And even granting that he meant Rabbi Yitzchak Napcha\u8217's version, perhaps he was endorsing only Rabbi Yossi\u8217's lenient ruling \u8212- not the stringent case involving {\i kiddushin}. That is: perhaps Shmuel\u8217's declaration that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yossi was intended to convey that, just as the husband may return to his original wife Rachel, so too Leah may return to her original husband Barak \u8212- in all cases, {\i l\u8217'kula}. Perhaps Shmuel was never endorsing any stringency at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hindsight Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic that the {\i gemara} is developing is essential to appreciate in full. The usual stringencies applied to a woman who remarries after a mistaken report of her husband\u8217's death \u8212- which function not merely as a punitive fine but as a consequence of the fact that her union with the second man genuinely appeared, to observers, to be a valid marriage \u8212- depend on a factual predicate: that people actually could have perceived the second marriage as binding. There are cases, however, in which that predicate fails. When it subsequently emerges that the first husband\u8217's brother is alive, or that the first husband\u8217's wife\u8217's sister is alive, no one in hindsight could conceivably have viewed the second union as a legitimate marriage. The bond of the {\i yevamah} relationship, or the prohibition of marrying one\u8217's wife\u8217's sister, is absolute and applies even post-divorce. In hindsight, then, it is impossible that anyone would have perceived the intermediate marriage as valid, and therefore the entire foundation for the stringencies collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This insight applies distinctly in the cases of wife\u8217's sister and husband\u8217's brother. In an ordinary case of a husband returning alive after being reported dead, observers could still in hindsight theorize that the husband had sent a {\i get} from {\i medinat hayam}, and so the second marriage still appears potentially legitimate even after the fact. But in the special cases at issue here \u8212- where it is the wife\u8217's sister or the brother who reappears alive \u8212- such a theory is impossible, since the prohibition on marrying one\u8217's wife\u8217's sister, or a woman to her husband\u8217's brother, applies even after divorce and even after {\i get}. Hindsight therefore conclusively forecloses any perception of validity. And the question on the table reduces to this: can we be lenient even when the first marriage was only {\i kiddushin}, since even then people would in hindsight realize that the second union could not have been binding? Shmuel, on this reading, answers yes \u8212- even {\i kiddushin} at the beginning does not give us reason to fear that the second marriage was perceived as valid, and leniency applies across the board.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Alt. Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers whether Rav Huna\u8217's framing of the Rav-Shmuel debate is even the correct one. {\i Dilma leita l\u8217'd\u8217'Rav Huna klal} \u8212- perhaps Rav Huna\u8217's interpretation is entirely off the mark. Perhaps the debate between Rav and Shmuel over whether a {\i yevamah} is {\i k\u8217'eishesh ish} concerns something altogether different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zina Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One alternative: perhaps the debate concerns the ruling of Rav Hamnuna, who teaches that {\i shomeret yavam she\u8217'zinta asura l\u8217'yivama} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibum} who engages in illicit relations ({\i zina}) with another man becomes forbidden to her brother-in-law. On this reading, Rav holds that a {\i yevamah} is sufficiently like a married woman that her {\i zina} disqualifies her from {\i yibum}, while Shmuel holds she is not treated as a married woman and therefore is not disqualified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin & Yevama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third possibility: the debate between Rav and Shmuel concerns whether {\i kiddushin} from another man can take effect with a {\i yevamah} while she still awaits {\i yibum}. Rav holds that her {\i zika} to the brother-in-law renders her sufficiently like a married woman that {\i kiddushin} from an outside man cannot bind her. Shmuel holds she is not treated as a married woman, and therefore {\i kiddushin} from another man would be effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Debate, Multiple Fra\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that Rav and Shmuel are recorded as having debated the {\i yevamah}\u8217's status using various formulations on multiple occasions, and raises the question of why the same dispute would be articulated twice. The answer: {\i chada miklal chaverta itmar} \u8212- the debate was actually stated only once, and the second formulation was derived inferentially from the first. The two framings are not independent disputes but different expressions of a single underlying {\i machlokes}. The last two cases \u8212- whether {\i zina} disqualifies and whether outside {\i kiddushin} can bind \u8212- fit together especially naturally, since both ask how thoroughly the {\i yevamah}\u8217's bond to her brother-in-law resembles the bond of marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The larger {\i chiddush} emerging from the entire extended discussion is worth pausing to appreciate. While the question of whether a husband can return to his wife after he mistakenly married her sister is relatively straightforward, the more striking case is whether the woman herself \u8212- having remarried, even if only on account of a mistaken report of her husband\u8217's death \u8212- can return to her first husband. The {\i gemara} has been exploring the narrow set of circumstances in which hindsight removes the very foundation of the stringencies: cases of wife\u8217's sister or husband\u8217's brother, where the reappearance of the \u8220"dead\u8221" party conclusively demonstrates, for all observers, that the intermediate marriage never could have been valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sequential Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Wives Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now presents a striking scenario. A man is told that his wife died; he marries her sister from their shared father (that is, a paternal half-sister of his first wife). He is then told that the second wife died; he marries her sister from their shared mother (a maternal half-sister of the second). And so on: each wife is reported dead, he marries the next, and ultimately all of them turn out to be alive. The women form a chain: wives one and two share a father but not a mother; wives two and three share a mother but not a father; and so the chain continues through a fifth wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruling on Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: he may remain married to wives one, three, and five; he is forbidden to wives two and four. The logic follows directly. Wife one is a valid marriage. When he married wife two \u8212- who is the paternal sister of wife one \u8212- his first wife being still alive meant the marriage to wife two was invalid: one may not marry one\u8217's wife\u8217's sister. Wife three, however, shares no prohibited relationship with wife one (she is related only to wife two, through their mother), so the marriage to wife three was valid. Wife four, the sister of wife three through their mother, was therefore an invalid marriage. Wife five, bearing no prohibited relationship to wife three, was valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since wives one, three, and five are his legitimate wives, they and their {\i tzaros} (co-wives) interact normally for purposes of {\i yibum}. Should he die, an act of {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} with one of the valid wives would exempt the co-wives as well. Wives two and four, however, are not his wives, and an act with them by a brother would carry no exempting power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Wife Truly Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds a case: what if the report about the first wife\u8217's death was actually true \u8212- she genuinely died? In that case, his marriage to wife two was valid (his first wife being truly dead, the marriage to her sister was permitted). Wife three, then, is the sister of his living wife two and is therefore forbidden to him. The pattern inverts: he is married to wives two and four and forbidden to wives one (dead), three, and five. And correspondingly, valid acts of {\i yibum} with wives two and four would exempt their {\i tzaros}, while acts involving wives three and five would not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor & Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine-Year-Old Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now transitions back squarely to the laws of {\i yibum}, introducing the case of a surviving brother who is nine years old and one day. At this age, an act of sexual intercourse ({\i bi\u8217'ah}) is legally cognizable \u8212- it is treated as a genuine act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- yet because the boy is not yet an adult, it cannot constitute a fully consummated act of {\i yibum}. The question is what legal consequences flow from such an act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disqualification Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i Ben tesha shanim veyom echad hu posel al yedei achim} \u8212- the minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i yevamah} disqualifies the adult brothers from performing {\i yibum} with her thereafter. This act is treated as analogous to {\i maamar} \u8212- one of the quasi-acts that, while not completing {\i yibum}, is nevertheless legally consequential enough to remove others from the picture. Conversely, {\i ve\u8217'achim poslim al yado} \u8212- if the adult brothers act first, whether by {\i bi\u8217'ah}, {\i chalitza}, {\i maamar}, or {\i get}, that act will disqualify the minor from later completing a full act of {\i yibum} when he comes of age. The {\i mishnah} then specifies the asymmetry: {\i ela hu posel techilla ve\u8217'achim poslim techilla ve\u8217'sof} \u8212- his acts disqualify only if he goes first, whereas the brothers\u8217' acts disqualify him whether they act first or second.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamar Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pauses on a question implicit in this ruling: does a minor actually have a legally cognizable {\i maamar} at all? The {\i mishnah}, having established that the minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} functions like {\i maamar} of an adult, and having then suggested that even other acts by the minor (such as {\i maamar} itself) are meaningful if performed first \u8212- prompts the question of whether the {\i mishnah} has now overstated the minor\u8217's legal capacity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bia vs.\u160 ?Maamar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita on Bia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} from the school of Rabbi Oshaya poses a direct challenge. The {\i baraita} teaches: if an adult brother performed {\i maamar} with the {\i yevamah}, and thereafter a minor brother \u8212- nine years old and one day \u8212- had {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her, that {\i bi\u8217'ah} disqualifies her to the first (adult) brother, even though the minor came second. This appears to contradict the {\i mishnah}, which stated that the minor\u8217's acts are only effective when he acts first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Reinterpreted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve the contradiction, the {\i gemara} proposes a revised reading of the {\i mishnah}: {\i bi\u8217'ah paslah afilu besof} \u8212- the minor\u8217's act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} disqualifies even when it comes second. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement that the minor only creates a problem at the beginning ({\i techilla}) must therefore be referring not to {\i bi\u8217'ah} but to lesser acts such as {\i maamar} \u8212- quasi-acts that are effective only if the minor acts first. {\i Maamar techilla posel, besof lo posel} \u8212- {\i maamar} by the minor disqualifies only if it precedes the brothers\u8217' acts; {\i bi\u8217'ah} by the minor disqualifies regardless of order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a strained reading of the {\i mishnah}, which appears to use the example of {\i bi\u8217'ah} when saying the minor is effective only first. But the {\i gemara} accepts this reinterpretation in order to maintain consistency with the {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Probes: Does a Minor Even Have Maamar or Get?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having now said that the minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} is powerful even at the end, while {\i maamar} is only effective at the beginning, the {\i gemara} probes further: does a minor have a legally recognized {\i maamar} at all? And does he have a legally recognized {\i get}? This question challenges the newly expanded picture of the minor\u8217's legal capacities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bia Always Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} addresses the matter directly: {\i ben tesha shanim veyom echad hu posel bedavar echad, ve\u8217'achim poslin al yado bearba\u8217'ah devarim} \u8212- the minor can disqualify the brothers with one act, while the brothers can disqualify him with four. {\i Hu posel al yedei achim bevia} \u8212- his disqualifying act is exclusively {\i bi\u8217'ah}. {\i Ve\u8217'achim poslin al yado bevia, be\u8217'maamar, beget, uvechalitza} \u8212- the brothers can disqualify him through any of the four acts: {\i bi\u8217'ah}, {\i maamar}, {\i get}, or {\i chalitza}. This {\i baraita} seems to say that the minor has only {\i bi\u8217'ah}, not {\i maamar} or {\i get}. The {\i gemara} responds: the {\i baraita} lists only those acts that are always effective \u8212- {\i bi\u8217'ah} for the minor is always effective regardless of order, and therefore it is listed as his single act. {\i Maamar}, which is only effective when it comes first and not when it comes later, is not listed \u8212- not because it is meaningless, but because it is not {\i pesika}, not uniformly reliable. The {\i gemara} has thus, through this chain of reasoning, arrived at a richer account of the minor\u8217's legal capacities than either the {\i mishnah} or the {\i baraita} alone would suggest: {\i bi\u8217'ah} is always effective; {\i maamar} (and potentially other acts) are effective when performed first.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor\u8217's Four Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Has Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additional {\i amoraic} statements now confirm and extend this picture. Rav Yehuda rules in the name of Shmuel: {\i yesh lo get} \u8212- the minor does have a legally meaningful {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Has Maamar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, Rav Tachlifa bar Avimi rules: {\i yesh lo maamar} \u8212- the minor has a legally meaningful {\i maamar} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then records that these positions align with the view of Rabbi Meir: {\i yesh lo get, yesh lo maamar, divrei Rabbi Meir}. The {\i gemara} then probes Rabbi Meir\u8217's position more carefully. The {\i baraita} had earlier taught: {\i asu biat ben tesha k\u8217'maamar begadol} \u8212- the Sages equated the minor\u8217's act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i maamar} of an adult. The analogy is precise: just as an adult\u8217's {\i maamar} does not fully consummate {\i yibum} but does disqualify the other brothers, so too the minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} does not fully consummate {\i yibum} but does disqualify the other brothers. Rabbi Meir then adds: {\i asu chalitzat ben tesha k\u8217'get begadol} \u8212- the Sages equated the minor\u8217's {\i chalitza} with an adult\u8217's {\i get}. One might ask: if Rabbi Meir also holds that the minor has a {\i get} of his own, why would one need to compare his {\i chalitza} to an adult\u8217's {\i get}? Why not compare it to his own {\i get}? The implication seems to be that the minor\u8217's {\i get}, while extant, is weaker than an adult\u8217's {\i get} \u8212- and therefore the comparison is made to the adult\u8217's {\i get}, to convey that the minor\u8217's {\i chalitza} rises to the level of an adult\u8217's {\i get}, even if the minor\u8217's own {\i get} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Equals Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua resolves this: {\i ait lei vezutar} \u8212- the minor does have a {\i get}, but it is weaker than an adult\u8217's {\i get}. The relevance of this distinction becomes clear in the context of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling that a second {\i get} after a first {\i get} carries no legal effect ({\i ein get achar get}). According to Rabban Gamliel, this rule applies symmetrically \u8212- adult after adult, minor after minor \u8212- but an adult\u8217's {\i get} following a minor\u8217's {\i get} would be effective, since they are not equivalent. Conversely, among the Rabbis who hold that a second {\i get} after a first {\i get} is effective, this too applies symmetrically \u8212- adult after adult, minor after minor \u8212- but a minor\u8217's {\i get} after an adult\u8217's is not effective, since the minor\u8217's act is weaker.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acts Correspondence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic can now be articulated cleanly. {\i Bi\u8217'ah} and {\i chalitza} are the two biblical acts pertaining to {\i yibum}; {\i maamar} and {\i get} are their rabbinic counterparts. For a minor, the biblical acts \u8212- when performed by the minor \u8212- carry the legal weight of the corresponding rabbinic acts performed by an adult. Hence, the minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} is equated to an adult\u8217's {\i maamar}, and the minor\u8217's {\i chalitza} is equated to an adult\u8217's {\i get}. This is what it means to say that a minor\u8217's {\i chalitza} is like an adult\u8217's {\i get}: it is not comparing his {\i chalitza} to his own {\i get} (which would be weaker), but to the full strength of an adult\u8217's {\i get}. The logic preserves the parallel structure of biblical and rabbinic acts while accounting for the diminished legal capacity of the minor.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Second Minor\u8217's Bia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Minor Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now addresses a further case: if a first minor has {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i yevamah}, and then a second minor \u8212- also nine years old and one day \u8212- likewise has {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her, does the second minor\u8217's act disqualify the first? The question is whether the logic that allows one minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} to disqualify an adult brother (as established above) applies equally when both parties are minors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Disqualification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related case: the same minor who had {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i yevamah} then also has {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her {\i tzarah} (co-wife). If his first act was quasi-effective (analogous to {\i maamar}), then his second act with the co-wife should, by the same logic, disqualify himself from completing {\i yibum} with the first \u8212- since one can build only one household ({\i bayit echad hu boneh}) and cannot do {\i yibum} with two wives. The {\i mishnah} indicates that in this case, {\i posel al yedei atzmo} \u8212- he disqualifies himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees with both cases. His position, explained in the {\i baraita}, is: {\i im biah rishonah biah, biah sheniyah einah biah} \u8212- if the first act was a full, binding {\i bi\u8217'ah}, then the second act is simply not a {\i bi\u8217'ah} at all (since the matter is already settled). And {\i v\u8217'im biah rishonah einah biah, biah sheniyah nami einah biah} \u8212- if the first act was not a full, binding {\i bi\u8217'ah}, then the second act is likewise not a {\i bi\u8217'ah}. Rabbi Shimon rejects the intermediate, fifty-percent position entirely. For him, the minor\u8217's act is either one hundred percent effective or zero percent effective \u8212- there is no quasi-status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All-or-Nothing Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This all-or-nothing position has decisive implications. When two minors each perform {\i bi\u8217'ah}, or when the same minor performs {\i bi\u8217'ah} with both co-wives, Rabbi Shimon reasons that the second act cannot do anything the first act did not already do. If the first act was fully effective, it is already settled and the second act is irrelevant. If the first act was not effective, the second act is equally ineffective \u8212- one cannot accumulate two zero-percent acts into a meaningful result. Therefore, Rabbi Shimon rules that neither a second minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} nor the same minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the co-wife creates any additional disqualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes: {\i matnitin de\u8217'lo k\u8217'Ben Azzai} \u8212- the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling (against Rabbi Shimon) is not in accordance with Ben Azzai. Ben Azzai teaches that {\i maamar} after {\i maamar} by two different brothers ({\i yevamim}) is effective \u8212- the second brother\u8217's {\i maamar} disqualifies the first \u8212- but {\i maamar} after {\i maamar} by the same brother with two different {\i yevamot} is not effective: having done {\i maamar} with one co-wife does not disqualify him from the other. Translating to the present case: the minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} with one wife followed by {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the co-wife should, according to Ben Azzai\u8217's logic, not disqualify him from the first wife. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that he does disqualify himself therefore runs contrary to Ben Azzai\u8217's principle.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor Dies & Yevama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complex Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} presents a more intricate scenario. The minor \u8212- nine years old and one day \u8212- performs {\i bi\u8217'ah} with his {\i yevamah}. Thereafter, once he has grown to adulthood, he marries another woman. He then dies without having consummated {\i yibum} with the {\i yevamah} as an adult (he had only the act as a minor, which was equivalent to {\i maamar}, not full {\i yibum}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zikat Shnei Yevamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates the unusual situation of {\i zikat shnei yevamim} \u8212- a dual {\i zika} (brother-in-law connection) encumbering the first woman. The surviving brothers are bound to the {\i yevamah} through two separate obligations: the original biblical obligation arising from her first husband (say, Er), and a rabbinic obligation arising from the minor\u8217's quasi-{\i maamar}-level {\i bi\u8217'ah} (say, Onan). These two {\i zikot} are of different legal grades \u8212- one {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, one {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- and they coexist because the minor\u8217's act, being only rabbinic in weight, never displaced the underlying biblical {\i zika} of the first husband. This situation would never arise when a full adult performs {\i yibum}, since a fully consummated act of {\i yibum} is entirely {\i d\u8217'oraisa} and completely absorbs and resolves the original {\i zika}. It is the uniquely intermediate character of the minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} that creates this dual-{\i zika} situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The surviving brother (Shelah, in this illustration) therefore faces obligations to two women: the {\i yevamah} from the first husband (Tamar, from Er), carrying {\i zikat shnei yevamim}, and the second wife the minor married as an adult (say, Devorah), to whom he has a straightforward {\i d\u8217'oraisa yibum} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Woman Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i harishona choletzet v\u8217'lo mityabemet} \u8212- with respect to the first woman (who carries the dual {\i zika}), the surviving brother may only perform {\i chalitza}, not {\i yibum}. This is because her status is complicated by the dual obligation: she stands in the {\i yibum} relationship to him both through the biblical {\i zika} of her original husband and through the rabbinic {\i zika} of the minor. Performing {\i yibum} with a woman in this doubly encumbered state is not permissible; only {\i chalitza} will suffice. {\i Ve\u8217'shniya o choletzet o mityabemet} \u8212- with the second wife (who has a straightforward {\i yibum} obligation), the brother may choose either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon, consistent with his all-or-nothing position, rules differently: {\i miyabem eizo sheyirtze, v\u8217'choletz lashniya} \u8212- he may perform {\i yibum} with whichever woman he chooses and then {\i chalitza} with the other. According to Rabbi Shimon, the minor\u8217's act is either fully effective or entirely null. If it was null, then Tamar stands only in the {\i zika} of Er, and there is no complicating dual encumbrance; she is no different from any other {\i yevamah}. If the minor\u8217's act was fully effective, then Tamar was fully Onan\u8217's wife, and the surviving brothers face two distinct and independent {\i yibum} obligations \u8212- one per wife \u8212- and must perform {\i yibum} with one and {\i chalitza} with the other, but there is no reason {\i yibum} cannot be done with either. The one thing Rabbi Shimon concedes is that {\i yibum} cannot be performed with both women, since it is possible that the minor\u8217's act was fully effective (meaning Onan had two full wives), and one may perform {\i yibum} with only one of them. Therefore he must do {\i yibum} with one and {\i chalitza} with the other \u8212- but he has free choice as to which.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Adult Without Puberty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pubic Hair Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} closes with a brief but significant ruling: {\i echad shehu ben tesha shanim veyom echad, v\u8217'echad shehu ben esrim shelo hevi shtei sa\u8217'arot} \u8212- both the nine-year-old and a twenty-year-old who has not yet developed two pubic hairs are governed by the same {\i halacha}. The physiological sign of adulthood \u8212- the presence of two pubic hairs \u8212- is the operative criterion, not chronological age alone. An individual who has reached twenty years of age but lacks this sign of physical maturity retains the same {\i halachic} status as a minor of nine years and one day, with all the attendant consequences for the laws of {\i yibum} discussed throughout this {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 97\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Underage Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bi\u8217'ah of Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the middle of 97b. The preceding {\i Mishnah} had established a case involving a young man who is nine years and a day old or more \u8212- past the threshold of {\i bi\u8217'ah} taking legal effect, yet still before {\i bar mitzvah} \u8212- whose act of intercourse with his sister-in-law functions as a kind of {\i ma\u8217'amar} but does not constitute {\i yibum} in the full halachic sense. The scenario that follows is this: if such an underage man performs {\i bi\u8217'ah} with his sister-in-law and subsequently dies, the next surviving brother faces what the {\i Mishnah} calls {\i zekat shnei yevamim} \u8212- an obligation to two sisters-in-law simultaneously. The first {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, deriving from the original marriage that was never actualized through proper {\i yibum}, remains outstanding; and a second, {\i derabbanan} obligation to the deceased minor\u8217's quasi-wife also attaches. The result is that the surviving brother is not permitted to perform {\i yibum} at all, and instead {\i chalitzah} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Co-Wife Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens its analysis with Rabbah\u8217's clarification: {\i Amar Rabbah: Hada amar rabbanan zekat shnei yevamim mechelatz chaletz yevamim lo meyabbem} \u8212- the rabbinic rule that a woman bearing two {\i zekot} from two brothers-in-law requires {\i chalitzah} and not {\i yibum}. Rabbah stresses that one should not think this ruling is limited to cases where there is a co-wife. The prior discussions in an earlier {\i perek} had involved a co-wife scenario, and one might have supposed that the restriction was tied specifically to that concern. Rabbah therefore insists: {\i lo teima heicha de\u8217'ika tzara}, do not say it applies only where there is a co-wife. In our case \u8212- {\i leka tzara}, there is no co-wife \u8212- and the rule of {\i chalitzah} rather than {\i yibum} still applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make the case concrete: using the paradigm of Er, Onan, and Shelah \u8212- if Onan, as an underage {\i kattan}, performed {\i bi\u8217'ah} with Tamar, this creates a {\i derabbanan} quasi-marriage but not a {\i de\u8217'oraita yibum}. When Onan then dies, Shelah\u8217's obligation to Tamar has two dimensions: a {\i de\u8217'oraita} obligation stemming from Er\u8217's genuine marriage to her, which was never resolved through {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}; and a {\i derabbanan} obligation stemming from Onan\u8217's quasi-marriage. Because Onan\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} was not {\i de\u8217'oraita}, it did not displace Er from the picture. Tamar therefore stands under two {\i zekot}, and the {\i halacha} is {\i chalitzah}, not {\i yibum}. In this version of the case there is no co-wife at all \u8212- that is Rabbah\u8217's point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Appearance Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then is the rationale for the restriction in the co-wife case that Rabbah is distinguishing from? In that scenario, where husband number two had done {\i ma\u8217'amar} and was also married to another woman, the concern is one of appearances. If the surviving brother performs {\i yibum} with the first woman \u8212- legitimately, on account of the {\i de\u8217'oraita} obligation from husband number one \u8212- it will appear as though he has performed {\i yibum} with two wives of the same deceased husband. People will not understand that one {\i yibum} is being done on account of Er and the other would have been on account of Onan. The obvious resolution of doing {\i yibum} with one and {\i chalitzah} with the other is itself rejected, because regardless of which direction one goes, the appearance of {\i yibum} with two women of the same deceased man will arise. The restriction on {\i yibum} in the co-wife case is therefore driven by this appearance concern. Rabbah\u8217's contribution is to establish that even in the absence of a co-wife \u8212- where that concern does not apply \u8212- the rule of {\i chalitzah} rather than {\i yibum} holds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regular Marriage Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then adds a brief but important note: if this {\i kattan} had simply contracted a normal marriage \u8212- {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin} \u8212- with a woman, and then died, that marriage carries no legal weight and the woman has no obligation of {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum} with his brothers whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Capacity & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shotim Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point is underscored by a {\i tannaic} teaching: {\i shoteh} \u8212- a person of unsound mind \u8212- and {\i katan} \u8212- a minor \u8212- who contracted marriage and died: {\i neshoteihem peturot min hachalitzah umin hayibum}, their wives are entirely exempt from both {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}. The marriage of a {\i shoteh} or {\i katan} carries no halachic recognition. The {\i Gemara} notes that the classic triad of those lacking legal capacity \u8212- {\i shoteh}, {\i katan}, and {\i cheresh} \u8212- is only partially represented here, since the Rabbis did institute {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin} for a {\i cheresh} at least on a rabbinic level, which is why the {\i cheresh} is treated differently and not mentioned in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Underage Kiddushin (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now takes up a case where the underage Onan did {\i bi\u8217'ah} with Tamar but was also married to Devorah. If he subsequently comes of age and consummates his relationship with Tamar as an adult, she becomes a genuine wife alongside Devorah. But if he did not consummate the relationship with Tamar as an adult and then dies \u8212- Devorah is a real wife, and Shelah may perform {\i yibum} with her; Tamar, however, was only rabbinically acquired, and Shelah must do {\i chalitzah} with her. The reason Tamar\u8217's {\i chalitzah} cannot be replaced by {\i yibum} with Devorah alone \u8212- the normal resolution in a {\i zekat shnei yevamim} scenario \u8212- is that the {\i de\u8217'oraita} obligation stemming from Er has never been resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts the {\i Gemara} to ask from an earlier passage: if the {\i bi\u8217'ah} of a {\i kattan} functions like a {\i ma\u8217'amar begadol}, then by analogy to the {\i ma\u8217'amar} case, shouldn\u8217't the surviving brother be required to do {\i chalitzah} with both Tamar and Devorah, not {\i yibum} with Devorah and {\i chalitzah} with Tamar?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: Lo asu biat ben tesha kemamar begadol} \u8212- Rav Ashi says that the Sages did not equate the {\i bi\u8217'ah} of a nine-year-old fully with a {\i ma\u8217'amar} by an adult. It carries legal weight in various respects \u8212- enough to disqualify the brothers and produce other halachic consequences \u8212- but it is weaker than a full {\i ma\u8217'amar}, and therefore Devorah is not displaced from the picture. Shelah can still perform {\i yibum} with Devorah. Shmuel disagrees: {\i Ushemuel amar asu va\u8217'asu} \u8212- the {\i bi\u8217'ah} of the {\i kattan} is fully equivalent to a {\i ma\u8217'amar begadol}. Rabbi Yochanan likewise: {\i vechan amar Rabbi Yochanan asu va\u8217'asu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then reframes the apparent contradiction between two {\i mishnayot} \u8212- one requiring {\i chalitzah} with both women, the other permitting {\i yibum} with the co-wife \u8212- not as a question about the strength of the minor\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'ah} relative to an adult\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'amar}, but as a {\i machloket tanna\u8217'im}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna} of the {\i Perek Arba Achim}, who rules that the co-wife also requires {\i chalitzah}, made a {\i gezeirah} on account of the co-wife scenario: {\i gazar mishum tzara}. He ruled that {\i yibum} is entirely prohibited in order to prevent the appearance of {\i yibum} being performed with two women simultaneously. This {\i tanna} would say the same {\i halacha} whether the deceased second husband had performed a {\i ma\u8217'amar} as an adult or was a {\i kattan} whose {\i bi\u8217'ah} is the equivalent \u8212- the {\i gezeirah} applies equally. The {\i tanna} of our {\i Mishnah}, by contrast, does not make this {\i gezeirah} at all. He permits {\i yibum} with the co-wife Devorah even while requiring {\i chalitzah} with Tamar. This {\i tanna} would similarly permit {\i yibum} with a co-wife even in the adult {\i ma\u8217'amar} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is striking: the real dispute between the two {\i mishnayot} is not whether {\i bi\u8217'ah} of a {\i kattan} equals {\i ma\u8217'amar begadol} \u8212- both {\i tanna\u8217'im} would treat them identically \u8212- but whether one makes a {\i gezeirah} to prevent {\i yibum} entirely when there is a co-wife. Each {\i tanna} applies his view equally to both {\i kattan} and {\i gadol} cases; the apparent difference in the {\i mishnayot} arises only because each {\i tanna} chose the example most directly relevant to his discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Attribution Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teaching Without Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar, the student of Rabbi Yochanan, went and presented this entire analysis \u8212- the framing of the contradiction as a {\i machloket tanna\u8217'im} \u8212- in the {\i beit midrash}. However, {\i v\u8217'lo amrah mishmei d\u8217'Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- he did not attribute the teaching to Rabbi Yochanan. When Rabbi Yochanan learned of this, {\i v\u8217'ikpad} \u8212- he was deeply offended that his {\i Torah} was being transmitted without his name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tiberias Synagogue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Assi came to calm Rabbi Yochanan down. They invoked the famous episode of the synagogue in Tiberias. A dispute had arisen there between Rabbi Elazar and Rabbi Yose concerning a bolt \u8212- {\i nagar sheyesh b\u8217'rosho glustra} \u8212- a door-bolt that has a rounded bulb at its end, making it usable for grinding and thus more of a {\i kli}, which would reduce concerns of {\i binyan} and {\i muktzah}. The question was whether one may insert it to lock a door on {\i Shabbat}. The debate between the two Sages became so heated that, while it was not intentional, a {\i sefer Torah} was torn in the tumult \u8212- {\i shenikra sefer Torah b\u8217'chamatan}. Rabbi Yose ben Kisma, who was present, declared: {\i temei\u8217'ani im lo yihyeh beit haknesset zu avodah zarah} \u8212- I would be utterly astonished if this synagogue does not ultimately become a house of idolatry. {\i V\u8217'chein havah} \u8212- and so it came to pass.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point was plain: it is not worth allowing a dispute to escalate to such destructive ends. But when Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Assi made this comparison, it only made matters worse. {\i Hadar ikpeid zina achrina} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan became still more angry, asking: {\i chavruta nami?} \u8212- are you comparing me to their situation, as if Rabbi Elazar is my colleague and peer? That dispute was between equals. Rabbi Elazar is my student. I have every right to be treated differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov bar Idi then came to try his hand at the matter. He cited the verse from Yehoshua: {\i Ka\u8217'asher tzivah Hashem et Moshe avdo kein tzivah Moshe et Yehoshua vecheyn asah Yehoshua, lo hesir davar mikol asher tzivah Hashem et Moshe}. The verse emphasizes how scrupulously Yehoshua carried out everything Moshe commanded. But, Rabbi Yaakov bar Idi asked: do you imagine that every time Yehoshua taught, he explicitly declared, \u8220"Here is what Moshe told me\u8221"? {\i Ela Yehoshua yoshev vedoresh stam} \u8212- Yehoshua would simply teach {\i Torah} without constant attribution. {\i Vehakol yod\u8217'im sheToraso shel Moshe hi} \u8212- everyone understood that everything he taught came from Moshe. The verse\u8217's very emphasis on his fidelity to Moshe demonstrated precisely that he did not need to label each teaching explicitly, because his role as the faithful channel of Moshe\u8217's {\i Torah} was self-evident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Af Rabbi Elazar talmidcha yoshev vedoresh stam} \u8212- similarly, your student Rabbi Elazar teaches without attribution, {\i vehakol yod\u8217'im sheshelcha hi} \u8212- and everyone knows that his {\i Torah} is yours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This argument succeeded where the earlier attempt had failed. Rabbi Yochanan said to Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Assi: {\i mipnei mah ein atem yod\u8217'in lepayeis keven Idi chaveireinu} \u8212- why did you not know how to appease me as Ben Idi does?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immortality via Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then explains what lay at the root of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's concern \u8212- and it was not mere pride. {\i De\u8217'amar Rabbi Yehuda amar Rav}: what does David mean when he says {\i agurah be\u8217'aholcha olamim} \u8212- I will dwell in Your tent in two worlds? Can a person actually dwell in two worlds? Rather, David was praying: {\i sheyomru davar shemuah mipi ba\u8217'olam hazeh} \u8212- that even after his death, people in this world would transmit his teachings in his name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for why this matters: {\i De\u8217'amar Rabbi Yochanan mishum Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai: kol talmid chacham she\u8217'omrim davar shemuah mipi ba\u8217'olam hazeh siftosav dovvot bakever} \u8212- any {\i talmid chacham} whose {\i Torah} is transmitted in his name in this world, his lips continue to murmur in the grave. The continuity of attribution is not about honor; it is about a form of continued presence in this world, a way of remaining alive while dwelling in the world to come. {\i Amar Rabbi Yitzchak ben Zeira} \u8212- and what is the verse? {\i Vecheichech kayayin hatov holeich ledodi lemeisharim doveiv sifsei yeshenim} \u8212- and your palate is like fine wine, going straight to my beloved, causing the lips of those who sleep to murmur. {\i Kechomer shel anavim} \u8212- like a press of grapes: the moment one lays a finger upon pressed grapes, they immediately flow. So too with the {\i Torah} of {\i talmidei chachamim} \u8212- the moment it is spoken in their name, their lips begin to murmur in the grave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's concern was not vanity but this: the transmission of {\i Torah} in a teacher\u8217's name is the mechanism by which a {\i talmid chacham} remains present in this world even after death. When his student taught his insight without attribution, Rabbi Yochanan felt that something vital had been severed \u8212- not his reputation, but his continued living connection to this world. The {\i Gemara} notes that the {\i pasuk} in {\i Shir Hashirim} invoked here is also from Shlomo, making it in some sense an apt citation in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Capacity & Status (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saris Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the question of the {\i kattan} who reaches the age of twenty without having developed pubic hair. The {\i Mishnah} treats such a person as still legally a {\i kattan}, still somewhat within the categories of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}. But a {\i baraita} seems to say otherwise: {\i ben esrim shana shelo hevi shtei searot} \u8212- if a twenty-year-old has not produced two pubic hairs, {\i yaviu reaya shehu ben esrim vehu hasaris} \u8212- bring proof of his age, and once it is established that he is twenty and lacks the hair, he is classified as the {\i saris} of whom the Rabbis spoke \u8212- {\i lo choletz velo meyabem} \u8212- exempt entirely from {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}. This would apparently undercut the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's premise that a twenty-year-old without pubic hair still functions as a {\i kattan} in these matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i Amar Rav Shmuel bar Yitzchak amar Rav: vehu shenoldu lo simanei saris} \u8212- it is not sufficient that the person be twenty and lack pubic hair; he must also manifest specific physiological signs of being a {\i saris}, which were discussed in earlier passages. {\i Amar Rava: deika nami d\u8217'katani vehu hasaris} \u8212- Rava notes that the {\i baraita} itself says \u8220"and he is the {\i saris},\u8221" implying that additional identifying characteristics are required beyond age alone. The {\i Mishnah} therefore governs the case of a twenty-year-old who lacks pubic hair but does not display those other signs \u8212- such a person is still treated as a {\i kattan} who may yet reach maturity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: if a person never develops those other signs of a {\i saris}, at what point must we conclude that he will never achieve biological maturity? {\i Tani d\u8217'vei Rebbi Chiya: ad rov shnotav} \u8212- until the majority of his years. Since seventy years is assumed to be the typical lifespan, once a person passes approximately thirty-six, he is presumed a {\i saris} even without the other specific indicators.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical dimension follows. When such cases were brought before Rava, he would not immediately declare the man a {\i saris}. If the person appeared lean and underfed, {\i amar lehu zilu avriyuhu} \u8212- Rava would say: go and feed him properly first. Malnutrition can prevent the development of pubic hair just as it prevents menstruation in women who are severely undernourished. Conversely, if the man was overweight, {\i amar lehu zilu acchishuhu} \u8212- Rava would prescribe an exercise regimen to reduce his weight. {\i D\u8217'hanei simanei zimnin d\u8217'nafli machamat kachishuta vezimnin d\u8217'nafli machamat briyuta} \u8212- these signs sometimes fail to appear due to being underfed, and sometimes due to being overfed. Only after addressing those external factors could a proper determination be made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ha-Isha Rabba End\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach Ha\u8217'isha Rabba} \u8212- and with that, the {\i perek} known as {\i Ha\u8217'isha Rabba} comes to its conclusion. It is called {\i Ha\u8217'isha Rabba} \u8212- the \u8220"great\u8221" {\i Ha\u8217'isha} \u8212- to distinguish it from a later chapter also beginning with {\i Ha\u8217'isha}, which is called {\i Ha\u8217'isha Shalom}. It was a rich and challenging {\i perek}, covering the case of the husband who returned from afar, then pivoting to the wife in the analogous situation, and ultimately returning to the core discussions of {\i yibum} centered on the underage young man whose {\i bi\u8217'ah} generates such complex legal consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oneis & Mefutah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Marital Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the opening of the new {\i perek}, the discussion shifts from {\i yibum} to the subject of familial prohibitions arising from non-marital sexual relations. The {\i Mishnah} opens: {\i nosein al ha-anusa ve\u8217'al ha-mefuta} \u8212- one may marry a woman who is related to him only through an act of rape or seduction, meaning through a sexual relationship that was not a marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is this: the prohibitions of wife\u8217's sister, wife\u8217's mother, and wife\u8217's daughter are predicated on the existence of a {\i wife} \u8212- on there having been a marriage. These prohibited relationships are defined by the man\u8217's marriage to the first woman. If no marriage took place, if the man merely had sexual relations with a woman outside of marriage \u8212- labeled by the {\i Mishnah} using the legal categories of {\i anusa} or {\i mefuta}, rape or seduction, since these are the two cases the Torah explicitly addresses involving non-marital intercourse \u8212- then the relational prohibitions that attach through wifehood do not apply. He may marry her sister, mother, or daughter. This is the rule of {\i nosein al ha-anusa}: when the first relationship was not a marriage, the subsequent relationship with the relative is permitted {\i de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then considers cases involving not the man\u8217's own wife but his father\u8217's or son\u8217's relationships. The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds: {\i nosei adam anusat aviv u-mefutat aviv} \u8212- a man may marry a woman his father merely slept with, since she was never the father\u8217's wife. Similarly, {\i anusat beno u-mefutat beno} \u8212- he may marry a woman his son merely slept with, since she was never the son\u8217's wife. The prohibition of stepmother and daughter-in-law requires an actual marriage; without marriage, the relationship does not generate the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees in one specific case: {\i Rabbi Yehuda oser be-anusat aviv u-mefutat aviv} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda prohibits a man from marrying a woman his father had sexual relations with, even outside of marriage. This exception for the father\u8217's partner, while not shared by the {\i Tanna Kamma}, has some resonance with the prophetic condemnation in Amos, where father and son going to the same woman is condemned as a profound moral degradation \u8212- even if it does not rise to the level of a formal {\i issur} of {\i arayot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} makes the principle more explicit: {\i oneis isha mutar liseis bita} \u8212- if a man had forced sexual relations with a woman, he is permitted to marry her daughter. The lack of marriage means that the woman-daughter chain of relationship never became a prohibited chain of {\i she\u8217'erah} \u8212- biological or marital kinship of the relevant type.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suspected Affair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises an apparent challenge from a different {\i baraita}: {\i ha-nichshad min ha-isha assur be-imah u-ve-vitah ve-achotah} \u8212- a man suspected of having an affair with a woman is prohibited from marrying her mother, daughter, or sister. This seems to contradict the principle just stated. If a man who actually slept with a woman may marry her relatives, why should a man {\i suspected} of an affair be {\i more} restricted?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that this prohibition is {\i mi-derabbanan} \u8212- rabbinic, not biblical. And the rationale is not difficult to see: if the suspicion has some basis, and the man proceeds to marry the woman\u8217's mother or daughter, it is likely that he will continue his relationship with the original woman after the marriage. At that point, he will be guilty of a biblical prohibition \u8212- sleeping with his wife\u8217's mother or daughter. The Rabbis therefore prohibited the marriage preemptively to prevent it from leading to a {\i de\u8217'oraita} violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But would the {\i Mishnah} then use the language of {\i nosein l\u8217'chatchilah} \u8212- permitted outright \u8212- in a case where there is at least a rabbinic concern? The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i ki tnan matnisin le-achar misah} \u8212- our {\i Mishnah} is speaking of a case where the first woman has already died. Once she is dead, the concern that the man will continue sleeping with her no longer applies, and the rabbinic prohibition accordingly falls away. He may freely marry her mother, daughter, or sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Removes Ties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a clarification of the overall structure. The fundamental biblical scheme is: first there is a marriage to woman one, and then an act of sex with woman two \u8212- the wife\u8217's sister, mother, or daughter \u8212- is what is prohibited. The {\i Mishnah} establishes that the reverse \u8212- first sex with woman one, then marriage with woman two \u8212- generates no {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition, since the relational chain begins with sex rather than marriage. The {\i baraita} about suspected affairs then adds that even this permitted path is rabbinically restricted during the first woman\u8217's lifetime, lest it lead to the prohibited reverse scenario. Once the first woman dies, however, even the rabbinic concern dissolves entirely, and the marriage is fully permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} employs the legal terminology of {\i anusa} and {\i mefuta} because those are the two categories through which the Torah addresses non-marital intercourse. In practice, for the purposes of this {\i halacha}, the distinction between them is irrelevant \u8212- both simply denote sexual relations outside of marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kichah vs.\u160 ?Shichvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terminology Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now investigates the textual basis for the entire framework: why does the prohibition depend on whether the first relationship was a marriage? {\i De-tanu rabbanan: be-chulhu ne\u8217'emar shichivah} \u8212- by all the {\i arayot}, the Torah uses the language of {\i shichivah} \u8212- lying with, i.e., sexual intercourse. {\i Ve-khan ne\u8217'emar kichah} \u8212- but in the specific cases of wife, mother, and daughter-in-law (and sister), the Torah uses the language of {\i kichah} \u8212- taking, i.e., marriage. Rashi points to the verse {\i ish ki yikach et ishah ve-et imah} \u8212- a man who {\i takes} a woman and her mother \u8212- and to {\i ve-achoto lo tikach} \u8212- his sister he shall not {\i take}. The Rabbis derive from this terminological distinction: {\i derech likuchin asra Torah} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's prohibition operates through the framework of marriage. Where the text says {\i kichah}, the operative relationship is a marital one; only once marriage is in place do the prohibited acts of {\i shichivah} with the relatives come into force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then poses a sharp question to Abaye: if the use of {\i kichah} in these passages means the prohibition is triggered only through marriage, consider the prohibition of a man\u8217's sister, where the verse reads {\i ish asher yikach et achoto bat aviv o vat imo} \u8212- a man who {\i takes} his sister. Would one say, by the same logic, that it is only prohibited to {\i marry} one\u8217's sister, but sexual relations with her outside of marriage \u8212- {\i derech shichivah} \u8212- would be permitted? Clearly not. So how can {\i kichah} be the operative trigger?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Determines\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i likuchin ketivah be-Torah stam} \u8212- the word {\i kichah} in the Torah is generic; it can mean marriage or, depending on context, simply an act of sexual taking. {\i Ra\u8217'uy likichah} \u8212- where the woman in question is one who could in principle be married, {\i kichah} means marriage in the full sense. This applies to the cases of wife, mother, and daughter-in-law, where a prior legitimate marriage is what establishes the relational framework. {\i Ra\u8217'uy lishchivah} \u8212- where the woman is one who could never be married \u8212- as with one\u8217's sister, where marriage is simply impossible halachically \u8212- then {\i kichah} in that context means {\i shichivah}, sexual intercourse. The prohibition against one\u8217's sister attaches to any act of intercourse, not to a marital act that can never occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The approach is admittedly not perfectly clean \u8212- {\i kichah} does not always mean what it most naturally means \u8212- but the distinction is workable: in cases where marriage is a meaningful legal possibility, {\i kichah} means marriage; where it is not, {\i kichah} is read as referring to sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Granddaughter Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conflicting Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now examines the case of a granddaughter through two apparently contradictory verses. The verse in Vayikra reads: {\i ervat bat bincha o vat bitcha lo tegaleh} \u8212- do not expose the nakedness of your son\u8217's daughter or your daughter\u8217's daughter. The plain sense seems to refer to one\u8217's own biological granddaughter. A separate verse reads: {\i ervat ishah u-vitah lo tegaleh et bat benah ve-et bat bitah lo tikach} \u8212- you may not expose the nakedness of a woman and her daughter, and you shall not take her son\u8217's daughter or her daughter\u8217's daughter. This second verse refers to {\i the wife\u8217's} granddaughter \u8212- a step-granddaughter who is not biologically the man\u8217's own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha ketsad? Kan be-onsin kan be-nisuin} \u8212- how do we reconcile these? The first verse prohibits one\u8217's own biological granddaughter, and that prohibition exists regardless of marriage \u8212- it is a bond of {\i she\u8217'er}, of flesh and biological kinship. The second verse prohibits the {\i wife\u8217's} granddaughter \u8212- but only when there is a marriage to the grandmother. If the relationship with the grandmother was only sexual and not marital, the wife\u8217's granddaughter is not a step-granddaughter in any meaningful sense and is therefore permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether to read the verses in reverse \u8212- prohibiting the wife\u8217's granddaughter in cases of non-marital relations and perhaps permitting the step-granddaughter in cases of marriage. But the {\i Gemara} rejects this: the prohibitions of {\i arayot} use the term {\i she\u8217'er} \u8212- {\i she\u8217'erah henah}, the flesh of one\u8217's flesh. {\i Be-nisuin ika she\u8217'er} \u8212- in marriage, a step-relationship carries a degree of {\i she\u8217'er}, even if it is relational rather than biological. {\i U-ve-onsin leka she\u8217'er} \u8212- in a purely sexual relationship outside of marriage, the granddaughter of that woman is not one\u8217's biological granddaughter, is not one\u8217's step-granddaughter, and cannot be described as {\i she\u8217'er} in any meaningful sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheir Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i she\u8217'er} thus does the decisive work in this analysis. Biological kinship always carries {\i she\u8217'er}; marital kinship generates a kind of derivative {\i she\u8217'er} through the step-relationship; but a purely sexual relationship, absent marriage, generates no {\i she\u8217'er} at all. The entire framework of what is prohibited and what is permitted in cases of non-marital relations flows from this principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father & Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomeret Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records that Rabbi Yose prohibits a man from marrying a woman his father had sexual relations with \u8212- {\i asar be-anusat aviv} \u8212- a position aligned with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view in the {\i Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knaf Aviv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Gidel cites Rav in explanation of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position: {\i de-chtiv lo yikach ish et eshet aviv ve-lo yigaleh knaf aviv} \u8212- the verse in Devarim first prohibits marrying one\u8217's stepmother, and then adds the prohibition of {\i lo yigaleh knaf aviv} \u8212- do not uncover the hem of the father. The {\i knaf ha-av} \u8212- the father\u8217's hem \u8212- refers to the nakedness that the father himself saw: the woman with whom the father had sexual relations. {\i Lo yigaleh} \u8212- this man may not have sexual relations with that woman, even if she was never formally the father\u8217's wife. This is Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's source for his stricter position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis, however, reject this reading. {\i Ei hava samich leh kedekamarat} \u8212- if the prohibition of {\i knaf aviv} were directly juxtaposed to the earlier passage dealing with rape and payment to the father \u8212- {\i venatan ha\u8217'ish hashochev imah la\u8217'avi ha-na\u8217'ara chamishim kasef} \u8212- one could concede that the context establishes that {\i knaf aviv} refers to a woman the father merely had sexual relations with. But {\i hashta d\u8217'lo samich leh} \u8212- since those passages appear much earlier in the text and are not immediately adjacent, the juxtaposition argument fails. Instead, the Rabbis read the verse in accordance with {\i Rav Anan} in the name of Shmuel: {\i beshomeret yavam shel aviv hakatuv medaber} \u8212- the verse is speaking of a {\i shomeret yavam}, a woman who stands in a quasi-marital {\i zekah} relationship with the father, awaiting {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. The prohibition of {\i lo yigaleh knaf aviv} refers to this woman \u8212- the father\u8217's {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- not to a woman with whom the father merely had casual sexual relations. {\i Knaf harauya l\u8217'aviv} \u8212- the hem appropriate to the father refers to the woman who is fit to be, in some legal sense, the father\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aunt Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises an objection: if the {\i shomeret yavam} of the father is the father\u8217's brother\u8217's wife, then she is already the man\u8217's {\i dodah} \u8212- his aunt \u8212- and as such she is already prohibited under the {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition of {\i arayot}, which carries {\i karet}. Why would one need an additional verse to establish a prohibition that is already in place? {\i La\u8217'avor alav bishnei lavin} \u8212- to add a second {\i lav} to the transgression, yielding two counts of prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, this woman is also forbidden as {\i yevama lashuk} \u8212- any woman who is a {\i shomeret yavam} is prohibited to other men until the {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} is completed. {\i La\u8217'avor alav bishloshav lavin} \u8212- that gives three {\i lavin} simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers a final possible resolution: {\i ve-iba\u8217'it eima le-achar mita} \u8212- or one could say the verse is addressing the case after the father\u8217's death. Once the father has died, she is no longer a {\i yevama lashuk} \u8212- if there was only one brother with the obligation and he has now died, the {\i zekah} lapses. She remains, however, a {\i dodah}, an aunt, and the {\i knaf aviv} prohibition adds a third {\i lav} even at that stage. {\i La\u8217'avor alav bishloshav lavin} \u8212- three counts of prohibition are nonetheless established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Relationship Riddles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stepdaughter Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded the legal analysis, the {\i Gemara} turns to a remarkable series of family-relationship riddles \u8212- puzzles about how a single person can bear multiple overlapping legal relationships to another. These were likely posed as intellectual exercises among {\i talmidei chachamim}, testing their ability to reconstruct unusual genealogical scenarios. As one observer once put it, this is what the Rabbis did before television.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first riddle: {\i achi me\u8217'av velo me\u8217'em, vehu ba\u8217'ala de\u8217'em, va\u8217'ana barta de\u8217'ittateih} \u8212- he is my half-brother on my father\u8217's side but not on my mother\u8217's, he is the husband of my mother, and I am the daughter of his wife. Rashi explains: my father\u8217's son \u8212- my half-brother \u8212- married my mother. He is therefore my half-brother (through the father) and simultaneously my stepfather (through his marriage to my mother). I am his wife\u8217's daughter \u8212- his stepdaughter. And crucially, Rami bar Chama notes that this scenario is only possible {\i d\u8217'lo k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda d\u8217'matnitin} \u8212- if we do not follow Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position in our {\i Mishnah}. Because the father must have had sexual relations with this man\u8217's mother without marrying her \u8212- otherwise the son could never have married his own stepmother. That makes her {\i anusat aviv}, and according to Rabbi Yehuda, this son would be prohibited from marrying her. Only the {\i Tanna Kamma}, who permits {\i anusat aviv}, allows the scenario to work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son-Brother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second riddle: {\i achi v\u8217'breih hu, achateih ana, d\u8217'hai d\u8217'darina aktephai} \u8212- he is my brother and my son; I am his sister; I am the sister of the one I am carrying on my shoulders. In other words: my son is my brother. How? {\i Mishkachat lah be-oved kochavim haba al bito} \u8212- the scenario involves a non-Jew who had sexual relations with his own daughter. Since the discussion of forbidden relations among non-Jews in this context does not carry the same formal prohibitions, the {\i Gemara} constructs these riddles using non-Jewish actors to avoid any implication that a Jew engaged in such conduct. The child born of this union is the mother\u8217's son and simultaneously her sibling, since both share the same father. She is thus the sister of the one she carries.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aunt Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third riddle: {\i shalom lach chavri, bat achatech ana} \u8212- greetings, my friend; I am the daughter of your sister. Meaning, this person\u8217's daughter is his sister\u8217's daughter \u8212- his niece through his sister. {\i Mishkachat lah be-oved kochavim haba al bat bito} \u8212- a non-Jew who had sexual relations with his granddaughter. The child born of this union is simultaneously the grandfather\u8217's daughter \u8212- and therefore the father\u8217's sister \u8212- and the mother\u8217's daughter. The daughter of the mother is therefore the father\u8217's sister\u8217's daughter \u8212- his niece on his sister\u8217's side. The child\u8217's own child stands in the relationship of \u8220"daughter of your sister\u8221" to the grandfather-father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multi-Relation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth riddle is the most elaborate. {\i Baia baia wo wo me\u8217'ach hu av v\u8217'hu ba\u8217'al v\u8217'hu bar ba\u8217'al v\u8217'hu ba\u8217'ala d\u8217'imei va\u8217'ana barta d\u8217'ittei v\u8217'lo yeihiv pita l\u8217'achoi yasmei b\u8217'nei bartei} \u8212- woe, woe, he is a brother and he is a father and he is a husband and he is the son of a husband and he is a stepfather, and I am his wife\u8217's daughter, and yet he will not feed the orphaned brothers who are the sons of his daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishkachat lah be-ovdei kochavim haba al imo, v\u8217'holid minah bat, v\u8217'chazar uva\u8217'al oto bat, v\u8217'chazar zakein uva\u8217'alah v\u8217'holid minah banim} \u8212- a non-Jew had sexual relations with his mother and produced a daughter. He then had sexual relations with that daughter and had children with her. From the perspective of the daughter: this man is her biological father and brother (since he was born of his mother, and she shares the same mother); he is her husband (since he had sexual relations with her); and since his father also had sexual relations with her, he is the son of her husband as well. He is thus simultaneously her brother, father, husband, and husband\u8217's son. And he withholds food from the orphaned sons \u8212- who are at once his children and the sons of his daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cousins Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fifth riddle: {\i ana ve\u8217'at achin, ana ve\u8217'avuch achin, ana ve\u8217'imach achin} \u8212- you and I are siblings; you and your father are siblings; you and your mother are siblings \u8212- meaning, I and your mother are siblings. {\i Mishkachat lah be-ovdei kochavim haba al imo v\u8217'holid minah shetei banot, v\u8217'chazar uva\u8217'al achat meihen v\u8217'holid minah ben} \u8212- a non-Jew had sexual relations with his mother and produced two daughters. He then had sexual relations with one of the daughters and had a son. The other daughter says to the son: you and I are siblings \u8212- we share the same father. I and your father are siblings \u8212- we also share the same father. I and your mother are siblings \u8212- your mother and I are sisters, born of the same mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Cousins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sixth riddle is, by comparison, almost refreshingly simple: {\i ana ve\u8217'at benei achi, ana ve\u8217'avuch benei achi, ana ve\u8217'imach benei achi} \u8212- you and I are the children of siblings (i.e., first cousins); I and your father are first cousins; I and your mother are first cousins. The {\i Gemara} notes that this case does not require any incest scenario at all \u8212- {\i habita nami mishkachat lah} \u8212- you can construct it from entirely regular marriages. For instance, {\i Reuven sheyesh lo shetei banot} \u8212- Reuven has two daughters. Shimon marries one of them. Levi\u8217's son marries the other. Then Shimon\u8217's son says to Levi\u8217's grandson: you and I are first cousins (our mothers are sisters); I and your father are first cousins (he is Levi\u8217's son and I am Shimon\u8217's son, whose mothers were sisters); and I and your mother are first cousins (your mother is Reuven\u8217's daughter, and so is my mother).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 I Am My Grandpa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's series of riddles calls to mind a famous American novelty song \u8212- {\i I Am My Own Grandpa} \u8212- which presents a superficially similar tangle of relationships arising from perfectly legal marriages:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 When I was 23, I was married to a widow who was pretty as can be. This widow had a grown-up daughter who had hair of red. My father fell in love with her and soon they, too, were wed. This made my dad my son-in-law and changed my very life. My daughter was my mother \u8217'cause she was my father\u8217's wife. To complicate the matters, even though it brought me joy, I soon became the father of a bouncing baby boy. My little baby then became a brother-in-law to dad, and so became my uncle, though it made me very sad. For if he was my uncle, then that also made him brother of the widow\u8217's grown-up daughter who of course was my stepmother. Father\u8217's wife then had a son, kept him on the run, and he became my grandchild, for he was my daughter\u8217's son. My wife is now my mother\u8217's mother, and it makes me blue, because although she is my wife, she\u8217's my grandmother too. If my wife is my grandmother, then I\u8217'm her grandchild, and every time I think of it, it nearly drives me wild. For now I have become the strangest case you ever saw. As husband of my grandmother, I am my own grandpa!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The crucial distinction, of course, is that the {\i Gemara}\u8217's riddles required incest \u8212- conducted, in its framing, by non-Jews \u8212- to generate the overlapping relationships, whereas the song achieves its tangle entirely through regular and entirely permitted marriages. {\i Beheter} \u8212- all above board. Though, as is worth noting, in most jurisdictions at present, not all of the marriages involved would be legally permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 99\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brother\u8217's Wife After Con\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger & Brother\u8217's Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a question that has occupied the preceding discussion: may a {\i ger} marry the wife of his biological brother after both have converted? The Gemara has been bringing various proofs, each of which it finds ways to deflect. The current attempt to resolve the matter comes from a {\i baraita} introduced with the formula {\i ta shema}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} opens with the case of {\i ger she-haysa lidato bi-kedushah ve-horato she-lo bi-kedushah} \u8212- a convert whose conception occurred before his mother\u8217's conversion but whose birth occurred after it. The ruling: {\i yesh lo she\u8217'ar ha-em, ve-ein lo she\u8217'ar ha-av} \u8212- there remains a prohibited-relative relationship on the mother\u8217's side, but not on the father\u8217's side. The practical illustration given is {\i nasa achoto min ha-em, yotzi} \u8212- if he marries a woman who is his maternal half-sister (biologically, though not legally, since she was born before their mother converted and the legal bond of kinship was severed), he must divorce her. But if she is only his paternal half-sister, {\i yekayem} \u8212- he may maintain the marriage. What is striking here is that despite the obvious biological incest, the paternal relation does not trouble us. The reason, as the preceding discussion has established, is that relationships on the mother\u8217's side are far more evidently perceived by others as genuine familial bonds, whereas paternal relationships among non-Jews are not perceived in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maternal vs.\u160 ?Paternal Si\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues with a further distinction. {\i Achot ha-av min ha-em, yotzi} \u8212- if his father\u8217's sister is a maternal sibling of his father\u8217's (i.e., the father and the aunt share a mother), he must divorce her. But if the father and his sister are only paternal siblings, {\i yekayem} \u8212- the marriage stands. The same logic operating on the sibling level is extended one generation upward: whether the relation is problematic depends on whether the sibling bond runs through the mother or through the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i achot ha-em min ha-em, yotzi} \u8212- a mother\u8217's maternal sister clearly falls under the prohibition. Then comes the interesting mirror case: {\i achot ha-em min ha-av} \u8212- a mother\u8217's sister who is related to his mother only through their shared father (a paternal sister). Here the mother is one link in the chain, but the sibling bond itself is paternal. {\i Rabbi Meir omer yotzi} \u8212- Rabbi Meir says he must still divorce, on the grounds that {\i kol ervah she-mishum she\u8217'ar ha-em yotzi, mishum ha-av yekayem}: any prohibited relation that involves the maternal line at any point requires dissolution. {\i Chachamim omrim yekayem} \u8212- the Sages, however, hold that what matters is the nature of the sibling bond, and a paternal sibling bond is not treated as a prohibited maternal relationship even when one generation removed through the mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying rationale for why the maternal side generates this prohibition while the paternal side does not is debated by {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot}. {\i Rashi} frames it as a {\i gezeirah}: if we allow this person to maintain a marriage to a woman who was his sister before conversion, we will come to allow him to marry a sister who was born after conversion \u8212- a woman who is legally his sister under Jewish law. {\i Tosafot} rejects the {\i gezeirah} framing and instead argues that the concern is one of appearance ({\i mar\u8217'it ayin}): because the maternal relationship is perceived by onlookers as a genuine sibling bond, allowing such a marriage looks inherently improper. According to {\i Tosafot}, there is no fear of a mistaken legal conclusion, merely of a disturbing social appearance. {\i Tosafot} notes that the practical implications of these two positions differ in certain cases, and takes the appearance-based rationale as more fundamental. The critical point for both opinions is that the concern is triggered specifically by the maternal relation, which is the one that registers publicly as a family bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Sibling Relatio\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The extension to the father\u8217's sibling relationship warrants brief clarification. Even though the case of {\i achot ha-av min ha-em} runs through the father \u8212- the ger\u8217's relation to this woman is via his father\u8217's sibling bond \u8212- {\i Rashi} explains that since the category remains one of sibling, and the sibling bond in question runs through a mother (the father and his sister share a mother), the same sensitivity applies. The logic is that we are still operating within the framework of maternal siblinghood, even if the pathway to the ger runs through his father. This is why Rabbi Meir\u8217's sweeping rule captures it: any {\i ervah} that reaches the convert through a maternal-sibling link requires divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother & Uncle\u8217's Wife P\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} now turns directly to the very question the Gemara has been investigating. {\i U-mutar be-eshet achiv me-imo} \u8212- he is permitted to marry his maternal brother\u8217's wife. This is exactly our question, and the {\i baraita} answers it affirmatively. Even though he may not marry his maternal sister herself, he may marry his maternal brother\u8217's wife \u8212- the reasoning being that while the blood relation on the maternal side is perceived as genuine, the marital extension (his brother\u8217's wife) is one further degree removed, and we are correspondingly more lenient. {\i U-ve-eshet achi aviv} \u8212- he is likewise permitted to marry his uncle\u8217's wife, since again the bond is through marriage rather than blood. {\i Ve-she\u8217'ar kol arayot mutarot lo} \u8212- and all other formerly prohibited relations are now permitted to him. The Gemara specifies that {\i le-atuyei eshet ha-av} \u8212- this includes, for example, his father\u8217's wife, who is no longer legally related to him since his father was a non-Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother-Daughter Marriage\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A striking case follows: {\i nasa isha u-vittah} \u8212- if before his conversion he had been married to both a woman and her daughter, upon converting {\i kones achat u-motzi achat} \u8212- he may keep one of them but must divorce the other. This might seem puzzling: if both women converted along with him, they are no longer legally mother and daughter \u8212- {\i ger she-nitgayyer ke-katan she-nolad dami}, a convert is like a newborn with no prior legal relations. So why can he not keep both? The answer is that this is a case of blood relation \u8212- a biological mother and daughter \u8212- and the perception of that bond is so powerful that we do not permit him to remain married to both simultaneously, even after conversion. The {\i baraita} adds {\i lechatchila lo yichnos} \u8212- he should not enter such an arrangement even initially.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita on Brother\u8217's Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now zeroes in on the section of the {\i baraita} relevant to its inquiry: {\i katani mihat u-mutar be-eshet achiv} \u8212- the {\i baraita} taught that he is permitted to marry his brother\u8217's wife. The Gemara asks: {\i mai lav de-nasvu ke-shehu ger} \u8212- does this not refer to the obvious case where his brother married this woman after the {\i ger} had already converted, making her legally his brother\u8217's wife while the {\i ger} and his brother share a genuine legal bond of siblinghood? If so, this {\i baraita} would directly answer our question in the permissive direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Marriage Resolut\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara deflects: {\i lo, de-nasvu ke-shehu akum} \u8212- no, the {\i baraita} speaks of a woman his brother had married while still a non-Jew. In that case, the brother\u8217's conversion dissolves the marriage, meaning the woman was never legally his brother\u8217's wife under Jewish law. The Gemara has now deployed the same escape it used in every prior case: the {\i baraita} speaks only of a pre-conversion marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Gezerah Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the Gemara adds: {\i mahu de-teima, ligzor ke-shehu akum atu ke-shehu ger} \u8212- one might have thought we should issue a decree prohibiting even a pre-conversion brother\u8217's-wife relationship lest it lead to permitting the post-conversion scenario. {\i Kamashma lan} \u8212- the {\i baraita} teaches us that we make no such decree. The practical upshot of the Gemara\u8217's entire discussion is thus: the evidence from all these cases, when taken together, clearly points to the conclusion that a {\i ger} marrying his biological brother\u8217's wife is permitted at least {\i mi-de-oraita}, and the evidence strongly suggests it is permitted {\i mi-de-rabbanan} as well \u8212- though the Gemara has not arrived at a definitive resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechatchila Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the {\i lechatchila} ruling in the {\i baraita}: {\i nasa isha u-vittah, kones achat u-motzi achat, lechatchila lo yichnos}. The Gemara objects: {\i hashtah mafik mafik, lechatchila mibaya?} \u8212- if he must divorce one of them even after the fact, is it necessary to add that he shouldn\u8217't do it {\i lechatchila}? The Gemara resolves this: the {\i lechatchila} clause does not refer to the mother-daughter case immediately preceding it, but rather to the earlier rulings \u8212- those cases where the Rabbis said he may maintain a marriage {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. On those, the {\i baraita} now adds: even though {\i bedi\u8217'avad} the marriage stands, one should not enter into such a situation {\i lechatchila}, even in something like a paternal sister case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Death & Mother-in\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} concludes with: {\i metah ishto mutar be-chamato} \u8212- after his wife dies, he may marry her mother (the biological mother, now a convert and hence legally no longer his mother-in-law). {\i Ve-ika de-amrei asur be-chamato} \u8212- some versions state that even after the wife\u8217's death he may not marry her. The Gemara identifies these two positions: {\i chada ke-Rabbi Yishmael ve-chada ke-Rabbi Akiva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stringent view follows Rabbi Yishmael, who holds that a man\u8217's mother-in-law remains fully prohibited even after his wife\u8217's death \u8212- {\i chamato le-achar mitah be-isura kayma}, the prohibition does not weaken. Accordingly, {\i le-gabei ger, gazru bei Rabbanan} \u8212- the Rabbis imposed the same rule on the {\i ger}, making no distinction for the post-death scenario. The lenient view follows Rabbi Akiva, who holds that after the wife\u8217's death the prohibition against the mother-in-law weakens \u8212- {\i kalash lei isura}. {\i Tosafot} notes that \u8220"weakened\u8221" for Rabbi Akiva still means a biblical prohibition carrying {\i karet}, but it is no longer a capital offense. Since there is some degree of weakening, and the prohibition applicable to the {\i ger} in any event is only {\i mi-de-rabbanan}, the Rabbis did not extend their decree to the post-death scenario according to this view. {\i Tosafot} concedes that the reasoning is not entirely obvious \u8212- why a non-capital prohibition should generate a more lenient rabbinic stance toward the {\i ger} \u8212- but accepts this as the Gemara\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixed Children Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Women Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah now introduces what is one of the more involved practical constructions in the tractate. {\i Chamesh nashim she-nisarvu vildoseihen} \u8212- five women whose newborn children became mixed up with one another. The scenario is that the women were all in the same location \u8212- a cave, or as one might modernize it, a darkened ward \u8212- and gave birth simultaneously, after which it became impossible to determine whose child was whose. The critical detail is that each of these five women also has an older child whose identity is certain. Thus there are two groups of sons: five young men of known identity, one per mother, and five young men of completely uncertain identity, any one of whom could be the son of any of the five mothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All five of the uncertain-identity sons grow up, marry women, and then die childless. This triggers the laws of {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}: the widows require either levirate marriage or release. The known brothers \u8212- one per mother \u8212- must step forward, but the problem is that nobody knows which of the five mixed sons is his brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rotation & Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's ruling: {\i arba\u8217'ah choltzin le-achat ve-echad meyabbemet} \u8212- for each widow, four of the known brothers perform {\i chalitzah} and one performs {\i yibum}. The rotation proceeds so that for each successive widow, a different known brother is the one who performs {\i yibum}. The Gemara clarifies that this is not the only valid arrangement \u8212- one could in principle have a single man perform {\i yibum} with all five widows \u8212- but the Mishnah teaches that the rotating arrangement is also permitted and perhaps preferable, as each man may thereby happen to perform {\i yibum} with his own sister-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara makes explicit why the rotation is preferable: {\i dilma mistarmia le-didei} \u8212- perhaps by this arrangement each man\u8217's rightful sister-in-law will fall to him, maximizing the chance that each act of {\i yibum} is halakhically optimal. From a purely combinatorial standpoint, if one man performs {\i yibum} with all five, exactly one of the five acts of {\i yibum} will be with his genuine sister-in-law, while the remaining four are simply permitted marriages to unrelated women. With the rotation, one could in a favorable case have all five {\i yibum} acts performed by the correct men \u8212- though one could equally have none done correctly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the widows\u8217' situation: it is worth noting that the Mishnah does not simply require all five known brothers to perform {\i chalitzah} and leave the women as {\i chalutzohs}. Rather, it actively structures a rotation to ensure each woman receives {\i yibum} as well, reflecting a concern either for the woman\u8217's interests or for the performance of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Before Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds an important procedural requirement: {\i vedavka machlatz ve-hadar yibbumei} \u8212- specifically, the {\i chalitzah} from the four must precede the {\i yibum} by the fifth. One may not begin with {\i yibum}: {\i aval yibumei be-reisha la, de-kapaga bi-yevamah la-shuk} \u8212- because if {\i yibum} is performed first, the man might be having relations with a woman who is in fact not his sister-in-law but still requires {\i chalitzah} from someone else to be released. To cohabit with such a woman would constitute {\i yevamah la-shuk}, a violation of her {\i yibbum}-bond without proper resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Brothers\u8217' Chalit\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah continues: {\i miktzatan achin u-miktzatan she-ein achin} \u8212- some of the known brothers are full brothers to some of the mixed sons and mere maternal half-brothers to others. The ruling seems strange at first: {\i ha-achin choltzin ve-she-ein achin meyabbemin} \u8212- the brothers do {\i chalitzah} and the non-brothers do {\i yibum}. The Gemara asks: {\i mai ka-amar} \u8212- what could this possibly mean? A maternal half-brother has no obligation of {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternal-Maternal Mix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Safra explains: {\i miktzat achin min ha-av u-miktzat achin min ha-em} \u8212- the case is where some of the known brothers are only paternal half-brothers and some are only maternal half-brothers to the mixed sons. {\i Achin min ha-em choltzin ve-achin min ha-av meyabbemin} \u8212- the maternal half-brothers perform {\i chalitzah} and the paternal half-brothers perform {\i yibum}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This too seems backwards. {\i Rashi} clarifies the underlying situation: suppose one of the known brothers is a paternal half-brother to one of the mixed sons and a maternal half-brother to another. He cannot perform {\i yibum}, because one of the widows might be the wife of his maternal half-brother \u8212- and cohabiting with his maternal half-brother\u8217's wife would constitute an {\i issur arayot} with no {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} attached. He must, however, perform {\i chalitzah} with all the widows, because he needs to free the wife of his paternal half-brother. The combined effect is: {\i chalitzah} for all, and no {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Miktzatan kohanim u-miktzatan she-ein kohanim} \u8212- some of the known brothers are {\i kohanim} and some are not. A {\i kohen} may not marry a {\i chalutzah}, but {\i chalitzah} must precede {\i yibum}. Therefore, once a widow has received {\i chalitzah} from others, the {\i kohen} may no longer perform {\i yibum} with her \u8212- she has become a {\i chalutzah}. Accordingly: {\i kohanim choltzin ve-she-ein kohanim meyabbemin} \u8212- the {\i kohanim} can only perform {\i chalitzah}, while the non-{\i kohanim} perform {\i yibum}. In this arrangement, a single non-{\i kohen} may end up performing {\i yibum} with more than one widow, since the equal one-to-one rotation of the Mishnah\u8217's first scenario no longer holds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Chalitzah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then presents the most restrictive configuration: {\i miktzatan kohanim u-miktzatan achin min ha-em} \u8212- some have the {\i kohen} restriction and others have the maternal-half-brother restriction, with no one in the group who has neither limitation. In such a case, {\i elu va-elu choltzin ve-lo meyabbemin} \u8212- every one of the known brothers can only perform {\i chalitzah}, and no {\i yibum} is performed at all. Neither category of men can perform {\i yibum}: those with a maternal half-brother among the mixed sons cannot, lest they commit an {\i issur arayot}, and the {\i kohanim} cannot, lest they marry a {\i chalutzah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Doubtful Incest Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother & Other Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a series of cases where a person may be obligated to perform {\i chalitzah} with a woman who is possibly his own close relative. These scenarios emerge from the same framework of mixed-up children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yesh choletz le-imo mi-safek la-achoto mi-safek le-vito mi-safek} \u8212- there are cases in which someone may be required to perform {\i chalitzah} with his mother under uncertainty, with his sister under uncertainty, or with his daughter under uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the mother: {\i imo ve-ishah acheret, ve-lahen shenei zecharim, ve-chazru ve-yaldu shenei zecharim be-me\u8217'arah} \u8212- his mother and another woman each have sons whose identity is known, and then both women give birth to two sons in a cave, with the identities mixed. {\i U-va benah shel zo ve-nasa imah shel zo u-meitu} \u8212- one of the known sons married the other woman, and then both men die childless. Now the widow requires {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. The obligation falls on the deceased husband\u8217's brother \u8212- but one of the mixed-identity sons is certainly the deceased\u8217's brother, while the other is the widow\u8217's son. Neither can perform {\i yibum}. But who does {\i chalitzah}? Since we do not know which of the two mixed-identity men is the brother, both must perform {\i chalitzah}. The result is {\i nimtza kol echad ve-echad choletz le-imo mi-safek} \u8212- at least one of the men is definitively performing {\i chalitzah} with his own mother, though under uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother-Sister Mix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the sister: {\i imo ve-ishah acheret she-yaldu shtei nekevot be-machba} \u8212- his mother and another woman gave birth to two daughters in hiding, with identities mixed. {\i U-va\u8217'u acheihem shelo mi-ota ha-em ve-nasa\u8217'um} \u8212- their paternal half-brothers (sharing the same father but different mothers) came and married these two daughters. This is permitted: since the brothers share only a father and not a mother with these daughters, and the daughters\u8217' identities are entirely unknown, these men may marry them. The husbands then die childless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the paternal half-brothers of the deceased must perform {\i chalitzah}. But two women must receive {\i chalitzah}, and one of the two women is the surviving brother\u8217's sister \u8212- the daughter born to his mother. He cannot perform {\i yibum} and must perform {\i chalitzah} with both. He is therefore definitively performing {\i chalitzah} with his sister, albeit under uncertainty as to which one she is. {\i Nimtza choletz achoto mi-safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternal Brothers & Daug\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the daughter: {\i isha ve-ishah acheret she-yaldu shtei nekevot be-machba} \u8212- his wife and another woman gave birth to two daughters in a cave, identities mixed. {\i U-va\u8217'u acheihem ve-nasa\u8217'um} \u8212- the man\u8217's brothers came and married these daughters. A man is permitted to marry his niece, so no prohibition applies at the time of the marriages. The brothers then die childless. The surviving brother \u8212- the father of one of the girls \u8212- must now perform {\i chalitzah} with both widows, since one of them is his daughter. {\i Choletz le-vito mi-safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} works to make each of these cases as precise and structurally parallel as possible, so that in each scenario the person performing {\i chalitzah} is certainly doing so with his close relative \u8212- not merely possibly doing so \u8212- while the uncertainty lies only in {\i which} of the women that relative is. The Gemara confirms this reading and notes that the parallel constructions for each sub-case can be built either with one brother performing {\i chalitzah} to both women or with two brothers on either side, the result in each case being the same.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niece & Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The subsection of the daughter case draws the section to a close. The Gemara confirms that in the scenario of {\i isha ve-ishah acheret}, his wife and another woman giving birth to daughters whose identities are mixed, and his brothers marrying those daughters \u8212- both daughters must receive {\i chalitzah} from the surviving brother. {\i Zeh choletz le-vito mi-safek ve-zeh choletz le-vito mi-safek} \u8212- each of the relevant men is performing {\i chalitzah} with his own possible daughter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five Categories Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Conversion Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a celebrated {\i baraita} attributed to Rabbi Meir: {\i pe\u8217'amim she-molidin chamesh umot} \u8212- sometimes a man and a woman can produce children belonging to five distinct national-halachic categories. {\i Yisrael she-lakach eved ve-shifcha min ha-shuk} \u8212- a Jew purchased a male and female non-Jewish slave from the marketplace. {\i Ve-lahem shenei vanim} \u8212- they already had two biological children. These children are not themselves slaves: either because children of non-Jewish slaves owned by non-Jews were not automatically considered the property of a new Jewish owner, or \u8212- as {\i Rashi} discusses \u8212- because the parents were enslaved only after the children had already been born.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-nitgayyer echad mehem} \u8212- one of those two children converted. The result: {\i nimtza echad geir ve-echad oved kochavim} \u8212- one child is a {\i ger} and one is a non-Jew. {\i Hitbilu le-shem avdut} \u8212- the two parents were then immersed in a {\i mikveh} to become {\i avdei Kena\u8217'ani} of the Jewish owner. {\i Ve-nizkak zeh la-zeh} \u8212- they then cohabited. A child born at this point is a slave. So now there are three children: a {\i ger}, a non-Jew, and a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer from Eved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shichrar et ha-shifcha} \u8212- the master then freed the female slave. {\i U-va aleha ha-eved} \u8212- the male slave continued to cohabit with her. The child of a slave and a free woman, following the position of Rabbi Meir (who defines a {\i mamzer} broadly to include such a union), is a {\i mamzer}. Thus the fourth category is added: {\i harei kan geir ve-oved kochavim ve-eved u-mamzer}. The Gemara confirms this is specifically the {\i chiddush} of the case: {\i oved kochavim ve-eved ha-ba al bat Yisrael havei mamzer} \u8212- an {\i eved} who cohabits with a free woman produces a {\i mamzer}, at least according to this {\i baraita} and its underlying authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Israeli-born Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shichrar shenehem ve-hisiam zeh la-zeh} \u8212- the master then freed both the male slave and the woman and caused them to marry one another. The child born of a freed male slave and a freed female slave who are now legally married is a full-fledged Jew born in Israel \u8212- a {\i Yisrael}. Thus: {\i harei kan geir ve-oved kochavim ve-eved u-mamzer ve-Yisrael} \u8212- five distinct categories from one couple.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Selling Father for Ketub\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother\u8217's Ketubah Lien\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another playful {\i baraita}: {\i yesh mocher et aviv le-hagbot et ketuvas immo} \u8212- there is a case in which a man sells his own father in order to pay his mother\u8217's {\i ketubah}. {\i Keitzad?} \u8212- how?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yisrael she-lakach eved ve-shifcha min ha-shuk, ve-lahem ben} \u8212- a Jew bought a male and female slave who had a son. {\i Ve-shichrar et ha-shifcha ve-nesa\u8217'ah} \u8212- the master freed the female slave and married her. He now owns his new wife\u8217's former husband as a slave. {\i Ve-amar ve-chasav kol nechasai le-vni} \u8212- after marrying this woman, the master transferred all his property to her son \u8212- the son she had had with the slave. When the master dies, his wife (the freed slave, now his widow) wishes to collect her {\i ketubah}. The lien for the {\i ketubah} falls on her son, who now owns all the property \u8212- including his own biological father, who remains a slave. In order to pay off his mother\u8217's {\i ketubah}, the son sells his father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Lien Chiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ma kamashma lan?} \u8212- what is the {\i chiddush}? {\i Kula Rabbi Meir hi} \u8212- this {\i baraita} follows Rabbi Meir throughout. {\i Ve-avda ke-metalteli, u-metalteli mishtabdi li-ketuvah} \u8212- the legal novelty is that Rabbi Meir holds that an {\i eved} has the status of movable property ({\i metalteli}), and Rabbi Meir further holds that movable property is subject to the lien of a {\i ketubah}. Alternatively, {\i ve-i ba\u8217'it eima, avda ke-mikarkei dami} \u8212- one might say the {\i chiddush} is that Rabbi Meir treats an {\i eved} as real property ({\i mikarka\u8217'i}), which is obviously subject to a {\i ketubah} lien. The point in either reading is a legal principle about {\i eved} and {\i ketubah} liens \u8212- not a moral lesson about selling one\u8217's father.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Uncle-Nephew Mix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncle-Nephew Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah now presents a variation: {\i ha-isha she-nitarev valdah be-vlad kallatah} \u8212- a woman whose newborn child was mixed up with her daughter-in-law\u8217's newborn child. The result is a man and his nephew of uncertain identity \u8212- neither the uncle nor the nephew knows who he is. Both men grow up, marry, and die childless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Other Brothers Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah distinguishes between different categories of brothers who must now respond. {\i Bnei ha-kalah choltzin ve-lo meyabbemin} \u8212- the other sons of the daughter-in-law (i.e., the additional brothers of the mixed sons from that side) may only perform {\i chalitzah}, not {\i yibum}. The reason: {\i safek eshet achiv safek eshet achei aviv} \u8212- each widow might be a brother\u8217's wife (requiring {\i yibum}) or might be an uncle\u8217's wife (an {\i ervah}). Since the {\i ervah} possibility exists, {\i yibum} is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flexible Chalitzah or Yi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bnei ha-zekenah} \u8212- the other sons of the older woman (from the grandmother\u8217's line, so to speak) are in a more favorable position. For them, {\i o choltzin o meyabbemin} \u8212- they may perform either {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}. The reason: {\i safek eshet achiv ve-eshet ben achiv} \u8212- each widow is either a brother\u8217's wife or a nephew\u8217's wife. Both possibilities permit {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} without any {\i ervah} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Role Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meitu ha-kesherim} \u8212- the Gemara specifies that when \u8220"the kosher ones\u8221" die, the reference is not that the others are disqualified ({\i pesulim}) in some way, but simply that those of certain identity ({\i ha-vadayim}) have now died. Rav Pappa accordingly emends the reading: {\i eima u-meitu ha-vadayim} \u8212- read it as \u8220"those of certain identity died.\u8221" The situation then reverses: {\i ha-ta\u8217'arovot li-vnei ha-zekenah choltzin ve-lo meyabbemin}, while {\i li-vnei ha-kalah echad choletz ve-echad meyabem} \u8212- the analysis mirrors what was laid out above, with the widow categories flipped. As before, the Gemara emphasizes: {\i vedavka be-reisha chalitzah ve-hadar yibum, aval yibum be-reisha la} \u8212- the mandatory order is {\i chalitzah} first, then {\i yibum}, never the reverse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen & Slave Child Mix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Sharing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final Mishnah of the daf introduces the case of {\i kohenet she-nitarev valdah be-vlad shifchatah} \u8212- a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife whose newborn child was mixed up with her slave\u8217's newborn child. One of the two mixed children is a {\i kohen} and one is a {\i kohen}\u8217's slave. Since the slave of a {\i kohen} eats {\i terumah}, both children may eat {\i terumah} \u8212- whichever one is the slave eats by virtue of his status as the slave of a {\i kohen}, and the other eats as the {\i kohen} himself. {\i Chelkei ke-echad ba-goren} \u8212- at the threshing floor they receive one combined portion, since regardless of which is which, together they are entitled to only a single family portion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restrictions While Mixed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-einam mitam\u8217'im le-meitim} \u8212- they may not contract corpse-{\i tumah}, since one of them is a {\i kohen}. {\i Ve-einam nosim nashim} \u8212- they may not marry, since the {\i kohen} among them may only marry certain categories of women while the slave may not marry at all; the two categories of permitted women are mutually exclusive. {\i Bein kesherot bein pesulot} \u8212- this restriction holds regardless of whether the prospective wife is of fully valid lineage or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Mutual Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Higdilu ha-ta\u8217'arovot ve-shichreru zeh et zeh} \u8212- when they grew up, they freed one another: each declared that if the other is the slave and he himself is the master, he frees him. Now both are free men. {\i Nosim nashim re\u8217'uyot li-khunah} \u8212- they may now marry women who are fit to marry {\i kohanim}, since whoever the {\i kohen} among them is, he must marry appropriately, and the freed slave may also marry such women. {\i Ve-einam mitam\u8217'im le-meitim} \u8212- they still may not contract corpse-{\i tumah}, as one remains a {\i kohen}. {\i Ve-im nitme\u8217'u einam sofgin et ha-arba\u8217'im} \u8212- if they did contract {\i tumah}, they do not receive lashes, because lashes require certainty as to which man is the definite {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Selling Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-einam ochlim bi-terumah} \u8212- they may no longer eat {\i terumah}. Previously, even the slave was permitted because a {\i kohen}\u8217's slave eats {\i terumah}. Now that both are free, neither can be presumed to be the {\i kohen}\u8217's slave, and the uncertainty means neither may eat. {\i Ve-im achlu einam meshalmin keren ve-chomesh} \u8212- if they did eat {\i terumah}, they need not pay the principal and the fifth that a non-{\i kohen} who eats {\i terumah} must pay, since either of them might in fact be a {\i kohen} eating his own {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-einam cholkim al ha-goren} \u8212- they cannot receive {\i terumah} at the threshing floor, since neither is certainly a {\i kohen} or a {\i kohen}\u8217's slave. {\i U-mochrin et ha-terumah ve-ha-damim shelahen} \u8212- however, the {\i terumah} they separate from their own produce they need not give to anyone else. They may sell it and keep the money. The rationale is {\i ha-motzi me-chavero alav ha-ra\u8217'ayah}: the claimant bears the burden of proof. Anyone seeking to take their {\i terumah} would need to prove that they are not {\i kohanim}, and this cannot be established. They themselves, however, may not eat it, since they might equally not be {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-einam cholkim be-kodshei ha-mikdash} \u8212- they do not receive a share in the {\i kodshim} of the {\i Beit Hamikdash}, since one may not be a {\i kohen}. {\i Ve-ein notnim lahem kodshim} \u8212- one does not give them {\i korbanot} to sacrifice, nor \u8212- as will be seen \u8212- does one give them a {\i bechor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Waiting for Mum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah addresses {\i bechor}: if one of them has a firstborn animal, {\i yireh ad she-yista\u8217'ev} \u8212- it is allowed to graze until it develops a {\i mum} (a blemish). Once the {\i bechor} has a {\i mum}, the {\i kohen} may eat it outside the {\i Beit Hamikdash} without the act of {\i korban} being brought. Since one of the two men is a {\i kohen}, and since neither needs to hand the animal to another {\i kohen} (by virtue of {\i ha-motzi me-chavero alav ha-ra\u8217'ayah}), they wait out the {\i bechor}\u8217's life until it is blemished and then eat it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding their own {\i korbanot}: {\i Rashi} addresses two possibilities. If they have a {\i bechor}, the procedure is as above. If they have an {\i olah} or {\i chatat} to bring, they may designate a {\i kohen} to perform the {\i avodah} on their behalf and declare that if they themselves are the {\i kohen}, they retain the right to the {\i or} (hide) or equivalent, since it may belong to them \u8212- but they cannot personally perform the {\i avodah} or eat from the {\i kodshim} portions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Stringencies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-notnim alav chomrei kohanim ve-chomrei Yisraelim} \u8212- they are treated with the stringencies of both {\i kohanim} and {\i Yisraelim}. This formulation encapsulates the entire preceding discussion: any stringency that attaches to {\i kohanim} applies because one of them may be the {\i kohen}, and any stringency that attaches to ordinary Israelites applies because one of them may be the non-{\i kohen}. The leniences of either status are unavailable to either man in their mixed condition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah & Kohen Percepti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's ruling that the two mixed children must go to the threshing floor together \u8212- {\i chelek ke-echad} \u8212- is now explained in light of a {\i machlokes} between Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Yossi. {\i Ein cholkin terumah le-eved ela im kein rabo imo, divrei Rabbi Yehudah} \u8212- Rabbi Yehudah holds that one does not distribute {\i terumah} to a slave unless his master is present with him. The {\i baraita} requires the two of them to come together: {\i chelek ke-echad} means they must appear together to collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yossi\u8217's Allowance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yossi omer yachol she-yomar: im kohen ani, tnu li bishvil atzmi; ve-im eved le-kohen ani, tnu li bishvil rabbi} \u8212- Rabbi Yossi says the slave may appear alone and state: \u8220"If I am a {\i kohen}, give it to me on my own account; if I am a slave of a {\i kohen}, give it to me on behalf of my master.\u8221" Under this reasoning, the slave\u8217's presence alone is sufficient, and there is no need for the master to accompany him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perception-Based Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's position: {\i bi-mkomo shel Rabbi Yehudah hayu ma\u8217'alin mi-terumah le-yuchasin} \u8212- in Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's locale, the practice of collecting {\i terumah} was treated as evidence of {\i kohen} lineage. If a man came to the threshing floor and collected {\i terumah}, people would assume he was a {\i kohen} and would arrange marriages for him accordingly \u8212- marrying him off to women appropriate for {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this communal expectation, if a slave were to go alone to collect {\i terumah}, bystanders would assume he was a {\i kohen} and treat him as such in matters of marriage and lineage. Therefore, in Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's locale, the rule is that a slave may only collect {\i terumah} in the presence of his master, making the nature of the arrangement evident. The logic is circular in an instructive way: because only {\i kohanim} collect {\i terumah}, people infer that {\i kohen} status from {\i terumah} collection; and because people draw that inference, the halacha must protect against it by ensuring no slave collects alone. {\i Bi-mkomo shel Rabbi Yossi lo hayu ma\u8217'alin mi-terumah le-yuchasin} \u8212- in Rabbi Yossi\u8217's locale, no such assumption was made. People understood that one who collected {\i terumah} might be a slave doing so on his master\u8217's behalf, so no presumption of {\i kohen} lineage attached, and the slave could collect independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elazar\u8217's Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} relates: {\i amar Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok, miyamai lo he\u8217'edeti ela eidut achat} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok said that in his entire life he gave only one testimony, and that testimony led to a result in which {\i he\u8217'elu eved li-khunah al pi} \u8212- a slave was elevated to {\i kohen} status on the basis of his words. He had testified that a certain man collected {\i terumah}, and on that basis the community treated the man as a {\i kohen} and arranged a marriage for him accordingly \u8212- only for it to emerge that the man was in fact a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prevented Error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara objects: {\i ve-he\u8217'elu salka da\u8217'atak?} \u8212- could we truly think that such an error actually occurred and was consummated, particularly through the testimony of a {\i tzaddik} of Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok\u8217's stature? {\i Hash-ta bi-vehemtan shel tzaddikim ein ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu meivi takala al yadam; tzaddikim atzmam lo kol she-kein} \u8212- if the Holy One does not allow even the animals of the righteous to stumble into transgression (as illustrated by the donkey of Pinchas ben Yair, which would not eat {\i tevel}), how much more would He not allow a {\i tzaddik} himself to cause such an outcome. Therefore: {\i ela eima, bikshu le-ha\u8217'alot eved li-khunah al pi} \u8212- emend the text to say that they were {\i about} to elevate a slave to {\i kohen} status on the basis of his testimony, but were prevented before the error was finalized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Locale Customs Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then explains how such a near-error came about. {\i Chaza be-asrei de-Rabbi Yossi} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok witnessed the event in Rabbi Yossi\u8217's locale, where slaves routinely collected {\i terumah} at the threshing floor without their masters present, and where no inference of {\i kohen} lineage was drawn from such collection. He then testified about what he had seen \u8212- \u8220"I saw this man collect {\i terumah}\u8221" \u8212- but he gave this testimony in Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's locale, where the same act of collecting {\i terumah} was taken as definitive evidence of {\i kohen} status. The testimony was accurate as a description of events, but its halachic implications were entirely different depending on the locale\u8217's custom. In Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's locale, his truthful testimony nearly caused a slave to be enrolled as a {\i kohen} \u8212- and the Gemara\u8217's emendation ensures that the {\i tzaddik} was spared from having his words actually produce that outcome.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 100\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ten Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens a {\i baraita} that emerges directly from the preceding discussion about the {\i kohen} and the slave of a {\i kohen} whose identities became confused \u8212- specifically, the question of whether one may distribute {\i terumah} to a slave in the absence of a {\i kohen} without creating the misleading impression that the slave is himself a {\i kohen}. With that context in mind, {\i tanu rabbanan}: there are ten categories of people who, although they possess a right to {\i terumah}, are not given their portion at the threshing floor. The {\i baraita} then proceeds to enumerate them: the {\i cheresh}, the {\i shoteh}, and the {\i katan}; the {\i tumtum} and the {\i androgynos}; the slave; the woman; the {\i arel} and the {\i tamei kohen}; and finally, a {\i kohen} who has married a woman he is forbidden to marry \u8212- for instance, a {\i gerushah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then adds that for all of these individuals, one does send the {\i terumah} to their homes, since they are in fact entitled to {\i terumah} and permitted to eat it. The exceptions are the {\i tamei} and the {\i kohen} married to an unsuitable woman, who are excluded even from home delivery. The reasons behind each category require the {\i gemara}\u8217's elaboration, since it is not immediately obvious why people who are fully entitled to {\i terumah} should be denied it at the threshing floor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh, Shoteh, Katan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} begins its analysis with the first group: {\i bishlama cheresh shoteh ve-katan} \u8212- understandable. {\i Lav benei dei\u8217'a inhu}, they are not people of mature intellect. But what exactly follows from that? Rashi explains that it is a matter of {\i ziluta de-terumah le-chalek lahem be-goren le-ein kol} \u8212- it is undignified for {\i terumah} to be distributed to them publicly at the threshing floor. The {\i goren} is a setting where all can observe who is receiving {\i terumah}, and handing it over to people whom society does not regard with full respect is seen as disrespectful to the sanctity of {\i terumah} itself. This is, of course, a striking basis for the ruling, since the concern is less about the legal standing of these individuals and more about how their receipt of {\i terumah} would be perceived by onlookers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a different concern. If the {\i cheresh} and {\i shoteh} lack mental competence, perhaps they will fail to guard the {\i terumah} in a state of {\i taharah} \u8212- which would seem to be the real problem. Yet the {\i baraita} itself establishes that you do send {\i terumah} to their homes, which seems to undercut that concern entirely. Tosafot resolves this by noting that presumably such individuals have an {\i apitropas} \u8212- a guardian \u8212- overseeing them at home. Even so, says Tosafot, you do not distribute to them at the {\i gronot} because there may be occasions when they arrive without their guardian. According to Tosafot, then, the {\i gronot} concern cannot primarily be about their inability to preserve the {\i terumah}\u8217's {\i taharah}, since that concern applies at home as well. The reasoning that ultimately prevails in the {\i gronot} setting must be something else \u8212- and here Tosafot presumably would concur with Rashi\u8217's account of {\i ziluta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumtum, Androgynous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i tumtum} and the {\i androgynos}, the {\i gemara} explains: {\i biryah bifnei atzmo hinhu} \u8212- they are their own distinct category of creature, falling outside the standard gender binary. Rashi frames this again as {\i ziluta de-kodshim} \u8212- the same principle of unseemly public distribution. People perceive these individuals as unusual, and because they do not fit neatly into the recognized social categories, distributing {\i terumah} to them publicly at the {\i goren} is not regarded as befitting the sanctity of {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a recurring theme in {\i hilchos kodshim} more broadly \u8212- the {\i kohanim} with physical blemishes are excluded from Temple service; everything associated with the {\i Beis Hamikdash} must conform to an aesthetic ideal of wholeness and dignity. There is a real tension here, since {\i kedushah} is presumably about internal quality, yet the {\i halacha} here accommodates prevailing social perceptions rather than correcting them. The {\i gemara} proceeds without pausing to resolve this tension; it simply legislates within the reality of how society actually functions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i eved} presents no difficulty: {\i dilma ati le-asuki mi-terumah le-yuchsin} \u8212- people will observe a slave receiving {\i terumah} at the threshing floor and conclude that he must be a free {\i kohen}, with the potential that they will then consider marrying him off as such. This is precisely the concern that gave rise to the {\i baraita} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arel, Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i arel} and the {\i tamei}, the {\i gemara} offers a different explanation: {\i mishum de-me\u8217'isi} \u8212- there is something abhorrent about it. These individuals are not in a condition that permits them to eat {\i terumah} at this moment, and handing {\i terumah} to someone who cannot eat it creates an inherently discordant situation. There is a meaningful distinction between this category and the earlier ones. {\i Ziluta} is a matter of social perception \u8212- how the act of distribution appears to onlookers. {\i Me\u8217'isi}, by contrast, may carry something more objective: it is the essential incompatibility of the transaction itself, giving {\i terumah} to someone for whom it is currently prohibited, regardless of whether anyone is watching. One might observe that {\i ziluta} concerns what people see, while {\i me\u8217'isi} concerns what the thing itself is \u8212- even if the status of the {\i arel} is known only to himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction also illuminates why the {\i arel} receives his {\i terumah} at home even though the {\i tamei} does not. The {\i arel} is {\i anus} \u8212- his condition is entirely beyond his control; if his brothers died in {\i bris milah} he is a hemophiliac and bears no responsibility for his status. Why should he be penalized? You therefore send it to his home, where he can distribute it to his wife, children, and slaves who are permitted to eat it. The {\i tamei}, one might think, deserves the same treatment \u8212- he too may have become {\i tamei} in the course of fulfilling a {\i mitzvah}, such as burying a relative. Yet the {\i gemara} answers: {\i hai nafush unsei ve-hai lo nafush unsei} \u8212- the degree to which the {\i arel}\u8217's condition is beyond his control is absolute and permanent, whereas the {\i tamei} can, in principle, immerse in a {\i mikvah}. As a general communal statement underscoring how important it is for {\i kohanim} to be zealous about maintaining {\i taharah}, there is value in not sending {\i terumah} to the {\i tamei} even at home \u8212- not necessarily as a condemnation of every individual case, but as a collective posture conveying that the community takes {\i tumah} seriously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remaining category is the woman, and the {\i gemara} notes that she does not fit any of the obvious explanations. She occupies a recognized place within the social order, she is fully {\i bat dei\u8217'a}, and she falls squarely within the standard gender binary. So {\i isha mai ta\u8217'ama lo}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Pligi bah Rav Pappa ve-Rav Huna breih de-Rav Yehoshua}. One says the concern is {\i mishum gerushah}: if you begin distributing {\i terumah} at the threshing floor to the wife or daughter of a {\i kohen}, people may continue to give it to her even after she has been divorced and lost her entitlement \u8212- either because they don\u8217't realize she has been divorced, or simply because the habit of giving has formed. The other says the concern is {\i mishum yichud}: a woman should not be going out to the threshing floor, an isolated location, in the manner that Boaz and Ruth illustrates \u8212- it is simply improper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies a practical difference between these two positions. Consider a threshing floor that is {\i de-makrav le-mata} \u8212- located close to the city \u8212- {\i ve-lo shchichi bah inshi} \u8212- but where there is not much foot traffic at the site itself. If the concern is {\i gerushah}, there is no problem: because the floor is close to the city, the woman is known there, people are aware of her marital status, and no one will mistakenly give her {\i terumah} after she is divorced. But because the site itself is quiet, there remains a {\i yichud} concern. Conversely, a floor that is distant from the city \u8212- so people do not know the woman\u8217's status \u8212- but has heavy traffic, presents no {\i yichud} concern while retaining a {\i gerushah} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Distribution Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the rule that all ten categories receive their {\i terumah} at home \u8212- except the {\i tamei} and the {\i kohen} married to an unsuitable woman \u8212- the {\i gemara} presses on the {\i tamei}. The {\i kohen} married to a forbidden woman is comprehensible: withholding even home delivery functions as a communal sanction, signaling societal disapproval and applying pressure for him to address the situation. But the {\i tamei}? Unlike the case of {\i tumah} being a permanent or semi-permanent state, a {\i tamei kohen} may well be able to immerse today and become {\i tahor} by nightfall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer \u8212- {\i hai nafush unsei ve-hai lo nafush unsei} \u8212- draws the line between a condition that is categorically beyond one\u8217's control (the {\i arel}) and one that, however understandable in any given instance, admits at least theoretically of remedy. The withholding of home delivery from the {\i tamei} is not so much a judgment of each individual case as it is a collective statement of communal seriousness about {\i tahara} among {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces a second {\i baraita}: {\i ha-eved ve-ha-isha ein cholkin lahem terumah be-veit ha-granot} \u8212- the slave and the woman do not receive {\i terumah} at the threshing floor, for the reasons already discussed. This much is simply a restatement. What follows, however, is more complex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women, Ma\u8217'aser Ani\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i u-ve-makom she-cholkin, notnim la-isha techila u-potrin otam mi-yad} \u8212- in a place where they do distribute, they give it to the woman first and send her off immediately. The {\i gemara} pauses: surely the {\i baraita} just said you don\u8217't give it to women at the threshing floor \u8212- so what does it mean to say \u8220"in a place where they do give\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hachi kamar}: the reference is not to {\i terumah} at all. The {\i baraita} means: in a case where one distributes {\i ma\u8217'aser ani}, you give it to the woman first. {\i Ma\u8217'aser ani} \u8212- the poor tithe of the third and sixth years \u8212- is owed to impoverished women just as to men, and the concern about {\i gerushah} simply does not apply, since a woman\u8217's entitlement to {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} has nothing to do with her marital status. If the concern driving the {\i terumah} restriction was {\i gerushah}, that concern drops away entirely here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What about {\i yichud}? Here too, {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} is different: Rashi explains that {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} is distributed not at the threshing floor but {\i be-toch ha-bayit ba-ir} \u8212- within the city \u8212- as derived from the verse {\i ve-achlu vish\u8217'arecha ve-save\u8217'u}, \u8220"they shall eat within your gates,\u8221" meaning in the city rather than out in the fields. In the city, people are present, the setting is populated, and there is no {\i yichud} concern. Rashi adds a further reason: {\i mishum de-lo lichsefu aniyim} \u8212- so that the poor are not embarrassed. The precise sense of this last point is not entirely transparent; one might suggest that it means the poor should not have to journey out to the fields and thereby advertise their poverty to the broader public, though Rashi\u8217's formulation leaves room for further interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once it is established that women do receive {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} in the city, the {\i baraita} specifies that they receive it first. {\i Mah ta\u8217'ama mishum ziluta} \u8212- because it is unseemly for a woman to be made to stand and wait among a crowd of men. Having established that there is no {\i yichud} and no {\i gerushah} concern in this context, propriety requires that the distribution be handled quickly and the woman sent on her way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds a striking personal observation. He says: {\i merish ki havu atu gavra ve-itata le-dina kamei, hava sharina tigra de-gavra be-reisha} \u8212- initially, when a man and a woman would come before him for a court case, he would attend to the man\u8217's case first, reasoning that the man\u8217's greater obligation in {\i mitzvot} confers a kind of priority status. {\i Keivan de-shamana lah le-ha} \u8212- once he heard this ruling about giving priority to women in the distribution of {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} \u8212- {\i sharina tigra de-itata be-reisha}, he changed his practice and began hearing the woman\u8217's case first. {\i Mah ta\u8217'ama mishum ziluta} \u8212- because making a woman stand and wait in such a public setting is itself a form of disrespect. Men are more habituated to the public sphere and to waiting in such contexts; women are not, and the norms of society make their prolonged presence in such settings uncomfortable for all. Rava draws a direct halachic inference from a procedural rule about agricultural distribution and applies it to judicial practice \u8212- a remarkable illustration of how sensitivity to {\i kavod ha-briot} can migrate across domains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen-Slave Mix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the central case that has run through this section: two infants \u8212- one the son of a {\i kohen} and one the slave of that {\i kohen} \u8212- who were mixed up, so that neither can be identified with certainty. The {\i mishnah} had discussed the scenario in which these two men, now grown, might free one another. The {\i gemara} probes the language: the {\i mishnah} says {\i she-yesh la-hem}, which might suggest that freeing one another is optional. But {\i amar Rava}: {\i eima kofin otan u-mishachrin zeh es zeh} \u8212- read it as compulsory. The {\i mishnah} means they are compelled to free one another. The rationale is that neither one can currently marry: he cannot marry a slave woman because he might be a {\i kohen}, and he cannot marry a free woman because he might be a slave. Such an intolerable state of affairs compels the master to free the slave, as is the rule in analogous cases throughout the {\i gemara}. The mechanism is a {\i tenai} \u8212- whichever of the two is actually the slave is freed by the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mincha Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the manumission, the {\i gemara} establishes that both men are treated with the stringencies of {\i kohanim} and the stringencies of {\i Yisrael}. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i le-mai hilchasa} \u8212- to what practical {\i halachah} does this double stringency apply, beyond what has already been spelled out?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Pappa}: {\i le-minchasam nikmetzes ke-minchas Yisrael, ve-einan ne\u8217'echeles ke-minchas kohanim} \u8212- regarding their {\i minchah} offering. When a {\i Yisrael} brings a {\i minchah}, the {\i kohen} takes the {\i kometz} \u8212- the handful \u8212- and burns it on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, while the remainder ({\i shiyarayim}) is eaten by the {\i kohanim}. When a {\i kohen} brings a {\i minchah}, the entire offering is burned: {\i ve-chol minchas kohen kalil tihyeh, lo te\u8217'achel}. What, then, is done with the {\i minchah} of a person who is uncertain whether he is a {\i kohen} or a {\i Yisrael}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If you perform the {\i kemitzah} and then eat the remainder, you may have treated as a {\i minchas Yisrael} something that is actually a {\i minchas kohen} and should have been fully burned. If you burn the entire thing, you may have burned the remainder of what is actually a {\i minchas Yisrael}, in which case the remainder ought to have been eaten by {\i kohanim} rather than burned. The {\i gemara} proposes: {\i ha keitzad} \u8212- how is it done? The {\i kometz} is burned first, by itself. The {\i shiyarayim} is then burned separately. But immediately the {\i gemara} raises an objection: {\i kol she-mimenu le-ishim harei hu be-val taktirun} \u8212- once any part of an offering has been placed on the altar fire, the remainder is prohibited from being burned on the altar. Burning the {\i shiyarayim} on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} would thus appear to violate this prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda breih de-Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi}: the solution is {\i de-masik leihu le-shem eitzim} \u8212- one places the {\i shiyarayim} on the altar not as a sacrificial offering but as fuel. By stipulating that the act is one of fueling the altar fire rather than offering a {\i korban}, the {\i halakhic} character of the act changes, and the prohibition of {\i val taktirun} is not violated. This is, as noted, a powerful illustration of how {\i kavanah} \u8212- intention \u8212- defines the nature of an act on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a further complication: this solution works according to Rabbi Eliezer, who accepts the principle of designating remnants as fuel in cases of uncertainty. But what of the Rabbis, who reject that principle? {\i Avid lei ke-Rabbi Eliezer ben Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- the {\i gemara} invokes a related ruling by Rabbi Elazar ben Rabbi Shimon, which concerns the {\i minchas chotei} of a {\i kohen} \u8212- a {\i minchah} brought as a {\i chattas}. The question there is whether the entire offering must be burned, as with an ordinary {\i minchas kohen}, or whether a {\i kemitzah} is taken. Rabbi Elazar ben Rabbi Shimon holds that the {\i kemitzah} is taken but the {\i shiyarayim} are neither burned on the altar nor eaten; instead, they are scattered on the {\i beis ha-deshen} \u8212- the ash pile located beside the ramp of the altar \u8212- which is where disqualified {\i korbanos} are disposed of.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sacrificial Remnants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes that even the Rabbis would agree in our case of the {\i safek kohen safek Yisrael}. Their dispute with Rabbi Elazar ben Rabbi Shimon concerns only the particulars of the {\i minchas chotei} of an actual {\i kohen} \u8212- where they hold that the {\i shiyarayim} should in fact be offered on the altar because it is a {\i bas hakrava}, a fully offered sacrifice. But in the present case, where one is genuinely uncertain and it is conceded that the {\i shiyarayim} cannot be eaten (lest the person be a {\i kohen}) and cannot be offered as a sacrificial remnant on the altar (due to {\i val taktirun}), even the Rabbis would concede that the {\i shiyarayim} are scattered on the {\i beis ha-deshen}, as is done with {\i pasul korbanos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Paternity Unknown\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to a fresh category of cases: {\i mi she-lo shahasa achar ba\u8217'alah shloshah chodashim} \u8212- a woman who did not wait the required three months after separating from her first husband before remarrying. The concern is that she may have been pregnant when she remarried, so that a child born within an ambiguous window \u8212- potentially nine months from the first husband or seven months from the second \u8212- has an uncertain paternity. The {\i mishnah} stipulates that she {\i nisas ve-yalda} \u8212- she remarried and gave birth \u8212- and {\i ein yadu\u8217'a ben tesha la-rishon im ben sheva la-acharon} \u8212- it cannot be determined whether the child is a nine-month child from the first or a seven-month child from the second.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further specifies that she also had unambiguous children from both husbands \u8212- establishing a clear sibling relationship between the child of uncertain paternity and children from each household, on the maternal side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When this son of uncertain paternity grows up, marries, and dies without children, the {\i mishnah} rules: {\i choltzin ve-lo meyabmin} \u8212- both sets of brothers must perform {\i chalitza}; none may perform {\i yibum}. This requires explanation. Why can one set not perform {\i chalitza} while the other performs {\i yibum}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider the scenario: the brothers from husband one\u8217's household are definitely the maternal brothers of the deceased. The brothers from husband two\u8217's household are also definitely his maternal brothers. Neither set knows with certainty that they are also his paternal brothers. If one set were to perform {\i yibum} \u8212- even conditionally, reasoning that if they are not the paternal brother, the other set has already released the widow through {\i chalitza} \u8212- there is a fatal flaw: the one who performs {\i yibum} may be the maternal brother and not the paternal brother. Marrying one\u8217's maternal brother\u8217's wife falls under the category of {\i arayos} \u8212- incestuous unions. Since maternal brotherhood is certain and paternal brotherhood is uncertain, none of them may risk performing {\i yibum}. Both sets of brothers must therefore perform {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayos Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i ve-chein hu lahem, choleitz ve-lo meyabem} \u8212- and symmetrically, if one of the brothers from either household dies, the son of uncertain paternity himself performs only {\i chalitza} and not {\i yibum} with the widow. The reason is the same: he is certainly the maternal brother of that deceased man, and since he cannot be certain he is also the paternal brother, {\i yibum} would expose him to the {\i issur arayos} of marrying his maternal brother\u8217's wife. Only certainty of paternal brotherhood, which would also establish the obligation of {\i yibum}, could override the prohibition, and that certainty is absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Siblings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Mothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces a variation: {\i hayu lo achim min ha-rishon ve-achim min ha-sheini she-lo me\u8217'osah ha-eim} \u8212- the first and second husbands each had children from {\i different} mothers. These half-brothers of the child of uncertain paternity are thus not his maternal brothers at all \u8212- they share only a possible paternal connection with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This changes the analysis entirely. Take, for example, a son of husband one born from a different woman. His relation to the child of uncertain paternity is: possibly his paternal brother (if husband one was the father), but definitely not his maternal brother. If he is not the paternal brother, he is simply an unrelated man. There is therefore no {\i arayos} concern if he were to marry the widow: either he is the paternal brother and performs a proper {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, or he is entirely unrelated to the deceased and the widow is simply a permitted woman to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Each Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules accordingly: {\i hu choleitz u-meyabem} \u8212- the individual in question may perform either {\i chalitza} or {\i yibum}. And when it comes to the brothers collectively: {\i echad choleitz ve-echad meyabem} \u8212- one may perform {\i chalitza} and another may perform {\i yibum}. Crucially, the order matters: {\i chalitza} must come first, and only then may {\i yibum} follow. If the one who performs {\i chalitza} is in fact the true paternal brother, the widow is thereby released from the {\i yibum} obligation entirely and may remarry freely; the second man then simply marries her as any other woman. If the one who performs {\i chalitza} is not the paternal brother, his {\i chalitza} was meaningless, and the second man \u8212- who is the true paternal brother \u8212- performs {\i yibum} properly. Either way, the outcome is halachically sound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen-Unsuitable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Diet, Purity, Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} turns to the child of uncertain paternity whose two possible fathers are, respectively, a {\i kohen} and a {\i Yisrael}. Since he might be a {\i kohen}, he is required to marry only a woman who is suitable for a {\i kohen} \u8212- {\i nosei ishah re\u8217'uyah le-kohen}. Similarly, he may not become {\i tamei le-meitim}, since he might be a {\i kohen} who is Biblically prohibited from corpse {\i tumah}. Yet if he does become {\i tamei}, he does not receive lashes \u8212- {\i eino sofeg et ha-arba\u8217'im} \u8212- because he might be a {\i Yisrael} to whom that prohibition does not apply, and in the absence of certainty there can be no punitive lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Sale, Kodshim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He may not eat {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i eino ochel bi-trumah} \u8212- because he might be a {\i Yisrael}. Yet if he does eat it, he does not pay the {\i keren ve-chomesh} \u8212- the principal plus a fifth \u8212- that a non-{\i kohen} who eats {\i terumah} inadvertently is required to pay, because he might be a {\i kohen} who was entitled to eat it. He does not receive a portion at the threshing floor \u8212- {\i eino cholek al ha-goren} \u8212- for the reasons established earlier. However, {\i terumah} that he himself separates from his own produce belongs to him in the sense that one cannot prove he is not a {\i kohen}; since he may not eat it but owns it, he sells it and retains the proceeds \u8212- {\i ve-ha-damim shelo}. He does not receive a portion in {\i kodshei kodashim} \u8212- {\i eino cholek be-kodshei kodashim} \u8212- since he cannot prove entitlement, nor is he given priestly gifts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If someone gives him a {\i bechor} \u8212- a firstborn animal \u8212- it is not taken away from him, since he might be a {\i kohen} who is entitled to it. But since he might not be a {\i kohen}, he may not eat it while it is unblemished. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i ye-hei ro\u8217'eh ad she-yista\u8217'ev} \u8212- it grazes until it develops a blemish, at which point a {\i kohen} of certain status may eat it. He is also exempt from the priestly gifts of {\i zero\u8217'a}, {\i lechayayim}, and {\i keivah} when he slaughters an animal \u8212- since he might be a {\i kohen} and {\i kohanim} do not give these gifts to themselves \u8212- and he may eat those portions himself, as they are not {\i kodshim}. The {\i mishnah} concludes, as with the earlier case: {\i ve-notnim lo chumrei kohanim ve-chumrei Yisra-elim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Both Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onen, Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If both possible fathers are {\i kohanim}, then the child is certainly a {\i kohen}, since the only circumstance in which a woman could legitimately have been married to a second {\i kohen} while the first was still alive would be that the first had divorced her \u8212- but if she is a {\i gerushah}, any child from the second {\i kohen} would be a {\i chalal}. For both possible sons to be valid {\i kohanim}, the first husband must have died and the woman remarried as a {\i widow}, not a {\i divorc\u233 ?e}. Certainty of {\i kohen} status is therefore established; what remains at issue is whose son he is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When either of his two possible fathers dies, he is an {\i onen} \u8212- in the acute mourning status of the day of death \u8212- {\i mi-safek}, out of doubt, and therefore refrains from Temple service. Similarly, when he himself dies, both possible fathers must observe {\i onen} status, since for each of them he might be their son. Despite all this, he does not become {\i tamei} to either of them upon death \u8212- he might not be that man\u8217's son \u8212- and they do not become {\i tamei} to him, for the same reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding inheritance: {\i hu eino yoresh otan} \u8212- he cannot inherit from either of them, because the heirs of each household tell him he cannot prove he is their father\u8217's son. {\i Ve-hem yorshim oto} \u8212- but if he dies without children, both potential fathers inherit from him, since one of them is certainly his father, and no third party has a superior claim. They divide the estate between them. If he strikes or curses either of his potential fathers, he is exempt from the Biblical prohibitions against striking or cursing one\u8217's father, since the paternity cannot be established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temple Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Oleh be-mishmaro shel zeh ve-shel zeh} \u8212- he may perform Temple service during the {\i mishmar} week of either family, since one of them is certainly his, and no one can stop him from working. However, {\i ve-eino cholek} \u8212- he does not receive a portion of the {\i korbanos} distributed to that {\i mishmar}, since he cannot prove that this particular week is his. If, however, both potential fathers happened to be from the same {\i mishmar}, then he receives one portion \u8212- not two \u8212- since he has only one father, whoever that father is, and that father is certainly of this {\i mishmar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shtuki\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtuki Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces the case of the {\i shtuki} in Shmuel\u8217's formulation. {\i Asarah kohanim omdim u-peirash echad meihen u-va\u8217'al} \u8212- ten {\i kohanim} were standing together, one of them went and had relations with a woman, and it is unknown which one. {\i Ve-ha-valad shtuki} \u8212- the child born of this encounter is a {\i shtuki}. The word itself carries the sense of being silenced or shut down: when the child asks \u8220"who is my father?\u8221" the answer is, in effect, \u8220"we don\u8217't know \u8212- be quiet.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mere fact of uncertain paternity might seem to be a question only of inheritance or personal identification, not of fundamental status, since we know with certainty that the child is the son of some {\i kohen}. Why should the term {\i shtuki} carry the weight of a significant legal category?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Genealogy Req.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies: {\i ilima de-mashtakinan lei mi-nechasei de-aviv} \u8212- if it merely means he is deprived of his father\u8217's estate, that is obvious and needs no special ruling. The real {\i chiddush} of {\i shtuki} is {\i de-mashtakinan lei mi-dinei kehunah} \u8212- he is excluded from the privileges of {\i kehunah} proper, specifically from serving in the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, even though we know he is a {\i kohen} by blood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} derives this from the verse: {\i ve-hayesa lo u-le-zaro acharav bris kehunas olam} \u8212- \u8220"and it shall be to him and to his seed after him a covenant of eternal priesthood.\u8221" {\i Ba\u8217'inan zaro meyuchas acharav} \u8212- we require that his lineage be traceable {\i after him}, meaning that a {\i kohen}\u8217's descendant must be able to identify his father in order to serve. {\i Ve-leika} \u8212- and in the case of the {\i shtuki}, that identification is absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rishon Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a challenge from an earlier {\i mishnah}. In the case discussed above \u8212- the child born during the ambiguous window whose mother married a second {\i kohen} after the first \u8212- the {\i mishnah} ruled that {\i rishon ra\u8217'ui lihayos Kohen Gadol} \u8212- even the first child, whose paternity between the two {\i kohanim} is uncertain, is considered fit to serve as {\i Kohen Gadol}. But if we require {\i zaro meyuchas acharav} for Temple service, how can someone who does not know which {\i kohen} was his father serve at all, let alone as {\i Kohen Gadol}? This appears to be a direct refutation of Shmuel\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Znus Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension: {\i zera meyuchas acharav derabbanan u-kra asmachta be\u8217'alma} \u8212- the requirement of traceable genealogy for Temple service is rabbinic in origin, with the verse serving merely as an {\i asmachta}. The Rabbinic decree, however, was enacted only in cases involving {\i znus} \u8212- illicit sexual relations. {\i Ve-chi gazar rabbanan bi-znus?} \u8212- the question becomes: when the Rabbis enacted this disqualification, did they intend it to apply to cases of {\i znus} specifically, or more broadly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asserts that the Rabbinic sanction applies specifically {\i bi-znus}: a child born of relations that were inherently illicit and outside the framework of marriage carries a more deeply compromised social and personal status, and it is there that the Rabbis imposed the disqualification from Temple service. {\i Be-nisu\u8217'in lo gazar rabbanan} \u8212- in cases of legitimate marriage, the Rabbis did not enact the disqualification. The child from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case was born to a woman who was legitimately married \u8212- first to husband one, then to husband two \u8212- without any {\i znus} involved. His uncertain paternity is a product of circumstances, not of illegitimacy. That child, therefore, can serve in the {\i Beis HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legit Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution, however, creates a new problem. The {\i gemara} challenges: {\i u-vi-znus mi gazar rabbanan?} \u8212- is it actually true that the Rabbis imposed this disqualification in cases of {\i znus}? Consider our very {\i mishnah}: {\i mi she-lo shahasa achar ba\u8217'alah shloshah chodashim} \u8212- and the case is parsed carefully. The {\i mishnah} mentions {\i onen} status for both potential fathers when the child dies, implying that both are alive. If the first husband died, the wife is an {\i almana} and there is no {\i znus} at all. For both to be alive and for the child still to be a valid {\i kohen} on either side, the woman must not have been a {\i gerushah} \u8212- for if she were, the child from the second union would be a {\i chalal}. The only scenario in which both fathers are alive, the child is a {\i kohen} on either account, and she has not been divorced is if there was no prior marriage at all \u8212- meaning the first relationship was indeed {\i znus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet that {\i mishnah} rules that {\i oleh be-mishmaro shel zeh ve-shel zeh} \u8212- the child of uncertain paternity works in the Temple during the {\i mishmar} of either family. This appears to be a direct refutation: here is a child born of {\i znus} who is permitted to serve in the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, contradicting Shmuel\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kidushi Taus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers one resolution: {\i amar Rav Shemaya be-mema\u8217'enes} \u8212- perhaps we are dealing with a case of {\i mi\u8217'un}, where the woman had been married to husband one as a minor, in a marriage contracted by her father or brothers, and she exercised her rabbinic right to repudiate it without a {\i get}. In that case, husband one is alive, she is not a {\i gerushah}, there was no {\i znus}, and yet both fathers could be alive. The difficulty with this answer is that a {\i mema\u8217'enes} \u8212- a minor girl young enough to exercise {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- is presumably too young to carry a pregnancy to term safely. As {\i Rav Bibi} taught before {\i Rav Nachman}: a {\i ketana} between the ages of eleven and twelve who becomes pregnant faces mortal danger, and younger than eleven she will not become pregnant at all. A child born of a marriage dissolved by {\i mi\u8217'un} would require the mother to have been a minor, making it nearly impossible for her to have also produced a viable child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i mishtakcha lah be-kidushi taus} \u8212- the scenario involves a case of {\i kidushi taus}, a marriage contracted under false pretenses or based on an erroneous assumption, which is dissolved without a {\i get} when the error comes to light. Such a marriage is rabbinic in nature; its dissolution leaves the woman neither a {\i gerushah} nor an {\i almana}, and any child born within that union was not born of {\i znus} but of a legitimately constituted (if ultimately invalid) marriage. The {\i gemara} supports this with the ruling of {\i Rav Yehuda} in the name of {\i Shmuel}, citing {\i Rabbi Yishmael}: a woman who was not taken by force is forbidden to her husband if she committed adultery, but a woman who was raped is permitted. And there is yet another case \u8212- a woman who lay with a man even without compulsion and is nonetheless permitted: this is the case of {\i kidushi taus}, where the original marriage is voided retroactively, rendering her subsequent relations outside that marriage not adultery at all. {\i Sheafilu benah murkav al kesefah, mema\u8217'enes ve-holechet lah} \u8212- even if the child is already perched on her shoulder, she walks away from the marriage. The child of that union was born within the framework of a marriage, however flawed; there was no {\i znus}. And therefore Shmuel\u8217's disqualification \u8212- which applies only {\i bi-znus} \u8212- does not apply, and the child may serve in the {\i Beis HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 101\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Fathers: Smiting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Fathers Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a case involving a child who does not know which of two men is his father \u8212- a situation arising when his mother remarried after leaving her first husband. Whatever the precise circumstances, it is certain that his father is a {\i kohen}; the uncertainty is only as to which of the two men that is. The Gemara on 101a explores the legal consequences of this ambiguity across several domains, beginning with the laws of smiting and cursing a parent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exemption Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If such a child strikes or curses either of the two potential fathers, he is exempt from liability. The reason is straightforward: since it is unknown which man is actually his father, one cannot say with certainty that the particular individual he is striking or cursing is the one to whom he bears the filial obligation. The act, taken in isolation, lacks the definitive quality that the prohibition requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then distinguishes cases based on how the acts are performed. If he cursed both men simultaneously \u8212- {\i killel shneihem bebat achat} \u8212- or struck them both in one act, he is liable. This case is actually the more intuitive of the two: in a single act targeting both men, he is definitively striking his father, even if he cannot identify which of the two that is. The larger {\i chiddush} is the sequential case: if he cursed one and then went back and cursed the other, or struck one and then struck the other, he is also liable according to the first opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doubtful Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sequential ruling turns on the principle of {\i hatra\u8217'at safek shemah hatra\u8217'ah} \u8212- that a doubtful warning counts as a valid warning for purposes of criminal liability. The scenario unfolds as follows: before the first act, witnesses warn him that this man might be his father. He disregards the warning and proceeds. Before the second act, they warn him again. He again disregards and proceeds. At that point, having completed both acts, he is liable, even though neither individual warning was directed at a certain transgression. The principle holds that the cumulative effect of two doubtful warnings \u8212- each acknowledged and disregarded \u8212- is sufficient to render him liable for the act that definitively constitutes the violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth being precise about what {\i hatra\u8217'at safek} means here. The doubt is not about what the person will do next \u8212- every warning involves some uncertainty about that. Rather, the doubt is whether, even if he proceeds, his act will constitute a definite violation. The first opinion holds that such doubtful warnings, when combined to cover both potential fathers, are sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} takes a more restrictive position: {\i bebat achat chayav, bezeh achar zeh patur} \u8212- if he performed both acts simultaneously he is liable, but if he acted sequentially he is exempt. According to Rabbi Yehuda, {\i hatra\u8217'at safek} does not suffice. A sequential warning \u8212- \u8220"do not strike that man, he might be your father\u8221" \u8212- targets a single act that, at the moment of its commission, remains doubtfully a violation. The warning cannot aggregate across two separate acts to create retroactive certainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a {\i baraita} suggesting that Rabbi Yehuda takes an even more lenient position \u8212- that even the simultaneous case renders him exempt. This creates an apparent contradiction with the view just attributed to him. The Gemara resolves this by invoking {\i trei tannai aliba de\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}: there is a tannaitic dispute about what Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's actual position is, with some authorities reporting that he exempts even the simultaneous case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God/Father Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To explain the reasoning of the more lenient version of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, the Gemara cites {\i Rabbi Chanina}, who derives it from a textual analogy. The Torah uses the term {\i berachah} \u8212- a euphemism for cursing \u8212- in two contexts: cursing God ({\i ish ish ki yekallel elohav}) and cursing one\u8217's father ({\i umkallel aviv ve\u8217'imo mot yumat}). Just as in the case of cursing God there is no ambiguity about the object of the curse \u8212- there is only one God, and no question of which divine being is being cursed \u8212- so too by cursing one\u8217's father, there must be no ambiguity about who the father is. This requirement of clarity applies not merely to avoiding dual targets, but to the very identification of who the father is. Where that identification is genuinely uncertain, even a simultaneous act targeting both candidates fails to satisfy the standard established by the analogy to cursing God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smiting-Cursing Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that {\i hukshah haka\u8217'ah liklalah} \u8212- smiting is compared to cursing. Therefore, the same requirement of clarity that applies to cursing applies equally to smiting. If the act of cursing requires unambiguous identification of the father as its object, so does the act of striking. This connection ensures that the analogy drawn by Rabbi Chanina governs both prohibitions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohanim Mishmarot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the consequences when both potential fathers are {\i kohanim}. The {\i mishnah} establishes that the child in such a case goes up and performs the {\i avodah} in whichever {\i mishmar} he appears in, but does not receive a portion of the {\i korbanot}. The {\i mishmarot} are the rotating priestly watches that serve in the {\i Beit HaMikdash} by week; each {\i mishmar} will refuse to give him a {\i korban} portion on the grounds that he may belong to the other {\i mishmar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Work Without Pay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately raises a question: {\i lama oleh} \u8212- why would he bother performing the {\i avodah} if he receives no portion? The Gemara offers one answer: he is not working for pay. He works because he wants the privilege and the {\i mitzvah} of performing the {\i avodah} in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. That said, this answer does not fully address the force of the question, because the language of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i oleh}, he goes up \u8212- sounds not merely permissive but compulsory. It sounds as though he is being compelled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Family Honor Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore reads the term {\i oleh} as indicating forced participation: {\i be\u8217'al korcho}, he is made to go up against his will. The reasoning is attributed in a long chain \u8212- {\i Rav Acha bar Chanina} said that {\i Abaye} said that {\i Rav Assi} said that {\i Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- to the concern of {\i pgam mishpacha}, family disgrace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dynamic works as follows. Both potential paternal families are {\i kohanim}. When it is their {\i mishmar}\u8217's week to serve, each family faces the same concern: if this young man is not seen performing the {\i avodah} alongside the rest of the {\i mishmar}, observers may assume he belongs to that family but is disqualified \u8212- a {\i pasul kohen}. Neither family wants the public to associate them with an unfit member. Each family therefore insists that he perform the {\i avodah} during their week, lest his absence create suspicion that falls on them. The result is that he ends up performing {\i avodah} in both {\i mishmarot}, even though he receives no {\i korban} portion from either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a pointed objection to this reading, noting \u8212- with characteristic understatement, {\i teima ktzat}, one might wonder a bit \u8212- that it is not the straightforward reading of the word {\i oleh}. If you focus on the language carefully, the more natural reading is not that he is forced to go up, but that he has the right to compel them to allow him to go up: {\i be\u8217'al korcham de-vnei mishmeret}. Even if the {\i mishmar} refuses to give him a {\i korban} portion, they cannot legitimately bar him from performing the {\i avodah} itself. He is entitled to insist on participation. This reading inverts the direction of the compulsion \u8212- it is not the families forcing him, but he insisting on his right against the families.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Av Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then complicates the picture further. Even granting that both potential fathers are from the same {\i mishmar} \u8212- the same weekly rotation \u8212- this does not resolve his inability to receive a {\i korban} portion. Within each {\i mishmar}, there are six {\i batei av}, each of which serves on a different day of the week, with all of them serving together on {\i Shabbat}. The {\i korban} portions are distributed daily based on which {\i beit av} is serving that day. If he does not know which {\i beit av} his father belongs to, he faces the same problem at a smaller scale: he goes to one {\i beit av} and they push him away, saying his father may be from the other one serving on a different day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa\u8217's Fix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Papa} resolves this by rereading the {\i mishnah}\u8217's condition. It is not sufficient that both potential fathers are from the same {\i mishmar}; for him to actually receive a {\i korban} portion, they must be from the same {\i mishmar} and the same {\i beit av}. Only then \u8212- {\i notel chelek echad} \u8212- does he work on that specific day and receive a definite entitlement to his portion. This significantly narrows the scope of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling, but it makes the internal logic precise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chapter Transition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf now moves into the next {\i perek}, which turns from the theoretical discussions of the preceding chapter to the practical mechanics of {\i chalitzah} \u8212- the ceremony in which the {\i yavam} who declines to perform {\i yibum} removes his sandal, enabling the {\i yevamah} to go free. The {\i perek} begins with the {\i mishnah}: {\i mitzvat chalitzah bishloshad dayanim} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i chalitzah} requires three judges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Judges Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for three judges is the Torah\u8217's requirement that she come {\i l\u8217'einei hazkeinim} \u8212- before the elders. The word {\i zekeinim} implies a {\i beit din}. The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i v\u8217'afilu shlosha hedyotot} \u8212- even three commoners suffice. They need not hold {\i semikhah} or be formally ordained; they need not be {\i mumchin}, experts in the technical sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: if even {\i hedyotot} suffice, why does the {\i mishnah} use the term {\i dayanim} at all? The answer is: {\i de\u8217'beinan shlosha she\u8217'yode\u8217'im lehakrot ke\u8217'ein dayanim} \u8212- we require three people who are literate enough to lead the recitation of the verses, functioning in the role of judges even if not possessing their formal qualifications. The practical need is clear: during the {\i chalitzah} ceremony, the {\i yavam} and {\i yevamah} must recite specified Biblical verses. Since many people were not literate, the judges would recite the text and have them repeat it. Accordingly, the judges must themselves be able to read and recite \u8212- they must be capable of {\i lehakrot}, of leading the recitation. The title {\i dayanim} in the {\i mishnah} thus sets a functional bar of literacy rather than a technical bar of ordination, reflecting the role these three individuals are serving rather than any personal credential they must possess.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoe Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses the type of shoe used in the ceremony. The ideal instrument is a {\i sandal} \u8212- a hard leather base with straps \u8212- which {\i Rashi} describes as cooked leather fashioned to be rigid. If she performed {\i chalitzah} with a regular shoe ({\i minal}), it is valid. If she used a soft slipper or sock ({\i anpilya}), it is invalid, since such a covering does not qualify as a shoe even after the fact. As for the {\i sandal} specifically: {\i besandal sheyesh lo akeiv kasher} \u8212- a sandal with a heel is valid; {\i veshein lo akeiv psula} \u8212- one without a heel is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leg Location Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further specifies that the location on the leg matters: {\i min ha\u8217'arkuva ulemata chalitzah kesheirah} \u8212- from the knee downward the {\i chalitzah} is valid; {\i min ha\u8217'arkuva ulema\u8217'alah chalitzah psula} \u8212- from the knee upward it is invalid. {\i Rashi} understands this as addressing a case where the man has a partially amputated leg. The shoe must be worn on a portion of the limb that falls below the knee. If what remains of his leg is only above the knee, and the shoe is placed on that portion, it does not qualify as a shoe in the relevant sense. The {\i chalitzah} must involve the removal of a shoe from a part of the body that functions as the lower leg or foot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoe Variations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with a series of cases. If the sandal does not belong to the {\i yavam} \u8212- {\i shein shelo} \u8212- or if it is made of wood rather than leather, or if it is his left sandal worn on his right foot, the {\i chalitzah} is nonetheless valid after the fact. If the sandal is oversized but he can still walk in it, or if it is undersized but covers the majority of his foot, it is likewise valid. These cases represent situations where the shoe is not ideal but remains sufficient to constitute the shoe that the Torah requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Literacy Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's discussion of why the {\i mishnah} calls the participants {\i dayanim} leads, as noted above, to the conclusion that the essential qualification is the ability to lead the recitation of verses. This literacy requirement distinguishes the role from a purely passive one: the three individuals are not merely witnesses but active participants who enable the {\i yavam} and {\i yevamah} to fulfill their verbal obligations within the ceremony. The Gemara confirms this by reading the {\i mishnah}\u8217's use of {\i dayanim} as shorthand for those who are {\i ke\u8217'ein dayanim} \u8212- functioning in the manner of judges \u8212- which carries with it this minimal literacy expectation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three vs Five Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Judges View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited that articulates the standard position more explicitly: {\i mitzvat chalitzah bishloshad sheyodim lehakrot ke\u8217'ein dayanim} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i chalitzah} requires three who know how to lead the recitation, functioning like judges. The {\i baraita} presents this as the normative rule, understood as a {\i lechatchila} standard that does not demand formally ordained judges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Judges View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} dissents: {\i bachamisha} \u8212- five judges are required. This is a notably uncommon requirement; while the first {\i perek} of {\i Sanhedrin} does discuss courts of various sizes, a {\i beit din} of five is not a common or familiar institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Word Count Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} derives the required numbers through a counting of the word {\i zekenim} in the relevant {\i pesukim}. {\i Zekenim} \u8212- elders \u8212- appears in the plural, indicating at minimum two. {\i Zekenim} without the definite article implies two; the additional occurrence of {\i ziknei} adds another two, bringing the total to four. Since a {\i beit din} cannot have an even number of judges \u8212- {\i ein beit din shakul} \u8212- one more is added.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Odd Numbers Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibition against an even-numbered court underlies the logic of both positions. The {\i tanna kamma} counts two occurrences of {\i zekenim} yielding two, adds one to avoid an even number, and arrives at three. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} counts additional occurrences yielding four, adds one to avoid an even number, and arrives at five. In both cases, the derivation is a counting exercise rather than a direct invocation of {\i beit din} as a formal legal institution \u8212- a point that carries significant implications, as we will see.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Textual Derivations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zekenim Counts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now examines what each {\i tanna} does with the words left over once the numerical derivation is complete. Since both positions use some occurrences of {\i zekenim} to count up their required number, the question arises: what does each position do with the remaining textual details? This produces a set of independent {\i derashot} from the {\i pesukim} of {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hedyotot Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the {\i tanna kamma}, one occurrence of {\i zekenim} is used not for counting but as a source permitting non-expert judges: it broadens the class of eligible participants to include {\i hedyotot}. Even though {\i zekenim} implies something like a {\i beit din}, the additional usage signals that the category is wider than formally ordained judges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yisrael Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} derives the permission to use non-experts from a different source: the word {\i b\u8217'Yisrael} in the phrase {\i veyikarei shemo b\u8217'Yisrael}. Since it says \u8220"among Israel\u8221" without further qualification, this includes any member of Israel \u8212- even one without {\i semikhah}. For Rabbi Yehuda, the {\i zekenim} occurrences are thus entirely consumed by the numerical derivation, and the permission for {\i hedyotot} must come from elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sight Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses the requirement that the judges have sight. This is derived from the word {\i l\u8217'einei} \u8212- \u8220"before their eyes\u8221" \u8212- in the phrase {\i l\u8217'einei hazkenim}: {\i prat lesumim}, this excludes blind judges. The Gemara adds an important inference: the very fact that the Torah found it necessary to exclude the blind implies that these judges are not required to be {\i semuchim}. If they were required to be formally ordained members of a {\i sanhedrin}, blind individuals would already be excluded by the general rules governing ordained courts \u8212- the special exclusion in {\i chalitzah} would be superfluous. The necessity of the exclusion thus demonstrates that {\i chalitzah} does not require ordained judges, who are already disqualified from being blind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Witness Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} offers an additional and more specific interpretation of {\i l\u8217'einei}: {\i tzrichei dayani lemichzi roka dekanfik mipuma deyevama} \u8212- the judges must actually see the spit exit from the mouth of the {\i yevamah}. This goes beyond merely possessing the faculty of sight; it demands that the judges directly witness the act of spitting at the moment it occurs. The source is the verse {\i veyorka befanav} \u8212- \u8220"and she shall spit before him\u8221" \u8212- which is governed by the earlier phrase {\i l\u8217'einei hazkenim}, meaning the {\i zekenim} themselves must witness the spitting as it happens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a textual accounting problem: if {\i l\u8217'einei} is used to teach Rava\u8217's requirement of directly witnessing the spit, it cannot simultaneously serve as the source for excluding blind judges. And if {\i l\u8217'einei} teaches the exclusion of the blind, where does Rava\u8217's requirement come from? The Gemara works through this tension carefully. The resolution is that {\i Rabbi Yehuda} uses {\i l\u8217'einei} exclusively for Rava\u8217's requirement \u8212- the judges must witness the spit \u8212- and derives the permission for {\i hedyotot} instead from {\i b\u8217'Yisrael}, as noted above. The {\i tanna kamma}, by contrast, uses one occurrence of {\i zekenim} to permit {\i hedyotot} and relies on {\i l\u8217'einei} for Rava\u8217's requirement as well, with the exclusion of the blind following as a natural implication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Convert Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another derivation from {\i b\u8217'Yisrael}: {\i b\u8217'Beis Din shel Yisrael velo b\u8217'Beis Din shel geirim} \u8212- the {\i beit din} for {\i chalitzah} must consist of native-born Jews, not converts. This is taught in a {\i braita} attributed to {\i Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda}, whose ruling will become practically relevant later in the daf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proclamation Duty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the phrase {\i veyikarei shemo b\u8217'Yisrael beis chalutz hana\u8217'al} \u8212- \u8220"and his name shall be called in Israel, the house of the one whose sandal was removed\u8221" \u8212- is read not merely as a future description but as an active directive. {\i Rabbi Tarfon} is cited as an example: when a {\i yevamah} came to perform {\i chalitzah} before him and those assembled with him, he instructed everyone present, {\i enu kulchem chalutz hana\u8217'al} \u8212- all of you should respond and say \u8220"the one whose sandal was removed.\u8221" This proclamation is part of the role of those constituting the {\i beit din}: they represent {\i Yisrael} in publicly calling him by this designation. The verse is thus not merely a passive prediction but a mandate for active declaration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakhic Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Judges Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} quotes {\i Rav Nachman}: the {\i halakhah} is {\i chalitzah bishloshad} \u8212- three judges. The basis for this ruling is that the {\i mishnah} in our tractate, which is a {\i stam mishna}, reflects the view of the {\i tanna kamma}. When a {\i stam mishna} exists, we follow it as the established {\i halakhah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mi\u8217'un Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} then presses {\i Rav Nachman} with a question: if we rule three for {\i chalitzah} on the basis of a {\i stam mishna}, should we not likewise rule three for {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- the procedure by which a minor girl refuses a marriage arranged by her mother or brothers? There is a {\i stam mishna} in {\i Sanhedrin} that lists both {\i mi\u8217'un} and {\i chalitzah} together as requiring three. On the same logic, {\i mi\u8217'un} should also require three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mi\u8217'un Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is complicated by a {\i baraita} concerning {\i mi\u8217'un}. {\i Beit Shammai} holds that {\i mi\u8217'un} requires a {\i beit din} of {\i mumchin} \u8212- experts. The reasoning is intuitive: {\i mi\u8217'un} effectively dissolves what is treated as a marriage, and dissolving a marriage might be thought to require a court of greater authority. {\i Beit Hillel} disagrees: {\i mi\u8217'un} may be performed with or without a formal {\i beit din}. Yet both {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} agree \u8212- {\i v\u8217'eilu v\u8217'eilu modim she\u8217'tzrichim shlosha} \u8212- that three are still required. Thus the {\i baraita} seems to confirm three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mi\u8217'un: Two Judges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, a further teaching \u8212- {\i Rav Yosef bar Minyumi} said in the name of {\i Rav Nachman} \u8212- rules that the {\i halakhah} follows the pair of {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Yehuda} and {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Rabbi Yosi}, who hold that even two suffice for {\i mi\u8217'un}. This appears to contradict the {\i stam mishna} pairing {\i mi\u8217'un} with {\i chalitzah} at three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source Count Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to justify this asymmetry through a counting of {\i stam} sources. For {\i chalitzah}, there are two independent {\i stam} sources requiring three: the {\i mishnah} in our tractate and the {\i mishnah} in {\i Sanhedrin}. For {\i mi\u8217'un}, there is only one \u8212- the {\i mishnah} in {\i Sanhedrin}. Therefore, the ruling for {\i chalitzah} is more firmly established. The Gemara, however, immediately pushes back: since when do we count {\i stam mishnayot} and give greater weight to two over one? A single {\i stam mishna} should suffice to establish the {\i halakhah}. Having two does not logically add force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then proposes a further count: even for {\i mi\u8217'un} there are two {\i stam} sources \u8212- the {\i mishnah} in {\i Sanhedrin} and a separate {\i mishnah} that discusses whether a judge who witnessed a {\i mi\u8217'un} may subsequently marry the woman, ruling that he may because he acted {\i b\u8217'Beis Din}, implying a court of three. For {\i chalitzah}, one might then count three {\i stam} sources. But again, the Gemara objects: why should three count for more than two? The differential between two and three sources does not explain why one {\i halakhah} overrides its {\i stam} and the other does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Concedes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately resolves the matter through a different argument. {\i Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak} explains: the reason we are confident that {\i chalitzah} is definitively three is because the {\i stam} ruling follows a {\i machloket} in which one party \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- implicitly conceded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The evidence comes from the {\i mishnah} in {\i Sanhedrin}. That {\i mishnah} lists {\i semichat zekenim} and {\i egla arufa} as requiring three, and attributes the alternative view of five to {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. The same {\i mishnah} then continues and rules that {\i chalitzah} and {\i mi\u8217'un} require three \u8212- and notably, {\i Rabbi Yehuda does not dispute this}. Since Rabbi Yehuda explicitly argued for five by {\i chalitzah} in the {\i baraita} we quoted earlier, his silence in the {\i mishnah} in {\i Sanhedrin} indicates that he retracted his position \u8212- {\i hadar bei Rabbi Yehuda}. He abandoned the view of five specifically with respect to {\i chalitzah}. This concession by the dissenting voice makes the ruling of three especially solid. No such concession exists for {\i mi\u8217'un}, which is why that ruling remains more open to challenge.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Procedures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Location Designation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} introduces a procedural requirement: {\i tzrichei dayani l\u8217'mikva duchtah} \u8212- the judges must designate a specific location for the {\i chalitzah} proceeding. They cannot conduct it in an ad hoc manner, convening wherever they happen to be. The source is the verse {\i v\u8217'altah yevamto hasha\u8217'arah el hazkeinim} \u8212- \u8220"and his sister-in-law shall go up to the gate, to the elders.\u8221" The {\i sha\u8217'ar ha\u8217'ir}, the city gate, was historically where elders would sit in judgment. Even in times when there is no such physical gate serving that function, the requirement that she \u8220"come to\u8221" the judges implies that the judges must have a designated and established place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Judges Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then brings examples of Amoraim who, in practice, assembled five judges for a {\i chalitzah}. {\i Avtolas} and {\i Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua} performed a {\i chalitzah} with five. The Gemara asks: like whom? Is this following {\i Rabbi Yehuda}? But we established that Rabbi Yehuda himself retracted his position. How can they be acting on a view he abandoned?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five for Publicity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i l\u8217'firsumei milta} \u8212- they assembled five not because of a technical halakhic requirement but in order to publicize the matter. The {\i chalitzah} has real consequences for the woman\u8217's status: a {\i chalutza} may not marry a {\i kohen}, and potential suitors should know she is now available. Assembling five rather than three serves the purpose of making the proceedings more public and well-known \u8212- a larger gathering ensures broader awareness. This is reinforced by the verse {\i veyikarei shemo b\u8217'Yisrael}: there is a built-in publicizing function to the ceremony. {\i Rashi} notes both dimensions: ensuring a {\i kohen} does not inadvertently marry her, and ensuring that eligible men know she is now free to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Publicity Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} once arrived at the {\i yeshiva} of {\i Rav Kahana}. Rav Kahana said to him: {\i salik mar l\u8217'gaban l\u8217'miluyei bei chamisha} \u8212- master, come join us to complete our number of five. Here too the purpose was {\i pirsumei milsa}: Rav Kahana wanted five specifically to publicize the {\i chalitzah} proceeding, not because the law demanded it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In a separate account, {\i Rav Kahana} recalled standing before {\i Rav Yehuda}, who told him to come stand on a particular woodpile \u8212- {\i sak l\u8217'tzikli d\u8217'kani} \u8212- to be counted among five, for the purpose of {\i l\u8217'firsumei milta}. This detail about the woodpile is also relevant to the {\i duchta} requirement: the \u8220"designated place\u8221" need not be a permanent or formal courtroom. Designating any specific spot \u8212- even a pile of wood \u8212- appears to satisfy the requirement. The designation itself is what matters, not the permanence or formality of the location. When those around him asked {\i Rav Yehuda} why he needed five, he replied explicitly: {\i ki heichi d\u8217'lifarsim milsa} \u8212- to publicize the matter. His reason was entirely one of publicity and not of legal necessity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ger Status Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records that {\i Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda} was in the presence of {\i Rav Yehuda} when the same invitation was extended \u8212- come stand on the woodpile and be counted among five, for the purpose of publicizing the {\i chalitzah}. Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda declined, citing the rule that he had studied: {\i b\u8217'Beis Din shel Yisrael velo b\u8217'Beis Din shel geirim} \u8212- the {\i beit din} for {\i chalitzah} must consist of native-born Jews, not converts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His refusal was grounded in the {\i pasuk}: {\i veyikarei shemo b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- the phrase {\i b\u8217'Yisrael} is read to require a {\i beit din shel Yisrael}, excluding a court composed of {\i geirim}. Since he himself was a {\i ger}, he could not participate. {\i V\u8217'ana geira ana} \u8212- and I am a convert. The {\i braita} that Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda himself taught had excluded {\i geirim} from the {\i beit din} of {\i chalitzah}, and he applied it scrupulously to himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Praised Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} responded with high praise: {\i k\u8217'gon Shmuel bar Yehuda} \u8212- someone like Shmuel bar Yehuda, who is so meticulous and {\i frum} that he disqualifies himself even when invited to participate in an honor \u8212- such a person I would trust so implicitly that I would treat his testimony as determinative enough to extract money. In other words, Rav Yehuda held Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda\u8217's integrity and scrupulousness in such esteem that he considered his word effectively equivalent to full testimonial proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Reliability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara does not accept Rav Yehuda\u8217's hyperbole at face value. {\i Mafkina salka deitach?} \u8212- would you really extract money on his testimony alone? The Torah requires two witnesses: {\i hasvei shnei eidim amar Rachmana}. The Gemara therefore reinterprets: {\i mar\u8217'ana shtara apumei} \u8212- rather, what Rav Yehuda meant is that if Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda testified that a particular document was forged or invalid, Rav Yehuda would trust him sufficiently to disqualify the document on that basis. That is a narrower but still meaningful expression of trust.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Bar Remains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final observation the Gemara draws from this exchange is striking. Recall that the assembled group already had its three valid judges \u8212- the technical requirement of {\i chalitzah} was already satisfied. Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda was being invited only to complete the number to five, which served merely the purpose of {\i pirsumei milta}. Yet he still declined. Even within the non-technical, publicity-oriented framework of adding a fourth or fifth person, he held that the {\i din} of {\i b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- which bars converts \u8212- applied. The conceptual framework of \u8220"five for publicity\u8221" did not operate in a legal vacuum; it was still understood as constituting a {\i beit din}, and a {\i beit din} requires non-{\i ger} status even for its non-essential members. The daf closes on this note, with the promise that tomorrow\u8217's discussion will continue exploring the role of a {\i ger} on a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 102\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ger Serving on Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah & Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the very bottom of 101b, with two words \u8212- {\i amar Rava} \u8212- that carry us into 102a. The immediate context is {\i chalitzah} and the question of whether it requires a {\i beit din} of three or five. The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion had been that three suffices halachically, yet we find stories of {\i rabbanim} who nonetheless ensured that five were present for the purpose of {\i lefirsumei milsa} \u8212- to make the matter more public. This concern for publicity may have been what led Rabbi Meir to require five as a matter of {\i halacha}, since part of the role of the {\i beit din} is not merely to adjudicate but to represent {\i klal Yisrael}. This is what the {\i gemara} expressed when it said that the {\i beit din} itself recites {\i chalutz ha-na\u8217'al}, because they are, in that moment, representing the community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concluded the previous discussion with a story in which a {\i ger} who was invited to participate in a {\i chalitzah} declined, citing the requirement of {\i b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- that the {\i beit din} must represent naturally born Jews. What is striking is that he was only being asked to serve as one of the additional two members beyond the core three. The three essential members were already in place, and his role was purely for the sake of {\i lefirsumei milsa}. Nevertheless, since the entire group of five was being conceived as an expanded {\i beit din}, he did not wish to be part of even positions four or five. The {\i gemara} now pauses from the {\i yibum} material to examine the question of a {\i ger} serving on a {\i beit din} more carefully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Dan Chaveiro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava}: a {\i ger}, though excluded in the ordinary case from sitting on a {\i beit din}, may judge a fellow {\i ger} \u8212- {\i devar Torah}, biblically speaking. The source is the verse in Deuteronomy: {\i som tasim alecha melech asher yivchar Hashem Elokecha bo mikerev achecha tasim alecha melech lo tuchal lases alecha ish nochri asher lo achecha hu}. The phrase {\i mikerev achecha} \u8212- from among your brothers \u8212- is understood to refer to those naturally born as Jews, and this requirement is extended beyond the institution of monarchy to encompass positions of appointed authority generally, including membership on a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi offers two explanations for why the {\i melech} passage applies to a {\i dayan}. The first is based on the verse {\i melech b\u8217'mishpat ya\u8217'amid aretz} \u8212- the king establishes the land through justice \u8212- and from this we learn that judges participate in that very system; like a king, a judge must be independent, beholden to no one, and free from outside influence. The second explanation, which Rashi says flows more naturally from the broader {\i sugya}, is that every position of appointed authority \u8212- every {\i simah} \u8212- is included in the requirement of {\i mikerev achecha}. This is the formulation: {\i kol simot she\u8217'atah mesim lo yehei ela mikerev achecha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Moshe & Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what constitutes a position of authority has broad practical implications. Rav Moshe Feinstein was asked whether a {\i ger} could serve as {\i rosh yeshiva} \u8212- a position that clearly carries with it significant authority: the power to admit or expel students, to impose disciplinary measures, and to govern institutional life. The question is not trivial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Moshe\u8217's approach begins from a fascinating premise: there is a {\i mitzvah} of {\i ahavas hager}, and that {\i mitzvah} creates a halachic pressure to read the restriction on positions of authority as narrowly as possible. The {\i mitzvah} does not dissolve the restriction, but it shapes the interpretive posture. From there, Rav Moshe develops a conceptual distinction that has become central to this entire area of {\i halacha}: the difference between {\i imposed} authority and {\i contractual} authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In a context where authority is genuinely imposed \u8212- where someone can be compelled, against their will, to submit to another\u8217's power \u8212- that is the paradigmatic case of {\i malchut}, of the kind of authority for which the Torah requires {\i mikerev achecha}. A king in an authoritarian society exercises precisely this kind of unilateral power over people who have not chosen it. By contrast, in modern democratic and institutional settings, most authority relationships are entered into voluntarily. When a student chooses to attend a yeshiva, he accepts, contractually, the authority of the {\i rosh yeshiva} over certain aspects of his conduct. That is a fundamentally different relationship. It is not {\i alecha} in the sense the Torah intends.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning naturally invites further examination. A {\i dayan} sitting in judgment is one of the few genuinely contemporary examples of imposed authority in the classic sense \u8212- a litigant can be summoned to court whether he consents or not. That kind of compelled submission to authority is precisely the scenario {\i mikerev achecha} addresses, and therefore the exclusion of a {\i ger} from serving as {\i dayan} retains its full force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with voluntary arbitration is telling. As Rav Moshe\u8217's analysis implies, if both parties freely choose to bring their dispute before a particular {\i dayan} or {\i beit din} \u8212- even one that includes a {\i ger} \u8212- that is a contractual relationship, and the prohibition would not apply. The authority is not being imposed; it has been accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might raise the example of a shul president. The Young Israel organization once issued a letter asserting, among other things, that neither women nor {\i geirim} may serve as shul presidents, citing the {\i issur} of {\i she\u8217'im alecha ish nochri} and the parallel concern about women in positions of authority. This caused considerable consternation when the letter circulated among shul presidents, some of whom had not known this restriction existed. For Rav Moshe, however, the very idea that a shul presidency constitutes a position of {\i malchut}-level authority would be difficult to sustain. Paradoxically, given his framework, treating a {\i ger} as categorically barred from a purely elective, voluntary position might itself be a form of oppression \u8212- a violation of the very {\i mitzvah} of {\i ahavas hager} that animates his analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question was also raised about the Herodian dynasty, whose members were {\i geirim} of a sort and who wielded authority over the Jewish people by arrangement with Rome. Rav Moshe\u8217's framework cuts through that comparison: what matters is not the origin of the authority relationship but its character vis-\u224 ?-vis those who are subject to it. The people over whom Herod ruled did not choose him; his power was imposed. That is the operative criterion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Parentage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces an additional and quite striking requirement specific to {\i chalitzah}. The general rule, as developed, is: {\i ger dan et chaveiro} \u8212- a {\i ger} may judge a fellow {\i ger}. And further: {\i v\u8217'im hayesa imo miyisrael dan afilu Yisrael} \u8212- if his mother was from Jewish stock, he may judge even naturally born Jews. One might expect this to resolve the matter entirely: if someone has a Jewish mother, he is, by every standard we employ, fully Jewish \u8212- not a {\i ger} at all. Yet the {\i gemara} apparently treats such a person as occupying something of a liminal status if his father was a convert, and it feels the need to specify that yes, this person may serve on a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} adds: {\i u\u8217'le\u8217'inyan chalitzah ad she\u8217'aviv v\u8217'imo miyisrael} \u8212- for {\i chalitzah} specifically, the requirement is more demanding still. It is not enough that the {\i dayan} himself not be a {\i ger}. His parents must also not be {\i geirim}. The derivation comes from the phrase {\i v\u8217'nikra shemo b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- the word {\i b\u8217'Yisrael} in the {\i chalitzah} passage imposes a heightened standard of natural Jewish lineage, beyond what is required for an ordinary {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable {\i halacha}, and it generates several observations worth pausing over. The first is almost paradoxical: the scenario of a {\i ger} being judged by a {\i beit din} that includes a fellow {\i ger} \u8212- the {\i ger dan et chaveiro} case \u8212- does not in practice arise in {\i chalitzah}. How would a {\i ger} have a paternal brother? Only if their father was a {\i ger} who had multiple sons. But if a son of a {\i ger} had a Jewish mother, then {\i horaso v\u8217'lidaso bikdusha} \u8212- he was born in holiness \u8212- and he would be considered fully {\i Yisrael}, not a {\i ger} at all. The very scenario the {\i gemara} might seem to be protecting against \u8212- a {\i ger} serving on the {\i chalitzah beit din} for another {\i ger} \u8212- may be practically impossible to construct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second important issue concerns the scope of the {\i ger\u8217's} exclusion from {\i beit din} entirely, independent of the {\i chalitzah} special case. Rashi, in the entry beginning {\i hager afilu chaveiro}, explains that the exclusion of a {\i ger} from judging naturally born Jews applies specifically to {\i dinei nefashot} \u8212- capital cases, and presumably cases of lashes. For {\i dinei mamonot}, Rashi reads the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language of {\i hakol kesherim la-don} \u8212- everyone is eligible to judge monetary cases \u8212- as explicitly including a {\i ger}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot disagrees. Elsewhere in {\i Shas}, Tosafot argues at length that a {\i ger} is excluded even from a {\i dinei mamonot beit din} over naturally born {\i Yisrael} when the authority is imposed. The conceptual rationale offered by Rashi \u8212- that imposed authority over a person\u8217's life and body is the core concern \u8212- would limit the restriction to capital and corporeal punishment. Tosafot\u8217's broader reading sees the imposition of any binding judgment as falling within {\i mikerev achecha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both Rashi and Tosafot, however, agree that if the parties have freely chosen to accept the {\i ger\u8217's} authority \u8212- if it is genuinely contractual \u8212- then the restriction does not apply. This is the point where Tosafot\u8217's analysis converges with the logic Rav Moshe would later develop so fully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the framework: a {\i ger} may judge a fellow {\i ger}. A {\i ger} may not judge naturally born {\i Yisrael} in a case of imposed authority. According to Rashi, this restriction is limited to {\i dinei nefashot}; according to Tosafot, it extends to {\i dinei mamonot} as well. But in cases of freely accepted arbitration \u8212- {\i peshara} or voluntary submission to the {\i beit din}\u8217's authority \u8212- both agree the restriction falls away. And then, as a category unto itself: {\i chalitzah} requires not merely a {\i dayan} who is not a {\i ger}, but one whose very parents were naturally born Jews.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sandals & Minhag\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliyahu & Minhag\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the type of footwear used in {\i chalitzah}, and introduces a teaching that is, on reflection, among the more theologically charged statements in this entire passage. {\i Amar Rav Kahana amar Rav}: if Eliyahu HaNavi were to come and declare that one may perform {\i chalitzah} with a {\i minal} \u8212- a shoe \u8212- we would listen to him. But if he were to come and declare that one may {\i not} perform {\i chalitzah} with a sandal, we would not listen to him \u8212- {\i she-kevar nahagu ha-am be-sandal}, for the people have already established the practice of using a sandal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef quotes essentially the same teaching with a subtle but revealing difference. According to his version, if Eliyahu were to say that one {\i cannot} use a shoe, we would listen \u8212- since there is no established practice one way or the other regarding shoes. But if he were to say one cannot use a sandal, we would not listen, again because the practice is fixed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difference between the two versions is the {\i ikka beineihu}: whether a shoe may be used {\i lechatchila}. One version assumes that shoes are already permissible {\i lechatchila} and that Eliyahu\u8217's possible contribution would be to tell us otherwise; the other assumes shoes are only {\i bedi\u8217'avad} and that Eliyahu might permit them outright. Both agree, however, that no voice \u8212- not even Eliyahu\u8217's \u8212- could overturn the established practice of using a sandal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} itself passes over the theological dimension of this statement without comment and moves on to the shoe-versus-sandal discussion. But it would be a mistake to let that dimension pass unexamined. This statement is sometimes placed alongside the principle of {\i lo bashamayim hi} \u8212- the Torah is not in heaven \u8212- as an expression of the autonomy of rabbinic legal process. But it is worth noting that what is actually being asserted here is somewhat different, and in one respect more modest and in another more pointed. More modest, because the classic {\i lo bashamayim hi} claim would be that we do not listen to Eliyahu about {\i any} halachic question \u8212- that a prophet carries no special authority in a halachic debate, that he would need to argue his position like any {\i amora} or {\i tanna}, and that his being a {\i navi} confers nothing. That would be a more absolute statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What this passage asserts is something narrower but in its own way more powerful: the reason we would not listen to Eliyahu is not that prophetic authority is irrelevant to {\i halacha} in general, but that established practice carries a weight that even a heavenly voice cannot overturn. The authority here is not the text against the prophet \u8212- it is {\i minhag} against the prophet. And that says something remarkable about the role of practice in the halachic system: that what the community has actually been doing, supervised by and in the presence of rabbis, acquires a standing that is not easily dislodged by theoretical authority of any kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important nuance deserves emphasis: this is not folk custom in the ordinary sense. {\i Chalitzah} is performed before a {\i beit din}. The sandal has been used in the presence of rabbis, in a formal halachic setting, over many generations. The practice is not merely tolerated; it is rabbinically endorsed and overseen. That gives it the weight of established {\i halacha}, not merely popular habit. It sits, in effect, at the authoritative end of the spectrum of {\i divrei rabanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoe vs.\u160 ?Sandal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the tanna\u8217'itic debate about shoes. The {\i mishnah} states {\i chaltza be-min\u8217'al, chalitzatah kesheirah} \u8212- if she performed {\i chalitzah} with a shoe, it is valid. The language sounds {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. But the {\i gemara} argues that even {\i lechatchila} a shoe would be fine according to one view: the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrasing is explained as a function of the parallel structure of the clause \u8212- since the {\i mishnah}\u8217's second case ({\i anpalya}, a sock or slipper) is invalid even {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, the {\i reisha} mirrors that phrasing, even though the {\i din} of a shoe is fully valid even {\i lechatchila} according to this opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then frames the question as a debate among {\i tannaim} and brings a {\i baraita} in which Rabbi Yosi recounts a journey to Netzivin. There he encountered an elderly man who reported that Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira, a regular Shabbat guest at his home, had performed {\i chalitzah} many times. Rabbi Yosi asked: did he use a shoe or a sandal? The man\u8217's response was incredulous \u8212- {\i ve-chi choltzin be-minal?} \u8212- as if the question barely warranted an answer. Of course a sandal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda B. Beteira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov, citing Rabbi Meir, states explicitly that one may perform {\i chalitzah} with a shoe {\i lechatchila}. The {\i gemara} then asks: according to the view that a shoe may {\i not} be used {\i lechatchila}, what is the reason?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first possibility raised is the structural concern of {\i me\u8217'al de-me\u8217'al}: when removing a laced shoe, one first undoes the laces \u8212- which sit atop the leather upper \u8212- and then removes the leather upper itself. The Torah says {\i me-al raglo}, from upon his foot, and not {\i me-al de-me\u8217'al}, from upon the thing that is upon his foot. If the straps are {\i me\u8217'al de-me\u8217'al}, perhaps there is a {\i derashah} problem. The {\i gemara} rejects this: if that were truly a concern, it would invalidate a shoe {\i bedi\u8217'avad} as well, not merely {\i lechatchila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} instead offers a {\i gezeirah mishum minal merupat} \u8212- a rabbinic precaution against using a torn or structurally compromised shoe. A shoe, unlike a sandal, provides substantial enclosure and support, such that even if it is torn beyond the point of halachic qualification, it can still be kept on the foot. Because it is difficult to evaluate on the spot whether a shoe in hand meets the standard, the rabbis ruled that shoes should not be used {\i lechatchila} \u8212- one should not create the risk of using a deficient shoe in the first place. A sandal, by contrast, is so minimally structured that if it is genuinely broken, it simply cannot be worn. The problem thus polices itself. An additional variation on this concern is the case of {\i chatzi minal} \u8212- half a shoe \u8212- which raises similar questions about what remains a valid {\i chalitzah} implement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents a statement of Rav Kahana \u8212- here the Rav Kahana who was a student of Rav \u8212- that had he not personally witnessed Rabbi Chiya perform {\i chalitzah} with a sandal that had {\i shnatzin} (straps), he would have thought the proper implement was the sandal of a nomadic tribesman, which binds tighter to the foot. Since {\i chalitzah} is the act of removing something from the foot, the more securely the sandal is fastened, the more complete and deliberate the act of removal. The current practice, he reports, is to fasten the sandal with straps tied in a knot, not merely a simple knot without wrapping. The reason: {\i ki heichi de-tehavi chalitzah me-alya} \u8212- so that the {\i chalitzah} should be a well-executed, fully valid act. This progression of reasoning \u8212- from the choice of sandal over shoe, to the preference for a tight sandal over a loose one, to the development of dedicated {\i chalitzah} sandals used by communities today \u8212- reflects how halachic requirements, once articulated, generate a corresponding set of practical tools.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shoe Removal Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heel vs.\u160 ?Foot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav}: the {\i yevamah} is released to remarry upon the removal of the majority of the heel \u8212- {\i bi-shmitat rov ha-akeiv}. That is, once the heel clears the sandal, even if the foot has not fully slipped out, the {\i chalitzah} is complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i meitivei} challenges this: the {\i mishnah} teaches that if the straps undid themselves, or the sandal slipped off on its own \u8212- {\i shamat rov ha-regel} \u8212- the {\i chalitzah} is invalid. This formulation speaks of the majority of the {\i regel} \u8212- the foot \u8212- not the {\i akeiv}, the heel. The implication is that we require the majority of the {\i regel}, and mere removal of the majority of the heel is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension by identifying the two: {\i hainu rov ha-regel hainu rov ha-akeiv} \u8212- when we say \u8220"majority of the foot,\u8221" we mean the heel. The reason: {\i d\u8217'kula cheila d\u8217'char\u8217'a a-leideh dachis} \u8212- the entire weight of the leg and body rests upon the heel. Since the heel bears the weight, it constitutes the functionally defining part of the foot for this purpose. When the heel is free, the foot is, in the halachically operative sense, removed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Process vs.\u160 ?Result\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion supports a teaching of Rav Yannai. {\i Bein she-hitir hu ve-shamatah hi} \u8212- whether he undid the straps and she removed the shoe, {\i bein she-hitirah hi ve-shamat hu} \u8212- or she undid the straps and he slipped his foot out \u8212- either way, the {\i chalitzah} is invalid. Validity requires that she both undo the straps and remove the shoe: {\i ad she-tatir hi ve-tishmot hi}. She must perform the entire act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this, Rav Yannai poses a further question: {\i kera\u8217'atehu mahu} \u8212- what if she did not remove the shoe by the ordinary act of undoing and sliding it off, but rather tore it off his foot? And {\i sraftehu mahu} \u8212- what if she burned it off? The underlying question is a classic {\i chakira}: {\i gilui kar\u8217'a ba\u8217'inan ve-ika, o dilma chalitzah ba\u8217'inan ve-leika} \u8212- do we require the end result of an exposed foot (achieved in either case), or do we require a specific {\i act} of {\i chalitzah} \u8212- removal in the formal sense \u8212- which would presuppose keeping the implement whole and drawing it off? This is the same question in two forms: one where the shoe is destroyed but the foot is exposed, and one \u8212- the double-shoe case below \u8212- where the shoe is removed without exposing the foot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question remains {\i teiku}, unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Shoes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'ei Rav Nechemiah me-Rabba}: the double-shoe scenario. Suppose a person wears two shoes, one on top of the other. If she removes the top shoe while the bottom remains \u8212- that is straightforwardly invalid: {\i me-al amar rachmana, ve-lo me-al de-me\u8217'al} \u8212- the Torah says from upon his foot, not from upon the thing upon his foot. She removed something that was not directly on the foot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the more interesting case is: she tears open the top shoe and then removes the bottom one, while the top shoe remains in place on his foot. The bottom shoe has been removed; the top shoe remains. So: {\i chalitzah be\u8217'inan ve-ika} \u8212- was the shoe removed? Yes. {\i O dilma gilluya de-khar\u8217'a be\u8217'inan ve-leika} \u8212- but was the foot exposed? No, the top shoe remains over it. This is the mirror image of the process-versus-result question: there, the foot was exposed without the shoe being removed; here, the shoe was removed without the foot being exposed. The {\i gemara} confirms that this scenario is not merely theoretical \u8212- the {\i gemara} notes that Rav Yehuda was observed going out into the marketplace wearing five pairs of shoes layered one atop the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Informal Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav}: a {\i yevamah} who grew up in the household of the brothers \u8212- waiting, as a young girl, until she reached halachic maturity \u8212- and then {\i yibum} falls to one of them: we are not concerned that perhaps at some point during those years she had removed the sandal of one of the brothers. The concern being: if she had, that might have constituted a {\i chalitzah}, which would preclude {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} probes the implication. If the reason we are unconcerned is simply that we never witnessed it, then had we witnessed it, we might be concerned. But the {\i gemara} notes that a {\i beraita} teaches: {\i bein she-nitkaven hu ve-lo nitkavna hi, o she-nitkavna hi ve-lo nitkaven hu, chalitzatah psulah, ad she-yitkavnu shneihem} \u8212- unless both parties have {\i kavanah}, the {\i chalitzah} is not valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot extends the {\i kavanah} requirement beyond mere interior intention. The {\i kavanah} must be evident \u8212- it must be contextually clear that what is happening is {\i chalitzah}. A child casually helping her brother-in-law remove his sandal after a long day, with no formal setting and no evident intent on either side, would not constitute {\i chalitzah} even if technically she removed the shoe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers two responses to the question of why we are unconcerned. The first: even if we had witnessed the removal, we would not be concerned \u8212- {\i ein chosheshin shema kivnu} \u8212- because we would not assume the requisite intent was present, and moreover, following Tosafot\u8217's reading, the context would not have manifested the intent that {\i chalitzah} requires. The second, more stringent position: {\i davka ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'lo chazinan} \u8212- the only reason we are unconcerned is that we did not see it. Had we seen it, we would be concerned. This second opinion then resolves the {\i kavanah} requirement by distinguishing between levels of {\i chalitzah}: a fully valid {\i chalitzah} \u8212- one that releases her to marry anyone \u8212- requires evident, bilateral intent. But a middling, defective {\i chalitzah} \u8212- one where the formal conditions are questionable \u8212- may still be sufficient to disqualify the brothers from performing {\i yibum}, even if it does not fully release her. She would need a second, valid {\i chalitzah} to remarry. The {\i gemara} thus allows for the possibility of a {\i chalitzah} that is {\i poseles} for the brothers without being {\i matir} for the world.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shoe Materials\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tachash Leather\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav}: a sandal sewn with flax \u8212- {\i sandal ha-tafur be-pishtan} \u8212- may not be used for {\i chalitzah}. Rashi offers two readings of what this means: either the threads used to sew the sandal are made of flax, or a flax insert has been sewn into the sandal as a cushioning sole. In either case, the sandal is disqualified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source is the verse in Ezekiel: {\i va-eneilech tachash} \u8212- I will shoe you with {\i tachash}. The {\i tachash} is understood to be an animal, and therefore the verse is read as establishing that the material of footwear must be of animal origin \u8212- leather. This is consistent with broader discussions of {\i chalitzah} footwear that emphasize leather as the primary material, and it connects to the related {\i halacha} of Yom Kippur and the prohibition of leather shoes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes further. {\i Teima tachash, in midi achrini lo} \u8212- perhaps only {\i tachash} skin may be used, to the exclusion of all other materials? The {\i gemara} rejects this: the verse also uses the word {\i na\u8217'al} ({\i na\u8217'al na\u8217'al ribba}), and the repetition implies a broader category. Yet the mention of {\i tachash} is not superfluous: it limits the category to leather, excluding plant-derived materials like flax entirely. {\i Im ken tachash mai ahani} \u8212- if all materials were permitted, the {\i tachash} reference would contribute nothing. The {\i tachash} therefore draws the line: the shoe must be leather.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Straps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'ei minei Rav Elazar meRav}: what about a shoe whose body is leather but whose straps ({\i trisiotav}) are made of hair? Rav responds: {\i u-mi lo karina beih ve-analach tachash} \u8212- does not such a shoe still fall within the description of the {\i tachash} verse? Hair is, after all, a product of the animal kingdom, consistent with the category the verse establishes. Rav therefore permits hair straps on a leather shoe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if hair straps are permitted, why not make the entire shoe of hair? The answer: a shoe made entirely of hair is called {\i karka} \u8212- which Rashi and the translation both render as a slipper \u8212- something so minimally protective and structurally insubstantial that it does not qualify as a proper shoe for {\i chalitzah} purposes. The principle that emerges is that the {\i ikar}, the primary substance of the shoe, must be leather; secondary components, such as straps, may include other animal-derived materials. What precisely falls within the permissible range of non-leather animal materials \u8212- wool, goat hair, or otherwise \u8212- is not spelled out with full precision, but the basic framework is clear: leather for the body of the shoe, and for the straps, something from the animal kingdom may suffice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Etymology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remove Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Kahana leShmuel}: how do we know that the word {\i chalatz} in the verse {\i ve-chaltzah na\u8217'alo me-al raglo} means to remove, rather than to strengthen or arm? After all, {\i chalatz} appears elsewhere with the latter sense: {\i hechalitzu me-it-chem anashim la-tzava} \u8212- arm yourselves, men for battle. Perhaps the verse in {\i chalitzah} means something like: help him put on his shoe, prepare him for the road?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana brings proof from the verse in Leviticus regarding {\i tzara\u8217'at} of a house: {\i ve-chiltzu et ha-avanim asher ba-hen ha-naga} \u8212- they shall remove the stones in which the affliction appears. There, {\i chalatz} unambiguously means to remove.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Shmuel pushes back: even in the military context, perhaps {\i chalatz} means to remove \u8212- specifically to remove men from their homes and send them out to war: {\i shalufi mi-beisa le-karba}. The word may be rooted in the concept of extraction, of drawing out, in both senses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} examines further verses. {\i Yachallaitz ani be-onyo} \u8212- will God strengthen the poor in his poverty? The {\i gemara} reads this as: because of his suffering, he will be removed from the judgment of {\i Gehennom} \u8212- {\i yechaltzu me-dina shel Gehennom}. Similarly, {\i choneh mal\u8217'ach Hashem saviv li-reyav vi-yachaltzem} \u8212- the angel of God encamps around those who fear Him and arms them \u8212- is read: because of their fear of God, they are removed from the judgment of {\i Gehennom}. And {\i ve-atzmotecha yachalitz} \u8212- God will strengthen your bones \u8212- which Rav Elazar calls the greatest of all blessings, {\i me\u8217'ula sheba-berachot} \u8212- is explained by Rava as {\i ziruzei garmei}, the invigorating of the bones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concedes: {\i mashma hacha u-mashma hacha} \u8212- the word {\i chalatz} carries both meanings, removal and strengthening, and context determines which applies. But in the {\i chalitzah} verse, the {\i gemara} demonstrates that removal must be the intended meaning through a {\i derashah} on the preposition {\i me-al}. If {\i chalatz} meant to strengthen or to put on, the Torah would have written {\i be-raglo} \u8212- on his foot. Had it written {\i be-raglo}, one might have thought that only the foot proper is included and not the calf. So the Torah wrote {\i me-al raglo} \u8212- from upon his foot. But {\i me-al} already implies removal; if the meaning were to put on, the verse would have said {\i b\u8217'me-al raglo} \u8212- on the part above his foot. Since {\i me-al raglo} is used, {\i shema mina mishlaf hu} \u8212- it must mean to remove.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heretic\u8217's Polemic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with a remarkable exchange. A {\i min} \u8212- a heretic, in this context typically understood as a Jewish Christian \u8212- approached Rabban Gamliel with a provocative claim: {\i ama de-chalatz marei minei} \u8212- you are a people whose Master has done {\i chalitzah} to you, has abandoned you. The proof text: {\i be-tzonam u-ve-vekaram yelchu le-vakesh et Hashem ve-lo yimtza\u8217'u, chalatz me-hem} \u8212- they will go with their flocks and herds to seek God and will not find Him; He has removed Himself from them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel responded sharply: {\i shoteh} \u8212- fool. {\i Mi ksiv chalatz la-hem} \u8212- it does not say He performed {\i chalitzah to} them. {\i Chalatz me-hem ksiv} \u8212- it says He removed Himself {\i from} them. And who performs {\i chalitzah} in the ceremony? The woman removes the shoe of the man. If God is cast as the man in this scenario, then for God to have done {\i chalitzah} \u8212- which is the woman\u8217's act \u8212- would be meaningless. {\i Ve-ilu yevamah de-chalatz la achi midei mashasha it bah} \u8212- and if a brother were to do the {\i chalitzah} to the {\i yevamah}, that would accomplish nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The force of the response operates on two levels. Formally, the {\i min}\u8217's grammatical reading is challenged: the verse says {\i chalatz me-hem}, not {\i chalatz la-hem}. But the deeper point is about who acts and who receives. The polemic sought to cast God as having turned away from the Jewish people. The response reframes the relationship: if anything, the verse describes {\i Bnei Yisrael} as having turned away from God \u8212- they sought and did not find because the withdrawal came from their side. The one who performs {\i chalitzah} is the one who chooses to walk away. And a {\i chalitzah} performed by the wrong party \u8212- by the man rather than the woman \u8212- carries no legal force whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 103\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitza & Leather\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slipper Invalidity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of the bottom of 102b, approximately fifteen lines from the end, at a new paragraph. The {\i Mishnah} establishes: {\i ba\u8217'anpalya chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- if the {\i yevamah} performed {\i chalitza} using a slipper, the {\i chalitza} is invalid. It is worth recalling the earlier ruling of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav, which required the shoe used for {\i chalitza} to be made of leather, derived from a {\i pasuk}. That requirement, however, is by no means straightforwardly established from the {\i tannaitic} material. The {\i Gemara} will work to reconcile everything into a consistent requirement of leather, but as the discussion unfolds it becomes clear that multiple sources complicate, rather than confirm, that position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slipper Not a Shoe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's ruling, the {\i Gemara} infers: {\i danpalya lav min\u8217'al hu} \u8212- a slipper is not legally classified as a shoe. This inference sets up the broader discussion of what, precisely, constitutes a {\i min\u8217'al} for purposes of {\i chalitza} and related laws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shekalim Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} supports this inference by citing a parallel from {\i Shekalim}. When a person brings his {\i trumat halishka} \u8212- the annual contribution to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- and goes in person to deposit it in the {\i pushka}, the {\i Mishnah} in {\i Shekalim} specifies: {\i ein hatorim nichnas} \u8212- the one who separates the funds may not enter {\i Har HaBayit} or the {\i Beit HaMikdash} with a cloak that has a hem ({\i lo bepargout chafut}), out of concern that he might conceal money in the hem, thereby appearing suspect ({\i nishchad}). Equally, he may not enter {\i v\u8217'lo b\u8217'anpalya} \u8212- with slippers \u8212- presumably, as Rashi explains, because slippers similarly create the appearance that money might be concealed within them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temple Shoe Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} in {\i Shekalim} then adds: {\i v\u8217'ein tzarich lomar bemin\u8217'al vesandal} \u8212- and needless to say, not with a shoe or sandal. The reason here is entirely different: not the suspicion of hiding money, but the prohibition against entering the {\i azarah} in an ordinary shoe. This derives from the verse {\i shal ne\u8217'alecha me\u8217'al raglecha} \u8212- remove your sandal from upon your foot \u8212- and from the {\i pasuk}, {\i lamma zeh lachem remos chatzerai} \u8212- why do you trample My courtyards. Entering the Temple precinct while wearing regular shoes is considered disrespectful, a different category of concern than the slipper issue. Thus the {\i Mishnah} in {\i Shekalim} places {\i anpalya} in a legally distinct category from {\i min\u8217'al vesandal}: the slipper is addressed separately and for a separate reason, reflecting its distinct {\i halachic} status. The {\i Gemara} then raises a contradiction ({\i reminihu}): another {\i braita} groups {\i min\u8217'al}, {\i sandal}, and {\i anpalya} together in the prohibition of walking in them on {\i Yom Kippur} \u8212- {\i lo yitayel adam bahen lo mibayit lebayit velo mimita lemita}. If the slipper is in a separate legal category from shoes, why does this {\i braita} lump them together?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yom Kippur Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 YK Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} states that one may not walk about \u8212- not from house to house and not from couch to couch \u8212- whether wearing a shoe, sandal, or slipper. This is the {\i Yom Kippur} context. The difficulty is immediate: if a slipper is not a shoe, why should it be forbidden on {\i Yom Kippur}, where the prohibition is specifically against wearing shoes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Pleasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers an initial resolution: the {\i braita} is speaking of a slipper that contains {\i ketiti} \u8212- small stuffed pieces of cloth \u8212- giving it a pleasant, cushioned feel. The prohibition in this case, he suggests, is {\i mishum ta\u8217'anug} \u8212- because of the pleasure it provides, which is inappropriate on {\i Yom Kippur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava immediately dismisses this reasoning. Since when, he asks, is pleasure that does not fall within one of the five formally prohibited categories of {\i Yom Kippur} a {\i halachic} problem? The {\i Yom Kippur} prohibition concerns wearing shoes, not deriving comfort for one\u8217's feet in general. To illustrate, Rava cites the well-known example: {\i v\u8217'ha Rabbah bar bar Chana karchis sudra akarei} \u8212- Rabbah bar bar Chana would wrap a cloth around his leg and go out on {\i Yom Kippur}. This cloth served the function of a shoe and provided comfort, yet it was entirely permissible, because halachically it was not a shoe. The point is clear: if it is not a shoe, it is not a shoe, regardless of the pleasure involved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Chana Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of Rabbah bar bar Chana\u8217's cloth wrapping serves as a compelling proof that the formal category matters, not the experiential comfort. Functional similarity to a shoe does not make something a shoe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore resolves the apparent contradiction differently: {\i kan be\u8217'anpileya shel or, kan be\u8217'anpileya shel beged} \u8212- here we are speaking of a leather slipper, there of a cloth slipper. The {\i braita} that forbids a slipper on {\i Yom Kippur} is referring to a leather slipper, which does count as a shoe; the {\i Mishnah} that renders {\i chalitza} with a slipper invalid is referring to a non-leather slipper, which is not a shoe at all. This solution reintroduces the leather criterion \u8212- not only as a {\i chalitza}-specific requirement derived from a {\i pasuk}, but as a general definitional principle in {\i halachah}: leather is what makes a slipper cross the threshold into being legally classified as a shoe.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Leather: Form vs.\u160 ?Functi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Shoes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction carries direct practical implications. The question of whether one may wear a well-made, highly supportive non-leather shoe on {\i Yom Kippur} turns on precisely this issue. Is the requirement of leather a purely formal, definitional {\i halachah} \u8212- in which case even the most supportive canvas sneaker would be permitted \u8212- or does it reflect a functional criterion, leather being the material that most effectively protects and supports the foot? If it is purely formal, non-leather shoes of any quality remain permitted on {\i Yom Kippur} and the question is closed. If it is functional, the analysis may shift as materials and norms evolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} keeps returning to leather as though it is the dispositive criterion, which can certainly give the impression of a formal requirement. On the other hand, one might argue the other way: that the distinction between leather and non-leather maps onto the distinction between what does and does not provide meaningful protection, and that the {\i halachah} tracks the function rather than the material itself. The lecturer notes his personal practice not to wear even comfortable cloth slippers on {\i Yom Kippur} or {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}, wearing only thin socks with minimal rubber traction, because experientially such an arrangement does not feel like it honors the spirit of the prohibition. Others hold that non-leather is simply not a shoe \u8212- pure formalism \u8212- and have no hesitation. Still others might argue that even Rava\u8217's formal leather distinction is less relevant in a time when non-leather shoes can be as protective and shoe-like as leather ones, though this approach would not apply to {\i chalitza}, where the {\i pasuk} anchors the requirement more firmly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: No Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's commentary on the {\i Shekalim} case illuminates his position. In the margin between Rashi and the {\i Gemara}, Rashi explains: {\i matnitin de\u8217'terumat halishka be\u8217'anpileya shel beged} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} concerning the {\i trumat halishka} is speaking of a cloth slipper. {\i Delav magen hu} \u8212- because it is not protective. {\i Delo havei min\u8217'al} \u8212- it is not classified as a shoe. And: {\i u\u8217'legabei azara nami leka bizayon} \u8212- nor does it constitute a disrespect relative to the {\i azarah}. He adds: {\i ve\u8217'ein derech lehachnisah betit} \u8212- and it is not the normal way to enter with mud underneath, which seems to refer to the lack of a ridged sole that would trap dirt. The crucial phrase is Rashi\u8217's {\i delav magen hu}: the slipper fails the definitional threshold not because it is categorically non-leather, but because it does not protect. By contrast, Rashi explains of the {\i Yom Kippur} case: {\i dehai d\u8217'kippurim beshel or} \u8212- that prohibition concerns a leather slipper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Form or Function?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges is a significant question: by {\i chalitza}, where a {\i pasuk} mandates the use of a shoe, the leather requirement appears to be an absolute formal criterion. But when the same distinction between leather and non-leather governs the laws of {\i Yom Kippur} and the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, is that too a formal definition \u8212- or is it grounded in the functional capacity of leather to protect and cover the foot, as Rashi\u8217's language of {\i magen} suggests? Rashi does not say that a non-leather slipper is definitionally not a shoe; he says it does not protect, and therefore is not a shoe. This functional reading has real consequences: if leather\u8217's relevance is protective function, then materials that provide equivalent protection might carry equivalent {\i halachic} weight, at least in non-{\i chalitza} contexts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita: Valid Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torn & Broken\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a {\i braita} enumerating cases of {\i chalitza} that are valid, at minimum {\i b\u8217'dieved}. The list begins: {\i chalitza b\u8217'min\u8217'al hanifram shechofeh rov haregel} \u8212- if she performed {\i chalitza} with a torn shoe, provided it covers most of the foot. Similarly, {\i basandal hanifchat she\u8217'mekabbel rov haregel} \u8212- a broken sandal that still supports most of the foot is valid. The structural integrity required is that the object cover or support the majority of the foot; so long as that threshold is met, damage does not invalidate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cork & Fiber\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'sandal shel sha\u8217'am v\u8217'shel siv} \u8212- a sandal made of cork ({\i sha\u8217'am}) or of fiber (rush or bass, {\i siv}). These are self-evidently non-leather materials. This is significant: the {\i braita} is validating {\i chalitza} performed with sandals that are not made of leather. This stands in apparent tension with Rav Yehuda\u8217's statement in the name of Rav that leather is required. The {\i Gemara} will have to reckon with this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prosthetic Shoe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then introduces the case of {\i v\u8217'kav hakite\u8217'ah} \u8212- the prosthetic used by an amputee. This is the wooden peg-leg used by someone whose leg has been severed. According to Rashi\u8217's reading of the {\i Mishnah}, if the amputation is below the knee, the prosthetic functions as the person\u8217's shoe and its removal constitutes valid {\i chalitza}; if the amputation is above the knee, it does not. In Rashi\u8217's framework, the reasoning is that when the leg is amputated below the knee, the remaining stump of the {\i shok} \u8212- the calf \u8212- is considered the foot for {\i halachic} purposes, and the prosthetic that covers and protects it is classified as a shoe upon that foot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} also mentions {\i b\u8217'mook} \u8212- using a felt covering. Rashi explains that {\i mook} denotes a hard felt, which does provide protection. So here we have another non-leather material being validated, this time even in the context of the prosthetic. This reinforces the growing picture that the list of valid {\i chalitza} objects includes numerous non-leather items.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then mentions {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'smichat haraglayim} \u8212- the supports for the feet. Rashi describes this as the situation of a person whose legs are not functional and who moves by dragging himself along the ground, propelling with his arms, with a protective mat or covering on his feet to prevent them from being scraped. This object, worn on the dragging legs, is also included in the list of valid {\i chalitza} objects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the list includes {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'anpilaya shel or} \u8212- a leather slipper. This confirms that for the slipper specifically, leather is the operative distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalid Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then turns to invalid cases. A torn shoe that does not cover most of the foot ({\i she\u8217'ein chofeh rov haregel}) is invalid. A broken sandal that does not support most of the foot is invalid. {\i U\u8217'vsmichat hayadayim} \u8212- the protective covering for the {\i hands} of the same person who drags himself \u8212- even though his hands are functionally his means of locomotion, we do not classify hand-coverings as shoes. {\i U\u8217'v\u8217'anpilya shel beged} \u8212- a cloth slipper is invalid. {\i V\u8217'choletzet min hakatan} \u8212- {\i chalitza} performed with a minor is invalid. {\i Chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- all of these are invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What this {\i braita} makes vividly clear is that the requirement of leather, while operative for the slipper, does not apply universally. Cork, fiber, felt, and wood all appear in the valid column without any qualification. This substantially complicates any claim that leather is a formal universal requirement for {\i chalitza} footwear. It appears that leather matters specifically \u8212- and perhaps only \u8212- in the case of the {\i anpilya}, where without leather the object functions more like a sock than a shoe. For genuinely shoe-like objects \u8212- sandals of cork or fiber, a prosthetic, a protective mat for dragging legs \u8212- leather is not demanded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question naturally arises regarding straps: the {\i Gemara} earlier mentioned undoing the straps as part of the {\i chalitza} act, and the {\i braita} there stated that if he undoes the straps and she removes the shoe, it is invalid \u8212- she must do both. But this should be read as: if the shoe happens to have straps, she must perform every component of the removal. It does not appear that straps are a mandatory feature of the {\i chalitza} shoe. The many cases in this {\i braita} that clearly involve no straps \u8212- a broken sandal, a prosthetic, a felt covering \u8212- bear this out. The {\i Gemara} never explicitly states that straps are required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Leather Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: All Leather\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having identified that the {\i braita}\u8217's valid cases include non-leather materials alongside the leather requirement for the slipper, the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i kav kita mani?} \u8212- who is the {\i tanna} behind the prosthetic ruling? The answer is Rabbi Meir, based on the {\i Mishnah} in {\i Shabbat}: {\i hakita yotzei b\u8217'kav shelo divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- an amputee may go out on {\i Shabbat} with his prosthetic, for Rabbi Meir classifies it as a shoe. Rabbi Yossi forbids it, considering the prosthetic an external object rather than a shoe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus the ruling that a prosthetic validates {\i chalitza} reflects Rabbi Meir\u8217's position \u8212- and implicitly, Rabbi Meir\u8217's position that leather is not required. The end of the {\i braita}, however, distinguishes between leather and non-leather slippers, which sounds like the position of the {\i Rabbanan}. How can the beginning of the {\i braita} follow Rabbi Meir (no leather requirement) while the end follows the {\i Rabbanan} (leather required)?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves this by insisting that the {\i braita} follows the {\i Rabbanan} throughout: {\i mide-seifa rabbanan, reisha nami rabbanan}. Since the end requires leather for the slipper, the beginning must also be read as requiring leather throughout. Accordingly, the prosthetic case must involve a prosthetic covered in leather \u8212- {\i v\u8217'reisha b\u8217'mechuppeh or}. The {\i braita} is not validating a plain wooden prosthetic; it is validating one that has been covered with a leather surface.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Functional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this reading. If we are going to require a leather-covered prosthetic, the {\i braita} should have said so explicitly \u8212- it should have distinguished between a leather-covered prosthetic and a non-leather one, just as it distinguished between a leather slipper and a cloth slipper. The fact that the {\i braita} draws no such distinction for the prosthetic undermines Abaye\u8217's interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore inverts the approach: {\i mide-reisha Rabbi Meir, seifa nami Rabbi Meir} \u8212- both the beginning and the end of the {\i braita} follow Rabbi Meir. And Rabbi Meir does not require leather as a formal criterion. How, then, does Rava explain the distinction between a leather slipper (valid) and a cloth slipper (invalid)? The answer is functional: {\i hai magein ve-hai lo magein} \u8212- one protects and one does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Protective Function\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's position, which Rashi\u8217's language in the {\i Shekalim} context foreshadowed, is that leather is never a formal, definitional requirement in its own right. It is a proxy for protection. When a non-leather material is genuinely protective \u8212- a cork sandal, a fiber sandal, a felt prosthetic covering, a wooden peg-leg \u8212- it qualifies as a shoe and validates {\i chalitza}. When a non-leather material is not protective \u8212- a cloth slipper, which functions more like a sock \u8212- it does not qualify. The cloth slipper requires leather precisely because without leather it fails the protective function entirely; with leather, even the slipper becomes sufficiently shoe-like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has significant {\i halachic} implications. If Rava\u8217's functional reading is correct, the leather requirement for {\i Yom Kippur} is likewise not a formal rule but a reflection of leather\u8217's historically superior protective and shoe-defining qualities. A non-leather shoe that genuinely protects and supports the foot might, on this reading, be considered a shoe for {\i Yom Kippur} purposes \u8212- though for {\i chalitza}, the {\i pasuk} may impose a more fixed formal standard regardless.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitza Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Planted Feet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar introduces a further requirement: {\i hai man de-chalitz tzarich le-midchasei le-char\u8217'ei} \u8212- the man performing {\i chalitza} must push his foot firmly to the ground. The reasoning, while not fully explicit, seems to be that the act of {\i chalitza} involves a transition \u8212- from foot firmly planted to foot unplanted as the shoe is removed. The moment of removal is defined against the prior state of the foot being fully planted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Position Flexibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi challenges Ameimar from the {\i braita}: {\i bein omed bein yoshev bein mutteh} \u8212- whether standing, sitting, or leaning, the {\i chalitza} is valid. If firm planting were essential, how could a reclining position qualify? Ameimar\u8217's response, as the {\i Gemara} understands it, is that these positions are not in conflict: whether sitting, standing, or leaning, the requirement is that the foot be firmly planted on the ground. Even someone reclining \u8212- much like sitting in a recliner \u8212- must have his feet pressing down on the ground. The position of the body is flexible; the grounding of the foot is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foot Disability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar adds a second ruling: {\i hai man de-masgei al lachusta de-char\u8217'ei lo chalitz} \u8212- a person who walks on the front of his foot \u8212- whose foot is twisted such that the upper part of the foot drags along the ground rather than the sole \u8212- cannot perform {\i chalitza}. Even if such a person is wearing a shoe, it is not functioning as a shoe for him: the sole of the shoe is not what protects his foot in contact with the ground; rather, it is the upper portion of the shoe. Since the shoe does not functionally serve the purpose of a shoe for this individual, its removal does not constitute valid {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to a famous incident in {\i Moed Katan}: two eulogizers who said something Rav Ashi found inappropriate received the evil eye from him, rendering them lame. Rav Ashi applies Ameimar\u8217's ruling to say that these individuals \u8212- {\i lav bar chalitz ve-lav bar kifaf chalitz} \u8212- cannot perform {\i chalitza} because their lameness prevents them from wearing shoes in a normal, functional way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prosthetic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Support Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises a question against Ameimar\u8217's ruling about the person who walks on the front of his foot. The {\i braita} listed {\i smichat haraglayim} \u8212- the supports for the feet of the person who drags himself \u8212- as a valid {\i chalitza} object. In that case, the person\u8217's feet are also not contacting the ground through their soles; they are dragging. Is that not precisely the same functional situation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} clarifies: no. The {\i braita} is not saying that this person himself uses those foot-supports for {\i chalitza}. Rather, {\i de-yahvi lei le-achrini ve-chalatz} \u8212- someone else wears those supports. The supports are valid {\i chalitza} objects, but only when used by a person whose feet function normally. The person who actually drags himself cannot perform {\i chalitza} with those supports, because for him they are not functioning as shoes on his feet. For a person with normal feet who wears the same object, it counts as a shoe and {\i chalitza} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Shok\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi addresses an apparent difficulty: if the person who walks on the front of his foot cannot perform {\i chalitza} because the shoe is not protecting the sole of his foot, why does the amputee below the knee fare differently? After all, the prosthetic is not covering the sole of a foot either \u8212- the foot is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains by invoking the principle that emerges from the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's ruling: {\i uve\u8217'af al gav de-tenan min ha-arkuvah u-le-mata kesherah} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} validates {\i chalitza} from below the knee. Rashi\u8217's reading: {\i chi nachis raglo kamei lei shok} \u8212- when the leg descends, what is there is the {\i shok}, the calf. {\i De-rachmana amar me-al raglo de-hainu shoko} \u8212- the Torah says {\i me-al raglo}, \u8220"above his foot,\u8221" meaning above what now serves as his foot, which is the {\i shok}. The prosthetic that covers and protects the base of the {\i shok} is therefore protecting what is halachically considered the foot. This is fundamentally different from the person who walks on the front of his foot: that person\u8217's actual foot exists and is present, but the shoe does not protect its sole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot takes a different approach entirely. Rather than distinguishing between the amputee and the front-foot walker on the grounds of what counts as the foot, Tosafot reads the prosthetic case in the {\i braita} identically to the dragging-foot case: in both instances, the {\i braita} is describing the object being used by someone other than the person who actually has the condition. The prosthetic, on this reading, is valid {\i chalitza} only when used by a person who does not have an amputated leg \u8212- perhaps by bending the leg underneath somehow. The amputee himself, just as the person who drags himself, cannot perform {\i chalitza} using his prosthetic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking position and creates significant practical consequences. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, an amputee below the knee cannot perform {\i chalitza} with his own prosthetic, just as someone who walks on the front of his foot cannot perform {\i chalitza}. Tosafot supports this by explicitly citing the {\i Gemara}\u8217's ruling about {\i smichat haraglayim} as the model: {\i yahvi le-achrini ve-chalatz, ke-de-amar somchos ha-reglayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Above Knee\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both Rashi and Tosafot agree that if the amputation is above the knee, {\i chalitza} is impossible. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's distinction \u8212- {\i min ha-arkuvah u-le-mata kesherah} \u8212- limits validity to below-the-knee amputation. Beyond that, the only debate between the {\i rishonim} is about below-the-knee amputation: can the amputee himself use his prosthetic ({\i Rashi}), or only a non-amputee wearing that same prosthetic ({\i Tosafot})?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a profound and sobering question. Suppose a man has no brothers other than one who is severely lame \u8212- unable to wear shoes normally \u8212- or whose leg is amputated above the knee. The {\i yevamah} cannot obtain {\i chalitza}. Everyone wants to do the right thing, everything is known, and yet there is no halachic solution. This is categorically different from the {\i agunah} cases where the problem is evidentiary uncertainty. Here, the facts are clear, all parties are willing, and still {\i chalitza} may be legally unavailable. What the {\i poskim} say about such a case would require separate investigation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Regel: Foot or Leg\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regel Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now addresses a foundational linguistic question that underlies all the above: does {\i regel} in the relevant {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'chaltzah na\u8217'alo me\u8217'al raglo} \u8212- mean {\i foot} specifically, or does it mean {\i leg}? This matters enormously. If {\i regel} means leg, then amputation above the knee should still leave enough of the leg to satisfy {\i me-al raglo}, and {\i chalitza} should remain possible. If {\i regel} means only foot, then we must work our way up from the foot and the knee joint becomes the outer limit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana raises a challenge: {\i beshelia yetzei mibein ragleha} \u8212- a miscarried fetus that comes out from between her {\i reglayim}. The {\i regel} in question here clearly refers to the thigh region, not the foot. This suggests {\i regel} can mean leg \u8212- even the upper leg.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Euphemistic Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds to Rav Kahana\u8217's challenge: {\i bisha\u8217'a she\u8217'kar\u8217'a lileid, no\u8217'etzet ekeveha beyarcheha} \u8212- when a woman crouches to give birth, she digs her heels into her sides. At that moment, what comes out literally comes out from between her feet. Abaye is reading {\i ragleha} as feet, not thighs, because of the crouching posture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then marshals further proofs. {\i Lo asa raglav velo asa sefamo} \u8212- he did not do his {\i raglav} and did not trim his mustache. The {\i Gemara} understands this as referring to pubic grooming, where {\i raglav} is used as a euphemism for the private area. Similarly, {\i vayavo Shaul lehasech et raglav} \u8212- Saul went to relieve himself \u8212- where {\i raglav} again euphemistically refers to the area between the thighs, not to the feet themselves. And {\i ach masi\u8217'ach hu et raglav} \u8212- he is relieving himself in an inner chamber.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In each of these cases the {\i Gemara} gives the same answer: {\i lishna me\u8217'alya} \u8212- refined language. The word {\i regel} is being used euphemistically to avoid direct mention of the thighs or private areas. The underlying denotation of {\i regel} remains {\i foot}; it is only deployed as indirect speech when referring to intimate areas. The fact that it is pressed into service as a euphemism does not mean it actually denotes the entire leg.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foot Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After working through all these cases \u8212- including {\i bein ragleha kara shachav} from the episode of Yael and Sisera, which the {\i Gemara} also answers with {\i lishna me\u8217'alya} \u8212- the conclusion is firm: {\i regel} means foot. The {\i Gemara} consistently deflects every apparent counterexample as euphemistic usage rather than evidence of a broader denotation. The consequence is maintained: {\i chalitza} requires the shoe to be removed from what is legally the foot, and amputation above the knee removes any possibility of meeting that requirement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sisera & Yael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Times\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having cited the {\i pasuk} from Yael and Sisera in the linguistic discussion, the {\i Gemara} pivots to an extended {\i aggadic} passage. Rabbi Yochanan states: {\i sheva be\u8217'ilot ba\u8217'al oto rasha be\u8217'oto hayom} \u8212- that wicked man had relations with Yael seven times that day. The source is a close reading of the {\i pesukim} in {\i Shoftim}: {\i bein ragleha kara nafal shachav, bein ragleha kara nafal, ba\u8217'asher kara sham nafal shadud}. Counting the verbs \u8212- {\i kara, nafal, shachav, kara, nafal, kara, nafal} \u8212- yields seven acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pleasure Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises a serious theological and moral difficulty: {\i v\u8217'ha kamistanya mei\u8217'aveira?} \u8212- but was Yael not deriving pleasure from a transgression? This question would seem to undermine the moral heroism for which she is celebrated. If she experienced physical pleasure in the course of her sin, even if the sin was committed for a righteous purpose, how does the tradition valorize her act? The concern is not merely whether she {\i could} do it \u8212- the concept of {\i aveirah lishmah} addresses that \u8212- but whether an act from which one derives pleasure can be celebrated as self-sacrifice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zuhama Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan resolves the question in the name of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: {\i kol tovasan shel resha\u8217'im ra\u8217'ah hi etzel tzaddikim} \u8212- any good that the wicked have to offer is, from the perspective of the righteous, only evil. The verse cited is God\u8217's warning to Lavan: {\i hishamer lecha pen tedaber im Yaakov mitov ad ra\u8217'} \u8212- take care not to speak to Yaakov from good to bad. The {\i Gemara} understands: not speaking bad to Yaakov is obvious; why also not speak good? This demonstrates that whatever good a {\i rasha} would offer is experienced by the righteous only as harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} does not accept this as a bare metaphysical principle and probes further: in Lavan\u8217's case, the explanation is clear \u8212- any blessing he would pronounce would invoke the name of his god ({\i Elohei Nachor}, etc.), which Yaakov would not want. But what is the harm in Sisera\u8217's case, where the concern seems to be purely physical pleasure? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i de-ka shadei bah zuhama} \u8212- because Sisera cast into her a {\i zuhama}, a spiritual stench or contamination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a broader theological statement from Rabbi Yochanan: {\i besha\u8217'ah sheba nachash al Chava hitil bah zuhama} \u8212- when the snake had relations with Chava, he instilled in her a {\i zuhama}. {\i Yisrael she\u8217'amdu al Har Sinai, paska zuhamatan} \u8212- when Israel stood at Sinai, their {\i zuhama} ceased. {\i Ovdei kochavim shelo amdu al Har Sinai, lo paska zuhamatan} \u8212- the idolaters, who did not stand at Sinai, retain their {\i zuhama} to this day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implied polemical context is worth noting. This tradition appears to be a Jewish response to the Pauline Christian doctrine of original sin, reversing its terms entirely: the Jews are not burdened with original sin \u8212- they were cleansed of it at Sinai. It is the nations who never received the Torah who retain the primordial contamination of the snake\u8217's encounter with Chava. Far from sex being intrinsically sinful, as early Christian theology would have it, the Jewish people were specifically liberated from the condition that makes it so. Hence, when Sisera \u8212- a non-Jew carrying the full {\i zuhama} of the unredeemed nations \u8212- had relations with Yael, whatever physical sensation she experienced was accompanied by a deep spiritual contamination that entirely negated any pleasure. The good that a {\i rasha} offers the righteous is not merely neutral; it actively does harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's engagement with this {\i aggadah} focuses on the {\i aveirah lishmah} dimension. Even granting that what Yael did was halachically justified as a great act done for the sake of {\i Klal Yisrael}, the {\i pesukim} celebrate her with a heroism that implies self-sacrifice. If she were experiencing genuine pleasure throughout, the celebration would be misplaced \u8212- there is no sacrifice in an act one enjoys. The answer, then, is not to deny the {\i aveirah lishmah} justification but to explain why it was also a genuine sacrifice: the {\i zuhama} ensured that what appeared to be pleasure was in fact causing her harm.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shoe Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fit Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to the question of whether {\i chalitza} may be performed using a shoe that belongs to someone else. The {\i braita} derives from the word {\i na\u8217'alo} \u8212- his shoe \u8212- that the shoe must be {\i ra\u8217'ui lo}, appropriate for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Borrowed Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation proceeds through {\i gezeirah shavah} or {\i ribbui}: the word {\i na\u8217'al} appears multiple times in the {\i pasuk}, and from the repetition the {\i braita} learns {\i na\u8217'al na\u8217'al mikol makom} \u8212- the word {\i shoe} in any context, meaning even a borrowed shoe is valid. The term {\i na\u8217'alo} thus does not require ownership but suitability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoe Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From {\i na\u8217'alo hara\u8217'ui lo} \u8212- a shoe appropriate for him \u8212- the {\i braita} derives several exclusions. A shoe too large, such that he cannot walk in it normally ({\i perat legadol she\u8217'eino yachol lahaloch bah}), is invalid. A shoe too small, such that it does not cover most of his foot ({\i perat lekatan she\u8217'eino chofeh rov raglo}), is invalid. And {\i u\u8217'perat lesandal hamasulayin she\u8217'ein lo akeiv} \u8212- a sandal of the type that has no heel is excluded. This last exclusion shifts from a personal fitness requirement to an objective structural standard: regardless of fit, a heelless sandal does not qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} relates an incident involving Abaye and Rav Yosef. Abaye was standing before Rav Yosef when a {\i yevamah} came to perform {\i chalitza}. Rav Yosef instructed Abaye: {\i hav lei sandalach} \u8212- give the {\i yavam} your sandal. Perhaps the {\i yavam} had only a non-leather slipper or otherwise unsuitable footwear. Abaye, apparently wishing to make a point, deliberately gave the {\i yavam} his left sandal \u8212- knowing that the right sandal is the {\i lechatchila} requirement. Rav Yosef rebuked him: when did the rabbis say a left sandal is acceptable? Only {\i b\u8217'dieved}. They never said {\i lechatchila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responded with equal sharpness: and when did you tell me to give him my sandal? When did the rabbis say a borrowed sandal is acceptable? Only {\i b\u8217'dieved}. So why are you asking me to give him a borrowed sandal {\i lechatchila} in the first place? My use of the left sandal is no more problematic than your instruction to lend him mine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transfer Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef clarified his original intent: {\i hav lei v\u8217'aknei lei} \u8212- give it to him and transfer possession to him. The instruction was not to lend the sandal but to make the {\i yavam} the owner of it for the duration of the {\i chalitza}, after which it would be returned. Since the sandal would be legally his at the moment of {\i chalitza}, the concern of borrowed footwear is entirely resolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wood & Disease\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wooden Sandals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks about wooden sandals: {\i sandal shel etz, man tanna?} \u8212- who is the {\i tanna} that holds a wooden sandal is valid for {\i chalitza}? The question circles back to the leather issue. If wood is valid, that is in tension with any formal leather requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel answers: it is Rabbi Meir, based on the same {\i Mishnah} \u8212- {\i hakiteia yotzei b\u8217'kav shelo divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- where Rabbi Meir classifies a wooden prosthetic as a shoe for purposes of {\i Shabbat}. Attributing the wooden sandal ruling to Rabbi Meir places this leniency in the same camp: Rabbi Meir does not require leather. Rabbi Yossi disagrees and forbids the prosthetic on {\i Shabbat} \u8212- for him, it is not a shoe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Covered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel then offers an alternative reading: {\i b\u8217'mechupah or divrei hakol} \u8212- the wooden sandal case is universally valid, because it is speaking of a wooden sandal covered in leather. On this reading, the permissibility of the wooden sandal is not evidence against a leather requirement; rather, the leather covering satisfies the requirement. This mirrors Abaye\u8217's earlier attempt to read leather covering into the prosthetic case, which Rava rejected. Here, though, it is Shmuel advancing the position, and the {\i Gemara} does not immediately challenge it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzara\u8217'at Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presents the rulings of Rav Papa in the name of Rava regarding sandals afflicted with {\i nega tzara\u8217'at} \u8212- signs of ritual leprosy that appear in leather objects. The case of {\i sandal hamusgar} is a sandal that has been sealed ({\i musgar}) \u8212- placed in quarantine pending inspection after the first week, not yet definitively determined to be {\i tamei}. The case of {\i sandal hamuchlat} is a sandal definitively ruled to be {\i metzora} and therefore subject to destruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to one version of Rav Papa\u8217's ruling in the name of Rava: a {\i musgar} sandal \u8212- {\i lo tachlotz bo} \u8212- should not be used for {\i chalitza lechatchila}, but {\i v\u8217'im chaltza chalitzatah kesherah} \u8212- if she did, the {\i chalitza} is valid {\i b\u8217'dieved}. A {\i muchlat} sandal, however \u8212- {\i lo tachlotz bo, v\u8217'im chaltza chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- must not be used, and if she did, the {\i chalitza} is invalid even {\i b\u8217'dieved}. The reason for this stricter position is the principle of {\i kitutei michtat shiurei} \u8212- an object that is going to be destroyed is considered as if already destroyed, or at minimum has its legally relevant thresholds undermined. A sandal that has been definitively ruled {\i metzora} and is headed for destruction cannot be considered a shoe in the full legal sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lenient View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second version of the ruling, attributed again to Rav Papa in the name of Rava, takes a more lenient position: {\i echad sandal hamusgar v\u8217'echad sandal hamuchlat} \u8212- both the sealed and the definitively afflicted sandal \u8212- {\i lo tachlotz bo lechatchila} \u8212- should not be used {\i lechatchila}; {\i v\u8217'im chaltza chalitzatah kesherah} \u8212- but if she did, the {\i chalitza} is valid in both cases. This view rejects the application of {\i kitutei michtat shiurei} to the {\i muchlat} sandal, holding that however undesirable its use, the sandal retains its status as a shoe sufficiently to validate {\i chalitza b\u8217'dieved}. The discussion of {\i kitutei michtat shiurei} and the resolution between these two positions will continue in the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 104\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sandal & Tzara\u8217'at\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bayit Hamusgar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 104, and we pick up somewhere in the middle of 103b, where the {\i gemara} raises a challenge \u8212- {\i meitivei} \u8212- concerning the status of a {\i bayit hamusgar}, a sealed house whose {\i tzara\u8217'at} status is being determined. The broader question under discussion is whether a sandal that has been afflicted with {\i tzara\u8217'at} may be used for {\i chalitza}. The relevant distinction is between {\i musgar} \u8212- a house or object whose status is being evaluated but has not yet been finalized \u8212- and {\i muchlat} \u8212- one whose status has been finalized and which is destined for destruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether something destined for destruction can still function as a meaningful halachic object. There is no per se quantitative {\i shiur} at stake, but the concern is whether the sandal retains its identity as a shoe. Can it be considered a significant shoe if it is slated for destruction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kitutei Mechatat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the principle of {\i kitutei mechatat shiurei} \u8212- the idea that something destined for destruction has already, in a conceptual sense, lost its identity as a functional object. It is not merely that a quantitative measure has been diminished, but that the very status of the object as a coherent, purposeful thing has been undermined. The {\i gemara} will use this principle repeatedly, and it is important to keep it clearly in view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappi/Pappa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical question is framed through two competing traditions in the name of {\i Rava}. {\i Rav Pappi} said in {\i Rava}\u8217's name that a {\i sandal muchlat} is not valid for {\i chalitza}, while {\i Rav Pappa} said in {\i Rava}\u8217's name that it is valid. The {\i gemara} then attempts to challenge and resolve these positions through a series of cases from the laws of {\i tzara\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges: a {\i bayit hamusgar}, once sealed, is {\i tamei} from the inside; a {\i bayit muchlat}, once its status is finalized and it is destined for demolition, conveys {\i tumah} from both inside and outside, and even causes {\i tumah} simply by entering \u8212- as the {\i pasuk} says {\i haba el habayit}, one who enters the house. If we apply {\i kitutei mechatat shiurei} \u8212- if the house scheduled for destruction is as if already ground up \u8212- how can it still function as a house for the purpose of the {\i tumah} of entry? Should it not lose its identity as a house, just as we are suggesting it loses its identity as a shoe?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shani hacha damar kra} \u8212- here the verse itself is different, because it says {\i venatatz et habayit}, \u8220"you shall demolish the house.\u8221" The {\i Torah} teaches that even in the act of demolition, the structure is still called a {\i bayit}. The {\i Torah} explicitly preserves the object\u8217's identity within the framework of {\i tzara\u8217'at} law, even as it is being destroyed. This, however, is an internal ruling confined to the laws of {\i tzara\u8217'at}. When one wishes to apply the object\u8217's identity to some other {\i halachic} context \u8212- such as using it for {\i chalitza} \u8212- that is an entirely different matter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matlit & Kezayit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matlit Garment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then says {\i tashma} \u8212- come and hear a further challenge. If a piece of cloth ({\i matlit}) is three finger-widths by three finger-widths \u8212- the minimum size that defines it as a {\i beged} \u8212- and it is a garment afflicted with {\i tzara\u8217'at}, it conveys {\i tumah} to a pure house when brought inside. Crucially, this holds even if the garment does not have the volume of a {\i kezayit}, a detail that will become significant momentarily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Majority Entry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative rule: {\i keivan shenichnas rubo leveit tahor tim\u8217'ahu} \u8212- once the majority of the garment has entered a pure house, it has rendered that house {\i tamei}. The majority entering is treated as if the whole object entered. This implies that the garment retains its identity as a unified {\i beged}. As {\i Rashi} explains, the {\i gemara} is not suggesting that a {\i muchlat} object loses its {\i shiur} and therefore fails to cause {\i tumah} \u8212- obviously, if that were true, nothing with {\i tzara\u8217'at} could ever cause {\i tumah}, since we would always say it has no {\i shiur}. Rather, the argument is about {\i coherence of identity} as a unified object: we said that something whose {\i shiur} is compromised loses its identity as a coherent object. Yet here, even a fractional majority is treated as representing the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Muchlat Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: are we not discussing a {\i muchletet} \u8212- a garment that is {\i muchlat} and destined for destruction? And yet it retains sufficient identity to cause {\i tumah}? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i lo} \u8212- {\i musgeret}. No, we are actually discussing a {\i musgar} garment, one whose status is still being evaluated, not yet finalized for destruction. So this does not prove anything about a {\i muchlat} object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i If} that is so, says the {\i gemara}, {\i eima seifa} \u8212- let us look at the end of this {\i braita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seifa Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i seifa} discusses a thick garment whose volume amounts to several {\i zeitim} of material. The ruling there is: {\i keivan shenichnas mimenu kezayit} \u8212- once a {\i kezayit} worth of the garment enters the house, even if it is not a three-by-three piece, it renders the house {\i tamei}. Why would a {\i kezayit} be the operative measure for a garment? Normally, {\i kezayit} is not a relevant measure for cloth at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meit Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i ei amrat bishlama muchletet} \u8212- if we are indeed discussing a {\i muchlat} garment, this makes sense. {\i Hainu de-itkash le-meit} \u8212- it is because a {\i metzora} is compared to a dead person, as Moshe prayed for Miriam: {\i al na tehi ke-meit asher bezeito me-rechem imo}. The comparison to a {\i meit} brings the measure of {\i kezayit} into play, since a {\i kezayit} of a corpse conveys {\i tumah}. But this {\i itkash la-meit} \u8212- this comparison to a {\i meit} \u8212- only applies in the {\i muchlat} state. If we were discussing a {\i musgar} garment, there would be no basis for the {\i kezayit} measure. Therefore, the {\i seifa} must be discussing a {\i muchlat} garment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And yet, even in the {\i muchlat} state, the garment retains a coherent identity such that a majority entering is treated as the whole entering. So the challenge to {\i kitutei mechatat shiurei} remains.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Object Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shani Hataim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds with the same logic it used for the house: {\i shani hatam de-amar kra ve-saraf et habeged} \u8212- the {\i Torah} says \u8220"and he shall burn the garment,\u8221" teaching that {\i afilu bish\u8217'at sreifa karui beged} \u8212- even in the moment of its burning, it is still called a {\i beged}. Just as the {\i Torah} explicitly preserves the house\u8217's identity as a house throughout the process of its destruction, it preserves the garment\u8217's identity as a {\i beged} throughout the process of its destruction. This preservation of identity, however, operates within the laws of {\i tzara\u8217'at} internally \u8212- it does not follow that the object carries its identity into other domains of {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises an obvious question: {\i ve-nigmor mineh} \u8212- why not derive from this that the {\i sandal muchlat} retains its identity for {\i chalitza} as well? Since within {\i tzara\u8217'at} we see the object is still called by its name even when destined for destruction, let us apply that to {\i chalitza}!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i issur mi-tumah lo gamrinan} \u8212- we do not derive {\i issur} rulings from {\i tumah} laws. As {\i Rashi} explains, the entire category of {\i hilchot tumah} is a {\i chidush} \u8212- it operates by its own internal logic, which is somewhat arbitrary and not necessarily generalizable to other {\i halachic} domains. The preservation of identity within {\i tzara\u8217'at} is internal to those laws; it does not flow outward into other categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Hilchasa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This gives us the framework for the two versions of {\i Rava}. The position that {\i bedieved} the {\i sandal muchlat} is not valid \u8212- {\i Rav Pappi}\u8217's version \u8212- holds that we do not learn {\i issur} from {\i tumah}, and therefore the preservation of identity within {\i tzara\u8217'at} cannot be applied to {\i chalitza}. The position that {\i bedieved} it is valid \u8212- {\i Rav Pappa}\u8217's version \u8212- holds that once we see the object retains its identity within {\i tzara\u8217'at}, it retains its identity for other {\i halachic} purposes as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking is that the {\i gemara} now quotes {\i Rava} directly: {\i amar Rava hilchasa} \u8212- the ruling is that both a sandal that is {\i muskav} (set aside) and one that is {\i muklat} (afflicted with {\i tzara\u8217'at} and destined for destruction) are valid for {\i chalitza}. This corresponds to {\i Rav Pappa}\u8217's version. One cannot help but note the irony: if {\i Rava} himself ruled explicitly, why did we need two competing traditions transmitted in his name?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Avodah Zarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 AZ Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} continues: {\i ve\u8217'echad sandal hamuklat ve\u8217'echad sandal shel avodat kochavim} \u8212- and even a sandal used as an object of idolatrous worship, {\i lo tachlotz} \u8212- one should not use it {\i lechatchila}, but {\i ve\u8217'im chaltzah chalitzatah kesherah} \u8212- if one did use it {\i bedieved}, the {\i chalitza} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ir Nidachas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i she-teikroves avodat kochavim} \u8212- if the sandal was offered as a sacrifice to an idol rather than being worshipped directly \u8212- or if it belonged to an {\i ir nidachas}, a city condemned to complete destruction \u8212- {\i lo tachlotz}, and {\i ve\u8217'im chaltzah chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- even {\i bedieved} it is invalid. For an {\i ir nidachas}, this is because {\i Rava} acknowledges that {\i kitutei mechatat shiurei} applies when something is genuinely destined for total destruction outside the special framework of {\i tzara\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zakein Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces the case of {\i zakein asui lichvodo} \u8212- a euphemism for a shroud shoe, a shoe prepared specifically to be placed on a corpse and not made for actual walking. Such a shoe, {\i Rava} rules, is invalid even {\i bedieved}. Why? The obvious answer: {\i delav lehilocha avid} \u8212- it is not made for walking. It does not qualify as a shoe in the functional sense required for {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding to the challenge this ruling raises, it is worth pausing to appreciate a remarkable detail that emerges here. It becomes clear that already in the {\i talmudic} era, the {\i beit din} maintained a dedicated {\i chalitza} shoe \u8212- a special shoe set aside specifically for performing {\i chalitza}. This is far from the straightforward scenario in which two people simply appear before a court wearing their ordinary shoes. The existence of a communal {\i beit din} shoe already reflects an institutional practice of considerable sophistication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the difference between {\i avodat kochavim} and {\i tikroves avodat kochavim} \u8212- why is one valid {\i bedieved} and the other not? {\i Rava} offers no explicit distinction in the text, so {\i Rashi} supplies one: a sandal used as an object of {\i avodah zarah} by a non-Jew could potentially have its status annulled \u8212- a non-Jew can be {\i mevatel} it by renouncing belief in it, after which it ceases to be {\i avodah zarah}. Because it has the potential of not being destroyed, we do not fully apply the principle of {\i kitutei mechatat shiurei}. {\i Tosafot} disagrees, understanding the case as referring to {\i avodah zarah} that has already been annulled, and argues on the basis of a related {\i sugya}. {\i Tosafot} further raises the question of whether using an object associated with {\i avodah zarah} is problematic for a {\i mitzvah} object \u8212- drawing a comparison to the {\i shofar} of {\i avodah zarah} \u8212- and treats the {\i chalitza} shoe in a similar conceptual category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi}, by contrast, invokes the principle of {\i mitzvot lav leihanos nitnu} \u8212- the fulfillment of {\i mitzvot} does not constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- to explain why using an {\i avodah zarah} sandal for {\i chalitza} would not violate the prohibition against deriving benefit from {\i avodah zarah}. This framing is itself significant: {\i Rashi} is conceptualizing {\i chalitza} primarily as a {\i kiyum mitzvah}, a ritual act, rather than as a quasi-transactional act of releasing the woman. Whether {\i chalitza} is more like a {\i mitzvah} or more like a legal transaction \u8212- analogous to {\i get} \u8212- has real {\i halachic} implications, and {\i Rashi}\u8217's deployment of {\i mitzvot lav leihanos nitnu} reveals his position clearly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Papa} poses a challenge to {\i Abaye}: what distinguishes the shroud shoe? Even granting that {\i delav lehilocha avid} \u8212- it is not made for walking \u8212- the same would seem to be true of the {\i beit din}\u8217's {\i chalitza} shoe. Who walks around in the {\i beit din}\u8217's special {\i chalitza} shoe? It too is kept exclusively for the performance of {\i chalitza} and is never used for ordinary walking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Shoe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ilu mash\u8217'il lei shulcha debei dina mi kapid alei dayana?} \u8212- if the court\u8217's attendant wanted to borrow the {\i chalitza} shoe, would the judges object? Of course not. The {\i beit din} shoe is available in principle for ordinary use; it simply is not used that way in practice. The shroud shoe, by contrast, is intrinsically defined by its purpose of accompanying a corpse \u8212- it is not made for walking by design, not merely by convention. The answer is logically coherent, though one cannot help noting the irony: the {\i gemara} is essentially defending the validity of the {\i beit din}\u8217's {\i chalitza} shoe on the grounds that, in theory, anyone could borrow it for a stroll.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Night & Left\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} teaches: {\i chaltzah balaylah} \u8212- if she performed {\i chalitza} at night, {\i chalitzatah kesheira} \u8212- it is valid. {\i Rabbi Eliezer posul} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer rules it invalid. Similarly, if the {\i chalitza} was performed on his left foot, {\i chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- it is invalid; {\i Rabbi Eliezer machshir} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer rules it valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes that the dispute about whether {\i chalitza} may be performed at night turns on the question of whether we link court proceedings to the laws of {\i negaim}. The {\i pasuk} says {\i al pihem yihyeh kol riv vechol nega} \u8212- on their word shall be every litigation and every {\i nega}. Since {\i tzara\u8217'at} declarations by the {\i kohen} must be made by day \u8212- {\i bayom hira\u8217'os bo hakohen yitma} \u8212- one might extend this requirement to court proceedings as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately rejects a sweeping application of this link: {\i d\u8217'chulei alma lo makshinan rivim lenegaim} \u8212- everyone agrees we do not make a global equation between litigation and {\i negaim}. Were we to do so fully, we could not even conclude a court case at night, which contradicts established {\i halacha}: while {\i dinei nefashot} must begin and be conducted entirely in the daytime, {\i dinei mamonot} may continue into the night once proceedings have begun.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate is then reframed: {\i mar savar chalitza k\u8217'techilas din damya} \u8212- one holds that {\i chalitza} is analogous to the beginning of a court proceeding, while {\i umar savar chalitza k\u8217'gmar din damya} \u8212- the other holds it is analogous to the conclusion of proceedings. The {\i gemara} itself provides no explanation of this distinction. One might suggest that viewing {\i chalitza} as the {\i gmar din} is natural because, like a verdict, it is the moment at which the legal change of status is effected \u8212- it is simultaneously the beginning and end of the proceeding, and the act itself produces the definitive outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether {\i chalitza} is more like a {\i mitzvah} or more like a {\i din} proves directly relevant here. {\i Rashi} notes that {\i chalitza} has a {\i din} element because the woman collects her {\i ketubah} as a result. More broadly, framing {\i chalitza} as a {\i din} explains why it would be placed in the category of {\i riv} at all, and the {\i machloket} over whether it is the beginning or end of that {\i din} flows naturally from there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought to derive the requirement of daytime from the word {\i le-einei}, since the witnesses must see the act \u8212- but this cannot be the source, as we have seen that even a {\i suma} poses complications, and the {\i gemara} does not pursue that derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The narrative then introduces {\i Rabba bar Chiya Katuspa\u8217'ah}, who performed a {\i chalitza} using a {\i mok} \u8212- a hard felt shoe, previously established as technically valid though not ideal \u8212- alone ({\i bi-yechidi}), without a {\i beit din}, and at night. {\i Shmuel} responded with what {\i Rashi} reads as sarcasm: \u8220"How great is this man,\u8221" meaning: look how freely he relies on minority opinions. The {\i gemara} analyzes which element actually troubled {\i Shmuel}. The {\i mok} was established as valid by an anonymous {\i braita}, not a minority view. The nighttime {\i chalitza} corresponds to the {\i stam} position of our {\i Mishna}, which overrides {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's lone dissent. The genuinely troubling element, says the {\i gemara}, is the absence of a {\i beit din} \u8212- {\i yechidai katani lah} \u8212- that position is sourced in a minority opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} on the following {\i amud} records that {\i chalitza} performed before only two judges, or before three where one turned out to be a relative or otherwise disqualified \u8212- effectively making it two \u8212- is invalid. {\i Rabbi Shimon} and {\i Rabbi Yochanan ha-Sandlar} validate it. And there is the famous case of one who performed {\i chalitza} privately in prison, before no court at all, which {\i Rabbi Akiva} nonetheless upheld. This is the {\i da\u8217'as yachid} upon which {\i Rabba bar Chiya} relied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef bar Minyumi} quoted {\i Rav Nachman}: {\i ein halacha ke-osah ha-zug} \u8212- we do not rule like {\i Rabbi Shimon} and {\i Rabbi Yochanan ha-Sandlar}, the pair who validated two-judge {\i chalitza}. Thus {\i Rabba bar Chiya} was going even further, relying on the even more isolated view of {\i Rabbi Akiva}. The {\i gemara} adds: and if you prefer, you could say that even all three leniencies \u8212- the {\i mok}, the nighttime, and the absence of a {\i beit din} \u8212- ultimately trace back to a single chain of minority tradition, as recorded in a {\i braita} reporting that {\i Rabbi Yishmael ben Elisha} himself once performed {\i chalitza} using felt, alone, and at night. {\i Rabbi Yishmael b\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} reports this \u8212- characteristically, as he is consistently the tradent of eyewitness accounts throughout {\i hazal}. Whether this means the other two leniencies should now be revisited is a separate question for the {\i poskim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} teaches that {\i chalitza} performed on the left foot is invalid, and the {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'rabbanan?} What is the Rabbis\u8217' source for requiring the right foot when the {\i Torah} does not say \u8220"right\u8221" anywhere in the {\i chalitza} passage? {\i Ulla} answers: {\i yalfinan regel regel mimitzora} \u8212- we derive \u8220"foot\u8221" from \u8220"foot\u8221" by the {\i metzora}. In the purification ritual of the {\i metzora}, the text specifies the right foot explicitly. We learn from there to here: {\i mah lehalan yamin af kan yamin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Position Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, who validates left-foot {\i chalitza}, does not accept this {\i gezeira shava} from {\i metzora}. Yet a {\i braita} reports {\i Rabbi Eliezer} himself deriving \u8220"right ear\u8221" for the case of ear-piercing of a slave from the {\i metzora} \u8212- {\i ne\u8217'emar kan ozen ve-ne\u8217'emar lehallan ozen}, a {\i gezeira shava} of \u8220"ear\u8221" to \u8220"ear.\u8221" If he accepts that {\i gezeira shava}, he might as well accept the one from {\i metzora} to {\i chalitza}. {\i Rabbi Yosef bar Yosef} said in the name of {\i Yochanan}: {\i muchlefet hashita} \u8212- reverse the positions, and restore consistency to {\i Rabbi Eliezer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} says: {\i le\u8217'olam lo tehipach} \u8212- do not reverse the positions. There is no need to. {\i Ozen ozen mufni} \u8212- the word \u8220"ear\u8221" appears redundantly in the slave passage, which makes it available to serve as one end of a {\i gezeira shava}. {\i Regel regel lo mufni} \u8212- the word \u8220"foot\u8221" does not appear redundantly in the {\i chalitza} passage, so {\i Rabbi Eliezer} has no available endpoint to anchor the {\i gezeira shava} from {\i metzora} to {\i chalitza}. Hence there is no inconsistency in {\i Rabbi Eliezer} accepting one {\i gezeira shava} but not the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Redundancy Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: {\i ve-chi lo mufni mai pircha ika?} \u8212- even granting that \u8220"foot\u8221" is not redundant by {\i chalitza}, what specific logical challenge would defeat the {\i gezeira shava}? After all, a {\i gezeira shava} need not have redundancy at both endpoints to function; the presence or absence of redundancy affects only whether it can withstand a challenge, not whether it can be made at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i mah le-metzora sheken ta\u8217'un erez eitz erez ve-eizov ve-shani tola\u8217'as} \u8212- {\i metzora} involves an elaborate ritual with cedar wood, hyssop, and crimson thread. Precisely because {\i metzora} is accompanied by such a detailed and demanding set of ritual criteria, its requirement of the right foot is contextually justified. {\i Chalitza} lacks these elaborate requirements, so one cannot assume its rules must mirror those of {\i metzora} in this respect. This challenge cannot be refuted, and therefore the {\i gezeira shava} from {\i metzora} to {\i chalitza} remains unavailable to {\i Rabbi Eliezer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This allows us to articulate a taxonomy of {\i gezeira shava} strength: the most robust form has redundancy at both endpoints and is immune to challenge; a somewhat weaker form has redundancy at one endpoint; the weakest has redundancy at neither endpoint, which means it may still be proposed but can be defeated by any contextual objection. This is precisely the structure the {\i gemara} is working with here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Components\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chaltzah Verakeka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} now turns to the three components of {\i chalitza}: removal of the shoe, spitting, and recitation of the verses. {\i Chaltzah verakeka aval lo kara} \u8212- she removed the shoe and spat but did not recite \u8212- {\i chalitzatah kesheira}, it is valid. Recitation is not {\i me\u8217'akev}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kara verakeka aval lo chaltza} \u8212- she recited and spat but did not remove the shoe \u8212- {\i chalitzatah pesulah}, it is invalid. The act of removal is the core of the entire proceeding; there is a reason it is called {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Spitting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chaltza vekara aval lo rakeka} \u8212- she removed the shoe and recited but did not spit \u8212- {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer chalitzatah pesulah}, invalid; {\i Rabbi Akiva omer chalitzatah kesheira}, valid. The debate concerns whether spitting is {\i me\u8217'akev}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acts Essential\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's reasoning: {\i kkah yeaseh} \u8212- \u8220"thus shall be done\u8221" \u8212- means {\i kol davar shehu maaseh me\u8217'akev}, anything that is an act is indispensable. Spitting is an act; therefore it is {\i me\u8217'akev}. By the same logic, recitation is not an act in the operative sense, and therefore is not {\i me\u8217'akev}. This raises the philosophically rich question of whether {\i dibbur} constitutes {\i maaseh} \u8212- a question {\i Tosafot} engages with at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 To the Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} responds: {\i misham raya?} \u8212- you want to bring proof from there? Read the verse carefully: {\i kacha yeaseh la-ish} \u8212- this shall be done {\i to the man}. {\i Kol davar shenaaseh ba-ish} \u8212- only what is done {\i to} the man is {\i me\u8217'akev}. Removal of the shoe is done to him. Spitting in front of him is not done to him \u8212- she spits before him, not upon him. Therefore spitting is not {\i me\u8217'akev}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Disqualified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hacheresh she-nichlatz veha-chereshet she-chaltzah} \u8212- if a deaf-mute man had the {\i chalitza} performed, or a deaf-mute woman performed it, {\i chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- it is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ketanah she-chaltzah} \u8212- a minor girl who performed {\i chalitza} \u8212- {\i tachlotz mishetagdil}, she must redo it when she reaches adulthood. {\i Ve\u8217'im lo chaltzah chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- if she does not redo it, it remains invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Size\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chaltza bishnayim o bishloshoh ve-nimtza echad mehem karov o pasul} \u8212- if she performed {\i chalitza} before two, or before three where one proved to be a relative or otherwise disqualified, {\i chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- it is invalid. {\i Rabbi Shimon} and {\i Rabbi Yochanan ha-Sandlar} validate such a {\i chalitza} after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Private Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-ma\u8217'aseh be-echad she-chalatz beino le-veinah be-veis ha-asurim} \u8212- there was an incident of a man who performed {\i chalitza} privately in prison, with no {\i beit din} present whatsoever \u8212- {\i va ma\u8217'aseh lifnei Rabbi Akiva ve-hichshir} \u8212- and when it came before {\i Rabbi Akiva}, he declared it valid. This is a striking ruling. The {\i psukim} repeatedly emphasize {\i le-einei ha-zekeinim} \u8212- in the presence of the elders \u8212- yet {\i Rabbi Akiva} validates {\i chalitza} with literally no court at all. {\i Rav Nachman} rules we do not follow this {\i da\u8217'as yachid}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The situation of the {\i chereshet} raises a deeply troubling practical question. If the {\i yavam} is a {\i cheresh} and cannot have {\i chalitza} performed with him, and cannot do {\i yibum} properly either, what becomes of the {\i yevamah}? {\i Rashi} acknowledges explicitly that a {\i chereshet} who needs {\i chalitza} has no remedy through {\i chalitza} \u8212- {\i ein la takana} \u8212- and her only possible relief is {\i yibum}, and if they subsequently wish to separate, he may give her a {\i get}. The {\i halacha} does contemplate situations in which {\i chalitza} is simply impossible and {\i yibum} is the only path, even when that outcome is profoundly difficult. This follows from the structure of the {\i Torah}\u8217's framework: because {\i yibum} remains an alternative, the impossibility of {\i chalitza} does not automatically release the woman from the {\i levirate} bond.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cheresh Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ileim Intellect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now probes the grounds for the {\i cheresh}\u8217's disqualification. {\i Rava} opens with the observation: since the {\i Mishna} unanimously agreed that {\i kriyah} \u8212- recitation \u8212- is not {\i me\u8217'akev}, the {\i cheresh}\u8217's disability need not be the cause of disqualification. Therefore {\i Rava} reasons that a mute person \u8212- {\i ileim} \u8212- who is fully hearing and of full intellect, merely unable to speak, should be perfectly valid for {\i chalitza}. {\i Hashta de-amarta kri\u8217'ah lo me\u8217'akva, lefichach ileim ve-ilemet she-chaltzah chalitzatam kesherah} \u8212- since recitation is not {\i me\u8217'akev}, a mute person may perform {\i chalitza} and it is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intellect Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges: but then what is the reason the {\i cheresh} and {\i chereshet} are disqualified? {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama?} If it is because they cannot recite \u8212- {\i lav mishum delav benei kri\u8217'ah ninhu} \u8212- then a mute person would be equally disqualified by the same reasoning, since a mute person also cannot recite. So is the {\i cheresh}\u8217's disqualification on account of the inability to recite, or on account of lacking full {\i da\u8217'at}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: the {\i cheresh} is disqualified {\i mishum delav benei da\u8217'ah ninhu} \u8212- because they are not of full intellect, not because of the inability to speak. The category of {\i cheresh} in {\i halachic} discourse reflects the reality that, in a society without schools for the deaf, an individual who could neither hear nor speak often developed without acquiring the conceptual framework of normal social and legal life. This is distinct from a mute person who can hear \u8212- who processes language, understands the world, and simply cannot produce speech. {\i Ufumaihu hu de-che\u8217'av lehu} \u8212- their mouth causes them difficulty, not their minds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recitation Ability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} then raises a further challenge from the school of {\i Rabbi Yannai}: {\i lefi she-eino be-omar ve-omrah} \u8212- the {\i cheresh} and {\i chereshet} are disqualified precisely because they are not in the category of \u8220"he says and she says,\u8221" meaning the inability to recite is itself the disqualifying factor, not the lack of {\i da\u8217'at}. This reading is actually more favorable to the dignity of the {\i cheresh} \u8212- it treats them as intellectually capable \u8212- but it has the practical consequence of excluding the {\i ileim} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concedes the point: {\i ela ki itmar de-Rava aseifa itmar} \u8212- {\i Rava}\u8217's statement must actually have been directed at the end of the {\i Mishna}, and his ruling is reversed. A mute person cannot perform valid {\i chalitza}, because although recitation is not {\i me\u8217'akev} as an act that must be performed, the person performing {\i chalitza} must at least have the capacity to recite. Even if the act is not required, the potential must exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Potential Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the principle articulated by {\i Rabbi Zeira}: {\i kol ha-ra\u8217'uy le-bilah ein bilah me\u8217'akevet bo, ve-chol she-eino ra\u8217'uy le-bilah bilah me\u8217'akevet bo.} By the case of the {\i mincha}, the {\i Torah} describes the flour as {\i belulah ba-shemen} \u8212- mixed with oil. If one fails to mix it, {\i bedieved} the {\i mincha} is valid. But if it was impossible to mix \u8212- because the volume of flour was too great \u8212- the {\i mincha} is entirely invalid. The principle: one does not actually have to perform the act, but the act must be performable in principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mincha Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} illuminates the conceptual basis: where the {\i Torah} frames a requirement as a descriptor \u8212- {\i belulah ba-shemen}, \u8220"mixed with oil,\u8221" an adjective rather than a verbal command \u8212- we read it as characterizing the nature of the object rather than mandating a specific act. Therefore, it suffices that the object has the {\i potential} to exhibit that characteristic, even if the act is not performed. By contrast, where the {\i Torah} says {\i ve-amar ve-omrah} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall say and she shall say\u8221" \u8212- one might think this demands actual performance. {\i Rava} resolves this by reading the verbal form as a characterization of the {\i gavra}, the person: the participants must be of the type of person who can say \u8212- they must have the capacity, even if the act is waived. A mute person, lacking that capacity, is therefore excluded, even though the act of recitation itself is not {\i me\u8217'akev}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Spitting Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spitting Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records a ruling sent to the father of {\i Shmuel}: {\i yevamah she-rakakah tachlotz} \u8212- a {\i yevamah} who spat alone, without performing any other component of {\i chalitza}, must subsequently perform full {\i chalitza}. The implication is that the mere spitting alone is enough to disqualify her from {\i yibum} with any of the brothers, leaving {\i chalitza} as her only path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate question: whose opinion does this reflect? If it is {\i Rabbi Akiva}, who rules that spitting is not {\i me\u8217'akev} even when one is in the midst of performing {\i chalitza}, then spitting alone \u8212- performed outside the context of {\i chalitza} entirely \u8212- should carry even less weight. {\i Rabbi Akiva} does not even require spitting when everything else is being done properly. How then could spitting alone, stripped of any further act, be sufficient to disqualify her from {\i yibum}? Surely spitting should be entirely trivial for {\i Rabbi Akiva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves: this must be {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's view, for whom spitting is {\i me\u8217'akev}. But then a further difficulty arises: {\i Rabbi Eliezer} requires two acts \u8212- removal of the shoe and spitting. The principle of {\i shenei devarim ha-matirin} is that when two elements are jointly required to produce a permitted status, neither element alone carries any legal weight. If you have not done both, you have done nothing. How then does spitting alone carry enough weight to disqualify her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eimurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws an analogy to the {\i emurim} \u8212- the internal sacrificial parts that must be burned on the altar before the rest of the animal may be consumed. If the {\i emurim} are lost or become {\i tamei} and cannot be burned, the meat may nonetheless be eaten. But if the {\i emurim} are present, the meat may not be eaten until they are burned. This suggests a nuanced position: when an element that would otherwise be {\i me\u8217'akev} is unavailable, its absence does not block the permitted act; but when it is present and available, it must be performed. By analogy, one might say that spitting, when present and possible, is {\i me\u8217'akev} within {\i chalitza} \u8212- yet its lone occurrence outside {\i chalitza} should still carry no weight, since the removal of the shoe has not been performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution invokes {\i Rabbi}\u8217's position in an unrelated case of {\i kivsei atzeret} \u8212- the lambs brought on {\i Shavuot}. Sanctification of the accompanying loaves requires both the {\i shechita} and the {\i zerakat hadam} of the lambs, performed with proper intent. If the {\i shechita} was proper but the {\i zerakat hadam} was not, {\i Rabbi} rules {\i kadosh ve-eino kadosh} \u8212- it is half-consecrated. There exists an intermediate state of partial sanctification. {\i Rabbi Elazar bar Rabbi Shimon} disagrees: it is either fully sanctified or not at all; there is no halfway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied to our case: according to {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, {\i chalitza} requires two acts. By analogy to {\i Rabbi}\u8217's view, if only one of the two required acts has been performed, the result is not zero \u8212- it is a partial state. The woman has done enough to be in an intermediate status: she has not completed {\i chalitza}, but she has done enough to sever the {\i yibum} bond. It is, in {\i Rabbi}\u8217's terms, a kind of fifty percent \u8212- enough to block {\i yibum} even though full {\i chalitza} has not been achieved. This is a genuinely striking {\i gemara}: the halachic consequence of spitting alone, for {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, flows from the recognition that a partially performed multi-element act can produce a real, if incomplete, legal effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 106\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Private Chalitza Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the bottom of 105b, with the conclusion of the {\i mishna}\u8217's treatment of {\i chalitza} performed outside the formal framework of a {\i beit din}. The {\i mishna} had cited a range of views regarding the minimum requirements for a valid {\i chalitza}, culminating in the opinion that perhaps even two witnesses \u8212- or fewer \u8212- suffice. Rabbi Shimon ben Yochanan HaSandlar is among those who held that a {\i beit din} is not strictly necessary. The practical ruling, however, is that {\i bedi\u8217'avad} one does need a {\i beit din}, and {\i lechatchila} three judges is sufficient \u8212- indeed, even {\i lechatchila} one need not convene five, though the {\i Gemara} from the previous daf established that doing it with five is preferable for the sake of greater publicization.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof of Event\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then records a story: {\i ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'echad she\u8217'chalatz beino l\u8217'veinah b\u8217'veit ha\u8217'asurim} \u8212- a man performed {\i chalitza} with his {\i yevama} privately, in prison, and the matter came before Rabbi Akiva, who declared it {\i kasher}. On this the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i beino l\u8217'veinah mi yadeinan} \u8212- if the act was entirely private, how do we know it occurred? Even if the parties themselves report it, on what basis can a {\i beit din} rule accordingly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eidim Mibachutz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel answers: {\i b\u8217'eidim ra\u8217'uhu she-tzofin mibachutz} \u8212- there were witnesses who observed the proceedings from the outside. They were not themselves a {\i beit din} and were not physically present as part of the ceremony, but they provided sufficient evidence that the event had in fact taken place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a deeper distinction. Witnesses in halachic contexts serve two distinct functions. {\i Eidei kiyum} are witnesses whose presence actually effectuates and validates the legal act \u8212- as in {\i kiddushin}, where even certainty that the act occurred is insufficient without witnesses who legally constitute the act. {\i Eidei birur}, by contrast, serve only the evidentiary function of establishing that something happened. Here, the witnesses observing from outside operated only as {\i eidei birur}. They were certainly not a {\i beit din}, and likely did not even fulfill the role of {\i eidei kiyum} for the {\i chalitza} itself. In principle the act was done entirely privately; there is simply sufficient evidence that it occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Location Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question is then raised: when the {\i mishna} says the story occurred in {\i beit ha\u8217'asurim} and came before Rabbi Akiva \u8212- who exactly was in prison? Did the {\i chalitza} take place outside, with the case subsequently brought to Rabbi Akiva who was imprisoned by the Romans? Or did the {\i chalitza} itself occur inside the prison, and was then reported to Rabbi Akiva who was elsewhere? Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav resolves it: {\i b\u8217'veit ha\u8217'asurim haya ma\u8217'aseh ul\u8217'veit ha\u8217'asurim ba ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- the event happened in prison, and it was brought to Rabbi Akiva in prison as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that this question is somewhat puzzling, since the {\i mishna}\u8217's own language appears unambiguous. The text reads: {\i ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'echad she\u8217'chalatz beino l\u8217'veinah b\u8217'veit ha\u8217'asurim u\u8217'va ma\u8217'aseh lifnei Rabbi Akiva v\u8217'hichshir} \u8212- clearly placing the event in prison and the reporting to Rabbi Akiva thereafter. Tosafot explains that the ambiguity only arises if one removes the {\i vav} of {\i u\u8217'va ma\u8217'aseh}, yielding a reading where {\i b\u8217'veit ha\u8217'asurim} modifies the arrival before Rabbi Akiva rather than the event itself \u8212- though even that reading is a stretch. The reason Tosafot pursues this is that the {\i Yerushalmi} preserves an explicit tradition that this question was brought to Rabbi Akiva while he was incarcerated, and the {\i Gemara} Bavli is attempting to read that tradition into the {\i mishna} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secret Message\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot cites the {\i Yerushalmi}\u8217's colorful account of how the question reached Rabbi Akiva in prison. Yochanan HaSandlar \u8212- himself mentioned in the {\i mishna} as one who ruled that a {\i beit din} is not required \u8212- passed by the prison of Rabbi Akiva. Posing as a peddler, he called out: {\i man ba\u8217'ei nach\u8217'tin, man ba\u8217'ei tzinon} \u8212- who needs needles, who needs pins \u8212- as cover for delivering a covert halachic message. Slipped into his peddler\u8217's cry was the question: {\i chalitza beno uveinah, mahu} \u8212- what is the law if {\i chalitza} was performed privately? Rabbi Akiva, understanding, thrust his head through the prison bars and replied in kind, using the word {\i kushin} \u8212- some type of pin or implement \u8212- whose sound evokes the word {\i kasher}: it is {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mutat & Muafit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Mutat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} moves to the top of 106a with a {\i baraita}: {\i chalitza mutat keshera} \u8212- a {\i chalitza} performed under deception or trickery is valid. The {\i baraita} then asks: what constitutes {\i chalitza mutat}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish defines it as a case where the {\i yavam} wished to perform {\i yibum} but was told: {\i chalot uv\u8217'chach konesah} \u8212- go through this {\i chalitza} ceremony and through it you will acquire her as your wife. In other words, he was misled into believing that the {\i chalitza} ceremony was itself a form of {\i yibum}. Since he may have been unaware of what the ceremony entailed, and since {\i bedi\u8217'avad} the recitation of the verses is not indispensable, the {\i chalitza} might be considered valid despite his misunderstanding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan objects sharply. He cites a teaching: whether he had intent and she did not, or she had intent and he did not, or neither of them had intent \u8212- {\i chalitzatah pesulah}. Both parties must intend for the act to take effect as a {\i chalitza}. How then can Reish Lakish say this is valid? If the man believed he was performing an act of {\i yibum}, his {\i kavanah} was for its very opposite \u8212- he cannot be said to have intended an effective {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan therefore reframes the definition of valid {\i chalitza mutat}: {\i kol she\u8217'omrim lo chalot lamah she\u8217'titein lach matayim zuz} \u8212- they tell him to perform {\i chalitza} and in exchange she will give him two hundred {\i zuz}, but she never actually intends to pay, and in the end does not. In this case, the man fully understood he was performing {\i chalitza}; he simply expected compensation that never materialized. Since he intended the act as {\i chalitza}, it is valid \u8212- the unkept promise is the {\i mutat}, the deception, but it does not undermine the {\i chalitza} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A corroborating {\i baraita} is then cited. {\i Ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'isha achas she\u8217'nafelah lifnei yavam she\u8217'einah haguna lo} \u8212- a woman fell before a {\i yavam} who was not appropriate for her. They told him: perform {\i chalitza} and she will give you two hundred {\i zuz}. The case came before Rabbi Akiva, who validated it even though the money was never paid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditions Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question: if the {\i chalitza} was made {\i al menat} \u8212- on the condition that she pay him two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- why is the {\i chalitza} valid when the condition was not fulfilled? A condition that is not met should render the act void. The answer lies in a principle cited by Rashi from the {\i Gemara} in Ketubot: any act that cannot be delegated to a {\i shaliach} cannot be made conditional. Agency is possible only where a person holds the legal power to effect a transaction and can therefore transfer that power. Where agency is impossible, conditions too cannot bind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a fundamental conceptual point developed by Rav Chaim. He asks why it is that {\i chalitza} performed by a minor woman is, according to a serious opinion, nonetheless valid, when ordinarily a minor cannot perform legally binding acts. His answer addresses the very structure of {\i chalitza} and {\i yibum}. The {\i Torah} does not invest the {\i yavam} or the {\i yevama} with power to effect a change of status. Rather, the {\i Torah} itself severs or creates the bond when the prescribed ritual is performed. The parties are not empowered actors \u8212- they are participants in a ritual whose legal consequence the {\i Torah} draws automatically. This is why {\i yibum} can take effect even through an inadvertent act of intercourse: the {\i Torah} creates the bond upon the act occurring, regardless of {\i da\u8217'at}. Similarly, {\i chalitza} does not require the parties to \u8220"do\u8221" something in the sense of exercising legal capacity \u8212- they go through the motions and the {\i Torah} effects the change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This understanding explains why agency is impossible: you can appoint a {\i shaliach} to exercise a power you hold, but if you hold no power \u8212- if it is the {\i Torah} that does the effecting \u8212- there is nothing to transfer. And since conditions are structurally linked to agency, the same reasoning applies: you cannot condition whether the {\i Torah} will or will not sever a bond upon the ritual\u8217's completion. Therefore, even if the money is not paid, the {\i chalitza} is fully valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Chiya Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a case involving Rabbi Chiya bar Abba. A woman had fallen before a {\i yavam} who was inappropriate for her. Her mother indicated to Rabbi Chiya bar Abba that the man was interested not in the woman but in her money \u8212- he intended to perform {\i yibum}, take her assets, and then divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Trick\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Abba attempted to induce the {\i yavam} to perform {\i chalitza} by telling him: {\i chalot lah betachat hakonsa} \u8212- perform {\i chalitza} and through it you will be married to her. This is precisely the maneuver that Reish Lakish had proposed and that Rabbi Yochanan had rejected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalid Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, the first {\i chalitza} was not a valid one, since the man\u8217's intent was for {\i yibum}, not {\i chalitza}. However, Rabbi Chiya bar Abba then confronted him after the fact: while the {\i chalitza} did not achieve what you thought it would \u8212- it does not make her your wife \u8212- it nonetheless constitutes a {\i chalitza pesulah}, a defective {\i chalitza}. And the {\i halacha} is that after a {\i chalitza pesulah}, {\i yibum} is no longer permitted. Since {\i yibum} is now foreclosed in any case, Rabbi Chiya bar Abba urged him: {\i chalotz lah chalitza ma\u8217'alyasa} \u8212- you might as well now perform a proper {\i chalitza} to free her entirely. Of course, this placed him in a precarious situation, since the man, feeling deceived, might well refuse the second {\i chalitza} out of spite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Papa Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money-Seeker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case is then introduced. The daughter of the father-in-law of Rav Papa \u8212- apparently a sister-in-law or close female relative \u8212- fell before a {\i yavam} who was not suitable for her. His motivation, too, was purely financial: he was interested only in consuming her money. Rav Papa, concerned for her welfare, came before Abaye.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Chiya Case (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Trick\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning briefly to the Rabbi Chiya bar Abba narrative, the better resolution \u8212- and the one relevant to the Rav Papa case \u8212- is to promise the {\i yavam} money as inducement for the {\i chalitza}, without intending to pay. Since, as established above, conditions cannot attach to {\i chalitza}, the {\i chalitza} is valid regardless of whether the money is delivered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Papa Case (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Trick\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, addressing the {\i yavam} in the Rav Papa case, initially proposed the same approach that Rabbi Chiya bar Abba had tried: {\i chalot lah betachat hakonsa} \u8212- perform {\i chalitza} and you will thereby be married to her. Rav Papa immediately challenged Abaye: does the master not hold the ruling of Rabbi Yochanan, that this form of deception invalidates the {\i chalitza}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye conceded and asked: so what should I tell him? Rav Papa replied: tell him {\i chalot lah al menat she\u8217'titein lach matayim zuz} \u8212- perform {\i chalitza} and she will give you two hundred {\i zuz}. This is the valid form of {\i chalitza mutat}. The {\i yavam} proceeded to perform {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the {\i chalitza} was completed, Abaye told the woman she should pay the man the two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- not because the {\i chalitza} required it to be valid, but because she had made a promise, and one ought to honor one\u8217's word. Rav Papa objected: she can say {\i meshata ni bach} \u8212- I was only joking with you, I never really intended to pay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the {\i Gemara} enters territory of {\i Choshen Mishpat} rather than {\i Even HaEzer}. Even granting that the {\i chalitza} is valid regardless, why should she be free of the monetary obligation? Rashi notes the distinction between a gratuitous promise \u8212- which is not legally binding \u8212- and {\i sechirus}, a fee for services rendered. If she agreed to pay him two hundred {\i zuz} for performing {\i chalitza}, that looks like a contracted fee, and ordinarily one is bound by a contracted fee.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 River Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa\u8217's answer draws on a {\i baraita}: a person fleeing from prison came to a river crossing and said to the boatman, {\i tol dinar v\u8217'ha\u8217'avireini} \u8212- take a {\i dinar} and ferry me across. He is obligated only to pay the normal going rate, not the inflated sum \u8212- because the boatman took advantage of the man\u8217's desperate circumstances, and in such situations the claim of {\i meshata ni bach} is available: the agreed price is not binding beyond what the service actually warrants. {\i Chalitza} is a {\i mitzvah}, not a commercial transaction. No one is normally paid for performing it. Any agreement to pay is therefore not a genuine commercial arrangement, and the woman may invoke {\i meshata ni bach} to be released from the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Curse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, apparently frustrated, asked Rav Papa: where is your father? In the city. Where is your mother? In the city. Abaye then cast his gaze upon them, and they died. The reason for Abaye\u8217's anger is not immediately apparent, since both he and Rav Papa were working toward the same goal. One explanation, cited in the name of the Ravyatz, is that Abaye did not intend to harm Rav Papa\u8217's parents; rather, by contemplating Rav Papa\u8217's good fortune in having his parents nearby, Abaye inadvertently caused divine scrutiny to fall upon them, with tragic results.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fee Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot adds an important qualification to the principle of {\i meshata ni bach}. The claim is available specifically in a case where an exorbitant sum was demanded far in excess of the normal rate \u8212- as with the boatman exploiting a fugitive\u8217's desperation. Where the agreed fee actually reflects the going rate, or where the person agreeing to perform the service bears genuine risk or cost, the claim of {\i meshata ni bach} is unavailable and the obligation stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get vs Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tricked Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} now draws the contrast systematically. {\i Chalitza mutat keshera} \u8212- a {\i chalitza} performed under financial inducement that is never paid is valid. {\i Get mutat pasul} \u8212- a {\i get} obtained under a broken promise of payment is invalid. The reason follows directly from the earlier analysis: a {\i get} is a legal act that can be given through a {\i shaliach}, and therefore conditions can attach to it. If the {\i get} was given {\i al menat} that she pay him a sum of money, and the money is not paid, the condition is not fulfilled and the {\i get} is void. {\i Chalitza}, by contrast, cannot be made conditional, and therefore the unkept promise does not affect its validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- that {\i chalitza} cannot be conditioned because it cannot be delegated to an agent \u8212- has already been fully developed above, and the {\i baraita} confirms it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i chalitza muafit pesulah} \u8212- a {\i chalitza} that was outright compelled, with no pretense of the {\i yavam}\u8217's agreement, is invalid. {\i Get meuaseh kasher} \u8212- a {\i get} that was forced by a {\i beit din} is valid, as will be learned in {\i Gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presses: what is the principled distinction? If the {\i yavam} says {\i rotzeh ani} \u8212- I am now willing \u8212- after being compelled, then surely {\i chalitza} too should be valid; and if he does not say {\i rotzeh ani}, then the {\i get} too should be invalid. On simple analysis there seems to be no asymmetry between them. The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the {\i baraita} must be read as distinguishing cases. {\i Chalitza mutat} is always valid; {\i get mutat} is always invalid. {\i Chalitza muafit} and {\i get meuaseh} are each sometimes valid and sometimes not \u8212- and the determinative factor is whether the party ultimately says {\i rotzeh ani}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} about {\i korbanot} anchors the principle: {\i yakriv oto} \u8212- he shall offer it \u8212- teaches that {\i beit din} compels a person who refuses to bring an obligatory {\i korban}. {\i Yachol al korcho} \u8212- might this mean against his will entirely? {\i Talmud lomar lirtzono} \u8212- the verse says it must be with his will. The resolution: {\i kofin oto ad she\u8217'yomar rotzeh ani} \u8212- one compels him until he says he wishes to do it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Willingness Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle applies to {\i gittin}: {\i kofin oto ad she\u8217'yomar rotzeh ani}. And the {\i baraita} draws the parallel explicitly. The forced act is validated not by the compulsion but by the eventual expression of willingness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motivation vs Desire\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reflects a striking philosophical distinction the {\i Gemara} draws between motivation and desire. The {\i Gemara} even permits, in a number of contexts, transactions entered into under extreme duress \u8212- on the theory that whenever someone sells property, it is because economic necessity forced them to, and external compulsion operates no differently. What matters is not the source of motivation but the present state of will. If the {\i yavam} now says {\i rotzeh ani}, his motivation for reaching that point is irrelevant \u8212- he presently desires it, and the act is valid. The distinction between external compulsion and internal desire, the {\i Gemara} teaches, is less sharp than one might assume.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Din & Docs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to a ruling transmitted through a chain: Rav said in the name of Rava who said in the name of Rav Sechora who said in the name of Rav Huna \u8212- {\i choltzin af al pi she\u8217'ein makirim}, a {\i beit din} may conduct a {\i chalitza} ceremony even if they do not personally know who the parties are. Similarly, they may conduct a {\i mi\u8217'un} ceremony without knowing the parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence follows directly: {\i lefikach ein kotvim get chalitza ela im ken makirim} \u8212- witnesses cannot write the documentary record of the {\i chalitza} unless they personally know who the parties are. The reasoning is that since the {\i beit din} performs the ceremony without verifying identity, there is a real possibility that the man was not in fact the {\i yavam} \u8212- a woman might arrange for a stranger to go through the ceremony. If the witnesses write the document without knowing the man, they would be attesting to something they cannot verify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Courts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern is specifically for {\i beit din} to\u8217'eh \u8212- a later {\i beit din} that receives the document and assumes it was properly conducted, not realizing that the original {\i beit din} may have been misled about who the {\i yavam} was. Therefore the witnesses, who do have the capacity to know the parties, bear the responsibility of verification before committing anything to writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, on his own account, takes a stricter position on the front end: {\i ein choltzin ela im ken makirim} \u8212- a {\i beit din} may not conduct the ceremony at all unless they know the parties. This is the straightforward rule, and it is difficult to argue otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safe Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence of Rava\u8217's view for the documentary question is the inverse: {\i v\u8217'lefikach kotvim get chalitza af al pi she\u8217'ein makirim} \u8212- the witnesses may write the document even if they themselves do not know the man. Since the {\i beit din} has already established the identity of the parties before proceeding, the witnesses need not independently verify. The first {\i beit din} has done its due diligence, and no later {\i beit din} will be misled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitza Ceremony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Advice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishna} of the {\i perek} describes the complete {\i chalitza} ceremony step by step. He and his {\i yevama} come before the {\i beit din}, and the {\i beit din} gives them appropriate guidance \u8212- {\i eitzah ha\u8217'hogenet lo} \u8212- advice suitable to his situation, counseling whether {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} is the proper course.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Declares\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the decision for {\i chalitza} is made, the woman recites: {\i me\u8217'ein yivmi l\u8217'hakim l\u8217'achiv shem b\u8217'Yisrael lo avah yivmi} \u8212- my {\i yavam} does not wish to establish his brother\u8217's name in Israel; he does not desire to do {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Declares\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The man then recites: {\i lo chafatzti lekachtah} \u8212- I do not desire to take her. The {\i mishna} notes that this recitation is not strictly {\i me\u8217'akev} but constitutes the proper procedure. Both declarations are made {\i b\u8217'lashon hakodesh}, in Hebrew \u8212- as the {\i mishna} in {\i Sotah} enumerates among the acts that must be performed in the original Hebrew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoe Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'nigshah yevimto eilav l\u8217'einei hazekenim v\u8217'chaltzah na\u8217'alo me\u8217'al raglo} \u8212- the {\i yevama} approaches him before the elders and removes his shoe from his foot, following the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spitting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'yarkah b\u8217'fanav} \u8212- and she spits before him. The {\i mishna} inserts: {\i rok ha\u8217'nireh la\u8217'dayanim} \u8212- the spit must be visible to the judges. Whether spitting is strictly {\i me\u8217'akev} has been debated, but the requirement that it be visible to the judges is essential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She then recites: {\i kachah ye\u8217'aseh la\u8217'ish asher lo yivneh et beit achiv} \u8212- thus shall be done to the man who does not build his brother\u8217's house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Horkanus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Until this point, the {\i beit din} would prompt her to recite each phrase after them, and the ceremony concluded with that verse. But {\i u\u8217'cheshe\u8217'hikri Rabbi Horkanus tachas ha\u8217'elah b\u8217'Kfar Eitam v\u8217'gamar kol haparasha} \u8212- when Rabbi Horkanus officiated under the {\i elah} tree in Kfar Eitam, he had her continue to the end of the verse: {\i v\u8217'nikra shemo b\u8217'Yisrael beit chalutz hana\u8217'al} \u8212- and his name shall be called in Israel: the house of the removed shoe. After Rabbi Horkanus instituted this, the practice spread and it became standard to have her recite the full verse through to that concluding phrase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Declares\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then states: {\i mitzvah b\u8217'dayanim v\u8217'lo mitzvah b\u8217'talmidim} \u8212- the obligation to make the declaration {\i beit chalutz hana\u8217'al} rests with the judges, not the students observing the proceedings. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: the verse says {\i b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- before Israel \u8212- implying that all those present at the ceremony should call out {\i chalutz hana\u8217'al}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Verse Recitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Recitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rules that the person conducting the {\i chalitza} ceremony \u8212- the member of the {\i beit din} who is having the parties recite their lines \u8212- must be careful not to have her recite the words with pauses that break the verse into misleading segments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Aba Yibmi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Specifically: do not have her say {\i lo} alone and then pause before {\i aba yibmi}. The verse is {\i lo aba yibmi} \u8212- my {\i yavam} does not wish \u8212- but if she says {\i lo} and pauses, the phrase {\i aba yibmi} standing alone means \u8220"my {\i yavam} does wish.\u8221" She must say the full phrase {\i lo aba yibmi} in one breath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Chafatzti\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, do not have him say {\i lo} and then pause before {\i chafatzti lekachtah}. That creates the impression {\i chafatzti lekachtah} \u8212- I do desire to take her. He must say {\i lo chafatzti lekachtah} as one continuous phrase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Lenient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava takes a more lenient position: {\i pesukei milta} \u8212- making a pause is simply a matter of completing one\u8217's sentence. The context makes clear what is meant, and a brief pause between {\i lo} and the rest of the phrase does not create a genuine misunderstanding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, Rava agrees that for the specific phrase {\i lo aba yibmi} there is no room for leniency, and it must be said as a single phrase. Rashi explains: unlike {\i lo chafatzti lekachtah}, which begins the verse and has nothing preceding it to create ambiguity, the phrase {\i lo aba yibmi} appears mid-verse, following {\i me\u8217'ein yibmi l\u8217'hakim shem l\u8217'achiv b\u8217'Yisrael}. In that context, a pause after {\i lo} is far more likely to be heard as a denial of what preceded: \u8220"that\u8217's not right \u8212- {\i aba yibmi}, actually he does want to.\u8221" Therefore this particular phrase must be delivered without interruption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Care Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} illustrates the importance of this ruling. Rav Papa and Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua once observed Rav Kahana going to great lengths to have the {\i yevama} recite the words {\i lo aba yibmi} all as one phrase. They asked: does the master not hold like Rava\u8217's lenient view? Rav Kahana replied: Rava concedes regarding {\i lo aba yibmi}. Even Rava agrees that for that specific phrase, pauses are not acceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fragment Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rules regarding the writing of the {\i get chalitza} \u8212- the documentary record of the ceremony. One should not write out the complete biblical verses. Rather: {\i akrinuhu l\u8217'didah min me\u8217'ein yibmi ad lo aba yibmi} \u8212- we had her recite from {\i me\u8217'ein yibmi} until {\i lo aba yibmi}, citing the opening and closing words but not writing out the full text. Similarly: {\i akrinuhu l\u8217'dideih min lo ad lekachtah} \u8212- we had him recite from {\i lo} until {\i lekachtah}, deliberately omitting the middle word {\i chafatzti}. And for her subsequent verse: from {\i kacha} until {\i vuchalutz hana\u8217'al}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sirtuv Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for these deliberate gaps is the prohibition against writing biblical passages without {\i sirtut} \u8212- the scored lines that must accompany Torah writing. If one were to write out the full verses, three or more consecutive words of scripture would appear in the document without the requisite scoring, raising a concern about treating biblical text improperly outside the context of a sacred scroll. By dropping a word in the middle, the document avoids transcribing a complete biblical phrase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man\u8217's Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the man\u8217's verse, the document records: from {\i lo} until {\i lekachtah} \u8212- with {\i chafatzti} omitted so that no three consecutive biblical words appear in sequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the woman\u8217's verse at the conclusion of the ceremony, the document records: from {\i kacha} until {\i vuchalutz hana\u8217'al}, similarly structured to avoid a complete biblical phrase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra followed a different practice: {\i mesartet v\u8217'chatav lechulah parasha} \u8212- he scored the document with {\i sirtut} and wrote out the entire {\i parasha}. He evidently held that with proper scoring the concern is resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa bar Idi challenged this: {\i veha lo nitenu lichtov} \u8212- one is not permitted to write out individual sections of the Torah as standalone passages. The {\i Gemara} in {\i Gittin}, Perek Hanizukin, teaches that one does not write a {\i megillah} \u8212- a miniature scroll of select passages \u8212- for a child to learn from, precisely because Torah must be written as a complete unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rules: {\i v\u8217'hilchata k\u8217'Mar Zutra} \u8212- the law follows Mar Zutra. The prohibition against writing isolated Torah passages applies where one is writing it {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'parasha} \u8212- as a small self-contained excerpt intended to function as a standalone Torah text. The {\i get chalitza} is different: {\i v\u8217'lo lekdusha hu} \u8212- it is not written as a sacred text but as a legal document, to record what was recited during the ceremony. Since the intent is documentary rather than scriptural, writing the full passage with {\i sirtut} is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Spitting Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wind Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rules: if the woman spat and the wind carried the spit away \u8212- {\i lo asa velo klum} \u8212- it counts for nothing. The {\i Torah} requires {\i v\u8217'yarkah b\u8217'fanav} \u8212- she shall spit {\i before him}, meaning the spit must arrive in his presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The requirement is not that the spit physically strike him, but that it land in front of him in a way that constitutes spitting {\i before} him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Height Factor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Height differences between the parties affect whether this requirement is met. If he is tall and she is short, then even if the wind blows the spit away after it leaves her mouth, it was already {\i b\u8217'fanav} \u8212- before his face \u8212- the moment it emerged, since he was towering above her as she spit in his direction. In that case the {\i chalitza} is valid even if the spit is carried off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Front of Face\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she is tall and he is short, the spit leaves her mouth at a height above his face. The requirement \u8212- {\i ba\u8217'inan ad demati lahadi apeih} \u8212- is that the spit must actually descend to the level of his face before any dispersion occurs. If the wind catches it before it reaches his face level, the requirement has not been met and the {\i chalitza} is compromised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds: if the woman ate garlic, or ate clay, and then spat \u8212- {\i lo asa velo klum} \u8212- it does not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own Accord\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\i v\u8217'yarkah me\u8217'atzma ba\u8217'inan} \u8212- the spitting must come from her own initiative and from her natural saliva. If she ate something that induced her to spit, or that altered the character of what she produced, the act is not genuinely {\i her} spitting but something caused by the food. This fails the requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judge Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava further rules: {\i tzrichi dayanei lemichzi roka ki nafik mipuma d\u8217'yevama} \u8212- the judges must see the spit at the moment it leaves the {\i yevama}\u8217's mouth. The verse states {\i l\u8217'einei hazekenim v\u8217'yarkah} \u8212- to the eyes of the elders and she shall spit \u8212- meaning the act of spitting itself must be directly witnessed by the judges, not merely inferred from its result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Proclamation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'nikra shemo b\u8217'Yisrael beit chalutz hana\u8217'al} \u8212- and his name shall be called in Israel: the house of the one whose shoe was removed. This declaration concludes the ceremony and formally fixes his new status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Declaration Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} rules: {\i mitzvah b\u8217'dayanim velo b\u8217'talmidim} \u8212- the obligation of this declaration falls upon the judges, not upon students or observers. Rabbi Yehuda holds, as noted above, that {\i b\u8217'Yisrael} implies all those present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarfon Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records: Rabbi Yehuda said \u8212- once we were sitting before Rabbi Tarfon and a {\i yevama} came to perform {\i chalitza}. Rabbi Tarfon said to all of us: say {\i chalutza na, chalutza na, chalutza na} \u8212- three times in succession, in a manner similar to other rabbinic proclamations of permitted status ({\i mutar lach, mutar lach, mutar lach}). This triple declaration publicly and ceremonially marks the finality of the {\i chalitza} and the new status of the {\i chalutzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek End\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach mitzvas chalitza} \u8212- with the conclusion of this {\i baraita}, the {\i perek} of {\i mitzvas chalitza} formally ends.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 107\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mi\u8217'un Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf opens a new {\i perek} \u8212- {\i Perek Beit Shammai} \u8212- which, despite its name, is not exclusively focused on {\i Beit Shammai} but rather opens with a series of debates between {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel}, returning us to the world of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mi\u8217'un Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i perek} centers on the institution of {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- refusal by a minor girl. The background is as follows: a girl whose father has died may be married off by her mother and brothers. Even though the marriage is of rabbinic force only, and even though she actually assents to it ({\i mi-da\u8217'atah}), she retains the right to be {\i mema\u8217'enet} \u8212- to refuse her husband \u8212- until she reaches adulthood. The critical and defining feature of {\i mi\u8217'un} is its retroactive effect: unlike a {\i get}, which dissolves a marriage that existed, {\i mi\u8217'un} renders the marriage as though it never took place at all. She is considered to have never been married. This distinction carries significant practical implications, not least for matters of {\i kehunah} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i perek} raises questions about precisely at which stages of the marriage process {\i mi\u8217'un} may or may not be exercised, and what its implications are for {\i yibum} when a husband dies and a brother-in-law enters the picture.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arusot/Nesuot Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} opens: {\i Beit Shammai omrim, ein mema\u8217'anin ela arusot} \u8212- only girls who are still in the {\i arusah} stage, the stage of betrothal, may perform {\i mi\u8217'un}. Once the girl has become a {\i nesuah} \u8212- once she has begun actually living with her husband and the marriage has become a lived reality \u8212- {\i Beit Shammai} holds that {\i mi\u8217'un} is no longer available to her. The marriage has reached a qualitatively different level when it has moved beyond the purely legal and technical into the genuinely lived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel} disagrees: {\i arusot u-nesuot} \u8212- at either stage, whether she is an {\i arusah} or a {\i nesuah}, she retains the right to perform {\i mi\u8217'un}. The {\i Mishnah} continues with additional disputes. {\i Beit Shammai} holds that {\i mi\u8217'un} is effective only against the husband ({\i b\u8217'ba\u8217'al}) but not against the {\i yabam}. {\i Beit Hillel} holds it applies {\i be-ba\u8217'al u-ve-yabam} \u8212- against both the husband and the brother-in-law. {\i Beit Shammai} further requires that the {\i mi\u8217'un} be performed in the presence of the husband and before a {\i Beit Din}; {\i Beit Hillel} permits it even not in his presence and even outside of a formal court setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then notes that {\i Beit Hillel} observes that a girl may be {\i mema\u8217'enet} as a minor even four or five times \u8212- her mother and brothers remarry her, she refuses again, and the cycle repeats. While this is stated as a legal observation, {\i Beit Shammai} responds with a sharp principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} declares: {\i ein benot Yisrael hefker} \u8212- daughters of Israel must not be treated as ownerless or unprotected. Even if the mechanism is technically her choice, allowing a girl to be serially married off and to repeatedly refuse reduces her to an object passed from one man to the next in a manner fundamentally at odds with her dignity and the protections she deserves. Therefore {\i Beit Shammai} rules: she may perform {\i mi\u8217'un} with the first husband, but thereafter she must wait until she reaches adulthood \u8212- {\i mamtenet ad shetagdil} \u8212- and only then marry again. The concern of {\i lo yehei Bnot Yisrael hefker} translates into a legal restriction: we prevent the second rabbinic marriage from being eligible for {\i mi\u8217'un} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A closer look reveals that this injunction is directed not purely at her but at the institution itself. While the girl assents to each marriage \u8212- the {\i Gemara} will later make clear that {\i kiddushei ketanah} by mother and brothers requires her {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- the concern of {\i lo yehei Bnot Yisrael hefker} operates at the structural level. The rabbis are not simply protecting her freedom; they are preventing a pattern that, however voluntary in form, compromises her standing. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's curious insertion of the word {\i tema\u8217'en} a second time \u8212- even in the instruction to wait until adulthood \u8212- is something the {\i Gemara} will address.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ein Tenai Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stipulations Fail\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to explain {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's position. {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel}: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama de-Beit Shammai? Lefi she-ein tenai be-nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- because there are no effective stipulations at the stage of {\i nisu\u8217'in}. The underlying assumption is that in a standard marriage, while stipulations ({\i tenaim}) might theoretically function at the stage of {\i kiddushin}, once the couple reaches {\i nisu\u8217'in} such conditions become inoperative. And therefore, in a normal marriage, there would be no mechanism by which the marriage could be retroactively dissolved after {\i nisu\u8217'in} through an external stipulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tnai vs Ta\u8217'ut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle requires some precision. {\i Ein tenai be-nisu\u8217'in} is not an absolute statement that stipulations are legally impossible at the {\i nisu\u8217'in} stage. The {\i Gemara} elsewhere explains that the reason {\i tenaim} are considered waived at {\i nisu\u8217'in} is {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} \u8212- a man does not want his acts of marital intimacy to be conditional, such that if the condition is not met, the sex retroactively becomes extramarital. Therefore, as a general presumption, any stipulation he might have made is considered voluntarily waived once the couple begins living together. This is distinct from {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- a case of fundamental error \u8212- which can retroactively nullify even a {\i nisu\u8217'in}-stage marriage, since it is not an external stipulation but a basic mistake about the nature of the transaction itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Misconception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this background, {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's concern becomes clear: if we allow a {\i nesuah} to perform {\i mi\u8217'un} and simply walk away without a {\i get}, observers who see a couple who have been living together for years suddenly separate will assume there must have been a {\i tenai} \u8212- a stipulation \u8212- that was activated. And from that they will draw the dangerous and false inference that {\i tenaim} are operative at the stage of {\i nisu\u8217'in}. The consequence would be that people would believe they can construct conditional marriages that dissolve retroactively even in full adult marriages, leading to the erosion of the entire institution of {\i nisu\u8217'in}. This concern for public misconception \u8212- {\i ati l-meimar yesh tenai be-nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- is the driving force behind {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's position according to this explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle of {\i ein tenai benisuin} became historically significant: it is the very title of a famous {\i sefer} compiled from the rabbinic responsa that opposed the mid-nineteenth-century proposal by French rabbis to use conditional marriage as a solution to the {\i agunah} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} here raises a pointed difficulty. The same {\i Shmuel} who formulates the reasoning of {\i Beit Shammai} as based on {\i ein tenai benisuin} appears elsewhere \u8212- in the context of {\i Perek HaMadir} \u8212- to hold that if a man betrothed a woman with a condition and then cohabited with her without repeating the condition ({\i kidshah al tenai ve-khansah stam}), she does not require a {\i get}, a ruling that {\i Abaye} and {\i Rava} explain according to {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's own reasoning of {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut}. But this implies that even according to {\i Beit Shammai}, in {\i Shmuel}\u8217's view, {\i yesh tenai benisuin} under certain circumstances. {\i Tosafot} works through the resolution, and the essential point is that {\i ein tenai benisuin} is not an absolute legal impossibility \u8212- it is a presumptive rule that may yield under specific conditions, and the principle is less categorical than the polemical use of its name might suggest.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 When Mi\u8217'un Ceases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuppah Stage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} probes the precise boundary of {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's position. Since {\i Beit Shammai} says only an {\i arusah} can perform {\i mi\u8217'un}, this means anyone who is no longer an {\i arusah} cannot \u8212- even if she has not yet become a full {\i nesuah}. {\i Rashi} highlights the logical asymmetry: {\i Beit Shammai} does not say \u8220"a {\i nesuah} cannot perform {\i mi\u8217'un}\u8221" \u8212- they say \u8220"only an {\i arusah} can.\u8221" These are not equivalent. The excluded middle matters. Someone who is no longer an {\i arusah} but not yet a {\i nesuah} is also excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Who occupies this intermediate status? The {\i Gemara} considers the case of {\i nichnesah lechuppah velo niv\u8217'alah} \u8212- a girl who has entered the {\i chuppah} but has not yet had sexual relations. She is beyond {\i erusin} but not yet fully {\i nesuah} in the sense of consummated cohabitation. Perhaps {\i mi\u8217'un} should work here, since the reasoning that {\i tenaim} fail at {\i nisu\u8217'in} is grounded in the concern about {\i beilat zenut}, and there has been no {\i beilah} yet?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuppat Issura\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i lefi she-ein tenai be-chuppah} \u8212- stipulations do not work even at the {\i chuppah} stage. The reasoning is that just as a man would not want his act of {\i beilah} to be retroactively recontextualized as {\i beilat zenut}, so too he would not want his {\i chuppah} \u8212- which is entirely suggestive of and oriented toward marital intimacy and cohabitation \u8212- to be retroactively rendered {\i chuppat issura}, a {\i chuppah} in a non-marital context. The {\i chuppah} is not merely a ceremony; it represents the crossing of the threshold into marital life, and one would not want it to have taken place under conditions that would render it as though the couple were never married. Therefore, {\i lo nicha lei d\u8217'teihavei chuppat issura} \u8212- he would never have allowed his {\i chuppah} to stand on conditional terms, meaning any {\i tenai} is considered waived there as well. Consequently, since there is no {\i tenai} operative at {\i chuppah}, there is also no {\i mi\u8217'un} after {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transfer to Agents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pushes further. Even before {\i chuppah}, there is a stage at which the girl has left the domain of her father \u8212- or in this case her mother and brothers \u8212- and been handed over to the husband\u8217's representatives for escort to his home: {\i masar av li-shluchei ha-ba\u8217'al}. At this point she is no longer a pure {\i arusah}, but she has not yet entered {\i chuppah}. Here there should be no concern of {\i beilat zenut} or {\i chuppat issura} \u8212- nothing intimate has occurred or been initiated. If {\i tenai} could work here, why not {\i mi\u8217'un}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers with the principle of {\i lo plug rabanan} \u8212- the rabbis did not draw fine distinctions between these intermediate stages. One might be right that, technically, {\i tenai} could be operative in this transitional state between {\i erusin} and {\i chuppah}. But once the rabbis established that {\i mi\u8217'un} does not apply once a girl is no longer an {\i arusah}, they did not distinguish between the subtle gradations between {\i arusah} and {\i nesuah}. All of those intermediate stages are treated as connected to the concerns of {\i nisu\u8217'in}, and allowing {\i mi\u8217'un} in any of them would create precisely the false impression of {\i tenai be-nisu\u8217'in} that {\i Beit Shammai} sought to prevent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bei\u8217'at Zenut Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabba} and {\i Rav Yosef} offer an alternative explanation for {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's position that moves away from the concern about public misconception and engages instead with the reality of the marital relationship itself. Their reasoning: {\i ta\u8217'ama de-Beit Shammai lefi she-ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} \u8212- a man will not allow his acts of marital intimacy to be categorized as fornication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Sanctity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern here is practical and relational. If we tell a man that even after {\i nisu\u8217'in} this young girl can at any moment perform {\i mi\u8217'un} and walk away \u8212- retroactively declaring that she was never his wife \u8212- then he will be unwilling to consummate the marriage at all. He does not want to be in the position of having had sexual relations with a girl who is not and never was his wife. The very integrity of the marital relationship demands that it not be subject to retroactive nullification through {\i mi\u8217'un}. This is not a concern about what observers will think; it is a concern about the husband\u8217's own experience of the relationship and his unwillingness to participate if its legitimacy is perpetually in abeyance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} again applies this reasoning through the stages of {\i chuppah} and the transfer to the husband\u8217's agents, yielding the same {\i lo plug rabanan} conclusion \u8212- once the rabbis established the rule, they did not subdivide it by stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Institution Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel} responds: {\i keivan d\u8217'ika kiddushin v\u8217'ketubah, lo atu l-meimar d\u8217'biato biat zenut} \u8212- since everything that happened took place within the framework of {\i kiddushin} and {\i ketubah}, no one will categorize the marital relations as {\i beilat zenut}. Even if {\i mi\u8217'un} is later performed and retroactively the marriage is dissolved, that does not recast the nature of the sex that occurred. It took place within a rabbinic institution, preceded by an act of {\i kiddushin} and a {\i ketubah}. It was not two people taking a hotel room \u8212- it was a structured, legally recognized framework. And therefore, even after {\i mi\u8217'un}, the sex that occurred within that framework will never be redefined as extramarital.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kehunah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point carries significant implications. If retroactively the marriage never existed, one might ask: is the girl considered to have had sex outside of marriage, with all the {\i halachic} consequences that might entail? For instance, some {\i rishonim} hold that {\i zonah} status for a {\i kohen} applies to any woman who has had sex outside of marriage. The answer here is no \u8212- even though legally, in retrospect, there was no marriage, the sex that occurred within the rabbinic institution of {\i kiddushei ketanah} will never carry the stigma of extramarital relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also bears on the broader question raised by the {\i Rambam}, who holds that any sex outside of marriage constitutes a {\i lav de-oraita} of {\i lo tihyeh kedeishah}. One might ask: how did the rabbis ever permit rabbinic marriages that are not {\i de-oraita} marriages, if all such sex is technically extramarital according to the {\i Rambam}? The answer is precisely this \u8212- even a rabbinic marriage, taking place within the legal and social institution of marriage with its formal acts of {\i kiddushin} and {\i ketubah}, redefines the nature of the sex. It is no longer {\i zenut} because the institution within which it occurs transforms its character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This line of reasoning has been extended in modern discussions of {\i agunah} solutions. If {\i ein tenai benisuin} is grounded in {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut}, some contemporary {\i poskim} have asked whether a couple who remain in a secular legal marriage \u8212- even if the {\i halachic} bond is contested \u8212- would similarly not be considered to have engaged in {\i beilat zenut}, since they remain within an institutional framework of marriage. The analogy to {\i kiddushei ketanah} with subsequent {\i mi\u8217'un} is real, though extending it further \u8212- to a couple cohabiting without any legal framework whatsoever \u8212- raises distinct concerns.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Financial Incentives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strip-Mining Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} offers an entirely different rationale for both {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel}, anchored not in marital intimacy or public perception but in financial considerations. Both sides, he says, are reasoning from the same economic concern \u8212- {\i nichsei milug}, the wife\u8217's property over which the husband holds usufruct rights ({\i peiri}) \u8212- but reaching opposite conclusions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's concern: {\i shama d\u8217'achil lehu minei, d\u8217'sof sof l-mafka ka-atya} \u8212- if the girl can perform {\i mi\u8217'un} at any time and walk away with her property, the husband will reason that his hold on the property is temporary and precarious. He will therefore strip-mine it \u8212- cut down the trees, extract what he can immediately \u8212- rather than tend to it, invest in it, and maintain it over time. The threat of {\i mi\u8217'un} thus creates a perverse incentive for destructive short-term exploitation of the wife\u8217's estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investment Incentive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel} inverts the argument entirely: {\i adaraba} \u8212- quite the contrary. {\i Kivan d\u8217'amart tema\u8217'en, ashvochei mashvach lehu} \u8212- because she {\i can} perform {\i mi\u8217'un}, the husband will take excellent care of the property. He knows that if he mismanages it, her relatives will advise her to leave him \u8212- {\i yatz\u8217'u lah kroveha} \u8212- and she will take her property back. The threat of {\i mi\u8217'un} thus functions as a disciplining mechanism, incentivizing the husband to be a responsible steward of her estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wedding Expenses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} adds yet another dimension to {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's reasoning: {\i she-ein adam tore\u8217'ach be-seudah u-mafsidah} \u8212- a man will not go to the trouble and expense of a wedding celebration if he knows it may retroactively be rendered meaningless. The concern is not merely financial in the abstract; it points to the social reality that the progression from {\i kiddushin} to {\i nisu\u8217'in} involves real investment \u8212- the wedding feast, the communal celebration \u8212- and a husband unwilling to commit will avoid making that investment. Since the rabbis created {\i kiddushei ketanah} as an institution serving genuine economic and social needs of the time, they wanted it to actually progress to {\i nisu\u8217'in}; creating conditions that discourage the move to {\i nisu\u8217'in} undermines the entire institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Celebration Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} responds to {\i Rava}\u8217's concern with a telling observation: {\i tara-vaihu nicha lehu} \u8212- both parties actually {\i want} the wedding celebration, even if it may later be retroactively dissolved. Why? {\i Kedei le-guzlei kalah kayetzei ishah} \u8212- people desire the social standing that comes with being publicly married. Even a young girl benefits from having had a wedding, from having this status in the community, regardless of what eventually happens. The desire for the celebration exists independently of the permanence of the marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mi\u8217'un & Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai: Ba\u8217'al Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i Beit Shammai omrim b\u8217'ba\u8217'al} \u8212- she can perform {\i mi\u8217'un} only against her husband, not the {\i yabam}. The scenario is readily imagined: the girl was given in this rabbinic {\i kiddushei ketanah}, her husband died before she performed {\i mi\u8217'un}, and now she stands before the brother-in-law with the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. Can the {\i mi\u8217'un} she failed to perform against her husband now be exercised against the brother-in-law? {\i Beit Shammai} says no; {\i Beit Hillel} says yes \u8212- {\i be-ba\u8217'al u-ve-yabam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oshaya: Ma\u8217'amar Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites {\i Rabbi Oshaya}\u8217's interpretation: {\i mema\u8217'enet le-ma\u8217'amaro v\u8217'einah mema\u8217'enet le-zikato} \u8212- she may perform {\i mi\u8217'un} in response to his {\i ma\u8217'amar}, but not in response to the {\i zikah}. To understand this, recall what {\i ma\u8217'amar} is: it is an act by the brother-in-law in which he formally gives her {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i harei at mekudeshet li be-taba\u8217'at zo} \u8212- a ring and declaration. From the outside it resembles normal {\i kiddushin}, and the reason it does not constitute full {\i de-oraita} binding {\i kiddushin} is only because she is a {\i yevamah} whose relationship is governed by {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. The rabbis encouraged this step of {\i ma\u8217'amar} before actual {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the scenario {\i Rabbi Oshaya} envisions is this: the husband died, the brother-in-law performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} with this minor girl \u8212- entering into what amounts to a new act of {\i kiddushei ketanah} \u8212- and now she wishes to perform {\i mi\u8217'un}. Her {\i mi\u8217'un} can reverse the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, undoing that new relationship. But it gets her only back to square one: she still has a {\i zikah} to the brother-in-law inherited from her deceased husband, and that {\i zikah} requires {\i chalitzah}. The {\i mi\u8217'un} cannot uproot the {\i zikah} \u8212- {\i einah mema\u8217'enet le-zikato}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} explains the reasoning behind {\i Rabbi Oshaya}\u8217's distinction: {\i dim-da\u8217'atah matzi akra} \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'amar}, like {\i kiddushei ketanah} itself when performed by mother and brothers, requires her willing acceptance. Since she entered into this relationship with her own {\i da\u8217'at}, she is empowered to uproot it through {\i mi\u8217'un}. The {\i zikah}, by contrast, is {\i de\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- it was imposed on her. She did not willingly enter into a relationship with the brother-in-law; the bond arose automatically through the death of her husband and the operation of the laws of {\i yibum}. Since she never willingly entered into it, she cannot unilaterally unilaterally undo it. {\i Lo matzi akra.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bi\u8217'ah Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this reasoning. The brother-in-law can also perform {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i ketanah}, and when he does so, because she is a minor, the {\i bi\u8217'ah} does not create full {\i de-oraita yibum} \u8212- it has the same rabbinic status as a {\i ma\u8217'amar}. Moreover, she may be {\i oker} \u8212- she may undo it through {\i mi\u8217'un}. But {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's reasoning was that {\i mi\u8217'un} applies where she willingly entered the relationship. Yet in the case of {\i bi\u8217'ah}, she did not necessarily do so willingly in the relevant sense. The {\i Gemara} therefore must refine the principle: it is not precisely about her {\i da\u8217'at} in entering the relationship; rather, the distinction is that the {\i ma\u8217'amar} and {\i bi\u8217'as ketanah} represent new acts undertaken by the brother-in-law \u8212- new relationships entered into with her as a {\i ketanah} \u8212- and for those, because they mirror {\i kiddushei ketanah}, the rabbis granted her the power of {\i mi\u8217'un}. The {\i zikah}, by contrast, flowed to her from her original husband \u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ? \u8212- it is not a new relationship initiated with the brother-in-law but something inherited through the first marriage. That inherited bond cannot be severed through {\i mi\u8217'un}, since the proper moment to sever the bond to \u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ? was while \u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ? was alive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ulla\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Allows Zikah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ulla} takes a far more expansive position: {\i mena\u8217'enet af le-zikato} \u8212- she may indeed perform {\i mi\u8217'un} even to sever the {\i zikah}. On {\i Ulla}\u8217's view, the {\i mi\u8217'un} she performs vis-\u224 ?-vis the brother-in-law actually uproots the original marriage \u8212- {\i nisu\u8217'ei kammai ka-akra} \u8212- she is retroactively undoing the first marriage to \u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ?, and therefore she is entirely free, without need of {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarat Ervah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} challenges {\i Ulla} from the {\i baraita}: {\i kol she-yecholah le-ma\u8217'en ve-lo me\u8217'anah, tzarasah choletzet ve-lo mityabbemet} \u8212- any woman who could have performed {\i mi\u8217'un} and did not is a woman whose co-wife ({\i tzarah}) performs {\i chalitzah} with the brother-in-law and not {\i yibum}. The scenario: the deceased husband was also married to a second woman who happens to be the brother-in-law\u8217's daughter \u8212- a case of {\i tzarat ervah}. If the first wife ({\i mema\u8217'enet}) could truly perform {\i mi\u8217'un} and make the entire first marriage disappear, why not instruct her to do so, thereby freeing the brother-in-law to perform {\i yibum} with the co-wife rather than merely {\i chalitzah}? The fact that the {\i halacha} does not permit this implies that {\i mi\u8217'un} cannot fully erase the status that crystallized at the moment of the husband\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rami bar Yechezkel} provides a source that clarifies the underlying principle: {\i mi\u8217'un be-ba\u8217'al muteret le-aviv; bi-yabam asurah le-aviv} \u8212- if a girl performs {\i mi\u8217'un} against her husband (\u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ?), she is permitted to marry his father (\u1497 ?\u1506 ?\u1511 ?\u1489 ?), since the marriage is retroactively dissolved. But if she performs {\i mi\u8217'un} against the brother-in-law (\u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1506 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ?), she remains forbidden to his father (\u1497 ?\u1506 ?\u1511 ?\u1489 ?). What accounts for the difference? In both cases {\i mi\u8217'un} was performed. The answer lies in what transpired at the moment of \u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ?\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Death Instantiates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moment of Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle the {\i Gemara} derives is: {\i bi-sh\u8217'at nefilah nirit ke-khallato} \u8212- at the moment of the husband\u8217's death, she is already viewed as his daughter-in-law in a meaningful sense, because that moment immediately generates demands: something must be done about her relationship with \u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1506 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ?. That demand \u8212- that she must either do {\i yibum}, {\i chalitzah}, or {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- concretizes her status as \u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ?\u8217's wife in a way that cannot be fully undone retroactively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzarat Ervah Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence: {\i hacha nami bi-sh\u8217'at nefilah nirit ke-tzarat bito} \u8212- when the husband died, \u1491 ?\u1489 ?\u1493 ?\u1512 ?\u1492 ?\u8217's status as {\i tzarat ervah} was already instantiated. Even if the first wife later performs {\i mi\u8217'un} and retroactively dissolves the marriage, that act of dissolution does not fully remove the {\i tzarat ervah} status that was created at the moment of death. The rabbinic prohibition on {\i yibum} with the co-wife therefore remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The general principle is therefore this: retroactive dissolution through {\i mi\u8217'un} does not undo the statuses that were created at the moment of the husband\u8217's death. At that moment, before any {\i mi\u8217'un} was performed, the world looked a certain way \u8212- she was his wife, there was a need for the brother-in-law to act, certain prohibitions crystallized. A subsequent {\i mi\u8217'un} can address the ongoing relationship and prevent further obligations, but it cannot reach back and erase what became real at that critical moment. This is why {\i mi\u8217'un} against the {\i yabam} leaves her forbidden to his father: her status as daughter-in-law to \u1497 ?\u1506 ?\u1511 ?\u1489 ? became a reality the moment \u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ? died, and no retroactive act can fully dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: All Disqualified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} rules: {\i mi\u8217'un b\u8217'zeh asurah le-zeh} \u8212- if she performs {\i mi\u8217'un} against one brother (\u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1506 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ?), she is thereby forbidden to the other brothers (\u1500 ?\u1493 ?\u1497 ?, etc.) as well. The {\i mi\u8217'un} operates on the {\i zikah} as a whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav}\u8217's reasoning draws on the analogy of {\i baalas ha-get}: just as if the brother-in-law gives her a {\i get} \u8212- which in the {\i yibum} context dissolves his rabbinic {\i zikah} \u8212- she becomes forbidden to all the brothers, so too {\i mi\u8217'un} against one has the effect of foreclosing the {\i yibum} option with all of them. {\i Lav kivan d\u8217'it\u8217'asrah le-chad, it\u8217'asrah le-kulhu?} \u8212- once she becomes forbidden to one through this mechanism, she becomes forbidden to all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Individual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel} disagrees: {\i mi\u8217'un b\u8217'zeh muteres le-zeh} \u8212- even after performing {\i mi\u8217'un} against \u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1506 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ?, she may still enter into {\i yibum} with \u1500 ?\u1493 ?\u1497 ?. The key distinction {\i Shmuel} draws is the identity of the actor. In the case of {\i baalas ha-get}, it is the man whose act changes her status, and his act can be said to represent or implicate all the brothers. But in {\i mi\u8217'un}, it is she who is acting. And she is not saying something general about all men \u8212- she is saying something specific: {\i lo ravine bech, lo tzevine bech} \u8212- I do not want {\i you}, \u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1506 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ?, specifically. {\i Ha becha u-ve\u8217'acharina} \u8212- as for \u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1506 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ?, I reject him; as for \u1500 ?\u1493 ?\u1497 ?, she may well be interested. The act of {\i mi\u8217'un} is directed at a specific individual, and it changes only her relationship with that individual, not her status in the world at large.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Her Action Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- that in {\i mi\u8217'un} it is {\i hee d\u8217'ka avdah beih}, she is the one acting upon him \u8212- is not merely technical. It reflects a genuine conceptual difference in how {\i mi\u8217'un} functions. Normally, the legal acts that define a woman\u8217's status \u8212- {\i get}, {\i chalitzah}, and so on \u8212- are acts performed upon her or for her. {\i Mi\u8217'un} is unique in that she is the agent, and her agency is directed at a specific person. {\i Shmuel} therefore holds that it affects only that specific person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi: Collective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} goes further: {\i mi\u8217'un b\u8217'zeh muteres afilu lo} \u8212- if she performs {\i mi\u8217'un} against \u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1506 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ? while \u1500 ?\u1493 ?\u1497 ? and other brothers remain, the {\i mi\u8217'un} is entirely meaningless, and she can even subsequently perform {\i yibum} with \u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1506 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ? himself. The reason will emerge from the {\i Gemara}\u8217's final analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 All-or-Nothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i Ravin} returned from Eretz Yisrael, he transmitted in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: {\i mi\u8217'un b\u8217'zeh muteres le-achim} \u8212- if she performs {\i mi\u8217'un} against one brother, she remains permitted to the other brothers. This accords with {\i Shmuel}\u8217's view that {\i mi\u8217'un} is individual and directed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's ID\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} identifies who among the previously cited authorities would disagree with {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: it is {\i Rav}, because {\i Rav} holds that {\i mi\u8217'un b\u8217'zeh asurah le-zeh} \u8212- refused of one, forbidden to all. {\i Rav}\u8217's position that {\i mi\u8217'un} operates wholesale on the relationship to all brothers is directly in conflict with {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's ruling that she remains permitted to the other brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's ID\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} identifies a different dissenter: {\i Rabbi Oshaya}. {\i Rabbi Oshaya} holds that there is no {\i mi\u8217'un} operative against the {\i zikah} at all \u8212- {\i mi\u8217'un} can only address a new {\i ma\u8217'amar} or {\i bi\u8217'as ketanah} entered into by the brother-in-law, not the underlying {\i zikah} inherited from the first husband. {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's ruling, read carefully, implies that {\i mi\u8217'un} against one brother would have been meaningful for the {\i zikah} to that brother while preserving the {\i zikah} to the others \u8212- precisely what {\i Rabbi Oshaya} denies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Indivisible Zikah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi}\u8217's position now becomes clear. The {\i Gemara} articulates the principle: {\i ein mi\u8217'un le-chatzi zikah} \u8212- one cannot sever half a {\i zikah}. The {\i zikah} that the brothers hold is not a collection of independent relationships, each severable on its own. It is a single {\i zikah} inherited from the original husband \u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1493 ?\u1489 ?\u1503 ?, transmitted collectively to all the brothers as a unit. If she wishes to use {\i mi\u8217'un} to sever the {\i zikah}, she must sever the whole of it \u8212- directing her refusal at all brothers collectively. Attempting to sever the {\i zikah} to one brother while leaving it intact as to the others is an attempt at {\i mi\u8217'un le-chatzi zikah}, and that is legally incoherent. The partial {\i mi\u8217'un} accomplishes nothing \u8212- {\i muteres afilu lo}, she can even subsequently perform {\i yibum} with the very brother she attempted to refuse, because the refusal had no legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The full range of positions thus emerges. {\i Rabbi Oshaya} holds that {\i mi\u8217'un} never touches the {\i zikah} at all \u8212- it can only address the new relationship created by the brother-in-law\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'amar} or {\i bi\u8217'as ketanah}. {\i Rav} holds that {\i mi\u8217'un} does operate on the {\i zikah} but, analogous to the {\i get} case, operates wholesale \u8212- refuse one brother and all are foreclosed. {\i Shmuel} holds that {\i mi\u8217'un} operates individually \u8212- she can refuse one brother and remain available to others, because her act is specifically directed. And {\i Rav Ashi}, as the {\i Gemara} finally clarifies, holds that the {\i zikah} is indivisible \u8212- it comes as a single inherited unit from the first husband \u8212- and therefore any {\i mi\u8217'un} must address the whole {\i zikah} or it is void. She must either reject all the brothers collectively or her {\i mi\u8217'un} is meaningless. The {\i Gemara} continues into the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 108\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miun: Beis Shammai vs.\u160 ?B\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dispute Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the first wide line of 107b. {\i Beis Shammai} holds that {\i miun} \u8212- the right of a minor girl married off without her father to refuse the marriage \u8212- is valid only when performed in the husband\u8217's presence and before a {\i beis din}. {\i Beis Hillel}, by contrast, holds that {\i miun} is effective whether or not it is performed in his presence, and whether or not it takes place before a {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Camel Driver Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} records {\i Beis Hillel}\u8217's counter-proof to {\i Beis Shammai}: the case of Pishon the camel driver, whose wife performed {\i miun} in his absence. Given that camel drivers are away for extended periods \u8212- a year at a time \u8212- it would be exceedingly difficult to insist that {\i miun} occur in the husband\u8217's presence. {\i Beis Shammai} responds that Pishon measured with a {\i mida kefusha} \u8212- an overturned or upended measure, an idiomatic expression for dealing improperly \u8212- and therefore was measured back against him in kind. He had mistreated her property, so they treated him in kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proportional Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i mida kefusha}, as Rashi explains, conveys something like the bottom of the barrel \u8212- a reversal, an upending. The specific impropriety, the {\i gemara} clarifies, is {\i mi-deka achal perei}: he consumed the produce of her {\i nichsei milug}, essentially strip-mining her property out of concern that she might perform {\i miun} and walk away. The rabbis responded by making it easier for her to do precisely that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Violations Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate difficulty. If he was eating the {\i peirot}, that implies the marriage had already reached the stage of {\i nisuin}. But {\i Beis Shammai} itself holds that a {\i nisuah} cannot perform {\i miun} \u8212- so there are in fact two violations present: the {\i miun} was performed outside his presence ({\i shelo befanav}), and it was performed after {\i nisuin}. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i trei kuteiri avdu bah} \u8212- they did him two knots, meaning they penalized him on both counts simultaneously, allowing her {\i miun} despite its violating both of {\i Beis Shammai}\u8217's normal conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking passage. {\i Tosafot} notes that the {\i Yerushalmi} asks pointedly: can it really be that simply because someone acts improperly we dissolve his marriage? {\i Tosafot} responds that since {\i kiddushei ketanah} is entirely rabbinic in origin, the rabbis retain the authority to uproot those {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i afkinu rabbanan l\u8217'kiddushei minei}. Yet even {\i Tosafot} acknowledges this is somewhat different from the standard cases of {\i hafka\u8217'at kiddushin}, where the {\i kiddushin} themselves were invalid from the outset. Here, {\i Beis Shammai} is granting special {\i miun} rights on a case-by-case basis in response to his improper conduct \u8212- a genuinely unusual application of rabbinic authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One participant raised an intriguing alternative reading of the {\i befanav} requirement: perhaps his presence is not a formal condition of the {\i miun} act itself \u8212- the way, say, the {\i get} must reach the woman or the {\i kiddushin} money must reach her hand \u8212- but rather a procedural protection of his rights, analogous to how testimony is not given in someone\u8217's absence. On this reading, the {\i befanav} requirement is less about the mechanics of the act and more about safeguarding his interests. This framing, however, would not resolve the separate problem the {\i gemara} raises about {\i nisuin}, though it offers an interesting way of conceptualizing the first requirement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitza & Miun: Three W\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Witness Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} transitions to a {\i mishna} from {\i Sanhedrin}: {\i ha-chalitza veha-miun bi-shlosha} \u8212- both {\i chalitza} and {\i miun} require three. The {\i gemara} asks: who is the author of this {\i mishna}? Its ascription of a three-member panel to {\i miun} reflects the position of {\i Beis Shammai}. Accordingly, Rabbah identifies the {\i mishna} as authored according to {\i Beis Shammai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye disagrees. He maintains that the {\i mishna} can even be attributed to {\i Beis Hillel}. When {\i Beis Hillel} says {\i miun} does not require a {\i beis din}, that means it does not require {\i mumchin} \u8212- ordained, expert judges \u8212- but three witnesses are still needed. A {\i braita} makes this explicit: {\i Beis Shammai} requires a {\i beis din}; {\i Beis Hillel} permits {\i miun} before or without a formal {\i beis din}; yet both houses agree ({\i ve-elu ve-elu modim}) that three are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Witness Minority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this consensus, Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon hold that {\i miun} with only two suffices. And the ruling follows them: Rav Nachman, cited by Yosef bar Minyumi, states that the {\i halacha} is in accordance with that pair \u8212- in contrast to {\i chalitza}, where the view of fewer than three was explicitly rejected.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miun Eligibility Standar\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Miun Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} asks whether a {\i ketana} can perform {\i miun} repeatedly. {\i Beis Hillel} permits it; {\i Beis Shammai} objects that allowing a minor to marry, perform {\i miun}, marry again, and perform {\i miun} again renders {\i benot Yisrael hefker} \u8212- treats Jewish women as disposable. The language of the {\i mishna} on this point is somewhat ambiguous: it speaks of her performing {\i miun}, then waiting until she grows up, or alternatively performing {\i miun} and then marrying immediately. This led to two readings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reaffirmation Upon Matur\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel reads the {\i mishna} as requiring that after performing {\i miun} as a minor, the girl must, upon reaching adulthood, reaffirm her {\i miun} \u8212- she must say {\i rotzah ani be-miyvunin ha-rishonim}, \u8220"I affirm my earlier refusal.\u8221" This is a striking idea with no prior parallel: a kind of confirmation, ensuring that the act of {\i miun} performed during minority is endorsed by the adult woman. The {\i gemara} objects that the {\i mishna}\u8217's language does not express this clearly \u8212- it should have said she \u8220"reaffirms\u8221" rather than she \u8220"does {\i miun}\u8221" \u8212- and acknowledges this as a difficulty ({\i kashya}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla offers an alternative reading: the {\i mishna} is presenting two options ({\i tarte katani}). Either she performs {\i miun} and waits until she becomes an adult before marrying again, or she performs {\i miun} and marries again immediately \u8212- but only on condition that this second marriage includes {\i nisuin} straightaway. Why would {\i Beis Shammai} require immediate {\i nisuin} in the second scenario? Because according to {\i Beis Shammai}, once {\i nisuin} has taken place, {\i miun} is no longer available. So if she wants to remarry as a minor, {\i Beis Shammai} insists that the marriage be consummated immediately with {\i nisuin}, thereby foreclosing the possibility of a second {\i miun}. The \u1493 ? in the {\i mishna} is thus read as \u8220"or,\u8221" presenting two alternatives \u8212- a reading also supported by a {\i braita} that phrases them disjunctively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor\u8217's Consent Requirem\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} asks: which minor must perform {\i miun}? The answer: one whose mother and brothers married her off {\i l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} \u8212- with her knowledge and consent. This is a remarkable requirement. When a father marries off his daughter, her personal awareness and consent are irrelevant \u8212- she could be an infant. Here, by contrast, the entire rabbinic institution is conditioned on her participation. The {\i Rambam}, it should be noted, largely drops the reference to the mother and brothers in his formulation, framing this institution more directly as the girl marrying herself off, with the mother and brothers acting alongside her rather than independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inverse case is likewise stated: if the mother and brothers married her off {\i shelo l\u8217'da\u8217'atah}, without her knowledge, she does not require {\i miun} \u8212- the marriage carries no binding force even at the rabbinic level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Capacity to Protect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Antignos adds a further threshold: any girl who is too young to be able to watch over ({\i lishmor}) her {\i kiddushin} does not require {\i miun}. If she is so young that she would simply drop or lose the token of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- unable to grasp its value or preserve it \u8212- then the {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect even rabbinically. This parallels a similar principle found in the law of {\i get}, where the recipient must have sufficient awareness to protect her {\i get}. The conceptual thrust is striking: the minor is being configured not merely as a passive recipient of the marriage act but as an active participant in it \u8212- almost as an agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's Rejection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer takes a radical position: {\i ein ma\u8217'aseh ketanah klum} \u8212- the acts of a minor are legally void. He does not deny that something occurs when a minor is married off by her mother and brothers with her consent, but he refuses to characterize it as {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan}. Instead, he likens her status to that of a {\i mefutah} \u8212- a seduced woman. The rabbis tolerate the couple living together, but they do not recognize the arrangement as any form of rabbinic marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position has a pointed internal logic. The {\i mishna} has been moving steadily toward treating the minor as the operative agent of her own marriage \u8212- her consent, her receipt of {\i kiddushin}, her capacity to protect it. Rabbi Eliezer accepts that trajectory and draws its conclusion: if she is the operative agent, her act cannot create binding {\i kiddushin}, because a {\i ketanah} has no legal capacity to perform binding acts. Therefore, what exists between them is not quasi-marriage but an unrecognized cohabitation. The practical consequences are total: the husband is not entitled to her earnings ({\i metziatah}), the produce of her property ({\i peirot}), or the annulment of her vows ({\i hafrasat nedarim}); he does not inherit her; and if he is a {\i kohen}, he does not become {\i tamei} for her. She is not his wife in any sense \u8212- except that if she wishes to terminate the arrangement, she requires {\i miun}. As for {\i trumah}: if she is a {\i bat Yisrael} married to a {\i kohen}, she may not eat {\i trumah}; if she is a {\i bat kohen} married to a {\i Yisrael}, she continues to eat {\i trumah}. In neither case is she treated as a married woman.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miun: Documentation (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Reform\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda, and a {\i braita} corroborates this, reports that originally the document recording an act of {\i miun} was written in an elaborate formula: {\i lo ra\u8217'ina beih} \u8212- I do not desire him; {\i v\u8217'lo tzavina beih} \u8212- I do not want him; {\i v\u8217'leis ana beih l\u8217'hitnasva leih} \u8212- I do not wish to marry him. This lengthy formulation was ultimately shortened. The reason: {\i ati l\u8217'chlufi b\u8217'gita} \u8212- scribes would confuse it with a {\i get}. Both Rashi and {\i Tosafot} explain this not as a fear that onlookers would mistake the woman for a {\i gerushah}, but rather that an ignorant {\i sofer}, having been called to write a {\i get miun} in its long form, would later be called to write a {\i get gerushin} and, remembering only the length and formality of the first document, would write the wrong type. This explanation is admittedly puzzling \u8212- a {\i get} is typically a far longer and more formalized document \u8212- but that is how the {\i rishonim} read it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miun: Documentation (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standard Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The shortened standard form retained only the essential: on such-and-such a day, {\i mi\u8217'anah plonit bat ploni} \u8212- so-and-so the daughter of so-and-so performed {\i miun} \u8212- {\i b\u8217'apana}, in our presence. Nothing more.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Valid Miun Contexts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Absence & Informality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} then defines what constitutes an act of {\i miun}. The girl says either {\i ee efshi b\u8217'ploni ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- I do not want so-and-so my husband \u8212- or {\i ee efshi b\u8217'kiddushin she\u8217'kiddushuni imi va\u8217'achai} \u8212- I do not want the marriage that my mother and brothers put me into. Beyond this, Rabbi Yehuda adds a series of expanding cases demonstrating just how informally {\i miun} can be expressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Carriage Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even if she is sitting in the wedding carriage ({\i apiryon}), being conveyed from her father\u8217's house to her husband\u8217's house, and while still in transit she says {\i ee efshi b\u8217'ploni ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- this constitutes {\i miun}. One might have thought: if she truly does not want this marriage, she should stop the procession and leave the carriage. The ruling is that even her statement from within the carriage, without halting the ceremony, is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Serving Guests Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More striking: even if she is already in her husband\u8217's home, guests are gathered, and she is standing and serving them drinks \u8212- and in the midst of doing so she says {\i ee efshi b\u8217'ploni ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- this too is {\i miun}. Rashi identifies the {\i chiddush} here as the absence of any {\i beis din}. But the broader force of the case is that she is, at that moment, visibly performing the role of a wife, yet her verbal declaration suffices to dissolve the marriage. The question of whether she is expressing genuine legal will or merely marital frustration appears to be deliberately sidelined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Storekeeper Complaint\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Further still: if her husband sends her to the storekeeper to collect something on his behalf, and while there she says {\i ee efshi} in reference to her husband \u8212- complaining, as it were, to the shopkeeper about the man who sent her on this errand \u8212- {\i ein lach miun gadol mizeh}: there is no greater {\i miun} than this. It qualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Will-Centered Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely striking series of rulings. The formal requirements appear to dissolve almost entirely: no {\i beis din}, no solemnity, no procedural acknowledgment that a legal act is taking place. The context in each case might easily be read as mere venting or frustration rather than a considered legal declaration. Yet the {\i gemara} insists each qualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One participant raised the question of how this squares with the ruling, agreed upon even by {\i Beis Hillel}, that three witnesses are required. Rashi notes the tension. It may be that Rabbi Yehuda here represents a minority view that no {\i beis din} is required at all \u8212- not merely that {\i mumchin} are unnecessary, but that three are not required either. But part of the {\i chiddush} is conceptual, not merely technical: even where three witnesses happen to be present (guests, bystanders at the store), they are not constituted as a {\i beis din} and the statement is not framed as a legal act. Yet it counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another participant offered an interesting perspective: perhaps these rulings are not about prospective {\i miun} but about retrospective identification. That is, if at a later point one needs to determine whether she had performed {\i miun} during her minority \u8212- say, she is now an adult and her husband refuses to give a {\i get}, and it is in her interest to establish that she had already performed {\i miun} while a {\i ketanah} \u8212- the {\i gemara} is teaching that these earlier statements, made in contexts that seemed informal or incidental, can be identified {\i ex post facto} as valid acts of {\i miun}. This reading would resolve some of the tension, though it is not how the {\i rishonim} generally frame these cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the cases collectively express is the principle embedded in the {\i mishna}\u8217's requirement of {\i l\u8217'da\u8217'atah}: the institution of {\i kiddushei ketanah} without a father was constructed as fundamentally grounded in her will, and therefore its dissolution is also anchored entirely in her will. All of her actions as wife may point in one direction; her words point the other way. The {\i gemara}\u8217's formulation \u8212- {\i ein lach miun gadol mizeh}, there is no greater {\i miun} than this \u8212- is not ironic but precisely literal: the more embedded in the wifely role she appears, the more forceful her verbal rejection becomes as an expression of autonomous will.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miun Via Alternative Act\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records that Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira holds: if a minor who was married without her father\u8217's involvement neither performs a formal {\i miun} but instead stands up and marries another man, her act of {\i nisuin} with the second man itself constitutes her {\i miun} from the first. The {\i mishna} states this regarding {\i nisuin}; a subsequent {\i braita} extends it to {\i kiddushin} as well: if she accepts {\i kiddushin} from a second man, that too is her {\i miun} from the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin vs.\u160 ?Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then poses a multi-layered question ({\i ibaya lehu}): Do the {\i rabbanan} dispute Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira at all? If they do, is their dispute limited to the {\i kiddushin} case, or does it extend even to {\i nisuin}? And if there is a dispute, do we rule like Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira \u8212- and if so, in which cases?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin as Miun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution comes from Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira}. The word {\i halacha} itself implies that the {\i rabbanan} disagree \u8212- for one rules {\i halacha} against an opposing position. Both Rashi and {\i Tosafot} infer from context that this ruling covers both the {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin} scenarios: we rule like Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira in both directions, meaning that even mere {\i kiddushin} from a second man constitutes {\i miun} from the first, and we assume the {\i rabbanan} contest this in both cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question then arises: does it matter what the nature of her relationship with the first man was? Is the ruling that {\i kiddushin} from guy number two constitutes {\i miun} from guy number one applicable even where she had already reached {\i nisuin} with guy number one \u8212- a far more actualized form of marriage? The {\i gemara} seeks to resolve this from a specific case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avdin\u8217's Daughters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daughters-in-law of Avdin \u8212- the same Avdin, as one participant noted, who appears earlier in the {\i daf} in connection with the humiliation of Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yosi \u8212- had married Avdin\u8217's sons while minors, without paternal authorization. Both performed {\i miun} against their husbands. Rabbi sent a pair of {\i rabbanan} to investigate \u8212- likely either because he did not trust the report or because he wanted to assess whether the statements were genuine expressions of will or merely complaints.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the two {\i rabbanan} approached, some women at the scene told the daughters-in-law that their husbands were coming. The girls, apparently confusing the approaching {\i rabbanan} for their husbands, responded: {\i le-havu gavreichu didchu lanan lav gavreinu} \u8212- let them be your husbands; we have no need of them. Rabbi ruled: {\i ein lecha miun gadol mi-zeh} \u8212- there is no greater {\i miun} than this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to use this case to resolve the earlier question: if we can identify these girls as having performed {\i miun} at the level of a mere verbal rejection addressed to bystanders, and if this occurred after {\i nisuin} with the first husband, it would demonstrate that even {\i nisuin} with guy number one does not require a more elevated form of {\i miun}. The {\i gemara} ultimately declines the proof, suggesting the marriages may have been at the stage of {\i kiddushin} only. The ruling, however, stands: {\i ve-halacha ke-Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira ve-afilu be-nisuin de-kama} \u8212- we rule like Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira, and even if the relationship with the first man had reached {\i nisuin}, the standard for {\i miun} remains accessible and low. The {\i gemara} makes it very easy for a {\i ketanah} to exit this institution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consistency Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. Shmuel, as transmitted by Rav Yehuda, articulates a pointed observation: {\i chozani kol tzaddei chachamim v\u8217'lo matzati adam she\u8217'hishveh midotav be-ketanah ke-Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- I have examined all the angles of the sages\u8217' positions and found no one as internally consistent in his treatment of the {\i ketanah} as Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consistency problem Rabbi Eliezer identified is this: the institution of {\i kiddushei ketanah} without a father has been progressively reconfigured so that the minor herself is the operative agent. Her consent, her receipt of {\i kiddushin}, her capacity to protect it \u8212- these are the conditions. If that is the case, then her act cannot create rabbinic {\i kiddushin}, because a {\i ketanah}\u8217's legal acts are void. The institution therefore cannot be {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan}. Instead, Rabbi Eliezer characterizes the relationship as {\i k\u8217'mefutah}: the rabbis tolerate cohabitation without recognizing it as marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Wife Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then draws out the full range of legal consequences. The husband is not entitled to her earnings ({\i metziatah u-ma\u8217'aseh yadeha}), cannot annul her vows ({\i hafrasat nedarim}), does not inherit her ({\i eino yorshah}), and \u8212- if he is a {\i kohen} \u8212- does not become {\i tamei} for her ({\i eino mitamei lah}). The governing principle: {\i eino k\u8217'ishto le-chol davar} \u8212- she is not his wife in any regard. The one exception: {\i ella she-tzricha mi\u8217'un} \u8212- if she wishes to terminate the cohabitation, she still requires {\i miun}. This is described as being like someone taking a walk in a garden together, sharing a bed, with her thereafter going to the {\i mikvah} and eating {\i trumah} (if she is a {\i bat kohen}) \u8212- no marital status attaches, only a cohabitation that can be ended by a simple statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua takes the diametrically opposite view. The husband is entitled to everything: her finds, her earnings, the right to annul her vows, inheritance rights, and if he is a {\i kohen}, he becomes {\i tamei} for her \u8212- the {\i gemara} earlier explained this is treated rabinnically like a {\i met mitzvah}, despite there being no {\i d\u8217'oraisa} basis. She is {\i k\u8217'ishto le-chol davar} \u8212- fully his wife in every respect. The one anomaly: {\i yotzah be-miun} \u8212- she can exit the marriage through {\i miun} alone, without requiring a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consistency Evaluation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi \u8212- referring here to Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi \u8212- evaluates the two positions: {\i nirein divrei Rabbi Eliezer mi-divrei Rabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position is more persuasive. The reason is precisely the consistency that Shmuel praised. Rabbi Eliezer {\i hishveh midotav be-ketanah}: he applied a uniform standard. If she is not a wife, she needs no {\i get} \u8212- and that is what he holds. Rabbi Yehoshua {\i chillek} \u8212- he made a distinction \u8212- treating her as a full wife in every respect yet exempting her from the requirement of a {\i get}. If she is truly his wife, {\i tibaye get} \u8212- she should need a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then probes Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's own consistency. If she is not his wife at all, why does she require {\i miun}? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ella b\u8217'kadi teipuk} \u8212- should she simply walk out with nothing? Even a cohabiting couple that decides to separate requires some formal acknowledgment of the breakup. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i miun}, on this reading, is not the severing of {\i kiddushin} but the formal acknowledgment that a live-in relationship has ended. It is not {\i aishut} at all \u8212- not rabbinic marriage \u8212- merely a relationship that must be terminated by some act. This, the {\i gemara} suggests, is an internally consistent position. And it also explains why {\i miun} is so easy to perform: if there is no real marriage, the act dissolving it need not be a formal legal instrument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical {\i halacha} does not follow Rabbi Eliezer. We rule that {\i kiddushei ketanah} without a father does create a form of rabbinic {\i kiddushin} \u8212- a pseudo-{\i kiddushin}, as it were \u8212- from which {\i miun} is the mechanism of exit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miun vs.\u160 ?Get Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refusal Signals Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's statement in the {\i mishna} \u8212- that if she holds back because of the man it is as though she is his wife, and if not, it is not \u8212- is cryptic. {\i Tosafot} offers a reading that places this not in the context of {\i miun} at all but rather in the context of the {\i mishna}\u8217's earlier condition: {\i l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} \u8212- that the {\i kiddushin} must have been performed with her knowledge and consent. How do we determine after the fact whether she consented?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: if a second suitor approaches her and proposes, and she refuses him saying {\i machmat ploni ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- \u8220"I cannot marry you, I am already married to so-and-so\u8221" \u8212- that refusal signals that she regards herself as married to guy number one. She is being {\i held back} because of him ({\i akavta de-minei de-ish}). This demonstrates that she consented to the original marriage. Conversely, if she refuses the second suitor by saying {\i machmat b\u8217'nei adam she-einam mehuganim li} \u8212- \u8220"I would consider marrying someone suitable, but not you\u8221" \u8212- this indicates she does not regard herself as bound by the first marriage, and therefore she had never truly consented to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reading, Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's statement is about identifying consent to {\i kiddushin} number one, not about the mechanics of {\i miun}. What she says to a potential second husband is a retrospective signal of her attitude toward the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consent Indicator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye and Rav Chanina bar Avin both offer a different reading: {\i natan lah get} \u8212- if guy number one gave her a {\i get} \u8212- {\i zu hi akavta de-minei de-ish}: that shows he was holding her back. Conversely, if she performed {\i miun}, {\i zu hi akavta d\u8217'lav minei de-ish} \u8212- it was she who was determining the outcome, not him. The practical implication of this reading follows immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miun After Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he gave her a {\i get} and then remarried her and she subsequently performed {\i miun}, the {\i miun} redefines her status retroactively. Even though she had been his {\i gerushah} after the first {\i get}, the {\i miun} following the second marriage erases that status \u8212- she is treated as though she was never his {\i gerushah} at all. Therefore, even after she marries a second man and that marriage dissolves, she may return to guy number one. There is no prohibition of {\i machzir gerushato}, because the {\i miun} has effectively rendered the {\i gerushin} status null.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does this work conceptually? Rashi\u8217's explanation is that the {\i miun} \u8212- by its nature \u8212- underlines that she is a {\i ketanah} and this entire institution is rabbinic. Once she performs {\i miun} the second time, it retroactively recharacterizes the entire relationship: she was never truly married to him, not even rabbinically. The first {\i get} disappears along with the marriage it purported to dissolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that on a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level, none of this creates any real prohibition in any case, since there was never a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} marriage to begin with. The concerns about {\i machzir gerushato} and about {\i gerushin} status for {\i kehunah} are entirely {\i mi-derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get After Miun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The converse: if the sequence is reversed \u8212- she performed {\i miun} first, then he remarried her, and then he gave her a {\i get} \u8212- her most recent status with him is that of a {\i gerushah}. The {\i miun} is now in the past; the {\i get} is the more recent and defining act. She is forbidden to return to him after marrying another man, because she is his {\i gerushah} and the prohibition of {\i machzir gerushato} applies. The rule is stated concisely: {\i get achar miun} \u8212- {\i asura lachzor lo}. {\i Miun achar get} \u8212- {\i muteret lachzor lo}. Whichever act came last defines her status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Partners Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Partner Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then presents a more complex scenario involving three successive husbands. She performs {\i miun} with guy number one; marries guy number two and receives a {\i get}; marries guy number three and performs {\i miun} with him. After all of this, can she return to any of them? The {\i gemara} works through the implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same-Partner Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} at first seems to assume a contradiction between the cases: in the first scenario, {\i miun} following {\i get} with the same man redefines her as a non-{\i gerushah}; in the three-partner scenario, {\i miun} with guy number three does not redefine her status with guy number two. The {\i gemara} initially suggests ({\i amru}) that the two halves of the {\i mishna} reflect a genuine dispute \u8212- {\i mi she-shanah zo lo shanah zo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava dissolves the apparent contradiction: there is no dispute at all. {\i Miun dide mevattel get dide} \u8212- his own {\i miun} can retroactively undermine his own earlier {\i get} \u8212- because the {\i miun} redefines the entire relationship with that specific person. {\i Miun de-chavrei lo mevattel git dide} \u8212- a different man\u8217's {\i miun} cannot undermine a previous man\u8217's {\i get} \u8212- because the relationships are distinct. The {\i miun} with guy number three says nothing about the nature of her relationship with guy number two. This is the obvious answer, and Rava offers it as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leverage Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i gemara} presses further. In principle, the {\i gemara} argues, any act of {\i miun} \u8212- even with a different man \u8212- announces that she is a {\i ketanah} and therefore her entire history of rabbinic marriage is questionable. On pure principle, even another man\u8217's {\i miun} could redefine an earlier {\i get}. So there must be a positive reason why we do not allow it. The {\i gemara} provides one: {\i eidi d\u8217'makaras bi-remi\u8217'zosav u\u8217've-kitzotzav azil meshabesh u-meisei lah} \u8212- because she has been intimate with guy number one and knows his manner and his signals, he can go and manipulate her, inducing her to perform {\i miun} with guy number two or three, which would then free her to return to him. Allowing a different man\u8217's {\i miun} to override her {\i get} from guy number one would give guy number one leverage to destroy her subsequent marriages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Failed Attempt Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a parallel question: why is the same concern not present in the case where the same man\u8217's {\i miun} is allowed to override his earlier {\i get}? If she performed {\i miun} from him the second time around and we let her return to him after her subsequent marriage dissolves \u8212- has he not also been given the leverage to signal her and bring her back?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer: {\i hacha tiv sheiv velo meshabesh} \u8212- in this case, he already tried and it did not work. The {\i miun} the second time around was {\i her} act of rejection. She wanted out, and he could not keep her in the marriage. Given that history, we are not concerned that he will subsequently be able to manipulate her into returning, because she has already demonstrated that she is not under his influence. By contrast, when he was the one to give the {\i get} \u8212- perhaps she wished to remain married but he rejected her \u8212- and she then marries someone else, he may retain emotional leverage over her and use it to encourage her to perform {\i miun} and come back to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution operates on the level of practical social concern \u8212- identifying where leverage exists and where it has been negated by prior events. The more formalist explanation would simply observe that a {\i miun} redefines only the specific relationship in which it occurs. But the {\i gemara} prefers to ground the rule in the concrete reality of power dynamics between the parties, and to explain both the rule and its limits in those terms.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 109\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'un After Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 108b, continuing the discussion of {\i me\u8217'un} following a {\i get}. The broader question is whether a {\i me\u8217'un} performed after a {\i get} redefines the woman\u8217's status \u8212- specifically, whether it undoes her classification as a {\i gerushah} and thereby permits what would otherwise be a violation of {\i machzir grushato}. The {\i mishnah} has already established that if the same man who gave the {\i get} subsequently remarried her and she then performed {\i me\u8217'un} against him, that {\i me\u8217'un} does redefine her status. But if a different man married her and she performed {\i me\u8217'un} against him, that does not redefine her status vis-\u224 ?-vis the first husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} had explained this distinction not on the intuitive ground that only the same man can redefine her status, but rather on a concern that the first husband will attempt to signal her \u8212- exploiting her recognition of his gestures \u8212- and thereby manipulate her into performing {\i me\u8217'un} with the second husband so she can return to him. With that resolved, the {\i gemara} moves to a new internal difficulty in the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes: {\i ika hashta dechavrei adechavrei kashya} \u8212- even setting aside the first problem, there remains a contradiction within the {\i mishnah}\u8217's own treatment of a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un}. The {\i mishnah} first describes a case in which the man performed {\i me\u8217'un} against her, then remarried her, then gave her a {\i get}, and she subsequently married someone else. The {\i mishnah} states that if that second man died or divorced her, she is forbidden from returning to the first husband on account of {\i machzir grushato}. The precise formulation \u8212- specifying death or divorce \u8212- implies that had the second man\u8217's dissolution of the marriage taken the form of {\i me\u8217'un} rather than death or divorce, she {\i would} be permitted to return to the first. This {\i diyuk} suggests that a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} does, in fact, redefine her status even vis-\u224 ?-vis the first husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the concluding rule of the {\i mishnah} states explicitly: {\i kol sheyatzat mimeno beget asura lachazor lo} \u8212- anyone from whom she departed with a {\i get} may not take her back, even if she subsequently performed {\i me\u8217'un} with another husband. And anyone from whom she departed with {\i me\u8217'un} may return to her. This is as clear a statement as possible that a {\i me\u8217'un} performed with a different man does {\i not} undo the {\i gerushah} status vis-\u224 ?-vis the first. So the two halves of the {\i mishnah} appear to contradict one another directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar\u8217's response is stark: {\i miy sheshana zu lo shana zu} \u8212- the two halves of the {\i mishnah} were authored by different {\i tanna\u8217'im} and reflect a genuine dispute. The first half operates under the assumption that a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} can redefine her status even vis-\u224 ?-vis the first husband; the second half denies this. Two distinct positions, two {\i tanna\u8217'im}, and no resolution between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing here to appreciate just how seriously the {\i gemara} entertains the position that a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} should work. The intuitive assumption \u8212- that only the same man can redefine the status, not an entirely different party \u8212- is not the {\i gemara}\u8217's operating premise. The {\i gemara} is genuinely prepared to accept that even a {\i me\u8217'un} performed with a second husband could, in principle, strip away the {\i gerushah} status the first husband created. The dispute is not about whether this is theoretically possible, but about whether we permit it in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One must also keep in mind throughout this entire discussion that everything here exists in the {\i derabbanan} world: the {\i kiddushin} is {\i derabbanan}, the {\i get} is {\i derabbanan}, and the {\i me\u8217'un} operates within that framework. It is precisely because all of this is rabbinic that the question of redefining statuses is even coherent. Were we dealing with {\i de\u8217'oraisa} marriage and divorce, there would be no mechanism for retroactive redefinition of this kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla offers an alternative resolution that avoids splitting the {\i mishnah} between two disputants. He argues that both halves can reflect the same {\i tanna} \u8212- but the final case of the {\i mishnah}, in which a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} does not redefine her status, is a specific scenario involving {\i shilsha begetin}: she had gone through a sequence of marriages and received three {\i getin} over the course of her history. After three {\i getin}, even if subsequent husbands performed {\i me\u8217'un}, the cumulative pattern of divorce documents has established her {\i gerushah} status so firmly \u8212- at least vis-\u224 ?-vis the husbands from whom she received those {\i getin} \u8212- that it cannot be overridden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic, as Ulla explains, is that {\i demichzi kegdola}: after three {\i getin}, she looks like an adult. Despite remaining a {\i ketanah} throughout, the repeated formal divorces create the appearance of full adult status, and that appearance fixes her {\i gerushah} identity at least with respect to those specific husbands. As a result, in this particular case, no subsequent {\i me\u8217'un} \u8212- even from a different man \u8212- can dislodge the {\i gerushah} classification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking resolution. The ordinary rule, according to Ulla, is that a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} can indeed redefine her status vis-\u224 ?-vis the first husband. The final case of the {\i mishnah} is a narrow exception: when there have been three {\i getin}, the accumulated weight of formal divorce creates an irreversible {\i gerushah} status. She may still return to husbands from whom she departed through {\i me\u8217'un}, but she cannot return to those from whom she received {\i getin}, because their {\i gerushah} status has become fixed and is no longer amenable to redefinition through a later {\i me\u8217'un}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The counterintuitive element here is worth highlighting. One would expect the rule to be that another man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} never works to redefine her status with the first husband \u8212- that seems self-evidently correct. Instead, the {\i gemara} reasons in the opposite direction, treating a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} as ordinarily effective, with the three-{\i getin} scenario as the exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: according to the first answer, which treats the two halves of the {\i mishnah} as a {\i machlokes tanna\u8217'im}, where do we find this debate represented among the {\i tanna\u8217'im}? The answer comes through Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav, introduced with a striking verse from {\i Eicha}: {\i meimenu bechesef shatinu ateinu bimchir yavo\u8217'u} \u8212- our water we had to purchase with silver, our wood we had to buy. The {\i gemara} applies this as a metaphor: just as water represents {\i Torah} and had to be acquired at great cost, so during the period of Hadrianic persecution, the following halachic question had to be pursued at enormous expense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question posed was: if a woman departed from the first husband with a {\i get} and from the second husband through {\i me\u8217'un}, may she return to the first? Scholars hired an intermediary for 400 {\i zuz} to smuggle the question into prison to Rabbi Akiva. He ruled: {\i ve\u8217'asar} \u8212- forbidden. A second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} does not redefine her status. They then traveled to ask Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira in Nitzivin, who similarly ruled {\i ve\u8217'asar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael bar Rabbi Yose, however, takes sharp issue with this account. He insists the question itself was obvious \u8212- the answer could not possibly be in doubt \u8212- and that the actual question brought to Rabbi Akiva in prison was an entirely different one. His reasoning: {\i issur kares hitarta} \u8212- if {\i me\u8217'un} has the power to permit what would otherwise be an {\i issur kares} \u8212- since a {\i ketanah} who is betrothed is considered halachically married and {\i me\u8217'un} dissolves that betrothal, allowing her to go to another man who would otherwise be having relations with a married woman \u8212- then certainly {\i me\u8217'un} should be powerful enough to remove the much lesser prohibition of {\i machzir grushato}, which is merely an {\i issur lav}. An {\i a fortiori} argument: if {\i me\u8217'un} can eliminate a {\i karet} prohibition, it certainly eliminates a {\i lav} prohibition. Therefore, Rabbi Yishmael bar Rabbi Yose maintains that a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} obviously does redefine her status and permits her return to the first husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's objection is noted: the {\i me\u8217'un} dissolves the relationship with the man she is performing {\i me\u8217'un} against, not vis-\u224 ?-vis a different man. There is a meaningful distinction between redefining her status with respect to husband number two versus redefining her {\i gerushah} status with respect to husband number one. Nevertheless, Rabbi Yishmael bar Rabbi Yose maintains that the power of {\i me\u8217'un} is broad enough to accomplish this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The actual question, Rabbi Yishmael bar Rabbi Yose insists, involved a far more elaborate scenario: where a man\u8217's brother\u8217's wife \u8212- who was a {\i ketanah} and therefore an {\i issur derabbanan} to him as {\i eshet achi imo} \u8212- had been married by a different paternal half-brother (who was not related to her on the maternal side and could therefore marry her without violation), and that half-brother died. Could she perform {\i me\u8217'un} retroactively on her first marriage \u8212- even though the first husband had already died \u8212- in order to undo the prohibited relationship and enable {\i yibum} with the surviving brother? This would constitute {\i me\u8217'un le\u8217'achar mita bimkom mitzvah}: a posthumous {\i me\u8217'un} aimed at enabling the fulfillment of {\i yibum}. The {\i gemara} concludes that even in Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's framework, the answer here is {\i assur} \u8212- a posthumous {\i me\u8217'un} stretching back to an already-dissolved prior marriage goes too far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divorce & Remarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'un After Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question arises in connection with retroactive {\i me\u8217'un} after death: specifically, whether {\i me\u8217'un} may be performed posthumously {\i bimkom mitzvah} \u8212- where performing it would enable {\i yibum} that would otherwise be impossible. Two scholars were dispatched at great cost to bring this question to Rabbi Akiva in prison, and both he and Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira ruled it forbidden. The case involved the {\i zikah} relationship and whether {\i me\u8217'un} can operate retroactively once the first husband has already died, effectively reaching back in time to unwind a prior marriage that no longer exists in any tangible form. The conclusion here is that {\i me\u8217'un} cannot function in that posthumous mode, even for the worthy purpose of enabling {\i de\u8217'oraisa yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'un After Get (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the main discussion, Rav Yitzchak bar Ashian makes an important practical observation: {\i u\u8217'modeh Rav shemuteret le\u8217'achiv shel zeh sheniesra alav} \u8212- even Rav, who rules that the second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} does not allow her to return to the first husband, would agree that the second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} does permit her to marry the first husband\u8217's {\i brother}. This is a remarkable concession that reveals how the {\i gemara} conceptualizes what is happening when we forbid her return to the first husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibition on returning to the first husband is not because the {\i me\u8217'un} failed to redefine her status as a non-{\i gerushah}. In principle, the {\i me\u8217'un} does work. The concern is specifically that the first husband, who is familiar with her personal signals \u8212- {\i d\u8217'makir be\u8217'mizatah u\u8217'v\u8217'kritzatah} \u8212- will manipulate her into performing {\i me\u8217'un} with the second husband in order to free her to return to him. We therefore forbid the return to the first husband as a precautionary measure. But since we genuinely view her as having had her {\i gerushah} status redefined \u8212- since the {\i me\u8217'un} is considered effective in principle \u8212- she is not a {\i gerushas achiv} at all, and the first husband\u8217's brother may marry her freely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a counterpoint: one might argue that since we have already forbidden her to the first husband, we should extend that prohibition by {\i gezeirah} to the first husband\u8217's brothers as well. Rav Yosef bar Ashian adopts exactly this position \u8212- {\i keshem she\u8217'asura lo kach asura le\u8217'achiv} \u8212- reasoning that once we have forbidden her to the first husband, a {\i gezeirah} should cover his brothers as well, lest they enable or facilitate the first husband\u8217's improper conduct. The first opinion disagrees: the concern is specific to the first husband himself, who knows her signals. There is no basis for extending the rabbinic fence to cover people who present no such risk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication is striking and worth stating explicitly: the {\i gemara} is operating on the assumption that a second man\u8217's {\i me\u8217'un} is genuinely effective as a matter of principle. The entire edifice of what the {\i gemara} forbids or permits flows not from doubts about the {\i me\u8217'un}\u8217's power but from precautionary concerns about the first husband\u8217's potential misconduct. And notably, the {\i gemara} leaves unexplored the question of whether she could thereby marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- since that too would seem to be answered in the affirmative if her {\i gerushah} status has genuinely been erased.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divorce & Remarriage (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simple Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces a new topic: {\i hamigaresh et ha\u8217'isha v\u8217'yachzirah} \u8212- a man who divorces his wife and then takes her back, without an intervening marriage to anyone else. The question for our purposes is whether her status as {\i grushas achiv} \u8212- the brother\u8217's divorcee \u8212- carries forward and prevents {\i yibum} when the husband subsequently dies, or whether the remarriage redefines her status so that she is once again simply {\i eshet achiv hamet}, the brother\u8217's wife, triggering the ordinary {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question has real conceptual depth: when she received the {\i get}, she became {\i grushas achiv} vis-\u224 ?-vis the surviving brother. When the first husband then remarried her, did that second marriage erase the earlier status? Or does the {\i grushas achiv} designation persist latently, re-emerging with full force once the husband dies?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The plain ruling of the {\i mishnah} is {\i muteres leyavam} \u8212- she is permitted to {\i yibum}. The remarriage redefines her status and she is no longer {\i grushas achiv}. But Rabbi Eliezer disagrees and rules she is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult Remarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents a parallel case involving a {\i yesomah} \u8212- a minor whose father has died, such that her original marriage was {\i kiddushin de\u8217'rabbanan}. He divorced her and then remarried her. Here too the {\i mishnah} rules {\i muteres leyavam}, and here too Rabbi Eliezer forbids {\i yibum}. At first glance this case seems identical in structure to the first, raising the question of why it is stated separately. The answer emerges only when we reach the third case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case illuminates everything: {\i ketanah she\u8217'hisi\u8217'ah aviha} \u8212- a minor who was married off by her father, making the original marriage {\i kiddushin de\u8217'oraisa}. He then divorced her, producing a {\i gerushin} of full {\i de\u8217'oraisa} force. After the divorce, she becomes {\i k\u8217'yesomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av} \u8212- like an orphan while the father is alive. She has left the father\u8217's household (since she was married and then returned), is no longer under her husband\u8217's control (since he divorced her), and is in a kind of emancipated status. If the same man then remarries her, this second marriage is only {\i kiddushin de\u8217'rabbanan}, because the father can no longer marry her off {\i de\u8217'oraisa} in this state. The {\i gemara} then rules {\i divrei hakol asurah leyavam} \u8212- everyone agrees that she is forbidden to {\i yibum}, because the {\i gerushin} was a full {\i de\u8217'oraisa} divorce, creating a genuine {\i grushas achiv de\u8217'oraisa} status, while the remarriage was only {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} and therefore cannot undo it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This third case is the key to understanding Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position throughout. The reason he forbids {\i yibum} even in the first two cases \u8212- the adult who remarried as an adult, and the {\i yesomah} who was at {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} level throughout \u8212- is to protect against confusion with this third case, where everyone agrees she is truly {\i grushas achiv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An interesting question arises from the structure of case three: if the father loses his {\i de\u8217'oraisa} authority to marry her off after the first marriage dissolves, who performs the second {\i kiddushin}? The {\i Rambam} does not mention the mother or brothers in this context, which might suggest she effectuates the {\i kiddushin} herself, or alternatively that the father retains {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} authority analogous to what the mother and brothers possess \u8212- but this remains an open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer\u8217's Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} (through Eifa) initially suggests Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's rationale is {\i ho\u8217'il ve\u8217'amdah lo sha\u8217'ah achas be\u8217'issura} \u8212- since she stood in a forbidden relationship for at least one moment (when she was {\i grushas achiv} following the {\i get}), that status persists even after remarriage. The rabbis challenge this: if so, she should not even require {\i chalitzah}, since she would simply be a full {\i grushas achiv de\u8217'oraisa}. But a {\i braisa} records that Rabbi Eliezer does require {\i chalitzah}, which demonstrates that he does not treat her as straightforwardly {\i grushas achiv de\u8217'oraisa}. He must be operating with some form of rabbinic prohibition or doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Eifa then concedes: {\i Rabbi Eliezer lo yadan mai ta\u8217'amei} \u8212- I do not know Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye provides the answer: {\i misafka lei ee misah mapil ee nisuin rishonim mapilin} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer is in doubt about what determines a woman\u8217's status for purposes of {\i yibum}. Is it the moment of the husband\u8217's death that triggers the assessment of her status? Or is it the moment of the original marriage \u8212- the {\i nisuin rishonim} \u8212- that determines all relevant classifications? If one assesses from the moment of the first marriage, then she was already {\i grushas achiv} at the time of the {\i get} and that status, once created, governs. If one assesses from the moment of death, then the relevant marriage is the second one, and she should be eligible for {\i yibum}. Since this is unresolved, Rabbi Eliezer requires {\i chalitzah} as a hedge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a different explanation: Rabbi Eliezer actually holds clearly that {\i misah mapil} \u8212- it is the death that triggers the assessment. In principle, then, she should be eligible for {\i yibum}. But a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} concern prevents it: {\i hakol beki\u8217'in be\u8217'geirushin ve\u8217'ein hakol beki\u8217'in be\u8217'hazara} \u8212- everyone knows about a divorce (since the community witnessed the {\i get}), but not everyone knows about the remarriage (which may have been private). If the brother performs {\i yibum} with her, onlookers who know about the divorce but not the remarriage will think he is performing {\i yibum} with his brother\u8217's ex-wife, which is ordinarily forbidden. To forestall this misimpression, Rabbi Eliezer requires {\i chalitzah} but not {\i yibum}. The {\i gemara} challenges this on the grounds that remarriage, since they live together, tends to be publicly known \u8212- but responds that one could imagine scenarios where he remarried her at night and died the next morning, meaning the community never learned of the reconciliation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father-Married Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers a third explanation that he argues is the most straightforward reading of the {\i mishnah}: {\i de\u8217'gazar hani mishum yesomah b\u8217'chayei aviha ve\u8217'hichzirah} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's prohibition in the first two cases is a {\i gezeirah} enacted to protect against confusion with the third case. The third case \u8212- father-married minor, full {\i de\u8217'oraisa} divorce, {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} remarriage \u8212- is a genuine, unanimous {\i grushas achiv} situation. To prevent people from mistakenly permitting {\i yibum} in that case by confusing it with the first two, Rabbi Eliezer extends the prohibition to all cases where the pattern of divorce-followed-by-remarriage exists, even when both stages were {\i de\u8217'oraisa} or both were {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is confirmed by a {\i braisa}: {\i modim chachamim le\u8217'Rabbi Elazar be\u8217'ketanah she\u8217'hisi\u8217'ah aviha ve\u8217'nisgarsha harei hi k\u8217'yesomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} agree with Rabbi Eliezer that in the third case she is genuinely {\i grushas achiv}, since {\i geirusheiha geirushin gemurin} \u8212- her divorce was a complete {\i de\u8217'oraisa} divorce \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'ein chazarasah chazara gemura} \u8212- and her remarriage was not a full {\i de\u8217'oraisa} remarriage. The {\i gemara} notes that the very inclusion of this agreed-upon third case in the {\i mishnah} serves precisely to frame Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's positions in the first two cases as a {\i gezeirah} extending from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} further clarifies: {\i bamei devarim amurim} \u8212- when does everyone agree she remains {\i grushas achiv}? When he divorced her as a minor and remarried her as a minor, so the remarriage was only {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}. But if he divorced her as a minor and remarried her as an adult ({\i ve\u8217'hichzirah k\u8217'she-hi gedolah}), or remarried her as a minor but she reached adulthood while still married to him ({\i gadla etzlo}), then the second marriage attains {\i de\u8217'oraisa} status \u8212- presumably because marital relations after she becomes an adult constitute {\i bi\u8217'as kiddushin} \u8212- and {\i o choletzet o misyabemes}, she may either perform {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}. Rabbi Eliezer, however, in his {\i gezeirah}, still requires {\i chalitzah} even in those latter cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then addresses a follow-up question from Rabbah to Rav Nachman: {\i tzarasah mahu} \u8212- what about the co-wife? If Rabbi Eliezer forbids {\i yibum} with this woman, can the brother perform {\i yibum} with the co-wife? If Rabbi Eliezer treats her as a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan erva}, then she would be a {\i tzaras erva} and the co-wife could not perform {\i yibum} either. But if Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's prohibition is purely a {\i gezeirah} \u8212- not a genuine {\i ervah} classification \u8212- then the co-wife remains eligible for {\i yibum}. Rav Nachman\u8217's initial instinct: the whole thing is a {\i gezeirah}, and one does not make a {\i gezeirah} atop a {\i gezeirah}, so the co-wife may perform {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} appears to contradict this, stating in the name of Rabbi Eliezer that both she {\i and} her co-wife perform {\i chalitzah}. The {\i gemara} resolves this by reading the conjunctive {\i vav} as disjunctive \u8212- meaning one or the other performs {\i chalitzah} \u8212- and then further revises: the woman herself performs {\i chalitzah}, while the co-wife may perform either {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}. This confirms that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's prohibition, while real, does not generate full {\i tzaras erva} status, and the co-wife retains access to {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'un & Yibum Conflict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces a new scenario that brings {\i me\u8217'un} and {\i yibum} into direct conflict: {\i shnei achim nesuim lishtei achayos ketanos} \u8212- two brothers married to two minor sisters. One of the brothers dies. Since both marriages are {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} (the {\i gemara} assumes the fathers are deceased, making these {\i yesomot ketanot}), all the relevant obligations \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} and the prohibition of {\i achos ishah} \u8212- exist at the same {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} level and therefore cancel one another out. She simply walks away without {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}, since there is no {\i de\u8217'oraisa} obligation that would require a formal release.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simple Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then works through a series of graduated cases. If one of the women is an adult ({\i gedolah}) and the other is a minor ({\i ketanah}), the outcome depends on which husband dies. If the {\i ketanah}\u8217's husband dies, the surviving brother has a {\i yibum de\u8217'rabbanan} obligation toward her, but she is his wife\u8217's sister at the {\i de\u8217'oraisa} level \u8212- {\i achos ishah de\u8217'oraisa}. The {\i de\u8217'oraisa erva} overrides the {\i de\u8217'rabbanan yibum}: she walks away. The {\i mishnah} states plainly: {\i teitzei ketanah mishum achos ishah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Dilemma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The genuinely difficult case arises when the {\i gedolah}\u8217's husband dies. Now the surviving brother has a {\i de\u8217'oraisa} obligation of {\i yibum} \u8212- the deceased was fully and biblically married to a {\i gedolah} \u8212- but he is {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} married to the sister, creating a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} prohibition of {\i achos ishah}. He cannot simply perform {\i yibum}, because he would then be perpetually violating a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan achos ishah} prohibition. He cannot simply perform {\i chalitzah} and remain married to his wife, because then she becomes {\i achos chalutzaso}. He cannot divorce his wife and perform {\i yibum}, because then she becomes {\i achos grushaso}. Every path creates a new forbidden relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem cannot be resolved by invoking {\i be\u8217'makom mitzvah lo gazru rabbanan} \u8212- the principle that rabbinic decrees do not apply in the presence of a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- because {\i yibum} is not a momentary act that overrides the prohibition in passing. If he marries her, he will be living with her in an ongoing {\i de\u8217'rabbanan achos ishah} violation. This is not a situation of two competing momentary obligations; it is a structural incompatibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additionally, even doing nothing creates a problem according to those authorities who hold that {\i zikah} \u8212- the legal bond that exists between the levir and the {\i yevamah} before he acts \u8212- is strong enough to create a prohibition of {\i achos zekukaso}. If {\i zikah} is real, then simply remaining married to his wife while the {\i zikah} to the sister-in-law persists is itself forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer cuts the knot: {\i melamdin et haketanah shetime\u8217'en bo} \u8212- instruct the minor wife to perform {\i me\u8217'un} against her husband. If she performs {\i me\u8217'un}, the marriage is retroactively treated as never having taken place, she is no longer his wife, there is no {\i achos ishah} impediment, and he may proceed to {\i yibum de\u8217'oraisa} with the {\i gedolah}\u8217's widow. The simplicity of the solution is elegant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel allows a middle path: {\i me\u8217'anah me\u8217'anah} \u8212- if the minor herself wishes to perform {\i me\u8217'un}, that resolves everything. But we do not instruct her to do so. If she does not wish to do {\i me\u8217'un}, she waits until she becomes an adult. Once she becomes an adult, the {\i kiddushin} attains {\i de\u8217'oraisa} force, and she then constitutes a genuine {\i achos ishah de\u8217'oraisa}, permitting her to exit without {\i chalitzah}. Rabban Gamliel\u8217's position presupposes that {\i zikah} is not strong enough to create a prohibition of {\i achos zekukaso}, so there is no problem with the brother remaining in his current marriages while waiting for the minor to come of age. The minor may, of course, be effectively an {\i agunah} for some period of years while this plays out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua rejects both solutions. He holds that one cannot instruct a woman to perform {\i me\u8217'un} \u8212- that is simply not a permissible intervention. And he holds that {\i zikah} is real enough to create {\i achos zekukaso}, so the brother cannot simply remain in his current marriage while the {\i zikah} to the sister-in-law persists. He therefore sees no exit that does not generate a new problem. His ruling: {\i motzi et ishto be\u8217'get ve\u8217'eshet achiv be\u8217'chalitzah} \u8212- he must both give his wife a {\i get} and perform {\i chalitzah} with the sister-in-law. He cannot do one without the other, because doing one first makes the other {\i achos chalutzaso} or {\i achos grushaso}. In theory both must happen simultaneously \u8212- and the result is that both women are released, and he ends up with neither. {\i Oy li al ishto oy li al eshet achiv} \u8212- woe to his wife, woe to his brother\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teaching Me\u8217'un\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the question that motivates Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position: is it permissible to instruct a woman to perform {\i me\u8217'un} at all? This challenge emerges from a teaching of Bar Kapparah, which introduces the next section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbinic Values\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Kapparah Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kapparah taught: a person should cleave to three things and distance himself from three things. Cleave to: {\i chalitzah} over {\i yibum}, bringing {\i shalom}, and annulling vows ({\i haforas nedarim}). Distance from: {\i me\u8217'un}, accepting objects for safekeeping ({\i pikdonos}), and being a cosigner ({\i aravon}). It is from the first of these \u8212- that one should prefer {\i chalitzah} to {\i yibum} \u8212- that the {\i gemara} derives its answer to the question about Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's solution. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i me\u8217'un de\u8217'mitzvah shanei} \u8212- ordinarily we do not instruct people to perform {\i me\u8217'un}, in keeping with Bar Kapparah\u8217's teaching. But when performing {\i me\u8217'un} enables a {\i de\u8217'oraisa} obligation of {\i yibum} to be fulfilled, it is an exceptional case. In that context, teaching the minor to perform {\i me\u8217'un} is not only permissible but appropriate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preference for {\i chalitzah} that Bar Kapparah cites follows the position of Abba Shaul. A {\i braisa} records: {\i ha\u8217'kones et yevamto le\u8217'shem noy, le\u8217'shem ishus, le\u8217'shem davar acher} \u8212- one who takes his {\i yevamah} for reasons of her beauty, for the desire for marriage, or for any other motivation other than purely {\i le\u8217'hakim shem achiv}, establishing his brother\u8217's name \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'ilu poga be\u8217'erva}, it is as if he engaged in a prohibited relationship, since the ordinarily-forbidden status of {\i eshet achiv} is lifted only by the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} performed with proper intent. When that proper intent is absent, the override does not fully function. Moreover, Abba Shaul states {\i ve\u8217'karov be\u8217'einai lihyos ha\u8217'vlad mamzer} \u8212- it is almost as though the child born from such a union is a {\i mamzer}. This harsh formulation reflects Abba Shaul\u8217's view that {\i yibum} performed without its proper {\i le\u8217'shem mitzvah} motivation is deeply compromised. Hence Bar Kapparah\u8217's teaching that one should incline toward {\i chalitzah}, which carries no such risk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peace Imperative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second thing one should cleave to is the pursuit of {\i shalom}. {\i Bakesh shalom ve\u8217'rodfehu} \u8212- seek peace and pursue it. Rabbi Abahu adds a {\i gezeirah shavah} connecting the language of {\i redifah} (running after) in this verse to a second verse: {\i rodef tzedakah va\u8217'chesed yimtza chayim tzedakah ve\u8217'chavod} \u8212- one who runs after righteousness and kindness will find life, righteousness, and honor. Life in this world, honor in the world to come. The active, relentless pursuit of peace is thus associated with the fullest range of divine reward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vows & Institutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third positive practice is {\i haforas nedarim} \u8212- annulling vows. This follows Rabbi Natan, who taught: {\i ha\u8217'noder ke\u8217'ilu banah bamah} \u8212- one who takes a vow is like someone who built a {\i bamah} (a private altar), which is forbidden when there is a central {\i Beis Hamikdash}. And {\i ha\u8217'mekaymo ke\u8217'ilu hikriv alav korban} \u8212- one who fulfills that vow is like one who offered a sacrifice on that {\i bamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The metaphor is illuminating. The underlying impulse to make a vow is a desire for personal religious expression beyond what the established system of {\i mitzvos} and {\i halacha} provides. One is not satisfied with communal religious institutions and seeks a private avenue for devotion. Rabbi Natan frames this as analogous to building a private altar: subjectively, the person believes they are performing a sacred act of dedication; objectively, from the perspective of those committed to centralized religious institutions, it represents an undermining of the communal framework. Vows therefore should not be made \u8212- and if made, they should be annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cosigners & Judges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to the three things from which one should distance: {\i me\u8217'un} is the first. Bar Kapparah\u8217's concern is {\i de\u8217'ma gadla u\u8217'micharta} \u8212- perhaps she will become an adult and regret it. {\i Pikdonos} is the second: accepting objects for safekeeping from a neighbor ({\i bar mata}), because {\i de\u8217'veiteih ke\u8217'veiteih dami} \u8212- his house is like your house; he will come and go freely, creating conditions under which he could take the object back and then claim you lost it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third is {\i aravon}, being a cosigner \u8212- specifically in the mode of {\i arvei shel Tzion}, where creditors would pursue the cosigner before even turning to the actual debtor. Rabbi Yitzchak explains via a verse: {\i ra yero\u8217'a ki arav zar} \u8212- evil after evil because one has entangled oneself as a guarantor for an outsider. The verse yields two further readings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerim Concerns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One reading: {\i ra\u8217'ah achar ra\u8217'ah tavo le\u8217'mekablei gerim} \u8212- evil after evil should come to those who accept converts. This is, taken at face value, a strikingly negative statement about accepting {\i gerim}. It reads {\i arav zar} as referring to involving an outsider in one\u8217's religious community. Rav Chelbo\u8217's teaching is cited in support: {\i kashim gerim le\u8217'Yisrael ke\u8217'sapachat ba\u8217'or} \u8212- converts are as difficult for Israel as a skin affliction, based on a wordplay between {\i ve\u8217'nispechu} (they will attach) and {\i sapachat} (an affliction).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the most negative statement in the {\i gemara} about accepting converts, and it must be read carefully. {\i Tosafot} immediately contextualizes and mitigates: the negative statement applies specifically in two circumstances \u8212- where one is proactively proselytizing and persuading people to convert, or where one accepts a prospective convert without adequate process. But if they themselves are pressing insistently to convert, {\i yesh lanu le\u8217'kablam} \u8212- we should accept them, and doing so is not merely permitted but meritorious. As proof, {\i Tosafot} cites the {\i midrash} that Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov were punished for failing to accept Timna when she sought to convert, with the consequence that {\i Amalek} descended from her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning to Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second reading of {\i arvei shel Tzion} concerns {\i tokeia atzmo lidvar halacha} \u8212- one who sticks himself onto a matter of {\i halacha}. This phrase requires unpacking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} records Rabbi Yose teaching: {\i kol ha\u8217'omer ein lo Torah ein lo Torah} \u8212- one who says \u8220"I have no interest in Torah\u8221" receives no reward from Torah. This seems obvious and barely worth stating. The {\i gemara} clarifies: the real case is {\i kol ha\u8217'omer ein lo ela Torah ein lo ela Torah} \u8212- one who says \u8220"I have only Torah, nothing else\u8221" \u8212- meaning one who learns but does not act, who remains in the {\i beis medrash} but never translates learning into practice, receives only the reward for learning and not the reward of {\i mitzvah} performance. Still, the {\i gemara} pushes further: even that seems obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The real {\i chiddush} is more radical: if one\u8217's learning never translates into action, {\i afillu Torah ein lo} \u8212- one does not even receive the reward for Torah study. The basis is the verse {\i u\u8217'lemadetem va\u8217'asitem} \u8212- learn and do. {\i Kol she\u8217'yeshno ba\u8217'asiyah yeshno ba\u8217'limidah} \u8212- only if one is embedded in a world of action does the learning have value. {\i Kol she\u8217'eino ba\u8217'asiyah eino ba\u8217'limidah} \u8212- if one is not in a world of doing, the learning itself yields no reward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An extension of this: what if one stays in the {\i beis medrash}, teaches others, and through teaching sends others out to perform {\i mitzvos}? One might think this vicarious doing should count. The {\i gemara} says no: if the teacher himself never acts, the fact that students go out and act does not earn the teacher reward for their actions. The teacher gets credit for the Torah taught but not for the actions performed by others. Learning must translate into personal action to be credited as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judge\u8217's Weight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final interpretation of {\i tokeia atzmo lidvar halacha} applies to a judge: a case comes before a {\i dayan} and he recognizes something vaguely analogous in what he has studied. He analogizes \u8212- {\i medami milsa le\u8217'milsa} \u8212- and renders a ruling based on that imperfect parallel, despite the fact that he has a {\i rebbe} he could consult and does not bother to do so. {\i Tokeia atzmo lidvar halacha}: he is stuck on the one thing he happens to know, applying it as if it were a hammer to every nail, never acknowledging the limits of his expertise. This is a grave failure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani in the name of Rav Yonasan: {\i le\u8217'olam yehei dayan atzmo ke\u8217'ilu cherev munachas lo bein yarcheisav} \u8212- a judge should always regard himself as though a sword is placed between his thighs, {\i ve\u8217'Gehenom pesucha lo mitachtav} \u8212- and {\i Gehenom} is open beneath him. The weight of judgment demands complete seriousness and genuine {\i yiras shamayim}. A judge who overreaches, who applies a single learned rule without seeking guidance where guidance is needed, risks far more than a wrong ruling. The verse from {\i Shir Hashirim} about the sixty warriors guarding the bed of Shlomo, each with a sword on account of {\i pachad baleilos} \u8212- fear of nights \u8212- is read as {\i pachad shel Gehenom shedomeh le\u8217'layla}: the fear of {\i Gehenom}, which resembles the night.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 110\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushei Ketanah & Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the bottom of 109b, with the Mishnah\u8217's discussion of {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's ruling. The case involves a man who contracted {\i kiddushei ketanah} \u8212- a marriage to a minor girl that was effected not through her father, and therefore binding only {\i derabbanan} \u8212- and whose brother subsequently died, leaving him with an obligation of {\i yibum derabbanan} toward his sister-in-law. To make the scenario concrete: Reuven is married to Rachel through {\i kiddushei ketanah} (a {\i derabbanan} marriage), and his brother dies leaving Leah as the {\i yevamah}. The complication is that Leah is Rachel\u8217's sister \u8212- i.e., {\i achot ishto} \u8212- and is therefore an {\i ervah} who normally cannot be subject to {\i yibum}. But is this {\i ervah} relationship truly operative here, given that the marriage to Rachel is only {\i derabbanan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's position is that one should teach the minor girl to perform {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- the minor\u8217's right to refuse and exit the marriage \u8212- thereby dissolving the {\i derabbanan} bond and permitting {\i yibum} with Leah. {\i Rabban Gamliel} takes a more nuanced stance: if the girl does {\i mi\u8217'un}, well and good; if not, one should simply wait until she matures. Once she becomes an adult, she becomes his wife {\i de\u8217'oraita}, and at that point Leah, as her sister, becomes a genuine {\i ervah} and may walk away entirely \u8212- without {\i chalitzah}, and without {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Core Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's position is immediate. The obligation of {\i yibum} begins at the moment the brother dies. At that moment, Rachel is only a {\i derabbanan} wife, and thus Leah is only a {\i derabbanan ervah} \u8212- not a {\i de\u8217'oraita ervah}. Given that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} had already taken hold when the {\i ervah} was merely {\i derabbanan}, how can Leah walk away entirely simply because Rachel later becomes an adult? Perhaps one should argue that since the {\i zikah} (the {\i yibum} bond) was already in force when the {\i ervah} was only rabbinic, the {\i mitzvah} should persist \u8212- and at minimum, {\i chalitzah} might be required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Elazar Asks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Elazar} posed this question directly to {\i Rav}: what exactly is {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's reasoning?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suspended Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first possibility {\i Rav} considers is that {\i Rabban Gamliel} holds {\i kiddushei ketanah} are {\i teluyim} \u8212- suspended or held in abeyance. The Aramaic phrase used is evocative: {\i kiddusheha mitla talu}, and {\i chi gadlah gadli bahadah} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} \u8220"grows up with her.\u8221" According to this view, when she contracts {\i kiddushin} as a minor, the act is not simply a {\i derabbanan} institution with no {\i de\u8217'oraita} dimension. Rather, the {\i de\u8217'oraita} marriage is latent within the original act and automatically comes into force when she reaches adulthood. There is no new act of {\i kiddushin} required; the original act, held in suspension, simply activates. On this reading, even retroactively, Rachel was in some sense already his wife when the {\i yibum} obligation began \u8212- and therefore Leah was already an {\i ervah} in some meaningful sense from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Kiddushin Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possibility is that {\i Rabban Gamliel} holds an entirely different view: the original {\i kiddushei ketanah} carry no {\i de\u8217'oraita} weight whatsoever. When Rachel becomes an adult, it is not that the old {\i kiddushin} activates \u8212- rather, a new act of {\i kiddushin} is created. This raises a separate question within {\i yibum} law: if a man has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with a woman and then, without having performed either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}, proceeds to marry her sister, mother, or daughter \u8212- creating an {\i ervah} situation after the {\i zikah} has already begun \u8212- does that {\i ervah} status dissolve the {\i zikah}? Can one simply generate an {\i ervah} after the fact and thereby neutralize the {\i yibum} obligation? {\i Rabban Gamliel} apparently holds yes: even if the {\i ervah} is created after the {\i zikah} began, she walks away \u8212- {\i nifterah yevamah ve-halchah lah}. On this reading, the crucial question then becomes: when does the new {\i kiddushin} with Rachel take effect? Since there is no ring or document, only {\i bi\u8217'ah} (intercourse) could create a new act of {\i kiddushin}. Hence the formulation: {\i ei baal in, ei lo baal lo} \u8212- if they have had {\i bi\u8217'ah}, yes; if not, no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two distinct analytical threads thus emerge: a {\i yibum} question about whether an {\i ervah} created after the {\i zikah} began dissolves it, and a {\i ketanah} question about what precisely happens when a minor girl grows up \u8212- whether the original {\i kiddushin} automatically activates, or whether a new act of {\i kiddushin} is required. The {\i Gemara} proceeds to focus on the latter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} dismisses {\i Rav}\u8217's framing sharply, suggesting that {\i Rav} must have been dozing off when he said it \u8212- a turn of phrase {\i Rav Sheshet} apparently favored. His basis is a {\i baraita} that, in his view, settles the matter clearly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita on Teluyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: {\i hamekadesh et ha-ketanah, kiddusheha teluyim} \u8212- one who betroths a minor, her {\i kiddushin} is suspended. {\i Rav Sheshet} reads this as unambiguous support for the position that once she grows up, the {\i kiddushin} automatically takes effect \u8212- {\i gadlah gadli bahadah} \u8212- without requiring any new act of {\i bi\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Avin\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Avin bar Rav Nachman} responds that {\i mitalyasa ve-kayma} \u8212- \u8220"in abeyance and pending\u8221" \u8212- does not necessarily mean the {\i kiddushin} will automatically crystallize upon adulthood. Rather, it means the situation remains unresolved: {\i ei baal in, ei lo baal lo}. The {\i kiddushin} will take effect only if {\i bi\u8217'ah} occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning offered is that the woman retains her own set of prerogatives parallel to his. He has advantages she lacks \u8212- the power to divorce her unilaterally \u8212- but she has advantages he lacks: the right to perform {\i mi\u8217'un} and exit the marriage on her own initiative. As long as she has not had relations with him as an adult, she has not yet relinquished that right, and the {\i kiddushin} has not yet resolved into something definitive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The core question thus crystallizes: is {\i kiddushei ketanah} automatically operative upon her reaching adulthood, or is a new act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} required to effectuate it? This is not merely a technical question about the minor\u8217's marriage \u8212- it ramifies into major areas of practical {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel/Sheshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} holds that a new act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} is required. When a {\i ketanah} grows up, the original {\i kiddushin} does not automatically become operative {\i de\u8217'oraita}; only if they subsequently have intercourse as adults does the marriage become fully binding at a Torah level. {\i Rav Sheshet} disagrees: the {\i kiddushin} activates automatically, without any new act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Rav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now challenges {\i Rav}\u8217's position. A {\i ketanah} who did not perform {\i mi\u8217'un} grew up, and then accepted {\i kiddushin} from a second man. What is the ruling?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Implied\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} rules that she does not need a {\i get} from the second man \u8212- the second {\i kiddushin} was ineffective. {\i Shmuel} rules that she does need a {\i get} from the second man. The implication of {\i Rav}\u8217's ruling seems to be that when she becomes an adult she is already {\i mekudeshet} to the first husband \u8212- automatically, even without intercourse \u8212- which appears to contradict his stated position that {\i bi\u8217'ah} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: the case where {\i Rav} says she does not need a {\i get} from the second man is specifically the case where she had already had {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the first husband after reaching adulthood. The second man\u8217's {\i kiddushin} failed because she was already fully {\i mekudeshet} {\i de\u8217'oraita} to the first husband \u8212- via that act of {\i bi\u8217'ah}. {\i Rav}\u8217's position is therefore internally consistent: {\i bi\u8217'ah} is indeed required, and it had occurred in this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} then presses: if she had {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the first husband as an adult, why does {\i Shmuel} say she still needs a {\i get} from the second man? How could the second {\i kiddushin} possibly be effective? {\i Shmuel}\u8217's answer is the crucial formulation: {\i kol ha-bo\u8217'el al da\u8217'at kiddushin ha-rishonim hu bo\u8217'el} \u8212- anyone who has intercourse does so within the framework of the original {\i kiddushin}. According to {\i Shmuel}, even when the adult woman has intercourse with her husband, that act is not a new, independent {\i kiddushin}; it is a continuation of the original {\i kiddushei ketanah}. Since the original {\i kiddushin} was only {\i derabbanan}, the subsequent intercourse \u8212- framed entirely by that context \u8212- never creates a new {\i de\u8217'oraita kiddushin}. Therefore, there is no {\i de\u8217'oraita} marriage, and when the second man gives {\i kiddushin}, it could potentially take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Way Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three-way structure of the debate is now clear. {\i Rav Sheshet} holds that upon reaching adulthood, the {\i kiddushei ketanah} automatically activates {\i de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- no intercourse required. {\i Rav} holds that intercourse after adulthood creates a new act of {\i kiddushin de\u8217'oraita}, independent of and superseding the original rabbinic framework. {\i Shmuel} holds that even post-adolescent intercourse does not create a new {\i kiddushin}, because all intercourse is understood within the context of the original {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and since that original {\i kiddushin} was {\i derabbanan}, the couple never achieves a {\i de\u8217'oraita} marital status unless they explicitly reframe the relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This three-way debate is not merely of theoretical interest. It bears directly on the practical reasoning of major twentieth-century {\i poskim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Moshe Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Moshe Feinstein}\u8217's well-known approach to cases of {\i agunah} and potential {\i mamzerut} frequently relied on invalidating the original {\i kiddushin} \u8212- by identifying a {\i pesul} in the witnesses, for instance \u8212- and thereby establishing that no valid marriage ever took place. A standing objection to this approach is that even if the original {\i kiddushin} were defective, the couple lived together for years and had repeated intercourse: why would each act of intercourse not itself constitute a new {\i ma\u8217'aseh kiddushin}? {\i Rav Moshe} answered by invoking precisely {\i Shmuel}\u8217's principle: {\i kol ha-bo\u8217'el al da\u8217'at kiddushin ha-rishonim hu bo\u8217'el}. The subsequent intercourse was not intended as an independent act of {\i kiddushin}; it was understood by both parties as occurring within the framework of what they believed was their already-established marriage. Since no one had in mind to create a new {\i kiddushin}, none was created. The difference from the {\i ketanah} case is that there the original {\i kiddushin} was merely {\i derabbanan}, while in {\i Rav Moshe}\u8217's cases the original {\i kiddushin} was attempted {\i de\u8217'oraita} but was {\i pasul}; the principle, however, operates analogously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Henkin\u8217's Disagreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef Eliyahu Henkin} disagreed with {\i Rav Moshe}\u8217's application of this principle. His argument was that {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- betrothal effected through intercourse \u8212- does not depend on subjective intent. The objective reality of sustained marital cohabitation, of a man and woman living together as husband and wife and engaging in intercourse within that context, itself creates {\i kiddushin}, regardless of whether either party consciously intends the act as a new {\i kinyan}. The {\i de\u8217'oraita} framework, in his view, is activated by the reality of the marital relationship, not merely by articulated intent. On this reading, {\i Rav Moshe}\u8217's leniency would not hold, since the years of cohabitation would themselves constitute valid {\i kiddushin}. One can see this disagreement as corresponding, at a theoretical level, to the debate between {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel}: {\i Rav} might hold that the objective act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} in a marital context creates {\i kiddushin} even without explicit intent, while {\i Shmuel} would say the act is always colored by the original framing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tnai Case & Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin B\u8217'Tnai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} have already debated this same underlying question in a different context: {\i kidesh al tnai ve-chanas stam} \u8212- a man performed {\i kiddushin} with an explicit condition ({\i tnai}), and then the couple began living together without reiterating the condition. They continued to live as husband and wife and to have intercourse, but the {\i tnai} was never formally addressed again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav on Tnai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} rules that a {\i get} is required. Since the husband has moved forward into married life with her, he has implicitly waived ({\i achulei achlah}) his original {\i tnai}. The act of living together and having intercourse overrides the earlier conditional framework and creates a new, unconditional marital reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel on Tnai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel} rules that no {\i get} is needed. All subsequent intercourse is understood as occurring within the framework of the original {\i kiddushin} \u8212- including its {\i tnai}. {\i Kol ha-bo\u8217'el al da\u8217'at kiddushin ha-rishonim hu bo\u8217'el}. Since the {\i tnai} was never fulfilled or waived explicitly, it remains operative, and the marriage was never fully effectuated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: if so, why does the debate need to be stated in both cases \u8212- the {\i ketanah} case and the {\i tnai} case? Perhaps one could derive the other. The {\i Gemara} explains that the two cases are not fully parallel. In the {\i tnai} case, the {\i tnai} is explicit and the husband is directly confronted with it; when he subsequently has intercourse, one can reasonably infer that he is consciously setting the {\i tnai} aside. Therefore, {\i Rav}\u8217's position \u8212- that the {\i bi\u8217'ah} overrides the earlier context \u8212- is most naturally at home in the {\i tnai} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possible Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, in the {\i ketanah} case, there is no explicit problem confronting the husband. He married a young girl, they grew up together, and nothing in his lived experience signals that a new act of {\i kiddushin} is needed. He simply does not perceive a problem. Therefore, {\i Rav} might in fact agree with {\i Shmuel} in the {\i ketanah} case \u8212- holding that since the husband has no awareness that a new {\i de\u8217'oraita} framework is called for, the {\i bi\u8217'ah} does not generate one. Conversely, in the {\i tnai} case, {\i Shmuel} might agree with {\i Rav} \u8212- since the problem is so explicit that the husband\u8217's continued {\i bi\u8217'ah} could be construed as a conscious act of waiver. Both cases need to be stated to prevent either such inference.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Challenge & Bei Korsaia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection to Rav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a further challenge to {\i Rav}\u8217's stated position: does {\i Rav} really hold that the {\i ketanah}\u8217's {\i kiddushin} only becomes {\i de\u8217'oraita} upon subsequent {\i bi\u8217'ah}? A story from {\i Narash} appears to cut the other way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abduction Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case: a girl who had been married as a {\i ketanah} grew up, and was being conveyed in the {\i bei korsaia} \u8212- a special seat or conveyance used to bring the bride to the {\i chuppah} \u8212- when another man appeared and abducted her, apparently accepting her {\i kiddushin} on the spot. In other words, she had become an adult, had not yet had {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the first husband, and the second man performed an act of {\i kiddushin} before the {\i chuppah} took place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna/Chananel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna} and {\i Rav Chananel}, both students of {\i Rav}, were present in {\i Narash} at the time, and ruled that she does not need a {\i get} from the second man \u8212- the second {\i kiddushin} was simply ineffective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The apparent implication is that she was already fully {\i mekudeshet} to the first husband the moment she became an adult \u8212- automatically, without {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- consistent with {\i Rav Sheshet}\u8217's position, not {\i Rav}\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Papa} resolves the difficulty by noting a local custom: in {\i Narash}, the couple has {\i bi\u8217'ah} before the ceremonial chariot ride, not after. The {\i chuppah} and consummation preceded the {\i bei korsaia} procession. Therefore the ruling of {\i Rav Huna} and {\i Rav Chananel} was issued in a case where {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the first husband had already occurred \u8212- fully consistent with {\i Rav}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} takes a different approach entirely, accepting the case more straightforwardly: the second {\i kiddushin} was indeed performed before any {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the first husband, and {\i de\u8217'oraita} she was not yet fully married to him. Nevertheless, the second man\u8217's {\i kiddushin} did not take effect \u8212- not because the first marriage had activated, but because {\i hu asa shelo ke-hogen}: he acted in a grossly improper manner by intercepting and abducting the bride on her way to the {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafka\u8217'as Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since he acted in violation of basic ethical norms, the Rabbis responded in kind \u8212- {\i anan nami na\u8217'avid leh shelo ke-hogen}: we too will deal with him outside the normal rules. They voided ({\i afkinhu}) his {\i kiddushin}. The {\i Gemara} raises the question: what if the second man performed {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} rather than {\i kiddushei kesef}? If he gave money, one might argue the Rabbis declared it {\i hefker} and the {\i kiddushin} collapsed. But if he had intercourse \u8212- what can the Rabbis do? The answer: {\i shavyuh rabbanan le-vi\u8217'alaso beilas zenus} \u8212- the Rabbis declared his act of intercourse to be {\i beilas zenus}, devoid of {\i kiddushin} significance. They refused to recognize it as a marital act at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hafka\u8217'as Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case-Specific Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This episode of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} is striking precisely because it is entirely case-specific. The more familiar instances of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} operate within pre-defined rabbinic rules: the Rabbis set up a system in advance, and if a person violates the parameters of that system, the {\i kiddushin} is automatically void \u8212- for example, if a husband attempts to annul a {\i get} in the absence of the agent ({\i shelo bifnei ha-shali\u8217'ah}), the Rabbis simply refused to recognize the transmission as valid. All of this flows from the principle {\i kol d\u8217'mekadesh ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabbanan mekadesh}: every act of {\i kiddushin} is implicitly performed within and subject to the rabbinic framework, and the Rabbis therefore retain the authority to void it when their rules are violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i bei korsaia} case is different. Here, the man is not working within the rabbinic system at all \u8212- he is brazenly circumventing it. Invoking {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabbanan} to void his {\i kiddushin} would be difficult, since he clearly had no intention of operating within rabbinic norms.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ruling & Implication (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reiteration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical ruling that emerges from the entire preceding discussion: {\i Rav Yosef} declares that the {\i halacha} follows {\i Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- one teaches the {\i ketanah} to perform {\i mi\u8217'un}. The Amora {\i Rabbi Elazar} similarly states: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer}, affirming that in the scenario involving sisters, the correct practical course is to have the minor perform {\i mi\u8217'un} rather than wait for her to mature.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hafka\u8217'as Kiddushin (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises an explicit question about the mechanism underlying {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} in the {\i bei korsaia} case. The formulation {\i lefichach asu lo shelo ke-hogen ve-afki\u8217'inhu kiddushin minei} is analyzed: the {\i Ri} was uncertain whether the voiding of the {\i kiddushin} is based on the standard principle \u8212- {\i kol ha-mekadesh ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabbanan mekadesh} \u8212- or whether it operates on the broader, more dramatic rabbinic power: {\i yesh koach b\u8217'yad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah}. The basis for the doubt is precisely that this case does not carry the standard formula seen elsewhere; the phrase {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabbanan} is conspicuously absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution turns on distinguishing between categories of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}. Most cases in which the Rabbis void a {\i kiddushin} involve scenarios that were anticipated and defined in advance within the rabbinic legal architecture. The rules are known at the outset, the parties operate within a defined framework, and when those rules are transgressed the {\i kiddushin} is dissolved as a function of the system itself \u8212- this is the {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabbanan} logic. The {\i bei korsaia} case stands apart: here the man is acting entirely outside the system, violating basic ethical norms in the very act of {\i kiddushin}, not exploiting a loophole within it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields two categories of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}: categorical {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}, where the rules are defined in advance and the voiding flows automatically from the system ({\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabbanan}); and ad hoc {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}, where the man acts outside the system entirely, and the Rabbis intervene on a case-specific basis to void what he has done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinical Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the ad hoc category, the operative principle is {\i yesh koach b\u8217'yad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min ha-Torah} \u8212- the Rabbis possess the authority to override Torah law in specific circumstances. This is a more powerful and more exceptional invocation, not something deployed routinely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied to the {\i bei korsaia} story: the second man\u8217's act of {\i kiddushin} was not voided because he violated an anticipated rabbinic rule, but because the Rabbis refused entirely to recognize an act of {\i kiddushin} performed through abduction and moral outrage. Critically, the improper conduct was the very act of {\i kiddushin} itself \u8212- not some later misconduct. This is an important limiting principle: {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} applied to the act of {\i kiddushin} as the transgression, not as a response to subsequent bad behavior.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugyot} thus align clearly: the {\i bei korsaia} case is the only instance in the {\i Gemara} in which the language {\i shelo ke-hogen} appears in the context of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}, and it is the only instance that does not invoke {\i kol d\u8217'mekadesh ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabbanan mekadesh}. All other cases of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} operate within the categorical rabbinic framework and rest on the {\i ada\u8217'ata} principle. This one case alone operates outside the system and requires recourse to the Rabbis\u8217' broader power to override Torah law.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ruling & Implication (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} rules that the {\i halacha} follows {\i Rabbi Eliezer}: in the case of the sisters, one teaches the {\i ketanah} to perform {\i mi\u8217'un}, and that is the operative practical ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Next Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} transitions to the next Mishnah, which opens a new set of scenarios involving a man married to two wives of different legal statuses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Wives Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Katanot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah presents the case: a man was married to two minor orphans \u8212- {\i shnei yetomot ketanot}, understood by {\i Rashi} here as non-sisters \u8212- and died. The {\i Gemara} affirms that {\i bi\u8217'ah} or {\i chalitzah} of the surviving brother with one of the {\i yevamot} releases the other. Since both women entered their marriages as {\i ketanot}, both occupy the same {\i derabbanan} status vis-\u224 ?-vis the deceased husband, and the rules of {\i yibum} operate symmetrically between them. Acting on one fully exempts the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Chereshot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle applies if both wives are deaf-mutes ({\i chereshot}). A {\i chereshet} cannot enter a {\i de\u8217'oraita} marriage, but {\i derabbanan} the Rabbis instituted valid marriage for her. Since both wives are at the same {\i derabbanan} level, one releasing the other through {\i bi\u8217'ah} or {\i chalitzah} works here as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pikachat & Choreshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then distinguishes a case in which one wife is a {\i pikachat} \u8212- a hearing, fully capable adult \u8212- and the other is a {\i chereshet}. Although both are the deceased\u8217's wives, they are not of equivalent standing. The {\i pikachat}\u8217's marriage was {\i de\u8217'oraita}; the {\i chereshet}\u8217's was {\i derabbanan}. They cannot be treated symmetrically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pikachat Releases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bi\u8217'at ha-pikachat poteret ha-chereshet}: if the brother performs {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} with the {\i pikachat}, the {\i chereshet} is released. Acting on the stronger, {\i de\u8217'oraita} bond discharges the entirety of the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 D\u8217'oraita vs.\u160 ?D\u8217'rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse, however, does not hold: {\i ein bi\u8217'at ha-chereshet poteret ha-pikachat}. Acting at the {\i derabbanan} level cannot dissolve a {\i de\u8217'oraita zikah}. The {\i derabbanan} has no traction on the {\i de\u8217'oraita} obligation; performing {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} with the wife of lesser standing leaves the {\i pikachat}\u8217's bond fully intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gedolah & Ketanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same asymmetry applies to the case of a {\i gedolah} and a {\i ketanah}: the adult\u8217's {\i de\u8217'oraita} marriage overrides the minor\u8217's {\i derabbanan} status. Acting on the {\i gedolah} releases the {\i ketanah}, but acting on the {\i ketanah} does not release the {\i gedolah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's reference to {\i chalitzah} as an option in the case of two {\i chereshot} immediately prompts a difficulty: how can a {\i chereshet} perform {\i chalitzah}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Choreshet & Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question on Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks directly: {\i chereshet bat chalitzah hi}? Is a {\i chereshet} even eligible to perform {\i chalitzah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Bat Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i chereshet} cannot perform {\i chalitzah} \u8212- the act requires specific capacities, including the recitation of verses, that a deaf-mute cannot fulfill.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalid Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} confirms: {\i chalitzah} performed by a deaf man ({\i cheresh}), or by a {\i chereshet}, or with a {\i katan} (minor male), is {\i pesulah} \u8212- invalid. This appears to categorically bar {\i chalitzah} by a {\i chereshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Gidel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Gidel} resolves the difficulty by reinterpreting the Mishnah\u8217's language: when the Mishnah speaks of the two {\i chereshot} case and mentions {\i bi\u8217'ah} or {\i chalitzah}, it meant only {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the {\i chalitzah} clause applies only to the two {\i ketanot} case, where {\i chalitzah} is at least theoretically discussable. For {\i chereshot}, only {\i bi\u8217'ah} is on the table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} disagrees. He maintains that {\i chalitzah} is indeed possible in the {\i chereshot} case, and the key lies in a distinction about the nature of the original marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} introduces the distinction: {\i chereshet me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- a woman who was deaf from birth and entered her marriage as a {\i chereshet} \u8212- versus {\i pikachat she-nishtareshet} \u8212- a woman who was hearing at the time of marriage and later became deaf. These two cases are fundamentally different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Always Choreshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she was always a {\i chereshet}, her entire marital relationship exists within the {\i derabbanan} world. The Rabbis admitted her to marriage as a {\i chereshet}, and they can correspondingly admit her to {\i chalitzah} as a {\i chereshet}: {\i ki heichi de\u8217'al hachi nefik} \u8212- just as she entered, so she exits. The same rabbinic framework that enabled the marriage enables the dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Became Deaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, she married as a hearing woman and her marriage was fully {\i de\u8217'oraita}, the subsequent {\i chalitzah} must also meet {\i de\u8217'oraita} standards. A {\i chereshet} cannot provide {\i de\u8217'oraita chalitzah} \u8212- she cannot recite the required verses. The rabbinic dispensation for her entry into marriage cannot retroactively downgrade the {\i de\u8217'oraita} exit requirement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} challenges {\i Rava}\u8217's distinction. Is it truly the case that a {\i chereshet me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- a woman who was always deaf \u8212- can perform {\i chalitzah} on the grounds that her marriage was {\i derabbanan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents the relevant Mishnah from the upcoming {\i perek}: two brothers, one hearing and one deaf ({\i cheresh}), married to two unrelated women, one hearing and one deaf-mute ({\i chereshet}). The marriages are matched: the hearing brother to the hearing woman, the deaf brother to the deaf woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Brother Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the deaf brother \u8212- married to the {\i chereshet} \u8212- dies, what does the hearing brother do with respect to the {\i chereshet}? He has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum derabbanan} with her. The Mishnah states: {\i koneis} \u8212- he does {\i yibum} \u8212- but {\i chalitzah} is not an option.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is {\i Abaye}\u8217's question: if the marriage between the deaf brother and the deaf woman was only {\i derabbanan}, why cannot the hearing brother do {\i chalitzah}? According to {\i Rava}\u8217's reasoning, since it was a {\i derabbanan} marriage, {\i chalitzah} should also be possible on a {\i derabbanan} level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attempted Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might attempt to resolve {\i Abaye}\u8217's challenge by suggesting that the {\i chereshet} in this Mishnah was not deaf from birth \u8212- perhaps she married the deaf brother while hearing, and later became deaf. If so, the marriage was {\i de\u8217'oraita}, and the inability to perform {\i chalitzah} is expected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Brother Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} builds his case further from the Mishnah\u8217's second scenario: if the hearing brother \u8212- married to the hearing woman \u8212- dies, what does the deaf brother do? {\i Koneis} \u8212- he does {\i yibum} with the hearing {\i yevamah} \u8212- and he cannot give a {\i get} to divorce his own wife, the {\i chereshet}. Why? Because his marriage to the hearing {\i yevamah} through {\i yibum} creates a {\i de\u8217'oraita} bond (the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} in a {\i yibum} context effectuates a {\i de\u8217'oraita} marriage), and he cannot have two wives where one might compromise the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But crucially: earlier in the Mishnah it emerged that the deaf brother {\i can} divorce his {\i chereshet} wife \u8212- {\i motzi et ishto be-get}. If he can divorce her, it must be that he married her as a deaf man \u8212- because a man who was hearing at marriage and subsequently became deaf cannot give a {\i get} ({\i nischaresh hu lo yotzi le\u8217'olam}). Therefore, the deaf brother must have been deaf from the beginning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the deaf brother was deaf from the beginning, and he was married to the {\i chereshet}, the reasonable inference is that she too was deaf from the beginning \u8212- {\i chereshet me\u8217'ikara}. Her marriage to the deceased was {\i derabbanan}. Yet the Mishnah states explicitly that {\i chalitzah} is not an option \u8212- {\i koneis, ein choletz lo}. This directly refutes {\i Rava}\u8217's position that a {\i chereshet me\u8217'ikara} can perform {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sisters Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sisters Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further Mishnah sharpens the difficulty. Two hearing brothers are married to two sisters: one sister is hearing, the other is a {\i chereshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the hearing brother \u8212- married to the hearing sister \u8212- dies, the hearing {\i yevamah} cannot be taken in {\i yibum} by the surviving hearing brother because she is his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- {\i achot ishto}, a full {\i de\u8217'oraita ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the deaf brother \u8212- married to the deaf sister \u8212- dies, the deaf {\i yevamah} cannot perform {\i chalitzah} because she is a {\i chereshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The surviving hearing brother is thus caught: he cannot do {\i yibum} with the {\i chereshet yevamah} because she is his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- {\i achot ishto}. He cannot do {\i chalitzah} with her because she is a {\i chereshet}. The only thing he can do is divorce his hearing wife \u8212- but even that does not help, since after divorcing her, the {\i chereshet} becomes {\i achot gerushato}, his divorced wife\u8217's sister, which is equally an {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Result\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chereshet yevamah} is thus permanently trapped \u8212- an {\i agunah} within the {\i derabbanan} framework. She cannot be released through {\i yibum}, cannot be released through {\i chalitzah}, and has no recourse. This is a genuine and troubling result of the rabbinic institution of marriage for the {\i chereshet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Resolution Attempts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the proof from the divorce case. Since the deaf brother can divorce his {\i chereshet} wife, he must have married her as a {\i cheresh} \u8212- because a man who became deaf after a hearing marriage cannot give a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Me\u8217'ikara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, the deaf woman in this Mishnah must have been a {\i chereshet me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- deaf from the beginning of her marriage. Her marriage was entirely {\i derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yet Chalitzah Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And yet \u8212- despite the marriage having been entirely {\i derabbanan} \u8212- {\i chalitzah} is not an option for her. This is the problem for {\i Rava}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah is explicit: {\i koneis, ein choletz lo} \u8212- he does {\i yibum} with her, he does not perform {\i chalitzah}. This formulation applies even in the scenario where the marriage was {\i derabbanan} from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Silent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} does not offer a satisfying resolution to {\i Abaye}\u8217's challenge. {\i Ishtik} \u8212- silence. The question stands unanswered at this point, and the discussion carries over into the next daf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What has been established, however, is the following: a {\i chereshet} cannot perform {\i chalitzah} \u8212- even when her original marriage was contracted as a {\i chereshet} and was therefore only {\i derabbanan} in character. {\i Rava}\u8217's elegant distinction \u8212- that {\i chereshet me\u8217'ikara} can perform {\i chalitzah} because she entered and exits within the same {\i derabbanan} framework \u8212- is challenged by the explicit ruling of the Mishnah, which {\i Abaye} has demonstrated must refer to a {\i chereshet me\u8217'ikara} case. The question of how to reconcile {\i Rava}\u8217's reasoning with the Mishnah\u8217's ruling will await resolution as the daf continues.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 111\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chereshet Me\u8217'ikara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chereshet Yibbum Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up near the bottom of 110b, continuing a discussion about whether a woman who was married {\i me\u8217'ikara} as a {\i chereshet} \u8212- a marriage that is only {\i derabbanan} \u8212- may perform {\i yibbum}. Normally, a {\i chereshet} cannot perform {\i yibbum}, both because she lacks full {\i bar da\u8217'at} status and because she cannot recite the required verses. The question raised was whether the rabbinic character of her marriage changes this. Rava had held that we would permit her to do {\i yibbum}, given that her marriage was only {\i derabbanan} from the outset. The {\i Gemara} attempted to bring evidence against Rava from the {\i Mishna} at the beginning of the next {\i perek}, which deals with a {\i chereshet} in various situations and consistently assumes she cannot perform {\i yibbum}. When the {\i Gemara} tried to read those cases as involving women who were originally hearing and only later became deaf, working through the {\i Mishna} revealed that some of the cases clearly involved women who had been {\i cherashot} from the beginning. The conclusion reached was that even when she was married {\i me\u8217'ikara} as a {\i chereshet}, she cannot perform {\i chalitza} \u8212- and that Rava is therefore incorrect. The remaining portion of this discussion re-proves that conclusion from a different section of the same {\i Mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Challenged\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ishtik} Rava \u8212- Rava fell silent. The {\i Gemara} then records that Abaye came before Rav Yosef, who said to him: {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama tistrei meha} \u8212- why did you challenge Rava from that earlier section of the {\i Mishna}? That section, dealing with two sisters who were both {\i cherashot}, Rava might have conceded is about women who were {\i cherashot me\u8217'ikara}. But the section about women who were {\i nokhriyot} \u8212- unrelated women \u8212- {\i pikchot ve\u8217'achar nitcharshu}, perhaps those were cases where the women were originally hearing and only later became deaf. So you have not fully proven your point from the most relevant section. Instead, Rav Yosef says, {\i ella iba\u8217'i lach le\u8217'akshuyei meha} \u8212- you should have challenged him from this section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shnei Achim Cherashim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} states: {\i shnei achim cherashim nesu\u8217'im shtei achayot pikchot} \u8212- two deaf brothers married to two hearing sisters. The {\i Gemara} then works through the various combinations. The key structural point is this: either both brothers are {\i cherashim} or both sisters are {\i cherashot}, and thus in every permutation, both marriages will be {\i kiddushin derabbanan}. Whatever the status of the other gender in any given case, the condition of one side \u8212- two deaf brothers or two deaf sisters \u8212- guarantees that both marriages remain within the rabbinic domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Marriage Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} enumerates the cases. Two deaf brothers married to two sisters \u8212- those sisters either are both hearing, or both deaf, or one hearing and one deaf. Similarly, two deaf sisters married to two brothers \u8212- those brothers either are both hearing, both deaf, or one of each. Because the case of two deaf brothers married to two deaf sisters appears in both lists, there are effectively five distinct combinations rather than six. In all of them, the result is the same: at least one side of each marriage is deaf, making every marriage {\i kiddushin derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Sister Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the {\i halakha}? {\i Harei elu pturot min ha-chalitza u-min ha-yibbum} \u8212- if one brother dies, the surviving brother is exempt from both {\i chalitza} and {\i yibbum}. Why? Because the women are each other\u8217's sisters \u8212- {\i achot ishto} \u8212- and since everything operates within the shared universe of {\i nissuei cheresh}, that exemption applies, and the woman simply goes free. But {\i ve\u8217'im hayu nokhriyot} \u8212- if the women were not related to one another \u8212- then the surviving brother has an obligation of {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitza} toward his deceased brother\u8217's wife, since she is his {\i yevamah} within the rabbinic world. When the women are unrelated, the {\i achot ishto} exemption does not apply, and the obligation remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chereshet Cannot Do Chal\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} says in that case: {\i yiknesu} \u8212- he may perform {\i yibbum}. But why does the text not also permit {\i chalitza}? Because in several of these configurations, the woman is herself a {\i chereshet}, and a {\i chereshet} cannot perform {\i chalitza}. Therefore {\i chalitza} is not discussed in this context \u8212- only {\i yibbum} is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh Gives Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} then adds: {\i ve\u8217'im ratzu lehotzi, yotzi\u8217'u} \u8212- and if they wish to divorce, they may divorce. This requires explanation. A {\i cheresh} normally cannot give a {\i get}, since he is not a full {\i bar da\u8217'at}. But since the entire marriage is rabbinic, the rabbis constructed a closed system: just as they allowed a {\i cheresh} to marry rabbinically, they allowed him to divorce rabbinically. The operative principle is {\i keshem she-koneis be-remiza kach motzi be-remiza} \u8212- just as he enters the marriage through gesture, so he may exit through gesture. The whole framework is self-contained within the rabbinic world of {\i nissuei cherashim}. So within this system, he may perform {\i yibbum} and thereafter give a {\i get}. What is strikingly not permitted \u8212- even though {\i yibbum} and a subsequent {\i get} are both allowed \u8212- is {\i chalitza}. The {\i Gemara} does not fully explain why {\i chalitza} is excluded while {\i yibbum} and {\i get} are permitted; it simply proves it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now establishes through the text of the {\i Mishna} itself that all of these cases involve people who were deaf from the outset. The question is raised: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what is the case? If the marriages began when all parties were hearing and they only later became deaf, then the marriages would be on a {\i de\u8217'oraita} plane, and a {\i get} would not suffice \u8212- as the {\i Mishna} states, {\i nitcharesh hu o nishtata lo yotzi olamit}, if a husband who married while capable later became deaf or mentally incompetent, he can never divorce her. Since the {\i Mishna} permits giving a {\i get}, it must be that everyone was a {\i cheresh me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- all the marriages were purely rabbinic from the beginning, and no party was ever hearing. This is confirmed: {\i umide\u8217'inun chereshin me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- since the text requires that the men were {\i chereshin me\u8217'ikara} (or else no {\i get} could be given), the women must similarly have been {\i cherashot me\u8217'ikara}. And having established this, the {\i Gemara} reads: {\i ve-katani im hayu nokhriyot yiknesu} \u8212- even so, what is permitted is only {\i yibbum}, {\i yiknesu in, yachaltzu lo} \u8212- {\i yibbum} yes, {\i chalitza} no. {\i Tiyuvta de-Rabba tiyuvta} \u8212- this is a conclusive refutation of Rava. Even when it is perfectly clear that everything is operating within a purely rabbinic framework of marriages of {\i cherashim}, the women may not perform {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ktana v\u8217'Chereshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Different Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} now introduces the case of a man who was married to a {\i ketanah} \u8212- a minor \u8212- and a {\i chereshet}, two wives simultaneously. The {\i chereshet} is an adult; she is a {\i gedolah}. He dies, and his brother now has an obligation of {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitza} toward both women. Both marriages are {\i derabbanan}, but here lies the crux of the problem: though both are rabbinic, they are rabbinic at {\i different levels}. In the previous case, two wives of identical status \u8212- both {\i cherashot} or both {\i ketanot} \u8212- could be treated symmetrically: {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitza} with one exempts the other. But when the two rabbinic marriages are of different quality, that symmetry breaks down, and the surviving brother cannot assume that what he does with one resolves his obligation to the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Without yet explaining the mechanism in full, the {\i Gemara} will shortly assume \u8212- and Rashi at the bottom of the {\i daf} demonstrates \u8212- that one of these marriages has the character of {\i mekudeshet u-meshuyeret}: a quasi-{\i kiddushin}, a partial but definite marriage, roughly described as fifty percent. The other has the character of {\i safek kiddushin} \u8212- {\i mekudeshet ve-einah mekudeshet} \u8212- either one hundred percent married or not married at all. Which type applies to which woman, and how that determination is derived, is what the {\i Gemara} now sets out to establish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman reports: {\i ashkachtei le-Rav Adda bar Ahava u-le-Rav Chana chasneih de-Chana} \u8212- I came upon Rav Adda bar Ahava and Rav Chana his son-in-law {\i de-yasvi kamei kavrei ve-kav\u8217'ei kavasah} \u8212- sitting and posing questions to one another, {\i be-shuka de-Pumbedita}, in the marketplace of Pumbedita. {\i Ve\u8217'amri} \u8212- and they were saying: {\i hazitan ketanah ve-chereshet, ein biat achat mehen poteret tzaratah} \u8212- in the case of a {\i ketanah} and a {\i chereshet}, the act of {\i yibbum} with one of them does not exempt the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Preference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then becomes: under what circumstances does this uncertainty apply? The answer given is: {\i hanei milei de-naflu leih me-ach pikei\u8217'ach} \u8212- this applies when the two women were married to a hearing, adult brother. In that case, {\i de-lo yadenan ei be-katanah nicha leih} \u8212- we do not know which wife he would have preferred. Would he have preferred the minor, {\i d\u8217'asya li-klal de\u8217'ah} \u8212- because she would eventually grow into a full adult, someone he could fully relate to? Or would he have preferred the {\i chereshet}, {\i de-gedolah hi u-vat bi\u8217'ah hi} \u8212- because she is already an adult with whom he could have relations now? The framing \u8212- phrased as which wife the husband would prefer \u8212- is admittedly curious. The more precise reading is a legal one: which marriage does {\i halacha} recognize as the primary, weightier marriage, based on assumptions about what men would generally value. The uncertainty about which marriage carries more weight is what prevents a single act of {\i yibbum} from resolving the obligation to both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative scenario is then proposed: what if both women had been married not to a hearing brother but to a {\i cheresh}? In that case, one might argue {\i vadai be-chereshet nicha leih} \u8212- surely it is clear that a deaf man would prefer his adult deaf wife over a minor. {\i De-vat bi\u8217'ah hi u-vas minei hi} \u8212- she can have relations with him now, and she is, so to speak, his counterpart, similarly situated. So perhaps when the husband was a {\i cheresh}, we should legally assume his marriage to the {\i chereshet} was the weightier one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Still Uncertain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman responds: {\i afilu naflu lo me-achiv cheresh nami mesapka lei} \u8212- even if the women fell to the brother from a deaf husband, I am still uncertain. This is not merely a question of what the husband would have preferred or what assumptions we make about men\u8217's preferences. There is a technical legal difference between {\i nissuei chereshet} and {\i nissuei ketanah} that is independent of who the husband was. It is that structural difference \u8212- the different legal quality of each rabbinic marriage \u8212- that produces the uncertainty, and no assumption about the husband resolves it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Obligation Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then becomes practical: {\i keitzad takanasam} \u8212- how does the surviving brother fulfill his obligation to both women? He has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitza} toward each, but of different qualities, and each affects the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Failed Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider the proposed solutions and why each fails. One might suggest: perform {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} and {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet}. The problem is that {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} \u8212- if the {\i ketanah}\u8217's marriage was the weightier one \u8212- would conclude the entire obligation of {\i yibbum}, leaving no {\i mitzvah} remaining for the {\i chereshet}. So {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet} after {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} would be unwarranted. Now reverse the order: perform {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet} first, and then {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah}. The problem there is that even after {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet}, because we do not know which marriage is stronger, {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} remains possible and perhaps necessary \u8212- and once {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} has occurred, if she were in fact the weightier {\i zikah}, he could not continue living with the {\i chereshet} as a {\i yevamah}, because she would now be the co-wife of a {\i chalutzah}. Regardless of order, the system locks up: one act undermines the other. And the simple escape \u8212- {\i chalitza} with both women \u8212- is unavailable, because a {\i chereshet} cannot perform {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda, citing Rav, provides the solution: {\i koneis et ha-chereshet u-motzi\u8217'ah be-get} \u8212- he performs {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet} through {\i bi\u8217'ah}, and then gives her a {\i get}. {\i Ketanah tamtin ad she-tagdil ve-tachloz} \u8212- the {\i ketanah} then waits until she becomes an adult and performs {\i chalitza}. The elegance of this solution is that it sidesteps the trap. He first performs {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet} and gives her a {\i get}, removing her from the picture entirely before {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} occurs. Once she has received a {\i get} and is gone, the subsequent {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} cannot retroactively undermine the completed relationship. The concern about preventing {\i agunot} motivates the entire construction: the solution is engineered so that no woman is left without recourse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Status Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Quasi, Minor All/No\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From Rav\u8217's solution \u8212- {\i yibbum} followed by a {\i get} for the {\i chereshet}, then {\i chalitza} for the {\i ketanah} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} draws an inference: {\i kasavar Rav chereshet kenuyah u-meshuyeret} \u8212- Rav holds that the marriage to a {\i chereshet} is a quasi-{\i kiddushin}, a definite but partial relationship. {\i Ketanah kenuyah ve-einah kenuyah} \u8212- and the marriage to a {\i ketanah} is an all-or-nothing proposition: either one hundred percent married or not married at all. Counterintuitively, the {\i ketanah}\u8217's marriage is the {\i safek} \u8212- all or nothing \u8212- while the {\i chereshet}\u8217's marriage is the fifty percent. The {\i Gemara} now demonstrates this through a counterfactual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counterfactual Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Di salka da\u8217'atach} \u8212- suppose one assumed the reverse: that the {\i chereshet} is the all-or-nothing {\i safek}, and the {\i ketanah} is the quasi-fifty-percent. If that were so, why would Rav\u8217's solution require {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet} followed by a {\i get}? There would be a simpler solution available: the {\i Gemara} will now show what that simpler solution would be, and how Rav\u8217's deliberate choice of the more complex path reveals his underlying assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If Deaf Were Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i chereshet} were the all-or-nothing {\i safek}, the following solution would present itself: perform {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet} and {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} \u8212- and simply allow him to remain married to the {\i chereshet}, {\i mimah nafshach}, no matter what. Why? Because if she is a full one hundred percent married to his brother, then his {\i yibbum} with her is a complete {\i yibbum de-oraita}, and {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} \u8212- a rabbinic, fifty-percent relationship \u8212- has no power to undermine it. And if she is zero percent \u8212- not married to his brother at all \u8212- then she is not his {\i yevamah}, she is simply a stranger, and again {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} cannot affect his relationship with her. Either way, {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} leaves his relationship with the {\i chereshet} intact. {\i Teishev gabei mimah nafshach} \u8212- she can simply remain with him. There would be no need for a {\i get} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Must Divorce Deaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The very fact that Rav\u8217's solution {\i requires} giving the {\i chereshet} a {\i get} demonstrates that her marriage is not all-or-nothing but fifty percent. Because she is definitely \u8212- if only partially \u8212- his {\i yevamah}, the subsequent {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} can affect his relationship with her. If the {\i ketanah}\u8217's marriage is one hundred percent, then after {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah}, he would have performed {\i chalitza} with a woman who is fully his sister-in-law, and this would undermine his continuing relationship with the {\i chereshet} \u8212- who is herself definitely, if partially, in the {\i yibbum} relationship. Since she is quasi-married and definitely in the picture, a real {\i chalitza} elsewhere can destabilize that quasi-relationship. Therefore, he must first do {\i yibbum} with the {\i chereshet} and give her a {\i get}, resolving her status completely before {\i chalitza} with the {\i ketanah} can occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An objection is raised: why go through the {\i chereshet} first at all? If the {\i ketanah} is all-or-nothing, why not simply perform {\i yibbum} with the {\i ketanah} instead? Either it will be a full {\i de-oraita} {\i yibbum}, or it will be a meaningless act toward a woman who was never his brother\u8217's wife. Either way, there seems to be no problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Must Help Deaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i im kein, chereshet be-mai tipok?} \u8212- yes, but then what happens to the {\i chereshet}? Even if {\i yibbum} with the {\i ketanah} could work in principle, it abandons the {\i chereshet} with no solution. The {\i chereshet} cannot perform {\i chalitza}, and if he has already resolved the obligation through {\i yibbum} with the {\i ketanah}, she has no exit. The solution must begin with the {\i chereshet} precisely because she is the one who cannot do {\i chalitza} and therefore must be helped first. Rav\u8217's solution \u8212- {\i yibbum} and a {\i get} with the {\i chereshet}, followed by {\i chalitza} when the {\i ketanah} grows up \u8212- is thus shown to presuppose that the {\i chereshet}\u8217's marriage is quasi (fifty percent) and the {\i ketanah}\u8217's is all-or-nothing. Rav Sheshet confirms: {\i hachi nami mistavra} \u8212- this is indeed the logical conclusion. It is further demonstrated from the {\i braita} that Rav Chisda explained the {\i halakha} according to Rav precisely on this basis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband of Deaf Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligation to Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is then cited: {\i shnei achim nesu\u8217'im shtei achayot yetomot, ketanah ve-chereshet} \u8212- two brothers married to two sisters, one a {\i ketanah} and one a {\i chereshet}, both marriages rabbinic but of different qualities, as we have now established. The two brothers die in sequence, leaving the surviving brother with obligations to both women, who are sisters of one another. Since the two marriages are of different qualities, neither woman can simply walk away on grounds of {\i achot ishto} \u8212- the interplay between them is precisely what creates the legal complication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Husband Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meit ba\u8217'al ha-ketanah} \u8212- the husband of the {\i ketanah} dies. This is the relatively tractable case. The {\i chereshet} \u8212- who is the surviving brother\u8217's wife \u8212- must be divorced first: {\i chereshet yotzet be-get}. Why must he divorce his own wife? Because the women are sisters, and his obligation toward his {\i yevamah} might be the weightier one; she cannot simply walk away as {\i achot ishto} without some resolution. But if he were to perform {\i chalitza} with her, he would then be unable to continue living with his own wife \u8212- his wife would become {\i achot chalutzato}. And if he were to divorce his wife first and then perform {\i yibbum}, his wife would become {\i achot gerushato} and then {\i achot ishto}. Every path is blocked unless he first divorces his wife cleanly, severs that relationship entirely, and then, once the {\i yevamah} is no longer his wife\u8217's sister, addresses his obligation: {\i ketanah tamtin ad she-tagdil ve-tachloz} \u8212- the {\i ketanah} waits until she becomes an adult and performs {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Husband Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meit ba\u8217'al ha-chereshet} \u8212- the husband of the {\i chereshet} dies. This is the harder case. The same logic applies: he cannot simply do {\i chalitza} with her because she is a {\i chereshet} and cannot perform {\i chalitza}. He cannot perform {\i yibbum} with her while still married to her sister, because the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} would be {\i achot ishto}. And he cannot divorce his wife and then immediately perform {\i yibbum}, because she would be {\i achot gerushato}. So: {\i ketanah yotzet be-get} \u8212- he divorces the {\i ketanah}, severs that relationship. But even so: {\i ve-chereshet asurah lo le-olam} \u8212- the {\i chereshet} remains forbidden to him forever. She cannot perform {\i chalitza}, she cannot be reached through any permitted {\i bi\u8217'ah}, and there is simply no halakhically sanctioned path to resolve her status. She is in a state of complete {\i agunah} with no remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Facto Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i braita} adds a post-facto provision: {\i ve\u8217'im ba al ha-chereshet noten lah get ve-hutrah} \u8212- if he did have relations with the {\i chereshet} \u8212- which he was not permitted to do, since it was {\i achot ishto} \u8212- he may now give her a {\i get} and she is released. The logic: if his obligation to the {\i chereshet} was the stronger one, then despite the transgression, the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} was functionally an act of {\i yibbum}, and a {\i get} can now dissolve it. This is a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} solution only, but it does resolve her status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biah Pesulah Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Doesn\u8217't Work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now uses the {\i braita} to prove once again \u8212- this time from the {\i siyata} of the {\i braita} \u8212- that the {\i chereshet} is the fifty-percent relationship and the {\i ketanah} is the all-or-nothing. {\i Ai amarta bishlama chereshet kenuyah meshuyeret ketanah kenuyah ve-einah kenuyah} \u8212- if the {\i chereshet} is the fifty percent and the {\i ketanah} is the all-or-nothing, then the {\i bedi\u8217'avad} case makes sense. When he sleeps with the {\i chereshet}, {\i mimah nafshach}: if the {\i ketanah} was one hundred percent married to his brother, then she \u8212- the {\i chereshet}\u8217's sister \u8212- was {\i achot ishto de-oraita}, not subject to {\i yibbum} at all, and walks away; the {\i chereshet}, having been dealt with through illicit {\i bi\u8217'ah}, can now receive a {\i get} and be released. And if the {\i ketanah} was zero percent, then she was never his {\i yevamah} at all, she is {\i nochreit be-alma}, and his {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i chereshet} was an uncomplicated act of {\i yibbum}, after which a {\i get} works perfectly well. Either way, the {\i chereshet} can be freed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now suppose the reverse: the {\i chereshet} is the all-or-nothing and the {\i ketanah} is the fifty percent. In that case, {\i ki ba la-chereshet, amai noten lah get ve-hutrah?} \u8212- why should sleeping with the {\i chereshet} enable him to give her a {\i get} and free her? Because if the {\i chereshet} is either zero percent (in which case there is nothing to resolve) or one hundred percent (in which case she is his full {\i de-oraita} {\i yevamah}), and if she is one hundred percent, then his {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her was a {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah} \u8212- a forbidden act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- because he was at the same time definitely, fifty-percent quasi-married to her sister. A {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah} does not complete {\i yibbum}; it does not permit a {\i get} to substitute for {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biah Pesulah Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bi\u8217'ah pesulah} is the halakhic category for a case in which the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} that would normally constitute {\i yibbum} is rendered invalid by circumstances that make it forbidden. Here the circumstances are that he is quasi-definitely married to the co-wife, the {\i chereshet}\u8217's sister, making the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i chereshet} the act of sleeping with his wife\u8217's sister. The exact contours of {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah} are a matter of discussion in Rashi and Tosafot, but for present purposes the operative principle is clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biah Pesulah No Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah} has occurred, the {\i Gemara} rules {\i de-Rabbanan} that it does not count as completed {\i yibbum}. A {\i get} alone is insufficient to release the woman; she still requires {\i chalitza}. Since {\i chalitza} is unavailable to the {\i chereshet}, the reverse scenario \u8212- where the {\i chereshet} is all-or-nothing and the {\i ketanah} is fifty percent \u8212- would leave the {\i chereshet} with no solution even {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. But the {\i braita} states that {\i bedi\u8217'avad} there is a solution. This proves that the reverse scenario is not the correct one: the {\i chereshet} must be the fifty percent and the {\i ketanah} the all-or-nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nechemiah View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, Rav Nechemiah enters the picture. Rav Nechemiah had ruled that {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah poteret} \u8212- that even a forbidden act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} is sufficient to permit a subsequent {\i get}, without requiring {\i chalitza}. If one adopts Rav Nechemiah\u8217's position, then the argument above collapses: even if the {\i chereshet} were all-or-nothing and the {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her were a {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah}, she could still receive a {\i get} and be freed. One could therefore maintain the opposite assignment \u8212- {\i chereshet} as all-or-nothing, {\i ketanah} as fifty percent \u8212- and still explain the {\i braita}\u8217's {\i bedi\u8217'avad} ruling. So the {\i braita}\u8217's conclusion can no longer be taken as proof of Rav\u8217's position unless Rav Nechemiah is set aside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Assumes Rav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve this, the {\i Gemara} turns to the {\i seifa} of the {\i braita}: {\i mi she-nasui shtei yetomot, ketanah ve-chereshet} \u8212- a man married to a {\i ketanah} and a {\i chereshet}, who then dies, and the brother-in-law comes. The {\i braita} describes the following: {\i u-va yavam al ha-ketanah ve-chazar u-va al ha-chereshet} \u8212- first {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i ketanah}, then {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i chereshet} (or another brother performed the second {\i bi\u8217'ah}); and similarly the reverse scenario. In both cases, {\i ne\u8217'esru shteihen alav} \u8212- both women become forbidden to him; he must divorce both. {\i Keitzad takanatan? Chereshet yotzet be-get ve-ketanah tamtin ad she-tagdil ve-tachloz} \u8212- the {\i chereshet} receives a {\i get}, and the {\i ketanah} waits until she grows and performs {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The crucial question: why does the {\i ketanah} require {\i chalitza} rather than simply a {\i get}? If it were Rav Nechemiah\u8217's position \u8212- {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah} works \u8212- then whichever {\i bi\u8217'ah} occurred second would have resolved the obligation, and a {\i get} for both women should suffice. The fact that the {\i braita} insists on {\i chalitza} for the {\i ketanah} can only be explained if we assume that: (a) the {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i ketanah} was a {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah}, and (b) {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah} does not suffice. {\i Bi\u8217'ah pesulah} arises for the {\i ketanah} only if, at the time of the {\i bi\u8217'ah} with her, he was definitely quasi-married to her sister \u8212- i.e., the {\i chereshet} must be the fifty-percent marriage. The {\i braita} thus operates on Rav\u8217's assumptions, not Rav Nechemiah\u8217's. {\i Ela shema mina rabanan hi, shema mina} \u8212- conclude from this that the {\i braita} follows the position of the {\i Rabbanan}, and Rav\u8217's framework is confirmed: the {\i chereshet} is the quasi-fifty-percent marriage and the {\i ketanah} is the all-or-nothing {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same Category Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through the underlying principles at length, the {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i Mishnah} itself, much of whose content has already been clarified in the preceding {\i sugya}. The {\i Mishnah} presents a series of cases based on the relationship between the two wives of the deceased brother \u8212- whether they are of the same legal category or of different categories, and what the result is when the surviving brother performs {\i yibbum} with one and then has relations with the other. The first cases are the symmetric ones: when both women are of the same type \u8212- both {\i ketanot} or both {\i cherashot} \u8212- so that each marriage carries equal weight. In that case, the first act of {\i yibbum} is definitive; it fully resolves the obligation as between equals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor First, Deaf Second\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba yavam al ha-ketanah ve-chazar u-va al ha-chereshet} \u8212- the surviving brother first performed {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i ketanah}, then with the {\i chereshet}. Or his brother performed the second {\i bi\u8217'ah}. Because the two marriages are of different qualities \u8212- and we do not know which is the weightier one \u8212- the second {\i bi\u8217'ah}, whichever marriage it strengthens, threatens to undermine the first. He is therefore in the position of having impaired his relationship with the first woman. {\i Pasal et ha-ketanah} \u8212- his relationship with the {\i ketanah} is ruined. Both women become forbidden to him, and he must divorce both and send them away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf First, Minor Second\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse case: {\i ba yavam al ha-chereshet ve-chazar u-va al ha-ketanah} \u8212- first {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i chereshet}, then with the {\i ketanah} (or a brother with the {\i ketanah}). Same logic applies: because neither marriage can be confirmed as the weightier, each subsequent act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} risks overturning the prior one. {\i Pasal et ha-chereshet} \u8212- his relationship with the {\i chereshet} is similarly rendered invalid, and both women are forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clear Hierarchy Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then considers the case where the two marriages are of {\i clearly} different halakhic weight \u8212- no genuine uncertainty about which is stronger. Two scenarios: a {\i pikachat} (hearing adult woman) and a {\i chereshet}, where the {\i pikachat}\u8217's marriage is {\i de-oraita} and the {\i chereshet}\u8217's is {\i derabbanan}; or a {\i gedolah} (adult) and a {\i ketanah}, where the {\i gedolah}\u8217's marriage is {\i de-oraita} and the {\i ketanah}\u8217's is {\i derabbanan}. In both pairings, one marriage is clearly and unambiguously stronger than the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stronger First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba yavam al ha-pikachat ve-chazar u-va al ha-chereshet} \u8212- if he began with the hearing woman, performing a full {\i de-oraita yibbum}, and then had relations with the {\i chereshet}: {\i lo pasal et ha-pikachat} \u8212- the second act does not impair the first. Once a full {\i de-oraita yibbum} has been completed, nothing in the rabbinic domain can undo it. The same applies to {\i gedolah} before {\i ketanah}: {\i lo pasal et ha-gedolah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weaker First, Stronger S\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba yavam al ha-chereshet ve-chazar u-va al ha-pikachat} \u8212- if he began with the {\i chereshet} (the rabbinic marriage) and then had relations with the {\i pikachat}, or if his brother did: {\i pasal et ha-chereshet} \u8212- this does impair his relationship with the first woman. The second act was a stronger, {\i de-oraita} act of {\i yibbum}, which retroactively overshadows the weaker rabbinic relationship. Even if the {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i pikachat} involved some element of {\i bi\u8217'ah pesulah} \u8212- since he was in some relationship with her co-wife \u8212- it certainly works {\i de-oraita} and now undermines the prior weaker relationship. The same logic applies symmetrically to {\i ketanah} and {\i gedolah}: beginning with the {\i ketanah} and then having relations with the {\i gedolah}, {\i pasal et ha-ketanah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mi\u8217'un Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Mi\u8217'un\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Elazar omer: melamdin et ha-ketanah she-tima\u8217'en lo} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer says: we instruct the {\i ketanah} to perform {\i mi\u8217'un}, to refuse the marriage. This is the solution that cuts through the entire tangle. Rather than navigating the maze of competing {\i yibbum} obligations with a {\i ketanah} who cannot perform {\i chalitza} until she grows up, and whose status introduces all the complications explored above, the rabbinic authorities teach the {\i ketanah} to exercise her right of {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- retroactive refusal of her minority marriage \u8212- which dissolves her marriage as if it never took place and removes her from the {\i yibbum} equation entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakha K\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel: halakha ke-Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: the {\i halakha} follows Rabbi Eliezer. And so too ruled Rabbi Elazar the {\i Amora}: {\i halakha ke-Rabbi Elazar ha-Tanna}. When there is a {\i ketanah} involved, we instruct her to perform {\i mi\u8217'un}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mi\u8217'un in This Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this ruling was stated in two different contexts \u8212- here, and also in an earlier passage ({\i 109a}) involving two brothers married to two sisters. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i utzricha} \u8212- why is it necessary to state this ruling in both places? Could one not have derived the rule for one case from the other? The {\i Gemara} explains the necessity. In the earlier case \u8212- two brothers married to two sisters, where the {\i ketanah} is peripheral to the {\i yibbum} obligation because the {\i achot ishto} dimension dominates \u8212- one might have said: the reason we tell the {\i ketanah} to do {\i mi\u8217'un} is {\i mishum de-lo kayma mitzvat yibbum}, because there is no genuine {\i mitzvat yibbum} at stake. The {\i ketanah} is in the picture only because of the complication of the sisters; she is not a proper candidate for {\i yibbum} in the ordinary sense. In such a case, we may encourage {\i mi\u8217'un} because the {\i yibbum} framework barely applies to her. But here \u8212- where {\i yibbum} is genuinely operative, and where he has already performed {\i bi\u8217'ah} with both women \u8212- one might have said we do not encourage {\i mi\u8217'un} after the fact; rather, we deal with the situation through {\i get} and {\i chalitza} as appropriate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both in Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if the ruling had been stated only in the current case, one might have reasoned differently. Here there are two women both genuinely within the {\i yibbum} framework, and the adult\u8217's obligation is clearly in play: {\i mishum de-gedolah ramya kamei} \u8212- since the stronger obligation is the adult {\i yevamah}, it makes sense to have the {\i ketanah} step aside through {\i mi\u8217'un} so that the {\i gedolah}\u8217's {\i yibbum} can proceed cleanly. But in the earlier case involving sisters \u8212- where both women have a complex and entangled status \u8212- one might have thought that {\i mi\u8217'un} would not be encouraged. Since each case presents a different reason why one might have hesitated to rule like Rabbi Eliezer, and since in each case there is a reason one might have said Rabbi Eliezer does not apply, both rulings are necessary. {\i Tzricha}. When a {\i ketanah} is involved in a {\i yibbum} scenario, we instruct her to perform {\i mi\u8217'un}, and this is the operative {\i halakha} in both types of cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 112\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin today with the {\i mishnah} that opens the new {\i perek} of {\i cheresh}, found on 112b. Much of this {\i mishnah} has already appeared in pieces throughout the earlier {\i perakim}, so we move through it with some dispatch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh & Pika\u8217'chat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i cheresh shenasa pika\u8217'chat} \u8212- a deaf person who married a hearing woman \u8212- {\i upike\u8217'ach shenasa chereshet} \u8212- or a hearing person who married a deaf woman \u8212- {\i im ratza lehotzi yotzi ve\u8217'im ratza lekayem yekayem} \u8212- if he wishes to divorce he may do so, and if he wishes to maintain the marriage he may do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is that where one party to the marriage is a {\i cheresh} \u8212- a deaf-mute \u8212- that person is not considered a {\i bar da\u8217'at} in the halachic sense, and therefore there is no {\i kidushin} at the biblical level. The {\i kidushin} is purely {\i derabbanan}. But the same rabbinic framework that creates the marriage also authorizes its dissolution. {\i Keshem shekones biremizah kach hu motzi biremizah} \u8212- just as the marriage was contracted through gestures, so too the divorce may be effected through gestures. The rabbis who established the institution of rabbinic marriage likewise sanction the rabbinic divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Became Deaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses a different scenario: {\i pike\u8217'ach shenasa pika\u8217'chat venistacharesha} \u8212- a hearing man who married a hearing woman, and she subsequently became deaf. In this case the {\i kidushin} was {\i de\u8217'oraita} from the outset, since both parties were fully competent at the time of marriage. Nevertheless, {\i im ratza yotzi ve\u8217'im ratza yekayem} \u8212- the husband may divorce her or maintain the marriage \u8212- because divorce is a unilateral act performed by the husband. So long as he retains his status as a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, he is capable of issuing a valid {\i get}, regardless of whether she has since lost hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Shotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i nishtatit lo yotzi} \u8212- if she became a {\i shotah}, mentally incompetent, he may not divorce her. This is a rabbinic enactment designed to protect her from exploitation. A woman who cannot reason for herself and cannot protect herself from sexual abuse or predation must remain bound within a marriage that affords her some shelter and protection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Cheresh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse situation is far more severe. {\i Nitcharesh hu o nishtateh} \u8212- if the husband became a {\i cheresh} or a {\i shoteh} after the marriage \u8212- {\i eino motzi olamit} \u8212- he can never divorce her. The {\i get} must be issued by the husband, and since he is no longer a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, he lacks the legal capacity to do so, however capable he may be in a purely practical sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asymmetry in Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan\u8217's Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records the challenge posed by Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri: {\i mipnei mah ha\u8217'ishah shenitcharesha yotza veha\u8217'ish shenitcharesh eino motzi?} \u8212- Why is it that when she becomes deaf after a {\i de\u8217'oraita} marriage she can still be divorced, yet when he becomes deaf he can never divorce her? The asymmetry demands explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agent vs Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response is crisp: {\i eino domeh ha\u8217'ish megeresh la\u8217'ishah mitgareshet} \u8212- the man who divorces is not comparable to the woman who is divorced. {\i Ha\u8217'ishah yotza lirtzonah veshela lirtzonah} \u8212- a woman is divorced whether she wills it or not; she is in the role of the recipient, the object of the act, not its agent. Since her own {\i da\u8217'at} is not required for the divorce to take effect, the fact that she has lost it is irrelevant. {\i Veha\u8217'ish eino motzi ela lirtzono} \u8212- the man, by contrast, divorces only through his own will and legal agency. He must be a {\i bar da\u8217'at} to do so. Once he has lost that status, the avenue of divorce is permanently closed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Gudgada\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada then offers testimony: {\i he\u8217'id Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada al hachereshet shehishi\u8217'ah aviha sheyotza beget} \u8212- he testified concerning a deaf-mute woman whose father had contracted her marriage while she was a minor. Because the father effected the {\i kidushin}, and the father was a fully competent agent, the marriage had {\i de\u8217'oraita} standing \u8212- even though the woman herself was a {\i chereshet}. And yet the {\i gemara} affirms that she may be divorced through a {\i get}. Here again, since she is the recipient rather than the agent of the divorce, her status as a {\i chereshet} presents no obstacle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} observes something striking about the phrasing {\i amru lo af zu kayotza bah} \u8212- \u8220"they said to him, this case is likewise.\u8221" {\i Tosafot} reads this as indicating that the Sages actually summoned Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada to the {\i beit midrash} \u8212- {\i hevi\u8217'u levet hamidrash lehavi re\u8217'ayah ledivreihem} \u8212- he was not ordinarily among those present, but they brought him specifically to use his testimony as evidence for their position. It is a vivid picture of the Sages marshalling a witness to clinch an argument.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Brothers Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now works through the many permutations of two brothers married to two sisters, where deafness is distributed variously among the parties. Because the {\i mishnah} introduces the cases systematically, we follow its structure. First: {\i shnei achim chereshim nesu\u8217'im lishtei achayot} \u8212- two deaf brothers married to two sisters, whether both sisters are hearing ({\i pikchot}), both deaf ({\i cherashot}), or one of each. Similarly, where both sisters are deaf and the brothers vary, or where one brother is deaf and one is hearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In all of these configurations \u8212- as long as both brothers are involved and the sisters are sisters \u8212- {\i harei elu peturot min hachalitzah umin hayibum}: the surviving widow is exempt from both {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}. This is because she is in a relationship of {\i achot isha} \u8212- wife\u8217's sister \u8212- with the surviving brother, and that prohibition operates here. Moreover, since all the relevant marriages are rabbinic, the entire structure operates on a single plane. If the women were unrelated ({\i nochriyot}), then {\i yibum} would be possible, and even a subsequent {\i get} \u8212- though {\i chalitzah} remains unavailable whenever one of the parties to that ceremony is a {\i cheresh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Each\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We turn now to the case of {\i shnei achim echad cheresh ve\u8217'echad pikeach} \u8212- one deaf brother, one hearing brother \u8212- married to two sisters, both of whom are hearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met cheresh ba\u8217'al hapikechat} \u8212- the deaf brother, who was married to the hearing woman, dies. The hearing brother is now faced with his wife\u8217's sister, a relationship of {\i achot isha}. But his own marriage is a {\i de\u8217'oraita kidushin} \u8212- he and his wife are both fully competent. So {\i tetzei mishum achot isha} \u8212- she walks away on the basis of the biblical prohibition of wife\u8217's sister. Straightforward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met pikeach ba\u8217'al hapikechat} \u8212- now the hearing brother dies. The surviving deaf brother finds himself in the position of owing {\i yibum} to his brother\u8217's widow. But that woman is also his own wife\u8217's sister, creating the problem of {\i achot isha}. However, his own marriage was only {\i derabbanan}. So at the {\i de\u8217'oraita} level, she is not truly his wife\u8217's sister, but rabbinically she is. He can neither perform {\i yibum} \u8212- that would be relations with his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- nor dismiss her through {\i chalitzah}, since he is a {\i cheresh} and {\i chalitzah} requires full competence from both parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stuck\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is expressed starkly: {\i motzi et ishto beget ve\u8217'eshet achiv asurah lo le\u8217'olam} \u8212- he must divorce his own wife (since he cannot maintain that marriage while a {\i zikah} \u8212- levirate bond \u8212- connects him to her sister), and his brother\u8217's wife is forbidden to him forever. He is caught: he must relinquish his own wife, yet he cannot release or acquire his sister-in-law. This is one of the most severe results the {\i mishnah} presents.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Hearing Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now consider {\i shnei achim pikchim nesu\u8217'im lishtei achayot} \u8212- two hearing brothers married to two sisters, but here the sisters differ: one is a {\i chereshet}, one is a {\i pikachat}. {\i Met pikeach ba\u8217'al hachereshet} \u8212- the brother married to the deaf sister dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister-in-Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The surviving brother is married to the hearing sister. He now faces his wife\u8217's sister as his potential {\i yevamah}. Since his own marriage is fully {\i de\u8217'oraita}, the prohibition of {\i achot isha} applies with full biblical force \u8212- {\i tetzei mishum achot isha}. The widow walks away without {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met pikeach ba\u8217'al hapikechat} \u8212- the other scenario: the brother married to the hearing sister dies. The surviving brother is married to the deaf woman \u8212- a rabbinic marriage. He now has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with the hearing sister-in-law. His own marriage, being only {\i derabbanan}, does not create a biblical {\i achot isha} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He therefore divorces his own wife \u8212- he cannot remain with her given the {\i zikah} \u8212- and then has recourse to {\i chalitzah} with the sister-in-law. Since he is hearing, {\i chalitzah} is available to him. {\i Ve\u8217'eshet achiv bechalitzah} \u8212- he performs {\i chalitzah} and releases her properly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixed Permutations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Mixed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final configuration involving two sisters: {\i shnei achim echad cheresh ve\u8217'echad pikeach} married to {\i shtei achayot achat chereshet ve\u8217'achat pikachat}, with the deaf brother married to the deaf woman and the hearing brother to the hearing woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met cheresh b\u8217'chereshet} \u8212- the deaf man married to the deaf woman dies. What does the hearing brother do? He is biblically married to his wife; the woman now falling into {\i zikah} is his wife\u8217's sister. {\i Tetzei mishum achot isha} \u8212- she walks away. Clean case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met pikeach b\u8217'pikachat} \u8212- the hearing man married to the hearing woman dies. Now the deaf man is faced with {\i yibum} obligations toward his brother\u8217's widow (a {\i de\u8217'oraita} obligation, since the deceased was biblically married). He must divorce his own wife \u8212- since maintaining that marriage while his brother\u8217's wife is bound to him by {\i zikah} would constitute {\i achot zekukato}. Yet he cannot perform {\i chalitzah} because he is a {\i cheresh}, and he cannot perform {\i yibum} because it would constitute relations with his ex-wife\u8217's sister. {\i Ve\u8217'eshet achiv asurah le\u8217'olam} \u8212- his brother\u8217's wife is forever forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unrelated Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now move to cases of two brothers married to two {\i nochriyot} \u8212- unrelated women \u8212- so the problem of {\i achot isha} does not arise. One brother is a {\i cheresh}, one is a {\i pikeach}; both women are hearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met cheresh b\u8217'pikachat} \u8212- the deaf brother dies. His widow enters {\i zikah} with the hearing brother. This is a rabbinic obligation of {\i yibum} \u8212- since the deceased\u8217's {\i kidushin} was only {\i derabbanan}. Since the women are unrelated, the hearing brother may do either {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum} as he chooses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met pikeach b\u8217'pikachat} \u8212- the hearing brother dies. Now it is the deaf brother who faces {\i yibum} with his brother\u8217's widow. This is a {\i de\u8217'oraita} obligation of {\i yibum} \u8212- the deceased was biblically married. The women are unrelated, so there is no {\i achot isha} complication. However, the {\i cheresh} cannot perform {\i chalitzah}. His only avenue is {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permanent Bond\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the {\i mishnah} delivers a conclusion that is both precise and jarring: {\i kones ve\u8217'eino motzi le\u8217'olam} \u8212- he performs {\i yibum}, and can never divorce her afterward. This is because {\i yibum} is a {\i de\u8217'oraita} act \u8212- it carries over the biblical {\i kidushin} from the deceased brother \u8212- and since the {\i cheresh} is not a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, he cannot issue a valid {\i get}. The contrast with the opening case of the {\i mishnah} is intentional and essential. When a {\i cheresh} initiates a marriage, it is a rabbinic {\i kidushin} that can be dissolved rabbinically. But when a {\i cheresh} performs {\i yibum}, the bond he acquires is {\i de\u8217'oraita}, and he is thereafter permanently unable to sever it. The woman is truly bound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- raised by a student\u8217's question \u8212- is the central halachic point threading through all these permutations: rabbinic marriage, rabbinic divorce; biblical {\i yibum}, no divorce possible. And for the woman who has no access to {\i chalitzah} and whose husband-by-{\i yibum} cannot write a {\i get}, the situation is one of permanent inescapable attachment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final set of cases: {\i shnei achim pikchim} \u8212- two hearing brothers \u8212- married to two unrelated women, one hearing and one a {\i chereshet}. {\i Met pikeach b\u8217'chereshet} \u8212- the brother married to the deaf woman dies. The surviving hearing brother faces {\i yibum} with the {\i chereshet}. He cannot perform {\i chalitzah} because she is deaf and {\i chalitzah} requires full participation from both parties. So {\i kones} \u8212- he does {\i yibum} \u8212- and {\i ve\u8217'im ratza lehotzi yotzi} \u8212- he may then give her a {\i get} if he wishes, since he is a hearing man and the {\i de\u8217'oraita} rule permits a hearing husband to divorce even a deaf wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met pikeach b\u8217'pikachat} \u8212- the other scenario: the brother married to the hearing woman dies. Now the surviving hearing brother, who is married to the deaf woman, faces {\i yibum} with the hearing sister-in-law. Since she is unrelated to his own wife, and since he is fully competent, he has all options: {\i o choletz o meyabem} \u8212- either {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final permutation with one hearing and one deaf brother and one of each type of woman confirms the same logic. When the {\i cheresh} married to the {\i chereshet} dies and the hearing man faces {\i yibum} with the {\i chereshet}: {\i kones, ve\u8217'im ratza lehotzi yotzi} \u8212- does {\i yibum}, can subsequently divorce. When the hearing man married to the hearing woman dies and the {\i cheresh} faces {\i yibum} with the {\i chereshet}: {\i kones ve\u8217'eino motzi le\u8217'olam} \u8212- does {\i yibum}, can never divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entire architecture of these cases ultimately rests on two pillars established at the {\i mishnah}\u8217's outset: a woman need not be a {\i bat da\u8217'at} for a {\i get} to be valid; and a {\i cheresh} who performs {\i yibum} acquires a {\i de\u8217'oraita} bond he can never dissolve.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Institutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to a beautiful expository passage, initiated by Rami bar Chama: {\i mah shena cheresh v\u8217'chereshet d\u8217'tikinu lehu rabanan nisu\u8217'in, u\u8217'mah shena shoteh v\u8217'shotah d\u8217'lo tikinu lehu rabanan nisu\u8217'in?} What is the difference between a {\i cheresh} and a {\i chereshet}, for whom the Rabbis did institute a framework of rabbinic {\i nisu\u8217'in}, and a {\i shoteh} and {\i shotah}, for whom they did not? The {\i gemara} knows this distinction is real because a {\i baraita} teaches explicitly that if a {\i shoteh} or a minor married women and those women died childless, the widows are exempt from {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} entirely \u8212- meaning even rabbinic {\i nisu\u8217'in} was never established for them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Institution Stability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i cheresh v\u8217'chereshet dekayma takeinta derabanan, tikinu lehu rabanan nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- for a deaf man or woman, the rabbinic institution will hold; therefore they established it. {\i Shoteh v\u8217'shotah d\u8217'lo kayma takeinta derabanan} \u8212- for someone mentally incompetent, the institution will not hold, because {\i ein adam dar im nachash bikfifah} \u8212- a person cannot live with a snake under the same roof. Life with a mentally unstable person is so fraught that any institution of marriage built upon it would be inherently fragile and unworkable. The rabbis therefore declined to create the institution at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises the obvious question: {\i u\u8217'mai shna katan d\u8217'lo tikinu rabanan nisuin?} Why was no rabbinic marriage instituted for a minor boy? Unlike a {\i shoteh}, a minor boy presents no danger of an unstable household \u8212- he will grow up. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i cheresh d\u8217'lo ati lichlal nisuin, tikinu rabanan nisuin} \u8212- for a {\i cheresh}, who will never on his own arrive at the capacity for biblical marriage, the Rabbis created a rabbinic substitute, lest he be entirely excluded from married life. Rashi adds that the concern is also {\i pru u\u8217'rvu} \u8212- enabling him to fulfill the spirit of procreation, even if not as a formal {\i mitzvah} obligation given his status. {\i Katan d\u8217'ati lichlal nisuin, lo tikinu rabanan nisuin} \u8212- a minor boy who will grow up and become capable of {\i de\u8217'oraita} marriage needs no rabbinic institution. The answer is simple: wait. There is no urgency, no institutional gap to fill.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Contrast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i u\u8217'mai shna ktana d\u8217'asya lichlal nisuin, ve\u8217'takinu rabanan nisuin?} A minor girl will also eventually mature into the capacity for biblical marriage \u8212- so why did the Rabbis establish rabbinic {\i kidushin} for her? The answer: {\i hasam shelo yinhagu bah minhag hefker} \u8212- so that she not be treated as ownerless, that is, to protect a girl without a father from sexual exploitation. Without a husband, such a girl is dangerously vulnerable. Establishing a marital framework \u8212- even a rabbinic one \u8212- provides her a measure of protection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then poses a further contrast: {\i u\u8217'mai shna ktana d\u8217'memaenes, u\u8217'mai shna chereshet d\u8217'lo memaana?} Why does a {\i ktana} have the right of {\i miun} \u8212- the option to refuse and nullify the marriage simply by declaring she does not want it \u8212- while a {\i chereshet} does not? The answer: {\i de\u8217'im ken mimni ve\u8217'lo nasbi lah} \u8212- because if a {\i chereshet} could exercise {\i miun} indefinitely throughout the marriage, no one would want to marry her. The uncertainty would be too great. For a {\i ktana}, by contrast, the window of {\i miun} is time-limited; once she reaches maturity the marriage becomes fully binding and the right of {\i miun} lapses. That temporal limit makes the institution workable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues: {\i u\u8217'mai shna ktana d\u8217'achla b\u8217'terumah, u\u8217'mai shna chereshet d\u8217'lo achla b\u8217'terumah?} Why may a minor girl married to a {\i kohen} eat {\i terumah}, while a {\i chereshet} married to a {\i kohen} may not? The {\i gemara} establishes this from the testimony of Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada, who testified about a {\i chereshet} whose father had married her off ({\i al hachereshet shehishi\u8217'ah aviha}) \u8212- that she may be divorced with a {\i get} \u8212- and about a minor girl married to a {\i kohen} that she eats {\i terumah}. The implicit contrast is that a {\i chereshet} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains the asymmetry through {\i gezeirah}: {\i gezeirah shema yashi cheresh b\u8217'chereshet} \u8212- we do not permit the {\i chereshet} married to a hearing {\i kohen} to eat {\i terumah} because of concern that people will then allow the same when she is married to a deaf {\i kohen}. But if she were married to a {\i cheresh kohen}, the marriage would be only rabbinic \u8212- a valid concern. One might counter: even so, let her eat {\i terumah}, since it is only a rabbinic prohibition and she is not personally obligated in {\i mitzvot}; just as we say {\i katan ochel neveilot hi} \u8212- a minor eating something forbidden is not subject to the same strictures as an adult. But the {\i gemara} pushes further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper concern is {\i gezeirah shema yashi cheresh b\u8217'pikachat} \u8212- we fear that if we permit a {\i chereshet} married to a hearing {\i kohen} to eat {\i terumah}, it will lead people to permit the reverse: a hearing woman married to a deaf {\i kohen} eating {\i terumah}. And that woman is an adult obligated in {\i mitzvot}, eating food that is forbidden to her. Even if one argues that at least the rabbinic {\i terumah} should be permissible \u8212- since we waived certain rabbinic concerns in the case of the {\i ktana} \u8212- the {\i gemara} says no: {\i gezeirah dilma ati le\u8217'meichal b\u8217'terumah d\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- we fear the slide from rabbinic {\i terumah} to biblical {\i terumah}. Once such a woman is in the habit of eating {\i terumah}, it will easily extend to eating produce to which the {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition applies, and she, as a fully obligated adult, would be committing a biblical violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the same {\i gezeirah} does not apply to the {\i ktana} is subtle. Rashi explains: in the case of the {\i cheresh}, both the marriage of a {\i chereshet} to a hearing man and the reverse \u8212- a hearing woman to a deaf {\i kohen} \u8212- involve the same rabbinic {\i kidushin}; the cases are structurally parallel and people will naturally move between them. But with minors, no rabbinic {\i kidushin} exists for a minor {\i boy} \u8212- only for a minor {\i girl}. Since the institution is not symmetrical, the slide to the mirror-image case does not occur. (The only exception would be a minor boy who performs {\i yibum}, but that is an entirely different mechanism.)\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah & Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} now establishes: {\i cheresh v\u8217'shoteh she\u8217'nas\u8217'u pikchot} \u8212- a {\i cheresh} or a {\i shoteh} who married hearing women \u8212- {\i ein lahem aleihem klum} \u8212- have no {\i ketubah} obligations at all toward those wives. Even though the Rabbis created a framework of rabbinic {\i kidushin} and {\i nisu\u8217'in} for the {\i cheresh}, they did not extend that institution to include a {\i ketubah}. The {\i shoteh} has no rabbinic {\i nisu\u8217'in} at all, so the absence of a {\i ketubah} follows trivially. But the {\i cheresh} case is more pointed: he has a recognized rabbinic marriage, yet no {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i v\u8217'ratzu lekayem yesh lahem ketubah} \u8212- if they subsequently recover \u8212- the {\i cheresh} regaining hearing, the {\i shoteh} regaining mental competence \u8212- and they choose to remain married, then a {\i ketubah} obligation now applies, since the marriage is now {\i de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voluntary Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse case: {\i pikeach she\u8217'nasa chereshet o shotah} \u8212- a hearing, fully competent man who married a deaf or mentally incompetent woman. Here the Rabbis established no institution of {\i ketubah} either. However, {\i afilo kasav lah mea maneh kesuvasah kayemes} \u8212- if he chose to write a {\i ketubah} for her, even for a very large sum, it is binding. Because he is a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, his voluntary assumption of a financial obligation is fully valid. The formula the {\i gemara} uses to explain this is {\i mipnei she\u8217'ratza lazuk binchasav} \u8212- because he wished to encumber his estate; that is entirely his prerogative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the Rabbis did not institute a {\i ketubah} for the deaf woman is the same consideration raised earlier: {\i de\u8217'im kein, mimne velo nasbi lah} \u8212- lest the {\i ketubah} obligation make her unmarriageable, the Rabbis deliberately did not create it. Making her easier to marry outweighs giving her the standard protection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a sharp question: when it is the {\i cheresh} who is the husband and the hearing woman who is the wife \u8212- why do we not insist that she at least have the normal {\i ketubah}? We said we waive the {\i ketubah} for the deaf woman to incentivize men to marry her. Fine. But by equally withholding the {\i ketubah} when he is the {\i cheresh}, are we not creating a disincentive for a hearing woman to marry him? The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer: {\i yoter mishe\u8217'ish rotzeh liso, ishah rotza lehinase} \u8212- more than a man wishes to marry, a woman wishes to be married. Since women are more desirous of marriage, no additional incentive is needed to attract a woman to marry even a {\i cheresh}, and therefore no rabbinic {\i ketubah} is established. This does not, however, adequately explain why she receives no protection at all \u8212- why not at least the standard protection? The {\i gemara} does not fully resolve this, and the most one can say is that since the {\i cheresh} is not considered a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, imposing a financial obligation on him presents difficulties \u8212- though that reasoning also sits uneasily, since we trust him enough to establish the marriage in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Malkio\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records a practical episode: there was a {\i cheresh} in the neighborhood of Rav Malkio, who married a woman and wrote her a substantial {\i ketubah} of 400 {\i zuz} from his own estate. Rav Malkio permitted this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Praise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava greeted this ruling with admiration: {\i man chacham keRav Malkio} \u8212- who is as wise as Rav Malkio? The reasoning: {\i ilu ratza shifcha leshamsho mi lo zavninan leh} \u8212- if this {\i cheresh} wanted to acquire a maidservant to attend to his domestic needs, we would clearly recognize that transaction as binding, since we allow him to engage in financial dealings necessary for the conduct of his life. A {\i ketubah} that creates a similar financial bond \u8212- and that motivates a woman to care for him as his wife \u8212- is {\i kal va\u8217'chomer} appropriate. The fact that this creates a genuine financial obligation on him is precisely the point: Rav Malkio understood that enabling the {\i cheresh} to voluntarily undertake such an obligation serves both his dignity and the practical stability of the marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deaf Status Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Asham Talui\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots to a fundamental question about the halachic status of the {\i cheresh} himself. Rav Chiyya bar Ashi said in the name of Shmuel: {\i eishes cheresh ein chayavin aleha asham talui} \u8212- the wife of a {\i cheresh}, if another man cohabits with her, does not give rise to an {\i asham talui} \u8212- the sacrifice brought for doubtful commission of a {\i de\u8217'oraita} sin. The implication is significant: the {\i kidushin} of a {\i cheresh} is not even a doubtful {\i de\u8217'oraita} bond; it is purely rabbinic, and so no adultery at the biblical level \u8212- not even a doubtful one \u8212- has been committed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to support this position from the well-known list of five who cannot separate {\i terumah}: {\i chamisha lo yitremu, ve\u8217'im tormu ein trumatan truma} \u8212- five people may not take {\i terumah}, and if they do, their separation has no halachic standing \u8212- not merely rabbinic invalidity, but complete nullity even at the biblical level. The five are: a {\i cheresh}, a {\i shoteh}, a minor, one who separates from produce that does not belong to him, and a non-Jew separating from a Jew\u8217's produce. If the {\i cheresh}\u8217's act of {\i terumah} has zero standing \u8212- not even a doubtful one \u8212- this would suggest that a {\i cheresh} is categorically and definitively excluded from the realm of legal agency, confirming Shmuel\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} resists this inference. It suggests that Shmuel may actually hold {\i like} Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- that a {\i cheresh} is not definitively outside the category of {\i bar da\u8217'at}, but rather a {\i safek bar da\u8217'at}, a person of doubtful status \u8212- and yet still rules that no {\i asham talui} is owed for cohabiting with his wife. The two positions need not be contradictory, for a technical reason in the laws of {\i asham talui}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position explicitly: {\i tanas Rabbi Yitzchak omer mishum Rabbi Eliezer, trumas cheresh lo teitzei l\u8217'chulin} \u8212- the {\i terumah} separated by a {\i cheresh} does not become ordinary {\i chulin} \u8212- {\i mipnei shehu safek} \u8212- because it is of doubtful status. Rabbi Eliezer thus explicitly treats the {\i cheresh} as a {\i safek} rather than a definitive non-agent. His {\i terumah} is neither fully valid {\i terumah} nor fully invalid; it occupies an uncertain middle ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asham Talui Tech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the technical question: if Shmuel holds like Rabbi Eliezer that a {\i cheresh} is a {\i safek bar da\u8217'at}, why does Shmuel rule that no {\i asham talui} is owed for cohabiting with his wife? After all, Rabbi Eliezer himself takes a broad view of {\i asham talui} \u8212- he holds ({\i detanya}) that one who eats the {\i chelev} of a {\i koy}, an animal of doubtful classification between domesticated and wild, is liable for an {\i asham talui}, even though there is no specific second object that was definitely forbidden. Rabbi Eliezer does not require two pieces \u8212- one forbidden, one permitted \u8212- to generate liability for {\i asham talui}. So if a {\i cheresh} is a {\i safek bar da\u8217'at} according to Rabbi Eliezer, and Rabbi Eliezer holds that abstract doubt suffices for {\i asham talui}, why would Shmuel deny the {\i asham talui} in this case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that Shmuel holds like Rabbi Eliezer in {\i one} respect \u8212- that a {\i cheresh} is a {\i safek} \u8212- but disagrees with him in {\i another} respect: Shmuel holds that {\i asham talui} requires {\i chaticha mishtei chatichot} \u8212- there must be two concrete objects, one permitted and one forbidden, and the doubt must be which specific object was consumed. An abstract doubt about the nature of a person\u8217's status \u8212- as in the case of the {\i cheresh}\u8217's wife \u8212- does not trigger {\i asham talui} according to Shmuel, even if the act might theoretically be a doubtful {\i de\u8217'oraita} violation. {\i Shmuel savar Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'chada u\u8217'puleig lei b\u8217'chada.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then offers an alternate version of Shmuel\u8217's statement: {\i ika d\u8217'amri, amar Rav Chiyya bar Ashi amar Shmuel, eishes cheresh chayavin aleha asham talui} \u8212- there is a version in which Shmuel explicitly rules that one {\i is} liable for an {\i asham talui} for cohabiting with the wife of a {\i cheresh}. In this version, Shmuel unambiguously holds that a {\i cheresh} is a {\i safek bar da\u8217'at} and that the {\i de\u8217'oraita} status of the {\i kidushin} is genuinely in doubt. The apparent contradiction with the {\i mishnah} about five who cannot take {\i terumah} is resolved: Shmuel holds like Rabbi Eliezer, who treats the {\i cheresh}\u8217's {\i terumah} not as null but as doubtful.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Eliezer\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises a pointed question: {\i mah ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- what precisely is the reasoning behind Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position that a {\i cheresh} is a {\i safek}? Two possibilities present themselves, and the difference between them has profound practical consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weak Intellect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first possibility: {\i mifsha pshita lei d\u8217'cheresh da\u8217'ata klisha hu} \u8212- it is clear to Rabbi Eliezer that a {\i cheresh} has weaker {\i da\u8217'at}, a diminished intellectual capacity. But this alone does not suffice to remove a person from the category of {\i bar da\u8217'at}. People vary in intelligence, and legal agency does not require a minimum IQ. Diminished intellectual capacity, as such, would not be disqualifying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clear vs Weak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What would be disqualifying is not weakness of intellect but the inability to think {\i clearly} \u8212- {\i tzalul}. The question therefore sharpens: {\i u\u8217'mi mesafka lei ee da\u8217'ata tzlusa ee lav da\u8217'ata tzlusa} \u8212- is Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's uncertainty whether the {\i cheresh}\u8217's thought process is clear ({\i da\u8217'ata tzlula}) or not? If that is the question, then there is a stable \u8212- if unknown \u8212- fact about a given {\i cheresh}: either he thinks clearly or he does not. We just do not know which. And that is what generates the {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Category vs Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the first interpretive possibility: that Rabbi Eliezer is making a categorical claim \u8212- all {\i chereshim} are of an unknown but stable mental status, and as a category we cannot determine whether they fall on the {\i bar da\u8217'at} side or not. The second interpretive possibility is more dramatic: {\i o dilma, pshita lei de\u8217'daiteih klisha ve\u8217'lav daiteih tzelula hu, ve\u8217'hacha hainu ta\u8217'ama, kivan de\u8217'it itim chalin ve\u8217'itim shota} \u8212- perhaps Rabbi Eliezer actually knows that the {\i cheresh} does not think clearly, but his uncertainty is different: a {\i cheresh} has variable states of mind. At certain moments he is {\i chalim} \u8212- lucid, thinking clearly; at other moments he is effectively in a state of {\i shoteh}. The {\i safek} is not a stable unknown fact about his category; it is an irreducible variability about this individual person at any given moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference between these two interpretations crystallizes in the question of whether a {\i cheresh} can divorce his wife after having married her: {\i le\u8217'hotzi ishto beget} \u8212- can he write a {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Variable Mind\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i I amarta chada data hu} \u8212- if we say that all {\i chereshim} have a single, consistent (if unknown) state of mind, then {\i ke\u8217'kidushin kach gerushin} \u8212- the {\i kidushin} and the {\i gerushin} are parallel. Whatever status the marriage had, the divorce has the same status. If the {\i kidushin} was {\i de\u8217'oraita}, the {\i gerushin} is {\i de\u8217'oraita}; if {\i derabbanan}, the {\i gerushin} is {\i derabbanan}. In either case, the divorce is valid in whatever domain it operates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i ve\u8217'i amarta itim chalin itim shota} \u8212- if the second interpretation is correct, and we are dealing with variable states of mind in the individual, then {\i kidushei matzi mekadesh, ve\u8217'gerushei lo matzi megaresh} \u8212- he can contract the marriage because we allow the act on the assumption that he was lucid at that moment, but he cannot then divorce, because perhaps when he performed the {\i kidushin} he was in a moment of clarity, while now that he seeks to write the {\i get} he is in a moment of impaired clarity. The two moments cannot be assumed to be equivalent, and therefore the {\i get} cannot be confirmed as the mirror of the {\i kidushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teyku\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} leaves this unresolved: {\i teyku} \u8212- the question remains open. Whether a {\i cheresh} can write a {\i get} after marriage, according to the variable-mind interpretation of Rabbi Eliezer, stands undecided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gaonim View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is critical to appreciate is the significance of this entire passage for later halachic development. Throughout {\i Shas}, the trio of {\i cheresh, shoteh, ve\u8217'katan} is listed as categorically excluded from legal agency. Here, however, the {\i gemara} is actively exploring a position \u8212- attributed to Rabbi Eliezer and possibly to Shmuel \u8212- that a {\i cheresh} is a {\i safek bar da\u8217'at}, not a categorical non-agent. And the second interpretation of Rabbi Eliezer goes further: it opens the possibility that in a given case, based on the evidence of how this individual person presents, a determination might be made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are {\i Gaonim} who appear to rule in accordance with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position and who indicate that if there is evidence that a particular {\i cheresh} functions as a {\i bar da\u8217'at} \u8212- that his mental processes are clear, that he communicates and reasons \u8212- he would be treated accordingly. This is the seed from which later halachic authorities, over the subsequent centuries as schools for the deaf developed and as sign language became a recognized medium of full communication, began to reconsider the category. The {\i gemara} itself does not take us there \u8212- the question ends in {\i teyku} \u8212- but the conceptual architecture is already present in Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's reasoning as Rav Ashi has unpacked it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 112b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deaf & Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present {\i daf} opens a new {\i perek} centered on the {\i cheresh} \u8212- the deaf-mute \u8212- and his or her status in the laws of marriage and divorce. The {\i mishnah} that occupies this {\i amud} has already been encountered in fragments throughout the preceding chapters; accordingly, the treatment here proceeds with relative dispatch, building on ground already covered while sharpening the principles that will drive the {\i gemara\u8217's} subsequent analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh & Pikeach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens with two cases: {\i cheresh she-nasa pikeachet} \u8212- a deaf man who married a hearing woman \u8212- and {\i pikeach she-nasa chereshet} \u8212- a hearing man who married a deaf woman. In both instances the {\i mishnah} rules {\i im ratzah le-hotzi, yotzi; ve-im ratzah le-kayem, yekayem}: if he wishes to divorce, he may do so, and if he wishes to maintain the marriage, he may do that as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is that where one party to a marriage is deaf, there is no {\i kiddushin d\u8217'Oraita}. Since a {\i cheresh} is not classified as a {\i bar da\u8217'at} \u8212- a legally capable agent \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is purely rabbinic. But precisely because the marriage was rabbinic, the Rabbis could equally authorize a rabbinic divorce. The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i kesheim she-hu kones bi-remizah, kach hu motzi bi-remizah} \u8212- the same way he contracted the marriage through gesturing, he may also dissolve it through gesturing. Whether or not the full verbal and written form of a {\i get} can be executed by a deaf man, the expression of will through gesture suffices within the rabbinic framework that established the marriage in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then distinguishes two further situations. First: {\i pikeach she-nasa pikeachet ve-nischershah} \u8212- a hearing man married a hearing woman, and she subsequently became deaf. This is {\i kiddushin d\u8217'Oraita}, contracted when both parties possessed full legal capacity. The rule is that {\i im ratzah yotzi ve-im ratzah yekayem} \u8212- he may still divorce her if he chooses, because the {\i get} is a unilateral act of the husband, and as long as he is hearing and retains his {\i da\u8217'at}, his act of divorcing is valid regardless of her diminished status. The {\i gemara} will explain explicitly that the woman need not be a {\i bar da\u8217'at} for the divorce to take effect, since she is the passive recipient of the {\i get}, not its author.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i nishtateit lo yotzi} \u8212- if she became mentally ill ({\i shotah}) rather than merely deaf, he may not divorce her. The Rabbis instituted this protection to prevent her from being abandoned in a state of vulnerability, with no capacity to protect herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i nitcharesh hu o nishtateh} \u8212- if the husband himself became deaf or mentally ill \u8212- then {\i eino motzi olamit}, he can never divorce her. He is the active party who must give the {\i get}, and since he is no longer a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, he lacks the legal capacity to do so, even if he is physically capable of the act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbinic Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Nuri Q.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri raises a pointed question: {\i mipnei mah ha-ishah she-nischershah yotzah, ve-ha-ish she-nitcharesh eino motzi?} Why, when the {\i woman} becomes deaf after a biblical marriage, may she be divorced, yet when the {\i man} becomes deaf, he cannot give a {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man vs Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response cuts to the structural asymmetry of Jewish divorce law: {\i eino domeh ha-ish ha-megaresh le-ishah ha-mitgareshet}. The man who divorces is not comparable to the woman who is divorced. {\i She-ishah yotzah li-rtzionah ve-she-lo li-rtzionah} \u8212- a woman is divorced with or without her consent. She is not the legal actor in the transaction; she need not be a {\i bar da\u8217'at}. The husband, however, {\i eino motzi ela li-rtzono} \u8212- acts only through his own volitional, legally cognizant will. He must therefore be a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, and if he has lost that status, the {\i get} cannot issue from him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gudgada Test.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada then offers a formal testimony: {\i he\u8217'id al ha-chereshet she-hisi\u8217'ah aviha she-hi yotzah be-get} \u8212- that a deaf-mute woman whose father arranged her marriage while she was a minor may be divorced by {\i get}. This is a case of {\i kiddushin d\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- the father\u8217's act of marriage on behalf of his minor daughter is biblically valid \u8212- yet the woman is deaf, and the husband can still divorce her. The Rabbis respond to Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri that this is precisely analogous to the case of a hearing woman who becomes deaf: {\i af zo ke-yotzei bah}, our case is just like it. {\i Tosafot} notes that the Rabbis actually summoned Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada to the {\i beis midrash} specifically in order to bring this proof.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Families\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Bros\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now proceeds to the intricate permutations of two brothers married to two sisters where one or more parties are deaf. The governing principle is introduced at the outset: when two deaf brothers are married to two sisters \u8212- whether those sisters are both hearing, both deaf, or one of each \u8212- all of the marriages are rabbinic, since the deaf brothers render each union merely {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. Accordingly, {\i harei eilu peturot min ha-chalitzah u-min ha-yibum} \u8212- if one brother dies, the surviving widow is exempt from both {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}, because the operative prohibition is {\i achot ishah} (wife\u8217's sister), and since both marriages function on the same rabbinic plane, neither {\i yibum} nor {\i chalitzah} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i im hayu nochriyot yichnasu} \u8212- if the women were unrelated to each other, the surviving brother could perform {\i yibum}, and {\i im ratzu le-hotzi, yotziu} \u8212- he could afterward give a {\i get} if he wished, since the whole framework is rabbinic. What cannot occur in any of these cases is {\i chalitzah}, because {\i chalitzah} requires full legal agency on both sides, and at minimum one party is always a {\i cheresh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Pairs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} turns to the case of {\i shenei achim, echad cheresh ve-echad pikeach, nesuim li-shtei achayot pikechot} \u8212- one deaf brother and one hearing brother, each married to one of two hearing sisters. The two marriages therefore occupy different legal planes: the hearing brother has a {\i kiddushin d\u8217'Oraita}, the deaf brother only a {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the deaf brother dies ({\i met cheresh ba\u8217'al ha-pikachet}), what must the hearing brother do? His {\i d\u8217'Oraita} marriage makes the widow his wife\u8217's sister in the fullest biblical sense; {\i teitzei mishum achot ishah} \u8212- she simply goes free on account of the prohibition of a wife\u8217's sister. Straightforward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the hearing brother dies ({\i met pikeach ba\u8217'al ha-pikachet}), matters become complicated. The deaf brother is now the surviving {\i yavam}. His brother\u8217's widow is his wife\u8217's sister, but his own marriage to his wife is only rabbinic. He cannot perform {\i yibum} \u8212- that would be sexual relations with his ex-wife\u8217's sister even after he divorces her. He cannot perform {\i chalitzah} \u8212- he is a {\i cheresh} and lacks the legal capacity to do so. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i motzi ishto be-get ve-eshet achiv asurah lo le-olam} \u8212- he must divorce his wife, and his brother\u8217's wife is forbidden to him forever. The deaf brother is entirely stuck.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel permutation \u8212- two hearing brothers, one married to a deaf woman and one to a hearing woman \u8212- yields a symmetric analysis. When the hearing brother married to the hearing woman dies ({\i met pikeach ba\u8217'al ha-pikachet}), the surviving hearing brother who was married to the deaf woman faces his wife\u8217's sister. His own marriage is only rabbinic (because his wife was deaf), so {\i teitzei mishum achot ishah} does not resolve the situation cleanly by itself; he must divorce his wife. But because {\i he} is hearing, he can perform {\i chalitzah} with his brother\u8217's widow \u8212- {\i ve-eshet achiv be-chalitzah}. This stands in contrast to the previous case, where the deaf brother was incapable of performing {\i chalitzah} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stuck Deaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents the case where both the surviving brother and the woman involved are deaf: {\i shenei achim, echad cheresh ve-echad pikeach, nesuim li-shtei achayot, achat chereshet ve-achat pikachet}, where the deaf man was married to the deaf woman. If the hearing brother and hearing wife\u8217's marriage ends and the deaf man with the deaf wife survives, the result once again is {\i motzi ishto be-get ve-eshet achiv asurah lo le-olam}. He must divorce his wife because living with her would render him bound to his deceased brother\u8217's wife as {\i achot zekukato}. Yet he cannot perform {\i chalitzah} because he is a {\i cheresh}, and he cannot perform {\i yibum} because that would mean consummating a union with his now ex-wife\u8217's sister. She is permanently forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This series of permutations yields an important contrasting principle: when a {\i cheresh} enters into {\i kiddushin} of his own accord, the marriage is rabbinic and he can likewise be divorced rabbinically. But when a {\i cheresh} falls into a {\i yibum} obligation \u8212- as will be shown \u8212- the {\i yibum} itself is a biblical act, since it carries over the {\i kiddushin} of the deceased hearing brother. In that case, the {\i cheresh} who performs {\i yibum} cannot subsequently give a {\i get}, because the bond is now {\i d\u8217'Oraita} and he is not a {\i bar da\u8217'at} who can dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having completed the cases involving two sisters, the {\i mishnah} turns to two brothers married to two unrelated women, where one brother is deaf and one is hearing. The critical case: {\i met pikeach ba\u8217'al ha-pikachet} \u8212- the hearing brother dies. What must the deaf brother do? His brother was married to this woman by {\i kiddushin d\u8217'Oraita}, and there is accordingly a biblical {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}. The women are unrelated, so there is no prohibition of {\i achot ishah}. One might think the deaf brother has free choice. But: {\i kones ve-eino motzi le-olam}. He can only perform {\i yibum}, and once he has done so, he can never give a {\i get}. He cannot perform {\i chalitzah} because he is a {\i cheresh}. His only avenue is {\i yibum}. And since the {\i yibum} is a biblical act that creates a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} bond \u8212- even though the man performing it is a {\i cheresh} \u8212- he cannot subsequently dissolve it, because he lacks the {\i da\u8217'at} to author a valid {\i get}. The woman, equally, has no recourse to {\i chalitzah}. The situation is: {\i yibum} and no divorce, ever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This stands in deliberate contrast to the first {\i mishnah} in the {\i perek}: when a {\i cheresh} marries, the marriage is rabbinic and so is the divorce. When a {\i cheresh} performs {\i yibum} on a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} bond, the {\i yibum} is binding {\i d\u8217'Oraita} and no divorce is possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hear/Deaf Yib.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} addresses the reverse: a hearing brother was married to a deaf woman, and that brother dies. The surviving hearing brother must reckon with his {\i yibum} obligation toward a deaf woman. Since {\i she} is deaf, she cannot participate in {\i chalitzah} \u8212- and accordingly, {\i chalitzah} is not an option. He performs {\i yibum}. But \u8212- because {\i he} is hearing \u8212- {\i ve-im ratzah le-hotzi, yotzi}: he can give her a {\i get} afterward. This returns to the foundational principle of the {\i perek}: the woman need not be a {\i bar da\u8217'at} for a {\i get} to be valid, so long as the man is hearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Combos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final permutation: {\i shenei achim, echad cheresh ve-echad pikeach, nesuim li-shtei nochriyot, achat chereshet ve-achat pikachet}, where the deaf man is married to the deaf woman and the hearing man to the hearing woman. If the deaf man dies ({\i met cheresh be-chereshet}), what does the hearing brother do? He has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with the deaf woman, but it is a rabbinic obligation because his brother\u8217's marriage was only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. Since {\i chalitzah} is unavailable (she is deaf), he performs {\i yibum} \u8212- {\i kones ve-im ratzah le-hotzi, yotzi} \u8212- and he can give a {\i get} afterward because he is hearing. If instead the hearing brother dies ({\i met pikeach be-pikachet}), what does the deaf brother do? He faces a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} {\i yibum} obligation with an unrelated woman. He cannot perform {\i chalitzah}; he can only do {\i yibum}. But now, since the {\i yibum} is {\i d\u8217'Oraita} and he is a {\i cheresh}, he cannot give a {\i get}: {\i kones ve-eino motzi le-olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The comprehensive takeaway from all these permutations: if a {\i cheresh} performs {\i yibum} on a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} bond, he is locked in \u8212- {\i yibum} only, no divorce. If the woman is the {\i chereshet} and the man is hearing, the {\i yibum} can be followed by a {\i get}, because the man\u8217's {\i bar da\u8217'at} status is what governs.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deaf vs Shoteh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami Q.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the entire rabbinic framework of marriage and divorce for the deaf, the {\i gemara} now steps back and asks the foundational institutional question. Rami bar Chama poses it sharply: {\i mai shna cheresh ve-chereshet de-takinu lehu rabanan nisuin, u-mai shna shoteh ve-shotah de-lo takinu lehu rabanan nisuin?} What distinguishes the deaf person, for whom the Rabbis established a rabbinic institution of marriage, from the mentally ill person, for whom they did not? How do we even know the Rabbis did not establish {\i nisuin} for a {\i shoteh}? The source is a {\i baraita}: {\i shoteh ve-katan she-nasu nashim u-metu, nesheihem peturot min ha-chalitzah u-min ha-yibum} \u8212- the wives of a mentally ill man and a minor who die are entirely exempt from {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}, without even rabbinic recognition of the prior bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inst. Stands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers by drawing a distinction based on institutional viability. {\i Cheresh ve-chereshet de-kayma takanta de-rabbanan, takinu lehu rabanan nisuin} \u8212- in the case of the deaf, the rabbinic institution can stand, and therefore the Rabbis established it. {\i Shoteh ve-shotah de-lo kayma takanta de-rabbanan} \u8212- in the case of the mentally ill, the institution cannot stand. Why? {\i De-ein adam dar im nachash be-kefifah achat} \u8212- because a person cannot live together with a snake under the same roof. Marriage to a mentally unstable person is inherently untenable; any such institution would constantly collapse, and it is therefore pointless to establish it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Diff.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then ask: a minor is also not a {\i bar da\u8217'at} \u8212- why did the Rabbis not establish rabbinic {\i kiddushin} for a minor boy? The {\i gemara} answers: a {\i cheresh} will never naturally arrive at the state of being capable of biblical marriage \u8212- {\i de-lo ati li-khlal nisuin} \u8212- and so if the Rabbis do not create an institution for him, he will never be able to marry at all. Therefore {\i takinu rabanan nisuin}. A minor boy, however, will grow up \u8212- {\i de-ati li-khlal nisuin} \u8212- and there is simply no need to create such an institution. Let him wait.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts the inverse question: a {\i ketanah} (minor girl) will also eventually be capable of biblical marriage \u8212- why then did the Rabbis establish an institution of {\i kiddushin} for her? The answer: {\i shelo yinhagu bah minhag hefker} \u8212- so that she should not be sexually taken advantage of. A young girl without a father to protect her is vulnerable to exploitation, and the Rabbis therefore instituted rabbinic {\i kiddushin} to provide her a measure of protection and social standing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miun & Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Mi\u8217'un\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a series of comparative questions, each asking why the {\i ketanah} and the {\i chereshet} \u8212- both subjects of rabbinic {\i kiddushin} \u8212- are treated differently with respect to specific {\i halachot}. The first: {\i u-mai shna ketanah de-mema\u8217'enet, u-mai shna chereshet de-lo mema\u8217'ana?} Why may a minor girl exercise {\i mi\u8217'un} (the right of refusal that dissolves her rabbinic marriage without a {\i get}), while a deaf woman may not? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i im ken, mimnei ve-lo nasbi lah} \u8212- if we were to allow a deaf woman to perform {\i mi\u8217'un} throughout the entire duration of her marriage, no one would ever agree to marry her. A rabbinic marriage subject to perpetual revocation at her unilateral discretion offers no meaningful commitment; it would serve as a permanent disincentive. A {\i ketanah}\u8217's right of {\i mi\u8217'un}, by contrast, is temporally limited to the period of her minority, after which the marriage becomes fully binding \u8212- and so the concern does not apply in the same way. (It should be noted that if a {\i ketanah} also happens to be a {\i chereshet}, she may still exercise {\i mi\u8217'un} on account of her minority, though her status as a {\i chereshet} does not independently grant her that right.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Gez.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second question: {\i u-mai shna ketanah de-achlah bi-terumah, u-mai shna chereshet de-lo achlah bi-terumah?} Within the institution of rabbinic {\i kiddushin}, a minor girl married to a {\i kohen} may eat {\i terumah}, but a deaf woman in the same position may not. The source for this distinction is the very testimony of Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada quoted earlier in the {\i mishnah}, which implies that a {\i ketanah} married to a {\i kohen} eats {\i terumah}, but says nothing comparable for a {\i chereshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why the difference? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i gezeirah shema yashia cheresh be-chereshet} \u8212- a rabbinic decree lest a deaf {\i kohen} marry a deaf woman, and the extension of {\i terumah} rights to her lead to the following dangerous permutation: a deaf {\i kohen} married to a hearing woman, whose {\i kiddushin} is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, and that hearing, fully obligated woman will eat {\i terumah}. The {\i gemara} initially objects: {\i katan ochel neveilot hu} \u8212- just as we are not concerned when a minor or someone who is not obligated in {\i mitzvot} eats something rabbinically prohibited, why not extend the same leniency? The answer carries the objection one step further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slippery Slope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper concern is expressed as {\i gezeirah dilma atya le-meichal be-terumah d\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- once a hearing woman married to a deaf {\i kohen} begins eating any form of {\i terumah}, the concern is that she will slide into eating {\i terumah d\u8217'Oraita}, violating a biblical prohibition. She is, after all, a fully obligated adult, and the line between rabbinic and biblical {\i terumah} can easily become blurred in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why don\u8217't we raise the same concern about the {\i ketanah} case? Why are we not afraid that permitting a minor girl married to a {\i kohen} to eat {\i terumah} will lead, by mirror image, to permitting an adult woman married to a minor {\i kohen} boy to eat {\i terumah}? Rashi explains that in the case of the {\i cheresh}, any variation of the case \u8212- whether she is deaf or he is deaf \u8212- involves the same rabbinic {\i kiddushin} framework, and it is easy to confuse the two directions. With a {\i ketanah}, the social reality was that it was invariably young girls who were married early, not young boys \u8212- and moreover, there is no institution of rabbinic {\i kiddushin} for a minor {\i boy}, so the mirror-image case simply does not exist. The {\i ketanah} concern therefore never arises.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubbah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ket. Compare\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks a third comparative question: {\i u-mai shna ketanah de-is lah ketubbah, u-mai shna chereshet de-leis lah ketubbah?} Why does the rabbinic institution of {\i kiddushin} for a minor girl include a {\i ketubbah}, while the rabbinic institution for a deaf woman does not? The answer: {\i de-im ken, mimnei ve-lo nasbi lah} \u8212- because if a {\i ketubbah} obligation were attached to marriage with a deaf woman, it would deter men from marrying her. Since she is already a difficult match in the social reality that the {\i gemara} is addressing, adding a financial obligation to the prospective husband would create a further disincentive. Waiving the {\i ketubbah} makes the marriage more accessible to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know a {\i ketanah} has a {\i ketubbah}? The {\i mishnah} elsewhere teaches: {\i ha-mema\u8217'enet, ve-ha-sheniyah, ve-ha-ailonit} \u8212- the girl who exercises {\i mi\u8217'un}, the woman in a secondarily prohibited relationship, and the {\i ailonit} (a woman who never developed typical physical characteristics, presumed to be a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut}) \u8212- {\i ein lahen ketubbah}. The negative implication is instructive: {\i ha-mema\u8217'enet} has no {\i ketubbah} specifically because she exercised {\i mi\u8217'un}, dissolving the marriage retroactively. But {\i aval yotzah be-get ketanah yesh lah ketubbah} \u8212- were a minor girl to be divorced via {\i get} rather than through {\i mi\u8217'un}, she would have a {\i ketubbah}. The right to a {\i ketubbah} is part of the rabbinic institution of {\i kiddushei ketanah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ket/Can Write\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know a {\i chereshet} has no {\i ketubbah}? A {\i baraita} teaches: {\i cheresh ve-shoteh she-nasu pikechot} \u8212- a deaf man or a mentally ill man who married hearing women \u8212- {\i afilu nispakeach ha-cheresh ve-nishta\u8217'peh ha-shoteh, ein lahem aleihem kelum} \u8212- even if they recovered and became hearing or mentally competent during the marriage, there is no {\i ketubbah} obligation. The institution of rabbinic {\i kiddushin} for a {\i cheresh} did not include a {\i ketubbah}. And though there might be a question whether even a voluntary {\i ketubbah} would be binding \u8212- since a {\i cheresh} is not a {\i bar da\u8217'at} \u8212- if they ultimately decide to continue the marriage after recovery, {\i ratzu le-kayyeman yesh lahem ketubbah}: now that the marriage is {\i d\u8217'Oraita}, the full rabbinic institution of {\i ketubbah} kicks in automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then states the contrasting rule: {\i pikeach she-nasa chereshet o shotah, afilu kasav lah me\u8217'ah maneh, ketubbatah kayyemet} \u8212- a hearing man who married a deaf woman or a mentally ill woman, even if he wrote her a {\i ketubbah} of one hundred {\i maneh}, it is fully binding. Because he is a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, his voluntary undertaking of financial obligation is legally enforceable. {\i Mipnei she-ratzah li-zakhot be-nechasav} \u8212- he chose to encumber his own property, and he is entitled to do so. There is no rabbinic institution of {\i ketubbah} in this case, but his personal commitment stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- between the deaf man\u8217's inability to undertake binding financial obligations on behalf of the institution, and the hearing man\u8217's full capacity to do so voluntarily \u8212- raises a genuine difficulty: if the Rabbis trust the {\i cheresh} enough to establish the institution of {\i kiddushin} for him, why is it unreasonable to also require the normal {\i ketubbah} protection for the woman who enters into that marriage? The {\i gemara} does not resolve this cleanly. The apparent logic is that since a {\i cheresh} is not a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, imposing a standard financial obligation on him is of uncertain propriety; but this does not fully explain why the woman should bear the cost of that uncertainty by forfeiting her normal protection. The answer based on {\i de-im ken mimnei ve-lo nasbi lah} explains the policy choice, not the underlying principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Incentive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises the question from the opposite direction: given that we waive the {\i ketubbah} for a deaf woman so that men will be willing to marry her, why not also waive it \u8212- or create an especially attractive {\i ketubbah} \u8212- to incentivize women to marry a deaf man? The {\i gemara} responds with the famous principle: {\i yoter mi-she-ish rotzeh li-sa, ishah rotzah le-hinasei} \u8212- more than a man wishes to marry, a woman wishes to be married. Therefore, men require greater inducement to marry than women do. Waiving the {\i ketubbah} requirement is necessary to bring men to marry a deaf woman; no such waiver or inducement is necessary to bring women to marry, even a deaf man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle reflects a perceived societal reality \u8212- that marriage for women has historically been harder to secure than for men \u8212- and the {\i gemara} is legislating accordingly. The asymmetry remains imperfect: the principle explains why no {\i extra} incentive is needed for women to marry a deaf man, but it does not satisfactorily explain why the woman who does marry him should be denied the ordinary {\i ketubbah} protection. This gap is apparent in the discussion and does not receive a fully satisfying answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Malkio Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An illustrative episode: there was a {\i cheresh} who lived in the neighborhood of Rav Malkio, and he married a woman and wrote her a {\i ketubbah} of four hundred {\i zuzim} \u8212- a substantial sum. The {\i gemara} records Rava\u8217's response: {\i man chacim ke-Rav Malkio, de-gavra rabba hu} \u8212- who is as wise as Rav Malkio, for he is a great man. The basis for this ruling is as follows: {\i ilu ratza shifcha le-shamshei mi lo zavninan lei} \u8212- if this deaf man wanted to buy a slave woman to attend to his domestic needs, we would certainly permit him to engage in that transaction and regard it as legally binding. A {\i cheresh} is not altogether barred from financial transactions necessary for his livelihood; we recognize such transactions as at least functionally valid. {\i Kol sheken hacha} \u8212- how much more so in the case of marriage, where the woman will care for his household and he is entering the full domestic arrangement of married life. Rav Malkio permitted the {\i ketubbah} on this reasoning, treating the deaf man\u8217's voluntary financial commitment as binding in the context of a transaction necessary and beneficial for his functioning life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This episode resonates with the earlier question: if we recognize a {\i cheresh} as capable of such transactions when the practical necessity is clear, why does the rabbinic institution not include a mandatory {\i ketubbah}? The Rav Malkio ruling shows that {\i at minimum}, a {\i cheresh} can voluntarily undertake one \u8212- which brings the policy gap into sharper relief.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bar Da\u8217'at Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asham Talui\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having exhausted the comparative analysis of the rabbinic institution, the {\i gemara} turns to a deeper question: to what extent is the {\i cheresh} definitionally excluded from the category of {\i bar da\u8217'at}, and to what extent might his status be one of doubt? This question has significant implications for the {\i d\u8217'Oraita} dimension of his marriage and for the broader {\i halachic} framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Chiya bar Ashi citing Shmuel states: {\i eshet cheresh ein chayavin aleha asham talui} \u8212- if a man commits adultery with the wife of a deaf man, he does not incur the obligation to bring a {\i korban asham talui}, the guilt offering for an uncertain transgression. An {\i asham talui} is brought when one may have violated a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} prohibition. The fact that no {\i asham talui} is required suggests that the marriage of a {\i cheresh} has no {\i d\u8217'Oraita} dimension whatsoever \u8212- it is purely rabbinic, and adultery with his wife carries no biblical weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To reinforce this, the {\i gemara} invokes a well-known ruling: {\i chamishshah lo yitremu, ve-im taramu ein trumatam terumah} \u8212- five categories of persons cannot take {\i terumah}, and if they did, their act is entirely void. These five are: a {\i cheresh}, a {\i shoteh}, a {\i katan}, one who separates from produce that does not belong to him, and a non-Jew acting on behalf of a Jew even with permission. The formulation {\i ein trumatam terumah} \u8212- their act of separation has no effect whatsoever \u8212- implies that a {\i cheresh} has no biblical standing at all. If even a rabbinic effect could be attributed to his action, we might expect the produce to be treated as doubtful {\i terumah}. The categorical nullity suggests complete exclusion from the domain of {\i bar da\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i gemara} immediately introduces a complication: {\i hu de-amar ke-Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- perhaps Shmuel\u8217's statement reflects the position of Rabbi Eliezer specifically. For Rabbi Yitzchak teaches in the name of Rabbi Eliezer: {\i trumat cheresh lo teitzei le-chulin, mipnei she-hu safek} \u8212- the {\i terumah} of a deaf person does not revert to the status of ordinary produce ({\i chulin}), because his status is one of doubt. Rabbi Eliezer thus holds that a {\i cheresh} is a {\i safek bar da\u8217'at} \u8212- a person of uncertain legal standing \u8212- not a definitive non-agent. His act of {\i terumah} separation is therefore not entirely void; it creates a state of {\i safek terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Shmuel holds like Rabbi Eliezer, then one might expect that sleeping with the wife of a {\i cheresh} would incur {\i asham talui}, since the marriage may have {\i d\u8217'Oraita} status. Why then did Shmuel say no {\i asham talui} is required?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by reference to the technical conditions for {\i asham talui}. The classical case requiring {\i asham talui} is {\i chaticha mi-shtei chatichot} \u8212- one piece from two pieces: two objects are before me, one forbidden and one permitted, and I ate one without knowing which. There is a concrete object that embodies the prohibition. Shmuel may follow Rabbi Eliezer in treating the {\i cheresh} as a {\i safek bar da\u8217'at}, but he may disagree about the scope of {\i asham talui}, holding that the obligation requires a concrete doubtful object \u8212- not a mere abstract uncertainty about a person\u8217's legal category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes that Rabbi Eliezer himself does not appear to require the {\i chaticha mi-shtei chatichot} formulation in all cases \u8212- for Rabbi Eliezer holds that one who eats the {\i chelev} (forbidden fat) of a {\i koy} (a creature of ambiguous species, neither clearly {\i behemah} nor {\i chayah}) is liable for {\i asham talui}, even though there is no second piece from which the forbidden one was distinguished. If Rabbi Eliezer applies {\i asham talui} to categorical doubts in that case, why not here? The resolution is that Shmuel adopts Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position on {\i cheresh} status but not on the extension of {\i asham talui} to categorical doubts. He holds Rabbi Eliezer in one matter ({\i safek bar da\u8217'at}) and disputes him in another ({\i asham talui} requirements): {\i savar lah ke-Rabbi Eliezer be-chada, u-fligei leh be-chada}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eliezer Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Da\u8217'ata Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi now asks a penetrating analytic question about the very basis of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama de-Rabbi Eliezer?} What is Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's reasoning? One option: {\i mi-fpashat peshita leh de-cheresh da\u8217'ata kelisha hu} \u8212- it is clear to him that a {\i cheresh} has attenuated {\i da\u8217'at}, diminished mental capacity. But reduced intelligence alone does not remove one from the category of {\i bar da\u8217'at}; people vary in intellectual capacity without losing legal standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more precise question is whether Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's doubt concerns {\i da\u8217'ata tzelula} \u8212- clear, lucid thinking \u8212- rather than intelligence per se. There is an important distinction: someone may be of lower intellectual capacity but still think clearly and reason consistently; such a person remains a {\i bar da\u8217'at}. But if a person is unable to think with clarity \u8212- if his reasoning process itself is compromised \u8212- that is what removes him from the legal category. Rabbi Eliezer may be asking precisely this: does a {\i cheresh} think clearly, or does he not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Category vs Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then bifurcates into two entirely different framings. The first possibility: {\i ul\u8217'olam chada da\u8217'ata hu} \u8212- the {\i cheresh} always has the same mental state; it is stable and consistent. We simply do not know which side of the line it falls on. Is the entire category of {\i cheresh} consistently clear-thinking or not? If this is the framing, then Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's doubt is a category-level uncertainty: all {\i chereshim} are in the same situation, and we simply cannot determine their collective classification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possibility: {\i itin chalim ve-itin shoteh} \u8212- sometimes he is clear and sometimes he is not. Under this framing, the doubt is not about the category but about the individual \u8212- and more specifically, about the individual at any given moment. A particular {\i cheresh} may be lucid when he performs {\i kiddushin} and not lucid when he attempts to give a {\i get}, or vice versa.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Outcome\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference between these two framings is decisive in one key case: {\i le-hotzi ishto be-get} \u8212- can a {\i cheresh} divorce his wife? If the doubt is a category-level question \u8212- all {\i chereshim} are in the same stable state, we just don\u8217't know which \u8212- then {\i ki-kiddushin kach geirushin}: whatever the status of the {\i kiddushin} was, the {\i get} would have the same status. If the {\i kiddushin} was {\i d\u8217'Oraita}, the {\i get} is valid {\i d\u8217'Oraita}; if rabbinic, the {\i get} is rabbinic. Since his mental state has not changed, there is no reason to question the {\i get} that would not equally apply to the {\i kiddushin}. A {\i get} would therefore be possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the doubt is case-by-case \u8212- {\i itin chalim ve-itin shoteh} \u8212- then {\i kiddushin matzi mikadesh}: the {\i kiddushin} is effective because at the moment of {\i kiddushin} he may have been lucid, and a {\i kiddushin} effected in a lucid moment stands. But {\i geirushin lo matzi megaresh}: he cannot give a valid {\i get}, because at the moment of the {\i get} he may not be lucid \u8212- and you cannot know. The {\i kiddushin} is binding (either by {\i d\u8217'Oraita} or at least {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}), but a {\i get} given in a moment of non-lucidity would be invalid. Since there is no way to verify his state of mind at the time of the {\i get}, he cannot divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiku Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} leaves the question unresolved: {\i teiku}. The doubt about Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's precise reasoning \u8212- whether the uncertainty is categorical or case-by-case \u8212- and its implications for the possibility of a {\i cheresh} giving a {\i get}, remain open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modern Implications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geonim Develop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking, and what the {\i gemara} itself has now foregrounded, is that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position represents a serious {\i halachic} alternative to the categorical exclusion of the {\i cheresh}. Rabbi Eliezer holds that a {\i cheresh} is a {\i safek bar da\u8217'at}, and the {\i gemara} engages this position extensively. Some among the {\i Geonim} appear to have ruled in accordance with Rabbi Eliezer, and their rulings seem to indicate not only that the {\i cheresh} is a doubt-category but that where evidence exists in a particular case that an individual functions as a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, that individual may actually be treated as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Changing Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has profound implications once circumstances change. The {\i gemara\u8217's} entire treatment of {\i cheresh} assumes a social context in which deaf individuals lack formal education, cannot communicate through language, and give every external appearance of a person unable to function as a full legal agent. Once that context shifts \u8212- once schools for the deaf develop, sign language emerges as a rich communicative medium, and deaf individuals demonstrably engage in sophisticated reasoning and deliberate decision-making \u8212- the case for treating them as {\i safek bar da\u8217'at} in Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's sense, or even as full {\i b\u8217'nei da\u8217'at}, becomes considerably stronger. The determination is then case-by-case: does this individual, in light of his background, education, and demonstrated capacity, function as a {\i bar da\u8217'at}? This trajectory, traceable from Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i safek} through the {\i Geonim} and into the {\i halachic} literature of the last several centuries, reflects one of the more remarkable instances in which the underlying category of {\i p\u8217'sak} evolves as the factual reality it was designed to address fundamentally changes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 114\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shotah Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yitzchak View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf picks up near the top of 113b with a discussion of {\i gerushin} as they apply to a {\i shotah} \u8212- a woman who is not of sound mind. The foundational principle at stake is whether {\i da\u8217'at} on the part of the wife is required for a {\i get} to take effect. Since divorce is a unilateral act initiated by the husband, the wife need not actively participate. Rabbi Yitzchak states this explicitly: {\i d\u8217'var Torah}, biblically speaking, {\i shotah mitgareshet} \u8212- a woman who is not of sound mind can be divorced. The proof is straightforward: just as a woman of full capacity can be divorced against her will, so too her own mental participation is not a prerequisite. The act is the husband\u8217's alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is true biblically, why did the Rabbis rule otherwise? The Gemara explains: {\i shelo yinhagu bah minhag hefker} \u8212- so that people should not treat her licentiously. A woman who is mentally incapacitated and no longer under the protection of the marital framework is dangerously vulnerable. The Rabbis therefore prohibited the husband from divorcing her, effectively conscripting him into the role of her protector. Once he has married her, the responsibility is his, and he cannot simply dissolve the marriage and leave her exposed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Preservation Capacity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case? If she possesses enough mental capacity to preserve her {\i get} and to protect herself from exploitation, then there is no concern of {\i minhag hefker} in the first place. And if she lacks even the capacity to preserve what is placed in her hand, then the biblical mechanism of divorce itself would not function \u8212- for the school of Rabbi Yannai taught that {\i v\u8217'natan b\u8217'yadah}, \u8220"and he shall place it in her hand,\u8221" implies a woman who has a {\i yad}, a domain of genuine control. {\i Yad} is not merely the physical hand; it signifies protected possession. If she cannot protect anything placed in her hand, there is no {\i yad}, and the divorce fails even biblically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the school of Rabbi Yishmael derived an additional requirement from the phrase {\i v\u8217'shillachah mibeito} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall send her from his house.\u8221" This teaches that the {\i get} is only effective for {\i mi shemeshullachat v\u8217'einah chozeret}, a woman who understands what it means to have been sent away and does not return. A woman who would be sent and simply wander back, not grasping that she is now divorced, is not biblically divorced \u8212- even though her role in the process is entirely passive. This is a striking point: even where the act is entirely imposed upon her, if she cannot comprehend the status being conferred, the divorce is ineffective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution, then, is: {\i lo tzricha} \u8212- the case in question is a woman who can preserve her {\i get} and understands the meaning of her divorced status, but cannot protect herself from abuse. At that level, there is no biblical deficiency in the {\i get}, but there remains a rabbinic prohibition \u8212- precisely the {\i shelo yinhagu bah minhag hefker} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefta Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} frames this concisely: if she knows to guard her {\i get} (implicitly including, it seems, an understanding of her divorced status), and the Rabbis nonetheless said {\i lo tifkah} \u8212- she may not be divorced \u8212- it is solely because of {\i shelo yinhagu bah minhag hefker}. The prohibition is thus entirely rabbinic in character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Idi Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Idi observes that this conclusion can also be inferred from the language of the Mishnah itself. When the {\i Mishnah} addresses the case of the wife becoming a {\i shotah}, it states {\i lo yotzi} \u8212- he shall not divorce her. But when the husband is the {\i shotah} or {\i cheresh}, the {\i Mishnah} uses the formulation {\i lo yotzi olamit} \u8212- he may never divorce her. Why the addition of {\i olamit} in his case and not in hers?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: in his case, the divorce would be ineffective even biblically \u8212- he is entirely outside the {\i parsha}. The {\i Mishnah} therefore says {\i olamit}, never, because there is no circumstance under which his act would be valid. In her case, however, the prohibition is rabbinic; biblically it would work. The {\i Mishnah} therefore does not say {\i olamit}, signaling that the bar is not absolute incapacity but a rabbinic enactment \u8212- and that {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, after the fact, the {\i get} would take effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cheresh Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inconsistency Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a question attributed to Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri. The inquiry concerns the asymmetry between the {\i cheresh} \u8212- a deaf man \u8212- and the {\i chereshet} \u8212- a deaf woman \u8212- with respect to divorce. The question is which direction his challenge runs: is it obvious to him that a deaf man cannot divorce his wife and he wants to know why a deaf woman {\i can} be divorced? Or is it the opposite \u8212- that it is obvious that a deaf woman can be divorced and he is puzzled as to why a deaf man cannot?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to resolve this from the content of the response given to him. They answered: {\i eino domeh ish m\u8217'garesh l\u8217'isha mitgereshet} \u8212- the man who divorces is not comparable to the woman being divorced, for a woman is divorced willingly or unwillingly, while a man can only divorce willingly. Since the response concludes with the point about the man requiring volition, it seems the question was about the man. {\i Shema minah}, the Gemara concludes: his question was why a deaf man cannot divorce if a deaf woman can be divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then challenges this reading: since the response also includes the testimony of Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada regarding a {\i chereshet} married by her father, the resolution addresses the woman\u8217's case \u8212- suggesting his question was actually about her. {\i Shema minah}, the Gemara then concludes the opposite: his question was why a deaf woman can be divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Actor Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the impasse: Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri was arguing {\i l\u8217'divrehem} \u8212- according to the position of the Sages. On his own view, just as a man who is a {\i cheresh} cannot divorce, a woman who is a {\i chereshet} cannot be divorced. But according to them, he challenges the inconsistency: what is the difference between a man and a woman? The Sages then explain both sides of the asymmetry. The man must be an {\i actor} in the process \u8212- he cannot be a {\i cheresh} and perform a valid act of divorce. The woman is not an actor; she is the recipient of the act. Therefore a {\i chereshet} can be divorced even if she lacks full {\i da\u8217'at}. There is no inconsistency once one understands the structural difference between acting and being acted upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask how this differs from {\i kiddushin}, where analogously the man is the actor and the woman the recipient. Indeed the Gemara in {\i Kiddushin} draws certain parallels between {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin}. But the decisive difference lies precisely in the element of {\i b\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- the woman can be divorced against her will, a dimension that has no parallel in {\i kiddushin}. And it is worth noting that biblically, the woman\u8217's willing consent to marriage is never depicted in the {\i pesukim}; the {\i Rishonim} read the verses as addressing a case of a minor whose father acts on her behalf, and the cases of an adult woman arranging her own marriage emerge from rabbinic inference, not explicit biblical text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gudgada Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada testified about a {\i chereshet} who was married by her father \u8212- a marriage that was binding {\i d\u8217'oreita} \u8212- and who could subsequently be divorced. This establishes that a {\i chereshet} stands in a position analogous to a {\i ketanah} married by her father: the {\i da\u8217'at} of the woman herself is not required for the marriage to take effect biblically, nor for the {\i get} to dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deception Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava draws a further inference from this testimony. Since Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada established that the wife\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} is not required in the {\i gerushin} process, Rava rules that the following case is also valid: if a man showed the witnesses a document, said \u8220"see this {\i get} I am about to give my wife,\u8221" and then handed it to her saying {\i kinsi shtar chov zeh} \u8212- here, take this promissory note \u8212- she is divorced. She was deceived into thinking she received a debt document, yet {\i harei zo megureshet}, it is a valid divorce. The reasoning is direct: just as Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada ruled that her {\i da\u8217'at} is not required, so too in this case it is not required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: is this not obvious? It responds: one might have thought that since he described it to her as a promissory note, this constitutes an act of {\i bittul} \u8212- nullification of the {\i get}. The husband does have the capacity to annul a {\i get} before delivery. Perhaps calling it something else should be read as such an act?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this: if he had genuinely wished to nullify it, {\i l\u8217'eidim hava amar lehu} \u8212- he would have told the witnesses. He made a point of showing the witnesses that it was a {\i get}. Since he did not inform the witnesses of any nullification, {\i lo batlei} \u8212- it was not nullified. And the reason he called it a promissory note when giving it to her? {\i Machamat kisufa} \u8212- he was embarrassed to tell her to her face that he was divorcing her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This carries an implicit {\i chidush} regarding the requirement of {\i mishullachat v\u8217'einah chozeret} discussed earlier. One might have thought that even in cases involving a {\i chereshet} or {\i ketanah}, the woman must be informed of her divorced status at the moment of delivery. The Gemara\u8217's ruling here seems to indicate that as long as she is the type of person capable of understanding what being divorced means, it is not necessary for that understanding to be operative at the precise moment the {\i get} is placed in her hand. The concept of {\i mishullachat v\u8217'einah chozeret} speaks to the woman\u8217's general cognitive capacity, not to a requirement of actual awareness in the moment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Katan Ochel Neveilos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intervention Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara transitions to a celebrated {\i sugya} that at first seems entirely unrelated to the foregoing discussion of {\i cheresh} and {\i shotah}, but which will be seen at the end to tie directly into the {\i Mishnah} of the current chapter. The question is: {\i katan ochel neveilos} \u8212- if a minor is eating {\i neveilos} or {\i treifos}, forbidden foods \u8212- {\i bei dina metzuvan l\u8217'hafrisho o lo}, is {\i beis din} \u8212- the court, representing the community \u8212- obligated to intervene and stop him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not the question of parental {\i chinuch}. The obligation of {\i chinuch} in the Gemara emerges almost exclusively in the context of a father training his son in positive {\i mitzvos} \u8212- taking {\i lulav}, wearing {\i tefillin}, donning {\i tzitzis}. Whether {\i chinuch} applies equally to preventing transgressions is itself debated, with some {\i Rishonim} arguing surprisingly that {\i chinuch} is confined to positive commandments. But bracketing that question entirely, the present inquiry is about the community\u8217's responsibility \u8212- and by extension the general obligation of any third party \u8212- to prevent a minor from sinning. Does such a responsibility exist?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several threads motivate the question. If a minor commits an act that would be a sin for an adult, is an {\i aveira} being done that, even if no individual is liable for it, represents a metaphysical reality requiring communal response? Or is it simply a {\i neutral} act from the standpoint of {\i din}? And if the former, does communal tolerance of it \u8212- failing to intervene \u8212- compromise the character of the community? These are the questions undergirding the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Keys Retrieval Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} opens with a case involving Rav Yitzchak bar Bisna, who lost the keys to the {\i beis medrash} in the {\i reshus harabim} before {\i Shabbos}. He came before Rabbi Padas, who instructed him: go take some children and have them stroll ({\i litaylu}) in the {\i reshus harabim}. If they happen upon the keys, they will bring them, and by that innocent discovery the keys reach you \u8212- without any {\i melacha} being performed by an adult, and without any explicit instruction to the children to carry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inference drawn is that Rabbi Padas holds: {\i katan ochel neveilos, ein beis din metzuvin l\u8217'hafrisho} \u8212- the community is not obligated to stop a minor from sinning \u8212- and moreover, it is apparently permissible to arrange circumstances in which the minor will incidentally perform an act that would be prohibited for an adult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a {\i baraisa} in support: a person may not tell a minor to bring his key or seal on {\i Shabbos} \u8212- that would be explicit instruction. But if the minor is pulling out grass or throwing objects into the public domain, one may leave him ({\i manicho}) \u8212- there is no obligation to stop him. This is consistent: you cannot direct a minor to do an {\i aveira}, but you need not intervene when he does one on his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges this, noting through Abaye that perhaps these cases involve only rabbinic violations \u8212- pulling plants from an unperforated pot, or throwing into a {\i karmelis} rather than a true {\i reshus harabim}. Perhaps the permissiveness of non-intervention applies only to rabbinic prohibitions, and a biblical violation would be different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fire Extinguishing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings a {\i baraisa} about {\i Shabbos}: if a non-Jew comes to extinguish a fire on {\i Shabbos}, one may neither tell him to extinguish it ({\i amira le-goy}) nor tell him to refrain \u8212- the non-Jew\u8217's {\i Shabbos} observance is not the Jew\u8217's responsibility. But if a minor comes to extinguish the fire, {\i omrim lo al tikhbe} \u8212- one must tell him not to do it \u8212- {\i she-shevisato aleihem}, because his {\i Shabbos} observance is the responsibility of those around him. This seems to establish a clear obligation to intervene when a minor would violate {\i Shabbos} \u8212- a biblical prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan explains the {\i baraisa}: the case of the minor extinguishing the fire is {\i be-oso da\u8217'as aviv} \u8212- the minor is doing it with his father\u8217's intent in mind, for the father\u8217's sake. He is not acting independently; he understands that his father wants him to put out the fire and is acting in that spirit. This makes the minor\u8217's act an extension of the father\u8217's will \u8212- not quite an explicit {\i amira}, but functionally close to one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate question: if a non-Jew similarly extinguishes a fire because he knows his Jewish neighbor would want it, should that not also be prohibited? The Gemara responds: {\i ovdei kochavim ada\u8217'ata de-nafshei avid} \u8212- a non-Jew always acts for his own interests. Even when doing something to please a Jew, he calculates his own benefit \u8212- perhaps the Jew will pay him, or treat him more favorably. His act is therefore fundamentally self-motivated. The minor, by contrast, acts essentially as an extension of his parent, without the self-interested calculation of an independent adult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretation is striking because it means the Gemara treats the obligation to intervene by the fire not as flowing from a unique biblical {\i mitzvah} to ensure one\u8217's children observe {\i Shabbos} \u8212- even though such a {\i mitzvah} would seem to exist from the {\i Aseres ha-Dibros}, which explicitly includes {\i binkha u-viskha} among those whose {\i Shabbos} rest the householder is responsible for. Rather, the Gemara frames the issue in terms of the minor acting as an agent of the father, analogous to {\i amira le-goy}. The positive biblical obligation to ensure one\u8217's children keep {\i Shabbos} is effectively not invoked. Later {\i Acharonim} note this and suggest that perhaps the {\i Shabbos} case is indeed a special one due to that biblical source, but the Gemara itself does not develop this point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the cases throughout this {\i sugya} \u8212- both the fire and the next case involving the grandfather\u8217's house \u8212- seem to involve parental figures. The Gemara treats them under the same rubric as the general community\u8217's obligation, implying that even parents, beyond whatever {\i chinuch} obligations they hold, are governed by the same framework as the broader {\i beis din} question. The implication is that if {\i beis din} need not intervene in cases of biblical violations, perhaps parents need not either \u8212- a conclusion that sits in tension with the {\i chinuch} obligation, though some {\i Rishonim} limit {\i chinuch} to positive commandments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's conclusion leaves the general question of whether one must intervene in biblical violations unresolved. He demonstrates only that by {\i Shabbos}, the minor doing it {\i be-oso da\u8217'as aviv} creates a problem akin to {\i amira}; it does not demonstrate an independent communal obligation to prevent biblical violations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grandfather Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings another case. A child of a {\i chaver} \u8212- one who maintains strict standards of {\i terumot u\u8217'maasrot} \u8212- regularly visits his maternal grandfather who is an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} and does not separate {\i tithes} properly. The Gemara rules: {\i ein chosheshin shema ya\u8217'achilenu devarim she\u8217'einam metukanim} \u8212- one need not be concerned that the grandfather will feed him improperly tithed produce. And if the child comes home with fruit in his hand, {\i ein zakuk lo} \u8212- there is no obligation to take it away from him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan limits the leniency: {\i b\u8217'demai hiklu} \u8212- they were lenient specifically with {\i demai}, produce that is {\i questionably} tithed, which is itself a rabbinic concern. This seems to imply that for a definite biblical violation \u8212- certain {\i tevel} \u8212- one would be obligated to intervene.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara points out the tension: did Rabbi Yochanan not say earlier that the problem by the fire was specifically because the minor was acting {\i be-oso da\u8217'as aviv} \u8212- for the father\u8217's sake? That reasoning would suggest that in general there is no obligation to intervene, regardless of the severity of the violation. So which is it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes: {\i Rabbi Yochanan tzericha mispaka lei} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan is uncertain. In each case he deflects: the fire case can be explained by the {\i da\u8217'as aviv} rationale; the {\i demai} case can be explained by its rabbinic character. Neither case forces a definitive ruling on whether there is a general obligation to intervene when a minor commits a biblical transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Impure Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a variant: the child of a {\i chaver} who is a {\i kohen} visits his maternal grandfather who is also a {\i kohen} but an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}. The concern now is {\i terumah tmeiah} \u8212- impure {\i terumah} \u8212- which is a biblical prohibition. The Gemara rules: {\i ein chosheshin shema ya\u8217'achilenu terumah tmeiah}. Even here, there is no obligation to intervene proactively. The Gemara, however, qualifies this as applying to {\i terumah d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- rabbinic-level {\i terumah}, such as fruit-based {\i terumah} or {\i terumah} in the post-Temple era. And so again we cannot derive from this case a ruling about biblical-level obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites the ruling that an infant may nurse from a non-Jewish woman and even from a non-kosher animal, and one need not be concerned: {\i ein chosheshin b\u8217'yoneik sheketz} \u8212- there is no worry about this nursing being a forbidden act. However, it is explicitly prohibited to actively feed the infant {\i neveilos u\u8217'treifos} or {\i shekatzim v\u8217'remashim} \u8212- the prohibition is on active feeding, not on passive non-intervention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: can this case be used to prove that {\i beis din} need not intervene? The answer is that the leniency here is entirely due to {\i sakana} \u8212- danger. {\i Stam tinok mesukan eitzel chalav} \u8212- an infant\u8217's need for milk is presumed to be a health consideration, and any restriction that might prevent the infant from obtaining sufficient milk is overridden on those grounds. The case therefore proves nothing about the general question of non-intervention in {\i aveiros} by minors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing Melacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites Abba Shaul\u8217's testimony: {\i nohagim hayinu she-yonkim m\u8217'beheimah tehorah b\u8217'Yom Tov} \u8212- they would directly suckle from a kosher animal on {\i Yom Tov}. The Gemara asks: if there is {\i sakana}, it should be permitted even on {\i Shabbos}; if there is no {\i sakana}, it should be forbidden even on {\i Yom Tov}. What then is the case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i de-ika tzaar} \u8212- there is distress but not danger. And the ruling is based on the principle that direct suckling from an animal is considered {\i mesfarek ke-lachar yad} \u8212- an unusual manner of extracting the milk, making it only a rabbinic violation ({\i shinui}). Given that it is only rabbinic: on {\i Shabbos}, whose violation carries the penalty of {\i sekilah}, the Rabbis maintained their decree even in cases of {\i tzaar}. On {\i Yom Tov}, whose violation is only a {\i lav} without capital punishment, the Rabbis did not extend their decree to a case of mere {\i tzaar}, and it is therefore permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a difficulty from a parallel {\i sugya} in the chapter {\i Af al pi}, which indicates that direct suckling from an animal is treated as {\i mesfarek ke-lachar yad} and permitted on {\i Shabbos} in cases of {\i tzaar} as well. This creates an apparent contradiction: can {\i tzaar} suffice to permit a rabbinic violation on {\i Shabbos}? {\i Tosafot} distinguishes between degrees of illness and distress \u8212- {\i choleh} versus {\i mitzta\u8217'er} \u8212- and acknowledges that different {\i sugyos} seem to apply different thresholds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} also raises a prior question: is expressing milk from an animal even a biblical violation? The argument that {\i dash} \u8212- threshing \u8212- applies only to {\i gidulei karka}, things that grow from the ground, would make milk entirely a rabbinic category, and the need for the {\i ke-lachar yad} dispensation would be under question. {\i Tosafot} acknowledges there are opinions that even without the {\i ke-lachar yad} reasoning, milk from animals would never be a biblical violation of {\i dash} since it does not come from the ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These discussions carry significant practical implications for the {\i halacha} of nursing mothers on {\i Shabbos}. If expressing milk by hand is either not a biblical violation because it is {\i ke-lachar yad}, or not a biblical violation because milk does not derive from {\i gidulei karka}, the threshold for permitting it is considerably lower. The {\i Tzitz Eliezer} is notably lenient on matters relating to nursing mothers and {\i Shabbos}, reasoning that the need to maintain the milk supply \u8212- even in anticipation of future nursing \u8212- falls within the category of {\i sakana} for an infant, since {\i stam tinok mesukan eitzel chalav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then returns to the core {\i sugya} with additional attempts to resolve the question through {\i derashos} from {\i pesukim}. The verse {\i lo sochlum} \u8212- do not eat them \u8212- regarding forbidden creatures generates the {\i derasha}: {\i lehazhir gedolim al ha-ketanim}, to warn adults about minors. Does this mean adults must actively stop minors from eating forbidden foods? Or does it only mean adults may not feed them directly \u8212- {\i de-lo lispei lei b\u8217'yadei}? The Gemara repeats this exchange for the verse {\i ki nefesh mikem lo tokhal dam} regarding blood, and again for {\i emor v\u8217'amarta} regarding {\i tumah} for {\i kohanim}. In each case the {\i derasha} sounds like an obligation of active intervention, and in each case the Gemara deflects: perhaps it only forbids active feeding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then explains why all three cases are necessary. Had only the case of {\i shekatzim} been stated, one might think {\i dam} (blood) is excluded since it requires a {\i revi\u8217'it} to violate. Had only {\i dam} been stated, one might exclude {\i shekatzim} since {\i dam} has the weight of {\i karet}. Had only those two been stated, one might exclude {\i tumat kohanim} since it applies only to {\i kohanim}, who have heightened responsibility. And had only {\i tumat kohanim} been stated, one might think the other cases, which apply universally, need not carry the same responsibility. Each case is necessary, and yet none definitively resolves whether the obligation is one of intervention or merely a prohibition on active feeding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deaf Siblings Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having covered the entire {\i sugya} of {\i katan ochel neveilos}, the Gemara now demonstrates how it connects to the {\i Mishnah} of this chapter, which deals with a {\i cheresh} and his obligations in {\i yibbum} and {\i chalitzah}. The case involves two brothers, one hearing ({\i pikeach}) and one deaf ({\i cheresh}), married respectively to two sisters, one hearing ({\i pikachet}) and one deaf ({\i chereshet}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deaf Dies First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the deaf brother \u8212- married to the hearing sister \u8212- dies first, the hearing brother is now potentially obligated in {\i yibbum} toward his brother\u8217's widow. But that widow is his own wife\u8217's sister \u8212- {\i achot isha} \u8212- and is therefore an {\i ervah} toward him. She requires neither {\i yibbum} nor {\i chalitzah}. {\i Tetzei mishum achot isha} \u8212- she simply goes free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Dies First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more complex case: if the hearing brother \u8212- married to the hearing sister \u8212- dies first, what is the deaf brother\u8217's obligation? He has a biblical obligation of {\i yibbum} toward his brother\u8217's hearing widow, but he is rabbinically married to her sister. He cannot perform {\i yibbum} because it is {\i achot isha}, and he cannot perform {\i chalitzah} because he is a {\i cheresh}. He must therefore give his wife a {\i get} \u8212- divorcing his rabbinic wife \u8212- and his brother\u8217's widow is {\i asurah alav le-olam}, forever forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Deaf Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara uses these cases to drive the {\i sugya} toward its real question. In the first variant it asks: why must the hearing brother not remain with both women when the deaf brother dies? The answer: because {\i she} \u8212- the widow \u8212- is a hearing woman, fully obligated in {\i halacha}, and allowing the configuration to continue would violate {\i her} prohibition. {\i Mishum issura didah.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second variant: why must the hearing brother married to the deaf woman divorce her when the hearing brother dies? Because {\i he} \u8212- the surviving brother \u8212- is a hearing man, and it is his prohibition at stake. {\i Mishum issura deilei.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two cases establish the principle: if at least one party in the forbidden arrangement is fully obligated halakhically, {\i beis din} will not permit the status quo. The question then crystallizes: what if {\i both} parties are deaf \u8212- {\i cheresh} and {\i chereshet} \u8212- married to {\i chereshes}? In that case, neither party bears a biblical obligation. Is {\i beis din} obligated to intervene?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} rules: {\i motzi ishto b\u8217'get} \u8212- he must divorce his wife. And so the Gemara asks: {\i v\u8217'hacha de-lav issura didah ika, v\u8217'lav issura deilei ika} \u8212- here, neither her prohibition nor his prohibition is at stake. Both are deaf. Why must they separate? The Gemara demands a {\i get}, yet on the logic of {\i katan ochel neveilos}, one would think there is no obligation to intervene.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shemaya answers: {\i gezeira mishum achot yevama lashuk} \u8212- it is a rabbinic decree, not because we demand they stop violating a prohibition that doesn\u8217't apply to them, but for an entirely different reason. If the deaf brother and his deaf wife simply continue living together without any {\i get}, observers will conclude that no {\i get} or {\i chalitzah} was necessary \u8212- they will infer that the {\i yevama}, the brother\u8217's widow, is free to remarry as if she were a simple case of {\i achot isha} with no {\i zikah}, no bond of {\i yibbum} at all. But that would be a serious error: the {\i zikah} is real, even if only rabbinic, and the {\i yevama} is not free to simply go out and remarry. Allowing the deaf couple to remain together without a {\i get} would cause people to treat the {\i yevama} as {\i mutar lashuk} \u8212- free for the marketplace \u8212- when she is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, the reason {\i beis din} demands a {\i get} in this case has nothing to do with the obligation to stop the {\i cheresh} couple from committing an {\i aveira}. The Gemara is explicit: the {\i sugya} of {\i katan ochel neveilos} remains genuinely unresolved \u8212- we do not know whether {\i beis din} must intervene when a minor (or a {\i cheresh}) commits a biblical violation. The {\i get} requirement here is a {\i gezeira} with an entirely independent rationale: to prevent the {\i yevama} from being wrongly released to the general public.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the chapter concludes: {\i Hadran alach ha-cheresh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Death Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final section of the daf opens a new chapter \u8212- the {\i perek} known as {\i Ha-Isha Shalom} \u8212- which concerns a woman who reports that her husband has died overseas and seeks to remarry or perform {\i yibbum}. The {\i Mishnah} identifies the conditions under which she is believed: first, that there was {\i shalom beino leveina} \u8212- peace within the marriage; second, that there was {\i shalom ba-olam} \u8212- peace in the world, i.e., no war; and third, of course, that she has come forward and stated that her husband died. When all three conditions are met, {\i tinasei} \u8212- she may remarry \u8212- and {\i tisyabeim} \u8212- she may perform {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If any of the conditions is absent, she is not believed. If their marriage was good but there is war in the world, she is not believed. If there is peace in the world but they were fighting \u8212- {\i ktata beino leveina} \u8212- she is not believed even then, and cannot remarry based solely on her testimony. The concern in the case of marital strife is that she might be deliberately fabricating, motivated perhaps by the knowledge that if she remarries and her husband later reappears, he will be compelled to divorce her \u8212- a result she may be seeking. The concern in the case of war is different: not fabrication, but honest misapprehension, as the Gemara will explain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehudah View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah takes a stricter position: {\i l\u8217'olam eina ne-emenes} \u8212- she is never believed based on her testimony alone, even when all the favorable conditions are present, {\i ela im kein ba-a bocha u\u8217'vgadeha kru\u8217'im} \u8212- unless she arrives crying with her garments already rent. Only the presence of corroborating behavioral evidence warrants credibility. The Sages reject this: {\i achas zu v\u8217'achas zu tinasei} \u8212- in either case she may remarry, whether or not she displays such signs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 War Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies the reason for the disqualification in the case of war. Rava explains: {\i mishum de-amra bididamei} \u8212- because she speaks based on {\i didamei}, on impressionistic inference rather than certain knowledge. She reasons: {\i bechol hanei de-iktal hu palat} \u8212- all those others died; surely my husband was not the one who escaped? If an entire battalion was wiped out and her husband was among them, she naturally assumes he is dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet it is possible that she did not actually witness his death. {\i Zimnin de-machvu lei bigira o vi-rumcha} \u8212- sometimes he is struck by an arrow or spear, and she assumes the wound was fatal. {\i V\u8217'ika de-avdi samtrei v\u8217'chayei} \u8212- but some injuries are dressed and the person survives. Even a faithful wife who would not intentionally deceive the court may, under the conditions of war and siege, honestly report a death that did not occur. The Gemara continues on the following {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 115\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chaotic Circumstances\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peace vs.\u160 ?War\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} establishes that a woman who reports her husband\u8217's death is believed, provided there is no known conflict between them and the world is at peace. The condition of domestic harmony will be addressed elsewhere; the condition of peace in the world is what the {\i Gemara} takes up first. When the world is in a state of war, a woman might report that her husband is dead based on her interpretation of events \u8212- {\i k\u8217'didami}, as things appeared to her \u8212- rather than on concrete, verified knowledge. She may see her husband struck down and assume he died when he has not necessarily done so. The {\i Gemara} therefore qualifies the {\i mishnah}\u8217's grant of credibility: in a chaotic or dangerous situation, her testimony is suspect not because she is lying, but because she may be drawing incorrect conclusions in good faith.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Famine vs.\u160 ?War\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava introduces a distinction that is not at all obvious from the {\i mishnah} itself. In a case of war, he says, she is only disbelieved if she reports that her husband died {\i in the war}. But if she says that during a time of war he died in his bed \u8212- a natural death \u8212- she would be believed. The existence of warfare in the world is irrelevant to a claim of natural death; there is no reason to suspect confusion there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then adds a striking observation: {\i ra\u8217'avon gariya mimilchama} \u8212- famine is in some ways worse than war. In a case of famine, even a report that the husband died naturally is suspect, because people may faint from starvation and appear dead when they have not actually died. The precise nature of the confusion during a {\i ra\u8217'avon} is not fully spelled out, but Rava\u8217's point is that the circumstances create a context in which what looks like natural death may not be death at all. Thus, the woman would not be believed even in a situation that might otherwise seem less fraught than active combat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Related Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} extends this principle to additional chaotic circumstances. A case of {\i mapolet} \u8212- a building collapse in which the couple was trapped \u8212- is treated like war: she will report based on perception rather than certain knowledge, and is therefore not believed. Similarly, a situation of {\i shiluach nechanishim ve\u8217'akravim} \u8212- an outbreak of dangerous snakes and scorpions \u8212- is likewise classified as equivalent to war, with the same concern that her report is based on how things appeared rather than on definitive fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pestilence Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i dever} \u8212- pestilence \u8212- generates a dispute. One opinion holds that it is like war: since people are dying all around, she may assume her husband has died when he has not. A second opinion holds that pestilence is {\i not} like war, because {\i samchi ada\u8217'ata} \u8212- she places her confidence in a well-known folk saying: {\i shev shanin havah moseina ve\u8217'ish belo shneih lo azil} \u8212- pestilence may last seven years, yet a person does not die before his time. Because of this widely held belief, she will not draw premature conclusions even in the midst of a plague; she knows better than to assume death simply because the disease is present. In the other categories \u8212- war, famine, building collapse, dangerous animals \u8212- no such folk wisdom insulates her from making assumptions, so the concern remains.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Migo & War Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 War as Migo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a further question at the end of 114b: suppose the woman is the one who first informs us that a war broke out \u8212- we had no independent knowledge of it. She comes from a distant country and says: war broke out there, and my husband died in it. Should she be believed on the grounds of {\i migo}? After all, she did not have to report the existence of war. She could have claimed her husband died during peacetime; had she wished to deceive, that simpler story was available to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migo Limitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} at the top of 115a frames the question precisely. On one hand: {\i mah lo leshaker} \u8212- she had no reason to lie; {\i i ba\u8217'ei amar shalom ba\u8217'olam} \u8212- she could have said there was peace in the world. On the other hand: {\i keivan de\u8217'ichzaka, amra bidedamei} \u8212- now that we have established that there was a war, we are still concerned that she is reporting based on perception, drawing conclusions that may not reflect what actually happened. The {\i migo} principle can only establish that she is not {\i intentionally} lying. It cannot establish that her conclusions are accurate. Since the entire concern is not that she is fabricating a story but that she is sincerely misreading the situation, the {\i migo} does nothing to resolve that problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migo Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot sharpens this point by noting that in fact the woman {\i always} has a {\i migo} available to her. Even in the standard case where we know there is a war, she could have said her husband died naturally \u8212- on his bed \u8212- rather than in combat. And yet we do not believe her in such a case either. This demonstrates conclusively that the {\i migo} provides no advantage here. Whether or not she could have told a different, less suspicious story, the underlying concern \u8212- {\i amra bidedamei}, that her report is based on impression rather than certainty \u8212- remains unaddressed by the {\i migo} argument. The {\i Gemara} ultimately concludes that even where the report of war comes entirely from her, this does not strengthen her credibility in any meaningful way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Smoke & Fire\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smoke Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} presents the following case: a woman comes and says, {\i ashinu aleinu bayis} \u8212- smoke filled our house \u8212- or {\i ishnu aleinu me\u8217'ara} \u8212- smoke filled our cave \u8212- {\i hu meis va\u8217'ani nitzalti} \u8212- he died in the smoke and I escaped. She is {\i einan ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- not believed. The {\i Gemara} notes that this is analogous to the war situation: everything we know about the chaotic circumstances comes from her own testimony, yet that does not strengthen her report, because we remain concerned that she is drawing incorrect conclusions. The circumstance being reported by her, rather than independently known, does not help.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miracle Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks whether this case could serve as a proof that reporting the dangerous circumstances oneself does not help \u8212- but answers that it is different: {\i shani hasam}. The reason she is not believed in the smoke case is not merely that she reported the circumstances herself. Rather, there is a specific argument: {\i keheichi delididi itrachish nisa ledidei nami itrachish nisa} \u8212- just as a miracle occurred for her and she escaped the smoke-filled house, so too a miracle may have occurred for him. The very fact of her escape is concrete evidence that someone can survive such a situation. Her escape undermines any confident claim that he could not have survived as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Attack Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bandits Attack\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another {\i baraita} presents a contrasting case: {\i nafalu aleinu ovdei kochavim} \u8212- non-Jews attacked us \u8212- {\i nafalu aleinu listim} \u8212- or bandits attacked us \u8212- {\i hu meis venitzalti} \u8212- he died and I was saved. Here, remarkably, she {\i is} believed. This is surprising. One might think that even here, running away during an attack, she could have drawn wrong conclusions about whether her husband actually died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Weapons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i hatam kerav idi} \u8212- there, the ruling follows Rav Idi. {\i De\u8217'amar rav idi: isha klei zaynah aleha} \u8212- a woman\u8217's weapons are upon her. This is a euphemistic expression meaning that a woman may be specifically targeted to be kept alive by attackers \u8212- bandits or enemies who wish to assault her \u8212- while a man would simply be killed. Therefore, the fact that she survived does not suggest that he survived. We have a coherent explanation for why she alone escaped: she was spared for a violent purpose she managed to evade, while he was simply killed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Believed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi identifies two distinct reasons why she is believed in this case, each of which distinguishes it from the general concern about {\i amra bidedamei}. First, the attack was not a large-scale war or pestilence \u8212- it was an event that happened specifically to the two of them. When the situation is not broadly chaotic but rather a discrete incident affecting a small group, the circumstances are less confusing and her reporting is correspondingly more reliable. Second, even though she escaped \u8212- which ordinarily would suggest that perhaps he escaped too \u8212- the principle of {\i isha klei zaynah aleha} provides a concrete explanation for why her survival is consistent with his death. These two factors together make this case fundamentally different from war or famine.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wedding Fire\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fire Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a concrete incident. There was a man at whose wedding, as the celebration concluded, {\i itli nura be-ganenei} \u8212- fire caught under the wedding canopy. His wife called out to the bystanders: {\i chazu gavrai chazu gavrai} \u8212- look at my husband, come quickly! People came. When they arrived, they found {\i gava charucha deshadi} \u8212- a charred body lying there \u8212- and {\i pisa deyada deshadi} \u8212- a severed, charred hand lying separately nearby.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smoke Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiyya bar Avin considered ruling that this case is comparable to the smoke-filled house \u8212- {\i hainu ishnu aleinu bayit ishnu aleinu me\u8217'ara} \u8212- meaning that she should not be believed, since there was fire and it is not certain that the husband died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejected this comparison. {\i Mi dami?} \u8212- how can you compare them? Two differences distinguish this case from the smoke case. First, in the smoke case she was not calling witnesses; here she is crying out {\i chazu gavrai} \u8212- look at my husband \u8212- summoning people to observe what is happening, which suggests she is reporting something concrete and visible rather than drawing a conclusion after the fact. Second, and more significantly, there is actual physical evidence: a charred body and a severed charred hand. This is not a case of drawing conclusions from chaos \u8212- there is something to point to. The combination of her calling witnesses and the presence of these remains constitutes a much stronger basis for belief.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Theories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiyya bar Avin, however, pressed back with alternative explanations for the physical evidence. Regarding the charred body: {\i eima inish achrina ata le\u8217'atzolei ve-achlei nura} \u8212- perhaps it is not the husband\u8217's body at all, but rather someone else who came to rescue the husband and was consumed by the fire. And regarding the severed hand: perhaps the husband himself caught fire, {\i ve-italya be-muma} \u8212- and became disfigured, his hand burning and falling off \u8212- {\i u-machamat kisufa azal ve-arak le-alma} \u8212- and out of shame at his disfigurement he fled and is still alive somewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This exchange illuminates a broader principle running through the entire {\i Gemara}. The concern about {\i amra bidedamei} is not limited to questions about the woman\u8217's state of mind. Even when we have physical evidence \u8212- a charred body, a hand \u8212- we face the independent question of what conclusions we can reasonably draw from those facts. The woman may be reporting accurately what she observed, yet the question remains: do the facts she has accurately reported actually establish that the husband is dead? Cases of {\i agunah} arising from uncertainty about a husband\u8217's fate often turn not on doubting the witness\u8217's sincerity but on precisely this kind of evidentiary ambiguity. What does it mean to \u8220"see\u8221" someone die when what one observed were circumstances that strongly implied death without confirming it absolutely?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Single Witness War\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ed Echad Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now poses a new question: {\i ed echad b\u8217'milchama mahu} \u8212- what is the law if a third-party witness \u8212- not the woman herself \u8212- reports that the husband died in war? The question is somewhat surprising. If the concern is not intentional deception but rather the drawing of incorrect conclusions in chaotic circumstances, why would a third party be any more reliable than the woman herself? Both were presumably present in the same chaotic environment and subject to the same perceptual limitations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One answer is that a third party, being less emotionally invested and not a direct subject of the events he is narrating, will be more careful and precise. When one presents oneself explicitly as a witness rather than as a party to the events, there is a greater awareness of the implications of testimony, a greater compunction about stating things as certain that are not fully verified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Believe Him\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents the argument for believing the single witness. The ordinary basis for accepting {\i ed echad} in cases of a husband\u8217's death is {\i milta de\u8217'avida le\u8217'igloyei hu} \u8212- the matter is one that will eventually become known \u8212- {\i lo meshaker} \u8212- and therefore he will not lie, since truth will surface in any case. The argument applies here too: {\i hacha nami lo meshaker} \u8212- here too he will not lie. Furthermore, the general basis for accepting a single witness\u8217's report of a husband\u8217's death is reinforced by the assumption that the woman herself will investigate before remarrying, knowing the grave consequences if the husband is still alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Believe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument against believing him in a war situation is as follows. If the ordinary basis for trusting a single witness is that she herself \u8212- {\i hi gufa daika uminisba} \u8212- will investigate and verify before remarrying, then in a war situation that backstop is undermined. The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i lo daika uminisba de\u8217'amra bidedamei} \u8212- she will not investigate carefully enough, because what the witness has told her coheres with what she herself perceived. She heard the witness say her husband died in war; this fits her own impressions; and so she proceeds to remarry without the careful independent investigation that normally underwrites the trust we extend to a single witness. The risk of drawing incorrect conclusions is therefore compounded: the witness may have made assumptions, and the woman may not independently verify because the witness\u8217's account confirms her own perceptions. Rashi similarly explains that the concern about {\i bidedamei} applies not just to the woman\u8217's testimony but to this entire chain of inference.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akiva\u8217's Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nehardea Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama brings a {\i tanna\u8217'itic} account as a potential proof. Rabbi Akiva reports: when I went to Nehardea {\i le\u8217'aber shanah} \u8212- to intercalate the year \u8212- I found Nechemia, a man from the house of Beit Dali, who told me: I have heard that in the Land of Israel they do not allow a woman to remarry on the basis of one witness, except for Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava, who permits it alone. Rabbi Akiva confirmed this. Nechemia then said: go back and tell them in my name \u8212- {\i atem yode\u8217'im hamedina hazzo meshubeshet begayasot} \u8212- you know that this country is disrupted by army troops \u8212- and this is the tradition I have received from Rabban Gamliel the Elder: {\i shemashi\u8217'in et ha\u8217'isha al pi ed echad} \u8212- that a woman may be allowed to remarry on the basis of one witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Troops Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is how to understand the mention of troops. Does Nechemia mean to say: even though this country is in a state of war and troops are passing through, the tradition from Rabban Gamliel the Elder is that a single witness is believed even under such circumstances? If so, this would be a direct answer to our question \u8212- a single witness {\i is} accepted in a war situation. {\i Alma ed echad meheman} \u8212- one witness is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Reinterprets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejects this reading. If the point were that one witness is accepted in time of war, he argues, Nechemia should have stated the rule in universal terms: {\i kol makom sheyesh gayasot} \u8212- wherever there are troops, a single witness is believed. Why would he refer specifically to {\i this} country? Moreover, the discussion was focused on the halakhic practice of {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- Nechemia was delivering a tradition about what Rabban Gamliel the Elder ruled. The mention of troops, Rava explains, is entirely incidental. Nechemia was explaining why he could not go deliver this tradition to the authorities in {\i Eretz Yisrael} himself: {\i ve\u8217'i efshar li lemishbak inshi beiti umaytei kamei rabbanan} \u8212- it is impossible for him to travel, because the region is in chaos due to military activity. He was therefore asking Rabbi Akiva to carry the message back. The ruling itself \u8212- that a woman may remarry on the basis of one witness \u8212- has nothing to do with war. It is simply the general {\i halakha} according to Rabban Gamliel the Elder, and the mention of troops explains only why Nechemia could not convey it in person. The incident is therefore not a proof regarding {\i ed echad b\u8217'milchama}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Drowned Scholars\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drowned Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents another account. Two Torah sages were traveling with Abba Yossi ben Simai on a boat. {\i Ve-tavu} \u8212- they drowned. {\i Ve-hesi Rabbi nishoteihan al pi nashim} \u8212- Rabbi permitted their wives to remarry on the basis of women\u8217's testimony. Women who had been present or had witnessed the sinking reported the deaths, and Rabbi acted on those reports.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water = War\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} observes: {\i mayim kemilchama dami} \u8212- water is treated like war in the relevant sense. In chaotic and dangerous circumstances on the water, those reporting what happened may be drawing conclusions based on perception rather than certainty. Furthermore, the testimony here is not that of two formal male witnesses. A hundred women, the {\i Gemara} notes, have the legal status of one witness \u8212- {\i bi\u8217'nashim afilu me\u8217'ah k\u8217'echad dami} \u8212- because women do not constitute formal {\i edut} in the technical sense. So Rabbi was relying on what is legally equivalent to {\i ed echad} testimony, in a situation analogous to war. This seems to be a strong proof that such testimony is accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this reading. {\i Mayim she\u8217'ein lahem sof ninhu} \u8212- this was open water without visible boundaries. And {\i mayim she\u8217'ein lahem sof isha asura} \u8212- in the case of water with no visible end, a woman may not remarry even if two formal witnesses testify that they saw her husband drown. The logic is counterintuitive: rather than reasoning that without a visible shore the man had nowhere to swim, the concern runs the other direction. If the water has no visible end, then somewhere beyond the witnesses\u8217' sight the man may have surfaced and survived. No testimony about drowning in such conditions is ever absolutely conclusive. If that is so, how could Rabbi have permitted remarriage at all? The case seems impossible to rely upon as a proof for anything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simanim Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i ela heichi dami} \u8212- what must the situation actually have been? The witnesses must have said {\i ashkachinhu kaman} \u8212- we found them before us, meaning the bodies were retrieved \u8212- {\i ve-chazinahu l\u8217'altar} \u8212- and we saw them immediately, before the water had time to completely disfigure the faces \u8212- {\i ve-amri simanim} \u8212- and they identified the bodies by specific bodily marks. Tosafot raises the question of why facial recognition alone would not suffice; the answer appears to be that even brief immersion can alter appearance enough that identification by {\i simanim} \u8212- distinctive physical marks such as a mole, a birthmark, or a physical abnormality \u8212- is needed to reinforce the identification. In that case, the {\i Gemara} concludes, {\i d\u8217'lav aleiyhu samchinan ela a\u8217'simanim} \u8212- we are not relying on the witnesses\u8217' general impression; we are relying on the concrete identifying marks they specified. There is therefore no concern of {\i amri bidedamei} \u8212- the testimony is grounded in specific, verifiable physical evidence, not in perception or assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} has thus not definitively resolved whether {\i ed echad} is accepted in a war situation without additional grounding. The drowned scholars case, which seemed to offer a proof, turns out instead to rest on the presence of {\i simanim} rather than on the testimony of informal witnesses under chaotic conditions per se.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sesame Seeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seeds Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} shifts from the weighty terrain of death and remarriage to a {\i dinei mamonot} case involving sesame seeds \u8212- but the analogy it draws will illuminate the broader question of when we may connect circumstantial dots. A certain man had deposited a quantity of sesame seeds with his friend for safekeeping. When he later asked for them back, the friend said: you already took them back. The first man pointed to a barrel and said: I gave you exactly this measure, and here is the same measure of sesame seeds sitting in this barrel \u8212- {\i ha kaila ve-kaila havyan} \u8212- the amounts match precisely. The friend replied: those are mine; yours you already took.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chisda\u8217's Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda sought to resolve the case by analogy to the drowned scholars. Just as in that case we matched two bodies to two men and assumed they were the same, so here \u8212- two matching quantities, same container, reasonable to assume it is the same deposit. The application from a case about remarrying widows to a property dispute over seeds raises immediate questions: do we apply different standards of proof in {\i dinei mamonot} versus cases of personal status? And how much circumstantial convergence is needed in each context to draw a confident conclusion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejected the analogy: {\i mi dami} \u8212- how can you compare? {\i Hatam ka\u8217'amar simanim} \u8212- in the drowned case, the witnesses identified specific bodily marks. That is what made the identification reliable. {\i Hacha shumshumei mai simanei it lehu} \u8212- here, sesame seeds have no identifying marks whatsoever. One measure of sesame seeds looks exactly like any other measure of sesame seeds. The analogy simply does not hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weight Not Enough\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: is not the matching quantity itself a kind of identifying mark? {\i Ha kaila ve-kaila havyan} \u8212- they are the same measure. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i chushbana itremi} \u8212- it may simply be a coincidence that the amounts match. Just as one cannot identify a person by height alone \u8212- many people share the same height \u8212- one cannot identify a deposit by quantity alone. Many quantities happen to be identical, and identical weight or volume does not constitute a meaningful {\i siman}. The claim fails.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Labeled Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessel Labels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Kashisha bar Rav Chisda raises a question to Rav Ashi: are we really concerned, in the sesame seeds case, that the borrower might have emptied the barrel and refilled it with different seeds? He cites a {\i mishnah}: if a vessel is found with the letter {\i kuf} written on it, one assumes it contains {\i korban} \u8212- something consecrated to the Temple; {\i mem} indicates {\i maaser}; {\i dalet} indicates {\i dimua} \u8212- a mixture of {\i chullin} and {\i terumah}; {\i tet} indicates {\i tevel} \u8212- untithed grain; and {\i tav} indicates {\i terumah}. The {\i mishnah} notes that in times of danger, people wrote {\i tav} in place of spelling out {\i terumah} in full. The principle behind all of this is that one assumes the vessel still contains what its label indicates \u8212- that is, we do not ordinarily suppose that a person emptied a labeled vessel and reused it for something else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Label Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale is intuitive: the whole point of labeling a vessel is to maintain consistent use. A person who has gone to the trouble of marking a storage container with its contents is unlikely to repurpose that container while leaving the old label in place. From this, one might conclude that we never assume a vessel was emptied and reused, which would support the view that the sesame seeds in the barrel are the original deposit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosei Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Ravina draws attention to the end of the {\i mishnah}: Rabbi Yosei says that even if one finds a whole barrel explicitly labeled {\i terumah}, one may still say that its current contents are {\i chullin}. His reasoning: {\i shani omer eshtakad havei meleiah terumah u-pina} \u8212- I can say that last year it was filled with {\i terumah}, it was then emptied, and now it is being used for ordinary produce. Rabbi Yosei explicitly entertains the possibility of emptying and reusing a vessel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Label Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction between Rabbi Yosei\u8217's view and the earlier assumption of consistency. The dispute turns on a specific question: {\i im issa de-panya michpar hava kafar} \u8212- if you were going to reuse the vessel for something else, would you not have erased the label? One position holds that no one would leave a misleading label on a vessel; therefore, the presence of the label proves continued consistent use. Rabbi Yosei, however, holds that there are two plausible ways the label could remain despite a change of contents: either the owner forgot the vessel was labeled \u8212- {\i ishtaluyei ishtali} \u8212- or he intentionally kept the {\i terumah} label as a deterrent, knowing that people will not touch something marked {\i terumah} and thus using the label to protect ordinary produce from being eaten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seeds Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication for the sesame seeds case: when it comes to explicitly labeled vessels, there is at least a basis for arguing that the label proves continuity of use \u8212- that is the majority position against which Rabbi Yosei dissents. But an unlabeled barrel of sesame seeds lacks even that basis. With no label, there is no reason to presume that the contents are original. It is entirely plausible that the barrel was emptied, the original seeds returned to their owner as the depositor claims, and then refilled with the friend\u8217's own seeds. The sesame seeds case therefore cannot be resolved on the basis of continuity of possession.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reish Galuta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a new story and a new question about identification. Yitzchak the {\i Reish Galuta} \u8212- the political head of the Jewish diaspora in Babylonia \u8212- who was the son of the sister of Rabbi Bibi, was traveling from Cordoba to Spain. {\i Ve-shakhiv} \u8212- he died en route. A message was sent back: Yitzchak the {\i Reish Galuta}, son of Rabbi Bibi\u8217's sister, was traveling from Cordoba to Spain and died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Worry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question posed: {\i mi chayishinan le-trei Yitzchak o lo} \u8212- must we be concerned that there might be two people named Yitzchak who fit these identifying details? At first glance this seems almost absurd. How many people named Yitzchak could there be who simultaneously hold the position of {\i Reish Galuta}, are nephews of Rabbi Bibi, and were traveling on that same route? And yet the {\i Gemara} treats this as a live question, because the broader principle at stake is one of general importance: when we receive a report about a person identified by name and other particulars, how much specific identification is required before we may act on the assumption that we know who is meant?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye holds: {\i chayishinan} \u8212- we are concerned. Even with multiple identifying details, one must verify that no other person fits the same description before relying on the report for a matter of consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava holds: {\i lo chayishinan} \u8212- we are not concerned. Once a person fits the specific identifying details provided, there is no need to investigate further for potential duplicates. Rava\u8217's position is that context and convergence of identifying information are, in principle, sufficient.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Androlinai Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Found\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava supports his position with the following proof. A {\i get} was found in Nehardea, written as follows: I, Androlinai, from Nehardea, hereby divorce and dismiss so-and-so my wife. The father of Shmuel sent this document to Rav Yehuda Nesi\u8217'ah \u8212- the grandson of Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi \u8212- asking: we know a man in Nehardea named Androlinai whose wife is named Sarah. May we assume this {\i get} refers to him and allow his wife to remarry?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investigation Need\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi notes that the {\i get} contained not only the husband\u8217's unusual name but also the wife\u8217's name and the city \u8212- all three identifying details matching someone known to the community. And yet the question was raised: must we verify that no other person in Nehardea shares these same particulars?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investigation Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda Nesi\u8217'ah responded: {\i zil bedok bi-Nehardea kullah} \u8212- go and check the entire Nehardea \u8212- meaning the entire district \u8212- to determine whether anyone else has the same name as this husband and the same name as his wife. He thus apparently required active investigation before assuming that the {\i get} referred to the known Androlinai.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, argues that this cannot be the actual {\i halakhic} ruling. If we truly could not draw logical inferences from identifying information, then {\i yidbok kulei alma mibei leh} \u8212- one would have to search the entire world. The {\i get} says the man is from Nehardea, but Rashi notes that perhaps he was merely {\i in} Nehardea when he wrote it; perhaps he had since moved on; perhaps there was once another Androlinai who lived there and left. There would be no end to such concerns. Rava therefore concludes: {\i mishum kevodo de-avuh de-Shmuel hu de-shalach hakhi} \u8212- Rav Yehuda Nesi\u8217'ah\u8217's instruction to investigate was not a true halakhic ruling but a gesture of respect to the father of Shmuel, who had raised the question. Rather than dismissing it as unnecessary, he gave him work to do. In reality, Rava maintains, once the known Androlinai fits all the identifying details, we do not require a search for hypothetical duplicates.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mirror Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 IOU Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers an additional proof from a case in Mechoza. Two loan documents \u8212- IOUs \u8212- were found from Mechoza, and the names on them were {\i Chavi bar Nigai} and {\i Ninai bar Chavi}: almost mirror images of each other, each component of one name appearing inverted in the other. These were very common names in that city, making the prospect of confusion quite real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collection Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava bar Avuha nevertheless permitted collection on the basis of these IOUs, notwithstanding the similar names and the possibility of confusion about which document referred to which party.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and Rava\u8217's conclusion is that context and circumstances are sufficient to identify the correct parties. Despite the commonality of these names and despite the mirror-image similarity between them, the combination of circumstances surrounding who holds the document, who is being approached for payment, and what the overall situation is allows us to draw a reliable conclusion about which document refers to which transaction and which parties.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 IOU Safety\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Protections\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye frames his own understanding of why the creditor holding these IOUs may be trusted, offering three reasons not to worry that the wrong person is presenting the document. First, Abaye raises the possibility that the creditor might have {\i dropped} the IOU \u8212- {\i ei l\u8217'nefilla} \u8212- and someone else found it. But creditors are careful: {\i mizahar beh} \u8212- they safeguard such documents precisely because their entire claim rests on them. The concern about accidental loss is therefore negligible. Second, one might worry the creditor gave the IOU to someone else for {\i pikadon} \u8212- safekeeping \u8212- who now misuses it. But as Abaye explains, {\i keivan d\u8217'shmei k\u8217'shmei lo mafkad gabei} \u8212- no creditor would be so foolish as to entrust a document bearing his debtor\u8217's name to another person who happens to share that exact name, since that person could then fraudulently use it to collect. Third, one might argue the IOU was transferred \u8212- {\i mesar leh} \u8212- sold or assigned to another \u8212- but the transfer was not completed and therefore the holder has no valid claim. But this too fails: {\i osiyos niknos b\u8217'mesira} \u8212- the transfer of written documents is effected by the very act of handing them over. If the creditor gave the document to someone, that act itself completes the acquisition, and the holder has a valid claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic threading through all three answers is that the circumstances themselves constrain the universe of plausible explanations. A creditor would not drop his IOU carelessly; a creditor would not entrust it to someone with an identical name; and if it was intentionally transferred, the transfer was legally complete. These considerations together mean that the person presenting the IOU is almost certainly the legitimate creditor, and we may rely on context to establish that identification without demanding additional proof.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Context & Conclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spectrum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stepping back, the cases discussed across 115a\u8211-b form a spectrum. At one extreme is the Yitzchak {\i Reish Galuta} situation \u8212- a case with an extraordinary concentration of identifying information, where Abaye nonetheless insists on concern that another individual might match all the same particulars. In the middle is the Androlinai {\i get} \u8212- a document with multiple identifying details including an unusual name, a wife\u8217's name, and a city, where the question of possible duplicates arises and Rava argues that practical logic forecloses the need for further investigation. At the near end is the mirror-name IOU case from Mechoza, where the names are common, the possibility of confusion is real, and yet collection is allowed based on the totality of circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Connecting Dots\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This spectrum is worth reading alongside the earlier discussion of {\i amra bidedamei}. The concern running through that entire section is that a woman \u8212- or any witness \u8212- might connect dots incorrectly, reasoning from circumstances to a conclusion that seems warranted but is not actually certain. The cases in this later section demonstrate that such dot-connecting is unavoidable. In the charred body case, in the drowned scholars case, and now in the name-identification cases, some inference from context is always required. The question is not whether we may ever reason from circumstances to conclusions \u8212- we must \u8212- but rather how many inferential steps we may take, what quality of identifying information we require, and how much weight we give to explanations that would undermine the most natural reading of the facts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investigation Missing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What runs through all of this as a striking omission is the question of why the {\i Gemara} so consistently treats the woman\u8217's testimony as a fixed datum to be believed or disbelieved, rather than as a starting point for investigation. If the concern is that she is drawing incorrect conclusions \u8212- that she saw her husband shot in battle and assumed he died, or saw smoke and assumed he perished \u8212- the obvious response would be to ask her detailed questions: what exactly did you see, when, from what distance, what happened immediately after? A careful interrogation might well reveal whether her report reflects direct observation or inference. The {\i Gemara} does not explain why this kind of probing examination is not the standard response, and that absence remains a notable gap in the analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 116\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witness Testimony Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Impressions vs Facts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yesterday\u8217's discussion centered on a fundamental problem in the law of witness testimony regarding a husband\u8217's death: when a woman reports facts that would permit her to remarry, we must ask whether she is conveying concrete knowledge or merely drawing conclusions from impressions. The Gemara uses the phrase {\i amar bedami} to capture this concern \u8212- she may be stating as fact something she perceived or inferred, perhaps without even being fully aware that she has done so. At the same time, the Gemara acknowledges that in many cases we simply will not have all the concrete facts available to us, and that we may nonetheless be entitled to draw certain conclusions from what we do know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Cases Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That tension \u8212- between requiring concrete facts and permitting reasoned inference \u8212- was the backdrop for the Gemara\u8217's discussion of cases such as a drowned man pulled from the water, a charred body, and reports of death delivered by messenger. The Gemara now turns to a case that sits precisely at the intersection of these two concerns: a message was sent that Yitzchak Reish Galuta, the son of the sister of Rav Bibi, had died. The question is whether we can act on that report \u8212- and underlying it is a question about whether knowing a person\u8217's name, and knowing someone who fits that name, is sufficient context for us to conclude that the report refers to the person we know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yitzchak Reish Galuta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity from Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case itself raises an immediate puzzle. The term {\i reish galuta} ordinarily refers to the Exilarch, a singular political office. How, then, could there be any question about which person named Yitzchak Reish Galuta died? It seems the term may have been used more broadly to refer to regional officials of some standing, or alternatively, the title here is secondary and the identifying detail is really \u8220"the son of the sister of Rav Bibi\u8221" \u8212- yet even that description raises the question of how many individuals might fit it. The Gemara\u8217's point is precisely this: we have a name and a identifying detail, and we know someone who matches. Is that enough to allow us to act?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces here the foundational debate between Abaye and Rava about what level of verification is required when we have a name \u8212- and possibly identifying details \u8212- that matches a known individual. Abaye holds that we must be concerned that another person might share the same name, and we are therefore obligated to investigate. Rava holds that if we are going to take that concern seriously, we would need to search the entire world, since a person who once lived in a given city might now reside elsewhere and still match the description. That standard would be impossible to meet, and so Rava concludes there is in practice no such requirement. As a gentle gesture toward Rav Bibi\u8217's father, who had asked the question, Rava suggests that when Rabbi Yehuda Nesiah told him to investigate all of Nehardea, he did not mean it as a formal legal requirement but as a courtesy response to an honorable inquiry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Found Get Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neharda\u8217'a Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye cites his own proof-text for his position. He says: where do I derive that we are concerned about another person with the same name? From a {\i get} found in Nehardea. The {\i get} identified the husband as Andulinay of Nehardea and named his wife. The father of Shmuel sent this {\i get} to Rabbi Yehuda Nesiah, the grandson of Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi, asking whether we must investigate to confirm there is no one else matching that description, or whether the person we already know who fits it is sufficient. Rabbi Yehuda Nesiah ruled: {\i tivdu Neharda\u8217'a kula} \u8212- you must check all of Nehardea. Despite knowing someone who matched the name, city, and wife\u8217's name, that is not enough; you must confirm that no one else fits the same profile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Check & Eliminate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's position is thus that while verification is required, it is not an infinite regress. If one checks and finds no other candidate, the investigation has done its job. The standard is not to canvass the whole world, but to make a reasonable and thorough inquiry within the relevant community. If after that inquiry there is no competing candidate, we may proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava: Common Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechuza Writs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava brings his own proof from practice. He reports that in Mechuza, his home city, two writs of debt circulated bearing the names {\i Chavi bar Nanai} and {\i Nanai bar Chavi} \u8212- clearly very common names in that locale. It is important to clarify a point about these cases: the common names belong to the {\i creditors}, not to both parties. The debtor\u8217's identity was presumably unambiguous. The question was whether the individuals presenting the writs and bearing those common names could be assumed to be the genuine creditors, given that many others shared those names. Rabba bar Avua \u8212- presumably a court authority in Mechuza \u8212- allowed collection on these documents. He was not deterred by the fact that many others in the city shared the creditors\u8217' names.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Found Get Case (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Money vs Issur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi draws an explicit analogy from this {\i dinei mamonot} case to the domain of {\i issur}. His reasoning runs as follows: here we have a monetary case where we concretely know that other people share the creditor\u8217's name \u8212- {\i shechichi tuva}, they are very common \u8212- and yet we do not require further verification beyond the fact that the person is presenting the document. If we do not demand exhaustive verification even under those conditions in money matters, then certainly in a case of {\i issur} \u8212- permitting a woman to remarry \u8212- where we do not actually know of anyone else with the same name, we should not be required to investigate the entire city. The analogy is admittedly imperfect, since one could distinguish in multiple directions, but the underlying principle is that not every circumstance of a shared name triggers an obligation to search and verify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava: Common Names (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document as Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, does not simply concede. He distinguishes the {\i Mechuza} case by pointing to the supporting evidence inherent in the act of {\i presentation}. In a case where a person presents a document bearing their name, that act of presentation is itself meaningful. The Gemara considers and eliminates several alternative explanations for how the writ might have come into the wrong hands. If we worry that the original creditor dropped it, the Gemara responds that people are careful with their financial documents. If we worry that the real creditor deposited it with someone of the same name for safekeeping, the Gemara responds that no one would be foolish enough to entrust such a document to a person who shares their own name. If we worry that the sale of the debt to this person was not legally completed, the Gemara responds: {\i otiyot niknot bimesirah} \u8212- documents are acquired through physical transfer, so the moment it was handed over, the sale was complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possession vs Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a subtle but important conceptual point, one that Tosafot makes explicit. We have a general principle in {\i dinei mamonot} that {\i chazakah mah shetachas yad adam harei hu shelo} \u8212- what a person holds is presumed to be theirs. Why, then, do we need all of these elaborate arguments to justify Abaye\u8217's position? Tosafot answers: the presumption of physical ownership covers the piece of paper itself. But a document\u8217's legal force \u8212- its capacity to function as testimony, to speak as a writ \u8212- depends on something more. For a {\i shtar chov} or a {\i get} to operate as a legal instrument, it must not merely be owned but must be {\i presented by the right person}, by the one to whom it testifies. The document, in this sense, does not merely sit passively in one\u8217's possession; it actively testifies on behalf of the person presenting it. That is precisely why Abaye argues that a {\i get} found on the street raises questions that a {\i get} being actively presented does not. When someone produces a writ and their name matches it, the document is engaged in an act of testimony on their behalf, and the concern about mistaken identity is substantially diminished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Anan bar Chiya Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get from Sura\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a concrete case that brings the Abaye-Rava debate into sharp relief. A {\i get} was found in Sura. It read: \u8220"In the city of Sura, I, Anan bar Chiya of Nehardea, have divorced my wife\u8221" \u8212- and named her. The man identified himself as Anan bar Chiya, from Nehardea, and the {\i get} was physically written in Sura.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Candidate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis conducted an investigation from Sura to Nehardea and found only one other individual named Anan bar Chiya \u8212- a man originally from a place called Chagra, but who had been residing in Nehardea. His wife\u8217's name presumably also matched. So there did exist a second person who fit the description in the {\i get}: his name matched, his city of residence matched (Nehardea), and his wife\u8217's name matched. However, witnesses testified that on the very day the {\i get} was written in Sura, this second Anan bar Chiya \u8212- the one from Chagra \u8212- had been with them in Nehardea. He was demonstrably not in Sura on the day the {\i get} was executed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Elimination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds: even according to my view, which requires investigation, this case presents no problem. I said you must check \u8212- and you checked. You found one other candidate, and you were able to eliminate him. Witnesses confirm he was in Nehardea on the day in question. {\i Ma ba\u8217'ei b\u8217'Sura?} \u8212- what would he be doing in Sura on that day? The investigation was done properly, a potential competing candidate was identified and eliminated, and therefore we may proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Concrete Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, characteristically, takes the opposite position. Even according to his general view that we are not concerned about other people with the same name, here \u8212- where a concrete second candidate has actually been identified \u8212- the concern becomes real and cannot simply be waved away. The very fact of investigation has introduced a concrete doubt where none existed before. And as for the evidence that the second candidate was in Nehardea that day: {\i dilma begamla parcha azal} \u8212- perhaps he traveled by some extraordinarily fast means and arrived in Sura in time. The point seems almost deliberately absurd, and indeed Rashi and Tosafot note that this likely refers to some form of miraculous transport or {\i sheid}. The argument appears to be that once a concrete alternative exists, even logically implausible explanations cannot fully dispel the doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mili Masar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers a third and considerably more reasonable possibility for Rava\u8217's concern: {\i mili masar} \u8212- it is not necessary for the husband to be physically present in the place where the {\i get} is written. The {\i get} names the city in which it is being executed, not the city where the husband issued his instructions. Rav Safra and Rav Huna both taught this explicitly to scribes: if you are sitting in Shili writing a {\i get} but the husband gave you instructions while in Hini, you write Shili as the place of the {\i get}. And conversely, if you are in Hini when the husband spoke to you in Shili, you write Hini. The language of the {\i get} is in the first person \u8212- \u8220"I, so-and-so, divorce my wife\u8221" \u8212- but the place recorded is the scribe\u8217's location, not the husband\u8217's. Therefore, the fact that the {\i get} says it was written in Sura does not tell us anything about where Anan bar Chiya himself actually was. The second candidate need not have traveled to Sura at all \u8212- his instructions could have been transmitted to a scribe who happened to be writing in Sura. This makes Rava\u8217's concern not only plausible but quite rational.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sesame Seeds Recap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apply Standards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then returns to a case that had been set aside \u8212- the case involving sesame seeds. Rav Yeimar rules {\i lo chaishinan} \u8212- we are not concerned. Rav Ashi rules {\i chaishinan} \u8212- we are concerned. And the {\i halachah} follows Rav Ashi: we are concerned. This conclusion is reached in what is effectively a {\i mammonot} context, one with perhaps fewer identifying marks than some of the other cases. The broader point is that all of these cases \u8212- the {\i get}, the death report, the drowned body, the writ of debt \u8212- are variations on a common set of questions about what level of identification is required, what kinds of evidence are sufficient, and at what point we may conclude that a report or document refers to a specific known individual. The cases are not necessarily governed by a single rule, and the standards likely vary depending on numerous factors: the number and specificity of identifying marks, whether there is supporting evidence, whether we concretely know of another candidate, and whether the domain is {\i mamonot} or {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sesame Seeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One correction to what was said previously: the case at issue involves sesame seeds, not sunflower seeds. Sesame seeds are in any case likely the more valuable commodity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketata Quarreling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketata Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah had mentioned two scenarios in which a woman\u8217's report of her husband\u8217's death is not believed: when there is a state of war ({\i milchama}) in the world, and when there is {\i ketata} \u8212- fighting \u8212- between husband and wife. The war scenario raises concerns about the fog of war and misperception; the {\i ketata} scenario raises a different set of concerns. The Gemara now asks: {\i heichi dami ketata}? What constitutes {\i ketata} in practice? Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel says: she tells her husband \u8220"divorce me\u8221" \u8212- {\i garsheni}. The Gemara objects: {\i kulan nami amru hachi} \u8212- all women say this in the heat of argument. Rashi explains that this is a common expression of frustration. The Gemara therefore clarifies: the operative case is not that she says \u8220"divorce me\u8221" but that she goes about telling people \u8220"{\i gerashtani}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"my husband has already divorced me.\u8221" She is publicly claiming to be divorced even though she has no {\i get} and even though we know it is not true.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that point, the Gemara raises a further question: if she is publicly claiming to be divorced, why not simply believe her about the divorce itself, based on the ruling of Rav Hamnuna? Rav Hamnuna held that a woman who tells her husband to his face that he has already divorced her is believed, on the principle that {\i ein isha me\u8217'iza paneiha bifnei baalah} \u8212- a woman would not be so brazen as to say this to her husband\u8217's face unless it were true. The Gemara resolves this by establishing a more specific case: she said she was divorced in front of named witnesses \u8212- Reuven and Shimon \u8212- and when those witnesses were asked, they said it never happened. She is thus a confirmed fabricator of the divorce claim, yet she does not have a {\i get}. This very fact \u8212- that she is publicly asserting a fictional divorce \u8212- demonstrates that she has emotionally and psychologically exited the marriage, and it is this posture that disqualifies her subsequent report of his death from being credited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lying vs Misinterpretati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents two distinct rationales for why {\i ketata} undermines credibility. Rav Chanina says: {\i mishum de\u8217'meshakra} \u8212- because she will intentionally lie. Rav Shimi bar Ashi says: {\i mishum de\u8217'amra bedidami} \u8212- not because she is deliberately deceptive, but because her emotional state colors her perception. She would like her husband to be dead, and so she interprets ambiguous or incomplete evidence in that direction, unconsciously constructing a narrative of his death from vague impressions. Tosafot raises the obvious question: if the {\i ketata} case involves a woman who has already been proven to lie \u8212- she claimed a divorce that witnesses denied \u8212- why does the Gemara need a separate explanation about unconscious misinterpretation? She is already a confirmed liar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blame Shifts Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between the two rationales becomes practically significant at the top of 27b, where the Gemara introduces the case in which {\i he} is to blame for the fighting \u8212- {\i adal hu ketata}. If it is clear that the husband\u8217's behavior is driving the conflict, that she is not seeking to leave the marriage but is being mistreated within it, then the concern about intentional lying is substantially reduced. She demonstrably wants to remain married and has no motive to fabricate his death. However, the concern about unconscious misinterpretation \u8212- {\i bedidami} \u8212- remains. Even if she would not deliberately lie, the ongoing difficulty and pain of the marriage might still unconsciously influence how she reads ambiguous information about his fate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses the apparent contradiction: if we know she has already lied \u8212- she made a false divorce claim \u8212- why don\u8217't we conclude she is a confirmed liar and disbelieve her regardless of who is to blame? Tosafot answers: lying about a divorce is one thing, but falsely claiming her husband is dead is another matter entirely. An earlier {\i perek} catalogued the severe consequences that fall upon a woman who claims her husband is dead, remarries, and is then confronted with his return. A woman would not be willing to expose herself to those consequences \u8212- losing both the first and second husband, and bearing children of uncertain status \u8212- merely to escape a marriage in which the husband is at fault. She would not go that far in her deception when she knows he bears the blame. So the concern narrows to unconscious misinterpretation, not intentional lying, in the case where he is responsible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ed Echad Ketata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Party Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks the parallel question it had asked regarding {\i milchama}: what if the report of the husband\u8217's death comes not from the wife herself but from an objective third-party witness \u8212- an {\i ed echad}? In the {\i milchama} context, the value of an outside witness was not obvious, since the fog of war affects everyone\u8217's perceptions. Here, by contrast, the concern about {\i ketata} is specifically tied to the wife\u8217's emotional state and her relationship with her husband. A third party has none of that emotional involvement. Should an {\i ed echad} be believed straightforwardly when there is a known {\i ketata}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Theories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer depends on which underlying theory explains why an {\i ed echad} is believed in the first place when testifying to a husband\u8217's death. The first theory: {\i milta de\u8217'avida le-igluya lo meshaker} \u8212- a person will not lie about something that will eventually come to light. If the husband is alive, he will show up, and the false testimony will be exposed. Under this theory, an outside witness in a {\i ketata} situation should indeed be believed; his concern about being found out is entirely independent of the state of the marriage. The second theory: {\i hi dayka u\u8217'minsava} \u8212- the reason we trust an {\i ed echad} is that we rely on the wife\u8217's own careful investigation before she acts on the testimony. She knows the consequences of remarrying incorrectly, and so she will rigorously verify the report before proceeding. The {\i ed echad}\u8217's testimony is thus only valid in combination with the wife\u8217's backup diligence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiku Unresolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the second theory, the case of {\i ketata} presents a real problem. {\i Ve-hacha keivan de-is lah ketata lo dayka} \u8212- since the marriage is in turmoil, she is not going to exercise careful, sober investigation. She is motivated, consciously or not, to accept the report and act on it without the diligent scrutiny that ordinarily backs up the {\i ed echad}\u8217's testimony. The Gemara leaves this unresolved: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modern Applications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 War & Tragedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical stakes of this unresolved question are significant. The concepts of {\i ketata} and {\i milchama} have very broad potential application. Many of the most devastating human catastrophes \u8212- wars, terrorist attacks, building collapses, plane disasters \u8212- fall into the category of {\i milchama}-type scenarios. And marriages, particularly under conditions of stress and tragedy, are not always in a state of tranquility. The {\i teiku} on {\i ed echad} in a {\i ketata} situation means that in precisely those circumstances where testimony is most urgently needed, its admissibility may be in doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This directly affects the scope of the rabbinic solutions to the {\i agunah} problem. The leniencies that the Sages developed over centuries to permit women to remarry when their husbands\u8217' deaths cannot be formally established depend heavily on the credibility of {\i ed echad} testimony and on the woman\u8217's own report. If {\i ketata} limits even third-party testimony, and if {\i milchama} scenarios are broadly defined, then the practical availability of these solutions is substantially curtailed. There is an extensive literature of responsa dealing with precisely these questions \u8212- following the Holocaust, after 9/11, and in other mass-casualty events \u8212- and the {\i poskim} have had to navigate all of these considerations with enormous care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehuda Stringent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mourning Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this backdrop, the Mishnah records the view of Rabbi Yehuda, who applies a more stringent standard across the board: {\i le\u8217'olam ein ne\u8217'emenet} \u8212- she is never believed without additional corroboration, regardless of whether it is wartime or peacetime and regardless of the state of the marriage. Specifically, Rabbi Yehuda requires that the woman exhibit visible signs of mourning \u8212- torn garments, weeping, and other outward expressions of grief \u8212- before her testimony about her husband\u8217's death will be credited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smart vs Foolish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages respond with a straightforward objection: {\i de\u8217'varecha pike\u8217'achas tinasei, shotah lo tinasei} \u8212- according to your position, a clever woman who knows how to perform grief convincingly will be permitted to remarry, while a simple woman who does not know to stage a display will not. That is an arbitrary and unjust standard. The Sages therefore conclude: {\i achas zu ve\u8217'achas zu tinasei} \u8212- whether she is shrewd or not, if the facts support her claim, she may remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Coached Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then records an illustrative incident. A woman came before Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's court \u8212- which required these outward signs of mourning \u8212- and the people around her coached her in advance. They told her: before you walk in, eulogize your husband, rend your garments, loosen your hair, and then enter and tell Rabbi Yehuda what happened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coaching Falsification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: did they thereby teach her to falsify evidence \u8212- {\i afu\u8217'ei shikra}? The Gemara answers no. Those who coached her held like the Sages, not like Rabbi Yehuda: in their view, she was fully believable even without the dramatic display. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's requirement was, in their opinion, an artificial condition not demanded by law. When they coached her to perform the display, they were not teaching her to lie but rather helping her satisfy what they considered to be an illegitimate technical hurdle in order to reach what they believed was the correct halachic outcome. The discomfort remains \u8212- there is something troubling about the entire episode, a kind of {\i limdu leshonam davar sheker} \u8212- but the Gemara accepts the explanation as technically exculpatory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beis Hillel Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Major Innovation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now reach the foundational principle that underlies this entire {\i perek}: the rule that a woman is believed to report her husband\u8217's death and may on that basis remarry. This is a striking {\i chiddush} on multiple levels. Ordinarily, a woman\u8217's testimony is not accepted in the same way as a man\u8217's, and certainly one would not expect a {\i nogea badavar} \u8212- a party with a personal interest in the outcome \u8212- to be credited. Yet here the Sages introduced exactly that rule: the wife\u8217's own testimony about her husband\u8217's death suffices to permit remarriage. This principle had already been assumed in earlier {\i perakim} of the tractate, which discussed the consequences when a woman remarried on the basis of such testimony and the husband later appeared. But the principle itself is introduced and established here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Hillel Limited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Interestingly, it is Beis Hillel who initially takes the more restrictive position. They rule: {\i lo shama\u8217'nu ela be-va min ha-katzir u\u8217've-otah medinah ke-ma\u8217'aseh she-hayah} \u8212- we have only heard this leniency as applying to the specific type of case that served as the original precedent. That precedent involved a woman returning from the grain harvest, reporting that her husband died, and the death occurring in the same locale \u8212- not in a distant country. Beis Hillel holds that the leniency cannot be extended beyond those parameters: the same type of agricultural work, the same local setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Shammai responds that this level of specificity is untenable. They ask: does it matter whether she came from the wheat harvest or the olive harvest or the grape harvest? Is the type of agricultural work actually relevant? And does it matter whether the death occurred locally or in a distant country? The principle behind the leniency, they argue, has nothing to do with those particulars; they appeared in the original case by historical accident. {\i Lo dibru chachamim ba-katzir ela ba-hoveh} \u8212- the Sages spoke of the harvest because that was the circumstance that happened, not because the harvest is legally significant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Generalize Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reductio Ad Absurdum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Shammai\u8217's argument is a {\i reductio ad absurdum}. If Beis Hillel insists on replicating the original case\u8217's details, then the leniency should apply only to wheat \u8212- {\i katzir chittim} \u8212- and not even to barley harvest. And should it extend only to grain and not to olive-picking, date-gathering, grape-harvesting, or any other form of agricultural work? Since Beis Hillel would surely agree that the type of crop is irrelevant, they must concede that at least {\i some} abstraction beyond the literal original case is required. And once that concession is made, Beis Shammai asks, why stop at the type of harvest? Why not also generalize with respect to geography?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Local vs Distant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Hillel, however, does not concede equally on both points. They hold that while the type of agricultural work is indeed irrelevant \u8212- that much they grant \u8212- the distinction between a local death and a distant one is substantive. When the death occurs locally \u8212- {\i be-otah medinah de-shchichi inshi} \u8212- people are present, people who know the husband and wife, and if the husband is alive, the lie will quickly be exposed. That social visibility is a real deterrent to false testimony. When the death is reported from a distant country \u8212- {\i de-la shchichi inshi} \u8212- no one there knows them, and the woman could plausibly lie without discovery. {\i Mirtata} \u8212- she is afraid to lie when she is locally known; {\i la mirtata} \u8212- she is not similarly afraid when the event occurred far away where no one recognizes them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caravans Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Shammai counters: {\i hachi nami shchichi shayarta} \u8212- there are caravans, there is regular travel and commerce between cities and countries, and the facts will surface regardless of distance. Word travels. The threat of exposure is not meaningfully diminished merely because the death occurred in a distant place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Concedes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ultimately, Beis Hillel concedes and rules like Beis Shammai, accepting the broader generalization. This concession is significant \u8212- it is one of the relatively rare instances where Beis Hillel acknowledges that Beis Shammai\u8217's reasoning is compelling and reverses course.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Original Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Snake Bite Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then identifies the original case that gave rise to this entire body of law. Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel reports: it was the end of the wheat harvest season, and ten men went out to harvest wheat. A snake bit one of them, and he died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investigation Confirmed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His wife came and reported the death to the {\i beis din}. The court did not simply accept her word; {\i veshalchu umatzu kidvareha} \u8212- they investigated and found her account to be accurate. There was independent corroboration. What is notable, given that ten people were present at the scene, is that it was specifically the wife who came to report to the {\i beis din}. And it is equally notable that despite the availability of corroborating witnesses, the case led the Sages to establish a principle based on the wife\u8217's testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One possible reading of the Gemara\u8217's thinking: the Sages found themselves asking what would have happened had the corroboration not been available. What if there had been no surviving witnesses, or they were inaccessible? Would the woman have been left without remedy? That hypothetical seems to have prompted the more general principle. Having confirmed through investigation that this woman told the truth, the Sages extrapolated to establish the rule: {\i ha-isha she-amrah meis ba\u8217'ali tinasei} \u8212- a woman who says her husband has died may remarry. And similarly, she may enter into {\i yibum}. The principle was crystallized from a concrete, verified case and then extended as a general rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parah Adumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prohibition Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a parallel case from a seemingly unrelated area of law to analyze the question of when we generalize from a concrete case and when we confine a ruling to its original circumstances. The {\i Braisa} teaches a series of prohibitions relating to the transport of the ashes and water of the {\i parah adumah}: one may not carry {\i mei chatas} and {\i efer chatas} \u8212- the purification water and ashes \u8212- and take them across the Jordan River, whether by boat, by throwing them from one bank to the other, by floating them on a raft, or by carrying them on the back of an animal or a person who is not standing with feet on the ground. However, it is permitted to carry them over a bridge, since the bridge is connected to the earth. And this applies to the Jordan and to all other rivers alike.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Narrow View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Akiva disagrees. He rules: {\i lo amru ela Yarden u\u8217'vesfinah} \u8212- the prohibition was stated only regarding the Jordan River, and only in a boat. Not other rivers, and not other means of crossing. His principle is {\i ke-ma\u8217'aseh she-hayah} \u8212- like the case in which it occurred. The ruling is limited to the precise parameters of the original incident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Broad View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan} take the broader view: once the prohibition is established for the Jordan and for boats, it extends to all rivers and all methods of crossing that share the relevant characteristics.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Generalize vs Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now analyzes whether the debate of Rabbi Chanina ben Akiva and the {\i Rabbanan} maps onto the debate of Beis Hillel and Beis Shammai, and what principles govern when to generalize and when to limit. The {\i Rabbanan} could say: even we, who generalize here, would not necessarily generalize in all cases. In the case of the woman\u8217's testimony about her husband\u8217's death, Beis Hillel initially did limit the ruling. But there was a clear reason Beis Hillel ultimately conceded and expanded: the rationale for believing the woman is {\i mirtata} \u8212- she fears exposure, she fears the consequences \u8212- and that rationale applies equally in all circumstances. There is an identifiable logic underlying the leniency, and because that logic applies broadly, the rule can be extended broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Logic Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here, with the {\i parah adumah} transfer prohibition, there is no such clear logic. Why should carrying {\i mei chatas} over a river on a boat be prohibited? There is no obvious reason that such transport is inherently problematic for the sanctity or purity of the ashes. The prohibition exists because something went wrong once \u8212- it is a {\i gezerah}, a rabbinic response to an event. And since there is no clear underlying logic, there is no principled basis for extending it beyond the specific circumstances of that event. Rabbi Chanina ben Akiva\u8217's view that we confine it to {\i ke-ma\u8217'aseh she-hayah} reflects exactly this principle: without a rationale to drive abstraction, we stay with the concrete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Corpse Incident\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The original incident was this: someone was transporting {\i mei chatas} and {\i efer chatas} across the Jordan in a boat, and {\i nimtza kezayis min ha-mes tachuv b\u8217'karkisa shel sefinah} \u8212- a piece of a human corpse, the size of an olive, was found lodged in the hull of the boat. That corpse piece rendered the contents of the boat {\i tamei}, contaminating the ashes. The Sages responded immediately \u8212- {\i b\u8217'otah sha\u8217'ah amru} \u8212- and prohibited this practice. But the prohibition was a reaction to a specific accident, not derived from a general principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman vs Parah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Akiva, conversely, could explain why he does not apply Beis Shammai\u8217's logic in the {\i parah adumah} case even while Beis Shammai generalizes in the woman\u8217's case. The reason Beis Shammai generalizes there is {\i dayka u\u8217'minsava} \u8212- there is always a structural logic at work: the woman will investigate carefully before acting, and the prospect of exposure will deter false testimony across all circumstances. That logic provides a principled basis for abstraction. In the {\i parah adumah} case, there is simply no equivalent logic. {\i Mishum ma\u8217'aseh she-hayah b\u8217'Yarden u\u8217'vesfinah d\u8217'hava ma\u8217'aseh gazru rabbanan} \u8212- the Rabbanan issued their prohibition because of that specific event. {\i B\u8217'she\u8217'ar naharos d\u8217'lo hava ma\u8217'aseh lo gazru rabbanan} \u8212- for other rivers, where no such event occurred, there is no basis for the {\i gezerah} to apply. The rule should therefore remain as narrow as its origin. In the case of the woman\u8217's testimony, there is a reason we want to extend the leniency broadly: we want her to be able to remarry, there is a rationale supporting the rule\u8217's application in all circumstances, and therefore we generalize. In the case of {\i parah adumah}, we have no such driving rationale and no logic compelling extension, and so we limit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 117\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a running discussion about the foundational {\i chiddush} of {\i Chazal}: the willingness to believe a woman who testifies that her husband died, enabling her to remarry. This innovation arose from a specific historical scenario involving a husband who died during the wheat harvest, and it generated a remarkable debate in which {\i Beis Shammai} took the more lenient position. The {\i mishnah} on 117b records another such debate \u8212- again, with {\i Beis Shammai} on the side of leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai omrim tinasei v\u8217'sitol kesubasah}: once she is permitted to remarry, she may also collect her {\i kesubah} from her husband\u8217's estate. {\i Beis Hillel omrim tinasei v\u8217'lo tital kesubasah}: they are prepared to believe her testimony for purposes of personal status and remarriage, but not to allow her to collect from the estate. The concern is not only one of testimonial weight. There is a secondary worry that allowing monetary collection alongside remarriage makes the entire arrangement more attractive \u8212- not merely liberating her from the {\i agunah} situation, but providing a financial incentive that could color, whether consciously or unconsciously, the quality of her testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Package Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai} presses back: {\i hishvitem ervah chamurah v\u8217'lo nakil mamon hakal?} You have already crossed the far more serious threshold \u8212- releasing her from the prohibition of {\i ervah}, treating her as no longer a married woman, permitting her to marry another man \u8212- and yet you refuse to cross what should be the easier threshold of monetary collection? {\i Beis Shammai}\u8217's argument is that the permissive ruling on {\i ervah} and the permissive ruling on {\i mamon} are a package deal. If she is believed enough to remarry, she is believed enough to collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Brothers Inherit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Hillel} responds with a striking argument: {\i matzinu she-ein ha-achim nichnasin lenachalah al piha} \u8212- we find that the brothers cannot enter into the inheritance on the basis of her testimony alone. This concession, made by {\i Beis Shammai} themselves, demonstrates that her belief is not across-the-board. If her testimony constituted genuine {\i eidus} in the full sense, the brothers \u8212- or father, or other heirs \u8212- would be entitled to divide the estate on that basis. But take that testimony to a court handling inheritance and they will demand proper witnesses; a single woman\u8217's testimony does not constitute the standard of proof required for probate. The fact that {\i Beis Shammai} agrees on this point shows that her believability is context-specific.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status vs.\u160 ?Monetary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges is a clean distinction: her testimony is accepted in the context of the court adjudicating her personal status \u8212- whether she is a widow permitted to remarry \u8212- but not in a separate court case concerning the distribution of her husband\u8217's estate. {\i Beis Hillel} carries this logic further: if the testimony is not universal but court-specific, then even within the domain of personal status, one can distinguish between her status as a widow permitted to remarry and her status as a widow entitled to collect her {\i kesubah}. These are two different claims, even if they often travel together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai} offers a textual counter: {\i v\u8217'lo mi-sefer kesubah nilmod?} Can we not learn the package-deal nature of the ruling from the {\i kesubah} document itself? The language of the {\i kesubah} as they understood it reads: {\i she-im tinasei le-acher titali mah she-kasuv lichi} \u8212- if you remarry someone else, you are entitled to collect what is written for you. The {\i kesubah} itself thus ties the permission to remarry to the right to collect, making them inseparable. When her testimony is accepted in family court, the {\i kesubah}\u8217's own language makes the monetary entitlement a built-in consequence. The probate court is a separate matter \u8212- that testimony remains irrelevant there \u8212- but within the framework of her own status and remarriage, the {\i kesubah} creates the package.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Concedes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'chozru Beis Hillel le\u8217'horot k\u8217'divrei Beis Shammai} \u8212- and {\i Beis Hillel} reversed their ruling and held in accordance with {\i Beis Shammai}. Even they ultimately acknowledged that once the {\i kesubah}\u8217's own language creates this connection, the monetary collection follows from the permitted remarriage. The two cannot be separated when the very document at the center of the case binds them together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adds a related ruling from Rav Chisda: if the {\i yevamah} performs {\i yibum} with her brother-in-law, he enters into the inheritance on her account \u8212- {\i nichnas lenachalah al piha} \u8212- because the inheritance in that case is not a separate proceeding but flows directly from his status as one who has performed {\i yibum} and thereby stepped into his brother\u8217's role. The {\i Torah} itself says {\i yakum al shem achiv}: he shall stand in his brother\u8217's name, and with that comes the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Derash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then comments that {\i Beis Hillel} and {\i Beis Shammai} essentially treated the language of the {\i kesubah} as quasi-scriptural \u8212- {\i heim darshu midrash ketubah} \u8212- subjecting it to the same kind of textual analysis one applies to {\i pesukim}. The {\i Gemara} uses this as an occasion to note that we, too, can do {\i midrash pesukim}, and the {\i pasuk yakum al shem achiv} allows us to derive that the estate passes with the act of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Tosafos} cited in this context notes that the approach of doing {\i midrash ketubah} \u8212- treating the {\i kesubah}\u8217's precise phrasing as determinative \u8212- is characterized in the {\i Gemara} in {\i Kesubot} as a {\i Beis Shammai} methodology. {\i Beis Hillel} does not generally engage in {\i midrash ketubah}. The broader question of what it means to treat a contractual document with the kind of textual weight one accords to Scripture \u8212- giving decisive weight to precise formulation rather than seeking intent or context \u8212- is a topic that will be developed extensively when the learning reaches {\i Maseches Kesubot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Nachman Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman introduces a crucial procedural distinction. When a woman comes to {\i beit din} and says {\i meis ba\u8217'ali \u8212- hiteiruni lehinasay} \u8212- \u8220"my husband died, permit me to remarry\u8221" \u8212- the ruling follows naturally: {\i matirin osah lehinasay v\u8217'nosnim lah kesubasah}, we permit her to remarry and she receives her {\i kesubah}. The intent is clear, the case is framed in the domain of personal status, and the {\i kesubah} follows as a package.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But now suppose she comes to {\i beit din} and says {\i tenu li kesubasi} \u8212- give me my {\i kesubah} \u8212- without expressing any interest in remarriage. In that case, Rav Nachman rules: {\i af lehinasay ein matirin osah} \u8212- not only do we not give her the {\i kesubah}, we do not even permit her to remarry. The reason is {\i ada\u8217'ata de\u8217'kesubah asai} \u8212- she came with the intention of collecting the {\i kesubah}. She has framed her case as a monetary claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wrong Courtroom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's explanation is precise: {\i uva\u8217'afokei mamona sahadusa ba\u8217'inan} \u8212- when the case is about extracting money, we require formal {\i eidus}. This is not primarily a suspicion that she is lying or manipulating the system in order to collect. Rather, she has walked into the wrong courtroom. The door she entered is the door of {\i dinei mamonos}, and in that courtroom the standard of proof is full testimony. The special rabbinic dispensation that allows her single testimony to carry weight was created for the domain of personal status \u8212- the family-court door, so to speak \u8212- not the civil-court door of monetary claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Locked In\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the obvious question: why can she not simply close that door and open the other one? The {\i sugya} strongly implies that she cannot \u8212- that once she has framed the case as a monetary claim, she is locked into that framing. The ruling does not suggest that we send her home, tell her to come back and say the right words, and then grant both permissions. She appears to be stuck. This is a striking and somewhat troubling feature of the analysis, given the lengths to which the rabbis went in creating the {\i agunah} dispensation in the first place. It is worth noting that the {\i Gemara} need not have gone down this path at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simultaneous Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Together\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises the {\i ibaya}: what if she demands both simultaneously \u8212- {\i hiteiruni lehinasay v\u8217'tenu li ketubati}, \u8220"permit me to remarry and give me my {\i kesubah}\u8221"? Two possible readings present themselves. On one reading, the mere mention of {\i kesubah} signals that this is a {\i kesubah} case \u8212- {\i kivun de\u8217'amra ketubati, da\u8217'ata de\u8217'kesubah avda}. On the other reading, people simply put all their claims before the court at once \u8212- {\i kol milei de\u8217'is lei inshi amru lah le\u8217'beit dina} \u8212- and since she opened with the marriage claim, the case is framed accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the first formulation leaves some ambiguity, the {\i Gemara} then poses a starker scenario: she says {\i tenu li ketubati v\u8217'hiteiruni lehinasay} \u8212- she leads with the {\i kesubah} and follows with the request to remarry. Here, the {\i Gemara} suggests, she has {\i vadai} framed it as a {\i kesubah} case \u8212- {\i hecha vadai da\u8217'ata de\u8217'kesubah avda}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ignorance Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i Gemara} raises a counterargument: {\i ho\u8217'il v\u8217'lo yad\u8217'a be-mai mistaryah} \u8212- perhaps she simply did not know through what mechanism she would be permitted to remarry. People commonly misunderstand procedure. Perhaps she mentioned the {\i kesubah} because she believed, based on popular misapprehension, that submitting the {\i kesubah} was part of the process of obtaining permission to remarry \u8212- {\i shem kasabra hu she-zo hatartah}. Her fundamental interest is in being freed to marry again, and her mention of the {\i kesubah} reflects a procedural misunderstanding, not an intention to frame the case as monetary.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Nachman Intent (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where the {\i sugya} becomes genuinely troubling, and it deserves to be flagged directly. The entire enterprise of believing a woman\u8217's testimony about her husband\u8217's death was built on the extraordinary concern for the {\i agunah} \u8212- the rabbis went to remarkable lengths, departing from ordinary evidentiary standards, in order not to leave women bound and unable to remarry. And yet here, that entire protection becomes precarious based on how she phrases her opening words to {\i beit din}. If she says the wrong thing \u8212- if she mentions her {\i kesubah} first, or even mentions it alongside her request to remarry \u8212- the door may slam shut entirely, leaving her not merely without the {\i kesubah} but without permission to remarry at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One could have constructed the ruling differently and with far less severity. The {\i Gemara} could have said: if she frames it as a {\i kesubah} case, we deny the {\i kesubah} on those grounds but still permit her to remarry on the basis of her testimony. That would have been a coherent and far less punishing result. The {\i Gemara} instead makes it sound as if the wrong framing locks her out of everything. The practical implication \u8212- one the {\i Gemara} does not shy away from \u8212- is that she desperately needs good legal counsel before she ever opens her mouth in front of {\i beit din}. Indeed, the role of the {\i to\u8217'ein rabbani} in contemporary rabbinic courts exists in part precisely for this reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} does raise the possibility that a {\i drasha} on the {\i kesubah} language may be involved in all of this \u8212- a theme, as {\i Tosafos} notes, that connects to debates in {\i Maseches Kesubot} about whether {\i midrash kesubah} is a legitimate methodology and whose position it actually reflects.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Who Testifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to a different dimension of the same subject: who may testify that a husband has died? The general rule, {\i hakol ne\u8217'emanim le\u8217'ha\u8217'idah}, is that all are believed to testify on her behalf. The exceptions, however, form a defined list: {\i chutz me\u8217'chamotah} \u8212- all are believed with the exception of her mother-in-law; and the mother-in-law\u8217's daughter ({\i bas chamotah}), meaning the husband\u8217's sister on the mother\u8217's side; her {\i tzarah} (co-wife); her {\i yevamah} (husband\u8217's brother\u8217's wife); and her {\i bas ba\u8217'alah} (stepdaughter). The {\i mishnah} also notes the distinction from the parallel ruling in {\i Gittin}: these same women are believed to deliver a {\i get} from the husband, because {\i she-ha-ksav mochiach} \u8212- the signed document itself provides the verification that makes their delivery reliable. Oral testimony about death is an entirely different matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic of the exclusion list is that these are women who may have reason to hate the wife \u8212- and specifically, who might lie not out of a desire to help her but in order to set a trap. If she remarries on the strength of false testimony and the husband subsequently reappears, she suffers the devastating consequences that the earlier chapters of the tractate describe. The excluded witnesses, on this reading, are women whose animosity toward the wife makes them capable of engineering precisely that outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother-in-Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mother-in-law\u8217's hostility toward her daughter-in-law is easy to characterize in personal terms \u8212- no one is ever quite good enough for her son. But the {\i Gemara} locates a less personal and more economically grounded motive as well. Property that the wife brought into the marriage will pass to the husband and, upon his death, may ultimately leave the family entirely, enriching the daughter-in-law rather than remaining within the husband\u8217's family of origin. The mother-in-law thus has both emotional and financial reasons to bear the wife ill will.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tzarah}\u8217's motive is the most direct: she is in direct competition with the wife. If she testifies that the husband died and the wife remarries on that basis, and the husband subsequently returns alive, the wife will be forced out of the marriage and the {\i tzarah} will remain as the sole wife. She has everything to gain from the wife\u8217's downfall and nothing to lose from testifying falsely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother\u8217's Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i yevamah} \u8212- the wife of the husband\u8217's brother \u8212- presents a paradoxical situation. Her concern is that if the husband dies without children, her own husband will be obligated to perform {\i yibum} with the wife, making the wife a co-wife in her household. One might think she would therefore want to testify that the husband is alive and thus prevent the {\i yibum} scenario from ever arising. In fact, the {\i Gemara} suggests the opposite dynamic: she may reason that it is better to trigger the {\i yibum} now, on her own terms, in the hope that the husband will turn up alive shortly thereafter \u8212- at which point the {\i yibum} marriage would be invalidated and the threat would be permanently eliminated. There is thus an ironic quality to her testimony: she is hastening the very outcome she fears, gambling that it will be only temporary. Nevertheless, her interest is compromised, and she is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stepdaughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stepdaughter\u8217's exclusion requires little explanation. The animosity between a stepdaughter and her stepmother \u8212- the wife in question \u8212- is presumed and obvious. She has no motivation to assist the woman who has, in her view, taken her mother\u8217's place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Sister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The husband\u8217's sister on the mother\u8217's side ({\i bas chamotah}) is excluded because she absorbs her mother\u8217's animosity. The mother-in-law hates the daughter-in-law; the mother-in-law\u8217's daughter learns and adopts that attitude. {\i Hi nami sanyah lah} \u8212- she too comes to hate her. The animus is personal, inherited from the household atmosphere created by the mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Side Sister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a {\i ibaya}: what about the husband\u8217's sister on the {\i father\u8217's} side \u8212- {\i bas chamiv} \u8212- who has a different mother entirely? Here the mother-in-law\u8217's personal hatred does not enter the picture, since there is no shared mother to transmit it. Does the exclusion still apply?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two possible reasons for the exclusion of the husband\u8217's sister are offered. The first grounds it entirely in the personal, matrilineal transmission of hatred: {\i mishum de\u8217'ika ima desanya lah} \u8212- it is because there is a mother who hates her, and {\i hi nami sanyah lah} \u8212- the daughter adopts it. On this reading, a sister with a different mother would {\i not} be excluded. The second grounds it in a financial concern that applies regardless of which side of the family: the sister feels that the wife is consuming the fruits of her family\u8217's labor \u8212- {\i ka\u8217'akhla li legeisana} \u8212- that property from her family is passing out through the marriage and will end up in the hands of this woman. On this reading, whether the family wealth at issue came from the mother or the father makes no difference; the animosity exists either way, and the exclusion applies to {\i bas chamiv} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes in passing that the term {\i bei nasha} \u8212- which appears in the phrase {\i geisana de\u8217'bei nasha}, referring to the toil of the father\u8217's household in the {\i bas chamiv} scenario \u8212- is an interesting locution. Normally {\i bei nasha} refers to the mother\u8217's side of the family, the side that tends to be forgotten in a patriarchal system (related to the root of {\i nashani}, \u8220"He has caused me to forget\u8221"). It can also appear in the formulary of a {\i kesubah} when the property brought into the marriage comes from the mother\u8217's house rather than the father\u8217's. Its appearance here as a reference to the father\u8217's household is somewhat unusual and the precise usage remains worth examining more carefully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} does not resolve the question conclusively; it ends with the observation that once we understand the financial motive to be the operative concern, {\i lo shena bas chamotah v\u8217'lo shana bas chamiv} \u8212- it makes no difference which side the sister is from. The companion {\i braisa} that lists five excluded women (rather than the {\i mishnah}\u8217's implicit listing) is reconciled by treating the two types of husband\u8217's sister as one category, since the underlying logic subsumes both.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudah Additions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Reverses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the view that a {\i braisa} reflects the position of Rabbi Yehudah: {\i Rabbi Yehuda mosif af eshet av v\u8217'ha-kallah} \u8212- Rabbi Yehudah adds the stepmother and the daughter-in-law to the list of those who may not testify. The Sages respond that these cases are already implied by the reverse relationships listed in the {\i mishnah}. If the {\i mishnah} already excludes the stepdaughter from testifying about her stepmother, then by symmetry the stepmother cannot testify about the stepdaughter. Similarly, if the mother-in-law cannot testify about the daughter-in-law, the daughter-in-law cannot testify about the mother-in-law. There is no need to enumerate them separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Listing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah maintains that the reverse relationships are not obvious and must be stated explicitly. In the case of the mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, {\i bishlama chamotah tanya} \u8212- we understand perfectly well why the mother-in-law hates the daughter-in-law: {\i d\u8217'amrah ka\u8217'achlah l\u8217'girsana\u8217'i} \u8212- she feels the daughter-in-law is consuming the fruits of her labor, or simply that nobody is good enough for her son. But {\i kallah mai ta\u8217'ama sanyah l\u8217'chamotah?} \u8212- what reason does the daughter-in-law have to hate her mother-in-law? That requires an explanation, and since the animosity runs in a less obvious direction, the exclusion must be stated independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i bishlama bas ba\u8217'al d\u8217'sanyah l\u8217'eshet av} \u8212- we understand why a stepdaughter hates her stepmother: {\i d\u8217'amrah ka\u8217'achlah l\u8217'girsana\u8217'i d\u8217'immi} \u8212- she feels this woman is consuming the property her mother brought into the estate. But {\i eshet av mai ta\u8217'ama sanyah l\u8217'bat ba\u8217'al?} \u8212- what does the stepmother have against the stepdaughter? That too is not obvious, and therefore Rabbi Yehudah must list it explicitly rather than relying on an implied reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secrets Exposed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then provides Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's reasoning. Why does a daughter-in-law hate her mother-in-law? {\i D\u8217'megalya l\u8217'vnah kol d\u8217'avdah} \u8212- because the mother-in-law sees everything that goes on in the house and reports it to her son. The daughter-in-law feels that her private life is exposed, that her faults and failings are being narrated to her husband by his mother, and she therefore comes to bear the mother-in-law deep animosity. This is not a financial concern but an entirely domestic one \u8212- the fear of surveillance and of being badmouthed in one\u8217's own marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stepmother Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic explains the stepmother\u8217's animosity toward the stepdaughter: {\i d\u8217'megalya l\u8217'aviah kol d\u8217'avdah} \u8212- the stepdaughter sees everything the stepmother does in the household and will report it to her father, the stepmother\u8217's husband. Just as the daughter-in-law fears being exposed to her husband through his mother, so the stepmother fears being exposed to her husband through his daughter. Once this dynamic is spelled out, Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's logic becomes clear: neither of these hostilities is intuitive, so both must be stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water Reflects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages respond with a principle: {\i kamayim hapanim lapanim ken lev ha\u8217'adam la\u8217'adam} \u8212- as water reflects a face back to the one looking into it, so the heart of one person reflects back the feelings of another. The principle is that animosity is reciprocal by nature: if A bears animosity toward B, B will come to bear animosity toward A, even without a specific practical trigger. Once the {\i mishnah} establishes that the mother-in-law may not testify \u8212- thereby acknowledging that there is animosity running from mother-in-law to daughter-in-law \u8212- the reciprocal animosity is automatic, and no separate ruling is needed to establish that the daughter-in-law cannot testify either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah demurs: {\i hahu b\u8217'divrei Torah ketiv} \u8212- that verse is written about words of Torah, meaning that if you invest your heart in Torah you will receive back in kind. It does not describe the general psychology of interpersonal relationships, and one cannot derive from it a principle that all animosity is automatically reciprocal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Mother-in-Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Yet MIL\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question was raised in {\i Eretz Yisrael} ({\i ba\u8217'u b\u8217'ma\u8217'arava}): what is the status of a {\i chamotah haba\u8217'ah l\u8217'achar mikan} \u8212- a future mother-in-law? How can there be such a thing? The scenario involves {\i yibum}: a woman\u8217's husband is absent or has died, and there exists a brother-in-law whose mother would, in the event of {\i yibum}, become the woman\u8217's mother-in-law. That brother-in-law\u8217's mother is thus a {\i potential} future mother-in-law. The question is whether such a woman is already in the category of those excluded from testifying, or whether since she has no actual present relationship of animosity, she may testify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mi mapka da\u8217'atah d\u8217'nasva v\u8217'naflah kamei yavam v\u8217'sanyah lah?} \u8212- Is this future mother-in-law already anticipating the possibility that this woman will fall before her son for {\i yibum}, and has she begun to hate her preemptively? Is she, so to speak, getting a head start on the inevitable animosity?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Die Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a {\i ta shema} from a related ruling. If a woman comes and says: \u8220"My husband died, and then my father-in-law died\u8221" \u8212- both men having been away together, say, in {\i medinat hayam} \u8212- the ruling is: {\i tinasei v\u8217'ticul kesubasah}, she may remarry and collect her {\i kesubah} based on her own testimony about her husband. But her mother-in-law is {\i asurah} \u8212- the mother-in-law may not remarry on the basis of this same testimony. Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The technical objection might seem easy to dismiss: once the husband died, the father-in-law was no longer her father-in-law, and the mother-in-law was no longer her mother-in-law, so she should be believed to testify about what happened to him. But the {\i Gemara} does not accept this reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruse Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mahu d\u8217'amrinan? \u8212- Lo ba\u8217'alah meis v\u8217'lo chamotah meis.} The concern is that perhaps this entire narrative is a ruse \u8212- that neither the husband nor the father-in-law actually died, and the wife has constructed the entire account precisely in order to harm her mother-in-law. She knows that if she testified directly about her mother-in-law she would not be believed, so she testifies first about her husband\u8217's death (which benefits herself and which she knows will be accepted), and then appends testimony about the father-in-law\u8217's death (which enables the mother-in-law to remarry and thereby creates the trap \u8212- because if the husband turns up alive, the mother-in-law will have remarried on the strength of false testimony). The entire scheme is designed to destroy the mother-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Savrah levatar de\u8217'asya sha\u8217'atah lo seisi v\u8217'sis\u8217'aran} \u8212- her reasoning, according to Rashi, is that she wants to ensure that in the future the mother-in-law will not come and continue to cause her trouble. Even though her husband is currently absent, she is acting now based on the animosity that already exists \u8212- animosity rooted in the fact that the mother-in-law has been badmouthing her to her husband. She is not acting out of an immediate threat but out of an accumulated resentment, and that resentment is real and pre-existing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-existing Animosity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} then draws the distinction that answers the original question about the future mother-in-law. {\i Dilma shani hasam de\u8217'mei-ikra de\u8217'asya sanyah lah} \u8212- the case of the existing mother-in-law is different because the animosity is already there, pre-existing and rooted in a real relationship. In the hypothetical above, the daughter-in-law\u8217's willingness to harm her mother-in-law through this elaborate scheme makes sense precisely because she already harbors deep animosity from the years of the marriage. The future mother-in-law, by contrast, has no relationship with this woman whatsoever. She has not yet become anyone\u8217's mother-in-law. There is no prior history, no accumulated grievance, no existing hostility. The concern about lying for the sake of a future animosity that has not yet taken root does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is: we do not extend the exclusion to those whose animosity is merely hypothetical or future. The rule applies only where there is a pre-existing relationship of actual animosity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conflict Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 1v2 Early\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now addresses cases of conflicting testimony. First: {\i ed echad omer meis venisat} \u8212- one witness says the husband died and she remarries on that basis, {\i uva acher v\u8217'amar lo meis} \u8212- and then a second comes and says he did not die. {\i Harei zu lo tetze} \u8212- she does not have to leave her new marriage. The principle is that once {\i beit din} has accepted the testimony and acted upon it, a subsequent conflicting testimony cannot unravel what has already been ruled upon and executed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2v1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If two say he died and she married, but one says he did not die: {\i af al pi she-niset, tetze} \u8212- even having already remarried, she must leave. Two witnesses prevail over one, and the remarriage must be undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if two say he died and one says he did not: {\i af al pi she-lo niset, tinasei} \u8212- even before she has remarried, the two witnesses establish his death and she is permitted to proceed. The implication of the {\i mishnah}, left implicit until the {\i Gemara} makes it explicit, is that if it is one against one before she has remarried, she may not remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 1v1 Later\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This implication \u8212- that one against one before remarriage leaves her unable to proceed \u8212- creates the analytical problem that the {\i Gemara} addresses. If it is {\i ed echad} saying he died against {\i ed echad} saying he did not, why should she not be permitted? The answer involves the principle of {\i ed echad}\u8217's special weight in this domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'amar Ulla: kol makom she-he\u8217'emina Torah ed echad harei kan shnayim v\u8217'ein davar she-echad b\u8217'makom shnayim.} Ulla\u8217's principle is that wherever the Torah gave special weight to a single witness, that testimony is treated as equivalent to two witnesses \u8212- and a single subsequent witness cannot override it, since one cannot stand against two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a broader set of cases where the Torah gives special weight to a single witness \u8212- {\i Sotah}, {\i Eglah Arufah}, and others. In those cases, the witness given special weight testifies to one side of the equation, and a single witness on the other side cannot override it. Here, the {\i Gemara} notes, the rabbis gave special believability to the testimony that the husband died; they gave no such special weight to testimony that he did not die. Therefore, even in a one-against-one scenario, the one who says he died should carry the weight of two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} reformulates Ulla\u8217's principle more precisely: {\i ed echad omer meis v\u8217'hityiruha linase} \u8212- it is not merely about the testimony being given; the testimony must be acted upon. {\i Ba echad v\u8217'amar lo meis lo tetze me-heteirah harishon} \u8212- once {\i beit din} has ruled on the basis of that first testimony, it acquires the weight of two, and a subsequent single witness cannot override it. Before {\i beit din} rules, the testimony does not yet carry that locked-in weight. This is why one-against-one before remarriage does not permit her to proceed: the first testimony has not yet been ruled upon, and so it has not yet acquired the weight of two.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ulla vs.\u160 ?Nechemiah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla\u8217's model is fundamentally one of formal testimony. When the Torah or the rabbis give special weight to a single witness in a particular domain, they are placing that witness within the framework of {\i eidus} \u8212- formal testimony \u8212- and elevating it to the status of two. The consequence is that the world remains one of formal evidentiary standards: one becomes two, two beats one, and only two legitimate witnesses on the other side would override it. The count never involves informal assessments or quantities of opinions; it is always about the quality and formal weight of testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nechemiah Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nechemiah articulates an entirely different model: {\i kol makom she-he\u8217'emina Torah ed echad halach achar rov de\u8217'os} \u8212- wherever the Torah believed a single witness, the determination follows the majority of opinions. This is a fundamentally quantitative approach. Rabbi Nechemiah holds that when the standard of proof departs from formal two-witness {\i eidus}, one has left the world of formal testimony entirely and entered a world of counting opinions. There is no elevation of one to two, no locking in, no special weight accorded to any one testimony. Instead, all testimonies are placed on a scale and the heavier side wins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Women Count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's model is stark: {\i v\u8217'asu shtei nashim b\u8217'ish echad kishnei anashim b\u8217'ish echad} \u8212- two women testifying against one man count the same as two men testifying against one man. Ordinarily, two women do not constitute formal {\i eidus} and cannot override a single male witness. But in Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's model, when we are in the domain of {\i ed echad} believability, we are no longer asking about formal {\i eidus} at all. We are simply counting: two against one, regardless of the gender or formal qualification of the testifiers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has severe implications for women in the {\i agunah} context. A woman remarries on the strength of a single permitted testimony that her husband died. If two women \u8212- even if they are {\i psulin}, formally disqualified \u8212- then testify that he did not die, Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's model would require her to leave the remarriage, because two opinions now outweigh one. This is a far more precarious protection than Ulla\u8217's model offers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers a reconciliation of the two positions that is worth examining carefully. {\i Kol heicha d\u8217'asa ed echad kasher mei-ikara} \u8212- wherever the first witness who came was a formally qualified ({\i kasher}) male witness, we are in Ulla\u8217's world. That single testimony, once ruled upon by {\i beit din}, is treated as two witnesses, is locked in, and can be overridden only by two formally qualified witnesses. {\i Afilu me\u8217'ah nashim k\u8217'ed echad damyan} \u8212- even a hundred women count as only one, and they cannot override the locked-in testimony of that {\i kasher} witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The domain of Rabbi Nechemiah applies when the first witness was not a formally qualified witness \u8212- {\i kigon d\u8217'asa isha mei-ikara}, such as when a woman testified first. In that case, we are not in the world of formal {\i eidus} from the outset, and Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's quantitative principle governs. {\i V\u8217'asu shtei nashim b\u8217'isha achas kishnei anashim b\u8217'ish echad} \u8212- two women against one woman is like two men against one man. We count opinions rather than weigh formal testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reconciliation thus turns on the identity of the first witness. If a formally qualified witness initiated the testimony, the case enters Ulla\u8217's framework and the testimony is locked in formally. If an informally qualified witness (a woman, a relative, or another {\i pasul}) testified first, the case operates under Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's quantitative model from the start.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This represents two genuinely distinct conceptual frameworks. Ulla\u8217's model maintains that even in the special domain of {\i ed echad} believability, we remain within the structure of formal testimony \u8212- one has simply been elevated to two. Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's model holds that once you have departed from the ordinary standard of two witnesses, you have left {\i eidus} territory entirely and are now simply doing arithmetic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Majority Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final line of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i shnayim omrim meis, echad omer lo meis} \u8212- states that if two say he died and one says he did not die, she may remarry even before having done so. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i pshita} \u8212- isn\u8217't that obvious? Two beats one. The answer is that the {\i chiddush} here operates under Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's model: the two who say he died may be {\i psulei eidus} \u8212- formally disqualified witnesses \u8212- and yet their two opinions outweigh the single opposing opinion. This is the domain of {\i rov de\u8217'os}, the majority of opinions, and the ruling teaches that the majority principle applies even to leniency, not only to stringency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought that when the quantitative model leads to a lenient outcome \u8212- permitting remarriage \u8212- we would refuse to apply it and instead require formal {\i eidus}. The {\i mishnah} teaches that this is not the case. Whether the majority of opinions points toward stringency or leniency, the principle is applied consistently: {\i halach achar rov de\u8217'os}. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's synthesis of Ulla and Rabbi Nechemiah thus determines not only how to handle conflicting testimony but how to understand the very nature of {\i ed echad} believability \u8212- whether it is an elevation within the world of formal testimony or a departure from that world altogether.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 118\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Single Witness Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Innovation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 118 is the last daf of the second-to-last {\i perek}, and we pick up with the {\i mishna} on the bottom of 117b. The broader context is the series of sugyot dealing with the specifics of believing testimony that a woman\u8217's husband has died. This represents a tremendous rabbinic innovation: the extension of legal credence to ordinarily disqualified testimony \u8212- from a single witness, and by extension from a woman \u8212- in order to permit a wife to remarry. As the preceding sugyot have established, this leniency applies not only to a single male witness but to a woman testifying about her own husband\u8217's death, and not to a woman testifying for her daughter-in-law, mother-in-law, or stepdaughter. The principle that emerges across all these cases is that any single qualified witness, including a woman, may testify to a husband\u8217's death and enable his wife to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?One/Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prior {\i mishna} addressed scenarios where testimony conflicts: one witness against one, or one against two. The {\i gemara} there gave greater weight to the testimony that the husband had died, such that even in the case of one against one, the side affirming death carries additional force \u8212- particularly when the testimony had already been accepted by {\i beit din} before it was contradicted. If it was accepted first and then contradicted, the contradiction cannot dislodge it. If the contradiction came simultaneously, or before formal acceptance, the competing claims may neutralize one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla vs.\u160 ?R. Nechemiah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two underlying theoretical models govern how this weighting works. Ulla understands the rabbinic enactment as operating within the framework of actual {\i edut} \u8212- testimony proper \u8212- in which the single witness testifying to death is treated as if he or she were two witnesses, thereby achieving the weight of full legal testimony. Rabbi Nechemiah, by contrast, frames it not as testimony at all, but as a form of evidence, and accordingly the weight assigned on each side is assessed numerically. The {\i gemara} works to reconcile these two models throughout the preceding discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Co-wives Contradicti (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Co-wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the bottom of 117b introduces the following case: {\i achat omeret met ve-achat omeret lo met} \u8212- one co-wife says the husband died, and the other says he did not die. The ruling is that the co-wife who says he died may remarry and collect her {\i ketubah}, while the co-wife who says he did not die may neither remarry nor collect her {\i ketubah}. The scenario is vivid: they were traveling together, one returns saying he fell and died, and the other says the whole thing never happened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-wife Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two distinct reasons why the co-wife who says \u8220"he did not die\u8221" cannot remarry. The first is obvious: she herself has declared that her husband is still alive, and one cannot remarry a living husband. The second reason \u8212- which the {\i gemara} will elaborate \u8212- is that even had she remained silent, a woman is not believed to testify on behalf of her co-wife ({\i tzaratah}). The concern is that the natural rivalry and animosity between co-wives, who share a husband, creates a presumption of bias sufficient to disqualify the co-wife\u8217's testimony entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a question, however. The earlier {\i mishna} suggested that when testimony is contradicted before it has been accepted in {\i beit din}, the contradicting testimony neutralizes it. If so, why doesn\u8217't the co-wife\u8217's denial neutralize the first wife\u8217's claim that he died \u8212- and thereby prevent the first wife from remarrying? The {\i gemara} will address this below: the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishna} is precisely that we completely dismiss the co-wife\u8217's denial. Since she cannot testify for her co-wife to say that the husband died, she equally cannot testify for her co-wife to say that he did not die. Her statement is simply disregarded in its entirety.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Died vs.\u160 ?Killed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Died vs.\u160 ?Murdered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues with a related but distinct case: {\i achat omeret met ve-achat omeret neherag} \u8212- one co-wife says the husband died of natural causes, and the other says he was murdered. Both agree he is dead; they differ only on the manner of death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir rules: {\i ho\u8217'il u-makchishot zo et zo harei elu lo yinase\u8217'u} \u8212- since they contradict one another, neither may remarry. This is a striking position. In the prior case, one wife said \u8220"he died\u8221" and the other said \u8220"he did not die\u8221" \u8212- and there, the second wife\u8217's claim was simply dismissed because a co-wife cannot testify against her co-wife. But here, Rabbi Meir treats the contradiction differently. Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains the distinction carefully. When one wife says \u8220"he did not die,\u8221" that claim carries no independent weight \u8212- the rabbinic institution gave heightened credibility specifically to testimony that a husband {\i died}, not to testimony that he did not die. Therefore, the co-wife\u8217's denial cannot neutralize the first wife\u8217's affirmation. But when one wife says \u8220"he died\u8221" and the other says \u8220"he was murdered,\u8221" both statements are in the affirmative about death \u8212- each co-wife is testifying about something she would herself be believed about under ordinary circumstances, namely, the death of her own husband. Each woman\u8217's testimony has a degree of independent weight, and precisely because they have weight, the fact that they describe the death in mutually incompatible ways creates a genuine contradiction sufficient to neutralize one another. That is Rabbi Meir\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda & Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon disagree. In their view, both wives agree on the central fact \u8212- the husband is dead \u8212- and therefore both may remarry. The discrepancy in the details does not rise to the level of mutual neutralization. If you split the decision, as in the first case, you accept one and reject the other. Here, because both are affirming death, you either accept both or reject both. Since there is no reason to reject them outright, both may remarry.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Co-wives Contradicti (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimshon Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now probes the reasoning behind the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling in the first case \u8212- that the co-wife cannot remarry. The {\i gemara} opens with a {\i diyuk}: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'amrah lo meit} \u8212- the reason the co-wife cannot marry is specifically because she said \u8220"he did not die.\u8221" But this implies that had she remained silent, she might have been able to marry on the strength of the first wife\u8217's testimony. The {\i gemara} challenges this immediately: {\i v\u8217'ha ein tzarah me\u8217'idah l\u8217'chavertah} \u8212- a co-wife cannot testify for her co-wife! How could the co-wife have benefited from the first wife\u8217's testimony even in silence?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers with a surprising twist. One might have thought that when the co-wife says \u8220"he did not die,\u8221" she should actually be {\i more} believed than if she remained silent \u8212- and should even be permitted to remarry. The logic would run as follows: the husband is in fact dead, the second wife knows this too, and the only reason she says \u8220"he did not die\u8221" is to spite her co-wife and prevent her from remarrying. She is, in other words, willing to hurt herself \u8212- sacrificing her own ability to remarry \u8212- in order to damage the other wife. The {\i gemara} gives this attitude a memorable name: {\i tamus nafshi im Plishtim} \u8212- \u8220"let me die with the Philistines,\u8221" the dying words of Shimshon. Just as Shimshon pulled down the hall to kill himself along with his enemies, so too the co-wife might say: I will forgo remarriage if it means my rival cannot remarry either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, the {\i gemara} reasons, perhaps we should interpret the co-wife\u8217's denial not as genuine testimony that he is alive, but as an expression of spite \u8212- which would reinforce our assumption that he is dead and might even allow the denying wife to remarry. The {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i lo meis itztrichah lah} \u8212- the {\i mishna} needed to tell us that even in this case we do not reason this way. We cannot be certain the denying wife is lying to spite her rival; she might also be lying to implicate the first wife in a false claim. In any event, we do not go ahead and allow either wife to benefit from this chain of speculation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Wife Marries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The genuine {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishna}, as the {\i gemara} makes clear, operates in the other direction. If a stranger \u8212- a non-co-wife \u8212- had contradicted the first wife\u8217's testimony that the husband died, and this contradiction came before the testimony was accepted in {\i beit din}, the two claims would neutralize and the first wife could not remarry. But here, the {\i chiddush} is that we completely dismiss the co-wife\u8217's denial \u8212- not only may the co-wife not remarry based on the first wife\u8217's testimony, but the co-wife\u8217's counter-testimony does not prevent the first wife from remarrying either. The co-wife\u8217's word is disregarded in its entirety. Just as she is not believed when she says \u8220"he died\u8221" (to benefit her rival), she is equally not believed when she says \u8220"he did not die\u8221" (to harm her rival). Either way, the testimony of the co-wife, directed toward the status of her rival, is simply voided.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Died vs.\u160 ?Killed (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elazar vs.\u160 ?Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines Rabbi Meir\u8217's position \u8212- that in the case of \u8220"died\u8221" versus \u8220"killed,\u8221" the testimonies neutralize one another \u8212- and asks: why does Rabbi Meir not also say the same in the first case, where one says \u8220"died\u8221" and the other says \u8220"did not die\u8221"? The implication is that if Rabbi Meir rules stringently in the \u8220"died/killed\u8221" case, he should rule stringently in the more obvious \u8220"died/didn\u8217't die\u8221" case as well. {\i Rav Elazar} responds: {\i bemachloket shenuya} \u8212- the first part of the {\i mishna}, which allows the first wife to remarry even over the co-wife\u8217's objection, is actually the position of Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon, not Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Meir would in fact also prohibit the first wife from remarrying in that case. So Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is internally consistent: in both the \u8220"died/didn\u8217't die\u8221" case and the \u8220"died/killed\u8221" case, the testimonies neutralize one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan reads the {\i mishna} differently: {\i afilu teima Rabbi Meir} \u8212- even according to Rabbi Meir, there is a meaningful distinction between \u8220"died/didn\u8217't die\u8221" and \u8220"died/killed.\u8221" In the former case, even Rabbi Meir would agree that the testimonies do not neutralize, because the claim \u8220"he did not die\u8221" in the context of testimony about a husband\u8217's death is treated as legally worthless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-wife Claim Void\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's principle requires careful qualification. It is not that any claim of \u8220"he did not die\u8221" is always ignored \u8212- the end of the {\i mishna} states plainly that when one witness says \u8220"he died\u8221" and another says \u8220"he did not die,\u8221" they do neutralize one another. So it cannot be a categorical rule that denials of death are void. The precise rule is this: a {\i co-wife\u8217's} claim that the husband did not die is voided, because just as a co-wife is not believed to say \u8220"he died\u8221" for the benefit of her rival, she is equally not believed to say \u8220"he did not die\u8221" to the detriment of her rival. The reasoning is the same in both directions \u8212- we do not trust any testimony the co-wife offers about her rival\u8217's husband\u8217's death or survival.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when each wife says \u8220"he died,\u8221" but one says \u8220"naturally\u8221" and the other says \u8220"by murder,\u8221" each woman\u8217's testimony is accepted {\i for herself}. It is at that point \u8212- once each woman\u8217's individual testimony has been admitted for her own case \u8212- that the contradiction between the two accounts, both of which carry independent weight, creates the neutralization that Rabbi Meir invokes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to reconcile this analysis with the end of the {\i mishna}, which, when it says one woman says \u8220"died\u8221" and another says \u8220"did not die,\u8221" could appear to include co-wives. According to Rabbi Elazar, that case in the {\i mishna} indeed reflects Rabbi Meir\u8217's position that co-wives can neutralize one another. According to Rabbi Yochanan, this case does not involve co-wives at all \u8212- and the difficulty for Rabbi Yochanan ({\i kashya}) is acknowledged. The overall upshot is clear: the co-wife\u8217's testimony, in any direction, is completely disregarded with respect to her rival.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Returns Testifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Classic Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} presents the archetypal case of overseas testimony: {\i ha-isha she-halcha hi u-va\u8217'alah limdinat ha-yam} \u8212- a woman who went with her husband overseas, and returned to testify that he died. The ruling is {\i tinasei ve-titol ketuvatah} \u8212- she may remarry and collect her {\i ketubah}. This is the foundational case of the entire rabbinic innovation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Tarfon & Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then adds: {\i ve-tzaratah asurah} \u8212- the co-wife is forbidden to remarry, since a woman\u8217's testimony about the death of a husband is not believed on behalf of her co-wife. The {\i mishna} then asks a further question about the co-wife\u8217's status: if the co-wife had previously been married to a {\i kohen}, may she continue to eat {\i terumah}? Rabbi Tarfon rules that she may \u8212- since we completely reject the co-wife\u8217's testimony, we do not treat it even as a {\i safek}, a doubt, and therefore the co-wife retains her presumed status as the wife of a living husband, allowing her to continue eating {\i terumah}. Rabbi Akiva rules more stringently: {\i ein zo derech motzi mi-yedei aveirah} \u8212- this approach does not protect her from sin. Since the testimony creates at least enough uncertainty to prevent her from remarrying, it creates enough uncertainty about eating {\i terumah} as well, and she must abstain from both. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is a {\i machmir} one precisely because treating the matter as a {\i safek} obligates caution on all sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father-in-law Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case follows: {\i amra meit ba\u8217'ali ve-achar kach meit chami} \u8212- a woman who says \u8220"my husband died and then my father-in-law died.\u8221" Ordinarily, a woman is not believed to testify on behalf of her {\i chamotah} (mother-in-law) or father-in-law, because we suspect the lingering animus of the in-law relationship. However, the {\i gemara} notes that once her husband has died, her father-in-law is no longer halachically her father-in-law \u8212- the relationship has dissolved. And a woman is believed to testify on behalf of an {\i ex}-father-in-law. So she is claiming: accept my testimony about my husband\u8217's death first, and then \u8212- since he is now my former father-in-law \u8212- accept my testimony about his death as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} rules: {\i tinasei ve-titol ketuvatah}, she may remarry and collect her {\i ketubah}, but {\i chamotah asurah} \u8212- the mother-in-law\u8217's status is not changed by this testimony. Why not? Because since the entire chain of reasoning rests on the woman\u8217's own testimony, we are concerned that she has constructed the whole account to harm her mother-in-law. Her motivation to do so is real: if the mother-in-law is married to a {\i kohen} and we accept the testimony that her husband died, she can no longer eat {\i terumah}. So even if the woman is lying about her husband\u8217's death \u8212- which the {\i mishna} says she is believed about \u8212- she still achieves something negative against her mother-in-law. That concern alone is sufficient to reject the mother-in-law\u8217's portion of the testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling for the mother-in-law therefore mirrors that of the co-wife: Rabbi Tarfon says the testimony is completely rejected and the mother-in-law may continue eating {\i terumah}, while Rabbi Akiva says it is not enough to protect her from sin and she must treat the matter as a {\i safek}, refraining from both remarriage and {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question worth raising is why the co-wife should suffer this liminal status when the testimony might be entirely true and she has done nothing wrong. This is a genuine difficulty, and it underscores the tension the rabbis created when they expanded the category of believed testimony: they recognized the leniency could be abused and therefore imposed limits, but those limits sometimes fall on an innocent party caught in the wrong formal relationship. Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's approach \u8212- completely rejecting the testimony and restoring the co-wife\u8217's unambiguous prior status \u8212- has real appeal precisely because it avoids leaving an innocent person in an undefined halachic limbo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Cases Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} establishes that both cases \u8212- the co-wife and the mother-in-law \u8212- are necessary, and neither can be derived from the other. Had the {\i mishna} taught only the co-wife case, one might have thought Rabbi Tarfon completely rejects the testimony because {\i tzarat ha-gufa} \u8212- the co-wife\u8217's animosity is intensely personal, rooted in the intimate fact that the husband sleeps with her rival rather than with her. One might then think Rabbi Tarfon would concede to Rabbi Akiva in the case of the mother-in-law, where the animosity is of a more ordinary social variety \u8212- perhaps she badmouths her daughter-in-law to her son, a complaint common enough but far less visceral. Conversely, had the {\i mishna} taught only the mother-in-law case, one might think Rabbi Akiva concedes in the co-wife case, where the bias is so extreme that even he might agree we completely reject the testimony. By teaching both cases, the {\i mishna} makes clear that neither tanna modifies his position based on the type of animosity involved \u8212- each maintains his view across both scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then reports: {\i amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel, halacha ke-Rabbi Tarfon} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Tarfon. The co-wife is not left in a state of ambiguity. The testimony is fully rejected, and she may go on living her life as before, without the paralysis of a {\i safek} she never created.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One of Five Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncertainty Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now takes up what the {\i gemara} presents as a digression occasioned by the debate between Rabbi Tarfon and Rabbi Akiva. The case is: {\i kidesh achat mi-chamesh nashim ve-eino yodea eizo kidesh} \u8212- a man performed {\i kiddushin} with one of five women but does not know which one. Each woman claims he married her. This is not an impossible scenario given the realities of the time, when {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin} could be separated by years and the formal act of {\i kiddushin} might be the transfer of money to a father on behalf of a daughter, leaving uncertainty about which daughter was intended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states that he must give a {\i get} to each one \u8212- since he cannot be certain which woman is his wife, he cannot simply live with any one of them or leave them all bound without resolution. As for the {\i ketubah}: {\i maniach ketubah beineihen u-mistalek} \u8212- he deposits the {\i ketubah} money among them and withdraws. He knows he is obligated to one of them but does not know which one, so he leaves the money for them to resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamotzi Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying legal question is how to apply the principle of {\i hamotzi me-chavero alav ha-re\u8217'ayah} \u8212- the burden of proof lies on the one seeking to extract from another. Each woman presents herself to him with a definite claim: you owe me a {\i ketubah}. He responds that he is uncertain. This is not precisely the case where a lender says \u8220"I lent you money\u8221" and the borrower denies it \u8212- there the certainty and uncertainty are on opposite sides. Nor is it quite the case where one says \u8220"I lent you money and I am not sure if you paid me back\u8221" \u8212- there the debtor concedes the debt. Here it is an intermediate case: he concedes the existence of an obligation to one of them, but not necessarily to the particular woman standing before him. This framing of the {\i hamotzi} analysis shapes the subsequent debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Tarfon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon says: he places the {\i ketubah} money among them and withdraws. He has discharged his definite obligation \u8212- to one of them \u8212- by depositing the full amount. They must resolve the distribution among themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva rules: {\i ein zo derech motzi mi-yedei aveirah} \u8212- the same phrase we encountered above \u8212- this does not protect him from sin. He must give a {\i get} and a {\i ketubah} to each woman individually. The question is whether Rabbi Akiva is speaking in strictly legal terms \u8212- that a court would compel him to pay each one \u8212- or whether he is articulating a moral obligation that, while not technically enforceable through {\i dinei mamonot}, nonetheless constitutes a real spiritual and ethical debt. The parallel the {\i gemara} raises elsewhere is illuminating: one who places poison before another\u8217's animal is exempt {\i be-dinei adam} but liable {\i be-dinei shamayim} \u8212- exempt in human courts but obligated before the heavenly court. The language {\i ein zo derech motzi mi-yedei aveirah} suggests Rabbi Akiva may be operating in that register: you have a specific, concrete moral obligation that a {\i beit din} will seek to enforce, even if strict {\i dinei mamonot} would not technically mandate it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Theft from Five\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theft Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case follows immediately: {\i gezel echad mi-chamishah ve-eini yodea me-eizeh gazal} \u8212- a man stole from one of five people and does not know which one. Each person claims he stole from them. Rabbi Tarfon rules that he deposits the stolen object among them and withdraws; Rabbi Akiva rules he must make full restitution to each one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heightened Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious moral distinction between the two cases is that in the {\i kiddushin} scenario, the man did nothing inherently wrong \u8212- he entered into a marriage, lost track of which woman it was, and now must extract himself. In the theft scenario, he committed a transgression. One would therefore expect the obligation to make amends to be greater in the theft case. The {\i mishna} seems to present Rabbi Akiva and Rabbi Tarfon as disagreeing in both scenarios equally \u8212- and it raises the question of whether the same analysis governs a morally neutral situation and a situation involving actual wrongdoing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon b. Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i braita} in which Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar clarifies the scope of the debate: {\i lo necheleku Rabbi Tarfon ve-Rabbi Akiva al she-kidesh achat mi-chamesh nashim ve-eino yodea eizo kidesh she-maniach ketubah beineihen u-mistalek} \u8212- Rabbi Tarfon and Rabbi Akiva did not disagree at all in the case of simple {\i kiddushin} without a more heightened involvement. Both agree that he deposits the {\i ketubah} and withdraws. Similarly, they did not disagree in the case where someone purchased an item from one of five merchants and forgets which one \u8212- there, too, both agree he deposits the payment and withdraws. The debate, says Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar, applies only in cases of heightened obligation: {\i kiddushin} accomplished through an act of intercourse ({\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah}), and theft.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a problem for how to read our {\i mishna}, which appears to present the debate in the cases of simple {\i kiddushin} (without {\i bi\u8217'ah}) and theft \u8212- one from each column. The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i le-olam Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} \u8212- our {\i mishna} is in fact consistent with Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's view. When our {\i mishna} says {\i kidesh}, it means {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} effected through intercourse, which carries a special moral weight because, regardless of whether {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} was halachically proper (and the rabbis disapproved of it, though it is valid), having been intimate with a woman and then walking away without ensuring she receives her {\i ketubah} creates a particular obligation. The {\i mishna} thus presents the two cases of heightened obligation \u8212- {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} and theft \u8212- as the arena of the dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within those cases, moreover, the {\i gemara} identifies a further gradation. {\i Kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} involves only a rabbinic prohibition ({\i issur d\u8217'Rabbanan}), whereas theft is an {\i issur d\u8217'Oraita}. The {\i mishna} therefore teaches Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling in the lighter case \u8212- {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- to demonstrate the strength of his position: even where the transgression was only rabbinic in character, he imposes the full obligation to pay each woman. Conversely, the {\i mishna} teaches Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's ruling in the heavier case \u8212- theft \u8212- to demonstrate the strength of his position: even where there was a genuine biblical transgression, he does not require full individual restitution to each claimant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's closing word on Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is {\i kanis} \u8212- a fine. This framing clarifies what Rabbi Akiva means: not that strict {\i dinei mamonot} technically obligates him to pay each woman, but that we impose a {\i knas} to ensure he fully discharges his responsibility to the one he actually wronged, since we cannot know which one that is.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Son & Yibum Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Then Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The last {\i mishna} of the {\i perek} presents a series of cases involving a woman\u8217's testimony about events overseas that affect her {\i yibum} status. The {\i mishna} opens with the case we touched on earlier: {\i ha-isha she-halcha hi u-va\u8217'alah limdinat ha-yam u-vnah imahem} \u8212- a woman who departed with her husband and her son. Since she left with a son, she would not be obligated in {\i yibum} if her husband died. She returns and testifies: my husband died, and then my son died. In this configuration, she is believed \u8212- the only thing she is specially credited to testify about is her husband\u8217's death, and the two statements about the son cancel each other out. We assumed she would not be obligated in {\i yibum} when she left, and nothing she says about her son changes that. She may act accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son First Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now consider a variation: she left with no son \u8212- so we assumed she {\i would} be obligated in {\i yibum}. She returns and says: I had a son born to me overseas, and my husband died, and then my son died. She is neutralizing her own claim: yes I had a son, but he died after my husband, so I am still not obligated in {\i yibum}. Since she is not trying to change her status in an advantageous direction \u8212- she is not claiming {\i yibum} rights or {\i yibum} exemption beyond what we already assumed \u8212- and her credibility extends to the statement about her husband\u8217's death, she is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if she says instead: my husband died, and then my son died \u8212- and she had left without a son \u8212- she is now claiming she is {\i exempt} from {\i yibum} based on a son whose existence and death she alone asserts. She is not believed to change her status in this way, because her special credibility pertains to her husband\u8217's death, not to the existence or death of a son. Nevertheless, {\i chosheshin li-dvareha} \u8212- we take her words into account enough to require caution. Since she claims exemption from {\i yibum}, we cannot permit {\i yibum} to proceed. The ruling: {\i choletzet ve-lo mityabemet} \u8212- she performs {\i chalitzah} but does not perform {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother-in-law Overseas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then turns to cases involving a brother-in-law. If she left without any brother-in-law and says a brother-in-law was born to her in-laws overseas, and she then reports his death in either order relative to her husband, her statement neutralizes itself: I had a brother-in-law who is now dead. She is believed, since she is not claiming any change of status \u8212- she ends up in the same position she would be in had there never been a brother-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overseas Events\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more difficult case: she departed together with her husband and a known brother-in-law \u8212- {\i halchah hi u-va\u8217'alah ve-yavamah limdinat ha-yam} \u8212- meaning we know there is a {\i yibum} obligation should her husband die. She returns and testifies that both her husband and her brother-in-law have died \u8212- in either order. Since either order leads to the same result \u8212- no brother-in-law, no {\i yibum} \u8212- one might expect she is believed. But the {\i mishna} rules: {\i einah ne\u8217'emenet} \u8212- she is not believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope Limited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is that she is testifying about her brother-in-law\u8217's death, which falls outside the scope of the special rabbinic credibility accorded to a wife. That credibility extends only to testimony about her own husband\u8217's death. The {\i mishna} articulates the principle broadly: {\i she-ein ishah ne\u8217'emenet lomar meit yavmi she-tinasei} \u8212- a woman is not believed to say \u8220"my brother-in-law died\u8221" so that she may remarry freely. Similarly, {\i ve-lo meitah achotah she-tikaneis le-veiso} \u8212- a woman is not believed to say \u8220"my sister died\u8221" so that she may marry her sister\u8217's husband. And {\i ve-ein ha-ish ne\u8217'eman lomar meit achi she-yiyabem et ishto} \u8212- a man is not believed to say \u8220"my brother died\u8221" in order to perform {\i yibum} with the brother\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last case is worth examining carefully. If a man returns and says his brother died, that statement is actually a form of testimony that a husband died \u8212- and one might think the sister-in-law could rely on it to remarry if there are children, in which case there is no {\i yibum} obligation. And indeed, in that context she might be able to. But she cannot rely on it to permit {\i yibum}, because his testimony in that case would {\i matir} an {\i issur} \u8212- it would be removing the prohibition of {\i eshet ach}, a brother\u8217's wife, which is otherwise biblically forbidden. The rabbinic credibility granted to single witnesses and wives applies only to permitting a woman to remarry as a widow, not to enabling an otherwise prohibited relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence for the woman in the brother-in-law case is severe: she testified that the brother-in-law died, she is not believed, and yet there is no brother-in-law present to perform either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. She is effectively an {\i agunah} \u8212- bound by a {\i yibum} obligation to a man whose whereabouts cannot be confirmed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get to Absent Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get via Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a question posed by Rava to Rav Nachman: {\i ha-mezakeh get le-ishto bimkom yavam mahu?} \u8212- if a husband wishes to give a {\i get} to his wife in a situation where there is a {\i yavam} (brother-in-law who would be obligated in {\i yibum}), but the wife is not present, can the husband appoint a third party to receive the {\i get} on the wife\u8217's behalf?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario is one in which the husband wants to preempt the {\i yibum} obligation \u8212- perhaps he is terminally ill or departing for war and fears he will die, leaving his wife subject to {\i yibum} with a brother-in-law she does not want. He would like to give a {\i get} now so that upon his death the {\i yibum} obligation never arises. But the wife is not present to receive the {\i get} directly and has not appointed an agent to receive it. Can the husband effectively appoint a representative on his wife\u8217's behalf?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezakeh Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative principle is {\i mezakeh} \u8212- one may acquire something on behalf of another person without their presence or knowledge, provided it is to that person\u8217's benefit ({\i zechus}). A husband can give his wife a {\i get} against her will, since once instituted, Cherem de-Rabbenu Gershom notwithstanding, {\i halacha} recognizes a unilateral {\i get}. But for the {\i get} to take effect, it must be received by the wife or her validly appointed agent. The question is whether the husband can himself appoint someone to represent the wife \u8212- treating this as an act of {\i mezakeh}, done on her behalf without her knowledge \u8212- given that a {\i get} would seemingly benefit her by freeing her from the {\i yibum} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benefit vs.\u160 ?Harm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames the question as a genuine uncertainty. On one hand, {\i keivan d\u8217'sanya lah} \u8212- since we can assume she hates the brother-in-law, being freed from {\i yibum} is clearly to her benefit, and {\i zakin le-adam she-lo be-fanav} \u8212- one may acquire benefit for another without their presence. On the other hand, {\i dilma zimnin de-rachma lei} \u8212- perhaps there are times when a woman actually likes or loves her brother-in-law and would prefer {\i yibum} to remaining a widow. In that case, being divorced precludes the {\i yibum} she would have wanted, which makes the {\i get} a {\i chov} \u8212- a harm rather than a benefit. And {\i ein chavin le-adam she-lo be-fanav} \u8212- one cannot impose a harm on another person without their presence and consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's framing is notable: it does not assume we can simply inquire about each woman\u8217's individual feelings. It is asking whether a general presumption can be established \u8212- can we rule categorically that a {\i get} in a {\i yibum} situation is always, or almost always, to the wife\u8217's benefit?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fighting Couple\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is pressed further by Ravina, who asks Rava: {\i ha-mezakeh get le-ishah bimkom ketatah mahu?} \u8212- what if the husband and wife are in the midst of a serious conflict? They are fighting, tensions are high, perhaps legal proceedings have begun. Surely, one might argue, in that case the {\i get} is clearly to her benefit \u8212- she obviously wants out of the marriage. Or perhaps not: {\i nicha de-gufa adif lah} \u8212- even when a woman is fighting bitterly with her husband, the physical and emotional security of being married may still matter more to her than being free of him. Even in the midst of a quarrel, a woman may not want to be divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?Widow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} invokes a famous statement of Reish Lakish: {\i tav lemetav tan du milemetav armalu} \u8212- better to sit as two than to sit as a widow. This is presented as a sociological assessment: women, as a general matter, prefer the state of being married \u8212- even to an imperfect husband \u8212- over the state of widowhood or singlehood. The statement carries enormous halachic weight and would later become the subject of a famous dispute between Rabbi Rackman and Rav Soloveitchik about whether changing social realities could alter this assumption. But in the {\i gemara} itself, it is offered as a presumption about women\u8217's preferences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Settle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several {\i amoraim} elaborate on this theme with increasingly pointed formulations. Abaye states: {\i deshumshmana gavra} \u8212- my husband could be as small as an ant, but as long as {\i kursiyei be-charta rami} \u8212- my seat is set among people of standing \u8212- I am content. A woman will accept a negligible husband if it means having the social standing of being married. Rav Pappa states: {\i denafsa gavra} \u8212- my husband could be a flax comber, but {\i tikrei be-seifei bava ve-teitiv} \u8212- as long as I am called to the door and given a place to sit, the dignity of being married suffices. Rav Ashi states: {\i dekulsa gavra} \u8212- my husband could be a cabbage head, but {\i lo ba\u8217'ei talfei le-kedira} \u8212- as long as I do not need to worry about putting food on the table myself, I will manage. The repeated motif is that women are willing to settle for husbands of very modest character and capacity rather than remain unmarried.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adultery Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, does not end on that note. A {\i braita} is cited that dramatically reverses the valence of all that has been said: {\i ve-kullan mezanot ve-tolot be-ba\u8217'aleihen} \u8212- all of them commit adultery and attribute the resulting pregnancies to their husbands. What began as a description of women\u8217's willingness to endure and settle within marriage ends as an indictment: the very willingness to remain with inadequate husbands generates unfaithfulness, because the unhappiness they suppress does not simply disappear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion \u8212- that we cannot give a {\i get} to a wife without her presence even when she is fighting with her husband, because we cannot assume she would want it \u8212- flows from the assumption encoded in {\i tav lemetav tan du milemetav armalu}. And this has real and troubling contemporary consequences. The presumption that women prefer marriage to its absence, that they will endure rather than leave, has been invoked by {\i batei dinim} \u8212- particularly those identified as deeply committed to {\i shalom bayit} \u8212- to resist granting a {\i get} even in situations involving documented physical, emotional, or sexual abuse. \u8220"Try to work it out\u8221" becomes a halachic default rooted in this Talmudic assessment of women\u8217's preferences. What began as a sociological generalization in the {\i gemara} functions in practice as a structurally powerful obstacle for women seeking to leave dangerous or degrading marriages. Reading this {\i gemara} in full \u8212- ending not with the neat statement of Reish Lakish but with the {\i braita}\u8217's unsettling reversal \u8212- should at minimum prompt honest reflection on how these assumptions shape rabbinic decision-making today.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 120\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Sisters-in-Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a brief review of the mishna on 119b. The case involves two women each married to one of two brothers. Each woman comes and reports that her own husband has died, and each is believed \u8212- a woman\u8217's testimony about her husband\u8217's death is accepted in order to permit her to remarry. The complication, however, is that each woman also has a {\i yavam}: her deceased husband\u8217's brother. And the testimony of one co-wife about her sister-in-law\u8217's husband carries no legal weight for purposes of releasing the sister-in-law from the obligation of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To map out the case concretely: Reuven and Shimon are brothers. Rachel is married to Reuven and Sarah is married to Shimon. Rachel testifies that Reuven is dead \u8212- she is believed, and in principle could remarry, but she cannot, because Shimon is still alive and she requires either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} from him. Sarah testifies that Shimon is dead \u8212- again, believed for her own purposes, but she cannot act on it because Reuven may still be alive, and his status has only been established by Rachel\u8217's testimony, which does not function as admissible testimony vis-\u224 ?-vis releasing Sarah from her {\i yibum} obligation. The mishna concludes that both women are stuck.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reb Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer introduces a leniency. If these women went ahead and performed {\i yibum} with the surviving brothers \u8212- that is, if Rachel performed {\i yibum} with Levi (Reuven\u8217's brother) and Sarah performed {\i yibum} with Yehuda (Shimon\u8217's brother) \u8212- and those {\i yevamim} subsequently died, then Rabbi Eliezer holds that both women are free to remarry anyone they wish. They need not worry that one of the original husbands may still be alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acting on Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final line of the mishna articulates Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's rationale: {\i ho\u8217'il vehutru leyevamim hutru lechol adam} \u8212- since they have been permitted to the {\i yevamim}, they are permitted to all men. The key move is that by allowing Rachel to perform {\i yibum} with Levi, the legal system has not merely accepted Rachel\u8217's testimony in the abstract \u8212- it has acted upon it. The death of Reuven has been established as a legal fact. Correspondingly, Sarah\u8217's {\i yibum} with Yehuda establishes the death of Shimon as a legal fact. Once both deaths have been legally acted upon in this way, each woman can rely on the other\u8217's established fact, and both are free to remarry after the {\i yevamim} themselves die.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Inquiry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with Rava\u8217's inquiry: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama deRabbi Eliezer?} \u8212- what is the underlying rationale of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling? Two possible explanations are offered. The first: {\i mishum dekasavar tzara me\u8217'ida lachavirtah} \u8212- perhaps Rabbi Eliezer holds that in general, a co-wife may testify on behalf of her competing co-wife. This would be a sweeping position, and both Rashi and Tosafot reject it as inconsistent with the language of the mishna. The mishna uses the formulation {\i hutru leyevamim}, not the language of testimony being accepted. Moreover, the entire prior discussion has established that co-wives, mothers-in-law, and daughters-in-law are precisely the category of women whose testimony is {\i not} accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more plausible explanation, suggested by Tosafot explicitly and Rashi implicitly, is that Rachel is fundamentally testifying about herself \u8212- about her own husband\u8217's death, for her own purposes of remarriage. The testimony is, at its core, testimony {\i for herself}. Rabbi Eliezer may be saying that once testimony is accepted as fundamentally self-directed, it carries weight even for the competing co-wife, even though that co-wife would normally fall outside the scope of acceptable testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a second layer of Rava\u8217's question. Even granting that Rabbi Eliezer extends the effect of self-directed testimony to the co-wife, {\i when} does that extension take effect? Two possibilities emerge: either the testimony alone suffices \u8212- the very act of testifying for oneself automatically confers permissibility on the co-wife ({\i tzara me\u8217'ida lachavirtah}) \u8212- or the extension only operates once the testifying woman has actually {\i acted} on her own testimony. Under the second reading, the operative factor is not the testimony per se but the willingness to personally stake one\u8217's own status on the truth of the claim: {\i mishum dehi lo mekalkela nafsha} \u8212- because she would not ruin herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-Wife Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference between these two interpretations is precisely the question of {\i le\u8217'insuvei letzara mikamei didah} \u8212- whether the co-wife may remarry before the testifying woman has herself acted. In a simpler case involving two co-wives whose joint husband went overseas: Sarah says the husband died. That testimony is accepted for Sarah\u8217's own purposes of remarriage. May Rachel immediately rely on that testimony to remarry as well, even before Sarah has herself taken any action?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's principle is that self-directed testimony automatically extends to the co-wife, then Rachel can act immediately \u8212- {\i af agav dela insava mansavinan lah letzara}, even before Sarah herself has married, we permit the co-wife to marry. But if the operative principle requires action \u8212- that Sarah must demonstrate her own genuine conviction by actually remarrying \u8212- then {\i ei lo insava lo mansavinan lah}: until Sarah acts, Rachel cannot rely on it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Textual Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to resolve Rava\u8217's question from the language of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling in the mishna. The phrase is {\i ho\u8217'il vehutru leyevamim hutru lechol adam} \u8212- \u8220"since they have been {\i permitted} to the {\i yevamim}, they are permitted to all men.\u8221" The operative word is {\i hutru}, \u8220"permitted,\u8221" not \u8220"married.\u8221" The mishna does not say \u8220"since they {\i married} the {\i yevamim}.\u8221" This linguistic observation becomes the pivot of the Gemara\u8217's analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Automatic vs.\u160 ?Conditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara argues: if Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's rationale requires action \u8212- that is, he only extends the testimony\u8217's effect once the woman has {\i married} her {\i yavam} \u8212- then the language {\i vehutru leyevamim} makes perfect sense, because {\i hainu dechi insav manzveina lah} \u8212- only because they married the {\i yevamim} does the permission flow to the co-wife. But if Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's principle is that the testimony alone automatically works for the co-wife even without any subsequent action, the mishna\u8217's wording becomes puzzling: it should have simply said {\i ho\u8217'il vehutru hutru}, without specifically tying it to the {\i yibum} having occurred. The fact that it anchors the permission in the {\i yibum} itself seems to indicate that action is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, deflects this reading. {\i Rabbi Eliezer lidvreihem ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer is arguing within the framework of the Rabbis\u8217' position, not necessarily expressing his own full view. According to his own reasoning, Rabbi Eliezer may well hold that the co-wife is automatically permitted once testimony is given for oneself, even without any subsequent action. But addressing the Rabbis on their own terms, he argues: at least concede that once she has performed {\i yibum} and thereby staked her own status on the truth of her testimony \u8212- {\i lo mekalkela nafsha} \u8212- surely you must agree the co-wife should be permitted. To this the Rabbis respond with the principle of {\i tamus nafshi im pelishtim} \u8212- she is willing to bring the stadium down on her own head if she can take down her co-wife in the process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then introduced: a woman and her husband went overseas. She returned and declared that her husband had died. She may remarry and collect her {\i ketuva}. Her co-wife, however, is forbidden. This is the classic formulation \u8212- believed for herself, not for the co-wife. Rabbi Eliezer says: {\i ho\u8217'il vehutra hi hutra nami tzarata} \u8212- since she has been permitted, her co-wife is also permitted. Crucially, the baraita uses the language of {\i hutra} \u8212- \u8220"she has been permitted\u8221" \u8212- rather than saying she actually married. This seems to vindicate the reading that Rabbi Eliezer does not require prior action; the mere permissibility itself extends to the co-wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to read the baraita as implying {\i ho\u8217'il vehutra veniseit} \u8212- \u8220"permitted and then married\u8221" \u8212- so that action is still required. But the plain reading clearly supports the automatic interpretation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hidden Divorce Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secret Get Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's principle requires the woman to act on her own testimony before her co-wife can benefit, a serious objection arises: even when she acts, her action may not reliably demonstrate the truth of her testimony. The Gemara raises the concern of {\i veleichush shema begita asai} \u8212- what if she is coming with a {\i get}? That is, what if her husband had secretly sent her a {\i get} that no one else knows about? In that case, she is already divorced and not bound by her own claim that he died. She goes ahead and remarries, ostensibly \u8220"acting on\u8221" her testimony \u8212- but in reality she knows she is safe because of the hidden {\i get}. Her entire purpose is to ruin her co-wife by inducing the co-wife to remarry while the husband is, in fact, still alive. In this scenario, her act of remarriage proves nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this difficulty by limiting the applicability of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's leniency to a specific circumstance: {\i de\u8217'insva l\u8217'Kohen} \u8212- she must have married a {\i kohen}. If there is a hidden {\i get}, then when the husband eventually returns alive, the truth will emerge, and at that point she will also be ruined \u8212- she will be divorced from the {\i kohen} because a {\i kohenet} who was previously divorced or who came through improper circumstances is {\i assura} to remain married to a {\i kohen}. Since she faces this additional exposure, she would not have concocted the {\i get} scheme in the first place. Marrying a \u1497 ?\u1513 ?\u1512 ?\u1488 ?\u1500 ?, by contrast, does not eliminate the hidden {\i get} scenario: her risk there is limited, and the concern remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legal vs.\u160 ?Factual Truth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This exchange opens a broader question about the nature of halachic truth. When a {\i beit din} rules that a woman may remarry, is it establishing a legal fact or merely permitting action under uncertainty? One might think the ruling functions like a judicial determination of fact, binding on subsequent proceedings. Indeed, that might be Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's point \u8212- particularly under the reading that does not require prior action: once testimony is accepted for one party, it is accepted as established fact, and others may rely on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara\u8217's willingness to entertain the hidden {\i get} scenario reveals that {\i beit din}\u8217's acceptance of testimony does not constitute a universal determination of fact. It is a permission granted within a particular legal context, for particular parties, and it does not automatically extend as a factual determination to all related questions. This parallels the concept of {\i palginan dibura} found in tractate Sanhedrin \u8212- the same statement can be believed in one direction and disbelieved in another. Halacha regularly operates within a framework of legal truth that does not always correspond to objective, verifiable fact, in the same way that legal systems employ constructs like {\i rov} and {\i chazaka} not because they guarantee factual accuracy but because they provide workable presumptions for adjudication.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Identifying the Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Face with Nose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf now shifts to a new mishna addressing the {\i content} of valid testimony \u8212- specifically, how one establishes that the body one has seen is indeed the person in question. {\i Ein me\u8217'idin ela al partzuf panim im hachotem} \u8212- one may only testify based on having seen the face together with the nose. {\i Af al pi she\u8217'yesh simanim b\u8217'gufo uvkhelav} \u8212- even if there are identifying marks on the body or on the clothing, that is not sufficient. The standard requires direct facial identification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Day Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An additional limitation: {\i ein me\u8217'idin ela ad gimmel yamim} \u8212- one may only testify within three days of the death. Beyond three days, the decomposition of the body alters the appearance to such a degree that reliable identification is no longer possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context-Dependent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava dissents from the three-day rule. He argues that no uniform rule can be applied: {\i lo kol ha\u8217'adam velo kol hamakom velo kol hasha\u8217'ot shavin} \u8212- not every person is the same, not every place is the same, and not every time is the same. Body decomposition varies by individual constitution, climate, and circumstance, and a rigid three-day cutoff fails to account for this variability.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Facial Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forehead & Nose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} elaborates on the mishna\u8217's requirement: {\i padachat velo partzuf panim, partzuf panim velo padachat \u8212- ein me\u8217'idin} \u8212- neither a forehead alone without a face, nor a face without a forehead, is sufficient for testimony. One requires both together, along with the nose: {\i ad she\u8217'yihyu shneihem im hachotem}. A full, head-on view of the face is necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye \u8212- and some attribute it to Rav Kahana \u8212- derives this from the verse {\i hakarat pneihem antah bam} \u8212- \u8220"the recognition of their face answered against them.\u8221" Rashi reads the verse as establishing the face, rather than any other part of the body, as the locus of reliable identification. Tosafot notes that the word {\i hakarat} \u8212- \u8220"recognition\u8221" \u8212- implies not merely that a face was seen, but that a full identification of the face was achieved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wax Disguise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara illustrates the sensitivity of facial recognition with a story about Abba bar Marta, also known as Abba bar Minyumi, who owed money to the house of the {\i Reish Galuta}. He took wax ({\i kira}), smeared it onto his forehead, and walked past their guards \u8212- {\i velo baskruha} \u8212- and they did not recognize him. The episode demonstrates that even minor alterations to the forehead and nose can defeat reliable identification, reinforcing why the requirement for a complete, unobstructed view of the face is necessary.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Whole Body Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam, at the top of Tosafot on this daf, offers a significant reinterpretation of the mishna\u8217's requirements. He argues: {\i hainu davka keshein sham ela hapartzuf} \u8212- the requirement of face-with-nose applies only when all one has seen is the face. But {\i aval kol gufo shalem} \u8212- if the entire body is intact and has been seen \u8212- {\i afilu ein kan padachat vechotem nikar hu heitev al yedei simanei haguf} \u8212- even without the forehead and nose, the body is clearly identifiable through its physical characteristics.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognition vs.\u160 ?Marks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam draws a further distinction. He suggests that when the mishna says {\i simanim} on the body are insufficient, that ruling applies only to a case where the witness is relying on specific {\i marks} \u8212- pointing to particular features and saying, \u8220"I see a mole here, therefore this is him.\u8221" That is fundamentally different from {\i tevi\u8217'as ayin} \u8212- genuine recognition, where the totality of what one sees produces confident identification. If the witness truly {\i recognizes} the person \u8212- not by pointing to individual marks but through the holistic experience of recognition \u8212- that may be perfectly valid even without the specific facial requirements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extended Timeframes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam extends this reasoning further: {\i va\u8217'afilu achar kama yamim} \u8212- even after many days, if the body is complete and the witness genuinely recognizes it, testimony may be valid. The three-day limit and the facial requirements, on this reading, apply specifically to the case of {\i simanim} \u8212- where one is relying on particular identifying marks rather than direct recognition. The creative thrust of this position is unmistakable: Rabbeinu Tam is deliberately reframing stringent Talmudic requirements as applying only to a narrower category of cases, opening the door to much more flexible standards of identification. Given the practical reality that these standards could leave many women unable to remarry, it is clear what pastoral and halakhic need is driving the reinterpretation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marks: Biblical or R (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simanim Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the mishna\u8217's statement that {\i simanim} on the body are insufficient for testimony. {\i Lemeimra desimanim lav de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- does this establish that identifying marks have no biblical validity? If so, that would be a significant and far-reaching ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges this inference from a ruling in {\i Gittin}. If a {\i get} was found tied to a pouch, wallet, or signet ring ({\i matza kashur bekis uve\u8217'arnaki uvetaba\u8217'as}), or found among the owner\u8217's possessions, it may be returned to its owner and used to divorce his wife \u8212- even after a considerable time has passed. The {\i get} is valid even for the weighty matter of {\i eishes ish}. The identification of the {\i get} as belonging to a particular person rests entirely on the {\i simanim} \u8212- the associated objects. This seems to demonstrate that {\i simanim} do have biblical validity, since the stakes here are clearly {\i de\u8217'oraita} in nature.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Everyone Accepts Simanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava proposes a resolution: {\i dechuli alma simanim d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- everyone agrees that identifying marks have biblical validity. The debate elsewhere \u8212- specifically the {\i baraita} involving testimony based on a {\i shuma} (mole) \u8212- is not about whether {\i simanim} work in principle, but about a more specific question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marks: Biblical or R (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mole Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} records a debate: {\i ein me\u8217'idin al hashuma} \u8212- one may not testify to a man\u8217's death based on a mole. Rabbi Elazar ben Mahavai disagrees: {\i me\u8217'idin} \u8212- one may. Abaye initially suggests this debate reflects a disagreement about whether {\i simanim} are {\i de\u8217'oraita} or {\i derabbanan}. But Rava\u8217's resolution \u8212- that everyone agrees {\i simanim} are {\i de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- redirects the question: if so, what exactly are Rabbi Elazar ben Mahavai and the Rabbis debating?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Resolution (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qualifying Marks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava explains that the debate about the mole concerns {\i shuma metzuya beven gilo} \u8212- whether a mole of that type is commonly found among people born under the same astrological influence ({\i ben gilo}), i.e., whether it constitutes a sufficiently distinctive identifying mark. One opinion holds that such moles are common enough to other individuals that they do not meet the threshold of a valid {\i siman}; the other holds they are uncommon enough to qualify. The underlying principle is that even if everyone agrees {\i simanim} are {\i de\u8217'oraita}, not every proposed {\i siman} actually meets the threshold. There is a spectrum: at the top is the {\i siman muvhak} \u8212- the distinctively unique mark, analogous to a fingerprint \u8212- about which there is no dispute. At the bottom is something so generic (tall, short) that no one calls it a {\i siman}. The debate about the mole lies somewhere in the middle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Change or Common\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot introduces two additional considerations that bear on the validity of a {\i siman}: first, whether it may {\i change after death} \u8212- certain bodily marks shift in appearance following death, and if so, they cannot serve as reliable {\i simanim}; second, whether the mark is simply too common to be identifying. The Gemara presents a second version of this debate \u8212- {\i shuma ha\u8217'asuya lehishtanos le\u8217'achar misa} \u8212- where the dispute is specifically about whether moles of this kind change after death. One opinion holds they do; the other that they do not. This version does not require invoking the {\i ben gilo} framework at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Generic vs.\u160 ?Specific\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generic Characteristics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the principle that {\i simanim de\u8217'oraita} established \u8212- at least according to one significant position \u8212- a new question arises: why does the mishna exclude {\i simanim} on the body from valid identification? The Gemara answers that the mishna\u8217's case is not one involving genuine {\i simanim} at all. {\i Gufo \u8212- d\u8217'arich vegutz}: the \u8220"identifying marks\u8221" referenced are nothing more than height \u8212- tall or short. Such characteristics are far too generic to count as a {\i siman}. They do not rise to the level of an identifying mark in any meaningful sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding clothing \u8212- {\i keilav} \u8212- the Gemara raises the concern of {\i she\u8217'ila}: people lend their clothes to others, so clothing cannot serve as a reliable identifier. This concern, however, generates a counter-challenge: if we are worried about objects being lent, how do we return lost items ({\i hashavas aveida}) based on {\i simanim} such as the saddle ({\i uchaf}) of a donkey, as is done in tractate Bava Metzia? The Gemara replies: {\i lo sheili inshi uchfa demaska lei lechara} \u8212- people do not lend out saddles, because a saddle not fitted to a particular donkey will irritate its back. The same concern that produces the rule also supplies its exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubot Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel challenge is raised regarding the {\i get} case: how can a {\i get} be returned based on its attachment to a pouch or signet ring if people lend such items? The Gemara answers: {\i taba\u8217'as chashinan leziyufa} \u8212- one does not lend out a signet ring for fear of forgery; {\i menachshi inshi velo moshli} \u8212- people treat their personal pouches and wallets as highly personal objects, associated with personal identity to such a degree that they will not lend them. The Gemara thus accounts for each category individually rather than resolving the lending concern universally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative resolution \u8212- {\i iba\u8217'is eima keilav d\u8217'chivri vesumki} \u8212- proposes that we are not concerned about lending of this kind in general, and that the mishna\u8217's exclusion of clothing applies only because the description was wholly generic: \u8220"he was wearing a white jacket\u8221" is too common to identify anyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's conclusion, at least according to one strand of analysis, is that {\i simanim de\u8217'oraita}: identifying marks have biblical validity. The mishna\u8217's rejection of body characteristics is because those characteristics fell below the threshold of {\i simanim} entirely \u8212- they were generic descriptions, not identifying marks. A genuine {\i siman}, and certainly a {\i siman muvhak}, would in principle suffice even without seeing the face. This ruling substantially expands the halakhic basis for Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's more flexible approach, which now appears less as a creative departure and more as a logical extension of the Gemara\u8217's own framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wounded vs.\u160 ?Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wounds Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna now addresses a second dimension of the testimony requirement: it is not enough to identify who the person was \u8212- one must also establish that he is actually dead. {\i Ein me\u8217'idin ela ad she\u8217'teitzei nafsho} \u8212- one may only testify once the soul has actually departed. {\i V\u8217'afilu ra\u8217'uhu meguyad} \u8212- even if witnesses saw him with severed arteries ({\i meguyad}, which Rashi explains as referring to severed {\i gidim}, and Jastrow renders as cut arteries, apparently a mode of execution in some places), {\i o tzaluv} \u8212- crucified, {\i o chayah ochelet bo} \u8212- or being consumed by a wild animal: none of these observations suffices. The man was still alive, however improbably he might have survived, and testimony of his death requires witnessing actual death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Severed Artery Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling seems to imply that a {\i meguyad} \u8212- someone with severed arteries \u8212- can in fact survive. The Gemara challenges this from a mishna elsewhere stating that a person does not cause corpse-{\i tumah} until he has literally expired: {\i afilu meguyad ve\u8217'afilu goses}, even someone with cut arteries or someone in his final death throes. The implication, the Gemara observes, is that while such a person has not yet died, it is clear he will die \u8212- {\i hamachya lo chaya}. If so, how can our mishna say we cannot testify to his death?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the contradiction: {\i ha Rabi Shimon ben Elazar veha rabbanan} \u8212- the two sources reflect a {\i tannaitic} dispute. A {\i baraita} records that one {\i may} testify about a {\i meguyad} but may {\i not} testify about someone crucified \u8212- but Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar says {\i af al hameguyad ein me\u8217'idin}, even about a {\i meguyad} one may not testify, {\i mipnei sheyakhol lachvot velichyot} \u8212- because the wound might be stanched and the person might survive. Our mishna follows Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar; the {\i baraita}\u8217's first position reflects the view of the Rabbis who hold that a {\i meguyad} will definitely die.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asia Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a further difficulty: if our mishna follows Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar, it seems to contradict itself. The later portion of the mishna (to be discussed in full on the following daf) records a case from Asia in which a man was lowered into the sea and when the rope was pulled back up only his leg remained \u8212- {\i velo alta beyadam ela raglo}. The {\i chachamim} ruled: {\i min harkuva ulemala tinasei} \u8212- if the leg was severed from above the knee, his wife may remarry; {\i min harkuva ulemata lo tinasei} \u8212- below the knee, she may not. Severing a leg above the knee necessarily involves severing major arteries, making this essentially a case of {\i meguyad}. Yet the {\i chachamim} permit remarriage in that case. How can the mishna simultaneously follow Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar and permit testimony in the Asia case?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fatal Wounds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water Irritation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i shani mayim demirzaz maka} \u8212- water is different, because water irritates the wound. When a man is submerged in the ocean with a severed leg, the seawater irritates and aggravates the wound in a way that makes survival impossible. This is unlike a standard case of {\i meguyad} on dry land, where cauterization or stanching might allow survival. The Asia case is therefore a special category where death is certain even according to Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Camel Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses a related challenge from a story told by Rabba bar bar Chana: {\i ledidi chazi li hahu taya} \u8212- \u8220"I myself saw a certain Arab nomad who took his sword and severed part of a camel, {\i velo afsiktiah line\u8217'urasei}\u8221" \u8212- and it did not cease its moaning.\u8221" Rashi explains this as meaning the camel moaned continuously until it died. The story seems to prove that a {\i meguyad} dies \u8212- which supports the Rabbis against Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar, who says survival is possible. But of course the fact that a camel died in one instance does not establish that all {\i meguyad} cases are fatal; it merely shows death is possible, not inevitable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative and more workable reading of the story is that the camel survived despite the severing \u8212- the moaning continued because it was still alive \u8212- which would prove that a {\i meguyad} can indeed live, supporting Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's position. The Gemara then confronts the challenge: if the camel {\i lived}, how does this coexist with the Rabbis\u8217' view that a {\i meguyad} certainly dies?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Circumstances\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two answers are offered. Abaye says: {\i haki kachisha havya} \u8212- it was a thin, sickly camel, and some particular feature of its weakened constitution explains the outcome. Rava offers a more satisfying resolution: {\i besakin meluvenet divrei hakol} \u8212- the sword was white-hot, and everyone would agree that in such a case the cauterizing effect of the heated blade would allow survival. The case of the Arab nomad was a special circumstance \u8212- a cauterizing wound \u8212- and proves nothing about ordinary {\i meguyad} cases. Both answers accept that the camel survived, and explain why the case is exceptional rather than representative.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gittin Get Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Simanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda cites Shmuel: {\i shachat bo shnayim o rov shnayim uvarach me\u8217'idin} \u8212- if the trachea and esophagus (the two {\i simanim} of {\i shechita}) or the majority of them were severed and the man fled, one may testify to his death. This ruling is consistent with the earlier discussion: just as an animal being consumed at the neck would permit testimony (since the vital site is directly involved), so too the severing of the two {\i simanim} at the throat establishes that death is certain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda further cites Shmuel in the {\i Gittin} context: {\i shachat bo shnayim o rov shnayim veramaz ve\u8217'amar kitvu get le\u8217'ishti harei elu yichtevu veyitnu} \u8212- if a man with his trachea and esophagus severed signals that a {\i get} should be written for his wife, it may be written and delivered. This is a striking ruling. Why would a man want his wife to receive a {\i get} at the moment of death? The answer \u8212- consistent with the {\i masechta} being studied \u8212- is to release her from {\i yibum}: she will receive the {\i get}, become a divorc\u233 ?e rather than a widow, and not require {\i yibum} from the surviving brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alive Until Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes an apparent tension: if the man with severed {\i simanim} will certainly die, how can a {\i get} be valid? A {\i get} requires the husband to be alive at the time of delivery. The Gemara resolves it simply: {\i chai hu vesofo lamut} \u8212- he is alive right now; his death is coming, but he has not yet died. The {\i get} is valid because he is halachically alive at the moment of delivery. This point carries significant weight for broader halakhic discussions of the definition of death. Even a person whose trachea and esophagus are both severed is halachically alive \u8212- capable of giving a {\i get}, presumably capable of other halachic acts \u8212- until actual death supervenes. This understanding has direct implications for contemporary discussions of brain death, a topic the Gemara here deliberately sidesteps by focusing on the biological threshold as understood in its own framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Galut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a difficulty: if severing the two {\i simanim} will certainly result in death, why does a {\i baraita} rule {\i harei ze eino goleh} \u8212- that someone who accidentally severs the trachea and esophagus of another person does not go into exile ({\i galut})? Accidental killing normally requires exile. If death is certain from this wound, exile should follow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Causation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wind or Self-Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hoshaya resolves the difficulty: {\i chayshinan shema haruach bilbelato} \u8212- we are concerned that perhaps the wind contributed to the person\u8217's death after the initial wounding. Or alternatively: {\i shema hu kirev mitato} \u8212- perhaps the victim himself hastened his own death, by convulsing or agitating the wound. The principle is one of multiple causation: when factors beyond the initial act may have contributed to death, the standard for {\i galut} is not met, because {\i galut} requires that the death be entirely and unambiguously attributable to the perpetrator\u8217's act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marble vs.\u160 ?Outdoors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara distinguishes between the two possibilities raised by Rav Hoshaya. {\i Deshachte beveisa deshisha uperches} \u8212- if the severing occurred in a marble house (completely enclosed, no wind) but the victim convulsed ({\i parches}), wind is not a factor but self-action may be. {\i Deshachte bevara velo parches} \u8212- if it occurred outdoors (wind possible) but the victim did not convulse, then wind is a factor but self-action is not. The Gemara explores how these scenarios map onto the two distinct explanations offered by Rav Hoshaya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intentional Acts Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot observes that the principle of multiple causation and the {\i galut} exemption are specific to the laws of {\i galut}, which have uniquely demanding standards for establishing causation. The analogy to the contemporary case of a person who was wounded decades ago and recently died from those wounds is instructive: in such a case, even if other factors over the intervening years contributed to the eventual death, the original wound remains the foundational cause. For {\i galut}, however, the halakha demands a much higher degree of exclusive causal attribution \u8212- if {\i any} other factor may have contributed, the exemption applies. If the severing had been intentional rather than accidental, there would be no question: the perpetrator would be liable, regardless of whether wind or other factors may have marginally accelerated death. The {\i galut} exemption is a function of the narrow and highly technical standards that govern that specific {\i din}, not a general principle about causation in homicide.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 121\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Body ID & 3-Day Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a {\i mishna} near the top of 121 in which the question of identifying a drowned man\u8217's body is governed by a three-day standard: after three days the body may bloat beyond recognition, so the window for valid identification closes. Rabbi Yehuda dissents, arguing that not all people are the same and not all conditions are the same \u8212- no fixed rule can be applied uniformly across every case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lenient or Strict?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava, likula pligi o lechumra pligi?} \u8212- Is Rabbi Yehuda being lenient or strict relative to the {\i chachamim}? Tosafos notes that the obvious answer is neither, or rather both: his position is that there is no fixed rule. He is strict in the sense that sometimes even fewer than three days may be insufficient, and lenient in the sense that sometimes more than three days may still allow valid identification. The point is that circumstance determines the outcome, not an arbitrary standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then brings two cases to demonstrate that Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position at minimum permits leniency beyond the three-day limit. In the first, a man drowned in a place called Charmi, his body was recovered in a location called Chadya, and Rav Dimi of Nehardea permitted his wife to remarry \u8212- after three days. In the second, a man drowned in the Tigris, his body was recovered at the bridge of Shavistana, and Rava permitted his wife to remarry based on the testimony of the deceased\u8217's {\i shushvini} \u8212- his close companions \u8212- after five days. The {\i gemara} argues: if Rabbi Yehuda is being lenient, these rulings make sense as following his position. But if he is only being strict \u8212- tightening the standard so that even fewer than three days may be insufficient \u8212- then who are Rav Dimi and Rava following?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water Shrinkage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects: {\i shani maye d\u8217'tsamtei} \u8212- water is different, because it shrivels the body. Therefore, in drowning cases specifically, there is more time within which identification remains possible. This means Rav Dimi and Rava may actually be following the {\i chachamim} who maintain the three-day standard, while applying a recognized extension for the category of drowning \u8212- distinct from Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's case-by-case approach. The irony is not lost: Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's whole point is that you cannot make categorical rules, yet the {\i gemara} proposes that drowning constitutes its own formal category warranting extended time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an apparent tension with a principle discussed previously \u8212- that {\i maya marzu maka}, water exacerbates a wound and makes even non-fatal injuries lethal. The {\i gemara} resolves this: that principle applies where there is an existing wound. Where there is no wound, {\i mitsmat tsamit} \u8212- the water will shrivel the body, permitting identification over a longer period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing of Exam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further qualification follows: this extended window applies only {\i dehachi aska chazi besha\u8217'atei} \u8212- if, when the body was brought out of the water, it was examined immediately. But {\i aval ishtahi}, if they waited before examining it, {\i mitpach tapach} \u8212- it will bloat once removed from the water and will no longer be recognizable. The {\i gemara} does not reach a firm conclusion about the precise scope of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's disagreement, but it is clear that even the {\i chachamim\u8217's} three-day standard should not be read as an absolutely rigid rule.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Edges of Water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition of Edges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now introduces one of the central distinctions in this chapter: {\i mayim she-yesh lahem sof} versus {\i mayim she-ein lahem sof} \u8212- water that has a visible edge versus water that does not. The formulation, {\i nafal lemayim bein she-yesh lahem sof bein she-ein lahem sof}, sets up the question of whether the wife of a drowning victim may remarry. The principle that emerges is counterintuitive at first glance: if the shores of the water are visible on all sides, the wife may remarry even without a body; if the shores cannot be seen, she may not. One might have thought that visible shores would give the man a better chance of escape and survival \u8212- which is precisely why the rule makes sense. If the shores are visible and he did not emerge, we can be confident he did not survive. If the water is boundless and the far edges cannot be seen, he may have emerged somewhere beyond sight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A student raises a sharp point: a river would always seem to qualify as {\i ein lahem sof}, since one can never see its terminus. The {\i gemara} will bear this out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens with the position of Rabbi Meir, clarified by a {\i baraita}: {\i nafal lemayim bein she-yesh lahem sof bein she-ein lahem sof ishto asurah divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- Rabbi Meir holds that in either case, without a body, the wife is forbidden. He supports this with a case: {\i ma\u8217'aseh be-echad she-nafal le-vor gadol ve-alah le-achar sheloshah yamim} \u8212- a certain person fell into a deep pit and emerged alive after three days. As long as there is no confirmed body, the man might have survived. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is therefore absolute: no body, no testimony, no remarriage, regardless of the water\u8217's extent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi disagrees, though characteristically he does not state a legal principle directly \u8212- he responds with stories. First, a blind man descended into a cave to immerse; another person apparently followed him down. The sages waited the time estimated to be sufficient for both men to die from lack of air or sustenance, and then permitted their wives to remarry. Second, in Asia, a man was lowered into the sea for deep-sea diving and only his leg was recovered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Severed Limb Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the recovered limb, the sages ruled: {\i min ha-arkuvah ule-ma\u8217'lah tinasei} \u8212- if the leg was severed above the knee, the wife may remarry, since a wound of that severity is certainly fatal. {\i Min ha-arkuvah ule-matah lo tinasei} \u8212- if it was severed below the knee, she may not. This case is somewhat distinct from the broader debate, since here physical evidence of a fatal wound exists; the question is merely whether the partial body is sufficient proof of death. The deeper disagreement between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yosi concerns whether one can declare death without any body at all, based solely on lethal circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a {\i baraita} that makes the positions explicit: {\i nafal lemayim bein she-yesh lahem sof bein she-ein lahem sof ishto asurah divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- Rabbi Meir forbids in all cases. {\i Ve-chachamim omrim, mayim she-yesh lahem sof ishto muteret} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} permit the wife to remarry if the water has a visible edge. {\i Ve-she-ein lahem sof, ishto asurah} \u8212- if the water has no visible edge, she is forbidden, since the man might have emerged somewhere beyond the observer\u8217's sight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 4-Direction Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what exactly constitutes {\i mayim she-yesh lahem sof}? Abaye defines it: {\i kol she-omed vero-eh me-arba ruchotav} \u8212- any body of water where one can stand and see its shores in all four directions. If even one direction is unobserved, one cannot rule out that the man emerged there.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Shila Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Married\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a case: a man drowned in the swamp of Samki. Rav Shila permitted the man\u8217's wife to remarry. Upon learning this, Rav said to Shmuel: {\i ta nishmateih} \u8212- let us excommunicate him, placing him in {\i shamta}, since this swamp appears to be a case of {\i ein lahem sof}, and Rav Shila had no authority to permit the wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Advice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel counseled restraint: {\i nishlach lei bereisha} \u8212- let us first send him a message and investigate before acting. They sent Rav Shila two questions. First: in water that has no visible edge, is the wife permitted or forbidden? He responded: {\i ishto asurah} \u8212- she is forbidden. Second: is the swamp of Samki considered {\i yesh lahem sof} or {\i ein lahem sof}? Pressed, he acknowledged it was {\i ein lahem sof}. They then asked him directly: given these concessions, why did you permit her to remarry?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Admission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shila admitted: {\i mita te\u8217'ina} \u8212- I made a mistake. {\i Ana savri, kivan d\u8217'ka-i v\u8217'kaimi k\u8217'mayim she-yesh lahem sof dami} \u8212- I assumed that since this is standing water \u8212- a swamp, with no moving current \u8212- even though the edges cannot be seen, it functions like {\i mayim she-yesh lahem sof}. My reasoning was that if the water is still, a person who survived would have surfaced near where he went under. There would be no current to carry him elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This was, on its face, a very reasonable assumption. Indeed, the puzzle in the story is how exactly Rav Shila came to reverse himself. When asked whether the swamp was {\i yesh lahem sof} or {\i ein lahem sof}, he presumably should have answered {\i yesh lahem sof} \u8212- since that was his original reasoning. Somewhere in the questioning he recognized that his interlocutors did not approve of his ruling, and he adjusted his position accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waves Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why the reasoning fails: {\i kivan d\u8217'ika gali, eimar gali ashpluhu} \u8212- since there are waves, or currents, in the swamp, perhaps the currents carried him beneath the surface. Even in seemingly still water, wind alone acting on the surface can generate waves sufficient to carry a body. Therefore, even standing water without visible shores remains categorically {\i ein lahem sof}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Shamta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once it became clear that Rav Shila had acted in good faith, making an honest legal error rather than willfully defying accepted practice, the question of {\i shamta} fell away. {\i Shamta} is reserved for one who brazenly violates established bounds, not for a {\i posek} who reasons carefully and errs. Accordingly, Shmuel applied to Rav the verse {\i lo ye-uneh la-tzadik kol aven} \u8212- no harm comes to the righteous man \u8212- since Rav was spared from the improper act of excommunicating someone who had made an honest mistake. And Rav applied to Shmuel the verse {\i ve-teshuah b\u8217'rov yo-etz} \u8212- salvation comes from good counsel \u8212- crediting Shmuel\u8217's prudent advice for saving him from that error. Regarding the woman herself, the {\i gemara} will address this shortly: even in a case of {\i ein lahem sof} without a body, if the wife was in fact permitted and married, she may remain married {\i bedi\u8217'avad}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Against Rabbi Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejected Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By this point in the sugya it has become clear that the {\i halacha} follows the {\i chachamim} against Rabbi Meir. In cases of {\i mayim she-yesh lahem sof}, even without a body, one may testify to the husband\u8217's death and the wife may remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi introduces a further extension: {\i hada amru rabanan mayim she-ein lahem sof ishto asurah, hani mili be-inish de-alma} \u8212- when the {\i rabbanan} say that in boundless water the wife is forbidden, that applies to an ordinary person. {\i Aval tzurba me-rabanan} \u8212- but if the deceased was a Torah sage, then even in {\i ein lahem sof}, one may assume he is dead. The reasoning is: {\i e d\u8217'salik, kala is lei} \u8212- if he had survived and emerged somewhere, word would have spread, since he is a person of consequence. The absence of any such report is itself evidence of his death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Fame Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i velo hi, lo shna inish de-alma velo shna tzurba me-rabanan} \u8212- this distinction does not hold. Fame does not create a legal category that transforms the evidentiary standard. The underlying concern about boundless water applies equally to sages and ordinary men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bedi\u8217'avad Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} now introduces, without extensive discussion, a principle that is not apparent in the {\i mishna} at all and is something of a surprise: {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, if the woman already remarried \u8212- even in a case of {\i ein lahem sof} without a recovered body \u8212- {\i le-chatchila lo}, she cannot be permitted to remarry at the outset, but if she did, she may remain with her new husband. This is a significant ruling that the {\i gemara} drops almost in passing, and it will resurface explicitly in the later discussion of the Chassa case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shipwreck Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Dimi Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita} in which Rabbi \u8212- Rebbe Yehuda HaNasi \u8212- reports a story of two fishermen casting nets in the Jordan. One entered an underwater tunnel where fish were found and became trapped when night fell and he could no longer see the exit. His companion waited long enough to conclude the man had died and informed his household. The next morning, when light returned, the trapped fisherman found the opening and returned home to find a {\i hesped gadol} \u8212- a great eulogy \u8212- already underway in his house. Rashi explains that there were air pockets within the cavern that allowed him to survive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel\u8217's Praise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi concludes this account with the words {\i kamah gedolim divrei chachamim} \u8212- how great are the words of the sages. The {\i gemara} will explain his precise intent in what follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel Shipwreck\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an objection: if this cavern-survival is possible, should we not also be concerned about it even in cases of {\i yesh lahem sof}? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i mayim she-yesh lahem sof, mechila shel dagin lo shchichi} \u8212- in smaller, bounded bodies of water, such underwater caverns with air pockets are uncommon. The rule for {\i ein lahem sof} is thus justified by the greater range of unusual possibilities that vast bodies of water present, including both distance of emergence and subterranean air pockets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records Rabban Gamliel\u8217's testimony: he was once sailing and saw another ship wrecked at sea. He was distressed in particular for the Torah sage aboard \u8212- Rabbi Akiva. When he later docked and Rabbi Akiva appeared before him to discuss {\i halacha}, Rabban Gamliel asked how he had survived. Rabbi Akiva told him that a plank from the shipwreck had come his way, and he had clung to it. Regarding each wave that approached, {\i naaneti lo roshi} \u8212- he bent his head so the wave passed over him rather than knocking him off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Survival\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this experience Rabbi Akiva derived a {\i mashal}: {\i im yavo-u resha-im al adam, yenane-a lo rosho} \u8212- when wicked people come against a person, bow the head, let the force pass. Discretion is preferable to a futile stand. Rabban Gamliel concluded: {\i kamah gedolim divrei chachamim she-amru mayim she-yesh lahem sof muteret mayim she-ein lahem sof asurah} \u8212- how great is the ruling that in boundless water the wife is forbidden, because he himself had witnessed a shipwreck in open ocean and yet the man survived, carried to safety on a plank, invisible to any observer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's 2nd\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraita} then presents the parallel account from Rabbi Akiva\u8217's own perspective. Rabbi Akiva reports that he was once at sea and saw another ship in distress, aboard which was a Torah sage \u8212- Rabbi Meir. When Rabbi Akiva later arrived at Cappadocia ({\i Kaftor}, identified with modern-day Turkey), Rabbi Meir appeared and wished to discuss {\i halacha}. Rabbi Akiva asked how he had survived. Rabbi Meir replied: {\i gal tardani le-chavero ve-chavero le-chavero, ad she-hikiani la-yabasha} \u8212- one wave cast me to the next, and the next to the next, until I was thrown ashore. Rabbi Akiva likewise concluded: {\i kamah gedolim divrei chachamim she-amru mayim she-yesh lahem sof isha muteret mayim she-ein lahem sof isha asurah} \u8212- since a person can survive in open water by being swept from wave to wave to a shore beyond sight, therefore we must forbid remarriage in {\i ein lahem sof}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this shipwreck \u8212- an event presumably of great consequence in Rabbi Akiva\u8217's life \u8212- appears here only as an aside. And as one might note, the stories have a somewhat formulaic quality: in each account, the teacher witnesses his student\u8217's ship in distress, the student survives through a remarkable means, and the teacher draws the same legal conclusion. Nevertheless, the Talmudic point they illustrate remains clear and vivid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Water Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lions Pit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to cases that do not involve water. {\i Nafal le-gov arayot, ein me-idim alav} \u8212- if a man fell into a pit of lions, one cannot testify to his death, because we cannot be certain the lions attacked him. Rashi suggests the lions may not have been hungry, and indeed observation from wildlife contexts suggests that lions do not attack simply out of proximity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Snakes/Scorpions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Le-chafura me-le-a nechashim va-akrabim, me-idim alav} \u8212- if he fell into a pit full of snakes and scorpions, the {\i tanna kamma} holds that one can testify to his death, since in such crowded conditions they will certainly attack him. Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira disagrees: {\i af le-chafura me-le-a nechashim va-akrabim ein me-idim alav} \u8212- even there one cannot testify, since we must consider the possibility that {\i shema chaver hu} \u8212- perhaps the man is a snake charmer who knows how to handle them. The {\i tanna kamma} responds: {\i agav itzam mat-ke-lei} \u8212- even a snake charmer, if he is being pressed and crowded by them, will be bitten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fiery Furnace\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nafal le-toch kivshan ha-esh, me-idim alav} \u8212- if thrown into a fiery furnace, one can testify to his death. This scenario recalls the story of Chananya, Mishael, and Azaryah, and it is understood that survival in such circumstances would require open miracle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boiling Liquids\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a man fell into a large vat, {\i yora}, filled with boiling wine or oil, a distinction is drawn. In the name of Rabbi Akiva: {\i shemen me-idim alav mipnei she-hu maveer} \u8212- in boiling oil one can testify to his death, since when he falls in, the oil splashes onto the fire and intensifies it. {\i Shel yayin, ein me-idim alav mipnei she-hu mechabeh} \u8212- but in boiling wine, one cannot testify to his death, since the wine splashes onto the fire and extinguishes it, potentially allowing escape. The sages respond to this: {\i techila hu mechabeh ve-sofo maveer} \u8212- it may initially douse the flame but the fire will reignite, so ultimately death can be assumed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Well Miracle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir reasserts his position: {\i ma-aseh be-echad she-nafal le-vor gadol} \u8212- a man fell into a large well and survived for three days. Therefore, absent a confirmed body, one can never testify to death. The {\i baraita} records the sages\u8217' response: {\i ein mazkirin ma-aseh nissim} \u8212- one cannot cite miracles as legal precedent. Halachic standards must be calibrated to normal circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sleep & Ledges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks what the miraculous element of the survival was. If it was merely that he went without food and water for three days \u8212- {\i tzomu alai ve-al tochlu ve-al tishtu shloshet yamim} \u8212- we know people can survive that long. Rather, the {\i gemara} suggests, the miracle is that he did not sleep. Rabbi Yochanan rules that a vow not to sleep for three days is a {\i shvuat shav} \u8212- an oath that cannot be kept \u8212- and the person is immediately lashed and permitted to sleep. Staying awake for three days is considered physiologically impossible and therefore miraculous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's counter: Rav Kahana suggests that perhaps {\i kifin al gabi kifin havu} \u8212- there were ledges within the well on which he could have positioned himself and slept. The Rabbis respond that {\i de-shisha havu} \u8212- the ledges were smooth marble, offering no secure foothold for sleep. Rabbi Meir maintains it is conceivable to lean against a small indentation and rest. The discussion leaves unresolved exactly how any of this maps onto the water-survival question, but the broader point stands: miracles cannot serve as the basis for legal standards.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nechunya Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter Saved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a well-known {\i aggadic} account: the daughter of Nechunya the well-digger \u8212- whose great {\i mitzvah} to the public was digging wells for the {\i olei regel}, those who ascended to Jerusalem for the pilgrims\u8217' festivals \u8212- fell into a large well. The people came and informed Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa. After the first hour he told them: {\i shalom}, she is fine. After the second hour: {\i shalom}. After the third hour \u8212- the point at which survival was considered no longer possible \u8212- he said: {\i alta}, she has been saved, she has come up.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ram & Old Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When asked how she survived, she reported: {\i zachar shel rachelim nizdamen li, ve-zaken manhigo} \u8212- a ram appeared for her, and an old man was leading it. Rashi identifies the old man as Avraham Avinu, and the ram as the ram of the Akeidah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzadik Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When people came to Rabbi Chanina and said {\i navi ata} \u8212- you must be a prophet, to have known she was saved \u8212- he replied: {\i lo navi anochi velo ben navi anochi}. His reasoning was not prophecy but inference: {\i ela davar she-ha-tzadik mit-asek bo, yikashel bo zar-o?} \u8212- could it be that the very thing this righteous man devoted his life to would be the instrument of his child\u8217's death? His whole life was spent ensuring the public had water. That his daughter would perish in a well was inconceivable to Rabbi Chanina. A {\i tzadik\u8217's} life\u8217's work cannot end in the undoing of his own household through that very work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son\u8217's Thirst\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba then introduces the jarring counterpoint: {\i af al pi chen, met b\u8217'no ba-tzama} \u8212- nevertheless, his son died of thirst. The man who spent his life making water available died having a son who perished for lack of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harsh Justice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse cited is {\i u-svivav nis-ara me-od} \u8212- around Him there is great storm \u8212- interpreted as {\i melamed she-ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu medakdek im svivav ke-chut ha-se-ara} \u8212- God exacts from those close to Him with the precision of a hair\u8217's breadth. Tosafos attempts to soften the juxtaposition, noting that at least it was not the same well Nechunya dug from which his son died. But the {\i gemara} does not allow the narrative to rest comfortably on the idea that the world is simply just or that {\i tzadikim} are shielded from tragedy. The juxtaposition is deliberate: yes, the daughter was saved, and yes, the son died. The world is not simply ordered by reward and punishment in predictable ways, and even those close to God are held to an exacting standard that brings suffering alongside blessing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hearsay Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Hearsay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now broadens the categories of acceptable testimony in agunah cases in a striking direction: {\i afilu shama min ha-nashim omrot met ish ploni dayo} \u8212- even if one merely heard from women saying that so-and-so has died, that suffices. While earlier {\i mishnayot} had already indicated that women could testify in these contexts, the novelty here is not the gender of the witness but the informality of the communication. This is not a formal act of testimony \u8212- it is hearsay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children Testify\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda goes further: {\i afilu shama min ha-tinokot omrim harei anu holchim lispod u-likbor et ish ploni} \u8212- even if one hears from children saying, \u8220"we are going to eulogize and bury so-and-so,\u8221" that suffices. Here the witness lacks legal standing on two grounds: children are not formal witnesses, and the statement is not presented as testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i bein she-nitkaven bein she-eino mitkaven} \u8212- whether the speaker intended to testify or not. This raises the question of whether intentionality, which typically strengthens testimony, is here a factor at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jew vs.\u160 ?Non-Jew\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava distinguishes between Jews and non-Jews. Regarding a non-Jew: {\i u-v\u8217'ovdei kochavim im haya mitkaven ein eduto edut} \u8212- if a non-Jew intentionally testified, it is not valid testimony. The concern is that an intentional non-Jewish declaration may be motivated by a desire to cause harm. Only when a non-Jew speaks innocently \u8212- {\i mesiach lefi tumo} \u8212- do we trust him, since there is no suspicion of an ulterior motive. Regarding a Jew, there are two variant readings in the text: one holds that even intentional testimony by a Jew is acceptable; the other, the inverse \u8212- that a Jew must testify formally and intentionally for his statement to carry weight, while mere passing remarks are insufficient.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intention Narrowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now works to narrow the category of {\i nitkaven} \u8212- intentional testimony \u8212- to a very thin slice. Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel rules: {\i lo shanu ela she-nitkaven le-hatir} \u8212- the disqualification of a non-Jew\u8217's intentional testimony applies only if his explicit intent was to permit the woman to remarry. {\i Aval nitkaven le-ha-id, eduto edut} \u8212- if he came merely to testify, stating the fact of the man\u8217's death without specifically invoking the wife\u8217's status, his testimony is valid. The practical result is that {\i nitkaven le-hatir} \u8212- explicitly coming to court to say \u8220"permit this woman to remarry\u8221" \u8212- becomes an extremely narrow category. How often does someone phrase his testimony that way? In almost every realistic case the man will simply say that the husband is dead, and that is accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: how would we even know which intent he had? Rav Yosef clarifies: {\i ba le-veit din ve-amar ish ploni met, hasi-u et ishto, zehu nitkaven le-hatir} \u8212- if he came to court and said, \u8220"so-and-so has died; you should permit his wife to remarry,\u8221" that is {\i nitkaven le-hatir}, and it is rejected. {\i Meit stam} \u8212- if he simply said \u8220"so-and-so is dead\u8221" without mentioning the wife, that is {\i nitkaven le-ha-id}, and it is accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish vs.\u160 ?Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish states the same principle: only explicit intent to permit her remarriage disqualifies the testimony. Rabbi Yochanan objects, citing a tradition that this very position \u8212- \u8220"only {\i nitkaven le-hatir} is disqualified, but {\i nitkaven le-ha-id} is valid\u8221" \u8212- was contested by eighty-five elders, who rejected it. According to Rabbi Yochanan, any intentional testimony by a non-Jew is disqualified, regardless of how he frames it. The {\i gemara} then presses Rabbi Yochanan: if any intentional statement is invalid, what does {\i mesiach lefi tumo} look like? The answer: {\i bemesiach lefi tumo} \u8212- a person simply speaking without any formal purpose. The subsequent cases illustrate this.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intention Narrowed (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Innocent Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents examples of {\i mesiach lefi tumo}. In one case, a man was walking about lamenting: {\i vai lah le-parshata de-zeriza dahavei be-Pumbedita de-shachiv} \u8212- woe to the zealous soldier from Pumbedita who has died. He was simply bewailing someone\u8217's death. On the basis of this, Rav Yosef \u8212- or according to another version, Rava \u8212- permitted the dead man\u8217's wife to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chassa Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In another case, someone was going about asking: {\i man ika bei Chassa? tava Chassa} \u8212- who is from the family of Chassa? Chassa drowned. Rav Nachman, upon hearing this, exclaimed: {\i achlu kavrei le-Chassa} \u8212- the fish have eaten Chassa. What is striking here is that Rav Nachman\u8217's exclamation itself constitutes an implicit ruling: by saying the fish ate Chassa, he was accepting the testimony of this non-Jew speaking innocently, and implicitly ruling that Chassa should be presumed dead \u8212- without apparent concern that Chassa might have emerged on another shore. From this it follows that Rav Nachman was treating this as a case he was willing to rule upon.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bedi\u8217'avad Remarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stays Married\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} uses the Chassa case to crystallize the {\i bedi\u8217'avad} principle. When the {\i rabbanan} say {\i mayim she-ein lahem sof isha asurah} \u8212- that in boundless water the wife is forbidden \u8212- this refers to {\i lechatchila}: at the outset, a {\i beit din} may not permit her to remarry in such circumstances. But that prohibition is not absolute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Upon hearing Rav Nachman\u8217's remark that the fish had eaten Chassa, Chassa\u8217's wife took matters into her own hands: {\i azlah devinhu de-Chassa ve-insava} \u8212- she went and remarried on her own, without rabbinic sanction. She had not received a formal ruling; she overheard a scholar make a loose remark and acted unilaterally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, {\i velo amru lah velo midi} \u8212- no one said anything to her, before or after. The {\i gemara} did not require her to divorce her new husband. This is a powerful illustration of the {\i bedi\u8217'avad} principle: even where remarriage was not permitted prospectively, after the fact she is not compelled to undo it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some say ({\i ika de-amri}) that Rav Nachman actually did formally permit her: {\i ansivah Rav Nachman lidevinhu} \u8212- Rav Nachman permitted his wife to remarry. His reasoning would then parallel Rav Ashi\u8217's earlier suggestion: {\i Chassa gavra rabba isei, im issa de-salik kala is lei le-milsa} \u8212- Chassa was a man of great stature, and had he survived and emerged elsewhere, word would have reached us. The {\i gemara} rejects this reasoning: {\i velo hi, lo shna gavra rabba velo shna lo gavra rabba} \u8212- fame creates no legal distinction. This is not a valid basis for a {\i lechatchila} ruling, neither for the famous nor for the ordinary man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechatchila/Bedi\u8217'avad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final distinction stands: {\i lechatchila}, a {\i beit din} may not permit remarriage in {\i ein lahem sof} without a body. {\i Bedi\u8217'avad} \u8212- if she remarried, she remains married. This principle, barely hinted at in the {\i mishna}, emerges through the case of Chassa\u8217's wife as one of the significant practical {\i chiddushim} of the entire sugya. The {\i gemara\u8217's} sustained drive throughout the daf has been to expand the conditions under which a woman can be freed from her {\i agunah} status, not by overriding the rules but by carefully defining their contours and by establishing that post-facto remarriage, even in borderline cases, is not undone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Yevamot 122\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jew Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Threat Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final daf of Masechet Yevamot picks up three lines from the bottom of 121b, in the midst of the broad question of who may be believed to testify that a man has died. The mishnah had already introduced the categories of women, children, and even incidental statements \u8212- {\i mesiach lefi tumo} \u8212- made in passing without deliberate intent to testify. The Gemara now turns to a specific case that tests the limits of accepting such testimony from a non-Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A certain non-Jew told a Jew: cut down this fodder and throw it to my animals on Shabbat. And if you refuse, I will kill you just as I killed a certain other Jew \u8212- Reuven ben Shimon \u8212- who refused to cook for me on Shabbat. The non-Jew\u8217's threat was overheard by the wife of the man he claimed to have killed. She came before Abaye, reasoning that the statement sounded like {\i mesiach lefi tumo}: the non-Jew was not trying to testify but was simply invoking the killing as a point of intimidation, incidentally revealing what had happened. Could she assume her husband was dead and remarry?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye kept her waiting through three {\i regalim}. Rashi offers two explanations for this delay. The first is that the {\i regalim} \u8212- the pilgrimage festivals \u8212- were occasions when Torah scholars gathered to hear discourses on the laws of each {\i chag}, and Abaye was using those assemblies to consult colleagues. The second reading comes from a responsum of the Geonim: that {\i regalim} as used by Amoraim does not refer to the pilgrimage festivals at all, but to the annual yahrzeit commemorations of great sages. According to this reading, Abaye waited three years. The Geonim describe the yahrzeit of a major {\i talmid chacham} as an occasion when scholars from the surrounding area would gather at his grave and hold a day of Torah study in his memory \u8212- essentially a yahrzeit lecture and commemorative gathering. This is fascinating not because the Gemara explicitly says any of this, but because the Geonim read the practice into it, revealing that such commemorations were already established by their time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any event, Abaye remained unresolved after all three consultations. Rav Adda bar Ahava told the woman she should go to Rav Yosef, Abaye\u8217's own teacher, who has a sharp knife \u8212- {\i deharif sakhina} \u8212- meaning he can cut through ambiguous questions decisively. She went, and Rav Yosef resolved the matter from a {\i braita} about a non-Jew selling fruit in the marketplace who said: these fruits are from a tree only in its second year, or from the place called Azaka, or from a fourth-year planting \u8212- {\i neta revai}. The Gemara rules that such a statement carries no weight \u8212- {\i lo amar klum} \u8212- because the seller was not speaking incidentally but with an ulterior motive: to make his wares sound more appealing. He was not {\i mesiach lefi tumo}; he had a reason to say what he said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A brief but significant digression arises regarding the term {\i azaka}. Rashi prefers the reading that it refers to a place in Eretz Yisrael, raising questions of {\i tevel} when the fruit is sold in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. He rejects the alternative reading \u8212- that the fruit came from a field that had been guarded during {\i shmita} \u8212- because he sees no reason why guarding a field during the sabbatical year would render its fruit forbidden. However, Tosafot cites Rabbeinu Tam, who brings the {\i Torat Kohanim} explicitly: \u8220"Do not harvest your grapes from the guarded\u8221" \u8212- {\i min hamshumar}; one may only harvest from produce that has been declared {\i hefker}. The act of guarding the field \u8212- exerting ownership over it \u8212- renders the fruit forbidden for harvest and likely forbidden altogether. It is striking that so central a question of {\i shmita} law finds its primary textual basis in the {\i Torat Kohanim} alone, with neither the mishnah nor the Yerushalmi addressing it directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the main thread: how is the fruit-seller case relevant to the woman whose husband may have been killed? Because in that case too, the non-Jew had a reason to say what he said \u8212- not to inform, but to threaten. He was coercing the Jew into violating Shabbat by claiming to have already killed someone who refused him. That is not {\i mesiach lefi tumo}. There is good reason to think he was lying, or at minimum not speaking as a disinterested party. The answer Rav Yosef gave was therefore that the woman could not remarry \u8212- not a useful result for her, though one might speculate that Abaye, who kept delaying, sensed this conclusion and was searching for a way to permit her rather than to forbid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might push back: the non-Jew was extremely specific \u8212- he named Reuven ben Shimon \u8212- and the man had not been seen for three years. That specificity might have been a reason to credit the testimony. But the Gemara is unpersuaded, because the motivation to threaten overrides the specificity. The principle that emerges is that {\i mesiach lefi tumo} is qualified: it does not operate when there is affirmative reason to think the speaker may be fabricating or exaggerating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnessing Standard (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geonic Responsum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As noted above, within the discussion of Abaye\u8217's extended deliberation, Rashi introduces a Geonic responsum identifying the {\i regalim} mentioned in the Gemara not as pilgrimage festivals but as the yearly yahrzeit gatherings held at the graves of major sages. The Geonim describe a practice in which, {\i midei shanah be-shanah}, scholars from the surrounding region would convene at the sage\u8217's burial site each year on the anniversary of his death, together with the broader community, to conduct a {\i yeshivah} \u8212- a day of Torah study \u8212- in his honor. This represents the earliest known reference to something resembling the yahrzeit lecture as an institution, though the Gemara itself says nothing of the kind. It is the Geonim who read this practice into the text, revealing both their interpretive creativity and the antiquity of the commemoration as a living custom by their era.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jew Testimony (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit Seller Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analysis of the fruit-seller case establishes the controlling principle for when non-Jewish testimony is rejected: not because of a categorical rule against non-Jews, but because in that instance there was an identifiable motive for the speaker to say what he said that was independent of its truth. When the non-Jew in the market claimed his fruit came from a young tree or a protected field or from Eretz Yisrael, he was doing so to enhance the perceived value of his merchandise. His statement was therefore not a genuine {\i mesiach lefi tumo} \u8212- it was not an incidental, unmotivated disclosure, but a sales pitch. If we do not treat such a statement as reliable enough to be {\i machmir} \u8212- to create a {\i halakhic} concern about {\i orla} or {\i tevel} \u8212- then certainly we cannot treat it as reliable enough to be {\i meikel} \u8212- to permit a woman to remarry. The asymmetry is significant: even the lenient application of non-Jewish testimony requires the baseline of {\i mesiach lefi tumo}, which is absent when the speaker has an ulterior motive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accepted Testimonies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In contrast, the Gemara proceeds to a series of cases in which non-Jewish testimony was fully accepted. Abba Yudan Ish Tzaidan reports: a Jew and a non-Jew were traveling together. The non-Jew returned alone and said, {\i chaval al Yehudi she-hayah imi baderech} \u8212- woe to the Jew who was traveling with me \u8212- he died on the way and I buried him. On that basis, his wife was permitted to remarry. This is a straightforward case of {\i mesiach lefi tumo}: the non-Jew was not being questioned, not trying to establish a legal fact, simply lamenting out loud.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second story involves a group of people, described as a {\i kolar shel bnei adam}, who were traveling to Antioch. A non-Jew who had come from the same direction \u8212- not even a member of their party \u8212- arrived and said: woe to that group of people, they all died and I buried them. Their wives were married off on this basis. A third story: sixty people traveling toward the besieged city of {\i Karkom Beitar} \u8212- where {\i karkom} means besieged, not a place name \u8212- and a non-Jew who reported that they had all died and he had buried them. Their wives too were permitted to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing on the context of these final two cases. If {\i kolar} means that the first group were prisoners shackled with collars, then there was already an elevated likelihood that something terrible had happened to them. And people traveling toward a city under siege face obvious danger. It is not entirely clear how much the surrounding context of likely peril contributed to the credibility of the non-Jew\u8217's report, but the Gemara does not explicitly make that a condition. The principle appears to be that {\i mesiach lefi tumo} \u8212- the incidental statement without identifiable motive \u8212- is sufficient, and the Gemara is willing to be quite broad in applying it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that in none of these cases does the Gemara describe any attempt to verify the facts \u8212- to locate the grave, to find corroborating witnesses, to identify the body. The question the Gemara addresses is purely one of whose testimony is admitted, not of evidentiary sufficiency in the modern sense. These {\i halakhot}, it is worth noting, have found real application in tragic historical circumstances, including in {\i responsa} literature dealing with mass casualties where identifying individual deaths was impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnessing Standard (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Light Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next mishnah shifts from the question of {\i who} may testify to the question of {\i how} clearly the death must have been witnessed. The mishnah rules: one may testify \u8212- and on that basis a woman may remarry \u8212- even if the witnessing occurred by candlelight or by moonlight. The requirement that the witness see the deceased\u8217's face does not mean he must have seen it under ideal conditions; limited illumination suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Kol Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah adds a remarkable ruling: {\i umasin al pi bat kol} \u8212- a woman may even be remarried based on a {\i bat kol}. This term, so common in the Bavli in the sense of a heavenly voice, carries a different and more primary meaning here. Rashi explains: {\i bat kol} means a disembodied voice \u8212- a voice whose source is not visible to the hearer. The witness heard someone announce that so-and-so had died, but did not see who was speaking. The {\i bat kol} is not a supernatural phenomenon; it is simply unattributed testimony heard without visual identification of the speaker. And since the Gemara has already extended acceptance to any category of testimony in this context, the identity of the speaker becomes irrelevant \u8212- which is precisely why even a {\i bat kol} is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mountain Voice Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah records: a person heard from the top of a mountain a voice declaring that so-and-so the son of so-and-so, from such-and-such a place, had died. They went to investigate who had spoken \u8212- and found no one. Even so, the wife was permitted to remarry. This case illustrates both the broad acceptance of unattributed testimony and also an important procedural instinct: when the source of testimony is unclear, one makes an effort to investigate further. Here the effort was made and came to nothing, yet that did not undermine the ruling. There was an attempt to verify; the attempt was inconclusive; the {\i bat kol} remained sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Snake Bite Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel story from the town of Tsalmon: a man declared \u8212- {\i ani Ish Ploni ben Ish Ploni nashchani nachash} \u8212- I, so-and-so the son of so-and-so, have been bitten by a poisonous snake, and I am dying. By the time people arrived, they could not identify him \u8212- his features had apparently changed due to the venom. Nevertheless, his wife was permitted to remarry. The combination of self-identification and the plausible mechanism of death was deemed sufficient, even without a confirmable corpse. Once again, the effort to gather more information was made; once again it was frustrated; once again the testimony stood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges across these cases is a pattern: the Gemara expects an attempt to investigate further when the source of testimony is unknown or unclear, but it does not require that the attempt succeed. And the standard for what constitutes testimony acceptable enough to permit remarriage is, by this point, remarkably permissive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Shammai/Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Kol Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Shmuel cites a {\i braita}: Beit Shammai says a woman may not be remarried on the basis of a {\i bat kol}; Beit Hillel says she may. The Gemara immediately asks: given that we have a {\i stam mishnah} \u8212- an anonymous mishnah \u8212- ruling in accordance with Beit Hillel, why does it matter that the {\i braita} records Beit Shammai\u8217's position? The Gemara answers: to tell us that if one finds another {\i stam} source appearing to say that a woman may not remarry on the basis of a {\i bat kol}, one should not conclude that the {\i halakha} is undecided. Rather, recognize it as the position of Beit Shammai and set it aside accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recording Minority Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer surfaces a well-known question from Masechet Eduyot: why record a minority opinion if we do not rule in accordance with it? Two quite opposite answers are offered there. The first is inclusive: minority opinions are recorded so that they remain available for future use in cases of pressing need \u8212- {\i sha\u8217'at hadechak}. The second is exclusionary and is the one the Gemara invokes here: minority opinions are recorded precisely so that if anyone ever advocates for that position, it can be identified and dismissed. Recording Beit Shammai\u8217's view on {\i bat kol} is not an invitation to reconsider it; it is a taxonomic act, filing it away so that it can never be mistaken for mainstream {\i halakha}. These two rationales are not complementary; they represent genuinely opposite approaches to the status of rejected legal positions within the tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an additional irony worth noting. The celebrated dispute between Beit Hillel and Beit Shammai over which school\u8217's rulings we follow was itself famously resolved by a {\i bat kol} that declared the {\i halakha} to follow Beit Hillel. Here it is Beit Shammai who rejects the evidentiary weight of a {\i bat kol}, and Beit Hillel who accepts it \u8212- which lends a certain poetic consistency to each school\u8217's position across these two quite different contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnessing Standard (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demon/Co-wife Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an obvious challenge to the mountain-voice case: perhaps the voice came not from a human being at all, but from a demon. Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav responds that the figure appeared to have the {\i dmus adam} \u8212- the form of a human being. The Gemara presses: but demons can appear in human form. What if a shadow was seen? Demons too can cast shadows. What if a shadow of a shadow was observed? Rav Chanina cites Rabbi Yonasan, who reports having been told by a demon directly: demons have shadows, but they do not have shadows of shadows. Seeing a shadow of a shadow is thus the identifying mark of a genuine human presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further concern is raised: perhaps the voice belonged to a {\i tzarah} \u8212- a co-wife \u8212- who has a clear motive to lie about her rival\u8217's husband\u8217's death and is explicitly excluded from this type of testimony. The Gemara invokes a parallel teaching from the school of Rabbi Yishmael: in a time of danger, one may write and deliver a {\i get} even without knowing the person, relying entirely on their self-identification, because the alternative is that the woman will be left without recourse. Similarly here \u8212- {\i kasha\u8217'at sakana damya} \u8212- if we cannot determine who spoke, and the only alternative is leaving the woman as an {\i agunah}, we proceed on the reasonable assumption that the speaker was not one of the five excluded categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is admittedly difficult to know exactly where the threshold of {\i mesiach lefi tumo} lies \u8212- the Gemara elsewhere counted as {\i mesiach lefi tumo} a person who came specifically intending to inform the wife, without being questioned at all. Yet that same Gemara did not consider it {\i nitkavein l\u8217'haid} \u8212- deliberate testimony. The category seems to resist clean definition. What is clear is that the further from a formal testimonial context, the more comfortable the Gemara is in accepting what is said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halacha Development\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva in Nehardea\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now delivers one of its most historically remarkable passages \u8212- a window into how a sweeping {\i halakhic} innovation actually developed. Rabbi Akiva says: when I went down to Nehardea \u8212- the phrase {\i yaradti} reflecting the geographical and perhaps spiritual descent from Eretz Yisrael to the diaspora \u8212- I went to intercalate the year, which itself is striking, since the authority for {\i ibbur hashanah} ordinarily resided in Eretz Yisrael. There I found a certain Nechemya from Beit Deli, who told me: I have heard that in Eretz Yisrael a woman is not permitted to remarry on the basis of a single witness \u8212- {\i ed echad} \u8212- except according to Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava. Only he holds that position; no one else does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This statement stops the reader cold. Everything the Gemara had seemed to take for granted \u8212- that {\i ed echad} is broadly accepted \u8212- turns out to have been the solitary position of one sage. The broad acceptance of testimony that has been the assumed framework throughout this {\i masechet} was not established practice at all; it was a contested minority view. Nechemya reported this to Rabbi Akiva and confirmed it was accurate. Nechemya then added: I cannot travel to testify myself because the country is {\i meshubbeshet begaysot} \u8212- riddled with military forces \u8212- but tell them in my name: {\i mekublani me-Rabban Gamliel Hazaken} \u8212- I have a tradition going back to Rabban Gamliel the Elder \u8212- that a woman may be remarried based on a single witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tel Arza Incident\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva returned and reported this tradition to Rabban Gamliel the Younger \u8212- Rabban Gamliel of his own generation, not the Zaken. Rabban Gamliel rejoiced, saying: we have found a {\i chaver} \u8212- a supporting authority \u8212- for Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava. And in the midst of this conversation, Rabban Gamliel recalled that there had been a group of people killed at Tel Arza, and that their widows had not been permitted to remarry because the reports of their deaths rested only on {\i ed echad}, which had not been established practice. Armed now with the tradition from Rabban Gamliel Hazaken, Rabban Gamliel the Younger acted: he married off the wives of those killed at Tel Arza based on {\i ed echad}. The {\i halakha} moved from contested theory to executed practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And once it became established through practice \u8212- {\i ve-huchzeku} \u8212- the expansion did not stop there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Expansion of Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reports: once the practice was consolidated, they went further and permitted remarriage based on {\i ed mipi ed} \u8212- hearsay testimony, where the witness did not personally observe the death \u8212- and based on the testimony of a slave, a woman, and a female slave. Until this point, slaves had not been mentioned as a distinct category; the Gemara does not assume that because non-Jews are believed, slaves are believed automatically. And the category of hearsay testimony is itself a major {\i chidush} \u8212- one may remarry based on testimony from someone who did not witness the death firsthand but merely heard it reported.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final line of the mishnah then reveals that this entire developmental history has a {\i tannaitic} antecedent. Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- who were Rabbi Akiva\u8217's own teachers \u8212- held that a woman may not be remarried based on {\i ed echad}; only two qualified witnesses or the wife\u8217's own testimony sufficed. Rabbi Akiva, in his earlier position before the Nehardea episode, agreed to accept {\i ed echad} but insisted it must be a qualified male witness \u8212- not a woman, not a slave, not a relative. The Gemara asks: but does Rabbi Akiva truly hold that a woman\u8217's testimony is insufficient? And it cites a {\i braita} in which Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar reports in Rabbi Akiva\u8217's name that a woman is believed to bring her own {\i get} based on a {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} runs as follows: the women whom the Sages said are {\i not} believed to testify that a husband has died \u8212- the co-wife, the mother-in-law, and the others in that category \u8212- are nonetheless believed to bring a {\i get} for a woman and attest that it was properly executed, since the physical document supports their word. If even those disqualified women are trusted in that context, how much more so should a woman who {\i is} believed to testify about death be trusted to bring her own {\i get}? The implication is that the five excluded women are the exception, and ordinary women are assumed to be reliable. Rabbi Akiva clearly held this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent contradiction between Rabbi Akiva\u8217's general principle of women\u8217's credibility and his specific ruling against accepting women\u8217's testimony in {\i eidut isha} cases as follows: {\i lo kashya} \u8212- the early position of Rabbi Akiva excluding women was {\i kodem she-hecheziku} \u8212- before the practice was consolidated by the Nehardea report and Rabban Gamliel\u8217's action. His later position accepting women was {\i me-acharei she-hecheziku} \u8212- after that consolidation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The picture that emerges is breathtaking in its historical specificity. At the earliest stratum \u8212- the Beit Hillel and Beit Shammai debate \u8212- all that had been established was that a {\i wife} could testify about her own husband\u8217's death in a narrow set of circumstances. The acceptance of any other category of witness \u8212- {\i ed echad}, women, non-Jews, slaves, hearsay \u8212- was not a natural outgrowth of that ruling but a subsequent and contested development. The last line of the mishnah preserves the early, pre-consolidation positions as a kind of archaeological stratum within the text, a reminder of a moment when the {\i halakha} could have taken an entirely different path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Innkeeper Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levites/Innkeeper\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next and final mishnah of the {\i masechet} presents a challenge to the pre-consolidation position of Rabbi Akiva. They said to him \u8212- to the early Rabbi Akiva who rejected women\u8217's testimony in {\i eidut isha} \u8212- there is a story about people called {\i Bnei Levi} who went to Tzo\u8217'a, the city of date palms. One of them fell ill, and they left him at an inn while they continued their journey. On their return, they asked the innkeeper: where is our companion? She said to them: he died and I buried him. On the basis of this testimony, his wife was permitted to remarry. If you can believe the word of this innkeeper, Rabbi Akiva, can you really maintain that women are not believed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's response, in his early position: {\i le-she-tihiye ke-fundakit ne\u8217'emenet} \u8212- if women in general were to be like this innkeeper, they would indeed be believed. What distinguished this innkeeper?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The innkeeper did not merely make a verbal report. She produced his effects: his staff, his traveling sack, and the {\i Sefer Torah} he had carried. {\i Mesiach lefi tumo} alone was not what made her credible in the early Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view \u8212- it was the physical evidence she brought forward that corroborated her statement. {\i Ze maklo ve-ze tarmilo ve-ze kever she-kevartiv bo} \u8212- this is his staff, this is his sack, and here is the grave where I buried him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then confirms, via Rav Kahana, that the innkeeper was in fact a non-Jewish woman \u8212- {\i pundakis ovedes kochavim hayasah} \u8212- which means the {\i kal vachomer} being pressed on Rabbi Akiva was even sharper than it had appeared. Not only was she a woman; she was a non-Jew. And she was believed \u8212- implicitly establishing that non-Jewish testimony was already a matter of {\i tannaitic} debate even at this early pre-consolidation stage, woven into the fabric of the dispute before Rabban Gamliel\u8217's decisive action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a technical challenge: the innkeeper\u8217's testimony was not truly {\i mesiach lefi tumo}, because she was being directly asked \u8212- {\i aychan chaveirenu} \u8212- where is our friend? A direct question would seem to elicit deliberate testimony rather than an incidental remark. The Gemara resolves this: {\i keivan de-chazatinhu bachya} \u8212- because she saw them return and began to cry before they questioned her, the emotional response came first, naturally, and only then did the questioning follow. The crying established the spontaneous, unguarded quality of the disclosure \u8212- and then the verbal statement, though prompted, rode on that foundation of {\i mesiach lefi tumo}. The operative principle seems to be that testimony ceases to be {\i mesiach lefi tumo} when the speaker is being formally drilled for information in a context that resembles examination \u8212- but a natural emotional response, even if followed by direct questioning, preserves the character of an incidental statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cross-Exam Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spontaneous ID\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents a case in which a man came to testify about a woman\u8217's status before Rabbi Tarfon. Rabbi Tarfon asked him: how do you know this testimony? The witness explained: I was traveling with this man when a military troop \u8212- {\i gayas} \u8212- pursued us. My companion grabbed the branch of an olive tree, tore it off, and used it to beat the pursuers back. Impressed, I called out: {\i Aryeh, yishar kochacha!} \u8212- Lion, may your strength be straight! He turned to me and said: how did you know my name was Aryeh? That is indeed what they call me in my city \u8212- Yochanan ben Yehonasan, Aryeh, from Kfar Shichayah. The name had come out spontaneously \u8212- it was a metaphor the witness offered, and it turned out to be the man\u8217's actual nickname. Some time later the man fell ill and died. Rabbi Tarfon accepted this testimony and permitted the wife to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case presents a striking feature: the identifying information emerged through coincidence, through the witness\u8217's use of a metaphorical epithet that happened to match the subject\u8217's actual name. The detail is concrete, the self-identification was unrehearsed, and the testimony was sufficiently specific.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rigorous Questioning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} tells what appears to be the same story with a crucial difference. In this version, after the witness delivered his account \u8212- this time involving a fig tree rather than an olive tree \u8212- Rabban Gamliel, not Rabbi Tarfon, put the witness through a rigorous consistency check. He said to him: wait \u8212- didn\u8217't you tell me his name was arranged differently? Was it not Yochanan ben Yehonasan from Kfar Shichayah, Aryeh \u8212- in a different order than you just gave? The witness pushed back: no, I said it exactly as I said it now. Rabban Gamliel pressed him two and three times \u8212- {\i dikdek oto b\u8217'shtayim u-beshalosh pe\u8217'amim} \u8212- trying to find any inconsistency, any shift in how the details were presented. The witness remained consistent throughout, and only then was the wife permitted to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a very different posture toward testimony. Rather than accepting what the witness said at face value, Rabban Gamliel was actively trying to induce an inconsistency \u8212- the kind of technique one might recognize from interrogations designed to detect unreliable accounts by repeatedly asking the subject to repeat their story and looking for variation. Had the witness altered even an apparently insignificant detail, one senses the testimony would have been rejected. The fact that he did not, and was then believed, shows that the rigor was real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drisha/Chakirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two versions of the story \u8212- one before Rabbi Tarfon accepting the testimony without such review, the other before Rabban Gamliel with elaborate consistency-checking \u8212- prompt the Gemara to ask: did Rabbi Tarfon really not require {\i drisha vechakirah}? And a {\i braita} is cited: the debate is explicitly a dispute between Rabbi Akiva and Rabbi Tarfon. Rabbi Akiva says {\i ein bodkin edei nashim b\u8217'drisha vechakirah} \u8212- witnesses in {\i eidut isha} cases are not subjected to {\i drisha vechakirah}. Rabbi Tarfon says they are. Some versions of the text read the lenient position as anonymous and Rabbi Tarfon as the stricter one; the implication is similar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monetary vs.\u160 ?Capital\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying dispute is traced to a principle stated by Rabbi Chanina: biblically, both monetary cases and capital cases require {\i drisha vechakirah} \u8212- the formal inquiry into time and place that is the classic tool of witness examination \u8212- as derived from the verse {\i mishpat echad yihye lachem}, one law for all proceedings. The Sages relaxed this requirement for monetary cases to avoid {\i neilas delet bifnei lovin} \u8212- locking the door before borrowers, meaning that if creditors had to subject their claims to full {\i drisha vechakirah} before recovering debts, lenders would simply stop lending.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then is how to categorize {\i eidut isha}. One view holds that since collecting the {\i ketubah} is involved, the case resembles a monetary case and {\i drisha vechakirah} is not required. The other view holds that since we are binding or releasing a woman from {\i isur eshet ish} \u8212- a prohibition of the severity of capital law \u8212- it resembles {\i dinei nefashot} and {\i drisha vechakirah} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takkanat Agunah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara does not leave the tension unresolved. Tosafot, citing {\i Chad Mi-Kamai}, states explicitly: since we have already extended {\i eidut isha} to include testimony from men, women, slaves, non-Jews, children, and even {\i ed mipi ed}, and we have done all of this {\i mishum takanat agunah} \u8212- on account of the enactment to free chained wives \u8212- we have so fundamentally reshaped the evidentiary framework here that it cannot be made to conform to the rules of either {\i dinei mamonot} or {\i dinei nefashot}. The Rambam rules accordingly, as does Rabbeinu Tam in Tosafot Yeshanim: {\i drisha vechakirah} is not required. The framing of the case as {\i dinei mamonot} by virtue of the {\i ketubah} is thus best understood not as a genuine classification but as a legal hook \u8212- a way of reaching the conclusion the {\i halakha} demands once the {\i takkanat agunah} lens is applied. The entire evidentiary edifice for {\i eidut isha} is its own category, shaped not by the standard rules of testimony but by the overriding imperative of preventing women from being trapped as {\i agunot} for life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion/Aggada\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scholars & Peace\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara closes with the statement of Rabbi Elazar in the name of Rabbi Chanina: {\i talmidei chachamim marbim shalom ba\u8217'olam} \u8212- Torah scholars increase peace in the world \u8212- as it is written, {\i ve-chol banayich limudei Hashem ve-rav shalom banayich} \u8212- and all your children will be students of God, and great will be the peace of your children. The choice to end Masechet Yevamot with this {\i aggadic} statement is not incidental. The final daf has shown, in remarkable historical detail, how the Sages expanded and reshaped the {\i halakha} of {\i eidut isha} \u8212- from narrow spousal testimony to the acceptance of any witness, in any form, under any ordinary circumstances \u8212- in order to free women from the trap of {\i iggun}. The peace the Sages increased was the peace of women whose lives were otherwise suspended indefinitely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maharsha Exegesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Maharsha, commenting on this closing {\i aggadata}, addresses the obvious tension directly. One might object that the entire final section of Yevamot \u8212- indeed, the final several chapters \u8212- appears to be a systematic setting aside of Torah evidentiary requirements: the rules of {\i shnei eidim}, the rules of {\i eidut}, the prohibition of {\i eshet ish}. Is this not {\i akiras davar min ha-Torah} \u8212- a uprooting of biblical law?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Maharsha answers: {\i ain zeh akirah ela mi-midat shalom} \u8212- this is not an uprooting but an expression of the quality of peace. The Torah itself declares {\i deracheha darchei no\u8217'am ve-chol nesivoteha shalom} \u8212- its ways are ways of pleasantness and all its paths are peace. A woman left permanently {\i me\u8217'ugenet} \u8212- chained \u8212- is incompatible with the Torah\u8217's own stated character. The Sages did not override the Torah; they read it through the lens the Torah itself provided. And the acceptance of {\i ed echad}, of women, of non-Jews, of {\i ed mipi ed}, of slaves \u8212- all of it flows from that same lens. {\i Mishum takanat agunah} is not a departure from Torah; it is Torah\u8217's way of peace made concrete in {\i halakha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The note that {\i banayich} may also be read as an acronym is left, appropriately, as the final word of the masechet\u8217's discourse \u8212- {\i hadran alach Ha\u8217'Ishah Basra ve-salikah lah Masechet Yevamot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro to Ketubot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin a new {\i masechta}. There is a well-known saying in the {\i yeshiva} world that three {\i masechtot} will, as it were, wipe you out \u8212- and the acronym for them is {\i Ani}, meaning poverty: {\i Eruvin}, {\i Niddah}, and {\i Yevamos}. Having just completed {\i Yevamos} \u8212- which was particularly challenging at the outset, though the middle and end proved highly relevant and engaging \u8212- we turn now to {\i Ketubot}. {\i Niddah} remains for the very end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ketubot} carries its own famous reputation in the {\i yeshiva} world: it is known as the {\i Shas Katan}, the mini-{\i Shas}, because so many diverse concepts from across {\i Shas} arise in the course of its discussions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin vs.\u160 ?Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The primary topic of {\i Ketubot} is, one might say, the {\i ketubah} \u8212- but more precisely, it is the legal and practical state of {\i nisuin}, as distinct from {\i kiddushin}. {\i Kiddushin} is the formal legal act of marriage: the giving of the ring in contemporary practice, which renders the woman fully and legally married such that any subsequent infidelity would constitute adultery. Yet at this stage, the couple has not begun living together. That actualization \u8212- the setting up of a shared home and life \u8212- is {\i chuppah}, the moment of {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In ancient practice, {\i kiddushin} could take place when a woman was still a minor, arranged between the prospective husband and her father, and might precede {\i nisuin} by many years. {\i Chuppah} was thus the moment when the marriage became real, not merely legal. As a result, it is {\i chuppah} that is the great communal event \u8212- the {\i simcha} with the large {\i seudah}, the {\i sheva brachot} requiring a {\i minyan} and representing the blessing of the community upon the {\i chatan} and {\i kallah}. The {\i bracha} of {\i eirusin}, by contrast, does not require a {\i minyan} and is far more private in character. It is worth noting that while one certainly requires {\i edim} for {\i kiddushin}, it is not entirely clear that formal {\i edim} are required for {\i chuppah} itself, since {\i nisuin} may be understood simply as the actualization of the {\i kiddushin} that had already taken place \u8212- potentially years earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Parts Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once {\i nisuin} begins, a range of reciprocal obligations take effect. The husband becomes obligated to provide food, lodging, and {\i mitzvat onah} \u8212- conjugal relations at prescribed intervals. The wife takes on obligations toward the husband as well, including the rabbinic institution that her earnings belong to him. These ongoing mutual obligations are referred to as {\i tnai ketubah}, the stipulations of the {\i ketubah} \u8212- not \u8220"conditions\u8221" in the technical sense, but rather the ongoing terms that govern the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ketubah} itself, then, has three major components. The first is this body of ongoing reciprocal obligations. The second is the lump-sum payment \u8212- 200 {\i zuz} for a virgin bride, 100 {\i zuz} for a widow or divorcee \u8212- which would be collected by the wife either upon her husband\u8217's death or upon divorce. Although the Torah does not provide for a wife to inherit her husband, the rabbis instituted that she may draw this sum from the estate. The third component is the documentation of whatever property, the {\i nedunyah}, the bride brings into the marriage as a dowry, which she is entitled to take back upon death or divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These three elements together constitute the subject matter of {\i Masechet Ketubot}. Crucially, these obligations are legally binding whether or not any document was actually written. The reciprocal obligations exist; the lump-sum liability exists \u8212- the document records them, but they do not depend on it. In a deeper sense, {\i Ketubot} is a tractate about the legal texture of {\i nisuin} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask about the modern {\i ketubah}, in which the dowry property is not specifically enumerated. The phrase {\i bein b\u8217'chesef bein b\u8217'zahav bein b\u8217'tachshitin} functions as a pro forma statement encompassing all property brought into the marriage at a stated valuation, without itemizing it in detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should also be noted that the ordering of the tractates may seem counterintuitive. {\i Kiddushin} \u8212- the legal act of betrothal \u8212- comes later in the order of {\i Seder Nashim}, and {\i Gittin} precedes it, simply because {\i masechtot} are ordered by length as measured in chapters. {\i Ketubot}, as the longest, comes first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Broader Themes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond these central themes, {\i Ketubot} earns its reputation as {\i Shas Katan} through the many broader halakhic discussions it occasions. One major area is the law of {\i kam lei b\u8217'derabba minei}: since part of the {\i ketubah} payment derives historically from the fine imposed upon a man who rapes or seduces an unmarried woman \u8212- a fine calibrated to the {\i ketubah} sum she might otherwise have expected \u8212- the tractate engages extensively with the cases of rape and seduction from the Torah\u8217's relevant {\i parshiyot}. This in turn generates discussions of when a more severe transgression incurred through a given act displaces a financial obligation, which is the principle of {\i kam lei b\u8217'derabba minei}. Another broad {\i Shas} theme introduced by this tractate concerns {\i edim}: questions of when witnesses can retract testimony on a signed document, whether because they were compelled to sign, were minors at the time, or for other reasons. Since {\i Ketubot} deals centrally with a signed legal document, such questions arise naturally \u8212- and their implications extend far beyond the {\i ketubah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Student Q: Dowry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As noted above, the listing of dowry property in the modern {\i ketubah} takes a pro forma form: the text encompasses all property brought by the bride without specific enumeration, ascribing it a stated value. The detailed itemization that characterized the classical {\i ketubah} is no longer the practice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wedding Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i masechta} opens with the {\i mishnah}: {\i betulah niset l\u8217'yom ha-revi\u8217'i} \u8212- a virgin would have her {\i nisuin} on Wednesday. {\i V\u8217'almanah l\u8217'yom hachamishi} \u8212- a widow or divorcee on Thursday. The {\i mishnah} then explains: {\i shepaamayim b\u8217'shabbat batei dinin yoshvin ba\u8217'ayarot} \u8212- because the courts would convene in the villages twice each week, on Monday and Thursday. Therefore, {\i she\u8217'im haya lo ta\u8217'anat betulim haya mashkim l\u8217'veit din} \u8212- if the groom had a {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}, a claim regarding his bride\u8217's virginity, he could appear in court the very next morning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth being precise about what is meant here. The {\i nisuin} referred to is {\i chuppah}, not {\i kiddushin}. The {\i kiddushin} could have taken place at any time \u8212- even years earlier, even when the bride was a minor. The {\i chuppah}, the communal {\i simcha} with the {\i seudah}, the {\i sheva brachot} before a {\i minyan}: that is the event the {\i mishnah} is discussing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ta\u8217'anat Betulim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ta\u8217'anat betulim} \u8212- the claim that one\u8217's bride was not a virgin \u8212- carries significant halakhic weight and will dominate the entire first {\i perek}. Two distinct consequences flow from it. The first is financial: the {\i ketubah} of a {\i betulah} is 200 {\i zuz}, while that of a non-virgin is 100 {\i zuz}, and the groom\u8217's claim that she was not a virgin therefore affects his payment obligations. The second and weightier consequence is the suspicion of adultery. Since {\i kiddushin} may have taken place years before {\i nisuin}, a bride who is found not to be a virgin on her wedding night raises the concern that she may have been with another man during the intervening period \u8212- that is, after the legal marriage of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- which would constitute adultery and render her forbidden to her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wednesday Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of the {\i mishnah} is therefore clear. By scheduling the {\i nisuin} on Wednesday, the groom who discovers grounds for a {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} on that night can appear before {\i beit din} the very next morning, Thursday, when the court is in session. The underlying concern is that if he waits, he may calm down, reconcile himself to the situation, and ultimately decide not to pursue the matter \u8212- while the halakhic problem, if she did in fact commit adultery, would remain unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Sunday?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An obvious question presents itself immediately: why not have the {\i nisuin} on Sunday, allowing the groom to appear before {\i beit din} on Monday, when the court also convenes? This question is one the {\i gemara} will address, and it fundamentally changes the nature of the explanation offered by the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} might be understood as describing a common social practice that developed organically \u8212- men found it convenient to marry on Wednesdays so that the courts would be available the next day. Alternatively, as the {\i gemara} will develop, it may represent a {\i takanat chachamim}, a formal rabbinic enactment requiring that {\i nisuin} take place on Wednesday. This distinction, seemingly technical, carries significant practical consequences, as will become clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A major debate between Rashi and {\i Tosafot} concerns the mechanism by which {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} operates. Rashi, in the very first line of his commentary, explains: {\i im hayu ta\u8217'anat betulim yashiv l\u8217'veit din b\u8217'od ka\u8217'aso alav} \u8212- while he is still angry; {\i shema yitpayes v\u8217'titkarer da\u8217'ato v\u8217'yikayemnah} \u8212- lest he cool down and they remain married; {\i v\u8217'shema hiznah tachtav v\u8217'ne\u8217'esra alav} \u8212- and perhaps she committed adultery and is forbidden to him; and therefore {\i mitoch she-yavo l\u8217'veit din yitbarer ha-davar kshe-yeitze ha-kol shemma yavo\u8217'u edim} \u8212- by bringing the claim before the court, word will spread and witnesses may come forward to clarify the matter. For Rashi, then, the groom\u8217's claim alone does not determine anything; it is the publicity generated by the court proceeding that may produce witnesses and objective clarification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} disagrees. Based on the {\i gemara}, {\i Tosafot} understands that the groom\u8217's claim alone already carries consequences, through the principle of {\i shavyeh anafsheh chaticha d\u8217'issura}: by claiming that his wife was not a virgin \u8212- and thereby implying that she may have committed adultery \u8212- the groom renders her forbidden to himself by the force of his own declaration. He is bound by his own statement. This is a principle familiar from the laws of {\i kashrut}, here applied \u8212- as one might well note, rather strikingly \u8212- to a human being.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet according to {\i Tosafot}\u8217's position, a difficulty arises. If the groom\u8217's declaration alone already creates the halakhic consequence, why is there a rabbinic interest in ensuring that he brings the matter to {\i beit din}? The objective clarification of witnesses, which was Rashi\u8217's answer, is not {\i Tosafot}\u8217's mechanism \u8212- and {\i Tosafot} does not appear to directly address this difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a separate question regarding why this situation does not generate a {\i sotah} proceeding, given the operative concern about adultery. The {\i sotah} process requires prior warning and testimony of seclusion. {\i Shavyeh anafsheh chaticha d\u8217'issura} is a different mechanism \u8212- it is the personal binding force of one\u8217's own claim, not an objective establishment of fact \u8212- and the two operate on distinct grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite their disagreement on mechanism, both Rashi and {\i Tosafot} agree, based on the {\i gemara}, that the point is not merely to describe a communal practice but to ensure that the claim reaches the court rather than being abandoned.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mezonot Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Higia Zman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a chain of tradition: {\i amar Rav Yosef amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel} \u8212- Rav Yosef transmitting in the name of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel. The question posed is not why the {\i mishnah} ordained Wednesday \u8212- {\i mipnei ma amru}, as the phrase is used here, means something more like: what is the practical significance of this teaching? Why do we care? And the answer offered is: {\i lefi sheshaninu}, because of what we taught in a later {\i mishnah}: {\i higia zman v\u8217'lo nisu} \u8212- the designated wedding date arrived and they did not marry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This later {\i mishnah} requires a word of explanation. The \u8220"year\u8221" referenced here is not a year from {\i kiddushin} \u8212- which might have taken place decades earlier. Rather, from the moment the groom declares his intention to proceed with {\i nisuin}, the bride is granted one year to prepare \u8212- to assemble her dowry, make her arrangements, and ready herself mentally, financially, and in all other respects. The wedding date is thus a year from that declaration of intent, not from the original {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The later {\i mishnah} rules: {\i ocheles mishelo v\u8217'ocheles b\u8217'trumah} \u8212- once that date arrives, even if the {\i chuppah} has not yet taken place, the husband must begin providing food and support for his bride. His marital obligations commence on the appointed date. Furthermore, if he is a {\i kohen}, she may even eat his {\i terumah}, despite the fact that ordinarily eating {\i terumah} requires full {\i nisuin} rather than merely {\i kiddushin}. The rabbis suspended that requirement because by this date she ought already to have been {\i nesu\u8217'ah}. It is in any case practically convenient: if he is obligated to feed her, it is simpler if the food may consist of {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sunday Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical question that Rav Yosef is raising is this: suppose the year expires on a Sunday. Is the groom now obligated to pay {\i mezonot} from Sunday onward \u8212- even though Wednesday has not yet arrived? One might think so, since a year has passed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wednesday Date\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is no. {\i L\u8217'kach shaninu besulah niset l\u8217'yom ha-revi\u8217'i} \u8212- that is precisely why the {\i mishnah} taught that virgins marry on Wednesday. The wedding date was never simply \u8220"one year from today.\u8221" The wedding date is the first Wednesday that falls a year after the declaration of intent. If the year expires on a Sunday, the groom has no obligation to pay {\i mezonot} for those intervening days \u8212- Sunday through Tuesday \u8212- because the actual wedding date had always been Wednesday, not Sunday. The three days are not a delay; they were never part of the obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mezonot Application (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dependency Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef, however, is troubled by the formulation he received from his teachers. {\i Mari d\u8217'Avraham} \u8212- God of Avraham! \u8212- he exclaims: {\i tali tanya bidla tanya?} You are making a {\i mishnah} that provides its own explicit reason dependent upon a different {\i mishnah} that gives no reason? The {\i gemara} challenges this immediately: {\i hai tanya v\u8217'hai tanya} \u8212- both are indeed {\i mishnayot}. To which Rav Yosef refines the objection: {\i ella tali tanya demefaresh ta\u8217'ama b\u8217'tanya d\u8217'lo mefaresh ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- you are making the {\i mishnah} whose reason is stated explicitly dependent, in terms of its practical import, upon a {\i mishnah} that states no reason. The explicit stated reason of the first {\i mishnah} is so that the groom can come to {\i beit din} the next morning. If you wish to derive an additional halakhic implication from it regarding {\i mezonot}, that is fine \u8212- but you cannot say that this implication is the point of teaching the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Wednesday\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef\u8217's objection, then, is not merely formal but conceptual. The {\i mishnah} about {\i nisuin} on Wednesday states its own rationale: the groom should be able to appear in {\i beit din} on Thursday. To then say that the deeper reason for teaching this {\i mishnah} is to clarify the {\i mezonot} obligation \u8212- which depends on a second {\i mishnah} that offers no stated rationale \u8212- is to misread the teaching. Rav Yosef is not denying that both are {\i mishnayot}, but insisting that a {\i mishnah} with an explicit rationale should not be read as serving the purposes of a {\i mishnah} without one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemarah Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ella ei itmar hachi itmar} \u8212- if the tradition was indeed transmitted, here is how it should have been formulated. The chain of transmission ({\i Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel}) should have framed the question differently: {\i mipnei mah amru besulah niset l\u8217'yom ha-revi\u8217'i she\u8217'im haya lo ta\u8217'anat betulim haya mashkim l\u8217'veit din} \u8212- why did they ordain that a virgin marries on Wednesday? So that if the groom had a {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} he could appear in {\i beit din} the next morning. This formulation cleanly raises the purpose question without subordinating it to the {\i mezonot} {\i mishnah}. And immediately the obvious challenge follows: {\i v\u8217'tinasai b\u8217'echad b\u8217'shabbat she\u8217'im haya lo ta\u8217'anat betulim haya mashkim l\u8217'veit din} \u8212- let her marry on Sunday, so he could appear in {\i beit din} on Monday?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanat Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer the {\i gemara} provides is entirely different from what one might have expected: {\i shakdu chachamim al takanas bnot Yisrael sheyehe tore\u8217'ach b\u8217'seudah shlosha yamim} \u8212- the rabbis were solicitous of the wellbeing of Jewish women, and they instituted that the groom should exert himself in preparing the {\i seudah} for three days. The {\i takanat chachamim} was designed not primarily around the question of {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} and court access, but around ensuring adequate preparation for the wedding feast.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Day Preparation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific logic is this: the bride had a full year to prepare, while men, proverbially, tend to address such things at the last moment. If the {\i nisuin} were scheduled for Sunday, the groom might begin his preparations only from {\i Motzaei Shabbat}. By requiring Wednesday, the rabbis ensured that the groom would spend Sunday, Monday, and Tuesday \u8212- three full days \u8212- in active preparation for the {\i seudah}, and only on Wednesday would the {\i chuppah} take place. The groom\u8217's family is, of course, responsible for the wedding celebration, and the rabbis wished to ensure a genuinely festive and properly prepared event.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Demand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implicit consequence of this reframing is significant. The {\i gemara} has now treated the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling not as a description of communal practice but as a rabbinic enactment \u8212- a {\i takanah} \u8212- that the wedding must take place on Wednesday. If it is a mere practice, then one might ask: what halakhic force does it carry, and what practical difference does it make? But if it is a {\i takanat chachamim} that the wedding shall not be held before Wednesday, then from the outset the wedding date was always the first Wednesday following the year of preparation. Sunday was never the date. The date was never postponed; it was always Wednesday. This is what allows the {\i gemara} to rule, in the context of the {\i mezonot} obligation, that the groom does not owe payment for the days between Sunday and Wednesday: those days were not a delay at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ones Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blame vs.\u160 ?Date\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now draws a broader principle from this framework. Having established that when it is a {\i takanat chachamim} that prevents the wedding from occurring on an earlier day, the groom bears no {\i mezonot} obligation for those days \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks whether the same reasoning extends to other situations where the wedding is postponed through no one\u8217's fault. The underlying question is: does the obligation to pay {\i mezonot} commence automatically on the wedding date, regardless of why it was not observed, or does it only engage when there is someone to blame for the delay?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom Illness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lefikach chala hu} \u8212- therefore, if the groom became ill and the wedding was postponed, does he pay {\i mezonot} from the appointed date or not? One view within the {\i gemara} frames it as a question of {\i ones}: {\i hatam ta\u8217'ama mai mishum d\u8217'ones} \u8212- the reason he did not owe for the Sunday-to-Wednesday period was that it was beyond his control, a product of the rabbinic institution. {\i Hacha nami ones} \u8212- here too it is beyond his control; therefore he should not pay. Alternatively: {\i hatam ones b\u8217'takantah d\u8217'takinu leh rabanan} \u8212- there, the reason it was beyond his control was built into the rabbinic institution itself, such that the date was never Sunday to begin with. Here, by contrast, the date actually arrived \u8212- it was Wednesday, and the wedding did not happen. Perhaps once the actual date arrives, the obligation begins regardless of why it was not fulfilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is sharp. When the rabbis say Wednesday, the pre-Wednesday period was never the wedding date. But when illness prevents a Wednesday wedding, the Wednesday date did arrive and was missed. These are halakhically different situations, and the question of whether {\i ones} constitutes an exemption in the latter case remains open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bride Illness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The uncertainty cascades further. {\i V\u8217'im timtzi lomar chala hu ma\u8217'aleh lah mezonot} \u8212- if you rule that when the groom falls ill he does pay {\i mezonot}, then {\i chaltsah hi mah} \u8212- what if she is the one who falls ill? Here the groom might argue: {\i ana kaina} \u8212- I am ready to marry; it is you who cannot proceed. Should he then be exempt from payment since the delay originates with her? Or does she respond: {\i nistachfah sadehu} \u8212- it is your field that flooded? The underlying logic of this latter phrase, as {\i Tosafot} on 2b explains, reflects a view that within the patriarchal framework of the marriage relationship, misfortune that befalls the marriage is seen as the husband\u8217's misfortune \u8212- he is the one bringing a wife into his domain, and if that is disrupted, the loss is categorized as his. Rashi on 2a suggests a parallel formulation: {\i mazlacha garam ki mehayom ani mutal alecha lazon} \u8212- your fortune has brought it about that from this day I am your responsibility to sustain. One might read this either as the wife\u8217's argument that luck cuts against him, or more simply as the position that once the date arrives, the obligation of support begins regardless of fault \u8212- as long as the postponement was not willful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third scenario: {\i pirsah niddah} \u8212- what if the bride becomes a {\i niddah} on the wedding day? There are well-known differences between {\i Sephardim} and {\i Ashkenazim} as to whether a {\i chuppah} is legally valid when the bride is a {\i niddah}, since cohabitation would not be possible immediately. But regardless of the legal question, in practice no one wished to hold a {\i chuppah} when the couple could not begin their married life together. The question is therefore whether the {\i niddah} situation creates an exemption from {\i mezonot} for the groom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Achai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Achai attempts to resolve these open questions from the language of the later {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} reads {\i higia zman v\u8217'lo nisu} \u8212- the time came and they were not married. Critically, the verb is {\i nistu}, passive: the women were not married by the men \u8212- not {\i nasu}, active, meaning the men did not marry. Rav Achai infers from this: {\i lo nasu lo katani, ella lo nistu} \u8212- it does not say the men did not marry; it says the women were not married. This implies that the women were somehow involved in the reason for the non-marriage \u8212- yet the {\i mishnah} still rules that he pays. Were it a case of the women willfully refusing \u8212- {\i d\u8217'kamakvan inu hi} \u8212- it would be obvious he should not pay. So it must be speaking of a case where the women were the involuntary cause of the delay \u8212- illness, or perhaps {\i niddah} \u8212- and yet the {\i mishnah} rules he pays. Rav Achai concludes that whenever the delay is involuntary on the woman\u8217's side, the man pays.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi rejects this reading. {\i L\u8217'olam eima lach, kol ones lo achla} \u8212- any situation of {\i ones}, anything beyond anyone\u8217's control, exempts the man from payment. The man pays only when he willfully delays. {\i V\u8217'd\u8217'kamaachvi inhu} \u8212- the triggering case for payment is the man deliberately postponing the wedding. Grammatically, Rav Ashi notes, the {\i mishnah} should indeed have said {\i lo nasu} \u8212- the men did not marry; it uses the passive form only because the beginning of that {\i mishnah} speaks from the woman\u8217's perspective, and the end follows suit stylistically. It is not a substantive implication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus leaves us with two fundamental positions. One holds that the obligation begins automatically on the wedding date \u8212- as long as the woman is not herself willfully postponing, the man must pay from that date regardless of circumstances. The other holds that the man pays only when he is personally and willfully responsible for the delay; any genuine {\i ones}, on either side, exempts him from payment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prenup Digression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a question that illuminates a dimension of this entire discussion: why did the rabbis stipulate that the {\i chuppah} must be held on Wednesday specifically during the day, rather than on Wednesday night \u8212- which is halakhically already Thursday? {\i Tosafot} answers that in Talmudic times there were frequently significant disputes surrounding the terms of the {\i ketubah}, and beginning the {\i chuppah} early on Wednesday allowed time for those disputes to be negotiated and resolved before the event proceeded. When the {\i ketubah} was a genuine financial document with real stakes \u8212- the specification of dowry property, the exact amount of the {\i ikar ketubah}, the various conditions \u8212- there was ample room for disagreement between the families, and the rabbis wished to ensure that such disputes did not derail the wedding at the last moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prenup Anecdote\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle resonates powerfully in contemporary halakhic practice. A prenuptial agreement is today\u8217's functional analogue to those {\i ketubah} negotiations. The dangers of leaving such discussions to the last moment are very real. There are cases where a groom, coming from a family that had experienced a particularly acrimonious divorce, arrived at his own wedding refusing to sign the prenuptial agreement \u8212- raising objections that could and should have been addressed weeks earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prenup Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical lesson is clear: a prenuptial agreement should be signed and notarized no later than two weeks before the wedding. The signing can certainly be acknowledged and celebrated at the {\i tish} if one wishes, but the document must not be left to the night of the wedding itself. Just as the Talmudic rabbis ensured that disputes over the {\i ketubah} would not disrupt the {\i chuppah} by building in lead time, so too contemporary practice should ensure that all such documents are fully in order well before the event.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ones in Gittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Ones Gittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now introduces a striking contrast. {\i Ul\u8217'inyan gittin eino kein} \u8212- regarding {\i gittin}, this principle of {\i ones} does not apply in the same way. {\i Alma kasavar Rava ein ones b\u8217'gittin} \u8212- Rava holds that the fact that something occurred beyond one\u8217's control is not a relevant factor in the laws of {\i gittin}. Where we have just seen that {\i ones} can be a factor in the {\i mezonot} discussion \u8212- according to Rav Ashi\u8217's position, any {\i ones} exempts the man from payment \u8212- in the realm of {\i gittin}, {\i ones} carries no weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Gittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for Rava\u8217's principle is sought in a {\i mishnah} in {\i Gittin}: a man says to his wife, {\i harei zeh gittech im lo basi mikan ad shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- here is your {\i get}; if I do not return from here within twelve months, the {\i get} takes effect. The scenario is familiar: the man is traveling overseas, perhaps to war or distant business, and fears he may not return. To protect his wife from becoming an {\i agunah} \u8212- permanently bound and unable to remarry \u8212- he gives a conditional {\i get}. {\i U\u8217'meis betoch shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- and he died within those twelve months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the end of twelve months, the condition appears to have been met: he did not return. Is this a valid {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Get Achar Misah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i eino get} \u8212- it is not a valid {\i get}. The reason Rashi articulates is {\i ein hameisim megarshin} \u8212- the dead cannot divorce; a {\i get} cannot take effect {\i l\u8217'achar misah}, after death. The {\i kiddushin} has in any event been dissolved by death, and one might ask why Rashi finds it necessary to invoke this principle rather than simply noting that widowhood has already dissolved the marriage. The {\i yeshiva} world enjoys posing this question, though in practice the principle of {\i ein get l\u8217'achar misah} stands on its own terms. The condition was technically met \u8212- he did not return \u8212- but the {\i get} fails because one cannot divorce a wife posthumously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones Factor?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now probes a deeper question. {\i Meiso de\u8217'eino get} \u8212- death prevents the {\i get}; that much is clear. But what if {\i chala} \u8212- what if he fell ill and was unable to return? In that case, the twelve months would also have passed without his return. Does the fact that his failure to return was due to {\i ones} \u8212- circumstances entirely beyond his control \u8212- mean that the condition is treated as if it were not met? That is: perhaps when he was prevented by force of circumstance, it is halakhically \u8220"as if he returned,\u8221" and therefore the condition was not fulfilled and the {\i get} is not valid. Or is {\i ones} entirely irrelevant \u8212- the condition was not met regardless of why, and the {\i get} is therefore valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i ones} is not a factor, then the {\i mishnah} teaches us only that death prevents the {\i get} via the principle of {\i ein get l\u8217'achar misah} \u8212- and nothing can be inferred about what would happen if he merely fell ill.\u160 ?The {\i get} in the illness case might well be valid. If {\i ones} is a factor, then even in the illness case the condition would be treated as unmet, and the {\i get} would be invalid \u8212- but then the {\i mishnah} about death is simply one application of the broader rule that {\i ones} negates condition-fulfillment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Social Policy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} anticipates where this leads. If {\i ones} were a relevant factor in determining whether a condition was met, the consequences for practical halakha would be severe: we could never know with confidence whether conditions in {\i gittin} had been fulfilled or not, because we would always need to investigate whether any circumstance beyond the party\u8217's control had intervened. Such a regime would be unworkable. This social policy concern \u8212- that one cannot run a halakhic system if conditions can always be retroactively analyzed for possible {\i ones} \u8212- is precisely what Rava\u8217's principle is designed to prevent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemarah Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is that the {\i mishnah} teaches one thing and one thing only: a {\i get} does not take effect after the groom\u8217's death, by virtue of the principle of {\i ein get l\u8217'achar misah}. The {\i mishnah} is not making any statement about the illness case, and nothing can be inferred from it regarding whether {\i ones} would affect the determination of condition-fulfillment. That question \u8212- whether {\i ones} serves to treat a condition as unmet \u8212- remains a separate discussion, and the resolution of Rava\u8217's principle {\i ein ones b\u8217'gittin} must be derived from elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ones BeGittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones Concept Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on the bottom of 3b takes up the question of {\i yesh ones begitin} \u8212- whether the claim of {\i ones}, an act of God or circumstance beyond one\u8217's control, is a relevant factor when a {\i get} has been given conditionally. If someone stipulates that a {\i get} should take effect under certain conditions, and those conditions are met only because of something entirely outside the husband\u8217's control \u8212- he became ill, was detained, or died \u8212- do we look at the situation as though the condition was not truly met in the sense he intended? The {\i gemara}\u8217's first position is {\i ein ones begitin}: we do not factor that in. As was already noted, if we were to factor it in, things would become very complicated, since one could never know with certainty whether the conditions had truly been met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get After Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to prove this from a {\i mishna} dealing with a case where a man says \u8220"This is your {\i get} if I die.\u8221" This would seem to be an example of {\i ones}, and the question is whether we can infer something about what happens if he said \u8220"This will be your {\i get} if I don\u8217't return,\u8221" and he then died \u8212- the condition being met only because of {\i ones}. However, the {\i gemara} concludes that the real point of that {\i mishna} is not to address the question of {\i ones} at all, but rather to establish the principle of {\i ein get achar misa} \u8212- that a {\i get} cannot take effect after a person has already died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} itself states: {\i harei zeh gitich im mati} \u8212- \u8220"This is your {\i get} if I die\u8221"; {\i harei zeh gitich mecholi zeh} \u8212- \u8220"This is your {\i get} if I don\u8217't recover from this illness\u8221"; {\i harei zeh gitich achar misa} \u8212- \u8220"This is your {\i get} after I die\u8221" \u8212- in all such cases, {\i lo amar klum}, it amounts to nothing. In each of these formulations, the husband is not merely imposing a stipulation; he is specifying the moment the {\i get} takes effect \u8212- when he dies, when he never rises from his sickbed. Since a {\i get} cannot take effect once the husband is dead, all these formulations fail. We thus already know the principle of {\i ein get achar misa} from the opening of that {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dating Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the principle of {\i ein get achar misa} is already established by the beginning of the {\i mishna}, why does the {\i gemara} need to teach it again through the case of one who says \u8220"If I don\u8217't return in twelve months\u8221"? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i dilma l\u8217'apokei merabboseinu} \u8212- perhaps the second teaching is necessary to exclude the position of {\i Rabboseinu}. As a {\i braita} teaches, {\i Rabboseinu hitiru lenasei} \u8212- those authorities permitted the woman to remarry even when the husband said \u8220"This is your {\i get} after I die.\u8221" How could they permit this given {\i ein get achar misa}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: who is this {\i Rabboseinu}? The answer is {\i Bei Dina Desharu Mishcha} \u8212- the {\i beis din} that permitted non-Jewish oil. This is the {\i beis din} of {\i Rabbi Yossi}, or more precisely, a {\i beis din} that held like {\i Rabbi Yossi}, and they have become identified by their most famous leniency. Their position follows from the principle {\i zmano shel shetar mochiach alav} \u8212- that the date written in a legal document demonstrates something. If a {\i get} is dated from the day it was written, even when it contains a condition that it take effect upon death, the date in the document signals that it retroactively takes effect from the time of writing. According to {\i Rabbi Yossi}, then, there could in principle be a {\i get achar misa} as long as the document is dated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely why the {\i mishna} must teach the principle of {\i ein get achar misa} twice: once as a general rule, and once to make clear that this holds even when the document bears a date \u8212- to reject {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's view. The case in the {\i mishna} of one who says \u8220"If I don\u8217't return in twelve months\u8221" and then dies is therefore not relevant to the question of {\i ones} at all. Its purpose is solely to reiterate {\i ein get achar misa} and to specify that a date in the document does not change this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Get Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the earlier sources do not speak to the question of {\i ones}, the {\i gemara} turns to the end of that {\i mishna}. The case is: {\i me\u8217'achshav im lo basi mikan v\u8217'ad shenem asar chodesh} \u8212- if a man says explicitly that the {\i get} should take effect as of now if he does not return within twelve months. One can readily understand why a husband would phrase it this way \u8212- so that his wife would not be left as an {\i agunah} if he fails to return and she has no way of knowing what happened to him. {\i Umes b\u8217'soch shenem asar chodesh} \u8212- and he dies within the twelve months. The condition has been met: he did not return within the year. It is not a case of {\i get achar misa} because he explicitly said \u8220"from now.\u8221" The {\i gemara} states: {\i harei zeh get} \u8212- it is a valid {\i get}. The fact that the condition was met only because of {\i ones}, because he died and could not control his return, is immaterial. {\i Ones} is not a factor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dying vs.\u160 ?Sickness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a qualification. {\i Mes v\u8217'hu hadin l\u8217'chola} \u8212- one might assume that the same would apply whether he died or merely became ill.\u160 ?But the {\i gemara} resists this inference. {\i Dilma mes davka} \u8212- perhaps dying is categorically different. {\i D\u8217'lo nicha lei d\u8217'tipal kamei yavam} \u8212- one of the main reasons a man might give his wife a conditional {\i get} in the first place, especially if he fears he might die, is so that she will not be subject to {\i yibum}, which was the entire subject of the previous tractate. If a man anticipates the possibility of death, that is perhaps precisely the kind of {\i ones} he had in mind when he formulated the condition. Death may well have been the very scenario he was thinking about. Getting sick, by contrast, is something he may never have anticipated \u8212- it represents a situation he would have wished he could have negated by returning, and therefore perhaps it should not be held against him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the real conceptual heart of the issue. The question of {\i ones} in {\i gittin} is not primarily about blame or legal culpability in the usual sense. The governing principle of {\i ones rachmana patrei} \u8212- that the Torah does not hold a person liable for what is beyond their control \u8212- does not translate neatly here, since the issue is not one of fault but of whether the conditions stipulated were truly met. Nor does that principle distinguish between dying and becoming ill, since both are objectively outside one\u8217's control. The deeper question, as the {\i gemara} frames it, is really one of {\i da\u8217'as}: under what circumstances did the husband actually intend the {\i get} to take effect? The premise is that the husband\u8217's intention governs, and {\i ones} matters \u8212- or doesn\u8217't \u8212- depending on whether the particular event was one he would have included in his intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Of course, the difficulty is that one cannot run a legal system trying to reconstruct what was going on inside someone\u8217's mind. The general principle is {\i devarim shebalev einam devarim} \u8212- what is in one\u8217's heart is not legally operative. There is, however, an important nuance: the {\i Rambam} and the {\i Tosafos Rid} explain that {\i devarim shebalev einam devarim} applies with full force primarily in bilateral transactions \u8212- a sale, for instance, where money and goods change hands and only what is accessible to both parties can be legally relevant. But in a unilateral transaction \u8212- a gift, or a {\i get}, where the other party has no real say \u8212- what is in the giver\u8217's mind may carry more legal weight. So the question the {\i gemara} is working through is not a categorical principle about {\i ones}, but a question about intent: did I intend the {\i get} to take effect in these circumstances?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 River Case Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 River Blockage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the conceptual framework, the {\i gemara} attempts to derive a clear ruling from a concrete case. {\i De-hu de-amar le-hu} \u8212- a man said to witnesses: {\i i lo asina mi-kan ve-ad tlasin yomin, le-havei gitta} \u8212- if I don\u8217't return within thirty days, let it be a {\i get}. {\i Asa be-sof tlasin yomin} \u8212- he came at the end of thirty days, {\i u-paska mabra} \u8212- but a river blocked his path. He could not cross in time. {\i Amar le-hu chazu de-asai chazu de-asai} \u8212- he called out across the river: \u8220"Look, I am coming, I am coming!\u8221" His {\i ratzon} was evident: he wanted to negate the condition of the {\i get}. But he could not cross in time. {\i Amar Shmuel: lav shemei masia} \u8212- it is very well that he announced his arrival, says {\i Shmuel}, but he did not come. And therefore the {\i get} takes effect. {\i Ones} is irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anticipated Ones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does not accept this as a conclusive proof, however. {\i Ve-dilma unsa de-shiyach shani} \u8212- perhaps a common type of {\i ones} is categorically different. Perhaps when the {\i ones} is something foreseeable, it simply cannot operate to negate the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foreseeability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De-keivan de-ibayei lei le-asnu-ei ve-lo asni} \u8212- since he could have stipulated otherwise and did not, he is responsible for the gap. He should have said: this will be a {\i get} if I do not return, unless I am held up by circumstances beyond my control. A river blocking one\u8217's path is a perfectly ordinary and foreseeable occurrence. Since he did not exclude it, we assume he intended the {\i get} to take effect regardless. {\i Ihu de-afsidei nafshiyei} \u8212- he has brought this upon himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is again consistent with the {\i da\u8217'as}-centered reading of the issue. Something foreseeable is assumed to have been included in the husband\u8217's intent even if unstated, because any reasonable person would have thought of it. The remaining question is what happens with a truly extraordinary {\i ones} \u8212- something no normal person would ever anticipate, like being struck by lightning. Is that also assumed to be within the husband\u8217's intent, or do we say it was so far outside normal expectation that it genuinely was never part of what he envisioned? The {\i gemara} cannot resolve this from the river case alone, and concedes there is no clear proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Raba\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raba\u8217's Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather than simply articulating {\i ein ones begitin} as a legal principle \u8212- which one might derive straightforwardly from {\i devarim shebalev einam devarim} \u8212- {\i Rava} formulates his position as a policy ruling, based on {\i sevara d\u8217'nafshei}, his own independent reasoning. He has no definitive source to cite. But the {\i gemara} then makes a critical move: it argues that the policy itself demands this position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Policy Problems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i mishum tznuos u-mishum prutzos} \u8212- because of women who are overly cautious and because of women who are overly ready. If we were to say that {\i ones} matters and can negate the {\i get}, we would create an impossible social situation. On the one hand, {\i mishum tznuos} \u8212- there will be times when there is no {\i ones} at all, the condition was met straightforwardly, and yet the woman will worry: perhaps there was some act of God I have no knowledge of, perhaps he was struck by lightning, perhaps something beyond his control occurred. {\i U-me\u8217'aguna v\u8217'yasva} \u8212- she will sit and remain an {\i agunah} when she has every right to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other hand, {\i mishum prutzos} \u8212- there will be women too eager to move on. Even when there is genuine reason to believe there was an {\i ones}, {\i v\u8217'azla u-minsva} \u8212- she will say it was not an {\i ones} and proceed to remarry, {\i v\u8217'nimtza get batel u-vaneha mamzerim} \u8212- and it will turn out the {\i get} was never valid and her children will be {\i mamzerim}. You simply cannot run a society in which the validity of every {\i get} depends on an invisible and unknowable question of whether some unforeseeable act of God occurred in a distant place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafka\u8217'as Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses back sharply: are you saying that merely for policy reasons \u8212- {\i mishum tznuos u-mishum prutzos} \u8212- we would declare a married woman permitted to the world? {\i Sharinan eshes ish l\u8217'alma?} We would say it is always a valid {\i get}, ignoring {\i ones}, even when legally {\i ones} should have negated it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i in, kol d\u8217'mekadesh ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabanan mekadesh} \u8212- yes, because anyone who marries does so on the terms set by the rabbis. We do not live in a world of pure biblical Judaism alone; every legal act is performed within the framework that the rabbis have constructed and defined. {\i V\u8217'afkinhu rabanan l\u8217'kidushin minei} \u8212- and the rabbis can retroactively uproot the {\i kiddushin}. Under these circumstances, even though the {\i get} is technically defective because {\i ones} should have negated the condition, the rabbis retroactively declare the {\i kiddushin} void, so that the {\i get} \u8212- whatever its legal infirmity \u8212- is treated as effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is one of the classic {\i sugyos} of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}, the retroactive uprooting of marriage, and it has obvious resonance in contemporary discussions about the {\i agunah} problem. Two important points about how it operates here deserve emphasis. First, this is not a case-by-case intervention. It is a general systemic rule, operative from the very moment of marriage. The {\i kiddushin} was contracted with this rule already in place: {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabanan mekadesh} \u8212- the marriage was entered into on the rabbis\u8217' terms, which include the possibility of retroactive uprooting in defined circumstances. Second, even within this mechanism, a {\i get} is still required \u8212- even if defective. {\i Rashi} consistently emphasizes this point throughout the {\i sugya}: the rabbis uproot the {\i kiddushin} specifically {\i al yedei gittin she-hikshiru chachamim} \u8212- through {\i gittin} that the rabbis have validated in this manner. The {\i Rashba} elaborates on this principle, explaining that {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} is not a bold direct exercise of rabbinic power. It operates as a hidden mechanism behind the scenes. The {\i Rashba} calls what is required a {\i srach get} \u8212- a semblance of a {\i get}, what one might call a fig leaf. The {\i get} may not function on its own terms, but it provides the formal occasion for the hidden mechanism of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} to operate. We do not walk in and announce that we are nullifying the {\i kiddushin} directly; rather, the {\i get} stands upfront, and the {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} works silently behind it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same logic, the {\i Rashba} notes, operates in the {\i sugyos} of {\i Yevamos} involving {\i ed echad} \u8212- a single witness to the husband\u8217's death. There the \u8220"fig leaf\u8221" is not a {\i get} but the reliance on the single witness\u8217's testimony, however legally insufficient on its own. Behind that reliance, the {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} provides the ultimate mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A small but illuminating point from {\i Tosafot}: the practice of reciting {\i kedas Moshe ve-Yisrael} at the moment of {\i kiddushin}, though nowhere explicitly mandated in the {\i gemara}, encodes this principle. {\i Moshe} represents {\i Torah de-oraisa}; {\i Yisrael}, the Jewish people as represented by the rabbis, signals {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'rabanan mekadesh} \u8212- that the marriage is entered into on the rabbis\u8217' terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hafka\u8217'as Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fig Leaf Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then arises of exactly how the mechanism of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} operates in this case. The {\i get} here is technically defective \u8212- the condition was ostensibly not met in a legally valid sense, since {\i ones} arguably should have negated it. Yet the {\i get} stands as the fig leaf behind which {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} operates. {\i Rashi}\u8217's language throughout this {\i sugya} is precise: {\i afkinu rabbanan le-kiddushin kshe-yavo get kazeh achareihen} \u8212- the rabbis uproot the {\i kiddushin} when a {\i get} like this follows. The {\i get} need not be fully valid on its own; what it provides is the formal occasion for the rabbinic mechanism to operate. The {\i Rashba} reinforces this: direct, unadorned exercise of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} without any {\i get} would undermine the institutions of Jewish marriage altogether. The power exists, but it must operate through proper legal form, however attenuated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money vs.\u160 ?Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina} poses a pointed question to {\i Rav Ashi}: {\i teynach de-kadesh be-chaspa} \u8212- this works well enough if the {\i kiddushin} was performed with money. In that case, the mechanism of {\i hefker beis din hefker} \u8212- the court\u8217's power to declare property ownerless \u8212- explains how the rabbis can retroactively undo the monetary {\i kiddushin}. They declare the money that effected the {\i kiddushin} to have been ownerless from the start, thus voiding the transaction. But {\i kadesh be-via mai ika le-meimar} \u8212- what happens if the {\i kiddushin} was {\i kiddushei biah}, effected through marital relations? The physical reality of the sexual act has occurred. How can the rabbis change that? How can they retroactively declare it not to have been an act of {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redefining Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shavyuha rabbanan le-veilaso veilas zenus} \u8212- the rabbis can redefine the context. Not every act of sex is {\i kiddushin}. Most sexual relations between people have no legal status of {\i kiddushin} at all; they are {\i zenus}, non-marital relations. What makes a sexual act into {\i kiddushin} is the legal and social context in which it occurs \u8212- and the rabbis are the ones who determine what that context means. Retroactively, they declare that this particular act, which appeared to be marital, is to be regarded as {\i zenus} \u8212- legally unrecognized as a marriage-effecting act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a striking {\i chiddush}. We might be dealing with a couple who have lived together for years, raised children, participated in communal life as a married couple \u8212- and retroactively we say no, that was all {\i beilas zenus}. One might ask: if witnesses were present who explicitly heard the man declare his intent to effect {\i kiddushei biah}, does the rabbinic recharacterization override even their testimony? The answer would have to be yes \u8212- the rabbis\u8217' framework for what constitutes {\i kiddushin} supersedes even how the witnesses understood the act they observed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternate View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a famous alternative interpretation of this passage, cited by {\i Rashi} though firmly rejected by him. According to this reading \u8212- which {\i Rashi} notes is reflected in some {\i rishonim} \u8212- the reason {\i kiddushei kesef} is easier to retroactively undo is not {\i hefker beis din hefker}, but rather because {\i kiddushei kesef} is itself only {\i derabbanan}. On this view, the only form of {\i kiddushin} that is {\i de-oraisa} is {\i biah}. {\i Kiddushei kesef} and {\i kiddushei shtar} are rabbinic constructs, and the rabbis can therefore undo what they created. As for {\i kiddushei biah}, which would be {\i de-oraisa}, this reading says the rabbis never recognized it as valid {\i kiddushin} in the first place \u8212- it was always treated as {\i beilas zenus} from the outset, given that the {\i gemara} elsewhere records that lashes were administered to one who effected {\i kiddushin} through {\i biah} because it was {\i pritzus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication of this view would be that the only form of {\i kiddushin} truly beyond rabbinic power to undo is {\i kiddushei shtar}. {\i Kiddushei kesef} is {\i derabbanan} and can be controlled; {\i kiddushei biah} is never recognized. {\i Rashi}, however, is sharply critical of this reading. He calls it {\i ta\u8217'us gomur} \u8212- a complete error \u8212- and points out that accepting it would mean that a woman who underwent {\i kiddushei biah} could simply walk away without a {\i get} on the basis of this reasoning alone, and {\i lo matzinu zos batalmud} \u8212- we find no such ruling anywhere in the Talmud. {\i Rashi} firmly rejects the entire construct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Alternative Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a second version of {\i Rava}\u8217's position: {\i ikha de-amri, amar Rava ve-chein l\u8217'inyan gittin} \u8212- there are those who say that {\i Rava} actually ruled that {\i ones does} matter in practice, not merely in theory. On this reading, {\i Rava} held {\i yesh ones begitin} \u8212- we genuinely factor in {\i ones}, not just in principle but as a practical legal ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this position from the {\i mishna}. The case of {\i me\u8217'achshav im lo basi mikan v\u8217'ad shenem asar chodesh}, where the husband said \u8220"from now if I do not return in twelve months\u8221" and he died within the twelve months \u8212- the {\i mishna} rules {\i harei zeh get}, it is a valid {\i get}. If {\i ones} mattered and could negate the {\i get}, then the fact that he died \u8212- surely an {\i ones} \u8212- should have invalidated it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Anticipated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i lo, meis davka} \u8212- only dying is different. {\i D\u8217'lo nicha lei d\u8217'tipal kamei yavam} \u8212- death is not treated as a disqualifying {\i ones} because that may well have been precisely the contingency the husband had in mind when giving the {\i get}. He wanted the {\i get} to protect her from {\i yibum} in the event of his death; he intended the {\i get} to take effect in that scenario. Getting sick, however, is different \u8212- that may have been something he never anticipated and would never have intended to include within his condition. So on this version of {\i Rava}\u8217's position, {\i ones} does matter, but dying is not classified as an {\i ones} in this context because the husband can be presumed to have intended that very outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} revisits the river case as well \u8212- {\i Shmuel}\u8217's ruling that the man who was blocked by the river and called out \u8220"I am coming\u8221" has not fulfilled the condition \u8212- but notes that this too can be explained as a case of {\i unsa de-shchiach}, a foreseeable {\i ones} the husband should have anticipated and excluded explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} works through the competing positions at length and concludes that we must adopt {\i ain ones begitin} \u8212- the ruling that {\i ones} does not negate a conditional {\i get} \u8212- as the operative position. The societal stakes are simply too high. With {\i yesh ones begitin}, the {\i tznuos} will become unnecessarily chained and the {\i prutzos} will remarry in situations they should not, producing {\i mamzerim}. The conclusion is thus driven not by pure legal logic but by the existential requirements of communal stability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} does note, however, that even at the end of the matter, a residual question remains: there may be extreme cases of {\i ones} \u8212- something so extraordinary that no one could ever have anticipated it \u8212- where even those who rule {\i ain ones begitin} as a matter of policy might concede that the {\i ones} is operative. The {\i gemara} does not arrive at a fully clean resolution, and that ambiguity in the extreme case persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wednesday Wedding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Day Prep\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded the discussion of {\i ones}, the {\i gemara} returns to the basic premise of the {\i mishna} that opens the tractate: virgins marry on Wednesday, so that if the husband has a {\i ta\u8217'anas besulim} \u8212- a complaint that she was not a virgin \u8212- he can go to court on Thursday morning. The choice of Wednesday rather than, say, Sunday was deliberate: the groom needed three days to prepare the wedding feast, so Sunday, Monday, and Tuesday were preparation days, with Wednesday as the wedding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Schedule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Shmuel bar Yitzchak}: {\i lo shanu ela mi-takanas Ezra v\u8217'eilach} \u8212- this rule applies only from the {\i takana} of {\i Ezra} onward. {\i Ezra} instituted regular courts that convened specifically on Mondays and Thursdays. Since Thursday was the fixed court day, Wednesday was the optimal wedding night \u8212- the husband could appear in court the very next morning. But before {\i Ezra}\u8217's {\i takana}, when courts sat every day, {\i ishah nisas b\u8217'chol yom} \u8212- a woman could marry on any day, since no single day had any special relationship to the court schedule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a difficulty here: did {\i Ezra}\u8217's {\i takana} actually {\i reduce} the frequency of court sittings? Before {\i Ezra}, courts apparently sat daily; afterward, only on Monday and Thursday. {\i Tosafot} offers two resolutions. The first: before {\i Ezra}, courts sat daily but only in the cities; {\i Ezra} decreed that courts should sit less frequently but extend their reach to the villages as well. The second: before {\i Ezra}, courts were convened on an ad hoc basis whenever the need arose; {\i Ezra} instituted fixed, regular courts with set hours and set days, replacing the ad hoc system with a structured institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Variable Courts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} makes clear that the historical question about pre-{\i Ezra} practice is not the point. {\i Hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- what the {\i gemara} is actually saying is: {\i ei ika batei dinin di-kvu-in idna} \u8212- if today you had regular courts sitting every day, or conversely if today there were no fixed court schedule at all, then {\i k\u8217'kodem takanas Ezra} \u8212- the situation would resemble the pre-{\i Ezra} era, and {\i ishah nisas b\u8217'chol yom} \u8212- a woman could marry on any day. The connection between Wednesday and Thursday courts is entirely instrumental: it was never about the day itself but about ensuring the husband had recourse to the court immediately after the wedding night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes an apparent problem with this reasoning: what about Friday? Even if courts are in session on every other day, a {\i beis din} cannot convene on {\i Shabbat}. How could marrying on Friday ever be permitted? {\i Tosafot} responds with two points. First, in practice the formal procedure that would be required \u8212- producing witnesses, conducting a full trial \u8212- is not what is needed today. All that is required is registering a complaint, which can be done informally before three people even on {\i Shabbat}. Second, {\i Rashi}\u8217's understanding of the court\u8217's role in the original {\i takana} was about gathering witnesses for a potential capital prosecution, which is not at all the legal landscape of later generations. The concerns we now face regarding {\i ta\u8217'anas besulim} can be addressed without the same formal {\i beis din} structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: even if there is no fixed Thursday court, does the three-day preparation requirement not still apply? {\i Ha ba\u8217'inan shakdu} \u8212- don\u8217't we still need the rabbis\u8217' conscientious provision that the groom spend three days preparing? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i tarach lei} \u8212- he can get married on any day, provided he has spent three days preparing. The {\i takana} imposes a minimum investment in wedding preparation, not a fixed calendar day. The day is entirely instrumental; the preparation is the substance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takana Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shakdu Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai shakdu} \u8212- where does this concept of {\i shakdu} \u8212- the rabbis\u8217' solicitude \u8212- come from? A {\i braita} provides the answer. {\i Mipnei ma amru besula nises l\u8217'yom ha-revi\u8217'i?} \u8212- Why did the rabbis say that a virgin marries on the fourth day of the week? {\i She\u8217'im haya lo ta\u8217'anas besulim, haya mashkim l\u8217'beis din} \u8212- so that if the husband has a complaint about her virginity, he can go to court first thing the next morning. And why not Sunday? {\i Shakdu chachamim al takanas bnos Yisrael} \u8212- the rabbis were conscientious, they took special care for the welfare of Jewish women, ensuring that the groom would invest in the wedding: {\i she-yehei torech b\u8217'seuda shlosha yamim} \u8212- that he should busy himself with preparing the feast for three days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Day Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three-day pattern that results is: {\i echad b\u8217'shabbos, sheni b\u8217'shabbos, shlishi b\u8217'shabbos} \u8212- Sunday, Monday, Tuesday are the days of preparation; {\i u-v\u8217'revi\u8217'i konesah} \u8212- and on Wednesday he marries her. This is the full structure of the {\i takana}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wednesday to Tuesday\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i u-mei-hasakana v\u8217'eilach, nahagu lichnos b\u8217'shlishi} \u8212- once a certain danger emerged, the practice shifted to marrying on Tuesday. {\i Ve-lo miku beyadam chachamim} \u8212- and the rabbis did not object to this departure from the original {\i takana}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monday Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for Monday: {\i u-v\u8217'sheni lo yichanes} \u8212- as a general rule one should not marry on Monday. There is a dispute between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} about how to read this \u8212- whether it refers to the danger scenario or to ordinary circumstances \u8212- but {\i ve\u8217'i machmat ha\u8217'ones}, if there were special extenuating circumstances, getting married on Monday is nonetheless permitted. So the {\i takana} maintained its formal structure even as practical circumstances drove the actual custom toward Tuesday, with Monday available as a further fallback in cases of pressing need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sakana Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruler\u8217's Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What was this {\i sakana} that caused the shift from Wednesday to Tuesday? The {\i gemara} considers and rejects the most direct possibility \u8212- that the non-Jewish authorities were persecuting the practice of Wednesday weddings as a religious observance, threatening death to those who continued it. If that were the case, the {\i gemara} notes, why not uproot the {\i takana} entirely rather than simply shift to Tuesday? A persecuting government that targeted Wednesday weddings would simply target Tuesday weddings next.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} therefore offers a different account of the danger: {\i betulah niseit beyom revi\u8217'i tiba\u8217'el le-hegmon techilah} \u8212- when a virgin married on Wednesday, the local ruler or governor would claim the right to sexual relations with the bride before the groom. This was the {\i droit de seigneur}, a real practice in medieval contexts where local nobles claimed first access to brides on their wedding night. The danger was thus not religious persecution of the {\i takana} as such, but a specific and predictable abuse that would reliably occur whenever a wedding took place on a fixed, known day. By shifting to Tuesday \u8212- a day the governor would not anticipate \u8212- the community could avoid the problem, since the ruler simply would not know when to show up.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tznuot Resistance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i hai sakana} \u8212- why do you call this a {\i sakana}, a danger to life? {\i Ones hu} \u8212- it is an act of forced sex, an {\i ones}, but not a life-threatening situation. The woman is not being killed; she is being raped. Why frame it as a {\i sakana}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is: {\i mishum de\u8217'ika tznuos de-masran nafshaihu le-katla} \u8212- because there are women, the {\i tznuos}, so deeply modest and principled, that rather than submit they would actually resist unto death. For these women, the situation would genuinely become a {\i sakana}. {\i Ve-asyan lidei sakana} \u8212- they would come to risk their lives. The {\i tznuos} here are clearly acting {\i lifnim mishurat ha-din}, beyond the letter of the law; their resistance, though admirable, is not legally required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Submission Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the {\i gemara} to an important halakhic ruling: {\i ve-lidrosh lehu de-ones shari} \u8212- why not simply teach these women that submission is permitted? One might expect that since the woman is married \u8212- already having undergone {\i kiddushin} \u8212- this would fall under {\i giluy arayos}, which is one of the three cardinal sins subject to the rule of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor}, dying rather than transgressing. Why does the {\i gemara} assume she may simply submit?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a critical {\i gemara} for the discussion of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor} as it applies to {\i giluy arayos}. {\i Tosafot} addresses the question with two answers. The first, advanced by {\i Rabbeinu Tam}, is that sex with a non-Jew belongs to a categorically different legal domain and does not constitute {\i giluy arayos} for purposes of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor}. {\i Tosafot} ultimately rejects this approach, though {\i Rabbeinu Tam} appears to have advanced it to deal with a specific practical case \u8212- a Jewish woman who had lived with a non-Jewish man and then sought to return to a Jewish husband after that man converted; {\i Rabbeinu Tam} argued that her relationship with the non-Jew did not trigger {\i asura le-bo\u8217'el} or the {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second answer, which is generally taken as the operative explanation, draws on the principle found in {\i Sanhedrin}: {\i isha karka olam} \u8212- a woman in the act of rape is categorically passive, and the rule of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor} applies only to acts one actively performs. If someone threatens to use a person as an instrument of killing \u8212- throwing them onto an infant \u8212- the person need not give up their life if they are entirely passive. The question is whether we genuinely conceive of the woman as performing no act in the context of rape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ramban} offers what is perhaps the most persuasive resolution. He argues that the very nature of rape makes the choice of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor} a false one. Even if a woman were to say \u8220"kill me rather than rape me,\u8221" the rapist could choose to rape her anyway. The anatomy and physics of the situation mean that she cannot truly choose martyrdom over the act in the way that a man could, for instance, refuse to perform an idolatrous ritual. It is therefore not genuinely a situation where the rule of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor} can be applied, since the act can be imposed on her regardless of her choice. This explains why a married woman may legally submit rather than give up her life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Education Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: if submission is permitted, why not simply educate the women? Why allow the situation to remain one of {\i sakana} when the {\i tznuos} could be told that halakha does not require them to die? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ika prutzos ve-ika nami kohanos} \u8212- because of two distinct concerns.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is the problem of the {\i prutzos}: a little knowledge in the wrong hands is dangerous. If women are broadly taught that in such circumstances submission is permitted, some will use this information to rationalize submission in cases where it is {\i not} truly an {\i ones} \u8212- where there is no real compulsion at all. They will claim {\i ones} retroactively to justify conduct that cannot be justified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second is the particular problem of {\i kohanos} \u8212- women married to {\i kohanim}. Even though submission is legally permitted in a genuine {\i ones} scenario, the halakhic consequence for a {\i kohenes} is irreversible: she becomes forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband. For such women, the calculus is entirely different. They may rationally conclude that the irreversible halakhic consequence of submission \u8212- the permanent destruction of their marriage \u8212- is worse than the alternative. For them, the situation genuinely remains a {\i sakana}, and teaching them broadly that submission is permitted would not help them; it might actually harm them by obscuring the specific and severe consequence they face.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore maintains the framing of {\i sakana} rather than {\i ones}, and explains why public education was not the solution. One might find it deeply troubling that the {\i tznuos} were left exposed to lethal risk rather than being taught a ruling that would save their lives \u8212- all because of concern about the {\i prutzot} misusing the information. The better reading of the {\i gemara}, and the one that avoids the alternative implication that the rabbis were prepared to tolerate actual deaths in order to preserve the {\i takana} of Wednesday weddings, is that the {\i gemara}\u8217's question is not about whether to move away from Wednesday. Of course the community moved to Tuesday. The {\i gemara}\u8217's question is only about the framing: why describe the situation as {\i sakana} rather than {\i ones}? And the answer is that even with all the education in the world, there would still be {\i kohanos} for whom it remained a genuine life-threatening situation, and there would still be {\i prutzos} who would misuse the ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takana Preservation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira vs.\u160 ?Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question remains: given all of this difficulty, why did the rabbis not simply uproot the {\i takana} of Wednesday weddings altogether? If the fixed Wednesday date was the source of the danger, why not abolish the fixed day entirely and permit marriage on any day? The {\i gemara} answers with a foundational principle: {\i gezeira avidah de-batlah} \u8212- a governmental edict will eventually pass away. But {\i takkanata de-rabbanan mi-kamei gezeira lo akrinan} \u8212- we do not uproot a rabbinic ordinance in the face of a governmental edict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reflects a deep institutional orientation. The rabbis took a long view. Legal systems and religious frameworks must be built to outlast the particular political pressures of any given era. To dismantle a {\i takana} every time history presents a threat would be to have no enduring legal structure at all. The {\i takana} of Wednesday weddings was not abolished; the community adapted around it, found a workaround, and waited for the edict to pass.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shift to Tuesday\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The adaptation was the shift to Tuesday. The {\i takana} remained formally in place \u8212- Wednesday was still the halakhically ideal day for a virgin to marry \u8212- but the custom shifted to Tuesday without the rabbis objecting. The {\i takana} was not repealed; it was preserved in its theoretical integrity while practical necessity drove behavior in a different direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tuesday Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks the obvious question: {\i ee hachi, beshlishi nami atei u-va\u8217'eil} \u8212- if they start marrying on Tuesday, won\u8217't the governor simply show up on Tuesdays instead? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i misfeka lo akar nafshei} \u8212- out of uncertainty, he will not uproot himself and come. Once there is no fixed and predictable day \u8212- once weddings occur on whatever Tuesday or other day they happen to fall on, with no reliable pattern \u8212- the governor cannot know in advance when to appear. He would have to be present every day in every village, which is simply not practical. The uncertainty itself becomes the protective mechanism. The number of successful interceptions drops dramatically. Whereas a fixed Wednesday schedule turned every wedding into a predictable target, the shift to a variable day \u8212- theoretically any day, in practice often Tuesday \u8212- introduced enough unpredictability to make the abuse far less systematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is how the {\i takana} was preserved: not by upending it, not by surrendering to the pressure, but by a careful and principled adaptation that maintained the legal framework while rendering the specific vulnerability much harder to exploit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 4\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ones & Early Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sakana vs.\u160 ?Ones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf picks up near the bottom of 3b, where a {\i braita} \u8212- essentially a {\i Tosefta} \u8212- discusses the established practice of getting married on Wednesdays. The {\i braita} records that during a period of {\i sakana}, a genuine risk to life, the community shifted weddings to Tuesday. The Gemara explains this as a response to a local ruler who was forcing brides to sleep with him on their wedding night, so that making the wedding day less predictable offered some protection. The {\i braita} then states that in a time of {\i ones} \u8212- a circumstance beyond one\u8217's control, but short of a life-threatening {\i sakana} \u8212- one may marry on another day, using Monday as the example.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately distinguishes between these two terms. If the prior case was already called a {\i sakana}, why is this one now called an {\i ones}? The change in terminology signals that we are dealing with an altogether different kind of circumstance. Rashi adds a further nuance: the earlier formulation described a communal {\i minhag} that developed organically, whereas here the language {\i mutar} \u8212- it is permitted \u8212- carries the flavor of a formal, proactive rabbinic allowance, perhaps even a {\i l\u8217'chatchila} ruling for an individual case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Military Threat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava identifies the {\i ones} in question: word spreads that a military commander \u8212- a {\i sar tzava} \u8212- is coming to the city with his troops, who will requisition all available food and supplies. Since the wedding feast has already been prepared and will be seized if left for Wednesday, the couple has grounds to move the wedding earlier. The financial disruption of having all the food confiscated constitutes a sufficient {\i ones} to justify departing from the standard Wednesday date.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duration of Threat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a challenge: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case? If the commander is simply passing through and will leave after a short time, Rashi reads the Gemara\u8217's response as: just wait, and hold the wedding the following Wednesday or the one after. There is no need to act early if the disruption is temporary. Tosafos reads the case differently, suggesting that if the commander is leaving on that same Wednesday, one should simply schedule the feast for later in the day \u8212- but this reading is difficult, since the problem is that once the food is gathered it will be seized regardless of timing. The Gemara accordingly sets the case as {\i de-ati ve-kava}: the commander is arriving and settling in indefinitely. Under those circumstances, there is no later Wednesday to wait for, and moving the wedding earlier is warranted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Advanced Retinue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a follow-up question: if the commander is arriving on Wednesday, why not simply hold the wedding on Tuesday? The answer is that in this case, there would be no {\i chiddush} \u8212- no novel ruling \u8212- since one is merely moving back one day to avoid the exact day of the disruption. The Gemara therefore frames the case more pointedly: the commander\u8217's {\i asafrava} \u8212- his advance retinue \u8212- is arriving on Tuesday, ahead of his own arrival on Wednesday. This means the food could be seized even earlier, justifying moving the wedding all the way back to Monday. That is the genuine novelty: the disruption threatens not merely Wednesday but the days leading up to it, and we permit the wedding as early as necessary to avoid the loss.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Death & Wedding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Food & Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having outlined the {\i ones} of military requisition, the Gemara now turns to what may have been the original referent of the word {\i ones} in the first {\i braita}, or at minimum a distinct and important case that several {\i braisos} address in their own right. The scenario: {\i harei she-haya pito afui} \u8212- the bread has been baked, fresh; {\i u-tivcho tavuach} \u8212- the meat has been slaughtered, freshly; {\i ve-yaino mazug} \u8212- the wine has been mixed; everything is prepared for the wedding feast and will spoil if not consumed imminently. And at that moment, {\i u-mais aviv shel choson o imah shel kallah} \u8212- the father of the {\i chosson} or the mother of the {\i kallah} dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not merely a theoretical case. It combines two pressing concerns: the catastrophic financial loss of irreplaceable prepared food, and the death of the very parent who was instrumental in preparing for the wedding. If they wait a month until after {\i shiv\u8217'ah} and {\i shloshim}, not only will the food be lost, but the father will not be there to oversee the {\i chosson}\u8217's preparations, nor will the mother be there to prepare the {\i kallah} \u8212- the people uniquely responsible for making this wedding what it is. That combination drives the entire {\i halacha} that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Postponing Burial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical opportunity created by this moment is that the body has not yet been buried. {\i Avelus} does not begin until burial \u8212- and that creates room to maneuver. The {\i braita} prescribes: {\i machnisin es ha-meis la-cheder} \u8212- bring the corpse into a room \u8212- {\i ve-es ha-chosson ve-es ha-kallah la-chuppah} \u8212- and bring the {\i chosson} and {\i kallah} to the {\i chuppah}. The wedding proceeds while the body remains unburied and before {\i avelus} has begun, while the couple are still technically {\i onanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\i chuppah} means in this context deserves attention. It may refer to a {\i yichud} room, in which case the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} would follow immediately within it; or it may refer to the formal wedding canopy, with the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} occurring afterward. Since {\i kiddushin} has already been performed, there is no question about {\i bircas erusin}, and the {\i sheva brachos} are recited by the {\i tzibbur}. The precise mechanics of what constitutes {\i chuppah} here can be addressed separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Sex Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i u-vo\u8217'el bi\u8217'as mitzvah u-foresh} \u8212- he has relations once, the act of {\i mitzvah}, and then separates. Why is this required? Two explanations present themselves. One is a kind of {\i rachmanus}: they have prepared for this night, they anticipated it, and it would be deeply cruel to deny it entirely. The second, and perhaps more structural, reason is that the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} concretizes and actualizes the status of {\i chosson va-kallah} \u8212- it makes the marriage genuinely real, not merely formal. Since the entire enterprise depends on establishing that the {\i shivas yemei ha-mishteh} status of {\i chosson va-kallah} precedes the onset of {\i avelus}, having the {\i bi\u8217'ah} makes that status more robust and unambiguous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Celebration Before Mourn\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having had {\i bi\u8217'as mitzvah}, the {\i braita} continues: {\i ve-nohag shivas yemei ha-mishteh, ve-achar kach nohag shivas yemei avelus} \u8212- the full seven days of the wedding feast are observed, and only afterward does the week of mourning begin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the crux of the entire arrangement, and it is worth dwelling on. The logic parallels the laws of {\i avelus} during a {\i regel}. Ordinarily, if a burial occurs before a festival, the {\i regel} cancels the remaining days of {\i shiv\u8217'ah}, because the public joy of the {\i regel} cannot coexist with the public mourning of {\i avelus}. Here, the {\i shivas yemei ha-mishteh} of {\i chosson va-kallah} functions as a kind of {\i regel}: because it began before the {\i avelus} \u8212- because the burial takes place after the {\i chuppah} \u8212- the status of the wedding week precedes and displaces the onset of {\i avelus} during that entire week. The {\i avelus} does not kick in until the wedding week concludes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a more significant leniency than it might first appear. Had {\i avelus} already begun, perhaps we would have permitted a single wedding night \u8212- but we would not have allowed the {\i shivas yemei ha-mishteh} to run its full course. By manipulating the timing to ensure that {\i avelus} had not yet commenced, the couple gains not just the first night but the entire week of celebration. The {\i avelus} then follows in full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several practical advantages flow from acting before burial: first, the status of {\i onen} may be less restrictive than that of {\i avel} regarding {\i nissuin} and regarding {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- since there is a {\i machlokes} whether sex is even prohibited for an {\i onen}, making it less severe than the explicit prohibition during {\i avelus}; second, and more fundamentally, the {\i chuppah} itself and the {\i simcha} associated with {\i nissuin} are deeply bound up with that status of {\i chosson va-kallah}, and it is far less problematic to initiate {\i nissuin} during {\i aninus} than during {\i avelus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos raises a striking point: the Gemara is in effect being more lenient regarding {\i aninus d\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- since {\i aninus} has a Torah root, at least with respect to {\i korbanot} \u8212- than it is regarding {\i avelus d\u8217'rabbanan}. Tosafos explains that although {\i aninus} may have a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} anchor, the practical obligations and restrictions of an {\i onen} are structured very differently from those of an {\i avel}: an {\i onen} does not sit on the ground, does not rend garments, does not observe the other symbolic mourning practices. The status of {\i onen} is simply a different kind of status, and within that status, the space for {\i nissuin} and even {\i bi\u8217'ah} is greater.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sleeping Separate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Although the {\i shivas yemei ha-mishteh} precedes {\i avelus}, the Gemara is clear that once the burial takes place \u8212- which occurs after the {\i bi\u8217'as mitzvah} \u8212- {\i avelus} begins. Even while the wedding week is in full swing, the private dimension of {\i avelus} obtains. The principle is that {\i devarim she-be-tzina} \u8212- private matters \u8212- apply even when {\i avelus} is otherwise suspended, just as they apply when a burial occurs during a {\i regel}. The most classic example of {\i devarim she-be-tzina} is the prohibition on marital relations. Accordingly, although the public celebrations of the week continue, the couple may not continue to have sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To enforce this, the {\i braita} prescribes: {\i hu yashen bein ha-anashim ve-hi yeshena bein ha-nashim} \u8212- he sleeps among the men and she sleeps among the women. This is stronger than merely sleeping in separate beds; they are placed in entirely separate company to prevent them from acting on the very powerful impulse to be together \u8212- having just gotten married \u8212- in violation of the {\i issur} that is now in effect. The reason this extreme measure is required, as the Gemara will explain through Rav Ashi, is that the rabbis were so lenient in enabling all of this manipulation in the first place that the couple might not take the residual {\i avelus} obligations seriously. Since the {\i avelus} has been pushed aside and only the {\i devarim she-be-tzina} remain, the sole live prohibition is the most invisible one. Extra measures are needed to ensure it is honored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe, as an interesting point, that requiring the couple to sleep in conspicuously separate quarters \u8212- surrounded by others of the same gender \u8212- turns what was meant to be a private matter into a very public one. The very act of enforcing the private prohibition makes it publicly visible. This tension remains unresolved in the Gemara.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bride\u8217's Beautification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} adds: {\i ve-ein monin tachshitin min ha-kallah kol shloshim yom} \u8212- one does not prevent the {\i kallah} from wearing jewelry and beautifying herself throughout the first thirty days, even though she is an {\i aveilah} and even though {\i shloshim} normally restricts such beautification. The rationale is clear: this is the first month of marriage, and doing everything possible to encourage mutual attraction between the new couple takes precedence over the standard requirements of {\i avelus} in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father/Mother Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} specifies: {\i ve-davka aviv shel chosson o imah shel kallah} \u8212- specifically the {\i chosson}\u8217's father or the {\i kallah}\u8217's mother, not the reverse. The reason is {\i d\u8217'ika inish d\u8217'tarach lehu}: these are the people who were actively engaged in preparing for the wedding. The father oversaw and coordinated his son\u8217's preparations; the mother prepared her daughter. If we were to say simply postpone the wedding until after the {\i avelus}, the argument that \u8220"it can be replicated later\u8221" fails \u8212- because what cannot be replicated is the preparation that parent performed. The {\i kallah}\u8217's mother prepared her for this wedding, and then died moments before it took place. The emotional and practical dimensions of that are real; it is that irreplaceability that drives the ruling. {\i Aval ipcha lo} \u8212- the reverse configuration does not qualify, because that presumption of active personal preparation does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Contemporary {\i poskim} have at times been less strict about insisting on the exact formulation of {\i aviv shel chosson}, particularly given the enormous financial losses involved in modern weddings \u8212- deposits, caterers, halls \u8212- that are also irretrievable. The Gemara does say {\i ve-davka}, which implies the specification is meaningful. Whether the financial loss alone suffices, or whether it requires both the financial factor and the irreplaceable-preparation factor together, is a genuine question. One could read the Gemara as requiring both: a purely financial loss, however large, may not be sufficient without the specific circumstance of the irreplaceable parental role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Food Spoilage Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda introduces a qualification: {\i lo shanu ela she-natan mayim agabi basar} \u8212- this leniency applies only when water has been applied to the meat, i.e., the meat has been rinsed in preparation for {\i melichah} to remove the blood. In that state, it cannot be preserved or resold; if not consumed immediately it is lost. But if the meat has not yet been rinsed and is still in a more preserved state, it can be sold and the financial loss can be recouped \u8212- and therefore the wedding should be postponed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava qualifies this further: in a large city, {\i u-va-krach}, even rinsed meat can be sold quickly \u8212- enough buyers exist that one can set up and sell it all within hours. The financial loss is therefore recoverable, and the wedding should be postponed. Rav Pappa offers the counter-qualification: in a village, {\i u-vi-chfar}, even unrinsed meat cannot be sold, because the population is too small to absorb it. So the wedding should always proceed in a village, and never needs to be postponed in one on account of rinsed versus unrinsed meat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leaves Rav Chisda\u8217's original distinction \u8212- rinsed versus unrinsed \u8212- without an apparent case. Rav Ashi resolves it: Rav Chisda\u8217's case is a medium-sized city, like {\i Masa Machasya}, which is neither a full metropolitan center nor a village. In such a town, rinsed meat cannot be sold fast enough to prevent loss, but unrinsed meat can still be preserved and sold. The {\i tosefta kevasei de-Rav Chisda} \u8212- the {\i braita} that accords with Rav Chisda \u8212- then repeats the full scenario, including the condition {\i ve-natan mayim agabi basar}, confirming that the operative halacha requires the meat to have been rinsed. The remainder of the {\i braita}\u8217's formulation is essentially identical to what we have already seen: the corpse into the room, the couple to the {\i chuppah}, {\i bi\u8217'as mitzvah}, the seven days of wedding feast, then seven days of {\i avelus}, and the sleeping-separate requirement for all those days.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chuppat Niddah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Sleeping Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues with a second scenario: {\i ve-chen mi she-pirsa ishto niddah} \u8212- similarly, a man whose wife becomes a {\i niddah} on the wedding night \u8212- {\i hu yashen bein ha-anashim ve-hi yeshena bein ha-nashim}, he sleeps among the men and she among the women. At first glance, this is startling, since the Gemara elsewhere is quite clear that a husband and wife share a room even when she is a {\i niddah}; isolating the woman to a separate dwelling is associated with practices of ancient cultures that maintained strict menstrual taboos, not with {\i halacha}. The key is context: the entire {\i braita} is addressing the wedding night specifically. Here, the couple has not yet had relations. When a {\i chuppat niddah} occurs \u8212- when the wife becomes a {\i niddah} before {\i bi\u8217'ah} on the wedding night \u8212- there has been no established marital intimacy, the desire is at its most intense, and the concern that they will not be able to withstand the temptation is acute. The requirement to sleep in separate company therefore applies specifically in this pre-{\i bi\u8217'ah} context. This is in fact a real and practically significant {\i halacha}, and the question of how to implement the requirement of sleeping {\i bein ha-anashim} and {\i bein ha-nashim} in contemporary circumstances is one that {\i poskim} address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Devarim Shebetzina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that this {\i braita} \u8212- specifically the requirement of sleeping separate even after the {\i bi\u8217'as mitzvah} in the death case \u8212- provides strong support for Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position: {\i af al pi she-amru ein avelus ba-moed, aval devarim she-be-tzina noheg} \u8212- even though {\i avelus} does not apply during the {\i regel}, the private dimensions of {\i avelus} are nonetheless observed. Here, the {\i shivas yemei ha-mishteh} functions as the {\i regel}, and yet they must sleep separate \u8212- demonstrating that the {\i devarim she-be-tzina} prohibition of {\i bi\u8217'ah} applies even within that week. The sleeping-separate requirement is precisely the {\i braita}\u8217's way of enforcing that private prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before vs.\u160 ?After\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef the son of Rav Ami in the name of Rava then introduces a distinction: {\i lo shanu ela she-lo ba\u8217'al, aval ba\u8217'al, ishto yeshena imo} \u8212- this separation requirement applies only where they have not yet had {\i bi\u8217'ah}; once they have had {\i bi\u8217'ah}, the wife sleeps with him normally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this: in the death case, the whole point is that they did have {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i bi\u8217'as mitzvah} \u8212- and still they are required to sleep separate. So how can Rava say that {\i bi\u8217'ah} removes the separation requirement? The Gemara resolves the apparent contradiction by re-reading the {\i braita}: the {\i ve-chen} \u8212- \u8220"similarly\u8221" \u8212- introducing the {\i niddah} case does not indicate identical circumstances. Rather, it indicates that the same {\i halacha} of sleeping separate applies. The {\i niddah} case itself, however, is specifically one where {\i bi\u8217'ah} has not occurred; it is {\i mi she-pirsa ishto niddah ve-lo ba\u8217'al} \u8212- she became a {\i niddah} and they never had {\i bi\u8217'ah}. In that situation \u8212- {\i chuppat niddah} before any {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the separation requirement applies. Once {\i bi\u8217'ah} has taken place and the woman subsequently becomes a {\i niddah} in the normal course of marriage, the couple continues to share a room.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Avelus vs.\u160 ?Niddah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Duties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a broader comparison, framing it as a question: does {\i avelus} exhibit greater stringency than {\i niddah} in this regard, such that the sleeping-separate requirement would make sense as a routine feature of any case of {\i avelus}? The Gemara cites Rabbi Yitzchak bar Chanina in the name of Rav Huna: {\i kol melachot she-ha-ishah osah le-va\u8217'alah, niddah osah le-va\u8217'alah} \u8212- all the domestic duties that a wife performs for her husband, a {\i niddah} continues to perform. The extensive list of domestic responsibilities that will be enumerated later in the tractate applies equally during {\i niddah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Harchakot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exceptions are three specific acts designated as intimate: {\i chutz mi-mezigat ha-kos} \u8212- she does not mix his drink, which is seen as an act of personal and affectionate service; {\i ve-hatza\u8217'as ha-mitah} \u8212- she does not make his bed in the final decorative, presentational sense (Tosafos clarifies this refers specifically to the finishing touches, not the basic spreading of sheets); {\i ve-harchatzat panav yadav ve-raglav} \u8212- she does not wash his face, hands, and feet. These constitute the {\i harchakot}, the specific intimate acts that a {\i niddah} refrains from performing for her husband as a form of distancing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avelus Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when a woman is an {\i aveilah}, a {\i braita} teaches: {\i af al pi she-amru ein adam rashai lichof ishto lihyot kochelet lihyot pokeset} \u8212- a husband cannot compel his {\i aveilah} wife to beautify herself; he cannot insist she apply eye makeup ({\i kochelet}, which Rashi may read as rouge) or braid her hair ({\i pokeset}). Even so, {\i be-emet amru} \u8212- the halakhic truth is \u8212- she still performs all the intimate domestic acts, including mixing his drink, making his bed, and washing his face, hands, and feet. The {\i harchakot} that apply by {\i niddah} do not apply by {\i avelus}. An {\i aveilah} may perform all of these acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beautification Prohibiti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry is noteworthy. During {\i niddah}, certain intimate domestic acts are prohibited to preserve appropriate boundaries; during {\i avelus}, those same acts are permitted \u8212- the restriction runs the other way: the husband cannot compel the {\i aveilah} wife to beautify herself against her will. Her state of mourning is hers to observe, and he may not override it by demanding she present herself in a way that contradicts her {\i avelus}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Avel Sleeping Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General Avel Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i avelus} is in fact {\i more lenient} than {\i niddah} when it comes to intimate acts, the Gemara confronts its earlier assumption. It had been reading the {\i braita}\u8217's sleeping-separate requirement as a general feature of any {\i avelus} situation. But if {\i avelus} is less stringent than {\i niddah} in terms of {\i harchakot}, why would we impose a more severe sleeping arrangement \u8212- separate rooms, sleeping among members of the same gender \u8212- in any ordinary case of {\i avelus}? This requires explanation. The Gemara\u8217's initial answer: {\i yetzer hara}. A man is assumed to be less in control of his sexual impulses, more capable of pressuring his wife even if she is reluctant, and more likely to override even his own {\i avelus} obligation. Therefore, when {\i he} is the {\i avel}, more protective measures are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband vs.\u160 ?Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the Gemara to distinguish between two configurations: {\i kan be-avelus didei, kan be-avelus didah} \u8212- in one case he is the {\i avel}, in the other she is the {\i aveilah}. When he is the {\i avel}, his {\i yetzer hara} may lead him to violate the prohibition on marital relations \u8212- and to bring his wife along, whether she wants to or not. When she is the {\i aveilah}, the same dynamic does not hold: she is in mourning, she will resist, and he will be unable to override her refusal. The Gemara therefore proposes that the sleeping-separate requirement applies specifically when the husband is the {\i avel}, and not when only the wife is the {\i aveilah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amended Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara faces an objection: the original {\i braita} specifies {\i aviv shel chosson o imah shel kallah} \u8212- either his father or her mother. That means both cases \u8212- he as {\i avel} and she as {\i aveilah} \u8212- result in the same sleeping-separate arrangement. How then can we say the requirement applies only when he is the {\i avel}? The Gemara resolves this by proposing that the {\i braita} be read differently: the {\i ve-davka} formulation of the father-of-the-{\i chosson} / mother-of-the-{\i kallah} applies to most of the {\i halachos} discussed, but the specific sleeping-separate rule should be understood as inserted only for the case where he is the {\i avel}. The {\i braita} is to be read \u8212- {\i teni} \u8212- with that clarification built in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spouse Respect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of who is the {\i avel} raises a further question from a second {\i braita}: when a man\u8217's father-in-law or mother-in-law dies \u8212- his wife\u8217's parent, not his own \u8212- he cannot compel his wife to beautify herself, and moreover, {\i kofeh mitaso ve-noheg imah avelus} \u8212- he overturns his own couch and himself observes {\i avelus} together with her. The same applies in reverse: if his parents die, she cannot beautify herself and she too turns over her couch and observes {\i avelus} with him. Neither of those {\i braisos} mentions any sleeping-separate requirement in its description of the spousal-respect dimension of {\i avelus}. This seems to undermine the Gemara\u8217's claim that sleeping separate is a feature of {\i avelus} when the husband is the {\i avel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's response is to amend the {\i braita} once more \u8212- {\i teni be-avelus didei hu yashen bein ha-anashim ve-ishto yeshena bein ha-nashim} \u8212- inserting the sleeping-separate rule into the {\i braita} where the husband is the {\i avel}. The Gemara also resolves the phrase {\i kofeh mitaso ve-noheg imo avelus} \u8212- \u8220"observing {\i avelus} with him\u8221" \u8212- not as meaning sharing his bed, but rather as meaning that in his presence she too practices {\i avelus} out of respect. This is grounded by the tradition in Rav\u8217's name to his son Chiya: when in your wife\u8217's presence during her {\i shiv\u8217'ah}, observe {\i avelus}; when not in her presence, you need not. The word {\i imo} \u8212- \u8220"with him\u8221" \u8212- refers not to physical proximity in bed but to observing {\i avelus} when together in the house.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Special Avelus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Normal Avelus Serious\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then intervenes with a resolution that reframes the entire discussion: {\i mi kamadmis avelus de-hacha le-avelus de-alma}? How can you compare the {\i avelus} in this specific case to ordinary {\i avelus}? The two are fundamentally different situations, and the sleeping-separate requirement in the {\i braita} does not indicate anything about routine {\i avelus}. {\i Avelus de-alma chamir} \u8212- ordinary {\i avelus} is treated seriously. People do not take it lightly, do not disparage it, and do not need additional safeguards to ensure the prohibitions are respected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 This Avelus Lenient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Avelus de-hacha}, however \u8212- this particular {\i avelus} \u8212- is a completely different matter. {\i Keivan d\u8217'akilu rabbanan} \u8212- since the rabbis themselves were so lenient in this case, enabling the entire arrangement to proceed \u8212- {\i asu lizilzuli bei} \u8212- the person may come to treat it lightly. When the {\i avelus} has been pushed aside and manipulated so that the wedding week could come first, the psychological weight of the mourning obligation is diminished. The person may not feel fully bound by even what remains of the {\i avelus} during the wedding week.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leniency Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then clarifies what the specific leniency was. It was not the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} itself \u8212- the Gemara states {\i hatam mishum de-lo chala alei avelus}: {\i avelus} had not yet begun when they had {\i bi\u8217'ah}, because the {\i meis} was still unburied. According to Rabbi Eliezer, {\i avelus} begins when the body is removed from the house on the way to burial; according to Rabbi Yehoshua, it begins when the {\i golel} \u8212- either the tombstone or, according to Rashi, the cover of the {\i aron} \u8212- is put in place. Either way, at the time of {\i bi\u8217'as mitzvah}, {\i avelus} had not yet commenced, and so permitting {\i bi\u8217'ah} then was no leniency at all. The leniency \u8212- {\i mai kula} \u8212- is precisely what the {\i braita} states: {\i nohag shivas yemei ha-mishteh ve-achar kach noheg shivas yemei avelus} \u8212- that the entire week of wedding celebration is permitted to precede the entire week of mourning. That is the rabbinic manipulation, the pushing-aside of {\i avelus} to enable the {\i simcha} to run its course first. And it is because of that extraordinary leniency \u8212- not a violation of {\i halacha}, but a deliberate restructuring of its onset \u8212- that the person may come to treat the residual obligations lightly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Private Laws Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is this: once the burial occurs, {\i avelus} begins; but since the {\i shivas yemei ha-mishteh} has already started and that status came first, most of the outward and public dimensions of {\i avelus} are suspended during that week. The only operative dimension of {\i avelus} during the week is {\i devarim she-be-tzina} \u8212- the private prohibition on marital relations. That prohibition is, by its very nature, invisible to others. And since the entire frame has been one of leniency \u8212- you were allowed to have your {\i bi\u8217'as mitzvah}, the wedding week is proceeding \u8212- there is a real concern that this one remaining, private prohibition will be taken lightly or ignored. It is precisely because {\i devarim she-be-tzina} is all that remains that we must go to the extreme of having him sleep among the men and her among the women, making the enforcement of a private prohibition through very public means.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 6\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubot Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Topics Survey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf we take up today \u8212- 6a \u8212- finds us in the middle of a challenging {\i sugya} concerning first-time intercourse on {\i Shabbat}. Before proceeding, it is worth pausing to appreciate just how much ground has been covered in the opening pages of this {\i masechet}. Beginning with the question of when a {\i besulah} gets married and whether the groom must pay support when the marriage is delayed, the {\i masechet} quickly moved far afield of its core theme. On 2b we encountered the laws of conditions in a {\i get} and whether an unfulfillable condition is nonetheless considered fulfilled \u8212- a major question in contract law and {\i gittin}. On daf 3 came the topic of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin}, the rabbinic power to retroactively dissolve a marriage. Then, on 3b, a case of a woman compelled to submit to intercourse with a local ruler under threat of death, bringing in questions of {\i yaharog v\u8217'al ya\u8217'avor}. From there the {\i masechet} turned to {\i aveilus} and {\i aninus} \u8212- whether a funeral may be postponed to permit a scheduled wedding, the laws of {\i devarim she\u8217'betzina noheg}, and related questions. On daf 5 came the question of what types of matters may be discussed on {\i Shabbat} and the permissibility of doing {\i cheshbonos} on {\i Shabbat}, before returning to the themes of {\i ketubot} proper: the date of the wedding, the {\i beracha she\u8217'ne\u8217'emra bo l\u8217'adam}, the {\i beracha she\u8217'ne\u8217'emra bo l\u8217'dagim}, and an {\i agadata} about why one should not listen to {\i lashon hara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mini-Shas Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The range of topics traversed in four {\i daf} \u8212- {\i niddah}, {\i aveilus}, {\i gittin}, {\i Shabbat}, and more \u8212- is precisely why {\i Ketubot} is famously described as a {\i Shas Katan}, a mini-tractate of the entire Talmud. And it is not merely the breadth of subject matter that earns it this title. The {\i sugyot} in {\i Ketubot} tend to arrive at the very heart of each conceptual question rather than working around its periphery for many sessions. Each discussion reaches the core of a central halakhic principle, which is one reason why this {\i masechet} is studied so extensively in {\i yeshivos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pedagogy Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That said, a word must be said about the pedagogical difficulties this particular {\i sugya} presents. The questions being analyzed here concern first-time intercourse, whether the act creates a wound, whether it creates an opening, what the groom\u8217's intent is, whether he wishes to see blood to confirm his bride\u8217's virginity, whether making her a non-virgin is seen as an act of desire or as damaging something pristine \u8212- all of this is typically taught to teenage boys in {\i yeshiva}. The difficulty is not the explicitness of the discussion per se; frank conversation about the body is appropriate. The deeper challenge is the orientation of the entire discussion: it is conducted entirely from the man\u8217's perspective. The woman\u8217's body is analyzed as an object of halakhic scrutiny. Nobody in the {\i gemara} asks whether the woman is permitted to have intercourse on {\i Shabbat}. That question is treated as simply irrelevant. This framing \u8212- focused on what the man is doing, what the man intends, what the man sees \u8212- and the way it objectifies the woman\u8217's body and renders her something of a passive subject in the analysis, creates real challenges for how this material ought to be taught. Those challenges, in the experience of most {\i yeshivos}, are never actually raised.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First-Time Sex Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To distill the halakhic structure: there are two potential prohibitions associated with first intercourse on {\i Shabbat}. The first is {\i chaburah} \u8212- the act of wounding \u8212- depending on how the {\i gemara} understands the underlying physiology. If the blood emerges because the hymenal tissue tears away from the wall and blood is released from vessels within that tissue, that constitutes wounding. The second possible prohibition is {\i petach} \u8212- making an opening \u8212- which would presumably fall under the category of {\i binyan}, an interesting application of the concept of building to a human body, though we do encounter that elsewhere in the {\i gemarot}. With these two possible prohibitions established, the {\i gemara} then asks about the groom\u8217's intent: is he {\i mechaven} for this outcome, or is he {\i eino mechaven}? Does his intent matter, and does the principle of {\i mekalkel} apply \u8212- the notion that since wounding is inherently a somewhat destructive act, perhaps there is a special {\i halakha} regarding {\i chaburot} such that even a {\i mekalkel} is {\i chayav}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mekalkel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a sharp conceptual question about the category of {\i chaburah} and its relationship to {\i melachehet machshevet}. {\i Chaburah} is categorized under {\i netilat neshamah} \u8212- the same prohibition that forbids {\i shechting} an animal. Making a wound is like a miniature act of taking life. But how precisely is it a taking of life? {\i Tosafot} suggests two possibilities: either wounding weakens the living being and thus represents a step toward death, or the very extraction of blood is itself the halakhic act, since blood represents life regardless of the physical harm caused. {\i Tosafot} argues that it must be the latter. If wounding were prohibited because it harms or weakens the person, there would be no basis for the {\i gemara} to consider it an intentional {\i melacha} in this context, since no one desires to wound the woman or cause her pain or weakness. The only way the {\i gemara} can speak of the groom being {\i mechaven} is if the blood itself \u8212- as a symbol of life \u8212- is the object of his intention: he wants to see the blood to know that his bride is a {\i besulah}. This frames {\i netilat neshamah} in an almost symbolic sense, independent of physical harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then presses a further question. If {\i mekalkel bechabura chayav} \u8212- if even a destructive act of wounding incurs {\i chiyuv} \u8212- then why does the {\i gemara} treat {\i kavanah} as relevant at all? If {\i Rabbi Shimon} holds you are {\i chayav} for {\i mekalkel bechabura}, why would {\i eino mitkaven} provide an exemption according to {\i Rabbi Shimon}? {\i Tosafot} answers with an important conceptual distinction: {\i mechaven} \u8212- having intent \u8212- is not a uniquely {\i Shabbat} requirement. It is a general requirement applicable to all prohibitions. What {\i Rabbi Shimon} is saying when he rules {\i chayav} by {\i mekalkel bechabura} is that he waives the special {\i Shabbat} criterion of {\i melachehet machshevet} \u8212- the requirement that a prohibited act be constructive \u8212- in the context of wounding. But the general principle that a person must intend the prohibited act in order to be liable is not a {\i Shabbat} criterion; it applies universally. Accordingly, {\i eino mitkaven} remains an exemption even by {\i chaburah} according to {\i Rabbi Shimon}, because that exemption derives not from the {\i Shabbat} requirement of {\i melachehet machshevet} but from a general principle of halakhic liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} makes this point explicitly at the top of 6a. He writes that the reason both {\i mekalkel} and {\i misasek} are exempt in other {\i melachos Shabbat} is: {\i detam sheneihem depeturin beshaar melachos Shabbos havei mishum debe\u8217'inan meleches machsheves} \u8212- because we require {\i meleches machsheves}, and that is the specifically {\i Shabbat} criterion. But {\i aval she-eino miskavein klal le-melachah} \u8212- if one is not intending to perform the prohibited act at all \u8212- {\i lo mishum debe\u8217'inan meleches machsheves mister} \u8212- that exemption does not derive from the {\i Shabbat} requirement of {\i meleches machsheves} \u8212- {\i deshaar mekomos nami piter aino miskavein} \u8212- because {\i eino miskavein} is an exemption in other prohibitions as well, such as {\i kilayim}, where those who sell garments may do so in their normal manner provided they do not intend a violation, and similarly a {\i nazir} may comb his hair even if strands may come out. {\i Hilkach be-chaburah nami shari} \u8212- therefore, {\i eino miskavein} remains an exemption by wounding as well, even where {\i mekalkel} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is not merely relevant to the present {\i sugya}. It applies practically wherever {\i mekalkel bechabura} might come up \u8212- for example, in the context of an injection on {\i Shabbat} that draws blood. Does {\i eino miskavein} still exempt in such a case even if {\i mekalkel bechabura} is {\i chayav}? {\i Tosafot} establishes clearly from this {\i gemara} that yes, {\i eino miskavein} remains an exemption, because it is a general halakhic principle, not a specifically {\i Shabbat} criterion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dispute Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents the central {\i amoraic} dispute. In the {\i yeshiva} of Rav, the tradition was: {\i Rav shari ve-Shmuel assur} \u8212- Rav permits first intercourse on {\i Shabbat} and Shmuel forbids it. In {\i Neharde\u8217'a}, the city of Shmuel, the tradition was reversed: Rav forbids it and Shmuel permits it. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak provides a {\i siman} \u8212- a mnemonic \u8212- to remember the correct version: {\i eilu mikilin le-atzman ve-eilu mikilin le-atzman} \u8212- each {\i yeshiva} attributed the lenient position to their own master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Rag Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then challenges Rav\u8217's permissive position. Does Rav truly permit first intercourse on {\i Shabbat}? But Rav Shimi bar Chizkiya transmitted in the name of Rav that a {\i mesukhraya d\u8217'nazeita} \u8212- a rag stuffed into the bunghole of a wine barrel \u8212- is forbidden to be tightened on {\i Yom Tov}. The implicit reasoning, as Rashi explains, is that tightening the rag wrings out wine absorbed in it, constituting {\i sechita}. And this ruling, the {\i gemara} assumes, indicates that Rav holds {\i eino miskavein} is {\i assur} \u8212- since the wine being squeezed is unintended \u8212- which would in turn make first intercourse on {\i Shabbat} far more likely to be forbidden.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First-Time Sex Issue (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A major {\i Tosafot} engages this passage and raises a series of layered difficulties. If the prohibition of tightening the rag is rooted in {\i sechita}, the nature of that {\i sechita} must be clarified. If the {\i issur} of {\i sechita} is like laundering \u8212- where one soaks a garment and wrings it out \u8212- then, {\i Tosafot} argues, the prohibition would apply only to water, since the wringing of water is part of the laundering process. It would not extend to wine, which does not belong to that category. {\i Tosafot} therefore identifies a second category of {\i sechita}, analogous to {\i dash} \u8212- threshing. Just as threshing removes grain from its husk, squeezing a lemon removes juice from its natural container. When wine has been absorbed into a cloth, wringing it out would similarly constitute removing liquid from its place of containment. This would be {\i d\u8217'Oraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i Tosafot} immediately raises a further difficulty: even granting this second category of {\i sechita}, the wine being squeezed out in this case is {\i holeich le-ibud} \u8212- it falls to the ground and is lost. An act that is {\i holeich le-ibud} is at least {\i mekalkel}, if not even less than {\i mekalkel} from the standpoint of halakhic liability, since there is no constructive purpose achieved even in the most basic sense. Why then is it prohibited?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But before reaching this difficulty, {\i Tosafot} raises another that is arguably more fundamental: even if we say this is a {\i pesik reisha} \u8212- something that will inevitably occur \u8212- if the person derives no benefit from the result, it is a {\i pesik reisha d\u8217'lo nicha lei}. Is such a case {\i mutar lechatchila} or {\i assur lechatchila}? This question, which is central to {\i hilchos Shabbat}, receives its primary treatment in this very {\i Tosafot}. {\i Rabbeinu Tam} argues that the act should be prohibited by virtue of {\i mefarek} \u8212- the {\i dash}-like {\i sechita} \u8212- even in a case of {\i lo nicha lei}. {\i Tosafot} then cites the {\i Aruch}, who writes: {\i dechol psik reisha d\u8217'lo nicha lei shari} \u8212- any {\i pesik reisha d\u8217'lo nicha lei} is permitted. The {\i Ri} disagrees and maintains that {\i psik reisha d\u8217'lo nicha lei} remains {\i assur}. This debate among the {\i rishonim} \u8212- whether {\i psik reisha d\u8217'lo nicha lei} is {\i mutar lechatchila} or forbidden at least {\i m\u8217'derabbanan} \u8212- is one of the most consequential discussions in practical {\i hilchos Shabbat}, and it is this {\i Tosafot} that is the primary locus of that question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further note from {\i Tosafot}: the distinction between {\i mekalkel} and {\i misasek} on one side, and {\i eino miskavein} on the other, clarifies why {\i psik reisha} matters. The {\i gemara}\u8217's discussion of {\i eino miskavein} as permissible according to {\i Rabbi Shimon} refers to cases where the prohibited outcome is not inevitable. Once the outcome is inevitable \u8212- {\i pesik reisha} \u8212- {\i eino miskavein} no longer exempts, as {\i Abaye} and {\i Rava} both rule: {\i modei Rabbi Shimon be-pesik reisha ve-lo yamut} \u8212- even {\i Rabbi Shimon} concedes that if one chops off an animal\u8217's head one cannot claim not to have intended its death. The exemption of {\i eino miskavein} is suspended by {\i pesik reisha}. The further question, introduced here by the {\i Aruch} and the {\i Ri}, is whether {\i pesik reisha d\u8217'lo nicha lei} \u8212- where the outcome is inevitable but unwanted \u8212- restores the exemption at the level of {\i d\u8217'Oraisa}, and whether it remains forbidden {\i m\u8217'derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the challenge against Rav from the case of the wine rag. Rav Chiya bar Ashi transmitted in the name of Rav that the {\i halakha} follows {\i Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- that {\i eino miskavein} is {\i assur}. Rav Chanan bar Ami transmitted in the name of Shmuel that the {\i halakha} follows {\i Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- that {\i eino miskavein} is permitted. And Rav Chiya bar Avin teaches the same positions without the chain of transmission, stating directly: Rav rules like {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, Shmuel rules like {\i Rabbi Shimon}. So the very same dispute between Rav and Shmuel on {\i eino miskavein} maps precisely onto their positions on first intercourse on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this creates an apparent contradiction: if Rav rules like {\i Rabbi Yehuda} that {\i eino miskavein} is {\i assur}, how can Rav permit first intercourse on {\i Shabbat}, given that the allowances discussed in the preceding analysis were largely predicated on {\i eino miskavein}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i l\u8217'olam Rav ke-Rabbi Yehuda sevira lei} \u8212- Rav indeed holds like {\i Rabbi Yehuda} that {\i eino miskavein} is {\i assur}. His permissive ruling on first intercourse, however, does not depend on {\i eino miskavein}. According to the position that {\i dam mizkar pakid} \u8212- that the blood is pooled behind the hymen and its release does not constitute a wound \u8212- the relevant prohibition is {\i petach}, making an opening. And here, {\i mekalkel hu etzel pesach} \u8212- it is {\i mekalkel} with respect to the opening, since from one perspective the husband regards his wife\u8217's prior virginal state as something to be preserved, and its transformation is therefore a form of destruction. According to the other position, {\i dam chiburei miptar} \u8212- that there is indeed a wound involved \u8212- {\i mekalkel bachabura hu} \u8212- it is similarly a {\i mekalkel} act of wounding, in the sense that causing the wound is not constructive from his perspective. In both cases the exemption rests on {\i mekalkel}, not on {\i eino miskavein}. {\i Tosafot} adds the observation that being {\i mekalkel} does not make the act {\i mutar lechatchila}; there are other considerations at play, namely that this is a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- but that is a separate question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tinokes Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda raises a challenge from a {\i Mishnah} in {\i hilchos niddah} concerning the case of a {\i tinokes she-lo higia zemana liros} \u8212- an underage girl who has not yet reached the age of menstruation \u8212- who is married and has intercourse. The {\i Mishnah} operates on the principle that {\i dam besulim} \u8212- hymenal blood \u8212- exempts from concern about {\i dam niddah}; bleeding in the days following the wedding night is attributed to the hymen rather than to menstruation. The question is how long this exemption extends.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai omrim nosna lo arba leilos} \u8212- Beis Shammai holds that the first four nights are covered; any bleeding within those four nights is not treated as {\i niddah}. {\i U-Veis Hillel omrim ad she-tiyeh hamakah} \u8212- Beis Hillel holds that the exemption is indefinite, until one is confident the wound has healed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where the girl has reached the age of menstruation, {\i Beis Shammai omrim nosna lo laila harishon} \u8212- Beis Shammai limits the exemption to the first night only. {\i U-Veis Hillel omrim ad motzei Shabbos arba leilos} \u8212- Beis Hillel says until {\i motzei Shabbat}, four nights \u8212- reflecting the institution that virgins marry on Wednesday, so that Wednesday night, Thursday night, Friday night, and Saturday night constitute the four nights, after which {\i motzei Shabbat} arrives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Nights Implication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mention of {\i motzei Shabbat} and four nights implies that the couple is having intercourse on Friday night \u8212- {\i Shabbat} eve \u8212- and that the resulting bleeding is not a concern. This seems to bear directly on the question of whether first intercourse is permitted on {\i Shabbat}. Even if by the third or fourth night the hymen has already been ruptured, the {\i gemara} understands that continued intercourse can still produce hymenal bleeding, so the scenario remains analogous to the central question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava vs.\u160 ?Abaye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai lav de-ilu bo\u8217'al matzi bo\u8217'al} \u8212- does this not mean that if he has not yet completed intercourse he is permitted to do so, implying even on {\i Shabbat}? {\i Amar Rava: lo, levar mi-Shabbos} \u8212- Rava says no; the four nights extend until {\i motzei Shabbat} but exclude {\i Shabbat} itself; they are not having intercourse on Friday night. {\i Amar lei Abaye: veha ad motzei Shabbos arba leilos katani} \u8212- but the {\i Mishnah} explicitly states four nights until {\i motzei Shabbat}, which implies every one of those nights, including Friday. {\i Ella amar Rava keshe-baal} \u8212- Rava responds: the case is that he has already had intercourse. By that rereading, the {\i chiddush} is not that they are having first intercourse on {\i Shabbat} but rather something else. {\i Veki ba\u8217'al mai ka mashma lan?} \u8212- but then what is the {\i chiddush} in mentioning {\i motzei Shabbat} and four nights at all? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ka mashma lan deshary lemiv\u8217'al beShabbos kedishmuel} \u8212- it teaches that intercourse on {\i Shabbat} is permitted even where it might entail some continued bleeding, in accordance with Shmuel\u8217's teaching. {\i De\u8217'amar Shmuel pirtzah dechukah mutteres likannes bah beShabbos ve\u8217'af al pi shemeshir tzeroros} \u8212- Shmuel taught with the image of a narrow breach in a stone wall: one may pass through it on {\i Shabbat} even if a few stones fall away in the process. The ongoing friction of intercourse that produces some residual bleeding is analogous. However, this does not straightforwardly prove permissibility of first intercourse on {\i Shabbat}, since the situation after the hymen has already been ruptured is meaningfully different.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatan Shema\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RY Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef raises a further challenge. The question is whether the four-night framework in the context of the {\i chatan}\u8217's exemption from {\i Shema} includes {\i Shabbat} night \u8212- and what this might imply about the permissibility of first intercourse on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatan Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i chassan patur mikrias shema laylah harishon} \u8212- a groom is exempt from reciting {\i Shema} on the first wedding night, because his mind is occupied by the fact that he is going to have intercourse. This exemption extends {\i ad motzei Shabbos} \u8212- until {\i motzei Shabbat} \u8212- {\i im lo asa ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- if he has not yet had intercourse. The structure here differs from the earlier {\i Mishnah}: there, bleeding continued for four nights after intercourse had occurred; here, the exemption applies for four nights because he is still in the process of attempting first intercourse. If they did not succeed the first night, they will be trying again; the second night he is still planning it; and so on for four nights, after which a certain {\i ye\u8217'ush} \u8212- a giving up \u8212- may set in. That intercourse may not have been consummated even after several attempts is a real phenomenon, with causes that may be psychological, physiological, or medical, and the {\i gemara} acknowledges it here matter-of-factly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} generally understands the {\i chatan}\u8217's exemption in one of two ways. The plain reading of the {\i Mishnayos} is that someone who is distracted \u8212- particularly by sexual anticipation, but perhaps more broadly \u8212- is exempt from {\i davening} and from {\i Shema}. The {\i gemara} in {\i Sukkah}, and the present sugya as well, frames it in terms of {\i osek bemitzvah} \u8212- being occupied with a {\i mitzvah}. Since the groom is mentally engaged in planning to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of first intercourse, he is considered involved in the {\i mitzvah} and therefore exempt. This is an unusual form of {\i osek bemitzvah}, since the engagement is purely mental rather than active, but the {\i gemara} treats it as sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava argues that the four-night exemption \u8212- which explicitly includes Friday night \u8212- proves that the groom intends to have first intercourse on {\i Shabbat} night. If first intercourse were forbidden on {\i Shabbat}, there would be no reason to extend the exemption to Friday night; the {\i chatan} would know he cannot have intercourse that night and his mind would not be occupied with it. The exemption on Friday night is thus evidence that first intercourse on {\i Shabbat} is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye counters: {\i lo de-tarid de-lo ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the groom is not planning to have intercourse on Friday night; he remains distracted nonetheless because he is anguished over not having succeeded the previous two nights. His mind cannot focus on {\i Shema} not because he is about to engage in a {\i mitzvah} that night but because the distress of not having accomplished it yet has not left him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Osek Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Reframe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's counter reframes the entire discussion. He is not arguing about whether first intercourse on {\i Shabbat} is permitted. He is arguing that the Tannaitic debate about the {\i chatan}\u8217's exemption from {\i Shema} concerns the grounds for that exemption \u8212- not whether intercourse on {\i Shabbat} is allowed. One view holds that the exemption is grounded in {\i osek bemitzvah} \u8212- active involvement in a {\i mitzvah} that one is about to perform \u8212- and on Friday night, if he cannot have intercourse, there is no such {\i osek}. The other view holds that the exemption is rooted in distraction, and even without intercourse planned for that night, his ongoing distress about not having yet succeeded is sufficient to exempt him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avel Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges Abaye\u8217's position: {\i u-mishum tirda patur?} \u8212- does mere distraction exempt someone from {\i Shema}? If so, a person whose boat sank in the sea, or who suffered a catastrophic financial loss, would also be exempt from {\i Shema} due to his inability to concentrate. {\i Hachi nami de-patur?} The implications would be far-reaching. And if you were to say yes \u8212- that such a person is also exempt \u8212- {\i ve-ha amar Rabbi Abba bar Zavda amar Rav: avel chayav be-chol ha-mitzvot ha-amurot ba-Torah chutz min ha-tefillin} \u8212- an {\i avel} is obligated in all {\i mitzvot} enumerated in the Torah except for {\i tefillin}, because {\i tefillin} are called {\i pe\u8217'er} and it is not fitting for a mourner to adorn himself with glory during {\i shiva}. But the {\i avel} is obligated in {\i Shema}. Yet surely an {\i avel} is profoundly distracted by grief and anguish over the deceased. If distraction exempts, why is an {\i avel} obligated?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi addresses this problem at a point further along in the {\i gemara} and his answer is instructive. He explains: {\i amar lach hacha nami betirda pligi} \u8212- and the {\i Tanna} who exempts does so for the following reason: {\i lav mishum deshurei lemeyal} \u8212- it is not because he is going to have intercourse that night, {\i ella kasavar mista\u8217'er tza\u8217'ar shel mitzvah patur mikriyas shema} \u8212- but rather because one who is suffering a {\i tza\u8217'ar shel mitzvah} \u8212- a distress that is rooted in a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- is exempt from {\i Shema}. The groom on Friday night, even if not having intercourse, is anguished because he has failed for two nights to fulfill a {\i mitzvah}. That {\i mitzvah}-rooted anguish is a {\i tza\u8217'ar shel mitzvah}, and it is that specific category of distress \u8212- not generalized distraction \u8212- that exempts him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains why the {\i avel} is not exempt. Rashi writes: {\i velo dami le-ovel detarid tirda d\u8217'reshus} \u8212- it is not similar to an {\i avel}, whose distraction is not {\i mitzvah}-related. {\i She\u8217'ein tza\u8217'aro mitzvah} \u8212- his anguish is not itself a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- {\i af al pi she\u8217'aveiluso mitzvah} \u8212- even though his {\i aveilut} is a {\i mitzvah}. This is a striking claim. Rashi appears to be distinguishing between the {\i mitzvah} of {\i aveilut} \u8212- its external observances \u8212- and the internal experience of grief itself. The {\i mitzvah} of {\i aveilut} consists of behavioral expressions of mourning; the anguish one feels, while natural and perhaps even appropriate, is not itself commanded. The groom\u8217's distress, by contrast, is directly caused by his not yet having fulfilled the {\i mitzvah} of {\i beilat mitzvah}; his suffering is a consequence of an unperformed {\i mitzvah}, and that renders it a {\i tza\u8217'ar shel mitzvah}. This is genuinely difficult: even granting Rashi\u8217's distinction between {\i aveilut} as a {\i mitzvah} and grief as an emotional state, it is not clear why the {\i avel}\u8217's very real distress would be any less of a legitimate exemption than the groom\u8217's frustration. The reason the {\i gemara} favors the {\i osek bemitzvah} framing over the distraction framework is precisely this: once you allow distraction to exempt, you have to explain why only certain distractions qualify, and that boundary is difficult to draw cleanly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Original Sugya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now arrives at what is likely the original {\i sugya} underlying the entire discussion. A {\i baraita} states explicitly: {\i ha-koneh et ha-besulah lo yavo betchilah be-Shabbat} \u8212- one who marries a virgin may not have intercourse for the first time on {\i Shabbat}. {\i Ve-chachamim matirin} \u8212- and the {\i chachamim} permit it. Here at last is a direct Tannaitic dispute on the question that has occupied the entire passage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim ID\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava identifies the {\i chachamim}: {\i amar Rava Rabbi Shimon hi} \u8212- it is {\i Rabbi Shimon}, {\i de\u8217'amar davar she-eino mitkaven mutar} \u8212- who holds that an unintended act is permitted. This {\i baraita} is almost certainly the original {\i sugya}. The elaborate logical framework constructed over the preceding pages \u8212- the decision tree of different scenarios and their {\i issur} or {\i heter} \u8212- is the work of the {\i stam}, the later anonymous redactional layer. The underlying Tannaitic source is this single {\i baraita}, a debate attributed to {\i Rabbi Shimon} on the permissibility of first intercourse on {\i Shabbat} based on the principle of {\i eino mitkaven}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baki Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesik Reisha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye immediately presses: {\i ve-modeh Rabbi Shimon be-pesik reisha ve-lo yamut} \u8212- and {\i Rabbi Shimon} concedes that {\i eino miskavein} does not exempt in a case of {\i pesik reisha}. If the hymen will inevitably rupture during intercourse, how can the {\i chachamim} permit it on the basis of {\i eino miskavein}? Is this not a {\i pesik reisha} \u8212- an inevitable consequence \u8212- and therefore no different from chopping off a chicken\u8217's head while claiming not to have intended its death?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baki Can Avoid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: {\i lo ka-elu Bavlayim she-ein beki\u8217'in be-hataya} \u8212- unlike those Babylonians who do not know how to incline, {\i ela yesh beki\u8217'in be-hataya} \u8212- there are those who know how to incline, meaning it is possible to engage in intercourse in a manner \u8212- at a more horizontal or angled approach \u8212- that does not rupture the hymen. If such a technique exists, then rupture is not inevitable, and the case is not a {\i pesik reisha}. The exemption of {\i eino miskavein} therefore remains applicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baki Tensions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer generates its own difficulties. {\i Im kein tarud lamah} \u8212- if men are capable of having intercourse without rupturing the hymen, why is the {\i chatan} so distracted? The whole basis of the exemption from {\i Shema} is that his mind is consumed by the difficulty of what he is attempting. If the {\i baki} performs it smoothly without rupture, why is there such distress? Conversely, if he is distracted because he is {\i she-eino baki} \u8212- not an expert \u8212- how can we permit the act on {\i Shabbat} on the grounds that it is not a {\i pesik reisha}? The permission rests on the assumption that a {\i baki} can avoid rupture; but the distraction argument seems to assume the person is {\i she-eino baki}. These cannot both apply to the same person. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i tarud lamah l\u8217'she-eino baki} \u8212- the distraction applies to the non-expert. But this generates yet another difficulty: {\i yomru baki mutar she-eino baki assur} \u8212- are we going to rule that an expert may have intercourse on {\i Shabbat} and a non-expert may not? This seems practically untenable, and conceptually bizarre. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i rov baki\u8217'in hen} \u8212- the majority of men are in fact {\i baki\u8217'in}. Yet this answer is deeply puzzling. How do most men, expected to arrive at the wedding night without prior sexual experience, come to possess such expertise? And even granting that the majority are {\i baki\u8217'in}, one would expect the rule to follow the {\i individual} before us rather than the statistical majority. The {\i gemara} here is genuinely difficult, and {\i Tosafot}\u8217's attempt to reconcile the tensions is strained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groomsmen Custom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava bar Rav Chanan raises a final challenge to Abaye: if men can and typically do have intercourse without rupturing the hymen, {\i shushbinin lamah mapah lamah} \u8212- why do the groomsmen wait outside the room and why is a pre-verified sheet prepared? The practice of {\i parsu hasimlah lifnei ziknei ha\u8217'ir} \u8212- spreading the sheet before the elders of the city \u8212- is described in the {\i Torah} itself as a means of verifying the bride\u8217's virginity through evidence of hymenal blood. Why establish an elaborate verification system if rupture is not reliably expected? The answer given is: {\i hasam shema yireh ve-yoveid} \u8212- the concern is not that rupture will fail to occur but that the evidence, once produced, may be lost before the groomsmen can confirm it. The custom of the {\i shushbinin} and the pre-checked {\i mapah} exists to ensure that if there is blood it is found and preserved, not to verify that rupture occurred. Rashi adds that if rupture did not occur and there is therefore no blood, the wife simply says the hymen has not yet been ruptured and they will repeat the act \u8212- and this time evidence will be present. The larger concern driving the institution is thus the preservation of evidence, not the occurrence of rupture.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 7\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blister Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Sex on Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya on daf 7b continues the discussion of {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah} \u8212- first-time sexual relations \u8212- on Shabbat, focusing on the concern that breaking the hymen may constitute a prohibited act. The Gemara picks up toward the bottom of 6b with the case of {\i hamefis mursa b\u8217'Shabbos}: one who lances a blister on Shabbat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blister Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is as follows: {\i im la\u8217'asos lah peh} \u8212- if the person\u8217's intent is to create an opening in the blister \u8212- {\i chayav}, he is liable. But {\i im l\u8217'hotzi mimenu leicha} \u8212- if his intent is merely to extract the pus \u8212- {\i patur}, he is exempt. The question that naturally arises is: for what exactly is one liable when making an opening? This is not an act of wounding in the conventional sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Opening = Boneh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains: {\i im niskavin la\u8217'asos lah peh, shemiskayvein l\u8217'yafosah, lihayos lah pesach zeh l\u8217'yom achar, chayav shehu mesakein pesach, v\u8217'chayav mishum boneh} \u8212- if one intends to create an opening so that the blister will have a fixed aperture going forward, this constitutes fixing an opening, and he is liable on account of {\i boneh}, the prohibited labor of building. Rashi cites the verse describing the creation of Chava \u8212- {\i vayiven es hatzela} \u8212- as the prototype for this category, the same source he invoked earlier in the discussion about first-time intercourse. The parallel is noted, though it remains conceptually curious why creating a small drainage opening in a blister should rise to the level of {\i boneh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pakid vs.\u160 ?Akir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then turns to the second half of the ruling \u8212- the exemption when one intends only to extract the pus \u8212- and considers how this relates to the earlier discussion about blood released during first intercourse. The Gemara\u8217's answer is: {\i hasam pakid v\u8217'akir, hacha pakid v\u8217'lo akir} \u8212- in the blister case, the pus is both stored and already fully uprooted from the flesh, contained entirely outside the skin. In the case of the hymen, the blood may be stored behind it, but it is not yet uprooted from within the skin. When the hymen breaks, blood is still being released from within the skin \u8212- it is bleeding in the full sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer is somewhat puzzling. One might have expected the Gemara simply to say that the blister case involves pus, not blood, and that the prohibition of {\i chavala} \u8212- wounding \u8212- is fundamentally defined by the extraction of blood. The Gemara\u8217's distinction between {\i pakid v\u8217'akir} and {\i pakid v\u8217'lo akir} seems to assume that the pus in the blister may contain some blood, which is why the answer focuses on degrees of uprootedness rather than on the substance itself. One might also ask what the Gemara\u8217's question was in the first place: the physical question of whether hymenal blood is truly comparable to blood drawn through a wound is not obviously answered by a case about pus in a blister. The Gemara\u8217's conclusion is simply that the blister case involves material already fully outside the skin, while the hymenal case does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pattur Aval Mutar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further point arises regarding the category of {\i patur} in the blister case. As established at the beginning of Maseches Shabbos, {\i patur} on Shabbat ordinarily means {\i patur aval assur} \u8212- exempt from liability but still forbidden. There are, however, three exceptions where {\i patur} means {\i patur aval mutar} \u8212- exempt and actually permitted. This blister case is one of them, on account of the element of {\i tza\u8217'ar}, physical suffering, involved. Rashi confirms this reading.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Amoraim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami Permits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that digression resolved, the Gemara returns to the central question of first-time intercourse on Shabbat. {\i Rav Ami sharah l\u8217'meiva batchilah b\u8217'Shabbos} \u8212- Rav Ami permitted {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah} on Shabbat. The debate among the {\i Tannaim} and earlier {\i Amoraim} on this question evidently continued into later generations. Upon hearing Rav Ami\u8217's ruling, the rabbis of the academy challenged him: {\i ha lo kesiva kesuva} \u8212- but the {\i ketuva} has not yet been written! The assumption behind this objection is that if the wedding took place on Friday afternoon or Friday night, there would not have been time to write the {\i ketuva} before Shabbat. And a husband and wife are not permitted to live together without a {\i ketuva} \u8212- indeed, the Gemara will later characterize such an arrangement as resembling {\i be\u8217'as znus}, because without the protections of the {\i ketuva} the wife lacks the commitment and security that sanctify the relationship and guard against capricious divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketuva & Collateral\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ami\u8217's response was: {\i atfuso matalten} \u8212- in the case under discussion, the woman had been given movable property as collateral to hold until the {\i ketuva} could be formally written. This ensures that even before the document is drawn up, she has concrete security for her eventual collection in the event of divorce. This exchange signals a broader theme of Maseches Ketubot: it is not merely the act of {\i nisuin} itself \u8212- the {\i chuppah}, the {\i sheva brachot} \u8212- that constitutes a fully sanctioned marriage. The protections of the {\i ketuva} are themselves a constitutive element of a committed marital relationship, providing reciprocal obligations and security for both parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Other Authorities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then records additional authorities who permitted {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah} in similar circumstances. {\i Rav Zvid sharah l\u8217'meiva betechila b\u8217'Shabbos} \u8212- Rav Zvid likewise permitted it, and some report that he personally did so at his own wedding. {\i Rav Yehuda sharah l\u8217'meiva betechila b\u8217'Yom Tov} \u8212- Rav Yehuda permitted it on Yom Tov.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappi vs.\u160 ?Pappa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara records a significant dispute between two authorities, each transmitting a ruling in the name of Rava. Rav Pappi in the name of Rava held: {\i lo teima b\u8217'Yom Tov desharei ha b\u8217'Shabbos assur} \u8212- do not suppose that Rava made a distinction between Yom Tov and Shabbat. Rather, {\i hu hadin d\u8217'afilu b\u8217'Shabbos nami sharei} \u8212- the same ruling applies even on Shabbat. The case simply happened to arise on Yom Tov. Rav Pappa in the name of Rava, however, held precisely the opposite: {\i b\u8217'Yom Tov sharei b\u8217'Shabbos assur} \u8212- Rava specifically permitted {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah} on Yom Tov but not on Shabbat. The question is immediately apparent: whether the issue is {\i petach} or {\i chavala}, the physical concern is identical on Yom Tov and Shabbat. What could be the basis for a distinction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitoch & Shaveh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa explains to Rav Pappi: {\i mitoch shehutra chabura letzorech hutra nami shelo letzorech} \u8212- since wounding ({\i chavala}) is permitted on Yom Tov for a purpose, namely {\i shechitah} for food as taught at the beginning of Maseches Beitzah, it is therefore permitted even in a context that is not directly related to food preparation. The concern about {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah}, on this view, is primarily a concern of {\i chavala} rather than {\i petach}, and on Yom Tov a specific leniency applies through the principle of {\i mitoch}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa then challenges this reasoning: {\i aval ela me\u8217'ata mutar la\u8217'asos mugmar b\u8217'Yom Tov} \u8212- if {\i mitoch} works that broadly, one should similarly be permitted to burn incense on Yom Tov, since fire ({\i havara}) is permitted on Yom Tov for cooking, and by {\i mitoch shehutra havara letzorech hutra nami shelo letzorech}, burning for any purpose should follow. The implicit premise of the objection is that burning incense is in fact {\i assur} on Yom Tov. The answer given is: {\i alecha amar kra, ach asher ye\u8217'achel lechol nefesh} \u8212- the verse says \u8220"only that which shall be eaten by every soul,\u8221" indicating that only something that is {\i davar hashaveh lechol nefesh} \u8212- universally beneficial, something that every person appreciates \u8212- may be performed on Yom Tov outside of direct food preparation. Burning incense benefits some people and repels others; it does not meet this threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This Gemara is a central {\i sugya} for understanding the two distinct models of Yom Tov leniency. One model, the blanket {\i mitoch}, tends to apply to fire-making and carrying \u8212- two {\i melachot} that are somewhat conceptually marginal: carrying is described by the {\i Rishonim} as a {\i melacha geru\u8217'a} because it is not inherently creative, and fire, though the one {\i melacha} explicitly mentioned in the Torah ({\i lo teva\u8217'aru bechol moshvoteichem beyom haShabbat}), has an opinion among the {\i Tannaim} that one is not {\i chayav misa} for it on Shabbat. By contrast, other {\i melachot} require something analogous to the permitted act of eating \u8212- something {\i shaveh lechol nefesh}, a pleasure or need that is both universal and bodily. This Gemara insists on this second model. Under this model, the distinction between sex and burning incense is clear: sexual relations are a basic human need and pleasure, universal in scope, whereas burning incense is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex vs.\u160 ?Incense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot elaborates on the {\i mitoch} principle, explaining: {\i perush l\u8217'tzorecho shel nefesh, ubilvad sheyihiyeh tzorech hana\u8217'at hayom o tzorech kiyum mitzva b\u8217'Yom Tov kigone hotza\u8217'at katan u\u8217'l\u8217'molo v\u8217'Sefer Torah likros bo} \u8212- it must serve some legitimate purpose, whether for personal enjoyment or for fulfilling a {\i mitzva} on Yom Tov, such as carrying a child to circumcise him or carrying a {\i Sefer Torah} for reading. Tosafot does not require {\i shaveh lechol nefesh} for the {\i mitoch} of carrying. This reinforces the point that the two models operate differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution \u8212- that {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah} on Yom Tov is permitted because {\i chavala} in that context is {\i shaveh lechol nefesh} while incense-burning is not \u8212- is itself worth examining. It is true that sexual relations in general are a universal human pleasure. One might raise the point that first-time intercourse, from the woman\u8217's perspective particularly, is not the pleasurable experience the category implies, and that the {\i chavala} component is not itself pleasurable at all. The Gemara, it must be said, is approaching this entirely from the man\u8217's perspective. Nevertheless, the principle being invoked is that within the context of a pleasure that is universally {\i shaveh lechol nefesh}, a {\i chavala} that is necessary to enable that pleasure is subsumed under the leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deer Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presents a further challenge: {\i nizdamen lo tzvi b\u8217'Yom Tov, ho\u8217'il v\u8217'eino shaveh lechol nefesh, hachi nami d\u8217'assur l\u8217'mishchatei} \u8212- would you say one is forbidden to slaughter a deer on Yom Tov because venison is a rare delicacy, not something universally enjoyed? The response is: {\i ana davar hatzarich lekhol nefesh ka\u8217'amina} \u8212- the rule of {\i shaveh lechol nefesh} applies to things that are {\i necessary} for all people, not merely enjoyed by all. The underlying logic seems to be that since deer is food, it falls under the general category of eating, and the leniency for food preparation on Yom Tov does not require that every individual enjoy that particular food. The {\i shaveh lechol nefesh} requirement operates to extend Yom Tov leniency to things that are {\i not} food-related, by analogy to food; once something is food, the ordinary rules of Yom Tov cooking apply regardless of the specific delicacy involved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yochanan & Lore\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hora\u8217'ah Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records: {\i hora Rabbi Yochanan b\u8217'Tzaidan, assur lavo betechila b\u8217'Shabbat} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan ruled in Tzidon that {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah} on Shabbat is forbidden. The Gemara immediately questions whether a ruling to forbid can properly be called a {\i hora\u8217'ah} at all. {\i Umi ika hora\u8217'ah le\u8217'issur} \u8212- is there such a thing as authoritative adjudication in the direction of prohibition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matir Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's explanation is illuminating. He writes: {\i mi kari issur hora\u8217'ah mishum dechol adam matzi lehachmir, she\u8217'afilu min hasafek she\u8217'ein lo barur lo hu ba ve\u8217'oser} \u8212- calling something forbidden does not demonstrate rabbinic authority because anyone can be strict, and even one who is uncertain about the law can simply declare something forbidden without any real knowledge or tradition behind the ruling. {\i Aval hamatir somech al shmu\u8217'ato o sevara chochmato vehi hora\u8217'ah} \u8212- but one who rules permissively must be relying either on a received tradition or on the strength of his own scholarly judgment, and that is what constitutes genuine {\i hora\u8217'ah}. The ability to say {\i mutar} is the true mark of judicial authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is illustrated by a famous anecdote involving the Noda BiYehuda. A {\i shochet} from a neighboring town would make the long journey to ask his {\i treifa} questions, but eventually stopped coming. When he resumed, he explained that he had found a simpler method: upon reading the verse {\i uvasar basadeh treifa lo socheilu lakelev tashlichun oto}, he realized he could simply throw questionable meat to the dog \u8212- if the dog ate it, it was {\i treifa}; if not, it was kosher. The Noda BiYehuda asked why he had then returned. The {\i shochet} replied: \u8220"The dog was too much of a {\i machmir}.\u8221" The point is well taken: being strict requires no expertise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers two examples of {\i hora\u8217'ah le\u8217'issur} to complicate this principle. First: {\i horu Beis Hillel shetei nezirut sheva shanim acherot} \u8212- Beis Hillel ruled that Heleni HaMalka, having fulfilled her vow of {\i nezirut} in {\i chutz l\u8217'aretz}, had to repeat the full seven years upon entering Eretz Yisrael. While this ruling is strict, it is a genuine {\i chiddush} \u8212- the ruling that her vow did not count requires real knowledge. Second: {\i chut hashidra shenifsak berubo, divrei Rabbi; Rabbi Yaakov omer afilu nikev; hora Rebbe ke-Rabbi Yaakov} \u8212- Rebbe first ruled that the spinal cord must be severed in its majority to render an animal a {\i treifa}, then reversed himself and ruled, like Rabbi Yaakov, that even perforation suffices. The latter case is more directly relevant because Rebbe\u8217's reversal could be understood as mere stringency rather than authoritative ruling. The Gemara accepts that such language can be used even for a strict ruling. {\i Amar Rav Huna: ein halacha ke-Rabbi Yaakov} \u8212- and in fact we do not rule like Rabbi Yaakov; the cord must be severed in its majority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presents the full account. Rabbi Abbahu reports: {\i sha\u8217'al Rabbi Yishmael ben Yaakov d\u8217'min Tzur et Rabbi Yochanan b\u8217'Tzaidan, ve-ana shemi\u8217'ah} \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael the son of Yaakov from Tzur asked Rabbi Yochanan in Tzidon whether {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah} on Shabbat is permitted, and Rabbi Abbahu overheard the response. {\i Ve-amar lei assur} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan ruled it forbidden. Nevertheless: {\i ve-hilchata b\u8217'tzlachot mutar liv\u8217'ol bitchilah b\u8217'Shabbat} \u8212- the final {\i halakha} is that it is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting, as Tosafot observes, that Rabbi Yochanan is an {\i Amora} of Eretz Yisrael, while most of the {\i Amoraim} who ruled permissively throughout this {\i sugya} appear to be Babylonian. The {\i hilcheta} here follows the Babylonian {\i Amoraim}. Tosafot at the end of this passage adds: {\i ve-hilchata mutar liv\u8217'ol bitchilah b\u8217'Shabbat, v\u8217'lo chayshinan shema yishchot ben of} \u8212- we do not also worry that the groom will come to perform {\i shechitah} for the wedding meal and accidentally violate another {\i melacha} in the process. {\i V\u8217'aha samchinan l\u8217'me\u8217'evad seuda b\u8217'Shabbat} \u8212- and it is on this basis that the Ashkenazic custom developed to marry on Friday afternoon and hold the wedding {\i seuda} on Shabbat, a practice that also had the practical benefit of allowing the costs of Shabbat hospitality to serve double duty as the wedding meal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sheva Brachot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} now transitions from {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah} to the laws of {\i birchat chatanim} \u8212- the wedding blessings. {\i Amar Rabbi Chelbo amar Rav Huna amar Rabbi Abba bar Zavda amar Rav: achas betulah v\u8217'achas almanah, te\u8217'unah bracha} \u8212- whether the bride is a {\i betulah} or an {\i almanah} (meaning a woman marrying for the second time), a {\i bracha} is required. The term {\i birchat chatanim} is the language the Gemara uses throughout; it does not use the phrase {\i sheva brachot}, in part because the Geonic period later added the {\i borei pri hagefen} to bring the count to seven, and the Gemara\u8217's set consists of six blessings. The {\i Mishnah} in Megillah establishes that {\i birchat chatanim} requires a {\i minyan} of ten: {\i ein birchat chatanim pachot m\u8217'asarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The teaching uses the striking formulation {\i te\u8217'unah bracha} \u8212- she requires a {\i bracha}, literally, the {\i bracha} is incumbent upon her. This language, centering the woman as the subject to whom the blessing attaches, will become conceptually significant as the discussion develops.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duration Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges Rav Huna\u8217's ruling by citing a contradictory statement attributed to him: {\i almanah einah te\u8217'unah bracha} \u8212- for a widow, no {\i bracha} is required. The Gemara resolves this with the distinction: {\i kan b\u8217'bachur shenasa almanah, kan b\u8217'alman shenasa almanah} \u8212- in one case a bachelor married a widow, in the other a widower married a widow. The principle that emerges is that if for either member of the couple this is a first marriage, the full seven days of {\i sheva brachot} are observed; if for both it is a second marriage, only one day is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also important to note the conceptual distinction between the {\i brachot} recited under the {\i chuppah} and the {\i brachot} recited at the meals during the week. The {\i Rambam} rules that if the {\i sheva brachot} were not recited at the time of {\i nisuin}, they may be recited many days later \u8212- but one cannot make up for {\i sheva brachot} missed at the meal by reciting them at a different time within the week. The {\i brachot} under the {\i chuppah} are attached to the act of {\i nisuin} itself, while the meal {\i brachot} belong to the ongoing celebration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara further addresses a {\i braita} that teaches: {\i she\u8217'kedu chachamim al takanas benos Yisrael she\u8217'yesame\u8217'ach imah gimel yamim} \u8212- the Sages enacted that a husband should rejoice with his wife for three days, which was achieved by the practice of marrying an {\i almanah} on Thursday, yielding Thursday, Friday, and Shabbat together. The Gemara asks: if it is a first marriage for him, do you not say seven days of {\i sheva brachot}? And if it is a second marriage for him, do you not say only one day? What then is the meaning of \u8220"three days\u8221"? The Gemara offers two resolutions: either it is a case of a second marriage for both, where the {\i bracha} requirement is one day but {\i simcha} continues for three; or it is a first marriage for him, where {\i brachot} extend for seven days but the three days refers to a separate rabbinic expectation of sustained {\i simcha} and companionship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a further challenge from a {\i braita}: {\i mevarchin b\u8217'betulah sheva ule\u8217'almanah yom echad} \u8212- we bless for seven days for a {\i betulah} and one day for an {\i almanah}. The {\i braita} makes no mention of the man\u8217's status. Does this not imply that the distinction is entirely based on the woman\u8217's status \u8212- a widow who marries a bachelor would still receive only one day?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this: {\i lo, kan b\u8217'alman shenasa almanah} \u8212- the {\i braita} refers only to the case where the man is also a widower. If a bachelor marries a widow, the ruling would be seven days. The {\i braita}\u8217's formulation is {\i milta psikta katani} \u8212- it states things in absolute terms: a {\i betulah} never has fewer than seven days, and an {\i almanah} never has fewer than one day, but sometimes an {\i almanah} also receives seven. The Gemara\u8217's answer is admittedly not entirely satisfying, as the {\i braita} does seem to ground the distinction entirely in the woman\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Shift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation leads to an important conceptual observation. The original framing of these laws \u8212- much like the {\i ketuva} system itself \u8212- orients entirely around the woman\u8217's status: is she a {\i betulah} or not, is her {\i ketuva} 200 {\i zuz} or 100 {\i zuz}? In that framework, the degree of communal celebration is entirely a function of what type of woman is being married. The Gemara\u8217's reframing, by introducing the man\u8217's prior marital status as the determining variable, represents a significant shift: the duration of {\i sheva brachot} now reflects the {\i simcha} of the couple as a couple \u8212- the degree to which this is a new beginning for either of them \u8212- rather than the personal status of the bride alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction carries conceptual weight. The {\i sheva brachot} are a communal celebration, which is why a {\i minyan} is required. The extent to which the community continues to celebrate corresponds to the degree of novelty and joy for both parties. If for both it is a second marriage, the communal celebration is more abbreviated. If for either it is a first, the full week of celebration is warranted. The three days of {\i simcha} in the {\i braita}, by contrast, belong to the couple themselves \u8212- a period of time spent together at the outset of their shared life, regardless of prior marital history.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minyan Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minyan Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i birchat chatanim} requires a {\i minyan}, the Gemara now examines the sources for this requirement. The {\i Mishnah} in Megillah states it as a rule alongside other rabbinic acts requiring ten men, but what is the textual or exegetical basis?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruth 4 Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Nachman amar lei Huna bar Nasan: tana, minayin l\u8217'virchat chatanim b\u8217'asarah? Shene\u8217'emar, vayikach asarah anashim mizeknei ha\u8217'ir vayomer shevu po vayeisheivu} \u8212- Rav Nachman, citing a tradition from Huna bar Nasan, grounds the requirement in Ruth 4:2, where Boaz took ten elders of the city and said, \u8220"Sit here,\u8221" and they sat. The blessings given to Boaz and Ruth by the assembled elders and people at the gate serve as the prototype for {\i birchat chatanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Makor Imagery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu derives the {\i minyan} requirement from a different verse: {\i Bemakhelos barchu Elokim Hashem mimakor Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"In the assemblies, bless God, the Lord, from the {\i makor} of Israel\u8221" (Psalms 68:27). {\i Makor} here points to matters connected with marriage and reproduction, and {\i bemakhelos} implies a gathering \u8212- hence a {\i minyan} of ten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Makor Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks what each authority does with the verse cited by the other. Rav Nachman uses the verse from Psalms for a different {\i drasha} altogether, as cited in a {\i braita}: {\i haya Rabbi Meir omer minayin she\u8217'afilu ubarim she-bimei imanim amru shira al ha-yam} \u8212- Rabbi Meir asks how we know that even fetuses in their mothers\u8217' wombs burst forth in {\i shira} at the crossing of the {\i Yam Suf}. The answer is from this very verse: {\i Hashem mimakor Yisrael} \u8212- praise arose from the {\i makor}, the womb, meaning from within the bodies of the Israelite women. On this reading, {\i makor} means womb, the source of fetal life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu responds: {\i im ken limra kra mibeten} \u8212- if the verse meant only to indicate the womb, it should have used the word {\i beten}. The use of {\i makor} instead points to something more specific: {\i al iskei makor} \u8212- related to matters of the {\i makor} itself. And {\i makor}, as is clear from the verse {\i es mekora hera} used in the context of marital law, refers not to the womb but to the genitals themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking and theologically significant point. If {\i birchat chatanim} finds its scriptural anchor in the verse {\i Hashem mimakor Yisrael}, and {\i makor} there refers to the genitals rather than the womb, then the blessings are oriented toward the sexual union of husband and wife rather than toward fertility and children. Indeed, a close reading of the text of the {\i sheva brachot} bears this out: there is no explicit reference to children in the blessings themselves. The {\i brachot} speak of the creation of {\i Adam} and {\i Chava}, of the joining of {\i chatan} and {\i kallah}, of the joy each finds with the other \u8212- but not explicitly of procreation. The implicit theme of {\i birchat chatanim}, understood in light of this {\i drasha}, is the sanctification of the sexual union itself, set in the context of creation and covenant. The {\i bracha} of {\i birchat eirusin} makes this even more explicit: {\i assar lanu es ha-arusos ve-hittir lanu es ha-nessuos} \u8212- He forbade for us the {\i arusos} and permitted for us the {\i nessuos} \u8212- the blessing is directly about the permissibility of marital sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pirsum Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then returns to the question of what Rav Nachman does with the verse in Ruth 4. On the surface, the ten elders there were needed to formalize the legal transaction \u8212- the redemption and the {\i chalitzah}-like proceeding \u8212- not to recite {\i brachot}. Rabbi Abbahu therefore says that what Rav Nachman needs the verse for is a different {\i drasha} entirely: {\i lemidrash amoni velo amonit moavi velo moavit} \u8212- to publicize the ruling that the prohibition on Moabites and Ammonites applies only to males, not females, and this is why Boaz needed elders present before he could marry Ruth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: if the ten were needed for the {\i drasha}, why specifically elders rather than any ten people? And if needed for the {\i bracha}, why elders rather than any {\i minyan}? The Gemara\u8217's answer is: {\i lefirsumei milta} \u8212- to publicize the matter. The {\i zekeinim} serve as a {\i beis din} for the legal ruling; the ten are needed for {\i pirsum}, public announcement. This is illustrated by the story of Shmuel who told Rav Chanan: {\i puk veaisi li bei asarah, ve-ima lach b\u8217'anpaihu: hamizakeh le\u8217'ubar kanah} \u8212- go gather ten people, and I will declare in their presence that a transfer of property to a fetus is legally effective. The need for ten here is specifically to publicize an innovative ruling. (The Gemara adds parenthetically: {\i ve-hilchata hamizakeh le\u8217'ubar lo kanah} \u8212- the final ruling does not, in fact, follow this view.)\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brachot Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatanim Brachot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} teaches: {\i mevarchin birchat chatanim beveis chatanim} \u8212- the {\i birchat chatanim} is recited in the house of the {\i chatanim}, meaning at the wedding venue or in the context of the {\i nisuin} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda Eirusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda, however, says: {\i af beveis ha\u8217'irusin mevarchin osah} \u8212- one also recites the blessings at the {\i eirusin}, the betrothal. This is not merely a question of physical location but of timing: Rabbi Yehuda holds that {\i birchat chatanim} \u8212- what we would understand as the blessings associated with {\i nisuin} \u8212- is recited not only at the time of {\i nisuin} but already at {\i kidushin}, which could precede the {\i nisuin} by months or years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye explains: {\i uviYehuda shanu, mipnei shemisyached imah} \u8212- this applies specifically in Yehuda, where the practice was that after {\i kidushin}, even before {\i nisuin}, the couple would spend time alone together in the bride\u8217's father\u8217's house in order to grow comfortable with one another. Since seclusion could lead to sexual relations \u8212- not as an explicit intention but as a natural result \u8212- special rules applied there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Kallah Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the necessity for {\i birchat chatanim} at this stage as follows: {\i kallah belo beracha asurah le-ba\u8217'alah keniddah} \u8212- a bride without having had {\i birchat chatanim} is forbidden to her husband like a {\i niddah}. On this view, the {\i sheva brachot} function as a {\i matir} \u8212- they are what renders marital sex permissible. Without them, the sexual act is forbidden. Therefore, if the couple in Yehuda might have sexual relations during the period of {\i eirusin}, the blessings must be recited beforehand to avoid a forbidden act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Nuance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's explanation differs somewhat. Tosafot writes: {\i u-lefi she-pe\u8217'amim ba aleha shelo leshem chuppah, osim beracha kedei shetehei kallah be-beracha} \u8212- since on occasion the husband would come to her not in the context of the formal {\i chuppah}, they recite the {\i bracha} so that the {\i kallah} should have the {\i bracha} in place. Tosafot does not go so far as Rashi in saying she is forbidden like a {\i niddah} without it; the concern is simply that there should be a {\i bracha} before any sexual act occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex vs.\u160 ?Joy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion raises a deeper conceptual question about the nature of {\i sheva brachot}. The blessings speak of {\i simcha}, of {\i chatan} and {\i kallah} rejoicing together, of {\i menucha} and {\i bayit ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- the language is that of marital joy and communal celebration. Yet here, the Gemara seems to root the necessity of the blessings specifically in the potential for sexual relations. The language of {\i te\u8217'unah bracha} \u8212- she requires a {\i bracha} \u8212- and the comparison to {\i niddah} point toward an understanding of {\i birchat chatanim} as a {\i matir}, a ritual permission-granting act, rather than simply a communal celebration. A parallel might be drawn to {\i birchat hanehnin} \u8212- blessings recited before deriving physical pleasure \u8212- where the act of blessing frames and sanctifies a physical experience rather than merely marking a social occasion. Whether {\i birchat chatanim} functions primarily as such a {\i matir} for marital sex, or primarily as a celebration of the couple\u8217's new life together, is a question the Gemara gestures toward without fully resolving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brachot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final {\i braita} clarifies: {\i mevarchin birchat chatanim b\u8217'veis chatanim u\u8217'virchat eirusin b\u8217'veis ha\u8217'eirusin} \u8212- the {\i birchat chatanim} is recited at the {\i nisuin}, and a separate {\i birchat eirusin} is recited at the {\i eirusin}. This is striking, because while {\i birchat chatanim} appears in the {\i Mishnah} in Megillah and has clear Tannaitic grounding, the idea of a distinct {\i birchat eirusin} has no explicit earlier source. The {\i Gemara} will open tomorrow\u8217's discussion with precisely this question \u8212- what is the {\i birchat eirusin}, and where does it come from?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sheva Brachos Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins embedded in the classic {\i sugya} concerning {\i birchas chasanim} \u8212- what we commonly call {\i sheva brachos} \u8212- and {\i birchas erusin}. The previous day\u8217's discussion had centered on the language of {\i te\u8217'una bracha}, the sense in which the {\i kalla} requires a {\i bracha}. Rashi and Tosafos, quoting Maseches Kalla, introduced the formulation {\i kalla blo bracha asura le\u8217'ba\u8217'ala} \u8212- that the {\i kalla} is in some sense forbidden and the {\i bracha} effects her permissibility. The {\i bracha} can thus be reframed as a sanctification of the act of marital relations, though the language also focuses quite specifically on the woman as the object being rendered permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruth & Boaz Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also raised the question of whether the requirement of a {\i minyan} derives from the {\i pasuk} concerning Ruth and Boaz \u8212- a blessing of their union by the assembled people \u8212- or from the verse {\i bemakhelos barchu Hashem mi\u8217'mekor Yisrael}, where {\i mekor} appears from the {\i gemara} to refer specifically to the sexual dimension rather than merely fertility. What emerges clearly is that this is a blessing given by the community to the couple for their union, with the sanctification of the sexual act as a central element. The {\i bracha} functions as a {\i matir} of sorts \u8212- not necessarily in a strictly technical sense, but as a framing that sanctifies and thereby enables the couple\u8217's union.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos also quotes a passage from Maseches Kalla concerning the blessing that Rivka\u8217's mother and brother gave her \u8212- a {\i bracha} recited before only two people, not a {\i minyan}, and directed to the {\i kalla} alone rather than the couple. As one participant astutely noted, that blessing \u8212- {\i achoseinu at hayi l\u8217'alfei revava} \u8212- is also a blessing of fertility rather than of the sexual union. Whether it constitutes a serious {\i makor} for {\i birchas chasanim} or for {\i birchas erusin} is therefore uncertain. Tosafos raises the possibility that it may serve as a source for {\i birchas erusin}, though that too seems somewhat tangential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin vs Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the {\i gemara} has established so far about {\i sheva brachos} is that it concerns {\i birchas nisuin} \u8212- the blessing of the couple\u8217's union, related to the sanctification of their shared life together. That the {\i minyan} is required for {\i birchas chasanim} makes sense precisely because this is a {\i bracha} of the {\i tzibur} to the {\i chasan} and {\i kalla}. The {\i gemara} had also noted the case of Yehuda, where the possibility of relations before {\i nisuin} \u8212- following {\i erusin} alone \u8212- might also require a {\i bracha}, which is somewhat ironic given that the text of the blessing clearly envisions the beginning of married life together. Yet that case focuses the mind on the sexual act as such, which is precisely what the blessing addresses. With that context established, the {\i gemara} now turns its attention to {\i birchas erusin}, a concept not found in any {\i mishna} \u8212- this is the first we hear of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birchat Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanya Idach Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces this with a {\i baraisa}: {\i mevarchin birchas chasanim be\u8217'beis chasan u\u8217'virchas erusin be\u8217'beis erusin} \u8212- we recite {\i birchas chasanim} in the home of the {\i chasan} and {\i birchas erusin} in the home of the {\i erusin}. What is the text of {\i birchas erusin}? Ravin bar Rav Adda and Rabba bar Rav Adda both say in the name of Rav Yehuda: {\i Baruch atta Hashem Elokeinu melech ha\u8217'olam asher kideshanu be\u8217'mitzvosav ve\u8217'tzivanu al ha\u8217'arayos ve\u8217'assar lanu es ha\u8217'arusos ve\u8217'hitir lanu es ha\u8217'nesuos}. Rabbeinu Tam added the word {\i lanu} to clarify that the only {\i nesua} with whom a man is permitted to have relations is the one married to him \u8212- {\i al yedei chupa ve\u8217'kiddushin}. Rav Acha the son of Rava concludes it in the name of Rav Yehuda with the {\i chasima}: {\i Baruch atta Hashem mekadesh Yisrael} \u8212- some versions have {\i amo Yisrael} \u8212- {\i al yedei chupa ve\u8217'kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asur/Hitir Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is immediately striking about this formulation is what it does {\i not} say. It could have said {\i asher kideshanu be\u8217'mitzvosav ve\u8217'tzivanu al ha\u8217'kiddushin} or {\i al ha\u8217'erusin}, yet instead it chooses to frame the entire blessing around the language of {\i isur} and {\i heter}: {\i ve\u8217'assar lanu es ha\u8217'arusos ve\u8217'hitir lanu es ha\u8217'nesuos al yedei chupa ve\u8217'kiddushin}. Once again, the focus falls squarely on marital relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is notable is that the {\i bracha} does not say {\i assar lanu penuyos} \u8212- it does not address the general prohibition of relations outside of marriage. What it says is that the {\i arusa} specifically is forbidden to us at this stage, and that she will become permitted when we proceed to {\i nisuin}. In a remarkable way, then, this is a {\i bracha} that openly acknowledges the present incompleteness of the marriage: we are not there yet. The {\i kiddushin} is a preparatory stage, and this blessing frames it as pointing forward toward the {\i nisuin} when the full married life will begin. The institution is described as one that operates in stages, and the {\i bracha} itself is constructed around that staging.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam 2 Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an appropriate place to note what the Rambam writes at the opening of Hilchos Ishus. Before {\i matan Torah}, he explains, the practice for a man and woman who wished to marry was simply to begin living together \u8212- marriage was a single, immediate act. This remains the structure for a non-Jew. The Torah introduced the innovation of two distinct stages: first {\i kiddushin}, then {\i nisuin}, after which they commence their shared life. The Torah divided into two what had previously been one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Contract\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the profound significance of this two-stage structure? If marriage begins entirely with the reality of living together, then the marriage is defined by that lived reality at any given moment. And reality fluctuates \u8212- there will be periods of tension, distance, and estrangement, times when the couple is not living as a married couple in any practical sense. Without something that precedes and transcends that lived reality, such moments would simply unravel the marriage itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kiddushin} is best understood as a binding contractual commitment that precedes the reality of married life precisely because there will be times when that reality falters. It establishes a legal and spiritual framework \u8212- not one that cannot be dissolved, but one that creates a genuine obligation to work through difficult periods. The binding nature of {\i kiddushin} is not contingent on the day-to-day happiness of the couple. It precedes that happiness and persists even when it is absent. That is the point of separating the two stages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geonic Variants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the text of the {\i bracha}: the formulation {\i mekadesh Yisrael al yedei chupa ve\u8217'kiddushin} raises an obvious question. We say {\i mekadesh Yisrael ve\u8217'hazmanim} in {\i kiddush}, but we do not ordinarily say {\i mekadesh Yisrael al yedei X} for any particular {\i mitzvah} \u8212- we do not say {\i mekadesh Yisrael al yedei tzitzis} and so on. The {\i geonim} therefore proposed an alternative reading: the {\i chasima} is simply {\i mekadesh Yisrael}, without the {\i al yedei}. That is a powerful statement on its own \u8212- that this {\i mitzvah} in particular is connected to {\i kedushas Yisrael}. Marriage and family life taking place within a framework of {\i kedusha} is what defines and hallows the people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the phrase {\i al yedei chupa ve\u8217'kiddushin} \u8212- in which {\i chupa} precedes {\i kiddushin} despite {\i kiddushin} coming first chronologically \u8212- the {\i geonim} offer an ingenious explanation. Originally, they suggest, the text read {\i be\u8217'kiddushin} with a {\i bet} rather than {\i ve\u8217'kiddushin} with a {\i vav}. Due to the phonological rules of {\i dagesh} following a {\i hey}, the {\i bet} loses its {\i dagesh} and becomes a {\i vet}, which was subsequently misread as a {\i vav}. The original meaning would therefore have been {\i chupa bi\u8217'kiddushin} \u8212- the {\i chupa} effected through the act of {\i kiddushin}. Regardless of whether one accepts that reading, the ordering does accomplish something important. By naming {\i chupa} first, the {\i bracha} points toward the ultimate destination of married life. And by concluding with {\i kiddushin}, the {\i bracha} ends on the word that describes what is actually happening now. Had it read {\i al yedei kiddushin ve\u8217'chupa}, a {\i birchas erusin} would have ended on the word {\i chupa} \u8212- and that would have been incongruous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chasima Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} addresses the question of whether {\i birchas erusin} requires a {\i chasima} \u8212- a closing {\i baruch atta} at the end. Two positions emerge: {\i man de\u8217'lo chasim} holds that it is like {\i birchas hapeiros} and {\i birchas hamitzvos}, which are short {\i brachot} that do not require a {\i chasima}. {\i Man de\u8217'chasim} holds that it is like {\i kiddush}, which does conclude with a {\i chasima}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Kedusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos frames the question as purely technical: the {\i bracha} is longer than a standard {\i birchas hamitzvah} but shorter than {\i kiddush}, so it is genuinely unclear which category applies. Rashi, by contrast, begins from the position that the {\i bracha} belongs in the short category \u8212- it does not introduce multiple themes the way {\i kiddush} does \u8212- but explains why one might nonetheless treat it like {\i kiddush}. His answer: {\i keivan de\u8217'vilashon kedusha hu ke\u8217'kiddush hayom chasminan beih} \u8212- since it employs the language of {\i kedusha}, with {\i kiddushin} and {\i mekadesh} throughout, it is structured like {\i kiddush} and therefore takes a {\i chasima}. For Rashi, then, the issue is not merely a matter of length but of the nature of the {\i bracha} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3 Models Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion opens up three genuinely distinct ways of understanding the function of {\i birchas erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is that it is a {\i birchas hamitzvah} \u8212- a {\i bracha} recited before performing a {\i mitzvah}, specifically the act of {\i kiddushin}. This is the most straightforward reading: {\i asher kideshanu be\u8217'mitzvosav ve\u8217'tzivanu}, just as one recites before taking a {\i lulav} or immersing in a {\i mikveh}. This raises an immediate question among the {\i rishonim}: is there actually a {\i mitzvah} of {\i kiddushin} per se, distinct from {\i peru urevu}? That is a major debate. If it is a {\i birchas hamitzvah}, the {\i bracha} should be recited by the {\i chasan} himself and should precede the act \u8212- consistent with our practice of reciting it before the {\i mesiras tabaas}. However, our actual practice is a hybrid: the {\i bracha} is recited by the rabbi, which reflects not a {\i birchas hamitzvah} but a {\i birchas hashevach} \u8212- a praise of God for the institution of marriage, recited by one who witnesses the act rather than performs it. A {\i birchas hashevach} need not be recited before the act; indeed, it would typically be recited afterwards, in response to what one has witnessed. Some {\i rishonim} do hold that {\i birchas erusin} is recited after the {\i mesiras tabaas}. At a wedding where the rabbi forgot to recite it beforehand, one can rely on those {\i rishonim} and have it recited afterwards, precisely because it can be understood as a {\i birchas hashevach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third model draws the comparison to {\i kiddush}. {\i Kiddush} involves not merely acknowledging that {\i Shabbos} is holy but actively participating in declaring it so. By reciting {\i kiddush}, one adds a human layer of sanctification. Similarly, one might understand {\i birchas erusin} not only as praise for a holy institution but as the act by which one sanctifies the union \u8212- the human element that consecrates the couple\u8217's shared life and their intimacy. That is the {\i kiddush} dimension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The comparison to {\i birchas hapeiros}, finally, connects to the previous day\u8217's discussion: it frames the sexual dimension of marriage as an act of pleasure that is elevated by directing it toward God through a {\i bracha}, just as one elevates physical enjoyment of food. Each of these models carries different {\i nafka minos}, and taken together they reflect a richly layered understanding of what {\i birchas erusin} is doing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birchat Chasanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Week Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} states: {\i mevarachin birchas chasanim be\u8217'asara kol shiva} \u8212- we recite {\i birchas chasanim} with ten people throughout the seven days. Note that {\i birchas erusin} carries no requirement of {\i borei peri hagafen}. The {\i sheva brachos}, by contrast, do eventually incorporate wine \u8212- though this addition comes from the {\i geonic} period rather than the {\i gemara} itself. The reason {\i borei peri hagafen} became so intrinsic to {\i sheva brachos}, while the same is not true of {\i birchas erusin}, is likely because {\i sheva brachos} is anchored in the context of meals, and wine is the natural accompaniment of a {\i seudah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda adds: {\i v\u8217'hu shebo panim chadashos} \u8212- this applies when new guests have arrived. Tosafos explains that {\i panim chadashos} refers specifically to people whose presence genuinely adds to the {\i simcha} \u8212- those whose arrival generates a qualitatively greater joy. The underlying logic is that the {\i brachos} are an expression of joy: either they are a continuation of the existing {\i simcha}, or they reflect something new. The presence of new guests who increase the {\i simcha} provides that new element.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shehakol/Yotzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda then transmits the text of the {\i brachos} themselves. The first is {\i shehakol bara lichvodo} \u8212- God created everything for His honor. The second is {\i yotzer ha\u8217'adam} \u8212- You have created the human being.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Image\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third is {\i asher yatzar es ha\u8217'adam betzalmo betzelem demus tavniso ve\u8217'hisin lo mimenu binyan adei ad} \u8212- who formed the human in His image, in the image of His likeness, and fashioned from him an everlasting structure. The {\i binyan adei ad} points to the woman, created from man, and the {\i adei ad} indicates human continuity \u8212- children and generations proceeding from that original creation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sos Tasis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i bracha} shifts register: {\i sos tasis ve\u8217'tagel ha\u8217'akara bekibbutz baneha le\u8217'socha be\u8217'simcha} \u8212- the barren one will greatly rejoice and exult as her children are gathered joyfully within her. This is {\i Yerushalayim}. The {\i chasima} is {\i Baruch atta Hashem mesameach Tzion bivneha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 National Themes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a notable pivot. We have moved from the creation of the human being and the fashioning of woman from man, and now \u8212- before returning to the couple themselves \u8212- we introduce the national dimension. This does not strike the note of mourning ({\i Im eshkachech}), but rather the positive one: the gathering of the children of Israel to Jerusalem will be cause for joy. This presages the redemption and frames the couple\u8217's joy within a larger messianic horizon. The reference to the {\i akara} carries the suggestion of fertility as well, though this remains implicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gan Eden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i bracha} of {\i sameach tesamach re\u8217'im ha\u8217'ahuvim ke\u8217'samechacha yetzircha be\u8217'Gan Eden mikedem} returns to the couple directly. The language is striking: may this couple rejoice as their Creator rejoiced with them \u8212- or rejoiced for them \u8212- in the Garden of Eden. {\i Gan Eden mikedem} may mean Eden in the east or Eden in the primordial past. In either case, the image is of the original union of man and woman, {\i zot hapa\u8217'am etzem me\u8217'atzami uvasar mibsari} \u8212- the moment of recognition and joy at coming together. The {\i chasima} is {\i Baruch atta Hashem mesameach chatan ve\u8217'kallah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sasson Brachos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i bracha} is {\i Baruch atta Hashem Elokeinu melech ha\u8217'olam asher bara sasson ve\u8217'simcha chatan ve\u8217'kallah gilah rinah ditzah ve\u8217'chedvah ahavah ve\u8217'achvah shalom ve\u8217're\u8217'us} \u8212- who created all these forms of rejoicing: joy and gladness, groom and bride, delight, song, mirth, and exultation, love, brotherhood, peace, and companionship. This is then followed by: {\i mehera Hashem Elokeinu yishama be\u8217'arei Yehuda u\u8217've\u8217'chutzos Yerushalayim kol sasson ve\u8217'kol simcha kol chatan ve\u8217'kol kallah} \u8212- may there soon be heard in the cities of Judah and the streets of Jerusalem the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the groom and the voice of the bride, concluding with {\i Baruch atta Hashem mesameach chatan im hakallah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure here is chiastic. We moved from the creation of man and woman, to the national-messianic theme of Zion and her children, back to the couple rejoicing in Gan Eden. Now the final {\i bracha} unifies both themes: the joy God created \u8212- {\i sasson ve\u8217'simcha chatan ve\u8217'kallah} \u8212- is the same joy that will ring out in the streets of Yerushalayim at the time of redemption, echoing the {\i pesukim} in Yirmiyahu. The couple\u8217's wedding joy and the national redemptive joy are woven together. This is one reason the {\i brachos} carry such power when recited {\i be\u8217'asara} \u8212- as a communal act. The blessing declares that this is not merely a private {\i simcha}. It is a {\i simcha} of the entire community, intertwined with national hope, and the presence of ten is what gives it that communal dimension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi notes the distinction between the {\i chasima} of the penultimate {\i bracha} \u8212- {\i mesameach chatan ve\u8217'kallah} \u8212- and the final one \u8212- {\i mesameach chatan im hakallah}. The former, he says, expresses the wish that each of them individually should have success and blessing \u8212- {\i besipuk mezonos ve\u8217'chol tuv}. The latter, {\i chatan im hakallah}, refers specifically to the joy they find {\i with one another} \u8212- that the {\i couplehood} itself should be a source of joy. Both dimensions matter, but they are distinct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baruch Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A technical question arises: why do some of the {\i brachos} open with {\i Baruch atta} while others do not? Tosafos provides a clear answer. The general rule is that a {\i bracha} which follows immediately upon another \u8212- {\i samuch la\u8217'chavertah} \u8212- does not require its own opening {\i Baruch atta}. However, when two consecutive {\i brachos} are very short, they must each open with {\i Baruch atta} or they will simply sound like a single continuous {\i bracha}. If one were to say {\i Baruch atta Hashem Elokeinu melech ha\u8217'olam shehakol bara lichvodo yotzer ha\u8217'adam}, without a new {\i Baruch atta} at {\i yotzer ha\u8217'adam}, the listener would take it for one strange undifferentiated {\i bracha}. Similarly, if {\i asher yatzar es ha\u8217'adam betzalmo} did not open with {\i Baruch atta}, one would hear {\i Baruch atta Hashem yotzer ha\u8217'adam asher yatzar} as a single unit. The brevity of those {\i brachos} requires the explicit opening to signal that a new {\i bracha} has begun.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once {\i asher yatzar es ha\u8217'adam} is clearly a distinct, somewhat longer {\i bracha}, the shift to {\i sos tasis} with its entirely new theme is obviously a different {\i bracha}, and therefore {\i sos tasis} does not require its own {\i Baruch atta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the final {\i bracha} \u8212- {\i asher bara sasson ve\u8217'simcha chatan ve\u8217'kallah} \u8212- it does open with {\i Baruch atta}, and Tosafos explains why: because this {\i bracha} may sometimes be recited independently, without the preceding {\i brachos}. Since it can stand alone \u8212- as we will see below in the discussion of the week following the wedding \u8212- it must be formulated as a self-contained {\i bracha} and therefore opens with {\i Baruch atta}. Rav Moshe Feinstein rules that once the {\i nusach} was established in this form, the opening {\i Baruch atta} or its absence is fixed regardless of how the {\i brachos} are distributed among different participants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Community Simcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i brachos} as a whole intertwine the joy of the couple with the joy of the community and the joy of national redemption. The making of these {\i brachos be\u8217'asara} is not incidental \u8212- the community is not merely an audience. The assembled ten are participants in a {\i simcha} that transcends the private moment of the wedding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Am Assembled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reading of {\i shehakol bara lichvodo} is particularly striking. Rashi says \u8212- and the text reads {\i einah min haseder ela asifas ha\u8217'am hane\u8217'esafim sham ligmol chesed, zecher chasdei haMakom she\u8217'nahag im Adam HaRishon shena\u8217'asu lo shoshvinin ve\u8217'nit\u8217'asek bo, ve\u8217'asifah zo mihu kevod haMakom} \u8212- that {\i shehakol bara lichvodo} is not primarily a cosmological statement but a {\i bracha} for the assembled people themselves. All who have gathered here to gladden the {\i chasan ve\u8217'kallah} are performing an act of {\i chesed}, following in the ways of God, who Himself attended to Adam HaRishon and was {\i mesameach chatan ve\u8217'kallah}. The gathering itself is {\i kevod haMakom} \u8212- an honor to God \u8212- because the assembled are imitating God\u8217's own conduct. Whether this is {\i peshat} in the {\i bracha} is debatable \u8212- {\i shehakol bara lichvodo} most naturally reads as a statement about creation and God\u8217's purpose \u8212- but Rashi\u8217's reading gives the opening {\i bracha} a remarkable social and ethical valence, framing the entire ceremony as a communal act of {\i chesed} modeled on the divine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the question of whether the six {\i brachos} include {\i borei peri hagafen}: the {\i gemara} records only six. The {\i borei peri hagafen} was added in the {\i geonic} period and is not part of the {\i talmudic} text. For {\i birchas erusin}, the addition of a cup of wine is clearly later and universally recognized as not intrinsic. The {\i borei peri hagafen} of {\i sheva brachos}, while also {\i geonic}, came to be seen as far more integral to the {\i brachos} \u8212- likely, as noted, because {\i sheva brachos} is so thoroughly anchored in the context of the meal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Count & Creation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi/Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records two incidents. Levi visited the household of Rebbi at the wedding celebration of Rav Shimon, Rebbi\u8217's son, and recited five {\i brachos}. Rav Ashi visited the household of Rav Ashi at the wedding celebration of Mar, his son \u8212- Mar bar Rav Ashi \u8212- and recited six. The {\i gemara} proposes that this reflects a {\i machlokes}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chad vs Shtei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Leima b\u8217'hai kamifligi: de\u8217'mar savar chada yetzira havai u\u8217'mar savar shtei yetziros havai} \u8212- one holds that {\i yotzer ha\u8217'adam} and {\i asher yatzar es ha\u8217'adam betzalmo} describe a single act of creation and should be collapsed into one {\i bracha}, while the other holds that they describe two distinct acts and therefore require two {\i brachos}. What is the basis for this question?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Together/Separate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi connects this to the familiar reconciliation of Bereishis 1 and Bereishis 2. In Bereishis 1, the human is created male and female simultaneously \u8212- {\i zachar u\u8217'nekeva bara osam}. One reading of this, familiar from the {\i midrash}, is that the first human was created as a single being comprising both male and female, joined back-to-back, subsequently divided into two. Under this reading, {\i chada yetzira havai} \u8212- it was always one creation, always male-and-female, and the separation was not a conceptually distinct second act but merely the division of what was already present. The two {\i brachos} can therefore be collapsed into one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plan vs Execution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the premise of this {\i machlokes}. {\i De\u8217'kulei alma chada yetzira havai} \u8212- everyone agrees there was one creation. The real question is {\i mar savar basar machshava azlinan u\u8217'mar savar basar ma\u8217'aseh azlinan} \u8212- do we follow the original intention or the actual execution? And this is {\i ki ha d\u8217'Rav Yehuda}, who raised a contradiction: it says {\i vayivra Elokim es ha\u8217'adam betzalmo betzelem Elokim bara oso}, a single male figure, and elsewhere {\i zachar u\u8217'nekeva bara am} \u8212- male and female. {\i Ha keitzad? Bet\u8217'chila ala be\u8217'machshava lehivaros shnayim \u8212- the original plan was to create two \u8212- u\u8217'le\u8217'vasof nivra echad} \u8212- but ultimately they were created as one, then divided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion that {\i chada yetzira havai} \u8212- reflecting the reading that male and female were already present together from the outset \u8212- carries a theological resonance that commends it to our contemporary sensibilities: woman is not an afterthought or appendage of man, but part of the original and complete creation. The two accounts of creation read together convey something profound: the individual human being bears {\i tzelem Elokim} and full dignity, while simultaneously being incomplete without the other. The original human comprised both; division into two created not lack but the possibility of wholeness through reunion. And there is something to the observation that the {\i machshava} envisioned two distinct beings while the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} began with one \u8212- the separation itself grounds a kind of autonomy and individual wholeness that is distinct from the wholeness of union.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Week Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 1 Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the {\i brachos} themselves, the {\i gemara} now turns to the conditions under which they are recited throughout the seven days. Rav Ashi came to the home of Rav Kahana. On the first day \u8212- {\i yoma kama barech kulhu} \u8212- he recited all of the {\i brachos}. From that point forward: {\i ei ika panim chadashot barech kulhu} \u8212- if there are {\i panim chadashot}, new guests, he recited all of them. If not \u8212- {\i afushei simcha be\u8217'alma hu} \u8212- it is simply more of the same {\i simcha}, not a qualitatively new one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shehasimcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In that case, {\i mivarech she\u8217'hasimcha bimono} \u8212- one recites the addition of {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono} in the {\i zimmun}, the opening of {\i birchas hamazon}: {\i Baruch Elokeinu she\u8217'hasimcha bimono ve\u8217'she\u8217'achalnu mishelo}. And together with that, {\i asher bara} \u8212- the final {\i bracha} of the {\i sheva brachos} \u8212- is recited on its own. This is precisely why {\i asher bara sasson ve\u8217'simcha} opens with {\i Baruch atta}: it can be recited independently, as it is in this situation during the week when {\i panim chadashot} are absent. The fact that {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono} functions as an insertion in the {\i zimmun} anchors the whole structure in the context of the meal, and makes explicit what the {\i gemara} had previously left implicit: {\i sheva brachos} is a practice of the meal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Week 2 Month\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishiva ve\u8217'ad shloshim} \u8212- from after the first week until thirty days: {\i bein amruhu machmat hilula bein lo amruhu machmat hilula} \u8212- whether or not one specifies that the meal is in honor of the wedding, one adds {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono} in the {\i zimmun}. There is no {\i asher bara} recited independently during this period \u8212- at most, the {\i zimmun} addition. It is worth noting the parallel structure with {\i aveilus}: the stages of seven days, thirty days, and twelve months mirror the structure of mourning. Whether this reflects a general pattern of how significant life events recede over time, or whether the parallels are more pointed, the {\i gemara} will make explicit by comparing {\i birchas chasanim} and {\i birchas aveilim} directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twelve Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the first month: {\i ei amar lehu machmat hilula} \u8212- if one explicitly frames the meal as being in honor of the {\i chasan ve\u8217'kallah}, as when celebrating with guests who were unable to attend the wedding, {\i mivarech she\u8217'hasimcha bimono}. If not \u8212- one does not. And to what point can one still frame a meal as {\i machmat hilula}? Rav Papa says in the name of Rava: {\i treisar yarchei shata} \u8212- twelve months, the first year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Early Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: from the earlier side, what is the earliest point from which one may begin reciting {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono}? The {\i gemara} considers whether it can be said even before the wedding, in anticipation of it. Rav Papa married off Abba Mar his son, and he recited {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono} from the time of {\i erusin} \u8212- at the engagement celebration, which was explicitly {\i leshem chatan ve\u8217'kallah}. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i shani Rav Papa de\u8217'hava tari\u8217'ach lei} \u8212- Rav Papa was different because he was wealthy and had people to manage all the preparations; there was no perceived obstacle between {\i erusin} and {\i nisuin}, and he was therefore genuinely already in the mode of {\i simcha}. Similarly, Ravina married off his son to the daughter of Rav Chaviva, and he too recited {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono} from the time of {\i erusin}, saying {\i kim li be\u8217'gavaihu de\u8217'la hadri behu} \u8212- I know they will not back out. As long as no obstacle is perceived, one is already in the mindset of {\i simcha} and may say {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono}. The {\i gemara} then adds with wry economy: {\i le\u8217'vasof lo istai\u8217'a milsa ve\u8217'hadru vehu} \u8212- in the end it did not work out and they did back out. The lesson: do not be overconfident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bris Milah Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chaviva came to a {\i bris milah} and added {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono} in the {\i zimmun}, reasoning that any {\i seudas mitzvah} might warrant the addition. {\i Ve\u8217'leis hilchasa ke\u8217'vosei} \u8212- we do not rule that way \u8212- {\i mishum de\u8217'tridi, de\u8217'is tza\u8217'ara le\u8217'yanuka} \u8212- because the participants are disturbed: the baby is in pain. This {\i gemara} is the source for the {\i Ashkenazi minhag} of not reciting {\i shehecheyanu} at a {\i bris milah}. Though a {\i bris} is undeniably a {\i simcha}, the baby\u8217's suffering introduces a dimension of distress that is incompatible with the full declaration of joy that {\i she\u8217'hasimcha bimono} \u8212- or {\i shehecheyanu} \u8212- would represent. {\i Sefardim} do recite {\i shehecheyanu} at a {\i bris}, treating it primarily as a {\i bracha} on a new {\i mitzvah}, but the {\i gemara} here reflects a more ambivalent assessment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minyan Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom Counts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman says in the name of Rav: {\i chassanim min haminyan} \u8212- the {\i chasan} counts toward the ten required for {\i birchas chasanim}. This may seem obvious, but it is not. If the {\i minyan} is conceptualized as a {\i tzibur} giving a blessing to the couple, one might argue that the recipients of the blessing cannot simultaneously constitute the quorum of blessers. The answer is that at a wedding, everyone present is part of the {\i simcha} \u8212- there is no division between the one who rejoices and those who cause his rejoicing. The {\i chasan} is not a passive recipient; he too is part of the {\i kehilla} assembled in joy, and therefore counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aveilim Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a parallel: {\i ve\u8217'ein aveilim min haminyan} \u8212- but for {\i birchas aveilim}, the {\i avel} does not count toward the required ten. Here the logic is the inverse: at a house of mourning, there are clearly distinct roles \u8212- the {\i avel} is the mourner, and everyone else is there to console. The {\i menachamim} constitute the quorum; the {\i avel} is its recipient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs Braisa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an objection: a {\i baraisa} states {\i chassanim ve\u8217'aveilim min haminyan} \u8212- that even the {\i avel} counts. The {\i gemara} resolves this by invoking the principle that {\i Rav tanna u\u8217'palig} \u8212- Rav, who belongs to the transitional generation between {\i tannaim} and {\i amoraim}, may dispute a {\i baraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yitzchak/Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same positions are then attributed to Rav Yitzchak in the name of Rav Yochanan: {\i chassanim min haminyan ve\u8217'ein aveilim min haminyan}. Here too the {\i gemara} raises the {\i baraisa} stating that {\i aveilim} count. But since Rav Yochanan is an {\i amora}, the solution of {\i tanna u\u8217'palig} is less available, and the {\i gemara} must find another resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shurah Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes between two contexts. The {\i baraisa} that counts {\i aveilim} in the {\i minyan} refers to {\i birchas hamazon} \u8212- there is a form of {\i birchas aveilim} that occurs in the context of {\i birchas hamazon} at the meals during the week of {\i aveilus}, and there the {\i avel} does count. When Rav Yochanan says the {\i avel} does not count, he refers to the {\i shurah} \u8212- the two lines formed by the consolers when the mourners return from the graveside, through which the {\i avel} passes to receive words of {\i nichum}. In the {\i shurah}, the {\i avel} is clearly distinct from the participants and cannot count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First vs Later\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: but the {\i baraisa} that excludes {\i aveilim} from the {\i minyan} specifically in the context of {\i birchas aveilim} \u8212- if {\i birchas aveilim} occurs at {\i birchas hamazon} and {\i aveilim} count there, where does this exclusion apply? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ki ka\u8217'amar Rabbi Yochanan be\u8217'rachava} \u8212- Rav Yochanan refers to the {\i rachava}, the open space or street where the first meal is served to the mourners upon returning from the cemetery. At that first meal, the roles are maximally differentiated \u8212- those who feed and console on one side, the {\i avel} on the other \u8212- and therefore the {\i avel} does not count toward the ten. This is a distinct {\i bracha} associated with that initial meal, not the {\i birchas aveilim} of later meals during the week.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mourner Panim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: can there really be a {\i birchas rachava} throughout all seven days? The {\i baraisa} says {\i ein aveilim min haminyan} for {\i birchas aveilim kol shiva} \u8212- all seven days. But the {\i rachava} is only the first meal. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i meshkachas la be\u8217'panim chadashot} \u8212- you find it through {\i panim chadashot}. Just as {\i sheva brachos} continues throughout the week when new guests arrive to augment the {\i simcha}, so too {\i birchas aveilim} can recur throughout the week when new {\i menachamim} arrive \u8212- new consolers who were not present before. Their arrival creates a new instance of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i nichum aveilim}, and a new occasion for the {\i bracha}. In those cases, the {\i avel} does not count toward the ten, for the same reason as always: the {\i avel} and the {\i menachamim} occupy fundamentally distinct roles, and the quorum must consist of those performing the {\i mitzvah} of consolation, not its recipient.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 9\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brachot Aveilim & Shiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro Aveilim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 8b introduces the institution of {\i birchat aveilim}, the special blessing recited for mourners, which likewise requires a {\i minyan} \u8212- with the important distinction that the {\i aveilim} themselves do not count toward that {\i minyan}. The Gemara also discusses the {\i rechava}, the practice of the mourners walking back from the burial while the community forms two rows to offer {\i nechama}, and the {\i seudat havra\u8217'ah}, the meal of comfort served to the mourner immediately upon returning. Whether the {\i minyan} requirement for that {\i seudat rechava} differs from the {\i minyan} required for the {\i birchat hamazon} at subsequent meals is not entirely clear from the Gemara, but what does emerge plainly is that {\i birchat aveilim} forms a recognized component of {\i birchat hamazon} in the house of mourning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: the statement of Rabbi Yitzchak in the name of Rabbi Yochanan that {\i birchat chatanim} is recited with ten throughout the entire seven days, with the {\i chatanim} counting toward the {\i minyan}, parallels a teaching that {\i birchat aveilim} is recited with ten throughout the entire seven days, but with the {\i aveilim} excluded from the {\i minyan} \u8212- since the roles of {\i menachamim} and {\i aveilim} are fundamentally distinct. The Gemara asks: where do we find {\i birchat rechava} repeated throughout the week? And it answers: {\i meshkachat lah bipanim chadashot} \u8212- just as {\i panim chadashot}, new faces, arriving during the week generate renewed {\i simcha} and therefore occasion the recitation of {\i sheva berachot} anew, so too when new {\i menachamim} arrive there is a fresh opportunity for {\i nechama}, and {\i birchat aveilim} would be recited again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the Rambam, in his discussion of {\i hilchot berachot}, records the {\i nusach} of {\i sheva berachot} in full but offers only a simplified version of {\i birchat aveilim}, which likely reflects the fact that by his time this practice had largely fallen into disuse. The Rambam\u8217's formulation adds to the fourth {\i beracha}, {\i hatov vehametiv}, language of {\i dayan emet} \u8212- affirming divine truth and judgment \u8212- and then provides an opportunity to add one\u8217's own words of {\i nechama} for the {\i avel} before the {\i harachamanim}, which function as a kind of appendage to the four main {\i berachot}. Whether this captures the full original {\i nusach} as the Gemara presents it is, as we shall see, an open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chiya Bar Abba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara relates the following story. Rabbi Chiya bar Abba served as the {\i mikrei b\u8217'nei} \u8212- the {\i cheder} teacher \u8212- of Resh Lakish\u8217's sons. A child of his died. On the first day Resh Lakish did not go to pay a {\i shiva} call, and on the second day he went, bringing with him Yehuda bar Nachmani, his {\i meturgeman}. A {\i meturgeman} was a kind of public announcer who would receive the Sage\u8217's words and then proclaim them to the assembled students or community; he was sometimes given a degree of latitude to elaborate. Resh Lakish instructed Yehuda bar Nachmani: {\i kum eima miltha kaveil yanuka} \u8212- rise and say some words in commemoration of the child who had passed away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yehuda bar Nachmani stood and opened with the verse {\i vayar Hashem vayina\u8217'atz mikas banav uvnotav} \u8212- \u8220"God saw and was provoked by the anger of His sons and daughters.\u8221" He interpreted this to mean: in a generation whose fathers provoke God, God\u8217's anger is directed at their children, and the children die young. He went on to cite a second possibility \u8212- that the deceased was a young adult, not an infant \u8212- and quoted another verse: {\i al kein al bachurav lo yismach Hashem v\u8217'et yetomav v\u8217'et almanotav lo yerachem}, \u8220"therefore God will not rejoice over his young men, nor will He show compassion to their orphans and widows, for all of them are faithless and evildoers, and every mouth speaks {\i nevala}.\u8221" The Gemara takes up this last phrase in a related digression. Rav Chana bar Abba teaches that {\i hakol yod\u8217'im kalla lama nichnesa l\u8217'chuppa} \u8212- everyone knows why the bride enters the {\i chuppa} \u8212- and yet whoever commits {\i nival peh}, who speaks of such matters explicitly, even if seventy years of good fortune were decreed for him, it will be reversed for evil. It is a striking formulation, particularly because the statement is framed entirely around the {\i kalla} rather than the groom \u8212- an unusual focus given the Gemara\u8217's general tendency to address male sexual consciousness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara itself voices the obvious critique: {\i ata lenichumei tzo\u8217'arei ka metza\u8217'er lei} \u8212- he came to offer consolation and instead caused anguish. The Gemara then offers an exculpatory reading: what Yehuda bar Nachmani meant to say was that Rabbi Chiya bar Abba was so important a figure that he bore the suffering of an entire generation \u8212- it was the generation\u8217's sin, but a person of such high {\i madrega} is the one who bears its consequences. Even granting that interpretation, however, the statement remained a painful one to deliver, and it stands as a vivid illustration of how people say hurtful things at a {\i shiva} call even with the best of intentions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Brachot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish then turned to Yehuda bar Nachmani and gave successive instructions, each producing a distinct {\i beracha}. The first instruction: {\i kum eima milta keneged shivcho shel Hakadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- rise and say words in praise of God. Yehuda bar Nachmani proclaimed:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\i el gadol berov godlo adir vechazak berov nora\u8217'ot mechaye metim bema\u8217'amaro ose gedolot ad ein cheker venifla\u8217'ot ad ein mispar \u8212- baruch ata Hashem mechaye hametim.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a full {\i baruch ata Hashem} blessing, framed as praise of God, but unmistakably resonant in the setting of a {\i beit avel}: God who revives the dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish then said: {\i kum eima milta keneged avelim} \u8212- now say something directed toward the mourners. This is different in character from the preceding blessing, which praised God. The {\i birchat aveilim} proper follows:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\i acheinu hameyuga\u8217'im hameduka\u8217'im ba\u8217'avel haze \u8212- tnu levavkhem lachkor et zot: zot omedet la\u8217'ad, nativ hu misheshet yimei Bereshit. Rabim shatu verabim yishtu \u8212- kemishte rishonim kach mishte acharonim. Acheinu ba\u8217'al nechamot yenachem etkhem \u8212- baruch menachem avelim.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The message is one of consolation through universal human experience: this is the way of the world since the six days of creation, many have drunk from these waters before you, and many will drink after \u8212- the Master of consolations should comfort you. The blessing concludes: {\i baruch menachem avelim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye comments that one should not say {\i rabim yishtu} \u8212- \u8220"many will drink\u8221" \u8212- referring to future deaths, so as not to invoke future suffering. And one should not say {\i kemishte acharonim} \u8212- \u8220"like the drinking of those who come after\u8221" \u8212- because, as Resh Lakish himself taught, and so too it is taught in the name of Rabbi Yosi: {\i le\u8217'olam al yiftach adam piv l\u8217'Satan} \u8212- one should never open one\u8217's mouth to the Satan by invoking the possibility of future misfortune. Rav Yosef adds a proof-text: when Israel said {\i k\u8217'Sdom hayinu l\u8217'Amora daminu} \u8212- \u8220"we have become like Sodom, we resemble Amora\u8221" \u8212- the very next verse addresses them as rulers of Sodom, fulfilling the feared comparison.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish then directed Yehuda bar Nachmani: {\i kum eima milta keneged menachmei avelim} \u8212- now say a blessing for the {\i menachamim}, for those who have come to comfort the mourners. This blessing celebrates the comforters as {\i gomlei chasadim bnei gomlei chasadim}, practitioners of {\i chesed} in the tradition of Abraham our father, and concludes: {\i baruch ata Hashem meshalem hagemul} \u8212- blessed is God who repays acts of kindness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally: {\i kum eima milta keneged kol Yisrael} \u8212- say a blessing on behalf of all of Israel. The text is a plea for divine protection:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\i Ribbono shel olam \u8212- pedeh ve\u8217'hatzel, malet ve\u8217'hosha amcha Yisrael min hadever u\u8217'min hacherev u\u8217'min habizah, u\u8217'min hashidafon u\u8217'min hayarkon, u\u8217'mikol minei puranyot hamitragshot u\u8217'va\u8217'ot la\u8217'olam. Terem nikra ve\u8217'atah ta\u8217'aneh \u8212- baruch ata otzer hamagefah.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from this extended passage is that {\i birchat aveilim} had a defined structure \u8212- one blessing for the deceased, one for God ({\i mechaye hametim}), one for the mourners ({\i menachem avelim}), one for the {\i menachamim}, and one for the community at large \u8212- while the precise wording within each blessing appears to have been more flexible. The {\i meturgeman} was given latitude to compose the content, provided the themes and conclusions ({\i siumim}) were honored. This stands in some contrast to the fixed text of {\i birchat chatanim} presented on the preceding {\i daf}. Whether these blessings were formally tied to {\i birchat hamazon} or could stand independently is not entirely resolved \u8212- the Gemara did not foreground a {\i minyan} requirement in this passage, though that may simply be because it had already been established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Cups Wine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla taught \u8212- and some say this was a {\i Baraita} \u8212- that the Sages instituted ten cups of wine to be drunk in the house of mourning: three before the meal, to open the appetite; three during the meal, to settle the food; and four after the meal, corresponding to the four blessings of {\i birchat hamazon} \u8212- {\i hazan}, {\i birkat ha\u8217'aretz}, {\i boneh Yerushalayim}, and {\i hatov vehametiv}. The logic of drinking wine in a house of mourning presumably reflects its role in dulling grief, echoing the imagery of {\i mishte} \u8212- the drinking party \u8212- that runs through the passage, and consistent with the halachic distinction between an {\i onen}, who does not drink wine before burial, and an {\i avel}, for whom wine was permitted as a way of bearing grief.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages later added four more cups: one in honor of the {\i chazanei ha\u8217'ir}, the communal functionaries; one for the {\i parnasei ha\u8217'ir}, the community leaders who supported the mourners; one {\i keneged beit hamikdash}; and one in memory of Rabban Gamliel. The first two reflect the communal support structure around the mourning family. The one {\i keneged beit hamikdash}, Rashi explains, is not merely a messianic gesture but an acknowledgment of communal {\i avelut} over the {\i churban} \u8212- we are all {\i aveilim} on the destruction of the Temple, and God should also console us and rebuild it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cup {\i keneged} Rabban Gamliel is explained by a {\i Baraita}: in earlier times, the cost of burial was so crushing to a family that relatives would abandon their dead rather than bear the expense. {\i Barishona hayu hotza\u8217'at hamet kashah likrovav yoter mimitato} \u8212- the funeral was harder for the family than the death itself. Rabban Gamliel \u8212- the {\i Nasi}, a man of great wealth \u8212- commanded that when he died he be buried in simple linen shrouds, and all the people followed his lead. In memory of this act of compassion, a cup was drunk in his honor. Rav Papa adds that in his own day the practice had become even simpler, with the most minimal burial garments costing almost nothing. It is worth emphasizing the deliberate message of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's choice: precisely because he was the most prominent figure of his generation, his insistence on a simple funeral carried maximum force as a statement of principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, once the count reached fourteen cups, people were drinking to excess and becoming drunk. The Sages therefore reversed course \u8212- {\i hechziru davar l\u8217'yoshno} \u8212- and reverted to the original ten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is striking that both the {\i birchat aveilim} and the {\i asara cosot} have largely disappeared from contemporary practice. The {\i daf} as a whole has a clear thematic unity: just as 8a presented the {\i birchat chatanim} for the wedding setting, 8b presents the {\i birchat aveilim} for mourning \u8212- a beautiful structural pairing of the liturgies that accompany life\u8217's two great threshold moments.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Virginity Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Patuach Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the central legal concern of the {\i daf}: the husband who, the morning after the wedding, claims his wife was not a virgin. There are two distinct ways he might make this claim. The first is the absence of blood \u8212- a relatively concrete form of evidence, especially given that the practices described later in the {\i perek} involved guards at the door and a special sheet to be inspected. The second is what the Gemara calls {\i tainas pesach patuach} \u8212- the claim that during the act of intercourse itself he experienced no obstruction, no hymen, and thereby concluded experientially that she was not a virgin. This second claim carries an additional layer of uncertainty: not only is it without external evidence, but one might argue that even in his own mind he does not actually know what he is talking about \u8212- his subjective experience may simply be an error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar states: {\i ha\u8217'omer pesach patuach matzati ne\u8217'eman l\u8217'osrah alav} \u8212- one who says \u8220"I found the door already open\u8221" is believed to the extent that she becomes forbidden to him. The principle at work is {\i shavya anafshei chaticha d\u8217'issura} \u8212- a person who declares something forbidden binds himself by that declaration, as when one declares a piece of meat to be {\i chazir}: even without objective confirmation, he is bound by his own characterization of reality. The effect here is that, given his claim that she is not a virgin and therefore may have committed adultery, a married woman who commits adultery being forbidden to her husband, he cannot continue living with her. The ruling contains two {\i chiddushim}: first, that {\i petach patuach} is a recognized claim and not dismissed as the speculation of someone who simply does not know what he is talking about; and second, that {\i shavya anafshei chaticha d\u8217'issura} can operate even where the forbidden status results not from a direct assertion (\u8220"this is forbidden\u8221") but from a factual claim whose halachic consequence is a {\i safek} of forbidden status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Sefeika Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately raises an objection: {\i ve\u8217'eima sfeik sfeika hu} \u8212- even granting his claim at face value, this is a case of double doubt, and a {\i sfek sefeika} should be permissive. The analysis runs as follows. The first {\i safek}: perhaps she had sexual relations {\i tachtav} \u8212- after the {\i kiddushin}, which would constitute adultery \u8212- or perhaps it was before the {\i kiddushin}, in which case she was unchaste but did not commit adultery against him and would not be forbidden. The second {\i safek}: even if we assume it occurred after {\i kiddushin}, perhaps it was {\i b\u8217'ones} \u8212- rape \u8212- in which case she did nothing wrong and is not forbidden to her husband \u8212- or perhaps it was willful. For both conditions to render her forbidden, the encounter must have been after {\i kiddushin} and consensual. A {\i sfek sefeika} in the direction of leniency should therefore permit her to remain with her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i lo tzricha} \u8212- the ruling is needed specifically in cases that reduce the double doubt to a single doubt. One such case is {\i eshet kohen}: the wife of a {\i kohen} is forbidden even if the violation was through rape, so the second {\i safek} disappears. The other case is where the father betrothed her before she was three years old: since the Gemara assumes that penetration before age three leaves no lasting physical effect ({\i biah} under three causes the hymen to regenerate), the absence of a hymen can only reflect an encounter after she turned three \u8212- at which point she was already betrothed \u8212- thus eliminating the first {\i safek} regarding timing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an additional question: what about a case where the act occurred willingly but she was under twelve? Such an act would be treated as {\i ones} under the principle of {\i pituy ketanah ones} \u8212- the seduction of a minor is legally equivalent to rape \u8212- and would not render her forbidden. Is this not yet another branch of {\i sfek sefeika}? Tosafot resolves this by observing that this scenario collapses into the same category as {\i ones}, since statutory incapacity and physical coercion are both subsumed under {\i ones}. For a {\i sfek sefeika} to function, the two doubts must be genuinely distinct questions; where they merge into the same legal category, they do not multiply into a {\i sfek sefeika}. The upshot is: in the ordinary case, the double doubt insulates the wife from being rendered forbidden. Only where the {\i sfek sefeika} is dismantled \u8212- through {\i eshet kohen} or {\i pachot mibat shalosh} \u8212- does the husband\u8217's claim take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a sharp internal contradiction. Rabbi Elazar himself elsewhere stated: {\i ein isha ne\u8217'eset al ba\u8217'ala ela al iskei kinu v\u8217'stira} \u8212- a woman is not rendered forbidden to her husband except through the process of {\i kinui v\u8217'stira}, the formal warning-and-seclusion procedure that initiates the {\i sotah} process. How can Rabbi Elazar simultaneously hold that a husband\u8217's subjective claim of {\i pesach patuach} is sufficient to forbid the wife? The one statement seems to demand the full {\i sotah} mechanism; the other allows a bare assertion to suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara first considers whether the {\i ma\u8217'aseh shehaya} \u8212- the story invoked to support the {\i sotah} principle \u8212- refers to David and Batsheva, and immediately questions this: was there any {\i kinui v\u8217'stira} in that story? And furthermore, why was Batsheva not rendered forbidden to David if adultery had clearly occurred? These questions stand as difficulties that the Gemara must address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara begins by untangling the statement itself. The correct reading is: {\i ein isha ne\u8217'eset al ba\u8217'ala ela al iskei kinu v\u8217'stira mi\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh shehaya} \u8212- we learn that the only mechanism is {\i kinui v\u8217'stira} from the case of David and Batsheva precisely because there was no {\i kinui v\u8217'stira} there, and therefore she was not rendered forbidden. The argument is: from the fact that she was not forbidden despite the adultery having demonstrably occurred, we infer that without the {\i sotah} process, no prohibition attaches.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses: {\i uto v\u8217'tisbara} \u8212- does this reasoning make sense? She clearly committed adultery; why should the absence of a formal procedure matter when the act is known? And more pointedly, a straightforward objection: {\i kinui v\u8217'stira in edim lo} \u8212- according to you, does the prohibition apply only through {\i kinui v\u8217'stira} and never through witnesses? Of course if there are two witnesses it applies. So the statement cannot be excluding all other mechanisms. Rather, it addresses cases below the threshold of two witnesses: absent {\i edim}, the only other operative mechanism is the {\i sotah} process. The question then remains: does a husband\u8217's lone assertion constitute a mechanism below or at the level of two witnesses?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Testimony Weights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is as follows: {\i petach patuach k\u8217'shnei edim dami} \u8212- a claim of {\i pesach patuach} carries the evidentiary weight of two witnesses. Why? Because {\i ne\u8217'eman adam al atzmo k\u8217'mea edim} \u8212- a person is believed against himself like a hundred witnesses. When a person makes a self-incriminating claim, it carries maximal credibility precisely because no one would voluntarily accept a disadvantageous ruling without good reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara is aware that the question is sharper than the answer. The original statement was that a woman is forbidden only through {\i kinui v\u8217'stira} \u8212- meaning that anything less than two witnesses is insufficient, and only the {\i sotah} process fills the gap. Now we are told that the husband\u8217's own claim counts as two witnesses. But this seems to undercut the entire force of the restriction: if I say my wife committed adultery and that statement alone counts as two witnesses, then the restriction adds little. The Gemara accepts this tension because both rulings are attributed to Rabbi Elazar and must be reconciled; the resolution is that the husband\u8217's self-testimony operates as a special mechanism, analogous to \u8212- but not the same as \u8212- objective testimony from outsiders.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that {\i ne\u8217'eman adam al atzmo} operates in the monetary sphere in a straightforward way \u8212- if one says \u8220"I owe a thousand {\i zuz}\u8221" one is bound. But in matters of prohibition and, critically, in criminal matters, no person can testify against himself to generate an objective criminal sanction. What the Gemara describes here is not criminal self-incrimination but a form of personal restriction: based on your own stated understanding of reality, you are bound to conduct yourself accordingly, even though it will have indirect consequences for the wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David Batsheva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the question of David and Batsheva directly. Tosafot frames the difficulty sharply: if the principle of {\i shavya anafshei chaticha d\u8217'issura} means that when a husband claims his wife committed adultery he is bound by that claim and cannot continue living with her, then why did David continue living with Batsheva? He knew he had committed adultery with her. Why was he not compelled to separate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers two answers. The first: {\i hasam ones havei} \u8212- in that case it was {\i ones}. David did not use physical violence, but what genuine choice did Batsheva have when the king summoned her? The Gemara is working with a broader conception of {\i ones} that encompasses situations of severe social coercion. The halachic consequence is significant and, as the Gemara notes, somewhat ironic: the prohibition on a woman who commits adultery applies only when the act was willful. If she was raped, she committed no wrong and is not forbidden to her husband \u8212- nor to the man who violated her. Since Batsheva was compelled, she did not commit willful adultery, and therefore no prohibition attached to David\u8217's living with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uriah Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second answer the Gemara offers is attributed to Rav Shmuel bar Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i kol hayotzei l\u8217'milchemes beis David get krisus kosev l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- anyone who went out to fight in the wars of the house of David first wrote a {\i get} for his wife. The practice of writing a conditional {\i get} before going to war is explained in two ways: Rashi says it was to spare the wife from the {\i yibbum} obligation if the husband died childless; Tosafot objects that {\i kol hayotzei} implies universal practice, not just men with brothers, and argues instead that the concern was {\i agunah} \u8212- a woman whose husband\u8217's fate is unknown. The {\i get} was written on the condition that it would take effect if the husband did not return within a specified period after the war\u8217's end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Uriah had therefore given Batsheva such a conditional {\i get} before going to war. When David ensured that Uriah did not return \u8212- as the narrative of Tanach makes plain \u8212- the {\i tenai} was fulfilled retroactively. The {\i get} took effect retroactively, Batsheva was divorced from Uriah, and technically the act between David and Batsheva did not constitute adultery, since she was no longer Uriah\u8217's wife in halachic terms from the moment the condition was fulfilled. The proof-text: {\i v\u8217'es arubasam tikach} \u8212- the word {\i aruvah} (bond/pledge) is interpreted by Rav Yosef as a reference to the instruments of the husband-wife bond, i.e., the {\i getin}, that David was to collect from the soldiers before they marched.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is cited alongside the {\i ones} resolution as an alternative ({\i v\u8217'iba\u8217'is eima}), and both stand in the Gemara.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wed Wedding Court\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wednesday Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the {\i takana} of the fourth-{\i amud} mishnah: {\i b\u8217'sula niseis l\u8217'yom harevi\u8217'i} \u8212- a virgin marries on the fourth day of the week, i.e., Wednesday. The Gemara has been assuming throughout that this is not mere custom but an enacted {\i takana} in which there is a communal interest. The specific mechanism identified is {\i icrurei da\u8217'ata} \u8212- the concern that if one waits too long, the husband will cool off and not pursue the matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takana Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why should the community care whether the husband cools off? The Gemara poses this directly: {\i ul\u8217'mai} \u8212- for what purpose do we want him to bring his claim to court the next morning? If the purpose is merely to allow him to deprive her of her {\i ketuva} \u8212- to protect his own financial interests \u8212- then this is purely his concern. If he cools off and decides not to pursue it, that is his prerogative. There is no communal interest in helping a husband reduce his wife\u8217's {\i ketuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer must therefore be: {\i ela l\u8217'osra alav} \u8212- the concern is that she may have committed adultery and therefore be forbidden to him. In that case, the community has a genuine interest in ensuring the matter is adjudicated promptly, because we do not want a husband and wife living together when they may be halachically forbidden to each other. This is the only rationale that justifies a {\i takana} to ensure the husband can appear in court the following morning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood vs Door\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that purpose established, a closer look reveals which type of claim the {\i perek} is primarily addressing. The Gemara asks: {\i v\u8217'dekhatani ta\u8217'anah mai} \u8212- what claim is the husband making when he comes to court the next morning? Is it the claim of {\i pesach patuach} \u8212- based on his subjective experience during intercourse \u8212- or the claim of {\i tainas damim} \u8212- the absence of blood on the sheet?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes: {\i lo dekhatani tainas damim} \u8212- the {\i perek} is dealing with the claim of absence of blood. This is the more concrete and objectively assessable claim: there were witnesses positioned at the door, there was a special sheet, and its inspection either confirmed or did not confirm blood. The {\i perek}\u8217's central scenario is therefore this evidentiary framework. The {\i pesach patuach} claim \u8212- that of subjective experiential inference \u8212- is Rabbi Elazar\u8217's ruling and stands as a significant {\i chiddush}, but it is not necessarily the baseline case that the whole structure of Wednesday weddings and next-morning court appearances was designed to address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judah Galilee\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a regional distinction. A {\i baraita} teaches that one who ate in the home of his father-in-law in Judah without witnesses \u8212- alluding to the known practice in Judah where a betrothed couple might be permitted to be alone together during the {\i erusin} period \u8212- cannot thereafter claim that his wife was not a virgin, because the obvious retort is that any non-virginity may be attributable to premarital relations with him. By contrast, in Galilee, where such seclusion was not permitted, the husband\u8217's claim retains validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then sharpens the question: in Judah, the husband cannot make the claim \u8212- for what purpose? If it is merely to make her forbidden to him based on his own say-so ({\i l\u8217'osrah alav}), then even in Judah this should apply, because the principle of {\i shavya anafshei chaticha d\u8217'issura} operates on his own consciousness regardless of external suspicion. The fact that in Judah his claim is undermined only makes sense if what he is trying to do is to hurt her \u8212- specifically, to deprive her of her {\i ketuva}. And that is the claim the regional distinction addresses: in Judah, where prior seclusion was possible, his claim that she was not a virgin is not accepted for the purpose of causing her monetary loss. In Galilee, it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the Gemara to a foundational question that will animate the entire remainder of the {\i perek}: on what basis is the husband believed at all, given that he has no witnesses and no objective supporting evidence, to cause his wife to lose her {\i ketuva}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel states: {\i ha\u8217'omer pesach patuach matzati ne\u8217'eman l\u8217'hafsidah kesubasah} \u8212- a man who says \u8220"I found the door already open\u8221" is believed to cause her to forfeit her {\i ketuva}. Rashi provides the foundational rationale, and it is significant: {\i de\u8217'amrinan ee lav dekushta hu, lo hayah mafsid se\u8217'udaso} \u8212- we reason that if his claim were false, he would not have wasted all the money he spent on the wedding feast. He threw a {\i se\u8217'udat nisuin}, invited guests, incurred great expense \u8212- and all that to make one night of marriage and then claim she was not a virgin? Moreover, if he hated her from the start, he would simply have divorced her without going to all this trouble.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reasoning is essentially one of implausibility: the claim made the morning after the wedding carries a built-in credibility that a claim made years later would never enjoy. A husband who has just spent a fortune on a wedding celebration and who comes to court the next morning with this complaint is presumed to be telling the truth, because no one would construct such an elaborate scenario merely to avoid paying a full {\i ketuva}. The moment he makes this claim, moreover, he faces real consequences \u8212- not just a reduced {\i ketuva} obligation, but potential forced divorce. A man who genuinely wanted out of the marriage without this claim could have found simpler means. Thus the timing and context lend the claim a degree of credibility that the law recognizes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling, and Rashi\u8217's explanation of it, will be central to the rest of the {\i perek}, which unfolds the full picture of what happens in court the morning after a Wednesday wedding: what claim the husband makes, how it is evaluated, and what its halachic consequences are both for him and for his wife.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 10\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Petach Patuach & Ketubah\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Petach Patuach Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a case that had been discussed at length: whether a husband is believed when he claims {\i petach patuach} \u8212- that upon consummating the marriage, he experienced what felt like an already-open entrance, as opposed to finding no {\i dam betulim} whatsoever. The stakes of this claim are significant. One consequence is that, in certain configurations \u8212- such as where the wife is the wife of a {\i kohen} \u8212- the claim effectively translates into an allegation of prior adultery, rendering her forbidden to him by operation of the principle {\i shavya anafshei \u7717 ?aticha de-issura}: he has bound himself by his own words. But the more immediately surprising consequence is that he is believed to cause her to forfeit her {\i kesuva}. The {\i gemara} now addresses both the mechanism by which he is believed and the deeper question of what the {\i kesuva} itself actually is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Institution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Itmar} \u8212- it was taught. Rav Na\u7717 ?man said in the name of Shmuel in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar: {\i \u7717 ?achamim tiknu lahem livnot yisrael masayim u-le-almanah maneh} \u8212- the rabbis instituted that a virgin receives a {\i kesuva} of 200 {\i zuz}, and an {\i almanah} \u8212- a widow, or more broadly, a woman who is not a virgin \u8212- receives 100 {\i zuz}. The entire institution, in other words, is rabbinic in origin. And since it is rabbinic, {\i ve-hem hem eminu\u8217'hu}: the very same rabbinic authority that created the institution chose to believe the husband when he claims {\i petach patuach matzasi} and to rule accordingly that she does not receive her {\i kesuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises the obvious challenge: {\i im kein mah ho\u8217'ilu \u7717 ?achamim betakkanatam}? If the rabbis created an obligation and simultaneously gave the husband an easy exit from it, what exactly did their enactment accomplish? A man need only say {\i petach patuach} and he avoids payment entirely. The {\i gemara} responds with Rava\u8217's principle: {\i \u7717 ?azakah ein adam tore\u8217'a\u7717 ? bise\u8217'udah u-mafsidah} \u8212- we may assume that a person does not go to the trouble of making a wedding feast only to destroy it. The expense and effort of a wedding creates a strong presumption that the husband is not fabricating this claim, because making it up would mean throwing away everything he invested. This presumption is sufficient to render the enactment functionally effective: cases of false claims will be rare enough that the {\i takkanah} retains its protective force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Qualification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a pointed objection. The logic of {\i ein adam tore\u8217'a\u7717 ? bise\u8217'udah u-mafsidah} presupposes that the husband is sacrificing something by making the claim. But what if he is not? {\i Tosafot} notes that there is a later dispute in the {\i gemara} about the consequences of a husband discovering that his wife was not a virgin as he had expected: is this a {\i meka\u7717 ? ta\u8217'os} \u8212- a transaction conducted on false premises \u8212- in which case the marriage is annulled and she receives no {\i kesuva} at all? Or does it merely warrant an adjustment of the {\i kesuva} downward from 200 to 100 {\i zuz}, leaving the marriage intact?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is critical. If the claim of {\i petach patuach} results in something drastic \u8212- she is forbidden to him because adultery is implied, or the entire marriage is void as a {\i meka\u7717 ? ta\u8217'os} \u8212- then indeed one would not fabricate such a claim after investing in a wedding, because the consequence is losing one\u8217's wife entirely. {\i Tosafot} states this explicitly: {\i tina\u7717 ? be-eshes kohen o bif\u7717 ?ut mibas gimmel} \u8212- in a case involving a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife or a girl married under the age of three, where the claim implies forbidden relations, {\i keivan d\u8217'mifsada}, since he loses his wife, we believe him. Similarly, according to the view that the result is {\i meka\u7717 ? ta\u8217'os} and she gets nothing, the drastic nature of the consequences again supports his credibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i Tosafot} continues: {\i aval le-man d\u8217'amar yeish lah kesuva maneh} \u8212- according to the opinion that the only result is reducing her {\i kesuva} from 200 to 100, {\i lo mafsid midei} \u8212- he loses nothing. He remains married, keeps the wedding gifts, and simply pays less. In that scenario, {\i amai meheiman}? Why should he be believed at all? {\i Ve-yeish lomar de-lo meheiman} \u8212- and one may answer that indeed he would not be believed. This is an important qualification on the {\i gemara}\u8217's blanket statement: the husband\u8217's credibility when claiming {\i petach patuach} applies only in contexts where his claim carries sufficiently drastic personal consequences for him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mohar to Kesubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Fine & Land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the {\i kesuva} is a rabbinic institution, the {\i gemara} now cites a {\i beraita}: {\i havi knas \u7717 ?achamim hu lo tigbeh ella min ha-ziburis} \u8212- since the {\i kesuva} is a rabbinic fine, she may collect only from {\i ziburis}. Land in rabbinic law is categorized into three grades: {\i idis} is Grade A, {\i beinonis} is Grade B, and {\i ziburis} is Grade C \u8212- the lowest quality. Ordinarily, a creditor collecting a debt is entitled to land of equivalent value, but the location and quality of that land matters enormously. The {\i beraita} reasons that since the {\i kesuva} is not a conventional debt arising from a prior obligation but rather a rabbinic creation, collection is limited to the least desirable category of land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the word {\i knas} \u8212- fine \u8212- requires explanation: what wrong did the husband commit that we are fining him? The {\i gemara} emends the framing: {\i ho\u8217'il ve-takkanat \u7717 ?achamim hu} \u8212- since it is a rabbinic institution, it is simply of lesser legal weight than a biblical or naturally arising obligation, and therefore collection is restricted accordingly. Underlying this ruling is also the rabbis\u8217' persistent concern for social equilibrium: the protective obligations upon the husband must be calibrated so as not to be so burdensome that men become reluctant to marry. The limitation to {\i ziburis} reflects that balance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Mohar Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel omer: kesubas isha min ha-Torah} \u8212- the {\i kesuva} is biblical in origin. The {\i gemara} challenges this immediately: did Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel actually say this? A {\i beraita} is cited in which he appears to say the opposite \u8212- that the {\i kesuva} is purely rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first discusses the biblical foundation itself. The verse {\i kesef yishkol ke-mohar ha-betulos} \u8212- he shall weigh out silver according to the {\i mohar} of virgins \u8212- appears in the context of a man who seduces a virgin. Because she is no longer a virgin, she loses her {\i mohar}, the traditional payment given by the groom to the bride\u8217's father when contracting a marriage. Accordingly, the seducer must compensate the father for that loss. The {\i pshat} of the Torah, and the position of biblical scholars as well, is that the {\i mohar} was a genuine bridal price, paid up front and given to the father \u8212- not a symbolic token like a ring or a {\i perutah}, but a substantial sum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By combining this passage with the case of the rapist in {\i Devarim}, who pays fifty units of silver, and identifying the currency as {\i shekel} \u8212- itself derived from the word {\i yishkol} \u8212- the Sages establish that the standard {\i mohar ha-betulos} was 50 {\i shekel}, which equals 200 {\i zuz}. {\i Mikan samchu \u7717 ?achamim le-kesubas isha min ha-Torah} \u8212- from here the rabbis found a biblical basis for the institution of a woman\u8217's {\i kesuva}. The 200 {\i zuz} that was once the {\i mohar} paid at the beginning of a marriage became the 200 {\i zuz} of the {\i kesuva} payable at the end, in cases of death or divorce. The rabbis transformed an upfront biblical payment into a deferred rabbinic institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciling Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now faces the contradiction: in one source Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says the {\i kesuva} is biblical, in another he says it is rabbinic. The {\i gemara}\u8217's first solution is {\i ipoch} \u8212- reverse the attribution in the {\i beraita} \u8212- assigning the position of biblical origin to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel and the rabbinic position to the {\i \u7717 ?achamim}. But why reverse the {\i beraita} rather than the earlier statement? Because we have independent evidence that Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel treats the {\i kesuva} as a serious, binding obligation: {\i de-tenan, Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel omer noten lah mi-me\u8217'os Kapotkia}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A later {\i mishnah} addresses a case where a man married in Kapotkia and divorced in Eretz Yisrael, or vice versa. The {\i shekel} of Kapotkia was worth more than the {\i shekel} of Eretz Yisrael \u8212- comparable to the relationship between US and Canadian dollars. The {\i \u7717 ?achamim} say one always pays in the local Israeli currency, the lesser amount, reasoning that since the {\i kesuva} is rabbinic we go easy on the husband. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says: if you contracted the obligation in Kapotkia, you pay in Kapotkia currency. This position \u8212- that the obligation is binding according to where and how it was contracted \u8212- reflects treating the {\i kesuva} as a weighty, serious obligation rather than a lenient rabbinic creation. The {\i gemara} therefore finds it consistent to attribute to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel the position that the {\i kesuva} is biblical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then offers a second resolution: {\i ve-iba\u8217'it eima, kulei Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel hi} \u8212- perhaps the entire {\i beraita} reflects only Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's view, and it is missing a crucial distinction. {\i Ve-\u7717 ?isurei me\u7717 ?sera ve-hachi tanni}: the {\i beraita} is incomplete and must be read as follows. The {\i kesuva} of a {\i betulah} \u8212- a virgin \u8212- has a biblical basis in the {\i mohar ha-betulos}. The {\i kesuva} of an {\i almanah}, however, {\i eina mi-divrei Torah ela mi-divrei Sofrim} \u8212- is purely rabbinic \u8212- because the Torah never assigns any {\i mohar} for non-virgins. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's statement that the {\i kesuva} is rabbinic referred only to the {\i kesuva} of a widow, not to the {\i kesuva} of a virgin. The contradiction is resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Nature Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note something striking even in the position that the {\i kesuva} is {\i de-Oraita}: the language is {\i samchu \u7717 ?achamim}, they found support \u8212- not that the obligation flows directly from biblical law. The Torah\u8217's {\i mohar} was paid up front, at the initiation of the marriage; the {\i kesuva} is paid at the end. The rabbis took a biblical phenomenon and transformed it into a different institution. So even according to the {\i de-Oraita} position, the {\i kesuva} as we have it is a rabbinic re-application of a biblical prototype, not a direct biblical mandate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dual nature resolves a well-known difficulty raised by {\i Tosafot} in their first comment on the {\i daf}. The anonymous {\i stam} of the {\i gemara} throughout {\i Shas} assumes the {\i kesuva} is {\i de-rabbanan} \u8212- the {\i gemara} in {\i Isha Rabbah}, for instance, gives the reason for the institution as {\i she-lo te\u8217'hei kalla be-einav le-hotzi\u8217'ah}, so that divorce should not be easy, which is a classic {\i de-rabbanan} rationale. Yet Ashkenazic {\i ketubot} include the language {\i mohar besulayichi de-\u7717 ?azi lichi mi-de-Oraita} \u8212- the 200 {\i zuz} of your virginal {\i mohar} that is fitting to you biblically. Sephardic {\i ketubot} use {\i de-\u7717 ?azi lichi} without the word {\i mi-de-Oraita}, but still employ the term {\i mohar}. How can we write {\i mi-de-Oraita} if the accepted ruling is {\i de-rabbanan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} offers one answer: the obligation is rabbinic, but the method of calculating the amount \u8212- the coinage, the specific figure of 200 {\i zuz} \u8212- is derived from biblical standards. {\i Tosafot} offers another approach, arguing that we do in fact rule it is {\i de-Oraita}. But perhaps the clearest answer is simply that both are true simultaneously. The {\i kesuva} is {\i de-rabbanan} in the form we have it \u8212- a deferred payment at the end of marriage \u8212- and it is {\i de-\u7717 ?azi lichi mi-de-Oraita} in that it ultimately originates in, and draws its weight from, the biblical obligation of {\i mohar ha-betulos}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's phrase {\i mikan samchu \u7717 ?achamim le-kesubas isha min ha-Torah} captures precisely this: it originates in the Torah but its current form is a rabbinic institution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Testing Non-Virginity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Na\u7717 ?man\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to practical cases of {\i petach patuach}. A man came before Rav Na\u7717 ?man and said {\i petach patuach matzasi} \u8212- he claimed that his experience of consummation indicated his wife had not been a virgin. Rav Na\u7717 ?man\u8217's response was striking: {\i asvuhi kufrei} \u8212- go beat him with palm branches, the barbed kind that cut as well as strike.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i mavrachta \u7717 ?aviva lei} \u8212- he must have had prostitutes available to him. In other words, the only way he would know the difference between a virgin and a non-virgin from physical experience alone is if he had been sexually active beforehand. Two interpretations follow. Rashi reads Rav Na\u7717 ?man as ordering the beating on account of the man\u8217's prior promiscuity \u8212- he has no standing to complain about his wife when he himself has been involved with prostitutes. {\i Tosafot} reads it in the opposite direction: precisely because only a promiscuous man would know the difference, and we cannot assume he is promiscuous, we must conclude he is lying \u8212- and so he is beaten for {\i motzi shem ra}, for slandering his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises the obvious tension: but Rav Na\u7717 ?man himself is the authority who said the husband is believed in such a case! The resolution is that he is indeed believed \u8212- {\i meheiman} \u8212- and we also beat him. The belief and the beating coexist: his {\i petach patuach} claim is accepted as evidence regarding his wife\u8217's status, but the claim simultaneously serves as evidence of his own prior sexual activity, for which lashes are appropriate. The practical effect of this approach is that very few men will come to {\i beit din} with such a claim once word spreads that making it results in a beating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether the man could simply say that he never experienced {\i petach patuach} but found no blood \u8212- {\i lo matzati dam betulim}. That would indeed be a different matter, and the answer is yes, that would be a different claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First vs.\u160 ?Remarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav A\u7717 ?ai draws a further distinction: {\i kan bevachur kan benasui} \u8212- it matters whether this is the man\u8217's first marriage or whether he has been married before. If he has been previously married, he has legitimate basis for comparison and would be believed when he claims {\i petach patuach}. If this is his first marriage, he would not be believed \u8212- and in that case, rather than lashes for promiscuity, he would receive lashes for {\i motzi shem ra}, for slandering his wife without any possible basis for the claim. This distinction significantly qualifies the earlier principle: the husband\u8217's general credibility regarding {\i petach patuach} applies only when he has had prior marital experience from which to draw a legitimate comparison.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Door in Darkness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A man came before Rabban Gamliel with the claim {\i petach patuach matzasi}. Rabban Gamliel responded: {\i shema hitita} \u8212- perhaps you approached at an angle. He then offered a {\i mashal}: a man walking in the dead of night and in total darkness approaches the door of his house. If he comes in at an angle, he may find it appears open \u8212- he slips through a crack without realizing that the door had in fact been closed. But had he come straight on, at a perpendicular, he would have discovered that the door was indeed locked. Because he did not know what he was doing, he came in at an angle and experienced it as open when it was in fact closed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parable maps onto the situation of a first-time husband who genuinely does not know what he is doing in consummating a marriage. His experience of {\i petach patuach} may be the result of his own inexperience rather than any prior breach on his wife\u8217's part.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second version of the story \u8212- {\i ika d\u8217'amri} \u8212- offers a related but distinct explanation: {\i shema be-mazid hitatso va\u8217'akarta le-dasha ve-avra} \u8212- perhaps you pushed through so forcefully that you ripped out the door and its bolt. That is, if the husband came with such force that he broke through without feeling the obstruction, he would falsely conclude that there had been nothing there. This version maps onto a different kind of inexperience \u8212- not going in at the wrong angle, but going in with such force that the presence of an intact barrier was not registered. In either case, the conclusion is the same: a man who has not been married before has no reliable basis for such a claim.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blood Evidence Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dirty Sheet Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A man came before Rabban Gamliel bar Rabbi \u8212- the son of Rabbi \u8212- and said {\i ba\u8217'alti ve-lo matzati dam}: I had relations with my wife on the wedding night and found no blood. This claim \u8212- the absence of blood on the sheet \u8212- is more objective than {\i petach patuach}; the couple had been supervised and the sheet was present for inspection. His wife, speaking for the first time in these cases, said: {\i Rabbi, betulah hayiti} \u8212- I was a virgin. She does not deny that no blood was found; she asserts nonetheless that she had been a virgin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel told them to bring him the sheet. He immersed it in water, cleaned it, and found several drops of blood. The sheet had been so grimy and dusty \u8212- conditions of the time \u8212- that the blood had been invisible against the general dirtiness. Once cleaned sufficiently to remove the surface dirt, the drops of blood became apparent. He told the husband: {\i lech zechei be-mik\u7717 ?acha} \u8212- go, take good possession of your purchase. The wife was vindicated. One cannot help but notice the language throughout these cases: the woman is consistently framed as an object of purchase \u8212- {\i mekach}, {\i mikchacha} \u8212- which relates directly to the legal framework being applied. The {\i kesuva} of 200 {\i zuz} reflects what he expected to be acquiring; if she is not a virgin, it is analogous to {\i meka\u7717 ? ta\u8217'os}, a transaction on false premises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cleaning Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Huna the son of Rava of Parzika raised a question to Rav Ashi: why can we not simply employ this same technique today \u8212- clean the sheet when there is a claim of {\i lo matzati dam} and look for blood?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi replied: {\i gihutz shelanu ke-kibus shelahem} \u8212- what counts as a simple laundering for them required only immersion in water, which was sufficient to remove the surface dirt without disturbing the blood. For us, basic laundering already involves the level of scrubbing and soap that they would call {\i gihutz} \u8212- a deep cleanse involving rubbing against a stone and the use of cleansing agents. And if we were to perform {\i gihutz} in order to remove the dirt, {\i ma\u8217'aver lei \u7717 ?umra} \u8212- the stone used in {\i gihutz} would also remove the blood. There is no method available to us that removes the dirt without also removing the blood, and therefore the test cannot be replicated today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Barrel Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another man came before Rabban Gamliel bar Rabbi with the same claim: {\i ba\u8217'alti ve-lo matzati dam}. His wife responded differently this time: {\i Rabbi, adayin betulah ani} \u8212- I am still a virgin. Her claim is not that she was a virgin who produced no blood, but that the consummation never actually succeeded; the husband had not achieved full penetration and was falsely attributing the absence of blood to her prior non-virginity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel said: bring me two maidservants, {\i achat betulah ve-achat be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- one who is a virgin and one who is not. They were brought. He had each of them sit on top of an open barrel of wine. {\i Be\u8217'ulah reicha nodef} \u8212- from the non-virgin, a smell of wine became detectable; {\i betulah ein reicha nodef} \u8212- from the virgin, it did not. Rashi explains: for a woman who is no longer a virgin, the fumes rising from the wine barrel travel upward through her body and emerge through her mouth, where the scent is detectable. For a virgin, the physical barrier prevents this from occurring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having confirmed through the controlled experiment that the test worked as he understood it should \u8212- the virgin and non-virgin responding exactly as his tradition described \u8212- Rabban Gamliel then applied the same test to the woman in question. {\i Ve-lo haya reicha nodef} \u8212- no smell was detectable from her. She was indeed still a virgin, and her husband had not achieved penetration. {\i Amar lo lech zechei be-mik\u7717 ?acha} \u8212- go, your wife is still yours, and still a virgin; go try again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why did he first test the maidservants before applying the test to the woman in question? The answer: {\i gemara hava shemia lei, ma\u8217'aseh lo hava chazi} \u8212- he had received the tradition that this test works, but he had never actually carried it out. One can know a technique in principle without knowing whether one is able to execute it correctly in practice. He therefore first ran the experiment on women whose status he knew, to confirm that he was administering the test competently. Only once that was established did he apply it where it mattered. Additionally, {\i ve-lav ora\u7717 ? ar\u8217'a le-zalzulei bivnot Yisrael} \u8212- it would not be respectful to subject a Jewish woman to this test if there were any doubt that it would yield reliable results. As to why we do not use this test today: we simply no longer know how to administer it correctly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Special Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Durkani Family Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A man came before Rabban Gamliel ha-Zaken \u8212- the elder Rabban Gamliel, at the time of the {\i Beis Hamikdash} \u8212- with the claim: {\i ba\u8217'alti ve-lo matzati dam}. His wife responded: {\i Rabbi, mi-mishpa\u7717 ?at Durkani ani} \u8212- I am from the Durkani family, {\i she-ein lahem lo dam niddah ve-lo dam besulim} \u8212- who have neither menstrual blood nor hymenal blood. She is asserting a hereditary physiological condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel investigated her relatives \u8212- {\i badak Rabban Gamliel bi-krovoseha} \u8212- and found that her account was accurate. He told the husband: {\i lech za\u7717 ?ei be-mik\u7717 ?acha} \u8212- go, take good possession of your purchase. He added: {\i ashrecha she-za\u7717 ?ita le-mishpa\u7717 ?at Durkani} \u8212- praised are you that you merited a woman from the Durkani family.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last remark is challenged by Rav \u7716 ?anina: {\i tan\u7717 ?umin shel hevel ni\u7717 ?amo Rabban Gamliel le-oso ha\u8217'ish} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel offered that man empty consolation. The reason is stated by Rav \u7716 ?iyya: {\i keshem she-has\u8217'or yafeh la-isah, kach damim yafim le-ishah} \u8212- just as yeast is beneficial for dough, blood is beneficial for a woman. Menstrual blood is a sign of fertility. A woman without such blood is, in a meaningful sense, like unleavened dough \u8212- the capacity for growth and new life is impaired. {\i Ve-harei zeh ke-nodos} \u8212- it is like an empty wineskin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, {\i tana mishum Rabbi Meir: kol ishah she-dameha merubim, baneha merubim} \u8212- any woman whose menstrual flow is abundant will have many children; she is highly fertile. The Durkani woman\u8217's condition thus raises serious questions about childbearing, making {\i ashrecha} a hollow blessing. Whether the phrase the {\i gemara} attributes to Rav Yirmiyah bar Abba \u8212- {\i zachei be-mik\u7717 ?acha} \u8212- or the one attributed to Rabbi Yosef and Rabbi Avin \u8212- {\i ni\u7779 ?-\u7717 ?ayeiv be-mik\u7717 ?acha} \u8212- you are stuck with your purchase \u8212- the tone differs markedly. The {\i gemara} resolves this by noting one genuine advantage: {\i de-lo asi lidei sefek niddah} \u8212- since she has no menstrual blood, there is never any question of {\i niddah} or doubt-{\i niddah} status. That is the grain of truth in Rabban Gamliel\u8217's blessing, though it is a thin consolation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Famine Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A man came before Rabbi \u8212- Rabban Gamliel\u8217's era having passed \u8212- with the same claim. The wife said she was still a virgin. Rabbi, however, observed that {\i sheni batzorot havei} \u8212- these were years of famine, and {\i ra\u8217'ah Rabbi she-pneihem she\u7717 ?orim} \u8212- he saw that their faces were blackened from malnutrition. He had them bathe, and then fed and gave them drink. Once they had recovered somewhat, he had them return to the bedroom, and this time {\i matzu dam} \u8212- blood was found. The verse Rabbi applied to them was {\i tzafad oram al atzmam, yavesh haya ka-etz} \u8212- their skin cleaved to their bones, it was as dry as wood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether the absence of blood in the original consummation was due to the hymen failing to bleed because of extreme dehydration and malnourishment, or due to insufficient physical capacity for full penetration under conditions of severe physical debilitation, the {\i gemara} does not specify precisely. What is clear is the pastoral lesson: {\i lo matzati dam} in such circumstances reflects bodily conditions, not prior unchastity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sequence of cases \u8212- {\i petach patuach} dismissed as inexperience or promiscuity, the dirty sheet revealing hidden blood, the failed penetration exposed by the wine barrel test, and now famine undermining the very physical evidence \u8212- creates a cumulative picture. The theoretical framework giving the husband credibility is maintained in the books, but in practice rabbis worked carefully and creatively to arrive at just outcomes, and the {\i halacha}\u8217's bark turns out to be considerably worse than its bite.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betulah vs.\u160 ?Almanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now states the foundational categories: {\i betulah kesuvatah masayim} \u8212- a virgin receives a {\i kesuva} of 200 {\i zuz}; {\i ve-almanah maneh} \u8212- a widow, or more broadly a non-virgin, receives only 100 {\i zuz}. The biblical basis for the figure of 200 {\i zuz} has already been laid out in the preceding {\i gemara}: it derives from the {\i mohar ha-betulos}, the standard bridal payment for virgins in the Torah, which was 50 {\i shekel} or 200 {\i zuz}. For the {\i almanah}, as established above, there is no biblical {\i mohar} equivalent, and the 100 {\i zuz} is purely a rabbinic institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow from Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds that a woman who is an {\i almanah}, {\i gerushah}, or {\i \u7717 ?alutzah} \u8212- widow, divorc\u233 ?e, or one who has undergone {\i \u7717 ?alitza} \u8212- but whose prior marriage consisted only of {\i eirusin} (betrothal) and never reached {\i nisuin} (the consummated marriage stage), receives a {\i kesuva} of 200 {\i zuz} for her next marriage, not 100. This produces a seeming paradox: a woman who has been married before yet whose {\i kesuva} is 200. The rule is that {\i eirusin} alone, absent {\i nisuin}, does not change the presumption of virginity. Since {\i eirusin} in standard practice did not involve cohabitation \u8212- unlike in Yehuda where couples sometimes had relations after {\i kiddushin} \u8212- a woman who was betrothed and then separated before {\i nisuin} is still treated as a {\i betulah} and receives the full {\i kesuva} of 200 {\i zuz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ta\u8217'anas Betulim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic that entitles her to 200 {\i zuz} also exposes her to {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}: the husband\u8217's claim that she was not a virgin. The two sides of this coin are inseparable. Because the presumption is that she is a virgin \u8212- sufficient to grant her the full 200 {\i zuz kesuva} \u8212- the husband has a legitimate expectation of virginity. If on the morning after he claims either {\i petach patuach} or {\i lo matzati dam}, he cannot be told that he should have expected otherwise given her prior marital history, because {\i eirusin} alone does not change that presumption. He is therefore entitled to raise {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} in such a case, with all the consequences that entails.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Almanah Etymology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maneh Etymology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai almanah?} \u8212- What is the origin of the word {\i almanah}? Rav \u7716 ?ana Bagdata\u8217'ah \u8212- possibly from Baghdad, though that city is typically dated somewhat later \u8212- offers an etymology: {\i almanah al shem maneh} \u8212- the word {\i almanah} derives from {\i maneh}, the hundred-{\i zuz} sum she receives. It is a functional etymology: she is called {\i almanah} because her {\i kesuva} is a {\i maneh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Anticipation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: a woman who is an {\i almanah} from {\i eirusin} receives 200 {\i zuz}, not 100 \u8212- so the etymology cannot apply to her. The response is that because the standard {\i almanah} from {\i nisuin} is called {\i almanah} on account of her {\i maneh}, the same term is extended to the {\i almanah} from {\i eirusin} by association, even though her {\i kesuva} is 200.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a deeper objection follows: {\i almanah di-chtiv be-Oraita mai ika lemeimar?} The word {\i almanah} appears throughout the Torah \u8212- long before any rabbinic institution of a 100-{\i zuz kesuva} existed. How can the Torah\u8217's use of the word be explained by a rabbinic institution that would not be created for a thousand years? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i de-atidin rabbanan de-matkenei lah maneh} \u8212- the Torah used the term {\i almanah} in anticipation of the future rabbinic enactment. And yes, the Torah does write with reference to future events: {\i ve-shem ha-nahar ha-shlishi \u7716 ?idekel hu ha-holech kidmat Ashur} \u8212- the third river is the \u7716 ?idekel, going east of Ashur. Rav Yosef teaches that {\i Ashur} here refers to Selika, a city that did not yet exist at the time of {\i Gan Eden}, when this verse was set. Thus the Torah names a future reality. Here too, the Torah called the widow {\i almanah} in anticipation of a future rabbinic institution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aggadic Wordplay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rain Drashah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since Rav \u7716 ?ana Bagdata\u8217'ah has been cited, the {\i gemara} brings several additional teachings in his name. On the word {\i matar} \u8212- rain \u8212- he offers an alliterative {\i drasha} on the letter {\i mem}: rain {\i mashkeh} \u8212- waters the ground; {\i meraveh} \u8212- sates it; {\i mezabel} \u8212- fertilizes it; {\i me\u8217'aden} \u8212- moistens it; and {\i mamshich} \u8212- draws out the fruit from the trees. Rava bar Yishmael, and some say Rav Yeimar bar Shelamya, identifies the scriptural basis in the verse {\i telameha raveh, na\u7717 ?at gedudeha bi-rvivim, temogegenah bi-rviv, tzim\u7717 ?ah tevarech} \u8212- you will sate its furrows, bring down its banks with raindrops, make it fertile with drops, bless its sprouting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Altar Drashah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar offers a similar alliterative treatment of the word {\i mizbe\u8217'a\u7717 ?} \u8212- altar. It {\i mazi\u8217'a\u7717 ?} \u8212- repels; {\i mazin} \u8212- sustains; {\i me\u7717 ?abev} \u8212- makes Israel dear; and {\i me\u7717 ?aper} \u8212- atones. The {\i gemara} asks whether {\i mazi\u8217'a\u7717 ?} and {\i me\u7717 ?aper} are not simply the same thing \u8212- both suggesting the removal of sin. The {\i gemara} distinguishes them: {\i mazi\u8217'a\u7717 ?} refers to {\i mazi\u8217'a\u7717 ? gezeirot} \u8212- casting off evil decrees \u8212- while {\i me\u7717 ?aper avonot} refers to atoning for sins themselves. Two distinct functions of the {\i mizbe\u8217'a\u7717 ?} are thus identified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dates Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav \u7716 ?ana Bagdata\u8217'ah then expounds on {\i tamrei} \u8212- dates. They {\i meshachnan} \u8212- warm the body; {\i mesav\u8217'an} \u8212- sate it; {\i meshalshelan} \u8212- act as a laxative; {\i me\u8217'ashran} \u8212- cause contentment; and {\i lo mefantan} \u8212- do not make one overly fastidious or spoiled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: {\i ekhol tamrei al yoreh} \u8212- if one has eaten dates, one should not issue {\i halachic} rulings, since they were thought to cloud thinking somewhat. The {\i gemara} challenges this from a teaching that dates in the morning and evening are beneficial, at midday they are unparalleled, and only at {\i Mincha} time \u8212- on an empty stomach in the afternoon \u8212- are they inadvisable. But clearly dates have many benefits; why should they impair judgment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is that the goodness of dates for the body \u8212- {\i ilu\u8217'ei ma\u8217'alu} \u8212- is not in question, but {\i le-fi sha\u8217'ata tarda} \u8212- at the moment of eating, they temporarily cloud one\u8217's thinking. They are analogous to wine in this respect: wine is beneficial, yet {\i ha-shoteh revi\u8217'it yayin al yoreh} \u8212- one who has drunk even three fluid ounces of wine should not issue rulings. A further resolution notes that the timing relative to a meal is decisive: {\i ha mikamai nahama, ha batar nahama} \u8212- the concern applies before bread, not after. Abaye\u8217's nursemaid taught him: {\i tamrei mikamai nahama ke-narga le-dikula} \u8212- dates before a meal are like a hatchet to a palm tree, destructive; {\i batar nahama ke-avra le-dasha} \u8212- after a meal they are like a bolt on a door, strengthening and beneficial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Wordplay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes with a series of wordplays. {\i Dasha} \u8212- a door \u8212- Rava explains as {\i derekh sham}, the path in. {\i Darga} \u8212- a step \u8212- is {\i derekh gag}, the way to the roof. {\i Puraiya} \u8212- a bed \u8212- Rav Papa explains as the place where {\i parin ve-ravin}, one procreates and multiplies. Finally, Rav Na\u7717 ?man bar Yitz\u7717 ?ak adds: {\i af anan nami neima} \u8212- let us similarly expound \u8212- {\i ilonit}, the term for a woman who has not physically matured and cannot bear children, is {\i dukhranita de-lo yadla} \u8212- like a male ram that cannot give birth. The {\i gemara} thus concludes this aggadic interlude with a series of playful but pointed observations about language, physiology, and the vocabulary through which the Sages described human life and its conditions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 11\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status Transitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Amounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 11 opens with a new {\i mishnah}. The previous {\i mishnah} had established the basic distinction in {\i ketubah} amounts between a {\i besulah} and a non-{\i besulah} \u8212- the difference between 200 and 100 {\i zuz} \u8212- grounded in the biblical verses, where the Torah recognizes only a {\i mohar}, an upfront payment, for a {\i besulah}. This {\i mohar} is not itself a {\i ketubah}, but it serves as its foundation. {\i Chachamim} subsequently instituted a transformation: they converted the upfront {\i mohar} into a deferred {\i ketubah} payment, and they further instituted a {\i ketubah} for a widow ({\i almanah}) as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether the {\i ketubah} is accurately described as alimony. The answer is that it is only partially analogous. In a case of divorce, the {\i ketubah} is indeed a lump-sum payment \u8212- but it is also collected upon the husband\u8217's death, functioning simultaneously as a substitute for inheritance, since by Torah law the wife does not inherit from her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remaining {\i mishnayot} of this section address cases that fall somewhere in between the clear categories of {\i besulah} and {\i almanah} \u8212- cases where it is not immediately obvious whether the woman\u8217's {\i ketubah} is 200 or 100. One such case was already addressed: a woman who is a {\i besulah} but had been married at the stage of {\i kiddushin} without {\i nisuin}, and therefore retains her status as a {\i besulah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the top of 11a introduces three new categories: {\i hagiyores vehashvuyah vehashifchah} \u8212- a female convert, a woman taken captive, and a maidservant who was a non-Jewish slave. In the first and third cases, the assumption is that as non-Jews, these women were presumed to be sexually promiscuous. In the case of the captive woman, sexual assault is assumed. If these women underwent their respective transitions \u8212- redemption, conversion, or manumission \u8212- while still under three years and one day old, then {\i ketuvasan masayim}, their {\i ketubah} is 200. The {\i mishnah} further states {\i veyesh lahen ta\u8217'anas besulim} \u8212- the husband retains a legitimate claim if, on the wedding night, she is discovered not to be a virgin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Under Three: Virginity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does being under three years old at the time of these transitions guarantee a {\i ketubah} of 200? The {\i gemara} operates with two interlocking assumptions. First, an act of intercourse with a girl under three years old is not halachically defined as an act of intercourse at all. Second, even if such an act occurred, the hymen regenerates \u8212- it does not remain ruptured. These two assumptions will be unpacked further in the following {\i mishnah}, which explores precisely what the definition of {\i besulah} requires: is it the absence of any act of intercourse, or is it the physical integrity of the hymen? As we will see, a girl under three satisfies both conditions: the act is legally a nullity, and the hymen is presumed to have restored itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ger Katan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does not immediately focus on the {\i ketubah} aspects of the {\i mishnah}. Instead, it pivots to the topic of {\i ger katan} \u8212- converting someone who is still a minor. This {\i sugya} dominates the remainder of the {\i amud}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Huna}: {\i ger katan matbilin oso al da\u8217'as beis din} \u8212- a minor who is converted is immersed on the understanding and with the approval of {\i beis din}. What is the underlying case? It could be an adopted infant, or it could be a situation in which someone wishes to convert a child born of a mixed marriage. The case {\i Rashi} describes, however, is somewhat different: the father is absent ({\i ein lo av}), and the mother \u8212- presumably herself a recent convert \u8212- brings the child to convert. The emphasis {\i Rashi} places on the father\u8217's absence is halachically significant, as we will discuss shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zachin Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai ka mashma lan} \u8212- what is the novel teaching here? The answer is: {\i dezchus hu lo}, that becoming Jewish is a benefit for the child, {\i vezachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav}, and one may perform a meritorious act on behalf of another person even without their presence. The minor is not considered \u8220"present\u8221" in the legally operative sense because he lacks the full adult capacity to give meaningful consent. The principle of {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav} therefore applies \u8212- {\i beis din} can act on his behalf precisely because this is considered to his benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benefit Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i gemara} immediately questions whether this is in fact the obvious application of {\i zachin}. One might have thought, {\i oveid kochavim be-hefkira nicha lei} \u8212- that a non-Jew prefers the unrestrained life of {\i hefker}, the freedom from the obligations that come with Jewish identity. If so, we could not objectively say that it is better for this infant to be Jewish. The {\i gemara} raises this further by noting the rule regarding a slave: {\i de-eved vadai be-hefkira nicha lei} \u8212- for a slave, we certainly assume he would prefer to remain a slave and live a life of {\i hefkerus}, rather than be freed and thereby become subject to Jewish obligations. The assumption is that the slave\u8217's life is even less constrained than that of a standard non-Jew, since the Sages assumed that slaves, lacking the structure of married life, lived with even fewer social constraints. If that is so for the slave, perhaps we should say the same for a non-Jewish infant: his subjective preference might be for a life without halachic obligation, and therefore we cannot assume that conversion constitutes a {\i zechus} for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taste of Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- the teaching is specifically telling us that we do consider it his benefit. The distinction is: {\i hani milei gadol}, this concern about subjective preference applies to an adult, {\i de-taim taim de-issura} \u8212- who has already tasted the forbidden fruit, who knows what that unrestrained life feels like and may be reluctant to give it up. {\i Aval katan}, for a minor, {\i zechus hu lo} \u8212- he has never had that experience. When you assess what is subjectively in his best interest, you assess it based on his current psychological reality and life experience, and from that vantage point conversion is unambiguously to his benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This establishes the underlying structure: we have a principle that one may act on another\u8217's behalf if it is to that person\u8217's benefit; we assert that conversion is objectively and even subjectively to the minor\u8217's benefit; and therefore {\i beis din} may proceed even though no adult is present representing the child\u8217's interests. The role of {\i beis din} here is to function as the adult in the room \u8212- to stand in for the minor and provide the legal acquiescence that makes the conversion effective. Just as parents make consequential decisions for their children all the time, legally authorized to act on their behalf and in their best interest, {\i beis din} is legally entitled to represent the interests of the minor and grant the necessary approval to proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains {\i Rashi}\u8217's position. {\i Rashi} specifies that the case involves a child {\i ein lo av} \u8212- without a biological father \u8212- and that the mother brings the child to convert. If the biological father is Jewish, he can make this decision for the child himself. Parents always make decisions for their children, and the father, as the legal head of the family in this patriarchal framework, can substitute for {\i beis din}. This is why {\i Rashi} specifically points to the absence of the father \u8212- it is precisely what necessitates {\i beis din}\u8217's involvement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if the father\u8217's role can substitute for {\i beis din}, why not the mother\u8217's role as well? It is a legitimate question, and the reality of the patriarchal legal structure means that the mother\u8217's role receives less legal recognition in this context. The practical upshot is that {\i Rashi}\u8217's case is describing a less common scenario \u8212- most cases of {\i ger katan} involve either an absent father or a Jewish father, and {\i Rashi} chose to illustrate the case where {\i beis din} is most clearly necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This foundational {\i gemara} sets the stage for what becomes in practice a three-way debate among later authorities regarding the standards for {\i geyrus katan}, particularly in the context of converting children in intermarried families. Some {\i achronim} argue that {\i beis din}\u8217's role is not merely to provide acquiescence as described above, but to perform the act of {\i kabbalas mitzvos} on behalf of the minor \u8212- since the minor himself cannot accept the commandments. This reading, it must be said, is not {\i pshat} in the {\i gemara} at all; this {\i gemara} does not mention {\i kabbalas mitzvos} even in passing, and most {\i rishonim} either say that {\i kabbalas mitzvos} is not required for a minor, or that the minor\u8217's subsequent adult life of Jewish observance constitutes a quasi-{\i kabbalas mitzvos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These different readings of {\i beis din}\u8217's role generate three distinct practical camps:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first camp holds that no {\i kabbalas mitzvos} is required at all. The only question is whether the conversion is a {\i zechus} for the minor. Even if the child is being raised in a non-observant home, this camp would argue that objectively it is still a {\i zechus} to be Jewish \u8212- the {\i mitzvos bein adam l\u8217'chaveiro} alone constitute a benefit, and the principle of {\i kol Yisrael yesh lahem chelek l\u8217'olam haba} may confer an objective advantage to Jewish status regardless of the level of observance in the home.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second camp \u8212- the mainstream middle position \u8212- holds that while full observance need not be guaranteed, the conversion must put the child on a reasonably likely path toward Jewish life. The family does not need to be {\i shomer Shabbos} or fully {\i shomrei kashrus}, but there must be some meaningful Jewish engagement \u8212- sending the child to Jewish day school, having a Jewish home environment, some connection to {\i shul}. This position attempts to make {\i zechus} a concrete and reasonable prediction rather than a pure abstraction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third camp, drawing on those {\i achronim} who understand {\i beis din} as performing {\i kabbalas mitzvos} on the minor\u8217's behalf, holds that {\i beis din} can only legitimately stand in for the minor and perform that {\i kabbalas mitzvos} if the child will in fact live a life of {\i shemiras mitzvos}. On this view, conversion is valid only when the child is being raised in a fully observant home.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Converts Too\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to bring a proof for {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's ruling from our very {\i mishnah} \u8212- a woman who converted under three years old must have done so with {\i beis din} approval. The {\i gemara} rejects this proof: {\i lo, hacha bemai askinan}, we are dealing here with a case of a convert who came with his children, {\i shenisgayer hu uvanav uvenosav imo}, where the father himself converted simultaneously with his children. In that case, {\i denicha lehu bemai de\u8217'avid avuhon} \u8212- because any child would naturally want what his father wants, the children\u8217's implicit will can be assumed, and there is no need to invoke the full mechanism of {\i beis din} acting through {\i zachin}. Therefore, this case provides no proof for the principle {\i Rav Huna} articulated, which addresses the more complex situation where no such assumption is available.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Objection Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yosef: higdilu yecholim limchos} \u8212- once minor converts reach adulthood, they can object and reverse their status. Since the conversion was done through the mechanism of {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav} \u8212- acting on behalf of the minor because it was deemed to be his benefit \u8212- the logic of that principle implies that when the minor actually becomes an adult and can speak for himself, he has the standing to say: this was not done for my benefit, and I reject it. The analogy is apt: if someone accepts a gift on my behalf while I am absent, that is valid through {\i zachin}. But the moment I am informed of the transaction and say I do not want it \u8212- invoking the principle of {\i soneh matanos yichye} \u8212- it is as though the acquisition never took place. We do not say I acquired it and must now divest myself of it; rather, the act retroactively loses its force. So too here: when the convert reaches adulthood, it is as though he is now present. If he objects, the conversion never fully took hold. If he does not object, it is sealed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eitivei Abaye}: Abaye challenges {\i Rav Yosef} from the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} states that a woman who converted under three years old has a {\i ketubah} of 200 {\i zuz}. If {\i higdilu yecholim limchos} \u8212- if she can reverse the conversion upon reaching adulthood \u8212- then how can we pay out her {\i ketubah}? {\i Yahavinan lah ktuva de\u8217'azla ve\u8217'achla begiyuta} \u8212- we would be giving money to a woman who will retroactively turn out to have been a non-Jew! The {\i gemara} answers: {\i lechi gadla} \u8212- we wait to pay the {\i ketubah} until she becomes an adult. If she reaches adulthood and does not object, her Jewish status is sealed, and only then do we make the payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sha\u8217'ah Achat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's challenge is then pressed further: {\i lechi gadla nami mimcha ve\u8217'afka} \u8212- even when she becomes an adult, she can still object and leave. Why does becoming an adult resolve anything? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i kivan she\u8217'gadla sha\u8217'ah achas velo michasa, shuv einah yechola limchos} \u8212- once she reached adulthood for even one moment and did not object, from that point forward she can no longer do so. The moment of reaching adulthood is the operative window. If at that first moment, upon being apprised of her situation, she does not protest, then it is as though she affirmed the transaction. After that, there is no reversal. Just as accepting a gift in the moment forecloses a later claim that you never wanted it, failing to object at the first moment of legal adulthood forecloses the possibility of later protest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} poses an analogous challenge regarding the {\i knas} \u8212- the fifty-{\i kesef} penalty for rape that applies to a {\i naarah} who is a virgin. The same problem arises: if we might pay this {\i knas} and she later objects to her conversion, the money would retroactively have gone to a non-Jew. The {\i gemara} gives the same structural answer, but the two {\i Amoraim} frame the concern differently. {\i Abaye} was not bothered by the {\i knas} case because, as the {\i gemara} explains, {\i she-lo ye-hei chotei niskar} \u8212- we do not want to benefit the sinner. Even if the money winds up going to someone who turns out to have been a non-Jew, the more pressing concern is that the rapist pays. {\i Rava}, for his part, was not bothered by the {\i ketubah} case because {\i she-lo te-hei kallah b\u8217'einav l\u8217'hotzi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the institution of {\i ketubah} exists to protect women in marriage, and that concern outweighs the possibility that in some cases the payment might go to someone who retroactively loses her Jewish status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What precisely constitutes the {\i sha\u8217'ah achas} \u8212- the critical moment in which the failure to object becomes irreversible? {\i Tosafos Yeshanim} addresses this directly, asking: {\i masai teida sha\u8217'as hamacha\u8217'ah} \u8212- how does one even identify the moment of objection? One answer given is that if, in the days immediately surrounding her {\i bat mitzvah}, she began behaving as a non-Jew \u8212- both shortly before and after \u8212- that constitutes a {\i micha\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} adds a further point in the context of the {\i knas} discussion. The {\i gemara} asks why we would pay out the {\i knas} when she might yet object upon becoming an adult. {\i Tosafos} ({\i Ri}) explains: {\i likhshe-t\u8217'gdal v\u8217'nahaga minhag yehudis} \u8212- we wait until she has grown up and conducted herself in the manner of a Jewish woman. This implies that it is not sufficient merely to refrain from objecting; the young woman must also have done something that affirmatively demonstrates Jewish conduct, even if only briefly, in the period immediately following her {\i bat mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the broader question: does the {\i micha\u8217'ah} require a formal act in front of witnesses, or can it be any behavior that demonstrates rejection of Jewish identity? And from the other side, does affirmation require a specific positive act? The {\i gemara} itself only speaks of the {\i absence} of {\i micha\u8217'ah} \u8212- it never demands a positive affirmation. Therefore, the standard is one of {\i i-michui}, not affirmative confirmation. What constitutes {\i michui} then becomes context-dependent: going for a cheeseburger in an observant household would likely constitute a {\i micha\u8217'ah}, while the same act in a non-observant household that nevertheless identifies as Jewish might not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In practice, many poskim hold that the rabbi performing the conversion should speak with the child before or at the time of {\i bar} or {\i bat mitzvah}, inform them of their status, and give them the opportunity to affirm or reject it. The child\u8217's continued Jewish conduct in the period immediately following {\i bar} or {\i bat mitzvah} \u8212- even if not explicitly articulated as an affirmation \u8212- would then constitute the operative non-{\i micha\u8217'ah}. These questions carry enormous practical weight in contemporary {\i gerut}, and the {\i sugya} of {\i ger katan} here is one of the central sources for navigating them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Besulah Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The following {\i mishnah} takes up the fundamental question underlying the entire discussion: what does it mean to be a {\i besulah}? Is virginity defined by the absence of any halachic act of intercourse, or by the physical intactness of the hymen?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents three cases. First, {\i ha-gadol she-ba al ha-ktana} \u8212- an adult man who had intercourse with a minor girl under three years old. Second, {\i v\u8217'katan she-ba al ha-gedolah} \u8212- a minor boy under nine who had intercourse with an adult woman. Third, {\i u\u8217'mukas eitz} \u8212- a woman whose hymen was ruptured by an external object rather than through intercourse. In all three cases, {\i Rabbi Meir} rules: {\i ketuvasan masayim}, they receive a {\i ketubah} of 200. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is internally consistent: virginity is defined as never having had an act of intercourse. In all three cases, there was no halachically recognized act of intercourse, and therefore they remain {\i besulos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vachachamim omrim}: the {\i Chachamim} disagree specifically regarding {\i mukas eitz}, ruling {\i kesuvosah maneh} \u8212- she receives only 100. For the {\i Chachamim}, the physical intactness of the hymen is part of the definition of {\i besulah}. A woman whose hymen has been ruptured, even not through intercourse, is not fully in the {\i besulah} category and therefore receives only a {\i maneh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Chuppah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses the case of a woman who was a {\i besulah} but had proceeded to {\i nisuin} \u8212- not merely {\i kiddushin} but {\i chuppah}, living in her husband\u8217's home \u8212- before being divorced or widowed without actually having had intercourse. Such a woman, {\i besulah almonah gerushah vechalutzah min ha-nisuin}, receives a {\i ketubah} of only 100, and there is no {\i ta\u8217'anas besulim} available to a subsequent husband. The rationale is formalistic: once a woman has entered into a state of {\i nisuin}, even if she never actually had intercourse, the legal presumption is that she has. We do not investigate every individual case; {\i lo plug}, the rule applies uniformly to all women who have been through {\i nisuin}. Correspondingly, since the {\i ketubah} is set at 100 on the assumption that she is not a virgin, a subsequent husband cannot complain that she was not a virgin \u8212- the two go together as a package. One cannot receive the reduced {\i ketubah} while simultaneously maintaining the expectation of physical virginity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Boy Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also addresses the converse of the first two categories: a convert, captive, or maidservant who underwent her status transition after three years and one day. In such cases, it is assumed that intercourse during that period would have constituted a halachic act and would not have allowed the hymen to regenerate. Therefore, {\i ketuvasan maneh ve\u8217'ein lahen ta\u8217'anas besulim} \u8212- the {\i ketubah} is 100 and there is no {\i ta\u8217'anas besulim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now takes up the case of {\i katan haba al ha-gedolah} \u8212- the minor boy who had intercourse with an adult woman. {\i Amar Rav Yehudah amar Rav: katan haba al hagedolah asaah mukas eitz} \u8212- this act, while not a halachic {\i biah} because the boy is under nine, nonetheless renders the woman a {\i mukas eitz}. The hymen has been physically ruptured, and that physical rupture has consequences regardless of whether the act is classified as {\i biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki amriseh kamei de-Shmuel}, but when this was reported before {\i Shmuel}, he responded: {\i ein mukas eitz be-basar} \u8212- one cannot create the status of {\i mukas eitz} through an act of flesh. If the rupture occurred through what was, even if not legally recognized as {\i biah}, nonetheless an act of intercourse, then the woman is neither a {\i mukas eitz} nor someone who has had a halachic act of sex, and she remains a {\i besulah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position of {\i Rav} is deeply counterintuitive, and {\i Tosafos} does not accept it. {\i Tosafos} argues that {\i Shmuel}\u8217's statement {\i ein mukas eitz be-basar} must be describing a scenario where the hymen was in fact not ruptured \u8212- {\i b\u8217'lo hipshir besulim}. If the hymen were ruptured, {\i Tosafos} cannot comprehend how the woman could be in a worse position than a {\i mukas eitz}: {\i lama yesh la lihyos p\u8217'chusa mi-mukas eitz?} \u8212- why should she be treated as less protected than a woman who experienced a traumatic external rupture? On the {\i Rav}\u8217's reading, a completely formalistic framework applies: the act was not {\i biah}, but it ruptured the hymen, and yet she is still a {\i besulah} in some fully valid sense. {\i Tosafos} finds this untenable. If the hymen is intact \u8212- which is what {\i Tosafos} argues {\i Shmuel} must be describing \u8212- then of course she remains a {\i besulah} and there is no issue. The debate of {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} therefore, on {\i Tosafos}\u8217' reading, is not about whether physical rupture creates the status of {\i mukas eitz}, but rather whether this particular kind of non-{\i biah} act can rupture the hymen at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mukat Eitz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 With Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rami bar Chama}: the debate between {\i Rabbi Meir} and the {\i Chachamim} regarding whether {\i mukas eitz} receives 200 or 100 is specifically in the case of {\i keshehikker bah} \u8212- when the husband knew about the woman\u8217's condition before marriage. He was aware that her hymen was not intact due to an external injury rather than intercourse, and he married her nonetheless. Even in that case, {\i Rabbi Meir} says 200 and {\i Chachamim} say 100. The entire debate therefore concerns the formal categorical question of which legal category she belongs to \u8212- not a question of mismatch between expectation and reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does {\i Rabbi Meir} classify her as a {\i besulah} and award 200? {\i Rabi Meir medamme lah l\u8217'bogeres} \u8212- he analogizes her to a physically mature woman ({\i bogeres}) whose hymen has naturally thinned with age but who is still considered a {\i besulah} because she has never had intercourse. The key distinction in {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's logic: {\i beulah asvid bah ma\u8217'aseh bidei adam} \u8212- a non-virgin is defined by the fact that a human act of intercourse took place. {\i Ha lo asvid bah ma\u8217'aseh bidei adam} \u8212- in the case of {\i mukas eitz}, no such act occurred. Without an act of intercourse, she remains in the {\i besulah} category, regardless of the physical state of the hymen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chachamim}, by contrast, analogize her to a {\i beulah} \u8212- a woman who has had intercourse. Why not classify her as a {\i bogeres}? {\i Bogeres lo asvid bah ma\u8217'aseh klal} \u8212- in the case of a {\i bogeres}, nothing external happened to her at all; the natural thinning of the hymen with physical maturity is not comparable to an external rupture. {\i Ha asvid bah ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- here, something did happen. An external event ruptured the hymen, and that rupture places her in the same functional category as a woman who has had intercourse, even if it was not an act of intercourse. The {\i Chachamim} therefore rule that she receives only 100.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Knowledge Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval lo hikker bah divrei hakol velo klum} \u8212- but if the husband did not know about the {\i mukas eitz} condition, then by unanimous agreement she receives nothing. In that case, the husband can claim {\i mekach taus} \u8212- a mistaken transaction. He was not merely asking whether she had had intercourse; he was expecting her to have an intact hymen, and she did not. When the reality diverges from what he was led to expect, the entire transaction is treated as entered into on false premises, and the {\i ketubah} obligation falls away entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Frameworks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus sets up two competing analytical frameworks. One centers on the question of formal halachic category: regardless of what the husband expected, what legal classification does this woman belong to? {\i Rabbi Meir} says she belongs in the {\i besulah} category; {\i Chachamim} say she belongs in the {\i beulah} category. This debate operates when the husband had full knowledge \u8212- expectation plays no role, and the question is purely formal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other framework is built around the expectation mismatch: when the husband did not know, the issue is not categorical but contractual. He entered the marriage with a certain expectation, that expectation was not met, and therefore the entire transaction is void. On this reading, the question of {\i mukas eitz}\u8217's formal category becomes irrelevant \u8212- what matters is whether there was a {\i mekach taus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues by opening the possibility that the entire debate between {\i Rabbi Meir} and {\i Chachamim} might in fact be structured the other way around: perhaps the disagreement concerns specifically the case where the husband did {\i not} know, and the question is how far the logic of {\i mekach taus} extends \u8212- whereas when the husband did know, perhaps all would agree that she receives the full 200. That alternative reading will be developed as the daf proceeds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 13\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Virginity Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Claims Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah that opens our discussion on 12b is the culmination of a theme that has run through the tractate from its very first pages. From the outset, Ketubot has been concerned with the day of the wedding and the morning appearance in court, with the varying amounts of the {\i kesubah} depending on the woman\u8217's status. Now we finally arrive at the scenario that makes all of that relevant: the morning after the wedding, when a dispute erupts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is introduced as follows: {\i hanoise es ha\u8217'isha velo matza lah besulim} \u8212- he married a woman and found no evidence of virginity. Notably, in this case the wife does not dispute the underlying fact; she does not claim she was a virgin at the time of the wedding. Instead, the debate is over the interpretation of events. She says, {\i mishe\u8217'erastani ne\u8217'enasti} \u8212- \u8220"After our {\i kiddushin}, I was raped.\u8221" In the striking idiom of the Mishnah: {\i venistachfa sadehu} \u8212- \u8220"his field was flooded.\u8221" The imagery is vivid if jarring; the point is that something happened to him, an event that befell his property after he had already acquired it, and therefore she is still entitled to the full {\i kesubah} of 200.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The husband\u8217's counter-claim is equally direct: {\i lo ki ela ad shelo erastich} \u8212- \u8220"No, you were not a virgin even before the {\i kiddushin}.\u8221" And with that he invokes the concept of {\i mekach taus} \u8212- the entire transaction was entered into under a misapprehension. The precise implications of {\i mekach taus} for the validity of the {\i kiddushin} itself remain complex, but at the very least the husband is arguing that he owes nothing close to 200, and perhaps owes nothing at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah records a three-way Tannaitic debate. {\i Rabban Gamliel ve\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer omrim ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Eliezer say she is believed. Rabbi Yehoshua demurs sharply: {\i lo mipiha anu chayim} \u8212- \u8220"We do not live from her mouth, from her words alone.\u8221" In his view, {\i harei zu bechezkas be\u8217'ula ad shelo tisarast} \u8212- she carries the presumption of having been a {\i be\u8217'ulah} before the {\i kiddushin}, and she has deceived him. Until she brings actual proof of her claim, that presumption stands against her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monetary Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One natural way to frame this dispute is through the lens of {\i dinei mamonos}. She holds a {\i shtar} \u8212- her {\i kesubah} \u8212- which she claims is worth 200. He disputes that valuation and contends it is worth 100 or perhaps zero. When there is a monetary dispute of this kind and no clear evidence, the governing principle should be {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'aya}: the party seeking to extract money from another\u8217's possession bears the burden of proof. The Gemara\u8217's initial approach to our Mishnah is precisely this \u8212- to apply that rule and determine who, on that basis, should prevail.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bari vs.\u160 ?Shema\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definite vs.\u160 ?Doubtful\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a parallel case to sharpen the analysis. {\i Maneh li beyadcha vehalla omer eini yodea} \u8212- one person says to another, \u8220"You owe me a hundred,\u8221" and the other responds, \u8220"I don\u8217't know.\u8221" This is to be distinguished from the case where the second party concedes the debt but claims he may have repaid it; there, everyone agrees he must pay, since he acknowledges the obligation without being able to affirm its discharge. Here, however, the claimant has a {\i bari} \u8212- a definite, certain claim \u8212- while the respondent holds a {\i shema} \u8212- a position of genuine uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definite Prevails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda and Rav Huna rule: {\i chayiv} \u8212- the respondent must pay. Their principle is {\i bari ve\u8217'shema bari adif}: when a definite claim confronts an uncertain one, the definite claim prevails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possession Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman and Rabbi Yochanan rule: {\i patur} \u8212- the respondent is exempt. Their principle is {\i oki mamona bechezkas mara} \u8212- money remains in the possession of the one currently holding it, and to extract it requires concrete proof. The {\i hamotzi mechavero} rule stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, Abaye reports to Rav Yosef a tradition in the name of Shmuel: the ruling of Rav Yehuda and Rav Huna \u8212- that the {\i bari} claim prevails \u8212- is in fact the position of Shmuel. The proof is from the very Mishnah we will be examining on 13a.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah there presents a case of a single woman who is pregnant. She is asked {\i ma tivo shel ubar zeh} \u8212- what is the nature of this fetus, meaning: is there any concern that this child is a {\i mamzer} or otherwise {\i pasul}? She responds: {\i me\u8217'ish ploni ve\u8217'kohen hu} \u8212- the father is so-and-so, and he is a {\i kohen}, meaning a man of unblemished status. Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Eliezer say she is believed, both regarding her own personal status and presumably the fetus\u8217's. Rabbi Yehoshua says she is not believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: {\i halacha ke\u8217'Rabban Gamliel} \u8212- we rule like Rabban Gamliel. Then Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda pressed Rav Yehuda further, calling him {\i Shinena} \u8212- \u8220"sharp one\u8221" \u8212- and reminded him that Shmuel had said {\i halacha ke\u8217'Rabban Gamliel af berishona}, meaning: Shmuel rules like Rabban Gamliel not only in the case of personal status, but even in the {\i first} Mishnah \u8212- the monetary dispute about the {\i kesubah} value. And what makes that first case a larger {\i chiddush}? Because there, one could have said {\i okim mamona bechezkas marei} \u8212- keep the money where it is. Despite that, Shmuel rules for her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This gives us the conclusion: {\i amar Rabban Gamliel bari adif} \u8212- even against the {\i chezkas mamona} of the husband, her {\i bari} claim carries the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks directly: perhaps the dispute between Rav Yehuda and Rav Huna on one side and Rav Nachman and Rabbi Yochanan on the other is simply a reprise of the Tannaitic dispute in our Mishnah \u8212- Rabban Gamliel versus Rabbi Yehoshua? To this question, Rav Nachman himself responds: the positions need not collapse into one another, and he can agree with Rabban Gamliel in our Mishnah even while maintaining his general rule. This is the burden of the next section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reconciling Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migo & Chazakah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman explains: {\i ad kan lo ka\u8217'amar Rabban Gamliel hatam ela d\u8217'ika migo} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling in the Mishnah is not simply about {\i bari adif} in the abstract. It is grounded in additional considerations that are particular to this case. Specifically, the woman has a {\i migo}. A {\i migo} is the principle that if a party could have made a stronger or more favorable claim, the fact that she chose a lesser or more vulnerable one gives that claim additional credibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Could Lie Better\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is her {\i migo} here? As we shall see, the next Mishnah involves the claim of {\i mukas etz} \u8212- that a woman\u8217's non-virginal state results not from intercourse but from a physical injury. In the first Mishnah, the wife is not claiming {\i mukas etz}; she is conceding she was raped. Had she wanted to lie in a more self-serving way, she could have claimed {\i mukas etz}, which would have affected her status less adversely. The fact that she volunteered the more damaging claim \u8212- that she was raped \u8212- lends her words greater credibility. She had a better lie available and chose not to tell it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively \u8212- or additionally \u8212- she holds a {\i chazakah}. Every person is born with the presumption of whatever status they began with. A woman is born a virgin, and therefore we maintain that presumption until the last possible moment. If we are uncertain when her virginity was lost, we push that event as late as we can \u8212- meaning after the {\i kiddushin}, which supports her claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Neither of these factors operates in the simple {\i maneh li beyadcha} case. The person demanding money has no {\i migo} \u8212- there is no \u8220"better lie\u8221" he declined to tell \u8212- and he has no {\i chazakah} of a previous debt. Hence Rav Nachman can say: by the clean {\i bari ve\u8217'shema} case, I maintain {\i okim mamona bechezkas mara}; but in our Mishnah, there are the additional supports of {\i migo} and {\i chazakah}, which justify the ruling of Rabban Gamliel independently of any general {\i bari adif} principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that Tosafos develops an even more textured analysis here by distinguishing between a {\i bari tov} and a {\i bari garu\u8217'a}, and between a {\i shema tov} and a {\i shema garu\u8217'a}. Not all {\i bari} claims are of equal strength, and not all {\i shema} positions are of equal weakness. In our Mishnah, the wife\u8217's {\i bari} is in one sense weak \u8212- she is the only one who knows the truth and faces no real check on her ability to lie \u8212- while the husband\u8217's {\i shema} is also correspondingly weak, since he really should have some independent knowledge of the situation. Tosafos notes that this actually makes our Mishnah a {\i bigger chiddush} than the simple {\i maneh li beyadcha} case, rather than a smaller one. But that analysis belongs to a deeper level of the discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara confirms that this answer is sound: {\i hach nami mistabra kidimishaninan} \u8212- it makes good sense the way we have answered. The proof is structural: we rule like Rav Nachman in monetary cases, which means our baseline {\i psak} is {\i lav bari adif}. Yet we also rule like Rabban Gamliel in our Mishnah, as Shmuel established. The only way to hold both positions without contradiction is to say that our Mishnah is a special case \u8212- that it is the {\i migo} or the {\i chazakah} (or both) that tips the scales for the woman here, not a general {\i bari adif} rule. {\i Shema mina} \u8212- this is a sound proof of that resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes by noting the practical ruling: {\i hilchesa} \u8212- the law \u8212- follows this structure, holding like Rav Nachman in general monetary disputes while affirming Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling in the specific context of our Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mukat Etz Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Competing Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah now introduces its second case, which runs in parallel to the first but with a significant shift in the nature of the competing claims. Again, both parties agree that the wife was not a virgin at the time of the wedding. The wife, however, says: {\i mukas etz ani} \u8212- I was injured by a piece of wood; my non-virginal state results from a physical injury, not from sexual intercourse. The husband counters: {\i lo ki ela drusas ish at} \u8212- no, you had intercourse with a man before the {\i kiddushin}. The same debate among the Tannaim follows: Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Eliezer believe her; Rabbi Yehoshua does not, maintaining her presumptive status as {\i drusas ish} until she proves otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Frame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: what exactly are the monetary stakes in this second Mishnah, and how do the opinions of Rabbi Meir and the Rabbanan determine the structure of the dispute? Rabbi Yochanan reads the Mishnah as a debate conducted within the framework of Rabbi Meir. She is claiming 200, and he is countering that she deserves only 100. Why? Rabbi Meir holds that {\i bein hikkir bah bein lo hikkir bah masayim} \u8212- whether or not the husband was aware that the woman was a {\i mukas etz}, her {\i kesubah} is the full 200. Hence, she claims: I am a {\i mukas etz}, and under Rabbi Meir\u8217's ruling I deserve 200. He responds: you are in fact a {\i be\u8217'ulah}, not a {\i mukas etz}, and a {\i be\u8217'ulah} deserves only 100. The dispute is between 200 and 100, situated entirely within Rabbi Meir\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Frame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar reads the Mishnah differently \u8212- as a debate conducted within the framework of the Rabbanan, with the stakes being 100 or zero. The Rabbanan hold that a {\i mukas etz} receives 100 regardless of whether the husband knew. She claims she is a {\i mukas etz} and therefore entitled to 100. He counters that she is a {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- and since he entered the marriage under the presumption that she was a {\i besulah} and discovered otherwise, she receives nothing. This is the {\i kinsa bechezkas besulah venimtzes be\u8217'ulah} scenario, which yields zero under his reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains why Rabbi Yochanan cannot read the Mishnah within the framework of the Rabbanan: under the Rabbanan\u8217's position, and with Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's own view that {\i kinsa bechezkas besulah venimtzes be\u8217'ulah yesh lah kesubah maneh} \u8212- even if she was discovered to be a {\i be\u8217'ulah}, she still receives 100 \u8212- the husband\u8217's claim and the wife\u8217's claim would yield identical amounts. If she says {\i mukas etz} she gets 100, and if he says {\i be\u8217'ulah} she also gets 100. There would be no dispute to adjudicate, no {\i nafka minah}. Hence Rabbi Yochanan must situate the Mishnah within Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Cases Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: given that Rabbi Elazar reads both Mishnayot within the framework of the Rabbanan, we can readily explain why two separate Mishnayot are needed, since each one delivers a distinct {\i chiddush}. But what is the purpose of having two cases according to Rabbi Yochanan?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Elazar, the first Mishnah \u8212- where the wife claims she was raped after {\i kiddushin} \u8212- establishes that {\i kinsa bechezkas besulah venimtzes be\u8217'ulah} yields zero. This rejects the position embraced by Rabbi Chiya bar Avin, who held that even when a woman enters as a presumed {\i besulah} and is found to be a {\i be\u8217'ulah}, she retains 100. The second Mishnah \u8212- the {\i mukas etz} case \u8212- establishes that {\i kinsa bechezkas besulah venimtzes mukas etz} still entitles her to 100. This rejects the position of Rami bar Chama, who held that any misrepresentation, even a {\i mukas etz} situation, results in zero. Each Mishnah is thus targeting a different mistaken view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yochanan, however, who situates the second Mishnah within Rabbi Meir, the {\i chiddush} about {\i mukas etz} not yielding zero is obviously present there. And since Rabbi Yochanan himself holds that {\i nimtzes be\u8217'ulah} still yields 100, the first Mishnah under the Rabbanan\u8217's framework would also not yield zero. So what is the second Mishnah adding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer, attributed to Rav Sheshet, is that the two Mishnayot are needed to demonstrate the full extent of the opposing Tannaitic positions. The first Mishnah reveals the strength of Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position: {\i af al gav de\u8217'ika le\u8217'meimar migo lo mehemena} \u8212- even though the wife has a {\i migo} in that case (she could have claimed {\i mukas etz} rather than rape, a less self-incriminating claim), Rabbi Yehoshua still does not believe her. The second Mishnah reveals the strength of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's position: {\i af al gav de\u8217'leka le\u8217'meimar migo ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- even though in the {\i mukas etz} case the wife has no {\i migo} (she is already making the best possible claim; {\i mukas etz} does not diminish her status), Rabban Gamliel believes her and she receives the full 200. The two cases are thus needed to trace the full arc of the disagreement between the Tannaim.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seen with Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah now shifts from monetary disputes to questions of personal status. {\i Ra\u8217'uah medaberes im echad} \u8212- a woman was seen speaking with a man. The Mishnah will ask what we are to make of this, and in particular whether we believe her account of who that man was. The question is not merely abstract; it bears directly on the woman\u8217's fitness to marry a {\i kohen} and on the status of any child she might have. If she had relations with a {\i mamzer} or a non-Jew, she would become {\i pasul} to a {\i kohen}, and the child\u8217's lineage would be compromised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting in passing that elsewhere in Ketubot, a wife who {\i medaberes im kol adam bashuk} \u8212- who speaks to strangers in the marketplace \u8212- is grounds for divorce without payment of the {\i kesubah}. This was a highly segregated society, and a woman\u8217's speaking to a strange man was not taken as an innocuous act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She is asked: {\i ma tivo shel ish zeh} \u8212- what is the story of this man? She responds: {\i ish ploni ve\u8217'kohen hu} \u8212- it was so-and-so, and he is a {\i kohen}. The mention of {\i kohen} is not strictly necessary; the point is that the man is of valid Jewish lineage and that nothing about the encounter could have compromised her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Again\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same Tannaitic debate recurs with the same formulations. Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Eliezer say she is believed. Rabbi Yehoshua says {\i lo mipiha anu chayim} \u8212- we do not live from her words. He holds that she carries the presumption of having had relations with a {\i nasin} or a {\i mamzer}, rendering her {\i pasul} to a {\i kohen}, until she can prove otherwise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seen with Man (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Medaberet Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before we can analyze the Tannaitic debate, we must establish what the Mishnah\u8217's first case actually involves. The Mishnah says she was {\i medaberes im echad} \u8212- \u8220"speaking with someone.\u8221" But does this mean she merely conversed with a man, possibly in seclusion, or does it mean something more?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seclusion vs.\u160 ?Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeiri: Seclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Zeiri says: {\i medaberes} means {\i nistera} \u8212- she went into seclusion with a man. The term is a description of private contact, not of sexual intercourse per se. Under this reading, the Mishnah is saying that even in a case where we only know she was alone with a man \u8212- without any direct knowledge of sexual activity \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua holds that her status is in suspicion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi: Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi says: {\i medaberes} means {\i niv\u8217'ala} \u8212- she actually had sexual relations. The word {\i medaberes} is used because it is {\i lishana me\u8217'alya} \u8212- a euphemism, a refined term. The Mishnah employs indirect language to describe what is in fact a known sexual encounter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mouth Euphemism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings a proof for Rav Assi\u8217's reading from the verse in Proverbs that describes the way of an adulterous woman: {\i achla umachta pi\u8217'a} \u8212- she eats and wipes her mouth. The \u8220"mouth\u8221" language is itself a euphemism for sexual activity. The Gemara connects this to the {\i medaberes} formulation in our Mishnah, suggesting that speech and mouth language function as established biblical idioms for sexual conduct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading has significant practical consequences. If Zeiri is correct and the Mishnah\u8217's first case is merely seclusion, then Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's ruling is extraordinarily stringent: even without knowing that any intercourse occurred, a woman\u8217's status becomes suspect based solely on private contact. This would seem to clash with the ruling of Rav, who holds that {\i malkin al ha-yichud ve-ein osrin al ha-yichud} \u8212- we administer rabbinic lashes for seclusion of a married woman with a stranger, but we do not thereby forbid her to her husband. If even a married woman does not lose her status from mere seclusion, why would a single woman\u8217's lineage-related status be compromised? The Gemara addresses this tension, suggesting that when it comes to {\i yuchasin} \u8212- questions of lineage and fitness to marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- a special stringency applies: {\i ma\u8217'ala asu be-yuchasin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Cases Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: according to Zeiri, why does the Mishnah need two separate cases \u8212- the case where she is {\i medaberes} and the case where she is pregnant? Each case adds a distinct dimension. The first \u8212- seclusion \u8212- tests how far Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's stringency extends (even without knowing she had sex). The second \u8212- pregnancy, where we know beyond doubt that she had sex \u8212- tests how far Rabban Gamliel\u8217's leniency extends (even when the fact of intercourse is established).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rav Assi, however, since the first case already involves known sexual relations, the two cases could seem redundant. The Gemara answers: {\i chada lehachsher ba ve\u8217'chada lehachsher be\u8217'vita} \u8212- one case establishes her own status as valid, and the other establishes that the fetus\u8217's status is also valid. Why might the fetus\u8217's status be treated differently? Because the woman herself has a {\i chazakah} \u8212- she started life with a presumptively valid status, and we are reluctant to undermine it. But the fetus has no prior status; its lineage is entirely unknown. One might therefore have said that even if we trust her account of her own status, we remain doubtful about the fetus. The second case teaches that Rabban Gamliel believes her even with respect to the child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis holds, the Gemara notes, according to the position that {\i le\u8217'divrei ha-machsher ba machsher be\u8217'vita} \u8212- according to those who validate her, they validate the child as well. But there is a competing view that {\i le\u8217'divrei ha-machsher ba posel be\u8217'vita} \u8212- even those who accept her own status would still consider the child of doubtful status. Under that view, both cases in the Mishnah are about her status only, and if the first case already establishes that point, the second adds nothing. The Gemara resolves this by concluding that Rav Assi held the former position \u8212- that validating the mother means validating the child \u8212- so that the two cases are indeed each necessary.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Location Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruins Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is now introduced to further elaborate the Mishnah\u8217's cases. The {\i baraita} speaks of a woman who went into a {\i churba} \u8212- a ruin \u8212- with a man. Rashi notes that a {\i churba} carries a stronger presumption of sexual activity than mere seclusion; ruins are places where people go specifically for trysts, away from public view, making the assumption of intercourse more reasonable than in a simple case of seclusion. The {\i baraita} then presents two sub-cases: {\i churba de\u8217'mata} \u8212- a ruin within the city \u8212- and {\i churba de\u8217'dabra} \u8212- a ruin in the field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: if the word {\i medaberes} in the Mishnah is understood in one way, we can explain why these two ruin cases are needed. But according to Rav Assi, who already interprets {\i medaberes} as actual intercourse, what is the point of distinguishing two kinds of ruins? The Gemara initially suggests that perhaps {\i seter} and {\i churba} describe a single scenario \u8212- the privacy of a ruin. But this is quickly rejected, since the text explicitly says {\i o le\u8217'churba} \u8212- \u8220"or a ruin\u8221" \u8212- implying two distinct situations. The Gemara then commits to the reading that there are genuinely two different cases of {\i churba}, distinguished by their location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kosher Majority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De\u8217'i ashmeinan churba de\u8217'mata} \u8212- had the {\i baraita} mentioned only the ruin in the city, we might have understood Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling narrowly. In that case, {\i rov ksheirim etzlah} \u8212- the majority of the people in the city who might have been her partner are halachically valid Jews; non-Jews and {\i mamzerim} are rare. Rabban Gamliel\u8217's willingness to believe her in that context would be unsurprising.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalid Majority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i churba de\u8217'dabra} \u8212- a ruin in the field \u8212- presents a different demographic reality. In the open countryside, {\i rov psulim etzlah} \u8212- the majority of men she might have encountered are non-Jews, who would render her {\i pasul} to a {\i kohen} if she had relations with them. One might have thought that even Rabban Gamliel would concede to Rabbi Yehoshua in that case, since the statistical likelihood cuts against her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yichus Severity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, had the {\i baraita} mentioned only the field case, we might have understood Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's ruling as limited to that scenario, where the majority are {\i psulim}. We might then have thought that in the city \u8212- where most are {\i kasher} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua would concede to Rabban Gamliel. By including both cases, the {\i baraita} teaches that Rabbi Yehoshua maintains his stringency {\i afilu be\u8217'rov ksheirim} \u8212- even where the majority are valid. His position is grounded not in statistics but in the categorical principle of {\i ma\u8217'ala asu be\u8217'yuchasin}: when it comes to questions of lineage and fitness for priestly marriage, we apply an elevated standard of scrutiny regardless of the demographic majority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara confirms this analysis by citing a statement of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i mi\u8217'divrei ha-machsher machsher afilu be\u8217'rov psulim, u\u8217'mi\u8217'divrei ha-posel posel afilu be\u8217'rov ksheirim} \u8212- those who validate her do so even where most are {\i psulim}, and those who invalidate her do so even where most are {\i ksheirim}. The positions are absolute in both directions, which explains why the comparison to the captive woman could be introduced without those who defend her immediately objecting that the cases are statistically incomparable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captive Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua now challenges Rabban Gamliel with a comparison: {\i i atem modim bishvuya she\u8217'nishbet ve\u8217'yesh lah edim she\u8217'nishbet ve\u8217'hi omeret tehora ani she\u8217'eina ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- do you not concede that if a woman has been taken captive by non-Jews, and there are witnesses to that captivity, she is not believed when she claims she was not violated? That case is one where you accept that a woman\u8217's assurances about her own sexual history cannot be credited. How, then, can you believe this woman when she identifies the man she was with?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Eliezer concede the captive case: {\i aval} \u8212- yes, that is true.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They then press back: {\i mah hefresh bein zo l\u8217'zo?} \u8212- what difference is there between the captive and this woman? The answer is: {\i l\u8217'zo yesh edim u\u8217'l\u8217'zo ein edim} \u8212- in the case of the captive, there are witnesses to the captivity itself, establishing that she was in the hands of non-Jews who we presume would violate her. Here, in the case of seclusion or private contact, there are no such witnesses establishing that she was with a man who would be {\i pasul}. The cases are therefore not comparable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Guardian\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua counters: {\i af l\u8217'zo yesh edim she\u8217'harei k\u8217'reisa bein sheineiha} \u8212- but in the pregnant case, there is visible evidence; her pregnancy is itself the proof that she has had relations. So why is the pregnant woman more trustworthy than the captive?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They respond: {\i rov ovdei kochavim prutzim ba\u8217'arayot hem} \u8212- non-Jews are presumed to be sexually aggressive; when a woman is in their captivity, we assume violation occurred with non-Jews specifically, which is what renders her {\i pasul}. But when a Jewish woman goes into private with an unidentified man, we do not presume that the man was one who would disqualify her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's rejoinder is the principle that has become one of the most cited phrases in this area of halacha: {\i ein apotropus la\u8217'arayot} \u8212- there is no guardian over sexual urges. The impulse toward forbidden relations does not discriminate by nationality. If we are going to assume that non-Jewish captors will violate a woman, we must equally assume that when a man and woman are in private, relations occur. The argument from the captive therefore applies to the seclusion case as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status & Shtuki\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} concludes on a notably stringent note. When does the {\i ne\u8217'emanus} principle \u8212- that she is believed about her own status \u8212- apply? Only when {\i bata hee gufa} \u8212- she herself comes forward and testifies. But when the question is about the child \u8212- {\i aval he\u8217'idah isha be\u8217'vita} \u8212- the {\i baraita} rules {\i divrei ha\u8217'kol ha\u8217'velad shtuki}: everyone agrees that the child is a {\i shtuki}, a person of doubtful paternity whose claim of \u8220"who is my father?\u8221" is met with silence. This is a striking and severe conclusion. Even according to Rabban Gamliel, who validates the woman\u8217's own status, the child\u8217's status cannot be established on the basis of her testimony alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara at this point draws together the threads of the debate, noting that the exchange between Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Yehoshua moves through several shifting positions \u8212- the seclusion case, the pregnancy case, the captive comparison \u8212- each party advancing and retreating depending on which sub-case is under discussion. The {\i baraita} also confirms the reading of Zeiri that {\i medaberes} means seclusion rather than known intercourse, since the whole discussion about the captive comparison only makes sense if the first case involves doubt about whether intercourse occurred at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's statement \u8212- {\i le\u8217'divrei ha-machsher bah machsher be\u8217'vita, u\u8217'le\u8217'divrei ha-posel bah posel be\u8217'vita} \u8212- and the question of whether those who validate her necessarily validate the daughter, will be taken up at the start of the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 14\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seclusion & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Fetus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens by picking up toward the bottom of 13b, in the context of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's dispute between Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Yehoshua regarding a woman who went into seclusion with a man of questionable status. What has already been established is that Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's stringency applies even to mere seclusion \u8212- not only to cases where intercourse is known to have occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this backdrop, Rabbi Yochanan formulates a sweeping principle: {\i le-divrei ha-machshir bah machshir be-vittah} \u8212- the authority who validates the woman validates the fetus as well; and correspondingly, {\i le-divrei ha-posel bah posel be-vittah} \u8212- the authority who invalidates the woman invalidates the fetus. The larger {\i chiddush} is the first half of the statement. The woman herself had a {\i chezkat kashrut} \u8212- a presumptive status of being permitted \u8212- before the encounter in question, and there is therefore a stronger basis to argue that a single doubtful incident should not strip her of that presumption. The fetus, by contrast, enters the world with no prior presumed status at all. Its very existence is bound up in the doubtful union. And yet Rabbi Yochanan maintains that according to Rabban Gamliel \u8212- who accepts her claim and rules her status unimpaired \u8212- the same leniency extends to the fetus. The specific concern is the daughter: if the woman slept with a man she was forbidden to, the resulting daughter would become a {\i chalala}, disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar disagrees. Even Rabban Gamliel, who validates the woman herself, would concede that her daughter born from this union is disqualified. The suspicion that she might be a {\i chalala} \u8212- or carry some other form of disqualification relevant to {\i kehunah} \u8212- is sufficient to render the child\u8217's status problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chezakat Kashrut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava articulates the underlying rationale for Rabbi Elazar\u8217's distinction. {\i Bishlama hi it lah chezakah de-kashrut} \u8212- it is understandable that the woman herself is protected, because she possessed an established presumption of valid status before this event ever occurred. {\i Bittah leit lah chezakah de-kashrut} \u8212- the daughter, however, never had any such presumption. She may have been conceived in a forbidden union from the very outset, and in the absence of a prior {\i chezakah} to fall back on, the doubt attaches to her fully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shetuki Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beraitah Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar then presses his challenge against Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position by citing a {\i beraita}: {\i bameh devarim amurim be-edut ishah be-gufah} \u8212- when is a woman\u8217's testimony credited? Only with respect to her own personal status. But {\i edut ishah be-vittah} \u8212- when it comes to her daughter \u8212- {\i divrei ha-kol ha-valad shetuki}: all authorities agree that the child is a {\i shetuki}. The word derives from {\i shetok} \u8212- silence, or \u8220"be quiet\u8221" \u8212- conveying the idea that when the child asks who its father is, the answer is, in effect, withheld. The term denotes a recognized status of doubtful paternity, and the presumption is that such a child cannot marry a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shetuki Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, however, refuses to concede the point. {\i Mai lav shetuki u-pasul?} \u8212- does the {\i beraita} not mean that the child is a {\i shetuki} and therefore disqualified? {\i Lo}, he replies: {\i shetuki ve-kasher} \u8212- the child is a {\i shetuki} but is nonetheless {\i kasher}. The {\i gemara} presses back: {\i u-mi ika shetuki kasher?} Is there any recognized case in which a child is designated a {\i shetuki} and yet remains in good standing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is yes \u8212- and the proof is from Shmuel: if ten {\i kohanim} are standing together and one steps away, has relations with a woman, and returns to the group, the resulting child is a {\i shetuki}. The mother did not sleep with anyone of disqualifying status \u8212- she is a single woman, the father was undoubtedly a {\i kohen} \u8212- and so the child\u8217's personal status is entirely {\i kasher}. There is simply no way to establish paternity. Why then is the child called a {\i shetuki}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first considers whether the term means the child is silenced from the father\u8217's estate \u8212- i.e., cannot claim inheritance from any of the ten {\i kohanim} should one die. But this is {\i pshita}, self-evident: since paternity is entirely unknown, of course no inheritance claim can be established. That cannot be the {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative meaning, the {\i gemara} concludes, is {\i ela she-mashtikin oto mi-din kehunah} \u8212- the child is silenced from the rights of {\i kehunah}. The verse states: {\i ve-hayta lo u-le-zaro acharav brit kehunat olam} \u8212- the covenant of {\i kehunah} shall be for him and for his seed after him. The Talmud derives from this that only {\i mi she-zaro meyuchas acharav} \u8212- one whose lineage is traced and attributed to him \u8212- may serve as a {\i kohen}. Since this child cannot identify his father, he is barred from performing the {\i avodah} of {\i kehunah}, even though there is no question that his father was a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shetuki vs.\u160 ?Pasul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is critical. Rabbi Yochanan is insisting that the {\i shetuki} designation in the {\i beraita} must be understood narrowly. The child\u8217's personal status \u8212- including the question of whether a daughter born of this situation may marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- remains entirely valid. The only consequence of {\i shetuki} status, in this case, is that a male child cannot serve in the {\i kehunah} because his specific patrilineal identity is unverified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast between the positions is thus quite stark. At one extreme, Rabbi Yehoshua is stringent even where the woman merely went into seclusion without confirmed intercourse; even if the majority of men she might have encountered would not have disqualified her, her status \u8212- and that of the child \u8212- is suspect. Rabban Gamliel occupies the opposite pole: even in a case where we know she had intercourse, even if it occurred in a field where the majority of men are non-Jews and would therefore disqualify her, her credible claim as to the identity of the man is sufficient to permit her. And according to Rabbi Yochanan, this leniency extends not only to the woman but to the fetus \u8212- though Rabbi Elazar disputes this on the grounds that the fetus lacks its own {\i chezakah de-kashrut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Betrothed Pair Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Admission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a case that falls at the top of 14a. A betrothed man and woman \u8212- {\i arus ve-arusato} \u8212- came before Rav Yosef. The woman was pregnant. She said: {\i minei hu} \u8212- it is his child. And he said: {\i in minai} \u8212- yes, it is mine. Both parties thus affirm the same paternity. In the region of Yehuda, it was not unheard of for betrothed couples to cohabit before the formal {\i nisuin}, but the situation still raised questions about the child\u8217's status. Rav Yosef, however, expressed confidence in ruling leniently, citing two grounds: first, {\i chada de-ha kamodeh} \u8212- the man himself admits paternity, which is the more significant concession; and second, citing the principle established by Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel \u8212- {\i halacha ke-Rabban Gamliel} \u8212- that we rule in accordance with Rabban Gamliel\u8217's lenient position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov Kasherim Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges this reasoning. He asks: are you suggesting that even had the husband {\i not} admitted paternity, Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling would apply? He recalls that Shmuel, when communicating this ruling to Rav Yehuda, added a crucial qualification: {\i ve-at lo ta\u8217'avid uvda ad de-ika rov kisherim} \u8212- and you, in practice, should not act on this ruling unless the majority of the men she might have had relations with are {\i kasher} and would not disqualify her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application here is important. For a single woman, very few men would disqualify her from marrying a {\i kohen} \u8212- only a non-Jew, a {\i mamzer}, a {\i chalal}, and the like. In a Jewish town, the great majority would be unobjectionable. But a betrothed woman is in an entirely different situation: every man other than her designated husband would disqualify her, since intercourse with any other man constitutes {\i zenut} in this context. {\i Ve-hakha rov psulim etzlah} \u8212- in this case, the majority of men are {\i psulim} relative to her. How, then, can one invoke Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling without the condition of {\i rov kasherim} being met?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha vs.\u160 ?Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a deeper question that the {\i gemara} confronts directly. If we say the {\i halacha} follows Rabban Gamliel, why does it follow that one should not act on it in practice? If it is the {\i halacha}, one should rule accordingly. The {\i gemara} resolves this by distinguishing between {\i lechatchila} and {\i bedieved}. When Shmuel cautions against acting on the ruling in practice, he means {\i lechatchila} \u8212- before the fact. Where the majority of men are {\i psulim}, we will not proactively permit the woman to marry a {\i kohen}. But when we say the {\i halacha} is like Rabban Gamliel even in such a case, we mean {\i bedieved} \u8212- after the fact. If she has already married a {\i kohen} without having sought guidance, or the situation has already occurred, we will not require them to separate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication is as follows. If the majority of men are {\i kasherim} \u8212- for instance, the woman is single and the encounter occurred in a Jewish city \u8212- we will even {\i lechatchila} credit her claim and allow her to marry a {\i kohen}. If the majority are {\i psulim} \u8212- as in a non-Jewish city, or as in the case of a betrothed woman where every other man disqualifies her \u8212- we will not permit the marriage {\i lechatchila}, but {\i bedieved}, once the marriage has already taken place, we will rule like Rabban Gamliel and allow them to remain together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the betrothed couple, then, the situation is already {\i bedieved} \u8212- the question before Rav Yosef is what to do now that the child exists and paternity is disputed. Since this is a {\i bedieved} scenario, the ruling of Rabban Gamliel applies even though the majority of men would have disqualified her, and the child\u8217's status is validated \u8212- all the more so given that both parties in this case openly affirm paternity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eduyos Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almanas Issa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a new challenge. Abaye poses a difficulty to Rava: is it truly the case that Rabbi Yehoshua holds that a woman is never believed regarding the identity of the man she slept with? This appears to be contradicted by a {\i mishnah} in Eduyos. There, Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Yehuda ben Beseira testify on behalf of an {\i almanas issa} \u8212- literally, a \u8220"widow of dough\u8221" \u8212- that she is permitted to marry a {\i kohen}. The image of dough ({\i issa}) conveys a mixture of uncertain composition, indicating a lineage in which some element of doubtful status has been blended in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the scenario as follows. A woman received a {\i get} from her husband, but it was unclear whether the {\i get} took effect \u8212- for instance, it was uncertain whether the document landed within her domain. The next day the husband died. She is therefore a {\i safek gerusha}, a {\i safek almana}. She then married a {\i kohen}. If she was indeed a {\i gerusha}, then by having relations with a {\i kohen} she became a {\i challala}, and the son born of that union became a {\i challal}. That son then married a woman. The son is a {\i safek challal}, his wife thereby becomes a {\i safek challala}, and she is now an {\i almanas issa} \u8212- a widow carrying an uncertain taint of {\i challal} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi also outlines a scenario that constitutes a genuine {\i safek sfeika}. Suppose, in addition to the doubt about the {\i get}, the woman also remarried within three months of her first husband\u8217's death and conceived a child shortly thereafter. Now there are two independent doubts: first, was the child conceived from the second man at all, or from the first husband? And second, even if conceived from the second union, was the mother a {\i gerusha} or an {\i almana} at the time? The child would be a {\i challal} only if both conditions obtain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Yehuda ben Beseira testify that such an {\i almanas issa} is {\i ksheira li-kehuna} \u8212- she may marry a {\i kohen}. This appears to stand in direct tension with Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position in the {\i mishnah}, where he insists that a woman\u8217's claim about who she slept with is simply not credited. Here, apparently, he rules leniently in a case of acknowledged uncertainty. How are these positions to be reconciled?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's initial answer is that the cases are structurally different. In the {\i mishnah}, the woman is {\i mezanah} \u8212- she engaged willingly in an act of {\i zenut}. One cannot assume that she investigated the status of her partner before acting. {\i Hatam isha nises bodeket ve-nises} \u8212- there, with a woman who is about to marry, we assume she looks into the man\u8217's status before doing so. But {\i hacha isha mezana bodeket u-mezana} \u8212- does a woman stop to verify a man\u8217's lineage before a casual liaison? The presumption simply does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definite vs.\u160 ?Doubtful Cl\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then raises a further complication. Not only does Rabbi Yehoshua seem to contradict himself \u8212- Rabban Gamliel does too. In the {\i mishnah} in Eduyos, following the testimony of Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Yehuda ben Beseira, Rabban Gamliel says: {\i kibalnu edutchem} \u8212- I accept your testimony \u8212- {\i aval ma na\u8217'aseh}, but what can we do? Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai enacted that a {\i beit din} should not rule leniently in such cases, because while {\i kohanim} will heed a stringency ({\i shom\u8217'im lachem le-rachek}), they will not accept a leniency ({\i aval lo le-karev}). The {\i gemara} reads this as Rabban Gamliel effectively declining to rule leniently in this case \u8212- yet in the {\i mishnah} he is prepared to credit a woman\u8217's claim about her own doubtful status and permit her to marry a {\i kohen}. How is that consistent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves both contradictions with a single principle: {\i hatam bari hacha shema}. In the {\i mishnah}, the woman makes a definite claim \u8212- she says she knows who the man was. Rabban Gamliel accepts that {\i bari} claim and rules accordingly. That is a case in which someone is actually asserting knowledge, and Rabban Gamliel\u8217's willingness to credit the claim is strong enough that he is even prepared to go against prevailing practice and tell the {\i kohanim} to trust the ruling. But in the {\i almanas issa} case, nobody is making a definite claim. The doubt arose from circumstances no one directly witnessed or can speak to with certainty; the woman\u8217's status is uncertain in an objective, external sense. With no {\i bari} claim to anchor the leniency, Rabban Gamliel is not prepared to stand behind a ruling the {\i kohanim} will refuse to follow anyway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once this is established, the {\i gemara} revisits Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's apparent contradiction. The initial answer \u8212- that we assume a married woman investigates her prospective husband\u8217's status while a woman engaged in {\i zenut} does not \u8212- is now supplemented. {\i Hatam chad sefeika} \u8212- in the {\i mishnah}, there is a single, direct doubt: who did this woman sleep with, and was that man {\i kasher}? Even if she claims to know, Rabbi Yehoshua says {\i lo mipi anu chayim} \u8212- we do not live by her mouth \u8212- and he disregards the claim. A single doubt is weighty, and her statement does not overcome it. {\i Hacha trei sefeika} \u8212- in the {\i almanas issa} case, however, the doubt is either twofold, or it is a doubt that has attenuated across two generations. That is objectively weaker, and Rabbi Yehoshua, who weighs the strength of the doubt on its merits rather than relying on the woman\u8217's claim, will accordingly rule leniently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel vs.\u160 ?Yehos\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now sets out the two positions with clarity and precision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hilkach le-Rabban Gamliel alim lei bari} \u8212- for Rabban Gamliel, the woman\u8217's definite claim is everything. It is so weighty that {\i afilu be-chad sefeika nami machshir} \u8212- even with a single, direct doubt, and even in a case of {\i rov psulim}, he is prepared to rule that she is {\i kasher}, because she has made a definite, credible assertion. {\i Ve-kil lei shema} \u8212- but when there is no definite claim, even if the doubt is objectively vague and attenuated \u8212- as in the {\i safek sfeika} of the {\i almanas issa} \u8212- he will not rule leniently. Without a {\i bari} claim to back the ruling, Rabban Gamliel is unwilling to permit what the {\i kohanim} will reject.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position is the precise inverse. {\i Alim lei chad sefeika} \u8212- a single doubt is weighty for him, and {\i afilu be-bari nami posel} \u8212- even a definite claim by the woman does not move him. He ignores her testimony and looks at the objective facts. {\i Ve-kil lei safek sfeika} \u8212- but a double doubt, or a doubt one generation removed, is objectively thin enough that {\i afilu be-shema nami machshir} \u8212- even absent any claim on the woman\u8217's part, he will rule that she is {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a precise and elegant distillation of the underlying {\i machlokes}. Rabban Gamliel\u8217's entire system is anchored in the force of a {\i ne\u8217'emenes} claim: when there is a {\i bari}, he will go far in backing her; when there is not, he will not. Rabbi Yehoshua entirely discounts her statement and simply calibrates his ruling to the objective weight of the doubt itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Silence Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beraitah Unpacked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i beraita} that provides the legal definition of {\i almanas issa} and, in the process, opens a sustained discussion of how silence functions as a form of status admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eizu hi almanas issa?} \u8212- What constitutes an {\i almanas issa}? {\i Kol she-ein bah lo mishum mamzerus, ve-lo mishum nesinus, ve-lo mishum avdei melachim} \u8212- any woman in whose background there is no question of {\i mamzerut}, no question of {\i nesinus} (descent from the {\i netinim}), and no question of slave lineage. The {\i avdei melachim} \u8212- servants of kings \u8212- are mentioned because figures like the servants of Shlomo, or Hordus who was himself seen in some traditions as of slave origin, wielded sufficient power that their relations with women might have occurred under duress.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Implicitly, then, the {\i almanas issa} is characterized specifically by the presence of a {\i safek challal} doubt \u8212- the one form of doubtful status that does {\i not} appear on this list of disqualifying concerns. But this raises an immediate question: {\i mai shena hanei de-oraisa, challal nami de-oraisa} \u8212- why distinguish? A {\i mamzer}, a {\i netin}, and a {\i challal} all derive their status ultimately from biblically prohibited unions. A {\i challala} \u8212- a woman who results from a {\i kohen} having relations with a {\i gerusha}, for instance \u8212- emerges from a Biblically prohibited act just as surely as a {\i mamzer} does. Why should the doubt about a {\i challal} be treated differently from the doubt about any of the others?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, as the {\i gemara} will develop, lies not in the formal biblical category but in the social and practical dynamics of how these statuses are known and contested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita} continues. Rabbi Meir says: {\i shamati kol she-ein bah echad mi-kol elu, masi\u8217'in li-khehunah} \u8212- I have heard that when none of these factors is present, the woman may be married off to a {\i kohen}. This seems to simply repeat what the {\i tanna kamma} already said, and the {\i gemara} notes the apparent redundancy: {\i hainu tanna kamma}. Similarly, what Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar says in the name of Rabbi Meir \u8212- that an {\i almanas issa} is characterized by {\i kol she-nitma bah safek challal}, anyone in whom a doubtful {\i challal} status has been intermixed \u8212- also seems to restate the same point, since the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's list excluded everything except the {\i challal} question. And the final line \u8212- {\i makkirin Yisrael mamzeirim she-beineihem, ve-ein makkirin challalim she-beineihem}, the Jewish people know who the {\i mamzerim} among them are and do not know who the {\i challalim} are \u8212- appears not only to not add anything but to actively contradict what immediately preceded it. If we do not know who the {\i challalim} are, why would a {\i safek challal} be {\i less} problematic than a {\i safek mamzer}? It should be {\i more} so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve this tangle, Rabbi Yochanan introduces an entirely new consideration: {\i mamzer tzove\u8217'ach ve-challal shotek ika beiniyahu} \u8212- the difference between these opinions turns on a case where a man is called a {\i mamzer} and protests, whereas when a man is called a {\i challal} he remains silent. In other words, the {\i beraita} is not merely classifying types of doubt in the abstract; it is addressing the evidentiary weight of a person\u8217's silence when he is identified by a particular status name. The {\i gemara} now reinterprets the entire {\i beraita} in this light.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma}, properly understood, is saying: an {\i almanas issa} is valid as long as neither she nor the man she married was in a situation where any of these names \u8212- {\i mamzer}, {\i nesin}, slave, or {\i challal} \u8212- was called out against him and he remained silent. The {\i tanna kamma} holds that any silence in the face of such a designation is an admission. He did not single out {\i challal} as categorically different; {\i challal} silence, like any other silence, counts as an admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir disputes this with regard to {\i challal} specifically. {\i Aval shetuk challal, kasher} \u8212- if a man is called a {\i challal} and does not respond, that silence is not an admission and he remains {\i kasher}. Why? {\i Mishum de-lo ikhpat lei} \u8212- because he does not care. A {\i challal} is barred from serving as a {\i kohen} and his daughters are barred from marrying {\i kohanim}, but his personal ability to marry any other woman is entirely unaffected. There is no compelling urgency to protest. His silence thus carries no probative weight. The {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's error, according to Rabbi Meir, is treating {\i challal} silence the same as silence in response to a designation that would severely curtail one\u8217's personal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar, transmitting a different tradition in the name of Rabbi Meir, reverses the logic entirely. It is {\i challal} silence that is an admission, and {\i mamzer} silence that is not. Why is a man silent when called a {\i mamzer}? {\i Savar mamzer kala it lei} \u8212- he reasons: everyone knows who the {\i mamzerim} are. Why should I bother responding? The public record speaks for itself, and there is nothing for him to clarify. His silence therefore does not indicate concession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why would a man be silent when called a {\i challal}? {\i Mistayid de-lo mafkei lei mi-kahal} \u8212- he calculates: if I respond and protest, they may investigate further and establish my status definitively. Better to let it remain a doubt. Remaining in a state of {\i safek challal} keeps him within the communal fold in ways that confirmed {\i challal} status would not. His silence is therefore strategic, not indifferent, and precisely because people do not maintain lists of {\i challalim} the way they maintain awareness of {\i mamzerim}, his silence cannot be explained away by reference to public knowledge. It therefore constitutes an admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The statement {\i makkirin Yisrael mamzeirim she-beineihem ve-ein makkirin challalim she-beineihem} \u8212- which seemed to contradict the earlier leniency regarding {\i challal} doubt \u8212- now makes perfect sense as the foundation of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's view. Because everyone knows who the {\i mamzerim} are, silence in the face of that accusation is meaningless. Because no one keeps track of the {\i challalim}, silence in the face of that designation is telling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a counterintuitive position. One might have expected that if everyone knows who the {\i mamzerim} are, then a false accusation of {\i mamzerus} would provoke vigorous protest, while a {\i challal} accusation \u8212- which carries no devastating personal consequence \u8212- would be greeted with indifference. That is precisely the logic of the first view attributed to Rabbi Meir. The reversal offered by Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar is striking, and the {\i gemara} acknowledges the tension by observing that a {\i mamzer} who actually does protest may in some sense be drawing more attention to his status than his silence would have.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dispute Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction between the two {\i beraitot} straightforwardly. One {\i beraita} states: silence in response to {\i mamzer} is {\i kasher}, silence in response to {\i challal} is {\i pasul}. Another states the opposite. {\i La kashya} \u8212- there is no contradiction. {\i Ha tanna kamma aliba de-Rabbi Meir, ha Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar aliba de-Rabbi Meir} \u8212- the first {\i beraita} reflects the position of the {\i tanna kamma} citing Rabbi Meir (in which {\i challal} silence is not an admission), and the second {\i beraita} reflects the view of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar in the name of Rabbi Meir (in which {\i challal} silence is an admission and {\i mamzer} silence is not). These are simply the two sides of the internal dispute within the name of Rabbi Meir, as transmitted through different tradents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is significant for the broader discussion is the conceptual apparatus now established: silence, in the halachic assessment of personal status, can function as a form of admission \u8212- but whether a given silence is probative depends on why a reasonable person in that position might be silent. The debate between the traditions attributed to Rabbi Meir is precisely about which psychological assumptions one applies: does a person not bother responding because the accusation is beneath notice, or does a person stay quiet for strategic reasons? That determination is what distinguishes {\i kasher} silence from {\i pasul} silence. And the reinterpretation of the entire {\i beraita} on {\i almanas issa} through this lens \u8212- mapping the three seemingly redundant statements onto the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's view, Rabbi Meir\u8217's exception, and Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's reversal \u8212- organizes what initially appeared to be a series of near-identical statements into a coherent three-way {\i machlokes}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This maps back as well onto the broader framework of Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Yehoshua. Rabban Gamliel\u8217's reliance on the woman\u8217's {\i bari} claim stands in structural parallel to the use of subjective statements as halachically operative; Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's focus on the objective weight of the doubt parallels the external evidentiary analysis that the silence doctrine employs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Raped Girl Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} on this {\i daf} introduces a new scenario. Rabbi Yossi relates a case of a young woman who went to draw water from a well and was raped ({\i ne\u8217'ensa}). Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri rules: {\i im rov anshei ha-ir masi\u8217'in li-kehunah, harei zu tinasei li-kehunah} \u8212- if the majority of the men in the city are such that women who slept with them may marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- that is, they are Jewish, not {\i mamzerim}, not {\i chalalim}, not {\i netinim} \u8212- then this woman too may marry a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case differs critically from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's dispute in one key respect: the woman has no ability to claim knowledge of her attacker\u8217's identity. The entire mechanism of {\i ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- whether Rabban Gamliel\u8217's reliance on her definite claim or Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's dismissal of it \u8212- is simply unavailable. No one is asserting anything. Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri therefore rules purely on the basis of {\i rov}, the halachic principle of following the majority. Since the majority of men in the city are {\i kasher}, we attribute the attack to a member of that majority and rule accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Position Identification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises the question to Rav Nachman: {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri de-amar ke-man?} \u8212- whose position does Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri follow? The difficulty is as follows. If he follows Rabban Gamliel, the result should be even more expansive: Rabban Gamliel rules leniently {\i even} when the majority are {\i psulim}, provided the woman makes a definite claim. But here, with no claim at all, Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri requires a {\i rov kasherim}. And if he follows Rabbi Yehoshua, the result should be stricter: Rabbi Yehoshua is {\i machmir} even when the majority are {\i kasherim}, since he does not rely on the woman\u8217's claim at all, regardless of the percentages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri thus seems to occupy a middle position \u8212- one that neither Rabban Gamliel nor Rabbi Yehoshua clearly maps onto. The {\i gemara} cites Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav: {\i be-karonot shel Tzippori haya ma\u8217'aseh}, this case involved wagons of Tzippori, {\i u-ke-Rav Ami} \u8212- and the ruling was in accordance with Rav Ami\u8217's position. The full unpacking of this citation and how it resolves the question of which tannaitic position Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri represents \u8212- along with the precise conditions under which {\i rov} suffices and under which a definite claim is required \u8212- will be taken up at the beginning of the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 15\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rape & Rov in Yichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah presents the case of a young girl who was raped. Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri rules that if most of the people in the city are those whose intercourse with the woman would leave her {\i ksheira l\u8217'kehunah} \u8212- that is, they are Jewish and not {\i mamzeirim} or {\i chalalim} \u8212- then she remains {\i ksheira} for marriage to a {\i kohen}. With this ruling, the Mishnah introduces the principle of {\i rov} (majority) into questions of {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov & Earlier Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately raises a difficulty. Until now, the tractate has been preoccupied with the debate between Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Yehoshua, a dispute centered entirely on the woman\u8217's claim and her credibility \u8212- the percentage composition of the surrounding population played no role. Rava therefore poses the question to Rav Nachman: according to whom does Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri rule? If he follows Rabban Gamliel, then Rabban Gamliel permits her even when the majority of the neighborhood consists of {\i psulin} \u8212- non-Jews or those otherwise disqualifying \u8212- provided she identifies the man and attests to his fitness. If he follows Rabbi Yehoshua, then even when the majority are {\i ksheirim}, she is {\i pesulah}, because Rabbi Yehoshua does not accept her unilateral claim. Tosafot works through this tension and suggests that perhaps the Gemara assumes the woman did identify her attacker; Rashi, however, reads the entire discussion as proceeding according to Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- a reading that will become significant as the {\i sugya} develops.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caravans of Tzipori\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer comes in the name of Rav Yehuda citing Rav: the story in the Mishnah involved the caravans ({\i kronot}) of Tzippori. Rabbi Ami qualifies the Mishnah accordingly \u8212- it applies only when a group of {\i ksheirim} was passing through the area. Rabbi Yannai adds that if she had intercourse at the time when the caravans were passing through, she is {\i ksheira l\u8217'kehunah}. The Gemara then introduces a complication: if, on the other hand, one man separated from Tzippori and had intercourse with her \u8212- even though the majority of Tzippori\u8217's residents are {\i ksheirim} \u8212- the child is a {\i shtuki}, a foundling of uncertain paternal lineage, whose status is therefore suspect. The question is: why should the presence of caravans make any difference at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kavuah vs.\u160 ?Parush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caravans Always Parush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer arrives when Rav Dimi brings a tradition from Eretz Yisrael in the name of Zeiri citing Rabbi Chanina: we follow the majority of the city ({\i rov ha-ir}), not the majority of the caravan ({\i rov siya}). This seems to contradict what was just said \u8212- the caravan is supposed to help! The Gemara resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i hani naydi v\u8217'hani kvi v\u8217'kaimi} \u8212- the caravan is moving, while the townspeople are fixed in place. The actual ruling is that one follows the majority of the city {\i combined with} the majority of the caravan. Both majorities are required simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason a double {\i rov} is necessary is rooted in a {\i gezeirah} \u8212- a protective decree. If one were permitted to rely solely on the majority of the caravan (which is always {\i parush}, separated, because the travelers are not in their home location), one might come to permit reliance on the majority of the city even in circumstances where the encounter took place in someone\u8217's home \u8212- a case of {\i kavuah}. Conversely, if one relied solely on the majority of the city, one might permit the case even when the woman went to the man\u8217's home rather than meeting him in the field, which transforms the situation from {\i parush} to {\i kavuah}. The requirement for both {\i rov ha-ir} and {\i rov siya} together prevents any such slide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol d\u8217'Parish & Kavuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the occasion for the Gemara to articulate the foundational principles at stake: {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza dami} \u8212- anything in a fixed location must be treated as a fifty-fifty doubt \u8212- and {\i kol d\u8217'parish me-rubah parish} \u8212- anything that has separated from a group is judged by the majority. These principles are formally strange; they resist simple probabilistic logic. The intuition behind {\i kavuah}, however imperfect, seems to be that when one confronts a bounded, fixed set of objects \u8212- among which one is definitively problematic \u8212- and asks about the identity of a particular member of that set, one cannot simply ignore the definite presence of the problematic element. One is forced to acknowledge a genuine fifty-fifty doubt, as opposed to the case of {\i parush}, where one approaches an object of unknown provenance and asks what the majority would indicate about its identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meat Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The classic paradigm is the case of meat. If one finds a piece of meat lying in the street and wishes to know its status, and in the city there are nine {\i kosher} butchers and one non-{\i kosher} butcher, one follows the majority: the meat is {\i kosher}. The meat has {\i parish} \u8212- it has separated from its source \u8212- and the inquiry is about its status, not about which fixed location it came from. However, if one returns home and finds meat there without remembering where it was purchased, the question becomes: which store did I enter? Those stores are fixed in their locations. The inquiry is now directed at a fixed set of objects, and therefore the ruling is {\i safek asur} \u8212- a doubtful prohibition \u8212- even though nine out of ten stores are {\i kosher}. That is {\i kavuah}: {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza dami}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape Case Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applying these principles to the rape case: if the man left Tzippori and encountered the woman in the field, he is {\i parush} \u8212- he has separated from his fixed location \u8212- and we therefore follow {\i rov}, which in Tzippori is {\i ksheirim}. But if the woman went to the man\u8217's home, that is {\i kavuah}: she is asking, in effect, which of these fixed domiciles did I enter? In that case, even a ninety-percent majority cannot help her; we treat it as fifty-fifty. The caravan is always {\i parush} by definition, because travelers are never in their fixed home location. The genius of requiring both {\i rov ha-ir} and {\i rov siya} together is that the caravan component guarantees a {\i parush} framework, preventing any confusion with a {\i kavuah} scenario. In principle, a single {\i rov} of the city would suffice when the encounter is genuinely {\i parush}, but because the circumstances can blur \u8212- sometimes she comes to him, sometimes he comes to her \u8212- the Rabbis required the additional safeguard of the passing caravan, ensuring the case always carries the character of {\i parush}. This is the {\i gezeirah}: insist on both majorities so no one mistakenly permits a {\i kavuah} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol D\u8217'Kavuah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine Stores Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to examine the principle of {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza} more rigorously. It opens with the classic {\i baraita}: nine stores all sell {\i shechutah} (properly slaughtered meat) and one sells {\i neveilah} (non-slaughtered meat). One purchased from one of the stores but cannot remember which. The ruling is {\i safek asur} \u8212- forbidden, because the doubt is framed around which store was entered, and the stores are fixed ({\i kavuah}). If, on the other hand, meat is found in the street, one follows {\i rov} \u8212- the question is about the meat\u8217's status, not the store\u8217's identity, and so it is {\i parush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This paradigm, the Gemara notes, only demonstrates the principle operating {\i le-chumra} \u8212- to generate stringency. Even though nine out of ten stores are {\i kosher}, if the doubt is {\i kavuah}, we treat it as fifty-fifty and forbid. But the formulation cited earlier in the name of Rabbi Zeira was {\i bein le-kula bein le-chumra} \u8212- the principle of {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza} applies whether toward leniency or stringency. Where does Rabbi Zeira get this? The nine-stores case only shows stringency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single vs.\u160 ?Double Rov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question that drives the entire inquiry is whether the meat case \u8212- the classic paradigm of {\i parush} \u8212- requires a single {\i rov} or a double {\i rov}. The Gemara suggests: perhaps we should reconstruct the meat case as involving a double {\i rov}, on the grounds that the gates of the city were open, meaning meat might have arrived from travelers passing through, just as in the caravan case. But Rabbi Zeira explicitly ruled that even when the gates of the city are locked \u8212- guaranteeing the meat came from one of the city\u8217's stores and nowhere else \u8212- one still follows {\i rov}. The meat case, therefore, operates on a single {\i rov}. This stands in contrast to the rape case, where a double {\i rov} was required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yichus Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the disparity with a single phrase: {\i ma\u8217'alah asu be-yuchsin} \u8212- the Rabbis enacted an extra stringency in matters of {\i yichus}, fitness for marriage to a {\i kohen}. Under ordinary {\i halakhic} principles, when the situation is one of {\i parush}, a single {\i rov} suffices. In the domain of {\i yichus}, however, the Rabbis demanded the additional layer of a second {\i rov}. This accounts for why the rape case requires both {\i rov ha-ir} and {\i rov siya}, while the meat case does not. The normal rules of {\i rov} \u8212- {\i kol d\u8217'parish me-rubah parish} \u8212- would have resolved even the rape case with a single majority, but the elevated standard of {\i yichus} necessitates more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rodents/Frog Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to Rabbi Zeira\u8217's claim that {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza} applies even {\i le-kula}, the Gemara works through the candidates. The nine-stores case shows stringency: {\i kavuah} overrides a nine-to-one majority in the direction of leniency. The case of nine frogs and one {\i sheretz} (a dead rodent that conveys {\i tumah}, while frogs do not), where one touches one of the ten without knowing which, is also {\i le-chumra}: even though nine of the ten are harmless, the doubt is {\i kavuah} and the ruling is {\i safek tamei}. Neither case demonstrates leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites a {\i Tosefta} in Taharot with the reverse configuration: nine dead {\i sheratzim} and one frog. One touches one of the ten without knowing which. Now ninety percent of the set conveys {\i tumah}. Under ordinary probability, one would expect a ruling of {\i vadai tamei} \u8212- certain impurity. Yet because the doubt is {\i kavuah}, the principle {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza} converts it into a fifty-fifty {\i safek}. And once it is a {\i safek}, the general rule of {\i safek tumah bi-reshut ha-rabbim tahor} applies: a doubt of {\i tumah} arising in a public domain leaves one {\i tahor}. Here, then, is the {\i le-kula} application \u8212- the {\i kavuah} principle generates leniency even when the majority would point toward impurity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reshus Ha-Yachid/Rabbim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling that {\i safek tumah bi-reshut ha-yachid tamei} and {\i safek tumah bi-reshut ha-rabbim tahor} is itself one of the striking features of this {\i sugya}. The distinction between private and public domain for doubts of {\i tumah} is not intuitively obvious, and the Gemara acknowledges it by tracing the rule to its source: the Torah describes a {\i sotah} \u8212- a woman suspected of infidelity \u8212- with the language {\i ve-hi nitma\u8217'ah}, she became {\i temeiah}. The moment of suspected {\i tumah} for a {\i sotah} is her act of seclusion with the man, which occurs in a private space. From this the rule is derived: a doubt of {\i tumah} that arises in a private domain (analogous to the secluded space of the {\i sotah}) is treated as {\i tamei}; a doubt arising in a public domain is treated as {\i tahor}. This framework then interacts with {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza}: by converting a ninety-percent likelihood into a fifty-fifty {\i safek}, {\i kavuah} places the case within the category of {\i safek}, which then triggers the {\i reshut ha-rabbim / reshut ha-yachid} distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks for a biblical source for {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza} as a Torah-level principle. The verse adduced is from the laws of murder: {\i ve-arav lo ve-kam alav} \u8212- he lay in wait for him and rose against him. This phrase teaches, according to Rabbi Shimon in Sanhedrin, that criminal liability for murder requires that one kill the specific individual one intended to kill. If one aimed at Reuven and killed Shimon, Rabbi Shimon holds this is {\i shogeg}, not {\i meizid}. The Rabbis disagree: intention to kill Reuven and killing Shimon is still murder. But what does the phrase {\i ve-arav lo} exclude, according to the Rabbis? The school of Rabbi Yannai answers: it excludes one who throws a stone into a group ({\i lego}) \u8212- that is, fires randomly into a crowd.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara works through the case. If the group consists of nine non-Jews and one Jew and one shoots randomly, one is obviously not liable \u8212- the majority are non-Jews, and even at fifty-fifty, the principle of {\i safek nefashot le-hakel} (leniency in capital matters) would apply. The novel case, the {\i chiddush}, is nine Jews and one non-Jew. One might think that shooting into such a group constitutes an act directed against a Jew, and that whoever was killed \u8212- even if it turned out to be the non-Jew \u8212- should generate liability on the analogy of intending to kill Reuven and killing Shimon. The Torah excludes this: the non-Jew is fixed within the group ({\i kavuah}), and {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza dami} \u8212- the act is therefore treated as fifty-fifty, not as definitively aimed at a Jew. The shooter is exempt. This is the biblical derivation of the principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Horat Sha\u8217'ah View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the principle of {\i kol d\u8217'kavuah k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza} and its implications for {\i yichus}, the Gemara now returns to the Mishnah. Two {\i amoraim} take different positions. Rabbi Chiya bar Ashi in the name of Rav rules {\i le-halakha} in accordance with the Mishnah \u8212- one does follow {\i rov} in the rape case. Rav Chanan bar Rava, by contrast, says {\i hora\u8217'at sha\u8217'ah hayta} \u8212- that ruling was a special circumstance, not a normative {\i halakhic} precedent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirmiya\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya challenges the first position. If Rabbi Chiya bar Ashi rules in accordance with the Mishnah, he is saying that for {\i yichus} a single {\i rov} suffices. But the entire preceding {\i sugya} has been premised on the assumption that {\i yichus} demands a double {\i rov}. How can one rely on a single {\i rov} for something as consequential as permitting a woman to marry a {\i kohen}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foundling Infant Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To sharpen the point, Rabbi Yirmiya cites the Mishnah at the top of 15b: if a foundling infant ({\i tinok mushlach}) is discovered and the majority of the city\u8217's residents are non-Jewish, the infant is presumed non-Jewish; if the majority are Jewish, the infant is presumed Jewish; and if the population is evenly split, the infant is still presumed Jewish. Rav qualifies this ruling: the presumption of Jewish status operates only {\i le-hachyoto} \u8212- in order to fulfill our obligation to sustain and care for him ({\i ve-chai achicha imach}) \u8212- but not for purposes of {\i yichus}. For {\i yichus}, even a majority of Jews in the city is insufficient. This shows that the Gemara is consistent: {\i yichus} demands more than a single {\i rov}, and therefore one cannot rule like the Mishnah as if a single {\i rov} were enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension by noting: {\i ishtamitei\u8217'eihu} \u8212- whoever was ruling in accordance with the Mishnah had simply forgotten the tradition of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav, that the Mishnah\u8217's case specifically involved the caravans of Tzippori \u8212- a double {\i rov} situation. Once reminded, the apparent contradiction dissolves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses a further difficulty: according to Rav Chanan bar Rava, who says {\i hora\u8217'at sha\u8217'ah hayta}, what is the meaning of calling it a special circumstance if in fact the case involved a double {\i rov}? Should a double {\i rov} not suffice as a matter of normal {\i halakha}? The Gemara\u8217's answer is clean: {\i man d\u8217'matni ha lo matni ha} \u8212- the two traditions are not held by the same authority. The one who says the Mishnah is ruled {\i le-halakha} because it was a double {\i rov} case (the caravans of Tzippori) would not say it was {\i hora\u8217'at sha\u8217'ah}. The one who says it was {\i hora\u8217'at sha\u8217'ah} holds that it was only a single {\i rov} \u8212- and precisely because it was only a single {\i rov}, it cannot serve as a normative precedent for {\i yichus}. Either way, the conclusion is the same: do not draw from this Mishnah the inference that a single {\i rov} suffices for {\i yichus}. Whether the case was ruled permissibly because of a double {\i rov}, or whether the ruling was an unrepeatable special circumstance, the general principle stands \u8212- {\i yichus} demands a double {\i rov}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Foundling Infant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns in full to the {\i gufah} \u8212- the body of the Mishnah about the foundling infant. If the majority of the city\u8217's residents are non-Jewish, the infant is treated as a non-Jew. If the majority are Jewish, the infant is treated as a Jew. If the population is evenly divided, the infant is treated as a Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav qualifies the Mishnah: {\i lo shanu ela le-hachyoto} \u8212- the assumption of Jewish status operates only for purposes of sustaining the infant\u8217's life, fulfilling the obligation of {\i ve-chai achicha imach}. For {\i yichus} \u8212- whether this child may marry a {\i kohen}, whether there is concern for {\i mamzerut} \u8212- a single {\i rov} is not enough. The Mishnah\u8217's ruling does not extend to genealogical status determinations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel & Pikuach Nefesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel goes further in a different direction: {\i le-fake\u8217'ach alav et ha-gal} \u8212- the basis for treating the infant as Jewish is not merely the general obligation of {\i hachyasah} but the imperative of {\i pikuach nefesh}, saving life on {\i Shabbat}. Shmuel\u8217's broader principle, transmitted by Rav Yosef in the name of Rav Yehuda, is this: {\i ein holchin be-pikuach nefesh achar ha-rov} \u8212- when it comes to saving a life, we do not follow majority reasoning. Even if there is only a small likelihood that the person is Jewish, one violates {\i Shabbat} to save them. Shmuel\u8217's qualification therefore applies not to the case where the majority is Jewish, but to the opening case of the Mishnah \u8212- where the majority is non-Jewish. Even there, {\i Shmuel} says, we do not withhold life-saving on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking position. The Gemara\u8217's implication is that even if for years one has treated this individual as a non-Jew for every other purpose \u8212- using him as a {\i Shabbat goy}, feeding him {\i treifot} \u8212- the original uncertainty of his birth is never fully extinguished. When the building collapses and he may be underneath, we remember that his parentage was never conclusively established, and we violate {\i Shabbat} to save him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Long-term Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has a counterintuitive quality that the Gemara itself highlights. Normally, once a {\i halakhic} status has been determined and acted upon, subsequent doubts do not revive the old uncertainty. The {\i safek} does not survive across generations of rulings. Yet in this case, the Gemara is prepared to say: even after twenty years of treating this person as a non-Jew in every practical sense, we remember for the purposes of {\i pikuach nefesh} that his identity was never resolved. The {\i safek} of his birth remains live \u8212- but only for that one purpose. This is precisely Shmuel\u8217's position: when the majority is non-Jewish and we have treated the infant accordingly, we have nonetheless never truly closed the file on his Jewish identity, and that open file obligates us to violate {\i Shabbat} on his behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dinei Mamonot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa then enumerates the practical applications of these distinctions. When the majority of the city is non-Jewish and the infant is presumed non-Jewish: {\i le-ha\u8217'achilo neveilot} \u8212- one may feed him non-{\i kosher} meat, use him as a {\i Shabbat goy}, and treat him in every active sense as a non-Jew. When the majority is Jewish and the infant is presumed Jewish: {\i le-hachazir lo aveidah} \u8212- one must return lost objects to him. The {\i nafka mina} between the fifty-fifty case (where one already treats him as {\i safek Yisrael} for {\i hachyasah}) and the clear majority case emerges specifically in monetary law. At fifty-fifty, the principle of {\i hamotzi me-chavero alav hara\u8217'aya} applies: since the claim to Jewish status has not been established by majority, one may say to him, \u8220"bring proof that you are Jewish,\u8221" and in the absence of such proof, one is not obligated to return the lost object. Once there is a majority of Jews, however, no such demand is necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ox Goring\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa\u8217's further illustration involves the laws of {\i nezikin}. The {\i halakha} assumes different rules for an ox goring depending on whether owner and victim are Jewish or non-Jewish. The Gemara works through the case: if our ox gored his ox and his status is uncertain (fifty-fifty), we can tell him, \u8220"bring proof that you are a Jew and then collect.\u8221" If, however, his ox gored our ox \u8212- a {\i tam} (first-time gorer) \u8212- he pays {\i palga}, half damages, which is what he would owe under the assumption he is Jewish. But if we then demand he pay more (as we would if he were non-Jewish and therefore always liable in full), he can respond: \u8220"bring proof that I am not Jewish, and I will pay you more.\u8221" The burden of proof operates symmetrically, cutting in both directions, always governed by the principle of {\i hamotzi me-chavero alav hara\u8217'aya}. The general rule holds throughout: we follow {\i rov} in all matters except {\i yichus}, where a double {\i rov} is demanded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Next Mishna Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i hadran alach Betula Nisat} \u8212- the conclusion of the chapter \u8212- the Gemara introduces the opening Mishnah of the next {\i perek}. The shift is significant. The previous chapter dealt with {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} \u8212- the claim regarding virginity on the wedding night. The new Mishnah addresses a different question entirely: not whether the woman was a virgin when married, but whether the marriage was a first marriage or a second marriage, a distinction that determines whether her {\i ketubah} is two hundred {\i zuz} or one hundred. Suppose the couple is divorcing or the woman is widowed twenty years after the wedding, and neither party has preserved the original {\i ketubah}. She claims it was a first marriage (two hundred); he claims she was already a widow at the time of marriage (one hundred). How is this resolved?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Marriage Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah identifies the evidentiary markers of a first wedding. If there are witnesses that she went out {\i be-hinuma} \u8212- in a wedding carriage \u8212- and that her head was uncovered ({\i rosho parua}), with her hair flowing down on her shoulders as Rashi describes, these are recognized signs that it was a first marriage. When such evidence exists, her {\i ketubah} is two hundred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parched Grain Sign\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka adds a further sign: {\i af chiluk kalyot raya} \u8212- even the distribution of roasted or parched grain to the guests is sufficient evidence of a first wedding. This was a custom specific to first marriages, analogous perhaps to other celebratory practices that distinguish the first wedding from subsequent ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peh She\u8217'asar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah concludes with the ruling of Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- presumably the same Rabbi Yehoshua from the previous {\i perek}, who consistently required external corroboration and refused to accept unilateral claims. Here, however, Rabbi Yehoshua concedes. The principle he invokes is {\i peh she\u8217'asar hu peh she\u8217'hitir} \u8212- the same mouth that created the prohibition is the mouth that permits it. The illustration is from monetary law: if one says to another, \u8220"this field was your father\u8217's and I purchased it from him,\u8221" he is believed, because without his own statement we would have had no grounds to question his title to the field. The very mouth that introduced the claim of the other\u8217's father\u8217's ownership is the mouth that asserts the subsequent purchase \u8212- and therefore that mouth is trusted. This is the limiting principle: Rabbi Yehoshua does not believe a woman who makes a claim {\i stam} to extract money. But when the claimant\u8217's own statement is what generates the doubt in the first place, the same statement can resolve it. If witnesses establish that the field originally belonged to the father, however, then the man who admits ownership and claims to have purchased it is no longer in a {\i peh she\u8217'asar peh she\u8217'hitir} position \u8212- external evidence has created the doubt, and he is not believed. The Gemara will explore in the next session how {\i peh she\u8217'asar peh she\u8217'hitir} applies to the woman\u8217's claim in this new Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 16\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First/Second Wedding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i perek} opens with a {\i mishnah} describing a dispute that arises not over the woman\u8217's physical state on the wedding night, but over the nature of the wedding itself \u8212- decades later, when she is being divorced and there is no {\i ketubah} to produce. The debate is not \u8220"why wasn\u8217't she a virgin?\u8221" but rather \u8220"was this a first or second wedding?\u8221" The {\i gemara} teaches that in such a case, the woman requires witnesses who can testify to the indicators of a first wedding: that she left her father\u8217's home with a full wedding procession, that roasted grain was distributed, that three hundred guests attended, or similar markers of a {\i nisu\u8217'in besulah}. Without those indicators, she would not be believed, because the operative principle is {\i ha-motzi mei-chaveiro alav ha-re\u8217'ayah} \u8212- the burden of proof rests on the one seeking to extract payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens on 16a by asking: since witnesses are required, does this mean the {\i mishnah} does not follow Rabban Gamliel? After all, Rabban Gamliel consistently rules that the woman is believed. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i afillu teima Rabban Gamliel} \u8212- one can say even this {\i mishnah} follows Rabban Gamliel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bari v\u8217'Bari Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes the case of the first {\i perek} from this one. In the earlier cases, the dispute was {\i bari v\u8217'shema} \u8212- she makes a definite claim and he responds only with uncertainty. Here, both parties make definite claims: she says with certainty that it was a first wedding, and he says with equal certainty that it was a second. This is {\i bari u\u8217'bari} \u8212- two equally firm competing claims \u8212- and that is a qualitatively different situation. The principle of {\i ha-motzi mei-chaveiro alav ha-re\u8217'ayah} applies with full force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel Concedes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to establish that Rabban Gamliel concedes this case. The logical structure of the {\i mishnah} supports this: the {\i mishnah} closes with the statement {\i u\u8217'modeh Rabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua concedes a certain case. The {\i gemara} argues that this concession only makes sense if the earlier portion of the {\i mishnah} represents Rabban Gamliel\u8217's concession in the {\i bari u\u8217'bari} scenario. If the entire {\i mishnah} were authored from Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's perspective, then to whom is Rabbi Yehoshua conceding? The structure implies a mutual yielding: Rabban Gamliel concedes that in a {\i bari u\u8217'bari} dispute she does not win, and Rabbi Yehoshua in turn concedes a different point \u8212- the case of {\i ha-peh she\u8217'asar hu ha-peh she\u8217'hitir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov Nashim Betulot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: even in a {\i bari u\u8217'bari} scenario, why not simply apply {\i rov nashim betulot nisos} \u8212- the statistical majority that most women marry for the first time? Since most weddings are first weddings, her claim is probabilistically more likely to be true. The {\i gemara} acknowledges this consideration and notes that this is precisely what might lead one to think Rabban Gamliel would still rule in her favor even in a {\i bari u\u8217'bari} case, treating her {\i bari} as somewhat stronger by virtue of the {\i rov}. The {\i gemara} will return to the question of {\i holchin b\u8217'mammon achar ha-rov} \u8212- whether a statistical majority is sufficient to extract money from someone \u8212- but signals here that this is not sufficient. A definite competing claim cannot simply be overridden by probability alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Migo vs.\u160 ?Peh She\u8217'Asar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua Concedes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots. It explains that Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's concession \u8212- {\i u\u8217'modeh Rabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- need not be read as a response to a concession by Rabban Gamliel in the current {\i mishnah} at all. Rather, {\i amigo ka\u8217'i v\u8217'pirka kamma ka\u8217'i}: Rabbi Yehoshua is going back to a {\i migo} that was raised in the first {\i perek}. In that earlier {\i perek}, Rabban Gamliel argued that the woman should be believed, at least in part because she had a {\i migo} \u8212- a more advantageous claim she could have made but did not. Rabbi Yehoshua rejected that {\i migo}. Now, in this {\i mishnah}, Rabbi Yehoshua declares that while he rejected that {\i migo}, he will concede a case that is {\i stronger} than a {\i migo} \u8212- namely, {\i ha-peh she\u8217'asar hu ha-peh she\u8217'hitir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case in question is the closing clause of the {\i mishnah}, where a man says: \u8220"This field that I have been occupying was originally your father\u8217's \u8212- and I bought it from him.\u8221" He is the one who introduces the entire scenario. The other party had no idea a claim was even forthcoming. Rabbi Yehoshua concedes that in such a case the man is believed. Rabbi Yehoshua is thus not conceding to anything Rabban Gamliel said in this {\i mishnah}; he is drawing a contrast with his own earlier position rejecting {\i migo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Migo Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand exactly what {\i migo} Rabbi Yehoshua rejected, the {\i gemara} works backward through all the {\i mishnayot} of the first {\i perek}, asking in each case whether there was indeed a {\i migo} that Rabban Gamliel relied upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case reviewed is {\i hi she\u8217'muberet} \u8212- she is visibly pregnant, and she identifies the father as a {\i kohen} of unimpeachable lineage. The {\i gemara} asks: what {\i migo} does she have? {\i Harei kreisa bein shineha} \u8212- her pregnancy is manifest; everyone already knows she had relations with some man. She is already making the best claim she could. There is no {\i migo} here at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case is {\i hi m\u8217'daberet im echad} \u8212- she was seen in conversation (or in seclusion) with a man, and she identifies him as a {\i kohen}. The {\i gemara} notes that this case depends on how one reads the language. According to Zeiri, who understands {\i m\u8217'daberet} as referring to {\i nistera} \u8212- seclusion \u8212- there is indeed a {\i migo}: she could have denied that any relations occurred ({\i migo d\u8217'i ba\u8217'i amra lo nistarti}), yet she concedes they did. That concession, under circumstances where she did not need to make it, gives her claim credibility. This is a {\i migo} that Rabbi Yehoshua refuses to accept. According to Rav Ashi, however, {\i m\u8217'daberet} means she actually had relations and that is already established \u8212- in which case there is no {\i migo}, and Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's rejection of her claim is unremarkable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is {\i hi omeret mukat etz ani} \u8212- she claims her lack of {\i betulim} is due to a physical injury, and he claims she had relations. Here too the {\i gemara} examines whether there is a {\i migo}. According to Rabbi Eliezer, who rules that if she was {\i be\u8217'ulah} she receives nothing, a {\i migo} exists: she could have claimed {\i mukat etz tachteich} \u8212- the injury occurred after the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and thereby claimed the full 200. Instead she claims the injury preceded the {\i kiddushin}, reducing her claim to 100. That self-reduction is a powerful {\i migo}. But according to Rabbi Yochanan, who rules that even a {\i mukat etz} from before the wedding receives 200, she is already making the maximum possible claim and there is no {\i migo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape Case Migo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth and earliest {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i ha-nosei et ha\u8217'isha v\u8217'lo matza lah betulim} \u8212- is the one where the {\i migo} is beyond dispute. She claims she was raped after the {\i kiddushin}; he claims she had relations before. Here she clearly possesses a stronger claim she did not make: she could have said {\i mukat etz ani tachteich}, an injury after the {\i kiddushin}, which would have entitled her to 200 and would not have disqualified her from marrying a {\i kohen}. Instead she claims rape, which entitles her to 200 but {\i does} disqualify her from a {\i kohen} \u8212- {\i d\u8217'lo pasla nafsha mi-kehuna}. By making the more damaging claim, she shows that she is not trying to maximize her position, which is the essence of a {\i migo}. Rabban Gamliel believes her, and the {\i gemara} explains that this is because she has a {\i bari} claim reinforced by a {\i migo}. Rashi adds that even in cases where there is no {\i migo}, Rabban Gamliel has another basis for belief: {\i chezkat ha-guf} \u8212- the presumption that she has not transgressed, that she did not sleep with a man who would have rendered her unfit. So Rabban Gamliel always has her {\i bari} claim, sometimes plus a {\i migo}, and always plus a {\i chazaka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position, by contrast, is stated in its full force: even a {\i bari} against a {\i shema}, even a {\i bari} reinforced by a {\i migo}, is not sufficient for him to believe her. He responds: {\i behai migo d\u8217'hacha modina lach, behahu migo d\u8217'hatam peligina alach} \u8212- the {\i migo} in this second {\i perek}\u8217's field case I concede; the {\i migo} back there in the first {\i perek} I rejected. This elegantly reviews the entire earlier debate while advancing the analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: {\i mai shena hai migo me-hai migo} \u8212- what distinguishes the {\i migo} that Rabbi Yehoshua accepts from the one he rejects? The {\i gemara\u8217's} answer is: {\i hacha ein shor shachut lefanecha, hatam harei shor shachut lefanecha} \u8212- here there is no slaughtered ox before you; there, there is a slaughtered ox before you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The image requires explanation. When a woman\u8217's lack of {\i betulim} is established \u8212- when the sheets have been examined, or the fact is simply not in dispute \u8212- that is a concrete, undeniable datum that demands an explanation. Like a slaughtered ox lying in the road, the fact is before you and you must account for it. Under those circumstances, the choice between alternative explanations is not the same as introducing a claim out of nowhere. Even if she chooses the more self-damaging explanation, she is still operating under the constraint of having to explain observable evidence. That is what Rabbi Yehoshua does not find convincing as a {\i migo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi/Tosfos Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and Tosafot understand this point differently, and the difference is significant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that the superiority of {\i ha-peh she\u8217'asar} lies in the fact that the claimant was not even being challenged. Nobody approached Reuven; nobody sued him or asked him about the field. He simply volunteers: \u8220"This field used to belong to your father \u8212- and I bought it from him.\u8221" He could have remained completely silent. {\i Migo d\u8217'i ba\u8217'i shatik} \u8212- since he could have said nothing at all, the fact that he makes this admission is the strongest possible form of trustworthiness. When, by contrast, a woman must explain an established fact about her body, she cannot say \u8220"I\u8217'm making nothing up,\u8221" because the datum is there and demands explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot does not accept this framing. According to Tosafot, the field case in the {\i mishnah} does involve a {\i ta\u8217'ana} against him \u8212- the other party is indeed claiming the field. The difference, Tosafot argues, is not about whether the person is being challenged, but about whether there is concrete evidence that must be answered. In the field case, the opposing party claims the field was his father\u8217's, but there is no evidence that forces an explanation. The defendant could simply say {\i lo hayu devarim me\u8217'olam} \u8212- I don\u8217't know what you are talking about, I never saw your father in my life. Since he could deny everything, his {\i migo} is powerful: he has the option of total denial. But when the evidence is already before the court \u8212- the {\i shor shachut} \u8212- no such total denial is available, and the range of alternative claims is constrained to explanations, not denials. That is what Tosafot means by {\i shor shachut lefanecha}: there is a dead animal in front of you; you cannot claim it is alive; you can only offer competing accounts of how it died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This debate between Rashi and Tosafot has concrete implications: according to Rashi, Rabbi Yehoshua accepts {\i ha-peh she\u8217'asar} only when the claimant was not being challenged at all. According to Tosafot, Rabbi Yehoshua would accept it even when challenged, provided the alternative available to the claimant was a complete denial rather than merely a different explanation of established facts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rov Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qol Counteracts Rov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the structure of the debate clarified \u8212- Rabban Gamliel concedes the {\i bari u\u8217'bari} case, Rabbi Yehoshua concedes the {\i ha-peh she\u8217'asar} case \u8212- the {\i gemara} returns to the question left open earlier. If {\i rov nashim betulot nisos}, if the statistical majority of women are married for the first time, why does the absence of witnesses leave her without recourse? Why not let the {\i rov} itself carry her claim, at least according to Rabban Gamliel?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina answers: {\i mishum de-ika le-meimar rov nashim betulot nisos u\u8217'mi\u8217'ut almanot, v\u8217'chol ha-nisos betula yesh lah kol} \u8212- it is true that most women\u8217's weddings are first weddings, but there is a countervailing consideration. Every first wedding generates {\i kol} \u8212- public knowledge, communal awareness, memory. A first wedding is announced; three hundred guests are invited; people remember. Therefore, when twenty years later no one can be found who remembers this as a first wedding, that absence of {\i kol} undermines the force of the {\i rov}. The statistical likelihood is balanced \u8212- and neutralized \u8212- by the inference that had it truly been a first wedding, people would have remembered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balance of Rov/Qol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses this reasoning. If every first wedding produces {\i kol}, then even the testimony of witnesses should be unnecessary \u8212- we would simply ask whether there is communal memory. And conversely, if the absence of {\i kol} is so telling, should we not distrust even witnesses who come forward, on the grounds that the absence of broad awareness is suspicious?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} refines Ravina\u8217's statement: {\i rov ha-nisos betulot yesh lahen kol} \u8212- most, but not all, first weddings generate persistent {\i kol}. The {\i rov} and the expected {\i kol} together create a presumption, but since neither is absolute, they balance one another. The result is that without witnesses, we have neither a strong presumption for her nor conclusive evidence against her \u8212- a genuine equilibrium that leaves the burden of proof on her. When witnesses do come, they provide the concrete evidence that tips the balance. And since the concern is not about {\i kol} in isolation but about the interplay between statistical likelihood and communal memory, witnesses testifying to concrete acts at the wedding override the abstract inference from the absence of general awareness.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rov: Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that witnesses can overcome the absence of {\i kol}, the {\i gemara} specifies what those witnesses must testify to. The case at hand is witnesses who testify that she went out in {\i behinuma} \u8212- the bedecked wedding procession indicative of a first marriage. This is the kind of direct, concrete testimony about a specific act that constitutes genuine evidence rather than an inference from probability or the presence or absence of communal memory.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Receipts/Shover\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Collection Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With witnesses established as sufficient to allow collection, a new problem emerges. If she collects her {\i ketubah} on the basis of witnesses in one {\i beit din}, what prevents her from going to another {\i beit din} in another city \u8212- where nothing is known of the first proceeding \u8212- producing a different set of witnesses from the same wedding, and collecting a second time? A first wedding would have had many guests; there is no shortage of potential witnesses. The {\i gemara} articulates this concern clearly: {\i dilma mafka edim be-hai bei dina ve-gavya, ve-hadar mafka le-kesuvatah be-hai bei dina ve-gavya} \u8212- perhaps she will produce witnesses in one court and collect, then produce her {\i ketubah} (or more witnesses) in another court and collect again. If the husband has no protection against this, the system is exposed to abuse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shovar Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba\u8217's response is direct: {\i zos omeres kotvin shovar} \u8212- this case teaches that we write a receipt. The husband, having paid out the {\i ketubah}, holds a {\i shovar} \u8212- a written receipt \u8212- that protects him against a subsequent claim. The conceptual challenge underlying this is significant. The normal principle in financial law is that a creditor holds the document, and once a debt is paid, it is the creditor\u8217's responsibility to return or destroy the document. Demanding that the debtor hold a receipt forever is a burden. One view in the Talmud elsewhere resolves this by simply refusing to allow collection without producing the original document: if you have lost your {\i shtar}, you cannot collect, regardless of whether you can prove the debt. Under that view, there is no need for receipts \u8212- but the cost falls entirely on the creditor who has lost documentation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa takes a more nuanced position. He does not simply accept the conclusion that receipts are written. Rather, he argues: {\i bi-makom she\u8217'ein kotvin ketubah askinan} \u8212- the entire discussion concerns a place where {\i ketubot} are not normally written at all. The {\i ketubah} obligation derives from rabbinic enactment and does not require a written document; some communities relied entirely on witnesses without producing a written {\i shtar}. In such a place, there is no document to return or destroy, so a system of receipts is necessary. But in a place where {\i ketubot} are normally written, Rav Pappa maintains that no receipts are written: the responsibility lies with the document, and if the document cannot be produced, collection cannot proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position has a stark implication. If a woman loses her {\i ketubah}, or if it is destroyed, in a community where {\i ketubot} are written \u8212- according to Rav Pappa she cannot collect at all, unless she can prove the document was definitively destroyed. The inability to produce the document is her problem, not the court\u8217's, and the court will not fashion an alternative mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that this same debate between Rabbi Abba and Rav Pappa appears not only in connection with the {\i mishnah} but also in connection with a {\i baraita}, and the {\i baraita} version is more explicit and therefore more revealing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} lists three scenarios: {\i avda kesuvatah} \u8212- she lost her {\i ketubah}; {\i hitmina kesuvatah} \u8212- she secreted it away; {\i nisrefa kesuvatah} \u8212- it was burned. In all three cases, the {\i baraita} says, she may produce witnesses to the wedding ceremonies \u8212- dancing before her, {\i kos shel besora}, the {\i mappa shel betulim} \u8212- and collect. The {\i baraita} then concludes: {\i kesuvatah masayim}, she collects 200.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the {\i baraita} clearly began with a case where a {\i ketubah} existed \u8212- it was lost, hidden, or burned \u8212- and yet allows collection on the basis of witnesses, this appears to be direct evidence that even in a place where {\i ketubot} are written, there is a mechanism for collecting without the document, which supports Rabbi Abba\u8217's position that receipts are written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses Rav Pappa: how can you maintain your position given that the {\i baraita} explicitly says the {\i ketubah} was lost and she can still collect? Rav Pappa responds with a forced but internally consistent reading. {\i Avda}, he says, means she claims it was lost \u8212- but claiming a loss is treated like hiding it: {\i ki hitmina bifneinu dami}. If she says \u8220"I lost it,\u8221" the court suspects she is hiding it somewhere to use later. Therefore, Rav Pappa reads the {\i baraita} as a dialogue: when she says {\i avda}, we say {\i hitmina} \u8212- we suspect concealment \u8212- and we do not let her collect. The only case where she may collect is {\i nisrefa} \u8212- the {\i ketubah} was burned and we have evidence of the destruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under this reading, the three terms in the {\i baraita} are not three parallel cases each permitting collection. Rather, {\i avda} is the opening claim, {\i hitmina} is the court\u8217's response expressing suspicion, and only {\i nisrefa} is the scenario where collection is actually permitted. This is a significant {\i chiddush} to extract from a {\i baraita} whose plain meaning seems to permit collection in all three cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Shovar Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the situation we have been circling: even where witnesses to the wedding can be produced, the double-collection risk remains. The {\i gemara} closes this discussion with a clear principle: {\i bi-makom d\u8217'lo efshar vadai kasvinan shovar} \u8212- where there is no alternative, a {\i shovar} is certainly written. This is the position that I had previewed earlier in the discussion, and the {\i gemara} arrives at it here as a {\i svara} that even Rav Pappa must acknowledge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is as follows. According to Rav Pappa: in a community where {\i ketubot} are written, no receipts \u8212- the document governs, and if it cannot be produced (other than in a case of proven destruction), collection is impossible. In a community where {\i ketubot} are not written, or where the document was proven destroyed, there is no alternative, and a {\i shovar} must be written to prevent double collection. The more permissive view \u8212- that of Rabbi Abba \u8212- would write a {\i shovar} even in communities where {\i ketubot} are normally produced, thereby allowing collection whenever witnesses can testify to the nature of the wedding, with the husband protected by his receipt.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wedding Practices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kos Shel Besora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that witnesses can testify to wedding ceremonies as proof of a first marriage, the {\i gemara} \u8212- and the {\i baraita} cited earlier \u8212- enumerates those ceremonies. The first is {\i kos shel besora}, the cup of good tidings. Rav Adda bar Ahava explains: {\i kos yayin shel terumah mevi\u8217'in lefaneha} \u8212- they would bring before her a cup filled with {\i terumah} wine. The symbolism, he says, is {\i re\u8217'uya hayta zu le\u8217'echol bi-terumah} \u8212- she is fit to eat {\i terumah}, meaning that if she is marrying a {\i kohen}, her status as a woman who has never had relations with a man forbidden to a {\i kohen} is being publicly affirmed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa objects: {\i atu almana mi lo achla bi-terumah?} \u8212- Does a widow not also eat {\i terumah} if she marries a {\i kohen}? Her being a non-virgin does not disqualify her from {\i terumah}, so this symbolism does not track the {\i betulah/be\u8217'ulah} distinction at all. Rav Pappa therefore offers his own explanation: {\i zu reishit ke-terumah} \u8212- this cup is like {\i terumah} in its quality of being {\i reishit}, a first. Just as {\i terumah} is the first portion separated, this night is her first, and the cup symbolizes that {\i rishonah}, that primacy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Barrel Symbolism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} cites Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view that they would bring before her a barrel of wine. Rav Adda bar Ahava again supplies the interpretive key: {\i besulah mevi\u8217'in lefaneha setumah} \u8212- for a {\i besulah} they would bring a sealed cask; {\i be\u8217'ulah mevi\u8217'in lefaneha petucha} \u8212- for a {\i be\u8217'ulah} they would bring an open one. The symbolism is entirely transparent, and the wine barrel \u8212- sealed or open \u8212- would serve as direct concrete evidence of the woman\u8217's status at the time of her wedding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concrete vs.\u160 ?Absence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why not simply use the sealed barrel as an indicator, and the {\i absence} of any barrel as the indicator for a {\i be\u8217'ulah}? Why go through the effort of displaying an open barrel?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer returns to the principle established earlier in the discussion of {\i shor shachut}: we need concrete positive evidence, not merely the absence of evidence. {\i Zimnin di-tafsa masayim} \u8212- sometimes she will have already seized her husband\u8217's property and will claim 200, forcing him to bear the burden under {\i ha-motzi mei-chaveiro alav ha-re\u8217'ayah}. If she claims {\i besulah havina} and the only counter-evidence is that no sealed barrel was displayed, she can readily explain: {\i itnusi hu di-itnusu} \u8212- they were too caught up in the celebration, or too drunk, and simply never got around to the barrel ceremony. The absence of the practice proves nothing in the face of that ready excuse. But if an open barrel was explicitly brought before her, that is a concrete act \u8212- a {\i shor shachut} \u8212- that cannot be explained away, and that will serve as definitive evidence of her {\i be\u8217'ulah} status at the time of the wedding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 17\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Praise the Bride Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens two lines from the bottom of 16b, in the midst of a broader discussion about the practices that served as evidence of a first wedding \u8212- relevant decades later when a couple might divorce and the amount of the {\i ketubah} is disputed. Against that backdrop, the Gemara transitions to one of the more famous debates between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel, introduced by the formula {\i tanu rabbanan}: How does one dance before the {\i kallah}? What does one say while performing this {\i rikud}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kallah Kemoshechi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai says: {\i kallah k\u8217'moshechi} \u8212- you praise her according to what she actually is. You tell it straight, you are honest about her. Beit Hillel says: {\i kallah na\u8217'ah vechasudah} \u8212- you say that she is beautiful and charming, regardless of the particulars.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kallah Na\u8217'ah Vechasudah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai challenges Beit Hillel directly: What if she was lame or blind \u8212- {\i chigeret o sumah}? Would you still say {\i kallah na\u8217'ah vechasudah}? It is worth pausing over the fact that Beit Shammai hones in specifically on physical disability as its test case. The Torah itself says {\i midvar sheker tirchak} \u8212- distance yourself from falsehood. How can Beit Hillel countenance saying something demonstrably untrue?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikach Ra Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel responds with an analogy: If someone made a bad purchase in the marketplace and comes to you and says, \u8220"What do you think of what I bought?\u8221" \u8212- {\i yishabchenu be\u8217'einav o yiganenu be\u8217'einav} \u8212- do you praise it in his eyes or disparage it? The obvious answer is {\i yishabchenu be\u8217'einav} \u8212- of course you say something nice. From here, the {\i chachamim} derive a broader principle: {\i le\u8217'olam tehei da\u8217'ato shel adam me\u8217'urevet im habriyot} \u8212- a person should always conduct himself in a way that is amenable to other people, sensitive to what they want and need to hear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting what Beit Hillel is {\i not} saying. They are not claiming that the praise is objectively true. Rather, the rationale is rooted in human sensitivity \u8212- in attending to what a person actually wants. Rashi captures this with the phrase {\i la\u8217'asot le-ish ve-ish kirtzono}: to do for each person according to his desire. The bride\u8217's husband is the one marrying her; the question is what will serve him and her at this moment. This dynamic, in many ways, captures something essential about the broader Beit Shammai\u8211-Beit Hillel divide: does one orient primarily toward some objective truth that human beings must accommodate themselves to, or does one begin with human realities and work outward from there? One way to frame that debate is the question of {\i mah nivra techilah} \u8212- whether {\i shamayim} or {\i aretz} was created first. Beit Shammai prioritizes the objective and transcendent; Beit Hillel begins with the human and the earthly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Finding Nice Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reading of {\i kallah k\u8217'moshechi} is that you emphasize what is genuinely beautiful about her, what is praiseworthy \u8212- {\i l\u8217'fi yofyah vachashivutah m\u8217'kalsim otah}. But this immediately raises a difficulty: what if there is simply nothing nice to say?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this directly. If she has a blemish \u8212- {\i im yesh bah mum} \u8212- then one should be silent, {\i yishtaku}. As the saying goes, if you have nothing nice to say, don\u8217't say anything at all. Alternatively, Tosafot says, {\i yishabchu b\u8217'davar na\u8217'eh sheyesh bah} \u8212- find something nice to say: perhaps her eyes, perhaps her hands, perhaps some other quality. But Beit Hillel, on Tosafot\u8217's reading, says you praise her {\i l\u8217'gamri} \u8212- in general terms, {\i kallah na\u8217'ah vechasudah}. And therein lies the difficulty: if for everyone else you say the full formula, but for {\i this} bride you mention her lovely eyes \u8212- people can read between the lines. The selective praise becomes an implicit critique.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's reading also illuminates Beit Hillel\u8217's position in a different way. {\i Kallah na\u8217'ah vechasudah} is to some extent a formulaic expression, and formulaic language operates differently from a literal claim. Everyone understands that it is a convention; no one hears it as a precise assessment. Furthermore, beauty is subjective \u8212- and in the eyes of her husband, she may indeed be all of that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maharsha Reconciles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Maharsha goes further and tries to tone down the apparent harshness of Beit Shammai\u8217's position. When Beit Shammai says {\i kallah k\u8217'moshechi}, he argues, it does not mean that each wedding requires a fresh individual assessment of the bride\u8217's qualities. Rather, it means that however this {\i kallah} is \u8212- whoever she is \u8212- she is beautiful for {\i her} husband. She is {\i k\u8217'moshechi} in the sense that she is exactly what he wants and needs. On this reading, Beit Shammai is also making a formulaic statement, just one that more explicitly underscores the subjective dimension: she is beautiful {\i as she is}, meaning in his eyes. The difference between the two schools, then, is simply that Beit Shammai calls attention to the subjective frame while Beit Hillel does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot makes a related point about the Beit Shammai position elsewhere in the discussion. Even Beit Shammai would not instruct one to disparage a new purchase. Everyone understands that social sensitivity requires saying something kind when someone asks your opinion. Beit Shammai\u8217's objection is narrower: one should not establish a rabbinic {\i takkanah} requiring a formulaic falsehood. {\i Ain lahem l\u8217'chachamim l\u8217'saken v\u8217'yizakku l\u8217'mar sheker} \u8212- the sages should not be institutionalizing the practice of saying something that is not true, given that the Torah says {\i midvar sheker tirchak}. Beit Shammai acknowledges social grace; they simply resist encoding it as law.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wedding Practices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Kchal v\u8217'lo Srak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Dimi came from Eretz Yisrael, he reported what they would sing before the bride in the west: {\i lo kchal v\u8217'lo srak v\u8217'lo pirchus v\u8217'ya\u8217'alat chen} \u8212- no eyeshadow, no rouge, no elaborate hairdo, and yet she radiates charm. The song celebrates natural, unadorned beauty \u8212- an inner grace that requires no embellishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smicha Celebration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Strikingly, the Gemara then reports that when the rabbis gave {\i smicha} to Rabbi Zeira, they sang this same song. They had a full celebration \u8212- singing and dancing at the ordination \u8212- and the song they chose for the {\i kallah} was the one they sang for the newly ordained {\i talmid chacham}. This is at once arresting and profound. The comparison of Torah scholarship to a bride, and of the {\i talmid chacham} to the beloved, runs deep in rabbinic culture. The relationship between the scholar and the Torah is, in certain registers, an intimate and even erotic one \u8212- husbands leaving their wives to go and learn, scholars described as married to the Torah. Here, the honoree of the {\i smicha} celebration is cast, in the song, in the role of the bride: naturally beautiful, requiring no adornment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ami and Assi Songs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i smicha} was conferred upon Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Assi, the song was different. The rabbis sang: {\i kol mindein v\u8217'kol mindein s\u8217'muchu lana} \u8212- anyone like this one and this one, we should ordain from among us. And: {\i lo t\u8217'samchu lana lo min sarmisin v\u8217'lo min sarmitin} \u8212- do not ordain for us from among the {\i sarmisin} and the {\i sarmitin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains: the {\i sarmisin} are those who distort the Torah, from the root {\i lesares} \u8212- to twist. The {\i sarmitin}, Rashi says, connotes rags, something worthless. There is an alternate version: {\i lo min chamisin v\u8217'lo min turmisim} \u8212- not from those who deliver only a fifth of the reasoning, and not from the empty or worthless ones. The Maharsha adds a pointed observation here: that in his own day, many rabbis had fallen into precisely these failings \u8212- {\i ki ba-avonoteinu rabbim me-ha-rabbanim she-nichshalim be-zeh ba-dor ha-zeh} \u8212- and that the matter deserved remedy at the hands of the communal and civic leadership. Whoever unpacks the full context of that Maharsha\u8217's complaint will find it worth studying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is somewhat deflating, of course, to be celebrated at your {\i smicha} with a song that says, in essence, at least you are not a corrupt distorter of the Torah. The song for Rabbi Zeira \u8212- natural beauty, no adornment needed \u8212- is rather more flattering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbahu\u8217's Prestige\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then describes Rabbi Abbahu: when he would come from the {\i metivta} to the house of the Caesar, the maidservants of Caesar\u8217's household would come out to greet him and sing: {\i rabba de-ameih u-madbarna de-umteih botzina de-nehora baruch matyeich le-shalom} \u8212- teacher of his people, leader of his nation, candle of light, blessed be your coming in peace. Presumably he would arrive radiating something from his learning, and even the non-Jewish servants of the imperial household recognized it. The Maharsha notes this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking throughout this passage is the persistent way in which discussions of weddings and brides fold into discussions of Torah learning and scholars. The woman as purchase, the {\i kallah} as beloved, and the {\i talmid chacham} as one who is himself like a bride \u8212- these images keep circling back on one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dancing with Hadassim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They said about Rabbi Yehuda be-Rabbi Ilai that he would take a branch of myrtle \u8212- {\i bad shel hadas} \u8212- and dance before the bride, {\i merakked lifnei ha-kallah}. And as he danced, he would sing: {\i kallah na\u8217'ah vechasudah}. So he enacted the practice fully \u8212- physical dancing with the {\i hadas}, combined with the Beit Hillel formula. This is the same Rabbi Yehuda be-Rabbi Ilai we will encounter again shortly, and his conduct here is entirely consistent with what we will find there: a person who was {\i mochel} on his own dignity in order to bring joy to others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Fold Dancing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak would dance {\i a-tlat} \u8212- by threes. Rashi understood this to mean he would juggle three branches of myrtle, though the Maharsha objects on both textual and practical grounds: the Aramaic would be {\i bi-tlat} rather than {\i a-tlat} for that meaning, and in any case, myrtle branches do not make for good juggling implements. The Maharsha prefers to read it as meaning he danced with three times the vigor of anyone else \u8212- he threw himself into the {\i rikud} with an abandon that put everyone around him to shame.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira, watching, said: {\i ka-machsif lan sabba} \u8212- the elder is embarrassing us. On Rashi\u8217's reading, Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak was simply undignified, carrying on in a way unbefitting a scholar. On the Maharsha\u8217's reading, he was embarrassing the younger scholars by outpacing them entirely \u8212- they were young, and the old man was dancing three times as hard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pillar of Fire\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak died, {\i afsik amuda de-nura bein dideih le-chulei alma} \u8212- a pillar of fire separated between him and all the rest of the world. And it is transmitted that a pillar of fire only appears for someone who is singular in the generation, or perhaps one of two people \u8212- {\i le-chad be-dara ei le-trei be-dara} \u8212- because it marks them as set apart, in a class unto themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same Rabbi Zeira who had said the elder was embarrassing them now said: {\i ahani leih shutita de-sabba} \u8212- it was the branch of the elder that helped him. Or, in the alternate versions: his {\i shtus} \u8212- his wildness \u8212- or his {\i shita} \u8212- his approach \u8212- that was what elevated him. The one who was {\i mochel} on his own {\i kavod} to bring {\i simcha} to the {\i chatan} was the one who merited this singular distinction. There is an unmistakable echo here of David dancing before the Ark and Michal\u8217's critique, and while the Gemara does not make that comparison explicitly, the structural parallel \u8212- dignity versus abandon in the service of something sacred \u8212- is very much present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bride on Shoulders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha would carry the {\i kallah} on his shoulders and dance. The rabbis asked him: are we permitted to do the same? He replied: {\i ei damya aleichu k\u8217'kashura l\u8217'chai} \u8212- if she is to you like a beam of wood, if there is no sexual dimension to it whatsoever, then yes, go ahead. {\i V\u8217'ei lo, lo} \u8212- but if it will arouse improper thoughts, then no. The ruling is entirely subjective. This opens onto a broader discussion among the {\i Rishonim} about which categories of male-female interaction are governed by subjective assessment and which by objective prohibition \u8212- a question with real practical implications.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bride vs.\u160 ?Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Looking All Week\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shmuel bar Nachmani said in the name of Rabbi Yonatan: {\i mutar le-histakel bifnei kallah kol shiva} \u8212- one is permitted to gaze admiringly at the {\i kallah} all seven days of the wedding celebrations. The purpose is {\i kedei le-chabevah al ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- to make her beloved in her husband\u8217's eyes: if everyone is looking at her admiringly, the husband will feel that she is genuinely beautiful and desirable. However, the Gemara notes: {\i leit hilcheta ke-vatei} \u8212- the law does not follow this ruling. That is simply asking for trouble.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'avirin Hamet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to other expressions of communal participation in the life-cycle moments of marriage and death. Just as there is {\i merakdin bifnei ha-kallah} \u8212- the community dancing before the bride \u8212- there is a broader principle of communal obligation at both weddings and burials. And just as there is a week of {\i shiva brachot} when the community participates in the joy, there is a week of {\i shivah} when the community participates in the mourning. These are not private affairs; they are communal experiences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the Gemara teaches: {\i ma\u8217'avirin et ha-met milifnei ha-kallah} \u8212- you move the funeral procession aside to make way for the wedding party. When the two groups converge in the street, the {\i kallah} has the right of way. This is a striking ruling. A reader of {\i Kohelet} might expect the opposite \u8212- {\i tov la-lekhet el beit avel milalechet el beit mishteh} \u8212- it is better to go to a house of mourning than a house of feasting. Yet the rabbis make a strikingly life-affirming ruling: attending to joy, to the next generation, to {\i simchat chatan ve-kallah}, takes precedence over the funeral. Both groups, in turn, yield to the king, who always has priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agrippas\u8217's Humility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara recounts that Agrippas the king stepped aside before the {\i kallah}, and the sages praised him for this. {\i Shibchu chachamim} \u8212- they commended him. But the Gemara immediately puzzles over this: if the sages praised him, does that mean it was the right thing to do?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Royal Honor Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi weighs in: even according to the view that a {\i nasi} \u8212- a prince or governor \u8212- who waives his honor has waived it legitimately ({\i nasi she-machal al kvodo, kvodo machul}), a {\i melech} who waives his honor has {\i not} waived it ({\i melech she-machal al kvodo, ein kvodo machul}). The verse says {\i som tasim alecha melech} \u8212- the double language of {\i som tasim} indicates that the king\u8217's authority must remain perpetual and inviolable. {\i She-tehei emato alecha} \u8212- his awe must remain upon the people. The Maharsha adds that royal honor is also an expression of {\i chalak michvodo le-vasar ve-dam} \u8212- it reflects a dimension of divine kingship that cannot simply be set aside by the king\u8217's personal choice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So how was Agrippas praised for doing something that seems halachically impermissible? The Gemara answers: {\i parashat derachim havai} \u8212- it was a crossroads. The two parties encountered each other at an intersection, and the king\u8217's entourage made a turn that happened to open the way for the wedding party. It was not an explicit act of stepping aside; there was no obvious {\i mechilah} on the king\u8217's {\i kavod}. The praise was real, but the mechanism was subtle enough not to constitute a formal waiver of royal dignity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Suspending Torah Study\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mevatlin Talmud Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the relationship between {\i talmud Torah} and the communal obligations of burial and wedding celebration. {\i Mevatlin Talmud Torah le-hotza\u8217'at ha-met ule-hachnasat kallah} \u8212- one sets aside Torah study for the procession of the dead and for bringing in the bride. This ruling reflects something deeper than a formal principle of {\i osek be-mitzvah patur min ha-mitzvah}. It captures something about the relationship between the scholar and the community, between the {\i beit midrash} and the lived realities of the people around it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leaving Beit Midrash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They said about Rabbi Yehuda be-Rabbi Ilai \u8212- the same one who danced with the {\i hadas} \u8212- that he would leave the {\i beit midrash} for both occasions. This is deeply consistent with everything we know about him. He was {\i mochel} on his {\i kevod ha-Torah} to be genuinely present with people in their {\i simchas} and their losses. There is something powerful here. The {\i Yerushalmi} formulation captures the underlying principle: {\i kol ha-lomed she-lo al menat la-asot, mutav lo she-lo lamad} \u8212- one who studies without the intention to act would have been better off not learning at all. Torah must translate into the real world, into how one actually shows up for people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai emerging from the cave is worth noting. There too the question is whether the scholar, at his elevated level of Torah, can connect to and participate in ordinary human life and joy. The image of the man running with two {\i hadassim} to honor {\i Shabbat} \u8212- that mundane, earthy expression of love for a mitzvah \u8212- is what opens Rabbi Shimon\u8217's eyes. Here, Rabbi Yehuda be-Rabbi Ilai models the same orientation from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enough People Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara qualifies this: {\i bameh devarim amurim beshein imo kol tzorko} \u8212- when is Torah study suspended? Only when there are not enough people for the occasion. {\i Aval yesh imo kol tzorko, ein mevatlin} \u8212- if there are already enough, there is no obligation to leave the {\i beit midrash}. The question then becomes: what counts as enough?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Required Numbers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Shmuel bar Ini mishmeih de-Rav}: twelve thousand men, and six thousand {\i shofarot} \u8212- trumpeters or horn-blowers who would either call people to attend or simply be part of the procession. An alternate version gives thirteen thousand men, with six thousand of those blowing {\i shofarot}. Ulla offers a different and more visual measure: that there are enough men to form a wall from the gate of the city all the way to the cemetery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natilatah Kentinatah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet \u8212- and some attribute it to Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- offers the most demanding standard: {\i netilatah kentinatah} \u8212- the taking away of Torah is like the giving of Torah. Just as the Torah was given with 600,000 people present, so too, for the death of a {\i talmid chacham}, there are \u8220"enough people\u8221" only when 600,000 are present. This makes clear what had perhaps not been obvious: these enormous numbers are not about any ordinary deceased person. They are about the death of a {\i talmid chacham}, and the obligation is rooted in {\i kevod ha-Torah}, not merely {\i kevod ha-met}. You leave your {\i beit midrash} to show honor to the Torah that is being taken from the world. This shifts the calculus significantly: the {\i beit midrash} is not competing with {\i chesed} to the dead; it is itself engaged in {\i kevod ha-Torah}, which is why it requires a demonstration of {\i kevod ha-Torah} on the other side to dislodge it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also raises a question about the {\i kallah} case: there, there is no threshold tied to the stature of the {\i chatan} or {\i kallah} \u8212- one simply goes. The asymmetry is worth sitting with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teachers Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds a final, remarkable qualification. {\i Ve-hanie milei le-man de-kari ve-tani} \u8212- these thresholds apply for someone who read Scripture and learned Mishnah but never taught. {\i Aval le-man de-matni, leit lei shiura} \u8212- for one who taught Torah, there is no measure; everyone must attend, and no number is sufficient to say there are already enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi draws the implication in the other direction as well: {\i shemeinan de-le-man de-la kari ve-la tani} \u8212- for someone who was entirely outside the world of Torah learning, the measure is purely pragmatic. If there are enough people present to accomplish the burial, you are not obligated to leave the {\i beit midrash} to make the crowd larger. For an ordinary deceased person, ten people may well be enough. The gap between the thresholds is vast. This further confirms that the entire framework is not primarily about dignity for the dead, but about {\i kevod ha-Torah} \u8212- and only {\i kevod ha-Torah} is weighty enough to pull one out of the {\i beit midrash}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First Marriage Evide (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hinuma Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the evidential practices that distinguish first weddings from second, the Gemara asks: {\i ei yesh edim she-yatzah be-hinuma} \u8212- if there are witnesses that she went out with a {\i hinuma}, that establishes that it was a first wedding. But what is a {\i hinuma}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Myrtle Dome\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Surhav bar Pappa in the name of Ziri says: {\i tanura d\u8217'asa} \u8212- literally, an oven of myrtle, understood by Rashi as a kind of dome constructed from myrtle branches, perhaps something like a canopy or archway for the bride to walk beneath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Veil Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i kviyasta d\u8217'mnamnu bah kallah} \u8212- a covering in which the {\i kallah} would doze off. Rashi understands this as a kind of veil covering her eyes, something that shielded her so thoroughly she could even catch a brief sleep. Both descriptions point toward practices that parallel, in one way or another, what we still recognize as the {\i chuppah} and the veil \u8212- two of the most ancient and enduring features of a Jewish wedding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Roasted Grain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka holds that the distribution of roasted grain is sufficient as evidence. A {\i Baraita} confirms that in {\i Yehudah}, the distribution of roasted grain was indeed the recognized sign of a first wedding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oil on Rabbis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in Babylonia? The Gemara asks: {\i B\u8217'Bavel mai?} Rav answers: {\i dardugei d\u8217'mishcha areisha d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- the dripping of oil on the heads of the rabbis. At a first wedding, they would go to the rabbis\u8217' table and pour oil over the heads of the scholars present. Once again, the intersection of the rabbinic world and the wedding celebration is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oil Ritual Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa challenged this: {\i mishcha d\u8217'chafifusa ka\u8217'amar mar} \u8212- are you saying they would take hair-washing oil and pour it over people\u8217's heads? Rashi suggests he was offended by the image \u8212- as though the rabbis needed a grooming treatment or something unseemly was being implied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Reminder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responded pointedly. He called Rav Pappa {\i yatma} \u8212- orphan \u8212- which is remarkable, since it is Abaye who was himself orphaned. Whether this is ironic or whether he is speaking loosely, the force of his reply is clear: {\i v\u8217'lo avda lach immach dardugei mishcha areisha d\u8217'rabbanan b\u8217'sha\u8217'as ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- did your own mother not do this at your wedding? Are you really going to take offense at a practice that was performed for you? The word {\i yatma} carries additional poignancy in Abaye\u8217's mouth given his own biography.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinical Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then records a concrete historical account: one of the rabbis married his son into the household of Rabbah bar Rav Ulla \u8212- or in the alternate version, Rabbah bar Rav Ulla married his son into another rabbinic family \u8212- and at the time of the wedding, {\i dardig mishcha areisha d\u8217'rabbanan b\u8217'sha\u8217'as ma\u8217'aseh}, oil was dripped on the heads of the rabbis. The practice is thus attested in actual rabbinic family life.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First Marriage Evide (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow Weddings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i almanusa mai} \u8212- what about a widow? If the evidential practices mark a first wedding, is there a corresponding practice that marks a second? The Gemara does not provide an affirmative answer. Rav Yosef teaches: {\i almanusa leisa kisnai} \u8212- for a widow, the roasted grain is not distributed. The evidence for a first wedding is thus largely defined by the presence of these practices, and for a second wedding by their absence. Tosafot notes the gap: one might have hoped for a more active marker of the second wedding, but the Gemara does not supply one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ha-Peh She-Asar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Admission Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to the principle of {\i ha-peh she-asar hu ha-peh she-hitir} \u8212- the mouth that bound is the mouth that permits, also called {\i modeh bi-miktzat} or, in this context, {\i ha-peh she-asar}. The case is: a man is found on a field and says to the person who challenges him, \u8220"This field belonged to your father, and I purchased it from him.\u8221" By admitting that it once belonged to the other party\u8217's father, he opens himself to that claim \u8212- but having made that admission, he is believed when he adds that he purchased it, because he could have remained silent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi and Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a fundamental debate between Rashi and Tosafot about what \u8220"he could have remained silent\u8221" means. Rashi holds that the other party \u8212- the one challenging him \u8212- was entirely oblivious. He had no awareness that this person was on the field, no complaint, no suspicion. In that situation, the occupant had nothing to answer; his voluntary admission and explanation together constitute a {\i ha-peh she-asar}. Tosafot argues that this is too narrow and that the actual case involves a genuine challenge: the other party says, \u8220"That\u8217's my field, what are you doing there?\u8221" There is, however, no {\i evidence} that needs answering \u8212- it is {\i stam kala}, an unsubstantiated claim. The occupant could have simply said {\i lo hayu devarim me-olam} \u8212- it is completely false \u8212- and the challenger would have had nothing. Instead, he concedes the historical ownership and claims a purchase. That concession, volunteered when it was not required, creates the {\i ha-peh she-asar}. This is already more of a {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Mentioned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One asks: why does the {\i Mishnah} frame the case as \u8220"I purchased it from your {\i father}\u8221" rather than \u8220"I purchased it from you\u8221"? On Rashi\u8217's reading, asking why the man doesn\u8217't say he bought it directly from the challenger is almost incongruous \u8212- if the challenger is oblivious, how could he have been the one who sold it? But on Tosafot\u8217's reading, the question is sharper: the challenger himself is present, claiming the field is his. Why not have the occupant say, \u8220"I bought it from you\u8221"? That would be an even stronger case of {\i bari u-bari} \u8212- both parties making certain claims \u8212- and yet Rabbi Yehoshua would still believe the occupant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains: {\i mishum de-ka-ba\u8217'ei le-mitni seifa} \u8212- it is because of the need to teach the {\i seifa}. The end of the {\i Mishnah} states: if there are witnesses that the field belonged to the challenger, the occupant is not believed. And the Gemara asks \u8212- what is the {\i chiddush} of the {\i seifa}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Year Chazakah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the occupant had three full years of uncontested residence \u8212- {\i achal shenei chazakah} \u8212- he should win regardless of witnesses to original ownership. If he did not have three years, of course he is not believed; that is obvious. So where is the novelty?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Katan Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i bishlama gabei aviv} \u8212- with the father framing, we can construct a genuine {\i chiddush}. The case is {\i mashkachat lah de-achal shtayim be-chayei ha-av ve-achat be-chayei beno} \u8212- the occupant was on the field for two years during the father\u8217's lifetime and one year during the son\u8217's. That gives him three years of residence. Yet he is not believed, because of Rav Huna\u8217's principle: {\i ein machzikin be-nichsei katan} \u8212- one cannot establish {\i chazakah} over the property of a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason {\i chazakah} works at all is that three years of uncontested residence is taken as evidence that the original owner waived his claim \u8212- he saw you on his field, knew about it, and said nothing. But a minor cannot be expected to register the presence of someone on his field and formulate a protest. His silence is therefore meaningless; it creates no presumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Afilu Higdil\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The larger {\i chiddush} of Rav Huna is {\i afilu higdil} \u8212- even once the minor becomes an adult, the years that began during his minority do not retroactively become valid years of {\i chazakah}. If someone was on that field during the child\u8217's minority, and the child grows up and says nothing for another nine years, those nine years of adult silence are tainted by the original period. The occupant would need to leave and return \u8212- or obtain a {\i shtar} \u8212- to establish a valid {\i chazakah} from scratch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i Mishnah} needed the father precisely to give the {\i seifa} a meaningful {\i chiddush}: you have three years of residence, witnesses confirm the field once belonged to the other party, and yet you are still not believed \u8212- because two of those years were during the son\u8217's minority, and {\i ein machzikin be-nichsei katan afilu higdil}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelo Befanav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: could the {\i Mishnah} not have achieved the same {\i chiddush} without introducing the father, by using the scenario of {\i shelo befanav} \u8212- years of residence when the owner was not present? {\i Chazakah} requires that the owner could have protested; if he was absent, his silence is not meaningful, and the {\i chazakah} does not take hold. One year\u8217's absence could thus undercut an otherwise complete three-year {\i chazakah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judea and Galilee\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara invokes the {\i Mishnah} in {\i Bava Batra}: {\i shalosh aratzot le-chazakah} \u8212- there are three regions with respect to {\i chazakah}: {\i Yehudah}, {\i Ever HaYarden}, and {\i ha-Galil}. If the owner was in {\i Yehudah} and the occupant was on his field in the {\i Galil} \u8212- or the reverse \u8212- it is not a valid {\i chazakah} until they are in the same region. The Gemara probes this: if a protest not in the owner\u8217's presence \u8212- {\i mecha\u8217'ah shelo befanav} \u8212- is legally effective, then news travels across regions and {\i chazakah} should work regardless of which region each party is in. And if such a protest is {\i not} effective, then even within the same region, so long as the parties are in different cities, {\i chazakah} should not work. What is the principled distinction that makes region matter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Emergency Situation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba bar Memel resolves this: {\i le\u8217'olam kasavar mecha\u8217'ah shelo befanav havya mecha\u8217'ah} \u8212- in principle, a protest not in the owner\u8217's presence is valid, and news is presumed to travel. {\i U-matnitin b\u8217'sha\u8217'at cheirum shanoy} \u8212- but our {\i Mishnah} is dealing with a time of emergency, when an edict restricts travel. In such circumstances, the normal presumption that news crosses borders does not hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why specifically {\i Yehudah} and {\i Galil}? {\i D\u8217'stam Yehudah ve-Galil ke-sha\u8217'at cheirum damu} \u8212- because as a general rule, the border between {\i Yehudah} and the {\i Galil} functioned like a time of emergency: crossing it was difficult and politically fraught. News did not flow freely between them. The ruling thus reflects not an abstract theoretical position, but an observed social reality \u8212- that information travels within a country\u8217's borders far more reliably than it crosses political and geographic boundaries, especially when those crossings are contested or restricted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes that the {\i shelo befanav} scenario could in principle have served to explain the {\i Mishnah\u8217's seifa} \u8212- a case where {\i chazakah} fails despite three years of residence \u8212- but it would have been a less striking {\i chiddush} than the case of the father and the minor son. Hence the {\i Mishnah} chose the father formulation, precisely because the {\i chiddush} of Rav Huna \u8212- that {\i chazakah} begun during a minor\u8217's infancy remains invalid even after he reaches majority \u8212- is less intuitive and more genuinely novel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 18\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Field vs.\u160 ?Loan Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Introduction & Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to a question left hanging from the previous discussion: why did Rabbi Yehoshua, when illustrating the principle of {\i peh she-asar hu peh she-hitir}, choose the example of a field \u8212- \u8220"this field belonged to your father, and I purchased it from him\u8221" \u8212- rather than some other example? The remainder of this section of the {\i gemara}, and indeed a substantial portion of the rest of this {\i perek}, will feel at times like a digression into the territory of Bava Metzia, exploring {\i to\u8217'en v\u8217'nitan}, {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat}, and related principles of monetary law. But this is not a digression. The {\i mishnayot} that follow in this {\i perek} are built around the principle of {\i peh she-asar} as it applies across a range of cases, many of them far removed from the original {\i ketubot} context. {\i Peh she-asar} in {\i dinei mamonos} is precisely the subject, and the {\i gemara} is using the question about Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's choice of example as a gateway into that broader discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loan Case Doesn\u8217't Fit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why not frame the case as a loan? Why not say that Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's example of {\i peh she-asar} is a person who tells his friend, \u8220"I borrowed a hundred dollars from you and I paid it back,\u8221" where no external evidence exists? That too would be a clean {\i peh she-asar} \u8212- the same mouth that admitted the debt also claimed repayment. The answer the {\i gemara} gives is that such a case would not work for the {\i mishnah}\u8217's structure. The {\i mishnah} is structured in two halves: when the person freely offers up all the information, {\i peh she-asar}, he is believed; but when witnesses independently establish the first part of his claim, he is not believed regarding the second. Applied to a loan, the {\i seifa} would have to say: if there are witnesses that he borrowed the money, and he now claims repayment, he is not believed. But that is simply not true.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repayment Without Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} has already established the principle: {\i ha-malveh et chaveiro b\u8217'edim eino tzarich l\u8217'poreo b\u8217'edim} \u8212- if someone lends money to another with only witnesses (as opposed to a written {\i shtar}), the borrower is not required to produce witnesses at the time of repayment. He may simply pay back the money, and his claim that he did so is believed. This is the established {\i halacha}. And if that is the case, then even where witnesses testify to the original loan, the borrower\u8217's unilateral claim of repayment remains credible. The {\i seifa} the {\i gemara} would have constructed \u8212- that witnesses to the borrowing render the repayment claim unbelievable \u8212- would therefore be halachically false. Hence, the loan scenario cannot serve as Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's example.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Business & Policy Impact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i halacha} is striking from a practical standpoint. One might reasonably worry about its societal effects. If a borrower can simply claim repayment without any corroboration even when witnesses exist to the original debt, that creates a significant disincentive for lenders. On the other hand, the ruling may function as a quiet push toward writing a {\i shtar} for every transaction, which has its own advantages in terms of documentation and clarity. But then again, the burdens of formalizing every loan also carry costs \u8212- they make lending more cumbersome, and history has shown that anything which makes lending more difficult tends to disadvantage those who most need credit. The tension here is real, even if the {\i halacha} is clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Audacity Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic of this ruling is a {\i chazakah} that will recur throughout the {\i gemara}: {\i ein adam meiz panav bifnei ba\u8217'al chovo} \u8212- no one has the audacity to look the person who did them the favor of lending money straight in the eye and brazenly deny repayment if they never actually paid. This {\i chazakah} is weighty enough, in the {\i gemara}\u8217's view, to counteract the evidentiary force of witnesses to the original loan. The logic is reinforced by the nature of a loan itself: unlike a disputed ownership claim, a loan is by its very nature meant to be repaid. Witnesses to a loan are witnesses to a transaction whose next natural step is repayment. Testifying to the existence of the loan does not foreclose the obvious possibility that the expected repayment followed. The {\i chazakah} then adds that a person would not lie about that repayment to the very creditor who helped him. The {\i halacha} stands, and consequently, the loan example cannot fit the {\i mishnah}\u8217's structure.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Loan to Father, Claim by\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case: Half Consumed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having explained why the loan example fails, the {\i gemara} presses further: why not use a different case? Specifically, why not have Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's example be a person who says to a friend, {\i maneh l\u8217'avicha b\u8217'yadi v\u8217'he\u8217'achalti pras} \u8212- \u8220"a hundred dollars of your father\u8217's is in my hands, and I consumed half of it\u8221"? In other words, the father lent me a hundred dollars, I still owe fifty, and I am now voluntarily disclosing this to you, the son. This too appears to be a clean {\i peh she-asar} scenario: the same mouth that disclosed the debt also limits it. And structurally, it seems like it could parallel the {\i mishnah}\u8217's two-part structure \u8212- free disclosure leads to belief, while witnesses to the borrowing would lead to a different result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modeh B\u8217'Miktzat Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before analyzing why this case cannot serve as Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's example, the {\i gemara} introduces a necessary background concept: {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat}. If someone is confronted with a claim \u8212- \u8220"you owe me a hundred dollars\u8221" \u8212- and responds, \u8220"I only owe you fifty,\u8221" that partial admission creates a legal obligation to take an oath regarding the disputed remainder. This is a Torah-level rule. And the obvious question, already anticipated by the {\i gemara}, is why this person does not simply have a {\i miggo} \u8212- a logical argument from a stronger position. If he were truly lying, he could have denied the entire claim. By admitting half, he is actually weakening his own position. So why should his partial concession not be treated as fully credible on the strength of the {\i miggo} that he could have denied everything?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, as the {\i gemara} will spell out through {\i Rabba}\u8217's principle, is precisely that he does not have a {\i miggo}, because the {\i chazakah} of {\i ein adam meiz panav bifnei ba\u8217'al chovo} forecloses the option he supposedly \u8220"could have\u8221" taken. He could not, psychologically and socially speaking, have denied everything to the very person who lent him the money. And therefore the \u8220"stronger position\u8221" from which the {\i miggo} would be drawn \u8212- complete denial \u8212- was never actually available to him. Since he could not have denied the whole debt, partially admitting it earns him no evidentiary credit, and he must swear about the remainder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, what happens when there is no formal {\i ta\u8217'anah} \u8212- no active claim being pressed? What if the son has made no demand, and the borrower simply volunteers the information? Is this person still a {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} with an obligation to swear? Or is the absence of a formal claim dispositive?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis: Meshiv Aveida\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains that this case cannot be used as Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's example because it does not work according to either of the two positions among the {\i tana\u8217'im}. According to the {\i Chachamim}, the borrower in this scenario is always treated as a {\i meshiv aveida} \u8212- someone who returns a lost object. The logic, as {\i Tosafot} frames it, is essentially a {\i miggo}: when dealing with the son rather than the father himself, the borrower is fully prepared to deny the entire debt. The son does not know his father\u8217's business dealings; there would be no social or psychological pressure preventing a complete denial. Therefore, any admission at all is entirely voluntary, an act of pure good faith, analogous to returning a found wallet. Just as we cannot demand an oath from someone who returns a found object \u8212- doing so would destroy the incentive to ever return anything \u8212- so too we do not impose an oath here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This logic holds even when witnesses independently establish the first part \u8212- that the father made the loan. After all, we have already established that even with witnesses to a loan, the borrower is believed to claim repayment. If even full repayment could be claimed credibly in the face of witnesses, then a partial repayment claim to the son, where the borrower could have denied everything, is all the more clearly a {\i meshiv aveida}. The {\i Chachamim} therefore hold that in all variants of this case \u8212- whether or not there are witnesses to the original loan \u8212- the borrower is exempt from an oath. This means the {\i mishnah}\u8217's structure cannot be reproduced: there is no scenario where witnesses to the first half of the claim render the second half unbelievable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Oath Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the other extreme, Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov holds that even when the borrower volunteers the information entirely on his own, he is still obligated to take an oath. This emerges from a {\i beraita}: {\i pe\u8217'amim she\u8217'adam nishba al ta\u8217'anat atzmo} \u8212- sometimes a person takes an oath even on a claim that he himself raised, not in response to any demand from the other party. The example given is precisely this case: if someone says \u8220"I borrowed a hundred dollars from your father and consumed half,\u8221" he must take an oath. The {\i Chachamim} respond that he is no different from a {\i meshiv aveida} and is exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So at first glance, Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's position seems to be that {\i peh she-asar} does not even work here to produce an exemption. The {\i gemara} questions this: does Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov not accept the {\i meshiv aveida} principle at all? The {\i gemara} introduces {\i Rav}\u8217's answer: the {\i beraita}\u8217's language of {\i ta\u8217'anat atzmo} is not to be taken literally. There is in fact a {\i ta\u8217'anah} \u8212- a claim \u8212- being pressed. It is just that the one pressing it appears to be a {\i katan}, a minor, whose claim would ordinarily carry no legal weight, and so it is described as if no claim were being made at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son\u8217's Claim Weight Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately questions this: but we know that one does not take an oath in response to a claim from a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, or {\i katan}! And so the {\i gemara} clarifies: {\i mai katan? gadol} \u8212- \u8220"what is meant by minor? A full adult.\u8221" He is called {\i katan} only in the metaphorical sense that he is uninformed regarding his father\u8217's affairs \u8212- a junior figure who lacks access to the information of his father\u8217's business dealings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This clarification then exposes the real heart of the dispute between Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov and the {\i Chachamim}, as {\i Tosafot} elegantly formulates it. The two sides are not really arguing about whether {\i meshiv aveida} is a valid principle. They are arguing about whether the son\u8217's claim carries the same psychological weight as the father\u8217's own claim. The {\i Chachamim} hold that a borrower is fully prepared to deny everything when facing the son \u8212- the son\u8217's claim, in the borrower\u8217's mind, carries no weight, and therefore any admission is purely voluntary, a genuine {\i meshiv aveida}. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov holds that the son\u8217's claim does carry weight \u8212- just as one would not be prepared to completely deny a debt to the creditor himself, one is equally unprepared to completely deny it to the creditor\u8217's heir. Therefore the admission of half is not freely voluntary; it is the same {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} dynamic as dealing with the father directly, and an oath is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i beraita} phrases it as {\i ta\u8217'anat atzmo} is not because Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov considers it literally a self-generated claim. Rather, the {\i Chachamim} consider the son\u8217's claim to have no weight \u8212- from their perspective it is {\i as if} no claim were being made. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov is correcting that premise, insisting that what the {\i Chachamim} call {\i ta\u8217'anat atzmo} is in fact a real {\i ta\u8217'anah}, and that he treats the son\u8217's position the same as the father\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Chutzpah to Creditor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now invokes {\i Rabba}\u8217's principle to spell out the underlying logic more fully. {\i Rabba} asks: why does the Torah require a {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} to take an oath at all? Why is there no {\i miggo} \u8212- I could have denied everything, so why not believe me when I concede half? The answer is the {\i chazakah}: {\i ein adam meiz panav bifnei ba\u8217'al chovo}. A person simply will not deny an entire debt to the very face of the creditor who helped him. And if he could not have taken the \u8220"stronger position\u8221" of complete denial \u8212- because human psychology forecloses it \u8212- then there is no {\i miggo}. He was never able to claim the whole thing, so admitting half earns him no credit. We therefore suspect him of lying about the other half, and the Torah imposes the oath to pressure a full admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then completes the psychological portrait: if this person is not prepared to lie completely, then one might ask \u8212- why is he lying at all? Why not just admit the whole debt? The answer is that he genuinely intends to pay eventually, but does not have the funds right now. He is buying time \u8212- {\i lishtamit} \u8212- rationalizing his partial denial as a delay tactic rather than outright theft. He has not psychologically committed to stealing; he is deferring. This explains both why the oath is effective (he is not a committed thief who would simply take a false oath) and why the partial denial occurs in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Compels Admission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framework then maps directly onto the debate about the son. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov holds {\i lo shna bo v\u8217'lo shna b\u8217'vno} \u8212- there is no difference between the father and the son. The borrower is not prepared to baldly deny the entire debt even to the son, and therefore any admission is not a free {\i meshiv aveida} but a constrained {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat}. The {\i Chachamim} hold {\i bo hu d\u8217'eino me\u8217'iz, aval b\u8217'vno me\u8217'iz} \u8212- he is not brazen enough to deny the whole thing to the creditor himself, but toward the son he is fully prepared to deny everything. Therefore any admission to the son is entirely voluntary, making him a genuine {\i meshiv aveida}, and he is exempt from an oath even where there are independent witnesses to the original loan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion is clear: according to the {\i Chachamim}, this case cannot illustrate the {\i mishnah}\u8217's structure because the borrower is always exempt regardless of whether witnesses exist. According to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, this case cannot illustrate the {\i mishnah}\u8217's structure because the borrower is always obligated to swear regardless of whether he volunteers the information. Neither position produces the bifurcation the {\i mishnah} requires \u8212- free disclosure yields exemption; witnesses to the first part yield obligation. Hence, the field example remains the uniquely appropriate illustration, and Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's choice is vindicated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Document Validation & Re\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Self-Validation\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now pivots to a new class of {\i peh she-asar} cases involving the authentication of documents. The scenario is as follows: a {\i shtar} is produced in court but has not yet been independently verified. The process of {\i kiyum shtarot} \u8212- validating a document\u8217's signatures \u8212- normally requires either bringing the signatories themselves to affirm their signatures, producing witnesses who recognize those signatures, or comparing them against a previously certified document. Until this process is complete, the {\i shtar} does not have the standing of formally delivered testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case, the witnesses themselves come forward and affirm: yes, that is our signature. But in the very same statement they add a disqualifying claim \u8212- we were minors when we signed it, or we were relatives of one of the parties, or we were coerced into signing a document we knew to be false. In the first two instances, the underlying transaction may well have occurred, but the testimony is invalid because the witnesses were legally disqualified. In the third instance \u8212- coercion \u8212- the transaction itself allegedly never occurred; the witnesses were forced at gunpoint, so to speak, to sign a fabricated document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules {\i harei elu ne\u8217'emanim} \u8212- they are believed. This is a {\i peh she-asar}: the same mouth that validated the document also neutralized it. However, if other witnesses independently verify the signatures, or if a previously authenticated document with matching signatures establishes their validity, then the signatories are no longer believed to add the disqualifying claim. At that point, the first half of their statement is no longer theirs to give \u8212- it has been established independently \u8212- and {\i peh she-asar} does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peh She-Asar in Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding, it is worth pausing to examine what {\i peh she-asar} actually means in this context, because there are two possible understandings, and they lead to different legal consequences. One understanding is that {\i peh she-asar hu peh she-hitir} means we accept both halves of the statement: we believe both that it is their signature and that the disqualifying circumstance is true. The alternative understanding is that the two halves of the statement cancel each other out entirely \u8212- as if nothing was said, and the document remains in its prior unvalidated state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference emerges in a case where accepting the first half of the statement would trigger some independent legal consequence. If, for example, the witnesses say \u8220"it is our signature, but we were minors,\u8221" and we treat the first half as believed and thus the {\i shtar} as validated, then we have effectively admitted live testimony into evidence. At that point, can the second half of the statement undo what the first half set in motion? Or has the first half already triggered an independent effect that the second half cannot reverse?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses this and indicates that in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's framework, the {\i peh she-asar} logic functions more like neutralization \u8212- the witnesses are treated as having said nothing. It is not that we validated the {\i shtar} and then un-validated it; rather, the entire combined statement never constituted a {\i kiyum} of the {\i shtar} at all. The two halves are so intertwined \u8212- indeed, {\i Rashi} even characterizes it as delivered {\i toch kedei dibbur}, as a single uninterrupted utterance \u8212- that they function as a unit that leaves the {\i shtar} exactly where it was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami bar Chama\u8217's Distinc\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rami bar Chama} introduces an important qualification that applies to the first part of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the case where the witnesses themselves are the ones affirming their signatures, before any independent verification. He distinguishes between two types of claimed coercion. When the witnesses say {\i anusim hayinu machmat nefashot} \u8212- we were coerced under mortal threat, a gun was held to our heads \u8212- they are believed. But when they say {\i anusim hayinu machmat mamon} \u8212- we were coerced by financial threat, the man threatened to destroy our property unless we signed \u8212- they are not believed as to the disqualifying claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for this distinction is the principle of {\i ein adam meisim atzmo rasha}. When witnesses claim that they signed a false document under threat of financial loss, they are admitting to having committed the biblical prohibition of {\i edut sheker} \u8212- bearing false testimony \u8212- for a reason that halachah does not recognize as sufficient justification. A threat to one\u8217's property does not permit transgressing a Torah prohibition; only a mortal threat creates that exemption. Therefore, by saying \u8220"we signed falsely because he threatened our finances,\u8221" they are declaring themselves {\i resha\u8217'im} \u8212- wicked, in the technical legal sense of having violated a biblical law. And the rule is that a court does not admit self-incriminating testimony of this kind. The statement is therefore inadmissible insofar as it declares them wicked, and we are left with only the first part of their combined statement: the validation of the {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a revealing illustration of how {\i peh she-asar} operates. The {\i gemara} here is not treating the two halves as mutually canceling. Rather, it is saying: we are prepared in principle to believe both halves \u8212- but sometimes, after accepting the first half, a legal rule prevents us from accepting the second. {\i Ein adam meisim atzmo rasha} is precisely such a rule. The court accepts the validation of the {\i shtar} and is then legally barred from registering the self-incriminating second half of the statement. The result is that the {\i shtar} stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when the claimed coercion was mortal \u8212- {\i machmat nefashot} \u8212- no transgression occurred. Signing a false document to save one\u8217's life is halachically permitted. Therefore there is no self-incrimination, no {\i rasha}, no bar to accepting the second half of the statement, and {\i peh she-asar} operates in its full form: both halves are believed, and the document is neutralized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of minors and relatives stands differently again. Claiming to have been a minor or a relative when signing does not constitute a claim of having borne false testimony at all. The witnesses in those cases are not saying the underlying transaction was fabricated \u8212- only that their testimony was legally invalid. They are not {\i resha\u8217'im}; they are simply disqualified witnesses. There is therefore no bar from {\i ein adam meisim atzmo rasha} to accepting the second half of their statement, and {\i peh she-asar} functions straightforwardly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers a further objection raised by {\i Rava}. Even granting that two witnesses speaking together constitute testimony \u8212- so that their combined declaration that they were coerced under mortal threat should be treated as live, valid testimony against the document \u8212- there is a fundamental problem: {\i keivan shehigid, shuv eino chozer umagid}. Once a person has testified, he cannot retract his testimony. This principle, rooted in the language of the verse {\i ve\u8217'im lo yagid venasa avono}, establishes that the moment testimony is delivered and is no longer within {\i toch kedei dibbur}, it is irrevocable. How, then, can these witnesses now contradict what they effectively testified to when they signed the document?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testimony Non-Retraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i keivan shehigid shuv eino chozer umagid} is absolute once testimony has been delivered. {\i Rava} raises this as a challenge to {\i Rami bar Chama}\u8217's ruling: even in the case of mortal coercion, once the document has been validated independently, the witnesses\u8217' subsequent oral declaration that it was all fabricated amounts to a retraction of their prior testimony, and such a retraction is legally ineffective. One might have thought that the retraction should be accepted because the original testimony was not delivered orally in court but was embodied in a signed document \u8212- perhaps a document does not carry the same weight as live courtroom testimony. But this is precisely where {\i Reish Lakish}\u8217's ruling intervenes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish on Document\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Reish Lakish} holds: {\i edim ha-chatumim al ha-shtar na\u8217'asu ke-mi she-nichkera edutam be-veit din} \u8212- witnesses who sign a document have the same legal standing as witnesses whose oral testimony has been formally cross-examined in court. The signature on a validated {\i shtar} is equivalent in evidentiary weight to direct courtroom testimony. Once the {\i shtar} has been independently validated, the witnesses cannot retract. The earlier part of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- where the witnesses themselves are the source of validation and are therefore believed to add the qualifying claim \u8212- accordingly applies only to that first scenario, before independent validation. Once the {\i seifa}\u8217's condition is met and the signatures are established through external means, no subsequent claim by the witnesses can dislodge the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Adam Meisim Atzmo Ra\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i ein adam meisim atzmo rasha} \u8212- that a person is not believed, and indeed that the court will not give legal force to testimony, when it would require treating the speaker as a {\i rasha} \u8212- applies specifically to the case of financial coercion. In that case, the witnesses have admitted to having committed {\i edut sheker} for an insufficient reason. Their self-incriminating declaration is inadmissible. The {\i shtar} stands, and the second half of their statement \u8212- the part that would have voided it \u8212- cannot be entered into evidence. This is the structure {\i Rami bar Chama} describes, and it holds for the {\i reisha} of the {\i mishnah} where independent validation has not yet occurred. The combined effect of {\i ein adam meisim atzmo rasha} and the rule of {\i keivan shehigid} means that in the {\i seifa} \u8212- where the document has been externally validated \u8212- the witnesses are never believed to retract their testimony under any circumstances.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Strict View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir: No Self-Disqualifi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i beraita} that records a further stringency. {\i Tanu Rabbanan}: {\i ein ne\u8217'emanim le-foslo} \u8212- they are not believed to disqualify the document \u8212- {\i divrei Rabbi Meir}. The {\i Chachamim} say they are believed. This applies to the {\i reisha} of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the case where the witnesses themselves are the ones validating the {\i shtar} and simultaneously claiming disqualification. The {\i Chachamim} hold that this is a classic {\i peh she-asar}: the same mouth that validated, disqualified. Rabbi Meir does not accept the disqualifying claim even here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumptions Override Cl\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's position requires explanation, since on the surface the two halves of the statement appear perfectly matched: the mouth that bound also released. The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer distinguishes between accepting the {\i peh she-asar} logic in principle and recognizing that the first half of the statement, once accepted, can trigger presumptions strong enough to override the second half. For Rabbi Meir, {\i peh she-asar} does not mean the two halves automatically neutralize each other. Rather, the first half is accepted on its own terms, and the second half must be weighed against whatever it stands against \u8212- including firm halachic presumptions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka: Valid Witnesses\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case where the witnesses claim to have been relatives of a party, the {\i gemara} explains Rabbi Meir\u8217's ruling through the following reasoning: {\i malveh gufeih meidak dayyek u-michtav} \u8212- a creditor who goes to the trouble of having a document written takes care to ensure that the witnesses he uses are valid witnesses. A person protecting his financial interests will not carelessly use relatives as signatories. This is a {\i chazakah}: an established pattern of human behavior. The witnesses\u8217' claim that they were relatives therefore runs directly against this presumption, and the presumption wins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i Reish Lakish} provides the parallel {\i chazakah} regarding minors: {\i ein ha-edim chotmim al ha-shtar ella im ken na\u8217'asa be-gadol} \u8212- witnesses do not sign a document unless it was executed when they were adults. Minors simply do not appear as signatories on formal legal documents. Once we have accepted the first half of the witnesses\u8217' statement \u8212- that the signatures are genuine \u8212- the {\i chazakah} kicks in and tells us that genuine adult signatories means the transaction was executed with adult witnesses. Their subsequent claim that they were minors is overridden by this presumption. The {\i peh she-asar} logic gives the second half of the statement a degree of credibility, but that credibility is not sufficient to overcome an established presumption operating in the other direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coercion Claim Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks about the case of claimed coercion. In the cases of relatives and minors, Rabbi Meir is able to invoke {\i chazakot} \u8212- it simply does not happen that creditors use relatives or minors as witnesses. But coercion is different. Sadly, coercion does happen. People do find themselves forced to sign documents at threat. There is no equivalent {\i chazakah} that coerced signatures are vanishingly rare. So why does Rabbi Meir not believe them when they claim coercion? The answer to this will be taken up in the next discussion \u8212- the {\i gemara} closes here, leaving that thread open for continuation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the overall structure of Rabbi Meir\u8217's position reveals, however, is a particular understanding of {\i peh she-asar}: it does not operate as an automatic mutual cancellation. It means that we grant the second half of the statement a claim to credibility \u8212- and in ordinary circumstances, that credibility is sufficient, which is why the {\i Chachamim} accept it. But Rabbi Meir holds that wherever the second half runs against a strong presumption \u8212- a {\i chazakah} that the disqualifying circumstance simply does not occur \u8212- the presumption takes precedence, and only the first half of the statement is accepted. The document stands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 22\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiyum Shtarot & Judges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peh She\u8217'Asar Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens three lines from the bottom of 21b, with a teaching transmitted in the name of Rabbi Abba, who said it in the name of Rav Huna, who said it in the name of Rav. Before turning to that teaching directly, it is worth noting the broader context. The overarching theme of this section of {\i Ketubot} is {\i peh she-asar hu ha-peh she-hitir} \u8212- the principle that the same mouth which creates a prohibition is believed to remove it. One of the central examples in the {\i mishnah} we have been working through involves witnesses who testify that a signature on a document is their own, but then introduce circumstances that would effectively invalidate it. That discussion has led us into a broader treatment of {\i kiyum shtaros} \u8212- the formal validation of documents by a {\i beis din} \u8212- and the rules governing contradictory testimony. The material we are about to address continues squarely within that world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding, it is worth briefly revisiting a question that arose in the previous session: whether it matters that a text is written before it is accurate, provided the signatories wait until it becomes accurate before signing. The concern is one of {\i me-chazi ke-mezuyaf} \u8212- the appearance of forgery. If the document declares \u8220"the three of us recognize this signature\u8221" but at the time of writing there are not yet three who have concurred, can it be pre-written in anticipation of the eventual signing? One practical echo of this question involves the {\i kesubah}, where some have the custom not to complete the {\i kuf} of {\i ve-kanina} until after the {\i kinyan} has been made, so that the text should not assert something as done that has not yet occurred. And yet, as was pointed out, the rest of the {\i kesubah} is also not yet true at the time of writing \u8212- it attests to a marriage that has not yet taken place, since {\i Ashkenazim} sign before the wedding. What makes the document valid is ultimately its signing, not its writing, and so the preoccupation with that single letter is puzzling. But this is an interesting tension and was the issue left at the end of the previous session.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Judges, Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The teaching of Rabbi Abba in the name of Rav Huna in the name of Rav runs as follows: when three judges convene to validate a document \u8212- {\i sheloshah she-yashvu le-kayem es ha-shtar} \u8212- and a challenge ({\i irer}) arises against one of them questioning his validity, the following distinction applies. {\i Ad she-lo chasmu} \u8212- before they have signed the document \u8212- the other two may testify on behalf of the third. The reasoning is that at this stage they are not {\i nogeia badavar}, not a party to the matter. Their own standing has not yet been formally committed to the document, and so if the third turns out to be invalid, they can simply ask him to step down and bring in a replacement. There is no self-interest compelling their testimony. {\i Vechasim} \u8212- they may then go ahead and sign. But {\i mishechasmu} \u8212- if they have already signed the document \u8212- they can no longer testify on his behalf. At that point, their testimony in support of the third judge is tantamount to testifying in support of their own document, and they are considered {\i nogeia be-eidus}, partial witnesses whose testimony cannot be credited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: what type of challenge are we dealing with? {\i Ei irur de-gazlanusa} \u8212- if the challenge is one of {\i gazlanusa}, meaning that two witnesses are asserting that this third judge is a {\i gazlan}, a thief, and therefore disqualified as a witness and a {\i dayan} \u8212- then the situation is {\i trei u-verei ninhu}, two against two. If two are saying he is a {\i gazlan} and the other two members of the {\i beis din} are countering that he is not, that would ordinarily be a standoff of equal testimony. Why would the second two be credited?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether the word {\i irer} here implies formal testimony at all, or merely a rumor. Other {\i gemaros} assume that {\i irer} refers to actual testimony by two people, and that is the assumption adopted here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giluy Milta Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore turns to a second type of challenge: {\i irur de-pgam mishpacha}, a challenge to his personal status \u8212- for example, a claim that he is a slave, which would disqualify him. In such a case, the {\i gemara} says: {\i giluy milsa be-alma hu}. This is not true testimony in the conventional sense; it is merely disclosure of a matter. The reasoning, as {\i Rashi} explains, is that a person\u8217's lineage and personal status are facts that are ultimately going to become known. They are not the kind of thing that is exclusively within the knowledge of two individuals. Other people know, there are records, the matter will be discovered. Because of this, the two {\i beis din} members who declare him valid are not regarded as offering formal {\i eidus} \u8212- they are merely providing information that is itself susceptible to public corroboration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} articulates this clearly: even after they have signed the document \u8212- {\i mishechasmu} \u8212- why can they not testify that he is not a slave? The concern would be {\i nogeia be-eidus}. But here, he writes, this is a matter that requires investigation and is of the type that will come to light on its own. They are not truly testifying; they are {\i megalei davar be-alma} \u8212- merely disclosing a fact. And therefore the concern about partiality does not apply in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a difficulty worth noting. The principle of {\i giluy milsa be-alma} seems to work symmetrically. Those who are claiming he is a slave are also disclosing a matter of public record \u8212- their claim is equally subject to the logic that it will become known. It is not immediately obvious why this logic tilts the scale in favor of those declaring him valid rather than those declaring him disqualified. {\i Rashi}\u8217's formulation does not fully resolve this asymmetry, and one is left wondering why the doctrine of {\i giluy milsa} gives greater weight to the validators rather than the challengers. The point that emerges from {\i Rashi} is perhaps more modest: the concern of {\i nogeia be-eidus} does not bar their testimony in either direction, since the whole inquiry is not truly {\i eidus} in the formal sense. Whether that fully resolves the asymmetry is less certain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question was raised: if {\i giluy milsa} applies to claims about personal status, why did the {\i gemara} earlier not apply the same logic to witnesses whose signatures appear on a document but who were claimed to be {\i ketanim} at the time of signing? That too is a question of personal status \u8212- how old were they then? \u8212- and one might think it is equally a {\i giluy milsa}. The answer offered is that at the time the document was signed, the question of whether they were of age is a historical question that may no longer be determinable. Now they may be middle-aged; their parents may be gone; the evidence may simply not exist. The {\i giluy milsa} doctrine presupposes that the matter can indeed be disclosed and investigated. If the facts are no longer recoverable, the doctrine loses its force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thief vs.\u160 ?Repentance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the case differently. {\i Le-olam eima lach irur de-gazlanusa} \u8212- in fact, the challenge is one of {\i gazlanusa}, theft. The reason the other two are credited is that they are not contradicting the challengers. Rather, they are saying: {\i yadeinan bei de-avad teshuvah} \u8212- we know that he performed {\i teshuvah}. They concede that he stole, but they add that he has since repented, and therefore his {\i kashrus} as a {\i dayan} has been restored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This opens an important and, sadly, recurring discussion. There are two distinct types of {\i teshuvah}. One is {\i teshuvah} between a person and God \u8212- an internal process of regret and resolve, which may be fully sincere and which one hopes God will accept. But that is not the {\i teshuvah} relevant to restoring a person\u8217's communal standing or halakhic {\i kashrus} as a witness or {\i dayan}. For that, a far more concrete standard applies, as developed at length in {\i Sanhedrin}: restitution of what was taken, and concrete, observable evidence that the person has genuinely changed. Expressions of regret, or returning to public religious observance, are not sufficient. This distinction has obvious relevance whenever questions arise about whether a figure who has acted improperly may be rehabilitated and trusted with a position of authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the present case, the second two witnesses are not contradicting the first two \u8212- they affirm the theft \u8212- but they add the critical fact of {\i aved teshuvah}. The testimonies are therefore not in direct conflict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before vs.\u160 ?After Signing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall principle is restated: {\i ad she-lo chasmu}, before signing, testimony about the third judge\u8217's validity may be heard; {\i mishechasmu}, after signing, it may not, because by then the two signing judges are {\i nogeia be-eidus} in an unambiguous sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Judge Dies Before Signing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Declaration of Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira introduces the next ruling, noting that he had heard it from Rabbi Abba \u8212- and that had Rabbi Abba of Akko not reminded him, he would have forgotten it. The teaching itself echoes the theme we encountered in an earlier {\i sugya} about a witness who had forgotten testimony and was reminded of what he had always known.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is as follows: {\i gimmel she-yashvu le-kayem es ha-shtar} \u8212- three sat together as a {\i beis din} to validate a document \u8212- {\i u-meis echad meihem} \u8212- and one of them died before they signed. The three had already deliberated and reached their conclusion, but only two now remain to sign. The ruling is that they must write explicitly on the document: {\i be-moshav tlasa havina} \u8212- we were a sitting of three \u8212- {\i ve-chad laytohei} \u8212- and one of us is no longer present. The echo of {\i ve-echad einenu} \u8212- \u8220"and one is not\u8221" \u8212- the brothers speaking of Joseph \u8212- is deliberate and striking. The surviving two must note that the third has died, explaining why only two signatures appear. This is necessary because otherwise observers will assume that only two judges validated the document \u8212- which would undermine the requirement that a {\i kiyum ha-shtar} rest on a {\i beis din} of three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Official Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak offers an alternative formulation. If the document contains the phrase {\i shtar denan nafak le-kadmana bei dina} \u8212- \u8220"this document was brought before us, the {\i beis din}\u8221" \u8212- followed by their signatures, that is sufficient. The statement that they were a {\i beis din} is understood to imply they were three, and so no explicit mention of the number is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatzuf Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this: perhaps it was a {\i beis din de-Chatzpa} \u8212- a brazen {\i beis din} of only two? The reference is to Shmuel\u8217's ruling that {\i shnayim she-danu dineihem din} \u8212- if two sat in judgment, their ruling stands after the fact \u8212- except that they are called a {\i beis din chatzuf}, an insolent court. So if Shmuel\u8217's view is that two can validate in principle, perhaps the term {\i beis din} on the document does not guarantee three were present. The mere use of the title may not be enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: the document does not merely say {\i beis din}; it says {\i bei dina de-Rabbana Ashi} \u8212- the {\i beis din} of Rav Ashi, the {\i rav} of Mata Mechasya. That institutional affiliation implies that all proper procedures were observed, and no one would imagine that the official court of Rav Ashi would operate with only two judges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Technical Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} presses further: perhaps even the rabbis of Rav Ashi\u8217's court followed Shmuel\u8217's view and were comfortable with two? The {\i gemara} resolves this by noting that the document contains yet a further phrase: {\i ve-amar lana Rabbana Ashi} \u8212- \u8220"and Rav Ashi commanded us\u8221" to sit as a {\i beis din}. Rav Ashi himself would never have directed a {\i beis din} to sit with fewer than three, and therefore this phrase conclusively establishes that three were present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an irony worth noting: the discussion began by saying that if you write {\i beis din} you need not specify the number three explicitly. By the time it concludes, the required language is quite elaborate \u8212- the institutional affiliation, the invocation of Rav Ashi\u8217's authority, his specific directive \u8212- which, as the {\i gemara} essentially concedes, amounts to proving three were present in a roundabout way. One might as well have simply written that there were three. But this elaborate language reflects what appears to be the actual technical formulation that was in use in the Geonic courts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Peh She\u8217'Asar Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal Statements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having completed the discussion of {\i kiyum shtaros} and the rules of documentary validation, the {\i gemara} now returns to the broader principle of {\i peh she-asar} and its intersection with matters of personal status. The {\i mishnah} presents two cases in which a woman\u8217's own statements determine whether she is permitted or forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Marital Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i ha-ishah she-amra eshet ish hayiti, u-gerushah ani} \u8212- a woman who declared that she was married, and then stated that she is now divorced. She is believed \u8212- {\i ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- since {\i ha-peh she-asar hu ha-peh she-hitir}: the same mouth that made her forbidden to all other men by declaring she was married is the mouth that is now saying she is divorced. However, {\i ve-im yesh eidim she-haitah eshet ish} \u8212- if there are independent witnesses who established that she was married \u8212- {\i ve-hi omeret gerushah ani} \u8212- and she merely claims to be divorced \u8212- {\i einah ne\u8217'emenes}, she is not believed. Once her married status is independently attested, her own word alone cannot dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captive Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case involves a woman taken captive. The general presumption is that a captive woman was violated, which would render a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife forbidden to her husband. Yet if the woman herself says {\i shiviti ve-tehorah ani} \u8212- I was taken captive but was not raped \u8212- she is believed, again on the basis of {\i peh she-asar}: we only know of her captivity through her own words, and therefore her own words are also credited regarding the conditions of that captivity. Again, if there are independent witnesses to the captivity, {\i einah ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- she is no longer believed about the consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then adds: {\i ve-im mi-she-nises bau eidim} \u8212- if, after she married a second man, witnesses came and established that she had been married previously \u8212- {\i harei zu lo tetzei}, she does not have to divorce her current husband. This is a significant ruling, and one that extends the discussion of {\i peh she-asar} into territory with real consequences for an established marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether the captivity case can properly be governed by {\i peh she-asar} at all. After all, the {\i gemara} elsewhere raises the possibility that when there is a strong countervailing presumption \u8212- as there is that a captive woman was violated \u8212- we might not credit the second part of the statement. {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position regarding witnesses who claim they were {\i ketanim} at the time of signing was understood by some as reflecting exactly that: even granting {\i peh she-asar} in principle, a counter-presumption can override the second half of the statement. But the {\i mishnah} here does not adopt that stringency for the captive woman case. The distinction, as {\i Rashi} explains {\i peh she-asar}, is between a case where the person volunteers the information without any prompting \u8212- which is the core of {\i peh she-asar} \u8212- versus {\i migo}, where a person has a strategic alternative available. {\i Tosafot} disagrees and maintains that {\i peh she-asar} can apply even when the person is responding to a challenge, but distinguishes it from {\i migo} in that there is no concrete piece of evidence \u8212- no {\i shor shachut lefanecha} \u8212- that must be explained away. These distinctions matter for determining when a counter-presumption can override the mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source of Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After several {\i daf} of applying {\i peh she-asar} to a range of cases, the {\i gemara} now finally raises the foundational question: {\i minayin le-ha-peh she-asar hu ha-peh she-hitir} \u8212- what is the source of this principle? Perhaps particularly in a case of personal status, where the stakes are an {\i issur} rather than merely a monetary question, we require a stronger basis than mere logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Assi cites a {\i pasuk}: {\i es biti natati la-ish ha-zeh le-isha} \u8212- I gave my daughter to this man as a wife. The reading is: {\i la-ish} \u8212- \u8220"to a man\u8221" \u8212- the father creates a prohibition on his daughter vis-\u224 ?-vis all other men; {\i ha-zeh} \u8212- \u8220"this man\u8221" \u8212- he then specifies who is permitted. So the same mouth that prohibited is the mouth that permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sevara Over Scripture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately pushes back: {\i lamah li kra? sevara hu} \u8212- why do you need a {\i pasuk} for this? It is simple logic. {\i Hu asar, hu hetir} \u8212- he is the one who created the prohibition, he is the one who removes it. If you are going to believe one part of a person\u8217's statement, you should believe the whole of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a point worth noting about the {\i derashah} itself. In the {\i pasuk} about {\i motzi shem ra}, nobody is actually debating whether the daughter was married \u8212- that is conceded. The father is there to defend her honor, not to establish the fact of her marriage. Using this {\i pasuk} to derive that a father\u8217's testimony creates a marital bond is a significant inferential leap from the context in which those words appear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ultimately, the {\i gemara} accepts that {\i peh she-asar} rests on {\i sevara} \u8212- logical reasoning \u8212- not on scriptural necessity. Everything that has been discussed for the past several sessions regarding {\i peh she-asar} is thus grounded in {\i sevara}, which is in keeping with how it has largely been understood throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i peh she-asar} does not require the {\i pasuk}, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i lamah li kra?} What then does the verse teach? {\i Ki itzterich le-chide-Rav Huna} \u8212- it is needed for a separate teaching of Rav Huna. For Rav Huna said in the name of Rav: {\i minayin le-av she-ne\u8217'eman le-esor es bito min ha-Torah} \u8212- how do we know from the Torah that a father is believed to establish that he has married off his daughter, such that she would be treated as an {\i eshet ish} as a result? The answer: {\i shene\u8217'emar, es biti natati la-ish ha-zeh} \u8212- as it says, \u8220"I gave my daughter to this man.\u8221" This, the {\i gemara} holds, establishes that the father\u8217's declaration carries legal weight to create her marital status, quite apart from the {\i peh she-asar} principle itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i ha-zeh lamah li?} \u8212- why is the word {\i hazeh} needed? Now that the {\i pasuk} is used to establish the father\u8217's credibility and not to derive {\i peh she-asar}, what work does {\i hazeh} do? The answer comes from what Rabbi Yona teaches in a {\i baraisa}: {\i es biti natati la-ish ha-zeh} \u8212- to this man, {\i ve-lo le-yavam} \u8212- and not to the brother-in-law. In the context of {\i motzi shem ra}, if it is the brother-in-law who spreads the false report about the woman\u8217's virginity, he is not subject to the laws of {\i motzi shem ra}, because those laws apply specifically to the husband. {\i Hazeh} \u8212- this specific man \u8212- is what distinguishes the husband from others, including the {\i yavam}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Retracting Statements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Retraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns from {\i peh she-asar} to a related but distinct question: can a person retract an earlier statement about personal status if they provide a reasonable explanation? The starting point is a {\i baraisa}: {\i ha-ishah she-amra eshet ish ani ve-chazra ve-amra penuyah ani} \u8212- a woman who went around saying she was married and then changed her statement and said she is single. In principle, {\i einah ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- she is not believed. But {\i im nasna amatla le-devareha} \u8212- if she provides a reasonable explanation for why she originally said she was married \u8212- {\i ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- she is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the very idea that she could be believed to retract. {\i Ve-shavya le-nafshah chaticha de-issura} \u8212- she has made herself a forbidden object. The principle of {\i shavya le-nafshah chaticha de-issura} holds that when someone makes a declaration that renders themselves forbidden, we accept that declaration as binding against them. We accept statements that restrict a person \u8212- we do not accept statements that benefit them \u8212- and therefore she would seem to be locked into her earlier declaration that she was married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amtla Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution comes from Rav, as transmitted by Rav Huna: the key is {\i k\u8217'gon she-nasna amatla le-devareha} \u8212- when she provides a reasonable explanation for her original claim. A person is believed to retract an earlier statement, even a statement that bound them in terms of personal status, if they can explain coherently why they said what they said initially. This is confirmed by a {\i baraisa} that restates the same rule and immediately follows it with a concrete historical illustration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The story is told of a woman {\i gedolah be-noi} \u8212- a woman of exceptional beauty \u8212- who had suitors constantly pressing her for marriage. She began telling them {\i mekudeshes ani} \u8212- I am married. {\i Li-yamim} \u8212- after some time \u8212- {\i amda ve-kidshah atzmah} \u8212- she went ahead and contracted a proper marriage. The {\i chachamim} asked her: what made you do this \u8212- first you said you were married, and now you have married? She replied: {\i be-tchilah she-ba-u elai anashim she-einam hagunim, amarti mekudeshes ani} \u8212- at first, when men who were unsuitable were pressing their suits, I said I was married to deter them. {\i Achshav she-ba-u elai anashim hagunim, kidashti es atzmi} \u8212- now that suitable men came forward, I went ahead and married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder why the proper suitors would even approach a woman who had been telling everyone she was married. Perhaps she had moved, or the right suitors came from elsewhere. In any case, the {\i gemara} does not censure her behavior in the slightest. The {\i halacha} of {\i midvar sheker tirchak} applies in the context of {\i beis din}, not to ordinary interpersonal dealings, and {\i muter lishanot mipnei ha-shalom} is well established. What matters halakhically is whether her explanation is reasonable, and here it clearly is. She is believed. This ruling was presented by Rav Acha Sar Ha-Bira before the {\i chachamim} in Usha, who affirmed: {\i im nasna amatla le-devareha, ne\u8217'emenes}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Retraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel asked Rav the following: {\i amra temei-ah ani ve-chazra ve-amra tehorah ani} \u8212- a woman told her husband she is a {\i niddah}, and then she retracted and said she is actually {\i tehorah}. What is the law? Rav replied: {\i af be-zo, im nasna amatla le-devareha ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- even in this case, if she provides a reasonable explanation, she is believed. Shmuel reportedly asked this forty times and received the same answer forty times.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-afilu hachi lo avad Shmuel uvda be-nafshei} \u8212- and yet, despite this, Shmuel was not prepared to rule on this for himself. {\i Tosafot} quotes a {\i Yerushalmi} that gives the backstory: Shmuel sought to be intimate with his wife, who told him she was a {\i niddah}. The next day she said she was {\i tehorah}. Shmuel pointed out that one cannot change from {\i niddah} to {\i tehorah} in a single day. She explained: {\i etmol lo havi bi cheila ki ha-hi sha-ata} \u8212- yesterday I did not have the strength at that moment. Shmuel then went and asked Rav, who confirmed that with a reasonable explanation she is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is a greater {\i chiddush} than the marriage case in several respects. First, the {\i amatla} here \u8212- exhaustion, not wanting to have relations at that particular time \u8212- requires the woman to have essentially fabricated a {\i niddah} status, which carries significant consequences extending well beyond a single night. Second, the possibility that she genuinely became a {\i niddah} is real \u8212- women sometimes have unexpected breakthrough bleeding \u8212- which introduces grounds for suspicion about the retraction rather than the original declaration. Third, this arises in an ongoing marital context, not a one-time social claim, and so its implications play out differently over time. One can understand why Shmuel resisted applying the ruling to himself, even after hearing it confirmed repeatedly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some sociologists who have studied the practice of {\i hilchos niddah} in traditional communities have noted that the laws can function in an empowering way for women, providing a halakhic basis for saying no to a husband at times when social dynamics would otherwise make refusal very difficult. The {\i sugya} here makes that dimension explicit in a striking way: the {\i gemara} records, without censure, that a woman may use the declaration of {\i niddah} status for that purpose and then retract it the next day if she provides a reasonable explanation. The question of what then happens to the credibility of future {\i niddah} declarations \u8212- and whether that is part of why Shmuel resisted \u8212- is left implicit but is clearly in the background of the discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Contradictory Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Testimony Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now moves to a series of cases involving contradictory testimony about a woman\u8217's status \u8212- specifically, whether her husband has died or whether she has been divorced \u8212- and what the consequences are when witnesses are in conflict. A {\i baraisa} teaches: {\i shnayim omrim meis ve-shnayim omrim lo meis} \u8212- two witnesses say the husband died and two witnesses say he did not die. {\i Harei zu lo tinasei} \u8212- the woman may not marry another man. With contradictory testimony canceling out, she retains her status as a married woman. {\i Ve-im nises, lo tetzei} \u8212- but if she went ahead and married, she does not have to divorce from the second marriage. This is the position of the {\i tanna kamma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Testimony Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Menachem bar Rabbi Yossi adds a parallel case with a different outcome: {\i shnayim omrim nitgarshah ve-shnayim omrim lo nitgarshah} \u8212- two say she was divorced, two say she was not. {\i Harei zu lo tinasei} \u8212- again, she may not marry. But {\i ve-im nises, tetzei} \u8212- if she married, she must divorce from the second marriage. Rabbi Menachem bar Rabbi Yossi himself then clarifies the timing: when he rules that she must leave, he means specifically when the witnesses came and contradicted each other {\i before} she married the second man. If she married first and the contradicting witnesses came afterward, {\i lo tetzei} \u8212- even he would agree she need not divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marrying Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks how the {\i tanna kamma} can rule that if she married she need not divorce, given that the testimony is neutralized and she retains a {\i chezkas eshet ish}. Any man who had relations with her under such circumstances would be liable for an {\i asham talui} \u8212- the suspensive guilt-offering brought for doubtful violations of {\i issur kares} \u8212- and yet we are tolerating the marriage after the fact?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet resolves this: the case is specifically where she married one of the witnesses who testified that the husband had died \u8212- {\i nises le-echad me-eideha}. That witness is saying, in effect: I personally know the husband is dead. From the perspective of the broader community and {\i beis din}, there is a contradiction and a {\i safek}. But from his own perspective, there is no {\i safek}. He knows what he saw.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a broader observation about the limits of {\i beis din} authority. We tend to think of {\i beis din} as overseeing all halakhic matters \u8212- but there is a meaningful distinction here. {\i Beis din} cannot impose {\i malkot} in a case of {\i safek}, and bringing a {\i korban asham} or {\i korban chatat} for personal {\i kapara} is not something {\i beis din} can compel. If the individual says \u8220"I know I am not sinning\u8221" and {\i beis din} cannot demonstrate otherwise \u8212- if all {\i beis din} has is a {\i safek}, not a {\i vaday} to the contrary \u8212- then the matter is between that person and God. {\i Beis din} will not force a divorce in such a case. This stands in contrast to other situations \u8212- such as where a wife has committed adultery and wishes to remain with her husband \u8212- where {\i beis din} does intervene actively to break up the marriage, because there the {\i issur} is objectively established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?One Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a different resolution of the entire {\i baraisa}. He says: {\i targema be-eid echad} \u8212- read the case not as two against two but as one against one. One witness says the husband died; one witness says he did not. Now, by the special rule of {\i Yevamos}, when one witness testifies that a husband died, the {\i rabbonim} gave that witness the weight of two \u8212- {\i heimnuhu rabbanan ke-vei trei}. This is based on the rule of Rabbi Ulla: whenever the Torah or the rabbis believe one witness, that witness is treated as two. So the one who says the husband died carries the weight of two, while the one who says he did not die carries only the weight of one. {\i Ein devarav shel echad bi-mekom shenayim} \u8212- one cannot override two. So, in the death case, one against one effectively becomes two against one in favor of death, and she is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, asks the {\i gemara}, why can she not marry {\i le-chatchila}? If the testimony in favor of death prevails, she should simply be permitted to remarry outright. The {\i gemara} answers by invoking Rabbi Assi\u8217's principle: {\i haser mimcha ikshus peh ve-lazus sefasayim harchek mimcha} \u8212- remove from yourself perversion of speech and keep scandalous rumor far from you. Even if halakhically the testimony resolves in her favor, the appearance of the situation \u8212- one witness against another \u8212- is such that it will generate {\i lashon ha-ra} and suspicion. Therefore, {\i le-chatchila} she should not marry, but {\i bedi-avad} she need not divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the parallel case of {\i gerushin}, however \u8212- one witness says she was divorced, one says she was not \u8212- both witnesses are conceding that she was a married woman. The one who says she was divorced is then just a single witness to the act of divorce, and {\i ein devarav shel echad bi-mekom shenayim}. There is effectively no valid testimony of divorce. In that case, even {\i bedi-avad}, if she married, she must divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?Two Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees with Abaye and maintains that the case is indeed two against two \u8212- {\i terei ve-terei ninhu} \u8212- in both the death case and the divorce case. The difference in outcome must therefore be explained differently. Rava says that Rabbi Menachem bar Rabbi Yossi was persuaded by the argument that {\i bedi-avad} she must leave only in the case of {\i gerushin}, but not in the case of {\i misah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death vs.\u160 ?Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction Rava draws is as follows: {\i misah einah yecholah le-hachchishot} \u8212- death cannot be contradicted. If witnesses say the husband died and the woman remarries on that basis, and the husband then appears alive, she cannot simply say \u8220"I knew he was alive all along.\u8221" The risk she took was total and irreversible. A woman therefore takes tremendous care before acting on testimony of death, and if she does act, we may credit that she had genuine certainty. In the death case, therefore, the {\i tanna kamma} rules that {\i bedi-avad lo tetzei} \u8212- she need not divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i gerushin yecholah le-hachchishot} \u8212- divorce can always be contradicted. If the husband appears and says he never gave a {\i get}, she could claim he is wrong. The risk is far smaller, and so the inclination to act on possibly insufficient testimony is much greater. We therefore do not extend the same degree of trust to her judgment in the {\i gerushin} case, and even {\i bedi-avad}, she must divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: but would she really be so {\i chatzuf} \u8212- so brazen \u8212- as to falsely claim she was divorced with the husband potentially able to deny it? The answer is provided by Rav Hamnuna\u8217's ruling: {\i ha-ishah she-amerah le-va-alah gerashtani, ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- a woman who says to her own husband \u8220"you divorced me\u8221" is believed. Why? {\i Chazakah ein ishah me-izah paneha bifnei ba-alah} \u8212- a woman would not be so brazen as to say this to her husband\u8217's face without it being true. Should this not mean we trust her in the {\i gerushin} case as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i hani mili heicha de-leika sahadei} \u8212- that principle applies only where there are no witnesses at all. {\i Aval heicha de-ika sahadei de-kamsayiah lah} \u8212- but where there are witnesses supporting her claim \u8212- {\i me-aza u-me-aza}, she is quite willing to be brazen. The presence of supporting witnesses is itself what emboldens her, removing the deterrent effect that would otherwise make us trust her. Therefore, when there are two witnesses saying she was divorced alongside two saying she was not, the logic of Rav Hamnuna does not apply, and even {\i bedi-avad} she must divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In sum: the {\i machlokes} between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Menachem bar Rabbi Yossi, and the further split between {\i misah} and {\i gerushin}, resolves as follows. When it is two against two and the testimony concerns death, {\i bedi-avad} she may remain married, because the very gravity of the situation ensures she acted with sufficient confidence. When the testimony concerns a {\i get}, even {\i bedi-avad} she must divorce, because the softness of the risk makes her willingness to act an insufficient guarantee of the truth of her claim.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 23\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Death vs.\u160 ?Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by completing a discussion carried over from the bottom of 22b. Rav Yochanan had distinguished between contradictory witnesses in cases of divorce and cases of death: when witnesses contradict one another regarding whether a woman\u8217's husband died, she may remain in a second marriage contracted in reliance on the original testimony, whereas if the contradictory testimony concerned whether she received a {\i get}, she would be required to leave. The {\i gemara} had been working through several explanations for this asymmetry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Asi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of those explanations involved the notion that in divorce cases, the woman would not have been sufficiently careful before remarrying, because she could always counter her husband\u8217's appearance with a direct claim of having been divorced. Now the {\i gemara} delivers its final answer to close the {\i sugya}. Rav Asi says: {\i kegon de\u8217'amri edim achshav met, achshav gershah} \u8212- the case is where the witnesses specified that the event happened just now, yesterday, this very day. One set says the husband died just now; another set says he was seen alive just now. Or: one set says she was just divorced; another says she was not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the death case, {\i mita lo salka l\u8217'verurah} \u8212- if the witnesses are wrong about the death, there is no further means of verification that specifically undermines their testimony. There is nothing structurally suspicious about the contradiction, and therefore {\i bedi\u8217'eved} \u8212- after the fact, if she has already remarried \u8212- she need not leave. But in the divorce case, {\i gerushin ika l\u8217'verurah}: if she claims she was just divorced yesterday, the obvious question arises \u8212- where is the {\i get}? You haven\u8217't had the {\i get} long enough to lose it. If you claim you lost it since yesterday, that alone raises serious doubts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} makes this explicit: {\i de\u8217'amrinan lah, i ita dehachi hava, achvi lan gitach} \u8212- we say to her, if this really happened, show us your {\i get}. The inability to produce a very recent {\i get} creates an additional layer of suspicion beyond the bare contradiction, and it is this additional factor \u8212- not present in the death case \u8212- that justifies the stricter ruling {\i bedi\u8217'eved} in divorce cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage vs.\u160 ?Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i braita} is introduced. {\i Shenayim omrim nitkadesha u-shenayim omrim lo nitkadesha} \u8212- two witnesses say she was betrothed, two witnesses say she was not. {\i Harei zo lo tinasei} \u8212- she may not marry another man {\i lechatchila}; {\i ve\u8217'im nisset lo tetzei} \u8212- but if she did remarry, she need not leave. Contradictory testimony creates a {\i chumra lechatchila}, but does not undo the second marriage {\i bedi\u8217'eved}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then the second case: {\i shenayim omrim nitgarshah u-shenayim omrim lo nitgarshah} \u8212- two say she was divorced, two say she was not, in a case where her prior married status is already established. Again, {\i lo tinasei} \u8212- she may not remarry. But here, {\i ve\u8217'im nisset tetzei} \u8212- if she did remarry, she must leave. The {\i gemara} at the top of 23a asks: {\i mai shna reisha u-mai shna seifa?} What is the difference between the first and second case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot immediately notes that the answer seems obvious: in the first case, she had a {\i chazakah} of being a {\i penuyah} \u8212- an unmarried woman \u8212- so contradictory testimony about whether she became {\i mekudeshet} leaves that prior status intact, and {\i bedi\u8217'eved} we revert to it. In the second case, her prior {\i chazakah} is that of a married woman \u8212- {\i eishet ish} \u8212- and so {\i bedi\u8217'eved} we revert to that status and require a divorce. The {\i gemara}, however, does not rest content with this answer and instead seeks a deeper analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye says: {\i targuma b\u8217'eid echad} \u8212- understand the case not as two witnesses against two witnesses, but as a single witness against a single witness. This reframing is clarifying. In the first case: {\i eid echad omer nitkadsha, v\u8217'eid echad omer lo nitkadsha} \u8212- one witness says she was betrothed, one says she was not. {\i Travaihu b\u8217'fnuyah kamas\u8217'hadi} \u8212- both witnesses actually share common ground: they agree she started out as a {\i penuyah}. The witness who says she was {\i nitkadesha} is therefore a single witness attempting to alter her established prior status. {\i V\u8217'ein devarav shel echad bimkom shnayim} \u8212- one witness does not prevail against the two-witness standard needed to override a presumed status. Her {\i chazakah} as an unmarried woman therefore holds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case mirrors this precisely: {\i eid echad omer nitgarsha, v\u8217'eid echad omer lo nitgarsha} \u8212- both witnesses agree she began as {\i eishet ish}. The one who claims she was divorced is a single voice trying to change the {\i chazakah} of a married woman, and again, a single witness cannot accomplish that. So the {\i chazakah} of being married prevails, and even {\i bedi\u8217'eved} she must leave the second marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key conceptual move here is that the {\i chazakah} does not simply cancel out contradictory pairs of witnesses \u8212- it defeats the single witness who stands against it. When testimony is presented as two against two, the {\i chazakah} does not independently resolve the contradiction, and the result would be {\i teiku}. But when we reframe it as one against one, the prior status functions as the second voice, and thus the one witness opposing the {\i chazakah} is effectively outnumbered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers a dramatically different reading \u8212- not only reversing which case permits and which forbids, but reconceiving the very structure of the testimony. {\i L\u8217'olam trei u-trei} \u8212- keep it as two witnesses against two, but {\i v\u8217'ipcha} \u8212- reverse the ruling. In the case involving a {\i penuyah} who may have become betrothed, Rav Ashi holds she must leave the second marriage; in the case involving a married woman who may have been divorced, she need not leave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His key move is to recharacterize what the contradicting witnesses are actually saying. {\i Shnayim omrim reinuha she-nitkadsha u-shnayim omrim lo reinuha she-nitkadsha} \u8212- two say \u8220"we saw her get betrothed,\u8221" and two say \u8220"we did not see her get betrothed.\u8221" These are not symmetrical claims. The second pair are not asserting that no betrothal occurred; they are merely reporting their own absence of observation. {\i Lo raiti einah re\u8217'ayah} \u8212- not seeing is not proof. The absence of evidence is not the evidence of absence. The second testimony is therefore effectively null. We are left with only the witnesses who say she was betrothed, and so she must leave the second marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazakah Factor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: if the second witnesses\u8217' testimony is meaningless, what is the {\i chiddush} of their testimony in this case? The answer is the specific circumstance: {\i tzricha d\u8217'dayri b\u8217'chatzer achat} \u8212- all parties lived in the same courtyard. In that situation, one might argue {\i kala it lei lemilta} \u8212- such a matter has a rumor, a communal resonance; people in the same courtyard would surely have heard about a wedding. The second witnesses are therefore not just saying \u8220"we didn\u8217't see it\u8221" but implying \u8220"we were around and heard nothing.\u8221" I might have thought that this argument should carry weight. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- the {\i chiddush} is that it does not, because {\i d\u8217'avdi inshi d\u8217'mikadshi betzina} \u8212- sometimes people get married quietly and privately, without communal notice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic applies to the corresponding {\i seifa}. The two witnesses say \u8220"we did not see her get divorced,\u8221" and again this is not an assertion that no divorce occurred. Even {\i bedi\u8217'eved}, we do not force her to leave on the basis of their silence. The possible {\i chiddush} here is that one might reason differently about divorce than about {\i kiddushin}: perhaps {\i mi ika d\u8217'migarsha kala it lei lemilta} \u8212- a divorce is the sort of thing that always gets talked about, so silence should be more probative. {\i Ka mashma lan d\u8217'avdi inshi d\u8217'mikadshi umigarshi betzina} \u8212- people conduct both marriages and divorces in private, and silence on the matter settles nothing, not even {\i bedi\u8217'eved}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Peh She\u8217'Asar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through those cases, the {\i gemara} returns to the {\i mishnah} itself, which rests on the principle of {\i peh she\u8217'asar hu ha-peh she-hitir} \u8212- the same mouth that bound is the mouth that frees. The {\i mishnah} presented two such cases. In the first, a woman says {\i eshet ish hayiti vegrusha ani} \u8212- I was a married woman and I am now divorced. In the second, {\i shvuya hayiti ve-tehorah ani} \u8212- I was taken captive but I am pure, I was not violated. In both cases, since she herself created the initial problem through her own admission, and since she supplied the resolution in the same breath, she is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i mishnah} then records: if witnesses come and establish the first part of her statement \u8212- corroborating that she was indeed married or indeed taken captive \u8212- without corroborating the second part, then the {\i peh she\u8217'asar} principle no longer applies and she is not believed. However, if she already married before those witnesses arrived, {\i lo tetzei} \u8212- she does not have to leave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now draws out the image of these stages in sequence. She makes her claim; she is {\i muteret}; she remarries on that basis; the witnesses then arrive. Because she acted on a valid {\i heter}, even the arrival of evidence undermining the {\i peh she\u8217'asar} does not undo the marriage {\i bedi\u8217'eved}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oshaya vs.\u160 ?Avin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records a dispute: {\i Rabbi Oshaya matni la reisha} \u8212- Rabbi Oshaya reads the ruling about witnesses arriving post-marriage as applying to the first case, where a woman claims to have been divorced; {\i Rabba bar Avin matni la seifa} \u8212- Rabba bar Avin reads it as applying to the second case, where a woman claims not to have been violated while in captivity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction has substantial consequences. If we say the ruling of {\i lo tetzei} applies even in the first case \u8212- where witnesses establish she had been a married woman and there is now no testimony that she was properly divorced \u8212- we are tolerating the possibility of an ongoing {\i eishet ish} relationship, a biblical prohibition. If we apply it only to the second case, where the concern is {\i shvuya} \u8212- a rabbinic presumption of violation that could bar her from a {\i kohen} \u8212- the leniency is far more palatable, since the original rabbinic concern does not override the fact that she married on a valid {\i heter}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is direct: {\i d\u8217'shvuya hi kilu} \u8212- the prohibition of the {\i shvuya} is rabbinic in origin, resting only on the presumption that we cannot know she was not violated. If we permit her to remain married even in the first case, where biblical adultery is conceivably at stake, then {\i kol sheken seifa} \u8212- certainly we permit it in the lesser case. Conversely, if we hold that the ruling applies only in the {\i shvuya} case, then in the first case \u8212- where she may be a {\i married woman} whose divorce is unestablished \u8212- we would require her to leave even {\i bedi\u8217'eved}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hamnuna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether this dispute tracks the position of Rav Hamnuna. Rav Hamnuna, cited the previous day, held that a woman who claims to her husband\u8217's face that she was divorced is believed \u8212- {\i chazakah ein isha me\u8217'iza paneha bifnei ba\u8217'ala} \u8212- on the presumption that a woman would not be so brazen as to make such a claim in her husband\u8217's presence. The {\i gemara} suggests that Rabbi Oshaya, who permits her to stay married even in the first case despite the arriving witnesses, must be relying on Rav Hamnuna\u8217's principle to supply the credibility her {\i peh she\u8217'asar} would otherwise have lost. And Rabba bar Avin, who does not extend the leniency to that case, would not accept Rav Hamnuna.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} declines this framing, however: {\i d\u8217'chulei alma it lehu d\u8217'Rav Hamnuna} \u8212- everyone may actually agree with Rav Hamnuna. The dispute is instead about the scope of his principle. One view holds that Rav Hamnuna\u8217's reasoning \u8212- that a woman would not fabricate in her husband\u8217's presence \u8212- applies {\i only} when she makes her claim directly before her husband: {\i befanav}. Without his physical presence, {\i me\u8217'iza} \u8212- she is willing to be brazen and lie, and therefore the {\i peh she\u8217'asar} does not carry sufficient weight once witnesses undermine the first half. The other view holds: {\i shelo befanav nami eina me\u8217'iza} \u8212- even when the husband is not physically present, she would not lie so brazenly, because he could always appear and contradict her. The prospect of being caught is itself a sufficient deterrent, and so her claim retains credibility even in his absence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means that in a case where a woman remarried, witnesses then established that she had been married to another man, and no {\i get} has been established \u8212- she may still remain in the second marriage, sustained by the weight of her own claim that she was divorced, even though her husband is not standing there to be confronted. The reasoning invokes also a related principle: {\i ein adam me\u8217'iz panav bifnei ba\u8217'al chovo} \u8212- one does not brazenly lie to the person to whom one owes a relational debt. The analogy to the creditor is apt: it is not merely that one avoids lying to those who know the truth, but that one\u8217's sense of obligation and relational accountability provides a natural brake on fabrication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avuha d\u8217'Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then extends the ruling still further. {\i Amar Avuha d\u8217'Shmuel: lo nisset nisset mamash} \u8212- the father of Shmuel says that \u8220"she remarried\u8221" need not mean she literally remarried. {\i Ela keivan she-hitiruha l\u8217'inases} \u8212- once {\i beit din} has issued a ruling permitting her to remarry, even if she has not yet done so, she retains the benefit of that {\i psak}. The {\i heter} is locked in from the moment of the ruling, not from the moment of the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking and far-reaching {\i chiddush}. Even in a case where witnesses later arrive to establish that the woman had been a married woman with no corroborated {\i get}, the {\i psak} of {\i beit din} issued in reliance on her {\i peh she\u8217'asar} cannot be retracted. The practical implication is pointed: a woman who presents herself to a new {\i beit din}, declares {\i eshet ish hayiti vegrusha ani}, and receives a {\i heter} before any complicating evidence surfaces, is thereafter protected. The {\i psak} cannot be undone even by subsequently arriving witnesses \u8212- provided those witnesses stop short of actually testifying to the disqualifying event itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shvuyah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captive Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is cited: a woman says {\i nishbeiti u-tehorah ani} \u8212- I was taken captive but I was not raped \u8212- {\i ve-yesh li eidim she-tehorah ani}, and there are witnesses who can confirm my purity. One might have thought the appropriate response is to wait for those witnesses to arrive before issuing a ruling. The {\i braita} says otherwise: {\i matirin otah miyad} \u8212- we permit her immediately. The {\i peh she\u8217'asar} applies now; the witnesses need not be awaited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case then develops: the {\i beit din} issues its ruling permitting her to marry; {\i ve-achar kach ba\u8217'u eidim} \u8212- and then witnesses arrive. They confirm she was indeed taken captive, thus undermining the {\i peh she\u8217'asar}. But they add: {\i lo yadanu} \u8212- we cannot testify as to what actually happened during the captivity. We were not always present; we cannot say she was not raped, but neither can we say she was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo yadanu harei zo lo tetzei} \u8212- since these witnesses cannot testify to the disqualifying event, and she had already been granted her {\i heter}, she does not leave the marriage. According to Avuha d\u8217'Shmuel, she would not even need to have acted on the {\i psak} \u8212- the ruling itself locks in the {\i heter}. But {\i ve-im ba\u8217'u edei tumah} \u8212- if witnesses arrive who can positively testify that she was violated \u8212- {\i afilu yesh lah kama banim}, even if she has had many children by the {\i kohen}, she must leave. Where the children are born after the disqualifying fact is established, they would be {\i chalalim}. Hard testimony of the event overrides all prior {\i heteirim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Daughters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents a famous episode. {\i Hani shevuyata d\u8217'atyan l\u8217'Nehardea} \u8212- certain captive women were brought to Nehardea in the process of being ransomed. Avuha d\u8217'Shmuel appointed guards to watch over them during this period, presumably to prevent any further harm while the transaction was being concluded. Shmuel said to him: {\i ve-ad idna man natrinhu} \u8212- who was watching them until now? They were in the hands of their captors. Tosafot understands Shmuel to have meant this as a {\i chumra}: whatever protection you are now arranging, we must already presume that events had occurred during their captivity, rendering any current precautions futile for purposes of halachic status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His father replied: {\i ilu bnatayikh havyon, mi havat mezalzel bekulhai hai} \u8212- if these were your own daughters, you would not be so dismissive of their welfare. The focus shifts from theoretical halachic analysis to the immediate human responsibility of protecting vulnerable women now present in the community. The {\i gemara} notes that this exchange functioned as an inadvertent {\i peh she\u8217'asar} of a different kind \u8212- {\i hava keshigaga sheyotzei milifnei ha-shalit} \u8212- it was like an error that issues from the mouth of a ruler, and {\i va-ishtabiyan bnatei demar Shmuel} \u8212- Shmuel\u8217's own daughters were subsequently taken captive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His daughters were transported to {\i Eretz Yisrael} by their captors. When they were brought to a Jewish town where a ransom might be arranged, they instructed their captors to wait outside. {\i Va-ayilu levei midrasha de-Rabbi Chanina} \u8212- they entered the {\i beit midrash} of Rabbi Chanina. There, each one in turn declared: {\i nishbeiti u-tehorah ani} \u8212- I was taken captive but I am pure. Rabbi Chanina issued the ruling immediately, granting them their {\i heter}. {\i Ve-sof ve-atu shvuyinhu} \u8212- only then did the captors walk in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina recognized the legal elegance of what had occurred: {\i bnan de-moran inun} \u8212- these are the daughters of a great sage. They had understood their grandfather\u8217's principle \u8212- that once a {\i psak} has been issued on the basis of {\i peh she\u8217'asar}, the {\i heter} is not retracted even when the disqualifying facts become publicly apparent \u8212- and they had acted on it with precision. By making their declaration and receiving a ruling before the captors entered the room, the visual presence of those captors, which might otherwise function as evidence equivalent to testimony that they had been captured, could not undo a {\i psak} already granted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina then said to Rabbi Shemen bar Abba: {\i puk itapel bikrovotaich} \u8212- go take care of your relatives, indicating that Rabbi Shemen bar Abba was somehow related to them and implicitly suggesting a marital arrangement. Rabbi Shemen bar Abba hesitated, noting: {\i ve-ika edei bimdinat ha-yam} \u8212- are there not witnesses overseas? The concern was that witnesses to the actual violation might exist elsewhere. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i hashta mihat leitnehu kaman} \u8212- right now they are not before us. {\i Edei betzad astan ve-te\u8217'aser?} Witnesses on the other side of the world render her forbidden?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captors Appear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses: {\i ta\u8217'ama de-la atu edei} \u8212- the reason things are permitted is only because the witnesses have not yet arrived. {\i Ha atu edei, mitsara} \u8212- if witnesses did come, they would become forbidden. But this creates tension with Avuha d\u8217'Shmuel\u8217's ruling: {\i keivan she-hitiruha shuv lo te\u8217'aser} \u8212- once the {\i heter} was issued it cannot be revoked. The {\i gemara} resolves the tension by noting that the {\i heter} is indeed not revocable \u8212- and the story of Shmuel\u8217's daughters itself bears this out, since the captors\u8217' very presence in the room after the fact did not undo the ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi clarifies the one exception: {\i edei tumah itmar} \u8212- the concern about witnesses refers to witnesses who can testify to the rape itself, not merely to the captivity. It is only such testimony \u8212- direct evidence of the disqualifying act \u8212- that could override the {\i heter}. Witnesses to captivity alone cannot, since the {\i heter} was built on the {\i peh she\u8217'asar} and the {\i shvuya} concern is rabbinic. The {\i heter} was properly issued; it holds. Only witnesses to actual violation are in a different category, and that concern had been established from the {\i braita} earlier: {\i ve-im ba\u8217'u edei tumah, afilu yesh lah kama banim, tetzei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader principle that emerges from this entire discussion is substantial. Even in a case involving a {\i shvuya} \u8212- and, according to some readings, even in the more serious case of {\i eishet ish} \u8212- once a {\i psak} has been issued on the basis of a valid {\i peh she\u8217'asar}, the {\i heter} is locked in and survives the subsequent arrival of witnesses to the captivity. Only witnesses who testify to the disqualifying act itself \u8212- actual violation or actual continued marriage \u8212- can undo what has been established.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Women Captive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self vs.\u160 ?Other\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to 23b, the {\i gemara} introduces a {\i braita} about two women who were both taken captive. {\i Zo omeret nishbeti u-tehorah ani} \u8212- this one says I was captive and I am pure; {\i ve-zo omeret nishbeti u-tehorah ani} \u8212- and so does the other. The presumption here is that there are witnesses establishing that they were taken captive \u8212- {\i shenishbu} \u8212- so their own testimony about themselves is discounted: {\i einan ne\u8217'emanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i bizman she-hen me\u8217'idot zo et zo \u8212- harei elu ne\u8217'emanot} \u8212- when each woman testifies about the other\u8217's purity, they are believed. This relies on the principle, already established, that {\i edut ishah} \u8212- even a woman\u8217's testimony \u8212- is credited in cases of {\i shvuya} when she testifies about someone other than herself. The {\i gemara} acknowledges the concern of {\i gomlin} \u8212- reciprocity \u8212- that one might suspect the two women struck an arrangement to testify for each other. Nevertheless, their cross-testimony is accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parsing Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A subsequent {\i braita} elaborates four cases involving compound statements. First: {\i ani tme\u8217'ah ve-chaverti tehorah} \u8212- I am impure and my friend is pure. {\i Ne\u8217'emenet} \u8212- she is believed; there is nothing problematic about testifying unfavorably about oneself and favorably about another. Second: {\i ani tehorah ve-chaverti tme\u8217'ah} \u8212- I am pure and my friend is impure. {\i Einah ne\u8217'emenet} \u8212- she is not believed, neither about herself (witnesses establish she was captured) nor about making her friend\u8217's situation worse. Third: {\i ani ve-chaverti tme\u8217'ah} \u8212- both of us are impure. {\i Ne\u8217'emenet al atzmah, ve-einah ne\u8217'emenet al chavertah} \u8212- she is believed about herself if she wishes to declare herself forbidden; she is not believed to make her friend forbidden. Fourth: {\i ani ve-chaverti tehorah} \u8212- both of us are pure. {\i Ne\u8217'emenet al chavertah ve-einah ne\u8217'emenet al atzmah} \u8212- she is believed about her friend but not about herself, since witnesses have established her captivity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately notes a structural inconsistency. If the presence of witnesses explains why she is not believed about herself in the first case and the last, then in the middle case \u8212- where she says her friend is impure and the ruling is that her friend is nevertheless presumed pure \u8212- witnesses about the friend would already render the friend presumptively impure. Why would her testimony about the friend be dismissed as if the friend\u8217's status is unaffected? This can only make sense if, in the middle case, there are {\i no} witnesses. But then how can the first and last cases, which follow directly in the same {\i braita}, assume the presence of witnesses?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye accepts the inconsistency and says plainly: {\i reisha ve-seifa d\u8217'ika edim, metzi\u8217'asa d\u8217'leika edim} \u8212- the first and last cases involve witnesses, the middle case does not. The {\i braita} simply shifts between scenarios without marking the shift explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa offers a more unified reading. {\i Kula d\u8217'ika edim} \u8212- the entire {\i braita} concerns a case where there are witnesses. But he introduces an additional element: {\i ve-ika ed echad d\u8217'apich} \u8212- there is also a single witness whose testimony runs contrary to what the woman is saying about either herself or her friend. This new factor resolves the internal tension without requiring the middle case to lack witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purity Wins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analysis of Rav Pappa\u8217's cases generates a major {\i chiddush}. Take the first scenario under his reading: the woman says {\i ani tme\u8217'ah ve-chaverti tehorah} \u8212- I am impure, my friend is pure \u8212- and a single witness says the opposite: {\i at tehorah ve-chavertach tme\u8217'ah} \u8212- you are pure and your friend is impure. For her own status: {\i ihi shavi l\u8217'nafshah chaticha d\u8217'issura} \u8212- she can render herself forbidden by her own word, even against a witness\u8217's testimony to her purity. For her friend: the woman says the friend is {\i tehorah} and the witness says {\i tme\u8217'ah} \u8212- and the ruling is that the friend is permitted, {\i ve-chavertah mishtraya apumah d\u8217'didah}, by the woman\u8217's testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is striking. Even though a specific {\i eid echad} has testified that the friend was violated \u8212- not merely that we are uncertain about what happened during captivity, but that this witness actively says the friend was raped \u8212- we nevertheless credit the single voice claiming {\i tehorah} over the single voice claiming {\i tme\u8217'ah}. The principle that emerges is that in a case of one witness against one witness regarding the purity of a captive woman, we always follow the voice saying {\i tehorah}. This parallels the principle established just days earlier regarding contradictory testimony about a husband\u8217's death: when one witness says he died and one says he did not, we follow the testimony that permits the woman to remarry. Here, in the captive-woman context, when one witness says she was violated and one says she was not, we follow the testimony of purity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocal Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa then works through the reverse and combined scenarios, and the {\i gemara} challenges each additional case as redundant. The case of {\i ani tehorah ve-chaverti tme\u8217'ah} \u8212- I am pure, my friend is impure \u8212- with a witness asserting the opposite is challenged as already established by the previous case. The {\i chiddush} of this case, however, is the following: one might have thought that when the woman says her friend is {\i tme\u8217'ah}, and a witness says the friend is {\i tehorah}, perhaps the witness should be disbelieved because the woman\u8217's self-damaging claim about herself \u8212- simultaneously asserting her own impurity \u8212- lends special credibility to her testimony overall. The {\i gemara} says: even so, we follow the witness who says {\i tehorah} for the friend.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final case introduced is {\i ani ve-chaverti tme\u8217'ah} \u8212- both of us are impure \u8212- with a witness saying {\i at ve-chavertach tehorot} \u8212- both of you are pure. One might have thought that since the witness exculpates both of them, and the woman may simply be seeking to bring her friend down with her \u8212- invoking the {\i tamus nafshi im plishtim} principle, being willing to harm oneself in order to harm another \u8212- we should not apply {\i shavya l\u8217'nafshah} and should not make the woman herself forbidden on her own word. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- the ruling teaches that a person who declares herself forbidden is bound by it, even when a plausible hostile motive could explain the statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cumulative {\i chiddush} that has emerged by the end of this discussion is the following: the {\i mishnah} had already established that a single witness \u8212- even a woman \u8212- is credited when testifying about {\i another} captive woman\u8217's purity. What Rav Pappa\u8217's elaboration now teaches is that this credibility holds even against a directly contradicting {\i eid echad} who testifies to the violation. As long as it is one voice against one voice, we invariably credit the testimony of purity when it concerns someone other than the testifying party herself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 24\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Captive Women & Single W\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captive Women Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by picking up from the {\i mishna} on the bottom of 23b, which addressed two women who had been taken captive. The fundamental {\i chiddush} established there is that although each woman is not believed to testify about her own status \u8212- if witnesses already established that she was taken captive \u8212- she is believed to testify about her fellow captive. This holds even where there exists a possible concern of reciprocity, a concern the {\i Gemara} will make explicit in the course of today\u8217's discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Witness Despite R\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the mutual testimony of the two women, the {\i mishna} also establishes a broader principle regarding {\i ed echad}: even a single witness is believed in matters relating to captivity. Moreover, the {\i Gemara} indicated that even in a case of one witness against one \u8212- where one {\i ed} says she was violated and another says she was not \u8212- we follow the witness who says she was not violated, even if that witness would normally occupy a weaker evidentiary position under standard {\i halacha}, as in the case where the exonerating witness is a woman testifying against a man\u8217's claim. This represents a particular leniency, parallel to principles encountered in Yevamos, in extending credibility to the {\i ed} who exonerates. Further implications of this principle will emerge as the daf proceeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohanim Witness Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim Testifying Mutual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the next {\i mishna}, which presents a parallel case in a different domain. Two men each claim to be a {\i kohen} \u8212- {\i zeh omer kohen ani v\u8217'zeh omer kohen ani}. Standing alone, neither man is believed about himself in the absence of an established {\i chazaka} that he is a {\i kohen}, and therefore neither would be given {\i terumah} at the threshing floor. However, when each testifies about the other \u8212- each saying his fellow is a {\i kohen} \u8212- they are believed, and {\i terumah} is given to both. This is the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishna} on two levels: first, that {\i ed echad} suffices to establish {\i kehuna} for purposes of {\i terumah}; second, that this is accepted even where there is a concern about collusion or reciprocity between the two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} dissents: {\i ein ma\u8217'alin l\u8217'kehunah al pi ed echad} \u8212- {\i kehuna} status may not be established on the basis of a single witness. This is a categorical position that will be examined against what appears to be a contradictory ruling of his elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Conditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} qualifies: when is it true that a single witness is insufficient? Only {\i bimkom she\u8217'yesh orerim} \u8212- where a concrete challenge has been raised against the person\u8217's {\i kehuna} status. But where there is no such concrete challenge, {\i ma\u8217'alin l\u8217'kehunah al pi ed echad} \u8212- one witness suffices to establish {\i kehuna}. The {\i Gemara} will later note that {\i Rabbi Eliezer} may also decline to accept the specific case of the {\i tanna kamma} \u8212- the case where two men testify about one another and reciprocity is a concern \u8212- and may only accept {\i ed echad} in the standard, uncontested case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} says, citing {\i Rabbi Shimon ben HaSgan}: {\i ma\u8217'alin l\u8217'kehunah al pi ed echad} \u8212- a single witness suffices to establish {\i kehuna} status. The difficulty is that his position is not immediately distinguishable from the preceding opinions. The {\i tanna kamma} permits even the case of mutual testimony despite the concern of collusion; {\i Rabbi Eliezer} permits {\i ed echad} where there is no concrete challenge; {\i Rabbi Yehuda} forbids {\i ed echad} entirely; and {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} restates the permissive view without specifying how he diverges from either camp. The {\i Gemara} will work through these distinctions in the course of the analysis that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Peh She\u8217'Asar Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Cases Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i kol hanei lama li} \u8212- why are all of these {\i mishnayyot} necessary? The reference is to the series of cases across the preceding {\i mishnayyot} involving {\i ha\u8217'peh she\u8217'asar hu ha\u8217'peh she\u8217'hitir}, as well as the subsequent {\i mishnayyot} about {\i ne\u8217'emanut} without a {\i peh she\u8217'asar} component \u8212- namely, trusting {\i ed echad} regarding {\i shvuya} and trusting {\i ed echad} regarding {\i kohen} status. The {\i Gemara} proceeds to explain why each case is independently necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case in the {\i perek} \u8212- where {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} agrees that the man who says \u8220"this field was your father\u8217's and I purchased it from you\u8221" is believed \u8212- is accepted because {\i de\u8217'ika drara demamona}, there is a {\i drara demamona} present. The {\i Gemara} relies on this concept to explain the conditions under which {\i peh she\u8217'asar} operates, and it is precisely around this term that a significant dispute between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} emerges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drara Definition Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} explains {\i drara demamona} as meaning loss of money \u8212- {\i hefsed mamon}. The significance for {\i Rashi} is that because the first half of the man\u8217's statement would cause him to lose the field if left unqualified, it is manifest that the second part of the statement \u8212- \u8220"but I purchased it from him\u8221" \u8212- is intended as part of the same continuous utterance. This bears on the implicit requirement, never stated explicitly by the {\i Gemara} but understood by the {\i Rishonim}, that the two halves of the {\i peh she\u8217'asar} statement must constitute a single statement, perhaps even {\i toch k\u8217'dei dibbur} of one another. When money is at stake, {\i Rashi} argues, it is clear that the qualifying clause is organically linked to the initial admission, eliminating any concern that the admission was made at one time and the qualification asserted separately later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} understands {\i drara demamona} differently: it means a {\i shaychus l\u8217'mamon}, a connection to money, rather than an actual loss. The simpler point, in {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reading, is that the man is already in possession of the field, and it is therefore more readily understandable that we would apply {\i peh she\u8217'asar} \u8212- he is already physically present and connected to the subject matter of his claim. The {\i Rosh} also notes that {\i Rashi} seems to read {\i demuchzak basadeh} into the term. In any case, the dispute between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} revolves around whether the operative concept is {\i hefsed mamon} or {\i shaychus mamon}. The {\i Gemara} does not resolve this explicitly, and it has remained a subject of debate among the {\i Rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captivity Case Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then explains why the case of {\i edim} \u8212- witnesses who say \u8220"yes, we signed this document, but we were {\i ketanim}\u8221" or \u8220"{\i anusim}\u8221" or \u8220"{\i krovim}\u8221" \u8212- was needed alongside the case of the field. In the {\i edim} case there is no {\i drara demamona} in {\i Rashi}\u8217's sense: the money has not changed hands, and the witnesses themselves are not the ones at risk of losing anything. One might have thought that {\i peh she\u8217'asar} would not apply there. Conversely, had only the case of the {\i edim} been taught, one might have thought that witnesses are believed only because they are helping someone else \u8212- the {\i loveh} \u8212- and the second half of their statement is therefore more readily credited. But in the case of the man claiming to have purchased the field, the second part of his statement benefits himself \u8212- {\i le\u8217'nafshei} \u8212- and one might have argued that {\i peh she\u8217'asar} should not apply there. Hence both cases are needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting, as {\i Tosafot} points out, that the case of the {\i edim} carried its own independent {\i chiddushim}: once their statement triggered the validity of the {\i shtar} in the first half, one might argue they cannot then be believed to undermine the {\i shtar} with the second half of their statement. Both elements \u8212- the field case and the {\i edim} case \u8212- were therefore required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} further explains: having established {\i peh she\u8217'asar} in monetary contexts, one might still have wondered whether it would apply in a case involving {\i issur} \u8212- such as a woman who says \u8220"I was married and then divorced.\u8221" The weight of that {\i issur ve\u8217'hetter} is more severe, and one might say we would not accept the second half of the statement in so grave a matter. Thus the {\i mishna} about the divorced woman was needed as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Captivity Case Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gomlin Concern Address\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: why was the case of {\i shtei nashim she\u8217'nishbu} \u8212- two captive women testifying about one another \u8212- necessary? The {\i mishna} about captivity had already appeared in the context of {\i peh she\u8217'asar}. The {\i Gemara} answers that the unique {\i chiddush} of the two-women case is the ruling that we are not concerned about {\i gomlin} \u8212- reciprocity. The concern would be that they have colluded: I will say you were not violated if you say the same of me. The {\i mishna} teaches {\i ka mashma lan} that we do not suspect {\i gomlin} in this context, which is a {\i chiddush} beyond the mere principle of believing {\i ed echad} or a woman\u8217's testimony in captivity matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim Case Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why was the case of two men each claiming {\i kehuna} needed? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i mishum d\u8217'kaba\u8217'i le\u8217'misni plugta de-Rabbi Yehuda ve-Rabbanan} \u8212- because the {\i Gemara} wanted to record the dispute between {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and the {\i Rabbanan}. Even if one accepted that {\i ed echad} suffices for {\i shvuya}, it does not automatically follow that {\i ed echad} suffices for {\i kehuna} status. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} is stricter by {\i kohen} than by {\i shvuya}, and this divergence requires explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kehuna Special Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two reasons are offered for why {\i kehuna} may warrant greater scrutiny than {\i shvuya}. First, by {\i shvuya} the principle of {\i shvuya hekilu} applies \u8212- the Rabbis were specially lenient regarding a captive woman, and {\i Rabbi Yehuda} himself accepts that leniency. The case of {\i kehuna} does not benefit from any such blanket rabbinic dispensation. Second, and more consequentially, allowing a man to eat {\i terumah} has downstream effects. People will come to treat him as a fully established {\i kohen}, and that status will be translated into other domains: into {\i yuchsin} considerations, into questions about whom he may marry, and potentially into his eligibility to serve in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. The {\i kehuna} context therefore carries a kind of ripple effect that makes it qualitatively different from the {\i shvuya} context, and this justifies greater caution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Donkey Drivers Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Quality Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a {\i baraita} that appears to present a striking reversal of the positions of {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and the {\i Rabbanan}. The case involves {\i chamarim} \u8212- merchants who travel from city to city with their goods loaded on donkeys \u8212- who enter a town. One of them declares: \u8220"My grain is {\i chadash} and my friend\u8217's is {\i yashan}; my grain has not had {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} taken, while my friend\u8217's has.\u8221" In other words, he testifies entirely in his friend\u8217's favor on all relevant counts: the friend\u8217's grain is of better quality and is halachically prepared for use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the question of {\i chadash} and {\i yashan}, both {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} conclude that the reference is not to the halachic {\i issur} of eating {\i chadash} grain before the {\i omer} \u8212- because the {\i Gemara} will establish shortly that we are dealing with {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} concerns here. Rather, {\i chadash} and {\i yashan} refer in a general sense to the quality of the grain: old, dried grain is better, much as old wine is considered superior, while fresher grain has not yet dried sufficiently. The {\i metukkan} component \u8212- the grain from which {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} have been taken \u8212- is the direct halachic concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collusion Concern Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with accepting such testimony is obvious: the concern about collusion operates differently here than in the {\i mishna}\u8217's case. In the {\i mishna}, the two men are simultaneously before us and each testifies about the other. Here, however, the merchant testifies in one city, and the implicit arrangement is that in the next city his friend will reciprocate, each gaining by the exchange across different locations and different audiences. This presents a particularly stark concern about collusion, and the {\i baraita} records a dispute: one opinion holds he is {\i eino ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- not believed \u8212- and {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds he is {\i ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This seems to flatly reverse the positions as we know them: in our {\i mishna}, {\i Rabbi Yehuda} was the one who did not believe, while the {\i Rabbanan} were more lenient. Here, {\i Rabbi Yehuda} is the lenient one and the unnamed {\i tanna} takes the stricter view. Everybody\u8217's positions appear reversed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversed Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Adda bar Ahava} says, citing {\i Rav}: {\i muchlefet hashita} \u8212- the attributions in the {\i baraita} are switched. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} should be read as saying {\i eino ne\u8217'eman}, and the {\i Rabbanan} should be read as saying {\i ne\u8217'eman}, which would be consistent with their positions in the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demai & Shvuya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} disagrees: {\i l\u8217'olam lo teihafuch} \u8212- there is no need to reverse the attributions. The positions can be reconciled if we attend to the specific subject matter of each case. The {\i baraita} concerns {\i demai} \u8212- grain purchased from an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- and {\i b\u8217'demai heikeilu}: the Rabbis were lenient regarding {\i demai}. The halachic reality is that most {\i amei ha\u8217'aretz} do in fact tithe their grain, and the concern about {\i demai} is only rabbinic in character. Just as we observed that {\i shvuya hekilu} \u8212- the Rabbis were lenient regarding a captive woman \u8212- so too {\i d\u8217'mai hekilu}: {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, who is strict by {\i kohen} status because of the weighty ramifications, is prepared to be lenient regarding {\i demai} because the only thing at stake is a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} concern, and there the stakes do not justify the same caution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} then presses the question: even if {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position is coherent in this framework, the {\i Rabbanan} are now the ones who appear contradictory \u8212- lenient toward {\i kohen} status in the {\i mishna} and stricter about {\i demai} in the {\i baraita}. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i d\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda a\u8217'd\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda lo kashya k\u8217'de\u8217'shaninan} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position is consistent for the reason just stated. As for the {\i Rabbanan}, their apparent inconsistency is resolved by invoking the principle that {\i Rabbi Chama bar Ukva} stated: {\i b\u8217'khlei uminuto b\u8217'yado} \u8212- when the instruments of his profession are in his hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Klei Umanto Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Potter\u8217's Vessels Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} traces the origin of the principle of {\i khlei uminuto b\u8217'yado} to a case involving a potter who leaves his jugs and pitchers unattended while he goes to drink water. The question is whether an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} may have touched them and rendered them {\i tamei}. There are three {\i baraitot} on this question, and {\i Rabbi Chama bar Ukva}\u8217's statement is the key to reconciling them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Store Open/Closed Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i baraita} holds that the innermost vessels \u8212- those not exposed to the passing public \u8212- are {\i tahor}, while the outermost ones are {\i tamei}. A second {\i baraita} holds that everything, even the innermost vessels, is {\i tamei}. A third {\i baraita} holds the opposite: everything is {\i tahor}, including the outermost ones. {\i Rabbi Chama bar Ukva} resolves the contradiction between the first two {\i baraitot}: when {\i khlei uminuto b\u8217'yado} \u8212- when the store is open for business and his instruments are visibly deployed \u8212- then {\i mipnei she\u8217'yad hakol memash\u8217'mesh bahen}, since everyone will be handling and inspecting the wares, even the innermost vessels are {\i tamei}, yielding the second {\i baraita}. When the store is not open for business, only the outermost vessels that are directly exposed present a concern, yielding the first {\i baraita}. When the instruments of the trade are entirely absent and the setup gives no indication of a store open for business, everything remains {\i tahor}, yielding the third {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edge Alignment Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This still leaves a question: what is the case of the first {\i baraita}, where the outer vessels are {\i tamei} and the inner ones are {\i tahor}? If the store is open, everything is {\i tamei}; if the store is closed, everything is {\i tahor}. {\i D\u8217'smicha l\u8217'reshut harabbim} \u8212- the answer is a case where the store is not yet open for business, but the vessels are arranged right at the edge of the public domain. Even without the full display of open commerce, the outermost vessels are pressed up against the {\i reshut harabbim}, and {\i u\u8217'mishum chifufei reshut harabbim} \u8212- because of the incidental rubbing of passersby in the public domain \u8212- people will inadvertently touch them even without intending to inspect them. The inner vessels, shielded from this incidental contact, remain {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application to the {\i baraita} about the donkey drivers follows: when {\i khlei uminuto b\u8217'yado}, when the merchant has his scales and commercial equipment visibly deployed, it is apparent that he is preparing to sell his grain in the next city. In that circumstance, the concern about reciprocal collusion is heightened and credibility is withheld. {\i Tosafot} reads the application of {\i khlei uminuto b\u8217'yado} in the opposite direction \u8212- as explaining why we do believe the {\i kohen} in the {\i mishna}\u8217's case: the {\i kohen} carries the vessels and apparatus associated with {\i kehuna} practice, which independently serves as circumstantial evidence of his status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah to Yichus Transl\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elevation Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the central issue raised toward the end of the discussion of the {\i kohen} case: {\i ma\u8217'alin mitrumah leyuchsin} \u8212- whether eating {\i terumah} halachically translates into establishing {\i yichus}. The underlying point is that {\i yichus} carries the highest evidentiary standard. If eating {\i terumah} is halachically treated as establishing full {\i kohen} status for {\i yichus} purposes as well, then one cannot allow {\i ed echad} to permit {\i terumah} while maintaining a stricter standard for {\i yichus}, because the two have been collapsed into a single status. Conversely, if we hold {\i ein ma\u8217'alin mitrumah leyuchsin} \u8212- that eating {\i terumah} does not halachically translate into {\i yichus} \u8212- the two standards remain distinct and can be applied independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related {\i iba\u8217'ia} is now posed: {\i mahu le\u8217'haalos mishtaros leyuchsin} \u8212- can a document establish {\i yichus} status? The {\i Gemara} first considers a case where the person himself is identified as a {\i kohen} among the signatories: {\i ani ploni Kohen chasamti eid}. This is rejected immediately \u8212- {\i man kamsahid lei} \u8212- he is merely testifying about himself and his self-identification carries no evidentiary weight for {\i yichus} purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore reformulates the case: a loan document in which the borrower is identified as {\i ploni Kohen} \u8212- {\i ani ploni Kohen lavisi maneh miploni} \u8212- and which bears the signatures of witnesses at the bottom. Does the witnesses\u8217' signature constitute testimony that all the contents of the document are accurate, including the borrower\u8217's identification as a {\i kohen}? The {\i Gemara} raises two possibilities. On one hand, {\i a\u8217'maneh shebashtar kamsahadei} \u8212- the witnesses are testifying only to the loan itself, not to incidental details of nomenclature; they sign to confirm a transaction took place and should not be understood to be corroborating every description in the document. On the other hand, perhaps {\i achula milsa kamsahadei} \u8212- once witnesses affix their signatures, they are treated as testifying to all aspects of the document\u8217's contents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna} and {\i Rav Chisda} dispute this: one holds {\i ma\u8217'alin} \u8212- the document suffices to establish {\i yichus}, and one holds {\i ein ma\u8217'alin}. This question has direct practical relevance for tractate Ketubot, where documents such as a {\i ketuba} may identify a woman as a {\i gerusha} or {\i giyores}, carrying implications for whether she may marry a {\i kohen}. The sensitivity around these identifications has given rise to the practice of using subtle formulations \u8212- {\i matrachta} instead of {\i besulta}, or {\i giyorta} rather than {\i besulta} \u8212- so that personal status is indicated without being read aloud.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duchaning Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i iba\u8217'ia} is raised: {\i mahu le\u8217'haalos mi\u8217'nesiat kapayim leyuchsin} \u8212- if someone performs {\i duchaning}, may that serve as sufficient evidence to establish {\i yichus} status? The {\i Gemara} notes that this question can be asked regardless of one\u8217's position on whether {\i terumah} translates into {\i yichus}, because {\i nesiat kapayim} may be distinct from {\i terumah} in ways that cut in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public vs Private Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even according to the view that eating {\i terumah} translates into {\i yichus}, one might argue that {\i nesiat kapayim} does not. Why? {\i Hani mili terumah, de\u8217'avon mita hi} \u8212- eating {\i terumah} is a capital offence for a non-{\i kohen} who does so knowingly, and therefore a man would not eat {\i terumah} unless he genuinely believed himself to be a {\i kohen}. By contrast, a non-{\i kohen} who performs {\i duchaning} transgresses only a positive commandment \u8212- {\i koh sevarchu et bnei Yisrael} is directed only at {\i kohanim} \u8212- and the {\i issur asei} is considerably lighter. One might therefore conclude that a person would be less deterred from performing {\i duchaning} improperly, making it a weaker indicator of genuine {\i kehuna} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, even according to the view that eating {\i terumah} does not translate into {\i yichus}, one might argue that {\i nesiat kapayim} does. The reason is that {\i terumah de\u8217'mitachla b\u8217'tzina\u8217'} \u8212- {\i terumah} is eaten privately, without public communal recognition. A person might eat {\i terumah} given to him by a few sympathetic farmers without the broader community acknowledging him as a {\i kohen}. But {\i nesiat kapayim defarhesya} \u8212- {\i duchaning} takes place publicly, before the entire congregation. {\i Ilav Kohen hu, kulei hai lo machtzif inish lenafshai} \u8212- if a person were not truly a {\i kohen}, he would not have the {\i chutzpa} to ascend the {\i duchan} before the entire community and pronounce the {\i birkat kohanim}. The public nature of the act, combined with the community\u8217's implicit acquiescence, gives it greater evidentiary weight than the private eating of {\i terumah}. {\i Rav Chisda} and {\i Rav Avina} dispute this question as well, one holding {\i ma\u8217'alin} and the other holding {\i ein ma\u8217'alin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohanim Return & Chazaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exile Return Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak} posed the question of {\i nesiat kapayim} directly to {\i Rava}: {\i mahu le\u8217'haalos mi\u8217'nesiat kapayim leyuchsin}? {\i Rava} replied that it is the subject of the dispute between {\i Rav Chisda} and {\i Rav Avina}. {\i Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak} pressed: what is the practical {\i halacha}? {\i Rava} responded that what he knew was a {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yosei} says: {\i gedola chazaka} \u8212- the principle of {\i chazaka} is powerful, as demonstrated by the following {\i pasuk} from {\i Ezra}. The {\i pasuk} describes {\i kohanim} who returned from exile and who could not produce genealogical documentation: {\i u\u8217'mibnei hakohanim bnei Chavaya ben Hakotz bnei Barzillai} \u8212- this family had intermarried into the non-priestly family of {\i Barzillai HaGiladi}, taking their name, and when they sought their genealogical records \u8212- {\i eileh bikshu kesavam hamityachasim} \u8212- they could not be found. {\i Vayigalu min hakehuna} \u8212- they were removed from the {\i kehuna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nechemia\u8217's Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i HaTirshatha} \u8212- identified by {\i Rashi} as {\i Nechemia} \u8212- then addressed them: {\i harei atem b\u8217'chezkatchem}, you retain your presumptive status. He asked: what were you eating in exile? {\i B\u8217'kodshei gvul} \u8212- {\i terumah}, the {\i kodshim} eaten outside the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. Very well, {\i af kan b\u8217'kodshei gvul} \u8212- you may continue eating {\i terumah}. But {\i asher lo yochlu mikodesh hakodashim} \u8212- they may not eat from the {\i korbanot}, and this restriction remains {\i ad amod kohen l\u8217'urim v\u8217'tumim} \u8212- until {\i mashiach} comes, since there was no {\i urim v\u8217'tumim} in the second {\i Beit HaMikdash} to verify status with certainty. The principle being illustrated is {\i gedola chazaka}: the fact that they had been eating {\i terumah} in exile serves as a {\i chazaka} that allows them to continue doing so, despite their inability to produce documentation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Prevents Escalat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: if we hold {\i ma\u8217'alin mi\u8217'nesiat kapayim leyuchsin}, and these {\i kohanim} were presumably also performing {\i duchaning} in exile as part of their established practice, then by allowing them to continue their practice upon their return, we would be allowing them to {\i duchan} \u8212- and through {\i duchaning} they would be elevated to full {\i yichus} status, thereby allowing them to work in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. But that is precisely what {\i Nechemia} was trying to prevent. The very structure of {\i Nechemia}\u8217's ruling \u8212- allowing {\i terumah} but withholding full {\i kehuna} recognition \u8212- therefore implies that {\i duchaning} does not translate into {\i yichus}. Or does it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Levels Distincti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i shani hacha d\u8217'raiah chezkaihu} \u8212- the case of {\i bnei Barzillai} is different, because their status had been openly stigmatized and thrown into question. Since everyone knew their status was doubtful \u8212- and indeed the {\i pasuk} says explicitly that they were excluded from the {\i kehuna} \u8212- no one would translate their {\i duchaning} into full {\i yichus}, because the stigma was communally recognized. This does not prove that in ordinary circumstances {\i duchaning} cannot translate into {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then notes that the same question applies to the view that eating {\i terumah} translates into {\i yichus}: if {\i Nechemia} allowed these {\i kohanim} to continue eating {\i terumah}, why would that not suffice to restore them to full {\i yichus} status and allow them to work in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}? The same answer applies: {\i d\u8217'raiah chezkaihu} \u8212- their status was publicly known to be in question, and therefore no translation from {\i terumah} to {\i yichus} would be inferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Gezeirah Applied\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a further question: if {\i gedola chazaka} allows these {\i kohanim} to continue eating {\i terumah}, what precisely is the {\i chiddush}? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i meikara achol bitrumah d\u8217'rabbanan v\u8217'hasha achol bitrumah d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- in exile they had been eating only {\i terumah d\u8217'rabbanan}, since {\i terumah} outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} is only of rabbinic force. The {\i chiddush} of {\i gedola chazaka} is that upon their return to {\i Eretz Yisrael}, we allow them to continue eating {\i terumah}, even though the {\i terumah} there is {\i d\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then offers an alternative resolution: {\i hacha nami bitrumah d\u8217'rabbanan achol} \u8212- even in {\i Eretz Yisrael} we only allow them to eat {\i terumah d\u8217'rabbanan}, such as {\i terumah} of fruits, which is rabbinic in character. {\i Bitrumah d\u8217'oraita lo achol} \u8212- biblical {\i terumah} of grain, oil, and wine is not permitted to them. And the corollary for the broader debate: {\i ki maskinu mitrumah leyuchsin, bitrumah d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- the principle of translation from {\i terumah} to {\i yichus} operates only when the {\i terumah} in question is {\i d\u8217'oraita}; {\i bitrumah d\u8217'rabbanan lo maskinan} \u8212- rabbinic {\i terumah} does not trigger the elevation to {\i yichus} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so, the {\i chiddush} of {\i gedola chazaka} here is not that we permit {\i terumah d\u8217'oraita}, but that we permit rabbinic {\i terumah} despite the possibility that this might, through some chain of reasoning, eventually lead to eating biblical {\i terumah}. One might have argued that we should apply a {\i gezeirah} \u8212- prohibiting even rabbinic {\i terumah} lest it lead to biblical {\i terumah}. {\i De\u8217'af al gav d\u8217'ika lemigzar mishum terumah d\u8217'oraita, lo gazrinan} \u8212- nevertheless, no such {\i gezeirah} is made. The power of {\i chazaka} is that it overrides even a preventive decree that might otherwise have been reasonable. The discussion continues on 25a.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 25\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah & Duchening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evidence Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a question that sits at the heart of the sugya: on what basis can we establish a presumption of {\i kehuna}? Specifically, if a person performs {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} \u8212- duchening \u8212- in public, and the congregation permits it, or if someone eats {\i terumah}, does that constitute sufficient evidence that he is a {\i kohen}, even for {\i yuchsin} purposes, meaning for marriage? That is the question at hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Nuance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important clarification must be made at the outset, one that Tosafot draws from other {\i gemarot}: when the Gemara speaks of {\i terumah} as evidence of {\i kehuna}, it is not referring to eating {\i terumah} in private. Rather, the issue is whether {\i terumah} is being publicly distributed to him at the threshing floor \u8212- the {\i goren}. This distinction matters throughout the entire discussion. Tosafot adds another nuance as well: a {\i kohen}\u8217's slave could also eat {\i terumah}, and it is conceivable that the {\i kohen} sent his {\i eved} to the threshing floor to receive it on his behalf. That too is a factor in assessing the evidentiary weight of {\i terumah} distribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ezra Narrative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara focuses on these questions through the lens of the Ezra narrative. There, a family of {\i kohanim} who could not prove their exact lineage was told they could not eat from {\i kodesh hakodashim} \u8212- the most holy things. A {\i baraita} on this episode holds that they could continue to eat {\i terumah}, merely being barred from {\i kodashim}. And, as the Gemara noted on the previous daf, they had apparently also continued to duchen. The question therefore arises: since they were still eating {\i terumah} and duchening, should we not infer from that their status even for {\i yuchsin}? The Gemara responds that this was a special case \u8212- the question about their lineage had already been raised, there was already a public stigma, and therefore no inference could be drawn. In a normal case, {\i terumah} or {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} might well establish a presumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} was also cited to underscore how powerful {\i hachazaka} \u8212- the presumption \u8212- can be. Since these {\i kohanim} had been practicing in exile, they were permitted to continue once they returned to Eretz Yisrael. But what is the {\i chiddush}? One would expect them to be permitted. The Gemara offers two answers: either they had been eating only {\i terumah derabbanan} in exile and the {\i chiddush} is that we now allow them to eat {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}, or alternatively, even in Eretz Yisrael they would only continue to eat {\i terumah derabbanan}, and the {\i chiddush} is that despite the concerns that arise from eating even {\i terumah derabbanan} in Eretz Yisrael, they were still permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the Gemara directly to the question of the {\i chidush} of the {\i chazaka}. According to the second possibility \u8212- that even in Eretz Yisrael they ate only {\i terumah derabbanan} \u8212- the Gemara asks: but does not the verse itself imply they could eat {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites the verse: {\i asher lo yochel mikodesh hakodashim} \u8212- they shall not eat from the {\i kodesh hakodashim}. Reading this carefully: it is only from the {\i kodesh hakodashim} that they are excluded; but other things, even {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}, they could eat. The Gemara responds: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- this is what the verse means. They are excluded from anything called {\i kodesh} \u8212- for the verse says {\i vechol zar lo yochal kodesh}, a non-{\i kohen} may not eat {\i kodesh}, which in context refers to {\i terumah} \u8212- and also from anything called {\i kodashim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Kohen Laws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand what {\i kodashim} means in this context, the Gemara cites the verse {\i uvat kohen ki tiheyeh le\u8217'ish zar hi bitrumat hakodashim lo tochel} \u8212- when a daughter of a {\i kohen} is married to an {\i ish zar}, she shall not eat of {\i trumat hakodashim}. The Gemara explains, based on a teaching: {\i bimuram min hakodashim lo tochel} \u8212- she may not eat what is lifted up from the {\i kodashim}, namely the {\i chazeh v\u8217'shok}, the breast and the thigh of the {\i shelamim}, called in the Torah itself {\i shok haterumah v\u8217'chazeh hatenufa} \u8212- the lifted shoulder and the wave breast. This portion is given to the {\i kohanim} and their households, including wives, slaves, and daughters, in a manner analogous to {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevant {\i halacha} here is this: a {\i bat kohen} who marries a non-{\i kohen} loses her right to eat {\i terumah}. If her husband dies without children, she returns to her father\u8217's household and resumes eating {\i terumah}. However, even upon her return, she does not regain the right to eat this portion from the {\i korbanot}. Rashi brings this from another {\i gemara}: once she was married to an {\i ish zar}, she can no longer eat {\i muram min hakodashim}, even after the marriage ends. That is the {\i halacha} derived from the verse. What is relevant to the present discussion is that {\i kodashim} in this context refers to {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accordingly, returning to the Ezra narrative: reading these {\i pesukim} together, the {\i kohanim} in question were excluded not merely from {\i korbanot}, as initially assumed, but also from {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}. According to this answer, since their status had been called into question and they had previously been eating only {\i terumah derabbanan}, they could only continue in that capacity \u8212- not {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Regional Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Chazaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a {\i baraita} on {\i chazaka l\u8217'kehuna}, a presumption of {\i kehuna}. The {\i baraita} teaches: in Bavel, a presumption of {\i kehuna} is established through {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim}. In Bavel there is no real {\i terumah}, and any that exists is only {\i terumah derabbanan}. Duchening, by contrast, is a {\i de\u8217'oraita} obligation, and for a non-{\i kohen} to duchen would be a {\i de\u8217'oraita} violation. Therefore, the most meaningful evidence of {\i kehuna} available in Bavel is that someone duchens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Surya \u8212- the territory immediately bordering Eretz Yisrael, including Syria and parts of Jordan \u8212- presents a different case. These lands may carry the {\i kedusha} of Eretz Yisrael, having been conquered by David. There is considerable complexity surrounding this, including the question of what is called {\i kibush yachid} \u8212- an individual conquest \u8212- meaning David perhaps acted without the authority of the Sanhedrin or before completing the conquest of the core territories of Eretz Yisrael. The {\i Chashmonaim} also conquered portions of those lands, which adds another dimension. In any event, Surya may very well carry {\i kedushat Eretz Yisrael}. Therefore, in Surya, the weightier evidence is that the individual eats {\i challah}, since {\i challah} has the status of {\i terumah} and a non-{\i kohen} eating it would face serious {\i halachic} consequence. {\i Nesi\u8217'at kapayim} also serves as evidence everywhere, but where stronger evidence is available, we apply it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evidence Hierarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a challenge: the {\i baraita} mentions {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} as evidence \u8212- but evidence for what? The suggestion is that this is evidence for {\i yuchsin}. The Gemara resists: perhaps it is only evidence for {\i terumah}, not for marriage. But the {\i baraita} also lists eating {\i challah} as evidence in Surya, and a person who is eating {\i challah} is already eating {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i challah} is a form of {\i terumah}. So clearly the {\i baraita} cannot be proving his right to eat {\i terumah}, since that is already established. It must therefore be evidence for something higher, namely {\i yuchsin}. The Gemara responds: not necessarily. Eating {\i challah} can itself be evidence for {\i terumah} purposes \u8212- because {\i challah} is lower than {\i terumah}. The {\i chiddush} would be that we can infer from the lower obligation to the higher. And this leads to the crux of the dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara invokes the position that {\i challah} in the present era is only {\i derabbanan}, while {\i terumah} remains {\i de\u8217'oraita}. According to that view, the {\i baraita} makes perfect sense as being about {\i terumah} purposes: a person who eats {\i challah} \u8212- which is only {\i rabbinic} \u8212- thereby establishes himself sufficiently to also eat {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}. That is the {\i chiddush} \u8212- that we infer from {\i challah derabbanan} to {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes this is in the spirit of what Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua did when he entered the house of Rav and found the rabbis there assuming the reverse: that {\i challah} is {\i de\u8217'oraita} and {\i terumah} is {\i derabbanan}. He reversed their positions, arguing instead that {\i challah} is {\i derabbanan} and {\i terumah} is {\i de\u8217'oraita}. We will examine that dispute shortly. For now, the implication is that depending on which position one holds, the {\i baraitot} about {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} and {\i challah} evidence can either be read as being about {\i terumah} or about something higher, but none of this has yet proven that {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} leads to {\i yuchsin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Babel vs Syria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a second {\i baraita} on {\i chazaka l\u8217'kehuna}. This one teaches: in Eretz Yisrael, the presumption of {\i kehuna} is established either through {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} or {\i chiluk granos} \u8212- distributing {\i terumah} at the granaries. In Surya and in any place reached by the {\i shlichei Rosh Chodesh} \u8212- the messengers sent from Eretz Yisrael to announce the new month \u8212- the evidence is {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} alone, but not {\i chiluk granos}, because in those places {\i terumah} is not {\i de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} presents an interesting shift: Surya has switched sides. In the previous {\i baraita}, Surya was treated as having {\i kedushat Eretz Yisrael}, with {\i challah} and {\i terumah} distribution serving as valid evidence. In this {\i baraita}, Surya does not have that status, and only duchening constitutes meaningful proof. Rashi explains that these two {\i baraitot} reflect a debate about the {\i halachic} status of Surya \u8212- whether it carries {\i kedushat Eretz Yisrael} or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlichei Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises: what do the {\i shlichei Rosh Chodesh} have to do with the evidentiary weight of {\i kehuna} practices? Rashi explains that the presence of these messengers indicates a functioning network of rabbinic infrastructure \u8212- satellite courts and {\i talmidei chachamim} who are attentive to communal standards. This is crucial to understanding the conceptual framework of the entire discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The presumption of {\i kehuna} rests on two foundations. First, the individual must be performing an act that would carry serious {\i halachic} consequence if he were not a {\i kohen} \u8212- duchening would be an {\i issur asei} for a non-{\i kohen}, and eating {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} carries {\i mita b\u8217'yadei shamayim}. Second, the community must be implicitly accepting this behavior, and that community must be one that would care. A community with no rabbinic oversight provides no meaningful silent acquiescence. But a community with functioning {\i beit din} infrastructure \u8212- one in which learned, observant people are paying attention \u8212- provides genuine communal validation. The fact that no one in such a community objects is itself meaningful testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why {\i chiluk granos} is only valid in cities and not in villages: in a city, one cannot simply walk in, claim to be a {\i kohen}, and receive {\i matnot kehuna} without someone calling him out. In a village, a stranger might pass through, make the claim, and receive the portions with no scrutiny, since there is no social accountability. Even though the {\i issur} for falsely claiming {\i matnot kehuna} is only {\i gezel} \u8212- theft \u8212- rather than the weightier ritual prohibitions associated with {\i terumah} or {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim}, the urban setting creates enough social pressure that a person would not make the claim falsely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alexandria Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} concludes with a striking ruling from Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel: Alexandria of Egypt was initially also such a location \u8212- one where behavior related to {\i kehuna} could establish a presumption. This is remarkable given Alexandria\u8217's reputation for a highly assimilated Jewish community. Yet it is quite possible that even in a largely assimilated environment, {\i kehuna} and {\i tahara} concerns were maintained with particular stringency, making behavior in those areas meaningful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical qualifier, however, is {\i barishona} \u8212- initially. Tosafot notes that by the time of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, the Alexandrian Jewish community had been destroyed by the Romans. The original reasoning for Alexandria\u8217's inclusion was the presence of a {\i beit din} there. Once that court was no longer functioning \u8212- whether because the community was destroyed or dispersed \u8212- behavior in Alexandria could no longer serve as the basis for presuming {\i kehuna}, since the communal structure that would have validated it no longer existed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Challah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challah D\u8217'Oraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now revisits the dispute over the {\i halachic} status of {\i challah} in the present era. The first position, reflected in the discussion the rabbis were having when Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua entered: {\i challah} has been {\i de\u8217'oraita} from the moment the Jewish people entered the land, while {\i terumah} requires something more \u8212- settlement and sovereignty \u8212- and therefore loses its {\i de\u8217'oraita} status when sovereignty is lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof for {\i challah}: the verse says {\i b\u8217'voachem el ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- upon your entry into the land \u8212- using a term that connotes immediacy, the moment of crossing in. This stands in contrast to the language used for {\i terumah} and {\i maasrot}, which includes {\i ki tavo\u8217'u el ha\u8217'aretz v\u8217'yashavtem bah v\u8217'yirashtem osah} \u8212- when you come to the land, dwell in it, and inherit it \u8212- language that implies prior settlement and conquest. Therefore, the obligation of {\i challah} was binding as soon as they entered, even during the seven years of conquest and the seven years of division. During that entire period, they were obligated in {\i challah} but not yet in {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has significant implications for the present era. {\i Terumah}\u8217's {\i de\u8217'oraita} status was tied to dominion over the land \u8212- {\i kibush} and {\i yishuv}. Once the Jewish people were exiled and sovereignty was lost, one can argue that {\i terumah} reverts to {\i derabbanan}. But {\i challah}, whose obligation is tied merely to presence \u8212- not dominion \u8212- might remain {\i de\u8217'oraita} even today, since we are physically present in the land even without sovereignty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua took the opposite position, reversing the analysis entirely: {\i challah} is {\i derabbanan} in the present era, while {\i terumah} is {\i de\u8217'oraita}. He reported that when he walked into the {\i beit midrash} and heard the rabbis arguing that {\i challah} is {\i de\u8217'oraita} and {\i terumah derabbanan}, he told them they had it exactly backwards.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bvoachem Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i b\u8217'voachem} that seemed to make {\i challah} immediately obligatory upon entry is now read differently. The Gemara\u8217's {\i drasha}: one might think {\i b\u8217'voachem} means even if two or three spies entered the land, they would already be obligated in {\i challah}. But no \u8212- {\i b\u8217'voachem} means {\i b\u8217'vias kulchem}, upon the entry of all of you, not a partial entry. The {\i chem} \u8212- the plural suffix \u8212- indicates a complete national entry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ezra Return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reinterpretation brings us back to the Ezra narrative. When Ezra led the return from exile, {\i lav kulu sallik} \u8212- not all of the people came up. Only a portion of the Jewish people returned, a minority. Therefore, the {\i b\u8217'vias kulchem} condition was not met in the time of Ezra, and the {\i kedusha} was not reinstilled for {\i challah} purposes in the same way. Accordingly, {\i challah} today remains {\i derabbanan} \u8212- not because of anything about the nature of {\i challah} itself, but because the national re-entry that would have sanctified it did not take place in its complete form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Terumah}, by contrast, according to Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua, remains {\i de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- presumably because the {\i kedusha shniyah} instilled in the time of Ezra, even with a partial return, was sufficient to maintain it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields a fascinating structural tension. One might argue that {\i challah} should be {\i less} connected to the {\i kedusha} of the land, since the obligation attaches not to the grain growing from the ground but only when the grain has been made into dough. That removes it one step from the land itself. From that perspective, {\i challah} could be {\i de\u8217'oraita} independently of the land\u8217's {\i kedusha}. But on the other hand, the {\i b\u8217'vias kulchem} requirement suggests that {\i challah} actually demands a {\i higher} level of national presence to activate \u8212- a complete gathering of all of Israel, not merely partial return or sovereignty. The two arguments pull in opposite directions, and both are plausible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Competing Frameworks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication of this dispute for the evidence question is now clear. If {\i challah} is {\i de\u8217'oraita} and {\i terumah} is {\i derabbanan}, then the {\i baraitot} about using {\i challah} as evidence for {\i kehuna} might already bring us to a very high level, and {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} can be understood as going one step further \u8212- perhaps toward {\i yuchsin}. But if {\i challah} is {\i derabbanan} and {\i terumah} is {\i de\u8217'oraita}, then the {\i baraitot} describe a chain where {\i challah} (rabbinic) serves as evidence to permit {\i terumah} (biblical), and the whole framework is about {\i terumah} purposes, not {\i yuchsin}. Every time the Gemara has tried to use these {\i baraitot} to prove that {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} establishes {\i kehuna} for {\i yuchsin}, the argument has been deflected by this same structural reversal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aliyah Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having exhausted multiple attempts through the {\i baraitot}, the Gemara now introduces a new line of evidence. A person came before Rabbi Ami and said: I have a presumption \u8212- a {\i chazaka} \u8212- that a certain individual is a {\i kohen}. Rabbi Ami asked: on what basis? The answer: he received the first {\i aliya} in the synagogue, in the presumed capacity of {\i kohen}. Rabbi Ami immediately pushes back: how do you know it was in the capacity of {\i kohen}? Perhaps he received the first {\i aliya} because he was a {\i gadol}, a person of stature, since in principle a {\i talmid chacham} may be given the first {\i aliya} ahead of a {\i kohen}. The response: not only did he receive the first {\i aliya}, but a {\i levi} received the second {\i aliya} after him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi Aliyah Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reply generates a rich dispute between Rashi and Tosafot. The question is whether giving a {\i levi} after a non-{\i kohen} who received the first {\i aliya} proves that the first person was in fact treated as a {\i kohen}. Rashi\u8217's first answer is that the practice, specifically so as not to cause confusion, is to {\i not} give a {\i levi} second when the first {\i aliya} was given on the basis of honor rather than {\i kehuna}. Therefore, if a {\i levi} did receive the second {\i aliya}, it demonstrates that the first {\i aliya} was structured as a {\i kohen}-{\i levi}-{\i yisrael} sequence. Rashi\u8217's second, more nuanced answer: if there are no {\i kohanim} present in the synagogue, a {\i levi} may be called second even after a non-{\i kohen} receives the first {\i aliya}. But if {\i kohanim} are present in the synagogue and one of them was passed over in favor of a person of stature, giving a {\i levi} second would be deeply offensive to the {\i kohanim} present \u8212- it preserves the structure {\i kohen}-{\i levi}-{\i yisrael} while substituting someone else for the {\i kohen}, which is more egregious. Therefore, if {\i kohanim} are in the synagogue and a {\i levi} still received the second {\i aliya}, it proves the first person was being treated as a {\i kohen}. Tosafot takes the analysis further: what may have happened is that a {\i levi} of lesser standing received the second {\i aliya}, and then a {\i yisrael} of greater standing received the third. The fact that the more distinguished {\i yisrael} came third \u8212- after the {\i levi} \u8212- proves the community was following the {\i kohen-levi-yisrael} sequence rather than a hierarchy of honor, in which case the {\i yisrael} would have preceded the {\i levi}. In any case, through one mechanism or another, the subsequent {\i levi aliya} confirms that the first person was treated as a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On that basis, {\i ve\u8217'alehu Rabbi Ami likhunah al piv} \u8212- Rabbi Ami was prepared to recognize his {\i kehuna} based on this testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aliyah Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A crucial question remains unresolved: for what purpose was this {\i kehuna} established? The entire preceding discussion demonstrates that we have been unable to prove that {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} establishes {\i kehuna} for {\i yuchsin}. Here we are dealing with something even less weighty \u8212- a synagogue {\i aliya} \u8212- and the Gemara does not specify for what level of {\i kehuna} it serves as proof. One would reasonably assume it does not reach {\i yuchsin}, perhaps only {\i terumah}, perhaps something else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edus Achazaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to the Gemara\u8217's concept of {\i edus achazaka} \u8212- testimony based on a presumption. One might read this simply as secondhand reporting: the {\i beit din} did not directly witness the relevant facts but heard them from a witness. Tosafot, however, reads the concept more precisely: unlike seeing someone eat {\i terumah} or duchen \u8212- where we directly observe an act that only a {\i kohen} would perform \u8212- the {\i aliya} evidence is indirect in a different sense. We did not observe him do something only a {\i kohen} would do. We knew he was a {\i kohen} only based on what happened {\i afterwards}, namely who received the second {\i aliya}. That is what makes this {\i edus achazaka} \u8212- testimony that establishes a {\i chazaka} not by direct observation of the defining act, but by inference from surrounding circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel story follows. A person came before Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi with a claim: I have reason to presume that this person is a {\i levi}. The basis: he received the second {\i aliya} in the synagogue. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi poses the same challenge: how do you know it was in the capacity of {\i levi} rather than in the capacity of a person of stature? The answer: because a {\i kohen} had received the first {\i aliya} before him \u8212- so the community was clearly following the {\i kohen-levi-yisrael} structure, and he was the second. Accordingly, {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi was willing to recognize him as a levi} \u8212- {\i de\u8217'lei Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi l\u8217'Levi al piv} \u8212- for purposes of giving him {\i maaser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now sharpens the debate through a case that came before Reish Lakish. A person testifies: I have reason to presume that this individual is a {\i kohen}, based on the fact that he received the first {\i aliya} in the synagogue. Reish Lakish is unimpressed and responds: {\i ra\u8217'ita she\u8217'chilek al hagranot?} \u8212- did you ever see him receive a distribution of {\i terumah} at the threshing floor? Reish Lakish\u8217's reasoning is consistent with the framework established earlier: what creates a meaningful presumption is performing an act that would be a serious {\i halachic} violation if the person were not a {\i kohen}. Duchening is an {\i issur asei} for a non-{\i kohen}; eating {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} carries {\i mita b\u8217'yadei shamayim}. But receiving an {\i aliya} \u8212- even the first one \u8212- is not itself such an act. Someone could walk into a synagogue, declare himself a {\i kohen}, receive the first {\i aliya}, and face no serious {\i halachic} consequence for doing so falsely. Therefore, Reish Lakish demanded the higher standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Goren Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar was present and responded to Reish Lakish: {\i v\u8217'im ein sham goren batla kehuna?} \u8212- if there is no threshing floor in this community, does {\i kehuna} cease to exist? This is a pointed practical objection. The higher evidentiary standard Reish Lakish demands may not always be available. In many communities \u8212- especially urban ones, or places without significant agricultural activity \u8212- there is simply no {\i chiluk granos}. If the only available evidence is the synagogue {\i aliya}, refusing to accept it as any basis for presumption effectively renders {\i kehuna} unrecognizable in most settings. One might note as well that {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} would seem to occupy a middle position \u8212- it is a weightier act than receiving an {\i aliya}, since a non-{\i kohen} who duchens violates a {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition, but it is not always available either. Rabbi Elazar\u8217's challenge remains: demanding threshing-floor evidence as the minimum standard is simply impractical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Embarrassed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The issue resurfaces when the same type of case came before Rabbi Yochanan, with both Rabbi Elazar and Reish Lakish present. Again someone testified about a person receiving the first {\i aliya}, claiming it established his {\i kehuna}. Again Reish Lakish challenged: {\i ra\u8217'ita she\u8217'chilek al hagoren?} \u8212- did you see him receive {\i terumah} at the granary? Rabbi Yochanan responded directly to Reish Lakish: {\i v\u8217'im ein sham goren batla kehuna?} \u8212- using the exact same formulation as Rabbi Elazar had used before. Reish Lakish then looked toward Rabbi Elazar with evident displeasure. Rashi explains: Rabbi Elazar had previously challenged Reish Lakish with this very argument, but had not revealed that it was Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position. Reish Lakish\u8217's reaction was: had you told me at the time that this argument came from Rabbi Yochanan, I would have reconsidered my position and not maintained it publicly. Instead, I persisted \u8212- and now I have been publicly corrected by Rabbi Yochanan himself. That is the source of Reish Lakish\u8217's embarrassment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion that emerges is striking. Throughout the preceding discussion, the Gemara strained to find evidence that {\i nesi\u8217'at kapayim} could establish {\i kehuna} even for {\i yuchsin}, and repeatedly deflected such conclusions. Here, Rabbi Yochanan is prepared to accept a {\i synagogue aliya} alone as the basis for a {\i chazaka} of {\i kehuna} \u8212- and the Gemara does not specify that this is limited to {\i terumah} purposes. Given that {\i terumah} distribution would already solve the {\i terumah} question, one might infer that what remains to be proven is something higher. If Rabbi Yochanan is accepting the {\i aliya} as sufficient, this would be a dramatic lowering of the evidentiary threshold \u8212- perhaps even extending toward {\i yuchsin}, which would be genuinely surprising in light of everything that preceded it. The Gemara does not spell out the final purpose explicitly, which itself leaves room for discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Family Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now shifts to a different mode of establishing {\i kehuna}: direct family testimony. The framework is as follows. Rabbi and Rabbi Chiyya each accepted a different form of testimony \u8212- one accepted a father\u8217's word that his son was a {\i kohen}, establishing him for {\i kehuna} purposes; the other accepted a brother\u8217's word that his brother was a {\i levi}, establishing him for {\i leviya} purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} clarifies Rabbi\u8217's position: if a person comes and says, \u8220"this is my son and he is a {\i kohen},\u8221" {\i ne\u8217'eman l\u8217'achilah bitrumah} \u8212- he is believed for purposes of allowing the son to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- but {\i eino ne\u8217'eman le\u8217'hasi\u8217'o ishah} \u8212- he is not believed for {\i yuchsin} purposes, meaning not to allow the son to marry into a distinguished lineage based solely on this testimony. That is the opinion of Rebbi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiyya challenges this distinction: if you are going to believe him for {\i terumah}, you must believe him for marriage; and if you are not going to believe him for marriage, you should not believe him for {\i terumah} either. This appears to reject entirely the graduated framework that the {\i sugya} has been building \u8212- the idea that the same evidence can be valid for one purpose but insufficient for another. Tosafot, however, has a lengthy discussion explaining that Rabbi Chiyya in principle accepts that graduated framework; his objection is specific to this particular case of family testimony, for reasons Tosafot develops at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi \u8212- Rebbi \u8212- responds to Rabbi Chiyya\u8217's challenge with a new and distinct reasoning: {\i she-beyado le\u8217'hachilo bitrumah} \u8212- because it is within the father\u8217's own power to feed the son {\i terumah} regardless of our ruling. The father is a {\i kohen}, the {\i terumah} is his, and he could give it to this person claiming to be his son even without any formal recognition from the {\i beit din}. Since the father could accomplish the {\i terumah} outcome on his own, we accept his testimony at that level. But marriage to a {\i meyuchas} family is not something the father can arrange unilaterally; it requires the community\u8217's recognition of the son\u8217's status. Therefore, the father\u8217's testimony is insufficient for {\i yuchsin}. This represents a meaningfully different conceptual basis from the earlier discussions: it is not merely that {\i terumah} is a lesser obligation, but that the father\u8217's control over the {\i terumah} outcome makes his testimony reliable at that level in a way it simply is not for marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother vs Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: Rabbi Chiyya challenged Rebbi\u8217's acceptance of the father\u8217's testimony for the son. Yet Rabbi Chiyya himself appears to have accepted a brother\u8217's testimony that another person is his brother and a {\i levi}. Why is brother testimony different from father testimony? Both are {\i karov} \u8212- close relatives \u8212- and both are {\i eid echad}. If the {\i karov} problem bars the father, it should equally bar the brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer introduces the concept of {\i mesiach lefi tumo} \u8212- speaking in all innocence. The distinction is not really between father-son and brother-brother relationships as such. The distinction is between formal testimony, where the witness is conscious of the {\i halachic} consequences and is deliberately trying to establish a status, versus casual speech, where a person mentions something in passing without any awareness that it carries legal weight. When someone speaks {\i lefi tumo} \u8212- innocently, without understanding the implications \u8212- there is a strong presumption that he is telling the truth, precisely because he has no ulterior motive and no awareness of the consequences. Formal testimony, even from a perfectly valid witness, carries the risk that the witness is consciously trying to achieve a particular outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara illustrates {\i mesiach lefi tumo} with a story. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: there was a person who was speaking casually and said \u8212- {\i zachurni keshe\u8217'ani tinok umurkav al ktefav shel Abba} \u8212- I remember when I was a small child, riding on my father\u8217's shoulders, {\i vehotzi\u8217'uni mibeit hasefer} \u8212- and they took me out of {\i cheder}, {\i vehipshituni et ktunti} \u8212- and they removed my cloak, {\i vehitbiluni} \u8212- and they immersed me in a {\i mikveh}, {\i le\u8217'echol bitrumah la\u8217'erev} \u8212- so that I could eat {\i terumah} that night. The person recounted this as a simple childhood memory, with no awareness that it served as evidence of anything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Innocent Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiyya himself completes the story: {\i vechaveirai bedilin mimeni} \u8212- and my friends would keep their distance from me \u8212- {\i vehayu korin oti Yochanan ochel chalot} \u8212- and they called me Yochanan the {\i challah}-eater, because they had to maintain distance from a {\i kohen} eating {\i terumah}. And based on this innocent, casual recollection \u8212- this {\i mesiach lefi tumo} \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'he\u8217'eluhu Rebbe likhunah al piv} \u8212- Rebbi was prepared to recognize his {\i kehuna} based on his testimony. The child Yochanan, now an adult, recalled these events without understanding their {\i halachic} significance. It was precisely that innocence \u8212- the absence of strategic intent \u8212- that made the testimony compelling and led Rebbi to act on it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 27\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Besieged City\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context & Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 27 continues the tractate\u8217's sustained focus on questions of personal status ({\i ishus}), and specifically on the status of women. The preceding daf addressed the case of a woman taken as collateral for a loan and under what circumstances she might be assumed to have been raped or to have consented willingly \u8212- a discussion with deep resonances in the stories of Esther and Achashveirosh. Today we turn to a more overtly violent scenario: a city under siege, and the status of the women found within it when the besieging army breaks through.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens with the phrase {\i ir she\u8217'kevashuha karkom} \u8212- a city that has been besieged and finally breached, with the invading army now inside. The word {\i karkom} itself is a subject of some interest; Rashi traces it to an Aramaic root, and it connotes a siege in which no one can escape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanic Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kol kohanot shenimtzu betochah psulot} \u8212- all wives of {\i kohanim} found in the city are invalidated to their husbands, on the presumption that they have been raped. The relevant concern here is specific to women who are married to, or who might wish to marry, a {\i kohen}: a woman who has had intercourse with a non-Jew, whether willingly or by force, is forbidden to a {\i kohen}. (She would still be permitted to eat {\i terumah} from her father\u8217's household if she is a {\i kohenet} by birth, and an Israelite woman who is raped does not become forbidden to her Israelite husband \u8212- but she is permanently disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}.) The {\i mishna} therefore addresses precisely this population.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i ve\u8217'im yesh lahen eidim} \u8212- but if there are witnesses that they were not raped, even normally invalid witnesses are believed. {\i Afilu eved afilu shifchah harei elu ne\u8217'emanim} \u8212- even a slave or slave woman. This echoes leniencies we have seen elsewhere in {\i Yevamot} regarding testimony about a potentially deceased husband, where a single witness, or even a normally disqualified witness, suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} closes with the statement {\i ve\u8217'ein ne\u8217'eman adam al yedei atzmo} \u8212- a person is not believed about himself. The shift to the masculine form is notable; the {\i gemara} will show that this applies not only to the woman testifying about herself, but also to a husband testifying on behalf of his wife \u8212- since his testimony in her favor is effectively testimony in his own interest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One earlier {\i mishna} had already gone further, permitting two women to testify on behalf of each other even where reciprocity might be suspected. The {\i chiddush} there was the basic acceptance of women as valid witnesses in captivity cases \u8212- and the rule that when one witness says she was raped and another says she was not, we always follow the lenient position, even if that witness would normally be disqualified.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Raised\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a {\i rominhu} \u8212- a challenge from a contradiction. A {\i baraita} discusses a marauding troop ({\i baleshet}) that enters a city. {\i Bishat shalom}, in peacetime, open barrels of wine ({\i chaviyot ptuchot}) are forbidden \u8212- we fear the soldiers helped themselves and their contact rendered the wine {\i stam yeinam} \u8212- while sealed barrels are permitted. But {\i bishat milchama}, in wartime, {\i eilu va\u8217'eilu mutarot} \u8212- even the open barrels are permitted, because {\i ein pnai l\u8217'nasech} \u8212- the soldiers do not have time for libation. The concern about deliberate idolatrous contact is set aside when troops are in the midst of active combat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is apparent: if soldiers at war have no time to attend to wine, how can the {\i mishna} assume they do have time to rape the women of a conquered city? The two rulings seem to pull in opposite directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priority Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mari offers the first resolution: {\i l\u8217'iba\u8217'el yesh pnai, l\u8217'nasech ein pnai} \u8212- for sexual violence there is always time; for wine libation there is not. Rape is a priority that soldiers will pursue even under combat conditions; attending to wine is not. This answer, while blunt, has considerable pragmatic truth to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Country Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak bar Eleazar in the name of Chizkiya offers a second answer: {\i kan b\u8217'charkom shel otah malchut, kan b\u8217'charkom shel malchut acheret} \u8212- it depends whether the city belongs to the same kingdom or to a different one. The question is how to understand this distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reads it as follows: when fighting within one\u8217's own country \u8212- say, suppressing a provincial rebellion \u8212- the army exercises greater restraint, since the city is ultimately their own. They will neither plunder the wine nor rape the women. Against a foreign enemy, no such restraint applies, and both concerns come into force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot reads the distinction in precisely the opposite direction, and this reading integrates more naturally with the {\i gemara}\u8217's subsequent development. According to Tosafot, when fighting in enemy territory, the army is operating under urgent pressure \u8212- surrounded by hostile forces, unable to linger \u8212- and so there is no time even for rape, let alone wine. But when the fighting is within one\u8217's own country, where the army is secure, unhurried, and in command of the surrounding territory, there is ample time for both. The {\i issur} on the women therefore applies specifically when the city is {\i shel otah malchut} \u8212- one\u8217's own country \u8212- while in enemy territory, neither concern is operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's reading is to be preferred because it better fits the {\i gemara}\u8217's broader thrust, which is to progressively narrow the circumstances under which the {\i mishna}\u8217's prohibition actually applies \u8212- a point that will become increasingly clear as we proceed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Escape Routes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Women Raped?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even accepting that the prohibition applies in one\u8217's own country, the {\i gemara} raises a further challenge: {\i iy efshar d\u8217'lo arak chada minayhu} \u8212- is it really impossible that even one woman escaped? Why should we assume that every single woman in the city was raped? Given any realistic scenario, some women must have found a way out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guards Prevent Escape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds with the answer of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel: {\i keshe\u8217'mishmarot ro\u8217'ot zo et zo} \u8212- when the conquering army has set up overlapping patrols within the city, each watching the others, so that there is truly no avenue of escape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guards Must Sleep\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} presses further: {\i efshar de\u8217'lo naima porta} \u8212- is it truly impossible that even one guard dozed off? Surely at least one person on patrol must sleep at some point. Perfect surveillance is not humanly achievable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alarm System\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Levi provides the definitive answer: {\i kigun de\u8217'mahaderah le\u8217'mata shushilta ve\u8217'kalba ve\u8217'gaza ve\u8217'avza} \u8212- they have ringed the city with chains, dogs, loose debris that rattles underfoot, and geese. The combination creates an alarm system so comprehensive that no one can slip through undetected even when a guard is momentarily inattentive. It is only under such extreme circumstances \u8212- total physical enclosure reinforced by an active alarm network \u8212- that the {\i mishna}\u8217's presumption of rape holds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} has now done something remarkable: it has essentially made the {\i mishna} moot in any ordinary scenario. We begin with a presumption that all the women have been violated, and then the {\i gemara} immediately begins dismantling the conditions under which that presumption could ever actually apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba bar Zavda reports a dispute between Rabbi Yehuda Nesi\u8217'ah and the Rabbis on precisely this question. One position resolves the wine-and-rape contradiction purely through the distinction between one\u8217's own country and a foreign one \u8212- and, having made that distinction, does not require any further narrowing. In that country where the prohibition applies, we simply assume all the women were raped, full stop, without entertaining further questions about possible escape. {\i Ve\u8217'lo kashya lei ve\u8217'lo midi} \u8212- he is not troubled by the objection at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other position, however, is genuinely troubled by the possibility of escape and can only sustain the {\i mishna}\u8217's presumption in the extremely narrow case where the city has been completely sealed with the alarm system described by Rabbi Levi \u8212- chains, dogs, debris, and geese. Short of that, the concern is always alive that a given woman may have managed to escape or hide, and this possibility is sufficient to suspend the blanket prohibition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One Tunnel Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Tunnel Permits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Idi bar Avin in the name of Rav Yitzchak bar Ashian states: {\i im yesh sham machvoah achas, matzelas al ha\u8217'kohanot kulan} \u8212- if there is even a single tunnel or hiding place in the city, it permits all the {\i kohanot}, all the wives of {\i kohanim}, even if the city is otherwise perfectly sealed. The reasoning is that since any particular woman might have been the one who used that hiding place, no individual woman can be presumed with certainty to have been among those violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Hiding Place\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya \u8212- who had a characteristic fondness for probing borderline cases, testing the precise limits of legal principles \u8212- poses the obvious follow-up question: {\i eina machzekes ela achas, mahu} \u8212- what if the hiding place can only conceal one person? Is it still true that all the women are permitted, on the theory that each one might have been that single individual?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Paths Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a comparison to the classic case of the two paths ({\i shtei shevilin}): one path has a corpse buried across its width, rendering anyone who walks it {\i tamei meis}, while the other is {\i tahor} \u8212- but no one remembers which is which. Two people each walked one of the paths and then handled {\i tahor} food. Rabbi Yehuda rules: if each comes to the {\i beis din} separately to ask his ruling, {\i tahorot} \u8212- each is declared {\i tahor} individually, because each has a {\i safek} and, in certain domains, {\i safek tumah birshut ha\u8217'rabbim} is always {\i tahor}. But {\i sheneihem ke\u8217'achas} \u8212- if both arrive together and present the question as a unit, they are {\i tmeim}, because we cannot simultaneously declare both {\i tahor} when we know with certainty that one of them is {\i tamei}. Rabbi Yossi rules {\i tmeim} in both cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava (and some say Rabbi Yochanan) clarifies: when they come {\i be\u8217'vat achas}, at the same time, everyone agrees they are {\i tmeim}; when they come {\i be\u8217'zeh achar zeh}, truly sequentially, everyone agrees they are {\i tahorim}. The dispute concerns only the intermediate case: someone who comes and asks about both himself and his companion in one presentation \u8212- is that treated as simultaneous or sequential?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's reading of the dispute is sharper: the intermediate case is where a person came on one day and received a ruling for himself, then returned the next day to ask about his companion. Can the {\i beis din} issue a second, contradictory ruling knowing what they already told this person? That is the question the {\i tannaim} debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to our case is then stated: {\i keivan d\u8217'shara lehu le\u8217'chulhu, ke\u8217'vat achas dami} \u8212- if we issue a ruling permitting all the women at once, it is as if they all came before us simultaneously. Can we give a blanket ruling that every woman is permitted when we know that not all of them could physically have been in that one hiding place?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suspicion vs.\u160 ?Certainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the analogy: {\i hachi hashta} \u8212- how can you compare the cases? {\i Hatam vadai ika tumah} \u8212- in the paths case, there is definite {\i tumah}; it is certain that one of the two walked across the buried corpse. {\i Hacha mi yeimar de\u8217'itmei} \u8212- here, who says any woman was actually raped? It is only a concern, a {\i chashasha} \u8212- we do not know for certain that every woman, or even most women, was violated. And since it is only suspicion rather than certainty that grounds the {\i issur}, we can freely apply the {\i machvoah} as a basis for leniency across the board, even if it cannot physically accommodate everyone. This is the principle of {\i bishvuya hekilu} \u8212- in captivity cases, the rabbis were lenient \u8212- operating through the recognition that the entire prohibition rests on doubt, not established fact.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi poses a further question: a woman says {\i lo nechbeisi ve\u8217'lo nitmeisi} \u8212- I did not hide in a cellar, and I was not raped. She is neither claiming to have used the one tunnel nor to have escaped the city. She simply says nothing happened to her. Do we believe her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The issue is whether she has a valid {\i migo}: since she could have said she was the one who hid in the tunnel \u8212- a claim we would have accepted \u8212- she is instead saying something weaker (she was not raped, without any explanation), and we ask whether that weaker claim is believed because she could have made the stronger one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Donkey Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a parallel to the case of a man who rented a donkey and was told explicitly not to travel by the road of Nahar Pekod because of the water there, but to go instead by the road of Narsh. He went by Nahar Pekod anyway, and the donkey died. When he came before Rava, he admitted the path he had taken but claimed there was no water that day. Rava applied {\i mah li leshaker} \u8212- he had no reason to lie, since he could have said he traveled the permitted route and no one would have known otherwise. Since he could have made the more advantageous false claim and did not, we believe the admission he did make along with the exculpatory detail. This is the operative logic of {\i migo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, challenges the application of {\i migo} in a famous limitation: {\i mah li leshaker be\u8217'makom eidim lo amrinan} \u8212- we do not apply {\i migo} against what is effectively established fact, something so widely known that it functions like witness testimony ({\i anan sahadei}). In the donkey case, everyone knows there is always water on the Nahar Pekod road; to claim otherwise is not simply an alternative version of events but a claim against general knowledge. Accordingly, the {\i migo} cannot save him in Abaye\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migo Applies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then applies this distinction to our case: {\i hatam vadai ika eidim de\u8217'ika maya} \u8212- in the Nahar Pekod case there is definite, established knowledge that water is present on that road; the claim to the contrary runs against certainty. {\i Hacha vadai itmei} \u8212- but here, is it certain that this woman was raped? It is not. It is only a {\i chashasha}. And {\i be\u8217'makom chashasha amrinan} \u8212- where there is only suspicion, not established fact, the {\i migo} does apply. The woman is believed when she says she was not raped, because she could have said she was in the cellar and we would have believed that too.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The progressive effect of the {\i gemara}\u8217's reasoning is now fully visible. Not only does the entire {\i mishna} depend on extreme and rarely occurring physical conditions \u8212- total enclosure with chains, dogs, debris, and geese, and no hiding place anywhere in the city \u8212- but even in those rare conditions, a woman\u8217's own statement, backed by {\i migo}, is sufficient to permit her. The {\i mishna}\u8217's prohibition is all but eliminated in practice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witness Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves Believed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the concern rarely applies and that women can free themselves from it easily, the {\i gemara} turns to the {\i mishna}\u8217's provision that even a slave or slave woman ({\i eved} or {\i shifchah}) can serve as a valid witness. The {\i mishna} states: {\i afilu eved afilu shifchah harei elu ne\u8217'emanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a contradiction ({\i u\u8217'rominhu}) from another {\i baraita} that limits the validity of a slave woman\u8217's testimony \u8212- and specifically singles out her own slave woman ({\i shifchasa}) as not believed. How are we to reconcile the two sources?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get & Seclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Seclusion Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} in question addresses a different context: a woman who has received a {\i get} (or whose {\i get} has been written but has not yet taken effect, or is to take effect at a later time \u8212- the precise scenario is the subject of a {\i machlokes} between Rashi and Tosafot). In any of these situations, if the woman enters into seclusion ({\i yichud}) with her soon-to-be or recently former husband, there is concern that they may have intercourse, which would invalidate or complicate the {\i get}. She will then circulate as a divorced woman when her status is in fact uncertain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To prevent this, witnesses are required who can attest that no {\i yichud} took place. The {\i baraita} rules that even a slave or a slave woman can serve in this capacity \u8212- by testifying, in effect, \u8220"I was present with her throughout,\u8221" their presence eliminates the concern of seclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Her Own Slave Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The one exception is {\i shifchasa} \u8212- her own slave woman. {\i Mipnei she\u8217'liba gas be\u8217'shifchasa} \u8212- because a woman feels completely uninhibited in the presence of her own slave woman, she will engage in intimate behavior even when the slave is present. The slave is treated as invisible, as furniture. This being so, the slave woman\u8217's presence does not constitute a real interruption of {\i yichud}, because the woman of the house does not modify her behavior on account of her slave\u8217's observation. From this it follows \u8212- or at least appears to follow \u8212- that we should not believe her own slave woman in captivity cases either.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witness Credibility (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Whose Slave?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa resolves the contradiction by distinguishing between whose slave woman is testifying. When the {\i mishna} says slaves are believed in captivity cases, it refers to the husband\u8217's slave woman. The wife\u8217's own slave woman ({\i shifcha didah}) is the one who cannot be trusted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this: a {\i baraita} states explicitly that {\i ein adam meid al atzmo} \u8212- a person cannot testify about herself \u8212- but that everyone else can testify for her. Surely that \u8220"everyone else\u8221" includes even her own slave woman? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shifchasa ke\u8217'atzma dami} \u8212- her own slave woman is treated as equivalent to herself and falls within the same prohibition. Just as the woman cannot testify on her own behalf, neither can her slave testify as if she were the woman herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Active Lies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers a more refined resolution that does not depend on {\i bishvuya hekilu} at all. He argues that the two sources address different types of conduct. A slave woman {\i meichazi chazya ve\u8217'shaski} \u8212- sees what she sees and stays silent. That is, in the {\i get} context, she was present while her mistress had relations with the husband and said nothing, because her mistress acts with complete freedom before her. This silence, or this withholding of information, is one thing. But when she comes to court and must make an affirmative statement \u8212- that too might involve simply saying \u8220"I was with her,\u8221" without actively vouching for something she knows to be false.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence vs.\u160 ?Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key distinction is as follows. In the {\i get} case, where her silence makes her mistress more permissible, the slave woman may simply not disclose damaging information. But in the captivity case, {\i de\u8217'shikuta osarta} \u8212- where silence would render her mistress forbidden, the only way to help her mistress is to actively testify that she was not raped. And that, Rav Ashi argues, she will do honestly. {\i Tartei lo avda} \u8212- she will not do two things wrong simultaneously. She may withhold; she will not lie outright. Therefore, when she does come forward and testify affirmatively that her mistress was not raped, she is believed \u8212- not as a special leniency for captivity cases, but because we are simply not concerned that she is fabricating testimony.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mari Bar Isak Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a parallel case that illuminates the same principle. Mari bar Isak (some say Chana bar Isak) had a long-lost brother appear from Bei Chozai claiming a share of their late father\u8217's estate. Mari replied that he did not recognize this person as his brother. When the matter came before Rav Chisda, Rav Chisda initially sided with Mari: {\i shapir ka\u8217'amar lach} \u8212- he is saying well to you. As proof that non-recognition does not imply bad faith, the {\i gemara} cites the verse {\i vayakker Yosef et echav ve\u8217'heim lo hikkiruhu} \u8212- Yosef recognized his brothers but they did not recognize him \u8212- and explains that Yosef had left without a beard and returned with one, making recognition genuinely difficult after many years. Mari\u8217's failure to recognize a brother he had not seen for two decades cannot be taken as evidence of fraud.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear Shifts Burden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda then told the claimant to bring witnesses to establish his identity. The claimant said he had witnesses, but {\i mistafu minei degavra alma hu} \u8212- they were afraid of Mari, who was a man of force, a powerful and intimidating figure. According to Tosafot, what actually happened is that the witnesses were brought and then refused to testify at all \u8212- neither confirming nor denying the claim. This conspicuous silence was itself suspicious; why would witnesses refuse to speak unless they were being intimidated into silence? Rav Chisda therefore took the striking step of reversing the burden of proof and instructing Mari to go bring witnesses that the claimant was {\i not} his brother \u8212- an essentially impossible evidentiary demand, but one that Rav Chisda justified by saying: {\i hachi danina lach u\u8217'le\u8217'chulhu amei chaverach} \u8212- this is how I deal with you and with all men of your kind. Where a party\u8217's intimidation of witnesses is evident, the ordinary rules of who bears the burden of proof can be adjusted accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear & False Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: but if this man is so terrifying, will witnesses not be induced to testify {\i falsely} on his behalf as well? The answer is the same principle stated earlier: {\i tartei lo avdi} \u8212- people do not do two things. They may be intimidated into silence; they will not be frightened into outright false testimony. The case of Mari bar Isak and the case of the slave woman both illustrate this single principle from different angles. The degree of suborning required to induce active false testimony is categorically different from the pressure required to induce silence or omission.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Many Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Can Testify\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} states that testimony about a woman\u8217's non-rape is accepted from the broadest possible range of witnesses: men, women, children, parents, siblings \u8212- virtually anyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own Children Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The one exception noted in this {\i baraita} is her own son and daughter \u8212- presumably because children are so closely identified with their mother\u8217's interests that their testimony is not considered independent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Except Self & Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraita} takes a different approach: {\i hakol ne\u8217'emanim le\u8217'ha\u8217'id}, everyone is believed to testify, {\i chutz mi\u8217'heimeina u\u8217'va\u8217'alah} \u8212- except for herself and her husband. This {\i baraita} does not mention the exclusion of her children but does explicitly add the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} observes that the positions of Rav Pappa and Rav Ashi \u8212- who disagree about her own slave woman \u8212- reflect this underlying {\i tannaitic} dispute. The first {\i baraita} (excluding her children and slaves) corresponds to one {\i tanna}\u8217's position; the second {\i baraita} (excluding only herself and her husband) corresponds to another\u8217's. The debate among the {\i Amoraim} is anchored in a genuine disagreement among the {\i tannaim} about precisely which witnesses are too closely identified with the woman\u8217's interests to be credited.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Innocent Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Masiach Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Masiach lefi tumo} \u8212- innocent speech, or testimony offered without awareness of its legal implications \u8212- is a recognized category in which we accord credibility even to normally disqualified witnesses or parties. When a person relates facts casually, as part of ordinary conversation, without realizing they are making a legally consequential claim, their account is treated as more reliable precisely because they have no strategic motive to shape it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother Captivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Dimi, upon returning from Eretz Yisrael, reported in the name of Rav Chanan of Carthage ({\i Kartagina}) a case that came before Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi (some say before Rabbi himself): a certain man was relating his experience of captivity among non-Jews. He said \u8212- apparently as part of a general account of what captivity was like \u8212- {\i ani ve\u8217'imi nishveinu levein ovdei kochavim} \u8212- my mother and I were taken captive together. {\i Yatzati lishov mayim, da\u8217'ati al imi} \u8212- when I went to draw water, my thoughts were always on my mother. {\i Le\u8217'laket eitzim, da\u8217'ati al imi} \u8212- when I gathered wood, my thoughts were always on her. The implication of his story was that he was constantly watching over her, never leaving her unguarded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'hishiah Rabbi le\u8217'khunah al piv} \u8212- Rabbi ruled, on the basis of this account, that the mother was permitted to marry a {\i kohen}. The son had not come to offer testimony; he was simply telling the story of their captivity. Yet his innocent account contained within it all the elements needed to establish that his mother had not been violated. This is {\i masiach lefi tumo} at its most striking \u8212- credibility derived precisely from the absence of testimonial intent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Moot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leniency Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} has now layered leniency upon leniency in a way that all but eliminates the practical reach of the {\i mishna}. The first leniency: the prohibition only applies in one\u8217's own country (per Tosafot\u8217's reading), not in enemy territory. The second: even in one\u8217's own country, it only applies if the city was sealed with a complete alarm system \u8212- chains, dogs, debris, geese. The third: even in that extreme case, if there is so much as one hiding place anywhere in the city, every woman may be presumed to have used it. The fourth: even if there is no hiding place, a woman who says she was not raped is believed by virtue of {\i migo}, since she could have claimed to have been in the hiding place. The fifth: even a slave woman\u8217's affirmative testimony is accepted, because {\i tartei lo avdi} \u8212- people do not both withhold information and testify falsely. The sixth: even casual, innocent speech ({\i masiach lefi tumo}) suffices to establish non-rape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Rarity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cumulative effect is that it is nearly impossible to construct a realistic scenario in which a woman would actually be rendered forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband. The {\i mishna}\u8217's presumption of violation was always a {\i chashasha}, a concern rather than a certainty, and the {\i gemara} has systematically provided tools to overcome it at every stage. In practical terms, almost no actual case will result in forbiddance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Zechariah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} opens with a striking historical account. Rabbi Zechariah ben HaKatzav \u8212- Rabbi Zechariah son of the butcher \u8212- invokes an oath by the {\i Beis HaMikdash} and declares that from the moment the enemy entered Jerusalem until they departed, his wife\u8217's hand never left his hand. He was with her constantly, and she was not harmed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Testimony Banned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response from the Sages is immediate: {\i ein adam meid al atzmo} \u8212- a person cannot testify about himself. The phrase is in the masculine because, as the {\i gemara} makes clear, a husband testifying on behalf of his wife is, in effect, testifying about himself \u8212- his ability to remain married to her depends on the outcome, and he is therefore a directly interested party whose testimony cannot be received.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Can\u8217't Permit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication is stark: even a {\i kohen} husband who was physically present with his wife throughout the siege, never separated from her for a moment, cannot by his own testimony render her permitted to him. His testimony, however honest and however corroborated by circumstance, is categorically excluded. This ruling reminds us that the {\i mishna}\u8217's framework, for all the {\i gemara}\u8217's leniencies, still has a floor: the husband\u8217's word alone will never suffice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Protective Arrangement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separate Housing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} describes the practical arrangement that Rabbi Zechariah ben HaKatzav put in place given his legal situation. Since he could not testify on his wife\u8217's behalf and therefore could not simply continue their marriage as before, he arranged for her to have her own house ({\i bayit}) within the family compound ({\i chatzeiro}). They remained married, but they would no longer share quarters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seclusion Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge was to ensure that they were never in {\i yichud} together, which would itself raise concerns and complicate her status further. The solution was carefully choreographed around the movements of their children. {\i U\u8217'che\u8217'she\u8217'yotza, yotza be\u8217'rosh baneha} \u8212- when she left the compound, she would walk at the head of the group of children, so that the husband would never be left alone with her. {\i U\u8217'che\u8217'she\u8217'nichnesa, nichnesa be\u8217'sof baneha} \u8212- when they returned, she would enter last, after the children had already gone in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Maintained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In this way, Rabbi Zechariah ben HaKatzav managed to maintain the marriage legally \u8212- keeping his wife within the family compound, within reach of the children, and part of the household \u8212- while ensuring that no opportunity for {\i yichud} ever arose that could further compromise her status or his. It is an arrangement that reflects both legal ingenuity and personal devotion: a {\i kohen} who could not testify for his wife found a way, through scrupulous arrangement of daily life, to keep the marriage intact within the law\u8217's constraints.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 28\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zechariah ben HaKatzav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews Enter City\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to the {\i mishna} at the bottom of 27b, which deals with the question of whether women\u8217's marital status is compromised when enemy troops enter a city. Although the {\i mishna} appears to treat the concern broadly \u8212- that any woman married to a {\i kohen} may be suspected of having been violated \u8212- the {\i gemara} effectively narrows the circumstances to the point where the rule would rarely apply. The {\i mishna} already establishes that a single witness is believed in such a case, though there is debate about a maidservant or the husband himself. Working within the framework of the {\i mishna}, which does not credit the husband\u8217's testimony, we arrive at the case of Zechariah ben HaKatzav.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zechariah Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} records: {\i Amar Zechariah ben HaKatzav: hamaon hazeh} \u8212- by this Temple, he swore, {\i lo zaza yada mitoch yadi mishesha nichnesu ovdei kochavim li\u8217'Yerushalayim v\u8217'ad sheyatzu} \u8212- from the time the non-Jews entered Jerusalem until they left, her hand never left my hand. Whether this refers to the destruction of the Temple or a later episode such as the time of Bar Kochba is uncertain. The claim is clear: he was with her the entire time, so she could not have been violated. Yet the Rabbis respond: {\i ein adam me\u8217'id al atzmo} \u8212- a person cannot testify on his own behalf. Because his testimony bears directly on his wife\u8217's permissibility to him, it is considered self-interested and therefore inadmissible. What is striking here is that both he and she presumably know the true facts \u8212- and yet the rabbinic posture is that they cannot simply continue their lives together on the basis of their own knowledge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Living Arrangements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tanna} adds: {\i v\u8217'af al pi chen yiched lah bayit bechatzeiro} \u8212- even so, he set aside a house for her within his courtyard. They did not \u8212- or could not \u8212- simply resume cohabitation, yet she remained within the family compound. The arrangement was carefully managed: {\i uch\u8217'she\u8217'hi yotza yotza berosh b\u8217'nei uch\u8217'she\u8217'hi nichneset nichneset besof b\u8217'nei} \u8212- when she left she went at the head of the children, and when she entered she came in at the rear of the group, so that she would never be left alone with him in the courtyard. Whether they divorced formally or simply ceased living as husband and wife is not entirely clear from the text, but the living arrangement itself was structured to prevent {\i yichud}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerusha Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case prompts Abaye to pose a question: {\i mahu la\u8217'asot begerusha kein?} \u8212- what about arranging a similar arrangement for a {\i gerusha}, a divorced woman? That is, if a {\i kohen} divorces his wife and she wishes to continue living within the same courtyard \u8212- perhaps to continue raising the children \u8212- can that arrangement be structured so as to prevent {\i yichud} and thereby be permissible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvuyah vs.\u160 ?Gerusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes the cases. In the earlier scenario, the leniency derives from the fact that we are dealing with a {\i shvuyah} \u8212- or a woman in analogous circumstances \u8212- where there is doubt about what actually occurred. {\i Hatam hu debishvuyah hikilu} \u8212- there they were lenient because it was not established that she was actually forbidden. {\i Aval hacha lo} \u8212- but here, in the case of a {\i gerusha}, there is no doubt: she is definitively forbidden to her former husband who is a {\i kohen}. The question remains whether this distinction is determinative: {\i o dilma lo shna} \u8212- or perhaps it makes no difference?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Shchunah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ta shema, ditanya} \u8212- come and hear what was taught in a {\i baraita}: {\i Hamgaresh ishto lo tinnasei bishchunutah} \u8212- if a man divorces his wife, she should not remarry and settle within the same {\i shchunah}, the same neighborhood. Rashi explains the concern: this previously married couple, who now belong to others, know each other\u8217's body language and habits well, and proximity will make it dangerously easy for them to fall into an illicit relationship. {\i V\u8217'im haya kohen lo tadur immo bemavui} \u8212- and if the divorcing husband was a {\i kohen}, she cannot even live with him in the same {\i mavui}, even if she has not remarried at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shchunah/Mavui Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth clarifying the spatial terminology. A {\i shchunah} refers to a neighborhood in the general sense \u8212- Tosafot defines it as within three houses. A {\i mavui} is an alley or passage into which multiple courtyards open, making it a larger area than the {\i chatzer}, the courtyard, where individual homes face each other. The earlier case of Zechariah ben HaKatzav involved housing within a single {\i chatzer} \u8212- {\i bayit bechatzeiro}. The {\i baraita} here speaks of a {\i kohen} and his {\i gerusha} needing to be separated by an even greater distance, namely the entire {\i mavui}, precisely because when she is not remarried the concern for renewed intimacy is even more acute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that the distinction maps onto the level of concern: when she has remarried another man, keeping them out of the same {\i shchunah} suffices. But when she is unmarried and forbidden to him as his {\i gerusha}, they must be entirely out of the same {\i mavui}. If the town is a small village, the {\i baraita} treats it as equivalent to a {\i shchunah}, though the Bach emends this to read {\i kemavui}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Must Move\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a practical question: {\i Mi nidche mipnei mi?} \u8212- who must actually leave? The language of the {\i baraita} \u8212- {\i lo tadur}, {\i lo tinnasei} \u8212- places the obligation on her. A further {\i baraita} confirms: {\i hi nidcheit mipanav, veino nidche mipaneha} \u8212- she is the one displaced by his presence; he need not move on account of her. The underlying logic is that upon divorce she leaves the household \u8212- {\i veyatza mibeito} \u8212- and so the question of where she resettles is hers to manage, not his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, this changes if she owns the property: {\i veim hayta chatzer shelah, hu nidche mipaneha} \u8212- if the courtyard was hers, he is the one who must move.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joint Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question is then raised: {\i hayta chatzer shel shneihem mahu?} \u8212- what if the courtyard was jointly owned? Both have a legal claim to remain. The {\i gemara} attempts to resolve this by examining the source of the rule that she is displaced: {\i hi nidcheit mipanav} \u8212- the initial statement assumed his courtyard. If it were hers, he moves. The joint case is left ambiguous, and the {\i gemara} considers the possibility that the ruling applies only when one party is a renter with no real ownership stake \u8212- {\i dilma de\u8217'oger miger} \u8212- in which case she, as the departing party, would be the one to go. But when there is true joint title, who prevails is genuinely unclear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the case of joint ownership by appeal to a verse and a ruling of Rav. The verse, {\i Hinei Hashem metaltelcha taltela gever}, speaks of divine exile as a punishment \u8212- {\i taltela gever}, the exile of a man. From this Rav derives: {\i taltulei de\u8217'gavra kashi mede\u8217'itata} \u8212- it is harder for a man to be uprooted than for a woman. Since this was framed as a curse, the inference is that displacement is more burdensome for a man. Therefore, even in a case of joint ownership, she is the one required to move, as her uprooting causes relatively less hardship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note the irony: the woman is called the {\i bayit}, the home; she is the one who raises the children and provides domestic stability. And yet the societal structure \u8212- the assumption that the household belongs to the man \u8212- leads to this conclusion. The practical reality in many divorce situations today, of course, runs in the opposite direction, precisely because the woman\u8217's stability is recognized as essential for the children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Legal Proceedings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loan Repayment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues in this vein, addressing further contact between a {\i kohen} and his divorced wife. {\i Tanu Rabbanan}: if when they were married he took out a loan from her \u8212- Rashi explains this refers to property she brought into the marriage, the {\i nichsei milog} \u8212- and now they are divorced, {\i ela einah nifraat ela al yedei acher} \u8212- she may only collect repayment through an intermediary. The concern is that having them meet privately for the purpose of a financial transaction will lead to intimacy. Similarly, she should not personally press the claim; she too should send a representative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet goes considerably further: {\i de\u8217'atu lekaman ledina, lo mizdakkinnan lehu} \u8212- if they come before us for a court case, we refuse to hear it. This is a striking and puzzling position. Any two people involved in a legal dispute must be able to bring their matter before a {\i beit din}. The concern, as Rav Pappa explains, is that litigation between a {\i kohen} and his {\i gerusha} will involve repeated meetings \u8212- at the courthouse, over discussions of the case \u8212- and familiarity will breed opportunity. One might ask: is the alternative really better? Refusing to adjudicate hardly eliminates the tension; if anything, it forces them to deal with their dispute without any structure at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shamta/Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternatives offered are even more puzzling. Rav Pappa says {\i shamutei mishamttinan lehu} \u8212- we place them under {\i shamta}, a minor form of excommunication. Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua adds {\i nagudei nami menagdinan lehu} \u8212- we even administer lashes. What this is meant to accomplish in practical terms is difficult to understand. It does not resolve their dispute or give either party a remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman cites a Tannaitic source: {\i Tana d\u8217'vei Abba Rabbati}, meaning the tractate known as {\i Masechet Semachot} (also called {\i Avel Rabbati}), a collection of Tannaitic and Amoraic statements. Though associated with mourning law, the passage here is not about {\i aveilut} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin vs.\u160 ?Eirussin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source clarifies: {\i bamei devarim amurim} \u8212- when do these restrictions apply? {\i Shenitgarsha min hanisuinn} \u8212- when they had actually lived together as husband and wife, having had full {\i nisuinn}. {\i Aval nitgarsha min haeirussin} \u8212- but if they had only been {\i mekudeshet} and the marriage was dissolved before {\i nisuinn}, with no prior sexual relationship, then {\i nifraat al yedei atzmah} \u8212- she may herself be present to collect the loan, {\i she\u8217'ein libo gas bah} \u8212- because he does not feel so comfortable with her that contact will lead to intimacy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Intermediary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A case then comes before Rava: a man and woman who had been betrothed \u8212- {\i eirusin} without {\i nisuinn} \u8212- came to him for a court case. Rav Adda bar Matna was sitting before him. Rava placed a third party between them \u8212- {\i oki Rava shlucha beinaihu} \u8212- so that they could each present their positions through a representative rather than address each other directly. This is the practical solution the {\i gemara} has been moving toward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Matna challenges Rava: we have just learned from Rav Nachman that in the case of {\i eirusin} alone, no such precaution is necessary. Rava responds: {\i k\u8217'chazina d\u8217'kagaissi b\u8217'hadadi} \u8212- I can see from their body language that they are very comfortable with one another, and so I am being appropriately cautious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implementation Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative version of the exchange presents the case in reverse. According to {\i ika d\u8217'amri} \u8212- some say \u8212- Rava did {\i not} initially put an intermediary in place. Rav Adda bar Matna suggested he should, citing Rav Nachman\u8217's leniency for {\i eirusin} cases. Rava replied that in this particular instance the couple\u8217's ease with one another was evident \u8212- {\i k\u8217'chazina d\u8217'ka gaissi b\u8217'hadadi} \u8212- and therefore caution was warranted. The two versions converge on the same outcome: the intermediary is required when the couple demonstrates a level of comfort that creates genuine risk, regardless of whether they had experienced {\i nisuinn}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One student\u8217's observation sheds light on the earlier puzzle of refusing to hear cases or imposing {\i shamta} and lashes: perhaps those rulings apply only where no intermediary arrangement has been established. Once the {\i gemara} arrives at Rava\u8217's solution, it retroactively supplies the practical mechanism that makes the other restrictions coherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Childhood Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} \u8212- the final {\i mishna} of the chapter \u8212- introduces a new set of rules: {\i v\u8217'eilu ne\u8217'emanim l\u8217'ha\u8217'id b\u8217'godlan mah she\u8217'ra\u8217'u b\u8217'kotnan} \u8212- the following are believed to testify as adults about what they saw when they were children. The underlying principle requires explanation. The {\i gemara} will establish that for standard testimony, a person must be a {\i kosher eid} \u8212- a valid witness \u8212- not only at the moment of testifying but at the moment of witnessing. A child, like a slave or non-Jew, is not a valid witness. Therefore, observations made in childhood would ordinarily be inadmissible. The {\i mishna} lists the exceptions, and the {\i gemara} will explain each one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i mishna} serves as a fitting conclusion to the chapter. The chapter began with the question of whether a woman was a {\i besulah} at the time of her first marriage \u8212- relevant to her {\i kesubah} of 200. That led to discussions of testimony about personal status, about weddings and witnesses, about women suspected of violation. Now the chapter closes by addressing how certain kinds of testimony about such matters \u8212- observed in childhood \u8212- may nonetheless be accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signature Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first category: {\i ne\u8217'eman adam lomar zeh ktav yado shel abba, v\u8217'zeh ktav yado shel rebbi, v\u8217'zeh ktav yado shel achi} \u8212- a person may testify that he recognizes the signature of his father, his teacher, or his brother, having seen it as a child. This is relevant to {\i kiyum shtaros}, the rabbinic requirement to validate the signatures on a document before it may be enforced in court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second category: {\i zachur hayiti b\u8217'ploni sheyatza behinuma verosha parua} \u8212- I remember that so-and-so went out in that bridal procession with her hair uncovered, meaning it was a first wedding, with all the ceremony and display that that entails. This ties directly back to the first {\i mishna} of the chapter, about whether a woman was a virgin at the time of her marriage, which determines whether her {\i kesubah} is 200 or 100.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Establishing Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third and fourth categories involve testimony establishing someone as a {\i kohen}. One may testify: {\i v\u8217'shayah ish ploni yotzei mibeit hasefer litvol leechol bitrumah} \u8212- I remember that a certain man would leave our school, go to the {\i mikvah}, and eat {\i terumah}. Or: {\i v\u8217'shayah cholek immanu al hagoren} \u8212- he would come with us and receive a portion at the granary. Both are forms of childhood observation that can establish someone\u8217's priestly status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hapras\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One may also testify: {\i vehamakom hazeh beit hapras} \u8212- this area is a {\i beit hapras}, a field in which a grave has been plowed over and is therefore off-limits to {\i kohanim} due to {\i tumah} concerns. At first glance this seems puzzling, since such testimony restricts rather than permits. Tosafot explains that the leniency runs in the opposite direction: when a {\i beit hapras} cannot be precisely located within a large plot of land, {\i kohanim} are barred from the entire area. A childhood witness who can pinpoint the location of the grave thereby {\i permits} access to the rest of the field. The testimony, properly understood, is a {\i kulah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Techumim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further category: {\i vead kan hayinu ba\u8217'im b\u8217'shabbat} \u8212- we would come only this far on {\i Shabbat}, or we would go as far as this point, establishing the {\i techum}, the Sabbath boundary for the town. Since it is laborious to verify the precise location of the last inhabited structure and measure 2,000 {\i amot} from it, the testimony of someone who recalls the boundary from childhood carries practical weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Believed Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then draws a sharp contrast. One is {\i not} believed to say {\i derech hayah l\u8217'ploni bemakom hazeh} \u8212- that a certain person had a right-of-way through this field \u8212- or {\i ma\u8217'amad umisped hayah l\u8217'ploni bemakom hazeh} \u8212- that a funeral procession with its attendant gathering of mourners would take place on this patch of land belonging to another. Both of these claims would effectively transfer property rights from one party to another. These are strict {\i dinei mamonot} cases, requiring formal {\i edut}. Childhood observation cannot supply it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast is instructive: matters of personal status, priestly standing, Sabbath boundaries, and document validation are all areas where the {\i mishna} makes exceptions. But the moment the testimony would result in taking property from one person and assigning rights to another, the full rigor of evidentiary law applies without exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiyum Shtarot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now works through each category in the {\i mishna} to explain why the exception is warranted. Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua opens with a general qualification: {\i vehu sheyesh gadol immo} \u8212- the child is believed only when testifying together with an adult witness. This qualification applies at minimum to the first case of signature recognition, where two witnesses are always required to validate a document. Whether it applies to some of the other cases as well is a matter of debate among the {\i rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also notes that all three examples of signature recognition \u8212- father, brother, and teacher \u8212- are necessary and cannot be derived from any two. If only the father\u8217's signature were mentioned, one might say the child was believed {\i mishum d\u8217'shchiach gabei} \u8212- because he spent so much time with his father and saw the signature frequently. But one might not extend this to the teacher. Conversely, if only the teacher\u8217's signature were mentioned, one would say the child was believed {\i mishum d\u8217'isa emteh d\u8217'rabbo} \u8212- because fear of the teacher made the child pay particularly careful attention, and he would never claim to recognize the signature unless he was certain. But one might exclude the father on that basis. And neither consideration applies to a brother, who is neither so constantly present as the father nor so awe-inspiring as the teacher \u8212- {\i d\u8217'lais lei lo hai velo hai}. Hence all three must be stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying reason the {\i mishna} accepts this testimony at all: {\i keivan d\u8217'kiyum shtaros m\u8217'drabbanan, heimnu rabbanan b\u8217'drabbanan} \u8212- since the validation of documents is itself only a rabbinic requirement, the rabbis were willing to be lenient in accepting this testimony for a rabbinic matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Wedding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the case of testimony that a woman was married with the full ceremony of a first wedding \u8212- {\i gilluy milsa b\u8217'alma hu} \u8212- the {\i gemara} explains that this is not really testimony in the formal sense at all. {\i Keivan d\u8217'rov nashim besulos nisas} \u8212- since most women who marry do so as virgins, in a first wedding, we are already operating from a strong presumption. The testimony of the childhood witness merely confirms what we would assume in any event. Rashi explains: {\i ein hadavar tzarich edus ela l\u8217'farseim sheyeitzei hadin l\u8217'or belo gimgum} \u8212- we do not truly need the testimony; it is only required so that the ruling can be issued with clarity and without hesitation. Since it is not substantive testimony so much as confirmation of an existing presumption, we are more lenient about its source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the testimony establishing someone as a {\i kohen} \u8212- that he would leave school, go to the {\i mikvah}, and eat {\i terumah} \u8212- the {\i gemara} raises an objection: {\i v\u8217'dilma eved kohen hu?} \u8212- perhaps he was a slave belonging to a {\i kohen} rather than a {\i kohen} himself? Slaves of {\i kohanim} are permitted to eat {\i terumah}, so the mere fact of eating {\i terumah} does not prove priestly lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers by invoking the principle of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i asur l\u8217'adam sheyelameid avdo Torah} \u8212- it is forbidden to give one\u8217's slave a formal Torah education. If the person in question was attending school \u8212- {\i beit hasefer} \u8212- he cannot have been a slave, since slaves were not sent for formal Jewish schooling. One might ask: what kind of slave is this? It is an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, a non-Jewish slave, who is on a trajectory toward partial observance but is not sent to {\i cheder}. The Talmud notes that such a slave might acquire some self-taught knowledge and might even read {\i pesukim} on his own \u8212- and a {\i baraita} confirms that even one who reads three {\i pesukim} in the synagogue, wears {\i tefillin} before his master, or serves as an {\i apitropos} over his master\u8217's estate does not thereby achieve freedom ({\i harei zeh lo yatza l\u8217'cheirus}). The {\i gemara} distinguishes between self-acquired knowledge and formal schooling: {\i d\u8217'kanoi bei minhag banim} \u8212- it is specifically sending a slave to school in the manner of sons that is forbidden. Since the person in question was being formally educated, he was clearly not a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah D\u8217'rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the person was a {\i kohen} and not a slave, the {\i gemara} turns to why we believe the childhood witness on this point. The answer: {\i b\u8217'terumah d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- we believe him only to the extent of permitting the person to eat {\i terumah d\u8217'rabbanan}, rabbinic {\i terumah}. We are not establishing his priestly status for more consequential matters \u8212- such as questions of lineage or marriage \u8212- on the basis of childhood observation alone. Again, the pattern holds: childhood testimony is accepted for rabbinic matters or for things where formal testimony is not strictly required, but not for biblical-level determinations or for the transfer of property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Distribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks about the second proof of {\i kehuna} \u8212- that the person would come to the granary and receive a portion \u8212- and raises the same concern: {\i v\u8217'dilma eved kohen hu?} \u8212- perhaps he was a slave of a {\i kohen}? Here the answer is different. A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i ein cholkin terumah l\u8217'eved ela im ken rabbo immo} \u8212- according to Rabbi Yehuda, you do not distribute {\i terumah} to a slave unless his master is present. The reason is that in a community where people draw an inference from receiving {\i terumah} to priestly status \u8212- {\i maalin mi-terumah le-yichusin} \u8212- giving {\i terumah} to a slave in the absence of his master would cause observers to wrongly conclude that the slave is himself a {\i kohen}. Therefore, if someone is receiving {\i terumah} independently, we may assume he is actually a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose disagrees, permitting a slave to go on his own and state: {\i im kohen ani tnu li bishvil atzmi, v\u8217'im eved kohen ani tnu li bishvil rabi} \u8212- if I am the {\i kohen}, give it to me for myself; if I am the slave of a {\i kohen}, give it to me on behalf of my master. This arises in a case where a free woman and a slave woman both gave birth simultaneously and the infants were mixed up, so that neither boy knows whether he is the master or the slave. Rabbi Yose permits one to go independently in this fashion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the positions of Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yose are perfectly correlated: in the place of Rabbi Yehuda, where receiving {\i terumah} serves as an indicator of priestly status for purposes of {\i yichusin}, one does not give to a slave without the master present. In the place of Rabbi Yose, where no such inference is drawn, one may give to a slave independently. The two rulings are logically interdependent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Tale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} concludes with a cautionary tale from Rabbi Elazar bar Rabbi Yose. He states: {\i mi-yamai lo he\u8217'edati} \u8212- throughout my life I was scrupulous about not giving testimony, out of fear of causing harm through error. But {\i paam achas he\u8217'edati} \u8212- once I did testify \u8212- {\i he\u8217'alu eved le-kehuna al pi} \u8212- and based on my testimony they nearly elevated a slave to priestly status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately asks: can we really say that happened? {\i Ve-hashta ma-behemtan shel tzadikim ein Hakadosh Baruch Hu mevi takala al yadam} \u8212- if God does not bring about stumbling even through the animals of the righteous (alluding to the famous story of the donkey of Pinchas ben Yair, which refused to eat food that was {\i tevel}), how much more so would God not allow the righteous themselves to sin? Therefore the {\i gemara} amends the account: {\i ela bikshu le-haalos eved le-kehuna al pi} \u8212- they were on the verge of doing so, and the mistake was caught at the last moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What happened? Rabbi Elazar had seen someone receive {\i terumah} independently in the locale of Rabbi Yose \u8212- where such practice is permitted even for a slave. He then went and testified about it in the locale of Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- where independent receipt of {\i terumah} is taken as proof of priestly status. The observation was accurate; the inference drawn from it was wrong, because the evidentiary standard was different in the two communities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, in several places, notes that the principle {\i ein Hakadosh Baruch Hu mevi takala} \u8212- that God does not cause the righteous to stumble \u8212- applies specifically to matters of forbidden food. The connection here is not exact, since it is about priestly status rather than directly about eating. But the {\i gemara} invokes the principle nonetheless. Whatever its precise scope, the tale is a vivid illustration of why testimony must be evaluated not only for its factual accuracy but for the interpretive framework within which it is being offered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Additional Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Family Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the remaining categories of the {\i mishna} and the {\i baraita} that supplements it. The {\i baraita} states that a child is believed to testify: {\i kach amar li abba, mishpachah zu tehorah, mishpachah zu tmeiah} \u8212- my father told me that this family is {\i tehorah} and this family is {\i tmeiah}. The {\i gemara} immediately flags the language: {\i tehorah u\u8217'tmeiah salka da\u8217'atach?} \u8212- can you really have families classified as ritually pure or impure in the relevant sense? The {\i gemara} clarifies that this is a euphemism \u8212- or more precisely, a substitution \u8212- and the actual content is: {\i mishpachah zu ksheirah, mishpachah zu psulah} \u8212- this family is of fit lineage ({\i ksheira}), this family is of disqualified lineage ({\i psulah}), meaning perhaps there are {\i challalim} or {\i mamzerim} among them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a considerably more sweeping claim than the other categories. Here the child is being believed not merely for the purpose of {\i terumah d\u8217'rabbanan} but apparently for personal status and lineage \u8212- {\i yichusin} \u8212- itself. The {\i gemara} does not dwell on this tension, moving on to the {\i ketzatzah} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akum/Eved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then addresses cases analogous to childhood: {\i im hayah akum v\u8217'nisgayer, eved v\u8217'nishtachrer, ein ne\u8217'emanim} \u8212- if a person was a non-Jew when he witnessed something and has since converted, or was a slave when he witnessed it and has since been freed, he is not believed. This is the default position. The question is whether such a person is in a position structurally parallel to a child \u8212- someone not yet a {\i kosher eid} who has now attained that status \u8212- or whether the gap is more fundamental.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} also reiterates that in neither the case of a convert nor a freed slave is the person believed to testify about rights of way or funeral procession rights \u8212- those remain strictly in the category of {\i dinei mamonot} requiring formal testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka dissents: {\i ne\u8217'emanim} \u8212- they are believed. His position is that a former non-Jew or slave who observed certain matters is believed to testify about them now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attention Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} analyzes the debate between the anonymous first opinion and Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka in psychological terms. {\i Tanna Kama savar kivan d\u8217'akum hu lo hava dayik} \u8212- the first {\i tanna} holds that since the person was a non-Jew or slave at the time of witnessing, he was not paying careful attention. The events of the Jewish world were not his concern; he lacked the context to understand what he was seeing. {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka savar kivan d\u8217'daiteih l\u8217'igiyurei mah daik yifei} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka holds that since the person intended to convert, he was paying close attention. The debate is thus not formal but psychological: was this person in a position to register what he witnessed with the care and comprehension that valid testimony requires?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note the difficulty with this framing: the {\i tanna kama}\u8217's position is understandable for someone with no connection to conversion at all, but Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka\u8217's leniency seems to assume the person was already on the path to conversion at the moment of witnessing \u8212- which may not always have been the case. For a freed slave, the question of whether he \u8220"intended\u8221" freedom at the time of witnessing is even more complex. The Talmud also suggests that this could be a case of {\i masiach l\u8217'fi tumo} \u8212- offhand, unselfconscious testimony \u8212- which carries its own evidentiary weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketzatzah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} finally turns to the {\i ketzatzah} case: {\i mai ketzatzah?} \u8212- what is this {\i ketzatzah} ceremony? The Rabbis taught: {\i Keitzad ketzatzah? Echad min ha\u8217'achim shenasa isha she\u8217'eina hogenes lo} \u8212- what is {\i ketzatzah}? If one of the brothers married a woman beneath his family\u8217's standing, {\i ba\u8217'in bnei mishpacha u\u8217'mevi\u8217'in chavit m\u8217'leia peiros, v\u8217'shovrin osa b\u8217'emtza rechova} \u8212- members of the family would come, bring a barrel filled with fruit, and smash it in the middle of the street. {\i V\u8217'omerim: acheinu beit Yisrael, shim\u8217'u \u8212- ploni nasa isha she\u8217'eina hogenes lo} \u8212- they would declare publicly: our brothers, the Jewish people, hear us \u8212- this man has married a woman unbefitting to him. {\i U\u8217'misyar\u8217'im anu shema yisarev zaro b\u8217'zareinu} \u8212- and we fear lest his offspring intermarry with ours, since by this marriage his children\u8217's {\i yichus} has been diminished. {\i V\u8217'kachu lachem dugma l\u8217'doros shelo yisarev zaro b\u8217'zareinu} \u8212- take this as a lesson for generations: his seed shall not mix with ours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the ceremony that a child may testify he witnessed. What he learns from it \u8212- and what he transmits \u8212- is {\i yichus} information: this family\u8217's status, and by extension the standing of those who marry into it or out of it. The weight placed on lineage throughout this chapter is nowhere more dramatically illustrated than here, where a family stages a public shaming of one of its own members rather than allow the perception that his descendants carry their name and their status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes the chapter by noting the symbolic resonance of the barrel. At a first wedding, the barrel of wine \u8212- associated with the imagery of the bride \u8212- was a sign of joy and wholeness. Here, the barrel is filled with fruit, and it is smashed. If wine in the wedding context evokes the bride herself, and children are the fruit of the union, then the smashing of a barrel of fruit in the public square is a declaration that the fruit of {\i this} union will not be grafted onto the family tree. The imagery with which the {\i perek} began \u8212- the status of a bride, the question of her first wedding, the {\i kesubah} she is owed \u8212- closes with this public reckoning with the consequences of a marriage made without regard for {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 29\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Kim Lei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth {\i perek} of Ketubot is a chapter studied extensively in {\i yeshivos}, though it connects only loosely to the earlier two {\i perakim}. Its subject matter is more theoretical in character, dealing with cases in the Torah concerning a woman who is raped or seduced, the fines imposed, and the {\i mitzvah} incumbent upon the violator to marry the woman. These laws were already largely theoretical in the time of the Gemara, and certainly in our own day no one is {\i paskening} them practically. Nevertheless, the {\i perek} is rich in {\i lamdus} and in conceptual issues that reverberate throughout {\i Shas}. There is also a considerable body of {\i Rishonim} and Tosafot on these pages, which means that each daf will contain a relatively modest amount of actual Gemara text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kim Lei Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the major theoretical issues running through the {\i perek} is {\i kim lei b\u8217'derabbah minei} \u8212- the principle that when a person is liable for two punishments simultaneously, only the more severe one is applied. This becomes directly relevant here because, in many of the cases to be discussed, the man who raped a woman was also violating an additional prohibition \u8212- for example, she is a relative \u8212- and the question arises whether that weightier punishment of {\i kares} or {\i misah} overrides the financial penalty the Torah imposes. These are among the central questions the {\i perek} addresses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Na\u8217'arah Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Na\u8217'arah vs.\u160 ?Bogeres\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i elu na\u8217'aros sheyesh lahem knas} \u8212- the following young women have a {\i knas}. A {\i na\u8217'arah} is a young woman who has reached {\i bas mitzvah} but has not yet become a {\i bogeres}. Halachically, {\i na\u8217'arah} is defined as a girl who is twelve years and one day old and has produced two pubic hairs, indicating the onset of puberty. Six months after that stage she is presumed to have reached {\i bagrut} and become a {\i bogeres} \u8212- fully physically mature, and legally independent. There are discussions in the Gemara suggesting that the transition may depend not solely on age but on additional physical indicators signaling the completion of puberty; biologically, of course, puberty takes considerably longer than six months. But halachically the framework is fixed: twelve years and one day with two pubic hairs marks the {\i na\u8217'arah}, and six months later the {\i bogeres}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bogeres Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is universally agreed upon that when the Torah describes the case of a {\i na\u8217'arah betulah} who is seduced or raped and imposes various fines and consequences, the word {\i na\u8217'arah} serves to exclude the {\i bogeres} \u8212- the woman who has completed puberty and is legally independent. Once she is a legal adult who stands on her own, no longer under her father\u8217's authority, the Torah\u8217's framework does not apply to her. The fine and the accompanying obligations are structured around a woman who remains under her father\u8217's control; the father, as we will see, is the recipient of the payments. This will also raise the question of what happens with a {\i ketannah} \u8212- a girl younger than {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- a matter the Gemara takes up in due course.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzeres & Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} begins its list with the more marginal cases \u8212- those where one might have supposed the {\i knas} would not apply \u8212- in order to teach explicitly that it does. {\i Haba al hamamzeres} \u8212- if he raped a woman who is a {\i mamzeres} \u8212- one might have thought that since the Torah bundles the fine together with the {\i mitzvah} to marry the woman, and since it is impossible to legally marry a {\i mamzeres} (unless one is also a {\i mamzer}), perhaps none of the {\i halachos} would apply at all. The {\i mishnah} therefore teaches that this is not so: even where he cannot marry her, he must still pay the {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rape & Ethics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechem & Dinah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the Torah\u8217's framework here, some orientation to the scenario the Torah is envisioning is essential, and it is genuinely difficult for a contemporary reader. The {\i mohar} \u8212- the fifty {\i shekel} of silver that the rapist pays \u8212- and its later transformation into the {\i kesuvah} exist to protect the woman. In the social world the Torah addresses, a woman who had been raped or seduced would face severe difficulty in finding a husband, since she was no longer a virgin. The {\i mohar} was the sum that would normally pass to the father upon her marriage; the {\i knas} ensures that money is available to protect her when the normal path to marriage becomes difficult. And in the most extreme case \u8212- that of the rapist \u8212- the Torah goes further and requires him to marry her and prohibits him from ever divorcing her, because in that social context marriage to him may represent the most reliable form of protection available to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a modern reader, the notion that the rapist must marry his victim is deeply troubling, and it is important to think carefully about what the Torah is imagining. The {\i Shechem v\u8217'Dinah} narrative is instructive. The text says {\i vayishkav otah vayan\u8217'ah} \u8212- he lay with her and violated her \u8212- but it also describes Shechem\u8217's profound attachment to Dinah afterward: {\i vatidbak nafsho}, his soul cleaved to her, and he sought to marry her. The Torah seems to be imagining cases that, while unambiguously constituting rape, involve a man who was deeply attracted to a woman, had no legitimate avenue to pursue her in a tightly controlled society with no courtship culture, and acted on impulse \u8212- while not necessarily being someone who would be characterized as a predatory rapist in the way that term is understood today. The Torah apparently envisions scenarios in which, if the woman acquiesces, a viable marriage might follow. This does not excuse the act, and there is a spectrum, but it helps explain why the Torah could conceive of marriage as a possible outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ethical Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A serious pedagogical problem attends the study of this {\i perek}, and it deserves to be named. Because the chapter is studied so widely in {\i yeshivos} as a vehicle for abstract legal analysis, the actual human reality that underlies it tends to disappear entirely from view. The words {\i ones} and {\i mefateh} \u8212- rapist and seducer \u8212- become technical legal categories, not descriptions of real acts committed against real women. Nobody uses the word \u8220"rape\u8221" in the learning, which would be deeply disturbing and would force a confrontation with the real world. The entire {\i perek} can be traversed as a set of interesting conceptual problems in {\i Shas} while the human situation that generated those laws remains completely bracketed. This is particularly acute in contexts where the Hebrew vocabulary does not carry the immediate emotional register it would carry for a native Hebrew speaker, and the Aramaic terminology of the Gemara adds another layer of abstraction. It is a real problem in how this material is transmitted, and it is worth pausing to acknowledge it before proceeding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Women (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Netinah Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i v\u8217'al hanetinah} \u8212- and also if he raped a woman who is a {\i netinah}. There is a significant dispute between Rashi and Tosafot regarding the identity of the {\i netinim}. Rashi holds that the {\i netinim} are the Gibeonites \u8212- the {\i Givonim} \u8212- who made peace with Yehoshua. When Yehoshua realized they had deceived him, he made them into slaves to serve the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, as woodchoppers and water-drawers ({\i chotevei etzim}). According to Rashi, the prohibition against marrying a {\i netinah} stems from their slave status. This is somewhat difficult, however, because the relevant prohibition against marrying a slave would normally apply only to a non-Jewish slave, not a {\i shifcha Kena\u8217'anit} or an {\i eved Ivri} \u8212- categories that can in various ways involve marriage. Tosafot therefore offers a different explanation: the {\i netinim} are forbidden not because of slave status but because they are descendants of the {\i shiv\u8217'at amamim}, the seven Canaanite nations, and the prohibition of {\i lo titchaten bam} \u8212- do not intermarry with them \u8212- continues to apply even after they converted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kutit/Samaritans\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then adds: {\i v\u8217'al ha-Kutit} \u8212- and also a Samaritan woman. The question of what is problematic about marrying a Samaritan turns on a dispute about the validity of their conversion. According to Rashi, the {\i mishnah} is operating on the assumption that the Samaritans\u8217' conversion during the period of the {\i Bayit Sheini} was not considered valid \u8212- that they were {\i gerei arayot}, converts who adopted the religion of the land only because lions came and frightened them, not {\i gerei emet}, genuine converts. On that reading they would be non-Jews, which immediately creates a difficulty: a {\i knas} is paid only to a Jewish woman, as is clear from the earlier Gemara. If they are non-Jewish, there should be no {\i knas} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this by assuming that the Samaritans did undergo a valid conversion and are considered Jewish. Why then might one have thought there would be no {\i knas}? Because the {\i Chachamim} were deeply concerned about maintaining separation between normative rabbinic Jews and the Samaritans. The Samaritans had their own place of worship on {\i Har Gerizim}, their own interpretations of law, and their own communal identity \u8212- they were essentially a separate sect, almost a separate religion, even if technically Jewish. The rabbis therefore worked hard to maintain firm communal boundaries. One might have thought: do not pay a {\i knas} to a Samaritan woman, because doing so might blur those boundaries and encourage the very intermingling that rabbinic policy sought to prevent. The {\i mishnah} teaches that even so, the {\i knas} is paid. To summarize the first three cases: a {\i mamzeres}, prohibited to marry but {\i knas} still applies; a {\i netinah}, prohibited to marry either because of the {\i shiv\u8217'at amamim} or slave status, but {\i knas} still applies; and a {\i Kutit}, where policy considerations might have militated against the {\i knas} but do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Virgin Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-3-Year-Old Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i haba al ha-giyores v\u8217'al ha-shevuyah v\u8217'al ha-shifchah} \u8212- if he raped a convert, a captive woman, or a freed slave woman. These three constitute a second category, distinct from the first. The issue here is not a prohibition against marriage, but rather the presumption that these women may not be virgins. A woman who converted from non-Jewish status, or who was taken captive, or who had been a slave, may have had prior sexual experience \u8212- in which case she would not be a {\i betulah} and the {\i knas}, which is tied to virginity, would not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} specifies: {\i shenifdu v\u8217'shenisgayru v\u8217'shenishtachreru pachos mibnos shalosh shanim v\u8217'yom echad} \u8212- if they were redeemed, converted, or freed before the age of three years and one day, then they do have a {\i knas}. The Gemara operates on the assumption that intercourse with a girl under the age of three is not halachically considered an act of {\i biah}, and moreover, that physiologically the {\i besulim} would have grown back. Therefore, even if something had occurred before that age, she would still be considered a {\i betulah} for these purposes. If these events \u8212- conversion, redemption, or freedom \u8212- occurred after the age of three, there is concern that virginity may have been lost and the presumption of {\i betulah} status cannot be maintained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Karat Relatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karat List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents a third category: {\i v\u8217'haba al achoso v\u8217'al achas aviv v\u8217'al achas imo v\u8217'al achas ishto v\u8217'al eshes achiv v\u8217'al eshes achi aviv} \u8212- if he raped his sister, his father\u8217's sister, his mother\u8217's sister, his wife\u8217's sister, his brother\u8217's wife, or his father\u8217's brother\u8217's wife. These are all cases of {\i chayavei krisos} \u8212- violations subject to {\i kares}. Several conditions must be met in each case for the {\i knas} to apply. In the case of the brother\u8217's wife, the brother must have either divorced her or died, since if the brother is still married to her she is a fully married woman, a different category entirely. And the woman must never have had intercourse with the brother, because she must still be a {\i betulah}. Similarly with the father\u8217's brother\u8217's wife: the uncle must have died or divorced her, and she must never have had relations with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah & Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i v\u8217'al ha-niddah} \u8212- and also a woman who is a {\i niddah}. This too carries an {\i issur kares}, and nevertheless there is a {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karat vs.\u160 ?Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes: {\i af al pi shehen b\u8217'hikhares ein bahen misat beis din} \u8212- although these women carry the punishment of {\i kares}, there is no {\i mitat beis din} \u8212- no court-imposed death penalty \u8212- and therefore in all these cases there is still a {\i knas}. This is the key distinction. The first category \u8212- {\i issur lav} \u8212- is notable because even though there is a negative prohibition, the {\i knas} applies, and moreover, while the marriage would be prohibited, it would be binding {\i bedi\u8217'eved}. The third category \u8212- {\i issur kares} \u8212- is more striking, because marriage would not be binding even after the fact, and one might have argued that the weightier punishment of {\i kares} overrides the monetary obligation through {\i kim lei b\u8217'derabbah minei}. The {\i mishnah} teaches that neither of these considerations eliminates the {\i knas}, provided the woman is still a {\i betulah} in each case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meir vs.\u160 ?Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that the {\i mishnah} speaks of a {\i na\u8217'arah} and infers that a {\i ketannah} is excluded. The Gemara asks: whose position is this? {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav} \u8212- Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav \u8212- {\i Rabbi Meir hi}, it is the position of Rabbi Meir, as taught in a {\i braisa}. The {\i braisa} states: {\i ketannah mi-bat yom echad v\u8217'ad she-tavi shtei searot yesh lah mecher v\u8217'ein lah knas} \u8212- from the day she is born until she produces two pubic hairs and reaches twelve years old, the father may sell her as a slave ({\i shifchah}), as we read in Parshas Mishpatim, and during this period there is no {\i knas}. {\i U-mi-she-tavi shtei searot v\u8217'ad she-tivgar yesh lah knas v\u8217'ein lah mecher} \u8212- once she has the two hairs until she reaches full maturity as a {\i bogeres}, she has a {\i knas} and can no longer be sold. These are mutually exclusive categories according to Rabbi Meir: {\i kol makom she-yesh mecher ein knas v\u8217'chol makom she-yesh knas ein mecher} \u8212- wherever there is {\i mecher} there is no {\i knas}, and wherever there is {\i knas} there is no {\i mecher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} disagree. They say: {\i ktana mi-bat shalosh shanim v\u8217'yom echad v\u8217'ad she-tivgar yesh lah knas} \u8212- from age three years and one day until she reaches full maturity, there is a {\i knas}. This creates an overlap with the period of {\i mecher}: from age three until twelve, the father can still sell her, since she is a {\i ketannah} under full paternal control, but at the same time the {\i knas} already applies because she is old enough for the act to be considered a halachic {\i biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies the positions. According to Rabbi Meir, {\i mecher} and {\i knas} are strictly exclusive: from zero to twelve there is {\i mecher} and no {\i knas}, from twelve to twelve-and-a-half there is {\i knas} and no {\i mecher}. According to the {\i Chachamim}, {\i mecher} applies from birth until twelve (since she is a {\i ketannah}), while {\i knas} begins at age three and continues until {\i bagrut}, creating an overlap between ages three and twelve. Everyone agrees that {\i mecher} applies only while she is a {\i ketannah}, and everyone agrees that acts of {\i biah} with a girl under three are not halachically considered {\i biah} for these purposes. The debate between Rabbi Meir and the {\i Chachamim} comes down to whether the {\i knas} applies to a {\i ketannah} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fitness to Marry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established this debate, the Gemara returns to the main point of the {\i mishnah}: that even these women who cannot be married by the rapist still receive a {\i knas}. The Gemara asks: {\i ve-hanei bnei knasa ninhu?} \u8212- are these women really subject to the {\i knas}? {\i Ve-amai?} \u8212- why? The Torah says {\i v\u8217'lo tihiyeh l\u8217'ishah} \u8212- she shall be to him as a wife \u8212- which implies, {\i isha ha-rauyah lo}, a woman who is fitting for him, someone he could in theory marry. Should not all these forbidden women be excluded? The reason the {\i knas} is not simply identical to a requirement of actual marriage is that the woman could refuse, or he might not be able to marry her for other reasons, and the {\i knas} is still paid. But at minimum she ought to be someone who {\i could} in principle be married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Textual Redundancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish answers that the Torah uses the word {\i na\u8217'arah} in redundant ways that allow for additional {\i drashos}. The {\i pasuk} says {\i ki yimtza ish na\u8217'arah betulah} \u8212- once \u8212- and then says {\i v\u8217'natan le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah} \u8212- to the father of the {\i na\u8217'arah}. That second mention, {\i ha-na\u8217'arah}, is understood as extra: the Torah could have said {\i le-aviha}, to her father. By saying {\i le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah}, the definite form counts as two words \u8212- {\i na\u8217'arah} and {\i ha-na\u8217'arah} \u8212- since it could have said simply {\i na\u8217'arah} without the definite article. {\i Chad legufei} \u8212- one usage is needed simply to establish that the victim must be a {\i na\u8217'arah} and not a {\i bogeres}. {\i V\u8217'chad le-atuyei chayavei lavin} \u8212- the extra word teaches that even women for whom there is a negative prohibition to marry are included. {\i V\u8217'chad le-atuyei chayavei krisos} \u8212- and the further extra word teaches that even women carrying an {\i issur kares} are included.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa derives the same provisions from a different source: {\i betulah betulah ha-betulos}. In the case of the seducer ({\i mefateh}), the Torah says {\i v\u8217'ki yefateh ish betulah asher lo orasa v\u8217'shachav imah} \u8212- if a man seduces a {\i betulah} who is not betrothed \u8212- which is needed for its own sake. It then says {\i ke-mohar ha-betulos} \u8212- like the {\i mohar} of virgins \u8212- where it could have simply said {\i ke-mohar} or {\i ke-mohar ha-betulah}. The plural {\i ha-betulos} is treated as contributing two words. {\i Chad legufei}, one for its own sake; {\i v\u8217'chad le-atuyei chayavei lavin}, one to include those with a negative prohibition; {\i v\u8217'chad le-atuyei chayavei krisos}, one to include those with {\i kares}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks why Rav Pappa did not simply use Reish Lakish\u8217's {\i drasha} from {\i na\u8217'arah}. The answer is that Rav Pappa needed the word {\i na\u8217'arah} to derive what Abaye teaches. {\i De-amar Abaye: ba\u8217'ah le-ha\u8217'amadah patur} \u8212- if the girl died before the time came to pay the fine, the rapist is exempt. The source for this ruling is {\i v\u8217'natan le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah} \u8212- and he shall give to the father of the {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- not {\i le-aviha}, to her father in the abstract. The definite form {\i ha-na\u8217'arah} implies that at the moment of payment she must be identifiable and present as a {\i na\u8217'arah}, meaning she must still be alive. {\i Le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah v\u8217'lo le-avi mesah} \u8212- to the father of the {\i na\u8217'arah}, not to the father of a dead woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling opens a deeper conceptual question: when the father receives the {\i knas}, is he receiving it directly in his own right, or is he collecting on behalf of the daughter? The ruling of {\i ba\u8217'ah le-ha\u8217'amadah patur} suggests the latter \u8212- that the payment flows through her, which is why her absence at the time of payment exempts the payer. One could argue it is simply {\i gzeiras ha-kasuv}, but the question will generate a broader discussion about the nature of the {\i knas} payment that the Gemara will pursue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Words Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then observes that both Reish Lakish and Rav Pappa need their respective words for different purposes, and asks how either of them can derive the inclusion of {\i chayavei lavin} and {\i chayavei krisos} if each extra word is already spoken for. The resolution is that there are in fact six words combined: {\i na\u8217'arah, na\u8217'arah, ha-na\u8217'arah} (from the {\i pasuk} dealing with the rapist) and {\i betulah, betulot, ha-betulos} (from the {\i pasuk} dealing with the seducer). {\i Trei l\u8217'gufeih} \u8212- two are needed for the plain sense of the {\i pesukim}, one {\i na\u8217'arah} and one {\i betulah}. {\i Chad l\u8217'chid\u8217'Abaye} \u8212- one of the {\i na\u8217'arah} forms teaches Abaye\u8217's rule that she must be alive at the time of payment. {\i V\u8217'chad l\u8217'gezeirah shavah} \u8212- one of the {\i betulah} forms is needed for the {\i gezeirah shavah} linking the two cases. And the remaining two \u8212- one extra {\i na\u8217'arah} and one extra {\i betulah} \u8212- serve to include {\i chayavei lavin} and {\i chayavei krisos} respectively. {\i Yasiri lehu trei, chad l\u8217'atuyei chayvei lavin v\u8217'chad l\u8217'atuyei chayvei keritot.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note what the {\i gezeirah shavah} accomplishes. The {\i pasuk} by the {\i mefateh} says {\i kesef yishkol ke-mohar ha-betulos} \u8212- he shall pay money like the {\i mohar} of virgins \u8212- without specifying a quantity. The {\i pasuk} by the {\i ones} says {\i v\u8217'natan le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah chamishim kesef} \u8212- fifty {\i kesef} \u8212- without specifying the denomination. From the seducer\u8217's case we know a number but not the coin; from the rapist\u8217's case we know the coin is {\i shekel} from context, but not which number applies to the seducer. The {\i gezeirah shavah} linking {\i betulos} here to the fifty over there allows us to derive: fifty {\i shekel} in both cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimeon Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatimni View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to alternative positions that the {\i mishnah} is implicitly rejecting. A {\i braisa} teaches the view of Shimon Hatimni: {\i v\u8217'lo tihiyeh l\u8217'ishah} \u8212- she shall be to him as a wife \u8212- this means only a woman for whom the {\i havayah} of marriage would be binding: {\i isha she\u8217'yesh bah havayah}. Since {\i kiddushin} does not take effect with {\i chayavei krisos}, those women would be excluded from the {\i knas} according to Shimon Hatimni. Women subject only to an {\i issur lav} would still be included, because marriage to them, while prohibited, would be binding {\i bedi\u8217'eved}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Azai goes further, deriving from {\i v\u8217'lo tihiyeh} \u8212- with emphasis on {\i lo}, for him \u8212- that the woman must be {\i isha ha-re\u8217'uyah lekayma}, a woman fitting for him to remain married to. This means she must be completely permitted ({\i mutar}), not merely capable of a binding marriage. On this reading, even {\i chayavei lavin} \u8212- where marriage is prohibited but would be binding \u8212- would be excluded from the {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Zeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira identifies the practical difference between these two positions: {\i mamzeres u\u8217'nesina ika beinaihu}. A {\i mamzeres} or a {\i netinah} \u8212- their marriages are prohibited, but marriage with them would be binding {\i bedi\u8217'eved}. According to Shimon Hatimni, who requires only that the marriage be binding ({\i havayah}), a {\i mamzeres} and a {\i netinah} would be included in the {\i knas}: {\i hai nami yesh bah havayah}. According to Rabbi Shimon ben Azai, who requires that the woman actually be fitting to remain married to ({\i re\u8217'uyah lekayma}), a {\i mamzeres} and a {\i netinah} would be excluded: {\i ha einah re\u8217'uyah lekayma}. Our {\i mishnah} rejects both positions and rules that the {\i knas} applies regardless \u8212- whether {\i issur lav} or {\i issur kares}, the fine is paid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara digresses to explore an adjacent question. According to Rabbi Akiva, {\i ein kiddushin tofsim b\u8217'chayavei lavin} \u8212- betrothal does not take effect with women subject to a negative prohibition. If that is so, then for Rabbi Akiva the distinction between the two positions above collapses: whether you require binding marriage or permitted marriage, the result is the same, since even {\i chayavei lavin} cannot be married bindingly according to Rabbi Akiva. What then would be the {\i nafka mina} between those two positions for Rabbi Akiva? The Gemara answers: {\i ika beinaihu almanah le-kohen gadol} \u8212- the case of a widow married to a {\i kohen gadol}. This is an {\i issur lav}, yet a unique {\i braisa} teaches: {\i detanya Rabbi Simai omer min ha-kol osir Rabbi Akiva mamzerim} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva would declare children {\i mamzerim} from all forbidden unions, {\i chutz me\u8217'almanah le-kohen gadol}, except for the case of a widow with a {\i kohen gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer & Binding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The assumption underlying the entire discussion is that these two principles \u8212- whether {\i kiddushin} is binding and whether the child is a {\i mamzer} \u8212- are two expressions of the same underlying rule. If Rabbi Akiva holds that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect by {\i chayavei lavin}, then the child born of such a union would be a {\i mamzer}; and conversely, the statement that he would make {\i mamzerim} from {\i chayavei lavin} implies that {\i kiddushin} is not binding in those cases. The standard ruling ({\i halacha}) is that {\i mamzer} status results only from an {\i issur kares}, and correspondingly, {\i kiddushin} is not binding only in {\i issur kares} cases. Rabbi Akiva extends both principles to {\i issur lav} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i almanah le-kohen gadol} case is different, the Gemara explains, is the {\i pasuk}: {\i lo yikach v\u8217'lo yechalel} \u8212- he shall not marry her and shall not desecrate his seed. {\i Chilui hu oseh v\u8217'ein oseh mamzer} \u8212- if the {\i kohen gadol} marries a widow, he produces a {\i chalal} (a desecrated {\i kohen}) but not a {\i mamzer}. This is because the prohibition of {\i almanah le-kohen gadol} is a prohibition that applies only to {\i kohanim}, not to all Jews ({\i asurah lakol}). When a prohibition pertains only to {\i kehuna}, it affects the {\i kehuna} status of the child \u8212- producing a {\i chalal} \u8212- but does not affect the validity of the {\i kiddushin} or generate {\i mamzer} status. This means that even within Rabbi Akiva\u8217's system there remains a gap between \u8220"not binding\u8221" and \u8220"not permitted\u8221": a {\i gerushah le-kohen hedyot}, for example, is prohibited to that {\i kohen} yet the {\i kiddushin} would remain binding \u8212- the child would be a {\i chalal}, not a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yeshevav Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeshevav All Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues: {\i u\u8217'le-Rabbi Yeshevav} \u8212- and according to Rabbi Yeshevav, {\i d\u8217'amar bo v\u8217'nitztavei\u8217'ach al Akiva ben Yosef} \u8212- who said: come, let us cry out against Akiva the son of Yosef \u8212- {\i she-haya omer kol she\u8217'ein bo biah b\u8217'Yisrael ha-vlad mamzer} \u8212- who taught that any forbidden act of intercourse whatsoever produces a {\i mamzer}. The expression of outrage built into the way Rabbi Yeshevav formulates his criticism is itself significant: it reflects the enormity of the category he is attributing to Rabbi Akiva. If literally every forbidden union produced a {\i mamzer}, the number of {\i mamzerim} would be staggering. The Gemara is interested in whether {\i kol} \u8212- all \u8212- truly means all, or whether Rabbi Yeshevav is only pushing back against one specific position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeshevav Limited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara considers two readings of Rabbi Yeshevav\u8217's statement. If he is responding specifically to Rabbi Simai \u8212- who said that {\i almanah le-kohen gadol} does not produce a {\i mamzer} \u8212- then Rabbi Yeshevav\u8217's {\i kol} might only mean: even that case produces a {\i mamzer}, not necessarily every conceivable case including {\i chayavei asei}. In that reading, {\i kol} is rhetorically expansive but not literally all-inclusive. However, if Rabbi Yeshevav is issuing a free-standing statement on his own authority, then {\i kol she\u8217'ein bo biah b\u8217'Yisrael} should be understood literally \u8212- any forbidden intercourse produces a {\i mamzer}, including cases of {\i issur asei}. In that case, the Gemara asks, {\i mai beinaihu} \u8212- what would be the difference \u8212- between the two formulations (binding versus permitted) for Rabbi Akiva according to Rabbi Yeshevav? And it answers: there would still be one case remaining where even Rabbi Akiva, according to Rabbi Yeshevav\u8217's broadest reading, might concede that {\i kiddushin} is binding \u8212- {\i be\u8217'ulah le-kohen gadol}, a non-virgin woman with a {\i kohen gadol}. This is an {\i issur asei}, and moreover it is an {\i asei she\u8217'eino shaveh ba-kol} \u8212- an obligation that does not apply equally to all Jews, only to {\i kohanim}. The combination of these two features \u8212- that it is merely an {\i asei} and that it applies only to {\i kohanim} \u8212- makes this the one case where even on the broadest possible reading of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position, marriage might still be binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beulah L\u8217'Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the Gemara to a final, unresolved ambiguity. The case of {\i be\u8217'ulah le-kohen gadol} has been identified as a potential exception \u8212- the one category where even the most expansive reading of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's principle does not dissolve the binding nature of {\i kiddushin}. But this raises a difficulty in the context of the {\i knas}: how can the case of {\i be\u8217'ulah le-kohen gadol} be cited at all in a discussion of {\i knas} liability, given that the fundamental requirement for a {\i knas} is that the victim be a {\i na\u8217'arah betulah}? If she is a {\i be\u8217'ulah}, a non-virgin, she cannot receive a {\i knas} regardless of her relationship to a {\i kohen gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi/Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi addresses this difficulty by suggesting that the case involves {\i biah shelo k\u8217'darkah} \u8212- anal intercourse \u8212- which does not rupture the hymen. On this reading, the woman is a {\i be\u8217'ulah} in the sense relevant to {\i kehuna} law (she has had intercourse), yet remains technically a {\i betulah} for the purposes of the {\i knas} because her physical {\i besulim} are intact. The same type of question arose earlier in the discussion of the {\i kesuvah} of 200 versus 100 {\i zuz}. Tosafot, however, rejects this bifurcated analysis and argues that the case is more straightforwardly understood: she was a {\i betulah} when the rape occurred \u8212- the rapist himself deflowered her \u8212- and the {\i knas} is owed because she was a virgin at the moment of the violation. After the rape she is indeed a {\i be\u8217'ulah}, but the {\i knas} crystallized at the moment of the act. The question then becomes whether a {\i kohen gadol} can marry a woman made non-virgin by his own act \u8212- {\i beulat atzmo} \u8212- and the Gemara leaves this as a continuing discussion to be taken up in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Knas & Prohibited Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knas Prohibited Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens with a statement of Rav Chisda, found approximately seven or eight lines into the daf. The background issue concerns the {\i knas} \u8212- the statutory fine imposed upon a man who rapes or seduces a young, unmarried girl \u8212- specifically in cases where the woman is someone he is prohibited from marrying, whether due to an {\i issur kares} or an {\i issur lav}. The {\i mishnah} has already stated that even in such cases a {\i knas} is owed. Yet the upcoming {\i mishnah} will rule that when the act entails a {\i chiyuv misah} \u8212- when the woman is forbidden to him under penalty of court execution \u8212- no {\i knas} is paid. The reasoning behind that latter ruling will become clearer as the discussion unfolds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before engaging the broader debate, Rav Chisda introduces a point of consensus: {\i hakol modim bevo\u8217'al haniddah meshalem knas} \u8212- everyone agrees that a man who has relations with a {\i niddah} does pay the fine, even though the act constitutes an {\i issur kares}. The question is why this case is uncontroversial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} addresses this from two angles, corresponding to the two opinions about what qualifies a woman for {\i knas}. According to the view that the woman must be someone to whom {\i kiddushin} is binding ({\i yesh bah havayah}), this too is unproblematic \u8212- {\i kiddushin} is perfectly valid when performed with a {\i niddah}. There is a separate and interesting question regarding {\i nisu\u8217'in}: can {\i chuppah} take place when the woman is a {\i niddah}? Ashkenazim permit it; Sephardim as a matter of principle do not, since {\i nisu\u8217'in} is bound up with the initiation of shared marital life, which presupposes the possibility of conjugal relations. But {\i kiddushin} unambiguously takes effect even when the woman is a {\i niddah}. According to the other view \u8212- that the question is whether the man is {\i permitted} to remain married to this woman ({\i re\u8217'uyah lekayyema}) \u8212- the answer is equally clear: a man is permitted to be married to a {\i niddah}, even though he may not currently have relations with her. Her status as a {\i niddah} is a transitory condition, not a permanent disqualification from marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis reveals a crucial conceptual distinction that runs throughout the entire discussion. Until now, the debate about {\i knas} when there is a prohibition has focused on the {\i woman\u8217's personal status} \u8212- whether she is someone to whom marriage can bind, or whom the man may lawfully keep as a wife. The {\i niddah} case raises something different: there is nothing wrong with her personal status, yet the {\i act itself} \u8212- the act of intercourse \u8212- was an {\i issur kares}. The same applies to any of the cases in our {\i mishnah} where the man engages in an act that is prohibited under penalty of {\i kares}, independent of any issue with her personal status. The question then becomes: should that {\i issur kares} of the act itself \u8212- not of the person \u8212- override the {\i knas} through the principle of {\i kim lei bidrabba minei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely the tension the {\i mishnah} seems to navigate. When there is a {\i chiyuv misah}, the next {\i mishnah} exempts from payment \u8212- and that is readily understood as an application of {\i kim lei}: the court cannot simultaneously prosecute for a capital crime and impose a monetary fine. But our {\i mishnah} assumes that an {\i issur kares} does {\i not} trigger {\i kim lei}. It is against this assumption that the {\i Gemara} now introduces a dissenting opinion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Nechunya Kim Lei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Nechunya Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces the phrase {\i ul\u8217'afokei mid\u8217'Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah} \u8212- the ruling that {\i knas} is paid even in the case of an {\i issur kares} comes to exclude, to reject, the position of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah. For Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah holds that even in a case of {\i kares} we apply {\i kim lei bidrabba minei}, and the weightier prohibition overrides the monetary liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haystack Yom Kippur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position is expressed in a {\i baraita}: {\i Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah hayah oseh Yom HaKippurim ke-Shabbos le-tashlumin} \u8212- Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah treated Yom Kippur like Shabbat with respect to payment obligations. The reference point is the classic case of burning down someone\u8217's haystack on Shabbat: the perpetrator is exempt from damages, because the very act that caused the loss \u8212- the burning \u8212- was simultaneously an act of {\i chilul Shabbos}, which carries capital liability. This is the principle of {\i kim lei}: the court must choose how to prosecute the matter, and the capital crime overshadows the civil one. Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah extends this: if one burned down a friend\u8217's haystack on Yom Kippur, even though that transgression is punishable by {\i kares} rather than court execution, it would equally exempt from payment. {\i Mah Shabbos mitchayev benafsho patur min hatashlumin, af Yom HaKippurim mitchayev benafsho patur min hatashlumin} \u8212- just as one who becomes liable with his very life on Shabbat is exempt from monetary payment, so too one who becomes liable \u8220"with his life\u8221" on Yom Kippur \u8212- understood as the excision of the soul \u8212- is likewise exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position is, as the {\i Gemara} signals, a significant {\i chiddush} on multiple levels. First, {\i kares} is objectively a less severe penalty than court-imposed execution. Second \u8212- and more fundamentally \u8212- {\i kim lei} is typically understood as a principle operating within the framework of the {\i beit din}. The court cannot prosecute a person simultaneously for a capital crime and for civil damages; it must, as it were, choose its docket. But {\i kares} is entirely a matter between the person and God. It is not a court sanction at all. Why should the {\i beit din}, adjudicating a civil claim for property damage, be deflected by a divine punishment that is not within the court\u8217's purview? One can understand why an act deserving of execution \u8212- even if technically unprosecutable due to lack of witnesses, lack of {\i hasra\u8217'ah}, or because the act was done {\i beshogeg} \u8212- still exempts from payment, since the {\i nature} of the act was a capital offense. But to say the same about something that is categorically and exclusively a {\i chiyuv} toward Heaven, never an issue for the court \u8212- that is the considerable {\i chiddush} of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ason Tragedy Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Ason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah?} \u8212- what is the underlying source for this position? Abaye derives it from the concept of {\i ason} in Scripture. The relevant passage describes two men fighting, and one of them strikes a pregnant woman standing nearby, causing a miscarriage. The Torah states: {\i im ason yihyeh venasata nefesh tachas nafesh} \u8212- if there is a tragedy, you give a life for a life \u8212- and by implication, {\i lo yihyeh ason}, if there is no tragedy, you pay the monetary fine for the miscarriage. The rabbinic reading is that {\i ason} refers to the death of an already-born person in the incident. If such a death occurs, the perpetrator faces the capital charge of murder and does not pay the fine for the miscarriage. This is the scriptural basis for {\i kim lei} itself: when a death arises from the same act, monetary liability is displaced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The significance for Abaye\u8217's derivation lies in the additional usage of the word {\i ason} \u8212- this time in the story of Yaakov and Binyamin, where Yaakov fears that if he sends Binyamin down to Egypt, {\i ason} will befall him on the way. This {\i ason} at the hands of Yaakov is understood as a divinely-ordained misfortune \u8212- {\i ason bid\u8217'ei shamayim}. The {\i ason} in the earlier passage, by contrast, is one caused through human agency \u8212- {\i ason bid\u8217'ei adam}. Abaye draws the {\i gezerah shavah}: just as the human-caused {\i ason} exempts from monetary payment, so too a divinely-ordained {\i ason} \u8212- death or excision brought about by God \u8212- similarly exempts. And {\i kares}, the divinely imposed cutting-off of the soul, falls within the category of {\i ason bid\u8217'ei shamayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Admittedly, the analogy is not seamless. In the original case of {\i im ason yihyeh}, the tragedy strikes a {\i victim} \u8212- the perpetrator\u8217's act caused someone else\u8217's death, and {\i that} death displaces the fine. Here, the {\i kares} falls upon the {\i perpetrator} himself. The two situations are structurally distinct. Nevertheless, this is Abaye\u8217's derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge Yaakov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba bar Abba challenges the derivation. The question is: how do we know that the {\i ason} Yaakov feared for Binyamin refers specifically to a divine punishment \u8212- the kind of providential misfortune ({\i tzinin u\u8217'pachim}, cold and heat, understood as God\u8217's direct intervention) \u8212- as opposed to something caused through human agency, such as lions and thieves ({\i arya u\u8217'ganavei d\u8217'viyedei adam ninhu})? The {\i Gemara} is operating here with a specific understanding of {\i bid\u8217'ei shamayim}: it does not simply mean \u8220"natural causes\u8221" (i.e., anything not done by a person), but specifically means {\i divinely directed} punishment \u8212- something God actively brings about as a consequence of sin. By contrast, {\i bid\u8217'ei adam} encompasses not only human acts but random or negligent occurrences that God did not specifically ordain. On this understanding, lions and thieves might not qualify as {\i bid\u8217'ei shamayim} in the relevant sense, since their actions are not necessarily a divine decree. The challenge, then, is that Yaakov\u8217's {\i ason} might refer to non-divinely-directed tragedies \u8212- and if so, it cannot serve as the source for {\i kares} being an exempting {\i ason bid\u8217'ei shamayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Tragedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} dismisses the challenge directly: {\i Yaakov ha mi-kuleihu azhir} \u8212- Yaakov was warning about all types of tragedies. When Yaakov says \u8220"lest an {\i ason} occur,\u8221" he encompasses the entire spectrum of possible misfortune, whether divinely directed, humanly caused, or naturally random. Since his usage of {\i ason} includes divinely ordained punishment, it suffices to establish the principle \u8212- even if it also includes other categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heat Cold Divine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then indulges a delightful digression prompted by the mention of {\i tzinin u\u8217'pachim} (cold and heat). A {\i baraita} states: {\i hakol bid\u8217'ei shamayim chutz mi-tzinin u\u8217'pachim} \u8212- everything is in divine hands except for cold and heat. The verse cited is {\i tzinim pachim b\u8217'derech itkesh; shomer nafsho yirchak me-hem} \u8212- cold and traps are in the path of the crooked; one who guards his life keeps away from them. Rashi and Tosafot read this as referring to hot and cold weather. The implication is striking: of all the misfortunes that might befall a person, extreme weather is the one that lies within human control \u8212- bundle up, seek shade, stay hydrated \u8212- and therefore God does not deploy it as an instrument of directed punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this creates an apparent contradiction. If cold and heat are {\i bid\u8217'ei adam} \u8212- within one\u8217's own hands to prevent \u8212- and lions and thieves are {\i bid\u8217'ei shamayim} \u8212- divinely directed \u8212- then the earlier challenge would be fully resolved. Yet wait: are lions and thieves really divinely directed? The {\i Gemara} then cites Rav Yosef, and so too the school of Rabbi Chiyya taught it, that since the destruction of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} and the cessation of the {\i Sanhedrin}, the four modes of execution were not abolished \u8212- {\i arba misos lo batlu} \u8212- meaning that divine providence ensures analogous fates befall those who deserve them. Someone liable to {\i sekilah} (stoning) will either fall from a roof or be trampled by an animal. Someone liable to {\i sereifah} (burning) will either perish in a fire or be bitten by a venomous serpent. Someone liable to {\i harigah} (the sword) will be handed over to the Roman authorities or attacked by bandits. Someone liable to {\i chenek} (strangulation) will drown or die of a respiratory ailment ({\i srunki}). The {\i Gemara} thus confirms that lions and thieves are indeed instruments of divine punishment \u8212- {\i bid\u8217'ei shamayim} in the fullest sense \u8212- while cold and heat are categorically in one\u8217's own hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, noting this, explains that while in principle any misfortune can serve as a vehicle for divinely ordained punishment, cold and heat are the one category God specifically does not use, because the relevant verse teaches that a person can always take preventive measures against them, and God does not override those measures. Everything else \u8212- including bandits and wild animals \u8212- can be the vehicle through which God exacts what is deserved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore reverses the terms of the {\i baraita}: {\i chaya v\u8217'ganavei bid\u8217'ei shamayim; tzinin u\u8217'pachim bid\u8217'ei adam} \u8212- lions and thieves are divinely directed; cold and heat are in human hands. And either way, the point stands: when Yaakov says {\i v\u8217'karah ason}, he is speaking about the full range of misfortune, including those that are divinely ordained. It follows that divinely imposed {\i ason bid\u8217'ei shamayim} \u8212- and by extension {\i kares} \u8212- belongs in the same category as the court-imposed death that exempts from monetary payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises at this juncture what is, frankly, an ancient theological question: if divine punishment invariably catches up with sinners, why do we observe so many sinners dying peacefully in their beds? The question receives a characteristically concise answer \u8212- the possibility of {\i teshuvah} entirely removes the punishment, or a meritorious deed is being repaid in this world, or the reckoning is deferred to the next. The brevity of the treatment is itself telling. As a legal tradition, the {\i Gemara} allots this question of theodicy two lines in Tosafot and moves on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Executions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The passage about divine execution also touches on the principle of collective responsibility implicit in the phrase {\i ba\u8217'ish ha\u8217'hu u\u8217'v\u8217'mishpachto} \u8212- \u8220"that man and his family.\u8221" Rashi explains that the family is included because no sinner operates without some degree of enablement from those around him. Onkelos, by contrast, translates {\i mishpachto} as {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'sa\u8217'adohi} \u8212- his supporters \u8212- avoiding the presumption that biological family members are necessarily complicit, and instead understanding the term figuratively as referring to those who actively enable him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava Kim Lei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Molech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers an alternative derivation for Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah\u8217's position. The proof text he adduces is from the passage concerning one who gives his child to {\i Molech}: if the people of the land avert their eyes and fail to execute him, God declares {\i v\u8217'samti ani et panai ba\u8217'ish ha\u8217'hu u\u8217'v\u8217'mishpachto, v\u8217'hichratti oto mikerev ammo} \u8212- I will direct My attention toward that man and his family and will cut him off from his people. This is a far more direct source: the Torah explicitly equates God\u8217's {\i kares} with the court\u8217's execution. Where the court fails to execute, God steps in with {\i kares} as the substitute. This establishes the equivalence of the two punishments as a general principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava vs Abaye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai ikka bein Rava l\u8217'Abaye} \u8212- what practical difference exists between deriving the principle from Abaye\u8217's {\i ason} source versus Rava\u8217's {\i Molech} source? The answer is the case of {\i zar she\u8217'achal terumah} \u8212- a non-{\i Kohen} who eats {\i terumah} belonging to a {\i Kohen}. In doing so, he simultaneously steals (a civil wrong entailing payment) and commits a transgression that is {\i chayav misah bid\u8217'ei shamayim}. The question is whether {\i kim lei} applies here to exempt him from payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note that {\i misah bid\u8217'ei shamayim}, despite containing the word {\i misah}, is understood by the {\i Rishonim} as actually less severe than {\i kares}. Rashi holds that {\i kares} additionally entails the premature death of one\u8217's children, while Tosafot disagrees about the precise details; but either way, the halakhic consensus treats {\i misah bid\u8217'ei shamayim} as a lesser category than {\i kares}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Abaye \u8212- who derives {\i kim lei} from {\i ason}, encompassing all forms of divinely ordained punishment regardless of degree \u8212- even {\i misah bid\u8217'ei shamayim} would suffice to trigger the exemption. {\i Abaye patur}: the non-{\i Kohen} who eats the {\i terumah} is exempt from payment. According to Rava \u8212- who derives it from the {\i Molech} passage specifically equating {\i kares} with court execution \u8212- only {\i kares}, as the direct divine analogue of {\i mitat beit din}, qualifies. Anything less severe, such as {\i misah bid\u8217'ei shamayim}, does not. {\i Rava chayav}: the non-{\i Kohen} is liable to pay.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kares vs Mita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Eating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the dispute between Abaye and Rava established, the {\i Gemara} turns back to scrutinize Abaye\u8217's position more carefully. {\i Vha\u8217'amar Rav Chisda: modeh Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah begonev chelvo shel chaveiro v\u8217'achalo shehu chayav} \u8212- Rav Chisda stated that even Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah concedes that one who steals another\u8217's {\i chelev} (forbidden fat) and then eats it is liable to pay. The reason: {\i shekevar nitchayev bigniva kodem sheba lidei issur chelev} \u8212- the obligation to pay arose at the moment of theft, {\i before} the transgression of eating the forbidden fat occurred. The moment he lifted the {\i chelev}, he acquired it through the act of theft and became liable for payment. The {\i chiyuv nefesh} \u8212- the liability that might trigger {\i kim lei} \u8212- only arose later, when he ate it. Since the two liabilities arose at separate points in time, one cannot overshadow the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One Act Temporal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theft Separate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle \u8212- that temporal separation between the civil and the religious liability prevents {\i kim lei} from applying \u8212- now becomes the central analytical focus. All the cases considered until now involved the civil and religious dimensions of the {\i same act}: the burning of the haystack was simultaneously the {\i chilul Shabbos} and the property damage; the sexual act was simultaneously the {\i kares} violation and the basis for the {\i knas}. But if the theft and the eating are separated even by a split second, they are two distinct events and each is prosecuted independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Food\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore constructs increasingly precise hypotheticals to create scenarios in which the two liabilities genuinely coincide. {\i Hacha bemai askinan? Kegon she\u8217'dachvo chaveiro letoch piv} \u8212- what is the case we are discussing? It is where one\u8217's friend forcibly thrust the {\i terumah} into his mouth. If that happened, the person never lifted the food himself and therefore never committed the act of {\i gneivah} through lifting. The act of eating might then simultaneously be the act of acquiring (and thus the theft) and the act of consuming {\i terumah} (the religious violation). But the {\i Gemara} objects: {\i sof sof, keivan dile\u8217'asei kanyei} \u8212- in the end, when he chews it, he acquires it. And {\i nitchayev benafsho lo havai ad de\u8217'balyei} \u8212- the religious prohibition of eating {\i terumah} is only transgressed at swallowing, not chewing. So even here, the civil liability (at chewing) and the religious liability (at swallowing) are still temporally distinct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spitting Out\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} refines the scenario further: {\i kegon she\u8217'dachvo lo letoch beit habli\u8217'ah} \u8212- the friend thrust it directly into the throat. Now, swallowing is the act in question, and swallowing would simultaneously be the act of eating {\i terumah} and the act of civil liability. But the {\i Gemara} challenges: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case? {\i Ei dematzi le\u8217'ahadura, nahadar} \u8212- if he can spit it back out, let him do so. {\i Ei lo matzi le\u8217'ahadura, amai chayav} \u8212- if he cannot, why is he liable at all, since everything was compelled?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's resolution is illuminating: if he {\i can} spit it out but chooses not to, it is that very choice \u8212- the decision not to return it \u8212- that constitutes the act of {\i gneivah}. And the subsequent swallowing, which is the act that triggers the religious prohibition, comes {\i after} the theft is already complete. So they are still not simultaneous. The {\i Gemara} therefore specifies: {\i lo tzricha dematzi le\u8217'ahadura adei d\u8217'chak} \u8212- the case is where he can spit it out, but only with great difficulty. Rashi explains that by this point the food has become revolting, such that spitting it out would return nothing of value \u8212- it is already effectively destroyed. Consequently, the friend who initially thrust it in is the one who caused the {\i nezek}, and our person cannot be said to have stolen anything by not spitting it out (since the object is already ruined). Yet when he swallows, he derives benefit from something that belonged to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stomach Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings in the concept of {\i hana\u8217'as meiav} \u8212- the benefit of one\u8217's stomach. Even when no act of {\i mazik} or {\i gneivah} can be clearly attributed to a person, if he derived tangible benefit from another\u8217's property, he owes restitution for the value of that benefit. The benefit is concretized precisely at the moment of swallowing, when the food enters and nourishes the body. And it is at that same moment that the religious violation of eating {\i terumah} is completed. The two liabilities thus converge at a single point, and the question of {\i kim lei} becomes applicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liquids Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa applies the same framework to a parallel case: {\i kegon shetachvo lo chaveiro mashkin shel terumah letoch piv} \u8212- where one\u8217's friend thrust liquids of {\i terumah} into his mouth. Rashi explains that liquids become immediately revolting when placed in someone else\u8217's mouth, so the same analysis applies: the friend has already caused the initial damage, the person faces only a liability of {\i hana\u8217'as meiav} upon swallowing, and this coincides with the consumption of {\i terumah} \u8212- such that the two arise simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi takes a completely different approach: {\i bezar she\u8217'achal terumah mishelo v\u8217'kara shirayin shel chaveiro} \u8212- a non-{\i Kohen} who eats his own {\i terumah} (legitimately owned {\i terumah}, say given to him) and at the same time tears his friend\u8217's fine silk garment. Here, there is no connection whatsoever between the two acts \u8212- one is the eating of {\i terumah} and the other is a separate act of {\i nezek} to an entirely different object. Yet they happen to occur at the exact same moment. Rav Ashi holds that temporal simultaneity is itself sufficient to trigger {\i kim lei}, regardless of whether the two acts are part of the same process or even related to one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking and counterintuitive position. If anything, one might expect the principle to apply more naturally in cases where the two acts are part of a single integrated process \u8212- like chewing and swallowing \u8212- even if separated in time. Rav Ashi seems to hold the opposite: only the fact of occurring at the same moment matters, not the relationship between the acts. On Rav Ashi\u8217's view, if a person happens to be eating {\i terumah} with one hand and tearing a garment with the other, the {\i kim lei} of the eating exempts him from payment for the garment. The conceptual difficulty is plain: the logic of {\i kim lei} has generally been understood as the court being unable to simultaneously prosecute the same act under two different legal categories. Applying it to two entirely unrelated acts that happen to coincide in time is a very different \u8212- and considerably more expansive \u8212- claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timeline Separate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda\u8217's foundational principle, then, is that liability attaches at the distinct moment of each act. When theft and eating are separated on the timeline, even if one leads naturally to the other, they generate independent obligations that do not cancel each other out.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arrow Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arrow Four Amos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks whether this ruling of Rav Chisda conflicts with a position of Rabbi Avin. Rabbi Avin states: {\i hazorek chetz mitchilat arba l\u8217'sof arba} \u8212- one who shoots an arrow from the beginning of four {\i amos} to the end of four {\i amos} in {\i reshut harabim} on Shabbat, {\i v\u8217'kara shirayin bahalichata} \u8212- and in the course of its flight the arrow tears through someone\u8217's fine garment \u8212- the ruling is {\i patur}: {\i kim lei} applies and the {\i chiyuv} of {\i chilul Shabbos} overrides the civil liability. The {\i chiyuv} for {\i chilul Shabbos}, strictly speaking, is only complete when the arrow lands \u8212- yet the tearing of the garment occurred mid-flight, {\i before} the {\i chiyuv Shabbos} was finalized. How, then, can one say {\i kim lei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akira Hanachah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in the principle {\i she\u8217'akira tzorech hanacha hi} \u8212- the picking up ({\i akira}) is a necessary part of the putting down ({\i hanacha}). In the laws of {\i Shabbos}, the prohibited act of carrying ({\i hotza\u8217'ah}) requires three components: {\i akira} (lifting the object), traversal of four {\i amos} in {\i reshut harabim}, and {\i hanacha} (setting it down). The entire sequence constitutes a single {\i ma\u8217'aseh aveirah}. Since the {\i akira} is legally part of the {\i aveirah}, the whole process \u8212- from the moment the arrow was launched to the moment it landed \u8212- is treated as one continuous act. The garment was torn in the middle of this legal act, and therefore it is within the compass of the {\i aveirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating No Pickup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} distinguishes this from the case of theft and eating. One might think: just as the picking up of the arrow was part of the process that led to its landing, so too picking up the food was part of the process that led to eating it \u8212- and therefore we should say {\i hagbahah tzorech achilah hi} and treat them as one process. The {\i Gemara} rejects this: {\i hasam ee efshar l\u8217'hanacha b\u8217'lo akira} \u8212- there, it is physically impossible to put the arrow down without first having picked it up. You cannot complete the act of carrying without the {\i akira}. {\i Hacha efshar l\u8217'achilah b\u8217'lo hagbahah} \u8212- here, you can eat without picking up, since {\i de-ba\u8217'ei gachin v\u8217'achil} \u8212- one can crouch down and eat directly off the ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interrupt Criterion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second, and likely stronger, answer: {\i hasam ee ba\u8217'ei l\u8217'ahadorei lo matzi mehadar lei} \u8212- there, if one wanted to interrupt the process and retrieve the arrow, one could not \u8212- once shot, the arrow cannot be recalled mid-flight. {\i Hacha matzi mehadar lei} \u8212- here, after picking up the food, one can always stop and put it back. The interruptibility of the process is decisive. When a process cannot be interrupted once begun, it is treated as a single continuous act and {\i kim lei} may apply to everything occurring within it. When a process {\i can} be interrupted \u8212- when at any moment the actor could choose to stop \u8212- then distinct stages on the timeline remain legally distinct, and each generates its own independent liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} here is notably bracketing the strictly legal definition of the {\i aveirah} and focusing on a physical criterion of continuity: either the practical necessity of the earlier stage for the later stage, or the impossibility of interruption once begun. One might argue \u8212- and there is something to this \u8212- that the principle of {\i akira tzorech hanacha} is properly a legal point: it is because the {\i akira} is legally constitutive of the {\i ma\u8217'aseh aveirah} of {\i hotza\u8217'ah} that the entire process is treated as one act. On this reading, anything that is part of the {\i halachically} defined act would be encompassed by {\i kim lei}, and the physical criteria the {\i Gemara} invokes are approximations of that legal definition rather than independent factors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knife Reshus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes the practical difference between the two formulations by means of a test case: {\i maavir sakin b\u8217'reshut harabim v\u8217'kara shirayin b\u8217'halichato} \u8212- one who carries a knife through {\i reshut harabim} on Shabbat and in the course of walking it tears someone\u8217's garment. According to the first formulation \u8212- that the criterion is whether the beginning of the process is physically necessary for the end \u8212- here too {\i ee efshar l\u8217'hanacha b\u8217'lo akira}: you cannot transport the knife four {\i amos} without first having picked it up. The {\i kim lei} of {\i chilul Shabbos} would apply and exempt from payment. According to the second formulation \u8212- that the criterion is whether the process can be interrupted \u8212- here {\i matzi mehadar lei}: one could stop walking at any moment, set the knife down, and reverse the act. The process is interruptible, and therefore the garment-tearing and the {\i chilul Shabbos} are not sufficiently fused to trigger {\i kim lei}; the civil liability stands independently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 31\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiyma Lei Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up in the middle of 31a, approximately ten lines before the lines widen, beginning with the words {\i lo hachi matnina hadola}. The word {\i gufa} signals that the Gemara is now returning to analyze a previously cited source in full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiyma Lei Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader context has already introduced the principle that if someone performs an act that renders them liable to execution by {\i beis din} \u8212- or, according to Rabbi Nechunia ben HaKanah, even liable to {\i kares} \u8212- that capital liability overrides any concurrent monetary payment or fine. The question driving the discussion is: what precisely constitutes the relevant \u8220"overlap\u8221"? In what sense must the capital and monetary obligations coincide in order for the capital one to override?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Act Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The earlier discussion began with cases in which a single act simultaneously generated both liabilities \u8212- for instance, burning a haystack on {\i Shabbos} or {\i Yom Kippur}. That is the clearest instance: one action, two consequences. But the Gemara has since moved to more complex territory, examining situations involving two distinct acts that either occur sequentially or appear to occur at the same time. An extreme formulation held that timing alone is dispositive: if someone is simultaneously eating {\i chelev} and, with his feet, destroying property, the two obligations coincide in time and the monetary one is overridden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Acts Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now return to a different framing: rather than focusing purely on the clock, the Gemara considers whether a series of actions can be understood as constituting a single unified {\i process}. If the act that makes a person deserving of the death penalty is defined as a process \u8212- with a beginning, a middle, and an end \u8212- then any monetary damage or fine incurred at any point within that process would be overridden. This is the conceptual lens through which the Gemara proceeds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arrow Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arrow Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with a teaching of Rabbi Avin: {\i hazorek chetz mit-techilas arba l\u8217'sof arba} \u8212- one who shoots an arrow from the beginning of four {\i amos} in {\i reshus harabim} to the end, and in its flight it tears fine silk garments, is {\i patur} \u8212- exempt from paying for the damage. The technical violation of {\i Shabbos} is not completed until the arrow lands \u8212- that is, until the end of its trajectory \u8212- yet the shooter is exempt from the monetary damages incurred along the way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Carrying Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason given is: {\i she-akira tzarach hanacha hi} \u8212- the {\i akira}, the picking up, is necessary for the {\i hanacha}, the putting down. One might understand this physically: once the arrow is in flight, the archer cannot retrieve it, so the entire arc of motion is one indivisible event. But the deeper and more compelling reading is a halachic one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Carrying Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The act of carrying is not defined merely by the moment of {\i hanacha}. One does not carry by depositing an object in {\i reshus harabim}; one carries by picking it up, traversing the domain, and setting it down. The entire sequence \u8212- from the initial {\i akira} through the traversal to the final {\i hanacha} \u8212- constitutes a single, unified act of carrying. Since liability is incurred at the end of this process, but the process itself began at the moment of pickup, any damage occurring anywhere within that process is subsumed under the same act. The exemption from monetary liability follows, not from some physical impossibility of interruption, but from the halachic conception of carrying as an integral whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: is the {\i patur} here referring to exemption from the monetary damages for the silk? It is. The archer remains fully {\i chayav} for {\i chillul Shabbos}. The exemption is solely monetary, not religious.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Theft & Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wallet Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a challenge from Rabbi Bibi bar Abaye. The {\i braisa} teaches: {\i hagonev kis b\u8217'Shabbos chayav} \u8212- one who steals a wallet on {\i Shabbos} is still liable to pay, because the obligation of {\i geneiva} was incurred before the {\i issur} of {\i sekila} (execution) came into play. The act of theft is completed the moment the thief picks up the wallet \u8212- that is the act of {\i kinyan} by {\i hagba\u8217'ah} \u8212- while the violation of {\i Shabbos} occurs only later, when the object is carried four {\i amos} or transferred from the owner\u8217's {\i reshus hayachid} to a {\i reshus harabim}. The two obligations arise from distinct acts at distinct moments, so the later capital liability does not retroactively override the earlier monetary one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dragging Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} continues: {\i ha im megorer v\u8217'yotzei, megorer v\u8217'yotzei, patur} \u8212- but if the thief was dragging it and going, dragging it and going, he is {\i patur}, because the {\i issur Shabbos} and the act of {\i geneiva} arrive simultaneously. To understand this, consider the following. If the thief drags the wallet along the ground rather than lifting it, he has not yet performed {\i hagba\u8217'ah}, and thus has not yet acquired it through a recognized act of {\i kinyan}. He drags it through the owner\u8217's domain \u8212- still not stolen \u8212- and then crosses the threshold into some other domain. That crossing is where both the {\i issur Shabbos} and the act of {\i geneiva} are completed at the same moment, and therefore they coincide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Domains Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate structural question: what counts as an act of {\i geneiva} in the first place? The general assumption is that theft is consummated through the same type of act by which property is legitimately transferred \u8212- that is, through an act of {\i kinyan}. Picking the object up ({\i hagba\u8217'ah}) in the owner\u8217's domain would accomplish this. But if one does not lift it, then {\i geneiva} can only be accomplished by moving it into a domain where one has some degree of control \u8212- one\u8217's own property, or at minimum some semi-private space in which one can be considered to have possession. That space is not {\i reshus harabim}. Meanwhile, {\i chillul Shabbos} requires precisely the transfer from {\i reshus hayachid} to {\i reshus harabim}. These two requirements seem at first to be mutually exclusive: the act that triggers {\i chillul Shabbos} is not the act that triggers {\i geneiva}, and vice versa. Much of the remainder of the Gemara\u8217's discussion will resolve this tension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Dragging?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks a pointed question. The arrow case established the principle that the entire process of carrying \u8212- from the moment of {\i akira} \u8212- counts as a single act. If so, why does the {\i braisa} require the unusual case of dragging at all? In the normal case of picking up a wallet and walking it out of the house, the theft by {\i hagba\u8217'ah} occurs first, and then the {\i Shabbos} violation follows when the carrier crosses into {\i reshus harabim}. The arrow principle would seem to apply here as well: the {\i akira} \u8212- the picking up that constituted the theft \u8212- is the beginning of the process of carrying, and therefore the two offenses should be considered part of the same act, rendering the thief {\i patur}. Why then does the {\i braisa} not simply state: if you picked it up and walked it out, you are {\i patur}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Process Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i kegon shehigbiha al menas lehatznio} \u8212- the case in the {\i braisa} is one where the thief picked up the wallet intending not to carry it out to {\i reshus harabim}, but to hide it somewhere else within the house. He then changed his mind and walked it out. In such a case, the theft by {\i hagba\u8217'ah} and the beginning of the carrying process are not the same act, because the act of {\i akira} that commences carrying must include the intent to carry it out. If he picked it up with intent to rehide it indoors, that picking up was an act of {\i geneiva} but not the beginning of the carrying process. When he subsequently decided to walk it out, that is when the carrying process began \u8212- but he did not pick it up again, so there is no {\i akira} at that moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet this immediately generates a further difficulty: if he is now walking toward the door with no fresh act of picking up, how can there be a valid {\i akira} for {\i Shabbos} purposes at all? The Gemara invokes the ruling of Rav Simon in the name of Rabbi Abba in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: one who moves objects from corner to corner within his house without intending to take them outside, and then changes his mind and carries them out, is {\i patur} \u8212- {\i she-lo hayetah akirah mi-sha\u8217'ah rishonah le-kach} \u8212- because the original picking up was not directed toward that purpose, and there was no subsequent valid {\i akira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rest vs Adjust\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then discards the \u8220"intent to hide\u8221" scenario and reformulates: {\i ela eima al menat le-hotzi} \u8212- say instead that he picked it up intending to carry it out. Still, the {\i braisa} describes him as pausing. The Gemara asks: {\i amad le-mai?} For what purpose did he stop? If he stopped {\i le-chateif} \u8212- to shift the object from one shoulder to the other, or to adjust his grip \u8212- {\i orcheih hu}, that is simply the normal manner of carrying, and such an adjustment does not constitute an interruption. It does not end one process and begin another. But if he stopped {\i be-omed la-fush} \u8212- to take a genuine rest \u8212- that is a meaningful pause. In that case, the object has \u8220"settled\u8221" in its current location, and when he resumes walking, that resumption functions as a new {\i akira}. Thus the theft, which occurred at the original {\i hagba\u8217'ah}, is temporally separated from the beginning of the carrying process, and the two offenses are distinct. He would then be liable for the monetary penalty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The insight here is precise: {\i akiras gufo ke-akiras chafetz} \u8212- when a person who has been stationary begins to move, that is treated as the object being lifted from its resting place. If he truly paused for rest, the object effectively settled; his subsequent movement constitutes a new {\i akira}. If he only adjusted without pausing, no such resetting occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that one who picks up the wallet, pauses, and then carries it out would be {\i chayav} \u8212- because the theft and the carrying process are separated \u8212- the Gemara asks again why the {\i braisa} chose the unusual case of dragging rather than formulating a simpler distinction within the normal pickup case. Specifically, {\i niflog venisni bedidei} \u8212- let the {\i braisa} draw the distinction internally: one who picks it up and carries it straight out without stopping is {\i patur} (subsumed in one process), but one who pauses for rest is {\i chayav} (two distinct acts). Why introduce the strange case of dragging?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i ben Azzai hi} \u8212- this {\i braisa} follows the view of Ben Azzai, who holds {\i mehalach keomed dami} \u8212- that walking is like standing. According to Ben Azzai, every step a person takes constitutes a momentary pause. Each footfall is a brief settling, so continuous walking is a series of micro-stops rather than one unbroken motion. This means that the only act truly qualifying as {\i akira} from {\i reshus hayachid} to {\i reshus harabim} is the single step crossing the threshold \u8212- the step just before the line is the {\i akira}, and the step over the line is the {\i hanacha}. There is no extended unified process that begins at the original pickup; each step resets. Consequently, picking up the wallet inside the house and walking it out is never a case where the theft and the {\i Shabbos} violation form part of the same continuous process, because every step effectively interrupts the process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The natural question follows: if walking is like standing under Ben Azzai\u8217's principle, how does one ever become liable for carrying {\i arba amos} in {\i reshus harabim}, since every step would reset the process? The {\i Yerushalmi} addresses this, suggesting that according to Ben Azzai, liability for the {\i arba amos} carrying would require actually jumping the four {\i amos} in one motion. Alternatively, for the {\i reshus hayachid}-to-{\i reshus harabim} transfer, only that single crossing step is the act \u8212- and it is enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under Ben Azzai\u8217's framework, then, in the normal case of picking up a wallet inside the house and walking it out, the theft (the {\i hagba\u8217'ah}) and the {\i Shabbos} violation (the final crossing step) are never part of the same unified act. They are invariably separate events, and the thief is always {\i chayav} for the monetary penalty. The {\i braisa} needed a case where they genuinely coincide \u8212- and that case is dragging, or alternatively, throwing it out. Yet the {\i braisa} chose dragging rather than throwing, and the Gemara explains why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara briefly raises the possibility of formulating the novel case as throwing rather than dragging \u8212- {\i aval zorek, patur} \u8212- since throwing, like the arrow, constitutes a unified process from the moment of release. But the Gemara deflects: {\i megarer deika itztaricha lei} \u8212- the case of dragging was specifically needed as a teaching in its own right. One might have thought that dragging an object along the ground is a {\i shinui}, an unusual mode of carrying, and that one would therefore be exempt from {\i Shabbos} liability. The {\i braisa} comes to teach that depending on the size of the object, dragging can be a perfectly normal mode of transport. If the object is large, dragging is obviously normal; if tiny, it is obviously abnormal. The {\i braisa} addresses the intermediate case \u8212- {\i b\u8217'mitziei} \u8212- where the object is medium-sized and dragging is just barely within the range of normal handling, sufficient to constitute {\i hotza\u8217'ah} for {\i Shabbos} purposes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dragging Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Destinations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established why dragging is the case of choice, the Gemara now asks the central structural question: {\i u\u8217'd\u8217'afkeih l\u8217'heicha?} \u8212- where exactly did the thief drag the wallet? This is not a casual question. As established above, {\i chillul Shabbos} requires transfer from {\i reshus hayachid} to {\i reshus harabim}, while {\i geneiva} \u8212- without {\i hagba\u8217'ah} \u8212- requires bringing the object into a space where the thief exercises some measure of control. These requirements seem to pull in opposite directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the thief dragged it straight into the major public thoroughfare: {\i issur Shabbos ika, issur geneiva leika} \u8212- there is a {\i Shabbos} violation, but no {\i geneiva}, because the public thoroughfare is not a space over which the thief has any personal control. If he dragged it into his own {\i reshus hayachid}: {\i issur geneiva ika, issur Shabbos leika} \u8212- there is {\i geneiva}, but no {\i Shabbos} violation. And if he dragged it from one {\i reshus hayachid} into another adjacent {\i reshus hayachid}, there is neither.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thoroughfare Sides\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then proposes: {\i d\u8217'afkeih l\u8217'tzidei reshus harabim} \u8212- he dragged it onto the sides of the public thoroughfare, the edges or shoulders flanking the main road. But this immediately raises the question of which authority governs. {\i K\u8217'man?} Like whom?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Eliezer, the sides of {\i reshus harabim} are legally equivalent to {\i reshus harabim} itself. Under that view, {\i issur Shabbos ika} \u8212- there is a {\i Shabbos} violation \u8212- but {\i issur geneiva leika} \u8212- still no {\i geneiva}, since it remains {\i reshus harabim}. According to the Rabbis, the sides are not equivalent to {\i reshus harabim}, in which case {\i issur geneiva ika} \u8212- one can acquire there \u8212- but {\i issur Shabbos leika} \u8212- no {\i Shabbos} violation. So the problem appears equally intractable under either authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this elegantly: {\i l\u8217'olam k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- the {\i braisa} follows Rabbi Eliezer. But Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's equation of the sides with {\i reshus harabim} applies only for purposes of {\i chillul Shabbos} \u8212- {\i hani mili l\u8217'chiyuva d\u8217'Shabbos} \u8212- because {\i d\u8217'zimnin d\u8217'dachki rabim v\u8217'asu l\u8217'hatam}, sometimes the press of the crowd overflows from the thoroughfare onto the shoulders, and the public uses them. This makes the sides sufficiently connected to, and occasionally part of, the main public artery that they count as {\i reshus harabim} for {\i Shabbos} purposes. However, {\i aval l\u8217'inyan mikna} \u8212- for purposes of acquisition \u8212- {\i d\u8217'la shechichi rabim} \u8212- since most of the time the public is not crowding those edges, the space is quiet enough that a person standing there has a measure of personal control over his immediate surroundings. He can therefore effect a {\i kinyan} there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates the solution: a space that simultaneously satisfies the {\i Shabbos} requirement (it is treated as {\i reshus harabim} for {\i chillul Shabbos}) and the {\i geneiva} requirement (it affords enough private control to effect acquisition). The thief drags the wallet from the owner\u8217's house across the threshold into this border space \u8212- the {\i simta}, in Rashi\u8217's terminology \u8212- and at that single moment both the {\i chillul Shabbos} and the {\i geneiva} are complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand Acquisition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers an alternative resolution: {\i k\u8217'gon shetziref yado l\u8217'mata mishlosha v\u8217'kiblo} \u8212- the case is one where the thief, as the wallet crosses into {\i reshus harabim}, reaches down with his hand and catches it. The legal principle underlying this is {\i kinyan yad} \u8212- if an object is on one\u8217's person, in one\u8217's hand, it is as though it is in one\u8217's own domain. Just as I can acquire an object by bringing it into my yard, I can acquire an object that comes to rest upon my person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key specification is that the hand must be kept below three {\i tefachim} from the ground. If the thief were to raise the wallet higher than three {\i tefachim} in order to catch it in his hand, that lifting would itself constitute {\i hagba\u8217'ah}, and the {\i geneiva} would be complete before the crossing into {\i reshus harabim} \u8212- which is the {\i Shabbos} violation \u8212- meaning the two offenses would once again be sequential rather than simultaneous. By keeping the hand below three {\i tefachim}, no {\i hagba\u8217'ah} occurs, and the moment the object lands in the thief\u8217's hand in {\i reshus harabim} is the moment both offenses are consummated together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Hand Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This solution draws on a principle of Rava: {\i yado shel adam chashuva lo k\u8217'arba al arba} \u8212- a person\u8217's hand is considered equivalent to a four-by-four {\i tefachim} space for {\i Shabbos} purposes. An object resting in one\u8217's hand is legally treated as though it has been set down on the ground, satisfying the {\i hanacha} requirement. Simultaneously, by virtue of {\i kinyan yad}, the object is acquired. So catching the wallet in one\u8217's hand in {\i reshus harabim} accomplishes both {\i geneiva} and {\i chillul Shabbos} at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina takes a more radical position. None of these special mechanisms \u8212- {\i tzidei reshus harabim} or catching in the hand \u8212- are necessary. {\i L\u8217'olam d\u8217'afkeih l\u8217'reshus harabim} \u8212- the simple case is that the thief dragged it straight into {\i reshus harabim}. And {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'reshus harabim nami kana} \u8212- one can actually effect acquisition even in {\i reshus harabim}. This challenges everything assumed up to this point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geneiva vs Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina and Rav Acha debate how to read this position, and both ground their dispute in a {\i mishna}. The {\i mishna} discusses a case of someone stealing an animal: {\i haya moshcho ve\u8217'yotze}, he was dragging it out of the owner\u8217's property. The animal then dies. If it died {\i b\u8217'reshus ba\u8217'alin} \u8212- while still in the owner\u8217's domain \u8212- the thief is {\i patur}, exempt. If he already performed {\i hagba\u8217'ah}, lifting it, or {\i she\u8217'hotzi\u8217'o} \u8212- took it out \u8212- and it then died, he is {\i chayav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question driving the debate between Ravina and Rav Acha is: what does it mean to have \u8220"taken it out\u8221" so as to become {\i chayav}? Where must the animal end up?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina deyiek me\u8217'reisha} \u8212- Ravina derives his interpretation from the opening clause of the {\i mishna}. The {\i mishna} says: if it died {\i b\u8217'reshus ba\u8217'alin}, in the owner\u8217's domain, {\i patur}. The implication of this phrasing, as Ravina reads it, is: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'meis b\u8217'reshus ba\u8217'alin} \u8212- the only reason for exemption is that it died while still in the owner\u8217's domain. {\i Ha hotzio me\u8217'reshus ba\u8217'alin, u\u8217'meis chayav} \u8212- as soon as it is removed from the owner\u8217's domain, even if it enters {\i reshus harabim}, the thief becomes {\i chayav}. The emphasis falls entirely on removal from the victim\u8217's domain. Where the object goes next is irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Acha deyiek me\u8217'seifa} \u8212- Rav Acha derives from the closing clause. The {\i mishna} states: {\i higbi\u8217'o o shehotzio} \u8212- he lifted it or he took it out. Rav Acha reads this as {\i hotza\u8217'ah dumiya d\u8217'hagba\u8217'ah} \u8212- the act of \u8220"taking out\u8221" must be analogous to {\i hagba\u8217'ah}. Just as {\i hagba\u8217'ah} means bringing the object fully into one\u8217's own control, so too {\i hotza\u8217'ah} \u8212- removal \u8212- must result in the object coming into the thief\u8217's control. It is insufficient merely to remove the object from the owner\u8217's sphere; the thief must bring it into his own sphere. Therefore, dragging it into {\i reshus harabim} alone does not constitute {\i geneiva}, because {\i reshus harabim} is nobody\u8217's controlled domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This debate is subtle and important. It would be a mistake to read this as a disagreement about {\i kinyan} in the ordinary sense. The assumption throughout {\i Shas} is that one cannot acquire an object in {\i reshus harabim} without {\i hagba\u8217'ah} or {\i yad}. Neither Ravina nor Rav Acha is challenging that general principle of property law. The dispute is specifically about the {\i definition of geneiva}. Does committing {\i geneiva} require an act structurally equivalent to a {\i kinyan} \u8212- taking the object out of the victim\u8217's control and into the thief\u8217's control? Or is {\i geneiva} defined solely from the victim\u8217's perspective: once the object is removed from the victim\u8217's domain, the theft is complete, regardless of where it goes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina holds the latter: the act of {\i geneiva} is the deprivation of the owner\u8217's possession. The moment the object exits the owner\u8217's {\i reshus}, the theft is complete. This is a perfectly coherent reading \u8212- from the victim\u8217's standpoint, what matters is that they no longer have control, not that the thief has assumed it. Under this view, dragging the wallet straight into {\i reshus harabim} causes {\i geneiva} and {\i chillul Shabbos} to coincide immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha holds the former: {\i geneiva} must mirror {\i kinyan}, requiring that the thief bring the object into his own control. Taking it out and leaving it in {\i reshus harabim} \u8212- where no one controls it \u8212- is not yet a completed act of theft.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then resolves how each Amora handles the textual difficulty posed by the other\u8217's preferred clause. The beginning of the {\i mishna} is not difficult for Rav Acha: he reads \u8220"owner\u8217's domain\u8221" expansively \u8212- {\i kama d\u8217'lo asyei l\u8217'reshusei, reshus ba\u8217'alin karinan beih} \u8212- as long as the object has not entered the thief\u8217's domain, it is still considered as though it remains in the owner\u8217's. And the end of the {\i mishna} is not difficult for Ravina: {\i hotza\u8217'ah dumiya d\u8217'hagba\u8217'ah lo aminan} \u8212- he simply does not accept the analogy between {\i hotza\u8217'ah} and {\i hagba\u8217'ah}. {\i Hagba\u8217'ah} is one method of theft effected within the owner\u8217's domain; {\i hotza\u8217'ah} is another method \u8212- removal from the owner\u8217's domain \u8212- and the two need not be structurally identical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note that this entire extended {\i sugya} \u8212- the arrow case, the wallet dragging, the analysis of {\i tzidei reshus harabim}, the debate between Rav Acha and Ravina \u8212- is a construction of the {\i stam}, the anonymous editorial voice of the Gemara. There is almost no case where an Amora steps forward to make a direct statement in his own right. The entire analysis is the {\i stam} weaving together statements made by various authorities in other contexts, forming a unified {\i sugya} around the concept of {\i kiymah lei} and the question of what it means for two prohibitions to arise \u8220"at the same time\u8221" or as part of \u8220"the same process.\u8221" Notably, the phrase {\i kiymah lei} itself never actually appears in this entire discussion \u8212- the concept has been explored throughout without the term ever being invoked.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Incest & Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incest Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions to a new application of the same underlying principle. The case is one who engages in sexual relations with his sister or his father\u8217's sister \u8212- an {\i issur kares}. Despite the severity of the prohibition, the Gemara records that such a person nonetheless pays the {\i knas}, the statutory fine associated with forbidden relations. The reason is that {\i kares} is a heavenly punishment; it does not on its own preclude a court-imposed monetary penalty. This is consistent with the framework established earlier in the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ain Lokeh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara now raises a different and sharper challenge. A {\i braisa} enumerates a series of forbidden sexual relationships whose violation is punishable by {\i malkus} \u8212- lashes administered by the court. These include relations with one\u8217's sister, father\u8217's sister, mother\u8217's sister, wife\u8217's sister, brother\u8217's wife, uncle\u8217's wife, and a {\i niddah}. And the Gemara states the established principle: {\i de\u8217'eino lokeh umishalem} \u8212- one who receives lashes does not also pay. This is {\i ain lokeh u\u8217'meshalem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge is immediate. If a man who has relations with his sister is subject to {\i malkus} \u8212- a real, court-administered punishment \u8212- then by the principle of {\i ain lokeh u\u8217'meshalem}, he should not also pay the monetary fine. Yet the {\i mishna} appears to say he does pay. This is a genuine tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to note a distinction that clarifies the structure of the question. The {\i kiymah lei} principle discussed throughout this {\i sugya} \u8212- the capital crime overriding monetary liability \u8212- operates even when the court never actually administers the punishment. The act is of sufficient gravity that one is considered \u8220"already situated in\u8221" the greater punishment, and the monetary obligation falls away. The principle of {\i ain lokeh u\u8217'meshalem}, by contrast, seems to be triggered specifically by actual administration of {\i malkus}: if the court actually gives you lashes, it will not also make you pay. But even that distinction may be too simple, and the Gemara is pressing the question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla begins to provide an answer. {\i Amar Ulla lo kashya} \u8212- Ulla says there is no difficulty. {\i Kan be\u8217'achoto na\u8212-} The Gemara cuts off at that point. Ulla appears to be drawing a distinction between different sub-cases within the list \u8212- perhaps between his sister as a {\i na\u8217'arah} (a minor girl subject to the fine) and other configurations. The full development of Ulla\u8217's answer will be taken up in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 32\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lashes vs.\u160 ?Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incest/Rape Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} opens by picking up from the bottom of 31b and the beginning of 32a, in the context of a broader discussion that has been unfolding across the preceding pages. The {\i mishna} has established that one who rapes a woman to whom he is forbidden under penalty of {\i kares} \u8212- such as his sister or his father\u8217's sister \u8212- still owes the monetary fine ({\i knas}) applicable in cases of rape of a {\i na\u8217'arah}. The {\i gemara} has already wrestled with the position of Rabbi Nechunya ben Ha-kanah, who holds that liability for {\i kares} overrides monetary liability, just as liability for court-administered execution does. That discussion occupied the preceding {\i sugya}. Now a new dimension of the problem comes into focus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i tannaitic} source from the tractate dealing with those who receive lashes: {\i elu hen ha-lokin} \u8212- the following people receive lashes \u8212- and proceeds to list precisely the cases of forbidden relationships that the {\i mishna} in Ketubot addresses: one who has relations with his sister, his father\u8217's sister, his mother\u8217's sister, his wife\u8217's sister, his brother\u8217's wife, his father\u8217's brother\u8217's wife, and a woman in a state of {\i niddah}. All of these carry the punishment of {\i kares}, and when prosecuted by the court, that punishment takes the form of lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Lashes & Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is now apparent. The {\i mishna} in Ketubot says these individuals pay the monetary fine. The other {\i mishna} says they receive lashes. And we have an established principle \u8212- {\i ein lokeh u-meshalem} \u8212- that the court does not administer both lashes and monetary payment to the same person for the same act. So which is it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to distinguish this problem from the one treated earlier. The principle of {\i kim lei bidravah minei} \u8212- the override of a lesser liability by a greater one \u8212- operates on the level of intrinsic severity: an act deserving of execution or {\i kares} is weighty enough to negate the concurrent monetary liability altogether, regardless of whether the court actually administers any punishment. That was Rabbi Nechunya ben Ha-kanah\u8217's position. The present question is different in character. It concerns the {\i actual administration} of punishment by the court: the court simply will not do both things to the same person. But this raises the question of which one yields. The easiest answer \u8212- and one the {\i gemara} will indeed offer \u8212- is that in our {\i mishna}\u8217's case, warning ({\i hatra\u8217'ah}) was never given, so lashes cannot be administered, and the monetary obligation therefore stands uncontested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Lokeh u-Meshalem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} presses harder: even if we assume a case where the court {\i was} prepared to administer lashes \u8212- where all procedural requirements including {\i hatra\u8217'ah} were met \u8212- how does the {\i mishna} justify requiring payment? Once we have a case deserving of lashes, does the rule of {\i ein lokeh u-meshalem} not eliminate the monetary obligation? This is the core tension the {\i sugya} will now explore.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ulla\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Na\u8217'arah vs.\u160 ?Bogeret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla offers the first resolution. The apparent contradiction between the two {\i mishnayot} dissolves once we distinguish between two different scenarios. When does one pay the fine? In the case of {\i achoto na\u8217'arah} \u8212- his sister who is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, a girl between twelve and twelve-and-a-half, to whom the special {\i knas} of the Torah applies. When does one receive lashes? In the case of {\i achoto bogeret} \u8212- his adult sister, who is past the age of {\i na\u8217'arah}, for whom no {\i knas} exists. Since there is no monetary obligation in that case, lashes can be administered without conflict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution, however, implicitly reflects a significant position on Ulla\u8217's part: when both lashes and payment apply, it is the {\i payment} that takes precedence, not the lashes. The {\i mishna} that speaks of payment is teaching that even where lashes are in principle warranted \u8212- even where warning was given \u8212- the monetary obligation overrides and the lashes are not administered. Lashes appear only where there is no monetary obligation at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boshet u-Pegam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this. Even in the case of a {\i bogeret}, where the special {\i knas} of fifty {\i shekel} does not apply, there remain other monetary obligations. Under {\i halacha}, rape is treated as an act of physical violence against a person, subject to the same framework of compensatory payments as any such act. Even without the special fine of the {\i na\u8217'arah betula}, the rapist still owes {\i boshet} \u8212- compensation for embarrassment \u8212- and {\i pegam} \u8212- compensation for the injury to the victim\u8217's dignity and diminished value, assessed analogously to the way one evaluates physical damage. This {\i pegam} is calculated as the difference between the market value of a slave woman who is a virgin and one who is not, an admittedly imperfect but legally operative measure of the harm inflicted. Beyond these two, there is also {\i tza\u8217'ar} \u8212- payment for pain and suffering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If all of these payments remain due even in the case of a {\i bogeret}, then even there the conflict between lashes and monetary obligation persists, and Ulla\u8217's distinction collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensual Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by narrowing the case further: {\i b\u8217'shotah} \u8212- in the case of a woman who is mentally incapacitated. Such a person would not experience embarrassment in the legally cognizable sense and therefore {\i boshet} would not apply. The {\i gemara} then anticipates the follow-up: even a {\i shotah} experiences physical pain, so {\i tza\u8217'ar} would remain. The answer: {\i bimfutah} \u8212- in a case where the act was consensual, not rape. In that case there is no {\i tza\u8217'ar} owed, and indeed no compelled harm at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the {\i gemara} arrives at the scenario of consensual relations, it draws a further conclusion. {\i Hashta d\u8217'atit l\u8217'hachi} \u8212- now that we have established that it must be a consensual case \u8212- the woman need not even be a {\i bogeret}. She could be a {\i na\u8217'arah}, provided she is a {\i yetomah u-mfutah} \u8212- an orphan who acts willingly. An orphan girl, even if she is only twelve years old, is legally emancipated from paternal authority since her father is deceased, and her own consent therefore governs. In such a case there is no {\i knas}, no {\i boshet}, no {\i tza\u8217'ar} \u8212- no monetary liability of any kind \u8212- and so lashes can be administered without conflict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of Ulla\u8217's position is now fully articulated: {\i kol heicha de\u8217'ika mammon u-malkus, mammona meshalem malka lo laki} \u8212- wherever there is both monetary liability and liability for lashes, one pays the money and does not receive lashes. The {\i mishna} that speaks of payment reflects cases where money is owed; the {\i mishna} that speaks of lashes reflects cases where no money is owed. This is the rule according to Ulla: money overrides lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Overrides\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what is Ulla\u8217's source for this principle? The answer is that he derives it by analogy from the case of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro} \u8212- one who physically injures another person. That case is paradigmatic: there is a negative prohibition against striking another person, derived by a {\i kal va-chomer} from the verse {\i lo yosif lehakkoto} which limits the lashes administered by the court, and yet the {\i halacha} requires monetary payment rather than lashes. This is taken as a model for the general rule that monetary obligation takes precedence over lashes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chovel b\u8217'Chaveiro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Payments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the paradigm of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro} more carefully. One who injures another person is liable for up to five distinct categories of payment: {\i nezek} (damages, assessed by the difference in the injured person\u8217's value as a slave before and after the injury), {\i tza\u8217'ar} (pain and suffering), {\i boshet} (embarrassment), {\i ripuy} (medical expenses), and {\i shevet} (lost income during the period of recuperation). These five payments make the monetary obligation in cases of injury particularly weighty. At the same time, the prohibition against striking carries a potential lashes-based sanction. The {\i halacha}, however, requires payment rather than lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Money?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges Ulla\u8217's use of this case as a general paradigm. The argument from {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro} can be read in two opposite ways, depending on how one ranks lashes against monetary payment in terms of severity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If monetary payment is considered the {\i more severe} punishment, then the rule that one pays rather than receives lashes in cases of injury could reflect the exceptional weightiness of the injury case \u8212- five separate payments make it an unusually heavy monetary liability \u8212- and one might therefore conclude that in less weighty cases, lashes would apply rather than payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if monetary payment is the {\i more lenient} option, then the injury case might be exceptional for a different reason: {\i shekein hutrah mikhlalah b\u8217'veit din} \u8212- the prohibition against striking is not absolute, since the court itself is permitted to administer lashes. Because the prohibition is not categorical, it is not as severe a transgression, and therefore the Torah imposes the lighter sanction of monetary payment in this context. But other prohibitions \u8212- like that of incest \u8212- are absolute, and one might say the Torah would insist on the heavier sanction of lashes there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Either way, the injury case cannot serve as a clean paradigm from which to derive a universal rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edim Zomemin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then proposes a second paradigm: {\i edim zomemin}, false witnesses. When witnesses are found to have testified falsely, they are subject to the sanction of {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam} \u8212- do to them what they intended to do to the accused \u8212- which in a monetary case means paying the sum of money they sought to extract from the accused. They also transgress the prohibition of {\i lo sa\u8217'aneh b\u8217'rei\u8217'acha eid shaker}, which carries lashes. Yet the {\i halacha} requires monetary payment under {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam} and not lashes. Here too, then, we have a case where money overrides lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If monetary payment is considered more severe, one might argue that {\i edim zomemin} are an exceptional case because they do not require {\i hatra\u8217'ah} \u8212- warning is not needed for the sanction of {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam} to apply. The absence of a warning requirement is itself a marker of the heightened severity of this category, and therefore it is not surprising that they receive the harsher sanction of monetary payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If monetary payment is considered more lenient, one might argue that {\i edim zomemin} are exceptional because {\i lo asu ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- they committed no physical act, only speech \u8212- and therefore the Torah goes easy on them, requiring only the lighter sanction of monetary payment. But this leniency would not extend to someone who commits a physical act such as incest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Denominator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Anticipating these objections to each individual case, the {\i gemara} proposes a {\i tzad hashaveh} \u8212- a common-denominator argument. Even if each of the two paradigms has its own special features, the one thing they share is precisely the relevant feature: each involves both monetary liability and lashes, and in each case the outcome is monetary payment rather than lashes. That common denominator should therefore yield the general rule: {\i kol heicha de\u8217'ika mamona u-malkus, mamona meshalem u-malka lo laki}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Special Features\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chovel Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the {\i tzad hashaveh} argument as well. When constructing a common-denominator argument, one must eliminate all features that distinguish the cases from each other and from the general rule one seeks to derive. But here, each case retains a distinctive feature that can explain the outcome independently. In the case of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro}, one can point to the five payments as a distinguishing feature \u8212- whether as a marker of weightiness (if money is more severe) or as a leniency trigger (the prohibition having exceptions in the context of court-administered lashes).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edim Zomemin Features\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of {\i edim zomemin}, one can point either to the absence of a {\i hatra\u8217'ah} requirement (a marker of severity) or to the purely verbal nature of the transgression (a marker of leniency). Each case, in other words, has a feature that explains why {\i that} case results in monetary payment rather than lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not a General Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because each case can be explained by its own special feature, the {\i tzad hashaveh} fails to establish a general rule. As Tosafot notes, taken to its logical conclusion this objection would seem to undermine the {\i tzad hashaveh} as a tool of derivation entirely, since one could always point to some distinctive feature of each case. Tosafot addresses this difficulty in their commentary. In any case, the {\i gemara} concludes here that neither paradigm individually nor their combination is sufficient to establish a universal rule. The question of where Ulla derives his position thus remains open, and the {\i gemara} now turns to a different type of derivation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezeirah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tachat Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla in fact derives his rule through a {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- a verbal analogy between two verses that share a common term. The verse describing the rapist\u8217's monetary obligation reads {\i tachat asher innah} \u8212- because he afflicted her \u8212- using the word {\i tachat}. The verse establishing the principle of monetary compensation for physical injury reads {\i ayin tachat ayin} \u8212- an eye for an eye \u8212- also using the word {\i tachat}. By this {\i gezeirah shavah}, Ulla links the two contexts: just as {\i ayin tachat ayin} is interpreted by {\i chazal} to mean monetary payment rather than literal retaliation, and just as that monetary payment takes precedence over any lashes-based sanction, so too in the case of rape \u8212- {\i ayin tachat ayin} being the locus classicus for monetary payment overriding lashes \u8212- the monetary obligation of the rapist takes precedence over the lashes he would otherwise receive for the associated prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Rape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offer an important interpretive observation here. It would be a striking and counterintuitive position to hold, as a universal principle across all of Torah, that monetary payment always overrides lashes whenever both apply. The evidence for such a sweeping rule is thin. Tosafot therefore suggest that at this point in the {\i sugya}, the {\i gemara} is shifting to a more limited claim: Ulla\u8217's {\i gezeirah shavah} from {\i tachat} to {\i tachat} is not establishing a general principle at all. Rather, it is a derivation specific to the case of rape. When rape coincides with a prohibition that would otherwise carry lashes \u8212- such as rape of a sister \u8212- Ulla holds that the monetary obligation for the rape takes precedence and the lashes are not administered. This is analogous to the case of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro}, where the logic is that compensating the victim takes priority over punishing the perpetrator through lashes. Ulla extends this logic to rape by means of the {\i gezeirah shavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, Ulla may well concede the general rule that lashes override monetary payment; his claim is confined to the specific case of rape and incest occurring together. In that case alone, the monetary obligation of the rape is treated like the monetary obligation of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro} \u8212- it takes precedence, and the lashes are not administered.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Warning Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan takes a fundamentally different position. His resolution of the conflict between the two {\i mishnayot} does not require distinguishing between {\i na\u8217'arah} and {\i bogeret} at all. He says: {\i afilu tima achoto na\u8217'arah} \u8212- even if we are dealing with his sister who is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, the classic case where both the {\i knas} and the lashes theoretically apply, there is no contradiction. {\i Kan she-hitru bo, kan she-lo hitru bo} \u8212- in one case warning was given, in the other it was not. When warning was given and lashes can be administered, lashes are given and money is not paid. When warning was not given and lashes cannot be administered, money is paid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means Rabbi Yochanan holds the opposite of Ulla\u8217's general rule: {\i kol heicha de\u8217'ika mamona u-malkuta ve-atru bei, milka laki mamona lo meshalem} \u8212- wherever there is both monetary liability and liability for lashes and warning was given, lashes are administered and money is not paid. Lashes override monetary payment, not the reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes Primary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical disagreement between Ulla and Rabbi Yochanan comes into sharp focus in the case of rape combined with incest where warning was given. For Ulla, even in that case the monetary obligation takes precedence and the man pays rather than receiving lashes. For Rabbi Yochanan, once warning was given, lashes are the operative sanction and no monetary payment is made.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedei Rishato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what is Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's source for the principle that lashes override monetary payment? He derives it from the verse {\i kedei rishato} \u8212- according to his wickedness \u8212- which appears in the context of lashes. The verse says to administer lashes commensurate with his wickedness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Wickedness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} derives: {\i mishum rish\u8217'ah achat atah mechayvo} \u8212- you make him liable on account of one act of wickedness, {\i ve-i atah mechayvo mishum shtei rishayot} \u8212- but not for two acts of wickedness arising from the same transgression. The verse singles out {\i his} wickedness \u8212- {\i rish\u8217'ato}, in the singular \u8212- indicating that for any single act, only one form of punishment is administered. And then, immediately adjacent in the text, the verse continues with the instruction to administer lashes. The juxtaposition teaches that when a choice must be made, lashes is the punishment imposed \u8212- lashes being identified as the primary punishment for a single act of wickedness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note an apparent tension: the {\i gemara} in Makkot discusses cases where a single act triggers liability for multiple prohibitions, resulting in multiple sets of lashes \u8212- if one eats a certain insect, one might be liable under several overlapping prohibitions simultaneously. So multiple lashes are possible. The principle here is narrower: it is specifically the combination of lashes and {\i monetary payment} that cannot coexist. Two separate lashes-based sanctions, each arising from a distinct prohibition, can both be administered. What cannot happen is lashes for one dimension of the act and monetary payment for another dimension of the same act. The verse {\i kedei rishato} teaches that these two distinct modalities of punishment cannot be combined.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Personal Injury\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perutah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's general rule is that lashes take precedence over monetary payment, how does he explain the exception of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro}, where the {\i halacha} clearly requires monetary payment and not lashes? One might suggest that this exception applies only where {\i hatra\u8217'ah} was not given \u8212- in which case lashes cannot be administered and monetary payment simply fills the vacuum. But if {\i hatra\u8217'ah} was given, perhaps even in a case of personal injury, one would give lashes and not require payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Any Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This suggestion is foreclosed by a teaching transmitted by Rav Ami in the name of Rabbi Yochanan himself: {\i haka\u8217'ah she\u8217'ein bah shaveh perutah lokeh} \u8212- if one struck another person in a manner that inflicted a wound of less than a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth of damage, the perpetrator receives lashes. This ruling is striking in its irony: the most trivial act of physical aggression, one that caused essentially no quantifiable harm whatsoever \u8212- no {\i nezek}, no {\i tza\u8217'ar}, no {\i shevet}, no {\i ripuy}, no {\i boshet} of any monetary significance \u8212- results in forty lashes. Meanwhile, cutting off a person\u8217's arm results in monetary payment with no lashes. The more serious the injury, the more certain one is to pay rather than be lashed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling necessarily implies that the case involves {\i hatra\u8217'ah} \u8212- if there were no warning, lashes could not be imposed for any reason. And yet lashes are imposed. This means that even {\i with} warning, the general rule in cases of personal injury is monetary payment \u8212- but in the singular edge case where no monetary obligation whatsoever exists, not even a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth, lashes apply. The moment there is any monetary obligation \u8212- even the smallest amount \u8212- monetary payment takes over and lashes are not administered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings Rabbi Yochanan back to his overarching position. His general rule is lashes over payment. The case of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro} is an exception, but it requires a textual basis \u8212- the Torah must explicitly identify it as such. And indeed, as Rabi Ila states, {\i bifiresh ribtah Torah eidim zomemim l\u8217'tashlumin} \u8212- the Torah explicitly includes {\i edim zomemin} in the category of monetary payment \u8212- and the same principle applies to {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro}: {\i bifiresh ribtah Torah chovel b\u8217'chaveiro l\u8217'tashlumin} \u8212- the Torah explicitly mandates monetary payment in cases of personal injury, making it an explicit exception to the general rule.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Edim Zomemin Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Witness Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines where exactly the Torah makes this explicit statement regarding {\i edim zomemin}. The relevant verse reads: {\i ve-asitem lo ka\u8217'asher zamam la\u8217'asot le\u8217'achiv} \u8212- do to him as he plotted to do to his fellow. This already establishes the principle of {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam}. Why then does the verse continue with the phrase {\i yad beyad} \u8212- hand for hand?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i edim zomemin} involves a range of possible sanctions, since the witnesses may have sought to impose upon the accused any manner of punishment \u8212- death, lashes, or monetary liability. The continuation of the verse, {\i yad beyad regel beregel ayin be\u8217'ayin}, might be read as a list of retaliatory penalties to be imposed literally. Rabi Meir indeed holds that false witnesses who sought to have the accused executed receive lashes (for the prohibition of {\i eidut shaker}) {\i and} pay monetary compensation as separate sanctions arising from distinct legal categories, since the prohibition of false testimony and the sanction of {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam} are legally independent of one another: one can transgress the prohibition of {\i lo sa\u8217'aneh} without being subject to the sanction of {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam} (e.g., if the procedural requirements for {\i hazamah} are not met), and the {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam} penalty is not a punishment for the personal transgression but a broader institutional mechanism for protecting the integrity of judicial proceedings. The rabbis, however \u8212- the position the {\i gemara} adopts \u8212- hold that one pays and does not receive lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand for Hand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The textual basis for the rabbinic position is derived from the phrase {\i yad beyad} itself. The {\i gemara} asks: given that the verse has already established the principle of {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam}, what additional meaning does {\i yad beyad} contribute? The answer: {\i davar hanitan miyad leyad} \u8212- something transferred from hand to hand, evoking the image of handing over money. The Torah thereby signals explicitly that the sanction of {\i edim zomemin} in monetary cases takes the form of monetary payment. And since the Torah has explicitly categorized this as a monetary obligation, and one cannot impose both lashes and monetary payment, the monetary obligation prevails and lashes are not administered.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chovel Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Injury Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the textual basis for {\i edim zomemin}\u8217's exception, the {\i gemara} turns to {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro}. The relevant verse reads: {\i ka\u8217'asher asa ken ye\u8217'aseh lo} \u8212- as he has done, so shall be done to him. This appears to mandate literal retaliation for physical injury. Why, then, does the verse then add a further phrase?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yinaten Means Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse continues: {\i ken yinaten bo} \u8212- so shall it be {\i given} in him. The {\i gemara} asks: given that the verse already stated {\i ke\u8217'asher asa ken ye\u8217'aseh lo}, why add {\i ken yinaten bo}? The answer derives from the word {\i yinaten} \u8212- {\i given}: {\i davar sheyesh bo netina}, u-{\i mai nihu mamon} \u8212- something that is given over, and what is that? Money. The word {\i yinaten}, evoking the act of handing something over, signals that the compensation for physical injury takes the form of monetary payment. This is the explicit textual basis \u8212- identified through {\i chazal}\u8217's reading \u8212- for the exception of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro}: the Torah itself has explicitly designated this as a case of monetary obligation, thereby establishing it as an exception to the general rule that lashes take precedence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Defending Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's general principle and its two explicit exceptions now established, the {\i gemara} presses a different challenge. Rabbi Yochanan holds that in the case of rape combined with incest, lashes take precedence \u8212- to pay money rather than administer lashes would, in his view, undermine the weight of the prohibition of incest, {\i bataltah ervat achoto lo tigaleh}. If money replaces lashes, the prohibition loses its deterrent force and its appropriate gravity as a sanction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this reasoning creates a difficulty with the very exceptions Rabbi Yochanan acknowledges. If paying money rather than lashing undermines the weight of a prohibition, why does he accept the exceptions of {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro} and {\i edim zomemin}? Does permitting monetary payment in those cases not equally undermine the weight of those prohibitions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What lies beneath this exchange is significant. The technical disagreements between Ulla and Rabbi Yochanan about how to read certain verses are not occurring in a vacuum. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's preference for lashes reflects a substantive jurisprudential commitment: serious sins require serious punishment, and monetary payment \u8212- whatever its practical benefits \u8212- is not an adequate substitute for the punitive weight of lashes when a genuine prohibition has been violated. This underlying philosophy shapes how he reads the relevant verses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Gerusha Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan responds to the challenge regarding {\i edim zomemin} as follows: {\i efshar lekiyumah b\u8217'ven gerusha u\u8217'ven chalutza} \u8212- it is possible to fulfill the prohibition\u8217's lashes-based sanction in the case of a {\i ben gerusha} or a {\i ben chalutza}. If the false witnesses testified that a certain {\i kohen} was a {\i ben gerusha} or {\i ben chalutza} \u8212- that is, born of a union that disqualifies priestly lineage \u8212- the sanction of {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam} cannot be applied in the normal way, because one cannot impose a status-based disqualification on witnesses who are themselves not {\i kohanim} or who already bear that disqualification. When {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam} is inapplicable, no monetary payment is due, and the witnesses instead receive lashes for the prohibition of bearing false witness. Thus the prohibition of {\i eidut sheker} remains one for which lashes can and do apply in certain configurations; the fact that monetary payment takes over in other configurations does not strip the prohibition of its lashes-based dimension. The underlying force of the prohibition is not undermined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perutah Edge Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, regarding {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro}: the edge case of {\i haka\u8217'ah she\u8217'ein bah shaveh perutah} \u8212- a wound of less than a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth \u8212- demonstrates that the prohibition against striking another person retains its lashes-based sanction. In the rare theoretical scenario where no monetary obligation whatsoever arises from the act, lashes are administered. One can therefore say that the prohibition is not undermined: it remains, at least in principle and in rare practice, a prohibition that carries the sanction of lashes. The fact that in most cases monetary payment takes over does not negate the underlying lashes-based character of the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incest Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan now turns the argument back in his own favor. If the logic for maintaining lashes as the appropriate sanction for those two categories is that there exist configurations in which lashes do apply \u8212- and this is considered sufficient to preserve the weight of the prohibition \u8212- then the same reasoning actually {\i strengthens} his position regarding incest. In the case of incest, the {\i majority} of cases will result in lashes: whenever the act is consensual, or whenever the woman is a {\i bogeret}, there is no monetary obligation and lashes are administered. The configuration where both rape and incest coincide \u8212- the scenario Ulla would resolve in favor of monetary payment \u8212- is a minority of cases. The prohibition of incest is overwhelmingly a lashes-based prohibition. To say that in this particular configuration money should replace lashes undermines a prohibition that in most of its applications carries lashes. Rabbi Yochanan therefore insists that even in the rape-and-incest case, lashes must prevail.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tachat Already Used\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes by addressing why Rabbi Yochanan does not adopt Ulla\u8217's {\i gezeirah shavah} from {\i tachat} to {\i tachat}. Rabbi Yochanan would say: {\i hai tachat asher innah} \u8212- the word {\i tachat} in the rape verse \u8212- {\i mibaya lei lekhed\u8217'Abaye}, I need that word to teach something else entirely, as Abaye derives from it a different {\i halacha} altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Deduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye derives from the phrase {\i tachat asher innah} that the fifty {\i shekel} fine paid by the rapist constitutes payment specifically for the act of sexual violation itself \u8212- and that this very specification implies, {\i michlal de\u8217'ika boshet u-pegam}, that there are additional payments owed beyond the fifty {\i shekel}, namely {\i boshet} and {\i pegam}, which remain as independent obligations. Since Rabbi Yochanan uses the word {\i tachat} for this derivation, he cannot simultaneously use it for Ulla\u8217's {\i gezeirah shavah}. Ulla, for his part, derives the same teaching about additional payments from a different verse, attributed to Rava: {\i venatan ha\u8217'ish hashochev imah la\u8217'avi ha-na\u8217'arah chamishim kasef} \u8212- the man who lay with her shall give fifty silver pieces to the father of the young woman. The phrase {\i hanat shechiva chamishim} \u8212- the fifty is for the pleasure of the act itself \u8212- indicates by implication that {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} are paid separately. With this derivation available to him, Ulla does not need {\i tachat asher innah} for that purpose and is therefore free to use it for the {\i gezeirah shavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bottom Line\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final picture is this. The general rule, as Rabbi Yochanan establishes from {\i kedei rishato}, is that when one must choose between lashes and monetary payment, lashes take precedence. The court administers lashes and does not collect monetary payment. There are two explicit exceptions \u8212- {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro} and {\i edim zomemin} \u8212- where the Torah itself mandates monetary payment through textual signals ({\i yinaten} and {\i yad beyad} respectively), making these deliberate exceptions to the general rule. In the case of rape combined with incest, Rabbi Yochanan holds that the general rule applies: lashes are administered and monetary payment is not collected. Ulla disagrees, holding \u8212- through a {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i tachat} to {\i tachat} \u8212- that in the specific case of rape, as in {\i chovel b\u8217'chaveiro}, the monetary obligation takes precedence. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, this is Ulla\u8217's limited claim: not a universal principle, but a rule confined to the rape case alone, derived through the verbal analogy and justified by the same logic that protects the victim\u8217's interests in cases of personal injury.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 35\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mechitzah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The topic of {\i mechitzah} opens onto a wide range of questions: What is its source? What are its requirements \u8212- how high must it be, must it be transparent or opaque, and when exactly is one required? These are practical, technical questions that demand careful attention. But underlying all of them is a deeper philosophical question about how we understand women\u8217's place within the synagogue. Does the {\i mechitzah} define the synagogue as a fundamentally male space, one in which women do not truly belong? Or is there another way to understand {\i mechitzah} that is not exclusionary and does not relegate women to the margins of the worship community?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Philosophy of Space\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not merely an abstract philosophical debate \u8212- it carries real consequences for how we structure our communal spaces and how we speak about them. The question, at its core, is whether women are understood as part of the {\i tzibbur} or as guests within a male domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Related Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Related questions flow naturally from this central one. Consider, for instance, the question of architectural changes to a synagogue: if a congregation wishes to expand one section at the expense of another, can the women\u8217's section be converted into men\u8217's space and vice versa? A number of {\i poskim} have ruled that converting the women\u8217's section into the men\u8217's section is permissible because it constitutes an elevation in sanctity, but that the reverse conversion \u8212- men\u8217's space into women\u8217's space \u8212- is forbidden because it represents a diminishment. Others disagree. But the very existence of that position reveals how some authorities conceptualize not merely the physical arrangement of the synagogue but the nature of women\u8217's presence within it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women & Minyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question concerns {\i davening be-tzibbur}. It is actually the ruling of almost all {\i poskim} that there is no individual obligation to {\i daven} with a {\i minyan} \u8212- the community as a whole has an obligation to maintain a regular {\i minyan}, and it is certainly preferred for individuals to {\i daven} with one, but it is not strictly obligatory on each person. The community\u8217's obligation to maintain a {\i minyan} is real and serious, particularly in a close-knit community where each person\u8217's participation is felt. But the individual {\i chet} for {\i davening} at home does not rise to the level of a formal prohibition. The more interesting question, however, is whether we say the same thing about women \u8212- that it is preferable and valuable for a woman to come to shul, to {\i daven} there, to answer {\i Kaddish} and {\i Kedushah} and hear {\i Kriat HaTorah}. Or do we say that for a woman it makes no difference whether she comes at all? On each of these questions \u8212- the sanctity of the women\u8217's section, women\u8217's encouraged attendance, and the nature of {\i mechitzah} \u8212- there is a clear division between those {\i poskim} who treat the synagogue as a fundamentally male space and those who do not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Historical Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gaonic Period\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Strikingly, in the {\i Gemara} itself there is no explicit source for the requirement of a {\i mechitzah} for {\i davening}. The first explicit references to men and women {\i davening} separately appear in the Gaonic period \u8212- roughly the sixth through eighth centuries \u8212- and even there, the mentions are incidental. For example, a discussion about whether one may accept women\u8217's testimony in the absence of men arises in the context of disputes over synagogue seating. The passage does not speak of a women\u8217's {\i section} but of a {\i Beit Knesset l\u8217'nashim} \u8212- evidently a separate structure entirely. The reference tells us that women were {\i davening} apart from men by the Gaonic period, but it tells us this only in passing. The {\i Gemara} contains no explicit ruling one way or the other on the question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Archaeology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Archaeological evidence is equally indeterminate. For many years, archaeologists discovering ancient synagogues would identify certain rooms as \u8220"women\u8217's sections\u8221" \u8212- only for later scholars to point out that this identification was entirely assumed. An additional room is simply an additional room; the label \u8220"women\u8217's section\u8221" was projected onto it based on contemporary practice rather than established from the evidence itself. What we can say is that separate {\i davening} for men and women is attested at least from the Gaonic period and possibly earlier, but the {\i Gemara} offers no explicit reference in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It only became necessary to articulate a halachic basis for {\i mechitzah} in the modern period, when the practice came under scrutiny. The {\i Chatam Sofer} \u8212- the great late-eighteenth and early-nineteenth century Hungarian authority \u8212- was one of the first to reach back into {\i Talmudic} sources and articulate a reasoned justification. He did so in the context of a responsum concerning a mixed choir that was proposed to perform for the Tsar in the main synagogue of Saint Petersburg, and in that {\i teshuvah} he discussed why men and women do not sit together in the synagogue in the first place. What had been an unquestioned practice for perhaps fifteen hundred years was now being asked to justify itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temple Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ezrat Nashim Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One major basis that was offered for the {\i mechitzah} requirement \u8212- particularly in response to the legal and social pressures of twentieth-century American Jewish life \u8212- was the model of the Temple. The argument runs as follows: the synagogue is a {\i mikdash me\u8217'at}, a temple in miniature, and therefore its architecture should mirror the Temple\u8217's arrangement of separate sections for men and women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A closer look at the Temple\u8217's structure, however, complicates this argument considerably. The {\i Ezrat Nashim} \u8212- the so-called \u8220"women\u8217's courtyard\u8221" \u8212- was in fact enormous. This size does not reflect a proportionally larger female attendance at sacrificial worship. Rather, the {\i Ezrat Nashim} functioned as a social hall. It was the gathering space where men and women mingled freely until it was time to proceed to the worship area proper. As will be discussed further, it was also the site of the {\i Simchat Beit Hasho\u8217'eiva}, the great celebration on {\i Sukkot}. The actual {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- the sacred precinct defined halachically as the temple \u8212- begins only at the wall separating the {\i Ezrat Nashim} from the {\i Ezrat Yisrael}. Everything to the east of that line was, functionally, a lobby.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mount Clemens Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Charter & Battle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The legal and communal battle that most forcefully drove Orthodox authorities to articulate explicit halachic justifications for {\i mechitzah} was the famous Mount Clemens case in Michigan. This synagogue had a charter provision stipulating that no change inconsistent with {\i halacha} could be made if at least ten members in good standing objected. In the 1950s or 1960s, as the congregation moved toward removing the {\i mechitzah} \u8212- a move that effectively marked the transition from Orthodox to Conservative affiliation \u8212- ten members invoked that clause. The question that reached the American civil courts was whether a {\i mechitzah} was in fact a halachic requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orthodox Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This legal battle played out against a backdrop in which the {\i mechitzah} had become the single most visible marker distinguishing Orthodox from Conservative synagogues in mid-twentieth century American Jewish life. Many congregations of that era had memberships and rabbis of similar backgrounds and education; the {\i mechitzah} \u8212- its presence or absence \u8212- was what defined a synagogue\u8217's denominational identity. The stakes were therefore enormous, and both sides sought the most authoritative responsa they could obtain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Responsa Collections\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Conservative side assembled responsa from the faculty and leadership of JTS, collected in an issue of {\i Conservative Judaism}. The Orthodox side gathered {\i teshuvot} from Rav Soloveitchik, Rav Moshe Feinstein, and other leading authorities of the day; these were published in a volume called {\i Sanctity of the Synagogue}, which for many years was actually distributed at one rabbinical school\u8217's {\i semichah} ceremony \u8212- a telling illustration of how central the {\i mechitzah} question had become to the definition of Orthodox rabbinic identity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temple Model (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Worship as Male Space\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to what the Temple model actually implies: if the {\i Ezrat Nashim} was not the sanctuary but the lobby \u8212- the space where mixed mingling was normal \u8212- and if the genuine worship space begins only at the {\i Ezrat Yisrael}, then using the Temple as the model for synagogue {\i mechitzah} is effectively saying that worship is a male space. Women\u8217's designated area was not the {\i Mikdash}; it was the antechamber. And the message imported into the synagogue by this model is correspondingly stark: the {\i Beit HaKnesset} is fundamentally male space, and women are present at its margins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a further, troubling detail. The section that men occupied in the Temple was not called the {\i Ezrat Gevarim} \u8212- the men\u8217's courtyard \u8212- but the {\i Ezrat Yisrael}, the Israelites\u8217' section. The name {\i Yisrael} functions in contrast to {\i Kohanim} on one level, denoting the non-priestly Israelite males; but it also implicitly contrasts with {\i Ezrat Nashim}, suggesting that it is the men who constitute {\i Klal Yisrael} in the primary sense, while women occupy a secondary or peripheral category. Even setting aside the question of whether the Temple model carries halachic weight as a normative source for synagogue architecture \u8212- and it does not, since the {\i Talmud} contains no such requirement \u8212- the model raises profound philosophical concerns about the nature of the worship space and who is understood to belong there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are also practical implications. When the {\i mechitzah} is placed at the back of the synagogue \u8212- rather than down the middle or along the sides \u8212- on the explicit grounds that this mirrors the Temple arrangement, women are pushed entirely to the periphery, effectively outside the congregation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin 52b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A striking {\i Gemara} in {\i Kiddushin} 52b illustrates the underlying assumption that the {\i Ezrat Yisrael} was a male space into which women entered only in exceptional circumstances. The passage begins with a halachic question: can a man effect {\i kiddushin} \u8212- betrothal \u8212- by giving a woman meat from his sacrifice, since he has a right to eat the meat but does not technically own it in a proprietary sense? The ruling is that such {\i kiddushin} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The context is established through a small drama. After the death of Rabbi Meir, Rabbi Yehuda told his students not to admit Rabbi Meir\u8217's disciples to their {\i Beit Midrash}, saying they were disputatious and came not to learn but to overwhelm him with questions. Nevertheless, Sumchus \u8212- one of Rabbi Meir\u8217's prize students \u8212- {\i dachak ve-nikhnas}, forced his way in, and transmitted a teaching in his master\u8217's name: if one betroths a woman using his portion of sacrificial meat \u8212- whether {\i kodshei kodashim} or {\i kodashim kalim} \u8212- the betrothal is invalid, since the meat is not truly his property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda was furious and repeated his warning. But then a logical problem arose: the case of betrothal with {\i kodshei kodashim} is particularly vexing, since those sacrifices cannot be taken out of the {\i Ezrat Yisrael} \u8212- and what would a woman be doing in the {\i Ezrat Yisrael}? Surely this is merely a theoretical puzzle, not a practical scenario. Rabbi Yosi, unwilling to remain silent while a genuine Torah teaching of Rabbi Meir went undefended, offered several solutions. Perhaps the father accepted the {\i kiddushin} on his minor daughter\u8217's behalf \u8212- a transaction between men, requiring no woman\u8217's presence at all. Or perhaps the woman had appointed a male agent to receive the {\i kiddushin} on her behalf \u8212- again, no woman need be physically present. And then the final answer: {\i v\u8217'od dachaka ve-nichnasa shema} \u8212- perhaps she simply forced her way in, just as Sumchus had forced his way into the {\i Beit Midrash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The echo is deliberate and the point is clear: the {\i Gemara} treats a woman\u8217's presence in the {\i Azara} as something so anomalous that it requires a battery of explanations, the last of which is that she simply pushed through. Women were not the expected population of that space. Their presence required justification; their absence was the default. One might observe, as was noted in discussion, that Rabbi Yosi ultimately concedes the possibility \u8212- implying that if a woman forced her way in, she could not simply be expelled, and that she had some legitimate standing to be there for the purpose for which she came. That is true, and worth noting. But it does not alter the fundamental characterization: the space was defined as male, and female presence was the exception that proved the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kook\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely the model that Rav Kook invokes in one of the responsa collected in {\i Sanctity of the Synagogue}. He writes: \u8220"According to the Talmud, the synagogues are to be looked on as the sacred temple in miniature. It is therefore our duty to exalt them to the same level of holiness as our holy temple, may it be rebuilt in our day. Our fathers in establishing two separate divisions for men and women, thereby continuing the system inaugurated in the temple and using that as the model.\u8221" That is the argument in its most direct form.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simchat Beit Hasho\u8217'eva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sukkah 51b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second major Talmudic source adduced for {\i mechitzah} is also Temple-derived but addresses a different concern. The passage is found in {\i Sukkah} 51b, and it describes the arrangements made for the {\i Simchat Beit Hasho\u8217'eiva} \u8212- the great nighttime celebration on {\i Chol HaMoed Sukkot} preceding the water-drawing ceremony. The {\i Gemara} asks what the {\i tikkun gadol} \u8212- the great arrangement \u8212- was that they would set up when {\i Yom Tov} ended. Rabbi Elazar explains: the {\i Ezrat Nashim} had originally been flat, and they surrounded it with a {\i gezustra} \u8212- apparently a balcony or elevated gallery \u8212- so that the women were above and the men below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then elaborates the historical development. Originally, women were on the inside and men on the outside. This arrangement led to {\i kalus rosh} \u8212- improper levity and mixing \u8212- because a woman making her way to her section would have to pass through the men\u8217's area. So they reversed it: men inside, women outside. But this too led to {\i kalus rosh}, since now men had to pass through the women\u8217's area. The final solution was to place women above and men below, eliminating the problem of crossing through each other\u8217's space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: how could they make a structural change to the Temple? The verse in {\i Divrei HaYamim} states that David declared the entire plan of the Temple was given to him by God \u8212- implying it cannot be altered. The answer is that they found a verse in {\i Zechariah} that prophesies the end of days: {\i ve-safda ha\u8217'aretz mishpachot mishpachot l\u8217'vad} \u8212- \u8220"the land shall mourn, family by family, apart\u8221" \u8212- and it specifies, {\i mishpachat beit David l\u8217'vad u-nesheihem l\u8217'vad}, the family of the house of David separately, and their wives separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this verse the {\i Gemara} derives a {\i kal va-chomer}: in the future, at the time of the mourning for {\i Mashiach ben Yosef}, people will be engaged in a eulogy \u8212- a solemn occasion \u8212- and moreover, the {\i yetzer ha-ra} will not dominate them as it does in this world. Yet the Torah still requires men and women to be separate. Now ({\i achshav}), when people are engaged in {\i simcha} \u8212- great joy and festivity \u8212- and the {\i yetzer ha-ra} does exert its pull: {\i al achat kama ve-kama}, how much more so must men and women be kept separate. This {\i kal va-chomer} becomes the basis for the separation enforced during the {\i Simchat Beit Hasho\u8217'eiva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This source is genuinely useful, but it presents a challenge: it is either too narrow or too broad as a basis for a general {\i mechitzah} requirement. If one focuses on the party context \u8212- the {\i Simchat Beit Hasho\u8217'eiva} \u8212- all one can establish is that mixed celebrations of that kind require separation. That tells us something important about mixed dancing and festive gatherings, but it does not straightforwardly extend to {\i davening}. If, on the other hand, one focuses on the {\i kal va-chomer} from the funeral context \u8212- the eulogies for {\i Mashiach ben Yosef} \u8212- then one seems forced to conclude that men and women must be separate at all times and in all serious contexts, a position that some {\i Haredi} communities do in fact adopt, extending the requirement even to lectures and study sessions. So the passage proves both too much and too little, depending on where one places the emphasis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatam Sofer Bridge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Directing Thoughts to God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chatam Sofer} bridges the gap between the {\i Simchat Beit Hasho\u8217'eiva} source and {\i davening} by identifying a common denominator. He argues that the requirement of separation applies whenever one is engaged in directing words or thoughts toward God \u8212- a religious activity in which the mind must be focused upward and not distracted. Prayer clearly qualifies. So does the {\i Simchat Beit Hasho\u8217'eiva}, since {\i Sukkot} joy is explicitly described in the Torah as {\i usmachtem lifnei Hashem Elokeichem} \u8212- rejoicing {\i before God} \u8212- making it a form of divine service rather than mere festivity. And a eulogy, too, carries a dimension of prayer. The {\i Chatam Sofer} thus argues that what these cases share is the directedness of the act toward God, and that in all such contexts the concern for distracted thought requires separation between men and women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Made Invisible\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific concern the {\i Chatam Sofer} identifies is that men will see women and have sexual thoughts, thereby disrupting their {\i davening}. This framing has a significant implication: the concern is entirely about the men. It is men\u8217's prayer, men\u8217's mental purity, and men\u8217's concentration that is at stake. Women are cast as the source of distraction. Since seeing women creates the problem for men, the solution is to make women invisible \u8212- to ensure that men cannot see the women\u8217's section at all. Whether women\u8217's prayer is disrupted by seeing men is a question the {\i Chatam Sofer} does not raise, reflecting a broader set of assumptions in the {\i Talmud} about whose sexual thoughts are considered a halachic concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full-Height Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of this reasoning is that the {\i mechitzah} must be full-height \u8212- ceiling to floor \u8212- so that women are completely invisible to the men. The {\i Chatam Sofer} was the dominant authority for Hungarian Jewry, and in Hungarian communities this was taken seriously enough that some refused to {\i daven} in a synagogue that had only a balcony, since a balcony actually maximizes visibility rather than eliminating it. From the perspective of the {\i Chatam Sofer\u8217's} reasoning, a balcony is among the worst possible arrangements.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Moshe Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single-Sex Space\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Moshe Feinstein offers a fundamentally different analysis. He argues that the requirement of {\i mechitzah} is not about preventing men from having sexual thoughts ({\i hirhur}) about women. The concern, he says, is not {\i hirhur} at all but {\i kalus rosh} \u8212- the qualitative difference between a single-sex environment and a co-ed environment. A co-ed space has a different social character than a single-sex space, and that difference is what is at stake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Education Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One way to understand what Rav Moshe means is through an analogy to single-sex versus co-ed education. The evidence in educational research tends to show that single-sex settings benefit girls while co-ed settings benefit boys \u8212- though the dynamic is more nuanced than any simple summary. The underlying mechanism seems to be that co-ed environments heighten self-consciousness: in a mixed setting, people become more attuned to how they are perceived by others of the opposite sex. For boys, the presence of girls tends to be moderating and self-regulating; for girls, it tends to be inhibiting \u8212- socialized patterns of deference and quietness are activated. When girls are in an all-female environment, they are less self-conscious in that particular way and more free to assert themselves intellectually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vertical Experience\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Translating this into the context of {\i davening}: Rav Soloveitchik, in a related context, spoke of wanting {\i davening} to be a vertical experience rather than a horizontal one \u8212- a turning upward toward God rather than a social awareness of those around us. The reality, of course, is that {\i shul} is often deeply horizontal \u8212- a place of reunion, conversation, and social reinforcement. But the aspiration is to create a space in which one can focus on God rather than on how one appears to others. The argument, following Rav Moshe\u8217's logic, is that a single-sex environment better enables that kind of vertical focus, for men and women alike \u8212- not because the opposite sex is a source of illicit thoughts, but because a co-ed environment is structurally more social and self-conscious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might of course push back: perhaps for some people a co-ed space is entirely natural and their {\i davening} is not enhanced by single-sex separation. Perhaps a men\u8217's section without women becomes more of a social club. These are legitimate observations, and no claim is being made that the argument is empirically airtight. But the crucial point is that Rav Moshe\u8217's framework does not define the synagogue as a male space or cast women as a problem to be managed. It says: we want single-sex prayer spaces, not co-ed ones, and that applies symmetrically to men and women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This philosophical difference translates directly into practical requirements. Since the concern is not visibility but the nature of the space, Rav Moshe rules that the {\i mechitzah} need not be full-height. Shoulder height \u8212- eighteen {\i tefachim}, translating to somewhere between fifty-four and seventy-two inches depending on the measurement of a {\i tefach}, with Rav Soloveitchik specifying fifty inches \u8212- is sufficient to create a meaningfully separate space. A balcony, far from being problematic as in the Hungarian tradition, is in Rav Moshe\u8217's view an excellent arrangement, since it clearly demarcates separate spaces even while permitting visual connection. Similarly, the {\i mechitzah} need not be opaque; transparency is not a problem because the issue is not seeing but the designation of separate spaces.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mechitzah Specificat (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Height Measurements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To recapitulate the technical parameters: Rav Moshe Feinstein specifies eighteen {\i tefachim} as the minimum {\i mechitzah} height, which translates to between fifty-four and seventy-two inches. Rav Soloveitchik set fifty inches. These figures reflect the principle that the {\i mechitzah} must be high enough to function as a genuine spatial designation \u8212- not merely a token marker \u8212- while not being required to eliminate all visual contact between sections.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'akeh Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Underlying these rulings is an important conceptual point: the {\i mechitzah} functions as a {\i ma\u8217'akeh} \u8212- a protective fence, a prophylactic measure \u8212- rather than as an absolute biblical prohibition. This means that while {\i davening} in a mixed space is not ideal and is contrary to established practice, it does not technically render one\u8217's prayer invalid. The distinction is between the character of the space one ideally creates and the validity of {\i tefillah} in imperfect circumstances. A person {\i davening} at home while men and women are present in the room is not in the same category as a congregation that has abandoned {\i mechitzah} in its synagogue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Variation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction helps explain why {\i mechitzah} requirements are applied differently in different contexts. When a group {\i davens Mincha} on a field trip, one creates separate spaces but does not erect a {\i mechitzah} in the full halachic sense. At a {\i shiva} house, if possible men and women will be on different levels or in different rooms, but if not, {\i davening} can proceed. At a wedding, similarly, one tries to arrange separate areas without necessarily requiring a formal {\i mechitzah}. None of this is inconsistent, because {\i mechitzah} in the synagogue is about the identity and character of the {\i shul} as a designated prayer space, and that is the context in which the full requirement applies. In all other contexts, the concern for appropriate separation remains, but it is calibrated to the setting. As was accurately observed in discussion, even a person {\i davening} alone in an airport, surrounded by men and women going about their business, is in a categorically different situation from a congregation {\i davening} together in a mixed setting.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Egalitarian Paradox (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sisterhood Comment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A striking observation came from a woman who noted that in certain social environments \u8212- particularly family-focused congregations organized around couples and children \u8212- she had felt excluded and peripheral. What she found, paradoxically, was that the {\i mechitzah} created a kind of {\i sisterhood} in which her marital status, family configuration, and social standing became irrelevant. Within the women\u8217's section she was simply one of the {\i tzibbur}, neither marked nor judged by the categories that structured the rest of congregational social life.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mechitzah Specificat (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Placement Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond height and opacity, the question of placement is also significant. A {\i mechitzah} running straight down the middle of the sanctuary sends a very different message from one that confines women to the back or sides. The Hebrew Institute of Riverdale, under Rabbi Weiss, aimed for an arrangement in which, when {\i davening} is not in session, a visitor cannot tell which is the men\u8217's side and which is the women\u8217's side. The {\i mechitzah} runs straight down the center; the {\i bimah} stands on an island surrounded by its own {\i mechitzah}; the two sections are visually parallel. This is not always architecturally achievable, but it represents an important aspiration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Egalitarian Paradox (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leadership Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The acknowledgment of this egalitarian structure carries with it an honest reckoning: even if the {\i mechitzah} runs down the middle and the physical space is divided equally, if men are the ones leading all of the {\i davening} and holding all of the participatory roles, it becomes very difficult for the {\i mechitzah} not to be perceived as a marker of differential status. The physical equality does not by itself resolve the deeper question of women\u8217's role within the prayer community.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cultural Definitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wide Aisle Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether a cultural marker or a wide aisle could substitute for a physical {\i mechitzah} \u8212- whether the {\i de facto} separation of spaces could be achieved without a physical partition. This is indeed a live question among {\i poskim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aisle Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda Herzl Henkin maintains that ten {\i tefachim} \u8212- roughly three feet, which in other areas of {\i halacha} is considered sufficient to designate separate domains \u8212- would suffice to define distinct prayer spaces. A small number of Orthodox synagogues have in fact operated on this basis, some with aisles as wide as thirty feet serving as the separation. Rav Moshe Feinstein, however, while agreeing that the concern is about the nature of the space rather than visibility, holds that a very low or narrow divider fails to actually create the distinct single-sex spaces that the requirement demands; the practical feeling of the space must reflect the separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inconsistency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It must be acknowledged frankly that actual practice in this area is not always logically consistent with any single underlying argument. The way {\i mechitzah} rulings developed \u8212- starting from a well-established communal practice and working backward to find Talmudic sources and conceptual foundations \u8212- means that the application of the rule does not flow neatly from one coherent principle. The result is an amalgam, and it can be difficult to {\i pasken} these questions in the way one would normally derive rulings: from the {\i Gemara} outward through the commentaries to practical application.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Egalitarian Paradox (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Variable Heights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even setting aside the theoretical questions, the practical reality is that people have different sensibilities. In one setting, when asked whether people would object to lowering an unusually high {\i mechitzah} at a {\i Limmud} {\i minyan}, the silence broke only when one woman said, plainly: \u8220"Please.\u8221" Yet in another setting, a woman expressed that she specifically did not want a lower {\i mechitzah} \u8212- she wanted to be able to {\i daven} without being conscious of men watching her, to focus inward, to connect to God without the self-consciousness that a low partition introduces. This is itself an expression of Rav Moshe\u8217's logic: the point is the environment, and different people may need different environments to achieve the vertical focus that {\i davening} demands. Accommodating this sometimes means having sections of the {\i mechitzah} at different heights, allowing people to sit where they feel most comfortable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cultural Definitions (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lower Mechitzah Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A lower {\i mechitzah} is not therefore simply and universally \u8220"more inclusive.\u8221" Inclusivity means taking seriously what different people need to {\i daven} well, and for some that means a higher barrier. The goal is not to impose a particular aesthetic or comfort level but to create a space in which all members of the congregation can genuinely engage in prayer.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sefardic/Ashkenazi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ovadia Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An ironic and illuminating contrast emerges when one compares Ashkenazi and Sefardi {\i halachic} responses to questions about women\u8217's participation. One might expect, given that women in Ashkenazi communities have historically played more active public roles in broader society, that Ashkenazi {\i psak} would be more inclusive. Sometimes it is \u8212- an Ashkenazi woman who takes a {\i lulav} makes a {\i bracha}, while a Sefardi woman who takes a {\i lulav} does not, which is not a minor distinction: the {\i bracha} is what transforms the act into a {\i mitzvah}. But on many questions of women\u8217's participation in synagogue life, Rav Ovadia Yosef is actually more permissive than his Ashkenazi counterparts, and the reason is instructive: he was not responding to Reform Judaism or to the feminist movement. He was not looking over his shoulder at what the non-Orthodox denominations were doing. When the question of a {\i Bat Mitzvah} ceremony was raised, Rav Ovadia said straightforwardly: of course one can have a {\i Bat Mitzvah} ceremony \u8212- in what way is it different from a {\i Bar Mitzvah}? It will increase people\u8217's involvement in Judaism; why would one not do it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Participation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, on the question of whether a woman may recite {\i Birkat HaGomel} in {\i shul}, Rav Ovadia\u8217's answer was direct: of course she may \u8212- why should she not? The elaborate considerations that occupied Ashkenazi {\i poskim} on this question were simply not operative for him. Because he was not engaged in a defensive posture against the challenge of Reform and feminism, he could assess each question on its merits and arrive at inclusive conclusions without the baggage of ideological boundary-drawing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a historical dimension to this comparison. In some communities \u8212- whether Hungarian Ashkenazi or certain Sefardi communities \u8212- women simply did not come to {\i shul} regularly. In those contexts, the question of {\i mechitzah} was largely moot: if women are not present, the {\i mechitzah} question does not arise in a practical sense. This may explain why the {\i mechitzah} did not become a live issue in those communities in the way it did in settings where women\u8217's attendance was more common.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women in Congregation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Home vs.\u160 ?Shul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A responsum from Rav Henkin directly addresses the question of whether women should be discouraged from attending synagogue, and it is worth reading carefully. The question presented to him concerned women who wished to come to {\i shul} and {\i daven} but brought their young children with them, creating noise and disruption. The questioner suggested that since the Torah exempted women from time-bound positive commandments precisely so that they could focus on child-rearing, women should be told to stay home with the children rather than come to {\i shul} and disrupt the {\i davening}. Rav Henkin responds sharply: {\i ta\u8217'ut hi ba-din} \u8212- this is a halachic error. Women are not exempt from {\i tefillah} as such, and the broader attitude that women\u8217's place is at home and not in the synagogue is not grounded in {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Reality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Henkin then makes a pointed observation about the contemporary reality: since women today go out of the house for countless reasons \u8212- work, socializing, errands \u8212- it would be far better if among those reasons were attending {\i shul}. {\i Halevai} \u8212- if only they would come to {\i batei kneisiyot} and places of {\i kedushah}. The exemption from time-bound {\i mitzvot} was not intended to strand women at home in a general sense; in a world where women are active participants in public life, that rationale for keeping them away from {\i shul} collapses entirely. On the contrary, women should be actively encouraged to attend.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Childcare Rotation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the practical problem of children creating disruption, Rav Henkin proposes a rotation: a system of {\i toranut shmirat ha-tinokot} \u8212- a childcare schedule \u8212- so that mothers can take turns attending {\i shul} while others remain with the children. He notes that when many women attend {\i shul} and only a few must remain outside to watch the children, those few feel marginalized and excluded, week after week bearing the burden so that others may participate. A rotation addresses this inequity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Childcare Funding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The further step \u8212- which flows from Rav Henkin\u8217's logic even if he does not himself state it \u8212- is that the {\i shul} itself should fund organized childcare, freeing all members of the community, women and men alike, to attend {\i davening} on equal terms.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ingrained Assumptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These issues are not merely matters of sources and legal analysis. They require a profound sensitivity to how deeply ingrained certain assumptions about the synagogue as male space have become \u8212- assumptions so embedded that they often operate invisibly, below the level of conscious reflection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Steps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Practical steps follow from this sensitivity. Adjusting childcare timing so that women feel fully included rather than treated as spectators. Using language that actually reflects who is present. Protecting women\u8217's space within the synagogue as genuinely equal, not as borrowed or conditional.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Subtle Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Etched Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A student once shared an observation from his wife after they stopped to {\i daven Mincha} in an unfamiliar {\i shul}. As they left, she said to him: \u8220"You never have to {\i daven} where the Hebrew writing is reversed on your side of the {\i mechitzah}.\u8221" The etched text on the glass {\i mechitzah} faced the men\u8217's side. Its orientation communicated silently but unmistakably: this space is for you. You are here at our leave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prayer Inscription\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related example concerns the phrase {\i Da Lifnei Mi Atah Omed} \u8212- \u8220"Know before Whom you stand\u8221" \u8212- commonly inscribed in large letters at the front of the {\i aron kodesh}. A student once gave an extended presentation on the history and sources of this inscription. Only afterward was it pointed out to him that he had spoken for seven minutes without once noting that the phrase is written in the masculine. {\i Atah}, {\i omed} \u8212- addressed to a man, not a person. Whether or not it registers consciously, the subconscious message of that inscription to a woman is that she is not the one being addressed; the primary audience is the men. An easy alternative is to use {\i Shiviti Hashem Lenegdi Tamid} \u8212- \u8220"I have set the Lord before me always\u8221" \u8212- a phrase written in the first person that addresses every worshipper equally, without grammatical gendering.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Communal Involvement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What these examples converge on is the importance of involving the entire community in these conversations rather than relying on rabbinical pronouncements alone. A rabbi who is genuinely trying to be sensitive can still miss things that congregants \u8212- particularly women \u8212- see and feel every day. The process of creating a genuinely inclusive {\i davening} community requires ongoing conversation, not a single corrective ruling issued from above. Sensitivity grows through dialogue, and the goal is to create a community in which all members feel that they belong \u8212- fully, not conditionally.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Subtle Exclusion (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Choices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further, subtle example of exclusionary language: a rabbi announces before the end of {\i davening} that \u8220"everyone\u8221" should put away their {\i tallit} and {\i tefillin}. \u8220"Everyone,\u8221" of course, means the men \u8212- but using the word \u8220"everyone\u8221" to mean a subset of those present invisibly redefines the actual audience. The women in the room are not \u8220"everyone.\u8221" Noticing this requires no special effort; it requires only the willingness to attend to how language constructs who the real community is.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Equal Space Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Men\u8217's Spillover\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A concrete and consequential expression of the male-space assumption is the common practice of men spilling over into the women\u8217's section during weekday {\i davening} when women are absent. Men put on their {\i tallit} and {\i tefillin} on the women\u8217's side, occupy those seats, use that space. If a woman then arrives, the men graciously make room for her. But the message this practice sends is unmistakable: fundamentally, all of this is our space. We will cede some of it when necessary. The women\u8217's section is conditional space \u8212- theirs only when they show up to claim it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeshiva Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A different practice reflects a different philosophy. In one {\i yeshiva}, before any {\i davening} begins \u8212- even though the {\i yeshiva} is composed entirely of men and perhaps only one or two women ever attend \u8212- the {\i mechitzah} is put straight down the middle, and it is completely forbidden for any man to cross to the women\u8217's side. It does not matter if he needs a {\i sefer} from that side. It does not matter if the other door is more convenient. The women\u8217's side is off-limits to men, period. The space belongs to the women; it is not borrowed from the men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Equal Portion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle that underlies this practice is simple and important: women must always feel that they have an equal portion as part of the {\i davening} community \u8212- not a contingent portion, not a graciously granted portion, but an equal one. The {\i mechitzah} going down the middle before {\i davening} begins, not after most people have already settled, is a structural statement that the women\u8217's space is as primary and inviolable as the men\u8217's. This principle \u8212- ensuring that women always feel genuinely included in the {\i davening} community \u8212- is the practical, communal expression of everything that the philosophical and halachic discussion has been working toward.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 36\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kim Lei Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kim Lei Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 36, and we pick up roughly two-thirds of the way down the page, at the words {\i ilanit ein lah knas}. We have, for the time being, finished with the extraordinary seven-{\i daf} {\i sugya} on {\i kim lei b\u8217'deraba minei}. By way of brief review: the {\i gemara} ultimately phrased the range of opinions as follows. Where the transgression carries a penalty of {\i misa beis din}, a biblical death sentence, the {\i gemara} at minimum implies \u8212- though {\i Tosfos} questions whether this is truly universal \u8212- that everyone would agree the death penalty overrides any monetary obligation, even in a case of {\i shogeg}. Where the penalty is {\i kares}, that was the position of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah and was subject to debate. Where the penalty is {\i malkus}, the debate was widest: Rabbi Meir held that both punishments could be administered simultaneously; Ulla held that the offender pays rather than receives {\i malkus} (or, according to {\i Tosfos}, this may have applied only to the specific {\i knas} of {\i ones umefate}); and the standard position was that {\i malkus} is administered and no monetary payment follows \u8212- within which there was a further dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish, Rabbi Yochanan maintaining that this principle applies only when {\i malkus} was actually administered, and Resh Lakish holding it applies even in cases of {\i shogeg}, without {\i hatara}, even when {\i malkus} was never carried out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ilanit & Age Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns back to the categories of women who do or do not receive a {\i knas}, not from the perspective of {\i kim lei} but from the perspective of their personal status. The {\i mishna} had established that even women in certain {\i arayos} relationships \u8212- as long as the offense is not in the category of {\i chayav misa} \u8212- can still give rise to a {\i knas}, though a {\i beraisa} reflecting the positions of either Rabbi Shimon Hatimani or Rabbi Shimon ben Menasya restricted the {\i knas} to cases where marriage to the woman is at least theoretically binding, or even more restrictively, where marriage is actually permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the question of whether the {\i knas} applies only to a {\i na\u8217'arah} or even to a {\i ketana}. Rabbi Meir holds it is limited to a {\i na\u8217'arah}; the {\i chachamim} extend it to a {\i ketana} as well. One practical application of this dispute involves the {\i ilanit} \u8212- a woman who never achieves physical maturity and therefore transitions directly from the status of {\i ketana} at age twenty to {\i bogeret}, never passing through the stage of {\i na\u8217'arah}. A {\i beraisa} stated that she receives no {\i knas}, presumably because she is never a {\i na\u8217'arah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Besula Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the {\i na\u8217'arah} question and the {\i arayos} question, a third axis of analysis concerns whether the woman can be presumed to be a {\i besula}. This issue was touched upon in connection with the {\i mema\u8217'enet} \u8212- a girl who is rabbinically but not biblically married and has been living with another man, and who therefore cannot be presumed a {\i besula}. The {\i gemara} now proceeds to examine other cases where the {\i besula} dimension of the definition is at stake.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ilanit Ein Lah Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ilanit Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i beraisa} stating: {\i ilanit ein lah knas} \u8212- an {\i ilanit} has no {\i knas} \u8212- and likewise no {\i knas} in the case of seduction involving her. This is consistent with what was established previously: she is never a {\i na\u8217'arah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ilanit Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately introduces a contradiction: {\i u-remihu} \u8212- and we raise a difficulty \u8212- for another {\i beraisa} lists the {\i chereshet}, the {\i shota}, and the {\i ilanit} together and declares {\i yesh lahem knas} \u8212- they do have a {\i knas}. Moreover, that same {\i beraisa} adds {\i ve-yesh lahem ta\u8217'anat betulim}: the husband has grounds to raise a claim of non-virginity the morning after the wedding night. This {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} is, of course, no advantage to the wife; it means the husband has a legitimate basis to challenge her presumed {\i besula} status and seek to reduce her {\i kesuvah} from two hundred to one hundred or eliminate it entirely. The {\i chereshet} and the {\i shota} are new information here and will be addressed in a moment. As for the {\i ilanit}: one {\i beraisa} says she has a {\i knas} and the other says she does not \u8212- an apparent contradiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir vs Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately resolves this: {\i ve-hai mai rumiya?} \u8212- what kind of contradiction is this at all? {\i Ha Rabbi Meir, ha Rabanan.} We already established that the entire debate about the {\i ilanit} traces directly to the {\i tanna\u8217'im} dispute about whether a {\i ketana} has a {\i knas}. The {\i beraisa} that denies her a {\i knas} reflects Rabbi Meir\u8217's position that only a {\i na\u8217'arah} is entitled, while the {\i beraisa} that grants her one reflects the {\i Rabanan}\u8217's view that even a {\i ketana} qualifies. There is no contradiction at all. The {\i gemara} therefore asks: {\i ve-ka\u8217'arei lei mai ka\u8217'arei lei?} \u8212- why did you even think to raise this as a difficulty, given that this is transparently a {\i machlokes} between {\i tanna\u8217'im}? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i mishum de-is lei de-mir me-achrisi aloyei} \u8212- he knew full well the {\i ilanit} question was answerable; he introduced it only to lay the groundwork for bringing a second {\i beraisa} that poses a genuine difficulty, one he truly wanted to examine.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chereshet & Shotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Have Knas & Betulim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more serious challenge involves the {\i chereshet} and {\i shota}. The {\i beraisa} just cited declares that a deaf-mute woman and a mentally ill woman \u8212- neither of whom is considered a {\i bar da\u8217'at} \u8212- are presumed virgins, have a {\i knas}, and are subject to {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}. This is itself a striking {\i chiddush}: these are women that people might readily take advantage of, and yet the {\i beraisa} presumes they remain virgins, entitling them to the full protections of a {\i besula}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ta\u8217'anat Betulim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A contradictory {\i beraisa}, however, lists {\i ha-chereshet ve-ha-shota ve-ha-bogeret u-mukat eitz ein lahem ta\u8217'anat betulim} \u8212- a deaf-mute, a mentally ill woman, a {\i bogeret}, and a {\i mukat eitz} are not subject to {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}. The {\i mukat eitz} is a woman who has sustained a physical injury that has ruptured the hymen; she obviously cannot be presumed a {\i besula} in the physical sense. The {\i bogeret} will be addressed separately. But the {\i chereshet} and {\i shota} are the live contradiction: one {\i beraisa} says the husband can raise a {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}, the other says he cannot. One might have assumed this reflects a dispute about whether we presume these women are virgins \u8212- and if we do not, then there would similarly be a debate about whether they are entitled to a {\i knas}. But the {\i gemara} rejects this framing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel vs Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya \u8212- ha Rabban Gamliel, ha Rabbi Yehoshua.} This answer is elegant. The question of {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} is not simply a function of whether we presume the woman to be a virgin. Even if we universally presume she is a virgin \u8212- and the {\i gemara} maintains that everyone agrees a {\i chereshet} and {\i shota} are presumed virgins, so that everyone agrees they are entitled to a {\i knas} \u8212- the husband\u8217's {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} claim may or may not succeed for entirely separate reasons. Recall the dispute in the first {\i perek} between Rabban Gamliel and Rabbi Yehoshua regarding the defenses available to a wife when her husband raises a claim of non-virginity. Rabban Gamliel accepts certain counter-claims \u8212- for instance, that she was violated after the {\i kiddushin}, or that she is a {\i mukat eitz} \u8212- and if she makes such a claim, the husband\u8217's assertion does not prevail. Rabbi Yehoshua, by contrast, holds {\i lo mipi anu chayim}: no defense she can offer will save her. Accordingly, the {\i beraisa} that says the husband has a valid {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} follows Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- since she cannot rebut him successfully, his claim stands. The {\i beraisa} that says there is no {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} follows Rabban Gamliel \u8212- not because we presume her not to be a virgin, but because there is always a counter-claim available that will defeat the husband\u8217's assertion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Din Representation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: granted that Rabban Gamliel validates such defenses \u8212- but Rabban Gamliel\u8217's validation was predicated on {\i her} making the claim. What if she cannot speak for herself? Does Rabban Gamliel\u8217's leniency apply when the wife never personally lodges any particular defense? The {\i gemara} answers affirmatively: {\i kivan de-amar Rabban Gamliel mehemana, she-kegon zeh ptach picha l\u8217'ilem hu} \u8212- since Rabban Gamliel established that a wife is believed when she offers such explanations, the principle of \u8220"open your mouth for the mute\u8221" applies, and {\i beis din} steps in to present the most advantageous position on her behalf. These women, as non-{\i bnei da\u8217'at}, cannot represent themselves, and so {\i beis din} assumes the role, arguing for them \u8212- whether the defense is that she was violated after the {\i kiddushin}, as {\i Rashi} suggests, or that she became a {\i mukat eitz}, as {\i Tosfos} prefers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the underlying tension here: some of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's defenses derive their persuasive force from the woman\u8217's {\i nes} \u8212- the brazenness required to lie in her husband\u8217's presence \u8212- or from the definite ({\i bari}) character of her own claim. When {\i beis din} makes the argument on her behalf, those considerations weaken. Nevertheless, the principle of {\i beis din} representing those without legal competence extends to cases of {\i ketanim} and others lacking {\i da\u8217'at}, and the {\i gemara} accepts that this representation can suffice to defeat the husband\u8217's claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is significant: the contradiction in the {\i baraitot} about {\i chereshet} and {\i shota} has nothing to do with whether they are presumed virgins. Universally, we assume they are virgins, and universally they are entitled to a {\i knas}. The contradiction concerns only whether the husband\u8217's {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim} succeeds \u8212- and that turns on the Rabban Gamliel\u8211-Rabbi Yehoshua debate about available defenses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bogeret & Suma Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bogeret Layla Rishona\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i bogeret}, who the {\i beraisa} listed as one who has no {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}. What is the basis for this? The {\i gemara} cites Rav: {\i bogeret nosnin lah layla rishona} \u8212- for a {\i bogeret}, all hymenal bleeding during the first night of marriage is attributed to the hymen rather than to {\i dam niddah}. This refers back to the {\i niddah} discussion in the earlier {\i perekm} where the question was how long after the wedding night a woman\u8217's bleeding could be attributed to the hymen and therefore not treated as {\i niddah}. For a younger woman or one who has not yet established a regular cycle, more time is granted; for a {\i bogeret}, who has been regularly menstruating, only the first night qualifies. The critical point is that this ruling presupposes that a {\i bogeret} does have a hymen that bleeds. We assume the hymen is intact. So if the husband maintains the hymen was not intact on the wedding night, why can he not make a {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood vs Petach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies the distinction between two possible claims. The discussion is at the top of 36b. If the husband claims {\i ta\u8217'anat damim} \u8212- that there was no blood on the wedding night \u8212- then {\i hacha nami}, he would have a valid claim, because we assume a {\i bogeret} does have an intact hymen and there will be blood; the absence of blood is meaningful evidence. But the case of {\i bogeret ein lah ta\u8217'anat betulim} is where he makes not a claim about blood but a claim of {\i petach patuach} \u8212- that physically the experience did not feel as it would with a virgin. This claim fails for a {\i bogeret}, because the {\i gemara} says her more developed physiology means the situation will feel different regardless, and no inference can be drawn from that sensation. As {\i Rashi} explains, {\i bo\u8217'el kama be\u8217'ilos} \u8212- the first night includes multiple acts, and all the bleeding throughout that night is attributed to hymenal rupture, which is why the analysis focuses on the night as a whole. But once we shift to the {\i petach patuach} claim rather than the blood claim, the {\i bogeret}\u8217's physical maturity renders that claim meaningless, and the husband loses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suma Accident Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} cited earlier included a position from Sumchos, in the name of Rabbi Meir: {\i suma ein lah ta\u8217'anat betulim} \u8212- a blind woman cannot be subject to a {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}, meaning the husband has no valid claim even if he finds evidence of non-virginity. The reasoning offered by Rav Zeira on behalf of Sumchos is that a blind woman is prone to stumbling and falling \u8212- {\i mipnei she-nichbetes al gabei karka} \u8212- and such a fall might cause a physical injury that ruptures the hymen, effectively making her a {\i mukat eitz}. Since one cannot rule out such an accident, the husband should not have assumed that an intact hymen was guaranteed, and his claim therefore carries no weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Women Have Accidents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this immediately: {\i kulan nami chabutei michabtan} \u8212- all women trip and have accidents, not only blind women. Sighted women sustain physical injuries too. Notably, the {\i gemara} does not respond by arguing that blind women are statistically more accident-prone. That is not the {\i gemara}\u8217's answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blind Woman Unawareness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Instead, the {\i gemara} offers a distinct principle: {\i kulan ro\u8217'ot u-mar\u8217'ot l\u8217'iman} \u8212- a sighted girl who has an accident and notices blood or some other indication will tell her mother what happened, and her mother will explain that the hymen has been affected. {\i Zo eina ro\u8217'a ve-eina mar\u8217'a l\u8217'ima} \u8212- this woman does not see what happened, and therefore she does not inform her mother, and she remains unaware that she has effectively become a {\i mukat eitz}. Accordingly, when she marries, she does not disclose anything to her husband \u8212- not out of concealment, but out of genuine ignorance. He, having had no reason to suspect an injury, enters the marriage assuming she is a {\i besula} in the full physical sense. According to Sumchos and Rabbi Meir, this is sufficient to deny the husband a valid {\i ta\u8217'anat betulim}: since she might have sustained such an injury without knowing it, he cannot now claim he was deceived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note \u8212- and this was acknowledged \u8212- that this reasoning sits uncomfortably with what a woman\u8217's own physical senses would detect. The {\i gemara}\u8217's formulation rests on the visual dimension of the experience, and the logic is somewhat strained when applied to a woman without sight. Nevertheless, the operative ruling according to Sumchos is that we place the blind woman in the category of one who might unknowingly be a {\i mukat eitz}, and the husband\u8217's claim is therefore not actionable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shem Ra & Promiscuity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shem Ra Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the first {\i beraisa} with which this section began, which listed another category of women who do not receive a {\i knas}: {\i ha-yotzet mishum shem ra ein lah knas v\u8217'lo pitu\u8217'i} \u8212- a woman who has been sent away on account of {\i shem ra} has neither a {\i knas} nor a claim arising from seduction. The {\i shem ra} referenced by the {\i Torah} in the case of {\i ve-hotzi aleha shem ra} is the husband\u8217's allegation of adultery that, if substantiated, results in the wife\u8217's execution. That is a woman who has already been married; what does this have to do with the laws of a {\i na\u8217'arah betulah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the obvious question: {\i b\u8217'skila hi} \u8212- she is subject to stoning. {\i Tosfos} addresses this with two lines of analysis. First, if she committed adultery she is definitively a {\i be\u8217'ulah} and self-evidently not a candidate for {\i knas}, which is limited to a {\i besula}. Second, one might have thought the issue is {\i kim lei b\u8217'deraba minei} \u8212- that the perpetrator\u8217's capital liability overrides the monetary {\i knas} \u8212- but {\i Tosfos} points out this is not the right framing here, because it is not the same individual facing both punishments in the usual sense of {\i kim lei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor vs Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet therefore reinterprets the case entirely: {\i mi she-yatza lah shem ra be-yaldutah ein lah knas} \u8212- we are not speaking of the biblical {\i shem ra} case of proven adultery. Rather, {\i yotzet mishum shem ra} means that a rumor has circulated in the community that this woman is promiscuous \u8212- not proven adultery, not judicial procedure, but public gossip suggesting she has been sexually active. On that basis, she is presumed to be a {\i be\u8217'ulah} and loses her entitlement to a {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Insufficient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa then draws an inference: {\i shma mina} \u8212- we can learn from this case that if there is only an unsubstantiated rumor, that alone is not legally sufficient. He analogizes to a {\i shtar} with a disputed validity: if people are merely gossiping that a document is a forgery \u8212- {\i nefik kala} \u8212- we do not refuse to enforce the document on that basis alone. The parallel would suggest that mere gossip about a woman\u8217's promiscuity is similarly insufficient to deny her a {\i knas}. And indeed, Rava states explicitly: {\i yatza lah shem mezana ba-ir ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- if a rumor circulates in a town that a woman is promiscuous, we give it no halachic weight whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses & Behavior\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, what level of evidence does suffice? {\i De-atu bei terei ve-amri} \u8212- two people come and give firsthand testimony. But what exactly do they testify to? Not that they personally witnessed her engage in intercourse \u8212- that would not be mere {\i shem ra} at all. Rather, in the parallel case of the {\i shtar}: two witnesses come and testify that the claimant personally approached them and asked them to forge a document on his behalf. They refused, but the fact that he solicited forgery creates concrete suspicion about the document now before the court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Analogously, by the woman: two men come and testify that she approached them and solicited intercourse. They declined, but the act of solicitation itself \u8212- firsthand knowledge of actual conduct, not mere rumor \u8212- creates a presumption that she has been {\i ba\u8217'alas} with someone, since such overtures, if repeated, will eventually find a willing recipient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document vs Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pauses to question whether these two cases are truly analogous. {\i Bishlama hasam shechichi prutzim} \u8212- in the case of the woman, it is entirely credible to assume that if she repeatedly propositioning men, eventually someone will say yes, because promiscuous men are not uncommon. {\i Ela hacha} \u8212- but in the document case, if someone is looking for a forger and has not found one yet, should we not assume that people generally have a {\i chezkas kashrus} and will refuse? Perhaps even with money offered, no one agreed to forge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this distinction: {\i kevan de-mahader a-ziyufa, eimar zeiyuphi zeiyeiph} \u8212- since he was actively searching for someone to forge the document, even if he found no partner, he likely forged it himself. So in both cases \u8212- the woman who propositioned witnesses, and the man who sought a forger \u8212- concrete evidence of actual behavior (not mere rumor) creates a sufficient presumption to change the legal outcome. In neither case does bare gossip suffice; in both, firsthand testimony about specific conduct does. The {\i gemara} notes, with some irony, that we are apparently more willing to assume a man will ultimately say yes to a sexual proposition than to assume he will agree to forge a document, even when financial inducement is involved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna: No Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Convert/Captive/Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now arrives at the next {\i mishna}, which mirrors the first and makes explicit what the preceding week\u8217's discussion had been assuming. {\i Ve-eilu she-ein lahem knas} \u8212- the following women are not entitled to a {\i knas}. The first group consists of women presumed to be a {\i be\u8217'ulah} by virtue of their prior status. {\i Ha-ba al ha-giyores ve-al ha-shvuya ve-al ha-shifcha} \u8212- a woman who is a convert, a woman who was taken captive, or a woman who is a non-Jewish slave \u8212- {\i she-nifdU ve-she-nitgeiyeru ve-she-nishtachreru} \u8212- and who have since been redeemed, converted, or freed \u8212- are presumed {\i be\u8217'ulot} if their transition out of that status occurred when they were older than three years of age. The reasoning is that in their prior condition \u8212- as non-Jews, slaves, or captives \u8212- we assume they engaged in or were subject to intercourse, and since this occurred after age three, it constitutes a legally cognizable act that ruptures the hymen permanently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda: Captive Virgin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda dissents regarding the captive woman: {\i shvuya she-nifdas harei hi be-kedushata} \u8212- a redeemed captive retains her sanctity, and we presume she was not raped, {\i af al pi she-gedolah} \u8212- even if she was an adult when captured. Accordingly, even an adult captive woman receives the {\i knas} and retains the full presumption of virginity. This immediately raises the question that will dominate the subsequent {\i gemara} discussion: if Rabbi Yehuda assumes she was not raped, why was this not stated earlier in the context of whether she may return to her {\i kohen} husband? The {\i gemara} will address this directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Capital Crimes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then addresses the second category of women without a {\i knas}: those whose cases involve capital offenses. {\i Ha-ba al bito, al bas bito, al bas bno, al bas ishto, al bas bnah, al bas bitah} \u8212- intercourse with one\u8217's daughter, granddaughter, stepdaughter, or the children or grandchildren of a wife \u8212- {\i ein lahem knas} \u8212- carries no {\i knas}. The reason is distinct from the {\i be\u8217'ulah} issue: here it is because the offense is {\i chayav misa}, a capital crime.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ason Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} provides the textual basis: {\i mipnei she-mischayev be-nafsho} \u8212- because the perpetrator is liable with his life. {\i Ve-chol ha-mischayev be-nafsho ein meshalem mamon} \u8212- anyone who has committed a capital offense does not pay a monetary penalty. The scriptural source is the {\i pasuk} in Exodus: {\i ve-im lo yihyeh ason anosh ye\u8217'anesh} \u8212- if there is no tragedy, then he shall pay the financial penalty. This implies that where there is a fatal consequence \u8212- where a {\i misa} attaches \u8212- the monetary penalty is set aside. This {\i pasuk} and the concept of {\i ason} are precisely what had been invoked throughout the preceding seven-{\i daf} {\i sugya} on {\i kim lei b\u8217'deraba minei}, and the {\i mishna} here provides the direct textual anchor for that entire discussion. One might ask whether the {\i pasuk} refers specifically to actual execution or more broadly to any capital-level offense even without {\i eidim} and {\i hasra\u8217'ah}; this connects to the {\i gemara}\u8217's earlier treatment of {\i tana devei Chizkiya} and the distinction between {\i ason} and {\i din ason}, which the {\i gemara} discusses but which the {\i mishna} treats as settled for present purposes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Captive Woman Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dosa\u8217's Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan observes that Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Dosa, though addressing different areas of {\i halacha}, effectively articulate the same underlying assumption. Rabbi Yehuda, as stated in our {\i mishna}, holds that a redeemed captive retains her presumed virginity. Rabbi Dosa, as taught in a {\i beraisa}, holds that a captive woman may continue to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i shvuya ocheles bi-terumah divrei Rabbi Dosa} \u8212- which implies that her captivity does not invalidate her priestly status. Had she had intercourse with a non-Jew, even involuntarily, she would be disqualified from eating {\i terumah}. Rabbi Dosa\u8217's reasoning is stated directly: {\i vechi mah asa lah Aravi halaz} \u8212- what did this captor do to her? {\i Vechi mipnei she-mishmesh lah bein dadeha pasla min ha-kehuna} \u8212- merely because he may have fondled her, does that disqualify her from {\i kehuna}? Rabbi Dosa\u8217's position is that captors do not actually rape their captives; whatever contact may occur does not rise to the level of intercourse, and therefore her status remains intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises the question of whether these two positions \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Dosa \u8212- necessarily cohere across all areas of {\i halacha}. Even if both assume the captive was not raped, perhaps each applied that assumption only within his particular domain. {\i Ad kan lo ka\u8217'amar Rabbi Yehuda hacha} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda may have made this assumption specifically in the context of {\i knas}, and would not extend it to {\i terumah}. Similarly, Rabbi Dosa may have been speaking specifically about {\i terumah} and would not apply the same assumption to {\i knas}. Each area of {\i halacha} may carry its own logic that leads to leniency or stringency independent of what another area demands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chotei Nishkar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then highlights the policy dimension of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position. {\i Shelo yihyeh chote nishkar} \u8212- the principle of ensuring that a sinner does not benefit from his transgression. The rapist is obligated to pay fifty {\i shekel}. If a technicality \u8212- the prior captive status of the victim \u8212- is used to exempt him from payment, then the sinner derives benefit: he commits a terrible act and escapes the full legal consequences. To prevent that outcome, Rabbi Yehuda is prepared to assume that the woman was not raped during captivity, so that a subsequent {\i ones} or {\i pituy} will not be exempted from its {\i knas}. This is not necessarily a categorical metaphysical claim about what captors do or do not do; it may be a policy-driven willingness to make the more protective assumption within the {\i knas} context specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universal vs Contextual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} tests whether Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position is purely policy-driven or truly universal. A {\i beraisa} states: {\i shvuya she-nishbis harei hi bi-kedushata, afilu bas eser shanim kesubatah masayim} \u8212- a captive woman retains her presumed sanctity, and even at a substantial age her {\i kesuvah} remains two hundred. This suggests that Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's assumption is applied even in the {\i kesuvah} context \u8212- not merely in the {\i knas} context. If it were only a policy response to {\i chote nishkar}, why extend it to {\i kesuvah}? The {\i gemara} responds that the {\i kesuvah} context also has its own policy driver: {\i dilma minsi ve-lo nasvi lah} \u8212- if we declare that a formerly captive woman is presumed to have been raped, people will simply avoid marrying her. Branding her as a {\i be\u8217'ulah} by assumption makes her effectively unmarriageable. Society\u8217's interest in facilitating her remarriage therefore provides an independent policy reason to maintain the presumption of virginity in the {\i kesuvah} context as well, entirely separate from {\i chote nishkar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesuvah Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion on this point is that the assumptions made by Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Dosa may well be context-specific rather than categorical. When it comes to {\i knas}, there is a policy reason \u8212- {\i chote nishkar} \u8212- to presume the woman is a virgin. When it comes to {\i kesuvah}, there is a different but equally compelling policy reason \u8212- the desire to ensure she remains marriageable. When it comes to {\i terumah}, the analysis may point in a different direction: since {\i terumah} is nowadays rabbinic in force, there is room for leniency on that front; but since {\i knas} is biblical and involves extracting money from a defendant, one might be more cautious about imposing it on the basis of a contested assumption. Each domain has its own considerations, and the {\i gemara} leaves open that Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Dosa may not have intended their assumptions to operate uniformly across all areas.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohein & Captive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redeem vs Testify\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines a {\i beraisa} that addresses the {\i kohen} who wishes to marry a redeemed captive. The {\i beraisa} states: if a {\i kohen} redeems a captive woman, he may marry her. If he merely testifies about her status \u8212- that she was not violated \u8212- he may not marry her. This is initially puzzling: the more he knows about her fitness, the less he is permitted to act on that knowledge?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies that the {\i beraisa} must be read conjunctively rather than disjunctively: {\i ha-podeh et ha-shvuya u-me\u8217'id bah yissanah} \u8212- the {\i kohen} who both redeems her and testifies about her may marry her; {\i me\u8217'id bah ke-dei lo yissanah} \u8212- testimony alone, without the act of redemption, is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money as Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is that testimony by itself, when offered by a man who wishes to marry the woman, is suspect: perhaps his {\i yetzer hara} has gotten the better of him and he is fabricating or embellishing. But if he also pays money to redeem her \u8212- {\i lo shadei inish zuzei bi-khdi} \u8212- people do not throw their money away for nothing. A man who is prepared to lay out real funds to redeem her is demonstrating through his actions that he is serious and truthful, and his accompanying testimony is therefore credible. The irony is apparent: one would think that a man willing to commit his life to marrying this woman would be all the more motivated to lie, but the financial outlay is treated as the threshold indicator of sincerity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda: Always Marry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position in the {\i beraisa} is that {\i bein kach u-vein kach lo yissanah} \u8212- in either case, whether he redeems her or merely testifies, he may not marry her. This appears to contradict Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's general assumption that captive women are presumed not to have been raped. If he genuinely holds that assumption, why should the {\i kohen} be unable to marry her regardless?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda vs Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa suggests emending Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position: read it as {\i bein kach u-vein kach yissanah} \u8212- either way he {\i may} marry her, since Rabbi Yehuda universally presumes the captive was not violated. Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua, however, defends the text as it stands and offers a different reading: {\i Rabbi Yehuda le-divrehem de-Rabbanan ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda is not stating his own view but is arguing {\i ad hominem} against the {\i Rabanan}. From his own perspective, {\i l\u8217'didi, bein kach u-vein kach yissanah} \u8212- either way he can marry her, because he holds categorically that captive women are presumed to be virgins. But he says to the {\i Rabanan}: {\i ela le-didchu, bein kach u-vein kach lo yissanah} \u8212- but according to you, who generally presume that captive women have been violated, you should logically prohibit the marriage in either case, because even a {\i kohen}\u8217's own testimony should be viewed with suspicion when he stands to benefit from it through marriage. The {\i Rabanan} respond that when redemption accompanies testimony, {\i lo shadei inish zuzei bi-khdi} \u8212- the financial commitment renders the testimony reliable \u8212- and they therefore permit marriage in that combined case. Testimony without redemption remains insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus concludes this section with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position understood not as mere policy calculation \u8212- whether about {\i chote nishkar} or {\i kesuvah} protection \u8212- but, as {\i Tosfos} emphasizes, as a genuine categorical principle: a captive woman is presumed not to have been raped for all purposes, including the question of whether a {\i kohen} may marry her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 37\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Captive Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Knas & Captivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to the {\i mishnah} introduced on 36b, which presented the opinion of Rabbi Yehuda that a woman taken captive retains the presumption of {\i bikdushatah} \u8212- that she has not been violated. The practical context of that {\i mishnah} was the law of {\i knas}, the statutory fine imposed upon a rapist, and the {\i gemara} had previously explored whether Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position was categorical \u8212- applying even to questions of {\i terumah} or eligibility to marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- or whether it was driven by a specific policy consideration: {\i lo yehei chotei niskar}, the principle that a sinner should not benefit. That consideration would naturally support the woman\u8217's interests in {\i knas} and {\i ketubah} contexts, where denying her the presumption would effectively reward the wrongdoer, but might not extend to other domains. The {\i gemara} now proceeds under the assumption that Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position is categorical, and sets out to test it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giyoret & Niddah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge comes from a {\i baraita} dealing with a {\i giyoret} \u8212- a woman who converts to Judaism \u8212- who sees blood shortly after her conversion. The ordinary rule governing {\i niddah} status is that impurity begins not when blood exits the body but when it passes internally from the uterus through the cervix into the vaginal canal. Since that internal transition cannot be directly observed, the Rabbis instituted a retroactive concern: unless the woman performed a {\i bedikah} more recently, she is considered to have been a {\i niddah} for the preceding twenty-four hours. If she converted twelve hours ago, the question is whether we extend that retroactive concern back through the twelve hours she has been Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} records a dispute. Rabbi Yehuda says {\i dayah sha\u8217'atah} \u8212- her moment is sufficient; she is treated as having become a {\i niddah} only now, and the retroactive window does not apply to her. Rabbi Yose, by contrast, says she is like all other women \u8212- {\i metama\u8217'ah mei\u8217'eit l\u8217'eit u-mipkidah l\u8217'fkidah} \u8212- and is {\i tame\u8217'ah} retroactively, bounded by either twenty-four hours or the time of conversion, whichever is shorter. The Ramban explains Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position as follows: since the rabbinic institution cannot be applied in its full normal form \u8212- the woman was not Jewish for the entire twenty-four-hour window \u8212- it is not applied at all, and we revert to the biblical baseline that she became a {\i niddah} only now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question arises in practice: may a woman convert while she is actively menstruating or in a state of {\i tumas niddah}? The answer is that it poses no obstacle. {\i Tumas niddah} is a form of {\i tumah} like any other, and just as {\i tumah} from a corpse does not prevent conversion, neither does {\i niddah} status. Immersion in the {\i mikvah} for conversion purposes is valid even if the woman\u8217's {\i niddah} period has not yet concluded and the immersion cannot simultaneously serve to purify her from {\i niddah} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Papa\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now Rav Papa bar Shmuel challenges Rav Yosef with a seeming contradiction within Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's own positions. The challenge begins with the {\i niddah} case as a lead-in, but the sharper problem arises from the continuation of the {\i baraita}, which states that a {\i giyoret} must wait three months before marrying. This waiting period exists because of concern that she may be pregnant from before her conversion. The {\i gemara} understands the debate between Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yose not as a dispute about whether the pre-conversion paternity matters halachically \u8212- though that too raises interesting questions about {\i yuchsin} that will be explored in Kiddushin \u8212- but as a dispute about whether we are simply concerned she might be pregnant. Rabbi Yehuda says yes; Rabbi Yose says no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The apparent contradiction is this: in the {\i mishnah}, Rabbi Yehuda shows no concern that a {\i shvuyah} was raped, yet here he expresses concern that a {\i giyoret} \u8212- who may have been sexually active as a non-Jewish woman \u8212- might be pregnant. The {\i gemara} immediately deflects this challenge: {\i giyoret lumpda nafshah} \u8212- a convert and a captive woman are entirely different cases. A non-Jewish woman living freely may well be sexually active by choice; that is a reasonable assumption. A Jewish woman taken captive, on the other hand, is not presumed to have consented, and we are not simply going to assume she was raped by her captors. The cases are not parallel, and the challenge fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then attempts a stronger version of the challenge \u8212- {\i rami shvuyah a-shvuyah}, a direct contradiction between two rulings about captive women. The {\i baraita} states explicitly that a {\i giyoret}, {\i shvuyah}, and {\i shifchah} \u8212- a convert, a captive woman, and a non-Jewish slave woman \u8212- who were redeemed, converted, or freed after the age of three must wait three months before marrying. This is Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling. Rabbi Yose permits immediate marriage. Here Rabbi Yehuda is treating a {\i shvuyah} as someone who may have been raped and may be pregnant \u8212- directly contradicting his position in the {\i mishnah} that she is presumed {\i bikdushatah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Month Wait\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This challenge is a genuine one. Rav Papa presents it to Rav Yosef, and Rav Yosef is stumped \u8212- {\i ishtik}, he fell silent. He then turns to Rav Papa and asks whether Rav Papa has heard any resolution. Rav Papa responds with the answer of Rav Sheshet: the case in the {\i baraita} where Rabbi Yehuda requires waiting is specifically one where we know with certainty that the woman had sexual intercourse \u8212- {\i she-ra\u8217'uha she-niv\u8217'alah}. Under those circumstances, Rabbi Yehuda naturally requires the three-month wait to establish paternity. The {\i mishnah}, by contrast, speaks of a case where no such concrete knowledge exists, and there Rabbi Yehuda maintains that we presume she was not violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an admittedly constrained reading \u8212- a {\i dachuk} {\i ukimta} \u8212- but it is the {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution. The immediate question, however, is: if that is what the {\i baraita} is discussing, why does it focus specifically on the {\i giyoret}, {\i shvuyah}, and {\i shifchah}? The answer is that Rabbi Yose\u8217's position still requires explanation, because even in a case where we know the woman has had intercourse, Rabbi Yose says no waiting is necessary. And that is what the {\i gemara} now turns to address.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birth Control Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moch & R. Yose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah explains Rabbi Yose\u8217's position: {\i isha mezanah meshamashet be-moch she-lo tit\u8217'aber} \u8212- a sexually active woman will use a {\i moch}, a contraceptive device, in order not to become pregnant. Under this assumption, even if we know the woman has had intercourse, we are not concerned she is pregnant, because she would have taken precautions. There is a dispute between Rashi and Tosafot as to whether the {\i moch} was used before intercourse, to block the seed, or after, to absorb it. Rashi\u8217's view that it was used after will become significant shortly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birth Control Debate (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Converts Planning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to examine how Rabbi Yose\u8217's assumption plays out across each of the three cases. The case of the {\i giyoret} is the most straightforward: {\i keivan de-da\u8217'atah le-igguyerei mi-natra nafshah} \u8212- since she was planning to convert, she would have been careful not to become pregnant beforehand, because she would not want to enter her new Jewish life burdened with a pregnancy from her pre-conversion past. The {\i gemara} therefore accepts that Rabbi Yose can reasonably presume she used contraception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captives Seeking Help\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the {\i shvuyah} is more striking. The {\i gemara} says: {\i de-lo yada heicha mamtu lah} \u8212- she does not know where she will be taken. Rashi explains this to mean that she would want the Jewish community to redeem her, and she understood that a pregnancy resulting from her captors would diminish their willingness to do so. She therefore would have taken steps to avoid becoming pregnant. This is a striking argument on several levels. It implies both that the woman had access to contraception while in captivity and that she was sufficiently calculating about her circumstances to use it strategically. One might naturally ask: using contraception implies some degree of agency over the sexual encounter, whereas the entire concern is that she was raped. The answer is that even in circumstances of coercion, if a woman anticipates the possibility of violation and has access to contraceptive means, she might still employ them \u8212- particularly if, as Rashi understands it, the {\i moch} is used after the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves Freed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i shifchah}, the reasoning runs similarly: {\i de-shama\u8217'at mi-pi marah} \u8212- if she heard from her master that he intended to free her, she would not have wanted to begin her free life pregnant by her master or another man in the household, and so she would have taken precautions. The {\i gemara} acknowledges that a slave who had no expectation of freedom might not have the same motivation to avoid pregnancy, but posits that when freedom was anticipated, the incentive to use contraception was present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape & Sudden Changes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses the limits of this reasoning. What about {\i yotza be-shen va-ayin} \u8212- a slave woman who is freed suddenly because her master struck out her eye or tooth? That freedom was not anticipated and came entirely without warning. Under those circumstances, what can Rabbi Yose say? One might argue that Rabbi Yose\u8217's leniency is limited to cases where the change in status was foreseeable and the woman could have planned accordingly, but does not apply to sudden, unplanned changes \u8212- {\i mimela} \u8212- where no such planning was possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} tests this by asking about {\i anusa} \u8212- a raped woman \u8212- and {\i mefuta} \u8212- a seduced woman. Rape is quintessentially {\i mimela}: nothing could have been anticipated or planned. Yet a {\i baraita} explicitly states that a raped woman must wait three months \u8212- that is Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position \u8212- and Rabbi Yose permits immediate marriage even there. So Rabbi Yose\u8217's position cannot be limited to cases of anticipated change; he maintains it even in cases of rape. The marginal note in the text observes that Rashi\u8217's text of the {\i baraita} mentions only {\i anusa} and not {\i mefuta}, since seduction, while perhaps sudden in one sense, does involve some degree of the woman\u8217's participation and is harder to characterize as purely {\i mimela}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explicit {\i baraita} is actually clarifying: what had been presented as a constrained {\i ukimta} \u8212- that the earlier {\i baraita} must have been discussing a case where we know she had intercourse \u8212- is now confirmed by this later {\i baraita}, which is explicit that even a raped woman is subject to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's waiting requirement, and Rabbi Yose permits her to marry immediately. The {\i ukimta} was not a stretch; it simply reflects what the sources explicitly teach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Internal Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i moch} before intercourse unavailable to a rape victim, and the {\i moch} after intercourse unavailable unless she has it ready immediately \u8212- as Rashi notes, a delay of hours would make it useless \u8212- how can Rabbi Yose exempt even a raped woman? Rabbah answers: {\i kasavar Rabbi Yose isha mezanah mithapechet kedei she-lo tit\u8217'aber} \u8212- a woman has internal means of preventing pregnancy even without any device, by repositioning her body after the act in a way that expels the semen. Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, responds: {\i chashinan shema lo nithapcha yafeh yafeh} \u8212- we cannot rely on this as a sufficiently reliable method; she may not have done it effectively, and we therefore remain concerned that she is pregnant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of this entire discussion is a coherent picture of the two positions. Rabbi Yose holds that even when intercourse is known to have occurred \u8212- whether consensually, through seduction, or by outright rape \u8212- we do not presume pregnancy, because women have means, physiological or otherwise, to prevent it. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: when intercourse is established, we are concerned about pregnancy and require the three-month wait. But where intercourse is not established \u8212- as in the case of the {\i shvuyah} in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda maintains that she is presumed {\i bikdushatah}, and for purposes of {\i knas} at least, she is not presumed to have been violated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Captive Women (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the foregoing analysis in view, the three cases of {\i giyoret}, {\i shvuyah}, and {\i shifchah} can be properly distinguished. The {\i giyoret}\u8217's sexual activity as a non-Jewish woman is presumed \u8212- she was free and potentially active \u8212- and so even Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's concern about the {\i shvuyah} and the {\i giyoret} are driven by different reasoning. The {\i shvuyah} in the {\i mishnah} is assumed not to have been violated in the absence of positive evidence; the {\i shvuyah} in the {\i baraita} requiring three months is a case where violation is confirmed. The {\i shifchah} similarly is treated leniently by Rabbi Yose based on the assumption of birth control use. These distinctions resolve what had appeared to be an internal contradiction within Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's rulings and clarify the scope of Rabbi Yose\u8217's principled leniency.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kim Lei Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Over Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to revisit the principle of {\i kim lei bi-draba minei} \u8212- that the greater penalty subsumes the lesser \u8212- by examining the scriptural sources that underpin it. The {\i mishnah} had cited the verse {\i velo yihyeh ason anosh ye\u8217'anesh}: if a man strikes a pregnant woman and there is no fatal outcome, he pays a fine; but if she dies, the monetary obligation falls away because a capital offense is involved. This is the {\i kim lei} principle in its most familiar form: a capital offense overrides a monetary obligation. The {\i gemara} now asks whether that verse is the actual source of the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that the primary source is a different verse: {\i kidei rishato} \u8212- \u8220"according to his wickedness\u8221" \u8212- from the passage in Devarim governing {\i malkos}. The Rabbis derive from this verse that a person is held liable for only one form of {\i rish\u8217'ut}, one category of wickedness, at a time: you may not impose both {\i malkos} and monetary payment simultaneously. The verse from {\i ason} therefore teaches a separate lesson, and both are needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks why both verses are necessary. Each teaches a distinct case: one establishes that the death penalty overrides monetary liability, and the other establishes that {\i malkos} overrides monetary liability. You cannot derive one from the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If only the verse about the death penalty were available, one might reason: the death penalty overrides money because we are taking the man\u8217's life \u8212- {\i ibud neshama} \u8212- and in the face of that severity, monetary claims become irrelevant. But in the case of {\i malkos}, where the man is not being executed, perhaps both can coexist: exact the lashes and collect the money. So the {\i kidei rishato} verse is needed to tell us that {\i malkos} too overrides monetary liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if only {\i kidei rishato} were available, one might reason differently: {\i malkos} overrides money because the sin involved was not so grave \u8212- it did not rise to the level of a capital offense \u8212- and the Torah therefore allows it to be discharged by one form of punishment. But for a sin so grave that it carries the death penalty, the greater weight of the offense might seem to argue {\i against} exempting the offender from additional obligations. So the {\i ason} verse is needed to confirm that the death penalty too overrides monetary liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Corporal Punishments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Meir, who holds {\i lokim u-meshalmim} \u8212- that both {\i malkos} and monetary payment may be imposed simultaneously \u8212- the {\i kidei rishato} verse cannot be used for the {\i malkos} and money case. He therefore must read it differently: both verses concern the death penalty, but they address distinct sub-questions. One tells us that the death penalty overrides monetary liability, and the other tells us that a death sentence overrides {\i malkos} \u8212- that if a person is subject to both execution and lashes, the execution alone is carried out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again asks why both are needed. If the death penalty overrides {\i malkos}, one might argue: both are bodily punishments \u8212- {\i chada b\u8217'gufei ve-chada b\u8217'gufei} \u8212- and perhaps they function as a single extended punishment, {\i misa arichta}, a prolonged form of the same act upon the body, so both should be imposed. The verse is therefore needed to tell us that they do not combine. Conversely, if the verse told us only that death and lashes do not combine, one might reason that this is because both are inflicted upon the body and it would be overly harsh \u8212- but death and money are categorically different, one upon the body and one upon the finances, so perhaps both can coexist. The other verse is therefore needed to tell us that death overrides monetary liability as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that the extended discussion of {\i kim lei} across many dapim focused exclusively on {\i mamon}. The question of whether the death penalty overrides {\i malkos} when the death penalty is not actually imposed \u8212- analogous to Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's rule that a capital offense overrides monetary liability even without actual execution \u8212- remains a distinct question not fully resolved here, and one that will be explored more fully in Makkos and elsewhere in {\i shas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Murder & Ransom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses a different category of verse: {\i velo tikchu kofer le-nefesh rotze\u8217'ach asher hu rasha lamus ki mos yumas} \u8212- do not take a {\i kofer}, a ransom, for a murderer deserving of death; he must be put to death. At first glance this might seem to be another iteration of the {\i kim lei} principle \u8212- do not impose both death and a monetary payment. But the {\i gemara} quickly clarifies: what this verse is actually prohibiting is something entirely different. It is prohibiting the use of money as a means of {\i escape} from the death penalty \u8212- allowing a wealthy murderer to buy his way out of execution. In ancient society, this was not an abstraction; powerful individuals could negotiate their way out of capital punishment through payment. The Torah categorically forbids it: {\i lo tishkol mamon minei ve-tiftarei mi-katol} \u8212- do not collect money from him and thereby exempt him from death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the broader question of what a {\i kofer} actually represents. There is one place in the Torah where {\i kofer} is recognized as a legitimate response to a death \u8212- the case of the {\i shor haniskal}, the goring ox. There, the verse says {\i gam be\u8217'alav yumat} \u8212- the owner too shall die \u8212- and the Torah then provides that a {\i kofer} may be imposed instead. The {\i gemara} in Bava Kamma debates whether the {\i kofer} in that case represents the value of the owner\u8217's life or the value of the victim\u8217's life \u8212- {\i demei mazik} or {\i demei nizak} \u8212- assessed by the slave-market value of whichever party\u8217's life is being measured. In any case, this is the Torah\u8217's lone concession to the concept of monetary redemption for a life. The verse about {\i rotze\u8217'ach} is telling us that when a human being deliberately or negligently causes another person\u8217's death, that concession does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Manslaughter Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to a second verse: {\i velo tikchu kofer lanus el ir miklato} \u8212- do not take a ransom for someone who is obligated to flee to the {\i ir miklat}, the city of refuge. This addresses the case of {\i shogeg} \u8212- unintentional manslaughter \u8212- where the killer must go into exile. One might think that since the killing was unintentional, a monetary payment to the victim\u8217's family could serve as an adequate substitute for exile. The verse prohibits this: {\i lo tishkol mamon minei ve-tiftarei mi-galus} \u8212- do not collect money from him and thereby exempt him from exile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why are both verses needed? If only the verse about intentional murder existed, one might reason: of course you cannot buy your way out of execution \u8212- the crime was monstrous and intentional, and justice demands blood for blood. But for unintentional manslaughter \u8212- {\i shogeg}, where the killer did not intend harm \u8212- the severity is lower, and a monetary settlement with the family might be seen as adequate. So the second verse is needed to preclude that option. Conversely, if only the verse about {\i shogeg} existed, one might reason: that verse precludes ransom for exile only because exile itself is not the destruction of life \u8212- {\i leika ibud neshama} \u8212- and we are not going to let money substitute for that particular obligation. But for intentional murder, where we must take a life \u8212- {\i de-ika ibud neshama} \u8212- perhaps the powerful human impulse to preserve life, even a murderer\u8217's, should lead us to allow a monetary settlement. So the verse about intentional murder is equally necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egla Arufa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces another verse: {\i ule-aretz lo yechupar la-dam asher shupach bah ki im be-dam shofcho} \u8212- the land shall not be atoned for the blood spilled upon it except by the blood of the one who spilled it. This verse appears in the context of the {\i egla arufa}, the heifer whose neck is broken when a corpse is found between cities and the murderer is unknown. The {\i gemara} asks: what legal point is this verse adding? We already know that a murderer cannot pay a ransom. What does this {\i egla arufa} context contribute?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer comes from a {\i baraita}: the verse teaches that if the community performed the {\i egla arufa} ritual and subsequently the murderer was found, he is not exempt from execution. One might have thought that the ritual accomplished an atonement and effectively closed the case. The verse is telling us otherwise: the land is not atoned until the murderer\u8217's own blood is shed. The {\i egla arufa} addresses the community\u8217's moral responsibility in the face of an unsolved crime but does not constitute a substitute for actual justice when the perpetrator is identified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Execution Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An additional verse follows: {\i ve-ata tiva\u8217'er ha-dam ha-naki mi-kirbecha} \u8212- and you shall purge the innocent blood from your midst. This appears at the end of the {\i egla arufa} passage, and the {\i gemara} asks what it adds beyond what we already know. The answer, derived from a {\i baraita}, is that this verse creates an analogy between the {\i egla arufa} and the execution of murderers: just as the heifer\u8217's neck is broken at the throat, so too those executed by the sword are killed at the throat \u8212- {\i min ha-tzavar}. The verse is connecting all cases of bloodshed to the {\i egla arufa}, teaching that the method of execution for murder should parallel the method used for the heifer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might press further: should the analogy be complete? The heifer is killed with a cleaver \u8212- {\i kufitz} \u8212- and at the nape of the neck \u8212- {\i mimul oref} \u8212- the back of the neck. Should murderers be executed the same way? Rav Nachman, citing Rabbah bar Avuha, answers by invoking the verse {\i ve-ahavta le-rei\u8217'acha kamocha} \u8212- love your neighbor as yourself. Even a condemned man remains your {\i rei\u8217'ach}, your fellow, and the obligation to choose a {\i mitah yafah}, a humane death, applies. Cutting the throat with a sword is considered more humane than a blow to the nape with a hatchet, and the concern for the dignity of the condemned shapes the specific method of execution. This reflects a broader principle in the laws of capital punishment in Sanhedrin, where the precise form of each execution is calibrated with sensitivity to the humanity of the condemned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cherem & Erech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to yet another verse: {\i kol cherem asher yucharam min ha-adam lo yipadeh mos yumat} \u8212- any person placed under the ban shall not be redeemed; he shall be put to death. The {\i gemara} understands this not as a verse about wartime {\i cherem} of enemy populations, but as a verse about any individual deserving of a capital sentence. We already know he cannot pay a monetary ransom \u8212- {\i lo tishkol mamon} \u8212- so what is this verse adding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} teaches: if a person is being taken out for execution and someone declares {\i erchi alai} \u8212- I accept upon myself to pay his {\i erech}, his fixed Torahic valuation \u8212- is the pledge valid? The {\i erech} system in the Torah sets fixed monetary values based on age and sex, independent of market value. One might think that since the {\i erech} is a fixed number unrelated to market conditions, it still applies even to a condemned man \u8212- one simply looks him up on the table and pays. The verse {\i lo yipadeh} teaches that no such pledge takes effect. The condemned man, at the moment of being led to execution, is regarded as standing outside the normal framework of human transactions; he has no {\i erech} that can be pledged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} further specifies that this applies from the moment his sentence is finalized \u8212- {\i mi-she-nigmar dino} \u8212- but not before. The qualifier {\i min ha-adam} \u8212- \u8220"from among mankind\u8221" \u8212- excludes everyone: meaning only some people, those whose sentence is sealed, fall under this rule. Before judgment is finalized, the pledge would take effect normally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chananya ben Akavia disagrees and holds that the condemned man does have an {\i erech} that can be pledged, reasoning that {\i damav ketzuvim} \u8212- his value is fixed by Torah schedule and does not depend on circumstances. For him, the verse of {\i kol cherem} must teach something else: it comes to address the position of Rabbi Yishmael, son of Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka, who observed that we find cases where those liable to death at the hands of Heaven can achieve atonement through monetary payment \u8212- namely, the {\i shor haniskal}, where the Torah itself authorizes a {\i kofer} in place of the owner\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ransom Ever\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might therefore have thought that the same option exists for human court-imposed capital penalties. {\i Yachol af bidei adam kein?} The verse {\i kol cherem min ha-adam lo yipadeh} forecloses this: any person liable to a humanly imposed death sentence cannot be redeemed monetarily. The verse then addresses the full spectrum of capital offenses. The {\i baraita} distinguishes between {\i misos chamuros} \u8212- weightier capital offenses for which even an unintentional violation ({\i shogeg}) carries no path to atonement through a {\i korban} (because the Torah never attaches {\i kares} to them, and therefore no {\i chatas} is brought for their inadvertent commission) \u8212- and {\i misos kalos}, lighter capital offenses for which inadvertent violation does carry {\i kares} and therefore obligates a {\i korban}. From the word {\i kol} in {\i kol cherem} \u8212- \u8220"every\u8221" person under the ban \u8212- we learn that even for the less weighty capital offenses, no monetary ransom is accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether this was not already known from the verse prohibiting {\i kofer} for the {\i rotze\u8217'ach}. The answer given by Rami bar Chama is that you need all three verses together. The verse about {\i rotze\u8217'ach} might have been understood narrowly \u8212- only for intentional murder committed in a manner whose inadvertent counterpart carries no atonement, i.e., a killing done with an upward motion, where if done inadvertently the killer would not go into exile. But what of a killing done with a downward motion, where the inadvertent killer {\i does} go into exile? That case has a {\i lighter} quality in one sense \u8212- it is more amenable to atonement \u8212- and one might have thought monetary settlement could be accepted there. The verse {\i kol cherem} tells us otherwise: regardless of the relative weight of the capital offense, no ransom is ever accepted, and no one condemned to death by the court can purchase release through money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This synthesis brings the discussion to a close: across all capital offenses, whether more severe or less, whether involving intentional or unintentional killing, whether addressed through the {\i rotze\u8217'ach} verse, the {\i galus} verse, or the {\i kol cherem} verse, the governing principle is consistent \u8212- no monetary substitute replaces the judgment of the court, and the principle of {\i kim lei bi-draba minei} is grounded in this uncompromising framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 39\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Knas: Betrothal & Di (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Opinions on Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 39, picking up from 38b. The {\i gemara} at the end of yesterday\u8217's daf worked out the implications of the various {\i psukim} centered on the phrase {\i asher lo orasa} \u8212- \u8220"who has not been betrothed.\u8221" The Torah states: {\i naara besula asher lo orasa veshachav ima}, a young virgin who has not been betrothed. The question is: what is the law when a girl has been betrothed but is no longer? If she is currently betrothed \u8212- even at the level of mere {\i erusin} rather than {\i nissuin} \u8212- everyone agrees there is no {\i knas} payment, because {\i kim lei bidraba minei}: she is a married woman, and the offender is liable to capital punishment, which supersedes the monetary penalty. But if she had been betrothed at the level of {\i erusin} only, and was subsequently divorced \u8212- such that she can still be a {\i besula} \u8212- and is then violated, a dispute emerges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose vs.\u160 ?Two Akivas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three distinct positions emerge. Rabbi Yose HaGlili holds that she does not satisfy the criteria of the verse: she is not fully a {\i naara} in the relevant sense, nor a {\i besula} in the relevant sense, and therefore no {\i knas} is paid at all. Rabbi Akiva, as he appears in our {\i Mishna}, holds that she gains a degree of independence from her father, and therefore the {\i knas} is paid \u8212- but it goes to her, not to him. This position requires explanation: normally, a girl who was betrothed at the level of {\i erusin} and then divorced does not become independent of her father. Full independence from the father\u8217's jurisdiction is achieved only through {\i nissuin} followed by divorce, not through {\i erusin} followed by divorce. Nevertheless, Rabbi Akiva reads the Torah as signaling that for the specific purposes of this {\i halakha}, she has sufficient independence that the {\i knas} goes to her. The third position is Rabbi Akiva as he appears in the {\i baraita}, who holds that the verse carries no exclusionary implication, and the standard law applies: in a case of {\i erusin} followed by divorce, the payment goes to the father, as in any other case. So there are three possibilities: no one receives the {\i knas}; the father receives it, with no change in the standard law; or she herself receives it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Knas to Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now picks up at the {\i baraita} beginning {\i tanu rabbanan}, approximately ten lines from the bottom of 38b. The {\i baraita} states: {\i knaso l\u8217'mi? l\u8217'aviha} \u8212- who receives the {\i knas}? The father. {\i Ve\u8217'yesh omrim le\u8217'atzmah} \u8212- and some say she receives it herself. Since this {\i baraita} appears without a limiting context, it sounds as though it is discussing an ordinary case. The {\i gemara} therefore asks: why would she receive it herself? Rav Chisda explains: {\i hacha b\u8217'naara shenisorasa venisgarsha askinan} \u8212- we are dealing with the case of our {\i Mishna}, where there was {\i erusin} followed by divorce. This {\i baraita} thus mirrors precisely the debate between the two versions of Rabbi Akiva: Rabbi Akiva of the {\i Mishna} who says she keeps it, and Rabbi Akiva of the {\i baraita} who says the father keeps it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Knas: Betrothal & Di (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligation vs.\u160 ?Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Underlying this entire discussion is a deeper conceptual question: is the {\i knas} fundamentally owed to the girl, with the father merely collecting on her behalf, or is it owed directly to the father? One way of understanding the father\u8217's entitlement is that the payment is conceptually made to her but then transfers to him \u8212- analogous to the principle of {\i ma shakana eved kana rabo}, what an {\i eved} acquires belongs to his master. Any financial benefit accruing from a {\i naara} is channeled to her father by virtue of the principle of {\i shvach neurim l\u8217'aviha}; the payment originates as hers but he is entitled to it. This distinction will have significant practical implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Death Before Trial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rules: {\i ba aleha u\u8217'mesa patur} \u8212- if he violated her and she subsequently died before the case came to {\i beit din}, he is exempt from payment, even though the payment would ordinarily go to the father. The source is the verse {\i venasan l\u8217'avi ha\u8217'naara} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall give to the father of the {\i naara}\u8221" \u8212- which implies that at the time of payment, one must be able to point to the girl and say she is alive. {\i Velo l\u8217'avi mesa} \u8212- it does not say the father of one who is dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fundamental Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling can be understood as a {\i gezeiras hakasuv}, a decree of Scripture with no further rationale required. But it also resonates with the conceptual framework described above. If the fundamental obligation is to her, and she is not alive to be the object of that obligation, then there is nobody for whom payment can be made. An important qualification emerges, however. There is a distinction between the moment he physically hands over the money and the moment {\i beit din} issues its ruling. Once {\i beit din} adjudicates and declares him liable \u8212- fifty {\i kesef} to the father \u8212- a concrete debt crystallizes. From that point forward, even if the girl were to die, the obligation to the father stands as an independent debt. Abaye\u8217's ruling therefore concerns the case where she died {\i before} the adjudication, before {\i beit din} rendered its verdict. At that point, since she is not alive, there is no one to bring him to court and no one in relation to whom the obligation exists. But once {\i beit din} rules and the obligation has crystallized as a debt owed to the father, her subsequent death does not dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bagrut & Death Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maturation in Grave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A passage the {\i gemara} presents as straightforward for Abaye was a matter of genuine uncertainty for Rava. The {\i gemara} introduces Rava\u8217's question: {\i yesh bagar bakever o ein bagar bakever?} \u8212- does maturation occur in the grave, or not? The scenario Rava envisions is this: a girl is violated while a {\i naara}, then she dies, and by the time the case reaches {\i beit din}, enough time has passed that, had she been alive, she would have attained the status of {\i bogeret}. Does the law take account of this hypothetical maturation? And if so, does the {\i knas} now pass to her child rather than to her father?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implications are striking. If we say {\i yesh bagar bakever} \u8212- yes, maturation occurs in the grave \u8212- then we treat her as though she were a {\i bogeret} at the time the case reaches court. A {\i bogeret} is fully emancipated from her father\u8217's jurisdiction, and everyone agrees the {\i knas} would go to her. Since she is dead but is treated as a {\i bogeret}, the father drops out of the equation, and the payment would pass to her child. If we say {\i ein bagar bakever} \u8212- no, we look at her actual status at death, which was {\i naara} \u8212- then the father remains in the picture and would collect. Crucially, both positions assume, against Abaye, that a payment can still be made even though she is dead. The only question is to whom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pregnancy Impossibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges the scenario itself: how can this woman have a child? For the scenario to work, she must have become pregnant as a {\i naara} and given birth before becoming a {\i bogeret}, yet died before reaching {\i bagrut}. But is that possible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Women & Moch\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} invokes the famous teaching transmitted by Rav Bibi before Rav Nachman: {\i shlosh nashim meshamshot bamoch} \u8212- three women may use a {\i moch}, a contraceptive sponge. These are: a {\i ktana}, an underage girl; a {\i meuberet}, a pregnant woman; and a {\i meinika}, a nursing woman. A {\i ktana} may use it {\i shema titaber vetamut} \u8212- lest she become pregnant and die as a result. A pregnant woman may use it {\i shema ta\u8217'aseh ubarah sandal} \u8212- lest the second conception harm the first fetus, either by crushing it (Rashi\u8217's explanation) or by adversely affecting it in some other way (Tosafot\u8217's approach). A nursing woman may use it lest a new pregnancy compromise her ability to nurse her current child, endangering its health.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} further specifies: which age of girl is considered a {\i ktana} for these purposes? From eleven years and one day until twelve years and one day. Younger than eleven, there is no concern about pregnancy. Older than twelve years and a day, pregnancy will no longer endanger her life. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is that these three women are permitted or obligated to use this form of contraception, while the Sages say {\i achas zu v\u8217'achas zu meshamshot kedarka v\u8217'oleches} \u8212- all of them, including the {\i ktana} and the pregnant woman, engage in relations normally, relying on the principle of {\i shomer psa\u8217'im Hashem} \u8212- God protects the simple. This passage is the central Talmudic source for contemporary discussions of permissible birth control. The debates about whether {\i moch} is used before or after intercourse (Rashi versus Tosafot), and whether the other women are merely not obligated to use it or are actively forbidden from using it, carry enormous weight for how halakha approaches various modern contraceptive methods, particularly regarding the issue of {\i zera l\u8217'vatalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Months Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is relevant to the present discussion is the premise embedded in the {\i ktana} ruling: a girl who becomes pregnant below the age of twelve may die as a result. The {\i gemara} therefore assumes she cannot become pregnant before twelve. But the status of {\i naara} begins at twelve, with the appearance of two pubic hairs, and Shmuel teaches: {\i ein bein na\u8217'arut l\u8217'bagrut ela shisha chodashim} \u8212- there are only six months between the status of {\i na\u8217'arut} and the status of {\i bagrut}. The {\i gemara} addresses whether Shmuel means this as a minimum (at least six months) or a maximum (no more than six months). It concludes that Shmuel\u8217's formulation means it will never take more than six months: {\i bagrut} is determined by the physical signs of bodily maturation, but Shmuel assures us those signs will manifest within six months of {\i na\u8217'arut} at the latest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is structurally analogous to other areas of {\i halakha} where a measurable time threshold substitutes for case-by-case physical assessment. The {\i gemara} elsewhere determines {\i chametz} formation by the visual appearance of the dough \u8212- cracking, crusting, and so forth \u8212- but a single time marker (eighteen minutes) is derived from the principle that the physical signs will never appear in less than that time, allowing us to dispense with individual inspection. Similarly, {\i bagrut} is definitionally a physical state of the body, but six months after {\i na\u8217'arut} we may declare it certain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reformulated Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given all this, the scenario of {\i yesh bagar bakever} as originally formulated is impossible. She cannot become pregnant as a {\i ktana} without dying, so she can only conceive after turning twelve. But she has only six months from {\i na\u8217'arut} to {\i bagrut}, and a woman cannot give birth in six months. She therefore cannot have a child before becoming a {\i bogeret}, and if she has no children, the question of whether her child inherits is moot \u8212- since her next heir would simply be the father, and then whether or not there is {\i bagrut bakever}, the father collects regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reformulated Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore reformulates Rava\u8217's question: {\i yesh bagar bakever ufaka av?} \u8212- does {\i bagrut} occur in the grave, thereby removing the father from the equation? Or {\i ein bagar bakever velo paka av} \u8212- is there no {\i bagrut} in the grave, leaving the father within the equation? If {\i yesh bagar bakever}, we treat her as a {\i bogeret} at the time the case reaches court: she is dead, but the father, who is only relevant in relation to a {\i naara}, is no longer relevant. Since she is dead and no longer a {\i naara}, nobody collects. If {\i ein bagar bakever}, she retains the status of {\i naara}, the father remains in the picture, and he collects. Both versions still assume against Abaye that even when she is dead the case can proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death as Emancipation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi offers yet another formulation: {\i mita osa bagrut o ein osa bagrut?} \u8212- does death itself function as emancipation from the father, the way {\i bagrut} does? The principle {\i lametim chofshi} \u8212- the dead are free \u8212- might suggest that death itself removes a woman from the father\u8217's jurisdiction. If so, since she died, the father is no longer relevant; since she is also not alive to collect, nobody collects. This is essentially Abaye\u8217's position, arrived at through different reasoning. Alternatively, death does not sever the father\u8217's entitlement, and the court case may proceed with payment to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether the father collects even after the girl\u8217's death is also bound up with the distinctive nature of {\i knas} as opposed to ordinary monetary obligations. An ordinary debt becomes part of an estate and can be collected regardless of whether the original obligee is alive. A {\i knas}, by contrast, is personal: it is assessed and collected in relation to a specific individual. The question {\i ein adam morish knas levanav} \u8212- whether one can bequeath a {\i knas} to one\u8217's heirs \u8212- reflects this tension. The {\i gemara} leaves these questions unresolved: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status Change Mid-Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betrothal After Rape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With these foundational questions in view, Rava poses a further question to Abaye: {\i ba\u8217'al ve\u8217'nisarsa mahu?} \u8212- he violated her, and then, before the case came to {\i beit din}, she became betrothed. What does Rabbi Akiva say? Rabbi Akiva of the {\i Mishna} holds that if she was betrothed and then divorced {\i before} the rape, the {\i knas} goes to her. But what if she was raped first and only became an {\i arusah} afterwards? Does Rabbi Akiva say the {\i knas} now follows her changed status and goes to her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time of Act Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds by pressing on the textual logic. The Torah does not say {\i venasan l\u8217'avi hana\u8217'arah asher lo orasah} \u8212- \u8220"he shall give to the father of the {\i naara} who is not betrothed\u8221" \u8212- referring to her status at the time of payment. The phrase {\i asher lo orasah} in the verse refers to her status at the time of the act itself. What matters is her condition when the violation occurred. Her status at the time of payment is irrelevant. She was not betrothed at the time of the rape, so the {\i knas} goes to the father regardless of what happens afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin Counterexample\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this: we know from a {\i baraita} that if she was raped and subsequently contracted full marriage \u8212- {\i nisuin} \u8212- before the case came to {\i beit din}, she retains the {\i knas} for herself. Where does that ruling come from? The Torah does not say {\i venasan l\u8217'avi hana\u8217'arah asher lo nesuah} \u8212- \u8220"the father of the girl who is not married.\u8221" Why, then, does her {\i nissuin} after the rape change who receives payment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General vs.\u160 ?Specific Rul\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye distinguishes: {\i bagrut} and {\i nissuin} are general modes of emancipation from the father. When a woman reaches {\i bagrut}, she is fully emancipated from her father in all areas of {\i halakha}. When a woman contracts {\i nissuin}, she likewise exits her father\u8217's complete jurisdiction in every domain. These are categorical, comprehensive legal transitions. Therefore, even without an explicit verse, there is sound legal logic to say that if she becomes a {\i bogeret} or becomes {\i nesuah} between the rape and the court case, and she is at that point fully emancipated, the {\i knas} follows her emancipated status and goes to her. Rashi notes that there is indeed a textual peg for the {\i bagrut} case: the verse says {\i l\u8217'avi hana\u8217'arah} \u8212- \u8220"the father of the {\i naara}\u8221" \u8212- at the moment of payment; if she is by then a {\i bogeret}, the father is no longer {\i avi hana\u8217'arah} in the relevant sense, and she retains the money. And since {\i nissuin} also constitutes complete emancipation just as {\i bagrut} does, the same conclusion applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erusin Lacks Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Erusin}, however, is a different matter entirely. As the {\i Mishna} elsewhere teaches, a {\i naara hame\u8217'orasah} \u8212- a betrothed young woman \u8212- has {\i both} her father and her betrothed able to annul her vows. She is not emancipated from the father; she is in fact under dual jurisdiction. {\i Erusin} is not a general mode of emancipation. Therefore, if she becomes betrothed {\i after} the rape, there is neither textual support nor logical compulsion to say the {\i knas} follows her to herself. Unlike {\i bagrut} and {\i nissuin}, which by their nature fully remove her from the father\u8217's domain, {\i erusin} does no such thing. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling in the {\i Mishna} \u8212- that a woman who had been betrothed and then divorced at the time of the rape stands in a special category \u8212- was a {\i gezeiras hakasuv} read from the specific language of the verse, not a general principle of emancipation. It therefore cannot be extended to cover a subsequent {\i erusin} after the act. The conclusion is that in such a case, there is neither a source nor a logic to move the {\i knas} from the father to her, and the father collects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mefateh vs.\u160 ?Ones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now arrives at the next {\i Mishna}. The {\i mefateh} \u8212- the seducer \u8212- pays three things: {\i boshet}, {\i pgam}, and {\i knas}. The {\i ones} \u8212- the rapist \u8212- pays four, adding {\i tzaar}, payment for pain and suffering. Beyond this difference in payments, there is a structural difference in obligation: the {\i ones} pays immediately and is required to marry the girl; the {\i mefateh} pays the {\i knas} only if he refuses to marry her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Payments Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three payments common to both cases require explanation. {\i Boshet} is the payment for shame and embarrassment \u8212- a standard element of assault liability. {\i Pgam} is the diminishment of the woman\u8217's value, the word the {\i Mishna} uses in place of the more general term {\i nezek}. The choice of {\i pgam} over {\i nezek} is significant: {\i nezek} connotes a visible, externally observable physical injury, while {\i pgam} carries a connotation of stigma and moral diminishment, appropriate for the loss of virginity and the reduction in marriage prospects that results from it. The standard elements of assault liability that are absent in these cases are {\i shevet} \u8212- compensation for lost wages during recovery \u8212- and {\i ripuy} \u8212- medical expenses. The {\i gemara} assumes these are not present even in rape, on the premise that no additional violence accompanied the act itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The absence of {\i tzaar} in the case of {\i mefateh} rests on the premise that since she consented, there was no pain and suffering to compensate. As to why {\i boshet} and {\i pgam} are paid at all when she agreed, this requires further examination \u8212- and that is precisely what the {\i gemara} will take up.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mefateh Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The seducer pays the {\i knas} only if he declines to marry her. The Torah\u8217's language in the case of {\i mefateh} is: {\i mahor yimahrena lo l\u8217'ishah} \u8212- he shall marry her as a wife \u8212- and {\i v\u8217'im ma\u8217'ein yima\u8217'ein aviha} \u8212- and if the father refuses, {\i kessef yishkol k\u8217'mohar habetulos} \u8212- he shall pay money equal to the bride-price of virgins. The {\i mefateh} is thus not compelled to marry against his will; if he chooses not to marry, he pays the {\i knas}. The father, too, retains veto power over the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ones}, by contrast, pays the {\i knas} immediately and is required to marry the woman. The {\i Mishna} expresses this with the vivid phrase {\i ha\u8217'ones shoseh b\u8217'atzitzo} \u8212- the rapist drinks from his own pottery shard. He made his choice; he must live with it, whatever she is like. Even if she is lame, even if she is blind, even if she is afflicted with boils, he must marry her and may not divorce her. The only exception is if she is forbidden to him {\i halakhically}: if she committed adultery, or if she is a {\i mamzeret} and is thus legally prohibited from marrying a Jew, the verse {\i velo tihyeh le\u8217'ishah} \u8212- \u8220"she shall be his wife\u8221" \u8212- is read as applying only to {\i ishah re\u8217'uyah lo}, a woman who is legally permitted to him. In that circumstance the obligation to marry does not apply, and the marriage may not be contracted or must be dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Key Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two major questions emerge from this {\i Mishna}. First: why is there any payment at all by the {\i mefateh} if the woman consented? Second: why does the {\i ones} pay immediately and must marry, while the {\i mefateh} pays only if he declines to marry, and the father may refuse the match? The {\i gemara} will address these, but it begins with a prior issue: why is there no payment for {\i tzaar} by the {\i mefateh}, even granting that all these other payments apply?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Definition of Tzaar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzaar Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what exactly is the {\i tzaar} that the {\i ones} pays? Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel answers: {\i tzaar shechivtah al gabei karka} \u8212- the pain of being thrown to the ground in the act of rape. The payment for {\i tzaar} is not primarily understood here as compensation for the act of intercourse itself, but rather for the attendant physical violence of the assault.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira challenges: if he threw her onto soft garments rather than bare ground, would there be no {\i tzaar} and no payment? The challenge points to the instability of grounding {\i tzaar} in the violence of the fall rather than in something more intrinsic to the act. The {\i baraita} of Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda, citing Rabbi Shimon, offers a more sweeping position: the {\i ones} does not pay {\i tzaar} at all, {\i mipnei shesofah lehitztayeir tachat ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- because she will eventually experience the same pain with her husband. This view sees the pain of first intercourse as an inevitable life experience that the rapist merely caused to happen earlier; since she would have suffered it eventually with a husband, the rapist owes no special payment for it. This reasoning, strikingly, presupposes no meaningful difference between the experience of forced intercourse and consensual intercourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones vs.\u160 ?Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds on behalf of the majority: {\i ein domeh niva\u8217'alah be-ones li-niva\u8217'alah be-ratzon} \u8212- intercourse by rape is not comparable to intercourse by consent. This seems to be exactly the right answer: the two experiences are fundamentally different. Yet, as will become apparent, the {\i gemara} does not press this insight to its full conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consent Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman, citing Rabbah bar Avuha, offers an alternative account of the {\i tzaar}: it is {\i tzaar she-pissuk haraglayim} \u8212- the pain of having one\u8217's legs forced open. The verse cited is {\i va-tifteki et raglayikh le-khol over} \u8212- \u8220"you opened your legs to all who passed,\u8221" interpreted here as referring to the physical act of forcing a woman\u8217's legs apart. This reformulation leads immediately to an objection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i tzaar} of the {\i ones} consists in forcing her legs open, why is there no such payment in the case of {\i mefateh}? He too opens her legs. Rav Nachman responds by analogy: {\i mashal de-mefuttah le-ma dabar domeh} \u8212- to what is the seduction case compared? To a person who says to his fellow: tear my silk garment and you are exempt. The act is the same; but since she consented, she has, in effect, waived the claim. The {\i gemara} then raises an objection: her waiver is not effective, since {\i d\u8217'avu\u8217'ah nihu} \u8212- it is her father\u8217's. This is why {\i boshet} and {\i pgam} are still paid even in the case of {\i mefateh}: she cannot give away rights that belong to her father. And if the same logic applied to {\i tzaar}, the father should collect that as well. The {\i gemara}\u8217's implicit answer is that in the case of consensual intercourse there is simply no real {\i tzaar} to speak of; the pain is so minimal relative to the pleasure that it does not register as a cognizable harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now puts this to the test: {\i pikchos she-bein omros mefuttah ein lah tzaar} \u8212- wise women say a seduced woman has no {\i tzaar}. But the {\i gemara} challenges: {\i va-ha ka chazinan de-it lah} \u8212- we observe that she does have pain, even in consensual first intercourse. Three {\i amoraim} report what their wives told them on this subject. Abaye reports that his nursemaid \u8212- his mother having died in his infancy \u8212- described the pain as {\i k\u8217'maya chamimei al reisha d\u8217'karcha}, like hot water poured over a bald head: somewhat painful but also pleasurable. Rava reports that his wife, the daughter of Rav Chisda, described it as {\i ki rivda d\u8217'kuslisa}, like the puncture of a bloodletter\u8217's instrument. Rav Papa reports that his wife, the daughter of Abba Sura, described it as {\i ki nahama d\u8217'kushk\u8217'shei b\u8217'chinkhei}, like coarse bread ground against the cheek \u8212- again a mix of sensation that implies pain alongside pleasure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pain-Pleasure Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These testimonies lead the {\i gemara} to its resolution: yes, there is some pain for the {\i mefuttah} in first intercourse, but in the consensual case the pleasure outweighs the pain. The act as a whole is experienced as pleasurable, and therefore there is no cognizable {\i tzaar} for which payment is owed. In the case of {\i ones}, however, the pain is not outweighed by pleasure, and payment is therefore owed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Critical Gap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking \u8212- and troubling \u8212- about this entire discussion is what the {\i gemara} never quite says directly. The line {\i ein domeh niva\u8217'alah be-ones li-niva\u8217'alah be-ratzon} is cited and then essentially set aside without being developed. Rav Nachman\u8217's formulation locates the {\i tzaar} of rape in the forcing open of the legs \u8212- an attendant circumstance \u8212- and the subsequent discussion turns on whether that circumstance is present or absent in seduction, with the only meaningful distinction identified as her consent rather than any difference in the experience itself. The {\i gemara} never directly acknowledges that the act of forced intercourse is intrinsically and dramatically more physically painful than consensual intercourse \u8212- quite apart from any attendant violence, and leaving aside entirely the question of psychological trauma and its lasting effects. Even if we bracket the traumatic dimension, the physical reality of intercourse when a woman is not physiologically prepared and is actively resisting is categorically different from the experience when she is willing. The {\i gemara} consistently focuses on peripheral circumstances \u8212- the fall to the ground, the forcing open of the legs, the question of consent as a waiver \u8212- rather than on the nature of the act itself. This is a genuinely difficult {\i gemara} to sit with.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 40\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 50 Shekel Fine & Other P\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} with which this {\i gemara} opens enumerates the full range of monetary obligations that arise in the case of rape. Beyond the well-known fixed payment of fifty {\i shekel}, the {\i mishnah} specifies {\i boshet} (embarrassment) and {\i pegam} (devaluation), calibrated to the persons involved, as well as {\i tzaar} (suffering), though this last item will not be addressed in the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis. {\i Boshet} is assessed according to the degree of shame \u8212- a function of both the perpetrator and the victim \u8212- while {\i pegam} is determined by recourse to the slave market. The {\i knas}, the fifty {\i shekel} fine, however, is fixed for everyone. With this framework established, the {\i gemara} immediately presses forward with a series of questions about the logical and scriptural basis of these rulings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's first question is foundational: how do we know that the additional payments of {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} are owed at all, beyond the fifty {\i shekel}? Perhaps the fifty {\i shekel} was intended to cover everything \u8212- the physical violation, the shame, the devaluation \u8212- as a flat, comprehensive payment. The {\i gemara} poses this as a sharp logical challenge: {\i bat melachim chamishim, bat hedyot chamishim} \u8212- should the daughter of a king and the daughter of a commoner each yield the same fifty {\i shekel} to the rapist? The intuitive sense of fairness rebels against such a result, suggesting that there must be additional variable payments layered on top of the fixed amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Zeira\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira reframes the question. The problem, he argues, is not the difference in social standing but rather a difference in physical state. Consider the following scenario: two men successively violate the same woman \u8212- the first in an unnatural manner ({\i shelo ke-darkah}), and the second naturally. Because the first act, being anal rather than vaginal, did not rupture the hymen, the woman retains the status of a {\i betulah} for purposes of the fifty-{\i shekel} obligation. Thus both men owe fifty {\i shekel} for violating the same woman. Surely it would be absurd to say, {\i ba shleimah chamishim, ba pegumah chamishim} \u8212- that the man who violated a fully intact woman and the man who violated a woman already compromised by a prior assault each pay an identical amount. This physical distinction, Rabbi Zeira argues, demonstrates that the fifty {\i shekel} cannot be all-inclusive, and additional variable payments must exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that from a moral perspective this reframing is, if anything, more troubling than the original question about social standing. Why it should be \u8220"obvious\u8221" that the second man\u8217's payment ought to be less \u8212- merely because the woman had previously been violated \u8212- is not self-evident. Yet the {\i gemara}\u8217's logic rests on the premise that the physical state of the woman\u8217's body renders the disparity manifest in a way that social status does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, refuses to concede even on these terms. He returns with a counter-analogy: by the case of an ox that gores a slave, the {\i Torah} likewise fixes a payment of thirty {\i shekel} regardless of the slave\u8217's earning potential or physical condition. {\i Eved nokev margaliyot shloshim, eved oseh ma\u8217'aseh machat shloshim} \u8212- the slave who could pierce pearls and the slave fit only for menial work each yield the same payment. That the {\i Torah} can set a fixed amount regardless of individual circumstance is therefore not logically incoherent. The same might be true of the fifty {\i shekel} in our case. Abaye\u8217's challenge thus stands: you cannot derive additional variable payments from logic alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye ultimately concedes that the derivation cannot rest on logic and instead grounds it in a verse. The {\i Torah} states, {\i tachat asher ina} \u8212- he shall pay in exchange for his having oppressed and violated her. Abaye reads this as indicating that the fifty {\i shekel} is specifically payment for the act of forced intercourse itself. {\i Michlal d\u8217'ika boshet u\u8217'fgam} \u8212- by implication there must be other payments for other dimensions of the harm, namely the embarrassment and the devaluation, which are not subsumed within the fifty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava arrives at the same conclusion from a different verse: {\i v\u8217'natan ha\u8217'ish hashochev imah l\u8217'avi hana\u8217'arah chamishim kasef} \u8212- the man who lies with her shall give to the father of the young woman fifty pieces of silver. The verse explicitly links the act of lying with her to the payment of fifty. From this, Rava reads {\i hana\u8217'at shchiva} \u8212- the pleasure of intercourse \u8212- as the specific object of the fifty-{\i shekel} payment. Since that payment covers only that pleasure, {\i michlal d\u8217'ika boshet u\u8217'fgam}, the attendant harms of embarrassment and devaluation must generate separate obligations. The label {\i hana\u8217'at shchiva} \u8212- the pleasure of the sex act \u8212- applied in the context of rape is admittedly arresting, but the {\i gemara}\u8217's legal point is clear: the fifty {\i shekel} is narrowly targeted, and everything else falls outside it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Who Receives Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question of Recipient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the existence of {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} payments established, the {\i gemara} turns to the next question: to whom do these payments go? The {\i Torah}\u8217's explicit statement that the fifty {\i shekel} goes to the father does not answer this question, since {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} are inferred obligations without a stated recipient. Perhaps they should go to the woman herself, who after all is the one violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seduction Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The immediate rejoinder \u8212- already flagged by {\i Tosafot} \u8212- is a practical one. In the case of {\i pituy}, seduction, the {\i Torah} also mandates {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} payments. But if these payments belong to the woman, there is a difficulty: she consented. On what basis would she collect payment for shame and devaluation she voluntarily incurred? The very existence of these payments in the context of seduction proves that they belong to the father, not the daughter. The father did not consent to the seduction of his daughter; his interests were violated regardless of her willingness. Hence the payments must flow to him. This observation also yields the curious implication that even a consenting daughter is considered to have been shamed \u8212- since the shame payment is hers and she receives it via her father, not because she personally objects, but because the legal categories operate independently of her consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevach Neurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: even accepting that the payments go to the father, what is the textual basis for this? It attempts to ground it in the verse {\i b\u8217'neureha beit aviha}, written in connection with the father\u8217's power to annul his daughter\u8217's vows: in her youth, in the father\u8217's house. From this the {\i gemara} tries to extract a general principle \u8212- {\i kol shevach neurim l\u8217'aviha}, all benefits that accrue from her youth belong to her father. If that principle were sound, any monetary right arising during her minority would belong to him automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earnings to Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As a parallel case, Rav Huna in the name of Rav asks: from where do we know that a daughter\u8217's earnings belong to her father while she is still a {\i ktana} or {\i na\u8217'arah}? The derivation is by way of analogy to a female slave: just as a slave\u8217's handiwork belongs to her master \u8212- {\i mah amah ma\u8217'aseh yadeha lerabbah} \u8212- so too a daughter\u8217's earnings belong to her father \u8212- {\i af bat ma\u8217'aseh yadeha le\u8217'aviha}. Underlying this is the logic of the father\u8217's power to sell his daughter as a maidservant: if he can transfer her labor to another master, that is precisely because he himself is her current master and already owns her productive output. The sale does not create the entitlement; it transfers it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vows Verse Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns this against the {\i shevach neurim} argument. If the verse {\i b\u8217'neureha beit aviha} already established a sweeping principle that all benefits from the daughter\u8217's youth belong to the father, then we would never have needed the separate derivation from the slave-sale verse to establish that her earnings belong to him. The very necessity of that second derivation proves that {\i b\u8217'neureha beit aviha} does not function as a general umbrella rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monetary vs.\u160 ?Ritual Righ\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for this limitation is now given: {\i mamona me\u8217'issura lo yalfinan} \u8212- we do not derive monetary rights from ritual obligations. The father\u8217's power to annul vows is a matter of {\i issur}, of religious obligation and its nullification. That power cannot be extended by analogy into the domain of monetary entitlement. Accordingly, {\i b\u8217'neureha beit aviha} tells us only about vows, the slave-sale verse tells us only about earnings, and neither reaches the question of payments for personal injury.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fines Cannot Establish R\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then forecloses yet another avenue: {\i vechi teima neilef miknasa} \u8212- perhaps we can learn the father\u8217's right to {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} from the fact that he already receives the fifty-{\i shekel knas}? This too fails. {\i Mamona miknasa lo yalfinan} \u8212- monetary compensation cannot be derived from a fine. A {\i knas} is always treated as a legal exception, not as a model from which general monetary principles can be extrapolated. Thus we know the father receives the {\i knas}, we know he receives her earnings, and we know he may annul her vows \u8212- but none of these sources establishes his right to payments for injury done to her person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Control Rationa\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty not through scriptural derivation but through a rational inference: {\i mistavra de\u8217'aviha havu} \u8212- it stands to reason that these payments belong to the father. The reasoning is as follows. The father has full practical control over precisely those aspects of his daughter\u8217's existence that generate {\i boshet} and {\i pegam}. He may marry her off to whomever he chooses \u8212- even, as the {\i gemara} notes starkly, to a repulsive or diseased man \u8212- and in doing so he controls whether she will suffer shame and whether she will lose her virginity. Since {\i pegam} arises from the loss of virginity and {\i boshet} from the experience of shame, and since the father has legal dominion over both, it follows that the associated monetary payments belong to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note the tension here. The shame paid under the category of {\i boshet} is presumably the daughter\u8217's shame, not the father\u8217's \u8212- yet she, in the case of consensual seduction, is held to have been shamed even when she consented. And the payment for {\i tzaar}, physical suffering, is not cleanly addressed by this rationale at all: the father\u8217's power to marry her off does not equate to a right to compensation for pain she experiences during an act of rape. The {\i gemara} does not fully resolve this tension, resting instead on the broader framework of his comprehensive control over her person and status during this period.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pegam Valuation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Virgin vs.\u160 ?Non-Virgin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established both that {\i pegam} exists and that it flows to the father, the {\i gemara} turns to its practical calculation. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i pegam ones k\u8217'ilu hi shifcha nimkeret} \u8212- the devaluation in the case of rape is assessed as if she were a maidservant being sold. The {\i gemara} asks how this evaluation is actually conducted. Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel explains: we estimate {\i kamah adam rotzeh liten bein shifcha betulah l\u8217'shifcha beulah l\u8217'shamshah} \u8212- how much more would a person pay for a virgin maidservant than for a non-virgin one, in order to employ her as a household servant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage to Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this formulation. If the maidservant is being bought merely to work in the household \u8212- cooking, cleaning, and so forth \u8212- what does it matter to the buyer whether she is a virgin? {\i Shifcha beulah l\u8217'shamshah, mai nafkali minei} \u8212- for purposes of domestic service, her prior sexual history is irrelevant. The {\i gemara} therefore revises the scenario: the valuation is based not on domestic service but on the buyer\u8217's wish to marry her to his slave. The master is paying to acquire a wife for his servant, and for that purpose, whether she is a virgin matters. He would pay more for a virgin. The {\i gemara} then asks why the slave himself cares \u8212- the master\u8217's goal, one might think, is simply to produce more slaves. The {\i gemara} answers that the case is one where the master wishes to please a slave he is fond of, and such a master would spend more to give his cherished slave a virgin wife. The entire valuation, then, is constructed around this scenario: the difference in price between a virgin and a non-virgin maidservant, as assessed in the context of being married to a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application of the slave market to calculate the monetary value of personal injury is of course deeply disquieting. The {\i halakhah} has no other instrument for assigning a monetary value to a human being, so it employs the only market that treats human beings as commodities \u8212- and then applies the differential values that market generates. Even granting that framework, one may ask whether it approximates a fair measure of actual loss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} presses precisely this concern. The problem with the slave market valuation here is that it fails to capture the actual magnitude of the victim\u8217's loss. Consider a woman of high social standing who was a virgin: she stood to marry a man of comparable status. Having been raped, that prospect is now compromised. That loss is enormous \u8212- far beyond what the difference in price between a virgin and non-virgin maidservant would reflect. {\i Tosafot} frames the question pointedly: {\i teima d\u8217'mai shuma hi zu v\u8217'chi isha chashuvah mimishpacha gedolah einah nifsedet b\u8217'fagma ela shaveh shifcha haniset l\u8217'eved?} \u8212- is this a valuation at all? Does a distinguished woman from a prominent family suffer no greater loss to her {\i pegam} than the price difference of a maidservant being married to a slave? The slave-market calculation may at least roughly approximate a loss in earning capacity when, say, a limb is severed \u8212- because a more able-bodied slave would command a higher price, and that price differential tracks, however crudely, the value of what was lost. But the value of a woman\u8217's virginity to her own matrimonial prospects is not mirrored by what it would mean for the same woman to be matched with a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} proposes a partial resolution: {\i kol isha shamin l\u8217'fi mah shehi b\u8217'inyan zeh} \u8212- we assess each woman according to who she is. The slave-market analogy is not applied generically. Rather, one asks: how much more would a person pay for a woman like this one \u8212- a woman of her precise social standing and background \u8212- in the virgin versus non-virgin state, for the purpose of marrying her to his slave? By factoring in her actual status, the evaluation at least partially accounts for the differences between women. {\i Tosafot} candidly acknowledges the difficulty remains, but at least places the problem on the table. The slave would still have to be the reference point, and that measure still falls far short of capturing the full social loss \u8212- but the intent is to calibrate the slave-market differential to the particular woman in question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sale vs.\u160 ?Fine Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents the position of Rabbi Meir, which had been quoted earlier: {\i kol makom she\u8217'yesh mecher ein knas, v\u8217'chol makom she\u8217'yesh knas ein mecher} \u8212- wherever there is sale, there is no fine; wherever there is fine, there is no sale. The two categories are mutually exclusive. This principle structures the entire dispute between Rabbi Meir and the Sages that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnaic Enumeration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} in the {\i braita} spells out Rabbi Meir\u8217's position in full. A {\i ktana}, a minor girl, can be sold by her father as a maidservant but bears no {\i knas}. A {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- a girl from the age of twelve until twelve and a half \u8212- has a {\i knas} but cannot be sold. A {\i bogeret}, a girl past twelve and a half who has reached full maturity, has neither: she cannot be sold nor does any {\i knas} apply to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages disagree sharply. Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav introduces the {\i braita} establishing this: the Sages hold that sale and {\i knas} can overlap. A {\i ktana} from the age of three years and one day \u8212- the age at which an act of intercourse has legal significance \u8212- through the onset of the two pubic hairs that mark her as a {\i na\u8217'arah} can indeed be sold and also bears a {\i knas}. The {\i knas} therefore extends downward to cover minors, overlapping with the period in which sale is possible. According to the Sages, {\i knas} exists from three years and one day all the way through the {\i bogeret} stage, without the sharp break Rabbi Meir insists upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda then examines the scriptural basis for Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. The verse in the context of rape states {\i v\u8217'lo tihiyeh l\u8217'ishah} \u8212- to him she shall be as a wife. Rabbi Meir understands that the verb {\i tihiyeh} is addressed to the woman herself, indicating that she is someone who can \u8220"make herself\u8221" a wife \u8212- that is, someone who can participate in the act of {\i kiddushin} in her own right. That describes specifically a {\i na\u8217'arah}, who can accept {\i kesef kiddushin} on her own behalf, not a {\i ktana}, who cannot act autonomously in matters of marriage. Thus the verse limits the {\i knas} to the case of a {\i na\u8217'arah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' Spelling Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rish Lakish then presents the Sages\u8217' counter-derivation. The word {\i na\u8217'arah} in the verse concerning rape is written without the letter {\i hei} \u8212- it appears in a defective spelling, as {\i naar} without the terminal vowel letter. The Sages read this defective spelling as a deliberate signal that the verse is not restricting itself to a full {\i na\u8217'arah}. Even a {\i ktana}, who is not a complete {\i na\u8217'arah}, falls within the scope of the {\i knas}. The absence of the {\i hei} thus expands rather than restricts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hei Letter Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious difficulty: if the absence of the {\i hei} always implies inclusion of a minor, how do we know that in the case of {\i motzi shem ra} \u8212- where a husband falsely claims his wife was not a virgin \u8212- the law applies only to a {\i na\u8217'arah} and not a {\i ktana}? After all, Rish Lakish himself states {\i hamotzi shem ra a-ktanah patur} \u8212- one who spreads ill repute about a minor is exempt from those special penalties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} addresses this with a two-step argument. In the passage of {\i motzi shem ra}, the word {\i na\u8217'arah} is written {\i malei} \u8212- in full, with the {\i hei}. And in any case, as Rav Adda bar Ahava points out, it is obvious that the laws of {\i motzi shem ra} apply only to an adult woman, because the passage itself contemplates the possibility that if she was found to have committed adultery, she is executed \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ktanah lav bat onshin hi}, a minor is not subject to capital punishment. So we know she must be an adult from the content of the passage itself, independent of any spelling. The spelling with the {\i hei} is not what tells us she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}; we learn that from the reference to execution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the contrast in spelling does teach us, now that we know from context that {\i motzi shem ra} applies only to an adult, is how to read the spelling in the rape passage. There the word is spelled without the {\i hei} \u8212- and since we know that a full {\i hei} corresponds to a full {\i na\u8217'arah}, the absence of the {\i hei} signals that even a {\i ktana} is included. {\i Afilu ktanah bemashma} \u8212- even a minor is implied. The two passages thus illuminate each other: the full spelling in {\i motzi shem ra} confirms adult status there, and that confirmation, read back against the defective spelling in the {\i knas} passage, yields the Sages\u8217' expansive ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fines vs.\u160 ?Payments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} establishes a foundational distinction between the {\i knas} and the monetary payments of {\i boshet} and {\i pegam}. It states: {\i ha-omer pititi et bitto shel peloni} \u8212- one who says \u8220"I seduced so-and-so\u8217's daughter\u8221" \u8212- {\i meshalem boshet u-pegam al pi atzmo}, he pays for the embarrassment and the devaluation on the basis of his own admission. However, {\i ein meshalem knas} \u8212- he does not pay the fine. This ruling instantiates a broader principle of {\i halakhah} that must be examined carefully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fine Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule is that a {\i knas} can only be imposed by a {\i beit din} on the basis of external testimony. It cannot be self-generated by confession. More than that: even if a person freely admits to an act that would normally carry a {\i knas}, not only does his admission fail to obligate him \u8212- there is a further principle that such an admission may actually exempt him from the {\i knas} even if witnesses subsequently appear. Our {\i mishnah} addresses only the narrower point: self-admission is insufficient to obligate payment of a fine. The {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} payments, by contrast, are classified as {\i mamon} \u8212- genuine monetary compensation \u8212- and therefore the normal rules of monetary obligation apply. Just as one who admits to owing a debt is bound to pay it, one who admits to causing shame and devaluation is bound to compensate for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Domain Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} extends this principle into adjacent legal domains to illustrate its scope. {\i Ha-omer ganavti} \u8212- one who says \u8220"I stole\u8221" \u8212- {\i meshalem et ha-keren al pi atzmo}, he pays the principal on his own admission. He thereby acknowledges that he wrongfully took someone else\u8217's money, and that is straightforward monetary liability. However, he does not pay {\i tashlumei kefel v\u8217'tashlumei arba\u8217'ah va-chamishah} \u8212- the double payment for theft, or the four- and fivefold payment for slaughtering or selling a stolen ox or sheep \u8212- because those additional amounts are {\i knas} and cannot be imposed through self-admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i hamit shori et peloni} \u8212- if someone says \u8220"my ox killed a certain person\u8221" \u8212- he pays on his own admission, because the payment for a goring death (the {\i kofer}, a ransom-type payment for the loss of life attributable to one\u8217's animal) is treated as monetary compensation, not as a fine. If the ox gored another ox, likewise, the owner pays on his own admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i hamit shori avdo shel peloni} \u8212- if my ox killed someone\u8217's slave \u8212- the case is different. The payment for a slave killed by an ox is a fixed thirty {\i shekel}, regardless of the slave\u8217's actual value. This fixed amount means that in cases where the slave was worth less than thirty {\i shekel}, the payment exceeds the actual damages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fine Criterion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's governing criterion: {\i zeh haklal \u8212- kol ham\u8217'shalem yeter al mah she\u8217'hizik ein m\u8217'shalem al pi atzmo} \u8212- this is the rule: whoever pays more than the actual damage does not pay on his own admission. The determinative question is not whether the payment is fixed, but whether it can ever exceed the actual damages. A fixed payment that always falls short of or equals actual damages might still be understood as partial compensation \u8212- the {\i Torah} capping or limiting what you owe. But once there exist scenarios in which the payment exceeds the actual loss, it is clear that the payment is functioning as a fine, a punitive measure imposed by the court, not as compensation. Since some slaves are worth less than thirty {\i shekel}, the thirty-{\i shekel} payment for a gored slave sometimes exceeds actual damages \u8212- and therefore the entire framework of that payment is treated as a {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seduction Admission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape vs.\u160 ?Seduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a question about the {\i mishnah}\u8217's choice of case. Why does the {\i mishnah} illustrate the distinction between fine and monetary payment by means of the seduction case rather than the rape case? {\i Lo mibaya anasti} \u8212- if the {\i mishnah} used rape, it would go without saying that the rapist pays {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} on his own admission. After all, by claiming to have raped her, he is placing the wrongdoing entirely on himself \u8212- {\i d\u8217'lo pagim lah} \u8212- he is not spreading any ill repute about her. There is nothing problematic about accepting such an admission. The case of seduction, however, is more striking and therefore more instructive. When a man admits to seduction, he is simultaneously claiming that the woman consented \u8212- and in doing so, {\i kapagim lah}, he is spreading ill repute about her, implying that she was willing to violate her own modesty. This raises the possibility that perhaps the court should refuse to accept the testimony altogether, to prevent the ill repute from being reinforced by a legal proceeding in which money changes hands. The {\i mishnah} teaches us, {\i kama\u8217'shma lan}, that we do accept such an admission for purposes of the monetary payments \u8212- the court is willing to bracket the accompanying narrative and process the monetary obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, a {\i tanna} in a {\i braita} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda in the name of Rabbi Shimon \u8212- takes a more stringent position. {\i Af poteh she-be-pgam eino meshalem al pi atzmo} \u8212- even in the seduction case, he does not pay {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} based on his own admission. {\i Lo kol heimenu she-yifgom bito shel peloni} \u8212- we do not extend credibility to someone who comes to court and, by his very admission, devalues and besmirches another man\u8217's daughter. In the case of rape, the man\u8217's admission does not cast aspersions on her; it condemns only himself, and therefore is accepted. But in the case of seduction, any testimony he offers necessarily implies her willing participation. According to Rabbi Shimon, the court will not be made an instrument for broadcasting that kind of claim, even at the cost of allowing him to pay money he genuinely owes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Family Shame Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa then puts a series of escalating questions to Abaye. Suppose the woman herself says she does not mind \u8212- she consents to the admission and wants the money. Does that change matters? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i dilma lo nicha lei le-avuhah} \u8212- perhaps the father is not agreeable to having this said publicly. His honor is also at stake. Suppose then that both she and her father say they are content to accept the money. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i dilma lo nicha lehu li-vnei mishpacha} \u8212- perhaps other members of the family would not be pleased. Their reputations are implicated even if the immediate parties have made peace with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And even if the entire family agrees? The {\i gemara} concludes that one cannot confidently say there is no family member anywhere who would be harmed: {\i ee efshar de-leika chad bi-mdinas hayam de-lo nicha lei} \u8212- it is impossible that there is not someone, even somewhere distant, who would object. The {\i beit din} therefore has a broader responsibility, rooted in policy considerations that transcend the preferences of the immediate parties. Even where the father and daughter both consent to the proceedings, the court protects against the spread of reputational harm to the wider family.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking intersection of policy and {\i halakhah}. As a matter of pure {\i halakhah}, one who admits to owing money is obligated to pay. Yet here, in order to shield innocent third parties from the collateral reputational damage caused by airing the admission in court, the {\i beit din} may decline to act on testimony it would otherwise be obligated to process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} draws a critical limiting distinction. The restriction articulated by Rabbi Shimon \u8212- and the policy concerns the {\i gemara} elaborates \u8212- apply specifically when the perpetrator is the one who comes forward and volunteers the admission. In that scenario, he is the one initiating the spread of ill repute, and the court refuses to be complicit. But {\i Tosafot} rules that if the father or the daughter is the one who brings the claim, initiating the lawsuit and effectively certifying that the event occurred, the situation is entirely different. In that case, the father himself has chosen to make this public, and the admission cannot be said to harm the family in a way they have not already accepted. {\i Hainu d\u8217'mishum d\u8217'kadam tva\u8217'u ha-av techilah, ein tzarich lomar lav kol heimenu} \u8212- if the father sued first, the rule of \u8220"we do not extend him such credibility\u8221" simply does not apply, and the perpetrator would be obligated to pay even on his own admission in such circumstances.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half Damages Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots to a debate that has broad structural implications: whether the half-damages payment for a {\i shor tam} \u8212- a non-habitual goring ox \u8212- constitutes monetary compensation or a fine. Rav Papa holds that {\i palga nizka mamona} \u8212- half damages is a monetary payment, genuine compensation. His reasoning is that ordinary unwarned oxen cannot be presumed to be naturally guarded against going out and goring. The owner therefore bears some baseline responsibility to watch his animal. In principle, given that negligence, full damages would be appropriate. The {\i Torah}, however, had mercy \u8212- {\i Rachmana hu d\u8217'chas alei} \u8212- because the animal had not yet been officially warned. The owner is barely over the line of negligence, and so the {\i Torah} reduces the liability to half. But it remains compensation, not punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua takes the opposing view: {\i palga nizka knasa} \u8212- half damages is a fine. In his view, unwarned oxen should indeed be presumed to be self-regulated and safe. The owner of a {\i tam} was therefore not negligent; he had no real reason to believe his animal would gore. {\i U\u8217'vdin hu d\u8217'lo l\u8217'shalem klal} \u8212- strictly speaking, he should owe nothing at all. Nevertheless the {\i Torah} imposed a fine \u8212- {\i ki heichi d\u8217'nintrei l\u8217'torei} \u8212- to incentivize owners to be more careful with their animals. The half-payment is thus not calibrated to actual fault or loss; it is a deterrent penalty. At its core, the dispute concerns which side of the negligence line the owner of an unwarned goring ox falls on. Rav Papa places him just over the line, making the half-damages a partial compensation. Rav Huna places him just under the line, making the half-damages a fine to encourage greater care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} brings a textual challenge from the {\i mishnah} in {\i Bava Kamma}, which states {\i ha\u8217'nizak ve\u8217'hamazik betashlumin} \u8212- the person damaged and the damager share in the monetary payments. This language of \u8220"sharing\u8221" seems to reflect the perspective of compensation: the victim suffers the full loss of the animal but receives only half payment, so he is in effect bearing half the cost himself. The {\i gemara} notes that this framing is intelligible if half damages is compensation \u8212- {\i bishlama lema\u8217'an d\u8217'amar palga nizka mamona, hainu d\u8217'shaich nizak betashlumin} \u8212- there is a meaningful sense in which the victim participates in bearing the financial burden. But if half damages is a fine, the situation is reversed: the victim is already receiving money he is not technically owed, since the owner was not negligent. To speak of the victim \u8220"sharing in the cost\u8221" when he is in fact receiving a windfall seems incoherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution on Carcass\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by limiting what the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language actually teaches. {\i Lo nitzrecha ella l\u8217'pchat neveila} \u8212- the statement that both parties share in the outcome addresses specifically the depreciation of the carcass. When the ox is killed by the goring, the victim takes possession of the carcass. If that carcass then depreciates in value over the subsequent days, the loss falls entirely on the victim, not on the owner of the goring ox. In this sense, and only in this sense, does the victim \u8220"share\u8221" in the financial outcome: the calculation of damages freezes at a certain point, and any subsequent depreciation of the carcass is the victim\u8217's problem. This narrow reading of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language neutralizes the apparent support for Rav Papa\u8217's position, since the {\i gemara} shows the language can be explained equally well under Rav Huna\u8217's fine-based view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Omitted Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} brings a further challenge from another {\i mishnah} in {\i Bava Kamma} that lists the differences between a {\i tam} and a {\i muad}. The {\i tam} pays half damages from the body of the goring animal itself, capped by the animal\u8217's own value; the {\i muad} pays full damages from the owner\u8217's best property with no ceiling. Conspicuously absent from this list is any mention of the distinction that the {\i tam} payment cannot be collected on self-admission while the {\i muad} payment can \u8212- a distinction that would obviously apply if {\i tam} damages were a {\i knas} while {\i muad} damages were {\i mamon}. This omission appears to support Rav Papa\u8217's position that both are {\i tashlumin} and the fine-versus-payment distinction simply does not exist between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects this with the principle {\i tana veshayer} \u8212- the {\i tanna} taught the list incompletely and left items off. The {\i gemara} then asks {\i mah shayer de-hai shayer} \u8212- if something was left off, what else was omitted? The answer: {\i shayer chatzi kofer} \u8212- the question of half-{\i kofer} was also left off the list. When an unwarned ox kills a person, the question of whether the owner owes half of the {\i kofer} ransom payment is itself disputed, and that difference between {\i tam} and {\i muad} was not listed either. Since the list is demonstrably incomplete in at least one other respect, its silence about the fine-versus-payment distinction carries no probative weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds one further point: even if one sought to use the {\i chatzi kofer} omission to demonstrate incompleteness, the response would be that according to the opinion of Rabbi Yose HaGelili, who holds that a {\i tam} does pay half-{\i kofer}, that distinction between {\i tam} and {\i muad} would not exist at all \u8212- and therefore its absence from the list might not indicate an omission. In sum, the list of {\i tam-muad} differences is either incomplete or, under certain opinions, covers the relevant cases, and in neither event does it resolve the underlying debate between Rav Papa and Rav Huna.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 40b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 50 Shekel Fine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 50 Shekel Coverage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a probing question on the {\i mishna}\u8217's framework of payments for rape. Having established that the rapist owes {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} in addition to the fifty {\i shekel} fine, the {\i gemara} asks: how do we know that the fifty {\i shekel} does not simply cover everything \u8212- shame, depreciation, and all associated injuries? Perhaps {\i rachmana} said fifty {\i zuzi} for the whole of it, {\i mi-kol milei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Virgin vs.\u160 ?Non-Virgin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira attempts to resolve this from logic. {\i Bas melachim chamishim, bas hedyotos chamishim?} Could it be that the daughter of a king and the daughter of a commoner each receive the same fifty {\i shekel}, with no adjustment whatsoever for the vastly different degree of shame and injury inflicted? The inequity seems self-evident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, pushes back: {\i i hachi, gabei eved nami.} The same apparent injustice exists in the case of an ox that gores a slave, where the Torah fixes the payment at thirty {\i shekel} regardless of the slave\u8217's earning capacity. An {\i eved nokeiv margoliyos} \u8212- a slave capable of boring pearls and commanding enormous productive value \u8212- receives the same fixed payment as a slave fit only to sew with a needle. Yet there the Torah plainly set a fixed amount. So too here, perhaps the Torah simply ordained fifty {\i shekel} as a fixed sum with no regard for individual variation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira then reformulates the logical argument on different grounds. Consider the following scenario: two men rape the same woman in sequence \u8212- {\i echad ke-darkah ve-echad she-lo ke-darkah}. Tosafot clarifies that the first assailant penetrates vaginally and the second anally \u8212- the ordering matters because the second perpetrator also owes the fifty {\i shekel}, which is possible only if she still retains the halakhic status of {\i besulah}. Since anal intercourse does not rupture the hymen, the woman is still halakhically considered a virgin for the purpose of the fifty {\i shekel} fine, and the second rapist pays as well. Now, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i yomru ba al sheleimah chamishim, ba al pegumah chamishim?} Will people not object that the man who raped an intact woman pays the same as the man who raped one who had already been violated? That seems patently unreasonable. Surely, since her physical state has changed between the two acts, the payment ought to differ \u8212- and this demonstrates that the fifty {\i shekel} cannot be meant to cover everything including {\i boshet} and {\i pegam}, for those would indeed differ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Market Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye again answers with the slave analogy: {\i yomar eved bari shloshim, eved mukeh shchin shloshim} \u8212- a healthy, able-bodied slave and one covered with boils both receive the same thirty-{\i shekel} payment when gored by an ox. Physical differences between the two slaves do not alter the fixed sum. So the Torah\u8217's use of a fixed amount does not by itself prove that individual variation in {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} is separately compensated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes that neither logical argument suffices on its own, and turns instead to explicit {\i pesukim}. Abaye derives it from the verse {\i tachat asher ina} \u8212- the fifty {\i shekel} is payment specifically for the act of coercive intercourse itself. The very formulation {\i tachat asher ina} implies that this payment is for the oppression, the act of rape \u8212- {\i miklal de\u8217'ika boshet u\u8217'pgam}, which tells us by implication that there are additional payments for the associated shame and depreciation that lie outside the scope of the fifty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava derives the same principle from a different verse: {\i v\u8217'natan ha\u8217'ish hashocheiv imah l\u8217'avi ha-na\u8217'ara chamishim kasef} \u8212- the man who lay with her shall pay fifty {\i kesef} to the father. By combining the act of lying with her and the payment, the verse indicates that {\i hana\u8217'at shchiva chamishim} \u8212- the fifty covers the pleasure of the sexual act. {\i Miklal de\u8217'ika boshet u\u8217'pgam}: the embarrassment and depreciation are separate injuries not subsumed under this payment. It is worth noting that the framing of this as {\i hana\u8217'at shchiva} \u8212- the pleasure of intercourse \u8212- is somewhat jarring from the victim\u8217's perspective, since this is manifestly a case of rape. The {\i gemara} is, however, simply using the verse\u8217's language to delineate the precise scope of the fixed payment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Payment Recipients\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman or Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} are paid in addition to the fifty {\i shekel}, the {\i gemara} turns to a further question: to whom do these additional payments go? The Torah explicitly directs the fifty {\i shekel} to the father, but the {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} were inferred implicitly. Perhaps they belong to the woman herself. {\i Ve\u8217'eima l\u8217'didah} \u8212- maybe those payments go directly to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes the most immediate counter-argument: if {\i boshet u\u8217'pgam} were the woman\u8217's payments, then in a case of {\i pitui} \u8212- consensual seduction \u8212- there would be no such payment, since a consenting woman has waived her right to compensation for shame and depreciation. Yet the {\i halacha} is that {\i boshet u\u8217'pgam} are owed even in {\i pitui}, demonstrating that these payments belong to the father. Even where the daughter consents, the father has not consented, and the obligation persists. Still, the {\i gemara} seeks a verse basis for this conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Youth Benefits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} invokes a verse from the laws of vow annulment: {\i binureha beit aviha} \u8212- while she is in her youth in her father\u8217's house. From this, a general principle is proposed: {\i kol shevach neurim l\u8217'aviha} \u8212- all benefits deriving from her youth belong to the father. If so, any monetary claim arising from her status as a young woman in her father\u8217's household would revert to him, including {\i boshet u\u8217'pgam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav: {\i minayin she\u8217'maaseh bat l\u8217'aviha?} \u8212- from where do we know that a daughter\u8217's earnings belong to her father? The answer is derived from the verse {\i vechi yimkor ish et bito le\u8217'amah} \u8212- that a man may sell his daughter as a maidservant. When she is sold, her earnings transfer to the new master; this teaches that when she is not sold, her earnings belong to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the broad reading of {\i binureha beit aviha}: if that verse established a sweeping principle that the father receives all benefits from his daughter, there would have been no need for the separate derivation regarding her earnings from the verse about selling her. The fact that a distinct source is required for her earnings shows there is no general umbrella principle. Why not? {\i \u1502 ?\u1502 ?\u1493 ?\u1504 ?\u1488 ? \u1502 ?\u1488 ?\u1497 ?\u1505 ?\u1493 ?\u1512 ?\u1488 ? \u1500 ?\u1488 ? \u1497 ?\u1500 ?\u1508 ?\u1497 ?\u1504 ?\u1503 ?} \u8212- rights pertaining to {\i issur}, such as the annulment of vows, do not automatically translate into monetary rights. The verse {\i binureha beit aviha} speaks only to the father\u8217's right to annul vows. The verse of {\i vechi yimkor} speaks to his right to her earnings. Neither covers the third category: payments arising from a physical injury done to her person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Tosafot observes, this is genuinely a distinct category. One can understand that a daughter\u8217's labor earnings go to her father, and separately that he may dissolve her vows. But when it comes to compensation for a bodily violation, the argument that she personally suffered and should personally receive payment is not without force. The {\i gemara} acknowledges this and declines to conflate the categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-chi teima nilaf mi-qenasa} \u8212- perhaps we should derive from the fact that the fifty {\i shekel} goes to the father that {\i boshet u\u8217'pgam} go to him as well? No: {\i mamona mi-qenasa lo yalpinan} \u8212- a fine is treated as an exceptional payment and cannot serve as a paradigm for regular monetary compensation. The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i mistavra de\u8217'aviha havei} \u8212- there is a reasoned basis to assign these payments to the father. {\i Ve\u8217'i bayit mosrah le-menuval u-le-mukeh shechin} \u8212- the father holds power over these matters because he controls who she marries. The {\i pegam}, the loss of virginity, is something he has the authority to effectuate by marrying her off. The {\i boshet}, the shame, is likewise something over which he has practical control \u8212- he can choose to marry her to a person of low social standing, which would itself constitute a form of degradation. Because he has this control over her status and honor, the payments for their impairment logically flow to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe that this reasoning has its tensions. The shame in question is presumably suffered by the woman herself, not primarily by the father. If she is the one shamed, why does she not receive the payment directly, especially in a case of consensual seduction where she willingly incurred the shame? The {\i gemara}\u8217's position seems to be that even in {\i pitui}, where the woman consented, she is nonetheless considered to have suffered {\i boshet} \u8212- and yet the payment goes to the father by virtue of his structural authority over these matters. The question of {\i tza\u8217'ar} \u8212- pain and suffering \u8212- remains somewhat unresolved in this framework, since the father\u8217's analogous control over her physical suffering is harder to establish.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave Market Valuation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Virgin vs.\u160 ?Non-Virgin Sl\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i pegam} is paid as a separate monetary compensation to the father, the {\i gemara} asks how its amount is determined. The {\i mishna} already stated that the evaluation is done as though she were a slave woman being sold. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i heichi shammenan lah?} \u8212- how exactly is she evaluated? Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: {\i omdim kamma adam rotze liten bein shifcha betula le-shifcha be\u8217'ula lishamesho} \u8212- we estimate how much more a person would pay for a virgin maidservant versus a non-virgin one in order to have her serve him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Marriage Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately objects: {\i shifcha be\u8217'ula lishamesho mai nafka lei minei?} If the slave woman is being purchased simply to work in the household, what difference does her virginity make to the buyer? The {\i gemara} therefore clarifies: the correct formulation is {\i bein shifcha be\u8217'ula le-shifcha she\u8217'eina be\u8217'ula le-hasi\u8217'ah le\u8217'avdo} \u8212- the buyer is purchasing her with the intention of marrying her off to his slave. In that context, the slave himself cares whether his wife is a virgin, and the master is willing to pay more to acquire a virgin for the slave\u8217's benefit. The {\i gemara} refines this further: specifically this applies to a slave for whom the master has affection \u8212- {\i be\u8217'eved she-yesh lo le-rabbo korat ruach heimenu} \u8212- and therefore wishes to do the slave a favor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a significant difficulty with this entire evaluative model. By the standard method of assessing damages through the slave market, there is at least a rough correspondence between the slave\u8217's market value and the actual economic loss. If a slave capable of highly skilled work is injured and loses that capacity, the depreciation in his market price would reflect the capitalized value of his lost earning potential. In that sense, the slave market serves as a reasonable proxy for actual loss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in the case of {\i pegam} for a violated woman, the correspondence breaks down. The relevant question is not how much more a slave master would pay to acquire a virgin for his slave\u8217's marriage. It is rather what the woman herself has lost by no longer being a virgin \u8212- which may include the ability to marry into a family of high social standing, a loss that could be far greater than any difference captured by the slave market price. Tosafot phrases this sharply: {\i teimah demai shuma hi zo vechi isha chashuva mi-mishpacha gedolah einah nifsedet be-pigmah ella shaveh shifcha ha-niset le\u8217'eved?} Is a woman of distinguished lineage not losing far more than merely the slave-market differential between a virgin and non-virgin maidservant?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's own resolution is that the evaluation must be carried out with reference to a slave woman {\i of the type that she is} \u8212- {\i kol isha shamin le-fi mah she-hi be-inyan zeh}. In other words, one would ask how much more a buyer would pay for a virgin of this particular standing and background versus a non-virgin of the same standing, in order to marry her off to a slave. Even so, Tosafot clearly acknowledges the difficulty: it remains the case that the measure is still tied to a slave marriage context and may not fully capture the actual loss to the woman. The {\i gemara} identifies the problem but the solution is not entirely satisfying.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meir vs.\u160 ?Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to a principle that was cited earlier: {\i kol makom she-yesh mecher ein kenas, ve-kol makom she-yesh kenas ein mecher} \u8212- wherever there is the possibility of sale, there is no fine, and wherever there is a fine, there is no sale. This is the position of Rabbi Meir. The operative distinction concerns the father\u8217's authority over his daughter at different stages of her development. A {\i ketana}, a minor girl, can be sold by her father as a maidservant \u8212- this is the {\i mecher}. Once she becomes a {\i na\u8217'ara} at the onset of puberty, she can no longer be sold, but it is at this stage that the fifty {\i shekel} fine for rape or seduction applies. And once she becomes a {\i bogeret} \u8212- fully mature at twelve and a half \u8212- neither applies. Thus for Rabbi Meir, the categories are mutually exclusive: {\i ketana} has {\i mecher} but no {\i kenas}; {\i na\u8217'ara} has {\i kenas} but no {\i mecher}; {\i bogeret} has neither.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages disagree. A {\i braita} records their view: {\i ketana mi-bat shalosh shanim ve-yom echad ve-ad she-tivager yesh lah kenas} \u8212- a minor girl from three years and one day until she becomes a {\i bogeret} has a {\i kenas}. The {\i kenas} and {\i mecher} therefore overlap. From three years and one day onward, intercourse with a minor is legally significant as an act of sex, and therefore the fine applies. Yet the father can still sell her during the years she is a {\i ketana}. The Sages thus hold that {\i kenas} can coexist with {\i mecher} for the period from age three to the onset of puberty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama de-Rabbi Meir?} \u8212- what is Rabbi Meir\u8217's textual basis? The verse by rape states {\i v\u8217'lo tiyeh le\u8217'ishah} \u8212- she shall become his wife. The use of a verb form that implies her own participation in the betrothal \u8212- since a {\i na\u8217'ara} can herself accept {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- indicates that the Torah is speaking of a {\i na\u8217'ara} specifically, one who is herself an active party to marriage. A {\i ketana} cannot independently effectuate her own marriage; only the father acts on her behalf. The Torah\u8217's framing therefore implies that the {\i kenas} applies only to a {\i na\u8217'ara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Letter Hei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish offers the Sages\u8217' response. The word {\i na\u8217'ara} in the passage concerning rape is written in the Torah without the letter {\i hei} \u8212- defectively spelled. This defective spelling suggests something less than a full {\i na\u8217'ara} is intended: {\i afilu ketana be-mashma} \u8212- even a minor is included. The absence of the {\i hei} signals that the halakhic scope of the verse is broader than the standard meaning of {\i na\u8217'ara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motzi Shem Ra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further teaching of Reish Lakish appears in this context and sharpens the analysis. Reish Lakish holds that {\i ha-motzi shem ra a-ketana patur} \u8212- the laws of {\i motzi shem ra}, the husband who falsely claims his wife was not a virgin, do not apply if the wife was a {\i ketana} at the time of marriage. This is true even according to the Sages who hold that a {\i ketana} is subject to the rape {\i kenas}. The basis for this distinction is that by {\i motzi shem ra}, the Torah writes {\i na\u8217'ara} spelled fully \u8212- {\i na\u8217'arah malei diber ha-katuv} \u8212- with the {\i hei}, indicating that only a genuine {\i na\u8217'ara} and not a {\i ketana} is meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava raises an objection: is it really necessary to derive the adult status of the woman in {\i motzi shem ra} from the full spelling of {\i na\u8217'arah}? The Torah itself describes the possibility of her being executed for adultery \u8212- {\i v\u8217'im emet hayah ha-davar ha-zeh lo nimtze\u8217'u vetulim la-na\u8217'arah ve-hotzi\u8217'u et ha-na\u8217'arah el petach beit aviha u-sekoluha}. A minor is not subject to capital punishment. The very content of the passage establishes she must be an adult; no inference from spelling is needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} accepts this challenge and reframes the analysis. We do not derive that {\i motzi shem ra} requires an adult woman from the full spelling of {\i na\u8217'arah}. Rather, we know she is an adult from the content of the passage. Once we have independently established her adult status there, the full spelling of {\i na\u8217'arah} with the {\i hei} can serve as a contrast: {\i ella hacha na\u8217'arah, ha kol makom she-ne\u8217'emar naar, afilu ketana be-mashma} \u8212- where we know it is an adult, the full spelling marks that explicitly; where {\i na\u8217'ara} appears without the {\i hei}, the implication extends even to a minor. This is how the Sages derive that the rape {\i kenas} applies even to a {\i ketana}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Admission & Fines\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confession Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to the distinction between fines and monetary compensation in the context of self-incrimination. {\i Ha-omer pitisi et bito shel ploni} \u8212- someone who says, I seduced this man\u8217's daughter: {\i meshalem boshet u\u8217'pgam al pi atzmo} \u8212- he pays for shame and depreciation on the basis of his own admission, {\i ve\u8217'ein meshalem knas} \u8212- but he does not pay the fine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fine vs.\u160 ?Compensation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle at work is fundamental: fines are punishments imposed by the court and can only be established through external testimony. An individual\u8217's self-incrimination does not generate an obligation to pay a {\i knas}. Indeed, it goes further \u8212- in some configurations, a prior admission can even exempt a person from a fine later, even if witnesses subsequently appear. But at a minimum, an admission alone is never sufficient to obligate payment of a fine. Monetary compensation, by contrast, operates on normal evidentiary principles: if you admit you caused someone a loss, you owe them for it. {\i Boshet u\u8217'pgam} are monetary payments \u8212- {\i mamon} \u8212- not punitive fines, and therefore they are payable on self-admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theft Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} extends the principle to other cases. {\i Ha-omer ganavti} \u8212- one who confesses to theft: {\i meshalem et ha-keren al pi atzmo} \u8212- pays the principal on his own admission, {\i ve\u8217'ein meshalem tashlumei khefel ve-tashlumei arba\u8217'a va-chamisha} \u8212- but does not pay the double payment or the fourfold and fivefold payments. Those are fines, and fines require external witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceeding Damages Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we identify a payment as a fine rather than compensation? The {\i mishna} states the rule: {\i kol ha-meshalem yeter al mah she-hizik eino meshalem al pi atzmo} \u8212- any payment that can exceed the actual damages is not payable on self-admission. If a payment can ever be higher than the real loss, it cannot be pure compensation; it must be a punitive surcharge, i.e., a fine. The thirty {\i shekel} for an ox that gores a slave illustrates this: since some slaves are worth less than thirty {\i shekel}, there are scenarios where the payment exceeds the actual loss. Therefore the thirty {\i shekel} is a {\i knas} and cannot be imposed on self-admission alone. It is important to note the precise formulation: the {\i mishna} does not say that a fixed payment is automatically a fine. A fixed payment that always fell below or at actual damages could simply be a cap on compensation. It is specifically the possibility of {\i exceeding} actual damages that marks a payment as a fine.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seduction vs.\u160 ?Rape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why does the {\i mishna} use the case of {\i pitui}, seduction, to illustrate the rule of admission? Why not use {\i ones}, rape? {\i Lo mivaiya anasti} \u8212- it is self-evident in the rape case: {\i de-lo kapagim lah} \u8212- he is not impugning her reputation. He admits to having committed a forcible act against her. In that case it is obvious that he would pay {\i boshet u\u8217'pgam} on his own admission, since his confession does not spread any ill repute about the woman \u8212- the wrongdoing is unambiguously his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seduction Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The seduction case, however, is more instructive. When he says he seduced her, he is also saying something about her \u8212- {\i de-kapagim lah} \u8212- he is spreading a damaging report about this woman\u8217's conduct. This raises the question of whether we should accept his admission at all, since doing so would in effect validate a claim that tarnishes her reputation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tanna} disagrees with the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling. A {\i braita} records that Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda says in the name of Rabbi Shimon: {\i af boshet u\u8217'pgam eino meshalem al pi atzmo} \u8212- even the monetary payments of {\i boshet u\u8217'pgam} are not payable on self-admission in the seduction case. {\i Lo kol heimenu she-yifgom bito shel ploni} \u8212- we do not empower him to impugn this man\u8217's daughter by accepting money on the basis of his self-serving confession. Accepting the money validates his account and thereby does harm to her reputation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Family Honor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a broader question about the scope of this concern. Even if the immediate parties \u8212- the daughter and the father \u8212- are willing to accept the money, perhaps the extended family would be dishonored by having the matter adjudicated in this way. The {\i gemara} considers progressively whether we protect the family\u8217's interest even when the woman consents, even when the father consents, and even when the family at large appears to consent. Ultimately the {\i gemara} suggests that we cannot be certain there is no relative somewhere who would object \u8212- {\i ee efshar de-leka chad be-medinas ha-yam de-lo nicha lei} \u8212- and therefore the court\u8217's responsibility to protect innocent parties from reputational harm may override the willingness of the immediate parties to proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot draws an important distinction within this discussion. {\i Hainu de-mishum de-kodam tev\u8217'u ha-av techilah, ein tzarich lomar lav kol heimenu} \u8212- if the father or the daughter was the one who initiated the lawsuit, that changes matters entirely. When the complainant themselves comes to court and asserts that the seduction occurred, they are the ones placing the matter before the court, not merely acquiescing to someone else\u8217's claim. In that case, even according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's approach, his admission would presumably result in payment, since the reputational damage is now coming from the aggrieved party themselves rather than from the alleged perpetrator.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half Damages Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now takes up a fundamental dispute about the nature of the half-damages payment ({\i palga nizka}) owed by the owner of a {\i shor tam}, a non-habitual gorer. Rav Pappa holds that {\i palga nizka mamona} \u8212- the half-damages payment is monetary compensation, not a fine. His reasoning: {\i stam shevarim lav be-chezkat shimur kaimi} \u8212- one cannot assume that ordinary oxen are naturally self-restrained. An owner has a genuine obligation to watch even a tame ox. {\i U-ve-din hu de-meshalem kulei} \u8212- strictly speaking, if his ox gores someone, he ought to pay full damages, because he is negligent in not having watched his animal. The Torah, however, was lenient \u8212- {\i rachmana hu de-chayis alei, de-achatti lo ya\u8217'id torei} \u8212- since the ox had not yet been forewarned and established as habitual, the Torah showed compassion and required only half. The half is thus a reduced compensation: the owner was barely over the line of negligence, so he pays half rather than the full amount he would otherwise owe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua disagrees: {\i palga nizka knasa} \u8212- the half-damages is a fine. His reasoning is the reverse: {\i stam shevarim be-chezkat shimur kaimi} \u8212- before an ox becomes habitual, one may assume it will not gore, and therefore the owner bears no genuine obligation to watch it against such an eventuality. {\i U-ve-din hu de-lo leshalem klal} \u8212- strictly, the owner should owe nothing, since he was not negligent. Yet the Torah nonetheless imposed payment \u8212- {\i rachmana hu de-kensei, ki heichi de-ninterei le-toreih} \u8212- as a deterrent fine to encourage owners to take precautions. The two positions thus argue about which side of the negligence threshold the {\i tam} owner falls on: Rav Pappa says barely above, making the half a partial compensation; Rav Huna says barely below, making the half a punitive fine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sharing Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i mishna} from Bava Kamma: {\i ha-nizak ve-ha-mazik ba-tashlumin} \u8212- the injured party and the injuring party share in the payment, a formulation suggesting that the {\i nizak} bears part of the loss. This language, it is argued, fits naturally with the view that half-damages is compensation \u8212- the {\i nizak} is entitled to full damages but effectively absorbs half the loss. If half-damages is a fine, however, the {\i nizak} is not really entitled to anything in principle, since the {\i mazik} was not negligent. How then can one speak of the {\i nizak} sharing in the cost?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Carcass Depreciation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by explaining that the language of {\i ha-nizak ve-ha-mazik ba-tashlumin} is taught for the {\i halacha} of {\i pechat neveila} \u8212- the depreciation of the carcass. After the goring, the {\i nizak} takes possession of the dead animal. If the carcass depreciates in value between the moment of death and the time of recovery, that loss falls entirely on the {\i nizak}; the owner of the goring ox bears no share of that depreciation. The {\i mishna}\u8217's language that both parties share in the payment refers specifically to this aspect: the {\i nizak} bears the full risk of carcass depreciation, which is why one can say he is included in the loss. This explanation sidesteps the question of whether the half-damages itself reflects compensation or fine logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further challenge is raised from a {\i mishna} in Bava Kamma that enumerates the differences between a {\i shor tam} and a {\i shor muad}. The {\i mishna} lists that a {\i tam} pays half damages collected only from the body of the goring animal, while a {\i muad} pays full damages from the best of the owner\u8217's property. Notably, the {\i mishna} does not list as a difference that the {\i tam}\u8217's payment is a {\i knas} and therefore cannot be collected on self-admission while the {\i muad}\u8217's payment can. This omission suggests that the two are parallel in kind \u8212- both are monetary compensation \u8212- which would support Rav Pappa\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i tanna ve-shayer} \u8212- the {\i mishna}\u8217's list is not exhaustive. The {\i gemara} asks, however, {\i mai shayer de-hai shayer} \u8212- if you say something was left off, you must identify what else was omitted, since a list does not simply leave out one item. The {\i gemara} answers that the {\i mishna} also omitted the question of {\i chatzi kofer}: when a {\i shor tam} kills a person, the question of whether half {\i kofer} is owed is itself a matter of dispute, with Rabbi Yose ha-Gelili holding that a {\i tam} does pay {\i chatzi kofer}. Since that distinction is also absent from the {\i mishna}\u8217's list, the list is plainly incomplete, and the absence of the fine-versus-compensation distinction cannot be taken as proof that the distinction does not exist.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 41\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Goring Ox Damages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tam vs.\u160 ?Muad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the top of 41b, wrapping up a {\i sugya} that has been unfolding since the previous session. The central question is whether the half-payment ({\i chatzi nezek}) imposed when an unhabituated ox ({\i tam}) gores is classified as a {\i knas} \u8212- a monetary fine \u8212- or as {\i mammon}, an ordinary compensatory obligation. The practical difference is significant: a {\i knas} cannot be collected based on the tortfeasor\u8217's own admission ({\i al pi atzmo}), whereas a {\i mammon} obligation can. The debate among the {\i Amoraim} turns on a conceptual question: are oxen presumed to be naturally guarded and non-dangerous, such that when a {\i tam} gores it falls shy of actionable negligence and the half-payment is therefore a punitive {\i knas}? Or is a goring ox just barely over the threshold of negligence, so that the half-payment represents partial compensation \u8212- {\i tashlumim} \u8212- rather than a fine?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Muad Direct Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with a {\i ta shma} \u8212- a proof from a {\i braita}. The {\i braita} states: if someone says, \u8220"My ox gored another person,\u8221" or \u8220"my ox gored another person\u8217's ox,\u8221" he pays based on his own admission. The Gemara asks: does this not refer to a {\i tam}, thereby demonstrating that the half-payment is {\i mammon}, since admission suffices? The Gemara rejects this: {\i lo bemuad} \u8212- we are speaking of a {\i muad}, a habituated ox. And the implication for a {\i tam} would be the reverse: {\i eino meshaleim al pi atzmo} \u8212- he does not pay based on his own admission \u8212- precisely because the half-payment of a {\i tam} is a {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Slave Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a further question on the {\i braita}. The {\i seifa} of the {\i braita} addresses a different case: if one\u8217's ox kills another\u8217's slave, he does not pay based on his own admission, since the thirty-shekel payment for a slave is a fixed amount \u8212- and as a fixed sum that can exceed actual damages, it is treated as a {\i knas}. The Gemara asks: why did the {\i braita} need to introduce the slave case to illustrate the {\i knas} exemption? It should have made the distinction within the original case itself, contrasting {\i muad} (where one pays {\i al pi atzmo}) with {\i tam} (where one does not). The very fact that the {\i braita} jumped to the slave case seems to imply that within the goring case itself \u8212- whether {\i muad} or {\i tam} \u8212- one always pays based on admission, indicating the payment is {\i mammon}. The Gemara answers: {\i kulah bemuad kamari} \u8212- the {\i braita} is speaking throughout of a {\i muad}, distinguishing between goring a free person or ox (which is {\i tashlumim}) and goring a slave (which is {\i knas}). The case of a {\i tam} simply was not the {\i braita}\u8217's focus, and its silence tells us nothing about whether {\i tam} is {\i knas} or {\i mammon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceeding Damages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara next turns to a {\i mishna} that formulates a general rule: {\i kol ha-meshaleim yoter al mah she-hizik eino meshaleim al pi atzmo} \u8212- anyone who pays more than the actual damages does not pay based on his own admission. Payment exceeding actual harm \u8212- whether because the amount is disproportionate (such as double payment for theft) or because it is a fixed sum like the thirty shekels for a slave \u8212- marks the payment as a {\i knas}. The implication seems to run in the other direction as well: if the payment is {\i less} than full damages, it would apparently be {\i tashlumim}, collected even based on one\u8217's own admission. This seems to directly support the view that half-payment of a {\i tam} is {\i mammon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Less Than Damages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses this: the {\i mishna} says if one pays more than damages, he does not pay {\i al pi atzmo}; by contrast, if one pays less than damages, the implication is that one does pay {\i al pi atzmo}. This strongly suggests that partial payment is {\i tashlumim}, not {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Resolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, refuses to accept this reading of the {\i mishna} as definitive. It reformulates: do not read the rule as drawing the line between \u8220"more\u8221" and \u8220"less.\u8221" Rather, the line is between paying {\i exactly} the amount of damages (which is {\i tashlumim}) versus anything that diverges \u8212- whether more or less \u8212- from that precise equivalence (which is {\i knas}). If so, why didn\u8217't the {\i mishna} simply say: \u8220"Whoever does not pay exactly according to the damages does not pay {\i al pi atzmo}\u8221"? The Gemara concedes: {\i tiyuvta} \u8212- this is indeed a challenge to the position that half-damages is a {\i knas}. The {\i mishna} strongly implies that only payment exceeding damages is a {\i knas}, and that less-than-full payment is {\i tashlumim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the Gemara then delivers a striking ruling: {\i ve-hilkheta palga nizka knasa} \u8212- the law is that the half-payment of a goring {\i tam} is indeed a {\i knas}. The Gemara itself acknowledges the apparent paradox: you have just presented a {\i tiyuvta}, and yet you rule in accordance with the contradicted position? The answer: yes \u8212- {\i tiyuvta ve-hilkheta}. The reason the {\i mishna} did not formulate the rule as \u8220"anything other than exact damages is a {\i knas}\u8221" is that such a formulation would have been imprecise. There exists a case of half-payment that is unambiguously {\i mammon}, not {\i knas}, and that exception prevented the {\i mishna} from adopting the broader formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pebbles Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That exception is the case of {\i tzurot} \u8212- pebbles. When a walking animal kicks up stones that cause damage, the halacha \u8212- a {\i halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} \u8212- is that one pays half-damages. This is categorized under {\i regel} (trampling), and the half-payment in this case is unambiguously {\i mammon}, not {\i knas}. The {\i mishna} therefore could not state as an absolute rule that any sub-full payment is a {\i knas}, because the {\i tzurot} case is a counterexample: it pays half the damages and is nonetheless {\i mammon}. Accordingly, the {\i mishna} confined its stated rule to the case of payment exceeding damages, without claiming that less-than-full payment is always {\i tashlumim}. The real underlying principle \u8212- that anything departing from exact equivalence to actual damages is a {\i knas}, apart from the {\i tzurot} exception \u8212- remains in force. And within that framework, the half-payment of a goring {\i tam} is a {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruling: Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion of this {\i sugya} is thus somewhat counterintuitive. All the evidence marshaled over the course of the discussion \u8212- the {\i mishna}\u8217's formulation, the logical argument that paying half of something suggests partial compensation rather than a zero-based fine, and the parallel of {\i tzurot} where half-payment is plainly {\i mammon} \u8212- points in the direction of treating {\i chatzi nezek} as {\i tashlumim}. Nevertheless, the Gemara concludes with a firm ruling: {\i palga nizka knasa}. The tradition is that a {\i tam}, by its nature, does not rise to the level of actionable negligence \u8212- one cannot be truly blamed for failing to prevent an ox that had never gored before from goring \u8212- and therefore the payment imposed is punitive rather than compensatory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling carries two practical consequences developed throughout the {\i sugya}. First, a {\i knas} is not collected based on the tortfeasor\u8217's own admission: {\i modeih bi-knas patur}. Second, a {\i knas} can only be imposed by a court with proper {\i semikhah} \u8212- the ordained authority tracing back to Moshe Rabbeinu \u8212- and therefore cannot be collected outside of Eretz Yisrael. Just as such a court is required to impose lashes, so too it is required to impose a monetary fine. An individual\u8217's admission cannot substitute for the court\u8217's imposition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unusual Animal Damage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshuneh Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded the discussion of {\i chatzi nezek}, the Gemara now extends the principles of {\i knas} to cases of {\i meshuneh} \u8212- unusual or unexpected animal behavior. The examples given are a dog that eats large lambs and a cat that eats large chickens. Such behavior is {\i meshuneh hu} \u8212- it is not the normal feeding behavior of these animals. Because it is anomalous and unexpected, it is treated analogously to the {\i keren} of an unhabituated ox, and only half-damages are assessed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knas Jurisdiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since this half-payment falls in the category of {\i knas}, it carries the jurisdictional limitations discussed above. {\i Ve-lo magbinan b\u8217'Vavel} \u8212- we do not collect this payment in Babylonia. A {\i knas} requires a court possessing the authentic chain of {\i semikhah} going back to Moshe Rabbeinu \u8212- courts that were only empowered in Eretz Yisrael. Outside of Eretz Yisrael no such court exists, and therefore a {\i knas} cannot be imposed there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an interesting conceptual question lurking here: why should {\i meshuneh} eating be classified under {\i keren} (intentional damage) rather than {\i shen} (an animal\u8217's ordinary feeding behavior)? Two approaches emerge from the broader discussion in Bava Kamma. One view holds that because the eating is abnormal it gets reclassified as {\i keren} \u8212- willful, unexpected damage. The other approach suggests it may remain categorized as {\i shen}, but since the animal was behaving in a way the owner could not have guarded against \u8212- analogous to the first gorings of a {\i tam} \u8212- perhaps half-payment and {\i knas} status apply even to what is technically a {\i shen}-type category. Either way, for our purposes the operative rule is: unusual behavior of this kind yields half-damages assessed as a {\i knas}, uncollectable in the Diaspora.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Normal Behavior\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if a cat eats small chickens or a dog eats small animals, {\i orchei hu} \u8212- that is simply normal behavior. In such a case, full damages are assessed and collected in the ordinary way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collection Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even in cases where a Diaspora court cannot formally adjudicate a {\i knas}, the damaged party is not left entirely without recourse. If the damagee has already seized property equivalent to his loss \u8212- {\i tafas} \u8212- the court will not compel him to return it. Of course, a court proceeding would still be necessary to determine the amount of damages and whether the seized property was proportionate. Additionally, if the damagee wishes to pursue the matter formally, {\i ve-ee amar akvu li zimna le-Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- he may ask the Diaspora court to set an appointment for him in a court in Eretz Yisrael. If the damager refuses to appear, {\i meshamtinan lei} \u8212- the Diaspora court places him under {\i cherem} until he complies. And regardless of whether such an appointment is set, the damager may be placed in {\i cherem} simply for maintaining a dangerous animal, on the grounds that {\i salek hezekach} \u8212- you must remove your hazard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to a {\i braita} citing Rabbi Natan: {\i minayin she-lo yigadel adam kelev ra be-toch beito} \u8212- how do we know that a person may not keep a dangerous dog in his house, nor maintain a shaky ladder? {\i She-ne\u8217'emar: ve-lo tasim damim be-veitecha} \u8212- as it is written, \u8220"you shall not place blood in your house.\u8221" The point is that the obligation to remove a hazard applies even in circumstances where the animal\u8217's behavior may be a first-time occurrence. Whether this applies universally or only once the dangerous pattern has become established remains an open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seduction & Rape Payments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three vs.\u160 ?Four\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to the {\i mishna} before us, which opens the fourth {\i perek}. The {\i mishna} states: a seduced girl ({\i mefutah}) receives three payments \u8212- {\i boshet}, {\i pegam}, and {\i knas} \u8212- while a raped girl ({\i anusah}) receives four, adding {\i tzaar} (pain and suffering) to the three. This distinction was already known from an earlier {\i mishna}, as the Gemara will shortly note, but the present {\i mishna} goes further in specifying to whom the payments go and under what circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payments to Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of these payments \u8212- the embarrassment, the injury, the fine, and in the case of rape the pain and suffering \u8212- go to the father. The {\i mishna} states this explicitly: {\i boshet u-pegam ve-knasah shel aviha}, and {\i ve-ha-tzaar bi-tfusah} \u8212- the {\i tzaar} in a case of forcible seizure likewise belongs to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Alive Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then details the distribution of payments according to the timing of various events. The baseline rule is straightforward: if the father is alive when the court case concludes and awards the payment, the money goes to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Dies After\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the court has concluded \u8212- the father was alive at the time of {\i gemar din}, the court\u8217's final ruling \u8212- and only thereafter the father dies, {\i harei hen shel achin}: the money passes to the brothers. The logic is that once the court has ruled, the debt is owed directly to the father; his death causes it to pass to his heirs like any other asset of the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Before Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if the court case had not yet been concluded before the father died \u8212- {\i lo hispika la\u8217'amod ba-din ad she-met av} \u8212- then {\i harei hen shel atzmah}: the money goes to the daughter herself. The conceptual framework underlying this, as the Gemara explains, is that the tortfeasor\u8217's obligation fundamentally runs to the daughter; the father is entitled to collect it through her by virtue of his legal relationship to her. Once the father has died before the court case, that parental relationship no longer exists, and the daughter receives the award directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Emancipated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel principle applies if the daughter has become a {\i bogeret} \u8212- a fully adult, emancipated woman \u8212- before the court case concluded. In that case as well, {\i hareihen shel atzmah}: the money goes to her. The father\u8217's entitlement depends on both his being alive and her being a {\i naarah} or {\i ketanah} under his authority at the time of the ruling. If either condition fails \u8212- either he dies or she becomes a {\i bogeret} \u8212- the money flows to her directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees with the {\i chachamim} regarding the effect of {\i gemar din}. He holds: {\i im lo hispika li-gbot ad she-met ha-av hareihen shel atzmah} \u8212- if the money was not yet actually collected before the father died, it goes to the daughter, even if the court had already ruled in the father\u8217's favor. According to Rabbi Shimon, {\i gemar din} alone is insufficient to convert the obligation into a direct debt owed to the father. Even after the court\u8217's ruling, the payment is still conceptually routed through the daughter \u8212- think of it as a kind of automatic transfer that activates only when the money is actually paid. If the father dies before receiving payment, the transfer never activates, and the money remains with the daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim}, by contrast, hold that {\i gemar din} converts the obligation into a direct debt to the father. Once he has been awarded the money by the court, it is his asset; his subsequent death causes it to pass to his sons as part of the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earnings vs.\u160 ?Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling of Rabbi Shimon is, however, limited to the {\i knas} context. The {\i mishna} immediately contrasts it with the daughter\u8217's earnings and found objects: {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha u-metzi\u8217'asah af al pi she-lo gavsa met av hareihen shel achin} \u8212- her wages and found property, even if not yet collected at the time of the father\u8217's death, pass to the brothers. The distinction is that wages and found property become an immediate direct debt to the father the moment they are earned or found. The {\i knas}, by contrast \u8212- according to Rabbi Shimon \u8212- continues to flow through the daughter even after {\i gemar din}, and therefore does not pass to the brothers if the father dies before collection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Knas & False Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens this section with a challenge: {\i mai kamashmalan?} \u8212- what is the {\i mishna} actually teaching us? The first part of the {\i mishna}, stating that the seducer pays three payments and the rapist four, was already known from an earlier {\i mishna}. And the fact that all payments go to the father \u8212- is that not obvious as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Seducer Pays\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then provides the answer: the {\i chiddush} that all payments go to the father is not entirely self-evident, but it can be inferred from a different principle. The fact that the seducer \u8212- whose act was consensual \u8212- still owes payment demonstrates that the payment runs to the father, not to the daughter. {\i De\u8217'ilei atzma, amai yahiv mefateh? Midaatah avad} \u8212- if the money were owed to the daughter herself, why would the seducer pay? She consented. The very existence of the seducer\u8217's liability proves that the injured party, in the relevant legal sense, is the father. The Gemara therefore concludes that the true {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishna} is the detailed rules about {\i gemar din} \u8212- the debate between the {\i rabanan} and Rabbi Shimon about what happens after the court rules and before the money is collected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones/Pituy Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now cites a {\i mishna} from tractate Shevuot to introduce the question of {\i korban shevuah} \u8212- the oath-offering \u8212- in the context of {\i knas}. The general rule of {\i korban shevuah} is that when a person falsely denies under oath an obligation that, had he admitted to it, he would have been required to pay, he brings a special {\i korban} and pays the principal plus a fifth. The question is whether this applies when the underlying obligation is a {\i knas}. The {\i mishna} in Shevuot records the case: a father says to someone, \u8220"You raped or seduced my daughter,\u8221" and the accused denies it under oath and then admits. Is he liable for a {\i korban shevuah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Korban for Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon holds: {\i she\u8217'eino meshalem knas al pi atzmo} \u8212- since you do not pay a {\i knas} based on your own admission, a false denial of a {\i knas} does not generate liability for a {\i korban shevuah}. The {\i chachamim} respond: even if that is true for the {\i knas} component, the accused would still pay for {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} based on his own admission \u8212- those are {\i mammon}, not {\i knas} \u8212- and therefore a false denial of those components does generate {\i korban} liability. The debate, as the Gemara notes, is not truly about the underlying legal principles, which both sides share. Both agree: no {\i korban shevuah} for {\i knas}, yes {\i korban shevuah} for {\i mammon}. The disagreement is about which component the father\u8217's claim primarily invokes \u8212- is the father demanding the {\i knas} (in which case denial is exempt), or the {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} (in which case denial obligates)?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abayes Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sets up the major inquiry of the {\i sugya}: Abaye poses a question to Rabba. The case: a father says, \u8220"You raped or seduced my daughter, and not only that \u8212- we already brought the case to court, {\i amadeticha ba-din}, and the court ruled that you owe me the money.\u8221" The accused denies everything \u8212- the act, the court case, and the resulting obligation \u8212- and swears falsely, then admits. {\i Le-Rabbi Shimon mah?} \u8212- what is the law according to Rabbi Shimon?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether {\i gemar din} \u8212- the court\u8217's final ruling \u8212- converts a {\i knas} into {\i mammon}. The logic supporting conversion is compelling: the reason a {\i knas} cannot be self-incurred through admission is that it requires a court to impose it; without the court, there is no debt. But once the court has in fact imposed the debt, the obstacle is removed. The person is no longer admitting to something that could not exist through his own admission \u8212- he is admitting to a debt already created by the court. Should that admission now obligate him, and should a false denial generate {\i korban shevuah} liability? Or does the obligation retain its character as {\i knas} permanently \u8212- since it originated as a {\i knas}, perhaps it always remains one?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara frames this precisely: {\i keivan de-amad ba-din mammon havei} \u8212- perhaps once there has been a court ruling, the obligation is now simply {\i mammon}, and we should be liable for the {\i korban shevuah} since we are disputing an established monetary debt. {\i O dilma, af al gav de-amad ba-din, knas havei} \u8212- or perhaps even after the court has ruled it retains its identity as {\i knas}, having originated as one, and therefore the {\i modeh bi-knas} exemption persists and there is no {\i korban} liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravas Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava answers Abaye: {\i mamona hu, mechayve lei korban shevuah} \u8212- after {\i gemar din}, it is {\i mammon}, and denying it generates {\i korban shevuah} liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this from a {\i braita}. Rabbi Shimon states in that {\i braita}: if someone demands of another \u8220"you raped or seduced my daughter,\u8221" or \u8220"your ox killed my slave,\u8221" or \u8220"you knocked out my slave\u8217's tooth\u8221" or \u8220"blinded my slave\u8217's eye\u8221" \u8212- all {\i knas} cases \u8212- and the accused swears falsely and then admits, one might think he is liable for a {\i korban shevuah}. The {\i braita} teaches: {\i yatzu elu she-hein knas} \u8212- excluded are cases of {\i knas}, from which one is exempt. And the {\i chachamim} hold \u8212- from the verse {\i u-ma\u8217'alah ma\u8217'al} \u8212- that one is {\i chayav} even in these cases. So we have a {\i machlokes}: {\i chachamim} say {\i chayav}, Rabbi Shimon says {\i patur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reisha/Seifa Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now probes whether the {\i braita}\u8217's ruling applies before or after {\i gemar din}. The argument proceeds as follows: the {\i reisha} of the {\i braita}, attributed to the {\i chachamim}, explicitly lists {\i ones u-mefateh} among the cases where one would be {\i chayav} for a {\i korban shevuah}. But there is a logical difficulty: if the obligation were purely {\i knas} with no {\i mammon} component whatsoever, and if one would be exempt even when admitting, how could the {\i chachamim} say one is {\i chayav} for denying? You cannot be liable for falsely denying something you would owe nothing for even if you admitted. The very fact that the {\i chachamim} impose {\i chiyuv} implies we must be speaking of a case where the {\i knas} has already been converted by {\i gemar din} \u8212- otherwise, how could denial generate liability?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And since the {\i reisha} (the {\i chachamim}\u8217's ruling) must refer to {\i she-amad ba-din}, the {\i seifa} (Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling of {\i patur}) must also refer to {\i she-amad ba-din}. This would demonstrate that Rabbi Shimon exempts even after {\i gemar din} \u8212- the {\i knas} retains its identity even then, yielding no {\i korban shevuah} and (by extension) no obligation upon admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds that he could technically distinguish the {\i braita}: the {\i reisha} speaks of {\i amad ba-din} and the {\i seifa} of {\i lo amad ba-din}, and the entire {\i braita} is Rabbi Shimon\u8217's own position rather than a debate with the {\i chachamim}. But he immediately rejects this answer: {\i shinuya de-chika lo meshanina lach} \u8212- I will not give you a forced answer. For if we read the {\i braita} that way, Rabbi Shimon\u8217's name should have appeared either at the beginning or consistently at the end; the way the {\i braita} is structured, with Rabbi Shimon appearing only in the {\i seifa} as a dissent, clearly indicates a debate between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i chachamim}. And since both must be addressing the same stage \u8212- {\i amad ba-din} \u8212- we see that Rabbi Shimon holds the {\i knas} remains {\i knas} even after {\i gemar din}, at least for the purposes of {\i korban shevuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore concedes: {\i u-modina lach le-inyan korban shevuah de-Rachmana patrei} \u8212- for the specific laws of {\i korban shevuah}, Rabbi Shimon is correct. There is a special verse \u8212- {\i ve-khichesh ba-amito} \u8212- that exempts obligations originating as {\i knas} from the {\i korban shevuah} regime, even after {\i gemar din}. But Rava maintains his core position: in other areas of {\i halakha}, after {\i gemar din}, the {\i knas} does convert to {\i mammon}. The exemption from {\i korban shevuah} is a {\i halakha} specific to the laws of oaths, grounded in a particular verse, and does not reflect the general legal identity of the post-{\i gemar din} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara next turns to our own {\i mishna} to challenge Rava\u8217's position. {\i Itivei} \u8212- we raise a contradiction. Rabbi Shimon rules in the {\i mishna}: if the money was not collected before the father died, it goes to the daughter. Now, if Rava is correct that after {\i gemar din} the obligation converts to {\i mammon} in general, then once the court rules it becomes a direct debt to the father, and upon his death it should pass as {\i yerusha} to his sons \u8212- {\i de-achin ba\u8217'ei le-hava}. Why then does Rabbi Shimon say she keeps it? This appears to demonstrate that according to Rabbi Shimon, {\i gemar din} does not convert {\i knas} to {\i mammon} \u8212- the obligation remains fundamentally routed through the daughter even after the court rules, and therefore upon the father\u8217's death it never becomes part of his estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 22 Year Struggle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then delivers a remarkable statement: {\i hai milta kashya ba le-Rabba ve-Rav Yosef esrin ve-tartin shanin} \u8212- this difficulty challenged Rabba and Rav Yosef for twenty-two years without resolution. The {\i Tosafot} raise an obvious question: if the difficulties were insurmountable, why not simply retract? All the evidence \u8212- the {\i korban shevuah} ruling and the {\i mishna} about inheritance \u8212- points toward Rabbi Shimon treating {\i knas} as {\i knas} even after {\i gemar din}. Why hold on to a tradition for twenty-two years if no reconciliation could be found? {\i Tosafot} answers: they evidently had a firm tradition that according to Rabbi Shimon, {\i gemar din} does in general convert {\i knas} to {\i mammon}. But they could not reconcile that tradition with the two exceptions \u8212- {\i korban shevuah} and the {\i mishna} about inheritance \u8212- for which the {\i knas} character clearly persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After Rabba\u8217's twenty-two years as {\i rosh yeshiva}, he passed away and Rav Yosef assumed the position. Rashi notes beautifully that in order to honor Rav Yosef \u8212- who was the greater {\i baki} even if Rabba had been the sharper analyst \u8212- he was assisted {\i min ha-shamayim} in resolving the question that had stymied his predecessor. The answer came to him when he assumed the leadership of the {\i yeshiva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef explains that the tradition Rabba had \u8212- that {\i gemar din} converts {\i knas} to {\i mammon} according to Rabbi Shimon \u8212- is indeed correct as a general rule, but there are two exceptions. The first exception: the laws of {\i korban shevuah}. Even after {\i gemar din}, when the original obligation was a {\i knas}, one is exempt from the {\i korban shevuah}. This is because the {\i Torah} requires the {\i korban} only for obligations that originated as {\i mammon}. The verse {\i ve-khichesh ba-amito} establishes that if it started its life as {\i knas}, the {\i korban} exemption applies regardless of subsequent conversions through {\i gemar din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones/Pituah Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second exception: the specific case of {\i ones u-mefateh} \u8212- rape and seduction. Even after {\i gemar din}, these payments are not considered a direct debt to the father; they continue to run through the daughter. The explanation lies in the verse: {\i ve-natan ha-ish ha-shochev imah le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah chamishim kasef} \u8212- \u8220"the man who lay with her shall give to the father of the girl fifty pieces of silver.\u8221" The word {\i ve-natan} \u8212- \u8220"he shall give\u8221" \u8212- connotes that the father\u8217's entitlement to the money crystallizes only at the moment of actual payment ({\i netinah}). {\i Lo zikhtah Torah le-av ella mi-sha\u8217'at netinah} \u8212- the Torah did not grant the father rights until the moment of giving. Therefore even after {\i gemar din}, the money remains conceptually the daughter\u8217's until it is actually handed over; if the father dies beforehand, it stays with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yiten vs.\u160 ?Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then tests whether this principle extends to the case of the slave: the verse regarding a goring ox that kills a slave also uses a payment term \u8212- {\i yitein} \u8212- \u8220"he shall give thirty shekels of silver to the master.\u8221" The Gemara distinguishes: {\i yitein lachud ve-natan lachud} \u8212- {\i yitein} (future) and {\i natan} (past/completed) carry different legal valences. The verse in the slave case uses {\i yitein} \u8212- \u8220"will give\u8221" \u8212- which does not imply the same condition of actual payment before the right vests. By contrast, the verse in {\i ones u-mefateh} uses {\i ve-natan} \u8212- connoting a completed act of giving \u8212- which establishes that the father\u8217's rights in {\i ones u-mefateh} arise only at the moment of actual payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final synthesis of the {\i sugya} can now be stated. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position, properly understood, is as follows: as a general rule, {\i gemar din} converts a {\i knas} into {\i mammon} \u8212- it becomes an established debt, transferable as part of an estate, and presumably triggering admission-based liability in the ordinary case. However, there are two domains where the {\i knas} character persists even after {\i gemar din}. First, the laws of {\i korban shevuah}: because the {\i Torah} requires the {\i korban} only for obligations whose inception was {\i mammon}, a false oath about an obligation that originated as {\i knas} \u8212- even if since converted through {\i gemar din} \u8212- remains exempt. Second, the payments of {\i ones u-mefateh}: because the {\i Torah} conditions the father\u8217's rights on actual payment, the obligation remains routed through the daughter after {\i gemar din}, and therefore does not pass to the brothers upon the father\u8217's death. In all other cases of {\i knas}, after {\i gemar din} the obligation is {\i mammon} for all purposes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 41b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half Damages as Fine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half Payment Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya occupying the top of 41b centers on a foundational debate in the laws of damages: is the half-payment ({\i chatzi nezek}) exacted from the owner of an unhabituated goring ox ({\i tam}) classified as a {\i knas} \u8212- a court-imposed fine \u8212- or as {\i mammon}, an ordinary monetary obligation? The practical difference is enormous: one who admits to owing a {\i knas} is not thereby obligated to pay it, whereas admission to a {\i mammon} debt does create a binding obligation. The {\i mishnah} established that anytime a payment exceeds actual damages, we can identify it as a {\i knas}, since it goes beyond compensatory logic. But when payment is {\i less} than full damages \u8212- as is the case with half-payment \u8212- it would seem intuitively to be partial compensation rather than a fine. The amoraic discussion turns on whether {\i chatzi nezek} of a {\i tam} falls on the {\i mammon} side of that line or the {\i knas} side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Muad vs.\u160 ?Tam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with an attempted proof. A {\i braita} states: if someone says \u8220"my ox gored so-and-so\u8221" ({\i hamish shoro es ploni}), he is obligated to pay {\i al pi atzmo} \u8212- on the basis of his own admission. The Gemara asks: does this not refer to a {\i tam}, thereby demonstrating that {\i chatzi nezek} of a {\i tam} is {\i mammon} \u8212- collectible by admission? The Gemara deflects: the {\i braita} speaks of a {\i muad}, a habituated ox, whose full-damage payment is unambiguously {\i mammon}. The {\i tam} case remains open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presses further: the {\i braita} contrasts goring a free person (where the owner pays {\i al pi atzmo}) with goring a slave (where the fixed thirty-{\i shekel} payment is a {\i knas} and therefore not payable by admission). Why must the {\i braita} shift to the slave case to illustrate exemption from {\i al pi atzmo}? Why not simply distinguish {\i muad} from {\i tam} within the same goring-of-a-person case \u8212- saying that by a {\i muad} one pays {\i al pi atzmo}, but by a {\i tam} one does not? The fact that the {\i braita} leaps to the slave scenario might imply that even a {\i tam} is considered {\i mammon} for the purposes of {\i al pi atzmo}, leaving the slave case as the first point of contrast. The Gemara responds: the {\i braita} may have chosen to remain entirely within cases of a {\i muad} ox, contrasting goring of a free person with goring of a slave, both in the {\i muad} framework. This does not prove anything about whether the {\i tam} is {\i mammon} or {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna\u8217's Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A stronger challenge arises from the language of the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} states the rule: {\i kol hameshalem yaser al mah she\u8217'hizik eino meshalem al pi atzmo} \u8212- whoever pays {\i more} than the actual damages does not pay by his own admission. The implication is that paying {\i less} than damages \u8212- as in {\i chatzi nezek} \u8212- would fall outside this exemption, and one would pay {\i al pi atzmo}, thus treating it as {\i mammon}. The Gemara attempts to reread the {\i mishnah}: perhaps the rule is that only {\i exact} payment constitutes {\i mammon}, and both more {\i and} less than damages qualify as {\i knas}. If that were the intent, the {\i mishnah} should have stated explicitly that any deviation from exact damages \u8212- whether greater or lesser \u8212- renders payment a {\i knas}. Since it said only \u8220"more,\u8221" the plain implication is that \u8220"less\u8221" is {\i mammon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion: Fine Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara accepts the force of the textual argument \u8212- {\i tshyufta} \u8212- yet immediately rules: {\i v\u8217'hilchasa palga nizka knasa} \u8212- the {\i halacha} is that half damages is a {\i knas}. This juxtaposition is striking: the {\i mishnah} nearly explicitly implies {\i mammon}, yet the ruling goes the other way. The Gemara asks directly: you contradict it and then rule that way? The answer is {\i in} \u8212- yes. The reason the {\i mishnah} did not formulate its rule symmetrically (\u8220"any deviation from exact damages is a {\i knas}\u8221") is that there exists a case of less-than-full damages that is unambiguously {\i mammon}, which would have made the broader formulation inaccurate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pebbles Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That exception is the case of {\i tzoros} \u8212- pebbles kicked up by a walking animal ({\i regel}). When an animal walks and directly tramples upon vessels, it pays full damages under the heading of {\i regel}. When it walks and kicks up pebbles that then break objects, it pays only half damages, and this is a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe MiSinai} \u8212- a tradition from Sinai. In this case, the half-payment is undeniably {\i mammon}, rooted in {\i regel} rather than in the logic of a fine. Because the {\i mishnah} could not say \u8220"any amount other than exact damages is a {\i knas}\u8221" without inadvertently including this case of {\i tzoros}, it said only \u8220"more than damages.\u8221" But the operative rule, bracketing {\i tzoros}, is that {\i chatzi nezek} of a goring {\i tam} is a {\i knas}. This fits the underlying logic as well: what negligence can really be attributed to someone whose ox gored unexpectedly for the first time? The lack of full culpability pushes the payment into the realm of a court-imposed fine rather than ordinary compensatory {\i mammon}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unusual Animal Damage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unusual Eating Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i chatzi nezek} of a goring {\i tam} is a {\i knas}, the Gemara extends the analysis: {\i v\u8217'hashta de\u8217'amarta palga nizka knasa} \u8212- now that you have declared half damages a {\i knas}, we consider cases of {\i meshuneh} \u8212- unusual and unexpected animal behavior. A dog ({\i kalba}) that eats large lambs, or a cat ({\i shunra}) that eats large chickens, are behaving in a manner that is genuinely abnormal. Such acts are classified as {\i meshuneh}, and accordingly the owner pays only half damages, with that half-payment again treated as a {\i knas}. This raises a conceptual question: {\i keren} (goring) is understood as willful damaging; here the animal is engaged in eating. Why is an unusual eating act categorized as {\i keren} rather than {\i shein} (tooth damage)? Two approaches exist: one holds that since the eating is abnormal it is simply re-categorized as {\i keren}; the other holds that even if it remains under {\i shein}, just as the {\i tam} pays only half damages because the goring was unexpected and the owner was not negligent, so too an animal that eats in a totally unexpected way pays only half damages and that half-payment is a {\i knas}. Either way, the practical outcome is the same. If the dog or cat eats small animals \u8212- {\i zutrei} \u8212- that is normal behavior ({\i orcheih hu}) and the owner pays full damages, collectible in any court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collection Outside Israel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The classification of {\i meshuneh} behavior as {\i knas} carries an important jurisdictional consequence. A {\i knas} requires not merely any court, but a court possessing {\i smicha} \u8212- the ordination chain tracing back to Moshe Rabbeinu \u8212- which confers on the court the authority designated in the Torah by the term {\i Elohim}. Such courts are empowered only within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where {\i smicha} is granted. Consequently, {\i knas} payments \u8212- including half damages for unusual animal behavior \u8212- cannot be collected in Babylonia ({\i lo magbinan be-Vavel}). This rule applies not only to {\i al pi atzmo} but to the entire process: without the properly ordained court, the {\i knas} simply cannot be imposed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaming Remedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite this jurisdictional limitation, the injured party is not entirely without remedy outside Israel. The Gemara offers three practical tools. First, if the damagee has physically seized property equivalent to his loss ({\i tafas}), the court will not compel him to return it \u8212- though a court proceeding may still be needed to determine the precise amount seized. Second, if the damagee wishes to pursue the matter formally, he may petition the Babylonian court to schedule a hearing for him in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and if the damager refuses to appear, the court will place him under {\i cherem} (excommunication) until he complies. Third \u8212- and this applies regardless of whether a court date has been set \u8212- the court will declare the damager under {\i cherem} until he removes his dangerous animal, invoking the principle of {\i de\u8217'amrinan lei silik hezeikach}, \u8220"we tell him: eliminate your hazard.\u8221" This last principle is attributed to Rabbi Nosson, who derives from the verse {\i velo sasim damim beveisecha} that one may not maintain a dangerous dog or a rickety ladder in one\u8217's home.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rape/Seduction Payments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three/Four Payments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} establishes the basic framework: a seducer ({\i mefateh}) pays three things \u8212- {\i boshes} (embarrassment), {\i pegam} (physical injury devaluing her marriage prospects), and {\i knas} (the fifty-{\i shekel} fine) \u8212- while a rapist ({\i ones}) pays four, adding {\i tza\u8217'ar} (pain and suffering). All of these go to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternal Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The father\u8217's right to collect is not absolute; it is conditioned on his being alive and the daughter not yet having reached majority ({\i bogeret}) at the time the court case is adjudicated. The {\i mishnah} walks through the permutations: if the case came before the court while the father was alive and the daughter was still a {\i na\u8217'ara} or {\i ktana}, the payments belong to the father. If the father dies after the court has ruled in his favor ({\i amda bedin}), the debt \u8212- now a monetary obligation owed to him \u8212- passes to his sons as heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter\u8217's Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two scenarios transfer the right to collect directly to the daughter. First, if the father died before the court case was held ({\i lo hispika la\u8217'amod bedin ad shemes ha\u8217'av}), she is the litigant when the case is finally adjudicated and she herself receives the payment. The underlying logic is that the obligation fundamentally runs to the daughter \u8212- the wrong was done to her \u8212- and the father collects through her. Once he is no longer alive when the case is decided, there is no channel transferring the payment to him, and it remains with her. Second, if by the time of the court case she has become a {\i bogeret}, she has been emancipated from her father\u8217's authority, and the payment similarly goes to her. A daughter reaching majority is treated equivalently to paternal death for these purposes: in both situations, the conduit through which the father would have collected has closed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon holds a more stringent position regarding the father\u8217's rights: even after {\i gmar din} \u8212- after the court has issued its ruling \u8212- the obligation is not yet considered a direct debt to the father. It continues to travel through the daughter to the father. Therefore, if the father dies after the court case but before the money has actually been paid out, it still goes to the daughter rather than to the brothers. The {\i Chachamim}, by contrast, hold that {\i gmar din} converts the obligation into a direct debt to the father, such that his death after {\i gmar din} transfers it to his sons as part of his estate. The {\i mishnah} notes an important contrast: with respect to her earnings ({\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha}) and found objects, even Rabbi Shimon agrees that the debt vests in the father as soon as it is earned, regardless of collection. It is specifically the {\i knas} payment for rape or seduction that \u8212- according to Rabbi Shimon \u8212- retains its character of flowing through the daughter rather than being directly owed to the father, even after the court has ruled.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 False Oaths & Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita on False Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a {\i mishnah} from {\i Shevuot}. The relevant law is that one who falsely swears to deny a financial obligation \u8212- invoking God\u8217's name to avoid paying \u8212- incurs liability to pay the principal plus a fifth, and to bring a special {\i korban} ({\i korban shvuah}). This liability is triggered only when the underlying claim is {\i mammon}: were the claimant to admit the obligation, he would be required to pay. When the underlying claim is {\i knas}, however, no such oath liability exists, because even an admission would not create a binding obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} in {\i Shevuot} presents the scenario: a father says to someone, \u8220"you raped or seduced my daughter\u8221" ({\i onasta u\u8217'fitita es biti}), the accused denies it, swears to that denial, and then admits. The question is whether he is liable for the false oath. Rabbi Shimon exempts him, reasoning that since one would not be obligated to pay a {\i knas} upon admission, there is no mechanism by which denial under oath can create a {\i korban} obligation. The {\i Chachamim} respond: even if the {\i knas} itself is not payable {\i al pi atzmo}, the {\i boshes} and {\i pegam} components are ordinary {\i mammon} and are payable by admission. One who denies having raped or seduced is implicitly denying the {\i mammon} components as well, and is therefore liable for the false oath on that basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qnes vs.\u160 ?Mammon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analysis reveals that the underlying dispute between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Chachamim} is not about the legal categories themselves \u8212- both sides agree that {\i knas} does not generate oath liability while {\i mammon} does \u8212- but rather about what the claimant is primarily demanding when he says \u8220"you raped my daughter.\u8221" Is the demand centered on the {\i knas}, in which case the denial relates to a non-obligating admission? Or does it encompass the {\i boshes} and {\i pegam}, in which case denial does generate oath liability? Everyone agrees one is not liable for swearing falsely about a pure {\i knas}, and everyone agrees one is liable for swearing falsely about {\i mammon}. The question is which component of the compound claim this particular denial is addressing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A sharper question is now raised. Abaye asked Rabbah: suppose the father says not merely \u8220"you raped my daughter,\u8221" but \u8220"you raped my daughter, and we took the case to court ({\i ve-amadtinu ba-din}), and the court ruled you owe me money ({\i ve-nischayavta li mamon}).\u8221" The accused denies the entire claim \u8212- no rape, no court case, no ruling \u8212- swears to that denial, and then admits. What is the law according to Rabbi Shimon?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The thrust of the question is whether {\i amad bedin} \u8212- the issuance of a court judgment \u8212- converts a {\i knas} into {\i mammon}. When the {\i knas} was first incurred, it could not generate oath liability because an admission would not have been obligating: there was no debt without a court imposing it. But after a court has already imposed the debt, the accused is no longer merely admitting to an act; he is admitting to the existence of a judicially imposed obligation. At that point, one might argue, the origin of the obligation as a {\i knas} is irrelevant \u8212- what exists now is a court-imposed debt, and denying a court-imposed debt is exactly the kind of denial that generates {\i korban shvuah} liability. Does the fact that it originated as a {\i knas} permanently color its identity, or does the court judgment transform it into {\i mammon}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava answered: it is {\i mammon} \u8212- after {\i amad bedin}, the obligation is monetary, and therefore if one swears falsely about it one is liable to bring a {\i korban shvuah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita & Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now examines this claim against a {\i braita}. The {\i braita} enumerates the cases that generate {\i korban shvuah} liability: the {\i Chachamim} ask, from where do we know that even cases of double payment ({\i kefel}), fourfold and fivefold payment, and {\i ones u\u8217'mefateh} and {\i motsi shem ra} \u8212- all of which are {\i knas} \u8212- require a {\i korban shvuah}? The answer is derived from the verse\u8217's additional language {\i u\u8217'ma\u8217'alah ma\u8217'al} (a trespass), which extends the obligation beyond purely {\i mammon} cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Exempts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon then states his position in this {\i braita}: cases of rape and seduction, the goring of a slave, and a master knocking out a slave\u8217's tooth or blinding his eye \u8212- all instances of {\i knas} \u8212- do not generate {\i korban shvuah} liability even if denied under oath. The derivation is from the verse {\i vekichesh ba\u8217'amito b\u8217'fikadon o viteshumat yad o vegazel} etc., which describes purely monetary wrongs; actions that began as {\i knas} are excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Judgment Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now advances a critical argument against Rava\u8217's original position. If Rabbi Shimon\u8217's exemption applied only before {\i amad bedin}, there would be no need for the {\i braita} to use a {\i pasuk} to prove the exemption. The logic is as follows: if denying the {\i knas} before {\i amad bedin} carries no {\i korban} liability, that is self-evidently because an admission at that stage would not be obligating either \u8212- there is nothing to deny oath-worthy. A {\i pasuk} would only be necessary to exempt someone who is denying something that \u8212- had he admitted it \u8212- would have obligated him. Rava initially responded that perhaps the {\i braita}\u8217's exemption speaks of a case before {\i amad bedin}, and the {\i muad}-like cases where the {\i Chachamim} impose liability speak of after {\i amad bedin}. The two positions within the {\i braita} would then represent the same speaker \u8212- Rabbi Shimon \u8212- at different stages of the proceedings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejects this reading himself, noting that the structure of the {\i braita} does not support it. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's name appears only at the point of exemption; if he were also the author of the liability ruling, his name would appear there as well, or the entire {\i braita} would be labeled his. The natural reading is that the {\i Chachamim} assert liability and Rabbi Shimon dissents. And since the {\i Chachamim} \u8212- in asserting liability \u8212- cannot be speaking of a denial that would have been entirely non-obligating even by admission, they must be speaking of the case after {\i amad bedin}. And since Rabbi Shimon is responding to the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's case, his exemption also applies after {\i amad bedin}. This is a powerful demonstration that Rabbi Shimon holds the {\i knas} retains its identity even after {\i amad bedin}, at least for the purposes of {\i korban shvuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava concedes this point specifically regarding {\i korban shvuah}: {\i u\u8217'modinu lach l\u8217'inyan korban shvuah d\u8217'Rachmana patrei} \u8212- regarding the {\i korban shvuah}, I concede that the Torah specifically exempts a {\i knas} from this obligation, even after {\i amad bedin}. The verse {\i vekichesh ba\u8217'amito} establishes that only what began its life as pure {\i mammon} generates {\i korban shvuah} liability. A {\i knas} \u8212- even one that has been adjudicated by {\i beis din} \u8212- retains its character as {\i knas} for this specific purpose and remains exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rava maintains that for other areas of {\i halacha}, the court judgment does convert {\i knas} to {\i mammon}. His primary example is {\i lehorish le-vanav} \u8212- bequeathal: if the claimant dies after {\i amad bedin}, the judicially imposed debt should pass to his sons as part of his estate, because at that stage it exists as a {\i mammon} obligation. The {\i korban shvuah} exemption is understood as a special {\i halacha} with its own textual basis, not as evidence that the {\i knas} retains its identity across the board.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Contradiction & Resoluti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna\u8217's Bequeathal Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now confronts Rava\u8217's position with the very {\i mishnah} under study. Rabbi Shimon explicitly rules: {\i im lo hispika ligbot ad shemes ha\u8217'av harei hen shel atzmah} \u8212- if the money was not collected before the father died, it goes to the daughter. This is directly in tension with Rava\u8217's claim that after {\i amad bedin}, the {\i knas} is converted to {\i mammon} for purposes of bequeathal. If it were indeed {\i mammon} at that point, the brothers \u8212- as the father\u8217's heirs \u8212- should inherit the debt, not the daughter. Yet Rabbi Shimon says the daughter collects. This seems to prove that for Rabbi Shimon, {\i knas} remains {\i knas} even after {\i amad bedin}, across the board and not merely for {\i korban shvuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 22-Year Puzzle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This contradiction apparently troubled Rabba and Rav Yosef for twenty-two years ({\i hai milsa kashya beh le-Rabba u-le-Rav Yosef esrin v\u8217'sartin shanin}). Tosafot raises an obvious question: why not simply retract the position? If all evidence points to Rabbi Shimon maintaining {\i knas} status throughout, why remain committed to the tradition that it converts to {\i mammon} after {\i amad bedin}? Tosafot answers that they possessed a firm tradition \u8212- not merely a logical inference \u8212- that according to Rabbi Shimon, {\i knas} does convert to {\i mammon} after {\i amad bedin}. Yet the evidence from both the {\i korban shvuah} laws and the {\i mishnah}\u8217's bequeathal rule contradicted this at every turn. They could not reconcile the tradition with the texts, and the difficulty stood unresolved for twenty-two years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution came when Rav Yosef became {\i rosh yeshiva} after Rabba\u8217's passing. Rashi notes beautifully that it was precisely to honor Rav Yosef \u8212- known as the greater {\i baki} though Rabba had been the sharper analyst \u8212- that heaven assisted him in resolving what his predecessor could not. Rav Yosef\u8217's answer: {\i shani hatam de\u8217'amar kra} \u8212- the case of {\i ones u\u8217'mefateh} is itself an exception, because the Torah itself specifies: {\i v\u8217'nasan ha-ish ha-shochev imah l\u8217'avi ha-na\u8217'arah chamishim kasef} \u8212- \u8220"the man who lay with her shall give to the father of the girl fifty pieces of silver.\u8221" The verb {\i v\u8217'nasan} \u8212- \u8220"he shall give\u8221" or \u8220"he has given\u8221" \u8212- implies that the father\u8217's entitlement is activated only at the moment of actual payment ({\i mishea\u8217's nesina}). Until the money physically changes hands, the Torah has not vested the right in the father; the obligation fundamentally belongs to the daughter, and the father receives only through her. Therefore even after {\i amad bedin}, in a case of {\i ones u\u8217'mefateh}, the father has not yet acquired a direct {\i mammon} claim \u8212- he would only acquire it upon actual receipt. If he dies before receiving it, the daughter collects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rule of Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The general principle articulated by Rabba regarding other {\i knasot} \u8212- that after {\i amad bedin} the {\i knas} converts to {\i mammon} and can be bequeathed to the sons \u8212- thus stands for all {\i knasot} other than {\i ones u\u8217'mefateh}. Two exceptions have now been established. In the laws of {\i korban shvuah}, there is a Scriptural exclusion: anything that originated as a {\i knas} is exempt from oath-offering liability even after {\i amad bedin}, since the verse conditions that obligation on an underlying {\i mammon} obligation. In {\i ones u\u8217'mefateh}, the verse {\i v\u8217'nasan} conditions the father\u8217's right entirely on the act of giving, meaning the father\u8217's claim never becomes a direct {\i mammon} debt independent of the daughter until payment is actually made. In all other {\i knasot}, Rabbi Shimon would agree that {\i amad bedin} converts {\i knas} to {\i mammon} \u8212- the debt vests in the claimant, passes to his heirs upon death, and is treated as a standard monetary obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 43\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 50 Shekel Fine Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knas Identity After Court\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya before us concludes a sustained discussion that properly belongs to tractate {\i Shavuot}, yet bears directly on our {\i mishnah} in Ketubot. The underlying question is whether the fifty {\i shekel} fine paid in cases of rape retains its identity as {\i knas} even after {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din} \u8212- that is, after court proceedings have been initiated \u8212- or whether {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din} converts it into ordinary {\i mamon}. The practical stakes are considerable: if it remains {\i knas}, the defendant cannot be obligated through his own admission, it cannot be bequeathed to the father\u8217's sons if the father dies before collection, and there is no liability for a {\i korban shavuah} if one falsely denied it under oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's position, as it emerges both from our {\i mishnah} and from the parallel {\i mishnah} in {\i Shavuot}, is that the fifty {\i shekel} fine retains its identity as {\i knas} throughout \u8212- even after {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din}. This is the source of his ruling in our {\i mishnah} that if the father dies before collecting the fine, it passes not to the brothers but to the daughter herself. Were it {\i mamon} once {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din} occurred, it would pass to the sons like any other monetary debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, maintains that {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din} does in fact convert {\i knas} into {\i mamon} \u8212- with one notable exception. By the law of {\i korban shavuah}, even after the conversion, a special verse \u8212- {\i ve-kichesh} \u8212- teaches that if the obligation began its life as {\i knas}, no {\i korban} is ever incurred for a false denial, even once it has become {\i mamon}. So for {\i Rabbi Shimon}, the fifty {\i shekel} fine is categorically different: it remains {\i knas} across the board. For the {\i rabbanan} and for Rava\u8217's reading of {\i Rabbi Shimon}, only the {\i korban shavuah} exemption is categorically preserved, while the {\i mamon} conversion is otherwise standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} arrives at this picture by parsing a detail: if {\i Rabbi Shimon} holds the fifty {\i shekel} is fundamentally {\i knas} in all respects, why does the {\i mishnah} in {\i Shavuot} need to cite the verse {\i ve-kichesh} to exempt it from {\i korban shavuah}? The verse {\i ve-natan} \u8212- which grounds the daughter\u8217's right to collect upon the father\u8217's death \u8212- should have sufficed to establish the {\i knas} identity of the fifty {\i shekel} for all purposes, including exemption from {\i korban}. The {\i gemara} answers that the verse {\i ve-kichesh} is needed for a specific and unusual scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bogeret Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario is: {\i kegon she-amdah be-din u-vagrah} \u8212- a case in which the girl first underwent {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din} and then became a {\i bogeret}, an emancipated adult. Once she matures, the obligation is no longer directed to the father under the verse {\i ve-natan le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah} but shifts to her. This combination of {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din} and her subsequent maturation together converts the fifty {\i shekel} into {\i mamon}, even according to {\i Rabbi Shimon}. The father may then inherit it back from the daughter \u8212- but he does so as {\i mamon}, not {\i knas}. In this scenario, then, even {\i Rabbi Shimon} concedes the conversion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore explains: {\i de-hasam ki ka-yaris aviha mina didah ka-yaris} \u8212- when the father inherits it, he inherits it as her property. Why, then, is there still no {\i korban shavuah}? Here the verse {\i ve-kichesh} is essential: it teaches that even when the obligation has been fully converted into {\i mamon} in every other respect, if it originated as {\i knas}, the {\i korban shavuah} exemption persists permanently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knas Origin Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: if even {\i Rabbi Shimon} agrees this is now {\i mamon}, how can we call it {\i knas} and thereby exempt it from {\i korban shavuah}? Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak resolves this with the principle: {\i yatzu eilu she-ikaran knas} \u8212- \u8220"excluded are those whose very origin is {\i knas}.\u8221" It is not necessary that the obligation currently retain its {\i knas} character; it is sufficient that it began as {\i knas}. The {\i korban shavuah} exemption, in {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's view, is categorical and permanent, anchored in the origin of the obligation rather than in its present legal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the layered structure: {\i Rabbi Shimon} holds (a) that generally the fifty {\i shekel} remains {\i knas} even after {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din}, unlike other fines; (b) that in the specific {\i bogeret} scenario it does become {\i mamon} even for {\i Rabbi Shimon}; and (c) that in all cases where the obligation originated as {\i knas}, {\i ve-kichesh} permanently exempts it from {\i korban shavuah}, whether or not it has since been converted into {\i mamon}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's Implicit Demand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Demand Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the surface-level dispute between {\i Rabbi Shimon} and the {\i rabbanan}. A {\i beraita} cites {\i Rabbi Shimon} as exempting from {\i korban shavuah} in the case where the father demands payment for the rape of his daughter, apparently because the demand is for {\i knas}, and one who admits to {\i knas} is exempt \u8212- {\i modeh be-knas patur}. The {\i gemara} notes that this implies it is still genuinely {\i knas} rather than merely a case where the original {\i knas} survives by force of verse. If {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din} had converted it to {\i mamon}, the exemption would need to be explained differently \u8212- not simply as \u8220"it is {\i knas}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by explaining that {\i Rabbi Shimon} is speaking {\i le-divreichem de-rabbanan} \u8212- framing his argument in terms his opponents will accept. His reasoning runs as follows: according to you, {\i rabbanan}, who hold that once it becomes {\i mamon} one incurs {\i korban shavuah}, you should at least concede that where there has been no {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din}, it is still purely {\i knas}, and {\i modeh be-knas patur} \u8212- so there should be no {\i korban shavuah} even on your own terms. As for Rav\u8217's own position, {\i ve-kichesh} tells him there is never a {\i korban shavuah} on anything that began as {\i knas}, regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The top of our {\i daf} \u8212- 43a \u8212- introduces the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's response and the analytical underpinning of the dispute. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i be-mai kamiplagei} \u8212- what exactly is the interpretive disagreement? When a father says to someone \u8220"you raped my daughter, pay me,\u8221" what is he implicitly demanding \u8212- the fixed fifty {\i shekel knas}, or the indeterminate {\i boshet} and {\i pagam}, the shame payment and the diminishment in her value? Rav Pappa frames the dispute in these terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed vs.\u160 ?Amorphous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon} holds: {\i lo shavik inish midi de-kaitz ve-tava midi de-lo kaitz} \u8212- a person does not abandon a claim with a fixed, determinate amount in order to demand something amorphous and indeterminate. The fifty {\i shekel} is concrete; {\i boshet} and {\i pagam} are subject to court assessment and could be anything. Therefore, the father is presumed to be demanding the {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Escape vs.\u160 ?No Escape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} hold the opposite: {\i lo shavik inish midi de-chi modi bei lo miftar} \u8212- a person does not abandon a claim from which the defendant cannot escape by confessing. If the demand is for {\i mamon}, an admission by the defendant obligates him to pay immediately, with no exit. By contrast, if the demand is for {\i knas}, an admission paradoxically exempts the defendant \u8212- {\i modeh be-knas patur} \u8212- and the father gains nothing from forcing a confession. The {\i rabbanan} therefore reason that the father is implicitly demanding {\i boshet} and {\i pagam}, not the {\i knas}, because that is the demand that can be enforced through admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is ultimately an argument about human psychology and litigation strategy rather than a principled legal distinction. Interestingly, the {\i gemara} seems to assume these are mutually exclusive demands \u8212- it does not consider why the father would not be presumed to be demanding both simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains clear as the halachic bottom line for our {\i mishnah} is {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's position that the fifty {\i shekel} retains its {\i knas} identity even after {\i ha\u8217'amadah be-din}, and therefore if the father dies before it is collected, the daughter \u8212- not the brothers \u8212- is entitled to collect it. That is the {\i chidush} of {\i Rabbi Shimon} that is directly relevant to our discussion in Ketubot.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Daughter\u8217's Earnings Righ\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earnings Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the foregoing {\i sugya} concluded, the {\i gemara} transitions to a new but related topic raised by Rabbi Avina to Rav Sheshet: {\i bat ha-nizonet min ha-achin} \u8212- a daughter who is being supported by the brothers from the estate after her father\u8217's death. What are the brothers\u8217' rights, if any, to her {\i maaseh yadayim}, her earnings?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some background is necessary. When a man dies, his estate passes to his sons. The daughters do not inherit. To protect the daughters, a standard {\i tenai ketubah} \u8212- indeed, one that applies even if not explicitly written \u8212- requires the estate to support any daughters until they marry. This is the case of {\i bat ha-nizonet min ha-achin}: the brothers have no discretion; the conditions of the {\i ketubah} bind the estate to provide for her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether the estate, in exchange for this support, acquires rights to the daughter\u8217's earnings. On the surface, this resembles the {\i ketubot} arrangement between husband and wife, where the husband\u8217's support obligation corresponds reciprocally to his right to his wife\u8217's earnings \u8212- a rabbinic exchange of {\i maaseh yadayim} for {\i mezonot}. One might extend the same logic here: since the estate is providing her sustenance, perhaps the estate should receive her earnings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocity Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is that the husband-wife exchange is explicitly a rabbinic reciprocal institution, created precisely because neither spouse has an entirely unilateral legal claim over the other. The father\u8217's relationship to his daughter\u8217's earnings, by contrast, is unilateral and biblical. The {\i gemara} grounds this in the fact that a father may sell his daughter as an {\i amah ivriyah} \u8212- a Hebrew maidservant \u8212- which is essentially a transfer of the right to her labor and earnings. This right is not conditional on his supporting her; it is a status right rooted in his paternal authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is reinforced by an important distinction: {\i hatam mididei mizna} \u8212- in the father\u8217's case, he feeds the daughter from his own pocket and at his own initiative, since the Torah does not actually obligate him to support her. The {\i rabbanan} instituted that he is entitled to her {\i maaseh yadayim} in part to avoid the resentment ({\i eivah}) that would arise if he were feeding her voluntarily while she pocketed everything she earned or found. By contrast, the brothers are not feeding her from their own earnings; they are disbursing funds from the inherited estate. Moreover, they have no choice \u8212- the estate is legally obligated to support her as a condition of the {\i ketubah}. The volitional, unilateral character of the father\u8217's support is entirely absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow vs.\u160 ?Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet attempted to answer the question from a {\i mishnah} regarding the {\i almana} \u8212- the widow. The {\i mishnah} states that a widow supported by the estate\u8217's heirs ({\i nizonet min nichsei yetomim}) surrenders her earnings to the estate in exchange. By analogy, the daughter supported by the estate should similarly surrender her earnings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the comparison: {\i mi dami?} The situations are not parallel, because the deceased husband\u8217's concern for his widow and his concern for his daughter are not equivalent \u8212- at least not along every axis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Priorities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes between two dimensions: {\i ziluta} (shame and degradation) and {\i harvacha} (profit and financial gain). The principle it advances is: {\i le-inyan ziluta}, when it comes to avoiding degradation, the father demonstrated greater concern for his widow than for his daughter \u8212- as evidenced by the rule in {\i Bava Batra} that in a small estate ({\i nichsim mu\u8217'atim}), if there is not enough to support both the widow and the daughters, the widow takes precedence and the daughters must go begging. Yet {\i le-inyan harvacha}, when it comes to retaining financial upside, the father is more concerned that his daughter pocket her earnings than that his wife do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially find this distinction arbitrary \u8212- the {\i gemara} could seemingly have argued the reverse. But one compelling explanation offered in discussion is that the father has an interest in his daughter accumulating a dowry in order to marry her off advantageously. He would therefore want her to keep whatever she earns. His wife\u8217's financial accumulation beyond what is needed for her maintenance is less of a priority in his thinking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this distinction by citing Rabbi Abba quoting Rabbi Yosei: {\i asu almana etzel ha-bat ke-vat etzel ha-achin} \u8212- they treated the widow vis-\u224 ?-vis the daughter as the daughter vis-\u224 ?-vis the brothers. This means the widow takes precedence over the daughter in a small estate, just as the daughter takes precedence over the brothers. Doesn\u8217't this prove the husband was more concerned with his wife than with his daughter? The {\i gemara} responds that this holds true only for the {\i ziluta} dimension \u8212- it is more degrading for a widow to beg than for a daughter. In terms of {\i harvacha}, the opposite priorities apply, and the father\u8217's posthumous concern for his daughter\u8217's financial gain exceeds his concern for his wife\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Implication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earnings During Life\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to our {\i mishnah} itself to attempt a proof about the daughter\u8217's entitlement to her own earnings. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i maaseh yadeha u-metziatah} \u8212- her earnings and her found objects \u8212- {\i af al pi she-lo gavtah}, even if she has not yet collected them, once earned during the father\u8217's lifetime, pass to the brothers upon the father\u8217's death. The {\i gemara} infers from this framing: {\i ta\u8217'ama de-ve-chayei ha-av} \u8212- the reason it goes to the brothers is specifically that she earned it while the father was alive; but {\i ha le-achar mitat ha-av le-atzmah} \u8212- had she earned it after the father\u8217's death, she would keep it herself. The {\i mishnah} thus implies strongly that earnings after the father\u8217's death remain hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All or Excess?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately tests this inference: {\i mai lav bi-mezonot} \u8212- is this not speaking even in a case where the estate is supporting her? The attempted response is to say {\i lo}, perhaps the {\i mishnah} refers only to a case where she is {\i not} being supported by the estate \u8212- and if she is unsupported, of course she keeps her earnings, so nothing has been proved about the case of {\i bat ha-nizonet min ha-achin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back: if it were referring to the unsupported case, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's implication would be obvious and thus no {\i chiddush} at all. Even according to the opinion that a master may say to a non-Jewish slave {\i aseh imi ve-eineni zoncha} \u8212- work for me without my feeding you \u8212- this does not apply to a Jewish slave, regarding whom the Torah writes {\i imcha}, \u8220"with you,\u8221" implying an obligation of care. Certainly the daughter is no worse than a Jewish slave in this regard. So if the estate is not supporting her, it is trivially obvious she keeps her earnings. The {\i mishnah} must therefore be speaking about the supported case and still teaching she keeps her earnings \u8212- which would be the {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hadafah Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava bar Ula offers a way to preserve the {\i gemara}\u8217's resistance. Even granting that the {\i mishnah} concerns a supported daughter, perhaps she does not keep {\i all} her earnings. Perhaps she only keeps the {\i hadafah} \u8212- the additional earnings beyond the cost of her upkeep. On this reading: if her weekly food and lodging costs one hundred units and she earns one hundred and fifty, she keeps only fifty, using the first hundred to repay the estate for her support. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's implication that she keeps earnings after the father\u8217's death would then prove only entitlement to the {\i hadafah}, not to the full amount.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Earnings & Found Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Found Object Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef dismisses this proposal and argues that the {\i mishnah} itself contains a stronger proof \u8212- one that cannot be deflected by the {\i hadafah} reading. The {\i mishnah} packages earnings together with {\i metziatah} \u8212- found objects. When the {\i mishnah} says that earnings not yet collected ({\i af al pi she-lo gavtah}) become debts to the father that pass to the brothers upon his death, it makes the same statement about found objects not yet collected. But this raises an obvious difficulty: {\i metziatah me-man gavya?} How does one \u8220"collect\u8221" a found object? A found object, the moment it is acquired, is in the finder\u8217's possession. There is no prior stage at which it is \u8220"owed\u8221" to her and then collected later. The formulation makes no sense as applied to found objects in isolation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earnings=Found Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i ella lav hachi ka\u8217'amar \u8212- maaseh yadeha ke-metziatah} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} is not making two parallel statements; it is equating earnings to found objects. Just as found objects: {\i mah metziatah be-chayei av le-av, le-achar mitat av le-atzmah} \u8212- found objects during the father\u8217's lifetime go to the father, but after the father\u8217's death they go to her \u8212- so too earnings: during the father\u8217's lifetime they go to him, but after his death they go to her. And crucially, when it comes to found objects, everyone agrees she keeps them entirely, not merely the {\i hadafah}. By packaging earnings together with found objects, the {\i mishnah} teaches that she keeps her earnings entirely as well, even when supported by the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is Rav Yosef\u8217's decisive argument: the {\i hadafah} theory cannot survive the equation with found objects, because no one ever suggested the daughter owes the estate for found objects found after the father\u8217's death. The full amount is hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Volitional Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is it taken for granted that she keeps found objects after the father\u8217's death? Rashi explains: {\i de-metziatah peshita lan de-le-atzmah} \u8212- even during the father\u8217's lifetime, the father\u8217's entitlement to his daughter\u8217's found objects was contingent on his actually supporting her \u8212- {\i semucha al shulchano}, eating at his table. The {\i gemara} in Bava Metzia establishes this. And why was it instituted that the father gets her found objects in the first place? Because of {\i eivah} \u8212- resentment. Since the father is not {\i legally} obligated to support his daughter yet is doing so volitionally, we worried that he would stop feeding her if she pocketed everything she found. So we gave him the found objects as an incentive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mandatory Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the father is dead, however, the entire rationale evaporates. The estate is {\i obligated} to support the daughter \u8212- it is a condition of the {\i ketubah}, not a voluntary act. There is no volitional benevolence to protect and no {\i eivah} to worry about. Therefore she unambiguously keeps her found objects. And since the {\i mishnah} equates earnings to found objects, the conclusion extends equally: she keeps all her earnings after the father\u8217's death, even the portion that would cover the cost of her upkeep.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brothers Don\u8217't Inherit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} reinforces this conclusion with a direct {\i amoraic} statement. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: {\i bat ha-nizonet min ha-achin maaseh yadeha le-atzmah} \u8212- a daughter supported by the brothers keeps her earnings. The brothers do not inherit the father\u8217's entitlement to her {\i maaseh yadayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scripture Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana asks: what is the reason? The {\i gemara} cites the verse in the context of non-Jewish slaves: {\i ve-hitnachaltem otam li-vneichem achareichem} \u8212- \u8220"you shall bequeath them to your sons after you.\u8221" {\i Otam li-vneichem} \u8212- your slaves you bequeath to your sons; {\i ve-lo benoteichem li-vneichem} \u8212- but not your daughters to your sons. The verse teaches that the paternal rights a father held over his daughter\u8217's labor and earnings are not heritable. Just as one bequeaths non-Jewish slaves to one\u8217's children \u8212- because the master\u8217's rights over a slave are a transferable ownership \u8212- by contrast, the father\u8217's rights in his daughter are not of that nature and do not pass to the sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba objects: perhaps the verse is speaking only about extraordinary payments \u8212- {\i fitzuyei ha-bat, kinsatah ve-chavalatah} \u8212- the damages, fines, and injury payments the father would collect on his daughter\u8217's behalf. These are by their nature more intrinsically the daughter\u8217's, since they compensate for wrongs done to her person, and they are less common. Perhaps it is specifically these that do not pass to the sons, while ordinary {\i maaseh yadayim} \u8212- regular earnings \u8212- does pass, since the father\u8217's biblical entitlement to earnings was more like a property right that should be inheritable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina indeed reads the verse this way: {\i be-fitzuyei ha-bat u-knasatah ve-chavalatah ha-katuv medaber} \u8212- the verse is specifically speaking about injury, seduction, and fine payments, not ordinary earnings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Injury Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} questions this reading by noting that {\i chavalos} \u8212- injuries \u8212- present their own complication. Even during the father\u8217's lifetime, injury payments ({\i tza\u8217'ara de-gufa}) are not simply pocketed by the father; they are held in a form of escrow for the daughter, since it is her body that was harmed. So the verse cannot be referring to injury payments as something the father would have claimed and now cannot bequeath, since he never had full claim to them in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Chanina resolves this: {\i she-patza\u8217'ah be-faneha} \u8212- the reference is to a blow to her face, a non-permanent injury. The distinction between permanent and non-permanent injury is what determines who collects. For a permanent injury \u8212- something that will diminish her for life, well beyond her period as a {\i ketanah} and {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- the payment is for the daughter, not the father, because it is fundamentally her loss. For a non-permanent injury, or one that affects only the period during which the father could have sold her or married her off, the father absorbs the loss and collects the payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot develops this logic further: an injury that heals before the daughter reaches adulthood \u8212- before she becomes a {\i bogeret} \u8212- is the father\u8217's loss, because it diminished the value of his asset during the period he controlled it. An injury that persists beyond that point is her loss, and she collects accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i Rabbi Shimon} and the {\i rabbanan} as to what the verse excludes \u8212- whether regular earnings or only fines and injury payments \u8212- thus remains analytically live. But the {\i gemara} has already demonstrated from the {\i mishnah} and from the Rav Yehuda/Rav tradition that ordinary earnings also remain hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with competing traditions. Ami bar Maimra told Rav Ashi that in Nehardea the tradition was {\i hilchesa ke-vateih de-Rav Sheshet} \u8212- we rule like Rav Sheshet, meaning the daughter\u8217's earnings go to the estate. Rav Ashi himself ruled {\i hilchesa ke-vateih de-Rav} \u8212- we rule like Rav: the daughter keeps her earnings; the brothers do not inherit the father\u8217's rights over them. And this is the accepted {\i halacha}: {\i ve-hilchesa ke-vateih de-Rav}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Rights (Betr (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eirusin Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to the question of {\i ketubah} collection rights when a father betrothes his daughter and the marriage subsequently dissolves. The relevant legal background is that after {\i eirusin} but before {\i nissu\u8217'in}, the daughter remains under her father\u8217's authority. If the {\i eirusin}-stage marriage ends \u8212- whether through divorce or the death of the husband \u8212- she reverts entirely to paternal control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i ham\u8217'ares et bito ve-gerashah} \u8212- the father betrothes his daughter and she is then divorced. The question is who collects the {\i ketubah}. Since she never reached {\i nissu\u8217'in} and therefore never left her father\u8217's control, the {\i ketubah} collection right belongs to the father. This ruling assumes, incidentally, that the {\i ketubah} obligation attaches already at {\i eirusin}, a position that is itself debated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eirusin Widowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same result obtains in the parallel case: {\i iresah ve-nit\u8217'almanah} \u8212- the father betrothed her and the husband died. Since she was still an {\i arusah} and had not yet undergone {\i nissu\u8217'in}, she remained under the father\u8217's authority. Therefore the father again collects the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nissu\u8217'in Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario shifts dramatically once {\i nissu\u8217'in} takes place. {\i Hisi\u8217'ah ve-gerashah} \u8212- the marriage was consummated and she was then divorced; {\i hisi\u8217'ah ve-nit\u8217'almanah} \u8212- consummated and she became widowed. In both cases: {\i kesuvatah shelah} \u8212- she collects her own {\i ketubah}. Indeed she may have a {\i ketubah} from two marriages, and she collects both. Once {\i nissu\u8217'in} occurs, the father loses all rights over her person and her monetary entitlements. She is in her own domain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Rights (Betr (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} introduces a refinement: {\i harishonah shel av} \u8212- even after {\i nissu\u8217'in}, the {\i ketubah} from the first marriage, the one the father initiated through {\i kidushei ketanah}, belongs to the father. His reasoning appears to be that since the father originated the {\i eirusin}, the financial obligations that arose from that {\i eirusin} remain directed to him even after the {\i nissu\u8217'in} converts her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Rejects Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} reject {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position categorically: {\i mi-she-hisi\u8217'ah ein av reshut bah} \u8212- once {\i nissu\u8217'in} has taken place, the father has no authority over her whatsoever, and any claim to her {\i ketubah} that might have existed before is extinguished. {\i Nissu\u8217'in} functions as a total legal break, severing all paternal rights regardless of whether the father originally arranged the match.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deaths vs.\u160 ?Divorces\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Deaths Avoidance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding to the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rulings, the {\i gemara} pauses to note a structural choice in how the {\i mishnah} composed its cases. The {\i mishnah} gives one scenario of divorce ({\i gerushah}) and one of widowhood ({\i almanah}) to illustrate a progression of two marriages. One might ask: why not give both scenarios as deaths \u8212- why not say she was widowed twice? Why the asymmetry of one divorce and one death?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Deaths Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i aval nit\u8217'almanah trei zimnei lo chazya le-inasba} \u8212- if two husbands had died on her, she would no longer be fit to remarry. The {\i gemara} notes that there is an unresolved debate about a woman whose husbands keep dying: at what point does a pattern of deaths create a {\i chazakah} \u8212- a legal presumption \u8212- that she is in some way the cause, such that a third husband should not take her? Is it after two deaths, or after three?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Two-Time Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes {\i agav orcha} \u8212- incidentally, in passing \u8212- {\i kasavar tana ke-Rabbi} \u8212- our {\i mishnah} is following the position of {\i Rabbi} (Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi). {\i Rabbi} holds {\i de-amar be-trei zimnei havei chazakah} \u8212- a presumption is established after two occurrences. Therefore, once two husbands have died on a woman, the presumption already applies and she may not remarry. By constructing its case as one divorce and one death rather than two deaths, the {\i mishnah} sidesteps this concern entirely, implicitly aligning itself with {\i Rabbi}\u8217's two-occurrence rule. We will continue from here in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 44\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketuva Collection Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 43b, approximately four lines after the new {\i gemara} begins. The {\i mishna} addresses the question of who collects a woman\u8217's {\i ketuva} depending on the stage of marriage at which she is divorced. If she is divorced from {\i erusin}, the father collects, because the daughter remains under his legal authority ({\i reshut av}) throughout {\i erusin}, and he retains the right to remarry her after such a divorce. If the divorce follows {\i nisuin}, she herself collects \u8212- with one important exception articulated by Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} directs its attention to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position: {\i harishona shel av} \u8212- even the payment from the first {\i nisuin} goes to the father, provided the father arranged the {\i erusin} when his daughter was a {\i ketana}. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- what is his reasoning?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba & Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba and Rav Yosef both explain: {\i hoil u-mishe\u8217'as erusin zachah bahen ha-av} \u8212- since the {\i ketuva} obligations commenced at the time of {\i erusin}, and at that moment the father would have been the payee had a divorce occurred, those obligations remain payable to the father even after {\i nisuin}. The argument is straightforward: the {\i ketuva} debt originated at a time when the father was the entitled party, and therefore he remains so even once {\i nisuin} takes place. It is a reasonably coherent position \u8212- yet the {\i gemara} will challenge it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bogeret Between Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a difficulty from the following {\i braita}: Rabbi Yehuda himself concedes that if the father arranged {\i erusin} when the daughter was a {\i ketana}, but she became a {\i bogeret} before {\i nisuin}, and then the {\i nisuin} took place \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ein l\u8217'aviha reshut bah} \u8212- the father no longer has any authority over her, and therefore the {\i ketuva} from this {\i nisuin} is not payable to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge is sharp. If Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's reasoning is that {\i erusin} established the father as the payee and that status persists, then even her becoming a {\i bogeret} between {\i erusin} and {\i nisuin} should make no difference \u8212- he was the payee, and nothing should change that. Why, then, does Rabbi Yehuda concede this case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To sharpen the point further: imagine the {\i erusin} occurs at a certain time, establishing the {\i ketuva} obligation with the father as payee. The daughter subsequently becomes a {\i bogeret}, exits her father\u8217's authority, and then {\i nisuin} takes place. According to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's stated logic \u8212- that the obligation began under the father\u8217's authority and therefore remains his \u8212- her transition to {\i bogeret} status ought to be irrelevant. The original payee was the father; that should not change. Yet Rabbi Yehuda concedes it does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unwritten vs Written\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution turns on a crucial distinction between the unwritten and written dimensions of the {\i ketuva} obligation. The obligation to pay a {\i ketuva} exists even before any document is written \u8212- it is a {\i takanas chachamim}, a Rabbinic enactment that takes effect from {\i erusin}. However, just before the {\i chuppah}, an actual {\i ketuva} document is written. This writing is not merely a record of the pre-existing obligation; it {\i redefines} and recreates the obligation. The written {\i ketuva} generates new obligations distinct from the earlier unwritten ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Formalist Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this distinction in hand, Rabba and Rav Yosef restate Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position more precisely: {\i hoil u-vireshuso nichtavin} \u8212- the determining factor is not when the {\i erusin} occurred, but under whose authority the {\i ketuva} is written. If the {\i ketuva} is written while she is still a {\i ketana} under her father\u8217's control \u8212- as typically happens just before the {\i chuppah}, at the {\i tish} \u8212- then those written obligations are created while she is in his {\i reshut}, and accordingly the father becomes the payee. In the scenario where she becomes a {\i bogeret} before {\i nisuin}, the {\i ketuva} is written after she has already exited her father\u8217's authority. At that moment she is her own person, and the newly created written obligations belong to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a notably formalist approach. Rabbi Yehuda is saying: we can only define the obligation in terms of who holds legal authority at the time the {\i ketuva} is written. Whatever the social purpose of the {\i ketuva} \u8212- and it is admittedly strange, since the {\i ketuva}\u8217's entire function is to protect her after divorce or widowhood, at a time when the father will no longer take her back \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda maps the payee onto the legal reality at the moment the document is created. If she is eleven years old and the {\i nisuin} occurs at a very young age, the {\i ketuva} gets paid to the father, even though the purpose of the {\i ketuva} is entirely oriented toward protecting her. It is a peculiar result, but Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's framework is a consistent one: the writing of the {\i ketuva} recreates and defines the obligation, and that definition is fixed by the legal authority in force at the moment of writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader implication, accepted even by Rabbi Yehuda, is that the writing of the {\i ketuva} before {\i nisuin} genuinely recreates the obligations rather than merely recording existing ones. This has direct consequences for the lien question the {\i gemara} is about to raise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Liens & Dating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Liens Exist\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses the question {\i u-migba mei-eimat gavya} \u8212- from what date does the {\i ketuva} carry liens against the husband\u8217's estate? The {\i mishna} assumes that {\i ketuva} obligations are in force already from {\i erusin}. If {\i erusin} occurred in 2010 and {\i nisuin} \u8212- with the writing of the {\i ketuva} \u8212- occurred in 2015, and she comes to collect in 2030, against which properties does she have lien rights? Does her lien reach back to 2010 and encompass property the husband sold between 2010 and 2015, or does it begin only from 2015, when the {\i ketuva} was written?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question is sharpened by the preceding discussion: if the writing of the {\i ketuva} at {\i nisuin} recreates and redefines the obligations, one might argue that the liens are equally reset \u8212- the old liens dissolved and new ones created at the time of writing. On the other hand, one could argue that the core obligation, which existed from {\i erusin} as an unwritten {\i takanas chachamim}, carries liens that were never interrupted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna takes a middle position. He distinguishes between the {\i ikar ketuva} \u8212- the core {\i ketuva} of 200 {\i zuz} (or the standard amount), which is obligated even without being written \u8212- and the {\i tosefet}, the additional amount the husband voluntarily adds at the time of writing. The {\i ikar ketuva} exists from {\i erusin}, and so its liens continue from {\i erusin} as well. The {\i tosefet}, however, is something that arises only at the time of the written {\i ketuva} and is associated specifically with {\i nisuin}; therefore its liens begin only from {\i nisuin}. Rav Huna in effect says: even though the writing recreates the obligations in some sense, the lien attached to the underlying debt that existed from {\i erusin} survives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi takes the more sweeping position: {\i echad zeh ve-echad zeh min ha-nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- both the {\i ikar ketuva} and the {\i tosefet} carry liens only from {\i nisuin}. Once we accept that the writing of the {\i ketuva} recreates the obligations, it follows naturally that all liens are reset and begin from the time of that writing. This is the more internally consistent view given the framework established earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Competing Ketubos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Documents Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks whether Rav Huna\u8217's position is self-consistent, by confronting it with another ruling attributed to him. The case: a woman who divorces produces two {\i ketubot} \u8212- one dated 2015 for 200 {\i zuz}, and one dated 2017 for 300 {\i zuz}. Rav Huna rules that if she wishes to collect the 200, she may do so using the earlier date, and her liens extend back to 2015; if she wishes to collect the 300, she may do so, but her liens run only from 2017.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Addition Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this ruling. If Rav Huna is willing to split the {\i ketuva} obligations in the {\i erusin}/{\i nisuin} case \u8212- allowing the 200 {\i zuz} core to carry liens from {\i erusin} while the {\i tosefet} carries liens only from {\i nisuin} \u8212- why does he not apply the same logic here? The 300 {\i ketuva} should simply be read as the original 200 plus an additional 100. Under Rav Huna\u8217's own reasoning, the woman ought to be entitled to collect 200 from the earlier lien date and 100 from the later one. And pushing the logic further: why not even read it as a total of 500 \u8212- the full 200 from the first document and the full 300 from the second, each with its own lien date?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Language Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} provides its answer: {\i keivan d\u8217'lo kasav lah} \u8212- since he did not write in the second {\i ketuva} the explicit language, {\i z\u8217'visi v\u8217'osifis lach slas me\u8217'ah a-masayim} \u8212- \u8220"I have desired and I have added to you 300 upon the existing 200\u8221" \u8212- we cannot read the second document as an addition to the first. If he had intended to supplement the first, he had language available to say so clearly. Similarly, if the 300 were meant to represent the original 200 plus 100 more, he should have written: {\i osifis lach me\u8217'ah a-masayim} \u8212- \u8220"I am adding 100 to the 200.\u8221" He did not write either of these formulations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Competing Alternatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since no such language appears, the two documents are read not as cumulative or as B incorporating A, but as separate and competing alternatives: {\i i mi-zman rishon gavis, gavis masayim; i mi-zman sheni gavis, gavis slas me\u8217'ah} \u8212- if you choose to collect from the first date, you collect 200; if you choose to collect from the second date, you collect 300. They are alternatives. She picks one. We do not aggregate them, and we do not read the later one as reinterpreting or supplementing the earlier one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule that flows from this is that in the ordinary {\i ketuva} case \u8212- where the husband at {\i erusin} incurs an unwritten base obligation of 200, and then at {\i nisuin} writes a {\i ketuva} that explicitly adds a {\i tosefet} on top of the core amount \u8212- the structure is different. There the {\i ketuva} document explicitly layers the {\i tosefet} onto the {\i ikar}, so that we do treat them as a layered obligation: the 200 carries liens from {\i erusin}, the {\i tosefet} from {\i nisuin}. But where no such explicit language exists, competing documents are read as alternatives, not as cumulative obligations. Furthermore, the {\i gemara} notes \u8212- {\i acholi achlei l\u8217'shibuda kama} \u8212- if she chooses to use the later document, she has implicitly waived her earlier liens. The second {\i ketuva} represents an entirely new obligation of 300, not the first obligation reconstituted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Supersession Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} introduces a broader principle through Rav Nachman. The case now shifts from two {\i ketubot} to two {\i shtarot} \u8212- documents of title transfer on the same piece of property. If Michael holds one document stating that a co-op was sold to him in 2012, and a second document stating the same co-op was sold to him in 2015, when did the sale occur? And more practically: from what date do Michael\u8217's liens on the seller\u8217's estate begin?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the date matters is this: when a seller transfers property, he implicitly undertakes to reimburse the buyer if any creditor of the seller later takes the property away from the buyer by virtue of a pre-existing lien. The buyer therefore acquires lien rights on the seller\u8217's estate from the date of purchase \u8212- so that if a creditor seizes the property, the buyer can collect from the seller\u8217's remaining assets. An earlier date means broader access to those assets, because it extends the buyer\u8217's lien further back in time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman rules: {\i bitel sheni es ha-rishon} \u8212- the second document supersedes the first. The buyer, by accepting the second document, has implicitly forfeited the earlier lien. His liens run only from 2015. The logic is that since the second document provides no additional benefit over the first \u8212- it is the same property, at a later date \u8212- the only thing the buyer could have been signaling by accepting it is a willingness to treat the first document as though it never happened. It is a worse deal at a later date with no compensating advantage. Accepting it therefore functions as a waiver of the rights established by the earlier document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa\u8217's Caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa qualifies Rav Nachman\u8217's rule: {\i u-modei Rav Nachman d\u8217'i hosif bah dikla l\u8217'tosefes kasveih} \u8212- Rav Nachman would concede that if the second document includes something additional \u8212- the classic example is a palm tree added to the property transferred \u8212- then supersession does not apply. When the later document conveys something the earlier one did not, the buyer is not simply accepting a worse version of the first; he has a genuine tradeoff. The later document carries a later lien date but confers an additional benefit. In that case, the two documents stand as competing alternatives, and the buyer may choose which to invoke.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sale vs Gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} extends the analysis: {\i pshita} \u8212- it is obvious \u8212- that if the first document framed the transfer as a sale and the second as a gift ({\i rishon be-mecher ve-sheni be-matanah}), the later document does provide a benefit, and choice is available. The benefit derives from {\i dina de-bar metzra} \u8212- the neighbor\u8217's right of first refusal. When property is sold, the neighboring owner has a halachic claim to be offered the property before a stranger is approached; but when property is given as a gift, no such claim applies. A person may give a gift to whomever he chooses. So if the second document frames the transfer as a gift, Michael acquires protection against the neighbor\u8217's claim under {\i dina de-bar metzra} \u8212- a distinct advantage not available under the first document. The two documents therefore offer different benefits, and Michael may choose between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 (It is worth noting that in practice, the {\i dina de-bar metzra} has indeed been applied halachically in modern times: the Heschel School board, when selling its 89th Street building, offered B\u8217'nai Jeshurun on 88th Street \u8212- a former owner of the building and its neighbor \u8212- the right of first refusal, and B\u8217'nai Jeshurun ultimately acquired the property.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds {\i ve-chol she-ken} \u8212- certainly the reverse also holds: if the first document was a gift and the second was a sale ({\i rishon be-matanah ve-sheni be-mecher}), the sale document is also the more beneficial in its own way, because a buyer who paid money can claim reimbursement if a creditor seizes the property, whereas a recipient of a gift has no such recourse. In each permutation, the later document carries a distinct advantage, and so Michael retains the right to choose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if the property was already sold in 2012, how can a 2015 document frame it as a gift? The answer is that this is an acknowledged legal fiction, permissible because both parties consent. In theory, Michael could have returned the property to the seller and the seller could then have given it as a gift. Since both parties agree to the framing, halacha allows it to be treated as such. It is, as acknowledged, a somewhat extraordinary construction, but a permissible one within the framework of {\i mamon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where the second document adds no benefit \u8212- both documents are, say, bills of sale for the same property on the same terms \u8212- supersession applies in full: {\i bitel sheni es ha-rishon}. Rav Pappa explains the mechanism: {\i eimar oduye odi leih} \u8212- Rav Pappa attributes this to what functions as an implicit admission ({\i hoda\u8217'as ba\u8217'al din}) on Michael\u8217's part. By accepting the 2015 document, he is treated as admitting that the 2012 sale never really took effect \u8212- that the true date of transfer was 2015. The legal fiction of {\i hoda\u8217'as ba\u8217'al din k\u8217'me\u8217'ah edim dami} \u8212- an admission by a party in interest carries the weight of a hundred witnesses \u8212- creates this result even if it is factually inaccurate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha offers a narrower reading: Michael need not be read as denying that the 2012 transaction occurred. Rather, he has simply {\i waived his liens} \u8212- {\i acholi achlei l\u8217'shibudeh} \u8212- from the earlier date. The 2012 sale happened; he merely gives up the lien rights that attached from that date.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference between these two positions \u8212- {\i nafka mina} \u8212- is substantial. First, concerning witnesses: if we accept that Michael is effectively denying the 2012 event ever occurred, then he is implicitly treating those witnesses as false. As {\i Tosafot} explains, this would not impugn those witnesses in any general sense, but it would affect Michael\u8217's own rights under any other document he holds bearing the same witnesses\u8217' signatures \u8212- since he cannot selectively invalidate their testimony only when convenient. Second, concerning the fruits of the property ({\i lishlumei peiri}): if Michael is merely waiving liens, he retains the benefit of whatever he derived from the property during those three years \u8212- he owned it, he ate its fruit. If however the entire 2012 transaction is treated as a fiction that never happened, the seller could demand back-payment for three years of use. Third, regarding taxes ({\i arnona}): whoever is deemed to have owned the property during those years bears the tax liability for that period. Under the waiver reading, Michael owned it and owes the taxes; under the denial reading, the seller is deemed to have owned it and the tax falls on him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obviously more proportionate and factually honest reading is the narrower one \u8212- that Michael waives his earlier liens. To treat his acceptance of a later-dated document as a denial of the very witnesses who signed the earlier one, with cascading consequences for all his other documents bearing those signatures, is a remarkably far-reaching implication of what appears to be a relatively modest act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketuva Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary & Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the original {\i ketuva} lien question and reaches its conclusion. {\i Tosafot} address the issue at this point. The position of Rav Huna \u8212- splitting between the {\i ikar ketuva} with liens from {\i erusin} and the {\i tosefet} with liens from {\i nisuin} \u8212- is contrasted with the position of the {\i chachamim}. The {\i chachamim} rule: {\i echad zeh ve-echad zeh min ha-nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- both the core {\i ketuva} and the {\i tosefet} carry liens only from {\i nisuin}. And the {\i halacha} follows the {\i chachamim}: when the {\i ketuva} is written at the time of {\i nisuin}, the old liens from {\i erusin} do not survive, and all lien rights are anchored to the moment of the {\i ketuva}\u8217's writing. Whatever obligations existed at the time of {\i erusin} do not continue to generate liens into the period following {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Motzi Shem Ra Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Case Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i ketuva} discussion concluded, the {\i gemara} pivots to the next {\i mishna}, which opens a new subject \u8212- {\i motzi shem ra}. It is worth appreciating how these topics connect. The {\i ketuva} as a Rabbinic institution traces its origins to the Torah\u8217's cases involving young girls and marriage-related payments. The {\i ones} and {\i mefateh} cases established the 50 {\i shekel} fine \u8212- itself rooted in the societal reality of {\i mohar}, the bride-price \u8212- and that fine became the template for the {\i ketuva}\u8217's base amount. Now the {\i gemara} turns to the second Torah case in this constellation: the {\i motzi shem ra}, where after the wedding night a husband falsely claims his wife was not a virgin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah\u8217's framework for {\i motzi shem ra} is as follows. If a husband makes this claim and it proves true \u8212- if she in fact committed adultery as a {\i na\u8217'arah betulah me\u8217'orasah} \u8212- she is executed by {\i sekilah} at {\i petach beit aviha}, the entrance of her father\u8217's house. The Torah specifies: {\i ki astah nevalah b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- because she committed an outrage in Israel. The location \u8212- the father\u8217's house \u8212- and the mode of execution \u8212- {\i sekilah} rather than the {\i chenek} that normally applies to adultery \u8212- are the distinctive features of this case. If, on the other hand, the husband lied, he receives {\i malkus} and a fine of {\i me\u8217'ah kesef} \u8212- 100 silver pieces, double the standard {\i ketuva} amount of 50 \u8212- which is given {\i l\u8217'avi ha-na\u8217'arah}, to the girl\u8217's father. This doubling is no accident: he attempted to have her killed and to deprive her of her {\i ketuva} (equivalent to 50 {\i kesef}), and so he pays double. He also loses the right ever to divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giyoret Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather than beginning with the standard case, the {\i mishna} opens with an unusual exception: {\i hagiores she-nisgayra bita ima} \u8212- a convert woman who converted together with her daughter, whose daughter then committed adultery as a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceived Pre-Conv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first scenario: the daughter was conceived before the mother converted and was also born before the conversion. In this case none of the special laws of {\i motzi shem ra} apply to her at all. She did not begin life as a {\i bat Yisrael}; the Torah\u8217's formulation {\i ki astah nevalah b\u8217'Yisrael} excludes her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Born Post-Conv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario: the daughter was conceived before the mother converted, but was born after the conversion \u8212- {\i horasa shelo bi-kedusha ve-lidasa bi-kedusha}. In this case she is executed by {\i sekilah} \u8212- because she was born as a Jew, and {\i b\u8217'Yisrael} is satisfied \u8212- but {\i ein lah petach beit av}, there is no father\u8217's house to bring her to, and {\i ein lah me\u8217'ah sela}, there is no fine of one hundred {\i sela}. She has no legal father, so those elements of the {\i motzi shem ra} framework simply do not apply. She receives {\i sekilah}, but without the full ritual specified in the Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third scenario: both conception and birth occurred after the mother\u8217's conversion. In this case {\i harei hi ke-vat Yisrael le-chol devareha} \u8212- she is treated in every respect as a daughter of Israel, and all the normal laws apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Deceased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth case introduced by the {\i mishna}: a daughter who does have a legal father, but whose father has died \u8212- she is an orphan. The {\i mishna} states {\i harei zeh bi-sekilah} \u8212- she receives {\i sekilah}. The {\i mishna} notes that {\i lo ne\u8217'emar petach beit av ela l\u8217'mitzvah} \u8212- the requirement of the father\u8217's house is only a {\i mitzvah} (an ideal, not an absolute requirement), and so its absence does not block the execution. What the {\i mishna} does not clarify, however, is whether the {\i me\u8217'ah kesef} fine applies in the case of an orphan. That question is left open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah\u8217's Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the Torah\u8217's law as it applies to the standard case: the subject is a {\i na\u8217'arah betulah me\u8217'orasah l\u8217'ish} \u8212- a young woman, a virgin, betrothed to a man. He claims {\i lo nimtze\u8217'u vetulim la-na\u8217'arah} \u8212- signs of virginity were not found in her. If the claim is true and she committed adultery during her {\i erusin}, {\i ve-hotzi\u8217'u et ha-na\u8217'arah el petach beit aviha u-sekaluha} \u8212- she is brought to the entrance of her father\u8217's house and stoned. The punishment is {\i sekilah} rather than {\i chenek} because the Torah treats this as a more severe form of adultery \u8212- she was still under her father\u8217's roof, still in the period of {\i erusin}. If the husband lied \u8212- {\i moti shem ra} \u8212- he receives {\i malkus} and pays {\i me\u8217'ah kesef}, double the {\i ketuva} value of fifty {\i kesef}, given to the girl\u8217's father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fine of {\i me\u8217'ah kesef} is explicitly structured as {\i kefel} \u8212- a doubling. He was trying both to kill her and to cause her to lose her {\i ketuva} entitlement of fifty {\i kesef}; therefore he pays double that amount in fine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphan\u8217's Fine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} has told us that an orphan girl receives {\i sekilah} but has left us without a ruling on whether the {\i me\u8217'ah kesef} fine applies in her case. Since the Torah says the fine is given {\i l\u8217'avi ha-na\u8217'arah} \u8212- to the girl\u8217's father \u8212- and she has no father, it is unclear whether the fine exists at all, and if so to whom it would be paid. This open question \u8212- whether an orphaned daughter receives the {\i me\u8217'ah kesef} \u8212- is what the {\i gemara} will take up in the following discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 44b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Motzi Shem Ra Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overview & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} we now enter is that of {\i motzi shem ra} \u8212- the case in which a husband rises the morning after his wedding and claims his wife was not a virgin. This topic is one of two places in the Torah where the framework of {\i na\u8217'arah betulah} and virginity converges, and it connects directly to the very first {\i mishnah} of the tractate. The institution of the {\i ketubah} itself is rooted in this passage: if the husband is proven a liar, he pays one hundred {\i kessef}, which is double the fifty {\i kessef} of the {\i mohar} \u8212- the bride-price that serves as the basis for the Rabbinic institution of the {\i ketubah}. (To be precise, fifty {\i kessef} equals two hundred {\i zuz}, so one hundred {\i kessef} equals four hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- the relationship is one of {\i keifel}, doubling.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} addressed several cases that bear on the application of these laws. One case involves a woman who converted \u8212- touching on the phrase {\i be-Yisrael} in the verse, since such a woman was not originally {\i Yisrael}. Another involves a woman whose mother converted while pregnant, meaning she was conceived before her mother\u8217's conversion and born after. This woman has no legal father in the relevant sense, implicating the phrase {\i beis aviha}. These cases raise the question of how much the special {\i halachos} of {\i na\u8217'arah betulah} depend on the full framing of the Torah\u8217's language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Halachos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The special {\i halachos} of {\i na\u8217'arah betulah me\u8217'orasah} are these: if she committed adultery, she receives {\i sekilah} rather than the ordinary {\i chenek} that applies to adultery in general; and if the husband is lying and spreading a false report, he pays one hundred {\i kessef} and receives {\i malkus}. The operative question throughout this {\i sugya} is which of these consequences depends on the full configuration of {\i na\u8217'arah betulah me\u8217'orasah}, and how much the surrounding phrases \u8212- {\i navala be-Yisrael}, {\i beis aviha} \u8212- affect each of these {\i dinim} individually.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sekilah Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with the question {\i mina hani mili} \u8212- from where are all these laws derived? Reish Lakish answers: from the word {\i u-mesah} in the verse. The verse states {\i ve-sakluha kol anshei ira ba-avanim va-mesah} \u8212- they shall stone her and she shall die. Since stoning obviously results in death, the word {\i u-mesah} is redundant and therefore available for a {\i derasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conception Non-Jewish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish uses this extra word {\i u-mesah} to include the case of {\i horasah she-lo bi-kdusha ve-liddasah bi-kdusha} \u8212- a woman conceived when her mother was not yet Jewish but born after her mother converted. To review the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling: if a woman converted as an infant, she is entirely outside this {\i parshah} and receives {\i chenek} rather than {\i sekilah}. But if her mother converted while pregnant with her, the {\i mishnah} says she does receive {\i sekilah} despite having no legal father. The source for retaining {\i sekilah} in this intermediate case is the extra {\i u-mesah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limitation to Meisa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges: {\i i hachi}, once we include this woman via {\i u-mesah}, why not include her for all the {\i halachos} \u8212- meaning, why shouldn\u8217't the husband also pay the hundred {\i kessef} and receive {\i malkus} if he is lying? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i le-misa israbsa ve-lo le-knas} \u8212- she was included only for the {\i din} of {\i misah}, not for the fine. The word {\i u-mesah} is the mechanism of inclusion, and it extends only to the punishment of death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: perhaps {\i u-mesah} is not including this intermediate case at all, but rather is simply including the ordinary case of a woman born after her mother\u8217's conversion \u8212- {\i horasah ve-liddasah bi-kdusha}? The {\i gemara} dismisses this: {\i hi Yisrael me-ikara hi} \u8212- such a woman is fully Jewish from the outset and requires no special inclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusion Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further possibility is raised: perhaps {\i u-mesah} includes even a woman who converted as an infant \u8212- {\i horasah ve-liddasah she-lo bi-kdusha}? The {\i gemara} rejects this as well: {\i be-Yisrael mai ahni lei} \u8212- if the extra {\i u-mesah} is doing that work, what purpose does the word {\i be-Yisrael} serve? Obviously {\i be-Yisrael} is not simply excluding non-Jews \u8212- that is self-evident. So {\i be-Yisrael} must be doing something, namely excluding someone who is in some sense not fully rooted in Jewish status from birth. That exclusion reaches the {\i halachos} of the hundred {\i kessef} and {\i malkus}, removing the husband from those obligations. Meanwhile, the extra {\i u-mesah} overrides that exclusion specifically with regard to {\i sekilah}, retaining her in the {\i parshah} for that punishment alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yesomah & Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yose on Yesomah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the case of the woman conceived as a non-Jew, the {\i gemara} turns to a second case \u8212- the {\i yesomah}, a woman whose father is dead. Rav Yose bar Chanina teaches: {\i hamotzi shem ra al ha-yesomah patur} \u8212- one who spreads an evil report against a fatherless woman is exempt from the special punishments. The {\i mishnah} had already indicated that such a woman still receives {\i sekilah} \u8212- that was not considered a great {\i chiddush}, since even the woman conceived as a non-Jew retained {\i sekilah}. What the {\i mishnah} did not address explicitly is whether the husband\u8217's obligations \u8212- the hundred {\i kessef} and {\i malkus} \u8212- still apply when there is no living father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yose bar Chanina\u8217's source is the verse {\i venatenu le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah} \u8212- the payment is given to the father of the {\i na\u8217'arah}. {\i Prat lezo she-ein lah av} \u8212- this excludes the case where she has no father. The payment of the hundred {\i kessef} is directed to the father, and without a father, the obligation does not attach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge from Mefateh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef bar Avin \u8212- some say Rav Yosef bar Zvida \u8212- raises a challenge from a {\i braisa} discussing the case of {\i mefateh}, seduction. The verse there uses the double formulation {\i ma\u8217'en yema\u8217'en aviha}, which Rabbi Yose HaGlili interprets as including the {\i yesomah} \u8212- even if there is no father, the {\i knas} is still paid in a case of seduction. If the {\i knas} applies by {\i mefateh} without a father, why should it not apply equally in {\i motzi shem ra}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligation Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the challenge and its resolution come from the same voice: {\i hu mosev lah ve-hu mifarek lah}. The distinction is {\i be-vallah ve-achar kach nis\u8217'asama} \u8212- the {\i braisa} about {\i mefateh} is discussing a case where the father was alive at the time of the act and died afterward. In that case the obligation had already crystallized, and it remains payable even after the father\u8217's death \u8212- to her, or to the brothers, as discussed elsewhere. But if there was no father at the time of the original act, the obligation never came into existence in the first place, since the {\i knas} is by its nature payable to the father. This reasoning applies equally to {\i ones}, {\i mefateh}, and {\i motzi shem ra}: if there was a father at the time of the act, the obligation exists and survives his subsequent death; if there was never a father, there is no obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, disagrees and rules {\i chayav} \u8212- the fine does apply even when there is no living father. Rava\u8217's position extends not only to {\i motzi shem ra} but presumably to {\i mefateh} and {\i ones} as well. His reasoning is drawn from the teaching of Ami.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betulat Yisrael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ami teaches: {\i betulat Yisrael ve-lo betulat gerim} \u8212- the verse {\i ki motzi shem ra al betulat Yisrael} specifies a {\i betulat Yisrael}, excluding the convert. This exclusion of converts is meaningful only against a baseline in which the ordinary case \u8212- a naturally born Jewish woman whose father happens to have died \u8212- still obligates the {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} makes this explicit: {\i i amarta bishlama ki hai gavna be-Yisrael mechayev} \u8212- if in the standard case of a Jewish woman with no living father the {\i knas} still applies, then the exclusion of {\i betulat gerim} is meaningful, because it tells us that the woman who came through conversion is a special exclusion beyond the mere absence of a father. But {\i ela i amarta be-Yisrael ki hai gavna patur} \u8212- if a natural-born Jewish {\i yesomah} were also exempt, then the exclusion of the convert would be entirely superfluous, since the convert is obviously in a more attenuated position than a Jewish {\i yesomah}. The force of the exclusion {\i betulat gerim} therefore proves that the ordinary {\i yesomah} who is naturally born Jewish remains fully obligated in the {\i knas}. The payment in that case would be made to her directly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Motzi Shem Ra & Ketanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish on Ketanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now shifts from the question of {\i yesomah} to the question of age. Reish Lakish teaches: {\i hamotzi shem ra al ha-ketanah patur} \u8212- one who spreads an evil report against a minor is exempt. While there was a debate between Rabbi Meir and the Sages regarding {\i ones u-mefateh} and whether a {\i ketanah} is included, Reish Lakish holds that in {\i motzi shem ra} everyone would agree the minor is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father-Based Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish\u8217's initial source is the verse {\i venatenu le-avi ha-na\u8217'arah}, where {\i na\u8217'arah} is written in full \u8212- {\i na\u8217'arah malei diber ha-katuv} \u8212- with the letter {\i hey}, indicating a full {\i na\u8217'arah}, which the {\i derasha} reads as specifying an adult. Beyond the textual point, there is also a compelling logical basis: the act of {\i motzi shem ra} against a minor is not nearly as consequential as against an adult, because even if the husband\u8217's claim were believed, the minor could never be executed as a result \u8212- since {\i pituyas ketanah ones} is the law, sexual activity with a minor is always treated as statutory rape. The minor would never be found guilty of voluntary adultery and thus could never receive the capital punishment this {\i parshah} contemplates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha bar Abba challenges Reish Lakish\u8217's derivation. He argues: the mere presence of the letter {\i hey} in {\i na\u8217'arah} is not sufficient to derive that we are discussing an adult. Without the {\i hey}, {\i ta\u8217'ima de-ketav beh} \u8212- without that {\i hey}, I would have thought even a minor is included. But, he continues, the Torah itself makes clear that an adult is meant from context: {\i ha-ketiv ve-im emet hayah ha-davar ha-zeh lo nimtzu betulim la-na\u8217'arah ve-hotzi\u8217'u et ha-na\u8217'arah petach beit aviha u-skaluha} \u8212- the text explicitly involves execution. Since a minor cannot be executed, the {\i parshah} is obviously discussing an adult. The extra {\i hey} therefore seems unnecessary \u8212- the context already implies adulthood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context & Execution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} accepts the force of this observation. {\i Ve-ketanah lav bat onshin hi} \u8212- a minor is not subject to capital punishment. Therefore: {\i ela kan na\u8217'arah} \u8212- the starting point is the explicit context of the {\i parshah}, which clearly addresses an adult. It is not the letter {\i hey} that teaches us we require an adult; rather, the entire scenario of execution makes it self-evident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Linguistic Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once we establish from context that this {\i parshah} discusses an adult, a general linguistic principle follows. {\i Ha-kol makom she-ne\u8217'emar na\u8217'arah} without the {\i hey} \u8212- written as {\i na\u8217'ar} \u8212- {\i afilu ketanah be-mashma} \u8212- even a minor is included. The derivation thus proceeds in stages: the context makes clear we are dealing with an adult, and once we know that, the presence or absence of the {\i hey} in other places becomes a systematic indicator of whether the reference is to an adult or potentially to a minor as well. By {\i motzi shem ra}, then, the woman must unambiguously be an adult {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- neither a {\i ketanah} nor, as will emerge, a {\i bogeret}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Cases Adultery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 1: After Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a {\i braisa} from Shila: {\i shalosh middot be-na\u8217'arah} \u8212- there are three scenarios involving the {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} who committed adultery. The first is the classical case of the Torah: {\i ba\u8217'u lah eidim be-veit chamiha she-zineta be-veit aviha} \u8212- witnesses come when she is already in her father-in-law\u8217's house, meaning after {\i nisuin}, and testify that she committed adultery while she was still in her father\u8217's house, meaning during {\i erusin}. This is the paradigmatic case of {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}, and one notes that {\i chazal} understand the Torah\u8217's apparently evidentiary procedure \u8212- producing the bedsheet \u8212- as in fact a shorthand for witnesses whose testimony is subsequently tested and either confirmed or refuted by {\i eidim zomemim}. In this case: {\i soklin osah al pesach beis aviha} \u8212- she is stoned at the doorway of her father\u8217's house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sekilah Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The symbolism here is explicit in the {\i braisa}: {\i re\u8217'u gidulim she-gidalten} \u8212- look at what you have raised. The stoning at the father\u8217's doorway is an act of casting shame back upon the household that produced her. The case differs from the second scenario, where the adultery is discovered before {\i nisuin}, in which case execution takes place at the city gate \u8212- {\i al pesach sha\u8217'ar ha-ir} \u8212- in keeping with the verse in the {\i parshah} of {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} that says {\i ve-hotzeisem et sheneihem el sha\u8217'ar ha-ir ha-hi}. Why is the location different? When the matter is discovered after {\i nisuin}, the husband and his family are the injured party in a specific and intimate way; the symbolism of shame cast back on the father\u8217's house fits that context. When it is discovered before {\i nisuin}, the act is equally severe but the dynamic of injury is not yet framed in the same way, so the punishment is public but without that directed element of shame. The two locations thus reflect not just the legal stage of the relationship but the distinct moral and symbolic messaging each scenario conveys.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 2: Becomes Bogerit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third scenario introduced by Shila is the most striking: {\i sarcha u-vasof bagra} \u8212- she committed adultery as a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}, but by the time the matter is discovered and adjudicated she has become a {\i bogeret}, an adult woman no longer in her father\u8217's domain. In this case, Shila rules: {\i tidon be-chenek} \u8212- she is judged by strangulation, not by {\i sekilah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shila\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable ruling. The sin was committed when she was a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} and was therefore deserving of {\i sekilah}. Yet Shila holds that the applicable punishment is determined not by the time of the sin but by the woman\u8217's current status at the time of judgment. Were she to commit the same act now as a {\i bogeret}, the punishment would be {\i chenek}; therefore, even though she deserved {\i sekilah} when she sinned, she now receives only {\i chenek}. The principle Shila articulates is: {\i kol heika de-ishtani gufa ishtani ktala} \u8212- when the body changes, the punishment changes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sin vs.\u160 ?Status Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a contradiction from another {\i braisa}. That {\i braisa} discusses a case where a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} committed adultery, and after she became a {\i bogeret} the husband spread the evil report \u8212- {\i u-mishe-bagra hotzi aleiha shem ra}. The {\i braisa} states: {\i hu eino lokeh ve-eino noten me\u8217'ah sela} \u8212- if he is lying, he does not receive {\i malkus} and does not pay the hundred {\i sela}, because the act of {\i motzi shem ra} occurred when she was already a {\i bogeret} and the category therefore does not apply to him. Up to this point, the ruling follows a logic consistent with Shila.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i braisa} continues: {\i hi ve-zomemeiha makdimim le-veit ha-sekilah} \u8212- she and the false witnesses go to be stoned. The {\i gemara} objects: {\i hi ve-zomemeiha salka da\u8217'atach} \u8212- it cannot be both! Either she did it and is stoned, or the witnesses lied and they are stoned; they cannot both be stoned. The {\i gemara} therefore emends: {\i ela o hi o zomemeiha makdimim le-veit ha-sekilah} \u8212- either she or the false witnesses are put to death by {\i sekilah}. The critical point follows: if it was true and she actually committed adultery as a {\i na\u8217'arah betulah}, even though it is now five years later and she is a {\i bogeret}, she receives {\i sekilah} \u8212- the punishment she deserved at the time of the sin. This is a direct contradiction of Shila, who said she should receive {\i chenek} based on her current status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} acknowledges this is apparently a {\i machloket tanna\u8217'im} and attempts to reconcile the sources before resorting to that conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava proposes a distinction: {\i motzi shem ra ka-amart} \u8212- are you speaking of the {\i motzi shem ra} scenario? {\i Shani motzi shem ra de-chidusho} \u8212- {\i motzi shem ra} is different because it is itself a {\i chiddush}. Normally, witnesses come and testify that a woman committed adultery; we judge her based on her current status. But the entire category of {\i motzi shem ra} is already exceptional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exceptional nature of {\i motzi shem ra} is illustrated by the following case. Consider a woman who entered the {\i chuppah} but the marriage was not yet consummated \u8212- {\i nichnesah le-chuppah ve-lo niv\u8217'alah}. She is technically a {\i nesu\u8217'ah}. If she were now to commit adultery, the punishment would be {\i chenek}, not {\i sekilah}, because the category of {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} no longer applies once she has entered the {\i chuppah}. Yet if this is the {\i motzi shem ra} scenario \u8212- if the husband makes his claim the morning after \u8212- and witnesses prove he is telling the truth, she receives {\i sekilah}. The fact that she is already a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} does not change the outcome; the {\i din} of {\i motzi shem ra} goes by the original act, not by her status at the moment of discovery. Rava thus argues: since {\i motzi shem ra} is already an exception in the case of the {\i nesu\u8217'ah}, one who holds Shila\u8217's position can say the same extends to the {\i bogeret} \u8212- {\i motzi shem ra} simply always follows the time of the original act, while for all other cases the principle of judging by current status remains intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this: why should {\i motzi shem ra} be treated as an exception to the current-status rule? The argument seems to use clear evidence against Shila\u8217's position as proof of an exception, rather than as a refutation. One who starts with Shila\u8217's assumption and then encounters the {\i nesu\u8217'ah} case could simply conclude that {\i motzi shem ra} is categorically different \u8212- it always reverts to the time of the original act \u8212- while other adultery cases continue to follow current status. But is this a principled distinction or merely a defensive maneuver?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Body Change Criterion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back through Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua. If Shila\u8217's rule is that when the {\i body changes} the punishment changes, then the {\i nesu\u8217'ah} case cannot establish {\i motzi shem ra} as an exception to that rule. Why? Because in the {\i nesu\u8217'ah} case the body has {\i not} changed \u8212- she is essentially the same person. Her status has shifted but her physical person has not. {\i Ki chidesh rachmana heicha de-lav ishtani gufa} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's novelty in that case operates where there is no significant change in the person. Therefore, one cannot infer from the {\i nesu\u8217'ah} case that {\i motzi shem ra} would similarly override the rule even when the body {\i has} changed, i.e., when she has become a {\i bogeret}. If Shila\u8217's premise is that a genuine change in the person demands current-status judgment, then the {\i nesu\u8217'ah} case does not dislodge that premise, and we are left with a genuine contradiction between Shila and the {\i braisa} that gives {\i sekilah} even when she is a {\i bogeret}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaitic Status Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priest Korban Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now invokes the solution of {\i machloket tanna\u8217'im}, supported by a {\i mishnah} that connects directly to this week\u8217's {\i parshah}. The {\i mishnah} in {\i Kodshim} discusses cases involving the {\i chatas} offering: a {\i nasi} brings a male goat and a {\i kohen gadol} brings a bull, whereas an ordinary {\i Yisrael} brings a female sheep or goat. The question is: if a person sinned before receiving a new appointment \u8212- as {\i nasi} or {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- and then was appointed before the obligation was discharged, by which standard is the obligation measured?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i chatu ad she-lo nitmanu ve-nitmanu harei hen ke-hedyotot} \u8212- if they sinned before their appointment and then were appointed, they bring the {\i korban} of a lay person, since the obligation was incurred at the time of the sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon vs.\u160 ?Tanna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees: {\i noda lahem ad she-lo nitmanu chayavim} \u8212- if they became aware of their sin before the appointment, they are obligated as lay persons. {\i Aval mishe-nitmanu pturim} \u8212- but if the awareness came only after they were appointed, they are exempt. Rabbi Shimon holds that there are two critical moments: the time of the sin and the time the obligation crystallizes (i.e., when the person becomes aware of the transgression). If those two moments straddle a change in status, the obligations do not align and the result is exemption. This parallels the debate about whether punishment follows the time of the sin or the time of execution: the {\i tanna kamma} holds that the time of sin is determinative throughout; Rabbi Shimon introduces the moment of crystallization as a second pivot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Difficulty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a difficulty. If we accept Rabbi Shimon\u8217's logic \u8212- that both the time of the sin and the time of the obligation are relevant, and a discrepancy between them produces exemption \u8212- then one would expect the same logic to apply to capital cases. If a woman deserves {\i sekilah} at the time of the sin but {\i chenek} at the time of execution, why should the result be {\i chenek} rather than nothing? Apples and oranges cannot be combined; by {\i korban} the result is exemption, so perhaps by capital punishment it should also be exemption. The {\i gemara} presses: {\i im kein leitei korban ki de-hashtah} \u8212- by the same logic applied to {\i korban}, the result should be bring the {\i korban} appropriate to the current status, just as you propose giving {\i chenek} based on current status. But Rabbi Shimon says bring nothing at all. Why, then, in the capital case would you give the lesser punishment rather than no punishment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that {\i Tosafot} points out an available answer that the {\i gemara} does not explicitly take up: unlike different types of {\i korbanot}, {\i sekilah} and {\i chenek} exist on a linear hierarchy, with {\i chenek} being the lesser. Indeed, elsewhere the {\i gemara} discusses a case where death-row inmates deserving different modes of execution are confused, and the ruling is to give them all {\i chenek} \u8212- demonstrating that {\i chenek} can legitimately discharge even a {\i sekilah} obligation. The common denominator of {\i sekilah} and {\i chenek} is {\i chenek}, and giving {\i chenek} to one who deserves {\i sekilah} is a legitimate mode of execution. {\i Tosafot} observes that this reasoning would resolve the {\i gemara}\u8217's objection, but the {\i gemara} itself does not pursue it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Execution Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather than resolving the {\i machloket tanna\u8217'im} approach, the {\i gemara} concludes that it cannot satisfactorily be sustained. The attempt to equate the capital-punishment question to the {\i korban} question \u8212- saying that if you follow current status you should impose the current punishment, and by parallel to {\i korban} where a discrepancy yields nothing, you should yield nothing here \u8212- leaves the {\i gemara} without a clean resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's Rewrite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, rather than continue developing these lines of reasoning, simply instructs the {\i tanna sefaina} \u8212- the reciter of {\i baraisos} \u8212- to amend the text: {\i tani tidon be-sekilah}. The {\i braisa} that Shila attributed to himself, which ruled {\i chenek}, should be rewritten to say {\i sekilah}. The ruling that punishment follows current status, giving the woman {\i chenek} because she is now a {\i bogeret}, is simply wrong and the text is to be corrected. Punishment follows the time of the sin, and she receives {\i sekilah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconsideration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even after the {\i braisa} is rewritten, the {\i gemara} raises one further question. The Torah specifies {\i na\u8217'arah ha-me\u8217'orasah} \u8212- perhaps this means the laws apply only when the woman is discovered {\i as} a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}, i.e., she must still be in that status at the time of the court proceedings? On this reading, {\i sheila}\u8217's original position might not have been wrong on its merits; rather, the Torah\u8217's language itself might confine the category to women who are currently {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} when brought before the court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ila\u8217's Interpretati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ila addresses this from the verse itself: {\i amar kra ha-na\u8217'arah} \u8212- the definite article in {\i ha-na\u8217'arah} signals {\i zu ha-na\u8217'arah she-asata kvar} \u8212- this refers to the one who {\i was} the {\i na\u8217'arah} at the time of the sin. She is called {\i ha-na\u8217'arah} not because she is a {\i na\u8217'arah} now but because she was the {\i na\u8217'arah} in question. This resolves any reading of the verse as demanding current status and confirms that the punishment tracks the time of the sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yitzchak Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the final resolution turns on a different axis entirely, introduced by Rav Yitzchak bar Avin \u8212- some say Rav Yitzchak bar Abba. When Rabbi Chanina had pressed Rabbi Ila with the implication that if punishment follows the time of the sin, then the {\i motzi shem ra} case should likewise follow the time of the alleged sin \u8212- meaning even if the husband libels her when she is a {\i bogeret}, he should incur the full obligations \u8212- Rabbi Ila responded with incredulity ({\i rachmana litzlan mei-hai da\u8217'ata}), and Rabbi Chanina returned the sentiment in kind ({\i rachmana litzlan mi-da\u8217'ata didach}). The exchange is pointed; the {\i gemara} then resolves it cleanly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Person/Act Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-ta\u8217'ama mai} \u8212- why is the {\i motzi shem ra} case genuinely different? Rav Yitzchak bar Avin teaches: {\i zu ma\u8217'aseha garmu lah ve-zeh kimatz sefasav garmu lo} \u8212- for her, it was her own actions that brought the punishment upon her, {\i keshe-hi zanesa na\u8217'arah zanesa} \u8212- when she committed adultery she committed it as a {\i na\u8217'arah}, and it is that act which is the basis of judgment. For him, however, it is the movement of his lips \u8212- his libel \u8212- that brings the punishment upon him. {\i Ve-eimas kamichayev} \u8212- when does he become obligated? {\i Ha-hi sha\u8217'ta} \u8212- at the moment he commits the {\i motzi shem ra}. {\i Ve-ha-hi sha\u8217'ta bogeret haviah} \u8212- and at that moment she is a {\i bogeret}. Therefore his act of slander simply does not fall within the category of {\i motzi shem ra}, which requires that the slander be directed against a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}. The two cases are thus entirely distinct in principle: her act and his act are different acts occurring at different times, and each is judged by the time at which the relevant act was committed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stoning Locations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Doorway\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the practical rules governing where {\i sekilah} takes place in these cases. The standard ruling: {\i soklin osah al pesach beis aviha} \u8212- she is stoned at the doorway of her father\u8217's house. This applies in the paradigmatic case where the matter is discovered after {\i nisuin} and the adultery occurred during {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 City Gate Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the father has no home \u8212- {\i ein lah pesach beis av} \u8212- then: {\i soklin osah al pesach sha\u8217'ar ha-ir ha-hi} \u8212- she is stoned at the gate of the city. This mirrors what the Torah prescribes in the general {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} case, and it also applies, as established earlier, when the discovery occurs before {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Din Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the city is majority non-Jewish \u8212- {\i ube-ir she-rubah ovdei kochavim} \u8212- the city gate carries no appropriate Jewish communal symbolism, and so: {\i soklin osah al pesach beis din} \u8212- she is stoned at the entrance of the {\i beis din}. The court itself then serves as the institutional expression of communal judgment. The same rule applies to the one who worships {\i avodah zarah}: {\i soklin oto be-sha\u8217'ar she-avad bo} \u8212- he is stoned at the gate of the city where the worship occurred, so that the consequence is visible to the community most affected by the idolatry. And if that city is predominantly non-Jewish: {\i soklin oto al pesach beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avodah Zarah Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes this parallel explicitly: {\i kayotze ba-davar atah omer} \u8212- and similarly you may say regarding {\i avodah zarah}. The motivation for stoning at the specific location of the idolatry is the concern about its pernicious communal effects; the statement must be made to the people in the place where the act occurred. This provides the structural parallel to the {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} case, and the two sets of laws illuminate each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i mina hani mili} \u8212- from where are these rules derived? The {\i baraisa} cites {\i she\u8217'arecha} \u8212- \u8220"your gates\u8221" \u8212- appearing twice in the passage about {\i avodah zarah}: once describing where the sin was committed and once prescribing where the stoning takes place. {\i Ne\u8217'emar she\u8217'arecha le-malah ve-ne\u8217'emar she\u8217'arecha le-matah} \u8212- the word {\i she\u8217'arecha} appears above and below. {\i Mah she\u8217'arecha ha-amor le-malah sha\u8217'ar she-avad bo} \u8212- just as the upper occurrence refers to the gate of the city where the worship took place, {\i af she\u8217'arecha ha-amor le-matah sha\u8217'ar she-avad bo} \u8212- so too the lower occurrence, prescribing execution, refers to the same gate. Furthermore, {\i she\u8217'arecha} \u8212- \u8220"your gates\u8221" \u8212- excludes {\i sha\u8217'arei ovdei kochavim}, the gates of non-Jewish cities, which establishes the {\i beis din} alternative. The {\i gemara} notes that if {\i she\u8217'arecha} were already used solely to identify which city\u8217's gate is meant, one could simply have written {\i shaar} without the possessive; the full form {\i she\u8217'arecha} therefore teaches both things simultaneously. {\i Shema mina tartei} \u8212- derive two laws from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} case, the Torah does not use {\i she\u8217'arecha} but rather {\i petach beis aviha}. The derivation connecting the two cases is attributed to Rabbi Abbahu: {\i gamar petach mi-petach u-fetach mi-sha\u8217'ar ve-sha\u8217'ar mi-she\u8217'arecha} \u8212- a {\i gezerah shavah} or {\i hekesh} linking {\i petach} as it appears in the {\i na\u8217'arah} passage to {\i petach} as used in the context of the {\i mishkan}, and from there to {\i sha\u8217'ar}, and from {\i sha\u8217'ar} to {\i she\u8217'arecha} in the {\i avodah zarah} passage. Rashi characterizes this derivation as {\i asmachta} \u8212- a textual support rather than a strict {\i derasha}. The practical upshot is clear: the standard case is stoning at {\i petach beis aviha}; if there is no {\i petach beis av} or if the discovery preceded {\i nisuin}, it is at the {\i sha\u8217'ar} of the city; if the city is non-Jewish, it is at the entrance of {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 46\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Motzi Shem Ra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context & Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 46, picking up from the bottom of 45b. We are wrapping up the {\i parsha} of {\i motzi shem ra} before transitioning, God willing, to what many consider the core subject matter of {\i masechet Ketubot} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The immediate context involves the {\i pesukim} concerning {\i petach beit aviha} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's specification that the proceedings of {\i motzi shem ra} take place at the doorway of the father\u8217's house \u8212- which the Gemara had established applies specifically in the case of after {\i nisuin}. A separate {\i pasuk}, concerning the {\i naarah meurasah} who is stoned, uses the expression {\i petach}, understood as {\i shaar ha\u8217'ir}, and this applies in a case of {\i erusin} rather than {\i nisuin}. Even in the classic case of {\i nisuin}, if there is no {\i petach beit ha\u8217'av}, the stoning is carried out at {\i shaar ha\u8217'ir}, and if there is no {\i shaar ha\u8217'ir} \u8212- for instance, in a city of non-Jews \u8212- it is done at {\i shaar beit din}. This parallel is drawn from the case of one found committing idolatry, who is executed in the city where the idolatry occurred rather than where judgment was rendered, presumably as a lesson to the inhabitants of that city.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes & Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a {\i baraita}: {\i ha-motzi shem ra lokeh v\u8217'nosein me\u8217'ah sela} \u8212- one who spreads false evil repute against his wife receives lashes and pays one hundred {\i sela} (equivalent to four hundred {\i zuz}). This is the standard formulation of the dual penalty in {\i motzi shem ra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda qualifies this. As far as lashes are concerned, {\i lokeh mikol makom} \u8212- he receives lashes under any circumstances. However, with respect to the hundred {\i sela}, {\i baal nosein, lo baal eino nosein}: only if he had intercourse with his wife and then brings the false claim does he pay; if he did not have intercourse, he does not pay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder how he spreads evil repute without having had intercourse. The answer lies in how {\i chazal} understand the entire {\i parsha}. The plain sense of the {\i pesukim} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'avo eleha v\u8217'lo matzasi lah betulim}, \u8220"I came to her and found no signs of virginity\u8221" \u8212- sounds like a straightforward claim the husband makes the morning after the wedding. The burden then appears to fall on the girl and her family to produce the bedsheets. {\i Chazal}, however, require a proper judicial proceeding. There must be witnesses, and not just any witnesses: the husband must have been the one who initiated, urged, or hired them. Furthermore, the father and the girl counter with {\i eidim zomemim}, witnesses who refute and invalidate the first set. The claim thus becomes entirely a matter of witness testimony, which means it is possible for a husband to manufacture such a proceeding without the wedding night ever having occurred \u8212- bringing false witnesses to claim she committed adultery after betrothal, without the physical predicate of their own intercourse. In that scenario, Rabbi Yehuda says, he still receives lashes but does not pay the hundred {\i sela}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic underlying Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's distinction is instructive. Spreading false and damaging repute about a young woman \u8212- ruining her standing \u8212- is a moral wrong regardless of whether it fits neatly within the {\i parsha} of {\i motzi shem ra}. But the hundred {\i sela} payment functions more like a {\i keifel}, a double payment, because the husband was in effect trying to rob the girl of her {\i mohar}, her bride price of fifty {\i shekel}. If they never had intercourse and the slander is exposed, she is still free to marry someone else and receive the standard bride price. The monetary penalty, then, is specifically anchored to the situation in which sex has occurred and the wedding-night claim is relevant \u8212- which is why Rabbi Yehuda limits it to that case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara identifies the underlying dispute as that between Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov and the {\i Rabbanan}. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov holds that the entire {\i parsha} of {\i motzi shem ra} in the Torah speaks only of a case in which intercourse has occurred. The {\i Rabbanan} hold that the {\i parsha} applies even without intercourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does this map onto the dispute in the {\i baraita}? On one reading, the {\i Tanna Kamma} reflects the {\i Rabbanan}: whether or not there was intercourse, both lashes and the monetary penalty apply, because the {\i parsha} is not limited to cases of intercourse. Rabbi Yehuda then emerges as reflecting Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's position that the {\i parsha} is only operative after intercourse, but extends lashes independently of the {\i parsha} \u8212- based on the prohibition of {\i lo telech rachil b\u8217'amecha}, the general prohibition against talebearing and slander. On this reading, anyone who spreads damaging false report, at least in a sufficiently provable and weighty manner, receives lashes based on this {\i lav}, regardless of whether they are technically within the {\i parsha} of {\i motzi shem ra}. The hundred {\i sela}, however, belongs exclusively to that {\i parsha} and is therefore conditioned on intercourse having occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a second reading of the {\i baraita} as well: {\i ika d\u8217'amri kulah k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} \u8212- perhaps the entire {\i baraita} assumes like Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov that the {\i parsha} operates only after intercourse. The {\i Tanna Kamma} says that in that scenario \u8212- one in which they have had intercourse \u8212- both lashes and payment apply. Rabbi Yehuda then adds that lashes are given even without intercourse, based on the general {\i lav} of {\i lo telech rachil}, while agreeing that payment requires the context of intercourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then challenges: does Rabbi Yehuda really hold that lashes are given under all circumstances? A {\i baraita} is cited in which Rabbi Yehuda himself says {\i ba\u8217'al lokeh, lo ba\u8217'al eino lokeh} \u8212- only if there was intercourse does he receive lashes, not otherwise. This appears to be a direct contradiction of the position just attributed to him, creating a {\i machlokes Tannaim} within Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's own rulings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak resolves this: when the first formulation says the husband receives lashes {\i mikol makom}, it means only {\i makas mardus}, rabbinic lashes \u8212- not Torah-level {\i malkot}. Rabbi Yehuda does not maintain a general biblical liability for talebearing. He holds that Torah-level lashes require being within the {\i parsha} of {\i motzi shem ra}, which itself requires intercourse. The earlier statement that he always receives lashes refers merely to a rabbinic disciplinary penalty for spreading evil repute, reflecting a societal interest in deterring such conduct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa offers a different resolution. He maintains the original reading: Rabbi Yehuda does hold that lashes apply under all circumstances in the broad sense. When the second {\i baraita} says {\i ba\u8217'al lokeh, lo ba\u8217'al eino lokeh}, the word {\i lokeh} there does not mean lashes at all \u8212- it means monetary liability. {\i V\u8217'kari lei mamon malkut} \u8212- can a monetary payment really be called {\i malkut}? The Gemara answers: yes. A {\i baraita} records a dispute regarding one who says {\i chatzi erchi alai}, \u8220"I obligate myself to pay half my assessed value.\u8221" Rabbi Yose berabbi Yehuda says {\i lokeh v\u8217'nosein erech shalem} \u8212- he is \u8220"{\i struck}\u8221" with the full bill. Rav Pappa explains that {\i lokeh} there means he is assessed the full amount, not that he receives lashes. The reason is a rabbinic decree: {\i gezeira chatzi ercho atu erech chatziyo} \u8212- saying \u8220"half my {\i erech}\u8221" might be confused with saying \u8220"the {\i erech} of half of me,\u8221" and since the {\i erech} of half of one\u8217's body encompasses a vital organ and therefore equals the {\i erech} of the whole, one pays the full amount in either case. The word {\i lokeh} in that passage simply means \u8220"he is hit with the full payment.\u8221" So too in the second {\i baraita}: {\i ba\u8217'al lokeh} means the husband is \u8220"hit\u8221" with the monetary obligation if there was intercourse, {\i lo ba\u8217'al eino lokeh} means no monetary obligation without intercourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then turns to the derivation of the lashes themselves. A {\i baraita} states: {\i v\u8217'anshu oso} \u8212- \u8220"they shall punish him\u8221" \u8212- refers to the monetary fine, as the Torah makes explicit: {\i v\u8217'anshu oso me\u8217'ah kesef v\u8217'nasnu la\u8217'avi hana\u8217'arah}. But {\i veyisru} \u8212- \u8220"they shall chastise him\u8221" \u8212- refers to {\i malkot}. How do we know {\i veyisru} means lashes? Rabbi Abbahu derives it through a chain: the word {\i yisru} is linked to a parallel {\i yisru} in the {\i parsha} of {\i ben sorer umoreh} \u8212- {\i veyisru oso v\u8217'lo yishma aleihem} \u8212- and through the word {\i ben} appearing there, it connects to the {\i parsha} of {\i vehaya im ben hakot harasha}, the classic Torah passage prescribing lashes. Once {\i veyisru} means lashes in the context of the {\i ben sorer umoreh}, it likewise means lashes in {\i motzi shem ra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i azhara lemotzi shem ra minlan} \u8212- what is the prohibitory {\i lav} that must be transgressed before lashes can be administered? Rabbi Elazar says: from {\i lo telech rachil b\u8217'amecha}. Rabbi Natan says: from {\i venishmarta mikol davar ra} \u8212- \u8220"guard yourself from every evil thing,\u8221" interpreting {\i davar} as {\i dibbur}, speech, so that it means one must guard against spreading harmful words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does Rabbi Elazar not use {\i venishmarta mikol davar ra}? Because that verse is needed for a teaching of Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair: {\i al yeharher adam bayom}, a person should not entertain improper thoughts during the day lest they lead to a seminal emission at night \u8212- a teaching rooted in the contextual juxtaposition of that verse with the {\i parsha} of {\i ki yihye vecha ish asher lo yihye tahor mikre laila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does Rabbi Natan not use {\i lo telech rachil b\u8217'amecha}? Because that verse is needed to instruct a {\i beit din} not to be {\i rach} \u8212- gentle \u8212- toward one litigant and harsh toward the other. Rashi notes that {\i rachil} may be read as a compound: {\i rach li}, soft to me. This is an explicit {\i pasuk} against judicial partiality in conduct, not merely in judgment. As one student aptly observed, the verse {\i lo telech rachil b\u8217'amecha} follows immediately upon {\i lo ta\u8217'asu avel bamishpat, lo tisa pnei dal v\u8217'lo tehedar pnei gadol}, providing the contextual basis for reading it as an instruction to the court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Liability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues. If the husband did not tell the witnesses to come and testify \u8212- {\i lo amar l\u8217'eidim bo v\u8217'heiduni} \u8212- and they testified on their own accord, then {\i hu eino lokeh v\u8217'eino nosein me\u8217'ah sela}: he receives no lashes and pays nothing. He is effectively a victim. In that case, {\i v\u8217'hi v\u8217'zomemeha makdimin l\u8217'beis ha-skila} \u8212- either she is stoned (if the witnesses are truthful) or the {\i eidim zomemim} are stoned (if they are refuted).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes the implication: if he did tell them to come testify, even without hiring them, he bears liability. This brings us to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's dissenting view: {\i eino chayav ad she-yiskor edim} \u8212- he is not liable unless he actually hired the witnesses. The Gemara\u8217's question is sharp: why should persuasion alone be insufficient? The principle of {\i ein shaliach l\u8217'dvar aveirah} \u8212- that there is no agency for transgression, since the agent should have heeded God rather than the sender \u8212- would seem to insulate the husband even if he hired them, let alone merely asked. The counter-intuition here is that by paying the witnesses, the husband transforms them into something closer to his employees and agents. The money concretizes his responsibility in a way that mere instigation does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu derives Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position through a {\i gezeira shava} of {\i sima sima}: the Torah uses the word {\i v\u8217'sam lah alilos devarim} \u8212- \u8220"and he placed upon her libelous words\u8221" \u8212- and elsewhere, {\i lo tasimun alav neshech} \u8212- \u8220"do not place interest upon him.\u8221" Just as \u8220"placing\u8221" in the context of {\i neshech} involves monetary conduct, so too \u8220"placing\u8221" false words against her must be through money, through hiring. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak cites a parallel teaching of Rabbi Yose the Sidonian in the school of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai making the identical {\i gezeira shava}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then records a series of unresolved questions posed by Rabbi Yirmeyah. If he hired the witnesses with land ({\i karka}) rather than money \u8212- does that qualify? What if he hired them for less than a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth? What if two witnesses together were hired for a single {\i perutah}, so that each individually received less than a {\i perutah}? These questions revolve around how formally the {\i gezeira shava} from {\i neshech} must be applied \u8212- whether the hiring must meet the technical minimum of {\i mammon} as defined in monetary law, or whether any act that concretizes the agency-like relationship suffices. The Gemara leaves these questions unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edge Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises further questions about the scope of {\i motzi shem ra}. {\i Hotzi shem ra al nisuin harishonim mahu} \u8212- what if he is spreading false repute regarding the first marriage? The scenario is: he married her, divorced her, remarried her, and now \u8212- in the context of the second marriage \u8212- he is bringing false witnesses to claim she had committed adultery before the {\i nisuin} of the first marriage. The classic {\i motzi shem ra} is the morning after the wedding night, but surely the claim need not literally be made the next morning; the husband might simply explain that the witnesses only came to him now. Nevertheless, is a second marriage a separate enough context that the {\i parsha} no longer applies?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question: {\i al nisuei achiv mahu} \u8212- what if it is the brother making the claim? The entire {\i parsha} in the Torah is framed around the husband, and as the Gemara makes clear, if any other man did the identical thing \u8212- hired witnesses, spread evil repute \u8212- he would still be exempt from the penalties of {\i motzi shem ra}, even if the witnesses themselves would be {\i eidim zomemim}. What then of a {\i yavam}, a brother-in-law who inherits the marriage through {\i yibum}? If Reuven died and Shimon married Reuven\u8217's widow through {\i yibum}, and Shimon now claims that witnesses have told him she committed adultery prior to the {\i nisuin} with Reuven \u8212- is Shimon perhaps a continuation of Reuven\u8217's marriage, such that his claim falls within the {\i parsha}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tension here, as one student rightly noted, is fundamental. Once {\i chazal} have reconceived {\i motzi shem ra} as a judicial proceeding centered on witnesses rather than the concrete experience of the wedding night, the logic of limiting the {\i parsha} to the husband \u8212- and only to the original marriage \u8212- becomes less obvious. Why shouldn\u8217't any man who manufactures such a proceeding be equally liable? The Gemara is navigating the balance between reading the {\i parsha} as a normal judicial category capable of generalization and remaining tethered to the Torah\u8217's specific framing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves one of these questions. From the {\i baraita} taught by Rav Yona: {\i et biti nasati la\u8217'ish hazeh} \u8212- \u8220"I gave my daughter to this man\u8221" \u8212- {\i lazeh v\u8217'lo l\u8217'yavam}, to this one and not to the {\i yavam}. At least the case of the brother is excluded. The question of {\i nisuin harishonim} remains unresolved, and the Gemara notes {\i pashat mi\u8217'achada} \u8212- we can answer one question, not both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesuk Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to the core dispute of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov and the {\i Rabbanan}, laying out the textual debate in detail. A {\i baraita} describes the procedure of {\i hotza\u8217'at shem ra}: the husband comes to {\i beit din} and says the daughter was not a virgin. If witnesses testify that she committed adultery {\i tachtav} \u8212- after betrothal, while under him \u8212- then she is stoned. The {\i baraita} first misstates this as her {\i ketubah} being reduced to a hundred, and the Gemara immediately corrects: she would be executed. The {\i baraita} is re-read: if witnesses confirm she committed adultery after betrothal, she faces {\i skila}; if it turns out she had intercourse before betrothal, then {\i nimtza shem ra eino shem ra} \u8212- the evil repute is not really evil repute, meaning the precise charge (post-betrothal adultery) was not substantiated \u8212- and he receives lashes and pays the hundred {\i sela}, {\i bein ba aleha bein lo ba aleha}, whether or not there was intercourse. This is the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov says: {\i lo ne\u8217'emru devarim halalu ela kesheba aleha} \u8212- the entire {\i parsha} applies only when intercourse has occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then walks through the {\i pesukim} systematically. According to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, {\i uba eleha v\u8217'kerav eleha} \u8212- \u8220"I came to her and drew near to her\u8221" \u8212- straightforwardly describes intercourse. According to the {\i Rabbanan}, {\i uba eleha} means he approached her with this charge, and {\i vekerav eleha} means he drew near her with words \u8212- a strained but not impossible reading of a verse that is somewhat redundant even on its surface. According to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, {\i v\u8217'lo matzasi lah betulim} plainly means he found no signs of virginity after intercourse. According to the {\i Rabbanan}, it means {\i lo matzasi lah kishrei betulim} \u8212- I did not find reliable vouchers for her virginity, i.e., I have no witnesses who can testify that she was a virgin, because the witnesses who appeared testified to her adultery. According to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, {\i ve\u8217'eleh betulei biti} \u8212- \u8220"and these are my daughter\u8217's tokens of virginity\u8221" \u8212- the father literally produces the bedsheet. According to the {\i Rabbanan}, {\i ve\u8217'eleh kishrei betulei biti} \u8212- these are those who validate my daughter\u8217's virginity, meaning the father produces counter-witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu then derives the metaphorical reading for {\i ufarshu ha-simla} \u8212- \u8220"and they shall spread out the garment.\u8221" He reads it as {\i pirshu mah she-samah} \u8212- they laid out and clarified the claim placed against her, a metaphor for the judicial clarification of competing testimonies. A {\i baraita} confirms: {\i melamed she-ba\u8217'in eidim shel zeh v\u8217'eidim shel zeh u-vorin es ha-davar ke-simla chadasha} \u8212- witnesses for one side and witnesses for the other come and clarify the matter like a brand-new cloak. Whether the metaphor derives from {\i simla chadasha} (a clean garment, white as clarity itself) or from {\i sam lah} (what was placed against her) is a point of minor debate: {\i Tosafot} argues it is not derived from the word {\i simla} at all, insisting it is not a wordplay on {\i simla}, while the reading that parses it as {\i pirshu mah she-samah} offers a more direct connection to the root {\i s-m} from {\i v\u8217'sam lah alilos devarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking here is that the Gemara\u8217's own presentation reveals that Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov is not merely making a technical argument that intercourse is required while the rest of the {\i parsha} is about witnesses. He is reading {\i devarim kiksavam} \u8212- the {\i parsha} as written. He says {\i simla mamash} \u8212- a literal garment. The implication is that according to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, what happens is literally what the Torah describes: the husband makes a claim about the wedding night, the parents produce the bedsheet, and that is what is being contested. It is the {\i Rabbanan} who have transformed the entire {\i parsha} into a metaphor for judicial witness proceedings. This is an important observation because it means the gap between the two positions is not merely about whether intercourse is technically required \u8212- it is a gap about whether the entire structure of the case is literal or metaphorical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This realization has a profound halachic dimension. Reading the {\i parsha} literally \u8212- as Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov appears to do in its fullest sense \u8212- creates a deeply troubling legal situation: the husband\u8217's unilateral claim, backed by no witnesses at all, would suffice to put the woman in jeopardy, with the burden of proof on her. {\i Chazal}\u8217's reinterpretation transforms it into a normal judicial proceeding, which is precisely why the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's metaphorical readings, however strained, are halachically necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedarkah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak bar Rabbi Yaakov bar Giyori sent a ruling from Eretz Yisrael in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: even though throughout the entire Torah, when it comes to prohibited sexual acts, the Torah does not distinguish between {\i biah kedarkah} (vaginal intercourse) and {\i biah shelo kedarkah} (anal intercourse) \u8212- both being equally actionable for the purposes of lashes and capital punishment \u8212- the case of {\i motzi shem ra} is different. {\i Eino chayav ad she-yivo shelo kedarkah v\u8217'yotzi shem ra kedarkah}: he is not liable unless the intercourse was {\i shelo kedarkah} and the false claim is framed as if it were {\i kedarkah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi notes that the phrase {\i she-yivo shelo kedarkah} means {\i afilu shelo kedarkah} \u8212- even if the intercourse was not the usual way. The Gemara will now explain the precise logic. The substance of the challenge is immediately apparent: according to whom does this ruling hold? If it follows the {\i Rabbanan}, then the whole case is about witnesses and what they testify \u8212- it has nothing to do with hymenal blood or the mode of intercourse. If it follows Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, then by his own logic {\i idi va-idi kedarkah beinan} \u8212- since the entire discovery is predicated on the absence of hymenal blood on the wedding night, both the intercourse and the claim must be about vaginal intercourse {\i kedarkah}, and the case of {\i shelo kedarkah} would never arise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore corrects: Rav Kahana sent a ruling in the name of Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- {\i eino chayav ad she-yivo kedarkah v\u8217'yotzi shem ra kedarkah}. He is not liable unless both the intercourse and the false claim are {\i kedarkah}. This follows Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov consistently: since the mechanism by which the husband discovers the claim is the absence of hymenal bleeding, both the intercourse that would have caused such bleeding and the claim about the absence of it must be {\i kedarkah}. The case requires that they had vaginal intercourse, that there was no blood, and that the claim is that she previously had vaginal intercourse with another man. The entire evidentiary structure revolves around the hymen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam in {\i Hilchos Naarah Bisulah} (chapter 3, {\i halacha} 6) rules accordingly: {\i keitzad motzi shem ra} \u8212- the case begins with the husband\u8217's own experience: \u8220"I came to her and found no signs of virginity,\u8221" after which he investigated and was then informed by witnesses that she had committed adultery after betrothal, and he brings those witnesses to {\i beit din}. The Rambam thus requires both elements: the concrete reality of intercourse and the judicial proceeding with witnesses \u8212- a kind of hybrid. Furthermore, the Rambam rules: {\i ba\u8217'alah shelo kedarkah v\u8217'amar lo matzasi betulim patur u-makin oso makas mardus} \u8212- if he had non-vaginal intercourse and claims she was not a virgin, he is exempt from the {\i motzi shem ra} penalties but receives {\i makas mardus}. As for hiring witnesses, the Rambam rules that he must have {\i heivi} the witnesses himself, but does not require that he actually hired them for payment. This raises a noteworthy question: if all he needed to do was produce them, could he in theory still have been acting in good faith, believing their testimony? The answer is not entirely clear, but presumably {\i heivi} assumes a knowing and malicious initiation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bookend\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the conclusion of {\i motzi shem ra}, it is worth pausing to note the structural significance of what we have just completed. The opening {\i perek} of {\i Ketubot} began with the question of when a woman gets married and what happens if the husband makes a claim the morning after regarding her virginity \u8212- a scenario directly rooted in the {\i parsha} of {\i motzi shem ra} in the Torah. The motivation for the practice of marrying on Wednesday, so that the husband could appear before {\i beit din} the following Thursday while still in the heat of the moment, is precisely to replicate the conditions under which the {\i parsha} operates. The intervening material \u8212- about the fifty {\i shekel} payment, the {\i mohar} \u8212- prefigures the foundational financial structures of marriage. The daf now turns decisively from that framework toward what is in some respects the true subject matter of the {\i masechta}: the financial and legal relationship between husband and wife, structured around the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternal Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i ha\u8217'av zakai bivito b\u8217'kiddusheha b\u8217'kesef b\u8217'shtar uv\u8217'viah} \u8212- the father has legal rights over his daughter, whether she is a {\i ketanah} or a {\i naarah}, to betroth her through money, through a written document, or through an act of intercourse. That is, the father has the authority to give her in marriage by whatever of the three modes of {\i kiddushin} he chooses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the right to betroth her, the father is also legally entitled to {\i metziatah uma\u8217'aseh yadeha} \u8212- anything she finds and her earnings. He may annul her vows ({\i hafarat nedarim}), and if she is divorced while still a {\i nesuah} while an {\i arusah}, her {\i get} is given to him as her representative. The one thing the father is not entitled to, the {\i Mishnah} notes, is {\i peiros} \u8212- the earnings or produce from her property. If, for example, she inherits from a maternal grandfather with no surviving sons, the father does not collect the income from that inheritance. Importantly, this contrasts with the husband\u8217's rights once {\i nisuin} occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} continues: once she enters into {\i nisuin} and passes from her father\u8217's control to her husband\u8217's, {\i yeseir alav ha-baal she\u8217'ochel peiros b\u8217'chayyeha} \u8212- the husband has all the rights the father had, and additionally he collects the produce of her property during her lifetime. This is the institution of {\i nichsei milog}: property that remains hers in title and returns to her upon dissolution of the marriage, but whose produce belongs to the husband throughout the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The transition from father to husband here reflects a model in which a woman\u8217's life between roughly the ages of three and twelve and a half is governed by her father\u8217's authority, and from {\i nisuin} onward by her husband\u8217's. The Mishnah frames this explicitly as the husband having {\i more} than the father \u8212- {\i yeseir alav} \u8212- not a different kind of authority but an extension of it. It should be noted, however, that {\i Masechet Kiddushin} fully recognizes the category of a {\i bogeret}, a young woman past the age of twelve and a half, who can betroth herself independently \u8212- a reality that creates a substantial window of personal autonomy entirely unaddressed by this Mishnah\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah also enumerates the husband\u8217's obligations. In exchange for these rights, he is {\i chayav b\u8217'mzonoteha} \u8212- obligated to feed and support her \u8212- {\i b\u8217'firkonah} \u8212- obligated to redeem her if she is taken captive \u8212- and {\i b\u8217'kvuratah} \u8212- obligated to pay for her burial. Rabbi Yehuda adds that even the poorest man in Israel may not stint on his wife\u8217's funeral below a bare minimum: {\i lo yifchos mishnei chalilin u-mkonenet} \u8212- he must provide at least two flutes and one wailer, the conventional minimum expression of public mourning.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Pockets Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: {\i bechessef minalan} \u8212- from where do we derive that the father not only has the power to betroth his daughter but also pockets the money received? Rav Yehuda cites the verse {\i v\u8217'yatza chinam ein kesef} \u8212- when the father sells his daughter as a servant and the conditions of the sale are not fulfilled, she goes free without money. The analysis: {\i ein kesef la\u8217'adon zeh} \u8212- for this master, no money changes hands upon her release; {\i v\u8217'yesh kesef la\u8217'adon acher} \u8212- but there is another master who does receive money for her departure. {\i U-mano aviha} \u8212- and who is this other master? The father. The Gemara is explicit and unapologetic: the father\u8217's legal relationship to his daughter is described in terms parallel to a master\u8217's relationship to his servant. The Torah itself structures it that way, and the Gemara is simply making the implicit explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father as Master\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the father functions as a kind of \u8220"master\u8221" who receives money when the daughter leaves his domain, the Gemara anticipates an objection: {\i v\u8217'eima ledidah} \u8212- perhaps while the father has the power to betroth her, the money goes to the daughter herself, not the father? The Gemara responds: {\i hatam aviha mikabel kiddusheha} \u8212- the father is the one who receives the {\i kiddushin}, as derived from {\i et biti nasati la\u8217'ish hazeh} \u8212- \u8220"I gave my daughter to this man.\u8221" The very framing of the verse, in which the father is the active agent giving the daughter, establishes that the transaction runs through him and that the money is received by him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah vs Naarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara refines further. One might grant that this applies to a {\i ketanah}, a minor who has no independent legal agency at all. But what of a {\i naarah}, a girl who has reached the age of twelve and is to some degree her own person \u8212- perhaps she could both betroth herself and pocket the {\i kiddushin} money? The Gemara responds with a verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevach Ne\u8217'urim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar kra bine\u8217'ureiha beit aviha \u8212- kol shevach ne\u8217'urim la\u8217'aviha}: while she is in her youth in her father\u8217's house, all the benefit and value of her youth ({\i shevach ne\u8217'urim}) belongs to her father. The Gemara notes, however, that this derivation will be examined further, as it does not yet fully satisfy the question. The underlying assumption being tested is that as a {\i naarah} she might have sufficient independent standing to act on her own behalf and retain the proceeds \u8212- and the verse is brought to foreclose that possibility. The discussion will continue on the following daf, where the Gemara will work through precisely how we know not only that the father controls the betrothal but that he is entitled to all the financial benefits that flow from his daughter\u8217's youth.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 48\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Framework of Ketubot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Status & Transit\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf we take up today \u8212- picked up in the middle of 47b, roughly ten lines below the two dots \u8212- sits at the heart of {\i masechet Ketubot}, the section that deals with the {\i ketubah} proper: the reciprocal rights and responsibilities between husband and wife. Before arriving at those obligations, however, it is worth situating the {\i masechet} within a broader framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The earlier chapters of {\i Ketubot} focused on the father\u8217's rights over his daughter: his power to annul her vows, to marry her off, and to claim her earnings \u8212- most of which are biblical. Notably, the father bears almost no reciprocal responsibilities. He has no legal obligation even to feed his daughter; he could, in principle, send her out of the house to earn her own keep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reflects a portrait of women in the {\i Torah} that is striking to the modern reader: the woman never appears as a fully autonomous agent directing her own fate. She moves from one man\u8217's house to another \u8212- from her father\u8217's house to her husband\u8217's house. The {\i Torah}\u8217's language bears this out explicitly; when a {\i kohen}\u8217's daughter is widowed or divorced, the verse says {\i v\u8217'shava el beit aviha kine\u8217'ureha} \u8212- she returns to her father\u8217's house. It is the Rabbis who open up the space for a woman\u8217's legal independence, ruling that the father\u8217's authority ceases when she becomes a {\i bogeret} at twelve and a half, at which point she becomes her own legal agent. That emancipation is a rabbinic innovation, not a biblical one; the baseline framework of the {\i Torah} is the transit from father\u8217's house to husband\u8217's house, and that is precisely the framework {\i masechet Ketubot} addresses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erusin vs.\u160 ?Nissuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within that framework, there is an important distinction between {\i erusin} \u8212- betrothal \u8212- and {\i nissuin} \u8212- the actual consummation of marriage and cohabitation. After {\i erusin}, the woman remains essentially in her father\u8217's domain. It is only with {\i nissuin}, when she begins living together with her husband, that the primary obligations and rights of the {\i ketubah} fully take effect. The base amount of the {\i ketubah} \u8212- 100 or 200 {\i zuz}, depending on whether she is a widow or a virgin \u8212- exists from the time of {\i erusin}, but whether the additional amounts, the {\i tosefes} and the {\i nedunya} (dowry), take effect before {\i nissuin} was a matter of debate, as was discussed in the preceding material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Main Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ongoing rights and responsibilities during marriage bear a distinct name: {\i t\u8217'nai ketubah} \u8212- the stipulations of the {\i ketubah}. This term refers not to conditions in the contractual sense, but to the ongoing obligations that govern the marriage itself, as distinct from the {\i ikar ketubah} (the base lump-sum payment of 100 or 200) and the {\i tosefes} (any additional amount the groom undertakes). The {\i ikar} and {\i tosefes} are payable at divorce or the husband\u8217's death, functioning essentially as alimony or, in the case of his death, as a substitute for inheritance \u8212- since a wife does not inherit her husband \u8212- protecting her when she is left without his support. The {\i t\u8217'nai ketubah}, by contrast, are the live, ongoing obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three primary reciprocal obligations structure this discussion: the husband\u8217's duty to provide {\i mezonot} (food and sustenance) in exchange for his wife\u8217's earnings; his duty to redeem her from captivity ({\i pirkyon}) in exchange for his right to the {\i peiros} \u8212- the fruits and earnings from her {\i nichsei milug} (property to which she retains title but whose income accrues to him); and his duty to bury her should she predecease him, in exchange for the {\i nedunya}, the dowry property she transfers to his estate at marriage ({\i nichsei tzon barzel}).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reciprocal Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanu Rabanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanu rabanan} at 47b articulates these exchanges formally: {\i tiknu mezonoteha tachat maaseh yadeha} \u8212- they established his obligation to feed her in exchange for her earnings; {\i upirkona tachat perot} \u8212- his obligation to redeem her from captivity in exchange for his right to the {\i peiros}; and {\i ukvorata tachat ktuvata} \u8212- his obligation to bury her in exchange for the {\i ketubah}, which here means the {\i nedunya}, the dowry property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Whose Rights Matter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks a pointed question: {\i lefikach baal ochel perot} \u8212- \u8220"therefore the husband eats the fruit\u8221" \u8212- why is this stated with the word {\i lefikach} (\u8220"therefore\u8221"), as if it were the conclusion to be derived, when all three obligations have already been stated? The question exposes an important ambiguity in how the entire structure is framed: is the husband the entitled party \u8212- in which case he holds the rights and the obligations are what he concedes in exchange \u8212- or is the wife the entitled party, in which case her rights are primary and his benefits are what he receives in exchange?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing question matters enormously for practical {\i halakha}. If the husband is the entitled party \u8212- if we read the {\i braita} as saying, essentially, he has a right to her earnings, and in exchange he feeds her \u8212- then it is {\i he} who can opt out: he could say, keep your earnings, I will not feed you. If, on the contrary, the wife is the entitled party \u8212- she has a right to be supported, and in exchange he takes her earnings \u8212- then {\i she} is the one who can opt out: she could say, don\u8217't support me, and I will keep my own earnings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit & Redemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the specific question of the {\i lefikach} as follows. One might think that since the husband\u8217's right to the {\i peiros} exists specifically to ensure he will redeem his wife from captivity, we should place those earnings in escrow \u8212- a dedicated emergency fund \u8212- rather than allow him to spend them freely. If the money is segregated, the reasoning goes, he will feel his obligation is discharged once that fund is exhausted, and if the ransom demanded exceeds the amount saved, he will simply refuse to pay the difference. {\i Lefikach baal ochel perot} \u8212- therefore we let him eat the fruit freely, without strict accounting \u8212- because if he has used the earnings over the years without segregating them, no exact accounting is kept in either direction: he is not limited to a fixed sum when the time comes to redeem her, and he understands that the obligation to redeem her is open-ended. {\i Zimnin d\u8217'lo malu} \u8212- sometimes the accumulated earnings will not be sufficient \u8212- and precisely because he has enjoyed them without limit, he will reach into his own pocket to make up the difference and redeem her fully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This exchange may be difficult for the modern reader to fully appreciate on two levels. First, it presupposes that capture and the need for ransom were not rare occurrences \u8212- the {\i masechet} returns to the topic repeatedly \u8212- though that reality is remote from contemporary experience. Second, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's assumption that a husband might hesitate to redeem his wife is jarring. Yet the discussion notes a real concern: if a wife taken captive might have been raped, or might even willingly engage in sexual relations in order to secure her freedom, a husband might pause before paying the ransom. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's frank acknowledgment of this dynamic is a striking window into the social and moral realities that shaped these laws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Ketubah Reform\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The framing question \u8212- whose rights are primary \u8212- is not merely academic. Returning to it: against the plain sound of the {\i braita}, which makes the husband appear to be the entitled party, the {\i halakhic} conclusion is that the wife is the protected party. She is the one entitled to support, and in exchange he receives her earnings. This means she is the one who can opt out of the exchange: she may declare that she will retain her own earnings and forego his support obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion has direct modern application. {\i Kolech}, a women\u8217's Orthodox organization in Israel, has created a rider to the standard {\i ketubah} in which the wife first waives her right to be supported and retains her own earnings and the income from her property, thereby severing the exchange on which the standard {\i t\u8217'nai ketubah} rest. Having done so, both parties are then free to enter a new, mutual set of obligations \u8212- pooling their earnings and accepting joint responsibility for one another\u8217's financial well-being. This restructuring is only legally available to the wife, and only because {\i halakha} recognizes her, not the husband, as the fundamentally protected party in the original exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the {\i ketubah} in Israel occupies a dual status \u8212- simultaneously a ritual document and a legally operative instrument before the rabbinical courts \u8212- {\i Kolech} issues this restructuring as a separate rider rather than altering the traditional {\i ketubah} text itself, preserving the ritual formula while achieving the legal goal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Common & Uncommon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the three paired exchanges, the {\i Gemara} asks why specifically these obligations are matched to these rights \u8212- why {\i mezonot} for earnings, {\i pirkyon} for {\i peiros}, and burial for {\i nedunya}, and not some other pairing. {\i Abaye} supplies the organizing principle: {\i tiknu matzui l\u8217'matzui ve\u8217'she\u8217'eino matzui l\u8217'she\u8217'eino matzui} \u8212- common things are matched with common things, and uncommon things with uncommon things.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Classification of Obliga\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The matching follows accordingly. {\i Mezonot} \u8212- the daily obligation to feed his wife \u8212- is an everyday, ongoing expense. It is matched against {\i maasei yadeha} \u8212- her earnings \u8212- which are likewise an ongoing, regular matter, whether from labor outside the home, or more commonly, from the domestic work she performs within it: cooking, sewing, running the household. Common is matched with common.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kevurah} \u8212- burial \u8212- is a one-time event, as is the {\i nedunya} the wife brings into the marriage: she brings it once, and it is transferred once. Uncommon is matched with uncommon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Pirkyon} \u8212- redemption from captivity \u8212- and {\i peiros} \u8212- the ongoing earnings from her property holdings \u8212- present a more complex case. The {\i peiros} are an ongoing accumulation, while the ransom may be a one-time and potentially very large demand. The {\i Gemara} already explained why the lack of exact accounting between these two serves both parties: the husband enjoys the earnings freely, and therefore is expected to redeem her without limit when the moment comes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} notes a further dimension relevant to the matching of {\i pirkyon} with {\i peiros}: while a {\i nedunya}, a dowry of transferred property, was extremely common \u8212- virtually every woman brought one \u8212- property that the wife {\i already owned} at the time of marriage, to which she retained title and from whose income the husband benefited ({\i nichsei milug}, producing {\i peiros}), was less common in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's world. In the contemporary context, by contrast, a professional woman is far more likely to bring personal assets and investments into a marriage than to bring a formal dowry, which inverts the historical frequency. This does not change the legal structure, but it does illuminate why {\i Rashi} treats {\i peiros} as the less common item in its pair.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Formulas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to examine the actual text of the {\i ketubah} document, which translates these abstract obligations into a formal written formula. The {\i ketubah} opens with the date \u8212- {\i b\u8217'echad b\u8217'shabbat}, on the first day of the week, in year such-and-such \u8212- and the groom\u8217's declaration: {\i amar lah l\u8217'hada betulta, havi li l\u8217'intu} \u8212- he said to this woman, be for me as a wife. This declaration corresponds to the moment of {\i kiddushin}; the {\i ketubah} obligations, however, take effect at {\i nissuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The groom then undertakes the central obligations: {\i eflach v\u8217'okir ve\u8217'ezon ve\u8217'afarnes yatich ke\u8217'hilchat guvrin yehuda\u8217'in} \u8212- I will work, honor, sustain, and provide for you in the manner of Jewish husbands. {\i Eflach} \u8212- I will work \u8212- means that he commits to going out and earning a living for her benefit. {\i Okir} \u8212- I will honor \u8212- reflects the principle {\i chayav adam l\u8217'chaved es ishto yoser m\u8217'gufo}, a man must honor his wife more than himself; this is a personal rather than financial obligation. {\i Ezon} \u8212- I will feed \u8212- corresponds to {\i mezonot}. {\i Afarnes} \u8212- I will provide \u8212- likely refers to {\i ksus}, clothing. The phrase {\i ke\u8217'hilchat guvrin yehuda\u8217'in} \u8212- in the manner of Jewish husbands \u8212- signals that he commits to doing all of this properly, without cutting corners.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Later in the document comes the formula {\i u\u8217'me\u8217'ayel lavasayichi k\u8217'orach kol ar\u8217'a} \u8212- I will enter upon you in the way of all the earth \u8212- which refers to {\i bi\u8217'ah}, the sexual relation, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i onah}. The phrase {\i k\u8217'orach kol ar\u8217'a} may be read in parallel with {\i ke\u8217'hilchat guvrin yehuda\u8217'in} \u8212- not merely as a bare description of intercourse, but as a commitment to fulfill this obligation in the proper, respectful manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Listed & Unlisted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ketubah} formula thus mentions {\i mezonot}, {\i ksus}, and {\i onah} \u8212- sustenance, clothing, and conjugal relations. What is conspicuously absent from the explicit text is {\i refuah} (the obligation to pay for his wife\u8217's medical care) and {\i kevurah} (burial). These obligations exist, but they are not spelled out in the formula, presumably because they do not require a fixed monetary amount to be stipulated in the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the husband\u8217's corresponding {\i rights} \u8212- his entitlement to her earnings, the {\i peiros} of her {\i nichsei milug}, and so forth \u8212- are not enumerated in the {\i ketubah} text. The {\i ketubah} is primarily a document of his obligations to her, not a ledger of mutual exchanges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed Amounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ketubah} does specify the {\i nedunya} \u8212- the dowry \u8212- listing what she brings from her father\u8217's house and fixing a monetary value for it. It is this fixed value that the estate must return to her in the event of divorce or the husband\u8217's death. In practice, rather than enumerating the specific assets \u8212- two sheep, ten garments, one camel \u8212- the document fixes the amount as a round sum: {\i hakol kibel alav chasan dena b\u8217'me\u8217'ah zkukan kesef tzaruf} \u8212- the groom accepted upon himself the equivalent of one hundred {\i zekukim} of refined silver as the value of the {\i nedunya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the {\i nedunya}, the {\i ketubah} also specifies the {\i tosefes ketubah} \u8212- the additional amount the groom undertakes above the base {\i ikar ketubah}. These two amounts \u8212- the fixed {\i nedunya} and the {\i tosefes} \u8212- together constitute the sum the wife collects at the dissolution of the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zuz to Kesef Tzaruf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The historical background to these numbers deserves explanation. The biblical {\i ketubah} amount \u8212- {\i ikar ketubah} \u8212- is 200 {\i zuz} for a virgin and 100 for a widow. By the time of the {\i Rishonim}, however, the {\i zuz} had become so debased in value that 200 {\i zuz} offered virtually no protection to the woman. To remedy this, the {\i Rishonim} effectively replaced 200 {\i zuz} with 100 {\i kesef tzaruf} \u8212- refined silver coins \u8212- a sum of substantially greater real value. They accomplished this through two mechanisms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First, the {\i nedunya} was fixed at 100 {\i kesef tzaruf}, even for a woman who brought nothing to the marriage \u8212- the document simply states that as the agreed valuation. Second, the {\i tosefes ketubah} was standardized at an additional 100 {\i kesef tzaruf}, making it a boilerplate requirement rather than a voluntary addition. The two amounts together \u8212- 100 for the {\i nedunya} and 100 for the {\i tosefes} \u8212- total 200 {\i kesef tzaruf}, and the document closes with {\i sach hakol mas\u8217'ayim zekukim kesef tzaruf}, the total being 200 {\i zekukim} of refined silver, effectively replacing the 200 {\i zuz} of the biblical standard with a figure of real monetary weight. The combination of a fixed {\i nedunya} and a fixed {\i tosefes} into a single total is admittedly an unusual conflation, but the purpose \u8212- protecting the wife \u8212- is transparent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Kolech} {\i ketubah} rider, mentioned earlier, represents the most recent significant modification to this framework, though no formal communal fix has been enacted since the {\i Rishonim}\u8217's standardization roughly six hundred years ago.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biblical & Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having outlined the structure of the reciprocal obligations, the {\i Gemara} turns to a fundamental question: which of these obligations are biblical ({\i m\u8217'deoraisa}) and which are rabbinic enactments ({\i m\u8217'derabanan})? {\i Rava} raises the question: the earlier {\i braita} framed the husband\u8217's {\i mezonot} obligation as a rabbinic enactment \u8212- the Rabbis established his duty to feed her in exchange for her earnings, implying it was not a pre-existing biblical duty. But the following {\i braita} appears to treat {\i mezonot} as biblical. Which is correct?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Torah Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} invoked is derived from the verse in Exodus describing the case of a man who acquires a female slave with the intention of marrying her. If he subsequently takes a second, free-born wife of higher social standing, the {\i Torah} warns: {\i she\u8217'erah kesutah ve\u8217'onatah lo yigra} \u8212- he may not diminish the first wife\u8217's {\i she\u8217'er}, {\i kesut}, and {\i onah}. The concern is that the husband, preferring his second wife, will treat the first as a second-class wife in every dimension. The {\i Torah} prohibits this, and from this prohibition we derive the affirmative obligations owed to every wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The classic reading, familiar from {\i Rashi} on the {\i Chumash}, maps the three terms as follows: {\i she\u8217'erah elu mezonot} \u8212- {\i she\u8217'er} (flesh) refers to food, derived from the verse {\i va\u8217'asher achalu she\u8217'er ami}, \u8220"they ate the flesh of my people,\u8221" meaning that flesh requires food to sustain it. {\i Kesutah k\u8217'mashma\u8217'an} \u8212- {\i kesut} means clothing, simply as the word implies. {\i Onatah zeh onah ha\u8217'amurah ba-Torah} \u8212- {\i onatah} refers to the regular conjugal times, derived from the verse {\i im ta\u8217'aneh benotai} \u8212- if you afflict my daughters \u8212- where withholding is the {\i inuy} and the obligation is its positive counterpart.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So according to this first reading, all three \u8212- food, clothing, and conjugal relations \u8212- are biblical obligations derived from the {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} disagrees with the assignment of terms. He reads {\i she\u8217'erah} not as food but as sexual relations: {\i she\u8217'er} in the {\i Torah} typically denotes flesh in the sense of bodily closeness \u8212- {\i ish ish el kol she\u8217'er besaro lo tikrevu l\u8217'galot ervah} \u8212- a man shall not draw close to any who is {\i she\u8217'er}, his close flesh, to uncover nakedness. Thus {\i she\u8217'erah} signals the fleshly, sexual relation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In his reading, then, {\i she\u8217'erah} is {\i onah}, {\i kesutah} remains clothing, and {\i onatah} becomes {\i mezonot} \u8212- sustenance \u8212- derived from {\i vay\u8217'anecha vayarchivcha}, God caused Israel to suffer hunger ({\i inuy}) in the wilderness before feeding them with the {\i man}. The same root that denotes the withholding of sex also denotes the withholding of food, and from either withholding we derive an obligation to provide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion is the same trio \u8212- food, clothing, sex \u8212- but the words from which each is derived are rearranged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer b. Yaakov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} offers a substantially different reading. He treats the verse not as a list of three separate obligations, but as two paired phrases: {\i she\u8217'er kesut} together, and {\i kesut ve\u8217'onah} together. {\i She\u8217'er} functions as an adjective qualifying {\i kesut}: {\i le\u8217'fum she\u8217'erah ten kesutah} \u8212- according to her flesh, give her clothing. That is, provide her with clothing appropriate to her body and stage of life. {\i Shelo yiten lo shel yaldah le\u8217'zekenah ve\u8217'lo shel zekenah le\u8217'yaldah} \u8212- do not give an old woman a young woman\u8217's garments or a young woman an old woman\u8217's garments. Similarly, {\i kesutah ve\u8217'onatah} \u8212- according to the season\u8217's time, give her clothing: {\i shelo yiten chadashim biyemot ha\u8217'chamah ve\u8217'lo shechakim biyemot ha\u8217'geshamim} \u8212- do not give new, thick garments in summer, nor worn, thin garments in winter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov}, the entire verse speaks only of clothing, with two specifications: that the clothing be appropriate to her age and that it be appropriate to the season. This is a striking position. It would seem to leave {\i mezonot} and {\i onah} entirely without a biblical source, at least from this verse. Several {\i Rishonim} note that this need not mean those obligations are nonexistent or unimportant, but rather that they understand them differently: the sexual relationship is not merely one item among a list of obligations, but is constitutive of the very identity of marriage. A husband who cannot fulfill this dimension of the marriage is not simply failing one obligation among many \u8212- he is undermining what marriage fundamentally is. This has halakhic implications when, for example, a husband is physically incapable of conjugal relations: the question is whether the wife can sue for divorce not merely on the grounds that one obligation is unmet, but on the grounds that there is, in some sense, no marriage at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiruv Basar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} cites yet another tradition: {\i she\u8217'erah zu kiruv basar} \u8212- {\i she\u8217'erah} means the coming-together of flesh. {\i Shelo yinhag bah minhag Parsiyim she\u8217'meshamshin mitosaihen belivushaihen} \u8212- he should not conduct himself with her as the Persians do, who have marital relations while clothed. This {\i braita} uses the exotic figure of the Persians \u8212- the {\i Gemara}\u8217's analogue, apparently, of the various cultural stereotypes every society projects onto others \u8212- to make the point that conjugal relations must involve {\i kiruv basar}, actual bodily union, not merely the act of intercourse while remaining clothed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This supports a ruling of {\i Rav Huna}: if a husband declares {\i ee efshi ela ani be\u8217'vigdi vehi be\u8217'vigdah} \u8212- I refuse to have relations unless we are both clothed \u8212- he must give a divorce and pay the {\i ketubah}. The intimacy of marriage requires nakedness; the {\i Gemara} assumes that a husband and wife share a bed unclothed. This is why the {\i Gemara} elsewhere considers whether, when a wife is a {\i niddah}, it suffices for the couple to wear nightclothes rather than having separate beds \u8212- since the assumption is that normally they are unclothed together, the wearing of clothes might itself constitute sufficient {\i hekker} (marking of separation). The {\i Gemara} ultimately rejects this and requires separate beds, but the very framing of the question reveals the baseline assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakhic Consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consensus position among the {\i Rishonim}, notwithstanding {\i Rashi}\u8217's well-known commentary on the {\i Chumash}, is that the only obligation that is definitively biblical ({\i m\u8217'deoraisa}) is {\i onah} \u8212- the conjugal relation. {\i Mezonot} and {\i ksus} \u8212- food and clothing \u8212- are rabbinic obligations. This is consistent with the plain language of the first {\i braita}, which framed {\i mezonot} as a rabbinic enactment in exchange for her earnings. What {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading captures is the content of the three obligations, not their legal source; the {\i halakhic} derivation of which is biblical and which is rabbinic is a separate question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ramban\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ramban}, in his {\i Torah} commentary, offers a reading that both accommodates the {\i halakhic} consensus and gives depth to all three terms. If the only biblical obligation is {\i onah}, and {\i she\u8217'erah} and {\i kesutah} cannot straightforwardly be read as food and clothing in this context, then what do all three words mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ramban} proposes that the verse describes three dimensions of the {\i sexual} relationship between husband and wife \u8212- particularly with reference to the first wife whom the husband might be inclined to treat contemptuously. {\i She\u8217'erah} refers to {\i kiruv basar} \u8212- actual bodily union, the quality of the intimate act itself, not merely its occurrence. The husband may not treat the first wife as a purely instrumental sexual partner while reserving emotional and physical tenderness for the second; even with the first wife, the intimacy must be real. {\i Kesutah} \u8212- \u8220"her covering\u8221" \u8212- the {\i Ramban} reads as referring to the setting and environment: the marital bed, the linens, the physical context in which intimacy occurs. Relations must not be conducted in a degrading or perfunctory manner, as one might visit a prostitute, but with the full dignity of the marital environment. {\i Onatah} \u8212- \u8220"her times\u8221" \u8212- refers to the regularity and ongoing rhythm of conjugal life: it must occur at appropriate intervals and reflect a sustained connection, not a sporadic or arbitrary one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the three words all speak to {\i onah}, but they articulate its three necessary dimensions: the quality of bodily union, the dignity of the environment, and the regularity of the relationship. This also explains, the {\i Ramban} argues, why the {\i halakhic} conclusion places only {\i onah} in the biblical category \u8212- because all three terms are ultimately about {\i onah}, not about separate obligations of food and clothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe that in the {\i ketubah} text itself, {\i onah} appears somewhat separately \u8212- {\i u\u8217'me\u8217'ayel lavasayichi k\u8217'orach kol ar\u8217'a} \u8212- and the formula {\i ke\u8217'hilchat guvrin yehuda\u8217'in} and {\i k\u8217'orach kol ar\u8217'a} may both carry the sense of the {\i Ramban}\u8217's understanding: not merely that the act occurs, but that it is carried out in the proper, dignified, and fully human manner.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Burial Standards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Minimum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the {\i mishnah}, which addresses the standard for the husband\u8217's obligation to bury his wife when she predeceases him. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} rules that even the poorest man in Israel must provide his wife with a burial that includes no fewer than two flutes ({\i chalilim}) and a wailer ({\i mekonenes}) \u8212- professional mourners of a minimum standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Family Practice vs.\u160 ?Mini\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} reads this ruling in contrast to the {\i Tanna Kamma}, who appears to accept a lower standard. The {\i Gemara} clarifies the precise case of dispute: everyone agrees that the husband must provide for the burial at {\i at least} the standard to which the wife\u8217's family is accustomed. The question is what happens when his family standard is higher than hers. In that case, does he owe her the higher standard?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that he need only meet her family\u8217's customary standard. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds that he must provide whatever is the {\i higher} of the two standards \u8212- his or hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ascent & Non-Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dispute reflects the broader principle of {\i olah immo ve\u8217'einah yoredes immo} \u8212- she ascends with him but does not descend with him. That is, if the husband is of higher social or economic standing than his wife, she is entitled to be maintained at {\i his} standard, not merely her own. Conversely, if he falls into poverty, he cannot reduce her maintenance to below what she was accustomed to before marriage. The principle governs his obligations of food, clothing, and housing during the marriage; the question here is whether it also governs the funeral.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Living vs.\u160 ?Deceased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} frames this as follows: everyone may agree that {\i olah immo ve\u8217'einah yoredes immo} applies during the couple\u8217's shared life \u8212- while both are alive, she is maintained at the higher of the two standards. The question is whether it continues to apply {\i after death}, when there is no longer a shared life to speak of. {\i Rav Chisda} rules in the name of {\i Mar Ukva}: {\i halakha ke-Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- we follow {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. Even after death, the burial must be conducted at the higher of the husband\u8217's and wife\u8217's family standards. The principle of {\i olah immo ve\u8217'einah yoredes immo} is not extinguished by her death.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Absent/Incapacitated Hus\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deranged Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} further rules in the name of {\i Mar Ukva} regarding a husband who has become a {\i shoteh} \u8212- who is in a state of mental incapacity, unable to manage his own affairs or provide for his household. {\i Beit Din} intervenes actively: {\i Beit Din yordin l\u8217'nechasav v\u8217'zanin u\u8217'mefarnesin es ishto u\u8217'vanav u\u8217'vnosav} \u8212- {\i Beit Din} descends to his property, selling it off as necessary, and uses the proceeds to provide {\i mezonot} and {\i parnasah} for his wife, his sons, and his daughters, as well as for a {\i davar acher} \u8212- another matter \u8212- to be specified below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conscious Abandonment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is contrasted with a different {\i braita}: a man who has gone abroad \u8212- who left on his own initiative, consciously, without making arrangements for his family. His wife comes before {\i Beit Din} demanding {\i mezonot}. Here too {\i Beit Din yordin l\u8217'nechasav} and sells off property to sustain his wife \u8212- but {\i aval lo vanav u\u8217'vnosav}, not his sons and daughters \u8212- {\i v\u8217'lo davar acher}, nor for the other matter. The children and the additional obligations are not covered when the husband has consciously abandoned his responsibilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legal vs.\u160 ?Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina} raises the question to {\i Rav Ashi}: what is the operative distinction between these two cases? The answer lies in a difference between legal obligation and presumed intent. A husband has a legal obligation to support his wife; he has no such legal obligation to support his children. {\i Beit Din} can enforce legal obligations against his estate in any case. But when {\i Beit Din} acts on behalf of a {\i shoteh} \u8212- a man who is incapacitated and not consciously choosing to abandon his family \u8212- it acts not merely to discharge legal obligations but also to fulfill what the man himself would presumably have wanted: to care for his children, to provide the additional comforts for his wife that go beyond the strict legal minimum. We assume that a man who has not made a conscious choice to abandon his family would, if he could, continue to care for them fully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When, however, a man has left consciously and deliberately \u8212- even if he may have expected to return \u8212- the fact that he did not provide for his children before leaving is read as a communication: he is not attending to those responsibilities. Since he is not legally obligated, {\i Beit Din} cannot override this. Only the wife\u8217's strict legal entitlement can be enforced against his estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Additional Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i davar acher} \u8212- the additional matter mentioned in both the case of the {\i shoteh} and the absent husband \u8212- is now identified. {\i Rav Chisda} says: {\i ze tachshit} \u8212- this refers to jewelry or personal adornments for the wife (or, according to {\i Rashi}, to perfume \u8212- something that is an ongoing personal expense but not within the strict legal category of {\i ksus}/clothing). {\i Rav Yosef} says: {\i tzedaka} \u8212- charitable donations that the husband regularly made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relationship between these two opinions: if one holds that even {\i tachshit} is provided for the wife even when the husband has consciously abandoned the family \u8212- because {\i delo nicha lei d\u8217'titnaval}, he does not want his wife to be degraded \u8212- then {\i tzedaka} would certainly be provided in the case of incapacity. But if one holds that {\i tachshit} is provided only in the case of incapacity, not conscious abandonment, then {\i tzedaka} would certainly not be provided in the case of conscious abandonment: if {\i Beit Din} will not even provide for his own family, it will surely not make charitable donations on his behalf. {\i Tzedaka} is provided only in the case of a {\i shoteh}, not when the husband has voluntarily left.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Predeceases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Abroad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Avin} cites {\i Rav Huna}: if a man has gone abroad and his wife dies during his absence, {\i Beit Din} again descends to his property and buries her \u8212- {\i lefi kvodo}, according to his status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Higher Status Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: why {\i lefi kvodo} \u8212- his status \u8212- and not {\i lefi kvodah}, her status? What if her family\u8217's standard is higher than his? The {\i Gemara} corrects: read it as {\i af lefi kvodah} \u8212- even according to her status, meaning the {\i higher} of the two. This is the application of {\i olah immo ve\u8217'einah yoredes immo} even after death: {\i Beit Din} uses the estate to bury her at the higher of his and her family standards. As {\i Rav Chisda} ruled in the name of {\i Mar Ukva} earlier, {\i halakha ke-Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- the principle of ascent without descent applies even posthumously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deathbed Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Matna} addresses a final case. A man on his deathbed declares: {\i im meta ishti lo tikberuha minichasai} \u8212- when my wife dies after I am gone, do not bury her from my estate. Is such a declaration effective?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially analyzes the case where the husband is instructing his heirs not to use the estate to bury his wife after he dies. Since the husband\u8217's obligation to bury his wife exists only if {\i she} predeceases {\i him}, and here {\i he} is the one dying first, his estate has no such obligation to begin with \u8212- the estate passes to the heirs, and the heirs owe her no burial obligation. So the declaration seems redundant; the heirs would not be obligated even without it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Community Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore reformulates: the operative case is where a man says, before his own death: {\i im meis hu lo tikberuhu minichasav} \u8212- when I die, do not bury me from my estate. He is telling his heirs to save the estate funds and let the community bear the cost of his funeral. Here, {\i ein shom\u8217'in lo} \u8212- we do not listen to him. The heirs may be compelled to use estate funds for his burial, regardless of his expressed wish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is stated as a principle: {\i lav kol heimenu sheya\u8217'ashir et banav ve\u8217'yapil atzmo al hatzibur} \u8212- it is not within his power to enrich his sons by transferring his own burial costs to the community. A man cannot, by declaration, disinherit the community of its right not to bear avoidable expenses, and cannot enrich his heirs at communal expense. The burial costs are properly charged to the estate, and the community\u8217's obligation arises only when the estate is genuinely insufficient \u8212- not when it has been artificially preserved by a deathbed instruction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 48b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father to Husband Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Masechet Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The central preoccupation of {\i Masechet Ketubot} is the transition of a woman from her father\u8217's control to her husband\u8217's, together with the obligations and rights that attend each stage. As has been observed across the preceding {\i dapim}, the {\i masechet} presents a vision that sounds, at first blush, very much like the {\i Torah} itself: a woman moves from one man\u8217's domain to another\u8217's, from father to husband, never independent. The {\i Chachamim} do carve out a significant space for the {\i bogeret} \u8212- a woman who has reached puberty at twelve and a half \u8212- recognizing her as fully independent, but that development is rabbinic and is not the picture that the {\i Torah} itself paints. Here, the concern is with the core of the {\i masechet}: the {\i ketubah} and its obligations, the wedding, and above all, the precise moment at which {\i kiddushin} gives way to {\i nisuin}. We call that moment {\i chuppah} \u8212- but what exactly it constitutes, and precisely when it occurs, is the sustained question of our sugya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even after {\i kiddushin}, even after betrothal, the woman remains in her father\u8217's house and under her father\u8217's control. She is technically a married woman, yet the actual transition \u8212- the move to the husband\u8217's house \u8212- takes place only at {\i nisuin}. How do we identify that moment? That is what our {\i Mishnah} and the {\i Gemara} that follows set out to determine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: Reshut Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} opens at the bottom of 48a with a statement that is at once sweeping and deliberately abstract: {\i le\u8217'olam hi b\u8217'reshut ha\u8217'av ad shetikanes l\u8217'reshut haba\u8217'al l\u8217'nisuin} \u8212- she is always in the father\u8217's {\i reshut}, his control and domain, until she enters into the husband\u8217's {\i reshut} for the purposes of {\i nisuin}. Some authorities had the {\i girsa} here as {\i l\u8217'reshut haba\u8217'al l\u8217'chuppah} \u8212- until she enters into the husband\u8217's {\i reshut} for {\i chuppah} \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} will later cite a {\i baraita} that uses precisely that language. But even {\i chuppah}, as we shall see, requires definition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking here is the governing conceptual framework: {\i reshut}. The key word is not intimacy, not ceremony, not a particular act, but domain and control. She passes from one man\u8217's {\i reshut} to another\u8217's. The {\i Mishnah} does not yet tell us exactly what that transfer consists in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Cases: Messengers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then works through the cases that define the edges of this transition. {\i Masar ha\u8217'av lishluchei haba\u8217'al} \u8212- if the father handed her over to the husband\u8217's messengers \u8212- then {\i harei hi b\u8217'reshut haba\u8217'al}: even though she has not yet arrived at the husband\u8217's house, she is considered to be under the husband\u8217's control. She is being transported, accompanied by those who represent the husband; that is sufficient to constitute a transfer of {\i reshut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i halach ha\u8217'av im shluchei haba\u8217'al} \u8212- if the father himself went along with the husband\u8217's messengers \u8212- or if the father sent his own messengers to accompany the husband\u8217's messengers, then {\i harei hi b\u8217'reshut ha\u8217'av}: she remains under the father\u8217's control. The presence of the father or his representatives preserves the ambiguity. She has not yet unambiguously crossed into the husband\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final case resolves the ambiguity in the other direction: {\i masru shluchei ha\u8217'av lishluchei haba\u8217'al harei hi b\u8217'reshut haba\u8217'al} \u8212- if the father\u8217's messengers handed her off to the husband\u8217's messengers, whether they reached a point of transfer on the road or the father\u8217's messengers encountered the husband\u8217's messengers and transferred her to them and turned back, then she is under the husband\u8217's control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The picture painted by the {\i Mishnah} is, as noted, almost jarring in its conception of the woman as an object being transported \u8212- passed from one man\u8217's hand to another\u8217's like an item being acquired. And the conceptual mechanism is unmistakably {\i reshut}: whose control is she under, who is responsible for her, who represents her? The idea of {\i kinyan}, of acquisition, hovers nearby, even if the {\i Mishnah} does not use that language. But the exact definition of what makes this moment constitutive of {\i nisuin} remains, as yet, unclear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara: Mai L\u8217'Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 L\u8217'Olam Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a question on the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's opening word: {\i mai l\u8217'olam} \u8212- what does \u8220"always\u8221" mean? What is the {\i Mishnah} trying to emphasize by saying she is {\i always} in the father\u8217's {\i reshut} until {\i nisuin}? The answer is: {\i lafokei mimishnah rishonah} \u8212- to exclude, or override, the ruling of an earlier {\i Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That earlier {\i Mishnah} taught: {\i higgia z\u8217'man velo nisu ocholos mishelo v\u8217'ocholos b\u8217'trumah} \u8212- if the time for {\i nisuin} arrived and the marriage had not yet taken place, the husband must begin supporting her, and she may even eat {\i terumah}. The logic of that earlier ruling is that once {\i nisuin} should have occurred and was delayed \u8212- whether through the husband\u8217's fault or otherwise \u8212- she is already treated as being in the husband\u8217's domain for {\i terumah} purposes, since she is technically considered his wife from the time of {\i kiddushin}, and the rabbis\u8217' postponement of {\i terumah} no longer applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might therefore have inferred that once the time for {\i nisuin} arrives, she can eat {\i terumah} even while still in the father\u8217's house. Our {\i Mishnah} teaches otherwise: {\i kamashma lan l\u8217'olam} \u8212- the word \u8220"always\u8221" tells us that she does not eat {\i terumah} while still in the father\u8217's house, even if the time for {\i nisuin} has already come. As will become clear, these two {\i mishnayot} reflect different and successive positions; the {\i Gemara} itself acknowledges that the earlier {\i Mishnah} represented an earlier stage in halakhic development.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delayed Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having clarified the import of {\i le\u8217'olam}, the {\i Gemara} proceeds to the central question: what is the precise moment of transition, and is it truly all-or-nothing? The {\i Mishnah} seems to treat the handover to the husband\u8217's messengers as a clean, categorical transfer into the husband\u8217's {\i reshut}. But the {\i Gemara} will show that even within this \u8220"transfer,\u8221" there remains an intermediate stage \u8212- she has left the father\u8217's house but has not yet fully entered the husband\u8217's \u8212- and that different legal consequences attach to different points along this continuum. The paradigm case that reveals the significance of the distinction is, as we saw when the {\i Mishnah} used the language of {\i le\u8217'olam}, the right to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Rav Assi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Exclusion Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav: mesirasah lakol chutz mitrumah} \u8212- Rav says that the handover to the husband\u8217's messengers transfers her for all purposes except {\i terumah}. Although our {\i Mishnah} seems to declare her categorically in the husband\u8217's {\i reshut} once handed over to his {\i sheluchiim}, Rav holds that she does not yet eat {\i terumah} at this stage. She is in a genuine intermediate status: out of the father\u8217's {\i reshut}, not yet fully in the husband\u8217's house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi: All Purposes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Assi amar: af l\u8217'terumah} \u8212- Rav Assi disagrees and holds that the {\i Mishnah} means what it says: the handover to the husband\u8217's messengers constitutes complete {\i nisuin} for all purposes, including {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meach Ta\u8217'us Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two distinct concerns underlying the general restriction on eating {\i terumah} prior to {\i nisuin}. The first is the problem of {\i mekach ta\u8217'us} \u8212- a mistaken transaction. After {\i nisuin}, on the wedding night, the husband may discover something about his wife that leads him to seek to void the marriage on the grounds that the original {\i kiddushin} was predicated on a false assumption. Were that to succeed, the {\i kiddushin} would be retroactively invalidated, and she would have eaten {\i terumah} without any entitlement. We therefore defer her {\i terumah} rights until the moment of {\i nisuin} proper, and Rav holds that we must await her actual arrival at the husband\u8217's house \u8212- where the possibility of discovering such a defect, and of the couple consummating the marriage, first becomes real. Until that point, even technically being \u8220"on the way\u8221" in the hands of the {\i sheluchiim} is not sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's House Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi\u8217's position rests on a different understanding of why {\i terumah} is withheld. In his view, the restriction on eating {\i terumah} before {\i nisuin} is not about the concern of {\i mekach ta\u8217'us} but about the practical problem of the father\u8217's house: if {\i terumah} is sent to her while she is living there, the other members of her family who are not entitled to eat {\i terumah} will consume it. As long as she is actually in her father\u8217's house, this concern applies. But once she has left the father\u8217's house \u8212- even if she has not yet arrived at the husband\u8217's house \u8212- this rationale no longer obtains, and she may eat {\i terumah} immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction, as Rashi understands it, is really a question about what {\i terumah} entitlement is fundamentally about. If it tracks her departure from the father\u8217's domain, then Rav Assi\u8217's position is correct and she eats {\i terumah} as soon as the {\i sheluchiim} take charge. If it tracks her full entry into the husband\u8217's domain \u8212- his house, their intimacy, the genuine beginning of married life \u8212- then she must wait, as Rav holds, until she has actually arrived and that life has become possible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Challenge: Chuppah Requi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Chuppah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eiteivi Rav Huna le-Rav Assi} \u8212- Rav Huna challenged Rav Assi. The challenge came from a text \u8212- apparently either a {\i baraita} or an alternate {\i girsa} of the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- which stated: {\i le\u8217'olam hi b\u8217'reshut ha\u8217'av ad shetikanes la-chuppah} \u8212- she is always in the father\u8217's {\i reshut} until she enters the {\i chuppah}. Some attribute the challenge to Chiya bar Rav rather than Rav Huna. Either way, the point is the same: the text specifies {\i chuppah} as the defining moment, not the handover to the {\i sheluchiim}. This appears to contradict Rav Assi, who holds that the handover to the messengers is itself fully constitutive of {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Defense: Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Remarkably, it is Rav himself \u8212- Rav Assi\u8217's interlocutor and opponent in this dispute \u8212- who comes to Rav Assi\u8217's defense against the challenge. {\i Amar lehu Rav: lav amina lachu lo techuzu batar ipcha?} \u8212- Haven\u8217't I told you not to look for that which can be interpreted in the opposite way, meaning a passage that could be read in two directions? {\i Yachol leshunayei lachu} \u8212- Rav Assi could answer you: what does \u8220"until she enters the {\i chuppah}\u8221" mean? {\i Mesirata zo hi knisata la-chuppah} \u8212- the {\i chuppah} referred to is the handover to the {\i sheluchiim} of the husband. That act is itself what the {\i baraita} calls {\i chuppah}. You are assuming that {\i chuppah} refers to a later, physically distinct stage; Rav Assi can maintain that {\i chuppah} and {\i mesirah} to the {\i sheluchiim} are one and the same.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel: Yerusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-Shmuel amar: leyerushata} \u8212- Shmuel says that at the moment of {\i mesirah} to the {\i sheluchiim} of the husband, the husband now inherits her. Should she die at this point, he inherits the dowry she is bringing with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exact scope of Shmuel\u8217's statement is left open by the {\i Gemara}. Is Shmuel saying that {\i yerusha} is the {\i only} thing that has transferred \u8212- and that he agrees with Rav that nothing else, including {\i terumah}, yet applies \u8212- or is he simply citing the most obvious and concrete expression of the husband\u8217's new {\i reshut}, while leaving open that other consequences follow as well? The {\i Gemara} will return to this question. What Shmuel emphasizes, in any case, is the proprietary dimension of the transfer: she is bringing property into his domain, and once she is under his control, that property is his. The most natural consequence of her being, as it were, under his ownership is that he inherits whatever she is carrying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina: Ketubah Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Ravina: lo \u8212- mar k\u8217'tuvosah me\u8217'acher manah.} Ravina clarifies what {\i ketubah} in this context means. If the husband dies after {\i mesirah} to the {\i sheluchiim} but before she has fully entered his house, she would be considered, for the purposes of any subsequent marriage, an {\i almana min hanissu\u8217'in} \u8212- a widow from a full marriage \u8212- rather than an {\i almana min ha\u8217'erusin}, a widow from {\i erusin} alone. The significance of this distinction is monetary: a widow from {\i erusin}, who has never had the opportunity to have sexual relations with her husband, retains the status of {\i betulah} and receives a {\i ketubah} of 200 {\i zuz} upon remarriage. An {\i almana min hanissu\u8217'in} is considered, by the logic of the institution, as one who has had sexual relations and therefore receives only 100 upon remarriage \u8212- even if, as in this intermediate case, she has never actually been with her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is striking: even though she is merely being transported by {\i sheluchiim} and physical intimacy is not yet possible, she is conceptually a {\i nesuah} to the degree that a subsequent {\i ketubah} would reflect the lower amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the {\i Gemara} to observe that there appears to be significant movement away from the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's seemingly categorical statement toward an analysis that parcels out different legal consequences to different stages. What began as \u8220"she is completely in the husband\u8217's {\i reshut} once given to his {\i sheluchiim}\u8221" is now being qualified: for some things, yes; for others, no. And it is not entirely clear from Shmuel\u8217's formulation alone how much falls into each category.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rishonim Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Status View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan veRabbi Chanina d\u8217'amri tarvaihu: mesirasah lekhol af leterumah} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Chanina both say, in agreement with Rav Assi\u8217's position, that {\i mesirah} to the {\i sheluchiim} is complete {\i nisuin} for all purposes, including {\i terumah}. The {\i Mishnah} should be read straightforwardly: she is in the husband\u8217's {\i reshut} fully and without qualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this position, the {\i Gemara} raises a {\i Tosefta} that lays out the relevant cases in detail. The {\i Tosefta} addresses a situation where they are traveling to the husband\u8217's house and the woman enters a {\i chatzer} \u8212- a courtyard \u8212- on the way to spend the night. If that courtyard belongs to her, and if the physical property of the {\i ketubah} (the dowry) has already been transferred to the husband\u8217's house, she is nonetheless not a {\i nesuah}: should she die, her father inherits the dowry. The determinative factor is not where the dowry is physically located but whether she is a {\i nesuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely: {\i masar ha\u8217'av lishluchei haba\u8217'al o shemasru shluchei av lishluchei haba\u8217'al}, or alternatively, they entered a {\i chatzer} that belongs to the husband on the way, {\i venichnesa imo leshem nisuin} \u8212- and they entered it with the explicit intent of beginning married life there \u8212- then even if the physical {\i ketubah} property remains in the father\u8217's house, she is a {\i nesuah}: should she die, the husband inherits her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} then draws an explicit and crucial distinction: {\i bameh devarim amurim liyerushasah} \u8212- in what case do these rules apply? For the purposes of {\i yerusha}, inheritance. {\i Aval leterumah: ein ishah ocheles biterumah ad shetikanes lachuppah} \u8212- but for {\i terumah}, a woman does not eat until she has actually entered the {\i chuppah}, which implies her having come to the husband\u8217's house in a full sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i Tosefta} is thus highly revealing. It confirms that Shmuel and Rav are both reflecting two distinct clauses of the same source: the {\i Tosefta} says explicitly that in the intermediate stage of {\i mesirah} to the {\i sheluchiim}, {\i yerusha} has transferred, and {\i terumah} has not. Rav\u8217's statement emphasizes one end of that formulation, Shmuel\u8217's the other. What the {\i Tosefta} does not specify is what happens to all the intermediate statuses \u8212- the vow annulment, the obligation to feed her, and so on. But the poles are clear: {\i yerusha}, yes; {\i terumah}, no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tyufta Dekulhu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tyufta dekulu tyufta} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} declares that this is a refutation of all of them. Rashi explains \u8220"all of them\u8221" as those who argued on Shmuel; Tosafot extend it to all who would say that the handover to {\i sheluchiim} is fully constitutive of {\i nisuin}, since the {\i Tosefta} explicitly withholds {\i terumah} rights until {\i chuppah} proper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's conclusion at this stage is thus: the {\i Tosefta} makes clear that this is a genuine intermediate stage. What is unambiguously transferred at {\i mesirah} is {\i yerusha} \u8212- and this is intuitive, because the conceptual model underlying {\i mesirah} is {\i reshut}, domain and ownership. She is being transferred to his property-sphere, and the most immediate expression of that transfer is that the dowry she carries now falls within his {\i reshut}. {\i Yerusha} thus flows naturally from the transfer of {\i reshut}. {\i Terumah}, by contrast, sits at the other end of the conceptual spectrum: {\i terumah} is associated with them actually living together, being intimate, the beginning of a genuine marital life. That requires something more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What about all the intermediate legal consequences \u8212- obligations to feed her, the right to annul her vows, liability for her protection? These are genuinely left unresolved at this point in the {\i Gemara}. The framework that is emerging is one of two models: {\i reshut} on one side and intimacy on the other, and the question is how much each model explains and what combination is required for a fully constitutive {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reshut vs.\u160 ?Intimacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatzer Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now confronts what it describes as an internal contradiction within the {\i Tosefta} itself. The {\i Tosefta} includes a scenario in which the woman has a {\i chatzer} \u8212- a courtyard \u8212- on the way to the husband\u8217's house, and enters it with him {\i lalin} \u8212- to sleep. The {\i Tosefta} specifies that this does not constitute {\i nisuin} because the explicit purpose was merely to sleep. The implicit inference is: were it not labeled as merely sleeping, we would assume it was {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Tosefta} also says, in the case of the husband\u8217's {\i chatzer}, that entry with intent {\i leshem nisuin} constitutes {\i nisuin} \u8212- implying that without that explicit label, we would not have inferred it. The two halves seem to cut in opposite directions: in her {\i chatzer}, the default assumption absent a label is {\i nisuin}; in his {\i chatzer}, the default assumption is apparently not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: stami stami katani} \u8212- Rav Ashi resolves this by saying the {\i Tosefta} is speaking to contextual defaults in each case. {\i Stam chatzer didah} \u8212- a courtyard that is hers, without any label \u8212- the default assumption is that they are merely sleeping there; the context does not naturally suggest the beginning of married life. {\i Stam chatzer dideh} \u8212- a courtyard that is his, without any label \u8212- the default assumption is that they are entering as a married couple beginning their life together, because the conventional model is the bride moving into the groom\u8217's domain. The same principle underlies the verse {\i al ken ya\u8217'azov ish et aviv ve\u8217'et imo vedavak be\u8217'ishto} \u8212- a man leaves his father and mother and cleaves to his wife \u8212- understood throughout to mean that it is the bride who moves into the groom\u8217's world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analysis of the {\i chatzer} scenario brings into sharp relief the two conceptual models that have been implicit throughout. The first model is {\i reshut} \u8212- domain and control. {\i Nisuin} on this model is about her entering his property, his sphere of ownership, whether physically (his house, his {\i chatzer}) or representatively (his {\i sheluchiim} taking charge of her). The second model is {\i yichud} \u8212- privacy, intimacy, and ultimately sex. After the {\i chuppah}, the classic model involves the couple being alone and beginning a sexual relationship; this is what our contemporary {\i yichud} room symbolizes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The intermediate stages mapped by the {\i Gemara} track the separation and sequential acquisition of these two elements. {\i Mesirah} to the {\i sheluchiim} represents the {\i reshut} transfer without the intimacy. {\i Chuppah} in the full sense represents both together \u8212- entering his domain with the possibility and context of intimacy. And {\i nisuin mamash}, as the {\i Gemara} will shortly say explicitly, is identified with {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- a woman who has had sexual relations. These are the two models, and the strongest interpretation of our {\i Gemara} is that the ideal {\i nisuin} requires both: his {\i reshut} and the reality or possibility of intimacy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Adultery Penalties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Na\u8217'arah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now approaches the same questions from the perspective of criminal law, which provides an independent triangulation on the stages of transition. A {\i baraita} teaches: {\i masra lishluchei haba\u8217'al vezinta, harei zu bechenek} \u8212- once she has been handed over to the husband\u8217's {\i sheluchiim} and commits adultery, she is executed by strangulation ({\i chenek}) rather than stoning. This is a significant shift: a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} who commits adultery while still betrothed is executed by stoning. Once she transitions to a higher degree of marital status, she is subject to the more standard penalty for a married woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Aviha Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mena hanei milei} \u8212- what is the scriptural source for this? {\i Amar Rav Ami bar Chama: amar kra, liznot beit aviha} \u8212- the verse, in the context of {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}, specifies \u8220"to fornicate in her father\u8217's house.\u8221" This is the precondition for the stoning penalty applicable to a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}. Once she has been handed over to the husband\u8217's {\i sheluchiim} and is no longer in {\i beit aviha}, the special penalty of that status no longer applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a question: {\i ve\u8217'eima prat shenichnesa lechuppah velo niv\u8217'alah} \u8212- perhaps the exclusion should be read as applying only once she has entered the {\i chuppah}, even if she has not yet had sexual relations? That is, perhaps the verse is excluding the case of a woman who is at the stage of {\i chuppah} but still a virgin \u8212- not the earlier intermediate stage of {\i sheluchiim}. The point is well-taken: the intermediate stage of {\i sheluchiim} is not the same as {\i chuppah}; she is being brought toward his domain but is not yet physically within it. Could the verse perhaps exclude only the {\i chuppah} stage and above?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scripture Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Breakdown\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava, amar li Ami: chuppah behedya ketiva} \u8212- Rava relates that Ami told him: the exclusion of {\i chuppah} can be derived from an earlier part of the verse and need not be derived from {\i beit aviha}. The verse reads: {\i ki yihiyeh na\u8217'arah betulah me\u8217'orasah le\u8217'ish} \u8212- if there is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, a virgin, betrothed to a man. Each qualifier in the verse does work: {\i na\u8217'arah} and not {\i bogeret} \u8212- a young woman, not yet of majority; {\i betulah} and not {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- a virgin, not a woman who has had sexual relations; {\i me\u8217'orasah} and not {\i nesuah} \u8212- betrothed, not having undergone {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuppah Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is: what does {\i me\u8217'orasah velo nesuah} \u8212- betrothed and not {\i nesuah} \u8212- add to the verse? If {\i nesuah mamash} means she has fully consummated the marriage, that is already excluded by {\i betulah velo be\u8217'ulah}. So the exclusion of {\i nesuah} must refer to something distinct: {\i shenichnesah l\u8217'chuppah velo niv\u8217'alah} \u8212- she has entered the {\i chuppah} but has not yet had sexual relations. She is still a virgin \u8212- not excluded by {\i betulah} \u8212- but she is excluded from the {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} category because she is now in the stage of {\i chuppah}. Once she has entered {\i chuppah}, even as a virgin, she is no longer a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} and the special stoning penalty does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from this analysis is a tripartite structure drawn directly from the verse\u8217's own language. The first stage is {\i beit aviha} \u8212- still in the father\u8217's house, under his {\i reshut}, betrothed but not yet given over. The second is the intermediate stage \u8212- she has been given over to the {\i sheluchiim} of the husband, she has left {\i beit aviha}, but she has not yet entered {\i chuppah}. The third is {\i chuppah} \u8212- she is now in the husband\u8217's domain in a full sense, even if still a virgin. And beyond {\i chuppah} is the fourth stage: {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- sexual consummation, the marriage fully realized in body as well as in legal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stages can be mapped to the verse\u8217's own exclusions: {\i betulah velo be\u8217'ulah} excludes the stage of full consummation; {\i me\u8217'orasah velo nesuah} excludes the stage of {\i chuppah}; and {\i beit aviha} excludes the intermediate stage of {\i sheluchiim}. This is a rich and elegant {\i drasha}, drawing three stages out of a single verse \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} treats it as dispositive on the existence and legal significance of the intermediate stage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nisuin Mamash Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Be\u8217'ulah Equation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ilima nesuah mamash, hainu be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- if you read {\i nesuah mamash}, \u8220"truly {\i nesuah},\u8221" as simply meaning she has consummated the marriage, that is already excluded by the verse\u8217's earlier word {\i betulah}. Since the verse cannot contain redundant exclusions, {\i nesuah} must refer to the stage of {\i chuppah} that precedes consummation. But this inference carries a profound implication: when the {\i Gemara} asks what {\i nesuah mamash} means and says {\i hainu be\u8217'ulah}, it is identifying the paradigmatic, fully realized {\i nesuah} with the woman who has had sexual relations. The fundamental model of {\i nisuin} \u8212- not a legal stage on the way to it, but {\i nisuin} itself in its truest sense \u8212- is the woman who is living with her husband and has consummated the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combination Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This identification of {\i nesuah mamash} with {\i be\u8217'ulah} is striking. It suggests that the deeper model underlying {\i nisuin} is not merely about {\i reshut} \u8212- domain and control and property \u8212- but about the sexual and intimate reality of marriage. At the same time, the preceding analysis has shown that {\i reshut} does real legal work at each stage. The resolution toward which the {\i Gemara} points is a combination model: full {\i nisuin} requires both {\i reshut} \u8212- the woman has entered the husband\u8217's domain and he has taken possession of her in the proprietary sense \u8212- and intimacy, the possibility and actualization of sexual life together. {\i Chuppah} in our contemporary practice reflects both dimensions: the {\i chuppah} structure itself symbolizes his domain, his home canopy, and the {\i yichud} room immediately following represents the intimacy and privacy of the couple. The question of whether either alone is sufficient for {\i nisuin}, or whether both are required, is precisely the debate carried forward in the {\i Rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divorce/Death Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Return Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a further question arising from the intermediate stage. Suppose that after the woman has been given over to the {\i sheluchiim} of the husband \u8212- she has left {\i beit aviha} \u8212- the husband dies or divorces her before she has fully entered his house. Does she revert to her prior status? Does she return, as it were, to {\i beit aviha}? One might think that since she has not yet fully entered the husband\u8217's {\i reshut} \u8212- she is in transit \u8212- the transfer is incomplete and she retains some connection to the father\u8217's domain. If the husband dies, perhaps she simply returns to the father\u8217's house and is again subject to the father\u8217's authority, including his right to annul her vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Never Returns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava, hahu kvar paska Tana d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- Rava says that this question was already resolved by a teaching from the {\i Tana d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael}. The verse reads: {\i v\u8217'neder almanah u\u8217'grushah kol asher asrah al nafshah yakum aleha} \u8212- the vow of a widow or divorc\u233 ?e, whatever she has bound herself by, shall stand. The {\i Tana d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael} asks: why does a verse need to tell me that a widow or divorc\u233 ?e\u8217's vow stands? Obviously it stands \u8212- there is nobody to annul it; she is out of the father\u8217's domain and no longer in the husband\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse must therefore be addressing the intermediate case: {\i harei shemasar av lishluchei haba\u8217'al o shemasru shluchei av lishluchei haba\u8217'al} \u8212- where the father handed her over to the husband\u8217's {\i sheluchiim}, or the father\u8217's {\i sheluchiim} handed her to the husband\u8217's {\i sheluchiim}, {\i v\u8217'nitarmalah baderech o nitgarshah} \u8212- and she was widowed or divorced on the way, before fully entering the husband\u8217's house. In that case, where is she? Is she still in her father\u8217's {\i reshut}, such that her father could annul her vows? Or is she fully independent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Never Returns Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela lomar lach: kivan sheyatzah sha\u8217'ah achas mereshut ha\u8217'av, shuv eino yachol lehafer} \u8212- the verse tells us that once she has left the father\u8217's {\i reshut}, even for a moment, he can no longer annul her vows. The formulation is precise and deliberate: it does not say {\i kivan shenichnesah l\u8217'reshut haba\u8217'al} \u8212- once she entered the husband\u8217's {\i reshut}. It says {\i kivan sheyatzah mereshut ha\u8217'av} \u8212- once she left the father\u8217's {\i reshut}. The exit from one domain does not require entry into the next to be legally consequential. Once she has been given over to the {\i sheluchiim} and has left the father\u8217's control, that exit is permanent and irreversible. She does not go back. If the husband then dies or divorces her, she becomes fully independent \u8212- an {\i almanah} or {\i gerushah} who controls her own vows \u8212- but she never returns to the status of being subject to the father\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is, as noted, elegant and significant. There is a genuine intermediate stage in which a woman has left one man\u8217's {\i reshut} without yet fully entering another\u8217's. In that stage, she has certain statuses that track the husband\u8217's domain ({\i yerusha}, and presumably the penalty of {\i chenek} for adultery rather than {\i sekilah}) and others that she does not yet have ({\i terumah}). But what she never reverts to is the father\u8217's {\i reshut}. The exit is a one-way door.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakhic Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Papa af anan nami tanina} \u8212- Rav Papa offers a further proof from a {\i mishnah} that the intermediate stage between {\i beit aviha} and {\i chuppah} is legally distinct. The teaching is: {\i haba al naarah hameorasah eino chayav ad shetehei naarah besulah meorasah v\u8217'hi b\u8217'veis aviha} \u8212- one who has relations with a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} does not incur the stoning penalty unless she is a young woman, a virgin, betrothed, and still in her father\u8217's house. The verse specifies all these qualifications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bishlama naarah velo bogeres} \u8212- the exclusion of {\i bogeret} makes sense; {\i besulah velo be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- the exclusion of one who has had sexual relations makes sense; {\i me\u8217'orasah velo nesuah} \u8212- she must be betrothed and not yet in the stage of {\i nisuin}, makes sense. But what does {\i b\u8217'veis aviha} \u8212- \u8220"in her father\u8217's house\u8221" \u8212- come to exclude? {\i Mai lav l\u8217'mauti masar av lishluchei haba\u8217'al} \u8212- does it not come to exclude the very intermediate stage where she has been given over to the {\i sheluchiim} but has not yet reached {\i chuppah}? The {\i sekilah} penalty requires {\i beit aviha}, and once she has left the father\u8217's house \u8212- even before reaching {\i chuppah} \u8212- that penalty no longer applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman: Strangulati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak af anan nami tanina} \u8212- Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak adds a parallel proof. The {\i mishnah} teaches: {\i haba al eshes ish kivan shenichnesah l\u8217'reshut haba\u8217'al l\u8217'nisuin, af al pi shelo niv\u8217'alah, haba alei harei zeh b\u8217'chenek} \u8212- one who has relations with a married woman, once she has entered the husband\u8217's {\i reshut} for the purposes of {\i nisuin}, even if she has not yet had sexual relations, the penalty is strangulation. {\i Nichnesah l\u8217'reshut haba\u8217'al b\u8217'alma} \u8212- merely entering the husband\u8217's {\i reshut}, not yet fully {\i chuppah}, not yet consummation, just beginning to enter his {\i reshut} once given over to the {\i sheluchiim} \u8212- that is sufficient. {\i Shma mina}: once she has left {\i beit aviha} and is in the hands of the {\i sheluchiim}, she is no longer subject to the {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} regime, and the husband\u8217's marital status attaches to her for the purposes of the {\i chenek} penalty. The husband inherits her. But the full suite of {\i nisuin} consequences \u8212- {\i terumah}, and all that {\i nisuin mamash} entails \u8212- awaits the full entry into {\i chuppah} and beyond.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Theoretical Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rishonim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains as the fundamental unresolved question, and the central debate in the {\i Rishonim}, is: what is {\i chuppah} in its essence? Is {\i chuppah} fundamentally an act of {\i reshut} \u8212- her entry into his domain and property-sphere \u8212- or is it fundamentally an act of intimacy \u8212- the couple\u8217's privacy, the possibility of sexual relations \u8212- or is it necessarily the combination of both?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our {\i Gemara} has pointed strongly in the direction of {\i reshut} throughout: the language of the {\i Mishnah} is {\i reshut}, the cases analyzed are about whose {\i chatzer} and whose {\i sheluchiim}, and the most immediate consequence of the transfer is {\i yerusha}, which is about property. But the equation of {\i nesuah mamash} with {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- the paradigmatic married woman as one who has had sexual relations \u8212- suggests that intimacy is not merely a byproduct of {\i nisuin} but is in some sense its telos. And the two elements that contemporary practice divides between {\i chuppah} and {\i yichud room} \u8212- his domain and their privacy \u8212- correspond precisely to these two models. The question of whether {\i chuppah} without {\i yichud}, or {\i yichud} without ownership of the space, satisfies the requirement of {\i chuppah} at all is one that flows directly from the implicit tensions in this {\i sugyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the structure that has emerged: there are at minimum four stages. The first is {\i beit aviha} \u8212- she is in the father\u8217's house, betrothed, under the father\u8217's full {\i reshut}. The second is the intermediate stage of {\i mesirah} to the {\i sheluchiim} \u8212- she has left {\i beit aviha}, she is under the husband\u8217's control in a limited sense, {\i yerusha} has transferred, the {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} regime of {\i sekilah} no longer applies, she does not return to the father\u8217's {\i reshut} even if widowed, but she does not yet eat {\i terumah} and is not yet fully {\i nesuah}. The third is {\i chuppah} \u8212- she is in the husband\u8217's domain in the full sense, even if still a virgin, {\i terumah} now applies (according to the {\i Tosefta}), she is fully {\i nesuah} for all purposes short of consummation. The fourth is {\i be\u8217'ulah mamash} \u8212- consummation, the fullest realization of {\i nisuin}. The different legal consequences \u8212- {\i yerusha}, {\i terumah}, the nature of the criminal penalty, the {\i ketubah} amount for subsequent marriage, the right of vow annulment \u8212- distribute across these stages in ways that reflect the two underlying models of {\i reshut} and intimacy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Support Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now moves to a new {\i Mishnah}: {\i ha\u8217'av eino chayav bimzonot bito} \u8212- the father is not legally obligated to support his daughters. This has been implicit in the discussions of the preceding days, and now it is stated explicitly. It is worth noting that the father is not legally obligated to support his sons either \u8212- but the {\i Mishnah} frames the ruling in terms of daughters, and the {\i Gemara} will explain why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midrash Banim/Banot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then tells us: {\i ze midrash darash Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah lifnei Chachamim be-Kerem be-Yavneh} \u8212- this is a {\i midrash} that Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah expounded before the sages at Kerem be-Yavneh, the rabbinic academy established after the {\i churban ha-bayit}. The connection to the occasion of his appointment as {\i Av Beit Din} is implicit in the familiar phrase {\i harei ani ke-ven shivim shanah} \u8212- \u8220"I am like one who is seventy years old.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i midrash} is: {\i ha-banim yirshu veha-banot yizonu} \u8212- \u8220"the sons shall inherit and the daughters shall be sustained.\u8221" What is the source of this text? It is one of the standard clauses of the {\i ketubah} \u8212- specifically, the tenaim, the conditional promises embedded in the {\i ketubah}. Expounding on the language of the {\i ketubah} as one would expound on Scripture \u8212- making precise inferences from its formulation \u8212- is itself noteworthy and testifies to the antiquity and authority the {\i ketubah}\u8217's language had achieved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah is drawing on two distinct clauses of the {\i ketubah}. The first is the promise to support the daughters: a husband promises his wife that if he dies and their daughters are not yet married, the daughters will continue to be supported by the estate until they reach {\i bagrut} \u8212- maturity \u8212- or until they marry. This protects the daughters who, unlike sons, do not inherit. The second is the promise regarding the sons: since a man may have children from multiple wives, and all children would ordinarily share equally in the estate including the dowry that each wife brought in, wife number one\u8217's dowry would otherwise be diluted by the claims of children of wife number two. To prevent this, the husband promises: the dowry that each wife brings in will be computed separately and will go only to the sons born of that wife. Hence the {\i ketubah} formula {\i banim yirshu} \u8212- sons shall inherit \u8212- specifically meaning: your sons, our joint sons, shall inherit your portion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah performs a {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- an analogy based on shared language \u8212- between these two clauses. Just as {\i banim yirshu} refers to inheritance that takes effect only after the father\u8217's death, so too {\i banot yizonu} \u8212- daughters shall be sustained \u8212- refers only to sustenance that takes effect after the father\u8217's death. While the father is alive, he has no legal obligation to support the daughters. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's ruling thus rests on this formal analogy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper reason for emphasizing that there is no obligation to the daughters specifically is this: one might have thought that since daughters need to be kept at home and protected \u8212- and indeed the father does protect them in the {\i ketubah} after his death \u8212- there is some legal obligation to protect them even during his lifetime. The {\i midrash} says no: the legal protection kicks in only after death, precisely analogous to the sons\u8217' inheritance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaim Debate: Chiyuv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the underlying {\i machlokes} among {\i Tannaim}. A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i mitzvah lazon et habbanot kal vachomer labbanim, lo chova} \u8212- it is a {\i mitzvah}, a religious-moral obligation but not a legal {\i chiyuv}, to feed the daughters, and certainly \u8212- by {\i kal vachomer} \u8212- to feed the sons. Why certainly the sons? The {\i Gemara} glosses: {\i de\u8217'askei baTorah} \u8212- because the sons are engaged in Torah study, which gives additional weight to their support. This gloss, as noted, is clearly an interpolation by the {\i Gemara}; the shift to Aramaic marks it as such. This is the position of Rabbi Meir. For both sons and daughters, no legal obligation \u8212- but yes, a {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah agrees that there is no legal {\i chiyuv} for either, but he reaches the same conclusion about daughters by the opposite {\i kal vachomer}. {\i Rabbi Yehudah omer: mitzvah lazon et habbanim vekal vachomer labbanot} \u8212- it is a {\i mitzvah} to support the sons, and certainly there is a {\i mitzvah} to support the daughters. Why certainly the daughters? The {\i Gemara} glosses: {\i mishum ziluta} \u8212- because of the shame and degradation that would result from daughters being forced to go out into the street and beg or fend for themselves. You protect your daughters\u8217' dignity even more than your sons\u8217', since the sons can apprentice themselves and enter the wider world, whereas daughters are kept at home. So if there is a {\i mitzvah} for sons, there is certainly a {\i mitzvah} for daughters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka omer: chova lazon et habbanot le\u8217'achar mitat avihen} \u8212- there is a legal {\i chiyuv} to feed the daughters after the father\u8217's death. {\i Aval bechayei avihen, elu ve\u8217'elu einan nizzonin} \u8212- but during the father\u8217's lifetime, neither sons nor daughters are legally entitled to support. And from the context, Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka appears to hold that during the father\u8217's lifetime there is not even a {\i mitzvah}. The legal obligation exists only as a posthumous commitment through the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mani matnitin?} \u8212- which {\i Tanna} does our {\i Mishnah} represent? The {\i Gemara} tests each possibility. If it is Rabbi Meir: his position is that there is a {\i mitzvah} but no {\i chiyuv} for both sons and daughters. But we have been reading our {\i Mishnah} as implying \u8212- by inference from its mention only of daughters \u8212- that there is a legal obligation for sons. That inference contradicts Rabbi Meir, who explicitly says there is no {\i chiyuv} even for sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If it is Rabbi Yehudah: he similarly holds there is a {\i mitzvah} but no {\i chiyuv} for either, which conflicts with the same inference from our {\i Mishnah} that a {\i chiyuv} exists for sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logical Inference Teachi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If it is Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka: he holds there is a {\i chiyuv} for daughters only after the father\u8217's death, implying no {\i mitzvah} at all during his lifetime. But our {\i Mishnah}, read inferentially, seems to say there is a {\i mitzvah} for daughters \u8212- since it says no {\i chiyuv} for daughters, implying there is a {\i mitzvah}. That contradicts Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by pointing out that all three of these inferences from our {\i Mishnah} are incorrect \u8212- or rather, that our {\i Mishnah} can be reconciled with each of the three {\i Tannaim} in turn. The key observation is that the {\i Mishnah} uses daughters specifically in order to teach us something by logical inference, but the inference drawn will differ depending on which {\i Tanna} one follows. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's purpose in singling out daughters is pedagogical: to teach us the inference in each direction from the more surprising case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Follows Yehudah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'iba\u8217'it eima Rabbi Yehudah, vehachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- if we follow Rabbi Yehudah, the {\i Mishnah} is saying: the father has no legal obligation to feed his daughter \u8212- and all the more so, no legal obligation to feed his son. We might have thought there was a legal obligation specifically to the daughter because of the father\u8217's heightened responsibility to protect her and because the {\i ketubah} obligates the estate to support her after death. The {\i Mishnah} therefore specifies daughters to teach us: even there, no {\i chiyuv}. And yet \u8212- {\i ha mitzvah bevito ika, vekal vachomer livanim} \u8212- there remains a {\i mitzvah}, and by {\i kal vachomer} for the sons as well. The most natural reading of the {\i Mishnah} follows the contours of Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's position, and the {\i Gemara} concludes accordingly. The implications of this for the practical obligation to support one\u8217's children, and the distinction between {\i chiyuv} and {\i mitzvah} as categories of obligation, will continue to be developed in the discussion that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 50\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Feeding Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 49b, continuing the Gemara\u8217's treatment of the father\u8217's obligation to feed his children. The Mishnah had ruled that there is no enforceable obligation to feed daughters; the Gemara extended this to sons as well \u8212- which is somewhat counterintuitive, since one might have expected a stronger obligation toward daughters, who had fewer means of self-support in that society. The apparent {\i pshat} of the Mishnah follows Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, which seems to reflect precisely that concern for daughters\u8217' vulnerability, and yet the Mishnah concludes that neither sons nor daughters carry an enforceable claim. There is, to be sure, a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- a moral and religious imperative \u8212- to feed one\u8217's children, but nothing the court can actually enforce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Infants Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to how this played out in practice, it is worth noting a brief {\i Tosafot} at the top of 49b, {\i d\u8217'Shem ketanim}. Tosafot observes: {\i aval kitanei kitanim l\u8217'chulei alma chayav} \u8212- that with respect to very young infants, everyone agrees there is an obligation. As the Gemara will demonstrate later, when children are still in their infant stage the obligation exists; it is only once they are a few years old that the enforceability falls away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanat Usha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then turns to the question of what happens when a father simply does not provide. Rabbi Ila says in the name of Reish Lakish in the name of Rabbi Yehuda bar Chanina: {\i be-Usha hitkinu she-adam zan et banav v\u8217'et benotav k\u8217'shehen ketanim} \u8212- in Usha they established that a person must feed his sons and daughters while they are still minors. Rashi explains that Usha was one of the stations to which the Sanhedrin relocated as conditions worsened in the period leading up to the {\i churban ha-bayit}, and in that period a series of enactments \u8212- {\i takanos} \u8212- were issued. Today\u8217's daf contains several of these {\i takanos}, which are largely unrelated to one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruling Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking is that the Gemara immediately raises a question: {\i ibaya lehu \u8212- hilchata kevataih o ein hilchata kevataih?} \u8212- do we actually rule in accordance with this {\i takanat Usha} or not? This is remarkable because, in general, {\i takanos Usha} are assumed to carry binding force \u8212- this was the Sanhedrin, after all, legislating formally. Yet the Gemara seems to understand that this particular {\i takana} was not universally accepted or embraced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Shaming\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to illustrate how the Amoraim actually handled cases where children appeared before them claiming their father was not feeding them. When such cases came before Rabbi Yehuda, he would say: {\i yarod yalda v\u8217'avnei mata shadya?} \u8212- would a snake give birth and cast its responsibility onto the city? As Rashi explains, {\i ha-tanin holid toldotav v\u8217'hital al b\u8217'nei ha-ir} \u8212- only the cruelest of creatures produces offspring and then burdens others with the responsibility of caring for them. The implication is clear: the Gemara reads this as proof that Rabbi Yehuda did not rule in accordance with the {\i takana}. If he had, he would simply have declared the father legally obligated to pay; instead, his response was rhetorical \u8212- an attempt to shame rather than to compel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marketplace Proclamation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When cases came before Rav Chisda, his method was different: {\i kofu lei asita b\u8217'tzibora} \u8212- he would have a mortar turned upside down in the marketplace, serving as a kind of soapbox, and someone would stand on it and proclaim: {\i orva bei b\u8217'nei} \u8212- even a raven cares for its children \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hahu gavra lo bei b\u8217'nei} \u8212- and yet this man does not care for his own children and allows them to starve while refusing to provide their food. Rashi notes some ambiguity as to whether it was the father himself who was forced to make this proclamation or a public crier who made it on his behalf, but either way the mechanism is the same: a public notice, in effect, naming a neglectful father \u8212- not a legal obligation. The social pressure is real, but the legal apparatus is not deployed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why the courts did not simply adopt the {\i takanat Usha} and use its full enforcement power rather than resorting to public embarrassment. This mirrors what is sometimes done today in {\i agunah} cases \u8212- when legal recourse is unavailable, shame becomes the instrument of last resort. The Gemara, whatever its reasons, clearly understood that reality in these cases was that the courts were not empowered to do more, and this is what they were left with. There is, it must be said, an unmistakable undertone of frustration in these narratives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raven Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The choice of the raven as the example of a creature that cares for its young immediately invites a challenge from the Gemara itself: {\i v\u8217'orva bei b\u8217'nei?} \u8212- does a raven actually care for its children? A verse is cited indicating that God provides for the children of the raven that cry out \u8212- implying that the raven does {\i not} take care of its own young and that divine providence steps in instead. How then can one say that failing to feed one\u8217's children is worse than a raven\u8217's behavior?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: {\i ha b\u8217'chiveri ha b\u8217'uchmi} \u8212- there are different types of ravens, some white and some black. Tosafot understands this as referring to different species, while Rashi explains it in terms of developmental stages: newborn ravens are white, and at that stage the parent ravens do not recognize them as their own and do not care for them. Once the young ravens begin to darken and look like ravens, the parents take over. So some ravens do in fact care for their young, and the proclamation in the marketplace remains apt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzedakah Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When cases came before Rava, his approach was more targeted but still not strictly legal enforcement: {\i nicha lach d\u8217'miznu bnach mi-tzedaka?} \u8212- are you comfortable with your children being supported by charity funds? He sought to stir shame, not issue a ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces an important qualification: {\i v\u8217'lo amran ela d\u8217'lo amid} \u8212- all of this discussion about shaming but not compelling applies only where the father is not a man of means. {\i Aval amid} \u8212- but if he is wealthy \u8212- {\i kafinan lei al korchei} \u8212- then we do compel him. The compulsion, however, is not based on a legal obligation to feed children per se; rather, it is based on the obligation of {\i tzedaka}. A court can compel a person of means to contribute to {\i tzedaka}, and if we can compel that, we can direct that {\i tzedaka} toward his children \u8212- for {\i tzedaka} begins at home, and his children are a primary obligation. This raises, as an aside, the interesting question of {\i ma\u8217'aser kesafim}: if technically a father is not obligated to feed his children, then the money he spends on their food is, strictly speaking, {\i tzedaka} \u8212- and one might ask whether that expenditure could count toward one\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'aser} obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As a proof that courts were indeed empowered to compel {\i tzedaka} contributions, the Gemara cites the case of Rava compelling Rav Natan bar Ami: {\i v\u8217'afik minei arba me\u8217'ah zuzei l\u8217'tzedakah} \u8212- he extracted four hundred {\i zuz} from him for {\i tzedaka}. This demonstrates the principle in action, though it is not specifically about feeding children \u8212- it is simply the precedent that coercive power over {\i tzedaka} exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that in terms of personal {\i tzedaka} obligations, there is no truly quantifiable biblical minimum that one is required to give. The city, however, would organize its own {\i tzedaka} collections \u8212- a {\i kupa}, a {\i tamchui}, and other mechanisms that functioned almost like a social services tax, assessing contributions based on wealth. It is that community-organized compulsion that empowers a court to force a man of means to provide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a sharp question on this entire mechanism: {\i v\u8217'im tomar heichi kafyei mishum tzedaka v\u8217'haktiv matan sechara b\u8217'tzida} \u8212- how can you compel someone to give {\i tzedaka} when the Torah itself indicates the reward for doing so, and the Gemara elsewhere in {\i Kol HaBasar} rules that any positive commandment whose reward is stated alongside it \u8212- {\i kol mitzvat asei she-matan secharan b\u8217'tzida} \u8212- is not one that {\i beit din} can compel? The example elsewhere is forcing someone to put on {\i tallit} and {\i tefillin} or sit in a {\i sukkah}. {\i Tzedaka} appears to fall into that same category, given the explicit blessing promised for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers two answers. First: {\i v\u8217'yesh lomar d\u8217'achfyei bi-dvarim} \u8212- perhaps the compulsion was only through words, not physical force, which then brings us back close to where we started, since verbal pressure is essentially what the shaming approach involved all along. Second, and more significant: {\i v\u8217'yesh omrim ee nami kitzvu beineihem bnei ha-ir latet kach v\u8217'kach l\u8217'chol chodesh} \u8212- the townspeople may have collectively stipulated a fixed monthly contribution for each resident, and as the Gemara in the first chapter of Bava Batra establishes, {\i resha\u8217'in bnei ha-ir l\u8217'hasia al kitzotan} \u8212- the members of a city are empowered to enforce their own communal ordinances. This is not about the biblical {\i tzedaka} obligation per se, with its uncertain minimum, but about a community\u8217's self-governing authority to set and enforce contribution quotas. Tosafot\u8217's analysis here has direct implications for the mechanisms of Jewish communal self-governance throughout the medieval period, when Jewish communities were working out precisely how to enforce internally generated rules.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The bottom line of this entire {\i takana} is that the obligation to feed one\u8217's children is either a {\i takana} that the Gemara seems not to have adopted in practice, or a general {\i mitzvah} within the framework of {\i tzedaka} \u8212- and the question of how to compel compliance when the father refuses involves a combination of social shame and, for the wealthy, the community\u8217's coercive power over {\i tzedaka} allocations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Property Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara moves to a second {\i takanat Usha}. Rabbi Ila says in the name of Reish Lakish: {\i be-Usha hitkinu ha-kotev kol nechasav l\u8217'vanav} \u8212- in Usha they established that if a person writes over all of his property to his sons during his lifetime \u8212- perhaps as a tax arrangement, perhaps simply as a gift \u8212- {\i hu v\u8217'ishto nizonin mei-hem} \u8212- he and his wife may continue to be supported from that property. The wife\u8217's {\i mezonot} rights arise from her {\i ketubah} obligations; the husband, who has signed everything away, has no obvious legal claim of his own. Nevertheless, this {\i takana} establishes that both may continue to draw support from the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira challenges this ruling \u8212- or, some say, it was Rav Shmuel bar Nachmani who challenges \u8212- by noting: {\i gedolim mizo amru} \u8212- they have said something even greater than this. What is that greater ruling? {\i Almanato nizonet mi-nechasav} \u8212- that even a man\u8217's widow is supported from his property after his death. If a widow\u8217's right survives, then surely the rights of a living husband and wife should follow {\i a fortiori}: {\i hu v\u8217'ishto mibaya} \u8212- that he and his wife can be supported goes without saying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow Lien\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for the widow\u8217's rights comes from a letter sent by Ravin from Eretz Yisrael. The case: {\i mi she-meis v\u8217'hini\u8217'ach almana u-vas} \u8212- a man died leaving a widow and a daughter. The widow is entitled to {\i mezonot} from the estate; the daughter is also supported by the estate until she marries. Now, the daughter subsequently marries and the property passes with her into the hands of her new husband, who exercises control over it. Nevertheless, {\i almanato nizonet mi-nechasav} \u8212- the widow continues to be supported from that property even now that it is in the son-in-law\u8217's hands. Further, if the daughter then dies and the husband inherits from her, the widow\u8217's claim persists still. This was the ruling stated by Rabbi Yehuda ben achoso shel Rabbi Yossi bar Chanina: {\i alai hayah ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- this is a case that actually came before me, and the widow continued to be supported even after the property passed through multiple hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying legal question involves the concept of {\i shibudim} \u8212- liens. Generally, the wife\u8217's {\i mezonot} lien does not follow property into the hands of a purchaser for value, because an open-ended, ongoing obligation of uncertain duration is considered unfair to impose on someone who paid money for the property in good faith. A daughter supported until she reaches {\i bogeret} has at least a defined endpoint; a widow could theoretically claim {\i mezonot} for decades. Normally such liens survive only against the estate itself \u8212- when the sons inherit, the lien is exacted against them. When it sells out of the estate, the lien ordinarily falls away. Yet Ravin\u8217's letter establishes that even when the property passes through the daughter to the son-in-law, and even when the daughter dies and the son-in-law inherits, the widow\u8217's claim persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that, says Rabbi Zeira\u8217's challenge, if the widow\u8217's lien survives transfer through the daughter and into the hands of the son-in-law \u8212- who is now a third party \u8212- surely in the case of the {\i takanat Usha} where the father merely transferred to his own sons and the property has not truly left the family\u8217's orbit, the husband and wife should plainly continue to be supported.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Living vs.\u160 ?Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, does not accept the {\i kal va-homer} as self-evident. It responds: {\i mahu d\u8217'teima, hasam hu d\u8217'leika detarach} \u8212- one might have thought those are actually different cases. There, the man is dead. The widow cannot reasonably be expected to go out and earn a living in that society, and so we extend the lien out of consideration for her situation. {\i Aval hacha nitrach l\u8217'didei u\u8217'l\u8217'dida} \u8212- but here the man is still alive. He chose to give everything away. One might have said: he has no money, let him go work and support himself and his wife. Therefore the {\i takana} establishes that even though he is still alive, having transferred all his property to his children, he and his wife retain the right to be supported from that estate \u8212- a ruling the Gemara likens, in its social dimension, to the tragedy of King Lear, who gave everything away and was then left without care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grateful Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks again whether we actually rule in accordance with this {\i takanat Usha}: {\i ibayu lehu \u8212- hilchasa k\u8217'vataih d\u8217'Usha o lo?} The proof-text offered is the story of Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Chanina standing together when a man approached, bowed down, and kissed Rabbi Yochanan on his feet in gratitude. Rabbi Chanina asked what prompted such extraordinary thanks. Rabbi Yochanan explained: this was a man who had written over all his property to his sons, and {\i v\u8217'asitinu l\u8217'zanei} \u8212- I compelled the sons to support him. The Gemara reasons: if we say this is not the strict law \u8212- {\i lav dina} \u8212- then this explains why the man was so deeply grateful, because Rabbi Yochanan had done something beyond the letter of the law through extra-legal persuasion. But if we say it is the law, then either Rabbi Yochanan was merely doing what was required \u8212- hardly cause for such gratitude \u8212- or the sons required compulsion despite being legally obligated, which also raises questions. Either way, the gratitude reflects that legal obligation was not at play.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruling Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion, again striking, is that both {\i takanos Usha} \u8212- the one about feeding minor children and the one about the man who transfers his property \u8212- are handled through social pressure and persuasion rather than strict legal enforcement. The Gemara does not explain why the {\i beit din} would not simply adopt rulings made by the Sanhedrin, and this remains genuinely puzzling, since one would expect precisely such a formal institution to have its enactments respected. Nevertheless, the Gemara\u8217's understanding is clear: these {\i takanos} were not fully adopted as binding law.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Charity Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 20% Cap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i takanat Usha} is perhaps the most famous: {\i amar Rabbi Il\u8217'a \u8212- be-Usha hitkinu ha-mevazbe z al yevazbe z yoter mi-chomesh} \u8212- a person who wishes to distribute his wealth should not give away more than one-fifth to {\i tzedaka}. This enactment appears against a historical backdrop in which certain movements \u8212- and the Gemara is implicitly pushing back against ascetic tendencies contemporary to the Rabbinic period \u8212- encouraged giving away all of one\u8217's wealth as a form of religious devotion. The Rabbis firmly opposed this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara confirms this with a {\i baraita}: {\i ha-mevazbez al yevazbe z yoter mi-chomesh shema yitzarech la-briyot} \u8212- one who scatters his wealth should not give away more than twenty percent, lest he himself become impoverished and dependent on others. Even the framing is instructive: it does not say give as much as you can up to twenty percent. It says {\i do not exceed} twenty percent. The concern is fundamentally social and economic: a productive, self-sufficient person who gives steadily is more valuable to the poor than one who impoverishes himself in a dramatic gesture and then becomes a burden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Excessive Giver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A story illustrates the principle: {\i u-ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'echad she-bikeish levazbe z yoter mi-chomesh} \u8212- there was someone who wanted to give away more than twenty percent, {\i v\u8217'lo hini\u8217'ah lo chaveiro} \u8212- and his companion did not permit him. Who was this? The Gemara offers two versions. In one, the one who wanted to give was Rabbi Yeshevav and his companion who stopped him was Rabbi Akiva. In the alternate version, it is reversed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scriptural basis is derived from the verse {\i v\u8217'chol asher titten li aseir a\u8217'asrenu lach} \u8212- \u8220"everything You give me, I will give a tenth thereof.\u8221" The doubling of the root \u8212- {\i aseir a\u8217'asrenu} \u8212- is read as two tithes: give a tenth, and then give a tenth again. That yields twenty percent as the maximum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Tithes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately notes a mathematical objection: {\i ha lo dami isura basra l\u8217'isura kama} \u8212- the second tenth is not the same as the first. If one begins with one hundred and gives ten percent, ninety remains; ten percent of ninety is nine, yielding a total of nineteen, not twenty. Rav Ashi resolves this: {\i basra k\u8217'kama} \u8212- the verse {\i a\u8217'asrenu} means the second tithe is computed on the same original base as the first. Both tenths are calculated from the original amount, yielding twenty percent in total.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chain Weakness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shimi bar Ashi then observes: {\i b\u8217'shmu\u8217'os hallalu mismat\u8217'os v\u8217'holchos} \u8212- these traditions become progressively weaker in their chains of transmission. What he means is structural: looking at the three {\i takanos Usha} presented thus far, each successive {\i takana} has a shorter citation chain than the previous one. The first was transmitted as: {\i amar Rabbi Il\u8217'a, amar Reish Lakish, mishum Rabbi Yehuda bar Chanina} \u8212- three links. The second dropped one: {\i amar Reish Lakish, mishum Rabbi Yehuda bar Chanina}. The third is cited simply as {\i amar Rabbi Il\u8217'a} \u8212- only one link. The {\i siman} the Gemara provides to remember the order is: {\i ktanim kasvu bizbezu} \u8212- children, wrote, squandered \u8212- corresponding to the three {\i takanos}: feeding minor children, writing over property, and squandering money in {\i tzedaka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: does a shorter chain actually mean weaker transmission? If the tradition reached Rabbi Il\u8217'a through his teachers regardless, does it matter how many links are cited? The answer is that the citation chain reflects not merely who received the teaching but who first publicly stated or formally published it. {\i Amar Rabbi Il\u8217'a} means that until Rabbi Il\u8217'a\u8217's time, this teaching had not been formally circulated \u8212- even if it had been passed down privately through a chain of teachers. So a shorter chain means that the tradition surfaced at a later stage, and the irony is that while the Gemara does not {\i pasken} like the first two {\i takanos Usha}, this third one \u8212- the twenty percent limit on {\i tzedaka} \u8212- is in fact accepted as {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a critical question: twenty percent of {\i what}? The {\i Yerushalmi}, Tosafot notes, understands it as follows: {\i pa\u8217'am rishona yachol levazbe z chomesh chomesh u-mikan va-eilach b\u8217'chol shana v\u8217'shana yitein chomesh me-ha-revach} \u8212- the first time, one may give twenty percent of one\u8217's principal, one\u8217's accumulated capital. Thereafter, one gives twenty percent of one\u8217's annual income, not of total assets, since giving twenty percent of total assets each year would rapidly reduce a person to poverty. This distinction between principal and income is significant: ordinarily {\i ma\u8217'aser} is calculated on income, but here, since we are speaking of someone who wishes to donate major assets, the {\i Yerushalmi} addresses that case specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The twenty percent figure also roughly parallels the total agricultural obligations of the earlier system: {\i teruma}, {\i ma\u8217'aser}, {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, along with {\i bikkurim} and other {\i matanos}, together approach approximately twenty percent of agricultural produce. In a monetary economy divorced from agricultural tithes, the {\i takana} essentially imports that same benchmark.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question raised in passing \u8212- whether the twenty percent limit applies even to the extraordinarily wealthy, for whom the social harm of reducing assets might be negligible \u8212- remains genuinely open. One might argue that large fortunes productively invested in the economy are themselves a social good, and that even the mega-rich should refrain from dispersing their capital beyond this threshold. The {\i takana} as stated draws no distinction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Education\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age 12\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents another {\i takanat Usha}, this time addressing not feeding children but educating them: {\i amar Rabbi Yitzchak \u8212- be-Usha hitkinu she-adam mitgalgel im beno ad sheim-esrei shana} \u8212- in Usha they established that a father must {\i mitgalgel} \u8212- roll along \u8212- with his son, meaning guide and nurture him in Torah study, until the age of twelve. The image is of a patient, supportive style of education.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After 12\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mikan va-eilach} \u8212- from that point, if the son is still not applying himself: {\i yored lo le-hayyav} \u8212- one descends into his very livelihood and life. Rashi explains this concretely: {\i be-dochka be-ratzu\u8217'a} \u8212- with physical force, striking him with a strap, {\i o be-hosekh lechem} \u8212- or by withholding food. This is a strikingly different educational philosophy from what modern readers expect, though it must be understood within the context of an era in which physical discipline was the norm, and the stakes of Torah education were considered existential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age 6 School\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara questions whether this picture is accurate, since Rav had told Rav Shmuel bar Shilat \u8212- a teacher of young children \u8212- {\i batzir mi-bar sheish lo takbil, bar sheish kabil} \u8212- do not accept a student under the age of six into school; from six, accept him. And then: {\i v\u8217'safi lei ke-tora} \u8212- stuff it into him like an ox. The image here is of force-feeding: just as oxen were force-fed by having food pressed directly into their throats, so too a six-year-old student should have knowledge driven into him with real intensity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demanding vs.\u160 ?Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent tension: {\i in, v\u8217'safi lei ke-tora \u8212- mihu eino yored lo le-hayyav ad le-achar sheim-esrei shana} \u8212- yes, you stuff the material in with force and intensity from age six, but you do not descend into his personal life \u8212- that is, you do not beat him or withhold food \u8212- until after the age of twelve. The distinction is between a demanding curriculum and demanding school environment on one hand, and physically invasive forms of discipline on the other. The former begins at six; the latter is reserved until twelve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further tension remains with the {\i Mishnah} in Avot, which assigns Torah study to age five, not six. The Gemara offers one resolution: {\i ha le-mikra ha le-mishna} \u8212- the age-six threshold applies to {\i Mikra} (written Torah), while the more patient, nurturing style continues until twelve in the context of {\i Mishnah} study, after which more forceful methods may be applied. A {\i memra} of Abaye adds further granularity: his nursemaid \u8212- who served as his foster mother \u8212- told him: {\i bar sheish le-mikra, bar eser le-mishnah, bar telasar le-tani\u8217'ta me-eit le-eit} \u8212- at six, begin Torah; at ten, Mishnah; at thirteen, full-day fasting. (For girls, the threshold for full fasting arrives at twelve \u8212- one year before they reach {\i bat mitzvah}, according to most {\i Rishonim}, though Tosafot disagrees on this point.) In the period before the relevant birthday, partial fasts are appropriate; the full fast-day is reserved for after {\i bar} or {\i bat mitzvah}. The implication for the {\i takana} is that from ten to twelve a nurturing approach continues, and from twelve onward more forceful educational methods may be employed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursemaid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, now having introduced Abaye\u8217's nursemaid as a source of wisdom, continues with additional teachings attributed to her. {\i Ha-bar sheish de-tarka lei akrava be-yoma de-mishlem shit \u8212- lo chayei} \u8212- if a scorpion stings a six-year-old on the very day he turns six, he will not live. The cure, as Rashi understands it (applicable once the sting has occurred), is {\i mrarta de-daya chivarta be-shichra} \u8212- the bile of a white vulture mixed with mead, which is then rubbed on the child and given to him to drink. Similarly, if a hornet stings a six-year-old on his birthday, {\i lo chayei} \u8212- he will not survive \u8212- unless one takes {\i atzpata de-dikla be-maya} \u8212- a vine from a palm tree, boiled in water, and rubs it on the child and has him drink it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Katina then adds: {\i kol ha-machnis et bno pachos mi-ben sheish \u8212- ratz acharav v\u8217'eino magio} \u8212- anyone who puts his son into school before the age of six will run after him and not reach him. Rashi understands this as a warning: the child will be too delicate and you will spend all your energy trying to maintain his health without success.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weakness Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, offers an alternative reading: {\i chaveirav ratzim acharav v\u8217'ein magiin oso} \u8212- his classmates will run after him and not be able to reach him intellectually. He will be so far ahead of his peers that no one can keep up. The Gemara then says: {\i u-trvaihu isnahu} \u8212- both are true simultaneously. {\i Chalish} \u8212- he will be physically weakened by starting too early \u8212- {\i v\u8217'gamir} \u8212- but intellectually exceptional. The two effects coexist. This is, the Gemara suggests, analogous to cases where children are pushed to peak achievement at very young ages at significant personal cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One final resolution is offered: {\i ha de-kachish ha de-bari\u8217'a} \u8212- if the child is constitutionally weak, do not start him before six; if he is strong and healthy, starting before six is permissible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nichsei Melog\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recovery Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to yet another {\i takanat Usha}: {\i amar Rav Yosef bar Chanina \u8212- be-Usha tiknu ha-isha she-machrah be-nichsei melog be-chayei ba\u8217'alah u-meisah \u8212- ha-ba\u8217'al motzi mi-yad ha-lekuchos} \u8212- in Usha they established that if a woman sold her {\i nichsei melog} during her husband\u8217's lifetime and then died, the husband may recover that property from the purchasers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nichsei melog} is property a woman brings into the marriage over which she retains title but whose {\i peiros} \u8212- the usufruct, the ongoing benefits \u8212- belong to her husband. If she sells this property, the purchaser acquires her title but not the husband\u8217's rights to the {\i peiros}; he continues to enjoy the {\i peiros} from property now in the buyer\u8217's hands. Then, if she predeceases him, the husband inherits her property \u8212- but this particular property has already been sold. The {\i takanat Usha} establishes that despite the sale, the husband may recover the property. In effect, he is treated not merely as someone with rights to {\i peiros}, but as having enough ownership over the property that upon her death he can take it from whoever holds it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means, strikingly, that the purchaser receives almost nothing from the transaction: during her lifetime the husband takes the {\i peiros}; upon her death the husband takes the property itself. The only scenario in which the purchaser benefits is if the husband predeceases his wife, at which point the purchaser becomes full owner. The purchaser presumably retains a claim in {\i acharayut} against the woman who sold, but in terms of the property itself, the protection is minimal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Fiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that this {\i takana} intersects with the dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish over whether {\i kinyan peiros ke-kinyan ha-guf dami} \u8212- whether ownership of the fruits of a property is equivalent to ownership of the body of the property itself. Depending on how one resolves that debate, the {\i takanat Usha} may or may not be necessary; the point in any case is that the husband who has rights to {\i peiros} is treated as sufficiently an owner that upon his wife\u8217's death he can reclaim the property from its purchasers.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nichsei Melog (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Usha Leadership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records an incidental exchange that sheds light on the transmission of all these {\i takanos Usha}. Rav Yitzchak bar Yosef and Rabbi Abbahu were present at a large public gathering in Usha. He asked who was the {\i marei d\u8217'shmata d\u8217'Usha} \u8212- the head of the teachings there, the {\i Rosh Yeshiva}. He was told: {\i Rabbi Yoshi bar Chanina}. And the Gemara records with some amusement that he had to review this forty times \u8212- {\i tana minei arbin zimnin} \u8212- before it stuck, {\i v\u8217'dami lei k\u8217'man d\u8217'manach b\u8217'chisteih} \u8212- and then it felt as settled as something in his back pocket. It is a slightly comic note: the kind of intensive review one normally associates with difficult {\i halachic} rulings is here deployed simply to remember a name. The practical point, however, is that all the prior {\i takanos Usha} transmitted in the name of {\i Rabbi Yehuda bar Chanina} may in fact trace back to {\i Rabbi Yoshi bar Chanina} \u8212- the two names being easily confused \u8212- and the Gemara\u8217's meticulous concern for the precise chain of attribution is characteristic of its broader insistence that {\i who} said {\i what} matters enormously.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Psalm Derash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child Support Charity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now offers a series of {\i derashot} on the verse {\i Ashrei shomrei mishpat oseh tzedakah b\u8217'chol eit} \u8212- \u8220"praised are those who keep justice, who do righteousness at all times\u8221" (Psalm 106:3). The verse is read through the lens of {\i tzedaka} in its rabbinic sense of charitable support. The question posed is: {\i v\u8217'chi efshar la\u8217'asos tzedakah b\u8217'chol eit?} \u8212- can a person really be giving {\i tzedaka} at every moment? The sages of Yavneh interpreted this, and Rabbi Eliezer explains: {\i zeh ha-zan banav u\u8217'vnotav k\u8217'she-hein ktanim} \u8212- this refers to one who feeds his sons and daughters while they are young. The circle closes back to the opening of today\u8217's daf: since feeding one\u8217's children carries no strict legal obligation, every meal one provides is a voluntary act of {\i tzedaka}, making a parent who feeds young children someone who performs {\i tzedakah b\u8217'chol eit} \u8212- righteousness at every moment. This also reinforces the mechanism by which a court can compel a man of means to feed his children: since feeding them is {\i tzedaka}, and {\i tzedaka} can be compelled, the compulsion is available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphan Care\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani offers a different interpretation: {\i zeh ha-migadel yasom vi\u8217'yesomah be-toch beito} \u8212- this refers to one who raises an orphan boy or girl within his own home. {\i U-masia\u8217'n} \u8212- and then arranges their marriages, ensuring they are provided for beyond the period they live in one\u8217's household. Perhaps, the Gemara implies, merely feeding one\u8217's own children \u8212- while technically {\i tzedaka} \u8212- is so socially universal as to be unremarkable. Taking in orphans and raising them as one\u8217's own, and then marrying them off to secure their future, represents something genuinely exceptional and continuous: truly {\i tzedakah b\u8217'chol eit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Wealth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse continues: {\i hon va-osher be-veito ve-tzidkato omedet la-ad} \u8212- \u8220"wealth and riches are in his house, and his righteousness endures forever.\u8221" Rav Huna and Rav Chisda apply this to Torah: one of them says this refers to {\i ha-lomed Torah u-m\u8217'lamdah} \u8212- one who learns Torah and teaches it to others. Torah knowledge is the wealth that abides in one\u8217's house, and because one\u8217's students teach their students in turn, the {\i tzedaka} of transmitting Torah endures across generations indefinitely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other opinion of Rav Huna and Rav Chisda interprets {\i tzidkato omedet la-ad} differently: {\i zeh ha-kosev Torah Nevi\u8217'im u-Chtuvim u-mash\u8217'ilan la-acheirim} \u8212- one who writes out {\i Tanakh} texts and lends them to others. Here the {\i tzedaka} is concrete and physical: a written scroll that continues to enable others to learn long after the act of writing is complete. Both interpretations \u8212- whether one understands the enduring righteousness as intellectual transmission or material provision of texts \u8212- speak to the same aspiration: an act whose effects propagate forward without limit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites the verse {\i u-re\u8217'eih vanim le-vanecha shalom al Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"may you see children to your children; peace upon Israel.\u8221" Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi explains: {\i keivan she-banim le-vanecha \u8212- shalom al Yisrael} \u8212- once your children have children, there will be peace in Israel. The reason: {\i d\u8217'lo asu l\u8217'yidei chalitza ve-yibum} \u8212- it will not lead to situations of {\i chalitza} and {\i yibum}. If a man dies and leaves children, his widow does not require {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}; those obligations arise only in the absence of children. And the act of {\i chalitza}, which involves a degrading ceremony \u8212- a matter the Gemara notes has attracted considerable public controversy in Israel in recent times \u8212- is thereby avoided. Ensuring one\u8217's children have children of their own is thus a form of {\i tzedakah} that preserves peace within Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Disputes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani offers a variant on this reading: {\i keivan she-banim le-vanecha \u8212- shalom al dayanei Yisrael} \u8212- once there are grandchildren, there will be peace for the {\i judges} of Israel \u8212- meaning the courts will not be burdened with contentious disputes. Rashi explains that the major inheritance disputes arise when there are no clear direct descendants and competing claims emerge among distant relatives. When an estate passes straightforwardly to known heirs, litigation is minimized. The {\i dayyan} is spared, and peace \u8212- at least in the beit din \u8212- prevails.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Daughters Estate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns back to the Mishnah\u8217's rules about daughters\u8217' maintenance from the estate, specifically in the context of the {\i benin dichrin} provision and the daughters\u8217' parallel right to support. The Mishnah had established that just as sons inherit their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} portion from the estate, daughters are supported by the estate after the father\u8217's death. The Gemara now sits at the top of 50b with Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah teaching before the sages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef was sitting before Rav Hamnuna when Rav Hamnuna stated: {\i keshem she-ein ha-banim yorshim ela min ha-karka \u8212- kach ein ha-banot nizonot ela min ha-karka} \u8212- just as the sons inherit only from the land, so too daughters are supported only from the land. The reaction was immediate: {\i avshu lei kulei alma} \u8212- everyone erupted in protest. The objection was sharp: {\i de-shavik ara hu de-yarti lei benei? de-lo shavik ara lo yarti benei?} \u8212- does this mean that sons inherit from their father {\i only} if there is real estate in the estate? If someone dies leaving nothing but a large bank account, do his sons not inherit? That cannot be right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef intervened and offered the correct reading of Rav Hamnuna\u8217's statement: {\i ve-dilma ketubat benin dichrin ka-amar mar} \u8212- perhaps what you meant by \u8220"the sons inherit only from land\u8221" is not that ordinary inheritance is limited to real estate, but rather that the {\i special} inheritance of {\i ketubat benin dichrin} \u8212- the sons\u8217' right to recover their mother\u8217's dowry from the estate before the estate is divided among all heirs \u8212- is limited to real estate. This is indeed a technical rule: that lien-based claim, being a form of a debt against the estate, only attaches to land. General inheritance, of course, applies to all property, movable or otherwise. And parallel to that limitation on sons, the daughters\u8217' {\i mezonot} claim \u8212- which is similarly structured as a lien against the estate \u8212- likewise attaches only to real estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Real Estate Lien\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna confirmed: {\i mar de-gavra rabba hu \u8212- yadaat mai ka-amina} \u8212- you are a great scholar and you understood exactly what I was saying. Both the sons\u8217' special {\i ketubah} claim and the daughters\u8217' {\i mezonot} claim are liens against the estate, and liens operate only on real property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Reality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hiyya bar Yosef then adds a significant practical note: {\i Rav zan mi-chitei de-aliya} \u8212- when Rav was obligated to provide for daughters from an estate, he fed them even from the grain stored in the attic. The import is that even though the legal analysis limits the daughters\u8217' {\i mezonot} claim to real estate, Rav in practice provided for daughters from whatever was available in the estate, including stored grain that was movable property. This mirrors the tension seen throughout today\u8217's daf: the legal framework on one side, the real human needs on the other \u8212- and the practical response of a posek making sure that children are actually fed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then begins to ask: {\i ibaya lehu \u8212- parnasa haviya\u8230 ?} \u8212- is this particular provision a case of {\i parnasa}, maintenance, or something more? This question will be taken up in the continuation of the daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 51\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Base Ketubah Amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} we now reach has, as has been noted many times, finally arrived at what one might have expected to be among the very first {\i mishnayos} of the tractate: the base {\i ketubah} amount. The {\i mishnah} establishes 200 {\i zuz} for a {\i besulah} and 100 for an {\i almanah}. If the husband wrote less than the standard amount \u8212- or failed to write it at all \u8212- it makes no difference, because these obligations exist as a {\i tenai beis din}: they are imposed by rabbinic mandate regardless of what appears in the {\i ketubah}. Similarly, the lien encumbering all of the husband\u8217's property for payment of the {\i ketubah} is not subject to limitation. If the husband attempted to restrict the {\i shibudim} to a single field, that restriction is without effect. And further, the obligation to redeem a wife taken captive is likewise a {\i tenai beis din}. The first portion of the discussion concerns whether the husband can validly write a {\i ketubah} below the standard amount and whether the {\i shibudim} can be contractually limited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a simple question: {\i mani} \u8212- whose view does this {\i mishnah} follow? The answer given is that it must be Rabbi Meir, {\i de\u8217'amar}: {\i kol hapoches libesulah mimasa\u8217'im ule\u8217'almanah mimana harei zu be\u8217'ilas zenus}. Anyone who writes a {\i ketubah} for less than the standard amounts \u8212- less than 200 for a {\i besulah} or less than 100 for an {\i almanah} \u8212- has rendered the union effectively one of {\i be\u8217'ilas zenus}: intercourse conducted without the protections of marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying idea is significant. One of the primary functions of the {\i ketubah} is to ensure that a husband cannot simply divorce his wife without cost. Knowing there will be a financial consequence causes him to pause before acting callously. If the woman lacks that protection, if she knows he can divorce her at will with little financial deterrence, then from her perspective the relationship feels like casual intimacy rather than a committed marital bond. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is that any {\i ketubah} below the standard amount fails this essential protective function entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a question: what exactly does Rabbi Meir mean when he calls such a union {\i be\u8217'ilas zenus}? Does he mean that writing a lesser amount is simply censured \u8212- a violation that renders the ongoing marital relationship objectionable \u8212- while the {\i ketubah} remains legally valid at the written amount? Or does he mean that the written amount is voided, and the {\i ketubah} automatically reverts to the standard 200 or 100?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} assumes these two consequences go together. Since Rabbi Meir utterly opposes any {\i ketubah} below the standard amounts, the natural implication is that if one were written for less, we void it and replace it with the standard amount. This is what the {\i mishnah} reflects: a {\i ketubah} below the threshold cannot stand, and the standard amount applies regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} contrasts this with the position of Rabbi Yehuda. Rabbi Yehuda taught: {\i rotzeh kosev libesulah shtar shel masayim vechosev hiskabalti mimech mane} \u8212- a man who wishes to effectively reduce his wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} may write the full 200, then have her write a receipt stating she has already received 100. Similarly, for an {\i almanah}, he writes 100 and she writes a receipt for 50. Two things emerge from Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's formulation. First, even he agrees that one must begin with the base amount \u8212- you cannot simply write a lesser figure from the outset. Second, however, Rabbi Yehuda has no objection in principle to the effective amount being below the threshold, provided the procedure of writing the receipt is followed. This means Rabbi Yehuda does not regard a {\i ketubah} that is effectively less than 200 or 100 as {\i be\u8217'ilas zenus}. Accordingly, a {\i mishnah} that prohibits writing a lesser amount from the start cannot reflect Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this analysis, the {\i gemara} concludes that the {\i reisha} of our {\i mishnah} \u8212- the rule that the {\i ketubah} must be at least 200 or 100 \u8212- follows Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Liens & Achrayus Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seifa Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i seifa} of the {\i mishnah}, which presents its own difficulty. If the husband wrote that his {\i ketubah} obligation is secured only by a field worth 100 {\i zuz} \u8212- and did not write the standard language ({\i kol nichasin d\u8217'is li achrayin l\u8217'chtubaseich}, all my property is encumbered for your {\i ketubah}) \u8212- he is nevertheless {\i chayav}, the full {\i shibudim} apply, because this is a {\i tenai beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achrayus Ta\u8217'us Sofer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that this {\i seifa} sounds like it follows Rabbi Yehuda. Why? Because Rabbi Yehuda holds {\i achrayus ta\u8217'os sofer} \u8212- the absence of a written lien in a document is treated as a scribal error. This means that any written loan automatically carries {\i shibudim} whether or not they are written explicitly. One might think that principle applies only when the scribe simply forgot to include the lien language. But in our case the husband {\i explicitly} restricted the lien to a single field \u8212- there is no scribal error here at all. Nevertheless, Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's underlying position is that a written document inherently generates liens, and therefore even an explicit restriction cannot override that. The {\i seifa}, which says the liens apply to all property regardless of what was written, thus follows Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir disagrees. He holds {\i achrayus lav ta\u8217'os sofer} \u8212- the absence of a written lien is not a scribal error, and a document without explicitly written liens carries no liens at all. This applies even to a {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Found Loan Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited as evidence for Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. If one finds a loan document in the street \u8212- a document recording that Reuven borrowed money from Shimon \u8212- the question arises whether to return it to Shimon. Even if Reuven acknowledges the debt and says it belongs to Shimon, may we return it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i im yesh bahem achrayus nechasim lo yachzir} \u8212- if the document contains liens, do not return it, {\i she\u8217'beis din yifre\u8217'u meihen} \u8212- because {\i beis din} would then exact those liens. The concern is that Reuven has already repaid the debt, and the two parties are colluding: Shimon will present the document, pretend the debt is still owed, Reuven will claim to have no money, and Shimon will then seize encumbered property from third-party purchasers of Reuven\u8217's fields. The document, found lying in the street, lacks the evidentiary weight of a properly held document \u8212- we are suspicious about its origins, and therefore we do not return it even when Reuven says it belongs to Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Im ein bahem achrayus nechasim, yachzir} \u8212- but if the document contains no liens, return it, because {\i beis din} cannot seize third-party property on the basis of such a document, and there is no mechanism for the alleged scheme to cause harm to others. This is {\i divrei Rabbi Meir}. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is explicit: a document without written liens carries no liens. {\i Vachachamim omrim, echad zeh ve\u8217'echad zeh lo yachzir} \u8212- the Sages say do not return it in either case, because the Sages hold that any written document automatically carries liens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} is significant because it explicitly extends this principle to a {\i ketubah} as well: {\i kesuvas isha she\u8217'ein bo achrayus} \u8212- a {\i ketubah} lacking written {\i achrayus} is listed among documents that carry no liens. So Rabbi Meir holds that a {\i ketubah} without explicitly written liens has no {\i shibudim}, just as an ordinary loan document does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an interesting observation. One might have expected Rabbi Meir \u8212- who is so insistent on the full standard {\i ketubah} amount \u8212- to be equally insistent on full {\i shibudim}. Yet Rabbi Meir draws a distinction, seemingly because the rights of third-party purchasers must also be protected: when someone purchases property, they are entitled to know precisely what liens encumber it. Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, appears to hold the reverse: a {\i ketubah} can be effectively less than 200 or 100, but the {\i shibudim} are always full.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now confronts the apparent contradiction directly: the {\i reisha} of our {\i mishnah} sounds like Rabbi Meir, the {\i seifa} sounds like Rabbi Yehuda. Can the {\i mishnah} be attributed to a single authority?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One possible resolution: perhaps the entire {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Meir, and Rabbi Meir simply draws a distinction between a {\i ketubah} and other loan documents. For ordinary documents, he holds that unwritten liens mean no liens. But for a {\i ketubah}, perhaps Rabbi Meir agrees that {\i tenai beis din} overrides, and therefore even without written liens \u8212- or even with explicitly restricted liens \u8212- the full {\i shibudim} apply by rabbinic mandate. However, the {\i gemara} is not satisfied with this resolution, as we shall see, because Rabbi Meir is not shown to make this distinction elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five Non-Lien Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechurarin Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} introduces a broader {\i baraita} that bears on the question. The {\i baraita} enumerates five things that can only be collected from {\i mechurarin} \u8212- free, unencumbered property, meaning property still in the direct possession of the debtor \u8212- and {\i not} from {\i meshuabad} property, meaning property already sold to a third party. The word {\i mechurar} carries the connotation of freedom ({\i ben chorin}), as opposed to {\i shibudim}, which carries connotations of bondage \u8212- an apt contrast as {\i Pesach} approaches.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The significance of this list is that it identifies specific obligations which, by their nature, cannot carry liens on alienated property. These are obligations that are either indefinite in amount, ongoing, or otherwise structurally incompatible with the certainty required for liens to bind third-party purchasers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peiros & Shevach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first two items are {\i peiros} and {\i shevach peiros} \u8212- fruit and the benefit of improvements. The scenario involves a {\i ganav} who steals a field and sells it to a buyer. The true owner eventually reclaims the field. The buyer, who purchased in good faith, then seeks reimbursement from the {\i ganav}. Since the {\i ganav} has no ready funds, the buyer looks to enforce liens against property the {\i ganav} had sold to other parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two components of the buyer\u8217's claim are at issue. The basic purchase price \u8212- a fixed, known amount \u8212- can indeed be collected from encumbered property, because when Reuven bought the {\i ganav}\u8217's field, he knew there was a fixed, quantifiable lien that might be exercised against it. But the {\i peiros} and {\i shevach peiros} \u8212- the ongoing investment the buyer made in the stolen field over the years, plowing, fertilizing, planting \u8212- cannot be collected from encumbered third-party property. The reason is that this is an open-ended, indefinite amount: it could represent twenty years of investment, continuously accruing. It would be fundamentally unfair to the third-party purchaser to hold their property subject to such an uncertain, indefinite claim. Therefore, the buyer can only recover his invested improvements from property currently in the {\i ganav}\u8217's direct possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezonot Stepchildren\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third item is the obligation to support stepchildren. If a man undertook as a {\i ketubah} condition to provide {\i mezonos} for his wife\u8217's children from a prior marriage, that is an ongoing, indefinite obligation. Since the amount is not fixed \u8212- it accumulates continuously \u8212- it similarly cannot be enforced against property sold to third parties. One may recall from the preceding {\i daf} the question of daughters being supported from estate property: the same logic applies. Ongoing support obligations are not of the kind that can generate liens on alienated property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Documents Without Liens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth and fifth items are: {\i get chov she\u8217'ein bo achrayus} \u8212- a loan document without written liens \u8212- and {\i kesuvas isha she\u8217'ein bo achrayus} \u8212- a {\i ketubah} without written liens. Neither carries enforceable {\i shibudim} on property sold to third parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion on Five Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} thus provides explicit support for Rabbi Meir\u8217's position: a {\i ketubah} lacking written {\i achrayus} is treated like any other document without written liens, and no {\i shibudim} are implied. The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i man tana d\u8217'amar achrayus lav ta\u8217'os sofer} \u8212- who is the {\i tanna} who holds that absence of written liens is not a scribal error? Rabbi Meir. And this {\i baraita} explicitly extends that rule to a {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So we now have clearly before us the full picture. Rabbi Meir: full standard {\i ketubah} amount required, but {\i shibudim} only if explicitly written, even in a {\i ketubah}. Rabbi Yehuda: {\i ketubah} can be effectively less than the standard (though you must start by writing the full amount), but {\i shibudim} are always full on any written document.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Attribution (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty stands: our {\i mishnah} demands both the full standard amount {\i and} full {\i shibudim}. It cannot straightforwardly follow either Rabbi Meir or Rabbi Yehuda, since each authority holds one of these requirements but rejects the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers its first substantive resolution: {\i i bayis eima Rabbi Yehuda, hasam kasav lah hiskabalti, hacha lo kasav lah hiskabalti.} If you want to say our {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- consider why Rabbi Yehuda formulated his ruling so oddly. Why did he not simply say: you may write a {\i ketubah} of 100? Instead he said: you must write 200 and then write a receipt for 100. The obvious implication is that according to Rabbi Yehuda, the base {\i ketubah} must be written at the full standard amount established by the Rabbis. Only after that base has been formally established may the parties reduce the effective amount through a receipt. Writing a lesser amount from the outset is not acceptable even for Rabbi Yehuda. Our {\i mishnah}, which prohibits writing below 200 or 100, therefore accords with Rabbi Yehuda perfectly. And the {\i seifa} \u8212- which imposes full {\i shibudim} regardless of what was written \u8212- naturally follows Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's principle of {\i achrayus ta\u8217'os sofer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Rabbi Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also offers an alternative reading: {\i ve\u8217'ibayis eima Rabbi Meir, mai chayav de\u8217'kasani mina meshu\u8217'abadi.} One could say our {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Meir even in the {\i seifa}, if we reread the {\i mishnah}\u8217's conclusion differently. When the {\i mishnah} says that if you wrote the lien only on one field you are still {\i chayav}, perhaps it does not mean that liens extend to all real estate \u8212- perhaps it means only that you are personally obligated for the full amount, collectible from whatever unencumbered property is in your possession. That would be consistent with Rabbi Meir\u8217's view that only written liens bind alienated property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Most Likely View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, this is not the simple reading of the {\i mishnah}. The natural sense of the {\i mishnah}, combined with the language {\i tenai beis din}, implies that the {\i shibudim} are full and extend beyond unencumbered property. Therefore, the most satisfying conclusion is that our {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Yehuda: you must start with the base amount \u8212- writing less from the outset is impermissible \u8212- and the {\i shibudim} are always full, consistent with {\i achrayus ta\u8217'os sofer}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ones vs.\u160 ?Ratzon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife of Yisroel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's treatment of a wife taken captive and the husband\u8217's obligation to redeem her. Before examining the {\i ketubah} obligations directly, the {\i gemara} addresses the underlying {\i halachic} difference between the wife of a {\i Yisroel} and the wife of a {\i Kohen} in the event of captivity and potential rape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Avuhu deShmuel} \u8212- the father of Shmuel declared: {\i eishes Yisroel shena\u8217'ansa asura leba\u8217'ala} \u8212- a non-{\i Kohen}\u8217's wife who was raped is forbidden to her husband. The reason given is {\i chayshinan shema techilasa be\u8217'ones vesofa beratzon} \u8212- we are concerned that although she was raped, perhaps at some point she became a willing participant. This is a deeply difficult {\i gemara}. The suggestion that in the midst of rape a woman might become willing is one that cannot be imagined from a female perspective, and yet it is the position articulated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones Becoming Ratzon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the {\i gemara} will later develop is the broader context of women held captive under ongoing threat. A woman in such a situation might act in seemingly willing ways \u8212- providing food, handing over weapons \u8212- while in fact doing so entirely out of fear for her life. Such conduct is still classified as {\i ones}. But the statement of the father of Shmuel here is not about captivity in the abstract; it is about a direct case of rape. The question the {\i gemara} is raising is whether a woman in the midst of a rape can be considered, at some point during the act, to have become a willing participant. One might suggest it refers to a longer-term psychological process \u8212- an identification with captors over time. That reading has some appeal, but as the {\i gemara} makes explicit, the scenario the father of Shmuel envisions is one in which a woman has ceased actively protesting during the act itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eisivei Rav la\u8217'Avuhu deShmuel} \u8212- Rav challenged the father of Shmuel from the {\i ketubah} text itself. The standard {\i ketubah} language reads: {\i im tishatvai afarkinach ve\u8217'osvinach li le\u8217'intu} \u8212- if you are taken captive, I will redeem you and restore you as my wife. This language clearly contemplates the return of a captive wife to her husband, contradicting the assertion that a {\i Yisroel}\u8217's wife who was raped or taken captive is forbidden. The father of Shmuel was silent \u8212- {\i ishtik} \u8212- unable to respond. Rav then applied the verse {\i sarim atzru bemilim, vechaf yasimu lefihem} \u8212- the princes were restrained in speech and placed their hands to their mouths \u8212- noting that the father of Shmuel need not have been silent, because there was an answer available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape vs.\u160 ?Captivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is {\i bishvuya heikilu} \u8212- the Rabbis were lenient in the case of captivity. The father of Shmuel\u8217's ruling that a wife is forbidden applies specifically to a case of known, witnessed rape. In such a case we know she was raped, and we are therefore concerned that at some point she became a willing participant. But the {\i ketubah} language addresses captivity, in which case we do not know whether she was raped at all. Since rape is uncertain, she is permitted to return to her husband. The two cases are therefore distinguishable: certain rape raises the concern of eventual {\i ratzon}; mere captivity does not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'pliga de\u8217'Rava} \u8212- this position of the father of Shmuel is in dispute with Rava. Rava states: {\i kol shetechilata be\u8217'ones vesofa beratzon} \u8212- whenever something begins with force, even if it ends with willingness, the woman is permitted to her husband. Rava accepts the factual premise that a rape victim might at some point during the act express willingness. He does not dispute this characterization of the events. What he disputes is the legal conclusion drawn from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overriding Willingness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Afilu hi omeret hanichu lo} \u8212- even if the woman herself says \u8220"leave him alone, don\u8217't stop him,\u8221" {\i she\u8217'ilmale nizkak lah hi socharto} \u8212- even if she says \u8220"had he not forced himself upon me I would have paid him,\u8221" even such explicit expressions of desire do not render her forbidden. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama? Yeitzer albasha} \u8212- her {\i yeitzer hara} got the better of her. Since the entire process began with {\i ones}, even the most explicit subsequent expression of {\i ratzon} is regarded as a consequence of that original {\i ones} and cannot be legally classified as genuine {\i ratzon}. This is Rava\u8217's formulation of the principle of {\i techilata be\u8217'ones vesofa beratzon}: the original {\i ones} defines everything that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the relationship to a parallel principle in the laws of torts: {\i techilata befoshia vesofa be\u8217'ones} \u8212- if one was initially negligent with another\u8217's property and then an accident befalls it, the initial negligence may define the outcome. Analogously here, the initial {\i ones} defines all subsequent states, such that no subsequent expression of {\i ratzon} \u8212- however sincere it may be \u8212- can override the original act of force. One might ask whether this principle extends to longer-term processes, such as a captive who over time genuinely identifies with her captors. The {\i gemara} does not address this directly, though it is a compelling question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Supporting Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanya k\u8217'vatei d\u8217'Rava} \u8212- a {\i baraita} is cited in support of Rava\u8217's position. The {\i baraita} interprets the {\i pasuk} regarding {\i sotah}: {\i vehi lo nitpasha asura} \u8212- if she was not seized [i.e., not raped] she is forbidden [to her husband if she committed adultery]. {\i Hanitpasha muteret} \u8212- if she was raped she is permitted. {\i V\u8217'yesh lach acheret sh\u8217'af al pi shelo nitpasha muteret} \u8212- and there is yet another woman who, even though she was not raped, is permitted to her husband. What is this case? {\i Kol shetchilata be\u8217'ones vesofa beratzon} \u8212- any case that began with force: even if it ended with willingness, she is permitted. This {\i baraita} directly supports Rava\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Alternative Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Techilata Be\u8217'ones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraita} addresses complementary cases. {\i V\u8217'hi lo nitpasha asura, hanitpasha muteret} \u8212- the same baseline: not raped is forbidden, raped is permitted. {\i V\u8217'yesh lach acheret sh\u8217'af al pi shelo nitpasha muteret} \u8212- another woman who is permitted to her husband even though she was not raped. {\i Kol shetchilata be\u8217'ones vesofa beratzon} \u8212- again, the case where it began with force, which defines the entire episode as {\i ones} regardless of what followed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife of Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'yesh lach acheret sh\u8217'af al pi shenitpasha asura} \u8212- and there is yet another woman who, even though she was raped, is forbidden to her husband. {\i V\u8217'eizo zo eshet kohen} \u8212- this is the wife of a {\i Kohen}. Even a rape victim who is the wife of a {\i Kohen} is forbidden to her husband, because rape renders her a {\i zonah} \u8212- a category that, in {\i halachah}, applies not only to a prostitute but to any woman who has had intercourse with a man she is forbidden to marry, regardless of her will \u8212- and a {\i zonah} is forbidden to a {\i Kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel mishum Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- a third formulation introduces yet another scenario. {\i V\u8217'yesh lach acheret sh\u8217'af al pi shelo nitpasha muteret} \u8212- a woman who is permitted to her husband even though she committed adultery willingly, not under compulsion. {\i V\u8217'eizo zo she\u8217'kidusheiha kidushei taut} \u8212- this is a woman whose marriage was based on fundamental error, {\i kidushei taut}. Even if she has been married for years, even if she has children \u8212- {\i sh\u8217'afillu bna murkav al kteifa, memaenet v\u8217'holechet la} \u8212- she may refuse the marriage and be released, because the entire marriage was founded on a misrepresentation. Since the {\i kiddushin} was invalid from the outset, there is no real marriage for her to have violated, and she is not forbidden. A contemporary illustration: if a husband concealed at the time of marriage something that would have been a decisive impediment to the match \u8212- the {\i poskim} discuss such cases \u8212- the logic of {\i kidushei taut} in principle should apply, even if practically the requirement for a {\i get} is maintained.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bandits vs.\u160 ?Government\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda: hanni nashei d\u8217'ganvi ganvei sharyon l\u8217'gavraihu} \u8212- women taken by bandits ({\i listos}) are permitted to return to their husbands, because if they were raped it was not done willingly, and the circumstances are regarded as {\i ones}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Willingness Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis challenged Rav Yehuda: {\i v\u8217'ha ka mamatyan lehu nahama} \u8212- but these women appear to be willing participants; they bring the bandits their bread and provisions. {\i V\u8217'ha ka mashl\u8217'chin lehu giri} \u8212- they even hand them their arrows in battle. This hardly looks like unwilling captivity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear Motivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Machmat yira} \u8212- all of this is done purely out of fear. Every ostensibly willing act performed by a woman in bandit captivity, from providing food to handing over weapons, is motivated entirely by the threat of what will happen to her if she does not comply. That is the definition of {\i ones} in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freedom and Ratzon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vadai shavkinhu v\u8217'azlu minafshaihu asiran} \u8212- the only case in which a woman would be considered to have acted from genuine {\i ratzon} is if the captors explicitly offered her freedom to leave and she chose to stay. At that point, continuing to remain is a genuine expression of will, no longer coerced by circumstance, and she would be forbidden to her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is closely paralleled in the {\i Rambam}\u8217's {\i Iggeret HaShmad}, where he discusses people who were forced under mortal threat to convert outwardly to Islam. The {\i Rambam} argues that such a person may be considered an {\i anus} \u8212- acting under compulsion \u8212- as long as they have no viable opportunity to leave. But once a genuine opportunity to escape or relocate presents itself and the person does not take it, the {\i Rambam} says they can no longer claim the status of {\i anus}. The principle is the same: coercion defines the conduct while escape is impossible, but once freedom becomes a genuine option, remaining is an act of {\i ratzon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from all of this is a nuanced and, on the whole, relatively broad rabbinic definition of {\i ones}. Physically compelled rape is obviously {\i ones}. Acting willingly out of fear for one\u8217's life is {\i ones}. Even explicit verbal expressions of desire, if they arose in the wake of an initial {\i ones}, are treated by Rava as {\i ones}. The only scenario that conclusively terminates the status of {\i ones} is a clear, genuine, uncoerced opportunity to leave that is not taken.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gov\u8217't vs.\u160 ?Bandit Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shvuyey malchut harey hen kishvuyin} \u8212- women taken captive by the government ({\i malchut}) are treated as captives whose relationship is considered {\i ones}, and they may return to their husbands. {\i Ginuvey listos einam kishvuyin} \u8212- but those taken by bandits ({\i listos}) are not treated as captives in the same sense, and may not return. This directly contradicts Rav Yehuda\u8217's ruling above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraita} says the opposite. {\i Vatanya ipcha} \u8212- and indeed there is a contradicting {\i baraita}. So we have two apparent contradictions: one about {\i malchut} and one about {\i listos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achashverosh & Ben Nezar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Malchut amalchut lo kashya} \u8212- the contradiction about government captivity is not a difficulty. {\i Ha bemalchut Achashverosh, ha bemalchut Ben Nezar.} Rashi explains: in the case of Achashverosh, who is of genuine royal blood, a captured woman knows she will never become his queen \u8212- she is simply a captive for his use, never a prospective wife. There is therefore no incentive for her to be a willing participant, and the act is classified as {\i ones}. In the case of Ben Nezar, however \u8212- a figure who was simultaneously a bandit and an emerging warlord carving out his own territory \u8212- a captured woman might reason that willing participation could lead to becoming his queen, since he lacks royal lineage and marrying her would be fully within his power. In that case, we fear she may have been a willing participant, and she is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relative Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Listos alistos lo kashya} \u8212- similarly, the contradiction about bandits is resolved. {\i Ha beben Nezar} \u8212- one {\i baraita} that classifies {\i listos} as a problem refers to Ben Nezar. {\i Ha belistin de\u8217'alma} \u8212- the other {\i baraita}, which permits a woman taken by bandits to return, refers to ordinary bandits who have no prospect of offering her elevation in status. And Ben Nezar himself is assigned to different categories in different {\i baraisos} depending on the reference point: {\i hasam kari ley malka hacha kari ley listim} \u8212- in one {\i baraita} he is called a king, in another a bandit. {\i Ein gabei Achashverosh listim} \u8212- compared to Achashverosh, who is genuinely of royal blood, Ben Nezar is a mere bandit. {\i Hu vegabei listin de\u8217'alma malka havey} \u8212- but compared to ordinary bandits, Ben Nezar is a king.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Humorous Aside\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is like the old story of the man who makes his first million, buys himself a yacht, puts on his captain\u8217's hat with the brass buttons, and brings his mother for a tour. Compared to actual royalty, he is just a new-money bandit; compared to the people in his old neighborhood, he is a king. The relative status is everything.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Wife & Redemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i ketubah} obligations themselves. For the wife of a {\i Yisroel}, the {\i ketubah} reads: {\i im tishatvai afarkinach ve\u8217'osvinach li le\u8217'intu} \u8212- if you are taken captive I will redeem you and restore you as my wife. For a {\i Kohenet} \u8212- a woman married to a {\i Kohen} \u8212- the language differs: {\i adrina l\u8217'medinateich} \u8212- I will return you to your city. The promise is of redemption and return, but not of restoration as a wife, because even a {\i Kohen}\u8217's wife who was raped is forbidden to remain with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye: almana l\u8217'Kohen Gadol chayav lifdosah} \u8212- if a {\i Kohen Gadol} married an {\i almanah}, or a {\i Kohen} married a {\i gerushah} \u8212- a marriage that is halachically forbidden though the {\i kiddushin} is technically binding \u8212- he is still obligated to redeem her if she is taken captive. The reason: {\i she\u8217'ani korei bah u\u8217've\u8217'Kohenet adrina l\u8217'medinateich} \u8212- the {\i ketubah} language \u8220"I will return you to your city\u8221" applies to her. The obligation to redeem therefore exists even though he will have to divorce her after the redemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redemption Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requires explanation. When a forbidden but binding marriage exists \u8212- such as a {\i Kohen Gadol} who married an {\i almanah}, or a {\i Yisroel} who married a {\i mamzeret} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} takes effect and a {\i get} is required. Moreover, certain {\i ketubah} obligations continue to apply. The principle is that obligations which are not intrinsically tied to the ongoing marital relationship remain in force even in a forbidden marriage. The lump sum payment due at divorce, for example, continues to apply because it is a payment made at the {\i time of} divorce, not as part of ongoing married life. By contrast, obligations that presuppose and sustain the ongoing marriage \u8212- food, {\i refuah}, and similar day-to-day provisions \u8212- are withheld, because we wish to discourage the continuation of the forbidden marriage and thereby encourage divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obligation to redeem falls into a distinct category: for a {\i Kohenet} or an {\i almanah l\u8217'Kohen Gadol}, redemption is followed necessarily by divorce. It is not an act that sustains the forbidden marriage; if anything, it facilitates its proper dissolution. Therefore, Abaye holds that the redemption obligation applies even here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzeres Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next line sharpens the point. {\i Mamzeret u\u8217'netinah l\u8217'Israel eino chayav lifdosah} \u8212- if a {\i Yisroel} married a {\i mamzeret}, he does not have the obligation to redeem her if she is taken captive. Why? {\i Ki ein ani korei bo v\u8217'hashivotecha li le\u8217'into} \u8212- because for this couple, the {\i ketubah} language \u8220"I will return you to me as my wife\u8221" does not apply. According to Rashi, the key distinction is whether the relevant {\i ketubah} language \u8212- either the version for an {\i Yisroel}\u8217's wife (\u8220"I will restore you as my wife\u8221") or the version for a {\i Kohen}\u8217's wife (\u8220"I will return you to your city\u8221") \u8212- is operative in the given case. For a {\i mamzeret} married to a {\i Yisroel}, the standard language implying restoration as a wife cannot apply since they should not be living as husband and wife; and since there is no alternative {\i ketubah} language providing for redemption without restoration, no obligation exists.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Refinement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific Impediment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava amar: kol she\u8217'issur shevuyah goreim lah chayav lifdosah, issur davar acheir goreim lah eino chayav lifdosah.} Rava articulates a more precise principle. When the {\i ketubah} formula states \u8220"I will return you to your city\u8221" \u8212- indicating an obligation to redeem even without restoring the marriage \u8212- that language applies only when the impediment to the ongoing marriage is caused by the captivity itself. That is, if the reason she cannot remain his wife is specifically {\i because} she was taken captive ({\i issur shevuyah goreim lah}), then the obligation to redeem applies. But if there is a pre-existing or independent reason they cannot be married ({\i issur davar acheir goreim lah}) \u8212- a reason having nothing to do with the captivity \u8212- then the fundamental redemption obligation between husband and wife is simply inapplicable, and no derivative obligation can flow from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Application Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a {\i Kohen} and a {\i Kohenet} whose marriage is otherwise valid: if she is taken captive, the only reason she becomes forbidden upon return is the captivity. Therefore the {\i ketubah} obligates him to redeem her even though she will not remain his wife. For an {\i almanah l\u8217'Kohen Gadol} or {\i gerushah l\u8217'Kohen}: the impediment to the marriage existed before the captivity and is entirely independent of it. According to Rava, no redemption obligation applies in such a case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the point on which Abaye and Rava diverge. Abaye, following the reasoning that redemption is not an \u8220"ongoing marital\u8221" obligation and therefore applies even in a forbidden marriage, would obligate the {\i Kohen Gadol} to redeem his {\i almanah} wife. Rava, applying the principle that the impediment generating the forbidden status must be specifically the captivity, would not impose a redemption obligation when the marriage was already forbidden prior to the captivity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Redemption Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow & Captivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Leima k\u8217'tana\u8217'ei} \u8212- the {\i gemara} suggests this dispute between Abaye and Rava may be rooted in a {\i tanna\u8217'itic} debate. The case: a husband takes a vow that his wife may derive no benefit from him ({\i hamadir es ishto}). Such a vow obligates him to divorce her, since husband and wife cannot continue to live together under such a vow. He is thus already in the process of being obligated to divorce her. Subsequently, she is taken captive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer podeh v\u8217'nosein lah kesuvashah} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer holds that the husband must redeem her and then give her the {\i ketubah} upon divorcing her. Both obligations apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua omer nosein lah kesuvashah v\u8217'einah podeh} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua holds that he need only pay out her {\i ketubah} upon divorcing her; he has no obligation to redeem her. Since they were already headed toward divorce before the captivity, the marital framework that generates the redemption obligation no longer fully applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Natan sha\u8217'alti es Sumchos} \u8212- Rabbi Natan reports that he posed to Sumchos, a student of Rabbi Meir, the following question: when Rabbi Yehoshua said that the husband need only pay the {\i ketubah} without redeeming her, did he mean this only when {\i keshedirah ul\u8217'vasof nishbeys} \u8212- the vow preceded the captivity, so that the couple was already obligated to divorce at the time she was taken? Or would it apply even if {\i nishbeys ul\u8217'vasof pedirah} \u8212- she was taken captive first, and the vow came only afterward?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sumchos Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'amar li lo shamati, v\u8217'nir\u8217'in hadevarim keshedirah ul\u8217'vasof nishbeys} \u8212- Sumchos replied: I did not hear this explicitly, but it seems that Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's ruling \u8212- that there is no redemption obligation \u8212- applies only when the vow preceded the captivity. When she was already in a state of required divorce before being taken captive, the redemption obligation does not apply. If, however, she was first taken captive and only then the husband took the vow, the obligation to redeem would remain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scheme Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i D\u8217'ee amart nishbeys ul\u8217'vasof pedirah, asi l\u8217'arumey} \u8212- were we to say that a husband can escape the redemption obligation by taking a vow after his wife is already held captive, we would be enabling a scheme. A husband facing a redemption payment of 100,000 \u8212- far exceeding the lump sum {\i ketubah} payment of 10,000 \u8212- could simply take a vow after the captivity, trigger the divorce obligation, and thereby reduce his financial exposure from 100,000 to 10,000. To prevent this manipulation, the ruling applies only when the vow demonstrably preceded the captivity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Linking to Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai lav bemadir ishto kamiflagi} \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks whether the debate between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua is precisely about a case comparable to that of the {\i Kohen}\u8217's wife: a situation in which the couple is obligated to divorce for reasons unrelated to the captivity, yet the question remains whether the redemption obligation persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye=R. Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye amar k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- Abaye\u8217's position aligns with Rabbi Eliezer. Even when there is an independent reason compelling divorce, the redemption obligation remains. Just as Rabbi Eliezer says the husband must redeem and then divorce, Abaye holds that a {\i Kohen} married to a woman forbidden to him must still redeem her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava=R. Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava amar k\u8217'Rabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- Rava\u8217's position aligns with Rabbi Yehoshua. Where there is an independent imperative to divorce \u8212- one that existed before and apart from the captivity \u8212- the redemption obligation does not apply. Only the {\i ketubah} payment upon divorce is owed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate, then, applies specifically to cases of forced divorce: situations in which the husband is obligated to divorce his wife for reasons independent of her captivity, yet she is subsequently taken captive. Must he redeem her? Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- and Abaye \u8212- say yes. Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- and Rava \u8212- say the {\i ketubah} payment upon divorce suffices, and redemption is not required. We will continue from here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 51b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Min. Ketubah Amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 100/200 Zuz Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on this {\i daf} arrives \u8212- somewhat belatedly, as has been noted throughout this tractate \u8212- at what one might have expected to be among the very first topics addressed: the baseline {\i ketubah} amount. The standard is 200 {\i zuz} for a {\i betulah} and 100 {\i zuz} for an {\i almanah}. The {\i mishnah} establishes that even if these amounts were not written explicitly in the {\i ketubah} document, or if the husband attempted to write a lesser figure, this makes no practical difference. These obligations exist as {\i t\u8217'nai beit din} \u8212- rabbinic conditions that bind the parties whether or not they appear in writing. The {\i mishnah} similarly addresses the lien structure of the {\i ketubah}: the encumbrance applies to all of the husband\u8217's property, and any attempt to limit the {\i shudim} to a particular field is ineffective. These too are mandated by {\i beit din} regardless of what the document states. The {\i mishnah} then proceeds to the obligation of redeeming a captive wife \u8212- but before reaching the {\i ketubah}\u8217's financial mechanics, the {\i gemara} pauses to analyze who authored the {\i mishnah}\u8217's various clauses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir: Be\u8217'ilat Zenut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with the question {\i mani} \u8212- who is this {\i mishnah} attributed to? The answer: {\i Rabbi Meir hi}. It must be Rabbi Meir, for it is Rabbi Meir who rules that anyone who writes a {\i ketubah} for a {\i betulah} for less than 200 {\i zuz}, or for an {\i almanah} for less than 100 {\i zuz}, has rendered the relationship {\i be\u8217'ilat zenut} \u8212- akin to fornication. The reasoning is significant: a woman\u8217's sense of marital security depends on the {\i ketubah}\u8217's protection. In a legal regime where the husband may divorce his wife against her will, the {\i ketubah} functions as both deterrent and guarantee. It deters callous divorce by imposing a financial cost on the husband and provides the wife with protection should divorce occur. If those protections are absent or inadequate, the ongoing marital relationship lacks the essential character of marriage and takes on the quality of casual, unprotected intimacy. That is Rabbi Meir\u8217's position: anything below the standard amounts constitutes {\i be\u8217'ilat zenut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Censure vs.\u160 ?Voiding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle raises an important distinction. Does Rabbi Meir\u8217's characterization of a sub-standard {\i ketubah} as {\i be\u8217'ilat zenut} mean only that the practice is censured \u8212- that writing less than 200 or 100 {\i zuz} is halachically condemned \u8212- or does it mean that writing a lesser amount is entirely void, such that the {\i ketubah} automatically reverts to the standard figure? The {\i gemara} treats these as equivalent: Rabbi Meir\u8217's categorical opposition to any {\i ketubah} below the standard implies that whatever lesser figure was written is simply disregarded, and the obligation defaults to 200 or 100 {\i zuz} as appropriate. On this reading, our {\i mishnah} \u8212- which likewise refuses to honor a sub-standard {\i ketubah} \u8212- follows Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda: Receipt Me\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this attribution, the {\i gemara} considers whether the {\i mishnah} might reflect Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position instead. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, as cited, is that a husband wishing to reduce the effective {\i ketubah} amount may write the standard 200 {\i zuz} for a {\i betulah}, and then have his wife write a receipt acknowledging that she has already received 100 {\i zuz} \u8212- thereby reducing the effective obligation to 100 {\i zuz}. Similarly, for an {\i almanah}, one writes the standard 100 {\i zuz} and she provides a receipt for 50. What is immediately striking about this formulation is that Rabbi Yehuda does not simply say: write 100 {\i zuz} for a {\i betulah}. He requires beginning with the standard amount. This implies that according to Rabbi Yehuda, the document must open at the baseline figure the Rabbis established \u8212- even if the parties subsequently arrange to reduce it in practice. Therefore, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule that the {\i ketubah} may not be written for less than the standard amount could reflect Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position: one must start at 200 or 100, even if a receipt later brings the effective amount lower.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah = Rabbi Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus concludes that the opening clause of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the requirement that the {\i ketubah} not fall below the standard amounts \u8212- accords with Rabbi Meir, {\i mishnah Rabbi Meir hi}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Liens & Encumbrances\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liens Full on All Proper\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the {\i seifa} of the {\i mishnah}: if the husband wrote that a particular field worth 100 {\i zuz} is encumbered as security for a {\i ketubah} of 200 {\i zuz} \u8212- omitting the standard formula by which {\i kol nechasin de\u8217'is li acharin l\u8217'kesuvaseich}, all of his property stands as surety \u8212- he is nonetheless {\i chayav}, and the lien attaches to all of his property. This is {\i t\u8217'nai beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achrayut = Default\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the {\i gemara} raises a difficulty. If the opening of the {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Meir, can the {\i seifa} \u8212- which refuses to honor the husband\u8217's attempt to limit his liens \u8212- also follow Rabbi Meir? The {\i gemara} argues that this clause actually reflects Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's principle. Rabbi Yehuda holds that {\i achrayut ta\u8217'us sofer hu} \u8212- the absence of an explicit lien clause in a loan document is treated as a scribal error. What this means practically is that any written loan instrument automatically carries liens ({\i achrayut}) on the borrower\u8217's property, whether or not they are written in the document. The rationale is that liens are simply presumed to accompany any written loan; their omission is treated as inadvertent. Accordingly, a husband who explicitly limits his {\i ketubah}\u8217's lien to a single field has not successfully circumscribed his obligation \u8212- because Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's principle is that liens attach to all property by virtue of the written instrument itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost Document Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} supports Rabbi Meir\u8217's contrary position \u8212- that absent an explicit lien clause, no lien exists \u8212- by citing the {\i mishnah} in {\i Bava Metzia} dealing with found loan documents. If one finds a {\i shtar chov} lying in the street, the concern is that the debt may already have been paid, or alternatively that the document could be exploited collusively. The hypothetical scheme is as follows: Reuven borrowed money from Shimon; the debt was repaid, but the document was not destroyed. The document is now found in the street. If it is returned to Shimon, he and Reuven could fraudulently use it to seize property that Reuven had already sold to third parties \u8212- Shimon would present the document in court, Reuven would claim insolvency, and Shimon would then collect from the encumbered property held by subsequent purchasers, after which they would divide the proceeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why, it might be asked, is this concern unique to a found document? Generally, we are not suspicious that paid-up documents will be reused fraudulently. The answer is that a {\i shtar} is only operative as testimony when held by the party it favors. A document lying in the street is not functioning as testimony at all; returning it to Shimon would, in effect, be creating testimony \u8212- and that requires a stronger basis of trust than the circumstances provide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i im yesh bahem achrayut nechasim, lo yachazir} \u8212- if the document contains a lien clause, do not return it, since {\i beit din} might execute the lien. {\i Im ein ba achrayut nechasim, yachazir} \u8212- if there is no lien clause, return it, since {\i beit din} will not execute a lien from property in third-party hands. This is the ruling of Rabbi Meir. The Sages, by contrast, rule {\i echad zeh ve\u8217'echad zeh lo yachazir} \u8212- do not return it regardless, because in the Sages\u8217' view a written instrument automatically carries liens even without an explicit clause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now notes something striking: this {\i baraita} itself states explicitly that even a {\i ketubat isha she\u8217'ein ba achrayut} \u8212- a {\i ketubah} lacking a lien clause \u8212- carries no lien according to Rabbi Meir. So Rabbi Meir consistently holds that liens must be written; their absence is significant, not merely a scribal oversight. This is the opposite of Rabbi Yehuda. And it applies to {\i ketubot} and regular loan documents alike.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Unencumbered Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} in question actually enumerates five categories of obligation that can only be collected from {\i b\u8217'nei chorin} \u8212- unencumbered property currently in the debtor\u8217's possession \u8212- and not from property that has passed to third-party purchasers via liens. The five are: {\i peirot}, fruit; {\i shevach peirot}, the enhanced value of fruit; obligations to support a stepchild ({\i hamekabel alav lizon et ben ishto u\u8217'vat ishto}); a loan document lacking an {\i achrayut} clause; and a {\i ketubah} lacking an {\i achrayut} clause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i peirot ve\u8217'shevach peirot} requires some elaboration. The scenario involves a {\i ganev} who steals a field and sells it. The rightful owner subsequently reclaims the field from the purchaser. The purchaser, having lost the field, seeks reimbursement from the {\i ganev}. If the {\i ganev} has no liquid assets but did own other fields that he has sold to yet other parties, those sales may be subject to liens in favor of the purchaser \u8212- since when the purchaser paid the {\i ganev} for the stolen field, the {\i ganev}\u8217's other properties were encumbered as implicit guarantors of the transaction. The purchaser may therefore collect the original purchase price from those encumbered properties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, while the purchaser held the stolen field before it was reclaimed, he invested in it: he plowed, fertilized, and planted it, and the field was returned to its owner in an improved state. The purchaser is entitled to reimbursement for that investment \u8212- but only from property currently in the {\i ganev}\u8217's direct possession, not from property the {\i ganev} has already transferred. The reason is that this reimbursement is not a fixed, predetermined sum. When a third party purchased land from the {\i ganev}, he could estimate the fixed lien on that property; he could not be expected to account for an open-ended, ongoing liability whose total would depend on how much the purchaser happened to invest over an indeterminate period. Imposing such an indefinite encumbrance on innocent third-party purchasers would be unfair, and so the entitlement to {\i shevach peirot} is collectible only from {\i b\u8217'nei chorin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic governs the obligation to support a stepchild. If a husband undertook in his {\i ketubah} to provide {\i mezonot} for his wife\u8217's children from a prior marriage, this is an ongoing, indefinite obligation \u8212- not a fixed sum. Should he then sell his property, subsequent purchasers cannot be subjected to an encumbrance whose extent is unknown. Collection is therefore limited to property the husband currently holds. The {\i gemara} also notes the resonance with recent discussions about daughters\u8217' support from an estate: because that obligation is similarly open-ended and indefinite, it is likewise not clear that it can be collected from property that has changed hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the fifth category \u8212- a loan document or {\i ketubah} lacking an explicit {\i achrayut} clause \u8212- has no liens at all by Rabbi Meir\u8217's reckoning, and therefore collection is necessarily limited to unencumbered property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i man tana lah de\u8217'amar achrayut lav ta\u8217'us sofer} \u8212- who is the {\i tanna} who holds that the absence of a lien clause is not a scribal error? The answer: {\i Rabbi Meir}. Rabbi Meir explicitly treats a document without liens as genuinely lacking liens, and the {\i baraita} confirms this applies to {\i ketubot} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is somewhat surprising is that Rabbi Meir, who is so insistent that the {\i ketubah} amount never fall below the standard \u8212- and characterizes any deviation as {\i be\u8217'ilat zenut} \u8212- nonetheless does not insist on the full lien structure. Perhaps his reasoning is that while the minimum amount is necessary to protect the wife, the lien question implicates third-party purchasers as well, and Rabbi Meir feels their interests in predictability and certainty must also be protected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, Rabbi Yehuda appears to hold the inverse position: the {\i ketubah} amount may be effectively reduced below the standard (though one must start at the standard figure), but the liens are always full and comprehensive regardless of what the document says.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation: R. Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an apparent problem for our {\i mishnah}, which seems to insist simultaneously on both the full amount {\i and} the full liens \u8212- a position that fits neither Rabbi Meir nor Rabbi Yehuda squarely. The {\i gemara} offers two possible resolutions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first and simpler answer: {\i ei ba\u8217'is eima Rabbi Yehuda}. The {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Yehuda throughout. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's formulation \u8212- write a {\i ketubah} of 200 and then have the wife issue a receipt \u8212- implies that one must at least begin with the standard amount. If Rabbi Yehuda requires beginning at the base, then the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule that one cannot write a {\i ketubah} for less than the standard reflects Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position: even though the effective amount can later be reduced via a receipt, the document itself must open at 200 or 100 {\i zuz}. And the lien clause of the {\i mishnah} is straightforwardly Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's {\i achrayut ta\u8217'us sofer} principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second answer: {\i ei ba\u8217'is eima Rabbi Meir}. The entire {\i mishnah} can be read as Rabbi Meir, but the ruling that one is still {\i chayav} despite having written the lien as applying to only a single field does not mean that liens extend over all sold property. It means only that the husband\u8217's personal liability \u8212- for property currently in his possession, {\i b\u8217'nei chorin} \u8212- remains full. He cannot cap his personal obligation at the value of one field. But liens on property already transferred to third parties might not exist, consistent with Rabbi Meir\u8217's position that an explicit lien restriction (or absence) is effective. This reading, however, is not the simple reading of the {\i mishnah}, which appears to say that the lien structure cannot be curtailed in any fashion. The simpler reading of the {\i mishnah} is therefore that it follows Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rape & Captivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father of Shmuel: Forbid\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} distinguishes between the fate of a captive wife married to a {\i Yisrael} and one married to a {\i Kohen}. The husband of a {\i Yisrael} redeems her and takes her back as his wife; the husband of a {\i Kohen} redeems her but must then divorce her. Before addressing the {\i ketubah} mechanics of redemption, the {\i gemara} examines the underlying question of what captivity or rape does to the wife\u8217's permitted status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The father of Shmuel states: {\i eshet Yisrael she\u8217'ne\u8217'ensah, asurah l\u8217'ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- the wife of a non-{\i Kohen} who was raped is forbidden to her husband. The reason: {\i chayshinan shema techilatah be\u8217'ones ve\u8217'sofah be\u8217'ratzon} \u8212- we are concerned that even though it began as rape, it concluded with her willingness. This is a deeply difficult passage. The {\i gemara} appears to be contemplating that at some point during a rape, the woman might become a willing participant. The halachic consequence, according to the father of Shmuel, is that she would then be forbidden to her husband just as a {\i zonah} is \u8212- having engaged in voluntary intercourse with a man she cannot marry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to note that the {\i gemara} is not here speaking primarily of captivity but of a straightforward case of rape. One might have imagined that the father of Shmuel\u8217's concern involves a woman held over a long period who eventually, psychologically, identifies with her captors and comes to act willingly \u8212- a dynamic that has observable parallels in certain captivity cases. But as the {\i gemara} will clarify, the case of captivity is actually treated more leniently than rape, because a captive woman may never have been violated at all. The father of Shmuel\u8217's concern is about a woman who was directly assaulted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav challenges the father of Shmuel from the language of the {\i ketubah} itself: {\i im tishtavai afarkinnach ve\u8217'osvinnach li le\u8217'intu} \u8212- if you are taken captive, I will redeem you and take you back as my wife. The {\i ketubah} explicitly contemplates the husband\u8217's obligation to redeem a captive wife {\i and} to take her back. This implies that the captive wife of a {\i Yisrael} who returns is permitted to her husband. The father of Shmuel had no answer and was silent. Rav remarks \u8212- citing the verse {\i sarim atzru ve\u8217'milim ve\u8217'kaf yasimu lefihem}, that great men restrained their words and placed their hands over their mouths \u8212- that the father of Shmuel\u8217's silence was unnecessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captivity vs.\u160 ?Rape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer the father of Shmuel {\i could} have given is: {\i bi\u8217'shvuyah hekilu} \u8212- in the case of captivity, the Rabbis were lenient. The {\i ketubah} clause refers to a woman taken captive, where we do not know that she was raped. Since there is at least a possibility she was never violated, she is permitted to return to her husband. The father of Shmuel\u8217's ruling \u8212- that she is forbidden \u8212- applies specifically to a case where it is known that she was raped. Captivity and rape are therefore distinct: captivity may permit the wife to return; confirmed rape triggers the concern of {\i techilatah be\u8217'ones ve\u8217'sofah be\u8217'ratzon}, which the father of Shmuel regards as sufficient grounds to forbid her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Coercion Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now notes that this position of the father of Shmuel is directly contradicted by Rava. The {\i gemara} asks: according to the father of Shmuel, in what circumstances does the Torah\u8217's own implication \u8212- that a raped woman is permitted to her husband ({\i ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasa asurah}, implying that {\i nitpasa}, i.e., seized by force, renders her permitted) \u8212- ever apply? The answer offered: only when witnesses testify {\i she\u8217'tzvacha mitechilah ve\u8217'ad sof} \u8212- that she cried out and protested from the very beginning of the assault to the very end. Any cessation of protest raises the concern that she became a willing participant. The discomfort of this position is acknowledged \u8212- it demands that we interpret a woman\u8217's silence during a violent assault as an expression of consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava holds the opposite extreme: {\i kol she\u8217'techilatah be\u8217'ones ve\u8217'sofah be\u8217'ratzon, afilu hi omeret hanichu lo, she\u8217'ilmaleh nizkak lah hi socharto, muteret} \u8212- even if the assault began against her will and she later says not only that she consented but that she would have paid the man to have relations with her, she is still permitted to her husband. The reasoning: {\i yetzer albasha} \u8212- her {\i yetzer} overcame her. The fact that an act began as {\i ones} means that any subsequent expression of {\i ratzon}, no matter how explicit, is understood as resulting from and rooted in the original coercion. It cannot be treated as genuine, independent {\i ratzon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a far-reaching principle. It is related to \u8212- but distinct from \u8212- the parallel concept in torts: {\i techilatah bifshia ve\u8217'sofah be\u8217'ones}, where an initial act of negligence determines the character of everything that follows. Here, Rava\u8217's principle is that once an intimate violation begins under coercion, all subsequent apparent desire is legally attributed to the original {\i ones} and cannot constitute {\i ratzon} sufficient to create a prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's position also has implications for a case where a woman is held captive over a long period and comes to act cooperatively \u8212- bringing food, handing weapons, and so on. If one frames the entire situation as originating in {\i ones}, perhaps everything that follows remains categorized as resulting from that original coercion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bandits & Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita} in support of Rava: {\i ve\u8217'yesh acheres she\u8217'af al pi she\u8217'lo nitpasa muteret} \u8212- there is a woman who, even though she was not seized by force, is nevertheless permitted to return to her husband. Which case is this? {\i Kol she\u8217'techilatah be\u8217'ones ve\u8217'sofah be\u8217'ratzon} \u8212- one whose situation began as coercion, even if it concluded with apparent willingness. A second {\i baraita} addresses the wife of a {\i Kohen}: even one who {\i was} raped is forbidden to her husband, because the prohibition of {\i zonah} applies to any sexual act outside the permitted relationship, regardless of the woman\u8217's consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda then rules: women stolen by bandits ({\i listim}) may return to their husbands, because we presume that any sexual contact was unwilling. The Rabbis challenge this: such women appear to be willing participants \u8212- they bring food to their captors, hand them arrows during combat. Rav Yehuda responds: {\i machmat yira} \u8212- all of this is out of fear. The appearance of willingness is entirely a function of the threat environment they inhabit; it does not reflect genuine {\i ratzon}. This is consistent with the {\i Rambam}\u8217's analysis in his {\i Iggeret HaShmad} regarding Jews who outwardly conformed to religious coercion: as long as one has no realistic opportunity to leave the threatening environment, apparent compliance is still {\i ones}. The limiting case is: {\i vadai shavkinhu ve\u8217'azlu minafshayhu assurun} \u8212- if the captors definitively offered them freedom and the women chose to remain, they would then be considered willing and forbidden to their husbands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King vs.\u160 ?Bandit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} states: women taken captive by the royal government ({\i malchut}) are considered like captives \u8212- i.e., we presume coercion. But another {\i baraita} appears to contradict this, ruling the opposite. The {\i gemara} resolves the contradiction: both statements about {\i malchut} are consistent \u8212- the distinction is between {\i malchut Achashverosh} and {\i malchut Ben Netzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of Achashverosh, Rashi explains, the king would not ordinarily marry the woman, so there is no realistic prospect of elevation in status that might induce willingness. The encounter remains coercive and the woman is presumed to have acted under {\i ones}. Ben Netzer, by contrast, was a bandit leader who had carved out his own territory and was building, as it were, his own kingdom. Because he was not of royal blood, a woman seized by him might conceivably {\i want} to become his consort \u8212- there would be no hereditary barrier to her becoming queen. In that case, we may be concerned that the woman was willing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains the apparent contradiction between the two {\i baraitot} regarding {\i listim} (bandits) as well: one {\i baraita}\u8217's reference to {\i listim} refers to Ben Netzer (treated stringently), while the other refers to ordinary bandits (treated leniently). And Ben Netzer himself is called a {\i malka} (king) in relation to ordinary bandits, and a {\i listim} (bandit) in relation to Achashverosh \u8212- a fitting illustration of the relativity of status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Redemption Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow/Divorcee to KG\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i ketubah}\u8217's redemption obligation across different types of marriages, including those that are halachically prohibited. Abaye raises the case of an {\i almanah} who married a {\i Kohen Gadol}, or a {\i grushah} who married a {\i Kohen} \u8212- forbidden unions, but ones where the {\i kiddushin} is nonetheless binding and requires a {\i get}. Is the husband obligated to redeem her if she is taken captive? Abaye rules: {\i chayav liftotah} \u8212- he is indeed obligated to redeem her, because the relevant line of the {\i ketubah}, {\i u\u8217'bekohenet adrinach le\u8217'adinetech} (\u8220"for a {\i kohenet} I will return you to your city\u8221"), applies to her. Even though the marriage was illicit, the {\i ketubah} clause obligating redemption remains operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ongoing vs.\u160 ?Redemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling requires an important clarification. It is a general principle that {\i kiddushin be\u8217'issurei la\u8217'avin} \u8212- marriage contracted in violation of a negative commandment \u8212- is binding and requires a {\i get}, and obligations associated with the marriage may still apply. However, not all {\i ketubah} obligations remain in force in an illicit marriage. The obligations that reflect the {\i ongoing} marital relationship \u8212- such as {\i mezonot} and medical expenses \u8212- are not upheld, because the court wishes to discourage the couple from remaining together and to create a financial incentive for the husband to divorce his wife. Maintaining those ongoing obligations would effectively subsidize their continued cohabitation in a forbidden relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The lump-sum {\i ketubah} payment upon divorce is treated differently: the wife still receives it, both because it represents her financial protection upon separation and because one of the {\i ketubah}\u8217's functions \u8212- deterring casual divorce \u8212- is arguably not the operative reason here, where we {\i want} divorce to occur. That discussion aside, the point is that some {\i ketubah} obligations survive and some do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Redemption occupies a distinct category. In the normal case of a {\i Kohen} and his wife, redeeming her does not lead to their remaining married \u8212- he redeems her and then must divorce her. Since redemption does not sustain the ongoing marital relationship, it is not analogous to {\i mezonot}; it is not an obligation that maintains a forbidden union. Therefore the obligation to redeem survives even in an illicit marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Captivity-Specific\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava refines this further. The {\i ketubah} clause that says \u8220"I will return you to your city\u8221" does not create a general obligation to redeem one\u8217's wife under all circumstances where the marriage cannot continue. Rather, Rava holds: {\i kol she\u8217'issur shvuyah gorem lah, chayav lispotah; issur davar acher gorem lah, eino chayav lispotah} \u8212- when it is the captivity itself that creates the impediment to remaining married (as in the case of a {\i Kohen} whose wife becomes a {\i shvuyah}), the obligation to redeem exists. But when the impediment to the marriage exists for some {\i other} reason \u8212- such as the forbidden nature of the union itself \u8212- the obligation to redeem does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why, according to Rava, a {\i Yisrael} who married a {\i mamzeret} is not obligated to redeem her if she is taken captive: {\i de\u8217'ein ani korei bo ve\u8217'osvinnach li le\u8217'intu} \u8212- the clause \u8220"I will take you back as my wife\u8221" does not apply to them at all, since the marriage was fundamentally forbidden. The redemption obligation is grounded in the husband-wife relationship; if that relationship should never have existed, the redemption obligation has no foothold, regardless of the {\i ketubah} clause\u8217's language about captivity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow & Divorce Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks whether this debate between Abaye and Rava parallels a {\i tannaic} dispute. A {\i baraita} is cited: if a man vowed that his wife may derive no benefit from him \u8212- which obligates him to divorce her \u8212- and she is subsequently taken captive, Rabbi Eliezer rules that he must redeem her and then pay out her {\i ketubah} upon divorcing her. Rabbi Yehoshua rules that he pays out the {\i ketubah} upon divorce but need not redeem her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Timing Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nassan reports that he posed a question to Sumchus (a student of Rabbi Meir): when Rabbi Yehoshua ruled that the husband need not redeem her, was that ruling limited to a case where the vow {\i preceded} the captivity ({\i hedirah ve\u8217'levassof nishbeis}) \u8212- meaning the couple was already on a trajectory toward divorce before she was taken captive? Or would it apply even if the captivity occurred first and the vow was taken afterward ({\i nishbeis ve\u8217'levassof hedirah})? Sumchus replied that he had not heard an explicit ruling, but it seemed to him that Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's exemption from redemption applies only when the vow preceded the captivity. If the captivity came first and the vow followed, the husband\u8217's motivation for taking the vow would be suspect \u8212- it would appear to be a scheme to avoid the expense of ransom. Since redeeming a captive wife can be far more costly than the lump-sum {\i ketubah} payment, a husband might deliberately take a vow in order to obligate himself only to the {\i ketubah} payment and escape the redemption obligation. To prevent such manipulation, the exemption is unavailable when the vow postdates the captivity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes that the debate between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua in this {\i baraita} parallels the debate between Abaye and Rava. The relevant case for the parallel is that of a {\i Kohen}\u8217's wife, since only in that situation \u8212- where the {\i ketubah} explicitly says \u8220"I will return you to your city\u8221" rather than \u8220"as my wife\u8221" \u8212- is there any basis to argue that the obligation continues even when the couple cannot remain married. The {\i gemara} suggests that Abaye follows Rabbi Eliezer (the obligation to redeem persists even where some other circumstance requires divorce) and Rava follows Rabbi Yehoshua (no obligation to redeem when the impediment to the marriage is independent of the captivity). The question at the heart of the debate is whether the language of the {\i ketubah} \u8212- which contemplates return without continued marriage, in the case of a {\i Kohen}\u8217's wife \u8212- creates a freestanding redemption obligation even when the prior state of the marriage already required divorce, or whether the obligation is only triggered when the captivity itself is what separates the couple.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 54\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Daughters & Ketubah Stat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Arusah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous session concluded with a series of cases involving doubtful {\i ketubah} situations \u8212- among them the case of {\i shneiyot}, women forbidden to marry {\i derabbanan}, where the Gemara left the question of whether their daughters receive {\i mezonot} unresolved with a {\i teiku}. We now turn to the opening of 54a, four lines from the top.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'ei Rava} \u8212- Rava poses a question: {\i bat arusah yesh lah mezonot o ein lah mezonot?} Does the daughter of a woman who was only {\i arusah} \u8212- engaged but not yet fully married \u8212- receive {\i mezonot}? The assumption here is that these are women who followed the practice of {\i nishei Yehuda}, who did not live together with their husbands before the formal {\i nisuin}, so the marriage had not yet been consummated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question turns on two competing considerations. On one hand, {\i keivan de-it lah ketubah it lah} \u8212- since an {\i arusah} does have the right to a {\i ketubah}, perhaps the {\i tnai ketubah} are operative, including the {\i mezonot} provision for daughters. On the other hand, {\i keivan de-la takinu rabanan ketubah ad sh\u8217'at nisuin leit lah} \u8212- perhaps since the Rabbis did not actually mandate the writing of the {\i ketubah} until the time of {\i nisuin}, this daughter does not benefit from those provisions, having never reached that threshold. The Gemara leaves this unresolved: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Anusah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa, an Amora from Bavel, now raises a related question: {\i bat anusah yesh lah mezonot o ein lah mezonot?} Does the daughter of a woman whose marriage was contracted because her husband raped her \u8212- an {\i anusah} \u8212- receive {\i mezonot}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the difficulty, one must recall the Torah\u8217's framework for this case: a man who rapes an unmarried young woman must pay a penalty and marry her, and is forbidden to divorce her. Given that he has no power of divorce, the usual protective function of the {\i ketubah} \u8212- serving as a financial deterrent against casual divorce \u8212- does not apply in the same way. The question, then, is whether a {\i ketubah} grounded in this situation has full legal force for purposes of the {\i tnai ketubah}, or whether its unusual nature diminishes the daughter\u8217's entitlement to {\i mezonot}. One might also note that upon the husband\u8217's death, the {\i ketubah} does become relevant, so the underlying concern is how we understand the nature and purpose of the {\i ketubah} in this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubat vs.\u160 ?Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies that according to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, there is no question: {\i de-amar yesh lah ketubah ketubat maneh} \u8212- he holds that the {\i anusah} receives a standard {\i ketubah} of a {\i maneh}, like any woman who enters marriage as a non-virgin. Since a proper {\i ketubah} is in place, the {\i tnai ketubah} apply and there is no question regarding {\i mezonot} for her daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question only sharpens according to the position of the {\i rabbanan}, who hold {\i yatza kesef knasah b\u8217'ketubatah} \u8212- the money paid as a {\i knas} by the rapist effectively stands in place of the {\i ketubah}. While this payment is technically made to the father, the assumption is that the father acts as a protector of his daughter\u8217's interests. Nevertheless, as the Gemara itself presses: the {\i rabbanan} may simply be saying that one cannot require multiple payments \u8212- the {\i knas} already paid counts, so no additional {\i ketubah} can be extracted. Whether this arrangement actually protects the wife\u8217's interests in the same fashion as a conventional {\i ketubah} is a separate question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this ambiguity, the Gemara asks according to the {\i rabbanan}: perhaps {\i keivan d\u8217'leit lah ketubah leit lah mezonei} \u8212- if she effectively has no standard {\i ketubah}, the daughter receives no {\i mezonot}. But a counter-argument follows: {\i o dilma ketubah ta\u8217'ama mai kedei shelo tehei kalah be\u8217'einav l\u8217'hotzia} \u8212- perhaps the entire rationale of a standard {\i ketubah} is that it should not be easy for the husband to send his wife away. Since {\i v\u8217'ha lo matzi mapik lah} \u8212- this husband cannot divorce her regardless \u8212- those aspects of the {\i ketubah} concerned with deterring divorce are indeed inapplicable. Yet other {\i tna\u8217'im} of the {\i ketubah}, which exist for reasons unrelated to the divorce deterrent, might still be in force. The Gemara again concludes with {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House & Resources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now cites a {\i piska} \u8212- a passage from the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- which introduces the standard {\i tnai ketubah} formulation: {\i at tehei yatva b\u8217'veiti} \u8212- \u8220"you shall sit in my house\u8221" all the days of your widowhood. Rav Yosef transmits a {\i tannaic} tradition on this: {\i b\u8217'veiti v\u8217'lo b\u8217'vikati} \u8212- in my house, but not in my shack. That is, if the estate lacks a proper dwelling, the widow cannot be housed there, and she must presumably return elsewhere. However, {\i aval mezonei it lah} \u8212- even in such a case she retains her right to {\i mezonot}, which must be provided regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi goes further, taking a stricter position: {\i afilu mezonei nami leit lah} \u8212- even {\i mezonot} lapses if there is no house, since the absence of a house indicates an absence of resources altogether, and the {\i tnai ketubah} is therefore inoperative. The Gemara rules: {\i v\u8217'leit hilchata k\u8217'Mar bar Rav Ashi} \u8212- we do not follow this view, even though Mar bar Rav Ashi is among the latest Amoraim. This is a striking ruling, and it reflects the principle that even later authorities can be overruled when there is sufficient basis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow\u8217's Mezonot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Proposal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the topic of a widow\u8217's right to {\i mezonot} during the period she remains in her late husband\u8217's household. Rav Nachman cites Shmuel: {\i tavu\u8217'ah lehinasei v\u8217'nitpaisah ein lah mezonot} \u8212- if a man proposed marriage to the widow and she agreed to marry him, she loses her {\i mezonot}. The reverse follows by inference: {\i ha lo nitpaisah yesh lah mezonot} \u8212- if she did not agree, she retains her entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises an immediate difficulty. She has already entered the marriage market in some sense \u8212- if someone has proposed to her, is she not already in a position of potential remarriage? Rav Anan clarifies: {\i ledidi mefarsha li minei d\u8217'Mar Shmuel} \u8212- I can explain what Shmuel actually intended. If she declines the proposal by saying {\i amrah machamat ploni ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- \u8220"I cannot remarry because it would dishonor the memory of my late husband,\u8221" then she is still considered to be living in true widowhood and retains {\i mezonot}. But if she declines because {\i machamat bnei adam she\u8217'einam mehuganim lah} \u8212- because the suitor is beneath her station \u8212- then she loses {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is between a refusal rooted in {\i lichvod hamet}, honoring the deceased, and a refusal grounded in personal social calculations. A widow who says \u8220"no one is worthy of replacing my husband\u8221" is in a different category from one who says \u8220"this particular suitor is not in my league.\u8221" The latter indicates a willingness in principle to remarry, and once that is apparent, the basis for continuing to draw on the late husband\u8217's estate is diminished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beautification Tests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda presents a further case: {\i zanta ein lah mezonot} \u8212- if the widow has an illicit sexual encounter, she loses {\i mezonot}. Rav Yosef goes a step further in one direction: {\i kichla ufirkesa ein lah mezonot} \u8212- if she begins to adorn herself, put on cosmetics, and make herself attractive to potential suitors, that alone suffices to end her {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then explores how these two positions relate to each other. The one who holds that a sexual liaison forfeits {\i mezonot} would certainly hold that beautifying oneself to attract suitors also forfeits {\i mezonot} \u8212- {\i kol she-ken}. But the one who holds that beautification alone suffices might still allow {\i mezonot} even after a single illicit encounter: {\i mahu aval zanta is lah. mai ta\u8217'ama yetzer ansa} \u8212- the {\i yetzer hara} overwhelmed her in a moment of weakness, but this does not necessarily indicate a settled intention to leave her widowed state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable passage. It reflects either a profound sensitivity to the impulsive nature of human behavior, or a very sharp assessment of what deliberate preparation for remarriage actually signals. The Gemara then sweeps all of these rulings aside: {\i v\u8217'lo hi hilchata k\u8217'chol hani shema\u8217'tata} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows none of these opinions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Demand Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative {\i halacha} is instead the tradition of Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel \u8212- a different transmission from Shmuel than the one cited earlier, which the {\i Tosafot} notes. The ruling: {\i hatova\u8217'at ketubatah beveit din ein lah mezonot} \u8212- a widow who formally demands her {\i ketubah} in {\i Beit Din} loses her {\i mezonot} from that point forward. This and only this is the moment that ends her entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mortgaging Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately raises a challenge from a {\i Braita} listing additional cases in which a widow forfeits {\i mezonot}: {\i machra ketubatah} \u8212- she sold her {\i ketubah}; {\i mishkena ketubatah} \u8212- she pledged it as collateral for a loan; {\i asta ketubatah apotiki le\u8217'acher} \u8212- or she designated her {\i ketubah} as an {\i apotiki}, a form of security tied to a specific asset. In all of these cases, {\i ein lah mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reconciles this: {\i hani bein beveit din bein shelo beveit din} \u8212- these {\i Braita} cases apply regardless of whether they were done through a formal {\i Beit Din} proceeding or not. The reason is that in each of these cases, the widow has effectively transferred legal control of her {\i ketubah} to another party, thereby demonstrating that she has moved beyond her dependent status. By contrast, the case of formally demanding the {\i ketubah} in court does not involve relinquishing control in this way. Therefore: {\i tova\u8217'at beveit din in} \u8212- if the demand is made through {\i Beit Din}, it is sufficient to remove {\i mezonot}; {\i shelo beveit din lo} \u8212- but an informal, out-of-court demand does not have that effect, and she continues to receive {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader question of how these laws apply today, given that the relevant {\i leshonot} of {\i tnai Beit Din} no longer appear in our standard {\i ketubot}, is a practical question that touches on the enforceability of the {\i ketubah} in contemporary legal settings \u8212- a matter the {\i acharonim} address.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geographic Variation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a significant geographic dimension to the laws of the widow\u8217's {\i mezonot}, returning to the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's text: the formulation that a widow may sit in the husband\u8217's house all the days of her widowhood. The {\i Mishnah} itself records two versions \u8212- that of {\i Anshei Yerushalayim} and {\i Anshei HaGalil}, according to whom the widow remains until she chooses to leave or until she demands her {\i ketubah}, and a parallel tradition tied to the wishes of the {\i yorshim}, the heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Itmar} \u8212- an Amoraic dispute is recorded. {\i Rav amar halacha ke\u8217'Anshei Yehuda} \u8212- Rav holds that the {\i halacha} follows the position in which the inheritors may proactively pay out the {\i ketubah} and thereby terminate the widow\u8217's {\i mezonot} and right of residence. {\i Ushmuel amar halacha ke\u8217'Anshei Galil} \u8212- Shmuel holds that it is the widow\u8217's choice, and she may remain until she decides to leave based on the criteria discussed above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechoza Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical application of this dispute depends on geography: {\i Bavel ukhol parvadah nahug ke-Rav} \u8212- the city of Bavel and its surrounding environs follow Rav\u8217's ruling. {\i Nehardea ukhol parvadah nahug ke-Shmuel} \u8212- Nehardea and its surrounding area follow Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An illustrative case is then presented: a woman from Mechoza \u8212- which falls in the Bavel-and-environs region \u8212- married a man from Nehardea. When her husband died and the question of {\i mezonot} arose, the parties came before Rav Nachman. Hearing her accent, he recognized her as being from Mechoza and initially ruled: {\i Bavel ukhol parvadah nahug ke-Rav} \u8212- her home region follows Rav, meaning the heirs could end her {\i mezonot} by paying out the {\i ketubah}. But when told that she had been married to someone from Nehardea, he reversed course: {\i Nehardea ukhol parvadah nahug ke-Shmuel}. The husband\u8217's geographic origin is the determining factor, since it is his unstated {\i tna\u8217'im} \u8212- his assumed cultural expectations at the time of writing the {\i ketubah} \u8212- that govern the terms of the agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kav Measurement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: how far does the Nehardean {\i parvadah} extend? The Gemara answers: {\i ad heicha de-sagei kava de-Nehardea} \u8212- as far as the Nehardean {\i kav}, their local unit of volume measurement, is in use. The geographic jurisdiction of Nehardea\u8217's custom is coextensive with the use of Nehardea\u8217's particular standard of measurement \u8212- a practical and elegantly concrete means of defining a region\u8217's legal culture.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow\u8217's Clothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Count or Not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new dispute: {\i itmar \u8212- almanah: Rav amar shamin ma she\u8217'aleha, veShmuel amar ein shamim ma she\u8217'aleha} \u8212- when a widow comes to collect her {\i ketubah}, do we assess and deduct the value of the clothes she is wearing? Rav says yes, we count them. Shmuel says no, we do not. The underlying question is whether clothing provided to the wife during the marriage \u8212- or in the period of her widowhood, during which the estate was obligated to provide {\i ksut} \u8212- now belong to her outright or remain, in some sense, chargeable against her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hired Worker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiya bar Avin notes a striking inversion: {\i u\u8217'vechilufa belakit} \u8212- the positions are reversed when it comes to a hired worker who received clothing from his employer during his term of work. Whatever position Rav held regarding the widow, he held the opposite for the worker, and similarly for Shmuel. One can understand the logic running in both directions: for a wife, one might say that items provided by the husband during the marriage were intended as gifts and become her property, or conversely that they are chargeable expenses; for a worker, one might say that his salary was fixed and his clothing are extraneous costs, or that they are simply tools of his labor and not to be deducted. The reversal implies a genuine structural difference between the two cases, though the precise reasoning behind each sage\u8217's position is not made fully explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphan & Widow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana preserves a {\i siman} \u8212- a mnemonic \u8212- for the ruling: {\i yatma v\u8217'almana shlach u\u8217'fok} \u8212- the orphan and the widow, strip off their clothes and leave. This striking formulation, attributed to Rav\u8217's position, captures the rule that both the worker (the {\i lakit}, referred to here by the charged term {\i yatma}) and the widow must account for what they are wearing. The {\i lashon} is notably unsentimental given the Torah\u8217's repeated concern for the widow and orphan, and the Gemara does not soften it. Rashi explains that the term {\i yatma} is applied to the worker because in being sent away without his clothing, he is accorded a kind of orphan\u8217's treatment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Resolves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman rules that even though the {\i Mishnah} appears on a superficial reading to support Shmuel\u8217's position, the {\i halacha} follows Rav. The relevant {\i Mishnah} states that one who consecrates all his possessions, or makes a vow assessing his own value, does not count in that calculation the clothing he purchased for his wife and children, the dyes he bought for them, nor the new shoes he acquired for them \u8212- implying these items belong to them, not to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges Rav Nachman: {\i v\u8217'chi me\u8217'achar d\u8217'tnan matnitin k\u8217'vatei d\u8217'Shmuel amai hilchata k\u8217'vatei d\u8217'Rav?} If the {\i Mishnah} reads like Shmuel, why rule like Rav? Rav Nachman responds: {\i l\u8217'ichora k\u8217'Shmuel rahita ki maayant bah hilchata k\u8217'vatei d\u8217'Rav} \u8212- at a surface reading it appears to favor Shmuel, but on deeper examination the {\i halacha} is actually like Rav.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning: {\i ki aknei lah adata d\u8217'meikam kamei, adata d\u8217'meikam u\u8217'lmeifak la aknei lah} \u8212- when the husband purchased these items, he did so with the expectation that he would see his wife and children wearing them, with him present. He did not purchase them with the intention that they would take these valuables and leave his household. This reasoning connects to a broader theme running through this chapter: the Gemara consistently attempts an {\i umdan da\u8217'at} \u8212- an assessment of the presumed intent behind legal acts \u8212- to determine what a person actually meant when giving, writing, or committing to something. The clearest analogue, noted here as well, is the case of a husband who becomes mentally incapacitated versus one who abandons his family: in the former case {\i mezonot} continues because we assume he would have wanted it; in the latter it does not because his abandonment signals otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might press the point: does this logic apply even to items given during the husband\u8217's lifetime, not just after his death? The {\i Mishnah} appears to discuss things given during life. The answer suggested by the discussion is that even so, the husband never intended those items to be extracted from his estate and carried away \u8212- whether in a divorce or upon predeceasing. A plausible counter-argument exists that this reasoning might apply specifically to divorce but not to the case where the husband dies and the wife is simply collecting what she is owed, though the Gemara does not pursue that distinction here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An illustrative story is recorded. A widow from the household of Rav Bar Eliyashiv was seeking to collect her {\i ketubah} from the {\i yetomim} and brought them to {\i Beit Din}. The orphans, wishing to make a favorable impression \u8212- or perhaps shrewdly aware of what was coming \u8212- told her it would be embarrassing for her to appear in such plain attire and encouraged her to dress well for court. She obliged, putting on all her finest garments and jewelry. When the parties came before Ravina, he ruled: {\i hilchata k\u8217'vatei d\u8217'Rav de\u8217'amar almana shaminan mah she\u8217'aleha} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rav, who says we assess what the widow is wearing. The implication is that what she had on her at that moment was counted against her {\i ketubah}. It appears from the context that only what she is actually wearing at the time \u8212- not the entire contents of her wardrobe \u8212- is assessed. The story also illustrates the kind of tactical maneuvering that the law can invite, and one might observe that familiarity with the relevant {\i halachot} would have served the widow well here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling in favor of Rav in a monetary dispute is itself worth noting \u8212- the general principle is that Shmuel\u8217's rulings prevail in financial matters, making this a notable exception.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dowry & Prices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine & Dowry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A man who stipulated that his daughter should receive a standard dowry ({\i nidunya}) upon his death \u8212- without specifying an amount \u8212- and then died: if the standard dowry has decreased in value by the time of collection, Rav Idi bar Avin rules {\i purna leyatmei} \u8212- the orphans retain the benefit; the daughter receives the lower amount now in effect. Conversely, if a man said give my daughter four hundred {\i zuz} worth of wine as her dowry, and the price of wine subsequently rose, leaving her with less wine for the same nominal sum \u8212- Rav Yosef rules {\i revacha leyatmei} \u8212- again the orphans gain; they retain the appreciated commodity and the daughter receives only what the monetary value at the time of death would have provided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stepmother Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relatives of Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- who had appeared before him on a previous occasion with a legal dilemma and received advice he later regretted \u8212- returned with a new problem: their stepmother, their father\u8217's still-living wife, was consuming the estate\u8217's resources at an alarming rate. They came to Rabbi Yochanan, who advised them: {\i izilu ve-imru le-avuchon de-nyached lah ara le-mezonei} \u8212- tell your father to designate a specific field from which her {\i mezonot} will be drawn. Once that field is exhausted, her claim on the estate ends.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The father followed this advice. When he later died, the stepmother demanded more than the field could provide. The orphans brought the matter before Resh Lakish, who ruled against them: {\i kol she-ken she-riba lah mezonot} \u8212- by specifying the field, the father actually added to her {\i mezonot} entitlement, on top of what she would have received in any case. The orphans protested: this advice came from Rabbi Yochanan himself. Resh Lakish was unmoved: {\i zilu havu lah, ve-ee la mapikna lechu Rabbi Yochanan mi-onaichu} \u8212- go and pay her, or I\u8217'll take Rabbi Yochanan right out of your ears. The {\i aggadah} recalls that Resh Lakish\u8217's history lent his threats a certain credibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parties then returned to Rabbi Yochanan, who demurred: {\i mah e\u8217'eseh she-kenavchi chaluk alai} \u8212- what can I do if colleagues disagree with me?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grammatical Nuance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abahu then steps in to explain what Rabbi Yochanan had actually intended his advice to convey. The distinction is linguistic: {\i amar li-mezonot riba la-mezonot} \u8212- if the father says {\i li-mezonot} (this field is {\b for} her {\i mezonot}, i.e., designated as an additional provision), he has added to her entitlement. But {\i amar bi-mezonot} \u8212- if he says {\i bi-mezonot} (this field {\b is} her {\i mezonot}, i.e., in place of it), then {\i katzatz la-mezonot} \u8212- he has actually capped and shortened her {\i mezonot}, limiting her to the yield of that field. What Rabbi Yochanan advised the sons to say was {\i bi-mezonot}, thereby limiting the stepmother\u8217's claim to that field alone. Through a miscommunication, the father said {\i li-mezonot} \u8212- and Resh Lakish correctly ruled that this formulation expanded, rather than curtailed, her rights. The difference between a {\i lamed} and a {\i beit} \u8212- between two prepositions \u8212- carries that much legal weight.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Standards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tractate\u8217's next chapter opens with a {\i Mishnah} that synthesizes several themes encountered throughout the preceding discussion: {\i af al pi she-amru betulah gova matayim ve-almanah maneh, im ratzah le-hosif afilu me\u8217'ah maneh yosif} \u8212- even though the fixed amounts are two hundred {\i zuz} for a virgin and one hundred for a widow, a husband may add as much as he wishes, even another hundred {\i maneh}. Furthermore: {\i nit\u8217'almanah oh nitgarshah, bein min ha-eirusin bein min ha-nisu\u8217'in, gova et ha-kol} \u8212- whether widowed or divorced, whether the dissolution comes from {\i eirusin} or from {\i nisu\u8217'in}, she collects the full amount, including the {\i tosefet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah qualifies this: {\i min ha-nisu\u8217'in gova et ha-kol, min ha-eirusin betulah gova matayim ve-almanah maneh} \u8212- the {\i tosefet} is only collectible if the marriage proceeded to {\i nisu\u8217'in}; if it ended at the stage of {\i eirusin}, she receives only the statutory amount. His reasoning: {\i she-lo katav lah ela al menat le-kochzah} \u8212- the husband\u8217's additions were given on condition that he would actually marry her; if {\i nisu\u8217'in} never occurred, the condition was never fulfilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda offers a practical mechanism for reducing the {\i ketubah} obligation by mutual consent: a husband may write a {\i ketubah} of two hundred {\i zuz} for a virgin, and she may simultaneously write him a receipt stating she has already received one hundred \u8212- even if she has not actually received it. Similarly for an {\i almanah}: he writes one hundred, she writes a receipt for fifty. This functions as a kind of {\i mechilah} \u8212- a formal waiver \u8212- on her part, enabling a couple to proceed with marriage even when the husband cannot realistically commit to the full statutory amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This appears to place Rabbi Yehuda in {\i machloket} with Rabbi Meir, who states that anyone who gives less than the full two hundred to a {\i betulah} or less than one hundred to an {\i almanah} renders the relationship {\i be\u8217'ilat zenut}. One might attempt to resolve the contradiction by noting that Rabbi Yehuda does technically write the full amount \u8212- it is her written receipt that reduces the effective obligation \u8212- but the Gemara reads them as genuinely disputing positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefet Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: why does the {\i Mishnah} need to tell us that a husband may add to the {\i ketubah}? Surely anyone may give money to whomever they wish. The answer: one might have thought the Rabbis fixed a maximum, not just a minimum, in order not to embarrass those who cannot afford to give more. Hence, {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} teaches that additions are permitted without limit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's choice of language is telling: it says {\i ratza le-hosif} \u8212- he wishes to {\b add} \u8212- rather than {\i ratza lichtov} \u8212- he wishes to {\b write}. This linguistic precision supports the principle articulated by Rabbi Ibo in the name of Rabbi Yannai: {\i tnai ketubah ke-ketubah damei} \u8212- the additions to a {\i ketubah} have the full legal status of the {\i ketubah} itself. The word {\i le-hosif} signals that the addition is being appended to the {\i ketubah}, not recorded as a separate gift. As Rashi explains: had the text said {\i ratza lichtov}, one might have understood the {\i tosefet} as a voluntary gift with its own independent legal character, carrying no {\i shem ketubah}; the formulation {\i lehosif al ha-ketubah} makes clear that the addition takes on the full identity of the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine Applications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara enumerates the practical consequences of the rule that {\i tosefet} carries the legal status of the {\i ketubah} proper. If she sells her {\i ketubah} \u8212- the {\i tosefet} is sold with it. If she is a {\i moredet} \u8212- a wife who rebels \u8212- the reductions apply beginning from the {\i tosefet} and work downward. If she is {\i pogemet} \u8212- she has collected part and written a receipt \u8212- the {\i tosefet} is subject to the same {\i shevuah} requirement as the principal {\i ketubah}. If she demands her {\i ketubah} in {\i Beit Din}, this includes the {\i tosefet}, and the {\i mezonot} entitlement ends. If she violates religious norms and thereby forfeits her {\i ketubah}, she forfeits the {\i tosefet} as well. If the property appreciated in value after the husband\u8217's death, she collects the {\i tosefet} at the value fixed at the time of death, just as with the principal {\i ketubah}; the first-born sons similarly do not receive a double share of post-mortem appreciation, whether of the {\i ketubah} or the {\i tosefet}. The {\i tosefet} is likewise not annulled in {\i shemitah}, as the {\i ketubah} itself is not subject to annulment during the seventh year. If the husband wrote all his property to his sons, the {\i ketubah} \u8212- together with the {\i tosefet} \u8212- is affected; Rashi notes that if he additionally designated even a minimal piece of land for his wife, she forfeits her {\i ketubah}, a {\i sugya} developed in Bava Batra 132. Collection of {\i tosefet} follows the same land-quality rules as the {\i ketubah} \u8212- from {\i karka} and from {\i ziborit}. And for purposes of {\i ketubat benin dichrin} \u8212- the provision by which sons of one wife inherit her {\i ketubah} before division of the estate \u8212- the {\i tosefet} is included. Finally, the time limit on collection when the widow is in {\i beit aviah} \u8212- her father\u8217's house \u8212- applies equally to the {\i tosefet}: after twenty-five years, neither the {\i ketubah} nor the {\i tosefet} may be collected.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 55\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Tosefet Basics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's official {\i daf} is 56, but we pick up on 55 with the {\i itmar} section. The {\i mishnah} at the opening of our {\i daf} has established the basic framework of the {\i ketubah}: the {\i ikar ketubah} of 200 for a {\i betulah} and 100 for a widow or divorc\u233 ?e, along with the {\i tosefet} \u8212- the additional sum a husband voluntarily adds to the base obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erusin vs.\u160 ?Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One matter that is not made fully explicit in our {\i mishnah} is the question of what happens when a {\i ketubah} has not been written at all. The {\i mishnah} addresses a written {\i ketubah} with a {\i tosefet} and asks whether the {\i tosefet} takes effect from the time of {\i erusin} or only from the time of {\i nisuin}. The sense one gets from the surrounding {\i sugyot}, however, is that the base obligations of 200 and 100 do take effect even when no {\i ketubah} has been written, already from the time of {\i erusin}. Our {\i mishnah}\u8217's particular focus is on the written {\i ketubah} that includes a {\i tosefet}, and the question of when that {\i tosefet} becomes operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefet Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then addresses a broader principle: when there is a {\i tosefet}, it acquires the same legal status as the {\i ketubah} itself. There is a substantial list of areas in {\i halachah} where it matters whether an obligation is merely an independent {\i shtar} obligation or specifically a {\i ketubah} obligation. The {\i gemara} proceeds from there into a terse series of debates between two great academies \u8212- Pumbedita and Mata Mechasya \u8212- across a range of cases, most of which bear directly on {\i ketubah} law.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Itmar Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benin Dichrin Lien\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first of these debates concerns {\i ketuvat benin dichrin} \u8212- the provision by which the sons of a wife who predeceased their father inherit their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} ahead of other competing heirs. The question is whether those sons\u8217' claim functions as a lien against the father\u8217's estate, such that they could collect from property the father sold during his lifetime, or whether it functions purely as an inheritance, entitling them only to whatever remains in the estate at the time of the father\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt vs.\u160 ?Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pumbedita rules: {\i lo tarfi mimshubedi} \u8212- the sons cannot collect from encumbered property. The language of the {\i ketubah}, they argue, reads {\i yarsun} \u8212- \u8220"they will inherit\u8221" \u8212- and an inheritance is not a lien against the estate. Mata Mechasya rules: {\i tarfi mimshubedi} \u8212- they can collect from encumbered property. The language, they contend, reads {\i yisbun} \u8212- \u8220"they will take\u8221" \u8212- indicating a debt-like claim with lien power. The debate thus turns on the {\i girsah} of the {\i mishnah}, or alternatively on what the standard {\i nusach ketubah} was in different communities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying question is sharp: did the sons acquire a lien against their father\u8217's estate \u8212- as creditors would \u8212- such that if the father sold property during his lifetime, they can subsequently seize it from third-party buyers? Or is their right merely contingent on the father having property in the estate when he dies?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehilchata lo tarfi mimshubedi yarsun tenan} \u8212- the {\i halachah} follows Pumbedita. We treat {\i ketuvat benin dichrin} as an inheritance, not as a debt, and therefore the sons can collect only from property that remains in the estate at the time of the father\u8217's death. If the father sold or encumbered property during his lifetime, that property is beyond the sons\u8217' reach.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow Shevuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevuah Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the question of the widow\u8217's {\i shevuah} when she comes to collect her {\i ketubah}. Normally, a widow must take an oath before collecting her {\i ketubah} from the orphans. The reason for this {\i shevuah} is the concern that the husband, before he died, may have already designated certain {\i metaltelim} for her {\i ketubah} \u8212- for example, he may have pulled aside specific chattel and told her: after I die, collect your {\i ketubah} from this, so you will not have to deal with the heirs. If he did that, then her {\i ketubah} may have effectively already been provided for, and she would not be entitled to collect again from the estate. The {\i shevuah} she takes is that her {\i ketubah} has not been prepaid or pre-designated in this fashion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metaltelim Designated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now considers a case where the husband explicitly designated {\i metaltelim} for the {\i ketubah} in the presence of witnesses \u8212- for example, he pointed to a specific set of china and declared it designated for her {\i ketubah} collection. In such a case, {\i belo shevuah} \u8212- she collects without a {\i shevuah}. The entire rationale for the {\i shevuah} is the concern that he may have designated other {\i metaltelim} for her {\i ketubah}, but here we know precisely what he designated. There is no residual concern that he had set aside something else, and therefore there is no need for the oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What if those designated {\i metaltelim} are no longer {\i be\u8217'einaihu} \u8212- they were broken or destroyed, and she now must collect from the {\i karka} of the estate? Here we have a hybrid situation: on one hand, she is now collecting from real property, which ordinarily triggers the {\i shevuah} requirement; on the other hand, we know the husband designated specific {\i metaltelim}, and there is no real concern that he had separately pre-designated other {\i metaltelim} or pre-paid the {\i ketubah} through some other means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pumbedita says: {\i belo shevuah} \u8212- she can still collect without a {\i shevuah}, because we can reasonably conclude there was no other {\i metaltelim} the husband would have pre-designated. Mata Mechasya says: {\i bishvuah} \u8212- since she is now coming to collect from {\i karka}, the general rule applies and she must take a {\i shevuah}. {\i Vehilchata belo shevuah} \u8212- the {\i halachah} follows Pumbedita.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Designated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case: {\i yiched lah ar\u8217'ah b\u8217'arba mitzreha} \u8212- the husband explicitly designated a specific piece of land for collection of the {\i ketubah}, identifying it by all four borders. Here too, {\i belo shevuah} \u8212- there is no concern that he had prepaid the {\i ketubah} through other means, and she collects without an oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Partial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the husband designated the land with only {\i chad mitzra} \u8212- only one border rather than all four \u8212- the designation is incomplete. It is unclear how much land he is actually setting aside for her, and one might argue this is tantamount to no designation at all. Perhaps we should revert to the concern that he may have pre-designated other property, and she should be required to take a {\i shevuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pumbedita rules {\i belo shevuah} \u8212- even a partial designation is sufficient to eliminate the concern. Mata Mechasya rules {\i bishvuah} \u8212- an incomplete designation is insufficient. {\i Vehilchata belo shevuah} \u8212- again the {\i halachah} goes with Pumbedita, and we are lenient in her favor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Widow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the two rulings thus far: {\i ketuvat benin dichrin} is treated as an inheritance and not as a debt, meaning the sons cannot collect from encumbered property. And as for the widow\u8217's {\i shevuah} \u8212- if {\i metaltelim} were designated, even if those {\i metaltelim} are no longer {\i be\u8217'einaihu}, and if land was designated, even if it was designated on only one border \u8212- that is sufficient to dispense with the {\i shevuah}. The logic is that in each of these cases there is enough evidence that the husband did not separately pre-pay or pre-designate the {\i ketubah}, and the widow collects without an oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting a subtle point that arose in this discussion. When the husband designates a specific piece of property for the {\i ketubah}, this is not intended to limit the wife\u8217's other liens or rights \u8212- it is to give her an enhanced or more direct right to collect from that specific property. The evidence for this reading is that when the designated {\i metaltelim} are no longer {\i be\u8217'einaihu}, she nonetheless collects from {\i karka} \u8212- her other rights are undiminished.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deathbed Gift Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Instructions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third debate between the two academies involves a case unrelated to {\i ketubah}. Someone \u8212- who may or may not have been on his deathbed \u8212- instructed witnesses: {\i kisvu ve-chasmu ve-havu lei} \u8212- write up a deed of gift, sign it, and give it to him. He wanted to transfer a piece of land to another person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the witnesses performed a {\i kinyan} \u8212- specifically a {\i kinyan sudar}, the handkerchief-based acquisition \u8212- before writing and delivering the deed, then {\i lo tzarich imluchei bei} \u8212- they do not need to check back with the giver before delivering the deed. The {\i kinyan} demonstrates full {\i gmiras da\u8217'as}, complete commitment to the transfer, and they may proceed. If, however, no {\i kinyan} was performed, and the instruction was purely verbal: Pumbedita says {\i lo tzarich imluchei bei} \u8212- verbal instruction is sufficient and no check-in is needed. Mata Mechasya says {\i tzarich imluchei bei} \u8212- without a {\i kinyan}, before finalizing the transfer, the witnesses must verify with the giver that he still intends it. {\i Vehilchata tzarich imluchei bei} \u8212- the {\i halachah} follows Mata Mechasya. Without a {\i kinyan}, a check-in is required before the deed is delivered.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Umdana Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Umdana Reliance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah, whose position was already introduced: even though the {\i tosefet} was written into the {\i ketubah} document at the time of {\i erusin}, the obligation does not take effect until {\i nisuin}, because we assume the husband only intended the {\i tosefet} to become operative at the time of the actual marriage. This reasoning is based on {\i umdana} \u8212- an assessment of the person\u8217's intent that overrides or qualifies the literal text of the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the question of which authority \u8212- Rav or Rabbi Natan \u8212- holds that the {\i halachah} follows Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah, and which holds that it does not. The {\i gemara} proposes to resolve this: {\i tistayem} \u8212- we can conclude \u8212- that it is Rabbi Natan who rules like Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah, because we know Rabbi Natan {\i azal batar umdana} \u8212- he follows the principle of {\i umdana}, assessing intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The evidence for Rabbi Natan\u8217's reliance on {\i umdana} comes from his ruling {\i ke-Rabbi Shimon ben Shezuri} in the case of a {\i mesukan} \u8212- a person in mortal danger. The {\i mishnah} in {\i Gittin} establishes that when a man says \u8220"write a {\i get} for my wife\u8221" while departing on a dangerous sea voyage or being led to execution, we assume he means write it {\i and give it} \u8212- because his intent clearly includes delivery, even though he only said \u8220"write.\u8221" Rabbi Shimon ben Shezuri extends this principle even to a {\i mesukan}, someone lying on his deathbed, who says \u8220"write a {\i get} for my wife.\u8221" Even in that case, Rabbi Shimon ben Shezuri holds, we assess his intent as including delivery, and the {\i get} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} already accepts {\i umdana} reasoning in the first two cases \u8212- the sea voyage and the execution. The debate between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Shimon ben Shezuri is not about whether {\i umdana} applies at all, but about how much contextual pressure is needed before we invoke it. When a man is being rushed to a ship or dragged to the gallows, one can easily assume he did not complete his sentence; a man dying on his deathbed, by contrast, is not pressed for time in the same way, and perhaps we should not fill in words he did not say. Rabbi Shimon ben Shezuri is willing to extend {\i umdana} even to that more relaxed context, and Rabbi Natan rules accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan also rules like Rabbi Shimon ben Shezuri in the case of {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser shel demai}: when a person bought grain from an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}, separated {\i ma\u8217'aser}, took {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser} from it, and then the {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser} fell back into the main grain \u8212- a situation with no ordinary remedy \u8212- Rabbi Shimon ben Shezuri permits asking the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} himself and relying on his answer, since otherwise the entire batch would have to be sold to a {\i kohen}. Whether the reasoning is that the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} would be more reluctant to lie about {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser}, or, as Rashi explains, that otherwise there is simply no available remedy, Rabbi Natan adopts this lenient position. Both rulings demonstrate Rabbi Natan\u8217's willingness to go by {\i umdana} even when the context is not self-evident.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deathbed Gift Kinyan (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the inference that it is Rav, rather than Rabbi Natan, who does not rely on {\i umdana}. Surely Rav also applies {\i umdana} in other contexts \u8212- specifically, the case of {\i matnat shechiv mera she-katuv bah kinyan}: a deathbed bequest in which a {\i kinyan} was performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ordinarily, a {\i matnat shechiv mera} requires neither writing nor {\i kinyan} \u8212- the Rabbis gave the words of a dying person the status of something already written and delivered. It is, however, conditioned on death: if the person recovers, the gift is reversed. In the case before us, the person was lying on his deathbed \u8212- the document was framed in the language of a deathbed bequest \u8212- but then he also performed a {\i kinyan sudar}, an act associated with the transfers of a {\i bari}, a healthy person. This created a hybrid document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i D\u8217'vei Rav mishmeih d\u8217'Rav amri: archevyei atrei rechi} \u8212- in the house of Rav, they say in the name of Rav that this person has ridden on two chariots simultaneously. The gift has the power of both a {\i matnat bari} and a {\i matnat shechiv mera}. {\i Harei hi ke-matnat bari} \u8212- like a healthy person\u8217's gift \u8212- in that {\i im amad eino yachol lachazor bah}: if he recovers, he cannot retract it. And {\i harei hi ke-matnat shechiv mera} in that it can accomplish things a normal {\i kinyan} cannot \u8212- for instance, it can transfer a debt. Normally, transferring a debt from one creditor to another requires elaborate formalities; a {\i shechiv mera} can accomplish this directly. The result, according to Rav, is that the gift combines both sets of advantages: it is irrevocable even if the giver recovers, and it operates over the full range of things that a deathbed bequest can accomplish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel says: {\i lo yadana mai adun bah} \u8212- I do not know what to make of it. His reasoning is that the hybrid nature of the document is self-undermining. The fact that the person is on his deathbed suggests he only wanted the gift to take effect upon his death \u8212- that is the essence of a {\i matnat shechiv mera}. But the fact that he performed a {\i kinyan} suggests he wanted it to function as a normal transfer by a healthy person. A normal transfer by a healthy person does not take effect after death \u8212- once a person dies, he can no longer transfer property; it passes to heirs. The two halves of the document thus contradict each other: one implies he wants it to work like a transfer at death, the other implies he wants it to work like a transfer during life \u8212- which is the one mode in which it {\i cannot} function as a {\i matnat shechiv mera}. Shmuel therefore leaves the matter unresolved; since he cannot determine what the person intended, no property is transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In short: Rav is willing to assess intent and give the document maximal effect in both directions, while Shmuel is paralyzed by the ambiguity and allows nothing to transfer. This shows that Rav, no less than Rabbi Natan, is prepared to rely on {\i umdana}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Umdana Method (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transaction Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the obvious question: if both Rav and Rabbi Natan are willing to go by {\i umdana}, what exactly is their debate regarding Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's position on the {\i tosefet}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both the Rambam and Tosafot Rid make the same essential point, expressed in different formulations. The principle of {\i devarim she-be-lev einam devarim} \u8212- that unspoken intent does not override what was explicitly stated \u8212- applies to two-sided transactions. When there is a buyer and a seller, or two parties to a contract, one party cannot unilaterally reinterpret the transaction based on what he was privately thinking. The other party has no obligation to guess at unstated intentions, and the transaction is defined by what is explicit. There is an exception: if the unstated assumption was so universal that it would be known to all ({\i devarim she-be-lev kol adam}), then it can affect the transaction \u8212- but that is a narrow category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Umdana Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Azlinan batar umdana} \u8212- following {\i umdana} \u8212- applies to one-sided transactions. All three of the cases before us are one-sided. A {\i get} is entirely in the husband\u8217's domain; the wife has no say. A {\i matnat shechiv mera} is a unilateral gift. The {\i tosefet ketubah} is also one-sided \u8212- the husband is choosing what additional amount to give; even if there are reciprocal aspects to the {\i ketubah} overall (a larger dowry might motivate a larger {\i tosefet}), the {\i tosefet} itself is not explicitly framed as part of an exchange. In all of these cases, because only one party\u8217's intent is operative, we are permitted \u8212- indeed required \u8212- to assess that intent, and the concern of {\i devarim she-be-lev einam devarim} does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even granting this shared commitment to {\i umdana}, however, the two authorities can still disagree about what the {\i umdana} actually yields in any given case. Both parties agree we must assess intent; they disagree about what that assessment reveals.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Elazar Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chanina Assert\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now tracks the {\i halachic} debate over whether we rule like Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah. {\i Yasiv Rav Chanina kamei de-Rebbi Yannai v\u8217'ka\u8217'amar halacha ke-Rebbi Elazar ben Azarya} \u8212- Rav Chanina sat before Rabbi Yannai and declared that the {\i halachah} follows Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yannai Reject\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yannai dismissed him sharply: {\i puk kari kerach l\u8217'bara} \u8212- go recite your verse outside. The {\i halachah} does not follow Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yitzchak Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rebbi Yitzchak bar Avdimi mishum Rabbeinu} \u8212- Rabbi Yitzchak bar Avdimi said in the name of Rabbeinu, which refers to Rav: {\i halachah ke-Rebbi Elazar ben Azarya}. We rule like him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Nachman amar Shmuel halachah ke-Rebbi Elazar ben Azarya} \u8212- Shmuel rules like Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah. Yet {\i Rav Nachman didei amar ein halachah ke-Rebbi Elazar ben Azarya} \u8212- Rav Nachman himself personally rules against him. Indeed, Rav Nachman went further: {\i kol dayana de-dayen ke-Rebbi Elazar ben Azarya} \u8212- any judge who rules like Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah and does not award the {\i tosefet} from the time of {\i erusin} \u8212- {\i hachi ve-hachi tehavei} \u8212- such and such should befall him, the {\i gemara} declining to specify the precise content of the curse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nahardai Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'v\u8217'Nahardai mishmei de-Rav Nachman amri halachah ke-Rebbi Elazar ben Azarya} \u8212- but in Nehardea, in the name of Rav Nachman himself, they transmitted that the {\i halachah} does follow Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Afilu hachi halachah ke-Rebbi Elazar ben Azarya} \u8212- notwithstanding Rav Nachman\u8217's own personal ruling and his curse, the {\i halachah} follows Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah. We overrule Rav Nachman. And lest there be any remaining doubt, the {\i gemara} adds: {\i ve-halachah le-ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- in practice, we rule like Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah. The {\i tosefet ketubah} does not take effect until {\i nisuin}. It is worth noting that {\i ve-halachah le-ma\u8217'aseh} is a Hebrew formulation rather than the Aramaic {\i hilchata}, an unusual choice that is worth observing even if its precise significance remains unclear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nisuin Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chupa vs.\u160 ?Bia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now that the {\i halachah} is settled that the {\i tosefet} takes effect at {\i nisuin}, the {\i gemara} raises Rava\u8217's question about what exactly constitutes that moment. This is not the ordinary question of when {\i nisuin} begins for standard {\i ketubah} obligations \u8212- that is well established. Here the question is specific to the {\i tosefet} under Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's rationale. Since Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's reasoning is {\i umdana} \u8212- the husband wrote the {\i tosefet} only in anticipation of the couple\u8217's actual life together \u8212- we must ask: what event in the husband\u8217's mind was he anticipating? {\i Nichnesa le-chupah ve-lo niv\u8217'alah} \u8212- if she entered the {\i chupah} but they did not yet have relations \u8212- {\i mahu}? Is the intimacy of the {\i chupah} ({\i chibat chupah}) sufficient to trigger the {\i tosefet}, or does it require the actual act of {\i bia}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Night\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ta shema de-tani Rav Yosef} \u8212- come and hear what Rav Yosef taught as an explanation of Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's position: {\i shelo chasav lah ela al chibat laila ha-rishon} \u8212- he wrote the {\i tosefet} only because of the intimacy of the first night. This formulation, \u8220"the first night,\u8221" is what the {\i gemara} now unpacks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trigger Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If we say the triggering event is {\i chibat chupah} \u8212- the {\i chupah} without {\i bia} \u8212- then the phrase \u8220"first night\u8221" makes sense: {\i chupah} occurs only once, at the beginning of the marriage, and it is naturally associated with the first night. Why then does he say \u8220"first night\u8221" rather than simply \u8220"{\i chupah}\u8221"? Because {\i chupah} is a lead-in to {\i bia}, which is what takes place at night \u8212- the phrase is meant to evoke the anticipated {\i bia} without being explicit about it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, on the other hand, the triggering event is the {\i bia} itself, one might ask: is there {\i bia} only on the first night and not thereafter? The {\i gemara} answers: of course there is {\i bia} at other times as well, but the phrasing {\i laila ha-rishon} is simply the normal way ({\i orach ar\u8217'a}) of speaking about the beginning of conjugal life, since {\i bia} typically takes place at night. As Rava himself rules, {\i bia} in a dark house during the day is also permitted \u8212- so {\i bia} is not exclusively nocturnal \u8212- but the natural way to speak about {\i bia} is with reference to night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the same token, one might ask: if the triggering event is {\i chupah}, why say \u8220"first night\u8221"? Is {\i chupah} only at night? Here too the {\i gemara} responds: since {\i chupah} is normally a prelude to {\i bia}, which takes place at night, one would naturally speak of the {\i chupah} in terms of \u8220"the first night,\u8221" either to indicate that it leads to {\i bia} at night, or simply because {\i chupah} itself customarily takes place at night. The phrase \u8220"first night\u8221" is thus equally consistent with either reading, and the question remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then adds a further question: {\i nichnesa le-chupah u-firsa niddah \u8212- mahu}? If she entered the {\i chupah} and became a {\i niddah} \u8212- what is the law? If we say {\i chibat chupah} is the triggering event, does that apply only to a {\i chupah} that was {\i chazya le-bia} \u8212- one that could lead to {\i bia} \u8212- but not to a {\i chupah de-lo chazya le-bia}, a {\i chupah} where {\i bia} was not possible? Or does it make no difference?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question as phrased by the {\i gemara} \u8212- \u8220"she entered the {\i chupah} and became a {\i niddah}\u8221" \u8212- is deliberately ambiguous. If she became a {\i niddah} after the {\i chupah}, the question is trivial: the {\i chupah} was already complete and she was already entitled to the {\i tosefet}, just as she would be entitled to it if he dropped dead a moment after the {\i chupah}. The question must therefore refer to a case where she was already a {\i niddah} at the time of the {\i chupah} itself. This reading connects to a major dispute among the {\i rishonim} about whether {\i chuppat niddah} counts as {\i chupah} for any purpose \u8212- not just for the {\i tosefet}, but in general. The {\i gemara} here raises precisely that ambiguity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiqu Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i teiku} \u8212- the question stands unresolved. The status of {\i chupah} when the woman is a {\i niddah} at the time of the {\i chupah} remains uncertain with respect to its ability to trigger the {\i tosefet} under Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Receipt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} turns to the position of Rabbi Yehuda. His view is that the husband may write a {\i ketubah} for a {\i betulah} in the full amount of 200, or for a widow in the full amount of 100 \u8212- and then have the wife write a receipt ({\i shover}) acknowledging that a portion has already been paid, thereby effectively reducing the actual obligation below the nominal amount in the document. Rabbi Yehuda thus permits the base {\i ketubah} to be less than the required minimum, as long as the document itself records the proper minimum and the reduction is accomplished through a receipt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda vs.\u160 ?Yossi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises from a {\i mishnah} elsewhere. In the case of a borrower who repays part of his debt, Rabbi Yehuda says the solution is to tear up the old {\i shtar} and write a new one for the remaining amount ({\i yachlif}). Rabbi Yossi says the lender should write the borrower a {\i shover} \u8212- a receipt for the amount already paid \u8212- and when the lender subsequently produces the original {\i shtar}, the borrower produces his receipt. What is the difference? The receipt arrangement places the burden on the borrower to preserve the receipt; Rabbi Yehuda objects to this, since the borrower is the more vulnerable party, and writing a new {\i shtar} is more protective. Moreover, writing a new {\i shtar} costs the lender his lien priority from the original date \u8212- Rabbi Yossi nonetheless prefers the receipt arrangement precisely because it does not disturb the lender\u8217's prior lien.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if Rabbi Yehuda is ordinarily opposed to receipts \u8212- because they unfairly burden the obligated party \u8212- how can he here require the wife to write a receipt to accomplish the reduction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirmiah Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Yirmiya: ke-she-shovra tvu\u8217'a mitocha} \u8212- Rabbi Yirmiah answers that in this case there is no separate receipt handed to the husband. The receipt is embedded in the {\i ketubah} document itself: the {\i ketubah} records a debt of 200, of which 100 has already been received. The husband does not need to preserve a separate piece of paper; the reduction is already written into the document he signed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a broader answer: {\i afilu teima be-she\u8217'ein shovra tvu\u8217'a mitocha} \u8212- even if the receipt is not embedded in the {\i ketubah}, Rabbi Yehuda would still permit a separate receipt here. In the case of the borrower who repaid three thousand out of ten thousand, real money changed hands, and Rabbi Yehuda is concerned that the receipt might be lost ({\i mirchas tavrata}), allowing the lender to produce the original ten-thousand-dollar {\i shtar} and collect the already-repaid amount a second time. That concern justifies the protective measure of writing a new {\i shtar}. Here, however, the husband did not pay any real money. The receipt is a legal fiction \u8212- a way of reducing the wife\u8217's claim to less than the nominal amount written in the {\i ketubah}. Since he paid nothing, there is no danger he will be double-charged. {\i Ai natar natar, ai lo natar ihu de-afsid anafshei} \u8212- if he preserves the receipt, well and good; if he loses it, he brought it on himself. No special protection is warranted for a husband who received a reduction in his {\i ketubah} obligation without paying a single coin.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Receipt (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written Form\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: given that Abaye\u8217's explanation is so straightforward \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda only opposed receipts in cases of real payment, and here no payment was made \u8212- why does Rabbi Yirmiah not simply adopt Abaye\u8217's reasoning and instead offer the more narrow answer that the receipt is embedded in the {\i ketubah} document?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer is that Rabbi Yirmiah operates with a concern about {\i gezeira}: since Rabbi Yehuda is in general opposed to the receipt mechanism, he would not allow it even in the case where no money was paid, lest people infer from this case that receipts are permissible generally and begin using them in cases where real money has changed hands, improperly burdening borrowers who really did pay. Only by embedding the \u8220"receipt\u8221" into the {\i ketubah} document itself \u8212- where it is not functioning as a standalone {\i shover} \u8212- does Rabbi Yehuda permit the reduction. {\i Taama de-kasav lei} \u8212- only because it is written \u8212- {\i aval al peh lo} \u8212- but verbally, or as a separate document in the ordinary sense of a receipt, it would not suffice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Monetary Stipulate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monetary Stipula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks a deeper question. If Rabbi Yehuda holds that {\i davar she-be-mamon tnao kayam} \u8212- that one may stipulate against monetary obligations \u8212- then why must the husband go through the elaborate two-step of writing the full amount and then having a receipt written? Why not simply stipulate at the outset that the {\i ketubah} shall be for less than 200 or 100?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braisa Void\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} is cited: if a man says to a woman, {\i harei at mekudeshet li al menat she-ein li alayich she\u8217'eir kesut ve-ona} \u8212- you are betrothed to me on condition that I owe you no {\i she\u8217'eir}, no {\i kesut}, and no {\i ona} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} takes effect but the condition is void, in the view of Rabbi Meir. The reason is {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} \u8212- he is stipulating against what the Torah has written \u8212- and such stipulations are null.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Distinct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees and holds that {\i be-davar she-be-mamon tnao kayam} \u8212- for monetary obligations, his stipulation stands. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's understanding is that the Torah\u8217's establishment of {\i she\u8217'eir} and {\i kesut} as marital obligations represents a set of default assumptions about what the parties intend in a marriage \u8212- defaults that can be overridden by an explicit stipulation between the parties. {\i Ona}, however, is a personal obligation, not a monetary one, and cannot be stipulated away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Rabbi Yehuda is prepared to permit stipulations even against biblical monetary obligations, the question becomes more pointed: why cannot the husband simply stipulate that the {\i ketubah} will be for less than 200? The {\i ketubah} is rabbinic, not biblical \u8212- if he can override Torah-level monetary obligations by stipulation, surely he can override a rabbinic one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Protect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, paradoxically, is this: {\i kasavar Rebbe Yehuda ketuvah de-rabbanan} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda holds that the {\i ketubah} is rabbinic. {\i Ve-chachamim asu chizku le-divreichem yoter mi-shel Torah} \u8212- and the Rabbis fortified their own enactments more rigorously than Torah obligations require. The very fact that the {\i ketubah} is a rabbinic institution meant to protect the wife caused the Rabbis to surround it with stronger protections than they would ordinarily apply. A direct stipulation to reduce the {\i ketubah} below the required minimum, they determined, would undermine the institution, and so they did not permit it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Protect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises a further question. The husband\u8217's right to the {\i peirot} \u8212- the fruits of his wife\u8217's {\i nichsei milug}, the property she retains title to in marriage \u8212- is also rabbinic. Yet we find in another {\i mishnah} that Rabbi Yehuda allows the husband to waive his rights to the {\i peirot} verbally, and the {\i gemara} elsewhere establishes that {\i mai kosev} \u8212- what the {\i mishnah} means by \u8220"he writes\u8221" \u8212- is that verbal waiver suffices. Why is the waiver of {\i peirot} not similarly fortified by the Rabbis?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye: le-chol yesh ketubah ve-lo le-chol yesh peirot} \u8212- Abaye answers: every woman has a {\i ketubah}, but not every woman has {\i peirot}. The {\i ketubah} is a universal entitlement; the {\i peirot} arrangement is contingent on the wife bringing in property over which the husband can exercise those rights. {\i Milta de-shchicha avdu Rabbanan chizuk} \u8212- something common, something that applies to every married woman, the Rabbis reinforced with special protections. {\i Milta de-lo shchicha lo avdu Rabbanan chizuk} \u8212- something that arises only in the more particular circumstance of a wife bringing in income-generating property, the Rabbis did not subject to the same degree of reinforcement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 56\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Meir vs.\u160 ?R. Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda\u8217's Mechila\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 56b opens with a discussion of {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position on {\i kesuvah} modification. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} permits a woman to be {\i mochel} \u8212- to waive \u8212- a portion of her {\i kesuvah}, so that the effective obligation falls below the base amounts of 200 or 100 {\i zuz}. What he does not permit is an initial stipulation that the {\i kesuvah} should begin at a lesser amount. The {\i gemara} presses this distinction: if one may be {\i masneh al mah she-kasuv ba-Torah} \u8212- stipulate against the monetary parameters of biblical obligations in marriage, such as {\i she\u8217'er kesut ve-onah} \u8212- why should it be impossible to modify a rabbinic institution? {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's answer is that the rabbis wished to strengthen their own institution of {\i kesuvah} more than the biblical monetary obligations: {\i asu chizuk le-divreihem yoter mi-shel Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chizuk LeKesubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle underlying {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position is therefore that certain rabbinic institutions receive a heightened level of protection \u8212- a {\i chizuk} \u8212- such that direct modification at the outset is disallowed, even where modification of analogous biblical obligations would be permitted. The base obligation must remain intact; only afterward may the woman be {\i mochel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chamorim v. Demai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an apparent tension from a {\i mishnah} concerning donkey drivers who enter a city. One driver says of his own grain that it is new \u8212- inferior in quality \u8212- while his companion\u8217's is old, and that his own has not had {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot} separated while his companion\u8217's has. The ruling in that {\i mishnah} is that they are not believed, the concern being that in the next city they reverse their claims as part of a scheme \u8212- with {\i Rabbi Yehuda} ruling that they are believed. This seems problematic: if {\i Rabbi Yehuda} is so committed to reinforcing rabbinic institutions, why does he not protect the rabbinic institution of {\i demai} \u8212- the requirement to separate {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot} from produce of {\i amei ha-aretz}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} itself notes that the case is not quite parallel: the {\i kesuvah} concerns a definitive rabbinic financial obligation, whereas {\i demai} involves a doubt about whether {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot} were separated in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye & Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} offers the following principle: {\i vadai de-divreihem avdu rabbanan chizuk} \u8212- a definite rabbinic institution, such as the {\i kesuvah} obligations, the rabbis reinforced and said cannot be directly modified. {\i Safek de-divreihem lo avdu rabbanan chizuk} \u8212- a rabbinic institution that is itself predicated on a doubt, such as the obligation to tithe in {\i demai} cases, has a weaker foundation and was not subject to the same reinforcement. {\i Rava} offers a slightly different framing: it is not merely the doubt-based structure of {\i demai} that explains {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's leniency there, but rather the fact that {\i demai} as a category is inherently treated with numerous leniencies, since in principle we believe that {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot} were in fact separated. Either way, {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's willingness to be lenient about {\i demai} does not undercut his position of strengthening the {\i kesuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir Tenai Batel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} takes a more stringent position. The {\i mishnah} states that anyone who stipulates less than 200 for a {\i betulah} or 100 for an {\i almanah} renders the relations {\i bi\u8217'as zenut} \u8212- more akin to fornication than to the protected institution of marriage. One might initially read {\i Rabbi Meir} as saying that the obligation can in fact be reduced, but the consequence is that one thereby transgresses; the act of living together in that condition is spiritually compromised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, goes significantly further. From the language {\i kol ha-pocheis} \u8212- anyone who \u8220"reduces\u8221" \u8212- the {\i gemara} infers that {\i Rabbi Meir} holds not merely that a transgression occurs, but that the attempt to reduce the amount entirely fails: {\i alma kasavar tenai batel} \u8212- the stipulation is void. She retains the full amount; the attempt at reduction does not succeed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate difficulty: if the {\i tenai} is {\i batel} and she in fact has the full 200, how can it be called {\i bi\u8217'as zenut}? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i lo samcha da\u8217'atah} \u8212- even though she actually retains the full amount, because the husband declared {\i lays lich ella maneh} \u8212- you are only entitled to a hundred \u8212- she subjectively does not feel the protections of marriage are in place. That subjective insecurity is sufficient to render the relationship {\i bi\u8217'as zenut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesuvah De\u8217'Oraisa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why {\i Rabbi Meir} holds the {\i tenai} to be {\i batel}. It is well established that {\i Rabbi Meir} rules that one who stipulates against a biblical monetary obligation \u8212- {\i kol ha-masneh al mah she-kasuv ba-Torah tenao batel} \u8212- fails to achieve the stipulation. But the {\i gemara} assumes that this specifically concerns biblical obligations; if a rabbinic obligation is involved, the stipulation would be binding even for {\i Rabbi Meir}. So why cannot one stipulate against the {\i kesuvah}, a rabbinic institution?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer is striking: {\i kasavar Rabbi Meir kesuvah de\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Meir} may hold that the {\i kesuvah} is a biblical obligation. Therefore the condition fails as an instance of {\i masneh al mah she-kasuv ba-Torah}, and the woman retains the full amount. The {\i gemara} thus transforms the {\i mishnah}: rather than reading {\i Rabbi Meir} as saying you can reduce the amount but transgress in doing so, the {\i gemara} reads him as saying the reduction never takes effect, the full biblical obligation remains, yet the subjective harm to the woman\u8217's sense of security still produces {\i bi\u8217'as zenut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is presented as a genuine {\i chidush} on the {\i gemara}\u8217's part. One might have simply said that {\i Rabbi Meir} reinforces the {\i kesuvah} institution more vigorously than other rabbinic obligations. Instead the {\i gemara} grounds it in {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's view of {\i kesuvah} as {\i de\u8217'oraisa}, which provides the principled basis for why the {\i tenai} is {\i batel} and why even an attempt at {\i mechila} would presumably be ineffective under {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meir, Yehuda, Yossi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanya 3 Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} presents three positions on the question of {\i kesuvah} modification. {\i Rabbi Meir} states that any attempt to stipulate less than 200 for a {\i betulah} or 100 for an {\i almanah} constitutes {\i bi\u8217'as zenut}. {\i Rabbi Yossi} says one is {\i rashai} \u8212- permitted \u8212- to stipulate a lesser amount from the outset. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} occupies the middle position: one may write a {\i shtar} of 200 and the woman writes a receipt saying she has already received 100, or, in the case of a widow, she writes a receipt for 50 of the 100. Thus {\i Rabbi Yehuda} requires the base amount to be formally present but allows it to be immediately reduced through a receipt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yossi Rashai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's position \u8212- that the parties may stipulate a lesser amount from the outset \u8212- is the most permissive of the three. He goes beyond {\i Rabbi Yehuda} in dispensing with the requirement to begin formally with the full base amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metaltelim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's permissive stance by citing a separate {\i baraita} that appears to show him as protective of a woman\u8217's interests: {\i ein osim kesuvat isha metaltelin mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- one does not designate chattel to serve as the lien for a woman\u8217's {\i kesuvah} for reasons of {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam}. The concern is that chattel, unlike real estate, can deteriorate or break \u8212- the Wedgwood china, as it were \u8212- leaving the woman unable to collect her full {\i kesuvah}. {\i Rabbi Yossi} in that {\i baraita} questions what {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam} is served, given that the chattel\u8217's value is not fixed and may diminish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At first glance {\i Rabbi Yossi} seems to be asking why the designation is even permitted \u8212- pointing to the very concern the {\i Tanna Kamma} used as a reason to prohibit it. The {\i gemara} clarifies that both authorities say designation of chattel alone is prohibited. The debate is about a case where the husband has accepted {\i achrayus} \u8212- personal liability \u8212- for the chattel\u8217's value. The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that once the husband accepts {\i achrayus}, the designation is permitted, since if the chattel breaks he remains liable for the full amount from his other property. {\i Rabbi Yossi} counters that this still does not fully protect the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achrayus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's objection is that even if the husband has accepted {\i achrayus} against the chattel\u8217's destruction, the chattel may not break \u8212- it may simply decline in value. If the wife is limited to collecting from the designated china, and the china is worth less when she comes to collect, she loses out. Accepting liability for the item\u8217's destruction is not sufficient; it does not address the risk of depreciation. Thus {\i Rabbi Yossi} holds that even with {\i achrayus}, one may not designate chattel for the {\i kesuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hadaat L\u8217'Isha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i gemara} poses the sharpest question on {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's permissive position regarding the initial stipulation. Over there, in the chattel case, {\i Rabbi Yossi} is concerned even about a potential decrease in value \u8212- he is protecting the woman against a scenario she did not agree to and may not have anticipated. Here, by contrast, where the parties are stipulating at the outset for a lesser {\i kesuvah} amount, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i hashta u\u8217'mah hasam d\u8217'dilma pochasi chayesh Rabbi Yossi} \u8212- there, where the concern is only a possibility of diminution, {\i Rabbi Yossi} worries; {\i hacha d\u8217'vadai kapachta} \u8212- here, where the woman is definitely receiving less \u8212- how much more should he object?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer resolves the tension cleanly: {\i hasam lo yada d\u8217'simchol} \u8212- in the chattel case, the woman does not know she is consenting to a risk of loss. She wants her full 200 {\i zuz} and the husband is creating a scenario in which she may not collect it. That is a unilateral compromise of her interests. {\i Aval hacha yad\u8217'ah ve-kamachla} \u8212- but here, the lesser amount was agreed to, she was a party to the stipulation. {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's concern is exclusively with unilateral undermining of the woman\u8217's position; where she knowingly and consensually agrees to the lesser amount, he permits it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three-way structure thus stands: {\i Rabbi Yossi} permits a lesser amount with the woman\u8217's consent; {\i Rabbi Yehuda} requires formal commencement with the full amount followed by a receipt; {\i Rabbi Meir} holds that no reduction can be achieved and that any attempt renders relations {\i bi\u8217'as zenut}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kesuvah Irkesa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Irksa Kesuvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implications of {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position are illustrated through a case involving the sister of {\i Rami bar Chama}, who was married to {\i Rabbi Avya}. Her {\i kesuvah} was lost \u8212- not that she was divorcing, but simply that the document had gone missing. This is in fact a real-world occurrence. A replacement document for a lost {\i kesuvah} is known as a {\i kesuvah d\u8217'irkesa}, from the Aramaic {\i irkas}, meaning lost. Such a document is rewritten by the original witnesses, who attest that the original {\i kesuvah}, which has been lost, was signed by them and contained such-and-such terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical question is whether the couple may continue to live together \u8212- including having marital relations \u8212- while the replacement has not yet been written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Dinur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They came before {\i Rav Yosef}. {\i Rav Yosef} cited a ruling transmitted by {\i Rav Yehuda} in the name of {\i Shmuel}: this prohibition \u8212- that living without the documentation of the {\i kesuvah} constitutes {\i bi\u8217'as zenut} \u8212- represents {\i divrei Rabbi Meir}, the position of {\i Rabbi Meir} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Permit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sages, however, hold that a person may go {\i shtayim ve-shalosh shanim b\u8217'lo kesuvah} \u8212- two or three years without a written {\i kesuvah}. The formulation of two or three years is understood as an idiom for an extended but not indefinite period; it expresses the principle that there is no urgent imperative to immediately replace the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially wonder why the absence of the written document should matter at all. The obligation itself \u8212- 200 or 100 {\i zuz} \u8212- exists regardless of whether a document is present; we have already learned that even without a written {\i kesuvah} the liability stands. However, the {\i gemara} applies {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's logic: just as he holds that a woman\u8217's subjective belief that she has only 100 {\i zuz} creates {\i bi\u8217'as zenut} even when she actually has 200, so too the absence of the physical document creates a subjective sense in the woman that she is unprotected. She may know the debt exists in principle, but without the document she may fear he will claim he already paid. The document provides proof of non-payment. Without it, she does not feel fully secured within the institution of marriage, and for {\i Rabbi Meir} that subjective insecurity is sufficient to classify the relations as {\i bi\u8217'as zenut}. The sages disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Gezerot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} raises a challenge: we have a tradition in the name of {\i Rav Nachman} citing {\i Shmuel} that {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Meir bi-gzeirosav} \u8212- we rule like {\i Rabbi Meir} in his {\i gzeiros}. This is a well-known principle. It is worth noting that the {\i gemara} refers to this ruling as a {\i gzeira}, which is somewhat striking, since {\i gzeiros} are typically rabbinic safeguards against potential violations. Here, the {\i gemara} treats {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's classification of living without a written {\i kesuvah} as {\i bi\u8217'as zenut} as a type of {\i gzeira} \u8212- a protective measure in service of the sanctity of the marital institution rather than a safeguard against a separate prohibition. {\i Abaye}\u8217's implication is clear: if we rule like {\i Rabbi Meir} in his {\i gzeiros}, go write her a substitute {\i kesuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Ma\u8217'aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical ruling emerges from this discussion: {\i l\u8217'maaseh}, one should move promptly to write a substitute {\i kesuvah}. This applies not only when a {\i kesuvah} is lost at a wedding \u8212- a real scenario that occurs \u8212- but also in cases where a couple married decades ago can no longer locate their original document, or where the signatures on the original have faded and become illegible. In all such cases, a new {\i kesuvah d\u8217'irkesa} must be written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises regarding duplicate documents: if the original witnesses rewrite the identical {\i kesuvah}, the concern is that the woman might now possess two documents evidencing the same debt and attempt to collect twice. For this reason the replacement {\i kesuvah} uses a different {\i nusach}, indicating that the original was lost and that the replacement document attests to the contents of the original. Once payment is eventually made and the document is surrendered, that concern dissolves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rama TRG\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rama} addresses the question of whether, according to {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position, a couple must actually cease living together \u8212- sleep in separate quarters \u8212- until the replacement {\i kesuvah} is written. The {\i Rama} rules that in our era, after {\i takanas Rabbeinu Gershom}, which prohibits a husband from divorcing his wife against her will, the concern underlying {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's {\i bi\u8217'as zenut} is fundamentally ameliorated. {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's concern is that without the protection of a {\i kesuvah}, the woman feels that the relationship lacks the full safeguards of marriage \u8212- that the husband might callously divorce her the next morning. The {\i kesuvah} protects against that fear. Now that {\i takanas Rabbeinu Gershom} prevents unilateral divorce entirely, the woman knows she cannot be dismissed against her will; her sense of security within the marriage is not dependent on the written document in the same way. Therefore, the {\i Rama} holds that today we do not require the couple to cease living together before the replacement is written, though the substitute {\i kesuvah} should still be produced as quickly as practicable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Techila v. Sof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R.Y. ben Levi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i Rav Dimi} came from {\i Eretz Yisrael}, he transmitted the following in the name of {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi} citing {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} in the name of {\i Bar Kappara}: the three-way dispute among {\i Rabbi Meir}, {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, and {\i Rabbi Yossi} as to whether the {\i kesuvah} amount may be changed applies only {\i be-techilah} \u8212- at the beginning, meaning before {\i chuppah}. {\i Aval be-sof} \u8212- but at the end, meaning after {\i bi\u8217'ah}, all agree that she cannot waive the base amount. Once the full obligation has been activated through consummation, even {\i Rabbi Yossi} would hold that modifying the base amount below 200 or 100 is impermissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} disagrees and holds {\i bein b\u8217'zo u-vein b\u8217'zo machlokes} \u8212- the three-way dispute applies both at the beginning and at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Abbahu Harmony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Abbahu} reports that this apparent dispute between {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} and {\i Rabbi Yochanan} was actually clarified for him personally by {\i Rabbi Yochanan}, who said that he and {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} do not in fact disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the harmonization, one must recognize that three stages are in play: before {\i chuppah}, during {\i chuppah}, and {\i bi\u8217'ah}. When {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} said the debate is at {\i techilah}, he meant {\i techilas chuppah} \u8212- prior to the {\i chuppah}. When he said that at {\i sof} all agree the amount cannot be changed, he meant {\i sof bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- after consummation. {\i Rabbi Yochanan}, when he said the debate applies in both {\i zo u-vein zo}, meant that the dispute extends through {\i techilas chuppah} and {\i sof chuppah} \u8212- the beginning and end of the {\i chuppah} event \u8212- which is the same as saying the debate extends from before the {\i chuppah} through the {\i chuppah} itself and up until {\i bi\u8217'ah}. Both authorities therefore agree: the debate among the {\i Tannaim} is relevant through and including the {\i chuppah} stage; once {\i bi\u8217'ah} has occurred, all agree the full obligation is locked in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Dimi Alt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravin} arrived with an alternative transmission of these same positions. In his version, {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} said the debate is {\i le-vasof} \u8212- at the end \u8212- while at {\i techilah} \u8212- the beginning \u8212- all agree that a modification is permissible. And {\i Rabbi Yochanan} again says the debate applies in both cases. {\i Rabbi Abbahu} then harmonizes this version as well, again in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: he and {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} do not disagree. What {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} called {\i le-vasof} in this version is {\i sof chuppah} \u8212- after the {\i chuppah} but before {\i bi\u8217'ah}. What all agree is permissible at {\i techilah} is {\i techilas chuppah} \u8212- before the {\i chuppah} begins. And when {\i Rabbi Yochanan} says both cases are in dispute, he means {\i techilas bi\u8217'ah} and {\i sof bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the stages surrounding consummation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two transmissions thus present mirror images. In {\i Rav Dimi}\u8217's version, all agree before the {\i chuppah} that a modification is possible and all agree after {\i bi\u8217'ah} that it is not, with the dispute situated in the middle stages. In {\i Ravin}\u8217's version, the arrangement is the reverse: all agree the stages prior to {\i chuppah} allow modification, and the dispute concerns the stages from {\i chuppah} onward. In both cases {\i Rabbi Abbahu}\u8217's harmonization collapses the apparent disagreement between {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} and {\i Rabbi Yochanan} by showing that what one called {\i techilah} the other called {\i sof} and vice versa, depending on which referent \u8212- {\i chuppah} or {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- they used as their anchor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Sevara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} remarks that had {\i Rabbi Abbahu} not transmitted the clarification from {\i Rabbi Yochanan} that he and {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} were saying the same thing, he himself would have preferred to read their positions differently: he would have said that {\i Rabbi Yochanan} and {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} are genuinely disagreeing \u8212- and therefore that {\i Rav Dimi} and {\i Ravin} are not disagreeing with each other at all, but are simply using different language to convey the same transmission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what practical difference does it make which pair we prefer to be in actual disagreement? {\i Rav Pappa}\u8217's answer, cited by both {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafos}, is a principle of transmission: {\i d\u8217'pligi trei amorai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'nafshaihu v\u8217'lo pligi trei amorai aliba d\u8217'chad amora} \u8212- it is preferable that two {\i Amoraim} be in genuine disagreement with each other based on their respective {\i sevara} \u8212- their independent reasoning \u8212- than that two {\i Amoraim} be in dispute about what a third {\i Amora} actually said. Two {\i Amoraim} reasoning differently about where the {\i machlokes} among the {\i Tannaim} logically falls is the normal stuff of {\i Shas} \u8212- each has a view about which stage of the marital process is the more plausible location for the dispute. But when the disagreement is about what a named {\i Amora} actually transmitted, that implies a problem in the chain of transmission: one of them got it wrong. {\i Rav Pappa} says that if he had to choose which debate to collapse, he would have preferred to keep {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} and {\i Rabbi Yochanan} as genuinely arguing from {\i sevara} and reconcile {\i Rav Dimi} and {\i Ravin} as expressing the same position in different language. Since {\i Rabbi Abbahu} ruled otherwise, we follow his report, but {\i Rav Pappa}\u8217's methodological preference is noted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mezonot & Trousseau\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shneim Asar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} introduces the following rule: {\i notnim le-vesulah shneim asar chodesh mishetav\u8217'ah ha-ba\u8217'al l\u8217'farnes es atzmah} \u8212- one gives a {\i betulah} twelve months from the time the husband demands that she prepare herself, meaning from when he indicates his readiness to proceed from {\i kiddushin} to {\i nissuin}. This is the period she is entitled to assemble her trousseau and prepare for marriage. Importantly, the twelve months is not counted from the moment of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the betrothal may have taken place years earlier \u8212- but from the moment the husband presses for the marriage to proceed. {\i Ve-cheshem she-notnin le-isha kach notnin le-ish} \u8212- and just as she is entitled to twelve months, so too he may claim twelve months to prepare his household and resources for the marriage, {\i l\u8217'farnes es atzmo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almana 30 Yom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For an {\i almanah} \u8212- a widow entering a second marriage \u8212- the preparation period is shortened to thirty days. The assumption is that she is more independent, more prepared, and that the second marriage is a less elaborate event. The question of whether the groom similarly receives only thirty days in the case of an {\i almanah} is presumed to follow in parallel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ochelet Mishelo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the twelve-month clock expires and the parties have not yet proceeded to {\i nissuin}, the husband\u8217's obligation of financial support begins. He must now pay for her {\i mezonot} \u8212- her food and expenses \u8212- even though the {\i nissuin} has not yet taken place. {\i Ochelet mishelo} \u8212- she eats from his resources. The beginning of this tractate had addressed the related question of whether fault matters when the deadline is missed; the {\i mishnah} here does not enter that discussion but simply establishes that from the expiration of the deadline onward, he bears the cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teruma Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the husband is a {\i kohen}, the {\i mishnah} adds that once she begins eating from his resources, she may eat {\i teruma}. This teaches two things simultaneously: that an {\i arusa} \u8212- a betrothed woman, not yet fully married \u8212- of a {\i kohen} is halachically entitled to eat {\i teruma}, and that his obligation of financial support may be discharged by providing {\i teruma}, which is less costly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarfon v. Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Tarfon} holds that the husband may satisfy his entire food obligation by providing {\i teruma}. {\i Rabbi Akiva} disagrees: at least half must be {\i chullin} \u8212- ordinary food that she may eat at all times. The reason is that for a substantial portion of the month she is {\i tamei} as a {\i niddah}, and during that period she cannot eat {\i teruma}. It would therefore be inequitable to give her only {\i teruma}, since she would be forced to sell it and receive in exchange a lesser quantity of {\i chullin}. {\i Rabbi Akiva} requires that at minimum half of his support obligation be discharged in {\i chullin} to ensure she is not disadvantaged during the periods of her {\i tum\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam Teruma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then introduces a case that might appear analogous but is treated differently: {\i ha-yabam eino ma\u8217'achil bi-teruma} \u8212- a {\i yabam} cannot feed his {\i yevamah} {\i teruma} even if he is a {\i kohen}. A woman whose husband \u8212- a {\i kohen} \u8212- has died and whose brother-in-law has not yet performed {\i yibbum} is a {\i shomeret yabam}. Despite her pending obligation to marry the {\i yabam}, and despite the existence of a {\i mitzvah} to perform {\i yibbum}, she does not gain entitlement to eat {\i teruma} through that waiting status. There is no twelve-month clock that runs after which she begins eating {\i teruma}; the category is simply different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Months Yabam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then considers a case where a woman has spent part of her twelve-month waiting period as the {\i arusa} of the original husband and part as {\i shomeret yabam}. For instance, six months elapsed as the {\i arusa} of the {\i kohen} and six months waiting for the {\i yabam}. Or the extreme cases: eleven months and twenty-nine days as the {\i arusa} and one final day as {\i shomeret yabam} before the deadline, or one day as the {\i arusa} and then the remainder as {\i shomeret yabam}. In all these configurations, she does not eat {\i teruma}. The {\i mishnah} notes that the last case \u8212- nearly all twelve months waiting for the {\i yabam} \u8212- is in a sense obvious, since even if one waited five years as a {\i shomeret yabam} she would never acquire entitlement to eat {\i teruma}; {\i Rashi} characterizes that case as somewhat of a rhetorical inclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then preserves an earlier ruling \u8212- introduced with {\i zu mishna rishona} \u8212- and reports a subsequent revision by a later {\i beis din}: {\i ein ha-isha ochelet bi-terumah ad she-tikanes la-chuppah} \u8212- a woman may not eat {\i teruma} until she enters the {\i chuppah}. This revision delays the entitlement to eat {\i teruma} until full {\i nissuin}, which actually increases the husband\u8217's food costs, since he must provide {\i chullin} throughout the betrothal period.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rivka Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Rivka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mina hani mili} \u8212- what is the biblical source for the twelve-month waiting period? {\i Rav Chisda} derives it from the verse describing the departure of {\i Rivka}: {\i vayomer achiha ve-immah teshev ha-na\u8217'arah ittanu yamim o asor} \u8212- her brother and mother said, let the girl stay with us {\i yamim} or ten. The request was made to delay her departure to marry {\i Yitzchak}, and one side requested a year while the other requested ten months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yamim Zman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} analyzes the word {\i yamim}. If it means merely two days \u8212- the minimum implied by a plural \u8212- that is not how people speak; no one bargains for \u8220"two days or ten days\u8221" in that order, since one normally bargains downward. Alternatively, could {\i yamim} mean a month? The {\i gemara} rules that out through a {\i gezeira shava}: we derive the meaning of unqualified {\i yamim} from the case in which the same word appears unqualified, as in the verse regarding the redemption period of a house in a walled city \u8212- {\i yamim tihyeh ge\u8217'ulato} \u8212- which is established as a year. One does not derive unqualified {\i yamim} from a case where the word is qualified by {\i chodesh}, as in {\i chodesh yamim}, since the modifier there shapes the meaning. Therefore {\i yamim} alone means a year. The brother and mother were requesting a year, or alternatively ten months, and the servant pressed them; but in principle the right to request a year is established from this narrative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketana Delay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites {\i Rabbi Zeira}\u8217's transmission of a {\i baraita}: {\i ketannah bein hi u-vein aviha yecholim le\u8217'akev} \u8212- when the girl is still a {\i ketannah}, either she or her father can delay the {\i nissuin}. The twelve-month clock is designed for a case where she is no longer a {\i ketannah}; while she remains a {\i ketannah}, the right to postpone is broader \u8212- they may hold back for the entire duration of her {\i ketannah} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask what practical force this right has: can a {\i bet din} force a woman into a {\i chuppah} against her will regardless? {\i Tosafos} addresses this by noting that if the withholding party did not have a recognized right to delay, financial consequences would follow \u8212- the recalcitrant party could be penalized, for example by reducing the {\i kesuvah} amount over time, which is the mechanism the later {\i gemaros} discuss for enforcing marital obligations. The right to delay removes the applicability of such penalties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Av Achrayut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately questions one aspect of this {\i baraita}: {\i bishlama ei ihi me\u8217'akva} \u8212- it is understandable that she herself can delay, since she has a personal stake in her readiness for marriage. {\i Ela aviha, ei nicha lah, aviha mai nafka lei mina} \u8212- but if she herself is willing to proceed to {\i nissuin}, what standing does the father have to object? Once {\i kiddushin} has taken place and she is ready, what legitimate claim does the father have to prevent the marriage from proceeding to its next stage?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Av Tima\u8217'as\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that the father\u8217's concern is the following: {\i hashta lo yade\u8217'ah} \u8212- right now, as a {\i ketannah}, she does not fully appreciate what she is agreeing to. {\i Le-machar mimreda} \u8212- tomorrow, once she is married, she may rebel, grow despondent with her husband, {\i ve-nafka} and he will divorce her. {\i Ve-asya ve-nafala ilavai} \u8212- and she will return and become my responsibility. The father foresees that a premature marriage may end badly and that he, socially if not legally, will bear the burden of the fallout. It is notable that the {\i gemara} could have invoked the father\u8217's financial entitlements during {\i arusah} \u8212- his rights to her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha} \u8212- as his basis for objection. Instead the {\i gemara} chose to anchor his right in paternal concern for the daughter\u8217's welfare and the anticipation of practical consequences he will face if the marriage fails.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ein Poskin Ketana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Nisuin Ketana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Abba bar Levi} is cited with an explicit prohibition: {\i ein poskin al ha-ketanah le-hasi\u8217'ah keshehi ketanah} \u8212- one does not make an arrangement to proceed to {\i nissuin} with a {\i ketanah} while she is still a {\i ketanah}. This goes beyond saying she has a right to delay; it is an affirmative prohibition against arranging the {\i nissuin} itself while she remains a minor. The {\i gemara} here is effectively signaling that {\i nissuin} during {\i ketanah} status is undesirable. {\i Kiddushin} during {\i ketanah} status is legally valid; they may be betrothed at a very young age and remain so for years. But the {\i gemara} here is drawing a firm line at the consummation of the marriage in the full sense of {\i nissuin}: we do not want that happening while she is a {\i ketanah}. This is worth noting as a significant statement by the {\i gemara} about the proper age at which marriage in its full sense should take place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poskin Lehashia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} continues: {\i aval poskin al ha-ketanah le-hasi\u8217'ah keshehi gedolah} \u8212- but one may make an arrangement now, while she is still a {\i ketanah}, that the {\i nissuin} will take place once she becomes an adult. So the father may plan ahead and commit to a {\i nissuin} date that falls after {\i bat mitzvah}, even while she is currently still a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mifchada Chalsha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue that even this should be prohibited: perhaps informing the girl now, while she is still young, that she is scheduled to be married on a specific date after her {\i bat mitzvah} will cause her distress. {\i Dilma mifchada me-hashta ve-chalsha} \u8212- perhaps she will be frightened from this moment onward and grow despondent. The {\i gemara} dismisses this concern. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- we are taught that we are not worried about this. The arrangement for a future {\i nissuin} after she reaches adulthood is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Huna Bogeret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna} introduces a further ruling: {\i bogeres yom echad ve-niskadshah notnim lah shloshim yom ke-almanah} \u8212- if a woman has reached {\i bagrus} \u8212- the age of 12 and a half \u8212- and then entered into {\i kiddushin}, she is given only thirty days rather than twelve months to prepare for {\i nissuin}. In {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's view, a {\i bogeres} is treated like an {\i almanah} in this respect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Bogeret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this from a {\i baraita}: {\i bogeres harei hi ke-tavu\u8217'ah} \u8212- a {\i bogeres} is treated as if the husband had already demanded that the marriage proceed. {\i Tavu\u8217'ah} is the terminology for when one party says it is time for {\i nissuin}, which triggers the twelve-month clock for a {\i betulah}. The {\i baraita} seems to say that reaching {\i bagrus} automatically triggers that clock \u8212- and it is a twelve-month clock, not thirty days. This constitutes a direct {\i tiyuvta} of {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's position. The {\i gemara} attempts to deflect this by suggesting that {\i ke-tavu\u8217'ah} here means like the demand-triggered clock for an {\i almanah}, which is thirty days \u8212- but this reading is rejected as too forced.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Reconciled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tva\u8217'ah Eidun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} clarifies the underlying mechanics: after {\i eirusin} has taken place, either party \u8212- husband or wife \u8212- may demand that {\i nissuin} proceed. {\i Bein she-tav\u8217'ah ha-ba\u8217'al ve-hi me\u8217'akeves} \u8212- whether the husband demanded and she sought to delay, {\i bein she-tav\u8217'ah hi u-va\u8217'al me\u8217'akev} \u8212- or she demanded and he sought to delay: in both cases, the clock for the preparation period is triggered and the other party is given time to prepare.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shas Tvuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} further specifies: {\i notnim lo shneim asar chodesh mi-shas tevi\u8217'ah} \u8212- twelve months are granted from the time of the demand, {\i aval lo mi-shas eirusin} \u8212- not from the time of {\i eirusin} itself. The {\i eirusin} may have taken place years before; the clock begins only when one of the parties actively presses for the transition to {\i nissuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bogeret Ktavuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then states: {\i u-vogeres harei hi ke-tavu\u8217'ah} \u8212- and a {\i bogeres} is treated as equivalent to having been demanded of. This means that the moment a woman attains {\i bagrus}, it is as if the demand for {\i nissuin} has been made, and her twelve-month preparation period begins automatically from that point. This directly contradicts {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's ruling of thirty days and is declared {\i tiyuvta de-Rav Huna tiyuvta} \u8212- a definitive refutation of {\i Rav Huna}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ein Poskin Ketana (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Resolve\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} resolves the tension by distinguishing between two scenarios involving a {\i bogeres}. The ruling that a {\i bogeres} gets twelve months \u8212- because her reaching {\i bagrus} functions as an automatic demand \u8212- refers to a woman who has just entered {\i bagrus} and whose {\i kiddushin} was contracted around that time. The ruling of thirty days, akin to an {\i almanah}, applies to a {\i bogeres} who has already been in {\i bagrus} for twelve months before entering into {\i kiddushin} \u8212- that is, a woman who is 13 and a half years old at the time of {\i kiddushin}. By that point, the automatic demand that came with {\i bagrus} has long since been triggered; she has already had the full benefit of the twelve-month window. When {\i kiddushin} is contracted now, after that window has elapsed, she receives only thirty days, like an {\i almanah}, since the delay is no longer attributable to preparation for an imminent marriage but to a situation that has been waiting far beyond the normal timeframe. {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's ruling was therefore not refuted but rather correctly limited to this specific scenario.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Reconciled (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Naara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises a structural difficulty with the entire framework. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's twelve-month rule \u8212- triggered by a demand for {\i nissuin} \u8212- appears to apply to the paradigmatic case. But when we examine the cases on either end: if the woman is a {\i ketanah}, the right to delay extends beyond twelve months for the entire duration of her {\i ketanah} status; if she is a {\i bogeres}, the clock starts automatically without any demand. The case where the twelve-month clock triggered by a {\i tevi\u8217'ah} \u8212- a demand \u8212- is the operative rule is specifically when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, the period between twelve and twelve and a half years old.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a complication: if she was a {\i na\u8217'arah} when the demand was made and the twelve-month clock began ticking, but she transitions to {\i bogeres} six months into that period, what happens? At the moment of {\i bagrus} the automatic demand kicks in independently \u8212- but she is already mid-way through a twelve-month period. {\i Tosafos} works through the implications of this timing, examining whether the clock that began during her {\i na\u8217'arah} status continues or is superseded by the automatic {\i tevi\u8217'ah} created by {\i bagrus}, and at what stage the various rules intersect. The {\i gemara}\u8217's basic architecture, as {\i Tosafos} articulates it, reserves the twelve-month period for the {\i na\u8217'arah} stage specifically; the {\i ketanah} has more than twelve months and the {\i bogeres} has twelve months counted differently \u8212- from the moment of {\i bagrus} itself, without the need for any explicit demand.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 56b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Meir: Ketubah Stipula\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda vs.\u160 ?Meir on Waiver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a discussion about the respective positions of Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Meir regarding whether the obligations of the {\i ketubah} can be modified. The prior context established that Rabbi Yehuda allows a woman to be {\i mochel} \u8212- to waive \u8212- part of the {\i ketubah}, so that effectively the operative amount falls below the standard 200 {\i zuz} for a {\i betulah} or 100 for an {\i almanah}. However, Rabbi Yehuda does not permit the amount to be stipulated as less than those figures from the outset. The {\i Gemara} had asked: why not? He could say that one is permitted to be {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} \u8212- to modify the parameters of the monetary aspects of biblical obligations of {\i kiddushin}, such as {\i shear} and {\i kesut} (though not {\i onah}). So why can he not modify a rabbinic one? Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's answer is that the rabbis wanted to strengthen the rabbinic institution of the {\i ketubah} \u8212- {\i asu chizuk l\u8217'divreihem yoter mishel Torah} \u8212- and therefore the base obligation must remain in place, even if the monetary obligations of the Torah could otherwise be modified. The woman must start with 200 or 100; only afterwards may she be {\i mochel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a challenge from the case of donkey drivers ({\i chamarin}): we were taught in the {\i Mishnah} that if merchants come to a city and one says {\i sheli chadash v\u8217'shel chaveirai yashan} \u8212- mine is new grain (and therefore inferior) and my friend\u8217's is old \u8212- or that his goods are untithed while his companion\u8217's are {\i metukan} with {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} separated, they are not believed ({\i einam ne\u8217'emanin}). But Rabbi Yehuda says they are believed ({\i ne\u8217'emanin}). The issue concerns {\i demai} \u8212- the rabbinic concern that an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} may not have separated {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros}. Why does Rabbi Yehuda not reinforce this rabbinic institution as he reinforces the {\i ketubah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answers: the rabbis reinforced only what was a definite ({\i vadai}) institution. The {\i ketubah} obligations are definite, and therefore the rabbis said they cannot be directly modified. The concern about {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} in the case of {\i demai}, however, is inherently based on doubt \u8212- it is only a {\i safek} \u8212- and something that is fundamentally weaker in that way was not reinforced in the same fashion. Rava adds a further point: it is not merely that {\i demai} is based on doubt in general, but specifically {\i demai} has many leniencies because in principle we actually believe that {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} were separated. Given that, the rabbis did not bother reinforcing it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The bottom line of this section is that Rabbi Yehuda is less strict than Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Meir does not allow the woman to be {\i mochel} any part of the {\i ketubah} at all \u8212- the effective obligation for Rabbi Meir must be the full 200 or 100. Rabbi Yehuda allows a woman to be {\i mochel} after the fact but does not allow the amount to be modified at the outset, because even though he permits modification of the biblical monetary obligations of marriage, this rabbinic one he wishes to reinforce, and therefore the base obligation must stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stipulation Nullity Theo\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to Rabbi Meir\u8217's position as it appears in the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- {\i kol hapocheis l\u8217'betulah mi\u8217'masayim u\u8217'l\u8217'almanah mi\u8217'maneh harei zu biat zenut} \u8212- one might initially have understood Rabbi Meir as saying that you cannot effectively make the obligation less, and that continuing to live in such a marital state constitutes something closer to {\i zenut} than to marriage. The whole weight of marriage \u8212- the {\i ketubah}, the reciprocal obligations and protections \u8212- is what gives {\i nissu\u8217'in} its character. So the simple reading of Rabbi Meir is that you may have lowered the amount but you transgress.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}, however, takes this one step further. From the word {\i hapocheis} \u8212- one who is {\i attempting} to reduce the amount \u8212- the {\i Gemara} infers {\i afilu b\u8217'tnai}, even if you explicitly stipulate that it should be less. {\i Alma kasavar tnai batel} \u8212- it seems that Rabbi Meir holds the stipulation itself is void. This is not a simple inference from the {\i Mishnah}, as the {\i Gemara} acknowledges and {\i Tosafos} addresses at length, but the {\i Gemara} assumes it as Rabbi Meir\u8217's position: the attempt to make the {\i ketubah} less does not succeed. The woman retains the full amount; {\i v\u8217'is lei} \u8212- she still has her 200.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biat Zenut Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the stipulation is void and the woman still has the full amount in principle, how can the situation constitute {\i biat zenut}? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i ve\u8217'cheivan de\u8217'amar lah leis lich ela maneh} \u8212- since the husband told her that she only has 100, {\i lo samcha daatah} \u8212- her sense of security is not established. Even though in truth and in law she has 200, her subjective sense that she is unprotected \u8212- because she believes she only has 100 \u8212- is sufficient to create {\i biat zenut}. {\i V\u8217'hava lah biatah biat zenut} \u8212- and therefore the relations take on the character of {\i zenut}. This is a striking position: even a legally full {\i ketubah} does not prevent the designation of {\i biat zenut} when the woman subjectively believes she lacks protection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah d\u8217'Oraisa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks why the stipulation is void at all. It notes that we know Rabbi Meir holds that if you try to stipulate against a Torah obligation, even a financial one, the condition is {\i batel} \u8212- but {\i habderabbanan tnai kayyam} \u8212- specifically against a rabbinic obligation, an explicit stipulation would be binding. So why can you not stipulate against the {\i ketubah}? Could one not simply say that Rabbi Meir is reinforcing the {\i ketubah} institution in a special way, given that the stakes are marriage and {\i biat zenut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} does not take that route. Rather than treating the {\i ketubah} as uniquely protected among rabbinic enactments, the {\i Gemara} says: {\i kasavar Rabbi Meir ketubah de\u8217'Oraisa} \u8212- Rabbi Meir may actually hold that the {\i ketubah} is a biblical obligation. That debate appeared earlier in the {\i masechet}. And since it is biblical, one cannot stipulate against it \u8212- {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} \u8212- and therefore the stipulation is void. This is a significant {\i chiddush}. Rabbi Meir, who in the {\i Mishnah} seems merely to be saying that you transgress by effectively lowering the amount, is reinterpreted by the {\i Gemara} to mean that the attempt does not even succeed \u8212- because the {\i ketubah} is {\i d\u8217'Oraisa}, and it is impossible to stipulate against a biblical financial obligation. And even though the amount therefore remains in full, the situation is still {\i biat zenut} because the woman believes otherwise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Positions: Stipula\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yosi: Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} now presents all three positions. Rabbi Meir says: {\i kol hapocheis l\u8217'besulah mi\u8217'masayim u\u8217'l\u8217'almanah mi\u8217'maneh harei zu biat zenut} \u8212- any attempt to lower the amount from the base is {\i biat zenut}. Rabbi Yosi says {\i rashai} \u8212- it is permitted. Rabbi Yosi goes one step further than Rabbi Yehuda: not only may the woman be {\i mochel} after starting with the base amount, but the parties may stipulate from the outset for a lesser amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda: Waiver Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda occupies the middle position. {\i Rotzeh koseiv l\u8217'vesulah shtar shel masayim v\u8217'hi koseves lo hikkabalti mimcha maneh} \u8212- the husband writes a {\i ketubah} for 200 and she writes a receipt acknowledging she has received 100. For an {\i almanah}, he writes 100 and she writes that she received 50. So Rabbi Yehuda requires the full base amount to be the starting point, but then permits the woman to effectively reduce it through a receipt \u8212- an indirect {\i mechilah} rather than a direct stipulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir: Stipulation Void\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's position remains as analyzed above: the stipulation fails entirely, and even the attempt to lower the amount creates {\i biat zenut}. The {\i Gemara} now asks how Rabbi Yosi \u8212- who showed himself so protective of the woman\u8217's interests, as we are about to see \u8212- could permit a lesser amount from the outset.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chattel Designation Disp\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chattel Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a {\i braisa}: {\i ein osin kesubas ishah mitaltlin mipnei tikkun ha-olam} \u8212- you do not designate {\i metaltalim}, movable property \u8212- chattel \u8212- as the lien on which the {\i ketubah} will be collected, for the sake of the benefit of society ({\i tikkun ha-olam}). This is not a reduction of the woman\u8217's liens on real estate \u8212- the standard formulation {\i kol nichasai achrayin}, which is a {\i tenai beit din}, remains \u8212- but rather a prohibition on affirmatively designating specific movable property as the designated source of collection. The {\i tikkun ha-olam} reason: chattel can be destroyed, lose value, or be lost, whereas real estate cannot be stolen away. If you designate only the Wedgwood China and it breaks, the woman is left with nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yosi: Liability Insuf\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi objects: {\i v\u8217'chi mah tikkun ha-olam yesh bazeh} \u8212- what societal benefit is served by this? {\i Vehalo ein kesuvos u\u8217'pochsin} \u8212- does not the value of such property diminish? At first glance this seems to simply restate what the {\i Tanna Kamma} already said. The {\i Gemara} observes: {\i Tanna Kamma nami ein osin ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the {\i Tanna Kamma} also said you are not allowed to make such a designation. So what precisely is their dispute?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unilateral vs.\u160 ?Mutual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this: the {\i braisa} must be reread as follows. {\i Bamah devarim amurim} \u8212- when is the designation of chattel as {\i ketubah} lien prohibited? {\i Beshelo kibel alav achrayus} \u8212- when the husband does not accept liability for its value, meaning the woman can only collect from the chattel itself. In such a case, if it breaks she is left with nothing. But {\i aval kibel alav achrayus} \u8212- if the husband backs it up by accepting personal liability for its worth even if the chattel is destroyed \u8212- then {\i osin}, you are allowed to designate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi then comes along and says: that is not sufficient protection. {\i Ki kibel alav achrayus amai osin} \u8212- even if you accept liability for breakage, why should that solve the problem? {\i Vehalo ein kesuvos u\u8217'pochsin} \u8212- the chattel may depreciate in value without breaking. Even if the China is intact when she comes to collect, it may be worth far less than her {\i ketubah} amount. So Rabbi Yosi holds that accepting liability for breakage does not fully protect the woman\u8217's interests when the value of the chattel itself may have declined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then uses this to ask the obvious question: if Rabbi Yosi is so concerned to protect the woman\u8217's interests that he objects even to a scenario where the husband accepts liability for breakage, how can he permit an outright stipulation for a lesser amount? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i hachi hashta} \u8212- what kind of comparison is that? In the chattel case, {\i hasam lo yade\u8217'ah detimchol} \u8212- the woman does not know she is conceding. She is demanding her full 200 and the husband is unilaterally setting up a scenario in which she might not be able to collect the full amount. That is one-sided and impermissible. But in our case, {\i yada\u8217'ah v\u8217'kamachla} \u8212- she knew about it and agreed to it. She was a party to the stipulation from the outset. Rabbi Yosi therefore holds that if she agrees, a lesser amount is permissible. His objection in the chattel case applies only to a unilateral arrangement that compromises her interests without her knowledge.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lost Ketubah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister of Rami: Lost Doc\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a practical case. The sister of Rami bar Chama was married to Rabbi Avya. {\i Irkas kesuvtasa} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} document was lost. Not a divorce situation \u8212- simply the physical document had been misplaced. This scenario, as a practical matter, arises not infrequently: a couple returns from a wedding and cannot locate the {\i ketubah}, or a couple married for twenty or thirty years moves and cannot find the document, or the signatures on a {\i ketubah} hanging on the wall have faded to illegibility. In such cases one must write a substitute {\i ketubah}, known as a {\i ketubah d\u8217'irkasa} \u8212- {\i irkas} being the Aramaic for \u8220"lost\u8221" \u8212- typically signed by the original witnesses, who testify that they originally signed a {\i ketubah}, that it was lost, and that this document reflects its contents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Citation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They came before Rav Yosef. He said to them: {\i hachi amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel \u8212- zu divrei Rebbi Meir} \u8212- the principle that it is {\i biat zenut} to live together without proper {\i ketubah} protection is the position of Rabbi Meir alone. {\i Aval Chachamim omrim} \u8212- but the sages say {\i masha adam im ishto shtayim v\u8217'shalosh shanim belo kesuvah} \u8212- one can live with his wife for two or three years without a written substitute {\i ketubah}. There is no great urgency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: who ever said Rabbi Meir would prohibit remaining together when the document is merely lost? The full amount is still owed, since we have already taught that even without a physical document the obligation exists. But the {\i Gemara} reasonably understands that just as the woman\u8217's subjective belief that she has only 100 when in fact she has 200 is sufficient to create {\i biat zenut}, so too the absence of the physical document is enough to create in her a subjective sense of vulnerability \u8212- she feels unprotected, she lacks proof that the debt exists, she cannot prevent him from claiming it was already paid. That subjective unease is, for Rabbi Meir, enough to constitute {\i biat zenut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages: No Rush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sages, however, hold that the mere absence of a document does not rise to that level. The underlying obligation remains intact and the woman is protected in substance even without the paper, so there is no prohibition against continuing to live together. There is no great rush.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Rule Like R. Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then interjects: {\i v\u8217'ha\u8217'amar Rav Nachman amar Shmuel halacha k\u8217'Rebbi Meir bigzeirotav} \u8212- but we have a ruling that we follow Rabbi Meir in his {\i gezeirot}, his edicts. It is noteworthy that the {\i Gemara} describes this as a {\i gezeirah}. Normally {\i gezeirot} are safeguards against transgression of some other prohibition. Here the {\i Gemara} uses the term to describe Rabbi Meir\u8217's protective stance toward the institution of marriage \u8212- a {\i gezeirah} in the sense of safeguarding the integrity and proper protections of the marital relationship. Since we rule like Rabbi Meir in his {\i gezeirot}, Abaye concludes: {\i zil katuv la} \u8212- go write her a substitute {\i ketubah}. So it sounds as though we rule like Rabbi Meir in this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the practical {\i halacha}? In principle, the answer follows Rabbi Meir \u8212- one should write a substitute {\i ketubah} as quickly as possible. However, the Rema notes that in the present day, given {\i takanas Rabbeinu Gershom} \u8212- the enactment that a husband cannot divorce his wife against her will \u8212- the underlying concern that generates {\i biat zenut} in Rabbi Meir\u8217's framework is substantially mitigated. The {\i biat zenut} concern arises because the woman feels that she is not truly protected: she could be casually divorced at any moment, and without the document she cannot prevent that or prove her claim. But now that she cannot be divorced without her consent, she knows she is protected even without the physical {\i ketubah}. The Rema therefore holds it is not {\i biat zenut} in such a case today. Nevertheless, one should take care of writing the replacement {\i ketubah} as promptly as possible; we do not tell the couple to cease living together until it is produced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises regarding {\i Sefardim}, who in various ways have adopted the spirit of {\i takanas Rabbeinu Gershom}, though the precise application is a matter that requires further analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question: if one is writing a substitute {\i ketubah} with the same original witnesses, why not simply write the identical document? The answer is that writing an identical document on the same date creates a concern that the woman may possess two equivalent documents and attempt to collect twice. The {\i ketubah d\u8217'irkasa} therefore uses a different formulation that explicitly records that the original was lost \u8212- thereby preventing double collection, since once the husband has paid and the document is surrendered, no second claim can be made.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Techilla vs.\u160 ?Sof Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Dimi: Debate Techilla\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to a question about the scope of the three-way debate \u8212- in particular the debate between Rabbi Yosi, who allows a lesser amount, and Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Meir, who do not. When exactly does the debate apply?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Dimi came from Eretz Yisrael, he reported in the name of Rav Shimon ben Pazi, in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, in the name of Bar Kappara: {\i machlokes b\u8217'techilla} \u8212- the debate applies only at the outset of the wedding. {\i Aval besof divrei hakol eina mocheles} \u8212- but at the end, everyone agrees that the amount cannot be changed. The question of what \u8220"the beginning\u8221" and \u8220"the end\u8221" refer to will require clarification, but the initial formulation is: there is a {\i machlokes} before a certain point, and universal agreement after another point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan: Both Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i bein b\u8217'zo u\u8217'vein b\u8217'zo machlokes} \u8212- they debate in both cases, whether at the beginning or at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Abbahu: Harmonization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu then clarifies: it was explained to him directly from Rabbi Yochanan that Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi are not actually disagreeing. When Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi says the {\i machlokes} is {\i b\u8217'techilla} \u8212- at the beginning \u8212- he means {\i techillas chuppah}, before the {\i chuppah}. And when he says that everyone agrees at the end, he means {\i sof biah}, after they have actually had relations. And when Rabbi Yochanan says the {\i machlokes} applies to both cases \u8212- beginning and end \u8212- he means {\i techillas chuppah} (before the {\i chuppah}) and {\i sof chuppah} (at the {\i chuppah} but before {\i biah}). So both authorities agree that the {\i machlokes} spans everything up through the {\i chuppah}, and that once {\i biah} has occurred, everyone agrees the amount cannot be changed. On this reading, Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi and Rabbi Yochanan are saying the same thing, simply using different terminology for adjacent points on the timeline.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravin: Debate Sof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Ravin came from Eretz Yisrael, he had the opposite version of the same tradition \u8212- transmitted in the same chain, Rav Shimon ben Pazi, Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, Bar Kappara \u8212- but with inverted positions. According to Ravin\u8217's version, Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said {\i machlokes l\u8217'vsof} \u8212- the debate is only at the end. {\i Aval b\u8217'techilla divrei hakol mocheles} \u8212- but at the outset, everyone agrees the amount can be changed. Rabbi Yochanan in this version still says {\i bein b\u8217'zo u\u8217'vein b\u8217'zo machlokes} \u8212- they debate in both cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu again harmonizes the two: when Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi says the {\i machlokes} is {\i l\u8217'vsof} he means {\i sof chuppah} \u8212- after the {\i chuppah} but before {\i biah}. And when he says everyone agrees at the beginning, he means {\i techillas chuppah}, before the {\i chuppah}. And when Rabbi Yochanan says they debate both, he means {\i techillas biah} \u8212- the beginning of {\i biah}, i.e., after the {\i chuppah} \u8212- and {\i sof biah}, after relations. So this version has everyone agreeing that before the {\i chuppah} the amount can be changed, and everyone agreeing that after {\i biah} it cannot \u8212- and the entire {\i machlokes} falling in the middle zone between {\i chuppah} and {\i biah}. This is the mirror image of the green version: there, the {\i machlokes} covered everything before {\i biah}; here, the {\i machlokes} is confined to the interval between {\i chuppah} and {\i biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa: Sevara Best\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa concludes the sugya with a methodological observation. Had Rabbi Abbahu not told us that Rabbi Yochanan himself said that he and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi were not really disagreeing, Rav Pappa says he would have preferred to read it differently: he would have said that Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi genuinely disagree \u8212- each one staking out a different position about where the {\i machlokes} of the {\i Tannaim} logically belongs \u8212- while the two transmissions of Rav Dimi and Ravin do not actually conflict. In that alternative reading, {\i sof} in Ravin\u8217's version equals {\i sof chuppah} and {\i techilla} in Rav Dimi\u8217's version equals {\i techillas biah}, making the two transmissions equivalent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: what difference does it make which debate we collapse? Rav Pappa\u8217's preference rests on a principle: {\i d\u8217'pligi trei amorai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'nafshaihu v\u8217'lo pligi trei amorai aliba d\u8217'chad amora} \u8212- it is preferable to say that two {\i Amoraim} are arguing based on their own reasoning and analysis rather than to say that two {\i Amoraim} are disputing what a third {\i Amora} actually said. When Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi debate where the {\i machlokes} logically occurs, they are disagreeing about {\i sevara} \u8212- about what makes sense, about in which domain the debate is most coherent. That is the natural stuff of the {\i Shas}. But if we instead say Rav Dimi and Ravin are reporting different versions of what Rabbi Yochanan said, we are implying that one of them got it wrong \u8212- that there is an error in transmission. It is always better, if we must reduce one dispute, to do so by harmonizing transmissions than by eliminating a genuine debate in {\i sevara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Twelve-Month Waiting Per\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: 12 vs.\u160 ?30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i Mishnah} opens: {\i notnin l\u8217'betulah shneim asar chodesh mishetav\u8217'ah ha-ba\u8217'al l\u8217'farnos et atzmah} \u8212- we give a {\i betulah} twelve months from the time that the husband demands to begin living together to prepare herself for marriage. This is not twelve months from the time of {\i kiddushin}, but from the time the husband says he is ready to proceed to {\i nissu\u8217'in}. She is entitled to use that time to prepare her trousseau. Similarly, {\i u\u8217'keshem shenotnin l\u8217'ishah kach notnin l\u8217'ish} \u8212- just as she is given time to prepare, so too he is given time to prepare the home if he needs it. And {\i v\u8217'almanah shloshim yom} \u8212- for a second marriage, only thirty days are required, since a widow is more independent and the wedding would not involve the same elaborate preparation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Higi\u8217'a zman v\u8217'lo nisu} \u8212- once the period expires and they are not yet married, {\i ochelet mishelo} \u8212- he must now begin supporting her financially. Once {\i nissu\u8217'in} should have occurred and it has not, his obligation of {\i mezonot} begins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Support Begins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The onset of this financial obligation is tied directly to the expiration of the waiting period. From that point forward, she eats from his account, regardless of who bears responsibility for the delay \u8212- a question addressed at the beginning of the {\i masechet} but not the focus here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Eligibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ochelet b\u8217'terumah} \u8212- if he is a {\i kohen}, he may now feed her {\i terumah}, since the wife of a {\i kohen} is entitled to eat {\i terumah} even before {\i nissu\u8217'in}, even at the stage of {\i erusin}. This ruling conveys two things: first, from the laws of {\i terumah}, she is permitted to eat it even as a mere {\i arusah} to a {\i kohen}; and second, in satisfying his obligation of {\i mezonot}, he may discharge it by providing {\i terumah}, which is cheaper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon says he may give her the entire amount in {\i terumah}. Rabbi Akiva disagrees: {\i mechtzah chullin u\u8217'mechtzah terumah} \u8212- half must be ordinary {\i chullin}. The reason is that roughly half the month \u8212- and more, given {\i chumras Rabbi Zeira} \u8212- she is {\i tme\u8217'ah} as a {\i niddah} and therefore cannot eat {\i terumah}. It is therefore unfair to give her only {\i terumah} that she will have to sell at a loss to obtain {\i chullin} she can actually consume when she is {\i tme\u8217'ah}. He must provide at least half as {\i chullin}, food she can eat at all times.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha-yavam eino ma\u8217'achil b\u8217'terumah} \u8212- the case of the {\i yavam} is different. If a {\i kohen} was married and died, and now his brother the {\i kohen} stands as {\i yavam} and she is {\i shomeres yavam}, she cannot eat {\i terumah} even after twelve months of waiting. There is no twelve-month clock after which she gains {\i terumah} eligibility; as long as she is awaiting {\i yibbum}, she does not eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Split Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then covers cases where the twelve-month period is split between time in front of the husband and time in front of the {\i yavam}. If she spent six months in front of the husband (after he said it was time to proceed) and six months awaiting the {\i yavam}, she does not eat {\i terumah}. Even if eleven months and twenty-nine days were in front of the husband and one day awaiting the {\i yavam}, or vice versa \u8212- in all such mixed cases, {\i einah ochelet b\u8217'terumah}. Rashi notes that the last scenario \u8212- almost all the time in front of the {\i yavam} \u8212- is in a sense obvious, since the {\i Mishnah} already indicated that even years of waiting for {\i yibbum} do not generate {\i terumah} eligibility. Tomorrow\u8217's daf will examine the {\i Tosafos} position on this.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 No Terumah Until Chuppah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Zu mishnah rishonah} \u8212- that was the first, original teaching. The term {\i mishnah rishonah} is significant: it refers to earlier collections of {\i mishnayot} that, even when subsequently revised, were not discarded. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's phrase {\i mishnah rishonah lo zazah mimkomah} \u8212- the original {\i Mishnah} has not moved from its place \u8212- reflects the principle that earlier texts are preserved, not silently replaced by later rulings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit din shel achareihem amru} \u8212- the later {\i beit din} then ruled: {\i ein ha\u8217'ishah ochelet b\u8217'terumah ad shetikanes l\u8217'chuppah} \u8212- a woman does not eat {\i terumah} until she has entered the {\i chuppah}, meaning until {\i nissu\u8217'in} has actually taken place. This is actually to the woman\u8217's detriment in terms of the husband\u8217's food bill \u8212- since he must now provide all {\i chullin} during the waiting period rather than satisfying his obligation with cheaper {\i terumah} \u8212- and it prevents the woman from giving {\i terumah} to her family. The source for the twelve-month right is derived from the verse in which the family of Rivkah says {\i teshev hana\u8217'arah itanu yamim o asor} \u8212- let the girl remain with us for \u8220"days\u8221" or ten. {\i Yamim} unqualified, as shown through a {\i gezeirah shavah} from {\i yamim tihyeh ge\u8217'ulato} (the year of redemption for a house in a walled city), means a full year. And \u8220"ten\u8221" \u8212- in context, ten months rather than ten days, paralleling the year reduced by two months. The {\i Gemara} thus derives that there is an established right to request a full year.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Postponement Rights: Ket\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah: Can Postpone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira cites a {\i baraisa}: {\i ketannah bein hi uvein aviha yecholim l\u8217'akeiv} \u8212- while the girl is still a {\i ketannah} \u8212- a minor \u8212- either she or her father can prevent the {\i nissu\u8217'in} from proceeding. This introduces a new principle: the twelve-month framework applies once the woman is past minority; as long as she is a {\i ketannah}, she and her father can in principle delay indefinitely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Right Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i bishlama hi matzi me\u8217'akeva} \u8212- it is understandable that she can postpone, since she is the one being married. {\i Ella aviha} \u8212- but the father: {\i ei hi nicha lah} \u8212- if she herself wants to proceed to {\i nissu\u8217'in} after {\i kiddushin} has already occurred, {\i mai nafka lei minnah} \u8212- what is the father\u8217's legitimate claim to stand in the way?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Concern Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i sevar} \u8212- the father reasons as follows. {\i Hashta lo yade\u8217'ah} \u8212- she is young, perhaps seven years old, and does not know what she is agreeing to. {\i Lemachar mimreda} \u8212- tomorrow, once married, she will be too young and will rebel against the marriage, reject her husband. {\i V\u8217'nafkah} \u8212- she will leave or be divorced. {\i V\u8217'asya v\u8217'nafala alavei} \u8212- and she will fall back on him, become his responsibility once again. This is a striking {\i Gemara}: the more obvious reason for the father\u8217's right to delay is that as long as she remains at home before {\i nissu\u8217'in}, he retains certain entitlements to her labor and earnings. The {\i Gemara}, however, deliberately grounds the father\u8217's right in a concern internal to the marriage itself \u8212- that he legitimately believes the {\i nissu\u8217'in} is premature and will ultimately fail, and that he will bear the consequences. The other reason \u8212- his financial interest in her remaining home \u8212- is not given standing here as a sufficient justification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Abba: No Nissuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba bar Levi states explicitly: {\i ein poskin al ha-ketannah lehasi\u8217'ah keshehi ketannah} \u8212- one does not make an arrangement to perform {\i nissu\u8217'in} with a {\i ketannah} while she is still a {\i ketannah}. This goes one step beyond the prior ruling. First, the {\i Gemara} established that she and her father have the right to prevent it \u8212- which already signals disapproval. Now it states affirmatively that one should not do it. {\i Aval poskin al ha-ketannah lehasi\u8217'ah keshehi gedolah} \u8212- one may, however, arrange while she is a {\i ketannah} that the {\i nissu\u8217'in} will take place once she reaches adulthood, once she is a {\i na\u8217'arah} after {\i Bat Mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is worth pausing over. People are rightly troubled when confronted with {\i kiddushei ketannah} \u8212- the betrothal of a minor. It is worth noting that {\i kiddushin} alone does not involve cohabitation; the sexual dimension of marriage begins only with {\i nissu\u8217'in}. And the {\i Gemara} here is clearly discouraging {\i nissu\u8217'in} during minority. Once she reaches {\i Bat Mitzvah}, the situation is different \u8212- and while that too may strike modern sensibilities as troubling, the {\i Gemara} is at least insisting that {\i nissu\u8217'in} not precede that threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psychological Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: is it not obvious that one may arrange while she is still a {\i ketannah} for {\i nissu\u8217'in} to take place after she grows up? What is the {\i chiddush}? The {\i Gemara} answers: one might have thought that even this should be prohibited \u8212- {\i dilma milai pachda mehashta v\u8217'chalsha} \u8212- that by telling an eleven-year-old that she will be married after her {\i Bat Mitzvah}, one fills her with fear now, and she grows despondent or psychologically burdened. The {\i Gemara} therefore had to teach that we are not concerned about this \u8212- {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- and such advance planning is permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bogeret Timing: 12 Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna: 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules: {\i bogra yom echad veniskadsha notnin lah shloshim yom k\u8217'almanah} \u8212- if a woman hits {\i bagrus} \u8212- majority at twelve and a half \u8212- and then has {\i kiddushin}, she is given only thirty days, not twelve months, in which to prepare for {\i nissu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraisa: 12 Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this from a {\i baraisa}: {\i bogeret harei hi k\u8217'tav\u8217'uha} \u8212- when a woman hits {\i bagrus}, it is as if one of the parties has made a {\i tvi\u8217'ah}, a demand to proceed to {\i nissu\u8217'in}. One might read this as: it is like the {\i tvi\u8217'ah} of a {\i betulah} \u8212- twelve months. {\i Mai lav k\u8217'tav\u8217'uha d\u8217'betulah} \u8212- does that not mean twelve months, as when a {\i betulah} is {\i tavu\u8217'ah}? The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i la, k\u8217'tav\u8217'uha d\u8217'almanah} \u8212- no, it is like the {\i tvi\u8217'ah} made of a widow, thirty days. But a further {\i baraisa} is cited: {\i bogeret sheshahata shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- a woman who has been a {\i bogeret} for twelve months. Rabbi Eliezer says that since the husband is now obligated in {\i mezonot}, he may annul her vows. Setting aside the question of vow annulment, the implication of this {\i baraisa} is clear: twelve months after {\i bagrus} he is obligated in {\i mezonot} \u8212- meaning the clock runs for twelve months, not thirty days. This directly contradicts Rav Huna.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to adjust the text: {\i eima bogeret v\u8217'sheshahata shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- read it as a case of a {\i bogeret} combined with a separate twelve-month period, not twelve months of {\i bagrus}. But this is acknowledged as far-fetched, {\i v\u8217'lo pshat}, and the {\i Gemara} moves on. A further {\i baraisa} settles the matter explicitly: {\i hama\u8217'ares et ha-betulah} \u8212- one who betroths a {\i betulah} \u8212- whether the husband demanded and she postponed, giving her twelve months, or whether she demanded and he postponed, giving him twelve months, {\i notnin lah shneim asar chodesh misha\u8217'as tvi\u8217'ah} \u8212- twelve months from the time of the demand. {\i Aval lo misha\u8217'as erusin} \u8212- not from the time of {\i erusin} itself, but from the moment one side says it is time to proceed. {\i U\u8217'vogeret harei hi k\u8217'tav\u8217'uha} \u8212- and a {\i bogeret} is treated as if there has been a {\i tvi\u8217'ah}. {\i Bogra yom echad veniskadsha notnin lah shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- she hit {\i bagrus} one day and then had {\i kiddushin}: she is given twelve months. This explicitly refutes Rav Huna. {\i Tyuvta d\u8217'Rav Huna tyuvta} \u8212- a full contradiction of Rav Huna\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa then resolves the apparent tension: {\i bogeres she\u8217'avru aleha shneim asar chodesh b\u8217'vagrus v\u8217'niskadshah} \u8212- if a woman is already a year into {\i bagrus} before {\i kiddushin} \u8212- she is the ripe old age of thirteen and a half \u8212- and then has {\i kiddushin}, she is given only thirty days. At that point the clock has long been running and one cannot give her a full additional year. The scenario in which a full twelve months applies is when she hits {\i bagrus} and then promptly has {\i kiddushin}; but if {\i bagrus} predates {\i kiddushin} by a year, the thirty-day rule applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} observes a broader structural point about the timeline. If a {\i ketannah} can postpone indefinitely, and a {\i bogeret} triggers the clock at {\i bagrus} itself, then the twelve-month scenario of the {\i Mishnah} applies specifically to the period of {\i na\u8217'arus} \u8212- between twelve and twelve and a half. The {\i tvi\u8217'ah} and the twelve-month clock begin in that window. But this raises a complication: what if the twelve-month period begins while she is a {\i na\u8217'arah} and she becomes a {\i bogeret} six months into that period? {\i Tosafos} works through the stages \u8212- {\i ketannah}, {\i na\u8217'arah}, {\i bogeret} \u8212- and their interaction with the {\i tvi\u8217'ah} and waiting periods, a matter that requires careful analysis of when precisely she transitions between those stages relative to the demand to proceed with {\i nissu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 58\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 12-Month Wait & Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin Delay Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by picking up from the final sugya of 57b, which introduced the twelve-month postponement available to either party when the time arrives to move from {\i kiddushin} to {\i nisuin}. As we learned, a {\i katana} \u8212- a minor girl \u8212- along with her father could defer the {\i nisuin} throughout the period of her minority; the actual cohabitation was not meant to begin until she was at least a {\i naara}, twelve and a half years old. By contrast, once a woman becomes a {\i bogeres}, the clock begins running automatically, without any formal demand. The debate about who caused the delay \u8212- whether it was the husband\u8217's fault, the wife\u8217's fault, or no one\u8217's \u8212- was addressed on the opening daf of the tractate and is not the concern here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the {\i gemara} now addresses is this: once the twelve-month period has arrived and the parties have not yet proceeded to {\i nisuin}, the husband must begin paying the wife\u8217's food bills and may provide her with {\i terumah} as part of that support \u8212- if the husband is a {\i kohen}. The immediate question is on what basis she is permitted to eat {\i terumah} at all, given that she is still only an {\i arusah} and not yet a {\i nesu\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla: Kinyan Kaspo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla teaches: {\i devar Torah arusah bas Yisrael ocheles biterumah} \u8212- biblically, a betrothed daughter of a {\i Yisrael} household is permitted to eat {\i terumah} from the moment of {\i erusin}. The source is the verse, {\i v\u8217'kohen ki yikneh nefesh kinyan kaspo} \u8212- \u8220"and a {\i kohen} who acquires a soul, the acquisition of his money\u8221" \u8212- which in its simple reading addresses a slave purchased by a {\i kohen}, who thereby becomes entitled to eat {\i terumah}. The {\i gemara} extends this: {\i v\u8217'hai nami kinyan kaspo hu} \u8212- a wife is likewise the acquisition of his money, since {\i kiddushin} is effected through a monetary transfer. This is the foundational teaching of the first {\i mishnah} in {\i Kiddushin}: a woman is {\i nikneis} through {\i kesef}. Accordingly, from the moment of {\i kiddushin}, she falls within the category of {\i kinyan kaspo} and is in principle entitled to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} takes a different approach. Rather than deriving the wife\u8217's right from {\i kinyan kaspo}, it derives it from {\i beiso} \u8212- \u8220"his household.\u8221" The verse speaks of the household of the {\i kohen} eating {\i terumah}, and {\i beiso zo ishto}, \u8220"his household \u8212- that is his wife.\u8221" Since {\i bayis} ordinarily implies the context of {\i nisuin}, the {\i Yerushalmi} appears to hold that biblically one must wait until {\i nisuin}, or at least until a stage sufficiently close to it. According to this view, the arrival of the twelve-month mark \u8212- at which point there is a binding obligation to proceed with {\i nisuin} and the husband is already paying the food bills \u8212- constitutes a state close enough to {\i nisuin} to qualify as {\i beiso}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Bavli}, by contrast, holds that even from the moment of {\i erusin} the wife is in principle permitted to eat {\i terumah} by virtue of {\i kinyan kaspo}. This reflects a genuine dispute between the two Talmuds: the {\i Bavli} requires no proximity to {\i nisuin} for the biblical entitlement to attach; the {\i Yerushalmi} does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concern: Sharing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the woman is biblically entitled to eat {\i terumah} from the time of {\i kiddushin}, why did the rabbis forbid it during that period? The {\i gemara} explains: {\i shema yimze\u8217'gu lah kos b\u8217'veit aviha v\u8217'tashkehu l\u8217'achia ul\u8217'achota} \u8212- lest they pour her a cup of {\i terumah} wine in her father\u8217's house and she go and give her brother and sister to drink. While she remains in her father\u8217's household, the practical danger of inadvertently sharing {\i terumah} with non-{\i kohanim} is too great, and the rabbis therefore prohibited it. Once the twelve months arrive, however, the prohibition is lifted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges: even when the twelve-month mark arrives and the wedding has been postponed, she is still not yet living with her husband \u8212- so why should we no longer be concerned that she will share with family members?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's House Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hatam duchtam m\u8217'yached lah} \u8212- once the twelve-month deadline arrives, the husband will designate a separate place for her to live apart from her father\u8217's household. Even if the wedding itself has been delayed for some reason, he will set her up in a distinct residence. It is a striking assumption on the {\i gemara}\u8217's part that this was so standard a social reality that it could be relied upon halachically \u8212- particularly when one considers that maintaining a third residence requires financial resources the groom may not always have.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises an analogous challenge: if a {\i kohen} is a hired laborer working for a non-{\i kohen}, should he not be permitted to bring his {\i terumah} food to work, lest he share it with his employer? The {\i gemara} dismisses the comparison: {\i hashta mididei safu leh} \u8212- there, the employer is the one providing the worker\u8217's food anyway; there is no realistic concern that the employer will reach for the {\i kohen}\u8217's sandwich. The domestic situation of a woman still in her father\u8217's household, surrounded by family, is categorically different.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simpon & Defect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defect Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yehuda offers a second, independent reason why the rabbis forbade an {\i arusah} from eating {\i terumah} even though she is biblically entitled: {\i mishum simpon} \u8212- because of the concern over a defect. The term {\i simpon}, apparently a Greek loanword, describes a material flaw in an acquisition that would render the entire transaction a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- a sale based on a mistake. Transposing this concept from commercial law to marriage: once the couple begins to live together and becomes physically intimate, the husband may discover something about his wife that he did not know before, something so fundamental that it constitutes a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} and retroactively nullifies the {\i kiddushin} altogether. Were she eating {\i terumah} in the interim \u8212- from the time of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- she would have been doing so without legal basis. A contemporary example that {\i poskim} grapple with: if it emerges that the husband was gay and had concealed this, the entire {\i kiddushin} may be void from the outset as {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'ut}, in which case no {\i get} would be required. These situations have arisen in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately presses: if the concern is {\i simpon}, how does anything change after twelve months? And more pointedly, how can we permit {\i terumah} after {\i chupah} \u8212- the actual wedding \u8212- if the couple has not yet been intimate? Perhaps we should wait until after {\i biah} before permitting {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i hatam mivdak badik lah v\u8217'hadar minasav} \u8212- before proceeding to the wedding itself, with its attendant expense and social commitment, the parties will have spent time together getting to know one another. Physical intimacy may not yet have occurred, but by the time of the {\i chupah}, there has been sufficient personal acquaintance that we are no longer meaningfully concerned about {\i bitul kiddushin}. The {\i gemara} concedes that this does not eliminate all risk \u8212- theoretically, something could still come to light after {\i biah} \u8212- but the remaining concern is diminished enough that providing for the woman outweighs it. The earlier stage, when the parties had barely met, was a categorically different situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Purchase Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then poses a parallel from the world of acquisition: {\i eved kohen she\u8217'lakacho mi\u8217'Yisrael lo yochal b\u8217'trumah mishum simpon} \u8212- should not a slave purchased by a {\i kohen} from a non-{\i kohen} be barred from eating {\i terumah} on account of {\i simpon}, lest the purchase turn out to be a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} and the slave will have eaten {\i terumah} retroactively without authorization?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i simpon b\u8217'avadim leika} \u8212- the concern of {\i simpon} does not apply to slaves. Why not? Two principles dispose of the cases: if the alleged defect is external and visible \u8212- such as a limp or a broken limb \u8212- then {\i de\u8217'ee d\u8217'eilai ha kachazi leh}, the buyer could see it, and if he did not look carefully, that is his own failing; he cannot claim {\i ta\u8217'ut}. And if the defect is internal or not readily apparent \u8212- things that would only be visible when the slave is undressed, for instance \u8212- {\i l\u8217'mlacha kaba\u8217'ei}, the slave is acquired for labor; what does the buyer care about private physical characteristics that are irrelevant to the work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Visible vs Hidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: does it truly not matter what is hidden? Some hidden facts are certainly significant. For instance, if the slave turns out to be a thief, or \u8212- and here the {\i rishonim} disagree on the translation \u8212- a {\i kuvyustus}: Rashi understands this as a kidnapper; Tosafot argue that it means a dice player, a gambler. ArtScroll follows Rashi\u8217's reading. Are these not hidden defects that one could not have known about?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} replies: {\i hachi hu} \u8212- these are on you. In the social reality in which the {\i gemara} operates, such attributes were considered something a buyer of a slave ought to anticipate. More precisely, as Rashi frames it, the buyer should have expected this; and Tosafot frames it even more squarely as a matter of buyer responsibility: these were things of public knowledge, and entering into a purchase without investigating them is the buyer\u8217's own problem. He cannot claim {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} for failing to do due diligence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Known Defects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then would constitute a genuine hidden defect that could undo a slave purchase? The {\i gemara} offers two examples: {\i listim mezuyan} \u8212- an armed bandit, not merely a petty thief or gambler, but a violent criminal \u8212- and {\i muchtav lamalchut} \u8212- one who was already registered for execution or designated as a felon by the government. These, however, also do not support a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} claim: {\i hanehu kala it lehu} \u8212- these are matters of public record. If the slave was wanted by the government or known to be a dangerous criminal, this was knowable information. Rashi\u8217's position is that the buyer effectively knew and accepted; Tosafot\u8217's position is that even if he did not personally know, the information was publicly available and it was his responsibility to investigate. Either way, one cannot claim to have been duped when the defect was a matter of common knowledge. The only genuine basis for {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} in a slave purchase would be something that was truly unknowable and not of public record at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then draws together the two concerns \u8212- sharing with family, and {\i simpon} \u8212- and asks {\i mai beinaihu}, what practical case would depend on which rationale is operative? One such case: {\i kabal u\u8217'masar v\u8217'halach} \u8212- if the husband said explicitly that he accepts whatever turns out to be true about his wife, whatever condition she is in, he waives any {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} claim. According to the {\i simpon} concern, once he has accepted the risk, she could eat {\i terumah} immediately from {\i kiddushin}. According to the concern about sharing with family, it would not help at all \u8212- she is still living in her father\u8217's house and the risk of sharing remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second case: {\i masar v\u8217'halach} \u8212- the situation discussed earlier where the father transfers the bride to the groom\u8217's emissaries and she is in transit between the two households, no longer in her father\u8217's home but not yet in the groom\u8217's. According to the sharing concern, she can now eat {\i terumah} \u8212- she is out of her father\u8217's house. According to the {\i simpon} concern, she still cannot \u8212- the intimacy that would reveal any defect has not yet occurred.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah Proportions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Tarfon: All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a different dimension of the same question: not whether the wife may eat {\i terumah}, but how much of her food allocation the husband may provide in the form of {\i terumah}. {\i Terumah} requires more careful handling than {\i chulin} \u8212- most critically, a woman in a state of {\i niddah} may not eat it, and {\i terumah} typically fetches less on the market than ordinary food. Rabbi Tarfon holds that the husband may give her {\i kol terumah} \u8212- her entire food allocation in the form of {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One should note that at the time of the {\i mishnah}, a woman was {\i niddah} for approximately one week out of the month \u8212- a strict seven-day period from the onset of bleeding. The later practice of counting seven clean days, which became universal and extended the period of {\i niddah} to roughly one third of the month, came about only in the {\i gemara}\u8217's era. At the time of the {\i mishnah}, then, a woman was a {\i niddah} for only about a quarter of the month \u8212- which makes the option of providing all {\i terumah} somewhat less extreme than it would later appear, though still a meaningful challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye clarifies the scope of the dispute: {\i machlokes be-vas kohen le-kohen} \u8212- the disagreement between Rabbi Tarfon and Rabbi Akiva applies only when the wife comes from a {\i kohen} household. If she herself is a {\i bas kohen}, she has grown up handling {\i terumah}, knows its laws, understands when she must refrain from eating it and how to arrange for its conversion to {\i chulin} when necessary. Only in that case does Rabbi Tarfon say the husband may give her all {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval be-vas Yisrael le-kohen} \u8212- if the woman is a daughter of a {\i Yisrael} household marrying a {\i kohen}, then {\i divrei hakol mechtza chulin mechtza terumah} \u8212- everyone agrees it must be half {\i chulin} and half {\i terumah}. A woman with no prior experience of {\i terumah} cannot reasonably be expected to manage the full complexity of an all-{\i terumah} food supply \u8212- knowing when to refrain, arranging for its sale when she is {\i niddah}, ensuring that {\i terumah} temeiah is not consumed. Providing her with only half {\i terumah} is both more manageable and removes the {\i michshol} of giving her {\i terumah} at times when she must be eating {\i chulin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye adds a second dimension: {\i machlokes be-arusa} \u8212- this entire dispute applies only to an {\i arusah} whose twelve-month deadline has arrived and whose husband is paying her food bills before the {\i nisuin} itself. {\i Aval be-nesu\u8217'ah} \u8212- if the {\i nisuin} has already taken place and she is living as a married woman \u8212- then again {\i divrei hakol mechtza chulin mechtza terumah}, everyone agrees it must be half and half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for this distinction, as Rashi explains, is tied to logistics: while she is still an {\i arusah} living in her father\u8217's house, the father is available to help her arrange the conversion of {\i terumah} to {\i chulin} when necessary. A {\i bas kohen} in her father\u8217's house has that practical infrastructure. But once she is a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} and is living separately from her husband \u8212- for instance, if he has gone on a business trip and is sending her food \u8212- she no longer has that support structure, and therefore even a {\i bas kohen} receives only half and half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises: during the weeks when she is {\i niddah} and cannot eat {\i terumah}, why should the husband be permitted to provide {\i terumah} at all? He has an obligation to feed her; during that period, the {\i terumah} is inedible. The answer implicit in these opinions is that she converts the {\i terumah} she cannot eat into {\i chulin} \u8212- selling it and purchasing ordinary food. The question of whether she can efficiently do so, and at what cost to her, drives the later opinions in the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Echo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited \u8212- {\i tanya nami hachi} \u8212- and what follows is, word for word, what Abaye himself said. This raises a genuine methodological question: why was Abaye\u8217's statement needed? Why not simply quote the {\i baraita}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is part of a recognized phenomenon that scholars have noted: there exist {\i baraitot} in the {\i Bavli} that have no parallel in the {\i Yerushalmi} or anywhere else in {\i Shas}, and these {\i baraitot} are often word-for-word identical to the statements of an {\i amora}. The possibility worth considering is that either an {\i amora}\u8217's statement was eventually preserved and transmitted as a {\i baraita}, or alternatively that the {\i amora} was quoting a {\i baraita} and the attribution shifted over time. Whatever the explanation, the phenomenon of an {\i amora}\u8217's statement being immediately confirmed by a {\i baraita} in identical language \u8212- where that {\i baraita} has no independent attestation \u8212- is a recurring and noteworthy feature of the {\i Bavli}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira offers a different ratio: {\i shtei yadot shel terumah v\u8217'achat shel chulin} \u8212- two-thirds {\i terumah} and one-third {\i chulin}. This more closely tracks the actual calendar of her {\i niddah} status. At the time of the {\i mishnah}, a woman was {\i niddah} for one week out of the month \u8212- approximately one quarter. Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira appears to reason that one third {\i chulin} provides an adequate buffer without giving her more {\i chulin} than strictly necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda takes yet another position: {\i notnin lah hakol terumah v\u8217'hi mocheret v\u8217'lokachat bi-dmei chulin} \u8212- one gives her all {\i terumah}, but she goes and sells it and uses the proceeds to purchase {\i chulin}. This sounds similar to Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's view, but Rashi identifies a crucial difference. {\i Terumah} does not sell at par; if {\i chulin} costs ten units and {\i terumah} sells for eight, then giving her the same nominal quantity of {\i terumah} as {\i chulin} leaves her unable to purchase the full {\i chulin} equivalent she needs. Rabbi Yehuda therefore holds that the husband must give her sufficient {\i terumah} \u8212- an augmented quantity \u8212- so that when she converts it to {\i chulin}, she receives the full equivalent of what she is entitled to eat. He must compensate her for the discount at which {\i terumah} trades.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R.S. ben Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel extends this logic further: {\i kol makom she\u8217'huzkera trumah noten lah kiflayim bachulin} \u8212- wherever {\i terumah} is mentioned as the currency of her food support, he gives her double the quantity in {\i terumah} compared to what he would give in {\i chulin}. The difference between Rabbi Yehuda and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, the {\i gemara} explains, is {\i tircha} \u8212- the inconvenience and effort involved in converting {\i terumah} to {\i chulin}. Even if the monetary value could be recovered by patient dealing, the practical burden of arranging the sale, waiting for a buyer, and navigating the market must be compensated. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel holds that the husband must give her enough of a surplus that she can convert the {\i terumah} quickly and without effort, rather than holding out for full market value.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yavam & Zika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam No Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i hayavam eino ma\u8217'achil b\u8217'trumah} \u8212- if the husband (a {\i kohen}) dies and the wife falls before the {\i yavam} for {\i yibum}, the {\i yavam} cannot feed her {\i terumah} during the period of {\i zika}. This applies whether the deceased husband was at the stage of {\i erusin} or {\i nisuin} \u8212- either way, the surviving brother has a {\i mitzvah} to perform {\i yibum}, and the {\i zika}, the legal bond that ties her to him, functions as a kind of quasi-{\i kiddushin}. One might have thought that since {\i zika} resembles {\i kiddushin} and since the {\i yavam} is in fact obligated to marry her, she should be permitted to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- perhaps even without waiting a further twelve months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rejects this. The {\i gemara} explains: {\i kinyan kaspo amar rachmana} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's basis for allowing a wife to eat {\i terumah} is {\i kinyan kaspo}, the acquisition through his money. The {\i kiddushin} of the original husband was an act of {\i kinyan kaspo}. The {\i zika} of the {\i yavam}, by contrast, is {\i kinyan d\u8217'achiv hu} \u8212- it is the {\i kinyan} of his brother, not his own. It does not carry the full legal force of his own {\i kiddushin} and therefore does not entitle her to eat {\i terumah} before the act of {\i yibum} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 6 Mo. Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further ruling: if the wife spent six months with the husband before his death and then six months with the {\i yavam} \u8212- or even eleven months and twenty-nine days with the husband and one additional day with the {\i yavam} \u8212- these periods do not combine to constitute the twelve-month threshold that would ordinarily permit {\i terumah} consumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes a certain redundancy in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation. If eleven months and twenty-nine days with the husband himself does not suffice, it is obviously true that the same period partially spent with the {\i yavam} would not. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i zu v\u8217'zu v\u8217'ein tzarich lomar zu katani} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} employs the rhetorical device of stating both cases in ascending order, even when the second follows logically from the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a related question about the {\i kinyan kaspo} derivation. The verse reads {\i v\u8217'chol kohen ki yikneh nefesh kinyan kaspo} \u8212- and one might wonder why the {\i gemara} specifically emphasizes the language {\i kinyan kaspo} when {\i kiddushin} need not be performed through money; {\i kiddushei biah} or {\i kiddushei shtar} would seem equally applicable. Rashi (or Tosafot \u8212- the {\i shiur} notes some uncertainty in attribution) addresses this: the {\i gemara} highlights {\i kinyan kaspo} not to exclude other modes of {\i kiddushin}, but rather to avoid simply equating the wife with the slave under a single generic {\i kinyan} rule. The extra language of {\i kinyan kaspo} specifically addresses {\i kiddushin} as a distinct category of acquisition. Regardless of the mode of {\i kiddushin}, the underlying {\i halacha} would apply equally.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simpon & Defect (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 12 Mo. vs Chupah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the question of {\i simpon} in its broader structural context. The {\i mishnah} notes that there was an earlier ruling ({\i mishna rishona}) permitting the wife to eat {\i terumah} after twelve months, and a later teaching requiring her to wait until the actual {\i chupah}. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- what was the reason for the later, more stringent ruling?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla, or according to another version Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yehuda, says: {\i mishum simpon} \u8212- the same concern over a defect leading to {\i bitul kiddushin} that was introduced earlier in the {\i sugya}. The {\i gemara} then asks the obvious structural question: if the concern is {\i simpon}, why did the earlier ruling permit {\i terumah} after twelve months at all? And why does the later ruling then change course and require waiting until {\i chupah}? The {\i simpon} concern is equally present at both stages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now draws out the important distinction: {\i bishlama le-Ulla} \u8212- according to Ulla, the progression is internally coherent. Initially the only concern was that she would share {\i terumah} with family members. Once the twelve months arrived and he designated a separate residence for her, that concern fell away, and she was permitted to eat {\i terumah}. Only later did an additional concern surface \u8212- {\i mishum simpon} \u8212- which led to the more stringent ruling requiring her to wait until {\i chupah}. Two separate concerns, discovered at two separate stages, explain why the law tightened over time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But for Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yehuda, who said from the very beginning that the reason for withholding {\i terumah} was {\i simpon}, the question is sharp: {\i kemeikara mishum simpon ul\u8217'vasof mishum simpon} \u8212- the concern at the outset was {\i simpon}, and the concern at the end was {\i simpon}. What changed? Why was twelve months once considered sufficient, and then later declared insufficient?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ika beinaihu bdikat chutz} \u8212- the point of departure between the two rulings is the question of whether an external background check \u8212- investigating the other party through third parties and acquaintances \u8212- constitutes adequate due diligence. One sage held that {\i bdikat chutz shma bdika}: an external background check is a meaningful investigation, and after twelve months one could reasonably have conducted such an inquiry; we were therefore not concerned that {\i simpon} would follow. The other sage held that {\i bdikat chutz lo shma bdika}: external investigation, however thorough, cannot substitute for actually living with a person. True knowledge of a spouse only begins with cohabitation, and therefore twelve months of external investigation does not suffice to allay the {\i simpon} concern. Only once they have reached the stage of {\i chupah} \u8212- when they are on the verge of actual life together \u8212- can we say the background check has been meaningful enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also explains, incidentally, why a post-discovery {\i simpon} claim is not defeated simply by the passage of time. The relevant question is not when the couple married but when the defect was discovered. If a husband concealed something and it came to light only years later, the {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} analysis runs from the moment of discovery, not from the date of {\i kiddushin}. Where the husband himself was aware of the defect and concealed it, {\i savar v\u8217'kibel} \u8212- the principle that one who knew and said nothing implicitly accepted the condition \u8212- would not apply against the wife.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sanctify Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to a new {\i mishnah}: {\i hamakdish ma\u8217'aseh yedei ishto} \u8212- a man declares that his wife\u8217's labor is {\i hekdesh}, consecrated to {\i hakodesh}. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i harei zo osah v\u8217'ochelet} \u8212- she goes ahead and does her work, and eats. The consecration is ineffective. The {\i gemara} will now explain why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand what is at stake, one must appreciate the underlying structure of the {\i ketubah} arrangement. The husband is obligated to provide {\i mezonot} \u8212- to cover his wife\u8217's food expenses. In exchange, he is entitled to {\i maaseh yadeha} \u8212- the proceeds of her labor. This reciprocal arrangement, however, raises the question of who is the fundamental rights-holder: is the entitlement to {\i mezonot} the primary right that generates the wife\u8217's obligation to hand over her earnings, or is the husband\u8217's entitlement to her labor primary, with {\i mezonot} established as the corresponding counterbalance?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additionally, one must note the {\i mishnah}\u8217's later teaching about {\i motar} \u8212- the surplus that a wife produces beyond the minimum defined work expectation. The {\i mishnah} will state that Rabbi Meir holds that the husband can effectively consecrate the {\i motar}, while Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar holds that it remains {\i chulin}. It is striking that the extra amount \u8212- the {\i motar} \u8212- is more susceptible to sanctification in Rabbi Meir\u8217's view than the core amount. The {\i gemara} will work out why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The minimum expectation of {\i maaseh yadeha}, it should be noted, does not require the wife to hold an outside job. The later {\i mishnah} will spell out that work performed within the home \u8212- including household tasks and child-rearing \u8212- satisfies the obligation. There is a defined minimum output, but it is not necessarily commercial in nature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Woman\u8217's Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna says in the name of Rav: {\i yechola isha lomar le\u8217'ba\u8217'ala, eini nizonet v\u8217'eini osa} \u8212- a woman may say to her husband, \u8220"You need not pay my food bill, and I will not hand over the proceeds of my labor.\u8221" She may, in other words, opt out of the entire reciprocal arrangement, retain her own earnings, and forgo the {\i mezonot} support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a statement of considerable significance for how we understand the {\i ketubah} framework. If the husband were the primary rights-holder \u8212- the one whose entitlement to {\i maaseh yadeha} was the bedrock \u8212- one would expect that it is he who determines the terms and she who must comply. But Rav frames it the opposite way: she has the power to reverse the arrangement unilaterally. This strongly suggests that the {\i ketubah} is fundamentally constructed for her benefit, and its terms are hers to waive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle has had practical legal application. In Israel, before the {\i Knesset} legislated in this area, organizations concerned with women\u8217's rights in the {\i beit din} system would arrange for the {\i ketubah} to include a rider \u8212- effectively an exercise of the woman\u8217's {\i eini nizonet v\u8217'eini osa} right \u8212- so that both spouses could then enter into a separate arrangement of shared or independently held financial interests on terms of their own choosing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important implication follows: since she retains the right to opt out at any time, she can equally reverse course at any time. If she loses employment and wishes to return to the {\i ketubah} arrangement, she may do so \u8212- because the entire framework exists in her interest, and she may invoke or waive it as her circumstances change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezonot Primary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains the theoretical basis for Rav\u8217's ruling: {\i ki takinu rabanan mezoni, mezoni ikar} \u8212- when the rabbis established the {\i ketubah} arrangement, {\i mezonot} \u8212- the obligation to provide for her sustenance \u8212- was the primary {\i takana}. {\i U\u8217'maaseh yadeha mishum eiva} \u8212- her corresponding obligation to hand over her earnings was added only secondarily, to prevent resentment ({\i eiva}) from arising if he were supporting her without receiving anything in return. Since {\i mezonot} is primary and its purpose is her protection, she is the one who holds the power: {\i v\u8217'chi amra eini nizonet v\u8217'eini osa, ha\u8217'reshut b\u8217'yadah} \u8212- if she says she neither wants the sustenance nor will hand over her labor, the right is hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Owns Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to draw support from the {\i mishnah} itself: {\i mai lav bimzonot} \u8212- the case of {\i hamakdish maaseh yedei ishto} must be a case where he is in fact paying the food bills, and even so, the consecration fails. Why? Because even though he is currently providing {\i mezonot} and receiving her labor, she retains the power to say no at any moment. Since she can at any time withdraw from the arrangement, she remains the true owner of her labor \u8212- and one cannot sanctify what one does not own. Even before she has exercised her right to withhold, the ongoing possibility of her doing so means that the earnings are fundamentally hers, not his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects this proof: {\i lav bimzonot} \u8212- no, the {\i mishnah} may be speaking of a case where he is {\i not} paying the food bills. Since he is not fulfilling his {\i mezonot} obligation, she is under no obligation to hand over her earnings, and therefore it is entirely unsurprising that he cannot consecrate them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resh Lakish View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish disputes the entire framework of Rav Huna citing Rav. Resh Lakish holds: {\i lo teima ta\u8217'amei de-Rabbi Meir mishum dekasavar adam makdish davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} \u8212- do not say that Rabbi Meir permits sanctification of the {\i motar} because he holds, characteristically, that one may sanctify things that have not yet come into existence. Rather, says Resh Lakish, the reason is different and the underlying model is entirely different: {\i mi-tokh she-yakhol l\u8217'kofah l\u8217'maaseh yadeha} \u8212- since the husband can {\i compel} his wife to work, {\i na\u8217'aseh ke-omer lah yikdeshu yadekh l\u8217'oseihem} \u8212- it is as though he said, \u8220"Let your hands be consecrated to their Maker.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is fundamental. In Resh Lakish\u8217's model, the husband is the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} \u8212- he is in control. The wife {\i cannot} say no. Because he can coerce her labor, he has a present, real monetary ownership in her labor capacity \u8212- not merely in future earnings that do not yet exist, but in the very ownership interest in her hands, right now. When he declares {\i hekdesh}, he is not sanctifying something that does not exist ({\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}); he is sanctifying a present ownership right that he already holds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This stands in direct opposition to Rav\u8217's view. For Rav, she is the {\i ba\u8217'alat habayit}; for Resh Lakish, he is the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit}. For Rav, she can say no; for Resh Lakish, she cannot. And because Resh Lakish holds that the husband has a genuine present ownership in her labor, the act of {\i hekdesh} is legally coherent \u8212- it sanctifies something he truly owns right now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir: Motar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This still leaves open the question raised by the {\i mishnah} itself: why can Rabbi Meir consecrate the {\i motar} \u8212- the surplus \u8212- but not the core {\i maaseh yadeha}? The {\i gemara} explains the structure this way: the core amount of {\i maaseh yadeha} is tied to the {\i mezonot} arrangement. He is obligated to feed her; her obligation to produce the minimum is the quid pro quo. His right to the core amount is entirely contingent on his fulfilling the {\i mezonot} obligation, and that contingency makes it insufficiently \u8220"his\u8221" to consecrate with full legal effect. But the {\i motar} \u8212- what she produces beyond that minimum \u8212- is tied not to {\i mezonot} but to the pin money ({\i ma\u8217'ah sela}) he gives her as discretionary spending. That amount is genuinely and independently his \u8212- it is not conditioned on the primary {\i mezonot} exchange in the same way \u8212- and therefore he can consecrate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Things\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes that Resh Lakish\u8217's explanation \u8212- that Rabbi Meir sanctifies his present ownership in her hands rather than future earnings \u8212- carries an implicit assumption: that Rabbi Meir holds one cannot sanctify {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}. But this is surprising, because Rabbi Meir is elsewhere among the {\i tannaim} who are most permissive on this very issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proves the point from a {\i baraita}: {\i ha-omer le-ishah harei at mekudeshet li le-akhar she-etgayer} \u8212- if a man says to a woman, \u8220"You are betrothed to me after I convert,\u8221" or \u8220"after you convert,\u8221" or \u8220"after I am freed from slavery,\u8221" or \u8220"after your husband dies,\u8221" or \u8220"after your sister dies,\u8221" or \u8220"after your {\i yavam} performs {\i chalitzah} for you\u8221" \u8212- {\i Rabbi Meir omer mekudeshet} \u8212- Rabbi Meir holds that such a conditional {\i kiddushin} is valid. This is structurally analogous to {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}, and Rabbi Meir accepts it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i me-hahu in} \u8212- yes, from that {\i baraita} one can prove that Rabbi Meir holds one may sanctify things that do not yet exist, and that could theoretically be the explanation for the {\i mishnah} as well. {\i Me-ha leika le-mishma minnah} \u8212- but from our {\i mishnah} alone, one cannot draw that conclusion. The {\i mishnah} is equally explicable on Resh Lakish\u8217's model \u8212- that Rabbi Meir is sanctifying a present ownership right in the wife\u8217's hands, rather than future produce. The {\i mishnah} therefore remains ambiguous between the two positions, and the deeper dispute \u8212- whether she is the {\i ba\u8217'alat habayit} who can say no, or he is the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} who can compel \u8212- remains the animating question underlying the entire chapter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 59\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife\u8217's Labor Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Labor Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to a discussion begun at the bottom of 58b. The {\i mishnah} establishes that if a husband attempts to sanctify the labor of his wife\u8217's hands, the act is void. The {\i gemara} explored two distinct reasons for this ruling, both of which turn on the foundational question: who is fundamentally the owner of the wife\u8217's labor?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two approaches emerge. Rashi understands that since the wife retains the power to refuse her husband\u8217's support \u8212- opting out of the arrangement by which he feeds her in exchange for her labor \u8212- she is considered the fundamental owner, and his ownership never fully takes hold, even when she does not in fact refuse. Tosafot, by contrast, limits this reasoning: only when she actually refuses to accept support from him does ownership revert to her; so long as the arrangement is operative, he controls her labor. A second approach altogether frames the case differently \u8212- it is the husband who is in control, but who has chosen to forfeit his claim by failing to provide for her, and it is this forfeiture that strips him of entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motar: Meir vs.\u160 ?Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The closing section of the {\i mishnah} presents a dispute regarding the {\i motar} \u8212- the amount that the wife produces beyond the minimum she is obligated to provide. Rabbi Meir holds that if the husband sanctifies this excess, it is {\i kadosh}; Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar holds it is not. The {\i gemara} now turns to examine what precisely underlies this disagreement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav & Shmuel View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Death Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav and Shmuel, speaking in tandem, offer a striking reading of Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. They interpret {\i motar le-achar misah kadosh}: the {\i kiddushin} takes effect only after the wife dies. Their reasoning reconstructs the case as one in which the husband is not, in fact, entitled to the {\i motar} during the wife\u8217's lifetime \u8212- the scenario being that he has failed to fulfill one of his obligations, thereby forfeiting his right to her excess labor. The husband will eventually come into possession of those earnings only when he inherits them after her death. At that point, the sanctification he declared during her lifetime finally takes effect, retroactively, once the property enters his domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading renders Rabbi Meir\u8217's position nearly vacuous as a statement about {\i motar} per se. The real issue is not who owns the excess labor but whether one can sanctify something before it comes into one\u8217's possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Davar Shelo Ba L\u8217'olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under this framework, the dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar reduces entirely to a question of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} \u8212- whether a person can sanctify something that has not yet entered the world or entered his ownership. Rabbi Meir holds that such a sanctification can take effect retroactively; Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar holds it cannot. The question of who owns the {\i motar} is, on this reading, a side condition of the case rather than the core of the debate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Adda: Alive Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motar Sanctified Alive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Abba dissents: {\i motar mechayim kadosh} \u8212- the {\i motar} is sanctified even during the wife\u8217's lifetime. On his reading, the {\i mishnah} is dealing with a case where the husband is in fact entitled to the {\i motar}, and the dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar is genuinely about who owns the excess labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forfeiture Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: precisely in which scenario does the husband forfeit his right to the {\i motar}? It frames the question by examining the system of reciprocal obligations. He is paying the {\i mezonot} \u8212- the regular food bill \u8212- but not the {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef}, the fixed weekly pin money he is obligated to provide for her personal use. The question becomes whether, in that configuration, he retains rights to the {\i motar} or not, and this is what the Tannaitic dispute is about.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Correspondence Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common-to-Common Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now articulates the underlying structural question: what exchange corresponds to what? One view holds {\i midi deshachich mimidi deshachich} \u8212- common things correspond to common things. The {\i mezonot}, the food bill, is a ubiquitous expense and a constant presence in every household; similarly, the wife\u8217's baseline labor \u8212- the minimum she is obligated to produce \u8212- is a regular, ever-present obligation. These two common quantities therefore correspond to one another: the food bill is the exchange for the minimum {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha}. Likewise, the {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef} \u8212- the pin money, less essential, more variable in its significance \u8212- corresponds to the {\i motar}, the excess labor that also varies and does not always arise. So on this view: regular food bill for regular labor, pin money for excess labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand what {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha} means concretely, the {\i gemara} later makes clear that it encompasses not only domestic chores \u8212- cooking, baking, laundry \u8212- which produce no separate income, but also productive work such as weaving and seamstress activity, through which the wife could earn money beyond household upkeep. Her minimum obligation in this latter category is quantified: the weight of five {\i sela\u8217'im} of warp thread in Judea, equivalent to ten {\i sela\u8217'im} in the Galilee, where coins were of a different weight. This is the minimum amount of productive spinning and weaving she is expected to contribute weekly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed-to-Fixed Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposing view holds {\i midi dekaitz mimidi dekaitz} \u8212- fixed amounts correspond to fixed amounts. The pin money is a precisely specified sum, a fixed quantity. The minimum {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha} is also a fixed obligation \u8212- she must produce exactly a certain amount, neither more nor less. These two fixed quantities therefore pair with one another. What is unfixed \u8212- the {\i mezonot}, which can fluctuate depending on the week\u8217's needs and expenses, and the {\i motar}, which can vary enormously depending on how much extra work the wife takes on \u8212- these two variable quantities correspond to each other. On this reading: pin money for minimum labor, variable food bill for excess labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This second approach makes a compelling point. The {\i motar} in a given week might be trivial, or it might be enormous \u8212- a wife who is exceptionally productive could generate a sum vastly exceeding any minimum. The food bill, similarly, is not truly a constant; it rises and falls. So it is more natural to pair the fixed with the fixed and the fluctuating with the fluctuating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} between these two positions becomes clear in the case of a refusal. If the husband stops paying one of his obligations, the question of what he loses access to depends on which view one adopts. If the common-to-common view is correct, refusing to pay the food bill costs him access to her minimum labor; refusing the pin money costs him the {\i motar}. If the fixed-to-fixed view is correct, the correspondences are reversed. The same logic applies symmetrically: if the wife refuses to perform a given category of labor, the husband is relieved of the corresponding obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Source Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pin Money Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges one of these positions by citing a Tannaitic source \u8212- apparently a {\i mishnah} \u8212- that states: if the husband does not give her the {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef} for her personal needs, {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha shelah} \u8212- she retains her labor. This seems to support the view that the {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef} corresponds directly to the core {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha}, i.e., the fixed-to-fixed alignment: pin money in exchange for minimum labor. The {\i mishnah} then goes on to describe what that minimum labor consists of \u8212- the weight of five {\i sela\u8217'im} of warp \u8212- which seems to confirm that what she reclaims when he withholds the pin money is precisely her core minimum obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Reinterprets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resists this reading. {\i Eima motar ma\u8217'aseh yadeha shelah} \u8212- what she retains is not the core {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha} but the {\i motar}, the excess beyond the minimum. The description of the minimum, the {\i gemara} argues, is included only so that one can calculate what exceeds it: {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha kama havei, de-layeida motar didah kama} \u8212- it tells you what the minimum is precisely so that you know how much is above and beyond it. The {\i mishnah}, in other words, describes the baseline not to define what she keeps, but to define the threshold above which she keeps the surplus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a reading that imposes something on the plain meaning of the text. The {\i gemara} acknowledges the tension: different Tannaitic formulations seem to pull in different directions on whether it is the {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef} that corresponds to the minimum labor or to the {\i motar}. The question of correspondence is genuinely unresolved across the sources.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now records Shmuel\u8217's ruling: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar} \u8212- we rule like Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar, who holds that the husband\u8217's sanctification of the {\i motar} does not take effect, i.e., it remains {\i chullin}. Under the framework of Rav and Shmuel \u8212- where the case is one in which the husband is not entitled to the {\i motar} until he inherits it \u8212- ruling like Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar means affirming that one cannot be {\i makdish} a {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam vs.\u160 ?Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} immediately raises a contradiction. Shmuel also rules {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri} in a different {\i mishnah}, which involves a woman who declares {\i konam she\u8217'ani oseh lefichah} \u8212- using the vow-formula {\i konam}, a shorthand for {\i korban}, she forbids the labor of her hands to her husband. The {\i Tanna kamma} holds that the husband need not annul this vow because it is inherently void: she does not own her labor, and one cannot forbid what one does not own. Rabbi Akiva holds he must annul it, lest she produce more than the minimum, in which case the excess \u8212- which is hers \u8212- would be caught by the vow. Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri holds he must annul it because if he subsequently divorces her, the vow will take effect at the moment of divorce, and once operative, it cannot be retroactively annulled; it would then prevent them from remarrying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel rules like Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri, which implies the vow can take effect at the moment of divorce \u8212- i.e., that a {\i konam} declared now over something she does not yet own can take hold later when she acquires it. But this seems to contradict ruling like Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar, who denies that a {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} can be sanctified. Why should a {\i konam} succeed where {\i hekdesh} fails?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to rescue the consistency of Shmuel\u8217's rulings by suggesting he only meant to rule like Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri regarding the concern about {\i hadafa} \u8212- the possibility she produces extra labor \u8212- effectively aligning him with Rabbi Akiva rather than with Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri\u8217's reasoning about the delayed effect of the vow. But this answer is immediately rejected as strained: if that were Shmuel\u8217's meaning, he should simply have said the ruling follows Rabbi Akiva, or that it does not follow the {\i Tanna kamma}. The {\i gemara} therefore abandons this resolution and turns to Rav Yosef for a different approach.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Vow Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam at Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying question is why the {\i konam} should be able to take effect at the moment of divorce. When the woman declares her labor forbidden to her husband, she does not own that labor \u8212- he is currently entitled to it. So how can her vow have any grip? The answer requires explaining what is distinctive about {\i konamot} relative to ordinary {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motar Yedei Hadachak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One possibility raised by the {\i gemara} is that even while he is entitled to the standard {\i motar}, there exists an additional category \u8212- {\i motar yedei hadachak}, an extraordinary surplus produced when the woman pushes herself beyond all normal expectations, working late into the night. That extreme excess remains hers even during the marriage, and it is to that excess that Rabbi Akiva is referring when he says the vow may take hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Trigger Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri\u8217's position goes further. He argues that the vow\u8217's concern is not about what she produces during the marriage but about what happens afterward. Even if the vow is entirely inoperative while she is married, it is poised to take effect the moment the marriage ends. Once the {\i get} is given, she regains ownership of her labor, and the {\i konam} she declared at the outset snaps into effect. At that point, her labor is forbidden to her ex-husband. The problem is not the present but the future: if they then wish to remarry, it will be too late for him to annul the vow \u8212- a vow can only be annulled on the day it is made. Therefore, Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri rules that the husband must annul the vow now, before this scenario ever materializes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Konamot vs.\u160 ?Hekdesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity Change Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef explains: {\i shani konamos} \u8212- {\i konamot} are different in kind from ordinary {\i hekdesh}. Normal {\i hekdesh} is {\i kedusha shel damim} \u8212- a transfer of ownership, where the Temple treasury becomes the owner of the sanctified object in the same way any other legal entity is an owner. One cannot transfer ownership of something one does not own, which is why {\i hekdesh} cannot work on a {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} or {\i davar shelo ba lirshuto}. But {\i konamot} operate through a different mechanism, comparable to {\i kedushat haguf} \u8212- they change the very identity of the object rather than merely reassigning its ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle has wider ramifications, articulated by Rava: {\i hekdesh, chametz, ve-shichrur mafkin mi-yedei shibud} \u8212- sanctifying an object as a {\i korban}, rendering {\i chametz} forbidden on Passover, and freeing a slave all remove existing liens, because these acts do not merely change an object\u8217's owner but change its identity. A creditor who holds a lien on an animal cannot collect once the animal becomes a {\i korban}, because a {\i korban} is not simply property under different ownership \u8212- it has been taken out of the ordinary world of ownable things entirely. Similarly, a lien on {\i chametz} that becomes {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah} on Passover is extinguished; and a lien on a slave evaporates upon manumission, because a free person cannot be the subject of a lien.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The power of {\i konamot} is that they operate through this identity-altering mechanism. A woman who declares {\i konam} on her hands is not merely transferring ownership of her labor; she is invoking the framework of {\i kedushat haguf}, which operates more fundamentally than a simple change in title.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef\u8217's initial formulation of why {\i konamot} are more powerful than {\i hekdesh} draws on the following observation: {\i mitokh she-adam oser peirot chaveiro alav, adam makdish davar shelo ba lirshuto} \u8212- since a person can, by vow, forbid another\u8217's fruit to himself, it follows that {\i nedarim} can reach beyond what one owns. Therefore, the woman can forbid her future labor even though she does not currently own it, since {\i konamot} belong to the realm of {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye objects with precision. He distinguishes two types of {\i neder}-formulas: I can forbid my fruit to you; I can also forbid your fruit to me. The common thread that makes both possible is that in each case, at least one party has some connection \u8212- either the owner forbids his own property, or the person becoming forbidden is the subject of the restriction. What one cannot do is forbid your fruit to you. That would require no connection whatsoever to either the owner or the one being restricted. Now apply this to the woman\u8217's case: her labor is his, and she is trying to make it forbidden to him. That is precisely the forbidden configuration \u8212- she is making his property forbidden to him, which has no parallel in the world of {\i nedarim}. The analogy to forbidding another\u8217's fruit therefore fails.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sanctify Hands Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hands Exist Now\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Rav Yehoshua offers a different approach: {\i be-omeret yakdishu yadai le-oseihem} \u8212- what if the woman frames her declaration as sanctifying her hands themselves, not the labor they will produce? The hands already exist in the world. The problem with {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} is that the object of sanctification is not yet present; if what she is sanctifying is the hands themselves, which are physically present now, perhaps that resolves the {\i lo ba l\u8217'olam} difficulty. The {\i gemara} engages this idea: {\i d\u8217'yadayim isnhu b\u8217'olam} \u8212- indeed, the hands are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But there is a second problem independent of whether the hands exist: she does not own them, in the relevant sense. While she owns her body in a fundamental way, the monetary rights in the work her hands produce currently belong to her husband. Even if we have resolved the {\i lo ba l\u8217'olam} issue by pointing to the physical existence of the hands, the ownership problem remains. He has the entitlement; she cannot sanctify what is his.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Delayed Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Ownership Trigger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore refines the formula: {\i d\u8217'amra lichshe\u8217'etgaresh} \u8212- she declares her hands sanctified {\i when she gets divorced}. By specifying the future moment of divorce, she is not trying to sanctify something she currently owns but rather the ownership she will regain once the marriage ends. This is more defensible: she does have a base, residual ownership in her own body and labor \u8212- an ownership that is currently suppressed by the husband\u8217's entitlement but that will resurface upon divorce. She is sanctifying specifically that latent, future-oriented ownership right now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resurfacing Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy offered is to China\u8217's rights over Hong Kong during the lease period: the underlying ownership existed and was real, even though it could not be exercised for ninety-nine years. The reversionary right was a genuine legal interest that could be assigned or acted upon during the lease period. Similarly, this woman holds a genuine, if dormant, ownership interest in her own labor \u8212- the interest she will have when single \u8212- and that interest is what she is sanctifying now. This formulation appears promising, and it is precisely what Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri\u8217's ruling presupposes: that the vow has no present effect but is primed to activate at the moment of divorce.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Uncertainty Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Before Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} poses a serious difficulty: {\i umi ika midi de-lo hashta lo kadish ul\u8217'kame kadish?} \u8212- is there really such a thing as a sanctification that does not work now but will work later? Rabbi Elai responds affirmatively and supplies an analogy. If someone says to his friend: \u8220"This field that I am selling you \u8212- when I repurchase it from you, it shall be sanctified,\u8221" the {\i gemara} takes it as obvious that this would be effective. The seller has an interest in the field \u8212- the right to buy it back \u8212- and sanctifies that interest now for the moment it is exercised. Similarly, she has an interest that will resurface upon divorce, and she sanctifies it now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Repurchase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rabbi Yirmiyah immediately challenges the analogy. In the field case, the one making the declaration is the owner at the moment of speaking \u8212- he owns the field before he sells it, so his act of sanctification at that moment is grounded in real ownership. Moreover, he has the power to bring the sanctification about: he can choose to repurchase the field. The woman is in a fundamentally different position in both respects.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 No Control Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lack of Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiyah reformulates the field analogy in a way that strips out the favorable elements. The apt comparison, he says, is not to a field being sold with a repurchase clause declared before the sale, but to someone who has already sold the field and then declares, post-sale, that when he repurchases it, it shall be sanctified. There he has no current ownership, and no guaranteed ability to repurchase. That, Rabbi Yirmiyah argues, is void \u8212- and that is the woman\u8217's situation. She has no current ownership of her labor\u8217's monetary rights, and she has no power to bring about the divorce that would restore them. She could die before him; he could never choose to divorce her. The future ownership is genuinely uncertain, not merely delayed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa pushes back by introducing a distinction: in the field case, the buyer owns both the {\i guf} \u8212- the body of the field \u8212- and the {\i peirot} \u8212- its produce. He owns everything. Here, the woman owns her own body \u8212- {\i gufa beyadah hu} \u8212- even while he owns the {\i peirot}, the entitlement to her labor. This is analogous, Rav Pappa suggests, to a field given as collateral for a loan: the borrower retains title while the lender holds and benefits from the field\u8217's produce. In such a case, the borrower can sanctify the field even now, because he retains title. So too the woman, who retains ownership of her body, might be able to sanctify her hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Body Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet son of Rav Idi further refines this: the better analogy is to a field given as collateral for a fixed term \u8212- say, ten years \u8212- during which the borrower cannot redeem it. After ten years, however, the right of redemption returns automatically. Even so, the {\i gemara} accepts, the borrower can sanctify the field for after the ten-year period, because he knows with certainty that the right will eventually return to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then makes the decisive objection: {\i hatam le-achar eser shanim miha beyado lifdotah} \u8212- after ten years, the borrower will certainly regain control. The certainty of eventual repossession grounds the sanctification. But the woman has no such certainty: {\i ein beyado legaresh atzmah l\u8217'olam} \u8212- she has no ability to divorce herself, ever. If he lives and refuses to divorce her, she will never regain her labor rights. There is therefore no sufficiently grounded reversionary interest to anchor the sanctification.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity vs.\u160 ?Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi ultimately returns to and reformulates Rav Yosef\u8217's approach, applying it with greater precision. {\i Shani konamos dekedushat haguf ninhu} \u8212- {\i konamot} are different because they operate through {\i kedushat haguf}, identity-level sanctity, rather than {\i kedusha shel damim}, which is merely ownership-level sanctity. This is the principle underlying Rava\u8217's statement: {\i hekdesh, chametz, ve-shichrur mafkin mi-yedei shibud} \u8212- these three \u8212- consecration as a {\i korban}, {\i chametz} on Passover, and freeing a slave \u8212- dissolve all liens, because they alter the object\u8217's identity rather than merely transferring title. A lien is a right attached to an owner\u8217's property; when the property ceases to be ownable property in the ordinary sense, the lien has nothing to attach to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i konam} the woman declares operates through this mechanism. She is not trying to transfer ownership of her labor to some third party. She is, as it were, changing the identity of her hands \u8212- making them like a {\i korban}, not in a literal sense (her hands will not be used in the Temple), but in the legal-structural sense that the {\i neder} invokes {\i kedushat haguf}. This is more powerful than what the husband attempted in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's original case, where he sought to sanctify the {\i motar} through {\i hekdesh} \u8212- a simple ownership transfer \u8212- which fails when the object is not in his possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Lien Strengthe\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet even this more powerful mechanism has limits. The {\i gemara} asks: if {\i kedushat haguf} can overcome liens, why does the woman\u8217's {\i konam} not take effect immediately, dissolving the husband\u8217's rights on the spot? The answer: {\i amru rabanan le-shibuda de-ba\u8217'al ki heichi de-lo tikdesh me-hashtah} \u8212- the Rabbis strengthened the husband\u8217's lien specifically so that the {\i konam} cannot take effect during the marriage. This is a rabbinic reinforcement of the husband\u8217's entitlement, designed to prevent the identity-altering power of {\i konamot} from unraveling the marital arrangement mid-marriage. The result is a carefully constructed compromise: the {\i konam} is real and will take effect upon divorce, but the Rabbis ensured that during the marriage, the husband\u8217's lien is robust enough to suppress it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} acknowledges that the mechanics here are not fully transparent. A {\i konam} is not truly a {\i korban} \u8212- the woman\u8217's hands are not going to perform Temple service. The identity change is structural rather than literal. And the exact boundary between \u8220"the husband\u8217's lien is strong enough to suppress it now\u8221" and \u8220"it will take effect at divorce\u8221" is left somewhat inexact. Nonetheless, the conclusion holds: the husband cannot sanctify his wife\u8217's labor that he is not entitled to until she dies \u8212- following Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar \u8212- and the wife can use a {\i konam}-formula to forbid her labor to her husband in a way that takes effect upon divorce, through the mechanism of {\i kedushat haguf}, with the Rabbis\u8217' having ensured it does not unravel the marriage prematurely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife\u8217's Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Duties List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having resolved the extended discussion of ownership and sanctification, the {\i gemara} at last arrives at the next {\i mishnah}, which enumerates the wife\u8217's actual domestic obligations. {\i Ve-eilu melachot she-ha-isha osa le-vaalah} \u8212- these are the labors the wife performs for her husband. The list is comprehensive and reflects a traditional household structure: she grinds the wheat ({\i tochenet}), bakes ({\i ofa}), does the laundry ({\i mechabbeset}), cooks ({\i mevashelet}), nurses the children ({\i meineika et benah}), makes the beds ({\i matzaat lo ha-mitah}), and works with wool ({\i osa ba-tzemer}). This last item \u8212- working with wool, weaving, and seamstress work \u8212- is particularly significant, as it represents the category of productive labor that generates actual income beyond mere household upkeep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves Reduce Duties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then introduces a sliding scale keyed to the number of {\i shefachot} \u8212- maidservants \u8212- the wife brings into the marriage as part of her dowry. If she brings one servant, she is relieved of the three most laborious tasks: grinding, baking, and laundry. Two servants relieve her also of cooking and nursing. Three servants relieve her of making the beds and the seamstress work as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Slaves Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she brings four servants, {\i yoshevet be-katedra} \u8212- she sits on her throne. {\i Katedra} is the Roman-derived term for a formal seat of authority, the root from which \u8220"cathedral\u8221" derives. Rashi explains that sitting on the {\i katedra} means not merely that she has no formal obligations, but that she need not even perform the smallest incidental task \u8212- fetching something from another room, bringing an object at her husband\u8217's request. Every conceivable request falls to the fourth servant. She is entirely exempt.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Eliezer Spin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Idleness Leads Zeimah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer dissents. Even if a wife brings a hundred servants into the marriage, the husband may still compel her to do the seamstress work \u8212- spinning and weaving. His rationale: {\i she-ha-batalah mevi\u8217'ah lidei zimmah} \u8212- idleness leads to licentiousness. If she has nothing productive to occupy her, she will seek out trouble. The choice of seamstress work as the irreducible minimum is notable; it is purposeful labor that keeps the hands and mind engaged without being as physically strenuous as grinding or laundry.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabban Shimon Shiamum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Against Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel takes an entirely different perspective, and from a different direction. He addresses the case of a husband who takes a vow \u8212- {\i madir ishah milasot melachah} \u8212- forbidding his wife\u8217's labor to himself. If he cannot benefit from her work, then requiring her to do that work makes no sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon rules: {\i yotzi ve-yiten ketubah} \u8212- he must divorce her and pay the {\i ketubah}. The reason he gives is arresting: {\i she-ha-batalah mevi\u8217'ah lidei shiamum} \u8212- idleness leads to {\i shiamum}, going out of one\u8217's mind, a kind of psychological disintegration. This is a notably different moral framework from Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's. Rabbi Eliezer is concerned that idleness will lead the wife to sin; Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel is concerned that idleness is itself harmful to the wife \u8212- that productive engagement is a basic human need, and that a situation in which she is barred from all meaningful work is intolerable for her wellbeing. The contrast between {\i zimmah} and {\i shiamum} captures two entirely different orientations: moral surveillance versus concern for the person.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grinding Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an immediate question about the first item on the {\i mishnah}\u8217's list: {\i tochenet salka deitach} \u8212- can you really mean she grinds wheat herself? Grinding grain was the province of millstones and water-wheels. The {\i gemara} resolves this with two options: {\i ela eima matchenet} \u8212- she causes it to be ground, meaning she brings the wheat to the mill, or alternatively, it refers to a case of {\i reichaya de-yada} \u8212- a hand mill, a scenario where hand-grinding is in fact expected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Like Rabbi Hiyya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's comprehensive list of domestic obligations does not accord with the view of Rabbi Hiyya. The {\i mishnah} assigns the wife significant labor responsibilities; Rabbi Hiyya\u8217's perspective implies a very different understanding of what a wife is primarily for.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Hiyya View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Purposes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Hiyya taught three formulations: {\i ein ishah ela le-yofi} \u8212- a woman is only for beauty; {\i ein ishah ela le-banim} \u8212- a woman is only for children; {\i ein ishah ela le-tachshitei ishah} \u8212- a woman is only for adornment. These are not presented as legal rulings in context but as statements of attitude \u8212- they echo the voice of the women who danced in the fields on Tu B\u8217'Av and Yom Kippur as recorded elsewhere, each group calling out what men ostensibly seek in a wife. Nevertheless, the {\i gemara} takes these statements seriously enough to note that if Rabbi Hiyya\u8217's view were adopted as a legal baseline, the wife would not be expected to perform work that might compromise her beauty or her ability to bear children, and the {\i mishnah}\u8217's extensive catalog of obligations would not apply. Our {\i mishnah}, the {\i gemara} concludes, does not follow Rabbi Hiyya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husbands\u8217' Duties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Hiyya also taught practical guidance that flows from these premises. {\i Ha-rotzeh she-yadin et ishto} \u8212- if a husband wishes his wife to have a radiant complexion, {\i yalbishena klei pishtan} \u8212- he should clothe her in linen garments. And {\i ha-rotzeh she-yalbin et bito} \u8212- if a man wishes his daughter to have fair skin so that she will be attractive in marriage, {\i ya\u8217'achilenu efrochim ve-yashkenu chalav samuch le-pirkah} \u8212- he should feed her chicks and give her milk to drink near the time of her maturity. The {\i gemara} closes with these notes of practical domestic counsel, a fitting if somewhat surprising coda to a daf that began with the most intricate questions of ownership, sanctification, and identity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 60\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nursing Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Nursing & Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins approximately ten lines from the bottom of 59b, with the Mishnah\u8217's discussion of {\i umeneket beno} \u8212- a wife\u8217's obligation to nurse her husband\u8217's child. Among the various domestic tasks incumbent upon a wife, nursing is enumerated as one of her obligations to her husband. The Gemara immediately raises a challenge: is our Mishnah consistent with the position of Beit Shammai?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Baraita is cited in which a woman takes a vow \u8212- {\i nadra shelo lehanik} \u8212- that prevents her from nursing the child. The Gemara clarifies what type of vow this could be. A vow functions by rendering something forbidden to a person. In this context, she might make the benefit of her nursing forbidden to her husband, who benefits indirectly because his child is being nursed. Alternatively, she might make the act itself forbidden to herself, given that the Gemara acknowledges that a woman derives a measure of pleasure from the act of nursing. Either way, the vow is structured so as to prohibit the nursing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what precisely is being forbidden by the vow \u8212- the benefit to the husband or the act itself \u8212- bears on the vow\u8217's validity and its interaction with her marital obligations. The Gemara\u8217's discussion implicitly recalls the earlier treatment of {\i maaseh yadayim}: just as a wife cannot render her labor forbidden to her husband because he holds a prior claim on it, so too there is a question of whether she can render her nursing forbidden when it is itself a marital obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai vs Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai rules: {\i shometet dad mi-pi} \u8212- she removes the breast from the child\u8217's mouth. Even if she is in the midst of nursing, she stops. The vow is binding and she has no legal compulsion to continue. Beit Hillel, by contrast, rules: {\i kofo umeneket oto} \u8212- her husband can compel her to nurse the child. The implication of Beit Hillel\u8217's position is that the vow was never binding in the first place, since she is not in a position to render forbidden a service that her husband already has a prior claim upon. Beit Shammai, it follows, holds that she bears no strict legal obligation to nurse her child, allowing the vow to take full effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce & Nursing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once she is divorced, {\i einah kofah} \u8212- he can no longer compel her, since her obligation to him has ended. At that point, presumably, the vow takes effect. This touches on the earlier discussion about a vow taking effect at a postponed moment in time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the child already {\i recognized} the mother \u8212- and Rashi adds, though it is not explicit in the Gemara, that this means the child will not nurse from any other woman \u8212- then even in the post-divorce situation, the husband pays her a nursemaid\u8217's fee and can compel his ex-wife to nurse the child: {\i notin lah secharah ve-kofo umeneket oto}. The Gemara underscores the clinical importance of this: it is understood as a genuine {\i sakanah} \u8212- a danger to the child\u8217's life \u8212- if a child who will only nurse from his mother is denied her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a penetrating question: if the vow is binding, is the woman now violating it when compelled to nurse? One possibility is that this involves {\i pikuach nefesh}, overriding the vow entirely. Yet if so, one would expect the Gemara to discuss annulling the vow through a {\i heter neder} rather than simply compelling her. A more coherent reading may be that in any scenario where the husband retains the legal authority to compel nursing \u8212- even with payment \u8212- the vow never achieved binding status to begin with, since it could never take hold in a domain subject to that compulsion. The Gemara does not develop this explicitly, but it is the most internally consistent reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blame & Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then entertains a reinterpretation of the entire Beit Shammai\u8211-Beit Hillel dispute. Perhaps neither side is debating whether the vow is binding or whether she can be compelled to nurse. Perhaps both agree the vow is binding, and the real dispute is about {\i who is to blame} \u8212- and therefore who bears the financial consequences. This reading is associated with Tosafot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sakana Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes explicitly: {\i mipnei hasakanah} \u8212- even where a vow might otherwise apply, the danger to the child represents an overriding concern. The recognition that a nursing mother\u8217's milk, and especially the act of nursing from one\u8217's own mother, constitutes a life-sustaining need for the infant is treated as a foundational {\i halakhic} principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Affirmed Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then frames a specific scenario: {\i shenadra hi vekiyam lah hu} \u8212- she made the vow and he not merely failed to annul it but actively {\i affirmed} it. Once a husband affirms the vow, he can no longer annul it even on the same day. Now the question becomes: who is culpable for the resulting situation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai holds: {\i hu nosein etzba bein shineha} \u8212- he put his finger between her teeth. He is to blame; he is the one who affirmed the vow. Beit Hillel holds: {\i hi nosna etzba bein shineha} \u8212- she put her finger between her teeth. She made the vow; she bears responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Tosafot\u8217's reading, what the two schools are debating is not whether she can be compelled to nurse but rather who must bear the financial cost of hiring a nursemaid. If he is to blame, she is exempt. If she is to blame, she must cover the expense. The Gemara, however, objects: if the debate were simply about blame and financial responsibility in cases where a wife\u8217's vow frustrates her {\i ketuva} obligations, the Tannaim could have framed the dispute in general terms about {\i ketuva} obligations \u8212- with the {\i nafka mina} being whether he must pay her {\i ketuva} upon divorce. Why single out the nursing case? Moreover, another Baraita states explicitly: {\i Beit Shammai omrim einah menikah} \u8212- Beit Shammai says she does not have to nurse. This makes clear that the dispute is indeed about the existence of the obligation itself. The Gemara concludes: {\i ela mehavarta kesimin d\u8217'lo ke-Beit Shammai} \u8212- the Mishnah simply does not follow Beit Shammai.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Child Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognition Age\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Baraita we examined establishes that if the infant already {\i makkira} \u8212- recognizes \u8212- his mother, and according to Rashi will refuse to nurse from another woman, the ex-husband can compel even a divorced wife to nurse for pay. The Gemara now asks: from what age do we assume that a child will recognize the mother in this operative sense?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three Amoraic opinions are given. Rava citing Rav Yirmiya bar Abba citing Rav: {\i shlosha chodashim} \u8212- three months. Shmuel: {\i shloshim yom} \u8212- thirty days. Rav Yitzchak citing Rabbi Yochanan: {\i chamishim yom} \u8212- fifty days. Rav Shimi bar Chiya then rules: {\i halacha} follows the last-cited position of fifty days, in the name of Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: the debate between Rav and Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- three months versus fifty days \u8212- is understandable, as it may reflect that different children develop recognition at different rates, each authority ruling according to the earlier or later range of typical cases. But Shmuel says the child recognizes the mother at thirty days \u8212- is such a thing possible at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara leaves this without definitive resolution. The practical question of what precisely \u8220"recognition\u8221" means is relevant here. Does it mean merely some inchoate awareness of the mother\u8217's presence, or specifically that the child refuses to nurse from anyone else \u8212- as Rashi\u8217's addition suggests? If it is the latter, testing would be more straightforward: simply offer the child another nursemaid and observe whether he accepts her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case-by-Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Dimi bar Yechezkel returned from Eretz Yisrael, he corrected the transmission. He said: do not follow these fixed rules that Yehuda my brother reported in the name of Shmuel. What Shmuel actually said is {\i kol zman shemakkira} \u8212- the determination is made case by case. Rather than applying a bright-line rule of thirty, fifty, or ninety days, one must examine the specific child to determine whether he recognizes his mother or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lineup Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then brings a story. A woman came before Shmuel: she had been divorced, and her husband sought to compel her to continue nursing. Shmuel instructed Rav Dimi bar Yosef: go and investigate whether the child recognizes her. Rav Dimi bar Yosef conducted a lineup \u8212- {\i dara d\u8217'neshei} \u8212- a row of women, and had the infant carried past them. When the child passed before his mother, {\i havat kemashfi le\u8217'apa} \u8212- he lit up, recognizing her face. She, seeking to avoid the obligation, averted her eyes \u8212- {\i kavashtanhu le\u8217'einei mineih}. Rav Dimi bar Yosef said to her: {\i netof einach kum dari brach} \u8212- lift your eyes, go carry your son. You must nurse him; he clearly recognizes you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This story is notable precisely because the test was recognition, not refusal to nurse from another woman. The far simpler test \u8212- offering the child to a nursemaid and observing whether he nurses \u8212- was not employed. This suggests that the Gemara\u8217's operative criterion is recognition itself, and Rashi\u8217's addition about refusing to nurse from others may be his explanatory gloss rather than the Gemara\u8217's own requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blind Child\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: how would a blind child \u8212- {\i suma} \u8212- recognize his mother, given that the test used by the Gemara was visual? Rav Ashi answers: {\i be-reicha uve-ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- through smell and taste. The other senses are sufficient to enable identification even where sight is unavailable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nursing Duration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 24 Month Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions from the question of a wife\u8217's nursing obligation to a broader treatment of nursing in general, citing a Baraita: {\i yonek tinok ve-holech ad esrim ve-arba\u8217'ah chodesh} \u8212- a child may continue nursing until twenty-four months. Beyond that point, says the first opinion \u8212- that of Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- nursing is {\i ke-yonek sheket}, like nursing from something abominable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The precise character of the prohibition is worth examining. The phrase {\i yonek sheket} suggests that the objection is not to the substance of human milk but to the {\i act} of nursing beyond a certain age. A nursing child beyond twenty-four months is described as nursing from something disgusting \u8212- the focus is on the act rather than the product. The {\i nafka mina} is significant: if a mother expresses her milk into a container and gives it to a child, would Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's objection apply? If the concern is the act of nursing, expressed milk would be unaffected; if the concern is the substance at that developmental stage, it would be equally problematic regardless of form of delivery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 4-5 Years Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua disagrees sharply: {\i afilu arba va-chamisha shanim} \u8212- even four or five years old, nursing remains permissible. The Gemara considers whether Rabbi Yehoshua means literally up to five years as an upper bound, or whether he means there is no practical limit whatsoever. The Gemara\u8217's treatment suggests he does intend some upper boundary around five years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua does, however, concede one point: {\i peiresh le-achar esrim ve-arba\u8217'ah chodesh ve-chazar} \u8212- if after the twenty-four-month mark the child ceased nursing for a period and then returned to it, then even Rabbi Yehoshua would agree this is {\i ke-yonek sheket}. Once the child has transitioned out of the nursing stage and entered the normal world of eating solid food, a return to nursing is considered inappropriate even according to the lenient view. How long a cessation constitutes {\i peiresh}? Rav Yehuda bar Chaviva cites Shmuel: three days of uninterrupted abstention from nursing suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Act vs Product\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As noted, the central distinction being drawn throughout this passage is between the {\i act} of nursing and the {\i product} \u8212- expressed milk. The discussion of {\i yonek sheket} appears to target the act. This distinction will be reinforced momentarily by the analogy to blood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kashrut Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now marshals a proof from {\i kashrut} reasoning. A {\i kal va-chomer} might suggest that human milk should be non-kosher: if the milk of a non-kosher animal is forbidden even though that animal does not convey {\i tumah} through touch while alive, then surely human milk should be forbidden, since humans {\i do} convey {\i tumah} when alive. The Torah, however, teaches from the verse concerning the camel \u8212- {\i et ha-gamal} \u8212- that the milk of non-kosher animals is forbidden, but through the same derivation, the verse specifies only non-kosher animals: {\i hu tamei} \u8212- {\i it} is {\i tamei}, implying that human milk, by contrast, {\i ve-ein chalav meha-mehalech al shtayim tamei ela tahor} \u8212- is not non-kosher but is in fact permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, one might think that human blood is forbidden: all animal blood is consistently forbidden, so human blood should be forbidden as well. The same verse teaches otherwise: the prohibition applies specifically to animal blood, not to human blood \u8212- {\i ve-ein dam meha-mehalech al shtayim tamei ela tahor}. Rashi and Tosafot work out the precise derivation from the verse, but the operative conclusion is clear: both blood and milk of human beings are not Biblically non-kosher. Rav Sheshet adds: {\i afilu mitzvat perishah ein bo} \u8212- there is not even a rabbinic prohibition against consuming human milk. It is entirely permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a sharp question: if human milk is entirely permitted, how can the Gemara speak of {\i yonek sheket} \u8212- as though nursing beyond a certain age involves something disgusting? The Gemara answers: {\i lo kashya \u8212- ha de-parish, ha de-lo parish} \u8212- there is no difficulty. The cases are categorically different. When the milk has been separated from the mother \u8212- expressed into a cup \u8212- there is no halachic concern and no element of impropriety. When the child nurses directly from the mother, the {\i act} of nursing is what is considered inappropriate beyond a certain developmental stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fascinatingly, the exact {\i converse} applies to blood. {\i Ve-chilufa be-dam} \u8212- the reverse principle governs blood. With blood, the concern runs in the opposite direction: if blood has been separated from the body and collected in a vessel, one may not drink it \u8212- not because human blood is itself non-kosher, but because an observer would assume the person is drinking animal blood and the appearance is impermissible. However, if a person\u8217's gums are bleeding and the blood remains {\i bein ha-shinayim} \u8212- between the teeth \u8212- or if one receives a cut and sucks the wound, {\i motzetzah ve-eino choshesh} \u8212- one may suck it out and need not be concerned. Here, an observer clearly understands that the person is ingesting blood from their own body, and there is no appearance of impropriety.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast is structurally elegant: expressed human milk is unproblematic; direct nursing beyond a certain age is considered inappropriate. Expressed human blood presents an appearance concern; blood sucked directly from one\u8217's own wound is entirely permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical question naturally arises in light of this analysis: human milk expressed and prepared as food \u8212- even as cheese \u8212- would be not only {\i kosher} but not even {\i milchig}. Tosafot also cites a {\i Torat Kohanim} to the effect that human flesh is likewise not Biblically forbidden, though there are significant discussions among {\i poskim} about whether it would be prohibited on other grounds, such as {\i bal tishaktzu}. The term {\i mehalechei shtayim} \u8212- two-legged beings \u8212- is used as a euphemism in these sources rather than specifying {\i adam} directly, possibly to avoid the discomfort of explicit discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef rules: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- the law follows Rabbi Yehoshua, permitting nursing up to approximately four to five years. This is the operative {\i halakha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shabbat Suckling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sick Suckling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Merinus taught: {\i gonei\u8217'ach yonek chalav be-Shabbos} \u8212- a person who is ill may directly suckle milk from an animal\u8217's udder on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shinui Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\i yonek mefarek ke-achar yad} \u8212- the act of suckling from the udder involves the forbidden labor of {\i mefarek}, a subdivision of {\i dash} \u8212- threshing \u8212- which means separating a substance from the medium in which it is bound. Normally one milks a cow with the hands; to suckle directly with the mouth is therefore a {\i shinui}, an unusual manner of performing the action, and constitutes {\i mefarek ke-lachar yad}. The Gemara adds: {\i u-vimkom tzara lo gazru rabbanan} \u8212- where there is genuine distress or pain, the rabbis did not extend their decree to prohibit even this modified form of the labor. The leniency is thus grounded in two factors: the {\i shinui} reduces the violation, and the {\i tzaar} of illness creates a threshold sufficient to permit a single Rabbinic-level prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this Gemara does not treat direct suckling from an animal as {\i yonek sheket}. An ill adult suckling from a cow\u8217's udder is a {\i Shabbat} question, not one of propriety. This further supports the inference that the impropriety of {\i yonek sheket} in the nursing context is specifically tied to something about the mother-child relationship beyond a certain developmental stage \u8212- perhaps related to the intimate character of nursing from a human being rather than the act of suckling per se.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother Expressing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This Gemara has critical implications for the question of a nursing mother expressing milk on {\i Shabbat}. Two preliminary issues arise. First, Tosafot notes that {\i dash} \u8212- from which the prohibition of milking derives \u8212- technically applies only to {\i gidulei karka}, things that grow from the ground. Human beings do not grow from the ground. Accordingly, Tosafot raises the possibility that expressing human milk may not be Biblically prohibited at all, but only Rabbinic \u8212- a position that, if accepted, significantly shifts the {\i Shabbat} analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, even setting aside that question, one might ask whether expressing milk by hand constitutes a {\i shinui} for a human mother. By a cow, the {\i normal} method of extraction from the human perspective is hand-milking; suckling directly is the deviation. For a human mother, the natural mode of the milk\u8217's release is through the infant nursing; expressing by hand might itself be considered the deviation, and therefore a {\i shinui} already. Or one might argue that either method is equally natural for a human mother, making neither a {\i shinui}. This is not easily resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the question of {\i shinui}, the {\i sakana} argument may apply: if failure to express milk threatens the mother\u8217's milk supply in a way that endangers the infant\u8217's nutrition, that {\i sakana} framework \u8212- which we have already seen the Gemara invoke prominently \u8212- would provide independent grounds for permitting expression. As a practical matter, an ideal solution is to attach an electric pump on a timer before {\i Shabbat} so that the device activates automatically, with the mother already connected; she then plays no active role in the expression itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drain Pipe Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case is brought from Nachum Ish Galia: if a drain pipe on a roof has filled with debris and rainwater is backing up into the house, one may use one\u8217's foot to compress and push through the blockage on {\i Shabbat} \u8212- {\i memachan be-raglo be-tzina be-Shabbos}. The reason is the same structure: using one\u8217's foot rather than one\u8217's hand is a {\i shinui}, and {\i u-vimkom psida lo gazru rabbanan} \u8212- in a case of financial loss, the rabbis did not extend their decree. Rav Yosef rules: {\i halacha ke-Nachum Ish Galia zu} \u8212- we rule in accordance with Nachum Ish Galia in this matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both of these rulings \u8212- the {\i gonei\u8217'ach} case and the drain pipe case \u8212- permit overriding a single Rabbinic prohibition on the basis of {\i tzaar} or {\i psida} combined with a {\i shinui}. This sits in some tension with other {\i sugyot} that appear to require a double Rabbinic qualifier before leniency applies, and the precise threshold of need that justifies which level of override remains a matter of ongoing discussion across the relevant {\i sugyot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nursing Post-Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Betrothal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents a new Baraita: {\i meneket she-met ba\u8217'alah be-tokh esrim ve-arba\u8217'ah chodesh} \u8212- a nursing mother whose husband dies within twenty-four months. Since the obligation to nurse is framed as a marital duty to the husband, one might think that with his death her obligation dissolves. Why, then, can she not simply hand the child to the heirs, declare herself free of responsibility, and move on?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Baraita\u8217's answer is that she may not: {\i harei zo lo t\u8217'ares ve-lo tinasei} \u8212- she may neither accept {\i kiddushin} nor enter {\i nissuin} for the duration of the twenty-four months. This reflects the concern that if she enters a new relationship, becomes pregnant, that pregnancy will compromise her milk supply and thereby harm the nursing child. Notably, the {\i Talmud} elsewhere acknowledges that active nursing itself typically functions as a natural contraceptive \u8212- it is generally understood that nursing suppresses fertility. Nevertheless, the {\i halakhic} framework addresses the reverse chain: not that nursing prevents pregnancy, but that a new pregnancy threatens nursing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milk Supply Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative concern is the integrity of the milk supply. Even if actual conception is unlikely during active nursing, entering {\i kiddushin} sets in motion a path that could eventually lead to pregnancy. The concern is precautionary: once betrothal is permitted, {\i nissuin} follows, and with {\i nissuin} pregnancy becomes a realistic risk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duration Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir holds that this restriction extends for the full twenty-four months. Rabbi Yehuda permits it after eighteen months, holding that the child\u8217's nursing needs diminish sufficiently by that point that the risk to the child no longer justifies continued restriction on the mother\u8217's freedom to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan bar Yosef notes that this is effectively the same debate as that between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel: Beit Shammai holds twenty-four months and Beit Hillel holds eighteen months. This is somewhat surprising given that Beit Shammai earlier maintained that a mother has no enforceable obligation to her husband to nurse at all \u8212- yet here Beit Shammai holds to the longer restriction vis-\u224 ?-vis the {\i child}. The reconciliation is that even Beit Shammai acknowledges an obligation where the infant recognizes the mother; in such circumstances, the twenty-four-month commitment to the child exists even without a living husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel offers a clarification \u8212- not so much a compromise between the positions as a reframing of each one. According to the view of twenty-four months, {\i muteret le-innasei be-esrim ve-echad chodesh} \u8212- she may actually get married at twenty-one months, because the first three months of a new pregnancy do not yet compromise the milk supply. {\i Lefi she-ein ha-chalav ne\u8217'echar ella achar shlosha chadashim} \u8212- the milk is not affected until after three months of pregnancy. So \u8220"twenty-four months\u8221" effectively means the mother may not get married more than three months before the twenty-four-month mark. Similarly, according to the view of eighteen months, she may get married at fifteen months, because again the first trimester does not impact nursing. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel is not adjudicating between the two positions but unpacking what each actually requires in practical terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraim Rulings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla rules: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- the law follows Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's eighteen-month position, though it is not entirely clear whether this means eighteen months as stated or fifteen months as reframed by Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel. Mar Ukva reports that Rabbi Chanina permitted him personally to marry a woman whose husband had died, once fifteen months had elapsed since the birth of the nursing child \u8212- confirming that the operative ruling is fifteen months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sharecropper Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's sharecropper came before Abaye with a case: his prospective wife was a nursing mother whose husband had died fifteen months earlier \u8212- may he now proceed with {\i kiddushin}? Abaye responded with confidence: the matter is completely clear. We rule like Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- eighteen months meaning fifteen. Beit Hillel, with whom we rule, also holds eighteen months meaning fifteen. Ulla ruled accordingly. And Rabbi Chanina permitted Mar Ukva\u8217's own case at fifteen months. With four independent convergent indications, and since this is {\i kiddushin} and not yet {\i nissuin}, the answer is obviously yes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Abaye reported this exchange to Rav Yosef, however, Rav Yosef corrected him sharply. Rav and Shmuel had both ruled: {\i tzricha le-hamtin arba\u8217'ah ve-esrim chodesh} \u8212- she must wait the full twenty-four months. Furthermore, the count does not even begin on the day of the birth \u8212- the clock starts the following day \u8212- and the day of the {\i kiddushin} itself does not count as the last day of the period. This is the most stringent possible reading, and it represents the ruling of Rav and Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye immediately ran after the sharecropper to stop the wedding \u8212- {\i ratz basei tlasa parsei}, three {\i parsangs}, and some say one {\i parsang} through sand \u8212- but could not catch him in time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's own conclusion from this episode is striking: {\i ha milsa de-amru rabbanan afilu ke-beitza be-kutcha} \u8212- even a question as obvious as whether one may eat an egg with a dairy dip \u8212- {\i lo lishri inish be-makom rabei} \u8212- one should not issue a ruling in the place of one\u8217's {\i rebbe}. The reason is not merely one of respect or deference, which would be waived if the {\i rebbe} grants permission. Rather, {\i mishum de-lo mistayeim milta} \u8212- such a ruling will not succeed, will not be confirmed by heavenly assistance. A student who presumes to rule in his {\i rebbe}\u8217's domain, even on an ostensibly clear matter, loses the {\i siyatta di-shemaya} that would otherwise guide him to the correct conclusion. In Abaye\u8217's own case: {\i deha ana hava gmirna leih la-hade de-Rav u-Shmuel} \u8212- I actually knew the ruling of Rav and Shmuel requiring twenty-four months. Yet even so, it slipped my mind at the critical moment. The impetuousness of confident self-sufficiency, even when the {\i rebbe} is present and available, is precisely what the episode illustrates.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 24-Month Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Baraita states: {\i natna bnah le-menekes} \u8212- if the mother gave the child over to a nursemaid, {\i o gemalto} \u8212- or weaned him, {\i o met} \u8212- or the child died, {\i mutteret le-hinnase miyad} \u8212- she may remarry immediately, within the twenty-four months. These are the three exceptions that remove the basis for the restriction: in each case, the infant is either being nursed by another or no longer nursing at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attempted Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa and Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua wished to rule in practice according to this Baraita. An elderly woman corrected them: this very case happened to me, and Rav Nachman forbade it even though I had satisfied one of these criteria. Rav Nachman did not permit me to remarry before the twenty-four months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resh Galuta Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: but did not Rav Nachman himself permit the women of the house of the {\i Resh Galuta} to remarry earlier, once they had weaned their children or made other arrangements? The Gemara answers: {\i shani bei Resh Galuta de-lo hadru behu} \u8212- the household of the {\i Resh Galuta} is different, because those women will not reverse themselves. Once a decision is made in the {\i Resh Galuta}\u8217's household \u8212- whether to give the child to a nursemaid or to wean \u8212- the new husband and the authority of that household ensure the decision will not be revisited. The fear that motivates the general restriction is that a woman who has weaned her child or arranged a nursemaid may have a change of heart, resume nursing, and then, having conceived with a new husband, find that the pregnancy compromises her milk. In the {\i Resh Galuta}\u8217's household, that reversal is simply not going to happen. Ordinary women are subject to the more stringent standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternity Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappi raises a further challenge. Rather than relying on the elderly woman\u8217's testimony, the ruling against the Baraita\u8217's leniency should have been derivable from an independent source. This leads to a discussion of the three-month waiting period before remarriage \u8212- a completely separate institution designed to prevent uncertainty about the paternity of a future child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3-Month Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Baraita lists women who must wait three months before remarrying even when there is no realistic concern that they are pregnant: a woman who is {\i redufah le-lech le-beit aviha} \u8212- always running to her father\u8217's house and presumably not having relations with her husband; a woman with severe marital discord in the home; a woman whose husband was imprisoned; a woman whose husband went overseas; a woman whose husband was elderly or infirm; and women who are clearly incapable of conception \u8212- sterile, elderly, {\i aylonit}, minor, or a woman who miscarried after her husband\u8217's death. In all these cases Rabbi Meir rules they must nonetheless wait three months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3-Month Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi permits immediate remarriage when there is no realistic concern of pregnancy. The ruling: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Meir bi-gzeirosuav} \u8212- we rule like Rabbi Meir in his categorical decree. We do not make case-by-case exceptions even when the underlying concern is clearly inapplicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consistent 24-Month\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws a structural parallel: just as we apply Rabbi Meir\u8217's categorical approach to the three-month waiting period and refuse to grant exceptions even where we know there is no pregnancy, the same categorical logic should govern the twenty-four-month restriction. Even if the child has been weaned or given to a nursemaid, we refuse to grant an exception. The {\i halakha} that emerges: {\i ve-hilkheta ve-halakha hi met} \u8212- the one case where we do permit remarriage within twenty-four months is where the child has died. {\i Gemaltathu} \u8212- if she weaned him \u8212- {\i asur}, she may not yet remarry, because we fear she may change her mind and resume nursing. All other exceptions fail for the same reason. Only the death of the child removes the concern entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Infanticide Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi goes further: {\i afilu met nami asur} \u8212- even if the child has died, she may not remarry within twenty-four months. The fear: {\i dilma katla lei} \u8212- we are afraid that a woman desperately eager to remarry might kill her infant child in order to clear the way. An actual case is cited in which a woman strangled her child \u8212- {\i hahi uvda ve-chanaktia} \u8212- apparently driven by the desire to be free to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this stringency: {\i ve-lo hi} \u8212- that is not the correct ruling. {\i Hahi shota havat} \u8212- that woman was mentally ill.\u160 ?{\i Ve-lo avdi inshi de-chanki be-naihu} \u8212- normal women do not kill their own children. The extreme case cannot generate a categorical rule. The operative {\i halakha} therefore stands: the one recognized exception to the twenty-four-month restriction is the death of the child. All other putative exceptions \u8212- nursemaid, weaning \u8212- do not permit early remarriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question concerns the three-month waiting period in the context of conversion: if a woman converts together with her husband, must they wait three months to continue living together, in order to clarify whether any child conceived around that time was conceived before or after her conversion? Some {\i poskim} have permitted pregnancy tests to resolve this question, arguing that modern diagnostic tools create a factual certainty that moves the case outside the framework of the Gemara\u8217's categorical decree \u8212- though others maintain that the {\i lo plug} logic of Rabbi Meir\u8217's position admits no exceptions regardless of what can be empirically verified today.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 62\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Onah & Domestic Duties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaseh Yadayim Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous discussion addressed {\i maaseh yadayim} and a wife\u8217's domestic duties. The Mishnah now turns to the husband\u8217's corresponding obligation: {\i onah}, the conjugal duty. The Mishnah had first dealt with a man who takes a vow not to have relations with his wife, giving him one or two weeks. It then addresses a list of circumstances in which a husband may be absent from home for extended periods without his wife\u8217's express permission \u8212- Torah students for a month, workers for a week. Even though a husband\u8217's {\i onah} obligation may be more frequent, occasional travel for business or to visit one\u8217's {\i rebbe} is permitted within certain limits. The Mishnah then enumerates the frequency of {\i onah} based on a man\u8217's profession, concluding with the phrase {\i divrei Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- a formulation that signals an implicit dispute, to which the Gemara will return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onah Frequency Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara at the opening of 62a \u8212- roughly ten lines down at the asterisk \u8212- takes up the {\i orcha d\u8217'milsa} passage from earlier: even where a husband has obtained his wife\u8217's permission for extended absence, what conduct is appropriate? This question is itself striking, because one might assume that legal permission settles the matter entirely. The Gemara insists otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Permission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i Rashi} on 62a is indispensable here. On the phrase {\i orcha d\u8217'milsa}, Rashi writes: {\i derech eretz shelo yisa alav cheit, v\u8217'afilu hu yachol l\u8217'pattosa shetitein lo reshut} \u8212- proper conduct demands that he not bear the burden of a sin, even if he is capable of persuading his wife to grant permission. There is permission and there is permission. The husband may be able to talk his wife into consenting to something she would not truly have chosen. Rashi\u8217's language \u8212- {\i lo yisa alav cheit} \u8212- is powerful: this is not merely a matter of sensitivity or etiquette, but carries the weight of sin. It is an important reminder that {\i halakha} and morality do not always fully overlap, and that legal entitlement does not exhaust one\u8217's moral responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmud Torah vs Home\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara had already quoted two opinions: Rav\u8217's view that a man may be away one month and home one month, and Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view that he may be away one month but must remain home two months. These positions are supported by respective {\i psukim}. What will emerge across today\u8217's {\i daf} is an ongoing reflection on the tension between a man\u8217's obligations at home \u8212- whether specifically {\i onah} or simply his presence and availability to his wife \u8212- and his obligation toward {\i talmud torah}. This tension has contemporary resonance wherever people are consumed by their work or their religious pursuits at the expense of their domestic responsibilities. In the Gemara\u8217's context, however, the competing obligations are both religiously freighted, which makes the tension sharper and more interesting. The dynamic will recur throughout the {\i daf} in different registers, with different voices landing differently on the question of where the balance lies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sighing Breaking the Body\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Half the Body\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Just as we had the dispute between Rav and Rabbi Yochanan regarding how long a man may be absent, we now encounter a parallel dispute between the same two authorities on a different matter. Rav states: {\i anachah shever chatzi gufo shel adam} \u8212- sighing breaks half of a person\u8217's body. The proof-text is from Yechezkel, where God instructs the prophet: {\i he\u8217'anach beshivron motnayim} \u8212- moan with the breaking of the loins. Since the verse specifies the loins \u8212- the middle of the body \u8212- Rav infers that sighing breaks only half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan: Full Body\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disagrees: {\i af kol gufo shel adam} \u8212- sighing breaks the entire body. His proof-text continues from the same prophetic context: {\i venamas kol lev, verafu kol yadayim, vechahatah kol ruach, vechol birkayim telechnah mayim} \u8212- all hearts melt, all hands weaken, all spirit dims, all knees dissolve into water. The imagery encompasses the whole person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses: how does Rabbi Yochanan handle the verse about the loins, which seems to specify only half the body? He answers: {\i hahi de\u8217'chi matchala mimotnayim matchala} \u8212- that is the starting point; the breakdown begins in the loins but spreads to destroy the whole body. And how does Rav handle the more encompassing verse? He says: {\i shani shemuat Beis HaMikdash detakifa tuva} \u8212- the destruction of the Temple is exceptional in its power to devastate. Ordinary sighing does not have that extreme effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Traveler Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers a story to illustrate the distinction between acute and habituated grief. A Jew and a non-Jew were walking together. The non-Jew could not keep pace with the Jew, who was walking quickly. The non-Jew then reminded his companion of the destruction of the Temple. The Jew sighed and slowed \u8212- yet even so, the non-Jew still could not keep up. The non-Jew said: didn\u8217't you just learn in {\i Gemara Kesuvos} that a sigh breaks half a person\u8217's body? The Jew replied: {\i hanei milei milta chadeta} \u8212- that is when the report is new, something freshly devastating. {\i Aval ha de\u8217'dashnan bah} \u8212- but this, which we have heard again and again, we have become inured to. People say: a woman who has suffered many miscarriages does not feel the same terror at the next one, because experience has numbed the initial shock. So too, the accumulated suffering visited upon Jews by non-Jews means that revisiting ancient tragedies no longer carries the same physiological impact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sighing & Absence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This parallel dispute between Rav and Rabbi Yochanan seems at first glance to be an independent literary unit, but there is a clear thematic connection to the earlier debate. Rav, who permits a man to be away from home for half the time, effectively says: your wife is sighing and distressed at home \u8212- {\i chatzi gufo}, she will survive it. Rabbi Yochanan, who insists the man be home two months out of three, says: no, {\i kol gufo shel adam} \u8212- the impact of your absence can be entirely destructive, and you cannot permit that. How one assesses the impact of one\u8217's absence on a spouse is inevitably filtered through prior assumptions about marriage and {\i talmud torah}. And the closing note of the story \u8212- once you are used to it, it is not so devastating \u8212- could plausibly be read as a Babylonian response to the critique of Eretz Yisrael: here in Bavel, our wives are used to it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 EY vs Bavel Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monogamy vs Polygamy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The divergence between Rav and Rabbi Yochanan connects to a broader and deeper dispute between Eretz Yisrael and Bavel \u8212- one that touches on the very nature of marriage. Eretz Yisrael was already a monogamous society; Bavel permitted polygamy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Intrinsic Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This institutional difference reflects a difference in how marriage itself was understood. In Eretz Yisrael, marriage appears to have been valued intrinsically. Statements like {\i kol adam she\u8217'eino shari belo isha shari belo tova} \u8212- anyone who dwells without a wife dwells without goodness \u8212- and the biblical {\i lo tov heyos ha\u8217'adam levado} reflect a sense that marriage is essential to human flourishing for its own sake. In Bavel, by contrast, marriage was understood more instrumentally: a man marries so that he has a legitimate outlet for his desires, which then frees him to devote himself to {\i talmud torah} without improper thoughts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Before/After To\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This difference manifests directly in the famous dispute in Kiddushin about whether a man should first marry and then learn {\i torah}, or first learn and then marry. In Bavel, the ruling is to marry first \u8212- once you have a wife, your desires are channeled appropriately, and you can go learn. In Eretz Yisrael, the ruling is to learn first, because once you are married \u8212- {\i reichaim betzavaro} \u8212- you have domestic responsibilities that you cannot simply set aside. You had better finish your learning before you take on those obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan EY Positi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is therefore entirely unsurprising that Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- the great Amora of Eretz Yisrael \u8212- insists on two months home and one month away, while Rav, working within the Babylonian framework, permits equal time. The sighing debate is not peripheral; it reflects the same underlying disagreement about what marriage demands and how seriously the husband\u8217's absence weighs on the wife. This is a real dispute between two civilizational orientations toward the relationship between marriage and {\i talmud torah}, not merely a technical disagreement about frequency.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Occupational Classificat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tayalim Daily\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states: {\i hatayalin bechol yom} \u8212- the {\i tayalin}, whoever they are, have a daily {\i mitzvat onah} when the wife is not in a state of {\i niddah}. There is an important background consideration here: in the era of the Gemara, {\i niddah} lasted approximately one week per month, whereas today it extends to nearly two weeks, which proportionally reduces the applicable {\i onah} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai tayalin}? Rava answers: {\i bnei pirkei} \u8212- those who come and study at a local {\i yeshiva} and return home each day. Because they are not worn out by physical labor and are at home each night, their {\i mitzvat onah} is daily, assuming the wife is interested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wives Torah Students\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye objects. He cites the verse {\i shav lachem mashkimei kum, me\u8217'acharei shevet, ochlei lechem ha\u8217'atzavim} \u8212- it is vain for you to rise early, to sit up late, to eat the bread of toil \u8212- and the continuation: {\i ken yitein liyidido shena} \u8212- God will provide for His beloved in sleep. Rav Yitzchak applies this to the wives of Torah scholars: {\i eilu neshoteihem shel talmidei chachamim shemenadedot shena me\u8217'eineihem} \u8212- these are the wives of Torah scholars who chase sleep from their eyes in this world and thereby merit the life of the world to come. These women are working through the night to support their husbands\u8217' learning. If the wives are losing sleep because of their domestic labors and the husbands are returning home only late at night, how can one describe the daily frequency as the norm? Abaye therefore concludes that the {\i tayalin} must refer to something else \u8212- specifically to people like Rav Shmuel bar Shilat, as Rav taught.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wealthy Independents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The correct referent for {\i tayalin} is the independently wealthy person who lives modestly off his own means. {\i Kegon Rav Shmuel bar Shilat de\u8217'achal midideih} \u8212- he eats and drinks from his own resources and sleeps in the shade of his own home, {\i velo chalif peristaka demalka avaveih} \u8212- and the king\u8217's tax collector does not pass his door, because he conducts himself inconspicuously and thus avoids the attention of the government. This person has no need to labor strenuously, no financial obligations to others, and no demands on his time and energy. For such a person, the {\i mitzvat onah} applies daily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravin on Pampered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Ravin came from Eretz Yisrael, he offered a parallel formulation: {\i kegon mefankei dema\u8217'arava} \u8212- like the pampered ones of the West, meaning Eretz Yisrael. The term {\i mefunack} suggests not spoiled in a pejorative sense, but independently sustained \u8212- wealthy individuals who do not work for a living, consistent with the category described above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bathhouse Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara provides two vivid anecdotes that illustrate what it means to be among the {\i mefankei dema\u8217'arava}. Rabbi Abbahu was in a bathhouse being supported by two servants \u8212- presumably because of his considerable physical bulk, which, as has been noted in scholarship on Babylonian imaginings of the sages of Eretz Yisrael, may reflect an orientalist projection of what wealthy and prominent figures looked like. The floor of the bathhouse collapsed beneath him, but he managed to catch himself on one of the supporting pillars \u8212- the kind visible at sites like Metzada, where the floor of the bathing area was suspended above the heated space below. Not only did he save himself, but he pulled both servants up as well. Similarly, Rabbi Yochanan was ascending steps, supported by Rav Ami and Rav Assi, when a step broke beneath him. He too pulled himself up and brought his attendants with him. When the rabbis asked why, if they were so strong, they needed to be supported in the first place, the reply was: {\i im ken ma ani mani\u8217'ach lezikna} \u8212- if I use my strength now, what will I preserve for old age?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These stories are presented in connection with the discussion of {\i mefankei dema\u8217'arava}, but they carry an additional resonance worth noting. Later in the {\i daf} we will encounter a story in which a roof collapses beneath a husband who has neglected his wife \u8212- a collapse that results in his death. Here, by contrast, things breaking underneath carries no such moral weight; the rabbis escape, even saving others. The contrast matters. The rabbis of Eretz Yisrael \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan, Rabbi Abbahu \u8212- are not the ones abandoning their wives. Their world is one of greater attentiveness to domestic obligation, which is precisely why the same image of collapse means something entirely different in their stories.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Workers & Onah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Workers Local vs Travel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states {\i v\u8217'hapolim shtayim bashabbat} \u8212- workers twice a week. But a {\i Braita} teaches {\i poel achas bashabbat} \u8212- once a week. Rabbi Yosi berabbi Chanina resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya kan be\u8217'osei melacha b\u8217'irono kan be\u8217'osei melacha b\u8217'ir acheres} \u8212- there is no difficulty; it depends on whether they are working in their own town or in a different town. Workers employed locally have more energy, return home each night, and thus fulfill {\i onah} twice weekly. Workers who commute to another town, even if they return the same day, are more worn out and are reduced to once a week. The {\i Braita} then confirms this distinction explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Donkey Drivers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hachamorim achas bashabbat} \u8212- donkey drivers are obligated once a week.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder & Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Week Neder Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Rav Chanan raises a question to Abaye. The Mishnah teaches that if a man takes a {\i neder} not to have relations with his wife, he must divorce her after one week according to Beis Hillel. But looking at the Mishnah\u8217's own classification of {\i onah} frequencies, very few men had an obligation more frequent than once a week \u8212- only the independently wealthy (daily) and local workers (twice weekly). Most men were obligated once a week or less. So if a man takes a {\i neder} for one week, what has he really withheld? For someone obligated once a week, he has missed exactly one {\i onah}. And for camel drivers or sailors, whose obligation falls far less frequently, a one-week {\i neder} withholds nothing at all. Why should such a {\i neder} compel divorce?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bread in Basket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye replies: {\i eino domeh mi sheyesh lo pas besalo l\u8217'mi she\u8217'ein lo pas besalo} \u8212- one who has bread in his basket is not comparable to one who does not. The operative factor is not the frequency of actual relations, but the wife\u8217's knowledge that her husband is {\i legally available} to her at any moment. When he is merely traveling for work or absent for legitimate reasons, there is no barrier in principle \u8212- he could return, the opportunity exists. But a {\i neder} creates an actual legal prohibition. Even for someone whose normal {\i onah} frequency is once every six months, the existence of a formal {\i neder} \u8212- which forecloses the possibility of intimacy \u8212- constitutes a sufficient rupture of the marital bond to warrant divorce after one week. The absence of the bread from the basket, not merely its infrequency, is what matters.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Job Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Occupation Switch\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Rav Chanan poses a further question to Abaye: {\i chamor vena\u8217'aseh gamal mai} \u8212- what if a donkey driver wishes to become a camel driver? Moving from donkey driving to camel driving means longer journeys, greater absence from home, and a corresponding reduction in {\i onah} frequency. May he make this change without his wife\u8217's permission?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Preference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i rotzah ishah be-kav ve-tiflut me-asarah kabin u-frishut} \u8212- a woman prefers one measure with {\i tiflut} over ten measures with abstinence. The word {\i tiflut} is somewhat loaded \u8212- it connotes something trivial or insubstantial, not a fully dignified expression of marital intimacy. The Gemara could have stated the principle in a more straightforward way: a man has a primary obligation to his wife and may not unilaterally reduce his presence. Instead, it frames the point through a somewhat unflattering characterization of women\u8217's preferences. The underlying {\i halakhic} conclusion is nonetheless clear: the husband may not unilaterally switch to an occupation that diminishes his availability to his wife without her consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rosh adds an important qualification to this principle: if the proposed change is not a different occupation but a commitment to {\i talmud torah}, the husband does not require his wife\u8217's permission. The obligation and value of {\i talmud torah} stands apart from the ordinary calculus of occupational change, and a husband is entitled to pursue it even if it increases his absence, without seeking her approval for that specific transition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sailors & Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sailors Six Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha-sapanim achat le-shishah chodashim \u8212- divrei Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- sailors, once every six months \u8212- this is the position of Rabbi Eliezer. Rav Bruna said in the name of Rav: {\i halakhah ke-Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- we rule in accordance with Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2-3 Year Absence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava said in the name of Rav: {\i zu divrei Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- this is the opinion of Rabbi Eliezer specifically. But the Sages say: {\i ha-talmidim yotzin le-Talmud Torah be\u8217' ve-gimel shanim shelo bi-reshut} \u8212- Torah students may go out for two or three years to study without express permission from their wives. This reveals that the debate implicit in the phrase {\i divrei Rabbi Eliezer} at the end of the Mishnah is not primarily about the general classification of occupational {\i onah} frequencies. It is specifically about how long a Torah student may be absent from home. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, as understood by the Gemara, is that the framework of the Mishnah applies \u8212- a month away without permission. The opposing Sages permit two or three years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted in passing that there is another possible axis of dispute with Rabbi Eliezer that the Gemara does not pursue here but which is developed elsewhere: how to conceptualize the fundamental nature of {\i onah} itself. Rabbi Eliezer understands {\i onah} as a calendar-based frequency. An alternative understanding \u8212- and there are {\i poskim} who hold this represents the {\i de-Oraita} obligation \u8212- is that {\i onah} is contextual and demand-based: a man is obligated to be available to his wife whenever she wishes. This reading may be reflected in Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position. But the Gemara here directs the debate toward the question of extended absence for {\i talmud torah}, and that is where it proceeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Students\u8217' Self-Reliance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava states: {\i samchu Rabanan ad\u8217'Rav Adda bar Ahava va\u8217'avdu uvda benafshayhu} \u8212- the rabbinic students relied on Rav Adda bar Ahava\u8217's articulation of the Sages\u8217' position and ruled for themselves accordingly, permitting themselves extended absence from home. Rashi presses the phrase {\i benafshayhu} \u8212- \u8220"with their own souls\u8221" \u8212- beyond the ordinary sense of ruling for oneself. He reads it as: they acted, but in doing so they were risking their own lives. The story that follows demonstrates exactly this.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Rechumi Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Risk Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ra\u8217'avad reads {\i va\u8217'avdu uvda benafshayhu} differently \u8212- not as risking one\u8217's life, but simply as ruling for themselves in a self-determined way. On the Ra\u8217'avad\u8217's reading, the position of the Sages permitting two to three years represents the operative {\i halakha}, and the students were entitled to rely on it. Rashi, by contrast, injects a note of moral caution: you may have the legal right, but you are playing with fire.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Annual Return Delayed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki ha de\u8217'Rav Rechumi} \u8212- like the story of Rav Rechumi. The name itself \u8212- {\i Rechumi}, cognate with {\i rachamim}, compassion \u8212- is resonant. He would regularly study with Rava in Mechuza. His practice was to return home once a year, on {\i Erev Yom Kippur}. One year, {\i yoma chad mashchateih shema\u8217'ta} \u8212- he became absorbed in a particularly engaging topic of learning and the learning stretched on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Tear Collapses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His wife was waiting at home, looking out repeatedly: {\i hashta ati, hashta ati} \u8212- now he will come, now he will come. He did not come. {\i Chalash da\u8217'ata} \u8212- she despaired. {\i Achit dima\u8217'ta me\u8217'eina} \u8212- a single tear fell from her eye. He was sitting on the roof. {\i Ifchit igra mituteih} \u8212- the roof collapsed beneath him, and {\i venach nafsheih} \u8212- he died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here again we have something collapsing beneath a rabbi \u8212- but this collapse is entirely different from the bathhouse stories above. There the collapse was incidental, almost comic, and the rabbis emerged stronger. Here a single tear causes the roof to give way and the scholar dies. The juxtaposition is stark, and the connection to the earlier discussion of sighing \u8212- {\i anacha shoveret gufo shel adam} \u8212- is unmistakable. Something did break; it was not the wife\u8217's body, but the roof, and the husband beneath it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'avad vs Rashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ra\u8217'avad, consistent with his more permissive reading, largely sets aside the cautionary implications of this story. He acknowledges that in principle the {\i halakha} permits two to three years of absence, but draws a practical lesson from the narrative: even if you are legally entitled to be away, make every effort to keep the one commitment you have made \u8212- come home on {\i Erev Yom Kippur} if that is what you promised. The Rif does not bring Rav Adda bar Ahava\u8217's ruling at all, holding that the {\i halakha} follows Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- which limits permissible unauthorized absence to one month, not two or three years. The Ramah rules like Rav Adda bar Ahava, based on the fact that the Gemara reports that the students {\i relied} on his ruling, treating it as the operative position. And the Ra\u8217'avad attempts to reintegrate both the legal entitlement and the moral responsibility: yes, we {\i pasken} like the Sages for two or three years \u8212- but let the story of the collapsing roof temper how you exercise that entitlement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Scholars Onah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Friday to Friday\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah discussed the {\i onah} for Torah scholars in terms of how long they may be absent without permission, but it does not specify the actual frequency of their {\i onah} obligation. The Gemara addresses this directly. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: {\i me\u8217'erev Shabbos l\u8217'erev Shabbos} \u8212- from Shabbat eve to Shabbat eve, meaning Friday night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oneg Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains: {\i shehu leil ta\u8217'anug u\u8217'shvita ve\u8217'hana\u8217'at haguf} \u8212- Friday night is a night of pleasure, rest, and bodily enjoyment, making it the appropriate and natural time for {\i onah} as part of {\i oneg Shabbos}. This is also significant in light of certain sectarian positions of the {\i Bayit Sheni} period that prohibited marital relations on Shabbat. The Gemara here affirms not only that Shabbat night is permitted, but that it is the designated {\i onah} time for Torah scholars. Rav Yehuda \u8212- and this is also cited in the names of Rav Huna and Rav Nachman \u8212- adds: {\i ze hamishamesh mitaso me\u8217'erev Shabbos l\u8217'erev Shabbos} \u8212- one who maintains relations on a weekly Friday-night cycle fulfills the verse {\i asher piryo yiten be\u8217'ito}, the fruit given in its proper time, which also implies that children conceived in this manner and at this time will be born well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehuda Bar Chiya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weekly Returns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yehuda the son of Rabbi Chiya \u8212- and son-in-law of Rabbi Yanai \u8212- would go and sit in the house of his {\i rebbe}. Every {\i Erev Shabbat}, at nightfall \u8212- as Tosafot notes, {\i bei shimshai} refers to the {\i bein hashmashos} of Friday evening \u8212- he would return home. And when he would return, {\i hava kachazi amuda d\u8217'nura} \u8212- a pillar of fire would be seen preceding him, marking his arrival.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missed Sign\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One Friday, {\i yoma chad mashcheih shema\u8217'ta} \u8212- he became absorbed in his learning and did not return. When the members of the household did not see the usual sign \u8212- the pillar of fire \u8212- Rabbi Yanai, his father-in-law, said to the household: {\i kifu mitasu} \u8212- overturn the bed, the sign of mourning and {\i aveilus}. His reasoning: {\i she\u8217'ilmalei Yehuda kayam, lo bital onaso} \u8212- if Yehuda were still alive, he would never have shirked his obligation and missed coming home. Therefore, his failure to appear could only mean that he had died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death from Word\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hava kishgaga sheyotzei milifnei hashalit} \u8212- it was like an error that goes out from the presence of a ruler, words spoken by an important person that take on the force of reality. And indeed, {\i venach nafsheih} \u8212- Rav Yehuda died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duty Message\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This story is striking in its severity. The wife is not described as weeping; there is no tear, no visible suffering. The mere fact that the husband failed to fulfill his {\i onah} obligation \u8212- that he was not home on {\i Erev Shabbat} as he always had been \u8212- was itself treated as proof of death and triggered a verbal decree that became reality. The message is unambiguous: the {\i onah} of Torah scholars is not optional or subject to casual revision, even for a single week. This stands in sharp contrast to the earlier stories about twelve-year absences, and the range of stories presented in this {\i daf} reflects genuinely different valuations of how far {\i talmud torah} may impinge on domestic responsibility.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Judah\u8217's Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bride Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now records that Rebbi \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi \u8212- arranged a match for his son with the family of Rabbi Chiya, to one of Rabbi Chiya\u8217's daughters. When they came to write the {\i kesuvah}, the bride died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yichus Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi was disturbed: {\i chas v\u8217'shalom, psulei ika} \u8212- is there perhaps some disqualification, some problem with this union, that caused it to be prevented? They sat and examined the lineages. Rebbi descended from Shefatya ben Avital \u8212- Avital being one of David\u8217's wives \u8212- establishing his descent from the royal house of David. Rabbi Chiya descended from Shimi, David\u8217's brother \u8212- an honorable lineage, but not the royal line itself. This mismatch of {\i yichus} was identified as the source of the problem, and the bride\u8217's death was understood as a providential intervention preventing an unsuitable match.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi then arranged a match for his son with the family of Rabbi Yossi ben Zimra.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 12 Years Pre-Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bride Walks Forward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The agreement reached with Rabbi Yossi ben Zimra\u8217's family was that Rebbi\u8217's son would be supported for twelve years of study in {\i yeshiva} before the marriage. This reflects the Eretz Yisrael approach: learn first, then marry, so that you are not compelled to abandon your new wife for the {\i beis medrash}. However, the {\i kallah}\u8217's family moved to accelerate matters. They had the bride walk before him \u8212- a pointed reminder of what was waiting for him \u8212- as if to ask: are you certain you want to wait?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marry Then Learn\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He responded: {\i nava shish shanin} \u8212- perhaps six years would suffice. They had her walk before him again. At that point he said, {\i ichnis v\u8217'hadar eizil} \u8212- I will marry now, and then go to learn. This represents a capitulation to the Babylonian model: marry first, then pursue {\i talmud torah}. He was embarrassed before his father, who had envisioned twelve years of unencumbered study.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His father reassured him: {\i beni, da\u8217'as koncha yesh becha} \u8212- my son, you think like your Creator. He drew an analogy from the {\i Shirat HaYam}: the verse first says {\i t\u8217'vi\u8217'eimo v\u8217'sita\u8217'eimo behar nachalatcha} \u8212- bring them and plant them on the mountain of Your inheritance \u8212- meaning the people would enter the land and only then build the Temple. Yet God was so eager that He immediately said {\i v\u8217'asu li mikdash v\u8217'shachanti b\u8217'tocham} \u8212- make Me a sanctuary and I will dwell among them \u8212- advancing the union. So too, the son should not feel that his choice was wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Return Wife Sterile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He went and studied for twelve years in {\i yeshiva}. When he returned \u8212- {\i ad d\u8217'ata itkar d\u8217'vattayhu} \u8212- his wife had become sterile. Apparently he had not been present to fulfill his marital obligations; the marriage, though contracted, had been effectively abandoned during those twelve years. He had sought the best of both models and wound up satisfying neither: he married, but then behaved as if he had not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav considered the options: {\i nigrisha} \u8212- should he divorce her and marry another woman to have children? But then people would say: {\i ani azo l\u8217'shav shimra} \u8212- this poor woman waited in vain for twelve years. Should he take a second wife to have children, as was possible in Babylonian practice? Then people would say: {\i zu ishto v\u8217'zu zonoso} \u8212- this is his wife and this is his mistress. Neither option was acceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prayer & Cure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So {\i ba\u8217'ei lei rachamei v\u8217'isis} \u8212- Rav prayed on her behalf, and she was healed. The problem was resolved through prayer. This reflects a distinctively Babylonian sensibility: yes, things went wrong, real harm was done \u8212- but the {\i Eibishter} can help, and solutions exist. The story does not minimize the consequences of neglecting domestic obligation, but neither does it leave the situation as irreparable tragedy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Chanina Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimeon Delay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Chachinai was preparing to travel to his {\i rebbe}\u8217's {\i yeshiva}. He was departing {\i b\u8217'shilhei hillula d\u8217'Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai} \u8212- at the conclusion of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai\u8217's wedding celebrations. Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai asked him to wait until the wedding week was over so they could travel to the {\i yeshiva} together. This request itself gestures toward the Eretz Yisrael sensibility: even one committed to {\i talmud torah} is expected to complete his week of marriage before departing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanina Proceeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina did not wait. He went immediately and sat in the {\i yeshiva} for twelve years. We hear nothing further of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai \u8212- presumably he spent more time at home before eventually departing, which the story implies was the appropriate path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost Roads\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After twelve years, Rabbi Chanina returned \u8212- but {\i ishtannu shivilei d\u8217'massa} \u8212- all the roads of the city had changed and he could not find his way home. He sat by the riverbank, disoriented, like someone waking after a very long absence from a world that had moved on without him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hears Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He overheard people calling out to a young woman: {\i bas Chachinai, bas Chachinai, malli kulskha v\u8217'sa nizil} \u8212- daughter of Chachinai, fill your basket and let\u8217's go. He said: I can infer from this that this girl is ours \u8212- she is my daughter. He followed her home.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Dies Shock\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His wife was sitting inside, working with flour. She lifted her eyes, saw her husband who had been gone for twelve years \u8212- and {\i savyei libba} \u8212- her heart seized. {\i Parach rucha} \u8212- her spirit departed. She died from the shock of his sudden reappearance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prayer Revives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He cried out: {\i Ribbono Shel Olam, aniya zo, zeh secharah} \u8212- Master of the Universe, is this her reward? He prayed for her, and she was revived. And here an important observation must be made: whereas in the story of the Rav Rechumi the husband died as a consequence of his neglect, in this story of Rabbi Chanina ben Chachinai it is the wife who suffers \u8212- and even then, she is revived through prayer. The husband does not bear the ultimate consequence. The asymmetry is significant: even in a story that is ostensibly cautionary, the man who abandons his wife for twelve years is not the one who pays with his life.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Chama Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Advance Notice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chama bar Bissa went and studied in the {\i beis medrash} for twelve years. When it was time to return, he said to himself: {\i lo e\u8217'evid ke-Rabbi Chanina ben Chachinai} \u8212- I will not do what Rabbi Chanina ben Chachinai did. He would not simply appear at home after twelve years and risk killing his wife with shock. Instead, he settled in the {\i yeshiva} at the edge of his hometown and sent word to his household that he was returning, giving his wife time to prepare herself emotionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His son Rabbi Oshaya came to the local {\i beis medrash} \u8212- not knowing, of course, that the scholar who had arrived was his father \u8212- and began studying with him. Rabbi Chama was immediately struck by the young man\u8217's sharpness and depth. {\i Chalash da\u8217'ateih} \u8212- he despaired and grew despondent, thinking: {\i i havai hacha, havai li zara ki hai} \u8212- had I stayed at home, I could have raised and educated a son as sharp as this. Of course, it was his son. He came into his home, and his son followed him inside \u8212- Rabbi Chama still did not recognize him, and rose to honor the distinguished visitor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Bound Cord\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His wife said to him: {\i mi ika abba d\u8217'kai mikmei bera} \u8212- is there a father who stands up for his own son? At that moment he realized who the young man was. He cited the verse about him: {\i hachut hameshulash lo bimehera yinatek} \u8212- a three-ply cord is not easily broken \u8212- for here was a chain of Torah scholarship: Rav Bissa, his father Rabbi Chama bar Bissa, and now his son Rabbi Oshaya, each a great {\i talmid chacham}. The Torah had passed through the generations despite \u8212- or perhaps alongside \u8212- the extended absences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The message of this story, like the one before it, is genuinely mixed. There is a poignancy to the moment: Rabbi Chama did not know his own son. He had missed twelve years of his son\u8217's development and formation. Yet the ending does not dwell on that loss. The {\i hachut hameshulash} passage frames the outcome triumphantly. The Torah continuity is intact, the son is remarkable, and the family is reunited. The Gemara is not pretending there are no costs to twelve years of absence \u8212- the estrangement, the strangeness of not recognizing one\u8217's own child \u8212- but the overall arc of these stories, told through a Babylonian lens, tends toward resolution and even vindication.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now come to the most famous story of the {\i daf}, which extends the twelve-year framework to twenty-four. Rabbi Akiva was the shepherd of Kalba Savua, one of the wealthiest men in Jerusalem. Kalba Savua\u8217's daughter perceived that he was {\i tznia u-mali} \u8212- modest and of fine character. She said to him: if I agree to marry you, will you go and study in {\i yeshiva}? He agreed. {\i Ikdashalah betzina} \u8212- he married her in secret. {\i Ve-shadrateih} \u8212- she sent him off to learn. When the father heard, he expelled her from his home and took a vow forbidding her from deriving any benefit from his considerable wealth, furious that his daughter had married this ignoramus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First 12 Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva went and learned in {\i yeshiva} for twelve years. When he was about to return, he overheard an old man speaking to his wife: {\i ad kama kamitbartat almanut chayut} \u8212- how long will you live the life of a living widow? She replied: {\i i ledidi tzaias, yativ treisar shani achrinei} \u8212- if my husband would listen to me, he should stay there another twelve years. At that, Rabbi Akiva said: {\i bi-reshut ka-avadna} \u8212- I am acting with her permission. And he turned back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This moment is worth pausing over. Tosafot notes that this marks a significant shift in the Gemara\u8217's overall arc. Near the beginning of the {\i daf}, the concern was precisely that a husband might pressure his wife into granting permission she did not truly wish to give. Here, the wife is the one actively urging the husband to continue learning. The difference between the stories is not only one of different legal opinions or cultural frameworks, but also of different types of wives and different types of marriages \u8212- ones in which the wife is a genuine, enthusiastic partner in the enterprise of {\i talmud torah}, not merely a reluctant or resigned participant. Many {\i kollel} wives today inhabit exactly this kind of partnership \u8212- though how much is intrinsic and how much is shaped by the surrounding culture is a question the Gemara does not answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Return with Students\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva returned after another twelve years \u8212- twenty-four in total \u8212- bringing with him twenty-four thousand students. His wife heard and came out to greet him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Knows Soul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Her neighbors urged her: {\i shili mani levushei ve-ichasi} \u8212- borrow some fine clothes and dress properly to greet your husband. She replied: {\i yodea tzaddik nefesh behemto} \u8212- the righteous man knows the soul of his animal. She was describing herself through this verse, which carries a somewhat discordant note \u8212- she speaks of herself as a beast, in a very self-effacing way, though one recalls that her name was Rachel, which is itself the word for ewe. The language is striking in its degree of submission, whatever the underlying intention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Partnership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When she reached him and fell at his feet \u8212- {\i nafla al apa, ka-menashka} \u8212- kissing his feet, his attendant moved to push her away, as it was unseemly. Rabbi Akiva stopped him: {\i shabkuna, sheli ve-shelachem shelah hu} \u8212- leave her; everything that is mine and yours is hers. The partnership he articulates is complete. All the Torah, all the students, all the achievement \u8212- she shares in it equally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Reconciles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Kalba Savua heard that a great {\i talmid chacham} had arrived in the city and went to seek an annulment of the vow he had made years earlier forbidding his daughter from his wealth. He did not yet know that this great scholar was his son-in-law. Rabbi Akiva asked him: when you made that vow, would you have made it even if you had known that she would marry a great Torah scholar? Kalba Savua replied: {\i afilu perek echad afilu halacha achas} \u8212- even if he had known one chapter, one {\i halacha} of Torah, I would never have made the vow. Rabbi Akiva then revealed: {\i ana hu} \u8212- I am the man. Kalba Savua fell on his face, kissed his feet \u8212- mirroring his daughter\u8217's gesture \u8212- and gave him half his wealth. The story that began with exile from wealth ends with its restoration, and more. Twelve plus twelve years of {\i talmud torah}, a wife\u8217's genuine consent, and the outcome is students, wealth, and reconciliation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter & Ben Azai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Barteih de-Rabbi Akiva avdah lei le-Ben Azai hachi} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva\u8217's daughter made the same arrangement with Ben Azai, who is elsewhere known as one whose {\i toraso umnasao} \u8212- his Torah was his entire occupation, such that he famously did not marry. Here his daughter of Rabbi Akiva made a similar pact with him. {\i Ve-hainu d\u8217'amrei inshi, racheila basar racheila azla} \u8212- that is what people say: one sheep follows another. The name {\i Rachel} is the word for ewe, and Rabbi Akiva\u8217's wife was named Rachel. {\i Ke-ovdei ima kach ovdei barta} \u8212- as the mother did, so the daughter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yosef Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Early Return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef the son of Rava was sent by his father to learn in the {\i yeshiva} of Rav Yosef. They agreed on six years \u8212- not twelve. After three years, it was {\i Erev Yom Kippur}, and he thought to return home for a visit before completing his six years. He got up and came home early.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Attacks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His father heard that his son was returning before the agreed six years were up. He took a weapon \u8212- {\i shakol mana} \u8212- and went out to confront him. He said: {\i zonachta nizkarta} \u8212- you remembered your prostitute? The implication: you were so consumed by your desire to see your wife that you broke your commitment to learning. An alternative version: {\i yonachta nizkarta} \u8212- you remembered your dove \u8212- which is less harsh but still dismissive, still insisting the son should not have returned early.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yom Kippur Fast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the confrontation that ensued, {\i itrid} \u8212- they became so agitated and absorbed that {\i lo mar ifsach ve-lo mar ifsach} \u8212- neither the father nor the son managed to eat their {\i seudah mafsekes}, the final meal before the {\i Yom Kippur} fast. The {\i Yom Kippur} connection recurs pointedly throughout the {\i daf}: Rav Rechumi came home once a year on {\i Erev Yom Kippur} and died when he failed to do so; Yehuda bar Rabbi Chiya came home every {\i Erev Shabbat}; here the son is coming home after three years and is met with anger for returning too early.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concluding Message\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} ends with this confrontation. And the implicit final message is one that the {\i yeshivot} in Eretz Yisrael sometimes invoke \u8212- but which the Gemara itself uses to draw a different conclusion. The stories of Rabbi Akiva involve twelve years and then another twelve years. Those in the {\i yeshiva} world who cite such stories to justify extended unbroken absence sometimes present twelve-plus-twelve as equivalent to twenty-four. But they are not the same. Rabbi Akiva {\i returned} after the first twelve years \u8212- he came home, heard his wife\u8217's voice urging him to go back, and only then went back. He did not simply disappear for twenty-four years without contact. The final story of Rav Yosef\u8217's son makes the same point in reverse: a son who tries to come home after three of six years is prevented from doing so \u8212- but a six-year agreement is a six-year agreement, not a twelve-year one. The structures and the commitments matter, and the Gemara\u8217's rich and sometimes contradictory collection of stories does not resolve easily into a single ruling, but it leaves the attentive reader with a sense that both poles \u8212- the obligations of {\i talmud torah} and the obligations of marriage \u8212- demand to be taken with full seriousness, and that collapsing one into the other comes at a cost.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 63\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moredet Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onah to Moredet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf begins towards the bottom of 63a, where the Mishnah transitions from the topic of {\i onah} \u8212- the husband\u8217's obligation to have marital relations at regular intervals \u8212- into the subject of the {\i moredet}, a wife who is not living up to her responsibilities in the marriage. The earlier Mishnah had framed {\i onah} in terms of frequency, and the preceding Gemara had explored the tension between {\i talmud Torah} and a man\u8217's obligations at home. Now the Mishnah turns to examine the range of mutual responsibilities between husband and wife, and what happens when one party fails to meet them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The term {\i moredet} \u8212- \u8220"one who rebels\u8221" \u8212- is the central concept here, along with its less common counterpart, {\i mored}, referring to a husband who similarly fails his obligations. This passage carries enormous practical weight, both for questions of {\i agunah} and for broader questions about how rabbinic courts should approach a marriage that is no longer functioning as it should.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Penalties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states: {\i hamoredet al ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a woman who rebels against her husband \u8212- {\i pofsin lach mi-ktuvatah}, we reduce the value of her {\i ketubah}. The mechanism is a progressive monetary fine: for each week that she remains in a state of rebellion, the total value of her {\i ketubah} is reduced. The {\i ketubah}\u8217's total value encompasses the base amount \u8212- 100 or 200 {\i zuz} \u8212- plus whatever {\i tosefet} the husband added, plus the value of the dowry she brought into the marriage. All of this is slowly diminished on a weekly basis, so that even if she subsequently reconciles herself to her obligations, she will be unable to collect the full value of the {\i ketubah} upon divorce or the husband\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah specifies the rate of reduction: {\i shiva dinarim b\u8217'shabbos}, seven {\i dinar} per week. Rabbi Yehudah says the rate is seven {\i tarpeikin}. The Gemara will clarify the precise monetary values involved and what they represent relative to the 200 {\i zuz} base amount. As to how long this process continues: {\i ad keneged ktuvatah}, until the total {\i ketubah} is reduced to zero. Rabbi Yosei disagrees and says {\i le\u8217'olam hu pofes v\u8217'holech} \u8212- one continues to reduce even into negative territory, so that she actually incurs a debt to her husband. The practical significance of this is that {\i she\u8217'im tipol lah yerusha mimakom acher} \u8212- if she ever comes into money from another source \u8212- {\i goveh mimena}, he can collect from her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mored Reciprocal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then turns to the reciprocal case: {\i v\u8217'chein hamored al ishto}, a husband who rebels against his wife. Here the remedy runs in the opposite direction: {\i mosifin al kesuvatah}, we increase the amount of her {\i ketubah}. The practical impact, however, is similarly deferred \u8212- it is only felt when the {\i ketubah} becomes payable. The rate of increase is {\i sheloshah dinarim b\u8217'shabbos}, three {\i dinar} per week \u8212- less than half the rate applied to the wife. Rabbi Yehudah sets his rate at three {\i tarpeikin}. Why the disparity between the fine imposed on her and the one imposed on him? The Gemara will address this, but the asymmetry is already striking.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sex vs.\u160 ?Labor Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna: Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the Gemara addresses the monetary mechanics, it raises a prior question: {\i moredet mimah} \u8212- in what domain is the wife rebelling? Rav Huna says: {\i mi-tashmish hamitah} \u8212- she is refusing sexual relations. This is noteworthy, because the earlier Mishnah never explicitly stated that she has an obligation to have sex with her husband; it was simply assumed. If the Mishnah already detailed his obligation to have sex with her at regular intervals, it implicitly took her reciprocal obligation for granted. What she is now refusing, on Rav Huna\u8217's reading, is precisely that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose bar Chanina: Lab\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Chanina says: {\i mim\u8217'lachah} \u8212- she is refusing to perform her domestic duties, the very obligations detailed at length in the preceding Mishnah, such as cooking, baking, and laundry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an important question: why not simply say that she can be a {\i moredet} with respect to either responsibility? The answer, Tosafot suggests, may lie in a position encountered earlier: {\i yacholah ishah shetomar l\u8217'baalah eini nizonas v\u8217'eini osah} \u8212- a woman is entitled to say to her husband, \u8220"I will not be supported by you, and I will not perform domestic labor for you.\u8221" If that is her right, then refusing her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} is not a rebellion at all \u8212- she is simply exercising a recognized option. On this reading, Rav Huna holds that the only thing she can genuinely rebel against is sex, since refusing {\i melacha} while waiving {\i mezonot} is a legitimate choice, not a transgression. Rabbi Yose bar Chanina, by contrast, holds that this option belongs to the husband, not the wife \u8212- it is his right to say he will not feed her and she need not work, but she cannot unilaterally declare that arrangement. Even if she offers to waive her {\i mezonot}, she is still obligated to perform her domestic duties, and refusing them constitutes a genuine {\i moredet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mored Parallel Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If Sex: Parallel Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks how the reciprocal case \u8212- the {\i mored} husband \u8212- fits into each position. {\i Bishlama l\u8217'man d\u8217'amar mi-tashmish} \u8212- if we are speaking about sex refusal, the parallel case is straightforward: the husband is refusing to have sex with his wife. That makes perfect sense as a mirror image.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If Labor: Parallel Suppo\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela l\u8217'man d\u8217'amar mimelocha} \u8212- if the {\i moredet} is one who refuses domestic labor, what is the parallel for the husband? He has no comparable domestic labor obligation to her. The Gemara answers: {\i be-omer eini zan v\u8217'eini mefarnes} \u8212- the case is one where he says he will refuse to feed her and support her. What does she receive in exchange for her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}? His {\i mezonot}. So if she rebels by withholding her labor, the parallel is his withholding her sustenance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Immediate Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara cites Rav: {\i ha-omer eini zan v\u8217'eini mefarnes yotzi v\u8217'yitein kesuvah} \u8212- a husband who says he will refuse to provide food and support must immediately divorce his wife and pay the {\i ketubah}. If that is so, there would seem to be no room for a drawn-out process of weekly increases to the {\i ketubah} \u8212- the obligation is immediate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds: {\i lo l\u8217'mimluchi beih bayinan} \u8212- we still need to discuss the matter with him. Even if the goal is for him to give a {\i get}, the practical process of bringing that about may take some weeks. During that period, while the beit din is working toward compelling the divorce, the {\i ketubah} amount continues to increase. If he changes his behavior in the interim, all the better, but the orientation is not to invest a year or two hoping he will reform \u8212- it is to move toward the {\i get} expeditiously, while leaving a short window during which his behavior might change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question \u8212- how quickly a rabbinic court should move to dissolve a troubled marriage versus how long it should work to preserve it \u8212- is not merely an academic one. It maps directly onto contemporary disputes about the proper role of {\i batei din} when one party is clearly not meeting their obligations. Too often, courts invoke the rubric of {\i shalom bayit} to delay resolution in situations that are plainly untenable, when a more direct reading of the Gemara would counsel moving promptly toward the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also ask what exactly is meant by {\i eini zan v\u8217'eini mefarnes}. Rashi understands it to mean the husband is refusing to pay the food bills. Tosafot raises the question of whether it might also mean he refuses to go out and earn a living at all. Tosafot cites Rabbeinu Eliyahu, who argues that a husband is indeed obligated to hire himself out \u8212- even to work as a schoolteacher, which was considered a low-prestige occupation \u8212- in order to provide his wife\u8217's {\i mezonot}: {\i mikan mochi\u8217'ach Rabbeinu Eliyahu shechayav adam l\u8217'haskir atzmo u\u8217'l\u8217'lamed tinokos o la\u8217'asos melacha acheres kedei lazon es ishto}. Rashi, however, understands the obligation only as paying the bills from whatever resources he has. Tosafot then cites Rabbeinu Tam, who limits the work obligation to farming one\u8217's own land \u8212- {\i avodas hakarka}, {\i eflach} as in plowing and tending fields \u8212- but stops short of requiring a man to hire himself out to someone else: {\i aval l\u8217'haskir atzmo eino chayav}. The distinction reflects a society in which working one\u8217's own land and hiring oneself out for labor were understood as categorically different in terms of dignity and social standing. The underlying question \u8212- whether a wife can demand that her husband stop being a deadbeat and go find employment \u8212- is very much embedded in the language of the {\i ketubah} contract itself, with its phrase {\i v\u8217'ana eflach}, \u8220"and I will work.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbis\u8217' Enactment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four-Week Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a later ruling by the Sages. {\i Raboseinu chazru v\u8217'nimnu} \u8212- the rabbis reconsidered and voted \u8212- to replace the gradual week-by-week reduction of the {\i ketubah} with a different mechanism: {\i machrizin aleiha arba shabbatot zu achar zu}, they make public announcements for four consecutive weeks. At the end of that process, the woman loses the entire {\i ketubah} at once, however large it may be. The beit din sends her a formal notification: {\i hevei yoda\u8217'at she\u8217'afilu kesuvatech me\u8217'ah maneh hafsadta} \u8212- know that even if your {\i ketubah} is worth a hundred {\i maneh}, you have forfeited it. The \u1489 ?\u1512 ?\u1497 ?\u1497 ?\u1514 ?\u1488 ?, {\i braita}, quoted here applies this ruling to a {\i moredet} who is an {\i arusah}, a {\i nesua}, even a {\i niddah}, even one who is ill, and even a {\i shomeret yavam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This shift from the Mishnah\u8217's gradual process to the four-week announcement reflects a decisional judgment \u8212- whether strategic, role-based, or diagnostic \u8212- that a drawn-out progressive fine is not the right tool. Whether the reasoning is that the rabbis do not see marriage counseling as their function, or that they believe a sufficiently severe and time-limited ultimatum is more effective, or that a marriage which has reached this point is unlikely to recover in any case, the result is a binary choice: change within four weeks, or lose everything and the {\i get} follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The announcements are made in public \u8212- {\i ein machrizin aleha ella bebatei knesiyot uvebatei midrashot} \u8212- in synagogues and study halls, plainly using social pressure and public shame as part of the mechanism. One notes the asymmetry: it is not clear from the sources that equivalent public announcements are made when the husband is the {\i mored}. As Rav Ameimar bar Chama notes, the choice of synagogues is deliberate: since the announcements are to be made on four consecutive {\i Shabbatot}, and {\i Shabbos} is when the public is gathered in those spaces, the two facts cohere \u8212- the four {\i shabbatot} of the {\i braita} are actual Sabbaths, not merely weeks, precisely because that is when the communities assemble.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When to Engage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A dispute arises regarding when during this four-week process the beit din should directly engage the woman. Rav Ami bar Chama teaches that the beit din sends her two notifications: {\i achas kodem hachraza v\u8217'achas achar hachraza} \u8212- one before the announcement process begins, and one after it concludes. This represents a middle position: the court is not merely making passive public announcements and waiting; it actively reaches out to her at both ends of the process. Rav Nachman bar Rav Chisda publicly declared that the {\i halacha} follows {\i raboseinu} \u8212- which, on Tosafot\u8217's reading, means we reach out only at the end, not at the beginning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects to Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling, calling it {\i burcha} \u8212- foolish. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak responds that the ruling was not original to Rav Nachman bar Rav Chisda but was transmitted from Rabbi Yose bar Chanina, a figure of considerable standing. The underlying dispute is then clarified through a further citation: {\i Rava amar Rav Sheshet, halacha nimlachim bah} \u8212- the {\i halacha} is that we do engage her, that we actively deliberate with her during the process. {\i Rav Huna bar Yehuda amar Rav Sheshet, halacha ein nimlachim bah} \u8212- no, we do not engage her at all; we make the public announcements, give her fair warning through those announcements, and then the {\i get} is given. There is no direct conversation with her from the beit din.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is three distinct positions along a spectrum: the Mishnah\u8217's drawn-out progressive fine over potentially many months or years; the four-week process with active engagement from the beit din at the start; and the four-week process with no direct engagement at all \u8212- announcements only, followed by loss of {\i ketubah} and {\i get}. Each reflects a different understanding of the court\u8217's role and its assessment of whether behavioral change in such cases is achievable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'is Alai Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimark Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the various procedural approaches to the {\i moredet}, the Gemara now introduces a critical distinction made by Ameimar. The cases discussed so far all involve a woman who is refusing relations or domestic labor but who has not said that the marriage itself is intolerable \u8212- she is using refusal as leverage, whether out of anger or some marital dispute. Ameimar describes this as {\i de-amra be\u8217'ina lei u-metza\u8217'arna lei} \u8212- she is saying, in effect, \u8220"I do want to be with him, but I am making him suffer.\u8221" In such a case, there is reason to believe that her behavior can change \u8212- she is angry, she has not abandoned the marriage in principle, and therefore the processes described above make sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet vs.\u160 ?Mar Zutra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar distinguishes this from the case of {\i ma\u8217'is alai} \u8212- where the woman says she finds her husband repulsive and will never be able to live with him again. In this case, {\i lo kaifinan lah} \u8212- we do not employ financial penalties to try to force a change in her behavior. Mar Zutra, however, disagrees: {\i amar Mar Zutra, kaifinan lah} \u8212- even in the case of {\i ma\u8217'is alai}, we impose financial penalties and attempt to compel her to continue the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to appreciate how disturbing Mar Zutra\u8217's position is. We are speaking of a woman who has declared that she finds her husband completely intolerable, that she cannot live with him, that she will never have sexual relations with him again. Mar Zutra\u8217's position is that we should apply financial pressure to coerce her back into the marriage \u8212- and into the marital bed \u8212- simply because sufficient financial threat might break her refusal. Even granting the most charitable interpretation \u8212- that Mar Zutra believes her attitude, not merely her behavior, might genuinely change \u8212- the posture of compelling a woman to have sexual relations with a man she explicitly finds repulsive is profoundly troubling. The language of Rashi\u8217's understanding of {\i lo kaifinan lah} is telling: {\i lo kaifinan lah lish\u8217'osei ela notnin lah get ve-yotzei be-lo ketubah} \u8212- we do not force her to remain with him; rather, the {\i get} is given and she leaves without her {\i ketubah}. That is the position the Gemara ultimately accepts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records that Mar Zutra actually acted on his ruling: {\i hava uvda ve-achfa Mar Zutra} \u8212- there was a case and Mar Zutra compelled the woman to remain with her husband even in the case of {\i ma\u8217'is alai}. The outcome was that {\i ve-nafik minei Rabbi Chanina mi-Sura} \u8212- the great Rabbi Chanina from Sura was born from her having been compelled to continue that marriage. The implication is that perhaps we should always force couples to remain together in such cases, since great things can emerge from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately dismisses this: {\i ve-lav hi, hatam siyata di-shmaya hava} \u8212- that cannot be used as proof; what happened there was a specially ordained divine intervention. The Gemara\u8217's rejection is striking in its reasoning. Whether one reads a case as evidence for a general rule or as a miraculous exception depends entirely on one\u8217's prior orientation. If one begins from the premise that we should keep marriages together at all costs, the story of Rabbi Chanina from Sura looks like compelling evidence. If one begins from the premise that a woman who says {\i ma\u8217'is alai} is describing an intolerable situation, the same story is read as an inexplicable exception from which no normative conclusions may be drawn. The Gemara chooses the latter reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Implications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more consequential question that the {\i ma\u8217'is alai} discussion opens up is not merely whether we impose financial penalties on the woman, but whether, once beit din declares {\i lo kaifinan lah}, we also compel the husband to give a {\i get}. The Gemara itself does not address this directly. The situation it describes \u8212- we will not try to force her to change, you are entitled to divorce her without paying the {\i ketubah} \u8212- leaves open the scenario in which the husband responds: I do not want to divorce her. I am content to remain married even without sexual relations. I know she will lose her {\i ketubah} if I eventually choose to give the {\i get}, but I choose not to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this gap directly. One opinion holds that {\i kofin oto lehotzi} \u8212- we compel him to give the {\i get}. Rabbeinu Tam rejects this: {\i denichos shema einah nassan b\u8217'acherin} \u8212- perhaps she is only claiming {\i ma\u8217'is alai} because she has her eye on someone else. Allowing her declaration alone to trigger compelled divorce would fundamentally shift the balance of marital power, enabling a woman to exit any marriage she chose simply by declaring it intolerable, forfeiting the {\i ketubah} but gaining freedom. Rabbeinu Tam views this as an unacceptable result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam, however, rules that if a woman says {\i ma\u8217'is alai}, we do compel the husband to give a {\i get}. The Rambam employs the formulation drawn from the Gemara: {\i ein adam dar bekhfifah im hanachash} \u8212- one cannot be forced to live under the same roof as a serpent. If a person finds their partner genuinely impossible to live with, beit din does not force cohabitation. On the Rambam\u8217's ruling, the woman\u8217's declaration of {\i ma\u8217'is alai} therefore generates a compellable obligation on the husband to divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rishonei Ashkenaz}, led by Rabbeinu Tam, disagree, and in Ashkenazic practice the Rambam\u8217's position has not been accepted. The practical consequences are enormous: the rejection of the Rambam\u8217's ruling is one of the pillars of the contemporary {\i agunah} crisis. If a woman could say {\i ma\u8217'is alai} and thereby trigger a beit din\u8217's compulsion of the {\i get}, the {\i agunah} problem \u8212- in which a husband refuses indefinitely to give a {\i get} \u8212- would be substantially resolved. The Ashkenazic response invokes {\i shalom bayit} and the concern of {\i shema natna eineha b\u8217'acher}, that she may simply want out of the marriage for her own reasons, as grounds for withholding that power from the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some historians have argued that the Rambam\u8217's ruling was partly shaped by the social reality he faced: women in his milieu were converting to Islam in order to escape intolerable marriages, since the Muslim legal system did not recognize their prior marriage and allowed them to remarry. Faced with women resorting to apostasy as the only practical exit, the Rambam may have been additionally motivated to establish a legitimate halakhic solution. If a beit din would actually compel the {\i get} upon a declaration of {\i ma\u8217'is alai}, conversion would be unnecessary. The observation connects the medieval context to a perennial question about rabbinic jurisprudence: how much does the practical urgency of the situation on the ground inform the willingness to employ halakhic tools that are formally available but rarely used?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is clear from the Gemara itself is that the entire range of questions \u8212- how long to work with the {\i moredet}, how quickly to move toward {\i get}, whether to engage her directly or only through announcements, and what to do when she declares the marriage intolerable \u8212- reflects a deep and unresolved tension between the court\u8217's protective role, its interest in preserving marriages, and its responsibility to the individuals trapped in untenable situations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Loss Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clothes & Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a practical question arising from the case of Rav Zevid\u8217's daughter-in-law, who rebelled against her husband and was in possession of a garment at the time. Ameimar, Mar Zutra, and Rav Ashi were convened as a beit din, with Rav Gamda sitting among them. The question before them: when a {\i moredet} loses her entire {\i ketubah}, does that include the worn garments that are in her possession \u8212- {\i belosei ha-kayamim}, literally the existing worn clothes? On one version of the text, they ruled that she does lose even those. On an alternate version, they ruled she does not lose the worn clothes in her closet. In both versions, Rav Gamda challenges the panel, suggesting in the first that they are ruling too harshly against her out of deference to the eminence of Rav Zevid, and in the second that they are ruling too leniently in his favor for the same reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying question \u8212- whether the worn clothes in her closet are part of the {\i ketubah} settlement she forfeits \u8212- was one that Rava raised but did not resolve. The practical consequence of that unresolved question: {\i tafsa lo mafkinan minah} \u8212- if she has those clothes in her possession, we do not take them from her, since she may be entitled to them; {\i lo tafsa lo yahavinan lah} \u8212- if she does not have them in hand, we do not give them to her. The default is determined by the status quo of physical possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The background to this dispute involves the nature of the dowry clothes. Rashi explains that these are garments she brought into the marriage as part of her trousseau, the value of which was recorded in the {\i ketubah}. He takes possession of them upon marriage, but she is entitled to their residual value upon dissolution. If she loses the entire {\i ketubah}, she loses that claim as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Timelines\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further variant ruling is recorded: {\i umashinan lah tresar yarchei shata agita} \u8212- we actually extend the process for twelve months rather than four weeks. During those twelve months, however, {\i leis lah mezoni miba\u8217'al} \u8212- she receives no {\i mezonot} from her husband. This represents yet another point on the spectrum of possible approaches: a longer time horizon to allow for change, but with an additional financial disincentive layered on, removing her food support for the duration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Talmud Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, at the beginning of 63b, records a significant post-Talmudic development. Rabbeinu Yehudah found in the writings of Rav Sherira Gaon that the Geonim enacted {\i shetekenu beyamav latet get le\u8217'ishah la\u8217'altar} \u8212- they ruled in his time to give the {\i get} to the woman immediately, without waiting four weeks or twelve months. This mirrors what was said earlier about the husband who is {\i mored} \u8212- immediate {\i get} without extended process. Tosafot further records that the {\i Rabbanan Savorai} \u8212- the transitional authorities between the Talmudic period and the Geonic era \u8212- enacted that we also reclaim whatever clothing she had taken from her closet and give her the {\i get} at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The comparison raises an interesting question about which approach is ultimately more favorable to the woman. The drawn-out process of the Mishnah or the four-week Talmudic process, especially if she is engaged at the beginning, gives her time to reconsider and an opportunity to preserve the marriage if she chooses. The immediate {\i get} of the Geonic enactment is faster, but it also means she exits with nothing, no {\i ketubah}, no clothes, no transitional period. The four-week process with beit din engagement \u8212- particularly where she is reached out to at the start \u8212- may in fact represent the most balanced of the available approaches.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arusah & Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusah vs.\u160 ?Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Tuvi bar Kisna, citing Shmuel, rules: {\i kotevin iggeret mered al arusah ve-ein kotevin iggeret mered al shomeret yavam} \u8212- we write a writ of rebellion against an {\i arusah} but not against a {\i shomeret yavam}. The reasoning is that an {\i arusah}, even though she is not yet in a stage where she would be obligated to have sexual relations, has made a declaration that she will refuse to do so \u8212- or refuse to proceed to {\i nisuim} \u8212- when the time comes, and this is sufficient to trigger the process. A {\i shomeret yavam}, by contrast, is not yet in a stage where these obligations apply, and we therefore do not initiate the process against her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braitah Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a challenge: the {\i braita} cited earlier specifically listed the {\i shomeret yavam} as one of the cases to which the {\i moredet} process applies \u8212- {\i achat le-arusah u-le-nesuah va-afilu niddah va-afilu cholah va-afilu shomeret yavam}. That seems to contradict Shmuel\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Demands?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya, kan she-tava hu, kan she-tav\u8217'ah hi} \u8212- there is no difficulty; the cases differ based on who is making the demand. When the man is the one asserting his right and the woman refuses, that is the case of the {\i braita} \u8212- we write the writ against the {\i shomeret yavam}. When she is the one demanding and he is refusing, that is Shmuel\u8217's case \u8212- we do not write the writ on her behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is confirmed by Rav Tachlifa bar Avimi citing Shmuel: {\i tava hu, nizkakim lo} \u8212- if he demands and she refuses, beit din attends to him and applies financial pressure on her; {\i tav\u8217'ah hi, ein nizkakim lo} \u8212- if she demands and he refuses, we do not attend to her demand. The Gemara\u8217's adjustment of Shmuel\u8217's formulation \u8212- reading {\i le-arusah} in the sense of the {\i braita} being written for her rather than against her \u8212- resolves the textual difficulty while preserving the substantive ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum vs.\u160 ?Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Yibum Compulsion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the question of who is making the demand determines whether beit din intervenes, the Gemara turns to the broader question of {\i yibum} versus {\i chalitzah}. The Gemara asks: why do we not compel a {\i shomeret yavam} to accept {\i yibum} when the {\i yavam} demands it? The answer: {\i de-amrinan lah, zil lo mifkadet} \u8212- we tell her, go, you are not commanded in {\i peru u-revu}. He has a {\i mitzvah} she does not share, and therefore his claim to compel her cooperation is stronger than hers would be in the reverse situation. She, for her part, can simply allow the relationship to remain unresolved or proceed to {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if a woman cannot compel {\i yibum}, can she at least compel {\i chalitzah} when she demands it and he refuses? Here the Gemara introduces a striking argument: {\i de-amra, ba\u8217'ina chutra leyada u-mara likvura} \u8212- she says, \u8220"I want a staff for my hand and a shovel for my grave,\u8221" meaning she wants children to care for her in old age and to bury her when she dies. She does not need to be commanded in {\i peru u-revu} to have a legitimate claim to children. This gives her standing to demand resolution of her situation \u8212- whether through {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- even without the formal obligation that applies to him. The Gemara affirms that this same claim applies to the {\i shomeret yavam}: {\i hachi nami shomeret yavam de-ba machmas ta\u8217'ana} \u8212- when she comes with a legitimate claim, we do attend to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Preference Sh\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a crucial historical distinction, cited from Rav Pada in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i tava lachalotz nizkakim lo} \u8212- if the {\i yavam} demands {\i chalitzah} and she refuses, beit din compels her; {\i tava le-yabbem, ein nizkakim lo} \u8212- if he demands {\i yibum} and she refuses, beit din does not compel her. The reason for preferring {\i chalitzah} and not compelling {\i yibum} is given through the teaching of the Mishnah: {\i mitzvas yibum kodemes lemitzvas chalitzah} \u8212- originally, {\i yibum} took precedence over {\i chalitzah}, because {\i be-rishona she-hayu miskavnim leshum mitzvah} \u8212- in earlier times, {\i yibum} was performed for the sake of the {\i mitzvah} itself. {\i Achshav she-ein miskavnim leshum mitzvah, amru mitzvas chalitzah kodemes} \u8212- now that it is no longer performed with that sincere motivation, we rule that {\i chalitzah} takes precedence. And since we favor {\i chalitzah}, when he demands {\i yibum} against her will, we do not compel her compliance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enforcement Measures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara addresses the apparent inconsistency: if she can refuse {\i yibum} and he is told to go marry someone else, why can she not similarly refuse {\i chalitzah} and he be told the same thing? He responds: {\i kivan de-agida bi, lo ka yahvi li achriti} \u8212- since she is bound to him, no one will agree to marry him; the unresolved status of the {\i shomeret yavam} effectively bars him from remarriage. The same argument should apply whether she refuses {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}. The Gemara ultimately resolves this by distinguishing between the earlier and later Mishnah positions on which is preferred: under the initial ruling, {\i yibum} was favored and we would have compelled her; under the later ruling, we prefer {\i chalitzah} and therefore we do not compel {\i yibum} even when he demands it, while we do compel {\i chalitzah} when he demands it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i Tosafot} of the sugya draws the parallel explicitly: just as the discussion of {\i ma\u8217'is alai} raised the question of what measures beit din may use to compel a husband to give a {\i get} when the wife declares the marriage intolerable, the analogous question here is what measures may be used to compel a {\i yavam} to give {\i chalitzah} when the woman demands it and he refuses \u8212- or worse, when he refuses both {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah} and simply leaves her in limbo. The woman is then precisely in the position of an {\i agunah}: bound to him, unable to remarry, with no resolution in sight. The tools available to the court in both scenarios \u8212- and the willingness to deploy them \u8212- remain very much an open and contested question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 64\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Overview & Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin near the bottom of 64a, picking up five lines from the end at the words {\i ad masai hu pocheit} \u8212- \u8220"until when does he reduce it.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Troubles Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preceding {\i sugya} dealt with the situation in which a marriage has broken down and one of the partners is failing to fulfill his or her obligations. The discussion centered on whether the goal is to achieve compliance over time or to move directly toward a {\i get}, and what is done in the interim. Two significant issues emerged from that discussion. First, whether the husband can actually be compelled to go out and find work \u8212- he has an obligation to pay the bills, but does the principle of {\i v\u8217'ana aflach} mean he can be forced to seek employment? Second, and more consequential for {\i agunah} concerns, when the wife declares the marriage intolerable, does the court move immediately to compel a {\i get}, or does the woman at once forfeit her {\i ketubbah}? The {\i sugya} also touched on {\i Tosafot}\u8217's discussion of whether a husband who refuses {\i chalitzah} can in some fashion be compelled or induced to grant it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Financial Penalties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} established financial penalties for marital defiance in either direction. When the wife is a {\i moredes} \u8212- refusing to fulfill her obligations \u8212- the {\i ketubbah} is reduced by seven {\i dinarin} per week. When the husband is a {\i mored}, the {\i ketubbah} is increased by three {\i dinarin} per week for the wife\u8217's benefit. A parallel opinion substitutes {\i tarpeikin} for {\i dinarin} in both amounts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Currency & Math\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dinar/Zuz/Sela\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand these figures, one must first establish the currency equivalencies. A {\i dinar} equals a {\i zuz}, and four {\i zuz} equal a {\i sela}. The significance of the {\i sela} here is that the {\i Torah}\u8217's {\i mohar} of fifty {\i shekel} equals 200 {\i zuz} \u8212- which is why the base {\i ketubbah} for a {\i betulah} is 200 {\i zuz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Depletion Calculations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i ketubbah} stands at 200 {\i zuz} and is reduced at seven {\i zuz} per week, it will be exhausted in roughly 28 weeks. If instead it is increased for the husband at three {\i zuz} per week, that three-{\i zuz} increment would take approximately 67 weeks to accumulate an equivalent sum \u8212- though of course a {\i ketubbah} typically includes {\i tosefet}, a dowry, and other additions beyond the base 200.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i mai tarpeikin} \u8212- what are {\i tarpeikin}? Rav Sheshet answers: an {\i astira}, which is {\i palga d\u8217'zuza} \u8212- half a {\i zuz}. This is confirmed by a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Yehudah says three {\i tarpeikin} equal nine {\i ma\u8217'in}, which works out to a {\i ma\u8217'ah} and a half per day (excluding Shabbat). The practical consequence is significant: seven {\i tarpeikin} per week equals three and a half {\i zuz}, so the second opinion in the {\i Mishnah} represents a rate half as severe as the first. At three and a half {\i zuz} per week, the base 200-{\i zuz ketubbah} would be depleted in roughly 56 weeks \u8212- twice as long as under the first opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tarpeikin & Meals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarpeikin Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the coinage: a {\i tarpeikin} is an {\i astira}, which equals half a {\i zuz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah specifies that three {\i tarpeikin} equal nine {\i ma\u8217'in}, yielding a daily rate of a {\i ma\u8217'ah} and a half when Shabbat is excluded from the count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Penalty Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Setting aside the {\i tarpeikin}-versus-{\i zuz} question, the structural differences between the two penalties can be stated clearly. For the {\i moredes} \u8212- the wife who refuses her obligations \u8212- the {\i ketubbah} is reduced by one unit per day, including Shabbat, for a total of seven per week. For the {\i mored} \u8212- the husband who refuses his \u8212- the {\i ketubbah} is increased by only half a unit per day, and Shabbat is not counted, yielding three units per week. These two asymmetries \u8212- full versus half, and Shabbat included versus excluded \u8212- are the focus of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's next inquiry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mored vs Moredes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half vs Full Fine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiyya bar Yosef poses the question to Shmuel: {\i mah bein mored l\u8217'moredes} \u8212- why is there a difference between the {\i mored} and the {\i moredes}? Why is her penalty double his, and why is Shabbat counted against her but not against him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Fining Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} addresses the Shabbat question first. When the wife is the defiant party and the {\i ketubbah} is being reduced, the fact that Shabbat is included does not create an appearance of payment for Shabbat labor \u8212- {\i lo michzi k\u8217'schar Shabbat} \u8212- because one is merely reducing a debt, not paying out wages. When the husband is the defiant party and the {\i ketubbah} is being increased, however, including Shabbat in the count would mean that the wife is effectively earning half a {\i zuz} on Shabbat \u8212- {\i michzi k\u8217'schar Shabbat} \u8212- which does create that problematic appearance. Hence Shabbat is excluded from his calculation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question was raised about {\i Yom Tov}: if there is concern about the appearance of {\i schar Shabbat}, why is {\i Yom Tov} not similarly excluded? {\i Tosafot} raises the related question of {\i havla\u8217'ah} \u8212- the practice of including Shabbat payment within a broader weekly wage so that it is not singled out \u8212- and whether that principle might apply here. The matter is left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prostitute Market Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the question of why the wife\u8217's penalty is double the husband\u8217's, Shmuel tells Rabbi Chiyya bar Yosef: {\i tzei u\u8217'lemad mi-shuk shel zonos} \u8212- go and learn from the marketplace of prostitutes, {\i mi socher es mi} \u8212- who hires whom? It is men who hire women. The market for female prostitution is vastly larger than that for male prostitution. This is taken as empirical evidence that men experience greater sexual desire, and therefore the husband is the more aggrieved party when conjugal relations are withheld. Since the {\i mored} and {\i moredes} framework is understood primarily in terms of withholding sexual relations, the wife who withholds is penalized more heavily, because she is inflicting greater harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biological Arguments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second argument follows: {\i davar acher} \u8212- {\i zeh yitzro mi-bachutz v\u8217'zeh yitzrah mi-bifnim} \u8212- one\u8217's genitalia is external, the other\u8217's internal. Rashi explains this in two ways. Symbolically, it reflects who harbors the greater innate desire. More concretely, {\i kishuyo nikar u-misganeh}: the husband\u8217's arousal is visible and thus more conspicuous in its frustration, whereas the wife\u8217's is not. Together, these two arguments \u8212- the evidence from the marketplace and the biological argument \u8212- establish that the husband is regarded as the more injured party when sexual relations are denied, which is why the penalty for the {\i moredes} is double that of the {\i mored}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It bears noting that even though the husband has a positive obligation of {\i onah}, he is nonetheless regarded as the party more harmed by deprivation. The overall asymmetry \u8212- double penalty, plus Shabbat \u8212- compounds a broader imbalance already present in the framework, since the husband retains the unilateral power to initiate a {\i get}, which the wife does not. As for the timeline: whether one follows the seven-{\i zuz} or seven-{\i tarpeikin} rate, the process of depleting the base {\i ketubbah} runs between roughly half a year and a full year \u8212- far longer than the more rapid resolution the {\i Gemara} elsewhere envisions, where one wants to move quickly toward the {\i get} rather than drag out the proceedings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Food Minimums\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamshrah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} now turns to the counterpart of the {\i moredes} and {\i mored} discussion: the husband\u8217's affirmative obligation to support his wife. The particular case treated here is one of {\i hamshrah} \u8212- the husband is providing for his wife through an intermediary, meaning they are not sharing a domicile. Whether he is away on a business trip, or they are separated pending resolution of their situation, or for whatever other reason, she is not in his house and he is having someone pay her food costs. The {\i Mishnah} therefore specifies the minimum she must receive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Allowance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He may not give her less than two {\i kav} of wheat per week, or, if she prefers barley, four {\i kav} of barley. The {\i kav} is a volumetric measurement; barley was considered a less desirable grain, which is why it requires double the quantity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Barley Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi objects: the only authority who said barley is permitted as an alternative was Rabbi Yishmael, who lived near Edom \u8212- {\i she-hayah samuch le-Edom}. The {\i Gemara} clarifies that Rabbi Yosi\u8217's objection is not that barley is never eaten elsewhere, but specifically that the rule requiring double the quantity of barley compared to wheat was particular to Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's region. The Edomite barley was of notoriously poor quality \u8212- {\i se\u8217'oreihen Adomiyot ra\u8217'ot hen} \u8212- so twice the wheat measure was required to produce equivalent sustenance. Elsewhere, the ratio between barley and wheat would differ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legumes & Fruits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In addition to the grain, the husband must provide a half-{\i kav} of {\i kitnis} \u8212- legumes \u8212- weekly, along with a half-{\i log} of oil (a {\i log} is four {\i revi\u8217'iyot}, so a half-{\i log} is approximately seven ounces), and a {\i kav} of {\i grogeros} \u8212- dried figs \u8212- or, alternatively, a {\i maneh} (a weight measure) of pressed dates, {\i deveilah}. If none of these fruits are locally available, he substitutes an equivalent measure of whatever fruit is at hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Durables\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the weekly consumables, the {\i Mishnah} enumerates durable goods: a bed, a {\i mapatz} (mat), and a {\i machatzeles} (a form of cushion or mat). These items are presumably provided once at the outset rather than weekly. He also gives her a head-covering ({\i kippah le-roshah}), a belt for her waist ({\i chagor le-mosneha}), and shoes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clothing Allowance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shoes are replaced {\i mi-mo\u8217'ed le-mo\u8217'ed} \u8212- once each {\i regel}, three times a year. For clothing, he provides fifty {\i zuz} worth annually. There is, however, an important specification about timing: new clothing \u8212- which is heavier and warmer, not yet worn thin \u8212- should not be purchased in summer, and worn, thinned clothing should not be given in winter. Rather, the fifty {\i zuz} of new clothing should be bought in winter. She wears them throughout the cold months and, as they wear out, the thinned garments serve her well in summer. At the end of the year, when new clothing is purchased, she keeps the old worn garments \u8212- {\i ha-shechakim shelah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pin Money & Meals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She also receives a {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef} each week \u8212- a small personal stipend for incidental needs, what we might call pin money. And even when they are living apart, the husband is obligated to eat at least one meal together with his wife every Friday night: {\i ochelet immo mi-leilei Shabbat le-leilei Shabbat}. This is striking. The law already mandates {\i onah} \u8212- conjugal relations at regular intervals \u8212- and here extends the same logic to the shared meal: even when circumstances have separated them, the weekly Friday-night meal together is enforceable. The Friday night Shabbat table functions as a minimum unit of shared domestic life, a requirement of presence and not merely of financial provision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the husband fails to give the {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef} stipend, then {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha shelah} \u8212- whatever work she produces belongs to her rather than to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aseh Yadayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then asks: {\i u-mah hi osah lo} \u8212- and what work does she do for him? The {\i Gemara} earlier debated whether {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} refers to the entirety of her domestic labor or only the surplus she earns beyond her basic obligations, {\i motar ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}. The plain sense of the {\i Mishnah} here, however, seems to single out a specific, quantifiable category of work \u8212- the weaving that she can actually earn income from outside the house. This is distinguished from the standard domestic duties (baking, laundry, cooking, etc.) enumerated earlier. Whether the {\i Gemara}\u8217's two opinions on this question fully account for this distinction is not spelled out there; nonetheless, the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's framing points toward the income-generating weaving as the primary content of {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} in the strict sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weaving Amounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific amounts are given by region. She must produce the equivalent of five {\i sla\u8217'im} of warp \u8212- {\i sheti} \u8212- as measured in Judah, which equals ten {\i sla\u8217'im} by Galilean weights (since Galilean weights were half the value of Judean ones). Alternatively, she must produce ten {\i sla\u8217'im} of woof \u8212- {\i erev} \u8212- by Judean measure, which equals twenty {\i sla\u8217'im} in Galilee. The woof is the easier of the two to prepare, which is why its required quantity is double.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing Adjustments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she is nursing, her required work output is reduced \u8212- {\i pochtin lah mi-ma\u8217'aseh yadeha} \u8212- both because nursing itself constitutes significant labor and, more importantly, because excessive physical exertion could compromise her capacity to nurse well. Correspondingly, her food allowance is increased \u8212- {\i mosifin lah al mezonoteha} \u8212- since she is eating for two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Adjustments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of these minimums apply specifically to the poor \u8212- {\i ba-ani she-be-Yisrael}. If the husband is of higher social standing, then {\i hakol le-fi kvodo} \u8212- everything is calibrated to his status. The minimums are a floor, not a universal standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meal Measures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eruv Techumin Reference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now questions which Tanna authored this {\i Mishnah}, and concludes it can be neither Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka nor Rabbi Shimon. The basis for this analysis is a {\i Mishnah} in {\i eruv techumin}: the minimum {\i eruv} contribution is the amount of food constituting two meals \u8212- {\i mazon shtei se\u8217'udot} \u8212- per person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir vs Yehudah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir says the reference meal is a weekday meal, not a Shabbat meal; Rabbi Yehudah says it is the Shabbat meal, not the weekday meal. The {\i Gemara} notes that each is being lenient \u8212- {\i zeh ve-zeh mitkavnim le-hakel} \u8212- but in opposite directions. Rabbi Meir holds that one eats more bread on Shabbat, so the weekday meal represents a smaller bread portion. Rabbi Yehudah holds that on Shabbat one eats more varied dishes and consequently less bread, so the Shabbat bread portion is smaller. Both are defining two meals by the smaller of the two bread quantities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan b. Broka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka defines the size of a two-meal loaf as the bread purchased for a {\i pundiyon} when four {\i se\u8217'ah} of grain are sold for a {\i sela}. Working through the arithmetic: a {\i sela} equals 48 {\i pundiyon}, and a {\i se\u8217'ah} equals six {\i kav}, so four {\i se\u8217'ah} equal 24 {\i kav}. This means 24 {\i kav} cost 48 {\i pundiyon}, which is one {\i kav} per two {\i pundiyon}, or half a {\i kav} per {\i pundiyon}. A loaf costing one {\i pundiyon} is therefore made from half a {\i kav} of wheat, and that loaf constitutes two meals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon takes a different measure: two-thirds of a loaf \u8212- {\i shtei yadot be-kikar} \u8212- when three loaves are made from one {\i kav}. If three loaves come from one {\i kav}, each loaf represents a third of a {\i kav}. Two-thirds of a loaf is therefore two-thirds of one-third of a {\i kav}, which equals two-ninths of a {\i kav} for two meals. This is considerably less than Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka\u8217's half-{\i kav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achilat Pras\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Half of a two-meal loaf \u8212- that is, one meal\u8217's worth of bread \u8212- is called a {\i pras}, from the Aramaic for \u8220"half.\u8221" Hence {\i kedei achilat pras} denotes the amount of time required to eat one meal\u8217's worth of bread, i.e., the time it takes to eat half a two-meal loaf. This becomes the standard measurement in a much broader range of halachic contexts. The {\i Gemara} notes related applications: {\i chatzi ha-pras} is the amount one must remain in a house afflicted with {\i tzara\u8217'at} to contract {\i tum\u8217'ah}; half of that is the amount of {\i tamei} food whose consumption renders one {\i tamei}; and half again \u8212- one-eighth of the full loaf \u8212- is {\i lekabbel tum\u8217'at ochlin}, the minimum food quantity susceptible to {\i tum\u8217'ah}, which Rashi identifies as a {\i beitzah}. These applications are noted but not pursued at length here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Kav Calculation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eight vs Eighteen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now applies these two opinions to the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's allowance of two {\i kav} of wheat. According to Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka, half a {\i kav} yields two meals, so two {\i kav} yield eight meals. According to Rabbi Shimon, two-ninths of a {\i kav} yield two meals, meaning one {\i kav} yields nine meals, and two {\i kav} yield eighteen meals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weekly Meal Mismatch\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Neither figure matches the expected weekly meal count. A week contains fourteen meals \u8212- two per day, seven days \u8212- or fifteen if one counts {\i seudah shlishit} on Shabbat. Eight is far too few; eighteen is too many. This is the {\i Gemara}\u8217's problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baker\u8217's Profit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda resolves the discrepancy for Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka\u8217's position by pointing out that the latter was not specifying the actual flour content of the loaf but rather its market price. The storekeeper-baker does not convert the entire purchase price into flour; he keeps a portion as profit. Rav Chisda says to subtract a third for the storekeeper \u8212- {\i tzei me-hen shelish la-chanvani} \u8212- meaning that only two-thirds of the money paid goes toward the wheat itself, while the baker pockets a third. Conversely, if one starts with the raw wheat \u8212- as the {\i Mishnah} does when it gives the wife two {\i kav} \u8212- one must add back the profit margin to calculate how many meals the raw grain represents. Adding a third to the eight meals derived from the market-price calculation yields twelve meals, not eight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adjusted Totals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Twelve meals is still not fourteen. The {\i Gemara} resolves this by noting that the {\i Mishnah} specifies the wife eats one meal together with her husband on Friday night \u8212- {\i ochelet immo la-leil Shabbat} \u8212- bringing the total to thirteen. The {\i Gemara} then reconsiders: some interpret {\i achila} in the {\i Mishnah} not as literal eating but as a euphemism for {\i tashmish}, conjugal relations. If so, that Friday night provision adds no meal, and even with the literal interpretation one only reaches thirteen. The {\i Gemara} therefore adopts Rav Chisda\u8217's second formulation: the baker\u8217's profit is not a third but a half. Half of the purchase price goes to the wheat, half is the baker\u8217's profit. Applying the reverse: two {\i kav} of raw wheat, with a 100% markup factored in, yields sixteen meals. Sixteen exceeds fourteen \u8212- but now a different problem arises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Meals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: is this calculation aligned with Rabbi Chidka, who holds that one is obligated to eat four meals on Shabbat rather than three? With four Shabbat meals, the week contains sixteen meals total, and sixteen matches perfectly. The {\i Gemara} replies that one can maintain the Rabbinic view of three Shabbat meals as well: the extra portion \u8212- one meal\u8217's worth of bread \u8212- is set aside {\i le-orchei u-farchei}, for guests who might arrive unexpectedly. Hence the two {\i kav} covers fourteen regular meals plus one for Shabbat\u8217's Friday night meal together (whether literal or euphemistic) plus one extra for hospitality, reaching sixteen. Returning then to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's eighteen: under the Rabbinic view of three Shabbat meals (fifteen total), three portions are reserved for guests; under Rabbi Chidka\u8217's four Shabbat meals (sixteen total), two portions go toward hospitality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Missing Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Protein/Veg\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One striking feature of this minimum allowance is what it omits. The wife receives grain (wheat or barley), legumes, oil, and dried fruit or dates. What is entirely absent is protein \u8212- no meat, no fish, no eggs, no milk, no cheese. Equally absent are vegetables. This is characterized explicitly as the minimum for the poor \u8212- {\i ani she-be-Yisrael} \u8212- yet even granting that qualifier, the diet described is extremely bare: essentially starch supplemented by a small quantity of fruit. The {\i Gemara} is not troubled by the absence of protein or vegetables; as we will see, what the {\i Gemara} focuses on instead is the absence of wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Wine Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's list of provisions includes no wine, and the {\i Gemara} takes this as support for the position of Rabbi Elazar, who states: {\i ein poskin yeinot le-ishah} \u8212- wine is not allocated to a woman living apart from her husband. Whether {\i beit din} is directing provision from a living but absent husband\u8217's assets or from a deceased husband\u8217's estate (since the obligation of {\i mezonot} for the widow from the estate follows the same framework as for the living separated wife), the same rule applies: wine is not part of the court-ordered food allocation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hosea Derashah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah of Kfar Nevirya (or, in another version, Kfar Nafor Chayya) offers a scriptural basis for this rule, deriving it from the verse in Hosea (2:7): {\i elchah acharei me\u8217'ahavai notnei lachmi u-maymai tzamri u-fishti shamni ve-shikuyai} \u8212- \u8220"I will go after my lovers who give me my bread and my water, my wool and my linen, my oil and my drink.\u8221" The verse lists what a woman seeks from her lovers. The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i shikuyai} in this context does not mean wine but rather {\i devarim she-ha-ishah mishtokeket aleihen} \u8212- things a woman desires \u8212- namely jewelry and adornments. The verse, on this reading, implies that wine is emphatically not what should be provided for a woman from outside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i derashah} is built on the narrative of Hannah at Shiloh. The verse reads: {\i va-takam Chanah acharei ochlo ve-Shilo ve-acharei shato} \u8212- \u8220"and Hannah arose after he ate in Shiloh and after he drank.\u8221" The {\i drasha} notes {\i shato} \u8212- his drinking \u8212- but not {\i shatetah}, her drinking; the implication is that she did not drink wine. The {\i Gemara} challenges this: should {\i ochlo} similarly be read as his eating alone, implying she did not eat? The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i anan be-shinui kera be-dibura ka-amrinan} \u8212- the {\i drasha} rests on the deviation in language. The verse is speaking about Hannah in the third person throughout; when it shifts to speak of {\i shato} \u8212- his drinking \u8212- without a corresponding form for her drinking, that deviation is the exegetical point. The eating is stated in a form that encompasses her as well, but the drinking is stated only for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accustomed Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} qualifies Rabbi Elazar\u8217's rule: {\i regila nosnin lah} \u8212- if a woman is accustomed to drinking wine, wine is allocated for her. {\i Regila shani} \u8212- an accustomed woman is treated differently, on the theory that she has developed a tolerance and the concern for licentious behavior is diminished. Rabbi Chanina bar Kahana in the name of Shmuel specifies: if she is accustomed, she receives one cup; if she is not accustomed, she receives two cups \u8212- a formulation that on its face seems counterintuitive and requires Abaye\u8217's clarification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Refinement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye explains that Shmuel\u8217's statement must be read comparatively, distinguishing between her situation {\i bifnei ba\u8217'alah} (when living with her husband) and {\i she-lo bifnei ba\u8217'alah} (when living apart). The full structure is: if she customarily drinks two cups when with her husband, she receives one cup when apart from him; if she customarily drinks only one cup when with her husband, she receives nothing when apart from him. The reasoning is that even for a woman accustomed to wine, the absence of her husband removes a check on her behavior \u8212- both because her marital desires may go unfulfilled and because her husband is not present to moderate her conduct \u8212- so the allocation is reduced by one full cup. And if she was already at the minimum of one cup with her husband, there is no further reduction available, so she receives nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cooking Wine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative resolution is offered: even when drinking wine is prohibited, wine for cooking \u8212- {\i le-tsikei kdera} \u8212- is permitted. The concern about licentious behavior attaches to drinking; using wine as a cooking ingredient does not implicate that concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nakdimon Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is illustrated by the story of the daughter-in-law of Nakdimon ben Guryon. The Rabbis allocated for her two {\i se\u8217'ah} of wine per week \u8212- {\i me-erev Shabbat le-erev Shabbat} \u8212- specifically for cooking. She responded, \u8220"May you merit to apportion the same for your own daughters\u8221" \u8212- whether sincerely or ironically is unclear. The narrator adds: she was a {\i shomeret yavam}, awaiting {\i yibbum}, and the Sages did not respond {\i amen} to her blessing, lest it be read as an ill omen for their own daughters to be left widowed awaiting {\i yibbum}. The detail about {\i yibbum} also illuminates why it is {\i beit din} rather than a living husband allocating her food: she is a widow drawing sustenance from the deceased husband\u8217's estate, which is precisely the situation in which the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's minimum provisions become most practically important for the {\i almanah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine & Behavior\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One to Three Cups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now states the {\i tanna}\u8217's teaching that underlies all these restrictions. One cup of wine is beneficial for a woman \u8212- {\i kos echad yafeh le-ishah}. Two cups is unseemly \u8212- {\i nival hu}. Three cups, and she will verbally proposition men for sex \u8212- {\i tova\u8217'at be-peh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Cups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Four cups, and she will solicit even a donkey in the marketplace without shame \u8212- {\i afilu chamor tova\u8217'at ba-shuk ve-eina ikpat lah}. This is, manifestly, a teaching composed from a male perspective, and reflects the assumption \u8212- already encountered in the {\i mored/moredes} discussion \u8212- that women\u8217's sexual self-control is more vulnerable to alcohol than men\u8217's. The social context is also relevant: women in this society had far less habitual exposure to wine, which would indeed have lowered their tolerance. Even granting all this, the extreme escalation is notable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava limits the entire teaching: {\i lo shanu ela she-ein ba\u8217'alah imah} \u8212- all of this applies only when the husband is not present. When the husband is with her \u8212- {\i ba\u8217'alah imah leit lan bah} \u8212- there is no restriction. This confirms the framework of the whole discussion: these wine-allocation rules operate only when {\i beit din} is the one providing food and wine, i.e., when she is living apart from her husband. When she is with her husband, what the two of them choose to drink together is their own business, and we assume the husband\u8217's presence serves as a sufficient check.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guest Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a challenge from the story of Hannah, who was with her husband Elkanah at Shiloh, yet did not drink \u8212- and we derived from this that wine is restricted even when the husband is present. Rava\u8217's ruling seems to contradict this. The {\i Gemara} resolves it: {\i achsanai shani} \u8212- guests are different. Rav Huna teaches: how do we know that a guest couple should not engage in marital relations while staying in someone else\u8217's home? From the verse describing Elkanah and Hannah: {\i va-yashkimu ba-boker va-yishtachavu lifnei Hashem va-yashuvu va-yavo\u8217'u el beitam ha-Ramatah va-yeida Elkanah et Chana ishto} \u8212- they rose in the morning, bowed before God, returned to their home in Ramah, and {\i only then} Elkanah knew Hannah. The sequence implies that the marital intimacy occurred only after they returned home, not while guests at Shiloh. The same principle governs wine: when one is a guest in another\u8217's home, the usual latitude that applies between husband and wife in their own home does not apply. Greater decorum is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Allocation Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Homa\u8217's Request\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Homa, who was Abaye\u8217's wife and apparently a woman of notable beauty, came before Rava. She requested that he allocate food for her \u8212- {\i psok li mezoni} \u8212- presumably either because Abaye was absent or, as later details suggest, possibly because he had died. Rava made the allocation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Private Wine Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She then asked him to also allocate wine \u8212- {\i psok li chamra}. Rava replied: I know Nachmani \u8212- Abaye\u8217's other name \u8212- and he would not drink wine. She responded: by your life, he would drink wine in a {\i shufrizi} \u8212- a long horn-shaped cup \u8212- {\i this} big, and she extended her arms to demonstrate the size. This exchange itself illustrates a point made earlier in the {\i sugya}: people behave privately in ways they do not display publicly. Rava was under the impression that Abaye never drank wine; his wife knew otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As she extended her arms to demonstrate the size of the cup, her sleeve fell back and her arm became exposed. The {\i Gemara} describes it: {\i nafal nehora be-vei dina} \u8212- light filled the {\i beit din}, meaning her arm was of such luminous beauty that it illuminated the courtroom. Rava was profoundly stirred. He rose, went home, and immediately propositioned his wife \u8212- the daughter of Rav Chisda \u8212- seeking to be intimate with her in the middle of the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Pursuit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When his wife asked what had happened in court, Rava told her that Homa, Abaye\u8217's wife, had appeared before him. His wife immediately ran after Homa and struck her with the strap from a chest \u8212- {\i be-kofei de-shida} \u8212- chasing her from the entire city of Mechuza. She shouted at Homa: {\i katlat leh} \u8212- you have already killed one man (meaning her husband), and now you want to kill another? The background is that Homa had been married three times, each husband having died, and Rava\u8217's wife feared that Homa\u8217's beauty would lead yet another husband \u8212- her own \u8212- to his ruin. Whether Abaye was indeed among the three deceased husbands is not entirely clear from the text; the narrative leaves open whether he was dead or merely absent, which is why Homa was seeking a food allocation in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel story follows. The wife of Rav Yosef the son of Rava came before Rav Nechemia the son of Rav Yosef. She too requested a food allocation \u8212- {\i psok li mezoni} \u8212- and he provided it. She then requested a wine allocation \u8212- {\i psok li chamra} \u8212- and he provided that as well. He explained his reasoning: {\i yadana behu bi-venei Mechuza de-shatu chamra} \u8212- I know the people of Mechuza, that they drink wine regularly. Because wine is a routine part of daily life in Mechuza \u8212- much as it would be in regions where wine accompanies every meal as a matter of course \u8212- allocating wine to a woman from that city does not implicate the concerns that motivated the restriction. Custom and local practice determine what counts as ordinary consumption and what constitutes dangerous excess.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 64b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Penalties & Reduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marital Disputes Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present {\i sugya} continues the discussion of marital dysfunction \u8212- specifically, the mechanisms by which we encourage compliance when a marriage is in trouble. The issues at stake include whether the husband can be compelled to go out and find work in order to meet his financial obligations (since his obligation is to pay the bills, but the question of whether he can be forced to find employment \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'ana eflach} \u8212- is debated), and, more significantly for {\i agunah} concerns, the degree to which we compel a husband to give a {\i get} when the wife declares the marriage intolerable. Related to that, {\i Tosafos} addresses the question of whether we may even \u8220"encourage\u8221" a husband to perform {\i chalitzah} when he refuses. The {\i daf} now turns to the financial penalties and why they differ between husband and wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Penalty Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} established the following asymmetric penalty framework: when the wife is not fulfilling her responsibilities, we reduce her {\i kesuvah} by seven {\i dinarin} per week; when the husband is not fulfilling his responsibilities, we increase the {\i kesuvah} (for the wife\u8217's benefit) by three {\i dinarin}. A second opinion substitutes {\i tarpikin} for {\i dinarin} in both cases \u8212- seven {\i tarpikin} per week for her and three {\i tarpikin} per week for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Currency Conversions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the penalty\u8217's practical significance, one must first be oriented in the relevant currency equivalences. A {\i dinar} equals a {\i zuz}, and four {\i zuz} equals a {\i sela}, which is equivalent to a biblical {\i shekel}. The Torah\u8217's fifty-{\i shekel} bride price ({\i mohar}) accordingly equals two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- which is precisely the basis of the standard {\i kesuvah} of two hundred {\i zuz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Penalty Duration Math\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i kesuvah} stands at two hundred {\i zuz} and is reduced by seven {\i zuz} per week, the base amount will be exhausted in approximately twenty-eight weeks. If the rate is instead three {\i zuz} per week \u8212- as is the case when the husband is being penalized \u8212- the base amount would last approximately sixty-seven weeks. These calculations apply only to the base {\i kesuvah} of two hundred {\i zuz}; the {\i tosefes} (additional sum) and {\i nidunya} (dowry) would extend the process considerably.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarpikin Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai tarpikin} \u8212- what exactly is a {\i tarpikin}? Rav Sheishes answers: {\i asteira}, a half-{\i zuz}. A {\i baraita} corroborates this: Rabbi Yehuda says that three {\i tarpikin} \u8212- which is three half-{\i zuz}, or one and a half {\i zuz} \u8212- equal nine {\i ma\u8217'in}, which works out to a {\i ma\u8217'ah} and a half per day (excluding {\i Shabbos}). Accordingly, when the {\i mishnah} says seven {\i tarpikin} for her, that is three and a half {\i zuz} per week; when it says three {\i tarpikin} for him, that is one and a half {\i zuz} per week.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asymmetrical Penalties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mored/Moredet Gap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Setting aside the distinction between the {\i dinarin} and {\i tarpikin} opinions, the structural asymmetry is this: for every day the wife is not fulfilling her responsibility, the {\i kesuvah} is reduced by one unit (whether that unit is a {\i zuz} or a {\i tarpikin}) \u8212- and this applies to all seven days of the week, including {\i Shabbos}. For every day the husband is not fulfilling his responsibility, the {\i kesuvah} is increased by only a half unit, and {\i Shabbos} is excluded. There are thus two distinct questions: why is she penalized at double the rate of him, and why is {\i Shabbos} counted against her but not against him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Fine Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiyya bar Rav Yosef posed the question to Shmuel: {\i mai shna ihu de-yavnan leh de-Shabbas} \u8212- why does she get penalized even for {\i Shabbos}, while he does not? The {\i gemara} answers: when the {\i kesuvah} is being {\i reduced} because the wife is withholding, the benefit to the husband takes the form of a debt reduction \u8212- it does not {\i appear} as if he is earning wages on {\i Shabbos}. But when the {\i kesuvah} is being {\i increased} because the husband is withholding, if we were to include {\i Shabbos}, it would appear as though the wife is effectively earning money on {\i Shabbos}, which is impermissible. The reduction of a debt is categorically different from the payment of wages, and the law tracks that distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prostitute Market Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then addresses why her penalty rate is double his: {\i tze le-mad mi-shuk shel zonot} \u8212- go and learn from the marketplace of prostitutes. There, the men hire the women \u8212- meaning the market for female prostitutes vastly exceeds the market for male prostitutes. This demonstrates that men are regarded as the more injured party when sexual intimacy is withheld.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi offers two explanations. The first is straightforward: the {\i gemara} assumes that the {\i mored/moredes} framework is primarily about withholding marital intimacy ({\i tashmish}), and men are seen as having a significantly greater sexual drive and therefore suffering greater harm from its denial. The second, {\i davar acher}: {\i ze yitzro mi-bachutz ve-ze yitzro mi-bifnim} \u8212- one\u8217's anatomy is external and the other\u8217's internal. Rashi explains: {\i kishuyav nikar u-mitganeh} \u8212- a husband\u8217's physical arousal is visible and thus a source of shame if unsatisfied, making his injury more apparent. Both explanations converge on the conclusion that the husband is the more acutely injured party when intimacy is denied, and accordingly, when the {\i wife} is the one withholding, she is fined at double his rate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Penalty Asymmetry Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the imbalances: the wife is penalized at twice the daily rate as the husband; she is penalized on {\i Shabbos} while he is not (since her withholding generates a debt reduction, while his generates something that would resemble earnings on {\i Shabbos}); and, as noted from the previous discussion, the husband always retains the ability to initiate a {\i get}, which the wife does not. As for the practical timeline \u8212- depending on whether the unit is a {\i zuz} or a {\i tarpikin}, the process of running down the base two-hundred-{\i zuz kesuvah} spans roughly between a half year and a year, not counting {\i tosefes} and {\i nidunya}. The later {\i gemara} traditions, as was noted, prefer a much faster resolution leading directly to the {\i get} rather than a prolonged attrition of the {\i kesuvah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Separate Maintenance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intermediary Provision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now addresses a related situation: {\i hamashre ishto al yedei shaliach} \u8212- a husband who is providing for his wife through an intermediary rather than sharing a home with her. The reasons for this arrangement could be various \u8212- a business trip, an unresolved marital dispute, or other circumstances \u8212- but the halakhic question is the same: what are the minimum provisions he must supply?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Allowance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He may not give her less than two {\i kav} of wheat or, alternatively, four {\i kav} of barley per week. Rabbi Yosi notes that the option of barley was stipulated only by Rabbi Yishmael, who lived near Edom \u8212- as the {\i gemara} will clarify, the Edomite barley was of inferior quality, which is why double the measure of barley was required to match the nutritional value of the wheat. Elsewhere, the ratio would differ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consumables List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In addition to grain, the husband must provide: a half {\i kav} of legumes ({\i kitnit}); a half {\i log} of oil (approximately seven ounces); and a {\i kav} of dried figs ({\i grogrot}) or, alternatively, a {\i maneh} (a weight measurement) of pressed dates. If those specific fruits are unavailable, he substitutes an equivalent amount of other available fruit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Durable Goods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond consumables, the {\i mishnah} specifies a bed, a mat, and a cushion, along with a head covering, a belt for her waist, and shoes provided seasonally \u8212- new shoes each {\i regel} (festival).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seasonal Clothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Clothing is allocated at fifty {\i zuz} worth per year. Importantly, the {\i mishnah} specifies that one does not give her {\i new} clothing in the summer \u8212- since new garments had not yet been worn thin and were therefore too warm \u8212- nor worn, thin clothing in the winter when warmth is needed. Rather, the new clothes of fifty {\i zuz} are purchased in winter; she wears them during the cold months, and as they become worn over the course of the year, she uses the now-thinned garments in the summer. The worn-out garments at year\u8217's end remain hers to keep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pin Money & Meals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each week she also receives a {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef} \u8212- a small coin of silver \u8212- as pin money for miscellaneous personal needs. Additionally, even when living apart, the husband must arrange for her to eat the Friday night {\i Shabbos} meal together with him. This is striking: just as the {\i gemara} mandates conjugal relations at fixed intervals, it similarly mandates a minimum of shared table time \u8212- specifically the Friday night meal. The underlying logic appears to be that {\i parnasah} encompasses not merely financial provision but a certain degree of continued personal relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Work Income Clause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the husband fails to provide the {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef}, she retains the income from her work ({\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}). The {\i mishnah} thus links the pin money to her earnings: if he does not give her even this small personal allowance, she keeps what she produces.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Work Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now asks: {\i u-mah hi osah lo} \u8212- what work does she perform for him? The term {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} is potentially ambiguous: does it refer to domestic duties performed within the home, or to the productive labor by which she generates income from outside the house? The {\i mishnah} here focuses on the latter \u8212- a fixed, measurable quota of weaving that she must produce each week. The distinction between basic domestic responsibilities and income-generating labor was never fully resolved in the earlier {\i gemara} discussion, though the {\i peshat} of the {\i mishnah} appears to treat the weaving quota as the measurable {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} that is offset by the {\i ma\u8217'ah kesef}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weaving Quotas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The quota is: five {\i sela\u8217'im} of warp ({\i sheti}) per week in Judea, which equals ten {\i sela\u8217'im} by Galilean weight \u8212- since Galilean weights were half those of Judea \u8212- or alternatively, ten {\i sela\u8217'im} of woof ({\i erev}) in Judea, equaling twenty {\i sela\u8217'im} in Galilee. The woof is the easier of the two to prepare, which accounts for the higher numeric quota assigned to it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Separate Maintenance (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the wife is nursing, her weaving obligation is reduced \u8212- both because she is expending energy in another demanding activity, and more significantly because excessive physical labor might compromise her ability to nurse effectively. In addition, her food allowance is increased because she is eating for two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Qualification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes: {\i b\u8217'mah devarim amurim \u8212- b\u8217'ani she\u8217'b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- all of the above minimums apply to the poorest standard of Israelite living. However, {\i aval b\u8217'mechubad \u8212- hakol l\u8217'fi k\u8217'vodo}: for a person of higher status, everything scales according to his standing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two-Meals Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eruv Techumin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established a weekly grain allocation of two {\i kav} of wheat, the {\i gemara} asks whose opinion this {\i mishnah} reflects \u8212- and finds that it corresponds neither to Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka nor to Rabbi Shimon. The {\i gemara} first establishes the basis for the calculation by invoking the law of {\i eruv techumin}: the standard food measure deposited for an {\i eruv techumin} must be {\i mazon shtei seudot l\u8217'chol echad v\u8217'echad} \u8212- two meals\u8217' worth per person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bread Measure Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A dispute governs how much bread constitutes \u8220"two meals\u8221": Rabbi Meir says the standard is the amount of bread consumed at a weekday meal (not a {\i Shabbos} meal), while Rabbi Yehuda says it is the amount consumed at a {\i Shabbos} meal. The {\i gemara} notes that each opinion is attempting to be lenient \u8212- Rabbi Meir because {\i Shabbos} meals involve more varied dishes and therefore less bread per meal, and Rabbi Yehuda because weekday meals involve a larger bread intake. Thus each opinion arrives at a smaller figure by its own reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RYbB Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka says: the standard is a loaf purchased for a {\i pundion} \u8212- a small coin \u8212- when the going rate of wheat is four {\i se\u8217'ah} per {\i selah}. Since a {\i selah} equals 48 {\i pundion}, and a {\i se\u8217'ah} equals six {\i kav}, four {\i se\u8217'ah} is twenty-four {\i kav}, and 24 {\i kav} for 48 {\i pundion} means one {\i kav} equals two {\i pundion}. Therefore, a loaf costing one {\i pundion} is made from a half-{\i kav} of wheat \u8212- and that loaf, he says, constitutes two meals. His measure is thus one half-{\i kav} of wheat per two meals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon states: two-thirds of a loaf, when three loaves are baked from one {\i kav}, constitutes two meals. Since three loaves per {\i kav} means each loaf is one-third of a {\i kav}, two-thirds of a loaf is two-thirds of one-third of a {\i kav} \u8212- that is, two-ninths of a {\i kav} for two meals. This is considerably less than half a {\i kav}, making Rabbi Shimon\u8217's standard significantly more lenient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A supplementary {\i gemara} notes that half of the two-meal loaf \u8212- a {\i pras} \u8212- is the amount used to define other legal measures. {\i Kedei achilat pras} (the time it takes to eat half a loaf, i.e., one meal\u8217's worth of bread) is the standard duration used in many other halakhic contexts, such as the time one must remain inside a {\i metzora}-afflicted house to contract {\i tum\u8217'ah}. Half of a meal\u8217's worth renders food susceptible to {\i tum\u8217'ah} of food, and so on, though the {\i gemara} does not pursue those applications here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meals Calculation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} allocates two {\i kav} of wheat per week. The problem is now evident: according to Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka, a half-{\i kav} equals two meals, so two {\i kav} yields only eight meals \u8212- far fewer than the fourteen meals (two per day over seven days) required for a week. According to Rabbi Shimon, two-ninths of a {\i kav} equals two meals, meaning one {\i kav} yields nine meals and two {\i kav} yields eighteen \u8212- more than the fourteen needed. Neither tanna\u8217's measure maps cleanly onto the {\i mishnah}\u8217's two-{\i kav} allocation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baker\u8217's Profit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the problem on the side of Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka by invoking a principle of Rav Chisda: {\i tze me-hen shelish le-chanvani} \u8212- deduct a third as the storekeeper\u8217's profit. Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka was not stating how much wheat actually goes into the loaf; he was stating the market price. When you pay a {\i pundion} for a loaf, the baker is not putting the entire value of that {\i pundion} into raw wheat \u8212- a third of his revenue is profit. Therefore, the actual wheat content per loaf is higher than the simple price calculation suggests. Once one adds back the baker\u8217's markup, the two {\i kav} of wheat actually yield twelve meals, not eight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then entertains a second version in which the markup is one-half rather than one-third, which would yield sixteen meals from two {\i kav}. The dispute between the two figures \u8212- one-third versus one-half \u8212- depends on local conditions: specifically, whether the supplier of grain also provides firewood at no extra cost. Where firewood is included, the baker\u8217's overhead is lower and his profit margin higher, yielding the half-markup figure; where firewood must be purchased separately, his overhead is higher and his profit is the smaller third.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat & Guests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then reconciles the various meal counts with the {\i mishnah}\u8217's two-{\i kav} allocation. Under the first version of Rav Chisda\u8217's principle (one-third markup), two {\i kav} yields twelve meals. Adding the Friday-night {\i Shabbos} meal that the {\i mishnah} already mandates \u8212- which the wife eats with her husband \u8212- brings the total to thirteen. The {\i gemara} notes that even this falls short of fourteen. A further solution: perhaps the {\i mishnah} in saying {\i ochla imo lel Shabbas} refers not to eating together but, as some suggest, to the euphemistic {\i tashmish} \u8212- in which case the Friday night meal still counts in her weekly bread ration. Still, thirteen does not equal fourteen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the second version (half markup), two {\i kav} yields sixteen meals. If we follow Rabbi Chidka, who requires four {\i Shabbos} meals rather than three, a weekly total of sixteen is arrived at naturally (twelve weekday meals plus four on {\i Shabbos}). Alternatively, even according to the rabbis who require only three {\i Shabbos} meals, one arrives at fifteen, with one additional meal allotted for {\i orchei u-farchi} \u8212- guests who might drop by unexpectedly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to Rabbi Shimon, under whose measure two {\i kav} yields eighteen meals: according to the rabbis (three {\i Shabbos} meals), that is fifteen meals needed, with three extra portions allocated for guests. According to Rabbi Chidka (four {\i Shabbos} meals), there are sixteen needed, leaving two extra for guests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One observation is worth noting: conspicuously absent from this entire weekly allocation is any protein \u8212- no meat, fish, eggs, or dairy \u8212- as well as any vegetables. The provision described truly reflects the {\i ani she\u8217'b\u8217'Yisrael} minimum: starch, a small amount of legumes, oil, and dried fruit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine Allocation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Wine Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} lists legumes and oil among the weekly provisions but conspicuously omits wine. The {\i gemara} notes this omission and reads it as supporting Rabbi Elazar\u8217's ruling: {\i ein poskin yayin le\u8217'isha} \u8212- {\i beis din} does not allocate wine for a woman who is being maintained separately from her husband. The term {\i poskin} here refers specifically to the court\u8217's directive: when {\i beis din} must determine provisions \u8212- whether because the husband is absent and has not made arrangements, or because his estate must provide for a widow or a woman awaiting {\i yibbum} \u8212- wine is not included in what they mandate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chana Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ish Kfar Nevoria (some say ish Kfar Nafor Chayya) expounds: how do we know that {\i beis din} does not allocate wine for a woman? From the verse describing Chana in Shilo: {\i vatakom Chana acharei achlo v\u8217'acharei shato} \u8212- Chana arose after {\i he} ate and after {\i he} drank. The verse uses the masculine singular forms for both eating and drinking. The drasha: {\i shato v\u8217'lo shatata} \u8212- he drank, but she did not drink. The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: why not also read the eating verb as indicating {\i he} ate but {\i she} did not \u8212- {\i achlo v\u8217'lo achla}? The {\i gemara} responds: the verse changes the expected formulation ({\i achla v\u8217'shata} would have been expected) and it is precisely that deviation that the {\i midrash} is drawing upon. Since the verse\u8217's subject was primarily Chana, the shift to male forms for drinking signals something meaningful \u8212- that she did not drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accustomed Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} challenges this: {\i regila notnin lah} \u8212- if a woman is accustomed to drinking wine, we do allocate wine for her. The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent conflict: {\i regila shani} \u8212- one who is accustomed is different; she has developed a tolerance. Rav Chinana bar Kahana then cites Shmuel: if she is accustomed, one cup is given; if she is not accustomed, two cups are given. This formulation is perplexing \u8212- why give {\i more} wine to someone unaccustomed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Refinement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye clarifies: what Shmuel\u8217's statement means is as follows. If she is normally accustomed to drinking two cups in her husband\u8217's presence, then when separated from him {\i beis din} allocates only one cup. If she normally drinks only one cup in her husband\u8217's presence, then when separated from him she receives nothing at all. The logic is that when the husband is absent, we are more cautious about wine consumption regardless of her accustomed level, and so we always reduce by at least one cup \u8212- and if she was already at the minimum of one, we reduce to nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cooking Wine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds an important exception: even where the general rule would deny her wine, {\i notnin lah l\u8217'tziki kdera} \u8212- wine used as a cooking ingredient is still permitted and allocated. Wine for cooking ({\i tziki kdera}) does not implicate the concerns that motivate the general restriction. This is supported by a story: Rabbi Abba said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan that the daughter-in-law of Nakdimon ben Guryon was allocated by the rabbis two {\i se\u8217'ah} of wine per week \u8212- from {\i erev Shabbos} to {\i erev Shabbos} \u8212- for use in cooking. She responded: {\i kach tifsaku livnoteichem} \u8212- may you allocate the same for your daughters. It is then noted that she was a {\i shomeret yavam} at the time, and the rabbis refrained from answering {\i amen} to her wish, lest it become an ill omen \u8212- for her blessing would mean their daughters\u8217' husbands would die.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This narrative also illuminates the broader context: the allocation mechanism described in the {\i mishnah} governs not only cases where the husband is away, but also where he is deceased. A widow or {\i shomeret yavam} drawing support from the estate would be subject to exactly these provisions \u8212- making the {\i mishnah} of critical ongoing practical importance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine & Behavior\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A tannaitic teaching underlies the caution: one cup of wine is good for a woman; two is unseemly ({\i nivul}); three, and she will verbally proposition men for sex ({\i tova\u8217'at b\u8217'peh}); four, and she will proposition even a donkey in the marketplace and will be completely without inhibition. This is, as the {\i gemara} itself suggests, a statement composed from a male perspective, and likely reflects a world in which women had far lower habitual exposure to wine. Nonetheless, the {\i halakha} flows from this assessment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava limits the entire discussion: {\i lo shanu ela she\u8217'ein ba\u8217'alah imah} \u8212- all of the above restrictions apply only when the husband is not present. {\i Aval ba\u8217'alah imah \u8212- leit lan bah} \u8212- when she is with her husband, we have no concern. The rationale is twofold: first, the husband\u8217's presence is presumed to provide a check on behavior; second, and more fundamentally, it is simply not {\i beis din}\u8217's business what a couple does privately in their own home. The wine restrictions are a function of {\i beis din}\u8217's authority to set the terms of court-supervised maintenance \u8212- not a general restriction on women drinking wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an apparent challenge: but Chana was with her husband Elkanah in Shilo \u8212- the very setting we used to prove that she did not drink \u8212- so why did the restriction apply there? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i achsanai shani} \u8212- guests are different. Rav Huna teaches that guests in someone else\u8217's home are forbidden from engaging in marital relations, derived from the fact that Elkanah and Chana only had marital relations after returning home to Ramah ({\i vayeida Elkanah et Chana ishto} \u8212- after their return, not while they were guests). The same elevated standard of propriety that restricts intimacy applies to wine consumption as well when one is a guest.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Narrative Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Choma\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to a series of vivid narratives that illustrate the wine-allocation principles in practice. Choma, the wife of Abaye \u8212- described as a woman of striking beauty \u8212- came before Rava to have her provisions allocated. Rava assigned her {\i mezonos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She then asked him to allocate wine as well. Rava replied: {\i yada\u8217'ana beih b\u8217'Nachmani d\u8217'lo hava shati chamra} \u8212- I know Nachmani (a name by which Abaye was also known), and he did not drink wine; therefore I cannot establish her accustomed level as including wine. She responded: {\i chayei d\u8217'mar} \u8212- by your life, he would pour wine for her in a large elongated cup ({\i shufrizi}) \u8212- {\i this} big \u8212- and she stretched out her arms to demonstrate the size of the vessel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Choma\u8217's Arm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As she gestured to indicate the size of the cup, her sleeve fell back and her arm became exposed. Her skin was so luminously beautiful that a radiant light ({\i nehora}) filled the {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Aroused\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava was overcome \u8212- aroused and agitated. He rose abruptly, went home, and immediately initiated marital relations with his wife, the daughter of Rav Chisda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Reaction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His wife asked what had happened in court that day. He told her: Choma, Abaye\u8217's wife, had come. His wife went out after Choma and struck her with a strap from a chest ({\i kulfi d\u8217'shidei}), chasing her out of the entire city of Mechuza. She said to Choma: you have already killed three husbands \u8212- {\i u-va\u8217'ita lemiktal achrina} \u8212- and now you want to kill a fourth? The reference to three dead husbands raises the possibility, though the text leaves it unresolved, that Abaye himself was among the deceased, which would explain why Choma had come to {\i beis din} to claim {\i mezonos} from the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second case follows immediately: the wife of Rav Yosef the son of Rava came before Rav Nechemya the son of Rav Yosef for {\i mezonos} allocation. She requested wine. Rav Nechemya allocated it, explaining: {\i yeda\u8217'na behu b\u8217'vnei Mechuza deshatau chamra} \u8212- I know the people of Mechuza, that they drink wine routinely. In a locale where wine is a normal part of daily meals \u8212- as common as it is in, say, France \u8212- there is nothing unusual or dangerous about allocating wine to the wife; it is simply part of her established accustomed standard of living.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 70\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Perek Hamadir Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Order Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin daf 70 with the opening of the new perek, {\i Hamadir}. It is worth noting briefly that there is some debate among the {\i Rishonim} about the proper ordering of the {\i perakim} in {\i Ketubot}. Nevertheless, the order before us is the order we work with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Main Themes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Part of the difficulty in establishing a definitive sequence is that the {\i perakim} weave in and out of two major thematic concerns. The first is the pair of {\i chiyuv mezonot} and {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} \u8212- the husband\u8217's obligation to provide sustenance and the wife\u8217's reciprocal obligation to produce handiwork. The second is the complex of issues surrounding marital relations: the {\i moredet} who withholds intimacy, questions of {\i neder} in that context, and the quantitative standards of the husband\u8217's obligation of {\i onah}. Because the {\i perakim} continually oscillate between these two themes, questions naturally arise about their proper sequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transition to Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the preceding perek dealt with the case of a father transferring responsibility for his daughters to a third party \u8212- a {\i shalish} \u8212- covering both the dowry question when the father dies and the establishment of a kind of trust arrangement. That perek was thus concerned not with the wife per se but with daughters. We now return to the wife and to the obligation of {\i mezonot}, and a {\i shalish} will reappear here as well \u8212- perhaps precisely that thematic link provides the transition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hamadir Ishto\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Forbids Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i hamadir ishto mileihanos lo} \u8212- if a man vows that his wife may not derive any {\i hana\u8217'ah} from him. The mechanism is straightforward: one may declare one\u8217's possessions or the benefit flowing from oneself as {\i asur} to another person. Here the husband declares that all {\i hana\u8217'ah} from him is forbidden to his wife. The {\i gemara} will focus specifically on the {\i chiyuv mezonot} \u8212- the husband\u8217's obligation to provide her sustenance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Onah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might immediately ask: what about {\i mitzvas onah}? How can the husband vow against the {\i mitzvah} of marital relations? The {\i gemara} largely sets this aside, and the {\i Rishonim} will explain why \u8212- that point is taken up shortly. For now, the {\i mishnah} focuses its analysis on {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 30-Day Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} provides the following resolution: {\i ad shloshim yom ya\u8217'amid parnas} \u8212- for up to thirty days, the husband may arrange for a third party, a {\i parnas}, to provide his wife\u8217's {\i mezonot}. Of course, the {\i gemara} will probe this immediately: if the husband arranges for the third party, is that third party not simply acting as his {\i shaliach}, such that the wife is still effectively receiving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the husband? We will see how the {\i gemara} resolves that. But the {\i mishnah}\u8217's basic structure is: up to thirty days, the arrangement with a third party is workable. Beyond that \u8212- {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten kesuvah} \u8212- he must divorce her and pay the {\i kesuvah}. Even in cases where, as we learned earlier, a husband and wife may maintain separate domicile with the husband providing for her, the contentiousness of a {\i neder} makes the relationship unsustainable beyond thirty days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Duration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question naturally arises about how to read the thirty-day threshold. Does the {\i mishnah} contemplate a {\i neder} that is {\i stam} \u8212- indefinite \u8212- and we simply wait thirty days to see whether the husband can obtain a {\i heter} for it? Or does the thirty days mark the dividing line between a permitted short-term {\i neder} and a prohibited long-term one: a {\i neder} of up to thirty days is tolerable, while one beyond thirty days requires divorce? Note that {\i stam nezirut} is indeed thirty days, but a {\i stam neder} is indefinite \u8212- so the thirty days here is not derived from a default duration of the {\i neder} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda: Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} introduces a distinction based on the identity of the husband. {\i B\u8217'Yisrael chodesh echad yekayem} \u8212- for a non-{\i kohen}, we permit the arrangement for up to one month; if it extends into the second month, {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten kesuvah}, he must divorce and pay the {\i kesuvah}. For a {\i kohen}, however, greater latitude is given \u8212- since a {\i kohen} may not remarry a divorc\u233 ?e, divorce is irreversible, and so we allow up to two full months before requiring it. Only upon entering the third month does {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten kesuvah} apply.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fruit & Adornment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Fruit Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i hamadir ishto shelo tit\u8217'am echad mikol hapirot} \u8212- if a man\u8217's wife is vowed not to taste even one type of fruit. The {\i gemara} will explain that this is in fact the wife\u8217's own {\i neder}, which the husband had the opportunity to annul ({\i leifer}) but instead upheld ({\i kiyem}). A question also arises about the mechanism: if she may not eat one fruit \u8212- say, a single variety \u8212- why should that necessitate divorce? What is the connection to the marriage itself? That question is addressed by the {\i gemara}. In any event, the {\i mishnah} rules: {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten kesuvah} \u8212- divorce and {\i kesuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda: Fruit Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here again {\i Rabbi Yehuda} distinguishes: {\i b\u8217'Yisrael yom echad yekayem} \u8212- for a non-{\i kohen}, we tolerate the deprivation of even one fruit for up to one day, but if it extends into the second day, {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten kesuvah}. For a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife: two days are tolerable; only upon entering the third day is divorce required. The underlying logic is consistent \u8212- the irreversibility of divorce for a {\i kohen} warrants more patience.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adornment Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds a third case: {\i hamadir ishto shelo tiskasheit b\u8217'echad mikol haminim} \u8212- a man whose wife is vowed not to adorn herself with even one type of adornment. {\i Rashi} explains that {\i minim} here includes perfumes as well as jewelry \u8212- so even a single fragrance is encompassed. Again, as with the fruit case, the {\i gemara} will explain that this is the wife\u8217's {\i neder} which the husband upheld rather than annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi: Rich/Poor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yosi} introduces a different framework here, distinguishing between poor and wealthy women. {\i B\u8217'aniyos shelo nasan kitzva} \u8212- for a poor woman who does not normally wear jewelry or perfume, the {\i neder} is problematic only if it is indefinite; if there is a defined endpoint, it may be tolerable. What length of time qualifies as {\i kitzva} remains to be clarified in the {\i gemara}. For wealthy women, however \u8212- {\i uva\u8217'ashiros shloshim yom} \u8212- thirty days is the limit, and beyond that, divorce is required. The {\i chiddush} of these latter two cases in the {\i mishnah} is striking: we are dealing not with the husband forbidding his {\i mezonot}, which is squarely a marital matter, but with a kind of self-denial the wife undertakes. And it is only a single fruit, a single piece of jewelry \u8212- yet the {\i mishnah} treats it with such gravity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rashi/Tosafot Onah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Onah Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We return now to the question raised at the outset: why does the {\i gemara} bracket {\i onah} entirely and focus on {\i mezonot}? {\i Rashi} addresses this directly. In his first comment, {\i Rashi} writes that {\i tashmisho} \u8212- the husband\u8217's marital relations \u8212- cannot be rendered {\i asur} to the wife by this {\i neder}, because the husband is already {\i meshubad} to her for {\i onah}; it is hers, as it were. Therefore, {\i bilshvil hatashmish ein lanu lakof lehotzi v\u8217'liyten kesuvah} \u8212- we have no basis to compel divorce on account of {\i onah}, since the {\i neder} simply does not take effect there. The {\i gemara} will then separately grapple with {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} also clarifies the mechanics: when the husband says {\i mai hana\u8217'asi asurah} to his wife, he is forbidding the {\i hana\u8217'ah} of himself on her \u8212- that is, his deriving pleasure from her is what he is forbidding to her. By contrast, if he were to forbid her {\i hana\u8217'ah} of {\i tashmish} on him, he would have no standing, since he is {\i meshubad} to her for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} approach the {\i onah} question differently. Rather than grounding the exclusion of {\i onah} in the greater weight of the husband\u8217's obligation there versus {\i mezonot}, {\i Tosafot} argue that the generic language of {\i lehanos} \u8212- deriving benefit \u8212- is simply not understood to encompass {\i onah}. It is a matter of linguistic scope: the term, as ordinarily used, refers to material benefit such as {\i mezonot}, not to marital relations. So the exclusion of {\i onah} from the {\i neder}\u8217's effect is not because {\i onah} is more categorically protected than {\i mezonot}, but because the language of the {\i neder} as spoken does not reach {\i onah} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezonot Level\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a further point about whether {\i mezonot} is {\i de\u8217'oraissa} or {\i derabbanan}, which will bear directly on whether the {\i neder} can take effect. {\i Rashi} appears to consistently hold that {\i mezonot} for a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} is a biblical obligation, deriving from the verse {\i she\u8217'era kesuta ve\u8217'onasah lo yigra} \u8212- where {\i she\u8217'era} is understood as {\i mezonot}. {\i Ramban} and {\i Tosafot} dissent sharply, ruling that {\i mezonot} and {\i kesut} are only rabbinic obligations. This disagreement will become pivotal when the {\i gemara} explores the {\i arusa} scenario below.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forced Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why in Ketubot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding to the {\i gemara}, it is worth pausing on a structural question: why do these issues \u8212- when a husband is compelled to divorce his wife \u8212- appear in {\i Ketubot} rather than in {\i Gittin}? The answer is that {\i Gittin} deals primarily with the mechanics of executing and delivering the {\i get}, whereas {\i Ketubot} concerns the viability of the marriage itself: is each party fulfilling their obligations, when is the marriage considered sustainable, when is it not, and when does the woman forfeit her {\i kesuvah} or the husband become obligated to pay it and divorce her? These are fundamentally questions about the nature and health of the marriage, and so they belong in {\i Ketubot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We have already seen the debate about a {\i moredet} who refuses {\i tashmish} \u8212- she loses her {\i kesuvah}, but the crucial {\i Rishonim} debate is whether we also compel the husband to give a {\i get}. The {\i Rambam} holds that we do, which effectively means a woman who is unwilling to continue the marriage can trigger divorce proceedings by declaring she finds her husband intolerable. In the present case, where the husband is clearly at fault, the {\i mishnah} uses the language {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten kesuvah} \u8212- but how forcefully should that be understood?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yotzi: Force or Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} directly address the question of whether {\i yotzi} means physical coercion or merely persuasion and social pressure. The {\i Ri} holds that in all the cases in the {\i mishnah} where the formulation {\i yotzi} appears, we do in fact coerce the husband \u8212- {\i she-kofin oso} \u8212- since he is acting improperly, {\i de-keivan she-lo kedin avid}, and therefore {\i kofin lehotzi}, we force him to give the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Force Used\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then confronts the possibility that {\i kefiyah} here might mean only words. They reject this by invoking the principle {\i debidvarim lo yivaser eved} \u8212- a slave is not corrected through words alone. There are situations where words are simply insufficient, and physical force becomes necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chananel: Words Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbeinu Chananel} takes a sharply different position. He rules that we do not use physical force unless the {\i gemara} explicitly and unambiguously states {\i kofin} \u8212- that compulsion is applied. Where the {\i mishnah} employs only the language {\i yotzi}, without the added term {\i kofin lehotzi}, the court\u8217's role is limited to words and social pressure: {\i omrin lo kechiyuva dechachamim lehotzi} \u8212- we tell him that the Rabbis have obligated him to give the {\i get}, and {\i ve\u8217'im lo totzi mutarin likrocha avaryana} \u8212- if he refuses, we are permitted to publicly denounce him as a sinner. But physical force, {\i aval lechfoso lo} \u8212- that is not employed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}\u8217's final position is that physical coercion is indeed applicable \u8212- {\i al korchacha ayiri beshoti}, we are speaking of using sticks \u8212- particularly since the language {\i yotzi} itself implies compulsion, and when the husband is the one who has wronged the wife, {\i heicha de\u8217'oseh avla}, the coercive power is appropriate. However, {\i Tosafot} immediately add a critical qualification: {\i ein lichof shum adam legaresh vela\u8217'asos ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- we should not actually compel a man to write and deliver a {\i get} unless there is absolutely clear proof that he is obligated to do so. The reason: {\i deha get me\u8217'useh beyisrael shelo kedin pasul} \u8212- a {\i get} that is coerced by a Jewish court improperly is invalid, which means the woman would remain a married woman in status even after the {\i get} is given, with all the devastating consequences that entails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Dynamics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i Tosafot} elegantly captures much of the dynamic that plays out today in the {\i batei din}. On one side are those who oppose applying any pressure whatsoever; on another are those who hold that in principle the court possesses wide coercive powers \u8212- revoking driving licenses, imposing imprisonment \u8212- though these measures fall short of the {\i beshoti} of physical beatings that characterized earlier enforcement. And then, even among those who accept the principle of {\i kefiyah}, the fear of producing a {\i get me\u8217'useh} \u8212- a coerced and therefore invalid {\i get} \u8212- introduces enormous hesitancy. The woman might then be technically still married, her subsequent relationship adulterous and any children potentially {\i mamzerim}. This is precisely the caution {\i Tosafot} articulates: only when the case is beyond doubt, {\i ad sheyimatze raya berura}, may coercion be applied. So the tension between the imperative to protect the woman and the fear of invalidating the {\i get} is very much a live one, and it is encapsulated here in a single {\i Tosafot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ownership & Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses its central question: {\i ve-keivan de-meshubad lah heichi matzi madar lah} \u8212- since the husband is obligated to her for {\i mezonot}, how can he make a {\i neder} against it? Does he have the power to uproot her {\i shibud}, her lien on those {\i mezonot}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel: Maaseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a parallel from the wife\u8217's side. We have a {\i mishnah} that addresses the case where a wife says {\i konam she-eini osah le-ficha} \u8212- whatever I produce with my hands is forbidden, like {\i hekdesh}, to your mouth. And the ruling is: {\i einu tzarich lehafer} \u8212- the husband does not need to formally annul the {\i neder}. The reason: since the {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} are already subject to his {\i shibud}, the wife has no standing to make them {\i asur} to him. {\i Alma keivan de-meshubda lei lav kol keminei de-mafka lei le-shibudei} \u8212- since she is obligated to him, she cannot simply uproot his claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to clarify what kind of barrier this is. This is not analogous to the principle of {\i mushba ve-omed me-har Sinai}, which prevents a {\i shevua} from contradicting an existing obligation. A {\i shevua} operates on the person \u8212- it is a personal undertaking \u8212- and a prior obligation ({\i mehar Sinai}) can outweigh a later {\i shevua}. A {\i neder}, by contrast, operates on the object, the {\i cheftza}. One can theoretically use a {\i neder} to indirectly circumvent an obligation \u8212- for instance, declaring the {\i hana\u8217'ah} of a {\i sukkah} forbidden to oneself, thereby removing the object rather than contradicting the obligation directly. So the question here is not: does his obligation outweigh the {\i neder}? The question is: who owns the object? A {\i neder} can only operate on one\u8217's own property. If the object already belongs to someone else, you cannot make it {\i asur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: how, by the same logic, can the husband make his {\i mezonot} forbidden to his wife? She has a {\i shibud} on those {\i mezonot} \u8212- they are in some sense hers. And the {\i gemara} responds: {\i al kaminei de-mafka lei le-shibudeih} \u8212- can he simply uproot her {\i shibud}? This is the same objection applied in reverse. So if the {\i neder} cannot work when the object belongs to the other party, how does the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case work at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boss Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer: {\i mitoche she-yachol lomar la tzeii ma\u8217'aseh yadayich bimezonotayich} \u8212- because the husband has the theoretical power to say to her: take your {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} and keep them; I will keep my {\i mezonot}. Since at any moment he could call off the {\i mezonot}\u8211-{\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} arrangement and retain the {\i mezonot} for himself, he is effectively the {\i baal habayit} of those {\i mezonot}. It is as if he already said {\i tzeii ma\u8217'aseh yadayich bimezonotayich}, and therefore the {\i mezonot} are considered his to render {\i asur} by {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Huna Opt-Out\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately complicates this picture. {\i Rav Huna amar Rav}: a woman has the power to say {\i eini nizonos ve-eini osah} \u8212- I will not be fed by you and I will not work for you; I will keep my {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}. So it is not only the husband who can call off the arrangement \u8212- the wife can as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Symmetry Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a symmetry problem. We said the husband can make his {\i mezonot asur} to his wife by {\i neder} because he is the {\i baal habayit} \u8212- he can call off the deal at any time. But if the woman also has the power to call off the deal and keep her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}, why can she not make her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim asur} to the husband? By parallel reasoning, {\i naaseh kemi she-omeret lo eini nizonos ve-eini osah} \u8212- it should be as though she already declared her independence. Why then does the {\i mishnah} rule that her {\i neder} on {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} is invalid and the husband need not even annul it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Potential vs.\u160 ?Actual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension: {\i lo teima naaseh} \u8212- do not say \u8220"it is as if she said.\u8221" The mere potential to call off the arrangement does not suffice. As long as the arrangement is still in place, neither party is yet the {\i baal habayit} in a way that empowers them to forbid the object by {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arrangement Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only way the {\i neder} works is {\i be\u8217'omer la tzeii ma\u8217'aseh yadayich bimezonotayich} \u8212- when the husband has explicitly declared that the arrangement is called off. Only at the moment of explicit declaration does he actually become the {\i baal habayit} of the {\i mezonot}, and only then can he render them {\i asur}. By the same token, if she explicitly says {\i eini nizonos ve-eini osah}, she becomes the {\i baalat habayit} of her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} and could at that point make them {\i asur}. Potential power, not actualized, does not confer ownership for purposes of {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Earnings Threshold\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Major vs.\u160 ?Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further. We now have the position that it only works if he explicitly called off the arrangement. But if he called it off \u8212- if he said {\i tzeii ma\u8217'aseh yadayich bimezonotayich} \u8212- then she is keeping her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} and he is keeping his {\i mezonot}. Why would they need a {\i parnas} at all? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i bidelo sapka} \u8212- in a case where her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} do not suffice to cover her full costs. {\i Rav Ashi} refines this: her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} cover the major expenses but not the minor ones, {\i devarim ktanim}. Therefore the husband would still be obligated for those minor items, and the {\i neder} cannot operate on them, and so a third party is needed to cover that remainder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Expenses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this immediately raises a further difficulty: if she is accustomed to those minor expenses, the husband is obligated for them, and if he is obligated, we are back to square one \u8212- the {\i neder} should not take effect on them. And if she is not accustomed to them, he has no obligation to begin with, the {\i neder} takes effect freely, and no {\i parnas} is needed. In either case, the {\i parnas} seems superfluous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accustomed Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with a three-way analysis: the case involves expenses she is accustomed to in terms of her standard of living, but which fall under a category where her actual obligation status shifts depending on circumstances. This brings us to the more nuanced scenario developed in the next section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Devarim Ktanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Luxury to Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} specifies the scenario: {\i deregila beveit nasha} \u8212- she was accustomed to a higher standard of living in her father\u8217's house. For example, she was accustomed to receiving, say, a weekly manicure. When she marries a husband whose standard of living is lower, the rule of {\i halacha} is {\i olah imo ve-eina yoredet imo} \u8212- she is entitled to rise with him but not to descend with him, meaning she should be maintained at her prior standard. However, she voluntarily accommodates him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mochel Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She is {\i mochelet} \u8212- she waives that entitlement \u8212- {\i kamgalgela bahadeih}, she rolls along with him. As long as they are living together in a functioning marriage, she absorbs the gap between her prior standard and his. That waiver is ongoing and contingent on the relationship\u8217's normalcy. During this period, since she is {\i mochelet}, that cost \u8212- the ten dollars a week for the manicure \u8212- is his money. He is the one who would otherwise be obligated, she has forgone the claim, and the money is therefore his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Breaks Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the husband takes his {\i neder}, everything changes. The {\i neder} introduces a rupture \u8212- a wall \u8212- in the marriage. She says: {\i ad hashta delo adartan, galgelina bahadeich} \u8212- until now that you had not taken a vow against me, I was willing to roll along with you. {\i Hashta de-adartan, lo matzina de-egalgel bahadeich} \u8212- now that you have taken this vow, I will no longer accommodate you. She revokes her {\i mechilah}. She is no longer {\i mochelet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here is the striking paradox, articulated clearly by {\i Tosafot}: at the precise moment the husband takes the {\i neder}, she is still {\i mochelet} \u8212- the revocation has not yet taken effect. Therefore at that exact moment the cost is his money, and the {\i neder} operates legitimately on something that is his. But the instant the {\i neder} takes effect, she is no longer {\i mochelet}, and he is now obligated \u8212- yet he cannot satisfy that obligation because of the {\i neder} he has just taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligation Crisis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result: he is obligated but cannot fulfill his obligation. He cannot directly provide for her \u8212- the {\i neder} prevents it. A third party must step in, or \u8212- if this continues beyond thirty days \u8212- the marriage must end. The {\i neder} has created an obligation it simultaneously prevents him from satisfying.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Thirty Days Public\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-mai shna shloshim yom} \u8212- what is special about thirty days? The {\i gemara} explains: {\i ad shloshim yom lo sham\u8217'ei bah inshi ve-lo zila bah milta} \u8212- for up to thirty days, people have not yet heard about the situation, it has not become public knowledge, and therefore it is not yet embarrassing to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tolerance Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond thirty days \u8212- {\i tfei sham\u8217'ei bah inshi} \u8212- it becomes public that the husband has taken a {\i neder} against his wife, that there is conflict in the marriage, and that the wife is in a degraded position. {\i Zila bah milta} \u8212- the matter becomes humiliating to her. Up to that point, before it becomes a public affair, we can attempt to manage the situation through the {\i parnas} arrangement and hope the {\i neder} is resolved. Once it crosses into public embarrassment, we will no longer tolerate it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arusa Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusa Not Obligated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers an alternative explanation for how the {\i neder} takes effect despite the husband\u8217's obligation. {\i Ei ba\u8217'it eima shehidira keshi\u8217'hi arusa} \u8212- perhaps the husband took the {\i neder} when his wife was still an {\i arusa}, a {\i betrothed} woman, at which stage he had no {\i chiyuv mezonot} to her. Since he had no obligation at that moment, the {\i neder} took effect freely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Year Passes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then how does an obligation arise? {\i Sheyagia zman velo niso} \u8212- once the appointed time for the marriage to take place has arrived and they have still not married, the husband becomes obligated for her {\i mezonot} even though {\i nisuin} has not yet occurred. This is an explicit {\i mishnah}: {\i higia zman velo niso, ochelet mishelo ve-ocheles bitruma} \u8212- once the time arrives, she eats from his resources. So the {\i neder} was taken before the obligation arose, it took effect, and now the obligation kicks in \u8212- but the {\i neder} is already in place and he cannot satisfy it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a sharp question. If the obligation that arises at the {\i arusa} stage \u8212- after the year has passed \u8212- is only {\i derabbanan}, does the {\i neder} actually take effect? The {\i neder} could not take effect once a full obligation attaches. But if the obligation is only rabbinic, perhaps the {\i neder} can override it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi De\u8217'oraissa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} addresses this directly. His comment states: {\i demide\u8217'oraisa lo mishtabed lah, hilkach chal haneder de\u8217'issur de\u8217'oraisa hu, umiderabbanan chayav limzona, hilkach yamid parnas} \u8212- since at the {\i arusa} stage there is no biblical obligation, the {\i neder} takes effect; but there remains a rabbinic obligation, and so a {\i parnas} must be arranged. This is consistent with {\i Rashi}\u8217's well-known position, evident also from his commentary on {\i Chumash}, that {\i mezonot} for a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} is a {\i de\u8217'oraissa} obligation \u8212- derived from {\i she\u8217'era kesuta ve\u8217'onasah lo yigra}, where {\i she\u8217'era} is understood as {\i mezonot}. Once {\i nisuin} occurs, the {\i de\u8217'oraissa} obligation blocks the {\i neder}; before {\i nisuin}, only a rabbinic obligation applies, which does not block it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} object strenuously. {\i Ramban} and {\i Tosafot} rule that {\i mezonot} is {\i derabbanan} even after {\i nisuin}. If so, by {\i Rashi}\u8217's own logic, the {\i neder} should take effect even after {\i nisuin} \u8212- since the obligation is only rabbinic both before and after. {\i Tosafot} therefore reject the framework of {\i de\u8217'oraissa} versus {\i derabbanan} as the operative distinction entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i Tosafot}, the correct analysis, as developed earlier, is that this is not a question of the weight of obligation at all \u8212- it is a question of ownership. The issue with a {\i neder} is not: does my obligation outweigh this {\i neder}? The issue is: do I own the object I am rendering {\i asur}? A {\i neder} of any kind \u8212- whether it would contradict a biblical obligation or merely a rabbinic one \u8212- cannot operate on an object that belongs to someone else. So if his wife owns the {\i mezonot} by virtue of his obligation, {\i even a rabbinic obligation} means the {\i mezonot} are hers and not his, and his {\i neder} cannot reach them. Therefore {\i Tosafot} understand the answer differently: the {\i neder} took effect when she was an {\i arusa} and there was as yet no obligation at all \u8212- neither biblical nor rabbinic \u8212- and the year had not yet elapsed. The {\i neder} was thus operative from that early stage. Once the twelve months pass and the obligation begins, the {\i neder} is already in place and the obligation finds no {\i mezonot} available to attach to.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nisuin Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Follows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents what appears to be the simplest version of the scenario: {\i b\u8217'didah k\u8217'shehi arusa v\u8217'niseis} \u8212- he took the {\i neder} when she was an {\i arusa} and they subsequently actually married. Structurally this is equivalent to the previous answer \u8212- the {\i neder} took effect before any obligation, and the obligation then arose \u8212- but the trigger here is the {\i nisuin} itself rather than the passage of twelve months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acceptance Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a difficulty: {\i niseis, hasabra v\u8217'kibla} \u8212- if she proceeded to the {\i chuppah} with the {\i neder} already in force, she considered the situation and accepted it. She was not compelled into the marriage; she walked in knowingly. Her proceeding to {\i nisuin} under these conditions constitutes an implicit acceptance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Grievance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she accepted, what is the new grievance? Why would there be grounds for {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten kesuvah} at all? She knew what she was getting into. Contrast this with the twelve-months-have-passed scenario: there, she did not agree to anything \u8212- she simply finds herself in a situation where he has an obligation but cannot satisfy it. But if she chose to marry under these conditions, what new claim does she have?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Known Expense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue that she did not fully appreciate how much the {\i neder} would create distance and emotional difficulty \u8212- that being in the same house with this wall between them was something she could not have anticipated. But the {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i eima d\u8217'amrinan hachi gabi mumim} \u8212- we accept the argument \u8220"I thought I could manage but now I find I cannot\u8221" specifically in the case of physical defects, {\i mumim}, where the intimacy of living together reveals something that was impossible to fully appreciate beforehand. But {\i l\u8217'inyan mezonei, mi amrinan hachi} \u8212- regarding {\i mezonot}, she knew exactly what the financial situation would be. There was nothing new to discover. {\i Mah chadash ba alav?} \u8212- what new circumstance arose that she could not have foreseen?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore rules: {\i machvarta ke-deshnin me-ikara} \u8212- the earlier answer is superior. The better construction is the one where she did not walk into the {\i nisuin} with full knowledge and acceptance, i.e., the twelve-months case where the obligation arises without her having agreed to the {\i neder}\u8217's consequences. The scenario of {\i niseis} is rejected.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Third Party Shliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shliach Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the mechanism of the {\i parnas}, the third party who provides for the wife while the husband\u8217's {\i neder} is in effect. The obvious problem: {\i ufarnas lav shlichusei ka-avid} \u8212- is the {\i parnas} not simply acting as the husband\u8217's agent? If so, the wife is still effectively deriving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the husband, routed through this intermediary, and the {\i neder} would be violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Announcement Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna} provides the answer: {\i be\u8217'omer kol hazan eino mafsid} \u8212- the husband does not instruct anyone to provide. Instead, he issues a general announcement: whoever chooses to provide for my wife will not lose out as a result \u8212- meaning, whoever voluntarily feeds her, I will compensate. The person who then comes forward and provides is not acting on the husband\u8217's command; they are acting on their own initiative, and the husband compensates them afterward. Since there was no directive, no {\i shlichut} was created.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific Person\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} probes whether this announcement formula truly avoids {\i shlichut}. After all, the {\i mishnah} in a different context teaches: if a man is thrown into a pit and calls out {\i kol hashome\u8217'a koli yichtov get le-ishto} \u8212- whoever hears my voice should write a {\i get} for my wife \u8212- those who hear may indeed write the {\i get}. So saying {\i kol} \u8212- whoever \u8212- does not prevent the formation of {\i shlichut} when someone opts in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fire Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the {\i deleika} case: when a fire breaks out on {\i Shabbat} and property is at risk, the Rabbis permitted saying {\i kol hamichabeh eino mafsid} \u8212- whoever chooses to extinguish the fire will not lose out. A non-Jew who then extinguishes has not done so as a {\i shaliach}, and no violation of {\i amira le-goi} attaches. This case is directly parallel to our scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 World Announcement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes the {\i get} case from the {\i mezonot} and {\i deleika} cases by focusing on the verb. In the pit case, the husband said {\i yichtov} \u8212- he should write \u8212- which is a directive: whoever opts in is being commanded to perform the action. In the {\i kol hazan eino mafsid} formula, no action is commanded. The husband never says \u8220"go feed my wife\u8221" \u8212- he says only that whoever happens to feed her will be compensated. The action is entirely initiated by the third party. Therefore the {\i parnas} arrangement is not {\i shlichut}, and the wife is not receiving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava Starvation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grocer Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} raises a related case that sharpens the distinctions. Suppose a man has a {\i neder} forbidding him to benefit Michael. Michael is starving. The man goes to the local {\i chanvani} \u8212- the grocer who regularly serves Michael \u8212- and says: {\i ish ploni mudar hana\u8217'ah mimeni ve-eini yodea mah e\u8217'eseh lo} \u8212- so-and-so has taken a vow against me and I don\u8217't know what I can do for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grocer Demands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The grocer, understanding the situation, provides Michael with food. He then turns to the man who approached him and demands payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generic Distress\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This arrangement is permitted because the man never specifically directed the grocer to feed Michael. He merely expressed distress \u8212- a kind of open-ended, unspecified lamentation. The grocer acted entirely on his own inference and initiative. No {\i shlichut} was created because no action was specified or commanded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific to Grocer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the question: but if the man had gone to the grocer and said {\i kol hazan eino mafsid} specifically to this one grocer, would that be more problematic? After all, now you have combined two elements: a specific person (the regular grocer) and a formula that at least implies someone should act. This begins to look more like {\i shlichut} than a generic expression of distress.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Whole World OK\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies: {\i lo mibaya kol hazan eino mafsid de-la-alma ka-amar} \u8212- it goes without saying that saying {\i kol hazan eino mafsid} to the world at large is entirely permitted. When the statement is addressed to no one in particular, it is maximally distant from any {\i shlichut}. The {\i chiddush} of {\i Rava}\u8217's case is that even the more problematic form \u8212- going specifically to this regular grocer and expressing distress \u8212- is also permitted, because you never actually commanded any specific action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combo Problematic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} draws the line clearly: the two elements that would individually each be borderline or permissible become {\i problematic} when combined. Saying {\i kol hazan eino mafsid} to the whole world \u8212- permitted. Expressing unspecified distress to a specific person \u8212- permitted. But going to a specific person \u8212- the grocer \u8212- and saying {\i kol hazan eino mafsid} specifically to him: that combination of a specific individual with a formula that implies an action to be taken would constitute {\i shlichut} and would not be permitted. In each of the permitted cases, at least one of the two problematic elements is absent. It is only when both converge \u8212- specific person and specific implied action \u8212- that the line into genuine {\i shlichut} is crossed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 71\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Third-Party Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow & Interim Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} begins by returning to a topic introduced earlier in the sugya: a man who has made a vow forbidding his wife from deriving benefit from him, and the practical question of how she is to be supported in the interim before the court demands a divorce. Since he is the one who made the vow, the halakha requires that he pay the {\i ketubah} upon divorce. But the more immediate question is: how does he provide for her in the meantime without violating his own vow? The Gemara\u8217's solution involves enlisting a third party who provides for the wife in a way that does not constitute a direct, explicit instruction from the husband \u8212- so that the support is not legally attributable to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nedarim Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara signals a return to an earlier source with the word {\i gufa} \u8212- \u8220"the body of the matter itself\u8221" \u8212- and cites the relevant Mishnah from Nedarim. The case is: {\i ha-mudar hana\u8217'ah me-chavero} \u8212- someone is forbidden by vow from deriving benefit from his friend \u8212- and yet the friend has nothing to eat today. The solution the Mishnah describes is indirect: the one who wishes to help goes to the local shopkeeper, with whom he is accustomed to doing business, and says to him, \u8220"So-and-so has taken a vow and cannot receive benefit from me, and he has nothing to eat \u8212- I don\u8217't know what I can do for him.\u8221" The shopkeeper, picking up on the hint, goes ahead and provides food. When he later returns and reports what he spent, the one who wanted to help gives him the money. The arrangement is deliberately indirect: no explicit instruction was ever given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Provision Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah continues with additional scenarios. Where the person\u8217's house needs to be built, his fence repaired, or his field harvested, the same method is employed: the helper goes to workers he regularly employs and says the same thing \u8212- \u8220"I don\u8217't know what I can do for him.\u8221" The workers go and do the labor, and afterward come back to receive their payment. It is worth noting that the language of the Mishnah \u8212- that they \u8220"take their wages\u8221" \u8212- implies they can actually demand it. In practice, if this arrangement were brought before a court, they likely could not press a formal legal claim, or it would look as though he had simply hired them directly. But within the understood norms of the arrangement, the transaction functions as a voluntary gift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further scenario: the two parties are traveling together and the person under the vow has no food. The solution is to find a third party, give him the food as a gift, and then the person under the vow approaches the third party and asks to share. The Mishnah says this is {\i mutar} \u8212- permitted. And if there is no third party at all? Then one places the food on a rock or wall and declares it {\i hefker} \u8212- ownerless \u8212- \u8220"available to anyone who wishes to take it.\u8221" The only person present is the one under the vow, who comes and takes it. The Mishnah rules this too is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah concludes: {\i Rabbi Yose oser} \u8212- Rabbi Yose forbids this last case. The Gemara explains his reasoning: {\i gzeirah mishum ma\u8217'aseh d\u8217'Beit Choron} \u8212- a rabbinic decree on account of an incident involving a place called Beit Choron, which is itself a Mishnah in Nedarim. In that case, a man\u8217's father was forbidden from deriving benefit from him. Wishing his father to attend his son\u8217's wedding feast, he transferred ownership of all the food to a third party \u8212- so that the food legally belonged to someone else, which would permit the father to eat from it. The third party, recognizing what was being done, promptly declared the food {\i hekdesh} \u8212- consecrated to the Temple \u8212- in effect saying: if you are truly giving this to me so that you can get around your {\i neder}, I will treat it as mine in the fullest sense. The stratagem collapsed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gzerah Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question arises: what exactly is the nature of Rabbi Yose\u8217's concern? It seems clear that Rabbi Yose forbids only this last case \u8212- the {\i hefker} scenario \u8212- and not all of the earlier cases involving third parties. After all, the incident of Beit Choron itself involved a third party. The lesson drawn from Beit Choron is not that genuine third-party arrangements are invalid, but rather that when the maneuver becomes so transparent and so obviously a legal fiction, it cannot be sanctioned. This is precisely what makes the {\i hefker} case different: when there is no one else in a radius of ten miles and you declare the food ownerless with one person standing right there, the entire arrangement crosses the line from a legitimate workaround into what can only be described as a farce. That, it seems, is the core of Rabbi Yose\u8217's {\i gzeirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing here to consider the broader context. Vows in their original conception served a serious religious function \u8212- a way for individuals to structure a more personal religious life, to create self-imposed restrictions analogous to the {\i chumrot} of later generations, except that these were formal legal acts rather than mere pious customs. The Gemara elsewhere compares taking vows to building a {\i bamah} \u8212- a private altar \u8212- a kind of tailored personal religious experience. But as these cases illustrate, once the power to make things forbidden is in one\u8217's hands, it can be weaponized \u8212- used out of spite, used to create barriers between people, used in ways that distort and abuse the original purpose. The cases of the Mishnah show not so much a failure of vow law as an illustration of how any legal mechanism can be put to uses for which it was never intended.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehudah Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah distinguishes between a man who is an ordinary Israelite and a {\i kohen}. For a non-{\i kohen}, the tolerable period is one month; for a {\i kohen}, two months, since divorce for a {\i kohen} is irreversible \u8212- he may not remarry a divorc\u233 ?e. The Gemara notes that Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's position regarding the non-{\i kohen} appears identical to the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's ruling of thirty days. Abaye resolves this: the {\i chiddush} of Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's formulation is the rule regarding the wife of a {\i kohen}. Rava adds a further distinction even with respect to the one-month period: the {\i tanna kamma} specifies thirty days as a fixed number, while Rabbi Yehudah says \u8220"a month,\u8221" which may sometimes be twenty-nine days depending on the calendar. Whether this means the month is always twenty-nine days or that it follows the actual calendar month is a question raised by Tosafot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the core dispute between Rav and Shmuel, which will animate the remainder of the discussion. {\i Amar Rav lo shanu ella bi-mefaresh} \u8212- Rav says: this one-month tolerance applies only when the husband has explicitly specified a duration. If he says the vow lasts fifteen days, or twenty days, or up to a month, we tolerate it. If he specifies more than a month, we demand divorce immediately \u8212- because the {\i neder} was explicitly made for a period exceeding what the marriage can sustain. And if he specifies no duration at all, then it is as though he said forever, and we demand divorce immediately as well. According to Rav, then, the question of whether to wait or to act is entirely determined by what the husband explicitly declared at the time of the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel takes a fundamentally different position. {\i Afilu bi\u8217'stam lo yotzi} \u8212- even when the vow is {\i stam}, made without any specified duration and therefore effectively forever, we do not demand divorce immediately. The one-month period mentioned in the Mishnah is not a description of how long the vow was made for, but rather a description of how long we will wait before demanding a divorce \u8212- regardless of the vow\u8217's stated duration. During that month, we give the husband an opportunity: {\i shema yimtza pesach l\u8217'nidro} \u8212- perhaps he will find a {\i pesach}, an opening, through which a {\i chacham} can release him from the vow. The mechanism of {\i hataras nedarim} was not a formality; it required a genuine substantive basis \u8212- a realization that had one foreseen certain circumstances, the vow would never have been made. But it was a real possibility. Shmuel thus frames the Mishnah\u8217's time periods not as assessments of the vow\u8217's duration but as a window for resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Articulation Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection: {\i ve-ha-ifligu bei chada zimna} \u8212- haven\u8217't Rav and Shmuel already debated this same question in the context of an earlier Mishnah? That Mishnah discussed a man who forbids his wife from marital relations \u8212- {\i tashmish ha-mitah} \u8212- by a vow. There too the same dispute appears: Rav holds that if the vow was indefinite, he must divorce immediately; Shmuel holds that we wait even so, up to a week or two weeks, to see if he can get out of it. Why must the debate be stated in both places?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i tzricha} \u8212- both are necessary. Had we only known their dispute regarding the {\i tashmish} case, one might have thought that Rav\u8217's stringency \u8212- demanding immediate divorce when the vow is indefinite \u8212- is specific to that case, because there is no third party who can substitute for the prohibited intimacy. Every day of waiting is a day in which she is denied something that cannot be provided another way. But perhaps Shmuel would agree with Rav in this case and reserve his lenient position only for cases of {\i mezonot}, where a third party can provide for her in the interim. Conversely, had we only heard the {\i mezonot} debate, one might have thought that Shmuel\u8217's leniency \u8212- waiting to see if a {\i pesach} can be found \u8212- applies only there, since she can be provided for otherwise, but that he would agree with Rav in the {\i tashmish} case where no substitute exists. Therefore the Gemara must state the dispute in both contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex vs.\u160 ?Sustenance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a precise articulation of the underlying logic. According to Rav, the distinction between cases is irrelevant \u8212- what matters is only the stated duration of the vow. According to Shmuel, even in the {\i tashmish} case, we give some time: one week according to Beit Shammai, two weeks according to Beit Hillel. The temporal thresholds differ between the sex case and the {\i mezonot} case \u8212- a week or two in one, a month or two months in the other \u8212- but the structural logic is the same in both.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We turn now to the second part of the Mishnah, which at first glance presents the husband as making a vow that his wife cannot benefit from a certain fruit or use a certain perfume. This raises an immediate question: how can a husband make a vow forbidding his wife from eating kumquats, for instance? He has no direct power to make her food forbidden. The mechanism must be indirect. What he can say is something like: \u8220"If you eat a kumquat, then the pleasure of sex with me will be forbidden to you\u8221" \u8212- or the pleasure of the {\i mezonot} he provides. He cannot directly forbid the kumquat by vow, but he can construct a conditional structure in which a vow he {\i can} make \u8212- about his own benefit \u8212- is triggered by her performing a certain action. Effectively, by making her access to marital intimacy or sustenance contingent on her avoiding that fruit, he has made the fruit practically off-limits for her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simple Reading Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this is the simple reading of the Mishnah \u8212- that the husband made this conditional vow \u8212- then one understands why they must divorce. But a difficulty arises when the Gemara\u8217's debate between Rav and Shmuel is brought to bear. The Mishnah here says divorce immediately, not after a month. According to Rav, this is easily explained: the vow was {\i stam} \u8212- no duration was specified \u8212- so there is no waiting period. But according to Shmuel, who holds that even a {\i stam} vow gets a month\u8217's waiting period, why here is there no waiting? That is the Gemara\u8217's question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve this difficulty, Shmuel is forced to reread the Mishnah in a way that departs from its literal surface. Instead of the husband having made the vow, the Gemara will say: she made the vow, and his role was to affirm it \u8212- either actively or silently, by not annulling it during the window in which he had the power to do so. Since he had the power to annul her vow and chose not to, he effectively made the vow binding. On this reading, the question becomes: who is to blame?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rishonim Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reframing creates its own difficulties. If she made the vow and he affirmed it \u8212- and we are no longer talking about a vow that directly forbids him from providing her with anything \u8212- why must they divorce at all? And if they do divorce, why does he pay the {\i ketubah}? Tosafot adds yet another layer to the Mishnah, suggesting that her vow was formulated specifically as conditional on their marriage: \u8220"Such-and-such fruit is forbidden to me as long as we are married.\u8221" Divorce thus releases her from the vow. But this still seems strange \u8212- is an inability to eat a certain fruit really grounds for divorce? The Ran takes a different approach: the divorce is not really about the practical deprivation at all. When the husband silently affirmed \u8212- or actively confirmed \u8212- this restriction on his wife, he communicated something about the emotional state of the marriage. His willingness to let the vow stand, to not protect her from it, functions as a symbol of indifference or distance. It is not the kumquat; it is what the affirmation of the vow says about their relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defense of Simple\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite these Rishonic efforts, the cleanest and most straightforward reading of the Mishnah remains the one that takes its language at face value: the husband made the vow \u8212- indirectly, through the conditional structure described above. This explains why they must divorce \u8212- because the vow effectively makes marital life untenable \u8212- and it explains why he pays the {\i ketubah} \u8212- because it was his vow. It does not require the linguistic gymnastics of positing that she made the vow, and it does not require the interpretive layering of Tosafot or the symbolic reading of the Ran. The simpler reading is clearly the better reading. The Gemara\u8217's move to the alternative reading is driven entirely by the need to accommodate Shmuel\u8217's position \u8212- not by any inherent strength of that reading.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Who Made Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immediate Divorce Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the framework now set, the Gemara proceeds to work through the specific cases in the Mishnah. The Mishnah states that if a man takes a vow forbidding his wife from benefiting from any fruit, or forbidding her the use of a certain perfume, he must divorce her immediately and pay the {\i ketubah}. The Gemara asks: why immediately? The earlier part of the Mishnah established that we wait a month before demanding divorce. Why here is it immediate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's answer is clean and direct: {\i kan bistama kan bimfarash} \u8212- there it speaks of a case where a duration was explicitly specified, here it speaks of a {\i stam} vow, one made without any stated duration. When the vow has no time limit, it is effectively forever, and therefore the Mishnah demands immediate divorce. There is no tension in Rav\u8217's reading: the one-month tolerance applies only to vows with a defined, acceptable duration; a vow of indefinite scope admits no waiting period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Shmuel, however, there is a serious problem. According to Shmuel, the time period in the Mishnah is always about how long we wait, not about how long the vow was made for. Both cases are {\i stam} \u8212- both involve indefinite vows \u8212- yet the Mishnah treats them differently. The first case gets a month; this case gets immediate divorce. Shmuel must explain that difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Made Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's solution, as noted above, is to reread the Mishnah: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan \u8212- k\u8217'she-nadrah hi ve-kiyem la ihu} \u8212- here we are dealing with a case where she made the vow and he affirmed it. He had the opportunity to annul it within the first day and did not. The Gemara notes that this reading attributes the {\i stam} Mishnah to Rabbi Meir, and Rabbi Meir holds: {\i hu nosein etzba bein shineha} \u8212- he put the finger between her teeth. The colorful idiom asks: who poked whom? Did he poke her in the teeth, or did she poke herself? In other words, who bears the blame?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of blame has real {\i halakhic} consequences: if she made the vow and he affirmed it, and then they divorce, does he pay the {\i ketubah} \u8212- implying it is his fault \u8212- or does she forfeit the {\i ketubah} \u8212- implying it is her fault? The Gemara indicates that even if she made the vow, since he had the power to annul it and did not, he bears sufficient responsibility that he must pay the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for why there is no waiting period in this case, Rashi explains: when he makes the vow, we assume he acted in anger and we give him time to cool off and find a {\i pesach}. But when she made the vow and he was calmly {\i mekayem} it \u8212- silently affirming it \u8212- there is no assumption of hot-headed anger to be resolved. That dynamic, as the Gemara will elaborate later, is more troubling and more urgent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now challenges the attribution of the Mishnah\u8217's principle to Rabbi Meir by bringing a {\i baraita}. {\i Ha-ishah she-nadrah b\u8217'nazir, v\u8217'shama ba\u8217'alah v\u8217'lo hefer lah} \u8212- a woman took a nazirite vow and her husband heard and did not annul it. Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehudah say: {\i hi nasnah etzba bein shineha} \u8212- she put her own finger in her teeth, she brought this on herself. Therefore, if he subsequently declares that he does not want a woman who makes vows \u8212- {\i ee efshi b\u8217'ishah nadranis} \u8212- and seeks a divorce, {\i teitzei b\u8217'lo ketubah} \u8212- she leaves without a {\i ketubah}, since the blame rests on her. Rabbi Yossi and Rabbi Elazar say the opposite: {\i hu nasan etzba bein shineha} \u8212- he put his finger in her teeth. He could have annulled the vow; he chose not to. If he now claims he cannot tolerate such a wife, he must divorce her {\i and} pay the {\i ketubah}: {\i yotzi v\u8217'yitein ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita}, then, directly contradicts the Gemara\u8217's assumption. If Rabbi Meir holds that it is her fault \u8212- {\i hi nasnah} \u8212- then how can our Mishnah, which is presumed to follow Rabbi Meir, hold that when she made the vow and he affirmed it, he pays the {\i ketubah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts several solutions, each involving a rearrangement of the names in the {\i baraita} to reconcile it with the Mishnah. First, it suggests simply reversing the attributions: let Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehudah be the ones who say {\i hu nasan} \u8212- he is to blame \u8212- and Rabbi Yossi and Rabbi Elazar be the ones who say {\i hi nasnah} \u8212- she is to blame. But this too creates a problem, because Rabbi Yossi appears explicitly in our Mishnah accepting the premise that the husband pays the {\i ketubah} \u8212- which implies he holds {\i hu nasan}. If we reassign him to the {\i hi nasnah} camp, he contradicts himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then tries mixing the pairs: let Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yossi say {\i hu nasan}, and Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Elazar say {\i hi nasnah}. But Rabbi Yehudah also appears explicitly in the Mishnah on the side of {\i hu nasan}, and so this too fails. At this point the Gemara considers a three-against-one formulation \u8212- Rabbi Meir, Rabbi Yehudah, and Rabbi Yossi all say {\i hu nasan}, and only Rabbi Elazar says {\i hi nasnah} \u8212- but the Gemara recognizes the absurdity of rewriting a {\i baraita} that was clearly structured as two against two into a three-against-one debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final resolution the Gemara offers is to maintain the two-against-two structure but reassign the pairs: Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Elazar say {\i hi nasnah} \u8212- she is to blame \u8212- and Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Yossi say {\i hu nasan} \u8212- he is to blame. Since Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Yossi are explicitly named in the Mishnah as holding the {\i hu nasan} position, they can stay there. And as for the {\i stam} Mishnah being attributed to Rabbi Meir \u8212- the Gemara concludes: {\i ve-stama de-lo ke-Rabbi Meir} \u8212- the {\i stam} Mishnah here does not follow Rabbi Meir. This is an unusual concession, but the Gemara accepts it to preserve internal consistency.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vow Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Innuy Nefesh Threshold\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that Shmuel reads the Mishnah as a case where she made the vow and he affirmed it, the Gemara now faces a further challenge regarding Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position. The Gemara asks: if that is how we read the Mishnah, then we are implicitly saying the husband had the power to annul her vow. But what category of vow is this? The Torah specifies two categories that the husband can annul. The first is {\i nidrei innuy nefesh} \u8212- vows of self-affliction. The second, derived from the phrase {\i bein ish l\u8217'ishto}, encompasses vows that come between husband and wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a Mishnah in Nedarim that defines which vows fall into the {\i innuy nefesh} category. The {\i tanna kamma} lists vows about bathing or not bathing, about wearing jewelry or not wearing jewelry. Rabbi Yossi, however, is stringent about the threshold: {\i ein eilu nidrei innuy nefesh} \u8212- these do not meet the standard of {\i innuy nefesh}. What does? Not eating meat, not drinking wine, not adorning oneself in {\i bigdei tzevonim} \u8212- colored garments. In other words, according to Rabbi Yossi, relatively minor deprivations \u8212- like not wearing jewelry or not using perfume \u8212- do not rise to the level of {\i innuy nefesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Annulment Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a sharp difficulty for Shmuel\u8217's reading of the Mishnah. Our Mishnah involves a vow about perfume and jewelry-type adornment. According to Rabbi Yossi, these are not {\i innuy nefesh} vows. But Shmuel\u8217's reading of the Mishnah presupposes that the husband was in a position to annul the vow \u8212- that is why his failure to do so makes him responsible. If the vow is not {\i innuy nefesh}, can he still annul it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer turns on the second category: {\i bein ish l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- vows that interfere with the marital relationship. The distinction between the two categories is practically significant. If a vow falls under {\i innuy nefesh}, the husband\u8217's annulment works universally \u8212- it removes the prohibition for everyone. If a vow falls only under {\i bein ish l\u8217'ishto}, the husband\u8217's annulment works only to the extent necessary to remove the barrier between them specifically; it does not release her from the prohibition vis-\u224 ?-vis the rest of the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beino L\u8217'Veinah Annul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the difficulty: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan \u8212- bidvarim she-beino l\u8217'veinah} \u8212- here we are talking about vows that fall in the {\i beino l\u8217'veinah} category. Even though Rabbi Yossi holds that these perfume and adornment vows are not {\i innuy nefesh}, they are still in the {\i beino l\u8217'veinah} category, which the husband can still annul. Rabbi Yossi is not saying the husband is powerless to annul these vows; he is merely downgrading them from one category to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises the position of Rav Adda bar Abba, who holds that {\i beino l\u8217'veinah} vows cannot be annulled at all \u8212- {\i ein ha-ba\u8217'al meifer}. He supports this with the adage: {\i shelo matzinu shual she-mes b\u8217'afar pir} \u8212- we have never seen a fox die in the dust of its own burrow. The implication is that domestic matters of this nature, which fall short of true {\i innuy nefesh}, are things people can manage within a marriage; they won\u8217't be destroyed by them. Tosafot clarifies that this is not a debate about whether the husband can annul {\i beino l\u8217'veinah} vows \u8212- that principle is taken as axiomatic \u8212- but rather a debate internal to Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position: when he said these things are not {\i innuy nefesh}, did he mean they are entirely beneath the threshold of any annulment, or merely that they fall into the lesser {\i beino l\u8217'veinah} category?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one takes the stricter view \u8212- that according to Rabbi Yossi these vows cannot be annulled at all \u8212- then Shmuel\u8217's reading of the Mishnah collapses, because it assumes the husband had the power to annul and chose not to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kahana Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore pivots to yet another reading. The Gemara cites the statement of Rav Kahana: {\i hana\u8217'at tashmishi alecha} \u8212- if the wife says \u8220"the pleasure of my marital relations is forbidden to you,\u8221" {\i kopeh u\u8217'meshamasht\u8217'o} \u8212- we compel her to be with him, or at least the vow is unenforceable, because she cannot forbid him what he is entitled to. But if she says {\i hana\u8217'at tashmishcha alai} \u8212- \u8220"the pleasure of your marital relations is forbidden to me\u8221" \u8212- that is an indirect formulation, and the vow does bind. In that case, {\i yeifer} \u8212- he must annul it \u8212- because the alternative is either they transgress by having relations while forbidden, or they refrain from relations, neither of which is acceptable. This vow clearly falls under both {\i innuy nefesh} and {\i beino l\u8217'veinah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reframe Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rav Kahana\u8217's principle established, the Gemara introduces a new and final reading of the Mishnah that resolves the remaining difficulties. The case is not really about perfume or fruit at all \u8212- not directly. What the Mishnah is describing is a vow structured as follows: {\i ye\u8217'aser hana\u8217'at tashmishcha alai im etkashat} \u8212- \u8220"the benefit of having sex with you will be forbidden to me if I adorn myself with jewelry.\u8221" The adornment is merely the condition; the substance of the vow is about marital relations. This is precisely the type of vow Rav Kahana discusses: an indirect but effective prohibition on {\i tashmish}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading resolves all the difficulties simultaneously. It explains why they must divorce \u8212- because the vow puts marital relations at risk, not merely a luxury food item. It explains why the husband can annul the vow \u8212- because it is unambiguously {\i innuy nefesh} and {\i beino l\u8217'veinah} when the ultimate subject is {\i tashmish}. And it explains why getting divorced actually helps \u8212- because the vow is structured to be triggered by something that is inevitable in normal life. The Gemara notes: if she does not adorn herself, she will be publicly embarrassed \u8212- {\i karu lah menuveles} \u8212- and the social pressure makes it inevitable that eventually she will put on the jewelry and the prohibition will take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Calm Affirmation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But one difficulty remains: why is there no waiting period? Even once the vow takes effect on {\i tashmish}, why not give them a week or two, as the Mishnah\u8217's earlier section suggests?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Emotional Dynamics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i hani milei heicha d\u8217'adrah ihu} \u8212- the waiting period applies when he made the vow. In that case, she reasons: {\i mirtach rasach alai} \u8212- he was furious at me; he\u8217'll calm down. She can tolerate a week or two because she understands it as an episode of anger that will pass. But in our case: {\i aval hacha d\u8217'nadrah ihi, ve-shtik lah} \u8212- here she made the vow and he was silent. He calmly affirmed it. The subject of the vow is marital relations, and he \u8212- silently, without anger \u8212- let it stand. Her conclusion: {\i savrah mid\u8217'ishtik, misna hu d\u8217'sani li} \u8212- from the fact that he was quiet, she infers he must hate her. The emotional distance created by his calm acquiescence is far more damaging than the heat of an angry vow. It creates an immediate and unbridgeable rupture \u8212- and therefore the Mishnah demands immediate action rather than a waiting period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Shmuel Reconcile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this stage, the approaches of Rav and Shmuel can be fully reconciled within the Mishnah\u8217's framework. According to Rav, the husband made the conditional vow \u8212- indirectly, about sex \u8212- and since it was made without a specified duration, divorce is required immediately. According to Shmuel, she made the vow about sex, he affirmed it calmly and without anger, and the emotional dynamics of that scenario make waiting impossible. Both positions, through different paths, arrive at the same outcome: immediate divorce with full payment of the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Duration Thresholds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah concludes with Rabbi Yossi\u8217's distinction between poor women and wealthy women with respect to the perfume case. For poor women, the vow is intolerable only if it has no stated limit at all; if a duration was specified, even a long one, we tolerate it. For wealthy women \u8212- {\i isha chashuvah} \u8212- even a month is intolerable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limit Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately asks: what exactly does \u8220"no stated limit\u8221" mean? Surely a vow made for twenty years has a stated limit, yet no one would say it is tolerable. At what point does a stated but very long duration become the equivalent of having no limit?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time Standards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three {\i amoraim} offer their standards. Rav Yehudah in the name of Shmuel: twelve months. More than a year is considered tantamount to having no limit. Rabbah bar bar Chana in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: ten years \u8212- a dramatically more lenient threshold. Rav Chisda in the name of Avimi: {\i regel} \u8212- until the next festival. His reasoning: {\i sheken bnot Yisrael miskashetos ba-regel} \u8212- Jewish women adorn themselves for {\i Yom Tov}. To deprive a woman of the ability to adorn herself even for the festival is intolerable even for the poor, and therefore if the vow extends beyond the nearest {\i regel}, it is effectively without limit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 30-Day Threshold\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then turns to the wealthy woman\u8217's one-month threshold. Why specifically thirty days? The Gemara asks: {\i peshita} \u8212- is this not obvious? And responds: {\i mai shana l\u8217'shloshim yom} \u8212- what is it about thirty days? Abaye explains: {\i sheken isha chashuvah nehenes me-rei\u8217'ach kishuteiha l\u8217'shloshim yom} \u8212- a woman of means still enjoys the residual fragrance of her perfume for up to thirty days. Beyond thirty days, even the lingering scent has dissipated. The Gemara thus makes explicit that the subject here is indeed perfume, and the thirty-day threshold corresponds precisely to the point at which even the most generous residual benefit of the perfume has been fully exhausted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 72\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's House Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on 71b introduces a case in which a man takes a vow preventing his wife from visiting her father\u8217's house. The mechanism by which he does so is worth pausing on: since a husband cannot directly obligate his wife through a vow, the operative structure is a conditional arrangement \u8212- something along the lines of \u8220"the pleasure of intimacy with you is forbidden to me if you go to your father\u8217's house.\u8221" The effect is that the condition makes it impossible for her to go without triggering the vow. The scenario has an obvious domestic resonance: whether he simply does not wish to visit his in-laws, or feels that his wife has not fully acclimated to her new home, the {\i mishnah} treats this as a recognizable, if problematic, marital situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same City Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} distinguishes between cases based on where the wife\u8217's parents live. If the father\u8217's house is in the same city \u8212- where more frequent visits would be the natural expectation \u8212- then {\i chodesh echad yakayem, shnayim yotzi}: a vow lasting one month is tolerable and the marriage may continue, but beyond that he must divorce her. The question of whether the vow itself specifies \u8220"one month\u8221" or is indefinite with the {\i beit din} determining how long to allow it to stand is left open, but the practical threshold is clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different City Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the parents live in a different city, the {\i mishnah} measures tolerance in {\i regalim} \u8212- pilgrimage festivals. {\i Regel echad yakayem, shelosha yotzi v\u8217'yiten ktubah}: up to one {\i regel} is sustainable; three consecutive {\i regalim} during which she cannot visit her father is unreasonable, and he must divorce her and pay her {\i ktubah}. This immediately raises the obvious question: what about two {\i regalim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Internal Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses on the internal tension: the opening clause implies that two {\i regalim} is the point of intolerability (since one is tolerable, {\i ha shnayim yotzi}), yet the closing clause implies that two is still manageable ({\i ha shnayim yakayem}, since three is the threshold). These cannot both be right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves this by distinguishing between two types of wives: {\i birdufa} \u8212- a woman who is prone to frequent visits, who constantly wants to return to her parents\u8217' home \u8212- and {\i she\u8217'eina redufa}, one who visits only occasionally. For the woman who constantly runs to her parents, two {\i regalim} is already intolerable; for the woman who visits only occasionally, the threshold is three. The distinction carries an additional layer of social reality. Given how young these women often were when they married, one can understand both why a husband might grow frustrated with a wife who never seems settled in her new home, and why the restriction on her visits is correspondingly more oppressive for such a woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's colleague Rava bar Ulla offers an alternative resolution: the difference between two and three {\i regalim} corresponds to a distinction drawn by Rabbi Yehuda between a woman married to a {\i yisrael} and one married to a {\i kohen}. Since divorce from a {\i kohen} is irreversible \u8212- a {\i kohen} may not remarry a divorc\u233 ?e \u8212- the rabbis are more reluctant to compel divorce in that case and therefore extend the tolerance to three {\i regalim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midrash Interlude\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} introduces a {\i midrashic} interlude citing the verse from {\i Shir HaShirim}: {\i az hayisi b\u8217'einav k\u8217'motzet shalom} \u8212- \u8220"then I was in his eyes like one who found favor.\u8221" Rabbi Yochanan interprets this as describing a bride who is at peace in her father-in-law\u8217's household \u8212- meaning her husband\u8217's home \u8212- and yet still feels drawn to return and share her happiness with her family: {\i redufa leilech lehagid shivchah b\u8217'veit aviha}. This may be setting up a contrast with the previous case: not every wife who wishes to visit her parents is doing so because she has failed to settle into married life. A woman can be genuinely content with her husband and still feel a strong, legitimate pull toward her parents. The {\i gemara} here acknowledges that longing as real and natural.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ish vs.\u160 ?Ba\u8217'al\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hosea Prophecy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this {\i midrashic} passage the {\i gemara} transitions to a verse in Hosea: {\i v\u8217'haya bayom hahu ne\u8217'um Hashem tikre\u8217'i ishi v\u8217'lo tikre\u8217'i li od ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- \u8220"On that day, says God, you will call Me \u8216'my man\u8217' and you will no longer call Me \u8216'my master.\u8217'\u8221" The plain meaning of the verse\u8217's conclusion, {\i vahasiroti et shem hab\u8217'alim mipihah} \u8212- \u8220"I will remove the name of the {\i ba\u8217'alim} from your mouth\u8221" \u8212- refers to the elimination of idolatrous associations, since the {\i ba\u8217'alim} were Canaanite deities. The prophetic vision is of a purified worship of God, free from any echo of idolatry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'al Mastery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the verse also says something about the nature of marriage. The word {\i ba\u8217'al} carries a double valence: it means master, and it is also the term for sexual possession \u8212- {\i bo\u8217'alaich osaich}, \u8220"He who possesses you made you.\u8221" The relationship structured around {\i ba\u8217'al} is one of male dominance in which even sexuality can be an expression of mastery. {\i Ish} and {\i ishah}, by contrast, suggest equality \u8212- man and woman \u8212- in the same register.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Feminist Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse\u8217's call to say {\i ishi} rather than {\i ba\u8217'ali} speaks directly to how one conceptualizes the marital relationship. Contemporary readers attuned to questions of gender equality find in this verse a powerful expression of partnership \u8212- {\i ish} and {\i ishah} standing on equal footing rather than the asymmetry of master and wife. The contrast is not merely linguistic but structural: saying \u8220"my man\u8221" rather than \u8220"my master\u8221" reflects an entirely different understanding of what marriage is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Union\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan articulates the verse\u8217's application as follows: {\i kekallah b\u8217'veit chamia v\u8217'lo kekallah b\u8217'veit aviha} \u8212- like a bride who is at home in her husband\u8217's household, not one who is perpetually drawn back to her father\u8217's house. The connection to {\i ishi} rather than {\i ba\u8217'ali} is not entirely transparent, but Rashi clarifies: the language of {\i ishut} and {\i nisu\u8217'in} evokes the model in {\i Bereishit} of {\i al kein ya\u8217'azov ish et aviv v\u8217'et imo v\u8217'davak b\u8217'ishto} \u8212- a man leaves his father and mother and cleaves to his wife, the two becoming one unit. {\i Ba\u8217'al} may connote mastery and sexual possession, but it does not capture that idea of genuine union. Rashi adds: the bride who has already married feels at ease with her husband and is no longer embarrassed before him. That ease, that sense of being genuinely at home, is what {\i ish} and {\i ishah} point toward.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Weddings & Funerals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i hamadir ishto shelo telech l\u8217'veit ha\u8217'evel o l\u8217'veit hamishte} \u8212- a man who vows to prevent his wife from attending a house of mourning or a house of festivity, which is understood to mean a wedding. In either case, {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten ktubah} \u8212- he must divorce her and pay her {\i ktubah}. No grace period is specified, presumably because the occasion of a funeral is unpredictable: one never knows when it will arise, and the vow is therefore immediately intolerable. The {\i mishnah} provides the reason: {\i mipnei sheno\u8217'el bifaneha} \u8212- he is locking the door in her face, cutting her off from normal social and communal life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Locking Door Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} accepts this reasoning without difficulty regarding the wedding: clearly, preventing a woman from attending celebrations closes off a dimension of normal social pleasure. But the {\i gemara} presses on the case of the house of mourning: {\i mai ne\u8217'ul bifaneha ika} \u8212- what \u8220"locking of the door\u8221" is involved in preventing her from attending a {\i beit ha\u8217'evel}? Going to a house of mourning is not a pleasure; in what sense is she being deprived?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocal Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} responds: {\i lemachar hi meita v\u8217'ein kol beryah sofedatah} \u8212- tomorrow she will die and no one will eulogize her. There is a reciprocal social fabric involved in attending funerals. The woman who is prevented from being present for others will find, when her own time comes, that no one comes for her. And some say the consequence is even starker: not merely that no one will eulogize her, but that no one will want to bury her. The harm, in other words, is not only spiritual but concretely social: it damages her standing in the community and severs the bonds of mutual obligation on which communal life depends.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} then cites Rabbi Meir interpreting the verse in {\i Kohelet}: {\i tov lalechet el beit ha\u8217'evel milalechet el beit hamishte ba\u8217'asher hu sof kol ha\u8217'adam v\u8217'hachai yitein el libo} \u8212- \u8220"Better to go to the house of mourning than to the house of rejoicing, for that is the end of all people, and the living should take it to heart.\u8221" What should one take to heart while at the house of mourning?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Funeral Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir spells out the reciprocal calculus in stark terms: {\i desapad yesafdunihu} \u8212- one who eulogizes will be eulogized; {\i dekavar yikvrunihu} \u8212- one who buries will be buried; {\i de\u8217'adli yadlunihu} \u8212- one who wails will be wailed over; {\i delavay yalvunuhu} \u8212- one who accompanies a coffin will be accompanied; {\i detaan yiteanunihu} \u8212- one who serves as a pallbearer will have pallbearers at his own burial. The deeper meaning of {\i v\u8217'hachai yitein el libo} is not merely memento mori \u8212- though certainly the verse means that too \u8212- but an instruction about the obligations of communal solidarity. Attending to the dead is not sentiment; it is the fabric of mutual responsibility without which no community can function.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Legitimate Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception Clause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} includes an important qualification: {\i v\u8217'im haya toen mishum davar acher rashay} \u8212- if he has another legitimate reason for the vow, it is permissible. This is a striking move: {\i chazal} are not simply adjudicating tolerability but making a more nuanced judgment about whether the harm to the wife is justified by competing considerations. The implication is that sometimes restrictions that would otherwise be intolerable can become acceptable when there is genuine cause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Promiscuity Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel identifies what counts as a legitimate other reason: {\i mishum benei adam perutzim she-metzuyim sham} \u8212- because there are people of promiscuous or morally loose character present at those gatherings. The husband\u8217's concern is not that he suspects his wife of anything specific, but that he objects to the environment and the company to which she would be exposed. Even granting that concern, the question remains: how much weight does his claim carry?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi sets a threshold: {\i lo amran ela de\u8217'itchazek} \u8212- this is only valid if the presence of such people at those gatherings has been objectively established. {\i Aval lo itchazek lo kol kemineih} \u8212- if it has not been established, his claim carries no weight. He cannot simply assert that the crowd is morally compromised; there must be some external, objective basis for that claim. This standard is important precisely because it prevents a jealous or controlling husband from manufacturing a pretext to isolate his wife. The distinction between tolerability and justifiability is thus not simply a matter of his subjective feeling, but is subject to some degree of communal verification.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Silence & Absurd Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secrets Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} lists two further cases requiring divorce with {\i ktubah}. The first: {\i al menat shetomri l\u8217'ploni mah she\u8217'amart li o mah she\u8217'amarti lach} \u8212- a vow conditioned on her revealing to a third party either what she said to her husband or what he told her in private. Whatever she said to him privately, she must now disclose to someone else, or else remain bound by the vow\u8217's consequences. This too requires divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shame Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel explains: {\i devarim shel kalon} \u8212- these are shameful matters, things that ought not to be disclosed. At minimum, the disclosure brings shame upon the one who originally spoke those words. Private marital communication has an inherent dignity that is violated when one spouse is compelled to broadcast it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meaningless Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: {\i o shetehei memale\u8217'ah um\u8217'arah l\u8217'ashpah} \u8212- or that she should fill things and spill them into the {\i ashpah}, the dunghill. This too requires {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten ktubah}. But what does it mean? The plain reading suggests some absurd, repetitive labor. However, the {\i gemara}\u8217's understanding is more specific.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel explains: {\i shetimaleh v\u8217'nofetzet} \u8212- the filling and spilling refers to intercourse: the husband ejaculates, her body is \u8220"filled,\u8221" and she then washes out the semen, preventing pregnancy. This is the {\i gemara}\u8217's primary reading: the husband is compelling his wife to ensure she cannot conceive. Even if a woman is not technically obligated in the commandment of {\i pru u\u8217'rvu}, she possesses the right to have children. Forcing her to prevent her own pregnancy \u8212- especially if she desires children \u8212- is an intolerable invasion. The phrase {\i memale\u8217'ah um\u8217'arah} is thus understood as a euphemistic description of this practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita}, however, presents a different version: {\i shetimaleh asarah kadei mayim v\u8217'saara l\u8217'ashpah} \u8212- fill ten jugs of water and pour them out into the dunghill. Pure, pointless labor. No preventative function, just meaningless repetition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foolish Appearance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this second reading, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i v\u8217'sa\u8217'avid} \u8212- so let her do it. What is so catastrophic about having to perform useless work? The first reading is clearly worse because of its impact on the possibility of pregnancy. But the second \u8212- filling and spilling water \u8212- seems merely tedious. Rabbah bar bar Chanah citing Rabbi Yochanan answers: {\i mipnei shenira\u8217'it k\u8217'shotah} \u8212- because it makes her appear to be a crazy woman. The public dimension matters: she is made to look foolish in the eyes of the community, and this is sufficient grounds for divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Domestic Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting what the {\i gemara} does not quite say but what the phenomenon clearly implies. The pattern of compelling a wife to perform absurd, meaningless tasks \u8212- completely at the husband\u8217's whim, simply because he demands it \u8212- is a recognizable structure of domestic control. Those familiar with patterns of marital abuse will recognize that hyper-controlling behavior of this kind \u8212- making a spouse perform arbitrary, demeaning acts \u8212- is not merely a question of appearances. It reflects a dynamic of domination that often precedes or accompanies other forms of abuse. The {\i gemara}\u8217's reasoning focuses on the public aspect, {\i nira\u8217'it k\u8217'shotah}, but the underlying reality it addresses is something more troubling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Borrowing & Lending\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessel Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana introduces a further case: {\i hamadir ishto shelo tishal v\u8217'shelo tash\u8217'il} \u8212- a man who makes a vow (or, as {\i Tosafot} notes, perhaps here a direct vow may function) preventing his wife from borrowing certain vessels or from lending them out. The vessels specified are a {\i nafa} (sieve), a {\i kivra} (a different type of sieve), a {\i reikayim} (millstone), and a {\i tanur} (oven) \u8212- all portable household implements of the kind regularly exchanged between neighbors. This too requires {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten ktubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bad Reputation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\i mishum she-mashi shem ra bishcheinosha} \u8212- because it gives her a bad reputation among her neighbors. A woman who never lends the cup of flour, who never lets anyone use her sieve, is seen as unfriendly, selfish, closed off from the normal give-and-take of communal life. This social standing matters, and the husband who creates that situation through his vow is the one at fault.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} teaches the same principle in the mirror case: {\i hamadir ishto shelo tash\u8217'il v\u8217'shelo tishal} \u8212- even preventing her from borrowing creates a bad reputation. One might think that never asking neighbors for anything is neutral or even virtuous \u8212- not imposing on others. But the {\i braita} rejects this: borrowing is itself part of community life. The woman who never asks her neighbors for anything is perceived as distant, and that too damages her standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clothing Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} extends this further: {\i v\u8217'shelo ta\u8217'arog begadim na\u8217'im livaneha} \u8212- if the wife herself vowed not to weave fine clothes for his children from a previous marriage. Weaving fine clothes for stepchildren is not among the wife\u8217's formal legal obligations. Yet the {\i braita} rules {\i teitzei shelo b\u8217'ketuva} \u8212- she leaves without her {\i ktubah}, because {\i mipnei she-mashi\u8217'aso shem ra bishcheinav} \u8212- she has given him a bad reputation among his neighbors. People ask: why don\u8217't your children have nice new clothes for {\i Yom Tov}? How comes your wife won\u8217't weave for them? His social standing suffers because of what his wife is \u8212- or is not \u8212- doing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shared Standing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader principle is clear: community standing is not simply an individual matter. A husband\u8217's reputation and a wife\u8217's reputation are intertwined through their shared social relations. This is captured well in the vision of {\i eishet chayil} \u8212- {\i noda bashe\u8217'arim ba\u8217'alah}, her husband is known at the gates \u8212- where the wife\u8217's behavior reflects on and shapes the husband\u8217's standing in the community. Reputation, the ability to borrow and lend, the quality of one\u8217's participation in the routines of communal exchange: all of these are part of what makes a marriage viable or not. And it is worth noting in this context that the parallel from {\i darchei shalom} applies here as well: the same {\i braita} that records that we give {\i tzedakah} to the poor of non-Jews also records that we collect {\i tzedakah} from non-Jews. The reason: {\i mipnei darchei shalom}. The relationship of giving and receiving is itself constitutive of community. A woman \u8212- or man \u8212- who is cut off from that reciprocity is cut off from something essential.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Transgressions Moshe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now pivots significantly. Until this point, virtually all the cases we have examined involved the husband as the source of the problem \u8212- it was {\i yotzi v\u8217'yiten ktubah}, he must divorce her and pay her {\i ktubah}, he was at fault. Here the {\i mishnah} shifts: {\i v\u8217'eilu yotzot shelo b\u8217'ktubah} \u8212- these are the women who leave without a {\i ktubah}. The emphasis falls on her behavior. She is transgressing \u8212- and because she is at fault, she forfeits her {\i ktubah}. That said, one should note that {\i yotzot} itself raises the same interpretive question we have seen throughout: does the {\i beit din} compel the divorce, or does it merely determine that if a divorce happens, she is not entitled to {\i ktubah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The categories of transgression are: {\i da\u8217'overet al dat Moshe v\u8217'Yehudit} \u8212- she is transgressing the Law of Moshe or the practices of Jewish women. \u8220"Law of Moshe\u8221" refers to biblical law; \u8220"Jewish women\u8221" refers to accepted norms of conduct among Jewish women. Importantly, the {\i mishnah} is not saying that any transgression \u8212- smoking on {\i Shabbat}, say, or some sin that is entirely between her and God \u8212- constitutes grounds for divorce without {\i ktubah}. The {\i mishnah} is quite specific, and the {\i gemara} will clarify why: what is at issue is transgression that directly impacts the husband and makes the marriage untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Food Without Tithes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first transgression under {\i dat Moshe}: {\i ma\u8217'achilato she\u8217'eino me\u8217'ushur} \u8212- she feeds him food that has not had {\i terumot} and {\i ma\u8217'aserot} properly separated from it, rendering it {\i tevel}, which is prohibited for consumption. This directly causes him to transgress, not merely a minor prohibition but a serious one \u8212- indeed, the class of transgressions listed here all carry severe consequences, many of them {\i karet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing on why the {\i mishnah} focuses on {\i tevel} rather than, say, feeding him {\i chazir}. At one level this reflects practical reality: feeding one\u8217's husband obviously non-kosher food was presumably rare and easily prevented, whereas the question of whether {\i terumot} and {\i ma\u8217'aserot} had been taken \u8212- especially from produce that passed through the household in various stages \u8212- was a live and common concern in the {\i mishnaic} period. The category of {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- the ordinary Jew who was not scrupulous about {\i ma\u8217'aserot} \u8212- was a real social phenomenon. Moreover, the {\i mishnah} places this under {\i dat Moshe}, which raises a question noted by the students: much of this could be rabbinic obligation depending on the period. Yet the {\i mishnah} treats it as {\i dat Moshe}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knowledge Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses immediately: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case? {\i Ei yada, nifrosh} \u8212- if he knows she is feeding him {\i tevel}, then don\u8217't eat it. The situation seems entirely within his control. {\i Ei lo yada, mena yada} \u8212- and if he doesn\u8217't know, how does he ever find out? If he trusts her, he trusts her, and if the transgression never surfaces, how does it become grounds for divorce?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Indirect Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i lo tzricha d\u8217'amra leih ploni Kohen tiken li et hakari} \u8212- the case is one where she told him that such-and-such a {\i Kohen} came and helped her separate {\i terumot} and {\i ma\u8217'aserot} from the pile of produce. {\i V\u8217'azal v\u8217'shaleih v\u8217'ishtakach shikra} \u8212- he went and asked, and it emerged that she had been lying. He trusted her \u8212- that is the foundation of the relationship \u8212- and evidence surfaced through a third party that her assurance was false. The transgression was committed and the lie compounded it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second transgression: {\i u\u8217'meshamshashu niddah} \u8212- she has sexual relations with him while she is a {\i niddah}. Again the {\i gemara} asks: if he knows she is a {\i niddah}, he should simply refrain. If he doesn\u8217't know, how does it emerge?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reliance Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} begins from an established principle: Rav Chinana bar Kahana states that a man may rely on his wife in these matters. Shmuel derives this from the verse {\i v\u8217'safra lah shivat yamim} \u8212- she counts for herself \u8212- meaning the {\i niddah} herself determines her count and her own purity status. The husband trusts her completely; this is the normative expectation. How, then, could the husband ever come to know that she was lying?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is one where she told him she had asked a {\i chacham} about a blood stain and he had ruled it permissible \u8212- {\i ploni chacham tihar li et hadam}. The husband then went and asked that same {\i chacham}, and discovered that she had never brought such a question. The lie surfaced through a third party, exactly as in the {\i tevel} case. A variant offered by the {\i gemara}: the case of {\i huchzeka niddah bishcheinosha} \u8212- her {\i niddah} status had been established through her neighbors. In the absence of modern conveniences, a woman who was {\i niddah} would wear distinctive clothing to protect her regular garments from soiling. If neighbors observed her wearing {\i niddah} clothing, that was sufficient indirect evidence of her status, such that if she then had relations with her husband \u8212- inferred from the pattern of those clothes being worn before and after \u8212- the husband could even receive lashes on the basis of that indirect evidence. The broader point is that external evidence can establish what the husband, relying on her word, did not know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} occasions a notable {\i Tosafot} excursus. The {\i tosafot} observes that the counting described here is technically associated not with the {\i niddah} but with the {\i zavah}, who counts seven clean days. Since contemporary women treat themselves as both {\i niddah} and {\i zavah} and count seven clean days accordingly, the {\i tosafot} asks: why do we not recite a {\i bracha} on this counting, just as we recite a {\i bracha} on {\i sefiras ha\u8217'omer}? The answer offered is that a {\i bracha} presupposes a stable, completable act of counting. {\i Sefiras ha\u8217'omer} runs for forty-nine days without interruption. The {\i zavah}\u8217's counting, by contrast, is liable to restart at any point if bleeding resumes during those seven days \u8212- meaning any individual day\u8217's count may turn out to have been invalidated. This makes it structurally different from the {\i omer}, which proceeds to completion. Most {\i poskim} resolve the {\i tosafot}\u8217's question differently, reasoning that the seven-day count is not a formal act of counting at all but simply a process of noting the passage of time; it is not a {\i mitzvah} in the same sense as {\i sefiras ha\u8217'omer} and therefore does not carry a {\i bracha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challah Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third transgression under {\i dat Moshe}: {\i v\u8217'lo kotza la challah} \u8212- she fails to separate {\i challah} from the dough. The {\i gemara} applies the same analysis: if he knew, he should not eat it; if he did not know, the case is one where she told him she had brought the dough to a kneader \u8212- {\i ploni gaval tiken li et ha\u8217'isa} \u8212- who had separated {\i challah} for her. He went and asked, and found she was lying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unfulfilled Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth transgression: {\i v\u8217'noderet v\u8217'eina mekayemet} \u8212- she makes vows and does not fulfill them. This one is less immediately obvious in its connection to the marriage. The first three transgressions directly cause the husband to eat forbidden food or engage in forbidden relations. What does her failure to fulfill her own vows have to do with him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children\u8217's Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains via a teaching of the Sages: {\i ba\u8217'avon nedarim banim metim} \u8212- for the sin of unfulfilled vows, children die. The source is the verse {\i al titein et picha lachati et b\u8217'sarecha} \u8212- do not let your mouth cause your flesh to sin \u8212- with the end of the verse: {\i v\u8217'chibbel et ma\u8217'asei yadecha}. The \u8220"work of your hands\u8221" is understood as {\i banav u\u8217'vnotav} \u8212- one\u8217's sons and daughters. Rav Nachman adds a parallel from Jeremiah: {\i lashav hikeiti et b\u8217'neichem} \u8212- for nothing have I smitten your children \u8212- where {\i lashav} is understood as relating to {\i shav}, unfulfilled vows. Whatever one\u8217's theological response to the idea that children bear consequences for their parents\u8217' failures, this is clearly the understanding that drives the halachic concern. The transgression affects not only the woman but the household, and the children are at risk. That is why unfulfilled vows are placed alongside the other three transgressions: in each case, the wife\u8217's conduct creates a consequence that falls upon the husband and the family.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Provocation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} cites Rabbi Meir: {\i kol hayodeia b\u8217'ishto shenoderet v\u8217'eina mekayemet} \u8212- a man who knows his wife takes vows and does not fulfill them. Rabbi Meir\u8217's advice: get her to take vows in your presence and then annul them. The {\i gemara} asks \u8212- {\i yadirna b\u8217'mai matkein lah} \u8212- how would making a vow on her help? The answer is: {\i ela yachzir v\u8217'yaknitena} \u8212- provoke her, make her angry, so that she will take a vow in front of him, and then he can be {\i mefer} it, annul it. Several problems with this approach are immediately apparent. Even if he manages to provoke her into taking a vow in his presence, what about the vows she takes when he is not there? Moreover, even if he is {\i mefer} at the end of the day, what happens to the period between when the vow was taken and when he annulled it \u8212- could she have transgressed in the interim? And whether the husband must actually hear the vow in order to annul it is itself a question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Snake Basket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response to Rabbi Meir\u8217's suggestion \u8212- and apparently also to the parallel suggestion about {\i challah} \u8212- is decisive: {\i ein adam dar im nachash b\u8217'chfifah} \u8212- a person cannot be expected to live with a snake in a basket. The point is not technical; it is existential. Even if there is a mechanism by which the problem can theoretically be managed \u8212- don\u8217't eat it, provoke her to take vows in front of you \u8212- it is not a viable response. The constant vigilance required, the ongoing need to be on guard against one\u8217's own spouse, is itself the problem. A marriage in which one must perpetually protect oneself against the other partner is not a tenable marriage. Rabbi Yehuda similarly says regarding {\i challah}: go back and separate it yourself. The answer is the same: {\i ein adam dar im nachash b\u8217'chfifah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The image here is powerful, and its applications extend beyond this {\i gemara}. The {\i Rambam} famously invokes this very principle \u8212- {\i ein adam dar im nachash b\u8217'chfifah} \u8212- in the context of a wife who declares that she finds her husband physically repugnant and cannot bear intimacy with him. Even though she is the one making the claim, and even though she therefore forfeits her {\i ktubah}, the {\i Rambam} holds that the husband must be compelled to give a {\i get} \u8212- because she too cannot be forced to live with a snake. One cannot require a person to cohabit with someone she finds impossible to be with.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Transgressions Jewish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now enumerates the transgressions that fall under {\i dat Yehudit} \u8212- the practices of Jewish women \u8212- as opposed to {\i dat Moshe}, biblical law. Four examples are given. Before the {\i gemara} addresses each one, it is worth noting what the category of {\i dat Yehudit} represents. These are not biblical prohibitions; they are norms of conduct observed among Jewish women. The concern, as will become apparent, is less about her sinning per se and more about the social and relational signals that certain behaviors send \u8212- particularly signals that could attract the attention of other men or compromise the exclusivity and dignity of the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncovered Head\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first example of {\i dat Yehudit}: {\i yotzah v\u8217'roshah parua} \u8212- going out in public with her head uncovered. The term {\i parua} connotes more than simply bare; it suggests disheveled, loose, free-flowing. This is the behavior that the {\i mishnah} categorizes as a violation of Jewish women\u8217's practice, making her liable to lose her {\i ktubah} in divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately presses: if {\i roshah parua} is {\i dat Yehudit} \u8212- a norm, not a biblical law \u8212- why is it sourced in the {\i Torah}? The verse cited is {\i u\u8217'phara rosh ha\u8217'isha} from the {\i sotah} passage in {\i Bamidbar}, where the {\i kohen} uncovers the suspected adulteress\u8217's head. The school of Rabbi Yishmael derives from here {\i azhara livnot Yisrael shelo yetzu b\u8217'farua rosh d\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- a prohibition for Jewish women against going out with an uncovered head, which is of biblical force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Norm vs.\u160 ?Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi, however, reads this differently. Rather than deriving a prohibition from the {\i sotah} ritual, Rashi understands the inference as follows: {\i mi-klal de-chtiv u\u8217'phara, mi-klal de-hahi sha\u8217'ata lav perua\u8217'h hi} \u8212- the fact that the {\i kohen} uncovers her head implies that, at that moment, it was covered. From this we can infer the norm: {\i ein derech b\u8217'not Yisrael latzeit b\u8217'farua rosh} \u8212- it was simply not the practice of Jewish women to go out with their heads uncovered. This is not a prohibition; it is a recorded norm. Rashi adds {\i v\u8217'chein ikar} \u8212- and this is the more accurate reading. A significant number of {\i rishonim} and {\i poskim} follow this position, treating the {\i Torah}\u8217's reference as no more than {\i asmachta} or a record of existing practice, rather than a binding {\i d\u8217'Oraita} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Law vs.\u160 ?Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the question of category by distinguishing between the biblical requirement and the {\i dat Yehudit} demand. Biblically \u8212- {\i d\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- a {\i kalta}, a simple basket-like head covering, is sufficient. The practice of Jewish women, {\i dat Yehudit}, demands more: {\i afilu kalta nami assur} \u8212- even a basket is insufficient. The exact content of this additional requirement is not fully specified \u8212- whether it concerns how much of the head is covered, the opacity of the covering, or some other quality \u8212- but the principle is clear: there are two thresholds, biblical and normative, and they differ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira then asks a clarifying question: {\i heicha}? Where is the context in which this minimal covering \u8212- the {\i kalta} \u8212- satisfies requirements? The {\i gemara} has just established that in the marketplace, {\i dat Yehudit} demands more than a {\i kalta}, so it cannot be referring to the public marketplace. Perhaps it refers to her own courtyard? But that introduces a problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye (and some say Rav Kahana) answers: {\i me-chatzer le-chatzer derech mavoi} \u8212- the minimal {\i kalta} is required and sufficient when moving from one courtyard to another by way of the {\i mavoi}, the alleyway. This is the intermediate zone between the privacy of one\u8217's own space and the full publicness of the marketplace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Layout\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand this answer, one must picture the physical layout of the ancient Jewish residential neighborhood. Each household had its own {\i chatzer} \u8212- a courtyard \u8212- opening off the main house. Multiple {\i chatzeirot} opened into a shared, semi-communal space, and those in turn opened into the {\i mavoi}, the alleyway, which connected to the broader {\i shuk}, the marketplace. So the {\i chatzer} itself, while not fully private \u8212- other houses open onto it, and other men may be present \u8212- is not a genuinely public space either. The {\i mavoi} is more public, the {\i shuk} most public of all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Space Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion the {\i gemara} reaches is important: in her own {\i chatzer}, even with other people around, a woman need not cover her head at all. The absence of any head covering becomes a problem only as one moves into more genuinely public space. And in the marketplace, a simple {\i kalta} is not enough \u8212- {\i dat Yehudit} requires a fuller covering. The intermediate case \u8212- crossing briefly through the {\i mavoi} between courtyards \u8212- requires at least the {\i kalta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This emphasis on public space as the operative context has significant implications for contemporary discussions of {\i kisui rosh}. The concern is not hair as {\i ervah} in the manner of the {\i gemara} in {\i Berachot} \u8212- {\i se\u8217'ar b\u8217'isha ervah} \u8212- which appears to treat exposed hair as categorically problematic. Here the concern is contextual and tied to norms of visibility to strangers. If the governing principle is about public presentation and signals sent to unknown men, then the relevant questions become: what counts as public space, what counts as an accepted norm, and how those norms can evolve. These are live questions for {\i poskim} across traditions, and different communities have reached very different conclusions depending on which {\i gemara} they emphasize and how they understand the category of {\i dat Yehudit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knitting Arms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second example: {\i v\u8217'tova ba-shuk} \u8212- she knits in the marketplace. Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel explains: {\i b\u8217'mar\u8217'eh zero\u8217'oteha livnei adam} \u8212- the problem is that knitting exposes her arms to men in public. The motion of knitting draws attention to the arms and makes them visible in a way that is considered immodest. It is striking that this particular behavior \u8212- which might seem innocuous in other cultural contexts \u8212- is listed as grounds for divorce without {\i ktubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Red Thread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda in the name of Avimi offers an alternative explanation: {\i bitova b\u8217'vrad k\u8217'neged paneha} \u8212- the issue is that she knits with a red thread positioned in front of her face. {\i Tosafot} reads this literally: a red thread ({\i vered}) catches the eye, draws attention to her face, and causes men to look at her. Rashi reads {\i paneha} as a euphemism, suggesting that the thread hangs down in front of her pubic area, attracting attention in a more directly suggestive way. Either reading points to the same underlying concern: the knitting creates an occasion for the eyes of strange men to rest on her in a way that the husband finds unacceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flirtation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third example: {\i u\u8217'medaberet im kol adam} \u8212- she speaks with every man. On its face this might simply mean conversing with strangers. But Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel specifies: {\i b\u8217'misacheket im bachurim} \u8212- she is joking around, being overly familiar, flirting with young men. The problem is not occasional conversation but a pattern of actively seeking flirtatious interaction with men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joking Young Men\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application is not limited to an explicitly sexual or romantic overture; it describes a woman who is {\i misacheket}, playful and familiar, in a way that signals an inappropriate openness to male attention. {\i Al tarbeh sicha im ha-isha} from {\i Pirkei Avot} may similarly be understood in this light \u8212- not a prohibition on conversation per se, but on the kind of excessive, flirtatious familiarity described here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arab Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A vivid illustrative anecdote: Rabbah bar bar Chanah citing Rabbi Yochanan recounts that he was once walking behind Rav Ukva when he noticed an Arab woman sitting and throwing a spindle while weaving, with the thread positioned in front of her \u8212- whether in front of her face or lower, per the dispute above. When she noticed them approaching, she deliberately snapped the thread and threw the spindle down in front of him \u8212- the gesture of dropping an object for a man to retrieve, a classic invitation to interaction. She then said to him: {\i hav li pilcha} \u8212- young man, give me back my spindle. The flirtatious intent was transparent. Rav Ukva applied to her a ruling from our {\i mishnah}. The {\i gemara} debates which case: Ravina says Rav Ukva was citing {\i tova ba-shuk} \u8212- she was knitting in the marketplace in the problematic manner. The Rabbis say he cited {\i medaberet im kol adam} \u8212- she was proactively engaging strange men. On the second reading, {\i medaberet im kol adam} is understood not as casual conversation but as actively seeking flirtatious contact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cursing Parents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Shaul adds a fourth category that stands apart from the theme of female modesty and male attention: {\i af ha-m\u8217'kalelet yeladav b\u8217'fanav} \u8212- even a woman who curses her husband\u8217's parents in his presence. Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel extends this: {\i b\u8217'm\u8217'kalelet yeladav bifnei molidav} \u8212- she curses his children in the presence of their grandparents. The mnemonic offered is {\i Efraim u\u8217'Menashe k\u8217'Reuven v\u8217'Shimon yihyu li} \u8212- grandsons are like sons \u8212- so insulting a grandfather in the presence of the grandchildren is equivalent to insulting a father before his children. The example given is the wife who says to her children, {\i neichlei arya l\u8217'sabba} \u8212- \u8220"let a lion eat your grandfather.\u8221" Whether said directly to the husband or in the presence of their children, this is placed in the category of conduct that makes the marriage untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loud Voice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon adds: {\i af ha-kolanit} \u8212- also the {\i kolanit}, the woman with the excessively loud voice. The {\i mishnah} then provides a definition: {\i likshehi medaberet b\u8217'toch beisah u\u8217'shcheneha shom\u8217'in kolah} \u8212- when she speaks inside her house and her neighbors can hear her voice. The plain reading is that she argues so loudly that the neighbors overhear their quarrels and private conversations \u8212- both embarrassing and invasive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loud Sexual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel, however, interprets this very differently: {\i b\u8217'mashma\u8217'at kolah al iskei tashmish} \u8212- her voice can be heard regarding matters of sexual relations. The issue is not domestic arguments but sounds during intercourse that carry beyond the walls of the bedroom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Pleasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi offers two readings of this phenomenon: either she cries out because sex is painful to her \u8212- a genuinely sad interpretation \u8212- or she is expressing pleasure loudly. Either way, the {\i gemara} treats this as a matter of privacy that, when violated, damages the husband\u8217's sense of dignity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defect Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an objection to this second reading: if the issue with the {\i kolanit} is that she cannot control the sounds she makes during intimacy, then this seems like an intrinsic physical characteristic \u8212- a {\i mum}, a defect \u8212- rather than a behavioral choice. It should therefore belong in the category of {\i mumim} addressed in a separate {\i mishnah}, not among the behavioral transgressions listed here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Controllable Behavior\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by preferring the first interpretation: {\i ela mechvarta kedishanin me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- the correct reading is the earlier one, that {\i kolanit} refers to a woman who speaks so loudly inside her house that the neighbors can hear her conversations. This is controllable behavior, a matter of how one conducts oneself, and therefore appropriately categorized among behavioral transgressions of {\i dat Yehudit} rather than among physical defects. The {\i mishnah} is about choices and conduct \u8212- things the wife can and should control \u8212- and the {\i kolanit} belongs there only if her loudness is understood as a behavioral pattern rather than an involuntary physical trait.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 73\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Head Covering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's learning is dedicated to the memory of those murdered in the {\i Shoah}. We transition from a {\i sugya} that dealt with the norms of married women\u8217's behavior \u8212- specifically the question of head covering \u8212- to the next {\i mishnah}, which opens a discussion of enormous contemporary relevance to {\i agunah} solutions and conditional marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head Covering Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous {\i mishnah}\u8217's treatment of head covering was notably distinct from the discussion in {\i Berachot}, which addresses hair as a form of {\i ervah}. In the {\i mishnah} under that discussion, the concern is not absolute: it applies specifically to married women, only in public contexts, and bears on the question of whether a woman is drawing undue attention to herself. The {\i mishnah} emphasizes the head rather than the hair per se, and the {\i gemara} there raises whether the operative standard is {\i dat Yehudit} \u8212- the norms of Jewish women, which might be subject to change as societal norms evolve \u8212- or {\i dat Moshe}, which may carry biblical weight and may or may not be similarly amenable to change. These remain live and important questions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cond. Marriage Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the {\i mishnah} at the bottom of 73b, which addresses {\i kiddushin al tnai} \u8212- conditional betrothal \u8212- and which will draw us into one of the most significant {\i sugyot} for discussions of {\i agunah} and possible {\i agunah} solutions in contemporary {\i halachah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Vows Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} begins: {\i HaMekadesh et haIsha al menat she\u8217'ein aleha nedarim} \u8212- a man betroths a woman on the condition that she has no binding vows upon her. {\i V\u8217'nimtze\u8217'u aleha nedarim} \u8212- and it is subsequently discovered that she does in fact have vows. {\i Eina mekudeshet} \u8212- the betrothal is void. Since the {\i kiddushin} was made conditional and the condition was not met, the {\i kiddushin} never took effect. This is straightforward: he verbalized the condition explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Condition, Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then continues with a second clause: {\i kinusah stam} \u8212- he brought her in, meaning he performed {\i nisu\u8217'in}, the stage of full marriage that is the focus of our {\i masechet}, without articulating any condition. {\i V\u8217'nimtze\u8217'u aleha nedarim} \u8212- and vows are discovered. {\i Teitze shelo biKetuvah} \u8212- she is divorced without receiving her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} will debate how to read this second clause. The most natural reading of the {\i mishnah} as a whole seems to be that the second clause builds on the first: the man had already stipulated at the time of {\i kiddushin} that he does not want a wife with vows, and then he proceeded to the {\i nisu\u8217'in} without re-articulating that condition. Because he had verbalized the {\i tnai} earlier, it is evident he cares about the matter \u8212- hence he may divorce her without a {\i ketubah}. Yet she may still require a {\i get}, since the {\i nisu\u8217'in} itself was performed without the condition being reiterated. Alternatively, one could read the second clause as entirely independent: a man simply married a woman with no condition stated at any stage, and the discovery of {\i nedarim} is, by itself, a sufficient basis for divorce without {\i ketubah}. This ambiguity is precisely what drives the subsequent {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blemishes Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with a parallel case: {\i al menat she\u8217'ein ba mumin} \u8212- he marries on the condition that she has no blemishes. The condition is not met. Again, {\i kiddushin al tnai}, condition unmet, {\i kiddushin} void. Then {\i kinusah stam} \u8212- the {\i nisu\u8217'in} proceeds without re-articulation \u8212- and blemishes are discovered. {\i Teitze shelo biKetuvah}. The structure precisely parallels the vows case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Define Mumim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i kol haMumim haposlim baKohanim poslim baNashim} \u8212- whatever blemishes disqualify a {\i kohen} from Temple service disqualify a woman in this context. This is the operative standard if the husband did not specify which kind of blemish he had in mind. The {\i gemara} notes that the same {\i mishnah} appears in {\i masechet Kiddushin}, where its purpose is to establish the laws of conditional {\i kiddushin}, while here in {\i masechet Ketubot} the teaching is needed to address the {\i ketubah} consequences \u8212- specifically that she may be divorced without receiving one. The {\i mishnah} is thus needed in each tractate for a different focal point: {\i kiddushin} is the subject there; {\i ketubah} is the subject here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Which Vows Matter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inui Nefesh Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to define which category of vows is meant by the {\i mishnah}. {\i Amar Rabbi Yochanan mishum Rabbi Yosi ben Yehotzadak}: {\i be\u8217'elu nedarim amru} \u8212- when the {\i mishnah} speaks of vows, it is not referring to all vows indiscriminately. Only certain categories are meant. A {\i baraita} confirms this: {\i be\u8217'elu nedarim amru \u8212- dvarim sheyesh bahen inui nefesh} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} refers specifically to vows that involve affliction of the person, {\i inui nefesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Affliction Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The examples given are: {\i shelo tochal basar} \u8212- that she may not eat meat; {\i shelo tishteh yayin} \u8212- that she may not drink wine; {\i shelo titkashet bivigdei tzevonin} \u8212- that she may not adorn herself with colored garments. These are the same categories the {\i gemara} earlier identified as constituting {\i inui nefesh} according to one {\i tanna}. The implication is that lesser, more trivial vows would not rise to the threshold of being a disqualifying factor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit vs Implied\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'ei mineih Rav Pappa}: on which clause of the {\i mishnah} does Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's limitation apply? {\i Ilima areisha} \u8212- if it applies to the first clause, where the man explicitly stipulated \u8220"on condition of no vows,\u8221" then since he expressed an explicit concern, surely any vow, however trivial, ought to matter. The threshold of {\i inui nefesh} seems out of place there. {\i Ela asifa} \u8212- it must therefore apply to the second clause, where no condition was stated. In that case, since nothing was articulated, the {\i mishnah} can only be referring to vows of meaningful consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reasonable Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi amar}: Rav Ashi disagrees with this framing and applies the limitation even to the first clause \u8212- even where the man explicitly said \u8220"no vows.\u8221" {\i Mihu d\u8217'kafid inshi havei kfeida} \u8212- what counts as a legitimate concern is determined by what people normally care about. If he said \u8220"no vows\u8221" without further specification, we interpret that statement to encompass only vows that normal people would genuinely object to. {\i Mihu d\u8217'la kafdi inshi} \u8212- things that people ordinarily disregard are not included, even if the man used broad language. Of course, if he is explicit and specific \u8212- \u8220"I don\u8217't want any vow whatsoever, no matter how trivial\u8221" \u8212- then he may indeed make such a condition binding. A person may set any stipulation he chooses. But where he speaks in general terms, we do not read the condition to extend beyond what a person would reasonably mean. One might ask why {\i inui nefesh} is the relevant measure here, since the concept designates an affliction of {\i her} body \u8212- why should that be the operative criterion for {\i his} concern? The most natural answer is that what significantly affects her wellbeing and daily life also shapes the texture of the shared marital relationship in ways that matter to him as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav v. Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Condition Persistence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Itmar}: the {\i gemara} now introduces the central dispute that emerges from this {\i mishnah}, and which has profound ramifications for {\i halachah}. The case: {\i kidsha al tnai} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} was made with an explicit condition \u8212- {\i v\u8217'kansah stam} \u8212- and the {\i nisu\u8217'in} was performed without reiterating that condition. What is the {\i halachah}? Does the {\i tnai} made at the time of {\i kiddushin} continue to operate at the time of {\i nisu\u8217'in}, even though it was not repeated?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav amar}: {\i tzricha heimenu get}. She requires a {\i get} from him. Once the {\i nisu\u8217'in} has taken place \u8212- even though the {\i tnai} was not repeated \u8212- she is considered married. The {\i tnai} made at the time of {\i kiddushin} does not persist through the {\i nisu\u8217'in} to void the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel amar}: {\i eina tzricha heimenu get}. She does not require a {\i get}. Since the {\i tnai} was articulated at the time of {\i kiddushin}, if the {\i kiddushin} never took effect because the condition was not satisfied, then the subsequent act of {\i nisu\u8217'in} and cohabitation does not create a marriage. In Shmuel\u8217's view, marital relations are meaningful only insofar as they rest upon an antecedent valid {\i kiddushin}. Without a binding {\i kiddushin} as the foundation, the act of {\i be\u8217'ilah} is simply {\i be\u8217'ilah} \u8212- it is not an independent act of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ain Adam Oseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye}: Abaye clarifies that Rav\u8217's reasoning is not, as one might suppose, that by proceeding to {\i nisu\u8217'in} without reiterating the {\i tnai}, the husband implicitly waived it \u8212- thereby retroactively validating the original act of {\i kiddushin}. That would be a remarkable notion: that a waiver occurring a year later could retroactively render the original giving of the ring binding. Abaye dismisses that reading. {\i Ela ta\u8217'ama deRav} \u8212- Rav\u8217's reason is {\i she\u8217'ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} \u8212- because a person does not wish his act of marital intercourse to be an act of fornication. The original {\i kiddushin} was not binding because the {\i tnai} was unmet. But the act of {\i be\u8217'ilah} itself is one of the recognized modes of effecting {\i kiddushin}, and we presume that within the context of a {\i chuppah} and the entire marital framework, the man intends this act to be a marital one. Because he would not want it to be an illicit act, we conclude that he wishes the intercourse itself to constitute {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marital Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning raises a deeper question: does the principle of {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} mean that the man actively intends the act of {\i be\u8217'ilah} to be an act of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- in which case {\i kavvanah} would be necessary \u8212- or does it mean that in a marital context {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} operates automatically, irrespective of explicit intent? Some {\i Acharonim} take the latter position, that {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} within a marital context requires no specific {\i kavvanah}. The practical concern with such a reading is significant, however: it would mean that any couple living together in an established marital arrangement, even without having contracted a halachically valid {\i kiddushin}, would be considered married and would require a {\i get} \u8212- a result with potentially sweeping implications for {\i agunah} cases and the like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds: once the {\i nisu\u8217'in} is performed and the couple cohabits, Rav holds they are married not because the original {\i kiddushin} has been retroactively validated, but because the act of {\i be\u8217'ilah} itself constitutes a new, independent act of {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah}. There is accordingly a {\i nafka mina} between these two theories: when precisely is the marriage considered to have taken effect? If it is from the time of the original {\i kiddushin}, one set of consequences follows; if from the time of {\i be\u8217'ilah}, another set applies. For example, if the woman had relations with another man between the {\i kiddushin} and the {\i nisu\u8217'in}, the question of when they are deemed married is directly material. In the {\i gemara}\u8217's framing, {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} and {\i nisu\u8217'in} here occur together at the time of {\i be\u8217'ilah}, with the earlier {\i kiddushin} not having been the operative act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor Girl Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ktanah Growing Up\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} observes that Rav and Shmuel have already disputed this issue once before in an analogous context. {\i Ktanah shemi\u8217'anah v\u8217'higdilah}: the case of a minor girl who was married off by her mother or brothers \u8212- as is permitted when she has no father \u8212- and who did not exercise her right of {\i mi\u8217'un} (refusal) while she was still a minor. She then became an adult and continued to live with her husband. The original {\i kiddushin} effected by her mother or brothers had only rabbinic force; it was not a fully binding {\i kiddushin deOraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav on Intercourse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amda venisset} \u8212- suppose she then goes and marries a second man. Rav holds: {\i ein tzricha get misheini} \u8212- she does not require a {\i get} from the second man. Why? Because by continuing to cohabit with the first husband after reaching adulthood, she effected a new, binding {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} with him. The continued marital intercourse in an established marital context is itself a valid act of {\i kiddushin}. Her subsequent \u8220"marriage\u8221" to the second man was therefore invalid from the outset, since she was already married. Consequently, the second {\i kiddushin} simply does not take effect and no {\i get} is required from that man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel on Original\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, by contrast, holds: {\i tzricha get misheini}. In his view, the continued marital cohabitation does not independently create {\i kiddushin} where none previously existed. Since the original {\i kiddushin} by her mother and brothers was not fully binding, and she never executed a new, independent act of {\i kiddushin} with her first husband as an adult, she was not halachically married to him when she contracted the second marriage. The second {\i kiddushin} is therefore valid, and if she now wishes to leave the second man she requires a {\i get} from him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to the {\i tnai} case is evident. In both cases, the original {\i kiddushin} was deficient \u8212- in the {\i tnai} case because the condition was unmet; in the {\i ktanah} case because her original {\i kiddushin} lacked full {\i deOraita} force. The question in both cases is whether continued cohabitation independently generates a binding marriage. Rav says yes; Shmuel says no, everything depends on whether the foundational {\i kiddushin} was valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Cases Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: since it is the same underlying dispute, why must it be stated in both cases? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i tzricha}. Each case is needed because it presents a scenario where one might have thought that even Rav would concede to Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had we only the {\i tnai} case, one might have said: that is where Rav says continued cohabitation is binding \u8212- {\i because} there is no explicit {\i tnai} in force at the time of {\i nisu\u8217'in}. But in the {\i tnai} case, where the man explicitly articulated \u8220"only on condition of no vows,\u8221" one might think that even Rav concedes that the explicit {\i tnai} continues to frame the relationship, and that continued cohabitation does not override it. We therefore need the {\i tnai} case to show that even there, Rav holds the {\i be\u8217'ilah} creates {\i kiddushin}. This, in fact, is a larger {\i chiddush} on Rav\u8217's part: where a {\i tnai} was explicitly stated, there is greater reason to think it should persist and frame all subsequent relations, and yet Rav holds that the continued marital {\i be\u8217'ilah} nonetheless creates {\i kiddushin} unconditionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, had we only the {\i ktanah} case \u8212- where no {\i tnai} was ever articulated \u8212- one might have thought that Shmuel, who says continued cohabitation is not binding, agrees only there because nothing at all was ever said. But in the {\i tnai} case, where at least something was verbalized (even if the condition ultimately was not met), perhaps Shmuel would agree with Rav that continued living together creates {\i kiddushin}. We therefore need both cases to fully establish the scope of their dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agunah Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Tnai Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our {\i amud} is brief, owing in part to the substantial {\i Tosafot} that occupies much of the page. The {\i Tosafot} engages with a statement found in another {\i gemara}: {\i ein tnai benis\u8217'uin} \u8212- conditions cannot operate at the {\i nisu\u8217'in} stage. This principle is of great moment for understanding whether conditional marriage can ever serve as a solution to the {\i agunah} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From our {\i gemara} itself, the conclusion seems to be that it is specifically because the {\i tnai} was not reiterated at the time of {\i nisu\u8217'in} that Rav holds the {\i be\u8217'ilah} creates {\i kiddushin} unconditionally. One might reason, accordingly, that had he explicitly reiterated the {\i tnai} before the {\i chuppah} \u8212- \u8220"everything that follows, including our living together, remains conditional on her having no {\i nedarim}\u8221" \u8212- then perhaps even Rav would agree the {\i tnai} remains operative. But this other {\i gemara}, with its categorical {\i ein tnai benis\u8217'uin}, suggests that in principle, no matter what is said, once the couple begins living together they are married. There is no room for conditions at the {\i nisu\u8217'in} stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Agunah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} directly engages this issue in the context of the {\i agunah} problem. The prospect of a solution through conditional {\i kiddushin} \u8212- making the original betrothal conditional on circumstances that would prevent an {\i agunah} situation \u8212- has been debated by {\i poskim} going back some 150 years, notably among the rabbis of France. The idea is straightforward: if the {\i kiddushin} is made conditional at the outset \u8212- for example, on condition that the husband will always give a {\i get} if the marriage breaks down, or on condition that some future circumstance does or does not come to pass \u8212- then if those conditions are not met, the {\i kiddushin} is retroactively void and no {\i get} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Tnai Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One approach discussed by {\i Tosafot} is whether a sufficiently explicit and carefully framed {\i tnai} at the time of {\i kiddushin} might nonetheless operate effectively. From our {\i gemara}, the limiting factor appears to be the {\i be\u8217'ilah} at the time of {\i nisu\u8217'in}, which in Rav\u8217's view creates an unconditional {\i kiddushin} regardless of what came before \u8212- grounded in {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut}. But as {\i Tosafot} notes, the principle of {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} operates {\i mistama} \u8212- as a default assumption in the absence of explicit statement. If the parties explicitly and unambiguously declare that their living together and cohabiting is itself still conditional, one could argue they have effectively acknowledged the possibility of {\i beilat zenut} and are consenting to the arrangement. In that case, perhaps the assumption no longer applies and the {\i tnai} could remain operative. The question then becomes whether the categorical {\i ein tnai benis\u8217'uin} from the other {\i gemara} would foreclose even this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secular Marriage Impact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further avenue discussed by later {\i poskim} is that the principle of {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} was formulated in a social context where religious marriage was the only form of recognized marriage. In contemporary reality, where civil marriage exists and is recognized by the state and by society at large, the couple may not perceive their cohabitation as {\i beilat zenut} even if the halachic {\i kiddushin} were conditional or void. If there is civil marriage in place, the marital setting is defined not only by the religious {\i kiddushin}, and therefore the presumption that the man would not want his {\i be\u8217'ilah} to be {\i zenut} may operate differently. This provides an additional basis to argue that a properly structured {\i tnai} might remain effective in contemporary circumstances \u8212- at least as a matter of theoretical {\i lamdanut}, even if the {\i halachah lema\u8217'aseh} remains contested.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Reconcile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Clauses Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines how our {\i mishnah} relates to the debate of Rav and Shmuel. The second clause of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i kinusah stam v\u8217'nimtze\u8217'u aleha nedarim, teitze shelo biKetuvah} \u8212- appears on its simple reading to be building on the first clause. That is, the {\i kiddushin} was already conditional, the {\i nisu\u8217'in} was done without repeating the {\i tnai}, vows are discovered, and she must be divorced. {\i Teitze} implies she requires a {\i get}. {\i Mai lav bid\u8217'katrei tnai} \u8212- is this not then explicitly a case where the {\i tnai} was stated at {\i kiddushin} and she still requires a {\i get} after {\i nisu\u8217'in}? That would be a clear contradiction of Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Reads Stam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav resolves this by insisting that the second clause is an entirely independent case. When the {\i mishnah} says {\i kinusah stam} \u8212- he brought her in without condition \u8212- it means that nothing was said at either the {\i kiddushin} or the {\i nisu\u8217'in} stage. It is a simple case: a man married a woman, and after {\i nisu\u8217'in} vows are discovered. In that case, a woman with {\i nedarim} constitutes grounds for divorce, and though no {\i tnai} was ever stated, the man is not required to pay the {\i ketubah} \u8212- a woman with binding vows is precisely the kind of situation he cannot have been expected to knowingly accept. She requires a {\i get} because of course there was {\i nisu\u8217'in}, but the {\i ketubah} is forfeited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Expects Chiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's interpretation of the {\i mishnah} proceeds differently. He argues that the {\i mishnah} would be more instructive if it presented the case where the {\i tnai} was made at {\i kiddushin} and was followed by {\i nisu\u8217'in}. After all, the obvious {\i chiddush} from Shmuel\u8217's position would be: even if there was {\i nisu\u8217'in} after the conditional {\i kiddushin}, she is still not married if the condition went unmet \u8212- {\i eina mekudeshet}. Why does the {\i mishnah} tell us only what happens before {\i nisu\u8217'in}, the obvious case? It should have told us the more striking ruling: that even after {\i nisu\u8217'in}, the original {\i tnai} renders her {\i lo mekudeshet} with no {\i get} required. The {\i gemara} responds on Shmuel\u8217's behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution: Any Stage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i hachi nami ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- that is indeed what the {\i mishnah} is saying. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's first clause, which states that where {\i kiddushin} was conditional and the condition was not met she is {\i eina mekudeshet}, is not limited to the case where {\i nisu\u8217'in} has not yet taken place. The {\i mishnah} speaks to the general principle: if the {\i kiddushin} was made conditional and the condition was not satisfied, she is not {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- regardless of whether {\i nisu\u8217'in} subsequently occurred. The second clause then addresses an independent scenario: where no {\i tnai} was ever articulated, she requires a {\i get} but forfeits her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vows Without Tnai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Reject Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further on the second clause \u8212- the case where no {\i tnai} was stated. If the man never said anything, why should we assume he objects to vows sufficiently to justify his withholding the {\i ketubah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taos Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the possibility that a woman with vows should be regarded as {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'os} \u8212- a mistaken betrothal. If men universally object to vowed wives, then perhaps the {\i kiddushin} itself is void from the outset, and she should not even need a {\i get}. {\i D\u8217'amar} \u8212- since we say {\i ee efshi b\u8217'isha nadranit}, I cannot accept a wife who takes vows, why does this not void the marriage entirely? {\i Get nami lo tiba\u8217'ei} \u8212- why should a {\i get} be required at all if it is a clear case of {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'os}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabba}: it is not a clear-cut {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'os}. Rather, it is a {\i safek} \u8212- a genuine uncertainty as to whether the {\i kiddushin} was valid. We are not certain whether a man\u8217's objection to {\i nedarim} is so universal and absolute that we can declare the {\i kiddushin} void without any prior stipulation. {\i Sfuki maspka lei}. {\i V\u8217'chen amar Rav Chisda tzricha get midivrehem.} Rabba and Rav Chisda both rule that a {\i get} is required, though the nature of that requirement is now qualified by the {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issura-Mamona Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of this {\i safek} is a well-known halachic split between {\i mamona} and {\i issura}. {\i Lgabei mamona l\u8217'kula} \u8212- on the monetary question of whether the {\i ketubah} must be paid, we rule leniently: since we are uncertain whether the {\i kiddushin} was binding, she bears the burden of proof, and {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'ayah} \u8212- the one seeking to extract money must prove entitlement. She cannot collect the {\i ketubah}. {\i Gabi issura lachumra} \u8212- on the question of personal status, however, where the concern is {\i safek eshet ish}, we are stringent: she requires a {\i get} because the {\i kiddushin} may have been fully valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intermediate Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Middle Ground\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking here \u8212- and the {\i gemara} does not resolve it \u8212- is that the {\i gemara} does not recognize an intermediate category of {\i hakpadah}. One might have thought there exists a middle ground: not quite {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'os} (which would void the {\i kiddushin} entirely), but enough of an objection that the man is entitled to divorce her without paying the {\i ketubah}. The {\i kiddushin} would be valid and binding, yet her failing to disclose the {\i nedarim} would constitute a kind of fault on her part sufficient to justify divorcing her without {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Warranty Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider a parallel from commerce: if a person purchases a car and discovers a major defect, we might say it is a {\i mekach ta\u8217'os} \u8212- the entire transaction is void. But if the defect is minor \u8212- the CD player does not work \u8212- it is not a {\i mekach ta\u8217'os}, yet the buyer is still entitled to compensation or repair. There is a middle tier. By analogy, one could have imagined a case of {\i nedarim} where the man cannot say \u8220"we were never married,\u8221" yet can legitimately say \u8220"this was not what I agreed to, and I am entitled to end the marriage without paying the {\i ketubah}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does not pursue this middle path. Instead, it limits itself to two options: either it is a {\i safek kiddushei ta\u8217'os} \u8212- in which case we split the ruling between {\i mamona} and {\i issura} \u8212- or it is a {\i get midivrehem}, a rabbinically required {\i get}, without entitlement to {\i ketubah}. Notably, earlier in the {\i masechet}, the {\i gemara} did grapple with an analogous middle category: the case where a man married a woman believing her to be a {\i betulah} and discovering she was not. There, the {\i gemara} entertained the possibility of a binding {\i kiddushin} combined with reduced {\i ketubah} entitlement. It is therefore genuinely puzzling that here the {\i gemara} does not deploy similar reasoning, and no explicit resolution is offered for this apparent gap in the analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava Limits Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Women Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava}: the {\i gemara} now presents Rava\u8217's attempt to narrow the scope of the Rav-Shmuel debate. {\i Machlokes b\u8217'ta\u8217'os shtei nashim} \u8212- the dispute applies only in the case of two women. That is: a man made a {\i tnai} with one woman, that {\i tnai} was not met or the {\i kiddushin} was invalid, and he then went ahead and married a different woman without stating any condition. The question is whether the {\i tnai} he made with the first woman is somehow presumed to apply to his marriage with the second woman as well. In that peculiar scenario, Rav and Shmuel disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Woman Consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval b\u8217'isha achat divrei hakol eina tzricha heimenu get} \u8212- but in the straightforward case of one woman, where he made a {\i tnai} with her at {\i kiddushin} and proceeded to {\i nisu\u8217'in} with her, everyone agrees the {\i tnai} remains operative. If the condition was not met, she does not need a {\i get}, because the {\i tnai} at {\i kiddushin} naturally carries through to the {\i nisu\u8217'in} stage. Rava\u8217's reading thus fundamentally limits Rav\u8217's position: Rav agrees that a properly framed {\i tnai} at {\i kiddushin} binds the entire subsequent relationship, including the {\i be\u8217'ilah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye}: Abaye challenges this immediately. {\i V\u8217'ha matnisin b\u8217'isha achas hi} \u8212- our {\i mishnah} deals with one woman, and we have just used it to challenge Shmuel\u8217's position. This presupposes that Rav and Shmuel\u8217's debate is relevant to the single-woman case. If Rava is correct that both Rav and Shmuel agree in the one-woman case, why would the {\i mishnah} be cited as a challenge to Shmuel at all? Rashi notes that an implicit consensus seems to have formed in the course of the {\i gemara} that the debate is indeed applicable to the one-woman case, precisely because we challenged Shmuel from our {\i mishnah} \u8212- which involves only one woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Refines\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava accordingly refines his position: {\i machlokes b\u8217'isha achas k\u8217'ein shtei nashim} \u8212- the debate applies even in the case of one woman, but only when the circumstances resemble those of two women. The case Rava now envisions, according to Rashi, is something like this: a man betrothed a woman with a {\i tnai}, they had a troubled relationship, he then divorced her before {\i nisu\u8217'in}, and subsequently they reconciled and contracted a new {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisu\u8217'in} without re-stating the {\i tnai}. Does the {\i tnai} from the first {\i kiddushin} carry over into the second? Rav says it does not \u8212- the new {\i nisu\u8217'in} without re-articulation is a fresh, unconditional marriage. Shmuel says the {\i tnai} from the first round persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is admittedly a strange reading of Rav and Shmuel, and Rava is clearly pressing to say that in an ordinary case \u8212- one woman, one {\i kiddushin}, one {\i nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- a {\i tnai} at {\i kiddushin} remains operative through {\i nisu\u8217'in} and the {\i be\u8217'ilah}, and this is universally agreed. The extraordinary {\i chiddush} of Rav, in Rava\u8217's reading, is limited to these peculiar edge cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Invalid K. + Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aisaivei Abaye} \u8212- Abaye challenges from a {\i baraita}: if a man performed {\i kiddushin} with a woman in error \u8212- {\i b\u8217'ta\u8217'us} \u8212- or with an object worth less than a {\i perutah}, such that the {\i kiddushin} was invalid; or a minor who betrothed \u8212- all cases where the {\i kiddushin} was not binding. {\i Af al pi sheshalach sivlonos l\u8217'achar mikan} \u8212- even if after these events he sent gifts ({\i sivlonos}) to her and her family in the customary manner between {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisu\u8217'in}, this does not constitute a new act of {\i kiddushin}. {\i Shemachmat kiddushin harishon shalach} \u8212- because the gifts were sent in the framework of the original (invalid) {\i kiddushin} and the social custom surrounding it, not with intent to effect a new {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'im ba\u8217'alu} \u8212- but if they subsequently had intercourse (after, for example, the minor became an adult): {\i kanu} \u8212- they are now married, according to one opinion. {\i Mishum Rabbi Yishmael omer}: {\i im ba\u8217'alu lo kanu} \u8212- the act of intercourse does not render them married. This is, the {\i gemara} observes, the precise dispute of Rav and Shmuel transposed to the level of {\i Tannaim}: an original {\i kiddushin} that was not binding, followed by cohabitation \u8212- does the {\i be\u8217'ilah} independently create {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same as Rav-Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} identifies this {\i baraita} dispute as identical in structure to the Rav-Shmuel debate. The case in the {\i baraita} is one of a deficient {\i kiddushin} followed by {\i be\u8217'ilah}; the question of whether {\i be\u8217'ilah} independently creates {\i kiddushin} is exactly what Rav and Shmuel argue about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taos v. Tnai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabba} objects: there is a meaningful distinction between {\i ta\u8217'us} and {\i tnai}. The {\i baraita} is dealing with a case of genuine error \u8212- {\i ta\u8217'us} \u8212- where the parties did not realize the original {\i kiddushin} was invalid. But a case of {\i tnai}, an explicit condition, is different. When there is an explicit {\i tnai}, the parties know exactly why the {\i kiddushin} might not be binding. That explicit framing arguably shapes all subsequent relations as well. Rabba thus maintains that even Rav, who holds that {\i be\u8217'ilah} creates {\i kiddushin} in a {\i ta\u8217'us} case, would agree that an explicit {\i tnai} frames all subsequent cohabitation \u8212- and that therefore the {\i baraita} deals with something different from our central case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Taos Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knowledge Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to clarify the internal logic of the {\i baraita}\u8217's dispute. One view: {\i adam yodeia she\u8217'ein kiddushin tofsin b\u8217'pachot mishaveh pruta} \u8212- a person knows that {\i kiddushin} effected with less than a {\i perutah} (or by a minor) is invalid. Therefore, when they subsequently have intercourse, {\i v\u8217'gamar u\u8217'ba\u8217'al l\u8217'shem kiddushin} \u8212- they do so with conscious intent to create a new, valid {\i kiddushin}. On this reading, the {\i be\u8217'ilah} is an intentional, knowing act of {\i kiddushin}. Hence it is binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ignorance Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposing view: {\i ein adam yodeia she\u8217'ein kiddushin tofsin b\u8217'pachot mishaveh pruta} \u8212- people are generally not aware that {\i kiddushin} with less than a {\i perutah} is legally defective. They believe themselves to be already validly married. {\i V\u8217'chi ka ba\u8217'al adaata d\u8217'kiddushin harishonim ba\u8217'al} \u8212- and when they subsequently cohabit, they do so entirely in reliance on and within the framework of the original {\i kiddushin}. Since the original {\i kiddushin} was invalid, the {\i be\u8217'ilah} does not constitute a new act of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- it is framed by the original (defective) {\i kiddushin} and has no independent effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Condition Frames All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual distinction Rabba draws between {\i ta\u8217'us} and {\i tnai} now becomes clearer: in the {\i ta\u8217'us} case, the question is one of knowledge \u8212- did the parties know or not know the original {\i kiddushin} was invalid? In the {\i tnai} case, that question is irrelevant. When a man explicitly says \u8220"I am marrying you only on condition of X,\u8221" that explicit condition announces itself as the frame for everything that follows, regardless of knowledge. All subsequent cohabitation is colored by that declaration. The man did not make the condition because he was confused or mistaken; he made it deliberately, and its explicit character means it continues to define the relationship even through the {\i nisu\u8217'in} and {\i be\u8217'ilah}. Rabba therefore holds that the {\i baraita} \u8212- which deals with {\i ta\u8217'us}, ignorance of invalidity \u8212- cannot resolve the question of {\i tnai}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Consent Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Consent Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aisaivei} \u8212- the {\i gemara} raises one further {\i baraita}. A man says to a woman: {\i hareini bo\u8217'alaich al menat sheyirtzeh abba} \u8212- \u8220"I am having intercourse with you on condition that your father consents to the marriage.\u8221" The intercourse is thus explicitly made conditional on the father\u8217's agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One opinion holds that despite the non-fulfillment of the stated condition \u8212- {\i af al pi shelo ratza ha\u8217'av}, even if the father did not explicitly agree \u8212- she is nonetheless {\i mekudeshet}, married. How so? Because the phrase \u8220"on condition that the father agrees\u8221" is interpreted to mean {\i al menat sheyishtok ha\u8217'av} \u8212- on condition that the father does not object. If the father was silent and raised no objection, that silence constitutes sufficient \u8220"agreement,\u8221" and the condition is deemed met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishum Rabbi Yehuda omer}: if the father agreed, {\i mekudeshet}; if the father did not agree, {\i eina mekudeshet}. Rabbi Yehuda requires genuine, positive agreement. The condition means what it says: the father must affirmatively consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallels Rav-Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'hacha bachada itteta dami} \u8212- and this case, the {\i gemara} notes, involves one woman with an explicit condition followed by {\i be\u8217'ilah}. And yet there is a {\i machlokes}: one {\i Tanna} holds that {\i be\u8217'ilah} renders them married even if the condition was not met, while Rabbi Yehuda holds that the explicit {\i tnai} continues to control. The {\i gemara} identifies this as paralleling the Rav-Shmuel dispute: even in the single-woman case, with an explicit {\i tnai}, followed by {\i be\u8217'ilah}, there is a dispute as to whether the {\i be\u8217'ilah} overrides the {\i tnai} or the {\i tnai} frames the {\i be\u8217'ilah}. This challenges Rava\u8217's claim that the single-woman case is universally agreed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} qualifies this: the {\i baraita} dispute may actually hinge not on whether {\i be\u8217'ilah} overrides the {\i tnai}, but on what the {\i tnai} means \u8212- does \u8220"if the father agrees\u8221" mean non-objection or affirmative consent? On that reading, both opinions in the {\i baraita} agree that if the condition is genuinely unmet, she would not be {\i mekudeshet}; they disagree only about what constitutes fulfillment of the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's ultimate contention, drawing this discussion together, is that when a {\i tnai} is explicit and clearly stated, it should always continue to frame the outcome \u8212- regardless of whether {\i be\u8217'ilah} subsequently takes place. The {\i be\u8217'ilah} does not override an explicit {\i tnai}; rather, the {\i tnai} colors all that follows. Where it appears that Rav holds otherwise \u8212- that {\i be\u8217'ilah} creates {\i kiddushin} unconditionally \u8212- Rava reads Rav as addressing a different configuration: cases where the situation is not simply one woman with one explicit {\i tnai}, but something more unusual that resembles the dynamic of two separate women. In the standard case of explicit conditional {\i kiddushin} followed by {\i nisu\u8217'in} with one woman, Rava maintains, the {\i tnai} ought to remain operative through all subsequent stages of the relationship. The discussion of this point continues in the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 74\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Raba\u8217's Tnai Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav v. Shmuel Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a foundational debate between Rav and Shmuel concerning a woman who is married subject to a {\i tnai} \u8212- a condition \u8212- and the condition is not ultimately met. The wrinkle is that after the {\i kiddushin}, the couple also proceeded to {\i nissuin}: they lived together and had marital relations. The question is how much the subsequent cohabitation and sexual relations, occurring in a fully marital context, operates to create a binding marriage despite the unmet condition, and how much the original {\i tnai} continues to frame and govern the relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's position is that the sexual relations subsequent to the {\i kiddushin} create a binding marriage regardless of the unfulfilled condition. Shmuel disagrees, holding that the {\i tnai} retains its framing force throughout. The Gemara at this stage is in the midst of Raba\u8217's attempt to situate this dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raba: Tnai Always Frames\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raba\u8217's proposal is that in a standard case of {\i kiddushin al tnai} \u8212- where the couple married subject to an explicit condition \u8212- both Rav and Shmuel would agree that the {\i tnai} continues to frame the ongoing marital relationship, and if the condition is not met, they are not married even if they have been living together and having relations. On Raba\u8217's reading, the dispute of Rav and Shmuel is confined to a different category of case: situations where the original marriage was somehow defective not because of a stated condition but for some other reason, such as a different set of women involved, or some other structural anomaly. The core claim is that an explicit {\i tnai} uttered at the outset operates continuously \u8212- it defines the meaning of every subsequent act within that marriage, including sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis carries enormous {\i halakhic} weight in contemporary discussions about using {\i tnai bekiddushin} as a potential solution to the {\i agunah} problem, and the Gemara will return to test and challenge it from multiple directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah Becomes Adult\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a {\i baraita} to challenge Raba\u8217's framework. The case involves a woman who was married as a minor, her husband divorced her while she was still a minor, and she then returned to him \u8212- also while still a minor. After {\i nissuin} with the first husband, the divorce was fully effective ({\i geirushin gemurin}), but the re-marriage was not biblically binding ({\i hazaratah einah hazarah gemurah}), since she was an emancipated minor \u8212- a {\i yetomah be\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av}, one who is no longer under her father\u8217's legal authority but has not yet reached adulthood, and whose capacity to effect a fully valid marriage is therefore only rabbinic at best.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When such a husband dies, the surviving brother-in-law may only perform {\i chalitzah} and not {\i yibum}, because the second marriage, being only rabbinic in nature, does not create the full legal status of {\i eshet ach} that would permit {\i yibum}, yet the original first marriage \u8212- which was fully biblical \u8212- creates a genuine {\i issur kares} risk for the brother. {\i Chalitzah} is therefore mandatory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then specifies: when is this so? Only if she was divorced as a minor and re-married as a minor. But if she was divorced as a minor and re-married as an adult, or divorced as an adult and re-married as an adult, or even re-married as a minor but then {\i gadlah etzlo} \u8212- matured into adulthood while still living with him \u8212- then the rule is {\i o choletzet o mityabemes}: the surviving brother may choose between {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum}, because by that point the marriage has acquired full biblical standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex After Ketanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i gadlah etzlo} \u8212- the girl who was re-married as a minor but became an adult while the marriage was ongoing \u8212- is directly relevant to the central question of the sugya. Here, the second marriage began as a merely rabbinic union, yet through the continued cohabitation and sexual relations once she had matured into adulthood, it became a full {\i kiddushin de-oraita}. This is evidence that continued marital relations can indeed elevate and transform the status of a defective initial {\i kiddushin} into a binding biblical marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that {\i Rabbi Eliezer} (in the name of {\i Rabbi Eliezer}) dissents even in these latter cases, ruling {\i choletzet ve-lo mityabemes} \u8212- only {\i chalitzah}, not {\i yibum} \u8212- out of concern that permitting {\i yibum} in the cases where the second marriage did become fully valid will lead people to permit it even in the case where the marriage remained merely rabbinic. This is a precautionary ruling. But the {\i tanna kamma} and Raba\u8217's analysis both take seriously the principle that continued relations can constitute new {\i kiddushin} in certain circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tnai vs.\u160 ?Ta\u8217'ut Distincti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presses Raba: the case of the {\i ketanah} who matured during the second marriage shows that continued sexual relations can indeed create binding {\i kiddushin} even when the original marriage was defective. How then can Raba claim that everyone agrees that a {\i tnai} negates the binding force of subsequent relations?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers, and Raba\u8217's distinction is clarified: the debate about whether continued relations create {\i kiddushin} when the original marriage was defective turns on a question of awareness and intent. In the {\i ketanah} case, one opinion holds that people are indeed aware that {\i kiddushei ketanah} have no biblical force, and therefore when they continue to have relations as husband and wife, the man {\i gamar u\u8217'ba\u8217'al leshum kiddushin} \u8212- he has specific intent for those relations to constitute an act of {\i kiddushin}. The other opinion holds that people are generally unaware that {\i kiddushei ketanah} are void, and therefore continued relations are understood by both parties simply as an extension of the original marriage, {\i ada\u8217'ata de-kiddushin rishonim ba\u8217'al} \u8212- performed in the spirit of the first {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and therefore they do not constitute an independent act of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This precise debate \u8212- whether continued relations are a new act or a continuation of the original frame \u8212- does not apply to the case of {\i tnai}. When an explicit condition was stated at the outset, says Raba, the {\i tnai} categorically and continuously frames all subsequent relations. There is no ambiguity about what the parties intended. Everyone, including Rav, would agree that if the condition is not met, those subsequent relations cannot be construed as an independent act of {\i kiddushin}, because they are definitionally understood within the framework of the stated condition. The debate of {\i ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- error about the binding force of the initial act \u8212- simply does not arise when the parties explicitly articulated a condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contemporary {\i halakhic} implications are significant. Rav Moshe Feinstein\u8217's approach to marriages contracted in non-Orthodox settings \u8212- where he argued that because the witnesses were affiliated with a movement that accepts {\i halitzah}, they are disqualified as {\i eidim}, rendering the original {\i kiddushin} void and the subsequent cohabitation legally framed by that void \u8212- closely parallels the Gemara\u8217's analysis here. If the original act was defective, the question is whether subsequent marital life cures it. Raba\u8217's framework, by distinguishing {\i tnai} cases from {\i ta\u8217'ut} cases, maps onto exactly this set of concerns.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tnai Validity & Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha: Tnai + Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now reports a teaching of Rav Acha bar Yaakov citing Rabbi Yochanan: {\i hamkadesh al tnai u\u8217'ba\u8217'al, divrei hakol} \u8212- if one performed {\i kiddushin} subject to a condition and then had sexual relations, by universal agreement she requires a {\i get} from him. This statement, read straightforwardly, appears to confirm Raba\u8217's view that the {\i tnai} continues to frame the marriage \u8212- everyone agrees the condition governs. The sexual relations do not override the {\i tnai}, and therefore if the condition is not met, she is not married and does not technically need a {\i get}, but the rabbis require a {\i get} as a precaution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Wait \u8212- the Gemara immediately introduces a challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Mutaat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge comes from the ruling that {\i chalitzah mutaat kesheirah} \u8212- {\i chalitzah} performed under mistaken circumstances is nevertheless valid. The Gemara asks: what constitutes {\i chalitzah} performed in error? Reish Lakish explains: where the rabbis told the {\i yavam} to perform {\i chalitzah} and told him that by doing so he would acquire her as his wife \u8212- they deceived him into performing {\i chalitzah} by misrepresenting it as the procedure for {\i yibum}. He performed {\i chalitzah} believing it would result in marriage, and the {\i chalitzah} is nonetheless valid and effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Intent Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, however, objects to that reading. He rules that {\i chalitzah} requires mutual intent \u8212- {\i bein shenitkavein hu velo nitkavnah hi, bein shenitkavnah hi velo nitkavein hu, chalitzatah pesulah} \u8212- whether only he intended it as {\i chalitzah} but not she, or only she intended it and not he, the {\i chalitzah} is invalid. It requires both parties to intend the act as {\i chalitzah} ({\i ad sheyitkavnu shneihem}). How then can we say that deceiving the {\i yavam} into thinking he was performing {\i yibum} constitutes valid {\i chalitzah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan therefore revises the definition of {\i chalitzah mutaat}: the case is where the rabbis told him to perform {\i chalitzah} on the condition that she would pay him two hundred {\i zuz}. He understood that this was {\i chalitzah} and consented to it, but the condition \u8212- the payment of two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- was never fulfilled. He intended to perform {\i chalitzah}, he performed it validly, but there was a deception regarding the condition attached to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acts Override Tnai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is now highly significant for the {\i tnai} discussion. The {\i chalitzah} was performed {\i al menat} \u8212- on condition \u8212- that she pay two hundred {\i zuz}, and the condition was not met. Yet the {\i chalitzah} was valid. The Gemara derives from this: {\i kol de\u8217'avid ma\u8217'aseh acholei achil letna\u8217'ei} \u8212- whoever performs an act has implicitly waived his condition. By going through with the act, the actor retroactively annuls his own {\i tnai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this principle is correct, then by the same logic, the man who performed {\i kiddushin al tnai} and then had sexual relations should equally be considered to have implicitly waived his condition through the act of {\i bia}. The {\i bia} would override the {\i tnai} just as the {\i chalitzah} overrode its condition, and the {\i kiddushin} would be fully binding \u8212- the exact opposite of Raba\u8217's universal-consensus claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why does any {\i tnai} work at all? When I sell an object conditionally, or give a {\i get} conditionally, the act of handing over the object or the document is itself an act \u8212- why doesn\u8217't that act equally override the condition? The Gemara\u8217's implicit answer is that acts like {\i chalitzah} and {\i bia} are qualitatively different \u8212- they are deeply personal, physically involving the actor in a way that handing over a ring or a document is not. The act of performing {\i chalitzah}, with all of its ritual degradation \u8212- the spitting, the declaration, the shoe removal \u8212- or the act of sexual union, are acts of such a personal and total nature that by going through with them one signals complete commitment to their legal effect, regardless of any stated condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Shaliach Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A student in the {\i yeshiva} raises the underlying conceptual question: where do we learn that conditions can render legal acts conditional at all? The source is the {\i tnai Bnei Gad u\u8217'Bnei Reuven} \u8212- Moshe\u8217's conditional assignment of the Trans-Jordanian territories. What is it about that case that serves as the paradigm? The answer given is: {\i tnai de-efshar le-kiyumei al yedei shaliach} \u8212- a condition is legally operative only when the act which the condition governs could in principle be performed through an agent. Since Moshe could delegate the apportionment of the land \u8212- indeed, Yehoshua ultimately performed it \u8212- the act was of the type where the principal\u8217's authority can be transferred. Where such delegation is impossible, the {\i tnai} has no legal force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot develops the underlying logic. Why specifically this criterion? The answer is: {\i ho\u8217'il ve-ha-ma\u8217'aseh kol kach biyado she-yakhol le-kayemo al yedei shaliach, svara hu she-t\u8217'nai k\u8217'ein biyado lismokh bo t\u8217'na\u8217'ei} \u8212- since the act is so thoroughly within the actor\u8217's power and control that he can transfer that power to another, he is equally empowered to determine the conditions under which the act will be binding. I sell my coffee cup: I can appoint an agent to sell it for me, which means I am genuinely the master of that transaction, and I can therefore make it conditional. I perform {\i kiddushin}: I can appoint an agent to perform the {\i kiddushin} on my behalf, which means I am genuinely the master of that act, and I can make it conditional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Empowerment Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle Tosafot articulates is one of {\i empowerment versus automatic status change}. If an act is one in which I am the empowered agent \u8212- I effect the transfer, I cause the status change \u8212- then I can also control {\i when} and {\i under what conditions} that change takes effect. But if the act is one in which the law simply says: if this act occurs, the status will change \u8212- and I am not so much the effector as the occasion \u8212- then I am not empowered in the relevant sense, and I cannot impose conditions on a status change that is not mine to control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chalitzah} exemplifies the latter. Nobody else can perform {\i chalitzah} on behalf of the {\i yavam}. This is not merely a physical limitation \u8212- it is a legal and conceptual one. The fact that the act cannot be delegated is evidence that the {\i yavam} is not empowered in the relevant sense. He performs certain actions and the law effects a status change, but he does not himself effect that change. He is therefore not in a position to condition it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same principle illuminates several other features of {\i chalitzah} and {\i yibum} noted in the context of tractate Yevamot. {\i Yibum} does not require {\i kavanah} \u8212- the very reality of the sexual act automatically creates the marital bond, regardless of intent \u8212- whereas normally a legal act requires the actor\u8217's conscious intention precisely because the actor is the one effecting the change. Similarly, there was a Talmudic position that even a {\i katan} might be able to perform {\i chalitzah}: if we understand that the {\i yavam} is not the agent effecting the change but merely the occasion through which the law effects it, the requirement of adult {\i da\u8217'at} is attenuated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bia & Tnai Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now directly confronts the apparent inconsistency: {\i bia} also cannot be performed through an agent \u8212- no one can have sexual relations on another man\u8217's behalf \u8212- yet we maintain that a {\i tnai} attached to {\i kiddushei bia} remains operative. How can this be?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i hasam mishum de-iskesh havayos le-hadadi} \u8212- because all three modes of {\i kiddushin} (money, document, and sexual relations) are textually linked to one another by the word {\i ve-haytah} in the verse. Since {\i kiddushei kesef} and {\i kiddushei shtar} can unambiguously be performed through an agent \u8212- the man is genuinely empowered in those modes \u8212- and since all three forms of {\i kiddushin} are halakhically equated, {\i bia} shares in that empowerment by association, even though the physical act of {\i bia} itself cannot be delegated. The {\i hekkesh} \u8212- the textual equation \u8212- extends the empowerment of the delegable forms to the non-delegable form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chalitzah}, by contrast, has no such {\i hekkesh} linking it to any delegable act. It stands alone, and standing alone it cannot carry conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution also brings into relief the deeper conceptual question running through the entire sugya: when marital cohabitation and sexual relations constitute the {\i de facto} reality of a couple\u8217's life together, is that reality itself the legally operative fact \u8212- automatic, objective, requiring no intent \u8212- or is it an act of {\i kiddushin} that the man effects with intention and over which he therefore has conditional control? The Gemara\u8217's answer, at least in the {\i tnai} framework, is the latter: because {\i bia} is equated with the other modes of {\i kiddushin} through the {\i hekkesh}, the man is understood as the empowered agent even in that context, and conditions therefore govern.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Universal Rule Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda: All Need Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a strikingly different teaching. Rav Yehuda, citing Ulla, citing Rabbi Elazar, rules: {\i hamikadesh be-milve u-va\u8217'al} \u8212- one who performs {\i kiddushin} using a forgiven loan and then has relations; {\i o al tnai u-va\u8217'al} \u8212- or with a {\i tnai} followed by relations; {\i o be-pachos mi-shaveh perutah u-va\u8217'al} \u8212- or with less than a {\i perutah} followed by relations \u8212- {\i de-kulei alma tzrichah heimenu get}: by universal agreement she requires a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the exact opposite of what Raba established. Raba argued that in the {\i tnai} case, everyone agrees no {\i get} is needed \u8212- the {\i tnai} governs and there is no binding marriage. Here Rav Yehuda rules that in all three categories \u8212- {\i milve}, {\i tnai}, and {\i pachos mi-shaveh perutah} \u8212- everyone agrees a {\i get} is required. Once sexual relations have occurred following even a defective {\i kiddushin}, the marriage is treated as binding enough to require dissolution. The implications for {\i hilchos mamzerut} and the {\i agunah} question are severe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef: Knowledge Mat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef bar Abba, in the name of Rav Nachman and Rav Ami, offers a narrower ruling: {\i hamikadesh be-pachos mi-shaveh perutah, tzarich heimenu get} \u8212- only the case of less than a {\i perutah} requires a {\i get} after subsequent relations. The reasoning is rooted in awareness. In the {\i pachos mi-shaveh perutah} case, everyone knows \u8212- or ought to know \u8212- that giving less than a {\i perutah} is not valid {\i kiddushin}. Because the defect is universally understood, the man\u8217's subsequent sexual relations are presumed to carry independent intent: he is aware the original act was void and therefore has {\i kavanah} for the {\i bia} to serve as a new, independent act of {\i kiddushin}. That intent transforms the sexual act into a binding {\i kiddushei bia}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i tnai} and {\i milve} cases, however, the man may not appreciate the defect. He may believe the original {\i kiddushin} was binding and that the subsequent relations are simply marital relations within an existing marriage, not a new act of {\i kiddushin}. Absent that awareness and intent, the subsequent {\i bia} cannot be construed as an independent act of {\i kiddushin}. Rav Yosef thus holds that intent is the operative variable: only where the parties know the original act was void will subsequent relations create binding {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kahana Rejects Raba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana rules in the name of Rav: {\i hamikadesh al tnai u-va\u8217'al, tzrichah heimenu get} \u8212- one who performs {\i kiddushin} subject to a condition and then has relations requires a {\i get}. This is a direct rejection of Raba\u8217's framework. Raba had argued that in the {\i tnai} case, everyone agrees the {\i tnai} governs and no {\i get} is needed. Rav Kahana rules, at minimum in the name of Rav, that the {\i tnai} case requires a {\i get}, and this opinion is adopted as the operative ruling \u8212- {\i l\u8217'chumra}. Even if Shmuel might disagree, we rule in accordance with Rav that {\i tnai} followed by sexual relations does require a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aseh: No Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reinforces this position with a precedent: {\i ve-zeh hayah ma\u8217'aseh, ve-lo hayah koach ba-chachamim le-hotziah beloa get} \u8212- this actually happened, and the rabbis did not have the power to release the woman without a {\i get}. A marriage had been contracted subject to a {\i tnai}, the couple had subsequently lived together and had relations, and when the question arose, the rabbis found themselves unable to permit the woman to walk away without a {\i get} from her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This statement is a striking one, particularly in the context of contemporary {\i agunah} discussions. Those who argue that the rabbinic authorities could resolve {\i agunah} cases through {\i hafka\u8217'at kiddushin} or other mechanisms often presuppose a broad rabbinic power to declare marriages void. The language here \u8212- {\i lo hayah koach be-yad chachamim} \u8212- asserts the opposite: the rabbis\u8217' hands were tied. They recognized opinions on both sides, they may have been aware of Shmuel\u8217's more lenient position, but they did not feel they could rely on those positions in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushei Ta\u8217'ut Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To balance the record, the Gemara cites a tannaitic source taking the opposite extreme. Rav Yehuda reports in the name of Shmuel, in the name of Rabbi Yishmael: {\i ve-hi lo nitpasha, asurah} \u8212- a married woman who committed adultery willingly is forbidden to her husband; {\i ha nitpasha, muteret} \u8212- if she was raped, she is permitted. And Rabbi Yishmael derives: {\i ve-yeish lecha acheret she-af al pi she-lo nitpasha, muteret} \u8212- there is another type of woman who, even though she was not coerced, is nonetheless permitted. Who is this? {\i Zo she-kiddusheha kiddushei ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- a woman whose original {\i kiddushin} was in error. Even if she has been living with this man for years, even if {\i bnah murkav al ktefah} \u8212- she is carrying their toddler on her shoulder \u8212- {\i mema\u8217'enet ve-holechet lah}: once it is established that the original {\i kiddushin} was a {\i ta\u8217'ut}, she declares her rejection of the marriage and walks away, and she is entirely free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This tannaitic position represents the outer pole of the opposing approach: no matter how many years of married life follow a {\i kiddushin} that was void from the outset, the subsequent cohabitation creates nothing, and the woman is free at any time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya thus presents three distinct positions. Raba: in a {\i tnai} case, everyone agrees the {\i tnai} governs and no {\i get} is needed; the debate is in other cases. Rav Yehuda citing Rabbi Elazar: in all cases \u8212- {\i tnai}, {\i milve}, {\i ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- everyone agrees a {\i get} is needed once relations have occurred. And the tannaitic teaching of Rabbi Yishmael: in a {\i ta\u8217'ut} case, even years of cohabitation with children create nothing, and the woman may walk away freely at any time.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Condition Fulfillment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Annulment Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now shifts to a related but distinct question: once we have established that a {\i tnai} governs a {\i kiddushin}, how do we determine whether the condition has been met or not? The {\i Mishnah} states that if a man betroths a woman {\i al tnai she-ein lah nedarim} \u8212- on condition that she has no vows \u8212- and she turns out to have vows, she is not {\i mekudeshes}. The Gemara now considers wrinkles in this basic rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} teaches: {\i halchah etzel chacham ve-hitira, mekudeshes} \u8212- if after accepting the ring she went to a rabbinic sage and had the vow annulled, she is {\i mekudeshes}. The rationale will become clear, but the basic ruling is that the subsequent annulment satisfies the condition retroactively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The countercase: {\i halchah etzel rofe ve-hirpasah, eina mekudeshes} \u8212- if she went to a doctor and was healed of physical blemishes that had been the subject of a separate condition, she is not {\i mekudeshes}. The healing does not retroactively satisfy the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sage vs.\u160 ?Doctor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what is the difference between the sage and the doctor? The answer is: {\i chacham oker et ha-neder mei-ikaro} \u8212- the sage uproots the vow from its very inception. The mechanism of {\i hatarat nedarim} operates retroactively: the sage asks the person whether, had she contemplated the full circumstances at the time of taking the vow, she would still have taken it. When she says she would not, the ruling is that the vow was never properly formed in the first place \u8212- it was never binding from the outset. The sage does not release her from an existing obligation; he establishes that the obligation never truly arose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, when we look back to the moment she accepted the ring, she never had a vow. The condition is satisfied retroactively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The doctor, by contrast, {\i eino mirapeh ela mikan u-le-haba} \u8212- heals her only prospectively from this point forward. The blemish existed at the time of {\i kiddushin}, and the doctor\u8217's intervention does not change that historical fact. The condition \u8212- that she had no blemish \u8212- was not satisfied at the relevant moment, and the subsequent cure does not alter this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradictory Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A contradictory {\i baraita}, however, teaches that {\i halchah etzel chacham ve-hitira, eina mekudeshes} as well \u8212- even going to a sage and having the vow annulled fails to satisfy the condition. Both the sage route and the doctor route are equally ineffective. This directly contradicts the first {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raba: Meir v. Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raba resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya \u8212- ha Rabbi Meir, ha Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- the two {\i baraitot} reflect the positions of Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Eliezer respectively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question driving this dispute is not merely whether the vow was retroactively voided. Even if we accept that the sage\u8217's annulment operates retroactively and the woman technically had no vow at the moment of {\i kiddushin}, the further question is: was the condition satisfied in a manner the husband would have accepted? And that depends on whether the husband is assumed to object to his wife going before a {\i beit din} to have a vow annulled. If a man considers it unseemly \u8212- {\i bizayon} \u8212- for his wife to appear before a rabbinic court for {\i hatarat nedarim}, then even though the vow was retroactively voided, the condition he set is not considered fulfilled, because he would never have intended to accept that route as a legitimate resolution of the condition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meir v. Eliezer Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying debate of Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Eliezer is found in a {\i Mishnah} dealing with a different context: a man who divorces his wife because she has vows ({\i mishum neder}) or because of a bad reputation ({\i biglal shem ra}) \u8212- {\i lo yachazir} \u8212- he may not remarry her. The concern is that if he is later permitted to remarry her, he will be in a position to retroactively claim the divorce was an error, thereby creating grave problems for any intervening marriage and its children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Unconditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's interpretation of {\i lo yachazir} focuses on a different mechanism than that of Tosafot. Tosafot understands the prohibition as removing the incentive to make retroactive claims \u8212- if he cannot remarry her, he has no reason to claim the divorce was defective. Rashi, however, reads the prohibition as establishing at the outset that the divorce was unconditional and therefore irrevocable. By telling the husband that no matter what happens later, he will never be permitted to remarry her, the rabbis ensure that when he divorces her he does so with {\i gemirut da\u8217'at} \u8212- full and final resolve. There can therefore be no legitimate subsequent claim that he divorced her in error, because his divorce was declared absolute and final from the moment he gave the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir: Sage Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir rules: {\i kol neder she-tzarich chakirat chacham lo yachazir} \u8212- for any vow that requires the intervention of a sage to annul, he may not remarry her; {\i ve-she-eino tzarich chakirat chacham, yachazir} \u8212- but if the vow is one that does not require a sage \u8212- meaning, a vow the husband himself could have annulled through {\i hafara} \u8212- he may remarry her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir: Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's reasoning is as follows. If the vow was one that the husband could have annulled himself through {\i hafara}, and he divorced her instead, he cannot later claim he would have acted differently had he known about his ability to annul. He knew, or should have known, that he had this power, and he divorced her anyway. The divorce therefore stands firmly, and remarriage is permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the vow required a sage to annul, then the husband could legitimately say: had I known you could go to a {\i chacham} and have this vow retroactively eliminated, I never would have divorced you. According to Rabbi Meir, this is a plausible and legitimate claim, because Rabbi Meir implicitly assumes that a husband would {\i not} object to his wife going to a sage for {\i hatarat nedarim}. It is a reasonable course of action in his view. Therefore, the possibility of {\i hatarat nedarim} is something the husband should reasonably be expected to have wanted, and we prevent remarriage precisely to prevent him from making this destabilizing retroactive claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer: No Bizayon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer takes the opposite position. He rules: {\i lo asru zeh ela mipnei she-eino zeh} \u8212- they only prohibited remarriage in the case requiring a sage as a {\i gezerah} on account of the case not requiring a sage. The fundamental case of concern for Rabbi Eliezer is the {\i hafara} case: where the husband himself could have nullified the vow but did not realize he had that power. In that case he can say {\i had I known I could have been mefer it, I never would have divorced her} \u8212- a fully legitimate claim of error. To prevent such claims, the rabbis also prohibited remarriage in the {\i chacham} case, even though in the {\i chacham} case there is no legitimate retroactive claim. Why not? Because, says Rabbi Eliezer, {\i ein adam rotzeh she-tit\u8217'bazeh ishto be-veit din} \u8212- a man does not want his wife to be subjected to the process of appearing before a {\i beit din}. It is a matter of dignity and reputation. The husband would never have wanted that scenario, and therefore he cannot come after the fact and say he would have preferred it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Applied to Tnai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this debate now in hand, Raba applies it back to our {\i kiddushin} case. When a man betroths a woman on condition that she has no vows, and she does have a vow but subsequently obtains {\i hatarat nedarim} from a sage \u8212- is the condition considered satisfied?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Meir, yes. Rabbi Meir holds that a husband finds it entirely acceptable for his wife to appear before a sage for {\i hatarat nedarim}. Since the sage\u8217's annulment operates retroactively to void the vow from inception, at the moment of {\i kiddushin} she effectively had no vow. And since the husband has no objection to the mechanism by which that retroactive elimination was achieved, the condition is considered fulfilled and she is {\i mekudeshes}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Eliezer, no. Rabbi Eliezer holds that a husband does {\i not} want his wife appearing before a {\i beit din} for {\i hatarat nedarim} \u8212- it involves a degree of public exposure and {\i bizayon} that he finds unacceptable. Therefore, even if the vow was retroactively voided through {\i hatarat nedarim}, the condition \u8220"she has no vows\u8221" was not satisfied in a manner the husband would have countenanced. He intended to marry a woman who had no vows {\i and} who would never need to seek rabbinic intervention of this kind. The condition is therefore not met, and she remains not {\i mekudeshes}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i baraita} \u8212- which rules that {\i hatarat nedarim} satisfies the condition \u8212- follows Rabbi Meir. The second {\i baraita} \u8212- which rules it does not \u8212- follows Rabbi Eliezer. And here the sugya rests.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 76\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kan Nimtzu Kan Hayu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens with a mishnah that frames a fundamental question: a husband stipulated that his wife have no blemishes, and after marriage it is discovered that she does. The critical issue is whether those blemishes pre-dated the {\i kiddushin}\u8212-the giving of the ring\u8212-or emerged only afterward. The stakes are significant. If the blemishes pre-existed the {\i kiddushin}, the condition was never satisfied, the marriage never took effect, and there is no {\i ketubah}. If they appeared after the {\i kiddushin}, the marriage is valid, a {\i get} is required for dissolution, and she is entitled to her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah resolves the question by reference to location of discovery. If the blemishes were found while she was still in her father\u8217's house\u8212-before {\i nissuin} and {\i chuppah}\u8212-the burden of proof falls on her (or her father) to demonstrate that the blemishes arose only after the {\i kiddushin}. If they were found only after she entered the husband\u8217's domain following {\i chuppah}, the burden of proof falls on him to demonstrate that the blemishes pre-dated the {\i kiddushin}. The first answer the Gemara offered to explain this distinction was that the mishnah is really two separate opinions stitched together\u8212-a rather unsatisfying resolution. The Gemara now provides a more substantive explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava proposes the principle of {\i kan nimtze\u8217'u kan hayu}\u8212-where they were found, there they occurred. The logic is as follows: if the blemishes were discovered while she was in her father\u8217's house, we presume they existed from before, as a longstanding feature of her body during her years growing up in that household. The burden of proof therefore falls on her to demonstrate otherwise. Conversely, if the blemishes were discovered only after she entered the husband\u8217's domain, we presume they arose there\u8212-that is, after the {\i kiddushin}. It then becomes the husband\u8217's burden to prove they pre-existed. The presumption tracks the location of discovery, and whichever stage she was in at the time of discovery determines where we assign the origin of the problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye raises a pointed challenge to Rava\u8217's principle. The mishnah states that when the husband bears the burden of proof, he must demonstrate that the blemishes existed from {\i before} the {\i kiddushin}\u8212-{\i ad she\u8217'lo titars}\u8212-and it is not sufficient to show merely that they existed at some point {\i after} the {\i kiddushin} but while she was still in the father\u8217's house. Abaye asks: why not? If Rava\u8217's principle of {\i kan nimtze\u8217'u kan hayu} operates symmetrically, then once the husband proves that the blemishes were observed while she was still in her father\u8217's house\u8212-even after the {\i kiddushin}\u8212-we should apply the same presumption and conclude that since they were found there, they were always there, pushing their origin back to before the {\i kiddushin}. Why does the husband need to prove directly that they pre-dated the {\i kiddushin}? Should it not suffice to demonstrate discovery in the father\u8217's house and let {\i kan nimtze\u8217'u kan hayu} do the rest?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate this with a timeline: there is the period in her father\u8217's house before {\i kiddushin}, then the {\i kiddushin} itself, then the continued period in her father\u8217's house post-{\i kiddushin}, and finally the {\i chuppah} and entry into the husband\u8217's domain. If the {\i mumim} were seen at any point in her father\u8217's house, even in the post-{\i kiddushin} stage, why can\u8217't the husband rely on {\i kan nimtze\u8217'u kan hayu} to extend that discovery back to a pre-{\i kiddushin} origin?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: {\i mishum de\u8217'ika lemeimar chazakah ein adam shoteh be\u8217'kos ella im ken bodko}\u8212-because there is a countervailing presumption that a person does not drink from the cup without first inspecting it. A man does not proceed to {\i chuppah} without examining what he is getting into. If the husband can prove the blemishes were discoverable in the father\u8217's house during the post-{\i kiddushin} period, the logical response is: if it was discoverable, we presume he discovered it, and if he then proceeded to the {\i chuppah}, he must have been acceptable with it. The act of proceeding to {\i chuppah} thus neutralizes the force of {\i kan nimtze\u8217'u kan hayu} in that direction. It is no longer enough to show the blemish existed in that intermediate period; he would have known about it, and his decision to proceed to {\i chuppah} signals his acceptance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presume Prior Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then Abaye presses further: if going through with the {\i chuppah} shows he was accepting even of something discovered in the father\u8217's house post-{\i kiddushin}, why does it not equally signal acceptance of blemishes discovered before the {\i kiddushin}? After all, he went through with the {\i chuppah} in that case too, and if it was discoverable, we presume he saw it. Why does proof of a pre-{\i kiddushin} blemish enable him to void the marriage when proof of a post-{\i kiddushin} blemish discovered in the father\u8217's house does not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds that the two cases are parallel: {\i ela mishenitarsah}\u8212-the same logic applies from the moment of betrothal. If the blemish was demonstrably pre-existing, the {\i chuppah} does not forfeit his claim, because the {\i ein adam shoteh be\u8217'kos} presumption operates equally in the later stage. The question then becomes: why does it specifically matter where the blemishes are found, and what happens to the competing presumptions once {\i chuppah} has taken place? The Gemara makes explicit that after {\i nissuin}, there are {\i two} considerations operating in her favor, and the resolution turns on how many presumptions each side can muster.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Competing Presumptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Presumptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the blemishes are discovered after {\i chuppah}\u8212-in the husband\u8217's domain\u8212-and he can demonstrate they were visible in the father\u8217's house post-{\i kiddushin}, there are two things that favor the woman. The first is {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf}\u8212-the presumption that her body was in its normal state, meaning she was not born with these blemishes, and therefore we presume they arose as late as possible. This presumption reinforces the claim that they emerged only after the {\i kiddushin}. The second is the {\i chuppah} itself: {\i ein adam shoteh be\u8217'kos ella im ken bodko}\u8212-the very fact that he went through with the {\i chuppah} suggests he examined her and found it acceptable. Together, these two arguments favor her position: the blemish probably came late, and even if it was visible, he was presumably accepting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counterargument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against these two arguments, the husband can deploy one counter-presumption: {\i chazakah ein adam mitpayes be\u8217'mumim}\u8212-a man does not simply make peace with blemishes, especially when he had explicitly stipulated against them. This counterargument directly neutralizes the {\i chuppah} presumption. He went through the {\i chuppah}, yes, but that does not necessarily mean he was aware of or accepting toward the blemish, since the presumption runs the other way: a man in his position would not be so cavalier about something he stipulated against.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neutralizing Arguments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is elegant: the {\i chuppah} presumption and the {\i ein adam mitpayes be\u8217'mumim} counter-presumption cancel each other out\u8212-{\i chad be\u8217'makom tarti lo amrinan}, one argument does not prevail against two. When those two are neutralized, what remains is {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf}, which operates uncontested in her favor, presuming the blemish arose as late as possible and thus after the {\i kiddushin}. That is why when the blemishes are found in the husband\u8217's domain, even if he proves they were visible in the father\u8217's house post-{\i kiddushin}, he still cannot win: her {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf} stands undisturbed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if he proves the blemish pre-dated the {\i kiddushin}, the {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf} can no longer help her\u8212-he has directly established that the blemish was there from before. At that point, the only argument in her favor would be the {\i chuppah} presumption, but that is neutralized by {\i ein adam mitpayes be\u8217'mumim}. And since money cannot be extracted without preponderant proof\u8212-{\i ve\u8217'oki mamona achezkatei}\u8212-he wins and the marriage is voided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, then, is the logic: when the blemishes are found post-{\i chuppah}, she wins unless he proves pre-{\i kiddushin} origin, because she always has {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf} on her side unless it is directly dislodged by evidence. The {\i chuppah} dimension neither helps nor hurts in itself, since the competing {\i chazakot} neutralize each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a question: we have now essentially neutralized the {\i chuppah} presumption through the counter-argument. But the original question was: why doesn\u8217't {\i kan nimtze\u8217'u kan hayu} by itself determine the result, as it does when the blemish is found in the father\u8217's house before {\i chuppah}? There, even the {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf} that would favor the woman is apparently overridden\u8212-we simply say, found in the father\u8217's house, always was there. Why does {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf} prevail post-{\i chuppah} but not pre-{\i chuppah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's answer introduces a powerful logical framework. In the case where {\i chuppah} has taken place, in order for the husband to win, two conditions must simultaneously be true: first, that going through the {\i chuppah} did not constitute acceptance\u8212-{\i ein adam mitpayes be\u8217'mumim}; and second, that the blemish in fact pre-dated the {\i kiddushin}\u8212-{\i kan nimtze\u8217'u kan hayu}. Call these condition A and condition B. Both A {\i and} B must hold for him to prevail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the woman to win, however, only one of the two needs to fail. Either: even if the blemish existed earlier, the {\i chuppah} shows he was accepting (not-A), or: the blemish did not pre-date the {\i kiddushin} (not-B). Since she needs only {\i one} of these to be true, while he needs {\i both}, the weight of the situation favors her. Tosafot states this explicitly: {\i afilu lo yehei ella echad maihem emes}\u8212-even if only one of these arguments is true, she wins and collects the {\i ketubah}. For him to escape liability, {\i both} must be true\u8212-and if even one is false, her claim prevails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains the asymmetry. Before {\i chuppah}, there is no factor A in play at all. The only operative question is whether the blemish pre-dated the {\i kiddushin}, and {\i kan nimtze\u8217'u kan hayu} determines that presumptively in his favor. But once {\i chuppah} has occurred, a second dimension enters the picture, and the logical structure changes: he now carries the burden of satisfying two conditions simultaneously, while she wins if either condition is unsatisfied. The practical implication for burden of proof is clear: after {\i chuppah}, the entire weight of proof shifts to him, and proving only that the blemish existed post-{\i kiddushin} but pre-{\i chuppah} is insufficient\u8212-he must reach all the way back to pre-{\i kiddushin} to win.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what does it mean in practice to prove that a man was or was not accepting of a blemish? The answer is that these are not individually provable facts so much as legal presumptions that shift the burden of proof. The force of {\i ein adam shoteh be\u8217'kos} is not that each case turns on a factual inquiry into the man\u8217's subjective state; rather, it means that having gone through with the {\i chuppah}, the legal burden on him is now heavier. He cannot simply point to a discoverable blemish and call the marriage void. He must establish in more absolute terms\u8212-pushing back to before the {\i kiddushin}\u8212-that the foundational condition was never satisfied. As soon as {\i chuppah} has occurred, that additional burden attaches, and without meeting it, the marriage stands and the {\i ketubah} is owed. Whether or not he had explicitly made a {\i tenai}, if he cannot satisfy both conditions, she is entitled to a {\i get} and her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers an entirely different explanation for the mishnah\u8217's distinction. When the blemishes are discovered while she is still in the father\u8217's house, this implies she is still in the legal status of a {\i ketanah} or {\i na\u8217'arah}\u8212-conceptually under her father\u8217's jurisdiction. In such a case, if the husband performed {\i kiddushin} and then {\i gerushin} without {\i nissuin}, who is entitled to the {\i ketubah}? The father. So the argument being made in court\u8212-that the blemish arose only after the {\i kiddushin}\u8212-is being made by the woman on her own firsthand knowledge of her body, but the beneficiary of the claim is the father, who lacks that firsthand knowledge. This weakens the claim. The woman knows when the blemish arose; the father does not. When the person with direct knowledge makes a claim on behalf of someone without that knowledge, it occupies a weaker legal footing. Therefore she bears the burden of proof: {\i mana l\u8217'abba biyadeich}\u8212-\u8220"you owe my father a hundred.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After {\i nissuin}, the woman is her own legal person and asserts the claim entirely in her own right: {\i mana li biyadeich}\u8212-\u8220"you owe me a hundred.\u8221" She is the one who experienced the blemish, she is the one with the information, and she is the one collecting. This places her in a stronger position, and accordingly the burden of proof shifts to the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha the son of Rav Avya challenges Rav Ashi from a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Meir concedes that when the blemishes are of the type that could naturally have accompanied her from her father\u8217's house\u8212-{\i ha-ra\u8217'uyin lavo immah mibeit aviha}\u8212-even if they are found after she has entered the husband\u8217's domain, the burden of proof falls on the father. But according to Rav Ashi\u8217's explanation, once she is in the husband\u8217's house she is her own claimant and should be in the stronger position. Why would the father bear the burden even in that case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Congenital Blemishes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by explaining that the {\i baraita} is referring to congenital blemishes\u8212-{\i yateret}, an extra finger or similar feature present from birth. If the blemish is congenital, the question of whose house it was \u8220"discovered in\u8221" is irrelevant: it obviously pre-dated the {\i kiddushin}. The only remaining question is whether the husband knew about it and was accepting. The \u8220"proof\u8221" the father or bride would bring in such a case is not to show when the blemish arose\u8212-that is not in dispute\u8212-but to demonstrate that the husband had been shown the blemish and consented, perhaps with witnesses present. This, then, is an entirely different category, and the {\i baraita} does not undermine Rav Ashi\u8217's position. The general framework of the mishnah applies to blemishes that are not congenital\u8212-those whose timing of appearance is genuinely uncertain\u8212-and the location of discovery determines where the burden of proof falls.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Donkey & Cow Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces an analogous case from the world of commerce to test and apply these principles. Rav Yehuda quotes Shmuel: if someone sells his cow in exchange for a donkey, and the buyer takes possession of the cow ({\i mashach}\u8212-which completes the transaction), and then the two go to collect the donkey only to find it dead\u8212-{\i al ba\u8217'al ha\u8217'chamor lehavi re\u8217'ayah she\u8217'hayah chamoro kayam bi\u8217'sha\u8217'at meshichat parah}\u8212-the owner of the donkey bears the burden of proof that his donkey was still alive at the moment the cow was taken. He must demonstrate that the transaction was valid and that the death occurred afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this striking is that at least three considerations would seem to favor the donkey-owner. First, there is a presumption that the transaction was a valid sale. Second, he has physical possession of the cow. Third, {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf} would seem to apply: we should presume the donkey died as late as possible, meaning after the transaction was finalized. And yet, despite all this, the burden of proof rests on him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that this appears to align with the mishnah\u8217's case of the {\i kallah}, and attempts to identify which part of the mishnah provides the parallel. The case where the blemish is found in the father\u8217's house might seem to match\u8212-in both cases the claimant has the burden\u8212-but the comparison does not hold: in the mishnah, the party bearing the burden is the one trying to extract money, whereas in Shmuel\u8217's case, the donkey-owner bears the burden to {\i keep} what he already has. The Gemara then considers the second half of the mishnah, where it is the husband who bears the burden even though he holds the money. Rav Chanina objects that even this comparison is imperfect: there, the husband bears the burden because {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf} operates in the woman\u8217's favor\u8212-a countervailing presumption offsets his possession. Here, the donkey-owner not only has the cow in hand but also has the {\i chazakah} that the donkey died late. He has both the possession and the favorable presumption, yet still bears the burden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Burden Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak resolves this by pointing to the first half of the mishnah after all\u8212-the {\i kallah} found with a blemish in the father\u8217's house. There too, she has {\i chazakat ha\u8217'guf} in her favor, and she still bears the burden of proof. Why? Because what she is really trying to protect is the {\i ring}\u8212-the object given over for {\i kiddushin}. The ring is in her possession, and she has a {\i chazakah} on her side, yet she must prove her case. The reason is that her possession of the ring is inherently contingent: the ring was given to her only on the assumption that the transaction\u8212-the {\i kiddushin}\u8212-was valid. Since the validity of that transaction is precisely what is in question, she cannot be considered to be in full undisturbed possession of the ring in the ordinary legal sense. Her claim to the ring is only as strong as her ability to establish that the {\i kiddushin} took hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to the donkey-and-cow case is therefore apt: the donkey-owner took the cow on the assumption that the exchange was valid. Since the validity of the exchange is the very thing in dispute\u8212-did the donkey die before or after the transaction was finalized?\u8212-he cannot stand on his possession of the cow as though it were unencumbered. His possession is conditional on the transaction having been good, and proving the transaction was good is his burden. This is, structurally, the same as the {\i kallah} keeping the ring: possession of something received conditionally does not relieve one of the burden of establishing the condition was met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ring Conditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara further explores whether the {\i kiddushin} ring must be returned if the transaction is voided. There is a broader dispute in the Gemara about whether {\i kiddushin} money is given {\i le\u8217'tivu\u8217'a}\u8212-\u8220"to be sunk,\u8221" meaning the woman keeps it regardless of what follows\u8212-or not. One might think that even if the {\i kiddushin} is voided, if the money was given {\i le\u8217'tivu\u8217'a}, she keeps the ring. The Gemara responds: that principle applies only to valid {\i kiddushin} that subsequently falls apart\u8212-for instance, if the husband dies before {\i chuppah}. There the {\i kiddushin} was real, the ring was given for good, and even misfortune afterward does not undo her acquisition. But here the claim is {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'ut}\u8212-the {\i kiddushin} was never valid in the first place because the condition was not met. There was never a real {\i kiddushin} to which the {\i le\u8217'tivu\u8217'a} logic could apply. If she can prove the {\i kiddushin} was valid\u8212-that the blemish arose only afterward\u8212-she keeps the ring and the {\i ketubah}. If she cannot, there was no {\i kiddushin} and the ring reverts. This makes clear that the act of {\i chuppah} and {\i nissuin} are treated as legally distinct from {\i kiddushin}, a point the case underscores throughout.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's True Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak bar Shmuel bar Marta arrives with a correction: do not rely on the principles transmitted by Yehuda\u8212-his brother\u8212-in Shmuel\u8217's name. Those principles misrepresent Shmuel\u8217's actual position. {\i Lo tetzi\u8217'tu lehu lehani klalei dechayil Yehuda achi mishmeih di-Shmuel}\u8212-do not follow those general rules that Yehuda my brother formulated in Shmuel\u8217's name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 True Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What Shmuel actually said is this: {\i kol she\u8217'nolad safek birshuto, alav ha\u8217'ra\u8217'ayah}\u8212-whoever\u8217's domain the doubt arose in, that party bears the burden of proof. The principle is not about who wants to extract money from whom; it is about where the uncertain event\u8212-the death of the donkey, the appearance of the blemish\u8212-is presumed to have occurred. And this maps directly onto the mishnah of the {\i kallah}: when the {\i mum} is found in the father\u8217's domain, the problem is presumed to have arisen there, and she bears the burden of proof. When it is found in the husband\u8217's domain, the problem is presumed to have arisen there, and he bears the burden. Shmuel\u8217's principle and the mishnah\u8217's structure are perfectly aligned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Needle Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings the case of a needle found embedded in the thick folds of {\i beit ha\u8217'kosot}\u8212-one of the animal\u8217's stomachs. If the puncture exits both sides, the animal is a {\i treifah}; if only one side, it is {\i kasherah}. If there is a clot of dried blood at the wound\u8212-indicating it formed before {\i shechitah}\u8212-the animal is a {\i treifah}. If there is no such blood clot, the puncture is assumed to have occurred after {\i shechitah} and the animal is acceptable. A scab forming over the wound takes three days. So if the animal was sold to the butcher ({\i shocheit}) within the last two days, and after {\i shechitah} a scab is found, we know the injury pre-dated the sale\u8212-and the seller owes the butcher. But if there is no scab and the timing is ambiguous\u8212-it could have occurred before or after the sale\u8212-who bears the burden of proof?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: {\i ha\u8217'motzi mei\u8217'chaveiro alav ha\u8217'ra\u8217'ayah}\u8212-whoever wishes to extract money from the other bears the burden of proof. This seems to say that if the butcher wants his money back, he must prove the animal was already injured at the time of sale. Yet Shmuel\u8217's principle says the burden follows where the doubt arose\u8212-and the doubt arose in the butcher\u8217's domain, since the animal was in his possession when slaughtered. These appear to contradict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: {\i depara damei}\u8212-the {\i baraita} is speaking in the case where the butcher has already paid. In that situation, saying \u8220"whoever wants to extract the money bears the burden\u8221" simply means the butcher always bears the burden, since he is the one seeking a refund. This is consistent with Shmuel\u8217's principle: the problem arose in the butcher\u8217's domain, the butcher bears the burden. The formulation \u8220"whoever wants to extract money\u8221" is not an independent principle; it is a description of the butcher, premised on the assumption\u8212-{\i stama demilsa}\u8212-that ordinarily a seller does not hand over his cow without first receiving payment. The normal case is that the butcher has already paid, and so the {\i baraita}\u8217's language, read in context, always describes the butcher as the one who would be extracting money if he seeks redress.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might object: why not state the rule directly\u8212-\u8220"the butcher bears the burden\u8221"\u8212-instead of using the roundabout formulation about extracting money? The Gemara concedes the force of this question ({\i u\u8217'mai piska}\u8212-what makes you read it so categorically?) and answers with {\i stama demilsa}: it is the ordinary presumption of commercial transactions that payment precedes delivery, so the {\i baraita}\u8217's language presupposes the butcher paid and simply follows from that assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the end, this sugya surfaces two distinct approaches to the allocation of the burden of proof, and the tension between them runs throughout. The first and more fundamental approach\u8212-which Rav Yitzchak bar Shmuel identifies as Shmuel\u8217's true position\u8212-is that the burden follows the location of the doubt: wherever the problem is presumed to have arisen, that party bears the burden of proof. This is {\i safek birshuto}, and it maps cleanly onto the mishnah\u8217's structure for blemishes discovered in the father\u8217's house versus the husband\u8217's house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second approach, which surfaced from the {\i treifah} case and initially seemed to represent Shmuel\u8217's view, is the more familiar rule of {\i ha\u8217'motzi mei\u8217'chaveiro alav ha\u8217'ra\u8217'ayah}: whoever seeks to extract money from another bears the burden of proof. This principle is not wrong\u8212-it is a standard rule of monetary law\u8212-but it is not the operative logic in these cases. The Gemara works to show that the {\i baraita} about the needle, properly read, does not rest on this principle independently; it is merely describing a situation where the butcher, who already paid, happens to be the one seeking to extract. The underlying logic remains: the problem arose in his domain, and so the burden is his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two approaches\u8212-location of the problem versus direction of money extraction\u8212-stand as the two frameworks through which burden of proof is allocated when the timing of a disqualifying event is genuinely uncertain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 77\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blemishes & Burden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stipulation & Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens two lines from the bottom of 76b, wrapping up the {\i mishnah} that addresses the situation where a husband stipulated \u8212- or perhaps even without stipulating \u8212- that his wife should have no blemishes, and blemishes were subsequently discovered. The central question is one of timing: did the blemishes predate the {\i kiddushin} or arise afterward?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's framework turns on location. If the blemishes are discovered while the wife was still in her father\u8217's house, the burden of proof falls on her. If they are discovered in his house, the burden falls on him. The underlying logic is that each party must prove what happened within the other\u8217's domain of possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} had examined this framework through an analogy involving the exchange of animals and the case of one who sells a cow to a {\i shochet}, only for the animal to be discovered a {\i treifa}. In working through that case, the {\i Gemara} held up two distinct models of burden of proof. The first is the familiar principle of {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'aya} \u8212- the burden falls on whoever is trying to extract money. The second model, which ultimately governs both the {\i shochet} case and our {\i mishnah}, is the {\i reshut} model: the burden of proof is determined by in whose possession, in whose domain, the problem was discovered. If the {\i shochet} finds the animal to be a {\i treifa} in his own {\i reshut} after purchase, it is his burden to prove the condition predated the sale. This result stands in some tension with the default assumption of {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'aya}, and the {\i Gemara} resolves it in favor of the {\i reshut} model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidden Blemishes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion concludes on 76b with the category of {\i mumin shebeseter} \u8212- blemishes that are concealed and not ordinarily exposed to observation. Here the {\i Chachamim} distinguish between {\i mumin shebegaluy}, blemishes visible to anyone, and those that are hidden. The ruling is that with respect to visible blemishes, or blemishes that could reasonably have been detected \u8212- even those covered by clothing \u8212- we presume the husband knew or could have known. This is buttressed by the bathhouse principle: since female relatives of the husband would attend the bathhouse together with the potential bride, the husband had a practical avenue through which to investigate hidden blemishes. If he did not raise an objection, we presume he was aware and consented.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Epilepsy Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within this context, Rav Nachman rules that a person with epilepsy has a condition that is classified like {\i mumin shebeseter}, since \u8212- as Rashi explains \u8212- if the epileptic episodes occur at a fixed and predictable time, it would be possible for the woman to conceal them. She could ensure she was not observed during those intervals, so the husband cannot claim he would certainly have known. However, {\i vehanei milei dekvia lei zman} \u8212- this applies only when the timing of the seizures is fixed. If the timing is not fixed, {\i kemumin shebegaluy dami} \u8212- it is treated like a visible blemish, since there would have been no way to reliably conceal episodes that could strike at any moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding to the next {\i mishnah}, one structural point about the passage deserves clarification. The {\i mishnah} began with the case where the husband stipulated {\i al menat she\u8217'ein bach mumin} at the time of {\i kiddushin}, and then conducted the {\i chuppah} without repeating the stipulation ({\i kinnesa stam}). The {\i Gemara} raised the question of whether the second clause \u8212- {\i kinnesa stam} \u8212- is dependent on the prior stipulation at {\i kiddushin}, or whether it stands as an entirely independent case where no stipulation was made at all, neither at {\i kiddushin} nor at {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the latter reading is adopted \u8212- that even without any stipulation, a husband may divorce a wife with certain blemishes without paying the {\i ketubah} \u8212- then the ruling is quite sweeping: the mere presence of certain pre-existing blemishes entitles the husband to divorce without {\i ketubah} payment, even in the absence of any explicit condition. This reading will bear on the {\i halacha l\u8217'maaseh} to be discussed shortly, particularly the {\i Rambam}\u8217's ruling that even when no stipulation was made, if the wife had blemishes prior to the marriage that the husband neither knew of nor could reasonably have discovered, he is entitled to divorce her without a {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Blemishes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Blemishes Post\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to the reverse scenario: {\i ha-ish she-noldu bo mumin}, a man who develops blemishes after the marriage. The {\i tanna kamma} rules {\i ein kofin oto lehotzi} \u8212- we do not force him to divorce his wife. The wife, in other words, must tolerate a husband who has acquired blemishes during the marriage. Notably, the parallel question about forcing a husband to divorce a wife who developed blemishes does not arise in the same way, since a husband who is dissatisfied may divorce of his own accord; the {\i mishnah} governing blemishes of the wife concerned primarily his entitlement to do so without paying the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel introduces a critical distinction. {\i Bameh devarim amurim bemumin ktanim} \u8212- the rule that we do not force a divorce applies only to small blemishes. {\i Aval bemumin gedolim kofin oto lehotzi} \u8212- for large blemishes, we do force him to give his wife a {\i get}. Tosafot notes, as a matter of course, that forcing a divorce in this context implies that the husband must also pay the {\i ketubah}; the wife is not required to tolerate these conditions, and she is entitled to both the divorce and full {\i ketubah} payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry is already apparent. When the husband is dissatisfied with his wife\u8217's blemishes, no discussion of forcing a divorce is necessary \u8212- he simply divorces her, subject only to whether he must pay the {\i ketubah}. When the wife is dissatisfied with the husband\u8217's blemishes, she cannot simply leave; the question is whether the {\i beit din} will intervene and compel a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Girsa Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a dispute over the correct text of the {\i mishnah}. Yehuda\u8217's version of the text reads {\i noldu} \u8212- the blemishes arose after the marriage. Chiyya bar Rav\u8217's version reads {\i hayu} \u8212- the blemishes existed even before the wedding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} reasons as follows. {\i Man d\u8217'amar noldu} \u8212- according to the reading that the blemishes arose after the marriage, and the {\i tanna kamma} rules we do not force a divorce, then {\i kol sheken hayu}: certainly if the blemishes existed from before the marriage, and she presumably saw them and accepted them, there would be no grounds to force a divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse also follows. {\i Man d\u8217'amar hayu} \u8212- if the {\i tanna kamma} is speaking of pre-existing blemishes and still rules we do not force a divorce, then {\i aval noldu} \u8212- when the blemishes arose after the marriage and she had no opportunity to anticipate or consent to them, perhaps in that case we {\i would} force a divorce, since she never accepted these conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon ben Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's distinction between {\i mumin ktanim} and {\i mumin gedolim} now comes into focus in light of the {\i girsa} debate. The {\i Gemara} observes that the distinction makes better sense according to the {\i noldu} reading. If the case involves blemishes that arose after the marriage and the {\i tanna kamma} says we do not force a divorce, Rabban Gamliel can logically respond: for small blemishes I agree, but for large blemishes we will force a divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If instead the case is pre-existing blemishes and the {\i tanna kamma} says she implicitly accepted them by going through with the marriage, it becomes harder to understand why large blemishes would be treated differently. The {\i Gemara} answers on behalf of the {\i hayu} reading: {\i savrah hi shehi yacholah lekabel ve\u8217'achshav einah yacholah lekabel} \u8212- even if she thought she could tolerate it at the time, perhaps she was swept up in the romance and persuaded herself it would be manageable. It now turns out that she genuinely cannot bear it. The fact that she said she was willing earlier cannot be held against her when the condition is one that is objectively intolerable. This is the logic attributed to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's position: for genuinely large blemishes, her original acceptance does not bind her permanently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The examples the {\i Gemara} gives of {\i mumin gedolim} according to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel include blindness in one eye, amputation of a hand, or loss of a foot \u8212- severe disabilities. In those times, before modern prosthetics and medical intervention, these were of course particularly serious conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba bar Yaakov cites Rabbi Yochanan: {\i halacha keRabban Shimon ben Gamliel} \u8212- the law follows Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel. This would mean that even for blemishes that arose after the marriage, if they are sufficiently serious, we force a divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this, Rava citing Rav Nachman rules {\i halacha k\u8217'divrei Chachamim} \u8212- the law follows the sages who do not force a divorce, even presumably for new and large blemishes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a difficulty: Rabbi Yochanan had himself endorsed the general principle \u8212- cited by Rabbah bar bar Chanah \u8212- that wherever Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel appears in our {\i Mishnah}, the {\i halacha} follows him, with only three exceptions ({\i areiv}, {\i Tzidon}, and {\i ra\u8217'ayah acharonah}). How then could Rabbi Yochanan rule against Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel here? The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i amora\u8217'ei ninhu ve\u8217'aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- there is a dispute among {\i Amora\u8217'im} as to what Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view actually was, and the authority who ruled {\i halacha k\u8217'divrei Chachamim} represents one valid position attributed to Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The combined effect of the strict readings of the {\i Gemara}, if one follows Rav Nachman, is striking: even a new blemish, even a large one, will not suffice to force a divorce against the husband\u8217's will. The asymmetry between husband and wife \u8212- already noted in connection with pre-existing blemishes and the binding force of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- is here magnified. If the wife has pre-existing blemishes, in certain circumstances the {\i kiddushin} itself may be void as a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut}. If the husband has pre-existing blemishes, we have noted that the {\i Gemara}\u8217's assumption is that she is more tolerant and the {\i kiddushin} remains binding. And even for new blemishes of the husband, the law may decline to force a divorce at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intolerable Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disgusting Professions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i mishnah} opens: {\i ve\u8217'eilu shekofin oto lehotzi} \u8212- these are cases in which, notwithstanding the general principle that we do not force a husband to divorce, we nonetheless compel a {\i get}. The list includes {\i mukei shechin} \u8212- one afflicted with boils or serious skin disease; {\i ba\u8217'al polipos} \u8212- a condition the {\i Gemara} will define shortly; {\i hamikametz} \u8212- a gatherer, which the {\i Gemara} identifies as one who collects dog excrement for industrial use; {\i hametzaref nechoshet} \u8212- a copper smelter; and {\i habursi} \u8212- a tanner. The {\i mishnah} specifies explicitly that these cases apply {\i bein shehayu ad shelo nis\u8217'u u\u8217'vein mishenis\u8217'u noladu} \u8212- whether these conditions or professions existed before the marriage or arose afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The common thread among these cases is not disability or impairment but rather noxious odor and conditions that are profoundly repellent. The contrast with the {\i mumin gedolim} of the previous {\i mishnah} is notable: losing a limb or going blind \u8212- severe by any measure \u8212- does not suffice to force a divorce under the majority view, yet persistent foul odors and revolting work conditions do. The category of the intolerable, apparently, is not defined by objective severity of physical impairment alone, but by what renders cohabitation genuinely unendurable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding all of these cases, Rabbi Meir adds: {\i af al pi shehitnah immah} \u8212- even if the husband explicitly made a condition with his wife before the marriage and she agreed, nevertheless {\i yacholah hi shetomar savrah hayiti she\u8217'ani yacholah lekabel ve\u8217'achshav eini yacholah lekabel} \u8212- she may say: I thought I could manage, and now I find I cannot. Even an explicit pre-marital agreement accepting these conditions cannot bind her permanently when the conditions are of this category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} qualify this: if she made an explicit agreement, generally she is bound by it and cannot retract \u8212- {\i except} in the case of {\i mukei shechin}, the one afflicted with boils, which we will return to below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Safeguard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an obvious concern: if the rule is that even an explicit pre-nuptial agreement can be set aside, and the consequence of forcing a divorce is that the husband must pay the {\i ketubah}, then every opportunistic woman could agree to marry a tanner or gatherer, execute the marriage, and then declare she cannot tolerate the conditions \u8212- effectively engineering a {\i ketubah} payment. The {\i Gemara} acknowledges this worry implicitly. Tosafot suggests that in a case where the {\i beit din} perceives fraudulent scheming, it may still force the divorce but decline to award the {\i ketubah}. The assessment is ultimately left to the judges, who must evaluate whether the woman\u8217's claim of intolerance is genuine or manufactured.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Polipus Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai ba\u8217'al polippus} \u8212- what exactly is this condition? Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel says {\i rei\u8217'ach hachotem} \u8212- a malodorous discharge from the nose, some kind of persistent nasal odor. A {\i baraita} however teaches {\i rei\u8217'ach hapeh} \u8212- bad breath. Rav Assi teaches the reverse attribution: he has Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel as the authority for bad breath, and the {\i baraita} as the source for the nasal condition. He offers a mnemonic to remember: Shmuel\u8217's name is associated with the mouth, so Shmuel is the one who says it is bad breath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This classification prompts a broader observation. The {\i mishnah} listed among the {\i mumin gedolim} that do not suffice to force a divorce: blindness in an eye, loss of a hand, loss of a foot. These are grievous physical injuries. Yet now we see that persistent bad breath or chronic nasal discharge is classified in a {\i more severe} category \u8212- one that compels a divorce even over explicit prior agreement. The criterion, evidently, is not the objective gravity of the physical condition but the subjective unendurability of living with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also records a case from Tzidon ({\i ma\u8217'aseh beTzidon}) involving a tanner who died, leaving his brother \u8212- also a tanner \u8212- as the potential {\i yavam} for his widow. The {\i Chachamim} ruled that the widow may say: {\i le\u8217'achicha hayiti yacholah lekabel, ulecha eini yacholah lekabel} \u8212- I could tolerate your brother because I genuinely loved him, but I cannot tolerate you. The practical effect is that the rabbis will strongly push the {\i yavam} to perform {\i chalitzah} rather than {\i yibbum}, since the widow has grounds to refuse the levirate union with a man in this profession with whom she had no prior bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Profession Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to define each profession. {\i Mah mikameitz} \u8212- what does the {\i mikametz} gather? Rav Yehuda says: {\i zeh mikabetz tzo\u8217'at kelavim} \u8212- this is one who collects dog excrement. Rashi explains that dog excrement was needed as a preparation material, applied to hides before washing in connection with the laundering or tanning process. Tosafot adds that the connection to tanning is direct, since dog excrement was used in the tanning process itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises an objection: if a {\i mikametz} is essentially a {\i bursi} \u8212- a tanner \u8212- then why does the {\i mishnah} list them separately? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i kan beborsi raba, kan beborsi zuta} \u8212- one refers to a large-scale tanner and the other to a small-scale one, with Tosafot noting they may differ in the intensity of the smell or the nature of the work. The {\i Gemara} then notes that {\i tannai hi} \u8212- this is in fact a dispute among {\i Tanna\u8217'im}: one view identifies the {\i mikametz} with the tanner, while another says the {\i mikametz} is specifically one who collects dog excrement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i metzaref nechoshet} \u8212- the copper smelter \u8212- two opinions are offered. Rav Ashi says it refers to one who beats out copper vessels, working with molten copper to shape pots and implements. Rabbah bar bar Chanah says it is one who extracts copper from its original ore \u8212- a {\i braita} supports this latter position. Both involve extremely noxious conditions; as one might note, processing metal sulfide ores produces hydrogen sulfide gas, the characteristic smell of rotten eggs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Food Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav introduces a further case: {\i ha\u8217'omer eini zan ve\u8217'eini mefarnes} \u8212- a husband who declares he will not support his wife financially, refusing to provide food and maintenance. The ruling is {\i yotzi veyitein ketubah} \u8212- he is forced to divorce and pay the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar presented this ruling before Shmuel. Shmuel responded with notable sharpness: {\i achisuhu se\u8217'arei le\u8217'Elazar} \u8212- feed Elazar barley, meaning this ruling deserves scorn. Shmuel\u8217's objection was: {\i ad shekofin oto lehotzi yichpuhu lazun} \u8212- rather than forcing a divorce, simply force him to fulfill his obligation to support her. Why go straight to compelled divorce?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's response invokes the principle {\i ein adam dar im nachash bekififa} \u8212- a person cannot live together with a snake in the same basket. The force of this is that a husband who must be compelled by the {\i beit din} to feed his wife has revealed a depth of hostility that makes the marriage genuinely unlivable. The existence of a theoretical remedy \u8212- forcing him to pay the food bill \u8212- does not cure the underlying marital breakdown. Indeed, requiring a court order to fulfill the most basic of marital obligations is itself a mark of an intolerable situation, and a {\i get} is the appropriate remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is instructive in illuminating the concept of intolerability. Noxious professions are intolerable; so is a marriage in which the husband\u8217's obligations can only be extracted through coercion. These are grouped together as conditions that warrant compelled divorce, in contrast to physical disabilities and impairments, however severe, which \u8212- under the majority view \u8212- do not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forced Divorce Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi introduces a general limiting principle: {\i ein masin ela psulot} \u8212- the mechanism of forced divorce applies only in cases of marriages that are {\i psulot}, meaning prohibited unions. His paradigm cases are a {\i kohen} married to a {\i grusha}, a {\i grusha} or {\i chalutzah} married to a {\i kohen hedyot}, a {\i mamzeret} or {\i netinah} married to a {\i Yisrael}, or a {\i bat Yisrael} married to a {\i mamzer} or {\i netin}. In these situations, an active biblical violation is ongoing, and the {\i beit din} has grounds to intervene.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Childlessness Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does this principle come to exclude? The {\i Gemara} indicates that Rav Assi\u8217's limitation is directed at the case of {\i nasa ishah ve-sha\u8217'ah imah eser shanim velo yaldah} \u8212- a man who has been married to a woman for ten years without children. Even though there is a {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, the {\i Gemara} rules \u8212- according to Rav Assi \u8212- {\i ein kofin oto}, we do not force a divorce. This is because the situation differs fundamentally from the cases of prohibited marriages or intolerable conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In all the cases discussed until now, at least one party is unhappy. The wife wants to leave the smelly tanner, or the husband wishes to divorce the wife with blemishes. The question was whether the {\i beit din} would intervene to compel the outcome one party sought. Here, however, the {\i Gemara} imagines a couple who are both entirely content with their marriage. They have not been able to have children \u8212- perhaps through no fault of either party \u8212- but they have no desire to separate. The {\i beit din} would be coming in from the outside to dissolve a happy marriage. That is a very different kind of intervention, and one that Rav Assi limits to cases of active biblical prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Words vs Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman offers a different analysis that attempts to reconcile the competing positions: {\i ha bedivari, ha beshoti} \u8212- one category of intervention uses words and persuasion; the other uses physical compulsion. Rav Nachman suggests that in the case of childlessness, one might indeed intervene, but only through persuasion and moral pressure, not through physical force. Since the {\i mishnah} is cataloguing cases of genuine compulsion ({\i kofin}), the childlessness case simply does not belong in that list \u8212- even according to those who hold that some intervention is warranted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this resolution by citing a verse from {\i Mishlei}: {\i bedevarim lo yivaser eved} \u8212- a stubborn servant will not be disciplined through words. The implication is that if the couple is resistant to persuasion, words are ultimately futile. Therefore, if one believes the childlessness case warrants {\i kefiya} \u8212- compulsion \u8212- it would logically have to be the same physical force used in other cases. The distinction between words and force collapses for an obstinate party. So we are back to the question: if one rules {\i kofin} in the childlessness case, why is it absent from our {\i mishnah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Happy Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves this by identifying the essential conceptual distinction. In all the cases in the {\i mishnah}, the issue is whether one party can be compelled against resistance \u8212- the husband does not want to divorce, but the wife insists, or vice versa. The {\i mishnah} governs forced divorce when one party is unhappy. {\i Hatam, ki amrah ha zina, u\u8217'v\u8217'hadei shafkinan} \u8212- if in those cases the wife says she is content to remain married, we allow it. The {\i mishnah} is addressing the unhappy party\u8217's claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The childlessness case is structurally different. {\i Af al gav de\u8217'amrah ha zina u\u8217'v\u8217'hadei shafkinan, lo shafkinan lah} \u8212- even if both parties declare they are happy to remain together, we do not allow it, because we are forcing ourselves into a genuinely happy marriage on grounds of a {\i mitzvah} they are not fulfilling. That is why it is in a different category and not listed in the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises an apparent counter-example: what about {\i mukei shechin} \u8212- the man with boils? There too, even if the wife says she is willing to remain married, we override her consent for his benefit, since sexual relations aggravate his condition. Does this not also involve disrupting a nominally consensual marriage? The {\i Gemara} distinguishes: in the {\i mukei shechin} case, if she is willing to maintain the marriage but the couple agrees to abstain from intimacy, and she can arrange to have witnesses ensure the separation of bedrooms, {\i shafkinan lah} \u8212- we allow the marriage to continue, because the concern was specifically about the harm caused by sexual activity. Remove the sex, and there is no obstacle to the marriage continuing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the childlessness case, by contrast, even separate bedrooms are no solution. {\i Lo shafkinan lah} \u8212- we do not allow the marriage to continue under any arrangement, because the problem cannot be sidestepped. The entire marriage must be dissolved if one insists on intervention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of the discussion is that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's catalogue of forced divorces all involve a complaining party, and the {\i beit din}\u8217's role is to adjudicate that claim. Forced dissolution of a happy marriage is a categorically different and more radical intervention \u8212- reserved for cases of active biblical prohibition, and contested even there in the context of non-fulfillment of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. Whether physical force is warranted in such cases, and whether the {\i mishnah}\u8217's silence on the childlessness case is because no intervention is warranted or merely because its character differs, is the question left unresolved by the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Practical Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rama Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rama, in {\i Even HaEzer} 154, rules: {\i d\u8217'bazman hazeh eino minhag lichof klal} \u8212- in our times, it is not the practice to ever force a divorce. This sweeping statement raises the question of its scope. Does it apply only to a case like childlessness \u8212- where the {\i Gemara} itself was uncertain about the propriety of intervening in what may be a happy marriage \u8212- or does it extend even to cases of active prohibited unions, such as a {\i kohen} married to a {\i grusha}? The Rama\u8217's language is broad: {\i lichof klal}, not to force at all. In an era when Jewish communities retained a degree of juridical autonomy and the physical means to enforce their rulings, this was a deliberate policy choice \u8212- not an admission of inability, but a principled withdrawal from coercive marital intervention, particularly where the marriage itself is happy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Pre-marital\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to the {\i Rambam}, chapter 25 of {\i Hilchot Ishut}: {\i hamotzi isha stam}, a man who marries a woman without explicit stipulation, and {\i nimtzu bah mumin}, blemishes are found on her \u8212- {\i tetze belo ketubah}, she leaves without a {\i ketubah}. The {\i Rambam} applies this even in the absence of an explicit condition, provided the husband neither knew of the blemishes nor had reasonable grounds to discover them. The {\i Rambam} then elaborates on the distinction between {\i mum shebegaluy} and {\i mum shebeseser}, and the bathhouse principle. He even addresses an interesting nuance: what if the husband has no relatives in the city to send to the bathhouse, but has friends? Can he send friends to investigate? The {\i Rambam} seems to treat this as a factor relevant to what counts as reasonable available knowledge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot: even without any stipulation at {\i kiddushin}, if the wife had pre-existing blemishes that the husband could not have known about, he is entitled to divorce her without paying a {\i ketubah}. His claim is essentially that the marriage was entered under false pretenses \u8212- a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- even if the {\i kiddushin} itself remains formally binding. The absence of a stipulation does not obligate him to pay when the foundational premise of the marriage was violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Post-marital\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} draws a clear line at timing. If the blemishes arose {\i after} the marriage \u8212- {\i achar shenasa} \u8212- then these are circumstances neither party could have anticipated, and the husband cannot invoke them to avoid {\i ketubah} payment. If he wants to divorce her, he may, but he must pay the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the husband\u8217's side, the {\i Rambam} rules: {\i ha-ish she-noldu bo mumin achar she-nasa} \u8212- a man who develops blemishes after the marriage, {\i afilu niktea yado o nismis eino}, even if his hand is amputated or his eye goes blind, {\i ein kofin oto lehotzi veleiten ketubah} \u8212- we do not force him to divorce and pay the {\i ketubah}. If the wife absolutely refuses to remain in the marriage, she may obtain a {\i get}, but the {\i Rambam} rules she leaves {\i belo ketubah kedin kol hamordot} \u8212- without a {\i ketubah}, as the law of a {\i moredet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this is truly asymmetrical or simply parallel: if she develops blemishes after marriage, he must pay the {\i ketubah} if he divorces her; if he develops blemishes, she is not forced to stay but she loses the {\i ketubah} if she leaves. The structural parallel holds in one respect \u8212- neither is blamed for post-marital conditions. But the asymmetry surfaces clearly: he retains the effective power of decision. If she develops blemishes after the marriage, it is his choice whether to stay or divorce (paying the {\i ketubah}). If he develops blemishes, the {\i Rambam} says she can insist on divorce, and {\i kofin} may apply if she genuinely insists \u8212- but she will not recover the {\i ketubah}. He cannot be blamed, but the consequences fall on her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} continues: {\i nasa ishah ve-sha\u8217'ah imah eser shanim velo yaldah kofin oto lehotzi veyiten ketubah} \u8212- if a man was married for ten years without children, we do force him to divorce and pay the {\i ketubah}. The {\i Rambam} rules that intervention is warranted in this case, even though the couple may be happy \u8212- a position that the {\i Gemara} left contested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Asymmetry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry emerging from all of this is substantial. Where the wife has pre-existing blemishes, the husband may divorce without {\i ketubah}, and in certain readings the {\i kiddushin} itself may be voided as a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut}. Where the husband has pre-existing blemishes, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's assumption is that the wife accepted them or could tolerate them; the {\i kiddushin} is binding and \u8212- according to the majority view \u8212- we will not force a divorce even if the problems are large. Where blemishes develop after the marriage, the symmetry is closer on the surface \u8212- neither party is blamed \u8212- but in practice the husband retains greater latitude: he may divorce at will (paying the {\i ketubah}), whereas she, if she wants out, may be treated as a {\i moredet} and forfeit the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} is candid that this asymmetry rests on a gendered presumption about tolerance \u8212- the assumption that women are more willing to tolerate an imperfect husband than men are to tolerate an imperfect wife. The {\i Gemara} itself registers the difficulty this creates for {\i get} and {\i agunah} situations where the husband has severe problems, yet the legal structure proceeds on this presumptive base.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ba\u8217'alei Raatan Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raatan Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan records a tradition he received from a man described as a coal-man ({\i pecham}) from the people of Jerusalem, relating to twenty-four types of skin disease \u8212- a list directly relevant to the coming Torah portion of {\i Metzora}. The {\i Gemara} states that the {\i Chachamim} ruled regarding all twenty-four that {\i tashmish kasheh lahem} \u8212- sexual relations aggravate the condition. The worst of all is the condition known as {\i ra\u8217'atan}, which the {\i Gemara} describes as involving a worm or bug that enters the skull and causes a particularly severe form of skin or neurological disease.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raatan Treatment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses the etiology and treatment of {\i ra\u8217'atan}. A {\i baraita} teaches that if a person with this condition ({\i ba\u8217'al ra\u8217'atan}) had blood let and then engaged in sexual relations, the children would be {\i vitikin} \u8212- weak. If both husband and wife had blood let and then had relations, the children would be {\i ba\u8217'alei ra\u8217'atan} \u8212- afflicted with this same condition. Rav Papa qualifies: this applies only if neither party ate anything after the bloodletting. {\i Aval ta\u8217'im midi leit lan bah} \u8212- if they ate something afterward, restoring their strength, we have no such concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The signs of {\i ra\u8217'atan} are described: {\i dalfan eineih vedayivin nechireih} \u8212- the eyes run with tears, the nostrils discharge mucus, and there is saliva dripping from the mouth, with flies hovering around the person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the cure, Abaye describes an elaborate procedure. A preparation is made from various plant materials and substances \u8212- species of bark, nutshell shavings, and, notably, the covering of a red unripe date ({\i mithla dedikla sumka}) \u8212- all boiled together. The patient is brought into a house built of marble, or if unavailable, a house with walls seven and a half bricks thick, providing protection from the elements and creating something approaching a sterile surgical environment. Three hundred cups of the boiled liquid are poured over his head, {\i ad dlayfei al ishta demokhayei} \u8212- until the floor of his skull is softened. The skull is then cut open ({\i ukaru lemokhayei}), and the worm in the brain is found to have four legs. The procedure requires placing a myrtle leaf under each leg before attempting to remove it \u8212- if the worm is pulled without the leaf in place, it grips the skull. Finally, the worm is removed with tweezers and burned in fire. {\i Ve\u8217'i la hadar amran} \u8212- if the worm is not burned, it will re-enter the man\u8217's skull.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contagion Care\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan announced a warning: {\i hizaharu mizvuvei shel ba\u8217'alei ra\u8217'atan} \u8212- beware the flies of those with {\i ra\u8217'atan}, since the flies may carry and transmit the disease. The awareness of contagion is reflected in the precautionary behavior of several sages. Rabbi Zeira would not sit in the same area of airflow as a person with {\i ra\u8217'atan}, understanding that the disease could be carried by wind. Rabbi Elazar would not enter the same tent. Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Assi would not eat even eggs from the same courtyard where a {\i ba\u8217'al ra\u8217'atan} lived \u8212- an indication of concern about indirect contamination extending through the food supply of a shared space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking cluster of precautions. The Babylonian Talmud here displays explicit awareness of contagious disease transmission \u8212- by air, by proximity, and apparently by indirect contact \u8212- that modern readers might not expect to find articulated in so concrete a form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, by contrast, took no such precautions. He would wrap himself among the {\i ba\u8217'alei ra\u8217'atan} and study Torah in their company, invoking the verse from {\i Mishlei} \u8212- {\i ayelet ahavim ve\u8217'ya\u8217'alat khen}, the doe of love and graceful mountain goat \u8212- to argue that Torah brings charm and protection upon those who learn it, shielding him from any harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that this behavior invites comparison with certain figures in the New Testament accounts who would go among the sick and diseased while the Pharisees maintained their distance. Here it is Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi who breaches that distance \u8212- but explicitly through the protective power of Torah study rather than ritual impurity considerations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi was dying, heaven instructed the angel of death: {\i zil avid lei re\u8217'uteih} \u8212- go fulfill his wishes. The angel appeared to him, and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi\u8217's first request was: show me my place in {\i Olam HaBa}. The angel agreed. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi then asked for the angel\u8217's knife, saying he was afraid of being frightened by it along the way. The angel handed it over.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When they arrived at the entrance to {\i Olam HaBa} and the angel pointed to Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi\u8217's place, Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi leaped over the fence and landed on the other side \u8212- alive \u8212- in {\i Olam HaBa}, seeking to enter without undergoing the experience of death. The angel grabbed him by the edge of his cloak. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi declared under oath that he would not return. God then ruled: if he ever had an oath annulled during his lifetime, we will annul this oath and send him back; if he never annulled an oath, his oath will stand and he will remain. Since he had never annulled an oath, he was allowed to stay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the angel of death demanded his knife back. {\i De-mit-bei le-biryata} \u8212- other creatures need to die, and without the knife, death cannot proceed. A {\i bat kol} declared: return the knife. He did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Elijah then announced in {\i Olam HaBa}: {\i panu makom le-bar Liva\u8217'i} \u8212- make way for the son of Levi. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi found Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai seated on thirteen golden stools. Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai asked him: did you ever see a rainbow during your lifetime? Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi answered: yes. Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai said: if so, you are not truly Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, and you do not deserve the honor being accorded to you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The explanation: a rainbow is the sign of the covenant that God will not destroy the world. If a truly righteous person exists in a generation, the merit of that {\i tzaddik} is itself sufficient to protect the world, rendering the rainbow unnecessary. Furthermore, as the Maharsha notes, a {\i tzaddik} who never violated an oath provides the guarantee of God\u8217's own covenant-oath not to destroy the world, making the rainbow redundant. If Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi saw a rainbow, it implies his generation lacked that perfect protection \u8212- which would suggest he was not at that highest level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then comments: {\i ve-la hi} \u8212- that was not actually the case. There was never a rainbow in Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi\u8217's lifetime. He really was that righteous, and his generation really was protected by his merit. He simply did not want to credit himself with that stature and so acknowledged having seen a rainbow rather than proclaiming his own spiritual greatness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Chanina Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa had been a {\i shushvin} \u8212- a close companion \u8212- either of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi or of the angel of death (the {\i Gemara}\u8217's phrasing is ambiguous). When Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa was dying, heaven sent the same message: go fulfill his wishes. The angel appeared to him. His first request was: give me thirty days to review my learning, for it is said: {\i ashrei mi she-ba le-chan ve-talmudo be-yado} \u8212- praised is the one who arrives in {\i Olam HaBa} with his learning in hand. He was granted the thirty days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After thirty days, the angel returned and showed him his place. Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa likewise asked for the knife. But the angel declined: {\i ke-chaverach de-chamsht le-avad Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} \u8212- you want to do to me what your companion Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi did? The angel then challenged him: bring the {\i sefer Torah} and show me if there is any commandment written in it that you did not fulfill \u8212- if so, you are as worthy as Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, and I should give you the knife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa could not make that claim. The angel asked specifically: {\i mi adbakt be-ba\u8217'alei ra\u8217'atan} \u8212- did you involve yourself with those afflicted with {\i ra\u8217'atan}, studying Torah in their midst? He had not. That was the distinction. One is moved to think of healthcare workers and others who place themselves at genuine personal risk in caring for the vulnerable; the {\i Gemara} seems to acknowledge that this kind of selfless exposure represents a particular level of {\i madrega} that not every {\i tzaddik} attains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, when Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa died, {\i ifsik lei amuda de-nura bein didey l\u8217'alma} \u8212- a pillar of fire separated him from the rest of the world. No one could come close to his coffin. The tradition is that such a pillar of fire accompanies only a single individual in a generation, or at most two. He was, in his own right, of that singular stature \u8212- even if he had not reached the precise level of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To address the practical problem of burial, a voice called out: lower the pillar of fire for the sake of the honor of the sages; for the honor of your father; for your own honor, so that you can be buried. {\i Mi-yad istalek} \u8212- immediately, the fire retreated, and they were able to bury him. But it had made its point about who he was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye comments on the significance of the pillar of fire: {\i l\u8217'afukei mi-man d\u8217'lo kiyem afilu os achas} \u8212- it serves to exclude from his space anyone who has not fulfilled even one letter of the Torah, who is not at the same {\i madrega} as Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi. Rav Adda bar Matna responds pointedly to Abaye: {\i l\u8217'afukei mi-mar} \u8212- like yourself, since you lack a {\i ma\u8217'akeh} on your roof! You don\u8217't fulfill every obligation, so you wouldn\u8217't be admitted into that space. The {\i Gemara} adds that Abaye actually did have a {\i ma\u8217'akeh}, but at that particular moment the wind had blown it down and he had not yet gotten around to repairing it \u8212- a very human detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Babylon Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina asks: {\i lama ein ba\u8217'alei ra\u8217'atan b\u8217'Bavel} \u8212- why are there no {\i ba\u8217'alei ra\u8217'atan} in Babylon? Because the Babylonians eat {\i silki} \u8212- beets \u8212- and drink {\i shichra d\u8217'hizmei}, a mead made from a particular shrub. These dietary habits apparently prevent the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan adds the parallel question: {\i lama ein metzoraim b\u8217'Bavel} \u8212- why are there no people with the skin disease {\i tzara\u8217'at} in Babylon? Because they eat beets, drink beer, and bathe in the waters of the Euphrates. Three protective practices \u8212- diet, drink, and bathing in a specific river \u8212- are credited with keeping this biblical affliction at bay in Babylonia.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach Hamadir es Ishto} \u8212- we have completed the chapter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 78\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro: Fruits Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous Perakim Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 78 opens the eighth {\i perek} of {\i Masechet Ketubot}, {\i Ha\u8217'ishah Shenafelu} \u8212- \u8220"the woman to whom property fell.\u8221" The previous {\i perakim} were largely devoted to the {\i tenai ketubah}, the reciprocal obligations of husband and wife in what one might call lived marriage: what each party\u8217's responsibilities are, what happens when those responsibilities are not met, how to handle tensions created by vows or unexpected personal deficiencies, whether a {\i get} is to be forced and whether it comes with or without a {\i ketubah}. These were real, practical questions about the nature of the marital relationship. Now the focus shifts to the financial dimension \u8212- specifically, the husband\u8217's entitlement to the {\i perot}, the income or fruits, of his wife\u8217's property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nichsei Milog & Perot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the {\i halachot} of the {\i ketubah}, one of the {\i tenai ketubah}, is that the husband {\i ochel perot} \u8212- he eats the fruit of his wife\u8217's property. The property under discussion here is {\i nichsei milog}: property that she owns and holds title to, which is not transferred to the husband at all and need not be listed in the {\i ketubah}, but whose income belongs to him for the duration of the marriage. This stands in contrast to the dowry \u8212- the {\i nidunyah} \u8212- which is also called {\i nichsei tzon barzel}, property whose title does actually transfer to the husband, with a fixed value written into the {\i ketubah} to be returned to her upon divorce or his death. {\i Nichsei milog}, by contrast, she never loses title to. He simply receives all the income it generates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In practical terms, this could be entirely literal: she owns fields, and the yield of those fields goes to him. Or she owns rental properties, and the rent flows to him. One might imagine an investment account where he receives the dividends. The husband bears the expenses and receives the net income. This arrangement is what the {\i Mishnah} calls {\i baal ochel perot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzoan Barzel vs Milog\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical distinction, then, is as follows. {\i Nichsei tzon barzel} \u8212- the dowry \u8212- is property whose value is written in the {\i ketubah} and the title passes to the husband; he is responsible for returning its value to her upon dissolution of the marriage. {\i Nichsei milog}, by contrast, is any property in the wife\u8217's name that she retains title to: he never formally acquires the property itself, only the right to its fruits. It is the {\i nichsei milog} arrangement that gives rise to the central question of our {\i Mishnah}: since she retains title, can she go ahead and sell that property to someone else?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Property Before Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ad Shelo Titaras Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} opens with the case of {\i ha\u8217'ishah shenafelu lah nechasim} \u8212- a woman to whom property fell, meaning she inherited property. The question is: when did she inherit it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ad shelo titaras} \u8212- before even the {\i erusin}, before there was a husband anywhere on the scene. In this case, {\i modim Beit Shammai u\u8217'Veit Hillel} \u8212- both schools agree \u8212- {\i shemocheret venotenet vekayam}: she may sell or give the property away {\i lechatchila}, and the transaction is fully valid. This is unremarkable. Presumably the sale remains valid even after {\i erusin} subsequently takes place, because all of these {\i halachot} really kick in only at the time of {\i nissuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Erusin Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then turns to a more complex case: the property fell to her after the {\i kiddushin} but before {\i nissuin}. Here the husband does not yet have legal rights to the property, but he is now on the scene. One can look into the proverbial crystal ball: most {\i erusin} leads to {\i nissuin}, and once there is {\i nissuin}, he will have rights to the {\i perot} of this very property. Should she be permitted to sell it now and deprive him of that future income? Legally, she is entirely entitled \u8212- they are not yet {\i nesu\u8217'im} \u8212- but is it appropriate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai omerim} \u8212- Beit Shammai say she may sell {\i lechatchila}. It is her property; they are not yet married in the full sense. {\i Beit Hillel omerim lo timkor}: she should not sell it {\i lechatchila}, because it is not fitting. Once there was already {\i kiddushin}, once he had reasonable expectations about this property, it would be inappropriate to extinguish those expectations. Nonetheless, {\i elu va\u8217'elu modim she\u8217'im machra venatna kayam}: both schools agree that if she went ahead and sold it, the sale is binding, because they are not yet {\i nesu\u8217'im} and he has no legal rights to this property yet.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zacha B\u8217'Ishah Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, Rabbi Yehuda introduces a challenge that was brought before Rabban Gamliel. {\i Amru Chachamim lifnei Rabban Gamliel: ho\u8217'il v\u8217'zacha b\u8217'ishah lo yizkeh b\u8217'nechasim?} Since he has acquired \u8212- or merited, or has legal rights to \u8212- the woman, should he not also acquire her property?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument invokes concepts we have encountered in {\i Masechet Kiddushin}. The word {\i zocheh} carries the connotation of {\i kinyan}, of acquisition. The {\i Chachamim} are not disputing that the full suite of marital obligations and rights \u8212- {\i perot}, {\i maaseh yadeha}, and so forth \u8212- all belong to the framework of the lived, ongoing marriage that begins at {\i nissuin}. Their argument is different: even at the level of {\i kiddushin}, there exists a form of {\i kinyan} over the woman herself. {\i Kiddushin}, on one understanding, is a form of acquisition of the wife. If that {\i kinyan} attaches to the woman, should it not extend to her property as well, thereby preventing her from selling it even before {\i nissuin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking argument. The husband at the stage of {\i kiddushin} has no obligation to provide food, clothing, or conjugal relations; he is not yet living with her; he is not yet entitled to {\i maaseh yadeha} or {\i perot} \u8212- all of that is {\i nissuin}. What precisely does he have at {\i kiddushin}? The discussion touches on a deep point: the real substance of his {\i kinyan} through {\i kiddushin} is, as the {\i Rambam} explains, that she becomes his sexual property. That is the core of what the marital {\i kinyan} means at the {\i kiddushin} stage, even if the {\i Rash} disagrees and seeks a different framing. And, it must be noted, that {\i kinyan} is far from absolute \u8212- he obviously cannot sell her, cannot transfer her to another as a wife, and so on. But the {\i Chachamim} are arguing that even this limited {\i kinyan} should be sufficient to give him some ownership over her property and prevent her from alienating it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anu Boshim Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's response is arresting: {\i al chadashim anu boshim} \u8212- we are embarrassed about the new property. Even in the most straightforward case, where she gets property after {\i nissuin} and we say her sale is void, we are already embarrassed about that ruling. She has title! The rabbis are saying that her sale is not valid \u8212- and that is itself an encroachment on her legal rights that admits of no simple justification. {\i Ela she\u8217'atem megalgelin aleinu et hayeshanim} \u8212- and now you want to roll into this and say he has rights even over the old property, property she had from before? That is too far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This response is not purely a legal argument; it is a moral one \u8212- almost a statement of self-awareness. Rabban Gamliel acknowledges that legislation preventing the wife from selling her own property \u8212- property to which she holds title \u8212- is problematic enough when it applies to property acquired after {\i nissuin}. The idea that it would extend to property from before {\i nissuin} is simply untenable. And yet there is a legal element buried here too: it is {\i her} property. He has no title. The whole regime is questionable from the outset.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Property After Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Naflu Lah Misheniset\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} continues: {\i naflu lah misheniset} \u8212- property fell to her after {\i nissuin}, after she was already fully married. In this case, {\i elu v\u8217'elu modim} \u8212- both Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel agree \u8212- {\i she\u8217'im machra venatna shehaba\u8217'al motzi miyad halakochot}: if she sold or gave away that property, the husband can take it from the hand of the purchasers and the transaction is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Title vs Perot Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates an immediate and important question: what exactly did she try to sell? There are two possibilities, and they have very different conceptual implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first possibility is that she tried to sell the property itself \u8212- the title. This seems to be the plain reading of the {\i Mishnah}. But this is a genuine {\i chiddush}: even though the husband is only entitled to the income, not the title \u8212- she holds title \u8212- why should she be unable to transfer that title? Perhaps the purchaser acquires the property and the husband simply continues to collect the income during the marriage, with the purchaser holding the underlying ownership. If so, preventing even a title transfer is a striking rabbinic enactment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possibility is that she did not try to transfer the title at all. What she tried to sell was merely the right to the income \u8212- the {\i perot} themselves. In that case, the prohibition is more readily understandable: the husband is the one entitled to the income, and she has no right to sell what is not hers to sell.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of which scenario the {\i Mishnah} addresses will bear on how we read the {\i Gemara}\u8217's later distinction from {\i takkanat Usha}, as we shall see. Notably, one could imagine a structure where she sells title while explicitly preserving the husband\u8217's {\i perot} rights \u8212- and if even that is void, it would be a considerable {\i chiddush}, and would further illuminate Rabban Gamliel\u8217's {\i anu boshim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Old vs New Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then presents a third scenario through the lens of a second version of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's debate: {\i ad shelo nisset v\u8217'nisset} \u8212- she had the property before the {\i nissuin} and now she is {\i nesu\u8217'ah}. Rabban Gamliel says {\i im machra venatna kayam} \u8212- {\i bedieved}, if she sold it, the sale is binding. His reasoning seems to be that since she started with this property independently, before {\i nissuin}, before he had any legal rights to it at all, she retains a stronger position with respect to this property even now that they are married. The only case where the husband can definitively block the sale is when she got the property after the {\i nissuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Akavya then reports a parallel version of this debate. The {\i Chachamim} again approach Rabban Gamliel, invoking the same {\i zacha b\u8217'ishah} argument: now that they are fully married and he has all his marital rights \u8212- {\i maaseh yadeha}, {\i perot}, and so forth \u8212- shouldn\u8217't those rights give him the power to block even a sale of property she brought into the marriage? Rabban Gamliel responds with the same {\i anu boshim}: we are already embarrassed about the case of new property \u8212- property she acquired after {\i nissuin} \u8212- and you want to extend this to the old property she had before? No.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i bedieved} qualification is important. Rabban Gamliel does not say {\i lechatchila} she may sell; he only says {\i im machra kayam} \u8212- if she already sold, it stands. This is consistent with Beit Hillel\u8217's position on the earlier case: {\i lechatchila} perhaps she should not, but the transaction cannot be undone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Merit & Couple Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Merit Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to explain the disagreement between the cases. {\i Mai shana reisha delo pligi u\u8217'mai shana seifa depligi?} Why do Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel not disagree when the property came before the {\i kiddushin}, yet they do disagree when it came after the {\i kiddushin}? In both situations she is selling before {\i nissuin}; in both situations she has full legal title. What is the operative difference?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The school of Rabbi Yannai answers: {\i reisha bizchutah naflu} \u8212- in the first case, when she got the property before the {\i kiddushin}, it came entirely through her own merit. There was no husband on the scene at all. Therefore she has unimpeachable rights to it, and even after {\i kiddushin} she may sell it {\i lechatchila}. In the {\i seifa}, however, she got the property after the {\i kiddushin}, before the {\i nissuin} \u8212- {\i bizchuto naflu}: perhaps it came through his merit. And therefore, the argument goes, he has some claim over it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately objects: if it truly fell through his merit, then {\i bedieved} it should not be valid either \u8212- yet we agree it is. So the {\i Gemara} reformulates: in the first case, {\i vadai bizchutah nafal} \u8212- it certainly came through her alone. In the second case, {\i eimar bizchutah eimar bizchuto} \u8212- it may have come through her, it may have come through him. That ambiguity is enough to say {\i lechatchila} she should not sell, but since fundamentally it is her property and perhaps it came through her own merit entirely, {\i im machra venatna kayam} \u8212- {\i bedieved} the sale stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As stated, this \u8220"merit\u8221" reasoning sounds remarkably non-legal \u8212- why should we be adjudicating property rights based on a speculation about why someone received an inheritance?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Reframing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reframes the entire discussion in a way that removes the metaphysical element. When the {\i Gemara} says {\i bizchuto}, Rashi reads it not as \u8220"because of his merit she received the windfall,\u8221" but as a legal-status claim: {\i kvar zachah bah uvenichaseha} \u8212- he was already in the picture, he already had certain rights over her and over her property. {\i Zechus} for Rashi is not merit but something closer to {\i reshut} \u8212- presence, entitlement, legal standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And the {\i Gemara\u8217's} ambiguity \u8212- {\i eimar bizchutah eimar bizchuto} \u8212- Rashi explains as follows: {\i de\u8217'eirusin oseh sefek nissuin} \u8212- {\i erusin} creates a situation of potential, incipient {\i nissuin}. The {\i erusin} does not make him a full husband, but {\i nissuin} is now on the horizon. {\i Shema tavo lidei nissuin shema lo tavo} \u8212- perhaps it will lead to {\i nissuin}, perhaps not. What this means practically is that since she got the property already as his wife in some sense, in the context of their being legally a couple \u8212- and since the natural progression from {\i kiddushin} is {\i nissuin}, at which point he will have rights to the {\i perot} \u8212- he can make a claim: \u8220"You acquired this property in the context of our already being a couple; I should have some standing to object to you selling it.\u8221" That standing is not sufficient to void the sale {\i bedieved}, since fundamentally she owns it and the {\i kiddushin} does not yet confer {\i perot} rights, but it is perhaps enough for a {\i lechatchila} objection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Zechus Gray Area\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading renders the {\i erusin} period not so much a legal ambiguity about whose merit generated the inheritance, but rather a genuine gray area of couple-status. She is unquestionably her own legal entity; the property is unquestionably hers. But they are in a stage that is neither fully single nor fully married \u8212- they are already legally a couple, with {\i nissuin} anticipated. That intermediate status may give him just enough of a foothold to object {\i lechatchila}, without rising to the level of a legally cognizable veto {\i bedieved}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita vs Mishna Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a question about the nature of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's report. When Rabbi Yehuda describes the {\i Chachamim\u8217's} challenge to Rabban Gamliel \u8212- the argument that the sale should be void even at the time of {\i erusin} because {\i zacha b\u8217'ishah} \u8212- was this a {\i lechatchila} argument or a {\i bedieved} argument? Were the {\i Chachamim} saying only that she should not sell {\i lechatchila}, thereby pressing for the position of Beit Hillel? Or were they pressing the stronger claim that even {\i bedieved} the sale should be void?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a {\i braita} to resolve this: {\i detanya, amar Rabbi Yehuda lifnei Rabban Gamliel} \u8212- the {\i Chachamim} said to Rabban Gamliel, {\i ho\u8217'il v\u8217'zo ishto v\u8217'zo ishto} \u8212- since this woman is his wife as a {\i nesuah}, and this woman is his wife as an {\i arusa}, just as the sale of a {\i nesuah} is void, so too the sale of an {\i arusa} should be void. This is clearly a {\i bedieved} claim: they want the sale to be null and void even at the stage of {\i erusin}. {\i Shma mina b\u8217'diavad ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- we see from this that the {\i Chachamim} were arguing for {\i bedieved} invalidity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hard Legal Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is here that the {\i Gemara} introduces Rabbi Chanina ben Akavya\u8217's version, which preserves a much sharper, more direct legal response from Rabban Gamliel \u8212- sharper than the {\i anu boshim} response \u8212- to the claim that the sale should be void at {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo kach heshivam Rabban Gamliel lachachamim} \u8212- that is not how Rabban Gamliel responded to them on the {\i erusin} question. {\i Ela kach heshivam}: his actual response was a hard legal one. {\i Im amartem b\u8217'nesuah} \u8212- why is the sale void when she is a {\i nesuah}? Because {\i ba\u8217'alah zakai bimtzi\u8217'ata u\u8217'v\u8217'maaseh yadeha u\u8217'v\u8217'hafarat nedareha}: the husband has full legal rights over her found property, her earnings, and her vows. And of course the most relevant right is his entitlement to the {\i perot} of her property. When she is a {\i nesuah}, all of those rights are in place and so the legal basis for voiding her sale is present. {\i Tomru b\u8217'arusa} \u8212- but would you say the same about an {\i arusa}? {\i She\u8217'ein ba\u8217'alah zakai} \u8212- the husband has none of those rights at {\i erusin}. Not to her {\i metziah}, not to her {\i maaseh yadeha}, not to independently annul her vows. Everything begins at {\i nissuin}. There is therefore a clear, hard legal answer to the {\i erusin} case that requires no embarrassment and no emotional register: before {\i nissuin}, he simply has no rights, and the sale is therefore fully binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i anu boshim} response, by contrast, is what Rabban Gamliel gave only when the argument was much stronger \u8212- when the {\i Chachamim} pressed him about old property sold after {\i nissuin}. There, the emotional and somewhat non-responsive nature of the answer is precisely because the {\i Chachamim} had a genuine point: right now she is a {\i nesuah}, he has all his rights, what difference does it make whether the property is old or new?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Old Property After Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And that is exactly the next step in the {\i braita}. The {\i Chachamim} concede the {\i erusin} point \u8212- yes, before {\i nissuin} he has no rights, the sale stands \u8212- but they press the case: {\i machra ad shelo niset} and then {\i niset v\u8217'achar kach machra} \u8212- what if she came into the {\i nissuin} with the property already in her name, and then sold it? He is now a full husband with all his legal rights. What difference should it make that she held the property from before?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel responds: {\i af zo mocheret venotenet vekayam} \u8212- even this she can sell and it is valid. And then the {\i Chachamim} press harder: {\i ha lazacha be\u8217'ishah lo yizke b\u8217'nechasim} \u8212- now that he has full {\i nissuin} rights, shouldn\u8217't he have rights over the property? This is where Rabban Gamliel finally gives the {\i anu boshim} answer: we are embarrassed about the new property, property she acquired after {\i nissuin} \u8212- and you want to extend this to property she brought into the marriage? The emotional quality of the answer reflects the genuine difficulty. She has title. The argument that he can stop her sale is hard enough to justify in any case. It is not going to be extended to old property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this striking is that the {\i Chachamim\u8217's} argument in this final iteration is actually quite strong. She is a {\i nesuah} now. He has all his rights now. What is legally different about when she got the property? Rabban Gamliel\u8217's answer amounts to: you are already pushing us to a rule we find uncomfortable; do not push further. It is an acknowledgment, as one might read it, that the whole regime of voiding the wife\u8217's sale rests not on a straightforwardly derived legal right but on some form of enacted policy \u8212- and that the moral weight of that should check how far the rabbis are willing to extend it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechatchila vs Bedieved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a discrepancy between the two tannaitic reports. In the {\i Mishnah} as Rabbi Yehuda presents it, Rabban Gamliel only permits the sale of old property {\i bedieved} \u8212- {\i im machra venatna kayam}. In the {\i braita} as Rabbi Chanina ben Akavya reports it, Rabban Gamliel permits the sale {\i lechatchila} \u8212- {\i mocheret venotenet vekayam} \u8212- even more lenient than Beit Hillel, who would not allow {\i lechatchila} even at {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zevid suggests simply emending the {\i Mishnah} to accord with the {\i braita}, so that Rabban Gamliel is read as permitting it {\i lechatchila} throughout. Rav Pappa offers a different resolution: there is no contradiction \u8212- this is simply a case of two different tannaim reporting the same discussion differently. {\i Ha Rabbi Yehuda aliba deRabban Gamliel, ha Rabbi Chanina ben Akavya aliba deRabban Gamliel}: Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's version has Rabban Gamliel permitting only {\i bedieved}; Rabbi Chanina ben Akavya\u8217's version has Rabban Gamliel permitting even {\i lechatchila}, and indeed according to that version Rabban Gamliel would hold that Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel do not even debate this question, since everyone would agree the sale is permitted {\i lechatchila} when property is brought from before the {\i nissuin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav & Shmuel Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav & Shmuel Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After this tannaitic discussion, the {\i Gemara} introduces a bold ruling by Rav and Shmuel. {\i Rav and Shmuel d\u8217'amri tarvaihu}: whether the property fell to her before {\i erusin}, before there was any husband on the scene \u8212- even if she acquired the property as a small child \u8212- or whether the property fell to her after {\i kiddushin} and before {\i nissuin}, once there is {\i nissuin}, {\i haba\u8217'al motzi miyad halakochot}: the husband can take it from the hand of the purchasers. In the last case of our {\i Mishnah}, they side with the {\i Chachamim} against Rabban Gamliel: once there is {\i nissuin}, it does not matter when the property was acquired. Any sale she attempts is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means that what Rav and Shmuel hold does not follow Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's version \u8212- who has Rabban Gamliel permitting the sale {\i bedieved} \u8212- nor does it follow Rabbi Chanina ben Akavya\u8217's version, which has Rabban Gamliel permitting it {\i lechatchila}. In both tannaitic accounts, Rabban Gamliel ultimately validates the sale of old property. {\i K\u8217'man} \u8212- who are Rav and Shmuel following?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabanan\u8217's Vote & Usha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i innu d\u8217'amri k\u8217'rabboseinu} \u8212- they are following our masters, a body distinct from the {\i Chachamim} who challenged Rabban Gamliel in our {\i Mishnah}. The {\i Tosefta} reports: {\i rabboseinu chazru v\u8217'nimnu} \u8212- our masters reconvened and voted. {\i Bein shenafloo nechasim ad shelo titaros u\u8217'vein shenafloo mishetitaros v\u8217'niset} \u8212- whether the property fell before {\i erusin} or whether it fell after {\i erusin} and she is now {\i nesu\u8217'ah}, {\i haba\u8217'al motzi miyad halakochot}. Once there is {\i nissuin}, the husband may void any sale, regardless of when the property was acquired.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable sociological moment in the {\i Gemara}. Rabban Gamliel makes his {\i anu boshim} argument and, by the force of moral suasion, silences the {\i Chachamim} in the moment. But subsequently, after that encounter, the rabbis reconvene, take stock, and arrive at a different ruling. The dynamic suggests that the policy rationale \u8212- giving the husband secure financial footing so that the {\i perot} arrangement functions properly \u8212- ultimately outweighed Rabban Gamliel\u8217's moral hesitation. The {\i perot} rights he has are real. If she can sell property out from under him at will, even property she owned long before the marriage, those rights become hollow. So they enacted \u8212- and the language {\i nimnu} suggests an enactment rather than a purely derived rule \u8212- that {\i nissuin} is the operative dividing line, and any sale after {\i nissuin} is subject to being voided.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takkanat Usha Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Usha Takkanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a question about our {\i Mishnah\u8217's} ruling \u8212- specifically, the case where she gets property after {\i nissuin} and both schools agree the husband can void the sale. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i leima tenina le-takkanat Usha} \u8212- are we simply teaching what was established at Usha? For, as Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Chanina reports, at Usha they enacted: {\i ha\u8217'ishah she-machra b\u8217'nichsei milog b\u8217'chayei ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a woman who sells her {\i nichsei milog} during her husband\u8217's lifetime \u8212- {\i veimetah} \u8212- and then she dies \u8212- {\i haba\u8217'al motzi miyad halakochot}: the husband can take the property from the purchasers. The husband, having been the rightful heir to his wife\u8217's property, can void a sale that was made to cut off that inheritance right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perot vs Guf Guf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} distinguishes: {\i matnitin be-chayeha le-peirot} \u8212- our {\i Mishnah} is dealing with a sale during her lifetime, and what she tried to sell were the {\i perot} \u8212- the income rights \u8212- that the husband is entitled to. The {\i Gemara} understands the {\i Mishnah} as dealing not with a transfer of title but with an attempt to sell away the right to the fruit, and since the husband holds that right, the sale is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Takkanat Usha}, by contrast, goes further: {\i b\u8217'gufo shel karka v\u8217'achar mitah} \u8212- it concerns the body of the property, the title itself, and the husband\u8217's right after her death to inherit it. The concern at Usha was that a wife might sell the underlying property, eliminating the husband\u8217's eventual inheritance. That is a genuine additional {\i chiddush} \u8212- why should he be able to stop a transfer of title? \u8212- and it required a specific rabbinic enactment at Usha.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i Gemara} moves in a direction that was already anticipated when we read the {\i Mishnah}. The plain reading of the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- that she was trying to sell the property itself \u8212- would make our {\i Mishnah} overlap with {\i takkanat Usha}. But the {\i Gemara} here insists that our {\i Mishnah} is really about the {\i perot}, and the {\i chiddush} of {\i takkanat Usha} is the title. Once the {\i Gemara} reads our {\i Mishnah} as concerned with the {\i perot}, the analysis opens onto a deeper question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own vs Receive Income\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That deeper question is how we conceptualize the husband\u8217's right to the {\i perot}. There are two models. Under the first model, the wife earns the income from her property and then is obligated to hand it over to her husband, much as the husband provides food and she provides her earnings \u8212- a kind of reciprocal transfer. Under this model, the income remains fundamentally {\i hers} in the moment of generation, and she merely has an obligation to give it to him. If so, one might argue she could sell that income stream to a third party, since it is hers to earn. Under the second model, the income never belongs to the wife at all \u8212- it goes directly to him, it is his income, she is simply the conduit through which it is generated. Under this model, she obviously cannot sell what is not hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate in the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- and the fact that there is a genuine question about whether the husband can void a sale of the {\i perot} rights \u8212- itself implies that the first model has some force. If it were simply his income in every sense, the question would barely arise. The {\i Gemara\u8217's} resolution, once the {\i Mishnah} is read as voiding the sale, suggests the second model wins out: his right to the {\i perot} is of the nature of ownership, not merely a right to receive what she earns.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land vs Chattels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} concludes with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position: {\i Rabbi Shimon cholek bein nechasim lenechasim} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon distinguishes between categories of property. {\i Nechasim hayeduim laba\u8217'al} \u8212- property that the husband knows about \u8212- {\i lo timkor}, she may not sell {\i lechatchila}, and if she did, the sale is void. {\i She\u8217'ein yeduim laba\u8217'al} \u8212- property the husband does not know about \u8212- {\i lo timkor} {\i lechatchila}, but {\i ve\u8217'im machra venatna kayam}: if she sold it, the sale stands {\i bedieved}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: what qualifies as {\i yeduin} and what as {\i she\u8217'ein yeduin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geographic Remoteness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Chanina says: {\i yeduin mekarkaei} \u8212- property he knows about means land, because land cannot be hidden away; by its nature it is visible and known. {\i She\u8217'ein yeduin} \u8212- property he cannot be presumed to know about \u8212- means {\i metaltelin}, chattel, movable property, which can in principle be concealed, and therefore falls into a legal category of \u8220"not necessarily known about\u8221" regardless of whether he in fact knows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan takes a different view: {\i elu v\u8217'elu yeduin} \u8212- both land and chattel can equally be \u8220"known about.\u8221" The criterion is not the category of property but geography: {\i she\u8217'ein yeduin} is {\i kol she\u8217'yoshevet kan v\u8217'naflu lah nechasim b\u8217'medinat hayam} \u8212- she is here, and property fell to her in a distant land. If the property is geographically remote, it falls into the category of \u8220"not known,\u8221" and the sale stands {\i bedieved}. This is confirmed by a parallel {\i braita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Expectation & Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final {\i Rashi} illuminates the underlying logic of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's distinction, and it returns us to a theme that has run throughout the {\i sugya}. Rashi on {\i yeduin mekarkaei} comments: {\i al menas osan nechasim nesaha} \u8212- he married her with that property in mind. If he knew about it, then when he entered the {\i nissuin} he was counting on it: {\i she\u8217'hayah metzapeh} \u8212- he was expecting it, he had built his expectations around it, it was part of the basis on which he married her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking criterion. Even granting that he is technically entitled to the {\i perot} of all her property, and even granting that the {\i rabboseinu} voted to extend his voiding power to property acquired before the marriage \u8212- Rabbi Shimon introduces a knowledge-and-expectation element. If he did not know about the property, he cannot claim to have had expectations about it, and therefore {\i bedieved} the sale stands, even though he was presumably still entitled to the {\i perot} in the interim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is, as was noted, a remarkable feature of this {\i sugya} that the purely emotional or relational quality of the parties\u8217' expectations ends up doing legal work. The husband\u8217's right to void the sale is not derived mechanically from his {\i perot} entitlement alone; it is also conditioned on the reality of his awareness and expectation. If the property was truly unknown to him, that reality limits the legal force of his claim, even if it does not entirely eliminate his income rights. We will continue from here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 79\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon & Shtar Mav\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by resuming the discussion of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position, which had been introduced at the bottom of 78b. The husband is entitled to the {\i peirot} \u8212- the income and produce \u8212- from the wife\u8217's property that she brings into the marriage, and we had surveyed a series of debates concerning the extent of his right to block the sale of that property. Clearly, this right to block a sale could only apply once there had been {\i nissuin}, not merely {\i erusin}. Where property came to the wife after {\i nissuin}, the husband could intervene; where it arrived before {\i nissuin} and there is now {\i nissuin}, the matter was subject to debate. But in any event, the husband receives the {\i peirot}, and in some circumstances he could not even block the sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon introduces a distinction not only regarding the {\i peirot} themselves but also regarding when he may block a sale: he may do so only with respect to property he knew about, not property of which he was unaware \u8212- for instance, property located overseas. Rashi explains that the basis for this distinction is that the husband cannot claim he was relying upon or anticipating those particular properties. Consequently, at least with respect to property that predates the marriage, he is not entitled to block the sale of assets that were unknown to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Hides Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this framework in place, the {\i sugya} now turns to the practical question of what legal devices a woman might employ to protect her property, given that the husband has been granted these substantial rights. The Gemara presents a case story: {\i hahi ita de-ba\u8217'ya d\u8217'tavrichinah l\u8217'nichseh mi-gavrah} \u8212- a certain woman wanted to spirit away her property from her husband. Rashi supplies additional background: she was a widow who wished to remarry, and in anticipation of the marriage she transferred her property to her daughter \u8212- {\i kedei l\u8217'avruchey zechus ba\u8217'al meihem}, so that the husband-to-be would not acquire rights in it. This is the well-known phenomenon of transferring property into another\u8217's name to protect it from creditors, here applied to protect it from a future husband\u8217's {\i peirot} rights.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The transfer was executed before the marriage, since had it been done afterward the husband might have been able to block it. She then married, and subsequently was divorced. The problem that brings this to a court arose after the divorce: the daughter, having received a valid {\i shtar}, refused to return the property to her mother. During the marriage the mother could not demand the property back \u8212- she needed it to remain formally in her daughter\u8217's name \u8212- but once divorced she wanted it returned, and the daughter declined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The woman came before Rav Nachman, who tore up the gift document \u8212- {\i kara Rav Nachman l\u8217'shetara}. The clear implication is that he regarded the entire transaction as a legal fiction, a mere ruse to shield the property from the husband. This is a striking ruling, because the daughter possessed a valid {\i shtar}, and Rav Nachman acted based on the circumstances alone. Rashi suggests that witnesses had testified explicitly that the gift was not genuine. Tosafot, however, finds this difficult: the Gemara is about to record a challenge to Rav Nachman\u8217's conduct, which would make little sense if there had been explicit witnesses. Tosafot concludes instead that Rav Nachman exercised a judgment call based entirely on contextual evidence, without any formal {\i moda\u8217'ah} \u8212- declaration of protest \u8212- having been delivered to witnesses. As Tosafot states: {\i v\u8217'nireh d\u8217'af al gav d\u8217'lo masrah moda\u8217'ah l\u8217'shum edim, d\u8217'mimaila hu nikar} \u8212- it was self-evident from the circumstances. {\i V\u8217'lo hayu devarim shebalev}, it was not a case of unexpressed inner intent that the law would ordinarily disregard, because the intent here was unmistakably manifest: {\i nikar hadavar shehu lo nitkavnah ela lehavriach} \u8212- it was evident that her only intention was to chase the property away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis is reinforced by the principle \u8212- attributed to Ramban and Tosafot ha-Rosh \u8212- that the rule of {\i devarim shebalev einam devarim} applies most forcefully in bilateral transactions, where the other party is paying consideration and cannot be expected to bear the risk of the transferor\u8217's secret reservations. In a unilateral gift, by contrast, we look more carefully to the state of mind of the one giving the gift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Mavrachat Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Anan witnessed this and went to complain to Mar Ukva, saying: {\i chazi Mar Nachman Chakla\u8217'ah heichi makra shtarei d\u8217'inshei} \u8212- did you see how Rav Nachman the farmer tears up people\u8217's documents? Mar Ukva, rather than responding to the rhetorical dismissal of Rav Nachman, asked to hear the full facts of the case: {\i eini izi gufa d\u8217'uvda heichi hava} \u8212- tell me in truth, how did the whole matter actually unfold? Once Rav Anan recounted the story, Mar Ukva responded: {\i shtara mavrachat ka\u8217'amrat?} \u8212- are you telling me about a {\i shtar mavrachat}, a document to chase away property? There was already a recognized name for this device. And Mar Ukva then cited what Rav Chanilai said in the name of Shmuel: {\i moreh hora\u8217'ah ani} \u8212- I am a duly authorized decisor, with authority from the Reish Galuta \u8212- {\i yoreh yoreh} \u8212- and if such a {\i shtar mavrachat} ever came before me, {\i ekra\u8217'enu}, I would tear it up as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This establishes that the practice of tearing up a {\i shtar mavrachat} was not an idiosyncratic ruling by Rav Nachman but a recognized position of Shmuel, one of the foremost authorities of the Babylonian amoraim. The court must assess the circumstances, and where the context makes clear that the document was a legal fiction, it may be voided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel & Rava Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then posed a question to Rav Nachman: {\i ta\u8217'ama mai} \u8212- what precisely is the reasoning behind tearing up the document? The presumed justification for Shmuel\u8217's ruling is the principle that {\i lo shavik inish nafshiah v\u8217'yahiv l\u8217'achrinei} \u8212- a person does not simply abandon their own interests and give their property to someone else. An unexplained {\i stam} transfer of one\u8217's entire property to a third party is highly suspicious and provides reasonable grounds to conclude that the gift was not sincere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Interest Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's question, however, is that this is not a transfer to a stranger but to the woman\u8217's own daughter. It is not uncommon for people to transfer property to their children even while alive and not on their deathbed. Does the presumption of self-interest still operate in such a case? The Gemara responds: {\i afilu hachi bimkom barta nafshai adifa} \u8212- even so, when it comes to one\u8217's daughter, one\u8217's own self-interest takes precedence. One does not simply sign away one\u8217's entire estate to one\u8217's children without reservation, even beloved children. {\i Chayecha v\u8217'chaye d\u8217'samchi chayecha kodmin} \u8212- even in matters of family, one\u8217's own life comes first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara thus maintains the principle even in the case of a daughter, establishing that an unqualified wholesale transfer of all one\u8217's property to one\u8217's child is presumed to be insincere absent a credible contrary explanation. Naturally, this raises the question of whether the same presumption applies if only part of the property was transferred; that distinction, as we will see, becomes relevant shortly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosefta Remedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fake Documents Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the concept of the {\i shtar mavrachat} and Rav Nachman\u8217's authority to void it, the Gemara now presents a challenge from a {\i Tosefta} that appears to lay out legitimate mechanisms by which a woman may protect her property from her husband\u8217's rights \u8212- with apparent rabbinic sanction. The {\i Tosefta} states: {\i harotzah shetavriach nechasia miba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a woman who wishes to transfer her property away from her husband, {\i keitzad hi osah} \u8212- what may she do? {\i Kotevet shtar pasim l\u8217'acherim} \u8212- she writes a fictitious document to others. That is the position of Shimon ben Menasya. Rashi explains the term {\i pasim} as {\i piyusim} \u8212- a kind of enticement or persuasion document \u8212- essentially meaning a document whose purpose is to give the appearance of a genuine transfer while in fact being a legal fiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} continues with the position of the Sages: {\i v\u8217'chachamim omrim ratza mesachek bah} \u8212- the Sages say that if she does this without more, the recipient may simply laugh in her face and walk away with the property. There is thus a tension: Rav Nachman voids a document that looks real but is secretly a {\i shtar mavrachat}, while the Sages here acknowledge that a {\i shtar mavrachat} can work \u8212- provided it is structured properly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages\u8217' requirement is explicit: {\i ad shetichtov lo mehayom u\u8217'lechisheerze} \u8212- she must write into the document a specific conditional formulation: the transfer is effective from today, but subject to my right to revoke it whenever I wish. This is precisely the kind of {\i tenai} that makes the protection viable \u8212- the recipient cannot simply walk away with the property because the right of revocation is built in from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This addresses the concern raised above: why did the mother in the original case not simply use such a conditional formulation? The answer is that with an explicit {\i tenai} of this kind, if the wife exercises her right to revoke upon divorce, the husband might argue that since the transfer was retroactively annulled, he is owed compensation for the {\i peirot} he would have received over the entire duration of the marriage. Rashi\u8217's comment is revealing in this regard: {\i bishtarei zeh mehayom yesumacha ucheshertzeh yesh kan hazvacha mefurash} \u8212- the explicit abandonment is contained within the document itself, so that when she declares {\i eini rotzeh} \u8212- I no longer want this \u8212- the question of what that does retroactively is not entirely settled. One could imagine a creditor-recipient colluding with the husband: the recipient assigns his rights to the husband under a power of attorney, the husband collects the fruits, and they divide the proceeds. These complications are real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fake Transfer Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presses the logical difficulty: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'chasav lei hachi} \u8212- it is only because the conditional language was written in that the wife retains the property. {\i Ha lo chasav lei hachi kanyei lo-keach} \u8212- but if it was not written, the recipient acquires outright. This seems to contradict Rav Nachman, who simply tore up the {\i shtar} without requiring any explicit language. The Gemara\u8217's response, through Rava, is that there is no real contradiction: {\i lo kashya ha b\u8217'kula ha b\u8217'miktzata} \u8212- in the case of an entire property transfer, the circumstance is so inherently suspicious that explicit conditional language is unnecessary; the court can infer from context alone that it was a {\i shtar mavrachat}. This is the {\i anan sahadi} \u8212- we ourselves are witnesses \u8212- that {\i lo shavik inish} the principle that no one signs away all their property without reason. But where only part of the property is transferred, the inference is no longer so obvious and more explicit language is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even so, the Gemara immediately raises a more fundamental problem: if the transfer was a legal fiction in any event \u8212- whether because it was the entire property and thus obviously fake, or because the explicit conditional language renders it revocable \u8212- then the wife does not truly own the property during the marriage. But {\i v\u8217'likniyah ba\u8217'al} \u8212- if it is not genuinely owned by the transferee, should the husband not then be entitled to it? How can the wife have it both ways: on the one hand, the document is not genuine, so she recovers the property after divorce; and on the other hand, the husband is excluded from it during the marriage?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unknown Property Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye provides the resolution: {\i asu-un k\u8217'nichasim she-ein yeduyim l\u8217'baal v\u8217'aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- they treated this as equivalent to property that is not known to the husband, in accordance with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. This is a striking application of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's principle. We had understood Rabbi Shimon\u8217's distinction between known and unknown property as referring primarily to the husband\u8217's right to block a sale. But here it emerges that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position extends to the {\i peirot} themselves: if the husband does not know of the property, he is not entitled to its {\i peirot}. The existence of a {\i shtar} \u8212- even one that is technically fictitious \u8212- operates formally to make the property appear as though it is not hers, and by extension not known as her property to the husband. That formal appearance suffices, according to this analysis, to bring the property within the category of {\i nichsaim she-einam yeduyim} and to exclude the husband from its {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable legal construction. The rabbis gave the husband sweeping rights to the {\i peirot} of his wife\u8217's property, and then simultaneously furnished her with a vehicle to shield her property from those very rights. The device is frankly a legal fiction, and the Gemara acknowledges this openly. And yet the rabbis validated it, apparently because they recognized that a woman is not always in a position of sufficient power to negotiate an explicit stipulation with her husband withdrawing his {\i peirot} rights \u8212- though such a stipulation was theoretically available in a later {\i perek}. The {\i shtar mavrachat}, properly structured, allows her to accomplish the same result unilaterally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the divorce she recovers the property in full, since the transfer was never binding. She is entitled to the income during that period as well. She need not have had explicit witnesses, nor formal {\i tenai} language in every case, to achieve this protection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbeinu Tam Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot records that a comparable case came before Rabbeinu Tam \u8212- a case not of husband and wife but of a debtor who wished to protect property from a creditor\u8217's lien, or from his wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} lien, by transferring it fictitiously to a third party before incurring the debt or the marriage. Rabbeinu Tam ruled that in such cases the {\i shtar} was genuine and the transferee actually acquired the property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His reasoning is crucial: {\i dedavka hacha dehavei mavrachet lo kani ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the {\i shtar mavrachat} works only in the wife-husband context, and only because there exists a legally recognized category \u8212- {\i nichsaim she-einam yeduyim} \u8212- within which a wife can own property that is formally inaccessible to her husband. The fictitious transfer is given effect because it mimics this established category: the property is on paper not hers, and the husband therefore has no legal basis to claim {\i peirot} from something that does not appear to be his wife\u8217's. The legal fiction operates within an existing legal framework that can accommodate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i aval bish\u8217'ar oirvin} \u8212- in all other cases, no such category exists. A creditor\u8217's rights attach to all property genuinely owned by the debtor; there is no recognized exception for property that is secretly the debtor\u8217's but officially in another\u8217's name. Therefore, in those cases, either the transfer is genuine \u8212- in which case the transferee truly owns the property \u8212- or it is not, in which case it remains exposed to the creditor. There is no middle ground. As Rabbeinu Tam states: {\i i lo havei kani loke\u8217'ach havei kani lei leba\u8217'al chovo ule\u8217'isha} \u8212- if the transferee does not acquire, then the creditor or wife acquires. The {\i shtar mavrachat} device, which allows one to own something without a particular party having rights to it, is specific to the case of a wife and her husband under Rabbi Shimon\u8217's framework. Extend it beyond that relationship and the logic collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes two positions on whether we actually rule like Rabbi Shimon: one view holds that we do not rule like him but invoke his logic specifically for this protective purpose \u8212- which makes the protection even more striking, since it rests on a principle we do not formally adopt. The other view holds that the {\i chachamim} do rule like Rabbi Shimon. Either way, the result is a powerful expression of rabbinic concern for protecting the wife\u8217's financial position even within a legal system that otherwise granted the husband extensive rights over her property.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Property After Betrothal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion now turns to a new {\i mishna}. The topic is property that comes to the wife and what happens to it once the husband is entitled to the {\i peirot}. Rather than dealing with real estate from the outset, the {\i mishna} considers what happens when the property takes forms other than fields, and what obligations flow from that. If she inherited money \u8212- {\i nafal lah kesafim} \u8212- and had a non-interest-bearing sum, {\i yilkach bahen karka v\u8217'hu ochel peirot}: they purchase land with it and he eats the fruits. The same rule applies to detached produce: if she inherited cut grain or other harvested goods, they are liquidated and the proceeds used to purchase land which then generates {\i peirot} for the husband. The point is that the husband is entitled to insist that the assets be converted into a form that is income-generating \u8212- specifically {\i karka} \u8212- so that the principal is preserved and the {\i peirot} flow to him continuously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Valuation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then addresses the case of {\i peirot hamichubarim lakarka} \u8212- produce still attached to the land. Here the question is more subtle. Suppose the wife inherited an orchard with fruit on the trees. Is the value of the fruit on the trees considered part of her {\i karka} \u8212- part of the principal she retains title to \u8212- or is it already considered {\i peirot} that will flow to the husband once harvested?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir rules: {\i shamim otam kamah hi yafah im peirot ve-kamah hi yafah belo peirot} \u8212- one assesses how much the property is worth with the fruit on the trees and how much it is worth without them. The difference represents the value of the attached produce. That differential is not treated as {\i karka}; rather, {\i yilkach bahen karka v\u8217'hu ochel peirot} \u8212- it must be used to purchase additional land, and the husband eats the {\i peirot} from that new land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate: suppose the orchard with fruit on the trees is worth a thousand. Once the fruit is picked and brought to market, it sells for fifteen hundred. The extra five hundred \u8212- the increase in value from picking and bringing to market \u8212- is {\i peirot} generated by the {\i karka} during the marriage, and it goes directly to the husband. But the original thousand that represented the value of the fruit on the trees at the time she inherited it \u8212- that is part of her principal. When the fruit is sold and that original value is recovered, she uses it to purchase more {\i karka}, retaining title, and that {\i karka} continues to generate {\i peirot} for the husband going forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit Distribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then states a general rule about which fruits belong to whom when the wife enters and exits the marriage: {\i eilu hein hanichasim shenichnasim veyotzim imah} \u8212- these are the assets that enter and exit the marriage with her. {\i Peirot hamichubarim lakarka umotar} \u8212- the value of fruit attached to the land and the surplus from their sale \u8212- {\i yilkach bahen karka v\u8217'hu ochel peirot}: this is part of her principal, used to purchase land, with the husband eating the {\i peirot} from that land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vahatlushin min hakarka shelo} \u8212- produce that was already detached from the land at the time she inherited it belongs to the husband. When that harvested grain or cut fruit is sold, the proceeds are his {\i peirot}. The {\i karka} itself \u8212- now bare of that detached produce \u8212- reverts to the category of principal she holds title to, and future produce from it will again be his {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Entry/Exit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon offers a symmetric formulation: {\i makom sheyafeh kochah bichnisatah hura kochah biyitziasah} \u8212- where her position is stronger at entry into the marriage, it is weaker at exit; where it is weaker at entry, it is stronger at exit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Concretely: {\i peirot hamichubarim lakarka bichnisatah shelo} \u8212- if when she enters the marriage the fruit is attached to the trees, that is his. The attached fruit is treated as part of the {\i karka}, and when picked during the marriage it is entirely his {\i peirot}. But {\i biyitziasah} \u8212- when they divorce and she leaves the marriage with fruit still on the trees \u8212- that attached fruit is hers. By the same logic that attached fruit is treated as {\i karka}, the fruit on the trees at the time of divorce is part of the {\i karka} she takes back; she is not required to pay him for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i vahatlushin min hakarka bichnisatah shelah} \u8212- detached produce that she had when she entered the marriage is hers, not yet his {\i peirot}. But {\i uvitziasah shelo} \u8212- produce that was detached during the marriage and is in his possession when she leaves: that is his, because it became his {\i peirot} at the moment it was detached from the land.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Investment Choices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field vs.\u160 ?Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now takes up the question of investment choices. Where the wife\u8217's property must be converted into income-generating assets, who decides what form that investment takes? The opening case: {\i ar\u8217'a u-vatei} \u8212- should the funds be invested in agricultural fields or in rental property? The Gemara rules: {\i ar\u8217'a} \u8212- agricultural land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reasoning is that rental property provides a more certain income stream and a more secure principal. Tosafot reads the case differently and more interestingly. According to Tosafot, fields maintain the value of the principal but generate comparatively modest income, while houses generate higher income but require ongoing investment for repairs and maintenance, causing the principal to erode over time. Naturally, the husband prefers the higher-income option \u8212- rental property \u8212- while the wife prefers the option that better protects her principal \u8212- the field. The {\i halacha} sides with the wife: she is entitled to insist on the investment that best protects the {\i keren}, even at the cost of lower income for the husband. This makes sense conceptually: the principal is ultimately hers and reverts to her upon divorce or widowhood; the husband\u8217's interest is in the {\i peirot}, not in the {\i keren} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this principle is directional \u8212- that is, whether it always favors the wife\u8217's preference \u8212- or whether it specifically favors whichever party wants to protect the principal. If, for some reason, the husband preferred the more conservative investment and the wife preferred the riskier higher-income option, the calculus according to Tosafot\u8217's reading would be that her preference for principal protection governs. But what if it is the reverse? That remains an open question that the Gemara does not directly address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asset Hierarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then runs through an ascending hierarchy of investment alternatives. {\i Batei u-diklei} \u8212- houses or palm trees? {\i Batei} \u8212- houses, because they offer more secure principal protection relative to palm trees. {\i Diklei u-ilanei} \u8212- palm trees or other fruit trees? {\i Diklei} \u8212- palm trees, for the same reason: the principal in palm trees is more secure than in other varieties of fruit-bearing trees. {\i Ilanei degufni} \u8212- other trees or vines? {\i Ilanei} \u8212- the other trees.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pattern throughout follows Tosafot\u8217's principle: at each decision point, the more conservative investment \u8212- the one that better preserves the principal \u8212- is favored, reflecting the wife\u8217's protected interest in the {\i keren}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timber & Fish Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presents a harder case: {\i arza de-veita u-feira de-chifrei} \u8212- a stand of trees from which timber is cut, and a pond or river stocked with fish. Here the question is whether the timber and the fish are to be categorized as {\i peirot} \u8212- income generated by the underlying asset \u8212- or as {\i keren} \u8212- part of the principal itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some say they are {\i peirot}: the husband chops down the trees and takes all the timber as his income. Others say they are {\i keren}: since the trees and fish do not regenerate automatically \u8212- unlike a field that reliably produces grain each season \u8212- selling the timber is actually depleting the principal, not enjoying its yield. Under this view, the proceeds of selling timber or fish belong to the wife as principal, to be reinvested in something that will generate {\i peirot} for the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara formulates the general principle: {\i d\u8217'giz\u8217'o machlif peirot} \u8212- if the thing replenishes itself, then what it produces is {\i peirot}; {\i ein giz\u8217'o machlif keren} \u8212- if it does not replenish itself, then what it produces is {\i keren}. This rule extends to non-regenerating resources more broadly: whatever is extracted from a source that does not regenerate is treated as principal, not income.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Offspring & Fruit of Fru\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal vs.\u160 ?Slave Offspri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi is cited \u8212- some attribute the tradition to Rabbi Ami, others to Rabbi Chiya bar Abba \u8212- with the following rule: one who steals the offspring of a {\i milog} animal \u8212- that is, an animal that a wife brought into the marriage \u8212- pays the double penalty ({\i tashlumei kefel}) to the wife. This implies that the wife owns the offspring. But this is puzzling: why should not the offspring of her animal be {\i peirot}, belonging to the husband?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara observes that this ruling follows neither the Rabbanan nor Chananyah. A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i vlad behemah milog l\u8217'baal} \u8212- the offspring of a wife\u8217's {\i milog} animal belongs to the husband, as {\i peirot}; {\i vlad shifchah milog l\u8217'ishah} \u8212- but the offspring of a wife\u8217's {\i milog} female slave belongs to the wife. Chananyah ben Akaviah disagrees and holds that the offspring of the slave is treated just like the offspring of the animal: it belongs to the husband as {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit of Fruit Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the difficulty: {\i afilu teima divrei hakol} \u8212- even according to all opinions, the {\i kefel} paid to the wife makes sense. The reason is the principle of {\i peirot tiktinu lei Rabbanan, peira de-peira lo tiktinu lei Rabbanan} \u8212- the rabbis established that the husband receives {\i peirot}, but they did not establish that he receives {\i peirot} of {\i peirot}. The {\i kefel} is a secondary benefit \u8212- an income generated by the lamb, which is itself already the husband\u8217's {\i peirot} \u8212- and therefore it falls into the category of {\i peira de-peira}, which was never granted to him. The husband owns the lamb, but the {\i kefel} for the lamb \u8212- a kind of income generated by what is already his {\i peirot} \u8212- belongs to the wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Irony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot points out a striking asymmetry. First, Tosafot clarifies that the principle of {\i peira de-peira} applies specifically to an anomalous benefit like {\i kefel}, which comes from outside rather than being a natural product. If the husband\u8217's lamb grows up and gives birth to more lambs, those subsequent generations are entirely his \u8212- {\i peira} of {\i peira} in a natural biological sense belongs to him indefinitely, once he owns the original {\i peirot}. It is only the artificial windfall of a theft penalty that is withheld as {\i peira de-peira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot then notes the irony: if someone steals the original ewe \u8212- the mother, which the wife owns as {\i keren} \u8212- the husband receives the {\i kefel}, because the {\i kefel} is {\i peirot} of the wife\u8217's principal, and the husband receives all {\i peirot}. But if someone steals the lamb \u8212- which the husband owns as his {\i peirot} \u8212- the husband does not receive the {\i kefel} for the theft of his own property; instead, the wife receives it, as {\i peira de-peira}. The result is genuinely bizarre: the husband collects the {\i kefel} when his wife\u8217's property is stolen, but not when his own {\i peirot} are stolen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Principal Protection Log\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then returns to the debate about the offspring of the slave. The question is posed: if the Rabbanan are concerned about the death of the animals \u8212- that is, if they recognize that animals, unlike {\i karka}, can die and thereby erode the wife\u8217's principal \u8212- then they should also not give the husband the offspring of the animals. The logic is that the offspring should be retained by the wife and used to reinvest in the principal, compensating for the inevitable attrition of the flock over time. Just as the dividends of a depreciating asset might be reinvested to maintain its value, so too the lambs born to the flock should replenish the principal rather than flowing to the husband as pure {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if they are not concerned about the death of the animals and simply treat the entire flock as durable principal, then the husband should receive the offspring of the slave as well, since by the same logic she is durable principal producing {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i ve-le-olam chayshi le-misa} \u8212- the Rabbanan are indeed concerned about death of the animals. The reason the wife still does not receive the offspring of the animals is distinct, as we shall see immediately below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains: {\i ve-shani beheima de-ika oros} \u8212- animals are different because there are hides. Even if an animal dies, the wife retains something: she can sell the hide and recover a minimal residual value. This is enough to distinguish the case from the slave. Because the wife is not entirely exposed to total loss of principal when an animal dies \u8212- there is some minimal recovery from the hide \u8212- the rabbis did not consider the principal sufficiently at risk to justify withholding the offspring from the husband. In the case of the slave, no such residual exists: if the slave dies, the wife recovers nothing, and the principal is entirely lost. Consequently, the offspring of the slave is retained by the wife to protect and reinvest in her principal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf ends with a final note from the Gemara: {\i amar Rav Zevid mishmiya de-Abimi halacha ke-Chananya} \u8212- Rav Zevid rules in the name of Abimi that the {\i halacha} follows Chananyah, who holds that the offspring of the slave is treated like the offspring of the animal and belongs to the husband. The discussion of the implications of this ruling will continue on the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 80\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nichsei Milog Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf continues from the middle of 79b, where the Gemara has been exploring the husband\u8217's entitlement to {\i peirot} \u8212- the usufruct \u8212- of {\i nichsei milog}, property that remains in the wife\u8217's legal title while the husband enjoys its income. The central tension throughout this discussion is the tension between protecting the wife\u8217's {\i keren} (principal) and honoring the husband\u8217's right to ongoing {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tif & Principal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara had just dealt with the question of {\i tif} \u8212- the natural increment of a flock or the offspring of female slaves \u8212- and whether some portion of that increment must be reinvested to protect the principal, since animals, unlike land, can die. The Gemara had noted that even when an animal dies, some residual value remains in the form of leather and the like, though one must acknowledge that this offers rather minimal protection of the original principal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanania Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now rules: {\i amar Ravana bar Chiya amar Shmuel, halacha ke-Chanania} \u8212- the ruling follows Chanania, who held that whether the case involves female slaves or animals, the increment belongs to the husband as {\i peirot}. Chanania\u8217's position was {\i lo chashinan le-mita} \u8212- we are not concerned about the eventual death or loss of the principal. Since the risk to the principal is distant and for the time being the principal remains intact, we rule accordingly and the {\i tif} of the flock passes directly to the husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fruits & Principal Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevach Beit Aviha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notwithstanding the ruling in Chanania\u8217's favor, the Gemara immediately introduces an important qualification. {\i Amar Rav amar Rav Nachman}: although we rule like Chanania and the offspring of the animals or the children of the female slave are considered {\i peirot} belonging to the husband, {\i modeh Chanania she-im nitgarsha notenet damim ve-notaltan} \u8212- Chanania concedes that if the wife is divorced, she may pay for those offspring and take them back with her. The reason given is {\i mipnei shevach beit aviha} \u8212- on account of the dignity and status of her father\u8217's house. This is a concept we will encounter again in the next {\i mishnah}. The image here is evocative: a woman who enters a marriage bringing a flock and departs twenty years later with a much larger one. Even though during the marriage those offspring were legitimately the husband\u8217's {\i peirot}, her right, upon divorce, to pay their value and reclaim them reflects a recognized interest in maintaining the status associated with the property she originally brought in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regular Fruit Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues with a new formulation from Rav Nachman: {\i hichnisah lo ez le-chalavah u-rachel le-gizatah ve-tarnegolet le-beitzatah ve-dekel le-peirotav} \u8212- if a woman brings into the marriage a goat for its milk, a ewe for its wool, a hen for its eggs, or a palm tree for its dates, the husband may continue eating the {\i peirot} \u8212- {\i ochel ve-holech ad she-tichleh ha-keren} \u8212- even until the principal is entirely consumed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These cases are somewhat different from the earlier discussion of offspring, where the increment arises less frequently. Here we are speaking of regular, ongoing yield: milk, wool, eggs, dates. These are the classic model of {\i karka ve-peirot} \u8212- a principal that produces regular fruit \u8212- and accordingly there is no question that the husband is entitled to consume that fruit. Rashi notes that in all these cases some residual value remains even upon the animal\u8217's death, such as the hide, though he acknowledges that this is a rather thin form of principal protection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Renewable Fruits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now extends this logic in a direction that is genuinely striking. {\i Amar Rav Nachman: ayeila le-glimei \u8212- peira havei. Michlei va-azeil ad de-kalyah.} If she brings in a cloak, the husband\u8217's use of that garment is considered its {\i peirot}, and he may continue using it even as it wears away entirely. This is the same conceptual framework: even where the principal itself is being consumed in the course of producing the benefit, we classify that benefit as {\i peirot} and grant it to the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} reinforces and extends this principle further: {\i ha-melach ve-ha-chol harei zeh peirot} \u8212- salt and sand extracted from a mine are classified as {\i peirot}. These are non-renewable resources. When you dig sand from a pit or extract salt from a deposit, you are not generating something new \u8212- you are removing the {\i keren} itself. After extraction, nothing remains to replenish it. And yet the Sages classify this as {\i peirot}. Similarly, {\i gafrit} \u8212- sulfur \u8212- and what Rashi identifies as {\i alum} (used as a dye for clothing and soap) extracted from a mine fall into this category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} here is considerable. If a woman brings into the marriage what amounts to oil fields, all of the oil extracted is classified as {\i peirot} going directly to the husband \u8212- even though once extracted, it is irreplaceable. After divorce, the well is no more productive than before; indeed, it is less so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit vs.\u160 ?Principal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara introduces the famous dispute between Rabbi Meir and the {\i Chachamim}: {\i Rabbi Meir omer keren va-Chachamim omrim peirot} \u8212- Rabbi Meir classifies these extracted resources as principal, while the {\i Chachamim} classify them as {\i peirot}. This maps onto a broader debate about whether fruit still attached to the tree ({\i mechubarim}) is considered part of the {\i karka} or already reckoned as {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position: {\i Rabbi Shimon omer makom she-yifeh kocha} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds that where the husband\u8217's position is stronger at the time of marriage, it is weaker at the time of divorce, and vice versa for the wife. The Gemara objects that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view is identical to that of the {\i Tanna Kamma}, since the {\i Tanna Kamma} also held that fruit attached to the tree at the time she enters the marriage is classified as {\i karka} and not {\i peirot}, consistent with the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's position against Rabbi Meir. {\i Amar Rava: mechubarim be-sha\u8217'at yetzi\u8217'ah ika beinaihu} \u8212- the practical difference between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Chachamim} concerns fruit attached to the tree at the moment of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rashi, the {\i Chachamim} hold that fruit which grew on the tree while the couple was married did so {\i bi-reshuto}, under his ownership, and therefore remains his. When the wife departs, she takes only the {\i karka} itself and must pay the husband for the value of fruit then attached to the tree. Rabbi Shimon, by contrast, holds that just as attached fruit is classified as {\i karka} when she enters, so too it is classified as {\i karka} when she exits \u8212- and she therefore takes it with her without payment. The {\i Chachamim} distinguish: entry and exit are not symmetric, because the fruit grew during his period of entitlement to {\i peirot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status vs.\u160 ?Income\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Old Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} takes up a new scenario. {\i Noflu lah avadim u-shfachos zekeinim} \u8212- she inherits elderly slaves. Old slaves produce little or no {\i peirot}; they may be more of a burden than a benefit. The {\i mishnah} rules that such slaves should be sold and the proceeds used to purchase land, from which the husband will then eat {\i peirot}: {\i yimachru ve-yilakach bahem karka ve-hu ochel peiros}. The husband, it appears, can compel this conversion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} dissents: {\i lo timkor mipnei she-hem shevach beit aviha} \u8212- she may not be compelled to sell them, because having servants, even infirm ones, contributes to her personal dignity and status. He cannot override that interest merely to secure a higher income for himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} applies the same framework to old olive trees and grapevines: {\i noflu lah zeisim u-gefanim zekeinim} \u8212- she inherits aging orchards with diminished productivity. Again, the rule is that they should be sold and land purchased from the proceeds. But here {\i Rabbi Yehuda} objects: {\i lo timkor mipnei shevach beit aviha} \u8212- she may not be forced to sell, because even a vineyard or an olive grove, even one past its prime, confers status. Rabbi Yehuda thus goes further than Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel: it is one thing to protect the status attached to a household of servants; Rabbi Yehuda holds that even the possession of an orchard, however unproductive, carries the same dignity interest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own vs.\u160 ?Borrowed Field\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a crucial distinction from Rav Kahana in the name of Rav: {\i machlokes she-noflu be-sadeh shelah} \u8212- the dispute between the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling and the dissenting opinions applies only when the vines or olive trees are situated in land that she herself owns. In that case, the argument about {\i shevach beit aviha} is fully operative, and there is a genuine disagreement about whether the husband may compel a sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i aval be-sadeh she-einah shelah} \u8212- if she owns only the trees and not the underlying land \u8212- {\i divrei ha-kol timkor}, everyone agrees she must sell. Why? {\i Mishum de-kalyah karna} \u8212- because in that case the principal itself is in the process of being destroyed. Old trees in a field one does not own will eventually die and leave nothing behind. The principal is actively at risk, and this transforms the analysis entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an interesting question: the risk to the principal is fundamentally {\i her} concern, since the principal belongs to her. Why should that risk be used to override {\i her} preference not to sell? Rashi, however, addresses this point from two directions simultaneously. The fact that the principal is at risk means {\i he} cannot object to a sale if {\i she} wants one, since she can invoke her interest in protecting her property. And the same fact drains the situation of any {\i shevach beit aviha}, since there is no status to be gained from trees on someone else\u8217's dying land \u8212- so she cannot invoke dignity to resist the sale either. Thus neither party can object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef raises an apparent difficulty: old slaves, even when infirm, are somewhat analogous to trees in another\u8217's field \u8212- the principal will eventually be consumed \u8212- yet the {\i mishnah} presents that as a case of genuine dispute (Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel protecting the wife\u8217's status even there). The Gemara resolves this by inverting Rav Kahana\u8217's formulation: the dispute is actually in the case where the field is {\i not} hers, and even there one opinion holds that {\i shevach beit aviha} applies. But {\i aval be-sadeh shelo} \u8212- if she owns the land itself and the vines, so the land will remain even after the vines die \u8212- {\i divrei ha-kol lo timkor mipnei shevach beit aviha}: everyone agrees she cannot be compelled to sell, since the status interest is fully present and the principal, in the form of the land, is secure.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Expenses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investment Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} shifts to a different but related question: what happens when the husband actually spends money \u8212- {\i hotza\u8217'ot} \u8212- on the wife\u8217's {\i nichsei milog} property? {\i Ha-motzi hotza\u8217'ot al nichsei ishto} \u8212- the husband who makes out-of-pocket expenditures on his wife\u8217's property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} establishes the baseline rule cleanly: {\i hotzi harbe ve-achal kima} \u8212- he invested a great deal and harvested very little, resulting in a net loss; or {\i kima ve-achal harbe} \u8212- he invested little and harvested a great deal, a significant net gain. In either case: {\i mah she-hotzi hotzi u-mah she-achal achal} \u8212- what he spent he spent, and what he ate he ate. He takes the risk and reaps the rewards. The investment is entirely his, the profit is entirely his, and the loss is entirely his. This makes straightforward sense: he bears the commercial risk and is entitled to the commercial upside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hotzi V\u8217'Lo Achal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more interesting case is: {\i hotzi ve-lo achal} \u8212- he invested but received no benefit whatsoever. The {\i mishnah} rules that in this case {\i yishava kama hotzi ve-yitol} \u8212- he may take an oath as to how much he invested and recover that amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially read this as applying whenever he received no gross income \u8212- even during an ongoing marriage, simply because a bad harvest or blight left him with nothing. But Rashi explicitly interprets the scenario more narrowly: {\i lo hispik le-echol ad she-gerasha} \u8212- the reason he received no {\i achal} is that he was divorced before the harvest came in. Rashi is evidently concerned about manipulation: if we allowed a husband to recover his investment simply by choosing not to harvest \u8212- deliberately letting the produce rot so as to claim {\i hotzi ve-lo achal} \u8212- it would invite serious {\i hara\u8217'mah}, strategic deception. Similarly, it would allow a husband to take enormous risks with his investment knowing he could always recover his principal if the harvest disappointed. Rashi therefore limits the case to a genuine divorce scenario, where the husband literally could not eat because the marriage ended before the harvest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minimum Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i ve-kama achila} \u8212- what is the minimum amount of benefit that disqualifies the {\i hotzi ve-lo achal} claim? {\i Amar Rav Assi: afilu grogeret achat} \u8212- even a single pressed fig.\u160 ?But this applies only {\i ve-hu she-achalah derech kavod} \u8212- if he consumed it in a dignified manner, meaning it was a meaningful act of consumption, not merely an incidental snack.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Abba amar Bei Rav: afilu shigra de-tamre} \u8212- even a pressed date suffices. The Gemara then records an unresolved question: {\i chuptza de-tamre mai?} \u8212- what about a mashed date? {\i Teiku} \u8212- no resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara further asks: if he did not eat even the minimal amount {\i derech kavod}, what then? {\i Amar Ulla: peigi bah terei amoraei be-ma\u8217'arava} \u8212- two Amoraim in the Land of Israel dispute the minimum monetary threshold: one says the equivalent of an {\i isar}, the other a {\i dinar}. Either way, it is not a large sum. If that small amount passed through his hands from the property, he cannot recover his investment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The judges of Pumbedita report that {\i Rav Yehuda} acted on a case involving {\i chazilei zemorot} \u8212- bundles of twigs taken from the field. Merely taking those twigs was considered sufficient benefit to deny the husband\u8217's recovery claim. This is consistent with Rav Yehuda\u8217's broader reasoning, as seen in the rule that consuming {\i orlah}, {\i shevi\u8217'it}, or {\i kila\u8217'im} produce from a field constitutes {\i chazakah} \u8212- establishing one\u8217's presence. Rashi there explains that \u8220"consuming\u8221" must refer to taking the wood or twigs (since the fruit itself is {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah}), and Tosafot raises the question of whether even the wood of {\i kila\u8217'im} might be {\i assur}. But the analogy holds: just as taking twigs establishes one\u8217's presence for purposes of {\i chazakah} in Bava Batra, here too it counts as extracting benefit that bars recovery of the investment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a special case: {\i amar Rabbi Yaakov amar Rav Chisda: ha-motzi hotza\u8217'ot al nichsei ishto ketanah} \u8212- a husband who invests in the property of his minor wife ({\i ketanah}), specifically one who was married off by her mother and brothers and who therefore retains the right of {\i mi\u8217'un}, the ability to dissolve the marriage retroactively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a distinctive problem. The husband invests money in the field, the wife allows him to harvest just enough to trigger the {\i achal} threshold, and then she performs {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- which retroactively annuls the marriage as if it never existed. Retroactively, he was never her husband; he simply spent money improving a stranger\u8217's field. His investment may be entirely at her mercy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rules: {\i ke-motzi al nichsei acher dami} \u8212- it is treated as though he invested in a stranger\u8217's property. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama?} \u8212- why? {\i Avdu bah rabanan takanta ki heichi de-la nifsadinehu} \u8212- the Rabbis enacted a protective ruling so that he should not act in ways that damage the property. The practical concern is obvious: if every dollar he invests is at risk of retroactive loss the moment she performs {\i mi\u8217'un}, he will minimize investment and maximize extraction, potentially at the expense of the principal. To induce prudent stewardship, the Rabbis protect his investment by treating him as a third-party investor with corresponding rights of recovery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also has a logical basis: since {\i mi\u8217'un} retroactively voids the marriage, at the time he was investing he was, retroactively speaking, a stranger. There is a famous position of Reb Chaim that explores just how far we look backwards after {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- whether we treat the period of the marriage as entirely void in retrospect or whether we recognize its status as it existed at the time. But for the purpose of this {\i halacha}, the Gemara treats it as investment in a stranger\u8217's field.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Inheritance Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a vivid case. {\i D\u8217'naflu lah arba me\u8217'ah zuzei be-Khuza\u8217'i} \u8212- a woman had an inheritance of 400 {\i zuz} located in a distant city. The husband went there \u8212- {\i azal gavrah, afik shesh me\u8217'ah} \u8212- and spent 600 {\i zuz} on travel and retrieval expenses. When he arrived, the property turned out to be worth only 400 {\i zuz}, which he liquidated and brought back. On the return journey, he needed one {\i zuz} for incidental expenses \u8212- {\i itztarikh leh chada zuza} \u8212- and took it from the 400 he had recovered. The case came before Rav Ami.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar leh Rav Ami: mah she-hotzi hotzi u-mah she-achal achal} \u8212- Rav Ami applied the standard {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule: he invested (600 {\i zuz}), he ate (the 1 {\i zuz} spent en route), and the arrangement stands as is. Since he received some benefit, even minimal, he cannot recover his investment. The net loss of 200 {\i zuz} is his to absorb.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis objected to Rav Ami: {\i hanei milei heicha de-achal peirot} \u8212- that rule applies only when what he consumed was genuinely {\i peirot} produced by the investment. Here, what he retrieved was the {\i keren} itself \u8212- the principal. {\i Ha karna ka achil} \u8212- he was eating the principal, not {\i peirot}. The one {\i zuz} he spent was drawn not from the income of an ongoing investment but from the very corpus of the property he had retrieved. {\i Havei leh hotzi ve-lo achal} \u8212- this is properly classified as a case where he invested and received no {\i peirot}. {\i Ei hachi havei lei hotzi ve-lo achal yishava kama hotzi ve-yitol} \u8212- and therefore he may swear how much he invested and recover it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point a critical limitation must be noted. Can he recover the full 600 {\i zuz}? The Rosh addresses this explicitly and rules: {\i veyitol ad shiur shevach de-shaminan kama havu shavin arba me\u8217'ah zuzei de-shata ve-lo yoter} \u8212- he may recover only up to the actual increase in value of the property, not more. The wife cannot be made to suffer a loss as a result of his investment. Since the property was worth 400 {\i zuz}, he may recover at most 400 {\i zuz}. He cannot force her to pay 600 for a property worth only 400.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This limitation has implications beyond the specific case of divorce that Rashi describes. In this scenario, the couple does not appear to have been divorced at all; this is simply money that went into a hole and produced no {\i peirot}. Nevertheless, the husband can recover his investment \u8212- but only up to the actual increase in value he delivered to the property. He spent 600 {\i zuz} to give his wife access to a 400 {\i zuz} asset; he may therefore recover 400 {\i zuz}. The Rosh\u8217's principle ensures that the wife never pays more than the benefit she actually received.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath Requirement (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the question of when an oath is required and what it accomplishes. {\i Amar Rav Assi: ve-hu she-yesh shevach ke-neged hotza\u8217'ah} \u8212- recovery through oath is available only where the actual increase in value is at least equivalent to the investment. This gives rise to two distinct scenarios: either the investment exceeded the increase (the case it comes to exclude from full recovery), or the increase exceeded the investment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye: she-im haya shevach yeter al ha-hotza\u8217'ah, notel et ha-hotza\u8217'ah belo shvu\u8217'a} \u8212- Abaye held that if the increase in value exceeds the investment, he need not take an oath; he simply claims his investment and is believed. Only when it is a break-even \u8212- investment equals increase \u8212- must he swear, since the wife leaves with nothing additional in her pocket.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar leh Rava: im ken ati l\u8217'arumei} \u8212- Rava objected that this invites deception. If an oath is required only when investment equals increase, a husband will always claim he spent just under the increase, since below that threshold he is believed without a {\i shvu\u8217'ah}. The temptation to inflate the expense report is simply too great.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela amar Rava: she-im hayta hotza\u8217'ah yetira al ha-shevach, ein lo ela hotza\u8217'ah shiur shevach u-veshvu\u8217'ah} \u8212- the ruling is as follows. He must always swear to collect. Moreover, if his investment exceeded the actual increase in value of the property, he is entitled only to recover up to the amount of the increase \u8212- not the full investment. {\i U-veshvu\u8217'ah} \u8212- and even then, always subject to an oath. This dual requirement \u8212- oath always required, recovery capped at actual increase \u8212- protects against both false claims and against making the wife bear losses that exceed the benefit she actually received from the investment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Expenses (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sharecroppers Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a new question: {\i ba\u8217'al she-horid arisin tachtav mahu?} \u8212- what is the law when the husband places sharecroppers ({\i arisin}) on his wife\u8217's property? The scenario: the husband arranges for sharecroppers to work the land on the standard arrangement \u8212- they invest their labor and expenses, take half the harvest, and he takes the other half. But before the harvest comes in, he divorces his wife. The sharecroppers have invested in the land; the husband, having made no out-of-pocket expenditure himself (since his payment to them comes from the harvest), has received nothing. The wife now holds the improved land. What are the sharecroppers\u8217' rights \u8212- against whom, and for how much?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents two possible frameworks. {\i Ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'va\u8217'al nachit} \u8212- they entered into their arrangement with the husband. As Tosafot elaborates, presumably the sharecroppers were aware this was {\i nichsei milog} and understood that the husband\u8217's right to {\i peirot} was contingent on remaining married through the harvest. By taking the arrangement, they accepted that risk. If so, {\i histalek lei va\u8217'al histalku lehu} \u8212- when the husband is removed from the picture by divorce, they are removed with him. Their claim against the wife is zero.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i O dilma ada\u8217'ata de-ara necheis} \u8212- or perhaps their real relationship is not with the husband but with the land itself. There is something to an {\i aris} arrangement \u8212- sharecropping \u8212- that is distinctively tied to the land rather than to the owner. Unlike a day laborer ({\i po\u8217'el}) whose relationship is purely personal and contractual, the {\i aris} invests in the land and participates in its yield. On this view, their entitlement inheres in the land regardless of the marital status of its owner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa bar Nachmani objects to the framing of this question entirely: even in the most unfavorable scenario, why should the sharecroppers receive {\i less} than a stranger who enters another\u8217's field without permission and plants it? Such a person is at minimum entitled to {\i yad al ha-tachtonah} \u8212- the lower hand \u8212- which is some form of recovery. So why are we considering zero as a possible outcome?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i hatam leika inish de-tarach, hacha ika va\u8217'al de-tarach} \u8212- when a stranger enters an ownerless or neglected field, there is objectively no one who would have worked it, and his uninvited investment therefore confers a real benefit. But here, the wife can say: had the husband done it himself, I would owe him nothing \u8212- because his uninvited investment, ending without any {\i achal}, would be governed by {\i hotzi ve-lo achal} only in the context of divorce (per Rashi), and even there it would be capped by the increase in value. Since the husband could have worked the land himself, the sharecroppers\u8217' entry displaces no genuine gap; the wife suffered no loss by their working rather than the husband working.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land-Based Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Huna breih de-Rav Yehoshua: chazina} \u8212- we examine the facts. {\i Ei va\u8217'al aris hu} \u8212- if the husband himself is capable of working the land as a sharecropper, then indeed {\i histalek lei va\u8217'al histalku lehu} \u8212- when the husband is gone, the sharecroppers\u8217' claim is gone with him, because the wife can say her husband would have worked it. Their deal was with the husband, and his removal ends their entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ei bela\u8217'av arisu} \u8212- but if the husband was not himself capable of working the land, then {\i ara le-arisuta kaima} \u8212- the land was, by its very nature, waiting for sharecroppers to come and work it. Someone had to do this work, and the natural arrangement is an {\i aris} relationship. In that case the sharecroppers are entitled not merely to their out-of-pocket investment but to the full {\i aris} arrangement, because that is what the land required. Presumably this also reflects that the husband made the actual deal with them, so their claim is grounded both in the nature of the land relationship and in the contractual arrangement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Transfer of Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Selling Peirot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i ibaya} opens: {\i ba\u8217'al she-machar karka l\u8217'peirot, mahu?} \u8212- may the husband sell his usufruct rights in his wife\u8217's property to a third party for a lump sum? The logic in favor seems straightforward: the husband legally holds a {\i kinyan l\u8217'peiros}, a recognized property right in the {\i peirot}. Ordinarily {\i kinyan peiros ke-kinyan ha-guf dami} \u8212- the right to {\i peirot} is treated like ownership of the body for many purposes. Why, then, should he be unable to transfer that right, receiving a lump sum in lieu of the ongoing annual yield?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara frames two competing rationales. {\i Mi amrinan mah d\u8217'kana akni} \u8212- does he sell what he owns, and therefore the sale stands? {\i O dilma ki tikinu lei rabanan peiros le-ba\u8217'al mishum revacha d\u8217'beisa} \u8212- or perhaps the Sages\u8217' grant of {\i peirot} to the husband was made specifically for the purpose of {\i revach ha-bayit}, providing a steady, ongoing income to the household? If that is the reason, then the right was given for a purpose and may not be redirected. A one-time lump sum deposited in a bank account \u8212- or spent on a luxury \u8212- is not the same as the regular annual income that the {\i peirot} arrangement is designed to provide. {\i Aval le-zabuni, lo} \u8212- it was not intended to be converted into a lump sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Validity Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yehuda Mar Bar Marmar mishmei de-Rava amar: mah she-asah asui} \u8212- in the name of Rava, Yehuda Mar Bar Marmar held that {\i be-di\u8217'eved} such a sale is valid. {\i Rav Pappa amar mishmei de-Rava: lo asah ve-lo klum} \u8212- Rav Pappa, also in Rava\u8217's name, held that it does not work at all; the sale is entirely void. Rav Pappa notes that Yehuda Mar Bar Marmar\u8217's attribution to Rava was not stated explicitly but inferred from a case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maidservant Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case from which Yehuda Mar Bar Marmar inferred: a woman brought two maidservants into the marriage. The husband, retaining the right to their service as {\i peirot}, then married a second wife and told her that a maidservant\u8217's service \u8212- drawn from his {\i peirot} rights over his first wife\u8217's property \u8212- would be at her disposal. The first wife cried out in protest: {\i tzavcha} \u8212- he was effectively transferring to the second wife the benefit he had in her maidservant. The case came before Rava, who did not intervene. The observer might infer that Rava held such a transfer to be valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Revach Beisa Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara clarifies: {\i ve-lo hi} \u8212- that inference is incorrect. {\i Mishum revach beisa} \u8212- Rava\u8217's real reason for not intervening was that even on his view, the transfer here remained within the framework of household benefit. The maidservant, though technically serving the second wife, was still performing domestic work within the household \u8212- cooking, cleaning, and so on \u8212- and this preserved the essential character of the {\i peirot} arrangement: an ongoing benefit to the {\i beisa}, the household. Selling the right externally for a lump sum is a categorically different act and would not be countenanced even by Rava.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prohibition Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exploitation Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now states the final {\i halacha} explicitly: {\i ve-hilcheta ve-lo hi de-kol she-machar nechasav le-peiros lo asah ve-lo klum} \u8212- the ruling is that any sale of {\i peirot} rights is entirely void. The Gemara then explores the underlying rationale, since it matters practically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye amar: chashinan shema tachsise} \u8212- Abaye\u8217's reason is concern that a third-party buyer will exploit the land and destroy the principal. The husband has a long-term stake in the marriage and in the property; he is incentivized to manage it prudently. A purchaser of the {\i peirot} rights, however, faces a different calculus: the husband could divorce his wife at any time, ending the purchaser\u8217's rights. Knowing this, such a purchaser will squeeze the maximum {\i peirot} from the land in the minimum time, without regard to the long-term health of the principal. The prohibition thus protects the wife\u8217's {\i keren}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Household Income\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava amar: mishum revach beisa} \u8212- Rava\u8217's reason is different. The {\i peirot} were granted to the husband not to protect the wife\u8217's principal but to ensure a regular, ongoing stream of income into the household. A lump-sum sale converts that ongoing yield into a one-time windfall, which serves neither the household\u8217's regular needs nor the purpose for which the arrangement was created. The prohibition, on Rava\u8217's account, is structural: the right to {\i peirot} is inherently bound up with the ongoing marital household and cannot be severed from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference between Abaye and Rava becomes clear in a series of limiting cases. {\i Ika beinaihu ara de-mekarva le-masa} \u8212- if the land is close to the city, the husband can keep an eye on it and prevent a purchaser from exploiting it; Abaye\u8217's concern about destruction of the principal is diminished, and on his view the sale might be permitted. But on Rava\u8217's view, even in that case the sale would be prohibited, since it still converts ongoing income into a lump sum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i I nami ba\u8217'al aris hu} \u8212- or if the husband himself is a skilled {\i aris}, knowledgeable in land management, he can monitor and control the third party\u8217's actions effectively, again addressing Abaye\u8217's concern but not Rava\u8217's. Finally, {\i zuzei ve-ka\u8217'avid behu iska} \u8212- if the asset is money rather than land and the husband invests that money in an {\i iska}, a structured business arrangement that generates a regular yield, this too addresses Abaye\u8217's concern (there is no land to destroy) and, notably, may also satisfy Rava\u8217's concern, since the {\i iska} arrangement generates an ongoing return rather than a one-time lump sum \u8212- preserving the essential character of {\i revach beisa}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 81\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shomeret Yavam Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomeret Yavam Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf before us \u8212- 81b \u8212- continues the Talmud\u8217's sustained examination of a married woman\u8217's property rights, turning now to a particularly complex intermediate case: the {\i shomeret yavam}. This is the woman who, upon her husband\u8217's death, is bound in a state of waiting for {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} from her brother-in-law. She is neither fully released nor fully remarried. The Gemara elsewhere describes her status using the phrase {\i safek nesuah}, though that term is somewhat imprecise; it is better understood as a quasi-{\i nesuah}, a carrying-over of the marital bond from her deceased husband through the force of {\i zikah} to the surviving brother-in-law. The {\i nissuin} with the brother-in-law has not yet been actualized \u8212- he could always perform {\i chalitzah} \u8212- but neither has it been dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ambiguity gives rise to the central question of our chapter: to what extent do the reciprocal financial obligations of marriage apply to a {\i shomeret yavam}? The ongoing domestic obligations between spouses may seem obviously inapplicable when the couple has never lived together and the relationship may never be formalized. But the property rights \u8212- which are less about lived dynamics and more about legal entitlements \u8212- are far less clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: Inheritance Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna on 81b opens with the case: {\i shomeret yavam shenoflu lah nechasim} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibum} who comes into possession of property, whether through inheritance or by prior ownership. Both Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel agree that she may sell it outright and the sale is valid. This stands in contrast to the case of an {\i arusah} \u8212- a betrothed woman \u8212- regarding whom there is a dispute about whether she may sell {\i lechatchila}. In the case of the {\i shomeret yavam}, the likelihood that the relationship will be dissolved through {\i chalitzah} rather than consummated through {\i yibum} is considered sufficiently probable that both schools agree she retains effective control over her assets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna then turns to the harder case: {\i metah} \u8212- she dies while still in this intermediate state. What becomes of her {\i ketubah} and the {\i nechasim hanichnesim vehayotzim imah} \u8212- the property that enters and exits with her, which is the {\i nichsei melog}? Beit Shammai rules that the heirs of the husband and the heirs of the father divide everything equally \u8212- a Solomonic solution that sidesteps the uncertainty. Beit Hillel, by contrast, takes a more principled approach: {\i nechasim bechezkatan} \u8212- each category of property remains in the possession of whoever currently holds it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah vs.\u160 ?Nikhsei Melog\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before analyzing the Beit Hillel position in depth, it is worth clarifying the two categories of property at stake. The {\i nichsei melog} is property over which the wife retains title throughout the marriage; the husband acquires only the right to {\i peirot} \u8212- the usufruct \u8212- but never the principal. Because he holds no title to it, it need not be listed in the {\i ketubah}. Upon her death, since she held title, it passes to her heirs. The {\i nichsei tzon barzel}, by contrast, is the dowry \u8212- the {\i nedunya} \u8212- which she transfers into his ownership at marriage. This is recorded in the {\i ketubah} with its value at the time of the marriage. If he dies first, she extracts it from his estate; if she dies first, it remains with him. The term {\i ketubah} in this Mishna most likely refers to the {\i nedunya} in this technical sense, though it can also encompass the basic {\i ketubah} sum of two hundred {\i zuz} and any {\i tosefet} \u8212- the whole bundle of financial obligations the husband undertakes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel\u8217's ruling is therefore straightforward in its logic: the {\i nichsei tzon barzel} \u8212- the dowry \u8212- has been in the husband\u8217's possession throughout the marriage. Even though the husband is now dead, the presence of the {\i yavam} means the situation has not fully resolved itself. Since the dowry was in the husband\u8217's domain when he died, it remains there and passes to his heirs. The {\i nichsei melog}, which was always in the wife\u8217's domain since she retained title, passes to her heirs. There is no need to split anything; one simply leaves each category where it was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying conceptual problem that Beit Hillel is navigating can be framed this way: normally, whether the husband or wife predeceases the other determines everything. If the husband dies first, she extracts her {\i nichsei tzon barzel}; if she dies first, he keeps it and inherits her {\i nichsei melog}. Here, the husband died first \u8212- which would ordinarily mean she should extract the dowry \u8212- but the presence of the {\i yavam} means the marital nexus has not terminated. It is as though the husband has and has not died simultaneously. Beit Hillel resolves this by treating the status quo of each asset\u8217's location as determinative: leave the {\i tzon barzel} with the husband\u8217's heirs, since it was in his possession, and leave the {\i melog} with her heirs, since title always remained with her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Dies: Property Spl\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai: Equal Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai\u8217's position when the {\i shomeret yavam} dies is crisp: {\i yachloku yoreshei habaal im yoreshei haav} \u8212- divide everything equally between the heirs of the husband and the heirs of the father. Faced with genuine uncertainty about whose rights should prevail \u8212- given the ambiguous status of the deceased \u8212- Beit Shammai declines to privilege either side and simply splits the assets down the middle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel: Status Quo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel\u8217's formulation proceeds by category. {\i Vechetuvah bechezkut yoreshei habaal} \u8212- the {\i ketubah}, understood here as the {\i nedunya}, remains under the {\i chazakah} of the husband\u8217's heirs, because that is where it has been throughout the marriage. {\i Nechasim hanichnesim vehayotzim imah bechezkut yoreshei haav} \u8212- the {\i nichsei melog}, which the wife always retained title to, passes to her father\u8217's heirs. Each category of property stays with the party in whose domain it has naturally resided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Types at Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper logic of Beit Hillel\u8217's ruling emerges when we consider why the death of the husband alone, without a {\i yavam}, would yield a different result. In a normal case where the husband dies and leaves no {\i yavam}, the wife\u8217's {\i nichsei tzon barzel} immediately becomes hers to extract. But where a {\i yavam} exists, the marital status has not truly dissolved. The dead husband\u8217's estate has not conclusively transferred to her because the brother might yet perform {\i yibum}, effectively stepping into the husband\u8217's shoes. Beit Hillel treats the situation as follows: she is simultaneously someone whose husband died before her \u8212- which would favor her family \u8212- and someone whose husband effectively has not died because the {\i yavam} persists. Since the legal question cannot be resolved by looking at simple precedence, Beit Hillel anchors each asset to its current location and leaves it there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: does the {\i mitzvat yibum} affect this analysis? It does, precisely because if {\i yibum} had actually been performed and she subsequently died, her family would come and extract the {\i nichsei tzon barzel} \u8212- for then the husband would have predeceased her in a completed sense. Here, however, husband one preceded her in death but husband two \u8212- the {\i yavam} \u8212- did not. The question of which husband she effectively survived is exactly what is in dispute, and Beit Hillel resolves the uncertainty by leaving assets where they are.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yavam\u8217's Estate Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Lien Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna now pivots to a different but related scenario: the husband has died, leaving behind a {\i yavam}, and the question is what the {\i yavam} may do with the property he has now inherited from his dead brother. To understand this fully, one must appreciate a foundational principle of {\i ketubah} law: a wife holds a lien on all of her husband\u8217's property for the value of her {\i ketubah}. The formula in the {\i ketubah} document itself captures this: {\i kol nechasim achrayin likhtuvatech} \u8212- all the property is liable for the {\i ketubah}, both what I currently own and what I will acquire in the future. This is a blanket, perpetual lien. A husband may sell his property as he wishes during his lifetime; the lien travels with the real estate to the purchaser, and if new property is acquired, the wife gains a lien on that as well. It is, in a sense, a renewable resource \u8212- the lien is never exhausted because the husband\u8217's estate is dynamic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restriction on Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the {\i yevamah}, the situation is fundamentally different and far more constrained. Her {\i ketubah} \u8212- including her {\i nedunya} and the basic {\i ketubah} sum \u8212- is a lien specifically on her {\i dead husband\u8217's} estate. She does not have a lien on the living brother-in-law\u8217's own property. He is now in possession of his dead brother\u8217's estate, but that estate is a finite, non-renewable resource. If the {\i yavam} were to sell that property to a third party, her ability to collect her {\i ketubah} would be severely compromised. While it is true that if real estate is involved, she could in principle pursue the lien against the purchaser, this is far more difficult than collecting directly from the estate. Therefore, the {\i yavam} is not permitted to sell the dead brother\u8217's property \u8212- it must remain available for her eventual collection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money to Land Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This restriction creates a practical problem. The dead brother\u8217's estate may include not only real estate but also liquid assets \u8212- cash, harvested produce, and the like. The Mishna addresses this directly: {\i hiniach achiv maot yilku vahen karka vehu ochel peirot} \u8212- if the dead brother left money, one uses that money to purchase land, and the {\i yavam} then consumes the {\i peirot} of that land. The reasoning is elegant: the principal must remain intact and under lien so that her {\i ketubah} can eventually be collected, but the {\i yavam} is the rightful heir and should derive some benefit from what he has inherited. Converting liquid assets into real estate accomplishes both goals \u8212- the principal is preserved and protected by the lien, and the {\i yavam} enjoys the usufruct without disturbing the underlying asset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic applies to harvested produce that was separated from the land, {\i peirot hatlushin min hakarka} \u8212- it too must be converted into land, with the {\i yavam} enjoying the {\i peirot} of the new holding. The structure mirrors, in some respects, that of {\i nichsei melog}, where the wife retains title while the husband enjoys usufruct \u8212- but here the purpose is different. It is not about the nature of the property rights but about protecting the lien.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lien on Chattel Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The harder case is produce still attached to the land at the time of the husband\u8217's death \u8212- {\i hamechubarim lakarka}. Rabbi Meir rules that one must calculate the increase in the field\u8217's value attributable to the standing crop, and that additional value must also be converted into land so that her lien is protected even on that increment. His position assumes that the wife\u8217's lien extends to chattel \u8212- to movable property, to money and produce \u8212- and therefore even liquid assets must be secured.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} disagree. {\i Peirot hamechubarim lakarka shelo} \u8212- produce that is attached to the land belongs to him outright. Once harvested, the {\i chachamim} say, it is simply {\i peirot} and does not need to be converted. Implicit in this is the broader principle that a {\i ketubah} lien applies only to real estate, not to chattel. The dead husband may have left behind cash and produce, but her lien reaches only the land. The {\i yavam} is therefore free to pocket whatever chattel he inherits; she will be able to collect her {\i ketubah} from whatever real estate remains in the estate, but she has no claim against the movable assets. This is the {\i chachamim}\u8217's position: {\i kol hakodem bahem zachah} \u8212- whoever acts first acquires those assets, with the consequence that if the {\i yavam} seizes them, he keeps them, and if she somehow takes possession of them while still in the {\i shomeret yavam} state, that possession itself gives her some basis for a claim \u8212- a nuance the Gemara will explore further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possession Rights Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna concludes this section by addressing what happens once {\i yibum} is actually performed: {\i harei hi ke\u8217'ishto lechol davar} \u8212- she is like his wife in every respect, and the ambiguities of the intermediate state dissolve. The one significant exception that persists even after {\i yibum} is that {\i shetehei ketuvatah al nichsei baalah harishon} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} remains a claim against the dead first husband\u8217's estate rather than against the living second husband\u8217's estate. This is the defining residue of her prior status. Even as a fully married woman, her financial security is tethered to a finite pool of assets rather than to the living husband\u8217's dynamic and growing estate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cannot Limit Ketubah Lien\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Yibum Ketubah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even after {\i yibum} has been performed and she has become fully his wife in every other sense, the {\i ketubah} remains tied to the dead first husband\u8217's estate. This asymmetry \u8212- full marital status in every other domain, yet a restricted lien for {\i ketubah} purposes \u8212- is the defining financial consequence of the {\i yibum} relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Pre-designation Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna therefore rules: {\i lo yomar lah harei ketuvatekh munachah al hashulchan} \u8212- the {\i yavam} cannot say to her, \u8220"Here is your {\i ketubah}, placed on the table.\u8221" The Gemara will explore whether this means a literal prepayment or a designation of specific assets, but the core prohibition is clear: he may not try to limit her lien to a defined parcel or pre-designated sum of money in order to free himself to deal with the remainder of the estate. The motivation for doing so is obvious \u8212- a prospective buyer would want property free of encumbrances, and limiting her claim would allow the {\i yavam} to sell unencumbered. The Mishna forbids this stratagem entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universal Lien Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibition applies not only in the context of {\i yibum} but as a general rule of marriage: {\i ve-chen lo yomar adam le\u8217'ishto} \u8212- no husband may say this to his wife. {\i Ella kol nechasav achrayin liketuvatah} \u8212- all of his property must remain responsible for her {\i ketubah}. The wife has a right to a lien over the entirety of her husband\u8217's holdings, and that right cannot be contracted away or pre-empted by the husband\u8217's unilateral designation of specific assets. This levels the playing field across all marriages \u8212- every married man\u8217's property is similarly encumbered, which makes the lien universal and therefore, in a sense, no more burdensome to any individual buyer than to any other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce-Remarry Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna then provides, somewhat obliquely, the solution to the {\i yavam}\u8217's predicament. {\i Girshah} \u8212- if after performing {\i yibum} he divorces her \u8212- {\i ein lah ella ketuvatah} \u8212- she collects only her {\i ketubah}. This is the implied solution: by divorcing her, he allows her to collect her {\i ketubah} from the dead brother\u8217's estate, which then clears that estate and frees him to deal with whatever remains. The divorce forces the collection and thereby liberates the property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage: Normal Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what if he then remarries her? {\i Hehzirah harei hi kechol hanashim ve\u8217'ein lah ella ketuvah bilvad} \u8212- if he takes her back in marriage, she is like any other wife and has only one {\i ketubah}. This is a general {\i halachah} of marriage: if a man divorces his wife and then remarries her, she does not acquire a second {\i ketubah}. It is all considered a continuation of the original obligation. The significance here, however, is transformative for the {\i yavam}\u8217's situation: once he divorces and remarries her, the {\i ketubah} obligation shifts. It is no longer a lien on the dead first husband\u8217's finite estate; it is now a lien on the living second husband\u8217's \u8212- the {\i yavam}\u8217's own \u8212- estate. This converts the obligation from a constrained, non-renewable liability into a normal marital lien on a dynamic, renewable estate. The {\i yavam} is now free to sell his property as any husband would be, because his wife\u8217's lien extends to all of his current and future holdings rather than being confined to what his dead brother left behind.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Burial Obligation Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Buries Her\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through the intricacies of property rights, the Gemara raises what might seem like a separate question but is in fact deeply connected: {\i shomeret yavam shemeisa \u8212- mi kovra?} If the woman awaiting {\i yibum} dies, who is obligated to bury her? The question arises from the peculiar split of her estate. As we have seen, the {\i nichsei tzon barzel} \u8212- the dowry \u8212- goes to the husband\u8217's heirs, while the {\i nichsei melog} goes to her father\u8217's family. There are, in effect, two sets of heirs, each receiving a portion of her estate. Normally, the obligation to bury a woman falls on whoever receives her dowry, because the husband acquires the dowry precisely in exchange for taking on the obligation of burial. But here, with two distinct sets of heirs, the question is genuinely open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Ketubah Heirs B\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Amram resolves the matter by citing a {\i baraita}: {\i shomeret yavam shemeisah, yorshei ketuvatah chayavin bikevuratah} \u8212- when a {\i shomeret yavam} dies, the heirs of her {\i ketubah} \u8212- that is, the heirs of the husband who receive her {\i nedunya} \u8212- bear the obligation of burial. The {\i baraita} explicitly acknowledges that there may be two sets of heirs but identifies specifically that the obligation falls on those who receive the {\i nedunya}. It is the receipt of the dowry that generates the burial obligation, not the receipt of the {\i nichsei melog}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contrast: Normal Widow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye reinforces this by pointing to a familiar ruling about an ordinary widow: {\i almanah nizonis minichsei yesomim} \u8212- a widow is supported by the estate of the orphans, and {\i maasei yadeha shelahen} \u8212- her handiwork belongs to them, reflecting the standard exchange of support for labor that existed during the marriage. {\i Ve\u8217'ein chayavin bikevuratah} \u8212- yet the heirs are not obligated to bury her. Why not? Because in the normal case, the husband predeceased her. When the husband dies first, she extracts her {\i nedunya} from the estate. Having recovered her dowry, she no longer has that financial connection to the husband\u8217's family. When she subsequently dies, the heirs have not received her {\i nedunya} \u8212- she took it back \u8212- and therefore they bear no burial obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This contrast sharpens the principle: it is the receipt of the {\i nedunya} that generates the burial obligation. In the {\i shomeret yavam} case, the {\i nedunya} remains with the husband\u8217's heirs because the status was never resolved before she died. Accordingly, those heirs must bury her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam\u8217's Escape Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a striking objection: why cannot the {\i yavam} simply disclaim any relationship to the woman and avoid the burial obligation? His argument, as developed by Tosafot, would run roughly as follows: \u8220"I don\u8217't recognize this woman as anyone to me. I inherited my dead brother\u8217's estate \u8212- including the Ferrari he acquired twenty years ago \u8212- not because she has any claim on it, but simply because I am his heir. I am not a continuation of my brother\u8217's marital relationship. She is a stranger to me. Why must I bury her?\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Both Ways Argume\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's response is incisive: {\i mishum deba\u8217'u lo mishnei tzedadim} \u8212- because the argument works against him from both directions. {\i Im achiv yoresh yikvor ishto} \u8212- if you are an heir of your brother in the sense that you are stepping into his shoes as a continuation of the marriage, then you stand in the husband\u8217's position vis-\u224 ?-vis the wife, and you must bury her. {\i Im eino kover ishto yiten ketuvatah} \u8212- and if you are not a continuation of your brother and have no marital obligation toward her, then when your brother died she was entitled to extract her {\i nedunya} back from the estate, and you must return it to her. You cannot claim the dowry on the grounds that it is simply part of your brother\u8217's general estate while simultaneously denying that you bear any marital obligations toward her. Either the marital nexus continues \u8212- in which case you bury her \u8212- or it does not \u8212- in which case she takes back the {\i nedunya}. You cannot have it both ways.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i yavam}\u8217's attempted counter-argument is that even if he is not a continuation of the marriage, she was not yet entitled to collect because {\i lo nitna ketuvah ligvot mechayim} \u8212- the {\i ketubah} cannot be collected during the husband\u8217's lifetime, and since he, the {\i yavam}, is still alive, it is considered {\i mechayim}. But as the Gemara notes, the only reason he constitutes a {\i mechayim} factor is precisely because of his ongoing connection to the marital situation \u8212- which brings him right back to the original problem. He cannot use his own existence as the reason she cannot collect while simultaneously denying that his existence creates any obligation toward her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Midrash Ketubah Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midrash Ketubah Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a conceptual framework that clarifies the underlying structure of the dispute: {\i midrash ketubah}. This refers to a precise, almost hyper-literal reading of the {\i ketubah} document\u8217's language. The {\i ketubah} specifies when the wife becomes entitled to collect: upon her husband\u8217's death, or more precisely, when she is free to remarry. {\i Midrash ketubah} means that the conditions specified in the document are read with exactness, and the wife\u8217's right to collect is triggered only when those conditions are literally met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai: Prepared=C\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai holds that {\i shtar ha\u8217'omed ligvot ke-gavuy dami} \u8212- a document that is in a position to be collected is treated as though it has already been collected. This is a general principle of Beit Shammai: once the legal mechanism for collection is in place and the entitlement exists in principle, the practical question of whether collection has yet occurred is secondary. The document\u8217's existence and validity give the holder constructive possession of the underlying asset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Case Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara illustrates this with the case of the {\i sotah} \u8212- a woman suspected of adultery who is being brought to the Temple to undergo the verification procedure. If her husband dies before she drinks, Beit Shammai rules {\i notlot ketuvah ve-lo shotot} \u8212- she collects her {\i ketubah} and does not drink, because the husband is no longer alive to participate in the procedure. Beit Hillel disagrees: {\i o shotah o lo notelet ketuvatah} \u8212- either drink or forfeit the {\i ketubah}. The reasoning is that since the husband must be present for the procedure and he is gone, she cannot drink; and since she cannot drink, the doubt about her conduct remains unresolved, and {\i ha-motzi me-chavero alav hara\u8217'ayah} \u8212- the burden of proof falls on the claimant; since she cannot clear herself, the property remains with the husband\u8217's heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai, however, holds that since she has a {\i ketubah} document \u8212- a {\i shtar} \u8212- that entitles her to this property, and since it was effectively standing ready to be collected, it is considered as though she had already collected it. The doubt about her conduct cannot override what is, in Beit Shammai\u8217's analysis, already in her constructive possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam Like Other Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi draws the connection to the {\i shomeret yavam} explicitly: {\i yavam nami ke\u8217'acher dami} \u8212- the {\i yavam} is considered like {\i another} husband for purposes of this analysis. The condition in the {\i ketubah} \u8212- that she be free to remarry \u8212- is satisfied in a functional sense, because she is free to enter {\i yibum} with the {\i yavam}, and that is equivalent. Therefore, the {\i ketubah} is already in a position to be collected, and under Beit Shammai\u8217's principle of {\i shtar ha\u8217'omed ligvot ke-gavuy dami}, the property is treated as though it has already come into her possession. This, in turn, explains why the {\i yavam} cannot simply disclaim the marital relationship and retain the dowry: whichever approach one takes, the property is considered under her constructive control, and the {\i yavam}\u8217's possession of it is only because he is recognized as a continuation of the marital relationship \u8212- which obligates him in burial.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Solutions & Ketubah Coll\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Option: Appeasement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} cited in connection with Rava\u8217's inquiry to Abaye provides the practical resolution for a {\i yavam} who wishes to sell some of the dead brother\u8217's estate. The {\i baraita} is introduced by Rabbi Abba, who reports that he posed the question to Sumchus: {\i harotzeh sheyimkor benichsei achiv keitzad hu oseh} \u8212- one who wishes to sell his dead brother\u8217's estate, what can he do? If the {\i yavam} is a {\i kohen}, his options are severely limited. A {\i kohen} may not divorce his wife and remarry a divorc\u233 ?e, and since she would become a divorc\u233 ?e upon receiving a {\i get}, he cannot use the divorce-and-remarry strategy. His only available path is {\i ya\u8217'aseh seudah veyefayeis} \u8212- throw her a feast, lavish her with gifts and attention, and try to get her to voluntarily waive her rights to the lien on specific properties. It is an entirely consensual process; he can do nothing unilaterally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Israelite: Divorce-Remar\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a non-{\i kohen} Yisrael, the superior solution is available: {\i im Yisrael hu megaresh be-get le-hachzir} \u8212- if he is a Yisrael, he divorces her and then remarries her. As the Mishna explained, this accomplishes a critical transformation. According to Rashi, he need not even pay her {\i ketubah} upon the divorce; the mere act of divorcing and remarrying shifts the lien. Once he remarries her, her {\i ketubah} obligation is no longer a claim against the dead husband\u8217's finite estate; it becomes a claim against the living husband\u8217's \u8212- his own \u8212- estate. He is now free to sell the dead brother\u8217's property because it is no longer the exclusive pool from which her {\i ketubah} must be paid. The constraint lifts, and he regains ordinary marital freedom over his property holdings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Can Ketubah Be Collected\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's inquiry to Abaye, sent through Rav Shemaya bar Zeira, presses the conceptual question: {\i vechi nitan ketuvah ligvot mechayim} \u8212- can the {\i ketubah} actually be collected while the {\i yavam} is still alive? The question has practical force. If one says that she already has some degree of entitlement to collect \u8212- that her rights have been triggered in some sense by the death of the first husband \u8212- then why not allow the {\i yavam} to pre-designate certain property as corresponding to the {\i ketubah} obligation and free the rest? {\i Meyached lah she\u8217'er ketuvah ve-hashar yimkor} \u8212- designate enough property to cover the {\i ketubah} and sell everything else. If she is already in a position to collect, surely the {\i yavam} ought to be able to satisfy that claim now and then deal freely with the remainder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer points back to the Mishna itself: {\i lo yomar lah harei ketuvatekh munachah al hashulchan \u8212- kol nechasim achrayin liketuvatah}. The Mishna explicitly prohibits pre-designation. All of the property must remain responsible for the {\i ketubah}. The prohibition is not because she has no current entitlement \u8212- the whole preceding discussion suggests she does, in some sense \u8212- but because the structure of the {\i ketubah} lien is universal and indivisible by its very nature. The husband, whether the original or the {\i yavam} acting in his stead, cannot unilaterally carve out a portion of the estate and declare the rest free of encumbrance. The full estate remains answerable, and the full estate must remain so until the obligation is finally discharged \u8212- whether through {\i yibum} followed by a natural resolution, or through the divorce-and-remarry strategy that converts the lien\u8217's character entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 86\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sale of Debt & Mechilah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Debt & Ketubah Sa\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 86 continues the Gemara\u8217's extended treatment of a husband\u8217's rights to his wife\u8217's property \u8212- and, in particular, the legal complications that arise when a wife holds debts owed to her by third parties. Can she perform {\i mechilah}, waiving those debts? And what happens when a woman sells her {\i ketubah} to a third party, subsequently dies, and her daughter seeks to waive that same debt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These questions expose a fundamental tension in {\i halacha} regarding the transfer of abstract obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abstract Debt Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem is this: if Reuven sells to Charlie the debt that Michael owes him, it is not the case that Michael now directly owes Charlie anything. A debt is an abstract thing \u8212- how does one transfer ownership of an abstraction? Tosafot raises exactly this difficulty. One possible answer is that where there are liens on land, the transfer is coherent, because liens represent a kind of partial ownership of a physical asset that can be conveyed. But what about a debt with no land attached, no lien at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The honest answer is that in {\i halacha}, it is exceedingly difficult to transfer a debt in a fully direct sense. What actually happens is closer to this: Michael still owes the money to Reuven; what Charlie has acquired is the exclusive right to collect and pocket those funds. The money is still, in a meaningful sense, owed to Reuven \u8212- Charlie merely has a kind of automatic-transfer arrangement, as though Reuven\u8217's account has been linked to Charlie\u8217's. One might contrast this with secular law, which handles non-physical objects \u8212- intellectual property, corporate shares, assigned receivables \u8212- with considerably greater conceptual ease. {\i Halacha} demands something more concrete, and it is precisely this concreteness that makes pure debt transfer so difficult to execute cleanly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is striking: since the money is still fundamentally owed to Reuven, Reuven retains the legal capacity to perform {\i mechilah} \u8212- to waive Michael\u8217's debt entirely. The {\i Gemara} states this explicitly: {\i HaMocher Shtar Chovo LeChavero VeChazar UMichalo, Machul} \u8212- if the seller subsequently forgives the debt, the forgiveness is effective. {\i VeAfiliu Yoresh} \u8212- even the heir of the seller can waive the debt before Charlie has had the opportunity to collect. This follows from the principle that an heir is not a transferee of an object but rather a continuation of the person himself; the money is still owed to that person\u8217's estate, and the heir steps into that identity rather than receiving a transferred asset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This arrangement is, as one would readily notice, a gaping loophole. No rational actor in a functioning economy would purchase a debt that the seller could annul at any moment. The {\i Gemara} is about to turn to several attempts to close this gap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Waiver Post-Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites Shmuel\u8217's ruling directly: {\i HaMocher Shtar Chov LeChaveiro VeChazar UMichalo, Machul} \u8212- it is waived \u8212- {\i VeAfiliu Yoresh Mochal} \u8212- and even the heir can waive it. This is Shmuel\u8217's {\i chiddush}: counterintuitive as it may seem, even after the sale, the original obligee or his heir retains the power of {\i mechilah}. The {\i Gemara} now proceeds to address the obvious commercial problem this creates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Workaround\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua offers a practical workaround. If Charlie wants to protect his investment, he should go to Michael, {\i mekarkesh lei zuzei} \u8212- jingle some coins in front of him \u8212- and induce Michael to write a new debt document directly in Charlie\u8217's name. The mechanics are straightforward: Charlie approaches Michael and proposes to tear up the old debt document \u8212- which reflects the thousand dollars Michael owes Reuven and which Charlie purchased \u8212- and in exchange for some cash payment, say one hundred dollars, Michael agrees to write a fresh {\i shtar} directly obligating himself to Charlie for nine hundred dollars. Charlie spends the hundred dollars but he has now cut Reuven out of the picture entirely. Michael\u8217's new debt runs straight to Charlie, not through Reuven\u8217's estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This solution, however, is imperfect in several respects. Charlie must spend money to execute it. The old liens attached to the original debt are extinguished \u8212- the new {\i shtar} is dated from now and carries no prior lien priority. And crucially, the arrangement requires Michael to be somewhat unaware that he could achieve a better outcome by approaching Reuven directly, offering Reuven, say, five hundred dollars in exchange for full {\i mechilah} of the thousand, cutting Charlie out entirely. Charlie must act before Michael realizes his leverage. One might also ask: what if Michael simply pays Reuven directly, since the debt is still technically owed to him? The answer is that while the {\i mechilah} would be effective, a direct payment to Reuven by Michael would not discharge Michael\u8217's obligation to Charlie \u8212- the rights to collect and pocket have been conveyed, and Michael cannot unilaterally reroute them. That asymmetry is, as noted, a peculiar feature of this mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dinei d\u8217'Garmi Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second, more systematic attempt to close the loophole comes from Meimar. {\i Amar Meimar: man d\u8217'dan dinei d\u8217'garmi magbi bei d\u8217'mei shetar mealya} \u8212- Meimar says: the one who adjudicates the laws of {\i garmi} can collect from the offender the full value of the debt document. That is to say: if Reuven performs {\i mechilah} on Michael\u8217's debt after having sold the {\i shtar} to Charlie, causing Charlie a direct financial loss, then under the doctrine of {\i dinei d\u8217'garmi}, Charlie can sue Reuven for the full value of what was waived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Causation Continuum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is {\i garmi}? It is related conceptually to the word {\i grama} \u8212- indirect causation \u8212- but the addition of the {\i yud} functions as an intensifier, denoting a higher degree of directness. The broader framework of tort liability in {\i halacha} requires, at minimum, both a direct physical act ({\i ma\u8217'aseh mazik}) and actual damage to a physical object. Two separate problems arise in borderline cases: the causation may be indirect ({\i grama}), or the damaged \u8220"thing\u8221" may not be a physical object at all \u8212- as when someone tears up a debt document, destroying not a valuable physical item but an abstract legal entitlement. In the case of the forged or waived {\i shtar}, both concerns apply: Reuven\u8217's act of {\i mechilah} is not a physical act against Charlie\u8217's property, and what Charlie lost was not a tangible object but an abstract right to collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Logically, both {\i grama} and the destruction of non-physical value should be exempt from {\i mazik} liability. But the Rabbis recognized a substantial zone of practical injustice: there are acts where the causation is so proximate and the loss so definite that exempting the actor entirely would be untenable. They therefore identified a portion of the causation continuum \u8212- those cases most closely resembling direct {\i mazik} \u8212- and designated them {\i garmi}, applying liability even though the formal requirements of classical tort are not met. There is a debate whether this is {\i d\u8217'oraissa} or {\i derabbanan}, but proceeding on the assumption that it is rabbinic, the principle is that cases falling into this category \u8212- high causal proximity, definite financial loss, even without physical damage to an object \u8212- are actionable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a further question: how much does Reuven owe Charlie if {\i dinei d\u8217'garmi} applies? Charlie purchased the debt for, say, seven hundred dollars on a thousand-dollar obligation. The phrase {\i d\u8217'mei shetar mealya} \u8212- full value of the debt document \u8212- suggests Charlie recovers the full thousand dollars. If so, the system is genuinely protected: whether Charlie collects from Michael or whether Reuven performs {\i mechilah} and Charlie sues Reuven, Charlie is made whole for a thousand dollars. That makes purchasing the debt commercially rational. If Charlie could only recover his purchase price of seven hundred dollars, the protection would be weaker \u8212- he would still have to absorb litigation costs and uncertainty to recover what he paid, and the market for debt sales would function poorly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rafram v. Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a concrete application of this principle: {\i Hava uvda ve-khafyei Rafram le-Rav Ashi ve-agbi be-khitsurei de-tzalmei} \u8212- there was an actual case, and Rafram compelled Rav Ashi to pay, like full-value wooden planks \u8212- an idiom meaning he made him pay in full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two interpretations appear in Rashi, one here and one in {\i Bava Kamma} where the same incident is recorded in parallel. The Rashi here understands that Rav Ashi was inclined to let a defendant go free and Rafram persuaded him to adjudicate the case as {\i dinei d\u8217'garmi}, ruling that the party had caused real, compensable damage rather than mere {\i grama}. The Rashi in {\i Bava Kamma} takes a more dramatic approach, suggesting that Rav Ashi himself, as a child, once destroyed someone\u8217's debt documents ({\i shtaros}), and Rafram \u8212- when Rav Ashi had grown \u8212- compelled him to pay for it under {\i dinei d\u8217'garmi}. This second interpretation opens a fascinating subsidiary question about a minor\u8217's culpability for damages: even though minors are generally exempt from monetary liability, there may be some form of {\i dinei shamayim} obligation that follows them into adulthood \u8212- a full discussion that belongs to {\i Bava Kamma}. In any event, the operative conclusion is that {\i dinei d\u8217'garmi} is the accepted doctrine and constitutes a genuine mechanism for closing the {\i mechilah} loophole.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Creditor vs.\u160 ?Wife Priori\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment Distribution Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this digression into {\i Bava Kamma}-territory behind us, the {\i Gemara} returns to its main track. Meimar cites a ruling in the name of Rav Chama: {\i Hai man de-ika lei kesuvas isha ve-baal chov} \u8212- if someone has against him both his ex-wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} claim and a creditor\u8217's debt \u8212- {\i ve-is lei ara ve-is lei zuzei} \u8212- and he possesses both land and cash \u8212- then {\i le-baal chov mesalkinan lei be-zuzei, le-isha mesalkinan lah be-ara} \u8212- to the creditor we pay cash, and to the wife we give land. {\i Hai ke-dinei ve-hai ke-dinei} \u8212- each according to her own legal category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Obligation Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is as follows: a creditor lent cash, and is therefore entitled to receive cash in return. The {\i ketubah}, by contrast, is not grounded in a cash transaction; it begins as an abstract matrimonial obligation with liens on land, and that is how it is fundamentally conceptualized. Rashi notes that even though part of the {\i ketubah} includes the {\i nedunya} \u8212- the dowry, which may well have been cash or moveable objects \u8212- this is bundled into the general framework, and the {\i ketubah} as a whole is understood as a land-based lien.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, building on Rabbeinu Tam, elaborates a three-way taxonomy. For the {\i malveh} \u8212- the lender \u8212- if cash is on hand, the debtor cannot pay him with land; you borrowed money, you repay money, as the {\i Gemara} rules here. For the {\i nizak} \u8212- one who suffered property damage \u8212- even if cash is available, the tortfeasor may satisfy the debt with land. This is counterintuitive: the innocent injured party receives something less liquid than cash, with no apparent benefit to him. Yet that is the rule. For the {\i sechir} \u8212- the hired laborer \u8212- even if the employer has no cash at hand, he must go ahead, liquidate assets, and produce cash to pay wages: {\i zil terach ve-zavin ve-aisi zuzei}. Three scenarios, three different obligations \u8212- the first requiring cash only if available, the second permitting land even when cash is available, the third requiring the party to actively acquire cash even when none is on hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Asset Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next complication arises when there is only a single plot of land. {\i Ve-i lo ika ella chada ara ve-lo chazya ella le-chad} \u8212- if the land is sufficient to pay only one creditor \u8212- {\i le-baal chov yahavinan lei, le-isha lo yahavinan lah} \u8212- we give it to the creditor and not to the ex-wife. This holds even if, as one might expect, the wife\u8217's lien is the older one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Policy Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai taama?} \u8212- what is the reason? {\i Yoseir mi-mah she-ish rotzeh lisa, isha rotzah le-hinasei} \u8212- women are more eager to marry than men. This is not a legal analysis in the narrow sense; it is a frank policy determination, as Tosafot explains. The rule cannot simply track lien priority, because the policy consequences of doing so would be intolerable for the credit market. People remain married for many years \u8212- often the wife\u8217's lien will predate any creditor\u8217's. If we consistently gave the single asset to the earlier lienor, which would frequently be the wife, creditors would have strong reason to hesitate before extending loans. The alternative \u8212- giving the land to the creditor \u8212- might give women some pause before marrying, but Tosafot\u8217's conclusion is that women will marry regardless. The more pressing systemic concern is {\i ne\u8217'ilat delet} \u8212- the closing of the door to credit. Someone must be the disadvantaged party in this zero-sum scenario; the policy judgment is that the risk of chilling the credit market outweighs the risk of deterring marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot also notes the apparent tension with an earlier {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i Lakushot shebahim}, where the ruling seems to favor the woman over the creditor \u8212- and works to distinguish the circumstances. The concern there is for the woman\u8217's welfare where a creditor has alternative recourse and the woman may not. Here, where there is a single asset and the question is purely one of priority, the credit-system rationale governs.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Payment as Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Land Sale Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa addresses a question posed in the name of Rav Chama: if someone owes money and has land, and the creditor comes to collect, and the debtor says \u8220"go ahead and take my land\u8221" \u8212- {\i amrinan lei lo}, we tell him no. He cannot simply hand over the land; he must sell it and give cash. This seemed to establish a rule that even when the debtor has only land, he must liquidate it and pay money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidden Cash Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rav Chama clarifies: that ruling applies only to the specific case where the debtor had cash on hand but artificially removed it from his own possession \u8212- {\i tola maosei be-oved kochavim hava} \u8212- he transferred or entrusted his money to a non-Jew so that when the creditor arrived he could claim he had no cash. {\i Hu asa shelo k\u8217'hogan lifichach asu vo shelo k\u8217'hogen} \u8212- because he acted improperly toward the creditor, the court treats him improperly in return, compelling him to sell the land and produce cash. But if the debtor genuinely has no cash and has not played any such games, he may pay the creditor with land. The forced-liquidation rule, in other words, is not a general principle but a punitive response to bad-faith evasion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvah Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa further teaches: {\i prias ba\u8217'al chov mitzvah} \u8212- paying off a creditor is a {\i mitzvah}. This is not merely a matter of monetary rights and liens; it carries the additional dimension of a positive religious obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Heirs Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} from this characterization is significant: if a debtor dies leaving minor heirs who are now responsible for the estate, can the creditor collect from the estate while the children are under age? Under a purely monetary-rights framework, the creditor holds a {\i shtar mekuyam}, his lien is established, and one would expect him to be able to exercise it against the estate regardless of the heirs\u8217' ages. But if the mechanism of collection depends in part on the obligor\u8217's being commanded in the {\i mitzvah} of repayment \u8212- if that religious dimension is part of what enables compelled collection \u8212- then minors, who are not yet obligated in {\i mitzvot}, cannot be compelled. Even with valid liens, the creditor must wait until the heirs reach majority before collecting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refusal Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana presses Rav Pappa: according to your position that paying is a {\i mitzvah}, what if an adult debtor simply says he does not want to perform the {\i mitzvah}? Does the entire enforcement mechanism collapse?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Compulsion Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa replies: {\i tanina} \u8212- we have learned this. The rule is that lashes apply to transgressions of negative commandments. But for positive commandments \u8212- {\i aseh sukkah ve-eini oseh, lulav ve-eini oseh} \u8212- if someone refuses to perform a positive obligation while still able to, {\i makin oto ad she-tetze nafsho} \u8212- one may beat him to the very verge of death. The court may use physical compulsion to enforce the performance of {\i mitzvot}. The refusal of a debtor to pay is therefore no escape; he is subject to {\i kfiyah datit} \u8212- coercive religious enforcement \u8212- precisely because repayment is a {\i mitzvah} and courts may compel its performance. The one exception remains minors, who are not yet bound by the {\i mitzvah} and therefore cannot be compelled under this mechanism.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get After 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Placement Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now pivots in a seemingly abrupt direction, though the conceptual thread \u8212- control, {\i kinyan}, and deferred effect \u8212- connects directly to earlier discussions in the {\i mishnah}. Rami bar Chama poses a question to Rav Chisda: a man says to his wife, {\i Harei zeh gittech} \u8212- here is your {\i get} \u8212- {\i velo tigarshi bo ela achar sheloshim yom} \u8212- but you may not be divorced through it until after thirty days. She takes the document and places it {\i betzidei reshut harabbim} \u8212- in the side areas of the public thoroughfare. Does the {\i get} take effect after thirty days?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question turns on what degree of control over the {\i get} is required at the moment it takes effect. For a {\i get} to be valid when its effect is deferred, there must be some residual sense of possession or control at the time of activation. Is placement in the {\i tzidei reshut harabbim} \u8212- the sidewalk-like margins of the public domain \u8212- sufficient to establish that control?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav & Shmuel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers first from Rav and Shmuel, who both rule: {\i einah megureshet} \u8212- she is not divorced. Their basis is an earlier ruling about a debtor\u8217's cattle found in the {\i reshut harabbim} at the time of his death: the Gemara there established that property in the public domain is not under the owner\u8217's control \u8212- that was precisely the condition enabling creditors or the wife to seize it. Since {\i reshut harabbim} is defined as space outside one\u8217's control, the {\i get} placed there is not under the wife\u8217's control, and therefore the {\i kinyan} cannot take effect. Rav and Shmuel apparently treat {\i tzidei reshut harabbim} as equivalent to {\i reshut harabbim} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this, the {\i Gemara} proposes Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling in the name of Rabba bar Avuha as a counter-source: {\i meshokh parah zu ve-lo tehei kenuyah lekha ad achar sheloshim yom, kanah} \u8212- draw this cow and you will own it only after thirty days \u8212- the acquisition works, {\i va-afilu omedet ba-agam} \u8212- even if after thirty days the cow is standing in a swamp. A swamp is like an alleyway ({\i simta}): not private property, but not a public thoroughfare either. Being in such a semi-private space is sufficient to count as being under the buyer\u8217's control for the {\i kinyan} to take effect later. Might not {\i tzidei reshut harabbim} \u8212- the side areas of the public domain \u8212- be analogous to a swamp?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Swamp-Sides Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rejects the analogy: {\i lo, agam le-chud ve-tzidei reshut harabbim le-chud} \u8212- the swamp is one category, the sides of the public domain are another. The {\i tzidei reshut harabbim} are so proximate to the thoroughfare that they are treated as part of it, as outside personal control. A swamp, by contrast, is sufficiently removed from public traffic to remain under the individual\u8217's domain. The underlying principle is clear: for a deferred {\i kinyan} to take effect, the object must be under the recipient\u8217's control at the moment of activation. Alleyways and swamps qualify; the margins of the public thoroughfare do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presents the two versions of this dispute in reverse order: one version begins from Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling and uses Rav and Shmuel to rebut it; the other begins from Rav and Shmuel and uses Rav Nachman as the challenge. In each version, the same resolution applies: the swamp and the {\i tzidei reshut harabbim} are distinct categories. Tosafot adds the important clarification that this entire discussion assumes the husband said {\i me-hayom} \u8212- from today \u8212- since without that formulation a deferred {\i get} of this type would not function at all. This case thus draws together several threads from the earlier {\i mishnah} \u8212- the {\i kinyan le-achar zman}, the significance of {\i reshut harabbim}, and the condition of ongoing control \u8212- and resolves them through the combined weight of Rav, Shmuel, and Rav Nachman.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife\u8217's Oath Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Storekeeper Oath Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} moves now to a new {\i mishnah}. {\i Ha-moshiv es ishto chanvanis} \u8212- if a man set his wife up as a storekeeper \u8212- {\i o she-minah apitropya} \u8212- or appointed her as an {\i apitropya}, a guardian or agent with power of attorney over his estate \u8212- {\i harei zeh mashbia kol zman she-yirtze} \u8212- he may make her take an oath whenever he wishes. The mechanism is not an audit or an accounting but a sworn declaration that she has not skimmed from the business, not pocketed money. This oath-compulsion right follows from the ordinary rules applicable to an employer and employee.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Expansion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer goes further: {\i afillu al pilchah ve-al issatah} \u8212- even regarding her spindle and her dough. That is, even without any formal commercial arrangement, even when the wife is engaged only in ordinary domestic tasks \u8212- spinning wool, preparing bread \u8212- the husband may still compel an oath. His reasoning: there will always be some moment in the course of domestic life when the wife is handling the husband\u8217's property, {\i she-ein lecha isha she-lo na\u8217'asit apitropya sha\u8217'a achat be-chayei ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- there is no woman who has not served at some point as a de facto steward of her husband\u8217's property. Since she is effectively always in some {\i apitropya} relationship, the oath right always applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Snake Proverb\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages respond: {\i ein adam dar im nachash bi-kfifa} \u8212- a person cannot live under the same roof with a snake. Allowing the husband to demand an oath at any time, over the grocery money or the dough, creates an intolerable atmosphere of suspicion. The {\i Gemara} works through the logical implications: if Rabbi Eliezer means the husband may do this entirely {\i le-chatchila} \u8212- that is, without any pre-existing oath context \u8212- then the Sages\u8217' response is apt and forceful. If he means it only as {\i gilgul} \u8212- the bundling doctrine \u8212- then one might ask why the wife should care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gilgul Bundling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: she cares even about the {\i gilgul} case. {\i Gilgul} means that once a party is already required to take an oath about one matter, additional obligations may be packaged into that same oath. Even this limited application of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position is psychologically devastating: {\i keivan de-daikat batrai kulei hai} \u8212- you are so exacting with me \u8212- {\i lo matzina le-meidar bahadach} \u8212- I cannot continue to live with you. It is one thing to be subject to oath-compulsion in the context of running your million-dollar business. It is another to have the same distrust extended to the few coins spent on groceries. The {\i ta shema} from the {\i baraita} confirms that Rabbi Eliezer does indeed mean the husband may act {\i le-chatchila}, and {\i shema mina} \u8212- we learn accordingly \u8212- that this is his explicit position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath Waiver Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The new {\i mishnah} introduces the mechanism by which the husband may waive his oath rights entirely. {\i Katav lah neder u-shvu\u8217'a ein li alayich} \u8212- if he writes to her that he has no right to impose a {\i neder} or {\i shvu\u8217'a} upon her \u8212- {\i eino yachol le-hashbia} \u8212- he can no longer compel her to swear. A {\i neder} would take the form: \u8220"If I stole anything from you, let the fruits of the world be forbidden to me.\u8221" A {\i shvu\u8217'a} is a direct oath: \u8220"I swear I did not steal from you.\u8221" By writing this waiver \u8212- whether in the {\i ketubah} or as a separate document \u8212- he relinquishes the right to demand either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heir Limitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the waiver is limited to what was in his power to waive. {\i Aval mashbia hu et yoresheha ve-et ha-ba\u8217'im bi-rshuta} \u8212- but he may still compel her heirs and those who come through her authority to take an oath. When the heirs come to collect the {\i ketubah}, for instance, he may make them swear they have not already been paid. The exemption he granted runs only to her personally, not automatically to all who derive rights through her. This closely parallels the earlier {\i mishnah}\u8217's discussion of {\i mechilah}: just as a seller cannot fully transfer a debt because the obligation runs to him and not to the buyer, so too a waiver of oath rights is interpreted narrowly unless its language is explicitly expansive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Language Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} establishes that the narrowest interpretation governs by default. A general waiver of the husband\u8217's own oath rights does not, without explicit language, extend to his heirs or to those to whom the wife sells her {\i ketubah}. The language must be spelled out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Waiver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To foreclose all avenues, the husband must state: {\i neder u-shvu\u8217'a ein li alayich ve-al yoreshaich ve-al ha-ba\u8217'im bi-rshutaich} \u8212- I have no oath right over you, your heirs, or those who come through your authority \u8212- and further, {\i ve-lo ani ve-lo yorshai ve-chol ha-ba\u8217'im bi-rshuti} \u8212- neither I, nor my heirs, nor anyone acting through my rights. {\i Eino yachol le-hashbia} \u8212- in that case, none of these parties can compel an oath \u8212- {\i lo hu ve-lo yorshav ve-lo ha-ba\u8217'im bi-rshuto} \u8212- not from her, her heirs, or those who come through her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Mortem Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What happens after the husband\u8217's death? {\i Halcha mikever ba\u8217'alah le-beit aviha} \u8212- she went from her husband\u8217's grave back to her father\u8217's house \u8212- or she returned to live in the home of her father-in-law \u8212- {\i ve-lo na\u8217'asit apitropya} \u8212- and no new stewardship arrangement was established. In that case, {\i ein ha-yorshim mashbi\u8217'in otah} \u8212- the heirs cannot compel her to take an oath \u8212- because Reuven, the husband, had waived all oath rights in his lifetime, and her current role involves nothing beyond what was covered by that prior waiver.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Stewardship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i ve-im na\u8217'asit apitropya} \u8212- if after her husband\u8217's death the heirs actively appoint her to continue managing the estate \u8212- {\i ha-yorshim mashbi\u8217'in otah al ha-atid lavo, ve-ein mashbi\u8217'in otah al ma she-avar} \u8212- the heirs may make her swear regarding future dealings but not past ones. The new appointment creates a fresh legal relationship with fresh oath rights; it is entirely unaffected by the deceased husband\u8217's prior waiver, which pertained only to obligations arising from her relationship with him. What he waived was his own authority and that of those acting through him \u8212- once that relationship ends and a new independent arrangement begins, the waiver does not reach it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The general principle running through all of these cases is consistent: a person may waive only what is within his authority to waive. Additional parties, additional relationships, and future obligations require explicit language and cannot be assumed from a general grant. Absent such explicit language, the most restrictive interpretation prevails.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 87\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oaths & Husbands\u8217' Author\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Review of Ketubot 86b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf, 87, opens as a golden continuation of the previous discussion. We pick up with the {\i gemara} at the very last line of 86b. The preceding material dealt with the idea that a husband whose wife manages his store may require her to take an oath that she has not misappropriated any funds. The {\i gemara} then turned to the next {\i mishnah}, which states that a husband may write for his wife \u8212- presumably at the time of their marriage \u8212- an exemption relieving her from ever having to take a {\i neder} or a {\i shevuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exemption from Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} thus contemplates a situation in which the husband anticipates future disputes and, at the outset of their marriage, formally waives any right he or his heirs might have to impose an oath upon her. This exemption covers the wife herself and, depending on the language employed, may extend to her heirs and his heirs as well \u8212- a question the {\i mishnah} explores in some detail. The discussion on 86b left open the precise meaning and scope of this exemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder vs.\u160 ?Shevu\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To clarify the terminology at stake: a {\i shevuah} is a direct oath \u8212- \u8220"I did not steal any money.\u8221" A {\i neder} operates differently; it is a vow of the form \u8220"If I misappropriated anything, let all the fruit in the world be forbidden to me.\u8221" These are two distinct mechanisms by which a person might be bound to affirm the truthfulness of an account, and the {\i mishnah}\u8217's exemption addresses both.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yehuda vs.\u160 ?Rav Nachm\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apotropaya Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with the question: {\i shevuah mai avidatei} \u8212- what type of {\i shevuah} are we talking about, and in what context would the husband be exempting his wife from it? Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav answers: {\i al apitropaya shenasit bechayei ba\u8217'ala} \u8212- the exemption refers to the wife\u8217's role as administrator ({\i apitropaya}) of the husband\u8217's business during his lifetime. In this reading, the husband, anticipating that his wife will manage his affairs, preemptively exempts her from any oath she might otherwise be required to take as a business manager.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pogemet Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman in the name of Rabba bar Avuha disagrees. He holds that the {\i mishnah} is not referencing the previous {\i mishnah} about the storekeeper but rather the following one, which begins: {\i hapogemes kesuvasah lo tipara ela bishvua} \u8212- a woman who \u8220"injures\u8221" her {\i ketubah} may not collect except by oath. What does {\i pogemes} mean? The word {\i pogem} means to cut into or injure. The case is one where the woman comes to collect her {\i ketubah} and the husband claims he has already paid it. She, however, need not admit anything \u8212- she holds the {\i shetar}. Yet if she herself concedes that part of the {\i ketubah} has been paid \u8212- say, a hundred out of a thousand \u8212- she has voluntarily reduced her own claim. Once she makes such a concession, a {\i shevuah} is required that the remainder is still outstanding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: doesn\u8217't she have a {\i migo} \u8212- an argument from the stronger position she could have taken? Had she said nothing, she would have been entitled to collect the full amount. The fact that she conceded more than she needed to might itself lend credibility to her claim. This question is noted here but deferred; it will be addressed as the {\i mishnah} is analyzed further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rav Nachman, then, what the husband writes in the {\i ketubah} is a preemptive waiver of this {\i shevuah} obligation \u8212- the obligation that arises precisely from the wife\u8217's own partial concession. He anticipates that at some point during the marriage she might need to draw on the {\i ketubah} as an advance, and he wishes to spare her the burden of a {\i shevuah} when the time comes to collect. As Rashi notes, the stakes of this debate are halachic: if the husband\u8217's waiver applies to the {\i apitropaya} case but not the {\i pogemet} case, then a woman relying on such language to avoid the {\i shevuah} of {\i pogemet} might find herself mistaken. The {\i shevuah} of {\i pogemet} is triggered not by the husband\u8217's demand but by the woman\u8217's own concession and, in a sense, by the court \u8212- which may mean it falls outside the scope of what the husband\u8217's waiver can reach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mordechai brought this debate before Rav Ashi. The question he laid before him was: given these two positions, which of them correctly explains the {\i mishnah}, and are they truly in debate with one another about the opening of the {\i mishnah}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Resolving the Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Apotropaya?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi responded with a striking reframing: {\i atun haha masnisula} \u8212- \u8220"you apply this statement of Rav Yehuda to the {\i mishnah}\u8221" \u8212- and then are puzzled that it yields an unusual scenario. The {\i gemara} indeed presses this point: {\i ela man d\u8217'amar apitropsha shenasis bechayei ba\u8217'ala, ee hava yad\u8217'a demoseiv apitropsha} \u8212- if the husband made his wife an administrator, why would it have occurred to her, at the time of the marriage, to preemptively request that he write an exemption from an oath arising out of business management? That is not the typical scenario one contemplates when drafting a {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot attempts a defense of this reading, suggesting that perhaps the woman was already serving as the husband\u8217's administrator before the marriage \u8212- they met through business, or he knew he would want her to continue in that role. There are indeed plausible scenarios: a husband who plans to travel and expects his wife to run the store, or one who had employed her previously. Nevertheless, the {\i gemara} concludes that this is not the {\i pshat} \u8212- the plain, standard reading of the {\i mishnah}. It is not impossible, but it is not the ordinary case that a {\i mishnah} would be expected to address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pogemet as Plain Sense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi therefore establishes that Rav Nachman\u8217's position \u8212- that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's opening refers to {\i pogemet ketubah} \u8212- represents the plain sense, and in fact reflects a consensus. Everyone agrees that the husband\u8217's waiver, written at the time of the {\i ketubah}, anticipates the future scenario of {\i pogemet}: the wife may one day take an advance on her {\i ketubah} and, when she later comes to collect, will concede that part has been paid. The husband, wishing to spare her the burden of a {\i shevuah} even in that circumstance \u8212- even though it is triggered by her own concession \u8212- writes the exemption in advance. This is the {\i reisha} of the {\i mishnah}, and Rav Yehuda\u8217's statement about {\i apitropaya} was never intended to address it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apotropaya in Seifa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, Rav Ashi explains, Rav Yehuda\u8217's statement belongs to the {\i seifa} \u8212- the end of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- not the beginning. The {\i seifa} of the {\i mishnah} reads: {\i halcha mikever ba\u8217'ala leveit aviha o shechazra leveit chamia} \u8212- she went from burying her husband to her father\u8217's house or returned to her father-in-law\u8217's house. If she was not appointed as administrator after his death, {\i ein hayorshin mashbi\u8217'in osah} \u8212- the heirs cannot make her swear regarding past business dealings, because the husband had already exempted her. But {\i ve\u8217'im nasis apitropsha} \u8212- if she does continue as administrator now for the heirs \u8212- {\i yoresh mashbi\u8217'in osah al ha\u8217'atid lavo} \u8212- they can make her swear regarding future dealings, {\i aval ein mashbi\u8217'in osah al she\u8217'avar} \u8212- but not regarding what is past.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is on this clause \u8212- \u8220"what is past\u8221" \u8212- that Rav Yehuda\u8217's statement is addressed. His formulation, {\i al apitropaya shenasit bechayei habaal}, underscores that the heirs may not impose a {\i shevuah} for any business conducted when the husband was alive. That much the {\i seifa} makes clear. What remains to be asked is: what about the brief period between death and burial?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is, in itself, a remarkable moment. Rav Ashi is explaining to Rav Mordechai that two statements which appeared to be competing explanations for the {\i mishnah}\u8217's opening were, in fact, never in dialogue with one another. Rav Nachman\u8217's statement belongs to the {\i reisha}, Rav Yehuda\u8217's to the {\i seifa}, and each addresses an entirely different question. The fact that they were juxtaposed and taken as competing readings reflects an understandable confusion about the context of each ruling \u8212- and Rav Ashi\u8217's correction illustrates how the original context of a statement could be obscured over time.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Between Death & Burial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah on Heirs\u8217' Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} in its {\i seifa} draws a clear line: past activities (those conducted during the husband\u8217's lifetime) are exempt from any oath the heirs might seek to impose, while future activities (those conducted by her for the heirs after his death) are subject to such an oath. But the precise boundary of \u8220"the past\u8221" is what now occupies the {\i gemara}: what about the period between death and burial?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav states: the exemption from the heirs\u8217' oath covers all business dealings conducted {\i bechayei habaal} \u8212- when the husband was alive. The husband who exempted her shielded her completely from any oath regarding the decade or more she may have managed his affairs. The heirs cannot say that they know she was cheating for years and demand she swear otherwise. The exemption stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Between Mita-Kevura\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rav Yehuda then adds: {\i aval bein mita likvura mashpinan lah} \u8212- between death and burial, they can impose an oath on her. The reasoning is that once the husband has died, the estate has technically passed to the heirs. If she sells property or handles funds during the period of {\i aninus} \u8212- the state between death and burial \u8212- she is now, in legal terms, handling the heirs\u8217' assets, not her husband\u8217's. That period constitutes {\i atid lavo} relative to the heirs\u8217' estate, and they may therefore demand accountability for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario is not as strange as it might seem. Consider: the husband has been running a business, or she has been running it for him, for many years. He dies. Before burial, there may be urgent financial needs \u8212- the burial itself must be funded, the household must be provisioned, and so on. She continues managing the affairs. The {\i gemara} acknowledges that technically this is now the heirs\u8217' business, and the heirs can therefore make her swear regarding the propriety of those transactions. That is Rav Yehuda\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Matna\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Matna disagrees: {\i afilu bein mita likvura lo mashpinan lah} \u8212- even between death and burial, no oath may be imposed on her. Despite the technical legal analysis that assigns those dealings to the heirs\u8217' estate, the practical reality demands protection for the woman managing affairs under extremely difficult circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Urgent Sales Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To support this, the {\i gemara} cites the teaching of the scholars of Nehardea: {\i lekarga u\u8217'lemezonei u\u8217'lekvura, mazbininan belo achrazta} \u8212- for the payment of a poll tax, for the provision of food, or for burial, one may sell property from an estate without going through the normal process of public announcement. Ordinarily, when property of an estate must be sold to pay debts, a formal announcement must be made so that the market is informed and a fair price may be obtained. But in these three pressing cases \u8212- government tax obligations that carry serious consequences if delayed, the immediate food needs of the widow or heirs, and the necessity of burying the deceased without delay \u8212- we dispense with the announcement process and sell immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevance to our question is that this doctrine establishes a recognized category of urgent, potentially imperfect transactions made under the pressure of death. The normal standards of careful stewardship cannot be applied to decisions made in this period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the connection as follows: the woman managing the estate in the brief window between death and burial may have been compelled to sell assets quickly and perhaps not at the best terms in order to raise funds for the burial. It would be unjust to then subject her to an oath in which she must account for every decision made under that pressure. The {\i halakha} accepts that such decisions will inevitably be somewhat imperfect, and therefore declines to impose an oath for the dealings of that period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, however, takes a different approach. One might ask: even granting Rashi\u8217's point, why can\u8217't the heirs at least make her swear that she did not personally pocket any money \u8212- that she did not steal \u8212- even if they cannot demand she justify every business decision made under duress? Tosafot answers that the real concern is entirely practical. The woman has been willing to manage the business and the estate throughout precisely because she has not been subject to oath obligations. If the heirs tell her that any dealings she conducts during this period will be subject to a {\i shevuah}, she will simply refuse. She will say: I am not handling the burial arrangements and the estate under those conditions. You manage it yourselves. The heirs, suddenly confronted with an estate they have never administered and with the urgent demands of burial bearing down on them, will be left stranded. To prevent this outcome, the {\i halakha} grants her a blanket exemption even from a {\i shevuah} about personal misconduct during this period \u8212- because threatening her with any oath at all creates an unacceptable risk that she will abandon her post at the worst possible moment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Language of Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dela vs.\u160 ?Naki\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a separate question: what happens when the husband, rather than using the precise language of the {\i mishnah} ({\i neder ushevuah ein li alaich} \u8212- \u8220"I have no {\i neder} or {\i shevuah} against you\u8221"), uses more passive formulations? The {\i mishnah} explored whether the husband\u8217's language included his heirs and hers. Here the discussion concerns two alternative phrasings: {\i dela neder, dela shevuah} \u8212- \u8220"without a {\i neder}, without a {\i shevuah}\u8221" \u8212- and {\i naki neder, naki shevuah} \u8212- \u8220"clean from a {\i neder}, clean from a {\i shevuah}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba in the name of Rav Chiya rules as follows. If the husband uses the language {\i dela neder, dela shevuah} \u8212- without a {\i neder}, without a {\i shevuah} \u8212- {\i hu eino yachol l\u8217'hashbia, aval yorshin mashbi\u8217'in osah} \u8212- he himself cannot make her take an oath, but his heirs can. The language is narrow and applies only to him. By contrast, if he uses the stronger formulation {\i naki neder, naki shevuah} \u8212- clean from a {\i neder}, clean from a {\i shevuah} \u8212- {\i bein hu bein yorshav ein mashbi\u8217'in osah} \u8212- neither he nor his heirs can make her take an oath. The word {\i naki}, clean, conveys a total absolution: {\i minakish mishvuasa} \u8212- I have cleansed you from any need of a {\i shevuah} whatsoever. That language is expansive enough to bind the heirs as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef in the name of Rav Chiya reaches a surprising and much harsher conclusion. He agrees with Rabba on the {\i dela} formulation: {\i dela neder, dela shevuah} \u8212- the husband cannot impose an oath, but the heirs can. On the {\i naki} formulation, however, he goes in the opposite direction from Rabba. Rav Yosef reads {\i naki neder, naki shevuah} to mean not exemption but affirmation of the obligation: {\i lah naki nafshach b\u8217'shvuasa} \u8212- \u8220"you will have to prove your own innocence through a {\i shevuah}.\u8221" On this reading, both he and his heirs can make her swear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking position. The practical implication is troubling: if the husband is writing language that merely restates the existing status quo \u8212- that she is required to make a {\i shevuah} \u8212- why would he write anything at all? As the {\i gemara} itself notes, {\i ein adam motzi d\u8217'varav livatalah} \u8212- a person does not issue statements for no purpose. If the {\i naki} language changes nothing, what was the point of writing it? The answer, apparently, is that a husband must be extremely careful about the language he uses; an ambiguous formulation may be read against the woman in ways that serve no one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Zakai\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zakai sent a ruling to Mar Ukva that cuts through the terminological debate entirely. Whether one says {\i dela shevuah} or {\i naki shevuah}, whether {\i dela neder} or {\i naki neder} \u8212- none of these linguistic distinctions determines whether the heirs are bound. The only distinction that matters is possessive language. If the husband writes {\i b\u8217'nichsai} \u8212- \u8220"from my property\u8221" \u8212- then {\i hu eino yachol l\u8217'hashbia, aval yorshin mashbi\u8217'in osah} \u8212- he cannot impose the oath but his heirs can. The possessive \u8220"my\u8221" limits the exemption to assets that are his; once those assets pass to the heirs and are no longer \u8220"his,\u8221" the exemption does not follow them. But if he writes {\i minichsaya ilin} \u8212- \u8220"from this property,\u8221" using a demonstrative rather than a possessive \u8212- {\i bein hu bein yorshin ein mashbi\u8217'in osah} \u8212- neither he nor his heirs can impose an oath. The property is defined by reference to the thing itself, not the person who owns it, and the exemption travels with the property regardless of who holds it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abba Shaul\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel in the name of Abba Shaul ben Ima Miriam \u8212- a {\i tanna}, and notably identified here through his mother\u8217's name \u8212- rules with maximum breadth in the woman\u8217's favor. Regardless of which language the husband uses \u8212- {\i dela shevuah, naki shevuah, dela neder, naki neder, b\u8217'nichsai, nichsai ihu} \u8212- in every case, {\i bein hu bein yorshav ein mashbi\u8217'in osah}: neither he nor his heirs can impose an oath on her. Every formulation, however narrow or passive, is interpreted in the most expansive way possible to protect the woman from being subjected to a {\i shevuah}. This ruling is subsequently cited as {\i halacha} by Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel: {\i halachah k\u8217'ven Ima Miriam} \u8212- we rule like the son of Ima Miriam.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a crucial caveat. Abba Shaul himself acknowledges it: {\i aval mah e\u8217'eseh, sheharei amru chachamim haba lifra minichsei yesomim lo yifra ela b\u8217'shvuah} \u8212- \u8220"but what can I do, for the Sages have ruled that anyone who comes to collect from the property of orphans may not collect except by oath.\u8221" This is a general rule of {\i halakha} applying to all creditors, entirely independent of any arrangement between husband and wife. Whoever comes to the heirs to collect a debt must swear that the debt has not been paid \u8212- because the heirs have no firsthand knowledge of the father\u8217's transactions and cannot be expected to know what was or was not settled during his lifetime.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Types of Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is that there are two distinct categories of {\i shevuah} in play here. The first is the oath of the {\i apitropaya} \u8212- the oath a business manager or administrator might be required to take to account for her stewardship. This is the oath the husband waives, and his heirs are bound by that waiver in all cases per Abba Shaul\u8217's ruling. The second is the oath of the general creditor \u8212- the oath required of anyone collecting a debt from heirs. This oath has nothing to do with the husband\u8217's trust of his wife or the specific relationship between them; it is a structural feature of the law of inheritance and debt collection. The husband cannot preemptively waive this obligation, because it is not an obligation owed to him \u8212- it is an obligation owed to the heirs, arising from their own ignorance of the prior state of accounts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction applies with equal force to the {\i pogemet ketubah} scenario. If the husband exempted her from the {\i shevuah} of {\i pogemet}, she need not swear when collecting from him that the remainder of the {\i ketubah} is unpaid. But if she is collecting from the heirs after his death, the ordinary exemption from {\i pogemet} is covered \u8212- yet she must nonetheless swear as a creditor collecting from an estate. The one exemption covers the oath owed to the father; it does not cover the oath owed to those who now hold his estate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pogemet Ketubah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having completed the discussion of the oath exemption, the {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i mishnah} that Rav Nachman identified as the primary subject of that exemption \u8212- the {\i mishnah} of {\i pogemet ketubah}. The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i hapogemes kesuvasah lo tipara ela bishvua} \u8212- a woman who \u8220"injures\u8221" her {\i ketubah} by admitting that part of it has been paid may not collect the remainder except by oath. The {\i mishnah} then identifies a second case: {\i ed echad meidah sheparuah} \u8212- a single witness testifies that the {\i ketubah} has been paid in full \u8212- and rules that she likewise may not collect except by oath. These are the first two scenarios in which the oath requirement applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshubadim & Absent Part\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with additional cases: {\i minichsei meshubadim} \u8212- collecting from liened property \u8212- and {\i shelo b\u8217'fanav} \u8212- collecting in the debtor\u8217's absence. In the first of these, the husband has sold his property to others and the wife comes to execute her lien against those purchasers. The purchasers naturally cannot know whether the {\i ketubah} debt has been settled between husband and wife, and so she must swear that it has not. In the second case, the husband has gone abroad \u8212- to a distant land, as the {\i mishnah} puts it \u8212- and under certain circumstances collection may proceed without him present. There too, since he cannot speak for himself, she must swear that the debt remains outstanding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case-by-Case Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then spells out each of these cases with unusual explicitness, almost as if anticipating confusion. For {\i pogemet}: the {\i ketubah} is a thousand, he says it has been paid in full, she says she received only a hundred \u8212- she must swear that the nine hundred remains unpaid. For the single witness: the {\i ketubah} is a thousand, he says it has been paid, she says she received nothing, and a single witness corroborates that it was paid \u8212- she must swear to collect. For {\i meshubadim}: he sold his property, she comes against the purchasers \u8212- she must swear. For the heirs: he died and left his estate to his heirs, she comes against them \u8212- she must swear. For the absent debtor: he has gone abroad, she collects without him present \u8212- she must swear. The {\i mishnah} finally records a view of Rabbi Shimon that as long as she is actively seeking her {\i ketubah}, the heirs may make her swear, and if she is not seeking it, they may not \u8212- a position whose elaboration is deferred to the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biblical vs.\u160 ?Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami Bar Chama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i mishnah} now before us, the {\i gemara} raises the foundational analytical question: is the {\i shevuah} of {\i pogemet ketubah} a {\i shevuah de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- a biblically mandated oath \u8212- or is it a rabbinic enactment? Rami bar Chama initially proposes that it is biblical. His reasoning: the case of {\i pogemet} resembles a classic instance of {\i modeh bemiktzat} \u8212- a partial concession to a claim. The rule of {\i modeh bemiktzat} is biblical: if someone claims two hundred from you and you concede a hundred while denying the other hundred, you take a biblical oath and are exempted from paying the disputed amount. Here, he argues, the woman\u8217's concession that a hundred has been paid is analogous to a {\i modeh bemiktzat}, and the resulting {\i shevuah} should therefore be biblical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Refutation I\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers two responses, both decisive. The first: {\i kol hanishba\u8217'in shebaTorah nishba\u8217'in velo meshalmin} \u8212- all those who swear biblical oaths do so and are thereby exempted from payment. In the classic {\i modeh bemiktzat} case, the debtor swears and does not have to pay the disputed portion. The {\i shevuah} functions to protect the swearer from payment. But here the situation is inverted: {\i vehi nishba\u8217'as venoteles} \u8212- she takes the oath and she collects. The {\i shevuah} enables her to extract money from the other party. No biblical oath operates in this direction. Biblical oaths protect the swearer from paying; they do not empower the swearer to collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth dwelling on how this distinction works structurally. In a standard {\i modeh bemiktzat}, there is no {\i shetar} \u8212- no written instrument establishing the debt. The claimant says \u8220"you owe me two hundred,\u8221" the respondent concedes a hundred and denies the rest, takes an oath, and pays only what he conceded. Without the {\i shetar}, the claimant\u8217's uncorroborated insistence on the additional hundred cannot extract payment. The oath resolves the dispute in favor of the swearer. In the {\i pogemet} case, however, the woman has a {\i shetar} \u8212- the {\i ketubah} document establishing the full debt. Even without any concession on her part, she would be entitled to collect the full amount. The {\i shetar} itself is her proof. Her concession that a hundred has been paid does not eliminate her entitlement to the remainder; it only raises a question, and the {\i shevuah} resolves that question in her favor and allows her to proceed to collect. The {\i shevuah} thus functions as a collection mechanism, not an exemption from payment \u8212- which is structurally incompatible with the biblical oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Refutation II\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's second response: {\i ein nishba\u8217'in al kfirat shibud karka\u8217'os} \u8212- no biblical oath is administered in connection with a denial regarding a lien on land. Since the {\i ketubah} operates as a lien on real property, and the biblical oath does not apply in disputes involving land, this case simply cannot be a {\i shevuah de\u8217'oraita} on that basis alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore concludes: {\i miderabbanan} \u8212- this is a rabbinic {\i shevuah}. The Sages enacted it for a specific purpose. Their reasoning is rooted in a realistic assessment of human psychology and attention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psychology of Memory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Depara dayek} \u8212- the one who is paying is careful and attentive. {\i Demifra lo dayek} \u8212- the one who is being paid is not necessarily so attentive. When a person is paying off a debt, that act of payment is significant and memorable; he knows precisely what he has paid and is motivated to keep accurate track. But the person receiving payment may not be equally attentive \u8212- she may not register with the same precision what she has and has not received, particularly if she is not the one initiating or controlling the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages therefore concluded that when a woman concedes that part of her {\i ketubah} has been paid, this creates a real possibility \u8212- not an accusation of conscious dishonesty, but a recognition of differential attention \u8212- that perhaps more has been paid than she recalls. {\i Urami rabanan shvuah alah, ki heichi detidok} \u8212- the Sages imposed an oath on her so that she should pay close attention. The act of swearing forces careful reflection; it compels her to go back through her recollections with full seriousness before asserting that the remainder is unpaid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framework is notable because it deliberately avoids the implication that the woman is lying. If we assumed she was consciously lying, the oath would be of questionable value \u8212- a familiar principle established at the beginning of tractate {\i Bava Metzia}: one who is suspected of financial dishonesty ({\i chashud al ha\u8217'mamonah}) may also be suspected of swearing falsely ({\i chashud al hashevuah}). The rabbinic rationale for the {\i shevuah} of {\i pogemet} specifically avoids this inference. The woman is presumed honest; the {\i shevuah} is there to calibrate her memory to the same level of precision as the one who paid her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Clarifying Mishnah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a series of questions about variations on the {\i pogemet} case. The first: {\i pogemes kesuvasah b\u8217'eidim mahu} \u8212- what if the partial payment that she concedes was witnessed? That is, there are witnesses who saw that a hundred was paid. Does she still need to swear that the remaining nine hundred is outstanding, or does the presence of witnesses on the partial payment actually strengthen her position?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument for exempting her from the oath runs as follows: if we observe that when he paid the first hundred, he ensured witnesses were present, then the inference is that this person is careful to document his payments. Had he paid the remaining nine hundred, he would likewise have secured witnesses or a receipt. Since there are no witnesses to any further payment, the absence of evidence is itself evidence of absence \u8212- and she should be able to collect without a {\i shevuah}. This is a version of the {\i migo} reasoning raised earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} seeks to resolve this by citing the {\i mishnah} that enumerates who takes oaths and collects: {\i kol hanishba\u8217'in shebaTorah nishba\u8217'in velo meshalmin} \u8212- all who swear in the Torah swear and do not pay. The {\i mishnah} then lists the exceptional categories where one swears and collects: the hired laborer ({\i sachir}), the person who was robbed ({\i nigzal}), the person who was injured ({\i nechbal}), the one against whom the opposing party is suspected of swearing falsely, and the storekeeper swearing by his ledger. Among these is also: {\i vehapogemet shtaro shelo v\u8217'edim} \u8212- one who injures a {\i shetar} without witnesses. The crucial qualifier is {\i shelo v\u8217'edim} \u8212- in the case where the partial concession was made without witnesses to the payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially reads this as implying that when there are witnesses ({\i be\u8217'edim}), she does not need to swear. But the {\i gemara} then reverses this reading: {\i lo mibaya ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} speaks in the form of \u8220"it goes without saying.\u8221" The obvious case \u8212- where there are witnesses to the partial payment \u8212- she certainly needs a {\i shevuah}, because there is concrete corroboration that part was paid, making it all the more plausible that the rest was paid too. The {\i mishnah} specifies the less obvious case: even {\i shelo v\u8217'edim}, even when there are no witnesses to the first payment and she could have said nothing (the {\i migo} argument), she still needs a {\i shevuah}. Because if there were witnesses, the case for a {\i shevuah} is all the stronger. The question about what happens {\i be\u8217'edim} is thus left genuinely unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precise Accounting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second question: {\i hapogemet ketubasah pachos pachos mishtei prutos mahu} \u8212- what if the woman\u8217's concession of partial payment is rendered in extremely precise terms, down to fractions of pennies? Rashi explains that the scenario envisions a woman who has kept careful, detailed records \u8212- she accounts for every payment with specificity, listing dates and exact amounts down to the last cent. Given this level of precision, one might argue that she is clearly on top of the accounting and is obviously truthful: {\i keivun dekadaika kulei hai, kushta ka\u8217'amrah} \u8212- since she is so precise in every detail, she must be telling the truth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precision Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But there is a countervailing possibility: {\i arumei kama\u8217'arimah} \u8212- she may be a sophisticated deceiver. By producing an elaborate and precise accounting, she hopes to project such an air of meticulous honesty that no {\i shevuah} will be required of her. The very precision may be a stratagem. After all, if she truly wanted to lie, she could claim nothing was paid at all \u8212- the {\i migo} runs in her favor on the fact of her concession regardless. But by combining a partial concession with extreme precision, she might achieve both the appearance of honesty and the avoidance of the oath. The {\i gemara} leaves this question unresolved: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reduced Amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third question: {\i pocheshes kesuvasah mahu} \u8212- what if the woman does not concede that part of the {\i ketubah} has been paid, but instead concedes that the original {\i ketubah} amount was less than what the document states? She is not saying \u8220"you paid me a hundred\u8221"; she is saying \u8220"we agreed from the outset that the real {\i ketubah} was only two hundred, not a thousand, even though a thousand is written.\u8221" Is this a case of {\i pogemet}, requiring an oath?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceding Lesser Amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument is that {\i pogemet} involves a concession of partial payment, creating the concern that perhaps full payment has been made. But here there is no partial payment at all. She simply says the amount was always lower. Since the underlying concern \u8212- that he may have paid the full claimed amount and she has forgotten \u8212- does not apply when she is not claiming any payment has been made, she should be able to collect the lesser amount without a {\i shevuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confirms this: {\i pocheshes tifrah shelo bishvuah} \u8212- one who concedes to a lesser original amount collects without a {\i shevuah}. The {\i mishnah} spells this out explicitly: the {\i ketubah} says a thousand, he claims it has all been paid, she says she was never paid a penny but the real {\i ketubah} was only a hundred \u8212- {\i tifrah shelo bishvuah}, she collects the hundred without an oath. This is structurally different from {\i pogemet}: she is not a {\i modeh bemiktzat} in any sense because she is conceding nothing about payments having been made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Document Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural difficulty arises: if the {\i ketubah} document says a thousand and she is conceding it should only be a hundred, she is effectively admitting the document is not accurate. How can she then use the document to collect anything? If the {\i shetar} is false, it is, as the {\i gemara} vividly puts it, {\i chispa be\u8217'alma} \u8212- a mere piece of pottery, worth nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava the son of Rava resolves this: {\i be\u8217'omeres amanah haisah li beini leveino} \u8212- she is saying that there was an understanding of trust between them. The couple agreed at the time of the marriage that they would write the larger number \u8212- perhaps to honor the expectations of parents, or to make a certain impression at the wedding ceremony \u8212- but with a mutual understanding that she would only ever collect the lesser amount. The {\i shetar} itself was validly executed, the witnesses signed a genuine document, and he was genuinely obligated. She simply agreed, by virtue of their private understanding, not to collect the full face value. The document is therefore not false; it is a valid instrument against which she has voluntarily agreed to collect less. On that basis, she may use the {\i shetar} to collect the agreed-upon lesser amount, and since she is not conceding any partial payment but only a lesser original obligation, she collects it without a {\i shevuah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 88\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modeh B\u8217'Miktzat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Cases Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 87b, amid a discussion of the various circumstances in which a woman must take an oath in order to collect her {\i ketubah}. The Gemara enumerates several distinct cases, each generating its own type of {\i shvua}, and it is worth pausing to understand what is at stake before working through them one by one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Payment Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case is where the woman herself concedes that part of the {\i ketubah} has already been paid. This might seem like a straightforward instance of {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} \u8212- the well-known biblical rule that one who partially admits a claim must take an oath \u8212- but it is not quite that. The classical {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} applies to the party from whom money is being extracted, whereas here it is the collecting party \u8212- the woman \u8212- who is making the partial admission. The technical conditions for the biblical oath therefore do not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 D\u8217'Rabbanan Oath Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The oath imposed in this case is accordingly a {\i shvua d\u8217'rabbanan}. The concern driving it is that she may have forgotten: the fact that part of the debt has been paid creates a real possibility that she has lost track of the full account. The oath functions as a mechanism to force her to review the matter carefully and be doubly certain before collecting. The rabbis instituted this {\i shvua} precisely because the partial payment introduces reasonable doubt, and the gravity of the oath is meant to prompt genuine recollection.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ed Echad & Shvua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness Testifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case shifts the dynamic considerably. Here, the woman comes to collect, the husband claims he has paid, and she denies it. Ordinarily she would prevail: she holds a {\i shtar}, and he has no receipt. But suppose the husband produces a single witness \u8212- an {\i ed echad} \u8212- who testifies that the {\i ketubah} has in fact been paid. She must now take a {\i shvua} to contradict that witness. This too has the structural feel of an oath rooted in biblical law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami Bar Chama Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami Bar Chama indeed argued that this constitutes a {\i shvua d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- a biblical oath. His reasoning proceeds from the verse in Deuteronomy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deut. Source & Ed Echad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse states: {\i lo yakum ed echad b\u8217'ish l\u8217'chol avon u\u8217'l\u8217'chol chatat} \u8212- one witness shall not rise up against a person for any sin or transgression. The Gemara derives from this that while an {\i ed echad} cannot prevail outright in court and impose liability, he does have the legal force to compel an oath from the opposing party. This interpretation is the foundation of the entire institution of the {\i ed echad} demanding a {\i shvua}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Demands Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative principle is that an {\i ed echad} does not win the case \u8212- he cannot substitute for the two witnesses required to obligate payment \u8212- but he destabilizes the status quo enough that the other party cannot simply ignore him. Without any witness, the default rule of {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'ayah} governs: the one seeking payment bears the burden of proof. An {\i ed echad} disrupts that default, and the only way to neutralize him and restore the status quo is to take an oath. The oath does not itself create a new obligation; it dissolves the threat posed by the single witness, returning the parties to where they would have stood without him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shnayim/Echad Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This logic is crystallized in a general principle: {\i kol makom she-shnayim m\u8217'chayvin oto mamon, echad m\u8217'chaivo shvua} \u8212- wherever two witnesses would obligate payment, one witness obligates an oath. It bears noting that this principle applies specifically in monetary matters. In the domain of {\i issurim}, a single witness is regarded as entirely without legal weight. But in monetary disputes, a lone witness has real, if limited, force: not to compel payment, but to compel an oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applying this to the {\i ketubah} case: she holds a {\i shtar} \u8212- which functions as two testimonies \u8212- and he has one witness who says it is paid. Two witnesses would have cancelled her {\i shtar} entirely. One witness demands that she take an oath to neutralize his testimony, after which the {\i shtar} reasserts itself and she collects. The structure is symmetrical with the classical {\i ed echad} scenario. Rami Bar Chama\u8217's position that this is a biblical oath therefore appears quite reasonable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Two Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Taker Benefits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, rejects this analysis and offers two distinct reasons why the oath imposed here cannot be biblical. His first objection goes to the heart of how biblical oaths function: {\i kol hanishba\u8217'in she-ba-Torah nishba\u8217'in v\u8217'lo m\u8217'shalmin} \u8212- every biblical oath in the Torah is one where the oath-taker swears and thereby avoids paying. The paradigm of the biblical {\i shvua} is defensive: I am accused of owing you money, you have an {\i ed echad} backing your claim, and I take an oath to neutralize that witness and establish that I owe nothing. The oath is a shield.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She Swears & Collects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i ketubah} case, however, the structure is inverted. The woman is not defending herself against a demand for payment; she is the one seeking to collect. She swears, and then she {\i takes} money \u8212- {\i nishba\u8217'at v\u8217'notlet}. That is the opposite of the biblical pattern. The oath here is a sword, not a shield, and the Torah\u8217's framework for the {\i shvua d\u8217'oraita} simply does not encompass this configuration. To be sure, the underlying logic is structurally similar: the {\i ed echad} creates a complication that must be neutralized, and the oath restores the status quo \u8212- but the status quo here is that the {\i shtar} entitles her to collect, not that she is exempt from paying. Biblically, one takes an oath to avoid paying; one does not take an oath in order to collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karka Lien Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's second reason is equally dispositive. A {\i shtar} with a {\i ketubah} carries {\i shibbud karka\u8217'ot} \u8212- liens on land. When the dispute concerns a document that creates encumbrances on real property, there is a general rule that oaths are not taken with respect to the denial of {\i shibud karka\u8217'ot}. Since the {\i ketubah} is precisely such a document, the oath cannot be biblical on this ground as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 D\u8217'Rabbanan to Appease\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With both biblical rationales eliminated, Rava concludes: this oath is purely rabbinic, instituted {\i kedei le-hafis da\u8217'ato shel ba\u8217'al} \u8212- in order to appease the husband. The husband is agitated; he has a witness who he believes confirms that he paid. The {\i shvua} is designed to calm him. One might fairly ask how an oath she takes really calms a husband who is convinced he is telling the truth and has a witness to back him up \u8212- the oath might strike him as just another layer of her alleged deception. The question is a real one, and the Gemara\u8217's choice of this rationale over more obvious alternatives deserves attention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath & Truthfulness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more obvious explanation would be that the {\i ed echad} creates an objective concern that she may not be telling the truth, and the oath addresses that concern by making her formally accountable before God. But the Gemara pointedly does not say this. The reason, it seems, connects to a deep structural tension in the institution of the oath: if someone is willing to steal, will they really hesitate to swear falsely? The Gemara in Bava Metzia raises precisely this question \u8212- {\i chashud al mammon chashud al shvuasah} \u8212- and if the answer is that a person who steals will also swear falsely, then the oath does not actually address the concern that she is lying. It only functions as a deterrent if we assume that she is not an outright thief, perhaps that she has rationalized her position or made an honest error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first case \u8212- where she conceded partial payment \u8212- that assumption was entirely plausible: maybe she forgot, maybe she lost track of the account. The oath prompts recollection. But in this case, where the dispute is total \u8212- he says he paid everything, she says he paid nothing \u8212- it is not plausible that she simply forgot the entire payment. So the honest-error explanation does not work here. And since the Gemara is implicitly reluctant to say that an oath deters an outright liar, it reaches instead for the relational explanation: it appeases the husband. The function of the oath, on this reading, is not evidentiary but social \u8212- it is designed to manage the interpersonal tension rather than to resolve a factual question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biblical Oath Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Clever Move\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a striking practical turn. Rav Pappa observes: {\i i pike\u8217'ach hu, mati lah liydei shvuah d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- if the husband is clever, he can maneuver the situation so that she ends up taking a biblical oath rather than a rabbinic one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Weightiness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining the strategy, the Gemara implicitly raises the question of why this matters. Rashi explains: a {\i shvua d\u8217'oraita} is qualitatively different from a {\i shvua d\u8217'rabbanan}. The biblical oath is pronounced using God\u8217's name \u8212- or at minimum a divine epithet such as {\i Elokim} or {\i Shaddai} \u8212- and is administered while the swearer physically holds a {\i Sefer Torah}. The verse {\i lo yenakeh Hashem es asher yisa shemo la-shav} \u8212- God will not absolve one who takes His name in vain \u8212- gives the biblical oath a metaphysical weight that a rabbinic oath lacks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Curse vs.\u160 ?Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i shvua d\u8217'rabbanan}, by contrast, is in essence {\i klala b\u8217'alma} \u8212- merely a curse, an invocation of ill fortune upon oneself if one is lying. The psychological and spiritual weight is considerably diminished. The husband, who is convinced he is in the right, has a strong incentive to force the more solemn form of the oath, which may either prompt her to recant or \u8212- should she proceed \u8212- carry far graver consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Payment Tactic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The husband\u8217's strategy, as the Gemara describes it, proceeds as follows: since he will likely have to pay out the {\i ketubah} anyway once she takes the rabbinic oath and collects, he should go ahead and pay her now \u8212- in front of a different witness, call him {\i Shimon} \u8212- even though he already paid her earlier (in front of the original witness, {\i Reuven}). He is paying her a second time, but calculating that the expenditure is worth the legal position it creates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combining Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having now paid her twice, he brings the two witnesses together \u8212- {\i Reuven} and {\i Shimon} \u8212- and confronts her: I have paid you the {\i ketubah}, I have two witnesses. She cannot deny the May payment because it is proven by both witnesses. Now he turns around and says: but what about the money I gave you in January? That was a loan. You owe me that money back. She denies the loan. He produces {\i Reuven}, who witnessed the January payment. Now the situation has been restructured: he is the one seeking to recover money, she is the one denying it, and he has an {\i ed echad} \u8212- the classic configuration of the biblical oath. She must swear a {\i shvua d\u8217'oraita} to get out of paying him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combining Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge with this scheme, however, is the problem of combining {\i Reuven} and {\i Shimon}: they witnessed different events at different times. Using {\i Reuven} first to establish that the {\i ketubah} was paid in January, and then turning around and using {\i Reuven} to claim that the January payment was actually a loan, creates an internal contradiction. The same hundred dollars cannot simultaneously be {\i ketubah} payment and a loan, and the same witness cannot serve both characterizations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshes Fix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshes, son of Rav Idi, therefore simplifies the scheme: in May, before making the second payment, bring both {\i Reuven} and {\i Shimon} together to witness it. The husband says to {\i Reuven}: come with {\i Shimon} and watch me pay her now. Now the May payment is unambiguously proven by two witnesses, and it stands on its own without any need to import {\i Reuven}\u8217's January testimony into the question of whether the {\i ketubah} was discharged. The January payment, witnessed by {\i Reuven} alone, can then be characterized purely as a loan, and {\i Reuven} is not being asked to play contradictory roles.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Ketubos Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi, however, raises a further objection: she can simply claim {\i bei kesuvos havai} \u8212- that there were two separate {\i ketubot}. She argues: you loved me so much that you wrote me one {\i ketubah} for a certain sum and another {\i ketubah} for the same sum. You paid off the first {\i ketubah} in January and the second in May. There was no loan; both payments discharged {\i ketubah} obligations. And since {\i Reuven} acknowledges that the January payment occurred in a {\i ketubah} context, he effectively corroborates her version: when asked whether there was any discussion of what the January money was for, {\i Reuven} will confirm it was a {\i ketubah} payment. She therefore owes the husband nothing and need not swear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, she need not produce documentation of two {\i ketubot}. The evidentiary burden remains on him: he is the one seeking to extract money from her, and absent a {\i shtar} evidencing the loan, his only leverage is the {\i ed echad}. If that {\i ed echad} acknowledges that the January payment was presented in a {\i ketubah} context, it no longer functions as testimony of a loan, and his entire scheme collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Confidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is that before bringing {\i Reuven} to witness the May payment, the husband takes {\i Reuven} into his confidence. He explains: look, you and I both know the January payment was the genuine {\i ketubah} payment. I am being forced to pay her again in May solely because she is denying the first payment and I do not have sufficient witnesses to prove it. This May payment is not a second {\i ketubah}; it is a forced repayment of the original one. {\i Reuven} now understands the full picture, and when the question of two {\i ketubot} arises, {\i Reuven} is in a position to clarify what he actually witnessed and understood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rarity Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What gives this resolution its legal traction is the sheer rarity of two {\i ketubot}. It is an extraordinary circumstance for a husband to write two separate {\i ketubot} for the same wife. Given that rarity, and given that {\i Reuven} \u8212- armed with the husband\u8217's explanation \u8212- can testify to his understanding of the January payment\u8217's purpose, the bet din has ample basis to be skeptical of the two-{\i ketubah} defense. The implausibility of the claim, combined with {\i Reuven}\u8217's informed testimony, is sufficient to recharacterize the January payment as a debt and proceed to the {\i shvua d\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The calculus for the husband is ultimately sound: if she would have taken the {\i shvua d\u8217'rabbanan} and collected anyway, he loses the {\i ketubah} sum regardless. The double-payment scheme costs him the same amount in the worst case, but in the best case \u8212- if she refuses to swear the more solemn {\i shvua d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- she repays the January sum and he breaks even. He stands to gain and cannot lose more than he already would have.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Collecting from Heirs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dead Husband Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third scenario in which a woman must take an oath to collect her {\i ketubah} is where the husband has died and she is collecting from his heirs. The underlying logic is consistent with what has already been established: the husband is no longer present to assert a counterclaim, to produce a receipt, or to defend himself against her demand. In his absence \u8212- now permanent \u8212- she must take an oath that the {\i ketubah} has not already been paid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvuot Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adduces a parallel from a {\i mishnah} in Shvuot: {\i v\u8217'chen hayetomim lo yifre\u8217'u ella bishvuah} \u8212- heirs of a creditor may likewise only collect on a debt by means of an oath. This provides a structural precedent: when the collecting party is not the original claimant, or when the debtor is no longer present to contest the claim, an oath is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Heirs Swear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately raises a difficulty. If the creditor himself could collect the debt without taking any oath \u8212- simply by producing the {\i shtar} and asserting that it has not been paid \u8212- why should his heirs, who have inherited that same {\i shtar}, be required to swear? They are in the same position he was; let them simply assert, as he would have, that to the best of their knowledge the debt has not been discharged, and collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debtor Dead Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that the {\i mishnah} in Shvuot is not discussing heirs collecting from the living debtor; it is discussing heirs collecting from {\i the debtor\u8217's heirs}. When the debtor is alive, he can defend himself \u8212- produce evidence of payment, dispute the amount, or make any other argument. The creditor\u8217's claim stands against the debtor\u8217's rebuttal, and no oath is needed. But when the debtor is dead and his heirs are the respondents, the debtor can no longer defend himself. In that circumstance, whoever is collecting \u8212- whether the original creditor or his heirs \u8212- must swear. The oath fills the void left by the debtor\u8217's absence and ensures that the claim is not collected without the check of a solemn affirmation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs\u8217' Statement Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zerika, citing Rav Yehuda, adds an important qualification. The oath requirement applies specifically when the heirs of the deceased debtor say: our father told us that he borrowed the money and paid it back. In that case \u8212- where they make a positive claim that the debt was discharged \u8212- the collecting party must indeed swear. Alternatively, if the heirs say nothing and simply do not know the status of the debt, the oath still applies, because the debtor is unavailable to confirm or deny.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Denial Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what if the heirs claim: our father told us he never borrowed this money at all? In that case, one might think that the creditor cannot collect even with an oath, since the heirs are denying the existence of the debt entirely \u8212- and the creditor has no one to contradict. The Gemara presents this as one possible reading: if they deny the debt wholesale, perhaps even the {\i shtar} cannot overcome a complete denial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi objects that this is precisely backwards. If the heirs say their father never borrowed the money, that very claim implies he never paid it back either \u8212- {\i kol omer lo lovisi k\u8217'omer lo parati dami}, one who says \u8220"I never borrowed\u8221" is in the same position as one who says \u8220"I never repaid.\u8221" And the creditor has a {\i shtar} proving the loan took place. The heirs\u8217' denial of the loan does not override the documentary evidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs Say Father Paid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara accordingly inverts the ruling. Rav Zerika\u8217's statement must be read as follows: the oath requirement applies when the heirs say their father told them he {\i paid} \u8212- in that case, the creditor or his heirs must swear before collecting. But if the heirs claim their father denied ever having borrowed in the first place, the creditor may collect {\i without} an oath \u8212- because the heirs have effectively conceded non-payment by denying the loan, and the {\i shtar} is not contradicted by their denial in any meaningful sense. {\i Shekol omer lo lovisi k\u8217'omer lo parati dami.}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Absent & Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband in Hawaii\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth scenario brings us to the case of {\i shelo befanav} \u8212- the husband is alive but absent, having gone to a distant land. She wishes to collect her {\i ketubah} \u8212- either because he divorced her before departing, or because she needs to collect {\i mezonot} from his estate while they remain married \u8212- but he is not present in court to contest or confirm the claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath for Absent Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here too, the rule is: {\i v\u8217'nifra\u8217'at shelo befanav lo tipara ela bishvua} \u8212- if she is collecting while he is not present, she may only do so by taking an oath. Again, the oath serves the same structural function: it substitutes for the debtor\u8217's absence, providing the bet din with a solemn assurance before allowing collection to proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishum China Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha Sar HaBirah reports a ruling of Rabbi Yitzchak in Antioch that introduces an important distinction: {\i lo shanu ela kesubas isha mishum china} \u8212- this dispensation to collect {\i shelo befanav} was granted specifically for a woman collecting her {\i ketubah}, and the rationale is {\i mishum china}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Standing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishum china} is the Gemara\u8217's compressed way of referring to concern for the woman\u8217's position in society. A divorced or widowed woman who cannot collect her {\i ketubah} is left in a precarious state \u8212- financially vulnerable and disadvantaged in her ability to rebuild her life. The institution of the {\i ketubah} exists precisely to protect her from this outcome. Allowing her to collect even in the husband\u8217's absence, provided she takes an oath, reflects the law\u8217's particular solicitude for her circumstances. To withhold collection until the husband could be physically brought before the court would expose her to prolonged hardship that the rabbis were unwilling to countenance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor Different\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha Sar HaBirah\u8217's ruling implies that an ordinary creditor whose debtor has gone abroad would {\i not} enjoy the same dispensation \u8212- he would have to wait until the debtor could be brought to court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Broad Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, citing Rav Nachman, disagrees and extends the rule broadly: {\i afilu baal chov} \u8212- even an ordinary creditor may collect from an absent debtor. The rationale is direct and practical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Absconding Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shelo yehei kol echad v\u8217'echad notel maosav shel chaveiro v\u8217'holech v\u8217'yoshev b\u8217'mdinas hayam} \u8212- we cannot allow a situation where any person can take another\u8217's money and simply relocate to a distant country with impunity. If the law provided no recourse against an absent debtor, it would create a standing invitation to defraud creditors by fleeing jurisdiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think that this concern primarily protects wealthy lenders. In fact the opposite is true. If the legal framework fails to protect creditors, no rational person will lend money, and it is ultimately borrowers \u8212- particularly poor ones with no other source of funds \u8212- who suffer. The protective instinct here runs in both directions. The {\i pruzbul} of Hillel illustrates the same logic: rules that seem to favor creditors are often essential for maintaining the lending market that borrowers depend on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking passage. The Gemara\u8217's conclusion, backed by Rava and Rav Nachman and apparently confirmed by the {\i stam} of the Gemara in {\i Erchin} as well, is that even a {\i baal chov} may collect from an absent debtor. Yet Tosafot notes that the actual practice was exactly the opposite: {\i maasin b\u8217'chol yom she\u8217'ein nifrin shelo b\u8217'fanav l\u8217'baal chov} \u8212- the daily practice was not to collect for an ordinary creditor in the debtor\u8217's absence. Rabbeinu Chananel ruled accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi Compromise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does Tosafot justify a practice that contradicts the plain reading of the Bavli? The answer given is remarkable: {\i samchu amman d\u8217'dachi lei b\u8217'Yerushalmi} \u8212- they relied on a counter-position found in the Yerushalmi. The Yerushalmi articulates what amounts to a procedural compromise: three letters of summons are sent to the absent debtor. If he appears in response \u8212- good; the matter proceeds normally. If after three summons he fails to appear, then the court seizes his property and executes the judgment without him. This graduated approach acknowledges both the creditor\u8217's legitimate claim and the absent party\u8217's right to be heard, while foreclosing the abuse of indefinite delay. The practice on the ground apparently aligned with this Yerushalmi position despite the Bavli pointing in the other direction \u8212- a genuinely unusual case of received practice overriding the {\i peshat} of our Gemara.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Only Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} records a cryptic ruling of Rabbi Shimon: {\i kol zman she-tova\u8217'as ketubatah, yorshin mashbi\u8217'in otah} \u8212- as long as she is demanding her {\i ketubah}, the heirs can make her swear; when she is not demanding her {\i ketubah}, they cannot. Taken at face value this seems almost tautological \u8212- of course the heirs would only seek an oath when she is demanding her {\i ketubah}. What is Rabbi Shimon adding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Identified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiyah identifies the context: Rabbi Shimon is addressing the {\i shelo befanav} case \u8212- the husband alive but absent in a distant land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maintenance & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That case in fact encompasses two distinct scenarios. In one, they remain married and she needs to put food on the table \u8212- she requires {\i mezonot} from his estate while he is abroad. In the other, he divorced her and she is collecting her {\i ketubah}. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's unqualified formulation of {\i shelo befanav} appeared to require an oath in both scenarios. Rabbi Shimon distinguishes between them: only when she is demanding her {\i ketubah} \u8212- in the divorce scenario \u8212- can the oath be required. When she is seeking {\i mezonot} for ongoing subsistence, we do not impose an oath. The woman must be able to put food on the table without first undergoing a formal swearing procedure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanan\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction maps precisely onto a debate recorded in an earlier {\i mishnah}, involving Chanan. In the case of a man who went to a distant land while still married, and his wife sought {\i mezonot} from his estate, Chanan ruled: {\i tishava basof v\u8217'lo tishava bitchila} \u8212- she swears at the end, not at the beginning. She may collect {\i mezonot} now without an oath; later, when she comes to collect her {\i ketubah} at the conclusion of the marriage, the bet din may then make her swear regarding any funds that may have been prepaid during the husband\u8217's absence. The oath is deferred, not waived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnei Kohanim View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bnei Kohanim Gedolim} \u8212- a group apparently identified by this title \u8212- disputed Chanan\u8217's position and said: {\i tishava bitchila u\u8217'vasof} \u8212- she swears at the beginning as well, both when collecting {\i mezonot} and when collecting the {\i ketubah}. On their view, any collection from the husband\u8217's estate in his absence triggers the oath requirement, without exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon = Chanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position aligns with Chanan: he holds that the oath is required only when she is demanding her {\i ketubah}, not when she is seeking {\i mezonot}. The urgency of subsistence, on this view, overrides the procedural requirement of the oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis = Bnei Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis who dispute Rabbi Shimon align with {\i Bnei Kohanim Gedolim}: they require an oath in both scenarios, treating the {\i shelo befanav} dispensation uniformly across all collection contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshes raises a difficulty with this entire reading: Rabbi Shimon\u8217's statement uses the word {\i yorshin} \u8212- heirs. If the context is simply that the husband is alive and merely abroad, there are no heirs in the picture at all. Why does Rabbi Shimon say \u8220"the heirs can make her swear\u8221" when the husband is still living?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Apotropos Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apotropos Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshes therefore proposes a different context entirely. Rabbi Shimon is addressing the scenario of the woman as {\i apotropos} \u8212- the manager of her late husband\u8217's estate on behalf of his underage heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Husband\u8217's Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} earlier described two paths a woman might take after her husband\u8217's death: she might return to her father\u8217's house, or she might continue living in her father-in-law\u8217's household. In either case, she may or may not have assumed a formal managerial role over the estate left behind for the orphaned heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Oath Without Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she returned to her father\u8217's house and did not become {\i apotropos}, the heirs have no basis to make her swear at all. The husband had exempted her from oaths relating to past dealings, and if she played no ongoing managerial role, there is nothing about which she could be made to account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future vs.\u160 ?Past\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, she did assume the role of {\i apotropos}, the {\i mishnah} ruled that the heirs may make her swear regarding {\i future} dealings \u8212- that is, regarding the ongoing management of the estate going forward \u8212- but they may not make her swear regarding {\i past} dealings, since the husband had exempted her from such oaths retrospectively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether and when an {\i apotropos} can be compelled to swear is itself the subject of a separate Tannaitic dispute between Abba Shaul and the Rabbis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Shaul holds: an {\i apotropos} appointed by the father of the orphans \u8212- appointed, that is, before the father\u8217's death \u8212- is exempt from swearing, because the father trusted him implicitly. Someone appointed by the court, however, must swear. The reasoning is that a court appointment carries institutional prestige; the appointee accepts the role willingly, knowing an oath may be required, precisely because the honor of a court appointment makes the responsibility worthwhile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis take exactly the opposite view: one appointed by the father must swear, because that appointment was a private arrangement that does not carry the force of a court order. One appointed by the court, however, is exempt \u8212- because to require an oath of someone the court has publicly entrusted with this responsibility would deter capable people from accepting court appointments. The exemption is an institutional incentive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon = Abba Shaul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon aligns with Abba Shaul. On this reading, when Rabbi Shimon says that the heirs cannot make the woman swear in her role as {\i apotropos}, it is because she was appointed by the father \u8212- and, following Abba Shaul, such an appointee is exempt. But when she comes to collect her {\i ketubah} from the orphans\u8217' estate, that is a different matter entirely: there, the general principle that one who collects from an orphans\u8217' estate must always swear applies, regardless of any exemption from oath in her capacity as {\i apotropos}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Waiver Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Waiver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to yet another context: the husband who explicitly wrote in his wife\u8217's {\i ketubah}: {\i neder ushvuah ein li alaich} \u8212- I have no vow and no oath authority over you. He has preemptively waived his right to impose an oath on her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Broad Waiver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The waiver could be formulated even more expansively: {\i neder ushvuah ein li, v\u8217'lo l\u8217'yorshay, v\u8217'lo l\u8217'ba\u8217'im birshuti alaich, v\u8217'lo l\u8217'yorshaich, v\u8217'lo l\u8217'ba\u8217'im birshusah} \u8212- not I, nor my heirs, nor anyone acting with my authority have any oath authority over you, nor over your heirs, nor over those acting with your authority. In its fullest form, this waiver extends to all successors on both sides. The question becomes: can even this sweeping waiver be circumvented?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Waive Heirs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Shaul ben Imma Miriam held that the waiver, however broadly written, is ineffective in one specific context. The husband can waive oaths that belong to him to waive \u8212- oaths that would have been demanded in his name, relating to claims he himself could have pressed. But there is a principle that stands independent of anything the husband can grant or withhold: {\i haba lipara minichsei yesomim lo yipara ella bishvuah} \u8212- one who comes to collect from an orphans\u8217' estate may only do so by means of an oath. This rule protects the orphans, who cannot protect themselves, and it belongs to no party in the dispute to waive it. The husband cannot prospectively extinguish a protection that exists for the benefit of heirs who have not yet lost their father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs Oath Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle, then, is categorical: collecting from an orphans\u8217' estate always requires an oath, regardless of any prior waiver.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa raises a question about the second half of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's formulation. The first half \u8212- that she must swear whenever she collects her {\i ketubah}, notwithstanding any waiver \u8212- makes sense in light of the foregoing analysis. But Rabbi Shimon also says that when she is {\i not} demanding her {\i ketubah}, the heirs cannot make her swear. Is this not obvious? If she has a broad waiver and is not even seeking to collect the {\i ketubah}, why would anyone imagine she could be made to swear?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Excludes R. Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa answers: the second clause of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling is not addressing an obvious case \u8212- it is explicitly excluding the stricter position of Rabbi Eliezer. Without this second clause, one might have thought that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view governs: that a husband can compel his wife to swear even during the course of the marriage, regarding her management of household affairs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position is that the husband may impose an oath on his wife about any matter relating to household management \u8212- even something as granular as the dough she handled. On this view, a husband\u8217's authority to demand an accounting from his wife is quite broad, and it would extend well beyond {\i ketubah} collection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Middle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} occupy a middle position: the husband may compel an oath if his wife runs the shop \u8212- that is, a business in which she handles money and accounts on his behalf \u8212- but he may not impose an oath regarding purely domestic matters like the preparation of dough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Balanced\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon takes a position that is simultaneously more stringent and more lenient than both alternatives. He is more stringent than Chanan regarding the {\i ketubah}: he insists that the oath is always required when she comes to collect it, no matter what was waived. But he is more lenient than the {\i Chachamim} and far more lenient than Rabbi Eliezer regarding everything else: during the marriage, whatever she does, she may not be compelled to swear. The two halves of his ruling are complementary \u8212- absolute accountability at the moment of final collection, and complete freedom from oath-pressure during the ongoing marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbeinu Tam Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashkenazi Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot brings this discussion to bear on a concrete contemporary situation \u8212- concrete, at least, in the world of medieval Ashkenaz. Women in that community frequently functioned as businesswomen, running shops and managing accounts, often serving as the commercial face of a household enterprise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Suspects Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In one such case before Rabbeinu Tam, a husband had installed his wife as the manager of their shop \u8212- a {\i chanunis} \u8212- and she handled large sums of money in and out of the household. A dispute arose in which the husband suspected her of financial impropriety, or at least sought an accounting of her stewardship, and he wished to compel her to swear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Oath in Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam refused to permit it. Even in this situation \u8212- a wife running the family business with full access to its funds \u8212- the husband could not force an oath during the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Empowerment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The legal basis is the final {\i halakha} as it emerges from the Gemara: we rule in accordance with Rabbi Shimon, and Rabbi Shimon holds that outside the context of {\i ketubah} collection, the husband has no authority to impose an oath. This applies even to a woman running a business enterprise on his behalf. The second half of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling \u8212- that when she is not demanding her {\i ketubah} the heirs cannot make her swear \u8212- translates in practice into a prohibition on the husband making her swear during the course of the marriage, whatever the scope of her commercial activity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling of Rabbeinu Tam is not merely a technical application of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. It reflects a recognition that women who functioned as businesswomen in Ashkenaz needed legal protection from a mechanism \u8212- the compelled oath \u8212- that could be weaponized to harass or undermine them in their commercial roles. The {\i halakhic} conclusion, arrived at through a chain running from Abba Shaul ben Imma Miriam through Rabbi Shimon through the final redaction of the {\i sugya}, thus lands in a place that is both doctrinally grounded and practically protective of women\u8217's economic agency.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 90\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Gets, Two Ketubos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the ninth {\i perek} opens with a case involving multiple documents: {\i shnei gittin u-shtei ketubot} \u8212- a woman produces two {\i gittin} and two {\i ketubot}, staggered in sequence. The pattern is a {\i ketubah} from one date, a {\i get} from a later date, then a second {\i ketubah} and a second {\i get}, indicating two marriages followed by two divorces with the same husband. In such a case, the {\i mishnah} rules {\i gova shtei ketubot} \u8212- she may collect two {\i ketubot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage & New Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling requires explanation, because as we have seen in earlier discussion, remarriage to the same husband does not automatically generate a new {\i ketubah} obligation. The default assumption is that the original {\i ketubah} continues as the basis of the second marriage. However, if the husband proactively chose to write a new {\i ketubah}, he has created a distinct, second obligation \u8212- and in that case, both are binding. The staggered pattern of the documents, with two {\i ketubot} and two {\i gittin}, demonstrates precisely that he did so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Ketubos, One Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses three additional configurations, each of which limits her to collecting only a single {\i ketubah}. The cases are: two {\i ketubot} and one {\i get}; one {\i ketubah} and two {\i gittin}; and one {\i ketubah}, a {\i get}, and the husband\u8217's subsequent death. The last case is self-evident \u8212- she was divorced, then her husband died, and she cannot collect again as a widow. The case of one {\i ketubah} and two {\i gittin} illustrates the default principle most clearly: even though the second {\i get} is evidence that he remarried her, the absence of a second {\i ketubah} means the remarriage was conducted {\i al menas ketubah ha-rishonah} \u8212- on the basis of the original {\i ketubah} \u8212- and no new obligation was created.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of two {\i ketubot} and one {\i get} is the one the {\i mishnah} identifies as needing explanation. One might have thought that both documents are simultaneously binding \u8212- perhaps the husband intended to accumulate additional obligations. Indeed, a reader familiar with earlier discussion will recall a scenario in which a woman possessed two {\i ketubot} and was permitted to collect from both. The present {\i mishnah} rules {\i eino gova ela ketubah achas} \u8212- she may collect only one \u8212- because the presumption is that the second {\i ketubah} was written in anticipation of a remarriage following a divorce that may or may not be documented here. But the question immediately arises: which {\i ketubah} may she use? The earlier one carries earlier liens and therefore broader collection rights; the later one might carry a higher monetary amount. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's silence on which one suggests that the choice belongs to her \u8212- a point the {\i Gemara} will address directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequential Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises the possibility that this choice contradicts the ruling of Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel: {\i shnei shtaros ha-yotzin zeh achar zeh, bitel sheni es ha-rishon} \u8212- when two documents emerge sequentially and clearly refer to the same underlying transaction, the second voids the first. The clearest example is two bills of sale for the same plot of land. Since one cannot purchase the same property twice, the later document supplants the earlier one, and the buyer may not rely on the earlier dating. The practical significance of the earlier date lies in the scope of liens: because the seller accepts responsibility if a prior creditor forecloses on the property, the buyer with earlier liens can recover from the seller\u8217's property of that earlier period. A later document narrows that window. Similarly, if a disputed claim of ownership arises, proof of an earlier acquisition date carries independent value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The apparent contradiction with our {\i mishnah} is thus: if a woman possesses two {\i ketubot} for the same marriage, the second should void the first, leaving her only the second. Yet the {\i mishnah} implies she may choose between the two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waiver Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the underlying logic of Rav Nachman\u8217's principle: no one compelled the holder to accept the second document. A second bill of sale generally weakens the buyer\u8217's position \u8212- it establishes ownership from a later date, with correspondingly later liens. By voluntarily accepting it, the buyer implicitly declares that he waives whatever rights and protections he had under the earlier document. The acceptance of the later, less advantageous document constitutes a form of {\i mechilah} \u8212- waiver \u8212- of the benefits conferred by the earlier one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Added Benefits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa then states the critical qualification: {\i umodu Abaye v\u8217'Rava d\u8217'ei hosif bah dikla l\u8217'tosefes kesavah} \u8212- Abaye and Rava concede that if the second document added even one additional palm tree, one additional parcel, or any incremental benefit beyond what the first document included, then acceptance of the second document does not constitute a waiver of the first. The reasoning is direct: if the second document is clearly and entirely inferior to the first in every respect, then accepting it can only be explained as a willing surrender of the first. But if the second document contains any dimension in which it exceeds the first, then the holder has a rational basis for accepting it that does not imply waiver \u8212- he is preserving his freedom to choose whichever instrument better serves his interests in a given situation. Acceptance of the second document under those circumstances signals choice, not surrender.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Choice of Ketubos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application to our {\i mishnah} follows directly. Rav Papa concludes: {\i hacha nami bid\u8217'osif lah} \u8212- here too, the second {\i ketubah} must have included some added amount, even a single additional monetary unit, beyond the first. This is what allows the woman to retain the option of choosing between the two. When the second {\i ketubah} is not uniformly inferior \u8212- because it carries a higher face value, even if it comes with later liens \u8212- she is not presumed to have waived the first by accepting it, and she retains the ability to elect whichever is more advantageous in the circumstances. The {\i Gemara} notes that a double, cumulative obligation \u8212- both {\i ketubot} simultaneously binding \u8212- would require explicit language in the second document expressing precisely that intent. Absent such language, the result is an {\i or}, not an {\i and}: she collects from one or the other, at her election.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get vs.\u160 ?Ketubah Dating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related {\i braita} addresses the case in which a woman produces a {\i get}, a {\i ketubah}, and relies on the husband\u8217's subsequent death to claim as a widow. The rule is as follows: {\i im get kodem l\u8217'kesuvah, gova shtei ketubot} \u8212- if the {\i get} is dated before the {\i ketubah}, she collects two {\i ketubot}, because the later {\i ketubah} proves that he chose to obligate himself anew in a second marriage. If, however, the {\i ketubah} is dated before the {\i get} \u8212- {\i eina gova ela ketubah achas} \u8212- she collects only one, because even with clear evidence of divorce and remarriage, the absence of a new {\i ketubah} means the remarriage was on the basis of the original document alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor\u8217's Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Katan Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces a new case: {\i katan she-hisio aviv} \u8212- a minor boy who was married off by his father. This is noteworthy because the earlier discussion throughout the tractate has focused predominantly on the father arranging the marriage of a minor girl. The {\i mishnah} contemplates actual {\i nisuim} \u8212- full cohabitation \u8212- even when the boy is a {\i katan}. It is worth reiterating what the {\i Gemara} elsewhere makes clear: even in the context of {\i kiddushei ketanah}, the {\i Gemara} stated that actual {\i nisuim} \u8212- the couple living together \u8212- would not occur until the girl was at least a {\i na\u8217'arah}, twelve and a half years of age. Nevertheless, the case of a minor boy is presented here as a recognized, if unusual, reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Continuity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule is {\i kesubasah kayamet} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} remains in force. Although the minor boy lacked the legal capacity to obligate himself in a {\i ketubah} at the time it was written, once he reaches adulthood and continues to maintain the marriage, it is understood that he does so {\i she-al menas kein kiymah} \u8212- on the basis of that original commitment \u8212- and the {\i ketubah} accordingly becomes binding upon him. The continuation of the marriage as an adult is itself the act that ratifies and assumes the earlier obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ger & Conversion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the {\i perek} presents a case of striking historical interest buried within a legal ruling: {\i ger she-nisgayerah ishto imo} \u8212- a man and his wife who both convert to Judaism together. Before their conversion, they had a marriage document drawn up according to the practices of their non-Jewish society. The {\i mishnah} notes, almost in passing, that non-Jews did indeed produce documents at marriage that functioned similarly to a {\i ketubah} \u8212- recording the dowry brought by the wife, and specifying the lump sum she would be entitled to in the event of divorce or the husband\u8217's death. This is not described as {\i pasak lah ketubah kedas Moshe v\u8217'Yisrael}, but the parallel institution is evident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After their conversion, the couple remains married without rewriting a {\i ketubah}. When the husband later dies, the {\i mishnah} rules {\i kesubasah kayamet} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} obligation continues \u8212- because by remaining married after conversion the husband implicitly maintained the terms of the original agreement, based on the understanding that their earlier obligations carry forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Base Amount Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna offers a reading of the {\i mishnah} that, as the {\i Gemara} notes, departs substantially from the plain meaning: {\i lo shanu ela maneh u-matayim} \u8212- the ruling applies only to the base {\i ketubah} amount. His position is that whatever additional amount ({\i tosefes}) appeared in the pre-conversion document does not carry over into a binding Jewish {\i ketubah} obligation. Only the standard statutory amounts \u8212- two hundred {\i zuz} for a {\i besulah}, one hundred for an {\i almanah} \u8212- remain in effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda disagrees: {\i afilu tosefes yesh lah} \u8212- even the additional amount she was promised continues to be binding. This appears to be the straightforward reading of the {\i mishnah}, which uses the unqualified language {\i kesubasah kayamet} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} stands \u8212- without any limitation to the base amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is then cited as a challenge to Rav Yehuda. The {\i braita} states: {\i chidshu} \u8212- if after conversion or after the minor boy came of age they {\i innovated}, meaning they added a new amount to the {\i ketubah} \u8212- {\i noteles mah she-chidshu}, she is entitled to collect what they newly added. The {\i Gemara} initially attempts to read this as meaning she receives both the additional amount and the original full amount from before. But the {\i braita} continues explicitly: {\i lo chidshu \u8212- besulah gova matayim ve-almanah maneh} \u8212- if they did not add a new amount, she receives only the base statutory amount. This is unambiguous: the {\i braita} contradicts Rav Yehuda directly. {\i Tiyuvta de-Rav Yehuda tiyuvta} \u8212- it is a complete refutation of his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains that Rav Yehuda was misled by the language of the {\i mishnah} itself: {\i hu savar ketubatah kayamet akula milta} \u8212- he read {\i kesubasah kayamet} as meaning the entirety of the {\i ketubah} stands. In fact, as the {\i Gemara} clarifies, it means only that the base {\i ketubah} obligation stands: {\i akula milta} refers merely to the base amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Innovation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} presses the question further: if the ruling is only that she receives the base statutory amount, what is the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah}? Any Jewish wife, even without a prior document of any kind, is entitled to the base amount by Rabbinic enactment. Why do we need the {\i mishnah} to tell us that a convert whose wife converted with him gets the standard hundred or two hundred {\i zuz}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} offers a brilliant resolution. The {\i chiddush}, they explain, lies in the determination of which base amount she is entitled to. The {\i mishnah} is teaching: {\i de-ashmianu heicha de-nisa besulah, af al gav de-chasha gadla ve-nitgayera ke-she-hi be\u8217'ulah, afilu hachi yesh lah ketubah matayim} \u8212- we are taught about a case where they married when she was a {\i besulah}, and now after conversion she is a {\i be\u8217'ulah}. The question is whether she receives two hundred {\i zuz} as a {\i besulah} or one hundred as an {\i almanah}. The {\i mishnah} teaches that since they were first married when she was a {\i besulah}, that original context defines the marriage, and she therefore receives the full two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- even though by the time of conversion she is no longer a {\i besulah}. The same logic applies in the case of the {\i katan}: the woman might now be a {\i be\u8217'ulah} at the time the obligation becomes binding, yet because the original marriage was to a {\i besulah}, that classification governs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One sharp-eyed observer noted that {\i Tosafos}\u8217' reading carries a significant subtext: by assuming that the woman is still characterized as a {\i besulah} based on the context of the original non-Jewish marriage, the {\i mishnah} is implicitly attributing to that non-Jewish couple a standard of {\i tzniut} that one might not readily assume. The {\i chiddush} cuts against any casual assumption about the moral comportment of non-Jewish society.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes: {\i Hadran alach kotev le-ishto} \u8212- and with that, the ninth {\i perek} comes to a close.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Estate Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tenth {\i perek} opens with a new {\i mishnah}: {\i mi she-hayah nasui shtei nashim} \u8212- a man married to two wives who then died. The {\i mishnah} addresses the competing claims against the estate. {\i Ha-rishonah kodemes la-shniyah} \u8212- the first wife has priority over the second. The two wives are positioned as creditors against the estate, and the earlier creditor has the right to collect first. If the estate is insufficient, the second wife receives only what remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle extends to the heirs: {\i ve-yorshei ha-rishonah kodmin le-yorshei ha-shniyah} \u8212- the heirs of the first wife take priority over the heirs of the second. The {\i mishnah} then describes a more complex scenario: {\i nasa es ha-rishonah u-mesah, nasa shniyah u-mes hu} \u8212- he married the first wife and she died; he then married the second wife and he died. In this case, {\i shniyah ve-yorsheha kodmin le-yorshei rishonah} \u8212- the second wife and her heirs come first, before the heirs of the first wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning reflects the distinction between debt and inheritance. The second wife ({\i Leah}, in our example) is a creditor of the estate: her husband died before her, and her {\i ketubah} obligation \u8212- say ten thousand and two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- was never discharged. She or her heirs come to collect that sum as a debt owed to her. The heirs of the first wife ({\i Rachel}), by contrast, are not creditors; they are coming by virtue of the principle of {\i kesubas benin dichrin} \u8212- the rabbinic institution by which the male sons of a wife who predeceased their father take out their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} amount from the estate as an inheritance before the estate is divided generally. Since creditors take precedence over heirs, Leah and her heirs collect first, and only thereafter do Rachel\u8217's heirs receive their special inheritance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seizure Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} examines the precise language of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i ha-rishonah kodemes la-shniyah}, the first comes before the second \u8212- and asks whether this phrasing carries implications about what happens when the second wife does not wait her turn. The {\i Gemara} draws an inference: since the {\i mishnah} uses the language of {\i priority} rather than saying outright that the second wife has no claim at all, it may be implying that if the second wife seized the property before the first, she is permitted to keep it. From this the {\i Gemara} formulates a broader principle: {\i ba\u8217'al chov me\u8217'uchar she-kadam ve-gava, mah she-gava gava} \u8212- a later creditor who jumped ahead and seized property is entitled to retain what was seized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises a powerful objection to this principle: {\i teima mah sevara hi zo, di-lo yehei ba\u8217'al chov ela ke-loke\u8217'ah} \u8212- what is the logic here? Let the later creditor be treated no differently than a purchaser. And we know that if someone purchased property {\i after} the earlier creditor\u8217's lien was established, the earlier creditor may foreclose on that property and take it even from the buyer who paid full value. If a creditor with earlier liens can take property from a bona fide purchaser, why should he not be able to take it from a later creditor who seized it? The later creditor\u8217's claim is, if anything, weaker than that of an innocent purchaser.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Social Policy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} quotes the {\i Rashbam}\u8217's answer: {\i ba\u8217'al chov me\u8217'uchar adif mi-loke\u8217'ah} \u8212- in this specific context, the later creditor who seizes actually has a stronger position than a purchaser would, because of a social policy consideration. The principle is {\i shelo tin\u8217'ol delet bifnei lovin} \u8212- we must not close the door before borrowers. If we were to hold that a later creditor who seizes property must always yield to an earlier creditor regardless of circumstances, the result would be that no one would lend money to a person who already has outstanding debts. By preserving at least the possibility that a later creditor who acts quickly may retain what he seizes, we keep open the incentive for continued lending.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} then grapples with whether this answer is fully satisfying: if a later creditor can seize and keep, would that not instead discourage the {\i earlier} creditor from lending, knowing that someone behind him in line might race ahead? The response is that each potential lender assumes he will be the one to act first. But {\i Tosafos} acknowledges the underlying tension honestly: the policy involves a genuine unfairness, and the question of where to draw the line \u8212- between protecting prior lienholders and keeping credit markets functioning \u8212- is genuinely difficult. What is notable is that the {\i Rashbam} explicitly adds {\i kesubas isha mishum china} \u8212- the wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} is included in this policy \u8212- indicating that the same principle extends to the competing claims of two wives against a single estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Permanent Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} ultimately declines to resolve the question from the language of the {\i mishnah} in either direction. One reading argues that {\i kodemes} \u8212- comes first \u8212- means fully excludes, and the second wife has no claim at all; if she were to seize property first, it would be taken from her. Another reading argues from the same word that she merely has a lower priority but is not entirely excluded, and seizure by the later party is valid. Each direction of inference is tested and found inconclusive, as the {\i Gemara} shows that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language can be explained away in both readings. The question of whether {\i mah she-gava gava} is resolved one way or the other thus remains without a definitive proof from this {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Benin Dichrin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Classic Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then sets out the classic scenario: {\i nasa es ha-rishonah u-mesah, nasa shniyah u-mes hu} \u8212- he married the first wife ({\i Rachel}) and she died; he married the second wife ({\i Leah}) and then he died. To give the facts concrete form: Rachel brought a large dowry and received a {\i tosefes} commensurate with her family\u8217's wealth, so that her {\i ketubah} totaled twenty thousand {\i zuz} (the base amount plus fifteen thousand in dowry plus an additional {\i tosefes} sum). Leah\u8217's {\i ketubah} totaled ten thousand and two hundred {\i zuz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Principle Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rachel died, her husband inherited her estate. But when Reuven subsequently died, Rachel\u8217's sons by him come forward not as ordinary heirs but under the principle of {\i kesubas benin dichrin}: the male heirs of a wife who predeceased her husband are entitled to take out the full amount of their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} from the estate as their inheritance, before the remainder of the estate is divided equally among all heirs. This ensures that the sons of the wife with the larger dowry receive the benefit of what their mother brought into the marriage, rather than seeing it absorbed into an undifferentiated pool to be shared with the children of another wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, Leah \u8212- or, if she has since died, her heirs \u8212- comes to collect her {\i ketubah} of ten thousand and two hundred {\i zuz} as a {\i creditor}. She was widowed when Reuven died, and her {\i ketubah} is an unpaid debt against the estate. As a creditor, Leah\u8217's claim is satisfied before any portion of the estate passes to heirs. Rachel\u8217's children, by contrast, are receiving their mother\u8217's share as an {\i inheritance}, not as a debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling \u8212- that Leah\u8217's heirs come before Rachel\u8217's heirs \u8212- thus reflects the fundamental rule that a creditor\u8217's claim is satisfied prior to any division among heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Inferences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proceeds to draw three inferences from this {\i mishnah}: {\i shema mina telat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inference One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first inference: {\i shema mina achas be-chayav ve-achas be-moso, ve-yesh lahen kesubas benin dichrin} \u8212- even when one set of heirs derives their claim from a wife who died during the husband\u8217's lifetime and the other set derives their claim from a death that occurred at or after his death, {\i kesubas benin dichrin} still applies to the former group, and we are not concerned about fights \u8212- {\i velo chaishinan le-intzuyei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This inference requires explanation. In the {\i classic} formulation of {\i kesubas benin dichrin}, both wives predeceased the husband, and all heirs are on an equal footing: Rachel\u8217's boys take their mother\u8217's chunk, Leah\u8217's boys take their mother\u8217's chunk, and whatever remains is divided. In that symmetric case, while one set may receive more than the other, both are operating under the same institutional framework and neither has grounds to object in principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the situation is asymmetric. Leah is coming as a {\i creditor} \u8212- her ten thousand {\i zuz} is a debt. Rachel\u8217's children, by contrast, are coming as {\i heirs} under a {\i rabbinic} institution. Leah\u8217's children might credibly object: our mother collected her debt, which is fine \u8212- but why do you get a special Rabbinic inheritance before the rest of the estate is divided? Our mother didn\u8217't die before father, so we don\u8217't get {\i kesubas benin dichrin}; you get a special chunk and we get nothing extra. That seems unfair, and one might fear that this perceived unfairness would generate conflict and litigation \u8212- {\i intzuyei} \u8212- severe enough that the Rabbis would have declined to apply {\i kesubas benin dichrin} in such a case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rules that we do not have that concern. Rachel\u8217's children receive their {\i kesubas benin dichrin} even when Leah\u8217's heirs are coming as creditors. The asymmetry in the basis of the respective claims does not deprive the {\i benin dichrin} of their entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah as Leftover\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second inference: {\i kesubah na\u8217'asis mosar le-chaverta} \u8212- a {\i ketubah} collection is considered as though it were the \u8220"leftover\u8221" ({\i mosar}) for the other {\i ketubah}. This requires context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mosar Dinar Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The standard rule of {\i kesubas benin dichrin} includes a critical limitation: the institution applies only when there remains in the estate at least one {\i dinar} ({\i mosar dinar}) beyond what is disbursed under {\i benin dichrin}, available for division according to the ordinary rules of Biblical inheritance. This {\i mosar dinar} need not be large \u8212- a symbolic amount above a {\i perutah} suffices \u8212- but it must exist. The rationale is that {\i kesubas benin dichrin} is a Rabbinic enactment, and the Rabbis did not intend to override {\i yerusha de-Oraisa} entirely: if applying the Rabbinic institution would leave nothing whatsoever for a division according to Biblical rules, the Rabbinic institution is set aside and the estate is divided equally among all heirs. Rachel\u8217's children, who had a large {\i ketubah} behind them, would in that case receive far less \u8212- but the Biblical entitlement to an undivided inheritance takes precedence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor as Mosar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the second inference addresses is this: suppose the entire estate is, say, twenty thousand {\i zuz}. Rachel\u8217's children want their twenty thousand as {\i benin dichrin}, and Leah\u8217's heirs come and take ten thousand as creditors. After Leah\u8217's heirs collect, only ten thousand remains. Is there a {\i mosar dinar} to support {\i kesubas benin dichrin} for Rachel\u8217's children, or have they been left with nothing for Biblical division?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} reasons that the very payment of Leah\u8217's {\i ketubah} debt {\i is} the functional equivalent of the {\i mosar dinar}. When a creditor holds a lien against an estate, the estate\u8217's assets conceptually include whatever is needed to satisfy that debt. Even if after the creditor is paid there is nothing left for equal division, the estate {\i had} money that was deployed to pay the creditor \u8212- and that payment is as if the money existed as surplus, was used to discharge a legitimate obligation of the estate, and thus satisfies the requirement that something existed beyond the {\i benin dichrin} entitlements. The critical formulation is: if, absent any creditors, the estate would have had just slightly more than the {\i benin dichrin} claims, then the fact that a creditor absorbed that surplus does not deprive Rachel\u8217's children of {\i kesubas benin dichrin}, because the surplus was there \u8212- it simply went to pay a legitimate debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} supports this by noting that the {\i mishnah} does not condition its ruling on the presence of a {\i mosar dinar} beyond the {\i benin dichrin} distributions. Since it does not mention this requirement, it must be that the creditor payment itself satisfies it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inference Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third inference: {\i kesubas benin dichrin lo tarfei mi-meshabdei} \u8212- heirs collecting under {\i kesubas benin dichrin} do not collect from encumbered property, i.e., property that had already been sold or pledged. This distinguishes their status from that of creditors, who {\i do} hold liens against the debtor\u8217's prior encumbered assets and may foreclose on property sold after the lien was established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} derives this from the structure of the {\i mishnah}. If Rachel\u8217's children were collecting as creditors \u8212- as the delayed collection of their mother\u8217's unpaid {\i ketubah} \u8212- then, because Rachel\u8217's {\i ketubah} predates Leah\u8217's {\i ketubah} in time, Rachel\u8217's children would have first priority even over Leah\u8217's children. A creditor with earlier liens collects before a creditor with later liens. But the {\i mishnah} rules that Leah\u8217's children come {\i first}. The only way to reconcile this is to conclude that Rachel\u8217's children are not creditors at all \u8212- they are {\i heirs}, receiving under the Rabbinic institution of {\i kesubas benin dichrin}. Since they are heirs rather than creditors, they cannot reach encumbered property, and when creditors (like Leah\u8217's heirs) compete with heirs, the creditors win.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi challenges all three inferences. His opening point: {\i dilma l\u8217'olam eima lach, achas be-chayav ve-achas be-moso, ein kan kesubas benin dichrin} \u8212- perhaps I would say that in a case where one wife\u8217's claim arises from events during the husband\u8217's lifetime and the other\u8217's arises from events at his death, there is in fact {\i no} application of {\i kesubas benin dichrin} at all. The first inference would then be invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And if so, {\i u-mai kodmin, l\u8217'nachala katani} \u8212- what does the {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement that Leah\u8217's heirs \u8220"come first\u8221" mean? Perhaps it means only that they come first in terms of the {\i nachala}, the inheritance \u8212- i.e., they collect their creditor\u8217's share first, and then the remaining estate is divided equally among all heirs. Rachel\u8217's children would receive their proportional share of what remains, but not any special {\i kesubas benin dichrin} entitlement above and beyond that. The word {\i yorshei rishonah} \u8212- the heirs of the first wife \u8212- appears in the {\i mishnah} only because the earlier clause mentioned Leah\u8217's heirs ({\i shniyah ve-yorsheha}), and the {\i mishnah} used parallel language for Rachel\u8217's children without meaning to indicate that they have any special status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mosar After Creditor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then challenges the second inference as well. The claim was that a creditor payment counts as the {\i mosar dinar} to support {\i kesubas benin dichrin}. Rav Ashi says: {\i dilma l\u8217'olam eima lach, ein kesubah na\u8217'asis mosar le-chaverta} \u8212- perhaps the {\i ketubah} payment to one wife does not serve as the {\i mosar} for the other\u8217's {\i benin dichrin} entitlement. You would need a genuine {\i mosar dinar} \u8212- an actual dollar remaining in the estate after all {\i benin dichrin} entitlements are distributed \u8212- and if that dollar was consumed by the creditor, it is gone. Perhaps the {\i mishnah} is simply operating in a case where there {\i was} a {\i mosar dinar} remaining after Leah collected and Rachel\u8217's children received their share \u8212- and the {\i mishnah} simply did not spell this out because it was assumed to be present in the background facts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In sum, Rav Ashi argues that the two inferences that strengthen Rachel\u8217's children\u8217's position \u8212- that {\i kesubas benin dichrin} applies even in this asymmetric scenario, and that a creditor payment counts as the {\i mosar dinar} \u8212- are not compelled by the {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} could be read as assuming, without stating explicitly, that the conditions for those inferences are met, rather than proving that those conditions are unnecessary. The {\i peshut} of the {\i mishnah} still favors the first reading, but Rav Ashi\u8217's point is that neither inference is {\i muchrach} \u8212- forced by the text \u8212- and the matter will require further analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 91\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Achas B\u8217'chayav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya before us involves a case of competing claims from two sets of children whose mothers were married to the same husband, Reuven. Reuven first married Rachel, whose {\i ketubah} was worth twenty thousand dollars. Rachel died during Reuven\u8217's lifetime, meaning he retained the funds he would otherwise have paid her. Under the rabbinic enactment known as {\i ketubat benin dichrin}, however, Rachel\u8217's sons are entitled, upon Reuven\u8217's death, to extract the value of their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} from his estate before the remainder is divided equally among all his heirs. Reuven subsequently married Leah, who survived him. When Reuven dies, Leah\u8217's children come to collect their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} as a debt owed by the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structural asymmetry is significant. Rachel\u8217's children come as heirs \u8212- they are invoking a special inheritance right to their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} amount. Leah\u8217's children come as creditors \u8212- they are collecting a monetary obligation the estate owes their still-living mother. The Mishnah\u8217's basic ruling for this scenario, called {\i achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso} \u8212- one wife died while the husband was alive, one died after him \u8212- is straightforward: debt takes priority over inheritance. Leah\u8217's children first collect their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah}, and then Rachel\u8217's children take their special portion, with whatever remains distributed equally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's resolution follows the general principle that a creditor\u8217's claim precedes an heir\u8217's claim. Once Leah\u8217's children, acting in the capacity of creditors, have collected their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah}, the remaining estate is available for the application of {\i ketubat benin dichrin}. Rachel\u8217's sons then extract the value of their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} from that remainder, and only what is left after that is divided equally among all heirs. This ordering \u8212- debt first, then the special inheritance, then equal division \u8212- reflects the hierarchy that governs estates in {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, is not satisfied with simply establishing the order of collection. It probes a deeper question: in a scenario where Leah\u8217's children are coming as creditors rather than as co-beneficiaries of {\i ketubat benin dichrin}, are Rachel\u8217's children still entitled to their special status at all? When both wives predeceased the husband, each set of children holds equivalent standing \u8212- both are special heirs under {\i ketubat benin dichrin}, each taking their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} amount before the equal division. But here, Leah\u8217's children are not invoking {\i ketubat benin dichrin}; they are simply collecting a debt. Why, Leah\u8217's children might argue, should Rachel\u8217's children enjoy a privileged position that we are not receiving ourselves?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Nannas/R. Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara establishes that this very question is the subject of a {\i tannaitic} dispute, introduced by the phrase {\i detanya} \u8212- as taught in a {\i baraita}. The case in the {\i baraita} is precisely {\i achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso}: Rachel died during Reuven\u8217's lifetime, Leah died after him. Ben Nannas rules that Rachel\u8217's children may say to Leah\u8217's children: {\i b\u8217'nei ba\u8217'alat chov attem} \u8212- you are the children of a creditor. Take your mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} and leave. The implication is that Rachel\u8217's children retain their full entitlement to {\i ketubat benin dichrin}, undisturbed by the asymmetry of positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva disagrees. He uses the vivid phrase {\i kvar kaftza nachalah lifnei bnei rishona v\u8217'nafla lifnei bnei shniya} \u8212- the inheritance has already leapt away from Rachel\u8217's children and fallen before Leah\u8217's children. The colorful language conveys that Rachel\u8217's sons, who seemed poised to take their special portion, have lost their grip on it. The Gemara initially reads this as a debate over a fundamental principle: does {\i ketubat benin dichrin} apply when the two sets of heirs come from different legal positions \u8212- one as special heirs, one as creditors? Ben Nannas says yes; Rabbi Akiva says no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, intervenes with a startling interpretive move. He reports that he encountered the Rabbis of the academy of Rav debating this very passage, and he challenged their reading. Their assumption had been that Rabbi Akiva and Ben Nannas dispute the fundamental question of whether {\i ketubat benin dichrin} applies in {\i achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso}. Rava argues otherwise: {\i d\u8217'kulei alma achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso yesh lahen ketubat benin dichrin} \u8212- everyone agrees that in principle Rachel\u8217's children are entitled to their special inheritance. The dispute, he insists, is not about whether the right exists but about a separate technical requirement: whether the conditions for activating that right have been met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motar Dina Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That separate technical requirement is the rule of {\i motar dina} \u8212- there must be leftover money in the estate beyond the amounts extracted by the special heirs. {\i Ketubat benin dichrin} cannot consume the entire estate; there must remain some residual amount subject to the ordinary rules of inheritance. The debate between Ben Nannas and Rabbi Akiva, on Rava\u8217's reading, is about whether paying out a {\i ketubah} debt counts as satisfying that requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider the arithmetic: Reuven dies leaving an estate of thirty thousand dollars. Leah\u8217's children come and collect their ten thousand dollar {\i ketubah} debt. Twenty thousand dollars remains \u8212- precisely the amount of Rachel\u8217's {\i ketubah}. If Rachel\u8217's children take their twenty thousand dollars, nothing is left for equal division. Has the requirement of leftover money been met? Ben Nannas says yes: the money used to pay Leah\u8217's children counts as the portion of the estate that was subject to ordinary inheritance rules. All the heirs equally bore the obligation to pay that debt, and in discharging it, the estate effectively underwent a normal process of inheritance \u8212- then Rachel\u8217's children may step forward and take their special portion. Rabbi Akiva says no: that arrangement does not constitute genuine leftover money in the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava sharpens the argument further. In the case of an ordinary third-party creditor \u8212- some stranger who had a claim against the estate \u8212- everyone would agree that paying that claim constitutes a {\i motar}, a leftover, sufficient to enable {\i ketubat benin dichrin}. The heirs collectively bore the debt, discharged it from the estate as a normal inheritance transaction, and Rachel\u8217's children may then take their portion. The dispute arises specifically when the creditor is Leah\u8217's children themselves. There, Leah\u8217's children might object: we are not first inheriting equally and then paying ourselves \u8212- we are simply being paid first, and the inheritance discussion begins only afterward. The payment to us is not a {\i motar}; it is merely the discharge of a prior claim. That is the crux of the debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two related but distinct concerns thus animate the entire discussion. First, is it objectionable in principle that Rachel\u8217's children receive a special inheritance status that Leah\u8217's children do not? Second \u8212- and this is the concern Rava centers \u8212- even granting that Rachel\u8217's children are entitled in principle, has the {\i motar dina} requirement been satisfied when the only available funds were consumed in paying the debt to Leah\u8217's children?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenges Rava\u8217's entire interpretive framework. If, as Rava claims, Rabbi Akiva agrees in principle that Rachel\u8217's children are entitled and disputes only the circumstantial question of whether there was sufficient {\i motar} in this case, then Rabbi Akiva should have expressed himself very differently. Instead of the dramatic phrase about the inheritance \u8220"leaping away\u8221" from Rachel\u8217's children, he should have said simply: {\i im yesh mosar dinar mibaei lei} \u8212- if there is leftover money, Rachel\u8217's children may collect; if not, they may not. The language Rabbi Akiva actually uses \u8212- the inheritance leapt away and they lost it \u8212- sounds like an absolute disqualification, not a circumstantial one. It sounds like Rabbi Akiva is saying that in {\i achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso}, Rachel\u8217's children categorically lose their special status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef therefore concludes: {\i ela amar Rav Yosef, be\u8217'achas b\u8217'chayav ube\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso kamiflagi} \u8212- the dispute between Ben Nannas and Rabbi Akiva is genuinely about the fundamental principle. Rabbi Akiva holds that {\i ketubat benin dichrin} can only operate when both sets of children stand in the same position \u8212- both as special heirs simultaneously. When one set comes as creditors and the other as heirs, the mechanism breaks down entirely for the heirs. Ben Nannas holds that Rachel\u8217's children retain their special status regardless of the position from which Leah\u8217's children collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then extends this analysis to a parallel {\i baraita} involving Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Tanna Kamma}, whose positions map onto the same underlying debate. In that {\i baraita}, the {\i Tanna Kamma} states only that Leah\u8217's children come and collect their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah}, conspicuously omitting any special treatment for Rachel\u8217's children \u8212- which appears to parallel Rabbi Akiva\u8217's categorical disqualification. Rabbi Shimon then states: if there is an extra {\i dinar} beyond the {\i ketubah} amounts, each set of children collects their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} through {\i ketubat benin dichrin}; if not, they divide equally \u8212- which appears to parallel Ben Nannas\u8217's position that Rachel\u8217's children remain entitled in principle, subject to the {\i motar dinar} requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, resists accepting this straightforward alignment. It proposes alternative readings in which both the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Shimon agree that Rachel\u8217's children are entitled in principle, while debating only the technical question of what constitutes a sufficient {\i motar}. The Gemara explores whether the dispute concerns the type of property that counts as leftover \u8212- specifically, whether {\i karka} (land) alone qualifies, or whether {\i metaltelin} (movable property, chattel) suffices \u8212- and whether encumbered property ({\i meshubadei}) can count, or only unencumbered property ({\i bnei chorin}). Each of these readings, however, runs into difficulties with the precise language of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's formulation, which, on close inspection, appears to be imposing a stricter standard rather than a more lenient one. The Gemara ultimately concludes \u8212- {\i ela ki hanech terei lishnei kamma\u8217'ei v\u8217'ipoch} \u8212- that we should revert to the first two structural explanations but reverse who is being lenient and who is being strict. On this reading, the {\i Tanna Kamma} is actually lenient: whenever any {\i motar dinar} exists, Rachel\u8217's children collect. Rabbi Shimon is being strict: he requires that the {\i motar dinar} consist specifically of land or of unencumbered property. The {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's silence about Rachel\u8217's children in the opening clause reflects only the circumstance that in the case he described there was no {\i motar dinar} at all \u8212- not that Rachel\u8217's children lack entitlement in principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Wives Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hilchata Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After this extended analytical journey, the Gemara delivers its {\i halachic} conclusion through Mar Zutra in the name of Rav Pappa: {\i hilchata, achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso yesh lahen ketuvat bnei dichrin} \u8212- the law is that even in {\i achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso}, Rachel\u8217's children are entitled to {\i ketubat benin dichrin}. This ruling vindicates the persistent interpretive instinct of the discussion, which kept trying to read every tannaitic position as agreeing in principle that Rachel\u8217's children are entitled. The social rationale is compelling: a father will give a generous dowry to his daughter only if he can be confident that those funds will remain within his grandchildren\u8217's inheritance, regardless of the circumstances surrounding the other wife\u8217's death or her children\u8217's legal posture at the time of collection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i hilchata} further establishes: {\i v\u8217'ketuvah na\u8217'asit motir l\u8217'chavertah} \u8212- a {\i ketubah} payment made to Leah\u8217's children counts as the {\i motar}, the leftover amount required to activate Rachel\u8217's entitlement. The logic is that all heirs equally bore the obligation to discharge Leah\u8217's {\i ketubah} debt, and the portion of the estate applied to that debt represents a legitimate predicate for the subsequent inheritance process. Concretely: if Reuven died leaving twenty thousand dollars and one additional dollar, and Leah\u8217's children collected their ten thousand dollar {\i ketubah} from that estate, that payment counts as establishing a {\i motar}. The remaining twenty thousand and one dollars is not fully consumed by Rachel\u8217's {\i ketubah}, because a real portion of the estate \u8212- even a single dollar \u8212- was independently inherited and paid out. Rachel\u8217's children may now take their special portion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why the {\i hilchata} needs to state both principles explicitly \u8212- first that Rachel\u8217's children are always entitled, and second that paying Leah\u8217's {\i ketubah} counts as {\i motar}. If you only knew the first, you might have thought that Rachel\u8217's children are entitled only when there is genuine leftover money remaining {\i after} both {\i ketubah} payments, not money used to pay Leah\u8217's claim. If you only knew the second, you might have thought the rule about payment counting as {\i motar} applies only when both sets of children share equal standing as {\i ketubat benin dichrin} heirs \u8212- not when one set comes as creditors. Each principle is independently necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Wives Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara demonstrates why both principles require explicit statement through the case of three wives. Reuven married three women. Rachel and Leah both died during his lifetime, so their children are both entitled to {\i ketubat benin dichrin} on equal footing \u8212- this is the standard case with no asymmetry of standing. The third wife survived Reuven and has only daughters. When Rachel\u8217's and Leah\u8217's sons come to divide the estate, they discover that after their two {\i ketubah} extractions, the only remaining money in the estate is needed to pay the third wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} debt. Her daughters, as females, are not heirs under biblical law and will not claim {\i ketubat benin dichrin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Both Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In this three-wife scenario, the second principle \u8212- that a {\i ketubah} payment counts as {\i motar} \u8212- is essential and would need to be stated even if one had never heard of {\i achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso}. The two boys (from Rachel and Leah) are on an equal playing field; their entitlement is not in question. What is in question is whether the money used to pay the third wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} qualifies as the {\i motar dinar}. Without the second ruling, one might think that only genuinely surplus funds \u8212- money left after all obligations are met \u8212- qualify, not money deployed to pay a creditor. The second principle answers this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, in the {\i achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso} scenario, one might have thought that even if a payment to a third-party creditor counts as {\i motar}, the payment specifically to Leah\u8217's own children does not \u8212- because Leah\u8217's children might say we were never part of the inheritance, we were just getting paid first. Or one might have thought that since Leah\u8217's children are coming as creditors and Rachel\u8217's children as heirs, the asymmetry in standing disqualifies Rachel\u8217's children from {\i ketubat benin dichrin} entirely. The first principle \u8212- {\i achas b\u8217'chayav v\u8217'achas b\u8217'moso yesh lahen ketubat benin dichrin} \u8212- directly rules out that concern. Both rulings together ensure that the system functions even in the most complex configurations of wives, deaths, and creditor relationships.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Motar Dinar Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Wives Equal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah now articulates the foundational mechanics of {\i ketubat benin dichrin} in the standard two-wife case. A man was married to two wives, both of whom predeceased him. He then died. The orphans \u8212- Rachel\u8217's sons and Leah\u8217's sons \u8212- each seek to extract their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} from the estate as special heirs. If the estate contains exactly the combined total of the two {\i ketubah} amounts and nothing more, the rule of {\i ketubat benin dichrin} cannot operate. There is no {\i motar dinar} \u8212- no leftover amount for the ordinary laws of inheritance to govern. In such a case, {\i cholkin b\u8217'shaveh} \u8212- they divide the estate equally, without any special priority. The desire to activate {\i ketubat benin dichrin} is understandable: the children with the larger {\i ketubah}, or even those with fewer siblings (since a single son with a ten-thousand-dollar {\i ketubah} walks away with the full amount while ten sons sharing the same {\i ketubah} receive a thousand each), have a strong incentive to establish the {\i motar}. But absent that extra amount, no special extraction is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 With Extra Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, there is a {\i motar dinar} \u8212- a single {\i dinar}\u8217's worth of value beyond the combined {\i ketubah} totals \u8212- then {\i elu notlin kesubas iman v\u8217'elu notlin kesubas iman}: each set of children extracts their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} amount, and the residual estate, however modest, is then divided equally by the ordinary rules of inheritance. The extra {\i dinar} is what makes the entire mechanism operable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Self-Assessment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural temptation arises: if the estate falls slightly short of having a {\i motar dinar}, can the heirs who want {\i ketubat benin dichrin} to apply simply declare that they will add money to the estate to create the needed surplus? The Mishnah answers emphatically: {\i ein shomin lahem} \u8212- we do not listen to them. Nor may they inflate the assessed value of estate property, claiming that a piece of land worth eight thousand dollars should be counted as ten thousand, thereby appearing to leave a surplus. The assessment of what the estate is actually worth is not in the hands of the interested heirs. {\i Ella shamin es hanechasim beit din} \u8212- rather, the court assesses the value of the assets. The {\i beit din} conducts an objective appraisal, and the legal outcome follows from that appraisal alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What Counts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah also addresses the question of anticipated but not yet received funds. If there is money that should be coming to the estate \u8212- whether because the estate expects to receive an inheritance from someone who has not yet died, or because there are outstanding receivables not yet collected \u8212- this does not count toward the {\i motar dinar}. {\i Einan kemuchzak} \u8212- such funds are not considered within the estate\u8217's current control. Only assets actually in hand at the time of assessment establish whether the {\i motar dinar} requirement has been met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon Land Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon adds a further stringency. Even if there is leftover money beyond the {\i ketubah} amounts, that money counts toward the {\i motar dinar} only if it consists of land \u8212- {\i karka}. Movable property, {\i metaltelin} \u8212- even valuable cattle or other chattel \u8212- does not satisfy the requirement. As Rabbi Shimon formulates it: {\i afilu yesh sham nechasim she-ein lahem achrayut einam klum} \u8212- even if there is property that carries no lien obligation (meaning it is technically free and clear but happens to be chattel), it counts for nothing. The extra {\i dinar} must be in real property, {\i nechasim she-yesh lahen achrayut}, encumberable land, in an amount exceeding the two {\i ketubah} obligations. The symbolic significance of land as the carrier of genuine ancestral inheritance \u8212- rooted in the biblical conception of the estate as a portion in the land \u8212- underpins Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. The {\i Tanna Kamma}, by contrast, accepts chattel as a qualifying {\i motar}. The Gemara earlier sought to read the dispute between the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Shimon as bearing on exactly this question \u8212- whether {\i karka} alone qualifies or whether {\i metaltelin} suffices \u8212- and the Mishnah now confirms that Rabbi Shimon indeed holds the more stringent position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unequal Amounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} supplements the Mishnah with the case of unequal {\i ketubah} amounts: one wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} is a thousand {\i zuz}, the other\u8217's is five hundred. The children of the wife with the larger {\i ketubah} have a particularly strong incentive to activate {\i ketubat benin dichrin}. The rule, however, is identical: if there is a {\i motar dinar} \u8212- in this case, any amount beyond fifteen hundred and one {\i zuz} \u8212- each set of children extracts their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} amount and the remainder is divided equally. If not, they divide the total estate equally. The {\i baraita} adds no new principle; it simply confirms that the mechanics apply regardless of whether the two {\i ketubah} amounts are equal or disparate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Property Appreciation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Depreciation Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to cases in which the value of the estate changes between the time of the husband\u8217's death and the time of collection. The straightforward case is depreciation: the estate was worth fifteen hundred and one {\i zuz} at the moment of death \u8212- sufficient to satisfy both {\i ketubah} amounts with a {\i motar dinar} to spare \u8212- but by the time the heirs come to collect, the value has fallen to fourteen hundred. The Gemara treats this as self-evident: {\i kevar zachu bahen yorshin} \u8212- the heirs already acquired their rights at the moment of death. The status of the estate is determined as of the date of death, and a subsequent decline in value does not retroactively strip the children of their entitlement. The {\i motar dinar} requirement was satisfied when it needed to be satisfied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Tsartsur Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More complex is the reverse: the property of the house of Bar Tsartsur presented the mirror image \u8212- at the time of death the estate was insufficient ({\i mu\u8217'atin}), but afterward it increased in value ({\i v\u8217'nisrabu}). The heirs came before Rav Ami seeking a ruling on whether the appreciation should trigger the application of {\i ketubat benin dichrin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ami did not issue a clear ruling. Instead, he instructed the parties to negotiate among themselves \u8212- specifically suggesting to those with the smaller {\i ketubah} that they try to reach a settlement ({\i zilu paysuhu}) with those who held the larger claim. The implication of his direction was that the children with the larger {\i ketubah} might have the stronger argument in principle, since the property now had sufficient value. When the parties refused to negotiate, Rav Ami threatened rabbinic sanctions \u8212- the painful but non-physical sanction of excommunication \u8212- to compel a settlement. The inability to reach resolution led the matter to be referred to Rav Nachman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman ruled with apparent symmetry: just as when the estate begins large and depreciates \u8212- {\i zachu bahen yorshin}, the special heirs already acquired their rights \u8212- so too when it begins small and appreciates \u8212- {\i zachu bahen yorshin}, the heirs acquire their rights. But this formulation raises an immediate question: {\i which} heirs? In the depreciation case, the answer is clear \u8212- the heirs who needed the {\i motar dinar} already had it, and they keep their entitlement despite the later decrease. But in the appreciation case, if you say {\i zachu bahen yorshin}, it is ambiguous whether you mean the heirs who were being excluded (because at death there was no {\i motar}) now gain the right due to the appreciation, or whether you mean something else entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi/Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and Tosafot dispute the import of Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling. Rashi understands it to mean that one always assesses as of the date of death. In the appreciation case, because there was no {\i motar dinar} at death, the heirs of the smaller {\i ketubah} wind up benefiting \u8212- the estate is divided equally, which favors them, and the later appreciation simply increases what each party receives from the equal division. The determining moment is fixed at death. Tosafot reads Rav Nachman\u8217's symmetrical language differently: just as in the depreciation case the rule of {\i ketubat benin dichrin} is applied because the {\i motar} existed at death, so in the appreciation case {\i ketubat benin dichrin} is also applied because the {\i motar} now exists at the time of collection. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, the principle is that one always resolves ambiguity in favor of applying {\i ketubat benin dichrin} \u8212- whether the qualifying {\i motar} appeared at death or at distribution. This disagreement reflects a fundamental question: is the determining moment fixed (Rashi) or does the system prefer, at any available snapshot in time, to activate the special inheritance mechanism (Tosafot)?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Houses Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final section of the {\i daf} introduces a case that initially appears to involve the rules of creditor collection but turns out to implicate broader principles about negotiation rights and asset valuation. A man owed a thousand {\i zuz} to a creditor. He had two houses \u8212- each worth five hundred \u8212- and sold them both to Michael for five hundred each. The seller then, presumably, dissipated the proceeds without paying his debt. The creditor now pursues Michael as the buyer of the encumbered properties. Michael acquired the houses subject to the seller\u8217's lien, so the creditor may collect from Michael\u8217's property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Michael\u8217's Offer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The creditor moved to seize the first house, and Michael saw that he was about to lose the second house as well. Michael approached the creditor with a thousand {\i zuz} and made the following proposal: I am prepared to tell you that the first house you already seized is worth a thousand {\i zuz} \u8212- that is, your full debt is satisfied. If you accept that valuation, consider your debt paid and leave me the second house. If you reject that valuation, then here \u8212- take the thousand {\i zuz} in cash and return the first house to me. Michael\u8217's gambit is elegant: he offers the creditor either a favorable valuation of one house covering the entire debt, or a cash payment of the full thousand {\i zuz}. Either way, Michael is trying to avoid losing both houses. What he is doing is effectively asserting his own assessment of the first house\u8217's value \u8212- claiming it is worth the full thousand \u8212- and daring the creditor to disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama raised the possibility that this is analogous to the Mishnah\u8217's case of orphans who sought to inflate the value of estate property in order to create a {\i motar dinar}: {\i im amru yetomim harenu ma\u8217'alim al nichsei avinu yafeh dinar} \u8212- if the orphans say we will assess our father\u8217's property at a higher value to generate the required surplus \u8212- the Mishnah says we do not listen to them. Is Michael\u8217's self-serving valuation similarly inadmissible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejected the analogy. {\i Mi dami?} \u8212- are the cases really comparable? {\i Hatam ika pseida l\u8217'yatmi} \u8212- in the Mishnah\u8217's case, when one set of orphans inflates the property value, the other set of orphans suffers a real loss, because the inflated valuation distorts the equal division they are entitled to. {\i Hacha mai pseida?} \u8212- but here, what loss does the creditor actually suffer? {\i Alfa yahiv v\u8217'alfa shakil} \u8212- he put in a claim for a thousand and he is going to collect a thousand. His debt is fully discharged either way. If the creditor suffers no loss from Michael\u8217's proposed valuation, the Mishnah\u8217's prohibition \u8212- designed to protect parties who would be harmed by self-serving assessments \u8212- may not apply. Michael may well have the right to negotiate on precisely these terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara leaves open the broader question of whether a purchaser of encumbered property generally has the right to pay the creditor in cash rather than surrendering the property \u8212- Tosafot notes and analyzes this as a potentially independent principle \u8212- but the immediate ruling flows from Rava\u8217's distinction: because the creditor is made whole either way, Michael\u8217's negotiating posture is not automatically disqualified by the Mishnah\u8217's rule against self-interested valuation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara thus proceeds on the assumption that Michael\u8217's offer is at least potentially legitimate, and the case is resolved in the creditor\u8217's favor in the following sense: the creditor, having now accepted one house in satisfaction of his full debt (whether because he agreed to the thousand-{\i zuz} valuation or because Michael paid cash and reclaimed the first house, retaining the second), has collected his thousand {\i zuz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina/Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final question arises regarding Michael\u8217's claim against the original seller. Michael purchased two houses for five hundred each. One was seized by the creditor. Michael is now out one house and is entitled to compensation from the seller who sold him encumbered property. But how much? Ravina says Michael is entitled to a thousand {\i zuz} \u8212- because the house effectively discharged a thousand-{\i zuz} debt, and that is the measure of what it was worth in the transaction. Rav Ashi says five hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- because the house\u8217's actual purchase price was five hundred, the underlying value is five hundred, and Michael\u8217's loss is five hundred regardless of the debt it happened to satisfy. The {\i halacha} follows Rav Ashi: {\i hilchata b\u8217'chamesh me\u8217'ah} \u8212- Michael recovers only five hundred from the seller. The creditor received his full thousand, Michael retained one house and was compensated five hundred for the one he lost, and the seller \u8212- who generated this entire predicament by selling encumbered property and failing to pay his debt \u8212- bears the consequences of the shortfall.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 93\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Wives Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a {\i mishna} presenting one of the most analytically challenging division problems in all of rabbinic literature. The case: a man married three women and died. Rashi specifies that all three were married on the same day, a necessary condition for the problem to arise at all \u8212- if they had married on different days, the earlier {\i kesuvah} would hold a prior lien, and the division would simply proceed in chronological order of priority regardless of claim size. The three women hold {\i ketubot} of 100, 200, and 300 respectively. The question is how the estate is to be divided among them when its total value may be less than the sum of their claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estate = 100\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the estate is worth only 100, the {\i mishna} rules that the three women divide it equally \u8212- each receiving 33\u8531 ?. This is intuitive: all three women have an equal and total claim on that first 100. The woman owed 100 says \u8220"give me the 100,\u8221" the woman owed 200 says \u8220"give me the 100,\u8221" and the woman owed 300 says the same. Their claims are indistinguishable in their force against that amount, so equal division follows naturally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estate = 200\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case is where the difficulty begins. When the estate is worth 200, the {\i mishna} prescribes a distribution of 50 to the woman with the 100 {\i kesuvah}, and 75 each to the women with the 200 and 300 {\i ketubot}. This is deeply puzzling. One might attempt to apply the logic of the first case: on the first 100, all three have equal claims and divide it equally, yielding 33\u8531 ? each. On the second 100, only the women with claims of 200 and 300 remain, and since both assert equal and total claims on that amount, they divide it equally \u8212- 50 each. That would yield 33\u8531 ?, 83\u8531 ?, and 83\u8531 ?. But the {\i mishna} gives 50, 75, 75. The problem is not merely that the numbers differ \u8212- it is that the {\i mishna}\u8217's division does not transparently follow any single coherent principle. Why 50 as the threshold for the first division rather than 40 or 60? The {\i gemara} itself will note this difficulty explicitly, and Tosafot acknowledges that the rationale has not been satisfactorily explained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estate = 300\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case, where the estate equals 300, yields a distribution of 50, 100, and 150. Unlike the second case, this result follows cleanly from a straightforward model: proportional division according to claim size. The claims of 100, 200, and 300 stand in a ratio of 1:2:3, totaling 6 parts. One-sixth of 300 is 50, two-sixths is 100, and three-sixths is 150. This model works elegantly here. The difficulty is that it does not explain either of the earlier cases \u8212- applied to the estate of 200, proportional division would yield 33\u8531 ?, 66\u8532 ?, and 100, not 50, 75, 75.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Models Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} thus appears to employ at least two distinct models across its three cases, and possibly three. The first case follows a principle of equal division among all claimants with equal standing. The third case follows proportional division by claim size. The second case follows neither, at least not in any obvious way. The final line of the {\i mishna} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'chein gimel she\u8217'hitilu lakis}, \u8220"and so too with three who invested into a common purse\u8221" \u8212- reinforces the proportional model for the third case: if three partners invest 100, 200, and 300 respectively, the profits and losses are divided proportionally to their contributions. This makes good sense as a model for investment. It does not, however, explain the middle case, and the {\i mishna} presents all three rules as if they constitute a unified system.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aumann\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aumann Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It was the economist and mathematician Robert Aumann \u8212- Nobel laureate and game theorist \u8212- who, roughly 1,800 years after the {\i mishna} was written, published a paper demonstrating that all three cases follow a single, coherent, and mathematically unique principle. The paper appeared in the collected papers of Robert Aumann and was also printed in the Hebrew Torah journal {\i Moriah}. Once the principle is understood, the response is immediate: this must be the {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shnayim Ochzim Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The foundation of Aumann\u8217's insight was already anticipated \u8212- at least partially \u8212- by the {\i Geonim} and by Rashi, who noted that the operative principle should be the one derived from the first {\i mishna} of Bava Metzia. In that famous case of {\i shnayim ochzim b\u8217'tallit} \u8212- two people grasping a garment \u8212- each claiming it entirely, the garment is divided equally. More precisely, what the {\i mishna} establishes is that what one side has already conceded to the other should be set aside, and only the contested portion is divided equally. If I claim half the garment and you claim all of it, I have implicitly conceded half to you. You receive that half outright; only the remaining half is in genuine dispute, and that is divided equally between us \u8212- yielding you three-quarters and me one-quarter. The principle, then, is: concede what is conceded, and divide equally only what is genuinely disputed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pairwise Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Aumann\u8217's critical insight \u8212- the step that eluded the {\i rishonim} \u8212- was that this principle must be applied not to all three women simultaneously as a group, but to every possible {\i pair} of claimants. In a three-claimant case, there are three such pairs: the 100 and 200 women, the 200 and 300 women, and the 100 and 300 women. Whatever amounts are assigned to any two of the claimants must satisfy the {\i shnayim ochzim} rule when those two are examined in isolation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Backwards Calculation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Working backwards from the {\i mishna}\u8217's answer of 50, 75, 75 in the case of an estate worth 200, Aumann demonstrates that the numbers are consistent. The woman with the 100 {\i kesuvah} receives 50, and the woman with the 200 {\i kesuvah} receives 75 \u8212- a combined total of 125. Consider those two women alone: if 125 were being divided between a claimant of 100 and a claimant of 200, the first 100 is disputed equally between them (neither concedes anything within that range), yielding 50 each; the remaining 25 is conceded entirely by the 100-woman (whose claim is exhausted), so it goes entirely to the 200-woman. The result is exactly 50 and 75. The same analysis applies to every other pair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pairwise Verification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verification proceeds across all three pairs. The women with 200 and 300 {\i ketubot} receive 75 and 75, a combined total of 150. If 150 is divided between a claimant of 200 and a claimant of 300, neither concedes anything \u8212- both have claims exceeding 150 \u8212- so the entire amount is in dispute and is divided equally: 75 and 75. This matches. Now consider the women with 100 and 300 {\i ketubot}, who receive 50 and 75, totaling 125. Of that 125, the first 100 is in genuine dispute between them; they divide it equally (50 each). The remaining 25 is conceded by the 100-woman and goes entirely to the 300-woman, yielding 50 and 75. Again, this matches precisely. The solution works for every pairwise combination without exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the case of the estate worth 300, the same verification holds. The 100 and 200 women receive 50 and 100, totaling 150. Dividing 150 between claims of 100 and 200: the first 100 is disputed equally (50 each), the remaining 50 is conceded by the 100-woman and goes to the 200-woman \u8212- giving 50 and 100. The 200 and 300 women receive 100 and 150, totaling 250. Dividing 250 between claims of 200 and 300: the first 200 is disputed equally (100 each), the remaining 50 is conceded by the 200-woman and goes to the 300-woman \u8212- giving 100 and 150. All pairs check out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uniqueness Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes the result publishable \u8212- and what transforms it from an observation into a theorem \u8212- is Aumann\u8217's proof that for any number of claimants and any estate size, there is always exactly one and uniquely one division that satisfies the pairwise {\i shnayim ochzim} rule for every possible pair simultaneously. It is something like a Sudoku: there are constraints, and those constraints determine a unique solution. The {\i mishna}\u8217's three cases, which appeared to follow unrelated rules, in fact all reflect this single underlying principle. One might add: it is striking that this explanation was apparently lost, that the {\i rishonim} could not reconstruct it, and that Tosafot itself acknowledges \u8212- in its laconic way \u8212- {\i lo ispareish shapir ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'hanei milta}: the rationale for these matters has not been well explained.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} focuses its critical attention on the second case, the one that resists obvious explanation. The {\i mishna} states that when the estate is worth 200, the woman with the 100 {\i kesuvah} receives 50. The {\i gemara} asks: why 50? She should receive only 33\u8531 ?. If we apply the principle of dividing equally what is disputed and conceding what is conceded, but we apply it to all three women together as a group, the reasoning would proceed as follows: on the first 100, all three have equal and total claims, so it is divided equally among them \u8212- 33\u8531 ? each. On the second 100, the 100-woman has already staked her full claim; only the 200-woman and the 300-woman remain as claimants, and they divide it equally \u8212- 50 each. The result would be 33\u8531 ?, 83\u8531 ?, 83\u8531 ? \u8212- not 50, 75, 75. The {\i gemara} thus implicitly accepts the principle of conceding what is conceded and dividing the remainder equally, but applies it group-wise rather than pairwise, and finds that the {\i mishna}\u8217's answer does not follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's First Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel offers an {\i okimta} \u8212- a contextualizing reinterpretation \u8212- to explain the second case. He says the {\i mishna} is dealing with a special situation: Mrs.\u160 ?200 has written to Mrs.\u160 ?100 a declaration that she waives any dispute with her ({\i din u\u8217'devarim ein li imach}) over the first 100. In other words, Mrs.\u160 ?200 has removed herself from the contest with Mrs.\u160 ?100 over that portion. With Mrs.\u160 ?200 standing aside, Mrs.\u160 ?100 is left dividing the first 100 with Mrs.\u160 ?300 only. Those two divide it equally, and Mrs.\u160 ?100 walks away with 50.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waiver Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: if Mrs.\u160 ?200 waived her claim against Mrs.\u160 ?100 in the first 100, then when we get to the second 100, shouldn\u8217't Mrs.\u160 ?300 be able to say to Mrs.\u160 ?200 \u8212- you have already removed yourself from this {\i kesuvah}, so you have no standing here either? And if the two of them do compete on the second 100, why do they each get 75, not 66\u8532 ? for Mrs.\u160 ?300 and 83\u8531 ? for Mrs.\u160 ?200?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers in Mrs.\u160 ?200\u8217's voice: {\i mi\u8217'din u\u8217'devarim hu de\u8217'siliki nafshi} \u8212- \u8220"I only withdrew from the legal dispute; I did not give her a gift.\u8221" The distinction is subtle. Mrs.\u160 ?200\u8217's portion in the first 100 was 33\u8531 ?, representing her contested claim as one of three competing creditors. She conceded her claim {\i against} Mrs.\u160 ?100 specifically, meaning she would not be counted as a competing force against Mrs.\u160 ?100. The effect is that Mrs.\u160 ?100 now faces only one competitor \u8212- Mrs.\u160 ?300 \u8212- over that first 100, and they divide it equally, yielding 50 to Mrs.\u160 ?100. Mrs.\u160 ?200 has not transferred her claim to Mrs.\u160 ?100 as a gift; she has simply stepped back from the contest. Now 50 remains. Mrs.\u160 ?200 and Mrs.\u160 ?300 stand in equal dispute over that remaining 50, dividing it 25 and 25 \u8212- giving 75 to each. This is the logic, though Tosafot itself notes, as mentioned earlier, that the rationale has not been fully and cleanly articulated within this framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Second Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the third case \u8212- the estate of 300 \u8212- Shmuel offers a second, entirely separate {\i okimta}. Here, it is Mrs.\u160 ?300 who has written to both Mrs.\u160 ?100 and Mrs.\u160 ?200 a declaration that she removes herself from dispute over the first 100 entirely. With Mrs.\u160 ?300 absent from the first 100, Mrs.\u160 ?100 and Mrs.\u160 ?200 divide it equally \u8212- each receiving 50. Now for the second 100, Mrs.\u160 ?300 has only waived her claim over the first 100; she re-enters for the second. All three divide the second 100 equally \u8212- another 33\u8531 ? each, but since Mrs.\u160 ?100 is already at her cap of 100, her claim is exhausted; in the contest over the second 100 between Mrs.\u160 ?200 and Mrs.\u160 ?300, they divide it equally \u8212- 50 to each. The final 100 is uncontested, going entirely to Mrs.\u160 ?300. The result: 50, 100, 150. Shmuel\u8217's explanation works arithmetically, but is candidly arbitrary \u8212- it posits an ad hoc legal fiction for each case with no unifying principle connecting them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Staged Seizure Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov from Nehar Pekod, in the name of Ravina, proposes a different framework for both cases \u8212- one that does not rely on declarations of waiver but on the physical reality of when property is seized. He argues: {\i reisha bishtei tefisot ve-seifa bishtei tefisot} \u8212- both the second and third cases of the {\i mishna} involve two separate acts of seizure, two distinct moments at which property is grabbed, each of which must be adjudicated independently. The {\i mishna} is not describing a beit din sitting down with a full estate and dividing it in one stroke; it is describing a scenario in which property becomes available in stages, and each batch of property seized triggers its own independent division.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Stages Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the estate of 200, Rabbi Yaakov says the property was seized in two batches: first, 75 was seized simultaneously by all three women, and then 125 was seized simultaneously. When 75 is seized and all three women have equal and total claims on it, it is divided equally: 25 each. When 125 is then seized, we apply the {\i shnayim ochzim} analysis to this batch. The 100-woman still has 75 of her claim outstanding, so within the first 75 of this 125, all three compete equally \u8212- 25 each. The remaining 50 of this 125 exceeds the 100-woman\u8217's remaining claim; she has no stake there, leaving the 200-woman and 300-woman to divide it equally \u8212- 25 each. The result per person across both seizures: Mrs.\u160 ?100 gets 25 + 25 = 50; Mrs.\u160 ?200 gets 25 + 25 + 25 = 75; Mrs.\u160 ?300 gets 25 + 25 + 25 = 75. This matches the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the third case, the estate of 300, the property is again seized in two batches: first 75, then 225. The first 75 is divided equally \u8212- 25 each. Within the 225: the first 75 is in equal dispute among all three (25 each); of the next 150, Mrs.\u160 ?100\u8217's claim is now exhausted (she has received 50), leaving Mrs.\u160 ?200 and Mrs.\u160 ?300. Rashi\u8217's treatment here is acknowledged to be descriptive rather than explanatory \u8212- he notes the outcome but does not fully derive why the 100 should come to be divided equally and the 50 given entirely to one party, and the underlying arithmetic of this final stage is difficult to reconstruct cleanly from first principles. Nonetheless, the conceptual thrust of the staged model is clear: the staged seizures force us to deal with the estate in segments, and each segment is governed by the {\i shnayim ochzim} principle applied to whoever retains an active claim at that moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The staged seizure model shares a structural intuition with Aumann\u8217's approach \u8212- both involve breaking the total amount into sub-units and identifying who has standing at each level \u8212- but Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's version is an {\i okimta} grounded in a specific physical scenario rather than a general mathematical theorem. The {\i mishna} according to this reading is not stating a universal rule but describing what happens when the circumstances on the ground happen to produce these particular seizure stages.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Rejects Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} now informs us: {\i tanya zu mishnat Rabbi Natan} \u8212- this {\i mishna} is the teaching of Rabbi Natan. On this, Rabbi \u8212- the redactor of the {\i Mishnah} itself \u8212- says: {\i eini ro\u8217'eh divrei Rabbi Natan b\u8217'elu} \u8212- I do not see the logic of Rabbi Natan\u8217's words here, or perhaps: I do not accept them. In place of Rabbi Natan\u8217's complex graduated division, Rabbi says: {\i ela cholkim b\u8217'shaveh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Authority Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a striking question: if Rabbi himself did not accept Rabbi Natan\u8217's ruling, why did he codify it as the {\i stam} \u8212- the anonymous, authoritative \u8212- {\i mishna}? It is a known phenomenon that Rabbi, as {\i mesader ha-mishna}, would sometimes record a minority or disputed opinion as the {\i stam mishna}, thereby lending it greater authority. But the question cuts the other way here: if the opinion is not his own, why give it that status? The text {\i tanya zu mishnat Rabbi Natan} might also suggest that this was a recognized independent collection \u8212- {\i mishnat Rabbi Natan} \u8212- perhaps connected to the tradition that Rabbi Natan\u8217's collection was characterized as {\i kav v\u8217'naki}, compact and precise. Whether Rabbi found the system conceptually valid but difficult to articulate, or simply chose to preserve an established tradition while registering his personal difficulty with it, remains genuinely unclear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Proportional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses the question of what Rabbi\u8217's alternative \u8212- {\i cholkim b\u8217'shaveh} \u8212- actually means. Rabbeinu Chananel rules that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi, so the entire elaboration of the Aumann solution, however brilliant, is {\i Torah lishmah} for practical purposes. As for the content of Rabbi\u8217's position: one reading of {\i cholkim b\u8217'shaveh} would be purely equal division \u8212- each woman receives an equal share regardless of her claim, up to the point her claim is satisfied. Tosafot rejects the implication that the larger claimant would receive no more than the smaller one, noting that {\i she\u8217'midat ha-din lo ken} \u8212- this does not conform to the measure of justice. Instead, Tosafot understands {\i cholkim b\u8217'shaveh} to mean proportional division in accordance with the size of each woman\u8217's claim \u8212- that is, the investment model applied universally. Each unit of {\i maneh} in the {\i kesuvah} entitles its holder to an equal share of that unit of the estate. Applied to the estate of 200: one-sixth of 200 is 33\u8531 ?, two-sixths is 66\u8532 ?, three-sixths is 100. Applied to the estate of 300: 50, 100, 150. This, according to Tosafot, is what Rabbi means, and it is the rule by which we rule.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Investment Partnership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Investment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes with the parallel case of three partners who pool their money \u8212- {\i hitilu la-kis} \u8212- contributing 100, 200, and 300 respectively. Shmuel draws attention to a related rule: {\i shnayim she\u8217'hitilu la-kis zeh maneh v\u8217'zeh matayim ha-sachar l\u8217'emtza} \u8212- two partners who invest 100 and 200 respectively divide their earnings equally. Shmuel\u8217's language \u8212- {\i ha-sachar l\u8217'emtza}, \u8220"the earnings to the middle\u8221" \u8212- is deliberately chosen. The equal division applies to profits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Indivisible Asset\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava qualifies Shmuel\u8217's ruling significantly: {\i mistavra milta de-Shmuel b\u8217'shor l\u8217'charisha v\u8217'omed l\u8217'charisha} \u8212- Shmuel\u8217's principle makes sense when we are dealing with an ox bought for plowing and used for plowing. The logic is that an ox engaged in agricultural labor is a single, indivisible asset. When it plows, it earns rental income as a whole unit. It is not the case that two-thirds of the plowing was done by Michael\u8217's two-thirds of the ox and one-third by my one-third \u8212- the ox plows as an ox. Therefore, while Michael has more capital at risk, the asset generating the return is indivisible, and the return it generates is treated as attributable equally to each partner\u8217's stake in that indivisible unit. This at least provides some conceptual grounding for Shmuel\u8217's otherwise counterintuitive rule, even if it remains economically strange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rava continues: {\i aval b\u8217'shor l\u8217'charisha v\u8217'omed l\u8217'tveicha} \u8212- but if the ox was bought for plowing and then the partners decided to slaughter it for meat, that is a different matter. Here, as Rashi explains, {\i omed} means not the original intention of the seller but the partners\u8217' own subsequent choice \u8212- they bought it for plowing but ultimately determined that slaughtering it was more profitable. Once slaughtered, the ox has been converted into a divisible asset: meat that can be parceled out, weighed, and sold in proportion. In that case, the division must follow each partner\u8217's proportional investment: {\i zeh notel l\u8217'fi ma\u8217'otav v\u8217'zeh notel l\u8217'fi ma\u8217'otav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamnuna\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna disagrees: {\i afilu shor l\u8217'charisha v\u8217'omed l\u8217'tveicha ha-sachar l\u8217'emtza} \u8212- even if the ox was bought for plowing and later slaughtered, the proceeds are still divided equally. His reasoning is that the nature of the partnership was established at the outset \u8212- they entered as partners in an ox bought for plowing, and that original characterization defines the partnership terms regardless of what subsequently happens to the asset. The intent with which the joint acquisition was made governs the division of whatever it yields.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plowing vs.\u160 ?Slaughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the dispute between Rava and Rav Hamnuna by invoking a {\i tosefta}. The {\i tosefta} states that two partners who invest 100 and 200 divide the earnings equally \u8212- which initially seems to support Rav Hamnuna\u8217's broader position. The {\i gemara} asks: is this not dealing with a case of an ox bought for plowing that was then slaughtered, thereby supporting Rav Hamnuna against Rava? The {\i gemara} pushes back: no, the {\i tosefta} is referring only to the case of {\i shor l\u8217'charisha v\u8217'omed l\u8217'charisha} \u8212- bought for plowing and used for plowing. The question then becomes: if the {\i tosefta} intended to address the case of an ox bought for plowing and then slaughtered, it should have said so explicitly and stated that even in that case the division is equal. The {\i gemara} resolves this: the {\i tosefta} is in fact implicitly consistent with Rava. Its equal-division ruling applies only to the standard plowing case; for the slaughter case, the {\i tosefta} itself provides that {\i zeh notel l\u8217'fi ma\u8217'otav v\u8217'zeh notel l\u8217'fi ma\u8217'otav} \u8212- each takes proportionally. The final ruling is that when an ox is bought for plowing and remains in that use, the earnings are equal; if it was bought for plowing but was then slaughtered, the division is proportional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafta Discrete Goods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tosefta} also addresses a further scenario: {\i lakach zeh b\u8217'shelo v\u8217'zeh b\u8217'shelo v\u8217'nitarevu} \u8212- each partner separately bought his own oxen with his share of the investment, and then the animals became mixed together so that it is no longer possible to identify which ox belongs to whom. In that case, even though the ownership is uncertain in fact, the proportional stakes are known \u8212- one partner owns a third, the other two-thirds \u8212- and the division follows accordingly: {\i zeh notel l\u8217'fi mamo v\u8217'zeh notel l\u8217'fi mamo}. Tosafot draws from this the broader principle that any investment structured around discrete, divisible goods is divided proportionally from the outset. The argument about equal division applies only where the partners genuinely entered a partnership in a single indivisible asset. Where the assets are inherently discrete and the investment is proportional in kind \u8212- whether it is livestock, goods, or money \u8212- all authorities agree that division follows the proportion of the investment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Investment & Wives Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investment Profit Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's final ruling on the investment case \u8212- {\i pichatu o hotiru kach hen cholkin}, if the investment lost or gained value they divide accordingly \u8212- is brought to bear on the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis. One might assume this refers simply to the normal case of a divisible investment that went up or down in value, with profits and losses divided proportionally in the obvious way. If so, it would be an unremarkable restatement. But the {\i gemara} raises the possibility that the {\i mishna} might be referring to something more unusual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intrinsic Value Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman in the name of Rabbah bar Avuha explains: {\i lo}, this is not about normal market gains or losses. {\i Hotiru} \u8212- \u8220"they increased\u8221" \u8212- means {\i zuza chadtiti}, the coins themselves became newer and thus worth more intrinsically. {\i Pichatu} \u8212- \u8220"they decreased\u8221" \u8212- means {\i astira de-tzunita}, the coins deteriorated to the point of being used as metal tokens to place under the heel for some medicinal purpose, indicating they lost their monetary value entirely. In other words, the {\i mishna} is describing a change in the {\i intrinsic value of the currency itself}, not merely a return on an investment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Principal Value Impact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is significant. If the partners had invested in an ox that earned rental income, and the question is how to divide that rental income, that is the case Shmuel addresses \u8212- and the division could be equal or proportional depending on the nature of the asset. But if what changed is the principal itself \u8212- the currency in which the investment was denominated went up or down in intrinsic value \u8212- then what is being divided is not profit from a jointly-owned indivisible asset, but a change in the value of what each partner actually contributed. In that case, since each partner owns his proportional share of the principal, any change in the value of that principal accrues or diminishes proportionally. The partner who invested 200 owns twice as much of the monetary principal as the partner who invested 100, and when the money itself changes in value, the gain or loss is distributed accordingly. This applies equally to the {\i mishna}\u8217's wives case: when the underlying value of the estate fluctuates at the level of principal, each woman\u8217's proportional stake governs the division of gains and losses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 94\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Wives & Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Wives Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the bottom of 93b introduces the case of {\i mi shehaya nasuy arba nashim umes} \u8212- a man who was married to four wives sequentially and then died. Unlike the preceding {\i mishna}, which dealt with the hypothetical scenario of a man who married multiple wives on the exact same date \u8212- so that all their liens were simultaneous \u8212- the present {\i mishna} addresses the normal, real-world situation in which the wives were married one after the other, creating sequential liens on the husband\u8217's estate. The governing rule is therefore straightforward: {\i harishona kodemes lashniya, veshniya lashlishit, veshlishit leriviit} \u8212- the first has priority over the second, the second over the third, and the third over the fourth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oaths Between Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When each wife comes to collect, the {\i mishna} imposes an oath requirement. The general rule is that anyone collecting from orphans \u8212- whether a creditor or a widow \u8212- must swear that none of the debt has already been paid. The rationale is that the orphans, had the father been alive, might have contested the claim and asserted partial or full payment; the orphans, particularly if minors, are not positioned to investigate their father\u8217's papers and press that claim themselves. Rashi assumes that the {\i mishna} here is dealing with adult orphans, who are in principle capable of making their own claims and examining the father\u8217's records \u8212- and therefore would not ordinarily compel an oath from a claimant arriving with a signed {\i shtar}. Nevertheless, the {\i mishna} establishes a distinct obligation: wife one must swear to wives two, three, and four, because those later wives can say that by exercising her lien and seizing property, she may be impacting assets that would otherwise flow to them. If part of her {\i ketubah} had already been paid, she would be seizing property that should rightfully reach them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the natural question of whether this oath obligation applies equally whenever any creditor collects and thereby affects other creditors with later liens. The answer may well be no in general, but the unique dynamic here \u8212- all the wives are widows of the same man, all their claims were triggered simultaneously by his death, and they all appear before us as a collective group \u8212- creates a relational context that generates the mutual oath obligation among them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fourth Wife Exempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i veriviit nifres shelo bishvua} \u8212- the fourth wife collects without any oath. Since she is last in line, no subsequent creditor is being harmed by her collection. The adult orphans, as noted, are in a position to press their own claims if they believe they have been wronged, so no oath is owed to them either. The fourth wife thus collects in peace, which might seem like a small consolation for receiving whatever is left at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Nanas Disagrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Nanas disagrees. His position is that the mere fact of being last in line does not exempt wife four from taking an oath. The {\i gemara} will clarify precisely why \u8212- but his objection rests on the possibility that one of the fields seized by an earlier wife may turn out not to have belonged to the husband, leaving an earlier wife\u8217's debt still uncollected and potentially requiring her to look to the fourth wife\u8217's field for satisfaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same-Day Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} addresses the scenario where all the {\i ketubot} bear the same date. Even where the marriages occurred on the same day, priority can still be established if the hour is known: {\i kol hakodemes l\u8217'chavertah afilu sha\u8217'ah achas zachsa} \u8212- even one hour\u8217's precedence is sufficient to establish priority. The {\i mishna} notes that in Jerusalem it was the practice to record the hour of the transaction in the document \u8212- {\i v\u8217'chach hayu kosvin b\u8217'Yerushalayim sha\u8217'os} \u8212- precisely to enable this kind of fine-grained determination. If, however, all the documents share the same hour with no further differentiation, and the estate is insufficient to satisfy all claims, then {\i cholekot b\u8217'shaveh} \u8212- they divide equally \u8212- with the detailed proportional rules of the previous {\i mishna} applying as needed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath Debate Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Framing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens its analysis with the question {\i b\u8217'mai kamifligi} \u8212- what precisely is the dispute between the {\i Tana Kamma} and Ben Nanas regarding whether the fourth wife must take an oath? Shmuel\u8217's answer, which Tosafot\u8217's reading of the {\i gemara} makes most sense, is this: the case at issue is one where it emerges \u8212- or where there is reason to believe \u8212- that one of the fields seized by wife one, two, or three was not actually the husband\u8217's property. The husband may have sold it innocently after purchasing stolen goods, or there may have been some other defect in his title. In any event, one of the earlier wives has seized a field that turns out not to have been validly his, meaning she has not in fact collected her {\i ketubah} debt through that seizure and will need to look elsewhere for satisfaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reads the scenario as one where this has already been established as fact. Tosafot, whose reading the lecturer follows as more consistent with the flow of the {\i gemara}, understands the debate as a general one: in ordinary circumstances, whenever wives one through three have taken their fields, does wife four need to take an oath because of the {\i possibility} that one of those earlier fields may turn out to have been problematic? On that reading, the debate is about the structural obligation in a normal case, not about what happens after a defect has been proven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying legal question that drives the debate is framed by the {\i gemara} as {\i u-va\u8217'al chov me\u8217'uchar she-kadum v-gava kamifligi} \u8212- they dispute what happens when a later creditor jumped ahead and collected, and it subsequently turns out that an earlier creditor had a superior lien. Does the later creditor get to keep what she collected, or must she return it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A vivid illustration: if a man died and two creditors both have liens on his estate \u8212- one lien dating back ten years, the other more recent \u8212- and the later creditor moved quickly and seized the only available field before the earlier one acted, can the earlier creditor come and demand the field back? Or does the later creditor\u8217's prior seizure give her a right to retain it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tana Kamma Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tana Kamma} holds {\i mah she-gava lo gava} \u8212- what the later creditor took, she did not truly take. She cannot keep the field against an earlier creditor\u8217's superior claim. Applied to the wives: if wife four seized the last available field and it subsequently turns out that wife two, whose earlier field was defective, has an unsatisfied claim with an earlier lien, wife four must surrender her field to wife two. Given that outcome, there is no reason for wife four to swear to the earlier wives. She can never hurt their interests by holding her field, because if any of them ever need it, they are simply entitled to take it from her. An oath would serve no function.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Nanas Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Nanas holds {\i mah she-gava gava} \u8212- what wife four took, she may keep. If wife two comes and says her field was defective and she has an earlier lien, wife four can respond that she already collected her field and is entitled to retain it. In that case, wife four\u8217's retention of the field does affect the earlier wives\u8217' ability to satisfy their debts. It is therefore appropriate to require her to swear that her own {\i ketubah} has not already been paid \u8212- because her keeping the field while potentially owing a debt to an earlier wife creates real harm to that wife\u8217's interests, and the oath is designed to address exactly that harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strip-Mining Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman offers an entirely different framing of the dispute. He holds that {\i d\u8217'chulei alma mah she-gava lo gava} \u8212- in truth, everyone agrees that wife four cannot keep the field if an earlier wife\u8217's lien proves superior. The dispute is not about whether she keeps the field, but about whether she will {\i tachsif} \u8212- strip-mine or devastate \u8212- the field while it is in her possession. Knowing she will eventually have to surrender it, she has every incentive to extract maximum value from it in the interim: selling off timber, exhausting the soil, or otherwise depleting whatever she can while the field is under her control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psychological Deterrent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing makes Ben Nanas\u8217's position both more surprising and more ingenious. According to Rav Nachman, the reason Ben Nanas wants wife four to take an oath is psychological: by requiring her to swear to the earlier wives that her {\i ketubah} has not been paid, we induce her to believe that she will be entitled to keep the field. After all, from her perspective, why would the court make her take an oath to the other wives unless she had some real claim to retain the field against their demands? She will reason that the oath is a precondition to keeping what she collected \u8212- and therefore she will handle the field responsibly, as a person who believes she will be its long-term owner, rather than strip-mining it as someone who knows she is a short-term custodian.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, as noted, a striking use of the oath mechanism. The oath is being imposed not because she is legally obligated to take it \u8212- everyone agrees she will have to return the field \u8212- but because making her take it creates a false impression that will deter destructive behavior. One might ask whether this involves a kind of deception. The response is that no real disservice is being done to the wife: all she is being asked to do is swear that she is entitled to collect what she is collecting. If along the way that oath causes her to believe she can keep the field, and if that belief in turn leads her to preserve the field\u8217's value, those are ancillary benefits. The oath itself is simply an affirmation of her legitimate claim to collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that Tosafot raises the broader question of why this kind of oath requirement \u8212- structured around the possibility of interconnected harm among multiple creditors \u8212- appears only in the context of a {\i ketubah} collection and not in ordinary creditor scenarios. The question does not receive a fully resolved answer, but the unique relational structure of the multiple-widow scenario, where all claims arise simultaneously from one death and all claimants are before us together, distinguishes it from the general creditor case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partners & Representation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Representation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a teaching by Rav Huna in the name of the elder Abaye \u8212- a {\i tanna} whose teaching frames the question of oaths to adult orphans \u8212- and then transitions to a related case. The teaching concerns {\i hanei trei achei vetrei shutfei} \u8212- two brothers who have inherited an undivided estate and are therefore effectively partners in it, or simply two partners in a jointly owned field. If a third party initiates a court case against the estate and only one of the partners appears to defend \u8212- {\i veazal chad mineihu bahadei ledina} \u8212- can the absent partner, upon learning of the outcome, say to the winning claimant: {\i at lav ba\u8217'al devarim didi at} \u8212- you never litigated against me, you only litigated against my partner\u8217's half, and I\u8217'm entitled to my own day in court?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna\u8217's position is {\i shelichusei avad} \u8212- the partner who appeared was implicitly acting as the agent of the whole estate, and the absent partner cannot subsequently disavow the result. The one who appeared in court represented the entire jointly held property, not merely his own half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not All Wives?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman, visiting Sura, was asked about this rule. He responded that it can be derived from the {\i mishna} itself. The {\i mishna} says wife one swears to wife two, wife two to wife three, wife three to wife four \u8212- but does not say that wife one must also swear directly to wife three and wife four, even though wife one\u8217's collection affects their interests as well. Why the omission? It would seem to be because wife two, when she receives wife one\u8217's oath, is implicitly representing the interests of wives three and four as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Oath Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately questions this inference. Perhaps the reason wife one need not swear separately to wives three and four has nothing to do with representation at all \u8212- it may simply be that {\i shvua le-echad shvua le-me\u8217'ah}, an oath made once is effective for everyone. The oath, once sworn, functions as evidence: it demonstrates to any interested party that the swearer would not have taken God\u8217's name in vain if she were lying. That evidentiary value does not depend on who was in the room when the oath was taken. There is no need to repeat the same oath to multiple parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} had initially explored the possibility that oaths operate more in the mode of personally satisfying the other party \u8212- giving them the subjective assurance of having received a direct commitment \u8212- in which case each wife would need her own oath. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution rejects this model: a single oath suffices for all, so the {\i mishna}\u8217's structure tells us nothing about implicit representation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context & Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of partners, the {\i gemara} refines Rav Huna\u8217's position. The rule that the appearing partner implicitly represents the whole is not categorical. Rather: {\i d\u8217'lo ite b\u8217'mata} \u8212- the rule applies when the absent partner was not in the city at the time of the court proceedings. {\i Aval ite b\u8217'mata, ibai leh l\u8217'meisei} \u8212- but if he was in the city, why didn\u8217't he show up? His presence in town and his failure to appear can be read as tacit authorization of his partner to represent the entire estate. The principle, then, is not a blanket presumption of representation, but a contextual one: one must look at the circumstances to determine whether the absent partner implicitly conferred authority on the one who appeared. If he was elsewhere and genuinely unaware, he may be entitled to his own proceeding. If he was present in town, the context suggests he acquiesced in the other\u8217's representation. As the {\i gemara} makes clear, these are not rigid categorical rules; context governs, and the de facto assumption is that each partner represents only his own share unless circumstances indicate otherwise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Same-Day Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now takes up a case structurally similar to the {\i mishna} \u8212- two claims on the same asset arising from the same date \u8212- but meaningfully different. In the {\i mishna}\u8217's scenario, both wives\u8217' claims are fully valid; the problem is simply insufficient assets. Here the question is different: two people present deeds of sale for the same field, both dated on the same day. Only one of them can be the true owner \u8212- the seller could not have sold the same field twice. One of the documents, in some sense, reflects a genuine transaction and the other does not, even if we cannot determine which is which.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is how a court should adjudicate this. Rav rules {\i cholkin} \u8212- divide the field. This is striking, because unlike the {\i mishna}\u8217's scenario, splitting here does not match any possible reconstruction of the facts. The field was sold either entirely to A or entirely to B; it was never half sold to each. Yet given that we have no way to determine the truth, Rav holds that division is the most judicious resolution available, even if it is guaranteed to be factually wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Guess\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel rules {\i shuda de-dayni} \u8212- the judges should exercise their judgment and award the field to one party. Shmuel\u8217's basic point is that the court should try to match what actually happened, even if it cannot be certain of success. Rav\u8217's split guarantees being fifty percent wrong every time. Shmuel would rather aim for the full truth \u8212- knowing the attempt might fail entirely \u8212- than settle for a compromise that is always partially incorrect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and Tosafot understand {\i shuda de-dayni} differently. Rashi holds that the judges should look at all available circumstances \u8212- whose story coheres better, who seems more credible, what other evidence can be assembled \u8212- and make their best informed guess. Tosafot, by contrast, understands {\i shuda de-dayni} as a genuinely arbitrary exercise of judicial authority: the judges simply award it to whomever they choose, without reference to any evidentiary considerations. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, the decision is essentially a crapshoot, and its legitimacy derives not from its accuracy but from the court\u8217's authority to resolve intractable disputes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav=Rabbi Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes that Rav\u8217's position aligns with that of Rabbi Meir, who holds {\i eidei chasima karsi} \u8212- it is the witnesses who sign the document who give it legal effect. The debate about {\i eidei chasima} versus {\i eidei mesira} originates in the law of {\i gittin}: is a {\i get} made effective by the witnesses who sign it, or by the witnesses who observe its delivery? Rabbi Meir holds it is the signatories. On that view, the operative legal reality of a document is entirely defined by its content \u8212- by what the signatures attest \u8212- not by when or to whom it was physically handed over. Since both documents in our case say the same thing and bear the same date, they carry identical legal weight. There is no extrinsic fact \u8212- such as which was delivered first \u8212- that could differentiate them. From Rabbi Meir\u8217's perspective, both documents took effect simultaneously and in contradiction to each other, so there is a genuine sense in which both parties acquired equal and simultaneous claims. Division is therefore not merely a fallback for uncertainty; it reflects an underlying legal reality in which neither party holds an unambiguous superior claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel=R. Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's position aligns with Rabbi Elazar, who holds {\i eidei mesira karsi} \u8212- it is the witnesses of delivery who make the document legally effective. On this view, what matters is not what the document says but when it was actually handed over. One of the two buyers received his deed before the other, and that buyer is the true owner. The documents\u8217' identical dates are legally irrelevant; the truth lies in the sequence of delivery. Since there is one real owner and one who is not, the court should try to determine which is which, rather than split what is in principle an undivided whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confirms this alignment through a ruling of Rav Yehuda: Rav Yehuda reported that Rav stated {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'gittin} \u8212- we rule like Rabbi Elazar in the laws of {\i gittin}. When Rav Yehuda reported this before Shmuel, Shmuel added {\i af b\u8217'shtaros} \u8212- not only in {\i gittin} but in all documents. This implies that Rav ruled like Rabbi Elazar only in {\i gittin}, not in documents generally \u8212- and therefore in the context of commercial deeds, Rav follows Rabbi Meir. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i ela machvarta, Rav k\u8217'Rabbi Meir u\u8217'Shmuel k\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- it is clear that Rav follows Rabbi Meir and Shmuel follows Rabbi Elazar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Citation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a challenge from a {\i braita}: {\i shnei shtaros hayotzim b\u8217'yom echad, cholkin} \u8212- two documents issued on the same day, divide. This appears to directly contradict Shmuel. Shmuel responds that the {\i braita} follows Rabbi Meir. But the {\i gemara} presses: if the {\i braita} followed Rabbi Meir, why does its concluding clause rule that the one who received the document first acquires the field \u8212- {\i zeh shemasar lo kanah}? That ruling reflects Rabbi Elazar\u8217's principle that delivery determines acquisition, not Rabbi Meir\u8217's. And yet even while operating under Rabbi Elazar\u8217's framework \u8212- where there genuinely is one true owner determined by sequence of delivery \u8212- the {\i braita} still says to divide. This poses a problem for Shmuel\u8217's position that under Rabbi Elazar you try to identify the real owner rather than split.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's ultimate response is that the question of whether to split or to exercise judicial discretion is itself a {\i tannaitic} dispute that runs independently of the Rabbi Meir / Rabbi Elazar debate. A further {\i braita} is cited involving a {\i shaliach} sent to deliver money to a party who had since died, whose sender had also died, leaving it unclear which estate the money belonged to. The sages say the agent should divide it between the two sets of heirs; the Babylonian tradition says the agent may do as he sees fit. This parallels the split-versus-decide question, demonstrating that both approaches have {\i tannaitic} support and that Shmuel can maintain his position as one legitimate opinion within that dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rami & Ukva Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Competing Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a concrete application. The mother of Rami bar Chama and Mar Ukva bar Chama \u8212- two brothers \u8212- wrote over her property to Rami bar Chama in the morning and then, the same halachic day toward evening, wrote it over again to Mar Ukva bar Chama. Each son presented his document to a different court: Rami bar Chama came before Rav Sheshet, who awarded him the estate; Mar Ukva came before Rav Nachman, who awarded it to him. Rav Sheshet then appeared before Rav Nachman to challenge his ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written Date Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet argued that Rami bar Chama\u8217's document came first \u8212- it was written and given to him in the morning \u8212- and therefore he should have priority. Rav Nachman rejected this: {\i atu be-Yerushalayim yasvinan de-kasvinan sha\u8217'os} \u8212- do we sit in Jerusalem where we write the hours into the document? Both documents bear the same date. We have no recorded indication in either document of when during the day the transaction took place. Since we do not record hours, we cannot go by who received it first; the two documents are on their face identical in date.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shuda De-Dayni Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman declared that he awarded the estate to Mar Ukva on the basis of {\i shuda de-dayni} \u8212- the judicial authority to decide between two equal claims. Rav Sheshet challenged this on two grounds. Rav Sheshet argued that he too had exercised {\i shuda de-dayni} when awarding the estate to Rami bar Chama, and therefore his prior ruling should stand. Rav Nachman responded with two counters. First: {\i ana dayana u\u8217'mar lav dayana} \u8212- I am a qualified judge and you are not. As Rashi explains, Rav Nachman was the supreme judicial authority of his generation, appointed and recognized by the relevant political and rabbinic institutions. The exercise of {\i shuda de-dayni} is not available to every judge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Limitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot articulates the tradition underlying this point with precision. The tradition, transmitted in the name of Rabbeinu Chananel, is that {\i shuda de-dayni} applies {\i davka be-mekarkei} \u8212- only with respect to land, not movable property. And it applies {\i davka dayan mumche ke-Rav Nachman be-doro} \u8212- only to the supreme expert judge of the generation, as Rav Nachman was in his time. Rav Sheshet, despite being an undeniably great authority, did not meet that standard. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, then, Rav Nachman\u8217's point is not merely that he outranks Rav Sheshet, but that Rav Sheshet fundamentally lacked the standing to invoke {\i shuda de-dayni} in the first place. The power is not a prerogative of any qualified judge; it is reserved exclusively for the singular supreme authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dovetails with Tosafot\u8217's broader understanding that {\i shuda de-dayni} is a genuinely arbitrary exercise of judicial power \u8212- not a reasoned best-guess, but an authoritative pronouncement whose legitimacy flows from the judge\u8217's supreme standing. Precisely because such an unbound discretion would be dangerous in lesser hands, it is restricted to the one judge in each generation whose stature and experience make it trustworthy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conscious Exercise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman\u8217's second response to Rav Sheshet\u8217's claim of having also exercised {\i shuda de-dayni} is equally important: {\i mei-ikara lav be-torat hachi asit lah} \u8212- when you awarded the estate to Rami bar Chama, you were not acting in the mode of {\i shuda de-dayni} at all. You gave it to him because you believed he deserved it \u8212- because his document came first, in the morning. You were attempting a normal legal adjudication, reasoning that prior receipt of the document confers priority. That was a mistaken judgment: since the hours were not recorded, we cannot go by the sequence of delivery. But it was an attempted legal ruling, not an exercise of {\i shuda de-dayni}. {\i Shuda de-dayni} requires a conscious, explicit invocation of judicial authority \u8212- the judge must be deliberately saying: I am empowered to award this to whomever I see fit, and I award it to X. That is not what Rav Sheshet did. One cannot retroactively reframe a failed legal ruling as an exercise of {\i shuda de-dayni}. The distinction matters: {\i shuda de-dayni} is a deliberate assertion of a judge\u8217's sovereign discretion, not a label applied after the fact to justify a decision that turned out to lack a proper legal basis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ambiguous Dates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Documents Compared\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents one final case. Two documents were brought before Rav Yosef. One bore the date {\i chamisha b\u8217'Nissan} \u8212- the fifth of Nissan \u8212- with full specificity. The other simply said {\i Nissan}, without specifying which day of the month.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific Date Wins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef awarded the field to the holder of the specifically dated document \u8212- {\i ukmi Rav Yosef le-hahu de-chamsha be-Nissan be-nechasim}. The logic is that the ambiguous document leaves open the possibility that the transaction it records occurred anywhere from the first to the twenty-ninth of Nissan. The holder of the ambiguous document may well have a claim that predates the fifth of Nissan \u8212- but equally, he may have a claim that postdates it. Given this uncertainty, his document places him in the weaker position, {\i at yadcha al hatachtona} \u8212- he cannot prove that his date precedes the other\u8217's. Perhaps his document was executed on the twenty-ninth of Nissan. The specific document establishes its date clearly; the ambiguous one does not. The specific claimant prevails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reimbursement Request\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The claimant holding the ambiguous document accepted the ruling but then raised the question of reimbursement. He had paid for the field, and now the court had awarded it to someone else. He proposed to exercise liens on other fields that the original seller had sold to third parties, arguing that even in the worst-case interpretation of his document \u8212- that it was executed at the very end of Nissan \u8212- he had a lien predating the first of Iyar. He therefore asked the court to write him a {\i tirfa} \u8212- a court order authorizing collection \u8212- from the first of Iyar onward, so that he could pursue those other buyers for reimbursement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buyer Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef explained that those other buyers could turn this request against the claimant. They could say: {\i at bar Nissan at} \u8212- you are a Nissan person; that field was actually sold to you in Nissan, and it is therefore still yours. The fact that a court awarded it to someone else does not change the underlying reality, which they maintain is that the seller validly conveyed the field to the ambiguous-document claimant. Since the field is actually his, he has suffered no real loss and is not entitled to reimbursement from us. This is a strikingly counterintuitive result: a court determination that he does not own the field is apparently not conclusive against third-party buyers who claim to know the true facts and use that knowledge to defeat his reimbursement claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Power of Attorney\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The solution proposed by the {\i gemara} is {\i harsha\u8217'a}: the two claimants must grant each other power of attorney. Specifically, the holder of the specific-date document \u8212- who was awarded the field \u8212- must grant the other claimant his power of attorney to pursue reimbursement from the other buyers. Here is the logic: the buyers insist that the field truly belongs to the ambiguous-document claimant, not to the person the court awarded it to. If so, it is the court-awarded party \u8212- who \u8220"officially\u8221" holds the field as far as the legal record is concerned \u8212- who has a cognizable reimbursement claim against those buyers. The ambiguous-document claimant cannot collect from them in his own name, because they say the field was really his all along. But armed with the other party\u8217's power of attorney, he can present himself as collecting on that party\u8217's behalf \u8212- and since the buyers acknowledge the court\u8217's awardee as the one who \u8220"lost\u8221" the field to them, they cannot deny his representative\u8217's claim. Through {\i harsha\u8217'a}, the ambiguous-document claimant can thus recover the value of what was taken from him, channeling the reimbursement claim through the party whose standing the buyers are forced to recognize.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 97\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow Selling Fields\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Introduction to Selling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with the word {\i keitzad} \u8212- \u8220"how does she sell?\u8221" \u8212- which signals that the Gemara is not merely continuing a narrative but is asking a structural question: what exactly is the mechanism by which a widow, dependent on her late husband\u8217's heirs for sustenance, goes about selling off their fields? This is daf 97a, and the scenario is one in which the heirs are failing to provide the widow with her {\i mezonot}, forcing her to take matters into her own hands by selling fields that belong to the estate \u8212- fields that could otherwise have been used to pay the {\i ikar ketubah} or to support the {\i yorshim} themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The complication is immediate and practical. The moment she remarries, her right to {\i mezonot} from the estate vanishes. If she has already sold a field to a buyer, and then gets married the following month, the heirs will turn to that buyer and demand the field back, arguing that the widow had no right to convey what was not truly hers to give. The buyer, naturally, is left in an awkward position. So the question of {\i keitzad mocheret} \u8212- how exactly does this sale work \u8212- is not a theoretical one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Daniel bar Rav Katina, a name that appears rarely in the Talmud, cites Rav Huna, the {\i Rosh Yeshiva} of Sura, as follows: {\i mocheret achat lishneim asar chodesh velokeach mefarnes achat lishloshim yom} \u8212- she sells enough to cover twelve months of sustenance, and the buyer pays her in monthly installments of one month at a time. The logic is structural rather than commercial. The buyer does not hand over the full value of the field at once, because doing so would immediately vest the property in him regardless of what happens to her {\i mezonot} rights. Instead, by paying month by month, the arrangement functions more like a rental with an option to purchase: each monthly payment covers one month\u8217's worth of {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the field, and the {\i yorshim} retain a lien on the property for as long as she remains entitled to {\i mezonot}. Should she remarry mid-course, the buyer returns whatever portion he has not yet paid, and the field reverts to the heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's language makes this explicit: {\i she\u8217'im tinasei yachazir mah shebeyado leyorshim} \u8212- if she remarries, he returns what remains in his hand to the heirs. The total sum, {\i kol hama\u8217'os}, represents the full purchase price of the field outright; the reason the buyer does not simply pay that sum upfront is precisely to preserve the heirs\u8217' lien against the eventuality of her remarriage. Once the full price has been paid out, the {\i yorshim} lose their claim entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda takes a more conservative position: {\i mocheret lishisha chodashim velokeach mefarnes achat lishloshim yom} \u8212- she sells for only six months at a time, with the same monthly payment structure. Where Rav Huna permits her to sell up to a twelve-month horizon, Rav Yehuda limits her to six months. In both opinions, however, the monthly installment arrangement is the same.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraitot & Ameimar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents a {\i baraita} in support of Rav Huna\u8217's twelve-month position, and separately a {\i baraita} supporting Rav Yehuda\u8217's six-month position: {\i tanya kavatei deRav Huna\u8230 ? tanya kavatei deRav Yehuda}. Having two {\i baraitot} supporting two contradictory opinions does not resolve the question of which is authoritative. Ameimar, however, rules: {\i hilcheta mocheret leshisha chodashim velokeach mefarnes achat lishloshim yom} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rav Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi challenges Ameimar: {\i veRav Huna mai?} \u8212- what do you make of Rav Huna? Both Rav Huna, the {\i Rosh Yeshiva} of Sura, and Rav Yehuda, the {\i Rosh Yeshiva} of Pumbedita, are contemporaries of roughly equal authority, and we have a {\i baraita} behind each of them. Why rule definitively like one over the other?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar responds: {\i lo shemi\u8217'a lei} \u8212- I have not heard it. The Gemara clarifies that this expression should be understood as {\i kelomar lo sevira lei} \u8212- that is, not that Ameimar was simply unaware of Rav Huna\u8217's ruling, but rather that he does not accept it. This is a recognized idiom in {\i Chazal}: because everything is oral tradition, saying \u8220"I have not heard\u8221" functions the way we might say in English \u8220"I don\u8217't hear you\u8221" \u8212- meaning, I don\u8217't agree. Ameimar is registering a substantive disagreement with Rav Huna\u8217's position, not pleading ignorance, and therefore {\i paskens} like Rav Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Liens & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next question is addressed to Rav Sheshet, and it is a genuinely intricate one: {\i mocheret lemezonot mahu shetachzor vetitrof lekhetuvatah?} \u8212- if a widow sells estate property in order to fund her {\i mezonot}, can she later return and seize that same property to collect her {\i ketubah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liens Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the difficulty, one must understand the mechanics of {\i shiabud} \u8212- the mortgage or lien that attaches to property in the context of debt. When a person takes a loan, all of his landed property becomes mortgaged to that debt. If he subsequently sells that property and then defaults, the creditor may approach the buyer and seize the land, leaving the buyer to seek reimbursement from the debtor. This is equally the case with a {\i ketubah}: when we write {\i ketubot}, we declare that everything the husband owns \u8212- even, as the formula puts it, the coat on his back \u8212- is mortgaged to the {\i ketubah}. If the husband dies insolvent, the widow may pursue property he sold during the marriage and seize it to satisfy her {\i ketubah}, leaving the purchaser to recover his money from the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah vs.\u160 ?Mezonot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical distinction here is that while the {\i ketubah} carries this power of {\i tereifah} \u8212- the ability to seize mortgaged property from a third-party purchaser \u8212- {\i mezonot} does not. A widow can sell property to fund her {\i mezonot}, but she cannot seize property as a lien for {\i mezonot}. The question is therefore whether this asymmetry creates a trap: she sells the field herself to obtain {\i mezonot}, the {\i yorshim} are bankrupt and cannot pay her {\i ketubah}, and she now wants to seize that very same field \u8212- the one she sold \u8212- to satisfy her {\i ketubah}. Is that permissible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is framed in terms of a ruling by Rav Yosef: {\i almanta dezabnah achrayut ayitmei} \u8212- when a widow sells land, the {\i achrayut} for that sale, meaning the obligation to make the buyer whole if the property is subsequently seized by a creditor, falls on the {\i yatmei}, the heirs of the original estate, not on her. The same applies to a {\i beit din} that sells property: the heirs bear the {\i achrayut}. This principle seems to suggest that she stands outside the transaction in a meaningful sense \u8212- she acted on their behalf, the responsibility rests with them, and therefore she should be free to step back in as a creditor in her own right and seize the property for her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Objections\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents two possible lines of argument, pulling in opposite directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first argument is that since the {\i achrayut} falls on the {\i yatmei} rather than on her, she is essentially acting as their agent in making the sale. Having not accepted responsibility herself, she remains a stranger to the transaction in the sense that matters, and she may therefore turn around and seize the property as a {\i ketubah} creditor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second argument, however, is the one the Gemara articulates with particular sharpness: {\i mineih de\u8217'achrayut de\u8217'alma lo kabalt alach, achrayut denafshach mi lo kabalt?} \u8212- You have not accepted responsibility for the general debts of the estate; but did you not accept responsibility for your own sales? The force of this objection is that the two transactions \u8212- selling the field for {\i mezonot} and then seeking to seize it for the {\i ketubah} \u8212- are both her doing. If she intended to preserve that field as collateral for her {\i ketubah}, she should not have sold it. Having sold it herself, she cannot now present herself as an innocent creditor exercising a lien. That is her business on both ends, and she should bear the consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is something intuitively compelling about the widow\u8217's counter-position. One might argue that from the beginning it was the heirs\u8217' obligation to provide her with {\i mezonot} and to honor her {\i ketubah}. If they failed in both, she was forced to act as their agent to feed herself, and she should not be penalized for having discharged their obligation on their behalf. The buyer, moreover, is not uninvolved in this calculus: anyone purchasing property that is known to be heavily mortgaged and sold under these circumstances is, in a sense, a venture capitalist \u8212- buying at a discount precisely because the title is uncertain, counting on the property eventually becoming fully his, and knowing that if it is seized he has a legal avenue to recover his funds from the party bearing {\i achrayut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheshet\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet resolves the question by citing a {\i tanna} who taught: {\i mocheret veholechet ad kedei ketuvatah} \u8212- she may continue selling fields for {\i mezonot} until only enough property remains to cover her {\i ketubah}. {\i Vesamach lah sheteigbeh ketuvatah min hashe\u8217'ar} \u8212- and she relies on collecting her {\i ketubah} from what remains. The {\i tanna} thus envisions a self-limiting principle: she has the ongoing authority to sell, but she must preserve a residuum of property sufficient to satisfy her {\i ketubah}. The grammatical subject of {\i samach} \u8212- who exactly \u8220"relies\u8221" \u8212- is uncertain in the text; the masculine singular does not obviously refer to her, nor does it clearly refer to {\i beit din}, which would usually take a plural form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eitza Tova or Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately raises the question of whether this {\i baraita} constitutes binding {\i din} or merely {\i eitza tova} \u8212- good practical advice. Perhaps the {\i tanna} is not establishing a legal rule but simply counseling wisdom: she ought to leave over enough property to cover her {\i ketubah} so that people will not call her unreliable, so that she will not acquire a reputation for doubling back on buyers. This echoes a theme from the prior day\u8217's learning, where {\i eitza tova derabnutah} was invoked in a similar context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leaving Fields\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resists the {\i eitza tova} reading. If the {\i baraita} were merely advisory, it would have been sufficient to say \u8220"and she collects her {\i ketubah} from the remainder.\u8221" The specific language \u8212- {\i vesamach lah sheteigbeh ketuvatah min hashe\u8217'ar}, with its implication of reliance rather than mere permission \u8212- suggests a binding legal rule. The inference drawn is: {\i shayra in, la shayra la} \u8212- if she leaves over sufficient property, she may collect her {\i ketubah}; if she does not, she may not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rescission of Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rescission Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now broadens its inquiry: {\i zevnah delo itzterichah lei \u8212- zuzei hadrei zevini o lo hadrei zevini?} \u8212- If someone sells property and it turns out that they no longer need the money for which they sold it, can the sale be rescinded? This is framed as a general question arising in connection with the widow\u8217's situation, though it quickly takes on a life of its own as the Gemara canvasses concrete cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oxen Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first example: a man needed oxen to work his land. Without livestock \u8212- or, in modern terms, without machinery \u8212- land in the ancient world was of limited productive value. He therefore sold a portion of his property to Rav Pappa in order to raise funds to purchase oxen. {\i Lesof lo itzterichah lei} \u8212- ultimately, it turned out he did not need the money; the oxen were cheaper than he had anticipated, or he found another source of funds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Piety\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ahadrei nihalei Rav Pappa lara\u8217'ei} \u8212- Rav Pappa returned the land. The natural assumption might be that this is what the law requires, but the Gemara corrects that: {\i Rav Pappa lifnim mishurat hadin hu de\u8217'avad} \u8212- Rav Pappa acted beyond the letter of the law, out of personal piety. He recognized that the man had sold land he did not ultimately need to sell, and out of generosity returned it. This is therefore not a proof for or against the legal rule; it establishes only that Rav Pappa was a {\i tzaddik}, not that the law compels rescission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Famine Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case is more instructive. There was a local famine in Nehardea. Rashi explains that the river rose, making it difficult for boats carrying grain to navigate, and the resulting shortage drove the price of wheat dramatically upward. In response, {\i zvinu kulei alma laafdanaihu} \u8212- everyone began selling off their {\i apadna}, their substantial homes, in order to purchase food. {\i Lesof atu chiti} \u8212- eventually, the grain boats did arrive, the artificial shortage collapsed, and wheat prices fell immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman ruled: {\i dina hu dehadrei apadnei lemarihu} \u8212- it is the law that those mansions should be returned to their original owners.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mekach Taaut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains the basis for this ruling: {\i hatam nami zvinei beta\u8217'ut havu de\u8217'iglai milta de\u8217'arba bekilei havah kayma} \u8212- this too was a sale made in error, because it subsequently became clear that the boats were already stationed just around the bend, waiting for the river to subside. Had the sellers known the boats were nearby, they would not have sold their homes. The transaction was therefore predicated on a false factual premise, making it a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi draws a careful distinction here. The ruling applies specifically because the boats were nearby and discoverable \u8212- their presence was knowable, even if the sellers did not know it. This is a different situation from one in which grain arrived from a genuinely unexpected source: for example, if a merchant from a distant region with a wheat surplus happened to arrive in Nehardea, flooding the market in a way no one could have anticipated. In that case, Rashi indicates, there would be no {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} and the sales would stand, because the change in circumstances was truly unforeseeable. The rule turns on the distinction between what was knowable and what was genuinely unknowable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buyer Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Shmuel raises a concern before Rav Nachman: {\i im ken nimtzeit machshilan le\u8217'atid lavo} \u8212- if you rule this way, you will end up creating obstacles for future transactions. Buyers, fearing that their purchases will be reversed whenever an artificial shortage dissipates, will decline to participate in such markets at all. In a time of famine, when people most need to convert assets into food, the very mechanism by which that conversion occurs will be paralyzed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman responds: {\i atu kol yoma betzorta shchicha?} \u8212- does such an artificial shortage occur every day? Do we really encounter these conditions so frequently that buyers will be deterred from ordinary commerce by the possibility of rescission? The other party in the conversation replies, yes, in fact, in Nehardea it does occur with some regularity \u8212- something about Nehardea\u8217's geography makes it susceptible to these supply disruptions. But Rav Nachman\u8217's point stands: the ruling addresses a specific and unusual situation, and the concern about chilling future sales does not override the basic claim of justice on behalf of people who sold their homes under conditions of artificial and transient crisis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Hilcheta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i hilcheta} is then stated: {\i zevin velo itzterichah lei zuzei hadar zevina} \u8212- if one sells property and it turns out that he did not need the money, the sale is reversed. This represents the standing law. The {\i hilcheta} formulas are, as is well known, the contributions of the {\i Rabanan Savorai}, the post-{\i Amoraic} scholars who edited the Talmud and appended definitive rulings to conclude {\i sugyot}; they carry full legal weight even if they arrive after the Amoraic discussion itself is concluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is one of legislated {\i rachmanus}: land was not mere commodity in the ancient world. To sell land was, in a sense, to give up a part of oneself, and the pain of that transaction did not disappear simply because the seller failed to announce his reasons at the time of sale. The fact that no explicit stipulation was made \u8212- that the sale appeared {\i stam}, without stated conditions \u8212- does not eliminate the equitable claim that a man who sold his land unnecessarily should be able to recover it upon returning the money.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kama Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} addresses the procedural requirements for a widow\u8217's sale of estate property. The {\i tanna kama} rules: {\i almana bein min ha\u8217'erusin bein min hanisu\u8217'in mocheret shelo b\u8217'veit din} \u8212- a widow, whether widowed from actual marriage or from {\i erusin} (engagement), may sell the heirs\u8217' property without the formal sanction of a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees and introduces a distinction: {\i min hanisu\u8217'in mocheret shelo b\u8217'veit din, min ha\u8217'erusin lo timkor ella b\u8217'veit din, mipnei she\u8217'ein la mezonot}. A widow from actual marriage may sell without {\i beit din}, but a widow from engagement may not \u8212- she must go through {\i beit din} \u8212- because she has no right to {\i mezonot}. The widow from marriage sells regularly because she has an ongoing need for sustenance, assuming the {\i yorshim} are failing to provide it. A woman widowed from engagement, however, has only a {\i ketubah} claim, not a {\i mezonot} claim; Rabbi Shimon therefore requires her to proceed through official channels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: the case of the widow from marriage \u8212- we understand why the {\i tanna kama} permits her to sell without {\i beit din}, since she has an ongoing need for sustenance. But the widow from engagement \u8212- what is the reason that the {\i tanna kama} permits her to sell without {\i beit din} as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla/Chen Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla answers: {\i mishum china} \u8212- because of {\i chen}. There is a {\i machloket} between Rashi and Tosafot as to exactly what {\i chen} means in this context, and the two interpretations yield meaningfully different analyses throughout the discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Chen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains {\i china} as follows: {\i sheyiheyu ba\u8217'aleihen lechen be\u8217'eineihen} \u8212- so that their husbands will be pleasant in their eyes, meaning so that the woman will retain a fond memory of the marriage. {\i Velo yimne\u8217'u nashim milinsa la\u8217'anashim} \u8212- and women will not be deterred from remarrying by the prospect of a cumbersome collection process. The idea is that if we make it burdensome for a widow to collect what she is owed, women may become averse to marriage altogether, knowing what awaits them if they are widowed. By streamlining the process, we make marriage a more attractive proposition and thereby serve a social interest. Rashi\u8217's reading has daughters in mind, one might say \u8212- a man ensuring that the women in his community do not face bureaucratic difficulty in their hour of need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretation becomes difficult in the continuation of the {\i sugya}, as will become clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan offers a different rationale: {\i lefi she\u8217'ein adam rotze shetitbaze ishto b\u8217'veit din} \u8212- because no man wants his wife to be embarrassed by having to appear in {\i beit din}. The leniency is not for her future benefit but reflects the {\i kavod} a husband feels toward his wife even after his death: the social norms of the community are such that a woman appearing in {\i beit din} to wrangle over money is regarded as undignified, and the institution is designed to spare her that indignity. This connects to a broader theme running through {\i Masechet Ketubot}, where there is a recurring concern that daughters not be reduced to going {\i bichozrot al hapetachim} \u8212- going door to door begging \u8212- and that the honor of families not be compromised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grushah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara identifies the practical difference between Ulla and Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ika beinaihu grushah} \u8212- the case of the {\i grushah}, the divorced woman. She too has a {\i ketubah} to collect, but she is neither a widow who needs to be encouraged toward remarriage nor a woman whose husband cares about her dignity in court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chen Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the view that the leniency exists {\i mishum china} \u8212- because of {\i chen} in Rashi\u8217's sense, to encourage remarriage \u8212- a {\i grushah} should also benefit, since she too may want to remarry and we similarly wish to make the process attractive for her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Embarrassment Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the view that the leniency exists because a man does not want his wife embarrassed in {\i beit din}, the {\i grushah} should not benefit: {\i grushah mai ikpat leh?} \u8212- what does he care about her? Once she is divorced, his concern for her dignity dissolves. There is no longer a husband who would be pained by her public appearance in court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grushah Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The very next {\i mishna} in the tractate states explicitly: {\i vegrushah lo timkor ella b\u8217'veit din} \u8212- and a divorced woman may not sell except through {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grushah Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that a {\i grushah} requires {\i beit din} seems to confirm Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position and to align with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's approach. However, the Gemara works through the implications carefully. If the {\i mishna} reflects the {\i chen} rationale, one would need to explain why a {\i grushah} does not need {\i chen}. The Gemara suggests: {\i almana min ha\u8217'erusin hu dela nafshuh lechen didah} \u8212- a widow from engagement has less {\i chen}, and it is for that reason that Rabbi Shimon requires her to go to {\i beit din}, while a {\i grushah} has more {\i chen} because she was involved in a real relationship \u8212- {\i aval grushah de\u8217'nafshuh chen didah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here Rashi\u8217's interpretation begins to strain. Why would a widow from engagement have less {\i chen} in the sense of needing encouragement toward remarriage? Rashi suggests she was not as emotionally attached to her husband in the first place. This is acknowledged to be somewhat forced. Rabbeinu Chananel, cited in Tosafot, offers two alternative readings. First, that {\i nafish chen didah} in the case of the {\i grushah} means she has a larger {\i ketubah} because she typically receives the {\i tosefet} in addition to the {\i ikar ketubah}, and therefore has more at stake in the collection process \u8212- giving her more need for the facilitation that {\i chen} provides. Second, and more persuasively, that a {\i grushah} is in a more socially vulnerable and stigmatized position than a widow \u8212- {\i hi bzuya yoter min ha\u8217'almana} \u8212- and therefore she has a greater need for {\i chen} in the sense of assistance and social support, not that she has more of it inherently. The phrase {\i nafish chen didah} then means she needs more {\i chen} rather than that she possesses more of it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Heirs Selling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i keshem shemocheret shelo b\u8217'veit din, kach yorsheha yorshei ketubatah mocherin shelo b\u8217'veit din} \u8212- just as the widow herself may sell without {\i beit din}, so too those who inherit her {\i ketubah} may sell without {\i beit din}. This rule extends the widow\u8217's procedural leniency to whoever steps into her position as a creditor with a {\i ketubah} claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male Heirs Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an immediate problem for the embarrassment rationale. If the reason a widow sells without {\i beit din} is that no man wants his wife to be embarrassed in court, why would the same exemption apply to her {\i yorshim}? One could argue that the concern extends to the {\i yorshim} as well \u8212- {\i ki heichi de\u8217'i\u8217'lei de\u8217'nicha lei de\u8217'titbazye yorshei nami de\u8217'nicha lei de\u8217'libazu} \u8212- just as he did not want his wife to appear in {\i beit din}, so too he did not want his {\i yorshim} subjected to the indignity of public litigation over money. On this reading, {\i bizayon} attaches to anyone \u8212- male or female \u8212- who has to go and haggle over an inheritance in court. It is simply beneath the dignity of the family.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grace Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chen} rationale, however, faces its own difficulty here. If the leniency exists to ease the widow\u8217's path toward remarriage or to maintain her favorable memory of the deceased, what does that have to do with male heirs? Sons who inherit their mother\u8217's {\i ketubah} claim have no need of the kind of {\i chen} being invoked on her behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Female Heirs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla resolves the problem with a {\i targum} \u8212- a reinterpretation of the case: {\i tirgmah Ulla kegon sheyirshtah bitah o achotah} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is referring to a case where the widow\u8217's heirs are her daughter or her sister. These female heirs do have a need for {\i chen} in exactly the way the widow herself did. It is not {\i banin dichrin} \u8212- the usual case of sons inheriting through the {\i ketubot} provision \u8212- but rather female relatives who have stepped into the widow\u8217's position as creditors, and who similarly benefit from having the collection process made accessible and dignified. The daf concludes here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 98\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Payment & Mezonot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a {\i mishnah} found at the bottom of 97b. The {\i mishnah} addresses the situation of a widow whose husband\u8217's estate is obligated both to pay her {\i ketubah} and to provide her with {\i mezonot} \u8212- sustenance \u8212- for as long as she remains a widow. The underlying structure of this arrangement is that the widow is entitled to be fed from the estate during the period of her widowhood, and her collection of the {\i ketubah} effectively terminates that entitlement. The {\i mishnah} further concerns her ability to sell estate property in order to realize payment of what is owed to her, and whether doing so requires the involvement of a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i makhra kesuvatah miktzatah} \u8212- if she sold her {\i ketubah} or even part of it; {\i mashkanah kesuvatah miktzatah} \u8212- if she collateralized her {\i ketubah} or a part of it; {\i nasanah kesuvatah le-acher o miktzatah} \u8212- or if she gave it as a gift, in whole or in part; then {\i lo timkor hash\u8217'ar ela beveit din} \u8212- she may not sell the remainder except through a court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} here proceeds on the assumptions associated with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position: first, that the only circumstance in which the widow may sell estate property without a {\i beit din} is when she is doing so to fund her {\i mezonot}, not to collect the base {\i ketubah} payment; and second, that once she begins to receive any payout toward the {\i ketubah} itself \u8212- even a partial payout \u8212- she loses her entitlement to {\i mezonot} entirely. With those two premises in place, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling follows logically: once she has begun collecting on the {\i ketubah}, any further sale is necessarily for the purpose of the {\i ketubah}, and that requires a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} may seem imprecise, because even the initial sale for the {\i ketubah} itself requires a {\i beit din} under Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view. What the {\i mishnah} is describing is a widow who has been selling property over time for her {\i mezonot}, and who has now crossed into the territory of receiving {\i ketubah} payment. From that point forward, two consequences follow: she no longer receives {\i mezonot}, and any further sale must proceed through the court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then cites what appears to be a continuation of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. A {\i baraita} makes explicit what Rabbi Shimon holds: even if she only sold or collateralized half of her {\i ketubah} \u8212- {\i makhra v\u8217'lo mishkan kesuvatah ela machtzitah} \u8212- she has nonetheless {\i avdah mezonoteha}, lost her {\i mezonot} entirely. This is the essence of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position regarding the relationship between partial collection of the {\i ketubah} and the ongoing right to {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} reject both of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's positions. Their view is stated in the {\i mishnah} as follows: {\i mocheret hi afilu arbaah vachamishah peamim} \u8212- she may sell even four or five times. This reflects their ruling that even when collecting the {\i ketubah} itself, piecemeal and repeatedly, she does not require a {\i beit din}. Furthermore, {\i umocheret mezonot shelo beveit din} \u8212- she may continue to sell for {\i mezonot} without a {\i beit din} as well. The crucial addition of the {\i chachamim}\u8217's position is that even after partial {\i ketubah} payment has begun, as long as the {\i ketubah} has not been fully paid out, she retains her right to {\i mezonot} and may continue selling for that purpose. She writes on the transaction record, as discussed in the previous daf, that the sale was for {\i mezonot} \u8212- {\i ha-kosevet limezonot machrati} \u8212- a formality whose purpose was analyzed there at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerushah Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes with a separate rule: {\i u-gerusha lo timkor ela beveit din} \u8212- a divorced woman may only sell through a {\i beit din}. The rationale is that the dispensation allowing the widow to sell without {\i beit din} \u8212- even according to the {\i chachamim} \u8212- is rooted in the concern that the husband would not have wished his widow to be subjected to the indignity of repeated court proceedings. That consideration applies to his widow, not to his divorced wife. Accordingly, the {\i gerushah} must transact through the {\i beit din}, and she does so only for collection of the base {\i ketubah}, as {\i mezonot} does not apply to a divorced woman.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miktzat Kesef Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution & Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens by identifying the {\i mishnah}: {\i masnisin mani Rabbi Shimon hi} \u8212- our {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Shimon \u8212- as demonstrated by the {\i baraita} which articulates the views of the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Shimon respectively. According to the {\i tanna kamma} (which may reflect the view of Rabbi Meir), once the entire {\i ketubah} is effectively collected \u8212- whether by sale, collateralization, or making it an {\i apotiki} \u8212- the widow loses her {\i mezonot}. Rabbi Shimon goes further: {\i af al pi she-lo machar v\u8217'lo mishkan kesuvatah ela machtzitah} \u8212- even if she only sold or collateralized half of it, {\i avdah mezonoteha} \u8212- she has lost her {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Payment Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then draws out the conceptual underpinning of the dispute. It states: {\i le-meimra de-Rabbi Shimon savar delo amrinan miktzat kesef ke-chol kesef} \u8212- this seems to say that Rabbi Shimon holds we do not apply the principle that partial money is like all the money. This phrasing is initially counterintuitive, since Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling seems to equate partial receipt with full receipt. The resolution is that the phrase refers to what remains outstanding: {\i miktzat kesef ke-chol kesef} means that as long as some portion of the {\i ketubah} remains unpaid, it is as though the entire debt is still outstanding. Rabbi Shimon rejects this principle \u8212- once some of the {\i ketubah} has been received, the outstanding balance does not preserve her {\i mezonot} entitlement. The {\i chachamim}, by contrast, affirm the principle: {\i amrinan miktzat kesef ke-chol kesef} \u8212- as long as part of the debt remains, the full weight of the outstanding obligation continues, and she retains {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Virginity Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises an apparent contradiction from a different context. It notes: {\i ha-ipcha shaminun lehu} \u8212- we have heard them hold the exact opposite positions. The proof comes from a {\i baraita} concerning the verse {\i ve-hu ishah bi-vsuleha yikach} \u8212- the {\i kohen gadol} must marry a woman in her virginity. The {\i baraita} records a dispute: {\i prat le-voggeret she-kalu visuleha divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- Rabbi Meir excludes a woman who has passed the age of majority, whose hymen has thinned with puberty. {\i Rabbi Eliezer ve-Rabbi Shimon makhshirin be-vogeret} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon permit the {\i kohen gadol} to marry her. This is a genuinely striking {\i gemara}, because the positions seem reversed: here it appears that Rabbi Meir holds that partial diminishment of the hymen is like total absence of it, while Rabbi Shimon holds that as long as some remains, it is considered fully present \u8212- the very opposite of their apparent positions in our {\i sugya} regarding the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Virginity Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The details of the dispute are elaborated further in the {\i baraita}. Rabbi Meir interprets the verse {\i bi-vsuleha} \u8212- with its possessive and expanded form \u8212- to require that {\i kol bisuleha} be present, the full and complete hymen. Under his reading, {\i ke-darkah ein she-lo ke-darkah lo} \u8212- even if she had only non-vaginal relations, as long as the hymen itself has thinned due to puberty, she is disqualified. His focus is entirely on the physical intactness of the hymen, not on the question of sexual experience. Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon interpret {\i besulah} in its base form to imply a complete virgin, while {\i bi-vsuleha} with its expanded construction means {\i afilu miktzat besulim} \u8212- even partial presence of the hymen suffices. Their interpretation shifts the disqualification to sexual activity itself: {\i bein ke-darkah bein she-lo ke-darkah} \u8212- whether vaginal or not, if she has had sexual relations she is disqualified regardless of puberty-related changes to the hymen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Analogy Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's introduction of this analogy may seem bizarre at first, but there is a genuine conceptual connection worth noting. The standard {\i ketubah} amount of two hundred {\i zuz} is traditionally associated with the {\i mohar} \u8212- the bride price for a {\i betulah}. The {\i ketubah} has its roots in the biblical institution of the bride price tied to virginity. There is thus an inherent connection between the intactness of the hymen and the amount of the {\i ketubah}: partial virginity maps, in a real sense, onto partial {\i ketubah}. The {\i gemara} may be reminding us of this deep structural echo \u8212- the transformation of an upfront bride price into a deferred insurance mechanism preserved the underlying logic that the {\i ketubah} reflects the status of {\i betulah} at the time of marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clarifying the Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite the apparent elegance of the analogy, the {\i gemara} ultimately rejects the cross-inference: {\i hasham bikra palgi} \u8212- there, in the case of {\i besulah}, the dispute is not about a general jurisprudential principle of whether partial presence equals full presence. It is a narrowly textual dispute about how to read the verse. According to Rabbi Meir, the base form {\i besulah} would have included even a partial {\i besulah}, and the expanded form {\i bi-vsuleha} signals that the full hymen is required. According to Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Shimon, {\i besulah} in its base form implies a complete virgin, and {\i bi-vsuleha} expands eligibility to include partial intactness. The disagreement is entirely about the exegesis of the verse, and cannot be extrapolated into a general principle governing whether partial payment functions as full payment in other contexts. The positions of Rabbi Shimon and the {\i chachamim} in the {\i ketubah} dispute therefore stand independently of what may appear to be their inverse positions in the {\i besulah} context.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Silver Cup Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a narrative. There was a certain woman \u8212- {\i hahi iteta} \u8212- who seized a cup of silver as partial payment toward her {\i ketubah}. This was not a {\i mezonot} transaction; she was beginning to collect the base {\i ketubah} itself. Having received this partial payment, she then came before the court seeking continued {\i mezonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case came before Rava. He ruled: {\i zilu havu lah mezonot} \u8212- go, continue to pay her {\i mezonot}. The partial receipt of {\i ketubah} does not sever her entitlement to ongoing sustenance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakhic Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava grounded his ruling explicitly: {\i leit dechash lah le-deRabbi Shimon} \u8212- nobody is concerned for the position of Rabbi Shimon. We rule in accordance with the {\i chachamim}: {\i amrinan miktzat kesef ke-chol kesef} \u8212- as long as a portion of the {\i ketubah} remains outstanding, the full debt is treated as outstanding, and {\i mezonot} continues. One might add that if the heirs wished to stop paying {\i mezonot}, the practical remedy was to pay out the full remaining {\i ketubah} balance \u8212- a choice they apparently preferred not to make.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shevuah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records that Rava\u8217's son sent a question to Rav Yosef: {\i mocheret shelo be-veit din tzerikhah shevuah o einah tzerikhah shevuah} \u8212- when a widow sells estate property without going through the {\i beit din}, as she is entitled to do \u8212- certainly for {\i mezonot}, and possibly even for the base {\i ketubah} depending on which authority one follows \u8212- must she take an oath? The concern would be that she swears the debt has not already been paid, ensuring that she is not collecting twice. As {\i Tosafot} immediately notes, the requirement for a widow to take an oath before collecting from the orphans\u8217' estate is itself already established in an earlier {\i mishnah} in {\i perek ha-kones}: {\i ein almanah nifra\u8217'at mi-nechasei yetomim ela bi-shevuah} \u8212- a widow may not collect from the orphans\u8217' estate except by oath. The question here must therefore concern something beyond that basic oath \u8212- as {\i Tosafot} develops at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hachrazah Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the framing of the question: {\i ve-tiba\u8217'ei leih hachrazah} \u8212- why not ask first about the requirement of public announcement? When property is sold under court supervision, a process of {\i hachrazah} \u8212- public advertisement and auction \u8212- is conducted to ensure the property fetches a fair price and protects the interests of the orphans. If the widow is selling without {\i beit din}, does she still need to conduct such a public process?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The questioner responds: {\i hachrazah lo kamiba\u8217'ya li} \u8212- I have no question about {\i hachrazah}. The reason is established by Rabbi Zeira in the name of Rav Nachman: {\i almanah she-shamah le-atzmah lo asata ve-lo kelum} \u8212- if a widow assessed the property for her own benefit, meaning she appraised it and then purchased it herself, the transaction is entirely void. The {\i gemara} asks: what are the circumstances? {\i Ei de\u8217'achriz} \u8212- if a proper process of public announcement had taken place, why should it matter that she was the winning bidder? If the auction was fair and open and she had the highest bid, who is harmed? The answer must therefore be that we are discussing a case where {\i no} public process occurred \u8212- {\i ela lav de-lo achriz}. And in that case, the rule is: {\i ha-la\u8217'acher mai de\u8217'avad avad} \u8212- if she sold to a third party without {\i hachrazah}, the sale stands. But she cannot sell to herself \u8212- {\i maan shamlich} \u8212- who was it that sold it to you? You cannot be both seller and buyer. {\i Lo yatzah karka me-reshut yetomim} \u8212- the land never left the orphans\u8217' domain. There must be two parties to a transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is thus reading the law as follows: {\i hachrazah} is indeed required in the context of a court-supervised sale, but the widow selling on her own authority does not require it \u8212- and if she did require it, she could then legitimately participate as a bidder. The law that her self-sale is void is therefore on different grounds: the inherent structural requirement of a two-sided transaction. This is explicitly stated by Rashi: {\i mimi kabalt mechirah zo? Lo mi-veit din, ve-lo mi-yasmei} \u8212- from whom did you receive this transaction? Not from the court, and not from the orphans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Sell to Self\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a striking observation: the widow, selling without {\i beit din}, has enormous latitude. She may select which portion of the estate to sell, she may conduct a private transaction without public announcement, and she may sell for whatever price she negotiates. The only firm prohibition is self-purchase. This understandably prompts concern about the orphans\u8217' interests. If she is ignorant of the property\u8217's value, or indifferent to the orphans\u8217' welfare \u8212- for instance if they are not her own children \u8212- the estate could suffer significant loss without any obvious remedy. At the same time, the {\i sugya} suggests the law may be structured this way precisely to incentivize the heirs to take proactive steps: going to {\i beit din} preemptively and establishing a structured arrangement, perhaps setting aside funds in escrow, rather than leaving the widow with unrestricted access to the estate\u8217's {\i karka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Assessment Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} illustrates the prohibition on self-assessment with a narrative. A certain man was entrusted with an item belonging to orphans \u8212- whether a flask, some coral, or fodder, the precise nature being uncertain. He was serving in an {\i apotropus}-like capacity as estate administrator. He assessed the item himself at 400 {\i zuz} and purchased it for that amount, directing the proceeds to the estate. The value subsequently rose to 600 {\i zuz}. The case came before Rabbi Ami, who ruled: {\i maan shamlich} \u8212- who sold this to you? The property never legitimately left the orphans\u8217' domain. The transaction was void regardless of whether the price he paid was fair, because a person cannot serve simultaneously as both parties to a sale. The item belonged to the orphans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses the nature of the {\i shevuah} at stake. The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is {\i tzrikhah shevuah} \u8212- she must take an oath. {\i Tosafot} points out that one reading would be that the oath concerns whether she has already been partially paid \u8212- ensuring she is not collecting twice in the course of selling off the estate piecemeal. She might be required to swear that the {\i ketubah} has not already been collected to a greater degree than she claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i Tosafot} in the name of {\i Rabbi} (the {\i Ri}) argues that the {\i shevuah} is not about overpayment at all \u8212- {\i lo ke-ferush ha-kuntreis shelo gavtah yoter} \u8212- it is not the oath that she has not collected more than she states. Rather, {\i ela miba\u8217'ei lei im mashbi\u8217'in otah shelo zilzelah ba-nechasim} \u8212- the question is whether we require her to swear that she did not deal carelessly or irresponsibly with the estate property. This reading is supported by the structure of the {\i gemara}\u8217's challenge about {\i hachrazah}: the connection between an oath and a public announcement process is not about prior payment but about the quality of the transaction itself. The {\i shevuah} and the {\i hachrazah} are both mechanisms for protecting the orphans against negligent or inadequate sale prices. The {\i gemara} concludes that {\i hachrazah} is not required, but an oath of responsible dealing is. Even so, the practical protection this affords the orphans is limited \u8212- an oath that one acted to the best of one\u8217's knowledge does not guarantee a competent or well-advised transaction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oversell/Undersell Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Undersell Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} addresses two scenarios of improperly calibrated sales. The first: {\i almanah she-hayetah ketuvatah matayim u-mocheret shaveh maneh be-matayim} \u8212- a widow whose {\i ketubah} was 200 sold a piece of estate property worth 100 for 200. In this case, {\i nigveta ketuvatah} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} is considered fully paid. She got 200; she was owed 200. The fact that the property was worth only half what she received is irrelevant \u8212- the debt is discharged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse is then stated: {\i o shavim matayim be-maneh} \u8212- or she sold property worth 200 for only 100. Again, {\i nigveta ketuvatah} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} is considered fully paid. Even though she undersold and received less cash than the property was worth, she is done. She sold a piece of the estate valued at the full amount of her {\i ketubah}, and the estate has discharged its obligation in the form of the property transferred, even if she made a bad deal in converting it to cash.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oversell Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario is different in kind: {\i hayetah ketuvatah maneh ve-makhra shaveh maneh ve-dinar be-maneh} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} was 100, and she sold a piece of the estate worth 125 for 100. Here, {\i mekherah batel} \u8212- the sale is entirely void. She sold property worth more than her outstanding debt. She was authorized to sell in order to collect what was owed to her, not to sell estate property of greater value. And even if she offers to return the excess \u8212- {\i afilu hi omeret akhzir et ha-dinar la-yorshim} \u8212- to give back the 25 to the heirs, whether in cash or by purchasing replacement property for them, the {\i mishnah} still rules the sale void. She sold property she was not authorized to sell, and that invalidity is not cured by a subsequent offer of restitution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel qualifies this last ruling: {\i le-olam mekhra kayam ad she-tehe sham kedei she-tishtayer ba-sadeh bat tish\u8217'ah kabbin} \u8212- the sale is always valid unless the overage constitutes a separate and independently viable parcel of land \u8212- specifically, a field of nine {\i kav} (or, for a garden, a half {\i kav}, and per Rabbi Akiva, a quarter {\i kav}). His logic is that absolute precision in the size of property sold is not always possible. A seller cannot realistically draw a line through the middle of a field to sell exactly the square footage corresponding to her debt. Therefore, modest overage is acceptable \u8212- the law does not require impossible precision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel draws the line at overage large enough to constitute an independently subdivisible parcel. If the extra portion is sufficiently large that it {\i could} have been sold as a separate piece of land, then the seller had no excuse for not subdividing and should have done so. The criterion is not monetary value but physical acreage, for the reason that what determines whether a parcel is independently viable is its size, not its per-unit price. Notably, the principle regarding the quality of land ({\i ziburit}) is not at issue in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Sales\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then extends the analysis to a case involving sequential sales: {\i hayetah ketuvatah arba me\u8217'ah zuz u-machru la-zeh be-maneh, ula-zeh be-maneh, ula-acharon yafeh maneh ve-dinar be-maneh} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} was 400 {\i zuz}, and she sold to one buyer a field worth 100, to a second a field worth 100, to a third a field worth 100, and to a fourth, a field worth 125 for 100. The ruling is {\i acharon batel ve-shel kulan kayam} \u8212- only the last sale is void; all the prior sales are valid. She had a 400 {\i zuz} debt, she had collected 300 {\i zuz} worth of legitimate sales, and the final sale exceeded her remaining entitlement of 100 by 25. The invalidity attaches only to the transaction that overstepped her authority, while the preceding sales, which were within her rights, stand.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Undersell/Oversell Analy\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 At Hifsadet Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} analyzes the rulings of the {\i mishnah}. The first question is why, when she sells property worth 200 for only 100, her {\i ketubah} is considered discharged. The answer: {\i de-amri la at hifsadet} \u8212- they say to her, you suffered the loss. The estate delivered to her a piece of property valued at her full {\i ketubah}; whatever she received in cash for it is her business. She chose to undersell, and the estate has done its part by having property of the requisite value available for her to collect. This also provides a practical incentive: the widow has every reason to ensure she is not underselling. While she may not be motivated to drive the price higher than necessary, she certainly has reason not to be cheated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why the reverse does not apply symmetrically. When she sells property worth 100 for 200 \u8212- a genuinely good deal \u8212- why can she not argue {\i ana arvachna} \u8212- I only took a piece of the estate worth 100, and the extra 100 I received was the product of my own business acumen? If the standard for underselling is based on the size of the property transferred, why not apply the same logic to overselling, treating the surplus as a personal gain separate from the {\i ketubah} payment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakol Leba\u8217'al Hama\u8217'ot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman citing Rav bar Avuha answers: {\i kan shana Rabbi, hakol le-ba\u8217'al hama\u8217'ot} \u8212- here the rule is that everything goes to the one with the money. The principle is that when a transaction is conducted on someone else\u8217's behalf \u8212- when the money in question belongs to the person who sent you to the market \u8212- any surplus generated through that transaction accrues to the owner of the money, not to the one conducting the transaction. The widow is not selling her own property; she is selling estate property, collecting against a debt. The extra money she received above the property\u8217's value is part of the price realized for the estate\u8217's asset, and it goes toward payment of her {\i ketubah} debt. She cannot claim it as a personal windfall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Fixed Amounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited that complicates this picture. The case concerns an agent sent to purchase goods: {\i hosifu lo achat yeteirah} \u8212- if the seller added an extra item. Rabbi Yehuda says it belongs to the agent \u8212- {\i hakol la-shaliach} \u8212- it was a gift to him personally. Rabbi Yossi says they split it \u8212- {\i cholkin}. But a separate {\i baraita} records Rabbi Yossi as saying {\i hakol le-ba\u8217'al hama\u8217'ot} \u8212- all of it goes to the one who provided the money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya \u8212- kan be-davar she-yesh lo kitzva, kan be-davar she-ein lo kitzva} \u8212- there is no difficulty. The cases differ. When there is a fixed, standard price for the item \u8212- a fixed quantity at a fixed rate \u8212- and the seller throws in an extra unit, that extra is a gift. It is not part of the agreed transaction; it is a bonus extended to the agent personally, and the agent may keep it or share it. But when the amount is not fixed \u8212- when the quantity or price is negotiable \u8212- then the extra is not a gift but part of the negotiated transaction, and it accrues to the person whose money funded the purchase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Sales Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application to the widow\u8217's case is then made explicit. The sale of land is always {\i ein lo kitzva} \u8212- there is no fixed price. Land is always subject to negotiation; there is no standard per-unit price at which real estate trades. Therefore, any surplus price she receives above the assessed value of the property is not a personal bonus to her \u8212- it is part of the negotiated price for that piece of land. The money all goes toward discharge of the {\i ketubah} debt. Accordingly, when she sells property worth 100 for 200, the entire 200 is treated as having been received by the estate against her {\i ketubah} claim, and her {\i ketubah} is discharged. Rav Pappa states the {\i halakha}: for items with a fixed price, they split the bonus between the agent and the owner; for items without a fixed price, {\i hakol le-ba\u8217'al hama\u8217'ot} \u8212- it all goes to the party who provided the money.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agent Excess Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agent Excess Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a question that circles back to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case of the widow who oversold. The scenario: {\i amri lei zavin li litkha} \u8212- you instruct your agent to go purchase for you a {\i litkha}, half a {\i kor} of grain. {\i Ve-azil ve-zavin leh kura} \u8212- and he goes and purchases a full {\i kor}. What is the legal status of this transaction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two possibilities are presented: {\i mosif al devarav havei} \u8212- perhaps he is considered to have merely added to his instructions. On this reading, the first half of the purchase \u8212- the {\i litkha} \u8212- was conducted as a valid agent acting within his authority, and that half is a binding transaction. The second half he exceeded his mandate, and on that portion he acted on his own account. Alternatively, {\i ma\u8217'avir al devarav havei} \u8212- perhaps by buying more than he was told, he has completely disregarded his instructions, and is therefore considered to have acted entirely on his own initiative for the {\i entire} purchase. On this reading, even the first {\i litkha} is not a valid agency transaction, and {\i ve-litkha nami lo kani} \u8212- he has not even validly acquired the half he was authorized to acquire.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'ilah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve this question, the {\i gemara} turns to the laws of {\i me\u8217'ilah} \u8212- misappropriation of consecrated property. The principle of {\i me\u8217'ilah} applies only {\i be-shogeg}, unwittingly; one who knowingly benefits from {\i hekdesh} property does not bring a {\i korban me\u8217'ilah} though other penalties may apply. One of the distinctive features of {\i me\u8217'ilah} is that it is among the few areas in which we apply {\i yeish shliach le-davar aveirah} \u8212- an agent\u8217's unwitting transgression on behalf of an equally unwitting principal is attributed to the principal. If both the one who sent the agent and the agent are {\i shogeg}, the {\i me\u8217'ilah} is treated as though committed by the sender.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meat Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevant case from {\i me\u8217'ilah} law: {\i amar ba\u8217'al ha-bayit li-shlucho} \u8212- a homeowner says to his agent, {\i ten lahem chaticha la-orchim} \u8212- give each guest one piece of meat. The meat is {\i kodashim}. The agent instead says to the guests: {\i telu shtayim} \u8212- each take two. The guests then take three pieces each. The ruling: {\i kulam ma\u8217'alu} \u8212- all of them have committed {\i me\u8217'ilah}. The homeowner is liable for the first piece, since that was within his agency. The agent is liable for the second piece, since that was on his own initiative. The guests are liable for the third piece, since that exceeded even what the agent authorized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then explains precisely when this apportionment applies. The case assumes that the agent explicitly distinguished between the two pieces: {\i telu achat mi-da\u8217'ato shel ba\u8217'al ha-bayit ve-achat mi-da\u8217'ati} \u8212- take one because the owner authorizes it, and take another because I say so. That explicit attribution is what creates the clean division of liability. If, by contrast, the agent simply said {\i telu shtayim} without any such distinction \u8212- presenting the double allocation as the owner\u8217's instruction \u8212- he would be treated as having completely overridden his agency, and the homeowner would bear no {\i me\u8217'ilah} liability at all, even for the first piece. The agent, by claiming to act for the owner while wildly exceeding his mandate, is considered to have been acting entirely on his own initiative. The {\i gemara} thus establishes: when an agent straightforwardly adds to what he was told, the first part remains valid agency and the excess is on his own account. But when the agent presents his excessive action as authorized \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'avir al devarav} \u8212- he has abandoned his agency entirely and acts solely for himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof from Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now attempts to bring proof from our {\i mishnah} to resolve the original question. The case is: {\i hayetah ketuvatah maneh ve-makhra shaveh maneh ve-dinar be-maneh} \u8212- her {\i ketubah} was 100 and she sold property worth 125 for 100. The {\i mishnah} rules {\i mekherah batel} \u8212- the entire sale is void. The {\i gemara} reads this as: she actually sold the property worth 125 for 125 \u8212- the phrase {\i be-maneh} means she sold it in exchange for the discharge of her 100 {\i maneh} debt \u8212- and the question is why the sale is entirely void rather than valid for the first 100 worth of property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} suggests: {\i mai lav de-zavin shaveh maneh ve-dinar be-maneh ve-dinar} \u8212- is this not a case where she sold a property worth 125 for 125, and the only issue is that she sold 25 more than she was authorized to? If the principle were {\i mosif al devarav} \u8212- exceeding one\u8217's authority only invalidates the excess \u8212- then the sale of the first 100 should remain valid. Yet the {\i mishnah} rules the entire sale void. This would appear to prove that exceeding one\u8217's authority \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'avir al devarav} \u8212- invalidates the entire transaction, including the portion that was within the agent\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna son of Rav Natan rejects this proof. The {\i mishnah} is not discussing a case where she sold the property at fair value and simply took an oversized parcel. Rather, {\i be-doshaya} \u8212- the case in the {\i mishnah} is one of underselling: the property was worth 125 and she sold it for only 100. The reason the sale is void is not that she overreached her agency by taking too large a parcel at a fair price, but rather that she undersold a piece of property she was not fully authorized to sell, and the orphans are left worse off. The voiding of the sale is therefore a remedy for the loss caused to the orphans by the inadequate price, not a ruling about agency doctrine. Consequently, our {\i mishnah} provides no proof one way or the other about the question of whether {\i mosif al devarav} leaves the authorized portion intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus leaves the original question unresolved. If the widow sold a piece of property of appropriate size at a fair price, but the parcel happened to be slightly larger than the precise value of her outstanding {\i ketubah} \u8212- in a manner that exceeds her authorized mandate but does not prejudice the estate through underselling \u8212- it remains uncertain whether the portion she was authorized to sell is valid while only the overage is void, or whether the entire transaction is invalidated because she exceeded her authority from the outset. This question continues into the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 99\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agency Boundaries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agent Mandate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 99a opens with a question that sits at the heart of agency law: under what conditions is a {\i shaliach} considered to be operating within the terms of his mandate, and at what point has he gone rogue and acted independently? The practical stakes are significant. When an agent buys or sells property on behalf of the person who appointed him, the question of whether those transactions are binding depends entirely on whether he was acting as an agent or on his own account. If he was told to sell land worth a hundred and he sold land worth fifty, or conversely he was told to sell fifty and he sold a hundred, do we say there is at least a zone of overlap in which he was genuinely functioning as an agent? Or do we say that because he deviated from his instructions, the entire transaction is void and he was acting only for himself?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question matters not only for the validity of commercial transactions but also for the parties\u8217' exposure to liability. The person who appointed the agent expects that what was done in his name is binding on him \u8212- but only to the extent he authorized it. The agent expects that his actions carry legal weight as an extension of his principal. When those expectations diverge, the {\i gemara} must draw lines.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that related discussions appear in tractate {\i Kiddushin}, where a {\i shaliach} is sent to betroth a woman for a specified sum of money and a dispute arises over whether the agent was authorized to act in a general or a precisely specified capacity. The underlying conceptual tension is the same, though the {\i sugyot} are scattered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meila & Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws in the laws of {\i meila} \u8212- misuse of {\i hekdesh}, sanctified property \u8212- as a productive test case for the limits of agency. Normally, the rule is {\i ein shaliach l\u8217'dvar aveira}, that an agent cannot be used to perform a transgression on one\u8217's behalf. But {\i meila} is an exception: the law of {\i yesh shaliach l\u8217'dvar aveira} applies here, at least in cases of unwitting misuse. If both the sender and the agent are unaware that the object is {\i hekdesh}, then the person who sent the agent is the one deemed to have committed {\i meila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This makes {\i meila} cases a particularly sharp testing ground: who bears the culpability depends directly on whose agency was operative at the moment of the misuse. If the agent deviated from his instructions, the question of whether he was still acting for the sender \u8212- or had stepped outside the bounds of agency entirely \u8212- determines which of them, or both, has transgressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overselling/Underselling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concrete scenario that sets up the discussion involves an agent authorized to sell (or buy) a specific quantity or value and who either exceeds or falls short of that authorization. You tell your agent: sell a half-acre field. He sells an acre. Or: go buy fifty {\i dinar} worth of goods, and he buys a hundred. In each direction, the question is the same \u8212- is there a binding overlap, or is the deviation total? The {\i gemara} will work through these variations systematically, using both the case of the woman collecting her {\i ketuba} and the {\i meila} cases to probe the answer.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Selling Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman as Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the {\i mishna} of our {\i daf}, which concerns a woman who sells from the estate of her deceased husband\u8217's heirs in order to collect the outstanding balance of her {\i ketuba}. She was not formally appointed by anyone, yet she is treated as a kind of implicit agent \u8212- appointed, as it were, by rabbinic authority or by the logic of the {\i beit din} \u8212- acting on behalf of the orphans, even though they may not wish her to be doing so. The {\i mishna}\u8217's case involves her selling property, and the question is whether her sale is binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why the {\i gemara} frames her role in terms of {\i shlichut} at all, since a creditor does not act as agent but simply seizes collateral through the court. The answer lies in the language the {\i mishna} uses: she sells {\i shelo b\u8217'beit din}, outside of court \u8212- language that is inapplicable to a creditor who exercises a lien through formal judicial process. Moreover, if she sells a piece of land worth two hundred for three hundred, the excess goes toward satisfying her debt. A creditor who seizes property and then sells it at a profit does not thereby reduce the underlying debt. The proceeds of her sale go to pay off what she is owed. That structure is characteristic of agency, not of simple debt-collection. The {\i gemara} therefore conceptualizes her as a {\i shaliach} of the heirs \u8212- a somewhat paradoxical framing, given that the heirs certainly did not appoint her \u8212- but that is the conceptual framework the {\i gemara} adopts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This arrangement is particularly associated with {\i mezonot}, the ongoing obligation to provide for one\u8217's widow from the estate. Rather than going to court once to collect a fixed sum, she may periodically need to sell off small portions of the estate to cover her living expenses. This recurring character of the transaction explains why the {\i mishna} thinks in terms of agency rather than one-time judicial seizure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overselling vs Underpric\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} contains a case that the {\i gemara} initially understands as involving a sale of too much land: she was only authorized to sell enough to satisfy one hundred {\i maneh} of outstanding debt, yet she sold property worth one hundred and twenty-five for one hundred and twenty-five. The {\i mishna} declares this void. The {\i gemara}\u8217's first reading is that this proves something about the rule for {\i shlichut}: that a {\i shaliach} who oversteps her mandate is treated as having acted entirely on her own \u8212- not merely for the excess, but for the whole transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna son of Rav Natan then reframes the case entirely. The {\i mishna} is not talking about overselling in terms of quantity; rather, she sold property worth one hundred and twenty-five for only one hundred. The problem is not that she sold too much \u8212- it is that she undersold. She had no right to sell at a loss that would be borne by the estate, and that is why the sale is void. This reading changes the evidentiary import of the case dramatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Mishna Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the end of the {\i mishna} is read carefully, Rav Huna\u8217's reframing becomes not only plausible but necessary. The {\i mishna} closes with an explicit case: her {\i ketuba} was four hundred {\i zuz}; she sold to each of three buyers for one hundred {\i maneh} each; and to the last buyer she sold property worth one hundred and twenty-five for one hundred. The {\i mishna} declares: the last sale is void, the earlier ones are binding. That closing case is unambiguously a case of underselling. The {\i gemara} therefore reasons: {\i mid\u8217'seifa bid\u8217'ozil ha\u8217'rei reisha bid\u8217'lo ozil} \u8212- since the end of the {\i mishna} is clearly about underselling, the beginning need not be about underselling at all. The beginning must therefore be about a different problem: she sold more land than she was entitled to sell \u8212- namely, property worth one hundred and twenty-five when her outstanding debt was only one hundred. The two cases are thus distinguished: the first case is about exceeding the scope of her authorization by quantity, the second is about underselling at a price that shifts the loss onto the estate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Underpricing Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Takes Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the {\i mishna}\u8217's first and last cases are distinct, the {\i gemara} now confronts an apparent difficulty. The first case of the {\i mishna} includes the scenario where her {\i ketuba} was two hundred and she sold property worth two hundred for one hundred \u8212- that is, she undersold it \u8212- and the {\i mishna} declares her {\i ketuba} satisfied. How can this be valid if, as the last case shows, underselling is void?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is a fundamental distinction between who bears the loss. In the first case, the outstanding debt is two hundred, the land was worth two hundred, and she sold it for one hundred. She undersold, but there is no injustice to the estate: she simply takes the hit herself. The estate does not suffer. The property she sold was worth what she was owed, and the shortfall in the sale price is simply deducted from the debt as if she had been paid the full value. She pocketed one hundred but we consider her {\i ketuba} as having been fully paid. She hurt herself, not the heirs, and therefore the sale stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle works because there is no {\i ona\u8217'ah} by {\i karka} \u8212- no law of overcharging or undercharging that voids a land transaction on the basis of price discrepancy alone. Land has no fixed market price in the way that movable goods do, and therefore the normal {\i ona\u8217'ah} rules that can void a sale of chattel have no application here. If a sale of land is going to be voided, it must be for a different reason \u8212- such as the agent acting beyond the scope of her authority, or causing injury to a party who did not consent to the loss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here, no party is being injured against their will. The heirs receive what they would have received had she sold at full value: the debt is paid. The woman absorbs the difference. The sale is therefore binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estate Takes Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The last case of the {\i mishna} presents the contrast. Her {\i ketuba} was four hundred {\i zuz}. She has already sold three parcels at one hundred each, leaving one hundred outstanding. She then sells a parcel worth one hundred and twenty-five for one hundred. This is underselling \u8212- but now there is no room for her to absorb the loss. Her remaining debt is only one hundred; there is no excess debt against which the extra twenty-five can be offset. The estate \u8212- meaning the orphans and heirs \u8212- must take the loss. They receive property worth one hundred and twenty-five, and in exchange they have satisfied only one hundred of her debt. The remaining twenty-five in value has simply been taken from them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because the estate bears the loss rather than the woman, the sale is void. The heirs can say: you were empowered to act as our agent in selling property to satisfy your debt; you were not empowered to cause us financial injury in the process. That is the principle: {\i litkunei shidartich velo la\u8217'avutei} \u8212- I sent you for my benefit, not to harm me \u8212- a formulation the {\i gemara} will deploy again later in the context of a general agent. Since the estate takes the hit, the sale is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pushes further and asks: why does the {\i mishna} need to tell us the last case separately? Is the principle not already implicit in the first case? The {\i gemara} answers that there is an important {\i chiddush} embedded in the {\i mishna}\u8217's structure, and it involves a {\i gezeira} \u8212- a protective rabbinic safeguard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider what would happen if the {\i mishna} only told us the first case and the last case, without implicitly authorizing the middle cases \u8212- that is, sales made when her {\i ketuba} is partially outstanding, say a parcel worth one hundred and twenty-five sold for one hundred, where she still has, say, two hundred in remaining debt. In that middle scenario, she could take the hit: the extra twenty-five would simply be deducted from her outstanding balance. The sale would be valid and she would absorb the shortfall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought we should void even the middle sales, on the grounds that allowing them would {\i eventually} lead to the final scenario \u8212- where there is no longer any remaining debt against which to offset the loss. If we permit middle-case underselling on the theory that she will take the hit, buyers may come to rely on this principle; and when she finally reaches the last sale, where there is no hit for her to take, someone might mistakenly think that sale is valid too. To prevent that confusion, perhaps we should void all undersales uniformly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this reasoning: {\i nigzor mana rishon atu mana acharon} \u8212- do we make a {\i gezeira} voiding the first-hundred case because it might lead to the last-hundred case? No.\u160 ?The {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishna} is precisely that we do {\i not} make this {\i gezeira}. The middle sales are valid because in those cases she genuinely takes the loss, and the estate is not harmed. Only when the estate would bear the loss \u8212- in the final sale \u8212- does the transaction become void. The {\i mishna} is teaching us that we do not collapse all undersales into a single category; we assess each on its own terms.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mosif Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doing More\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now abstracts to the general principle governing an agent who does {\i more} than he was authorized to do. The term is {\i mosif al devarav} \u8212- he adds beyond his mandate. The example: you told your agent to go sell a half-acre field, and he went and sold a full acre. In this case the {\i gemara} treats the agent\u8217's action as divisible: the half-acre that was within the scope of the mandate is a valid sale; the additional half-acre, which exceeded the mandate, was his own independent action. He was {\i mosif}, not {\i maavir} \u8212- adding on top of the authorized act, rather than going entirely rogue. The base transaction is therefore binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is importantly different from {\i maavir al devarav}, where the agent steps entirely outside his authorization such that the entire act is his own and none of it is binding as agency. When he merely adds, there is genuine overlap between what was authorized and what he did; that overlap portion retains its status as {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the woman and her {\i ketuba}: if she sold one hundred and twenty-five for one hundred and twenty-five, the part of the sale up to her authorized one hundred would be valid under this {\i mosif} principle. It is only the extra twenty-five that was done on her own. The reason the first case of the {\i mishna} is void is not, therefore, that the {\i mosif} principle fails \u8212- it is that the {\i mishna} as Rav Huna reads it is not a {\i mosif} case at all, but an underselling case, as discussed above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doing Less\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The harder question is the reverse: what happens when the agent does {\i less} than he was asked? You told him to sell an acre and he sold a half-acre. There is no clean overlap in the sense of inclusion: he did not do part of what you asked and add something else. He simply did less. Is the half-acre sale binding as {\i shlichut} for that amount?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames this as a genuine question with competing considerations. On one hand, the agent might say to the sender: I did you a favor. If you had needed all the cash right away, you would have needed to sell the whole acre; but by selling only half, I left you the option to come into funds another way and retain the rest of your land. Land, after all, is the primary source of wealth; no one sells it unless in genuine financial distress. To sell half is to preserve the other half against future necessity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other hand, the sender might respond: {\i lo nicha li d\u8217'lifush shetarei ilavai} \u8212- I do not want to accumulate a history of multiple sales documents against me. To sell piecemeal is to create a record of repeated transactions, which in the context of this society signals a pattern of financial difficulty, a kind of bad credit history. The owner may well have preferred a single large transaction to multiple small ones \u8212- whether for reputational reasons, for the practical advantage of a better price on a larger parcel, or simply because he specified what he wanted and wants his instructions followed. The {\i gemara} leaves the tension explicit: when an agent does less than authorized, and there is a real consequential difference between what was done and what was asked \u8212- not merely a question of quantity \u8212- the question of whether the agent was acting within {\i shlichut} is genuinely unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shirt Purchase Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meila Shirt Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina of Sura introduces a {\i meila} case that places the question of underdoing in sharp relief. A man gives his {\i shaliach} a golden {\i dinar} and says: go buy me a shirt. Unbeknownst to either of them, the {\i dinar} was {\i hekdesh} \u8212- consecrated property. The agent takes the {\i dinar}, goes to the market, and with three {\i sela} \u8212- half the {\i dinar} \u8212- buys a shirt; with the other three {\i sela} he buys a {\i talit}, a cloak, on his own initiative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling: {\i shneihem ma\u8217'alu} \u8212- both of them committed {\i meila}. The sender committed {\i meila} in connection with the shirt, because the agent was acting on his behalf when he bought it. The agent committed {\i meila} in connection with the {\i talit}, because that purchase was entirely his own \u8212- the sender never authorized it. For the {\i talit}, the agent was operating outside {\i shlichut}, so the sender bears no culpability there; the agent does. For the shirt, the agent was operating within {\i shlichut}, so the sender is implicated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But notice: the sender said to buy a shirt worth six {\i sela}, and the agent bought a shirt worth only three {\i sela}. He did less than he was asked. And yet he is still considered to have been acting as {\i shaliach} for that purchase. The {\i gemara} will now examine what exactly this case proves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shirt Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reading the case straightforwardly: the sender asked for a six-{\i sela} shirt and received a three-{\i sela} shirt. Tanna Kamma holds that the agent was nevertheless acting within {\i shlichut} for the shirt purchase \u8212- the sender committed {\i meila}. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: {\i chaluka di-veina lei sagi u-ma\u8217'alya ve-ayteit li chaluka katina ve-ra} \u8212- I wanted a large, superior garment and you brought me a small, inferior one. Since what was bought was categorically different from what was requested \u8212- not just a lesser quantity of the same thing, but an inferior object \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda holds the agent was not acting on behalf of the sender at all. The sender did not commit {\i meila}; only the agent did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This maps directly onto the question of whether doing less constitutes valid {\i shlichut}. If I ask for a six-{\i sela} shirt and I receive a three-{\i sela} shirt, have I received what I asked for in diminished form, or have I received something different? Tanna Kamma says the former; Rabbi Yehuda says the latter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sale vs Inferiority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then reframes the debate. Perhaps the dispute between Tanna Kamma and Rabbi Yehuda is not actually about whether a cheaper shirt counts as doing less of what you were asked. Perhaps everyone agrees that doing less of the same thing does not break {\i shlichut}. The actual scenario is: the shirt was on sale. The agent went to the market, found a six-{\i sela} shirt available for three {\i sela}, bought it at that bargain price, and then used the remaining three {\i sela} to buy a {\i talit} for himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under this reading, Tanna Kamma says the agent did exactly what he was asked \u8212- he obtained the requested shirt \u8212- and did so efficiently, with funds left over. He was acting within {\i shlichut} for the shirt, and on his own for the {\i talit}. Rabbi Yehuda dissents: even if the shirt was on sale, the agent should not have pocketed the savings or spent them on a {\i talit}. Instead, he should have used the full six {\i sela} to obtain an even finer shirt \u8212- since if the shirt that cost three {\i sela} was worth six {\i sela} at full price, spending six {\i sela} could have obtained something worth twelve. The agent was therefore not fully operating as {\i shaliach} even for the shirt; he should have maximized the sender\u8217's benefit with the full sum entrusted to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under this reframing, the underlying question about {\i doing less} \u8212- asking an agent to do one hundred and having him do fifty \u8212- is not actually resolved by this {\i beraita}. Both Tanna Kamma and Rabbi Yehuda might agree that when an agent does less of the same thing, the {\i shlichut} for the portion he did accomplish is binding. Their dispute is about a more specific scenario: whether buying a shirt at a bargain price and saving the rest (rather than spending the remainder on a better shirt) constitutes faithful execution of the mandate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legumes Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda himself concedes the point in a different scenario: {\i modeh Rabbi Yehuda b\u8217'kitnit she-shneihem ma\u8217'alu} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda agrees that both committed {\i meila} in the case of legumes. If the agent was told to buy legumes and bought half the quantity, he agrees the sender is implicated. Why? Because with legumes, it is purely a question of quantity. There is no question of inferior quality or missed opportunity to buy a better product. The agent bought legumes \u8212- the right commodity \u8212- just less of them. In that case, even Rabbi Yehuda concedes that the portion he did purchase was done within {\i shlichut}, and the sender therefore committed {\i meila} for that amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is then examined further. The {\i gemara} asks: what exactly is the case with the legumes? If produce is sold by rough estimate rather than by precise measure, then presumably buying more would have yielded a better deal \u8212- and one could ask why Rabbi Yehuda does not say here too that the agent should have spent the full sum to maximize the benefit. Rav Pappa answers: the case is in a place where legumes are sold by precise, individual measure \u8212- {\i de\u8217'amar leh kaneh kaneh b\u8217'peruta}, each unit costs a fixed {\i peruta}, with no bulk discount and no estimation. There is therefore no possible advantage to be gained by spending more, and no argument that the agent should have done otherwise. In that context, there is no ground for Rabbi Yehuda to dispute, and the half-purchase is clearly within {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of all this is: when the deviation from instructions is purely quantitative and there is no meaningful difference between the partial act and the full act (other than scale), {\i shlichut} applies to the portion accomplished. When the deviation is qualitative \u8212- a different object, a different grade, a different kind of transaction \u8212- the question is harder, and that is where the debate between Tanna Kamma and Rabbi Yehuda, as the {\i gemara} ultimately understands it, is located.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agent Discretion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs Many Buyers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a new scenario that shifts the focus from quantity and quality to the manner of executing a mandate. You tell your agent: sell this piece of land to someone. You do not specify {\i not} to sell it to multiple buyers \u8212- you simply say to find a buyer. The agent proceeds to sell it to two or even more buyers, dividing the parcel. Is the sale binding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna holds: {\i amar l\u8217'echad v\u8217'lo l\u8217'shnayim} \u8212- when I say \u8220"one,\u8221" I mean one and not two. Saying \u8220"find someone to buy this\u8221" implicitly limits the agent to a single transaction. Rav Chisda and Rabba bar Rav Huna disagree: {\i afilu l\u8217'echad v\u8217'afilu l\u8217'me\u8217'ah} \u8212- even one hundred buyers would be acceptable. Saying \u8220"one\u8221" does not exclude others; it is simply an indefinite reference, not a restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mistakes vs Latitude\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Nachman visited Sura and Rav Chisda and Rabba bar Rav Huna came before him, they posed this very question. Rav Nachman responded in line with the more permissive position: {\i l\u8217'echad v\u8217'afilu l\u8217'shnayim, l\u8217'echad v\u8217'afilu l\u8217'me\u8217'ah} \u8212- one is not a limitation, and selling to multiple buyers is within the agent\u8217's discretion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They then pressed him: so you are giving the agent broad latitude \u8212- would you therefore also say that if the agent made an outright mistake, such as underselling the land significantly, that too would be binding? Rav Nachman drew a sharp line: {\i d\u8217'b\u8217'ta\u8217'ei shaliach lo ka\u8217'amina} \u8212- I am not talking about mistakes. Discretion about the manner of execution \u8212- one buyer or several, a particular day or another \u8212- is one thing. An outright financial error that injures the sender is quite another. Latitude in execution does not extend to mistakes that fall outside any reasonable range of what the sender could have contemplated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Owner vs Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The questioners then note a seeming tension: you said that if the agent makes a mistake it is void \u8212- but we know that there is no {\i ona\u8217'ah} by {\i karka}! Real estate has no fixed price, and therefore the rules of {\i ona\u8217'ah} \u8212- which void a transaction when the price deviates by more than one-sixth from the fair market value \u8212- simply do not apply to land. If there is no fixed price, what does it mean for an agent to have made a \u8220"mistake\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hani mili heicha de-ta\u8217'ah ba\u8217'al ha-bayit} \u8212- the {\i ona\u8217'ah} exemption applies when the {\i owner} himself buys or sells. Whatever price the owner accepts, that is a legitimate price for the land; there is nothing to void. But {\i aval shaliach} \u8212- an agent is different. When the owner sends a {\i shaliach}, he sends him with the instruction {\i litkunei shidartich velo la\u8217'avutei} \u8212- I sent you to benefit me, not to harm me. The {\i shaliach} therefore must operate within a reasonable range of the owner\u8217's intent. What the owner could do freely on his own account, the agent cannot do if it amounts to acting against the owner\u8217's interests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trumah Range\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now cites a proof from {\i trumah} \u8212- the priestly portion separated from grain \u8212- to demonstrate that acting within the owner\u8217's range is the standard of valid {\i shlichut}, while acting outside it is not. The principle is established in a {\i tanna\u8217'itic} source: if the owner tells his {\i shaliach} to separate {\i trumah} from his grain, the agent should ideally follow the owner\u8217's usual practice. If he does not know the owner\u8217's practice, he defaults to the middle standard of one in fifty \u8212- that is, two percent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the agent takes one in forty (slightly more generous) or one in sixty (slightly more conservative), his {\i trumah} is still valid \u8212- those figures represent the edges of the normal range: a generous person gives one in forty, a stingy person gives one in sixty. The agent has deviated somewhat from the middle, but he is still within the normal range of practice, and therefore the {\i trumah} is binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if the {\i owner himself} separated {\i trumah} at one in ten \u8212- five percent, well above the normal range \u8212- his {\i trumah} is nevertheless valid: {\i taram ba\u8217'al ha-bayit afilu echad me\u8217'asara, trumato trumah}. The owner acting for himself is not constrained to the normal range; whatever he takes is a legitimate exercise of his ownership. There is no {\i ona\u8217'ah} standard that governs his own action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is precisely the parallel to land sales: the owner acting on his own is not limited by a \u8220"normal range\u8221" of acceptable price; whatever he agrees to is binding. The {\i shaliach}, however, must operate within the range of what the owner would have sanctioned. Deviating beyond that range \u8212- whether in the context of {\i trumah} or in the context of land sales \u8212- takes the act outside the bounds of {\i shlichut} and renders it void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes by returning to the {\i mishna}\u8217's scenario: the woman who sold four hundred {\i zuz} worth of land in pieces \u8212- three parcels for one hundred each and a final parcel worth one hundred and twenty-five for one hundred. The earlier sales are binding; the last is void. But one might ask: if she was supposed to sell on behalf of the estate, why is it permissible for her to sell in multiple transactions, piecemeal, when we have seen that multiple sales may not be in the interest of the seller?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet son of Rav Idi answers: {\i b\u8217'katinei} \u8212- these are small, scattered plots of land. There is no single large parcel to sell as a unit. Under those conditions, the only available method of collection is to sell the small plots as they come, and the concern about multiple sales documents and the cumulative record of transactions simply does not arise as a practical matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader principle that emerges from the {\i gemara}\u8217's full discussion is this: agency requires operating within reasonable bounds of the owner\u8217's intent. An agent who does more than asked may still be valid for the overlapping portion of the mandate. An agent who does less raises a harder question \u8212- with the answer depending on whether the deviation is merely quantitative or qualitative, whether the agent\u8217's judgment can plausibly be said to serve the owner\u8217's interests, and whether the act falls within a normal range of what the owner would have countenanced. What the owner may do freely for himself, the agent may not do arbitrarily; the agent is always constrained by the implicit instruction: I sent you to benefit me, not to harm me.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 100\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow\u8217's Sale & Pricing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Memorial Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the daf, we pause to note the passing of Belda Lindenbaum. Though her funeral was a small, family-only gathering \u8212- itself a testament to her values, as it could easily have drawn tens of thousands \u8212- her impact on {\i klal Yisrael} was enormous. Countless women have posted tributes describing how every Torah institution they benefited from was built through her vision and philanthropy. She demonstrated what guided, purposeful generosity can do to reshape the face of the Jewish community. She was 76. May her memory be a blessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dinar Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up with the {\i mishnah} on 99b, which deals with a widow selling off estate property in order to collect her {\i ketubah}. Before proceeding, a correction is necessary regarding a point raised in the previous daf. When the {\i Gemara} spoke of a widow selling something worth a {\i maneh} and a {\i dinar} for only a {\i maneh}, the earlier discussion incorrectly assumed this referred to a golden {\i dinar} \u8212- worth 25 {\i zuz} \u8212- making the discrepancy roughly 20%. Rashi, both in the previous {\i sugya} and here, makes clear that we are dealing with a silver {\i dinar}, which is essentially a single {\i zuz} \u8212- representing a difference of approximately 1%. This correction is significant not only for today\u8217's daf but for all the preceding discussion about the widow over- or under-selling estate property. The two scenarios the {\i Gemara} addressed \u8212- selling something worth 101 for 100, or selling property worth 101 for 101 \u8212- reflect a trivially small, roughly 1% discrepancy. The question of how much latitude to grant a representative must be understood against that backdrop.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna on Court Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judges\u8217' Assessment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} introduces a new scenario: {\i shum ha-dayanim} \u8212- the judges themselves assess and sell the estate property. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i she-pichesu shtu\u8217'sh o hosifu shtu\u8217'sh} \u8212- if they were off by a {\i shtu\u8217'sh}, the sale is void, {\i mekhan batel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i shtu\u8217'sh} (a sixth) is ordinarily the threshold for {\i ona\u8217'ah} \u8212- the laws of overcharging or undercharging that can void a commercial sale. However, there is no {\i ona\u8217'ah} for {\i karka\u8217'ot}, real property. So if an ordinary individual were selling his own land at a loss, the rules of {\i ona\u8217'ah} would simply not apply. The reason the court\u8217's sale can be voided is different: the court is not the owner selling on its own behalf, but a representative acting for the orphans. Just as the previous {\i mishnah} discussed the widow\u8217's role as a {\i shliach} of the orphans, here the court acts in that representative capacity. When a representative steps too far outside the bounds of proper valuation \u8212- selling at a discount of more than a sixth \u8212- they are understood to have exceeded their authority, and the sale does not stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implicit logic of the {\i mishnah} is therefore that up to a sixth, the sale remains binding; beyond a sixth, the representatives have stepped outside their authorized role. This stands in notable contrast to the widow\u8217's case: given the correction noted above, even a 1% underprice voids her sale. The court, by contrast, is given considerably more latitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} disagrees: {\i mekhan kayam} \u8212- even if they are off by more than a sixth, the sale stands. His reasoning is captured in the phrase {\i she-im ken mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh} \u8212- \u8220"if so, what power does the court have?\u8221" This can be read as a policy argument: if every court decision is subject to reassessment and reversal, the authority of the {\i beis din} is fundamentally undermined. Alternatively, one can invoke the principle of {\i hefker beis din hefker} \u8212- the court has the institutional authority to define legal realities through its rulings, and its acts are therefore binding by virtue of that authority alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A pointed question emerges from {\i Tosafos}: even if we accept {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}\u8217's position that the sale is not reversed, must the excess be returned? In ordinary {\i ona\u8217'ah} cases, there are two distinct questions \u8212- whether the sale is void, and whether even a binding sale requires returning the overage. {\i Tosafos} asks the same question here: perhaps the court\u8217's action stands as a valid sale, yet the party who received the benefit of the mispricing must still repay the difference. Whether concern for undermining court authority extends even to requiring repayment of the overage is not resolved from the {\i mishnah} itself, though the plain reading appears to be that the sale is simply final.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Igeret Bikoret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes with an important addendum, attributed presumably to the {\i chachamim}: {\i im asu igeret bikoret beineihem} \u8212- if they produced a {\i writ of assessment} among themselves \u8212- then {\i afilu machru shaveh maneh be-masatayim}, even if they sold something worth 100 for 200, or 200 for 100, {\i mekhan kayam}, the sale is entirely binding. Even the {\i chachamim}, who ordinarily require voiding a sale that is off by more than a sixth, agree that when this document was produced, the sale stands regardless of the discrepancy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The precise meaning of {\i beineihem} \u8212- literally \u8220"between them\u8221" \u8212- is somewhat puzzling. {\i Tosafos} suggests it might refer to a letter passing between courts, though that does not fit the context naturally. The phrase most likely refers to the members of the {\i beis din} producing an official written assessment among themselves. But what precisely is this {\i igeret bikoret}, and why should its existence render even a wildly mispriced sale binding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Hachrazah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi rejects the reading that the {\i igeret bikoret} simply means the court commissioned an assessor and wrote up a report. He explains: {\i u-leshon bikoret she-mevakrin oto bnei adam al yedei ha-hachrazah} \u8212- the {\i bikoret} refers to a process by which the public examines and evaluates the property through public announcement. In other words, the {\i igeret bikoret} does not mean a written appraisal but rather a public auction. Just as estates in later periods required advertisements and open bidding processes, the {\i hachrazah} ensures that the widest possible pool of buyers has had the opportunity to evaluate and bid on the property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic follows naturally: if you have done everything humanly possible to achieve market price \u8212- made public announcement, held an open process, and let the market speak \u8212- then whatever price resulted {\i is} the market price by definition. Even if it deviates dramatically from a prior estimate, the process itself validates the outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Valuation Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a sharp question: if it is so difficult to establish an objective value for land, how can we meaningfully speak of a 1% underprice as rendering the widow\u8217's sale void? The gap between 100 and 101 {\i dinarim} seems impossible to detect with any precision, given that land valuation inherently involves a range. The answer, while not fully resolved by the {\i Gemara}, likely parallels what the {\i Gemara} will discuss regarding {\i terumah}: there is an accepted range of reasonable prices, and the widow\u8217's sale would only be void if it fell below even the lowest end of that range. This is admittedly speculative, but the need for some such framework is clear. Tellingly, the formal exclusion of land from {\i ona\u8217'ah} ({\i eino la-karka\u8217'ot}) itself reflects the impossibility of setting a precise value \u8212- which makes the standard applied to the widow\u8217's sale all the more noteworthy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Classification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now pivots from the specific cases of the widow and the court to a broader question about the law of agency: {\i iba\u8217'ya lehu, shaliach ke-man?} \u8212- into which category does an ordinary {\i shaliach}, an agent, fall? We now have two clearly defined poles. The {\i almanah}, the widow, is held to a near-zero tolerance standard: even a 1% underprice voids her sale. The {\i beis din} is given substantially more room: up to a sixth off, the sale remains valid. When someone is simply appointed as an agent to sell property on another\u8217's behalf, which standard applies?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava & R. Shmuel Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a dispute in the name of Rav Nachman. {\i Rava amar Rav Nachman: shaliach ke-dayanim} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} is like the court. The parallel reasoning is: {\i ma dayanim lo le-didhu, af shaliach nami lo le-didei} \u8212- just as judges sell for the benefit of others with no personal stake in the outcome, so too an agent is acting for someone else\u8217's account with no vested interest of his own. That disinterested posture warrants the same latitude as the court: as long as the sale is not more than a sixth off, it stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Shmuel bar Bisna amar Rav Nachman: ke-almanah} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} is like the widow, and even a trivial underprice voids the sale. The reasoning here: {\i mah almanah yechidah, af shaliach yechidah} \u8212- the widow is an individual, and so is the {\i shaliach}. The strict standard applied to individuals is the baseline; it is only the {\i beis din}, as an institutional body of multiple judges \u8212- {\i de-rabbim ninhu} \u8212- that earns the relaxed standard. Whether the leniency derives from the collective nature of {\i beis din} or from its special institutional authority, a single individual acting as agent shares neither of those qualities and must be held to the individual standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is another angle on the widow\u8217's case: one might suspect that she has a personal incentive to undersell, whether to conclude the sale quickly or even to arrange a side deal at the buyer\u8217's expense. The court has no such incentive. Does the {\i shaliach} resemble the court in that respect? One could argue he does \u8212- he has no personal stake in a lower price. But against that, he is enough self-motivated in accepting the agency that greater precision can be demanded of him. The {\i Gemara} does not fully adjudicate these competing intuitions, but they frame why the question is genuinely difficult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Like Widow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-hilchasa: shaliach ke-almanah} \u8212- the ruling is that the {\i shaliach} is treated like the widow, and even a tiny underprice voids the sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paradox of Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling sounds harsh, but there is a striking irony embedded in it that works to the seller\u8217's advantage. Precisely because the {\i shaliach}\u8217's sale is void at even the slightest underprice \u8212- and because there is no {\i ona\u8217'ah} for {\i karka\u8217'ot} when one sells for oneself \u8212- a seller who routes his land sale through an agent gains a protection unavailable to someone selling directly. If the agent gets even a dollar less than the property\u8217's value, the seller can void the transaction entirely. If you sell your own land, no {\i ona\u8217'ah} applies and you have no remedy at all for a below-market sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry is interesting in the other direction too: if the {\i shaliach} oversells \u8212- gets more than the market value \u8212- there is no reason to void the transaction. There is no {\i ona\u8217'ah} for land, and the seller is perfectly happy to receive more. So the rule that the {\i shaliach} is like the {\i almanah} only cuts in favor of the seller when the price was too low; there is nothing in the sources suggesting it would void an oversale, which, as noted, may not be true when {\i beis din} oversells \u8212- since {\i beis din} represents all parties and cannot legitimately impose an unfair burden on the buyer either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Be a Shaliach?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might reasonably wonder: given how easily a {\i shaliach}\u8217's act can be invalidated, why would anyone agree to serve as an agent? And from the buyer\u8217's side, how does commerce function if a transaction can always be undone by the seller claiming the agent underpriced? The answer is partly pragmatic: in the ancient world of large landholders, managing every transaction personally was not possible, and agents \u8212- much like lawyers handling deals today \u8212- were a practical necessity. If a buyer wants security, he needs to deal with someone who genuinely holds full authorization; the law is simply clear about what that authorization entails. The business reality meant that agency relationships were understood and accepted as such.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Range\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a comparison from a different {\i mishnah} to probe what it means to be \u8220"off.\u8221" The {\i mishnah} states: if one tells his {\i shaliach} to go and separate {\i terumah} on his behalf, the {\i shaliach} does so according to what he estimates the owner would have wanted. If he does not know the owner\u8217's preference, he takes the average \u8212- one fiftieth, two percent. {\i Picheisu asarah o hosifu asarah} \u8212- if he took one sixtieth or one fortieth, his {\i terumah} is still valid. So here we see an individual {\i shaliach} operating with a recognized range \u8212- one fortieth to one sixtieth is all acceptable \u8212- rather than being held to an exact figure. How does this square with the ruling that a {\i shaliach} selling land is like the {\i almanah}, voided even by a tiny discrepancy?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Range vs.\u160 ?Error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i ha-tam nami, kivan de-ika de-toreim be-ayin ra\u8217'ah ve-ika de-toreim be-ayin yaffah} \u8212- in the {\i terumah} case, there is a known range of legitimate practice. Some people are naturally generous in separating {\i terumah} and some are characteristically stingy, and both ends of that spectrum are recognized norms. The {\i shaliach} can be told: I estimated your practice falls at the generous or stingy end of this known range. {\i Aval hacha ta\u8217'usa hi} \u8212- but in the case of a sale, we are dealing with an objective error, not a range of practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Market Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer itself raises a challenge: if land valuation is so imprecise as to exclude it entirely from {\i ona\u8217'ah}, in what sense is a slight underprice an \u8220"objective error\u8221"? The resolution implicit in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's logic is that one must work with an assessed range of market prices. If an appraiser would say that the property realistically could fetch anywhere between 110 and 140, then the sale is only a {\i ta\u8217'us} \u8212- an error \u8212- if it falls below the bottom of that range. This parallels the {\i terumah} case: it is not a question of a single precise number but of whether you have fallen outside any defensible range. The practical problem is that markets move: prices can be cut repeatedly below the assessor\u8217's estimate in a slow market, or surge well above it in a hot one. The {\i Gemara} seems to assume the existence of a relatively stable, determinable range, without which the entire framework becomes difficult to apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wheat Fields Pricing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The clearest scenario in which such a stable range exists is agricultural land with a clear, commodity-like character. Thousands of acres of wheat fields, for example, have a fairly objective per-unit price that does not fluctuate as wildly as residential or commercial property, where location and other idiosyncratic factors dominate. This probably represents the Talmudic default assumption \u8212- land value that, while not perfectly precise, is stable enough to support the notion of an objective error when the price falls sufficiently far outside the expected range.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Huna bar Chinana amar Rav Nachman: halacha ke-divrei chachamim} \u8212- we rule like the {\i chachamim}, not like {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. The court\u8217's sale is void when the price diverges from fair value by more than a sixth; it is not always binding simply by virtue of the court\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphans\u8217' Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling immediately generates a tension. Does Rav Nachman not hold the principle of {\i mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh}? For he himself says in the name of {\i Shmuel}: when minor orphans come to divide their father\u8217's estate, {\i beis din} appoints a separate court-appointed representative \u8212- an {\i apitropus} \u8212- for each, and those representatives negotiate and divide the estate on behalf of their respective orphans. {\i Higdilu, yecholim limchos} \u8212- once they reach adulthood, Shmuel holds that they may object to the division.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge Division?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rav Nachman himself disagrees with Shmuel and rules: {\i higdilu, ein yecholim limchos, im ken mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh} \u8212- they cannot object once they come of age, because the court-appointed division is binding. If the division could be unraveled years later simply because one of the orphans now prefers a different share, the entire institution of court-appointed guardianship collapses. How then does Rav Nachman simultaneously rule like the {\i chachamim} \u8212- voiding the court sale when it is off by more than a sixth \u8212- and insist that a court-appointed division of an estate is permanently binding?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Error in Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Error vs.\u160 ?Preference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo kashya} \u8212- there is no contradiction. {\i Ha d\u8217'ta\u8217'ah, ha d\u8217'lo ta\u8217'ah} \u8212- one case involves a genuine error, the other does not. When the court over- or under-assesses property by more than a sixth, that is an objective factual error of significant magnitude, and Rav Nachman would say it is not binding. But when the court appoints representatives to divide an estate and those representatives negotiate a division, there is no comparable objective error \u8212- there is at most a question of preference. One orphan might later wish he had received the plot closer to the city rather than the one closer to the river; another might prefer a parcel oriented differently. {\i B\u8217'ruchot} \u8212- matters of direction and preference \u8212- are not objective errors. For cases of preference, Rav Nachman says the division stands; for large objective errors in valuation, it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim}\u8217's position is therefore internally coherent within Rav Nachman\u8217's framework: a court decision is binding when it falls within a reasonable range \u8212- even if not perfect \u8212- but becomes voidable when the error is sufficiently large and objectively demonstrable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki ata Rav Dimi amar}: when Rav Dimi came, he reported a relevant incident. {\i Ma\u8217'aseh va\u8217'asa Rebbe ke-divrei chachamim} \u8212- there was a case in which {\i Rebbe} (Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi) ruled like the {\i chachamim}, holding the court\u8217's sale void because it was off by more than a sixth. {\i Parta}, the son of Rabbi Elazar ben Parta and grandson of Rabbi Parta the Great, then said before him: {\i im ken mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh} \u8212- if that is your ruling, where is the power of the court? {\i Ve-chazar Rebbe ve-asa ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- Rebbe reversed his ruling and allowed the court\u8217's sale to stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Story Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ika de\u8217'amri}, there is an alternate version. Rav Safra taught it differently: {\i uvikeish Rebbe la\u8217'asot ke-divrei chachamim} \u8212- Rebbe was {\i about} to rule like the {\i chachamim} when Parta interjected with the {\i mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh} argument, and {\i lo asa Rebbe et ha-ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- Rebbe never issued the ruling in the first place. In this version, there is no reversal, only a ruling withheld.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversal Irony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first version is particularly striking. The very principle that courts do not reverse their own rulings \u8212- {\i mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh} \u8212- was invoked to cause Rebbe to reverse {\i his} ruling. He declined to reverse the court\u8217's sale, but he was perfectly willing to reverse his own {\i pesak}. The irony is impossible to miss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Law Always Reversed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lema bahai kamifligi?} \u8212- perhaps this is what the two versions of the story actually dispute. {\i Mar savar to\u8217'eh bidvar mishnah chozer, u-mar savar eino chozer} \u8212- one view holds that when a court errs in a matter of black-letter law, the ruling is reversed; the other holds it is not. The {\i Gemara} rejects this: {\i lo de-kuli alma to\u8217'eh bidvar mishnah chozer} \u8212- everyone agrees that an error in black-letter law ({\i to\u8217'eh bidvar mishnah}) requires reversal. The two versions of the story simply differ on what actually happened historically, not on the underlying {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the {\i Gemara} draws out a deep structural parallel. There are two axes of error: error in fact and error in law. On the factual axis, the {\i mishnah} debates whether a court sale mispriced by more than a sixth must be reversed \u8212- the {\i chachamim} say yes, {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} says no. On the legal axis, there is a distinction between {\i to\u8217'eh be-shikul ha-da\u8217'at} \u8212- a judgment call where reasonable authorities might disagree, where the ruling stands even if the judgment was wrong \u8212- and {\i to\u8217'eh bidvar mishnah}, a clear error in established law, where everyone agrees reversal is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Authority Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel illuminates the conceptual framework underlying {\i mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh}. An agent \u8212- whether a {\i shaliach} or the {\i beis din} itself \u8212- acts within their authority when they operate inside the law and within a reasonable range of factual judgment. The {\i mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh} principle protects their acts within that zone: even mistakes of fact do not undo what they have done, because they were authorized. But when they act outside the law \u8212- when they are {\i to\u8217'eh bidvar mishnah} \u8212- they are no longer functioning as {\i beis din} at all. Their authorization extends only to lawful action; unlawful action is not protected by institutional authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a precise parallel with the {\i shaliach} who undersells by a dollar: the moment he steps outside the authorized range, he is no longer acting within his {\i shlichus}. Correspondingly, {\i beis din} acts are binding when they stay within their lawful mandate \u8212- even with errors in fact \u8212- but not when they depart from it. The distinction is not about probability of error or quality of judgment; it is about whether one is acting within or outside the scope of one\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow\u8217's Warranty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estate Liability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef states: {\i almanah de-zabina, achrayus ayasmi} \u8212- when a widow sells estate property to collect her {\i ketubah}, the {\i achrayus} \u8212- the legal responsibility for warranty of title \u8212- falls on the estate, not on the widow personally. If the property turns out to have been improperly sold, if the original owner appears and reclaims it from the buyer, the buyer\u8217's claim for reimbursement goes against the orphans\u8217' estate, not against the widow who conducted the sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is intuitive: the widow was selling the estate\u8217's property to satisfy the estate\u8217's debt to her. She was not selling something she owned. Similarly, {\i u-veis din de-zabni, achrayus ayasmi} \u8212- when {\i beis din} sells estate property, the warranty obligation likewise rests with the estate. The {\i Gemara} notes that while the widow\u8217's case is obvious, the court\u8217's case is not, since one might have thought that buying through a formal court process carries an implicit assurance. When property is sold by court order after extensive public announcement, every potential claimant has had ample opportunity to come forward. Perhaps the buyer implicitly waives his right to warranty by assuming no claim will arise. The {\i Gemara} rejects this: the buyer\u8217's assumption does not rise to an actual waiver, and if a title defect emerges, his claim against the estate stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Announcement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ameimar} then states in the name of Rav Yosef: {\i beis din she-machru belo hachrazah, na\u8217'asu ke-mi she-ta\u8217'u bidvar mishnah ve-chozrin} \u8212- if {\i beis din} sold estate property without going through the required public announcement process, the sale is treated as if the court committed an error in a matter of law ({\i to\u8217'eh bidvar mishnah}), and it is reversed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is the failure to conduct a {\i hachrazah} classified as {\i to\u8217'eh bidvar mishnah} rather than as an error in fact? The answer is structural: the {\i hachrazah} is a procedural requirement, not a factual judgment. A court operating without it is not making a bad assessment of value \u8212- it is simply not following the procedure it is authorized to follow. It has stepped outside its lawful mandate, and actions outside that mandate are not protected by {\i mah ko\u8217'ach beis din yafeh}. This is precisely analogous to the principle established above: {\i beis din}\u8217's authority covers errors within the law, not departures from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that the {\i mishnah} itself states {\i shum ha-yetomim shloshim yom} \u8212- assessing orphans\u8217' estate requires a thirty-day public announcement process \u8212- and {\i shum ha-hekdesh shishim yom} \u8212- for {\i hekdesh} property, sixty days \u8212- with announcements morning and evening. This is not an optional enhancement but a required procedure. Selling without it is an error in law.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Public Auction Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Announcement Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} mandates two distinct announcement periods: thirty days for orphans\u8217' property and sixty days for {\i hekdesh}, with announcements morning and evening throughout. The logic of the thirty-day and sixty-day windows is to maximize exposure: the longer the window, the larger the pool of potential buyers who will hear of the sale and have the opportunity to evaluate and bid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Right Price Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi poses a sharp question to Ameimar: the {\i mishnah} states that when {\i beis din} undersells or oversells by a sixth, {\i machran batel} \u8212- the sale is void. {\i Ha shaveh be-shaveh, machran kayam} \u8212- if they got the right price, the sale is valid. Now, if the failure to hold a public announcement renders the sale void regardless of the price achieved, then the {\i mishnah}\u8217's discussion of correct pricing would be irrelevant \u8212- even a perfectly priced sale without a {\i hachrazah} should be void. Yet the {\i mishnah} makes clear that a correctly priced sale stands. This implies that the {\i mishnah} is discussing a case {\i without} a public announcement, which contradicts the claim that any sale without {\i hachrazah} is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's response: {\i lo, be-d\u8217'achriz} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} is actually discussing a case {\i with} a public announcement. We know this from the end of the {\i mishnah}, which says that if an {\i igeret bikoret} \u8212- which Rashi identifies with the {\i hachrazah} process \u8212- was conducted, the sale is binding regardless of the price. The {\i mishnah} thus discusses a situation where public auction was held: the beginning addresses the case where the right process was followed but the price was wrong; the end addresses the case where the right process was followed and the price was also drastically off. In both cases, the process was conducted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What Requires Announcement\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this resolution creates its own problem: if the {\i mishnah} only addresses sales {\i with} a public announcement, how can it say that a correctly priced sale stands? The {\i Gemara} resolves this through a distinction among the categories of property being sold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela le-olam de-lo achruz} \u8212- fine, the {\i mishnah} may indeed address cases without a public announcement. The apparent contradiction disappears once one recognizes that the {\i hachrazah} requirement applies specifically to land. There are categories of property for which no public auction process is required at all: {\i ha-avadim ve-ha-metaltelin ve-ha-shtarot} \u8212- slaves, chattels, and documents of debt. For slaves, the reason is {\i shem\u8217'ei ve-arki} \u8212- they will hear they are about to be sold and flee. For chattels and documents, {\i shema yignavu} \u8212- during the extended public viewing period, items might be stolen. You cannot steal land. The {\i hachrazah} requirement therefore applies only to land, and the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that a correctly priced sale stands without formal announcement may simply be addressing a sale of these other categories \u8212- categories for which public auction is never required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, why might the widow be collecting her {\i ketubah} through the sale of non-land property? Ordinarily, a {\i ketubah} lien applies only to real property. But the {\i Gemara} acknowledges this is a special case where the lien was extended to other property as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Auction Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the distinction between property types, there are situational exceptions to the {\i hachrazah} requirement. The {\i Gemara} cites the practice articulated in Neharde\u8217'a: {\i le-kharga, u-le-mezonei, u-le-kevura} \u8212- when there is a need to pay government taxes immediately, when the widow needs funds for food support ({\i mezonot}), or when burial expenses must be met without delay. In these urgent circumstances, {\i mazbininan belo akhrazta} \u8212- one sells without public auction. Urgency overrides process. This also provides a natural reading of the main {\i mishnah}: the widow seeking to collect her {\i ketubah} for living expenses is a paradigmatic case of {\i mezonot}, and her sale can proceed without the thirty-day announcement, yet still remains binding as long as the price is within the acceptable range.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neharde\u8217'a Custom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman adds a third exception \u8212- one based not on property type or urgency, but on locale. {\i Me-olam lo asu igeret bikoret bi-Neharde\u8217'a} \u8212- they never conducted the public auction process in Neharde\u8217'a. One explanation offered is {\i de-vaki\u8217'ei bi-shuma} \u8212- the scholars and residents of Neharde\u8217'a were such expert assessors that no public bidding process was necessary to achieve a fair price; their expertise substituted for the market mechanism. A different explanation, attributed directly to Rav Nachman by Rav Yosef bar Minyumi, is more social in character: buyers at public auctions in Neharde\u8217'a were called {\i benei achli nichsei akhtekhasta} \u8212- \u8220"those who devour the property of the disadvantaged\u8221" \u8212- and the reputational stigma was severe enough that the practice was simply abandoned. The paradox is that the public announcement, designed to protect orphans by maximizing competition, ended up suppressing it because buyers were reluctant to participate. Neharde\u8217'a resolved this by foregoing the process altogether.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sales Timing Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perishable Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} closes with a series of practical cases about how a court-appointed guardian should optimize the sale of orphans\u8217' property. {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel: metaltelin de-yatmi, shamminan le-hu u-mazbini le-hu altar} \u8212- chattel belonging to orphans should be assessed and sold immediately. The concern is that such goods are perishable: if value depreciates over time, delay works against the orphans\u8217' interests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda says in the name of Avimi: {\i mazbininan le-hu le-yomei de-shuka} \u8212- you wait for market day. Even if the goods lose some freshness, selling on market day ensures a broader buyer pool, complete sales, and potentially better total returns than selling immediately to a thin local market. {\i Ve-lo peligi} \u8212- these two views do not actually disagree in principle. {\i Ha de-mikrav shuka, ha de-merachak shuka} \u8212- if market day is near, you wait; if it is far off and goods will significantly deteriorate in the interim, you sell at once. The principle in both cases is to maximize the orphans\u8217' return within the constraints of the situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holiday Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana had beer belonging to the orphan Rav Mesharshiya bar Chilkai in his care. He waited until the {\i regel} \u8212- the pilgrimage festival \u8212- to sell it. {\i Af gav de-nafal beih itzatza} \u8212- even though the beer had begun to turn slightly by the time of the festival, he reasoned {\i mai zuzei charifa} \u8212- at the {\i regel}, he would sell all of it immediately and collect payment in cash. Rashi adds that selling locally in the interim would have meant extended credit to buyers, whereas festival crowds pay on the spot. The small reduction in quality was therefore more than offset by the certainty and completeness of the cash sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shipping Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina had in his possession wine belonging to his orphaned nephew. He was also transporting his own wine to Sichra by ship and asked Rav Ashi whether he should take the orphan\u8217's wine along. The concern was risk: if the ship sank, the orphan\u8217's wine would be lost. {\i Amar lei: zil, lo adif mi-didach} \u8212- Rav Ashi told him: go ahead, his wine is no different from yours. If you judge the profit opportunity to be worth the risk for your own wine, then it is a reasonable risk to take with his wine as well. The guardian is not required to be more conservative with the orphan\u8217's assets than he is with his own; he must simply exercise the same judgment he would apply to his own affairs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Discretion Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These cases close a full circle. The daf opened with a framework of sharply limited discretion \u8212- a {\i shaliach} who undersells by even a trivial amount has exceeded his authority and the sale is void. Yet the {\i Gemara} closes by recognizing that those responsible for orphans\u8217' assets must exercise real-world judgment: whether to sell immediately or wait for market day, whether to hold goods until a festival, whether to accept the risk of shipping. The tension between strict accountability and practical discretion defines the role of any fiduciary representative, whether {\i shaliach}, {\i apitropus}, or {\i beis din} itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 101\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Women, No Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mema\u8217'enes Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the bottom of 100b opens with the case of the {\i mema\u8217'enes} \u8212- the minor girl who refuses her husband. When a girl\u8217's father was absent, her mother and brothers were permitted by rabbinic enactment to marry her off while she was still underage. This institution, known as {\i kiddushei ketanah}, was established not as a financial arrangement but as a form of protection: the Rabbis were concerned that without a father present, a young girl would be vulnerable to exploitation. Crucially, the {\i kiddushin} in such a case is only rabbinic in force, not biblical, and the {\i nisuin} \u8212- the full consummation of the marriage \u8212- would ordinarily not take place until after she became a {\i bat mitzvah}. Since the marriage was not biblically binding, she retained the right to refuse at any point before she came of age. When she exercises that right of {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- refusal \u8212- the marriage is retroactively annulled, as though it had never taken place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shniya Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second woman listed is the {\i shniya}, meaning a woman who is forbidden as a \u8220"secondary\u8221" relation \u8212- not a biblically defined relative, but one prohibited by rabbinic extension of the incest laws, as elaborated in Masechet Yevamos. She is not biblically forbidden; the prohibition is purely rabbinic, covering degrees of relationship removed enough that the Torah itself does not address them. Nevertheless, the Rabbis treated this category with seriousness, and the consequences for entering such a marriage are severe, as the {\i mishnah} goes on to describe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonis Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is the {\i ailonis} \u8212- a woman who not only is infertile but exhibits what the Rabbis understood to be physiologically male characteristics, indicating that she never developed normally as a female. The term itself derives from {\i ayil}, the ram, reflecting this association with maleness. The key halachic significance of this category is that it is treated as a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- a mistaken transaction \u8212- on the theory that had the husband known she was an {\i ailonis}, he never would have agreed to marry her. There is therefore a presumption that the marriage was entered under a fundamental error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ketubah Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In all three of these cases \u8212- the {\i mema\u8217'enes}, the {\i shniya}, and the {\i ailonis} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} rules that these women receive no {\i ketubah}. The reasoning differs by case. The {\i mema\u8217'enes} receives no {\i ketubah} because her {\i mi\u8217'un} retroactively erases the marriage entirely; there was never, in the end, a marriage at all, and therefore no {\i ketubah} obligation was ever created. The {\i ailonis}, similarly, exits through the logic of {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- the marriage was a transaction entered in error, and so likewise never legally materialized. The {\i shniya} is the most interesting case: the marriage was in fact binding, but the Rabbis, wishing to discourage such unions, strip her of her {\i ketubah} as a punitive measure. If the woman knows that there is no {\i ketubah} awaiting her when she leaves, the hope is that she will be less inclined to enter the forbidden marriage in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Peiros/Mezonos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds that not only do these women forfeit the {\i ketubah}, they also lose {\i peiros}, {\i mezonos}, and {\i bla\u8217'os}. Rashi explains that in this context {\i peiros} does not refer to the husband\u8217's right to enjoy the income from his wife\u8217's property \u8212- which is his benefit \u8212- but rather to what the wife is normally entitled to receive in exchange, namely the right to be redeemed if she is taken captive. {\i Mezonos} refers to her ongoing food support; she is not entitled to it, and indeed if she borrowed money to cover her food expenses during the marriage she cannot seek reimbursement. {\i Bla\u8217'os} \u8212- worn-out or consumed property \u8212- means either, as Tosafot reads it, that she cannot be reimbursed for property she brought into the marriage that was consumed during it, or, as Rashi reads it, that she cannot even take with her property that is still present but has become worn. The general thrust is that she walks away with nothing \u8212- an outcome even more severe than the departure of a freed slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Prohibition Par\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The end of the {\i mishnah}, however, is striking. The {\i mishnah} lists cases of biblically forbidden marriages \u8212- a {\i kohen gadol} marrying a widow ({\i almanah}), an ordinary {\i kohen} marrying a divorc\u233 ?e or a woman who performed {\i chalitzah}, and a {\i mamzeret} or {\i nesinah} marrying a {\i yisrael} \u8212- and rules that in all these cases, even though the marriage is prohibited by the Torah itself, {\i yesh lahen ketubah}: these women do receive a {\i ketubah}. This is genuinely puzzling. One would think that the Rabbis\u8217' institution of the {\i ketubah}, designed to protect the wife and structure the marriage, would be the last thing one would apply to a biblically prohibited union. Extending the {\i ketubah} to such a marriage seems almost to confer on it a stamp of rabbinic recognition, even legitimacy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Rabbinic vs.\u160 ?Bibl\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's explanation addresses this paradox directly, and it rests on a specific sociological assumption about who is the driving force behind each type of forbidden marriage. In the case of the {\i shniya}, a rabbinically forbidden marriage, Rashi reasons that the woman is unlikely to be deterred by the prohibition on her own, because the consequences fall lightly on her: her personal status is unaffected, she is not rendered forbidden to a {\i kohen}, and her children\u8217's status is not impaired. She therefore has an incentive to pursue the marriage and, in the Rabbis\u8217' framing, to seduce or persuade the man into it. The penalty of losing the {\i ketubah} is thus directed at her as a disincentive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in a biblically forbidden marriage \u8212- say, a {\i kohen gadol} marrying a widow \u8212- the woman faces real consequences. Rashi explains: {\i mipnei she-hi psulah v\u8217'valadah pasul al yedei nisuin} \u8212- the marriage renders her a {\i chalalah} and her children {\i chalalim}, which is a serious degradation of her and her offspring\u8217's status. She therefore has no reason to pursue such a marriage; the one with an incentive is the man, since his own status is not compromised. Thus the financial burden of the {\i ketubah} is placed on him \u8212- not as an endorsement of the marriage, but as a disincentive targeted at the party who might otherwise be inclined to enter it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explanation is elegant but not entirely satisfying. As the discussion in the shiur makes clear, not all the cases listed involve {\i kohanim}, so the status-change argument does not universally apply. More broadly, there is something genuinely strange about the Rabbis institutionalizing the {\i ketubah} within a biblically prohibited marriage. The {\i ketubah} is an ongoing legal instrument that sustains the structure of the marriage relationship. Attaching it to a biblically forbidden union does seem to implicitly recognize and reinforce that relationship in a way that sits uncomfortably alongside the prohibition. The contrast is almost ironic: the rabbinically forbidden marriage loses the {\i ketubah}, while the biblically forbidden one retains it. One might say the Rabbis were using the {\i ketubah} purely as a practical device to affect behavior, not as an expression of approval \u8212- but once one says the marriage is binding, the force of that logic becomes harder to maintain cleanly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a separate question, not resolved in this {\i mishnah}, of whether the husband in a biblically forbidden binding marriage has an ongoing obligation to pay {\i mezonot} during the marriage itself. It is possible that other {\i gemarot} would say he does not \u8212- since requiring him to sustain the marriage financially would be a much more direct form of support for it. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling about the {\i ketubah} on divorce is one thing; the question of ongoing obligations during the marriage is another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: No Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a dispute between Rav and Shmuel regarding the {\i ketanah} \u8212- the minor girl \u8212- who finds herself exiting a rabbinic marriage. Rav\u8217's version of the {\i mishnah} was more expansive than Shmuel\u8217's: Rav held that not only does the {\i mema\u8217'enes} \u8212- the girl who refuses \u8212- receive no {\i ketubah}, but even a {\i ketanah} who was divorced by her husband while still a minor receives no {\i ketubah} either. The reasoning is that Rav views the entire institution as so fundamentally not-a-marriage that even when the husband goes through the formal process of giving a {\i get}, the transaction is treated as having been essentially play-acting. If the marriage never rose to the level of a real marriage, the {\i get} cannot transform it into one retroactively. And since there was never a real marriage, there is no {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Miaun vs.\u160 ?Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, by contrast, draws a sharp distinction. He teaches: {\i mema\u8217'enes ein lah ketubah} \u8212- the girl who refuses gets no {\i ketubah}, because her {\i mi\u8217'un} retroactively uproots the marriage. But {\i yotzei beget yesh lah ketubah} \u8212- if the husband gave her a {\i get} while she was still a {\i ketanah}, she does receive a {\i ketubah}. The reason is that in Shmuel\u8217's view, the {\i get} is not a nullification of the marriage \u8212- it is a termination of one. A {\i mi\u8217'un} says: there never was a marriage. A {\i get} says: there was a marriage, and now it is over. Even if the marriage was only rabbinic in origin, it was real while it lasted, and the {\i get} that ends it entitles the woman to the protections that normally accompany divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel draws out the full implications of this distinction: after {\i mi\u8217'un}, the man is permitted to marry her relatives and she is permitted to marry his, since retroactively they were never husband and wife. She is also not {\i pesulah} \u8212- not disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen} \u8212- because {\i mi\u8217'un} leaves no trace of a prior marriage. But if he gave her a {\i get}: {\i paslah min ha-achim} \u8212- she may not marry his brothers \u8212- and {\i paslah min ha-kehunah} \u8212- she is halachically a divorc\u233 ?e and may not marry a {\i kohen}. Even though it was only a rabbinic marriage and a rabbinic divorce, Shmuel insists that these consequences follow as they would from any recognized marital dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Three-Month Wait\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel adds a further ruling: a {\i mema\u8217'enes} does not need to wait three months before remarrying, while a {\i ketanah} who received a {\i get} does. The three-month waiting period exists to prevent uncertainty about the paternity of a potential child. One might think the concern is irrelevant in the case of a {\i ketanah}, since we do not expect a minor girl to conceive. Rashi addresses this with a {\i lo plug} rationale \u8212- the Rabbis did not subdivide their enactment: wherever there was a genuine marriage and a genuine divorce, even a rabbinic one, the three-month waiting period applies, regardless of whether pregnancy is practically possible. The analogy is to a post-menopausal woman who converts together with her husband \u8212- the Rabbis still require the three-month separation even when conception is demonstrably impossible, because the rule was applied broadly across all cases of recognized marital dissolution. The {\i mema\u8217'enes}, by contrast, never had a recognized marriage at all, so no waiting period applies. This ruling \u8212- the three-month requirement for a {\i ketanah} who received a {\i get} \u8212- was the specific {\i chiddush} that Shmuel was adding beyond what was already known from earlier sources.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether this dispute between Rav and Shmuel can be traced back to a {\i tanaitic} disagreement, and it finds one. A {\i baraita} records an explicit debate between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua on exactly this question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer: No Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer holds: {\i ein maaseh ketanah klum} \u8212- the legal acts of a {\i ketanah} are without effect. This sweeping statement encompasses the entire rabbinic institution of {\i kiddushei ketanah} when performed by the mother and brothers rather than the father. Because the father is absent and the marriage was not biblically authorized, Rabbi Eliezer treats it as non-marriage. The husband is not entitled to her found objects, her earnings, or the annulment of her vows; he does not inherit her; if he is a {\i kohen} he does not become {\i tamei} upon her death. {\i Klalo shel davar} \u8212- the summary principle \u8212- is that she is not his wife in any legal sense. The sole {\i halacha} that does apply is that she requires {\i mi\u8217'un} to exit: either she must affirmatively refuse before becoming a {\i bat mitzvah}, or else, if she remains with him past that point, she becomes his wife fully. But while she is a {\i ketanah}, nothing about the arrangement constitutes marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua: Binding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua disagrees fundamentally: {\i maaseh ketanah klum} \u8212- the acts of a {\i ketanah} do mean something. He holds that this rabbinic marriage is a genuine marriage in every respect. The husband is entitled to her found objects, her earnings, and the annulment of her vows; he inherits her; he becomes {\i tamei} to her. {\i Harei hi k\u8217'ishto l\u8217'chol davar} \u8212- she is his wife in every legal dimension. The one difference between her status and that of a fully adult wife is that she can exit by {\i mi\u8217'un} alone, without requiring a {\i get}. For an adult woman, only a {\i get} terminates the marriage; for this {\i ketanah}, {\i mi\u8217'un} suffices. But absent {\i mi\u8217'un}, the marriage is fully in force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraim Align with Tanna\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then maps the {\i amoraic} dispute onto the {\i tanaitic} one. Rav, who says that even a {\i get} does not give the {\i ketanah} any {\i ketubah}, follows Rabbi Eliezer: since the marriage was never real, giving a {\i get} changes nothing about its essential non-status, and no {\i ketubah} was ever owed. Shmuel, who distinguishes between {\i mi\u8217'un} and {\i get}, follows Rabbi Yehoshua: since the marriage was real \u8212- rabbinically binding and carrying all the normal marital consequences \u8212- a {\i get} is a genuine termination of a genuine marriage, and the {\i ketubah} is owed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} refines this, however. It notes that according to Rabbi Eliezer, there is no debate to be had: if the marriage was never real, obviously neither {\i mi\u8217'un} nor {\i get} changes that, and obviously there is no {\i ketubah}. The real dispute occurs {\i aliba deRabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- within the framework of Rabbi Yehoshua, who agrees the marriage was real. Shmuel simply applies Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's view consistently: real marriage, real divorce, real {\i ketubah}. But Rav, even granting Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's framework, introduces an asymmetry: Rabbi Yehoshua only said the marriage is real {\i mididah l\u8217'didei} \u8212- in the direction from her to him, meaning the husband enjoys all the marital benefits that flow from her. He did not say the marriage is real {\i mididei l\u8217'didah} \u8212- in the direction from him to her, meaning she is entitled to extract financial obligations from him. The {\i ketubah}, which flows from husband to wife, falls into that second category, and Rav denies it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asymmetrical Marriage Ri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Husband Incentive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the logic behind Rav\u8217's asymmetrical position. The institution of {\i kiddushei ketanah} by the mother and brothers was created entirely for the girl\u8217's benefit \u8212- to protect her in the absence of a father. But for the institution to function, a man had to be willing to enter it. Why would a man agree to marry a minor girl under these circumstances, especially given that, as Rashi notes, {\i ki heichi delo limnu mil\u8217'misbah} \u8212- we need to ensure that men not refrain from marrying her? An arrangement that gives him nothing while requiring financial obligations from him would provide no incentive whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother\u8217's Protection Moti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mother\u8217's motive for arranging such a marriage is clear: she either cannot afford to support another child, or she fears the girl will be taken advantage of without male protection. Rashi cites the formulation from the relevant chapter in Yevamos: {\i shema yinhagu bah minhag hefker} \u8212- lest people treat her as ownerless, with no one to protect her \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ein ketanah yoda\u8217'at lishmor atzmah me\u8217'lehitpatot} \u8212- since a minor girl does not know how to protect herself from being seduced. The marriage is thus a protective institution, not primarily a financial one. The girl moves into the man\u8217's household, where he provides for and protects her, until she comes of age and the marriage becomes fully binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the critical asymmetry in the arrangement is this: even once he takes her in and fulfills this protective role, she retains the right to walk away at any moment before she becomes a {\i bat mitzvah}. Unlike a marriage arranged by her father, which is biblically binding and locks in the arrangement, this rabbinic marriage carries no guarantee. The man can perform his obligations for years and then have her exercise {\i mi\u8217'un} and leave. Given this uncertainty, the only way to make the institution viable \u8212- the only way to give men any incentive to enter into it at all \u8212- is to grant the husband the full range of marital benefits from her side of the equation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Asymmetry Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is Rav\u8217's logic: we give the man everything that flows from her to him \u8212- her earnings, her found objects, her {\i peiros}, inheritance \u8212- because without that incentive no one would enter this arrangement, and the institution would fail in its purpose of protecting vulnerable minor girls. But we do not require him to pay the {\i ketubah} upon divorce, which is a financial cost that flows from him to her, because that cost is borne precisely in consideration of a real, locked-in marriage \u8212- and this arrangement is neither locked in nor guaranteed to become permanent. Rav may even concede, in other respects, that rabbinic consequences of the marriage apply \u8212- that she is {\i pesulah la-kehunah} after a {\i get}, that the relatives cannot intermarry \u8212- while still maintaining that the {\i ketubah}, as a particular institution requiring specific justification, was never attached to this arrangement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blaos Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel on Milog\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also states that these women do not receive {\i bla\u8217'os} \u8212- worn or consumed property. Rav Huna bar Chiya reports a ruling in the name of Shmuel that clarifies the scope of this provision: {\i lo shanu ela nichsei milog} \u8212- the rule that she does not recover property applies only to {\i nichsei milog}, the property over which she retained title throughout the marriage. {\i Nichsei milog} is property she brought into the marriage that was never formally transferred to the husband \u8212- the Ferrari in the marriage, so to speak, still legally hers, though he had the right to enjoy its use ({\i ochel peirot}). Normally, even if such property was consumed or destroyed during the marriage, she would be entitled to recover its value upon divorce, since it was always her principal. But in these cases, she is denied even that recovery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, says Shmuel, {\i nichsei tzon barzel} \u8212- the dowry that she formally assigned to him and that was recorded in the {\i ketubah} \u8212- she does recover. {\i Nichsei tzon barzel} is property over which the husband received legal title: she brought in the Jaguar as a dowry, it became his, but the obligation was that upon divorce he must return it or its equivalent value. In these cases, that obligation remains in force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling is counterintuitive. One would expect the reverse: that she recover the property over which she always held title ({\i nichsei milog}) and lose the property whose title had passed to him ({\i nichsei tzon barzel}). That she loses what was hers and keeps what was his seems backwards.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pappa\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa asks: on which case in the {\i mishnah} does Shmuel\u8217's ruling apply? The {\i mishnah} addresses the {\i mema\u8217'enes}, the {\i ailonis}, and the {\i shniya} \u8212- and each case presents different problems for Shmuel\u8217's formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider the {\i ailonis} case. The marriage was a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- a mistaken transaction. If the property is still present, she should simply walk away with all of it: there was no marriage, it was all hers. If it has been consumed or destroyed, the question is whether he was ever legitimately entitled to use it. The logic would seem to say: as for the {\i nichsei milog} \u8212- the property that was always legally hers \u8212- he was never entitled to sell or consume it without her consent, so if he did, he owes her reimbursement. As for the {\i nichsei tzon barzel} \u8212- the property over which he held title during what they both thought was a marriage \u8212- he was entitled at the time to treat it as his, and she has no legal instrument, namely no {\i ketubah}, to compel him to repay it. So the {\i ailonis} case suggests she should recover {\i nichsei milog} and lose {\i nichsei tzon barzel} \u8212- exactly the reverse of Shmuel\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shniya Reciprocal Penalty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes that Shmuel\u8217's ruling applies to the {\i shniya} case. Here the reasoning is explicitly punitive: {\i deknasu Rabbanan ledida bedidei uledidei bedida} \u8212- the Rabbis penalized each party through the other\u8217's entitlements. Normally she would recover her {\i nichsei milog}, since it was always her property. The Rabbis take that away from her as a penalty. Normally he would retain the {\i nichsei tzon barzel}, since title had passed to him. The Rabbis take that away from him and give it to her. Everyone loses something they were entitled to. The psychology is that the very deprivation of something one has a right to serves as a genuine disincentive \u8212- it is not merely a neutral reallocation but an experienced loss. Whether this fully explains the ruling or not, the {\i gemara} accepts it as the operative logic for the {\i shniya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cloak as Principal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From Rav Kahana\u8217's discussion of this ruling, the {\i gemara} draws a broader principle about consumable property in marriage. The case of a cloak brought into the marriage illustrates the point. One might think that when a wife brings a cloak into a marriage, we should conceptualize it as simply providing ten years of {\i peiros} \u8212- ten years of use \u8212- with no separate {\i keren}, no principal, that she is entitled to recover. On this view, the husband uses the cloak, it wears out, and there is nothing left for her to claim, because there was no conceptual principal separate from the use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this is not the {\i halacha}. The {\i gemara} infers from Rav Kahana\u8217's formulation that {\i karna havei} \u8212- a cloak brought into a marriage is treated as a principal, not merely as a stream of fruit. The husband cannot simply use it until it is worn out. The proper procedure is to sell it, convert the proceeds into land, and derive the {\i peiros} from the land\u8217's income \u8212- leaving an intact principal for her to recover. The existence of a {\i ketubah} obligation in a normal marriage reflects precisely this understanding: even consumables brought into a marriage are conceptualized as principal that she is entitled to take back out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman Disagrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman, however, disagrees. He holds that {\i peira havei} \u8212- a consumable brought into the marriage is treated as {\i peiros}, not as principal. In his view, when she brings a cloak, it is simply ten years of use, and the husband is entitled to wear it until it wears out, with nothing owed when it is gone. The {\i gemara} notes that this position would contradict the implication of the earlier ruling, since the whole discussion of {\i bla\u8217'os} and recovery of consumed property presupposes that there is a principal to be recovered in the first place. Rav Nachman\u8217's position disagrees with that framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Supplementary Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Base vs.\u160 ?Tosefes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further ruling of Shmuel addresses the {\i tosefet ketubah} \u8212- the supplementary amount that a husband adds to the {\i ketubah} beyond what the Rabbis required. The base {\i ketubah} is {\i maneh} for a non-virgin or {\i masayim} for a virgin; this is the rabbinically mandated amount. But many husbands added more as a personal gift to their wives. Shmuel rules: {\i lo shanu ela maneh masayim} \u8212- when we say these women forfeit their {\i ketubah}, we mean only the base amount mandated by the Rabbis. {\i Aval tosefes yesh lahen} \u8212- they do retain the supplementary gift. The reason is that the {\i tosefes} was not a rabbinic institution; it was a private gift from husband to wife. Even if the rabbinic marriage is retroactively undone, even if she is penalized for having entered a forbidden union, the gift he gave her personally \u8212- in recognition of the fact that they would be living as husband and wife, whatever the legal status of that arrangement \u8212- remains hers. A {\i baraita} confirms this reading: {\i ein lahem maneh masayim aval tosefes yesh lahem} \u8212- they do not have the hundred or two hundred, but they do have the additional amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anti-Halachic Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A different category of cases, however, receives harsher treatment. The {\i mishnah} elsewhere describes women who are divorced for actively violating the norms of Jewish law and marriage \u8212- women who behave in ways that constitute {\i overet al das}, going against the standards of conduct expected of a Jewish wife: flirting with other men, going out with her hair uncovered, feeding her husband non-{\i kosher} food. In those cases, not only does she forfeit the base {\i ketubah}, she forfeits the {\i tosefes} as well. The formulation there is not merely that she lacks a {\i ketubah} but that she must leave without one \u8212- a stronger formulation expressing active rabbinic censure of her conduct. {\i Ein lahem tosefes, v\u8217'chol sheken maneh masayim} \u8212- she loses the supplementary gift and certainly loses the mandated base amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adultery Case: Possessio\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related but distinct case involves a woman who is divorced because of a {\i shem ra} \u8212- a suspicion of adultery. Even in this extreme case, the ruling is: {\i notelet mah she-lefaneha v\u8217'yotzet} \u8212- she takes whatever property is physically present before her and leaves with it. The focus here shifts from the {\i ketubah} \u8212- which she does not collect \u8212- to the physical property she owns that is still present. She brought certain items into the marriage; they are still in the house; she is entitled to take them with her even in disgrace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna: Adultery/Clothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling supports a position of Rav Huna, who states: {\i zinsah lo hifsidah bla\u8217'oseha} \u8212- even if a woman commits adultery, she does not forfeit her {\i bla\u8217'os}, her worn clothing and physical belongings that are still present. The act of adultery, however grave, does not strip her of ownership of her physical possessions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clothing Forfeiture Deba\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tanna} taught before Rav Nachman an opposing view: {\i zinsah hifsidah bla\u8217'oseha kayamin} \u8212- if she commits adultery, she loses even her clothes that are still present. Rav Nachman\u8217's response is terse and pointed: {\i im hi zinsah, kalei mi zanai?} \u8212- if she committed adultery, did her clothing commit adultery? The penalty falls on her moral conduct; her physical property is an entirely separate matter. It would be a kind of absurdity to penalize inanimate objects for their owner\u8217's moral failing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensus: Property Pres\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the dispute by attributing the stricter position \u8212- that she loses even her clothing \u8212- to a {\i stam baraita} in the name of Rabbi Menachem, a minority opinion. Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbah bar bar Chana confirms: {\i aval chachamim omrim, zinsah lo hifsidah bla\u8217'oseha kayamin} \u8212- the sages hold that even an adulteress does not forfeit the physical property that is still present and in her possession. The consensus of the {\i chachamim} is that whatever she may have done, any property over which she held title and which is still physically present remains hers.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Knowledge Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna: Ailonit & Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i ailonis}, the {\i mishnah} states: {\i im techilah nisa\u8217'ah l\u8217'shem ilonis} \u8212- if from the outset he married her knowing she was an {\i ailonis}, then {\i yesh lah ketubah} \u8212- she receives a {\i ketubah}. If he knew, it was not a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut}; he entered with full knowledge and consented to this woman as his wife. Rav Huna draws a general principle from this: {\i ailonis isha v\u8217'einah isha} \u8212- an {\i ailonis} is in some respects his wife and in some respects not. The determining factor is his knowledge. {\i Heikir bah} \u8212- if he recognized her condition \u8212- {\i yesh lah ketubah}. {\i Lo heikir bah} \u8212- if he did not \u8212- {\i ein lah ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almanah: No Knowledge Ne\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, Rav Huna holds that an {\i almanah} \u8212- a widow who married a {\i kohen gadol}, or analogously a divorc\u233 ?e who married an ordinary {\i kohen} \u8212- is {\i ishah gemurah}, fully his wife in every respect, and she always receives a {\i ketubah} regardless of whether the {\i kohen} knew of her status or not. In Rav Huna\u8217's reading, the biblical prohibition does not transform the marriage into a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut}. She was not hiding anything about her personal nature \u8212- she is simply a widow, a status that does not affect her fitness as a woman but happens to disqualify her for a {\i kohen}. Whether he knew or not, the marriage was real, and she is entitled to the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehudah: Knowledge for B\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees. He holds: {\i achat zu v\u8217'achat zu isha v\u8217'einah isha} \u8212- the same rule applies to both the {\i ailonis} and the {\i almanah}. In both cases, knowledge is the determining factor. {\i Heikir bah, yesh lah ketubah; lo heikir bah, ein lah ketubah}. If the {\i kohen} did not know he was marrying a woman forbidden to him, then however we classify the marriage legally \u8212- whether or not it is technically binding \u8212- she is not entitled to a {\i ketubah}, because there was a fundamental failure of informed consent on his part.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metivta: Declaration vs.\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i metivta} \u8212- a {\i yeshiva} tradition \u8212- challenges the position of Rav Huna on the {\i almanah}. The {\i baraita} states: {\i kinsah b\u8217'chezkat shehi ken v\u8217'nimtzat shehi ken, yesh lah ketubah} \u8212- if he married her in the presumption that she was an {\i almanah} and she turned out to be an {\i almanah}, she receives a {\i ketubah}. The implication appears to be that this is precisely because he knew; had he not known, and had she turned out to be an {\i almanah}, she would not receive a {\i ketubah}. A second {\i baraita} makes this fully explicit: {\i kinsah biyeduah v\u8217'nimtzas biyeduah, yesh lah ketubah} \u8212- he married her knowingly and she turned out as expected, she gets the {\i ketubah}. {\i Kinsah stam v\u8217'nimtzat ken} \u8212- he married her without any explicit discussion and she turned out to be an {\i almanah} or {\i gerushah} \u8212- {\i ein lah ketubah}. This is a direct {\i tiyuvta} \u8212- a refutation \u8212- of Rav Huna\u8217's position that an {\i almanah} always receives a {\i ketubah} regardless of the {\i kohen}\u8217's knowledge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic makes intuitive sense: why would we presume that a {\i kohen} would be content to marry a woman biblically forbidden to him if he did not know of the prohibition? Since we cannot presume such consent, the absence of knowledge makes it a {\i ta\u8217'ut}, and she is not entitled to a {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna\u8217's Misreading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} accounts for Rav Huna\u8217's error: {\i veRav Huna matnisin at\u8217'yei} \u8212- Rav Huna was misled by the {\i mishnah}. He observed that the {\i mishnah} explicitly introduced the knowledge distinction in the case of the {\i ailonis} \u8212- stating that if he knew she was an {\i ailonis} she gets a {\i ketubah}, implying that without knowledge she does not \u8212- but made no such explicit distinction in the case of the {\i almanah}, simply stating she gets a {\i ketubah}. He reasonably concluded that the {\i almanah}\u8217's {\i ketubah} was unconditional. This was a defensible reading of the {\i mishnah} on its face.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution: Distinction\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty: when the {\i mishnah} moved from the {\i ailonis} to the {\i almanah}, it did so within the context of the distinction it had already established for the {\i ailonis}. The knowledge requirement was already on the table. When the {\i mishnah} then stated that the {\i almanah} receives a {\i ketubah}, it meant within that same framework \u8212- namely, that she receives it when he knew of her status, just as the {\i ailonis} does. The {\i mishnah} did not need to restate the distinction because it had already been set as the operative framework. Rav Huna read the silence as a contrast; the {\i gemara} reads it as continuation. In the end, both the {\i ailonis} and the {\i almanah} share the same rule: {\i yesh lah ketubah} only when he married her with knowledge of her status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 102\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stepdaughter Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek 12 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin the twelfth {\i perek} of {\i Ketubot} at the bottom of 101b, with the opening {\i mishnah} of the new chapter. The preceding chapter dealt extensively with the widow\u8217's collection from the estate \u8212- how she negotiates with the orphans, how assets are liquidated, and the mechanics of collecting the lump-sum {\i ikar ketubah} at the dissolution of the marriage. We now shift back to the ongoing obligations that subsist during the marriage itself, the {\i tnai ketubot} \u8212- the conditions and responsibilities that the {\i ketubah} imposes as a living framework. This particular {\i mishnah}, however, occupies an interesting middle ground: it does not concern the standard {\i tnai ketubot} imposed automatically by rabbinic legislation, but rather special contractual arrangements a woman might negotiate at the outset of a marriage to protect interests that the standard {\i ketubah} does not cover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Stipulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i hanosei et ha\u8217'ishah upaskah imo} \u8212- a man marries a woman and she arranges with him a specific condition. The initiative here is hers. She has a daughter from a prior marriage or another relationship, and she stipulates before the wedding that her new husband must support that daughter for five years. The husband bears no automatic legal obligation toward a stepdaughter \u8212- she is not his child, and the standard conditions of the {\i ketubah} do not extend to her. So the wife makes this a precondition: {\i kedei sheyezanet bitah chamesh shanim} \u8212- that he undertake to feed her daughter for five years. He agrees in order to marry her. The {\i mishnah} states simply: {\i chayav lezanah chamesh shanim} \u8212- he is obligated to feed her for five years. The force and mechanism of that obligation is precisely what the {\i gemara} will examine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage & Dual Obliga\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with the less obvious scenario. She subsequently divorces this first husband and remarries. With the second husband, she again stipulates: {\i paskah imo kedei sheyezanet bitah chamesh shanim} \u8212- you must agree to feed my daughter for five years. He agrees. The {\i mishnah} states that the second husband is also now obligated for five years of support \u8212- and critically, this obligation is entirely independent of the first husband\u8217's. The first husband\u8217's duty does not dissolve simply because she has remarried.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Husband\u8217's Liability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first husband cannot argue: {\i lichishetavo etzli azanah} \u8212- let her come stay with me and I will feed her. He assumed he was obligated only for the duration of the marriage, but that is not what he stipulated. He committed to feeding the daughter for five years, full stop, with no limitation to the period of the marriage. Accordingly, {\i ella molic lah mezonoteha limkom she\u8217'imah} \u8212- he must bring the food to wherever the daughter is living, which is now at the second husband\u8217's home with her mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Independent Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vechain lo yomru shneihem} \u8212- nor may the two husbands collude and say: {\i harei anu zanin otah ke\u8217'echad} \u8212- between the two of us we will manage to keep her fed, and we will divide the responsibility. Each bears an independent, full obligation. Therefore: {\i ella echad zanah ve\u8217'echad noten lah dmei mezonot} \u8212- one actually provides the food, and the other, since she is already being fed, must pay the monetary equivalent of the food costs. Both obligations run simultaneously and neither cancels the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Stepdaughter Marri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: if the daughter herself marries, her husband becomes obligated to feed her, as is standard. Nevertheless, {\i vehein notnim lah dmei mezonot} \u8212- both former stepfathers must still pay the food costs. She is being fed at her husband\u8217's table, but both stepfathers continue to pay for that food. The situation resembles what occurs in secular legal contexts as well: if a divorced woman remarries, the prior husband\u8217's child support obligation does not automatically terminate simply because the child now lives in a new household.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Stepfather Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meisu} \u8212- now both stepfathers have died. The {\i mishnah} addresses what happens when the estate is opened. The standard {\i tnai ketubot} includes the obligation to support natural daughters from the estate until they reach adulthood \u8212- {\i mezonot habanot}. So the natural daughters of both Reuven and Shimon are entitled to support from their respective estates. But the stepdaughter also has a claim against both estates for the remainder of her five years of support. How do these claims rank?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bnoteihem nizonot minechasim bnei chorin} \u8212- the natural daughters can only be supported from unencumbered property, meaning property that is still present in the estate and has not been sold off. {\i Vehi nizonis minechasim meshubadim} \u8212- but the stepdaughter can collect even from {\i meshubadim}, encumbered property, meaning property that was once in the estate but was subsequently sold to third parties. She has a lien that reaches those purchasers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is stated explicitly: {\i mipnei shehi kebaalas chov} \u8212- because she stands in the position of a creditor. Someone formally obligated himself to her with a debt, and she may collect from any property on which that debt created a lien. As for the natural daughters: their entitlement is one of the standard {\i tnai ketubot}, and {\i Chazal} explicitly ruled that this automatic obligation does not create liens on property sold from the estate. The reasoning is that the support obligation for daughters is inherently indeterminate \u8212- one cannot know in advance how many daughters there will be or for how many years the estate will need to support them. A purchaser of property from the estate would have no way to assess what liens might descend upon what he is buying. Protecting those purchasers from an unknowable and potentially unlimited exposure, the rabbis ruled that the daughters\u8217' claim applies only to property remaining in the estate. The stepdaughter\u8217's claim, by contrast, is a concrete, finite, specified debt \u8212- one girl, five years \u8212- and carries full liens accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this distinction is actually to the natural daughters\u8217' benefit. Since they are in any case limited to property remaining in the estate, having an additional claimant who is similarly limited would intensify the competition. The fact that the stepdaughter\u8217's claim reaches sold property removes her from competition with the natural daughters over what remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smart People\u8217's Clause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes with an observation: {\i hapikhin hayu kotvin} \u8212- prudent people, when accepting such an obligation, would write: {\i al menat she\u8217'ezon et bitkha chamesh shanim kol zman she\u8217'at imi} \u8212- on condition that I will feed your daughter for five years only as long as you are with me. {\i Rashi} explains the clause excludes three scenarios: if I die, if you die, or if we divorce \u8212- any of those terminates the obligation. A man who accepts this condition in unconstrained terms finds himself obligated regardless of what happens to the marriage; the wise man conditions it explicitly on the continuation of the marriage itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Admissions & Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oral Admission Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a dispute: {\i ha\u8217'omer l\u8217'chavero chayav ani lecha maneh} \u8212- if someone says to his friend, \u8220"I owe you a hundred {\i zuz}.\u8221" Rabbi Yochanan says {\i chayav} \u8212- he is obligated. Resh Lakish says {\i patur} \u8212- he is exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conversation vs Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: in precisely what case are they debating? If the person said {\i atem eidai} \u8212- \u8220"you be my witnesses\u8221" \u8212- designating bystanders to formally testify to his admission, then it is established elsewhere that there is no dispute: such a formal admission creates a binding obligation. Conversely, if he merely said it in passing conversation, with no designation of witnesses, it is likewise established elsewhere that such words have no legal weight. So what is the disputed case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i d\u8217'amar lei chayav ani lecha maneh b\u8217'shtar} \u8212- he wrote it in a document, without signing it, without witnesses to the document itself, but wrote in his own handwriting \u8220"I owe you a hundred {\i zuz}\u8221" and handed it over. Is such an informal written admission equivalent in weight to a formal oral declaration of {\i atem eidai}? That is the debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to understand the underlying principle. Within {\i halacha}, a monetary admission creates a legal fact independent of the underlying reality. If someone formally admits to a debt \u8212- even one that did not actually occur \u8212- the admission itself constitutes an enforceable obligation. {\i Rashi} notes from other {\i gemarot} that a signed document with witnesses is unquestionably a valid {\i shtar}; even a signed document without witnesses creates a {\i shtar}, albeit without {\i shiabud}, without liens on sold property, since it is insufficiently public. The question here is narrower: an unsigned, unwitnessed piece of paper in one\u8217's own handwriting, handed over. Is that weighty enough to create an obligation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan holds: {\i alima milta d\u8217'shtara k\u8217'man d\u8217'amar lehu atem eidai dami} \u8212- the written document is as powerful as an explicit oral designation of witnesses. Resh Lakish holds: {\i lo alima milta d\u8217'shtara} \u8212- the document is not that powerful; without explicit witness designation, even a written admission does not obligate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written vs Oral\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes that our {\i mishnah} itself supports the position of Rabbi Yochanan. The opening clause \u8212- that if a man stipulates to feed the daughter for five years he is obligated \u8212- should be unremarkable if he signed a formal {\i shtar}. The {\i chiddush}, the novelty, must lie in the mechanism of obligation. The {\i gemara} therefore reads the {\i mishnah} as speaking of a case where he wrote an informal written admission, and yet is held obligated \u8212- which would vindicate Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stipulations Before Wedd\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} declines this reading, however. It suggests instead that the {\i mishnah} concerns {\i bishtarei psikta} \u8212- stipulation documents, the type of financial arrangements negotiated and committed to prior to the wedding. Such obligations, the {\i gemara} argues, occupy a special legal category and become binding through a mechanism distinct from ordinary debt creation \u8212- even where ordinary means would be insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 In-Law Negotiations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This category is illustrated from the teaching of Rav Giddel in the name of Rav. When the prospective in-laws \u8212- the {\i shver} and {\i shvigger} on both sides \u8212- gather before the wedding and negotiate their respective financial contributions ({\i kama ata noten l\u8217'vincha} \u8212- how much are you giving your son; {\i kama ata noten l\u8217'bitkha} \u8212- how much are you contributing to your daughter), and they agree to specific amounts, and then {\i amdu ve\u8217'kidshu} \u8212- the couple proceeds and the {\i kiddushin} takes place \u8212- {\i hen hen had\u8217'varim hanniknin b\u8217'amira}: these are matters that become binding through speech alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ordinarily, a contractual obligation requires a formal act \u8212- a {\i kinyan}, such as the transfer of a {\i sudar} (a garment effecting {\i kinyan chalupin}), or the signing of a witnessed {\i shtar}. Here there was none of that. Yet the obligation is binding. Why? Because the verbal stipulations were conditions and prerequisites of the marriage, and when the marriage then occurred, the act of {\i kiddushin} itself validated and formalized those prior commitments. The {\i kiddushin} is the act that gives the words their binding force \u8212- this is what is called {\i kinyan amira} in this context, not a {\i kinyan} in the ordinary transactional sense, but the binding of words through the subsequent act to which they were attached as conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: is the binding force here contingent on the father of the daughter pocketing the {\i kesef kiddushin}, receiving something tangible that creates his {\i gmirat da\u8217'at} \u8212- his settled intent to obligate himself? The {\i gemara} addresses this directly. Rava says the rule applies even when the daughter is a {\i bogeret} and pockets the money herself, so the father receives nothing tangible. And certainly it applies to the father of the son, who receives nothing at all from the {\i kiddushin}. Rather: {\i b\u8217'hahu hana\u8217'ah d\u8217'kamichtani ahadadi} \u8212- the benefit they both receive is the pleasure of having their children married to one another. That satisfaction is the thing of value that creates the {\i gmirat da\u8217'at} and makes the obligation binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also clarifies a terminological subtlety. It is noted that the phrase {\i shtar psikta} is somewhat ironic, since nothing was necessarily written down \u8212- the obligation arose through speech followed by {\i kiddushin}. The {\i shtar}, if any, was a subsequent recording of what had been agreed, but the {\i shtar} itself did not effect the obligation. What effected it was the spoken commitment followed by the act of marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forms of Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pidyon Haben Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now brings a {\i tashma} \u8212- a case designed to test the scope of written obligations. Consider a father who owes five {\i sela\u8217'im} for {\i pidyon haben} \u8212- the redemption of the firstborn son. He writes to a {\i kohen}: {\i she\u8217'ani chayav lecha chamishah sela\u8217'im} \u8212- I owe you five {\i sela\u8217'im} \u8212- and hands him the document. He has not paid the money; he has only given an IOU. The ruling: {\i chayav litten lecha chamishah sela\u8217'im} \u8212- he now owes that {\i kohen} specifically five {\i sela\u8217'im}, but {\i uvno eino paduy} \u8212- his son is not redeemed, because the {\i pidyon haben} requires the actual transfer of money, not merely an acknowledged debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 IOU & Pre-existing Debt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: does this not prove that a simple written IOU creates a debt? He wrote it down, there was no signing, no witnesses mentioned, yet he owes. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i shani hasam d\u8217'meshubad lei mid\u8217'oraita} \u8212- the case there is different, because there already exists a Torah-mandated debt of five {\i sela\u8217'im}. The IOU does not create the obligation from nothing; it merely directs and specifies an existing obligation toward this particular {\i kohen}. The question of whether a written admission creates an obligation in a vacuum therefore remains unanswered by this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses on the internal logic. If the father already owed five {\i sela\u8217'im} to some {\i kohen}, why does he need to write the IOU at all? {\i K\u8217'dei l\u8217'varer l\u8217'kohen} \u8212- to specify and designate which {\i kohen} will receive the payment. Fine, but then: {\i b\u8217'no amai eino paduy?} \u8212- if the IOU effectively directs the {\i pidyon} obligation to this {\i kohen}, and when he pays it he is satisfying that very obligation, why is his son not redeemed upon payment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers with the ruling of Ulla: {\i dvar Torah paduy l\u8217'khesheyitten} \u8212- technically, by Torah law, once he pays, the son is redeemed, because the payment does fulfill the {\i pidyon} obligation. The rabbinic ruling that declares the son {\i eino paduy} even after payment is a {\i gezeirah} \u8212- a protective enactment: {\i shema yomru podin b\u8217'shtarot} \u8212- lest people conclude that one may redeem a firstborn son by handing over a document rather than actual money. If people saw the IOU handed over at the {\i pidyon haben} ceremony and concluded that documents alone suffice, they would err about the fundamental requirement of actual transfer. Therefore the rabbis required payment of a second five {\i sela\u8217'im} to formally accomplish the redemption, making it clear that the IOU alone was insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guarantor Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava: k\u8217'tana\u8217'ei} \u8212- this broader question of whether a written obligation creates a debt is in fact a dispute among the {\i Tanna\u8217'im} themselves. The case: {\i arev hayotzei achar chitum shtarot} \u8212- a guarantor whose commitment appears after the signatures of the witnesses. The loan document records that Reuven borrowed a hundred {\i zuz}; the witnesses sign. Then, beneath the witnesses\u8217' signatures, Shimon writes that he undertakes to guarantee the loan. The ruling: {\i goveh minechasim bnei chorin} \u8212- the lender may collect from property currently in Shimon\u8217's possession, but not from property Shimon has sold, because the guarantor\u8217's commitment does not carry liens. Without being part of the main, publicly attested document, the guaranty is binding but insufficient to create {\i shiabud} on sold property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strangling Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ma\u8217'aseh ba lifnei Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- a case of precisely this sort came before Rabbi Yishmael, and he ruled: {\i goveh minechasim bnei chorin} \u8212- you may collect from the guarantor\u8217's available property. Ben Nanas disagreed sharply: {\i eino goveh lo minechasim bnei chorin v\u8217'lo minechasim meshubadim} \u8212- you cannot collect anything from the guarantor, neither from property in his possession nor from property he has sold. Why? Ben Nanas offered his famous {\i mashal}: {\i harei shehayah chonek et chaveiro bashuk} \u8212- imagine someone was strangling his fellow in the marketplace over a debt. A third party intervenes and says: let go of him, and I will give you a hundred {\i zuz}. He lets go. {\i Patur} \u8212- the third party is not obligated to pay. {\i Shelo al emunato hilvahu} \u8212- the lender did not extend credit based on the guarantor\u8217's trustworthiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mashal} was puzzling until clarified through discussion: the strangling is not a metaphor for the lender withholding a loan \u8212- it represents the lender who has already lent the money and is now seeking to compel repayment from the borrower by force. The guarantor intervenes after the fact and offers to cover the debt. Since the lender\u8217's extension of credit predated the guaranty entirely, the guaranty was not a condition of the loan. {\i Shelo al emunato hilvahu} \u8212- the guarantor\u8217's promise was not what induced the lending. It is therefore not a serious, binding commitment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying legal principle is one of {\i gmirat da\u8217'at} \u8212- settled, serious intent. Ben Nanas holds that a commitment made after the debt is already established, when there are no special conditions making it obligatory, lacks the requisite {\i gmirat da\u8217'at}. The written signature does not suffice to supply that seriousness independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan vs Resh L\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now maps the {\i Tanna\u8217'im} onto the {\i Amoraim}: {\i leima Rabbi Yochanan d\u8217'amar k\u8217'Rabbi Yishmael, v\u8217'Reish Lakish d\u8217'amar k\u8217'Ben Nanas}. Rabbi Yochanan holds like Rabbi Yishmael \u8212- writing obligates, even when the commitment came after the loan was made, even without special preconditions; the act of writing is itself sufficient {\i gmirat da\u8217'at}. Resh Lakish holds like Ben Nanas \u8212- writing alone does not obligate; there must be special conditions, and a post-facto signature without those conditions is an empty gesture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} refines the comparison, however. {\i Aliba d\u8217'Ben Nanas kulei alma lo pligi} \u8212- according to Ben Nanas, everyone agrees that a simple IOU creates no obligation. The question is only whether one can derive something from Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's position. Rabbi Yochanan says yes \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael holds that writing obligates in all cases. Resh Lakish says no \u8212- even Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's ruling is limited to situations where a pre-existing debt already exists. {\i Ad kan lo ka\u8217'amar Rabbi Yishmael hatam ela d\u8217'shaycha lei l\u8217'shibuda d\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael only held the guaranty binding because there was already a real debt, and the guarantor was effectively including himself within that pre-existing obligation. Without a pre-existing debt, Rabbi Yishmael would agree that writing alone creates nothing. So both the {\i pidyon haben} case and the guarantor case involve pre-existing debts; neither proves that a bare IOU, written in a vacuum, obligates.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stipulation Binding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Words + Marriage = Bindi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the statement of Rav Giddel cited earlier. The in-laws gather, negotiate their financial contributions to the couple, agree on specific amounts, and then the couple proceeds to {\i kiddushin}. {\i Hen hen had\u8217'varim hanniknin b\u8217'amira} \u8212- these become binding obligations through speech alone, by virtue of the {\i kiddushin} that followed. The mechanism is {\i kinyan amira} \u8212- the words acquire binding force through the subsequent act of marriage, without any conventional {\i kinyan} of handkerchief or coin. Because both families wanted the marriage to occur, and committed financially in order to make it happen, and the marriage then occurred, the benefit they sought and received \u8212- the {\i hana\u8217'ah d\u8217'kamichtani ahadadi}, the joy of their children\u8217's marriage \u8212- serves as the consideration that seals the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Can Oral Become Written?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina then poses a sharp question to Rav Ashi: {\i devarim halelu nitnu lehikatev v\u8217'lo nitnu lehikatev} \u8212- can these verbal stipulations, once they have become binding through {\i kiddushin}, subsequently be formalized in writing as a {\i shtar} with liens? The practical import is significant: a verbal obligation, even a binding one, does not carry {\i shiabud} over sold property. If one can convert it after the fact into a {\i shtar}, the creditor would gain the ability to collect from purchasers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Retroactive Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi answers: {\i lo nitnu lehikatev} \u8212- they cannot be so written. Obligations that originated as verbal stipulations, even those that became binding through {\i kiddushin}, cannot be retroactively converted into a formal {\i shtar} with liens. The document must originate as a document to carry the weight of one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina then attempts a challenge from the {\i mishnah}: {\i hapikhin hayu kotvin} \u8212- the prudent people would write. Does this not demonstrate that these verbal stipulations were in fact written down? Rav Ashi responds: {\i mai kotvin \u8212- omrin} \u8212- the word {\i kotvin} here does not mean \u8220"write\u8221" in the literal sense; it means they would {\i say}. The {\i mishnah} uses the idiom of writing to describe a verbal formulation. This is unusual, and the {\i gemara} acknowledges as much, but draws a parallel: the {\i Mishnah} elsewhere says {\i ha-kosev l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- one who writes to his wife \u8212- and {\i Tanna Rabbi Chiya} reformulates this as {\i ha-omer l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- one who says to his wife \u8212- demonstrating that the Talmudic literature uses \u8220"writing\u8221" and \u8220"saying\u8221" interchangeably in certain contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Kiddushin Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further challenge is raised from another {\i tanna\u8217'itic} teaching: {\i ein kotvin shtarei eirusin v\u8217'nisuin ela mi-da\u8217'as shneihem} \u8212- documents of {\i eirusin} and {\i nisuin} may only be written with the consent of both parties. If both consent, they are written. Does this not imply that the pre-wedding financial stipulations \u8212- the {\i shtarei psikta} \u8212- can be written down as formal documents when both sides agree? The {\i gemara} rejects this reading: {\i lo shtarei eirusin mamash} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} refers not to financial stipulations but to the actual {\i shtar kiddushin} itself, the document by which a man marries a woman through writing rather than through a ring or money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unilateral Writing Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then engages the specifics of the {\i shtar kiddushin}. {\i Kasvu li-shma v\u8217'she-lo mi-da\u8217'atah} \u8212- if a man wrote a document stating {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, with this specific woman in mind ({\i li-shma}), but without her participation or knowledge at the time of writing, and then presented it to her \u8212- is this a valid {\i kiddushei shtar}? Rabbah and Ravina say {\i mekudeshes} \u8212- it is valid. Rav Papa and Rav Shavaya say {\i einah mekudeshes} \u8212- it is not. The teaching that {\i shtarei eirusin} require the consent of both parties aligns with Rav Papa and Rav Shavaya: the woman must be a party to the writing of the document that effects her {\i kiddushin}. In any case, the {\i mishnah} there is about the {\i shtar kiddushin}, not about the financial pre-wedding stipulations \u8212- and so it does not prove that those verbal obligations can be reduced to writing after the fact.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Liens Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stepdaughter Creditor St\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We return to the end of our {\i mishnah}. {\i Meisu} \u8212- when the stepfathers die, their natural daughters are supported from {\i nechasim bnei chorin} \u8212- property in the estate. The stepdaughter, however, {\i nizonis minechasim meshubadim} \u8212- may collect even from encumbered property, property that has been sold, {\i mipnei shehi kebaalas chov} \u8212- because she stands as a creditor with a formal debt against the estate. The {\i mishnah} is explicit: she has liens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Shtar Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking feature of the {\i mishnah}. If her obligation arose as a verbal stipulation \u8212- the mechanism we have been analyzing throughout the {\i sugya} \u8212- how does she have liens? Verbal obligations, even those that become binding through {\i kiddushin}, do not carry {\i shiabud} over sold property. Liens require a public, formally attested {\i shtar}. The fact that the {\i mishnah} grants her liens therefore appears to imply that her obligation was in fact created by a formal {\i shtar} \u8212- a document with {\i kinyan} and witnesses \u8212- from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Standard Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confirms this: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan b\u8217'shekanu miyado} \u8212- the case here is one where a formal {\i kinyan} was performed. The {\i mishnah}, when it reaches the topic of liens and the stepdaughter\u8217's status as a creditor, is describing a scenario where the obligation was formalized in a classic {\i shtar} \u8212- {\i kinyan}, witnesses, the full apparatus of debt creation. The {\i gemara} then asks: if so, why do the natural daughters not have liens as well? Because {\i eihi d\u8217'leisa b\u8217'tnai beit din} \u8212- the stepdaughter is not within the standard automatic obligations that rabbinic law imposes on the husband. She is outside the {\i tnai beit din} entirely. Since there is no automatic obligation to her, the stepfather must create one from scratch; that requires a formal {\i kinyan} and a formal {\i shtar}, and naturally that formal {\i shtar} carries liens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption for Daughters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The natural daughters, by contrast, stand within the {\i tnai beit din} \u8212- their support is an automatically imposed obligation of the {\i ketubah}. Even if a father wished to create additional formal obligations to his natural daughters through a {\i kinyan} and {\i shtar}, the {\i gemara} holds that it would not generate liens in their favor. The reason: {\i eima tzerorei apistinhu} \u8212- purchasers of property from the estate can always presume, in the case of natural daughters, that the father made prior arrangements for them. Because the obligation to support daughters is so well established and deeply imposed, a purchaser may defend himself by arguing that the father surely set aside funds or made other arrangements; the daughters cannot overcome that presumption to collect from the purchaser\u8217's property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The simpler dimension of this principle is already familiar: the obligation to support natural daughters is inherently indeterminate in scope \u8212- one cannot know at the time of purchase how many daughters there are or for how many years of support might remain. Protecting purchasers from an unknowable exposure, the rabbis ruled categorically that daughters\u8217' support claims reach only property in the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary: Shtar vs Default\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} thus arrives at a clean structural distinction. The stepdaughter\u8217's obligation is a formal {\i shtar} with {\i kinyan} and witnesses \u8212- created from scratch, outside the scope of any automatic obligation, carrying full {\i shiabud} and the right to collect from sold property. The natural daughters\u8217' entitlement is an automatic imposition of rabbinic law, part of the {\i tnai beit din} of the {\i ketubah} \u8212- never formalized in a {\i shtar}, carrying no liens, reachable only from property remaining in the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} can also be read in its simplest sense as having always been about a formal {\i shtar}: he stipulated, she demanded, he signed a formal document with a {\i kinyan} \u8212- and that is why she is a creditor, that is why she has liens, that is the {\i peshita} of the {\i mishnah}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's extended analysis sought to determine whether perhaps the opening clauses reflected a simpler or more ambiguous form of obligation, and found in the end that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's grant of creditor status to the stepdaughter presupposes the full apparatus of formal debt creation. The verbal stipulations preceding {\i kiddushin} create real and binding obligations \u8212- but the kind of lien-bearing creditor status our {\i mishnah} describes belongs to the formal {\i shtar} alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 104\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening & Amud ID\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amud Corrections\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 104. We pick up on 103b \u8212- not 104b or 105a as one might initially suppose \u8212- in the middle of a series of narratives concerning the final days of Rebbe and the transition of leadership that followed his death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe\u8217's Final Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most recent episode in the {\i gemara} had described how Rebbe appointed Chanina bar Chama as {\i rosh yeshiva}. The {\i gemara} had further noted how Chanina bar Chama deferred to Rabbi Afas, who was older, allowing Rabbi Afas to occupy the position for several years before him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Not R. Chiya?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya Death Chronology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this background established, the {\i gemara} raises a striking difficulty. The passage begins approximately ten lines before the wider-set lines, just below the citation from Iyov 22. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i veha havi Rabbi Chiya} \u8212- but Rabbi Chiya was present. Why was Rabbi Chanina appointed {\i rosh yeshiva} rather than Rabbi Chiya, who was by all accounts Rebbe\u8217's preeminent {\i talmid} and the editor of the {\i Tosefta}? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i nach nafshe} \u8212- Rabbi Chiya had already passed away before Rebbe died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this answer immediately invites a challenge. Does the {\i gemara} not record a statement by Rabbi Chiya himself \u8212- {\i ani ra\u8217'isi kivro shel Rebbe} \u8212- that he had seen Rebbe\u8217's grave and wept over it? This implies Rabbi Chiya survived Rebbe, not the reverse. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ipocha} \u8212- reverse the attribution. It was Rebbe who saw Rabbi Chiya\u8217's grave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source Contradictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a second difficulty: a {\i braisa} records Rabbi Chiya\u8217's statement that {\i oso yom shemeis Rebbe batla kedusha} \u8212- on the day Rebbe died, holiness ceased. This again implies Rabbi Chiya was alive to witness Rebbe\u8217's death. Again the {\i gemara} proposes: {\i ipocha} \u8212- reverse it, and understand it as Rebbe saying this about Rabbi Chiya\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third source complicates matters further. Another {\i braisa} records that when Rebbe fell ill, Rabbi Chiya came to visit him and found him weeping. Rabbi Chiya asked: {\i mipnei ma ata boche} \u8212- why are you crying? The {\i braisa} had just stated that dying while laughing is a good sign ({\i siman yafe lo}) while dying in tears is a bad sign ({\i siman ra lo}), and the list of omens continues: dying face-up is a good sign, face-down a bad sign; facing the people, a good sign; facing the wall, a bad sign; a sallow, greenish complexion is a bad sign; a bright and ruddy face is a good sign. Dying on {\i erev shabbos} is a good sign \u8212- one enters {\i olam haba} as {\i shabbos} begins; dying at {\i motzaei shabbos} is a bad sign. Dying on {\i erev Yom Kippur} is a bad sign, for one dies before one\u8217's sins are atoned; dying at {\i motzaei Yom Kippur} is a good sign. And dying from an illness of the digestive tract \u8212- {\i cholei me\u8217'ayim} \u8212- is a good sign, {\i mipnei sheruban shel tzaddikim meisim becholi me\u8217'ayim}, because we know that most righteous people die from such illness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbe\u8217's answer to Rabbi Chiya\u8217's question was: {\i ana al Torah umitzvos kavachina} \u8212- I am weeping on account of the Torah and {\i mitzvos} I am about to lose. This is not quite an answer to the concern about the omen of dying in tears, but the {\i braisa} clearly depicts Rabbi Chiya present at Rebbe\u8217's bedside while Rebbe was still alive. The {\i gemara} again proposes {\i ipocha}, reversing the attribution \u8212- but by now, the evidence is piling up that it was in fact Rebbe who predeceased Rabbi Chiya, and the reversals are growing strained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya\u8217's Mitzvot Work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore offers a decisive alternative: {\i le\u8217'olam lo siphach} \u8212- do not reverse any of these sources. Rabbi Chiya did indeed outlive Rebbe. The reason he was not appointed {\i rosh yeshiva} is that {\i Rabbi Chiya asak bemitzvos hava} \u8212- Rabbi Chiya was deeply engaged in going out into the world and doing {\i mitzvos}, not remaining cloistered in the {\i beis medrash}. The {\i gemara} adds: {\i Rebbe savar lo afagnei} \u8212- Rebbe did not wish to injure him, meaning he did not want to deprive Rabbi Chiya of the opportunity to continue that work by tethering him to the {\i rosh yeshiva} appointment. Crucially, this does not imply Rabbi Chiya was intellectually inferior to Chanina bar Chama \u8212- if anything, the framing suggests the opposite. Rebbe simply refused to strip him of the freedom to pursue his broader mission.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chanina vs.\u160 ?Chiya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanina\u8217's Intellect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} to a celebrated passage. Rabbi Chanina and Rabbi Chiya were once engaged in vigorous debate \u8212- the {\i gemara} uses the word {\i mintzi}, they were fighting, not with fists, but fiercely with words. Rabbi Chanina said to Rabbi Chiya: {\i bahadi didi minatzit?} \u8212- do you think you can contend with me? {\i De\u8217'im chas veshalom mishtakcha Torah miYisrael} \u8212- for if, God forbid, Torah were forgotten from the Jewish people, {\i mehadarna lah mipilpuli} \u8212- I could restore it all through my intellectual analytical powers alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya\u8217's Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya replied: {\i ana avdi dela mishtakcha Torah miYisrael} \u8212- I am the one who ensures that Torah will not be forgotten from Israel in the first place. You focus on how brilliant you are and how you could reconstruct everything after the fact; I will go out and prevent the loss from occurring at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya\u8217's Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then described his method in striking detail. {\i De\u8217'aisina kitna} \u8212- I take flax and plant it. From what grows I weave a net. {\i Vetzaidna tavya} \u8212- I trap deer. {\i Umachilna bisra leyasmi} \u8212- I feed the meat to orphans. {\i Va\u8217'arichna megilta mimishchei detavya} \u8212- I prepare Torah scrolls from the hides of the deer. {\i Vesalikna lemata delais bah makrei dardakei} \u8212- I go to a city that has no teacher for children, no {\i cheder} teacher. {\i Vuchisivna chamisha chumshei lechamisha yanukei} \u8212- I write out the five books of the Torah for five different children. {\i Umasnina shita sidrei leshita yanukei} \u8212- and I teach the six orders of {\i Mishnah} to six different children, one order per child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distribution Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then comes the key instruction: {\i ulechol chad vechad amrei lei, atni sidrach lechavrach} \u8212- to each child I say: teach what you know to your friend. The result is a network in which everyone is simultaneously student and teacher, the responsibility is distributed broadly, and no single person bears the entire weight. One need not be the most brilliant mind in the room to take part in the sacred work of sustaining Torah. This is also, implicitly, a pointed critique of Rabbi Chanina\u8217's posture: the idea that Torah rests entirely on one person\u8217's extraordinary intellect \u8212- that it is all about {\i me} and {\i my} Torah \u8212- is precisely the kind of thinking Rabbi Chiya\u8217's model challenges. And beyond the intellectual dimension, there is a further point: a great {\i talmid chacham} need not confine himself to the {\i beis medrash}. He can go out and plant flax, catch deer, feed orphans. His responsibilities extend into the real world, not only into the intellectual sphere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe Approves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vahaynu de\u8217'amar Rebbe} \u8212- and this is the meaning of Rebbe\u8217's famous statement: {\i kama gedolim ma\u8217'asei Chiya} \u8212- how great are the deeds of Chiya. The contrast the {\i gemara} draws is pointed: the {\i rosh yeshiva} kept in his intellectual tower, and the person who goes out into the world. And again, the {\i gemara} nowhere suggests Rabbi Chiya was incapable of being {\i rosh yeshiva} \u8212- only that Rebbe refused to confine him to it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Clarifications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon bar Rebbe then pressed Rebbe: {\i afilu mimcha?} \u8212- is Rabbi Chiya even greater than you? Rebbe answered: {\i in} \u8212- yes, his deeds certainly are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshaphat Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael the son of Rabbi Yossi then asked: {\i afilu me-abba} \u8212- even greater than my father? Rebbe answered: {\i chas veshalom, lo tehei kazot be-Yisrael} \u8212- God forbid, may no such thing be said in Israel. The precise point of the Rabbi Yossi comparison is left somewhat opaque by the {\i gemara}, though it is worth noting that Rabbi Yossi, unlike a pure {\i rosh yeshiva}, bore political responsibilities as {\i nasi}. In any case, the {\i gemara} moves on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of the challenge regarding whether Rebbe\u8217's instruction to Rabban Gamliel \u8212- to hold his leadership {\i beramim}, in heights, and to inspire awe in students \u8212- contradicts the verse {\i ve\u8217'et yirei Hashem yechabed}, that one should honor those who fear God, is addressed directly. The {\i gemara} cites the tradition that this verse refers to Yehoshaphat king of Yehuda, who would rise from his throne, embrace, and kiss a Torah sage, calling out {\i Rabbi, Rabbi, Mari, Mari} \u8212- my teacher, my teacher, my master, my master. Surely even a king is obligated to show honor to Torah sages. The {\i gemara} resolves the tension: {\i lo kashya ha betzina ha befarhesya} \u8212- it is not difficult. In private, one must cultivate {\i yirat cheit} and genuine humility; in public, one must embody the dignity of the office. It is a difficult balance to maintain \u8212- to hold genuine humility in one\u8217's inner life while not allowing that humility to undercut the authority the position demands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deathbed Instructions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Youngest Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lehu livni katana ani tzarich} \u8212- Rebbe said, I need my youngest son. Rabbi Shimon entered. {\i Umasar lo sidrei chochmah} \u8212- Rebbe transmitted to him the {\i sidrei chochmah}, the tools or structures of wisdom. The precise content of this transmission is left unspecified \u8212- commentators suggest it concerned how a sage should conduct himself, how to present himself to the larger community, perhaps certain approaches to learning. The narrative suggests something secret or esoteric, because one does not transmit the full practical skills of a vocation in the final moments of life; what one transmits at such a moment is something of a different order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eldest Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lehu livni gadol ani tzarich} \u8212- Rebbe then said, I need my eldest son. Rabban Gamliel \u8212- who was to become the {\i nasi} \u8212- entered. {\i Umasar lo sidrei nesiyut} \u8212- Rebbe transmitted to him the structures of leadership. Again, the language of transmission at the deathbed suggests something beyond ordinary professional instruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leadership Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbe\u8217's explicit charge to Rabban Gamliel was: {\i beni nehog nesiyutecha beramim} \u8212- conduct your leadership in heights, assume positions of honor. Rashi explains {\i beramim} as meaning {\i she-tehei yoshev bein gedolim} \u8212- that one must sit among the great, assume the appropriate posture of position, even if not by personal inclination. {\i U-zerok mara be-talmidim} \u8212- and cast fear into the students. Even if that is not one\u8217's natural disposition, one must act in a way befitting the office. The {\i gemara} immediately asks whether this conflicts with the obligation to honor those who fear God, and resolves it as noted above: privately one cultivates humility, publicly one must own the role.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Clarifications (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Private vs.\u160 ?Public\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution \u8212- {\i lo kashya ha betzina ha befarhesya} \u8212- draws the distinction between private conduct and public conduct. In private, genuine humility and {\i yirat cheit} are the measure. In public, the dignity and authority of the office must be upheld. The challenge, as the {\i gemara} implicitly acknowledges, is maintaining both simultaneously: that the {\i yirat Shamayim} not collapse into a performance, and that the public role not harden into arrogance. This tension runs through the entire sequence of deathbed instructions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rebbe\u8217's Relocation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Air Quality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} then records: {\i Rabbi mutal be-Tzippori} \u8212- Rebbe was sick in Tzippori, yet {\i umakom muchan lo be-Beit She\u8217'arim} \u8212- the place designated for his burial was Beit She\u8217'arim. Rebbe had relocated to Tzippori in the final years of his life, having previously maintained his seat of {\i nesiyut} in Beit She\u8217'arim for many years \u8212- the famous {\i kuchin}, catacombs, remain there. The {\i gemara} notes that a {\i braisa} had stated {\i tzedek tzedek tirdof, halach achar Rabbi le-Beit She\u8217'arim}, suggesting Rebbe was based in Beit She\u8217'arim. The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i Rabbi be-Beit She\u8217'arim havah, ella keivan de-chalash, amtivuhu le-Tzippori} \u8212- he had been in Beit She\u8217'arim, but once his health declined they brought him to Tzippori, {\i demadalya de-ayara u-vasim avira} \u8212- because it is elevated and the air is sweeter, the air quality being better for his condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fast Day Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hahu yoma de-nach nafshei de-Rebbi} \u8212- on the day that Rebbe was to die, it was not yet the moment of death but it was clear that the end was imminent. {\i Gazru rabbanan taanita} \u8212- the Rabbis decreed a fast day and prayed intensely. {\i Ve-amri: kol man de-amar nach nafshei de-Rebbi, yidaker be-charev} \u8212- they declared that anyone who announced Rebbe had died would be run through with a sword. This reflects both the raw denial of the moment and their wish to sustain their prayers without any disruption.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maidservant Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prayer for Life\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Selika amteih de-Rebbi} \u8212- Rebbe\u8217's maidservant ascended to the roof. She declared: {\i elyonim mevakshim et Rebbi, ve-tachtonim mevakshim et Rebbi} \u8212- the supernal realms desire Rebbe\u8217's soul, and those below wish to keep him here. {\i Yehi ratzon she-yakufu tachtonim et elyonim} \u8212- may it be God\u8217's will that the lower realms prevail over the upper, and that the prayers to keep Rebbe alive be accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Observing Suffering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then she observed Rebbe\u8217's condition. {\i Keivan de-chazai kama zimnei de-ayeil le-veit ha-kissei ve-chalatz tefillin u-manach le-hu ve-ka mitzta\u8217'er} \u8212- she saw how many times he had to go to the bathroom, removing his {\i tefillin} each time and replacing them, and how much anguish he was suffering. It is worth noting \u8212- as a participant in the {\i shiur} pointed out \u8212- that this suffering is precisely the {\i cholei me\u8217'ayim}, the digestive illness, that the {\i braisa} just described as a good sign for a righteous person; the maidservant, however, saw only the unbearable pain. She reversed her prayer: {\i yehi ratzon she-yakufu elyonim et ha-tachtonim} \u8212- may the upper realms prevail, may Rebbe be released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moment of Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-la havu shatkei rabbanan milimeiv\u8217'ei rachamei} \u8212- but the Rabbis would not stop praying, and their prayers were effectively keeping Rebbe\u8217's soul tethered to this world. {\i Shakla kuza ve-shadya mei-igra le-ar\u8217'a} \u8212- the maidservant took a jug and threw it from the roof to the ground. {\i Ishtiku me-rachamei} \u8212- the Rabbis were startled into momentary silence. {\i Ve-nach nafshei de-Rebbi} \u8212- in that moment, Rebbe died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Medical Ethics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} is invoked in discussions of medical ethics regarding end-of-life care. The question it raises is not one of actively hastening death, but whether there exists an obligation to employ every possible measure to sustain life down to its last second, or whether it can sometimes be an act of {\i rachamim} to allow the natural process of death to proceed without further intervention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel is worth noting, raised in the {\i shiur}: the story of David HaMelech, who similarly would not stop learning, so that {\i malach ha-mavet} had to resort to a distraction to accomplish its task. It is likely an intentional resonance, given that Rebbe traced his lineage back to David HaMelech. David had {\i malach ha-mavet} on one side, Rebbe had the Rabbis\u8217' prayers on the other \u8212- in both cases, continuous engagement prevented the moment of death, and in both cases an external distraction broke the continuity. The parallel is further enriched by the observation that in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, unlike in {\i Bavel}, political and Torah leadership were not neatly separated. Rebbe, as {\i nasi}, was himself appointing the {\i rosh yeshiva} \u8212- something striking in its own right. And just as Yehoshaphat had been mentioned moments earlier, the implicit threads connecting Rebbe to the Davidic line run through the entire passage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bar Kappara Eulogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Garment Torn\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amru lei rabbanan le-Bar Kappara: zil ayein} \u8212- the Rabbis sent Bar Kappara to see what was happening. Perhaps this was prompted by the sound of the thrown jug, perhaps by some other sign that something had changed. {\i Azal ashkacheih de-nach nafshei} \u8212- he went in and found Rebbe had died. {\i Kareih le-levushei ve-ahadreih le-kareih le-achorei} \u8212- he rent his garment and turned the tear to the back, presumably so as not to immediately shock the assembled sages and students outside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poetic Eulogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Patach ve-amar} \u8212- he came out and declared: {\i erelim u-metzukim achazu ba-aron ha-kodesh} \u8212- the angels and the righteous ones grasped the holy ark. {\i Nitzchu Areilei et ha-Metzukim} \u8212- the angels prevailed over the righteous ones. {\i Ve-nishbah aron ha-kodesh} \u8212- and the holy ark was taken captive. The {\i aron ha-kodesh} refers to Rebbe himself; the {\i metzukim} are the righteous ones \u8212- from {\i metzukei eretz}, those who are the foundations of the earth. The heavenly forces won the struggle over the earthly ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evasion Technique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amru lei: nach nafshei?} \u8212- the Rabbis asked him: is he saying Rebbe has died? {\i Amar lehu: atun kamritu, va\u8217'ana lo kamina} \u8212- you said it, not I. Bar Kappara was not about to risk being run through with the sword the Rabbis had sworn to apply to anyone who made the announcement. The maneuver recalls the principle that one should not be {\i motzi dibah}, not voice something ruinous \u8212- by constructing his statement in poetic, deniable form, Bar Kappara communicated the news while maintaining that he himself had never said it outright.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Fingers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bish\u8217'at ptirato she\u8217'Rebbe} \u8212- at the moment of Rebbe\u8217's death, {\i zakaf eser etzba\u8217'otav klapei ma\u8217'alah} \u8212- he raised his ten fingers toward heaven and declared: {\i Ribono shel olam, galui veyadu\u8217'ah lefanecha she\u8217'yagati be\u8217'eser etzba\u8217'otai batorah} \u8212- Master of the Universe, it is known and revealed before You that I have labored with all ten fingers in Torah, {\i v\u8217'lo neheneti afilu be\u8217'etzba ktanah} \u8212- and I did not derive benefit from this world even so much as a little finger\u8217's worth. {\i Yehi ratzon milfanecha she\u8217'yehei shalom bimnuchati} \u8212- may it be Your will that there be peace in my rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The claim that he derived no benefit from the world is striking in light of other {\i gemaros} that describe Rebbe and Antoninus sharing fine delicacies at their table. One resolution, noted in the {\i shiur}, lies in the earlier distinction between private and public conduct: Rebbe may have participated in such meals as a matter of necessity for his public role as {\i nasi}, without genuinely desiring those pleasures for himself. Another reading, suggested during the discussion, is that {\i lo neheneti} refers specifically to Torah work: he never took {\i honoraria} for giving a {\i shiur} or used his Torah scholarship as a means of personal gain. {\i Tosafot}, however, reads the phrase literally, citing a {\i midrash} that while one prays for Torah to enter one\u8217's body, one should also pray that delicacies not enter one\u8217's body \u8212- the statement being about not investing one\u8217's life in physical pleasure rather than spiritual endeavor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heavenly Voice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yatzta bat kol ve-amrah} \u8212- a heavenly voice responded with the verse: {\i yavo shalom, yanuchu al mishkevotam} \u8212- they come in peace, let them rest on their resting places. The {\i gemara} notes a slight grammatical puzzlement: since the {\i bat kol} was responding to Rebbe\u8217's prayer for peace in his rest, one might have expected the singular {\i mishkevecha} \u8212- your resting place \u8212- rather than the plural {\i mishkevotam} \u8212- their resting places. This question opens the next teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous Greeters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mesiyi\u8217'a lei} \u8212- this supports the teaching of Rabbi Chiya bar Gamda (a different Rabbi Chiya than the one discussed earlier) citing Rabbi Yose bar Shaul: {\i bish\u8217'a she\u8217'hatzaddik niftar min ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- when a righteous person departs from the world, the ministering angels say before God: {\i Ribono shel olam, tzaddik ploni ba} \u8212- Master of the Universe, the righteous person so-and-so is arriving. God responds: {\i yavo\u8217'u tzaddikim ve-yeitzu likrato ve-omeru lo yavo veshalom} \u8212- let all the righteous people of the heavenly realm come out to greet him and say to him, come in peace. Then, once they have greeted him, {\i yanuchu al mishkevotam} \u8212- all the righteous ones return to their resting places. The plural refers to the righteous ones of the heavenly realm who came out to welcome the newly arrived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Angel Groups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar adds further detail: {\i bish\u8217'a she\u8217'tzaddik niftar min ha\u8217'olam, shalosh kitot shel mal\u8217'achei hasharet yotzot likrato} \u8212- three groups of ministering angels come out to greet the righteous person. One group says: {\i yavo veshalom} \u8212- come in peace. The second says: {\i holech nechucho} \u8212- go in your righteousness. The third says: {\i yavo shalom yanuchu al mishkevotam} \u8212- come in peace, and let all rest on their resting places.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i bish\u8217'a she\u8217'rasha ne\u8217'evad min ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- when the wicked person is destroyed from the world \u8212- three groups of destructive angels come out to meet him. The first declares: {\i ein shalom amar Hashem la-resha\u8217'im} \u8212- there is no peace, says the Lord, for the wicked. The second: {\i l\u8217'ma\u8217'atzevah yishkav} \u8212- in suffering he shall lie down. The third: {\i redei vehashkevah et areilim} \u8212- go down and lie with the uncircumcised, descending to the depths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Worldly Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to Rebbe\u8217's declaration that he never derived benefit from even a little finger\u8217's worth of this world: as noted, this stands in some tension with other sources depicting Rebbe\u8217's considerable material circumstances. The resolution that his public role as {\i nasi} required certain outward trappings of position, without his inwardly desiring them, is plausible. The alternative reading \u8212- that {\i lo neheneti} means he never used his Torah learning as a means of personal material gain, never took payment for teaching \u8212- offers a sharper and more textually integrated {\i pshat}, though {\i Tosafot} reads the phrase in its more straightforward sense of physical asceticism.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mourning Period\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holiness Died\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the statement {\i oso yom shemeis Rebbe batla kedusha} \u8212- on the day Rebbe died, holiness ceased. This was one of the statements the {\i gemara} earlier proposed reversing, attributing it to Rebbe about Rabbi Chiya rather than vice versa. A related {\i braisa} at the end of Sotah records: {\i mishemeis Rebbe batla anavah ve-yirah} \u8212- when Rebbe died, humility and fear ceased as well. The {\i gemara} notes that {\i kedusha} encompasses all of these qualities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priestly Purity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then cites a remarkable tradition. {\i Rav Rabbi Chaim Kohen haya omer} \u8212- and one notes that he was a {\i Kohen} \u8212- {\i ilmalei hayisi kesheniftar Rabbeinu Tam, hayisi mitamei lo} \u8212- had I been present when Rabbeinu Tam died, I would have made myself {\i tamei} for him. His reasoning: {\i de-batla kedusha de-ka\u8217'amar hacha hinu kedushas kehuna} \u8212- the cessation of holiness spoken of in the {\i gemara} refers to the holiness of {\i kehuna}; on that day it was as though there were no operative laws of {\i kehuna}, as when an immediate relative dies and even {\i Kohanim} would properly become {\i tamei}. The Talmudic phrase, he argues, applies only to {\i oso yom} \u8212- that specific day \u8212- and not beyond it, as indicated by the precise language {\i oso yom davka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raavad Instance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} further notes that the Yerushalmi in {\i Berachot}, in the chapter of {\i mi shemeiso}, states explicitly: {\i de-keshemeis Rebbe amru ein kehuna hayom} \u8212- when Rebbe died, they said there is no {\i kehuna} today. {\i Tosafot} adds a note of caution, suggesting the Yerushalmi may be speaking specifically of {\i tumas beis haperas}, which is {\i de-Rabbanan}, and perhaps not of full {\i Torah}-level {\i tum\u8217'ah}. Nevertheless, the historical precedent is noted: when the {\i Raavad} of Posqui\u232 ?res died, the {\i Kohanim} of that community in fact became {\i tamei} in order to participate in his burial \u8212- an event recorded in Twersky\u8217's study of the {\i Raavad}. This reflects the concept that upon the death of the {\i gadol ha-dor}, the laws of priestly purity are, for that singular day, treated as suspended for all of {\i Klal Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Duration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new {\i Mishnah} on 104a. {\i Kol zman she\u8217'hi be-veis aviha, gova kesuvasa le\u8217'olam} \u8212- as long as the widow resides in her father\u8217's house, she may collect her {\i ketubah} forever. Since it is a debt owed to her, the mere passage of time does not constitute a waiver. {\i Kol zman she\u8217'hi be-veis ba\u8217'ala} \u8212- but if she is living in her deceased husband\u8217's household and being supported by the estate, {\i gova kesuvasa ad esrim ve-chamesh shanim} \u8212- she may only collect her {\i ketubah} for 25 years, according to Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages, however, hold the inverse: {\i kol zman she\u8217'hi be-veis ba\u8217'ala, gova kesuvasa le\u8217'olam} \u8212- when she is in her husband\u8217's household, she may always collect it. {\i Kol zman she\u8217'hi be-veis aviha, gova kesuvasa ad esrim ve-chamesh shanim} \u8212- when she is in her father\u8217's house, she may only collect it within 25 years, after which her silence is construed as {\i mechila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 25-Year Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's position rests on an accounting theory: {\i she-yeish be-esrim ve-chamesh shanim she-ta\u8217'aseh tovah keneged kesuvasa} \u8212- over the course of 25 years, the small incremental benefits she has received from the estate \u8212- gifts here, use of funds there, various informal advantages \u8212- will have cumulatively added up to the total value of her {\i ketubah}, even if none of it was formally designated as payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Embarrassment Factor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages\u8217' inverse ruling \u8212- that a widow in her husband\u8217's house never loses her claim through silence \u8212- rests on a different logic entirely. When she is living among the heirs, being housed and fed by them, and she has not demanded payment, it is not because she has forgiven the debt. Rather, she is simply embarrassed to ask. She cannot easily turn to the heirs who are actively supporting her and demand: now pay me my {\i ketubah}. Her silence is therefore not {\i mechila} but {\i kissufa} \u8212- embarrassment. When she is in her father\u8217's house and entirely separate from the heirs, however, 25 years of silence is interpreted as genuine waiver.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs Continue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meisa} \u8212- if the widow dies within the 25-year period, {\i yoresheha mazkirim kesuvasa ad esrim ve-chamesh shanim} \u8212- her heirs continue the count. If she died after 10 years, her heirs have the remaining 15 years to pursue the claim. After those 15 years, the silence of her heirs is similarly treated as {\i mechila}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poor Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines Rabbi Meir\u8217's theory. {\i Amar lei Abaye le-Rav Yosef}: consider {\i aniya she-beYisrael} \u8212- the poorest woman in Israel, whose {\i ketubah} is the bare minimum. Rabbi Meir says that over 25 years she will have received little benefits adding up to the {\i ketubah} value. That is at least conceivable with a small amount. But what of the 25-year rule on its own terms for such a woman?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wealthy Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And on the other end: {\i Marta bat Baisos} \u8212- the enormously wealthy woman known from elsewhere in the tractate \u8212- whose {\i ketubah} must have been worth an enormous sum. How can 25 years of small informal benefits possibly add up to the total of her {\i ketubah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Camel Burden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei: lifum gamla shichna} \u8212- the burden is proportional to the camel. The benefits a wealthy woman receives living in her late husband\u8217's household are not a few pennies here and there. She might buy herself expensive jewelry, attend costly entertainments, make use of resources on a scale commensurate with her station. The informal benefits scale with the wealth of the estate, and therefore they add up to the {\i ketubah} amount within 25 years even for a woman of great wealth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pro-Rata Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Iba\u8217'u lehu} \u8212- a question is then raised: {\i le-Rabbi Meir, mahu she-titshales?} \u8212- does Rabbi Meir\u8217's theory allow for pro-rating the {\i ketubah}? If the {\i ketubah} is paid off gradually through small benefits accrued over time, ought one not assume that after, say, 12 and a half years, half the {\i ketubah} has already been effectively paid off \u8212- such that a claim brought at that point would only be for the remaining half? The question is left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sages\u8217' Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waiver Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages\u8217' position, by contrast, has nothing to do with gradual payment. Their theory is not that small benefits are credited against the {\i ketubah}, but rather that 25 years of complete silence constitutes {\i mechila} \u8212- a waiver. If she has not demanded her {\i ketubah} in 25 years, she has implicitly relinquished it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sunset Precision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Abaye le-Rav Yosef}: if the widow comes to collect her {\i ketubah} on the last day of the 25-year period \u8212- {\i atai kodem shekias hachama}, she comes before sunset \u8212- she collects her full {\i ketubah}. {\i Ve-achar shekias hachama}, one minute after sunset \u8212- {\i lo gavya}, she receives nothing? Does that one brief moment of additional silence transform her waiver?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defining Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei: in. Kol middos chachamim kein hu} \u8212- yes, this is the nature of the Sages\u8217' defined measures. One must draw a line somewhere. {\i B\u8217'arba\u8217'im se\u8217'ah tovel} \u8212- with 40 {\i se\u8217'ah} of water one may immerse in the {\i mikvah}. {\i B\u8217'arba\u8217'im se\u8217'ah chasar kurtov, eino yachol litbol bahen} \u8212- if even a minuscule amount is missing, the immersion is invalid. Defined thresholds require defined boundaries; that is the nature of rabbinic legislation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Document Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Possession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav}: Rabbi Yishmael bar Rabbi Yosi testified before Rebbe, citing his father\u8217's ruling \u8212- {\i lo shanu ela she-ein shtar ketubah yotzei mitachas yadah} \u8212- when is {\i mechila} assumed after 25 years? Only when she is not holding onto the {\i ketubah} document. If she has no written document, one might explain her silence as passive neglect of a verbal obligation. But if she holds the {\i shtar}, she has no need to be pressing for collection \u8212- the document is there whenever she wishes to use it. One cannot construe her silence as {\i mechila} in such circumstances. {\i Aval shtar ketubah yotzei mitachat yadah, gova kesubatah l\u8217'olam} \u8212- if she is holding the {\i shtar}, she may always collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Irrelevant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar disagrees: {\i afilu shtar ketubah yotzei mitachas yadah, eino govei ela ad esrim ve-chamesh shanim} \u8212- even if she holds the {\i ketubah} document, she still only has 25 years. The document does not insulate her from the waiver rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Masiv Rav Sheshes}: the {\i gemara} objects from a principle concerning ordinary creditors. {\i Ba\u8217'al chov gova she-lo be-hazkarah} \u8212- a creditor can collect at any time without ever needing to announce his intent to collect. We never assume a regular creditor has been {\i mochel} his debt through silence. If so, why would we treat a widow differently? {\i Heichi dami?} \u8212- what is the case of the {\i ba\u8217'al chov}? If he holds no {\i shtar}, how does he collect? It must be that he holds a {\i shtar}. And we say the {\i ba\u8217'al chov} with a {\i shtar} never waives \u8212- yet for the widow, even holding a {\i shtar}, we say she waives after 25 years? That seems inconsistent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admission Clause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hu masiv lah ve-hu m\u8217'farek lah} \u8212- he who raised the difficulty resolves it. The comparison breaks down because the cases are not identical. The widow case can be constructed without a {\i shtar}, because a widow does not need a document to prove her debt \u8212- {\i tenai beis din}, the condition is established by the marriage itself; she need only prove the death of her husband. A regular creditor without a {\i shtar} generally cannot prove his claim. The comparison must therefore be on equal terms: a {\i ba\u8217'al chov} in a case where the debtor admits the debt \u8212- {\i d\u8217'chayiv modeh} \u8212- would similarly not require a {\i shtar}, and in such a case we may well be comparing two parallel scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-amar Rabbi Ila shonin gerusha harei hi ke-ba\u8217'al chov} \u8212- Rabbi Ila teaches that a divorced woman is treated like a {\i ba\u8217'al chov}, because a {\i gerusha} would never be {\i mocheles} her {\i ketubah}; the relationship was by definition adversarial, and forgiveness of the debt on her part would be most unlikely. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i heichi dami?} \u8212- if she holds no {\i ketubah}, how is she collecting? She must be holding one. And we say about the {\i gerusha} holding her {\i ketubah} that she is never {\i mocheles}. Does it follow that a widow holding her {\i shtar} is, by contrast, {\i mocheles}? The {\i gemara} again insists: {\i hacha nami keshechayav modeh} \u8212- we are speaking of a case where the other party admits the debt; one can construct a case without a {\i shtar} for a divorced woman just as for any creditor. The conclusion toward which the {\i gemara} is moving is that {\i mechila} by an {\i almanah} applies only in the absence of a {\i shtar}, and that possessing the document insulates her from any waiver claim.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Later Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reset on Demand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak tani Rav Yehuda bar Kaza be-matnisa d\u8217'bei bar Kaza} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda bar Kaza teaches in the {\i baraisos} of {\i Bei bar Kaza}: {\i tav\u8217'ah kesuvasa, harei hi ke-vatchila} \u8212- if she has formally demanded her {\i ketubah}, the clock begins again. Once she comes before a court and registers her intent to collect, she has another 25 years before any further silence would be construed as {\i mechila}. She need not actually collect the {\i ketubah} within those 25 years \u8212- she only needs to have made her intention known, and then the 25-year period recommences from that demand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Protects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-im hayta ketubah yotzeis mitachat yadah, gova kesuvasa le\u8217'olam} \u8212- and if she is holding the {\i shtar ketubah}, she may collect forever, without any 25-year limitation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Document Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question sent from Rav Nachman bar Rav Chisda to Rav Nachman bar Yaakov clarifies the structure of the debates: {\i keshtar ketubah yotzei mitachat yadah machloket, o ke-she-ein shtar ketubah yotzei mitachat yadah?} \u8212- is the debate between Rabbi Yishmael bar Rabbi Yosi and Rabbi Elazar taking place when she is holding the {\i shtar}, or when she is not? {\i Shelach lei}: the response was {\i be-she-ein shtar ketubah yotzei mitachat yadah machloket} \u8212- the debate concerns the case where she is not holding a {\i shtar}. When she holds the {\i shtar}, everyone agrees she may collect forever. {\i Ve-halacha ke-divrei chachamim} \u8212- and the {\i halacha} follows the Sages, meaning that when she is in her father\u8217's house without a {\i shtar} and has been silent for 25 years, {\i mechila} is assumed, but when she is in her husband\u8217's household, her silence is never interpreted as {\i mechila} \u8212- the {\i kissufa} factor prevails and she retains her right to collect regardless.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Base vs.\u160 ?Addition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Base Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki ata Rav Dimi}: when Rav Dimi came from {\i Eretz Yisrael}, he transmitted a ruling in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi citing Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, in the name of Bar Kappara: {\i lo shanu ela maneh matayim} \u8212- the entire framework of {\i mechila} after 25 years applies only to the base {\i ketubah} amounts of 100 and 200 {\i dinars}. {\i Aval tosefet yesh lah} \u8212- but the {\i tosefet}, the additional amounts voluntarily added by the husband, she retains in any event. The reasoning is that the {\i tosefet} is in the nature of a gift, and one does not assume {\i mechila} in the context of a gift the way one might in the context of a standard marital obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafet Waiver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu, however, citing Rabbi Yochanan, rules otherwise: {\i afilu tosefet ein lah} \u8212- even the {\i tosefet} is forfeited after 25 years. The support for this position comes from Rabbi Aibu citing Rabbi Yannai: {\i tnai ketubah ke-ketubah dami} \u8212- the conditions and additions of the {\i ketubah} are treated as equivalent to the base {\i ketubah} itself; the 25-year waiver rule applies to the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbahu View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute between these positions thus turns on whether the {\i tosefet} retains the character of a gift \u8212- and is therefore never subject to {\i mechila} \u8212- or whether, once incorporated into the {\i ketubah} structure, it is treated identically to the base amount for all purposes including the 25-year waiver rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafet Disputes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Itmar nami}: Rabbi Abba citing Rav Huna citing Rav rules that only the base 100 and 200 are subject to the waiver \u8212- the {\i tosefet} she retains. Rabbi Abba then challenged Rav Huna: {\i amar Rav hachi?} \u8212- did Rav really say this? Rav Huna replied: {\i ashtukin kaamart o ashkayin kaamart?} \u8212- when you challenge whether Rav said this, are you trying to silence me ({\i ashtukin}), meaning you disagree, or would you like to raise a drink to this ruling ({\i ashkayin}), meaning you approve and want to be sure of its attribution? {\i Amar lei: ashtukin kamina} \u8212- I want to silence you, Rav Huna replied; he did not accept the ruling as stated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Case Study\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a concrete case illustrating these principles. The mother-in-law of Rabbi Chiya Aricha \u8212- a different Rabbi Chiya \u8212- was the widow of his brother, who had died leaving Rabbi Chiya Aricha as the surviving heir and thus responsible for the estate. She had been living in her father\u8217's house \u8212- {\i be-vei nasha} \u8212- rather than in her deceased husband\u8217's household. {\i Ve-zana esrim ve-chamisha shanim} \u8212- Rabbi Chiya Aricha supported her for 25 years. He brought her food on his own shoulder, by his own account, taking great personal care of her provision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heir Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After 25 years she said to him: {\i hav li mezonei} \u8212- continue to pay my food bill. He replied: {\i leis lach mezonei} \u8212- you have no more right to {\i mezonot}, the 25-year period has elapsed. She then said: {\i hav li ktuvah} \u8212- give me my {\i ketubah}. He replied: {\i lo mezonei is lach ve-lo ktuvah is lach} \u8212- you have neither {\i mezonot} nor {\i ketubah}; you have waived everything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judge Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Taviesay le-dina kamai d\u8217'Rabba bar Shila} \u8212- she brought him to judgment before Rabba bar Shila. The judge asked him to describe exactly what had occurred. Rabbi Chiya Aricha recounted that he had supported her for 25 years while she lived in her father\u8217's house, personally delivering the food with great care and dedication. Rabba bar Shila said: and this is precisely the point. {\i D\u8217'amrinan mishum kissufa hu d\u8217'lo tavah} \u8212- the reason she did not demand payment in 25 years is that she was {\i embarrassed} to ask, because you took such excellent care of her. The Sages\u8217' rule that living in the husband\u8217's household suspends {\i mechila} due to {\i kissufa} applies here as well: when a creditor is so attentive and generous that the widow feels she cannot press a demand, her silence is not waiver but {\i kissufa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment Ordered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Zil havlah} \u8212- go pay her. The judge ruled that the silence was not {\i mechila} and ordered continued payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Escalation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-la ashgach} \u8212- he did not comply. {\i Katav lah adrachta anichsei} \u8212- the court then issued a written seizure order against his property, empowering her to collect from it. The case then came before Rava.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seizure Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Produce Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the case came before Rava, Rabbi Chiya Aricha complained: {\i chazi mar heichi danyin} \u8212- do you see how they judged me? They issued a seizure of my property. Rava replied: {\i shappir duncha} \u8212- that was a sound judgment; you must continue to pay her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The widow then pressed further. Since she now held the seizure document, she claimed: {\i neizil ve-neihadar lei peira mi-hahu yoma ad idna} \u8212- let him return to me the produce of that property from the time the seizure document was written until now. She had been entitled to that property, she argued, and he had withheld it; she should be compensated for the produce generated from it during that period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Defect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lah: achvi li adrachtich} \u8212- Rava said, show me the document. {\i Ve-lo hava katav bah: ve-ishtemodana de-nichsei de-misna inun} \u8212- and he found that the document did not contain the specification: and it is known to us that these properties belong to the deceased. A proper seizure order in this context must identify the specific property the widow has a right to seize \u8212- namely, the property that had belonged to her dead husband. The document lacked this specification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wrong Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Adrachta la shappir ktiva} \u8212- the seizure document was not properly written, Rava ruled, because without proper identification, the widow might go and seize the wrong property entirely. If she were to seize the heir\u8217's own property rather than her deceased husband\u8217's estate, she might improve it, and meanwhile her deceased husband\u8217's property \u8212- the one she actually has rights to \u8212- might depreciate. Later, the heir would come to court and say: take your property and give me back mine. This creates potential for significant confusion and loss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Property Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's ruling was therefore precise: she is entitled to seize and collect from her deceased husband\u8217's property for her support, not from the heir\u8217's own property. The defect in the seizure document, which failed to identify which property was subject to seizure, rendered the document invalid and prevented her from claiming produce from the time of its writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment Remains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The widow then attempted a fallback position: even if not from the time of the document, she argued she should receive produce from the time the auction process had concluded and property had been assigned, prior to actual written seizure. {\i Amar lah: hani milei heicha de-lo ktiv tauta be-adrachta} \u8212- Rava ruled that this argument would hold only where there was no error in the seizure document itself. {\i Aval heicha de-ktiv tauta be-adrachta, leis lan bah} \u8212- where there is an error in the document, the entire process is voided, and actual possession is required before any rights in the produce can be claimed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The heir argued that Rava himself held that {\i achrayut ta\u8217'us sofer hu} \u8212- an omission in a document is treated as a mere scribal error and does not invalidate the fundamental transaction. Rava responded: {\i beha lo matzi lemeimar ta\u8217'us sofer hu} \u8212- in this case, that principle cannot apply, {\i deha chazina de-veshivna ta\u8217'ei} \u8212- because this was a genuine error in judgment, not a scribal oversight. {\i Me\u8217'ikara hu savar ha ve-ha didei hu} \u8212- the court officer who drafted the document actually believed that all the property belonged to the heir, making it immaterial from which specific portion the widow collected. {\i Mai mehai u-mai mehai?} That was an error: she is not entitled to any of the heir\u8217's own property. {\i Zimnin de-azil u-meshabbach lehu ve-havu lehu nichsei} \u8212- if she were to take the heir\u8217's property, she might improve it, while her deceased husband\u8217's property simultaneously depreciates, and then the heir would come and demand: take yours and return mine \u8212- a result that would cast disrepute on the {\i beis din}. She is therefore limited to the property of her deceased husband for purposes of support collection, and the technical defects in the seizure document preclude any claim to produce from an earlier point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notwithstanding all these technical complexities, however, Rava\u8217's fundamental ruling that the heir must continue paying her support stands \u8212- the defects in the seizure document speak to which property may be seized, not to whether the underlying obligation to pay exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chazak u-varuch.}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 105\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Admon & Chanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin today the final {\i perek} of {\i Ketubot}, opening at the very bottom of 104b. The {\i daf} itself is 105.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Judges Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i shne dayane gzerot hayu biYerushalayim} \u8212- two judges who made decrees were in Jerusalem: {\i Admon} and {\i Chanan ben Avishalom}. As Rashi explains, {\i Chanan} said two things \u8212- meaning two rulings for which he was known, rulings with which the Sages did not agree \u8212- and {\i Admon} said seven. The subsequent discussion in the {\i perek} will bring in all of the distinctive rulings of each, some tying directly into the themes of {\i Ketubot}, others less so. The hook is here in the opening of the {\i mishnah}, and the package of rulings follows from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Support Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i mi shehalach limdinat hayam veishto tova mezonot} \u8212- someone who went overseas and his wife demands her {\i mezonot}, her support. Obviously she must be able to put food on the table while her husband is away. The concern, however, is that he is not present to defend himself \u8212- perhaps he already prepaid her support, or there is some other outstanding question about what she has already collected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanan\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chanan} rules: {\i tishava basof velo tishava batchila} \u8212- she takes an oath at the end, not at the beginning. Rashi explains that \u8220"the end\u8221" means when the husband eventually dies and she comes to collect her full {\i ketubah}; at that point she takes the normal oath a woman takes when collecting a {\i ketubah}, and that oath will simultaneously cover any questions about what she collected during the period she was receiving her {\i mezonot}. If he does not die but eventually returns, the matter will presumably be dealt with at that point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priests\u8217' Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nechlaku alav bne kohanim gdolim} \u8212- the sons of the {\i Kohanim Gdolim} disputed this ruling. It is a notable fact that this group had a collective voice in {\i halacha}; during the period of {\i Bayit Sheni} there was indeed considerable turnover among the {\i Kohanim Gdolim} and presumably a large cohort of their sons. They maintained: {\i tishava batchila uvasof} \u8212- she must take an oath at the beginning when she comes to collect {\i mezonot}, and again at the end when collecting the {\i ketubah}. Rabbi Dosa ben Horkinas ruled in accordance with their position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Agrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Veamar Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai yafe amar Chanan} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai sides with {\i Chanan}: the oath should only be at the end. One might ask whether Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai is simply echoing {\i Chanan} without adding anything new. In general, when the {\i Gemara} records an opinion that appears merely to echo an earlier one, it seeks to understand the {\i chidush}. Here, however, it is clear that Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai is simply picking sides in an existing dispute, and the weight of his authority as a backing for {\i Chanan}\u8217's position is itself significant, even without a novel ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that the {\i Gemara} today will not actually discuss this debate about {\i mezonot} and oaths at all. The discussion will focus entirely on the opening phrase \u8212- {\i shne dayane gzerot} \u8212- and use it as a platform for an extended treatment of the role, compensation, and integrity of judges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the {\i Gemara}, there is an interesting {\i Tosafot} on 104b worth noting. {\i Tosafot} states: {\i hachi gares Rabbeinu Tam} \u8212- Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's reading of the name is {\i Avishalom} rather than {\i Avshalom}. The reasoning: Rabbi Meir states in {\i Perek Chelek} that {\i Avshalom} \u8212- the son of David \u8212- has no portion in the {\i Olam Haba}. How then could anyone name their child after him? Therefore Rabbeinu Tam insists the name is the distinct form {\i Avishalom}, and pursues the discussion at some length. This, however, raises an obvious question that {\i Tosafot} elsewhere addresses: how do we have a {\i Tanna} named Rabbi Yishmael, if {\i Yishmael} in rabbinic literature is associated with wickedness? One could equally ask: who says the name was chosen after that particular person? Names recur without necessarily carrying reference to any specific historical bearer \u8212- just as modern Hebrew speakers use the name {\i Nimrod} without invoking the biblical figure. The observation stands, but the concern may be overstated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two vs Three Judges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita on Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a contradiction from a {\i braita}: rather than {\i dayanei gezeirot} \u8212- judges who made decrees \u8212- the {\i braita} describes {\i dayanei gezeilot} \u8212- judges who ruled on matters of theft \u8212- and lists not two but three: {\i Admon ben Gadai}, {\i Chanan HaMitzri}, and {\i Chanan ben Avishalom}. So there are two difficulties: this source has three where the {\i mishnah} has two, and this source says {\i gezeilot} where the {\i mishnah} says {\i gezeirot}. As the {\i Gemara} puts it: {\i kashya tlata atrei, kashya gezeirot agezeilot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Most Important Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first difficulty \u8212- three versus two \u8212- is resolved readily. {\i Dechashiv lei katani, delo chashiv lei lo katani}: the {\i mishnah} lists the most significant figures and omits the less prominent ones. The {\i mishnah} names {\i Admon} and {\i Chanan ben Avishalom} as the two most important, and leaves out {\i Chanan HaMitzri} as the third.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirot & Gezeilot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second difficulty \u8212- {\i gezeirot} versus {\i gezeilot} \u8212- is resolved by Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak: {\i shehayu gozrim gezeirot al gezeilot} \u8212- they would issue edicts in connection with matters of theft. Hence, depending on one\u8217's angle of description, they could be referred to either as {\i dayanei gezeirot} or as {\i dayanei gezeilot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yosei Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A supporting {\i braita} is then cited: {\i kitema netiah} \u8212- if an animal enters a neighbor\u8217's property and nibbles at young plantings. Rabbi Yosei invokes {\i gozrei gezeirot she\u8217'biYerushalayim} \u8212- those who issue edicts in Jerusalem \u8212- and states that they would assign fixed values: a one-year-old planting is assessed at two silver coins, a two-year-old planting at four. This {\i braita} explicitly uses the phrase {\i gozrei gezeirot} in the context of what we might classify as {\i nezek} \u8212- tortious damage \u8212- rather than outright theft, which itself helps bridge the gap between {\i gezeirot} and {\i gezeilot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nature of Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage opens an important conceptual point. We ordinarily think of a {\i gezeirah} in the rabbinic sense as a protective fence around the law \u8212- do not read by candlelight lest you tilt the flame, and similar safeguards. But here the term {\i gezeirah} is applied to something quite different: the act of judges establishing fixed parameters and guidelines for monetary liability. Why would these figures be called {\i dayanei gezeirot} rather than simply {\i dayanim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer seems to be that while an ordinary judge applies existing law to a given case, these judges had a specialized role: they were not merely adjudicating but actually setting down certain operative rules \u8212- fixing valuations for categories of damage, establishing procedures and frameworks \u8212- functioning in what we might call a legislative as well as a judicial capacity. In that sense, calling them {\i gozrei gezeirot} makes good sense: they were issuing binding rulings that set parameters for how the law would be applied. This also fits with the observation that later in the {\i perek} we will see a series of rulings attributed to {\i Admon}, some relating to {\i ketubot} and some not. The {\i mishnah} has collected this package around the hook of their identity as {\i gozrei gezeirot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachum HaMadai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} is then cited listing three {\i dayanei gezeirot} in Jerusalem: {\i Admon}, {\i Chanan}, and {\i Nachum} \u8212- with Nachum here being {\i Nachum HaMadai}, a figure familiar from the {\i Bameh Madlikin} chapter recited each {\i Shabbos} eve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachum Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa identifies the source of this tradition: it is Rabbi Natan, as taught in a {\i braita} \u8212- {\i af Nachum HaMadai mi-gozrei gezerot she-biYerushalayim haya} \u8212- even Nachum HaMadai was among those who issued edicts in Jerusalem, {\i velo hodu lo Chachamim} \u8212- but the Sages did not agree with him. The debate appears to be not about whether he adjudicated cases at all \u8212- evidently he did \u8212- but whether he held the specific official status of {\i gozrei gezeirot}. That question the Sages resolved against him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Jerusalem Institutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 394 Numbers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presses further: are we really saying there were only two or three such figures? Rav Pinchas cites Rav Oshaya: {\i shelosh me\u8217'ot tishim ve-arba\u8217'ah batei dinim she-hayu biYerushalayim} \u8212- there were 394 courts in Jerusalem. And correspondingly, 394 synagogues and 394 study halls \u8212- a remarkable claim. The number 300 is often a stylized large number in rabbinic literature, but the additional 94 makes this figure feel unexpectedly precise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Clarifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi notes that the {\i batei dinim} in question are {\i batei dinim shel esrim u-shelosha} \u8212- courts of 23, meaning major {\i batei dinim}, not the minor courts of three. Three hundred and ninety-four courts of 23 in Jerusalem is a striking number. Rashi also glosses \u8212- perhaps unnecessarily \u8212- that {\i batei knesiyot} are for prayer and {\i batei midrashot} for {\i mishnah} and {\i talmud}, and also that {\i batei sofrim} refers to elementary schools for children. That last clarification is useful; one might have read {\i batei sofrim} as houses of scribal work, but Rashi indicates these are what we would call {\i cheder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific to Edicts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent tension: {\i ve-chi ka-amrinan a-gozrei gezerot ka-amrinan} \u8212- when we were speaking of two or three, we were speaking specifically about those who held the distinctive role of {\i gozrei gezeirot}, not about the full complement of judges in the city. The 394 courts are a different category entirely; the two or three figures were specialists in a particular legislative-judicial function.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Judge Compensation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 99 Talents Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda citing Rav Assi states: {\i gozrei gezerot she-biYerushalayim hayu notlin sacharam tishim ve-tisha maneh mi-trumat ha-lishka} \u8212- these figures who issued edicts in Jerusalem would receive their compensation of 99 {\i maneh} of silver from the {\i trumat ha-lishka}, the funds collected from the {\i machatzit ha-shekel} beyond what was needed for the {\i korbanot}. The surplus from those communal funds was directed toward ongoing Temple and communal needs, and the salary of these {\i gozrei gezeirot} was one such use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately raises a question: how could judges receive payment at all? The prohibition on {\i sachar la-dun} \u8212- taking a fee for rendering judgment \u8212- is well established, and the problem of {\i shochad} looms over any arrangement in which a judge receives money in connection with his judicial function. We will address this in detail shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sustenance Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo ratzu} \u8212- if they did not want to accept that sum, {\i mosifin lahem} \u8212- the amount would be increased. The {\i Gemara} initially asks with some incredulity: {\i atu berishaye askinan} \u8212- are we dealing with wicked people who refuse their salary and need to be persuaded? The answer is: {\i ella lo safku} \u8212- it simply was not enough to cover their needs. The principle here is that the community must ensure these judges are provided for; if the fixed sum was insufficient, it would be increased. And even if the judges themselves were inclined to refuse the additional compensation, {\i mosifim aleihem} \u8212- you still increase it, because the obligation is to ensure their sustenance, not merely to offer what they are willing to accept.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Karna\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karna Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Karna \u8212- a first-generation {\i Amora} \u8212- would accept a coin from each side when litigants came before him: {\i hava shakil istira mizakai veistira mechayav} \u8212- he would take an {\i istira} from the one who was ultimately exonerated and an {\i istira} from the one who was ultimately liable. He would do this before rendering judgment, and then {\i vedayan lehu dina} \u8212- he would adjudicate the case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bribe Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i veheichi avid hachi} \u8212- how could he do this? {\i Vehachtiv shochad lo tikach} \u8212- the Torah says, do not take a bribe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Side Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} entertains a possible distinction: perhaps the prohibition is specifically {\i heicha deshakeil mechada gisa} \u8212- where one takes only from one side, because then {\i dilma ati leatzloyei dina} \u8212- one may come to tilt the judgment in favor of the side one took money from. But Karna, since he took equally from both sides, would not be drawn to tilt toward one over the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i vechi lo ati leatzloyei dina mi sharei} \u8212- just because it will not lead to tilting the judgment, does that make it permitted? A {\i braita} is cited: {\i shochad lo tikach} \u8212- do not take a bribe. What does the Torah need to tell us? If the concern were only about perverting judgment, it would have been sufficient to say {\i lo tateh mishpat} \u8212- do not pervert justice. By adding the prohibition on bribery, the Torah is telling us something beyond the prohibition on corrupt verdicts. {\i Ella afilu lezakot et hazakai ulechayev et hachayav amrah Torah shochad lo tikach} \u8212- even if your intent is to acquit the innocent and convict the guilty, even if you plan to judge correctly and fairly, the Torah says you may not accept a bribe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} is identifying a scenario in which someone pays a judge and says: I only ask you to judge me honestly; if I am innocent, find me innocent; I am not asking you to rule in my favor if I am guilty. Even that arrangement is prohibited, because money paid in connection with the final judgment \u8212- even a just one \u8212- falls within the category of {\i shochad}. Given this, taking equal fees from both sides would seem no better: the {\i Gemara} finds it difficult to see why that should be categorically different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge Equal Fees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara\u8217's} challenge stands: {\i vechi lo ati leatzloyei dina mi sharei} \u8212- even granting that equal fees from both sides remove the directional bias of a traditional bribe, how does that remove the underlying prohibition? The answer will turn on a different distinction entirely, developed in the following section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sachar Batala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fee Voids Judgment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states plainly: {\i hanoteil sachar ladun dinav betelim} \u8212- one who takes a fee for rendering judgment, his judgments are void. This goes beyond a simple prohibition; it is grounds for retroactive invalidation of the ruling itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sachar Batala Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the question about Karna: {\i hani milei agar dina} \u8212- the voiding applies when you are paid for the act of judgment itself. {\i Hai tanna betorat agra hava shakeil} \u8212- but Karna was taking the money as a service fee, as compensation for his time, not as payment for the content of his ruling. This is the principle of {\i sachar batala} \u8212- compensation for lost opportunity, for the work one is not doing because one is sitting in judgment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle has broad implications beyond the courtroom. The Torah states, in the phrase the {\i Gemara} invokes, {\i mah ani bechinam af atem bechinam} \u8212- just as I taught without charging a fee, so too you must teach the people without charge. This extends to teachers of Torah, doctors, and other roles where one might otherwise argue that payment is for a {\i mitzvah}. The conceptual problem is real: if rabbis and judges cannot be paid for their services, how do they support themselves?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is {\i sachar batala}: we are not paying you for doing a {\i mitzvah}, we are paying you for the job you are not doing while you do the {\i mitzvah}. This is technically opportunity cost \u8212- compensation for the income foregone by spending time in a judicial or teaching role rather than in one\u8217's usual occupation. One might note, somewhat wryly, that taken strictly this would mean better-compensated judges are not the more skilled ones but the ones with higher market value in other fields; the person who left a lucrative law practice to become a rabbi would theoretically command a higher {\i sachar batala} than someone with no other marketable skill who happens to be an outstanding scholar. These complications are real, and the {\i Gemara} acknowledges that {\i sachar batala} as a practical mechanism involves a degree of legal fiction. Nevertheless, that is what permits it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disgusting Fees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} complicates the picture: {\i mechoar hadayan shenotel schar ladun, ella shedino din} \u8212- it is disgusting for a judge to take fees for his judgment, yet his judgment nevertheless stands. The {\i Gemara} asks: what case is this describing? If it is {\i agar dina} \u8212- a fee for rendering judgment \u8212- how can the {\i braita} say the ruling stands, when the {\i mishnah} says {\i dinav betelim}? It must therefore be describing {\i agar batala} \u8212- compensation for lost work. Yet the {\i braita} calls it disgusting. So even {\i sachar batala} is considered {\i mechoar}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obvious Loss Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i hani milei batala dela muchach} \u8212- that is when the lost opportunity is not obvious. If it is not apparent to onlookers that the judge is genuinely forgoing other income by sitting in court, the arrangement looks like being paid for the judgment itself, even if technically it is {\i sachar batala}. That situation is {\i mechoar} \u8212- disgraceful in appearance, even if technically permitted. {\i Karna batala demuchach havei shakil} \u8212- but Karna\u8217's situation was one of obvious, demonstrable lost opportunity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Tasting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How was it obvious in Karna\u8217's case? {\i Dehavei tohei be\u8217'ambara dechamra} \u8212- he worked as a wine assessor, smelling in warehouse-style wine storage to determine which casks were approaching spoilage, and advising the owners accordingly. {\i Veyahavi lei zuza} \u8212- they would pay him for this service. When Karna left that work to sit in judgment, anyone could see that he was forgoing readily available income. The arrangement of collecting a fee from each litigant was therefore transparently a reimbursement for lost earnings, not a payment for the judgment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel story: Rav Huna, when a case came before him, would say to the litigants: {\i havu li gavra dedali li becharitai} \u8212- provide me a man to draw water in my place. He drew water for a living, and would not sit in judgment without someone covering his ordinary daily labor. {\i Ve\u8217'idun lechu dina} \u8212- and then I will judge your case. Again, the lost opportunity was concrete, visible, and undeniable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Compensation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Tam View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of how the {\i gozrei gezeirot} of Jerusalem were paid from {\i trumat ha-lishka} \u8212- public Temple funds \u8212- {\i Tosafot} offers two distinct approaches. Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's view: {\i mishum de-lo assur ela mi-ba\u8217'alei dinin} \u8212- the entire problem is when money comes from the litigants themselves, because that is where the risk of {\i shochad} arises. When payment comes from communal funds, it is outside that ambit entirely. The concerns about {\i sachar ladun} and {\i shochad} apply specifically to the money-for-judgment relationship between judge and litigant; public funding from {\i trumat ha-lishka} simply does not implicate those concerns, and no further legal fiction is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Community Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second approach \u8212- attributed in this {\i Tosafot} to {\i Rebbi}, which, it should be noted, is the {\i Tosafot} of the school of Rabbeinu Shimshon, as stated at the opening of the tenth {\i perek} at 90a \u8212- offers a different framework. It is not that payment from public funds is simply permissible because it avoids the litigant relationship. Rather: these judges sat in court at all hours and engaged in no other occupation whatsoever; they had no means of support. The community had effectively required them to have no other source of income. Having placed them in that position, the community now bore a responsibility to sustain them \u8212- not as payment for services rendered, but as an obligation of {\i parnasah} toward people who are impoverished as a direct consequence of communal need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking distinction. In the {\i sachar batala} model, one is compensated for a real alternative that one is forgoing. For a full-time judge with no alternative occupation, there is no such alternative \u8212- but the community has created the very condition of his poverty. The payment is therefore not for the {\i mitzvah} of judging; it is in fulfillment of a communal duty to support someone the community has rendered unable to support himself. The two models thus represent quite different understandings of the jurisprudential problem: one sidesteps it by removing the litigant relationship; the other works around it by recharacterizing the payment as communal welfare rather than compensation for services.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bribes Blind\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eye Disease Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abahu opens a striking reflection on the nature of {\i shochad}. He asks: come and see how blind are the eyes of those who accept bribes. A person who has a problem with his eye gives money to a doctor, and it is uncertain whether the doctor will heal it or not \u8212- he pays without any guarantee of benefit. Yet those who accept bribes take even a trivial sum and it blinds them. The asymmetry is the point: medical treatment cannot reliably heal a diseased eye even with payment, but bribery blinds infallibly even for a trifling amount.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bribes Blind (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exodus Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the verse from Exodus 23:8: {\i ki hashochad ya\u8217'aver pikchim} \u8212- for bribery blinds the open-eyed. The phrase {\i pikchim} \u8212- the open-eyed, the perceptive \u8212- is precisely the target.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wise & Foolish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} extends this: {\i ki hashochad ya\u8217'aver einei chachamim} \u8212- bribery blinds the eyes of the wise; {\i kal v\u8217'chomer l\u8217'tipshim} \u8212- how much more so the foolish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous & Wicked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And further: {\i v\u8217'isaleif divrei tzadikim} \u8212- it perverts the words of the righteous; {\i kal v\u8217'chomer l\u8217'rasha\u8217'im} \u8212- how much more so the wicked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quality Judges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises an obvious difficulty: {\i atu tipshim u\u8217'rasha\u8217'im bnei dina ninhu} \u8212- would we appoint foolish and wicked people as judges in the first place? The very framing of the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} seems to assume we are speaking about sitting judges. Why would the {\i braita} be warning about fools and wicked men on the bench?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous Corrupted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution reframes the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} entirely. The statement {\i ki hashochad ya\u8217'aver einei chachamim} means: even someone who is a great sage at the time he accepts a bribe, {\i eino niftar min ha\u8217'olam b\u8217'lo smiyut halev} \u8212- he does not leave the world without his heart becoming blinded. The first time he accepts {\i shochad} he is still a {\i chacham}; by the second time, the {\i shochad} has already made him into a {\i tipesh} \u8212- the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} is thus a temporal progression, not a contrast between two different categories of judges. Similarly: {\i afilu tzadik gamur v\u8217'loke\u8217'ach shochad} \u8212- even a complete {\i tzadik} who accepts {\i shochad eino niftar min ha\u8217'olam b\u8217'lo tiruf hada\u8217'at} \u8212- does not leave the world without his mind being perverted, effectively becoming a {\i rasha}. The {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} again describes what the bribe does to him over time.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 King & Judge Self-Suffic\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Dimi cites a teaching of Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak on the verse: {\i melech b\u8217'mishpat ya\u8217'amid aretz} \u8212- a king through justice establishes the land, {\i v\u8217'ish trumot yeherseina} \u8212- and a man of {\i trumot} will destroy it. The word {\i trumot} is read not as donations but as related to neediness \u8212- one who is always extending his hand. The interpretation: {\i im domeh dayan l\u8217'melech she\u8217'eino tzarich l\u8217'klum} \u8212- if the judge is like a king who needs nothing from anyone, who is fully self-sufficient, {\i ya\u8217'amid aretz} \u8212- he will establish the land. {\i Aval im domeh l\u8217'kohen she\u8217'mechazer al hagranot} \u8212- but if he is like a {\i kohen} who goes from one threshing floor to the next seeking his {\i terumah}, who is needy, whose hand is perpetually out, {\i yeherseina} \u8212- that judge will destroy it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Needy Judge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The image of the {\i kohen} going from threshing floor to threshing floor is vivid: not corrupt in the obvious sense, perhaps, but dependent \u8212- always needing things from people, never approaching a case from a position of independence. That dependence, even without explicit bribery, creates the conditions for compromised judgment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Borrowing Judge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Rav Shila adds a practical ruling: {\i hai dayana d\u8217'shail she\u8217'ilta} \u8212- a judge who borrows objects, {\i pasul l\u8217'meidan dina} \u8212- is invalid to render judgment. The {\i she\u8217'ilta} here is literal borrowing of objects \u8212- asking a neighbor for a ladder, a few eggs, use of a car. Rashi clarifies: {\i d\u8217'ragil l\u8217'shail mivnei iro b\u8217'hemot v\u8217'kelim} \u8212- it must be a habitual pattern, not a one-time occurrence. Such a judge presents himself to the community as someone with needs, as someone dependent on others \u8212- and that dependence creates precisely the dynamic of the {\i kohen ha\u8217'mechazer al hagranot}. People who have lent him things may have their day in court; people who anticipate being able to do him favors may calculate accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocal Borrowing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'lo aman ela d\u8217'leis lei l\u8217'oshulei} \u8212- and this applies only when the judge has nothing to lend others. {\i Aval is lei l\u8217'oshulei} \u8212- but if he also lends to others, then {\i leis lan bah} \u8212- it is not a problem. When there is genuine reciprocity \u8212- borrowing a cup of sugar and lending one in return, so to speak \u8212- the relationship is that of neighbors rather than that of petitioner and patron. The power dynamic disappears, and with it the concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava & Bar Maryon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i Gemara} challenges this: {\i Rava shashilta midbei Bar Maryon} \u8212- Rava borrowed objects from the household of Bar Maryon, {\i af gav d\u8217'lo shailei minei} \u8212- even though they never borrowed from him in return, yet he remained willing to serve as their judge. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i hasam lachshuvinhu hu d\u8217'baei} \u8212- there, Rava was not borrowing out of genuine need; he was doing it in order to honor them, to confer a sense of distinction upon them by having the great Rava come and borrow something from their household. One might still wonder whether the perception problem remains \u8212- whether those litigants, or observers, might think that Rava was now beholden to Bar Maryon \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} lets this stand as Rava\u8217's practice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unconscious Bias\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Identification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers what is perhaps the deepest analysis of why {\i shochad} corrupts. {\i Mai tama d\u8217'shochda} \u8212- what is the operative mechanism of bribery? {\i Keivan d\u8217'kablah shochda minei} \u8212- once a judge accepts a gift from someone, {\i ikrav leh daatei legabei} \u8212- his mind draws close to that person. {\i Vahavei k\u8217'gufei} \u8212- and the person becomes like himself to the judge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Blindness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ein adam roeh chov l\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- a person cannot see his own faults. This is not a character failing but a structural feature of human psychology: no one can be fully objective about oneself. Once a judge has accepted something from a litigant and that person has become, subconsciously, like an extension of the judge\u8217's own self, objectivity is structurally impossible \u8212- not because the judge is wicked or even negligent, but because the mechanism of self-perception precludes it. Even the most sincere attempt at fairness cannot fully overcome the identification created by the gift. This is why the Torah\u8217's prohibition applies even when the judge intends to rule correctly: the corruption is not in the intent but in the psychology.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shochad Etymology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai shochad} \u8212- what does the word {\i shochad} itself mean? {\i Shehu k\u8217'chad} \u8212- that he becomes as one with the person from whom he received it. The word encodes the very dynamic it prohibits.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Love & Hate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recusal Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa rules: {\i lo lidun inish dina l\u8217'man d\u8217'rachim leh} \u8212- a person should not judge a case involving someone he loves, {\i v\u8217'lo l\u8217'man d\u8217'sani leh} \u8212- nor someone he hates. This applies even to a mild positive or negative inclination, not only to the formally established categories of {\i ohev v\u8217'soneh} with which {\i halacha} is familiar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Love & Hate Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning follows directly from Rava\u8217's analysis: {\i d\u8217'rachim leh lo chazi leh chova} \u8212- one who loves the litigant will be unable to perceive any negative point against him; {\i d\u8217'sani leh lo chazi leh zachusa} \u8212- one who hates him will be unable to perceive any merit in his favor. The blindness is not necessarily willful; it is the natural consequence of a distorted lens.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Community Love\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beloved Rabbi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a sobering observation about communal dynamics: {\i hai tzurva meirabanan d\u8217'merachmin leh bnei masa} \u8212- a Torah scholar who is universally beloved by his community \u8212- {\i lav mishum d\u8217'maali tvei} \u8212- do not take this as evidence that he is exceptional. {\i Ella mishum d\u8217'lo mochech lhu b\u8217'milei d\u8217'shmaya} \u8212- rather, it likely reflects the fact that he is not doing his job: he is not rebuking them, not setting them straight, not challenging them in matters of heaven. They love him because he tells them what they want to hear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Realization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a personal reflection. {\i Meireish havah amina hani bnei Mechuza kulhu rachmu li} \u8212- at first I thought that all the people of Mechuza loved me. We know that Rava had a complicated relationship with the cosmopolitan inhabitants of Mechuza. Once he became a judge and was forced to rule against some of them, he revised his assessment: {\i minaihu sanu li u\u8217'minaihu rachmu li} \u8212- some of them hate me and some of them love me. He had initially assumed that universal love reflected his personal qualities; he later understood that the moment one begins ruling against people, love becomes selective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accept Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper realization: {\i d\u8217'man d\u8217'mechayev leh hai idna mazchei leh l\u8217'machar} \u8212- the one I ruled against today I may rule in favor of tomorrow. Since the same person who is ruled against in one case will be ruled for in another, the idea that one can calibrate one\u8217's rulings to achieve universal approval is simply false. Rava concluded: {\i im rachmu kulhu rachmu li v\u8217'im sanu kulhu sanu li} \u8212- whatever they feel about me, they feel. A judge who is trying to be universally liked is not a good judge; you cannot satisfy everyone, and eventually you must accept that some will love you and some will hate you depending on when they appear before you and what you are forced to rule. This is, as the {\i Gemara} presents it, a genuinely liberating insight for anyone who must render judgment or teach with integrity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Monetary Bribes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verbal Bribes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} establishes: {\i eino tzarich lomar shochad mamon} \u8212- it goes without saying that monetary bribes are prohibited; {\i ela afilu shochad devarim nami assur} \u8212- even {\i shochad devarim}, which the {\i Gemara} will illustrate with actions and services rather than words, is also forbidden. The word {\i devarim} here may be read as \u8220"things\u8221" or \u8220"matters\u8221" rather than specifically \u8220"words,\u8221" and the examples that follow make clear it encompasses non-verbal favors and services.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Bridge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel was crossing a bridge when a certain man came and gave him his hand to help him across. Shmuel asked: what brings you here? The man replied: I have a court case before you \u8212- I was coming to the city for the judgment, so I helped you along the way. {\i Psilna lach ledina} \u8212- Shmuel declared: you have disqualified me from judging your case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimar Feather\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar was seated in judgment when a feather fell on his head. A certain man came and removed it. Ameimar asked him what he was doing; the man replied that he had a case before him. {\i Psilna lach ledina} \u8212- you have disqualified me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Ukva Spit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Ukva spat in front of him. A certain man came and covered the spit. Again, the man said he had a judgment before him. {\i Psilna lach ledina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is notable in all three cases is that in each instance the person explicitly mentions the upcoming court case. This raises the question of whether the problem is specifically the framing \u8212- the fact that the favor was articulated in connection with the judgment \u8212- or whether any unsolicited favor, even without such mention, would suffice to disqualify. If someone had helped Shmuel across a bridge and only appeared in his court three hours later without any explicit connection being drawn, would recusal be required? Perhaps; but the {\i Gemara\u8217's} examples all feature the explicit verbal linking of the favor to the judgment. Whether {\i shochad devarim} requires that conscious framing by the benefactor, or whether the mere receipt of the favor creates the disqualification, is a question the {\i Gemara} does not resolve explicitly here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sharecropper Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Early Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael beRabbi Yossi had a sharecropper who, every Friday, would bring him a basket of produce \u8212- his share from the fields the sharecropper worked. One Thursday, the sharecropper brought the basket a day early. Rabbi Yishmael asked: why the change? The sharecropper explained that he had to come into the city early because he had a court case, and since he was passing by anyway, he thought he would bring the produce ahead of schedule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recusal & Replacement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael refused to accept the basket: {\i psilna lach ledina} \u8212- you have disqualified me from judging your case. He appointed two other rabbis to hear the case in his stead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unconscious Arguments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While the two rabbis were deliberating and Rabbi Yishmael was walking about nearby, he found himself thinking: {\i ei ba\u8217'ei ta\u8217'in hachi, ve\u8217'ei ba\u8217'ei ta\u8217'in hachi} \u8212- if he wants to argue this way he should, if he wants to argue that way he should. He was spontaneously generating arguments in favor of the sharecropper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Subconscious Bias\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He exclaimed: {\i tipach nafsham shel mekablei shochad} \u8212- blasted be the souls of those who accept bribes! And then he reflected on his own situation: I refused to take the produce. And even had I taken it, it was mine \u8212- it was my legitimate portion of the field that was merely being delivered one day early. Yet even so, that slight early delivery, that small favor never actually accepted, was already generating unconscious advocacy on his behalf in my mind. {\i Al achat kama vechama hamekablei shochad} \u8212- how much more so those who actually accept bribes, even with every intention of judging fairly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First Shearings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shearings Delivery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael beRabbi Elisha \u8212- a {\i kohen} \u8212- was entitled to receive {\i reishit hagez}, the first shearings of a flock, from anyone in his vicinity. A certain man traveled a considerable distance to bring him these shearings. Rabbi Yishmael asked where he was from; when told, he asked: was there truly no {\i kohen} anywhere between there and here to whom you could have given this? The man admitted: I have a court case before you, and since I was traveling to the city anyway, I thought I would bring the shearings to you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Recusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael declared: {\i psilna lach ledina} \u8212- you have disqualified me. {\i Lo kabil minei} \u8212- he declined to accept the shearings. He again appointed two other rabbis to hear the case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arguments Again\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And again, while the rabbis were deliberating, Rabbi Yishmael found himself pacing and thinking: {\i ei baisun hachi, ei baisun hachi} \u8212- if they argue this way, if they argue that way. Again, arguments in favor of the man who had been coming to bring him his due were forming in his mind unbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Entitled Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His reflection again: {\i tipach nafsham shel mekablei shochad} \u8212- blasted be the souls of those who accept bribes. I did not accept the shearings. And even had I accepted them, I was legally entitled to them \u8212- they were literally mine by right. Yet even the entitlement, even the knowledge that this man had made a journey to bring me something I was owed, was enough to color my thinking subconsciously. {\i Mekablei shochad al achat kama vechama} \u8212- those who actually accept bribes, however certain they are of their own intentions, cannot escape the mechanism that Rava identified: the gift creates identification, the identification creates blindness, and the blindness is impervious to good intentions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 106\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Anan & Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fish Gift Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a story about {\i Rav Anan}. A certain man brought him a basket of small fish and, when asked what brought him there, openly stated that he had a case in judgment. {\i Rav Anan} refused the gift. {\i Lo kabil minei} \u8212- he did not accept it \u8212- and told the man: \u8220"You have invalidated me from serving as your judge.\u8221" This mirrors the earlier stories on the daf, where the rabbis not only recused themselves from judgment but refused to receive the accompanying gifts as well, even though in principle one could imagine doing one without the other. The man responded that he was content to have his case heard elsewhere \u8212- {\i dina demar lo beina} \u8212- but asked that {\i Rav Anan} at least accept the gift as a {\i mitzvah}, independent of any judicial role, because he wished to perform the act of {\i kiruvei bikkurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gifts as Bikkurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The man\u8217's argument invokes a {\i braita}: the verse in the story of {\i Elisha} describes a man bringing bread of {\i bikkurim} \u8212- twenty loaves of barley bread and {\i karmel} \u8212- to the {\i ish ha\u8217'Elohim}. The {\i gemara} asks: did {\i Elisha} eat {\i bikkurim}? {\i Rashi} attempts to prove that {\i Elisha} was not a {\i kohen}; {\i Tosafot} notes the simpler answer that they were not in {\i Yerushalayim} and this was not being brought to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. Rather, {\i ela lomar lach} \u8212- the verse comes to teach \u8212- that anyone who brings a gift to a {\i talmid chacham} is as if he brought {\i bikkurim}. That is the {\i mitzvah} the man wished to perform.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aliyah & Rebbe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This moment opens a broader reflection on how {\i Chazal} dealt with the absence of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} and created meaningful substitutes. Most striking in this regard is the idea that just as there is a {\i mitzvah} of {\i aliyah laregel}, so too the {\i gemara} teaches there is a {\i mitzvah} of {\i mekabel pnei rabo baregel} \u8212- presenting oneself before one\u8217's {\i rebbe} on the festivals. The {\i rebbe} in some sense takes the place of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} as the destination of spiritual pilgrimage. This is already hinted at in the {\i pesukim} themselves. The husband of the {\i Shunamit} says to her: {\i madua at holechet elav hayom lo chodesh velo Shabbat} \u8212- why are you going to him today, it is neither {\i Rosh Chodesh} nor {\i Shabbat}? \u8212- indicating that one of the practices of the time was to travel to the {\i ish Elohim} on {\i Shabbat} and spend the day in spiritual communion. And in {\i Yeshayahu} the vision of the end of days includes {\i umidei Shabbat beshabbato yavo kol basar lehishtachavot lefanai} \u8212- each {\i Shabbat} all flesh will come to prostrate before God. The {\i ish Elohim} in {\i Shmuel} and the eschatological vision in {\i Yeshayahu} share the same rhythm, and that pattern becomes the foundation for {\i mekabel pnei rav} as the post-{\i Beit HaMikdash} substitute for {\i aliyah laregel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Accepted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the story: the {\i gemara} concludes that {\i Rav Anan}, upon hearing the man\u8217's reasoning, accepted the gift. {\i Hashta d\u8217'amart li ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- now that you\u8217've given me a proper rationale \u8212- perhaps the explanation also freed the gift from any taint of {\i shochad} by reframing it entirely, {\i mikabelna}. He accepted it. But he would still not serve as the man\u8217's judge. And, as we will see, the story does not end there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nachman & Bias\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Referral Letter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because the man still needed his case heard, {\i Rav Anan} sent him before {\i Rav Nachman} and accompanied the referral with a letter: {\i dayneye l\u8217'mar l\u8217'hai gavra} \u8212- please hear this man\u8217's case \u8212- {\i d\u8217'ana Anan pasilna lei l\u8217'dina} \u8212- for I, {\i Anan}, am invalid to serve as his judge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Misinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are multiple reasons one might be invalid as a judge. When {\i Rav Nachman} received this message, {\i mideshlach li hachi shma mina kriveye hu} \u8212- since he sent me this, it must be that this person is his relative \u8212- and that is why {\i Rav Anan} had to recuse himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that moment, {\i Rav Nachman} had before him a case involving orphans. When the letter arrived requesting he hear this other man\u8217's case, he recognized that he faced two {\i mitzvot}: the obligation to adjudicate the orphans\u8217' case \u8212- a general {\i mitzvah} of {\i ushfatetem tzedek}, and as the {\i Maharsha} adds, also the {\i mitzvah} of {\i lo takiru panim bamishpat k\u8217'katon k\u8217'gadol tishm\u8217'un} with its particular application to vulnerable parties like orphans \u8212- and, on the other side, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kavod Torah}, since hearing this man\u8217's case first would show honor to {\i Rav Anan} and thereby to {\i Torah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphans Suspended\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} resolved to set aside the orphans\u8217' case and hear this man\u8217's case first: {\i salik l\u8217'dina d\u8217'yasmei v\u8217'achtei l\u8217'dineye}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silenced Party\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence was immediate and devastating. When the opposing litigant saw that {\i Rav Nachman} was setting aside the orphans\u8217' case to hear this man\u8217's \u8212- apparently because he had arrived with a letter from {\i Rav Anan} \u8212- it appeared to him as unambiguous favoritism. {\i Istatam tanateye} \u8212- he was silenced. He did not feel free to fully articulate his claim and position, because he perceived that the outcome was already determined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bias & Perception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This story is striking in several respects. The earlier stories on the daf illustrated how a bribe, even when refused, exerts subconscious influence on the judge. Here, {\i Rav Nachman} received no bribe at all. He merely believed the man to be {\i Rav Anan}\u8217's relative, and he was not even trying to favor the litigant himself \u8212- he was trying to honor {\i Rav Anan}. Yet even that action, taken for entirely legitimate reasons, was sufficient to silence the opposing party and potentially pervert the course of justice. The question is not only whether the judge will be impartial, but what messages his actions communicate and how those messages affect the people whose cases are being heard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dynamic appears in real-world settings as well. In cases heard before {\i batei din}, including those with strong reputations for fairness, similar dynamics can arise: a woman appearing before an all-male court may already feel inhibited from fully stating her case; if the {\i dayanim} happen to know the other party socially and exchange greetings with him at the outset, the effect on the opposing side can be immediate and silencing, regardless of whether any actual bias exists. The lesson is that it is not sufficient that the judge be impartial in his own mind; the culture, conduct, and visible signals within the courtroom must themselves be beyond reproach. {\i Kavod Torah} is a genuine consideration, but when it conflicts with the integrity of {\i din}, the {\i gemara} here suggests it must yield.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether there is also an issue of {\i chalishut ha-da\u8217'at} \u8212- the harm caused to a party who has had their case interrupted mid-hearing. The {\i Maharsha} discusses this in connection with {\i Sanhedrin}. But the primary emphasis here is on the way in which even well-intentioned judicial conduct communicates bias and suppresses the speech of litigants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Anan & Eliyahu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliyahu Visits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Anan} had a standing relationship with {\i Eliyahu HaNavi}: {\i Rav Anan havei ragil Eliyahu de-ati gabei} \u8212- {\i Eliyahu} would regularly come to visit him. During these visits, {\i Eliyahu} taught him {\i Seder de-Eliyahu}, that collection of stories and teachings associated with {\i Eliyahu HaNavi} \u8212- specifically {\i Seder Eliyahu Rabba} and {\i Seder Eliyahu Zuta}, as we will see in a moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliyahu Withdrawn\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Keivan de-avad hachi} \u8212- once {\i Rav Anan} acted as he did \u8212- sending the letter, accepting the fish \u8212- {\i Eliyahu} withdrew. {\i Istalek}. This is a remarkable consequence, particularly because {\i Rav Anan} had not acted with malicious intent. Yet as noted, the problem is often not what one deliberately does, but what one fails to think through \u8212- the unintended consequences of one\u8217's actions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fast & Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Anan} sat in a fast, {\i yativ be-ta\u8217'anita}, and prayed for mercy, {\i u-va\u8217'ei rachamei}, and {\i Eliyahu} returned. But when {\i Eliyahu} came back, {\i Rav Anan} was frightened of him \u8212- {\i havei miv-it lei be-vuta\u8217'ei} \u8212- perhaps in the wake of the withdrawal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Box Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To manage his fear, {\i Rav Anan} constructed a box and sat inside it while {\i Eliyahu} continued to teach him \u8212- {\i ve-avad teivuta ve-yativ kamei} \u8212- until {\i Eliyahu} finished imparting the complete {\i seder} to him. This is the origin of the two versions: {\i Seder Eliyahu Rabba} and {\i Seder Eliyahu Zuta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba vs Zuta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} explains that {\i Seder Eliyahu Rabba} is what was taught {\i outside} the box, and {\i Seder Eliyahu Zuta} is what was taught {\i inside} the box, without explaining why one is larger than the other. {\i Tosafot} explains: {\i Eliyahu} had already taught {\i Rav Anan} the majority of the material before the incident, and so the first, larger portion \u8212- taught before the disruption \u8212- is called {\i Rabba}, and the smaller remaining portion \u8212- taught within the box \u8212- is called {\i Zuta}. Perhaps, too, the teaching inside the box was of somewhat lesser quality or depth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mida Kneged Mida\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Maharsha} identifies a principle of {\i mida keneged mida} in the two aspects of {\i Rav Anan}\u8217's response. The {\i ta\u8217'anit} served as atonement for having accepted the fish \u8212- even though his acceptance was grounded in a legitimate argument about {\i bikkurim}, he now acknowledged it was a mistake. The trembling before {\i Eliyahu} and the need to create a barrier \u8212- the box \u8212- corresponded to what {\i Rav Anan}\u8217's actions had caused the opposing litigant to experience: the silencing, the pulling back, the fear to speak freely when one senses that the dynamic in the room is working against you. {\i Rav Anan} now experienced, in a different register, precisely what his conduct had imposed on another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosef & Famine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Famine Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} shifts to a passage set in the years of {\i Rav Yosef}. There was {\i ritcha} \u8212- divine anger, meaning famine \u8212- and the rabbis asked {\i Rav Yosef} to pray for its removal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elisha Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} responded by pointing to the precedent of {\i Elisha}: in {\i Elisha}\u8217's time there was also divine anger, {\i de-havi rabanan miftari mikamei} \u8212- when the rabbis would leave {\i Elisha}\u8217's learning \u8212- {\i havei payshei trei alfein u\u8217'masan rabanan} \u8212- there would still be 2,200 rabbis remaining in the {\i beis medrash}. And even in a time of {\i ritcha}, {\i Elisha} did not pray to remove the divine anger \u8212- or at least there is no record in {\i Tanach} that he did. {\i Rav Yosef}\u8217's implicit point: even at that level of Torah stature, prayer was not offered. How much more so should I not be expected to do so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2200 Students\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: how do we know that {\i Elisha} had 2,200 students? The source is the verse in which {\i Elisha}\u8217's attendant says {\i u\u8217'mah eten zeh lifnei meah ish} \u8212- how shall I set this before 100 men? The verse refers back to the same {\i pasuk} that opened our discussion of {\i bikkurim}: the man from {\i Ba\u8217'al Shalishah} brought twenty loaves of barley bread and {\i karmel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bread Math\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The calculation: if those 22 loaves were distributed, one per 100 men \u8212- each loaf set before 100 people \u8212- then 22 loaves times 100 people equals 2,200 people total. That is the size of the {\i talmidim} who remained in {\i Elisha}\u8217's {\i beis medrash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yeshiva Decline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: 1,200\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} traces the decline of {\i yeshiva} enrollment across generations. When the rabbis would leave the {\i yeshiva} of {\i Rav}, there would remain {\i alfa u\u8217'masan rabanan} \u8212- 1,200 rabbis still in the {\i beis medrash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna: 800\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the rabbis left the {\i yeshiva} of {\i Rav Huna}, there would remain 800 rabbis. {\i Rav Huna} taught with 13 {\i amoraim} \u8212- not {\i amoraim} in the sense of the rabbinic generation, but in the technical sense of the role: the {\i amora} was the {\i rav}\u8217's loudspeaker, who would repeat and project the teacher\u8217's words to different sections of the large assembly hall. With 13 such figures distributed throughout the room, the scope of {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's audience can be appreciated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dust Covers Sun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the rabbis would rise from {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's {\i masivta} and shake out their garments, {\i havei salik avka} \u8212- dust would rise up \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'kasei leh leyoma} \u8212- and cover the sun, darkening the day. In {\i Eretz Yisrael} they would say: {\i kamu lehu mimasivta deRav Huna deBavel} \u8212- the Babylonian {\i yeshiva} of {\i Rav Huna} has finished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava & Yosef: 400\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the rabbis left the {\i yeshiva} of {\i Rava} and {\i Rav Yosef}, there remained 400 rabbis. {\i Ve-karu le-nafshaihu yasmei} \u8212- they called themselves orphans. Given the size of the previous generations \u8212- 2,200 under {\i Elisha}, 1,200 under {\i Rav}, 800 under {\i Rav Huna} \u8212- the descent to 400 felt like bereavement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later: 200\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the rabbis left the {\i yeshiva} of {\i Abaye}, or according to others the {\i yeshiva} of {\i Rav Pappa}, or {\i Rav Ashi}, there remained only 200 rabbis. {\i Ve-karu le-nafshaihu yasmei de-yasmei} \u8212- orphans of orphans \u8212- so small a remnant compared to what had been.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temple Payments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blemish Inspectors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the principal theme of the chapter, which emerged from the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's reference to {\i shnei dayanei gezerot} and the question of their payment from communal funds. {\i Rav Yitzchak bar Redifa} said in the name of {\i Avani}: those in {\i Yerushalayim} whose role it was to inspect animals for blemishes \u8212- {\i mevakrim mumin she-be-Yerushalayim} \u8212- received their salary from {\i terumat ha-lishka}, the communal funds collected annually for the {\i korbanot}. When there were surplus funds from the {\i lishka}, they were also available for other Temple-related or communal services. The inspection of blemishes is clearly {\i tzorchei korban} \u8212- directly connected to the sacrificial service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechita Teachers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} said in the name of {\i Shmuel}: the {\i talmidei chachamim} who taught the {\i kohanim} the {\i hilchot shechita} also received their pay from {\i terumat ha-lishka}, for this too falls under {\i tzorchei korban}. This calls to mind the stories at the beginning of {\i Yoma} about teaching the {\i Kohen Gadol} the laws of the {\i avoda}. The role of teaching the {\i halachot} is distinct from the physical transmission of practical skill \u8212- a {\i kohen} may receive the practical skill through tradition, but the legal framework governing each scenario had to be formally studied. Hence it is classified as a Temple service deserving communal support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kemitza Teachers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Giddel} said in the name of {\i Rav}: those {\i talmidei chachamim} who taught the {\i kohanim} the {\i hilchot kemitza} similarly received their salary from {\i terumat ha-lishka}. The same principle applies: it is the legal dimension of the service \u8212- what constitutes a valid {\i kemitza}, what invalidates it \u8212- that requires instruction, and that instruction is considered integral to the {\i korban} service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Proofers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabba bar bar Chana} said in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: those who proofread {\i sifrei Torah} in {\i Yerushalayim} \u8212- {\i maggihei sefarim she-be-Yerushalayim} \u8212- also received their payment from {\i terumat ha-lishka}. This is more surprising, because proofreading {\i sifrei Torah} is not directly connected to the {\i korbanot}. As we will continue to see on this daf, {\i terumat ha-lishka} and particularly {\i shiyarei terumat ha-lishka} served as a broad catch-all for a range of Temple-related and communal needs. {\i Rashi} here notes the relevant justification: {\i lev beit din matne aleihem} \u8212- the {\i beit din} reserved the authority to reassign these funds as it saw fit. Anyone donating the {\i shekalim} understood that the ultimate disposition of the money lay with the {\i beit din}\u8217's discretion, even if the primary purpose was the {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parochet Weavers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} said in the name of {\i Rabbah bar Avuha}: the women who wove the {\i parochet} \u8212- the curtain for the {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- also received their payment from {\i terumat ha-lishka}. This immediately prompts a question: is the {\i parochet} related to the {\i korbanot} or is it really a structural element of the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, which would place it under {\i bedek ha-bayit}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a dissenting view: {\i ve\u8217'ani omer mi-kodshei bedek ha-bayit} \u8212- I say it comes from {\i bedek ha-bayit} \u8212- {\i hu parochet tachat binyan asuyot} \u8212- because the {\i parochet} is made underneath the roofed structure. The logic is that something hanging within a roofed enclosure becomes part of the structure itself, not merely a suspended object. This distinction has a fascinating parallel in {\i hilchot Shabbat}: if one hangs a curtain across an open doorway, according to the {\i Chazon Ish} one is adding to the structure and not merely decorating it, making it more like installing a door than hanging a picture on a wall. The {\i parochet} hanging within the {\i heichal} functions similarly \u8212- it is integral to the architectural space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises an objection from a {\i braita}: the women weaving the {\i parochet}, the {\i Beis Garmo} who taught the preparation of {\i lechem ha-panim}, and the {\i Beis Avtinas} who knew how to prepare the {\i ketoret} \u8212- all of them received their pay from {\i terumat ha-lishka}, not from {\i bedek ha-bayit}. The {\i gemara} resolves the contradiction: {\i hatam be-divavi} \u8212- that {\i braita} refers to the curtains hanging in the gates, which were in the open, unroofed areas of the Temple complex. Those would indeed be classified differently \u8212- more like a {\i kli} than part of the {\i binyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 13 Parochet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Zeira} said in the name of {\i Rav}: there were thirteen {\i parochet} curtains in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. Seven corresponded to the seven gates. One was for the opening of the {\i heichal}, one for the opening of the {\i ulam}. Two were in the {\i dvir} \u8212- the {\i Kodesh HaKodashim} \u8212- and two corresponding ones above, since the {\i heichal} complex was a double-story structure. The curtains within the roofed structure \u8212- the {\i ulam}, {\i heichal}, and {\i dvir} \u8212- fall into the category of {\i bedek ha-bayit}; those at the gates in the open courtyards fall under {\i terumat ha-lishka}. Where a {\i parochet} does not come under {\i bedek ha-bayit}, it defaults to {\i terumat ha-lishka}, even though {\i terumat ha-lishka} is in principle designated for {\i korbanot} rather than all of these auxiliary purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fund Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Para Aduma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues with another case. The women who raised their children for the {\i para aduma} service also received their support from {\i terumat ha-lishka}. The background: the {\i Tzadokim} maintained that the {\i kohen} performing the burning of the {\i para aduma} could never have been {\i tamei} at any point. The {\i Chachamim}, in a remarkable counter-move, deliberately arranged for the {\i kohen} to become {\i tamei} and then immerse in a {\i mikveh}, so that he would be a {\i tvul yom} at the time of the service \u8212- thereby demonstrating through practice that the law followed their understanding, not the {\i Tzadokim}\u8217's. This very leniency on one end was balanced by extraordinary stringencies in other areas of {\i tahara} related to the {\i para aduma}. One of those stringencies involved raising children in conditions of guaranteed {\i tahara} from birth. Pregnant women would give birth in specially constructed spaces above caves, where the cave ceiling would block any potential {\i tumat ha-met} from bodies buried in the earth below. The children born there were raised entirely in those conditions, and they would assist in the preparatory stages of the {\i para aduma} service. The communal funds of {\i terumat ha-lishka} provided the financial support for the women maintaining these households.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abba Shaul} offers an alternative: the prominent women of {\i Yerushalayim} \u8212- {\i nashim yakirot she-be-Yerushalayim} \u8212- would voluntarily provide food, clothing, and other support for these women and children, so that the burden did not need to fall on {\i terumat ha-lishka} at all. {\i Rashi} specifies {\i levush u-chsut} as among the provisions \u8212- echoing the {\i zona u-mefarnes} language familiar from the laws of {\i ketuba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna} asked {\i Rav}: what is the source for the {\i klei shares}, the Temple service vessels? Do they come from {\i kodshei bedek ha-bayit}, or not? The framing of the question reflects what we have learned: {\i bedek ha-bayit} funds are for structural matters, while {\i terumat ha-lishka} is the catch-all for everything else. The vessels could be seen as {\i tzorech mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- connected to the altar, which is so large and fixed it functions almost as part of the structure itself, suggesting {\i bedek ha-bayit} \u8212- or as {\i tzorech korban} \u8212- used in the actual bringing of {\i korbanot}, suggesting {\i terumat ha-lishka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} answered: they come from {\i terumat ha-lishka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this from {\i Divrei HaYamim}: {\i vaya\u8217'asu keilim le-veis Hashem klei shares} \u8212- they made service vessels for the {\i Beit HaShem} \u8212- and the context is the leftover money from the {\i bedek ha-bayit} collection. This appears to show that the {\i klei shares} came from {\i bedek ha-bayit} funds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melachim Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel account in {\i Melachim} resolves this: {\i ach lo ye\u8217'aseh beis Hashem safos kesef\u8230 ? hem le-melacha yisnehu} \u8212- the money collected for the {\i Beit HaShem} was not to be used for vessels, but was to be given to the craftsmen who would work on the structure. This directly contradicts the {\i Divrei HaYamim} passage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lev Beis Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya} \u8212- there is no difficulty. {\i Kan she-gavu ve-hosiru kan she-gavu ve-lo hosiru} \u8212- in one case they had collected and had a surplus; in the other they had collected and did not have a surplus. In principle, {\i bedek ha-bayit} funds are collected and designated specifically for structural purposes, not for {\i klei shares}. But if there were leftover funds, the {\i beit din} could reassign them. How is this reassignment legitimate? {\i Rabbi Abbahu} invokes the principle of {\i lev beis din matne aleihem}: the {\i beit din} stipulates in advance \u8212- {\i im hutzrechu hutzrechu, ve-im lav yehu le-klei shares} \u8212- if the money is needed for {\i bedek ha-bayit}, it will be used for that; if not, it will go to {\i klei shares}. Anyone contributing to the fund does so with the understanding that the {\i beit din} retains ultimate discretion over its use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle consistently recurs in these discussions. The underlying assumption is that the {\i beit din} \u8212- conceived in the {\i gemara}\u8217's framework as the rabbinic authority \u8212- holds ultimate oversight over Temple finances, an idealized picture that, historically, would have been complicated by the dominant role of the {\i kohanim} in actual Temple administration. As one might observe, the historical reality was that the {\i kohanim} ran the institution and the rabbis were largely outside its precincts, often in tension with the {\i Tzadokim} among the priestly class. The {\i gemara} retrospectively imagines an arrangement in which rabbinic authority supervises everything \u8212- {\i lev beit din matne aleihem}, not {\i lev kohanim matne aleihem} \u8212- a projection of rabbinic self-understanding onto the Temple era.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tana debei Rabbi Yishmael} states: {\i klei shares ba\u8217'in mi-terumat ha-lishka} \u8212- the Temple vessels come from {\i terumat ha-lishka}. The proof text is {\i es shear ha-kesef} \u8212- the remainder of the money. In context, this verse appears in the narrative about money collected for {\i bedek ha-bayit}, but {\i tana debei Rabbi Yishmael} understands {\i shear ha-kesef} as a reference to the {\i terumat ha-lishka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shirei Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: perhaps {\i shear ha-kesef} means literally the {\i shirei terumat ha-lishka} \u8212- the leftover surplus \u8212- rather than the main body of the {\i terumat ha-lishka} fund? On that reading, the vessels would come not from the primary {\i terumat ha-lishka} but only from any unspent remainder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef Rishon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this. The word {\i hakesef} \u8212- with the definite article \u8212- focuses attention on the money itself, not on its remainder. Drawing on {\i Rava}\u8217's interpretation of {\i zot torat ha-olah} \u8212- where the {\i heh} indicates the primary {\i olah}, the {\i olat ha-tamid}, which precedes all others \u8212- so too here: {\i hakesef} signals {\i kesef rishon}, the main fund. The vessels come from the primary {\i terumat ha-lishka}, not merely from its surplus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Main Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The list of items that come from {\i terumat ha-lishka} thus includes: the {\i ketoret}, all communal {\i korbanot}, the golden altar, and the service vessels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motar Nesachim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, a competing {\i braita} presents a different source for the golden altar and the {\i klei shares}: they come from {\i motar nesachim} \u8212- the surplus of the libation funds. The practice was that when one donated funds for the {\i nesachim} accompanying a {\i korban}, one would intentionally provide slightly more than the required amount to ensure sufficiency; the {\i Beit HaMikdash} would level off the measure, and the small excess would be set aside for {\i hekdesh}. Over time, all these small surpluses accumulated into meaningful sums. According to this view, it was those accumulated funds \u8212- {\i motar nesachim} \u8212- that financed the {\i klei shares}, a somewhat counterintuitive assignment that may reflect the practical reality that the {\i klei shares} did not need frequent replacement and the accumulated {\i motar nesachim} was roughly equivalent in scale to what was needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Building Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The outer altar \u8212- {\i mizbe\u8217'ach ha-olah} \u8212- together with the {\i lishkot} and the {\i azarot}, the chambers and the courtyards of the Temple complex, come from {\i bedek ha-bayit}. This is clear: these are the fundamental structural elements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 City Needs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Any construction outside the walls of the {\i azara} \u8212- but still on the Temple Mount or in its vicinity \u8212- comes from {\i shiyarei ha-lishka}, the surplus of the {\i terumat ha-lishka} fund, which was regularly used for a broader range of communal purposes. The same source provides for the walls and towers of the city of {\i Yerushalayim} and all the {\i tzorchei ha-ir} \u8212- the general municipal needs of the city. The walls of {\i Yerushalayim} are not merely civil infrastructure; they are bound up with the {\i kedushah} of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} itself, since it is the wall of {\i Yerushalayim} that defines the {\i kedushah} of the {\i Mikdash}\u8217's environs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Leftover Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kayitz Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} addresses the {\i tannaitic} dispute over what to do with the leftover {\i truma} funds. The first opinion: {\i motar peirot l\u8217'kayitz ha-mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- the surplus was used to keep the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} active, {\i kayitz ha-mizbe\u8217'ach}. The etymology is disputed: some derive it from {\i l\u8217'hakitz}, to awaken or rouse; others associate it with {\i kayitz}, the summer, as in something left idle \u8212- and just as one does not want fruit to sit unused in summer, so one does not want the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} to stand idle. The concern was genuine: if there were periods \u8212- even within a single day \u8212- when no {\i korbanot} were being brought, the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} would stand empty, an embarrassment to the institution. The solution was to ensure that communal {\i korbanot} would be offered during those intervals, funded by the surplus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buy Low Sell High\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operational model under this first opinion: the {\i Beit HaMikdash} would purchase fruit cheaply and sell it at higher prices, and the profits would go toward the {\i kayitz ha-mizbe\u8217'ach}. The {\i Beit HaMikdash} was in a unique position to execute this \u8212- it had something approaching a monopoly and a captive market of pilgrims arriving for the {\i regalim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael} proposed a more targeted version of this business model: buy wines, oils, and fine flours \u8212- precisely the materials that pilgrims arriving at the {\i Beit HaMikdash} would need for their {\i nesachim} and {\i korbanot} \u8212- and use the profits from these sales to fund the operating expenses of the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. The strategic logic is clear: these were items in guaranteed demand, and the institutional position of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} made it uniquely positioned to profit from that demand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} objected on principle: {\i ein mishtakrin be-shel hekdesh} \u8212- one does not use {\i hekdesh} money for commercial profit. There is something fundamentally degrading about turning the funds of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} into business capital. {\i Ein aniyut bi-mkom ashirut} \u8212- there is no poverty where there is wealth \u8212- meaning that {\i hekdesh} ought to embody and project an image of transcendent abundance, and subjecting it to the contingencies and indignities of the marketplace undermines that dignity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poor People\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} pressed a second objection regarding the parallel case of charity funds: {\i af lo be-shel aniyim} \u8212- one also does not invest funds belonging to the poor. {\i Dilma mitrami lehu ani ve-leika le-mitav leh} \u8212- a poor person may need the money immediately, and if the funds are tied up in an investment, they will not be available when urgently needed. The principle extends naturally to institutional funds generally: an endowment may earn returns, but its primary obligation is to the constituency it serves, and availability matters as much as yield. The {\i gemara} here touches on a set of questions with obvious resonance for any institution that holds funds in trust for those who depend on them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 107\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the very bottom of 106b, where the Gemara takes up the mishna concerning the {\i shnei dayanei gezerot} and the case of {\i Admon}. Among Admon\u8217's rulings was the scenario of someone who traveled overseas ({\i limdinat hayam}) and left his wife without provision. Admon holds that the wife takes an oath at the end of the process \u8212- presumably when it comes time to collect her full {\i ketuba} upon his death, at which point she swears that nothing had been prepaid and that her collection of {\i mezonot} was conducted properly. The {\i bnei kohanim gedolim}, by contrast, require an oath at the beginning as well, ensuring that before she draws on the estate she genuinely deserves the money. Admon\u8217's concern is the wife\u8217's immediate need: should the {\i shvua} stand in the way of her being provided for? The Gemara does not fully spell out what divides the two positions, but it opens, at the top of 107a, with the dispute between Rav and Shmuel, which frames the entire discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is stated plainly: {\i mi shehalach limdinat hayam} \u8212- a husband has gone overseas. Rav rules {\i poskin mezonot l\u8217'eshet ish}: {\i beit din} assesses the situation and provides an allowance for the wife. As Rashi explains, {\i beit din yordin lenichsei mi shehalach limdinat hayam uposkin mezonot leishto} \u8212- the court descends to the husband\u8217's property and liquidates assets as necessary to feed his wife, since he carries a clear {\i ketuba} obligation to support her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees: {\i ein poskin mezonot l\u8217'eshet ish}. One should not do this. Shmuel himself, however, concedes that Rav would agree with him on one point: {\i bishlosha chadashim harishonim} \u8212- in the first three months, even Rav would not yet seize the property. The reason is {\i lefi she\u8217'ein adam maniach beito reikam}: we do not assume a person would be so irresponsible as to leave his household entirely unprovided for. This already signals the logic behind Shmuel\u8217's broader position \u8212- we simply do not know what arrangements may have been made, and we must give the husband some benefit of the doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then clarifies where agreement ends and dispute begins. If the husband has been heard to have died ({\i sheshamu bo shemet}), nobody disagrees: the wife is supported as a widow from the estate. The dispute concerns only the case where no such report has arrived and the husband is simply absent. Rav rules {\i poskin}, since he is obligated to support her and is merely not physically present. Shmuel rules {\i ein poskin}, and two explanations are offered for his position. One: {\i emma tzerorei atfsa} \u8212- perhaps he had already set aside money for her, a bundle of funds deposited on her behalf. The other, offered by Rav Pappa: {\i chaishinan shema amar lah tzei ma\u8217'aseh yadayich bimzonotayich} \u8212- perhaps he had told her that in his absence her own labor would substitute for his obligation to feed her, so that she would keep her earnings in lieu of receiving {\i mezonot} from him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and Tosafot both address whether the husband could make such an arrangement unilaterally or whether her agreement was required, and both conclude \u8212- though this is not straightforward from the Gemara itself \u8212- that the case involves her having agreed to it. The underlying issue goes back to earlier discussions about whose interest is protected by the exchange of {\i mezonot} for {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}. In any case, Shmuel\u8217's point is that we cannot know which of these arrangements was in place, and therefore we cannot move to seize the husband\u8217's property on the basis of her claim alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then ask: why not simply require her to take a {\i shvua} and resolve the doubt? The general rule of {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'aya} places the burden of proof on the one seeking to extract money. Here, no one is explicitly denying her claim; the husband is simply absent. Yet Shmuel apparently feels the doubt is serious enough that a {\i shvua} would not suffice to override it and justify seizing someone\u8217's property. That seems to be at least part of the reason why the debate between Admon and the {\i bnei kohanim gedolim} about the timing of the {\i shvua} is relevant, though the Gemara leaves this somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks why there is no dispute in the case where the husband has actually died. Even there, after all, he might have set aside money for her, or they might have made the arrangement about her labor. Rashi addresses the {\i tzerorei} concern by noting that when she eventually collects her {\i ketuba} she must take a {\i shvua} that she has not pre-collected, and that resolves the matter. As for the {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} arrangement \u8212- that cannot apply to the heirs; whatever he agreed to during his lifetime does not automatically bind the estate, and any new arrangement would have to be contracted afresh. Tosafot takes a different approach: the presumption that the husband made some arrangement ({\i ein adam maniach beito reikam}) is only reasonable when he is alive and presumably acting responsibly. Death, however, is not something people arrange for, and therefore we cannot extend the presumption to a man who has actually died. That is why, once he is dead, the doubts Shmuel raises no longer apply, and everyone agrees to support the wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye points out a practical difference between the two explanations of Shmuel\u8217's position. The case to consider is either: an adult wife who does not earn enough to support herself ({\i gedola v\u8217'lo safka}) \u8212- perhaps she has no marketable skills, or as Rashi explains, it is a time of famine and food is prohibitively expensive \u8212- or: a minor wife who does earn enough to support herself ({\i ktana v\u8217'safka}). These two scenarios pull in opposite directions depending on which of Shmuel\u8217's rationales one adopts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the concern is {\i tzerorei} \u8212- that the husband set aside funds \u8212- that presumption applies only to an adult wife, someone capable of taking possession of money and managing it responsibly. A husband would not deposit a sum of money for a minor wife who cannot be entrusted with it. So if the case involves a minor, the {\i tzerorei} concern evaporates, and we might well go ahead and provide for her. But if she is an adult who cannot earn her own keep, then {\i tzerorei} remains a live concern and we would not provide. Conversely, the {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} concern \u8212- that he told her to work for herself \u8212- is meaningful only when she can actually do so. If she is an adult who cannot earn enough to live on, that arrangement was never a real option, and therefore we would provide for her. But if she is a minor who can earn, we would say perhaps such an arrangement is operative here. These are the {\i ikei beinayhu}, the practical differences between the two explanations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna & Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admon & Priests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the text of the mishna itself. {\i Mi shehalach limdinat hayam v\u8217'ishto tova\u8217'at mezonot} \u8212- a man goes overseas and his wife demands support. {\i Chanan omer tishava basof} \u8212- Chanan says she takes an oath at the end, not at the outset. The {\i bnei kohanim gedolim} disagree and say she must swear both at the beginning and at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately clarifies: {\i ad kan lo pligi ela l\u8217'inyan shevu\u8217'ah} \u8212- their dispute is only about the oath. But as for actually giving her {\i mezonot}, all agree that she receives them \u8212- {\i aval mezonei yahvinan lah}. This directly contradicts Shmuel, who holds that we do not provide {\i mezonot} when the husband is merely absent. The Gemara therefore reports that Shmuel interpreted the mishna \u8212- {\i targama Shmuel b\u8217'sheshamu bo shemet} \u8212- as referring to a case where the husband has been reported dead, and the woman is being supported as a presumed widow. Of course, this reading is strained: if the man is dead, why does the mishna introduce him as someone who went overseas? It should simply say {\i mi shemet}. Nevertheless, this is Shmuel\u8217's {\i tirutz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 He Set Aside Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is cited that appears to expand on the mishna. The husband goes overseas, the wife demands support, and the {\i bnei kohanim gedolim} say she must take an oath while Chanan says she need not. Then: {\i v\u8217'im ba v\u8217'amar pasakti lah mezonot ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- if the husband returns and says he had already made arrangements for her support, he is believed. The question is when this claim is being made: if he has returned and made the claim, why did the matter need to go to {\i beit din} in the first place? It seems the scenario is that {\i beit din} had already acted \u8212- selling off property to feed her \u8212- and only then did the husband return and protest that he had already made arrangements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi & Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that the husband is saying he had set aside money for her \u8212- {\i af tafsa tzerori} \u8212- a box of funds she could have drawn from. He is believed, and she must return what {\i beit din} disbursed \u8212- {\i umachzeret mah shenatna lah beis din}. But why is he simply believed if she currently holds the money? Rashi resolves this by saying he must take a {\i shvua} in support of his claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's Ri offers a different reading: {\i ne\u8217'eman l\u8217'hatrichah shvuah} \u8212- he is believed to the extent that she must now swear to him. That is, rather than forcing her to return the money, we impose an oath on her: she swears that he did not in fact make the arrangement he claims. She does not automatically pay back the money, but she must now substantiate her position under oath. These two readings leave the practical mechanics of the case somewhat unclear, but both agree that the original seizure of property \u8212- before any such claim was made \u8212- was legitimate, and this is precisely the kind of scenario where Shmuel has to intervene and explain things away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumors Exaggerated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds: {\i hacha nami b\u8217'sheshamu bo shemet} \u8212- this too, says Shmuel, is the case where the husband had been reported dead. But then how could he come back? {\i Im ba achar shemuah} \u8212- like Mark Twain, the rumors of his death were greatly exaggerated. We had heard that he died, we treated his wife as a widow and supported her from the estate, and it turned out he was alive all along. According to Shmuel, this actually makes the ending of the {\i braita} more intelligible: since Shmuel holds that one should never sell off the property of a living husband, the fact that we did so and then he returned to protest makes perfect sense \u8212- we shouldn\u8217't have done it at all, and now that he\u8217's back and saying he had set aside money, we\u8217'll believe him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} makes the same point. The husband is overseas, the wife demands support, and the text says: {\i im ba v\u8217'amar tzei ma\u8217'aseh yadayich bimzonotayich} \u8212- if he comes and says she should have provided for herself from her own labor, he is permitted to say so. Again, Rashi notes that if {\i beit din} had already acted and sold property \u8212- {\i ma shepasqu pasqu} \u8212- that stands. And again, this {\i braita} explicitly acknowledges the two scenarios Shmuel uses to explain his position: the possibility that money was set aside ({\i pasakti lah mezonot}) and the possibility that a {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} arrangement was made ({\i tzei ma\u8217'aseh yadayich bimzonotayich}). From Shmuel\u8217's perspective, the {\i braisos} themselves show these are live possibilities, which is precisely why we should not assume they are absent and proceed to seize property. Yet the simple reading of all these {\i braisos} is that {\i beit din} does provide for the wife as a matter of course, and deals with any such claims by the husband only if and when he comes forward with them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Children Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Not Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third {\i braita} is cited and proves to be the most powerful challenge to Shmuel. {\i Beit din yordin lenichsav vezonin umfarnisin l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- the court descends to the husband\u8217's property and provides for his wife \u8212- {\i aval lo banav uvenosav} \u8212- but not for his sons and daughters, and not for anything else. This is a decisive proof against Shmuel\u8217's reading that all these cases presume the husband to be dead. If we were presuming the husband dead, then his sons would inherit and his daughters would be supported as {\i yetomot} from the estate. The very fact that the sons and daughters are excluded demonstrates that we are presupposing the husband is {\i alive} \u8212- we are simply exacting his existing legal obligation to feed his wife. Sons and daughters are excluded because a father has no legal obligation to feed his children beyond a very young age; his wife is a different matter entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trustee Appointed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet responds: {\i b\u8217'mashlish et ishto bidei shalish} \u8212- this is the case where the husband had appointed a trustee to manage disbursements to his wife. That trustee has now either left for overseas himself or the funds in his care have been exhausted. So we are not acting in ignorance of what arrangement the husband made \u8212- we know precisely what arrangement he made, and we know it has lapsed. That is why we now descend to his property: the previously-known arrangement is gone, and the doubt Shmuel raises about possible alternative arrangements no longer applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: if so, why not also provide for the sons and daughters? Presumably if the husband was organized enough to appoint a trustee, he cared about his whole household. The Gemara answers: {\i kesheshlish lezu v\u8217'lo hishlish lezu} \u8212- he made the arrangement specifically for his wife, not for his sons and daughters. His legal obligation ran to his wife; it is entirely reasonable that he funded his legal obligation and not beyond. The Gemara then asks {\i mai piska} \u8212- what is the logical basis for splitting? Why assume he provided for one and not the other? Tosafot finds this question puzzling, noting that the distinction between legal obligation and voluntary generosity is obvious. Perhaps the Gemara is suggesting that if he cared enough to appoint a trustee at all, he would not have abandoned his family. In any case, this response narrows the {\i braita} considerably: it now addresses only a specific, unusual scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa offers an alternative explanation of the {\i braita}. {\i Kesheshama bo shemet b\u8217'ed echad} \u8212- we heard that the husband died through the testimony of a single witness. This creative solution draws on the well-known principle from Yevamot: a woman is permitted to remarry on the basis of {\i ed echad} testifying to her husband\u8217's death, because she is trusted to take such a consequential step only when she is genuinely certain. The practical difference between wife and children follows directly. The wife, because of the special dispensation of {\i ed echad} regarding remarriage, is treated as if she can presume her husband dead. Since even when her husband is alive she would be entitled to support, and since {\i ed echad} allows her to treat him as dead, we provide {\i mezonot} for her. Her sons and daughters, however, have no comparable dispensation: {\i ed echad} does not allow them to treat their father as dead for purposes of inheritance. From their perspective, the father is still alive, and therefore they may not descend to his property. The result is a peculiar situation: from the wife\u8217's standpoint her husband is dead, from the children\u8217's standpoint their father is alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this is entirely coherent \u8212- in Yevamot, the reliability of {\i ed echad} was predicated on the wife\u8217's willingness to expose herself to severe consequences if she is wrong. Here she is only seeking {\i mezonot}, not remarriage; the stakes are lower. This is a fair question, and Tosafot addresses under what circumstances {\i ed echad} would suffice for various monetary claims related to the husband\u8217's presumed death. But the Gemara here uses this mechanism to preserve Shmuel\u8217's position: even this powerful {\i braita}, with its explicit exclusion of sons and daughters, can be explained without conceding that we support a wife whose husband is simply absent and alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara also addresses {\i mai davar acher} \u8212- the other things the {\i braita} excludes. Rav Chisda says we do not provide {\i tachshit} \u8212- jewelry or, as Rashi notes, perfume \u8212- from the estate. Rav Yosef says we do not draw from the estate for {\i tzedakah} contributions. The one who excludes {\i tachshit} would certainly exclude {\i tzedakah}, since that is not even for the wife\u8217's benefit. The one who excludes {\i tzedakah} would still provide {\i tachshit}, on the reasoning that {\i lo nicha lei detitgana v\u8217'detinval} \u8212- the husband would not want his wife going about in a degraded state simply because he is away. Some basic expenditures on her appearance and wellbeing are still covered.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sister-in-Law & Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevamah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites another {\i braita}: the {\i yevamah} \u8212- a sister-in-law awaiting {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- is supported from her deceased husband\u8217's estate for the first three months. During this period she cannot yet perform {\i yibbum} because she may be pregnant. {\i Mikan ve\u8217'eylach} \u8212- thereafter, she is supported neither by her dead husband\u8217's estate nor by the {\i yavam}\u8217's. This leaves her in a difficult middle state: the obligation to perform {\i yibbum} is still pending, but as long as it is pending she is no longer simply a widow drawing on her late husband\u8217's estate, and since the marriage to the {\i yavam} has not occurred, she is not yet his responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i amda badin u-varach} \u8212- she brought him to {\i beit din} to compel him to act, and he fled. In this case, he is responsible to support her. The Gemara asks whether this applies specifically because he ran away \u8212- evading his legal responsibilities \u8212- or whether it applies more broadly whenever {\i beit din} has formally called upon him to act and his inaction, whether culpable or not, traps her in this state of {\i agunah}. In any case, once {\i beit din} has summoned him and he is not fulfilling his obligation, he must provide for her even in his absence. The Gemara asks: is this not exactly the same as a husband being absent overseas and {\i beit din} providing for his wife? Shmuel responds: {\i lemai neichush lah} \u8212- what are we really worried about here? The two concerns that Shmuel raises in the main case do not apply here. {\i Ai mishum tzerorei} \u8212- maybe he set aside money? {\i Lo mikrav daiteih legabah} \u8212- he has no closeness to her; they are not yet married, he has only this pending {\i mitzva} of {\i yibbum} and moreover he ran away from it, so obviously he would not have prepared a fund for her. {\i Ai mishum ma\u8217'aseh yadeha} \u8212- maybe he told her to work for herself? {\i Lo mishtabda lei} \u8212- she is not obligated to give her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} to him; in this middle state before {\i yibbum} is performed, her labor does not belong to him, though his obligation to support her exists. Since neither concern applies, we provide for her, but this tells us nothing about the case where those concerns are live.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Claiming Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i braita}: {\i ha\u8217'isha shehalcha hi uva\u8217'alah limdinat hayam} \u8212- she and her husband went overseas together, and she returned saying {\i met ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- my husband died. She is believed. She may either be supported from the estate as an {\i almana} or collect her {\i ketuba} in a lump sum. But: {\i im amra geirashani ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- if instead she says he divorced her, a different rule applies. {\i Mitparneset v\u8217'holekhet ad kedei kesuvatah} \u8212- she is supported incrementally until the total value of her {\i ketuba} is reached. This is because a woman is believed when she claims her husband died but not when she claims he divorced her. Since we do not fully accept her claim of divorce, we cannot give her the {\i ketuba} as a lump sum \u8212- perhaps she is still married. But {\i mi\u8217'man nafshach} \u8212- either way there is a liability: if still married, he owes her {\i mezonot}; if divorced, she has a {\i ketuba} claim. So we pay out {\i mezonot} until it reaches the value of the {\i ketuba} and then stop. The {\i chiddush} is that by saying {\i geirashani} rather than {\i met}, she has actually harmed herself: had she said nothing, we would have continued supporting her indefinitely as a presumed widow. By introducing the claim of divorce, she capped her own entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises this {\i braita} as evidence that we support a woman from the estate even though the husband\u8217's status is uncertain \u8212- precisely what Rav holds. Shmuel, predictably, replies {\i hacha nami deshamu bo shemet} \u8212- here too, it is a case where the husband had been reported dead.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor Refused\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mi\u8217'un Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a further {\i braita} on the theme of {\i mi\u8217'un}. {\i Keitzad amru mima\u8217'enet ein lah mezonot} \u8212- in what case is a girl who refuses ({\i mima\u8217'enet}) her minor marriage left without {\i mezonot}? It cannot be while she is still living together with the husband under normal marital arrangements, because {\i ba\u8217'alah chayav bimzonoteha} \u8212- as long as they are together, the husband is obligated to support her. The case must therefore be: {\i ella k\u8217'gon shehalach limdinat hayam} \u8212- the husband has gone overseas. {\i Lavta v\u8217'achalah} \u8212- she borrowed money and used it to feed herself. {\i V\u8217'amda u\u8217'mi\u8217'anah} \u8212- and then she performed {\i mi\u8217'un}, retroactively dissolving the marriage as though it had never existed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} is not merely that from this point forward there is no marriage \u8212- that is obvious. The {\i chiddush} is the retroactive effect: since {\i mi\u8217'un} unwinds the marriage entirely, she is not in a position to collect from her ex-husband the borrowed money she had spent on {\i mezonot}, because retroactively she was never truly married and he never had that obligation. Importantly, however, the {\i braita} implies that {\i had she not done mi\u8217'un}, she would have been entitled to recover that borrowed money from the estate. The husband, even while overseas, had an obligation to support her, and her borrowed expenditures during that period would have been collectible. {\i Ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'mi\u8217'anah} \u8212- it is specifically because she did {\i mi\u8217'un} that she loses the claim; {\i ha lo mi\u8217'anah, yehavinan lah} \u8212- had she not done {\i mi\u8217'un}, we would have provided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is again strong evidence for Rav\u8217's position. Shmuel responds: {\i amar lach Shmuel hacha b\u8217'mai askinan} \u8212- the case here is a special one. {\i Ei mishum tzerorei, l\u8217'ktana lo masar} \u8212- the concern that the husband set aside money does not apply to a minor wife; one does not entrust funds to a minor. {\i V\u8217'ei mishum ma\u8217'aseh yadeha, l\u8217'ktana lo safka} \u8212- and the concern that she would earn her own keep does not apply as a general rule to a minor; minors typically cannot support themselves. So in this case, uniquely, both of Shmuel\u8217's concerns are absent, and therefore even Shmuel agrees that she should be supported \u8212- but this is a narrow exception that tells us nothing about adult wives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises about how a minor could have borrowed money in the first place. The Gemara presupposes transactions that minors can engage in, either under the rabbinic category of {\i pe\u8217'utot} \u8212- transactions by children of sufficient understanding \u8212- or perhaps the borrowing was done by the mother on her behalf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Dimi\u8217's Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After exhausting the textual evidence, the Gemara turns to recorded practice. {\i Ki ata Rav Dimi} \u8212- when Rav Dimi came from Eretz Yisrael, he reported: {\i ma\u8217'aseh ba lifnei Rabbi b\u8217'Beit She\u8217'arim} \u8212- a case came before {\i Rabbi} (Rebbi Yehuda HaNasi) in Beit She\u8217'arim, and he {\i pasak lah mezonot} \u8212- ruled to provide {\i mezonot} for the wife whose husband had gone overseas. {\i Lifnei Rabbi Yishmael b\u8217'Tzippori, v\u8217'lo pasak lah mezonot} \u8212- a similar case came before Rabbi Yishmael b\u8217'Rabbi Yose in Tzippori and he refused to provide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tamah Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan was astonished, and asked: {\i v\u8217'chi mah ra\u8217'ah Rabbi Yishmael shelo pasak lah mezonot?} What could Rabbi Yishmael have seen to refuse support to this woman? After all, {\i halo nechelku Bnei Kohanim Gedolim v\u8217'Chanan ela l\u8217'inyan shvuah} \u8212- the only dispute in our mishna between Chanan and the {\i bnei kohanim gedolim} concerns the oath. {\i Aval mezonei yahvinan lah} \u8212- everyone agrees that {\i mezonot} is provided. How could Rabbi Yishmael simply refuse?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shemen bar Abba replied: {\i kvar tirgemah Rabbeinu Shmuel b\u8217'Bavel} \u8212- our master Shmuel already dealt with this mishna in Babylonia. His interpretation \u8212- {\i ksheshamu bo shemet} \u8212- explains the mishna as applying only to the case where the husband is presumed dead. Rabbi Yishmael presumably followed Shmuel\u8217's position and therefore refused to provide when the husband was merely absent. Rabbi Yochanan responded: {\i pastrisu bekulei hai?} \u8212- you are going to such lengths, twisting the plain meaning of the mishna by that much? Clearly that is not the {\i pshat}, and to do that to a woman who needs to eat is not a defensible {\i tirutz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravin\u8217's Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki ata Ravin} \u8212- when Ravin came from Eretz Yisrael, he reported the same story in reverse: the case came before {\i Rabbi} in Beit She\u8217'arim and {\i he} refused to provide, while the case before Rabbi Yishmael in Tzippori resulted in an order to provide. The same exchange between Rabbi Yochanan and Rav Shemen bar Abba follows verbatim. The two reports from Eretz Yisrael \u8212- Rav Dimi\u8217's and Ravin\u8217's \u8212- simply contradict each other on who ruled which way, and the Gemara makes no effort to reconcile them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'hilchesa kevasei d\u8217'Rav} \u8212- the {\i halakha} follows Rav. We provide {\i mezonot} for a married woman whose husband is away. The entire sustained effort by Shmuel to explain away every {\i braita} and every recorded practice ultimately fails to persuade, and the final ruling goes with the straightforward reading of the mishna and all the supporting {\i braisos}: {\i poskin mezonot l\u8217'eshet ish}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Rulings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezonot Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara summarizes three {\i hilchesos} as the masekhta draws to its close. The first follows directly from the foregoing discussion: {\i u-paskinan mezonot l\u8217'eshet ish} \u8212- we rule that {\i beit din} provides {\i mezonot} for a wife whose husband has gone overseas.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second ruling: {\i v\u8217'hilchesa kevasei d\u8217'Rav Huna} \u8212- we rule in accordance with Rav Huna citing Rav, who said: {\i yechola isha she-tomar l\u8217'ba\u8217'alah eini nizonet v\u8217'eini osa} \u8212- a woman may unilaterally declare to her husband that she does not wish to receive {\i mezonot} from him and will not turn over her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} to him. The exchange of {\i mezonot} for labor was established to protect {\i her} interests, not his. She is therefore entitled to waive {\i mezonot} and retain her own earnings. This is not a decision reserved to the husband \u8212- she may invoke it herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kunia Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third ruling: {\i v\u8217'hilchesa kevasei d\u8217'Rav Zvid b\u8217'kunya} \u8212- we rule in accordance with Rav Zvid regarding {\i kunya}, which are glazed pottery vessels. The topic is included here apparently because the Gemara favors grouping {\i hilchesos} in threes. Rashi understands the glaze to be a metallic coating; Tosafot understands it as glass glaze.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zvid rules: {\i hanei mane d\u8217'kunya chivarei v\u8217'uchmei sharei} \u8212- white and black {\i kunya} vessels are permitted. {\i Yerokei, asirei} \u8212- green ones are forbidden. Rashi identifies the problematic substance in the green glaze as {\i alum}, a specific mineral compound (not the modern aluminum) that causes the glaze to absorb and then retain absorbed taste so that it cannot be extracted. The context is most likely wine of non-Jews ({\i stam yeinam}) stored cold \u8212- the question being whether the pottery absorbs enough of the wine\u8217's flavor that the taste cannot be expelled through normal {\i kashering}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even permissible white or black vessels must meet an additional condition: {\i ella d\u8217'reish bei kartufi} \u8212- there must be no ridges, cracks, or indentations. {\i Aval it beih kartufei, assur} \u8212- if there are such imperfections, the taste enters the crevices and cannot be expelled, rendering the vessel forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, proceeding from the premise that the glaze is glass, adds an important observation. {\i Aval b\u8217'hataxas zechuchis nicha d\u8217'afilu konyah shel zechuchis einu ne\u8217'asar} \u8212- in principle, genuinely glass-glazed vessels would not absorb, {\i lefi shenisbashel heiteiv v\u8217'eino nivla} \u8212- because well-processed pure glass is non-porous. The problem with {\i kunya} is that the glass in question was not fully processed and contained metallic impurities or alloys that caused it to absorb. This sets up a broader question that Tosafot engages implicitly: does the non-porous character of true glass extend to hot liquids as well as cold, and what is the general {\i halakhic} status of glass vessels? These questions carry through into the wider {\i halakhic} literature on {\i kashering} glass, where there is significant debate about whether glass is entirely non-absorbent under all circumstances or only under specific conditions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 108\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chanan: Provisions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third-party provisions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second ruling of Chanan \u8212- like the first \u8212- arises in the context of a husband who has traveled overseas and left no provisions for his wife. In the previous {\i mishnah}, the discussion centered on whether the wife must take a {\i shevuah} at the outset of receiving support from her husband\u8217's estate or only at the conclusion, with Chanan holding she swears only at the end. The present ruling addresses a related but distinct scenario: while the husband is abroad, a third party \u8212- perhaps a storekeeper, or simply a compassionate neighbor \u8212- notices the wife\u8217's predicament and, out of his own initiative and expense, provides her with food for the entire duration of her husband\u8217's absence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No reimbursement owed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the husband returns, can this benefactor demand reimbursement? Chanan says: {\i ibad et me\u8217'otav} \u8212- he wasted his money. No contractual arrangement was ever made, no loan was extended, and therefore the husband owes him nothing. This is a striking ruling, given that the husband\u8217's obligation to feed his wife was entirely real, and the third party effectively discharged it. The obvious question is whether the objective reality of the benefit \u8212- that the wife was sustained \u8212- itself generates a legal debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 BKG: oath required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Bnei Kohanim Gedolim} disagree with Chanan, consistent with their stance in the previous {\i mishnah}. True to their characteristic approach, they require an oath: {\i yishava kama hotzi ve-yitol} \u8212- the third party takes a {\i shevuah} attesting to his expenditures and is then entitled to reimbursement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dosa & Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The positions align precisely as they did in the prior dispute. Rabbi Dosa ben Harkinas sides with the {\i Bnei Kohanim Gedolim}, while Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai endorses Chanan: {\i yafeh amar Chanan} \u8212- Chanan has spoken well. He adds the evocative phrase: {\i hinniach me\u8217'otav al keren hatzvi} \u8212- he placed his money on the horns of a gazelle, the Talmudic idiom for money irretrievably lost.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Michael\u8217's question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pivotal question framing this entire discussion is: does an unsolicited act of benefit ever, by itself, create a legal obligation? Even absent any borrowing or hiring, is there a circumstance in which the objective improvement of my position gives you a cognizable claim against me?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lawn/field examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two illustrative scenarios clarify the range of possibilities. In one, a person is away and a neighbor mows his lawn. In the Talmudic idiom, the equivalent is someone planting another\u8217's field without permission. The question of whether this creates an obligation turns on whether we can imply a quasi-contractual arrangement \u8212- specifically, whether this is a {\i sadeh ha-asuya lita}, a field of the type that is typically planted, such that a reasonable owner would obviously desire the work done. Even absent explicit consent, the presumed desire to have the work done might approximate a constructive hire.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property vs.\u160 ?obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even where there is no presumed consent, however, there is a second possible basis for liability: the actual, tangible increase in property value. If you fattened my cattle, I now possess fatter cattle \u8212- something of yours, as it were, is embedded in my property, and I am obligated to compensate you for it. This principle can be conceptualized as: where your effort has materially enhanced the value of my assets, you have in some sense transferred something of yours into my estate, and I must return it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The crucial distinction in the case before us is that feeding the husband\u8217's wife does not increase the husband\u8217's property value. His estate has not grown; he has no more assets at the end than at the beginning. What occurred was the discharge of an obligation, not an enhancement of his holdings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lion analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an analogy that sharpens the analysis considerably. The Gemara elsewhere discusses {\i mavriach ari mi-nichsei chaveiro} \u8212- one who scares off a lion that is about to attack his neighbor\u8217's flock, incurring personal expense in the process. Here too there is no increase in property value \u8212- the owner ends up with exactly the sheep he had before. Yet the Gemara is clear that reimbursement is required. This appears parallel to our case: just as the lion-chaser prevented harm to the owner\u8217's assets, so too did the third party prevent the wife from seizing the husband\u8217's estate \u8212- a genuine benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Policy difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's resolution is grounded in public policy. In the case of the lion, we have an affirmative societal interest in encouraging individuals to come to one another\u8217's aid in emergencies, and it is therefore a {\i takanat chachamim} \u8212- a rabbinic enactment \u8212- that such a person be reimbursed, so that people will not hesitate to act. No parallel policy concern exists in our case. The wife already has a readily available remedy: she can simply appear before {\i Beit Din} and the court will authorize her to take food from her husband\u8217's property. There is no need to encourage third parties to pay off the husband\u8217's debt to his wife when she has a direct avenue of relief. The situation becomes even clearer according to Chanan\u8217's view in the previous {\i mishnah} \u8212- that the wife\u8217's access to the estate is uncomplicated and requires no oath at the outset \u8212- making any third-party intervention doubly unnecessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's conclusion is thus two-pronged. First, no direct benefit passed from the third party to the husband: the money went to the wife (effectively, the husband\u8217's creditor), and only indirectly and consequentially did the husband benefit from the removal of her claim. Second, there was no increase in the value of the husband\u8217's property. Both factors together \u8212- the indirect nature of the benefit ({\i grama be-alma}) and the absence of any {\i shevach nechasim} \u8212- explain why Chanan says the third party has no claim. The relevant lines from Tosafot make this explicit: {\i hatam ha-shevach b\u8217'ayin hu ve-din hu she-yitol} \u8212- in cases of actual property improvement, the enhanced value is tangibly present and reimbursement is appropriate \u8212- {\i aval hacha lo hishbi\u8217'ach lo shum shevach nechasim, ella she-hetzilo mi-geviyat ba\u8217'al chov, ve-lo midei yahiv lei, ve-havei ke-mavriach ari mi-nichsei chaveiro, ve-af al gav de-mithanno lo mitchayev} \u8212- but here no property value was enhanced; the third party merely protected the husband from his creditor\u8217's collection, gave him nothing tangible, and is therefore analogous to one who scared off a lion, who despite the benefit conferred creates no legal obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to the public policy point appears explicitly later in Tosafot: {\i takanat chachamim hatam she-yeshalmu lo kedei she-yashiv aveidato shel chaveiro} \u8212- the enactment requiring reimbursement in the lion case and in the case of returning lost objects exists to incentivize socially beneficial behavior. {\i Aval poreya chovo\u8230 ? lamah lanu la\u8217'asot takanah zo} \u8212- but for paying off another\u8217's debt, or for providing another\u8217's wife with food, why institute such a policy? Let the wife exercise her own legal rights. The husband is not a lion\u8217's prey.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nedarim vow cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-shekel case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara in {\i Nedarim} introduces a related set of cases involving {\i hanaah} \u8212- benefit \u8212- in a different but illuminating context. The scenario: one person is under a vow ({\i mudar hanaah}) prohibiting him from deriving benefit from his friend. Yet the friend wishes to assist him. What may the friend do?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three actions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} in {\i Nedarim} enumerates three actions the friend may perform without violating the vow: he may pay the person\u8217's {\i shekel} (the annual half-shekel contribution for the {\i korbanot}); he may pay off the person\u8217's debt to a creditor; and he may return the person\u8217's lost object. Each of these, despite conferring what would intuitively seem to be real benefit, is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The return of the lost object is perhaps the least surprising of the three. After all, the item already belongs to the person \u8212- he is merely receiving back his own property. Moreover, the one returning it is fulfilling a {\i mitzvah}, and {\i mitzvot lav le-hanot nitnu} \u8212- the commandments are not given for the purpose of benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost object payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, a complication arises: in places where it is customary to compensate the finder for his efforts in returning a lost object ({\i sechar batelah}), a new difficulty emerges. If the person under the vow is obligated to pay for the return, but the friend has also taken a vow against receiving benefit from him, then accepting payment would violate the friend\u8217's vow. Conversely, if the friend simply waives the payment \u8212- says \u8220"forget it, keep your money\u8221" \u8212- he has forgiven a debt the person owes him, which is itself a form of {\i hanaah} flowing to the person from the friend, in violation of the original vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's solution is elegant: {\i tatil hanaah le-hekdesh} \u8212- the payment is dedicated to {\i hekdesh}. The person does not keep the money, the friend does not receive it, and neither party benefits from the other in a forbidden way. It is a well-established approach to such entanglements: when neither party may receive the value at stake, the funds are channeled to {\i hekdesh} as a neutral resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi/Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i shekel} payment, Rashi and Tosafot offer divergent explanations for why paying another\u8217's half-{\i shekel} does not constitute {\i hanaah}. The {\i mishnah} in {\i Shekalim} teaches {\i tormin al ha-avud, ve-al ha-gavuy, ve-al ha-atid ligbot} \u8212- the {\i terumah} taken from the {\i shekalim} chamber is brought on behalf even of those whose money was lost in transit, those whose money has been received but not yet brought to the {\i Beit Hamikdash}, and even those who have not yet paid at all. The {\i korbanot} purchased from the collected {\i shekalim} are thus brought on behalf of the entire community, even those who have yet to fulfill their obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi understands this broadly: since the {\i korbanot} are brought on behalf of everyone, including those who may never ultimately pay, the {\i shekel} obligation carries no real monetary consequence \u8212- the person would receive his share of the {\i korbanot} regardless. Paying his {\i shekel} therefore merely assists him in fulfilling a {\i mitzvah} obligation; it does not relieve him of any actual monetary debt. Under this reading, the {\i shekel} case is the weakest of the three in terms of {\i hanaah}, since the person was not truly obligated in any enforceable monetary sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot rejects this. In Tosafot\u8217's view, one who does not pay his {\i shekel} does genuinely forfeit his share in the {\i korbanot} \u8212- the text {\i al ha-atid ligbot} refers only to those who will eventually pay, not to permanent non-payers. Paying another\u8217's {\i shekel} therefore does discharge a real obligation and constitutes real benefit. To rescue the ruling that the friend may pay it, Tosafot narrows the case: the {\i mishnah} in {\i Nedarim} is describing a situation where the person has technically already fulfilled his obligation (for example, his money was sent but lost in transit before reaching Jerusalem, so he is halachically exempt), and the friend is merely paying a second time for his peace of mind. This is admittedly a {\i dachuk} reading of a case that the {\i mishnah} presents as standard. As Rashi would understand it, the straightforward reading is simply that the payment of a {\i shekel} for another person, under any normal circumstances, is permissible \u8212- because it only assists in performing a {\i mitzvah} and does not represent a transfer of substantial monetary benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ultimately, both Rashi and Tosafot agree that {\i hanaah} in this context refers to monetary benefit, not emotional satisfaction. They disagree on why the {\i shekel} case falls outside that category \u8212- whether because the obligation was illusory all along (Rashi) or because the particular case involves an already-exempt individual (Tosafot).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Debt repayment grama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oshaya attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the most conceptually significant of the three permitted actions: paying off another\u8217's debt. The Gemara asks: surely this is {\i kamesh-taresh lei} \u8212- a genuine and deeply rooted benefit? The person\u8217's net worth has increased; he is freed from a liability that burdened him. How can this not be {\i hanaah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Oshaya resolves this: {\i ha mani Chanan hi} \u8212- this ruling reflects Chanan\u8217's position. Just as Chanan holds that discharging the husband\u8217's obligation to his wife creates no actionable debt in the husband\u8217's favor for the one who paid, so too here: paying off another\u8217's debt is not considered {\i hanaah} in the legally cognizable sense. The two rulings share the same conceptual foundation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct/indirect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot articulates the underlying principle in terms of the direct/indirect distinction. When I pay Michael\u8217's creditor, the money flows from me to the creditor \u8212- not from me to Michael. Michael\u8217's benefit is a consequence, a {\i grama be-alma}, an indirect downstream effect of my transaction with his creditor. He himself received nothing directly from me. Compare this to mowing Michael\u8217's lawn: in that case, the improvement flows directly from me to him, from my effort to his property. Even if I could not legally compel him to pay me for the lawn mowing, he has still received something {\i directly} from me. That is the line Tosafot draws: {\i de-hatam mi-didi le-didei ka-mitahni} \u8212- there, from me directly to him he derived benefit \u8212- whereas in the debt case, the money went to the creditor, and only consequently did Michael benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Storekeeper analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case reinforces this framework. The Gemara in {\i Nedarim} elsewhere discusses a scenario where a person under a vow of {\i hanaah} is starving: {\i yelech eitzel chanvani ha-ragil etzlo} \u8212- the friend goes to a storekeeper familiar with the person and arranges for food to be provided. Even though the friend\u8217's action ultimately results in Michael receiving food \u8212- concrete, measurable benefit \u8212- it is permitted, because the friend acted only as a {\i grama be-alma}, indirectly. The money went from the friend to the storekeeper; the storekeeper then gave food to Michael. At no point did anything pass directly from the friend to Michael.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot draws the explicit contrast: {\i de-lo avid ela grama be-alma\u8230 ? ve-dela dami le-oseh lo melachah} \u8212- because the friend did not act directly upon Michael, it is unlike performing labor for him directly. Performing labor directly for someone, even if you could not compel payment, still constitutes direct benefit and would be prohibited under the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Tosafot notes an important nuance in the storekeeper case: unlike the debt-repayment case, Michael actually did receive something of tangible, increased value \u8212- food that he now has. The debt case is arguably stronger because there, there is not even an increase in property value, only the removal of a liability. Together, then, the two factors that insulate these actions from the category of {\i hanaah} are: (1) the indirect, {\i grama} character of the benefit, and (2) in the debt case specifically, the absence of any actual increase in the person\u8217's property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a challenge to Rav Oshaya\u8217's attribution of the debt-repayment rule to Chanan. Rava says: {\i afilu teima Rabbanan} \u8212- one could even reconcile the ruling with the majority position of the {\i Bnei Kohanim Gedolim}, without invoking Chanan at all. The explanation would be that the debt in question was borrowed {\i al menat she-lo lifroa} \u8212- on condition that it never had to be repaid, meaning the creditor could never bring the debtor to court to collect. Such a debt, though technically outstanding, does not truly burden the debtor\u8217's estate in a practical sense \u8212- it is a nominal obligation at best. Paying it off, therefore, barely qualifies as meaningful benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks why Rav Oshaya preferred the Chanan attribution rather than Rava\u8217's narrower reading. The answer: {\i nehi de-hana layt lei, kissufa mi layt lei?} \u8212- even if there is no monetary benefit from the debt payment (since the creditor could never sue), is there no benefit from the removal of embarrassment? Running into one\u8217's creditor and being reminded of an unpaid debt \u8212- even one that is legally unenforceable \u8212- is a source of genuine shame. Paying off that debt, under Rava\u8217's reading, would still confer real benefit by eliminating that embarrassment. Therefore, Rav Oshaya concluded that Rava\u8217's construction is untenable, and the only way to permit the debt repayment is through Chanan\u8217's broader conceptual framework of {\i grama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also confirms that Chanan\u8217's approach carries significant practical weight, notwithstanding that the {\i Bnei Kohanim Gedolim} constitute the majority. The logic of {\i grama} \u8212- that indirect benefit, however substantial in practical terms, does not create direct legal liability \u8212- is the animating principle behind multiple rulings, and it is Chanan who gives that principle its clearest expression.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Admon estate case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters/sons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now transitions to the seven rulings of Admon. The first concerns the laws of inheritance in an estate too small to satisfy all claims. The general rule: when a man dies leaving both sons and daughters, in a large estate {\i ha-banim yirashu ve-ha-banot yizonu} \u8212- the sons inherit and the daughters are provided with food and maintenance from the estate. In a small estate, however, where there is not enough to both provide for the daughters\u8217' maintenance and leave a remainder for the sons to inherit, {\i ha-banot yizonu ve-ha-banim yechaizru al ha-pesachim} \u8212- the daughters are fed and the sons must go begging.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admon objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Admon objects: {\i bishvil she-ani zachar hifsadeti}? Just because I am male I lose out? The implication is that the differential treatment is unjust \u8212- males are systematically privileged in inheritance law, yet when the estate is small, they bear the full loss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye misreading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara struggles to identify the precise nature of Admon\u8217's complaint. Abaye offers one reading: {\i bishvil she-ani zachar ve-ra\u8217'ui la-asok ba-Torah hifsadeti} \u8212- because I am male and therefore the one who engages in Torah study, I have lost out? The suggestion seems to be that those who occupy themselves with the more important religious activity in society ought to be prioritized in financial matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava firmly rejects this: {\i man de-asik be-Torah hu de-yaris, man de-lo asik be-Torah lo yaris?} Would one who learns Torah inherit while one who does not, does not? Monetary rights and religious significance are entirely separate domains. An argument grounded in religious importance is irrelevant to questions of property entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore reformulates Admon\u8217's complaint: {\i bishvil she-ani zachar ve-ra\u8217'ui liyarash bi-nechasim merubim hifsadeti bi-nechasim muatim?} \u8212- because I am a male and therefore the primary heir in a large estate, I should now, when the estate is small, be disadvantaged relative to the daughters? There is an internal inconsistency in a legal system that privileges males in large estates but places the entire burden of a small estate on them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel endorses Admon\u8217's position: {\i ro\u8217'eh ani et divrei Admon} \u8212- I see the logic of Admon\u8217's argument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot reinterpret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara elsewhere rules that whenever Rabban Gamliel says {\i ro\u8217'eh ani et divrei Admon}, we rule accordingly. Yet Tosafot notes that other {\i sugyot} consistently maintain that in a small estate, the daughters are fed before the sons \u8212- a rule reaffirmed by Rav Assi in the name of Rabbi Yochanan with no hint of dissent. Rabbeinu Tam resolves the tension by reinterpreting the nature of the dispute: {\i Admon lav peligi a-milsa de-chachamim avdu} \u8212- Admon is not actually arguing against the established {\i halacha}. He concedes that the law gives priority to daughters in a small estate. Rather, {\i ella misma be-alma} \u8212- he is simply voicing outrage. His statement is a rhetorical expression of indignation, not a formal halachic challenge. And when Rabban Gamliel says {\i ro\u8217'eh ani et divrei Admon}, he too is not issuing a {\i psak din} \u8212- he is affirming: {\i mishum she-yesh bo kedei litmo\u8217'ah} \u8212- yes, I share his sense that this is astonishing. The established rule stands; Admon\u8217's statement registers a moral protest, not a legal position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Jugs of oil case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jugs claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Admon\u8217's second ruling involves an application of {\i modeh be-miktzat} \u8212- partial admission. The case: Reuven claims that Shimon owes him jugs of oil. Shimon concedes that he owes the jugs but denies owing the oil. Can this constitute the partial admission ({\i hoda\u8217'at miktzat ha-ta\u8217'anah}) that triggers an obligation to take a {\i shevuah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admon: oath required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Admon holds that it does: {\i hu hodeh be-miktzat ha-ta\u8217'anah, yishava} \u8212- he has admitted to part of the claim, and therefore he must take an oath. The jugs are included in the claim of \u8220"jugs of oil,\u8221" and conceding them while denying the oil is a classic partial admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chakhamim: no oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chakhamim} disagree: {\i ein hoda\u8217'at miktzat mi-min ha-ta\u8217'anah} \u8212- this is not an admission of part of the actual claim. What was being demanded was the oil; the jugs were merely incidental. One who concedes something that was not truly the subject of the claim has not made a meaningful partial admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?one claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara initially attempts to locate the dispute within the broader question of {\i ta\u8217'ano chitin u-se\u8217'orin ve-hodeh be-se\u8217'orin} \u8212- if one claimed wheat and barley and the defendant conceded only the barley, is that {\i modeh be-miktzat}? This depends on whether we treat a single claim for two objects as one unified claim (conceding part of it = partial admission) or as two separate claims (conceding one entirely while denying the other = no partial admission at all). The Gemara initially suggests that the {\i chakhamim} hold that wheat and barley are two separate claims, and therefore there is no {\i modeh be-miktzat} here \u8212- the oil and the jugs likewise constitute two separate claims, and the full concession of jugs with full denial of oil is not a partial admission. This would conflict with the position of Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel, who holds that claiming two objects in a single demand is treated as one claim, such that conceding one part of it does create a {\i shevuah} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Measurement language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda resolves the apparent contradiction by introducing a critical distinction in language. If the claimant said {\i melo asarah kadei shemen yesh li be-vorch} \u8212- the fill of ten jugs of oil I have in your pit \u8212- then clearly he is measuring a quantity of oil; the jugs are purely a unit of measurement and are not themselves being claimed. In that formulation, there is obviously no claim for the jugs, and conceding jugs alone is entirely irrelevant. At the other extreme, if he said {\i asarah kadei shemen mele\u8217'in yesh li etzlecha} \u8212- ten jugs filled with oil I have with you \u8212- then he is explicitly claiming both: the physical jugs and the oil within them. Everyone would agree that in this case, conceding five jugs would constitute {\i modeh be-miktzat}, because both objects were clearly claimed together as one composite demand \u8212- consistent with Rav Nachman amar Shmuel that a combined claim for two objects is treated as unified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute of Admon and the {\i chakhamim} thus concerns only the intermediate formulation: {\i asarah kadei shemen yesh li etzlecha} \u8212- simply \u8220"ten jugs of oil I have with you.\u8221" Is this phrasing understood to be a claim for the jugs themselves, or only for the oil?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava had earlier suggested a different resolution, drawing on an analogy to {\i rimon bi-kelipato} \u8212- a pomegranate with its rind. Even those who would ordinarily treat wheat-and-barley as two separate claims would agree that a pomegranate and its rind are one indivisible thing; conceding the rind while denying the pomegranate flesh would therefore constitute {\i modeh be-miktzat}. Similarly, oil within jugs might be considered one thing, not two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina challenges this analogy: {\i rimon belo kelipato lo mintar} \u8212- a pomegranate cannot be preserved without its rind; the two are physically inseparable. {\i Shemen mintar belo kankanin} \u8212- oil, however, can be stored and preserved without any particular jug. The oil and the jugs are not intrinsically bound to one another. They are therefore more like wheat and barley \u8212- two separate objects \u8212- than like a pomegranate and its rind, and the analogy does not hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately resolves the entire discussion by anchoring the debate exactly where Rav Yehuda had located it: in the question of linguistic meaning. The final formulation: the claimant said {\i asarah kadei shemen yesh li etzlecha} \u8212- \u8220"ten jugs of oil I have with you\u8221" \u8212- and the defendant replied: as for the oil, {\i lo hayu devarim me-olam} \u8212- there was never any such thing; as for the jugs, {\i chamishah it lach ve-chamishah leit lach} \u8212- five I owe you, five I do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Admon\u8217's position \u8212- {\i yesh ba-lashon hazeh lashon kankanim} \u8212- is that the phrase \u8220"ten jugs of oil\u8221" intrinsically includes a claim for the jugs. Since the defendant conceded five jugs out of ten, he has admitted to part of a unified claim, and therefore takes a {\i shevuah}. Moreover, {\i u-migo de-kamishtava a-kankanin, mishtava nami a-shemen} \u8212- since he must take an oath regarding the jugs by force of partial admission, that oath is extended to the oil through {\i gilgul shevuah}: once an oath is obligated on one component of a claim, it rolls onto the related component as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chakhamim} hold \u8212- {\i ein ba-lashon hazeh lashon kankanin} \u8212- that when one says \u8220"jugs of oil,\u8221" he is in ordinary usage referring only to the oil. The jugs are a colloquial measure, not an independent object of the claim. Since the jugs were never truly claimed, conceding them is conceding something that was never demanded \u8212- {\i mah she-ta\u8217'ano lo hodeh lo, u-mah she-hodeh lo lo ta\u8217'ano} \u8212- and no {\i shevuah} is triggered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate thus reduces, at its core, to a question of ordinary linguistic convention: when a person says \u8220"jugs of oil,\u8221" does that phrase carry within it a claim for the physical jugs, or does it refer exclusively to the oil measured in jug-units? Admon says yes, it includes the jugs; the {\i chakhamim} say no, it does not. That question of interpretive convention is, in the end, what determines whether a {\i shevuah} is required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Ketubot 109\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Reneges Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the very bottom of 108b, where the {\i mishna} introduces the first of {\i Admon}\u8217's rulings. The case involves a father-in-law who made a financial agreement with his prospective son-in-law: marry my daughter and I will provide you with a sum of money in support. The Aramaic idiom {\i u\u8217'pashat lo es haregel} \u8212- literally, sticking out one\u8217's foot \u8212- captures the father-in-law\u8217's posture of reneging on the deal in a dismissive, almost contemptuous way. {\i Rashi} suggests the image may connote something like demanding that one clean the sole of his shoe, conveying disrespect, though the operative meaning is simply that the father-in-law is backing out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A crucial detail: the couple had completed {\i kiddushin} but not {\i nisuin}. They are betrothed but not yet fully married. The son-in-law\u8217's response to being stiffed is stark \u8212- {\i teshev ad shetyalbin roshah}, let her sit until the hair on her head turns white. He will neither complete the marriage nor give her a {\i get}, leaving her in the position of an {\i agunah}. She cannot marry anyone else, and he exploits this leverage without paying what was promised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} raises the question of whether the original financial agreement was legally binding at all. There is a principle \u8212- {\i hein hein ha-devarim ha-niknin ba-amira} \u8212- that an oral agreement can be binding when the marriage follows immediately upon it. {\i Tosfos} offers two readings: one, that the agreement here was not binding because some time elapsed between the agreement and the {\i kiddushin}, so the son-in-law cannot sue for the money; or two, that the agreement was indeed binding but would not take full effect until {\i nisuin} was consummated. Either way, the son-in-law finds himself unable or unwilling to pursue the money in court \u8212- perhaps the father-in-law has no assets, perhaps the litigation is not worth the trouble \u8212- and instead uses the {\i get} as a weapon. Whether or not the agreement was technically enforceable, the son-in-law\u8217's position is that his acquiescence to completing the marriage was conditioned on the support, and since that support is not forthcoming, he will not move forward. The result is a woman held hostage through no fault of her own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admon\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Admon} pushes back directly. The woman is permitted to say: {\i ilu ani pasakti l\u8217'atzmi} \u8212- if I had been the one who made this deal and I had reneged, then perhaps I would understand sitting here until my hair turns white, because the injury would have originated with me. But {\i abba pasak alai} \u8212- it was my father who made the agreement and my father who reneged. {\i Ma ani yechola la\u8217'asot} \u8212- what can I do about it? The conclusion is {\i o kenos o patur}: either complete the marriage or release me with a {\i get}. She cannot be made to suffer for her father\u8217's breach of contract.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking ruling. {\i Admon} is establishing that a woman cannot be held as an {\i agunah} as leverage for a financial grievance that is not her doing. The fact that the son-in-law was wronged by the father-in-law does not translate into a right to trap the daughter indefinitely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Own Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a {\i beraita} attributed to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda that reframes where the actual dispute lies. According to this {\i beraita}, {\i Admon} and the {\i chachamim} do not actually disagree in the case described by the {\i mishna}, where the father made the deal and reneged. In that scenario, everyone agrees the woman can say {\i abba pasak alai} \u8212- she bears no responsibility \u8212- and the husband cannot hold her hostage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate, says Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, is specifically {\i al she-paska hi al atzma} \u8212- when the woman herself made the financial commitment to the marriage. In that case, the {\i chachamim} hold that since she made the deal and she is the one who is reneging, the consequence falls on her and she may indeed sit until her hair turns white. {\i Admon}, however, says that even then she has a valid claim: {\i svura hayiti she-abba noten alai} \u8212- when I made that commitment, I assumed my father would be the one to write the check. Now that my father is not in fact providing the money, {\i ma ani yechola la\u8217'asot} \u8212- it is out of my hands. I made a promise I genuinely expected to keep; the non-fulfillment is beyond my control. Therefore, {\i o kenos o patur} \u8212- even in this case the husband must either marry her or release her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Gamliel} weighs in: {\i ro\u8217'eh ani et divrei Admon} \u8212- I see the force of {\i Admon}\u8217's position. The {\i gemara} then addresses a {\i beraita} that asks: when does all of this apply? The answer is {\i gdola} \u8212- when she is an adult. But {\i aval b\u8217'ktana}, if she was a minor when she made the commitment, {\i kofin} \u8212- we actually compel. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i kofin le-mi}, compel whom? If the answer is compel the father to pay, that makes little sense \u8212- a minor\u8217's commitment is less binding than an adult\u8217's, so it would be illogical to apply greater force in that case. Rather, says {\i Rava}, {\i kofin l\u8217'ba\u8217'al liten get} \u8212- in the case of a minor, the husband is actually compelled to give a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is that the entire debate only arises when an adult woman made the agreement and backed out. Even in that case, {\i Admon} says she cannot be held hostage. But if she was a minor when she made the commitment, her agreement carries no binding weight whatsoever, and even the {\i chachamim} would agree that she cannot be held. In that case, the husband is compelled to act \u8212- either to complete the marriage or to release her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakhic Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Affirmed Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded the first group of {\i Admon}\u8217's rulings, the {\i gemara} turns to the question of practical {\i halacha}. Rabbi Yitzchak bar Elazar says in the name of Chizkiya: {\i kol makom she-amar Rabban Gamliel ro\u8217'eh ani et divrei Admon halacha kemoto} \u8212- wherever {\i Rabban Gamliel} says \u8220"I see {\i Admon}\u8217's position,\u8221" we rule like him. This establishes a clear halakhic standard: affirmation by {\i Rabban Gamliel} is the criterion for ruling in accordance with {\i Admon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} then asks {\i Rav Nachman}: does this apply even to the {\i beraita}\u8217's version of the case \u8212- the scenario where the woman herself made the deal and is reneging, which is the terrain of the {\i beraita} rather than the {\i mishna}? {\i Rav Nachman} responds: I did not say \u8220"wherever {\i Rabban Gamliel} says this in the {\i mishna}.\u8221" I said {\i wherever} he says it \u8212- {\i be-chol makom she-amar Rabban Gamliel ka-amina}. Since {\i Rabban Gamliel} affirms {\i Admon} in the {\i beraita} as well, we rule like {\i Admon} there too. Even when the woman herself made the commitment and is backing out, she cannot be held hostage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 \u1495 ?\u1504 ?\u1503 ? vs Admon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces a statement of Rabbi Zeira in the name of Rabbah bar Yirmeyah: regarding the two rulings of {\i Chanan}, {\i halacha kiyotzei vo} \u8212- we rule like him, and {\i kiyotzei vo}. The phrase {\i kiyotzei vo} is the subject of a dispute between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosfos}. {\i Tosfos} understands it to mean that we not only rule like {\i Chanan} in the specific cases he addressed, but we extract the underlying principle and apply it to analogous cases. {\i Rashi}, by contrast, reads {\i kiyotzei vo} as referring to the one who affirmed the ruling \u8212- that is, since {\i Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai} affirmed {\i Chanan}\u8217's two positions, we rule in accordance with that affirmation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding {\i Admon}\u8217's seven rulings, Rabbi Zeira says {\i ein halacha kiyotzei vo}. On {\i Tosfos}\u8217's reading, this means we do not extend {\i Admon}\u8217's rulings to analogous cases. On {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading, it means we do not rule in accordance with whoever affirmed him. The {\i gemara} immediately presses: if taken literally, this would seem to say we reject {\i Admon} entirely \u8212- but we know that cannot be right, since we just established from Rabbi Yitzchak bar Elazar that wherever {\i Rabban Gamliel} affirms {\i Admon} we rule like him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Acceptance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through the formulation carefully. An initial reading \u8212- {\i halacha kemoto u-kiyotzei vo} for {\i Chanan}, and {\i ein halacha kemoto} for {\i Admon} in all seven \u8212- is rejected because it contradicts the explicit ruling that we follow {\i Admon} in the cases where {\i Rabban Gamliel} affirmed him. Another reading \u8212- that {\i halacha kemoto} applies to all seven of {\i Admon}\u8217's cases concretely, just not to analogous extensions \u8212- is also rejected for the same reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} settles on the following resolution: by {\i Chanan}, we rule like him in both of his rulings and we apply his principles to analogous cases as well \u8212- {\i halacha kemoto u-kiyotzei vo}. By {\i Admon}, the picture is mixed \u8212- {\i yesh mehem she-halacha kemoto ve-hayotzei vo}, some of his rulings we rule like him and apply to similar cases, {\i ve-yesh mehem she-ein halacha kemoto ve-hayotzei bo}, and some we do not rule like him even in the concrete case. The determinant is straightforward: {\i ve-chol makom she-amar Rabban Gamliel ro\u8217'eh ani et divrei Admon halacha kemoto} \u8212- wherever {\i Rabban Gamliel} affirmed him, we rule like him and extend the principle; in the other four rulings of {\i Admon}, we rule neither like him in the specific case nor in analogous ones. This final formulation simply restates what was established earlier, resolving the apparent contradiction by making clear that Rabbi Zeira\u8217's statement referred to the non-affirmed cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On {\i Tosfos}\u8217's reading, the point being made is that for {\i Chanan} we abstract a general principle, whereas for the unaffirmed {\i Admon} rulings we do not even do that much. On {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading, the structure is about the voice of affirmation \u8212- we follow {\i Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai} who affirmed {\i Chanan} in both cases, and we follow {\i Rabban Gamliel} where he affirmed {\i Admon}, but we do not follow {\i Admon} where no affirmation was given.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Field & Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to {\i Admon}\u8217's second ruling, which concerns a different sort of case entirely \u8212- one involving a claim of stolen property and the complications that arise from having signed as a witness on a document. The case: Reuven claims that Shimon stole his field. Reuven has evidence that the field was originally his \u8212- Shimon cannot produce a bill of sale, and Shimon does not have three years of {\i chazaka}. The absence of those would normally be sufficient for Reuven to press his claim successfully. There is, however, one complication: {\i ha-over al ha-sadeh ve-chatam aleha be-ed} \u8212- Reuven himself signed as a witness on the document by which Shimon sold that very field to a third party, Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How can Reuven now claim the field was his if he was a witness to Shimon\u8217's sale of it? The implicit logic is that by signing as a witness, Reuven appears to have conceded that the field belonged to Shimon \u8212- otherwise, why would he have signed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admon Excuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Admon} holds that signing the document as a witness does not forfeit Reuven\u8217's claim to the field. The reasoning: {\i ha-sheni no\u8217'ach li ve-ha-rishon kasheh himenu} \u8212- it is easier to litigate against Levi, the buyer, than against Shimon, the original thief. Shimon is a dangerous or powerful figure; Reuven may have had good reason not to confront him directly. By signing the document facilitating the sale from Shimon to Levi, Reuven may simply have been positioning himself to retrieve the field more conveniently from Levi \u8212- allowing the field to change hands to someone less threatening, and then pursuing his claim against the new holder. This is a fully articulated rationale for why a signature on a document of sale does not necessarily constitute a concession of ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Disagree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} reject this entirely. Their position is {\i ibad et zechuto} \u8212- he has forfeited his rights. If Reuven signed as a witness to a document in which Shimon presented himself as the owner selling the field to Levi, then Reuven has implicitly conceded that the field was Shimon\u8217's. The {\i halacha} follows the {\i chachamim} here, not {\i Admon} \u8212- this is one of the four rulings of {\i Admon} that {\i Rabban Gamliel} did not affirm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judge Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} introduces an important distinction: {\i lo shanu ela ed} \u8212- this dispute, and the implicit concession, applies only when one signed as a {\i witness} to the document. {\i Aval dayan} \u8212- but if one served not as a witness but as a judge validating the signatures on the document, the rule is different. {\i Abaye} derives this from a teaching of Rabbi Chiyya: {\i ein ha-eidim chotmim al ha-shtar ela im ken kerauhu} \u8212- witnesses may not sign a document unless they have read it, because they are attesting to its content and must know what they are attesting to. But {\i aval ha-dayanim} \u8212- judges who are validating signatures do not need to have read the document. {\i Chotmim af al pi shelo kerauhu} \u8212- they may validate the signatures even without reading the full contents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy to a notary public is apt: a notary attests that the signatures are genuine, not that the contents of the document are true. Since a judge validating signatures need not have read \u8212- and may not have read \u8212- what the document says, his signature carries no implicit endorsement of the document\u8217's claims. Therefore, {\i aval dayan lo ibad et zechuto} \u8212- if Reuven served as a validating judge rather than as a substantive witness, he does not forfeit his right to claim the field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boundary Marker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further distinction is introduced regarding a scenario where the field in question was not the field being sold but was mentioned only as a boundary marker. If Shimon sold Levi a neighboring field, Field B, and in that document described Field A \u8212- the field Reuven claims was stolen \u8212- as one of Field B\u8217's borders, then even {\i Admon} concedes that Reuven\u8217's signature on that document constitutes an implicit admission that Field A belongs to Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is that in the original dispute \u8212- where Shimon was selling Field A itself to Levi \u8212- {\i Admon}\u8217's rationale for Reuven\u8217's signature was that Reuven wanted the field to change to a less threatening holder. But here, Field A is not being transferred. It appears in the document only as a descriptive boundary marker. Reuven gains nothing from facilitating that transaction \u8212- there is no strategic reason for him to have signed a document that, in passing, describes Field A as belonging to Shimon. His signature therefore concedes Shimon\u8217's ownership, and {\i Abaye} rules {\i ibad et zechuto} \u8212- he has forfeited his claim.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Coercion Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own Field Used\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} continues with another variation: {\i lo shanu ela le-achar} \u8212- the previous analysis applies when Shimon is selling the field to someone else. But {\i aval le-atzmo} \u8212- what if Shimon is selling a different field directly to Reuven himself, and in the document of that sale Shimon uses Field A \u8212- the field Reuven claims was stolen \u8212- as a boundary marker? In that case, {\i lo ibad et zechuto} \u8212- Reuven does not forfeit his right to claim Field A.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why not? Because Reuven can say: {\i de-amar i lo avdi lei hachi, hava lo mazbin li} \u8212- if I had not agreed to let him write the document that way, he would not have sold me the other field. Reuven needed to acquire Field B from Shimon. Shimon, who already took Field A from him, was insisting on describing Field A as his in the document of sale. Reuven had no realistic option but to go along with it in order to complete the transaction. His acquiescence to that language does not constitute a concession; it reflects economic compulsion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moda\u8217'a Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects: if Reuven felt genuinely coerced, {\i ibai lei le-mimsar moda\u8217'a} \u8212- he should have issued a {\i moda\u8217'a}, a formal protestation made before witnesses. The institution of {\i moda\u8217'a} allows someone who anticipates being coerced into signing a document or entering a transaction to declare beforehand to witnesses: \u8220"Know that whatever I am about to sign or agree to, I am doing so under duress, and my apparent agreement should not be taken as genuine consent.\u8221" Had Reuven issued such a {\i moda\u8217'a}, he could later produce those witnesses to challenge the document. Why did he not do so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secrecy Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reuven\u8217's response: {\i chavrach chavra is lei, ve-chavra de-chavrach chavra is lei} \u8212- your friend has a friend, and your friend\u8217's friend has a friend. The {\i gemara}\u8217's formulation is its version of the principle that secrets cannot be reliably kept. If Reuven had issued a {\i moda\u8217'a} to witnesses, the word would inevitably have reached Shimon, who would then have refused to proceed with the sale of Field B at all \u8212- and Reuven would have lost his only leverage to acquire anything. The impossibility of keeping a {\i moda\u8217'a} secret is itself sufficient justification for why Reuven did not pursue that course.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is therefore a reasonable explanation for Reuven\u8217's conduct: he allowed the document to proceed as written because he had no viable alternative, and the concern about secrecy adequately accounts for why he did not protect himself through a {\i moda\u8217'a}. Given that reasonable explanation, his signing cannot be read as a concession that Field A belonged to Shimon.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Orphans Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now brings a {\i ma\u8217'aseh}, a concrete case, that illustrates these principles. A man who had claimed that a field had been stolen from him used that same field as a boundary marker in another transaction. He then died \u8212- {\i e\u8217'ar ve-shachiv} \u8212- before being able to press his claim in court. The court appointed an {\i apotropas}, a guardian, to represent the interests of the orphans, his heirs, with respect to the contested field. When the {\i apotropas} appeared before {\i Abaye} and presented evidence that the field had in fact belonged to the deceased father, {\i Abaye} ruled against him: {\i asa siman le-achar ve-ibeid zechuto} \u8212- by allowing the field to be used as a boundary marker in another document, the father had implicitly conceded ownership and forfeited the right to claim it. The orphans, as heirs to their father\u8217's claim, inherited that forfeiture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guardian Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i apotropas} was not finished. He argued: {\i i havi avuhon de-yasmei kayam, havi to\u8217'en ve-amar telem echad asiti lach} \u8212- had the father still been alive, here is what he would have claimed. He would have said: I conceded only one furrow\u8217's worth of land \u8212- a thin strip \u8212- not the entire field. The logic is that when the father\u8217's field was described as the boundary of another field in the document, the literal implication is only that a narrow strip at the edge of his field \u8212- the actual boundary \u8212- belongs to the other party. There is no reason to read the use of a field as a boundary marker as a concession of the entire plot. The father was conceding the furrow that formed the edge, no more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a sophisticated argument for minimizing the scope of the implicit admission. The field appears in the document as a boundary, not as a subject of sale. A boundary, by definition, is an edge \u8212- a line, not a mass. To say that Field A\u8217's western edge borders Field B does not require that all of Field A belongs to Field B\u8217's owner; it requires only that the very edge of Field A \u8212- perhaps a furrow\u8217's width \u8212- does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} acknowledged the force of this argument and cited Rabbi Yochanan: {\i im ta\u8217'an ve-amar telem echad asiti lach, ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- if the claimant says that all he conceded was one furrow, he is believed. On this basis, {\i abaye} ruled to award the field to the orphans \u8212- {\i shakluha ve-havu lehu le-yasmei} \u8212- with the qualification that the narrow strip be returned to the other party. The {\i apotropas}, however, pressed further: that strip happened to contain a dense stand of palm trees, {\i richba de-diklei}, making it quite valuable. He was reluctant to hand it back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So he argued again: {\i ee hava avun de-yasmei kayam, hava ta\u8217'in ve-amar chazarti ve-lakachtiha heimenu} \u8212- had the father been alive, he would also have claimed: yes, I once conceded that strip to you, but afterward I repurchased it from you. This is a startling claim \u8212- an admission followed immediately by a counter-claim of subsequent repurchase, with no bill of sale to show for it. {\i Tosfos} is troubled by why this should be believed and suggests that the answer lies in the fact that the document itself lacked properly validated witnesses, which undermined its evidentiary weight and gave additional latitude to reinterpret the scope of the concession. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling covers this as well: {\i ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- even the claim of repurchase is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Praise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} affirmed the {\i apotropas} on this second point as well, quoting Rabbi Yochanan both on the partial-concession argument and on the subsequent-repurchase argument. He then praised the {\i apotropas} directly: {\i hai man de-mokim apotropa, lokim ke-hai, de-yada le-hafocha bi-zchuta de-yasmei} \u8212- whoever appoints an {\i apotropas} should appoint someone like this one, who knows how to turn arguments in favor of the orphans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} raises a pointed question: why was this adversarial advocacy necessary at all? A {\i beis din} is supposed to argue the best case for orphans without being prompted. Why did {\i Abaye} initially rule against the orphans rather than make these arguments himself? {\i Tosfos} answers honestly: {\i Abaye} had simply forgotten what Rabbi Yochanan had said. It was only when the {\i apotropas} made the argument that {\i Abaye} recalled the ruling and recognized that the {\i apotropas} had arrived at precisely the correct position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lost Path\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Path Lost\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} moves to {\i Admon}\u8217's third ruling, introduced in a new {\i mishna}. The scenario: {\i mi she-halach li-medinat ha-yam ve-avda derech sadehu} \u8212- a man went overseas, and while he was away, the path leading to his field was lost. The physical setting is important. Imagine a large agricultural district in which many fields are held by different owners. Nestled in the interior is Reuven\u8217's field, accessible only by passing through or alongside the surrounding fields. Reuven had an established right of access \u8212- a path \u8212- but after years away, no one can identify where it ran. Whether through genuine forgetting, neglect, or the self-interest of neighbors, the path has simply disappeared from collective memory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admon Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Admon omer yelech lo be-ketzara} \u8212- {\i Admon} rules that Reuven is entitled to be given the shortest available path through the surrounding property. The reasoning is that everyone knows Reuven is owed {\i some} path; the fact that the precise location has been forgotten does not extinguish the right. Since the right exists and the path must go somewhere, Reuven should receive the most efficient route \u8212- the shortest one. He cannot be asked to prove which specific path was his; it is enough that a path was his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Harsh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} take a much harder line: {\i yikneh lo derech be-meah maneh, o yifrach ba-avir} \u8212- let him purchase a path for a hundred {\i maneh}, or fly through the air. In other words, if Reuven cannot identify which path was specifically his, he has no enforceable claim against any particular neighbor. Each neighbor can say \u8220"my field is not where your path was,\u8221" and since Reuven cannot prove otherwise, he has no recourse. The neighbor is free to name any price for access, however exorbitant, and if Reuven cannot pay, he simply has no way in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking ruling. As one listener in the shiur observed, it seems less like a legal principle and more like a neighbor squeezing a vulnerable party. And yet, the {\i gemara} takes it seriously as a {\i halachic} position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} seeks to understand the logical basis of the dispute. {\i Rav Yehuda} says in the name of {\i Rav}: the {\i chachamim}\u8217's position makes sense in a case where {\i ke-gon she-hekifuha arba\u8217'ah b\u8217'nei adam me-arba ruchoteha} \u8212- four different individuals each own one of the four surrounding fields. In that scenario, any one of them brought to court can say: the path was never in my field \u8212- go prove it was. With four separate defendants each able to deflect responsibility to the others, Reuven has no effective legal recourse against any of them individually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} asks: {\i ei hachi, mai ta\u8217'ama de-Admon?} \u8212- if that\u8217's the scenario, then what is {\i Admon}\u8217's basis for disagreeing? Even he should concede that Reuven cannot win. So the {\i gemara} concludes that the dispute must occupy a middle case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} identifies that middle case. The debate is not about four separate owners who were always separate ({\i arba\u8217'ah de-atu mi-koach arba\u8217'ah}) \u8212- in that scenario, everyone agrees Reuven cannot successfully sue anyone. Nor is it simply about four fields originally held by the same owner. The key case is {\i be-chad de-atu mi-koach arba\u8217'ah}: originally, each of the four surrounding fields belonged to a different farmer. Then a single entity \u8212- {\i Rava} describes it as one person who acquired from four \u8212- came and purchased all four fields. Now they all belong to one owner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Admon}\u8217's position: {\i mikol makom darchai ika gabech} \u8212- regardless of how those fields were originally held, the path exists somewhere within property that now belongs to you. I can bring a single litigant to court and demand my path. {\i Rabban} (the {\i chachamim}) respond: {\i ai shatakt shatakt, ve-ai lo, mehadarna shtra le-marihu} \u8212- if you leave this alone, we can work something out quietly; but if you insist on litigating, I will simply transfer the fields back to the four original owners, and then you will be back to square one \u8212- four separate defendants, each able to deflect responsibility. Since your rights originated in a situation where you could not have successfully forced any one person to provide the path, you gain no additional rights now merely because I happened to consolidate those fields.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} acknowledges the strangeness of this argument \u8212- it has the character of an economic threat rather than a principled legal defense. One would not expect the court to take seriously the claim \u8220"I will undo a commercial transaction to disadvantage you in litigation.\u8221" And sympathies here rest naturally with {\i Admon}. Yet this is how the {\i gemara} frames the {\i chachamim}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Palm Tree Legacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bequest Ignored\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes with a case that echoes the structure of the lost-path problem in a different context. A man on his deathbed said to his children: {\i dikla le-brat} \u8212- give a palm tree to my daughter. He did not specify which palm tree. He then died. The male heirs proceeded to divide up the entire estate among themselves without setting aside a palm tree for their sister. Now she comes to court demanding what her father bequeathed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially considers comparing this to the lost-path case: just as each surrounding field owner could deflect responsibility to the next, perhaps each brother can say \u8220"your palm tree was in the other brother\u8217's portion, not mine.\u8221" {\i Rav Yosef} is inclined toward this comparison.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half vs Whole\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} rejects the comparison. {\i Mi dami} \u8212- the cases are not alike. In the lost-path case, {\i kol chad ve-chad matzi madchei lei} \u8212- each owner can genuinely deflect, because the path was originally located in a specific field and no one knows which one. But here, {\i dikla gabaihu} \u8212- there was never any question that the estate as a whole owed her a palm tree. The obligation ran against the estate, not against any particular plot. The division of the estate among the brothers does not discharge that obligation; it only complicates how it is fulfilled. The solution is {\i leihavu lah dikla} \u8212- give her a palm tree \u8212- and then {\i ve-hadar ve-lipalgu me-reisha} \u8212- re-divide the estate from the beginning, with the bequest having been satisfied first. If the brothers feel that one of them is bearing a disproportionate cost by giving up a palm tree, that is a matter to resolve among themselves; but the sister\u8217's right cannot be extinguished simply because they divided without her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halves Equal Whole\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents a related case. A man died with a similar bequest \u8212- {\i dikla le-brat}, a palm tree to his daughter \u8212- and it emerged that among the assets of the estate were two palm trees in which the deceased had a half-ownership interest in each. The heirs wished to discharge their obligation to the daughter by giving her the two half-interests \u8212- a full palm tree\u8217's worth, distributed across two trees \u8212- rather than one whole palm tree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} was uncertain: {\i mi karu inshi le-trei palgei dikla dikla} \u8212- would people ordinarily refer to two half-palm-trees as \u8220"a palm tree\u8221"? The question is whether the deathbed bequest of {\i dikla} \u8212- a palm tree \u8212- can be satisfied by two partial interests, or whether the word {\i dikla} necessarily implies an undivided whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Mordechai} conveyed to {\i Rav Ashi} the ruling of {\i Abimi me-Hagronya} in the name of {\i Rava}: {\i karu inshi le-trei palgei dikla dikla} \u8212- yes, people do call two halves a palm tree. The ordinary usage of the term encompasses this configuration. Since the language of the bequest is not so precise as to demand a single, undivided tree, the heirs may satisfy the obligation with the two half-interests, and their obligation is thereby discharged.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro: Vows & Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder vs.\u160 ?Shvuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Masechet Nedarim concerns the laws of vows, and it is worth pausing at the outset to clarify what a {\i neder} is and how it differs from a {\i shvuah} before examining how the tractate understands these concepts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the Torah, there are two principal modes by which a person undertakes a binding verbal commitment: the {\i shvuah} and the {\i neder}. A {\i shvuah} is an oath. It involves calling upon God to witness a statement about something one has done or will do \u8212- whether a monetary claim, a past action, or a future undertaking. \u8220"I will give you a hundred dollars,\u8221" \u8220"I will not eat bread tomorrow\u8221" \u8212- these are the kinds of statements that constitute a {\i shvuah}. Central to the concept of {\i shvuah} is the invocation of God\u8217's name: it is, at its core, a declaration made with God as witness, and swearing falsely in that context constitutes {\i lo tissa shem Hashem lashav} or {\i v\u8217'lo tishavu bishmi lashaker}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i neder}, on first reading of the biblical text, does not appear to be categorically distinct from a {\i shvuah}. Yaakov Avinu\u8217's famous vow \u8212- {\i vayidar Yaakov neder lemor im yihiyeh elohim imadi ushmarani baderech hazeh\u8230 ? v\u8217'chol asher titten li asser a\u8217'assrenu lach} \u8212- sounds very much like an oath about future conduct: if God does this for me, I will do such-and-such. The distinguishing feature might be the absence of direct invocation of God\u8217's name as a witness, since a {\i shvuah} classically entails calling upon God in precisely that way. But the difference is not sharply delineated in the {\i pesukim} themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several biblical contexts employ the word {\i neder}. In addition to Yaakov\u8217's vow, there is the vow of Israel before the battle with Canaan: {\i vayidar Yisrael neder lemor im nason titten es ha\u8217'am hazeh b\u8217'yadi v\u8217'hacharamti es areihem} \u8212- if You deliver this nation into my hand, I will dedicate their cities to God. That language of dedication, of giving something over to God, seems to be a common thread running through many Torah uses of {\i neder}. Similarly, the category of {\i korban} includes the possibility of a {\i neder} or a {\i nedavah}: {\i im neder o nedava zevach korbano} \u8212- the {\i neder} in that context is an obligation upon oneself to bring an offering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then there is Parshat Matos, which becomes the paradigmatic locus for this entire tractate. The verse reads: {\i ish ki yidor neder l\u8217'Hashem o hishava shvuah le\u8217'esor issar al nafsho lo yachel devaro} \u8212- a man who takes a {\i neder} or a {\i shvuah} to make something forbidden upon himself, he must not violate his word. Here the {\i neder} is explicitly about making something forbidden, and although the verse connects it to a {\i shvuah} as well, it is this formulation \u8212- the {\i neder} as an act that renders an object prohibited \u8212- that Chazal take as the defining model for the entire tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazal\u8217's Paradigm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What Chazal identify as the defining character of a {\i neder} is a focus on the {\i object} rather than on the {\i person}. A {\i shvuah} is person-oriented: \u8220"I swear that I will not eat this bread\u8221" \u8212- the person undertakes an obligation, and the object remains unchanged. A {\i neder}, by contrast, changes the status of the object itself: this bread becomes a forbidden object, much like other objects whose status is transformed by a verbal act. The object is the locus of the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This understanding requires some reframing of the more prominent biblical examples. The cases of Yaakov and of Israel, where a {\i neder} sounds like a personal undertaking contingent on divine assistance, are reconceptualized by Chazal as {\i nidrei mitzvah} \u8212- vows to perform a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- a subcategory distinct from the standard {\i neder} discussed in this tractate. Similarly, the {\i neder} of a {\i korban}, while an obligation the person takes upon themselves, involves the verbal transformation of an object\u8217's status into the realm of the sacred. It is that element \u8212- the speech act that changes the object \u8212- which Chazal identify as the essential feature of a {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question naturally arises: is there a unifying conceptual thread connecting all these cases? Even where the {\i neder} begins with a personal obligation, there is always an object whose status is being transformed. When Yaakov says he will give a {\i ma\u8217'aser}, he is verbally designating an object for God. When Israel vows to dedicate the cities as {\i cherem}, those objects are being given over through a declaration. When a {\i korban} is designated, the animal\u8217's status changes through words. The common denominator, then, is the power of verbal declaration to transform the identity and status of an object \u8212- {\i motzi b\u8217'peh} as the engine of change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with a {\i shvuah} sharpens this point. In a {\i shvuah}, God\u8217's name is the key: you invoke God as witness to your action or commitment. In a {\i neder}, the key is not God\u8217's name but rather the object and your power \u8212- through your words \u8212- to alter its status. A {\i neder} draws upon a recognized domain in which human speech has transformative force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the concept of {\i ein nodrin ela bedavar hanadur} \u8212- one may only make a {\i neder} using the model of a {\i davar hanadur}, something whose forbidden status was itself produced by a human verbal act. If one says \u8220"this object should be like {\i chazir},\u8221" one has accomplished nothing, because the forbidden status of {\i chazir} derives from God\u8217's decree and its own nature, not from any human verbal transformation. One cannot, through speech, make something into {\i chazir}, because that domain is entirely outside the scope of human declarative power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if one says \u8220"this object should be like a {\i korban},\u8221" one is invoking a domain where human speech unambiguously transforms object-status. Just as one can make an animal a {\i korban}, or designate produce as {\i ma\u8217'aser}, or declare something {\i cherem}, one can render an object forbidden by likening it to these categories. The {\i korban} serves as the paradigmatic {\i davar hanadur} \u8212- the exemplary case of an object made forbidden through human declaration \u8212- and it is from this model that the standard vocabulary of {\i nedarim} is derived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Importantly, a statement such as \u8220"this food is like {\i basar v\u8217'chalav}\u8221" or \u8220"like {\i chametz b\u8217'Pesach}\u8221" or \u8220"like {\i chelev}\u8221" would similarly accomplish nothing as a {\i neder}, even though those things are forbidden. The reason is the same: those prohibitions are divinely ordained categories, not the product of human verbal transformation. The valid model is exclusively the {\i davar hanadur} \u8212- something whose prohibited status flows from a human speech act of the same type.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The classic term for this type of {\i neder} is {\i konam}, which is a variation \u8212- or euphemism \u8212- of the word {\i korban}. When one says {\i konam davar zeh alay} \u8212- \u8220"this thing should be like a {\i korban} to me\u8221" \u8212- one is drawing on that domain of human verbal transformation of object-status. {\i Konam} is thus not merely a substitute word; it is a word whose very form signals the conceptual underpinning of the {\i neder}: this object is to be treated like a sacrificial offering, whose status has been changed by my words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting in passing that even today, {\i bezman hazeh} when the {\i Beis HaMikdash} does not stand, one can still make a {\i neder} using this model. If something is declared like a {\i korban}, it becomes sanctified \u8212- it cannot be used, must be left to graze if it is an animal, and so forth \u8212- even without the possibility of actual sacrifice. The mechanism of declaring something like a {\i korban} remains operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Speech Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of this points to the broader category of {\i haplaa} \u8212- the speech act that transforms status. The Torah itself uses the root in {\i nazir ki yafli lindor neder nazir}, where {\i yaflia} means to enunciate or articulate, and in the rabbinic understanding it denotes specifically a speech act with transformative legal force. This is the category under which {\i nedarim}, {\i nezirut}, {\i ma\u8217'aser}, {\i hekdesh}, and {\i cherem} all fall: verbal declarations that change the ontological status of an object. A {\i shvuah} does not belong to this category in the same way \u8212- it does not change the status of an object but rather commits the person to a course of action or attests to a fact. The tractate before us is, at its heart, a sustained treatment of {\i haplaa} as applied to the domain of making objects forbidden.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Masechet Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashon Yerushalmi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before beginning the {\i masechet} itself, it is worth noting several unusual features that set Masechet Nedarim apart from the rest of the Babylonian Talmud. The language of this tractate \u8212- its grammar, certain technical terms, and stylistic patterns \u8212- differs noticeably from the standard Babylonian Aramaic of the rest of the {\i Bavli}. This was already observed by Tosafot and the {\i rishonim} generally; Rashi designates this distinctive idiom in several places as {\i lashon Yerushalmi}, Palestinian Aramaic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the scholarly literature, five tractates \u8212- Nedarim, Nazir, Meilah, Keritot, and Tamid \u8212- share a range of linguistic and grammatical peculiarities that distinguish them from the rest of the Babylonian Talmud. Notably, parts of Masechet Temurah exhibit the same phenomenon through what are labeled there as {\i lishna achrina} passages: one section of the text is in the familiar Babylonian Aramaic, and then an alternate version of the same passage appears written in this different idiom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geonic Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Connected to this linguistic distinctiveness is a remarkable testimony concerning Geonic practice. The work {\i Otzar HaGeonim}, which collects and organizes Geonic responsa by tractate, records very little material on Nedarim. The reason given is telling: the {\i Geonim} who arose after the sealing of the Talmud and devoted themselves to clarifying its {\i halachot} \u8212- {\i hiziru atzmam mimasechet Nedarim} \u8212- they kept away from Masechet Nedarim. Within their {\i yeshivot} they formally announced: {\i de\u8217'anan la gersinan Nedarim} \u8212- \u8220"we do not study Nedarim.\u8221" And further: {\i velo yediana le\u8217'asur ulehateir bah lo neder velo shevuah} \u8212- \u8220"we do not know how to prohibit or permit in this domain, neither by {\i neder} nor by {\i shevuah}.\u8221" Consequently, they did not engage in {\i hataras nedarim} either on Rosh Hashanah or on Yom Kippur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The text notes, however, that {\i be\u8217'Eretz Yisrael hayu nohagin bekach kol hayamim} \u8212- in the Land of Israel they maintained the practice throughout. One of the reasons cited for the Babylonian Geonic abstention is instructive: if {\i nedarim} are widely studied and discussed, people become aware of them as a readily available religious instrument and begin making them, sometimes inadvertently or inappropriately. Keeping the tractate out of the religious consciousness reduces the incidence of {\i nedarim}. This is analogous to the concern one might have about teaching certain sections of Masechet Kiddushin in a context where students might experiment with what they learn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This warrants reflection. We tend not to appreciate how central the practice of making {\i nedarim} was to the religious lives of earlier generations. Vows were a standard mechanism for bringing God into one\u8217's daily world, for giving expression to one\u8217's religious impulses, for adding personal {\i chumrot} or taking on additional commitments. The {\i Gemara} elsewhere describes making a {\i neder} as analogous to building a {\i bamah} \u8212- with all the ambivalence that image carries. It can be an expression of genuine religious seriousness, but it also risks introducing a personal religiosity that operates outside communal norms, and the transgression risks involved in making and violating {\i nedarim} are considerable. The Geonic decision to deliberately abstain reflects a pastoral judgment about the balance of those risks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pumbedita Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of the tractate\u8217's linguistic distinctiveness connects naturally to questions of provenance. Those who follow Rashi in calling the language {\i lashon Yerushalmi} have argued that the tractate ultimately reflects Palestinian usage and may be closer in origin to the {\i Yerushalmi} tradition than to the standard {\i Bavli}. This view is reinforced, they argue, by the fact that the Babylonian {\i Geonim} essentially did not engage with the tractate, while it remained part of living practice in Eretz Yisrael throughout the Geonic period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, this view is significantly contested in the scholarly literature. The more widely accepted position today is that the standard {\i Bavli} reflects the Babylonian usage of Sura \u8212- the academy where Rav Ashi edited the Talmud \u8212- while the distinctive idiom of Nedarim and the related tractates reflects the Babylonian usage of Pumbedita, the other great Babylonian academy, whose Aramaic differed in precisely these ways. On this account, there is no need to posit a Palestinian origin; the difference reflects a difference between the two great Babylonian centers of learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The further questions of relative dating \u8212- whether the Pumbeditan material is earlier, contemporaneous, or later than the Suran redaction, and whether it was re-worked or left in its original form \u8212- remain matters of scholarly debate, with multiple positions advanced. But the emerging consensus appears to favor the Pumbedita theory of origin rather than a Palestinian one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given the unusual character of the tractate, the question of which {\i rishonim} to rely on for commentary deserves attention. The commentary printed with the designation \u8220"Rashi\u8221" in standard editions of Nedarim is, by general scholarly acknowledgment, not actually Rashi\u8217's. The attribution is uncertain, and the commentary itself is considerably less rigorous and less precise than authentic Rashi \u8212- less consistent, less clear, and less analytically sharp. In many {\i yeshivot} that study this tractate, the pseudo-Rashi commentary is not treated as a primary learning resource.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The central commentary is instead the {\i Ran} \u8212- Rabbeinu Nissim of Gerona \u8212- who lived in the thirteenth century. The {\i Ran} is among the clearest of the {\i rishonim}, though not necessarily the most creatively innovative. He synthesizes the positions of earlier {\i rishonim}, presents the {\i Gemara}\u8217's flow with admirable clarity, and brings in contradictions, difficulties, and resolutions in a manner that makes him function as something of a {\i rashi-tosafot} combined for this tractate. He is expansive, as the size of his commentary makes evident, but consistently lucid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot and the Rosh are also present in the standard {\i Vilna} edition, appearing in their usual positions. The Tosafot here tend toward more straightforward commentary rather than the intensive {\i shakla vetarya} one finds in tractates like Bava Kamma or Gittin. Notably, despite the Geonic abstention, the {\i Rif} did write {\i Halachot} on Nedarim \u8212- his characteristic extraction of bottom-line {\i halachic} rulings \u8212- and the Ramban likewise composed {\i Hilchot HaRamban} on Nedarim, printed in the back of the {\i Gemara} parallel to the Rif. A range of {\i acharonim} have also written commentaries. The tractate is thus not without {\i rishonic} resources, even if they differ in character from those found in the more centrally studied {\i masechtot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinuyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinuyim Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i mishna} of Masechet Nedarim opens with a broad principle: {\i kol kinnuyei nedarim kenedarim} \u8212- all {\i kinuyim} of {\i nedarim} have the status of {\i nedarim}. A {\i kinnuy} is a substitute word, a variation or euphemism \u8212- a term that is not exactly the standard vocabulary of a {\i neder} but is used in its place. The {\i mishna} establishes that such substitute language is legally effective and counts as if the standard term itself were used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Other Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle is then extended in parallel formulations: {\i vacharamim kacharamim} \u8212- variations of {\i cherem} language carry the force of an actual {\i cherem}; and {\i ushvuot keshvuot} \u8212- variations of {\i shvuah} language are binding as a {\i shvuah}. The tractate also includes {\i nezirut kenezirut}: language that approximates or echoes the terminology of {\i nezirut} is binding as if one had formally declared oneself a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} notes, in the context of {\i cherem}, that the tractate\u8217's treatment focuses on {\i cherem} understood primarily as a form of {\i neder} \u8212- that is, as a declaration making an object forbidden to oneself \u8212- rather than as the full category of dedicating objects to God or to the {\i kohanim}, which would belong more properly in the realm of {\i kodshim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam Variations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} does not immediately provide examples of these {\i kinuyim}; the examples appear only several {\i mishnayot} later, on daf 10. There the {\i mishna} states: {\i ha\u8217'omer konam konach konas harei elu kinnuyim l\u8217'korban} \u8212- one who says {\i konam}, {\i konach}, or {\i konas} has used a {\i kinnuy} for {\i korban}. One who says {\i cherek}, {\i cherech}, or {\i cheref} has used a {\i kinnuy} for {\i cherem}. One who says {\i nazik}, {\i naziach}, or {\i paziach} has used a {\i kinnuy} for {\i nezirut}. And terms like {\i shvuta} or {\i shkuka} are {\i kinuyim} for {\i shevua}. These words are recognizable echoes of their standard counterparts, with key letters altered or substituted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purpose of Kinuyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} addresses the origin and purpose of these invented terms. One possibility is that they derive from some other language \u8212- Aramaic or another Semitic tongue \u8212- that happens to sound similar to the Hebrew with slight variations. But the more straightforward explanation, and the one the {\i Gemara} endorses, is {\i lashon shebadu lahem chachamim} \u8212- it is a language the rabbis invented, a kind of technical terminology deliberately constructed to serve a purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That purpose, as the {\i Gemara} explains, is to prevent the inadvertent utterance of {\i shem Hashem levatala}. If one uses the standard terms \u8212- {\i neder l\u8217'Hashem}, {\i korban l\u8217'Hashem}, {\i shvua l\u8217'Hashem} \u8212- one naturally appends God\u8217's name. By training people to use these unusual technical terms instead, the rabbis reduced the chance of God\u8217's name being used vainly. One might add that the strange and specialized nature of these terms also serves to make the speech act more conscious and deliberate \u8212- a person using {\i konam} knows they are engaging in a formal legal act, and the unfamiliarity of the word may introduce a moment of hesitation that the ordinary vocabulary would not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whatever the precise rationale, the {\i mishna} establishes that using these substitute terms is fully effective: {\i kol kinnuyei nedarim kenedarim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yadot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modrani Mimcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then turns to a second concept, distinct from {\i kinuyim}: the {\i yad}. The {\i mishna} states: {\i ha\u8217'omer l\u8217'chavero modrani mimcha} \u8212- one who says to his fellow \u8220"I am vowed from you,\u8221" {\i mufrashani mimcha} \u8212- \u8220"I am separated from you,\u8221" or {\i meruchakani mimcha} \u8212- \u8220"I am distanced from you\u8221" \u8212- with respect to eating or tasting of his fellow\u8217's food, is {\i assur}, forbidden, from doing so. These formulations, though not standard {\i neder} vocabulary and not {\i kinuyim} in the sense of corrupted euphemisms, are nonetheless effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Variations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The terms {\i mufrashani} and {\i meruchakani} are essentially translations or paraphrases of the concept of a {\i neder} \u8212- expressing the idea of separation or distance from the other person or their property. One might initially think these are additional {\i kinuyim}. The {\i Gemara}, however, distinguishes them: {\i kinuyim} are corrupted or variant phonetic forms of the standard words, while {\i modrani}, {\i mufrashani}, and {\i meruchakani} are ordinary, grammatically correct Hebrew expressions. The {\i Gemara} understands them instead as examples of {\i yadot} \u8212- abbreviated or elliptical statements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a textual question about whether the {\i mishna}\u8217's language should be read as {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} \u8212- \u8220"those things that I eat of yours\u8221" \u8212- or {\i she\u8217'eini ochel lach} \u8212- \u8220"that I will not eat of yours.\u8221" The distinction matters because a {\i neder} is object-focused: what becomes forbidden is the object, the food. If the formulation is \u8220"I will not eat,\u8221" that sounds more like a {\i shvuah}, which is person-focused. The {\i Gemara} will engage with this question, and the precise text will bear on how we understand these abbreviated formulations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Menudeh Ani Lach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} further records a case involving the phrase {\i menudeh ani lach} \u8212- \u8220"I am {\i menudeh} to you\u8221" \u8212- which borrows the language of {\i niduy}, Rabbinic excommunication. This usage is problematic as a {\i neder} formulation because it does not clearly invoke the model of a {\i davar hanadur}, nor does it straightforwardly transform the status of an object. Rabbi Akiva, as the {\i mishna} records, was inclined to rule stringently in such a case \u8212- not certain that it qualifies but not prepared to permit it \u8212- given the difficulty of fitting this language into the standard model of a {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yad Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains the concept underlying the cases of {\i modrani}, {\i mufrashani}, and {\i meruchakani}: these are examples of {\i yadot nedarim}, literally \u8220"handles\u8221" of {\i nedarim}. A {\i yad} is a handle in the sense of an abbreviated or elliptical statement \u8212- one that gestures toward a {\i neder} without articulating all of its required elements. The {\i Gemara} elsewhere discusses this concept in the context of {\i nezirut} as well: if someone is walking by and says \u8220"I will be\u8221" \u8212- stopping there \u8212- and the context makes clear that he meant \u8220"I will be like that {\i nazir},\u8221" the statement may be binding. The degree to which context must supply the missing elements distinguishes between {\i yadayim mochiachos} \u8212- handles that are self-evident from context \u8212- and {\i yadayim she\u8217'einam mochiachos} \u8212- handles where the intended meaning is not obvious and might be understood otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Yad Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} explains precisely why {\i modrani mimcha} and similar formulations qualify as {\i yadot} rather than complete {\i nedarim}. He offers two distinct analyses. The first: {\i mishum delo masik diburei lemeimar kikorban} \u8212- the statement is abbreviated because the speaker did not complete it by saying \u8220"like a {\i korban}.\u8221" On this understanding, the fundamental formulation of a {\i neder} requires the invocation of the {\i korban} model \u8212- {\i davar zeh assur kikorban} or {\i konam davar zeh alay} \u8212- and when that element is absent, the statement is only a {\i yad}, effective because we understand the {\i korban} reference to be the implicit completion of the sentence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second analysis: {\i i nami delo amar issura beheya} \u8212- alternatively, the statement is abbreviated because the speaker did not even say that something is {\i assur}, forbidden. A {\i neder} must change the status of an object; it must predicate {\i issur} of something. \u8220"I am separated from you\u8221" does not explicitly declare anything forbidden. Even setting aside the {\i korban} model, the very predication of prohibited status is missing. On this reading, the {\i yad} is effective because we understand the intent to create a prohibition, even though neither the language of prohibition nor the {\i korban} model was articulated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two analyses correspond to two different questions about what is strictly necessary for a complete {\i neder}: is explicit reference to the {\i korban} model required, or is an explicit statement of {\i issur} required? If one said {\i davar zeh assur alay beneder} \u8212- \u8220"this thing is forbidden to me as a {\i neder}\u8221" \u8212- without invoking the {\i korban} language, is that a complete {\i neder} or itself a {\i yad}? The two positions in the {\i Ran} reflect that underlying question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Object vs.\u160 ?Person\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Listing Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with an apparent discrepancy between our {\i mishna} and the opening {\i mishna} of Masechet Nazir. Our {\i mishna} provides a full list: {\i kol kinnuyei nedarim kenedarim, vacharamim kacharamim, ushvuot keshvuot, unezirut kenezirut} \u8212- all four categories. The opening {\i mishna} of Nazir, by contrast, states only {\i kol kinnuyei nezirut kenezirut} and lists nothing else. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai shna gabei Nazir} \u8212- what is different about Nazir that it does not give the full list? And conversely, what is different about Nedarim that it does give the full list?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i neder} and {\i shvuah} appear together in the same {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i ish ki yidor neder o hishavei shvua} \u8212- and therefore when discussing {\i neder}, one naturally includes {\i shvuah} as well. Once those two are mentioned together, one might as well fill out the complete list by adding {\i cherem} and {\i nezirut}. In Masechet Nazir, where the natural focus is {\i nezirut} alone, there is no comparable reason to list all the others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heftzah vs.\u160 ?Nafsha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this answer immediately generates a further question. If {\i neder} and {\i shvuah} are paired because they appear together in the {\i pasuk}, then the {\i mishna} should have listed them adjacently: {\i neder}, then {\i shvuah}, then the others. Instead, the {\i mishna} lists {\i neder}, then {\i cherem}, and only then {\i shvuah}. Why is {\i cherem} inserted between {\i neder} and {\i shvuah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the {\i Gemara} articulates one of the most fundamental conceptual distinctions of the tractate: {\i neder} and {\i cherem} both operate by making the {\i heftz} \u8212- the object \u8212- forbidden: {\i demis\u8217'ar heftza alei}. A {\i shvuah}, even when it concerns behavior toward an object, operates differently \u8212- {\i de\u8217'kasar nafshia min hacheftza} \u8212- it binds the person, not the object. Therefore, {\i neder} and {\i cherem} are grouped together as members of the same conceptual category, and {\i shvuah} is listed separately, after them. The Torah pairing of {\i neder} and {\i shvuah} explains why {\i shvuah} appears at all in this list; the conceptual distinction between {\i heftz}-focused and {\i nefesh}-focused prohibitions explains why {\i cherem} is placed between them. The {\i Gemara} thus uses this seemingly minor question of listing order to articulate what will prove to be the defining conceptual axis of the entire tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinuyim First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a second structural question about the {\i mishna}: having established that the {\i mishna} discusses both {\i kinuyim} and {\i yadot}, why does it first mention the rule of {\i kinuyim} and then proceed directly to give examples of {\i yadot} \u8212- without first offering examples of {\i kinuyim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is genuinely puzzling. The {\i mishna} opens with the general principle that {\i kinuyim} are effective. One would expect it then to illustrate that rule with examples of {\i kinuyim}, and only afterwards introduce the separate concept of {\i yadot} with its examples. Instead it introduces the rule of {\i kinuyim} and jumps immediately to examples of {\i yadot} \u8212- examples that won\u8217't be given until several {\i mishnayot} later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's solution is that the {\i mishna} is a case of {\i chasorei mechasra} \u8212- the text is incomplete and must be supplemented. Reading the {\i mishna} as it stands is impossible without inserting a line that was implied but not written. The corrected reading should be: {\i kol kinnuyei nedarim kenedarim, v\u8217'yadot nedarim kenedarim} \u8212- all {\i kinuyim} of {\i nedarim} are like {\i nedarim}, and all {\i yadot} of {\i nedarim} are like {\i nedarim}. Once that missing line is understood to be present \u8212- at least implicitly \u8212- the {\i mishna} follows the chiastic ABBA structure: it states the rules for A ({\i kinuyim}) and B ({\i yadot}), and then provides examples beginning with B (the {\i yadot} examples, which follow immediately), returning to A ({\i kinuyim}) only in the later {\i mishna}. It chose to discuss B first in the examples because it had, in its implicit final clause, most recently mentioned {\i yadot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony that the {\i Gemara} acknowledges is that the entire argument for ABBA structure depends on an inserted line that was not actually there \u8212- it is a {\i yad}, one might say, in the {\i mishna} itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chiastic Patterns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 ABBA vs.\u160 ?ABAB\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following Rashi\u8217's invocation of the ABBA chiastic structure, the {\i Gemara} explores this pattern more broadly. It asks whether the rule is that a {\i mishna} always gives examples beginning with the item most recently mentioned \u8212- the ABBA pattern \u8212- or whether sometimes it begins with the first item mentioned, producing an ABAB structure. The {\i Gemara} brings a series of examples from across the {\i mishnah} to examine both patterns.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avot Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three opening chapters of Masechet Shabbat provide the clearest examples of ABBA. {\i Bameh Madlikin u\u8217'vameh ein Madlikin} \u8212- with what may one light Shabbat candles and with what may one not? The {\i mishna} then begins its discussion with {\i ein madlikin}, the \u8220"may not\u8221" category \u8212- that is, B before A in the examples, having stated A then B in the rule. Similarly, {\i Bameh Tomnin u\u8217'vameh ein Tomnin} \u8212- with what may one insulate food and with what not? \u8212- followed by examples of what one may not use, again B first. And {\i Bameh isha yotzah u\u8217'vameh einah yotzah} \u8212- what may a woman wear in the public domain on Shabbat and what may she not \u8212- again followed by examples of what she may not wear, the second-mentioned category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nochlim Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against these examples, the {\i Gemara} brings cases that follow the ABAB pattern. The {\i mishna} in Bava Batra: {\i yesh nochlin u\u8217'manchilim} \u8212- some inherit and bequeath \u8212- {\i nochlin velo manchilim} \u8212- some inherit but do not bequeath. The examples then begin with {\i v\u8217'elu nochlin u\u8217'manchilim} \u8212- those who both inherit and bequeath \u8212- the first-mentioned category. That is ABAB. An example of someone who inherits but does not bequeath would be a son relative to his mother: a son inherits from his mother, but the mother does not inherit from the son. Meanwhile a father and son illustrate the first category: a son inherits from his father, and if the son predeceases his father without other heirs, the father inherits from the son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel structure appears in Yevamot: {\i yesh mutarot l\u8217'va\u8217'aleihen va\u8217'asurot l\u8217'yivmeihen} \u8212- some wives are permitted to their husbands but forbidden to their brothers-in-law for {\i yibum} \u8212- {\i mutarot l\u8217'yivmeihen va\u8217'asurot l\u8217'va\u8217'aleihen} \u8212- and some are the reverse. A widow ({\i almanah}) married to a {\i kohen} whose husband\u8217's brother is a {\i kohen gadol} illustrates the first: she was permitted to her husband but the {\i kohen gadol} cannot perform {\i yibum}. A widow married to a {\i kohen gadol} illustrates the second: the marriage binds but she is forbidden to her husband (as a {\i kohen gadol} may not marry a widow), yet his ordinary-{\i kohen} brother can perform {\i yibum}. The {\i mishna} then begins its examples with the first category \u8212- again ABAB.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mincha Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the laws of {\i menachot}: {\i yesh te\u8217'unah shemen u\u8217'levonah} \u8212- some {\i menachot} require both oil and frankincense \u8212- {\i shemen v\u8217'lo levonah} \u8212- some require oil but not frankincense. The {\i mishna} then provides examples of those requiring both \u8212- ABAB again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i yesh te\u8217'unah hagashah v\u8217'ein te\u8217'unah tenufah} \u8212- some offerings require {\i hagashah}, bringing them to the corner of the altar, but not {\i tenufah}, waving \u8212- {\i tenufah v\u8217'lo hagashah} \u8212- and some require waving but not {\i hagashah}. The examples begin with the first category \u8212- again ABAB.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the laws of the firstborn: {\i yesh bechor l\u8217'nachala v\u8217'ein bechor l\u8217'Kohen} \u8212- some are considered firstborn for inheritance purposes but not for {\i pidyon haben} \u8212- {\i bechor l\u8217'Kohen v\u8217'ein bechor l\u8217'nachala} \u8212- and some are firstborn for {\i pidyon haben} but not for inheritance. A boy born after a miscarriage is the first viable son, so he receives the double inheritance portion, but since he did not breach his mother\u8217's womb he does not require {\i pidyon haben}. Conversely, a {\i kohen} who already has a firstborn son and then has another son by a different wife: that second son is the {\i peter rechem} of his mother and requires {\i pidyon haben}, but since he is not the firstborn of his father he does not receive a double portion. The {\i mishna} begins its examples with the first category \u8212- ABAB once more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Length-Based Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Facing this array of contradictory cases, the {\i Gemara} proposes a rule: {\i hanei mishum d\u8217'avshei lehu mefaresh hahu depatach berisha} \u8212- those that follow ABAB do so because the discussion of each category is long. In a chiastic ABBA structure, by the time you have given the rule for A, then B, and then a lengthy discussion of B examples, the reader will have lost track of what A was. The ABBA structure only works when the discussion is compact enough that the final return to A does not come so late as to be disorienting. When the treatment of B is going to be extensive, the {\i mishna} must restate A before going on to B, producing ABAB. The cases from Shabbat \u8212- the candles, the insulation, the woman\u8217's garments \u8212- are all relatively brief and thus can sustain the ABBA structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Fixed Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i Gemara} notes a counterexample: the {\i mishna} regarding what animals may wear on Shabbat ({\i yotzei gamal} and similar) also follows ABAB despite involving only a brief list. This undermines the proposed rule, since brevity alone does not determine the choice of structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes accordingly: {\i ella lav davka} \u8212- there is in fact no fixed rule. {\i Zimnin de-mafarash de-patach be-reisha} \u8212- sometimes the {\i mishna} gives examples beginning with the first-mentioned category, producing ABAB \u8212- {\i zimnin hahu de-sayeim mafarash be-reisha} \u8212- and sometimes it gives examples beginning with the last-mentioned category, producing ABBA. The {\i mishna} of Nedarim chose to present its examples in ABBA fashion, beginning with {\i yadot} before {\i kinuyim} \u8212- even though, as noted, the clause about {\i yadot} was implicit rather than explicit \u8212- and both patterns are legitimate. With that, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's opening structural digression concludes, and the tractate will proceed to substantive analysis of {\i yadot} and {\i kinuyim} beginning tomorrow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shabbat Lamplight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading by Lamplight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a {\i halacha} found in the {\i gemara} in {\i Masechet Shabbat} that a person is not permitted to read by the light of an oil lamp on {\i Shabbat}. The reason the {\i chachamim} gave for this is a {\i gezeirah} of {\i shema yiteh} \u8212- lest a person forget that it is {\i Shabbat} and tilt the lamp in order to bring the oil closer to the wick, which would constitute a violation of the {\i melacha} of {\i mavir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Electric Lights Today\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question naturally arises as to whether this {\i halacha} applies nowadays when we have electric lights. The majority of {\i poskim} rule that it does not apply, both because there is no analogous way to tilt an electric lamp to make the light stronger, and because we are so accustomed to electric lights that the original concern simply does not obtain. Nevertheless, some are {\i machmir} and maintain that one should still exercise caution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Muktzeh Candles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another important concept in this area concerns the {\i muktzeh} status of candles. If a candle was lit during {\i bein hashmashos} \u8212- the twilight period between Friday and {\i Shabbat} \u8212- it becomes {\i muktzeh} for the entire day. This follows from the principle of {\i migo d\u8217'itaktzai l\u8217'bein hashmashos itaktzai l\u8217'kuly yoma}: since the object was set aside at the onset of the holy day, that status persists through the whole of {\i Shabbat}. Accordingly, even if the candle goes out in the middle of the night, one still may not move the candlestick, because it was a {\i basis l\u8217'davar assur} \u8212- a base for a forbidden object \u8212- at the moment {\i Shabbat} began.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyan: Gold & Silve (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gold Acquires Silver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the bottom of the preceding page \u8212- 46b \u8212- the {\i mishnah} states: {\i haZahav koneh et haKesef vehaKesef eino koneh et haZahav}, gold acquires silver, but silver does not acquire gold. According to the {\i mishnah}, gold is considered {\i peira} \u8212- a commodity \u8212- while silver is considered {\i matbeia}, currency. The governing rule of {\i kinyanim} is that the commodity acquires the currency: if one performs {\i meshichah} on the commodity, the currency is acquired by the seller wherever it may be. But if one pulls the currency, the commodity is not acquired to the buyer until the buyer separately performs {\i meshichah} on the commodity itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyan: Gold & Silve (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Levi Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 47a asks why gold should be classified as the commodity. A {\i machloket} emerges between Rav and Levi on this point. One holds that gold is {\i matbeia} \u8212- currency \u8212- because it is more valuable, while the other holds that silver is {\i matbeia} because it is more common. The {\i halacha} follows the view that silver is the currency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshichah Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} further establishes that {\i mi-de\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- on the level of biblical law \u8212- {\i maot konot}: money itself constitutes a valid {\i kinyan}. However, the {\i chachamim} enacted a {\i takana} requiring {\i meshichah} to complete the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buyer Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i takana} was enacted to protect the buyer in the event of a fire \u8212- a principle expressed by the phrase {\i nisrefu peirotecha ba\u8217'aliyah}. So long as the seller retains responsibility for the goods, he will exert himself to save them. If, however, a party retracts after having given money, the {\i beit din} subjects him to the curse of {\i Mi ShePara}, declaring that the One who punished the generations of the flood and the dispersion will punish one who does not keep his word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Taanis Tzibur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Fasts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} in {\i Masechet Ta\u8217'anit} addresses the institution of {\i taanis tzibur}. When a {\i tzarah} befalls {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- such as a drought \u8212- the {\i beit din} would declare a series of public fasts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hissorerus Address\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 During these periods of communal crisis, the people would gather in the {\i rechovah shel ir}, the city square, where a {\i zaken} would deliver words of {\i hissorerus} to inspire the community to engage in genuine {\i teshuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aron & Ashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As part of this assembly, the {\i aron} would be brought out and {\i eifer} placed upon it, expressing the community\u8217's distress and {\i hachna\u8217'ah} before Hashem. The {\i sak} \u8212- sackcloth \u8212- was also part of this public display of submission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tikun Ma\u8217'asim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} emphasizes, however, that it is not the {\i sak} or the {\i taanis} itself that brings forgiveness, but rather the {\i tikun hama\u8217'asim} \u8212- the genuine improvement of one\u8217's actions. This is the true goal of any {\i taanis}: to move a person toward authentic {\i chazara b\u8217'teshuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nineveh Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is illustrated from the story of Nineveh. The {\i posuk} does not say that {\i Elokim} saw their fasting; rather, it says {\i vayar Elokim es ma\u8217'aseihem} \u8212- He saw their deeds. The emphasis on actions over mere fasting is explicit and instructive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Between Man & God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We must examine our behavior and ensure we are conducting ourselves appropriately in {\i bein adam l\u8217'chavero} and {\i bein adam l\u8217'makom}. Through this dual rectification, we merit the {\i rachamim} of the {\i Borei Olam} and, ultimately, the {\i geulah sheleimah} speedily in our days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sukkah & Ohel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a pointed question against Rav: if Rav holds that even a {\i sukkah} higher than twenty {\i amot} can be valid when its {\i defanos} reach all the way up to the {\i sechach}, why is it that in the case of a bed one requires {\i asara tefachim}? Even if the bed stood less than {\i asara tefachim} high, it should already be considered an {\i ohel} \u8212- for according to Rav, a space of {\i asara tefachim} is not a prerequisite for {\i ohel} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent of Walls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers on Rav\u8217's behalf: {\i hasam defanos le\u8217'ohel avidi} \u8212- there, in the case of the {\i sukkah}, the {\i defanos} are constructed for the sake of the {\i ohel}. But in the case of the bed: {\i hacha karayei hamita lav le\u8217'ohel avidi} \u8212- the legs of the bed are not made for the sake of forming an {\i ohel}. Since they were not constructed with {\i ohel} intent, they can only confer {\i ohel} status under specific conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asara Tefachim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the bed legs reach a height of {\i asara tefachim}, the space beneath them is considered significant enough to constitute an {\i ohel} regardless of the original purpose for which the legs were made. But if the height falls short of {\i asara tefachim} and the structure was not constructed for {\i ohel} purposes, it does not attain {\i ohel} status. Consequently, it would not create a {\i chatzitzah}, and one would fulfill the relevant obligation. This is how Rav would answer Rava\u8217's challenge from the later {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elul & Rosh Hashanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birkat HaChodesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a well-established custom to recite a special prayer on the {\i Shabbat} before the beginning of each new month, petitioning Hashem to bless the coming month with life, peace, health, and prosperity. This prayer is known as {\i Birkat HaChodesh}. Yet one month each year is conspicuously absent from this practice: the month of {\i Tishrei}. On the {\i Shabbat} before {\i Rosh Hashanah}, we do not recite {\i Birkat HaChodesh}. Since {\i Rosh Hashanah} is {\i Rosh Chodesh} \u8212- the first day of {\i Tishrei} \u8212- the question demands an explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elul as Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rebbe, Rabbi Menachem Mendel of Rymanov, explains that we do not bless the month of {\i Tishrei} because the entire preceding month \u8212- the month of {\i Elul} \u8212- is itself one long blessing for {\i Tishrei}. {\i Elul} functions as the blessing; the month of preparation is the very vehicle through which {\i Tishrei} receives its sanctity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elul Preparation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The month of {\i Elul} is a month of preparation, of introspection, and of {\i teshuva}. Through the work we undertake during {\i Elul}, we provide the blessing for the coming year. These days are designed for reflection: we look back at the year that has passed, consider the choices we have made, the actions we have taken, and the words we have spoken, and we ask ourselves where we can improve and how we can grow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal Reflection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the essence of {\i teshuva} \u8212- often translated as repentance, but more precisely meaning return: a return to our true selves and a return to our relationship with the Almighty. We look at the areas where we have fallen short and ask honestly how we can become more aligned with who we truly wish to be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Teshuva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam teaches that {\i teshuva} involves several distinct steps: {\i viduy} \u8212- verbalizing one\u8217's mistakes; {\i charata} \u8212- feeling sincere regret; and {\i kabbalah al ha-atid} \u8212- making a firm commitment to do better in the future. Yet the goal is not merely to repair the past; the goal is to genuinely transform oneself for the future.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blessing Year Ahead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As we draw closer to {\i Rosh Hashanah} and {\i Yom Kippur}, we should take full advantage of these final days of {\i Elul}. The coming year is blessed through our own efforts and growth. May we all be inscribed and sealed in the book of life for a good, healthy, and meaningful year \u8212- {\i ketiva v\u8217'chatima tova}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neshama vs.\u160 ?Guf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neshama Tikkun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i neshama} descends from a very elevated place specifically in order to perform its {\i tikkun}. We observe that different people are drawn to different {\i mitzvot} with greater intensity than others, and this is no accident \u8212- it reflects the particular {\i tikkun} that each {\i neshama} has come to accomplish. A person must recognize that the reason he is in this world is to fix something specific, and that this specificity is meaningful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arizal Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Arizal HaKadosh teaches that certain {\i mitzvot} call to a person more powerfully than they do to others \u8212- not that they are easier, but that one finds oneself drawn to be especially careful and exacting in their performance. That particular {\i mitzvah} is very likely one\u8217's {\i tikkun}. And conversely, something that a person finds exceptionally difficult may also be part of what he must rectify \u8212- whether it manifests as a love for a {\i mitzvah} or as a struggle against a particular inclination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neshama vs.\u160 ?Guf (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neshama as Royalty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Chovos HaLevavos, in {\i Sha\u8217'ar HaBechina}, Rabbeinu Bachya describes the {\i neshama} as royalty \u8212- like a king or queen \u8212- that comes from a supremely elevated realm. When it is placed within the {\i guf}, it finds itself in a kind of prison: something lowly, physical, and coarse. The {\i guf} desires the physical and the mundane; the {\i neshama} yearns for the spiritual and the elevated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Soul-Body Battle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is a constant battle between {\i neshama} and {\i guf}. The {\i guf} pulls downward; the {\i neshama} reaches upward. The work of a person in this world is precisely to make the {\i guf} subservient to the {\i neshama} \u8212- to render the physical subordinate to the spiritual. This is the central task of human existence, and it is the reason the battle exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reward & Struggle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nor should one be discouraged by the difficulty of this struggle, for the greater the struggle, the greater the reward: {\i lefum tza\u8217'ara agra} \u8212- according to the pain, so is the reward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parshat Vayera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avraham\u8217's Guests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This week\u8217's {\i parsha} is {\i Parshat Vayera}. At its opening, the {\i posuk} states {\i Vayera Elav Hashem}. Rashi explains that it was the third day following the {\i brit milah} of Avraham Avinu, and despite the intense heat of the day \u8212- {\i k\u8217'chom hayom} \u8212- Avraham was actively searching for guests to welcome. When three {\i malachim} appeared in the guise of travelers, he ran to greet them. This exemplifies the {\i midah} of {\i chesed} and the practice of {\i hachnasat orchim} at its most exalted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orchim Greatness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking {\i gemara}: the {\i gemara} in {\i Masechet Shabbat} states {\i gedolah hachnasat orchim mi\u8217'kabalat pnei hashechina} \u8212- welcoming guests is even greater than receiving the Divine Presence. Avraham\u8217's conduct in the opening of {\i Parshat Vayera} stands as the paradigmatic illustration of this teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Animals Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restraint Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fifth chapter of {\i Masechet Shabbat}, {\i Perek Bameh Behemah}, opens with the question: with what may an animal go out on {\i Shabbat}, and with what may it not go out? The {\i mishnah} enumerates: a camel may go out with a halter ({\i afsana}); a dromedary with a nose ring ({\i chotem}); a Libyan donkey with a bit ({\i farumbiya}); and a horse with a chain ({\i shir}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chains Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further states: {\i ve-chol ba\u8217'alei ha-shir yotze\u8217'im be-shir ve-nimshachim be-shir} \u8212- all animals that have chains go out with a chain and are led by the chain. One may sprinkle on them or immerse them in their places ({\i u-mazin aleihen ve-tovlin bi-mekoman}) if they become {\i tamei}. This provision applies because the metal chains are susceptible to {\i tumah} by virtue of their status as a {\i kli} \u8212- a vessel \u8212- and yet the {\i mishnah} permits immersing them in a {\i mikvah} while they remain on the animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Rest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying concern throughout is the prohibition against an animal carrying a burden on {\i Shabbat}. The {\i Torah} commands {\i lema\u8217'an yanuach shorecha va-chamorecha} \u8212- so that one\u8217's ox and donkey may rest \u8212- which means an animal may not perform {\i melacha} on {\i Shabbat}. Carrying a {\i masa}, a burden, in the {\i reshus ha-rabim} constitutes {\i melacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Burden Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if an animal is wearing something for its own protection or to prevent it from running away, that item is not a {\i masa} but a {\i tachshit} \u8212- an ornament, or necessary equipment. The distinction between a {\i masa} and a {\i tachshit} is critical: if more restraint is placed on an animal than it actually requires, the excess weight becomes a burden. A heavy halter placed on a small goat, for instance, is not necessary for restraint and therefore constitutes a prohibited {\i masa}; the same halter on a camel is indispensable and therefore permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restraint Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains the specific restraints listed in the {\i mishnah} with reference to the nature of each animal. The {\i afsana} for a camel is a simple rope halter, sufficient for its control. A dromedary, however, is considerably more difficult to lead, and therefore requires the {\i chotem} \u8212- a ring passed through the nose \u8212- which provides the necessary degree of control. Each animal\u8217's permitted restraint is calibrated to what is genuinely required for its management.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water & Stone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva and his wife Rachel chose to endure the most difficult conditions, understanding that they were building their foundation upon {\i Torah} and upon learning. Rabbi Akiva was not always a great scholar; for many years he was a simple shepherd who could not even read the {\i alef-bet}. One day, while tending his flock, he noticed a stone with a deep hole carved into it. Upon being told that the hole had been worn by water dripping on it day after day, Rabbi Akiva drew the lesson: if soft water can pierce hard stone through sheer persistence, then the words of {\i Torah} \u8212- which are like iron \u8212- can certainly penetrate his heart.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning Begins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the age of forty, Rabbi Akiva went to the schoolhouse with his young son and the two of them began to learn the {\i alef-bet} together, starting completely from the beginning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Study Success\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He spent the next twenty-four years in intensive study until he became the leader of tens of thousands of {\i talmidim}. What began as an adult learning the {\i alef-bet} with his child culminated in one of the greatest rabbinic careers in Jewish history.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rachel\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabbi Akiva finally returned home, he told his students that all of his {\i Torah} knowledge was only thanks to his wife Rachel. His words were direct and unambiguous: {\i sheli v\u8217'shelachem shela hu} \u8212- everything that I have and everything that you have belongs to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosef Na\u8217'ar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef Grooming\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i posuk} describes Yosef as a {\i na\u8217'ar} \u8212- a young boy \u8212- in the phrase {\i v\u8217'hu na\u8217'ar et bnei Bilhah v\u8217'et bnei Zilpah}. Rashi explains that this refers to {\i na\u8217'arut}: Yosef would fix his eyes, tend to his hair, and conduct himself with the bearing of a young man conscious of his appearance. The question is striking \u8212- what was a {\i tzaddik} of Yosef\u8217's stature doing concerning himself with his grooming while enslaved in Egypt, while his father mourned him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simcha Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that Yosef understood, with great wisdom, that in order to survive his situation, he needed to maintain {\i simcha} and a sense of self-worth. Had he allowed himself to deteriorate, had he succumbed to depression and ceased to care about himself, he would never have been able to withstand the {\i nisayon} of {\i Eshet Potiphar}, and he would never have risen to become the ruler of {\i Mitzrayim}. He needed to keep himself together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Melech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yosef\u8217's self-presentation was not an expression of {\i ga\u8217'avah} \u8212- it was not vanity or self-absorption. It was a tool for survival. He reminded himself: I am a {\i ben melech}, a son of Yaakov Avinu; I am of worth; I matter. That sense of identity and dignity kept him going. This carries a vital lesson for us: when we find ourselves in difficult circumstances, in something of a {\i golus}, we must keep our heads high, remember that we are children of the King, and maintain that {\i simcha} \u8212- because {\i simcha} is the very means by which we overcome the challenges we face.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chanukah Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Greek Threat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This theme connects directly to {\i Chanukah}. The Greeks sought to erase Jewish identity \u8212- to strip away our sense of self-worth and make us feel indistinct from everyone else. Their assault was not merely physical but existential: they wanted us to believe we were no different, no more special, no more connected to anything beyond the material world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chashmonaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chashmonaim} refused. Their response was an unequivocal assertion: we are {\i kadosh}, we are different, and we have a connection to the {\i Ribbono Shel Olam} that cannot be severed. Their resistance was grounded not in politics but in identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanukah Simcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What we celebrate on {\i Chanukah} is precisely that sense of identity and that {\i simcha} \u8212- the joy of knowing who we are and refusing to relinquish it. Wishing everyone a {\i freilichen Chanukah} and a {\i gut Shabbos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aseret Yemei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judgment Sincerity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah Berurah} explains that during these days, when we stand before {\i HaKadosh Baruch Hu} in judgment, we wish to demonstrate that we are taking our {\i mitzvot} seriously \u8212- that our {\i teshuva} is genuine and not merely performative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chumrot Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the ways we express this sincerity is by adopting {\i chumrot} during the {\i Aseret Yemei Teshuva} \u8212- stringencies that go beyond our ordinary year-round practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pas Yisroel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A classic example is {\i pas Yisroel}. Many people rely all year on the more lenient position and eat {\i pas paltar} \u8212- bread baked by a non-Jewish baker. During the {\i Aseret Yemei Teshuva}, however, the practice is to be more careful to eat only {\i pas Yisroel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sincere Effort\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a concrete way of showing Hashem that we are making an effort \u8212- that we wish to live more carefully and more holily. It is not about the bread itself; it is about the gesture of striving beyond the baseline requirement of {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yesod Avodas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a fundamental {\i yesod} in the {\i avodas Hashem} of these days. We want to demonstrate that our {\i teshuva} is sincere and that we are willing to go beyond the minimal requirement in order to find greater {\i chen} in the eyes of the {\i Ribbono Shel Olam}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 4\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yad l\u8217'Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yad Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens approximately six lines down, with Rav Ashi\u8217's statement, picking up from the {\i baraita} that established the parallel between {\i yad l\u8217'nazir} and {\i yad l\u8217'nedarim} \u8212- that is, {\i l\u8217'hazir l\u8217'hazir}, the principle that an abbreviated statement counts as a full statement. This was the crucial point developed on the previous daf: the concept of {\i yad}, by which an incomplete verbal formulation is nonetheless legally effective as a {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thought & Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Underlying this discussion is the recognition that a valid {\i neder} requires two distinct components: correct thought and correct speech. One must say the right thing, and one must intend the right thing. The focus now shifts to what constitutes \u8220"the right thing to have said\u8221" \u8212- specifically, whether a complete verbal formulation is required or whether an abbreviated statement suffices. The principle of {\i yad} answers that an abbreviated statement can, under the right conditions, serve as a complete and binding declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbreviated Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} is equally concerned with establishing that just as there is {\i bal taachar} in the context of {\i nedarim} \u8212- the prohibition of postponing the fulfillment of a vow \u8212- so too does {\i bal taachar} apply to {\i nezirut}. The parallel drawn between {\i neder} and {\i nazir} thus operates on two axes simultaneously: the effectiveness of abbreviated speech, and the obligation to fulfill one\u8217's commitment without delay.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bal Taachar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Link to Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the {\i yad} framework, the {\i Gemara} turns to the application of {\i bal taachar} to {\i nezirut}. The prohibition of postponement, classically understood as the obligation not to delay bringing a {\i korban} past three {\i regalim}, is extended here to the domain of {\i nezirut}. The same way one transgresses {\i bal taachar} by delaying a vow of sacrifice, one transgresses it by improperly delaying the commencement or proper continuation of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Postponement Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question immediately arises: since accepting {\i nezirut} takes effect automatically upon utterance, how can there be a scenario in which one is obligated to be a {\i nazir} but has not yet become one \u8212- the very precondition for {\i bal taachar}? Two scenarios were proposed. First, if someone declared \u8220"I will become a {\i nazir} before I die,\u8221" then every passing day on which he has not yet accepted {\i nezirut} constitutes a potential postponement, since he might die at any moment; each day of delay is thus a separate violation of {\i bal taachar}, even as the {\i nezirut} has not yet formally begun. Second, if one declared {\i nezirut} while standing inside a {\i beit hakevarot} (a cemetery), the {\i nezirut} may be delayed from taking proper effect by virtue of his remaining in that impure location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cemetery Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} went further in the cemetery case: even if the {\i nezirut} does take effect in the cemetery, by remaining there one delays the transition from {\i nezirut d\u8217'tumah} \u8212- the nazirite state under conditions of impurity \u8212- to {\i nezirut d\u8217'taharah}, the proper nazirite state of purity. The implication is striking: {\i bal taachar} is not merely about initiating {\i nezirut}, but about initiating {\i nezirut} in its proper form. It is insufficient to have technically begun; one must begin correctly, and delay in achieving that proper beginning is itself a violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Chiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi extends this principle further with a significant {\i chiddush}. He rules that a {\i nazir} who intentionally renders himself {\i tamei} \u8212- impure \u8212- transgresses not only the prohibition against a {\i nazir} becoming {\i tamei}, but also the prohibition of {\i bal taachar d\u8217'nezirut d\u8217'taharah}. The reason is that intentional {\i tumah} forces the {\i nazir} to lose all days counted until that point \u8212- {\i hayomim harishonim yiplu ki tamei nizro} \u8212- and to restart the entire count from the beginning after completing a process of purification. Since the entire period of {\i nezirut d\u8217'taharah} must effectively begin anew, the gap between the original declaration of {\i harei ani nazir} and the eventual restart of the proper count constitutes a postponement of the beginning of {\i nezirut d\u8217'taharah}. The {\i chiddush} is that this applies even though the {\i nazir} did formally begin his {\i nezirut} weeks earlier \u8212- since the count must be entirely redone, the original start effectively did not count, and the substantial gap between declaration and the re-commencement of the proper {\i nezirut} is treated as {\i bal taachar}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Giluach & Bal Taachar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha son of Rav Ika offers a different account of what constitutes {\i bal taachar} in the context of {\i nezirut}. In his view, the violation consists of postponing the {\i tighluach} \u8212- the shaving of the head that marks the conclusion of {\i nezirut}. Here, the concern is not with delaying the {\i beginning} of {\i nezirut} but with delaying its {\i end}. Once the period of {\i nezirut} is complete, the {\i nazir} is obligated to shave his head and bring his sacrifices, and postponing this ritual constitutes a violation of {\i bal taachar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nature of Giluach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes the conceptual ambiguity surrounding {\i giluach}. One might view the shaving as an essential ritual that itself terminates the {\i nezirut}; according to this view, delaying {\i giluach} directly delays the conclusion of {\i nezirut}. Alternatively, one might understand {\i giluach} not as constitutive of the ending but rather as an independent {\i mitzvah} performed in the {\i Beis HaMikdash} at the close of {\i nezirut}. According to this second view, {\i Abaye} and {\i Rava} each rule in accordance with their respective positions: the one who holds that {\i giluach} is indispensable ({\i me\u8217'akev}) for ending {\i nezirut} would naturally say that postponing it is {\i bal taachar} of {\i nezirut} itself; while even the one who holds that {\i giluach} is {\i not} indispensable agrees that one transgresses {\i bal taachar} of the {\i mitzvah} of shaving by delaying it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giluach Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This remains a conceptually strange category of {\i bal taachar}. Classic {\i bal taachar} involves the postponement of something given or consecrated to God \u8212- a freely accepted obligation in the mold of a {\i korban}. The {\i nezirut} itself can be understood as giving oneself to God ({\i l\u8217'hazir l\u8217'Hashem}). But postponing the ritual of {\i giluach} \u8212- which is either the act of ending {\i nezirut} or an accompanying ritual in the {\i Beis HaMikdash} \u8212- sits uneasily within this framework. It is not an act of giving to God; it is, at most, a procedural rite that concludes a previously accepted state. The {\i Gemara} acknowledges this difficulty and proceeds to consider a more intuitively natural account of {\i bal taachar} in the context of {\i nezirut}: the postponement of the {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korbanot & Bal Taachar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra son of Rav Ashi offers the most straightforward explanation: when we say that a {\i nazir} transgresses {\i bal taachar}, what we mean is that he has postponed bringing his {\i korbanot}. A {\i nazir} who has completed his {\i nezirut} is obligated to bring three sacrifices \u8212- an {\i olah}, a {\i chatat}, and a {\i shelamim} \u8212- and if he delays past three {\i regalim}, he violates {\i bal taachar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special vs.\u160 ?General\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this explanation: if {\i bal taachar} applies to all obligatory {\i korbanot} \u8212- a principle derivable from general {\i halacha} \u8212- why does the {\i Torah} need to link {\i nazir} to {\i neder} at all? The answer should already be obvious from the general rule. This pushes the {\i Gemara} to examine the textual derivation of {\i bal taachar} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesukim Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the relevant {\i pesukim}. The simple reading of the verse is {\i ki sidor neder l\u8217'Hashem Elokecha lo t\u8217'acher l\u8217'shalmo} \u8212- when you make a free-will vow to God, do not delay fulfilling it. The plain sense thus limits {\i bal taachar} to freely accepted obligations, {\i nedarim} and {\i nedavot}, not to {\i korbanot} that the {\i Torah} itself imposes as a consequence of sin or circumstance. However, the doubled language \u8212- {\i darosh yidreshenu} \u8212- extends the prohibition: God will {\i demand} it of you, implying that even {\i korbanot} that are demanded by the {\i Torah} (such as {\i chataot} and {\i ashamot}) fall under {\i bal taachar}. Once a {\i chiyuv korban} exists, whether arising from a freely accepted vow or from a Torah-imposed obligation, one who postpones it transgresses {\i bal taachar}. Given this general principle, of course there would be {\i bal taachar} for the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir} \u8212- no special derivation should be needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatat Chelev\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} reinforces this point by invoking the {\i chatat chelev} \u8212- the sin-offering brought by one who inadvertently ate forbidden fat, a paradigmatic sin carrying {\i chiyuv karet}. One cannot bring a {\i chatat chelev} through a {\i neder}; it is only possible if one has actually committed the relevant sin. And yet there is clearly {\i bal taachar} by a {\i chatat chelev}. This proves that {\i bal taachar} applies to obligatory {\i korbanot} entirely outside the category of {\i neder}. Thus, the {\i nazir\u8217's korbanot} \u8212- being Torah-mandated obligations \u8212- should self-evidently fall under {\i bal taachar}, and we still have not identified what {\i chiddush} the linking of {\i nazir} to {\i neder} was meant to teach.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Korban Chiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hypothetical Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presses the question: what was the {\i chiddush} that the {\i Torah} needed to teach explicitly regarding {\i bal taachar} and the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir}? Two answers are proposed, each identifying a reason one might have thought that the {\i nazir\u8217's korbanot} specifically would {\i not} be subject to {\i bal taachar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Package Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first answer: {\i nezirut} is an unusual, package-like category. The {\i Gemara} cites the ruling that if someone declares {\i hareini nazir min hachartzan} \u8212- I am a {\i nazir} from [eating] the pits of the grapes \u8212- he becomes a {\i nazir} in the full sense, obligated in all the restrictions of {\i nezirut}, even though he articulated only one component. The Rabbis and Rabbi Shimon debate what happens in such a case: the {\i Chachamim} hold that he acquires the entire package, while Rabbi Shimon holds that since he did not accept all the obligations, he acquires nothing. Crucially, both agree that {\i nezirut} operates as an indivisible package \u8212- nobody holds that one can be a {\i nazir} with respect to grapes alone. The debate is only over whether a partial acceptance triggers the full package or triggers nothing. Given this all-or-nothing structure, one might have argued that because {\i nezirut} is such an anomalous category, {\i bal taachar} would not apply to its {\i korbanot} in the ordinary way. The {\i Gemara}, however, rejects the logical force of this reasoning: the strange, encompassing nature of {\i nezirut} is not a reason to conclude that one would not transgress {\i bal taachar} for postponing its {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weighting Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second and more compelling answer is introduced: {\i hanicha l\u8217'man d\u8217'amar i gilach al achat mi-shloshtan yatza} \u8212- since a {\i nazir} who performs the {\i giluach} in conjunction with any one of the three {\i korbanot} may be considered to have discharged the essential end of his {\i nezirut}, the remaining two {\i korbanot} have a diminished sense of urgency. Having in some sense already \u8220"finished,\u8221" there is less felt pressure to bring the remaining {\i korbanot} promptly. One might therefore have thought that the obligation to bring these remaining sacrifices lacks sufficient weight to generate a {\i bal taachar} violation upon delay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lack of Urgency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This line of reasoning is further sharpened by comparing the {\i nazir\u8217's korbanot} to other obligatory {\i korbanot}. When one sins and is obligated to bring a {\i chatat} for {\i kaparah} \u8212- atonement \u8212- there is an urgent underlying pressure: one is in a state of unremedied sin and needs {\i kaparah}. When one is {\i tamei} and needs to bring a {\i korban} to return to a state of {\i taharah}, there is again an urgent underlying pressure: the desire \u8212- at least conceptually \u8212- to be able to eat {\i kodashim} and enter the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. Both of these cases involve not merely a formal {\i chiyuv} but an existential pressure that makes the postponement genuinely weighty. The {\i nazir\u8217's} remaining {\i korbanot}, by contrast, involve no such urgency. There is no sin requiring atonement \u8212- even the {\i chatat nazir}, while it exists, is best understood (as the {\i Rambam} explains) not as atonement per se but as a change of personal status \u8212- and the {\i nezirut} has in some sense already concluded. One might therefore have thought that the obligation to bring the remaining {\i korbanot} is insufficiently weighty to generate {\i bal taachar}, since postponing them involves no deferred {\i kaparah} and no unresolved {\i tumah}. The {\i Torah\u8217's} linkage of {\i nazir} to {\i neder} therefore teaches \u8212- {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- that even these relatively \u8220"weightless\u8221" obligatory {\i korbanot} are subject to {\i bal taachar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second formulation reinforces the same conclusion from a different angle: since the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir} cannot be created through a {\i neder} \u8212- one cannot say {\i harei zo alai chatat nazir} and thereby obligate oneself independently, since the {\i chatat nazir} is only available to one who is actually a {\i nazir} under {\i chiyuv} \u8212- one might have thought that since {\i bal taachar} is conceptually rooted in the domain of {\i neder}, it would not apply to a {\i korban} so entirely outside that domain. The {\i Gemara} responds that a {\i chatat chelev} similarly cannot arise through a {\i neder} and yet clearly carries {\i bal taachar}. The rejoinder, however, is that a {\i chatat chelev} carries {\i kaparah} as its purpose, and that urgency of {\i kaparah} is what grants it sufficient weight for {\i bal taachar}. The {\i chatat nazir}, which is about change of status rather than {\i kaparah}, lacks this urgency. The {\i chiddush} is thus that even such a {\i korban} \u8212- neither arising from {\i neder} nor serving {\i kaparah} \u8212- is subject to {\i bal taachar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatat Yoledes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a parallel case: the {\i chatat yoledes}, the sin-offering brought by a woman after childbirth. This {\i korban} involves no {\i neder}, no sin, and no {\i kaparah} in the conventional sense \u8212- it is a {\i korban} of status change, brought to return the {\i yoledes} from a state of {\i tumah} to {\i taharah}. Yet the {\i Gemara} affirms that there is {\i bal taachar} for the {\i chatat yoledes} \u8212- and explains this on the basis of Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's principle: there is an urgency to becoming {\i tahor}, because once one is {\i tahor} one regains the capacity to eat {\i korbanot} and to enter the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. Even if these practical privileges are rarely exercised, the conceptual importance of {\i tahara} \u8212- the potential to eat {\i kodashim}, the ability to access the {\i Beis HaMikdash} \u8212- creates a genuine pressure to resolve the {\i tumah} promptly. This urgency is sufficient to ground {\i bal taachar}, even without {\i neder} or {\i kaparah}. The {\i nazir\u8217's korbanot}, by contrast, carry no such urgency \u8212- not the urgency of {\i kaparah}, not the urgency of resolving {\i tumah} \u8212- and the {\i chiddush} that they nonetheless carry {\i bal taachar} is therefore genuinely novel. The {\i Gemara} thus arrives at a clear formulation of why a special derivation was necessary: the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir} are the weakest possible case of an obligatory {\i korban}, with the least urgency behind them, and the {\i Torah} teaches that {\i bal taachar} applies to them nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Annulment Powers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Linkage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states the second principle derived from the linking of {\i nazir} to {\i neder}: just as a father may annul his daughter\u8217's vows and a husband may annul his wife\u8217's vows, so too a father may annul his daughter\u8217's {\i nezirut} and a husband may annul his wife\u8217's {\i nezirut}. The {\i Gemara} asks: why is an explicit textual linkage necessary? One might have derived this by analogy \u8212- {\i bemah matzinu} \u8212- directly from the laws of {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirut as Neder Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The force of the question is this: {\i nezirut} is not a separate category parallel to {\i nedarim} \u8212- it is a {\i type} of {\i neder}. If that is so, there are not two independent categories (apples and oranges) between which a {\i gezeirah shavah} or textual link is needed. Rather, {\i nezirut} is a subset of {\i nedarim}, the way a specific variety is a subset of a broader category. If annulment applies to {\i nedarim}, it should self-evidently apply to this particular form of {\i neder} without requiring any additional derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time Limit Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: one might have thought to distinguish {\i nezirut} from ordinary {\i nedarim} on the following grounds. An ordinary {\i neder} has no intrinsic time limit; by default it is a permanent change of status, and therefore the {\i Torah} granted the father and husband the power to annul lest someone remain bound indefinitely. {\i Nezirut}, by contrast, has a built-in time limit \u8212- standard {\i nezirut} is thirty days \u8212- and might therefore have been excluded from the annulment power, on the reasoning that it will expire on its own shortly regardless. The textual linkage thus teaches that annulment applies to {\i nezirut} as well, despite its time-bounded nature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Permanence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} briefly addresses the converse possibility: why not learn {\i in reverse} and say that since {\i nezirut} is thirty days, perhaps all {\i nedarim} default to thirty days? The answer lies in the conceptual model underlying {\i nedarim}. A {\i neder} operates on the paradigm of {\i hekdesh} \u8212- sanctification. When one consecrates an object as a {\i korban}, the change of status is permanent; there is no inherent clock on sanctification. Since a {\i neder} functions by changing the status of an object (rendering it forbidden as though it were sanctified), the default model of a {\i neder} is a permanent, open-ended change of status. The time-limited character of {\i nezirut} is thus a feature specific to that institution, not a general model for {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hamudar Hanaah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now transitions to a new section of the {\i Mishnah}: the case of {\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah machaveiro} \u8212- one who has taken a vow prohibiting himself from deriving benefit from another person. This section of the {\i Mishnah} is understood by the {\i Gemara} as a presentation of {\i yadim} \u8212- abbreviated statements that function as effective vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Statement Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} lists several formulations that constitute effective prohibitions: {\i mudarani mimcha} (\u8220"I am vowed away from you\u8221"), {\i mofreshani mimcha} (\u8220"I am separated from you\u8221"), {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} (\u8220"the things I would eat of yours\u8221"), and {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em lach} (\u8220"the things I would taste of yours\u8221"). Each of these is an abbreviated formulation \u8212- none states the full content of a {\i neder} \u8212- yet each is legally effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Statement Components\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand why these are {\i yadim}, it is useful to analyze the components of a complete {\i neder} formulation. A full statement would specify: the object (e.g., \u8220"your food\u8221"), the relationship of the speaker to the object (e.g., \u8220"to me\u8221"), and the legal status of the object (e.g., {\i assur} \u8212- forbidden \u8212- or {\i konam} \u8212- like a {\i korban}). A {\i neder} operates by changing the status of an object through a declaration modeled on {\i hekdesh}: \u8220"Your food is {\i konam} \u8212- like a {\i korban} \u8212- to me.\u8221" This yields three elements: the object, the relationship, and the status. The formulations in the {\i Mishnah} each supply only one or two of these elements, leaving the remainder implicit. {\i Mudarani mimcha} indicates the relationship of separation but does not specify the object. {\i She\u8217'ani ochel lach} specifies the object class (food) but does not supply the status word. Neither supplies the word {\i assur} or {\i konam}, which explicitly establishes the forbidden status. Yet because these formulations occur within a recognizable context of {\i neder}-making, the missing elements are understood as implicit \u8212- and that is precisely the function of {\i yad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} observes that the phrase {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} avoids ambiguity because the particle {\i she-} marks it as a definite reference \u8212- \u8220"that which I would eat of yours\u8221" \u8212- rather than an invitation. If someone meant to say \u8220"I would like to eat with you,\u8221" he would say {\i ani ochel lach}, not {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach}. The {\i she-} construction signals that the speaker is identifying an object class \u8212- the food he would eat \u8212- and intending to change its status. That is the {\i yad}: the object is indicated, and the context of {\i neder}-making supplies the rest.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel states: {\i mudarani mimcha} alone accomplishes nothing \u8212- it is not even a {\i yad}. One must say {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} or {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em lach}. By itself, {\i mudarani mimcha} is too vague to function as an abbreviated {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braitta Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} objects from a {\i baraita}: {\i mudarani mimcha}, {\i menudeh ani mimcha}, {\i mishamtani ani mimcha} \u8212- {\i harei zeh assur}. The {\i baraita} rules that even the \u8220"separation\u8221" formulation alone is sufficient to create a prohibition. Similarly, {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach}, {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em lach} \u8212- {\i harei zeh assur} \u8212- even just specifying the object class alone is forbidden. Either phrase alone, the {\i baraita} says, constitutes an effective {\i yad}. A second {\i baraita} that reverses the order of the phrases further forecloses the interpretation that the two phrases must be used together: it is clear that each phrase independently creates a prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading Narrowly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After multiple attempts to harmonize Shmuel\u8217's statement with the {\i baraitot} \u8212- first by suggesting that the {\i baraita} describes the combination of both phrases, then by noting that the second {\i baraita} reverses the order, making such harmonization impossible \u8212- the {\i Gemara} ultimately reinterprets Shmuel. The {\i Gemara} reads Shmuel\u8217's statement not as ruling that {\i mudarani mimcha} alone is ineffective in all circumstances, but as addressing the differential legal effect of the phrases. Specifically: {\i she-k\u8217'var amar mudani} \u8212- when one has already said {\i mudarani mimcha} and then adds {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach}. The reinterpretation shifts Shmuel from a ruling about validity to a ruling about scope.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Phrases Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the {\i Gemara} has reinterpreted Shmuel, his actual ruling comes into focus. Shmuel holds that when someone says {\i both} phrases \u8212- {\i mudarani mimcha} together with {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} \u8212- the result is that only he himself is forbidden from eating the other\u8217's food: {\i hu d\u8217'assur v\u8217'chavero mutar}. He has specified the object (the food he would eat from the other) and indicated his separation from it; the vow is therefore directed and limited \u8212- his eating of the other\u8217's food is forbidden, but the other\u8217's eating of his food is not affected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Phrase Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When, however, one says only {\i mudarani mimcha} \u8212- \u8220"I am separated from you\u8221" \u8212- without specifying the object, the result is different: {\i shneihem asurim}, both parties are forbidden to one another. This ruling is supported by Rav Yosef bar Chanina: {\i mudarani mimcha} \u8212- {\i shneihem asurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Filling Blanks Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of this distinction is the logic of filling in blanks. When both phrases are used together, the second phrase ({\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach}) narrows and specifies the meaning of the first: the separation is with respect to \u8220"the food I would eat of yours,\u8221" i.e., your food is forbidden to me. The {\i neder} is thus directed and unilateral. When only the first phrase is used \u8212- \u8220"I am separated from you\u8221" \u8212- no object is specified, and the court must fill in what \u8220"separation\u8221" means. Separation, in its unqualified sense, is bidirectional: I am separated from you and you from me. Since there is nothing to indicate that the speaker meant only one direction, the prohibition is interpreted to run both ways \u8212- your food is forbidden to me, and my food is forbidden to you. This is not the speaker imposing a {\i neder} on the other person; rather, the speaker is understood to have changed the status of all items relevant to the relationship of benefit between them, in both directions. Should the other party then take food from the speaker\u8217's table and eat it, it is primarily the speaker who has transgressed the {\i neder} by allowing the prohibited benefit to flow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sheani Ochel Yad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Girsa Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A textual question arises regarding the phrase in the {\i Mishnah}: should the reading be {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} (\u8220"that which I eat of yours\u8221") or {\i she\u8217'eini ochel lach} (\u8220"I will not eat of yours\u8221")? The distinction carries significant legal weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the correct {\i girsa} is {\i she\u8217'eini ochel lach} \u8212- \u8220"I will not eat of yours\u8221" \u8212- then the {\i Ran} observes that one appears to have stated a complete sentence. There is ostensibly nothing missing. Yet the {\i Ran} argues that precisely {\i here} lies the {\i yad}: what the speaker has formulated is not a {\i neder} but a {\i shvuah}. A {\i neder} is object-based \u8212- it changes the status of an object \u8212- while a {\i shvuah} is action-based and person-based, restricting what the person will do. \u8220"I will not eat of yours\u8221" is formulated as an oath about the speaker\u8217's behavior, not as a declaration about the status of the food. The function of {\i yad} here is therefore not to supply a missing element of a {\i neder} formulation, but to {\i reinterpret} a statement that was phrased as a {\i shvuah} and treat it as though it were a {\i neder}. The abbreviated or hybrid nature of the statement \u8212- its blending of {\i shvuah}-language with {\i neder}-context \u8212- is what makes it a {\i yad}: we read \u8220"I will not eat of yours\u8221" as \u8220"the food of yours that I would eat is forbidden to me.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretive mechanism creates a noteworthy practical consequence. If {\i yad} can reinterpret an action-oriented statement as an object-oriented prohibition, then a person who uses {\i shvuah} language in a {\i neder} context cannot escape the {\i neder} simply by pointing out that he formulated it as an oath rather than a vow. The {\i yad} will do its work and convert the formulation into a {\i neder} regardless. This principle, taken to its logical conclusion, closes what might otherwise appear to be a convenient loophole: one who impulsively declares \u8220"I will never again drive on this road\u8221" cannot easily argue that since the formulation sounds like a {\i shvuah}, no {\i neder} was created. If the context is one of {\i neder}-making, the {\i yad} reinterprets accordingly \u8212- and the road itself becomes, in some sense, {\i assur alav}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 5\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading the Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on the previous daf had been working through a statement of Shmuel, who held that the Mishnah\u8217's language was obscure. The Mishnah had listed a series of formulations \u8212- {\i mudrani mimcha}, {\i mufrehani mimcha}, {\i she\u8217'ochal lcha}, {\i she\u8217'at\u8217'oam lcha} \u8212- each of which functions as a {\i yad}, an abbreviated or elliptical statement that attaches to a full {\i neder}. The question was how to read that Mishnah: does each formulation work independently, or does Shmuel require them in combination?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mudrani Mimcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara demonstrated that each formulation works on its own, which forced a modification of Shmuel\u8217's position. As refined, Shmuel holds that when one says {\i mudrani mimcha} \u8212- a broad, generic statement of separation \u8212- without any qualifying predicate, the {\i neder} operates in both directions simultaneously. That is, not only is your property forbidden to me, but my property becomes forbidden to you as well. It is only when the speaker adds the phrase {\i she\u8217'ochal lcha} \u8212- specifying the things of yours that I would eat \u8212- that the prohibition is directionally limited: your property is forbidden to me, but mine remains permitted to you. The added qualifier thus narrows, rather than broadens, the scope of the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yossi bar Chanina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this understanding in hand, the Gemara brings a statement of Rabbi Yossi bar Chanina, who teaches: {\i mudrani himcha shneihem assurin} \u8212- if one says \u8220"I am separated from you,\u8221" both parties are forbidden. This parallels the revised reading of Shmuel. But the Gemara immediately raises a challenge from the Mishnah. The Mishnah states: {\i hareini alecha cherem} \u8212- behold, I am to you {\i cherem}, meaning my property bears the status of {\i cherem} relative to you. In that case, the Mishnah rules {\i hamudar assur} \u8212- the {\i mudar}, the person to whom the {\i neder} is directed, is forbidden to benefit from the {\i magdir}\u8217's property \u8212- but the {\i magdir} himself remains permitted to benefit from the {\i mudar}\u8217's property. The question is sharp: if we interpret {\i mudrani mimcha} as working in both directions, why does this formulation, {\i hareini alecha cherem}, operate only in one direction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciling Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is that the cases in the Mishnah are not {\i stam}, not plain and unqualified. In each instance, the speaker went on to provide an explicit clarification. For instance, when the Mishnah says {\i hareini alecha cherem}, it turns out the speaker also said {\i ve\u8217'ani alecha lo} \u8212- \u8220"but I to you, no.\u8221" And conversely, when the Mishnah says {\i ve\u8217'at alai cherem} \u8212- \u8220"you are to me {\i cherem}\u8221" \u8212- that is also qualified by the addition that the reverse does not apply. In both cases the outcome was already explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i aval stama mai} \u8212- but what if nothing explicit was added? The answer follows: in the unqualified case, {\i hu assur vechavero mutar} \u8212- only one direction applies. This appears to stand in direct tension with Rabbi Yossi bar Chanina, who said that {\i mudrani himcha} generates bilateral prohibition. How can the unqualified use of {\i cherem}-language operate unilaterally when {\i mudrani mimcha} operates bilaterally?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this through a careful restatement of Rabbi Yossi bar Chanina\u8217's position: {\i mudrani lcha shneihem assurin; mudrani mimcha hu assur vechavero mutar}. The critical distinction lies in the directional particle. {\i Mudrani lcha} \u8212- \u8220"I am separated to you\u8221" \u8212- is syntactically ambiguous. It can be read as \u8220"my property is forbidden to you,\u8221" but it can equally be read, by supplying a {\i misha} prefix, as \u8220"I am removing myself from your property as well.\u8221" Because {\i lcha} lacks a directional prefix that firmly fixes one reading, the phrase is genuinely open in both directions and therefore generates bilateral prohibition. {\i Mudrani mimcha}, by contrast \u8212- \u8220"I am separated from you\u8221" \u8212- contains the prefix {\i mimcha}, which unambiguously establishes the direction of removal: from your property. There is no legitimate grammatical way to read {\i mimcha} as simultaneously generating a prohibition running in the opposite direction. Hence it is unilateral.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lach vs.\u160 ?Mimcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution, however, circles back to the original difficulty with Shmuel\u8217's formulation. Shmuel had said that one needs to combine {\i mudrani mimcha} with {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} \u8212- the things of yours that I will eat. But if {\i mimcha} already operates in only one direction, what is the additional work being done by {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach}? The need for that qualifier must mean something other than fixing directionality. The Gemara accordingly offers a reformulation: {\i hachi itmar} \u8212- here is what Shmuel actually meant. If one says {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach}, the prohibition is limited to {\i achilah}, eating. {\i Mudrani mimcha} alone, without any qualifier, would be broader \u8212- potentially an {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}, a prohibition against all forms of benefit. The qualifying phrase {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} narrows the scope of the {\i neder} to the domain of eating rather than all benefit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Specificity Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope of Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a general and important principle. If someone makes a {\i neder} using generic language \u8212- {\i cherem}, {\i konam}, {\i neder} \u8212- without specifying the nature of the prohibition, the default is {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah}: any and all forms of benefit are forbidden. If one wishes to limit the {\i neder} to {\i achilah} alone, one must say so explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achilah vs.\u160 ?Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach v\u8217'she\u8217'ani toeim lach} \u8212- the things of yours that I will eat and that I will taste \u8212- establishes that the operative prohibition is one of eating. Since {\i te\u8217'imah}, tasting, is itself a subset of {\i achilah}, citing both narrows the {\i neder} to the category of consumption and excludes all other forms of benefit. Without that explicit narrowing, the prohibition stands at the broader level of {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguity Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further issue emerges. One might assume that {\i mudrani mimcha}, stated alone, at least generates a {\i neder} \u8212- even if a maximally broad one. But the Gemara raises a more radical possibility: perhaps {\i mudrani mimcha} does not constitute a {\i neder} at all. The phrase may not even be referring to the forbidden status of objects. {\i Mudrani mimcha} could mean nothing more than: I am distancing myself from you. I do not want to conduct {\i masa u\u8217'matan}, business dealings, with you. I refuse to stand within your {\i arba amos}. I will not speak with you. All of these are modes of social and personal separation, and none of them necessarily involves the prohibition of an object under the framework of {\i neder}. The phrase is thus radically ambiguous \u8212- it might mean that your objects are {\i assur} to me for eating, or for benefit, or it might simply mean I want nothing to do with you personally. Given that ambiguity, who is to say that any {\i neder} was made at all? It is only when the speaker says {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach} \u8212- the things of yours that I will eat \u8212- that we know unambiguously that the speaker intends to forbid objects in the domain of {\i achilah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yad Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Levels of Yad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now crystallizes a general conceptual framework. There are three distinct levels to consider when evaluating an elliptical speech act, a {\i yad}. The first is a {\i yad mochiach} \u8212- a statement where what the speaker means is entirely clear even without explicit articulation. An illustrative case: at a {\i chuppah}, when the {\i chosson} says {\i harei at mekudeshet} but fails to add {\i li} \u8212- to me \u8212- the omission does not necessarily invalidate the {\i kiddushin}. Since the entire ceremony is oriented around this man marrying this woman, the {\i li} is implicit; the {\i yad} is {\i mochiach}, clear from the entire context. The second level is a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mochiach}: the statement most likely means what we think it means, but there is some possibility it means something else. For instance, {\i harei at mekudeshet} said outside the context of a {\i chuppah} \u8212- in a world where men sometimes send agents \u8212- might refer to a {\i shaliach} acting on behalf of a third party. The meaning is probable but not unambiguous. The third level is no {\i yad} at all: the statement is so open-ended that no particular meaning can be ascribed to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguous Yad Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: does a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mochiach} \u8212- an ambiguous but probably-clear statement \u8212- constitute a valid {\i yad} or not? The formulation it proposes is: {\i leima kasavar Shmuel yadayim she\u8217'einan mochichot lo havyan yadayim} \u8212- perhaps Shmuel\u8217's entire position rests on the principle that handles which are not unambiguous do not count as handles at all. {\i Mudrani mimcha} falls into this category: most likely it is a {\i neder}, but it might mean nothing more than social distancing. That ambiguity, however slight, disqualifies it as a valid speech act, and without the clarifying phrase {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach}, nothing takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara confirms this: {\i ein, Shmuel mokim lah matnitin k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- yes, Shmuel is interpreting our Mishnah in accordance with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position. For the Mishnah in the laws of {\i gittin} teaches: {\i gufo shel get harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam} \u8212- the core text of a {\i get} is \u8220"behold you are permitted to all men.\u8221" Rabbi Yehuda, however, holds that one must add the phrase {\i v\u8217'den d\u8217'yehevei lichi minai sefer teiruchin v\u8217'igeret shevukin} \u8212- \u8220"and this shall be to you from me a writ of severance and a document of release.\u8221" Without this addition, says Rabbi Yehuda, the statement {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam} is insufficient, because it does not unambiguously identify the document itself as the instrument effecting the divorce. Perhaps the man is merely making a verbal declaration of release, and the written text is simply that declaration recorded. Only when the text says explicitly \u8220"this document shall be to you from me\u8221" does it become clear that the document itself is the operative instrument. This is a direct application of {\i yadayim she\u8217'einan mochichot lo havyan yadayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i amai dachik Shmuel l\u8217'ukma l\u8217'matnitin k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- why did Shmuel strain to interpret our Mishnah according to Rabbi Yehuda, a minority opinion? We have {\i baraitot} that explicitly say {\i havyan yadayim} \u8212- ambiguous handles do work. Why not interpret the Mishnah according to the majority {\i Rabbanan}, who hold {\i havyan yadayim}? Rava answers: the language of the Mishnah itself forced Shmuel\u8217's hand. The Mishnah says {\i she\u8217'ani ochel lach v\u8217'she\u8217'ani toeim lach} \u8212- things of yours that I will eat, things of yours that I will taste \u8212- including the word {\i lach}, to you. But why is {\i lach} necessary? The speaker is already addressing the person directly and has already said {\i mimcha} \u8212- from you. The word {\i lach} is superfluous unless it is signaling a principle: when making a {\i neder}, the language must be maximally clear and unambiguous. The superfluous {\i lach} is itself evidence that the Mishnah demands {\i yadayim mochichot}, and that is why Shmuel read the Mishnah as he did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to state the {\i amora\u8217'ic} debate directly. {\i Abaye amar havyan yadayim} \u8212- Abaye holds that an ambiguous but probable {\i yad} is a valid speech act. {\i Rava amar lo havyan yadayim} \u8212- Rava holds that any ambiguity disqualifies the speech act entirely. Rava reports that Rav Ibi provided him with the proof from the verse {\i nazir l\u8217'hazir l\u8217'Hashem}. The word {\i l\u8217'hazir} teaches, through the principle of {\i hekkesh}, that {\i yadei nezirut} \u8212- elliptical statements indicating a {\i nazir} vow \u8212- must satisfy the same standard as {\i nezirut} itself. And {\i nezirut} requires {\i hafla\u8217'ah} \u8212- articulation and clarity. The {\i hafla\u8217'ah} requirement is derived from the root {\i l\u8217'hafli}, meaning to articulate distinctly and concretely. Just as the {\i neder} of {\i nezirut} must be made with complete clarity, so too must the {\i yad} of {\i nezirut} be unambiguous. And since we learn the {\i yad} of {\i neder} from the {\i yad} of {\i nazir}, the same standard applies across the board.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran makes an important conceptual clarification regarding the {\i get} debate. When the Gemara says the {\i Rabbanan} do not require the text of the {\i get} to be explicit, this should not be misread as the {\i Rabbanan} accepting ambiguity. Rather, the {\i Rabbanan} hold that what renders the text clear is not the intrinsic language of the statement alone, but the surrounding context. There is a distinction between a {\i yad mochiach} \u8212- where the statement itself, read in isolation, is unambiguous \u8212- and a case where the statement is technically a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mochiach}, but the context supplies the clarity. In the latter case, according to the {\i Rabbanan}, the {\i yad} still works \u8212- not because ambiguity is acceptable, but because the context provides the {\i mochiach} that the language lacks. The implication is that even the {\i Rabbanan} may require some form of clarity; they simply allow context to supply it. The live debate between Abaye and Rava, then, is specifically about cases where even context does not resolve the ambiguity \u8212- whether that residual ambiguity disqualifies the speech act entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir L\u8217'hazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's proof rests on the verse {\i nazir l\u8217'hazir l\u8217'Hashem}. The word {\i l\u8217'hazir} \u8212- to vow as a {\i nazir} \u8212- is the source for the principle that elliptical statements ({\i yadayim}) are legally operative in the context of {\i nezirut}. The Gemara performs a {\i hekkesh}, a textual linkage: {\i makish yadei nezirut l\u8217'nezirut} \u8212- the {\i yad} of {\i nezirut} is compared to {\i nezirut} itself. From this comparison follows the principle: {\i mah nezirut b\u8217'hafla\u8217'ah af yadei nezirut b\u8217'hafla\u8217'ah} \u8212- just as full {\i nezirut} requires {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, so too must a {\i yad} of {\i nezirut} satisfy the standard of {\i hafla\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafla\u8217'ah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hafla\u8217'ah} is a technical term in this context, derived from the root {\i l\u8217'hafli}, meaning to articulate or declare distinctly. The word appears in connection with speech acts \u8212- {\i neder} and {\i shevuah} \u8212- and it conveys the requirement that such acts be performed with concreteness and clarity. Things created through {\i hafla\u8217'ah} are, by definition, created through fully articulated speech acts. The {\i hafla\u8217'ah} standard thus requires not merely that the speaker had the intention to make a {\i neder}, but that the words themselves express that intention with sufficient clarity to constitute the operative act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication of the {\i hafla\u8217'ah} requirement is illustrated through the case of {\i hareini nazir im zeh nazir} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if this person is a {\i nazir}.\u8221" One opinion holds that such a vow is ineffective even if we subsequently discover that the person in question was indeed a {\i nazir}, because at the moment the vow was uttered there was genuine uncertainty whether it was taking effect. The concept of {\i hafla\u8217'ah} requires that the speech act be determinate at the moment of utterance. If the efficacy of the statement depends on a fact not yet known, the speech act lacks the requisite clarity \u8212- not merely because of the speaker\u8217's subjective uncertainty, but because the act itself is constitutively conditional and indeterminate. Whether one rules strictly on this question is a matter of some debate, but the conceptual point is clear: {\i hafla\u8217'ah} mandates that the speech act be unambiguous in its operation at the moment it occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Applying to Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the {\i hafla\u8217'ah} requirement governing {\i nezirut} itself, the Gemara derives through {\i hekkesh} that the same standard governs a {\i yad} of {\i nezirut}. And since the {\i yad} of {\i neder} is learned from the {\i yad} of {\i nezirut}, the {\i hafla\u8217'ah} standard applies to {\i nedarim} as well. The consequence is Rava\u8217's position: a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mochiach} \u8212- a {\i yad} with any residual ambiguity \u8212- does not meet the {\i hafla\u8217'ah} standard and therefore cannot function as a valid speech act. The {\i neder} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye holds {\i havyan yadayim}: even an ambiguous {\i yad}, one where the meaning is probable but not certain, constitutes a valid and operative speech act. A degree of ambiguity does not negate the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava holds {\i lo havyan yadayim}: any degree of ambiguity disqualifies the {\i yad} entirely. The speech act must be unambiguous in order to take legal effect. This is Rava\u8217's application of the {\i hafla\u8217'ah} principle derived through the {\i nazir} {\i hekkesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks whether Abaye and Rava are essentially replicating the dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i Rabbanan} in the Mishnah about the text of a {\i get}. The Mishnah rules that the core text of a {\i get} is {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam}. Rabbi Yehuda requires the addition {\i v\u8217'den d\u8217'yehevei lichi minai sefer teiruchin v\u8217'igeret shevukin} \u8212- \u8220"and this shall be to you from me a writ of severance and a document of release.\u8221" The Gemara maps the positions: {\i Abaye de-amar k\u8217'Rabbanan} \u8212- Abaye rules like the {\i Rabbanan}, who do not require explicit articulation; {\i Rava de-amar k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- Rava rules like Rabbi Yehuda, who does. The debate over {\i yadayim she\u8217'einan mochichot} thus appears to be the same debate, applied to different legal contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kritut Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, refuses this mapping of his position to the {\i Rabbanan}. He says: {\i ana de-amri afilu k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- I rule even according to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, and yet I still hold {\i havyan yadayim}. How? Because Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's stringency in the law of {\i get} derives from a special requirement unique to {\i gittin} \u8212- the Torah requirement of {\i kritut}, severance. {\i Kritut} demands that the divorce effect a complete and total rupture of the marital bond. Every ambiguity, every loose thread, every possible residual connection must be eliminated. Given that uniquely elevated standard, Rabbi Yehuda requires the text of the {\i get} to be explicitly unambiguous. But this is not a general statement about speech acts. In ordinary contexts \u8212- {\i nedarim}, {\i nezirut} and the like \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda might well accept an ambiguous {\i yad} as operative. The {\i kritut} requirement is a specifically heightened standard, and its demands cannot be extrapolated to areas outside the law of {\i get}. Therefore Abaye can hold {\i havyan yadayim} universally while simultaneously accepting Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position regarding {\i gittin} as applying only in that specific context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava in turn responds symmetrically: {\i ana de-amri afilu k\u8217'Rabbanan} \u8212- I hold even according to the {\i Rabbanan}, and yet I still hold {\i lo havyan yadayim}. The {\i Rabbanan} do not require explicit articulation in the text of the {\i get} \u8212- but this is not because they are tolerant of ambiguity. It is because in the context of a {\i get}, there is no ambiguity at all. {\i Ein adam megaresh ishet chaveiro} \u8212- a man does not divorce another man\u8217's wife. Given the entire context of a {\i get} proceeding, who is divorcing whom is perfectly clear, as is the fact that this document is the instrument of divorce. The {\i Rabbanan} say the text is sufficient not because they permit ambiguity, but because there is no ambiguity here. The context supplies the clarity that the intrinsic language might otherwise lack. In general, Rava still requires {\i yadayim mochichot} \u8212- the position that ambiguity nullifies the speech act \u8212- but contextual clarity can satisfy that requirement. The {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's leniency in {\i gittin} is not a statement about accepting ambiguity; it is a statement that in this particular case there is none.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran presses on this point and introduces a conceptual refinement. The Gemara states: {\i ad kan lo ka\u8217'palgei Rabbanan de-be-yadayim mochichot} \u8212- the {\i Rabbanan} say you do not need {\i yadayim mochichot} by {\i get}. But this seems to contradict the explanation just offered \u8212- that the {\i Rabbanan} do require {\i yadayim mochichot}, they just hold that the requirement is satisfied by context. The Ran resolves this by distinguishing between two different sources of clarity. A {\i yad mochiach} refers to a case where the statement itself \u8212- the intrinsic semantic content of the language, read apart from any context \u8212- is unambiguous. When it is the context, rather than the statement itself, that provides the clarity, this is not technically a {\i yad mochiach} at all; it is contextual {\i mochiach}. The text of a {\i get} as written \u8212- {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam} \u8212- is not, in isolation, a {\i yad mochiach}. It is a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mochiach} by its intrinsic language alone. What the {\i Rabbanan} hold is not that {\i yadayim she\u8217'einan mochichot} are valid in general, but rather that in the case of a {\i get}, contextual clarity \u8212- {\i ein adam megaresh ishet chaveiro} \u8212- is an independent and sufficient source of {\i mochiach} that operates entirely apart from the text. Therefore when the Gemara says the {\i Rabbanan} do not require {\i yadayim mochichot}, it means precisely that: the statement\u8217's intrinsic {\i yadayim} do not have to be {\i mochichot}, because context provides its own distinct form of clarity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot for the Abaye-Rava debate is this. Both agree that when there is clear context or a clear statement, the speech act is fully operative. The disagreement is confined to cases where even context does not eliminate the ambiguity \u8212- where the statement remains genuinely open to multiple interpretations and no external factor resolves it. In that situation, Abaye holds the speech act is still valid, while Rava holds it is not. This is the precise scope of {\i yadayim she\u8217'einan mochichot lo havyan yadayim} according to Rava, and the conceptual distinction the Ran draws makes clear why the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position in {\i gittin} does not commit them to accepting ambiguity in general.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 7\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yad Sheino Mochiach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition of Yad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up at the very top of daf 7, at the concept of {\i yesh yad}. We are in the middle of a discussion that began with a debate between Rav and Rav Yehuda over whether {\i yad sheino mochiach} qualifies as a {\i yad} or not. Tosafot raises a preliminary definitional question: what exactly is {\i yad sheino mochiach}? It must be distinguished from something that is not a {\i yad} at all \u8212- that is, a case where the speaker\u8217's meaning is completely uncertain and open-ended. {\i Yad sheino mochiach}, as we discussed yesterday, is best understood as a case where it is fairly clear what the person means, but not entirely so. There is some ambiguity, and if there is a concept of {\i yad}, we go by the most reasonable presumed meaning while acknowledging that some other meaning remains possible. This is distinct from a case where the statement is so open-ended that it could mean any number of things. {\i Yad sheino mochiach} involves one or two or three reasonable possibilities \u8212- not an infinite range of interpretations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Speech Acts vs Implicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader question running through this section of the {\i gemara} is where the concept of {\i yad} applies at all. The fact that the {\i gemara} asks this question presupposes that in these domains, we are not merely interested in {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- in subjective intent \u8212- but in the precise words used: in the {\i speech act} itself. With {\i kiddushin}, as Tosafot notes by drawing on the language of {\i hekdesh}, the declaration {\i harei at} functions as a formal {\i hekdesh}-like consecration, and therefore there is a genuine speech-act dimension: the question arises whether one must be entirely explicit or whether an abbreviated, slightly equivocal expression can still be effective. That is the question of {\i yad} in the context of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i pe\u8217'ah} is even more striking, because the classical form of {\i pe\u8217'ah} involves no speech whatsoever \u8212- one simply refrains from cutting the corner of the field. But Tosafot\u8217's analysis requires us to envision a context where one does perform a speech act of designation: one says \u8220"this is {\i pe\u8217'ah}\u8221" rather than merely leaving the corner. In such a case, the question of {\i yad} becomes relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Sentence Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a subtle but important distinction. Why does it matter whether there is some ambiguity in the expression? After all, even if the meaning were completely unambiguous, if there is no concept of {\i yad}, the person never fully articulated what they meant, and the designation should be ineffective regardless. Tosafot\u8217's answer is this: there is a difference between a case where a full sentence was never begun and a case where a full sentence was already stated. If someone has already said {\i harei at mekudeshet li} to one woman, and then turns and says \u8220"and this one\u8221" \u8212- if that second expression is completely unambiguous, then {\i and this one} simply applies the entire first sentence to the second woman. It is like saying {\i amen} after hearing a {\i berachah}: it is as if you have said the full thing yourself. The Rambam makes this precise point \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'omer amen k\u8217'mevarech hu dami} \u8212- the one who says {\i amen} has, in a sense, said the entire {\i berachah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since in such a case the second designation would be fully explicit by virtue of the first sentence, there would be no {\i yad} in play at all. The {\i gemara} therefore introduces a degree of ambiguity into these cases in order to ensure that the second expression cannot be read as a simple repetition of the first sentence. And once there is that ambiguity \u8212- once we cannot say with certainty that the second utterance was meant to extend the first \u8212- then the person has not said the thing explicitly, and the question of {\i yesh yad o lav} becomes relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is this: the concept of {\i yad} addresses whether an inexplicit statement can be effective even when the meaning is clear. {\i Yesh yad} says yes \u8212- even without full articulation, if the meaning is discernible, it counts. {\i Ein yad} says no \u8212- you must say it explicitly, and knowing what you meant is not sufficient.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzedakah & Yad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Units Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the question of {\i yad} in the context of {\i tzedakah}. What is the case? If someone says, \u8220"This dollar is for {\i tzedakah},\u8221" and then says, \u8220"and this one also\u8221" \u8212- that is no question at all. The word \u8220"also\u8221" in this context is entirely explicit: it applies the first sentence to the second dollar without any ambiguity. The Rambam\u8217's formulation applies perfectly here: saying {\i v\u8217'hadein nami} when it is entirely clear what is meant is equivalent to restating the first sentence. No {\i yad} is involved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The genuine {\i yad} question arises when someone says \u8220"this dollar is for {\i tzedakah}\u8221" and then simply says \u8220"and this\u8221" \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hadein} \u8212- without adding \u8220"also for {\i tzedakah}.\u8221" What does {\i v\u8217'hadein} mean here? Perhaps he means, \u8220"and this one too is for {\i tzedakah}.\u8221" Or perhaps he means, \u8220"and this one I\u8217'll use for something else entirely.\u8221" There is just enough ambiguity that the second utterance cannot be read as a transparent repetition of the first full sentence. It is precisely to introduce that ambiguity that the {\i gemara} frames the case this way. Without it, as we said, there would be no {\i yad} question \u8212- the second expression would simply extend the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korbanot Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i mi amrinan keivan d\u8217'itkesh l\u8217'korbanot d\u8217'chtiv b\u8217'ficha zu tzedakah} \u8212- since {\i tzedakah} is compared to {\i korbanot} through the {\i pasuk} which teaches that \u8220"that which passes your lips\u8221" applies to {\i tzedakah}, and there is a prohibition of {\i bal te\u8217'acher} \u8212- of failing to fulfill a verbal commitment to give {\i tzedakah}, particularly when one pledges at a communal appeal \u8212- does the comparison to {\i korbanot} extend also to the concept of {\i yad}? Just as {\i korbanot} was the original domain from which {\i yad} was derived, does {\i tzedakah}\u8217's linkage to {\i korbanot} carry that over as well? Or is the comparison limited: {\i bal te\u8217'acher} applies to {\i tzedakah}, but the structural category of {\i yad} does not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzedakah as Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is striking that the {\i gemara} introduces {\i tzedakah} at this point in the discussion, placed after {\i kiddushin} and {\i pe\u8217'ah}, which are both quite distant from the {\i neder} category. {\i Tzedakah}, by contrast, is in many ways very close to it. When someone designates a specific sum as {\i tzedakah}, that money acquires a {\i tzedakah} status. It is not exactly {\i hekdesh}, but it is modeled on that structure \u8212- one cannot simply redirect those funds to a different purpose and replace them with a different donation later. The object itself becomes earmarked. Furthermore, when someone accepts upon themselves a {\i tzedakah} obligation through speech, there is the prohibition of {\i bal te\u8217'acher} if they fail to fulfill it. This combination \u8212- the designation affecting the status of an object, and the spoken commitment creating a personal obligation \u8212- makes {\i tzedakah} closely parallel to {\i nedarim} in structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is therefore interesting that the {\i gemara} raises this question alongside {\i kiddushin} and {\i pe\u8217'ah}, because {\i tzedakah} is more naturally in the {\i neder} mold than either of those. Much of what applies to {\i tzedakah} reflects the {\i korban}-{\i neder} model directly, and one would expect the answer to be that {\i yesh yad} applies here more readily. Much of this, of course, is a question between a person and God \u8212- the courts may have limited jurisdiction over many of these verbal commitments \u8212- but the halachic effect is real.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzedakah vs Hefker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i ma shehu l\u8217'hefker} \u8212- is there a concept of {\i yad} when someone declares property {\i hefker}? If I take my old possessions and place them outside, renouncing ownership so that anyone may take them, does that act of renunciation admit of a {\i yad} parallel? The {\i gemara}\u8217's initial response is: {\i haynu tzedakah} \u8212- this is the same as {\i tzedakah}, since both involve making something available for others to take. The question is whether they operate through the same mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beneficiary Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then distinguishes: {\i shani tzedakah}, {\i tzedakah} is different, because {\i tzedakah lo chazya ela l\u8217'aniyim} \u8212- {\i tzedakah} goes only to the poor. {\i Hefker}, by contrast, {\i mi aniyim u\u8217'mi ashirim} \u8212- is available to rich and poor alike. This difference in the scope of beneficiaries may constitute a meaningful distinction. The logic is not entirely explicit \u8212- it is not immediately obvious why the fact that {\i hefker} can be taken by wealthy people should make it less susceptible to the {\i yad} principle \u8212- but the {\i gemara} raises this as a genuine differentiating factor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechanisms Differ\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the question of who can benefit, there is a deeper structural difference. {\i Tzedakah} functions, as we have noted, somewhat like a {\i neder}: one designates an object with a {\i tzedakah} status, or accepts upon oneself a {\i tzedakah} obligation. In either case, the verbal act creates or defines a status. {\i Hefker}, by contrast, is not really about designating a status to an object. It is about renouncing ownership. When I declare something {\i hefker}, I am removing my control over it \u8212- severing my relationship to the property. The paradigm is {\i kol chamira vachamiya d\u8217'ika birshuti} \u8212- a declaration of non-ownership, of disavowal. Whether such a mechanism of renunciation is naturally susceptible to a concept of {\i yad}, which seems to operate through verbal acts of designation, is itself a genuine {\i chiddush} to consider. The inclusion of {\i hefker} in this list is, accordingly, quite striking.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hazmanah Designation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Toilet Designation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina now joins the discussion by asking a related question: {\i ve-yesh yad le-beit hakiseh o lo} \u8212- is there a concept of {\i yad} for a {\i beit hakiseh}? The {\i halakhic} relevance of designating a space as a toilet is that such a space acquires a status that forbids certain activities within it, such as Torah study or reciting {\i Kri\u8217'at Shema}. So what is the case? If someone says, \u8220"This house should be a {\i beit hakiseh},\u8221" and then says \u8220"and this one also\u8221" \u8212- that is fully explicit and presents no question. The question arises when he says \u8220"this house should be a {\i beit hakiseh}\u8221" and then simply says \u8220"and this\u8221" \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hadein} \u8212- leaving ambiguous whether he meant \u8220"this too should be a {\i beit hakiseh}\u8221" or \u8220"this one I\u8217'll use for something else.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hazmanah & Yad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that before Ravina can even get to the {\i yad} question, there is a prior issue: Ravina apparently takes for granted that {\i hazmanah} \u8212- verbal designation \u8212- gives a {\i beit hakiseh} its {\i halakhic} status even before the space has been used as such. But Ravina himself had asked elsewhere whether {\i hazmanah le-beit hakiseh mahu} and {\i hazmanah le-beit ha-merchatz mahu} \u8212- whether designating a space for use as a toilet or bathhouse, without yet having used it that way, confers the relevant status. Does {\i hazmanah milta hi o lav milta hi} \u8212- does designation create status or not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent tension by explaining that Ravina asked one question because of the other: {\i chada migu chada ka mibaya lei}. First: does {\i hazmanah} work at all? And if you say it does, then second: is there a {\i yad} for {\i hazmanah}? The two questions are nested \u8212- the {\i yad} question is conditional on the {\i hazmanah} question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiku Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i tiba\u8217'ei lei} \u8212- let these questions stand, which is the formulation in {\i Masechet Nedarim} for what elsewhere appears as {\i teiku}. Tosafot notes this explicitly: {\i tiba\u8217'ei lei peirush k\u8217'mo teiku di-leshon Nedarim meshuneh} \u8212- {\i tiba\u8217'ei lei} means the same as {\i teiku}, but the language of {\i Masechet Nedarim} is distinctive, as we have noted before. All of these questions \u8212- {\i tzedakah}, {\i hefker}, {\i hazmanah}, {\i beit hakiseh} \u8212- are left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ran\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Psak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then is the {\i halakhah}? The Ran addresses this directly. He begins with a principle articulated by the Ramban: when the {\i gemara} poses a conditional question in the form of {\i im timtzei lomar} \u8212- \u8220"if you say A, then what about B?\u8221" \u8212- this structure implies that the {\i gemara} has already, implicitly, {\i pasak}\u8217'd the first premise. By building the question about {\i hefker} on the prior question about {\i tzedakah}, the {\i gemara} has effectively accepted that {\i yesh yad l\u8217'tzedakah}. One could argue that the conditional structure is merely a logical device for posing related questions and implies nothing about {\i psak}, but the Ramban maintains that {\i im timtzei lomar} carries an implicit resolution of the first issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Issurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second principle the Ramban invokes is the general rule that {\i kol heikha de-itamar teiku be-issurin le-chumra} \u8212- whenever a {\i teiku} arises in a matter of {\i issur}, we are {\i machmir}. Since the question of {\i yad} in {\i tzedakah} is left as a {\i teiku}, we treat it as binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Ran finds this startling. He notes that there is an explicit {\i sugya} at the end of {\i Perek Elu Metziot} that {\i safek mamona le-kula} \u8212- in doubtful monetary matters, we are lenient. And is not this precisely a monetary question? If it is uncertain whether these funds belong to the {\i aniyim}, then we should apply {\i hamotzi me-chavero alav ha-reayah} \u8212- the one claiming to take from another bears the burden of proof. The poor person cannot prove that the money belongs to them, so the owner would retain it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a broader conceptual question, discussed by Reb Shimon Shkop in his {\i Sha\u8217'arei Yosher}: why do we not apply {\i safek issur} logic to monetary disputes? If I genuinely do not know whether money belongs to me or to another person, should I not be {\i machmir} regarding the {\i issur} of {\i gezel} and simply hand it over? Reb Shimon\u8217's answer is that monetary questions are governed first by civil law \u8212- by how the law determines property rights \u8212- and the question of whether one violates {\i lo tignov} flows from that legal determination, not from an independent objective inquiry into who \u8220"really\u8221" owns the object. The civil law determines ownership, and only then does the {\i issur} question arise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzedakah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Ran\u8217's resolution is precisely the point we have been developing throughout: {\i tzedakah} is not simply a monetary dispute. When someone says {\i harei zo tzedakah}, the money acquires an actual {\i tzedakah} status \u8212- it is not merely that the {\i aniyim} now have a claim on those funds, as in a standard monetary dispute, but that the object itself has been designated, in a manner structurally similar to {\i hekdesh}, with a {\i tzedakah} identity. If one then uses those funds for something else, the violation is not simply one of stealing from the poor \u8212- it is a violation of the {\i tzedakah} status itself. That is why the Rambam and the Rashba are {\i machmir}: they treat this as {\i safek issur}, not {\i safek mamona}. The money is not simply owed to the poor the way a debt is owed; it has been invested with a quasi-sacred status through the act of verbal designation, parallel to a {\i neder}. And precisely because {\i tzedakah} functions in this {\i neder}-like capacity \u8212- affecting both the status of the object and creating a personal obligation \u8212- it belongs in the {\i safek issur} category, where we are {\i machmir} in a {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Menudeh Ani Lach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now move up to the top of 7a and into a new {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} presents the expression {\i menudeh ani lach} \u8212- \u8220"I am distanced from you\u8221" \u8212- and records that Rabbi Akiva {\i chochech bezeh lehachmir}: he was conflicted but inclined toward stringency, treating this as a {\i lashon neder}. The question is why this expression should be treated any differently from clearly recognized {\i lashon neder} formulations such as {\i murchak mimcha}, {\i mechunak mimcha}, or {\i kanuy mimcha}, all of which are recognized as {\i lashon neder} without dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is that {\i menudeh} most naturally evokes {\i nidui} or {\i cherem} \u8212- excommunication \u8212- rather than the language of personal vow-making. {\i Cherem} is not exactly a {\i neder}: it is an objective status that a person acquires, not something done vis-\u224 ?-vis a specific individual in the manner of a personal {\i neder}. That is why Rabbi Akiva does not rule with full confidence but rather is {\i chochech lehachmir} \u8212- he acknowledges the linguistic difficulty while leaning toward treating the expression as effective. And Abaye immediately concedes that even Rabbi Akiva agrees that one would not receive {\i malkot} for violating such a {\i neder}: it is at most a rabbinic {\i chumra} or a matter of {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nadina vs Mashmatena\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa sharpens the analysis. He distinguishes between two formulations. If someone says {\i nadina minach} \u8212- \u8220"I am separated from you\u8221" \u8212- {\i kuli alma lo peligi de\u8217'asur}: all agree it is forbidden. The word {\i nadina} can be understood as \u8220"I am distancing myself from you,\u8221" and this distancing \u8212- in the context of interpersonal relations \u8212- is the very mechanism of a {\i neder}: I am forbidding your possessions to me, thereby removing myself from you. {\i Minach} \u8212- \u8220"from you\u8221" \u8212- is the classic {\i lashon neder} of distancing. There is no dispute about this formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if someone says {\i mashmatena minach} \u8212- \u8220"I am in {\i cherem} from you\u8221" \u8212- {\i kuli alma sheru}: all agree it is permitted. The word {\i shamta} unambiguously means {\i cherem} in its technical sense. To say \u8220"I am in {\i cherem} from you\u8221" is meaningless as a {\i neder}: {\i cherem} is an objective communal status, not a relational prohibition between two individuals. It conveys nothing that functions as a {\i lashon neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between Rabbi Akiva and the Rabbis, then, focuses on the intermediate case: {\i menudeh ani lach} \u8212- \u8220"I am {\i menudeh} to you.\u8221" Rabbi Akiva holds that {\i lishna denidui hu} \u8212- the word {\i nidui} retains a connotation of distancing, akin to the root of separation. Even with the word {\i lach} (\u8220"to you\u8221"), it can be read as expressing a relational distancing from that person, which is the core of {\i lashon neder}. The Rabbis, by contrast, hold that {\i lishna deshamta hu} \u8212- the word {\i lach} transforms the meaning into something like \u8220"as far as you are concerned, I am in {\i cherem},\u8221" which is not {\i lashon neder} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Etymology Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why should {\i nidui} carry a connotation of separation distinct from {\i cherem}? Tosafot suggests, somewhat hesitantly, that the root {\i nidui} may be related to {\i niddah} \u8212- a term that itself connotes separation or removal. Some hold that {\i niddah} derives from {\i nad}, meaning \u8220"moved\u8221" or \u8220"displaced,\u8221" evoking the image of someone separated from the community, as a menstruating woman was, in certain ancient societies, removed to a separate space. In that etymological reading, {\i menudeh} means simply \u8220"separated\u8221" or \u8220"distanced\u8221" \u8212- without the specific technical connotations of {\i cherem} and {\i shamta}. If so, saying {\i menudeh ani lach} could naturally be read as \u8220"I am separating myself from you,\u8221" which is precisely the mechanism of a {\i neder}: forbidding your possessions to me and thereby creating distance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye thus concludes: {\i nadina minach} \u8212- using the verbal form that conveys relational distancing \u8212- is clearly {\i lashon neder}. {\i Mashmatena minach} \u8212- using the technical {\i cherem} language \u8212- is clearly not. {\i Menudeh ani lach} falls in between, and that is where Rabbi Akiva and the Rabbis disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes that the ruling of the {\i mishnah} conflicts with the position of Rav Chisda. A certain man had said {\i mashlemna be-nichsei de-berei de-Rav Yirmiyah bar Abba} \u8212- expressing himself in explicit {\i cherem} language regarding the possessions of the son of Rav Yirmiyah bar Abba. He came before Rav Chisda asking whether he was now forbidden from those possessions. Rav Chisda ruled: {\i leit de-chash lah le-ha de-Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- nobody follows Rabbi Akiva on this point. You are permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling reveals something important about how Rav Chisda reads Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position. The fact that Rav Chisda said \u8220"we do not follow Rabbi Akiva\u8221" \u8212- in a case where explicit {\i cherem} language was used \u8212- implies that Rabbi Akiva would have said even explicit {\i shamta} language creates a {\i neder} obligation. Rav Chisda thus reads Rabbi Akiva as holding that {\i kasavar de-bi-mishamteina paligi} \u8212- even the explicit {\i cherem} formulation falls within Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view of {\i lashon neder}. This is indeed a far-reaching position: even if you say unambiguously \u8220"I am in {\i cherem},\u8221" Rabbi Akiva would treat that as generating a {\i neder} status. The practical {\i halakhah}, as stated by Rav Chisda and accepted by the {\i rishonim}, follows neither version of Rabbi Akiva \u8212- neither the one who says {\i menudeh ani lach} creates a {\i neder} nor certainly the one who says explicit {\i cherem} language does.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nidui Laws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 In-Person Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having discussed {\i cherem} and {\i nidui} as linguistic categories, the {\i gemara} now pivots into a substantive discussion of the {\i halachot} governing {\i nidui} itself. Rav Ila reports in the name of Rav: {\i niduho be-fanav ein matirin lo ela be-fanav} \u8212- if someone was placed in {\i cherem} in his presence, the {\i cherem} can only be reversed in his presence as well. Conversely, if the {\i cherem} was imposed in his absence, it may be reversed either in his presence or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rishonim} debate the reason. One explanation holds that the act of removal must match the force of the act of imposition \u8212- if the {\i cherem} was imposed in full formal fashion with the person present, then the reversal must carry equivalent gravity. A {\i she\u8217'lo be-fanav} reversal would be insufficient to fully undo what was done {\i be-fanav}. A second, more interpersonal explanation holds that since the person was publicly humiliated by being brought before a {\i beit din} and placed in {\i cherem} in his presence, the restoration of his dignity requires an equally public acknowledgment \u8212- a reversal {\i be-fanav} \u8212- so that the community knows his status has changed. According to this second view, if the reversal was done {\i she\u8217'lo be-fanav}, it would be valid {\i bediavad}, since the concern is honor and public knowledge rather than the formal act itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shem Shamayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chanan then teaches in the name of Rav: {\i hashome\u8217'a azkarat hashem mipi chaveiro} \u8212- one who hears a fellow Jew utter God\u8217's name unnecessarily, {\i shelo letzorech}, is obligated to place that person in {\i cherem}. And if he fails to do so, {\i hu atzmo yehei venidui} \u8212- he himself deserves to be placed in {\i cherem}. The underlying authority is broad: this is not a matter limited to formal {\i batei din}. The Rabbis have effectively authorized anyone who witnesses such an act to impose {\i cherem} as a form of communal censure \u8212- a kind of citizen\u8217's enforcement on behalf of the halachic order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name & Poverty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} grounds this in a striking chain of reasoning. {\i Shechol makom she\u8217'azkarat hashem metzuya, aniut hi metzuya} \u8212- wherever God\u8217's name is treated too casually, poverty is found in its wake. And {\i aniut kemita} \u8212- poverty is like death, as we learn from the {\i pasuk} in which God tells Moshe that all those who sought to kill him have died. Since Datan and Aviram were still alive at that point, the {\i gemara} concludes they must have become poor, which demonstrates that poverty is considered equivalent to death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some {\i rishonim} connect this to the {\i pasuk}: {\i bechol hamakom asher azkir et shemi avo eilecha uverachticha} \u8212- wherever My name is properly invoked, I will come and bless you. By implication, where My name is invoked improperly, the opposite of {\i uverachticha} follows \u8212- poverty rather than blessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The chain is then completed: {\i kol makom shenatnu chachamim eineihem, o mita o oni} \u8212- wherever the Rabbis directed their displeasure toward someone, the result was either death or poverty. {\i Nidui} and {\i cherem} are the instruments of that rabbinic censure. Someone who treats God\u8217's name carelessly is bringing poverty and virtual death upon the community; the Rabbis respond with {\i nidui}, which itself carries the consequence of poverty or death. The {\i mida keneged mida} symmetry is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to {\i Masechet Nedarim} is also worth noting. The prohibition of {\i lo tisa et shem Hashem Elokecha lashav} is primarily associated with {\i shevuot}, not {\i nedarim}. But since {\i nedarim} and {\i shevuot} are closely linked, and since carelessness with language \u8212- whether of {\i nedarim} or of God\u8217's name \u8212- represents the same broader failure to honor the weight of words, this discussion finds its appropriate home here. The power of speech runs through all of it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Immediate Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Story Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba relates: he was standing before Rav Huna when Rav Huna overheard a woman utter God\u8217's name without necessity. {\i Shamteh v\u8217'altar shara lah} \u8212- he immediately placed her in {\i cherem} and then immediately reversed it. He imposed the censure and undid it in the same moment, as a demonstrative act of rabbinic displeasure, with no intention of allowing it to take lasting effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Inferences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws three {\i halakhic} inferences from this episode. First: {\i hashome\u8217'a shem shamayim mipi chaveiro tzarich l\u8217'nadoto} \u8212- one who hears God\u8217's name uttered needlessly is obligated to place the offender in {\i cherem}, as Rav Huna demonstrated by acting immediately. Second: {\i mendeh befanav va\u8217'afillu mendeh shelo befanav} \u8212- one can reverse a {\i nidui} whether in the person\u8217's presence or not; Rav Huna made a point of reversing it {\i befaneha}, in her presence, which indicates that had he chosen to reverse it {\i she\u8217'lo befaneha}, that too would have been valid. Third: {\i ein bein niddui l\u8217'hafara v\u8217'lo kelum} \u8212- there is no required waiting period between the imposition of {\i nidui} and its reversal; it may be undone immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This appears to contradict other {\i gemarot} that require a thirty-day wait. The resolution offered by the {\i rishonim} is that the thirty-day requirement applies to cases of genuine, serious violation \u8212- where someone persistently showed disrespect to halachic authority or to the Rabbis in a grave manner. For minor infractions, such as casual utterance of God\u8217's name, the {\i cherem} is purely expressive. It is a momentary act of censure, demonstrating that the community does not condone what occurred, without any intention to impose lasting social consequences. In such a case, immediate reversal is not only permitted but appropriate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting that the reversal of {\i cherem} is called {\i hafara} rather than {\i hattarah}. {\i Hattarah} is the process by which a {\i neder} is undone through a {\i chacham}, by establishing that the original intent never encompassed the present situation. {\i Hafara} is the annulment exercised by a husband over his wife\u8217's vow \u8212- an act of administrative power without reference to original intent. That the release from {\i cherem} is described as {\i hafara} underscores its nature: it is not a finding that the {\i cherem} was inapplicable or mistaken, but a simple exercise of authority to annul what authority had imposed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Self-Nidui Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Reversal Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Gidel teaches in the name of Rav: {\i talmid chacham menade l\u8217'atzmo u\u8217'meifer l\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- a {\i talmid chacham} can place himself in {\i cherem} and can also release himself from that {\i cherem}. The Ran qualifies this: the principle applies specifically in cases like those we have been discussing, where the person does not genuinely deserve the {\i cherem} \u8212- the self-imposed {\i nidui} is a formal or expressive gesture rather than a deserved sanction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Imprisonment Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this: {\i pshita} \u8212- is this not obvious? And the reply is: {\i ein chavush matir atzmo m\u8217'beit ha\u8217'asurim} \u8212- a prisoner cannot free himself from prison. If a {\i talmid chacham} truly deserves a {\i cherem} \u8212- if he has genuinely acted in a manner requiring rabbinic censure \u8212- then he cannot unilaterally reverse that {\i cherem} on his own authority. The act of reversal requires an external authority. The self-reversal power exists only where the self-imposed {\i cherem} was never deserved in the first place, or was imposed as an expressive or procedural act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paradigm case is that of Mar Zutra the Pious. When a {\i yeshiva} student was required to be placed in {\i cherem} for some misconduct, Mar Zutra would first place himself in {\i cherem} \u8212- {\i mashmat nafshia b\u8217'reisha} \u8212- and only then place the student. Two explanations are offered for this practice. The first is that even when the {\i cherem} is necessary, it is a necessary evil \u8212- putting a {\i talmid chacham} in {\i cherem}, even for genuine misbehavior, carries a weight of gravity, and Mar Zutra felt that doing so required him to share in some of that gravity by subjecting himself to it as well, a form of solidarity or humility before the act. The second is practical and almost mnemonic: since someone in {\i cherem} may not reside normally with his household, when Mar Zutra returned home at the end of the day he would be reminded that he himself was in {\i cherem} \u8212- and thus prompted to reverse both his own {\i cherem} and then the student\u8217's. The sequence of reversal was also deliberate: {\i shari lei l\u8217'nafshia} first, placing himself back in a state of full standing, and only then {\i v\u8217'hadar shari lei}, reversing the student\u8217's {\i cherem} \u8212- paralleling the principle of the {\i Kohen Gadol} who first performs {\i kaparah} for himself before doing so for others. The student would thus remain in {\i cherem} for several hours, which was the intended period of censure.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Niddui & Cherem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 8 continues from the preceding discussion of {\i niddui} and {\i cherem}. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's language of {\i mudrani mimcha} and {\i menudani mimcha} had drawn the {\i gemara} into an extended treatment of these forms of excommunication. On the one hand, there are clear parallels between {\i niddui} and {\i cherem} and the world of {\i nedarim} \u8212- the {\i gemara} had discussed performing {\i hatara} to annul a {\i cherem}, and there was a question whether certain formulations might constitute {\i lashon neder}, which is Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position. The {\i gemara}, however, rules that we do not {\i pasken} accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Power of Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What lends this entire discussion its coherence within {\i Masechet Nedarim} is the centrality of speech. The preceding {\i daf} addressed the case of someone who uses God\u8217's name loosely \u8212- {\i levatala} \u8212- and is consequently placed in {\i cherem}, itself an act of speech, which is then released through another act of speech. This cycle of verbal creation, verbal sanction, and verbal release speaks directly to the animating theme of the {\i masechet}. It is in this context that the {\i gemara} now introduces another statement of Giddel in the name of Rav, one that bears even more directly on the concerns of {\i Masechet Nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath & Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the top of 8a, Giddel in the name of Rav asks: {\i m\u8217'nayin she\u8217'nishba\u8217'im l\u8217'kayem et hamitzvah} \u8212- from where do we know that one may take a {\i shevuah}, an oath, to fulfill a {\i mitzvah}? The source is the verse {\i nishbati va\u8217'akayemah lishmor mishpatei tzidkecha} \u8212- \u8220"I have sworn and I will fulfill, to observe Your righteous edicts.\u8221" The {\i gemara} understands this to mean either that it is a praiseworthy thing to do \u8212- not a concern of {\i shem shamayim levatala}, since the intent is to spur oneself to action \u8212- or that such a {\i shevuah} is actually binding. The {\i gemara} focuses on the second possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Oath Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is immediately apparent: {\i v\u8217'halo mushba ve\u8217'omed meHar Sinai} \u8212- is the person not already foresworn from Sinai to observe the commandments? Two related principles apply. If one takes a {\i shevuah} to {\i batel} a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- saying, for instance, that he will not sit in the {\i sukkah} \u8212- it is non-binding because he is pre-foresworn to observe it. And conversely, if one takes a {\i shevuah} that he {\i will} sit in the {\i sukkah} and then fails to do so, he has not transgressed the violation of that {\i shevuah}, because {\i ein shevuah chala al shevuah} \u8212- a second oath cannot take effect upon an existing oath. The {\i gemara} is thus pressed to explain how an oath to perform a {\i mitzvah} could have any legal force at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath as Zeruzon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by saying {\i lo} \u8212- technically it is not binding \u8212- but this teaches us {\i l\u8217'zeruzon}, {\i mishum l\u8217'ziruzei nafshei}: one is permitted to take such an oath even without binding force, because it serves as a powerful means of encouraging oneself to fulfill an existing obligation. The point is pragmatic: we know that having obligations does not guarantee their fulfillment, and finding ways to motivate ourselves to perform what we are already supposed to do is entirely legitimate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} is not satisfied with this simple reading and advances an alternative. He questions whether {\i ein shevuah chala al shevuah} necessarily means the second oath has no effect whatsoever. Perhaps, the {\i Ran} suggests, it only means one does not bring a {\i korban} for it \u8212- {\i de\u8217'ha lo mechayev korban} \u8212- but there may still be a transgression of {\i bal yachel}, the prohibition against violating one\u8217's oath, {\i afilu bedvar mitzvah}. He proceeds to argue from a comparison between {\i shevuah} and {\i neder} found elsewhere in the {\i gemara}, concluding that the principle of {\i ein shevuah chala al shevuah} pertains only to liability for a {\i korban}, while liability for the {\i lav} of {\i bal yachel} \u8212- and thus {\i malkos} \u8212- remains in force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran}\u8217's reading means that the {\i chiddush} of Giddel in the name of Rav is not to tell us that the oath takes effect \u8212- that is already assumed \u8212- but rather to tell us it is an affirmatively good thing to do as a way of encouraging fulfillment of one\u8217's obligations. The {\i Sifsei Avraham} at the bottom of the {\i daf} cites the {\i Ramban} in {\i Parshas Chukas} who takes the opposite position from the {\i Ran}: if one takes a {\i shevuah} to perform a {\i mitzvah} and then fails to do so, he incurs neither a {\i korban} nor {\i malkos} \u8212- the oath has no effect at all. On this reading, the point of Giddel\u8217's statement is simply that it is a legitimate and even meritorious practice to take such an oath to encourage oneself, even though it carries no halachic consequence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder Mitzva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashne Perek Zeh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a second and closely related statement of Giddel in the name of Rav: {\i ha\u8217'omer eshne perek zeh} \u8212- one who says \u8220"I will study this {\i perek} today,\u8221" or \u8220"I am studying this {\i masechet}\u8221" \u8212- {\i neder gadol nadar l\u8217'Elokei Yisrael}, he has made a great vow to the God of Israel. This is a striking {\i gemara}. The person used no {\i lashon neder} and no {\i lashon shevuah} \u8212- he simply said he was going to do something. How, then, does any form of {\i shevuah} or {\i neder} take effect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Binding Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} treats the statement {\i eshne} \u8212- \u8220"I will study\u8221" \u8212- as {\i lashon shevuah}, as a verbal commitment to perform an act. That immediately raises the same problem as before: {\i v\u8217'halo mushba ve\u8217'omed hu}, is he not already foresworn from Sinai? {\i V\u8217'ein shevuah chala al shevuah} \u8212- how can a second oath take effect here? Nor can we say this is simply another formulation of the first principle, that it is permitted as an act of {\i zeruzon} to encourage oneself \u8212- if that were the whole point, it would be identical to what was already taught in the first statement of Giddel and would add nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Optional Learning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore resolves the difficulty differently here: {\i d\u8217'ei bei patar nafshei bikrias Shema shacharis ve\u8217'arvis} \u8212- a person can actually discharge his basic obligation of {\i Talmud Torah} through recitation of {\i Shema} morning and evening. The minimum threshold of {\i Talmud Torah} is derived from the verse {\i v\u8217'shinantam l\u8217'vanecha}, together with {\i uvshochbecha uvkumecha}, from which we learn that the explicit biblical mandate of {\i Talmud Torah} is fulfilled through {\i Krias Shema}. This is not a coincidence of formulation: the {\i psukim} from which we derive the {\i halacha} of {\i Krias Shema} are themselves, at the {\i pshat} level, {\i psukim} about {\i Talmud Torah} \u8212- which is why there are serious positions among the {\i Tannaim} that {\i Krias Shema} is itself only {\i m\u8217'derabanan}, since those {\i psukim} in their plain sense are about {\i Talmud Torah}, not about {\i Shema} as such. This also explains why the {\i brachos} of {\i Krias Shema} discharge the {\i brachos} of {\i Talmud Torah}: the recitation of {\i Shema} is understood as a fulfillment of {\i uvshochbecha uvkumecha}, which at the {\i pshat} level is the {\i mitzvah} of {\i Talmud Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the bare minimum of {\i Talmud Torah} can be discharged through {\i Krias Shema}, a person is not obligated to learn any particular {\i perek} or {\i masechet}. He could learn all day and satisfy his obligation by reviewing material he has already covered, without ever getting to this specific {\i perek}. Therefore, he is not {\i mushba ve\u8217'omed} with respect to this particular study, and a {\i shevuah} \u8212- or, as the {\i gemara} calls it, a {\i neder} \u8212- can take effect on it. {\i Mishum hachi chala shevuah aleiya.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmud Torah Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether {\i Krias Shema} really constitutes {\i Talmud Torah} in the full sense, given that it functions primarily as {\i kabalas ol malchus shamayim}. The broader question of the extent of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i Talmud Torah} is significant: the plain sense of this {\i gemara} appears to be that the minimum is {\i Krias Shema shacharis ve\u8217'arvis}, which is consistent with a number of other {\i gemaras}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's formulation here \u8212- that one can exempt oneself through {\i Krias Shema} \u8212- is the {\i pshat} of the passage, and has been used as a source for discussions about the scope of {\i mitzvas Talmud Torah} ever since, making it an apt text for a {\i shiur} on {\i Shavuos} night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran on Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran}, however, is uncomfortable with the {\i gemara}\u8217's apparent implication that a person is not obligated to learn {\i Torah} all day. In his view \u8212- citing the {\i gemara} in {\i Menachos}, {\i lo nitnu divrei Torah ella l\u8217'ochlei haman}, and the principle of {\i v\u8217'hagita bo yomam valayla} \u8212- every person is required to learn {\i Torah} to the maximum of his ability, {\i yomam valayla}, using every available moment. The {\i Ran} therefore resolves the {\i gemara} differently: {\i d\u8217'hacha d\u8217'mishtava l\u8217'kayeim mitzvah she\u8217'eina meforeshes b\u8217'hedya} \u8212- a {\i shevuah} to fulfill a {\i mitzvah} takes effect when the obligation is not stated explicitly in the {\i Torah}. Since the only {\i explicitly} stated obligation is {\i Krias Shema shacharis ve\u8217'arvis}, all learning beyond that \u8212- though obligatory \u8212- is not explicit, and therefore a {\i shevuah} does take effect upon it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This alternative reading preserves both the {\i Ran}\u8217's view of maximal {\i Talmud Torah} obligation and the {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion that the {\i shevuah} is effective. One could also propose a simpler resolution even granting maximal obligation: even if one is obligated to learn every available moment, one is not obligated to learn specifically {\i this masechet}. One could spend every moment in {\i chazara} on {\i Bava Kamma} and never arrive at {\i Bava Metzia}, and have discharged one\u8217's obligation entirely. Therefore there is no {\i mushba ve\u8217'omed} with respect to this specific {\i perek}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevuah vs Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that a statement of intent to perform a {\i mitzvah} creates some form of binding obligation, the {\i gemara} calls it a {\i neder gadol} \u8212- a great {\i neder}. This creates two interrelated difficulties. First, the person used neither {\i lashon shevuah} nor {\i lashon neder} \u8212- he simply said he would do something. Second, and more conceptually significant, is the question of what the {\i gemara} means by calling this a {\i neder} at all, since the {\i gemara} simultaneously treats it as raising the problem of {\i ein shevuah chala al shevuah}, which is the language of {\i shevuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual distinction between {\i shevuah} and {\i neder} is fundamental. A {\i shevuah} is a sworn declaration in the name of God: if one fails to fulfill it, one has violated an oath and has, in a sense, desecrated God\u8217's name by invoking Him as witness. A {\i neder}, by contrast, is not a promise in this sense \u8212- it is an act of creation. When one says {\i harei zeh asur}, one is not making a promise; one is bringing about a change in the metaphysical status of an object. The wrongness of violating a {\i neder} lies not in breaking a sworn promise but in failing to live up to the obligation one has constructed for oneself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yaakov\u8217's Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The biblical model of this phenomenon is {\i vayidar Yaakov neder lemor} \u8212- and Jacob made a vow. Yet when one reads the actual words Jacob spoke \u8212- if God protects me and provides for me, then whatever You give me I will tithe to You \u8212- there is no formal invocation of an oath, no declaration in God\u8217's name. Jacob spoke with a quality of personal commitment and religious conviction, and the {\i Torah} characterizes the result as a {\i neder}. This is precisely the phenomenon the {\i gemara} is describing: a person who says {\i eshne perek zeh} with genuine commitment and the sense of creating an obligation is engaging in the same kind of act as Jacob\u8217's vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashon Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also clarifies why no particular formula is required for a {\i neder}. We have already seen in the {\i mishnah} that {\i harei zeh asur alai} \u8212- \u8220"this is forbidden to me\u8221" \u8212- constitutes {\i lashon neder} even without the word {\i konam} or any other technical term. Similarly, when one says \u8220"I am committed to learning this {\i perek},\u8221" that can function as an act of {\i kabbalah} \u8212- of accepting upon oneself an obligation \u8212- without any magic words. The line between an ordinary expression of intent and a genuine {\i neder} may not always be obvious, but the concept itself is entirely grounded in the {\i Torah}\u8217's own use of the term: {\i vayidar Yaakov neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this a {\i neder} conceptually rather than a {\i shevuah} is precisely that it creates a metaphysical reality \u8212- a personal obligation \u8212- rather than merely invoking God\u8217's name as witness to a future act. When one says \u8220"I am accepting upon myself the obligation to do this,\u8221" the wrong in failing to fulfill it lies in having failed to live up to the status one constructed for oneself, not in having violated a sworn oath. The {\i gemara}\u8217's language of {\i neder gadol nadar l\u8217'Elokei Yisrael} thus captures something real: this is the Torah\u8217's own category of {\i neder mitzva}, a religious commitment to perform a specific act of divine service, modeled on the biblical narrative of Jacob\u8217's vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question that then arises is where {\i Masechet Nedarim} has until now recognized anything of this kind. The entire {\i masechet} has been built around {\i nedarim} as a mechanism for forbidding objects \u8212- {\i konam}, {\i harei zeh asur}, objects rendered prohibited by one\u8217's declaration. Here we are encountering something different: a {\i neder} that creates a personal obligation to {\i do} something rather than making something forbidden. And notably, the {\i gemara} still raises the language of {\i ein shevuah chala al shevuah} in connection with this {\i neder}, suggesting that when a {\i neder} creates a personal obligation it is treated, for certain purposes, as equivalent to a {\i shevuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh: Neder Tzedaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} addresses this directly. In the first wide line of his commentary, he writes: {\i velav haynu neder mamash} \u8212- this is not a {\i neder} in the strict sense of {\i matfis bedavar hanadur}, making something forbidden by reference to a prohibited object. Rather, {\i ela mechashvinan lei ke\u8217'ilu nadar litzdaka} \u8212- we treat it as though one made a {\i neder tzedaka}. The source he invokes is {\i neder beficha zu tzedaka}: a commitment to give {\i tzedakah} creates a genuine binding obligation. The {\i Rosh} thus uses {\i neder tzedaka} as the halakhic model for {\i neder mitzva} \u8212- one can similarly obligate oneself to perform other {\i mitzvos} by adopting the same form of commitment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is that {\i neder tzedaka} itself represents a recognized halachic category in which one\u8217's words create a personal obligation, not merely a change in an object\u8217's status. Whether this category is {\i d\u8217'oraisa} or {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} is not entirely clear from the {\i Rosh}, but it functions as a real obligation with real consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder on Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important question: can a {\i neder} take effect upon an existing {\i mitzvah} obligation? The {\i gemara} elsewhere \u8212- in the {\i perek} of {\i elu nedarim} \u8212- rules that a {\i neder} can indeed take effect on a {\i dvar mitzvah}, treating it like a {\i dvar hareshus}. A {\i shevuah} to not sit in the {\i sukkah} is ineffective because it directly countermands the {\i mitzvah}. But a {\i neder} that renders the {\i sukkah asur} is an indirect mechanism: the {\i sukkah} becomes forbidden and then when the {\i mitzvah} time arrives, there is a collision \u8212- this is treated differently, as the {\i neder} itself is effective even as it conflicts with the {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i Ran} draws a distinction. A regular {\i neder} \u8212- the classic {\i matfis bedavar assur} mechanism \u8212- is one thing. But a {\i neder} of the {\i tzedakah} type, which creates a personal obligation rather than forbidding an object, is effectively functioning like a {\i shevuah}: both obligate the person to do something. Therefore, the {\i Ran} argues, such a {\i neder} follows the rules of a {\i shevuah} with respect to existing obligations \u8212- it cannot take effect on something one is already obligated to perform. {\i V\u8217'gamrinan mishvua d\u8217'einah chala al devar mitzvah.} The {\i chiddush}, then, is precisely that it {\i can} take effect on a {\i mitzvah} one is not specifically obligated to fulfill in this particular form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a broader conceptual point. Until now the world of {\i nedarim} in the {\i masechet} has been the world of forbidden objects. Here we enter a different realm: the {\i neder} as a mechanism for creating personal religious obligations. This realm is rooted in the {\i Torah}\u8217's own narratives \u8212- {\i vayidar Yaakov neder}, and the use of {\i neder} language in connection with {\i cherem} and {\i tzedakah} throughout {\i Tanach}. In all these cases, the underlying act is a commitment made to God in a religious context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why does {\i neder tzedakah} work at all, given that the standard {\i neder} mechanism operates on objects? A possible answer \u8212- one attributed to the {\i Ramban} \u8212- is that a {\i neder tzedakah} effectively creates a kind of lien on one\u8217's property, even if no specific property is identified. The money ultimately becomes a physical object upon which the {\i neder} rests, even if indirectly. If so, one might use {\i neder tzedakah} as a model for {\i neder mitzva} in other contexts: the {\i neder} to learn a specific {\i perek} is similarly treated as creating an object-like obligation in the sphere of religious life, modeled on the {\i tzedakah} paradigm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, there is no suggestion in any of these discussions that one can use this form of {\i neder mitzva} to create an obligation for a purely secular act. The entire framework operates only in the religious sphere \u8212- {\i vayidar Yaakov neder}, one is {\i noder} to God. One cannot use a {\i neder} to obligate oneself to mow one\u8217's lawn. The category is specifically that of making a religious commitment to God, in the space where the act is not explicitly required in this specific form, and where one\u8217's words can construct a genuine personal obligation of divine service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Also closely related is the well-known principle that performing a {\i davar tov} three times creates the equivalent of a {\i neder} \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'ilu kibel al atzmo beneder}. This again points to the same underlying category: through repeated action, one can implicitly construct a personal religious obligation, just as through explicit verbal commitment. This is also the framework invoked in {\i hatarat nedarim}, where one declares {\i lo mitzad shevuah velo mitzad neder} while seeking release from {\i chovoteinu} \u8212- the accumulated commitments, verbal or behavioral, that have created personal religious obligations. All of this is ultimately rooted in the same biblical {\i psukim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vow to Travel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nesak V\u8217'Niul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues: {\i ha\u8217'omer lechavero neissak v\u8217'niul l\u8217'bei Tarmodei} \u8212- one who says to his friend, \u8220"Let\u8217's get up and go to such-and-such a place.\u8221" Even if the friend does not comply, the one who made the statement is personally obligated to go. Moreover, the implication is that he must make sure to arrive before his friend \u8212- to be there first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ezekiel Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source is God\u8217's command to Ezekiel: {\i kum tzei el habik\u8217'ah}, get up, go out to the valley, and there I will speak with you. When Ezekiel went out, the {\i kavod Hashem} was already there waiting for him. The one who initiates the arrangement has an obligation not only to follow through, but to be present and ready before those he has invited.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Niddui Dreams\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dream Niddui\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the topic of {\i niddui}. Rav Yosef teaches: if a person has a dream in which he is placed in {\i cherem}, he must take it seriously as a possible sign of divine displeasure \u8212- {\i kdeniddin min haMakom}. This concept, that a person can be {\i niduya l\u8217'Shamayim}, appears prominently in {\i Masechet Taanit} in the context of drought, where the community is called upon to act {\i kdeniddin min haMakom} in response to the apparent withdrawal of divine favor. A dream of being placed in {\i cherem} is treated as a potential signal of such heavenly censure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten People\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To be released from such a {\i niddui}, the person requires ten people \u8212- {\i tzrichah asarah b\u8217'nei adam l\u8217'hatirah}. The {\i gemara} specifies further: {\i v\u8217'hu d\u8217'tani hilchata} \u8212- only if these ten have actually studied {\i halachos}, meaning Talmudic learning, not merely {\i Mishnah} alone. The reason ten specifically are needed is that ten constitute a {\i minyan}, in the presence of whom there is {\i Shechinah sheryuyah} \u8212- divine presence. If God\u8217's presence rests among ten people, they can act as divine representatives to release the person from the heavenly {\i niddui}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crossroads Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If ten people who have studied {\i halachos} cannot be assembled, the {\i gemara} offers an alternative: {\i neizil v\u8217'nisvei a\u8217'parashat drachim} \u8212- go sit at a crossroads, and keep offering {\i Shalom aleichem} to passersby until one has encountered ten people who have learned {\i halachos}. The {\i rishonim} debate the precise mechanism here. Some understand it to mean one continues greeting people until ten {\i talmidei chachamim} reciprocate and say {\i Shalom aleichem} back, and this somehow functions as a form of release. The majority of {\i rishonim}, however, incline toward reading this as a form of protection \u8212- greeting and being greeted by ten learned people provides some measure of divine protection until a proper {\i beis din} can be convened to effect a full release.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Releaser Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha posed a question to Rav Ashi: if one knows in real life who it was that placed him in {\i cherem} in the dream, can that person release him from it? Rav Ashi responds: {\i dilma shlicha hu d\u8217'shadruhu mishmeih d\u8217'Shamaya} \u8212- perhaps in the dream that person was acting as a divine agent, sent from Heaven to execute the {\i niddui}. If so, going to him in real life accomplishes nothing: he was authorized to impose the {\i niddui} on Heaven\u8217's behalf, but he has no authority to rescind what Heaven decreed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dream Cancellation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question was posed: what if, within the same dream, the very same person who placed one in {\i niddui} subsequently released him from it? The dream would appear to cancel itself out, leaving no remaining obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dream Nonsense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa responds with a striking observation: {\i keshem she-ee efshar l\u8217'bar belo teven, kach ee efshar lachalom belo devarim betelim} \u8212- just as it is impossible for grain to exist without straw, so it is impossible for a dream to exist without some nonsense in it. Not every element of a dream carries prophetic weight. One must therefore remain {\i choshesh} that the part of the dream in which one was placed in {\i niddui} was the true, meaningful element \u8212- and the part in which one was released was the meaningless straw. The obligation to seek proper release therefore remains.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband as Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a case: Ravina had a {\i neder} that his wife had made upon herself \u8212- a {\i neder} of a type that was either beyond the window for a husband\u8217's unilateral annulment, or was not the kind that a husband can annul through {\i hafara} alone. He came before Rav Ashi seeking guidance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Representation Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina asked: {\i ba\u8217'al mahu she\u8217'ye\u8217'aseh shaliach l\u8217'charatat ishto} \u8212- can a husband serve as an agent representing his wife in the process of {\i charata}, the expression of regret that is the basis for {\i hatarat nedarim}? The scenario is that the wife has explained the circumstances to her husband, who would then present the grounds for {\i charata} before the {\i beis din} on her behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Already Gathered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi answered: {\i ee michnafim in, ee lav lav} \u8212- if the {\i beis din} is already assembled, yes; if not, no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Inferences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} extracts three inferences from this exchange. First: {\i ba\u8217'al na\u8217'aseh shaliach l\u8217'charatat ishto} \u8212- a husband can indeed represent his wife. Second: {\i lo sharei l\u8217'mishrei nidra b\u8217'atra d\u8217'rabeih} \u8212- it is not appropriate to annul a {\i neder} in the location of one\u8217's {\i rebbe}, which explains why Ravina did not convene his own {\i beis din} and instead came to Rav Ashi \u8212- raising the larger question of whether a man can annul his own wife\u8217's vow, and the broader principle of deferring to one\u8217's {\i rebbe}\u8217's authority. Third: {\i ki michnafim shapir dami} \u8212- if the {\i beis din} is already gathered, it is entirely proper to proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an important dispute among the {\i rishonim} about the underlying principles here. The {\i Rambam} understands the husband\u8217's representation of his wife as a unique exception grounded in {\i ishto k\u8217'gufo} \u8212- wife and husband are treated as a single legal unit in certain respects \u8212- and not as an instance of a general rule permitting representation before {\i beis din}. For the {\i Rambam}, the fundamental requirement is that every individual appear personally before the {\i beis din}. The condition that the {\i beis din} already be assembled is, on his reading, a further constraint on this already exceptional leniency: since we are permitting something inherently unusual, we do not extend it to cases where it would require specially convening a {\i beis din} for this purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} disagrees sharply. {\i Tosafot} holds that representation before {\i beis din} is permissible as a general matter \u8212- one may even send a letter, and the {\i beis din} may annul a {\i neder} without the person\u8217's physical presence. The specific problem in the case of a husband representing his wife is not representation per se, but the husband\u8217's vested interest in obtaining the {\i hatara}. Because he benefits from his wife\u8217's release, he may unconsciously \u8212- or even consciously \u8212- present the circumstances in a slanted manner to make the case for {\i charata} seem stronger than it truly is. Therefore, the requirement that the {\i beis din} already be assembled serves as a check: if he is not going to all the effort and expense of specially convening a {\i beis din}, his investment in the outcome is less conspicuous and the concern about biased representation is accordingly reduced.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shamta in Rebbe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shamta Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues: {\i v\u8217'shamta afilu b\u8217'asrei d\u8217'rabbai} \u8212- in a case of {\i niddui}, even in the location of one\u8217's {\i rebbe}, one may release the person from the {\i shamta}. This is a surprising ruling. One would have thought the opposite: releasing someone from {\i cherem} is a more significant assertion of rabbinic authority than annulling a {\i neder}, since {\i hatarat cherem} involves representing communal standards and accepting someone back from a state of religious censure \u8212- it is more public, more institutional, more clearly an exercise of {\i rabbinic} power. It would therefore seem that performing {\i hatarat cherem} in one\u8217's {\i rebbe}\u8217's presence would be a greater usurpation of authority than performing {\i hatarat nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kavod HaRav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses this tension directly. {\i Tosafot} explains: {\i shamta afilu b\u8217'asrei d\u8217'rabbai shapir dami lehatir hashamta d\u8217'pe\u8217'amim shelo yehei rav mzuman} \u8212- sometimes the {\i rebbe} is not available or is otherwise occupied, {\i v\u8217'ein l\u8217'akev heter hashamta} \u8212- and it is not fair to delay the release of a {\i shamta}. Leaving a person in a state of {\i niddui} is an ongoing harm that cannot simply wait for a more convenient moment. The principle is analogous to what the {\i gemara} says elsewhere \u8212- {\i b\u8217'makom sheyesh chilul Hashem ein cholkin kavod l\u8217'rav} \u8212- when the honor of Heaven demands action, the honor due to a {\i rebbe} yields. Here the pressing need of the person standing before you demands that one act immediately, even if doing so involves assuming an authority that would normally be deferred to one\u8217's {\i rebbe}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yachid Mumche\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore: {\i v\u8217'yachid mumche sharei shamta} \u8212- even an individual expert, without a full {\i beis din}, may release someone from a {\i shamta}. This principle applies even in the location of one\u8217's {\i rebbe}, combining both of the preceding points into a single halakhic ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sun of Righteousness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear of God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now offers a closing teaching on the theme that has run through this entire section. A chain of seven {\i Amoraim} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon ben Zavid citing Rabbi Yitzchak bar Tavla, citing Rabbi Chiya Aricha of Rabbi Acha\u8217's academy, citing Rabbi Zeira, citing Rabbi Elazar, citing Rabbi Chanina, citing Rabbi Mayasha in the name of Rabbi Yehuda bar Ilai \u8212- transmit an interpretation of the verse {\i v\u8217'zarcha lachem yirei shmi shemesh tzedakah u\u8217'marpe}: to those who fear My name, the sun of righteousness will shine with healing. The {\i gemara} identifies {\i yirei shmi} as {\i bnei adam shehen yere\u8217'in l\u8217'hotzi shem shamayim l\u8217'vatala} \u8212- people who are careful not to pronounce God\u8217's name loosely. This circles back neatly to the discussion that preceded this {\i daf}, where a person who uses God\u8217's name {\i levatala} is placed in {\i cherem}: those who fear God\u8217's name precisely avoid that misuse of speech.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Olam Haba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} offers one reading of the verse: {\i shma mina charga d\u8217'yoma masi} \u8212- the {\i mefarshim}, including the {\i Ran} and {\i Tosafot}, explain {\i charga d\u8217'yoma} as the fine particles or motes visible in sunlight. On this reading the verse is taken straightforwardly: sunlight brings physical healing, and that is what the verse promises to those who fear God\u8217's name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Reish Lakish} reads the verse very differently, applying it to {\i olam haba}: {\i ein gehenom l\u8217'olam haba} \u8212- there is no {\i gehenom} in the world to come in the conventional sense. Instead, {\i hakadosh baruch hu motzi chamah m\u8217'nartika} \u8212- God will draw the sun out of its sheath, and the same sun will serve two opposite purposes simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous Wicked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tzadikim mitrap\u8217'in bah u\u8217'rshaim nidonin bah} \u8212- the righteous will be healed by that sun, and the wicked will be judged and burned by it. Not only will the righteous be healed, but {\i shemitangin bah} \u8212- they will derive actual pleasure from it, as the verse continues: {\i v\u8217'yatzatem u\u8217'fishtem k\u8217'eglei marbek}, you will go forth and frolic like calves released from the stall. The wicked, by the same sun, will be punished. The same reality, the same light \u8212- experienced entirely differently depending on who one is.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mumche Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smicha Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's ruling that {\i yachid mumche} may release from {\i shamta} raises an important halakhic question: what precisely qualifies one as a {\i yachid mumche} for this purpose? Does it require {\i smicha} in the classical sense \u8212- ordination traceable back to {\i Moshe Rabbeinu}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taz View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Taz} addresses this question. He rules that {\i yachid mumche} for purposes of {\i shamta} does not necessarily require classical {\i smicha}. Rather, what is required is that the individual be a genuine {\i chacham} who is {\i baki b\u8217'hilchos nidduyin} \u8212- expert in the laws of {\i niddui}. Knowledge and expertise in the relevant halachic domain is the defining criterion, not formal ordination in the chain from Sinai.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Shamta Diff\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a meaningful distinction between the requirements for {\i hatarat nedarim} and the requirements for releasing from {\i shamta}. For {\i hatarat nedarim}, the {\i gemara} elsewhere and {\i Tosafot} raise the question of whether {\i parshas elohim} \u8212- the scriptural basis for the authority of those who annul {\i nedarim} \u8212- requires {\i semuchim}, formally ordained judges. The {\i Taz} discusses the difference between these two domains: the requirements for {\i hatarat nedarim} and the requirements for {\i shamta} may not be identical, and although both topics have been treated in parallel throughout this {\i daf}, they remain distinct areas with their own rules. This remains a matter of ongoing halakhic discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 9\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Knidrei Reshaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Mishnah Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ninth daf of Nedarim opens with a new mishnah \u8212- one that is, from the outset, slightly peculiar. The mishnah introduces a category of vow phrased as {\i knidrei reshaim}, \u8220"like the vows of the wicked,\u8221" and proceeds to establish whether such a formulation creates a binding {\i neder}, {\i nezirut}, or {\i shvuah}. The broader context is that of {\i yadot} \u8212- the continuation of the discussion about partial or indirect formulations that may or may not create binding vows. The question is always: when someone leaves part of the sentence unspoken, do we know how to fill in the blank, and is that incomplete formulation effective?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The full range of the mishnah covers three categories: the person who says something {\i knidrei reshaim} may be intending {\i nezirut}, may be intending an obligation regarding a {\i korban}, or may be making a {\i shvuah}. The opening clause \u8212- {\i knidrei reshaim} \u8212- is what drives the entire analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah\u8217's statement is {\i nadar binazir u-vekorban u-vishvuah}: one who vows regarding {\i nazir}, {\i korban}, and {\i shvuah}. The basic scenario is that someone says {\i knidrei reshaim hareini} \u8212- \u8220"like the vows of the wicked, behold I am\u8221" \u8212- and leaves the end of the sentence incomplete. Depending on context, {\i hareini} might be followed by {\i nazir}; or one might say {\i alai} (\u8220"upon me\u8221"), suggesting a {\i korban}; or one might say {\i heimenu} (\u8220"from it\u8221"), gesturing toward a {\i shvuah} not to eat from something. In each case, part of the critical content of the statement is missing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the standard {\i yad} problem: a {\i yad mochiach} \u8212- a probative indication \u8212- suffices, but a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mochiach} \u8212- an unclear indication \u8212- does not, and the question is always whether there is enough context to determine what was meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiddush Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} of the mishnah, however, lies not in the incomplete end of the sentence but in the opening qualifier. The mishnah tells us: {\i knidrei reshaim} works. {\i Knidrei kesherim lo amar klum} \u8212- if someone says \u8220"like the vows of {\i kesherim},\u8221" righteous people, they have said nothing, because {\i kesherim} do not make {\i nedarim}. {\i Knidvosam}, however \u8212- if someone says \u8220"like the {\i nedavah} of {\i kesherim}\u8221" \u8212- that does work. And by implication, {\i knidvos reshaim} would not work either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also an item conspicuously absent from the end of the mishnah: {\i shvuah} does not appear in the closing formulation. The mishnah says {\i nadar binazir u-vekorban}, but does not add {\i u-vishvuah}. This asymmetry will require explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the mishnah is fundamentally communicating, then, is that {\i reshaim} make {\i nedarim} and {\i kesherim} make {\i nedavos} \u8212- and not vice versa. This sets up a crucial discussion about the intrinsic religious value, or disvalue, of making {\i nedarim} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder vs.\u160 ?Nedava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran explains that the word {\i nedava} \u8212- freely given donation \u8212- carries the connotation expressed in the verse {\i v\u8217'chol nediv lev asher yidvenu libo}: the generosity of the heart. A {\i nedava}, then, refers to an act performed out of sincere, uncomplicated religious motivation \u8212- a pure desire to serve God or to give something to God. A {\i neder}, by contrast, reflects a more complex motivational landscape. A person making a {\i neder} may have religious intentions, but there are other things operating in the background: guilt, shame, an impetuous desire to correct some failing, a wish to appear more {\i frum}, or simply complicated feelings that have been channeled into a religious act. The religious component is present, but it is not the clean, freely flowing, uncomplicated motivation that a {\i nedava} represents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It follows, then, that calling someone who makes a {\i neder} a {\i rasha} does not mean they are wicked in all their ways. The Ran qualifies this as {\i bedavar zeh} \u8212- in this particular act. Within the narrow scope of making {\i nedarim} and {\i nedavos}, a {\i neder} reflects a lesser or at least more problematic act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction, it should be noted, is not a formal legal one. It is not about the mechanics of how you produce the {\i neder} or {\i nedava}. It is about describing the religious mindset and motivation of the person making it. Therefore: {\i nidrei reshaim} is a meaningful phrase \u8212- it refers to vows made from complicated motivation. {\i Nidrei kesherim} is not meaningful \u8212- {\i kesherim} simply do not operate that way. {\i Nidvos kesherim} is meaningful \u8212- that is precisely what a {\i kasher} person does. And {\i nidvos reshaim} is not meaningful \u8212- the {\i nedava} of a {\i rasha} is not a coherent category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran then maps this distinction onto the technical language of {\i korban} vows. A classic {\i neder} of a {\i korban} is {\i harei alai} \u8212- \u8220"it is upon me,\u8221" creating a personal obligation. A classic {\i nedava} of a {\i korban} is {\i harei zo} \u8212- \u8220"behold this [animal],\u8221" sanctifying the object itself rather than creating an obligation on the person. The Ran suggests that this technical legal distinction reflects the underlying motivational one: {\i harei alai} shows a certain hesitation \u8212- you are not yet giving it, you are promising to give it in the future. {\i Harei zo} is more wholehearted, because you are giving it over immediately. The act of {\i neder} thus maps onto the more complex, holding-back motivation, while {\i nedava} maps onto the fully generous one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a debate between the Ran and Tosafot about what {\i bekorban} means within the mishnah\u8217's context. The Ran understands it as a literal {\i korban} \u8212- a sacrificial offering, with the {\i neder/nedava} distinction playing out as just described. Tosafot reads {\i bekorban} as {\i konam} \u8212- the classic formulation of {\i nidrei issur} \u8212- meaning it is a standard prohibition vow, where the object is treated as though it were a {\i korban} and therefore forbidden. Tosafot\u8217's reading fits better with the three classic types of {\i hafla\u8217'ah} under discussion in this tractate: {\i neder}, {\i nazir}, and {\i shvuah} \u8212- with the {\i neder} category being the {\i konam} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirut Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran then extends this framework to {\i nezirut}. Technically, there is only one way to become a {\i nazir} \u8212- you say {\i hareini nazir} \u8212- so there is no formal legal parallel to {\i harei alai} versus {\i harei zo}. Nevertheless, the Ran explains that someone whose {\i nezirut} flows from an uncomplicated, purely religious motivation \u8212- a true gift of the self to God \u8212- has made a {\i nezirut} of the {\i nedava} type. Someone whose {\i nezirut} flows from more complex, troubled, or impetuous motivations has made a {\i nezirut} of the {\i neder} type. This is not a legal distinction; it is a characterization of the act\u8217's religious character.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Knidrei Reshaim (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvuah Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does the mishnah omit {\i shvuah} from its closing list? The basic answer is that {\i kesherim} never take {\i shvuos} at all. The Ran states this directly. The underlying concern is the danger of {\i shem shamayim l\u8217'vatalah} \u8212- the non-weighty invocation of God\u8217's name. And we have already seen earlier in the tractate that someone who hears {\i shem shamayim l\u8217'vatalah} must put the offender in {\i cherem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a rich tradition on this point. Rashi on the {\i pasuk} {\i es Hashem Elokecha tira v\u8217'oso sa\u8217'avod u\u8217'vo sidbak u\u8217'vishmo tishaveia} says that only someone who possesses all of those qualities \u8212- fear of God, service, cleaving \u8212- is at the appropriate {\i madreiga} to take a {\i shvuah b\u8217'shem Hashem}. The Ramban there appears to disagree and reads {\i vishmo tishaveia} as meaning that if one is going to take a {\i shvuah} it must be in God\u8217's name, as opposed to the name of another deity \u8212- not that there is ever a positive circumstance for taking it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Either way, the practical conclusion is clear. Since taking a {\i shvuah} in God\u8217's name is intrinsically fraught \u8212- {\i lo yenakeh Hashem es asher yisa es shemo lashav} \u8212- a {\i kasher} person would simply not do it. Therefore, one cannot invoke the concept of {\i knidvos kesherim} to validate a {\i shvuah}, because there is no such thing as a {\i shvuah} of the {\i kesherim}. Hence, {\i shvuah} is absent from the closing clause of the mishnah.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Three Ways\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i chiddush} of the opening qualifier established, the Gemara turns to the question of the incomplete end of the sentence. {\i V\u8217'dilma hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- perhaps what the speaker meant was \u8220"like the vows of {\i reshaim}, I will not vow\u8221" \u8212- in other words, they were disavowing {\i nedarim}, not making one. How do we know the speaker intended to become a {\i nazir}, or to obligate a {\i korban}, or to make a {\i shvuah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel answers: {\i b\u8217'omer k\u8217'nidrei reshaim hareini alai v\u8217'heimenu} \u8212- we are dealing with three distinct formulations. {\i Hareini}, behold I am, points toward {\i nezirut}. {\i Alai}, upon me, points toward a {\i korban} obligation. {\i Heimenu}, from it, points toward a {\i shvuah} not to eat. Each of these formulations provides a partial indication of what was meant, which is why the question of {\i yad} arises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yad Sheeinah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately presses: for {\i hareini} specifically, how does it point toward {\i nezirut}? {\i Dilma hareini betaanit ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- perhaps the speaker meant to be in a fast. Or any number of other things. At best this is a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mochiach}, an unclear indication, and Shmuel is on record holding that a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mochiach} is not effective. One might even say it is not a {\i yad} at all, since {\i hareini} is so vague that there is no way to determine what was intended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Okimta: Nazir Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's response is an {\i okimta} \u8212- a recontextualization: {\i keshehayah nazir over lefanav} \u8212- when there was a {\i nazir} walking in front of the speaker. In that context, when the speaker said {\i hareini}, it was obvious that he meant {\i hareini nazir}. The surrounding circumstance provided the necessary clarity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i okimta} reads a great deal into the mishnah \u8212- both the triggering word {\i hareini} and the contextual fact of the passing {\i nazir} \u8212- but the point is that these are not really where the mishnah\u8217's weight lies. The mishnah is not primarily interested in how we fill in the end of the sentence. The end of the sentence is assumed to be clear enough by whatever means \u8212- a {\i nazir} walking by, an explicit continuation, or some other contextual indicator. The {\i chiddush} of the mishnah is entirely concentrated in the opening qualifier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely where the Ran and Tosafot diverge. The Ran holds that the {\i chiddush} of the mishnah operates even if the end of the sentence is completely explicit. If someone says {\i knidrei reshaim kikar zeh alai konam} \u8212- \u8220"like the vows of {\i reshaim}, this loaf is forbidden to me as a {\i konam}\u8221" \u8212- the opening qualifier {\i knidrei reshaim} makes the vow effective, because that is a meaningful thing for a {\i rasha} to say. And if someone says {\i knidrei kesherim kikar zeh alai konam}, the opening qualifier {\i knidrei kesherim} prevents it from taking effect, because {\i kesherim} do not make {\i nedarim}, and therefore that qualifier signals that the vow is not seriously intended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot disagrees. According to Tosafot, the qualifier at the beginning matters only because the end of the sentence is somewhat ambiguous \u8212- there is an element of {\i yad} in play. Given that ambiguity, if the opening qualifier is one that would not normally \u8220"work\u8221" \u8212- like {\i knidrei kesherim} \u8212- we do not resolve the ambiguity in favor of effectiveness. But if the end of the sentence were completely explicit, the opening qualifier would be irrelevant: {\i knidrei kesherim kikar zeh alai konam} would in that case be binding, because the commitment is unambiguous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's reading, in a sense, is simpler. There was a {\i nazir} walking by; the speaker said {\i hareini}; we know what was meant. The entire force of the mishnah lies in the opening clause, regardless. One might ask why anyone would ever say {\i knidrei reshaim} when trying to create an effective vow \u8212- and this is genuinely curious. It may be that the mishnah is using this formulation to express a certain displeasure with the whole phenomenon of {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut}, communicating that disapproval through the very {\i halacha} it is teaching. The phrase {\i knidrei kesherim}, for its part, has come down in popular usage precisely as that sort of hedge \u8212- an acknowledgment, very much like the contemporary {\i bli neder}, that the speaker is not fully committing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raba: Hemenu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to the third formulation \u8212- {\i heimenu}, \u8220"from it\u8221" \u8212- the Gemara asks: {\i dilma heimenu d\u8217'achila ka\u8217'amar}, perhaps the speaker meant \u8220"I will eat from it,\u8221" not \u8220"I will not eat from it.\u8221" There is simply no {\i shvuah} implied at all. Raba clarifies: the case is where the speaker said {\i heimenu shelo ochal} \u8212- \u8220"from it I will not eat.\u8221" That makes the content explicit. The Gemara then asks: if so, what is the {\i chiddush}? The speaker said everything! The answer is: {\i kamah shmalan} \u8212- the speaker did not say the word {\i shvuah}. The {\i chiddush} is that even without explicitly pronouncing the word {\i shvuah}, saying \u8220"I will not eat from it\u8221" functions as a {\i yad lishvuah} and constitutes a binding {\i shvuah}. The significance of the end of the sentence, in other words, is also partly that it is incomplete \u8212- the keyword is absent \u8212- and nevertheless it is effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the Gemara recognizes two layers of {\i chiddush} in the mishnah: the opening qualifier determines whether the statement is the kind of thing that would or would not take effect, and the remainder of the statement demonstrates that a {\i yad} without the explicit keyword is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder Values Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now pivots to a broader question: who is the {\i tanna} of this mishnah that distinguishes between {\i neder} and {\i nedava}? The Gemara suggests it fits neither Rabbi Meir nor Rabbi Yehudah \u8212- and uses this as an entry point to discuss a fundamental {\i machlokes} about the religious value of making {\i nedarim}. {\i D\u8217'tanya}: the verse says {\i tov asher lo tidor mishetiddor v\u8217'lo tishalem} \u8212- better not to vow than to vow and not fulfill. {\i Tov mizeh umizeh she\u8217'eino noder kol ikar divrei Rabbi Meir}. Rabbi Meir says: best of all is simply not to vow at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Risk & Non-Fulfillment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's position can be understood on two levels. The more pragmatic reading is that making a {\i neder} is inherently risky: you have now created a personal obligation, and if you fail to fulfill it \u8212- {\i ki tidor neder l\u8217'Hashem Elokecha lo te\u8217'acher l\u8217'shalmo} \u8212- you have transgressed. People may feel that the very act of raising their hand at a communal appeal has intrinsic religious value, but if they do not follow through, it would have been far better not to have made the commitment at all. The risk of non-fulfillment is simply too great.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But there is a deeper theological reading as well. One\u8217's {\i avodat Hashem} should consist of what God demands of a person. The obligations are divinely imposed \u8212- heteronomous, in the philosophical sense. When a person takes it upon themselves to create new religious obligations, they are in a sense serving their own religious impulses rather than the divine will. That is not true {\i avodat Hashem}. This may be the underlying concern: {\i kol hanoder k\u8217'ilu banah bamah v\u8217'chol hamkaymo k\u8217'ilu hikriv aleha korban} \u8212- every one who vows is as though he built a {\i bamah}, and one who fulfills it is as though he brought a {\i korban} upon it. A {\i bamah} may be a religious act, but unauthorized, individual religious construction is not self-evidently a positive phenomenon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah takes a different position: {\i tov mizeh umizeh noder umshalem} \u8212- best of all is to vow and fulfill it. Rabbi Yehudah appears to regard the making of a {\i neder} as a genuine religious act, provided one sees it through. The vow, on this view, is not inherently problematic; it is the failure to fulfill it that is the danger.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah & Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to our mishnah: the mishnah seems to distinguish between {\i neder} (associated with {\i reshaim}) and {\i nedava} (associated with {\i kesherim}). This fits uncomfortably with Rabbi Yehudah, who seems to regard {\i nedarim} positively. But it also seems uncomfortable with Rabbi Meir \u8212- because Rabbi Meir, who says it is better not to vow at all, apparently allows for the positive phenomenon of a {\i nedava}, whereas the mishnah seems to treat {\i nedavos} as the clearly superior category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Consistency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: {\i afilu teima Rabbi Meir} \u8212- it can still be Rabbi Meir. {\i Ki ka\u8217'amar Rabbi Meir b\u8217'neder, b\u8217'nedava lo ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- when Rabbi Meir objects to vowing, he objects to the {\i neder}, not to the {\i nedava}. A {\i nedava} he does not include in his critique. This distinction has conceptual grounding beyond the risk of non-fulfillment: a {\i neder} creates a personal religious obligation that the person has imposed upon himself \u8212- something other than divinely mandated duty. A {\i nedava} merely sanctifies an object; it does not generate a personal obligation in the same way. One can thus argue that the concern about self-generated religious heteronomy applies to {\i neder} but not to {\i nedava}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara presses: a {\i nedava} also carries risks. {\i Dilma ati lidei takala} \u8212- what if the {\i nedava} also leads to stumbling? If you sanctify an animal and it wanders around your flock for months, you might come to misuse it, violating {\i meila}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hillel Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel\u8217's Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers with a historical precedent: {\i k\u8217'Hillel HaZaken} \u8212- like the practice of Hillel the Elder. {\i Amru alav al Hillel HaZaken shelo ma\u8217'al adam b\u8217'olato kol yamav} \u8212- they said of Hillel that no one ever committed {\i meila} with his {\i olah} throughout his lifetime. How did he accomplish this? {\i Meivi keshehei chulin la\u8217'azara, umakdisha, v\u8217'someich aleha, v\u8217'shochta} \u8212- he would bring his animal unsanctified to the outer precincts of the {\i azara}, sanctify it there and then, perform {\i semichah}, and immediately bring it for {\i shechita}. There was no window of time between sanctification and slaughter during which anything could go wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nedava Safety\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why not do the same with a {\i neder}? Why not walk to the {\i beis hamikdash}, make a {\i neder} right at the entrance, and immediately bring the {\i korban}? The answer is that a {\i neder} creates a personal obligation \u8212- that step is simply unnecessary. Since you are going to be there and sanctify the animal anyway, why first take a {\i neder} that creates an obligation on your person? You can go straight to the {\i nedava}, sanctify the animal on the spot, and bring it immediately. The {\i nedava} allows a person to give a {\i korban} in a manner that involves no personal obligation, no window for {\i meila}, and no possibility of non-fulfillment. That is the paradigm of a {\i nedavat kesherim} \u8212- a {\i nedava} of the righteous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a question raised by both Tosafot and the Ran: does this mean that the mishnah\u8217's phrase {\i knidvat kesherim} can only take effect if the person is standing at the entrance to the {\i beis hamikdash}? That seems forced. Tosafot resolves this cleanly: the phrase {\i knidvat kesherim} works because the concept of a {\i nedavat kesherim} \u8212- a {\i nedava} in a fully unproblematic mode \u8212- genuinely exists. There is a real phenomenon that fits that description, even if it requires a particular set of circumstances. Because such a phenomenon exists, the phrase is meaningful and the vow takes effect. By contrast, there is no such thing as a {\i nidrei kesherim} \u8212- righteous people do not make {\i nedarim} \u8212- and therefore that phrase is not meaningful and the vow does not take effect. The mishnah is not requiring that one literally be at the gates of the {\i beis hamikdash}; it is simply invoking a concept that has a genuine referent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reality of Nedava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Nedava Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i nedavat kesherim} is a real and achievable phenomenon when it comes to {\i korbanos}, the Gemara turns to {\i nezirut}: {\i nedava d\u8217'nezirus mai ika l\u8217'meimar?} Is there truly such a thing as an uncomplicated, purely motivated {\i nedavah}-type {\i nezirut}? The question is pressing precisely because the Gemara elsewhere associates {\i nezirut} with the expression {\i asher chata al hanefesh} \u8212- the {\i nazir} is said to have \u8220"sinned against the soul\u8221" \u8212- implying that something inherently problematic attaches to the {\i nazir}\u8217's situation. Can {\i nezirut} ever truly be a pure religious gift?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i savar lah k\u8217'Shimon HaTzadik} \u8212- this view follows that of Shimon HaTzadik.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon HaTzadik\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} is introduced: {\i amar Shimon HaTzadik, miyamai lo achalti asham Nazir tamei} \u8212- Shimon HaTzadik said, \u8220"In all my days I never ate from the {\i asham} of an impure {\i nazir}.\u8221" The {\i asham} of a {\i nazir tamei} is an offering brought when a {\i nazir} has become {\i tamei} and must begin the {\i nezirut} again. Shimon HaTzadik, who was the {\i kohen gadol} and entitled to eat from such offerings, consistently refused to do so in the case of a {\i nazir tamei}. Only one time did he consent to eat, and that occasion he describes in detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beautiful Nazir Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba adam echad Nazir min hadarom} \u8212- once a man came from the south, a {\i nazir}, and Shimon HaTzadik observed that he had beautiful eyes, was strikingly handsome, and had his hair arranged in flowing locks. Shimon asked him: what led you to accept upon yourself {\i nezirut} and thereby commit to shaving off this beautiful hair? The man replied: I was a shepherd for my father in my village. I went to draw water from the spring, and I saw my reflection. Suddenly my {\i yeitzer hara} seized me \u8212- {\i v\u8217'pachaz alai yitzri} \u8212- and it sought to take me away from this world, to lead me toward a life dominated by physical indulgence and vanity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In response to his {\i yeitzer hara}, he said to himself: {\i lamah atah mitga\u8217'eh b\u8217'olam she\u8217'eino shelcha} \u8212- why do you glorify yourself in a world that is not yours? {\i B\u8217'mi she\u8217'asid lihayos rimah v\u8217'tole\u8217'ah} \u8212- in this body that will ultimately become food for worms. {\i She\u8217'agalechacha l\u8217'shamayim} \u8212- I will shave you for the sake of Heaven. Immediately he arose, kissed the young man on the head, and said: {\i bni, kamochah yirbu nozrei nezirus b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- my son, may those who take {\i nezirut} in Israel multiply like you. Regarding such a person the verse speaks: {\i ish ki yaifli lindor neder Nazir l\u8217'hazir l\u8217'Hashem} \u8212- the emphasis falls on {\i l\u8217'Hashem}, for God\u8217's sake.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Motivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Nazir Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now interrogates Shimon HaTzadik\u8217's practice more carefully. {\i Matkif leh Rav Mani: mai shna asham nazir tamei de-lo achal?} \u8212- Rav Mani presses: what is the particular reason Shimon HaTzadik refused to eat the {\i asham} of a {\i nazir tamei}? The stated rationale would seem to be that the offering comes as a result of sin \u8212- the {\i nazir} became {\i tamei}. But then {\i kol asham nami lo leachul} \u8212- he should refuse to eat any {\i asham} offering at all, since every {\i asham} comes to atone for some sin!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yonah Regret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yonah responds: {\i hainu ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- this is the real reason. {\i Kishe\u8217'hen tohin, nozrin} \u8212- when people are in a state of being troubled or agitated about their own moral condition, filled with guilt and a desire to correct themselves, they impulsively accept {\i nezirut}. {\i Ukshe\u8217'hen mitamein, v\u8217'rabbim aleihem yemei nezirot, mitchartin bahen} \u8212- but when they then become {\i tamei} and the period of {\i nezirut} extends far longer than they had anticipated, they regret it. They wish they had never begun. {\i Venimtze\u8217'u mevi\u8217'in chullin la\u8217'azara} \u8212- and it turns out, in a sense, as though they are bringing {\i chullin} \u8212- non-sacred items \u8212- into the {\i azara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motivation Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The insight here is significant. The {\i nazir tamei} reveals a person whose original motivation was impetuous rather than deeply rooted. The guilt or shame that drove them to accept {\i nezirut} was real, but it was not the product of a profound, settled conviction. When the {\i nezirut} becomes difficult \u8212- when it extends due to {\i tumah} \u8212- the depth of that commitment is exposed. They never had the endurance for it, because the conviction was never truly deep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shimon HaTzadik, seeing this dynamic, would not eat the {\i asham} of a {\i nazir tamei}, because such a person\u8217's {\i nezirut} was almost certainly of the {\i neder} type \u8212- complex, guilt-driven, impetuous \u8212- not the {\i nedava} type. The {\i nazir tahor}, whose period ended without interruption, remains at least formally in the category of someone who may have had genuine conviction. Perhaps the conviction was complicated, but there was no opportunity for regret to manifest. The {\i nazir tamei}, by contrast, had that opportunity, and the regret demonstrated what the conviction had really been.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pushes: {\i i hachi afilu nazir tahor nami} \u8212- if the logic is impetuous motivation leading to eventual regret, should we not worry about even a {\i nazir tahor}? The answer is: {\i lo, de-amudei amei nafshia de-achul lemidor} \u8212- no, because the {\i nazir tahor} had assessed that he could endure the standard thirty days, and he did. The absence of demonstrated regret allows us to take his {\i nezirut} at face value, even if we remain somewhat skeptical. The {\i nazir tamei}\u8217's regret, by contrast, has been publicly demonstrated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudah Nedava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehudah Reread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established all of this for Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, the Gemara now constructs a parallel reading for Rabbi Yehudah. {\i Afilu teima Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- even according to Rabbi Yehudah, the mishnah can be explained consistently. {\i Ki amar Rabbi Yehuda} when he says it is best to vow and fulfill \u8212- {\i b\u8217'nedavah}, he really meant {\i nedavah}. His objection was only to {\i neder}, not to {\i nedavah}. The Gemara then reads back Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's statement in the {\i braisa} \u8212- {\i tov mizeh umizeh noder umekayeim} \u8212- and says we should actually read it as {\i tani nodev umekayeim} \u8212- he was speaking of {\i nedava}, not {\i neder}. This is explicitly not the plain sense of Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's words, but the Gemara is working to show that the mishnah can fit Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's view on this reinterpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then replicates the earlier analysis: {\i mai shna noder d\u8217'lo \u8212- dilma ati lidei takala}, same concern about stumbling. {\i Nedava nami dilma ati lidei takala} \u8212- so why is {\i nedava} any better? And here, unlike the case of Rabbi Meir where we needed to bring proofs from Hillel HaZaken, we have Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's own statements. {\i Rabbi Yehuda l\u8217'taameih \u8212- adam meivi kivshato l\u8217'azara, umakdisha, v\u8217'someich aleha v\u8217'shochta} \u8212- Rabbi Yehudah himself describes the practice of bringing one\u8217's animal unsanctified to the {\i azara}, sanctifying it there, performing {\i semichah}, and bringing it for {\i shechita} immediately. This is exactly what we said of Hillel, now attributed directly to Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's own teaching. And so the same analysis follows: {\i neder} is problematic because it creates a personal obligation at risk of non-fulfillment; {\i nedava} performed in this manner is entirely safe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the same question reemerges: {\i teinach nedava d\u8217'korbanos} \u8212- fine for {\i korbanos}, what about {\i nezirut}? Here Rabbi Yehudah also speaks from his own tradition. {\i Rabbi Yehuda omer: chasidim harishonim hayu mis\u8217'avim l\u8217'havi korban chatas} \u8212- the great righteous ones of earlier generations longed to bring a {\i korban chatas}, but they faced a fundamental obstacle: {\i lefi she\u8217'ein HaKadosh Baruch Hu meivi takala al yadeihem} \u8212- God would never bring them to unintentional sin, because that is the only circumstance under which a {\i chatas} may be brought. They could not sin intentionally \u8212- that would disqualify the offering \u8212- and God would not allow them to sin inadvertently. So how could they bring a {\i chatas}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Omdim umisnadbim nezirus l\u8217'Makom} \u8212- they would accept {\i nezirut} upon themselves, not for its own sake, but as a vehicle: {\i k\u8217'dei sheyischayeiv korban chatas l\u8217'Makom} \u8212- so that they would incur the obligation of a {\i korban chatas} for God. The {\i chatas} of a {\i nazir} who becomes {\i tamei} is the mechanism. They accepted {\i nezirut} knowing that they might become {\i tamei} and thereby be required to bring a {\i chatas}, which is what they ultimately sought to offer. This, according to Rabbi Yehudah, is the example of a genuinely righteous, purely motivated {\i nezirut} \u8212- a {\i nedavat nezirut} in the fullest sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimon Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees: {\i lo nadru b\u8217'nazir} \u8212- they would not have done this. They would not have accepted {\i nezirut} upon themselves even to obtain the ability to bring a {\i chatas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Instead, Rabbi Shimon says, those who wished to bring {\i korbanos} freely would do so through other means. One who wanted to bring an {\i olah} would make a {\i nedava} of an {\i olah} and bring it. One who wished to bring {\i shlamim} would make a {\i nedava} of {\i shlamim}. One who wished to bring a {\i todah} with its four kinds of bread could do so as a {\i nedavah} as well. All of these could be freely offered as {\i nedavot} without {\i nezirut} being involved at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sinner Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for {\i nezirut}, {\i aval binezirus lo hisnadvu} \u8212- they would not accept {\i nezirut} even for this purpose. The reason: {\i k\u8217'dei shelo yikar\u8217'u chotim} \u8212- so that they not be called sinners. For even a {\i nazir} who has committed no wrong is described in the Torah\u8217's language as one who has {\i asher chata al hanefesh} \u8212- who sinned against the soul. The verse speaks in the context of a {\i nazir tamei}, and the Ran and others note that this refers specifically to that case, but Rabbi Shimon learns from it a broader principle about the nature of {\i nezirut} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Denial Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i asher chata al hanefesh} points to the ascetic dimension of {\i nezirut}. A {\i nazir} separates himself from wine and certain aspects of ordinary life. He marks himself visibly as different from the rest of society. Rabbi Shimon sees this self-denial as intrinsically a form of sin \u8212- against the soul, against the self. {\i Nezirut} does not merely risk impure motivation from the outside; it contains within itself an element that is religiously problematic, regardless of motivation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asceticism Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kadosh vs.\u160 ?Choteh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's objection opens onto a profound theological question: is the ascetic a {\i kadosh} or a {\i choteh}? Is the person who sits in a {\i taanis} \u8212- a fast \u8212- holy, or a sinner? This is not merely a question of motivation, though motivation is certainly at issue. It is a question about the nature of religious life itself. Is the model of the religious ideal one who withdraws from the physical world, denies bodily pleasures, and focuses entirely on the spiritual? Or is that very withdrawal itself a kind of failure \u8212- a rejection of the world that God created and declared good?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Embodied Religion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Judaism has, by and large, been understood as an embodied religion \u8212- one in which the physical world is not the enemy of the spirit but the medium through which God is served. We are not at war with the physical world. The pleasures of {\i Shabbos}, the joy of the {\i yom tov}, the {\i mitzvos} that engage the body and the senses \u8212- all of these reflect a fundamentally different orientation than that of a religion that sees the soul as imprisoned in the body, longing to escape. Yet the tension has always existed. Figures of {\i prishus} \u8212- separation from worldly pleasure \u8212- appear throughout the tradition, and the question of when separation is {\i kedushah} and when it is a betrayal of the world God made is genuinely unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam addresses this tension directly in his {\i Peirush Mishnayos}. He criticizes at length the approach of the {\i Chasidei Ashkenaz} \u8212- the medieval German pietists \u8212- who practiced extensive self-denial and asceticism. The Rambam argues that this entire orientation was absorbed from Christian religious culture and has no authentic Jewish foundation. {\i Lo dayecha mah she\u8217'asra lacha Torah} \u8212- the prohibitions of the Torah are sufficient; one need not add to them by denying oneself what the Torah permits. To do so is not {\i kedushah} but a kind of unhealthy religious distortion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet even acknowledging the Rambam\u8217's sharp critique, the tension remains. The debate between Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Shimon about whether the {\i chasidim harishonim} would have accepted {\i nezirut} in order to bring a {\i chatas} is one expression of that tension. Is {\i nezirut} a form of {\i nedavah} \u8212- a gift of the self \u8212- or is it always, by definition, a {\i chatas al hanefesh}, a sin against the soul? The question of whether a {\i nazir} is {\i kadosh} or {\i choteh}, and whether taking a {\i taanis} is holy or sinful, maps directly onto this larger question about the place of asceticism within Jewish religious life \u8212- a question that the Gemara introduces here and does not fully resolve.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 10\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vows & Nazir Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nidrei Reshaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is daf 10, and we find ourselves continuing from where we left off in the {\i gemara}. The {\i mishnah} that opened our discussion frames the fundamental tension of {\i masechet Nedarim} in stark terms: {\i nidrei reshaim} \u8212- the vows of the wicked \u8212- as opposed to {\i nidrei kesherim} and {\i nidvot kesherim}. The governing principle, put simply, is that {\i resha\u8217'im} make {\i nedarim} while {\i kesherim} make {\i nidavot}. It is worth pausing on the word {\i resha\u8217'im} here. The {\i Ran} explains the phrase as {\i resha\u8217'im badavar zeh} \u8212- wicked in this particular matter \u8212- and one should not read into it the full harshness that the term normally carries. And yet, however we soften it, it remains a striking characterization. Here we are in a tractate entirely devoted to {\i nedarim}, and the very opening move is to attach a disparaging label to those who take them. The implicit tension borders on the ironic: who, after all, would aspire to vow {\i as the resha\u8217'im do}? The {\i gemara}\u8217's concern operates on at least two levels. First, there is the practical worry that a person who makes a {\i neder} may come to violate it. But second, and perhaps more fundamentally, there is something inherently questionable about the religious phenomenon itself \u8212- the idea that one must manufacture one\u8217's own religious realities, as though what God has commanded is insufficient, imposing upon oneself a self-styled spiritual regime rather than fulfilling what has actually been asked of us. Both concerns remain very much alive as the {\i gemara} develops.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Nidavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From there, the discussion turned naturally to the {\i nazir}, and the question of what it would even mean for {\i nezirut} to qualify as a {\i nidavah}. In this context, the distinction is not a technical legal one as it is for {\i korbanos} \u8212- a {\i korban} can formally be either a {\i neder} or a {\i nidavah}. Here the question is one of motivation: under what circumstances, if any, does a person\u8217's acceptance of {\i nezirut} reflect a genuinely worthy impulse?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon HaTzadik\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question brought the {\i gemara} to the celebrated position of Shimon HaTzadik, who declared that throughout his entire tenure he had never eaten from the {\i korban} of a {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- except for one singular instance. That exception involved a young man who had adopted {\i nezirut} for what amounts to a reverse-narcissism reason: confronting his own dangerous vanity, he chose to use his striking appearance as an occasion for sincere {\i avodas Hashem} rather than self-admiration. Shimon HaTzadik recognized this as a case of genuine {\i l\u8217'shem shamayim} and therefore ate from his {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir/Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The positions of Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda were then brought into the picture. Rabbi Meir holds: {\i tov shelo yiddor mizeh u\u8217'mizeh she\u8217'eino noder kol ikkar} \u8212- better not to vow at all than to vow and not fulfill. Rabbi Yehuda initially appears to disagree, holding that {\i noder u\u8217'mekayem} is better than not vowing at all. But the {\i gemara} ultimately collapses the apparent dispute: Rabbi Yehuda is referring only to a {\i nidavah}, not a {\i neder}. Even Rabbi Yehuda agrees that a {\i neder} is problematic. The {\i gemara} further cites Rabbi Yehuda himself as offering a model of a legitimate {\i nazir}: one who becomes a {\i nazir} specifically in order to be able to bring a {\i korban chatas}. The analogy offered is to a person who, upon learning that it is a {\i mitzvah} to give a {\i get} to one\u8217's wife, deliberately goes out and divorces her in order to fulfill the obligation \u8212- using an ostensibly ordinary act as the vehicle for a genuine religious gesture. Similarly, someone who becomes a {\i nazir} in order to bring a {\i korban chatas} is treating the {\i nezirut} as a kind of {\i nidavah} vehicle for {\i korban}-giving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nedarim vs Nidavot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall framework that emerges is thus: {\i nedarim} are something the {\i gemara} looks upon with suspicion and criticism, while {\i nidavot} are praiseworthy. The question of how to apply this framework to {\i nezirut} \u8212- which sits in an ambiguous middle position \u8212- generates multiple positions that will now be examined more closely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Nadvu BeNazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Picking up approximately fifteen lines into the relevant section, the {\i gemara} presents the position of Rabbi Shimon: {\i lo nadvu b\u8217'nazir} \u8212- people who genuinely wanted to give {\i korbanos} and to do so entirely {\i l\u8217'shem shamayim} would nonetheless not have accepted upon themselves to become a {\i nazir}. Instead, if one wished to bring an {\i olah}, one simply brought an {\i olah}; if one wished to bring {\i shelamim}, one brought {\i shelamim}; if a {\i todah} with its four types of bread, one brought that. {\i Aval b\u8217'nezirut lo hitnadvu} \u8212- even the most sincere and well-motivated individuals did not embrace {\i nezirut} as their vehicle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vichiper Alav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason Rabbi Shimon gives is drawn from the {\i pasuk}: {\i vichiper alav asher chata al hanefesh} \u8212- the Torah says the {\i nazir} requires atonement for having sinned against the soul. And this is not being read here, in Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view, as referring specifically to the {\i nazir tamei}. Rather, Rabbi Shimon understands it as indicating that the very acceptance of {\i nezirut} constitutes a form of injury to oneself \u8212- specifically, {\i shetzier atzmo min hayayin}, that the person denied himself wine. A further {\i kal vachomer} is drawn: if one who denied himself merely wine is called a sinner, how much more so one who denies himself from everything. And from here the {\i gemara} extrapolates: {\i kol hayoshev b\u8217'taanis nikra chotei} \u8212- anyone who accepts upon himself a {\i taanis} is called a sinner. The counterpoint is also noted: an opposing opinion holds that {\i kol hayoshev b\u8217'taanis nikra kadosh} \u8212- such a person is called holy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye at this point performs one of his characteristic syntheses, collecting disparate Tannaitic opinions under a single organizing rubric. He declares: {\i Shimon HaTzadik v\u8217'Rabbi Shimon v\u8217'Rabbi Elazar Hakapar kulan shita achas hen} \u8212- all three hold essentially the same {\i shita}, or school of thought. It should be noted, as Abaye himself implies and the {\i rishonim} confirm, that this does not mean they said literally the same thing. The language here is {\i kulan shita achas hen} \u8212- they are of one orientation \u8212- not {\i kulan amru davar echad}. There is a recognized difference in formulation, perhaps even in the specific language associated with {\i nedarim}, but the underlying thrust is the same.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Chotei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The common principle uniting all three: {\i denazir chotei havei} \u8212- that the {\i nazir} is to be considered a sinner. Shimon HaTzadik and Rabbi Shimon, the {\i gemara} notes, we have already addressed. Rabbi Elazar Hakapar beRabbi is cited from a {\i braita}: {\i vichiper alav asher chata al hanefesh \u8212- vichi be\u8217'eizo nefesh chata?} Against what soul did this person sin? His answer: {\i ella shetzier atzmo min hayayin} \u8212- he denied himself wine. The text could simply have said {\i asher chata}; the addition of {\i al hanefesh} implies a personal injury, a harm done to oneself. From there the {\i kal vachomer} proceeds as above, concluding with the sweeping principle about one who accepts a {\i taanis}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chotei Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Tamei Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rishonim} are careful to note that the three opinions gathered by Abaye are not identical in their emphases. Shimon HaTzadik\u8217's concern was focused specifically on the {\i nazir tamei}. His worry was essentially about the sincerity and depth of the {\i nazir}\u8217's conviction: when a {\i nazir} becomes {\i tamei}, the vow extends, the count resets, and the person is forced to confront whether he really meant what he undertook. The {\i nazir tamei} is the test case for Shimon HaTzadik precisely because the impurity occasion reveals whether the commitment was genuine. His concern is thus primarily one of motivation and the quality of conviction behind the vow, not about {\i nezirut} as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a difficulty: the {\i pasuk} of {\i nikra chotei} is written in the context of a {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- so how can Rabbi Shimon apply it to {\i nezirut} in general, as though the problem is the very acceptance of {\i nezirut} regardless of {\i tumah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Denial Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's emphasis, in contrast to Shimon HaTzadik, has nothing to do with {\i tumah}. His concern is with something intrinsically problematic about the phenomenon of self-denial \u8212- {\i shetzier atzmo min hayayin} \u8212- irrespective of what happens afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repeated Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the textual difficulty by noting: {\i mishum deshana b\u8217'cheit} \u8212- because this person has repeated the sin. Either the sin of self-denial is compounded when {\i tumah} occurs \u8212- in that the period is extended and the self-denial lengthened \u8212- or because the {\i nazir} has now piled upon the original sin of denying himself wine the additional sin of becoming {\i tamei}. One might object that becoming {\i tamei} is not always within the {\i nazir}\u8217's control \u8212- the Torah itself uses the language of {\i b\u8217'feta pitom}, sudden, unexpected {\i tumah}. This is acknowledged; nonetheless, the situation of the {\i nazir tamei} is understood to be a particularly compounded and therefore particularly problematic case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asceticism Philosophy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motivation Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before moving forward, it is worth stepping back and asking what precisely the source of the problem is \u8212- why is the motivation questioned at all in the first place? Shimon HaTzadik seemed to emphasize that the motivation behind {\i nezirut} is often impure. Consider: becoming a {\i nazir} is a highly visible, socially marked act. Long hair, abstention from wine, refusal to come in contact with the dead \u8212- these behaviors signal to everyone around you that you are trying to be holier, that you are among the spiritually elevated. This creates fertile ground for {\i yihra} \u8212- for a kind of spiritual vanity \u8212- even when the person is not consciously aware that this is driving them. There are serious questions about how pure somebody\u8217's motivation can really be when they adopt behaviors that so visibly separate them from ordinary society and signal membership among the {\i frum} elite. That, it seems, was Shimon HaTzadik\u8217's core concern: that when the {\i nazir} becomes {\i tamei} and faces the possibility of extending and restarting his {\i nezirut}, it emerges that the commitment was never deep enough to sustain itself. The conviction was not coming from a genuine place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asceticism Trends\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The latter opinions \u8212- Rabbi Shimon and Rabbi Elazar Hakapar \u8212- seem to be pointing at something different: not a defect in the individual\u8217's motivation, but something intrinsically problematic about the phenomenon of self-denial as a religious ideal. And in any religion, one will always find ascetic tendencies, because the idea that religion is something spiritual, something beyond the physical, can easily shade into a form of mind-body dualism, in which the spiritual life is conceived as being at war with the body, and denying the body becomes the religious ideal. This is especially prominent in Christianity, though it is somewhat ironic there, given that the foundational Christian theological claim is of an embodied God. Nevertheless, the ascetic strand runs deep in that tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewish Embodiment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Judaism, by contrast, is a far more embodied religion. On {\i Shabbat} one is obligated in {\i oneg} \u8212- pleasure. There were sects in the Second Temple period that forbade marital relations on {\i Shabbat} out of an impulse toward the spiritual, but normative {\i halacha} holds the opposite: conjugal obligation is very much a feature of {\i Shabbat}. Judaism, in short, does not endorse the notion that freeing oneself from bodily desires is the path to holiness. And yet it is not surprising that the ascetic impulse would find its way into Jewish life as well \u8212- which is precisely why one finds both the opinion that {\i kol hayoshev b\u8217'taanis nikra chotei} and the competing opinion that {\i kol hayoshev b\u8217'taanis nikra kadosh}, and one finds both strands genuinely operative in the tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam addresses this directly. He argues that the trend toward asceticism found among {\i Chasidei Ashkenaz} in the medieval period \u8212- the extreme fasting, self-mortification, and bodily denial that characterized that movement \u8212- was a product of Christian influence, and he regarded it as a fundamentally anti-Jewish idea. And he is right that it represents a departure from the mainstream Jewish orientation, even if one can find textual support for some strain of it. The debate between {\i nikra chotei} and {\i nikra kadosh} shows that both positions are genuinely internal to the tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is one further point worth making before leaving this topic, and it is one that tends to be overlooked. We naturally think of {\i nezirut} in terms of asceticism and self-denial, but consider what the Torah actually prohibits: wine. That is the primary prohibition. Now, the prohibition of wine is not primarily about denying oneself a sensual pleasure. A {\i nazir} can eat a lavish meal every night, can enjoy all the pleasures of the table, can engage in marital relations \u8212- none of that is restricted. The prohibition on wine is about something specific to wine: it clouds the mind, it leads to inebriation, it diminishes one\u8217's mental clarity and judgment. The concern is not sensual indulgence per se but the loss of cognitive and moral clarity that comes with alcohol. So the {\i nazir}\u8217's abstention from wine may be less about the denial of pleasure and more about the aspiration to maintain a certain sharpness of mind.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine & Society\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Symbol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to a broader theme. Throughout {\i Tanach}, wine is indeed associated with {\i ta\u8217'anugei olam hazeh}, with the pleasures and temptations of this world \u8212- {\i yayin yesamach levav enosh}. But one should be careful here. The danger of wine in the tradition is not simply that it tastes good. It is that it pulls a person \u8212- {\i nimshachim acharei hayayin} \u8212- it draws one in through the mechanism of inebriation and addiction, leading one to want to return again and again. The power of wine is its particular capacity to compromise judgment and to perpetuate its own consumption. It is about becoming an alcoholic, about the progressive clouding of one\u8217's faculties, not merely about the enjoyment of a good beverage. So the label of asceticism does not quite capture what is happening. The {\i nazir}\u8217's wine prohibition is specifically about clearing the head, keeping one\u8217's thinking unencumbered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Societal Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Still, the {\i nazir}\u8217's regime as a whole does serve the function of keeping him separate from ordinary society. Not becoming {\i tamei} to the dead keeps him away from burial society and from the social obligations of mourning. The long hair marks him visibly. Together, these practices isolate the {\i nazir} and enforce a kind of {\i perisha}, a separation. It is possible to understand the wine prohibition in this light as well: not just clarity of mind in the abstract, but specifically keeping the {\i nazir} out of the social contexts in which wine is consumed \u8212- the dinner party, the symposium, the ordinary social gathering. In this sense, the prohibition on wine is as much about keeping the {\i nazir} separate from society as it is about any intrinsic harm of alcohol. Some traditions indeed use altered states \u8212- including intoxicants and other substances \u8212- as vehicles for spiritual elevation. {\i Chasidim} embrace a good {\i l\u8217'chayim}; various indigenous traditions use {\i peyote} or other plants in ritual contexts; certain Buddhist traditions have analogous practices. The point is that seeking a higher spiritual plane through such means is itself a recognizable phenomenon. The {\i nazir} is doing something different: he is seeking elevation precisely by refusing that path, maintaining sobriety in order to access a different kind of heightened awareness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raisins Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that the prohibitions extend even to raisins and grape products \u8212- which are not themselves intoxicating. These are extensions, safeguards on the core prohibition, which is itself centered on the wine and what wine does.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinuyim Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam Substitutes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to a new topic, or rather to the topic that the {\i gemara} has been building toward all along: the subject of {\i kinuyim}, the substitute terms used in making vows. The {\i mishnah} presents these systematically. Someone who says {\i konam}, {\i konach}, or {\i konas} \u8212- {\i harei elu kinuyim l\u8217'korban} \u8212- these are recognized substitutes for the word {\i korban}. The structure is evident: the {\i kaf} and {\i nun} of {\i korban} are preserved, and the ending varies. {\i Konam} preserves the {\i nun}; {\i konach} substitutes a {\i chet}; {\i konas} a {\i samach}. These are the standard {\i kinuyim} for {\i korban}, and they serve as the operative language of a {\i korban neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cherem Substitutes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i cherek}, {\i cherech}, and {\i cheref} \u8212- {\i harei elu kinuyim l\u8217'cherem}. These preserve the root of {\i cherem} while substituting the final letter. The structure is parallel: the essential phonetic body of the word is maintained, but the last element is altered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirut Substitutes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i nezirut}, the substitutes are {\i nazik}, {\i naziach}, and {\i paziach} \u8212- {\i harei elu kinuyim l\u8217'nezirut}. The first two preserve the {\i nazi} beginning; {\i paziach} is the more striking entry, having altered both the first letter and the end. The {\i gemara} will address this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Substitutes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i shvuah}, the substitutes given are {\i shvuta} and {\i shkuka}. These carry the phonetic echo of {\i shvuah} in modified form and function as its recognized {\i kinuyim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bemosi/Bemohi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The last entry in the {\i mishnah} on this topic is the most interesting: {\i noder bemohi} \u8212- or in all the textual traditions of the {\i mishnah}, {\i noder bemosa}. The word {\i mosa} is simply the Aramaic for {\i shvuah}; one finds it in {\i Targum Onkelos}, rendered as {\i mumsah} or a related form. The word {\i noder} here is also striking, since we are dealing with what is technically a {\i shvuah}, not a {\i neder} \u8212- but this reflects a looser popular usage in which the two terms bled into each other, where one would say {\i noder} even when meaning to take a {\i shvuah}. A {\i neder belashon shvuah} and a {\i shvuah belashon neder} were realities of the vernacular.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our textual version reads {\i bemohi}, and the {\i Yerushalmi} offers a remarkable reading: {\i mohi} is a {\i kinuy} for {\i Moshe} \u8212- that is, for {\i Moshe Rabbeinu} \u8212- and the phrase means that one is taking a {\i shvuah} in the manner of, or invoking, the {\i shvuah} that {\i Moshe} took. When did {\i Moshe} take such an oath? The {\i Yerushalmi} cites the {\i pasuk} {\i vayoel Moshe laveshes es ha\u8217'ish}, which is normally translated as \u8220"Moshe consented to dwell with the man,\u8221" but the {\i Yerushalmi} reads {\i vayoel} from the root {\i alah} \u8212- a curse or oath \u8212- understanding it to mean that {\i Moshe} swore. {\i Bemohi}, on this reading, means: in the {\i shvuah} that {\i Moshe} swore.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinuyim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now records a dispute about the nature of these {\i kinuyim}. Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i lashon umos hein} \u8212- they are some form of foreign language. This presumably refers to a Semitic language with phonetic proximity to Hebrew, given how close the {\i kinuyim} sound to the original terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish says: {\i lashon shebadu lahem chachamim veyihei noder bahem} \u8212- a language that the rabbis invented to serve as substitute terms, precisely to avoid using the actual official words. It is worth recalling that this dispute has halachic consequences: the {\i Ran} argues strongly that even on Reish Lakish\u8217's view \u8212- that these are invented terms \u8212- they are binding {\i deoraisa}. His reasoning is twofold. First, all language is ultimately convention, so the fact that the rabbis created these terms does not make them less real as language. Second, even if the {\i kinuy} is not technically the official word, it is at minimum a kind of {\i yad} \u8212- the speaker\u8217's intent is unmistakable \u8212- and {\i yad} is sufficient to create a binding {\i neder}. There is a minority view that {\i kinuyim} are only binding {\i derabbanan}, but the dominant position, and the {\i Ran}\u8217's position, is that they are {\i deoraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Badu Milibo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} supports Reish Lakish\u8217's formulation by citing a {\i pasuk}: {\i bachodesh asher bada milibo} \u8212- the month that he invented from his heart. This is a somewhat puzzling proof-text to deploy. Why cite a verse about someone who invented a calendar month \u8212- apparently a criticism \u8212- to support the legitimacy of rabbinic linguistic invention? The {\i gemara} seems to be noting that {\i badu} is the operative word: it is used to mean \u8220"invented\u8221" or \u8220"created from nothing.\u8221" But perhaps there is an interesting irony being invoked as well. The very phenomenon of {\i nedarim} is a kind of {\i badu milibo} \u8212- a person has invented a religious reality, transformed a permitted object into something forbidden through the sheer force of their declaration. The rabbis, in turn, create a language to go along with this invented phenomenon. And perhaps the reference to {\i asher badu milibo} carries a subtle signal: like the invented calendar month, this too is a created thing, a constructed reality. The {\i gemara} may be gently flagging the somewhat problematic character of the entire enterprise even as it regulates it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Kinuyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did the rabbis establish {\i kinuyim} in the first place? This question is clearly most pressing according to Reish Lakish, but even according to Rabbi Yochanan, insofar as the rabbis endorse using these terms rather than the standard ones, the question of their rationale applies. The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer: {\i delo leima korban} \u8212- so that people should not say the official word {\i korban}. But why not say {\i korban}? Because then the natural completion of that formulation would be {\i korban la\u8217'Hashem} \u8212- and once one says {\i korban}, it naturally triggers {\i la\u8217'Hashem} in the speaker\u8217's mind. And if one were to say {\i la\u8217'Hashem korban}, reversing the order, one would be invoking {\i shem shamayim} first, without having yet given it the proper context \u8212- which risks {\i levatala}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shem Shamayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevant {\i braita} presents this concern precisely. Rabbi Shimon asks: from where do we know that one should not say {\i la\u8217'Hashem olah}, {\i la\u8217'Hashem mincha}, {\i la\u8217'Hashem toda}, {\i la\u8217'Hashem shelamim} \u8212- leading with {\i la\u8217'Hashem}? The Torah says {\i korban la\u8217'Hashem}: first {\i korban}, then {\i la\u8217'Hashem}. The divine name receives its proper context only after the subject matter has been established. To lead with {\i la\u8217'Hashem} is to invoke it without that grounding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levatala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is cited further: {\i ha-mazkir shem shamayim levatala} \u8212- one who mentions God\u8217's name {\i levatala} \u8212- {\i al achat kama v\u8217'chama}, how much more severely is this to be condemned. If even for a genuine {\i korban} the Torah insists on the ordering {\i korban la\u8217'Hashem} so as not to invoke the name without its proper framing, then someone who says {\i la\u8217'Hashem} in a loose or incomplete way \u8212- potentially without ever completing the phrase \u8212- has violated the name in vain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} follows: if even for a genuine {\i korban} \u8212- where the intent is entirely appropriate, where one is doing something entirely sanctioned \u8212- the Torah still insists on care about how the divine name is introduced, then certainly one who mentions it {\i levatala}, in a context where the invocation adds nothing or is incomplete, has done something far worse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dedication Irony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a further irony worth noting here. The paradigm of {\i neder} \u8212- {\i vayidor Yaakov neder}, vowing to give {\i ma\u8217'aser}, to bring a {\i korban} \u8212- is genuinely about dedicating something to God, which is why the formula {\i la\u8217'Hashem} is relevant in that context. But when a person makes a standard {\i neder} today \u8212- saying this loaf of bread is forbidden to me \u8212- the {\i la\u8217'Hashem} framing is actually not relevant at all. The person is not dedicating anything to God; he is simply imposing a personal prohibition on himself. To invoke {\i la\u8217'Hashem} in that context is therefore doubly inappropriate: it misrepresents the nature of what one is doing, and it risks invoking the divine name without genuine contextual grounding. The {\i gemara} just two dapim earlier had treated the concern of {\i mazkir shem shamayim l\u8217'vatala} at length, and that anxiety is very much alive here. There is a pervasive hesitancy around the entire phenomenon: the vow-maker may be a {\i rasha}, may be doing something religiously questionable, may be deploying the wrong language, and may now be pulling God\u8217's name into a context where it does not properly belong. The answer is {\i kinuyim} \u8212- a vocabulary that preserves the communicative function of the vow without triggering the cascade toward {\i shem shamayim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinuyei Kinuyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks whether the dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish corresponds to a Tannaitic dispute. It finds a potential parallel: {\i Beis Shammai omrim kinuyei kinuyim asurim} \u8212- derivatives of {\i kinuyim} are binding \u8212- {\i u\u8217'Veis Hillel omrim kinuyei kinuyim mutarim} \u8212- derivatives of {\i kinuyim} are not binding. A {\i kinuy} of a {\i kinuy} means a further derivation from the already-substitute term: rather than {\i konam}, one might say something like {\i mekanamna} \u8212- a verbal, conjugated form derived from {\i konam} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis: {\i man de-amar kinuyei kinuyim asurin kasavar kinuyim lashon umos hein} \u8212- the view that holds these derivatives binding presupposes that {\i kinuyim} are a real foreign language. And if they are a real language, then corruptions and dialectal variants of that language are still real speech, still legitimate expressions of the underlying concept.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-le-man de-amar mutarim kasavar lashon shebadu lahem chachamim} \u8212- the lenient view presupposes that {\i kinuyim} are an invented language. And if they are invented by the rabbis, then only what the rabbis actually invented carries validity; further derivations and conjugations that the rabbis did not specifically create do not participate in the original rabbinic enactment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then entertains a second reading: {\i lo de-chulei alma kinuyim lashon umos hein} \u8212- perhaps everyone agrees they are a foreign language. On this reading, {\i Beis Shammai} holds that these further corruptions are still considered legitimate speech in that language \u8212- a recognizable dialect variant \u8212- while {\i Beis Hillel} holds that they are not {\i me-ikar ha-lashon}, not part of the genuine linguistic core; they are corruptions that no one actually speaks, not even as a dialect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third resolution is also offered: {\i Beis Shammai gazrinan kinuyei kinuyim mishum kinuyim} \u8212- even if these derivatives are not genuinely binding in their own right, Beis Shammai decrees that they are treated as binding as a safeguard for the {\i kinuyim} themselves. {\i Beis Hillel} declines to extend the rabbinic decree to this second level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks for concrete examples. Rav Yosef teaches: instead of {\i konam}, one says {\i mekanamna}; instead of {\i konach}, {\i mekanachna}; instead of {\i konas}, {\i mekanasna}. These are verbal, conjugated forms of the {\i kinuyim}, constructed as first-person verbal expressions \u8212- \u8220"I am making this a {\i konam}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cherem Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i cherem}: the {\i braita} gives {\i charkim} and {\i charchim} as the {\i kinuyei kinuyim}, which are effectively pluralizations of {\i cherekh} and {\i cherech}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirut Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef also teaches the {\i nezirut} examples: from {\i naziach}, the derivative is {\i menazachna}; from {\i paziach}, it is {\i maphichna}; and there is {\i mechazakna} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problematic Forms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes that certain further forms were brought as questions: {\i maphazna}, {\i methazna}, {\i metazna} \u8212- even more remote derivations. These apparently go beyond even {\i kinuyei kinuyim} in their distance from the original term, and Beis Shammai would presumably be uncertain how to categorize them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ambiguous Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinama Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina said to Rav Ashi: {\i kinama mai?} \u8212- what is the status of someone who says {\i kinama}? The word sounds like {\i konam}, but {\i kinmon bosem} \u8212- cinnamon, the spice \u8212- is also called {\i kinamon} or {\i kinama}. Is the person invoking the vow language, or merely naming a spice? The ambiguity is genuine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kina Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha berei d\u8217'Rav Chiyya raised a parallel question to Rav Ashi: {\i kina mai?} \u8212- is {\i kina} a reference to {\i konam}, or is it {\i kina shel tarnegolim}, a chicken coop? The word appears in {\i Eicha} in other senses. The speaker\u8217's intent is unclear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiku Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both questions are left as {\i teiku} \u8212- unresolved. The practical difficulty this creates is particularly acute for Beis Hillel\u8217's position. If {\i kinuyei kinuyim} are not binding according to Beis Hillel, and we cannot determine whether a given word is a {\i kinuy} or a {\i kinuy} of a {\i kinuy}, we are left without a clear ruling. The {\i teiku} leaves the matter in the domain of unresolved uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath Kinuyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to {\i kinuyei shvuah} \u8212- substitute terms for oaths. The forms given are {\i shvuel}, {\i shvutiel}, and {\i shkukiel}. These expand upon {\i shvuta} and {\i shkuka} from the {\i mishnah} by appending the divine name {\i El} \u8212- which is itself an interesting feature, suggesting that for {\i shvuah}, the invocation of a divine name is part of the linguistic structure even of the {\i kinuy}-level terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Confusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately questions {\i shvuel}: does it not simply sound like a proper name? {\i Shvuel ben Gershom} was indeed a person. The {\i gemara} therefore substitutes {\i shvuta-el} as the more clearly recognizable {\i kinuy} form for {\i shvuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verbal Forms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel then rules: {\i amar ashishta lo amar klum} \u8212- if someone tries to make a verbal form out of these \u8212- {\i ashishta}, {\i ashkika}, {\i karinsa} \u8212- these are nothing, they are not effective declarations. The rabbinic construction of {\i kinuyim} does not extend to verbal conjugations of {\i kinuyei shvuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} adds what are probably the most intuitively natural examples of all: {\i ha-omer yamin harei zeh shvuah, ha-omer smol harei zeh shvuah} \u8212- if one says \u8220"my right hand\u8221" or \u8220"my left hand,\u8221" that constitutes a {\i shvuah}. This is grounded in the biblical imagery of oath-taking by the upraised hand \u8212- {\i harimoti yadi el Hashem}, and {\i nishbati bi-ymini}. The right hand is entirely understandable as a form of oath-invocation. As for the left hand \u8212- perhaps for a left-handed person.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lieberman Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gentile Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Saul Lieberman, in his essay \u8220"Oaths and Vows\u8221" in {\i Greek and Jewish Palestine}, provides extensive historical context for these phenomena. He demonstrates that among the non-Jewish population of the Greco-Roman world, oath-taking had become a ubiquitous and thoroughly debased practice. People would pile oaths one upon another, swear by absurd objects \u8212- by the dog, by the lettuce, by the fishnet, by the life of the fig picker \u8212- sometimes because they wished to avoid invoking the names of their gods, and so used these as a form of {\i kinuy}, and sometimes simply out of a kind of verbal prolificacy in which the oath had lost all weight through sheer repetition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Philo Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Philo of Alexandria describes the phenomenon in sharp terms. Writing about the Alexandrian Jewish community, he observes: \u8220"So highly impious are they, that on any chance matter the most tremendous titles are on their lips, and they do not blush to use name after name, one piled upon another, thinking that the continual repetition of a string of oaths will secure them their object.\u8221" The oath has become a social lubricant, a verbal tic, entirely drained of the gravity that should attend it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Palestinian Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In Palestine the situation was similar. Lieberman documents how people swore on every occasion \u8212- in business, in everyday conversation, when accepting or declining invitations, when vouching for stories of questionable credibility. The rabbis consistently preached that it is better entirely to abstain from swearing, and held that one who swears unnecessarily is a sinner. The rabbis themselves swore, but they were understood to be in a position to assess whether the necessity genuinely warranted it. The ordinary person, by contrast, swore in houses, in the street, and in the market without discrimination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinical Regulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lieberman\u8217's central thesis is that the {\i kinuyim} in the {\i mishnah} are not purely rabbinic inventions \u8212- they are the product of a pre-existing popular practice that had already generated a welter of substitutes and near-substitutes for the formal vow words. People had been using these modified forms on their own, partly out of anxiety about invoking the sacred terms, partly simply out of the linguistic improvisation that characterizes popular speech. The rabbis\u8217' role was not to invent this vocabulary from scratch but to impose order on it \u8212- to determine which substitutes were close enough to the original to be genuinely binding, and which were so remote as to be meaningless. What looks like {\i lashon shebadu chachamim} may, in Lieberman\u8217's account, be better described as {\i lashon sheBireru lahem rishonim} \u8212- a language that earlier generations had selected and used, which the rabbis then ratified and regulated. The discussion of {\i kinuyei kinuyim} fits naturally into this picture: the masses had progressed from the reasonable substitute to the progressively more attenuated derivation, and the rabbis had to draw a line determining which level of derivation retained sufficient connection to the original to carry legal weight. The analogy to contemporary speech patterns is apt: just as certain expletives in English have spawned substitutes ({\i gosh}, {\i golly}, {\i gosh darn}) that everyone recognizes as proxies for the original \u8212- and which carry something of the original\u8217's social register \u8212- so too the {\i kinuyim} functioned in their cultural setting. The parallel also illuminates why the rabbis could not simply abolish the practice: it was too deeply embedded in the linguistic culture of the population. They could only regulate it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sacred Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lachulin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to another category of vow language. {\i Ha-omer l\u8217'chulin she\u8217'ochel lach} \u8212- in Palestinian Aramaic, the prefix {\i la-} functions as a negation: {\i l\u8217'chulin} therefore means \u8220"not {\i chulin}\u8221" \u8212- not profane, not ordinary. And if something is declared not-{\i chulin}, the implication is that it is {\i kodesh}, sacred, like a {\i korban}. So the declaration {\i l\u8217'chulin she\u8217'ochel lach} \u8212- \u8220"what I eat of yours shall be un-profane\u8221" \u8212- functions as a {\i neder}, treating the object as though it were {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kasher Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i lo kasher} or {\i la\u8217'kasher} \u8212- \u8220"not kosher\u8221" or \u8220"un-kosher\u8221" \u8212- can function as vow language. {\i Lo dachi} and {\i lo tahor} \u8212- \u8220"not pure\u8221" \u8212- are parallel formulations. The {\i Ran}, based on the {\i Rambam}, understands that the correct reading of the {\i mishnah} is {\i la\u8217'tahor} for that final form as well, mirroring the pattern of the others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tahor Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially understand {\i lo kasher} and {\i lo tahor} as referring to ordinary ritual unfitness \u8212- like {\i chazir}, which is not {\i kasher}. But {\i chazir} is not a {\i davar hanadur}, not the sort of object one uses as a reference point for vowing. The {\i gemara} understands these terms as referring to forbidden states of a {\i korban} specifically \u8212- {\i notar} (leftover {\i korban} eaten beyond its permitted time), {\i pigul} ({\i korban} rendered invalid by improper intent), and {\i tamei} (a {\i korban} that has contracted ritual impurity). All of these are categories of forbidden consumption that belong to the world of {\i korbanos}, and are therefore legitimate {\i divrei hanadur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechumra Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} articulates the governing principle here explicitly. The case of {\i lo kasher} or {\i lo tahor} is one of genuine ambiguity: the speaker\u8217's words could be interpreted as referring to {\i chazir} or other ordinary forbidden foods, in which case no {\i neder} would be created; or they could be interpreted as referring to the forbidden states of {\i korbanos}, in which case a {\i neder} would be created. In cases of ambiguity \u8212- what we might call a {\i kinuy she\u8217'eino mochiach}, a {\i kinuy} whose reference is unclear \u8212- we go {\i lechumra}, we interpret strictly. This is distinct from a {\i yad she\u8217'eino mochiach}, where the issue is that a sentence is elliptical and we don\u8217't know what word to supply. Here the word is present but genuinely ambiguous between two meanings. And the principle of {\i stam nedarim le\u8217'hachmir} \u8212- that ambiguous vow language is interpreted stringently \u8212- dictates that we treat it as a {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sacred Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then lists objects that, when invoked using the comparative {\i kaf} \u8212- like a sheep, like the pens for sheep, like wood, like fire-offerings, like the altar, like the {\i heichal}, like Jerusalem \u8212- constitute effective vow language. All of these belong to the world of {\i korbanos} and Temple service. One might have intended {\i imra} as someone\u8217's lamb, or the {\i etzim} as ordinary firewood; but these terms carry sufficient association with the Temple service that they are interpreted as {\i korban} references and treated accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kaf Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} raises the question: why does this latter group require the {\i kaf} \u8212- the word \u8220"like\u8221" \u8212- whereas {\i notar}, {\i pigul}, and {\i tamei} do not? His answer is illuminating. The words {\i notar}, {\i pigul}, and {\i tamei} are inherently defined by their forbidden status. The very identity of something as {\i notar} is inseparable from the fact that it is forbidden; the prohibitedness is constitutive of what {\i notar} means. Therefore, to say {\i harei zeh notar} is already to declare it forbidden \u8212- one does not need to say \u8220"let it be {\i like} notar\u8221" because the entire meaning of {\i notar} is its forbidden state. By contrast, Jerusalem or the altar or a sheep are not defined by being forbidden objects. A sheep is just a sheep. The altar is a holy place, but holiness is not the same as prohibition. Therefore, to make clear that one is invoking these objects as a {\i davar hanadur} \u8212- as the reference point for a vow \u8212- one must say {\i ke-imra}, {\i ke-mizbeach}, {\i ki-Yerushalayim}, making explicit through the {\i kaf} that one is declaring something to be forbidden {\i like} that object.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Altar Appurtenances\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mizbeach Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the daf states: if one takes a {\i neder} invoking any of the items that serve the altar \u8212- {\i meshamshei ha-mizbeach} \u8212- even if the word {\i korban} is not explicitly mentioned, {\i harei zeh nadar b\u8217'korban}, it is treated as a vow by a {\i korban}. The logic is that these items \u8212- the utensils, the vessels, all that directly serves the {\i mizbeach} \u8212- are so entirely bound up with the {\i korban} service that invoking them is effectively invoking the {\i korban} itself. They constitute a form of {\i kinuy} for {\i korban}, not because they are words that sound like {\i korban}, but because they are objects whose entire existence and identity is defined by their role in {\i korban}-service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda enters with a dissenting opinion regarding Jerusalem specifically: {\i ha-omer Yerushalayim lo amar klum} \u8212- if one simply says \u8220"Jerusalem\u8221" without the {\i kaf}, it is meaningless. The question \u8212- whether the issue is the absence of the {\i kaf} in particular, or something more specific to Jerusalem as an object that does not qualify even with the {\i kaf} \u8212- will require further examination.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 11\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinnuyim Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinnuyim Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The eleventh daf of Nedarim is brief in terms of raw text \u8212- as the lecturer observes, the entire daf amounts to only a handful of sentences \u8212- yet it demands close attention to the logic of vow-language. To orient ourselves, we return briefly to the {\i mishna} at the bottom of 10b, which introduces the category of {\i kinnuyim}. Unlike the concept of {\i yad}, which involves filling in a blank where part of a sentence has been left incomplete, {\i kinnuyim} operates differently: the sentence is formally complete, but we must determine what certain words mean \u8212- that is, what particular terms stand in for. The first category of {\i kinnuyim} explored earlier in the tractate involved phonetic variants of the word {\i konam} ({\i konas}, {\i konach}, and similar forms). Here we encounter a different type: words that are not variants of {\i konam} at all, but rather serve as stand-ins for the word {\i korban} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} introduces the formula {\i ha\u8217'omer lachullin she\u8217'ochal lach} \u8212- \u8220"one who says: that which I eat of yours is {\i lachullin}.\u8221" The word {\i lachullin} is a Palestinian Aramaic construction meaning \u8220"non-{\i chulin},\u8221" i.e., non-secular, and thus the statement is interpreted to mean that what he eats is {\i hekdesh}. The {\i mishna} continues: {\i lo kasher velo dachi} \u8212- \u8220"not {\i kasher}\u8221" or \u8220"not pure ({\i tahor})\u8221" \u8212- noting that some have the variant reading {\i lo tahor}. These formulations are surprising: {\i kasher} and {\i tahor} are not inherently associated with {\i hekdesh}, and {\i tamei} does not exclusively describe {\i korban} status. Nevertheless, the principle of {\i stam nedarim l\u8217'hachmir} \u8212- that ambiguous vow-language is interpreted stringently \u8212- leads us to read these terms as invoking {\i korban} status. More directly, {\i tamei}, {\i notar}, and {\i pigul} are explicitly listed as forbidden formulas, since {\i notar} and {\i pigul} occur only in the context of {\i korbanot}, and even {\i tamei}, though applicable elsewhere, is understood here as invoking the {\i issur} status of a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Altar Associations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then lists a further set of expressions: {\i ke\u8217'imra} (like a sheep), {\i kedirim} (like the pens), {\i ke\u8217'etzim} (like the wood), {\i ke\u8217'ishim} (like the fires on the altar), {\i ke\u8217'mizbeach} (like the altar), {\i ke\u8217'heichal} (like the sanctuary), {\i ke\u8217'yerushalayim} (like Jerusalem). None of these objects is itself a {\i davar hanadur} in the primary sense \u8212- one does not vow the pens or Jerusalem itself \u8212- but all are associated with the {\i korbanot} and the {\i mizbeiach}, and thus the {\i mishna} rules that {\i neder be\u8217'achad mikol hameshamshim mizbeach}, a vow invoking any object that serves the altar, even without explicit mention of {\i korban}, constitutes a binding vow as if {\i korban} had been stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalayim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes with a named position: {\i Rabbi Yehudah omer ha\u8217'omer Yerushalayim lo amar klum} \u8212- Rabbi Yehudah holds that one who simply says \u8220"Yerushalayim\u8221" has said nothing of legal consequence. The question raised immediately is whether Rabbi Yehudah is rejecting the formula {\i ke\u8217'Yerushalayim} as well, or whether his objection is specifically to the bare form \u8220"Yerushalayim\u8221" without the comparative prefix {\i ke}. The {\i Gemara} will clarify this distinction in the discussion that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miklal Lav Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miklal Lav Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens its analysis with the word {\i savruha} \u8212- \u8220"they assumed\u8221" \u8212- signaling a preliminary interpretation that may subsequently be revised. The working assumption is that {\i lachullin} operates via the principle of {\i miklal lav ata shomea hen}: from a negative statement one infers its positive counterpart. On this reading, saying \u8220"what I eat of yours is non-{\i chulin}\u8221" invokes, by logical inference, the positive status of {\i korban}, thereby rendering the food forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises a problem: if this is the mechanism, then whose position does the {\i mishna} reflect? If it is Rabbi Meir\u8217's, there is a difficulty \u8212- for Rabbi Meir explicitly rejects the principle of {\i miklal lav ata shomea hen}. The {\i Gemara} cites the relevant {\i mishna}: {\i Rabbi Meir omer kol tnai she\u8217'eino k\u8217'tnai B\u8217'nei Gad u\u8217'B\u8217'nei Reuven eino tnai} \u8212- any conditional stipulation not formulated like the condition of the tribes of Gad and Reuven is not a valid {\i tnai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnei Gad Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What was unique about the formulation in that episode? The Torah stated both sides of the condition explicitly: if they cross over with you, they will receive their inheritance on the eastern bank; and if they do not cross over, they will not receive it there. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is that you cannot rely on one side of a conditional to imply the other. One might ask: is the Torah not simply being explicit about something logically obvious? Rabbi Meir\u8217's answer is precisely the opposite \u8212- the Torah states both sides {\i because} one side does not logically entail the other. The explicit statement of both halves demonstrates that without such explicit formulation, we may not infer the reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two distinct ways to understand Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. The first is that this is fundamentally a requirement of {\i speech acts}: regardless of what is logically deducible, the rules governing {\i tnai} or {\i neder} require that both sides be explicitly articulated. Perhaps in ordinary matters of intent ({\i da\u8217'as}) an implicit understanding would suffice, but a {\i tnai} is not merely a question of subjective intent. A {\i tnai} is an artificial legal mechanism \u8212- distinct from a case where someone simply never would have entered an agreement under certain circumstances \u8212- and such a mechanism demands precise verbal formulation. Similarly with {\i nedarim}: it is not what is in one\u8217's mind that counts, but what one actually said. The second way to understand Rabbi Meir goes further: he may be making a claim about logic itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Propositional Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Anyone familiar with basic propositional logic will recognize that \u8220"if P then Q\u8221" does not entail \u8220"if not P then not Q.\u8221" If it rains I will give you ten dollars \u8212- but even if it doesn\u8217't rain, perhaps I will still give you ten dollars. The only valid inference from \u8220"if P then Q\u8221" is \u8220"if not Q then not P.\u8221" Thus, saying that if the tribes cross over they will receive their inheritance in {\i Ever HaYarden} does not logically imply that if they do not cross over they will forfeit it. There may be other grounds on which they would receive or not receive. On this reading, Rabbi Meir\u8217's objection is not merely procedural but substantive: the reverse proposition is simply not derivable from the original statement, and therefore one who says only \u8220"if P then Q\u8221" has not legally committed to \u8220"if not P then not Q.\u8221" Applied to {\i nedarim}: saying \u8220"the food I would eat of yours is {\i chulin}\u8221" does not logically entail that the food is {\i korban}, since \u8220"not {\i chulin}\u8221" does not necessarily mean {\i korban} by logical necessity alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yad vs.\u160 ?Kinnuy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that the {\i Gemara} has established that if {\i lachullin} works through {\i miklal lav}, the {\i mishna} cannot reflect Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, the {\i Gemara} initially suggests the {\i mishna} must follow Rabbi Yehudah \u8212- who does accept {\i miklal lav ata shomea hen}. But this creates a new problem: the {\i mishna} concludes with Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's own distinct ruling ({\i Rabbi Yehudah omer ha\u8217'omer Yerushalayim lo amar klum}), which implies that the rest of the {\i mishna} is not Rabbi Yehudah. Citing the end of the {\i mishna} as Rabbi Yehudah necessarily implies the beginning is not \u8212- a standard Talmudic inference ({\i mideseifa Rabbi Yehudah, reisha lav Rabbi Yehudah}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Rid Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely where the {\i Tosafot Rid} enters with a far-reaching observation. The initial assumption of the {\i Gemara} \u8212- the {\i savruha} \u8212- that {\i lachullin} works via {\i miklal lav} is, according to the {\i Tosafot Rid}, fundamentally mistaken, and this is the very assumption the {\i Gemara} ultimately rejects (though the exact point of rejection is debated). The {\i Tosafot Rid} argues that saying \u8220"this object is {\i lachullin}\u8221" is not a conditional inference at all. It is a direct description: the word {\i lachullin} is simply a synonym, or {\i kinnuy}, for {\i hekdesh}. If I say \u8220"you are impure,\u8221" I am not inferring anything \u8212- I am saying you are {\i tamei}. The word \u8220"impure\u8221" means {\i tamei}; it does not mean \u8220"not pure\u8221" in the sense of a logical negation from which the positive must be inferred. Similarly, {\i lachullin} \u8212- non-{\i chulin} \u8212- simply means {\i korban}, and this has nothing to do with Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yad Kinnuy Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis illuminates a deeper distinction: the difference between {\i yad} and {\i kinnuy}. A {\i yad} arises when a sentence is grammatically incomplete and something must be supplied to fill a blank; it is a kind of literary ellipsis. A {\i kinnuy} arises when the sentence is formally complete but a particular word requires interpretation \u8212- we must determine what that word stands for. When the {\i mishna} lists {\i tamei}, {\i notar}, {\i pigul}, {\i ke\u8217'imra}, {\i ke\u8217'mizbeach}, and {\i ke\u8217'Yerushalayim}, it is plainly operating in the register of {\i kinnuy}: each of these is a stand-in word for {\i korban}. Similarly, {\i lachullin} is a stand-in for {\i korban}. The person uttered a full sentence; the task is to decode what the word means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why it is, in truth, strange that the {\i Gemara} introduced {\i miklal lav ata shomea hen} at all in this context \u8212- it is a principle about conditional propositions, not about vocabulary. If I call something \u8220"like the pen of sheep\u8221" ({\i kedirim}), I am not making a conditional statement; I am naming the object with a certain term. We do not say this involves {\i miklal lav}. The same logic applies to {\i lachullin}. The {\i savruha} imported a logical framework that does not naturally fit the category of {\i kinnuyim}, and the {\i Tosafot Rid} argues this is precisely what must be abandoned as the {\i sugya} proceeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} offers a complementary analysis that explains another structural feature of the {\i mishna}: why some {\i kinnuyim} require the prefix {\i ke} (like) and others do not. His answer concerns what each expression is actually doing linguistically. Terms like {\i tamei}, {\i notar}, and {\i pigul} foreground the {\i issur} status of the object itself \u8212- they describe it as having the identity of a forbidden {\i korban} substance. These terms highlight the halachic character of the thing being vowed, making the sentence meaningful without any comparative prefix.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when one says \u8220"this is {\i mizbeach}\u8221" or \u8220"this is Yerushalayim,\u8221" those expressions do not themselves identify the object as a {\i davar assur}. If I say \u8220"this loaf of bread is the {\i mizbeiach},\u8221" the sentence is meaningless \u8212- bread is obviously not the altar. But if I say \u8220"this bread is {\i ke\u8217'mizbeiach}\u8221" \u8212- like the altar \u8212- I am drawing an associative comparison: I am relating this bread to the concept of things brought on the {\i mizbeiach}, and within that conceptual framework the bread acquires the status of a {\i davar hanadur}. The {\i ke} prefix creates the necessary semantic bridge. Without it, when the reference object is something like Yerushalayim or a sheep-pen, the statement is simply incoherent as a vow. This is exactly what Rabbi Yehudah is saying when he rules that {\i Yerushalayim} alone \u8212- without the {\i ke} \u8212- is ineffective: the bare word creates no meaningful associative link.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ki Yerushalayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this framework in place, the {\i Gemara} resolves the authorship question by asserting {\i kulah Rabbi Yehudah hi} \u8212- the entire {\i mishna} is Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's position. Rabbi Yehudah is not arguing with the body of the {\i mishna}; he is adding a specification to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalayim Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Specifically, Rabbi Yehudah draws a line between {\i ke\u8217'Yerushalayim} and bare {\i Yerushalayim}. The earlier portion of the {\i mishna}, which lists {\i ke\u8217'Yerushalayim} among the valid {\i kinnuyim}, is Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's own ruling \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'Yerushalayim} works. What Rabbi Yehudah then adds, in the clause attributed to him, is that {\i Yerushalayim} without the {\i ke} does not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalayim Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is not arbitrary. {\i Ke\u8217'Yerushalayim} works because it directs the listener toward Yerushalayim\u8217's identity as the site of {\i korbanot} \u8212- the comparative form channels the association toward the {\i Beis HaMikdash} and the sacrificial service. But the bare word {\i Yerushalayim} encompasses an enormous range of things: the Knesset, museums, the Biblical Zoo, countless institutions and objects that have no connection to {\i korbanot} whatsoever. Without the {\i ke} to focus the comparison, saying \u8220"Yerushalayim\u8221" does not reliably invoke {\i korban} identity, and the vow is therefore without legal effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ki Yerushalayim (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Strictness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then introduces a {\i braita} which presents an even stricter position: {\i Rabbi Yehudah omer ha\u8217'omer ki\u8217'Yerushalayim lo amar klum} \u8212- even {\i ke\u8217'Yerushalayim} is ineffective \u8212- {\i ad sheyidor bidvar hakorev biYerushalayim}, unless one explicitly vows by something actually offered in Yerushalayim. Even the comparative prefix does not suffice when the reference point is Yerushalayim itself, which remains too diffuse. The {\i Gemara} resolves the tension between this {\i braita} and the {\i mishna} by invoking {\i trei tanna\u8217'ei aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Yehudah} \u8212- there are two distinct traditions within Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's school, one of which holds that {\i ke\u8217'Yerushalayim} suffices and one that does not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Phrasing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permissible Formulas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to a {\i braita} that analyzes several formulations involving the word {\i chulin} itself. The {\i braita} rules that {\i chulin}, {\i hachulin}, and {\i kachulin} \u8212- whether one says \u8220"secular,\u8221" \u8220"the secular,\u8221" or \u8220"like the secular\u8221" \u8212- are all non-binding formulations. And this applies whether the person says {\i she\u8217'ochal lach} (that which I eat of yours is {\i chulin}) or {\i shelo ochal lach} (that which I do not eat of yours is {\i chulin}). These formulas are {\i mutar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, the {\i braita} rules that {\i lachullin she\u8217'ochal lach} \u8212- the food I eat of yours is {\i lachullin} \u8212- is {\i assur}. Here the prefix {\i la} transforms the word: {\i lachullin} means non-{\i chulin}, i.e., {\i korban}, and declaring one\u8217's food to be {\i lachullin} is therefore equivalent to declaring it {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Rid Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is here that the {\i Tosafot Rid} locates the reversal of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's opening assumption. The {\i braita} clearly demonstrates that {\i lachullin} is simply a word meaning {\i hekdesh} \u8212- a definitional synonym, not a logical inference. Even Rabbi Meir, who rejects {\i miklal lav ata shomea hen}, can accept that {\i lachullin} renders the food forbidden, because no conditional reasoning is involved. The word {\i lachullin} directly names the object as {\i korban}, just as the word \u8220"impure\u8221" directly names something as {\i tamei} without requiring any inferential step. The {\i Tosafot Rid} concludes that the {\i mishna} can therefore reflect Rabbi Meir\u8217's position after all, and the {\i savruha} at the top of 11a should be entirely abandoned. For completeness: other {\i rishonim} who do not accept the {\i Tosafot Rid}\u8217's approach resort to more strained readings \u8212- one excises the problematic phrase from the text entirely, and the {\i Ra\u8217'avad} eliminates the {\i vav} and reads the formula as {\i le-challin}, i.e., {\i challot todah} (loaves of the thanksgiving offering), an interpretation the lecturer candidly characterizes as extremely forced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional vs.\u160 ?Direct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's structure at this point becomes clearer when we attend to the precise mechanism at work in each formula. The cases of {\i chulin lo ochal lach} and {\i shelo ochal lach} that are ruled {\i mutar} both involve a kind of conditional or inferential structure: \u8220"your non-food is {\i chulin}\u8221" might seem to imply, via {\i miklal lav}, that your food is {\i korban} \u8212- but Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle blocks that inference, and therefore those formulas are {\i mutar}. By contrast, {\i lachullin she\u8217'ochal lach} is not a conditional statement at all. It directly describes the food as non-{\i chulin}, and non-{\i chulin} simply is {\i korban}. No inference is required or invoked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third formula in the {\i braita} \u8212- {\i lachullin lo ochal lach}, \u8220"that which I will not eat of yours is {\i lachullin}\u8221" \u8212- is ruled {\i mutar}. Why? Because even granting that {\i lachullin} means {\i korban}, the person has not declared any specific food to be forbidden. They have said that the things they do not eat are {\i lachullin} \u8212- but this does not render the food in front of them {\i assur}. The statement does not focus on the {\i davar hanadur} in any meaningful sense; at most it refers abstractly to non-food objects or to the act of non-eating, neither of which constitutes a binding {\i neder}. The {\i Gemara} eventually clarifies, via the analysis of {\i she-ani} versus {\i she-eini}, that a valid {\i lashon neder} must attach to the object itself, not to the speaker\u8217's action.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Le-Korban Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Le-Korban Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a later {\i mishna} presenting a parallel case: {\i le-korban lo ochal lach} \u8212- \u8220"that which I will not eat of yours is {\i le-korban}\u8221" \u8212- and records that Rabbi Meir rules this {\i assur}. This is initially puzzling. If Rabbi Meir rejects {\i miklal lav ata shomea hen}, why should declaring one\u8217's non-food to be {\i le-korban} render anything forbidden? The apparent reasoning would be: \u8220"my non-food is {\i korban},\u8221" therefore \u8220"my food is not {\i korban}\u8221" \u8212- but that is precisely the inference Rabbi Meir claims cannot be drawn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Abba Reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this through Rav Abba\u8217's reading: {\i na\u8217'aseh k\u8217'omer le-korban yehei, lefikhach lo ochal lach} \u8212- we interpret the person as having said \u8220"let it be a {\i korban}; therefore I will not eat it.\u8221" On this reading, {\i le-korban} is parsed as {\i lekorban} \u8212- it should become a {\i korban} \u8212- and {\i lo ochal lach} is a natural consequence: since it is now a {\i korban}, I will not eat it. This is standard {\i konam} language: the speaker declares the object a {\i korban} and states the practical upshot. There is no {\i miklal lav} involved; the person simply said {\i konam} by another route, and even Rabbi Meir accepts this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks the obvious parallel question: if {\i lachullin} means {\i korban}, then {\i lachullin lo ochal lach} should be identical to {\i le-korban lo ochal lach}. Why, then, does the {\i braita} rule {\i lachullin lo ochal lach} as {\i mutar}? We should be able to apply the same parsing strategy \u8212- \u8220"it is {\i lachullin} (i.e., a {\i korban}), therefore I will not eat it\u8221" \u8212- and rule it {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Author Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers with a principle of partial agreement: {\i hai tanna savar lah ke-Rabbi Meir b\u8217'chada u\u8217'palig leh b\u8217'chada} \u8212- the author of this {\i braita} agrees with Rabbi Meir in one respect but disagrees in another. The {\i braita} follows Rabbi Meir in rejecting {\i miklal lav ata shomea hen} \u8212- that is the basis for ruling the {\i chulin lo ochal lach} formulas {\i mutar}. But the {\i braita} does not follow the reading of the later {\i mishna} in which we parse the sentence as \u8220"it is a {\i korban}, therefore I will not eat it.\u8221" Without that interpretive move, {\i le-korban lo ochal lach} would simply mean \u8220"my non-food is a {\i korban},\u8221" which says nothing about the food in front of the person and therefore carries no binding force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abstract Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this established, the {\i Gemara} can explain why {\i lachullin lo ochal lach} is {\i mutar} for this {\i tanna}. The sentence, read straightforwardly and without the \u8220"therefore\u8221" parsing, declares that the things one does not eat are {\i lachullin} \u8212- i.e., {\i korban}. But this refers to no concrete food object. The statement is, in effect, abstract \u8212- it either refers to non-food items or to the concept of non-eating, neither of which constitutes a {\i davar hanadur} with any practical force. Since a valid {\i neder} must attach to a real object that one would otherwise eat, this formula leaves all food {\i mutar}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 She-Ani Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical illustration helps clarify the underlying principle. One cannot make one\u8217's computer or one\u8217's car forbidden through the language of {\i nedarim}, because these are not foods \u8212- there is no relationship of eating to be governed. The language of a {\i neder} must address an object that is in the domain of eating, not arbitrary items in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She-Ani Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why the classic formulation is {\i konam she-ani ochal lach} \u8212- \u8220"that which I would eat of yours is {\i konam}.\u8221" The focus is on the food object: those things I would ordinarily eat are hereby declared {\i korban}. The second half of the statement (\u8220"therefore I will not eat\u8221") is not the {\i neder} itself but a natural description of its consequence. The {\i neder} is in the first clause, where the object is identified and its status declared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Object Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle that emerges is that {\i lashon neder} must focus on the object, not on the speaker\u8217's action. A {\i neder} is not a {\i shevuah}. A {\i shevuah} binds the person \u8212- \u8220"I swear I will not eat.\u8221" A {\i neder} binds the object \u8212- \u8220"this food is {\i hekdesh} to me.\u8221" If the language points to the speaker\u8217's action rather than to the object\u8217's status, it may constitute {\i lashon shevuah} at most, but it does not qualify as a {\i neder} in the technical sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She-Eini Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle generates a significant dispute among the {\i rishonim} concerning two variants of the same formula. {\i Konam she-ani ochal lach} \u8212- \u8220"that which I eat of yours is {\i konam}\u8221" \u8212- clearly refers to the food object, and is unambiguously a valid {\i neder}. But what of {\i konam she-eini ochal lach} \u8212- \u8220"that which I do not eat of yours is {\i konam}\u8221"? There is a {\i machloket rishonim} on this question. On one reading, this can still be parsed as \u8220"it is {\i konam}, therefore I will not eat it\u8221" \u8212- the {\i she-eini} being the consequence rather than the subject. On another reading, {\i she-eini ochal lach} is fundamentally a statement about what the speaker will do, not about what the object is, and therefore it does not constitute proper {\i lashon neder}. This maps directly onto the tension in the {\i Gemara} between the formula ruled {\i assur} in the later {\i mishna} (which requires the sentence-splitting interpretation) and the formula ruled {\i mutar} in the {\i braita} (which refuses to split the sentence and therefore reads everything as referring abstractly to the act of non-eating rather than to the food itself).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korban Statuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now opens a new section of the {\i mishna}: {\i tahor v\u8217'tamai notar u\u8217'pigul assur} \u8212- one who declares food to be like that which is {\i tahor}, {\i tamei}, {\i notar}, or {\i pigul} has made a binding vow. The question is what, precisely, is being invoked by each of these terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelamim Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama poses a pointed question: what is the status of one who says {\i harei alai ke-vasar zivchei shelamim le-achar zerikat damim} \u8212- \u8220"this [bread] should be upon me like the meat of {\i shelamim} offerings after the blood has been thrown\u8221"? The complication is that at the moment of the statement, the meat in question is in its permitted state \u8212- it is the very meat one is currently eating. Does this formulation function as a valid {\i kinnuy} for {\i korban}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted State\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} first clarifies the question. If the person explicitly stated the full formula \u8212- \u8220"like {\i shelamim} meat after blood-throwing\u8221" \u8212- they have clearly referenced the meat in its {\i mutar} state. Grabbing onto something in its permitted condition ({\i be-heteira kamatpis}) seems problematic as a basis for a {\i neder}: you have named a {\i davar hanadur} that is currently {\i mutar}, and a {\i davar hanadur} should be an object in a state of {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Live Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore refines the scenario: imagine that someone is actively eating {\i shelamim} meat \u8212- the blood has been thrown, the meat is permitted, and the person is mid-meal \u8212- and another individual comes and places a piece of bread nearby, saying {\i ze ke-ze}: \u8220"this bread should be like this meat.\u8221" What is the halachic status of the bread?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates the {\i Gemara}\u8217's central question: {\i be-ikaro kamatpis o be-heiter kamatpis} \u8212- is the person \u8220"grabbing onto\u8221" the original identity of the meat (as a {\i korban shelamim} prior to {\i zerikat damim}, when it was {\i assur}), or are they grabbing onto its current permitted status? The concept of {\i matpis} \u8212- latching the {\i neder} onto a pre-existing identified object to define the status of a new one \u8212- is crucial here. If the speaker intends to invoke the original {\i korban} identity of the meat, then this is a valid {\i kinnuy}: the bread is being declared like a {\i korban}, and the {\i neder} is binding. If the speaker is relating to the meat as it currently stands \u8212- a {\i mutar} food \u8212- then there is no {\i davar hanadur} with {\i issur} status onto which the {\i neder} can be anchored, and the {\i neder} would be ineffective. This question \u8212- whether {\i be-ikaro kamatpis} or {\i be-heiter kamatpis} \u8212- is left unresolved as the daf concludes, a fitting cliffhanger for a section whose every sentence proves inexhaustible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 12\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 B\u8217'Ikaro vs.\u160 ?B\u8217'Hashta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelamim After Blood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens by picking up from the bottom of 11b, where Rami bar Chama poses a question about a case in which someone says: \u8220"This should be forbidden to me like the meat of a {\i shelamim} after {\i zrikat hadam}.\u8221" The Gemara immediately objects: after {\i zrikat hadam}, a {\i shelamim} is permissible to be eaten \u8212- so the speaker has explicitly invoked something that is not in a forbidden state, making the question moot. The Gemara therefore reformulates the case. The man has before him a piece of {\i shelamim} meat after the blood has been sprinkled \u8212- he is in fact eating from it \u8212- and he gestures at a loaf of bread and says {\i zeh kazeh}, \u8220"this is like this.\u8221" The question is: when he says \u8220"this,\u8221" to what status of the {\i shelamim} is he referring?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two possibilities present themselves. Either he refers to the object\u8217's current state ({\i b\u8217'hashta kamatpis}), in which case the {\i shelamim} meat is presently permitted and therefore not a {\i davar hanadur} in a forbidden sense, and the {\i neder} would have no effect. Or, he refers to the object\u8217's original state ({\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis}) \u8212- meaning the moment he originally sanctified the animal as a {\i shelamim}, when it was indeed a {\i davar hanadur} in a fully forbidden state \u8212- in which case the {\i neder} would take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Notar & Pigul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: come and hear. Our {\i Mishnah} lists {\i notar} and {\i pigul} as valid objects of {\i matpis b\u8217'davar hanadur}. The {\i Mishnah} states that one may declare \u8220"behold this is like {\i notar}\u8221" or \u8220"behold this is like {\i pigul},\u8221" and this constitutes an effective {\i neder}. Crucially, both {\i notar} and {\i pigul} arise only {\i after zrikat hadam}. {\i Notar} is meat from a {\i korban} that was permitted for eating during the designated time, but which the owner left past the deadline; it is now forbidden by the Torah as {\i notar}, a prohibition entirely distinct from the original {\i neder}. The {\i pigul} prohibition \u8212- arising from an improper thought ({\i machshava}) during the {\i shechita} or {\i zrika} \u8212- conceptually precedes the {\i zrika} even if it is identified {\i l\u8217'achar zrikat hadam}, since the disqualifying {\i machshava} prevents the {\i zrika} from ever rendering the meat permitted. The {\i rishonim} therefore either excise {\i pigul} from the proof or treat it as {\i lav davka}, taking the operative case to be {\i notar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The significance of {\i notar} is this: during the period when the meat was permitted, the {\i neder} was not operative upon it \u8212- it was {\i mutar}. The {\i issur notar} that now applies to it is not a product of the original {\i neder}; rather, it is a distinct Torah prohibition comparable to {\i basar b\u8217'chalav}. As such, in its current state, the {\i shelamim} meat that became {\i notar} is a {\i davar ha\u8217'assur}, not a {\i davar hanadur}. Yet the {\i Mishnah} treats \u8220"behold this is like {\i notar}\u8221" as an effective {\i neder}. This implies that when one says \u8220"{\i zeh kazeh},\u8221" he is understood as referring back to the object\u8217's original state as {\i shelamim} \u8212- {\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis} \u8212- when it was indeed a fully operative {\i davar hanadur}. The word \u8220"{\i notar}\u8221" identifies the object; what the speaker intends is the status it held before it became permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why does the nature of {\i pigul} matter here? {\i Pigul} is indeed a function of {\i machshava} \u8212- the wrong intention at the time of the sacrificial act distorts the status of the offering, rendering it perpetually forbidden. The {\i rishonim} note that since {\i pigul} never passed through a state of being {\i mutar}, it is in a different category from {\i notar}, and the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's mention of it alongside {\i notar} is not essential to the question of {\i b\u8217'ikaro} versus {\i b\u8217'hashta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Notar Shel Olah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bei d\u8217'Rav Nasan objects to the proof from {\i notar}: perhaps the {\i Mishnah} is specifically discussing {\i notar shel olah}. An {\i olah} is never eaten by anyone \u8212- even after {\i zrikat hadam} it remains entirely forbidden to lay persons, and that prohibition is a direct consequence of the {\i neder} by which it was sanctified. Thus {\i notar shel olah} never passes through a stage of being permitted; it remains a {\i davar hanadur} in its forbidden state at all times. If so, one could invoke {\i notar shel olah} in its current state and still be {\i matpis b\u8217'davar hanadur} without needing to go back to an earlier status at all. This would mean the {\i Mishnah} offers no proof for {\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis} \u8212- it might be entirely consistent with {\i b\u8217'hashta kamatpis}, so long as there exists some scenario in which the word \u8220"{\i notar}\u8221" describes an object currently forbidden as a {\i davar hanadur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the principle of {\i stam nedarim l\u8217'hachmir} \u8212- that an ambiguous {\i neder} is interpreted stringently \u8212- would lead us to read a bare utterance of \u8220"behold this is like {\i notar}\u8221" as referring to {\i notar shel olah}, the stricter category, rather than to {\i notar shel shelamim}, the lenient one. If, however, the speaker specified \u8220"behold this is like {\i notar shel shelamim},\u8221" we would interpret his words according to the current state of the object, which is no longer forbidden as a {\i davar hanadur}, and the {\i neder} would not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Call It Notar?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a pointed question against this reading: if the concern is the {\i olah} category, why say \u8220"{\i notar shel olah}\u8221" at all? Why not simply say \u8220"{\i basar olah}\u8221" \u8212- the meat of an {\i olah}? There is no question that \u8220"behold this is like an {\i olah}\u8221" constitutes {\i matpis b\u8217'davar hanadur}; the animal is sanctified through your speech act and is entirely forbidden. What is added by labeling it as {\i notar}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution on Olah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i lo mibaya ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- it goes without saying that \u8220"behold this is like an {\i olah}\u8221" is forbidden, since you are explicitly being {\i matpis b\u8217'korban}, invoking a straightforwardly forbidden {\i davar hanadur}. The {\i chiddush} is precisely the case of \u8220"{\i notar} of an {\i olah}.\u8221" One might have thought that by saying \u8220"{\i notar},\u8221" the speaker emphasizes the {\i issur notar} dimension of the object rather than its {\i korban} dimension \u8212- and the {\i issur notar} is not a {\i davar hanadur} but a {\i davar ha\u8217'assur} \u8212- and therefore the {\i neder} should not take effect. The Gemara teaches us that this is not so: since the object is still an {\i olah}, since it never passed through a stage of being permitted, the invocation of its {\i notar} status merely emphasizes a layer of its {\i issur}, but the speaker is understood as being {\i matpis} in its {\i davar hanadur} dimension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy is instructive. If one were to say \u8220"behold this is like {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} of an {\i olah},\u8221" one would clearly be invoking the {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} prohibition \u8212- a {\i davar ha\u8217'assur} \u8212- and the {\i neder} would not take effect even though the underlying object is an {\i olah}. By contrast, \u8220"{\i notar shel olah}\u8221" is so deeply intertwined with the {\i korban} identity of the {\i olah} that we read it as invoking the {\i davar hanadur} status. Whether this analysis applies only when the speaker explicitly says \u8220"{\i notar shel olah},\u8221" or whether a bare statement of \u8220"{\i notar}\u8221" triggers {\i stam nedarim l\u8217'hachmir} to read it as {\i notar shel olah}, remains a point worth considering, though the Gemara does not resolve this definitively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader interpretive question underlying all of this concerns how we determine what a speaker meant by his words. The answer, as this tractate makes clear throughout, is that it does not primarily matter what the speaker privately intended \u8212- it matters what the words objectively mean. A {\i neder} requires not merely intent but the correct verbal formulation, and even the speaker himself is bound by the meaning of his words as assessed by {\i halacha}, not by whatever he may have privately meant. If a statement has multiple possible meanings, the task is to determine which {\i halachic} meaning the words carry \u8212- and {\i stam nedarim l\u8217'hachmir} is the operative principle for resolving ambiguity. This is why, in many of these cases, we do not simply ask the speaker; even if we did, the answer could not override the {\i halachic} reading of what was said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Taanit & Yahrtzeit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meat & Wine Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a {\i Mishnah} that brings a different kind of case to bear on the question of {\i b\u8217'ikaro} versus {\i b\u8217'hashta}. The {\i Mishnah} describes a person who says: \u8220"Behold I am [forbidden from eating] meat and drinking wine like the day on which my father died\u8221" ({\i k\u8217'yom shemeis bo aviv}), or like the day his {\i rebbe} died, or like the day Gedaliah ben Achikam was killed, or like the day he visited Yerushalayim and saw it in its destruction. The practice of accepting a {\i ta\u8217'anis} or forms of self-denial on a {\i yahrtzeit} is thus attested as a phenomenon going back to the earliest layers of the tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case introduces a conceptual abstraction. Whereas in the {\i shelamim} case the {\i davar hanadur} was a piece of meat, here the object of the {\i neder} appears to be a {\i day}. The model seems to parallel the case of {\i tzedakah}: just as money set aside for {\i tzedakah} acquires the status of a {\i davar hanadur}, and just as bread can become a {\i davar hanadur} through a declaration of {\i konam}, so too a day can be given a status of {\i ta\u8217'anis} through the spoken acceptance. The day itself becomes, in a sense, the {\i davar hanadur}. Five years ago, the man declared that a particular day \u8212- his father\u8217's {\i yahrtzeit}, or {\i Tzom Gedaliah} \u8212- was {\i assur alav} for eating meat and wine. That day thereby became a {\i davar hanadur}. Now, five years later, he says: \u8220"This [day] is like that day.\u8221" He is being {\i matpis} in the {\i davar hanadur} of the earlier day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel clarifies the {\i Mishnah}: {\i v\u8217'hu shenadar u\u8217'va m\u8217'oso hayom v\u8217'eilach} \u8212- the case is specifically one where he has been making this {\i neder} continuously from that original day forward. It is not that there was a single {\i neder} five years ago with four intervening years of non-{\i neder} status; rather, every year since his father\u8217's death he has accepted the same {\i neder} upon himself. Thus when he says on the current {\i yahrtzeit} \u8220"this is like my father\u8217's {\i yahrtzeit},\u8221" every instantiation of that day \u8212- including the most recent ones \u8212- carries the status of {\i davar hanadur}, and there is no ambiguity about which day he is invoking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof from Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might attempt to read the {\i Mishnah} as a proof for {\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis}: even if there were four intervening years in which the day passed without a {\i neder}, the speaker is still understood as invoking the {\i neder} status of the original day five years ago, going back past four years of {\i mutar} instantiations. The Gemara rejects this reading in light of Shmuel\u8217's clarification. Since Shmuel requires that the {\i neder} be ongoing \u8212- {\i shenadar u\u8217'va m\u8217'oso hayom v\u8217'eilach} \u8212- every year has been a {\i davar hanadur}, and there is no scenario in the {\i Mishnah} where the speaker is reaching back past years of {\i heter}. The {\i Mishnah} therefore offers no proof for {\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis}; it is perfectly consistent with {\i b\u8217'hashta kamatpis}, since in its current and ongoing state the day is a {\i davar hanadur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual elegance here is striking. The day of a {\i yahrtzeit} or {\i Tzom Gedaliah} cycles back each year as the same day with a potentially new or renewed status. When one says \u8220"this day is like that day,\u8221" the question is which instantiation of the day one is invoking \u8212- the original {\i neder}-bearing instance, an intermediate {\i mutar} instance, or the most recent one. Shmuel\u8217's ruling forecloses the ambiguity by requiring that all instantiations share the same {\i davar hanadur} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder on Prohibited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran discusses what the {\i chiddush} of the {\i Mishnah} actually is, if the {\i Mishnah} presents no proof for or against {\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis}. The Gemara in {\i Shevuos} addresses this same passage and identifies the {\i chiddush} as follows: one might have thought that since {\i Tzom Gedaliah} is already {\i assur m\u8217'drabanan}, a {\i neder} accepted upon that day would not take effect \u8212- {\i ki nadar nami lo chayil neder}. Perhaps a {\i neder} cannot take hold on something already forbidden. The {\i Mishnah} teaches us that this is not so: even when a day is already forbidden by rabbinic enactment, a {\i neder} accepted upon that day does take effect. The person then bears both the rabbinic prohibition and the {\i neder} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kraft Cheese Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran illustrates this with a practical example. If someone says \u8220"{\i neder konam} all [non-Jewish cheese] upon me\u8221" \u8212- even though such cheese is already {\i assur m\u8217'drabanan} \u8212- the {\i neder} takes effect and adds an additional layer of prohibition. One who thereafter eats such cheese violates not only the rabbinic prohibition but also his own {\i neder}. Consequently, if this same person were then to take a piece of permitted cheese and say \u8220"{\i harei zo} like [that non-Jewish cheese],\u8221" he would be {\i matpis b\u8217'davar hanadur} \u8212- piggybacking on the {\i neder} he had placed upon the first cheese \u8212- and the permitted cheese would also become forbidden. The Ran states explicitly: {\i v\u8217'hitpiso bahen d\u8217'matpis b\u8217'neder havei v\u8217'assur} \u8212- the {\i matpis} act upon something already {\i assur d\u8217'rabbanan} is effective, and a subsequent {\i zeh kazeh} on the {\i neder}-bearing object also takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teruma & Todah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teruma Permissible\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a further case from the {\i Mishnah}: {\i k\u8217'chalas Aharon u\u8217'k\u8217'trumaso mutar} \u8212- if one says \u8220"behold this is forbidden to me like the {\i challah} given to the {\i kohanim}\u8221" or like {\i teruma} given to the {\i kohanim}, it is {\i mutar}. Even though {\i teruma} is a {\i davar hanadur} \u8212- it acquires its status through the verbal act of separation \u8212- it is not fully {\i assur} to all people; {\i kohanim} may eat it freely. Since {\i teruma} does not carry an absolute prohibition flowing from the {\i neder}, it cannot serve as an effective anchor for {\i matpis b\u8217'davar hanadur}. For {\i matpis b\u8217'davar hanadur} to work, the {\i davar hanadur} must also be something that is fully {\i assur}. The Ran notes the distinction: a {\i davar ha\u8217'assur} does not take effect as an anchor for {\i matpis} because its prohibition is not {\i neder}-derived; a {\i davar hanadur} that is only partially forbidden likewise fails to serve as an anchor. Both aspects \u8212- {\i nadur} and {\i assur} \u8212- must be fully present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lachmei Todah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then continues: {\i ha k\u8217'trumas lachmei todah assur} \u8212- if, however, one says \u8220"behold this is like the {\i terumah} of {\i lachmei todah},\u8221" it is {\i assur}. The {\i terumas lachmei todah} is the loaf separated from the forty loaves brought as a {\i todah} offering. Someone who brings a {\i todah} brings forty loaves: thirty {\i matzah} of three different types, and ten {\i chametz}. From each group of ten, one loaf is given to the {\i kohen} \u8212- this separated portion is called {\i terumas lachmei todah} and carries a status analogous to {\i teruma}, restricted to {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i todah} offering presents a case directly analogous to the {\i shelamim} after {\i zrikat hadam}: by the time the {\i terumas lachmei todah} is separated and given to the {\i kohen}, the blood has already been sprinkled and the bread is now permitted to {\i kohanim}. It is no longer in a forbidden state. Yet the {\i Mishnah} rules that invoking {\i terumas lachmei todah} renders the {\i neder} effective \u8212- apparently because we look back to its earlier status, before {\i zrikat hadam}, when it bore the {\i kedushah} of a {\i korban} and would have involved {\i me\u8217'ilah} to eat. This seems to be a proof for {\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis}: we attribute the object\u8217's earlier forbidden status to the invocation, even though its current status is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separation Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a complication: is {\i terumas lachmei todah} truly always only found after {\i zrikat hadam}? In fact, it is possible to separate the {\i terumah} from the loaves before the blood is sprinkled. Moreover, the Gemara introduces an even more exotic scenario through a teaching of Rabbi Tuvi bar Kisna in the name of Shmuel. If someone baked his forty loaves as four enormous loaves \u8212- one of each of the four types, each the size of ten \u8212- he has fulfilled his obligation ({\i yatza}), though this is only a {\i lechatchila} deficiency, since the Torah specifies forty. However, the practical problem now arises: how does one separate the {\i terumas lachmei todah}? One cannot take one of the four gigantic loaves as the {\i terumah} for all the others, since {\i one must take one from each type separately} and may not substitute one type for another. Nor can one break off a partial loaf ({\i prusa}), since the requirement is for a whole unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only solution is {\i d\u8217'aphrashinhu b\u8217'lisha} \u8212- one separates the dough designated as {\i terumah} while still kneading it. One takes a portion of the {\i chametz} dough and sets it aside, and similarly for each of the three {\i matzah} types \u8212- the {\i chalot}, the {\i rekikin}, and the {\i revucha}. The remaining dough of each type is then combined into a single large loaf. This demonstrates that it is possible \u8212- if unusual \u8212- for {\i terumas lachmei todah} to be separated before {\i zrikat hadam}, even at the stage of raw dough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 B\u8217'Hashta Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore reinterprets the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's ruling on {\i terumas lachmei todah}. Rather than a proof for {\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis}, the case can be explained as {\i b\u8217'hashta kamatpis} applied with maximal stringency. When one says \u8220"behold this is like {\i terumas lachmei todah},\u8221" we interpret the words as invoking {\i terumas lachmei todah} in its current status as a {\i davar hanadur} \u8212- meaning the unusual but extant scenario of {\i terumas lachmei todah lif\u8217'nei zrikat hadam}, dough-stage {\i terumah} separated before the blood has been sprinkled. Just as the bare invocation of \u8220"{\i notar}\u8221" is read stringently as {\i notar shel olah} \u8212- because that is a scenario in which {\i notar} is currently a {\i davar hanadur} \u8212- so too {\i terumas lachmei todah} is read as the case in which the {\i terumah} is currently forbidden, namely the pre-{\i zrikat hadam} scenario. We are prepared, under {\i stam nedarim l\u8217'hachmir}, to find a reading of the speaker\u8217's words that places the object in a {\i currently} forbidden state as a {\i davar hanadur}, even if that scenario is unusual. What we may not be prepared to do is attribute to the speaker a reference to an {\i earlier} status that no longer obtains at all. The question of whether {\i b\u8217'ikaro kamatpis} is ever operative thus remains open, and the Gemara has not yet provided a conclusive proof for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bechor Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a {\i tannaitic} dispute that may directly bear on the question. Rabbi Yaakov holds that saying \u8220"behold this is forbidden to me like a {\i bechor}\u8221" is {\i assur}, while Rabbi Yehuda holds it is {\i mutar}. The central question is whether a {\i bechor} qualifies as a {\i davar hanadur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor After Blood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what exactly is the case? If the reference is to a {\i bechor} before {\i zrikat hadam} \u8212- while the animal is still alive and walking \u8212- why would Rabbi Yehuda permit? At that stage the {\i bechor} is a {\i korban} before {\i zrikat hadam} and seems fully forbidden. And if the reference is to after {\i zrikat hadam}, when the {\i bechor} has been slaughtered and its blood sprinkled and its meat is now permitted \u8212- why would Rabbi Yaakov forbid? The Gemara concludes that the case must be like the {\i shelamim} case: the meat of a {\i bechor} after {\i zrikat hadam} is lying in front of the person, along with some other permitted food, and he says \u8220"{\i zeh kazeh}\u8221" \u8212- this permitted food is like this {\i bechor} meat. The dispute thus maps precisely onto the question of {\i b\u8217'ikaro} versus {\i b\u8217'hashta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Davar Ha\u8217'Assur View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's permitting opinion rests on a fundamental claim about the nature of {\i bechor}: a {\i bechor} is a {\i davar ha\u8217'assur}, not a {\i davar hanadur}. The Torah requires invoking a {\i davar hanadur} for {\i matpis} to be effective ({\i ki yiddor neder}, the principle being that one must be {\i matpis b\u8217'davar hanadur}), and a {\i bechor} does not qualify because it does not derive its {\i kedushah} from your verbal act. A {\i bechor} is {\i kadosh} from the moment of birth \u8212- {\i kadosh mei-imei imo} \u8212- without any declaration on your part. Its sanctity is inherent and automatic, not the product of a {\i neder}. Therefore invoking \u8220"behold this is like a {\i bechor}\u8221" is {\i matpis b\u8217'davar ha\u8217'assur}, which does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanctification View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's forbidding opinion does not deny that a {\i bechor} is {\i kadosh} from birth, but argues that a {\i bechor} is nonetheless a {\i davar hanadur} because there is a {\i mitzvah} to sanctify it. A {\i baraisa} teaches in the name of Rebbi: from where do we know that one born with a {\i bechor} in his house has a {\i mitzvah} to be {\i makdish} it? From the {\i pasuk} {\i hazachar takdish} \u8212- \u8220"you shall sanctify the male.\u8221" Even though the animal is already {\i kadosh}, there is an obligation to add one\u8217's own verbal {\i hakdasha} to it. This means that a {\i bechor} also carries a {\i neder}-derived dimension of {\i kedushah}, and therefore it falls within the category of {\i davar hanadur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanctity Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's response to this argument is telling: {\i ki lo makdish leh mi lo mikdash} \u8212- even if one never sanctifies it, is it not already sanctified? The {\i mitzvah} of {\i takdish} is real, but its non-performance does not deprive the {\i bechor} of its {\i kedushah}. The fundamental identity of the {\i bechor} as a {\i korban} is not contingent on your words \u8212- it is inherent from birth. Whatever {\i kedushah} you add through your verbal sanctification is supplementary, not constitutive. The {\i bechor}\u8217's basic identity is therefore that of a {\i davar ha\u8217'assur}, not a {\i davar hanadur}, and invoking it does not create an effective {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov, for his part, could respond that even if the {\i bechor}\u8217's inherent {\i kedushah} persists without your declaration, the fact that one\u8217's words {\i do} add a genuine dimension of {\i kedushah} is sufficient to classify it as a {\i davar hanadur} for purposes of {\i matpis}. The added human {\i kedushah} is not merely declaratory; it is real, and this reality places the {\i bechor} in the {\i neder} category. The rebuttal, however, is that this added dimension is not the central or essential identity of the {\i bechor} as a {\i korban} \u8212- and it is therefore insufficient to transform it from a fundamentally {\i ha\u8217'assur} object into a fundamentally {\i hanadur} one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- between inherent sanctity and added sanctity, between {\i kedushah} that exists independently and {\i kedushah} that is constituted through human speech \u8212- carries profound implications well beyond {\i nedarim}. Rav Soloveitchik employed precisely this model in analyzing the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kiddush} on {\i Shabbos}. {\i Shabbos} possesses its own intrinsic {\i kedushah}, just as a {\i bechor} does. Yet there is a {\i mitzvah} of {\i kiddush} \u8212- to sanctify it with our words. The question is whether {\i kiddush} is merely a declaration acknowledging an existing reality, or whether it adds a genuinely human dimension of {\i kedushah} to what is already divinely given. Following the model of the {\i bechor}, one could argue that just as one has a {\i mitzvah lehakdisho} \u8212- to add one\u8217's own sanctification \u8212- even when the object already possesses independent {\i kedushah}, so too {\i kiddush} on {\i Shabbos} represents the human contribution of {\i kedushah} to the {\i kedushah} that {\i Shabbos} already bears in itself. This understanding of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kiddush} \u8212- as the genuinely constitutive human act of sanctification rather than mere verbal acknowledgment \u8212- was, the lecturer notes, already anticipated by an earlier {\i rishon} who drew on this very comparison.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 14\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Davar Ha-Nadur Paradigm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Davar Ha-Nadur Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by revisiting the fundamental principle established in the preceding {\i mishnah}: that when a person wishes to invoke a model or paradigm for a {\i neder}, the object of comparison must be a {\i davar ha-nadur} \u8212- something whose forbidden status was itself created through a person\u8217's verbal act of dedication \u8212- and not a {\i davar ha-assur}, something forbidden by virtue of the Torah\u8217's own prohibition. No matter how severe the prohibition of {\i chazir}, {\i shekatzim u-remasim}, or {\i avodah zarah}, none of these qualify as a {\i davar ha-nadur}, because their forbidden status derives entirely from the Torah and not from any human speech act of consecration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah/Challah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question carries over from the previous discussion: why are {\i terumah} and {\i challah} not considered {\i davar ha-nadur}? After all, their status as {\i terumah} or {\i challah} is unquestionably created through a person\u8217's verbal designation. The {\i Ran} offers one answer \u8212- that since {\i terumah} is not forbidden in all respects, it reflects a Torah-imposed prohibition rather than a purely person-created status \u8212- though this reasoning is difficult to sustain, since the implications of the {\i terumah} status are in many ways analogous to those of an animal consecrated as a {\i korban}, which certainly qualifies as a {\i davar ha-nadur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another approach suggested is that since the grain was already forbidden as {\i tevel} before the designation of {\i terumah}, the person is not truly creating a new forbidden status. One might even argue that the entire status of {\i tevel} is itself a function of the potential {\i terumah} latent within the grain, so that when one designates {\i terumah}, one is not so much creating a prohibition as completing or clarifying an existing one. An interesting test case for these questions would be {\i bikkurim}, which similarly is permitted to {\i kohanim} and forbidden to others, yet unlike {\i terumah}, failure to separate {\i bikkurim} does not render the produce {\i tevel}. Notably, no discussion in the {\i Gemara} or in the {\i Rambam} appears to treat {\i bikkurim} as a candidate paradigm for {\i nedarim}, which itself is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\u8217's Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} takes a substantially different approach to the requirement of {\i davar ha-nadur}, one that reconnects the entire institution of {\i nedarim} to the biblical concept from which it derives. Rather than defining {\i davar ha-nadur} solely as an object whose forbidden status was generated by a human speech act, the {\i Rambam} grounds it in the original biblical idea of a {\i neder} as a free-will offering to God. On his view, the {\i davar ha-nadur} must be the kind of thing that not only was created through one\u8217's speech but that emerged from a voluntary dedication to the Divine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious challenge to this position is: how then can one say \u8220"this should be like a {\i chattat}\u8221" or \u8220"like an {\i asham},\u8221" since those offerings arise from obligation rather than free will? The {\i Rambam} responds by noting the {\i Gemara} discussed previously: if one accepts upon oneself {\i nezirut}, one will under certain circumstances be obligated to bring a {\i chattat} and an {\i asham}, and so these offerings can in principle flow from an antecedent voluntary act. This also explains, according to the {\i Rambam}, why {\i terumah} and {\i challah} fail to serve as valid paradigms: they are not initiated by free will but by halakhic obligation, and therefore cannot be considered {\i davar ha-nadur} in the biblically grounded sense he requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is particularly illuminating about the {\i Rambam}\u8217's approach is how it restores the original biblical texture of the {\i neder}. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's analysis elsewhere tends to shift attention away from the biblical model \u8212- in which the {\i neder} begins with a person voluntarily obligating himself to dedicate something to God, as when Yaakov took a {\i neder} to give {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- toward a more abstract analysis of object-status. The concept of {\i nidrei mitzvah}, in which one takes a {\i neder} to perform a {\i mitzvah}, itself reflects this personal-obligation dimension: it begins with a personal commitment and ends with an object. The {\i Rambam} returns the analysis to precisely that framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} raises a penetrating question at the very outset of the {\i masechet} that bears directly on all of this: what is, in fact, the classic formulation of a {\i neder}? The {\i pasuk} says {\i ki yidor neder}, and the requirement is {\i ad sheyidor bedavar hanadur} \u8212- until one vows with reference to a {\i davar ha-nadur}. But what does that mean for the structure of the vow itself?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One way to read it is that the paradigmatic statement of a {\i neder} involves the language of {\i korban} \u8212- saying {\i konam davar zeh alai} or {\i korban davar zeh alai}, with {\i konam} serving as the operative {\i kinuy}. On this reading, if one simply says {\i pat zeh asurah alai} without invoking {\i konam} or {\i korban}, that formulation works only as a {\i yad} \u8212- an implied reference to the full statement \u8212- because the concept of {\i korban} is implicit even if unstated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Ran} actually raises the possibility that the reverse is true: perhaps the paradigmatic statement of a {\i neder} is simply {\i davar zeh assur alai}, directly invoking one\u8217's own power to render something forbidden, which is itself the content of the biblical phrase {\i le\u8217'esor issar al nafsho}. On this reading, when one says {\i korban} or {\i konam}, it is that language which functions as a {\i yad} to the underlying direct statement of prohibition. The {\i Ran} observes that the rule of {\i davar ha-nadur} applies only when one chooses to use a comparative model \u8212- saying \u8220"this should be like that\u8221" \u8212- in which case the comparator must be a {\i davar ha-nadur} rather than a {\i davar ha-assur}. But the foundational speech act of {\i nedarim} does not require invoking any external paradigm at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Speech Act Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question cuts to the heart of how we understand the power of {\i nedarim}. One instinct is to see the power of making something forbidden as borrowed or derived from the institution of {\i korban} \u8212- so that when one says {\i konam}, one is invoking the sanctifying power that attaches objects to the altar, and the {\i neder} draws its force from that. On this view, {\i konam} or {\i korban} is the paradigmatic statement, and saying {\i assur} without it is merely a shorthand or {\i yad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Ran} suggests something more fundamental: that the Torah grants a person an intrinsic power to make something forbidden through speech alone. One does not need to borrow the conceptual force of {\i korban} or any other institution. Simply declaring {\i davar zeh assur alai} suffices to create a binding {\i neder}, because that is an independent power the Torah vests in human speech. The use of {\i korban} or {\i konam} language is an optional mode of expression, and if one chooses to employ a comparative model, that model must be a {\i davar ha-nadur}. But the {\i neder} itself is constituted by the direct declaration of prohibition, not by derivation from the {\i korban} paradigm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely striking position, because it locates the force of {\i nedarim} not in the person\u8217's ability to replicate the consecrating act of {\i hekdesh}, but in an autonomous halakhic power of speech that stands on its own.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife Like Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harei At Alai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} turns to the case of a man who says to his wife {\i harei at alai ke-ima} \u8212- you are forbidden to me as my mother. The {\i mishnah} illustrates an important principle: a man may render sexual relations with his wife forbidden to himself, notwithstanding the fact that marital relations constitute an obligation and a {\i mitzvah}. This works because the {\i neder} does not directly prohibit the {\i mitzvah} itself; rather, it renders the object \u8212- here, in effect, his wife insofar as he would derive a specific benefit \u8212- forbidden, and the inability to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} is a consequence of that status. The technical formulation would be {\i konam hanas tashmishcha alai} \u8212- the pleasure of intimacy with you is {\i konam} to me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the present case, however, the man has not used {\i konam} or {\i korban} language. He has said \u8220"like my mother,\u8221" invoking an incest prohibition \u8212- that is, {\i matpis bedavar ha-assur}, not {\i bedavar ha-nadur}. The {\i mishnah} nevertheless requires him to undergo {\i hataras nedarim}, the process of annulment before a sage. The {\i Ran} explains this requirement on practical grounds: forbidding one\u8217's wife to oneself is not an uncommon occurrence in moments of anger, and if we were to say outright that no process is required because the language is technically invalid, people would draw the wrong lesson and become cavalier about {\i nedarim} that are halakhically valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a challenge: a {\i baraita} states that if a man says {\i harei at alai kevasar ima} \u8212- you are to me like the flesh of my mother \u8212- or like his sister, or like {\i orlah} or {\i kilei hakerem} (both of which are forbidden in {\i hana\u8217'ah}), {\i lo amar klum} \u8212- he has said nothing. This seems to contradict the {\i mishnah}, which requires {\i hataras nedarim}. Abaye resolves the tension by drawing a distinction between levels of prohibition: {\i lo amar klum} means that the {\i neder} has no biblical force, but rabbinically one must still undergo the process of {\i hataras nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmid Chacham\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a different resolution: the {\i baraita} and the {\i mishnah} refer to different types of people. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's requirement of {\i hataras nedarim} applies to an {\i am ha-aretz}, while the {\i baraita}\u8217's ruling that nothing more is needed applies to a {\i talmid chacham}. The logic, as the {\i Ran} understands it, is not primarily about knowledge but about consequence: the concern driving the rabbinical requirement is that a person who knows his technically invalid {\i neder} requires no process will become lax and extend that casualness to genuine {\i nedarim}. A {\i talmid chacham} who understands the distinction between valid and invalid formulations will not draw the wrong conclusion from being told he need not go through {\i hataras nedarim} in this case. One might ask why a {\i talmid chacham} would utter such a formulation in the first place \u8212- perhaps, as noted, it was a way of venting anger with the knowledge that the words carry no legal force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Vow Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a {\i baraita} that maps precisely onto the three-part structure just discussed: {\i hanoder baTorah lo amar klum} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} \u8220"with the Torah\u8221" has said nothing, since the Torah\u8217's sanctity is not a {\i davar ha-nadur} \u8212- any more than it is a {\i davar ha-assur} in the conventional sense. Rabbi Yochanan adds that nevertheless one must undergo {\i she\u8217'eilah lechacham}, the process of seeking annulment from a sage. And Rav Nachman adds that a {\i talmid chacham} need not do so. This {\i baraita} thus provides a precise parallel to the three positions in the previous discussion: the base ruling ({\i lo amar klum}), the rabbinic requirement ({\i tzerich she\u8217'eilah}), and the exemption for the {\i talmid chacham}. It is worth noting that the statements of Rabbi Yochanan and Rav Nachman function as {\i amora\u8217'im} clarifying the {\i baraita}, in the same way that {\i amora\u8217'im} routinely clarify a {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern underlying the rabbinic requirement, as the {\i Ran} frames it, is not that one should be discouraged from taking such vows in the first place, but that one might extrapolate from the lenient treatment of an invalid {\i neder} to laxity about genuine ones. The {\i Ran} suggests that {\i amei ha-aretz} were in general considered conscientious in their observance of {\i nedarim}, which is precisely what makes the risk of mislearning significant: someone who takes {\i nedarim} seriously may incorrectly generalize from the ruling here and become lax about a binding {\i neder} on the assumption that it similarly requires no follow-through.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hanoder BaTorah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to develop the case of {\i hanoder baTorah} in greater depth, examining what exactly is meant by the phrase {\i uvmah shekasuv bah} in the {\i baraita}: \u8220"one who vows with the Torah has said nothing; but one who vows with what is written in it \u8212- his words stand.\u8221" A {\i tanya} on 14b teaches this formulation and then adds that if one says {\i bah uvmah shekasuv bah} \u8212- with it and with what is written in it \u8212- those words also stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The immediate question is: what is the difference between saying {\i bah} (in it) and {\i bemah shekasuv bah} (with what is written in it)? And why is it obvious, as the {\i Gemara} seems to imply, that adding {\i bah} alongside {\i bemah shekasuv bah} should suffice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers an answer through Rav Nachman: {\i ha d\u8217'mancha oraissa a\u8217'ara} \u8212- if the Torah is lying on the ground \u8212- {\i ha d\u8217'nakit lah bidei} \u8212- and if one is holding it in one\u8217's hands. If the Torah is on the ground and one says {\i bemah shekasuv bah}, one may be referring to the parchment itself \u8212- the physical material on which things are written \u8212- rather than to the content. In that case, one is treating the Torah merely as a physical object, {\i geitei agvilei}, parchment, and the {\i neder} carries no force. But when one holds the Torah in hand and says {\i bemah shekasuv bah}, the mind is directed \u8212- {\i da\u8217'atei al hazkarot shebah} \u8212- toward the divine names written within it, and it is to those that the person is referring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis raises an underlying conceptual question: what exactly makes {\i bemah shekasuv bah} work as a {\i neder}? If one is referring to the {\i shem Hashem} \u8212- God\u8217's name written in the Torah \u8212- how does that serve as a {\i davar ha-nadur}? God\u8217's name is not a {\i davar ha-nadur} in any ordinary sense; its sanctity does not arise from a human speech act of dedication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvuah Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first explanation offered by the {\i Ran} is, in a sense, the most radical departure from the context: perhaps {\i hanoder baTorah} here does not mean a {\i neder} at all, but a {\i shvuah}. The {\i Ran} notes that the word {\i hanoder} sometimes denotes one who takes an oath rather than a vow in the technical sense. The scenario being described is one in which a person grasps a {\i sefer Torah} and declares \u8220"by this Torah\u8221" \u8212- much as one might place a hand on a Bible and swear in God\u8217's name. In that case, when one says merely {\i baTorah}, one is swearing by the physical Torah, without invoking God\u8217's name \u8212- and the {\i shvuah} is therefore ineffective. But when one says {\i bemah shekasuv bah}, one is invoking what is written within it \u8212- God\u8217's name \u8212- and is thereby taking a genuine {\i shvuah beshem Hashem}. On this reading, no question of {\i davar ha-nadur} arises at all, since we are dealing with a {\i shvuah}, not a {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raavad Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Raavad} proposes a second, more structurally interesting explanation, one that preserves the reading of {\i hanoder} as a genuine {\i neder}. His claim is that the {\i azkarot} \u8212- the divine names written in a {\i sefer Torah} \u8212- are themselves a form of {\i davar ha-nadur}, because their sanctity is instilled through the intentional act of the {\i sofer} who writes them. The {\i sofer} must write God\u8217's name with specific {\i kavana} \u8212- conscious intent to write it as a divine name \u8212- and it is through that act of intentional inscription that the holiness of the name is created.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a subtle but important question: do we understand the {\i sofer}\u8217's act of writing as one in which he is the agent who instills the {\i kedushah} in the name? Or do we say that if he writes it correctly with the proper {\i kavana}, the Torah\u8217's own framework recognizes the result as {\i kadosh} \u8212- but it is not the {\i sofer} who is the source of that holiness? The {\i Raavad} leans toward the former view: the {\i sofer} instills the {\i kedushah} through his act of writing with intent, and therefore the {\i azkarot} can be said to be {\i davar ha-nadur} \u8212- their status originates in a human act of creation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An analogy suggested to illuminate this is the requirement that a {\i get} be written {\i lishmah} \u8212- for the sake of the specific woman being divorced. There is a discussion among {\i rishonim} and {\i acharonim} about whether the husband\u8217's {\i lishmah} should be understood as him actively instilling in the {\i get} its directedness toward his wife, and whether it is precisely this instillment that explains the concept of {\i bitul get} \u8212- the nullification of a {\i get} before delivery \u8212- through which the husband can retroactively void it by declaring it null. If he instilled the {\i get}\u8217's directedness, he can theoretically reverse that instillment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Raavad}\u8217's position here has an additional dimension: the sanctifying act of the {\i sofer} is not a pure speech act but a physical act of writing, accompanied by intent. One might wonder whether this suffices to create the quality of {\i davar ha-nadur}, which we have been analyzing as arising from a speech act. A possible response, however, is that the act of writing is itself a concretization of thought \u8212- the {\i sofer}\u8217's {\i kavana} given physical form \u8212- so that it may be viewed as something closer to a speech act than, say, the physical separation of {\i terumah}. The {\i Ran} cites the {\i Raavad}\u8217's position thus: {\i azkarot she-bah nodrin hein o nedurin hein}, the divine names in the Torah are either the act or the result of an act of dedication, {\i she-al yedei ha-ketivah ve-hachanah le-sheim kedushah hein kedushot} \u8212- because through the writing and preparation with intent toward holiness, they are made holy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting that the divine names are not {\i assur behana\u8217'ah} in the ordinary sense. The Torah is used for reading and study; we do not typically categorize a {\i sefer Torah} as forbidden in benefit, subject to the rule of {\i mitzvot lav lehanot nitnu}. It has {\i kedushah} that demands a certain mode of treatment, but that is not the same as the standard {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} that attaches to {\i korbanot}. This makes the {\i Raavad}\u8217's identification of the {\i azkarot} as {\i davar ha-nadur} all the more striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third explanation comes from the {\i Yerushalmi}, and it takes a different approach altogether. Rather than focusing on the {\i shem Hashem} written in the Torah, the {\i Yerushalmi} understands {\i bemah shekasuv bah} \u8212- \u8220"with what is written in it\u8221" \u8212- as a reference to the {\i korbanot} mentioned in the Torah. The {\i Yerushalmi}\u8217's text reads: {\i ba-Torah lo amar klum, bemah shekasuv bah assur ke-kayamin ba-Torah bi-kdushat ha-Torah} \u8212- if you said \u8220"with the sanctity of the Torah,\u8221" that is nothing; but if you said \u8220"with what is written in it,\u8221" meaning the {\i korbanot} recorded therein, those {\i korbanot} are themselves {\i davar ha-nadur}, and one has made a valid {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is analogous to the case of {\i bi-Yerushalayim}: Jerusalem itself is not a {\i davar ha-nadur}, but by saying \u8220"like Jerusalem\u8221" one may be taken to mean the {\i korbanot} brought there, and on that reading the vow is valid. Similarly, {\i bemah shekasuv bah} evokes the {\i korbanot} of the Torah, which are the paradigmatic {\i davar ha-nadur}, and therefore the {\i neder} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related point: if someone says {\i harei zeh alai ke-korban u-ke-vasar chazir}, one might ask whether the inclusion of an invalid comparator ({\i davar ha-assur}) undermines the valid one ({\i davar ha-nadur}). The intuition is that it should not \u8212- as long as the valid formulation is present, the additional invalid comparator does not cancel it. The {\i neder} remains in force on the strength of the {\i davar ha-nadur} element. This logic also supports the {\i Yerushalmi}\u8217's position: {\i bemah shekasuv bah} encompasses many things, including {\i korbanot}; the fact that it also encompasses other material does not prevent the {\i korbanot} from doing the work of validating the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Bemah Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses why it states explicitly that {\i bah uvmah shekasuv bah} \u8212- with it {\i and} with what is written in it \u8212- also works, given that {\i bemah shekasuv bah} alone already suffices. Rav Nachman\u8217's first answer, as noted above, distinguishes between whether the Torah is on the ground or held in hand: if it is on the ground and one says {\i bemah shekasuv bah}, one may be understood to mean the parchment ({\i geitei agvilei}), and the {\i neder} fails; but if one holds it, one is presumed to have one\u8217's mind on the {\i azkarot}, and {\i bemah shekasuv bah} alone works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then offers a second formulation: {\i ve-i ba\u8217'is eima}, perhaps in both clauses the Torah is on the ground, and the point is to teach that even when it is on the ground, saying {\i bemah shekasuv bah} suffices \u8212- and it goes without saying ({\i ein tzarich lomar}) that {\i bah uvmah shekasuv bah} also works, so the latter formulation is mentioned only in a {\i zu ve-eino tzarich lomar zu} construction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third formulation reads the entire matter as applying when one is holding the Torah: when holding it, even saying {\i bo} alone suffices, because holding it demonstrates that one\u8217's mind is on the content. On this reading, {\i bah uvmah shekasuv bah} teaches that when holding the Torah, one need not say {\i bemah shekasuv bah} \u8212- saying {\i bo} alone, which otherwise might be taken as a reference to the parchment, is treated as equivalent to {\i bemah shekasuv bah} because the physical act of holding it clarifies one\u8217's intent. In this third reading, the {\i vav} in {\i bah uvmah shekasuv bah} functions as \u8220"or\u8221" rather than \u8220"and.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize: the {\i Gemara} has offered three distinct explanations for why {\i bemah shekasuv bah} works \u8212- as a {\i shvuah beshem Hashem} rather than a {\i neder}; as a {\i neder} in which the {\i azkarot} are themselves {\i davar ha-nadur} by virtue of the {\i sofer}\u8217's instillment of {\i kedushah}; and as a {\i neder} in which the reference is to the {\i korbanot} written in the Torah, which are the paradigmatic {\i davar ha-nadur}. All three are genuinely illuminating, and each carries significant conceptual implications for the nature of {\i nedarim} and the mechanism through which a {\i neder} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Konam on Actions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces a different class of {\i nedarim}: those in which a person appears to make an {\i action} rather than an {\i object} forbidden. The cases given are {\i konam she-ani yashen} \u8212- sleep is {\i konam} to me; {\i konam she-ani medaber} \u8212- speech is {\i konam} to me; and {\i konam she-ani mehalech} \u8212- walking is {\i konam} to me. The {\i mishnah} states {\i harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} \u8212- the prohibition of {\i lo yachel dvaro} applies. This formulation is itself notable: rather than simply stating that the person is {\i assur}, the {\i mishnah} frames the result in terms of the verse prohibiting the violation of one\u8217's word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i mishnah} stands in contrast to the final {\i mishnah} of the previous chapter, which addressed making a body part forbidden \u8212- there, the {\i mishnah} simply ruled {\i assur}. Here, where no body part and no physical object is mentioned, the {\i mishnah} uses the language of {\i belo yachel dvaro}, which sounds, if anything, even more emphatic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Nachman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a dispute: {\i konam einai besheina hayom im ishan lemachar} \u8212- my eyes are {\i konam} in sleep today, if I sleep tomorrow. The scenario is that today is the {\i tnai} (condition) and tomorrow is when the {\i neder} would take effect. If the person sleeps today, that triggers the condition; whether he then sleeps tomorrow will determine retroactively whether his action today constituted a violation of his {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: {\i al yishan hayom shema yishan lemachar} \u8212- one should not sleep today, lest one come to sleep tomorrow. The concern is that if the person sleeps today, activating the {\i tnai}, he will not be careful enough tomorrow to avoid sleep, and will retroactively have violated his {\i neder} by sleeping today. Rav Nachman disagrees: {\i yishan hayom velo chaishinan shema yishan lemachar} \u8212- one may sleep today; we trust the person to be sufficiently careful about his {\i neder} tomorrow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute turns on whether we trust a person who has already triggered the {\i tnai} \u8212- and thus placed himself in a situation where sleeping tomorrow would mean a retroactive violation of today\u8217's {\i neder} \u8212- to be sufficiently vigilant about tomorrow\u8217's {\i neder} obligation. Rav is concerned that the retroactive nature of the violation makes it psychologically remote enough that the person will not take it seriously. Rav Nachman holds that people can be trusted to fulfill their {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda concedes, however, that all agree in the reverse case: {\i be-omer konam einai besheina lemachar im ishan hayom} \u8212- if my eyes are {\i konam} in sleep {\i tomorrow} if I sleep {\i today}, then one may sleep today. {\i Ki lo mizdahar bitnai\u8217'ah aval be-issura mizdahar} \u8212- because people may be lax about a {\i tnai}, but when the {\i issur} itself is tomorrow\u8217's obligation, we trust them to be careful about keeping it. The debate only arises when sleeping today merely triggers a condition, and tomorrow\u8217's sleep is both the content of the {\i neder} and the variable on which retroactive violation depends.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grammatical Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A more fundamental problem with the {\i mishnah} is now surfaced. We know from the {\i mishnah} in the previous chapter ({\i chomer bishvuot}) that {\i shvuah} differs from {\i neder} in one significant respect: a {\i shvuah} can apply to {\i davar she-ein bo mamash} \u8212- something non-physical \u8212- while {\i nedarim} apply only to {\i davar she-yesh bo mamash}, something with physical substance. Sleep, speech, and walking are all non-physical \u8212- they have no {\i mamash}. How, then, can a {\i neder} in the form of {\i konam she-ani yashen} take effect at all? The {\i mishnah} appears to assume that it does, but on what basis?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eyes Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the problem by rereading the {\i mishnah}. The cases of {\i konam she-ani yashen}, {\i konam she-ani medaber}, and {\i konam she-ani mehalech} must be understood as shorthand for a formulation that does invoke a physical object. The {\i mishnah} must mean {\i konam einai besheina} \u8212- my eyes are {\i konam} in sleep \u8212- which inserts the eyes as the physical object to which the {\i neder} attaches. This mirrors the formulation of the final {\i mishnah} of the previous chapter, where body parts were explicitly mentioned. The {\i mishnah} here is read as doing the same thing implicitly, and the {\i Gemara}\u8217's reinterpretation confirms that a {\i neder} cannot attach to a pure action without any physical referent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Impossibility & Nede (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvuah Sleep\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even after inserting the eyes as the physical object, a further problem emerges. If the {\i mishnah} means {\i konam einai besheina} without a {\i tnai} \u8212- that is, my eyes are always forbidden in sleep, full stop \u8212- then we face a different difficulty. Rabbi Yochanan teaches: {\i shvuah she-lo yishan shelosha yamim, malkin oto ve-yashen le-alter} \u8212- one who swears that he will not sleep for three days receives lashes immediately and may go to sleep at once. The reason is that such a {\i shvuah} cannot possibly be fulfilled; it is physically impossible for a human being to go without sleep for three days. Because it is impossible, the {\i shvuah} is treated as a {\i shvuat shav} or {\i shvuat sheker} from the moment it is uttered, it never takes effect, and the person is free to sleep immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question posed by the {\i Gemara} is whether the same reasoning applies to a {\i neder}. If a person says {\i konam einai besheina} without qualification, he is in the same position: he cannot possibly fulfill this {\i neder}, since it is humanly impossible never to sleep. Does this mean the {\i neder} never takes effect?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Konam on Actions (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Itmar Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents the {\i itmar} \u8212- a recorded amoraic dispute \u8212- on the conditional vow {\i konam einai besheina hayom im ishan lemachar}. This is the precise case in which today is the {\i tnai} and tomorrow is the {\i neder}. The debate among the {\i amora\u8217'im} concerns whether the person may sleep today (trusting he will observe the {\i tnai} tomorrow) or must refrain today (lest he fail to fulfill the {\i tnai} and thereby retroactively violate the {\i neder}). This is the dispute between Rav and Rav Nachman discussed above. Rav holds that since a violation of the {\i tnai} would retroactively constitute a transgression \u8212- a form of retroactive violation \u8212- we cannot trust the person to be vigilant enough about the {\i tnai} and must therefore prevent him from sleeping today. Rav Nachman holds that we trust people to honor their {\i nedarim}, even when fulfillment depends on a future condition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Impossibility & Nede (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concept Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual comparison between {\i shvuah} and {\i neder} in cases of impossibility is illuminating and worth developing carefully. A {\i shvuah} is an obligation that the person takes on himself directly: \u8220"I will do such-and-such.\u8221" When a {\i shvuah} contradicts what is halakhically permitted or what is physically possible \u8212- for instance, swearing to do something the Torah forbids, or swearing not to sleep for three days \u8212- there is a direct head-on collision between what the person is declaring and what he is able or permitted to do. In such cases, the {\i shvuah} is void from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i neder}, by contrast, does not directly mandate or prohibit the person\u8217's conduct. It assigns a status to an object: my eyes are {\i konam} in sleep. The indirect consequence is that if the person sleeps, he will have derived benefit from those eyes in a way that violates their {\i konam} status \u8212- but the {\i neder} itself does not say \u8220"I will not sleep.\u8221" It says \u8220"my eyes are forbidden.\u8221" The impossibility argument therefore operates differently: when a {\i shvuah} to not sleep for three days is impossible, the {\i shvuah}\u8217's content directly contradicts physical reality. When a {\i neder} renders the eyes forbidden in sleep, the {\i neder}\u8217's content (a status assigned to an object) does not directly contradict physical reality in the same way \u8212- even if the practical consequence is that the {\i neder} cannot be honored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This parallels the distinction between saying {\i tefillin} is forbidden to me, which creates a situation where I cannot fulfill my obligation of {\i tefillin}, versus saying I will not put on {\i tefillin}, which directly violates my obligation. The {\i neder} addresses the object; the consequences for my obligations or physical capacities are downstream. Thus the {\i Gemara}\u8217's invocation of the {\i shvuah} comparison is noteworthy: it raises the question of whether impossibility of fulfillment is as fatal to a {\i neder} as to a {\i shvuah}, even though the mechanism of each is conceptually different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} address this question directly. From the {\i Gemara}\u8217's discussion \u8212- {\i mikan perushu de-neder she-iy efshar lekayem eino chal} \u8212- {\i Tosafot} derive that a {\i neder} which cannot be fulfilled does not take effect. The {\i Gemara} here appears to compare a {\i neder} that is impossible to fulfill to a {\i shvuah} that is {\i shav} \u8212- vain or impossible \u8212- and on that basis conclude that such a {\i neder} never takes hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then raise a potential counterexample: what about a person who takes a {\i neder} on all the fruit in the world? Can one really go through life eating no fruit whatsoever? {\i Tosafot} clarify that such a {\i neder} is {\i not} the kind of impossibility that invalidates, since it is in principle possible to live without eating fruit. The point is not that we cannot imagine someone fulfilling it in practice, but that there is no categorical physical necessity that prevents fulfillment. The impossibility relevant to {\i nedarim} is one where fulfillment is inherently physically impossible \u8212- not merely difficult or practically unlikely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the specific case of the eyes. The {\i neder konam einai besheina} \u8212- my eyes are forbidden in sleep \u8212- is not analogous to a {\i neder} on all fruit. It is not merely difficult for a person never to sleep; it is physically impossible, because the eyes will necessarily close. A person does not choose to sleep in the same way one chooses not to eat fruit; the closing of the eyes in sleep is a physical inevitability, not a volitional act that can simply be avoided indefinitely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The comparison to food is thus instructive. If I declare all food forbidden to me, it is not possible for me to live without eating \u8212- but the food itself has no inherent tendency to be consumed by me. The physical necessity of eating is about my biological needs, not about the nature of the food. The {\i neder} makes the food a forbidden object; the food does not have to become available to me. By contrast, when I declare my eyes {\i konam} in sleep, the very nature of the eyes \u8212- as a physical organ that will necessarily close \u8212- means that the {\i neder} is aimed directly at an inevitability of the physical object\u8217's own nature, not merely at a human need that may or may not interact with the object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether this distinction is fully persuasive or not, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's analysis proceeds on the assumption that the impossibility of never sleeping is sufficiently analogous to the impossibility of a three-day sleepless oath to be relevant in the context of {\i nedarim} as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Belo Yachel Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reframed Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now undertakes a careful reanalysis of the {\i mishnah} to understand what case it is actually addressing and what exactly {\i harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} means. After the foregoing discussion, the simple reading \u8212- that the {\i mishnah} is telling us that {\i konam einai besheina} (my eyes are {\i konam} in sleep) binds the person such that sleeping is a violation of {\i belo yachel dvaro} \u8212- turns out to be either too trivial or too problematic to be what the {\i mishnah} intends.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i mishnah} merely meant to say that {\i konam einai besheina hayom} is a valid {\i neder} for today, that would be nothing new \u8212- it simply parallels the {\i mishnah} from the end of the previous chapter. If it meant that {\i konam einai besheina} applies indefinitely without any {\i tnai}, then as the {\i Gemara} has demonstrated, such a {\i neder} cannot take effect because it is impossible to fulfill.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i Gemara} argues that the {\i mishnah} must be addressing the {\i tnai} scenario. The case must be {\i konam einai besheina hayom im ishan lemachar} \u8212- my eyes are {\i konam} in sleep today, if I sleep tomorrow. Today is the {\i neder}; tomorrow is the {\i tnai}. One might have thought that {\i harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} means we forbid the person from sleeping today out of concern that he will sleep tomorrow and retroactively transgress. But the {\i Gemara} challenges this: if he is careful not to sleep tomorrow, there is no {\i belo yachel} at all \u8212- so the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation of {\i harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} would only make sense if the {\i mishnah} is actually telling us that the person may sleep today, and the {\i belo yachel} is what he must avoid tomorrow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Semantic Puzzle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a genuine semantic puzzle. {\i Harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} \u8212- in its natural sense \u8212- sounds like a statement of prohibition: this is the prohibition of {\i lo yachel}, meaning one must not violate one\u8217's word. But the {\i Gemara} is now being pushed to read it as a statement of permission: one may sleep today ({\i harei zeh}), and the {\i belo yachel} ({\i lo yachel dvaro}) is simply the rule that would apply tomorrow if one slept tomorrow. On this reading, {\i harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} does not mean \u8220"this constitutes a violation of {\i lo yachel}\u8221" but rather \u8220"this [situation \u8212- sleeping today] will subject one to the rule of {\i belo yachel} [tomorrow].\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} takes note of the difficulty in reading the phrase this way and works through the problem carefully. The {\i Gemara} had initially wanted to read {\i harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} as prohibiting sleep today out of concern for tomorrow\u8217's {\i tnai}, but the argument was then made that {\i alman ka-issura mizdahar} \u8212- when it comes to tomorrow\u8217's actual {\i issur} (the {\i neder} itself), people are careful \u8212- and therefore sleeping today and trusting the person tomorrow is the correct approach. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's {\i harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} would then be a warning about what awaits tomorrow if the {\i tnai} is not kept, rather than a prohibition of today\u8217's sleep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's final resolution reads the {\i mishnah} as follows: the case is that the person {\i already slept} today \u8212- the {\i tnai} has been triggered. Given that, {\i harei zeh belo yachel dvaro} means that he must now be careful to keep his {\i tnai} tomorrow, i.e., not to sleep tomorrow, because if he does, he will retroactively have violated his {\i neder} through today\u8217's sleep. The {\i mishnah} is thus not deliberating about whether to permit or prohibit sleeping today; it is instructing the person who has already slept today that he is now under obligation to observe the {\i tnai} tomorrow, and the rule of {\i belo yachel dvaro} applies to that obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution accords with the position of Rav Nachman, who held that we trust a person to observe the conditions of his {\i neder}. The debate between Rav and Rav Nachman was about a prospective situation \u8212- whether to allow someone to sleep today knowing that doing so triggers the {\i tnai}. But the {\i mishnah} is addressed to a retrospective situation \u8212- the person has already slept today, and the {\i mishnah} tells him what he must do tomorrow. In that context, all agree: if you fell asleep today, you must keep your {\i tnai} tomorrow, for otherwise you will have transgressed {\i belo yachel dvaro}. The nuance is that the {\i mishnah} is not granting or withholding permission for today\u8217's sleep but rather delineating the consequent obligation that follows from it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 15\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yehuda vs.\u160 ?Rav Nachm\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna on Konam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 15. We pick up where the {\i gemara} says {\i Ravina amar}, beginning with the word {\i le\u8217'olam}, approximately fifteen lines from the bottom of the page. By way of orientation, it is worth recalling what has been under discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the end of the previous {\i perek} dealt with a case in which someone said {\i konam pi medaber imach} \u8212- a {\i neder} directed at one\u8217's mouth as a physical body part, which constituted a {\i davar sheyesh bo mamash}, a prohibition bearing on something concrete. The {\i mishna} before us now, by contrast, speaks of a {\i konam} formulated as follows: my sleeping is {\i konam}, my speech is {\i konam} \u8212- which would seem to be a {\i davar she\u8217'ein bo mamash}, a prohibition directed at an entirely abstract entity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Condition-Dependent Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, opened not with this question about abstract objects but with a distinct debate that will occupy us through the better part of this {\i daf}: the disagreement between {\i Rav Yehuda amar Rav} and {\i Rav Nachman}. The case is one in which a person declares a {\i neder} \u8212- making something forbidden to himself today \u8212- conditional upon some action he will or will not take tomorrow. The question is whether we permit the person to act upon the {\i neder} today, trusting that he will be careful tomorrow not to trigger the {\i tnai} (the condition), or whether we are concerned that once the matter turns on the {\i tnai}, he will not treat it with the same weight he would give to a direct violation of the {\i neder} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To clarify the mechanics: if the person does trigger the condition, then the {\i neder} takes retroactive effect, meaning that in hindsight he has violated the {\i neder}. But because the violation is established only retroactively and was uncertain at the time of the act, perhaps he will not take the {\i tnai} seriously enough. {\i Rav Yehuda amar Rav} holds that we do not permit the person to act today, precisely because he will not treat the {\i tnai} with the gravity he would apply to the {\i neder} itself. {\i Rav Nachman}, by contrast, holds that a person will indeed treat the {\i tnai} with the same seriousness and may therefore be trusted to observe it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Connecting Mishna to Deb\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempted, somewhat forcibly, to read our {\i mishna} as a special case of this debate \u8212- as addressing the situation in which what one does today depends on what one will do tomorrow. The {\i gemara} itself acknowledges that this is a strained reading. One reason cited for why the {\i mishna} could not simply be taken at face value was a {\i mimanashach} argument: if the {\i mishna} is discussing making sleep forbidden as an abstract idea, a {\i neder} to that effect would not work; and if it means making one\u8217's eyes forbidden to sleep, that was already taught in a prior {\i mishna}. Since it cannot straightforwardly be either, the {\i gemara} proposed that it must be addressing the {\i tnai} case. That is where the previous {\i daf} concluded.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abstract Neder Derabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now offers a different and important answer. {\i Ravina amar}, beginning with {\i le\u8217'olam kedekatani}: the {\i mishna} means exactly what it says \u8212- that the {\i neder} was formulated as {\i konam} on the sleeping itself, as an abstract act. The natural question is how {\i bal yachel} can apply to a {\i davar she\u8217'ein bo mamash}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer is {\i umai bal yachel mi-derabbanan} \u8212- the {\i bal yachel} that applies here is {\i mi-derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derabbanan Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a critically important point that was touched upon earlier but now receives explicit formulation: even though a {\i neder} takes effect at the biblical level only on something concrete, the rabbis extended the category of {\i neder} to encompass abstract matters as well. Accordingly, {\i konam} on my sleeping, {\i konam} on my walking \u8212- these take effect {\i mi-derabbanan}. From a conceptual standpoint, a significant gap remains between saying {\i shvua} I will not walk (which bears on the person himself) and saying {\i konam} on my walking (which is directed at the abstract act). But many of the practical distinctions collapse once we accept that both function {\i mi-derabbanan}. According to {\i Ravina}, then, the {\i chiddush} of our {\i mishna} is precisely this: even a {\i neder} formulated against an abstract action takes effect {\i mi-derabbanan}, and it is that rabbinic {\i bal yachel} which the {\i mishna} invokes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bal Yachel Derabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the {\i gemara} to pose a sharp question: {\i umi ika bal yachel mi-derabbanan}? Would we ever apply the language of {\i bal yachel} \u8212- which is a {\i pasuk} in the {\i Torah} \u8212- to something that is only {\i derabbanan}? The {\i gemara} is not suggesting there are no rabbinic {\i nedarim}; there is ample evidence otherwise. The question is specifically about invoking the {\i pasuk} of {\i bal yachel} in this context. To illustrate the strangeness of the proposition: one would not say that eating chicken cooked in milk violates {\i lo tivashal gedi be-halev imo} \u8212- one would say it is {\i asur mi-derabbanan}. Would we similarly apply a {\i pasuk} of {\i bal yachel} to what is only a {\i derabbanan}? That is the {\i gemara}\u8217's challenge.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minhag as Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Minhag Issur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers {\i in} \u8212- yes \u8212- and cites a {\i baraita} in support: {\i devarim ha-muttarim va-aherim nahagu bahen issur} \u8212- things that are permitted, which others have treated as forbidden through practice \u8212- {\i i attah rashai le-hattiran bifnehem}, you may not go ahead and tell them that their practice is baseless and that what they are doing is really entirely permissible, {\i she-ne\u8217'emar lo yachel devaro}, because it says, \u8220"his word shall not be violated.\u8221" The {\i baraita} thus establishes that a pattern of religious practice creates a binding commitment, and that the verse of {\i lo yachel devaro} applies to it \u8212- even though no one imagines that this is a formal {\i neder} in the technical sense. There is therefore a category of {\i bal yachel mi-derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Minhagim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage opens onto an extraordinarily important domain of thinking about {\i nedarim} that is, interestingly, almost entirely absent from the explicit discussions of our {\i masechet}, which consistently focuses on the power of {\i neder} as it operates on objects. Yet it surfaces here and there, and the implications are far-reaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i neder} that most of us think of in everyday life \u8212- when we say {\i bli neder}, for instance \u8212- tends to invoke the idea of personal religious commitment, of obligating oneself to do something. This is closer to the concept of {\i nidrei mitzvah}, such as {\i ashne ha-shana perek ze} \u8212- the commitment to study a particular chapter \u8212- and even there some {\i rishonim} held it was really a {\i shvua} rather than a {\i neder}. The biblical concept of {\i neder}, as in {\i vayiddor Yaakov neder lemor}, carries both the personal obligation and the dedication of an object. But for most people in practice, the concept of {\i neder} centers on that sense of personal religious obligation one takes upon oneself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One classic formulation is what is said during {\i hattarat nedarim}: {\i kol davar tov she-nahagti shalosh pe\u8217'amim ve-lo amarti she-ye-he davar ze bli neder} \u8212- that performing some positive religious act three times in a row, without having explicitly exempted it from the status of a {\i neder}, constitutes a kind of {\i kabbalat neder} to continue the practice going forward. This is not about formal {\i mitzvah} obligations, which would not generate a {\i neder}-related consequence, but about voluntary religious practices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatam Sofer Minhag\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Hatam Sofer}, who devoted considerable energy to combating religious reform and opposing the introduction of changes into traditional practice, drew heavily on precisely this idea. Even when a proposed change could be defended on technical halachic grounds, one powerful tool for resistance was the argument of {\i minhag} \u8212- that this is simply not the practice, and our practice is not the practice. The obvious weakness of this argument, standing alone, is that {\i minhagim} do change, and it is not in itself an overwhelming response if something is {\i halachically} permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the {\i Hatam Sofer} did to strengthen the force of {\i minhag} was to identify it with {\i neder}: every {\i minhag} is essentially a {\i neder} to act in that way, making every {\i minhag de-oraita} in force. In one {\i tshuva}, discussing what may have been a case of {\i bishul akum} or something similar, he reasons: even if the practice in question falls only in the {\i derabbanan} category, or does not fall in the {\i derabbanan} category at all and is therefore purely a matter of {\i minhag}, the {\i minhag} itself elevates it to {\i de-oraita} status through the force of {\i neder}. This is a strikingly powerful application of the {\i gemara}\u8217's principle here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to appreciate how significant a {\i chiddush} this represents, and how far it takes us from what one might expect to find discussed in {\i Masechet Nedarim}. One opens this {\i masechet} expecting a discussion of vows about objects \u8212- and indeed that is what fills most of the tractate. And yet, through passages like this one, the concept of {\i neder} quietly encompasses the entire domain of adopted personal religious practices and communal {\i minhagim}, with consequences reaching into major halachic controversies of the modern period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note in this context that {\i Tosafot} actually resists reading the {\i baraita} as an argument by mere analogy to our case of {\i neder} on abstract acts. {\i Tosafot} argues \u8212- see the line beginning {\i vachen nahagu bo issur teima mah inyan zeh le-nahagu bo issur}, followed by {\i yesh lomar de-hai namei she-nadar ve-nitkaven le-esor atzmo gam be-davar she-ein bo mamash, ein lecha minhag gadol mi-zeh} \u8212- that making a {\i neder} to forbid oneself from something abstract is itself the greatest possible form of {\i nahagu bo issur}; they are thus not two separate things but the same phenomenon. The simpler reading, however, is that the {\i gemara} is reasoning by analogy: just as a religious {\i minhag} generates a {\i derabbanan bal yachel}, so too does a rabbinic extension of {\i neder} to abstract matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minhag Issur vs.\u160 ?Heter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also important to note that the specific language of the {\i baraita} \u8212- {\i devarim ha-muttarim va-aherim nahagu bahen issur} \u8212- is carefully limited. It speaks of treating something as a {\i davar asur}, and this brings the discussion much closer to the world of {\i nedarim}. Consider, for example, someone who refrains from eating certain foods that mimic the taste or feel of {\i chametz} on {\i Pesach} even though they are technically permitted, or who does not travel on Fridays out of concern for arriving home before {\i Shabbos} in time, as the {\i gemara} in {\i Pesachim} discusses. In both cases one is treating something as {\i asur} \u8212- and whether the subject is an object (the pseudo-{\i chametz} food) or an abstract action (traveling on Friday), the prohibition is structured analogously to the world of {\i nedarim}, including the concept of {\i davar she\u8217'ein bo mamash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This stands in contrast to the other major category of {\i minhag}, which involves positive religious acts \u8212- taking on a practice of performing a {\i mitzvah}-adjacent act on a regular basis. That category is more analogous to the discussions of {\i ashne ha-shana perek ze} type commitments, which shade toward the {\i shvua} end of the spectrum. The {\i gemara} here, with its invocation of {\i bal yachel}, is addressing specifically the {\i minhag issur} form. The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion, in any case, is that a {\i bal yachel mi-derabbanan} does exist, and this suffices to answer the challenge to {\i Ravina}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife\u8217's Hana\u8217'ah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Timeline Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the question of {\i bal yachel mi-derabbanan} resolved, the {\i gemara} returns to the unfinished debate between {\i Rav Yehuda} and {\i Rav Nachman}. The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i mishna} \u8212- one we will be studying in approximately a month \u8212- and attempts to derive from it a ruling in the debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents the following case: a husband declares to his wife, {\i she\u8217'at nehenis li ad haPesach im telchi le-veit avicha ad heChag} \u8212- you may not derive benefit from me through {\i Pesach} if you go to visit your father\u8217's house at any point through {\i Succos}. The chronological structure is clear: {\i Pesach} falls first in the calendar year, followed by {\i Succos}. So the {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} runs from now until {\i Pesach}, and the {\i tnai} \u8212- the condition that would trigger it \u8212- is whether she visits her father at any point from now through {\i Succos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To lay this out concretely: if she visits her father\u8217's house during the period before {\i Pesach}, she has triggered the {\i tnai} and the prohibition on {\i hana\u8217'ah} takes effect, meaning she cannot derive benefit from her husband through {\i Pesach}. But the more interesting question is what happens during the period before she has triggered the {\i tnai}: may she be deriving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from her husband in the meantime? That is precisely the question of {\i Rav Yehuda} and {\i Rav Nachman}. {\i Rav Yehuda} would say no \u8212- we cannot trust her to refrain from visiting her father, so we do not let her derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} now. {\i Rav Nachman} would say yes \u8212- she can be trusted, and she may derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} with the expectation that she will not trigger the {\i tnai} later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inference Hana\u8217'ah Permit\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to examine the language of the {\i mishna}. It says: {\i halcha lifnei haPesach asurah behana\u8217'aso ad haPesach} \u8212- if she went to her father before {\i Pesach}, she is forbidden from deriving benefit through {\i Pesach}. The {\i gemara} infers: {\i halcha lifnei haPesach asurah} \u8212- only if she has gone do we say she is forbidden; {\i lo halcha lo} \u8212- if she has not yet gone, it is permitted for her to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah}, and we simply trust her not to trigger the {\i tnai} later. This inference would appear to support {\i Rav Nachman}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Abba}, however, rejects this reading. {\i Halcha lifnei haPesach asurah ve-loka} \u8212- the {\i mishna} means: if she has already gone to her father\u8217's house before {\i Pesach}, then the {\i tnai} is triggered, the prohibition is clearly in force, and if she were then to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah}, she would receive lashes. {\i Lo halcha} \u8212- if she has not yet gone, then {\i asurah be\u8217'alma} \u8212- she is simply forbidden, but without lashes, because we do not yet know whether the {\i tnai} will ultimately be triggered. The {\i mishna}\u8217's statement {\i halcha asurah} is thus not saying it is only when she has gone that we prohibit; it is specifying the more severe consequence. Even when she has not yet gone, {\i Rav Yehuda} would hold that we do not trust her to maintain the {\i tnai} and would therefore not permit her to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- though the prohibition would not carry lashes at that stage, since it would have the character of {\i hatra\u8217'at safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point a critical question arises: who, in fact, receives lashes? He made the vow; she derived the {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- why should she bear the burden of his {\i nedarim} and be the one who faces lashes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} addresses this question directly. He says: {\i lokah hi} \u8212- it is she who receives lashes. {\i Aval ihu eino loke she-harei lo asah shum issur} \u8212- he has not done anything wrong, since {\i she-hedirah}, he merely made the vow; it was she who violated it. {\i Ude-tana nami hashmi\u8217'enu me-agav orchei} \u8212- perhaps part of the point of the {\i mishna} is to tell us that {\i de-alah rechiv issura} \u8212- the prohibition rests on her, {\i aval lo alav klal} \u8212- not on him at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran vs.\u160 ?Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} disagrees. {\i Loke hu she-hedirah} \u8212- he is the one who gets lashes, but only if {\i im he-henah otah} \u8212- if he actively caused her to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah}, say, by giving her a massage or directly providing the benefit himself. In that case, he has violated his own {\i neder}. {\i Aval hi eina loka} \u8212- she does not receive lashes, because {\i de-kivan de-lo nadrah klal} \u8212- she never took a {\i neder}. {\i Lo shayach bah lo yachel devaro} \u8212- the verse says do not violate {\i your} word; this is not her word, so even if she acts against it, she has not violated the biblical prohibition of {\i bal yachel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran}, however, argues that the {\i Rambam}\u8217's position is untenable, citing a {\i sugya} in the later chapter {\i Ein Bein ha-Mudar} regarding {\i yesh milah be-konamos}. He arrives at the following principle: {\i ein ha-oser over klal} \u8212- the one who creates the {\i issur} does not transgress by virtue of the other person\u8217's violation \u8212- {\i ve-ha-mudar nami vadai avar} \u8212- and the one against whom the {\i neder} was directed is indeed the one who transgresses. The reasoning turns on the key phrase {\i lo yachel devaro}: {\i lo yachel a-dibur ka-amar} \u8212- what cannot be violated is the {\i word}, the {\i dibur}, as an abstract entity. It does not matter ultimately whose word it is; what matters is that a prohibition-status was created through speech, and anyone who violates that status violates that {\i dibur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing is entirely consistent with the central emphasis of our {\i masechet} on {\i neder} as creating an objective status in an object. I can make my coffee {\i asur} on you; the coffee acquires that status. If you then drink it, you have violated that status \u8212- you have violated the word that created it, {\i lo yachel devaro} \u8212- even though it was not your word. Just as one can sanctify an animal as a {\i korban}, and if another person misuses it they transgress, so too here: the person who crosses the line that should not be crossed is the one who transgresses, even though they did not draw that line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam}, by contrast, does not fully shift the focus away from the person who took the vow. Consistent with his view (discussed in the previous {\i daf}) that a {\i davar ha-nadur} must be the kind of thing one can bring as a free-will offering to God \u8212- connecting {\i neder} to the classical biblical act of obligating oneself to give something to the Divine \u8212- the {\i Rambam} understands {\i lo yachel devaro} as addressed first and foremost to the one who made the vow. For the {\i Rambam}, getting lashes requires that both conditions be met: the objective status of the object be violated, and the violation be committed by the very person who took the vow. Without both elements, there may still be a biblical prohibition, but the formal penalty of lashes does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence is striking. If I declare {\i konam} on this coffee regarding you, and you drink it: according to the {\i Ran}, you receive lashes, because you violated the {\i dibur}-created status. According to the {\i Rambam}, you do not receive lashes because it was not your {\i dibur}, and I do not receive lashes because I did not perform the violating act \u8212- unless I actively caused you to drink it. In that case, the {\i Rambam} would say I get lashes as the one whose vow has now been violated by my own hand. If neither actively violated \u8212- if you simply drank what I passively left for you \u8212- then according to the {\i Rambam}, no one receives lashes, even though there may well be a biblical {\i issur} in play.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Later Condition Violation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaatnez Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the debate and presents what amounts to a mirror-image {\i mishna}. The case here is: {\i shaatnez li al hachag im telchi le-veit avicha ad haPesach} \u8212- she cannot derive benefit through {\i Succos} if she goes to her father\u8217's house before {\i Pesach}. Here the {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} extends all the way to {\i Succos}, and the {\i tnai} is limited to the period before {\i Pesach}. The structure is the reverse of the earlier case: the {\i tnai} window comes first, and the {\i issur} period extends far beyond it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i halcha lifnei haPesach asurah behana\u8217'ah ad haChag} \u8212- if she went to her father\u8217's house before {\i Pesach}, she is forbidden from deriving benefit all the way through {\i Succos}. {\i U-muteres leilech achar haPesach} \u8212- and she is permitted to go to her father\u8217's house after {\i Pesach}. The {\i rishonim} discuss what the {\i chiddush} of this latter clause is; one reading is that we are not concerned that permitting her to go after {\i Pesach} will somehow lead retroactively or by extension to a prohibited going before {\i Pesach}. Then the {\i mishna} says: {\i halcha asurah, lo halcha lo} \u8212- if she went, she is forbidden; if she did not go, she is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again presses: {\i lo halcha lo} \u8212- does this mean that as long as she has not triggered the {\i tnai}, she is permitted to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah}? That would support {\i Rav Nachman}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} answers: {\i hu hadin afilu lo halcha asurah} \u8212- the same logic applies here: even if she has not yet gone to her father\u8217's house, she is not permitted to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah}. {\i Halcha asurah ve-loka} \u8212- if she has already gone and it is before {\i Pesach}, then the {\i tnai} is triggered, the {\i issur} is in full force, and if she derives {\i hana\u8217'ah} she will receive lashes \u8212- whether she or he, depending on one\u8217's reading of the {\i Ran} and {\i Rambam}. {\i Lo halcha asurah be\u8217'alma} \u8212- if she has not yet gone, she is still forbidden from deriving {\i hana\u8217'ah}, but it is a lesser prohibition without lashes, because the {\i tnai} has not yet been triggered and we do not yet know its ultimate status. The {\i mishna}\u8217's {\i lo halcha lo} is therefore not saying she is permitted to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} in the interim; it is saying she will not face lashes in that period. Whether she may derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} in the interim remains the open question between {\i Rav Yehuda} and {\i Rav Nachman}, and the {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna} remains difficult for {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meisivei Bread Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bread Condition Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a {\i meisivei} \u8212- a challenge from a {\i mishnaic} source. The case is: {\i kikar zo alai hayom im eilech le-makom ploni le-machar} \u8212- this loaf of bread is forbidden to me today if I go to Philadelphia tomorrow. The {\i mishna} then states: {\i achal, harei zeh be-val eilech} \u8212- if he ate the bread, he is now under a prohibition against going to Philadelphia tomorrow, as though there were a new biblical prohibition created by the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permit or Forbid Eating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes with some amusement the formulation {\i harei zeh be-val eilech} \u8212- as if there were now a new {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, \u8220"do not go to Philadelphia,\u8221" which he has generated through his {\i neder}. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's question is pointed: {\i mi katani ochel?} \u8212- does the {\i mishna} say he is {\i permitted} to eat the bread? It says {\i achal katani} \u8212- it says \u8220"if he ate,\u8221" not \u8220"he may eat.\u8221" The {\i mishna} is simply describing the consequences of having already eaten the bread; it never addresses whether eating the bread was permitted in the first place. This distinction is crucial for the debate between {\i Rav Yehuda} and {\i Rav Nachman}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} explains accordingly: the {\i mishna} sets up the case by assuming the bread was already eaten \u8212- not because it was permitted to eat, but because, from {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's perspective, he was not supposed to eat the bread to begin with. Since the {\i mishna} could not say \u8220"you are permitted to eat\u8221" \u8212- because {\i Rav Yehuda} would say you are not \u8212- it framed the case as {\i achal}, taking the act as a fait accompli. Once the bread has been eaten, regardless of whether it should have been, the {\i mishna} then addresses the consequence: you are now under {\i bal eilech}, do not go to Philadelphia. {\i Ela ayadi de-tana reisha achal, de-lo mistani leh ochel} \u8212- since the first clause had to be framed around the fact of having eaten rather than the permission to eat, the {\i seifa} continues in kind, addressing what follows from that fact. Nothing can be inferred from this {\i mishna} about whether one was originally permitted to eat the bread before the {\i tnai} could be determined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's {\i maskana} is thus that the debate between {\i Rav Yehuda} and {\i Rav Nachman} remains unresolved by the {\i mishnaic} sources. Many {\i rishonim} observe that the {\i gemara} works so hard to deflect challenges to {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's position \u8212- consistently providing him with answers \u8212- that one might infer the {\i gemara} is inclined to rule with him. The {\i Ran} leans toward {\i Rav Yehuda}: where a {\i neder} has been taken with a condition whose fulfillment is uncertain, we tell the person not to transgress the {\i neder} out of concern that he will trigger the {\i tnai} later.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder for Action Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Indirect Action Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i sugya} invites us to step back and appreciate a broader phenomenon at work throughout these cases. A {\i neder} is about an object \u8212- it creates a status in a physical or semi-physical entity, making it {\i asur}. A {\i shvua}, by contrast, is about the person who swears. And there is a strong reluctance to take {\i shvuot} precisely because of {\i lo yenakeh Hashem et asher yisa et shemo lashav} \u8212- the gravity of invoking God\u8217's name in a false or unfulfilled oath. How, then, does one obligate oneself to do or to avoid doing a particular action, when one wants to avoid the weightier instrument of {\i shvua}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is to use {\i neder} instrumentally, directing it at an object in such a way that the {\i neder} will create indirect pressure on one\u8217's actions. The {\i neder} does not forbid the action; it makes the object {\i asur}, and then the stakes of the {\i neder} bear upon whether the action is taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit Trip Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A concrete example: suppose I am trying to ensure that I actually make a long-deferred trip to Philadelphia. Rather than swear that I will go \u8212- which would invoke {\i shem shamayim} \u8212- I can say: all fruit in the world shall be {\i asur} to me if I fail to make this trip. Now I am strongly motivated to make the trip, not because the act of not going is directly forbidden but because I do not want to suffer the consequence of losing access to all fruit. The {\i neder} has translated into behavioral compulsion through the object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the loaf of bread works the same way, though it introduces a twilight zone of uncertainty. I said: this loaf is {\i asur} to me today if I go to Philadelphia tomorrow. Now, during this period, I do not know whether the {\i neder} will ultimately kick in. This is what generates the entire debate of {\i Rav Yehuda} and {\i Rav Nachman}: may I eat the bread and trust myself not to go tomorrow? The phenomenon is perfectly natural \u8212- using {\i nedarim} on objects to push oneself toward or away from certain actions \u8212- but the {\i tnai} structure introduces complexities about how much we can trust the person to maintain the condition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Forbids Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam Sex Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a {\i mishna} that asks whether a husband can use {\i konam} to forbid conjugal relations: {\i ha-omer le-isha konam she-ani mishamshech harei zeh be-val yachel dvarav} \u8212- if the man says to his wife {\i konam} that I will not have sex with you, this is subject to {\i bal yachel}. Here we are operating, as the {\i mishna} established, in the {\i derabbanan} realm \u8212- the husband is forbidding not his body per se but the {\i hana\u8217'ah} of the act, the abstract pleasure of the sexual encounter, which is a {\i davar she\u8217'ein bo mamash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She\u8217'ar Kesut Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges: {\i ve-ha mishtabed lah mid\u8217'oraita} \u8212- but he is biblically indebted to her, as it says {\i she\u8217'erah kesutah ve\u8217'onatah lo yigra} \u8212- he cannot withhold her food, clothing, or conjugal relations. One might think that this biblical obligation simply overrides any {\i neder} he could take. But that cannot be the full answer \u8212- after all, a person can make a {\i neder} that {\i tefillin} be {\i konam} on him, thereby indirectly undermining his biblical obligation to wear {\i tefillin}, since the {\i neder} forbids the object rather than directly confronting the {\i mitzvah}. The same logic would seem to apply here: let him forbid the {\i hana\u8217'ah} on her, and then when it comes time for the {\i mitzvas onah}, he simply cannot fulfill it because the object \u8212- as it were \u8212- is {\i asur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Property Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i gemara} rejects this approach, however, is not the existence of an obligation but the existence of a {\i shibud} \u8212- a property claim. The {\i Ran} formulates this with precision: {\i de-lo matzi avid de-mid\u8217'oraita meshubed lah ve-havei ke-oser nichsei chavero al chaveiro} \u8212- he cannot make this {\i neder} because biblically he is {\i meshubed} to her, and his attempt to make his sexual pleasure {\i asur} on her is like trying to forbid a friend\u8217's property to that same friend. She has a form of property rights, a lien, on his body with respect to conjugal relations. It is hers, in a meaningful sense. And one cannot make {\i asur} on another person what already belongs to that person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is important. Obligation alone would not block the {\i neder}; the indirect structure of {\i neder}-on-object routinely allows a person to undermine his own obligations. What blocks the {\i neder} here is {\i ownership}: she effectively owns a claim to his conjugal attention, and he therefore cannot make it {\i asur} to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pleasure Forbidden on Se\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore specifies the loophole: {\i be-omer hana\u8217'as tashmishech alai} \u8212- if instead the husband says, \u8220"the pleasure I would derive from you is {\i konam} on {\i me},\u8221" that formulation works. He is not forbidding her property to her; he is forbidding her property to himself. Anyone may make anything {\i asur} to themselves, including another person\u8217's property. He can make her car {\i asur} to himself; he can similarly make her sexual pleasure {\i asur} to himself. The {\i neder} thus takes effect. Indirectly, this prevents him from fulfilling {\i mitzvas onah}, but as we have established, indirect undermining of a {\i mitzvah} through {\i neder}-on-object does not invalidate the {\i neder}. They could not sustain the marriage under these conditions and would be required to divorce, but the {\i neder} itself is valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife Forbids Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Forbids Pleasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the reverse case. {\i Rav Kahana} states: if a wife says {\i tashmishi alecha} \u8212- my sexual pleasure is {\i konam} on you \u8212- then {\i kofin oso u-meshamshaseto} \u8212- we compel her nonetheless to have conjugal relations with her husband. She cannot make this {\i neder} effective against him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Shibud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is exactly parallel to the earlier case. {\i De-shibuda meshubada lei} \u8212- her body, with respect to conjugal relations, is {\i meshubed} to him. Just as she has a property claim on him for {\i onah}, he has a corresponding claim on her. Therefore, when she attempts to forbid her sexual pleasure on him, she is in the same position as the husband who tried to forbid his sexual pleasure on his wife: she is attempting to make {\i asur} on the other person something that already belongs to that person. It cannot be done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if she were to say {\i hana\u8217'as tashmishecha alai} \u8212- the sexual pleasure I would derive from you is {\i konam} on {\i me} \u8212- that formulation would work, on the same logic as the husband\u8217's case. She is making something {\i asur} to herself, and {\i she\u8217'ein machilin lo le-adam davar ha-asur lo} \u8212- one does not cause a person to receive what is forbidden to them. At that point, she is forbidden to have conjugal relations, and they would be required to divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Equal Marital Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} is, in the context of the broader {\i masechet}, extraordinarily significant \u8212- and not only for the halachic mechanics of {\i nedarim}. What is striking is that the {\i gemara} begins with the husband\u8217's case, and there it posits not merely that he has a {\i mitzvah} to have sex with his wife, but that she holds an actual {\i shibud} on him \u8212- a property claim on his conjugal attentions. That framing is then replicated precisely and symmetrically in the wife\u8217's case: his {\i shibud} on her mirrors hers on him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conventional framing of marital sexuality in halachic literature centers on the verse {\i she\u8217'erah kesutah ve\u8217'onatah lo yigra} \u8212- his {\i mitzvah} to provide for her conjugal needs \u8212- and the wife\u8217's right to demand sex at regular intervals. What receives far less explicit attention is his corresponding right to have sex with her, and whether that right is personal obligation or property-claim. This {\i gemara} answers that question by using the exact same language in both directions: {\i meshubed lah} and {\i meshubeda lei}. The {\i shibud} is equal and parallel. One spouse cannot make forbidden to the other what that other person has a property claim upon, and this principle applies identically regardless of which direction the {\i neder} is attempted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In a broader cultural context where woman as sexual property of the man has been the dominant framework for millennia, this {\i sugya} \u8212- encountered here in {\i Masechet Nedarim} of all places \u8212- quietly posits the man as equally the sexual property of the woman within the marriage, using precisely the same legal language in both cases. It is a remarkable conceptual statement about the nature of marital rights, and it emerges through the very particular lens of how {\i nedarim} interact with the property structure of conjugal obligations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 16\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder vs.\u160 ?Shevuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous Daf Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present {\i daf} picks up from the {\i mishnah} at the bottom of 15b, which addressed a range of fundamental conceptual issues. Among them: the husband\u8217's and wife\u8217's respective rights over one another\u8217's person, and how that mutual quasi-ownership bears on the enforceability of a {\i neder}. The sugya also treated the concept of {\i bal yachel} \u8212- specifically, a rabbinic {\i bal yachel} applying even to abstract matters such as sleeping or walking, where no physical object is directly involved. Finally, it examined whether a {\i minhag} of treating certain things as forbidden can rise to the level of a {\i neder}, particularly in the context of {\i mitzvah}-related acts, which have their own relationship to the more biblical dimensions of the prohibition on vow violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran vs.\u160 ?Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One critical point from the previous discussion deserves brief review: the dispute between the Ran and the Rambam regarding who receives lashes for violating a {\i neder}. The Ran holds that lashes follow the violation of the object\u8217's status \u8212- once the object has been rendered forbidden, anyone who violates that status receives lashes. The Rambam disagrees. He maintains that {\i lo yachel devaro} \u8212- the biblical prohibition \u8212- is violated only by the person who both made the vow and then broke it. It is not that the Rambam permits a third party to violate the {\i neder}; rather, in his view, the biblical lashes are reserved for the person whose own word is being desecrated. The Ran focuses exclusively on the object\u8217's status; the Rambam focuses on the act of personal betrayal of one\u8217's own oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevuah Actions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now states: {\i shevuah she\u8217'aini yashen, she\u8217'aini medaber, she\u8217'aini mehalech \u8212- assur}. If someone takes a {\i shevuah} that he will not sleep, not speak, or not walk, it is forbidden. This is relatively straightforward, since a {\i shevuah} concerning actions \u8212- committing oneself not to perform a particular act \u8212- is the classic form of a {\i shevuah}. The notable {\i chiddush} was actually in the prior {\i mishnah}, which established that even a {\i konam} formulation applied to abstract actions like sleeping or speaking is binding, albeit only rabbinically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also an important linguistic distinction between the two instruments. A {\i shevuah} is articulated in the negative: {\i she\u8217'aini mehalech} \u8212- \u8220"I will not walk.\u8221" A {\i neder}, by contrast, is articulated in the positive: {\i she\u8217'ani mehalech} \u8212- \u8220"my walking is forbidden,\u8221" rendering the act itself a kind of {\i korban}-like prohibition. Furthermore, as the earlier analysis established, when the {\i neder} concerns an abstract action rather than a concrete physical object, its force is only rabbinic, whereas the {\i shevuah} in the same case operates at the biblical level.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korban Formulations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Lo Ochal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to a series of {\i neder} formulations involving the word {\i korban}, contrasting those that are binding with those that are not. Three formulations are presented as non-binding: {\i korban lo ochel lecha} (\u8220"a {\i korban} is my not-eating of yours\u8221"), {\i ha-korban she-ochel lecha}, and {\i lo korban lo ochel lecha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case is relatively clear. When someone says {\i korban lo ochel lecha}, they are framing the {\i neder} around the non-eating \u8212- essentially saying \u8220"a {\i korban} is my not eating your food.\u8221" But a {\i neder} operates by rendering the object forbidden; the classic formulation is {\i korban she-ochel lecha} \u8212- \u8220"that which I would eat of yours is a {\i korban}.\u8221" Framing the {\i neder} around the negative does not accomplish this. At most it could be read as an attempt to swear in the name of a {\i korban} that one will not eat \u8212- which is {\i lashon shevuah}, not {\i lashon neder} \u8212- and in any case one cannot take a {\i shevuah} in the name of a {\i korban}. So this formulation fails entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ha-Korban Pause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The middle case is the most interesting. {\i Korban she-ochel lecha} \u8212- without the {\i heh} \u8212- would indeed be a binding {\i neder}: \u8220"that which I eat of yours is a {\i korban}.\u8221" But if one says {\i ha-korban she-ochel lecha}, the addition of the {\i heh} followed by a pause transforms the meaning. Rather than using {\i korban} as the predicate describing the object\u8217's status, the speaker is now using {\i ha} as an interjection \u8212- \u8220"Behold! By a {\i korban}, I will eat of yours!\u8221" \u8212- which shifts the formulation into the realm of {\i lashon shevuah}: an oath taken in the name of a {\i korban}. And since one cannot take a {\i shevuah} in the name of a {\i korban}, this formulation also fails to bind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klal Lav Atah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third formulation \u8212- {\i lo korban lo ochel lecha} \u8212- might seem promising. If it is not a {\i korban} that I will not eat, one might reason by {\i miklal lav atah shomea hen} that what I will eat is indeed a {\i korban}. However, the {\i mishnah} does not accept this inference. Our {\i mishnah} follows the view of Rabbi Meir, who does not apply the principle of {\i miklal lav atah shomea hen} in this context. The double negative does not suffice to constitute a positive {\i neder}, and therefore the formulation is {\i mutar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Mani\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mani matnisin?} \u8212- whose view does our {\i mishnah} represent? The answer is Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Yehuda would not distinguish between {\i korban} and {\i ha-korban} at all, because in his view the very word {\i korban} without a prefix such as {\i ke-} does not constitute a valid {\i neder} formulation. Rabbi Yehuda requires {\i ke-korban lo ochel lecha} \u8212- without the {\i kaf}, neither formulation works. The {\i mishnah}, however, implies that {\i korban she-ochel lecha} would be sufficient, which reflects Rabbi Meir\u8217's position that prefixes are not required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the end of the {\i mishnah} and notes an apparent tension. Elsewhere we have learned {\i li-korban lo ochel lecha} \u8212- Rabbi Meir says it is forbidden. This is explained by Rabbi Abba: {\i li-korban} is read as a sentence with a pause \u8212- \u8220"Let it be a {\i korban}\u8221" \u8212- full stop \u8212- \u8220"and therefore I will not eat of yours.\u8221" But if that is so, why does the {\i mishnah} here rule that {\i lo korban lo ochel lecha} is permitted? The {\i gemara} answers: there is no difficulty. When one says {\i li-korban}, the prefix {\i lamed} signals an affirmative commitment \u8212- it should be a {\i korban}, therefore I will not eat. But when one says {\i lo korban}, one is saying it is not a {\i korban} \u8212- and to then infer that what I will eat is a {\i korban} would require applying {\i miklal lav atah shomea hen}, which Rabbi Meir rejects. The two rulings are thus fully consistent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shevuah Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevuah Classic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established which {\i neder} formulations fail, the {\i mishnah} now contrasts these with the parallel {\i shevuah} formulations \u8212- all of which are binding. {\i Shevuah lo ochel lecha} \u8212- a {\i shevuah} that I will not eat of yours \u8212- is the straightforward case, a classic prohibitory oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ha-Shevuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more complex cases are {\i ha-shevuah she-ochel lecha} and {\i lo shevuah lo ochel lecha}, both of which the {\i mishnah} rules are {\i assur}. This is striking because the exact same linguistic structures that rendered the {\i neder} formulations invalid are now said to produce binding obligations. The puzzle is particularly sharp regarding {\i she-ochel lecha} \u8212- \u8220"that I will eat.\u8221" Why should saying one will eat be interpreted as a prohibition against eating? And regarding {\i lo shevuah lo ochel lecha}, even if one reverses the double negative, one arrives at {\i shevuah she-ochel lecha} \u8212- and the same question recurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately seizes on this: {\i miklal d\u8217'ha shevuah she-ochel lecha \u8212- de-lo achilna mashma?} Are we really saying that a {\i shevuah} formulated as \u8220"I will eat\u8221" means one may not eat? The {\i gemara} cites the well-known {\i mishnah} in {\i Shevuot} \u8212- {\i shevuot shtayim she-hein arba} \u8212- where the four categories of {\i shevuah} include both {\i she-ochel} (\u8220"I will eat\u8221") and {\i she-lo ochel} (\u8220"I will not eat\u8221") as distinct and opposite types. Clearly, saying {\i she-ochel} in that context means one is obligating oneself to eat \u8212- not forbidding it. So how can our {\i mishnah} treat {\i shevuah she-ochel lecha} as a prohibition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves this by appealing to context. The words {\i she-ochel} carry two potential meanings depending on the situation in which they are uttered. If people were pressing someone to eat \u8212- insisting again and again \u8212- and he finally relented and said {\i shevuah she-ochel} (\u8220"I swear I will eat\u8221"), then indeed {\i de-achilna mashma} \u8212- it means he is committing to eat. But if the person had been refusing \u8212- {\i lo achilna, lo achilna} \u8212- \u8220"I\u8217'm not eating, I\u8217'm not eating\u8221" \u8212- and then declared {\i shevuah she-ochel lecha}, the phrase functions differently. In that context, {\i she-ochel} is understood as a conditional: \u8220"Should I eat of yours, let me have violated a {\i shevuah}\u8221" \u8212- meaning, were I to eat, I would be transgressing. This is a refusal reinforced by oath, not a commitment to eat. The {\i she-ochel}, in context, means {\i de-lo achilna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The default meaning, as established by the {\i mishnah} in {\i Shevuot}, is that {\i she-ochel} means one will eat. Context of refusal can reverse this, but the reversal is entirely context-dependent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi takes a different approach entirely. Rather than appealing to contextual interpretation, he proposes an emended reading of the {\i mishnah}: the {\i mishnah} does not actually say {\i she-ochel} but rather {\i she-ei ochel} \u8212- with a prolonged {\i alef}, signifying \u8220"that I do not eat.\u8221" This would make the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling entirely straightforward \u8212- {\i shevuah she-ei ochel} is transparently prohibitory \u8212- without requiring any appeal to context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if that is what the {\i mishnah} means, what is the {\i chiddush}? The answer is that one might have thought: perhaps someone who says {\i she-ei ochel} has simply mispronounced {\i she-ochel} \u8212- slipping up \u8212- and should be understood as intending to eat. The {\i gemara} therefore teaches that we do not reinterpret the words on that basis; the unusual phrasing {\i she-ei ochel} is taken at face value and treated as binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Abaye does not accept Rav Ashi\u8217's approach. The {\i gemara} notes, in strikingly unusual language \u8212- {\i Abaye lo amar ta\u8217'am k\u8217'Rav Ashi} \u8212- that Abaye distanced himself from Rav Ashi\u8217's reading. The reason is that Rav Ashi is changing the {\i girsa} of the {\i mishnah}: the {\i mishnah} as transmitted says {\i she-ochel}, and Rav Ashi emends it to {\i she-ei ochel}. Abaye refuses to alter the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi, in turn, does not say like Abaye \u8212- {\i nadi min ta\u8217'am d\u8217'Abaye} \u8212- because he rejects the entire framework of context-dependent meaning. His objection is principled: if {\i she-ochel} can mean \u8220"I will not eat\u8221" depending on context, then equally {\i she-lo ochel} could mean \u8220"I will eat\u8221" in the right circumstances. One could imagine a scenario where someone is rhetorically asked, \u8220"Should you not eat?\u8221" and responds {\i shevuah she-lo ochel} \u8212- meaning, were I not to eat I would have violated a {\i shevuah}, thus implying he must eat. If every formulation is context-dependent, nothing means anything with certainty, and the {\i mishnah} cannot be teaching reliable rules. The {\i mishnah} must therefore be taken to establish fixed, context-independent meanings: {\i she-ochel} means \u8220"I will eat\u8221" and {\i she-lo ochel} means \u8220"I will not eat.\u8221" Abaye\u8217's contextual approach, however sensible in theory, cannot serve as the basis for a {\i mishnah} whose purpose is to teach definitive rules, since a teacher cannot transmit context along with a ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further difficulty with the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling remains: why is {\i lo shevuah lo ochel lecha} binding? Even granting that one reverses the double negative to derive {\i shevuah she-ochel lecha}, we are back at the question of why that formulation is prohibited. Tosafot addresses this with a distinct argument: the binding force of {\i lo shevuah lo ochel lecha} rests not on logical inference but on a rabbinic {\i gezeira}. The concern is that a person might accidentally drop the first {\i lo}, transforming {\i lo shevuah lo ochel lecha} into {\i shevuah she-lo ochel} \u8212- a classically binding prohibitory oath. To prevent this slippage, the rabbis treated even the full {\i lo shevuah lo ochel lecha} as binding. This stands in contrast to the {\i neder} parallel \u8212- {\i lo korban lo ochel lecha} \u8212- where even if one drops the first {\i lo} and arrives at {\i korban lo ochel lecha}, that formulation is also non-binding. Hence there was no reason to decree stringency in the {\i neder} case, but there was such a reason in the {\i shevuah} case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shevuah Stricter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeh Chomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} summarizes: {\i zeh chomer b\u8217'shevuot mib\u8217'nedarim} \u8212- this is the stringency of {\i shevuah} over {\i neder}. The cases just discussed \u8212- where the same linguistic formulations are binding when expressed as a {\i shevuah} but not when expressed as a {\i neder} \u8212- illustrate one dimension of this stringency. But there is also an earlier and more fundamental contrast. When the {\i mishnah} earlier taught that {\i shevuah she\u8217'aini yashen, she\u8217'aini medaber, she\u8217'aini mehalech \u8212- assur}, this too reflects a stringency of {\i shevuah} over {\i neder} \u8212- not because a {\i neder} on such matters fails entirely (for {\i konam she\u8217'ani mehalech} is indeed binding), but because the {\i shevuah} is biblically binding, whereas the {\i neder} on abstract actions \u8212- on walking, speaking, sleeping, things that are not physical objects \u8212- is only rabbinically binding. Biblical versus rabbinic: that is the weight of the contrast in the earlier formulations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder Stricter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzva Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having shown how a {\i shevuah} is stricter than a {\i neder} in certain respects, the {\i mishnah} now turns to the reverse: {\i v\u8217'chomer b\u8217'nedarim mib\u8217'shevuot}. The example given: {\i amar konam sukkah she\u8217'ani oseh, lulav she\u8217'ani notel, tfillin she\u8217'ani maniach} \u8212- one declares the {\i sukkah}, {\i lulav}, or {\i tfillin} as {\i konam} upon him, whether for building, taking, or wearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By a {\i neder}, such a formulation is binding. The person has not directly contradicted his biblical obligation to sit in a {\i sukkah} or take a {\i lulav}. He has instead spoken about the status of the object \u8212- rendering it forbidden to himself. When {\i Sukkot} arrives and he cannot use the object, his failure to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} is a downstream consequence, not a direct countermanding of God\u8217's command.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevuah Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By a {\i shevuah}, however, this does not work \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ein nishba\u8217'in la\u8217'avor al ha-mitzvot}. One cannot take a {\i shevuah} to transgress a {\i mitzvah}. A {\i shevuah} operates in the domain of direct personal obligation: one commits to do or refrain from doing something. To take a {\i shevuah} not to sit in a {\i sukkah} is to directly pit one\u8217's oath against God\u8217's command \u8212- operating in {\i cheftzei Shamayim}, in God\u8217's domain rather than one\u8217's own. Such a {\i shevuah} does not take effect and remains non-binding. The person may sit in the {\i sukkah} without any violation of the oath. However, the act of having taken such a {\i shevuah} is itself a transgression: it constitutes a {\i shevuat shav} \u8212- an oath taken in vain \u8212- for which one receives lashes. The {\i shevuah} was stillborn from the moment of its utterance, but the utterance itself was a biblical violation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shevuah Stricter (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical vs.\u160 ?Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes a possible ambiguity in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrasing {\i zeh chomer b\u8217'shevuot mib\u8217'nedarim}. Does this refer to the cases just discussed regarding {\i korban} formulations, or to the earlier cases about sleeping and walking? The {\i gemara} concludes that it refers to the earlier section \u8212- {\i aseifa d\u8217'eidach baba katani} \u8212- the language about {\i shevuah she\u8217'aini yashen, she\u8217'aini medaber, she\u8217'aini mehalech}. In those cases, both a {\i shevuah} and a {\i neder} are binding, but the {\i shevuah} is weightier: it is a biblical prohibition, whereas the {\i neder} on abstract actions that are not physical objects is only rabbinic. That is the stringency: identical subject matter, but different levels of normative force.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cheftzei Shamayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Gidel Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now takes up the basis for the rule that one cannot take a {\i shevuah} to countermand a {\i mitzvah} obligation. Rav Kahana teaches in the name of Rav Gidel in the name of Rav; and Rav Tavyomi teaches the same in the name of Rav Gidel in the name of Shmuel. The question is: {\i minayin she\u8217'ein nishba\u8217'in la\u8217'avor al ha-mitzvot?} From where do we know this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Yachel Devaro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is {\i lo yachel devaro}. This verse applies both to {\i neder} and to {\i shevuah}, as the full {\i pasuk} reads: {\i ki yidor neder l\u8217'Hashem o hishava shevuah le\u8217'esor issar al nafsho lo yachel devaro} \u8212- whether he vows a {\i neder} to God or swears a {\i shevuah} to bind himself, he shall not desecrate his word. The prohibition of {\i lo yachel devaro} thus governs both instruments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal vs.\u160 ?Divine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} derives from this verse: {\i devaro lo yachel aval meichalu hu l\u8217'cheftzei Shamayim} \u8212- regarding his own matters, he shall not violate his word; but regarding {\i cheftzei Shamayim} \u8212- God\u8217's matters \u8212- it may be violated, because it never took effect. The verse\u8217's application of {\i lo yachel} is to {\i devaro} \u8212- his word, in his domain. When a person attempts to make a binding commitment in God\u8217's domain, the commitment does not attach, and what was never binding can be set aside without transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- {\i cheftzei} as a division between personal and divine domains \u8212- appears elsewhere in halachic literature as well. Regarding {\i Shabbat}, the verse {\i mimtzo cheftzecha v\u8217'daber davar} gives rise to the rule: {\i cheftzecha asurim ve-cheftzei Shamayim mutarim} \u8212- one\u8217's personal affairs are restricted on {\i Shabbat}, while matters of {\i cheftzei Shamayim} are permitted, which is why a {\i shul} may make announcements about communal Torah activities. Similarly, the principle of {\i osek b\u8217'mitzvah patur min ha-mitzvah} flows from {\i beshivtecha b\u8217'veitecha uvelachtecha vaderech} \u8212- {\i beshivtecha didach uvelachtecha didach} \u8212- when you are in your own sphere of action, the obligations of {\i mitzvot} apply; but when already engaged in God\u8217's domain, the calculus shifts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A sharp question then arises. Both {\i neder} and {\i shevuah} appear in the same {\i pasuk} under the same rubric of {\i lo yachel devaro}, and both are said {\i l\u8217'Hashem}: {\i ki yidor neder l\u8217'Hashem} and {\i o hishava shevuah l\u8217'Hashem}. If the {\i l\u8217'Hashem} of the {\i shevuah} signals that one cannot operate in God\u8217's domain and thereby removes the binding force of such a {\i shevuah}, why does the {\i l\u8217'Hashem} of the {\i neder} not produce the same result? Why is a {\i neder} on {\i mitzvah} objects binding while a {\i shevuah} on {\i mitzvah} acts is not?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benefit vs.\u160 ?Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answers that the difference lies not in the wording of the verse but in the inherent structure of the two acts. The critical formulations are: {\i hanat sukkah alai} \u8212- the benefit of the {\i sukkah} is forbidden to me \u8212- versus {\i shevuah she-lo ehena min ha-sukkah} \u8212- a {\i shevuah} that I will not derive benefit from the {\i sukkah}. In the {\i neder} formulation, one is not directly forbidding the act God commanded; one is rendering the object\u8217's benefit forbidden to oneself. When {\i Sukkot} comes, one cannot execute the {\i mitzvah} because the object is unavailable in the relevant sense \u8212- but one has not directly said \u8220"I will not sit in a {\i sukkah}.\u8221" The {\i shevuah}, by contrast, directly targets the act itself and places a personal oath against the fulfillment of a divine command. That is why it fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava accepts the logical structure of Abaye\u8217's distinction but objects to the specific articulation. {\i V\u8217'chi mitzvot lehanot nitnu?} \u8212- Are {\i mitzvot} given in order to derive benefit from them? Performing a {\i mitzvah} is not classified as {\i hana\u8217'ah} in halachic terms. Therefore, saying {\i hanat sukkah alai} \u8212- the benefit of the {\i sukkah} is forbidden to me \u8212- would not prevent one from sitting in the {\i sukkah}, since sitting in a {\i sukkah} to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} does not constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah}. The {\i neder} on {\i hana\u8217'ah} would be ineffective in preventing the {\i mitzvah} in those terms.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hana\u8217'ah Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvot Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle at stake here is {\i mitzvot lav lehanot nitnu} \u8212- {\i mitzvot} are not given for the purpose of deriving pleasure. This is not a sweeping theological statement about our attitude toward the commandments; it is a halachic classification of what counts as {\i hana\u8217'ah}. Halachic {\i hana\u8217'ah} is defined in this-worldly, physical terms. Fulfilling a religious obligation \u8212- accumulating, as it were, merit or spiritual satisfaction \u8212- does not qualify as physical benefit for the purposes of {\i neder} law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knowledge Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel illustration: may a non-{\i kohen} study a cadaver for the purpose of medical and anatomical education? The {\i poskim} generally permit it, despite the general prohibition against deriving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from a corpse. The reason is that intellectual expansion \u8212- gaining knowledge \u8212- is not classified as {\i hana\u8217'ah} in the operative halachic sense, even though from a broader perspective it may represent the highest form of human fulfillment. Similarly, {\i mitzvah} performance is not {\i hana\u8217'ah} in halachic terms, however central it is to Jewish life. This has a further wrinkle in contexts like {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}, where Torah study is prohibited \u8212- but there the reason is {\i simcha}, joy, not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, pleasure, and the distinction between the two is meaningful.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Distinction (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that {\i mitzvot lav lehanot nitnu}, Rava reformulates the operative {\i neder} language. Instead of {\i hanat sukkah alai} \u8212- which would be ineffective because {\i mitzvah} performance is not {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- one must say {\i yeshivat sukkah alai} \u8212- the sitting in this {\i sukkah} is forbidden to me. This targets not the benefit derived from the object but a specific use of it. A {\i neder} can render an object forbidden with respect to particular actions or uses, not only with respect to conventional {\i hana\u8217'ah}. This is an important expansion of the concept of {\i neder}: it can prohibit certain modes of engagement with an object even where no conventional physical benefit is involved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Object Mention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Benefit Actions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran draws out an important implication of this principle. Since a {\i neder} can operate on actions rather than only on {\i hana\u8217'ah}, one can in principle make a {\i neder} on a completely non-benefit action \u8212- his example is {\i konam zerikat tzor le-yam alav} \u8212- declaring this rock as {\i konam} upon him with respect to throwing it into the ocean. Even though there is no {\i hana\u8217'ah} in throwing a pebble into the sea, the {\i neder} is binding: {\i af al pi she\u8217'ein lo bi-zerikah zo hana\u8217'ah, assur le-zorko}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the Ran adds a critical qualification: this works only if one is explicit. {\i Davka bi-meforesh} \u8212- one must specify the forbidden action. If one says {\i konam sukkah alai le-yeshivatah}, or {\i konam zerikat tzor} with the explicit action named, it is binding on that specific use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Default Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if one simply says {\i konam sukkah alai} or {\i konam tzor alai} \u8212- a generic declaration without specifying the forbidden mode of engagement \u8212- the default operative meaning of {\i konam} is {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah}. The person would be forbidden to derive conventional benefit from the object but would be permitted to throw the rock into the ocean or to sit in the {\i sukkah} for {\i mitzvah} purposes (to the extent that is itself problematic). The word {\i konam}, standing alone, defaults to forbidding {\i hana\u8217'ah}. If the intention is to forbid something other than {\i hana\u8217'ah}, it must be made explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers a different position on a related question. The Ran holds that saying {\i yeshivat sukkah alai} \u8212- \u8220"the sitting in a {\i sukkah} is upon me as forbidden\u8221" \u8212- without mentioning the {\i sukkah} as a concrete object might be only a rabbinic {\i neder}, since the thing being forbidden is a {\i davar she\u8217'ein bo mamash} \u8212- an action without physical substance. Tosafot disagrees. In their view, the {\i neder} becomes fully binding, and is not classified as a {\i davar she\u8217'ein bo mamash}, as long as one mentions the name of some object in the formulation. Since {\i yeshivat sukkah alai} includes the word {\i sukkah}, even though the explicit subject is the act of sitting rather than the object itself, the mention of the object is sufficient to give the {\i neder} the status of one bearing on a physical thing, and it is therefore binding at the biblical level.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 17\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder vs.\u160 ?Shevua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Prior Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens a new {\i mishna}, continuing the trend of the preceding pages in moving from technical language distinctions toward more fundamental conceptual issues. By way of review: earlier {\i mishnayot} had drawn a distinction between {\i shevua} and {\i neder} in terms of language, and then moved to discuss the ways in which {\i nedarim} are more stringent, or at least broader in their effect, than {\i shevuot}. The key point established there was that a {\i neder} can indirectly prevent the performance of a {\i mitzvah} by rendering an object forbidden \u8212- since the {\i neder} operates on the object itself \u8212- whereas a {\i shevua} directed against one\u8217's own obligation would not take effect, given that {\i mitzvot} are not considered a halakhic form of {\i hana\u8217'ah}. This also generated an important discussion about the scope of what a {\i neder} can prohibit: whether it is limited to the domain of eating and benefit, or whether it can extend to other types of interactions with an object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language & Effect Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Central to those earlier discussions was the structural distinction between {\i neder} and {\i shevua}: a {\i neder} operates on an object, changing its status relative to the person; a {\i shevua} operates on the person, generating a personal obligation. The {\i neder}\u8217's capacity to act on objects gives it a wider potential reach \u8212- it can, at least in principle, touch areas beyond benefit and eating \u8212- whereas the {\i shevua}\u8217's direct confrontation with a pre-existing personal obligation creates its own set of limitations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Mishna Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} on daf 17 now introduces a further dimension of this {\i neder}-{\i shevua} distinction, one connected, though perhaps less directly, to the object-versus-person dichotomy: {\i yesh neder betoch neder ve\u8217'ein shevua betoch shevua} \u8212- one may make a {\i neder} within, or on top of, another {\i neder}, but one may not make a {\i shevua} on top of another {\i shevua}. The discussion that follows unpacks precisely what this asymmetry means and why it exists.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Status Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir as Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's immediate example of a {\i neder} is striking: it chooses {\i nezirut}. This is not incidental. The Torah itself uses the word {\i neder} in connection with {\i nazir} \u8212- {\i ish ki yafli lindor neder nazir} \u8212- using the term twice, signaling that {\i nezirut} is a species of {\i neder}. More importantly, {\i nezirut} illustrates something essential about the nature of a {\i neder}: it is a speech act that changes a {\i status}. Rather than rendering an external object forbidden, the declaration of {\i nezirut} imparts upon the person himself the status of being a {\i nazir}. This, like other {\i nedarim}, is about using speech to effect a halakhic transformation \u8212- here applied to the person rather than to an object, but still operating through the logic of status-imposition. As will become clear, {\i nezirut} turns out to be the only form of {\i neder} that fits the {\i mishna}\u8217's case, and the reason for this will emerge from the analysis below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents the case as follows: a person says {\i harei ani nazir im ochal} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if I eat\u8221" \u8212- and then immediately repeats the same declaration: {\i harei ani nazir im ochal}. He then eats. The {\i mishna} rules {\i chayav al kol achat ve\u8217'achat} \u8212- he is obligated for each one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hovering Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A simple reading might suggest he becomes a double {\i nazir} \u8212- requiring two sets of {\i korbanot} at the end of thirty days, or receiving two sets of lashes for willful violation. But the {\i Gemara} does not read it that way. Rather, the {\i Gemara} understands the ruling to mean that after the first {\i nezirut} concludes, the second {\i nezirut} then takes effect. The result is a total of sixty days, structured as two successive periods of thirty: he completes the first, brings its {\i korbanot}, finishes that {\i nezirut}, and then begins and completes the second. The second {\i nezirut} was, during the first, in a kind of suspended state \u8212- hovering, waiting to take effect once the first status was removed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Overlay Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This hovering phenomenon emerges from a fundamental principle that applies equally to {\i neder} and {\i shevua}: a second prohibition, status, or obligation cannot take effect on top of an existing one. The principle is {\i ein issur chal al issur} \u8212- one prohibition cannot take effect on top of another. Applied to {\i neder}, it becomes {\i ein neder chal al neder}; applied to {\i shevua}, {\i ein shevua chala al shevua}. What distinguishes {\i nezirut} is that the underlying status is intrinsically time-bound and terminal: it will end. Because it ends, the second {\i nezirut} does not simply disappear \u8212- it remains in suspension and takes effect the moment the first status is removed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shevua Overlay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Shevua Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel {\i shevua} case in the {\i mishna} is this: a person declares {\i shevua shelo ochal} \u8212- a {\i shevua} that he will not eat \u8212- and then immediately declares the same {\i shevua} again. He eats. The {\i mishna} rules he is {\i eino chayav ela achat} \u8212- liable only once. The second {\i shevua} simply did not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mushba Ve\u8217'omed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason invoked is the principle of {\i mushba ve\u8217'omed}: the person is already bound \u8212- either from {\i har Sinai} in the case of standing obligations, or from his first {\i shevua} in this case. A {\i shevua} cannot create an obligation on top of a pre-existing one. Since the person is already obligated not to eat this bread, the second {\i shevua} adding the same obligation is without effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevua Concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle deserves careful examination, because it is less self-evident than it might appear. One must distinguish it from the case of a {\i shevua} that countermands an existing obligation \u8212- for example, a {\i shevua} that one will not sit in a {\i sukkah}. That would be a {\i shevuat shav}, a vain oath, and it would be a transgression at the moment of utterance, but it would not itself become a binding obligation. But a {\i shevua} to perform something one is already obligated in is a different matter. Why should it not take effect? Much depends on how one conceptualizes the nature of a {\i shevua}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two ways to think about a {\i shevua} directed at the future. One view is that by swearing in God\u8217's name that one will do something, one has not so much created an abstract obligation as committed to a true or false statement about the future: if one does not do the thing, one will have sworn falsely, having invoked God\u8217's name for a declaration that turned out untrue. Under that framing, the question of whether the person was already obligated is somewhat beside the point \u8212- the issue is whether the oath was ultimately honored or not. If a person swears to put on {\i tefillin} and fails to do so, he has sworn falsely regardless of whether he was already biblically obligated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the rule of {\i ein shevua chala al shevua} implies a different conceptualization: the {\i shevua} is understood primarily as generating an obligation. The oath creates a halakhic duty. Under that model, one cannot generate an obligation on top of an already-existing obligation, because there is nothing new to obligate. I am already obligated \u8212- what does it mean to obligate myself a second time? This is the framework in which the rule makes internal sense, and it is a meaningful {\i chiddush}, because it moves the analysis away from the question of true-and-false swearing and toward the language of obligation and its creation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevua on Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This tension surfaced earlier in the {\i Gemara} \u8212- on daf 8 \u8212- in the famous passage asking {\i minayin shenishba\u8217'in lekayem es hamitzvah}: how do we know one may swear to fulfill a {\i mitzvah}? The verse cited is {\i nishbati va\u8217'akayema lishmor mishpetei tzidkecha}. The {\i Gemara} there raised the objection: {\i vehalo mushba ve\u8217'omed mehar Sinai hu} \u8212- is he not already bound from {\i Sinai}? The resolution was {\i desharei lei le\u8217'inish lezarzuzei nafshei} \u8212- it is permitted for a person to do this in order to encourage and motivate himself. This implies that such an oath is not technically binding in a full halakhic sense, but is permitted as an act of self-encouragement, perhaps operative {\i miderabbanan} or in some looser form. The underlying analysis is precisely the point made above: if a {\i shevua} generates an obligation, you cannot generate what already exists; what is left is only a gesture of personal reinforcement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status vs.\u160 ?Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Structures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structural parallelism between the two principles can now be stated clearly: {\i ein neder chal al neder} and {\i ein shevua chala al shevua} are formally parallel, but they derive from different conceptual foundations. The {\i neder} operates by imposing a status of prohibition on an object; the {\i shevua} operates by generating a personal obligation. In both cases, the rule is that you cannot impose on what already exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic Constraints\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a significant tension in the {\i neder} case that demands attention. The general principle {\i ein issur chal al issur} already applies to objects independently of any speech act: if something is already forbidden as, say, {\i neveilah}, does it then also become forbidden as {\i basar bechalav}? Perhaps not \u8212- because the object already carries a forbidden status, and one cannot layer a second forbidden status on top of the first. This is, admittedly, conceptually puzzling on its face, since one might ask why an object cannot carry multiple prohibitions simultaneously. But this principle exists and is operative in halakha. Given that, one might expect a {\i neder} \u8212- which also operates on an object \u8212- to face the same bar. And indeed it does. The crucial clarification is this: a standard {\i konam} declaration \u8212- {\i konam kikar zeh alai}, repeated twice \u8212- would be entirely ineffective the second time. The second {\i neder} on a normal forbidden object means nothing; {\i ein issur chal al issur}. It is only {\i nezirut} that behaves differently, for the reasons now to be examined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel question arises for {\i shevua}: if we understand a {\i shevua} as generating an obligation, then {\i ein shevua chala al shevua} follows directly. But if we instead emphasize the dimension of true-and-false swearing, the logic is less obvious, and the rule becomes a genuine {\i chiddush}. The fact that the rule exists at all thus tells us something important about how the {\i Gemara} conceptualizes the {\i shevua}: primarily as obligation-generating rather than primarily as a truth-commitment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resulting picture is this: by {\i shevua}, we focus on creating an obligation, and we say one obligation cannot take effect on top of another. By {\i neder}, we focus on creating a status, and we say one status cannot take effect on top of another. What we are left with as the genuinely distinctive case is {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirut Termination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nezirut} is intrinsically a bounded, terminal unit. Unlike a vague or indefinite prohibition, {\i nezirut} is structured: it is a defined period \u8212- thirty days by default \u8212- with a clear endpoint marked by the bringing of {\i korbanot}. When a person attempts to accept two {\i neziruyot}, the first is seen as a time-bound, temporary status; the second remains in suspension, hovering, ready to take effect the moment the first concludes. This hovering is the genuine {\i chiddush} of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises: why is hovering not available for a {\i shevua} or a standard {\i neder}? Consider a {\i shevua} not to eat a loaf of bread today: could a second identical {\i shevua} not simply hover and take effect the next day when the first one has run its course? The problem is that if the {\i shevua} is temporally defined \u8212- \u8220"not today\u8221" \u8212- then when today ends, there is no longer anything to hover over. And if the second {\i shevua} is open-ended \u8212- \u8220"not to eat this bread\u8221" \u8212- then one might wonder whether hovering could apply. But ultimately, the conceptual difference is that {\i nezirut} is a self-contained temporal unit, intrinsically designed to end and restart, which is precisely what makes the hovering model natural. For {\i shevua}, focused on personal obligation, there is no comparable termination-and-restart structure. The hovering model, then, is a {\i chiddush} peculiar to {\i nezirut} \u8212- and the interesting observation is that even a standard {\i neder} on an object does not share it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Huna\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinction Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna} addresses the question of precisely when the second {\i nezirut} can take effect. His answer: {\i lo shanu ela de\u8217'amar hareni nazir hayom hareni nazir lemakhar} \u8212- the rule of {\i yesh neder betoch neder} applies only when the person said \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} today\u8221" and then \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} starting tomorrow.\u8221" What is required is that the two {\i neziruyot} not be completely coextensive \u8212- that there be some temporal distinction between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extra Day Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\i demigo deka mitosef yoma yetira} \u8212- since the second declaration adds an extra day beyond the first, the two {\i neziruyot} are not identical. The second will end one day later than the first. Because of this non-overlap, it takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two ways to understand what {\i Rav Huna} requires. The first and perhaps more straightforward reading is that what matters is simply that the two {\i neziruyot} be conceptually distinct from one another. If you say the same thing twice, you have not introduced a second, hoverable {\i nezirut} \u8212- you have merely repeated yourself. But if you say something different, even slightly, you have designated a distinct second {\i nezirut}, and that distinctness is sufficient for it to be recognized as hovering. Under this reading, {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's requirement is about the clarity of the speaker\u8217's intent to create something new.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} offers a different understanding. He argues that the issue is not merely conceptual distinctness but actual effect: because the second {\i nezirut} begins one day later than the first, there is one day \u8212- day thirty-one \u8212- on which the second {\i nezirut} exists entirely by itself, with no first {\i nezirut} obstructing it. On that day, the second {\i nezirut} actually takes effect. And since the principle of {\i ein neziros pachot mishloshim yom} \u8212- {\i nezirut} cannot be for fewer than thirty days \u8212- once it takes effect for that one day, it retroactively obligates thirty days from that point. The extra day is therefore not just a signal of distinctness; it is the mechanism by which the second {\i nezirut} actually gets purchase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction between the two readings has practical consequences. Consider a person who accepts, say, a sixty-day {\i nezirut} and then, a week later, accepts an additional thirty-day {\i nezirut}. The two are clearly conceptually distinct \u8212- different durations, accepted at different moments. Yet according to the {\i Ran}\u8217's reading, the second may never actually take hold, because there is no day on which it exists by itself, free of the first. On the simple reading, by contrast, their conceptual distinctness should be sufficient for the second to hover and await the conclusion of the first.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel} takes a broader position: {\i afilu amar hareni nazir hayom hareni nazir hayom} \u8212- even if the person said \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} today\u8221" and then immediately said \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} today\u8221" again, using an identical declaration with no temporal shift, {\i chala neziros aleha} \u8212- the second {\i nezirut} takes effect and hovers, waiting until the first concludes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presses {\i Rav Huna} from the {\i mishna} itself: if {\i Rav Huna} holds that only a shifted second {\i nezirut} takes effect, the {\i mishna} should have made this qualification explicit. It should have distinguished between {\i hareni nazir hayom hareni nazir lemakhar}, which would constitute {\i yesh neder betoch neder}, and {\i hareni nazir hayom hareni nazir hayom}, which would not. The {\i mishna}\u8217's blanket statement \u8212- {\i yesh neder betoch neder} \u8212- without any such qualification suggests that even a completely identical repetition is sufficient, as {\i Shmuel} holds. The {\i Gemara} acknowledges this as a difficulty for {\i Rav Huna}: {\i kasha le\u8217'Rav Huna}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shvuot: Figs Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Figs-Grapes Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the parallel {\i shevua} case and asks a sharper question. According to {\i Rav Huna}, the key feature that allows {\i nezirut} to be doubled up is that the two {\i neziruyot} are not completely coextensive \u8212- the second adds an extra day. The analogous case for {\i shevua} would then be: a person says a {\i shevua} that he will not eat figs, and then says a {\i shevua} that he will not eat figs and grapes. Here, too, there is a portion of the second {\i shevua} \u8212- the prohibition on grapes \u8212- that does not overlap with the first. Why, then, should the second {\i shevua} not take effect, at least with respect to the grapes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At first glance, this might seem like an apples-and-oranges comparison. The {\i nezirut} case involves two {\i neziruyot} that are largely coextensive but for one additional day; the {\i shevua} case involves a prohibition on figs and an additional prohibition on an entirely different item. But the {\i Ran} argues that the {\i Gemara} is drawing a more precise analogy. The question is whether having a non-overlapping component is sufficient to allow the second item to take effect. On the {\i Ran}\u8217's reading, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's logic is: just as in {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's view of {\i nezirut}, the extra day \u8212- the non-overlapping portion \u8212- is what grants the second {\i nezirut} its purchase, so too, the grapes \u8212- the non-overlapping portion of the second {\i shevua} \u8212- should grant the second {\i shevua} its purchase. And since we say it does not take effect, there must be a reason it fails even when there is non-overlap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i shevua}, it should be understood precisely: the {\i Ran} reads it not as a {\i shevua} not to eat figs, combined with an independent {\i shevua} not to eat grapes, but as a single {\i shevua} not to eat figs and grapes together \u8212- i.e., in the same act or combination. Under that reading, the question becomes: can one be foresworn against eating figs-and-grapes-together when one is already foresworn against eating figs? One might say yes, since eating figs-and-grapes is conceptually a distinct act \u8212- and someone who violates the first {\i shevua} by eating figs would, upon then eating grapes, have also transgressed the second. Alternatively, one might say no: since one is already prohibited from eating figs, a prohibition against eating figs-and-grapes is without practical meaning, because one can never reach the state of eating figs-and-grapes while keeping {\i shevua} number one. The second {\i shevua} is effectively rendered redundant by the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} weighs in with a striking example that reveals his position. He posits: if a person took a {\i shevua} not to eat figs, then separately took a {\i shevua} not to eat figs and grapes, and then ate figs \u8212- thereby violating {\i shevua} number one \u8212- and then separated a {\i korban} for {\i shevua} number one, and thereafter ate grapes, what is the status of that grape-eating relative to {\i shevua} number two? {\i Rava} rules: {\i havei lei anavim chatzi shiur} \u8212- the grapes alone constitute only half a transgression of {\i shevua} number two. And since {\i ein mevi\u8217'in korban al chatzi shiur} \u8212- one does not bring a {\i korban} for half a transgression \u8212- no {\i korban} is owed at that point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication of {\i Rava}\u8217's ruling is critical. By characterizing the grapes as {\i chatzi shiur} relative to {\i shevua} number two, {\i Rava} is implicitly affirming that {\i shevua} number two is operative \u8212- it took effect. Were it not in force, there would be no {\i shevua} number two to measure the grapes against. What the {\i korban}-separation does is segregate the fig-eating as a distinct, concluded act; now, viewing the grape-eating in isolation, it is only half of what {\i shevua} number two prohibits. But had the person eaten the figs and then, without separating a {\i korban}, immediately eaten the grapes, {\i Rava}\u8217's logic implies he would have transgressed {\i shevua} number two as well \u8212- eating figs-and-grapes, the full prohibited combination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a direct challenge to the view that the second {\i shevua} in the figs-and-grapes case simply does not take effect. If {\i Rava} is correct, it does take effect. And if that is so, why does the {\i Gemara} present this as a case where the second {\i shevua} fails, in contrast to {\i nezirut}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Huna vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disagreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the tension directly: {\i Rav Huna lo sevira lei ke\u8217'Rava} \u8212- {\i Rav Huna} does not hold like {\i Rava}. The two {\i amora\u8217'im} disagree on the underlying question of whether the figs-and-grapes {\i shevua} takes effect at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redundancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's position is that the second {\i shevua} is entirely without effect. The reasoning: since the person is already prohibited from eating figs by virtue of {\i shevua} number one, a prohibition against eating figs-and-grapes is meaningless. If one keeps {\i shevua} number one, one will never eat figs, and therefore will never be in a position to eat figs-and-grapes. The second {\i shevua} adds nothing to one\u8217's real-world behavior. It is not merely overlapping in some portion; it is substantively redundant, because the very act it prohibits \u8212- the combination \u8212- is already foreclosed by the first prohibition. This is the conceptual difference between {\i shevua} and {\i nezirut}: {\i nezirut} imposes a status, and a second status waiting to replace the first as it terminates has a clear identity as something distinct. But a {\i shevua} concerns what one will and will not do, and if you already cannot do something because of a prior {\i shevua}, it makes no halakhic sense to pile on an obligation that can never be independently triggered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinctness Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava}, by contrast, holds that as long as the second {\i shevua} is not the exact same thing as the first \u8212- as long as it has some distinct content \u8212- it takes effect. The grapes are something beyond what the first {\i shevua} covered, and that distinctness is sufficient. Under {\i Rava}\u8217's view, the model works similarly to how {\i Rav Huna} himself applied it to {\i nezirut}: a non-overlapping element gives the second item a foothold, and once it takes hold in that area, it is effective across its full scope. {\i Rava} does not apply a separate conceptual framework for {\i shevua} versus {\i nezirut} \u8212- the distinctness principle operates the same way for both.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two at Once\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a further case: {\i mi shenazar shtei nezirus} \u8212- someone who accepts two {\i neziruyot} in a single statement, declaring both at once rather than sequentially. This case will bear directly on the question of what {\i Rav Huna} truly requires and where his position finds its limit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hataras Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario is as follows. Someone accepts two {\i neziruyot}. He counts through the first thirty days, and then, having completed the period and separated a {\i korban}, he goes to a {\i chacham} and performs {\i hataras nedarim} \u8212- the process by which a {\i chacham} retroactively dissolves a {\i neder} upon finding grounds for regret ({\i charata}). The possibility of regret even after the fact, even after one has lived through a {\i nezirut} and separated a {\i korban}, is presumably available \u8212- the {\i chacham} can inquire whether, had the person known how difficult it would be, he ever would have accepted it. Whatever the specific mechanism, the {\i chacham} dissolves the first {\i nezirut} retroactively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling: {\i alsa lo shniya barishona} \u8212- the second {\i nezirut} is deemed to have been fulfilled during those last thirty days. Retroactively, the person was not observing {\i nezirut} number one during that period \u8212- the {\i hatara} has eliminated it entirely, looking backward. But {\i nezirut} number two had been hovering throughout. When we now look back at those thirty days, we realize that {\i nezirut} one did not exist; consequently, {\i nezirut} two took effect during that time. The person has, retroactively, fulfilled {\i nezirut} number two. The {\i korban} he separated can be applied accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling poses a challenge to {\i Rav Huna}: the case as described says the person took two {\i neziruyot} \u8212- it does not specify that he said {\i hayom} and {\i lemakhar}, that is, that the two were temporally offset. If it was simply a double acceptance of identical {\i neziruyot}, {\i Rav Huna} would say the second one never took effect, and there should be nothing to retroactively credit. The {\i Gemara} therefore defends {\i Rav Huna} by reading the case as one where he had indeed said {\i hareni nazir hayom hareni nazir lemakhar} \u8212- with the required temporal offset. That would mean the second {\i nezirut} did hover and wait; but when the {\i hatara} retroactively eliminates the first, the second now accounts for only twenty-nine of the thirty days, one day remaining. Or, alternatively, the {\i Gemara} will propose a different reading of the case entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two at Once (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then introduces the case of {\i kibel shtei nezirus be\u8217'vas achas} \u8212- the person accepted two {\i neziruyot} simultaneously, in a single statement, explicitly designating two distinct {\i neziruyot} together. The {\i Gemara} indicates that even {\i Rav Huna} would agree in this case: the second {\i nezirut} hovers and, upon completion of the first, kicks in, even though the two are entirely coextensive in time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a crucial clarification of {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's position. His requirement of temporal offset \u8212- {\i hayom} versus {\i lemakhar} \u8212- is not a reflection of some conceptual difficulty with two coinciding {\i neziruyot} as such. {\i Rav Huna} has no objection to the model of complete overlap, where the second {\i nezirut} coincides entirely with the first and simply waits. What he objects to is creating that model through sequential, identical declarations. When someone says {\i hareni nazir hayom} and then, with the first {\i nezirut} already in place, repeats {\i hareni nazir hayom}, the second statement is simply a repetition \u8212- it is not doing anything. It does not introduce a second {\i nezirut}; it merely restates the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequential vs.\u160 ?Simultane\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction {\i Rav Huna} draws is therefore not about conceptual structure but about the mechanics of halakhic speech acts. A simultaneous declaration \u8212- \u8220"I accept two {\i neziruyot} beginning today\u8221" \u8212- is unambiguous: both are accepted at once, one takes effect, the other hovers. But a sequential declaration \u8212- {\i hareni nazir hayom}, and then later {\i hareni nazir hayom} \u8212- is a problem not because of any impossibility of overlapping {\i neziruyot} but because the second utterance, made after the first has already taken hold, is indistinguishable from mere repetition. If someone wanted to create a hovering second {\i nezirut} through sequential speech, he would need to say something meaningfully different \u8212- such as {\i hareni nazir lemakhar} \u8212- to signal that he is designating a distinct, second {\i nezirut} and not simply echoing the first.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hataras Nedarim (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} offers an alternative resolution to the challenge posed by the {\i hataras nedarim} case that does not require reading the case as one where {\i hayom} and {\i lemakhar} were explicitly specified. His argument is this: even if the two {\i neziruyot} were stated in identical terms \u8212- {\i hareni nazir hayom hareni nazir hayom} \u8212- and even according to {\i Rav Huna}, the retroactive voiding of the first {\i nezirut} through {\i hatara} is sufficient to make the second statement meaningful in hindsight. At the time of the second declaration, it appeared to be a meaningless repetition \u8212- the first {\i nezirut} was already in place, and the second seemed to add nothing. {\i Rav Huna} would indeed have said at that moment that it took no effect. But when the {\i chacham} subsequently performs {\i hatara} and retroactively eliminates the first {\i nezirut}, we now look back and see that the first statement was, in hindsight, void. With the first statement retroactively void, the second statement \u8212- which at the time seemed redundant \u8212- is now seen to have been meaningful after all: there was no first {\i nezirut} to block it, and so it took effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the force of the {\i Ran}\u8217's approach: retroactive voiding ({\i lemafrei\u8217'a}) can transform the second statement from apparent superfluity to actual efficacy, because it changes the retrospective reality against which that statement is evaluated. {\i Rav Huna} need not concede that sequential identical {\i nezirut}-declarations work in the ordinary case; he can maintain his position while still accounting for the {\i hataras nedarim} ruling. One might raise the objection that if the second statement was not meaningful at the time it was uttered, it cannot retroactively become meaningful. This is a genuine difficulty, and it is the question with which the discussion concludes \u8212- a fascinating debate whose full resolution remains open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 18\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder Betoch Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at 18a, approximately four lines down, with {\i mative rav hamnuna} \u8212- a challenge posed by Rav Hamnuna. We are in the midst of the debate between Rav Huna and Shmuel concerning what the {\i mishnah} means when it says {\i yesh neder betoch neder} \u8212- that there is a neder within a neder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The classic illustration involves the acceptance of {\i nezirut}. If someone says {\i hareini nazir hayom} and then says {\i hareini nazir lemachar}, Rav Huna requires that the two {\i neziruyot} not be identical \u8212- that there be some non-overlapping period. The Ran explains that when one day does not overlap between the two acceptances, that day \u8212- day thirty-one \u8212- causes an entirely new thirty-day {\i nezirut} to take effect. That is Rav Huna\u8217's position. Shmuel, reading the {\i mishnah} in its simpler sense, holds that even if the second acceptance is word-for-word identical to the first \u8212- {\i hareini nazir hareini nazir} \u8212- it nonetheless takes effect: not immediately, but held in abeyance until the first {\i nezirut} concludes, at which point the second kicks in automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerged in the prior session is that even Rav Huna would concede that if someone accepted both {\i neziruyot} simultaneously \u8212- saying, for example, {\i harei alai shtei nezirut} \u8212- the phenomenon of one taking effect while the other hovers in abeyance is valid. His objection applies specifically to the sequential case, where one {\i nezirut} is already in force: attempting to accept the identical state upon oneself after it is already operative, he maintains, is a meaningless act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the Ran actually presents two distinct ways of reading the entire {\i sugya}, and we have been following only one of them. The approach we have been working with understands {\i neder betoch neder} as the second {\i nezirut} being suspended and held in abeyance, to take effect when the first concludes. This explains naturally why the phenomenon is unique to {\i nezirut}: only {\i nezirut} has a finite, time-bound character, which creates conceptual space for the second to hover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the Ran also acknowledges a quite different reading: that {\i yesh neder betoch neder} means both {\i neziruyot} take effect simultaneously, making one a double {\i nazir}. Under this reading, if someone were to drink wine, he would incur two sets of lashes. The Ran does not favor this reading, but he recognizes it as a legitimate interpretation of the {\i Gemara}. More significantly, under this reading Rav Huna\u8217's insistence that the two acceptances differ makes excellent sense, because if they are absolutely identical, the principle of {\i ein issur chal al issur} \u8212- one prohibition cannot take effect upon another \u8212- would prevent the second from having any force. If, however, they differ slightly, the second qualifies as an {\i issur mosif} or {\i issur kolel}, and that distinction permits one to take effect upon the other within the {\i Gemara}\u8217's framework for {\i ein issur chal al issur}. Crucially, this second reading would not confine the phenomenon to {\i nezirut} at all. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's use of {\i nezirut} would be merely illustrative, and in principle one could say {\i konam kikar zeh alai konam kikar zeh alai} and create two stacked prohibitions on that loaf of bread, with the person incurring two sets of lashes for eating it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These are genuinely two very different frameworks for understanding the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Verse Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} says {\i yesh neder betoch neder} but goes directly to the case of {\i nezirut} without explaining what makes {\i nezirut} distinctive. Once we operate within the hovering framework \u8212- that the second {\i nezirut} waits and takes effect when the first ends \u8212- we understand the uniqueness of {\i nezirut}. But the question arises: why does a {\i pasuk} need to teach us this? The {\i Gemara} asks {\i heichi dami} \u8212- precisely which case requires the scriptural derivation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qal VaChomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the need for a {\i pasuk}, the {\i Gemara} first reconstructs the {\i qal vachomer} one might have made in the opposite direction. The {\i Gemara} states: {\i ma shvua chamura, she\u8217'ein shvua chala al shvua} \u8212- a {\i shvua} is weightier, yet a {\i shvua} cannot take effect upon a {\i shvua}. If you take an oath not to eat a loaf of bread and then take a second oath to the same effect, the second one does not take effect, because you are already {\i mushba v\u8217'omed} \u8212- already obligated \u8212- and you cannot obligate yourself anew in the same matter. Therefore, reasons the {\i qal vachomer}, {\i nezirut kala lo kol sheken} \u8212- how much more so should the lighter category of {\i nezirut} be unable to double up?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then states {\i talmud lomar nazir l\u8217'hazir} \u8212- the doubled language in the verse teaches that {\i nezirut chala al nezirut}, that {\i nezirut} does indeed stack. So the {\i pasuk} overrides what the {\i qal vachomer} would have predicted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case requiring that override? If it is Rav Huna\u8217's case \u8212- {\i hareini nazir hayom hareini nazir lemachar} \u8212- why would you even need a {\i pasuk}? Since there is a day of non-overlap, it is perfectly obvious that the second should take effect; no {\i pasuk} is needed. The {\i Gemara} therefore concludes: {\i ela lav d\u8217'amar hareini nazir hayom hareini nazir hayom} \u8212- it must be the case where someone said the identical thing twice, where the second acceptance completely coincides with the first. And the {\i Gemara} notes that since the {\i mishnah} teaches {\i nezirut chala al nezirut} \u8212- that even this works \u8212- it implies we are dealing with the non-obvious case. This seems to be a strong proof against Rav Huna.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan k\u8217'gon shekibel alav shtei neziruyot b\u8217'vat achat} \u8212- here we are dealing with a case where the person accepted two {\i neziruyot} at once. That too is not obvious: since both are accepted for the same period beginning today, the second has no time in which it alone is operative. It is not obvious that the second one should be meaningful. Yet the {\i pasuk} teaches that it is \u8212- that the second hovers and will take effect when the first concludes. Crucially, Rav Huna agrees to this case. His objection is specifically to the sequential case where one is already in force when the attempt is made to add the second. When they are created together as a package, even he accepts the hovering phenomenon. And it is precisely that non-obvious case \u8212- {\i bat achat} \u8212- that requires a {\i pasuk}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weight of Shvua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i umai chumra d\u8217'shvua} \u8212- in what sense is a {\i shvua} said to be weightier, such that the {\i qal vachomer} runs from {\i nezirut} (the {\i kal}) to {\i shvua} (the {\i chamur})? The {\i Gemara} first raises a possible answer and immediately complicates it: perhaps {\i shvua} is weightier because it takes effect even on matters that are non-physical \u8212- it obligates you to perform an action rather than conferring status on an object. But then {\i neder nami chamir} \u8212- a {\i neder} has its own weightiness: {\i sheken chal al mitzva k\u8217'reshut} \u8212- it can take effect on {\i mitzvah} objects and prevent you from performing a {\i mitzvah}. For instance, one can forbid all {\i sukkot} or all {\i tefillin} upon oneself, which a {\i shvua} cannot do, because a {\i shvua} that directly contradicts a Torah obligation is invalid, whereas a {\i neder} accomplishes the same end-result through a different mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since each has an advantage over the other, the {\i Gemara} cannot identify a clean technical superiority. Instead, it concludes: {\i ela mishum d\u8217'chtiv ba bishvua lo yenakeh} \u8212- the reason {\i shvua} is considered weightier is that the Torah writes regarding it {\i ki lo yenakeh Hashem et asher yisa shemo lashav} \u8212- God will not cleanse one who takes His name in vain. This is not a technical distinction about the formal scope of each instrument; it is a statement about weight and gravity. A {\i shvua} invokes the divine name, and its violation carries a dimension of offense against God that a {\i neder} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Lo Yenakeh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a point worth flagging here, which Tosafot addresses. The {\i pasuk} of {\i lo yenakeh} is written in the context of {\i shvuat sheker} \u8212- a false oath about the past, where one invokes God\u8217's name to testify to the truth of a statement that is in fact false. That is the paradigmatic desecration of God\u8217's name: calling on God to bear witness to a lie. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's case, however, involves {\i shvuat bituy} \u8212- an oath about future behavior, which creates an obligation. These are conceptually distinct: {\i shvuat bituy} is not primarily about calling God to witness a falsehood; it creates a positive obligation on the person. When you fail to fulfill it, retroactively you have backed your word with God\u8217's name and then not delivered on it \u8212- which resembles {\i shvuat sheker}, but the conceptual emphasis is on the obligation created rather than the invocation of the divine name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot acknowledges that the {\i pasuk} of {\i lo yenakeh} is technically written in the context of {\i shvuat sheker}, and the {\i mishnah} deals with {\i shvuat bituy}, but concludes: {\i mikol makom chumra chashivuta shava hi} \u8212- nevertheless, as a category, {\i shvua} as a whole is weightier than {\i neder}, and the {\i Gemara} simply uses the most accessible expression of that weightiness. There are other respects in which {\i shvua} is more serious, which the {\i Gemara} in Shvuot elaborates; here the {\i Gemara} takes the straightforward one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic of Qal VaChomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a good moment to clarify the logical structure at play. A {\i qal vachomer} runs from the {\i kal} to the {\i chamur}: if something is true for the lighter category, it certainly holds for the weightier one. Here, you might have wanted to argue: since {\i neder betoch neder} works by {\i nezirut}, which is {\i kal}, it should certainly work by {\i shvua}, which is {\i chamur}. But we know it does not work by {\i shvua}. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's move is the logical contrapositive: if it does not hold for the {\i chamur}, it certainly should not hold for the {\i kal}. If {\i shvua} \u8212- which is {\i chamur} \u8212- does not permit {\i ein shvua chala al shvua} to be overridden, then {\i nezirut} \u8212- which is {\i kal} \u8212- certainly should not permit it. This is the structure of {\i if P then Q; therefore if not-Q then not-P}. You would have expected that just as {\i shvua} cannot double up, neither should {\i nezirut} be able to \u8212- and the {\i pasuk} comes to teach otherwise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issur Cheftza vs.\u160 ?Ga (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order of Neder/Shvua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before moving further in the {\i Gemara}, there is an important and conceptually rich discussion that the Ran develops on the basis of the {\i Yerushalmi}, concerning the interaction between {\i neder} and {\i shvua} across different categories \u8212- specifically, what happens when someone makes a {\i neder} following a {\i shvua}, or a {\i shvua} following a {\i neder}, on the same object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question: {\i shvua shelo ochal kikar ze} \u8212- I take an oath I will not eat this loaf \u8212- followed by {\i kikar ze konam alai} \u8212- I now make this loaf a {\i konam}, forbidden. Or conversely: first {\i konam}, then {\i shvua}. Does the second instrument take effect on top of the first?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheftza Implies Gavra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's answer hinges on a fundamental conceptual distinction: {\i neder} operates as an {\i issur cheftza} \u8212- a prohibition that attaches to the object itself \u8212- while {\i shvua} operates as an {\i issur gavra} \u8212- a prohibition that attaches to the person. The Ran\u8217's core principle is that {\i issur cheftza} implies {\i issur gavra}, but not the reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sukkah Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran illustrates with a paradigmatic case. If I have a {\i mitzvah} to sit in a {\i sukkah} \u8212- which is an {\i issur gavra}-style obligation upon me as a person \u8212- I can nonetheless subsequently make the {\i sukkah} forbidden to myself by declaring it a {\i konam}. The personal obligation and the object\u8217's status operate on different planes and do not directly contradict one another. Practically, the result is that I will be unable to honor both simultaneously, but the act of conferring a status on the object is not in direct conflict with the personal obligation. They are not in conversation with one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asymmetry of Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this emerges an asymmetry that is, at first glance, counterintuitive. If someone first says {\i shvua shelo ochal kikar ze} \u8212- I take an oath I will not eat this loaf \u8212- and then says {\i konam kikar ze alai}, the {\i neder} takes effect. Why? Because the {\i shvua} says nothing about the status of the loaf. The loaf is entirely neutral; the {\i shvua} is purely a statement about what I will do. Since the loaf retains neutral status, I can subsequently confer a prohibited status upon it through a {\i neder}. Similarly, if the {\i shvua} is in the reinforcing direction \u8212- I swear I will eat the loaf, and then I make it a {\i konam} \u8212- the {\i neder} still takes effect, even though it now creates a direct conflict between the {\i neder} and the {\i shvua}. The point is structural: the {\i shvua} made no claim about the object\u8217's nature, so the {\i neder} finds the object in a neutral state and can act upon it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reverse the order, however, and the result changes entirely. If someone first declares {\i konam kikar ze} \u8212- making the loaf forbidden \u8212- and then attempts to take a {\i shvua} on that loaf, whether to eat it or not to eat it, the {\i shvua} does not take effect. This is the surprising direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Speaks to Person\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why? Because when the loaf is declared forbidden through a {\i neder}, that status is not merely a claim about the object in isolation. The Ran argues: what does it mean for an object to be forbidden if there are no people in the world to be affected by it? The forbidden status of the object is inherently a statement {\i to} the person \u8212- it says, you may not eat this. Once the {\i neder} has conferred a forbidden status on the loaf, that status has already implicated and addressed the person. It has already generated, as a direct consequence, an {\i issur gavra} \u8212- a personal prohibition upon the individual. Therefore, when the person subsequently attempts to make a {\i shvua} \u8212- a personal statement about what he will or will not do \u8212- the {\i shvua} finds that the personal dimension has already been spoken to. It is {\i lo chal}: the {\i shvua} cannot take effect, because there is no open space for it. The {\i issur gavra} generated by the {\i neder} preempts it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the Ran\u8217's formulation: {\i issur cheftza} of {\i nedarim} generates {\i issur gavra} as well \u8212- {\i issur gavra nami ika bei} \u8212- because once the loaf is forbidden, the prohibition of {\i lo yachel devaro} applies, which is a personal obligation not to violate the {\i neder}. So the {\i neder} both establishes a status on the object and, as a consequence, speaks to the person. Whereas a {\i shvua} speaks only to the person and leaves the object\u8217's status entirely untouched. Hence the asymmetry: {\i neder} before {\i shvua} forecloses the {\i shvua}; {\i shvua} before {\i neder} does not foreclose the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Lav Laws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a broader and genuinely important question about {\i issurim} in the Torah. When one first encounters the categories of {\i issur gavra} and {\i issur cheftza}, the natural case to consider is something like {\i chazir}. Our intuitive religious sense is that there is something intrinsically problematic about pork \u8212- that it is not merely neutral matter with a personal directive not to eat it, but that the thing itself has a problematic status. Yet the Torah\u8217's language often says {\i lo tochal} \u8212- you shall not eat \u8212- which is directed at the person and sounds like an {\i issur gavra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are indeed those who argue that every prohibition in the Torah is technically an {\i issur gavra}, since the Torah always speaks to what you are not permitted to do, not about some inherent ontological status of the object. But the Ran\u8217's position, and the more nuanced view, is that these two categories are not mutually exclusive. A prohibition can start with an {\i issur cheftza} \u8212- the object has a problematic status \u8212- and then translate into an {\i issur gavra}: {\i and therefore} you may not eat it. Any {\i issur cheftza} carries with it an {\i issur gavra}. The converse, however, is not true: an {\i issur gavra} does not necessarily imply any {\i issur cheftza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication the Ran draws: since every {\i issur cheftza} entails an {\i issur gavra}, once you have started with a {\i neder} \u8212- which is an {\i issur cheftza} \u8212- you have, by the same act, already generated an {\i issur gavra}. There is therefore no room for a {\i shvua} to add anything on the personal level. But if you start with a {\i shvua} \u8212- which is purely {\i issur gavra} \u8212- the object remains wholly untouched, and a {\i neder} can still act upon it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the Ran explains, every {\i lav} in the Torah is an {\i issur gavra} \u8212- that is, it is addressed to the person and tells them what not to do \u8212- but this does not mean it is {\i only} an {\i issur gavra}. Some {\i lavim} begin with the recognition that the object itself has a problematic character, and the prohibition on the person follows from that. The Ran\u8217's statement that a {\i lav} functions {\i kechol lo sa\u8217'aseh sheba-torah} \u8212- like any negative commandment in the Torah \u8212- does not collapse the distinction; it means the Torah\u8217's prohibitions always land, at minimum, on the person, but they may simultaneously reflect a status of the object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Text Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Let us read the relevant passage in the Ran carefully. The Ran frames the question this way: one might have thought that just as an {\i issur cheftza} of {\i nedarim} can take effect upon an {\i issur gavra} of {\i shvuot} \u8212- since they are operating on different planes \u8212- so too should an {\i issur gavra} of {\i shvuot} be able to take effect upon an {\i issur cheftza} of {\i nedarim}. After all, if the {\i sukkah} argument works in one direction, why not in the other?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's answer: {\i mishum de-vinedarim ke-heichi de-ishei issur cheftza, issur gavra nami ika bei} \u8212- because in {\i nedarim}, once the {\i issur cheftza} exists, the {\i issur gavra} is already embedded in it. The moment the loaf is forbidden, the halacha of {\i lo yachel devaro} \u8212- you shall not violate your word \u8212- kicks in and is already speaking to the person. {\i Ve-hai lav issur gavra hu kechol lo sa\u8217'aseh sheba-torah} \u8212- this is a personal prohibition just like any negative commandment in the Torah. Thus the {\i neder}, as an {\i issur cheftza}, carries its {\i issur gavra} along with it. {\i Hilkach ein makom lishevuah shetachol al nedarim} \u8212- therefore there is no room for a {\i shvua} to take effect once a {\i neder} is already in place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran then cites the {\i Yerushalmi} in the first chapter of our tractate, which states: {\i bekikar echad mikeivan shehizkir alav shevuah keneveilah hu} \u8212- once someone declares a {\i neder} on a loaf, it is like a {\i neveilah}. From that point forward, if one attempts to make a {\i shvua} on it, it is as if one is trying to make a {\i shvua} take effect on top of an existing {\i issur} \u8212- and {\i ein shvuah chala al issurin}. Then {\i Rav Yudan} in the {\i Yerushalmi} states: this is specifically when the person first mentioned the {\i neder} and then the {\i shvua} \u8212- the {\i shvua} does not take effect. But if the {\i shvua} came first and the {\i neder} second, the {\i neder} does take effect, since the {\i shvua} left the object\u8217's status entirely open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises: what about {\i hekdesh}? When one is {\i makdish} something \u8212- consecrates it \u8212- it is simultaneously an {\i issur cheftza} and generates an {\i issur gavra} for any individual who might benefit from it. Is this not the same principle?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Precisely \u8212- the Ran\u8217's point applies equally to {\i hekdesh}. Every {\i issur cheftza} translates into an {\i issur gavra}. What is distinctive about {\i hekdesh}, one might note, is that the {\i issur cheftza} is capable of generating an {\i issur gavra} not just for the one who sanctified it but for any person who might interact with it. But this is not categorically different from the Ran\u8217's point; it simply illustrates the broader reach of an {\i issur cheftza}. A {\i neder} can similarly forbid a loaf to the entire world if the person who makes the {\i neder} owns that loaf. The principle that {\i issur cheftza} implies {\i issur gavra} holds across these cases. Indeed, the very concept of {\i kedushah} carries this inherently: the object\u8217's sanctified status is not merely an internal metaphysical fact about the object but speaks directly to what any person may or may not do with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meat and Milk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual framework of {\i issur gavra} versus {\i issur cheftza} opens onto a rich set of questions about Torah prohibitions generally. Take the prohibition of {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} \u8212- meat and milk. One approach holds that the primary prohibition of the Torah is simply not to eat them together; everything else \u8212- the prohibition on cooking them, on deriving benefit \u8212- is secondary and constructed to protect the eating prohibition. Under this view, the orientation is fundamentally personal and action-centered: there is nothing problematic about the combination per se, only about what you do with it. This is closer to a pure {\i issur gavra} orientation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposing approach holds that the very phenomenon of meat and milk cooked together is intrinsically problematic \u8212- something akin to {\i kilayim} or {\i shatnez}, a fundamental disruption of natural categories. Under this view, the prohibition is not merely about protecting me from eating; it reflects a status of the {\i combined entity} itself, which then generates a series of downstream prohibitions: against cooking, against benefiting, against eating. This is the {\i issur cheftza} orientation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difference has concrete implications. The scope of the prohibition on {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- benefit \u8212- is more naturally explained if there is something fundamentally objectionable about the object\u8217's status, which demands complete hands-off treatment, rather than merely a personal directive not to perform a specific action. The {\i issur achilah} alone suggests a narrower focus: it is about what you may or may not do, not about the thing being categorically tainted. These distinct orientations can be mapped onto the {\i issur gavra} / {\i issur cheftza} distinction and carry consequences for how we analyze the {\i halachot} of {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} throughout the tractate of Chullin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the purposes of {\i Nedarim}, the operative question is not whether a Torah prohibition is {\i issur gavra} or {\i issur cheftza} in the abstract, but whether the object in question is a {\i davar hanadur} \u8212- something whose forbidden status derives from speech-act dedication \u8212- or not. That is the criterion for whether a {\i neder} can use it as a reference point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Retroactive Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Retroactivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now transitions to a related question. The {\i mishnah} establishes that when someone makes a {\i shvua} and then makes a second {\i shvua} on the same matter \u8212- {\i shvua shelo ochal v\u8217'achal eino chayav ela achat} \u8212- he violates only once. The second {\i shvua} does not take effect because one is already {\i mushba} and {\i omed}. Rava, however, asserts: {\i nish\u8217'al al rishona, shniya chala alav} \u8212- if the person petitions a {\i chacham} and has the first {\i shvua} dissolved through {\i heter}, the second {\i shvua} takes retroactive effect. Even though the second {\i shvua} was meaningless at the moment of utterance \u8212- since the first was already operative \u8212- once the first is removed, the second comes into force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking principle. One might have argued that a statement which is entirely without force at the moment it is uttered is simply null \u8212- a {\i davar she\u8217'ein bo mamash}, a nothing. How can it hover and then retroactively take effect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Revach Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's language is illuminating. It does not say the second {\i shvua} is null; it says {\i revacha hu de-leit lei} \u8212- it has no {\i revach}, no space, no room in which to operate. This language is remarkable because it treats the {\i shvua} as something concrete \u8212- an obligation-object that requires physical space in which to exist. The first {\i shvua} occupies that space entirely, leaving no room for the second. But the second is nonetheless present, hovering, awaiting an opening. When the first is dissolved, the space opens, and the second takes effect. The obligations and statuses created by speech are being conceptualized with something close to physicality \u8212- they occupy space, they compete for room, they hover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Neziruyot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to derive Rava\u8217's principle from the case of two {\i neziruyot}. If someone accepted two {\i neziruyot} \u8212- {\i mi she-nadar shtei nezirot u-manat et ha-rishona ve-hifrish korban} \u8212- counted the first {\i nezirut}, separated a {\i korban}, and then obtained a {\i heter} on the first {\i nezirut}, {\i ala lo shniya bi-rishona} \u8212- the second {\i nezirut} is considered to have been operative from the start, and the days already counted apply retroactively to the second. The second {\i nezirut} hovered and, upon dissolution of the first, retroactively took its place. This seems to be strong support for Rava\u8217's principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Achat Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects the proof: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan k\u8217'gon shekibel alav shtei neziruyot b\u8217'vat achat} \u8212- perhaps the case of two {\i neziruyot} refers specifically to the scenario where both were accepted simultaneously in a single utterance. In that case, we have already accepted that the simultaneous acceptance creates the hovering phenomenon: one takes effect, the other hovers, and if the first is dissolved, the second retroactively fills its place. This is structurally different from the sequential case, where one {\i shvua} was already in force before the second was uttered. In the {\i bat achat} scenario, because they were created together as a single package, the hovering status is constitutive and built-in. The {\i Gemara} is suggesting that perhaps Rava\u8217's extension to the sequential {\i shvua} case is not proven from the {\i nezirut} example.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequential vs.\u160 ?Simultane\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And yet Rava\u8217's assertion stands: even in the sequential case, where the second {\i shvua} was uttered after the first was already operative, the second one nonetheless hovers \u8212- {\i revacha hu de-leit lei} \u8212- and will take effect if the first is dissolved. The {\i lishna achrina} of the {\i Gemara} reinforces this: {\i chiyuva hu de-leka, ha-shvuah ika} \u8212- the obligation to bring a {\i korban} or the liability for violation may not exist, but the {\i shvua} itself is still present. The practical implication \u8212- {\i le-mai hilchata} \u8212- is precisely {\i de-Rava}: if you dissolve the first {\i shvua}, {\i chala lo shniya tachteiha}, the second takes its place. Even by {\i shvua}, where we say {\i ein shvua chala al shvua}, the focus remains on the obligation-creating dimension of the {\i shvua}, and even there the second one hovers with just no room to operate, until the first is removed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issur Cheftza vs.\u160 ?Ga (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Power of Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing over what {\i Masechet Nedarim} reveals about the nature of speech. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's language \u8212- that the second {\i shvua} has no {\i revach}, no space \u8212- treats obligations and statuses created by words as if they were concrete, quasi-physical entities that occupy space, hover, and compete. This is not incidental to the {\i sugya}; it is, in a sense, its deepest teaching. When one says {\i konam} or takes a {\i shvua}, one is not merely expressing an intention. One is creating a status \u8212- of an object, of oneself \u8212- that now has an independent existence in the world of {\i halacha}. It competes with other statuses, it occupies conceptual space, it can hover waiting for room to take effect. The tractate of {\i Nedarim} is, among other things, a meditation on the creative power of speech: how words construct religious realities, generate obligations, transform the status of objects, and build what might be called a personal religious world alongside the pre-existing structure of Torah obligations. Much as a {\i bamah} was a personal, do-it-yourself site of worship \u8212- the {\i Gemara} elsewhere compares {\i nedarim} to this \u8212- so one who makes {\i nedarim} is in a sense constructing his own overlay of sanctity and prohibition on a neutral world. The statuses thus created are real, weighty, and interact with one another in all the ways we have been tracing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stam Nedarim Lehachmir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now presents a principle that addresses a question that has been hovering throughout the tractate: {\i stam nedarim lehachmir u-peirusham lehakel} \u8212- unspecified {\i nedarim} are given the stricter interpretation, while an articulated clarification is given the more lenient one. This principle speaks directly to the recurring question of why we do not simply ask the person what he meant. The answer embedded in the {\i mishnah} is that we do ask \u8212- and when he tells us, we follow the lenient interpretation. But when he does not tell us, or cannot tell us, we default to strict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sacrificial Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Keitzad} \u8212- what are the examples? The {\i mishnah} says: {\i harei alai k\u8217'basar maliach k\u8217'yayin nesech} \u8212- let this be forbidden to me like salted meat or like wine of libation. Two possible meanings present themselves. Salted meat is associated with sacrificial meat, since {\i kol korban chovatcha takriv melach} \u8212- all your obligatory offerings require salt. {\i Yayin nesech} \u8212- wine of libation \u8212- can refer either to {\i nesachim}, the wine poured on the altar as part of the Temple service, or to wine poured as a libation to {\i avodah zarah}. These are radically different referents with opposite halachic consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Im b\u8217'shel shelamim nadar} \u8212- if he meant like a sacrifice to God, {\i assur}: it is forbidden, because this is a {\i davar hanadur}, an object made forbidden through human speech-dedication. {\i Im b\u8217'shel avodat kochavim nadar} \u8212- if he meant like idolatrous libation or sacrifice, {\i mutar}: it is permissible. Why? Although food offered to {\i avodah zarah} is prohibited, it is not a {\i davar hanadur}. Its forbidden status does not derive from a speech-act of dedication; it derives from another mechanism entirely. Therefore it cannot serve as a reference point for a {\i neder}. {\i V\u8217'im stam} \u8212- and if he did not specify \u8212- {\i assur}: we give it the stricter interpretation and treat it as if he meant the sacrificial-to-God meaning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} thus teaches both components of the principle simultaneously. The strict default ({\i stam lehachmir}) operates when nothing else is known. The lenient interpretation ({\i peirusham lehakel}) operates when the person clarifies that he meant the lenient referent \u8212- and his clarification is accepted and binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what if the person says he had no particular meaning in mind, that he simply heard the phrase and used it without thinking of either the {\i shelamim} or the {\i avodah zarah} referent? The Ran and Tosafot address this. If someone says he intended to create the {\i issur} but had no idea what exactly the words meant, we would still apply the strict default. The requirement of intent ({\i piv velibbo shavin}) pertains primarily to the intention to create the status \u8212- the intention to obligate \u8212- not necessarily to detailed knowledge of precisely which referent is meant by the words chosen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cherem Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Harei alai kecherem} \u8212- let this be upon me like {\i cherem}. {\i Cherem} admits of two referents: {\i cherem shel shamayim} \u8212- consecrated to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- and {\i cherem shel kohanim} \u8212- dedicated to the {\i kohanim} for their use. The former is forbidden to all; the latter is permissible to those who receive it. {\i Im kecherem shel shamayim, assur} \u8212- that is a {\i davar hanadur} and forbidden. {\i V\u8217'im kecherem shel kohanim, mutar} \u8212- that is not forbidden to people, so it cannot serve as a {\i neder} reference point. {\i V\u8217'im stam, assur} \u8212- unspecified, we give it the strict meaning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Harei alai kema\u8217'aser} \u8212- let this be upon me like {\i ma\u8217'aser}. Again, two referents: {\i ma\u8217'aser behemah} \u8212- the animal tithe \u8212- and {\i ma\u8217'aser goren} \u8212- the grain tithe. {\i Im kema\u8217'aser behemah nadar, assur}: the animal tithe is a {\i korban} and is forbidden to be eaten by ordinary people; it is both a {\i davar hanadur} and a {\i davar ha\u8217'assur}. {\i V\u8217'im shel goren, mutar}: the grain tithe ({\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} or {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}), while it is a {\i davar hanadur} in the sense that it is set aside through designation, it is not a {\i davar ha\u8217'assur} \u8212- it is permissible for consumption. Therefore it cannot serve as a reference point for creating a prohibition. {\i V\u8217'im stam, assur} \u8212- without clarification, the strict meaning governs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Harei alai kiterumah} \u8212- let this be upon me like {\i terumah}. Two referents: {\i terumat halishkah} \u8212- the annual donation to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} treasury (the {\i machatzit hashekel}) \u8212- and {\i terumah goren} \u8212- the priestly tithe separated from grain. {\i Im kiterumat halishkah nadar, assur}: this is forbidden to all and dedicated to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, a clear {\i davar hanadur}. {\i V\u8217'im shel goren, mutar}: {\i terumah} of the grain is not a {\i davar hanadur} for the purposes of {\i neder} \u8212- either because it is not forbidden to all (being permitted to {\i kohanim}) or, as the Rambam suggests, because it is not a freely undertaken vow but a Torah-imposed obligation, and thus does not qualify as a {\i davar hanadur}. {\i V\u8217'im stam, assur} \u8212- the last statement is attributed specifically to Rabbi Meir, and it is this attribution that opens the door for Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's qualification.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehudah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Regional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah says: the strict default for unspecified {\i terumah} applies only in {\i Yehudah}, not in the {\i Galil}. {\i Stam terumah bi\u8217'Yehudah asurah, ba\u8217'Galil muteret} \u8212- in the {\i Galil} it is permitted even without clarification. The reason: {\i she\u8217'ein anshei Galil makirim et terumat halishkah} \u8212- the people of the {\i Galil} are not familiar with {\i terumat halishkah}. The {\i Beit HaMikdash} is not in their environment; the concept of {\i terumat halishkah} as a donation to the Temple treasury is not part of their daily consciousness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Default Modifier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the deeper principle of what counts as a {\i stam} \u8212- a default meaning \u8212- and what requires a modifier. A word\u8217's default meaning is determined by its sociological and linguistic reality in a given context. In the {\i Galil}, when someone says {\i terumah}, they mean {\i terumah goren} \u8212- that is what the word means to them. If they want to say {\i terumat halishkah}, they will use the full phrase; the modifier is necessary. {\i Terumat halishkah} is not a {\i stam} meaning in the {\i Galil} because it is not part of the ordinary linguistic repertoire.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phenomenon of what becomes the default and what requires modification is always a function of usage and familiarity. The default is what the unmodified word naturally evokes in its context. When digital watches were introduced, {\i digital watch} required the modifier \u8212- {\i watch} was {\i stam} the analog kind. Eventually, if enough shifts occur, the defaults can reverse, and the formerly default meaning needs a modifier. {\i Stam nedarim lehachmir} applies when the stricter meaning is a genuine, equally likely candidate for what the word means \u8212- when it could plausibly be what someone saying that word intends. Where one meaning has so dominated the word\u8217's use that the other meaning requires a modifier to convey, {\i stam} simply means the default meaning, and if the default is lenient, the result is {\i lehakel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cherem Regional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah then applies the same logic in the opposite direction for {\i cherem}: {\i stam charamim bi\u8217'Yehudah mutarin, baGalil asurin}. In {\i Yehudah}, unspecified {\i cherem} is permissible; in the {\i Galil}, it is forbidden. Why? {\i She\u8217'ein anshei Galil makirin et charmei kohanim} \u8212- the people of the {\i Galil} are not familiar with the concept of {\i cherem} given to {\i kohanim}. In the {\i Galil}, the word {\i cherem} evokes only {\i cherem} consecrated to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, which is the strict, forbidden category. {\i Cherem} given to {\i kohanim} would need a modifier there. Therefore {\i stam cherem} in the {\i Galil} is the strict meaning and the result is {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In {\i Yehudah}, by contrast, proximity to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} and familiarity with the {\i kohanim} means the word {\i cherem} commonly evokes {\i charmei kohanim} \u8212- the lenient category. Therefore {\i stam cherem} in {\i Yehudah} is treated as {\i mutar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logical Tension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} will press what seems to be a logical inconsistency in Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's ruling on {\i cherem} in {\i Yehudah}. Even granting that {\i cherem} in {\i Yehudah} commonly evokes {\i charmei kohanim}, the word can also mean {\i cherem haMikdash} in {\i Yehudah} \u8212- the Temple {\i cherem} is not unknown there. If both meanings are available in {\i Yehudah}, why does {\i stam lehachmir} not apply, as it does when there are two possible interpretations, one strict and one lenient? The principle of {\i stam nedarim lehachmir} would seem to demand that we take the strict meaning whenever both meanings are live options. Why does the existence of the lenient meaning ({\i charmei kohanim}) tip the entire default in {\i Yehudah} toward {\i mutar}, rather than triggering {\i lehachmir} precisely because both are possible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer the {\i Gemara} will develop is that {\i stam lehachmir} presupposes that both meanings are roughly equally available and plausible. Where one meaning so dominates the sociological and linguistic reality that the other, while technically possible, is not the word\u8217's natural default meaning, we follow the actual default \u8212- whether strict or lenient. In {\i Yehudah}, {\i cherem} is simply what people mean when they say {\i charmei kohanim}; the {\i mikdash} meaning requires the fuller phrase. That dominance controls the {\i stam}, and no {\i lehachmir} override applies where the default itself is the lenient reading. This is not merely a logical rule but a sociological and linguistic reality that the {\i halacha} incorporates: what a word means \u8212- in its actual usage, in its actual community \u8212- determines what {\i stam} means.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 19\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stam Nedarim Discrepancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Discrepancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf continues a discussion of the principle of {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir, peirushan le-hakel} \u8212- that an ambiguous formulation of a {\i neder}, even when both possible interpretations would constitute a binding vow, is given the stricter construction. The {\i mishnah} dealt with cases where the language could be understood in two ways, one rendering the object a {\i davar hanadur} and the other not, and ruled that when the meaning is ambiguous we adopt the stricter interpretation. At the same time, the {\i mishnah} indicates that {\i peirusham le-hakel} \u8212- if the person were to clarify that he intended the lenient meaning, we would believe him. This raises the question of why we do not simply ask the person every time. Two explanations were offered: either the person is no longer available to ask, or the person genuinely had no specific intention, using language he heard others use without committing to one reading over another, in which case we supply the stricter meaning as a matter of legal principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preceding {\i mishnah} also recorded a debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda concerning regional variation \u8212- specifically, how the standard meaning of the words {\i cherem} and {\i teruma} differs between {\i Galil} and {\i Yehuda}, such that the same term may produce a strict result in one region and a lenient result in the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens by pressing a contradiction. We say {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir}, yet a different {\i mishnah} rules that {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} \u8212- a doubtful {\i nezirut} is resolved leniently. Since {\i nezirut} is itself a species of {\i neder}, why should the two be treated differently? The {\i gemara} identifies three possible directions for resolving this tension. The first would distinguish between cases where the doubt arises from the ambiguity of external circumstances versus cases where the doubt arises from the meaning of the vow\u8217's language. The second would draw a distinction intrinsic to {\i nezirut} itself, given its particular characteristics. The third \u8212- which the {\i gemara} actually pursues \u8212- is to declare the discrepancy a {\i machlokes tannaim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Zeira Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira offers the resolution: {\i lo kashya, ha Rabbi Eliezer, ha Rabbanan} \u8212- there is no true contradiction, because the two rulings reflect the positions of Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i Rabbanan} respectively. The source is a {\i baraita}: if one consecrates all his {\i chaya} (wild animals) or all his {\i behema} (domesticated animals) to {\i hekdesh}, the question arises whether a {\i koi} \u8212- a hybrid creature of disputed zoological classification \u8212- is included. Rabbi Eliezer says it is not consecrated; the {\i Chachamim} say it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Koi Consecration Cas (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Koi Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i koi} is an animal whose status is genuinely uncertain, some holding it to be a {\i min bifnei atzmo} \u8212- a distinct species \u8212- rather than simply a doubtful case between {\i chaya} and {\i behema}. According to the {\i Ran} or {\i Tosafos}, Rabbi Eliezer holds that the {\i koi} is indeed a {\i min bifnei atzmo}, meaning it belongs to neither category, and therefore even if one says he is consecrating all his {\i chayos} and all his {\i behemos}, the {\i koi} would not be consecrated \u8212- it falls into neither. The {\i Chachamim}, by contrast, hold that the {\i koi} definitely falls into one of the two categories; we simply do not know which one, and therefore if one consecrates both categories it is certainly {\i kadosh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} reads this dispute as reflecting a broader disagreement about what to do with a {\i safek} in the context of {\i hekdesh} \u8212- and since {\i nedarim} draws on the model of {\i hekdesh}, the same dispute should govern {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut} alike. The {\i Chachamim}, who are {\i machmir} about consecrating property under conditions of doubt, would similarly be {\i machmir} when one makes himself a {\i nazir} under doubtful conditions. Rabbi Eliezer, who is {\i meikel} about consecrated property under doubt, would likewise be {\i meikel} about {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} makes a significant observation. One might have thought that there is a natural distinction between {\i nedarim}, which govern the status of objects, and {\i nezirut}, which governs a person\u8217's own status \u8212- and that people are more reluctant to impose obligations upon themselves than upon their property. Indeed, this distinction would have neatly resolved the original contradiction: for objects, we rule {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir}; for one\u8217's own person, {\i safek nezirut le-hakel}. The {\i gemara} explicitly signals awareness of this possible answer but sets it aside, preferring instead to treat both cases under a single framework and declare it a {\i machlokes tannaim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Woven into this discussion is the concept of {\i ma\u8217'eil inish nafshei le-sfeika} \u8212- whether a person would willingly subject himself to a doubtful status \u8212- and {\i ma\u8217'eil mamonei le-sfeika} \u8212- whether a person would willingly subject his property to such doubt. Until this point, one might have assumed that {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} is purely a legal interpretive principle \u8212- a rule for how courts construe ambiguous language \u8212- with no reference to the vower\u8217's subjective intent. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's introduction of the {\i ma\u8217'eil} framework suggests that the question of what a person would have wanted is itself part of the legal analysis. This can be understood as follows: rather than asking about this particular individual\u8217's intent, we adopt a general interpretive presumption \u8212- that people making these types of declarations typically intend either the broader or the narrower meaning \u8212- and apply that presumption uniformly as a matter of law. The {\i Ran} supports the more formalist reading: even if the person says he had no specific meaning in mind, the legal principle determines the meaning of his words regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Bechoros Digre (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Bechoros\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges the identification of {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} with Rabbi Eliezer. If that leniency is Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, then the same {\i mishnah} that rules {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} should reflect his position throughout. Yet the {\i mishnah} also rules on {\i safek bechoros} \u8212- cases of doubtful firstborn status \u8212- and the {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita} stating that a {\i safek bechor} is forbidden for shearing and labor. If a cow gave birth to twins and it is unknown whether the firstborn was male or female, the male calf\u8217's {\i bechor} status is uncertain. In terms of monetary law ({\i Choshen Mishpat}), the principle of {\i hamotzi me-chaveiro alav ha-rayah} applies \u8212- the {\i kohen} cannot collect without proof. But in terms of {\i issur ve-heter}, since {\i bechorah} is a biblical obligation, {\i safek d\u8217'oraita le-chumra} applies and the animal is forbidden for shearing or work. This is a straightforward {\i safek d\u8217'oraita}, with nothing to do with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position \u8212- and yet it rules strictly, in contrast to the leniency by {\i nezirut}. The question stands: why are some doubtful cases treated leniently and others strictly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Koi Consecration Cas (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedusha Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers by drawing a fundamental distinction: {\i ami ka-medamit kedusha ba\u8217'ah mei-eilya li-kedusha ba\u8217'ah bi-yedei adam} \u8212- why are you comparing a {\i kedusha} that arrives on its own to a {\i kedusha} that is conferred by a human act? The {\i bechor} animal either is or is not a {\i bechor} \u8212- its sanctity, if it exists, arrived independently of any human declaration. The question is purely one of fact: was the male calf born first? There is one factual reality, unknown to us, and based on that reality the animal is either definitively {\i assur} or definitively {\i muttar}. This is a classic {\i safek d\u8217'oraita}, and the rule {\i safek d\u8217'oraita le-chumra} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Facts vs.\u160 ?Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when a person makes a {\i neder} or declares himself a {\i nazir} using ambiguous language, there is no unresolved factual question \u8212- the facts are fully known. The person spoke certain words. The question is entirely one of legal interpretation: how do we construe those words? What scope do we assign to that language? This is not a question about what happened in the world; it is a question about the legal meaning of a speech act. And if we adopt the legal principle that we interpret such language narrowly \u8212- {\i stam nezirut le-hakel} \u8212- then the {\i kedusha} was simply never conferred. The rule of {\i safek d\u8217'oraita le-chumra} does not apply because we are not uncertain about a fact; we have chosen, as a matter of interpretive law, to give the declaration its narrower construction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- facts versus interpretation \u8212- is analytically powerful and the {\i gemara} presents it as resolving the question about {\i safek bechoros}. The case of {\i bechoros} cannot be compared to {\i nedarim} or {\i nezirut} because in {\i bechoros} we are uncertain about a fact, while in {\i nedarim} we are choosing how to interpret language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Bechoros Digre (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liquids Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having resolved the {\i bechoros} question, the {\i gemara} turns to a more difficult challenge from the same {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i safek mashkin le-tamei tamei, le-tamei acheirim tahor} \u8212- if there is doubt whether liquid was rendered {\i tamei}, we rule it {\i tamei} regarding itself but {\i tahor} regarding its capacity to transmit {\i tumah} to other objects. This is the ruling of Rabbi Meir, and the {\i mishnah} adds that Rabbi Eliezer agreed with this ruling. But the {\i gemara} challenges: does Rabbi Eliezer really hold that liquids become {\i tamei} under conditions of doubt? For there is a {\i baraita} stating that Rabbi Eliezer holds {\i ein tumah le-mashkin kol ikar} \u8212- there is no biblical basis for {\i tumah} of liquids whatsoever. The proof is the famous testimony of Rabbi Yose ben Yoezer of Tzereda, who testified about the {\i ayyal kimtza} locust that it is {\i dakhan} (the Aramaic equivalent of {\i tahor}, meaning kosher for consumption), and that {\i mashkin beit mitbachaya} \u8212- the liquids in the Temple slaughterhouse \u8212- are likewise {\i dakhan}, meaning they do not transmit {\i tumah}. The underlying principle is that {\i tumat mashkin} is only rabbinic, and in the {\i Beis HaMikdash} we revert to the biblical rule, eliminating the concern entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i tumat mashkin} is only rabbinic, then in a case of doubt whether liquid was touched, the rule of {\i safek d\u8217'rabanan le-kula} should apply, and we should be lenient. Yet Rabbi Eliezer, if he agrees with the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling, appears to be strict even in this rabbinic doubt. How can that be reconciled with his leniency by the {\i koi}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers a resolution attributed to Shmuel: the testimony of Rabbi Yose ben Yoezer should be understood as stating that the Temple liquids are pure only in the sense that they do not make other things {\i tamei} \u8212- {\i le-tamei acheirim} \u8212- but they themselves do become {\i tamei} at the biblical level. According to Shmuel, {\i tumat mashkin} at the level of the liquid becoming {\i tamei} itself is biblical; only its capacity to transmit {\i tumah} to other objects is rabbinic. This maps perfectly onto the {\i mishnah}\u8217's split ruling: for the liquid becoming {\i tamei} itself, it is a {\i safek d\u8217'oraita} and we rule strictly; for the liquid making other things {\i tamei}, it is a {\i safek d\u8217'rabanan} and we rule leniently. {\i Safek d\u8217'oraita le-chumra, safek d\u8217'rabanan le-kula} \u8212- this is a clean and precise application of the principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, according to Rav, who holds that {\i mashkin} are completely {\i tahor} at the biblical level ({\i dakhan mamash}), the entire {\i tumah} of liquids is rabbinic \u8212- yet Rabbi Eliezer is still strict when there is doubt. The {\i gemara} finds this difficult: if it is all rabbinic, why would Rabbi Eliezer be strict by {\i safek mashkin} yet lenient in the case of the {\i koi}? The {\i Ran} acknowledges that the comparison is not rigorous \u8212- the {\i bechoros} distinction (facts versus interpretation) does not map cleanly onto this new question, and the {\i gemara} seems to be reasoning more loosely, along the lines of: if Rabbi Eliezer is strict even about a rabbinic {\i safek} of liquids, it would be strange for him to be lenient about {\i safek nezirut}. This is admittedly not a tight argument. The {\i gemara} itself appears to treat it as something less than fully compelling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any event, the {\i gemara} concludes by abandoning the approach of Rabbi Zeira entirely. The whole framework identifying {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position may have been based more on his particular view of the {\i koi} as a {\i min bifnei atzmo} than on a general principle about doubtful {\i kedusha}, and it does not survive scrutiny. The {\i gemara} therefore returns to the original problem fresh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehuda-Shimon Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Condition Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes a new resolution: {\i ha Rabbi Yehuda, ha Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- the discrepancy between {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} and {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} reflects a debate between Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon specifically about {\i nezirut}. The source is a {\i baraita} from {\i Masechet Nazir}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\i Haraini nazir im yeish be-keri zeh me\u8217'ah kor} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} if there are a hundred {\i kor} in this grain pile.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The person then goes to measure the grain and finds it was stolen or lost. There is now no way ever to determine whether the condition was met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Matir Shimon Oser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda says {\i matir} \u8212- the person is not a {\i nazir}. Rabbi Shimon says {\i oser} \u8212- the person is a {\i nazir}. This is a direct dispute about {\i safek nezirut}: Rabbi Yehuda rules leniently, Rabbi Shimon rules strictly. The {\i gemara} identifies Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's lenient position with {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} and Rabbi Shimon\u8217's strict position with the principle of {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir}, treating {\i neder} and {\i nezirut} as parallel throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note an important conceptual difficulty that the {\i gemara} does not fully address. There is a significant difference between the question of how to interpret ambiguous language \u8212- which is the subject of {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} \u8212- and the question of what to do when a clearly-intended vow is conditioned on a fact that remains unknowable. In the {\i stam nedarim} discussion, the person\u8217's language is ambiguous; we must choose a legal construction for words of uncertain meaning. In the grain case, the person clearly intended to become a {\i nazir} if a condition was met; the question is simply whether that condition was ever satisfied. These are logically distinct questions \u8212- one turns on interpretive principle, the other on factual uncertainty \u8212- and one could easily hold differently in the two cases. The {\i gemara}, however, treats them as equivalent and lumps them together as a single debate about whether {\i safek} produces stringency or leniency in the realm of {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Chamer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a contradiction against Rabbi Yehuda himself: {\i mi amar Rabbi Yehuda lo ma\u8217'eil inish nafshei le-sfeika} \u8212- does Rabbi Yehuda really say that a person would not willingly subject himself to doubtful status? This is offered as the logical underpinning of his leniency in the grain case: since being a {\i safek nazir} is worse than being a {\i vadai nazir}, a person would never have intended for an ambiguous condition to bind him. Yet the same Rabbi Yehuda elsewhere states {\i stam terumah bi-Yehudah assurah} \u8212- that an ambiguous declaration of {\i teruma} in {\i Yehuda} is forbidden \u8212- and the {\i mishnah} records his position that {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} applies whenever the language is genuinely ambiguous. This shows that Rabbi Yehuda endorses {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} as a general principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves the contradiction: {\i gabi keri kasavar kol safek chamur mi-vadai, lo ma\u8217'eil nafshei le-sfeika} \u8212- specifically regarding the grain pile case, the reason for leniency is that being a {\i safek nazir} is in fact worse than being a {\i vadai nazir}, and therefore a person would never have intended for his conditional vow to create that status. A {\i vadai nazir} completes his {\i nezirut} after thirty days, shaves his head, and brings his {\i korbanot}. A {\i safek nazir}, by contrast, cannot bring his {\i korbanot} \u8212- this would be {\i safek chulin ba-azarah} \u8212- and therefore can never formally conclude his {\i nezirut}. He is permanently suspended in an unresolvable doubtful status. Since this situation is demonstrably worse than being a definite {\i nazir}, the interpretive presumption is that no person would have wished to enter it. Therefore, any ambiguity in the condition is resolved in favor of the vow never having taken effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a {\i neder}, by contrast, there is no analogous problem. If one\u8217's {\i neder} is treated as binding {\i mi-safek}, the consequence is that the object is prohibited \u8212- but this is a manageable restriction, not an impossible situation. One can live with a {\i safek neder} in a way that one cannot live with {\i safek nezirut}. Therefore by {\i nedarim} we say {\i stam le-hachmir}, while by {\i nezirut} we say {\i le-hakel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is elegant and analytically compelling. It means that the answer to the {\i gemara}\u8217's original question \u8212- why {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} but {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} \u8212- does not require positing a {\i machlokes tannaim} at all. The difference is intrinsic to the subject matter: a doubtful {\i neder} is tolerable, while a doubtful {\i nezirut} is a trap. Therefore we always interpret {\i nezirut} conditions in the more lenient direction, not as a matter of general dispute, but because of the specific nature of {\i nezirut} and its consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Yehuda pressed Rava: what if the conditional vow was phrased as {\i Haraini nazir olam} \u8212- a {\i nazir} forever? In that case, why would {\i safek} be worse than {\i vadai}? A {\i nazir olam} by definition never concludes his {\i nezirut}; the inability to bring concluding {\i korbanot} is no additional burden. There should therefore be no reason to resolve the doubt leniently. Rava answered that even by {\i nazir olam} the {\i safek} is worse than the {\i vadai}. A definite {\i nazir olam} has a special leniency: {\i hikhvid se\u8217'aro meikel be-ta\u8217'ar} \u8212- if his hair becomes too burdensome, he may trim it by bringing three {\i korbanot}, even though this does not terminate the {\i nezirut}. This is an unusual {\i halacha} that recognizes the extraordinary burden of lifelong {\i nezirut} and permits periodic relief through the {\i korban} process. A {\i safek nazir olam}, however, cannot avail himself of this leniency \u8212- bringing the {\i korbanot} would again raise the problem of {\i safek chulin ba-azarah}. Thus even {\i nazir olam} as a {\i safek} is worse than {\i nazir olam} as a {\i vadai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Shimshon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Yehuda pressed further: what about a case where the person declared {\i Haraini nazir Shimshon} conditionally? {\i Nazir Shimshon} is a special category derived from the life of Shimshon, who was a {\i nazir} yet became {\i tamei la-meitim} and could never cut his hair. From Shimshon we learn that there is a form of {\i nezirut} in which one may become {\i tamei} to the dead \u8212- unlike a standard {\i nazir} \u8212- but can never cut his hair under any circumstances, even with the leniency that applies to {\i nazir olam}. By {\i nazir Shimshon}, neither a definite nor a doubtful status carries the leniency of hair-trimming, so there would again seem to be no difference between {\i vadai} and {\i safek}. Why then should the condition not be binding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responded: {\i nazir Shimshon lo tanya} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} does not deal with the case of {\i nazir Shimshon}; it was not contemplated in the standard ruling. Abaye bar Avin replied that there is in fact a {\i baraita} that does address this scenario \u8212- a conditional vow of {\i nazir Shimshon} \u8212- and Rabbi Yehuda still rules leniently even there. Rava conceded: {\i im tanya, tanya} \u8212- if there is such a {\i baraita}, it stands, though he remained unpersuaded by its logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ki Yafla Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarfon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers yet another approach. The leniency of {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} is not Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's own position at all \u8212- it is a ruling transmitted by Rabbi Yehuda in the name of Rabbi Tarfon. The {\i baraita} reads: {\i Rabbi Yehuda mishum Rabbi Tarfon omer, ein echad me-hem nazir} \u8212- neither person is a {\i nazir}. The case described involves two people walking and seeing a figure in the distance: one says \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if that is so-and-so\u8221" and the other says \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if that is not so-and-so.\u8221" Since one of the two propositions must be true, it seems that one of them must be a {\i nazir}. Nevertheless, Rabbi Tarfon rules that neither becomes a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Yafla Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\i she-lo nitna nezirut ela le-hafla\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i nezirut} was given only for {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, a term derived from the biblical verse {\i ish o isha ki yafli lindor neder nazir le-hazzir la-Hashem} (Bamidbar 6:2). The word {\i yafli} is understood to mean an articulate, precise, and unambiguous declaration. {\i Hafla\u8217'ah} is the concept underlying all speech-act {\i kedusha} \u8212- {\i neder}, {\i shevua}, {\i nezirut}, and {\i hekdesh} are all effectuated through {\i hafla\u8217'ah}. Here, however, {\i ki yafli} is read as a requirement for clarity: if there is any ambiguity at the moment of declaration, the {\i nezirut} does not take effect. This is a pure legal principle, entirely independent of what the person wanted or what is in his mind. Even if the person believed with certainty that it was or was not the person in the distance \u8212- and was objectively correct \u8212- {\i nezirut} does not bind when the declaration was made in a context of uncertainty apparent to observers. {\i Nezirut} requires that it be unambiguously established from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle, rooted in the {\i pasuk}, provides the cleanest and most legally grounded answer to the question. It is not about what a person would or would not want; it is not about the consequences of doubtful {\i nezirut}; it is a textual requirement that {\i nezirut} take effect only through unambiguous declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Lost Detail\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes a textual precision in the grain case: the {\i baraita} specifically says the grain was stolen or lost. If the principle of {\i ki yafli} means that {\i nezirut} cannot take effect whenever there is any ambiguity at the time of vowing, then even if the grain were present and measurable, since it was not clear at the time whether the condition would be met, the {\i nezirut} should not be binding \u8212- and the detail of theft or loss would be unnecessary. The {\i gemara} answers: you are correct that according to Rabbi Yehuda, citing Rabbi Tarfon, even if the grain were present and measurable, the person would not be a {\i nazir}, since the declaration was made in a state of uncertainty. The {\i baraita} specifies the case of theft or loss as a {\i chiddush} for Rabbi Shimon\u8217's strict position: Rabbi Shimon holds that a person is willing to enter into doubtful status ({\i ma\u8217'eil inish nafshei le-sfeika}), and therefore even though the condition can never be verified, the {\i nezirut} takes effect. The more striking {\i chiddush} is actually Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's leniency, but the {\i baraita} is framed around the case that provides the greatest {\i chiddush} for the strict position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stam Teruma Regions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teruma Strict Lenient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i mishnah} regarding regional linguistic variation. Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i stam terumah bi-Yehudah assurah, uva-Galil muteret} \u8212- an ambiguous declaration of {\i teruma} in {\i Yehuda} is forbidden, but in {\i Galil} it is permitted. The reason: in {\i Yehuda}, the word {\i teruma} can mean either {\i terumat hagoren} (the ordinary agricultural {\i teruma}) or {\i terumat halishka} (the Temple chamber contribution), and since {\i terumat halishka} is a {\i davar hanadur} while {\i terumat hagoren} is not, the ambiguity generates a genuine doubt, and {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} applies. In {\i Galil}, however, the people are not familiar with {\i terumat halishka} \u8212- the term {\i teruma} in that region refers exclusively to {\i terumat hagoren}, which is not a binding {\i neder}. Since the word has only the lenient meaning in that locale, the declaration is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} infers from this: if in {\i Galil} the people {\i were} familiar with {\i terumat halishka}, making the word ambiguous in their region as well, the declaration would be forbidden \u8212- {\i ha makirim, assurin}. This confirms that Rabbi Yehuda endorses the principle of {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} in any case of genuine ambiguity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stam Charamim Region (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Charamim Permitted Forbi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Immediately following is the parallel ruling about {\i charamim}: {\i stam charamim bi-Yehudah mutarin, uva-Galil assurin} \u8212- an ambiguous declaration of {\i cherem} in {\i Yehuda} is permitted, while in {\i Galil} it is forbidden. The reasoning: in {\i Galil}, the people are unfamiliar with {\i charmei kohanim} \u8212- the type of dedication that goes to the {\i kohanim} rather than to {\i hekdesh} \u8212- so the word {\i cherem} can only mean {\i cherem} dedicated to the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, which is a binding {\i neder}. In {\i Yehuda}, the word {\i cherem} could mean either {\i cherem} to {\i hekdesh} or {\i charmei kohanim}, and since the latter is not a {\i neder}, the ambiguity renders it permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this is the problem. If in {\i Yehuda} the word {\i cherem} can mean either the strict form ({\i cherem} to {\i hekdesh}) or the lenient form ({\i charmei kohanim}), why are we lenient? By the logic of {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir}, whenever there is genuine ambiguity we should be strict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stam Teruma Regions (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Consistency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof that Rabbi Yehuda holds {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} despite the leniency in {\i Galil} for {\i teruma} was already established \u8212- in {\i Galil} the word is not ambiguous, so there is nothing to be {\i machmir} about. The rule {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} applies precisely when there is genuine ambiguity. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position is therefore entirely consistent: where there is real ambiguity he is strict; where the word has only one meaning in that locale he is lenient. This is not a {\i kula} in {\i stam nedarim}; it is a determination that the word is not, in that context, a {\i stam} case at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stam Charamim Region (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elazar b\u8217'Tzadok\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The anomaly of the {\i cherem} ruling \u8212- where Rabbi Yehuda appears to be lenient despite genuine ambiguity in {\i Yehuda} \u8212- is resolved by Abaye: {\i seifa Rabbi Elazar be-Rebbi Tzadok hee} \u8212- the final ruling about {\i charamim} in {\i Yehuda} is not Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position at all. It is the position of Rabbi Elazar be-Rebbi Tzadok. Rabbi Elazar be-Rebbi Tzadok holds that in {\i Yehuda} the word {\i cherem} is permissible precisely because there is ambiguity \u8212- and implicit in his ruling is the position that {\i stam nedarim le-hakel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking conclusion. The entire lengthy {\i sugya} grappled with the question of who holds {\i safek nezirut le-hakel}, passing through the positions of Rabbi Eliezer, the distinction between {\i kedusha ba\u8217'ah mei-eilya} and {\i kedusha al yedei adam}, the debate of Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon, Rava\u8217's explanation based on {\i safek chamer mi-vadai}, and Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's principle of {\i ki yafli} \u8212- and now, at the very end of the {\i daf}, the {\i gemara} points to Rabbi Elazar be-Rebbi Tzadok as someone who apparently says {\i stam nedarim le-hakel} directly. As the {\i Ran} notes, one could have explained the entire discussion by attributing {\i safek nezirut le-hakel} and the corresponding lenient positions to this view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One could, alternatively, read Rabbi Elazar be-Rebbi Tzadok more narrowly: perhaps he agrees in principle with {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir} but holds that in {\i Yehuda} the word {\i cherem} is not genuinely ambiguous \u8212- that the usage of {\i charmei kohanim} is so prevalent there that the word {\i cherem} alone refers exclusively to that category, and anyone who wishes to say {\i cherem} to {\i hekdesh} would say {\i charmei mikdash} explicitly. On this reading, Rabbi Elazar be-Rebbi Tzadok is making a linguistic determination, not announcing a general leniency in {\i stam nedarim}. The {\i gemara}, however, does not adopt this reading and appears to understand his ruling as reflecting a general position that {\i stam nedarim le-hakel} \u8212- a position that, after all the preceding analysis, turns out to have a clear talmudic advocate. The connection between {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut} underlying this entire discussion runs through the shared hermeneutical principle of {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, derived through a {\i gezeira shava} linking the language of {\i neder} and {\i nezer} \u8212- which is why the rules governing one apply to the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 21\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marital Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Completing Daf 20\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin today by completing Daf 20, picking up at the bottom of 20b with the verse {\i velo tasuru acharei levavchem}. The preceding {\i sugyah} on Daf 20 constituted one of the most remarkable discussions in all of {\i Shas}, dealing at length with the question of which physical acts are permissible between husband and wife during marital relations. We encountered two poles: the position associated with {\i Imma Shalom} and Yochanan ben Dahavai \u8212- closely aligned with the stringent posture of Rabbi Eliezer, who would rush through the marital act lest he entertain an improper thought \u8212- and the opposing voice of Rabbi Yochanan, who rules definitively: {\i ein halakhah k\u8217'Yochanan ben Dahavai, ella kol mah she-adam rotzeh la\u8217'asot b\u8217'ishto oseh}. Whatever a man wishes to do with his wife, he may do. The language, it should be noted, reflects the male perspective of the {\i Gemara}, though it is typically paraphrased today as the couple being permitted to do whatever they wish together \u8212- and the underlying position is that this is entirely unproblematic, not merely technically permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One striking feature of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's rhetorical strategy here is worth noting. The discussion began with anxieties about how improper conduct would affect the children \u8212- a claim that, had it remained in the {\i halakhic} domain, would have had to contend with Rabbi Yochanan on {\i halakhic} terms. But Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's framing as {\i ein halakhah} essentially relocates Yochanan ben Dahavai\u8217's concerns outside the {\i halakhic} conversation altogether. Whatever metaphysical ideas one might hold about proper and improper conduct, the {\i Gemara} is insisting that those anxieties belong to a non-{\i halakhic} register. {\i Halakhically}, there is nothing problematic here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent vs.\u160 ?Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, however, the {\i Gemara} makes an important shift. It introduces the verse {\i velo tasuru acharei levavchem} \u8212- you shall not stray after your hearts \u8212- and cites Rebbe: {\i al yishteh adam b\u8217'kos zeh v\u8217'yiten einav b\u8217'kos acher}. A person should not drink from one cup while setting his eyes on another \u8212- a metaphor for having marital relations with one\u8217's wife while thinking of another woman. Ravina sharpens this: {\i lo nitzrecha ella d\u8217'afilu b\u8217'shnei nashav} \u8212- this applies even between a man\u8217's two wives; he should not be intimate with one while his thoughts are directed toward the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage, notably, focuses not on {\i acharei eineichem} but on {\i acharei levavchem} \u8212- it is not about what the eyes perceive but about what the heart strays toward. The man sees the woman before him but is mentally elsewhere, straying after his own inner fantasy. The problem the {\i Gemara} is now identifying is not the nature of the physical act at all, but whether the act of intimacy is an act of genuine {\i relating} to the person in front of you, or merely {\i using} that person as a stand-in for someone else you would rather be with. That is the distinction \u8212- not the particular activity, but whether the person before you is truly present to you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an important question lurking here about the role of fantasy in marital life generally, and some {\i poskim} do address it. However, the {\i Gemara} appears to be speaking specifically about a case where one imagines oneself with another person entirely and treats one\u8217's actual spouse as merely playing that role \u8212- a functional displacement rather than ordinary arousal. That is what is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine Traits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with the verse {\i uvaroti mikem hamordim vehaposheim bi} \u8212- I will sift out from you those who rebel and sin against Me. Rabbi Levi reads this as a reference to {\i bnei tisha middot} \u8212- children conceived through nine types of deficient conduct. Where yesterday the {\i Gemara} was asking whether particular physical acts would produce physically flawed children \u8212- a claim resoundingly rejected \u8212- now it asks whether a different kind of deficiency, one of character and relational disposition, will produce children of poor character. The problem has shifted from the physical to the moral, from what kind of act was performed to what kind of relating was occurring between the spouses. The acronym {\i Asnat Mashgaach} encodes the nine types.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear & Hatred\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is {\i bnei eima} \u8212- children conceived when the wife is acting out of fear of her husband. The {\i Ran} explains: {\i lashon yira shehetil eima al ishto lehizakek lo shelo kirtzonah} \u8212- the husband has instilled fear in her such that she submits against her genuine will. Then {\i bnei anusa} \u8212- children of a woman who is literally coerced. Then {\i bnei senuah} \u8212- when he hates her; he feels no love, no care, but treats her as an available means of physical gratification. The {\i Ra\u8217'avad}, in his discussion of this passage, pointedly symmetrizes the case: it is the same whether he hates her or she hates him. Either way, one party is using the other purely for self-gratification rather than relating to them out of love or care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bnei nidui} \u8212- children conceived while the husband is under a ban \u8212- is the one case in this list that stands apart, as it is simply a {\i halakhic} violation unrelated to the relational theme. {\i Bnei tmurah} \u8212- exchange \u8212- refers to a case of confusion, where one believes he is with one wife but is in fact with another. {\i Bnei meriva} \u8212- children of strife \u8212- means they are in the middle of a fight, not as makeup sex after reconciliation, but mid-conflict, where one thinks: we may be fighting, but we might as well have relations. {\i Bnei shichrut} \u8212- intoxication \u8212- means one or both are drunk, according to the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}, and not mentally fully present. {\i Bnei grushat halev} \u8212- divorced in the heart \u8212- means the husband has already decided, internally and unilaterally, to divorce his wife but has not yet told her; he proceeds with intimacy as if the marriage is still intact in spirit, which it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bnei irvuvya} \u8212- children of confusion \u8212- is somewhat unclear: the {\i Ran} understands it as referring to relations with multiple partners, which would make it a case of adultery; Rashi suggests a case where a man cannot distinguish which of his wives he is with, similar to {\i tmurah}. Finally, {\i bnei chatzufa} \u8212- the audacious woman \u8212- we will return to shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the exception of {\i tmurah} and {\i chatzufa}, all of these cases share a single common feature: the spouse is being used as an object of self-gratification rather than genuinely encountered and related to as a person. This is what the {\i Gemara} identifies as the source of harm \u8212- not some physical impropriety, but the failure of genuine relational presence. Yesterday\u8217's discussion was about which physical acts are permitted; today\u8217's conclusion is that the real {\i halakhic} demand is not about the type of act but about whether intimacy is an act of connection or an act of use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Female Initiation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to {\i bnei chatzufa} \u8212- the audacious child \u8212- which the {\i Gemara} understands to mean that the wife demanded relations with her husband. One might have thought this was entirely praiseworthy. Indeed, the {\i Gemara} itself immediately raises the objection: {\i ayni?} Is that really true? Does it not say \u8212- {\i de\u8217'amar Rav Shmuel bar Nachmani amar Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- that any man whose wife is {\i tova\u8217'ato}, who actively solicits him, will have children of extraordinary quality, surpassing even those of the generation of Moshe Rabbeinu? The proof: when Moshe called for men who are {\i chachamim u\u8217'nevonim}, he could not find people of {\i tevunah}. But the tribe of Yissachar, who are {\i yodei binah l\u8217'ittim}, did possess that quality \u8212- and Yissachar was conceived when Leah was {\i tova\u8217'at} Yaakov. The plain implication is that a wife actively soliciting her husband produces exceptional children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the contradiction: {\i hahi d\u8217'martza artzuyei} \u8212- that was a case of seduction and persuasion, not outright demand. Leah seduced; she did not command. The permissibility, even the virtue, lies in {\i martza artzuyei} \u8212- hinting, enticing, drawing close \u8212- not in {\i tova\u8217'at b\u8217'peh}, an explicit verbal demand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction, however, naturally raises the question of where exactly the line lies between the two. The {\i teshuvah} of the {\i Yehuda Ya\u8217'aleh} reasons that since the {\i Gemara} validates what Leah did \u8212- and Leah said {\i elai tavo}, \u8220"come to me,\u8221" which is already quite explicit \u8212- any expression short of an absolute demand (\u8220"you have no choice in the matter\u8221") would fall within the permitted category of {\i martza artzuyei}. What the wife cannot do is strip the husband of any say in the matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Connection & Care\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even so, the very fact that the {\i Gemara} takes Leah\u8217's {\i tova\u8217'at} as a model that produces children greater than those of Moshe\u8217's generation is a striking recalibration of what might have been assumed to be a patriarchal assumption \u8212- that the husband always initiates, that the wife has no voice. The {\i Gemara}, far from affirming that assumption, holds it up and says: not only is female initiative acceptable, it is the paradigm of the ideal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And with that, we complete the {\i perek} with {\i hadran alach ve\u8217'elu mutarin}. The conclusion of this entire extended discussion about marital intimacy is that {\i halakhah} is not primarily concerned with which physical acts are performed. What {\i halakhah} demands is that intimacy be an act of genuine connection \u8212- that each partner be present to and for the other, not using the other as a means to some private end. That is the core of what the {\i Gemara} is teaching about marital relations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to a new area of the laws of {\i nedarim}. So far the {\i masechta} has been dealing largely with questions of interpretation: what words constitute a {\i neder}, how we read ambiguous formulations, to what extent a person\u8217's own interpretation of their language is credited, and so forth. One question that has not yet been addressed directly is that of {\i piv v\u8217'libo shavin} \u8212- the alignment of one\u8217's words and one\u8217's heart. What happens when someone utters the unambiguous language of a {\i neder} but was not truly intending to bind themselves? Not a case of misused words, where one meant {\i cherem} in the sense of a fishing net \u8212- but a case where the language was clearly {\i neder}-language, yet the speaker\u8217's frame of mind was simply not in sync with a genuine intention to be bound. That is what the {\i Mishnah} now addresses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Arba\u8217'ah nedarim hitiru chachamim} \u8212- the sages permitted four categories of vows, meaning these vows are not halakhically binding. The four are: {\i nidrei zeruzin} \u8212- spurring vows; {\i nidrei havai} \u8212- vows of nothingness or hyperbole; {\i nidrei shegagot} \u8212- vows made in error; and {\i nidrei onsin} \u8212- vows made under compulsion. In each case, the speaker used what sounds like {\i neder}-language and was aware that those were {\i neder}-words \u8212- yet because of their particular frame of mind at the time, the vow does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spurring Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The example of {\i nidrei zeruzin}: a seller says {\i konam she-ani pocheis lach min ha-sela} \u8212- this object should be forbidden to you if I sell it for less than a {\i sela} (four {\i zuz}). The buyer counters: {\i konam she-ani mosif lach al ha-shekel} \u8212- it should be forbidden to me if I pay more than a {\i shekel} (two {\i zuz}). {\i Shneihem rotzim b\u8217'shloshah dinarim} \u8212- both of them are in fact aiming for three {\i dinarim}. Neither side actually intends to forbid anything; the vow-language is deployed as a haggling tool, a way of pressing the other side. Since neither party is genuinely serious about binding themselves, the vows do not take hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a question about the {\i tanna} behind this {\i mishnah}. Rav Abba bar Mamal reports in the name of Rabbi Yehuda Nesi\u8217'ah (the grandson of Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi) that the author is Rabbi Yehuda bar Ilai, who said in the name of Rabbi Tarfon: {\i l\u8217'olam eino nazir lefi shelo nitnah nezirut ela l\u8217'hafla\u8217'ah} \u8212- a conditional vow of {\i nezirut} never takes effect because {\i nezirut} requires {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, precision and absoluteness at the moment of utterance. The relevance: the {\i zeruzin} case involves a conditional structure (\u8220"if I go lower than\u8230 ?\u8221"), and Rabbi Yehuda bar Ilai may therefore be reading the permissibility as stemming from the conditionality itself, not from lack of seriousness. Even a completely serious conditional {\i neder} might not be binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects: {\i afilu teima rabanan} \u8212- one can explain this even according to the {\i rabbanan} who disagree with Rabbi Yehuda bar Ilai on conditional {\i nezirut}. The {\i mishnah} does not say {\i shneihem ratzu}, \u8220"both wanted the same middle outcome and the conditions were therefore never really triggered.\u8221" It says {\i shneihem rotzim}, they {\i want} the same thing \u8212- meaning from the outset, they never intended the vow to be binding at all. That is the reason it is not a {\i neder}: not some technical feature of conditionality, but the straightforward absence of genuine intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina then poses a sharper case to Rav Ashi: what if the seller said he will not go lower than {\i more than a sela} \u8212- say, four {\i zuz} and a fraction \u8212- and the buyer said he will not go higher than {\i less than a shekel}, say one {\i zuz} and a fraction? The parties are now being quite precise; they are not using round numbers. Precision in a financial negotiation might suggest genuine seriousness \u8212- the more exact your formulation, the more it looks like a real commitment rather than theatrical haggling. Perhaps in that case the vow {\i would} be binding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exaggeration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds by pointing to a {\i baraita}: if someone was being persistently pressed by his friend to come eat at his house and finally said {\i konam beitecha she-ani nichnas}, \u8220"your house should be forbidden to me \u8212- I won\u8217't enter it,\u8221" or even {\i tipat tzonen she-ani to\u8217'eim}, \u8220"I won\u8217't drink even a drop of cold water by you\u8221" \u8212- in such a case the person {\i is} permitted to enter the house and drink cold water. {\i Shelo nitkavein zeh ela l\u8217'shum achilah u-shtiyah} \u8212- the speaker\u8217's genuine intention went no further than refusing to participate in the meal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} explains: {\i af al gav d\u8217'nachis l\u8217'diyuka} \u8212- even though the person went down to minute precision, specifying \u8220"a drop of cold water,\u8221" and thereby distanced himself far from what his friend wanted, nevertheless he did not literally mean it. The language of a single drop is recognizable as hyperbole, not as a real commitment to avoiding the house altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case raises a significant point: do we say that the exaggerated extensions simply fall away while the core intention remains binding, or that since the entire formulation was hyperbolic nothing is binding at all? The {\i baraita} implies the former: the person is permitted to enter the house and to drink water, but is presumably still bound by the core refusal to sit down for a meal. The exaggerated shell dissolves, but what the speaker genuinely intended \u8212- declining the invitation to eat \u8212- remains. That is a notable {\i chiddush}: {\i neder}-annulment is not always all-or-nothing; the exaggerated perimeter can be stripped away to reveal and enforce the actual intent at the core.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of precision: the {\i Gemara} distinguishes. {\i Tzaddikim omrim me\u8217'at v\u8217'osim harbeh} \u8212- righteous people promise little and deliver much. A person who says \u8220"I will give you just a drop of water\u8221" and then produces a feast is not being imprecise; he is using the conventional idiom of understatement. Inversely, someone who says he won\u8217't even accept a drop is using the conventional idiom of overstatement. In both cases the speaker\u8217's actual intention diverges from the literal words, and this is recognized social usage. The precise number in the haggling scenario, however, may be different \u8212- there it might genuinely signal that the speaker means exactly what he said. The {\i Gemara} concludes: {\i teiku}. The question of whether extreme numerical precision in a {\i zeruzin}-type context creates a real {\i neder} remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakhic Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda then reports in the name of Rav Assi: {\i arba\u8217'ah nedarim hallalu tzerichin she\u8217'eilah lehacham} \u8212- these four categories of vows still require formal release by a {\i hacham}. Even though the {\i chachamim} declared them permissible, one should still go through the process of {\i hatarat nedarim} to avoid treating vow-language lightly. When Rav Assi reported this ruling before Shmuel, Shmuel challenged it: the {\i tanna} explicitly teaches that the {\i chachamim permitted} these vows \u8212- which implies they are automatically non-binding and require no formal release. How can you say they require a {\i hacham}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In light of this challenge, Rav Yosef teaches the tradition in a different formulation that does not carry the same vulnerability: {\i ein chacham rashai lehattir ella ke\u8217'ein arba\u8217'ah nedarim hallalu} \u8212- a {\i hacham} is not permitted to release a {\i neder} unless it approximates one of these four categories. This version of the statement shifts the function of the four categories: rather than being cases that themselves require release, they become the paradigm by which all {\i hatarat nedarim} is measured. A {\i hacham} releasing any {\i neder} must be able to bring the case into alignment with one of these four models.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vow Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Model of Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that formulation \u8212- {\i ein chacham rashai lehattir ella ke\u8217'ein arba\u8217'ah nedarim hallalu} \u8212- we arrive at the heart of the {\i halachah} of {\i hatarat nedarim}. The {\i Gemara} now uses these four categories as the template for understanding what {\i hatarat nedarim} is doing and how it works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider the case: a person comes to a {\i hacham} to be released from a {\i neder} they clearly meant at the time. The question is, on what basis can the {\i hacham} permit it? The underlying logic of the four categories is that the person, in each case, was never truly and fully intending for the vow to be binding. In the haggling case this is obvious to everyone \u8212- no one seriously meant to forbid anything. But that same underlying structure \u8212- \u8220"at the time, I did not fully mean it to be binding\u8221" \u8212- is what the {\i hacham} must be able to reconstruct in any case where he releases a {\i neder}. The case before him will of course have appeared, from the outside, to be a genuine {\i neder}. That is why it needs a {\i hacham}. But the {\i hacham}\u8217's work is to find the point at which the person\u8217's actual frame of mind at the time was not fully in sync with the binding force of what he said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Petach & Charata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the central distinction in {\i hatarat nedarim} between two modes of release: {\i petach} and {\i charata}. A {\i petach} \u8212- an \u8220"opening\u8221" \u8212- is the identification of some consideration that the person had not thought through at the time of the vow; had they considered it, they would not have made the vow. A {\i charata} \u8212- \u8220"regret\u8221" \u8212- is the person\u8217's retrospective wish that they had never made the vow at all, independent of any specific unconsidered circumstance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relationship between {\i petach} and the {\i arba\u8217'ah nedarim} model is clear: the {\i petach} says, \u8220"you never fully meant it, because had you thought it through, you wouldn\u8217't have done it.\u8221" That is structurally analogous to the haggling case \u8212- in both situations, the person\u8217's genuine will, properly understood, was not behind the vow. {\i Charata}, by contrast, concedes that at the time the person fully intended and meant the vow \u8212- but now, in hindsight, wishes they had not. That is a different kind of claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Starting w/ Petach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Hence the principle \u8212- which follows directly from {\i ein chacham rashai lehattir ella ke\u8217'ein arba\u8217'ah nedarim hallalu} \u8212- of {\i ein potchin b\u8217'charata}: one does not open a {\i hatarat nedarim} process by asking the person whether they regret making the vow. You start with {\i petach}. You try to find some circumstance the person had not fully considered, such that you can say: had you thought this through, you would not have made the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Charata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} draws an important further distinction within {\i charata} itself. There is {\i charata me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- regret from the root \u8212- which means the person wishes they had never made the vow at all. And there is {\i charata dehashtah} \u8212- regret of now \u8212- which simply means that the person no longer wants to be bound going forward. The {\i Ran} notes that the second is not {\i charata} in any meaningful sense at all: every person who shows up seeking release from a {\i neder} no longer wants to be bound. That is why they came. True {\i charata} requires that the person genuinely wish that the vow had never been made in the first place \u8212- a retroactive reframing of the original moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a series of cases. Someone came before Rav Huna, who asked him: {\i libecha alecha?} \u8212- is your heart upon you? The person said: no. And Rav Huna released him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} explains this question as probing the person\u8217's frame of mind. The {\i Ran} paraphrases: {\i haya libecha meyushav alecha b\u8217'sha\u8217'ah shenadarta} \u8212- were you of settled mind when you made the vow? {\i V\u8217'gam achshav eincha toheh b\u8217'nidrekha ad hayom} \u8212- and have you also had no regret about it until now? {\i Ela shemehayom ulehalan ata rotzeh lachzor bach} \u8212- or is it only from today onward that you want to withdraw? That would be {\i charata dehashtah}, not {\i charata me\u8217'ikara}, and would not suffice. But if the person\u8217's answer of \u8220"no\u8221" means: no, I am {\i not} only changing my mind now \u8212- I actually regret ever having made it \u8212- then that is {\i charata me\u8217'ikara}, and Rav Huna releases him. This is therefore a case where {\i charata} is used as the basis for release, not {\i petach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this stands in tension with the principle of {\i ein potchin b\u8217'charata}. The answer appears to be that {\i ein potchin b\u8217'charata} means you do not {\i start} with {\i charata} \u8212- you prefer to find a {\i petach} \u8212- but {\i charata} remains a valid basis for release when it is genuine {\i charata me\u8217'ikara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Ground\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar bar Chana took a somewhat different approach in his case. He asked the person: {\i ilu hayu asara bnei adam sheyefaysucha b\u8217'ota sha\u8217'a, mi nadarta?} \u8212- if ten people had been there to calm you down and persuade you at the time, would you still have made the vow? The person said no, and Rabba bar bar Chana released him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} identifies this as occupying a middle ground between {\i petach} and {\i charata}. It is more than pure {\i charata} \u8212- rather than simply asking whether you regret it, it asks whether you could have been persuaded otherwise at the very moment of the vow. That slightly retrojects the counterfactual into the original moment, getting closer to the idea that the vow was not fully intended. But it falls short of a standard {\i petach}, which identifies a concrete circumstance the person had failed to consider \u8212- an internal lapse of reasoning rather than an external persuasion that might have intervened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's formulation: {\i omrim lo l\u8217'adam, lev zeh alecha} \u8212- you say to a person: is this same heart upon you? If he says no, you permit him. The {\i Ran} explains this similarly to Rav Huna: you are asking whether the person is simply changing their mind now, or whether they genuinely feel, in retrospect, that the vow should never have been made. \u8220"No, this is not the same heart I have now\u8221" \u8212- meaning: I am not the same person in the same state of mind who made that vow; I deeply wish it had never happened \u8212- is {\i charata me\u8217'ikara}, and suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a practical point. The principle {\i ein potchin b\u8217'charata} means that a {\i hacham} should not begin with the {\i charata} question \u8212- not because {\i charata} never works, but because we take the language of vows seriously, and the preferred approach is to locate a {\i petach} that shows the person\u8217's genuine will was never fully behind the vow. {\i Charata} is harder \u8212- it concedes the full intentionality of the original moment and asks for release on the basis of subsequent regret. It works, but it is not where one begins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi asked a person who came before him: {\i kiduta shita} \u8212- are you astounded at what you originally did? The person said no \u8212- meaning, effectively, of course, how could I not be regretful of it? \u8212- and Rav Assi released him. The {\i Ran} notes the somewhat puzzling idiom: the person answers in the negative to what seems to be a question whose affirmative answer would grant release. The {\i Ran} suggests it was a rhetorical formulation \u8212- \u8220"am I {\i not} astounded?\u8221" \u8212- but in any event the substance is clear: this is another formulation aimed at eliciting {\i charata me\u8217'ikara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Someone came before Rabbi Eliezer, who asked: {\i be\u8217'ita nadur?} \u8212- do you want your vow to remain? The person replied: {\i ilu lo margizin li, lo be\u8217'inan klum} \u8212- had they not angered me at that time, I would never have wanted any of this. Rabbi Eliezer said: {\i tehei k\u8217'va\u8217'is} \u8212- let it be as you wished. The vow is released, because you only made it in anger and genuinely wish those circumstances had never arisen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final case: a woman had made her daughter forbidden to benefit from her \u8212- a severe vow of estrangement. She came before Rabbi Yochanan to be released. Rabbi Yochanan asked her: {\i ilu avita yadat d\u8217'amrin megirataikh, alu d\u8217'bartakh, ilu lo chama bat imma milin d\u8217'aziva, bechdi lo adarta} \u8212- had you known that your neighbors would say, \u8220"Look at her daughter \u8212- if her own mother made such a vow against her, the daughter must have done terrible things worthy of abandonment,\u8221" would you have made the vow for no reason? The woman said no, and Rabbi Yochanan released her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a clear case of {\i petach}. Had the woman stopped to think through the social implications of her vow \u8212- what it would signal to the neighbors about her daughter\u8217's character \u8212- she would never have made it. She was acting on anger without fully thinking through all the consequences. The {\i petach} here is not an internal circumstance she failed to consider but an external social reality: the vow itself, once made, would broadcast a devastating statement about her daughter to the world. That unconsidered implication is the {\i petach} through which Rabbi Yochanan opens the release. And it is a perfect illustration of the paradigm: you did not fully mean it, because had you fully thought it through, you would not have done it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 22\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Charata vs.\u160 ?Pesach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Charata and Pesach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the midst of a discussion about the practical process of {\i hataras nedarim} \u8212- releasing individuals from vows. Two foundational concepts govern this process. The first is {\i charata}: simple retrospective regret, the sense that \u8220"I wish I had never done this.\u8221" The second is {\i pesach}: a retroactive disavowal grounded in an element of error, the sense that \u8220"had I been thinking clearly at the time, I never would have made this vow in the first place.\u8221" This latter concept resembles the {\i arba nedarim} of our {\i Mishna}, where it is clear from the outset that the vow-taker did not fully mean what he said. The {\i pesach}, by contrast, involves demonstrating in hindsight that had the person been fully deliberate, he would not have intended the vow \u8212- making it a subtler but related form of the same underlying principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening with Charata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since {\i pesach} is the more substantive and concrete basis for release \u8212- it demonstrates with greater precision why the vow was never truly binding \u8212- one might assume it is always required. The question the {\i Gemara} is working through, then, is whether {\i potchin b\u8217'charata}: whether one may open the process of {\i hataras nedarim} with simple regret, or whether a specific {\i pesach} is always necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yannai Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heaven Opens Ledger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents a series of cases in which different {\i Amoraim} released vows, some on the basis of {\i charata} and others on the basis of {\i pesach}. We now pick up three lines from the top of 22a. The grandson of Rabbi Yannai came before his grandfather seeking release from a vow. Rabbi Yannai opened with the following: \u8220"Had you known that when one takes a vow, the heavenly ledger is opened and your deeds are scrutinized \u8212- that the act of vowing triggers a divine audit of your entire conduct \u8212- would you have made the vow?\u8221" The grandson replied that he would not, and Rabbi Yannai thereupon released him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba identifies the scriptural basis for this principle: {\i ve-acharei nedarim levaker} \u8212- \u8220"after vows you will be inspected.\u8221" The logic is that the vow-taker implicitly considers himself righteous enough to go beyond what the {\i Torah} has forbidden. The {\i Torah}, accordingly, responds by scrutinizing whether the person truly merits that self-assessment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Too Generic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is interesting on several levels simultaneously. At one level it concerns the formal {\i halakha} of how {\i hataras nedarim} operates. At another level it reflects the idea that the process depends on {\i pifo v\u8217'libo shavin} \u8212- the mouth and heart must be in agreement \u8212- so that a {\i chacham} can inquire into whether the vow-taker truly and fully intended the vow. And at yet another level, the case serves as an occasion to explore the religious status of vowing in general.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet whatever its virtues, Rabbi Yannai\u8217's opening is entirely generic. There is nothing case-specific about it: any vow-taker, in any situation, could be told that taking a vow triggers heavenly scrutiny. A proper {\i pesach} is meant to be concrete and particular \u8212- \u8220"had you been thinking about {\i this} specific consideration, you would not have made {\i this} vow.\u8221" Rabbi Yannai\u8217's formulation, whatever its rhetorical power, does not achieve that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rejecting Scare Tactics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Heavenly Ledger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rules explicitly: even though Rabbi Yannai opened with the heavenly ledger, we do not open this way. The objection is twofold. First, as noted, it is too generic to function as a genuine {\i pesach}. Second, and crucially, the Ran explains that such an opening is a scare tactic: by confronting the petitioner with the specter of divine punishment, you may frighten him into saying he regrets the vow even if he does not genuinely feel that way. The danger is that you will elicit an answer that reflects fear rather than authentic reconsideration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bamah Statement Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} extends this ruling to another formulation. We also do not open with what was taught in a {\i braisa} in the name of Rabbi Natan: {\i ha-noder ke\u8217'ilu bana bamah, ve-ha-mekaymo ke\u8217'ilu makriv alav korban} \u8212- one who makes a vow is as if he built an unauthorized altar ({\i bamah}), and one who goes through with fulfilling it is as if he offered a sacrifice upon it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bamah Philosophy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking and philosophically rich statement about the nature of vowing. The {\i bamah} metaphor deserves careful attention. The person who makes a vow is attempting to do more than what the {\i Torah} demands \u8212- to adopt additional prohibitions beyond those the {\i Torah} has already established. The implicit assumption is that self-denial is the paradigmatic religious act, so that doing more of it constitutes a higher level of piety. But this reasoning reflects a fundamental misunderstanding of what religious practice is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i bamah} comparison makes precisely this point. During a period when all sacrifice must be offered at the central {\i Beit HaMikdash}, building a private altar is not an act of greater piety \u8212- it is a transgression. Religion, in this view, is not a private, self-initiated enterprise but a communal, God-defined one. One does not earn credit by going off on one\u8217's own and constructing a personal religious supplement. And of course, the sin compounds itself: building the {\i bamah} is one violation, but actually offering upon it is a further and graver one. By analogy, making the vow is already problematic, but fulfilling it only deepens the problem \u8212- which is precisely why seeking {\i hataras nedarim} is so important.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It bears noting that there were historical periods when {\i bamot} were permissible \u8212- when there was no central {\i Beit HaMikdash}, private altars served a legitimate religious function. One can understand why a person might feel that the institutionalized framework of religion does not fully answer his spiritual needs, and that some personal religious initiative is called for. The {\i Gemara} is not denying that impulse entirely. But the specific context here \u8212- making things forbidden upon oneself beyond what the {\i Torah} requires \u8212- is the target of the critique.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran addresses this directly. He writes that the reason we compare the vow-taker to one who builds a {\i bamah} is precisely because the vow-taker believes he is performing a {\i mitzvah}. His reasoning runs as follows: the {\i Torah} prohibits many things, and since the prohibition of things is itself a religious value, he can make himself even more religious by prohibiting yet more things upon himself. The {\i Gemara} \u8212- and the Ran in expounding it \u8212- declares this a fundamental error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran draws on a passage in the Yerushalmi in the perek {\i potchin lo}: \u8220"Is it not enough what the {\i Torah} has forbidden, that you must go and make more things forbidden upon yourself?\u8221" This is a trenchant formulation. The Rambam echoes this sentiment extensively in his discussions of the golden mean and the dangers of excessive self-denial: more restriction is not always a religious good, and may in fact be a religious failure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran adds a further dimension specific to the {\i neder} as a legal form: a {\i neder} draws its force from the {\i korban} model, since the prohibiting formula {\i konam} renders an object like a {\i korban}. If that quasi-{\i korban} is not desired by God \u8212- because the vow was not a legitimate religious act \u8212- then it is as though one has offered a sacrifice outside the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. Moreover, the Ran notes, the end of the {\i braisa} \u8212- \u8220"one who fulfills the vow is as if he offered a sacrifice upon the {\i bamah}\u8221" \u8212- can function as a {\i pesach} in the positive sense: to get out of the vow is to stop the process of bringing this unwanted quasi-sacrifice. Not seeking {\i hataras nedarim} means continuing to \u8220"offer\u8221" on an unauthorized altar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye-Rava Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite all this, a debate arises as to whether we may at least open with the {\i beginning} of the statement \u8212- that making the vow was like building a {\i bamah} \u8212- even if we do not use the {\i end} of the statement about offering a sacrifice upon it. Building a {\i bamah} does not involve the same severity as actually bringing a sacrifice upon it; perhaps we can use the milder comparison without the scare-tactic concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye says we open with the beginning of the statement; Rava says we do not. There is also a version of this dispute attributed differently to Rav Tavyumi, in which everybody agrees we do not open with the end \u8212- that it would be like bringing a {\i korban} outside the {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- but Abaye and Rava dispute the beginning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i halakha} is: {\i lo patchinin lo bereisha velo besayfa} \u8212- we open with neither the beginning nor the end. We do not say it is like building a {\i bamah}, and certainly do not say it is like bringing a {\i korban} upon it. The upshot is that we do not open with any of these scare-tactic formulations, however instructive they may be as theological statements about the nature of vowing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Anti-Scare Tactics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Vow-Keepers Wick\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: we also do not open with the statement of Shmuel. Shmuel taught that even one who fulfills a vow is called a {\i rasha} \u8212- a wicked person. This too would function as an extreme scare tactic: even going through with your obligation is sinful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse on Ceasing Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu identifies the verse: {\i ve-chi techdal lindor lo yihyeh becha cheit} \u8212- \u8220"if you cease to make vows, there will be no sin in you.\u8221" He then applies a {\i gezeira shava}, linking the word {\i chadala} (ceasing) in this verse to the same word appearing elsewhere: {\i sham reshaim chadlu rogez} \u8212- \u8220"there the wicked have ceased their anger and discord.\u8221" The linguistic connection associates the cessation of vowing with the domain of the {\i resha\u8217'im}, confirming that one who makes vows and fulfills them is in some sense counted among the wicked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef notes that the same idea appears in material we ourselves have studied: {\i kenidrei kesherim lo amar klum} \u8212- if someone says \u8220"this should be binding upon me like the vows of upright people,\u8221" he has said nothing, because upright people do not make vows. But {\i kenidrei resha\u8217'im} \u8212- like the vows of wicked people \u8212- that is a binding formula, invoking {\i nazir}, {\i korban}, or {\i shvua}. Thus even the {\i Mishnayos} themselves encode the assumption that vow-taking is the province of the wicked, while those who are truly righteous do not vow at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader conclusion of this entire section is that the {\i Gemara} in {\i Nedarim} consistently presents vowing in a negative light \u8212- not as the only view in {\i Chazal}, but certainly as the dominant thrust of this {\i masechet}. The statements cited as potential {\i pesakim} are rejected not because they are wrong, but because they would function as scare tactics, potentially inducing people to claim regret they do not genuinely feel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Anger Digression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gehinnom & Anger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now pivots into what it frames as a digression \u8212- though the connections to the surrounding material are not entirely incidental. Since the previous discussion mentioned {\i resha\u8217'im} and their anger, the {\i Gemara} transitions into a series of statements about the consequences of anger. Rabbi Yonatan \u8212- or Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- teaches: {\i kol ha-ko\u8217'es kol minei gehenom sholtim bo} \u8212- anyone who gets angry, all forms of {\i gehenom} take hold of him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse on Anger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scriptural basis comes from {\i Kohelet}: {\i haseir kaas milibecha veha\u8217'aveir ra\u8217'ah mibsarecha} \u8212- \u8220"remove anger from your heart and remove evil from your flesh.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} identifies {\i ra\u8217'ah} with {\i gehenom}, citing: {\i vegam rasha leyom ra\u8217'ah} \u8212- \u8220"and also the wicked person is destined for a day of evil\u8221" \u8212- where the \u8220"day of evil\u8221" denotes a concrete punishment awaiting him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing over what the {\i Gemara} means here. The text of {\i Kohelet} is saying something relatively straightforward: anger eats away at you internally, affects how you think and act, and corrodes your life from within. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's more graphic formulation \u8212- that all forms of {\i gehenom} control the angry person \u8212- may well be understood not as a description of otherworldly punishment but as a vivid depiction of the natural consequences of rage. Anger makes a person irrational; it leads him to transgress things he would never otherwise violate; it burns him from within and destroys his relationships and his better judgment. That is the {\i peshat} of the {\i pasuk} in {\i Kohelet}, rendered in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's characteristic style.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adds a further consequence: {\i ha-tachtoniyot sholtot bo} \u8212- hemorrhoids take hold of him as well. The {\i pasuk} is brought from {\i Devarim}: {\i venatan Hashem lecha sham lev ragaz} \u8212- God will give you there an angry heart \u8212- along with {\i vechilyon einayim} (dimming of the eyes) and {\i vedaavon nefesh} (languishing of the soul). The {\i Gemara} asks: what is the ailment that simultaneously afflicts the eyes and pains the soul? The answer: hemorrhoids. The connection to {\i kaas} and internal burning is the underlying physiological intuition \u8212- that extreme emotional agitation produces physical inflammation and internal distress. Indeed, the entire phenomenon here reflects a sophisticated understanding that emotional states have concrete bodily consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuza Murder Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents a striking narrative illustrating where unchecked anger leads. When Ulla traveled up to {\i Eretz Yisrael}, two men from Chuza accompanied him on the journey. During the trip, one of them fell into a violent rage and killed the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Fear Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The murderer then turned to Ulla and asked: {\i ya\u8217'u avdi?} \u8212- \u8220"Did I do well? Was it all right what I did?\u8221" Ulla, fearing for his own life, replied yes, and \u8212- depending on the textual variant \u8212- either himself exposed the neck of the murder victim or told the murderer to do so. When Ulla later came before Rabbi Yochanan, he expressed concern: {\i dilma chas veshalom achzeiki} \u8212- \u8220"perhaps, God forbid, I strengthened the hands of a sinner by affirming what he did?\u8221" Rabbi Yochanan reassured him: {\i nafshecha hatzalta} \u8212- you saved yourself. Under normal circumstances such a response would be impermissible, but the imperative of self-preservation overrides it here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also raise a related question about the instruction to expose the murder victim\u8217's neck \u8212- whether hastening the death of someone already dying might touch on the {\i halachic} question of {\i chayei sha\u8217'ah}, and whether the imperative of {\i chiyuv hatzala} applies differently to someone whose life is terminal. These are complex questions that belong to the {\i poskim}, but the narrative at least provides a concrete case around which to think through the issues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exile Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What astonished Rabbi Yochanan was not Ulla\u8217's conduct but the fact that such murderous anger could have erupted at all. The verse says {\i venatan Hashem lecha sham lev ragaz} \u8212- God will give you {\i there}, in exile, an angry heart. The context is {\i Bavel}, the place of exile. How, then, could such anger manifest before crossing the Jordan into {\i Eretz Yisrael}? Ulla replied: {\i hahu sha\u8217'ata lo avrinan Yardena} \u8212- at that moment we had not yet crossed the Jordan; we were still in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, and so the condition of exile-driven anger still applied. This is somewhat ironic given what is known historically of the intense sectarian passions that characterized life in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, but the {\i Gemara} reads the verse as limiting this particular form of rage to the exilic condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anger Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with several additional statements about the consequences of anger. Rabbah bar bar Chana in the name of Rabbi Yochanan teaches: {\i afilu Shechina eina chashuva k\u8217'negdo} \u8212- when a person is angry, he does not even care about God\u8217's presence. The {\i pasuk} cited: {\i rasha k\u8217'govah apo bal yidrosh ein Elokim kol mizmosav} \u8212- the wicked person, in the height of his anger, does not seek God; God is absent from all his thoughts. This rings psychologically true: rage is totalizing. Nothing matters to the person in its grip \u8212- not prior commitments, not relationships, not religious obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yirmiyah of Difti adds: anger causes a person to forget his Torah learning and to become increasingly foolish. The verse: {\i ki cha\u8217'as b\u8217'cheik k\u8217'silim yanu\u8217'ach} \u8212- anger rests in the bosom of fools. And Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak adds a diagnostic dimension: if a person gets angry, it is a known indicator that his sins outweigh his merits. The verse: {\i u-va\u8217'al cheima rav pasha} \u8212- the person given to anger has abundant transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sin and Scripture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ada, son of Rav Chanina, offers a remarkable observation. Had Israel not sinned in the {\i cheit ha-miraglim} \u8212- the sin of the spies \u8212- they would have received nothing more than the five books of the {\i Torah} and the book of {\i Yehoshua} alone. Why {\i Yehoshua}? Because {\i Yehoshua} addresses the value and the division of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, providing narrative closure to the {\i Torah}\u8217's foundational story of the people entering the land. But the rest of {\i Tanach} \u8212- the {\i Nevi\u8217'im Acharonim} in particular \u8212- exists primarily to deliver {\i mussar} to {\i Bnei Yisrael} for their repeated failures. Had they not sinned, all the religious wisdom they needed would have been contained in the five books of Moshe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse cited: {\i ki berov chochma rov kaas} \u8212- for in the abundance of wisdom there is an abundance of anger. The accumulation of wisdom through the prophetic books was itself a product of the accumulation of sin, which required a divine response. There is something simultaneously humbling and moving in this formulation: the magnificent corpus of {\i Nevi\u8217'im} that contains some of the most eloquent articulations of Torah values exists precisely because the people needed to be called back from their failures. The wisdom was real, but its occasion was loss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elohei Yisrael Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns now to the main discussion. Rav Assi teaches: {\i ein nizkakim l\u8217'Elohei Yisrael} \u8212- we do not attend to vows made in the name of the God of Israel, with one exception: {\i shalom bayis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Vow Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The illustrative case: {\i konam ishti nehenet li} \u8212- \u8220"may my wife derive no benefit from me\u8221" \u8212- taken because she stole his wallet or struck his son. If it then becomes clear that she did neither of those things, the question of how to release the vow arises. One might note that if he explicitly stated these false premises as the basis of the vow, it could well be a case of {\i nidrei shegagot} \u8212- vows taken on false premises \u8212- which are inherently non-binding and would not require formal {\i hataras nedarim} at all. The Ran discusses this point and some commentators accordingly bracket that portion of the case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder or Shvua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a significant interpretive dispute as to what precisely is meant when someone vows \u8220"in the name of {\i Elokei Yisrael}.\u8221" The Ran, along with {\i Tosfot} and the Rambam, understands this as referring to a {\i shvua} \u8212- an oath. Taking an oath in God\u8217's name is a distinct and weightier act than an ordinary {\i neder}, which derives its force from the {\i korban} framework rather than from invoking God directly. On this reading, the passage is discussing a situation that arose in the context of {\i hataras nedarim} but actually involves the question of whether a {\i chacham} can release a {\i shvua} taken in God\u8217's name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to the broader dispute between {\i Beit Shamai} and {\i Beit Hillel}: {\i Beit Shamai} holds that one never performs {\i hatara} for a {\i shvua}, since a {\i shvua} engages God\u8217's name and is about one\u8217's personal integrity and honesty in a way that a {\i neder} is not; {\i Beit Hillel} holds that {\i hatara} is available for a {\i shvua} as well. According to Rav Assi, even {\i Beit Hillel} would agree that a {\i shvua} taken specifically in the name of {\i Elokei Yisrael} is too weighty to release \u8212- unless it stands in the way of {\i shalom bayis}. The Ran draws a beautiful parallel: just as the {\i midrash} teaches that God allows His name written in holiness to be erased in water in order to make peace between husband and wife (in the {\i sotah} procedure), so too a {\i shvua} taken in His name can be undone for the sake of marital peace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi, however, reads the case differently: the formula {\i konam ishti nehenet li} is explicitly {\i lashon neder}, the language of a vow, and so we are speaking of an actual {\i neder} \u8212- but one made with God\u8217's name invoked alongside it, creating a hybrid between the {\i neder} and {\i shvua} forms. Even without the full weight of a {\i shvua}, using God\u8217's name in the context of a {\i neder} adds sufficient gravity that we are reluctant to release it absent a compelling reason like {\i shalom bayis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi to Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the woman comes before Rav Assi to seek release, he says to her: {\i i nadart b\u8217'mohi, mizdakkekna lach} \u8212- had you used the term {\i mohi}, which functions as a {\i kinuy} (euphemism) for an oath, I would have attended to you and found a way to release you. {\i Hashta d\u8217'lo nadart b\u8217'mohi ela b\u8217'Elokei Yisrael, lo mizdakkekna lach} \u8212- but since you used not a {\i kinuy} but the name {\i Elokei Yisrael} itself, I cannot help you. The terminological distinction carries decisive {\i halachic} weight: a {\i kinuy} or euphemism for a {\i shvua} is releasable; a vow or oath taken explicitly in the divine name is not \u8212- except for {\i shalom bayis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The confusion in terminology throughout this passage \u8212- the word {\i nadart} used to describe what appears to be a {\i shvua} \u8212- is itself revealing. The {\i Gemara} is not always precise in distinguishing between {\i nedarim} and {\i shvuot}, a point the Ran explicitly acknowledges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kahana-Yosef Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents a telling anecdote. Rav Kahana came to the home of Rav Yosef, who offered him something to eat. Rav Kahana declined: {\i mari kula \u8212- lo ta\u8217'imna} \u8212- in the name of the Master of all, I will not eat anything. Rav Yosef replied in kind: {\i lo mari kula lo ta\u8217'imna lei} \u8212- in the name of the Master of all, I will not feed you anything. The {\i Gemara} notes that this makes sense from Rav Kahana\u8217's side \u8212- he took a {\i shvua} not to eat. But why did Rav Yosef need to invoke God\u8217's name at all? The {\i Gemara} concludes that Rav Yosef was not taking a new {\i shvua} of his own, but was rather communicating: since you have invoked God\u8217's name in your {\i shvua} not to eat, I am constrained \u8212- I cannot help you out of it. I am therefore unable to give you anything to eat. The story is instructive in demonstrating how casually and consequentially God\u8217's name was being invoked in everyday situations \u8212- something the {\i Gemara} clearly views as problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakha & Geonic Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now delivers the bottom-line {\i halakha} through Rav Nachman: {\i hilchata potchin b\u8217'charata venizkakim l\u8217'Elohei Yisrael} \u8212- the law is that we open with {\i charata} (simple regret), and we do attend to vows taken in the name of {\i Elohei Yisrael}. Both rulings cut in the direction of accessibility and leniency: we do not require a specific {\i pesach}, and we do not treat the invocation of God\u8217's name as an absolute bar to release.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran clarifies the scope of Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling: {\i potchin b\u8217'charata bein b\u8217'neder bein b\u8217'shvua} \u8212- we open with {\i charata} for both a {\i neder} and a {\i shvua}; and {\i nizkakim nami l\u8217'chatchila l\u8217'Elohei Yisrael} \u8212- we attend {\i l\u8217'chatchila} even to vows taken in God\u8217's name. The Ran further adds that this applies even if the person was holding a {\i Sefer Torah} while taking the oath. Being in physical contact with a {\i Sefer Torah} during an oath is a psychological intensifier \u8212- analogous to placing one\u8217's hand on a Bible \u8212- but it does not alter the fundamental {\i halachic} analysis. The {\i Sefer Torah} is relevant in the context of {\i shvuat ha-dayanim}, a court-administered oath, where such tangible contact is used to heighten solemnity; it does not render the {\i shvua} irrevocable for purposes of {\i hatara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gaon Disagreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran records that an unnamed {\i Gaon} ruled that one who swears in the name of the {\i Sefer Torah} or the {\i Aseret Ha-Dibrot} has no release ever \u8212- {\i ein lo hafara olamit}. The Ran dismisses this position unequivocally: {\i leita le-milta klal} \u8212- it has no standing whatsoever. The {\i din} is the {\i din} of the {\i Gemara}, which releases even {\i shvuot} through {\i charata}, and {\i kol she-ken} for {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hai Gaon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Hai Gaon takes a more measured but still significantly stricter position. Against the plain ruling of the {\i Gemara}, Rav Hai holds that for {\i nedarim} one must open with a {\i pesach} \u8212- not {\i charata}. His reasoning is that we should practice as Rav Ashi described, releasing vows only in a manner analogous to the {\i arba nedarim}: there must be a genuine ground for retroactive disavowal. {\i Charata} alone is too vague. Furthermore, Rav Hai holds that for {\i shvuot}, even a {\i pesach} is insufficient \u8212- we never release a {\i shvua} except in a case of {\i davar mitzvah} or {\i shalom bayis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Yehudai Gaon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Yehudai Gaon goes further still. In his view, {\i nedarim} are to be treated like {\i shvuot}: we do not release either except in cases of {\i mitzvah} or {\i shalom bayis}, and even then only through a genuine {\i pesach}, not {\i charata}. He adds that the {\i pesach} in question must be one established by a {\i chacham} \u8212- {\i petach d\u8217'chacham} \u8212- not the husband\u8217's unilateral {\i petach} ({\i petach d\u8217'ba\u8217'al}), which is a different mechanism belonging to the realm of {\i hafara} rather than {\i hatara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both the Rambam and the {\i Shulchan Aruch} rule in accordance with the {\i Gemara}, not the {\i Geonim}. Their position is that we are always available to release someone from either a {\i neder} or a {\i shvua}, whether taken in God\u8217's name or not, whether through {\i pesach} or through {\i charata}, and that no additional barriers beyond what the {\i Gemara} itself establishes should be placed in the path of one seeking release. The {\i Geonim} may have had policy reasons for their stringency \u8212- making vows feel weightier so that people would not take them lightly, knowing release would always be available \u8212- but as a matter of {\i ikar hadin}, their positions were not accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a real tension embedded in this entire question. The easier it is to obtain release from a vow, the more it may encourage people to make vows casually. Yet the harder it is to obtain release, the more trapped people become in obligations that may be causing them real harm. The {\i Gemara} resolved this tension decisively in favor of accessibility \u8212- and the {\i Rambam} and {\i Shulchan Aruch} affirmed that resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 24\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol Nidrei & Stipulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Nidrei Recap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 24. Yesterday\u8217's shiur, delivered by a substitute, covered the central issues surrounding {\i kol nidrei} and the mechanism of making a {\i tnai} \u8212- a stipulation \u8212- to prevent one\u8217's vows from taking effect. By way of review: the {\i mishnah} presented the position of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, who introduced the category of {\i nidrei zeruzin}, vows that are not genuinely intended to be binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nidrei Zeruzin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov identified two related phenomena. The first and more transparent example involved two people haggling over the price of something, each making a {\i neder} in the course of the back-and-forth \u8212- a process that was obviously a kind of game, with no real intent that the vows should take effect. The second, somewhat less obvious case involved using a vow as leverage or pressure to get someone to do something \u8212- for instance, vowing that a friend\u8217's food will be forbidden unless he agrees to come for dinner. This calls to mind the story of Abaye and his wife, where a vow was deployed as an ultimatum; the inherent problem with ultimatums is that one must ultimately make good on them. The question is whether one can truly claim retroactively that the vow was never intended to be binding. That is precisely Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's position: in such cases, where the person was using the vow purely as a form of pressure or encouragement and never genuinely wanted it to take effect, the vow is categorized as {\i nidrei zeruzin} and is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stipulation Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alongside the {\i nidrei zeruzin} discussion, the {\i gemara} identifies a separate and independent mechanism: making an explicit stipulation at the outset \u8212- at the beginning of the year, for instance \u8212- declaring that any future vow one may take should not be considered binding if one does not remember having made it. The {\i gemara} raises an internal tension here: if you remember the stipulation and you go ahead and make a vow anyway, you presumably want the vow to be binding. Conversely, if your intent was always that it not be binding, you shouldn\u8217't be making the vow in the first place. The stipulation therefore helps only in cases where one genuinely forgets about it. This mechanism is analogous to, though not identical with, the institution of {\i moda\u8217'ah} in {\i dinei mamonot}: if someone is pressured into selling a plot of land, he may tell witnesses in advance that even though he will sign the document, he does not truly consent to the sale. Similarly here, one can declare in advance that any future vow should not be considered sincere. Rava offered a nuanced answer addressing certain intermediate cases where the person half-remembers the stipulation, but he did not, in principle, dispute Abaye\u8217's basic framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatarat Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is that one may make such a declaration at the beginning of the year \u8212- at {\i Rosh Hashanah} \u8212- stipulating that any vows taken during the coming year, if forgotten, should not be binding. This is precisely the formula recited at the conclusion of the {\i hatarat nedarim} ceremony on {\i erev Rosh Hashanah}: {\i hareini mosar moda\u8217'ah lifneichem} \u8212- \u8220"I hereby make a proclamation before you\u8221" \u8212- followed by the declaration that any vow made from this day forward without being remembered shall be annulled from now. Of course, one observes that if this declaration truly worked comprehensively, one would not need to repeat {\i hatarat nedarim} every year; a single {\i moda\u8217'ah} would suffice indefinitely. But that, in any case, is the {\i gemara}\u8217's mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran then takes up the question of how all this bears on {\i kol nidrei}. The {\i hatarat nedarim} ceremony performed on {\i erev Rosh Hashanah} combines both dimensions explicitly: it annuls past vows through a generic expression of {\i charatah} \u8212- regret \u8212- and takes a stipulation for the future. The question of whether {\i charatah} alone, without specifying a {\i petach}, suffices for a generic annulment is one we have addressed previously; a generalized declaration of regret on behalf of the entire {\i tzibur} is somewhat unusual, since ordinarily the person seeking release must present himself and indicate {\i mischarait} \u8212- that he regrets the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Nidrei Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kol nidrei} presents its own complications. Unlike the formal {\i hatarat nedarim} ceremony, one is not presenting oneself individually, the formula combines past and future in an ambiguous way, and the halachic mechanism through which it operates is not entirely clear. The simple reading of the text suggests it is past-oriented, though Rabbeinu Tam, apparently for reasons related to this sensibility, proposed reorienting it toward the future \u8212- requiring certain grammatical adjustments to the text. Some {\i machzorim} preserve both the past-oriented and future-oriented {\i nuscha\u8217'os}, presenting them as alternate versions. An article on the grammar of {\i kol nidrei} by Dr.\u160 ?Richard Steiner offers a thorough analysis of this question for those interested in pursuing it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this question had significant real-world stakes. For much of Jewish history, the integrity of a business transaction with non-Jews was backed by a sworn vow. The perception that Jews would publicly release themselves from their vows on {\i Yom Kippur} night \u8212- effectively nullifying all commitments made over the preceding year \u8212- created serious problems and was used as grounds to question Jewish trustworthiness in commerce. This was part of the reason Rav Hirsch removed {\i kol nidrei} from the {\i Yom Kippur} liturgy in his community. The Ran\u8217's position that {\i kol nidrei} cannot coherently be understood as future-oriented \u8212- given the {\i gemara}\u8217's concern, discussed below, about not publicizing the stipulation mechanism too broadly \u8212- also bears on how one understands the practical halachic meaning of the text.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Huna & Rava Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a question: should this mechanism of stipulation have been publicly announced? Rav Huna bar Chinana\u8217's position was that it ought to have been publicized broadly \u8212- at a {\i pirka}, a public gathering \u8212- so that everyone would know they could make a stipulation at the beginning of the year and thereby protect themselves from inadvertently transgressing their vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagreed sharply. He pointed out that the {\i tana} had deliberately concealed this information \u8212- {\i mistam lei satumei} \u8212- by weaving the stipulation mechanism subtly into the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case about {\i nidrei zeruzin}, rather than stating it as a separate and explicit principle. The reason: {\i kedei shelo yinhagu kalos rosh benedarim} \u8212- so that people should not treat their vows lightly. If everyone knew that a simple advance stipulation could nullify any future vow, there would be a strong temptation to take vows casually, confident that no real obligation was being incurred. Rava therefore challenges Rav Huna bar Chinana: the {\i tana} took care to obscure this information \u8212- and you propose to announce it in a public forum?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage is striking for two reasons. First, it offers a window into how the {\i gemara} sometimes understands its own hermeneutic activity. When the {\i gemara} reads a {\i mishnah} and extracts two distinct legal principles from what appears on its surface to be a single unified case, one might wonder whether the {\i gemara} truly believes this to be the {\i pshat} of the text or whether it is simply offering a legal reformulation that need not be taken as a reading of the {\i mishnah} itself. Here, remarkably, the {\i gemara} seems to be affirming that this is genuinely {\i pshat}: the very fact that the information was buried in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation, rather than stated explicitly, is itself legally significant \u8212- it was hidden intentionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sin vs Sloppiness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second noteworthy dimension of this passage is the broader tension it reflects. This tension has run throughout {\i masechet Nedarim}: on one hand, you want to prevent people from actually transgressing their vows; on the other hand, if you make it too easy to avoid transgression \u8212- by providing ready-made mechanisms for annulment or by assuring people that certain vows are not really binding \u8212- you risk encouraging sloppiness in how people take vows in the first place. We have seen this tension elsewhere in the tractate: sometimes a {\i neder} is not technically binding, yet we require the person to go through the process of {\i hatarat nedarim} precisely so that the gravity of vow-taking is not undermined. Similarly, the {\i gemara} elsewhere formulates the principle of {\i halacha v\u8217'ein morin kein} \u8212- a ruling exists but is not publicized \u8212- and some halachic handbooks reflect this by presenting the public-facing ruling in the main text while reserving more nuanced leniencies for footnotes in Hebrew accessible only to {\i talmidei chachamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Chinana\u8217's position, it should be noted, was presumably motivated by genuine concern: people were stumbling over vows they did not fully intend, and a publicly known mechanism could spare them that sin. Rava\u8217's response is not that the mechanism is invalid, but that broadcasting it too widely creates different harms. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's deliberate obliqueness was thus not a failure of transparency but a considered halachic policy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbis vs R. Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a new question: did the {\i rabbanan} disagree with Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's categorization of {\i nidrei zeruzin}, or did they accept it? The stipulation mechanism, the {\i gemara} notes, would presumably be agreed upon by everyone \u8212- the debate about the {\i tnai} at the beginning of the year seems unrelated to the question of {\i nidrei zeruzin} per se. The real point of contention is whether the {\i rabbanan} accepted Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's broader category: when someone makes a vow not in the context of transparent haggling but as a form of one-sided pressure to get a result \u8212- \u8220"if you don\u8217't come to dinner, my food will be forbidden to you\u8221" \u8212- is that truly {\i nidrei zeruzin} and therefore not binding? One can imagine someone who genuinely would be offended at being refused, and who would sincerely intend the vow. Perhaps the {\i rabbanan} maintain that when the situation is not transparently a game, the vow stands. And a secondary question: if the {\i rabbanan} do disagree, how do we rule \u8212- like them or like Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Acceptance Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adduces evidence from a {\i mishnah}. The case: someone says to his friend, {\i konam she\u8217'ani neheneh lecha im i attah notel livnecha kor shel chitin ushtei chaviyot shel yayin} \u8212- \u8220"my benefit from you shall be {\i konam} if you do not accept for your son this measure of wheat and these two barrels of wine.\u8221" The speaker is trying to give a gift \u8212- perhaps a wedding present \u8212- and the intended recipient keeps refusing. In the middle of this friendly exchange, the giver declares that if the gift is not accepted, all benefit from the recipient will be forbidden to him. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i harei ze yachol lehatir nidro shelo pi chacham} \u8212- he can get out of the vow without consulting a {\i chacham}, meaning the vow is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Honor Satisfaction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is as follows: {\i sheyachol lomar lo klum amarta ela bishvil kvodi zehu kvodi} \u8212- the recipient could say, \u8220"You only made that vow for the sake of my honor. Well, this is my honor \u8212- that you made me such an offer and I had the standing to decline it.\u8221" In other words, the underlying purpose of the vow \u8212- to confer honor \u8212- has been achieved even without the gift being accepted. The person who refused the gift can claim he was honored by the very offer and by his ability to graciously decline. Therefore, it is as though the condition of the vow was satisfied, and the vow does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe that there is something not entirely gracious about this response. By insisting that the honor lies in declining, the recipient in some sense dishonors the giver by denying him the satisfaction of giving. The {\i Yerushalmi} addresses this directly: it says that if the giver responds, \u8220"I did not make the vow for your honor \u8212- I made it for mine, because it meant something to me to give this to you,\u8221" then the vow is binding. The {\i Yerushalmi} thus contemplates the possibility that {\i lechvodi}, not {\i lechvodecha}, was the animating motivation, in which case the honorific logic fails. When the motivation is ambiguous, the question of whose honor drove the vow becomes determinative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a broader cultural dimension here. In the Roman world of patronage, the acceptance of a gift from a social inferior was itself a powerful statement of status. A {\i mishnah} in {\i Kiddushin} reflects something similar: one of the modes of {\i kiddushin} is the woman giving a gift to the man, with his agreement to accept it serving as the act of acquisition. In a world structured around patronage dynamics, the willingness of a more prominent figure to accept a gift carried real social weight, and the entire exchange could not be reduced to simple friendship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Classification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having laid out the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling, the {\i gemara} draws an inference. The {\i mishnah} says the vow is not binding because {\i zehu kevodi} \u8212- it is as though the honor was received. But this implies: were it not for that reasoning, the vow {\i would} be binding. So this is a genuine {\i neder}, not {\i nidrei zeruzin}. One might therefore conclude that the {\i rabbanan} argue with Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov \u8212- that a one-sided vow used to pressure someone into accepting a gift is a real vow, not mere {\i zeruzin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rebuffs this inference: {\i le\u8217'olam Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov hu} \u8212- this {\i mishnah} can still be according to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov. But here, Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov would concede that the vow is serious: {\i modeh Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov be\u8217'hai denidra havai}. Why? Because the giver says to the recipient: {\i lo kalba ana d\u8217'mit\u8217'hanina minech v\u8217'lo mit\u8217'hanita minai} \u8212- \u8220"I am not a dog, that I would be deriving benefit from you while you get nothing from me.\u8221" Here the dynamic is reciprocal: the giver is saying, \u8220"I give things to you all the time; if you will not accept even this, then it is simply not a relationship where I can continue to take from you either.\u8221" The vow runs in the opposite direction \u8212- if you do not accept from me, I will not take from you \u8212- and that introduces an element of genuine sincerity that distinguishes it from the simpler case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reciprocal Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giver\u8217's Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces the inverse case. {\i Ha\u8217'omer l\u8217'chavero konam she\u8217'ata nehenit li im ee ata noten livni kor shel chitin ushtei chavyot shel yayin} \u8212- \u8220"Your benefit from me shall be {\i konam} if you do not give my son this measure of wheat and these two barrels of wine.\u8221" Here, instead of the giver making a vow about his own benefit from the recipient, he makes a vow about the recipient\u8217's benefit from him. Rabbi Meir says: {\i ad sheyiten} \u8212- until the recipient actually gives the gift, he cannot derive benefit from the giver. The {\i chachamim} say: {\i af zeh yachol lehatir et nidro b\u8217'lo pi chacham} \u8212- even here the vow is not binding without consulting a {\i chacham}. Why? {\i Sheyachol lomar hareni k\u8217'ilu hitkabalti} \u8212- because the giver can say, \u8220"It is as though I received the gift.\u8221" This is similar in structure to the previous case: though the condition was not met, the giver declares it as effectively satisfied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocity Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before returning to the question of {\i nidrei zeruzin}, the Ran raises an important question about the meaning of {\i ke\u8217'ilu hitkabalti}. One intelligible understanding is as follows: the giver wanted to receive an object; he could imagine a scenario in which Michael had given him the barrels of wine and he had then given them back. In that case, saying {\i ke\u8217'ilu hitkabalti} \u8212- \u8220"it is as though I received them\u8221" \u8212- has a plausible legal logic, because there is a concrete transaction one can imagine having occurred and been reversed. But what if the condition was not about a physical object? Suppose instead the condition was: \u8220"This is forbidden to you unless you go to the gym twice a week.\u8221" In that case, {\i ke\u8217'ilu hitkabalti} is much harder to parse \u8212- what exactly is being treated as though it happened? The Ran raises this difficulty. He suggests that perhaps in all cases, the underlying logic is simply that the giver set a condition, the purpose of the condition was to satisfy a desire, and the giver can always declare that desire satisfied \u8212- which amounts to waiving the condition. Whether this is a conceptually clean legal mechanism or more of a pragmatic escape valve is a question the Ran leaves somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any case, the thrust of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- before the {\i gemara} addresses the question of {\i nidrei zeruzin} \u8212- is that this is a genuine {\i neder}, but one from which the giver can extract himself by invoking {\i ke\u8217'ilu hitkabalti}. The logical consequence: were that not available, the {\i neder} would be binding. So this seems to point toward the {\i rabbanan} holding that these pressure-vows are real vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again deflects: {\i le\u8217'olam Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov hu} \u8212- and the Ran\u8217's explanation makes this intelligible in terms of the {\i gemara}\u8217's language. When the direction of the vow runs one way \u8212- \u8220"if you don\u8217't come to dinner, my food will be forbidden to {\i you}\u8221" \u8212- one might argue this is just an empty threat, a form of {\i zeruzin}. The person may be using the vow to encourage or pressure, but perhaps it is so hollow that even Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's category cannot make it binding. By contrast, when the vow runs in the reciprocal direction \u8212- \u8220"if you don\u8217't accept this from me, I won\u8217't get any benefit from {\i you}\u8221" \u8212- that reflects a genuine concern about the nature of the relationship. One is saying: what kind of relationship would it be if I give to you and you give me nothing in return? If this dynamic is allowed to continue, I simply do not want that relationship. Someone could sincerely feel this way, and therefore even Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov would concede that such a vow is binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rashba reads the distinction differently: he argues that what makes the case more serious is the weight of the gift involved \u8212- a larger, more significant gift warrants a more sincere vow, whereas an invitation to dinner is trivially easy to dismiss. However, this reading is difficult to reconcile with the actual language of the {\i gemara}, which never focuses on the size of the gift. The {\i gemara}\u8217's language \u8212- {\i rav malka ana de-mahnina lach v\u8217'at la mahnit li} \u8212- points squarely to the reciprocity concern, not to the monetary value of the gift. When someone says, in effect, \u8220"Do you think I\u8217'm some kind of king who gives and expects nothing back?\u8221", the issue is relational symmetry, not the magnitude of any particular item. As anyone who has experienced that hollow feeling when the same person keeps arriving at {\i Shabbat} meals without ever hosting in return will recognize \u8212- that relational dynamic is a real source of grievance, and a vow expressing it could well be sincere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initiated Vows & Ones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Kashisha son of Rav Chisda raises yet another attempted proof from the {\i mishnah} dealing with {\i nidrei onsin} \u8212- vows that became impossible to fulfill due to circumstances beyond one\u8217's control. The case: I pressure someone to come eat at my house, and if he does not come, my food is forbidden to him. He was on his way but fell ill, or his son fell ill, or a river flooded and he could not cross. The {\i mishnah} holds that the vow is not binding, because I surely never intended the vow to apply in a case of genuine {\i ones}. What I wanted was for him to make his best effort to come; had he done so and been thwarted, I would never have wanted the prohibition to take hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case sounds like {\i nidrei zeruzin}, since it is a one-directional vow used to encourage attendance at dinner. Yet the {\i mishnah} appears to treat it as a real vow that happens to be suspended due to {\i ones}. If it were truly {\i nidrei zeruzin}, the question of {\i ones} would be irrelevant \u8212- the vow would simply not be binding at all. This seems to imply that the {\i rabbanan} hold such a vow is genuinely binding when the condition is willfully not met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} once again deflects by reframing who initiated the vow. The case is not one where the inviter made the vow against the invitee; rather, {\i dizamina adrei l\u8217'mizamna} \u8212- the invitee initiated the vow. Michael had been wanting to come to dinner and felt that his host was not sincere about the invitation. So Michael pressed: \u8220"Come on, do you really want me to come? Make a vow to show me.\u8221" The host, thus challenged, made the vow. In this scenario, the vow was not a {\i zeruzin} \u8212- it was not deployed to push Michael into doing something he was reluctant to do; Michael was already eager to come. The host made the vow to demonstrate sincerity in response to Michael\u8217's prompt. Therefore, the vow is a genuine {\i neder}, and when Michael cannot come due to {\i ones}, the {\i gemara} holds the vow does not take effect \u8212- precisely because it was not a vow of pressure but a vow of sincere commitment that circumstances rendered impossible to fulfill. This, the {\i gemara} argues, is still consistent with Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov; it does not prove the existence of a {\i rabbanan} who dispute him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After all this, the {\i gemara} has provided an answer to each attempted proof, but the weight of the evidence continues to suggest that there is indeed a stricter position in the background \u8212- one that treats these one-directional pressure-vows as genuine {\i nedarim}. The {\i gemara} has managed each time to distinguish the specific case, either through reciprocity, initiation, or {\i ones}, but the category of {\i nidrei zeruzin} as applied to simple one-sided encouragement-vows remains contested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Extended\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, the {\i gemara} introduces a further statement: {\i yeter al ken amar Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov went further. He said: {\i ha\u8217'omer l\u8217'chavero konam she\u8217'ani neheneh lecha im i attah mit\u8217'areach etzli v\u8217'tokhal immi pat chamah v\u8217'tishteh immi kos chamim} \u8212- \u8220"My benefit from you shall be {\i konam} if you do not come and stay with me as a guest, eat hot bread with me, and drink a cup of tea with me.\u8221" And if the other party then responds in kind \u8212- saying similarly that he will derive no benefit from the host if the host does not reciprocate \u8212- {\i af elu nidrei zeruzin} \u8212- even these are {\i nidrei zeruzin}, says Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov. The {\i chachamim} did not agree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This statement is significant. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov is here extending his leniency to include even the reciprocal case \u8212- the very case the {\i gemara} had been treating as more sincere throughout the preceding discussion. Not only do the {\i rabbanan} refuse to follow him in this extension, but the {\i gemara} now asks what exactly the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's disagreement covers: {\i lav afillu b\u8217'kammaita} \u8212- do the {\i rabbanan} not agree even with the original, non-reciprocal case? {\i U-shema mina pligi rabbanan alei} \u8212- from the phrasing here, it seems the {\i rabbanan} reject Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's position across the board.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} acknowledges, however, that there is an alternate {\i girsa} of the text according to which the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's disagreement is limited to the reciprocal case alone; on the non-reciprocal case they might agree. What is clear in all {\i girsaos} is that Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov himself holds that both cases are {\i nidrei zeruzin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ruling & Nidrei Havai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakha Decision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i mai havya alah} \u8212- what is the practical ruling? {\i D\u8217'amar Rav Huna halakha k\u8217'Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov, v\u8217'khen amar Rav Adda bar Ahava halakha k\u8217'Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} \u8212- Rav Huna ruled like Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, and Rav Adda bar Ahava said the same. We rule like Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran observes that this ruling effectively renders the first question \u8212- whether the {\i rabbanan} actually disputed Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov \u8212- moot. The entire investigation was driven by a {\i halachic} interest: does Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov stand alone or does he have opponents, and if opponents, who do we follow? Once we have the ruling, the prior question loses its urgency. The Ran notes that this is analogous to the Rambam\u8217's approach of presenting the final ruling without the surrounding dialectic \u8212- if the only interest is knowing what the {\i halacha} is, all the preceding discussion becomes academic. Of course, one might respond that learning the {\i gemara} has value beyond the bottom-line ruling, and the reasoning behind a position matters for understanding edge cases and borderlines. This also connects to the broader question of the principle of {\i yachid v\u8217'rabim halacha k\u8217'rabim} \u8212- ordinarily we follow the majority \u8212- and why in this case we deviate from it to follow Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov. The considerations that go into such a ruling \u8212- the logic of the positions, the accepted practice in the field, the authority of the {\i Shulchan Aruch}, the clarity of the {\i gemara}\u8217's own {\i pshat} \u8212- are the same considerations any {\i posek} must weigh, and there is no fixed hierarchy among them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical scope of {\i nidrei zeruzin} as now defined is quite broad: not just the transparent mutual haggling case, but even one-sided pressure-vows and even reciprocal vows of the form \u8220"if you don\u8217't take from me, I won\u8217't take from you.\u8221" All of these Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov \u8212- and now the {\i halakha} \u8212- considers {\i nidrei zeruzin}. The difficulty that remains is knowing precisely where the line falls. Abaye\u8217's own case, after all \u8212- where he made a vow as an ultimatum and still required a {\i hatarat neder} \u8212- seems to fall in the {\i zeruzin} category on its face. Yet it was treated as a genuine vow that needed formal release. Knowing which pressure-vows are mere {\i zeruzin} and which are sincere commitments that a person genuinely entertained \u8212- even if reluctantly \u8212- and which are the middle category of Abaye where the person was sincere but assumed the other party would comply, is a question that the {\i gemara} leaves without a fully explicit answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nidrei Havai Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} introduces the second category: {\i nidrei havai} \u8212- vain or empty vows. {\i Amar konam im lo ra\u8217'iti baderech hazeh k\u8217'olei Mitzrayim} \u8212- \u8220"This loaf is {\i konam} to me if I did not see on this road as many people as the Jews who came out of Egypt.\u8221" Or: {\i im lo ra\u8217'iti k\u8217'nachash k\u8217'korat beit ha-bad} \u8212- \u8220"if I did not see a snake as thick as the beam of an olive press.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exaggeration vs Falsehood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran distinguishes two different phenomena at play in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's examples. The first case \u8212- seeing as many people as {\i olei Mitzrayim} \u8212- the Ran actually treats as a form of {\i guzma}, rhetorical exaggeration. When someone says \u8220"there were as many people as the Jews who came out of Egypt,\u8221" everyone understands this as hyperbole meaning there were enormous crowds. On this reading, the person is actually telling the truth \u8212- he really did see massive throngs of people, and his vow condition was genuinely satisfied, expressed in the conventional idiom of exaggeration. The vow is therefore not binding, but for a different reason than one might expect: the {\i tnai} was actually fulfilled. The Ran\u8217's point here is really a principle about how to interpret exaggerated language in vows: {\i guzma} is read in its figurative meaning, not its literal one, so a vow conditioned on a patently hyperbolic statement is assessed according to what the speaker clearly meant, not the literal words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case \u8212- a snake the size of an olive-press beam \u8212- is more interesting and more clearly within the {\i nidrei havai} category. This is not rhetorical exaggeration but an outright fantastical claim. The person is making a vow conditional on something everyone knows to be false. If you genuinely wanted to make the bread forbidden, you would simply say \u8220"this bread is forbidden to me.\u8221" Why attach it to a condition you know to be false? The only sensible answer is that you are not actually trying to make the bread forbidden at all. What you are doing is using the vow to add rhetorical weight and colorfulness to your fish story \u8212- you want people to appreciate how enormous the snake was, and the vow formula serves as a rhetorical intensifier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purpose Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the governing principle of {\i nidrei havai}: a {\i neder} is an instrument designed to make an object forbidden. When a vow is conditioned on something patently false \u8212- something everyone present knows to be untrue \u8212- the speaker clearly has no interest in making the object forbidden. He is using the vow for an entirely different purpose: to add drama and emphasis to a boastful or exaggerated statement. Since the vow is not being deployed for its intended function, it is not a {\i neder} at all, and nothing is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nidrei vs Shevuot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevuot Shav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i braita} that contrasts {\i nidrei havai} with {\i shevuot havai}. The version of the text before us reads: {\i nidrei havai mutarim shvuos havai asurim} \u8212- vows of the {\i havai} type are permitted, but oaths of the {\i havai} type are forbidden. An alternate {\i girsa} cited in the margin reads that both are permitted. To understand which is correct, the {\i gemara} points to the {\i mishnah} in {\i Shevuos} 29, which enumerates examples of {\i shvuat shav} \u8212- a false or vain oath. {\i Nishba l\u8217'shanot et hayadua la\u8217'adam} \u8212- swearing to contradict something universally known. For example: swearing that a stone pillar is made of gold, that a man is a woman, or that one did not see a snake like the beam of an olive press, or a flying camel. This is called {\i shvuat shav}. The {\i mishnah} in {\i Shevuos} thus categorizes these precisely as the type of oath the {\i gemara} is calling {\i shvuat havai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder vs Shevua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual distinction between a {\i neder} and a {\i shevua} is essential here. The purpose of a {\i neder} is to make an object forbidden. The purpose of a {\i shevua} \u8212- particularly an oath about the past or present \u8212- is to back up one\u8217's word, to assert the truth of a statement with the weight of a divine invocation. These are fundamentally different instruments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted vs Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a person makes a {\i neder havai} \u8212- attaching a vow condition to a patently false or absurd statement \u8212- he has no interest in making any object forbidden. He is not using the {\i neder} for its proper purpose at all; he is co-opting the vow formula to add rhetorical color to a story. Therefore the vow is null, and nothing is forbidden. The {\i neder} simply does not function as one here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a person makes a {\i shevuat havai}, however \u8212- invoking an oath to back up a patently false claim \u8212- he is using the {\i shevua} for {\i exactly its intended purpose}: to lend credibility and weight to a statement. The fact that the statement is false does not mean the {\i shevua} is being misused; quite the contrary. Taking a {\i shevua} in order to back up one\u8217's word, even a false word, is precisely what a {\i shevua} does. And when it is invoked to back up a patently false claim, that is {\i shvuat shav} \u8212- forbidden by the Torah. So the asymmetry is principled: {\i nidrei havai} are permitted because the {\i neder} is not functioning as a {\i neder}; {\i shevuot havai} are forbidden because the {\i shevua} is functioning exactly as a {\i shevua}, and a {\i shevua} used to assert a falsehood \u8212- even an obvious one \u8212- is {\i shvuat shav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevuat Havai Refined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: what exactly would constitute a {\i shevuat havai}? If one says {\i shvua im lo ra\u8217'iti} \u8212- \u8220"an oath if I did not see such-and-such\u8221" \u8212- {\i midi ka\u8217'amar}? What kind of oath is that? An oath {\i if} you saw something is incoherent; the oath needs to be that you saw something. Abaye offers the straightforward formulation: {\i d\u8217'amar shvua she\u8217'ra\u8217'iti} \u8212- \u8220"a {\i shevua} that I saw a snake of such a size.\u8221" That is indeed {\i shvuat havai} \u8212- you are invoking an oath to back up a patently false claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects, however, that this formulation is too obvious: it is simply the well-known category of {\i shvuat shav}, not a new principle. And furthermore, {\i dumiya d\u8217'neder katani} \u8212- the {\i shvuat havai} should be structurally parallel to the {\i nidrei havai} case. The case of {\i nidrei havai} involved a conditional structure: \u8220"this bread is forbidden to me if I did not see\u8230 ?\u8221" Rather than a direct assertion of the oath, Rava therefore reformulates: {\i b\u8217'omer ye\u8217'asru peros ha\u8217'olam alay b\u8217'shvua im lo ra\u8217'iti baderech hazeh k\u8217'olei Mitzrayim} \u8212- one says \u8220"by oath, all the fruit of the world is forbidden to me if I did not see on this road as many people as came out of Egypt.\u8221" This is the genuinely novel case of {\i shvuat havai}: it is not a direct false assertion backed by an oath, but a conditional oath attached to a fantastical or exaggerated claim. That formulation is less obviously a simple {\i shvuat shav}, and it is here that Rava locates the real content of the category. Here too, one might observe intuitively, the oath is being used to give {\i oomph} to one\u8217's statement \u8212- the speaker is trying to convey the magnitude of what he witnessed, and the oath-formula is the intensifier he reaches for. We will resume this analysis in the next day\u8217's learning.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 25\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nidrei Zeruzin & Havai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Piv Velibo Shavin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up at the bottom of 24b, where the {\i mishnah} has already introduced us to the four categories of {\i nedarim} that are not binding. The unifying principle underlying all four is {\i ein piv velibo shavin} \u8212- the words spoken did not reflect the speaker\u8217's genuine intent. Even when the correct formulation was used, the vow lacks the essential congruence between speech and will that is required for it to take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nidrei Zeruzin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first category is {\i nidrei zeruzin} \u8212- vows made for the purpose of incentivizing or encouraging someone else. Whether the other party understood the speaker was not serious (as in the classic case of haggling) or genuinely believed the vow to be sincere, the bottom line is that the speaker never truly intended it to be binding. The vow was deployed as a social or rhetorical tool, not as a genuine act of prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nidrei Havai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second category is {\i nidrei havai}. Here too the speaker never actually wants the object to become forbidden. Instead, the {\i neder} is used to lend weight to a statement \u8212- typically a patently false one. The {\i neder} functions as a kind of rhetorical emphasis, not as a genuine act of prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fish Stories & Guzma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents two examples of {\i nidrei havai}. The first \u8212- swearing that one saw as many people as those who left Egypt \u8212- is treated by the {\i Ran} as a case of {\i guzma}, a hyperbolic exaggeration. On his reading, the statement may not even be technically false; it is simply not to be taken literally, and therefore it does not properly belong to the category of {\i nidrei havai} at all. Setting aside the {\i Ran}\u8217's somewhat distracting analysis, the essential case is the second one: claiming one saw a snake as large as the beam of an olive press. This is a transparently false statement \u8212- a \u8220"fish story,\u8221" as it were. Just as people telling tall tales tend to embellish and pile on, so here the speaker adds a {\i neder} to back up the claim and give it emphasis. Since everyone understands the statement is false and the speaker never genuinely wanted anything forbidden, the {\i neder} does not take effect. In {\i nidrei zeruzin}, nobody takes the vow seriously, or at least the speaker does not. In {\i nidrei havai}, everybody knows the underlying claim is false, and the {\i neder} is merely its rhetorical backing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shvuot Havai Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tana: Nidrei/Shvuot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} on 25b opens with a {\i tanna} teaching: {\i nidrei havai mutarim} \u8212- vows of exaggeration are not binding \u8212- while {\i shvuot havai assurim} \u8212- oaths of exaggeration are forbidden. A second {\i girsa} reads instead {\i keshem shenidrei havai mutarim kach shvuot havai mutarim} \u8212- just as such vows are not binding, so too such oaths are not binding. The distinction between these two versions matters considerably, because the question of whether something is {\i assur} or {\i mutar} depends entirely on what one is looking at: is the question whether taking the {\i shvua} was forbidden, or whether the object the {\i shvua} was meant to prohibit actually becomes forbidden?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Translation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: what exactly is a {\i shvuat havai}? If one says \u8220"a {\i shvua} that I did not see as many Jews as the {\i yotzei mitzrayim},\u8221" the {\i Gemara} objects \u8212- {\i midi ka\u8217'amar}? That formulation is grammatically incoherent; an oath attached to an \u8220"if\u8221" clause that goes nowhere says nothing. Abaye therefore reformulates: the oath is stated positively \u8212- {\i shvua she\u8217'ra\u8217'isi} \u8212- I swear that I did see so many Jews. This is the direct translation of the {\i neder} form into the oath form. Where the {\i neder} says \u8220"this will be forbidden if I did not see,\u8221" the {\i shvua} says \u8220"I swear that I did see.\u8221" In such a case \u8212- swearing to back up a patently false statement \u8212- the {\i shvua} would constitute a {\i shvuat shav}, an oath taken in vain, since everyone understands the statement to be false.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects to Abaye\u8217's framing. If the case is simply that someone swore to back up an obviously false statement, we already know that is a {\i shvuat shav} \u8212- what is the novelty? Moreover, the {\i mishnah} in {\i Shvuos} frames the case in a way that must be analogous to the {\i neder}: not just backing up a statement, but actually making an object forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvuat Shav vs.\u160 ?Forbiddi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore presents the properly analogous case: {\i be\u8217'omer ye\u8217'asru peiros ha\u8217'olam alai bishvua im lo ra\u8217'isi baderech hazeh ke\u8217'olei mitzrayim} \u8212- one says that all fruit of the world shall be forbidden to him by {\i shvua} if he did not see as many people as the {\i olei mitzrayim} on this road. This mirrors the {\i neder} precisely: in the {\i neder} case, one uses {\i konam} to prohibit food as backing for a false claim; here, one uses a {\i shvua} to achieve the same effect. Now the analysis splits into two distinct questions. First, was it permitted to take such a {\i shvua}? The {\i Ran} says no \u8212- it constitutes a {\i shvuat shav}, and one would receive lashes for it, because one is invoking God\u8217's name to back up what is obviously a false statement with no genuine intent to prohibit anything. Second, does the food actually become forbidden? Here the answer is no \u8212- the food remains permitted, because just as in the {\i neder} case the speaker never genuinely wanted the object to be prohibited, so here too the {\i shvua} was never about making food forbidden but only about lending rhetorical weight to a false claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains the two {\i girsaot}: {\i shvuot havai assurim} refers to the act of taking such a {\i shvua}, which is forbidden as a {\i shvuat shav}. {\i Shvuot havai mutarim} refers to the food, which remains permitted. The parallel with cases like one who swears he will not sleep for three days or will not sit in the {\i sukkah} is instructive: those {\i shvuot} are not binding, which is precisely why they constitute {\i shvuat shav} \u8212- because they are purposeless, they were forbidden to take in the first place. So here too: because the {\i shvua} cannot genuinely prohibit the food, the act of swearing was itself a {\i shvuat shav}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Interpretation in Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina: Anthill Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina poses a challenge to Rav Ashi. The {\i Gemara} had been discussing the case where one swears that one saw as many people as {\i olei mitzrayim} on a road. Ravina asks: perhaps this man had an anthill at home that he called \u8220"{\i olei mitzrayim}\u8221" \u8212- and perhaps he saw forty ants, which is exactly how many people he saw in the road? In that case, the {\i shvua} would actually be true, and the food might genuinely be forbidden. One might note that this is in a sense the inverse of {\i perushan le-hakel}: there, a lenient private interpretation is invoked to escape a {\i neder}; here, a stricter private interpretation is invoked to render the food forbidden \u8212- a kind of {\i perushan le-hachmir}. And there is a practical dimension as well: if the {\i Gemara} was about to impose {\i malkus} on someone for taking an oath in vain, that person might well claim he genuinely meant his anthill and therefore took no {\i shvuat shav} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi: Standard Meani\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi responds: {\i ki mishtava ada\u8217'ata didan mishtava} \u8212- when a person takes an oath, it is based on what we understand, not on whatever private meaning was going through his head. This is another expression of the principle of {\i devarim shebalev einam devarim}: one cannot retroactively assign a private meaning to words that everyone understands in a standard way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling appears to be in tension with the earlier discussion of {\i perushan le-hakel} by {\i nedarim}. The distinction is significant. A {\i neder} is fundamentally about establishing the status of an object \u8212- it operates between the person and the Almighty. A {\i shvua}, by contrast, is oriented toward other people: it is a way of backing up statements, making commitments, communicating promises. Because {\i shvua} is inherently communicative and interpersonal, it must go by what the words are understood to mean by others. You cannot unilaterally determine what your oath means when oaths exist precisely for the purpose of conveying meaning to others. The phrase {\i ada\u8217'ata didan} \u8212- based on what we think \u8212- captures this orientation exactly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: Beit Din Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a challenge from a {\i braita}: when {\i beit din} administers an oath, they explicitly say to the oath-taker: {\i hevei yodea she-lo al tnai she-be-libcha anu mashbi\u8217'in otcha} \u8212- know that we are not administering this oath on the basis of whatever mental stipulation you have in your heart \u8212- {\i ela al dateinu ve-al da\u8217'at beit din}, but rather based on our understanding and on the understanding of {\i beit din}. If the general rule were already that oaths are interpreted according to communal standard meaning and never according to private intent, why would {\i beit din} need to state this explicitly? The need to declare it implies that in the ordinary case \u8212- outside {\i beit din} \u8212- one could in fact rely on one\u8217's own private interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chips of Wood Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: what case is {\i beit din}\u8217's declaration coming to exclude? It is not coming to exclude the anthill interpretation \u8212- that would never be valid in any context, since calling one\u8217's anthill \u8220"{\i olei mitzrayim}\u8221" is a crazy interpretation that no one would accept. Rather, the declaration is coming to exclude a case like this: a borrower repaid his debt not in actual money but in chips of wood, to which he privately assigned the name \u8220"dollars.\u8221" He then swears in {\i beit din} that he paid back the money. He is technically telling the truth \u8212- he gave \u8220"dollars\u8221" \u8212- but the word means something that no one else would recognize as payment. This type of technically-accurate-but-privately-defined usage is what {\i beit din} explicitly forecloses by declaring {\i al dateinu}. Outside {\i beit din}, one might have been entitled to rely on such a technically accurate \u8212- if misleading \u8212- private meaning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Staff Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A famous story illustrates this principle. There was a man who owed money to a creditor. Rava instructed him to go and pay. The debtor claimed he had already paid. Rava said, then go swear to him. The debtor took his staff, had it hollowed out, placed the coins inside, and gave it to the creditor to hold while he picked up a {\i sefer Torah} to swear on. He then declared \u8212- according to the {\i Ran}\u8217's {\i girsa} \u8212- {\i yahavti}, I gave you the money, which was technically accurate, since the coins were at that moment in the creditor\u8217's hands inside the staff. The creditor grew furious, smashed the staff, and the coins spilled out. The debtor declared: you see, I was telling the truth all along.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} adjusts the {\i girsa} from {\i parei} (I repaid) to {\i yahavti} (I gave), because if he had said {\i parei}, even handing over money would not constitute repayment of a loan in the technical sense. But the broader point stands: the permissible range of interpretation extends to statements that are technically accurate, even if not what the other party would have assumed. What is never permissible is a genuinely crazy interpretation \u8212- calling chips of wood \u8220"dollars,\u8221" or calling one\u8217's anthill \u8220"{\i olei mitzrayim}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crazy vs.\u160 ?Technical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus draws a clear line. There are two distinct categories. The first is a genuinely absurd private meaning: I called my anthill \u8220"{\i olei mitzrayim}\u8221"; I call these chips of wood \u8220"dollars.\u8221" Such interpretations are never valid, in {\i beit din} or outside it. The second category is a technically accurate meaning: the words, understood according to their normal semantic range, could validly describe what the speaker meant, even if that meaning was not what the listener assumed. Outside {\i beit din}, the speaker may rely on this technically accurate meaning. Inside {\i beit din} \u8212- or wherever a party explicitly declares {\i al dateinu} \u8212- even technical accuracy is insufficient; the words must carry their fully shared, standard meaning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe & Arvot Moav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a further proof from a {\i braita}: {\i ve-chein matzinu be-Moshe Rabbeinu} \u8212- we find the same principle by Moshe. When Moshe caused {\i Bnei Yisrael} to take an oath in {\i Arvot Moav}, he declared: {\i hevei yode\u8217'im she-lo al da\u8217'atchem ani mashbi\u8217'a eschem} \u8212- know that I am not administering this oath based on your understanding \u8212- {\i ella al da\u8217'ati ve-al da\u8217'as haMakom}. The verse supports this: {\i ve-lo itchem levadchem}, which the {\i Ran} interprets as \u8220"not what is within you alone\u8221" \u8212- not the private thoughts in your hearts. It was necessary for Moshe to make this explicit declaration in order to prevent {\i Bnei Yisrael} from later claiming that when they swore to worship God alone, they had privately meant their own gods by the word {\i Eloha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God vs.\u160 ?Idols Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: does this prove that outside such an explicit declaration, one {\i can} rely on one\u8217's private interpretation? The {\i Gemara} answers no. The case of calling an idol \u8220"{\i Eloha}\u8221" is not a crazy interpretation at all \u8212- the Torah itself uses the word {\i Eloha} to refer to foreign gods, as in the verse {\i u-le-elohei Mitzrayim} (Shemos 12:12). So calling an idol \u8220"{\i Eloha}\u8221" is not a private eccentricity but a technically accurate usage. Moshe\u8217's declaration was therefore coming to exclude precisely the technically-accurate-but-non-standard case, not the crazy case. The crazy case \u8212- calling one\u8217's anthill \u8220"{\i olei mitzrayim}\u8221" \u8212- would never be valid regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tech Accurate Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus arrives at its conclusion. The general rule, outside {\i beit din} or an explicit {\i al dateinu} declaration, is that one may rely on a technically accurate interpretation of one\u8217's words. What one may never do is give a genuinely absurd interpretation that no one would recognize as a valid meaning of the words. Inside {\i beit din}, even technical accuracy is foreclosed \u8212- the oath is administered on the basis of {\i da\u8217'at beit din}, the standard shared understanding. This is precisely why both Moshe and the institution of {\i beit din} needed to make their declarations explicit: they were extending beyond the ordinary rule to close off even the technically-accurate loophole.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unambiguous Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific Language Alt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now entertains a digression. If the concern was that {\i Bnei Yisrael} might claim they understood \u8220"God\u8221" to mean their idols, why not simply formulate the oath in language that admits no other possible interpretation? The {\i Gemara} works through a series of alternatives. Why not have them swear to fulfill {\i mitzvos}? Because {\i mitzvos} can mean the commands of any king. Why not {\i kol mitzvos}? Because that might be interpreted as referring only to {\i mitzvas tzitzis}, which the {\i amora} says is equivalent to all the {\i mitzvos} together. Why not {\i Torah}? Because {\i Torah achas} might mean just one instance. Why not {\i Toros}? Because that would refer to {\i Toras haMincha}, {\i Toras haChatas}, {\i Toras haAsham} \u8212- the specific legal sections \u8212- but not to the whole. Why not {\i Toros u-mitzvos} together? Because they might be understood as two specific commandments rather than as a comprehensive phrase. Why not {\i Torah kula}? Because rejecting {\i avodah zarah} is itself equivalent to affirming the entire Torah, as the {\i braita} states: {\i chamura avodas kochavim she-kol ha-kofer bah ke-ilu modeh ba-Torah kula} \u8212- one who denies {\i avodah zarah} is as if he affirms the entire Torah. Even formulating it as {\i she-lo ya\u8217'avdu avodas kochavim ve-Torah kula} does not fully close the gap. Why not {\i taryag mitzvos}? Perhaps one might say, I call my dog {\i taryag mitzvos} \u8212- though the {\i Gemara} concedes that this might well have been unassailable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe Practical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes: {\i ela Moshe milta de-la tricha nakas} \u8212- Moshe simply did not want to go through the exercise of constructing a perfectly airtight formulation. He took the straightforward approach and declared: it means what I say it means, end of discussion. There was no point in going through the entire exercise when a simple {\i al da\u8217'ati} declaration accomplished the same thing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Snake Beam Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Snake Exists?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the {\i nidrei havai} case involving the snake as large as a beam of an olive press. It raises the question: is such a statement truly impossible? Perhaps the claim is not as patently false as assumed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shavor Malka Snake\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} provides historical evidence: {\i de-hahu chivya de-hava be-shnei Shvor Malka} \u8212- there was a snake in the time of Shvor Malka to which they threw thirteen stableloads of grain. Snakes of extraordinary size, it seems, were not entirely unknown. So the statement may not be transparently false after all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Notched\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel therefore clarifies: {\i be-taraf} \u8212- the case of {\i nidrei havai} concerns a snake that is {\i taruf}, notched or spotted along its back. {\i Kulo nachash mitraf tarfi} \u8212- all snakes have notching of some kind. No, the {\i Gemara} objects \u8212- {\i agavo taruf kamina} \u8212- the reference is to a snake notched specifically on its back, and snakes with that particular characteristic do not grow to the size of an olive-press beam. That is the kind of snake one would typically encounter, and swearing one saw such a snake that large is therefore a patently impossible claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beam Business Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: why phrase it in terms of the snake being like a beam of an olive press, rather than simply saying the snake was {\i taruf}? The answer is: {\i milta agav urcha kama-shma lan} \u8212- the comparison incidentally teaches us a practical rule about the beam itself. {\i De-koras beit ha-bad gabo taruf} \u8212- a beam of an olive press has a notch on its back as part of its standard construction, used to attach the mechanism for pressing. {\i Le-mai nafka mina? Le-mekach u-mimkar} \u8212- this matters for business law. If one sells a beam designated for an olive press and the beam lacks the notch, the buyer may insist on a properly finished beam, since the term {\i korat beit ha-bad} by definition implies one equipped with that notch. The comparison between snake and beam thus simultaneously defines what kind of snake is meant and conveys a legal teaching about the commercial specifications of the beam.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nidrei Shegagot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now arrive at the third category: {\i nidrei shegagot} \u8212- vows made in error. The first type involves a false factual premise. One says: this bread is forbidden to me if I had breakfast this morning \u8212- and at the moment of the {\i neder}, one genuinely believed one had not eaten. It subsequently emerges that one had in fact eaten. The {\i halacha} is that the {\i neder} is not binding. The speaker imposed a condition that he was certain would not be met; the condition was in fact met, but through no genuine intention on his part. Since when he took the {\i neder} he never wanted it to be binding, it does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forgot Vow Later\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second type of {\i nidrei shegagot} involves a future-oriented condition. One says: {\i konam} this bread to me if I eat anything later today \u8212- genuinely intending to commit oneself to a diet. Then, later that day, one absent-mindedly reaches for a snack and eats it, having entirely forgotten the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forget at Exec\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is genuinely striking. Unlike the first type, when the {\i neder} was taken here the speaker was completely serious and fully intended it to be binding. Yet when the triggering act occurred \u8212- the snacking \u8212- the speaker had no memory of the {\i neder} at all. And the ruling is: the {\i neder} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Piv/Libo at Trigger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual basis is an extension of {\i ein piv velibo shavin}. One might object: the act of snacking is not an act of taking a {\i neder} \u8212- it does not require the intention or presence of mind associated with establishing the {\i neder} in the first place. Why should the speaker\u8217's state of mind at the moment of triggering matter? And yet the {\i Gemara} treats it as decisive. The underlying reasoning appears to be that {\i nidrei shegagot} are invalidated not only at the moment of taking but also at the moment of triggering, provided that at that moment there was a genuine disconnect \u8212- in this case, complete forgetfulness \u8212- between what the {\i neder} required and what the speaker understood himself to be doing. The {\i neder}, as a creation of human language operating one step removed from direct divine prohibition, requires a minimal continuity of awareness. When that is wholly absent at the moment the condition is triggered, the {\i neder} fails to become {\i chal}. The contrast with ordinary violation is important: if one simply forgot the {\i neder} and ate the forbidden food, that is an {\i issur be-shogeg} \u8212- a violation committed inadvertently. But here, crucially, the {\i neder} is treated as never having taken effect at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mistaken Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second type of {\i nidrei shegagot} involves a mistake about the people involved. The {\i mishnah} gives the case of one who saw figures eating figs from his tree and declared: {\i harei aleichem korban} \u8212- let all these figs be {\i korban} to you. He was furious at what he took to be strangers helping themselves to his property. It then emerged that the group included his own father and brother. The {\i mishnah} adds another case: one who declared {\i konam ishti nehenet li} \u8212- my wife is forbidden to derive benefit from me \u8212- on the grounds that she had stolen his wallet or struck his child, and it later emerged that neither happened. In this case, the speaker did not use the word {\i im} \u8212- \u8220"if\u8221" \u8212- yet the {\i neder} was clearly premised on a factual assumption. Since the assumption was false, and the {\i neder} was transparently based on that assumption, it does not take effect. This is distinct from a {\i petach}: a {\i petach} is when one failed to consider some consequence of the {\i neder} that, had one thought about it, would have prevented one from taking it. Here, the {\i neder} was based on a mistaken belief about a core factual matter \u8212- who was present, or what had occurred \u8212- and that error goes to the very substance of the {\i neder} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Group Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What if the group included both the father and brother and other people as well? {\i Beit Shammai} say: the father and brother are permitted, but the others remain forbidden. {\i Beit Hillel} say: once any part of the {\i neder} is invalidated, the entire {\i neder} is invalidated \u8212- {\i neder she-hutar miktzato hutar kulo}. The case must be understood carefully: obviously, if one had said \u8220"Group A is forbidden and Group B is forbidden\u8221" and Group B was one\u8217's father and brother, Group A would plainly remain forbidden. The force of {\i beit Hillel}\u8217's ruling is that when a single undivided statement is made \u8212- {\i harei aleichem} \u8212- and part of that statement was based on an error, the {\i neder} as a whole may fall away.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shvuot Shegagot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvuot Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} teaches: {\i kesheim shenidrei shegagot mutarin kach shevuot shegagot mutarot} \u8212- just as {\i nidrei shegagot} are not binding, so too {\i shvuot shegagot} are not binding. The parallel cases from the {\i mishnah} would apply straightforwardly: one swore that a loaf would be forbidden if one had eaten breakfast, while mistakenly believing one had not; or one swore and then forgot the {\i shvua} at the moment of triggering. But the {\i Gemara} presents a far more interesting case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kahana Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kegon Rav Kahana ve-Rav Assi} \u8212- it is like Rav Kahana and Rav Assi, who would sometimes dispute what their teacher Rav had actually said. One would declare: {\i shevuasa de-hachi amar Rav} \u8212- I swear that Rav said this! The other would respond: {\i shevuasa de-hachi amar Rav} \u8212- I swear he said no such thing; he said this other thing! {\i Ve-chol chad ve-chad ada\u8217'ata de-nafshei shapir kamishtava} \u8212- each one believed entirely that he was telling the truth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheker Beshogeig\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the {\i chiddush}: if one of them was mistaken, that person took what amounts to a {\i shvuat sheker be-shogeig} \u8212- a false oath taken inadvertently. The {\i Gemara} teaches that such an oath is not binding and carries no culpability, because the person genuinely believed he was telling the truth. This is {\i shvuot shegagot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oaths Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling is genuinely surprising. One might have thought: the entire purpose of a {\i shvua} is to back up one\u8217's words with the force of God\u8217's name. If those words turn out to be false, it might not matter that the speaker was in {\i shogeg} \u8212- the speaker was, after all, swearing to back up a claim, and that claim was false. The intuition would be that at least with regard to {\i shvuat sheker}, one ought to have been more careful before invoking God\u8217's name, and if one was wrong the {\i shvua} should still be problematic even in {\i shogeg}. The {\i chiddush} of this {\i Gemara} is that even here, where the {\i shvua} is functioning precisely as a vehicle for backing up a statement, if the speaker genuinely believed the statement to be true and was in error, the {\i shvua} is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} focuses the analysis on whether fruit would become forbidden in such a case \u8212- i.e., if one said {\i shvua} that if Rav did not say X then fruit would be forbidden, and one was wrong about what Rav said. Even granting that this involves a mistake about the condition rather than a direct false backing of a statement, the {\i Ran} treats the question of the food\u8217's status as the analogue to the {\i neder} case. Whether the person committed any {\i issur} in taking the {\i shvua} the {\i Ran} leaves somewhat open. But the simpler reading of the {\i Gemara} \u8212- the case of Rav Kahana and Rav Assi swearing directly about what Rav said \u8212- suggests that even the direct function of a {\i shvua} as a verbal commitment to truth is subject to the {\i shegagot} principle.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Petach & Void\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequence vs.\u160 ?Fact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A conceptual distinction must be drawn between two different ways in which part of a {\i neder} can be invalidated. In {\i nidrei shegagot}, the invalidation flows from a factual error \u8212- the speaker did not know that his father was among the group, or did not know that his wife had not actually struck the child. The error concerns what actually happened or who was actually present; it goes to the substance of the {\i neder} itself. A {\i petach}, by contrast, involves not a factual error but a failure of foresight: the speaker knew the relevant facts but did not think through the consequences of the {\i neder}. The classic {\i petach} is the {\i shabbat} and {\i yom tov} case: one who vowed never to drink wine clearly knows that {\i shabbat} exists and that a year includes {\i shabbat}; one simply did not stop to consider that the vow would apply there. That failure of attention requires a {\i petach} to undo; it does not automatically render the {\i neder} invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Days Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The earlier teaching \u8212- prior to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling \u8212- held: {\i otam hayamim mutarim ve-she\u8217'ar kol hayamim assurim} \u8212- those particular days (like {\i shabbat} and {\i yom tov}) are permitted, and all other days remain forbidden. The {\i petach} got the person out of the {\i neder} only with respect to the days he had not contemplated; the rest of the {\i neder} remained in force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hutar Miktzato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva then taught: {\i neder she-hutar miktzato hutar kulo} \u8212- a {\i neder} that has been released in part has been released in its entirety. Once any portion of the {\i neder} is annulled, the whole {\i neder} falls away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Qualifier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava clarifies the parameters of the debate between {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel}. Everyone agrees, he says, in the following case: if the person would have said, had he known his father was there, {\i kulchem assurim chutz me\u8217'abba} \u8212- you are all forbidden except for my father \u8212- then all agree that only the father is excluded and the rest remain forbidden. Why? Because the speaker himself says he would have maintained the statement {\i kulchem assurim}; he would simply have added a qualifier. Since the core statement was not in error, and the whole group would still have been included with just that one exception noted, the rest of the {\i neder} stands. The {\i machloket} between {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} arises only when the person says: had I known my father was there, I would have said {\i Ploni va-Ploni assurim ve-abba mutar} \u8212- I would have named the others and excluded my father \u8212- meaning he would have formulated an entirely different statement. In that case, according to {\i Beit Hillel}, since the speaker would never have uttered the words {\i kulchem assurim} at all, the entire {\i neder} is void. {\i Beit Shammai} apparently maintains that even here the rest of the {\i neder} stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Rava\u8217's first framing, then: where the speaker would have added a qualifier to the same basic language, everyone agrees the rest holds. Where the speaker would have used completely different language \u8212- never starting with {\i kulchem assurim} at all \u8212- that is where {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} disagree, with {\i Beit Hillel} voiding the entire {\i neder} by {\i hutar miktzato hutar kulo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Second\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then presents an alternative framing of where the {\i machloket} lies. On this second approach, the case of completely different language \u8212- where the speaker says he would have used an entirely different formulation \u8212- is so strong that even {\i Beit Shammai} would agree the entire {\i neder} is void. The {\i machloket} is then relocated to the qualifier case: where the speaker says he would have maintained {\i kulchem assurim} but added {\i chutz me\u8217'abba}, {\i Beit Hillel} still voids the entire {\i neder} by {\i hutar miktzato hutar kulo}, but {\i Beit Shammai} holds that the rest remains binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We close at the question of {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's position in the qualifier case. What precisely is {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's reasoning when the speaker says he would have kept the core language and merely added an exception? This analysis will be taken up in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nidrei Shgagot Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mistaken Assumptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We return briefly to a topic from yesterday\u8217's daf: {\i nidrei shgagot}, the category of vows made on the basis of a false assumption. The paradigmatic case is one who forbids something upon himself contingent on some state of affairs, believing that state of affairs does not obtain \u8212- when in fact it does. Because the vow was never intended to be binding under the actual circumstances, it is not binding. The {\i gemara} extends the same logic to {\i shvuat shgaga}, the mistaken oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two distinct scenarios of {\i shvuat shgaga}. The first, which the {\i Ran} treats as the more primary case, involves a person who simply swears that something happened or did not happen, genuinely believing his statement to be true \u8212- yet being mistaken. The second, to which the {\i gemara} gravitates, involves a conditional oath: \u8220"I swear I will not eat this if such-and-such occurred,\u8221" where the person\u8217's false assumption about the facts renders the oath inapplicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Shvuat Shgaga\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this analysis striking is the implication for the more obvious case: the person who takes an oath to back up a factual claim he sincerely believes to be true, only to discover he was wrong. One might have thought that since the entire point of taking a {\i shvua} is to stand behind a statement as true, someone who swears and turns out to be mistaken has committed {\i shvuat sheker}. Should he not have been more careful to verify the facts before invoking the divine name?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the plain reading of the {\i gemara} is that such a person has not violated any prohibition \u8212- and this is precisely how the {\i Rambam} rules. In {\i Hilchot Shvuot}, Perek 3, the {\i Rambam} analyzes the category of {\i shvuat shgaga} and works through the implications with characteristic rigor. He notes that if a person swore he had not eaten and then recalled that he had \u8212- or swore he would not eat and then forgot and ate \u8212- these are classic instances of acting {\i beshogeg}, yet the {\i Rambam} classifies all such cases as {\i shvuat shgaga} for which the person is {\i patur}, entirely exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Subjective Truth Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates a paradox that the {\i Rambam} himself surfaces: if one is exempt whenever one swears falsely while believing oneself to be telling the truth, and exempt whenever one violates a future-oriented oath through forgetfulness, then the only remaining scenario of culpable {\i shvuat shgaga} \u8212- the one that incurs a {\i korban oleh v\u8217'yored} \u8212- is exceedingly narrow. The {\i Rambam} answers directly: the case of liability is where a person swore falsely knowing he was lying, and knew that swearing falsely was forbidden, yet did not know that such a violation incurs a {\i korban}. That ignorance of the {\i korban} obligation is the {\i shgaga} for which one is liable. In other words, the subjective intention of the oath-taker is the governing standard \u8212- if in your own mind you were telling the truth, you have not committed {\i shvuat sheker}, full stop. The {\i gemara} uses the term {\i mutarot}, indicating that such cases are not merely {\i patur aval assur} but reflect a fundamental non-violation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Exclusion Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine & Shabbat Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that review in place, we return to the central principle under discussion: {\i neder she\u8217'hutar miktzato hutar kulo} \u8212- when part of a vow is released, the entire vow is annulled. The {\i gemara} picks up from the previous daf, beginning from the passage on 25b. The foundational case involves a person who vowed never to drink wine. He is brought before a court and asked: had you considered that this vow would prevent you from drinking wine on {\i Shabbos} and {\i Yom Tov}? He says no, he had not thought that through. The early position was that only those days \u8212- {\i Shabbos} and {\i Yom Tov} \u8212- are released from the vow while all other days remain forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then came {\i Rabbi Akiva}, who taught the principle of {\i neder she\u8217'hutar miktzato hutar kulo}: once any portion of a vow is released, the entire vow is annulled. The question now engaging the {\i gemara} is: what exactly are the parameters of this principle, and how do {\i Rabbi Akiva} and the {\i chachamim} disagree?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabba} draws a distinction that structures the entire discussion. Everybody agrees, he says, that if the vow-maker would say: \u8220"Had I known my father was among you, I would have said: all of you are forbidden {\i except for} my father\u8221" \u8212- that is, he would have made the same vow with a qualifier added \u8212- then the {\i neder} is not fundamentally undermined. The father alone is excluded, and the rest remain forbidden. In the {\i Ran}\u8217's terminology, adding such a qualifier is called {\i mekayem devarav}: the speaker is essentially maintaining his original statement and merely appending a limitation to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} in our {\i mishna}, says {\i Rabba}, concerns a different case: where the vow-maker says, \u8220"Had I known my father was there, I would never have said \u8216'all of you\u8217' \u8212- I would have said specifically: so-and-so and so-and-so are forbidden, but my father is permitted.\u8221" Here he claims he would have made a fundamentally different statement altogether \u8212- what the {\i Ran} calls {\i meshane devarav}. {\i Beit Hillel} says the entire original vow is void; {\i Beit Shammai} says only the father is released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mekayem vs.\u160 ?Meshane\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual hinge is thus the distinction between {\i mekayem devarav} and {\i meshane devarav}. When the speaker would merely have qualified his statement \u8212- adding an exception while leaving the underlying {\i neder} intact \u8212- {\i Rabba} holds that the {\i neder} stands and only the excepted person is released. When the speaker would have replaced his statement entirely with a different formulation, the question arises whether the original words retain any force at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabba}\u8217's reading of {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} places their dispute squarely in the {\i meshane devarav} case. Both schools agree that a pure {\i mekayem} \u8212- a qualifier added to an otherwise unchanged {\i neder} \u8212- leaves the {\i neder} intact for everyone except the excluded individual. Their dispute is over the case where the speaker insists he would have formulated an entirely different statement: {\i Beit Hillel} says the whole original {\i neder} is void, and {\i Beit Shammai} says the {\i neder} survives and only the father is released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} adopts a more lenient reading of the same positions. He says that in the case of {\i meshane devarav} \u8212- a fundamentally different statement \u8212- everyone agrees the entire {\i neder} is void, even {\i Beit Shammai}. The dispute of {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel}, says {\i Rava}, is precisely over the {\i mekayem devarav} case: where the speaker would only have added a qualifier. {\i Beit Hillel} says even that degree of deviation from what he actually said is sufficient to void the entire {\i neder}. {\i Beit Shammai} says it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In practical terms, {\i Rava}\u8217's position is more lenient than {\i Rabba}\u8217's. For {\i Rava}, holding like {\i Beit Hillel}, even a qualifier \u8212- {\i mekayem devarav} \u8212- is enough to collapse the entire vow. For {\i Rabba}, the entire vow collapses only when the speaker would have been {\i meshane devarav}, producing a categorically different statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir & Yose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} maps this dispute onto the well-known debate between {\i Rabbi Meir} and {\i Rabbi Yose} regarding the integrity of a statement. The case there involves someone who says of an animal: \u8220"{\i harei zo tmurat olah tmurat shelamim}\u8221" \u8212- a dedication that begins as an {\i olah} and ends as a {\i shelamim}. Since an animal cannot be both, the question is which declaration governs. {\i Rabbi Meir} holds {\i tfos lashon rishon}: the first expression is binding, and what is said later is irrelevant. {\i Rabbi Yose} holds {\i begmar devarav adam nitfas}: you cannot truncate a person\u8217's statement; you hear the whole thing and the complete statement determines the outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} applies this as follows. {\i Beit Shammai} holds like {\i Rabbi Meir} \u8212- the original {\i neder} stands because the first statement is what was actually said, and a qualifier at the end does not undo it. {\i Beit Hillel} holds like {\i Rabbi Yose} \u8212- you cannot freeze the statement midway; you look at the complete statement the speaker would have made, and since it differs from what he said, the original is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Class Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several questions arise naturally from this analysis. First, there is a technical point about whether the people subject to the {\i neder} must actually hear it pronounced \u8212- a question touching on {\i nidrei onsim}, which the {\i gemara} will address separately. More conceptually pressing is the question of why the {\i gemara} at all cares about what the speaker would {\i hypothetically} have said. The {\i neder} was made; it is in the world. Should we not simply assess the effect of removing one member of the group on the {\i neder} as it actually stands?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is indeed a striking feature of the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis. The {\i gemara} is assessing not the structural quality of the {\i neder} itself \u8212- whether the group of people was named as a single unit, a collection of individuals, or a chain \u8212- but rather what the speaker would have articulated in an alternate reality. The reason the hypothetical matters seems tied to the principle of {\i piv velibbo shavin}: the binding force of a {\i neder} requires alignment between speech and intention. When a false assumption disrupts that alignment for part of the {\i neder}, the {\i gemara} asks how deep that disruption runs \u8212- and it answers by reconstructing the speaker\u8217's hypothetical alternative. This is admittedly unusual, since once language has been uttered, one would expect the analysis to focus on what was said rather than on a counterfactual. The discussion continues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also important to note that the case of the father being unknowingly included falls under {\i nidrei shgagot} and requires no formal {\i hatarat nedarim}: once it is clarified that the father was present, the {\i neder} simply never applied to him. By contrast, the wine case \u8212- where the speaker knew all the facts but failed to think through their implications \u8212- does require a formal {\i hataras neder}, because there was no false assumption about the facts, only a failure to think them through. Both cases share the structural question of what happens to the remainder of the {\i neder} once part of it is removed, but they arrive at that question by different routes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language & Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preceding discussion raises a deeper question about language and its binding power. What the {\i gemara} is effectively doing, one might observe, is reaching back into the speaker\u8217's mind after the fact \u8212- not to assess what he meant by the words he used, but to imagine what words he would have chosen under different circumstances. This is, as noted, a somewhat unusual mode of analysis. Ordinarily the question would be: given the {\i neder} as formulated, what is the structural impact of releasing one part of it? The {\i gemara} chooses instead to ask what the speaker would have said, and uses that hypothetical to determine how tightly bound the remaining elements of the {\i neder} are to the released portion. The intrinsic relationship between thought, language, and the facts on the ground is the governing concern \u8212- but the direction of analysis is from the hypothetical back to the actual, which is counterintuitive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Unit Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i mishna} that, at least in its initial formulation, seems to take the more intuitive approach. {\i Rav Pappa} raises a challenge to {\i Rava} from a {\i mishna} that elaborates on {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's principle. The {\i mishna} asks: how did {\i Rabbi Akiva} articulate {\i neder she\u8217'hutar miktzato hutar kulo}? If someone says {\i konam she\u8217'eini neheneh lekulchem} \u8212- my benefit is forbidden to all of you as a group \u8212- and for one member of the group the {\i neder} turns out not to apply (through {\i nidrei shgagot}, {\i hatarat nedarim}, or any other means), then it is void for all of them. The reasoning is that all of you together constitute a single unified {\i neder}, and removing one element dissolves the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chain Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then presents a contrasting formulation: {\i she\u8217'eini neheneh lo laze velo laze velo laze} \u8212- not to this one, and not to this one, and not to this one, enumerating each person individually. Here the rule operates differently. If the {\i first} person named is released, all are released. If the {\i last} person named is released, only he is released and the rest remain forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Middle Last\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of the {\i mishna} is best understood as a chain structure. When someone says \u8220"not to you, and not to you, and not to you,\u8221" each successive element is conceptually linked to what came before \u8212- like cells in a spreadsheet where each depends on the reference above it. Remove the first link and the entire chain collapses. Remove the last link and only that final element is affected, since nothing downstream depends on it. The {\i mishna} later adds that if a middle person is released, everyone from that point downward is released and those above remain forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This structure is categorically different from the group-unit case. In the group-unit case ({\i lekulchem}), the entire {\i neder} is a single whole; in the chain case ({\i lo laze velo laze}), the {\i neder} is a sequence of linked but distinct prohibitions, and the effect of releasing one depends entirely on its position in the sequence. Crucially, this analysis is about the nature of the {\i neder} as actually formulated \u8212- not about what the speaker would hypothetically have said. The question is whether the word structure creates a unified unit, a collection of independent units, or a daisy-chain of linked units.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reisha/Seifa Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Reisha/Seifa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} now challenges {\i Rabba}: how does {\i Rabba}\u8217's framework, which turns on what the speaker would hypothetically have said, accommodate this {\i mishna}? The {\i gemara} attempts to read the {\i mishna} through the lens of the {\i Rabba}\u8211-{\i Rava} dispute \u8212- specifically, to read the two cases of the {\i mishna} (the {\i lekulchem} case and the {\i lo laze velo laze} case) as corresponding to {\i meshane devarav} and {\i mekayem devarav} respectively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Rabba}\u8217's reading, the {\i gemara} proposes: the {\i reisha} \u8212- the case where releasing one releases all \u8212- corresponds to a situation where the speaker would have said something fundamentally different ({\i lo laze velo laze} instead of {\i lekulchem}). The {\i seifa} \u8212- where releasing the first releases all \u8212- corresponds to a situation where the speaker would have said {\i lekulchem} (a completely different formulation) in place of the {\i lo laze velo laze} he originally used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a forced reading. The {\i gemara} is attempting to map the structural analysis of the {\i mishna} \u8212- which asks about the intrinsic architecture of the {\i neder} \u8212- onto the hypothetical-speech analysis of {\i Rabba} and {\i Rava}, which asks what the speaker would have said in an alternate scenario. The two frameworks are not naturally aligned, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's attempt to reconcile them requires assigning hypothetical alternative statements to the cases of the {\i mishna} in ways that are not immediately obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} presses his own objection against this reading. He argues that if the governing question is simply whether the speaker would have said a {\i qualifier} (which is sufficient to release all, according to {\i Rava}), then how does the {\i mishna}\u8217's distinction between {\i rishon} (first) and {\i acharon} (last) make any sense? Whether one releases the first person or the last person has no bearing on what the speaker would hypothetically have said as an alternative formulation. The hypothetical-speech framework cannot explain why position in the sequence matters. The very distinction that the {\i mishna} draws \u8212- releasing the first releases all, releasing the last releases only him \u8212- is unintelligible if what we truly care about is the speaker\u8217's counterfactual intention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a sharp observation. {\i Rava} is effectively pointing out that the {\i mishna} is operating on a different axis entirely. The {\i mishna} cares about the structure of the {\i neder} as uttered \u8212- specifically, whether the sequential formulation creates a dependency relationship among the named individuals. The {\i gemara}\u8217's attempt to retrofit this into a framework of hypothetical speech does not hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daisy-Chain Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ultimately concedes the point and provides the correct reading. The {\i reisha} of the {\i mishna} \u8212- the {\i lekulchem} case \u8212- involves a single unified {\i neder} in which all named individuals form one coherent whole. The {\i seifa} \u8212- the {\i lo laze velo laze} case \u8212- involves individuals linked in a chain: {\i tla\u8217'an zeh bazeh}, each one is made dependent on the next. This is not about hypothetical speech; it is about the actual structure of what was said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita} that corroborates this reading: {\i hutar ha\u8217'emtza\u8217'i} \u8212- if a middle person is released \u8212- then everyone from him downward ({\i heimenu ulemata}) is released, and those above him remain forbidden. This confirms the chain-dependency logic: a release propagates downstream through the chain but not upstream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus arrives at a position that, at least for the {\i mishna} of {\i Rabbi Akiva}, does not ultimately rest on the hypothetical-speech analysis of {\i Rabba} and {\i Rava}. The relevant variable is the structural quality of the {\i neder} itself \u8212- whether the language creates a unified unit, a collection, or a chain \u8212- and the effect of releasing one element is determined by that structure, not by what the speaker would have said otherwise. The {\i gemara} does not, however, abandon the hypothetical-speech framework entirely; it continues to apply it in subsequent cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kufri Onions Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onion Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Adda bar Ahava} challenges {\i Rava} with a new case. A person declares: {\i konam batzal she\u8217'ani to\u8217'eim} \u8212- all onions are forbidden to me as a {\i korban} \u8212- {\i shehabatzal ra lalev}, because he has heard that onions are bad for the heart. This is the rationale behind the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kufri Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He is then told: but the {\i Kufri} onion \u8212- a particular variety from a certain locale \u8212- is actually good for the heart. Therefore, it is clear he never intended to include the {\i Kufri} onion in his vow; regarding that variety, there was a {\i shgaga}. He is thus released from the {\i Kufri} onion: {\i hutar beKufri}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then states: and not only from the {\i Kufri} is he released, but from all onions \u8212- {\i velo beKufri bilvad hutar ela bechol habetzalim}. This is an application of {\i hutar miktzato hutar kulo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question on Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge to {\i Rava} is as follows. The {\i gemara} assumes that the speaker in this case would have said something like: \u8220"All onions are forbidden except for the {\i Kufri}\u8221" \u8212- that is, he would have maintained his basic statement with a qualifier ({\i mekayem devarav}). If {\i Rava} holds that even a qualifier is sufficient to release the entire vow, then this case would follow naturally from his position \u8212- no challenge to him here. But this also generates a question on {\i Rabba}, since {\i Rabba} holds that only {\i meshane devarav} releases the whole, yet the case seems to come out as though it does release the whole. The {\i rishonim} note that the question applies to {\i Rabba} as well, for reasons connected to {\i Rabba}\u8217's association of his position with the stricter view of {\i Rabbi Meir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishne Devarim Reply\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer is simple and decisive: who told you what this person would have hypothetically said? Perhaps he would have said not a qualifier but a fundamentally different statement \u8212- {\i meshane devarav}. He would have specified: these onions and those onions are forbidden, but the {\i Kufri} is permitted \u8212- enumerating types rather than adding a blanket qualifier. Under that reading, the case is one of {\i meshane devarav}, which releases the entire {\i neder} according to everyone: it satisfies {\i Rabba}\u8217's requirement for complete nullification, and it resolves the difficulty with {\i Rabbi Meir} as well, since even {\i Rabbi Meir} would concede when the statement would have been entirely different, in accordance with {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper point, which the {\i gemara} is implicitly acknowledging throughout, is that there is no independent evidence in these cases for what the speaker would have hypothetically said. The {\i gemara} can always assign the hypothetical that fits the outcome. If the outcome demands {\i meshane devarav}, the {\i gemara} says that is what he would have said; if the outcome demands {\i mekayem devarav}, the {\i gemara} says that instead. This makes the hypothetical-speech framework somewhat less determinate than it might first appear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Figs in Basket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basket Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina} poses a further question to {\i Rava}. The case is: {\i nadar min hakalkala} \u8212- someone vowed that a basket ({\i kalkala}) is forbidden to him, intending to forbid everything in the basket. The basket turns out to contain {\i benot shuach}, a prized variety of fig.\u160 ?He then says: {\i ilu hayiti yode\u8217'a shebenot shuach betocha, lo hayiti noder} \u8212- had I known there were {\i benot shuach} in it, I never would have vowed. He loves {\i benot shuach} and would never have wanted to forbid them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Outcomes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this declaration, two possible outcomes present themselves. Either the entire vow is released \u8212- because {\i hutar miktzato hutar kulo} \u8212- or only the {\i benot shuach} are released and the rest of the basket\u8217's contents remain forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishne Devarim Assumed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially assumes that this case reflects {\i meshane devarav}: the speaker would have made a fundamentally different statement had he known. Rather than saying \u8220"the basket is forbidden,\u8221" he would have enumerated: these figs and those contents are forbidden, but the {\i benot shuach} are permitted. Under this reading, {\i Rava} \u8212- and indeed everyone \u8212- would say the entire vow is released, since {\i meshane devarav} produces universal nullification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qualifier Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} then considers the counter-assumption: perhaps the speaker\u8217's alternative formulation would have been a qualifier rather than a fundamentally different statement. He would have said: the basket is forbidden except for the {\i benot shuach} \u8212- {\i kol hakalkala asura uvenot shuach mutarot}. Under this reading, it is a case of {\i mekayem devarav}, and only the {\i benot shuach} would be released. {\i Tosfot} notes the apparent asymmetry: in the {\i Kufri} onion case, the {\i gemara} assumed a qualifier ({\i mekayem devarav}), while here it assumes a fundamentally different statement ({\i meshane devarav}). In both cases, however, the {\i gemara} essentially assigns the hypothetical that yields the desired result, and {\i Ravina}\u8217's question remains formally unresolved \u8212- both alternatives are live possibilities, and the {\i gemara} does not definitively close the question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five-Person Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes by identifying the {\i tanna} behind a {\i baraita} that synthesizes the entire discussion. The {\i baraita} states: {\i nadar michamishah benei adam ke\u8217'echad} \u8212- someone made a vow against five people as a single group. Two rules follow: (1) {\i hutar le\u8217'echad mehen hutru kullan} \u8212- if he was released from one of them, they are all released; (2) {\i chutz me\u8217'echad mehen, hu mutar vehen asurin} \u8212- except for one of them, that one is permitted and the others remain forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two statements appear to contradict each other. The first says releasing one releases all. The second says excluding one leaves the rest forbidden. The {\i gemara} understands this as two distinct positions, reflecting two different opinions \u8212- one in line with {\i hutar miktzato hutar kulo} and one that does not apply that principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabba} reads the {\i baraita} as internally coherent under {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's view. The {\i reisha} \u8212- {\i hutar le\u8217'echad hutru kullan} \u8212- reflects the case where the speaker would have been {\i meshane devarav}: he would have said an entirely different statement, and therefore the entire {\i neder} collapses. This is {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's position. The {\i seifa} \u8212- {\i chutz me\u8217'echad vehen asurin} \u8212- reflects the case where the speaker would have been {\i mekayem devarav}: he would have said the same statement with only a qualifier. In this case, {\i Rabba} says, even {\i Rabbi Akiva} agrees that the {\i neder} survives and only the excluded individual is released. Thus according to {\i Rabba}, the entire {\i baraita} is authored by {\i Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- the two clauses describe two scenarios within his framework, and the {\i seifa} captures the boundary of his own principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} reads the {\i baraita} differently. The {\i seifa} \u8212- {\i chutz me\u8217'echad vehen asurin} \u8212- represents the position of the {\i rabanan} who disagree with {\i Rabbi Akiva}. These {\i rabanan} hold that when the speaker would have been {\i mekayem devarav}, the {\i neder} stands and only the qualifier-exception is released. But the {\i rabanan} concede the {\i reisha}: when the speaker would have been {\i meshane devarav}, the entire {\i neder} is void \u8212- {\i divrei hakol}. Thus according to {\i Rava}, the {\i baraita} is authored by the {\i rabanan}, not by {\i Rabbi Akiva}, and its two clauses represent the {\i rabanan}\u8217's own graduated position: {\i meshane devarav} \u8594 ? all released; {\i mekayem devarav} \u8594 ? only the one excluded. {\i Rabbi Akiva} himself goes further, holding that even {\i mekayem devarav} releases the entire {\i neder} \u8212- a position that does not appear in this {\i baraita} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} thus serves as the conceptual anchor for the entire sugya. The debate between {\i Rabba} and {\i Rava} over the scope of {\i neder she\u8217'hutar miktzato hutar kulo} \u8212- whether {\i meshane devarav} alone triggers universal release, or whether even {\i mekayem devarav} suffices \u8212- finds its textual basis precisely in this {\i baraita}\u8217's juxtaposition of {\i hutar le\u8217'echad hutru kullan} against {\i chutz me\u8217'echad vehen asurin}. According to {\i Rabba}, the {\i baraita} is {\i Rabbi Akiva} articulating the limits of his own principle. According to {\i Rava}, the {\i baraita} is the {\i rabanan} articulating a position that partially concedes to {\i Rabbi Akiva}. Either way, the underlying machinery of the sugya \u8212- the hypothetical-speech analysis, the {\i mekayem}/{\i meshane} distinction, and the question of when partial release voids a {\i neder} entirely \u8212- is illuminated by this final {\i baraita}, which the redactor of the {\i Bavli} has placed at the conclusion of the discussion to crystallize what was at stake throughout.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 28\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vows Under Duress\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We open on daf 28 by returning to the {\i mishnah} that was introduced at the close of 27b, since it forms the primary focus of today\u8217's discussion. The {\i mishnah} belongs to a series of categories of {\i nidarim} that are not binding, and this particular category is {\i nidrei onasin} \u8212- vows made under duress.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nidrei Onasin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader context of the chapter has already explored the first category, which concerned {\i onus} in the sense of circumstances beyond one\u8217's control that prevent a stipulation from being fulfilled \u8212- a general question applicable to conditional formulations in any setting. The present {\i mishnah}, however, treats a more specific case: a {\i neder} made under a degree of compulsion, not absolute coercion, but pressure exerted to protect oneself from robbery or violence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i nodrim le-hargin u-le-charamin u-le-mochsin} \u8212- one may make a {\i neder} to those who would kill for money, to {\i charamin} (a similar category of extortionists), and to {\i mochsin}, tax collectors. The inclusion of tax collectors raises an immediate question: why are they treated as illegitimately taking one\u8217's money, given that taxes are ostensibly a legal obligation? The {\i Gemara} will address this directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i she-hi terumah} \u8212- one may vow that the produce is {\i terumah}, thereby deterring Jewish bandits who would not dare touch sanctified priestly portions. As the {\i Tosafos} observes with some wryness, there is a certain consistency to the moral hierarchy at play: people who had no compunction about robbery drew the line at consuming {\i terumah}. To make the claim credible, one takes a {\i neder} to back it up \u8212- {\i af al pi she-eino shel terumah}, even though it is not actually {\i terumah}. Similarly, one may claim the goods belong to the royal house, {\i she-hen shel beit ha-melech}, and swear accordingly, {\i af al pi she-einam shel beit ha-melech}, even though they do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai/Hillel Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then records the dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel. Beit Shammai rules: {\i be-chol nodrim chutz mi-bishvua} \u8212- one may use any form of {\i neder} except a {\i shvua}. Beit Hillel rules: {\i af bishvua} \u8212- even a {\i shvua} is permitted. A further layer of the dispute concerns whether one may initiate the vow or must wait to be prompted. Beit Shammai holds {\i lo yiftach lo beneder} \u8212- do not open with a {\i neder} on your own initiative; wait for the extortioner to demand it. Beit Hillel rules {\i af yiftach lo} \u8212- one may even take the initiative. And regarding scope: if the extortioner says \u8220"vow that your wife will receive no benefit from you,\u8221" may one expand it to include one\u8217's children as well? Beit Shammai rules that the wife is then permitted and the sons remain forbidden, while Beit Hillel holds {\i elu va-elu mutarin} \u8212- both are permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai\u8217's general stringency regarding {\i shvua} reflects their more exacting treatment of oath-taking. At one level, this is a technical matter: a {\i shvua} invokes, explicitly or implicitly, the divine name, and carries with it the weight of {\i lo yenakeh Hashem} \u8212- God does not hold blameless one who takes His name in vain. But there is also a broader conceptual dimension. Beit Shammai\u8217's characteristic position \u8212- evident in the famous debate about how one praises a bride, where Beit Shammai insists one describe her only as she actually is, {\i kallah ka-mot she-hi} \u8212- reflects a thoroughgoing commitment to the alignment of word and truth. A {\i shvua}, whose very purpose is to affirm the veracity of one\u8217's statement, is for Beit Shammai not merely a formal halachic act but a claim to truthfulness. To deploy it in a context of deliberate misrepresentation, even under duress, compromises that integrity in a way that a {\i neder} \u8212- which functions more indirectly \u8212- does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framework also illuminates the dispute about initiation and expansion. One reading of Beit Shammai\u8217's position is purely quantitative: whatever you do not say under direct compulsion is not compelled. If the extortioner did not demand a {\i neder} and you offered one, or if he demanded a {\i neder} about X and you added Y, those additional words are freely chosen and therefore binding, even if the overall circumstances were coercive. Beit Hillel, by contrast, holds that the entire situation compels you \u8212- without initiating or elaborating, you may not be believed, and so all of it falls within the scope of {\i onus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Beit Shammai may also be making a deeper conceptual point. If the extortioner is essentially feeding you a script \u8212- demanding that you say precisely X \u8212- then in a sense those are not your words at all; you are merely voicing what he dictated. The compulsion is total and the speech-act is his, not yours, and therefore it carries no binding force. Any degree of initiative or elaboration on your part, however, reintroduces your own agency, and once those are your words, the duress of the surrounding circumstances is insufficient to sever you from your {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Student Q&A\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question naturally arises about the structure of the {\i machlokes}: is the debate about the {\i neder} formulation a question of whether the {\i neder} is binding, or a question of whether one is permitted to make it in the first place? The answer differs depending on whether one is dealing with a {\i neder} or a {\i shvua}. With a {\i neder} \u8212- the formulation of {\i konam} making something forbidden \u8212- no one disputes that one is {\i permitted} to say it. There is no category of {\i neder sheker} or {\i neder shav}. The entire dispute concerns whether it is {\i binding}: the {\i neder} was never sincerely meant, and the question is whether the duress is sufficient to disassociate the speaker from those words. The {\i shvua}, by contrast, raises a prior question: are you {\i permitted} to make it at all? When you swear that this produce is {\i terumah}, you are asserting the truth of a false claim. That is where the concern of {\i shvuat sheker} or {\i shvuat shav} enters, and that is precisely what Beit Shammai forbids.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Begins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this framework in place, the {\i Gemara} opens its analysis by pressing on the classification of tax collectors. If {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} \u8212- the halachic principle articulated by Shmuel that the law of the government is the law \u8212- governs our relationship to state authority, why does the {\i mishnah} treat the tax collector as an illegitimate extortioner rather than a lawful agent? That question launches the {\i Gemara\u8217's} extended discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dina D\u8217'Malchusa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel/Tax Collector\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises the challenge directly: Shmuel\u8217's principle of {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} holds that we are halachically bound by the laws of the governing authority. If so, why is the tax collector treated as someone from whom one may legitimately protect oneself through misrepresentation? The {\i mishnah} seems to assume that such taxation is unjust \u8212- but how can that be?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Illegitimate Tax\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers with two distinct explanations. The first, transmitted in the name of Shmuel himself through a chain of {\i amoraim}: {\i b\u8217'mochs she\u8217'ein lo kitzva} \u8212- we are speaking of a tax collector who operates without a fixed rate. The amount demanded is entirely arbitrary, governed by nothing more than his whim at the moment of collection. The second explanation, from the school of Rabbi Yannai: {\i b\u8217'mochs ha\u8217'omeid me\u8217'eilav} \u8212- a tax collector who is self-appointed, meaning one who simply positions himself and claims the right to collect by virtue of his own presence, not any governmental authorization. In this case, it is not a legitimate government whose laws are being enforced; it is, in effect, a private individual with a weapon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} clarifies the conceptual distinction between the two cases precisely. In the first case, the problem lies in the {\i din} component: {\i lav dina hu ela chamsanusa} \u8212- it is not law but robbery. An arbitrary, uncapped demand is not a {\i din}, a genuine legal norm with some objective standard; it is mere extortion dressed in official garb. In the second case, the problem lies in the {\i malchusa} component: there is no legitimate governmental authority standing behind the collector. Thus, {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} requires both elements \u8212- a genuine {\i din} that meets some objective standard of lawfulness, and a genuine {\i malchusa} that constitutes a legitimate governing power. Where either element is absent, the principle does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Roman/Babylon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Understanding why the {\i mishnah} does not articulate these qualifications explicitly requires attention to historical context. Throughout the {\i mishnayos}, tax collectors are treated as presumptively illegitimate \u8212- a stance that reflects the reality of Roman rule in the Land of Israel. Roman taxation in that period was often implemented through the practice of {\i tax farming}: the government sold the right to collect taxes in a given province to a private purchaser, who would then extract as much as he could, keeping the proceeds above whatever he had paid for the franchise. This arrangement is precisely the model of {\i mochs she\u8217'ein lo kitzva} \u8212- and explains why the figure of the tax collector appears alongside prostitutes and bandits in the literature of the period as emblematic of social degradation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The statement of {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina}, by contrast, originates not from the {\i mishnah} but from Shmuel, who operated in Babylonia under the Sassanid Persian government \u8212- a regime that treated the Jewish community fairly and extended it considerable autonomy. Shmuel himself had a close personal relationship with the Persian king Shapur. It is therefore entirely coherent that a positive, binding relationship to civil authority would be articulated in that context, and that this principle would then be applied {\i retroactively} to explain the limiting conditions of the {\i mishnah}: when the {\i mishnah} treats tax collectors as illegitimate, it must be because they fall into one of the two categories \u8212- uncapped assessment or self-appointment \u8212- that fall outside the scope of genuine {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dina Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion opens onto a broader set of questions about what precisely {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} obligates. It clearly does not extend to ritual matters: a civil government cannot redefine the parameters of Jewish marriage, divorce, or any domain of religious law. That much goes without saying. A somewhat more contested question concerns the laws of inheritance. Civil inheritance law differs significantly from {\i halachic} inheritance law, and the overwhelming consensus among {\i poskim} has been that secular inheritance law does not override {\i din Torah} in this domain \u8212- hence the halachic importance of properly structured {\i halachic} wills. But even in the monetary domain where {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa} is most clearly applicable, an important distinction must be drawn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt vs.\u160 ?Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical distinction is between the {\i creation of obligations} and the {\i obligation to discharge them}. The principle of {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} operates, based on the {\i sugya} in Bava Kamma, primarily in the domain of {\i dinei mamonos} \u8212- it determines questions of ownership and establishes who owes what to whom. What it does not do is override the separate halachic rules governing whether one is obligated to {\i pay} a debt one owes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate: when a government levies a tax, {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa} determines that one genuinely {\i owes} that sum. The money the government collects in taxes is not considered stolen, and public infrastructure built with tax revenue may be used without concern. Similarly, when the government grants someone \u8212- say, through the mechanism of tax farming \u8212- the right to collect those taxes, one owes that individual the money, and failing to pay constitutes theft. But if the government itself is the direct creditor, {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa} establishes the debt without necessarily imposing the full halachic obligation to discharge it. The {\i Ran} makes this explicit in the passage found in the portion of his commentary preserved in the {\i Chisronos HaShas}: the appointed collector \u8212- one who collects on behalf of the government rather than having purchased the franchise \u8212- cannot {\i compel} a fellow Jew to pay, {\i im lo miyiras hamalchus}, unless the fear of governmental consequences comes into play. Because {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina}, in the end, creates no more than a debt, and halachic tradition recognizes that {\i hafka\u8217'as halva\u8217'ah} \u8212- extinguishing a debt without payment \u8212- is in principle permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical and moral stakes of this position are considerable. There is a reported view \u8212- circulated privately and always disavowed publicly by its author \u8212- that on this basis one need not pay one\u8217's taxes. The halachic foundation invoked was this very principle: {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa} creates the obligation to owe but not necessarily to pay, and {\i hafka\u8217'as halva\u8217'ah} is permissible. The profound problem with this position, leaving aside its halachic merits, is the {\i chilul Hashem} it creates \u8212- a concern that the {\i Gemara} itself treats as dispositive even in discussions where some technical halachic latitude might otherwise exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tax Evasion Ethics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} in Bava Kamma that deals with related questions about obligations toward non-Jews introduced the issue of tax evasion in precisely these terms. The discussion there was sufficiently uncomfortable that the text was modified: references to {\i gezel goy} \u8212- taking from a non-Jew \u8212- were altered in many printed editions to {\i gezel Kena\u8217'ani}, so that the church, should it read the text, could be told that the discussion pertained only to the ancient Canaanites. Whatever one makes of the underlying halachic categories, the {\i Gemara} consistently inserts the {\i chilul Hashem} consideration as an override. When one\u8217's conduct \u8212- even if arguably within a technical halachic framework \u8212- creates the public impression that Jews cheat on their taxes or dishonor their financial obligations, that alone is sufficient grounds for prohibition. The recurring news stories of prominent figures prosecuted for financial crimes make this concern anything but theoretical. More fundamentally, it is ethically problematic to reduce the entire question to {\i chilul Hashem} as if the sole concern is the risk of being caught; the {\i rishonim} and {\i acharonim} increasingly recognize that the differential legal treatment of obligations toward non-Jews in earlier sources was predicated on specific historical conditions of oppressive and hostile regimes, conditions that do not characterize the contemporary context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Israel State\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran}\u8217's discussion of {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} carries significant implications for the question of the halachic legitimacy of the modern State of Israel \u8212- a matter of ongoing controversy. The {\i Ran} quotes {\i Tosafos} to the effect that {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} applies only to non-Jewish kings, and explains the underlying rationale: the basis for the king\u8217's authority to legislate is that he owns the land, and those who live on it are subject to his rules as a condition of residence \u8212- a feudal theory of sovereignty. By this logic, the principle does not apply in the Land of Israel, because {\i Eretz Yisrael kol Yisrael shutafim bah} \u8212- the entire Jewish people holds a share in the Land of Israel, and no individual sovereign can invoke the landowner\u8217's prerogative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i Ran} has been invoked by those who wish to deny the halachic legitimacy of the secular State of Israel: since {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} does not apply in Israel, and the state does not operate through a recognized framework of {\i din melech} or {\i din Torah}, its legislative authority lacks halachic grounding. Others ground the state\u8217's legitimacy differently \u8212- either by arguing that the {\i Knesset} exercises something analogous to the authority of a {\i melech} in its contemporary democratic form, or by arguing that a democratically elected government represents a form of collective consent that generates its own binding norms. Each of these positions engages differently with the {\i Ran}. It should be noted that the {\i Ran} himself, in his {\i Drashos HaRan}, articulates the concept of {\i din hamelech} \u8212- the king\u8217's independent legislative authority to govern society beyond the parameters of {\i Torah} law \u8212- which suggests that even within the Land of Israel, some form of governing authority with real legislative power is recognized, even if it does not operate under the rubric of {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Tax Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} draws a further distinction between two types of tax collection that bears directly on the original {\i mishnah}. If an individual \u8212- including a Jew \u8212- {\i purchased} the right to collect taxes (the tax farming model), then one is fully obligated to pay him, because {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa} has legitimately created that right and the purchaser holds it. Attempting to misrepresent one\u8217's goods as {\i terumah} in order to avoid payment to such a collector would not be permitted. If, on the other hand, the collector has been {\i appointed} by the government to collect on its behalf \u8212- collecting for the king rather than for his own account \u8212- then one may not swear falsely, {\i deleika unsa}, because the situation is not one in which you are being forced to surrender what you do not legitimately owe. The debt is real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Payment Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But \u8212- and this is the {\i Ran}\u8217's striking final point, preserved in the {\i Chisronos HaShas} passage \u8212- even in the case of a legitimately appointed government collector, the collector cannot {\i compel} payment. {\i Mihu ein memuneh zeh rashai lehachriach Yisrael chaveiro lifro\u8217'a lo et hameches} \u8212- this appointee does not have the right to force a Jew to pay him the tax. The reason: {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina lo yehei ela halva\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa} creates no more than a debt, and {\i hafka\u8217'as halva\u8217'ah muteres} \u8212- extinguishing a debt without payment is halachically permissible. Therefore, one cannot swear falsely (since the debt is real), but one is not obligated to pay it either (since the duty to pay debts to non-Jews follows its own halachic logic). The {\i Ran}\u8217's position thus creates an unusual asymmetry: {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa} generates genuine obligations of {\i ownership} and {\i debt-creation}, but leaves the question of actual payment discharge to separate halachic principles.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Words vs.\u160 ?Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidden Reservation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the question of precisely how the vow described in the {\i mishnah} functions \u8212- that is, what formulation one actually uses when taking a {\i neder} to deflect bandits or tax collectors, and whether a purely mental reservation suffices to prevent it from being binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Piv Ve-Libo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites Rav Amram in the name of Rav: the formulation is {\i ya\u8217'asru peirot olam alai im einam shel beit ha-melech} \u8212- let all the fruit of the world be forbidden to me if these do not belong to the king. The implication is that in one\u8217's heart, one does not mean it. This would appear to be a case of {\i ein piv ve-libo shavin} \u8212- one\u8217's mouth and heart are not in agreement. But the {\i Gemara} rejects this immediately: {\i keivan de-amar ya\u8217'asru, yetasru lei kol peirei alma} \u8212- once you have said \u8220"let them be forbidden,\u8221" they are in fact forbidden. The Gemara will not allow a simple disclaimer of intent to dissolve the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Modifiers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore resolves the matter differently: {\i be-omer be-libo hayom} \u8212- one mentally qualifies the vow as applying only {\i today}, while the words spoken aloud are unqualified. Although in general {\i devarim she-be-lev einam devarim} \u8212- thoughts in one\u8217's heart do not constitute speech-acts with legal force \u8212- {\i legabei onsin shani}, in cases of compulsion the rule is different. The mental qualification is sufficient to limit the scope of the {\i neder} to the present day. Crucially, however, the {\i neder} is still operative for that day; one cannot eat the fruit today. The mental reservation narrows the {\i neder}; it does not nullify it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeruzin/Havai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution raises a deeper question. At first glance, the {\i mishnah} appears to permit something stronger: a {\i neder} declared without any genuine intent, with the understanding that the duress alone prevents it from being binding. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's limitation \u8212- that one may only {\i qualify} the vow, not entirely disclaim it \u8212- calls for explanation, particularly in light of the earlier categories of {\i nidarim} that are not binding. Consider {\i nidrei zeruzin}: if a seller says \u8220"I will not take less than ten {\i zuz} for this,\u8221" the {\i neder} is not binding because the words patently do not reflect actual intent. Or {\i nidrei havai}: exaggerated speech like \u8220"I saw a snake as large as a house\u8221" is simply not treated as a sincere speech-act, regardless of what the words literally say. In both of those cases, the person\u8217's words do not match their intent \u8212- and we accept that the intent controls.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qualification Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does the {\i Gemara} not permit the same in the case of a vow to bandits? The distinguishing factor appears to lie in the {\i context} in which the {\i neder} is made. In {\i nidrei zeruzin} and {\i nidrei havai}, the surrounding social context makes it immediately apparent \u8212- to everyone present, including the other party \u8212- that the words are not to be taken as a sincere vow. The vow is, as it were, transparent to all. But in the bandit scenario, the entire purpose of the {\i neder} is precisely to {\i project} sincerity \u8212- to convince the extortioner that the speaker genuinely means what he says. The {\i neder} is made within a context in which its literal import is the entire point; the other party is demanding it as a truth-verification mechanism. In that setting, one cannot invoke a purely private intention to dissolve what has been publicly performed as a sincere vow. The {\i beit din} cannot look past the context of the {\i neder} and treat it as though the speaker might simply not have meant it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What one {\i can} do is reinterpret the vow\u8217's scope \u8212- the principle of {\i perushen lehakel}, that one may offer a limiting interpretation of what one\u8217's words meant. This is a legitimate move even in normal circumstances, though in some contexts it is limited to a {\i talmid chacham} capable of articulating such distinctions. In the {\i onus} context, it may be that even an ordinary person may invoke this interpretive approach. But the category of complete non-intent \u8212- {\i lo kavanti} in the fullest sense \u8212- is not available when the {\i neder} was made in a context where sincerity of intent was, by design, the entire point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vows vs.\u160 ?Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna/Braisa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now addresses an apparent tension between the {\i mishnah} and a {\i braisa} on the question of what one may offer under duress. Our {\i mishnah} frames the debate between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel in terms of a {\i neder}: Beit Shammai rules {\i lo yiftach lo be-neder} \u8212- do not initiate a {\i neder}; Beit Hillel rules {\i af yiftach lo} \u8212- one may initiate. Rav Huna then cites a {\i braisa} that frames the debate differently: Beit Shammai rules {\i lo yiftach lo bishvua} \u8212- do not open with a {\i shvua}; Beit Hillel rules {\i af yiftach lo bishvua} \u8212- one may even offer a {\i shvua}. At first reading, these two formulations seem contradictory. The {\i braisa} implies that Beit Shammai permits initiating a {\i neder}, since only the {\i shvua} is restricted; the {\i mishnah}, however, implies that Beit Shammai restricts even the {\i neder}. And the {\i mishnah} implies that even according to Beit Shammai one may not respond with a {\i shvua} when the extortioner demands it, whereas the {\i braisa} seems to limit the prohibition only to {\i initiating} one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by recognizing that the {\i mishnah} and the {\i braisa} are each designed to teach a different {\i chiddush}. The {\i mishnah} addresses the {\i neder} case in order to convey the full stringency of Beit Shammai: Beit Shammai does not even permit {\i initiating} a {\i neder}. But when it comes to a {\i shvua}, Beit Shammai goes even further \u8212- one may not even {\i respond} with a {\i shvua} when it is demanded. The {\i braisa}, in turn, addresses the {\i shvua} case in order to convey the full permissiveness of Beit Hillel: Beit Hillel permits not merely responding with a {\i shvua} but initiating one. Thus: {\i tana mateisein be-neder lehodiah kocha de-Beit Shammai} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} teaches through the {\i neder} to show how far Beit Shammai restricts (no initiation even of a {\i neder}); {\i tana braisa bishvua lehodiah kocha de-Beit Hillel} \u8212- the {\i braisa} teaches through the {\i shvua} to show how far Beit Hillel permits (even initiating a {\i shvua}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion raises a serious ethical question that the {\i Gemara} does not entirely sidestep. If Beit Hillel permits offering a {\i shvua} to deflect a bandit \u8212- and one swears, say, that these goods are {\i terumah} \u8212- is one not swearing a {\i shvuat sheker}? The {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer, developed through the mechanism of {\i be-omer be-libo hayom}, suggests that the {\i shvua} too must be structured in a way that involves a qualifying mental reservation, not a wholesale false assertion. Whether swearing with {\i shem Hashem} that something is {\i terumah} \u8212- even with a mental qualifier in the form of a time-limitation \u8212- constitutes a truly permissible {\i shvua}, or whether some formulation short of directly swearing to the falsehood is required, remains an underlying tension in the analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers a further reading of the {\i braisa} that reframes the issue entirely. The phrase {\i lo yiftach lo bishvua} in the {\i braisa} may not be referring to the bandit scenario at all. {\i Yiftach lo} \u8212- \u8220"opening\u8221" for someone \u8212- is the technical language of {\i she\u8217'elah}, the process by which a {\i beit din} releases someone from a {\i neder} or {\i shvua} through the mechanism of finding a {\i petach} or {\i charata}. Beit Shammai\u8217's position, on this reading, is {\i ein she\u8217'elah bi-shvua} \u8212- there is no process of halachic release from a {\i shvua}. One may obtain {\i hataras nedarim}, but Beit Shammai does not recognize an analogous {\i hataras shevuos}. This is entirely consistent with Beit Shammai\u8217's characteristically weighty treatment of the {\i shvua} as an irreversible commitment. One who takes a {\i shvua} and later regrets it has no recourse to a {\i beit din} for release. The gravity of a {\i shvua} is not something that can be undone by retroactive {\i charata}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to the episode of {\i Yiftach Ha-Gil\u8217'adi} is striking and noted: Yiftach did not have his vow released precisely because no one opened ({\i yiftach}) the mechanism of {\i she\u8217'elah} for him. Beit Shammai\u8217's position may represent a principled insistence that such a mechanism ought not to exist for a {\i shvua}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel, by contrast, rules {\i yesh she\u8217'elah bishvua} \u8212- the same process of halachic release available for a {\i neder} applies equally to a {\i shvua}. The full apparatus of {\i petach} and {\i charata} is available to someone who has taken a {\i shvua} and now seeks release from it. This is of a piece with Beit Hillel\u8217's broader approach throughout this chapter: the comprehensive circumstances of duress or regret are sufficient to engage the mechanisms of release, whether the original speech-act was a {\i neder} or a {\i shvua}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kodashim Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kadosh Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the analysis of {\i nidrei onasin} substantially complete, the {\i Gemara} transitions to a new {\i mishnah} that appears at first to be entirely unrelated. The four categories of non-binding {\i nidarim} \u8212- {\i havai}, {\i shegagos}, {\i onsin}, and the opening category of conditional {\i nedarim} \u8212- have all been addressed. Now the {\i mishnah} introduces a different conceptual dimension: the question of whether a {\i neder} can acquire a status analogous to {\i hekdesh}, sanctified property dedicated to the {\i Mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question arises from the very linguistic foundation of the {\i neder}. When one says {\i konam}, one is invoking a term drawn from the world of {\i korbanos} \u8212- {\i konam} is a cognate of {\i korban} \u8212- to render something forbidden. Rabbi Meir holds that this is not merely a formal parallel: when one says {\i konam} and renders something forbidden to all benefit, one has effectively instilled upon the object a status that draws genuinely from the world of {\i hekdesh}. Consequently, two concepts from {\i hilchos kodashim} apply: {\i me\u8217'ilah} and {\i pidyon}. {\i Me\u8217'ilah} is the transgression of deriving benefit from sanctified property, and according to Rabbi Meir, one who benefits from an object declared {\i konam} transgresses {\i me\u8217'ilah}, not merely the prohibition of {\i lo yachel devaro}. And {\i pidyon} \u8212- redemption \u8212- is the mechanism by which the sanctified status of an object may be transferred to a monetary substitute, after which the original object becomes permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a conceptually remarkable position. {\i Me\u8217'ilah} in its classical sense involves misappropriating property that belongs to the {\i Mikdash}; but an object declared {\i konam} does not belong to the {\i Mikdash}. The operative concept in Rabbi Meir\u8217's view must be that {\i me\u8217'ilah} is not primarily about ownership but about violation of sanctified status \u8212- and that status can exist in the domain of {\i nedarim} just as it does in the domain of {\i hekdesh}. An analogy might be found in {\i ma\u8217'al ba-cherem}: the violation of {\i cherem} is not a theft from any party but an abuse of the object\u8217's declared status of inviolability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Pidyon} is even more conceptually challenging in this context. When one redeems a sanctified animal that has developed a {\i mum} (blemish), the money paid transfers the sanctified status from the animal to the coins, the animal becomes permitted, and the coins go to the {\i Mikdash}. In the {\i konam} scenario, who receives the redemption money? The answer is that the mechanism here is less about ownership transfer and more about transfer of prohibitory status. One substitutes another object, which now carries the {\i issur}, in place of the original, which is then released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The use of the word {\i konam} specifically \u8212- rather than a more generic formulation of {\i issur} \u8212- appears to be what activates these {\i kodashim}-like consequences. {\i Konam} is the {\i neder} term most directly rooted in the language of {\i korban}, and it is this linguistic connection that provides the basis for applying the categories of {\i me\u8217'ilah} and {\i pidyon} to the {\i neder}-world. Whether one says {\i konam} in a formulation that restricts the object to oneself alone, or to all people, also matters: the {\i korban}-like quality of universal prohibition ({\i assur le-kulam}) is more fully realized when no one at all may benefit, which aligns more closely with the status of {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Trees/Garments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unlimited Pidyon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now presents two sets of cases to illustrate when {\i pidyon} applies and when it does not. The first clause states: {\i harei netios elu korban im einan niktzatzos} \u8212- these saplings are a {\i korban} if they are not chopped down; {\i tallis zo korban im eina nisrefes} \u8212- this garment is a {\i korban} if it does not get burned. In both cases, {\i yesh lahen pidyon} \u8212- there is {\i pidyon}. The formulation uses the word {\i korban} explicitly, which could suggest either genuine {\i kedushas korban} or {\i konam}-style {\i neder}. The preferred reading, given the context of {\i Nedarim}, is the latter: these are {\i konam} declarations that have acquired, per Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, the halachic attributes of {\i hekdesh}, including the availability of {\i pidyon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limited No Pidyon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second clause states: {\i harei netios elu korban ad she-yikatzatzu} \u8212- these are {\i korban} until they are chopped down; {\i tallis zo korban ad she-tisaref} \u8212- this garment is {\i korban} until it is burned. In these cases, {\i ein lahen pidyon} \u8212- there is no {\i pidyon}. The difference appears to lie in the temporal scope of the {\i neder}. When one sanctifies something without qualification, the status applies permanently, which is analogous to genuine {\i hekdesh}, and the apparatus of {\i pidyon} is therefore available. When one gives the sanctification an expiration point \u8212- \u8220"until it is chopped down\u8221" or \u8220"until it burns\u8221" \u8212- one has imposed a time limit that is fundamentally inconsistent with genuine {\i hekdesh}. You cannot sanctify something as an {\i olah} for ten hours; the sanctified status either applies absolutely or it is not genuine {\i kedushas hekdesh}. A time-limited sanctification therefore lacks the full character of {\i hekdesh} and consequently does not trigger the mechanism of {\i pidyon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam/Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether the {\i mishnah} is speaking of genuine {\i kedushas korban} or of {\i konam} declarations runs as a structural theme through the {\i Ran}\u8217's commentary, where he develops two parallel explanations: one treating the {\i mishnah} as referring to actual {\i hekdesh be-damim} (sanctification of the object\u8217's monetary value for the purpose of purchasing a {\i korban}), and the other treating it as referring to {\i konam}-style {\i nedarim} that have acquired the attributes of {\i hekdesh} through Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle. Given that we are in {\i Maseches Nedarim} and that the language of {\i konam} pervades the surrounding discussion, the latter reading has much to recommend it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Time-Bound Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Condition Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the specific {\i mishnayos} of the previous section to analyze them more carefully. The first point of inquiry is why the {\i mishnah} found it necessary to state the condition explicitly \u8212- {\i im einan niktzatzos} \u8212- in the first clause, when the real {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah} appears to be the second clause\u8217's ruling that there is no {\i pidyon}. The answer is that the first clause\u8217's condition is stated precisely to contrast it with the second, and also because the first clause itself contains a {\i chiddush} that is not self-evident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar explains: the first clause is speaking of a case where one says \u8220"let these trees be {\i korban} if they are not chopped down {\i today}\u8221" \u8212- and then the day passes and they are not chopped down. One might have thought that because the person {\i expected} them to be chopped down \u8212- that expectation being the implicit motivation for the vow \u8212- the {\i neder} was never entirely sincere, and therefore not binding when the condition happened to be satisfied. Ameimar\u8217's ruling is that this is incorrect: once the condition stipulated in the {\i neder} is satisfied, the {\i neder} is binding, regardless of what the person anticipated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strong Wind\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presses further: if the {\i chiddush} is simply that a satisfied condition binds, that seems obvious. The {\i Gemara} responds that the case is specifically one where there is {\i zika nefisha} \u8212- a strong wind \u8212- such that destruction of the saplings seemed near-certain. The vow thus had the character of a conditional deal: \u8220"If somehow, against all odds, these saplings survive the day, they shall be {\i korban}.\u8221" In such circumstances, one might argue that the person never truly intended to sanctify the trees \u8212- the condition was formulated as a virtual impossibility. Nevertheless, the {\i Gemara} rules that since the condition was formally stated and formally satisfied, the {\i neder} is binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fire Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel scenario is applied to the garment case. A garment declared {\i korban} \u8220"if it is not burned\u8221" draws its meaning from a situation where there is {\i deleika} \u8212- a fire in the vicinity \u8212- making destruction of the garment a near-certainty. Again, one might characterize the vow as a practical impossibility and question whether genuine intent is present; and again, the {\i Gemara} rules that once the condition is stipulated and satisfied, the {\i neder} binds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic connects to the earlier discussion of {\i nidrei zeruzin}: the question of whether a {\i neder} reflects sincere intent is contextually determined, and a condition-based vow, even one predicated on an expected outcome that did not materialize, does not fall into the category of obviously-insincere speech.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pidyon Preview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the binding character of the first clause, the {\i Gemara} has confirmed that these {\i konam} declarations \u8212- made without fixed time limits, or with conditions that happen to be satisfied \u8212- carry the attributes of {\i hekdesh} and therefore have {\i pidyon}. The remaining and more complex question \u8212- why the time-limited formulations in the second clause of the {\i mishnah} result in {\i ein lahen pidyon} \u8212- is where the analysis will continue. The assumption going forward is that we are dealing with {\i konam}-style {\i nedarim}, not literal {\i kedushas korban}, and that the {\i chiddush} of the second clause concerns the relationship between a time-bound {\i neder} and the preconditions for the mechanism of {\i pidyon} to apply at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 29\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pidyon & Meilah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 29 is a relatively compact daf, and while the sheer volume of {\i Ran} commentary might appear intimidating, the conceptual difficulty is more manageable than many other pages of the tractate. The {\i gemara} picks up at the bottom of 28b and moves into a {\i mishna} dealing with the concepts of {\i pidyon} and, relatedly, {\i meilah} in the context of {\i konamot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents two cases. If someone declares that certain saplings ({\i netiot}) or a garment ({\i tallit}) are {\i korban}, that declaration carries with it the possibility of {\i pidyon} \u8212- the object\u8217's status can be transferred through redemption. However, if the declaration is temporally limited \u8212- \u8220"it is {\i korban} until it is burned\u8221" or \u8220"until it is cut down\u8221" \u8212- then {\i ein la pidyon}: no redemption is possible. Understanding why a time-bound {\i konam} eliminates {\i pidyon} is the central problem of the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konamot & Korbanot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before entering the particulars of the {\i mishna}, it is necessary to establish the foundational framework. The key principle is that {\i konamot yesh bahen mishum meilah} \u8212- {\i konamot} draw from the world of {\i korbanot}. This applies specifically when the {\i konam} formula is used and when the prohibition is directed not merely at a particular individual but at everyone, making the object universally forbidden in the manner of a genuine {\i korban}. Under these conditions, the {\i konam} generates two significant legal consequences: first, the concept of {\i meilah} \u8212- misuse of the object, not merely a violation of the {\i neder}, but an act akin to trespass of {\i hekdesh}; and second, the concept of {\i pidyon} \u8212- the capacity to transfer that status onto something else, just as one may redeem an object consecrated to the Temple.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meilah Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i meilah} in this context is one of trespass and misuse rather than theft. When something is {\i hekdesh}, using it improperly constitutes {\i meilah}. The same logic, when imported into the world of {\i konamot}, means that benefiting from an object declared {\i konam} to everyone carries this added dimension beyond the mere {\i neder} violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pidyon Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Pidyon} here operates not as a transfer of ownership but as a transfer of status \u8212- and it is important to appreciate the distinction. If one sanctifies an animal as a {\i shelamim}, the Temple does not own the animal in the usual sense; ownership remains with the individual, but the animal now carries a status. When the animal becomes blemished, that status can be transferred onto money or another object through {\i pidyon}. The same logic applies here: the {\i konam} creates a status, and that status \u8212- not ownership \u8212- is what {\i pidyon} acts upon. This is the working presumption with which the {\i gemara} enters its analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bar Pada\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pidyon Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a challenge: {\i ule\u8217'olam} \u8212- is it truly the case that these objects can never be redeemed? Given that the principle is that {\i konamot} carry the mechanism of {\i pidyon}, why should a time-limited {\i konam} eliminate that mechanism entirely?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Auto-Cessation Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Pada\u8217's answer is as striking as it is counterintuitive. He argues that it is conceptually impossible for a halachic status to simply dissolve on its own, automatically, without an affirmative act of removal. The {\i gemara} illustrates this with two analogies: if someone were to say to a woman \u8220"you are my wife today but not my wife tomorrow,\u8221" she would not cease to be married without a {\i get}; similarly, if one were to say \u8220"this animal is an {\i olah} today but not an {\i olah} tomorrow,\u8221" the {\i olah} status would not simply evaporate. Something that pertains to an object\u8217's fundamental identity and status cannot be placed on a clock. You can transfer ownership for a fixed period \u8212- a {\i kinyan le\u8217'zman} is possible \u8212- but you cannot limit a status that goes to the very nature of what something is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ongoing Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the basis of this principle, Bar Pada reinterprets the phrase {\i ad she\u8217'yikatzetz} \u8212- \u8220"until they are cut down.\u8221" Rather than reading it as a simple terminus, he reads it as expressing an ongoing, continuous reapplication of the {\i konam} status. The declaration means: this status will persistently apply until the saplings are cut. The word {\i ad} does not mean \u8220"up to the point at which,\u8221" after which the status ceases; it means the status is continually being reinstalled throughout the entire period preceding the cutting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cyclical Pidyon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading explains the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that {\i ein lahem pidyon} \u8212- not that {\i pidyon} is categorically inoperative, but that it cannot achieve a lasting effect. If one attempts {\i pidyon}, the act does transfer the {\i konam} status onto the object used for redemption \u8212- in that sense {\i pidyon} works \u8212- but simultaneously the original object immediately reacquires its {\i konam} status, because the declaration is an ongoing mechanism running until the cutting. One can repeat the {\i pidyon} indefinitely; each time the other object picks up the status, and each time the original is immediately re-{\i konam}-ed.\u160 ?Once the saplings are actually cut, the ongoing mechanism ceases, and a single act of {\i pidyon} then suffices \u8212- {\i padaan pa\u8217'am achat vedayo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why {\i ein lahem pidyon} does not mean {\i pidyon} is impossible in principle; it means that {\i pidyon} cannot achieve a permanent result in the pre-cutting period. The distinction matters: saying {\i pidyon} fundamentally does not operate would leave the mechanism unexplained; saying it operates but is immediately reversed by an ongoing reinstallation is the logically coherent reading Bar Pada requires.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ulla\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Automatic Cessation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla reads the {\i mishna} differently. He holds that {\i keivan she\u8217'niktzetzu shuv ein podan} \u8212- once the saplings are cut, there is no need to redeem them at all, because their {\i konam} status ceases automatically upon cutting. He reads {\i ad she\u8217'yikatzetz} in its most natural sense: these saplings have this status until they are cut, and once they are cut, the status is simply gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ad Semantics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entire dispute thus hinges on the semantics of {\i ad}. Bar Pada maintains that {\i ad} cannot mean \u8220"until,\u8221" in the sense of automatic cessation, because no status can dissolve without an affirmative removal. {\i Ad she\u8217'yikatzetz} must therefore mean \u8220"ongoing until\u8221" \u8212- an active, continuous application of the status up to the point of cutting, with a permanent result achievable only through {\i pidyon} after cutting. Ulla, by contrast, holds that {\i ad} means precisely \u8220"until\u8221": the status is present and static up to the point of cutting, and then it ends \u8212- no act of removal required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Pidyon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A significant implication follows for Ulla\u8217's position regarding the pre-cutting period. The {\i Ran} presents two approaches to understanding Ulla. One approach holds that even according to Ulla, {\i pidyon} during the pre-cutting period would repeatedly re-establish the status, similar to Bar Pada\u8217's mechanism \u8212- though the status then ceases automatically at cutting regardless. The other approach, taken as obvious by {\i Tosafot}, is simpler: since Ulla reads {\i ad} as a plain terminus and rejects the idea of any ongoing reinstallation mechanism, if one were to perform {\i pidyon} during the pre-cutting period, the {\i pidyon} would be permanently effective. The object would be redeemed and done. Ulla needs no cyclical mechanism. According to this reading, {\i ein lahem pidyon} for Ulla means only that redemption is unnecessary after cutting \u8212- not that it fails during the preceding period.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedusha Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces Rav Hamnuna\u8217's challenge to Ulla\u8217's position. {\i Kedusha shebahen lehakan halcha?} \u8212- where did the {\i kedusha} go? How can Ulla countenance a {\i kedusha} simply vanishing? The challenge is given vivid form: if a man says to a woman \u8220"you are my wife today and tomorrow you are not my wife,\u8221" she does not cease to be married without a {\i get}. A status that pertains to something\u8217's fundamental identity requires an affirmative act to alter it; it does not dissolve by itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value vs.\u160 ?Essence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds by drawing a sharp distinction: {\i mi ka medamit kedushat damim l\u8217'kedushat haguf?} \u8212- how can you compare sanctity of value to sanctity of essence? {\i Kedushat damim paka b\u8217'chedi} \u8212- a {\i kedusha} rooted in value can cease automatically. {\i Kedushat haguf lo paka b\u8217'chedi} \u8212- a {\i kedusha} rooted in the identity and essence of the thing itself cannot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time-Bound Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does this distinction mean in practice? {\i Kedushat haguf} is exemplified by an animal sanctified as a {\i korban}: the animal\u8217's very identity has changed; it is an {\i olah}; no clock can be placed on that. {\i Kedushat damim} is more like a transfer of ownership-interest: one has dedicated the value or proceeds of an object to the Temple, which is closer to a transactional relationship and can, in principle, be time-bound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then arises: into which category do {\i konamot} fall? Logically, {\i konamot} are about status, not ownership \u8212- the saplings are not owned by the Temple; they simply bear a prohibition. The word {\i konam} draws on the language of {\i korban}, which is a status concept rather than a {\i bedek habayis} concept. Yet because the actual objects \u8212- saplings, a {\i tallit} \u8212- will never themselves be offered as {\i korbanot}, what is really being consecrated is their value or proceeds. It is precisely because these objects can only function in the {\i kedushat damim} world that Rava can maintain that their status can be time-bound and can cease automatically according to Ulla.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is thus: Rav Hamnuna\u8217's challenge \u8212- that {\i kedusha} cannot simply vanish \u8212- applies fully to {\i kedushat haguf}. Bar Pada accepts this and therefore insists the {\i konam} status cannot dissolve automatically. Ulla\u8217's response, as articulated by Rava, is that {\i konamot} of this type operate in the domain of {\i kedushat damim}, and {\i kedushat damim} can indeed be placed on a clock and can cease without an affirmative act of {\i pidyon}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ran\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal vs.\u160 ?Universal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Ran}, citing Rabbeinu Yona, raises a fundamental clarification that prevents a misreading of the entire {\i sugya}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's discussion \u8212- {\i pidyon}, {\i meilah}, the impossibility of automatic cessation, {\i kedushat haguf} versus {\i kedushat damim} \u8212- might seem to apply to all {\i nedarim}. That would be a mistake. Not every {\i neder} enters this framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} draws a distinction between what he calls a {\i konam prati} \u8212- a personal {\i konam} directed at a specific individual \u8212- and a {\i konam klali} \u8212- a universal {\i konam} that renders the object forbidden to everyone. Only the latter imports the laws of {\i korbanot} into the analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} supports this from a {\i gemara} in the chapter {\i Ha-Noder Min Ha-Yerek}, which discusses a case where someone says to his wife: \u8220"I forbid you from benefiting from me until Pesach if you go to your father\u8217's house before Sukkot.\u8221" The {\i gemara} there treats this as a straightforward {\i neder} with a time terminus \u8212- before Pesach the prohibition applies, and after Pesach it automatically lapses: {\i paka issura mimmeila}. No one there raises any concern about {\i kedusha} dissolving automatically or the need for {\i pidyon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The explanation is that this case involves a personal {\i konam} \u8212- {\i konam prati} \u8212- directed at one individual. Such a {\i neder} remains in the ordinary world of {\i nedarim}: the prohibition is less absolute, the {\i kedusha} thinner, and it can therefore lapse automatically. Only when one uses the {\i konam} formula and renders the object forbidden to {\i everyone} \u8212- {\i assur le-khulei alma} \u8212- does the {\i neder} acquire the weight of a genuine {\i korban}, attracting the full framework of {\i meilah} and {\i pidyon} and the insistence that the status cannot dissolve without affirmative removal. Bar Pada\u8217's position, and the entire tension between Bar Pada and Ulla, belongs exclusively to this latter type of {\i konam}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now presses further, challenging the claim that {\i kedushat haguf lo paka b\u8217'chedi}. A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i shor zeh olah kol shloshim yom u-le\u8217'achar shloshim yom shelamim} \u8212- if one declares that this ox shall be an {\i olah} for the first 30 days and a {\i shelamim} after 30 days, the declaration is effective. Within the first 30 days, if one brings it as a {\i korban}, it is treated as an {\i olah}; after 30 days, it is treated as a {\i shelamim}. This is a case of {\i kedushat haguf} \u8212- the very identity of the animal as a specific type of {\i korban} \u8212- and yet that identity changes automatically with the passage of time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Radical Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's intent is to extend Ulla\u8217's position even further. If even {\i kedushat haguf} can shift automatically from one form to another, then the entire basis for insisting that {\i kedusha} cannot dissolve without an act of removal is undermined. Abaye appears prepared to argue that in principle, even a full {\i kedushat haguf} \u8212- an animal genuinely sanctified as a {\i korban} \u8212- could be set on a clock, such that after a defined period it might revert to {\i chulin} status entirely, with no act of {\i pidyon} required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} notes that Abaye would still limit this radical position to the world of {\i kodashim} and {\i hekdesh}, where everything is effected through speech and the mode of sanctification is fundamentally different from {\i kiddushin}. No one disputes the {\i kiddushin} case \u8212- no one argues that a woman can cease to be married simply because a time limit was declared. But within the world of {\i korbanot} and {\i hekdesh}, Abaye is prepared to say that even {\i kedushat haguf} can be time-bound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Cases Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, Abaye argues from the structure of the {\i baraita} itself. The {\i baraita} presents two cases: first, {\i olah} for 30 days and then {\i shelamim}; second, {\i shelamim} now and {\i olah} after 30 days. If both cases are really about {\i kedushat damim} \u8212- as the {\i gemara} might wish to argue \u8212- why give two cases at all? The second case, moving from {\i shelamim} to {\i olah}, is obviously less of a {\i chiddush} than the first, since it represents an upgrade in sanctity. You would not need two cases to make a point about {\i kedushat damim} when one would suffice and the second adds nothing. The only reason to give two cases, Abaye argues, is to demonstrate that the principle operates across all scenarios \u8212- including {\i kedushat haguf} \u8212- and that even {\i kedushat haguf} is capable of automatic transformation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds to Abaye\u8217's challenge by reinterpreting the {\i baraita}. {\i Hacha be-mai askinan de-amar le-damei} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is discussing a case where one has not sanctified the animal as an {\i olah} or {\i shelamim} in the full sense of {\i kedushat haguf}, but rather has declared it for its value: the proceeds of this animal shall go to purchase an {\i olah} or a {\i shelamim}. The {\i rishonim} clarify that this reading requires the animal to have a {\i mum} \u8212- a blemish \u8212- because if the animal is unblemished and one says \u8220"let it be for an {\i olah},\u8221" it automatically receives {\i kedushat haguf} since the animal itself could be offered. But a blemished animal cannot itself be brought as a {\i korban}, and so the declaration necessarily means its monetary value will be used. Under this reading, both cases of the {\i baraita} involve {\i kedushat damim}, not {\i kedushat haguf}, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's earlier insistence that {\i kedushat haguf lo paka b\u8217'chedi} remains intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Directions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this reinterpretation, the {\i gemara} can account for why the {\i baraita} gives two cases without conceding anything about {\i kedushat haguf}. Under {\i kedushat damim}, status can change with time. The {\i baraita} gives two cases \u8212- {\i olah} to {\i shelamim} and {\i shelamim} to {\i olah} \u8212- to demonstrate that this principle operates in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Case Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now explains the structural logic. If one wished to argue that the {\i baraita} deals with {\i kedushat haguf} in one case and {\i kedushat damim} in the other, the two cases would be necessary to teach that even {\i kedushat haguf} can change \u8212- lest one think only {\i kedushat damim} can shift. But if both cases are {\i kedushat damim}, one might ask: why mention both? The answer is precisely to preempt the inference that you could suffice with one. Had only one case been given \u8212- say, {\i olah} becoming {\i shelamim} \u8212- one might have thought that only {\i kedushat damim} of a lesser nature ({\i shelamim} replacing {\i olah}) can change, but not {\i kedushat damim} of a more stringent nature. By giving both cases, the {\i baraita} demonstrates that {\i kedushat damim} is equally malleable in either direction, and no special inference about {\i kedushat haguf} need be drawn.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bar Pada Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simple Case Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to how Bar Pada would explain the first case of the {\i baraita} \u8212- the animal sanctified as {\i olah} for 30 days and then as {\i shelamim} afterward. According to Rav Pappa, explaining Bar Pada\u8217's position, this case is perfectly intelligible without any automatic status change. If one said {\i le\u8217'achar shloshim yom olah} \u8212- after 30 days this animal shall be an {\i olah} \u8212- that declaration takes effect at the 30-day mark. Bar Pada has no difficulty with this: you can sanctify something to take effect in the future, just as a man can say to a woman \u8220"be married to me after 30 days\u8221" and the {\i kiddushin} takes effect then. Before day 30, the animal has not yet acquired {\i olah} status; at day 30, it does. This is a legitimate future-directed sanctification, not an automatic change of an existing status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bar Pada Explained (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Added Status Sticks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem for Bar Pada arises in the second formulation: one first says {\i me\u8217'achshav shelamim} \u8212- from now it is a {\i shelamim} \u8212- and then adds {\i le\u8217'achar shloshim yom olah}. Here the animal has already acquired {\i shelamim} status from the moment of the declaration. Bar Pada insists: {\i lo paka bichdi} \u8212- that status does not dissolve automatically. Once {\i shelamim} status is in place, it cannot be replaced by {\i olah} status simply because a clock runs out. The {\i shelamim} identity is locked in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to {\i kiddushin} is instructive. If one says to a woman \u8220"be married to me after 30 days,\u8221" the marriage takes effect at day 30 \u8212- a straightforward future-directed {\i kinyan}. But if one has already established a present status and then attempts to overlay it with a future-directed change, the existing status persists. The same logic governs Bar Pada\u8217's reading of the {\i baraita}: the {\i seifa} only works \u8212- the {\i olah} after 30 days \u8212- if one did not first say {\i me\u8217'achshav shelamim}. Once the {\i shelamim} status is introduced, it cannot be dislodged by the passage of time alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Amira Lagavoa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Word Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now explains why the first case of the {\i baraita} \u8212- sanctifying something for after 30 days \u8212- works at all, and introduces the principle of {\i amira lagavoa k\u8217'mesirato lahedyot}: declaring something to belong to {\i hekdesh} is like physically transferring it to an ordinary person. Normally, if one says to another person \u8220"this object is yours,\u8221" nothing happens \u8212- a {\i kinyan} requires a physical act: lifting, pulling, placing in one\u8217's domain. Mere words accomplish nothing. Yet when one says {\i harei zeh l\u8217'hekdesh}, the words alone suffice. The explanation is this principle: speech directed toward Heaven carries the weight of a physical act of transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Backtrack\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle, however, does more than merely dispense with the need for a formal {\i kinyan}. It generates a {\i chiddush} regarding the ability to retract. In the context of {\i kiddushin}, if one says to a woman \u8220"be married to me after 30 days\u8221" and gives her a ring, there is a dispute among the {\i rishonim} as to whether she \u8212- or he \u8212- can retract before the 30 days are up. According to one opinion, retraction is possible during the interim period, because the {\i kiddushin} has not yet taken effect. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i gemara} is that when it comes to {\i hekdesh}, even the opinion that permits retraction in {\i kiddushin} would agree that no retraction is possible. The reason: since the relationship with {\i hekdesh} is mediated entirely by words, and since words directed to {\i Gavoah} carry the full force of a completed physical transfer, the declaration is equivalent to having said {\i me\u8217'achshav} \u8212- from this moment. Once the words are spoken, it is as though the transfer has already occurred, and there is no backing out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Remnant Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle also generates a second {\i chiddush}, noted separately by the {\i gemara}: {\i afilu nisachlu hamaos} \u8212- even if the money used for the sanctification has been consumed or no longer exists, the sanctification takes effect at the appointed time. In {\i kiddushin}, if a man gives a woman a ring and says \u8220"be married to me after 30 days,\u8221" and the ring is then destroyed before day 30, the {\i kiddushin} still takes effect \u8212- because the woman remains indebted for the value of the ring; the {\i kinyan} has a residual presence in the form of that debt. But if the {\i kinyan} were effected by a {\i shtar} that was subsequently destroyed, the {\i kiddushin} would not take effect, because the physical instrument of the {\i kinyan} has no remnant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Substitute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When it comes to {\i hekdesh}, no such remnant is required, because the act of sanctification was never physical to begin with. The \u8220"transfer\u8221" to {\i hekdesh} is effected entirely through speech \u8212- there is no physical {\i kinyan} whose remnant must persist. Since the entire relationship is constituted by language and abstraction, it can take effect at the future date without any physical reality needing to survive into that moment. This is why {\i nishachlu hamaos} \u8212- the money being gone \u8212- poses no obstacle: there was never a physical dimension to the {\i kinyan} that needed to remain present.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seifa Difficulty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} observes that Bar Pada\u8217's position faces its sharpest challenge from the {\i seifa} of the {\i baraita} \u8212- the reverse formulation: one first says {\i me\u8217'achshav shelamim} and then adds {\i le\u8217'achar shloshim yom olah}. Here, the animal already bears {\i shelamim} status from the moment of the declaration. The subsequent specification that it should become an {\i olah} after 30 days would, on the face of it, seem to represent an attempt to upgrade the sanctity \u8212- moving from a lesser {\i kedusha} to a greater one. One might have thought this should be more achievable: the {\i shelamim} status is weaker, and the intended transformation elevates the animal, so perhaps there are grounds to allow it. Moreover, one might argue that by specifying the {\i olah} status in advance \u8212- before 30 days have elapsed \u8212- one has already \u8220"put a stopper\u8221" in place, establishing where the status is heading even as the {\i shelamim} is introduced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Lock-In\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, Bar Pada maintains his position absolutely: {\i lo paka bichdi}. Once the {\i shelamim} status is in place \u8212- even a weaker {\i kedusha} \u8212- it does not dissolve or transform simply because a clock has been set or because the intended replacement carries greater sanctity. The only scenario in which {\i le\u8217'achar shloshim yom olah} can take effect is the one where no prior status was established \u8212- where one said simply {\i le\u8217'achar shloshim yom olah} without the preceding {\i me\u8217'achshav shelamim}. Once any present status is in place, no time-bound declaration can displace it. The {\i seifa} thus confirms, in Bar Pada\u8217's reading, the comprehensive scope of the principle: halachic status, once established, does not change itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bar Padda Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up at the bottom of 29b, in the middle of the fascinating {\i sugia} concerning the status of {\i konamot}. When one makes a {\i neder} using the word {\i konam} \u8212- a {\i kinuy}, a substitute term for {\i korban} \u8212- the object acquires something of the status of {\i korbanot}, at least according to Rabbi Meir, with respect to both {\i me\u8217'ilah} and the possibility of {\i pidyon}, redemption, whereby the sanctified status can be transferred to another object. The preceding discussion established that when one uses the word {\i konam} to render something forbidden, it is {\i assur} to all, not merely to a single individual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion that immediately precedes our passage concerns what happens when that sanctified status is assigned for a limited period of time. {\i Bar Padda} holds that the status cannot expire automatically; it can only be removed through an active act of {\i pidyon}. What, then, does a time-limiting phrase accomplish? According to {\i Bar Padda}, it means that the object is to be continuously and perpetually re-sanctified throughout that period, such that even a successful redemption is immediately overridden by the original statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Continuous Re-sanctifica\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bar Padda}\u8217's principle is that {\i pade\u8217'an chozerot vekodshot} \u8212- if you redeem them, they re-acquire their sanctified status. A person cannot simply limit a {\i neder} to a fixed period and expect the status to dissolve automatically when that period ends, because sanctified status can only be removed through the act of {\i pidyon}. Accordingly, the phrase \u8220"until such-and-such a time\u8221" is interpreted not as a terminal condition but as an ongoing directive: I want these objects to continuously hold this status throughout that entire period. Any act of redemption during that interval causes the status to be immediately reinstated by virtue of the original declaration. The {\i dibbur} \u8212- the original statement \u8212- is understood as perpetually re-sanctifying the object for as long as the designated period has not elapsed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saplings Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application is as follows: if one declared saplings {\i konam} until they are cut down, the phrase {\i ad sheyikatztzu} does not mean that the status dissolves at the moment of cutting. Rather, it means that throughout the entire period until they are cut down, the object is to maintain its {\i konam} status without interruption. Even if during that interval someone redeems the saplings and removes their sanctified status, the original declaration immediately reinstates that status. The act of redemption works conceptually \u8212- the status is genuinely removed for a moment \u8212- but the original statement simultaneously kicks the status right back in. Thus {\i ad sheyikatztzu} delineates the duration of this ongoing re-sanctification, not a terminal point at which the status naturally lapses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Rings Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Oshaya Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the top of 30a, the Gemara introduces the question that {\i Rav Oshaya} had posed. The case: a man gives two coins \u8212- two rings \u8212- to a woman, and says to her, \u8220"With this one, be betrothed to me today\u8221" ({\i be\u8217'achat hitkadshi li hayom}), and \u8220"With this other one, be betrothed to me after I divorce you\u8221" ({\i uve\u8217'achat hitkadshi li le\u8217'achar she\u8217'agareshich}). The question is whether the second act of {\i kiddushin} would take effect automatically upon the delivery of the {\i get}, based on the original declaration made at the time of the first {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saplings Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two students sitting before {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Avin} and {\i Rabbi Yitzchak berebi} \u8212- were discussing this question while their teacher dozed. They proposed that it be resolved from {\i Bar Padda}\u8217's principle. Just as the saplings are continuously re-sanctified by virtue of the original declaration whenever the sanctified status is removed, so too here: when the man gives the woman her {\i get} and changes her status to that of a single woman, the act performed a week earlier should take effect. He gave her a second ring and stipulated that it serve as an act of {\i kiddushin} taking effect after the divorce. When that condition is met, why should the earlier act of {\i kiddushin} not now come into force?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Act, Dual Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of the parallel is this: just as one may say \u8220"let this ring take effect in thirty days\u8221" \u8212- and if neither party has changed position, {\i kiddushin} takes effect at that future time \u8212- so too one may designate a specific future circumstance as the triggering condition. The second ring is given to a woman not currently his wife \u8212- because one cannot perform {\i kiddushin} on one\u8217's existing wife \u8212- and the declaration is: let this be an act of {\i kiddushin} that takes effect after the divorce. This is structurally parallel to saying \u8220"after thirty days.\u8221" What makes it equivalent to the saplings case is that one single {\i dibbur}, one statement made here and now, declares that at a future moment \u8212- after a change of status \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} should take hold. The statement re-sanctifies the saplings whenever they lose their status, just as this statement would re-betroth the woman whenever she loses the status of {\i eshet ish}. It is one act with a dual and temporally extended effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yirmiyah Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirmiyah Wakes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara delivers one of its memorable narrative moments: {\i itar behu Rabbi Yirmiyah} \u8212- Rabbi Yirmiyah woke up. Evidently hearing his students reason their way into what he considered an error was sufficient to rouse him. He challenged them: how can you compare this to the case of the saplings without distinguishing between {\i peda\u8217'am hu} \u8212- the owner redeeming them himself \u8212- and {\i peda\u8217'am acherim} \u8212- a third party redeeming them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Owner vs.\u160 ?Third Party\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's ruling, as transmitted by Rabbi Yirmiyah, draws precisely this distinction: {\i peda\u8217'am hu chozrot vekodshot} \u8212- if he himself redeems them, they become re-sanctified; {\i peda\u8217'am acherim ein chozrot vekodshot} \u8212- if others redeem them, they do not become re-sanctified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Control Determines Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic is as follows. When one declares these objects sanctified and later redeems them, the act of redemption transfers the objects back into his own possession \u8212- he buys them back, as it were, from the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, and they come directly under his control once more. The moment they leave their sanctified status they return to his domain. His original statement, which declared that they should perpetually hold this status, can then take effect continuously, because he never loses control over them. His {\i dibbur} is operative at every moment and can govern the object\u8217's destiny because he owns it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if a third party \u8212- call him Michael \u8212- redeems the sanctified object, then at that moment of redemption the object passes into Michael\u8217's ownership. The original owner\u8217's statement cannot sanctify something belonging to someone else. Even if Michael subsequently sells it back to the original owner, it does not matter: there was a break, a period during which the object was entirely outside the original owner\u8217's control, and that break severs the chain. The original {\i dibbur} can no longer govern the object\u8217's status because its continuity was interrupted by an interval of third-party ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman as Third Party\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now applies this distinction to the woman: {\i veha\u8217'ishah kepeda\u8217'uhu acherim damya} \u8212- the woman is like one who was redeemed by a third party. When the husband gives the {\i get}, she is no longer under his control. The moment the {\i get} is delivered, she can marry whomever she chooses. Even if she continues to desire the reinstatement of the original {\i kiddushin}, even if she never expressed any wish to withdraw from the original agreement \u8212- it makes no difference. She is no longer under his control at that moment, and the object of the original act of {\i kiddushin} is therefore not within his dominion when the triggering condition is met. His original statement cannot determine her status because she is, at that moment, a fully independent entity \u8212- like an object that has passed into the possession of a third party. Just as Michael\u8217's subsequent willingness to return the object, or his declaration that he intends to give it back, cannot cause the original sanctification to re-activate at that moment, so too the woman\u8217's own continued consent cannot substitute for the legal requirement that the object remain within the original owner\u8217's sphere of control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point is that conceptually, {\i Bar Padda}\u8217's mechanism of continuous re-sanctification works precisely because the owner redeems the object and it flows back into his control instantaneously \u8212- there is no actual temporal gap, though conceptually one act follows the other. The moment a third party intervenes and takes the object out of that chain, the chain is broken, and the original {\i dibbur} cannot regenerate itself. Since the wife, upon receiving the {\i get}, occupies exactly that position, the original act of {\i kiddushin} cannot reassert itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then notes: {\i itmar nami} \u8212- this same ruling was stated independently: {\i amar Rabbi Ami amar Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- {\i lo shanu ela shepedao hu}, the re-sanctification applies only when he himself redeems it; {\i aval peda\u8217'u acherim, ein chozeret lekidushatah} \u8212- if others redeemed it, it does not return to its sanctified status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ran\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's conclusion is analytically clean, but the {\i Ran} raises a significant difficulty that deserves careful attention \u8212- particularly given how widely it is cited in discussions of {\i kiddushin}. The {\i Ran} asks: I understand that after the {\i get} is delivered, the woman is no longer under the husband\u8217's control. But why should that matter? Two people are parties to the act of {\i kiddushin}. She is one of those parties. She agreed here. Why is her continued consent \u8212- her continued status as an active participant \u8212- not sufficient to keep the original act operative?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Participation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} frames the objection this way: if both the man and the woman are understood as parallel participants in {\i kiddushin} \u8212- both agreeing to be married, both actors in a joint agreement \u8212- then even after the {\i get}, she remains an active subject who still wants the {\i kiddushin} to take effect. She is not in some third party\u8217's control; she is in her own control and she still wants the marriage to be reinstated. Why is she treated as though she were an object that has been redeemed by an outsider, rather than as a co-participant whose ongoing consent keeps the chain intact?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Object vs.\u160 ?Subject\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} resolves his own question, and in doing so, exposes the conceptual framework underlying the Gemara\u8217's entire comparison. The Gemara looks at the woman as the {\i object} upon whom the act of {\i kiddushin} is performed, not as a {\i subject} who performs it. It is, in the Gemara\u8217's framework, the man\u8217's act \u8212- something done {\i to} her or {\i upon} her \u8212- and the relevant question is always whether that object remains within the actor\u8217's control. Since the question is framed entirely from the man\u8217's perspective, the moment she exits his domain, it is irrelevant that she herself remains under her own control and continues to consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} states this explicitly. The Torah says {\i ki yikach ish isha} \u8212- \u8220"when a man takes a woman\u8221" \u8212- and not {\i ki teilakach isha le\u8217'ish} \u8212- \u8220"when a woman takes herself to a man.\u8221" She does not have the legal capacity to enact {\i kiddushin} upon herself. She can consent or withhold consent, and her consent is necessary, but she is not the actor. The man takes her; she is taken. {\i Lav kol heimena she-tachnis atzmah le-reshut ha-ba\u8217'al} \u8212- it is not within her power to bring herself into the husband\u8217's domain through her own act. She can agree to his act, she can refuse it, but the act itself is his and his alone. For this reason, even though {\i kiddushin} requires her consent, she is conceptually positioned as the object of his act rather than an equal actor alongside him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consequently, the comparison to the saplings is fully apt. The saplings are the object; as long as they remain in the owner\u8217's control, his original {\i dibbur} governs their status. The woman is the object; as long as she remains in the husband\u8217's domain, his original {\i dibbur} can govern her status. Once the {\i get} removes her from his domain, she is exactly like the sanctified object that has passed into a third party\u8217's hands \u8212- and the chain is severed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evolution of Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this framework has evolved significantly over time. In the Torah\u8217's original structure, the woman was entirely an object \u8212- she required no consent whatsoever, and the operative legal actor was her father, who transferred her. After {\i bat mitzvah}, once she passed beyond her father\u8217's control, the woman could not be given in marriage without her own agreement; but conceptually, as the {\i Ran} makes clear, the model remained that the man is the actor and the woman is the one acted upon. She must agree, but she does not marry; he marries her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the era of the {\i Chatam Sofer}, within the last two centuries, even a traditionalist posek felt the need to reconceptualize the institution: he framed {\i kiddushin} as a bilateral contractual agreement in which both parties take one another, entering as equal actors into a mutual exchange. This represents a meaningful shift in the underlying model, even if the formal legal acts remain structurally unchanged.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seafarers Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Seafarers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now shifts to a different and more linguistically focused portion of the {\i perek}, concerning the scope of generic terms used in {\i nedarim}. The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i ha-noder mi-yordei ha-yam mutar beyoshvei hayabashah} \u8212- one who vows against {\i yordei ha-yam}, seafarers, is permitted to derive benefit from {\i yoshvei hayabashah}, those who dwell on the dry land. Conversely, {\i mi-yoshvei hayabashah assur mi-yordei ha-yam} \u8212- one who vows against land-dwellers is forbidden from seafarers as well, since seafarers are a subset of land-dwellers. The Mishnah then adds: {\i lo ka\u8217'elu sheholchin me\u8217'Akko le\u8217'Yafo} \u8212- this does not refer to those who travel from {\i Akko} to {\i Yafo}, but {\i ela bemi shedarko lefaresh} \u8212- only to those who undertake far journeys.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Readings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} and {\i Rav Acha brei de-Rav Ika} dispute to which clause of the Mishnah this final line attaches. {\i Chad matni areisha}, one applies it to the opening clause; {\i chad matni aseifa}, the other applies it to the closing clause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Short-trip Sailors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The one who reads the final line as a gloss on the opening clause understands it as follows: when the Mishnah says that a vow against seafarers does not include land-dwellers, it also specifies what counts as a seafarer in the first place. Short-distance maritime travelers \u8212- those who regularly make the crossing from {\i Akko} to {\i Yafo} \u8212- are not seafarers in the legally meaningful sense, even if they travel by water daily. Only those who undertake genuinely far-reaching ocean journeys qualify as {\i yordei ha-yam}. Accordingly, the local ferry passenger remains in the category of {\i yoshvei hayabashah}, and a vow against seafarers does not reach him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The one who reads the final line as a gloss on the closing clause understands it differently. When the Mishnah says that {\i yoshvei hayabashah} includes {\i yordei ha-yam}, one might think this applies only to those who make short trips and effectively live on land. The Mishnah therefore comes to teach: do not think that only the {\i Akko}-to-{\i Yafo} traveler is subsumed under land-dwellers \u8212- even those who make extended ocean voyages are ultimately {\i yoshvei hayabashah}, since {\i sofo leyabashah salik}, eventually they return to land. On this reading, the scope of {\i yoshvei hayabashah} is maximally inclusive, encompassing even long-distance seafarers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two readings thus work in opposite directions: one restricts the category of {\i yordei ha-yam} to far-journey travelers only, while the other expands the category of {\i yoshvei hayabashah} to absorb even those same long-journey travelers. The {\i Rishonim} note further that even where the Mishnah establishes that far-journey sailors qualify as {\i yordei ha-yam}, it does not fully specify the required regularity of travel \u8212- a separate question that the authorities address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion also touches on a deeper methodological issue that will become explicit shortly: when a person uses a term without specifying his meaning, what criterion determines its legal scope? One possibility is to look to {\i lashon Torah} \u8212- the way the Torah uses the term, functioning as a kind of authoritative lexicon. Another is to follow {\i lashon bnei adam} \u8212- the way people actually use the word in common speech, regardless of what the formal or biblical usage would dictate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sun Seers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blind Included\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i Mishnah} presents a striking example of non-literal interpretation. {\i Ha-noder me-ro\u8217'ei ha-chamah} \u8212- one who vows against \u8220"those who see the sun\u8221" \u8212- {\i assur af ba-sumin}, is forbidden from deriving benefit even from blind people. On the surface, a blind person plainly does not see the sun. Nevertheless, the vow applies to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reinterpreted Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The explanation is that the person did not intend the phrase literally. {\i Shelo nitkaven zeh ela lemi shehachamah ro\u8217'ah otan} \u8212- what he meant was not \u8220"those who see the sun\u8221" but \u8220"those whom the sun sees.\u8221" The Gemara notes: {\i midela ka\u8217'amar min haro\u8217'im} \u8212- had he meant only those with the faculty of sight, he would simply have said \u8220"those with sight.\u8221" The fact that he invoked the sun suggests that the operative criterion is exposure to sunlight, not the possession of vision. Accordingly, the phrase is reinterpreted: the sun sees them, they are in the world illuminated by the sun, and therefore the vow applies regardless of whether they themselves can perceive it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} adds a precision: since the phrase still contains the word \u8220"those who see,\u8221" it operates within the universe of beings with sight. Inanimate objects like fruit and plants are also \u8220"seen\u8221" by the sun, but they are excluded because the language of seeing implies reference to animate creatures. Within the universe of people and animals, the criterion is not whether they literally see the sun, but whether they are exposed to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fish and Fetuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i La\u8217'apokei dagim ve-ubarim} \u8212- the interpretation serves to exclude fish and fetuses. Fish, submerged in water, are not exposed to the sun in the relevant sense; and fetuses, not yet born into the world, are similarly not \u8220"seen\u8221" by the sun. These two categories therefore fall outside the vow even on the reinterpreted understanding of the phrase.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Black-headed Men\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Black-headed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The following {\i Mishnah} addresses another term whose conventional usage diverges from what a literal reading would suggest. {\i Ha-noder mi-shcharei ha-rosh} \u8212- one who vows against \u8220"the black-headed\u8221" \u8212- {\i asur be-kerachim u-va\u8217'alei seivot}, is forbidden from deriving benefit even from bald men and from men with white hair. But {\i mutar be-nashim u-ve-ktanim} \u8212- he is permitted to derive benefit from women and from minors. Why? {\i She-ein nikra\u8217'im shchorei ha-rosh ela anashim} \u8212- because the term {\i shchorei ha-rosh}, \u8220"black-headed,\u8221" is not a literal description of hair color but rather a generic designation for men, as opposed to women and children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Men Not Hair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is initially counterintuitive. If the term meant what it literally says \u8212- people with black hair \u8212- then it would include women and girls with dark hair, and exclude men who happen to be bald or gray. Yet the Mishnah says exactly the opposite: bald men and white-haired men are included, while women and children are excluded. Clearly, the Gemara explains, {\i shchorei ha-rosh} is not functioning as a descriptor of hair color at all. It is functioning as a category label for men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coverage Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains why this term came to mean \u8220"men.\u8221" {\i Anashim zimna dimichasu reisha\u8217'eihu ve-zimna dimiglu reisha\u8217'eihu} \u8212- men sometimes cover their heads and sometimes leave them uncovered. When their heads are uncovered, their hair is visible; and since the hair of Jewish men tends, more often than not, toward black or dark brown, the visible hair of men became associated with the category of men itself. The phrase {\i shchorei ha-rosh} thus came to denote those whose hair is sometimes visible \u8212- i.e., men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval nashim le\u8217'olam michasu} \u8212- women\u8217's hair is always covered, so one would not refer to its color as a categorical descriptor. {\i Ve-ktanim le\u8217'olam miglu} \u8212- minors\u8217' hair is always uncovered. If one wanted to refer to minors, one would say \u8220"those with uncovered heads,\u8221" not \u8220"black-headed.\u8221" If one wanted to refer to women, one would say \u8220"those with covered heads.\u8221" The category where hair is {\i sometimes} visible \u8212- sometimes covered, sometimes not \u8212- is the category of adult men. And so the phrase {\i shchorei ha-rosh} becomes a term of art for that category, not a statement about pigmentation. A bald man and a white-haired man are both men whose hair is sometimes visible; they fall within the category. Women and children are defined by an invariant covering pattern and thus fall outside it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Girls Hair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One question the Gemara leaves open is whether the {\i ktanim} whose hair is {\i le\u8217'olam miglu} \u8212- always uncovered \u8212- refers only to young boys, or includes young girls as well. {\i Tosafot} addresses this directly: {\i ktanim le\u8217'olam miglu bein zacharim bein nekeivot} \u8212- even young girls, before marriage, have their hair uncovered as a regular practice. {\i Tosafot} states that {\i nekeivot ktanot she-ein nesuot} \u8212- unmarried minor girls \u8212- customarily keep their heads uncovered. On this reading, young girls fall into the \u8220"always uncovered\u8221" category alongside boys, and both are excluded from the designation {\i shchorei ha-rosh}, which remains specific to adult men. The question of precisely how to read the Gemara here \u8212- whether {\i ktanim} encompasses girls whose hair is also uncovered \u8212- is one the {\i Rishonim} engage with, and {\i Tosafot}\u8217's position is that it does.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birth Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeludim Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i Mishnah} on the daf addresses a different categorical question: the distinction between {\i yeludim} \u8212- those who have already been born \u8212- and {\i noladim} \u8212- a term whose scope is disputed. The Mishnah opens: {\i ha-noder min ha-yeludim mutar ba-noladim} \u8212- one who vows against {\i yeludim}, those who have been born, is permitted to derive benefit from {\i noladim}, those yet to be born. {\i Yeludim} thus refers unambiguously to the past \u8212- those currently alive by virtue of having already been born.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Noladim Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah continues: {\i min ha-noladim assur af ba-yeludim} \u8212- one who vows against {\i noladim} is forbidden from both the past-born and the future-born. This represents the position of the {\i Tanna Kamma}: the word {\i noladim} is broad enough to encompass all those who come into the world through birth, whether they have already done so or will do so in the future.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} disagrees. He holds that {\i noladim} refers exclusively to those who will be born in the future. Just as {\i yeludim} means \u8220"born in the past,\u8221" {\i noladim} means \u8220"to be born in the future,\u8221" and the two terms are strictly symmetrical and mutually exclusive. On his reading, a vow against {\i noladim} does not reach those who have already been born.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} maintain that {\i lo nitkaven zeh ela bemi shedarko lehivaled} \u8212- the word {\i noladim} denotes those whose nature it is to come into the world through birth, without temporal limitation. Whether one has already been born or will be born, one falls under this description. The {\i Chachamim} thus treat {\i noladim} as a term that transcends tense, encompassing all those who enter the world through the act of birth, past and future alike.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} challenged {\i Abaye} with a question directed at the premise underlying {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position. If {\i noladim} means only those who will be born \u8212- {\i de-miya\u8217'aldin} \u8212- then how do we explain the verse {\i shenei vanecha ha-noladim lecha be\u8217'eretz mitzrayim} \u8212- \u8220"your two sons who were born to you in the land of Egypt\u8221"? There, {\i noladim} clearly refers to children already born in the past. This seems to refute {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's contention that {\i noladim} means only future-born.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pushes back: perhaps {\i noladim} means only the past? That too cannot be sustained, for the verse {\i hineh ben nolad le-veit David, Yoshiyahu shemo} \u8212- \u8220"behold, a son will be born to the house of David, Yoshiyahu is his name\u8221" \u8212- uses {\i nolad} to refer to a birth that has not yet occurred, a prediction made before even {\i Menashe} had been born. The Gemara thus demonstrates elegantly that {\i nolad} in the Torah carries neither an exclusively past nor an exclusively future meaning. {\i Ela mashma hachi u-mashma hachi} \u8212- it means both, depending on context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Usage Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This demonstration supports the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's position that {\i noladim} encompasses both past and future. But it raises the question of how {\i Rabbi Meir} can maintain that {\i noladim} means only the future. The Gemara\u8217's answer is the ruling principle: {\i u-vindarim halach achar lashon bnei adam} \u8212- in matters of {\i nedarim}, we do not follow the biblical or formally authoritative meaning of a word, but rather the way that word is actually used by people in ordinary speech.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Usage Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} hold that {\i lashon bnei adam} here tracks {\i lashon ha-Torah}: people use {\i noladim} in the same broad, tenseless way the Torah does, meaning both those who have been born and those who will be born. {\i Rabbi Meir} holds that {\i lashon bnei adam} has diverged from {\i lashon Torah}: in ordinary spoken usage, {\i noladim} means only those who will be born \u8212- the future tense \u8212- while the past is expressed by {\i yeludim}. People use the two terms as a clean past/future distinction, regardless of how the Torah deploys them. The debate, at its core, is an empirical one about how speakers actually use these words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fish and Birds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the Gemara asks what the word {\i noladim} \u8212- on the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's broad reading \u8212- comes to exclude. {\i La\u8217'apokei dagim ve-ofot} \u8212- fish and birds. These creatures do not come into the world through live birth; they hatch from eggs. The act of hatching is categorically distinct from {\i leivaled}, from being born, and therefore fish and birds fall entirely outside the scope of {\i noladim}, whichever temporal interpretation one adopts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 31\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Daf 31 Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek 3 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 31 marks the lighter conclusion of the third {\i perek}. The opening section of the {\i perek} was weighty and dense \u8212- concerned with the question of {\i piv ulibo shavin}, whether a person\u8217's mouth and heart are aligned \u8212- and the middle {\i mishnah} opened a fascinating discussion about the status of a {\i neder} that renders an object like {\i hekdesh}, requiring a form of {\i pidyon}. Now we arrive at a series of {\i mishnayot} that carry us to the end of the {\i perek}, each addressing a relatively contained question: when a person declares a certain group of people forbidden, who exactly is included in that group and who is not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Topic Progression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnayot} of this concluding section move through a progression of such group-definition problems \u8212- from {\i shabbat} observers and garlic eaters, to {\i bnei Noach} and {\i zera Avraham}, to Jews as a category of forbidden beneficiaries, and finally to the terminology of circumcised and uncircumcised. The {\i perek} then closes with an extended {\i aggadic} discussion of {\i brit milah} that, while formally belonging to the {\i mishnah}, stands out as quite striking for this genre.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shabbat & Garlic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Observers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i HaNoder MiShovsei Shabbos} \u8212- one who says \u8220"I will not derive benefit from those who rest on {\i shabbat}\u8221" \u8212- is forbidden from benefiting from Jews and also from Samaritans, who likewise observe {\i shabbat}. The {\i mishnah} then adds the parallel case: {\i MeOchlei Shum}, those who eat garlic \u8212- again, forbidden from both Jews and Samaritans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Garlic Custom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevance of garlic requires explanation. As recorded in the {\i Gemara} in Bava Kama and Ketubot, and spelled out more fully in the {\i meforesh} here than in the {\i Ran}, Jews customarily ate garlic on Friday night on account of {\i ona} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of conjugal relations \u8212- since men would fulfill this obligation {\i me\u8217'erev shabbos le\u8217'erev shabbos}, and garlic was understood to increase male potency, as the {\i Gemara} elsewhere connects the verse {\i asher piryo yitein be\u8217'ito} to {\i talmidei chachamim} who fulfill this duty every Friday night. There is an obvious irony in this \u8212- one might wonder whether a partner would appreciate the garlic \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} records that there was specifically a practice to eat garlic on Friday night for this reason. Accordingly, \u8220"garlic eaters\u8221" functions as a marker for Jews and Samaritans, who apparently shared this custom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i Mai Shovsei Shabbos}? What exactly does {\i shabbat} observer mean? If it simply means anyone who happens to keep {\i shabbat}, then why limit this to Samaritans? Non-Jews who observe {\i shabbat} would also be included \u8212- and there is a known phenomenon of non-Jews who kept {\i shabbat} without having converted. In the Roman world, the class of {\i yirei Hashem} \u8212- God-fearers who were attracted to Jewish practice \u8212- was real and recognized; the phrase appears in {\i Hallel} as a distinct group alongside {\i beit Aharon}, and one tradition identifies it with non-Jewish worshipers of God in the {\i mishnaic} era. Indeed, it is worth noting that the New Testament itself, in Acts chapter 15, speaks of Gentiles observing {\i shabbat} before the Council of Nicaea shifted Christian practice to Sunday.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore proposes that {\i shovsei shabbat} means not those who merely happen to observe it, but {\i mi metzuvah v\u8217'oseh} \u8212- those who are commanded and who perform it. This would imply that Samaritans are considered Jews for this purpose, since the whole question of their status turned on whether they were to be treated as Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jerusalem Pilgrims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then challenges this from the {\i seifa} of the {\i mishnah}: {\i Me\u8217'olei Yerushalayim} \u8212- those who make pilgrimage to Jerusalem \u8212- are forbidden from Jews but permitted from Samaritans. If Samaritans are commanded on {\i shabbat}, they are equally commanded on {\i aliyah l\u8217'regel}. Why then are they excluded from this case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves this: the {\i mishnah} means {\i metzuvah v\u8217'oseh} \u8212- commanded and performing. In the first two cases of {\i shabbat} and garlic, Jews and Samaritans are both commanded and both in practice observe {\i shabbat}. Non-Jews who happen to keep {\i shabbat} are performing but not commanded. With respect to {\i olei Yerushalayim}, Jews are commanded and perform, while Samaritans are commanded but do not perform it \u8212- they make {\i aliyah} to Har Gerizim, not to Jerusalem. There remains an interesting question about the framing: is it that we consider them commanded, or that they consider themselves commanded? The {\i Gemara} appears to take the former position, though in the end the operative principle in {\i nedarim} is always {\i da\u8217'at hanoder} \u8212- the intent of the one who vowed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Noahides & Seed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Noahides Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah}: {\i Konam she\u8217'eini nehene l\u8217'Vnei Noach} \u8212- one who vows not to benefit from {\i bnei Noach} \u8212- is permitted to benefit from Jews but forbidden from non-Jews. All non-Jews are {\i bnei Noach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jews & Noach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: but Jews are also {\i bnei Noach} \u8212- they too descend from Noah. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i keivan d\u8217'ikdish Avraham itkaru ashmei} \u8212- once Abraham was sanctified and they received the identity of being Jewish, they are no longer identified under the rubric of {\i bnei Noach}. When one says {\i bnei Noach}, one means Gentiles, not Jews \u8212- even though technically Jews are biological descendants of Noah. This could simply be a matter of vernacular usage, which is always available in the laws of {\i nedarim} through the principle of {\i halach achar leshon bnei adam}. In the common idiom, \u8220"Noahides\u8221" refers to non-Jews, and so that is how the term is read.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Philosophical Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the question carries a deeper dimension as well. There is a {\i Gemara} in Sanhedrin that states there can be no prohibition that applies to {\i bnei Noach} but is permitted to Jews. When that question arises \u8212- whether parameters of theft, {\i ever min hachai}, or other categories of {\i sheva mitzvot bnei Noach} apply to Jews \u8212- the {\i Gemara\u8217's} framing seems to suggest that technically Jews could be included, but we would not allow for such a conclusion in practice because it would make it appear as though Jews have fewer religious obligations in certain areas than non-Jews do. One might instead suggest that it is because Jews are simultaneously Jews {\i and bnei Noach}, so that any human-universal obligations apply to them as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, this reading does not seem to fit the {\i halacha}. In practice, non-Jews are {\i chayav mita} for violating many of the {\i sheva mitzvot} where Jews are not. This strongly suggests that even as a {\i halachic} category, Jews are not subsumed within {\i bnei Noach} \u8212- at least not for purposes of obligation and liability. The passing remark in {\i Nedarim} may not itself elaborate on this, but the question surfaces naturally here, and is treated more fully in Sanhedrin and, to some extent, in Avodah Zarah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zera Avraham\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: {\i she\u8217'eini ne\u8217'eneh le\u8217'zera Avraham} \u8212- one who vows not to benefit from the seed of Abraham \u8212- is forbidden from Jews but permitted from non-Jews. The {\i Gemara} asks: but Ishmaelites are also descendants of Abraham biologically. The answer is {\i ki ve\u8217'Yitzchak yikarei lecha zara} \u8212- through Isaac shall your seed be called \u8212- so that biologically Ishmael descends from Abraham, but in terms of how that lineage is identified, only the line of Isaac carries the designation. And what of Esau, who is also a descendant? The verse says {\i be\u8217'Yitzchak} \u8212- within Isaac \u8212- {\i velo kol Yitzchak}, not all of Isaac. So Esau is equally excluded. One might add {\i bnei Ketura} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is notable here is the {\i Gemara\u8217's} approach. It could simply have invoked {\i halach achar leshon bnei adam} \u8212- in common vernacular, {\i zera Avraham} means Jews, not non-Jews \u8212- and left it at that. But the {\i Gemara} instead demonstrates that even at the level of the {\i pesukim} themselves, the label {\i zera Avraham} does not apply to all of Abraham\u8217's biological descendants. It was always a more restricted designation, limited to those identified as Abraham\u8217's line in the covenantal sense. This makes the ruling more principled: it is not merely a concession to popular usage, but a reflection of how the {\i pesukim} themselves define the category.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Benefit from Jews\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overpaying Buyer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to a case with direct practical consequences: {\i she\u8217'eini ne\u8217'eneh mi\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- I will not benefit from Jews. How does one then conduct commerce? The {\i mishnah} answers: {\i lokeach beyoter umocheir bepachot} \u8212- one must overpay when buying and undersell when selling. If I go into Michael\u8217's store and I have vowed not to benefit from Jews, then I must overpay for whatever he sells me. The logic is that if I am paying more than the item is worth, I am not truly receiving a benefit \u8212- I am receiving less than my money\u8217's worth, so he has not done anything for me in any meaningful sense. The {\i chiddush} here is that merely being available to sell me milk so I need not travel elsewhere is not conceptualized as benefit. If, however, I am paying five dollars for a three-dollar gallon of milk, then whatever transaction occurred, I have not come out ahead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This naturally raises the question of {\i ona\u8217'ah} \u8212- the prohibition against fraudulent overcharging. If one knowingly overpays and accepts that arrangement willingly, the rules of {\i ona\u8217'ah} may not apply in the ordinary sense, since {\i ona\u8217'ah} depends on the parties not having consented to the deviation. There is also a question of degree: does one dollar over market price suffice, or must the overpayment be more substantial? The analysis here touches on the {\i sugya} of {\i al menat she\u8217'ein bo ona\u8217'ah}, and the question of whether one must actually take a loss or merely receive less than market value. This is a genuinely important question that the {\i Gemara} does not resolve in full here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Underselling Seller\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse case: {\i sheYisrael ne\u8217'enin li} \u8212- Jews shall not benefit from me. In that case, {\i lokeach bepachot umocheir beyoter} \u8212- one must underpay when buying and overcharge when selling. If I run a store and declare that no Jew may benefit from me, then when Michael walks in I must charge five dollars for three-dollar milk. Michael, of course, will simply go elsewhere. The {\i mishnah} acknowledges this: {\i ve\u8217'ein shom\u8217'in lo} \u8212- no one will agree to these terms \u8212- though some versions read {\i im shom\u8217'in lo}, if anyone does agree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final and combined case: {\i she\u8217'eini ne\u8217'eneh lahen vehen lo yehenu li} \u8212- I will not benefit from them and they will not benefit from me. One cannot overpay and cannot undersell. What then? {\i Yehenu le\u8217'ovdei kochavim} \u8212- one must do all business exclusively with non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think such a {\i neder} is automatically {\i battel} \u8212- like a vow never to sleep for a week, which is simply impossible to fulfill. But the {\i Ran} rejects this: it is not automatically void because it is possible to fulfill it. One need only conduct all interactions with non-Jews. The arrangement is admittedly {\i takanah rechokah}, a remote and difficult solution given how much of Tractate Avodah Zarah is devoted to navigating the constraints of commercial interaction even with non-Jews. But difficult is not the same as impossible, and the {\i neder} therefore stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A crucial conceptual point emerges here that the {\i Ran} makes explicit and that will have direct bearing on the next {\i perek}. One might ask: why does the {\i mishnah} need to spell out the remedy of overpaying or underselling? If I had vowed {\i nikhasai alecha} \u8212- my property is forbidden to you \u8212- then it would make no difference whether you overpay or underpay: any transfer of my property would be prohibited. Why then does overpaying resolve anything?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that in the cases of our {\i mishnah}, the object being forbidden is not property \u8212- it is persons. I am the forbidden object, or you are. When I say I will not benefit from Jews, I am declaring that Jews \u8212- as persons \u8212- are the forbidden entity, and the question is what types of interactions with them constitute deriving benefit from them. Selling something to me at fair market value is a form of benefit I receive through our interaction. But if I overpay, I have not come out ahead in that interaction, and therefore I have not derived benefit from you as a person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if my property were the forbidden object, no transfer of my property could occur regardless of price. But since I am the forbidden object \u8212- not my property \u8212- the question is whether any given transaction constitutes someone deriving benefit from me. If the terms are sufficiently skewed against the beneficiary, it does not. This distinction between the person as the forbidden object and the property as the forbidden object is, as the {\i Ran} stresses, a foundational conceptual shift. It will remain essential as the next {\i perek} turns to analyzing exactly what types of acts constitute benefit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Test Drive Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Shmuel}: one who takes a vessel from a craftsman {\i le\u8217'vakro} \u8212- to inspect it before purchasing \u8212- and it is damaged by {\i oness} while in his possession, is liable. The paradigm is something like a test drive: you have not yet agreed to buy the item, you are taking it to evaluate it, and an accident occurs that is entirely not your fault. Are you liable?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer turns on whether you are considered a {\i sho\u8217'el} \u8212- a borrower. A {\i sho\u8217'el} is liable even for {\i oness}, accidents beyond his control, because he is the exclusive beneficiary of the arrangement: he gets use of the item at no cost, while the owner receives nothing. If the potential purchaser is in the same position \u8212- sole beneficiary \u8212- then he too bears {\i sho\u8217'el} liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Intuitively, one might resist this. The seller is also getting something out of the arrangement: he is hoping to make a sale and presumably earning a commission. The interaction seems mutually beneficial. But {\i Shmuel} holds otherwise: in such a transaction, the purchaser is the genuine beneficiary. The seller is not really gaining from letting you take the item \u8212- his gain depends entirely on whether you buy it, and if you return it without buying, he has received nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle \u8212- that the purchaser is the real beneficiary in a normal transaction \u8212- is, frankly, a recurring {\i Gemara} assumption that is difficult to fully absorb. It seems to reflect the social and economic world of the {\i Gemara}, which was primarily agricultural rather than commercial. In a commercial craftsman\u8217's context, one would expect the seller to be equally invested in making sales. Yet the {\i Gemara} often frames the purchaser as the one who wins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The classic expression of this sensibility appears in the {\i midrash} on {\i ki lekach tov natati lachem torati al taazovu}: the verse describes {\i Torah} as a {\i lekach}, a purchase, to underscore that God is not like a human seller. In a human transaction, the {\i midrash} says, {\i mocher atzev veloke\u8217'ach samei\u8217'ach} \u8212- the seller is sad and the buyer is happy. The seller experiences loss \u8212- he is letting go of something he made or owned \u8212- even if he is in the business of selling it. A craftsman has invested himself in what he produced, and parting with it involves a psychological loss, however rational the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hot Items Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then challenges {\i Shmuel\u8217's} ruling from our {\i mishnah}. If the purchaser is always the sole beneficiary in a transaction, then even in a normal sale at market price, the seller should not be considered to benefit. But our {\i mishnah} implies that at market price both parties benefit \u8212- which is precisely why one must deviate from market price in order to ensure that only one side benefits. If only the purchaser ever benefits, the {\i mishnah\u8217's} structure makes no sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to resolve this by suggesting that our {\i mishnah} concerns {\i zvina deramei al apeih} \u8212- wares that the seller is desperate to unload, goods that are not moving and that the seller urgently wants to sell. In such a case, even a normal sale benefits the seller. But this creates new difficulties: the {\i reisha} of the {\i mishnah} says that the buyer must overpay, which would also be strange if these are wares the seller is desperate to sell \u8212- in that case, even a normal-priced purchase would benefit the seller, so why must the buyer overpay?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After working through several such difficulties, the {\i Gemara} settles on the obvious conclusion: {\i matnisin bizvina mitza} \u8212- our {\i mishnah} speaks about normal, mid-range goods. For such items, a transaction at fair market price benefits both parties. The seller is content to have made the sale; the buyer is content to have acquired the item at a fair price. That is the assumed baseline.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, accordingly, must be limited. His ruling that the purchaser is the sole beneficiary \u8212- and therefore bears {\i sho\u8217'el} liability \u8212- does not apply to all transactions. {\i Udishmuel, bizvina charifa} \u8212- Shmuel is speaking specifically of a hot item, something in high demand where the seller has no shortage of willing buyers. In that case, the seller loses nothing by declining to sell to you; he can sell to the next person in line. You are the one who benefits from the opportunity to inspect it. The seller is not dependent on your purchase in any meaningful sense. Limit Shmuel to that case, and our {\i mishnah} is coherent: it speaks of normal goods where both sides genuinely benefit from a fair transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewelry to In-Laws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita} in support of {\i Shmuel}\u8217's position: one who purchases vessels from a merchant to send to his in-laws \u8212- essentially, one sends a gift on approval, meaning he has not yet paid but will do so if the gift is accepted \u8212- and an accident befalls the items, is liable {\i behalicha} (on the way there) but exempt {\i uvechazarah} (on the way back).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paradigm is sending jewelry to one\u8217's prospective in-laws before the purchase is finalized. While bringing it to them, one is a hopeful purchaser: one is the sole beneficiary of having the opportunity to show it, one has paid nothing, and one is seen as a {\i sho\u8217'el}. If the item is damaged by {\i oness} on the way there, one is liable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the return trip, however, the situation has changed. The prospective in-laws declined the gift. One is no longer a hopeful purchaser. One will now pay the merchant a rental fee for having had use of the item \u8212- which places the arrangement in the category of {\i nosei sachar}, a paid custodian, who is exempt from {\i oness} but liable for theft, loss, and negligence. {\i Uvechazarah patur} \u8212- exempt on the return \u8212- because the relationship is now mutually beneficial: one pays the rental fee, the merchant receives it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} directly supports {\i Shmuel}: the sole beneficiary \u8212- the hopeful purchaser \u8212- bears {\i sho\u8217'el} liability. Once the relationship becomes bilateral, that liability is reduced to the level of a {\i shomer sachar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safsira Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i safsira} is a middleman who takes goods on a particular kind of consignment. Unlike a standard consignment arrangement where the owner retains title and the middleman takes a percentage of the sale, the {\i safsira} agrees to pay the owner a fixed price if he finds a buyer. Conceptually, the {\i safsira} is a hopeful purchaser of the goods \u8212- not selling the owner\u8217's item, but agreeing to buy it from the owner if he can locate a customer to whom he will then sell his own item at a markup.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i safsira} took a donkey ({\i chamra}) to sell \u8212- notably, the {\i Ran} clarifies that this is a {\i beheimah}, an animal, not a barrel of wine, as {\i chamra} could in another vocalization suggest \u8212- and was unable to find a buyer. As he was returning, the donkey suffered an accident and died, entirely through {\i oness}. Rav Nachman ruled that he was liable to pay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenged this from the {\i baraita} about the jewelry: one returning the jewelry to the merchant after it was declined is {\i patur} from {\i oness}. Why should the {\i safsira} be different when he too is returning?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman replied: {\i chazara de\u8217'safsira holacha hi} \u8212- the return journey of a {\i safsira} is, legally speaking, still a \u8220"going.\u8221" The {\i safsira} has not truly abandoned his hope of selling. {\i De\u8217'ilu mashkach lezvunei afilu abava deveitei, mi lo mazbin leih} \u8212- if he found a buyer at his own doorstep, he would of course have sold immediately. As long as he retains the item and remains a potential purchaser, he is still in the posture of the sole beneficiary and retains {\i sho\u8217'el} liability. Only when he actually returns the item to the owner and formally relinquishes his stake in the transaction does that liability dissolve.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Circumcision Markers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncircumcised Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces the terminology of circumcised and uncircumcised as group identifiers. {\i Konam she\u8217'ani neheneh la\u8217'areilim} \u8212- one who vows not to benefit from the uncircumcised \u8212- is {\i mutar be\u8217'areilei Yisrael} (permitted to benefit from uncircumcised Jews) and {\i assur bemulei ovdei kochavim} (forbidden to benefit from circumcised non-Jews).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is striking. Many non-Jews are circumcised \u8212- Muslims, many Arabs, and as a matter of common practice in twentieth-century America, many non-Jewish men as well. Conversely, there are Jews who, for whatever reason, are uncircumcised. Yet none of this matters: the terms {\i arel} and {\i nimul} as used in the language of {\i nedarim} function as identity markers for non-Jews and Jews respectively, not as descriptions of physical status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Circumcised Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse: {\i she\u8217'ani ne\u8217'eneh le\u8217'mulim} \u8212- one who vows not to benefit from the circumcised \u8212- is {\i assur be\u8217'areilei Yisrael} (forbidden from uncircumcised Jews) and {\i mutar bemulei ovdei kochavim} (permitted to benefit from circumcised non-Jews). Here too, uncircumcised Jewish men are still called {\i mulim} \u8212- circumcised \u8212- in identity terms, and circumcised non-Jews are still called {\i areilim} \u8212- uncircumcised \u8212- as a matter of designation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity Markers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} explains the principle: {\i she\u8217'ein ha\u8217'arla keruyah ela leshem ovdei kochavim} \u8212- the foreskin is a term that refers specifically to non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} grounds this in two {\i pesukim}. {\i Ki kol hagoyim areilim} \u8212- all the nations are uncircumcised (Jeremiah 9:25) \u8212- and {\i vechol beit Yisrael arle lev} \u8212- yet the entire house of Israel are uncircumcised of heart. The verse acknowledges that Israel may be spiritually deficient in some respects, but they are not called uncircumcised in the default, physical sense. The term {\i arel} unqualified ({\i stam}) refers to non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i ve\u8217'haya haplishtiti ha\u8217'arel hazeh} \u8212- and this Philistine, this uncircumcised one (I Samuel 17:26). The use of the term there is obviously derogatory \u8212- it would not have been remarkable simply to note that a Philistine was uncircumcised \u8212- and so it clearly functions as a marker of otherness and contempt. A further {\i pasuk}: {\i pen tismachna bnos plishtim pen ta\u8217'aloznah bnos ha\u8217'areilim} \u8212- lest the daughters of the Philistines rejoice, lest the daughters of the uncircumcised triumph (II Samuel 1:20). Here too, {\i areilim} is used as a general designation for the enemy, not a physiological description.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derogatory Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Azarya adds: {\i me\u8217'usa hi ha\u8217'arla} \u8212- the foreskin is repugnant \u8212- in that it was employed as a way of deprecating the wicked, {\i shenitganu ba reshaim}, as the verse says {\i ki kol hagoyim areilim}. This is not merely a neutral label. The {\i mishnah} is quite candid: the term carries a strongly negative connotation, rooted in a categorical identification of non-Jews as {\i reshaim}. This reflects a pronounced {\i anachnu v\u8217'hem} \u8212- us-and-them \u8212- orientation, in which the foreskin becomes not merely a physical distinction but an identity and moral marker. And correspondingly, {\i milah} \u8212- circumcision \u8212- becomes not just a ritual act but the defining marker of Jewish identity, to the point where the label attaches even when the physical act was not performed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brit Milah Aggada\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brit Milah Greatness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then opens into an extended {\i aggadic} collection on the greatness of {\i brit milah}, a passage notable for its placement within a {\i mishnah} on {\i nedarim}. The {\i Albeck} edition notes that this material appears to have been carried over from the {\i Tosefta}, where it reads as an expansion. Yet even the Kaufmann manuscript \u8212- generally considered the best manuscript of the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- already contains it within the body of the {\i mishnah}. That itself is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opinions are stacked one upon another. Rabbi Yishmael: {\i gedola mila shenichretu aleha shelosh esrei britos} \u8212- the greatness of {\i milah} is that thirteen covenants were cut over it, as the word {\i brit} appears thirteen times in the passage in which God commands Abraham regarding circumcision. Rabbi Yosi: {\i gedola mila shedocha es hashabbos hachamura} \u8212- great is {\i milah} in that it overrides {\i shabbat}, severe as {\i shabbat} is; the {\i brit milah} of the eighth day is performed on {\i shabbat} when it falls on that day. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korcha: great is {\i milah} in that not even the righteous Moses \u8212- and it is notable that the {\i mishnah} uses {\i Moshe HaTzadik}, \u8220"Moses the Righteous,\u8221" a designation one rarely encounters; the usual formulation is {\i Moshe Rabbeinu} \u8212- was given a moment\u8217's latitude when he was lax about performing the {\i milah} for his son at the {\i malon}. Rabbi Nechemyah: great is {\i milah} in that it overrides the laws of {\i nega\u8217'im} \u8212- a spot of {\i tzara\u8217'at} on the foreskin would be excised. {\i Rebbi}: with all the {\i mitzvot} that Abraham performed, he was not called {\i tamim} \u8212- whole, complete \u8212- until he circumcised himself: {\i His\u8217'halech lefanai ve\u8217'heye tamim}, walk before me and be perfect, followed immediately by the commandment of {\i milah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ultimate Importance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final opinion reaches the highest level of claim: {\i gedola mila she\u8217'ilmale hi lo bara HaKadosh Baruch Hu es ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- were it not for {\i brit milah}, God would not have created the world. The source: {\i koh amar Hashem im lo brisi yomam va\u8217'laila chukos shamayim va\u8217'aretz lo samti} \u8212- so says God: if not for My covenant day and night, I would not have established the laws of heaven and earth. The word {\i brit} in this verse, usually taken to refer to {\i Torah}, is here read as referring to {\i milah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milah Wordplay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask \u8212- and this question has no clean answer \u8212- why this collection appears here, in {\i Masechet Nedarim}, in the middle of a {\i mishnah} concerned with group-definition rules in {\i nedarim}. One might also ask why the discussion of {\i brit milah} does not appear in the {\i sugya} of Rabbi Eliezer in {\i Shabbat}, where the {\i halachic} details of performing a {\i brit milah} are analyzed at length, and where such {\i aggadic} material might seem more at home.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One thought \u8212- admittedly somewhat speculative \u8212- is the wordplay on {\i milah} itself. {\i Milah} means both circumcision and word. The entire third {\i perek} has revolved around the power of speech: what words create a {\i neder}, whether mouth and heart are aligned, what linguistic categories people use when they invoke a group\u8217's identity. At the conclusion of that {\i perek}, a reflection on {\i milah} in its dual sense may be intentional. And yet, there is a deep tension here: a {\i neder} takes a neutral world and gives it {\i halachic} status through words alone, with no physical change; whereas the entire weight of the {\i brit milah} discussion \u8212- especially {\i Rebbi\u8217's} opinion that Abraham was not {\i tamim} until he underwent {\i milah} \u8212- emphasizes the centrality of the physical act. Indeed, one might note the irony that a Jewish male who was never circumcised is nevertheless called a {\i nimul} in the language of the {\i mishnah}, suggesting that the {\i identity label} can function independently of the {\i physical reality}. There is something deeply interesting at the intersection of those ideas, even if the {\i mishnah} does not develop it explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On a different note: one might also observe that the {\i brit milah} discussion of Hatafat dam brit \u8212- as noted in connection with a convert who is already circumcised \u8212- demonstrates that the act is more than purely physical. The drop of covenantal blood required even of one already circumcised reflects a ritual and covenantal dimension irreducible to surgery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Dilemma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The bottom of the page brings a {\i baraita} keyed to Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korcha\u8217's ruling that Moses was not given any grace period when he was lax about his son\u8217's {\i milah}. The {\i baraita} asks: but was Moses truly lax? Surely he had a reason. Indeed he did. He reasoned: if I perform the {\i brit milah} and then immediately set out on the journey God commanded \u8212- {\i lech shuv Mitzrayim}, return to Egypt \u8212- the danger to a newborn in the first three days after {\i brit milah} is well documented, as the verse says {\i vayihi bayom hashlishi biheyotam ko\u8217'avim}. Traveling with an infant three days post-circumcision is a genuine {\i sakana}. On the other hand, if I wait the three days, God has explicitly commanded me to go immediately. Moses was thus caught between two obligations, each of which made the other impossible to fulfill immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then was Moses punished at all? The {\i baraita} answers: {\i mipnei shenitasek b\u8217'malon techilah} \u8212- because upon arriving in the town, Moses first attended to his lodging arrangements before performing the {\i brit milah}. Had circumcision been his absolute first priority upon reaching the {\i malon}, the threat would not have materialized. But by addressing accommodations first \u8212- even understandably, even reasonably \u8212- he demonstrated that {\i brit milah} was not at the very top of his hierarchy in that moment. And for that, the verse says {\i vayifgeshehu Hashem vayivakesh hamito} \u8212- God encountered him and sought to kill him. The lesson, conveyed through the {\i aggadic} reading, is that even a man of Moses\u8217's stature and righteousness \u8212- and even when the delay is logically explicable \u8212- the {\i brit milah} admits of no postponement once the moment for it has arrived.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 32\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening & Brit Milah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We open today on daf 32a, completing the third {\i perek} before transitioning into the fourth. As we will see, this is not merely the next chapter but represents a genuine category shift from the issues we have been dealing with. We pick up two lines from the top of the page with Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brit Milah Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} here is focusing entirely on the {\i aggadata} appended to the {\i Mishna} concerning the importance of {\i brit milah}. The {\i Mishna} entered this territory because it discussed whether the terms {\i mulim} and {\i arelim} refer to the physical reality of who has or has not undergone {\i brit milah}, or whether they refer to Jews and non-Jews respectively. The {\i Mishna} determined the latter \u8212- these are identity categories, not anatomical descriptions. Accordingly, the {\i Gemara} proceeds with a series of {\i aggadot} about the supreme importance of {\i brit milah}. It is also worth noting that the {\i Gemara} in {\i Avodah Zarah} discusses this {\i Mishna} in the context of whether a woman can serve as a {\i mohelet}, again underscoring that {\i nimolim} is not purely about physical reality but about who is considered within the {\i brit} \u8212- an issue of identity. And of course, {\i ve-havai Arami hu} \u8212- one who is not within the {\i brit} is, by definition, outside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical vs.\u160 ?Conceptual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This placement at the close of the third {\i perek} of {\i Nedarim} is fascinatingly apt. {\i Nedarim} is a tractate about words creating reality, and {\i milah} itself also means word \u8212- so there is already something poetic here. The {\i aggadata} emphasizes how {\i brit milah} is about changing the physical reality of one\u8217's body, which stands in striking contrast to the phenomenon of {\i nedarim}, which changes the identity of things through words rather than through any alteration of the physical world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milah vs.\u160 ?Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the dialectic is not so simple, because returning to the beginning of the {\i Mishna}, who is considered {\i nimol} and who is considered {\i arel} is not determined purely by physical reality but also by the categories and words we use. There is thus an interesting tension throughout these first three {\i perakim} between the physical world of {\i issur ve-heter} \u8212- of things that create identity through bodily change \u8212- and the conceptual world shaped by thoughts and words, which is the world of {\i nedarim}. There may be no clean resolution, but the tension itself is illuminating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzipporah\u8217's Circumcision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Delay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel teaches: it was not Moshe Rabbeinu whose life was at risk. The verse says that Moshe tarried and should have seized the first opportunity to perform the {\i brit milah} rather than first settling into the inn. The risk, however, fell not on Moshe but, as Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel teaches, {\i ella le-oto tinnok} \u8212- upon the infant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatan Damim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof text is {\i ki chatan damim atah li} \u8212- \u8220"for you are a bridegroom of blood to me.\u8221" The argument runs: go and see who is called a {\i chatan} here \u8212- it must be the infant. Admittedly, this is difficult, since if Tzipporah is the speaker, one would more naturally expect her {\i chatan} to be Moshe rather than her son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh & Ran\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh}, in the left-hand column, addresses why the infant is called a {\i chatan}: {\i she-mitchatnin oto be-mitzvah rishonah} \u8212- one marries him off, as it were, through his first {\i mitzvah}, initiating him into the world of {\i mitzvot}. The {\i Ran} similarly explains {\i she-na\u8217'asah chatan al yedei brit} \u8212- he becomes a {\i chatan} by means of the {\i brit}. Both explanations have a somewhat circular quality \u8212- the infant is called {\i chatan} because through the {\i brit} one makes him a {\i chatan} \u8212- but there they stand. What is genuinely fascinating, however, is the underlying theological question: if it is the infant who was at risk, this is less a punishment of Moshe than a statement about the vulnerability of someone who deserves a {\i brit milah} and does not yet have one \u8212- someone who is, in a sense, not yet fully part of the Jewish nation, not yet within the {\i brit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Symbolism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the way we actually perform a {\i brit milah}, the idea of {\i dam} \u8212- blood \u8212- is central to the ritual. There is the concept of {\i hatafat dam brit} for a conversion candidate who lacks a foreskin. The focus on {\i metzitzah b\u8217'peh} likewise reflects an intense ritual concern with blood. There is even a tradition that when a {\i brit milah} falls on {\i Rosh Hashanah}, the {\i ba\u8217'al tokei\u8217'a} who is also the {\i mohel} should not wipe the blood from his lips before sounding the {\i shofar}, so that the blood of the {\i brit milah} and the {\i korban} of Yitzchak are joined together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This begins to tie the blood of {\i brit milah} to the blood of sacrifices. The entire episode of Tzipporah is, in fact, a {\i parasha} that foregrounds blood \u8212- blood as protection, blood as saving someone vulnerable from death. This clearly foreshadows what will happen in Egypt: the blood of the {\i Korban Pesach} saves those in the house from the {\i mashchit}. Here, too, something comes to destroy, and blood is what saves \u8212- presumably the infant. The blood of {\i brit milah} functions as a salvific, protective force, but this is unique to this {\i parasha}. Everywhere else in the {\i Torah}, {\i brit milah} is described in terms of removing the foreskin or making the covenant \u8212- {\i bayom ha-shmini yimol b\u8217'sar orlato}, {\i yimol lachem kol basar} \u8212- without any mention of blood. Only here does blood appear so prominently, tapping into the world of {\i korbanot}, which can itself be understood as a sublimation of child sacrifice: instead of sacrificing the child, you cut off the foreskin, shed some blood, and the child lives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Af & Cheima Angels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Destructive Angels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda bar Bizna expounds: at the moment that Moshe Rabbeinu was tardy regarding the {\i brit milah}, two destructive angels called {\i Af} and {\i Cheima} \u8212- wrath and rage \u8212- came and swallowed him up. The phrase {\i vayevakesh hamito} is here being translated into these personified destructive forces. {\i Ve-lo shayru mimenu ela raglav} \u8212- the only part of his body they did not swallow was his legs, though the {\i Ran} notes this is a euphemism for his penis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzipporah Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Miyad vatikkach Tzipporah tzor} \u8212- immediately Tzipporah grasped what was happening and picked up a flint. {\i Vatikrot et orlat b\u8217'nah} \u8212- she circumcised her son, and {\i vayeiref mimenu} \u8212- the angels released Moshe at once. One might wonder how Tzipporah intuited the connection. Perhaps she tried many things and this was the one that worked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Wrath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Be-ota sha\u8217'ah bikesh Moshe Rabbeinu lehorgan} \u8212- at that moment Moshe wished to destroy those two angels, as the verse is read: {\i heref me-af va-azov cheimah} \u8212- release {\i Af} and abandon {\i Cheima} \u8212- understood as Moshe wishing to seize and eliminate them. God, however, indicated that the idea of divine anger is a necessary part of the world. The old question of why evil forces exist in a good world underlies this passage: to some degree these forces are necessary, even if the question remains whether they need to be quite as destructive as they are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheima Destroyed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-yesh omrim le-Cheima harag} \u8212- some say Moshe actually did succeed in destroying {\i Cheima}, as implied by the phrase {\i Cheima ein li} \u8212- I no longer have {\i Cheima}. But then a later verse quotes Moshe himself saying {\i ki agorti mipnei ha-af ve-ha-cheima} \u8212- I was fearful of {\i Af} and {\i Cheima} \u8212- suggesting {\i Cheima} still existed at the end of his career. The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i trei Cheima havu} \u8212- there were two types of {\i Cheima}. Moshe destroyed one but not the other. Alternatively, {\i Gundat de-Cheima} \u8212- the verse refers not to {\i Cheima} itself but to the forces and retinue of {\i Cheima}, which Moshe could not eliminate even after destroying {\i Cheima} proper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yetzer Hara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This episode calls to mind the {\i midrashim} that relate how, in the time of the Second Temple, the {\i Anshei Knesset Ha-Gedolah} convened and succeeded in destroying the {\i yetzer hara} of {\i avodah zarah}. The same theological question animates both traditions: if God gave us a good world, why do destructive forces persist? The answer seems to be that to some degree they are necessary \u8212- but the question of whether they must be as powerful and pervasive as they are remains open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brit Milah Greatness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avraham\u8217's Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is introduced: {\i Rebbi omer: gedola milah she-ein lecha mi she-nit\u8217'asek be-mitzvot ke-Avraham Avinu} \u8212- great is {\i brit milah}, for there was no one who was as devoted to {\i mitzvot} as Avraham Avinu \u8212- and yet he was not called {\i tamim}, whole and complete, except on account of {\i brit milah}. {\i She-ne\u8217'emar: hithalech lefanai ve-heyeh tamim} \u8212- walk before Me and be complete \u8212- followed immediately by {\i ve-etna briti beini u-veinecha} \u8212- and I will establish My covenant between Me and you. The verse from {\i brit milah} follows directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a reflection worth pausing over. Avraham symbolizes, in one sense, Jewish identity as an ethnic or familial category \u8212- he predates the full system of {\i taryag mitzvot}, and all who descend from him are part of the Jewish family. This is the language of {\i Bnei Yaakov} to Shechem: {\i ki lo nuchal latet et achotenu le-ish asher lo orlah ki cherpah hi lanu} \u8212- it is shameful to give our sister to one who is uncircumcised. Here {\i brit milah} is pure ethnic-familial identity. By contrast, in the episode of Moshe, {\i brit milah} is connected to {\i Moshe Rabbeinu} and the world of {\i mitzvot} proper. These are two distinct models of what {\i brit milah} symbolizes, and they have implications for how one understands {\i geirus} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i baraita} here, even while invoking Avraham, frames the greatness of {\i brit milah} squarely in the language of {\i mitzvot}: no one was as devoted in performing {\i mitzvot} as Avraham Avinu \u8212- not in the technical, commanded sense, but in the sense of alacrity and zeal in doing everything God asked \u8212- and still he was not {\i tamim} without {\i brit milah}. No matter how many {\i mitzvot} a person performs, this is the one that makes him, at least speaking of men, complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avraham Complete\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Davar acher}: great is {\i brit milah}, for without it Avraham was not {\i tamim} before God. The {\i pasuk} makes this explicit: the command to walk before God and be whole precedes and connects directly to the commandment of {\i brit milah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Equal to All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Davar acher}: {\i gedola milah she-shkula keneged kol ha-mitzvot she-ba-Torah} \u8212- great is {\i brit milah}, for it is of equal weight to all the other {\i mitzvot} in the {\i Torah}. The proof text is {\i ki al pi ha-devarim ha-eleh karati itcha brit ve-et Yisrael} \u8212- because of these matters I have established a covenant with you and with Israel. Since all {\i mitzvot} are part of a {\i brit}, and {\i brit milah} is itself a {\i brit}, it is therefore equal to all of them combined. One hears in such formulations an echo of the pressures of the Hellenistic period, when Greeks viewed {\i brit milah} as a disfiguring of the body and many Jews reversed their circumcision in order to compete in athletic contests in the nude. In that climate, declaring {\i brit milah} equal in weight to all other {\i mitzvot} was a powerful statement about whether one stands within or outside the Jewish people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sustains World\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Davar acher}: {\i gedola milah she-ilmalei milah lo nitkaymu shamayim va-aretz} \u8212- great is {\i brit milah}, for were it not for {\i brit milah}, heaven and earth would not be sustained. The proof text: {\i im lo briti yomam va-layla chukot shamayim va-aretz lo samti} \u8212- if not for My covenant, day and night, I would not have established the laws of heaven and earth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 vs.\u160 ?Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-fliga de-Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- this position argues with Rabbi Eliezer, who taught: {\i gedola Torah she-ilmalei Torah lo nitkaymu shamayim va-aretz} \u8212- great is {\i Torah}, for were it not for {\i Torah}, heaven and earth would not be sustained \u8212- citing the very same verse. Both readings draw on the same {\i pasuk} but identify its referent differently: one says {\i brit milah}, the other says {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elitism Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This disagreement is itself quite fascinating from a sociological perspective. The claim that {\i Torah} keeps the world going feeds into a certain kind of elitism: historically, only a very small percentage of people could truly say they devoted themselves to {\i talmud Torah} in the deep sense. If it is {\i Torah} that sustains the world, then it is the {\i talmidei chachamim} who sustain the world. If it is {\i brit milah}, it excludes fifty percent of the population, but fully and equally includes the other fifty percent. There is a meaningful difference between a populist and an elitist framing embedded in this dispute. An additional dimension worth noting: {\i brit milah} is something done {\i to} a person \u8212- the infant has no consciousness of it, no intention. {\i Nedarim}, by contrast, is all intention. The most important things are sometimes entirely outside one\u8217's control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Avraham Confused\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avraham Trembles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: at the moment that the Holy One Blessed be He said to Avraham Avinu {\i hithalech lefanai ve-heyeh tamim} \u8212- walk before Me and be complete \u8212- {\i ahazato ra\u8217'adah} \u8212- he was seized with trembling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Avraham said to himself: {\i shema yesh bi davar meguneh} \u8212- perhaps there is something unseemly, something disgraceful within me, that God must tell me to now become whole and complete? Is there some character deficiency I need to correct?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God Clarifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then God said to him {\i ve-etna briti beini u-veinecha} \u8212- and I will establish My covenant between Me and you \u8212- and {\i nitkarrera da\u8217'ato} \u8212- Avraham\u8217's mind was cooled, calmed. He understood: it is not that his character is incomplete. It is something about his physical body, a {\i mitzvah} God has not yet commanded him. The incompleteness was not moral but physical \u8212- and the {\i brit milah} was the remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mazal Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avraham\u8217's Mazal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vayotze oto ha-chutza} \u8212- God took him outside. This passage is actually two {\i perakim} earlier in the {\i Torah} than the {\i brit milah} narrative. Avraham said before God: {\i Ribono Shel Olam}, I have looked into my {\i mazal} \u8212- my astrological sign \u8212- and one {\i girsa} reads {\i ein li ela ben echad} \u8212- I have only one son \u8212- though this does not fit, since this episode precedes the birth of Yishmael. The preferable {\i girsa} is {\i ve-eini ra\u8217'ui l\u8217'holid ben} \u8212- I am not suited to have children \u8212- which makes sense given Avraham\u8217's advanced age and lack of offspring until that point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Mazal Israel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God responded: {\i tze me-istagninus shelcha} \u8212- leave your astrology \u8212- {\i ein mazal le-Yisrael} \u8212- there is no {\i mazal} for Israel. The Jewish people are {\i lemala min ha-mazal}, above and beyond the forces of the astrological constellations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi cites this teaching on the {\i Torah} and concludes: {\i Avram eino molid, Avraham molid} \u8212- Avram does not have children, that is what you see in your {\i mazal}, but Avraham \u8212- when your name changes \u8212- will have children. This is not merely a legal loophole. In the same {\i parasha} where God commands {\i brit milah}, God adds the letter {\i heh} to Avraham\u8217's name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transcendence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, I would suggest, is the deeper point connecting this passage to the {\i brit milah} discussion. The Greeks criticized {\i brit milah} as disfiguring what God gave you \u8212- the body is perfect, why mutilate it? But the message of {\i brit milah} is the opposite: we are not subservient to the forces of nature. We can change the course of things, rise above what the natural order would dictate. {\i Ein mazal le-Yisrael} \u8212- the commandment of {\i brit milah} is itself an act of transcendence over the natural order. You transform yourself, you change the course of nature, and with your new name you become capable of things that seemed naturally impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tamim Character\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocal Wholehearted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak said: {\i kol ha-matmim atzmo ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu matmim imo} \u8212- whoever conducts himself with wholeheartedness, God deals with him wholeheartedly. The proof text: {\i im chasid titchasad, im gibbor tamim titamam} \u8212- with the righteous You act righteously, with the strong in his wholeheartedness You deal wholeheartedly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Success Follows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Oshaya said: {\i kol ha-matmim atzmo sha\u8217'ah omedet lo} \u8212- whoever makes himself {\i tamim} will have great success. The proof text: {\i hithalech lefanai ve-heyeh tamim} \u8212- followed by {\i ve-hayita le-av hamon goyim} \u8212- and you will become the father of a multitude of nations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical to Internal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A subtle but important shift has occurred here. We have been speaking about the supreme greatness of {\i brit milah}, and how Avraham was not complete until he made this physical change in his body. Now we are saying that {\i tamim} \u8212- complete, whole, wholehearted \u8212- is a character trait, not a reference to {\i brit milah} per se. It is about how you conduct yourself in your inner life. The message of the {\i pasuk} has been extended from the physical act to the internal disposition. One might say the physical act is the external expression and the character is the internal: the external-internal relationship. But this is nonetheless a {\i chiddush} \u8212- it is not obvious, and the {\i Gemara} makes a point of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divination Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposite of {\i tamim} is illustrated next. {\i Kol ha-menachesh lo nachash} \u8212- whoever practices divination will have the snake come back at him. {\i Tamim} means having pure, simple faith in God \u8212- not trying to beat the system, not trying to use magical devices to divine the future. Divination ({\i nachash}) reflects an anxiety-driven attempt to secure oneself against the unknown rather than trusting in God. The proof text cited is {\i ki lo nachash be-Yaakov} \u8212- there is no divination in Yaakov \u8212- though the {\i Gemara} notes the word is spelled {\i lo} with a {\i lamed-alef}, meaning \u8220"there is none,\u8221" not {\i lo} with a {\i vav}, meaning \u8220"to him.\u8221" The teaching is therefore grounded not in a textual reading but in the principle of {\i mida ke-neged mida}: if you try to circumvent God\u8217's way and figure things out through divination, it will come back to harm you. Simple faith is the best protection against anxiety about the future.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pure Faith Reward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ahava bar Rabbi Zeira taught: {\i kol adam she-eino menachesh machnisin oto be-mechitza she-afilu malachei ha-sharet ein yecholim likanes betochah} \u8212- whoever does not practice divination is brought into an inner chamber so close to God that even the ministering angels cannot enter there. The proof text: {\i ki lo nachash be-Yaakov ve-lo kesem be-Yisrael} \u8212- there is no divination in Yaakov and no witchcraft in Israel \u8212- and the end of the verse: {\i ka-et ye\u8217'amer le-Yaakov u-le-Yisrael mah pa\u8217'al El} \u8212- now it will be told to Yaakov and to Israel what God has done. If you have pure faith in how God acts in the world and what God will do, you will know better than the angels what God is going to do. Actively trying to divine God\u8217's plans causes you to forfeit that closeness; simply trusting God elevates you beyond the angels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Avraham Punished\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egypt Slavery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu said in the name of Rabbi Elazar: why was Avraham Avinu punished and his descendants enslaved in Egypt for 210 years?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drafting Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first explanation: {\i mipnei she-asah angarya be-talmidei chachamim} \u8212- because he conscripted {\i talmidei chachamim} into his army. The verse: {\i vayarek et chanichav yelidei beito} \u8212- he armed his {\i chanichav}, those trained in his household. The word {\i chanich} is understood, based on the {\i Ran}, as {\i le-chanech ba-Torah} \u8212- those initiated into {\i Torah} study \u8212- the {\i Torah} of Shem and Ever, where Yaakov later studied, the Torah inherited from Noach. This {\i Gemara} has been cited extensively in {\i Charedi} circles in Israel as a basis for exempting {\i yeshiva} students from military service: Avraham drafted {\i talmidei chachamim}, and for that his descendants were enslaved in Egypt. It is one of the more consequential applications of {\i aggadata} in contemporary policy debates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceeded Attributes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel offers a second, more textually direct explanation: {\i she-hifriz al middotav shel ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- he went too far regarding God\u8217's attributes, exceeded what was appropriate in challenging God. The verse: {\i bama eida ki irashenah} \u8212- by what shall I know that I will inherit the land of Israel? Rather than simply trusting in God\u8217's explicit promise, Avraham pushed further and demanded proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Faith vs.\u160 ?Demand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects directly to the earlier {\i tamim} discussion. The theme of {\i tamim} is: have faith, trust that things will work out, and certainly when God explicitly promises you something, do not say {\i bama eida} \u8212- how will I know? That is the antithesis of the pure trust we described. Avraham, the one time he failed to embody {\i tamim} and demanded proof rather than accepting God\u8217's word, was punished accordingly. The drafting of {\i talmidei chachamim} is something of a digression from the central thematic thread; it is Shmuel\u8217's explanation that ties most naturally into the surrounding discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lost Souls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separated People\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan said: the problem was that Avraham separated people from coming under the wings of the {\i Shechina}. He had an opportunity to bring people into the covenant, under God\u8217's protection, and he let it slip away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melech Sedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse: {\i ten li ha-nefesh ve-ha-rechush kach lach} \u8212- the King of Sodom said to Avram: give me the souls, the people you recaptured, and you may keep the spoils. Avram declined the booty \u8212- but he should have done the reverse: keep the people, let Sodom have the money. He had an opportunity to bring these people into his community, to serve God, and he lost them. He refused everything \u8212- the money and the people \u8212- when he should have insisted on keeping the people and surrendered only the spoils.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geirus Today\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching lends itself to applications as pointed as those from the {\i talmidei chachamim} passage, but in a very different direction. One could observe that when there are large populations \u8212- such as halachically non-Jewish {\i olim} \u8212- who could be brought under the wings of the {\i Shechina} through a proactive approach to {\i geirus}, and instead impossibly high standards and communal inaction mean they are left outside, the parallel to Avraham\u8217's lost opportunity is uncomfortable. The {\i aggadata} of the Talmud contains a multiplicity of voices, and one must be honest about the fact that different communities selectively cite those that support their agenda. Both the {\i talmidei chachamim} passage and this one are genuine voices in the tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gematria Teachings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer = 318\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with the episode of Avraham arming his household to pursue Lot\u8217's captors: {\i shmona asar u-shlosh me\u8217'ot} \u8212- 318. Rabbi Ammi bar Abba says: {\i Eliezer kineged kulam} \u8212- Eliezer was equivalent to all 318 in his capacity to wage war. Some say further: the 318 did not literally mean 318 people at all \u8212- it was just Eliezer alone. The proof: the {\i gematria} of {\i Eliezer} is 318. The {\i reish} is 300, the {\i lamed} and {\i ayin} together are 100, and the {\i yud-zayin-alef} are 18, totaling 318.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ammi bar Abba further taught: {\i ben gimel shanim hikir Avraham et boro} \u8212- Avraham recognized his Creator at the age of three. The proof: {\i ekev asher shama Avraham be-koli} \u8212- because Avraham listened to My voice. The {\i gematria} of {\i ekev} is 172. Avraham lived to 175. Therefore Avraham listened to God\u8217's voice for 172 of his years \u8212- meaning he recognized God at age three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the occasion for a notable dispute between the {\i Rambam} and the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}. In the opening of {\i Mishneh Torah}, the {\i Rambam} describes Avraham coming to know God through philosophical inquiry and suggests Avraham became aware of God at the age of 40. The {\i Ra\u8217'avad} quotes this very {\i Gemara} in objection: the age was three, not 40. The disagreement reflects a deeper divide: the {\i Rambam} understood knowing God as an intellectual achievement requiring mature philosophical reasoning, and therefore could not accept the notion that a three-year-old {\i knew} God in any meaningful sense \u8212- he therefore preferred a different tradition, apparently from a {\i Midrash}, which gave the age as 40. The {\i Ra\u8217'avad}, more mystical in orientation, had no difficulty accepting that a three-year-old could intuit or experience God in a manner that counts as {\i recognition}. The {\i Rambam} effectively discounts the {\i aggadic} tradition of age three by privileging the philosophical model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Satan = 364\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Abba said: the {\i gematria} of {\i ha-Satan} \u8212- {\i shin-tet-nun}, along with the definite article {\i heh} \u8212- is 364. There are 365 days in the solar year. This means Satan has no power over Israel for one day of the year \u8212- which is Yom Kippur. The {\i gematria} works by the solar calendar even though our calendar is lunar; the point is the numerical symbolism, not calendrical precision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 248 Limbs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ammi bar Abba said: one verse says {\i Avram} and another says {\i Avraham} \u8212- what is the significance of the name change? Initially, God gave Avraham dominion over 243 of his limbs. A person has 248 limbs, and the {\i gematria} of {\i Avram} is 243. Ultimately, God gave him dominion over all 248 \u8212- {\i u-le-vasof himlicho al matayim ve-arba\u8217'im u-shmonah} \u8212- the addition of the {\i heh} in the name {\i Avraham} accounting for the five additional limbs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Control Limbs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Which were the five limbs over which he did not originally have dominion? {\i Shte einayim} \u8212- his two eyes, {\i u-shte oznayim} \u8212- his two ears, and {\i ve-rosh ha-geviyah} \u8212- the tip of his penis. The connection to {\i brit milah} is obvious. These are the organs of perception and desire: what we see and hear can lead us astray \u8212- {\i lo tasuru acharei levavechem ve-acharei eineichem} \u8212- and the sexual drive is what compels one to pursue what the eyes and ears have stirred. Avraham, through {\i brit milah}, gained mastery over those organs. One can try to stop up one\u8217's ears or close one\u8217's eyes, but the drives of perception and desire are not always within voluntary control. The {\i brit milah} represents symbolic \u8212- and perhaps more than symbolic \u8212- mastery over them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Control Drives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} in fact follows precisely this reading of {\i brit milah}: its purpose is to curb the sexual appetite. The {\i Rambam} states explicitly that {\i brit milah} decreases sexual pleasure, and that this is its intended function \u8212- we are meant to control and master our sexual drive rather than be controlled by it. This view has obvious contemporary resonance in debates about whether circumcision increases or decreases sexual pleasure, but regardless of the empirical question, the {\i Gemara} here establishes that {\i symbolically}, what {\i brit milah} is about is gaining control and mastery over one\u8217's drives rather than letting them dominate. And the insight of one of the students is well-taken: {\i brit milah} is done {\i to} the infant \u8212- he is passive \u8212- and perhaps the result is mastery over precisely those things in life over which one is passive. You cannot always control what enters your eyes and ears; what you can control is how you respond. The organs of reception become subject to your will.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 City Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Siege Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Abba expounds on a verse in {\i Kohelet}: {\i ir ketana} \u8212- a small city, {\i va-anashim bo me\u8217'at} \u8212- with few people \u8212- and a great king comes and lays siege to it, surrounding it. {\i Ir ketana zeh ha-guf} \u8212- the small city is the body. {\i Va-anashim ba me\u8217'at elu evarim} \u8212- the few inhabitants are the limbs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Uva eleha melech gadol} \u8212- and a great king comes to besiege it: {\i zeh yetzer hara} \u8212- this is the {\i yetzer hara}. The small, weak body is besieged by the powerful forces of lust and drive seeking to take control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sins Traps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-vanah aleha metzudim ve-charamim} \u8212- and he builds against it catapults and traps: {\i elu avonot} \u8212- these are the sins. The {\i yetzer hara} lays sins as pitfalls and snares for you to fall into. The siege is not only the initial assault but the accumulated network of temptations that progressively encircle the person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yetzer Hatov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-matza vah ish misken ve-chacham} \u8212- and there was found in the city a poor but wise man: {\i zeh yetzer tov} \u8212- this is the {\i yetzer hatov}, that small quiet voice of conscience. {\i U-milat et ha-ir be-chochmato} \u8212- and he saved the city with his wisdom. And what is that wisdom? {\i Zu teshuvah u-ma\u8217'asim tovim} \u8212- repentance and good deeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teshuvah Future\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i teshuvah} is striking here because it normally refers to repairing past sins \u8212- you have already transgressed, and now you repent. But in this {\i mashal}, the {\i teshuvah} is described as {\i saving} the city, that is, protecting it from future conquest. The resolution: when the {\i Gemara} says the sins are traps, it means not merely that you might sin, but that once you have committed some sins, those past sins become traps for the future. The {\i yetzer hara} whispers: you have already sinned, you are not a {\i tzaddik}, why try to live up to a higher standard? Past sins become an argument for future capitulation. And what saves you? The {\i yetzer hatov} saying: you can do {\i teshuvah}, you can actually live differently. So {\i teshuvah} functions here not only as a remedy for the past but as a protection against being snared by the past into further sin in the future.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forgotten Helper\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-adam lo zachar et ha-ish ha-misken ha-hu} \u8212- but no one remembered that poor man. The city was saved, but not through a visible act of heroism \u8212- through quiet wisdom \u8212- and so the man was forgotten. {\i De-visha\u8217'at yetzer hara leit de-midkar leh le-yetzer ha-tov} \u8212- when your lusts and drives are seizing control of you, it becomes very difficult to remember the voice of conscience, to recall how it saved you before. The power of desire crowds out the memory of the quieter voice that once protected you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wisdom Strength\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha-chochmah ta\u8217'oz la-chacham me-asarah shalitim} \u8212- wisdom gives strength to the wise man more than ten shields. {\i Zo teshuvah u-ma\u8217'asim tovim} \u8212- again, repentance and good deeds. And the ten shields that wisdom surpasses are identified as: {\i shte einayim} \u8212- two eyes, {\i u-shte oznayim} \u8212- two ears, {\i u-shte yadayim} \u8212- two hands (the sense of touch, which also leads astray), {\i u-shte raglayim} \u8212- two legs, which physically bring a person to places of sin, {\i ve-rosh ha-geviyah} \u8212- the tip of the penis, and {\i ve-ha-peh} \u8212- the mouth, through which one talks, arouses appetite through speech, and violates {\i lashon hara}. These are the ten things that will lead a person astray \u8212- and the {\i yetzer hatov}, the wisdom of repentance and good deeds, is stronger than all ten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Temptations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The thematic arc here is important. We have moved from {\i brit milah} as the source of mastery over the five organs \u8212- the eyes, ears, and tip of the penis \u8212- to the {\i yetzer hatov} as the inner force that gives strength over all ten instruments of temptation. Just as we observed the shift from physical {\i tamim} to {\i tamim} as a character trait, here too the {\i Gemara} moves from the physical {\i brit milah} giving control over drives to the developed inner conscience of the {\i yetzer hatov} providing protection. {\i Brit milah} is the foundational physical reality; {\i yetzer hatov} is the internalized extension of that same principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohenim Origin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shem Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zechariah said in the name of Rabbi Yishmael: God originally intended to bring the {\i kehunah} \u8212- the priesthood \u8212- from Shem. Not from within Bnei Yisrael, not from among any particular Jewish tribe, but from the descendants of Shem son of Noach. They would be the {\i kohanim} of the world for all future generations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Malki-Tzedek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof: {\i ve-hu kohen le-El elyon} \u8212- and he was a {\i kohen} to the Most High God \u8212- referring to Malki-Tzedek, king of Shalem, whom {\i Chazal} identify as Shem son of Noach. This establishes that Shem already served as priest, and his descendants were slated to continue in that role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universalism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One should pause to appreciate the striking universalist dimension of this idea. Our conventional framework is that the Jewish people are chosen, and within the Jewish people the {\i kohanim} occupy the highest rung of sacred service. But this {\i Gemara} envisions an alternative reality: the category of {\i kohanim} would have been {\i non-Jews} \u8212- descendants of Shem, but not Jews \u8212- serving in a role of highest holiness, perhaps even in the Temple. It is a genuinely remarkable universalist vision, at least in potential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blessing Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i keivan she-hikdim birkat Avraham le-birkat ha-Makom} \u8212- since Malki-Tzedek placed the blessing of Avraham before the blessing of God \u8212- {\i hotzi\u8217'ah me-Avraham} \u8212- God transferred the {\i kehunah} to Avraham\u8217's line. The verse: {\i va-yevarechehu va-yomar baruch Avraham le-El elyon koneh shamayim va-aretz, u-varuch El elyon} \u8212- blessed is Avraham to the Most High God, owner of heaven and earth, and blessed is the Most High God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avraham Correct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Avraham immediately objected: {\i ve-chi makdimin birkat eved le-birkat kono?} \u8212- does one give the blessing of the slave before the blessing of his master? You blessed me before you blessed God. {\i Miyad nitnah le-Avraham} \u8212- immediately the {\i kehunah} was given to Avraham. The proof text: {\i ne\u8217'um Hashem la-adoni} \u8212- God said to my master \u8212- where {\i adoni} refers to Avraham \u8212- {\i shev li-ymini} \u8212- sit at My right \u8212- {\i ad ashit oyvecha hadom le-raglecha} \u8212- until I make your enemies your footstool. And then the verse continues: {\i nishba Hashem ve-lo yinachem, atah kohen le-olam al dibrati Malki-Tzedek} \u8212- God has sworn and will not relent: you are a priest forever, because of ({\i al dibrati}) Malki-Tzedek.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Words Matter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does {\i al dibrati Malki-Tzedek} mean? The {\i Gemara} reads it as {\i al diburo shel Malki-Tzedek} \u8212- because of what Malki-Tzedek {\i said}, because of how he misspoke. {\i Ve-hu kohen, hu kohen, ve-ein zaro kohen} \u8212- he was a {\i kohen}, but his descendants are not {\i kohanim}. His improper speech cost his line the {\i kehunah} for all time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Words Final\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And this is a remarkable note on which to conclude the extended {\i aggadata} of these three {\i perakim}. Throughout this entire {\i sugya}, a profound tension has run: the physical reality of {\i brit milah} versus the world of character and inner traits. Are you {\i tamim} because of the physical {\i brit}, or because of the integrity of your inner life? Does {\i brit milah} give you mastery over your drives, or does your {\i yetzer hatov}? And through all of this we are in {\i Masechet Nedarim} \u8212- the tractate of words. So what ultimately determines who is the {\i kohen}? Words. How {\i Malki-Tzedek} used words \u8212- whether properly or improperly \u8212- determined the entire future of the sacred service. In the end, the physical realities in which we live and the words that shape our realities are both indispensable. One might frame this entire {\i sugya} in contemporary terms as a tension between a behaviorist and a psychodynamic orientation: does a person change through behavior and physical action, or through the inner transformation of the self? The Talmud holds both in tension without resolving them \u8212- and perhaps that is the point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fourth Chapter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the fourth {\i perek}, which, as noted at the outset, is not merely the next chapter but a genuine category shift. The first three {\i perakim} addressed how {\i nedarim} are created: what words and intentions are required, what population is included when one uses particular formulations, and so forth. The fourth {\i perek} assumes the {\i neder} is already in force \u8212- it is valid and binding \u8212- and asks: what is the scope of the restriction? What exactly is forbidden and what is permitted?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pleasure vs.\u160 ?Food\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} states: {\i ein bein ha-mudar hana\u8217'ah me-chavero le-mudar heimenu ma\u8217'achal} \u8212- there is no difference between one who is forbidden to derive any benefit ({\i hana\u8217'ah}) from his fellow and one who is forbidden only to derive food benefit ({\i hana\u8217'at ma\u8217'achal}). This seems counterintuitive: surely there is a vast difference between being forbidden all benefit and being forbidden only food-related benefit \u8212- there are countless types of benefit that have nothing to do with food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} goes on to enumerate the only differences: {\i ella drisat ha-regel} \u8212- the right to walk through someone\u8217's yard. If one is forbidden all benefit, one cannot cross through the person\u8217's property. But if the prohibition is limited to food benefit, one may traverse the yard. Similarly: {\i ve-ha-keilim she-ein osim bahem ochel nefesh} \u8212- vessels not used for food purposes. These too fall outside the scope of a food benefit prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money & Food\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} will explain why these are virtually the only differences. When someone says {\i hana\u8217'at ma\u8217'achal} is forbidden, the correct interpretation is: you are forbidden from any benefit that can translate into money, because anything worth any money at all could potentially become food \u8212- if it is worth even a small amount, one could go and purchase something to eat. Therefore, the scope of a food benefit prohibition is nearly as broad as a general benefit prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Charge Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only benefits that escape this broad reading are those that have no monetary value associated with them \u8212- things for which no one would normally pay. Cutting through someone\u8217's yard is an example of such a benefit: one {\i could} charge for it, but ordinarily no one does, so it carries no dollar value and therefore cannot translate into food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels vs.\u160 ?Clothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What about {\i keilim} \u8212- vessels? Here a further distinction applies. If one would normally rent out a particular item \u8212- say, a rake \u8212- then borrowing it constitutes a monetizable benefit, and the person under a food benefit prohibition cannot borrow it. But if one normally lends such items freely as an act of neighborliness, without charge, then borrowing it is not a benefit that could translate into money, and it is permitted. However \u8212- and this is the key exception \u8212- even if one normally lends out food vessels without charge, those vessels remain forbidden to the person under a food benefit prohibition, because they relate directly to food preparation. By contrast, non-food items such as clothing \u8212- a shirt, a ring, a cloak, earrings \u8212- may be borrowed freely, since they bear no connection to food even when their monetary value might otherwise render them relevant. This distinction between food-related vessels and non-food items like clothing will be developed further in the discussion that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 33\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Transition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Welcome & Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 33, and we begin once again from the bottom of 32b \u8212- from the opening of the new chapter, the new {\i perek} \u8212- and its fresh {\i mishnah}. As noted in the previous session, we are now shifting from a discussion of {\i when} a vow is binding to a discussion of {\i what} is included in a vow once it has been established as binding. Specifically, the subject is what constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- benefit \u8212- since the classic {\i neder} forbids the subject from deriving benefit from a particular person or object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous Perek Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous {\i perek} addressed the conditions under which a {\i neder} is binding: examining the reasons behind it, the context in which it was made, and the motivations of the one who made it, in order to determine whether the person truly intended the vow and under what circumstances it takes effect. That entire discussion assumed the question of whether the {\i neder} is valid at all. The current {\i perek} makes a decisive shift: we assume the vow is valid and binding. The question now is what falls within its scope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope of Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- benefit \u8212- is the organizing category. A generic {\i neder} forbids deriving benefit from a person or object in all its forms. But what counts as benefit? This is the central question that will occupy the daf. Closely related is the narrower formulation \u8212- forbidding only food-related benefit \u8212- and understanding the precise difference between these two types of vows is what the {\i mishnah} and subsequent {\i gemara} set out to clarify.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Generic vs Food Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Bein Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ein bein hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechavero lemudar heimenu ma\u8217'achal} \u8212- there is no difference between one who is forbidden all benefit from his friend and one who is forbidden only food from him. This formulation comes from the list of {\i ein bein} distinctions, familiar from Tractate Megilla. At first glance, one would expect an enormous gap between a comprehensive prohibition on benefit and a prohibition limited to food. The literal translation of {\i mudar heimenu ma\u8217'achal} is simply \u8220"forbidden from his food,\u8221" which would seem to leave the vast majority of human interactions untouched. The {\i gemara} will have to explain why, in practice, the gap is so narrow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states that the only difference between the two is {\i drisas ha-regel} \u8212- the ability to cut across his yard \u8212- and {\i keilim she-ein osim bahem ochel nefesh} \u8212- the borrowing of vessels not used in food preparation. Under a generic prohibition on all benefit, one may not cut across his neighbor\u8217's yard and may not borrow anything at all: a rake, a car, anything whatsoever. But if the vow is limited to food benefit, one may cut through the yard and may borrow those categories of vessels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Permitted Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i hamudar ma\u8217'achal mechaveiro} \u8212- if one is forbidden from deriving food benefit from his friend \u8212- {\i lo yash\u8217'ilennu} \u8212- his friend should not lend him {\i nafah ve-kivar ve-reichayim} \u8212- a sieve, another type of sieve, and a millstone or mill \u8212- {\i ve-tanur} \u8212- and an oven. All of these are prohibited because, even if one has vowed only against food benefit, it clearly encompasses things that lead to food or assist in obtaining it: objects that help one make food. A plate would fall into the same category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other hand, {\i aval mash\u8217'ilo chaluk ve-tallit ve-tabba\u8217'at ve-nezamim} \u8212- one\u8217's friend may lend him a shirt, a cloak, a ring, and various other adornments. Between the forbidden category of food-preparation vessels and the permitted category of clothing, however, there remains a vast middle ground \u8212- most things in the world \u8212- whose status is not yet addressed by the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gap in Understanding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question naturally arises regarding the structure of the {\i mishnah}: the {\i seifa} \u8212- the second clause enumerating specific permitted items \u8212- appears redundant to the general phrase {\i ve-keilim she-ein osim bahem ochel nefesh}. It likely functions as a clarification of that terse {\i ein bein} formulation, which in its original context in Megilla was highly compressed. The {\i seifa} here may represent a later gloss or expansion, unpacking that concise statement with concrete examples.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vittur & Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i man tanna} \u8212- who is the author of this {\i mishnah}? {\i Amar Rav Adda bar Ahava: Rabbi Eliezer hi} \u8212- it is Rabbi Eliezer. This is demonstrated from a {\i braita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vittur Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De-tanya: Rabbi Eliezer omer, afilu vittur assur be-mudar hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- even something that is a {\i vittur} \u8212- something not normally charged for, a giveaway, a freebie, something that people simply waive \u8212- is forbidden in a case of {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah}. Even something that no one would ever assign a monetary price to, that no one normally minds about, constitutes benefit if it passes from one person to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vittur Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The example given is the baker\u8217's dozen: if I sell twelve loaves and throw in a thirteenth at no charge, that thirteenth item is a {\i vittur}. More directly relevant is the case of {\i drisas ha-regel} \u8212- cutting through someone\u8217's yard. A person generally does not mind if his neighbor occasionally takes a shortcut across his lawn; he would never charge for it and would never think twice about it. Nevertheless, under a general prohibition on benefit, even this is forbidden. And so, if Michael has taken a vow that he cannot receive benefit from me, he may not cut across my yard \u8212- even though this is precisely the sort of thing people routinely overlook and do not care about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept extends to other everyday informalities: borrowing someone\u8217's books, picking up a volume and leafing through it for a few minutes. People generally do not object. Perhaps the best translation of {\i vittur} is simply \u8220"things people don\u8217't mind about.\u8221" Nonetheless, there is something received from the other person, and that is sufficient to constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abstract Benefits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that examples involving transactions \u8212- like the baker\u8217's extra item \u8212- are a somewhat lesser {\i chiddush}, because in those cases something concrete is being transferred as part of an existing commercial exchange. The more striking point is that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling extends to entirely abstract benefits: things that normally carry no monetary value and that people would never think to withhold. Even those, under {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah}, are forbidden. The ruling thus establishes that {\i hana\u8217'ah} is not defined by economic value or normal social practice, but by the bare fact of receiving something from another person.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hana\u8217'ah Leads to Food\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sieve Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We turn now to the {\i gemara}\u8217's central analytical challenge: {\i veha mi ma\u8217'achal nadar} \u8212- you only forbade food. So why is it forbidden to borrow the sieve? A sieve is not food. If the vow was limited to food benefit, on what basis do we prohibit borrowing vessels used in food preparation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Shimon Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Shimon ben Levi: be\u8217'omer hana\u8217'as ma\u8217'achalcha alai} \u8212- the formulation is not simply \u8220"your food is forbidden to me\u8221" but rather \u8220"the benefit of your food is forbidden to me.\u8221" This subtly shifts the scope: not just eating the food, but deriving benefit from it. What would benefit from food look like? One could not use a cucumber to scratch one\u8217's back; one could not take wheat, chew it without swallowing, and apply it as a poultice to a wound. These are forms of benefit from the food itself that go beyond eating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos notes that {\i hana\u8217'as ma\u8217'achalcha} might even encompass causing food to serve as the vehicle for benefit \u8212- not just using food directly but participating in a process by which food is made useful in some way. However, even with this expansion, the formulation does not obviously reach the sieve: borrowing a sieve is not benefit {\i from} food in any sense; it is benefit that helps one {\i process} food. The problem therefore remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore advances a broader interpretation. {\i Amar Rava: be\u8217'omer hana\u8217'ah hamevia lidei ma\u8217'achalcha alai} \u8212- I have forbidden myself any benefit that brings about \u8212- that leads to \u8212- food-related benefit. Accordingly, borrowing vessels that assist in the preparation of food is prohibited, because in doing so one is receiving something from the other person that ultimately translates into food benefit. The sieve helps one sift flour; the millstone grinds grain; the oven bakes bread. Each is a link in the chain from {\i hana\u8217'ah} to {\i ma\u8217'achal}. On this reading, any benefit received that constitutes a practical step toward food benefit falls within the scope of the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monetary Savings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle \u8212- {\i hana\u8217'ah hamevia lidei ma\u8217'achal} \u8212- also explains why the gap between generic {\i hana\u8217'ah} and food {\i hana\u8217'ah} is so narrow in practice. A subsequent {\i mishnah} sheds light on this: {\i vechol davar she\u8217'ein osim bahem ochel nefesh, makom shemashkirin kiyotze bahem, assur} \u8212- any item not used for food preparation, if such items are normally rented out, is also forbidden. The logic is as follows: if Michael\u8217's neighbor normally rents out his donkey and Michael says to him, \u8220"Sure, go ahead, use it, no charge,\u8221" then by not charging Michael, he has saved Michael money. And money saved is money that can be used to purchase food. Thus, monetary benefit \u8212- any concrete financial saving \u8212- is subsumed under food benefit, because money is the most direct path to food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The picture that emerges is this: food benefit encompasses two categories. First, things that practically or directly contribute to the preparation or obtaining of food. Second, things that are normally rented \u8212- that is, things whose free provision saves money, which in turn can be used to acquire food. Anything that is neither used in food preparation nor normally rented \u8212- a category like cutting through one\u8217's yard or borrowing a book no one would ever charge for \u8212- remains outside the prohibition when the vow is limited to food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This broader discussion also reflects a principle noted elsewhere in the {\i gemara}: the reason that the word {\i lo yochel} \u8212- do not eat \u8212- is understood, as Rashi explains, to encompass all forms of {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} is that virtually any benefit one receives can ultimately be converted into food. Food is the most fundamental category of human need, and therefore a prohibition on food benefit is inherently expansive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Pappa Expansion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transportation Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Pappa}: the category of food-related benefit includes not only objects used in the preparation of food but also objects used for the transportation of food. A sack to carry fruit \u8212- {\i sak lavei peirot} \u8212- or a donkey to transport produce \u8212- {\i chamor lehavi alav peirot} \u8212- are both included. A shopping cart would be the modern equivalent. Even {\i tzana be\u8217'alma} \u8212- a simple basket \u8212- falls under this category if used for the transport of food, because transporting food is part of the chain of benefit that leads to {\i ma\u8217'achal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Queries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rav Pappa raises a series of further questions. {\i Sus lirkov alav} \u8212- a horse to ride on \u8212- {\i ve-tabba\u8217'at lera\u8217'ot bah} \u8212- a signet ring to display \u8212- {\i ve-mifsaya umizal be\u8217'armaya} \u8212- and cutting through someone\u8217's yard: what is the ruling in each of these cases?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Getting to Food\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key distinction Rav Pappa is probing is the difference between objects that bring food to me or help me prepare food, and objects or situations that bring {\i me} to food. These cases are not about the food itself or its preparation; they are about facilitating my journey toward food. The yard example is perhaps the clearest: if I cut through your yard in order to get to a neighbor\u8217's barbecue, that does seem to be a {\i hana\u8217'ah hamevia lidei ma\u8217'achal} \u8212- but it is less directly connected to the food itself and more about my movement toward it. The horse is similar: borrowing a car to get to a dinner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clothing & Appearance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The signet ring is more unusual. The {\i rishonim} explain that if one borrows impressive clothing or a signet ring and arrives at a meal looking important and distinguished, the hosts may seat him in a better section and serve him better food. So the ring or garment, by affecting one\u8217's appearance, can indirectly determine the quality of the food one receives. Is that {\i hana\u8217'ah hamevia lidei ma\u8217'achal}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inconclusive Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to resolve the question from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's {\i seifa}, which permits lending clothing, rings, and the like. But the {\i gemara} responds: {\i eilima shelo lera\u8217'ot bahen} \u8212- perhaps the {\i mishnah} is discussing the ordinary case where the clothing is not being used to appear important, in which case {\i tzricha lemeimar} \u8212- of course it is permitted, since there is no food-related dimension at all. The {\i mishnah} need not be addressing the specific case of borrowing clothes to look distinguished at a meal. The {\i gemara} concludes {\i lo \u8212- leolam shelo lera\u8217'ot} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} is indeed speaking of the ordinary case, and the {\i seifa} only follows naturally from the {\i reisha}, without intending to resolve the harder question. The question of benefits that help one {\i get to} food or present oneself advantageously at a meal remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Items Normally Rented\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monetary Substitution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i mishnah} explains why the practical gap between generic {\i hana\u8217'ah} and food {\i hana\u8217'ah} is so narrow: any item that is normally rented out effectively constitutes monetary benefit when lent for free, and monetary benefit translates into food benefit. This is the mechanism by which the scope of food {\i hana\u8217'ah} expands far beyond what one might initially expect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashkirin Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mekom shemashkirin kiyotze bahen} \u8212- in a place where such items are rented out. The {\i gemara} raises a question about exactly what this criterion means. Does {\i mashkirin kiyotze bahen} require that people regularly rent this specific item on a personal, neighborly basis? Or does it mean that when one cannot borrow the item from a friend, one would have to rent it elsewhere \u8212- so the category is defined by the availability of a commercial alternative?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Car Rental Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The modern equivalent clarifies the principle well. Suppose Michael cannot get food benefit from his neighbor, and the neighbor says, \u8220"Of course, borrow my car, no problem.\u8221" Even if neighbors routinely lend each other cars without any thought of charging, the fact remains: had the neighbor been unable to lend it, Michael would have had to go to a rental agency and pay. By lending the car, the neighbor has saved Michael money \u8212- money that could be used to buy food. That savings constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah}, even if no one would ever have dreamed of charging in this particular social context. The point is not whether this neighbor {\i would} have charged, but whether the object is the sort of thing that can be and is rented commercially. If so, the free loan constitutes a monetary benefit, which in turn falls under food benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution to Rabbi Eli\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i michlal de-reisha af al pi she\u8217'ein maskirin} \u8212- from the earlier clause, which deals with things like sieves and millstones, it appears that even items not normally rented are forbidden when they contribute to food preparation. This is true even though no one charges for the use of such items. {\i Man tanna hu?} \u8212- who holds this position? {\i Amar Rabba bar bar Chana: Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- as established earlier, it is Rabbi Eliezer, consistent with his ruling that even {\i vittur} \u8212- things normally overlooked and not charged for \u8212- constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah}. Even if no one in the world would charge to lend a sieve to a neighbor, if it contributes to food preparation it is forbidden. Perhaps the deeper principle here is: the question of whether people normally care is irrelevant. Whatever one receives from another person, even something routinely given for free, constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah} in a legal sense if it falls within the prohibited domain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mudar Hana\u8217'ah Paradoxes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Permitted Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now shifts to {\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro} \u8212- one who is entirely forbidden from deriving benefit from his friend \u8212- and enumerates three things that are nonetheless permitted. Each of these is, on its surface, a substantial benefit, and understanding why each is permitted requires careful analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temple Tax\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shokel et shiklo} \u8212- one may pay the other\u8217's {\i shekel} obligation to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. On the surface, this appears to be a very real benefit: someone else is fulfilling my financial obligation to the Temple on my behalf. Why is this not {\i hana\u8217'ah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paying Off Debts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Poreia et chovo} \u8212- one may pay off the other\u8217's debts. This is even more striking. If Michael owes a million dollars to a creditor and his neighbor pays it off, Michael\u8217's net worth has just increased by a million dollars. His liabilities column has been entirely erased. One would think this is among the most direct and significant forms of benefit imaginable. Yet the {\i mishnah} permits it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Umachzir lo et aveidato} \u8212- one may return his lost object to him. This third case is the most intuitively understandable: the finder is simply giving the owner back something that was already his. The finder performs a {\i mitzvah} of {\i hashavat aveidah}, and the owner receives his own property. Whatever service was rendered, in terms of what the owner actually receives, he receives only his own thing. One might wonder whether the gratitude or relief experienced constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah}, but in terms of the actual transfer of property, no new benefit has been conferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment for Return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further nuance arises in cases where one would normally pay the finder for his time and effort \u8212- {\i makom she-notnu aleha sechar} \u8212- because the finder has taken time away from his regular work. In such cases, {\i tipol hana\u8217'ah le-hekdesh} \u8212- that {\i hana\u8217'ah} goes to {\i hekdesh}. Why must it go to {\i hekdesh}? The scenario is as follows: Reuven cannot get benefit from Shimon. Shimon finds Reuven\u8217's wallet and returns it \u8212- so far, so good: Reuven is only getting back his own property. Reuven wants to give Shimon twenty dollars for his trouble. Shimon says, \u8220"Forget it, keep your money.\u8221" Now Reuven is in a bind: if Shimon waives the payment, and Reuven keeps the twenty dollars that he was prepared to give, he is now receiving something from Shimon \u8212- namely, Shimon\u8217's waiver of the fee. That becomes {\i hana\u8217'ah}. So the money must go to {\i tzedakah}, and Reuven cannot simply pocket it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scaring Away Lion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditors as Lions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} provides a striking conceptualization of why paying off someone\u8217's debt is not considered conferring {\i hana\u8217'ah} upon him. {\i Avruchei ari be\u8217'alma hu ve-shari} \u8212- it is merely the scaring away of a lion, and therefore it is permissible. The image is of creditors as a lion bearing down on the debtor; paying them off does not give the debtor anything \u8212- it merely prevents an external force from taking something away from him. One is not providing a positive benefit; one is removing a threat. The lion metaphor is, admittedly, somewhat strained: a lion represents an undeserved, unjust attack, whereas a creditor has a legitimate claim. But the structural logic holds: one is not giving the debtor anything directly; one is pushing away an external claim. A model of {\i grama} \u8212- indirect causation \u8212- might have served equally well: you are not directly giving the debtor something good, but removing something bad.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanan Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Oshaya: zu divrei Chanan} \u8212- this ruling reflects the opinion of Chanan. The {\i gemara} identifies the position with Chanan, a figure whose ruling appears in a {\i mishnah} in Ketubot. The case: a man went on a business trip without making any financial provision for his wife. A neighbor \u8212- Chanan \u8212- stepped in and provided money so the wife could purchase food, then sought reimbursement from the husband upon his return. The husband refused. Chanan ruled: {\i ibbad et me\u8217'otav} \u8212- the neighbor lost his money. Because no explicit stipulation was made with the husband, the neighbor was simply paying off the husband\u8217's creditor \u8212- his wife, who was owed maintenance. Since there was no agreement for repayment, and since paying off a creditor does not constitute concretely giving anything to the debtor, the neighbor cannot collect. The {\i benei kohanim gedolim} disagreed: {\i yishava kamma hotzi ve-yitol} \u8212- the neighbor may take an oath as to what he spent and collect accordingly. This is the debate about {\i mavriach ari} \u8212- whether removing a creditor\u8217's claim is or is not considered a genuine benefit to the debtor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava amar: afilu teima divrei hakol} \u8212- Rava argues that even the Rabbis who disagree with Chanan and hold that the neighbor can collect in that case would nonetheless agree that the {\i mishnah} here is permissible. He explains: {\i de-yahiv al menat shelo lifro\u8217'a} \u8212- the case in the {\i mishnah} involves a debt structured such that repayment could never be legally enforced. It is a loan in the sense that there is a personal moral obligation, but the creditor explicitly stipulated that he would never demand repayment in {\i beit din}. In such a case, even if paying off a real, enforceable debt would constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah}, paying off a non-enforceable debt would not \u8212- because it never appeared in the debtor\u8217's liabilities column in any legally meaningful sense. Shimon\u8217's payment freed Reuven from a moral weight, perhaps from social embarrassment, but never from any real financial liability. Accordingly, even the Rabbis would agree it is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}. This reading, however, is an {\i okimta} \u8212- a forced narrowing of the {\i mishnah} to a special case \u8212- and does not represent the straightforward {\i pshat} of the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deer\u8217's Horn Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then quotes the full {\i mishnah} from Ketubot. {\i Amar Rabbi Dosa ben Horkinas ke-divrehem} \u8212- Rabbi Dosa ben Horkinas sided with the {\i benei kohanim gedolim} that the neighbor can collect. {\i Amar Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai: yafeh amar Chanan} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai sided with Chanan. {\i Henniach me\u8217'otav al keren ha-tzvi} \u8212- the neighbor placed his money on the horn of a deer; he flushed it away. Since no stipulation was made with the husband, and since the act of paying a creditor is not seen as directly giving anything to the debtor, the neighbor cannot claim reimbursement. And by the same logic, it would not be prohibited by {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hoshaya Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava lo amar ke-Rav Hoshaya} \u8212- Rava did not follow Rav Hoshaya\u8217's approach. Rava preferred to read the {\i mishnah} as acceptable to all opinions \u8212- both Chanan and the Rabbis \u8212- by limiting it to the special case of a non-enforceable debt. Rav Hoshaya, on the other hand, would not accept this {\i okimta}, for two reasons. First, it requires an uncomfortable narrowing of the {\i mishnah}. Second, and more importantly: {\i gezerah shelo lifro\u8217'a mishum lifro\u8217'a} \u8212- even if we were to say that paying off a non-enforceable debt is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, if we permit Shimon to pay off Reuven\u8217's non-enforceable debts, people will come to permit him to pay off Reuven\u8217's real, enforceable debts as well. As a protective {\i gezerah}, one would prohibit even the marginal case to prevent violation of the clearer case. Rav Hoshaya therefore maintains that the {\i mishnah} follows Chanan: paying off any creditor, real or nominal, is simply {\i mavriach ari}, and that is why it is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pshat} of the {\i mishnah} clearly aligns with Rav Hoshaya: Chanan\u8217's position is that {\i mavriach ari} is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, and the {\i mishnah} can be read straightforwardly on that basis without requiring any special limiting case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Returning Lost Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami & Rav Assi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses the third case in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i machzir et aveidato}, returning a lost object \u8212- and reports a debate between Rav Ami and Rav Assi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own Property Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chad amar: lo shanu ela be-she-nichsei machazir asurin al ba\u8217'al aveidah} \u8212- one opinion holds that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's permission for the finder to return the lost object applies only when the finder\u8217's property is what is forbidden to the owner. That is, the owner cannot get benefit from the finder. In that case, {\i d\u8217'chi mahadar lei midam d\u8217'nafshei ka-mahadar lei} \u8212- when the finder returns the object, he is simply returning to the owner something that was already his own property. No new benefit passes from finder to owner; the owner merely receives back what was already his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Irony of Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval nichsei ba\u8217'al aveidah asurin al machazir, lo ka-mahadar lei} \u8212- but here is the irony. What if the prohibition runs in the other direction: Shimon cannot get benefit from Reuven, and it is Shimon who has found Reuven\u8217's object? One would intuitively think this is entirely unproblematic \u8212- Shimon is giving {\i to} Reuven; Shimon is not getting anything. Why would that be a problem? The answer is surprising.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pruta of Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De-ka-mahanei lei pruta de-Rav Yosef} \u8212- because Shimon is in fact getting benefit from Reuven, in the form of the {\i prutah} of Rav Yosef. The background: in Bava Metzia, there is a question about what level of {\i shomer} \u8212- guardian \u8212- the finder of a lost object is considered. Ordinarily one might say {\i shomer chinam} \u8212- an unpaid watchman \u8212- since no one is paying him to hold it. Rav Yosef, however, ruled that the finder is a {\i shomer sachar} \u8212- a paid watchman with a higher level of liability. The reason is that {\i osek be-mitzvah patur min ha-mitzvah} \u8212- one who is engaged in performing a {\i mitzvah} is exempt from other {\i mitzvos} that present themselves in the meantime. Therefore, if a {\i schnorrer} \u8212- a collector for charity \u8212- were to approach Shimon while Shimon is in the process of returning the lost object, Shimon would be exempt from giving {\i tzedakah} in that moment. That exemption \u8212- that saved dollar \u8212- is the {\i prutah} of Rav Yosef. It is a concrete benefit Shimon receives in the course of fulfilling the {\i mitzvah} of {\i hashavat aveidah}. And since it is Reuven\u8217's object that creates the occasion for this benefit, Shimon is, in effect, deriving benefit from Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The striking result: paying off a million-dollar debt is considered {\i mavriach ari} and not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, because the benefit is indirect. But the theoretical possibility of saving a single dollar in charity is considered {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- because it is a concrete, direct benefit that accrues to the finder in the course of handling the owner\u8217's object. The contrast is remarkable, and it speaks to the precision with which the {\i gemara} defines {\i hana\u8217'ah}: not by magnitude, but by directness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rarity of Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second opinion in the debate \u8212- {\i ve-chad amar afilu nichsei ba\u8217'al aveidah asurin al ha-machzir, mahadar lei} \u8212- holds that even when Shimon cannot get benefit from Reuven, Shimon may still return the lost object. {\i U-mishum pruta de-Rav Yosef lo shechiach} \u8212- the scenario of the {\i prutah} of Rav Yosef is simply too rare to take into account. The likelihood that a {\i tzedakah} collector will actually appear while Shimon is in the process of returning the object is low enough that we do not treat the act of returning the {\i aveidah} as inherently one that generates benefit. If it happens that a collector does appear and Shimon saves a dollar, that is a contingent outcome of a rare event \u8212- not a defining characteristic of the act itself. And therefore, in principle, returning the {\i aveidah} is not considered an act by which Shimon derives {\i hana\u8217'ah} from Reuven, and it remains permissible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 36\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'ila & Konamot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'ila Paths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 36, and we pick up in the middle of 35b. The previous session covered rich and significant ground, in particular the whole issue of {\i me\u8217'ila} by {\i konamot}: whether the concept of trespass applies when something is forbidden by a vow. {\i Konam} is especially susceptible to this analysis because it borrows from the language of {\i korban}, and when the object is forbidden to everyone \u8212- not merely to one individual \u8212- it resembles consecrated property even more closely. According to at least Rabbi Meir, there is a {\i me\u8217'ila} concept even when the prohibition applies only to particular individuals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'ila as Gezeilah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second point from the previous discussion deserves brief appreciation. When we say there is a concept of {\i me\u8217'ila}, there are generally two distinct paths by which a person commits {\i me\u8217'ila}: one is by deriving {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- benefit or pleasure \u8212- from something that is sanctified, and the other is by appropriating it, taking it for oneself or transferring it to another. The question in the study of {\i me\u8217'ila} is always whether these are two separate paths or whether they ultimately reduce to one. Reb Chaim holds that everything is a form of {\i gezeilah}: to take benefit from something that is not yours is in essence to steal its use. Alternatively, one could say everything is an expression of {\i hana\u8217'ah}, and that appropriating an object is itself a mode of benefiting from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What was particularly striking in the previous session is the application of {\i me\u8217'ila} in the sense of appropriation \u8212- not mere benefit \u8212- to the realm of {\i nedarim}. If I declare this {\i kikar} of bread {\i konam} to me, and I then give it to a poor person, I have committed {\i me\u8217'ila}, even though I derived no benefit whatsoever from that act. The implication is that a vow, by changing the status of an object, makes it as though it belongs to some entity, such that the very act of taking possession \u8212- giving it away, transferring it \u8212- constitutes a form of theft. That is a significant {\i chiddush}, extending the concept of {\i me\u8217'ila} well beyond the ordinary notion of deriving pleasure from forbidden property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defining Indirect Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that context established, we turn to today\u8217's {\i mishna}, which continues the theme of narrowly defining what counts as {\i hana\u8217'ah}. When I am forbidden from deriving benefit from a particular person, what types of activities performed by that person are considered direct benefit and which are merely indirect? The {\i mishna} already taught that the person may pay off my creditors \u8212- a potentially enormous financial service \u8212- and yet that is not considered {\i hana\u8217'ah}. Similarly, that person may return my lost object, since the object was mine to begin with and the person is merely performing a {\i mitzvah} act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Services\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues with several further examples. {\i Hatoreim trumato umasrotav leda\u8217'ato} \u8212- the person may separate my {\i trumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} for me with my knowledge. Who exactly \u8220"the person\u8221" is and whether they are using their own grain or mine will be addressed in the {\i gemara}, but the basic question is whether this constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah}. The {\i mishna} then states that a {\i kohen} may bring for me the bird offerings of a {\i zav}, a {\i zavah}, a {\i yoledet}, and the animal offerings of {\i chataot} and {\i ashamot}. And further: {\i umelamdo midrash halachot ve\u8217'agadot} \u8212- he may also teach me {\i midrash}, {\i halachot}, and {\i aggadot}, even if I am prohibited from receiving benefit from him. However, he may not teach me Scripture \u8212- {\i mikra} \u8212- directly; though he may teach {\i mikra} to my sons and daughters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvos Lo\u8217'av L\u8217'Henos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The organizing principle here is the concept of {\i mitzvos lav l\u8217'henos nitnu}: {\i mitzvos} are not considered a form of {\i hana\u8217'ah}. This principle is sometimes framed theologically, with Rashi in several places noting that {\i mitzvos} are meant to be a burden and a responsibility rather than a benefit \u8212- though that framing stands in tension with the well-known statement of Rabbi Chananya ben Akashya that {\i retza Hakadosh Baruch Hu l\u8217'zakos es Yisrael, lifikach hirba lahem Torah u\u8217'mitzvos} \u8212- God wished to grant merit to Israel and therefore multiplied Torah and {\i mitzvos} for them. And indeed, whenever one recites {\i asher kideshanu b\u8217'mitzvosav v\u8217'tzivanu}, one is presumably affirming that {\i mitzvos} are an opportunity and a privilege, not merely a burden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halachic} dimension of the principle, however, is distinct from the theological one. The real point is that {\i halacha} measures benefit exclusively in physical, real-world terms: financial gain or bodily pleasure. Even the most significant spiritual acquisitions fall outside this definition. Teaching someone {\i Torah} is a case worth examining carefully, since unlike returning a lost object \u8212- which is a service that leaves the recipient exactly where they were \u8212- Torah instruction actually imparts something. The recipient now possesses knowledge they did not have before. This is not a mere service; it is a transfer of something substantive. And yet it is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, because what was transferred is not physical and not monetary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical vs.\u160 ?Spiritual B\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same logic applies beyond the context of {\i mitzvos}. One is forbidden to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} from a corpse, yet one may use a corpse for anatomical dissection and the study of medicine. The benefit there is enormous \u8212- the acquisition of medical skill and knowledge \u8212- and yet it is not {\i halachically} considered {\i hana\u8217'ah}, because it is neither physical pleasure nor monetary gain. The {\i halacha} operates with a thoroughly this-worldly conception of benefit. Thus when the {\i mishna} permits returning a lost object, separating {\i trumos u\u8217'maasros}, and bringing {\i korbanos}, the underlying logic is the same: to the degree that all of these are the performance of a {\i mitzvah}, the service rendered is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, because {\i mitzvah} performance as such is not a legally cognizable form of benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might raise the question of a case in which the {\i mitzvah} act also produces a financial saving \u8212- for instance, if I am forbidden {\i hana\u8217'ah} from certain candles, and it is Chanukah, and using those candles spares me the cost of purchasing others. In such a case, the benefit I derive is not purely the {\i mitzvah} itself but a financial saving \u8212- and yet the financial saving is only relevant because I needed to perform the {\i mitzvah}. Whether that constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah} is a genuine question left open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohanim\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agency Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a fundamental question: {\i hani kohanei shluchei didan havu o shluchei d\u8217'Rachmana havu} \u8212- are the {\i kohanim} in the {\i Beit HaMikdash} considered to be performing our service, representing {\i Klal Yisrael}, or are they serving God? This is not merely a philosophical question; it bears directly on our {\i mishna}. When I bring my {\i korban}, at what point have I transferred it to God\u8217's domain? If the {\i kohen} represents me and acts on my behalf, then he is doing something for me up until the moment the offering reaches the {\i Mizbe\u8217'ach}. If, on the other hand, the {\i kohen} is God\u8217's representative \u8212- part of God\u8217's domain in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, so to speak \u8212- then once I hand over the animal, I have discharged my obligation and the {\i kohen} is no longer acting on my behalf at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that elsewhere it concludes that {\i kohanim} are {\i shluchei Deshmaya} \u8212- God\u8217's emissaries \u8212- for a compelling structural reason: a person cannot appoint an agent for an act they themselves are not permitted to perform. Since a non-{\i kohen} cannot perform the {\i avodah}, the {\i kohen} cannot logically be seen as that person\u8217's agent. He must therefore be understood as God\u8217's representative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agency & Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence for our topic is as follows. Even if the act of bringing a {\i korban} is a {\i mitzvah} and would not in itself constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah}, there is a separate concern: being appointed as someone\u8217's direct agent and representative \u8212- regardless of the specific task involved \u8212- is itself a form of {\i hana\u8217'ah}. If I appoint you to throw a rock into a river, even though throwing a rock into a river is not in itself any benefit, the very act of serving as my appointed {\i shaliach} and acting on my behalf constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah}. Therefore, if the {\i kohen} is my {\i shliach}, his bringing of a {\i korban} for me \u8212- even though the bringing of a {\i korban} might otherwise be categorized as a {\i mitzvah} act \u8212- would constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah} by virtue of the agency relationship itself. Hence the {\i gemara}\u8217's question: how does the {\i mishna} permit this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechusrei Kapara Excepti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes an answer from the {\i mishna} itself. The {\i mishna} does not say generically that the {\i kohen} may bring any {\i korban} for me; it specifically lists the {\i kinei zavim}, {\i kinei zavot}, {\i kinei yoldot}, {\i chataot}, and {\i ashamot}. The {\i gemara} asks: if a {\i kohen} could bring any {\i korban} for me without restriction, why would the {\i mishna} specify only these categories? {\i Ela mechussar kapara sha\u8217'ani} \u8212- the case of {\i mechusrei kapara} is categorically different. These are individuals for whom a {\i korban} is brought not to atone for a sin in the ordinary sense but to effect a transition from a state of {\i tumah} to {\i tahara} \u8212- to cleanse and change their ritual status. It is not the person\u8217's act of connection to God; it is a ritual procedure performed upon them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rab Yochanan\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is given a precise formulation by Rabbi Yochanan: {\i hakol tzrichin da\u8217'at chutz mimechusrei kaparah} \u8212- all {\i korbanos} require the awareness and knowledge of the person on whose behalf they are brought, with the sole exception of {\i mechusrei kapara}. The proof: {\i sheharei adam meivi korban al banav u-venosav ha-ketanim}, a father can bring a {\i korban} for his minor children, as derived from the verse {\i zot toras ha-zav, bein gadol bein katan} \u8212- the word {\i toras} is understood to include both adults and minors. A minor cannot meaningfully appoint an agent or give {\i da\u8217'at}, yet the {\i korban} is valid. This establishes that the category of {\i mechusrei kapara} does not require the awareness of the person being cleansed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Types of Korbanot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction Rabbi Yochanan draws has a deep conceptual basis. The familiar teaching that {\i korban} derives from {\i karev} \u8212- drawing close to God \u8212- captures the nature of most offerings: the {\i olah}, the {\i shelamim}, the ordinary {\i chatat} brought for a sin, all involve the person in a relationship of connection, confession, and atonement. {\i Chazal} understand the normal process of {\i teshuva} as involving {\i viduy}, remorse, and engagement with the act of atonement. In all these cases, the person must be present and aware because the {\i korban} is an expression of their inner state and their relationship with God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zav & Zavah Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the verse {\i zot toras ha-zav, bein gadol bein katan}, we learn that for {\i mechusrei kapara} \u8212- a {\i zav}, a {\i zavah}, a {\i yoledet} \u8212- a {\i korban} can be brought even for a minor who cannot give informed consent or appoint a representative. This is a strong proof that {\i da\u8217'at} is not required, since the {\i halacha} explicitly includes the case of a {\i katan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karev vs.\u160 ?Cleansing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast being drawn is between two fundamentally different types of {\i korban}. One type is an act of {\i karev} \u8212- drawing close \u8212- through which the person connects to God in a metaphysical and religious sense. The other type, exemplified by {\i mechusrei kapara}, functions as an instrument of ritual cleansing and change of status. Just as the sprinkling of the ashes of a {\i parah adumah} removes {\i tum\u8217'at meis} not through any act of the person but through a ritual applied to them, so too the {\i korban} of a {\i mechusrei kapara} \u8212- whether for a {\i zav}, {\i zavah}, {\i yoledet}, or {\i metzora} \u8212- does not represent the person\u8217's reaching toward God. It is a procedure done to the person, not by the person. Accordingly, it does not require the person\u8217's knowledge. They may be completely oblivious to the fact that the {\i korban} is being brought, and it will still be effective in transitioning them from {\i tumah} to {\i tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the discussion back to the question of {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah}. Even if we accept that {\i kohanim} are in principle {\i shluchei didan} \u8212- our representatives \u8212- in the bringing of {\i korbanos}, the {\i mishna} can still permit it in these specific cases because {\i mechusrei kapara korbanos} by their very nature do not require the person to appoint a representative. The {\i kohen} is not acting as my {\i shliach}; the {\i korban} is simply a ritual being done for my benefit that does not require any act of representation on my part. Therefore, even an individual from whom I am forbidden to receive benefit may arrange for this type of {\i korban} on my behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that a general {\i da\u8217'at} suffices where {\i da\u8217'at} is required; the person does not need to know precisely which {\i kohen} is performing the service or at exactly what moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exception Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Shimi Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the principle, the {\i gemara} continues to probe its limits. Rav Shimi bar Abba raises a challenge from a {\i braisa} which states: {\i im haya Kohen, yizrok alav dam chataso vedam ashamo} \u8212- if the person from whom I cannot receive benefit is a {\i kohen}, he may sprinkle for me the blood of my {\i chatat} and my {\i asham}. This sounds like a {\i kohen} can bring any {\i korban} for me without restriction, which would suggest that {\i kohanim} are not my representatives at all and the concern about {\i hana\u8217'ah} through agency never arises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects this challenge. The {\i braisa}\u8217's reference to {\i chatat} and {\i asham} need not be understood as referring to all {\i chataot} and {\i ashamot}; it may specifically mean {\i dam chataso shel metzora vedam ashamo shel metzora} \u8212- the {\i chatat} and {\i asham} brought for the {\i metzora}, which are themselves cases of {\i mechusrei kapara}. This is confirmed by the verse {\i zot tihye toras hametzora, bein gadol bein katan} \u8212- here too the word {\i toras} indicates the inclusion of a minor, reinforcing the same principle. Thus the {\i braisa} does not establish that a {\i kohen} can bring any {\i korban} on my behalf; it remains consistent with the view that the only exception to the {\i da\u8217'at} requirement \u8212- and the only case where the agency concern is eliminated \u8212- is {\i mechusrei kapara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pigul Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pigul Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to another attempted proof, this time from the laws of {\i pigul}. The {\i mishna} teaches: {\i hakohanim shepiglu be-mikdash} \u8212- when {\i kohanim} in the {\i Beit HaMikdash} render a {\i korban} invalid by forming an intent to consume it beyond its proper time, {\i b\u8217'mezidin chayavin} \u8212- if they did so willfully, they are liable for damages, having knowingly destroyed the owner\u8217's animal. However, {\i hashogegin} \u8212- if they did so unintentionally, perhaps because they mistook a one-day {\i korban} for a two-day one \u8212- {\i ella shepigulan pigul}: their {\i pigul} is nonetheless effective and the {\i korban} is invalid, even though they are exempt from damages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pigul Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why is the {\i pigul} effective even when unintentional? {\i Iy amarta bishlama shluchei dish\u8217'maya havei} \u8212- if the {\i kohanim} are seen as God\u8217's representatives, the answer is straightforward. They are physically performing an act upon the {\i korban} \u8212- slaughtering it, performing the service \u8212- and their intent in that moment defines the identity of the act, even if the intent was mistaken. As God\u8217's representatives engaged in a physical transaction with the {\i korban}, their thought can render it {\i pigul} regardless of whether it was deliberate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pigul Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i ella iy amarta shluchei didan havu} \u8212- if the {\i kohanim} are our representatives, a difficulty arises. When a {\i shaliach} makes an honest mistake while acting within the scope of the agency, the principal can say: {\i shlicha shavisach litkuni velo l\u8217'avusi} \u8212- I appointed you as my agent for my benefit, not to do something that harms me. An agent who inadvertently acts outside the parameters of his appointment \u8212- not because he went rogue but because he made an honest error \u8212- is arguably not acting within the valid scope of his {\i shlichus} at that moment. If so, neither the principal nor the agent would have intended for the {\i pigul} intent to be operative, and the {\i korban} should not be {\i pigul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran addresses this difficulty. When a {\i kohen} deliberately forms an intent to eat the {\i korban} at the wrong time, knowing what he is doing, he has unquestionably gone beyond the scope of his appointment and is acting on his own volition, not as the owner\u8217's representative. In that moment he is simply a {\i kohen} with physical access to the animal, and his unauthorized act damages the owner\u8217's property \u8212- this is the case of willful {\i pigul}, and the {\i kohen} is liable. The problematic case is {\i beshogeg}: if he is sincerely trying to act within his mandate but makes an honest mistake, can that mistake produce valid {\i pigul}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers a resolution: {\i amar Yitzchak, shani gabei pigul de\u8217'amar kra lo yeichashev lo, mikol makom} \u8212- perhaps the verse\u8217's additional language \u8220"it shall not be considered for him\u8221" is itself expanding the effect of {\i pigul} intent beyond what the ordinary rules of {\i shlichus} would produce. That is, even if an argument from {\i shlichus} theory could have led us to conclude that an unintentional {\i pigul} is invalid, the verse overrides that conclusion and renders it effective regardless. The {\i gemara} thus leaves the fundamental question unresolved: it remains possible to maintain that {\i kohanim} are in principle {\i shluchei didan}, with {\i pigul beshogeg} being a special case handled by the verse; and the reason the {\i mishna} permits the {\i mudar} to receive their service is the narrow, specific exception of {\i mechusrei kapara}. As noted, other {\i sugyot} in the {\i gemara} ultimately conclude that {\i kohanim} are to be regarded as {\i shluchei Deshmaya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband & Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas Cheilev\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to elaborate on Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling and explore its limits. The {\i gemara} proposes: {\i ela me\u8217'ata yavi adam chatat cheilev al chaveiro} \u8212- if the principle is that {\i mechusrei kapara} do not require {\i da\u8217'at}, why not extend this further and say one can bring a {\i chatat} for a friend without the friend\u8217's knowledge? The {\i chatat cheilev} is the paradigmatic sin offering \u8212- a {\i chatat} brought by one who inadvertently ate forbidden fat ({\i cheilev}), for which one incurs {\i karet} and therefore must bring a {\i korban} if done {\i beshogeg}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar rules explicitly: {\i hifrish chatas chelev al chaveiro lo asa klum} \u8212- one who separates a {\i chatat cheilev} on behalf of another person has accomplished nothing. A person must bring their own {\i korban chatat}; even the act of sanctifying the animal must be done by the person who sinned. This is consistent with Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling: the only exception to the {\i da\u8217'at} requirement is {\i mechusrei kapara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Shoteh State\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} however points to an apparent tension. We established that a husband can bring {\i korbanos} for his wife even when she is a {\i shoteh} \u8212- a woman not in possession of her full mental faculties. Since the husband is financially responsible for his wife\u8217's {\i korbanos}, as Rabbi Yehuda teaches \u8212- {\i adam mevi korban ashir al ishto}, a man brings a {\i korban} at the level of a wealthy person for his wife, calibrated to the husband\u8217's finances rather than the wife\u8217's \u8212- one might think this extends to all her {\i korbanos}, including a {\i chatat} for a sin. And if the husband can bring for his wife even when she is a {\i shoteh}, why can he not bring for any person without their awareness?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing on the particulars of this financial obligation. A wife in that era typically had no independent income, and even if she earned something, the {\i halakhot} of the {\i ketuva} entitled the husband to those earnings in exchange for his obligation to provide for her. If she incurred an obligation to bring a {\i korban oleh veyored} \u8212- a sliding-scale offering calibrated to financial means \u8212- she would technically be classified as destitute. The {\i braisa} rules that the scale is determined by the husband\u8217's wealth, since he bears the financial responsibility for her {\i korbanos}. This is the core {\i chiddush}: {\i adam mevi korban ashir al ishto}. The {\i gemara} notes that this obligation is referenced in the language of the {\i ketuva} or its receipt, though the precise textual source is somewhat opaque.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoteh Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction by noting that the case of a {\i shoteh} wife bringing a {\i korban} for a sin is in practice impossible. If she ate the forbidden fat while she was a {\i shoteh}, she bears no halachic liability at all \u8212- a {\i shoteh} does not have the legal standing to transgress. If she ate it while of sound mind and subsequently entered a state of {\i shoteh}, a different problem arises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 State Makes Pasul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling of Rabbi Yochanan, cited here via Rabbi Yirmiya citing Rabbi Abahu: {\i achal chelev vehifrish korban venishtata} \u8212- if someone ate forbidden fat, separated a {\i korban} in response, and then entered a state of {\i shoteh}, {\i ve-chazar venishtapa} \u8212- and later recovered, {\i pasul} \u8212- the {\i korban} is invalid. The principle is {\i ho\u8217'il venidcha yidcha}: since the {\i korban} entered a state during the {\i shoteh} period during which it could not be brought, it was in a state of being unbringable, and once a {\i korban} becomes unbringable for any period it is considered {\i pasul} and cannot subsequently be offered. The person would need to separate a new animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is that one can never practically have a case where a husband brings a sin offering for his {\i shoteh} wife because of a sin she committed. Therefore, the ruling that a husband brings {\i korbanos} for his wife \u8212- even when she is a {\i shoteh} \u8212- is limited strictly to cases of {\i mechusrei kapara}, which by their nature do not require {\i da\u8217'at} and are unaffected by the {\i nidcha} problem. The exception Rabbi Yochanan identified remains narrow and precise: only {\i mechusrei kapara} operate outside the ordinary {\i da\u8217'at} requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pesach Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the principle, the {\i gemara} now addresses an attempted proof that the exception could be extended more broadly. If a father can bring {\i korbanos} for his minor children \u8212- which is how Rabbi Yochanan proved that {\i mechusrei kapara} does not require {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- why not use that precedent as a model for all {\i korbanos}, including the {\i korban Pesach}? One should be able to bring a {\i korban Pesach} for a friend without the friend\u8217's active participation, by analogy to what a father does for his children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Ineffective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this, Rabbi Elazar rules explicitly: {\i hifrish pesach al chaveiro lo asa klum} \u8212- one who separates a {\i korban Pesach} on behalf of another has accomplished nothing. The {\i korban Pesach} requires active participation; the obligation of {\i minuyav} \u8212- being enrolled in the group \u8212- must be fulfilled by each participant. It cannot be arranged for someone by another party without their direct involvement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seh Not D\u8217'Oraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira provides the conceptual key: {\i seh l\u8217'veis avos lav d\u8217'Oraisa} \u8212- the concept of {\i seh l\u8217'veis avot}, the family unit collectively sharing in a single {\i Pesach} offering, is not a biblical requirement. Biblically, one need not actively bring the {\i korban} specifically for one\u8217's minor children. When a father brings a {\i korban Pesach} and his minor children eat from it, it is not because he has formally included them in the {\i shechita} as obligated participants; it is because minors are not themselves obligated in {\i korban Pesach} and therefore may eat from the father\u8217's offering without having been included in the {\i shechita} on their behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Son Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof comes from a {\i mishna}: {\i ha\u8217'omer l\u8217'vanav, harei ani shocheit es hapesach al mi sheya\u8217'aleh mikem rishon l\u8217'yerushalayim} \u8212- a father announces to his sons that he will bring the {\i korban Pesach} for whichever of them arrives in Jerusalem first. Once the first son enters Jerusalem \u8212- his head and the majority of his body crossing the threshold \u8212- he acquires his portion. Moreover, {\i umezakke et achiv immo} \u8212- he also conveys a portion to his brothers together with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Retroactive Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is obvious: {\i avbisra kai u-mazki lehu}? The {\i korban} was already slaughtered \u8212- it is already meat \u8212- before the other brothers were formally included. If {\i seh l\u8217'veis avot} were biblical, one would need the {\i shechita} to have been performed with each participant explicitly in mind. How can a retroactive inclusion work once the animal is already slaughtered? The answer is that it works precisely because {\i seh l\u8217'veis avot} is not {\i d\u8217'Oraita} and the minor children do not require the {\i shechita} to have been performed on their behalf. The father never needed to include them for their sake; they eat from his {\i Pesach} not because it was brought for them but because they are minors exempt from the obligation and therefore also from the restriction against eating from an offering in which one is not enrolled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the father made the announcement \u8212- to race to Jerusalem \u8212- was {\i kedei le-zarzem be-mitzvot}, to motivate his children to show alacrity in the performance of {\i mitzvos}. A {\i braisa} confirms this: {\i ma\u8217'aseh hayah}, in a story that actually occurred, the daughters outpaced the sons, and the daughters were found to be {\i zerizot} \u8212- displaying alacrity \u8212- while the sons were {\i shephelot} \u8212- rather less impressive. But nothing about this story suggests the sons were excluded from eating; it merely records that they were the slower ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minors Eat Pesach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is precise and important. The reason minor children eat from their father\u8217's {\i korban Pesach} is not that the father brings the {\i korban} on their behalf as obligated participants. They are not obligated. The {\i shechita} need not be performed with them in mind. They eat because they are minors and the restriction on eating from an offering in which one has not been enrolled does not apply to those who bear no obligation in the first place. Therefore, this case cannot serve as a precedent for bringing a {\i korban Pesach} on behalf of an adult who is fully obligated and has not been enrolled. The only category for which someone else can truly arrange a {\i korban} without the person\u8217's awareness or active participation \u8212- whether that person is a minor, a {\i shoteh}, or anyone else \u8212- remains the specific exception of {\i mechusrei kapara}, whose {\i korbanos} function as ritual cleansing rather than as the person\u8217's own act of connection and atonement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 37\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Trumah & Shlichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara begins with the {\i mishna} of {\i hatorim et trumato} \u8212- the case in which one who is forbidden to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} from a second party may nevertheless have that party separate {\i trumah} on his behalf. The reasoning is that performing a {\i mitzvah} service for someone is not classified as {\i hana\u8217'ah} in the conventional sense. But the Gemara perceives a further dimension of the {\i hana\u8217'ah} problem that requires examination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} specifies that the separation is performed {\i leda\u8217'ato} \u8212- with the awareness of the grain\u8217's owner. This qualification immediately raises the issue discussed the previous day: even a service that is not itself {\i hana\u8217'ah} can become {\i hana\u8217'ah} if the one performing it does so in the capacity of the other person\u8217's appointed {\i shaliach}. The {\i kohanim} who bring a {\i korban} on a person\u8217's behalf, for instance, are understood to be acting as that person\u8217's representative, and that representative relationship is itself classified as {\i hana\u8217'ah}. The same question now arises regarding the separation of {\i trumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Service vs.\u160 ?Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The central tension of the {\i sugya} can be stated precisely: performing a {\i mitzvah} service for someone is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, but acting as that person\u8217's formally appointed {\i shaliach} is. The Gemara must therefore locate a scenario in which the separation of {\i trumah} is valid and effective, yet does not require a direct appointment of {\i shlichut}. If such an appointment is necessary, the very act of serving as one\u8217's agent \u8212- whatever the nature of the service \u8212- constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah} and would be prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question thus sharpens: if Shimon separates {\i trumah} on Reuven\u8217's grain using Reuven\u8217's own grain, Shimon cannot act on his own initiative. The {\i Torah} confers the right to separate {\i trumah} exclusively on the {\i ba\u8217'al hakri}, the owner of the pile. Just as one cannot marry a woman on another\u8217's behalf without formal {\i shlichut}, one cannot separate another person\u8217's {\i trumah} without being appointed an agent. If Shimon therefore needs Reuven\u8217's authorization, Shimon has become Reuven\u8217's {\i shaliach} \u8212- and that is precisely what creates the {\i hana\u8217'ah} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own Grain Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a specific question: {\i hatorim mishelo al shel chaveiro} \u8212- what if Shimon separates {\i trumah} using his own grain to cover both his grain and Reuven\u8217's grain simultaneously? Here Shimon is not touching Reuven\u8217's pile at all; he is using his own grain as the {\i trumah} to exempt Reuven\u8217's stack. This is an even greater act of generosity \u8212- he saves Reuven from using any of his own produce for the {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i tzarich da\u8217'ato o lo} \u8212- does Shimon require Reuven\u8217's awareness and consent to do this? The argument for not requiring consent is {\i keivan d\u8217'zechus hu lo} \u8212- since it is objectively a benefit to Reuven, the principle of {\i zachin} may operate automatically and Shimon may proceed without permission. The counterargument is {\i mitzvah deilei} \u8212- {\i venicha lei lim\u8217'evad b\u8217'gufei}: it is Reuven\u8217's own {\i mitzvah}, and a person may prefer to spend his own money to fulfill his own {\i mitzvot} rather than have someone else do it for him, even at a cost saving. The question is therefore not whether {\i zachin} applies in principle, but whether the facts are sufficiently unambiguous to invoke it \u8212- whether the benefit clearly outweighs the loss of the {\i mitzvah} opportunity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zachin Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav} \u8212- that one may acquire something on behalf of another without that person\u8217's knowledge when it is objectively beneficial \u8212- is well-established. The question among the {\i Rishonim}, however, is what the principle is grounded in. One possibility is that {\i zachin} operates as a form of {\i shlichut}: we presume, given the obvious benefit, that the beneficiary would have wanted this done and therefore retroactively appoints the one acting on his behalf as his {\i shaliach}. The other possibility is that {\i zachin} is not {\i shlichut} at all but rather a distinct mechanism \u8212- sometimes called {\i zachin mishum yad} \u8212- whereby the one acting on another\u8217's behalf is treated as a physical extension of the beneficiary, analogous to placing an object in his yard or in his hand, and this mechanism functions specifically in the domain of {\i kinyanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The significance of this distinction is acute here. If {\i zachin} is merely a {\i yad} mechanism for transferring property, it cannot explain the {\i trumah} scenario, because here the question is not about acquiring property but about performing a religious act and serving as a representative. For {\i zachin} to permit Shimon to separate {\i trumah} on Reuven\u8217's behalf without formal appointment, {\i zachin} must work as a form of presumed {\i shlichut}. This {\i sugya} therefore becomes a central source for the position \u8212- cited by {\i Tosafot} \u8212- that {\i zchiya} operates as a form of {\i shlichut} generally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical stakes of this question are considerable. Some authorities have sought to apply {\i zachin} in certain {\i agunah} situations \u8212- for instance, where a {\i beit din} might deliver a {\i get} on a husband\u8217's behalf, treating themselves as his agents on the grounds that it is objectively beneficial for him to free his wife. If {\i zachin} is limited to the {\i yad} model and cannot create {\i shlichut}, such a mechanism is unavailable. But if the {\i sugya} here establishes that {\i zachin} can function as presumed {\i shlichut} \u8212- that wherever something is clearly to a person\u8217's benefit, another may act as his agent \u8212- the possibility gains traction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the conversion of a minor infant is classically justified through {\i zachin}: since conversion is unambiguously beneficial, the {\i beit din} may be presumed to act as the infant\u8217's agents, and the conversion takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran on Zachin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} addresses this in a concise formulation. On the opening question of {\i hatorim mishelo al shel chaveiro tzarich da\u8217'ato o lo}, he writes that {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav u\u8217'mistama havei lei kishluchi} \u8212- by operation of {\i zachin}, the actor becomes the other\u8217's {\i shaliach} automatically. {\i Tosafot} is even more explicit, stating that {\i zchiya mita\u8217'am shlichut} \u8212- {\i zachin} works on the basis of {\i shlichut}, and this is the {\i sugya} from which that principle is derived throughout the Talmud. The fact that the Gemara here is willing to apply {\i zachin} not merely in the context of acquiring property but in the context of performing a religious act and serving as another\u8217's representative demonstrates that {\i zachin} can function as genuine assumed {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hana\u8217'ah & Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual distinction the Gemara is navigating must be stated precisely. Performing a {\i mitzvah} service for someone \u8212- teaching him {\i Torah}, bringing a {\i korban} on his behalf as an independent act of generosity \u8212- is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}. The act of doing a {\i mitzvah} does not create a {\i hana\u8217'ah} relationship even when the beneficiary gains from it, because {\i mitzvot lav le-hanos nitnu} \u8212- {\i mitzvot} are not given for personal enjoyment in the conventional sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem arises specifically when the person doing the service is doing so in the capacity of an appointed representative. If I say to Shimon \u8220"be my {\i shaliach} to throw this rock into the river,\u8221" throwing a rock into a river is not in itself {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- but being appointed my agent to carry out my will is itself a form of {\i hana\u8217'ah}, regardless of what the appointed task is. The gratification of one\u8217's will through a designated representative is classified as {\i hana\u8217'ah}. This is the Gemara\u8217's operative assumption: even when the underlying act is neutral, {\i shlichut} itself is {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is what distinguishes the {\i trumah} case from other improvements. One person mowing another\u8217's lawn, for instance, would be prohibited as {\i hana\u8217'ah} simply because the pruning provides a physical benefit. But separating {\i trumah} on someone\u8217's grain, if done as a private act of service without formal appointment, is not prohibited. The difficulty is that separating {\i trumah} on someone else\u8217's grain is one of those acts that inherently requires authorization \u8212- unlike mowing a lawn, it is not something one may simply do on another\u8217's property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara considers two possible ways the {\i mishna} may be read, corresponding to two distinct scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first scenario: Shimon takes {\i trumah} from Reuven\u8217's own grain ({\i mishelo shel ba\u8217'al hakri}). In this case, the {\i mishna}\u8217's phrase {\i leda\u8217'ato} must refer to Reuven\u8217's awareness. But if Reuven must be aware of and consent to what Shimon is doing with his grain, then Shimon has been appointed a {\i shaliach}, and that is precisely the {\i hana\u8217'ah} problem. The Gemara initially considers this reading and rejects it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario: Shimon uses his own grain ({\i mishelo}) to cover Reuven\u8217's pile. Here, one might think Reuven\u8217's awareness is unnecessary, since it is objectively beneficial. But the Gemara presses: if Reuven must nonetheless be aware, has Shimon not again been made a {\i shaliach}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is to distinguish between formal {\i shlichut} and something more diffuse \u8212- the mere absence of objection. Drawing on a statement of Rava that will be quoted shortly, the Gemara frames the operative model as: {\i kol harotzeh litrom yavo veyitrom} \u8212- whoever wishes to separate {\i trumah} is welcome to do so. Reuven has not appointed any specific {\i shaliach}; he has simply expressed that he does not object to whoever wishes to act on his behalf. This differs categorically from a formal appointment. {\i Shlichut} requires direct empowerment of a designated agent; general acquiescence that anyone may act is something else. Reuven is {\i beda\u8217'ato} \u8212- aware \u8212- but not {\i bishlichuso} \u8212- not through formal agency. And since Shimon was not formally appointed, the {\i hana\u8217'ah} of carrying out Reuven\u8217's {\i shlichut} does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading may also imply that {\i trumah} separation, unlike actions such as {\i kiddushin}, does not require formal {\i shlichut} as a matter of {\i halakha}. If the owner of the pile simply does not object, the separation may be valid even in the absence of a formally constituted agency relationship \u8212- and it is this legal peculiarity of {\i trumah} that makes the {\i mishna}\u8217's case possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Trumah Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distribution Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having resolved the threshold question of how Shimon may separate {\i trumah} on Reuven\u8217's behalf without creating a formal {\i shlichut}, the Gemara turns to a second and distinct question, posed by Rabbi Yirmiyah to Rabbi Zeira: when Shimon uses his own grain ({\i mishelo}) to separate {\i trumah} that exempts Reuven\u8217's pile, {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah shel mi} \u8212- who has the right to decide which {\i kohen} receives that {\i trumah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tovas hana\u8217'ah} is the right to designate to which {\i kohen} the {\i trumah} will be given. The {\i trumah} must go to some {\i kohen}, but the choice of which {\i kohen} belongs to someone, and that choice has value \u8212- one may wish to benefit one\u8217's son-in-law, a favorite student, or a beloved community {\i kohen}. The question is whether this right belongs to Reuven, whose grain was exempted, or to Shimon, whose grain actually became the {\i trumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument for Shimon: {\i ilav peira de\u8217'ha\u8217'ich mi mitkna kirya de\u8217'hai} \u8212- if not for Shimon\u8217's grain, Reuven\u8217's pile would never have been rendered permissible. The {\i trumah} came from Shimon\u8217's property; it is his grain that became sanctified. The argument for Reuven: {\i ilav kirya de\u8217'hai lo haryan peira de\u8217'hai trumah} \u8212- without Reuven\u8217's pile of grain, Shimon\u8217's act would have accomplished nothing. Both elements were necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hana\u8217'ah Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i hana\u8217'ah} concern intrudes here in a new way. If it is determined that Reuven retains the {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- that Reuven gets to decide which {\i kohen} receives the {\i trumah} \u8212- then Shimon has given Reuven something of genuine value. Shimon\u8217's grain became the {\i trumah}, yet Reuven controls its disposition. Reuven has acquired a right over property that was never his, property whose {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} has some monetary value. This would constitute a tangible benefit flowing from Shimon to Reuven, which would be prohibited if Reuven is {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this through a {\i pasuk}. The verse states: {\i es kol tvuas zar\u8217'acha venasata} \u8212- \u8220"all the yield of your grain, and you shall give.\u8221" The {\i trumah} is defined in relation to the grain from which it was separated and of which it is a portion. The owner of the pile from which the {\i trumah} was taken is the one who \u8220"gives\u8221" it \u8212- who holds the right of designation. Since the {\i trumah} was taken to exempt Reuven\u8217's grain, it is identified with Reuven\u8217's pile. Reuven is therefore the one who gives the {\i trumah} to the {\i kohen}, and Reuven retains the {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a challenge from a {\i baraita}: {\i torim et trumato ve\u8217'et masrotav leda\u8217'ato} \u8212- someone may take {\i trumot} and {\i ma\u8217'asrot} with his awareness. If this {\i baraita} is read as Shimon taking from Reuven\u8217's grain on Reuven\u8217's grain, and if {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} belongs to Reuven as established above, then Shimon has given Reuven a benefit \u8212- not just performing the {\i mitzvah} act but actually conferring on Reuven the right of disposition over grain Reuven did not previously control. This would constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah}, rendering the entire arrangement problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own Grain Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara entertains the possibility that the {\i baraita} refers to Shimon using his own grain \u8212- the scenario in which his grain becomes the {\i trumah}. On this reading, the {\i baraita}\u8217's {\i leda\u8217'ato} would refer to Shimon\u8217's own awareness, and no formal appointment of Shimon as {\i shaliach} would be required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consent Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately declines this reading and returns to the earlier resolution. The {\i baraita} may indeed concern Shimon working with Reuven\u8217's own grain, and {\i leda\u8217'ato} refers to Reuven\u8217's awareness \u8212- but this awareness does not constitute formal {\i shlichut}. It is the model of {\i kol harotzeh litrom yavo veyitrom}: Reuven expresses general acquiescence, and whoever acts under that general permission is not a formally constituted agent. Reuven is {\i beda\u8217'ato} but not {\i bishlichuso}. This preserves the validity of the separation while avoiding the {\i hana\u8217'ah} of formal agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Abba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The section concludes with a statement of Rav Abba citing Rav Yochanan: {\i hamakdish mosif chomesh umitkaper oseh trumah uvo mishelo al she\u8217'eino shelo tovas hana\u8217'ah shelo} \u8212- the last clause being directly on point. One who separates {\i trumah} using his own grain to exempt grain that belongs to another retains the {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- it is he who designates which {\i kohen} receives the {\i trumah}. The reasoning is symmetric with the {\i pasuk}-based analysis: since it was his grain that became the {\i trumah}, he retains the rights that attach to that grain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kedusha & Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The statement of Rav Abba contains two additional clauses that illuminate the same conceptual distinction from different angles. Consider the case of Shimon who, wishing to help Reuven fulfill his obligation to bring a {\i korban chatat}, takes one of his own sheep, declares it {\i hekdesh}, and designates it as a {\i chatat} for Reuven. The sanctification itself Shimon can perform unilaterally \u8212- he can give Reuven a sanctified animal as a gift. Yesterday\u8217's discussion established that actually bringing the {\i korban} on Reuven\u8217's behalf without Reuven\u8217's awareness is problematic, but the sanctification is a separate act that Shimon may perform with his own property. Reuven then has a sanctified animal with which to fulfill his obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chomesh Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now suppose the animal develops a {\i mum} \u8212- a disqualifying blemish \u8212- and must be redeemed. The {\i halakha} requires that when the owner redeems sanctified property, he must add a {\i chomesh} \u8212- a fifth (which is actually one quarter of the new value, becoming one fifth of the total). The question is: if either Reuven or Shimon could redeem the animal, which of them must add the {\i chomesh}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba\u8217's statement answers: {\i hamakdish mosif chomesh} \u8212- the one who originally sanctified the animal adds the fifth. That is Shimon. Even though the animal is Reuven\u8217's {\i korban} and Reuven is the one seeking {\i kapparah}, the monetary ownership relationship traces back to Shimon. The animal was Shimon\u8217's property; he is the one who transferred it into {\i hekdesh}; and when it comes to the transaction of redemption \u8212- which is essentially a property matter \u8212- Shimon retains the ownership relationship that triggers the obligation to add a fifth. If Reuven redeems it, he pays the standard redemption value without the {\i chomesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temura Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The clause {\i hamitkaper oseh tmurah} addresses the reverse question. {\i Tmurah} is the prohibited but legally operative act of attempting to substitute one animal for a sanctified one \u8212- both animals then become {\i kadosh}, and the actor has violated a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}. The {\i halakha} restricts who may perform {\i tmurah}: only the {\i ba\u8217'al hakorban} \u8212- the owner of the {\i korban}, the one for whom it is being brought \u8212- can effect a {\i tmurah}. If someone else attempts it, the act is legally inert.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So who is the {\i ba\u8217'al hakorban} here? Rav Abba rules: {\i hamitkaper oseh tmurah} \u8212- Reuven, the one seeking {\i kapparah}, is the {\i ba\u8217'al hakorban}. If Reuven attempts to perform {\i tmurah}, it takes effect (and he has violated a prohibition). If Shimon attempts it, nothing happens. This is because {\i tmurah} is not a property transaction; it is an act grounded in the religious identity of the {\i korban} \u8212- whose atonement it is, for whom it is being brought. From that perspective, the animal belongs to Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual distinction that emerges is precise and elegant. From the perspective of monetary-property ownership \u8212- the kind of ownership that governs transactions, redemptions, and the addition of a {\i chomesh} \u8212- the animal traces back to Shimon, who brought his own property into the sacred domain. From the perspective of religious ownership \u8212- whose {\i korban} it is, who has the relationship to the {\i kedusha} that enables acts like {\i tmurah} \u8212- the animal belongs to Reuven, whose {\i kapparah} is at stake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i trumah} case follows the same structure. When Shimon uses his own grain as {\i trumah} to exempt Reuven\u8217's pile, the grain that becomes {\i trumah} was Shimon\u8217's. The right to designate which {\i kohen} receives it \u8212- {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- traces to ownership of the grain that became {\i trumah}, and therefore belongs to Shimon. This parallels the {\i chomesh} ruling: the one whose property became the sacred object retains the property-adjacent rights.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teaching Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states that one who is forbidden to benefit another may nonetheless teach that person {\i midrash}, {\i halachot}, and {\i aggadot} \u8212- all forms of {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh} \u8212- but may not teach him {\i mikra}, the written Biblical text. He may, however, teach {\i mikra} to that person\u8217's children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction requires explanation. {\i Midrash} here means {\i midrash halacha} \u8212- the derivation of {\i halacha} from {\i psukim}. {\i Halachot} refers to straightforward rulings in the style of the {\i mishna}. {\i Aggadot} covers non-legal homiletical material. All of these are forms of {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh} and may be taught freely. {\i Mikra} \u8212- the Biblical text itself \u8212- is the one category that may not be taught directly to the person who cannot receive {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The permission to teach the person\u8217's children {\i mikra} is explained on the model of paying off a creditor: the benefit flows to the children directly, and only indirectly reaches the father, who is relieved of the cost of hiring a teacher. Indirect benefit of this kind \u8212- analogous to paying one\u8217's debt \u8212- does not constitute the prohibited {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why No Mikra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious question is: if teaching {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh} is permitted because performing a {\i mitzvah} service is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, why is teaching {\i mikra} any different? Teaching {\i psukim} is also a {\i mitzvah} act. What distinguishes it from teaching {\i midrash} or {\i halachot}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paid vs.\u160 ?Unpaid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel answers: {\i bemakom shenotlin sachar al hamikra ve\u8217'ein notlin sachar al hamidrash} \u8212- in a place where teachers are paid for teaching {\i mikra} but not for teaching {\i midrash}. The prohibition is not about the content per se but about the economic structure. When teaching {\i mikra} is a paid service and teaching {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh} is not, then the one who teaches {\i mikra} for free is effectively giving the recipient money \u8212- he is forgoing payment he would otherwise be entitled to collect. That waived payment is a form of {\i hana\u8217'ah}. Teaching {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh}, by contrast, is not a service that commands a fee, and therefore the act of teaching it creates no {\i hana\u8217'ah} even when the recipient benefits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies that this teaches us something precise: even in a place where it is permissible to charge for teaching {\i mikra}, one may only take a fee for {\i mikra} itself \u8212- not for {\i midrash}. The principle is that while {\i mikra} may be compensated in certain circumstances, {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh} may not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah & Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the deeper question: why is it acceptable to pay for {\i mikra} at all, and why is {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh} categorically different? The Gemara will address this through the specific nature of what is being compensated, but the underlying norm requires articulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibition on payment for teaching {\i Torah} is grounded textually. The verse states: {\i va\u8217'oti tziva Hashem ba\u8217'eis hi lilamed eschem chukim u\u8217'mishpatim} \u8212- God commanded me at that time to teach you statutes and ordinances. And another verse: {\i re\u8217'eh limadti eschem chukim u\u8217'mishpatim ka\u8217'asher tzivani Hashem elokai} \u8212- \u8220"see, I have taught you statutes and ordinances as Hashem my God commanded me.\u8221" The emphasis on {\i ka\u8217'asher tzivani} \u8212- \u8220"as He commanded me\u8221" \u8212- is read to mean that Moshe taught within certain parameters set by the divine model. {\i Mah ani bechinam af atem bechinam} \u8212- just as I taught you without charge, so too you must teach others without charge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The precise force of the {\i derashah} is interesting. The simplest reading of {\i ka\u8217'asher tzivani} may be that God taught Moshe {\i bechinam}, and Moshe is bound to replicate that model in his own teaching. Alternatively, the verse may mean that the manner in which Moshe taught Israel should be the model for how Israel teaches subsequent generations. Either way, the {\i halacha} is established: Torah may not be taught for pay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates the well-known practical difficulty: how are Torah teachers and rabbis paid? The standard {\i halachic} answer is {\i s\u8217'char batala} \u8212- compensation for opportunity cost, for the other productive activities the teacher foregoes by devoting himself to teaching. This framework, however, produces counterintuitive results when pressed. One would expect that the teacher with the best alternative economic opportunities should command the highest salary, while the teacher whose only skill is teaching \u8212- however gifted \u8212- should command the lowest. The highest salary would go to the computer programmer who also happens to teach, not to the finest educator who has devoted himself entirely to Torah study. The tension between {\i s\u8217'char batala} as a theoretical framework and professional compensation as a practical reality is a recognized difficulty, though its full exploration lies beyond the immediate scope of the Gemara here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minors & Babysitting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recipient Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presses Shmuel\u8217's distinction. If the only reason one may not teach {\i mikra} to the person from whom one cannot take {\i hana\u8217'ah} is that teaching {\i mikra} is a paid service in a given locale, then the prohibition should apply only when the teacher would normally be paid. But the {\i mishna} treats the prohibition as categorical. How does Shmuel\u8217's answer account for this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a different model: {\i s\u8217'char shimur} \u8212- compensation for babysitting. When children are sent to study {\i mikra}, the parents are not only paying for instruction in Torah; they are also paying the teacher to watch their children. The payment for {\i mikra} is therefore really payment for childcare, and this explains why teachers of {\i mikra} to young children can collect a fee while teachers of {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh} cannot. {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh} is studied by adults who do not need to be watched, and therefore no fee is attached. {\i Mikra} is associated with young children who do require supervision, and the fee reflects that supervision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this model, when Charlie is forbidden to provide {\i hana\u8217'ah} to a given person and that person sends his children to be taught {\i mikra}, Charlie is giving the parent a benefit: the parent does not have to pay a babysitting fee. That constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, working with the {\i s\u8217'char shimur} model, must now account for the {\i mishna}\u8217's text. The {\i mishna} says he may not teach {\i mikra} to the person himself but may teach it to the person\u8217's children. Reading the {\i mishna} with {\i s\u8217'char shimur} in mind, the Gemara proposes: {\i lo yilamedenu mikra bekatan} \u8212- he may not teach {\i mikra} to a minor. The prohibition applies specifically when the recipient is a child, because that is when a supervision fee would normally be charged. {\i Im haya gadol} \u8212- if the person is an adult, {\i melamed lo ulbanav mikra} \u8212- one may teach him directly, and may teach his children as well, the latter because the benefit to the adult is only indirect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a difficult reading of the {\i mishna}. The text of the {\i mishna} does not obviously refer to minors, and the {\i sugya} acknowledges the strain of this interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Indirect Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of teaching the adult\u8217's children is governed by the principle of indirect benefit. Shimon teaches Reuven\u8217's children; Reuven benefits because he does not have to pay for a teacher or babysitter. But this benefit reaches Reuven only through a third party \u8212- his children \u8212- and indirect benefit of this kind, analogous to having one\u8217's debt paid, does not constitute prohibited {\i hana\u8217'ah}. The prohibition on {\i hana\u8217'ah} applies to direct benefit; what arrives through an intermediary is categorically different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shabbat Lessons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i tinokot lo korin batchila beshabbat ela shonin barishon} \u8212- children may not begin studying a new lesson for the first time on {\i Shabbat}, but they may review material they have already learned. The natural assumption is that this distinction tracks some {\i halachic} concern about {\i Shabbat}, and the Gemara initially frames it in terms of whether the teaching constitutes a paid service \u8212- which would raise the question of earning wages on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Earnings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the reading that payment for {\i mikra} is for {\i s\u8217'char pisuk ta\u8217'amim} \u8212- compensation for teaching cantillation \u8212- the {\i baraita} makes sense. The first lesson involves learning the {\i trop} for the first time, which is the paid component. Teaching the {\i trop} on {\i Shabbat} would mean earning wages on {\i Shabbat}, which is problematic. Review, by contrast, focuses on meaning and comprehension rather than the initial instruction in cantillation, and carries no fee. Therefore the distinction between first lesson and review maps onto the distinction between a paid and an unpaid service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i s\u8217'char shimur} model, however, creates a difficulty. If the teacher is always being paid for babysitting regardless of whether it is the first lesson or a review, the distinction between the two should be irrelevant from a {\i Shabbat} earnings perspective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Havlah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara deflates the entire wages-on-{\i Shabbat} framework by noting that {\i havla} \u8212- absorption of {\i Shabbat} compensation within a larger fee \u8212- is permitted. The concern about earning wages on {\i Shabbat} applies only where the {\i Shabbat} payment stands alone and is unconnected to any broader work arrangement. When payment for {\i Shabbat} services is absorbed into a weekly, monthly, annual, or multi-year wage, the {\i Shabbat} component is legally dissolved into the larger arrangement and there is no prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Teaching arrangements are almost always structured as ongoing employment agreements. The teacher who is hired for the school year, the week, or the month is not separately earning wages for {\i Shabbat}; his {\i Shabbat} services are bundled into his overall compensation. On this basis, the concern about {\i Shabbat} wages is inapplicable to the teaching context, and the {\i baraita}\u8217's prohibition on first lessons cannot be derived from a wages-on-{\i Shabbat} analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weekly Wages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited to confirm that {\i havla} is operative in precisely these care-based employment relationships. One who is hired to watch a child, a {\i parah adumah}, or seeds planted for {\i ktziras ha\u8217'omer} for a single day on {\i Shabbat} is not paid for the {\i Shabbat} service, and consequently is treated as a {\i shomer chinam} \u8212- an unpaid guardian \u8212- with the lower standard of liability that entails. But if the employment is structured as a weekly ({\i sechir shabbat} \u8212- meaning here a week), monthly ({\i sechir chodesh}), annual ({\i sechir shana}), or seven-year ({\i sechir shavua}) arrangement, then the {\i Shabbat} service is absorbed into the overall fee. The worker does receive pay for {\i Shabbat}, and accordingly is treated as a {\i shomer sachar} \u8212- a paid guardian \u8212- with the higher liability that follows. This confirms that {\i havla} applies and that {\i Shabbat} earnings integrated into a broader arrangement are permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is that the {\i baraita} prohibiting first lessons on {\i Shabbat} cannot be about wages. Under normal employment conditions, wages for {\i Shabbat} teaching are absorbed via {\i havla} and present no problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simcha Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore offers an entirely different reason for the prohibition on first lessons on {\i Shabbat}: {\i mishum deyifnu avotehen deyinukei bemitzvasa deshabbosa} \u8212- so that the fathers of these children should turn their attention to the {\i mitzvah} of {\i Shabbat}. The purpose is that parents should be free to spend {\i Shabbat} with their children, to enjoy {\i oneg Shabbat} together, to eat the {\i Shabbat} meal together without being pulled away by the demands of a child who has to learn new material. Beginning a new lesson is taxing; it demands sustained focus and extended time, and it disrupts the {\i Shabbat} atmosphere of rest and family enjoyment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} formulates this as: the fathers of the children turn to the {\i mitzvot} of {\i Shabbat} and enjoy time with their children \u8212- {\i umishtasha\u8217'im im hatinokot} \u8212- and if the children were learning new lessons, the fathers would not want to take them away from their study and would therefore be separated from them. {\i Tosafot} frames it more prosaically: beginning the lesson would cause the children to arrive very late to the {\i Shabbat} meal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Eating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara appends an additional explanation: {\i mishum d\u8217'beshabata achlin veshasin} \u8212- on {\i Shabbat} people eat and drink beyond their normal patterns, {\i veyakar aleihon alma} \u8212- and the world feels heavy upon them. When one eats outside one\u8217's normal routine, one is not in the optimal mental state for focused first-time learning. Beginning new material under these conditions is poor pedagogy; the child will not absorb the lesson well. This is supported by a statement of Shmuel: {\i shinuy veset techillat choli me\u8217'ayim} \u8212- a change in one\u8217's normal routine is the beginning of intestinal trouble. Hard material encountered for the first time while in an altered physical state will not be properly retained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Babysitting Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i s\u8217'char shimur} model therefore fails to account for the {\i baraita}\u8217's distinction between first lessons and review on {\i Shabbat}. If the teacher is always collecting a babysitting fee whether he is giving a first lesson or a review, there is no basis within that framework to differentiate the two \u8212- the fee is the same, the {\i havla} applies equally, and no {\i Shabbat} wages concern arises in either case. The prohibition on first lessons must rest on the reasons identified \u8212- either the demand on parental attention or the pedagogical effects of unusual eating \u8212- not on any analysis of fees.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ta\u8217'amim & Pisuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Charging Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the analytical framework: the debate between {\i s\u8217'char shimur} and {\i s\u8217'char pisuk ta\u8217'amim} determines the scope of what may be charged for when teaching {\i mikra} to children. If {\i s\u8217'char pisuk ta\u8217'amim} is correct \u8212- that payment is for the {\i trop} instruction \u8212- then charging is permitted specifically for that component and not for the teaching of the textual content itself. This is what the Gemara means when it says that even in a place where it is acceptable to pay for teaching {\i mikra}, one may charge only for {\i mikra} and not for {\i midrash}; and within {\i mikra}, the chargeable component is the cantillation instruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 De\u8217'Oraita Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a striking question. The one who holds {\i s\u8217'char shimur} does not adopt {\i s\u8217'char pisuk ta\u8217'amim}, and the Gemara asks why. The answer given is: {\i kasavar pissuk teamim de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- he holds that {\i pisuk ta\u8217'amim} is {\i de\u8217'oraita}. If the cantillation and division of the text is part of the {\i Torah} itself \u8212- intrinsic to its identity, not extrinsic \u8212- then it cannot be charged for any more than teaching the words of the {\i Torah} can be charged for. One who holds {\i pisuk ta\u8217'amim de\u8217'oraita} cannot justify a fee for teaching {\i trop} on the grounds that it is a peripheral or ancillary skill.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nechemia\u8217's Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a statement of Rav Ika bar Avin in the name of Rav Chananel in the name of Rav, based on a verse in Nechemiah: {\i vayikre\u8217'u besefer Torat Elokim meforash veshum sechel vayavinu bamikra} \u8212- \u8220"and they read in the book of the Torah of God distinctly, and they gave the sense, and they caused the people to understand the reading.\u8221" This verse is parsed as containing multiple distinct components of Torah transmission, each of which is identified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Components Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vayikre\u8217'u besefer Torat Elokim} \u8212- they read in the book of the Torah of God \u8212- {\i zeh mikra}: this refers to the Biblical text itself. {\i Meforash} \u8212- distinctly, explicitly \u8212- {\i zeh targum}: this is the Aramaic translation. And on this the Gemara notes parenthetically: does this mean the {\i Targum} itself is {\i de\u8217'oraita}, given at Sinai? The question is whether {\i de\u8217'oraita} is being used to mean \u8220"commanded by the Torah\u8221" or simply \u8220"central to the Torah\u8217's identity.\u8221" {\i Veshum sechel} \u8212- and they gave the sense \u8212- {\i elu hapessukim}: these are the divisions of the {\i psukim}, the knowledge of where one verse ends and the next begins. This is not yet {\i pisuk ta\u8217'amim} in the full sense but the more basic knowledge of {\i pessukim} demarcation. {\i Vayavinu bamikra} \u8212- and they caused them to understand in the reading \u8212- {\i zeh pissuk ta\u8217'amim}: this is the cantillation system itself, the {\i trop}, which indicates the relationship between words, analogous to commas and semicolons in punctuation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'amrei lah} \u8212- and some say \u8212- {\i elu hamesorot}: the final component is alternatively identified as the {\i masorot} \u8212- the tradition governing {\i chaserot viyeterot}, which letters are written fully and which are written defectively, and other questions of orthography and textual transmission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 De\u8217'Oraita Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is thus a dispute: is {\i pisuk ta\u8217'amim} included among the components identified in Nechemiah\u8217's verse, or is it the {\i masorot}? Either way, the verse is read as establishing that at least some of these components \u8212- {\i targum}, the demarcation of {\i psukim}, cantillation or {\i masorot} \u8212- are part of the {\i Torah}\u8217's core identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper question is what {\i de\u8217'oraita} means in this context. It may mean that these elements were literally given at {\i Har Sinai}, in which case the {\i Targum} \u8212- and the {\i trop} \u8212- would have been transmitted to Moshe together with the text. Alternatively, and perhaps more straightforwardly, {\i de\u8217'oraita} may mean that these elements are part of the essential identity of {\i Torah}, not peripheral tools but constitutive components of what {\i Torah} is. The question is analogous to asking whether Hebrew biblical grammar belongs in {\i limudei kodesh} or {\i limudei chol} \u8212- whether one recites {\i birchat ha\u8217'Torah} before studying biblical grammar, whether it is considered {\i Torah} proper or a scholarly discipline about {\i Torah}. The {\i trop} shows which words are connected to which, shapes meaning, and affects {\i psak halacha} in some contexts; yet the Gemara seems to suggest that one authority views it as sufficiently distinct from {\i Torah} content itself that payment for teaching it may be justified. The other authority, holding {\i pissuk teamim de\u8217'oraita}, regards it as too central to {\i Torah} to permit compensation, and must locate the justification for teachers\u8217' fees elsewhere \u8212- in {\i s\u8217'char shimur}. This debate and its further ramifications will continue into the following {\i amud}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 38\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Teaching & Fee (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Teaching Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the bottom of 37b, in the middle of a {\i mishnah} discussing the laws of a {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah}: a situation in which Reuven is prohibited from deriving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from Shimon. The {\i mishnah} establishes that Shimon may nonetheless teach Reuven {\i Torah}, because {\i mitzvos lav lehenos nitnu} \u8212- the commandments were not given for physical or monetary pleasure, and imparting Torah knowledge is therefore not technically classified as {\i hana\u8217'ah}. One exception, however, is teaching {\i Torah she-bichtav}. The reason offered is that teaching written Torah \u8212- particularly to children \u8212- typically carries a fee. One cannot charge for teaching {\i Torah she-be\u8217'al peh}, but for {\i Torah she-bichtav} a fee is permitted, either on account of the babysitting function involved in teaching children, or because the instruction is assumed to include {\i pisuk te\u8217'amim} \u8212- the {\i trop} \u8212- which, while necessary for understanding the {\i pesukim}, is not considered {\i Torah} proper. It is somewhat analogous to biblical grammar: a subject one might classify under {\i limudei kodesh} or {\i limudei chol} depending on one\u8217's framework. Accordingly, if Shimon teaches Reuven {\i Torah she-bichtav} without charge, he is effectively waiving the fee he could have collected, thereby conferring a monetary benefit \u8212- which would be prohibited under the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Torah Study\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A striking corollary emerges from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that Shimon may teach Reuven\u8217's sons and daughters, even though this saves Reuven the cost of hiring a teacher elsewhere. This is permitted because the benefit reaches Reuven only indirectly \u8212- analogous to paying off a creditor \u8212- and is not something given directly to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More significant, however, is what this ruling implies about the Torah education of daughters. The fact that daughters are included in this permission indicates that women were in fact taught {\i Torah she-bichtav}, and according to this reading of the {\i mishnah}, they were taught the {\i trop} as well. This need not be because they were expected to perform a ritual reading in the {\i beis knesses} \u8212- although a {\i braisa} does state in theory that women may receive {\i aliyos}, the practice being set aside only on account of {\i kevod hatzibur} \u8212- but at minimum they were being taught to grammatically parse the verse through its punctuation. That women received such instruction is, as noted in the course of discussion, a genuinely fascinating datum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikra & Pisuk Te\u8217'amim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the question of whether {\i pisuk te\u8217'amim} \u8212- the {\i trop} \u8212- has the status of {\i Torah} itself, that is, whether it is {\i de\u8217'oraisa}. The term {\i de\u8217'oraisa} can be understood either in the historical sense of going back to Har Sinai, or in the definitional sense of whether it counts as {\i Torah} proper for purposes of obligations such as {\i birchas haTorah} or the restrictions on where one may study. This same question arises regarding {\i Targum}, which some hold also traces back to Sinai. The practical consequence here is that according to the view permitting a fee for teaching {\i pisuk te\u8217'amim}, such instruction is not considered {\i Torah} per se; whereas the opposing view would prohibit charging, treating it as fully Torah. Both positions remain in play.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Teaching & Fee (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sofrim Traditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with a statement of Rabbi Yitzchak. It draws on the verse in Nechemia, {\i vayikre\u8217'u basefer toras elokim meforash vesum sechel vayavinu bamikra} \u8212- \u8220"they understood in the {\i mikra}\u8221" \u8212- which the {\i Gemara} had earlier applied to {\i pisuk te\u8217'amim} or the {\i masoret}. Rabbi Yitzchak now introduces a different teaching: {\i mikra sofrim}, {\i ve\u8217'itur sofrim}, {\i vekaryan velo kesivan}, {\i vekesivan velo karyan} \u8212- the reading tradition of the scribes, the embellishments of the scribes, words that are read but not written, and words that are written but not read \u8212- all of these are {\i halakha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}. They trace all the way back to Moshe at Sinai. The {\i Gemara} now proceeds to unpack each category in turn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scribal Traditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vowel Shifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mikra Sofrim} refers to the tradition of how certain words are vocalized differently depending on their position in the verse \u8212- specifically, whether they fall at a major pause such as an {\i esnachta} or at the end of a {\i pasuk}. The {\i Gemara} cites the examples of {\i Eretz}, {\i Shamayim}, and {\i Mitzrayim}. The Ran explains this as the phenomenon of vowel shift: a word such as {\i Eretz} may be read with a {\i segol} in the middle of a verse but shift to a {\i kamatz} at an {\i esnachta} or end of verse, yielding {\i Aretz}. Similarly, {\i Shamayim} and {\i Mitzrayim} shift from a {\i patach} to a {\i kamatz} at these junctures. The Ran also offers an alternative explanation rooted in the written text: words like {\i Mitzrayim} or {\i Shamayim} could in theory be read differently without the stabilizing vowel letters \u8212- the scribal tradition of how such words are pronounced fills this gap. Whether this reflects a contrast with Judeo-Arabic conventions or points to the significance of the {\i kamatz}-{\i patach} alternation, the underlying principle remains the same: there is an ancient tradition, going back to Sinai, of how to vocalize these words in their varying syntactic positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Embellishment Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ittur Sofrim} \u8212- the \u8220"adornment\u8221" or \u8220"embellishment\u8221" of the scribes \u8212- refers to extra words appearing in certain verses whose presence requires explanation. The examples given are occurrences of the word {\i achar} in several contexts: {\i achar ta\u8217'avoru} (Genesis 18:5), {\i achar teleich} (Numbers 12:14), and {\i achar te\u8217'asef} \u8212- as well as {\i keharerei el} (Psalms 36:7).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran offers two readings of this category. The first is a matter of punctuation and sense: the word {\i achar} divides a verse in a way that could easily be misread. For instance, in the verse {\i sa\u8217'adu libchem achar ta\u8217'avoru}, the question is whether to read \u8220"fortify your hearts, and then after that you shall pass,\u8221" or \u8220"fortify your hearts after you pass\u8221" \u8212- a reading that would make no sense in context. The {\i ittur sofrim} tradition tells us where to place the pause, saving the meaning of the verse from confusion. Understood this way, the category seems functionally similar to {\i pisuk te\u8217'amim}, and the distinction between them is not immediately clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second reading the Ran offers is that these words were simply not grammatically necessary. The verse could have read {\i sa\u8217'adu libchem vetaavoru}, or {\i teshev hana\u8217'arah yamim itanu vetelech}, or {\i Miriam tisager shivat yamim veteaseif} \u8212- the word {\i achar} in each case is an additional embellishment that makes the verse sound more natural or melodious. Similarly, {\i keharerei el} in Psalms 36:7 has a {\i kaf} of comparison where the verse\u8217's parallel structure \u8212- {\i mishpatecha tehom rabah} \u8212- would have suggested simply {\i harerei El} without the {\i kaf}. The Ran identifies this as {\i tiferet halashon}, an aesthetic enhancement. The claim that such embellishments are {\i halakha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} would then mean that these words were always part of the original text, against any position that they represent later additions. Neither explanation, it must be said, is entirely satisfying: the first merges uncomfortably with {\i pisuk te\u8217'amim}, while the second raises the question of what the {\i hava amina} would have been \u8212- why would anyone have thought these words were later additions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Read Not Written\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Keri velo ketiv} \u8212- words read in the biblical text but not actually written there \u8212- constitutes the third category. The {\i Gemara} enumerates several instances:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Prat debelachto}: In a verse in Samuel, the word {\i Prat} \u8212- the Euphrates River \u8212- is read but absent from the written text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ish dekaasher yishal ish}: The word {\i ish} is read in the verse but not written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'im denivnetah}: In Yirmiyahu, the verse {\i hineh yamim neum Hashem venivnetah hair} has {\i ba\u8217'im} read but not written \u8212- the text would simply say {\i hineh yamim neum Hashem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i La depleitah}: In the final chapters of Yirmiyahu, the word {\i la} \u8212- \u8220"for it\u8221" \u8212- appears in the reading of a verse about {\i pleitah} but is absent from the written text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Et dehaged hugad}: In Ruth, Boaz speaks to Ruth \u8212- {\i haged hugad li kol asher asit chamotech} \u8212- and the word {\i et} is read before {\i kol asher} but is not written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eilai dehagoren}: Also in Ruth, the word {\i eilai} \u8212- \u8220"to me\u8221" \u8212- is read in the verse but not written, as in {\i asher tomri eilai eeseh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Alai de-has\u8217'orim}: Again in Ruth, in the phrase {\i shesh has\u8217'orim ha-eleh natan li ki amar alai}, the word {\i alai} is read but absent from the written text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written Not Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ketiv velo keri} \u8212- words written in the biblical text but not read aloud \u8212- is the fourth category. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's examples are as follows:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Na de-yislach}: In the request of Na\u8217'aman \u8212- {\i yislach na Hashem le-avdecha} \u8212- the word {\i na} is written but not read aloud; we read simply {\i yislach Hashem le-avdecha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Zot de-hamitzvah}: The {\i Gemara} claims a word {\i zot} is written but not read in {\i parashas va\u8217'etchanan}. Here the Ran notes an important discrepancy: he found this cited in his sources, but writes {\i kach matzati katuv velo nimtza be-sfarim she-lanu} \u8212- it appears in the tradition but is absent from the manuscripts he possessed. He tentatively identifies it with a verse in Yirmiyahu \u8212- {\i chai Hashem asher asah lanu et hanefesh hazot} \u8212- where {\i hazot} may be the word written but not read.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yidroch deha-dorech}: In Yirmiyahu, the verse {\i yidroch ha-dorech et kashto} (or {\i el ha-dorech kashto}) has the word {\i yidroch} written but not read.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chamesh de-pa\u8217'at negev}: In Yechezkel, the verse {\i ve-elu midoteha chamesh chamesh me\u8217'ot ve-arba alafim} has the word {\i chamesh} written twice but read only once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Im de-ki goel anochi}: In Ruth, the verse {\i ve-atah ki omnam ki im goel anochi} has the word {\i im} written but not read; we read simply {\i ki goel anochi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} also takes care to demarcate the two lists clearly. After listing the {\i keri velo ketiv} examples, it states {\i hani keryan velo ktivan} \u8212- these are the ones read and not written \u8212- before introducing the {\i ketiv velo keri} list, and then restates the identification at the end of the second list with {\i halin ktivan velo keryan}. This redundancy is deliberate: with two parallel lists flowing into each other, a reader could easily lose track of which category is under discussion, and the bracketing language prevents the confusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara vs.\u160 ?Mesora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A significant methodological observation emerges from the Ran\u8217's discussion of these lists. The Ran notes that in the {\i mesora} \u8212- the systematic tradition codified by the school of Ben Asher, probably in the seventh or eighth century, shortly after the close of the {\i Gemara} \u8212- the list of {\i keri u-ketiv} examples is a superset of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's list: {\i chashiv kol hani ve-tapya lehu achrinei} \u8212- it records all of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's cases and adds others. Yet more striking is that the {\i mesora} does not record {\i et ha-mitzvah} or {\i zot ha-mitzvah} among the {\i ketiv velo keri} cases, indicating that the lists do not perfectly align. The {\i Gemara} has some items that are not corroborated by the {\i mesora} as we have it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This points toward a broader and important structural observation. According to scholars of the biblical text, the phenomenon of {\i keri u-ketiv} likely reflects the existence of two distinct schools of tradition operating in partial independence from one another. One school preserved the written text \u8212- the scribes ({\i sofrim}) who specialized in the craft of writing, producing parchment and copying letters with exactitude according to their received tradition, and who were expert in matters such as {\i malei} and {\i chaseir} (full and deficient spellings). A second school preserved the oral tradition of how the text was read: vocalization, grammatical parsing, {\i pisuk te\u8217'amim} \u8212- the entire apparatus of reading, which is a distinct skill from scribal copying. A scribe who knows the letters of {\i Shamayim} does not thereby know whether to read it with a {\i kamatz} or a {\i patach} at the end of a verse; that belongs to the reader\u8217's tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two schools did not always match perfectly. The reading tradition might include a word the writing tradition did not record, or vice versa \u8212- not because either school was in error, but because they preserved different layers of transmission. The {\i Gemara}, by attributing all of these categories to {\i halakha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}, resolves the apparent tension: both traditions are equally ancient and authoritative, and their occasional divergence reflects not a contradiction but a dual transmission going back to Sinai itself. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's framing is itself noteworthy in how it attributes the reading tradition to {\i sofrim} \u8212- {\i mikra sofrim}. Rather than presenting the scribes and the readers as separate or competing schools, the {\i Gemara} converges them: the tradition of {\i how to read} is itself attributed to the scribal class, synthesizing what might otherwise appear as two opposed streams.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A separate but related category is {\i tikkun sofrim} \u8212- the scribal practice of substituting a softer word for a harsh or explicit one, as in {\i yishkavenah} for {\i yishgalenah} in the {\i kelalos}. This is not the same phenomenon as {\i keri u-ketiv}, because it does not represent an ancient transmission of two parallel textual layers; rather, it is a later ritual adjustment for the sake of propriety in public reading, comparable to the practice of not reading {\i ma\u8217'aseh Dinah} in a congregational setting. There is, in fact, a well-known Rashi in the {\i Torah} \u8212- the precise location requires looking up \u8212- that explicitly characterizes {\i tikkun sofrim} as a later scribal intervention for the sake of {\i tikkun ha-lashon}, distinct from any {\i halakha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pasuk Divisions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Pasuk Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak bar Abba reports that in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- {\i b\u8217'ma\u8217'arava} \u8212- a verse that we divide as one {\i pasuk} was divided into three {\i pesukim}. The verse in question is the one beginning {\i Vayomer Hashem el Moshe hinne Anochi ba elecha ba\u8217'av he\u8217'anan ba\u8217'avur yishma ha\u8217'am b\u8217'daberi imach v\u8217'gam b\u8217'cha ya\u8217'aminu l\u8217'olam vayyashev Moshe et divrei ha\u8217'am el Hashem}. The final clause, {\i vayyashev Moshe et divrei ha\u8217'am el Hashem}, could readily stand as an independent {\i pasuk}, and the opening phrase through {\i ba\u8217'av he\u8217'anan} could similarly be separated. The intermediate phrase beginning {\i ba\u8217'avur yishma ha\u8217'am} is a somewhat awkward way to open a {\i pasuk}, but the point stands: there existed a regional tradition of breaking this verse into three separate units, acknowledging that the boundaries between {\i pesukim} were not universally fixed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe\u8217's Attributes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Wealth Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now transitions into a series of {\i drashot} that, while occasioned by the theme of scribal activity, constitute a rich midrashic exploration of Moshe\u8217's character and the nature of his relationship to Torah. Rabbi Chama bar Chanina teaches: {\i lo he\u8217'eshir Moshe ela mi-psoletan shel luchot} \u8212- Moshe became wealthy from nothing other than the leftover fragments of the {\i luchot}. The tradition holds that the {\i luchot} were made of precious stone, and the verse {\i pesol lecha shnei luchot avanim karishonim} \u8212- \u8220"carve for yourself two tablets of stone like the first\u8221" \u8212- is read not only as {\i pesol lecha} (\u8220"carve for yourself\u8221") but as {\i psolatan shelcha} (\u8220"the cuttings are yours to keep\u8221"). Moshe carved the stone, and the sapphire dust or the fragments that fell away were his to keep. As one student astutely observed, this reading works even better according to the understanding that {\i pesol lecha} means Moshe himself did the cutting and shaping from a larger stone \u8212- the {\i pesolas} being the material carved away in forming the tablets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Given to Moshe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Chanina teaches a remarkable {\i aggadah}: {\i lo nittna Torah ela l\u8217'Moshe u\u8217'zaro} \u8212- the Torah was given only to Moshe and his descendants, not to the rest of Israel. The derivation is from the same verse: just as {\i pesol lecha} is read as \u8220"the cuttings are for you,\u8221" so too {\i ketov lecha et hadvarim ha-eleh} \u8212- \u8220"write for yourself these words\u8221" \u8212- is read as {\i katvanvan shelcha}, meaning what is written down belongs to you, to Moshe and his offspring alone. Nevertheless, {\i Moshe nahag ba tovat ayin v\u8217'nattna l\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- Moshe acted with a generous spirit and gave it to the Jewish people as a whole. The verse applied to him is {\i tov ayin hu yevorach} \u8212- the one with a generous eye shall be blessed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teaching Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda raises a powerful challenge to this position. If the Torah was given only to Moshe, what of the verse {\i v\u8217'oti tziva Hashem ba\u8217'et ha-hi l\u8217'lammed etchem} \u8212- \u8220"God commanded me at that time to teach you\u8221"? This appears to be an explicit divine command to transmit Torah to the entire people, not merely a personal act of generosity. Similarly, {\i va\u8217'ani limadeti etchem chukim umishpatim ka\u8217'asher tzivani Hashem Elokai} \u8212- \u8220"I have taught you statutes and laws as God commanded me\u8221" \u8212- indicates that the teaching itself was commanded, not merely voluntary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pilpula & Generosity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds to Rav Chisda\u8217's challenge by offering a forced but creative re-reading: {\i v\u8217'oti tziva Hashem\u8230 ? va\u8217'ani lachem} \u8212- God commanded me, and I decided to teach you. On this reading, God commanded Moshe for himself, and the teaching to Israel was Moshe\u8217's own initiative. The {\i Gemara} attempts similar readings of the other verses. In the end, however, the {\i Gemara} retreats: {\i ella pilpula be\u8217'alma} \u8212- what was uniquely given to Moshe and what he generously shared was not the {\i Torah} itself (which was always commanded to be transmitted to Israel) but {\i pilpula be\u8217'alma}, the capacity for incisive analytical engagement with the text. The word {\i pilpul}, from the root meaning pepper, connotes the kind of penetrating, nuanced interpretive analysis that is the hallmark of deep Torah learning. This is what was uniquely Moshe\u8217's gift, and what he shared with all of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah she-bichtav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Maharsha offers an alternative and, in this context, more contextually fitting reading. He notes that virtually all the {\i pesukim} cited in this passage concern written dimensions of Torah: {\i pesol lecha}, {\i ketov lecha}, {\i kitvu lachem et hashira hazot}. The {\i hava amina} is therefore not about who was commanded to observe the {\i mitzvos} \u8212- clearly all of Israel was \u8212- but about who possessed {\i Torah she-bichtav}, the written text itself. The claim of Rabbi Yose bar Chanina is that in principle, the sacred written scripture was intended for Moshe and his descendants alone, to be protected and transmitted within that line, while Israel would have had the {\i mitzvos} and the {\i halacha} without necessarily possessing access to the derivational process rooted in the written word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading resonates with the broader history of religious tradition: ancient religions often maintained a protected class of priests who guarded access to sacred texts, while the people received only the transmitted message. The medieval church famously resisted translating scripture into the vernacular for analogous reasons. The Maharsha suggests the {\i Gemara} is taking seriously \u8212- not dismissing \u8212- the idea that there is something appropriate about keeping the written Torah in a protected sacred domain, close to its divine source. What is theologically significant is that in the end the {\i Gemara} abandons this position entirely, insisting that {\i Torah she-bichtav} belongs to all of Israel. And the closing move is revealing: what was uniquely Moshe\u8217's, what he gave as an act of generosity, was {\i pilpula} \u8212- precisely the tool of {\i Torah she-be\u8217'al peh}, the analytical engagement that has nothing to do with protecting the written text. The {\i Gemara} has thus dramatically inverted the {\i hava amina}: the written Torah was always meant for everyone, and what distinguished Moshe\u8217's unique contribution was something entirely in the oral-analytical realm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Attributes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan teaches that the divine presence \u8212- {\i shechinas} \u8212- rests only upon one who possesses four qualities: {\i gibor} (physical might), {\i ashir} (wealth), {\i chacham} (wisdom), and {\i anav} (humility). The Talmud then states {\i v\u8217'chulam mi-Moshe} \u8212- Moshe embodied all of these \u8212- and proceeds to prove each one from scripture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Strength\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof of Moshe\u8217's {\i gevurah} begins with the verse {\i vayifros es ha-ohel al ha-mishkan} \u8212- he spread the tent covering over the {\i Mishkan} \u8212- with the tradition that Moshe did this personally, without assistance. Given that the {\i Mishkan} stood ten {\i amos} \u8212- approximately fifteen feet \u8212- high, spreading the covering unassisted would have required extraordinary physical stature and strength. The {\i Gemara} entertains the objection that perhaps Moshe was simply very tall but physically weak \u8212- {\i arich v\u8217'katin} \u8212- but then advances a more compelling proof: the verse {\i va-etpos b\u8217'shnei ha-luchos va-ashlichem me-al shtei yadai va-ashabrem}, in which Moshe physically gripped the two tablets and hurled them. A {\i braisa} teaches that each tablet was six {\i tefachim} long, six {\i tefachim} wide, and three {\i tefachim} thick \u8212- approximately a foot and a half by a foot and a half by nine inches of solid stone. Holding and throwing such a mass with bare hands bespeaks enormous physical power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wealth Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For wealth: {\i psal lecha psoltan shelcha} \u8212- as established above, Moshe retained the precious stone fragments from carving the tablets, and from this he became wealthy. One might note that this enrichment came relatively late in Moshe\u8217's career, at the second giving of the tablets, which raises the interesting question of whether Moshe possessed all four qualities earlier, when the {\i shechinah} first rested on him. This chronological difficulty is acknowledged but left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wisdom Gates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For wisdom: Rav and Shmuel both teach that fifty gates of understanding \u8212- {\i chamishim sha\u8217'arei binah} \u8212- were created in the world, and all were given to Moshe save one, as the verse states {\i vatachasreihu m\u8217'at me\u8217'elokim} \u8212- \u8220"You have made him but little less than God.\u8221" Moshe\u8217's wisdom was thus as close to divine perfection as a human being can attain. Notably, the {\i Gemara} says these gates were {\i given} to Moshe \u8212- which raises the same question as before: is this about an innate capacity or a divine gift? The Rambam\u8217's position in his discussion of {\i nevuah} is relevant here: prophecy rests on one whose own intellect has been developed to apprehend the divine, not merely on one passively chosen by God. From that perspective, the gift and the developed capacity are not in tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Humility Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For humility: {\i ve-ha\u8217'ish Moshe anav me\u8217'od} \u8212- \u8220"the man Moshe was exceedingly humble.\u8221" This is the most straightforward of the four proofs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prophets\u8217' Wealth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan teaches further that all of the prophets were wealthy. Since prophecy rests upon one who possesses all four attributes including wealth, it follows as a general principle. The {\i Gemara} grounds this claim in four specific cases \u8212- Moshe, Samuel, Amos, and Jonah \u8212- from which we extrapolate to all the prophets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Moshe, the proof text is {\i lo chamor echad mehem nasati} \u8212- \u8220"I have not taken even one donkey of theirs.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} asks: does this just mean he didn\u8217't steal? Surely that goes without saying. The {\i Gemara} therefore reads it more specifically: Moshe is declaring that he never even rented a donkey from them, even with payment. To which the {\i Gemara} objects: perhaps he refrained from renting not because he was wealthy but because he was poor. The {\i Gemara} therefore falls back on the earlier source: the proof that Moshe was wealthy comes from {\i psal lecha} \u8212- the retention of the precious stone fragments \u8212- rather than from this verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samuel\u8217's Wealth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Samuel, the proof text is the famous passage from his farewell address: {\i hineni anu bi neged Hashem v\u8217'neged meshicho et shor mi lakachti v\u8217'chamor mi lakachti} \u8212- \u8220"here I am, testify against me: whose ox have I taken, whose donkey have I taken?\u8221" Parallel to the discussion of Moshe, the {\i Gemara} asks whether Samuel is merely denying theft. The answer is the same: he is denying even having rented an animal from them, even for fair compensation. But again, this could reflect poverty rather than wealth. The actual proof of Samuel\u8217's wealth comes from the verse {\i utshuvato haRamatah ki sham beito} \u8212- \u8220"his return was to Ramah, for his home was there\u8221" \u8212- and Rava\u8217's interpretation: {\i kol makom shehalach beito imo} \u8212- wherever Samuel traveled, his household accompanied him. This does not mean he was homeless; it means he never required anyone\u8217's hospitality because he traveled with all his servants, provisions, and resources. He was wealthy enough to be entirely self-sufficient wherever he went.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samuel\u8217's Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava draws a comparison between Moshe and Samuel that favors Samuel. By Moshe, the verse says {\i nasati} \u8212- \u8220"I did not take\u8221" \u8212- implying that Moshe never initiated a request to borrow or rent an animal, since even asking would have been an abuse of his authority over people who could not comfortably refuse him. But by Samuel, the verse says {\i lo ashatanu velo ritzotanu} \u8212- \u8220"you have not oppressed us and have not exploited us\u8221" \u8212- and the word {\i ritzotanu}, read as deriving from {\i ratzon}, indicates that even when people offered Samuel the use of their animals of their own free will, he refused. Moshe held back from asking; Samuel refused even when offered. In this specific dimension of scrupulous avoidance of benefit from one\u8217's position, Samuel\u8217's standard exceeded even that of Moshe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amos\u8217's Possession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Amos: {\i lo navi anochi velo ben navi anochi ki boker ani uboles shikmim} \u8212- \u8220"I am neither a prophet nor a son of a prophet; I am a {\i boker} and a {\i boles} of sycamores.\u8221" As Rav Yosef translates: {\i arei marei getei ana} \u8212- I am an owner of cattle ({\i marei}, meaning master or owner, not merely a hired herder), and I have sycamore groves in the valley. The term {\i marei} confirms that Amos was not a laborer but a proprietor of his flocks and lands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jonah\u8217's Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Jonah: {\i vayiten sechara vayered bah} \u8212- \u8220"he gave its fare and went down into it.\u8221" Rabbi Yochanan explains that Jonah paid the fare for the entire ship, not just his own passage. Rav Romanus adds that the fare for the whole ship amounted to four thousand gold {\i dinari}. The explanation offered is that Jonah wished to depart immediately and could not wait for the ship to fill with other passengers; he therefore purchased all the remaining tickets himself in order to set sail at once. That he was able to do so speaks to his considerable wealth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah as Gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forgetting & Gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan teaches: {\i betchila haya Moshe lomed Torah umeshakecha} \u8212- at first, Moshe would learn Torah and immediately forget it, until it was given to him as a gift. The verse cited is {\i vayiten el Moshe ke-chaloto ledaber ito} \u8212- \u8220"God gave it to Moshe upon finishing speaking with him\u8221" \u8212- read as a divine gifting of the Torah to Moshe rather than merely a conclusion to a conversation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Theology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage stands in a provocative relationship to the earlier claim that Moshe was a {\i chacham} who possessed fifty gates of understanding. If Moshe\u8217's wisdom was so extraordinary, why did he initially forget? One way to read this is that the intellectual apprehension and the stable possession of Torah are two different things. Moshe could receive, process, and analyze \u8212- but to actually {\i hold} the Torah, to retain it as an enduring possession, required an act of divine gifting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a direct connection back to the opening discussion of this daf: the prohibition against charging for teaching Torah. If even Moshe received the Torah not through his own efforts but as a {\i matanah}, a divine gift, then Torah at its root is not a commodity to be earned or purchased. {\i Ma ani be-chinam af ata be-chinam} \u8212- just as I gave it freely, so too you shall transmit it freely. The entire Torah that any person acquires is ultimately not the fruit of their own efforts alone but a gift from God. To charge for it, then, is to treat as merchandise something that was always in the domain of divine generosity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The passage also closes a circle opened earlier by Rabbi Yose bar Chanina\u8217's {\i aggadah} about Torah belonging to Moshe and his descendants. That {\i aggadah} explored the question of ownership \u8212- could the written Torah be the protected possession of one family? The {\i Gemara} rejected it. And here, at the conclusion of the sugya, the {\i Gemara} quietly makes the deepest point of all: even Moshe did not fully {\i own} the Torah. He could not even retain it on his own. It had to be given to him as a gift. The Torah belongs to God, and is given freely \u8212- to Moshe, through Moshe, and ultimately to all of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mudar Hana\u8217'ah Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Feeding Family\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the laws of {\i nedarim} and the case of a {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah}. If Reuven may not receive benefit from Shimon, Shimon is nonetheless permitted to provide food for Reuven\u8217's wife and children. Although Reuven has a legal obligation to provide his wife\u8217's sustenance under the terms of the {\i ketubah}, Shimon\u8217's feeding her is categorized not as a direct benefit to Reuven but as the indirect benefit of paying off a creditor. The money or food goes to someone else who has a claim against Reuven; it does not come directly to Reuven himself. The rule applies {\i af al pi shehu chayav bimzonotan} \u8212- even though Reuven is legally obligated to support them, the indirectness of the benefit means it does not constitute prohibited {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Feeding Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same permission does not extend to Reuven\u8217's animals. {\i Velo yazon et behemto bein tmeiah bein tehorah} \u8212- Shimon may not feed Reuven\u8217's animals, whether they are kosher or non-kosher. The distinction is clear: feeding someone\u8217's livestock directly increases the value of their property. A fatter or stronger animal is worth more. This is not analogous to paying off a creditor or providing for a dependent; it is directly enriching the {\i mudar\u8217's} estate. The benefit passes through no intermediary \u8212- it is an immediate enhancement of Reuven\u8217's asset, which is precisely the kind of {\i hana\u8217'ah} the prohibition targets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kosher vs.\u160 ?Non-Kosher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer, however, draws a distinction within the category of animals: {\i zan et hatmeiah ve\u8217'eino zan et hatehorah} \u8212- he may feed the non-kosher animal but not the kosher one. His reasoning is striking: {\i shehatehorah nafsha lashamayim vegufa shelo} \u8212- a kosher animal\u8217's soul goes to heaven, but its body belongs to the owner. Therefore, making the body of a kosher animal heavier or stronger does increase its owner\u8217's value. {\i Utmeiah nafsha vegufa lashamayim} \u8212- but a non-kosher animal, both its soul and its body are, in a sense, destined for heaven; the owner cannot eat it. So fattening a non-kosher animal confers no real benefit to its owner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value vs.\u160 ?Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying conceptual framework here is the distinction between {\i increasing value} and {\i preventing loss}. A useful analogy: if you give someone\u8217's car a new paint job or repair its dents, you have actively increased its market value \u8212- that is a direct benefit. But if you take the car for an oil change it was due for, you have performed maintenance that staves off future damage without increasing present value. The maintenance prevents a loss; it does not generate a gain. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's reasoning about non-kosher animals follows this logic: feeding a donkey prevents it from weakening, but it does not actively increase the owner\u8217's wealth, since the owner has no way to monetize the animal\u8217's physical condition through consumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran reads the case differently. In his interpretation, the discussion concerns a situation where the animal has already received its basic daily portion. Giving it that basic portion would be a genuine benefit to the owner \u8212- maintaining the animal\u8217's value \u8212- and would presumably be prohibited. What the discussion concerns is whether one may provide {\i additional} food beyond what the animal requires. Extra feeding, on the Ran\u8217's reading, does not increase the animal\u8217's value and is therefore permitted. However, this reading may not align with the plain sense of the {\i sugya}, where the simpler reading appears to be that any feeding of an animal is prohibited because it maintains or increases value, and the question is only about the conceptual category \u8212- active enhancement versus passive maintenance \u8212- rather than about the quantity of food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Values\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages respond to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position: {\i mah bein tmeiah letehorah} \u8212- what is the real difference? Even a non-kosher animal has market value. {\i She\u8217'im yirtzeh harei hu mochrah le\u8217'ovdei chochavim uma\u8217'achilah leklavim} \u8212- if the owner wishes, he can sell the animal to non-Jews for consumption, or use it as dog food. Accordingly, a larger, healthier non-kosher animal is worth more than a weaker one, and feeding it does increase the owner\u8217's wealth. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's distinction between kosher and non-kosher animals is therefore rejected: since both types can be monetized, feeding either one constitutes a direct benefit to the {\i mudar}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 39\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife, Children, Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Feeding Wife & Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with the tail end of the Mishnah on 38b, which addresses a situation in which Reuven is forbidden to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} (benefit) from Shimon. Despite this prohibition, the Mishnah rules that Shimon may still feed Reuven\u8217's wife and children. The underlying logic is that doing so is not considered a direct conferral of benefit upon Reuven himself \u8212- it is analogous to paying off Reuven\u8217's creditors. Because Reuven bears an obligation to feed his wife and children, Shimon\u8217's providing for them discharges what is, in effect, a debt, and that is not classified as direct {\i hana\u8217'ah} to the person who is off-limits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, feeding Reuven\u8217's animals is prohibited. There, the act of providing food directly increases the monetary value of Reuven\u8217's estate \u8212- a fatter animal is worth more \u8212- and that constitutes a tangible, direct benefit. The Mishnah\u8217's discussion of non-kosher animals, whose value is not measured primarily by weight gain but rather by their capacity for labor (a donkey fed to continue working, for example), raises a further question about whether that too constitutes impermissible {\i hana\u8217'ah}. The general principle emerges clearly: feeding the wife is not {\i hana\u8217'ah}, but feeding the animals is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult Daughter Marrying\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with a teaching cited in the name of Rav Yitzchak bar Chananya in the name of Rav Huna: if one is forbidden to benefit from another ({\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro}), it is still permitted for the second party to marry off his daughter to the first. Rabbi Zeira immediately raises a difficulty: in which direction does the prohibition run?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If it is Reuven who cannot benefit from Shimon, then Shimon\u8217's marrying his daughter to Reuven would seem to be a straightforward conferral of {\i hana\u8217'ah} upon Reuven \u8212- after all, as the Gemara evocatively notes, a wife brings a {\i shifcha leshamsho}, a maidservant to attend to his needs, which is precisely what the tractate Ketubot establishes as one of a wife\u8217's marital obligations. On the other hand, if the direction is reversed and Reuven cannot give benefit to Shimon, then Reuven\u8217's marrying off his daughter to Shimon would appear to give Shimon something of value. Either way, the ruling seems problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to resolve this by positing that it must be Shimon (the bride\u8217's father) whose property is forbidden to Reuven (the groom). Yet the Gemara immediately challenges this resolution: if Shimon can already feed Reuven\u8217's daughter even without marrying her off to him \u8212- and that is not considered {\i hana\u8217'ah} to Reuven \u8212- then it goes without saying ({\i gedola mizu amru}) that once Reuven has married her and she is now his wife, Shimon can continue to provide for her. There is no {\i chiddush} there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore settles on the opposite formulation: it is Reuven, the bride\u8217's father, whose property is forbidden to Shimon, the groom. The case is one in which Reuven\u8217's daughter is a {\i bogeret} \u8212- an adult woman of at least twelve and a half years \u8212- and she is acting entirely {\i mida\u8217'atah}, of her own volition. Since she is no longer a minor over whom her father exercises custodial authority, and since she is the initiating party in this marriage, Reuven is not the one transferring anything to Shimon. The daughter is marrying herself off independently, and Reuven is not regarded as conferring any benefit upon Shimon through this act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran adds an important nuance: the {\i chiddush} of the phrase {\i umida\u8217'atah} is not merely that the father is uninvolved, but that Shimon himself is also not the initiator. If Shimon were to approach Reuven and ask him to persuade his adult daughter to accept his proposal, Reuven would be performing a service for Shimon, which would itself constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah} and would therefore be forbidden. The ruling is thus conditioned on the daughter acting entirely on her own initiative, with no orchestration from either father or groom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion also touches briefly on the distinction between {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin}. The verb used in the teaching \u8212- {\i lehasi\u8217'o et bito}, to \u8220"marry off\u8221" his daughter \u8212- implies the full {\i nisuin}, the actual commencement of cohabitation. The obligations of a wife attendant to {\i shmisha} and domestic life attach only after {\i nisuin}, not merely after {\i kiddushin}. The Gemara acknowledges that while the father technically effects the {\i kiddushin} for a minor daughter, the {\i nisuin} \u8212- actually taking her to live in the husband\u8217's home \u8212- is a more complex matter, and practically, the father\u8217's role in transferring even a minor daughter does seem to extend to that stage as well, as the language of {\i masra avi hakallah} (the bride\u8217's father \u8220"hands over\u8221" his daughter) in Ketubot suggests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited that confirms the ruling: if one is forbidden to receive benefit from another, he may nonetheless marry off his adult daughter to that person, provided she is acting of her own accord.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father & Son Study Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov teaches: {\i hamadir beno letalmud Torah} \u8212- one who takes a vow {\i regarding} his son in connection with Torah study. The Ran explains this on the basis of a supporting {\i Tosefta}: the father declares that he will derive no benefit from his son. The purpose of this declaration is to free the son entirely from the burdens of {\i kibud av va\u8217'em} \u8212- honoring his father \u8212- so that he need not interrupt his Torah study to attend to the father\u8217's needs. The {\i mefaresh} (the commentary in place of Rashi on this tractate) offers an alternative reading: the father vows that all his property will become forbidden to the son if the son does not devote himself to Torah study \u8212- a leveraged threat designed to compel the son toward learning. The shiur reads primarily in accordance with the Ran\u8217's interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that reading, the Gemara rules that the son may nonetheless still fill a barrel of water for his father ({\i lemallos lo chavis shemayim}) and light a candle for him ({\i lehadlik lo et haner}). These would plainly seem to fall within the category of {\i hana\u8217'ah}, yet the Gemara permits them. This is a striking departure from the otherwise formalistic framework the tractate has employed. Until this point the analysis has been essentially structural: does the benefit flow directly, does it pay off an obligation, does it fatten an asset? Here, however, the Gemara deploys context. In the relationship of a father and son, and given the explicit purpose of the vow \u8212- to enable Torah study rather than to create estrangement \u8212- one must assume that certain basic domestic services were never intended to be included in the declaration, even if the words would technically encompass them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak then goes further, stating that the son may even fry a small fish ({\i litzlos lo dag katan}) for his father. This only sharpens the question of where the line is drawn. The answer, it emerges, is that no formal line can be drawn by recourse to the words of the vow alone; one must instead ask what the parties could reasonably be presumed to have intended given the context of their relationship and the stated purpose of the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context & Implied Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preceding cases crystallize a methodological tension. For most of the tractate, the rules governing {\i hana\u8217'ah} prohibitions have been applied formally and categorically. But the Gemara here introduces the principle that context can effectively remove certain actions from the scope of a prohibition, even when the literal language would include them. The operative question becomes: what would the person who took the vow actually have intended to include?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran articulates the governing distinction with precision: no use of context can permit the actual transfer of an object of value from one party to another. If the son cannot give his fish to his father, he cannot. The contextual reasoning operates only at the level of {\i services} \u8212- acts of assistance or labor that, given the nature of the relationship and the purpose of the vow, can be presumed to have been implicitly excluded. Where the vow was taken in the spirit of advancing Torah study rather than imposing a relational rupture, certain elemental services between father and son fall outside its scope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cup of Peace & Consolation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya citing Rabbi Yochanan teaches: one who is forbidden to benefit from another ({\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro}) may nonetheless fill a cup of greeting for him \u8212- a {\i kos shel shalom}. This ruling is not limited to the father-son relationship but applies generically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what precisely is a {\i kos shel shalom}? In Babylonia ({\i hacha targimu}) it is understood as the cup filled during {\i nichum aveilim} \u8212- consoling a mourner. As part of the visit to a house of mourning, one fills the {\i avel}\u8217's cup for him, and this customary act of comfort is deemed not to constitute prohibited {\i hana\u8217'ah}. The Ran adds the important qualification that in this case, as in the earlier examples, it must be the {\i avel}\u8217's own water being used. Giving one\u8217's own water or food to someone would always constitute a direct transfer of a valuable object and would be forbidden regardless of context; it is only the {\i service} of pouring and filling that is being exempted. In the West ({\i b\u8217'ma\u8217'arava amri}), the {\i kos shel shalom} is understood even more broadly as the cup of water offered after a bath \u8212- the act of handing someone water after they emerge from a {\i beit hamerchataz}. Even this minimal, customary courtesy falls outside the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale, as the Ran frames it, is {\i darchei shalom} \u8212- the ways of peace. To refuse such a basic act of human interaction, given that {\i nedarim} are so often born of interpersonal conflict, would be so profoundly contrary to basic norms of civil society that one could never have intended to include such things in the vow. This again confirms the governing principle: objects of value may not be transferred, but contextually assumed services of a basic, customary, or humane character remain permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Servants vs.\u160 ?Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the Mishnah\u8217's prohibition on feeding the animals, a {\i baraita} is cited in the name of Yehoshua Ish Uzza, who draws a further distinction: one may feed another\u8217's Canaanite male and female servants ({\i avadim} and {\i shfachot}), but one may not feed his animals, whether kosher or non-kosher.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks for the underlying logic of this distinction. The answer is that Canaanite servants are fed in order to perform their tasks ({\i lemancharusa avdin}) \u8212- they do household work, carry out labor, and the feeding simply maintains their capacity for service. It does not appreciably increase their monetary value. Animals, by contrast, exist to be fattened ({\i behemah lefituma avida}); providing them with food increases their weight and thus directly increases the asset value of the owner\u8217's estate. This is a concrete, direct {\i hana\u8217'ah} that is impermissible regardless of whether the animal is kosher or not \u8212- since even a non-kosher animal can be sold, e.g.\u160 ?as animal fodder, and its fattened state increases what it commands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction being drawn is thus analogous to the question of whether changing someone\u8217's oil constitutes giving them {\i hana\u8217'ah}: preventive maintenance that forestalls decline is not the same as an act that affirmatively increases value. Feeding the servants falls in the category of maintenance; feeding the animals falls in the category of value-enhancement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Visiting the Sick\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stand vs.\u160 ?Sit Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next Mishnah: {\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro venichnas levakro} \u8212- one who is forbidden to receive benefit from another may nevertheless enter and pay him a {\i bikur cholim} call when he is sick. The Mishnah then qualifies: he may stand ({\i omed}), but he may not sit ({\i aval lo yoshev}). The difference between standing and sitting is not immediately self-evident and will occupy the Gemara at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refuat Nefesh & Mamon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah continues: if the visitor is a physician, he may provide {\i refuat nefesh} \u8212- medical care for the sick person\u8217's body \u8212- but he may not provide {\i refuat mamon}, care for the person\u8217's property or animals (i.e., veterinary care). This distinction is striking: rendering medical assistance to a human being, which plainly produces tangible physical benefit, is permitted, while veterinary care, directed at the person\u8217's assets, is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The intuition the distinction tracks is the same one underlying the entire tractate: {\i hana\u8217'ah} in the relevant sense is measured in financial and property terms. Care for another person\u8217's body, even when medically significant, falls into the category of a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- it is not the kind of benefit the law of {\i nedarim} contemplates as forbidden. Care for another\u8217's animals, however, directly preserves or augments the value of his property and is therefore impermissible. The term {\i refuat nefesh} (literally, healing of the person) is used here in contradistinction to {\i refuat mamon} (healing of property) rather than to {\i refuat haguf} (healing of the body); the liturgical pairing of {\i refuat hanefesh verefuat haguf} in the {\i Mi Shebeirach} is a later development and not the framework operative here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fee-Based Services\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens the analysis by identifying whose property is forbidden to whom, since the determination controls the resolution. If it is Shimon\u8217's property that is forbidden to Reuven (i.e., Reuven cannot benefit from Shimon), then when Shimon comes to pay Reuven a {\i bikur cholim} visit, Shimon is performing a {\i mitzvah} and giving Reuven no direct object of value at all. In that case, the Gemara asks: why should it matter whether he stands or sits? He should be permitted to do either. On the other hand, if it is Reuven\u8217's property that is forbidden to Shimon, then Shimon\u8217's very entry into Reuven\u8217's house involves enjoying Reuven\u8217's property \u8212- a right of access that would itself constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- and Shimon should not even be permitted to stand there, let alone sit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore has Shmuel resolve the question: the Mishnah is speaking in the first direction \u8212- Reuven cannot benefit from Shimon. The distinction between standing and sitting corresponds to whether a fee is conventionally charged. In a place where people are paid for paying a {\i bikur cholim} call, the fee attaches to sitting \u8212- spending real time with the sick person \u8212- but not to merely dropping in ({\i uvemakom shenotnim sachar leyeshiva ve\u8217'ein notnim sachar al amidah}). Standing is the basic performance of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i bikur cholim}; demanding a fee for it would be like demanding payment for teaching Torah. Sitting represents a more extended, quasi-professional engagement, the kind for which chaplaincy-style compensation would be appropriate, and by not charging for it the visitor effectively donates the monetary equivalent of that fee to Reuven \u8212- which constitutes impermissible {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what is the actual distinction, and why does sitting generate a fee obligation? The Gemara suggests that just as one may not forgo the right to charge for teaching {\i Torah she-bikhtav} when that right exists (since doing so gives the student a monetary benefit), one may not forgo the right to charge for prolonged bedside presence when that right conventionally exists. Standing is the {\i mitzvah}; sitting goes beyond it and enters the domain of compensable service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises an alternative: even according to the approach of Rabbi Shimon ben Yakim (invoked in a closely related context several dapim later), one might argue that even in a place where sitting is not conventionally charged for, sitting would still be forbidden \u8212- as a {\i gezeira} lest one remain too long, at which point the extended stay would be the kind of thing that would normally attract a fee. The underlying logic is the same throughout: the {\i hana\u8217'ah} consists in forgoing a fee that would otherwise be owed, and the line between permitted standing and forbidden sitting tracks the line between that which is conventionally compensable and that which is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Essential Well-Being\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla offers an alternative reading of the Mishnah. Rather than saying that the Mishnah speaks in the direction where Reuven cannot benefit from Shimon, Ulla maintains that the Mishnah speaks in the opposite direction \u8212- it is Reuven\u8217's property that is forbidden to Shimon ({\i b\u8217'nichsei choleh asurin al hamevaker}). On this reading, Shimon in principle ought not to enter Reuven\u8217's house at all, since walking on another\u8217's property is a form of benefiting from it. Yet the Mishnah permits the visit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran explains Ulla\u8217's position as a further application of the contextual principle introduced earlier. When Reuven declared that Shimon may not benefit from his property, he obviously did not intend to exclude things essential to his own well-being. Just as the father making a vow about his son\u8217's Torah study cannot have intended to exclude having a barrel of water filled, and just as the person who made a vow during a conflict cannot have intended to exclude a basic cup of courtesy water after a bath, Reuven cannot have intended to forbid Shimon from entering his home to pay him a {\i bikur cholim} visit when he is lying ill.\u160 ?That falls so squarely within what anyone in Reuven\u8217's position would want that it is implicitly excluded from the vow. Sitting, however, is not essential; that remains forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Person vs.\u160 ?Family Visit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then introduced that distinguishes between visiting the sick person himself and visiting his child. The {\i baraita} rules: if the sick person falls ill, the visitor may enter and see him. But if the sick person\u8217's son falls ill, the visitor may only inquire after the son\u8217's health in the marketplace \u8212- he may not enter the house to visit the son. The Gemara notes that this distinction fits seamlessly with Ulla\u8217's approach: Reuven\u8217's property is off-limits to Shimon except insofar as entry is essential to Reuven\u8217's own well-being. Shimon entering to see Reuven himself \u8212- the person whose property is at stake \u8212- is something Reuven would obviously have wanted to carve out of his declaration. Shimon entering to see Reuven\u8217's son is not essential to Reuven\u8217's well-being, so it remains forbidden, and Shimon is limited to asking after the son\u8217's welfare from the public domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direction of Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Shmuel\u8217's reading of the Mishnah, however, this {\i baraita} poses a difficulty. If the Mishnah speaks in the direction where Reuven cannot benefit from Shimon, then Shimon\u8217's visiting the son should be perfectly permissible \u8212- he is not giving Reuven any {\i hana\u8217'ah} by doing so, any more than he gives {\i hana\u8217'ah} by feeding Reuven\u8217's daughter or paying off Reuven\u8217's creditors. Indeed the Ran notes that if anything, visiting the son should be {\i more} permissible than visiting Reuven himself, since visiting Reuven directly involves providing him a service that might normally be charged for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension by distinguishing the two sources: the Mishnah, Shmuel concedes, is indeed best read in his direction (Reuven cannot benefit from Shimon), and on that reading visiting the son would be permitted. The {\i baraita}, by contrast, is to be read in Ulla\u8217's direction (Reuven\u8217's property is forbidden to Shimon), and on that reading the son\u8217's visit is naturally prohibited since it is not essential to Reuven\u8217's well-being. The Mishnah and the {\i baraita} thus speak to different configurations of the vow, and the apparent contradiction dissolves once the direction of each prohibition is properly identified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, citing Shmuel, adds that this reading is actually compelled by the language of the Mishnah itself: the Mishnah\u8217's specific formulation distinguishing {\i standing} from {\i sitting} makes sense only in Shmuel\u8217's direction. If the prohibition ran the other way (Reuven\u8217's property forbidden to Shimon), the distinction between standing and sitting would be irrelevant \u8212- either the entire visit is excluded as essential to Reuven\u8217's well-being, in which case sitting too should be permitted, or it is not so excluded, in which case even standing should be forbidden. The graduated distinction the Mishnah draws between standing and sitting is intelligible only on the premise that Reuven is forbidden to benefit from Shimon, and the question is which forms of Shimon\u8217's service constitute a benefit being waived.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biblical Sources & Theol\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korach & Visitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now shifts into a more aggadic register to explore {\i bikur cholim} as a theological and biblical value. Reish Lakish asks: where does the Torah provide a {\i remez}, a hint, for the institution of {\i bikur cholim}? This question is set against the Rambam\u8217's formulation that {\i bikur cholim} is itself a fulfillment of the biblical commandment {\i ve\u8217'ahavta lere\u8217'acha kamocha} \u8212- love your neighbor as yourself. As the Rambam explains, the rabbis gave very concrete, specific expression to this sweeping commandment through identified obligations such as {\i bikur cholim}, {\i nichum aveilim}, {\i hachnasas orchim}, and {\i hachnasas kallah}. There is a profound rationale to this concretization: a commandment as all-encompassing as {\i ve\u8217'ahavta lere\u8217'acha kamocha} risks becoming so diffuse as to generate no actual sense of obligation. By naming specific, identifiable expressions of it, the rabbis gave the commandment actionable force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish locates the scriptural anchor for {\i bikur cholim} in the Korach narrative. Moses declares: {\i im kemot kol ha\u8217'adam yemutun eileh ufekudat kol ha\u8217'adam yipaked aleihem, lo Hashem shelachani} \u8212- \u8220"if these men die as all men die, and the fate of all mankind befalls them, then God has not sent me\u8221" (Bamidbar 16:29). The word {\i ufekudat}, from the root meaning to visit or to attend to, is understood here as referring specifically to the customary visits people receive while lying ill.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death & Visiting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava elaborates on Moses\u8217' meaning. The phrase {\i kemot kol ha\u8217'adam} \u8212- dying as all people die \u8212- describes the normal arc of human mortality: one falls ill, lies in one\u8217's sickbed, and people come to visit. The phrase {\i ufekudat kol ha\u8217'adam yipaked aleihem} then refers to this act of visitation. Moses is thus saying: if the men of Korach\u8217's faction die in the ordinary way, lying sick with visitors attending them, then people will say that God did not send me, that this was nothing more than a natural death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Pre-World Creations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads into the famous aggadah regarding Gehinnom. Moses says, {\i im briah yivra Hashem} \u8212- if God will create a creation, namely that the earth will open its mouth and swallow Korach\u8217's company. Rava interprets this conditionally: if Gehinnom has already been created, well and good \u8212- let it be used here; and if not, let God create it now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this: is it really conceivable that Gehinnom had not yet been created? A {\i baraita} teaches that seven things were created before the world itself was made: {\i Torah, teshuva, Gan Eden, Gehinnom, kisei hakavod, beit hamikdash}, and the name of {\i mashiach}. The Ran notes that this should not be taken entirely literally \u8212- prior to the world\u8217's creation, nothing was literally created in a spatial sense \u8212- but that these seven were part of the foundational plan and intention of creation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The theological statement embedded in this list is striking. {\i Torah} is cited from Mishlei 8:22, where Wisdom \u8212- understood as Torah \u8212- declares that God acquired it as the first of His paths. {\i Teshuva} is anchored in Tehillim 90:2-3, where even before the mountains were born, the possibility of human return was already inscribed: {\i tashev enosh ad daka} \u8212- \u8220"You return man to contrition,\u8221" read as God\u8217's provision for human fallibility existing before the world itself. The pre-existence of {\i teshuva} is among the most profound statements in the aggadic literature: free will and the capacity for moral renewal were intrinsic to the design of creation, not afterthoughts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Gan Eden} is derived from Bereishit 2:8, where God planted the garden {\i mikedem} \u8212- from the beginning \u8212- understood here not merely as the primordial paradise of Adam and Eve but as the eschatological promise of reward. {\i Gehinnom} is derived from Yeshayahu 30:33, {\i ki aruch me\u8217'etmol Tofteh} \u8212- \u8220"for yesterday Tofteh is arranged\u8221" \u8212- establishing that the reality of consequence and judgment preceded the world as well. {\i Kisei hakavod} \u8212- the divine throne \u8212- is derived from Tehillim 93:2, {\i nachon kis\u8217'acha mei\u8217'az} \u8212- \u8220"Your throne was established of old.\u8221" The {\i beit hamikdash} is derived from Yirmiyahu 17:12, {\i kisei kavod marom mei\u8217'rishon} \u8212- \u8220"a throne of glory on high from the beginning,\u8221" understood as the terrestrial expression of divine sovereignty, complementing the heavenly {\i kisei hakavod}. And the name of the {\i mashiach} is derived from Tehillim 72:17, {\i yehi shemo le\u8217'olam} \u8212- \u8220"may his name endure forever\u8221" \u8212- the assurance that history has a redemptive direction built into it from before the beginning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gehinnom Creation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that the {\i baraita} establishes Gehinnom as one of the seven pre-world creations, what can Moses have meant when he said {\i yivra Hashem} \u8212- let God create it? The Gemara resolves this in two stages. First, it reframes Moses\u8217' conditional: what Moses said was that if {\i Gehinnom\u8217's mouth} \u8212- the specific opening into which Korach and his company would fall \u8212- has already been positioned here, good; and if not, let God create it. This narrows the question from whether Gehinnom exists to whether a mouth of Gehinnom happens to be located at this precise spot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a further objection: {\i ein kol chadash tachat hashemesh} \u8212- there is nothing new under the sun (Kohelet 1:9). How could even the mouth of Gehinnom be newly created? The final resolution: Moses was not asking for a new creation but for a {\i relocation} \u8212- let the mouth of Gehinnom that exists elsewhere be moved to this spot. Moving an existing feature is not the creation of something entirely new.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sun & Moon Strike\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava expounds on the verse from Chavakuk 3:11: {\i shemesh yarei\u8217'ach amad zvula} \u8212- the sun and moon stood in their lofty abode. The Gemara asks what the sun and moon were doing in {\i Zvul}, one of the upper heavens, when they are normally fixed in the {\i rakia}, the visible firmament. The answer is that they had ascended from the {\i rakia} to {\i Zvul} to lodge a protest before God. During Moses\u8217' confrontation with Korach, the sun and moon appeared before God and declared: Master of the Universe, if You execute judgment on behalf of the son of Amram, we will continue to give light; and if You do not vindicate Moses\u8217' honor, we will not shine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God\u8217's response was to hurl arrows and spears at them ({\i yara vahen chitzim ve\u8217'chanitot}). The divine rebuke follows: every single day the nations bow down to you and you continue to illuminate the world for them without objection, yet for My honor you raise no protest \u8212- and only for the honor of a human being do you threaten to go on strike? Return to your posts. The sun and moon had no answer and returned. From that day forward, Rava teaches, God must each day hurl arrows and spears at the sun and moon to compel them to fulfill their function \u8212- which is why the verse in Chavakuk describes them: {\i l\u8217'or chitzecha yeileichu} \u8212- \u8220"at the light of Your arrows they move.\u8221" The shiur concludes here, with the discussion of {\i bikur cholim} and its associated aggadot to be continued on the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 40\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bikur Cholim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Limit to Bikur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 39b opens with a {\i baraisa}: {\i bikur cholim ein lah shiur} \u8212- there is no limit to the obligation of {\i bikur cholim}. The immediate question is: what precisely does this mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef proposed that {\i ein lah shiur} refers to the reward \u8212- that the reward for {\i bikur cholim} is limitless. Abaye pushes back sharply: is {\i bikur cholim} unique in this respect? Is there any {\i mitzvah} whose reward is capped? The Mishnah itself teaches {\i hevei zahir bemitzvah kalah kebachamurah she\u8217'ein atah yodeia matan secharan shel mitzvot} \u8212- be as scrupulous about a light {\i mitzvah} as about a weighty one, because we do not know the reward for the various {\i mitzvot}. That very formulation implies that all {\i mitzvot} may have unlimited or at least unknowable reward. To single out {\i bikur cholim} would imply the others do not share this quality, which is untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gadol Etzel Katan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye therefore offers a different interpretation: {\i afilu gadol etzel katan} \u8212- the statement that there is no limit to {\i bikur cholim} means that there is no threshold of social status that exempts one from performing it. A person of great standing cannot say that it would be beneath his dignity to visit someone of a lower station in life. The obligation admits of no such calculus. One sees this instinct at work during {\i shiva} calls as well \u8212- an important person does not decline to make a condolence call simply because the mourner is less prominent. The same holds for {\i bikur cholim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Visits & Healing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers yet another reading of {\i ein lah shiur}: {\i afilu me\u8217'ah pe\u8217'amim bayom} \u8212- even one hundred times in a day. Having visited once, a person cannot simply check the obligation off his list. If the sick person needs human contact and no one else is providing it, the obligation to return remains. This parallels the formulation used elsewhere \u8212- {\i hakeim takem imo afilu me\u8217'ah pe\u8217'amim} \u8212- where the same act must be repeated as many times as circumstances demand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites Rav Acha bar Chanina: {\i kol mevaker choleh notel echad meshishim betza\u8217'aro} \u8212- whoever visits a sick person removes one-sixtieth of his suffering. (Some versions read {\i michol\u8217'yo}, of his illness, rather than {\i betza\u8217'aro}, of his anguish \u8212- a meaningful distinction, as the latter implies the visitor absorbs the suffering rather than eliminating it.) The Gemara immediately raises the obvious objection: if this is so, why not simply bring sixty visitors and cure the patient entirely? The answer is that this formula operates like the rule of {\i issur nechasim} from the house of Rebbi \u8212- in which each daughter\u8217's portion is calculated as a tenth of the remainder after the previous portions have been taken, rather than a flat tenth of the original estate. Similarly, each visitor removes a sixtieth of what remains of the suffering, not a sixtieth of the original whole, so that sixty visitors in sequence would not bring the patient to zero. Even if all sixty come simultaneously, the result is calculated as though they came sequentially and divided equally among them. Moreover, the effect applies only when the visitor is a {\i ben gilo} \u8212- someone of a cognate {\i mazal} \u8212- who possesses some sympathetic or harmonizing influence over the sick person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chelbo & R. Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chelbo fell ill.\u160 ?Rav Kahana went and made a public announcement \u8212- {\i Rav Chelbo bish}, Rav Chelbo is sick \u8212- but no one came to visit. Rav Kahana then rebuked the people: did they not know the story of one of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's students who fell ill and the Sages did not come to visit him? Rabbi Akiva himself came, and because he honored the student \u8212- sweeping the floor and settling the dust of the room \u8212- {\i chaya}, the student recovered. The student said to him: {\i Rebbi, hechiyasani} \u8212- you have brought me back to life. Rabbi Akiva then went out and taught publicly: {\i kol mi she\u8217'eino mevaker} \u8212- anyone who does not visit the sick is as though he is spilling blood, since the act of visiting sustains the sick person\u8217's life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting what Rav Kahana\u8217's rebuke conspicuously does not record: that the people, having heard the {\i mussar}, then went and prayed for Rav Chelbo or visited him. The moral instruction stands on its own, whether or not it was successful in motivating action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prayer & End-of-Life\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Dimi came from {\i Eretz Yisrael}, he reported: {\i kol hamevaker chole gorem lo sheyichye} \u8212- whoever visits the sick causes him to live; {\i vechol mi she\u8217'eino mevaker es hachole yesh gorem lo sheyamus} \u8212- whoever does not visit causes him to die. The {\i Gemara} immediately scrutinizes the word {\i gorem}. Does it mean that the non-visitor actively prays for the patient\u8217's death? {\i Sheyamus salka deitach?} \u8212- can one seriously entertain that thought? Rather, the {\i Gemara} explains: one who does not visit simply does not pray \u8212- not for the patient\u8217's recovery, and not for his death. The passivity is the culpable act. By not being present, one never reaches the point of praying for the person, and therefore one bears some moral responsibility if the patient deteriorates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking pivot in the Gemara\u8217's framing. The earlier passages \u8212- Rabbi Akiva sweeping the floor, Rav Acha bar Chanina\u8217's one-sixtieth principle \u8212- point to very concrete, practical dimensions of {\i bikur cholim}: attending to physical needs, providing human contact, emotional presence. Now the {\i stama deGemara} reframes the entire institution around prayer. One might ask why the Gemara makes this shift. The answer is left implicit, but the effect is the same: whether through practical care or {\i tefilah}, presence sustains the sick person, and absence \u8212- even if not malicious \u8212- is a form of abandonment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point there is a remarkable {\i Ran} that carries great significance for discussions of medical ethics. The {\i Ran} begins: {\i nireh be\u8217'einai dehachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- it seems to me that this is what the Gemara is driving at. He writes that {\i pe\u8217'amim shetzarich levakesh rachamim al hachole sheyamus} \u8212- there are times when one ought to pray for a sick person to die. Specifically, when someone is {\i mitzta\u8217'er} greatly \u8212- in severe suffering \u8212- and {\i ve\u8217'i efshar lo sheyichye} \u8212- there is no possibility of recovery \u8212- then the most one can wish for this person is a swift and painless death, not a prolonged dying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} grounds this in the famous passage from {\i Perek Hanose} about Rabbi\u8217's maidservant. She observed that Rebbi was in great distress \u8212- having to go to the bathroom repeatedly and replace his {\i tefillin} each time \u8212- while his students continued to pray fervently for his recovery. She prayed: {\i yehi ratzon sheyachofu elyonim es hatachtonim} \u8212- may it be the will that the upper spheres overcome the lower spheres. The students\u8217' prayers were the lower spheres; clearly in heaven, it was Rebbi\u8217's time to die. She effectively prayed that his death not be prolonged by the students\u8217' well-intentioned but ultimately misdirected {\i tefilot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} then applies this to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's language: one who visits the sick person helps even through prayer \u8212- {\i afilu lehachyos}, even toward recovery if that is possible \u8212- {\i mipnei shehi tefilla yoser mo\u8217'eles}, because prayer offered in the presence of the patient is more effective. But one who does not visit fails on both counts: not only does he fail to pray for recovery when recovery is possible, but {\i afilu heicha de-ika lahna be-mituta} \u8212- even where the more modest prayer is called for, praying that the person should be able to die without prolonged suffering \u8212- {\i afilu ha nami lo mahanya}, even that prayer will not be effective, because it was never offered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} thus identifies a category of prayer for death that is not only permitted but at times obligatory \u8212- not a prayer that the person should die in a malicious sense, but a prayer that death, when it is the right outcome, should come gently and without delay. This has become foundational in discussions of end-of-life {\i halacha}. The central orienting principle is the distinction between prolonging life and prolonging death \u8212- one does not wish to stand in the way of a natural process of dying, but equally one does not interrupt interventions that are genuinely sustaining life. The {\i Ran} articulates this with precision: when recovery is not possible, the most one can do is pray that the passing be swift and merciful. The further question of whether a patient has personal autonomy in these decisions is a distinct and layered discussion. Rav Moshe Feinstein found a kind of indirect approach, arguing that when a patient\u8217's wishes are overridden, the resulting anguish can itself worsen the medical condition \u8212- so that respecting the patient\u8217's will is often the medically as well as halakhically indicated course. This argument does not translate easily to cases where the patient is unconscious, which is precisely where the hardest questions arise. The status of a {\i goses} \u8212- one already in the active process of dying \u8212- is a related but distinct category, involving a traditional prohibition against interfering with the natural process at that stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's own practice regarding illness is instructive. On the first day he fell ill, he told those around him: {\i lo tiglu le-inshei} \u8212- do not tell people, {\i de-lo litra mazali} \u8212- so that his {\i mazal} should not be weakened. There is a sense that publicizing an illness makes it more of a concrete reality, and Rava wished to leave open the possibility that it would pass quickly and quietly. But once it became clear the illness was not passing, he reversed course entirely: {\i puku v\u8217'akhrizu b\u8217'shuka} \u8212- go and announce it in the marketplace. Two reasons converge here. First, {\i de\u8217'chol de\u8217'sani li richdu li} \u8212- those who hate him will rejoice at the news of his illness. Far from being a problem, Rava welcomes this, because the verse says {\i binfol oyvecha al tismach\u8230 ? pen yireh Hashem v\u8217'ra b\u8217'einav v\u8217'heshiv me\u8217'alav apo} \u8212- do not rejoice when your enemy falls, lest God see your rejoicing and remove His anger from your enemy. If Rava\u8217's enemies rejoice at his illness, God\u8217's attention will shift toward them and away from Rava, working to his benefit. Second, {\i u\u8217'derachim li libai rachamei} \u8212- those who love him will pray for him. Either way Rava gains. This passage carries a layer of biographical texture: Rava was known to have many detractors in {\i Mahuza}, a cosmopolitan city where he was an outspoken rabbinic authority whose rebuke was not always welcomed. His equanimity in invoking his enemies\u8217' anticipated pleasure is itself remarkable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rewards for Visiting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then enumerates the rewards for {\i bikur cholim}. Rav teaches: {\i kol hamivakeir et hacholeh nitzol midina shel Gehennam} \u8212- whoever visits the sick is saved from the punishment of {\i Gehennam}. The proof-text is {\i ashrei maskil el dal} \u8212- praised is the one who attends to the {\i dal}, the downtrodden or the poor, who here is understood to refer to the sick person, {\i ein dal ela choleh}, as supported by the verse {\i midala yivtza\u8217'eni}. {\i Biyom ra\u8217'ah yimalteihu Hashem} \u8212- on the day of evil, God will save him \u8212- and {\i ein ra\u8217'ah ela Gehennam}, \u8220"evil\u8221" here denotes {\i Gehennam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: is being saved from {\i Gehennam} not reward enough? What about reward in this world? The verse continues: {\i Hashem yishmereihu v\u8217'ichayeihu v\u8217'ushar ba\u8217'aretz ve\u8217'al titneihu b\u8217'nefesh oyvav} \u8212- God will preserve him and give him life and bless him in the land and not deliver him into the hand of his enemies. Each phrase corresponds to an aspect of the {\i bikur cholim} experience. {\i Hashem yishmereihu} \u8212- God will protect him from the {\i yeitzer hara}. {\i V\u8217'ichayeihu} \u8212- God will preserve him from suffering. {\i V\u8217'ushar ba\u8217'aretz} \u8212- that all will honor him, which the {\i Maharsha} connects back to the earlier principle of {\i gadol etzel katan}: just as even the great visit the lowly, so too will the one who visits be honored in turn by all strata of society.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i ve\u8217'al titneihu b\u8217'nefesh oyvav} \u8212- and not deliver him into the hand of his enemies \u8212- is phrased in terms of {\i nefesh} rather than simply \u8220"enemies,\u8221" to convey something about the quality of one\u8217's friendships. The Gemara says {\i sheyihiyu lo rei\u8217'im keNa\u8217'aman sheripu es tzaraso} \u8212- may he have friends like those of Na\u8217'aman who helped cure him of his affliction. This is precisely what {\i bikur cholim} embodies: being a friend who shows up when someone has a problem. Conversely, {\i ve\u8217'al yizdamnu lo rei\u8217'im keRechavam shechilku es hamalchus} \u8212- may he not have friends like those of Rechavam, whose advice led to the splitting of the kingdom. The {\i nefesh} language alludes to the deep connection between a person and his advisors, whose counsel can either sustain or destroy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} expands on this: Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar teaches that if young people tell you to build and elders tell you to tear down, listen to the elders \u8212- because {\i binyan yeladim stirah}, the building of the young is ultimately a destruction, and {\i stiras zkeinim binyan}, the tearing down of the elders is ultimately a construction. The paradigm is Rechavam ben Shlomo, who listened to the young advisors who urged him to increase the people\u8217's burdens, with catastrophic consequences for the kingdom \u8212- while the elders\u8217' advice, which might have seemed like a weakening of royal authority, would in fact have preserved it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing & Divine Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshes the son of Rav Idi teaches: a person should not visit a sick person in the first three hours of the day, nor in the last three hours of the day. The reason: {\i ki heichi delo lisach da\u8217'ateih min rachamei} \u8212- so that he not lose the impulse to pray. In the morning ({\i tlas sha\u8217'ei kadmayasa}), the sick person often feels a temporary surge of energy and appears relatively well, so the visitor might conclude there is no urgent need for prayer. In the evening ({\i basrayasa}), when {\i takif chulsheih} \u8212- the illness is at its most acute \u8212- the visitor might feel that the situation is already hopeless and prayer futile. The middle of the day is therefore the appropriate time: the visitor sees the person in a state that properly motivates prayer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth observing what this implies. If {\i bikur cholim} were primarily about practical assistance and emotional support, the final hours of the day \u8212- when the patient is most distressed \u8212- would be precisely when the visit is most needed. The fact that the Gemara discourages visits at that time shows that prayer is being treated as the central function of the visit, and the timing is calibrated to maximize its effectiveness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravin cites Rav for two further teachings. First: {\i minayin she\u8217'Hakadosh Baruch Hu zan es hacholeh} \u8212- how do we know that God sustains the sick person? From the verse {\i Hashem yisadeinu al eres devai} \u8212- God supports him on the bed of suffering \u8212- linking {\i sod} to the concept of {\i seuda}, sustenance. When a sick person cannot eat, it is God who sustains him. Second: {\i minayin she\u8217'hashechina shruya lemala mi\u8217'mitaso shel choleh} \u8212- how do we know that the divine presence hovers above the sick person? From the same verse: God supports him, which implies a physical presence above the bed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} makes this practically operative: when visiting the sick, one should not sit on a couch, stool, or chair \u8212- {\i ella mita\u8217'atef ve\u8217'yoshev al gabei karka} \u8212- but should wrap oneself and sit on the ground, because the {\i shechina} is present above the sick person\u8217's bed and one must not position oneself at the level of the divine presence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rivers & Mikvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Euphrates Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to what follows is that the preceding passage concluded with statements made in the name of Ravin citing Rav, and the Gemara now appends further statements attributed to the same chain of transmission. Ravin cites Rav: {\i mitra be\u8217'ma\u8217'arava sahada rabba Perat} \u8212- when there is rain in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, the great testimony to this is the Euphrates. When the Euphrates rises dramatically and its current runs fast and strong, this is evidence that there was heavy rainfall in Israel. The {\i mefarshim} explain that {\i Eretz Yisrael} sits at a higher elevation than the surrounding lands, so its rainfall runs off and eventually feeds into the Euphrates, causing it to swell. The observation has {\i halachic} significance, as we shall see.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Underground Springs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This statement of Ravin stands in tension with a view of Shmuel. Shmuel holds {\i nahara mikafih mitbarech} \u8212- a river draws its blessing from its bedrock, meaning that rivers are primarily fed by underground springs rather than by rainfall. Shmuel even states elsewhere that every drop of rain from above is met by two drops drawn up from below \u8212- so that even heavy rainfall does not fundamentally alter the character of river water, which remains predominantly spring-fed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halachic} stakes of this dispute are significant. A {\i mikvah} constructed from collected rainwater is valid only {\i be\u8217'ashboren} \u8212- when the water is settled and still, not flowing. A natural body of water fed by a spring, however \u8212- being a form of {\i mayim chayim}, living water \u8212- is valid even when moving ({\i zochalin}). If a river is primarily spring-fed, it retains the status of {\i mayim chayim} and may be used even when its waters are in motion. But if a river\u8217's water is predominantly rainwater or melted snow, then the moving water cannot be used for {\i tahara}, since it would have the status of a {\i mikvah} with the disqualifying property of {\i zochalin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moving Water & Tishrei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's second statement, which contradicts his first, resolves the apparent consistency: {\i ein hamayim metaharim b\u8217'zochalin ela Perat miyomei Tishrei bilvad} \u8212- moving water is not valid for purification except for the Euphrates specifically during the month of Tishrei. The reasoning is that at other times of year there is cause for concern that the majority of the river\u8217's water derives from snowmelt (in spring) or from rainfall (in fall and winter), which would render the moving water invalid. Only during Tishrei \u8212- the fall, before the heavy rains arrive and after the snowmelt has subsided \u8212- can one be confident that the Euphrates\u8217's water is not predominantly rainwater, and only then is its moving water acceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This second statement of Shmuel effectively concedes the point made by Ravin citing Rav: rivers cannot be assumed to be primarily spring-fed, and one must account for the possibility that their majority is rainwater or snowmelt. The two statements of Shmuel therefore conflict with each other, and it is this second, more stringent position \u8212- along with Ravin\u8217's observation about the Euphrates \u8212- that defines the operative {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Mikvahs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's father gave practical expression to this concern. {\i Avuha d\u8217'Shmuel avid lehu mikvatah l\u8217'banatei b\u8217'yomei Nisan} \u8212- he constructed {\i mikvahs} for his daughters during the spring ({\i Nisan}), when snowmelt would swell the rivers and render their moving water suspect as predominantly rainwater-derived. {\i Umartzipi b\u8217'yomei Tishrei} \u8212- and during Tishrei, he provided them with mats. The mats served a practical purpose: using a riverbed directly is uncomfortable and potentially dangerous (sharp rocks, slippery surfaces), and walking on the riverbed could raise concerns of {\i chatzitzah}. By providing mats, he enabled his daughters to use the Euphrates in Tishrei \u8212- when it was permissible \u8212- without those complications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} elaborates considerably on the {\i halachic} application of these principles. He notes that even at normal water levels, if a river frequently runs dry, there may be reason to suspect that even its ordinary flow is primarily rainwater-fed, since a river sustained by underground springs would not typically dry up. He thus extends the concern beyond high-water periods. On the question of how to {\i pasken}, Rabbeinu Tam attempts to rule leniently in accordance with Shmuel\u8217's first position \u8212- that rivers are always primarily spring-fed \u8212- but the {\i Ran} and virtually all other {\i Rishonim} reject this, holding that wherever there is genuine concern about rainwater or snowmelt, the river may not be used as moving water. The {\i Ran} also discusses a second possible leniency: that within the river, one might distinguish between sections that would have been valid {\i mikvah}-water even without the additional rainwater (since their baseline flow was spring-fed) and sections that exist only because of the rainfall. According to this approach, the former sections might remain usable even when the overall water is a majority rainwater, since the rainwater did not create or define those sections. This is a nuanced position and the {\i Ran} treats it at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of all this is significant. Oceans and natural lakes, being stationary, generally do not raise these concerns \u8212- though one must verify that a given lake is not man-made. Rivers, however, present a real and ongoing issue, since they are moving and their water composition varies substantially by season, geography, and precipitation patterns.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exile & Poverty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels of Exile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a series of statements attributed to Ravin citing Rav, or in some textual traditions to Rabbi Ami citing Rav. The shift in topic is notable \u8212- these have nothing to do with {\i bikur cholim} or {\i Nedarim} \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} strings them together because they share the same chain of transmission. The verse in {\i Yechezkel} reads {\i ve\u8217'ata ben adam asei lecha k\u8217'lei gola} \u8212- and you, son of man, make for yourself vessels of exile. The {\i Gemara} identifies these minimal vessels as {\i ner}, a candle; {\i kearah}, a plate; and {\i shatiach}, a mat. These are the bare essentials for someone being driven from his home into exile, or in modern terms, the equivalent of the absolute minimum one needs to survive while displaced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lacking Everything\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads into an interpretation of the phrase {\i b\u8217'choser kol} \u8212- lacking everything \u8212- from {\i Devarim}. What does it mean to truly lack everything? Rabbi Ami citing Rav: {\i belo ner} \u8212- without even a candle \u8212- {\i belo shulchan}, without a table. Rav Hisda: {\i belo isha}, without a wife. Rav Sheshes: {\i belo shamash}, without an attendant \u8212- some household support. Rav Nachman: {\i belo de\u8217'ah}, without intellect. The {\i baraisa} adds: {\i belo melach}, without salt \u8212- the basic preservative and condiment \u8212- and {\i u\u8217'belo revav}, without something to dip one\u8217's bread in, some basic oil or fat. Each Amora identifies a different essential that, if missing, constitutes a state of total deprivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intellect & Sufficiency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye draws a striking conclusion from Rav Nachman\u8217's position: {\i naktinan ein ani ela b\u8217'de\u8217'ah} \u8212- we hold that the only true poverty is poverty of intellect. This resonates with the Mishnah\u8217's formulation {\i eizehu ashir hasameach b\u8217'chelko} \u8212- who is wealthy? One who is satisfied with his portion. Abaye\u8217's teaching inverts this: true poverty is not a matter of material lack but of the absence of the wisdom and understanding that allow a person to navigate life and find meaning and sufficiency within whatever circumstances he faces. If one has {\i de\u8217'ah}, one can manage everything else; without it, no material possession is truly adequate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Aramaic saying from the west ({\i be\u8217'ma\u8217'arava}) captures the same thought in aphoristic form: {\i da de-bi kula bi} \u8212- if you have this ({\i de\u8217'ah}, understanding), you have everything; {\i de-la da bi mah bi} \u8212- if you lack this, what do you truly have? {\i De-kane da kane mah chasar} \u8212- if you acquire this, what are you missing? {\i De-la kane mah kane} \u8212- and if you have not acquired this, what have you acquired? The rhetorical structure reinforces the point: the possession of intellect and understanding is the one truly constitutive good, in the absence of which everything else is hollow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 42\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah: Nedarim & Shemi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 42. We continue the discussion of what is or is not forbidden to someone who is {\i assur} \u8212- who cannot derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} from another person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Before Shemitah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah opens: {\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechavero lifnei shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- one who is forbidden from deriving benefit from his fellow before {\i shevi\u8217'it}. This Mishnah turns out to be as much about {\i shevi\u8217'it} as it is about {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}. The ruling is: {\i eino yored letoch sadehu ve\u8217'eino ochel min haneti\u8217'ot} \u8212- he may neither enter the field nor eat from branches overhanging outside the field boundary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As we have discussed previously, {\i hana\u8217'ah} is not limited to eating the fruit. Walking through someone\u8217's yard \u8212- the right of access to another\u8217's property \u8212- is itself a form of {\i hana\u8217'ah}. Trespassing on property one is forbidden to use constitutes a violation. Accordingly, someone forbidden from {\i hana\u8217'ah} may neither enter the field nor pluck fruit from overhanging branches outside it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: During Shemitah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then states: {\i uveshevi\u8217'it} \u8212- and during {\i shevi\u8217'it}. This clause admits two readings, and the Gemara will explore them at length. On one reading, the {\i neder} was taken before {\i shemitah} and the question is what happens once {\i shemitah} arrives. On another reading, the {\i neder} itself was taken {\i on shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- meaning Reuven made the vow during the sabbatical year itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On {\i shevi\u8217'it}, the ruling is: {\i eino yored letoch sadehu} \u8212- he still cannot enter the field \u8212- {\i aval ochel hu min haneti\u8217'ot hanotos} \u8212- but he may eat from the overhanging fruit. Why? Because on {\i shevi\u8217'it}, the fruit is no longer Reuven\u8217's to forbid. The fruit belongs, in a meaningful sense, to everyone; it is not within the owner\u8217's power to make it forbidden. The land itself, however, remains Reuven\u8217's property \u8212- ownership of the land is not lost on {\i shemitah} \u8212- and so access to the field can still be rendered {\i assur} through a {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate question: on {\i shevi\u8217'it}, do the poor not have an inherent right of access to one\u8217's property for the purpose of gathering fruit? If so, how can Reuven make entry to his yard forbidden through a {\i neder}? After all, if that device were effective, every landowner could simply forbid access to his yard on {\i shemitah} and thereby prevent anyone from entering. The Gemara will address this at length; the short answer, for now, is that the fruit is not Reuven\u8217's to forbid, but the property and access to it remain his, and that access can still be made {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Readings Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second, clarifying section of the Mishnah addresses the case where only food \u8212- not general {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- was forbidden: {\i nadar mimenu ma\u8217'achal lifnei shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- if the vow was only about food benefit, taken before {\i shevi\u8217'it}. Then {\i yored letoch sadehu ve\u8217'eino ochel min haperot} \u8212- he may enter the field (since entering the yard is not food), but he may not eat the fruit. And {\i uvashevi\u8217'it yored ve\u8217'ochel} \u8212- during {\i shevi\u8217'it}, he may both enter the field and eat the fruit, because on {\i shevi\u8217'it} the owner cannot make the fruit forbidden; it is no longer his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah is thus fairly straightforward in its basic contours. The central analytical question \u8212- which will dominate the Gemara\u8217's discussion \u8212- is whether the first part of the Mishnah, dealing with full {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}, means that {\i once shevi\u8217'it arrives} the previously forbidden fruit becomes permitted, or whether it means that {\i if the neder was taken on shevi\u8217'it} the fruit is not subject to prohibition. This question will turn into a deeper debate about the very nature of a person\u8217's power to forbid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav/Shmuel vs.\u160 ?R. Yochan\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav & Shmuel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav and Shmuel both say: if someone declared {\i nechasim elu alecha} \u8212- \u8220"these properties are forbidden to you\u8221" \u8212- before {\i shevi\u8217'it}, then {\i eino yored letoch sadehu ve\u8217'eino ochel min haneti\u8217'ot} \u8212- he cannot enter the field and cannot eat from the overhanging fruit. And crucially: {\i af al pi shehigi\u8217'a shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- even once {\i shevi\u8217'it} arrives, this status does not change. Once the {\i neder} is in effect, it does not cease merely because the fruit becomes ownerless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is as follows. If I say \u8220"this book is forbidden to you\u8221" while I own it, and then I sell it to someone else, the forbidden status remains \u8212- because when I made the {\i neder} it was mine to make. The fruit that was in existence at the time of the {\i neder} and was rendered {\i assur} retains that status even as {\i shemitah} arrives, even though ownership of the fruit is effectively lost. New fruit that grows during {\i shemitah} would be a different matter entirely \u8212- the owner never had proprietary rights over that, and could not have forbidden it. But fruit that was already subject to the {\i neder} remains forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accordingly, Rav and Shmuel read the Mishnah\u8217's statement {\i uveshevi\u8217'it} as referring to a {\i neder} taken {\i on shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- only then is the fruit permissible. But if the {\i neder} was taken before {\i shevi\u8217'it}, the prohibition persists even after {\i shemitah} arrives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish both say: if someone declared {\i nechasai alecha} \u8212- \u8220"my property is forbidden to you\u8221" \u8212- before {\i shemitah}, then {\i eino yored letoch sadehu ve\u8217'eino ochel min hanotos} \u8212- naturally, he cannot enter the yard or eat the overhanging fruit. But {\i hegia shvi\u8217'is} \u8212- once {\i shevi\u8217'it} arrives \u8212- {\i eino yored letoch sadehu aval ochel hu et hanotos} \u8212- he still cannot enter the yard, but he may now eat the overhanging fruit. The vow made before {\i shemitah} ceases to apply to the fruit once {\i shemitah} arrives, because the owner no longer owns that fruit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Access & Gezeir\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position gives rise to a pointed observation from within the sugya. If forbidding access to one\u8217's field through a {\i neder} is effective on {\i shevi\u8217'it}, that would seem to be a highly convenient tool: one could effectively shut the poor out of one\u8217's land merely by declaring the property forbidden. But the Gemara does not regard this as an insuperable problem, because the person cannot forbid the new fruit that grows during {\i shemitah} \u8212- that was never his. And the stuff that already existed before {\i shemitah} could equally have been harvested and stored away before the sabbatical year began.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might press further: what about fruit that exists on the tree but has not yet fully grown \u8212- fruit that will continue to develop once {\i shemitah} begins? Presumably the owner cannot forbid the additional growth, but practically speaking, how would anyone separate the permissible portion from the {\i assur} portion? This is a genuinely difficult question, and it points toward the broader tension in this Mishnah between formal legal categories and the practical realities of {\i shemitah} observance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership & Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most conceptually compelling way to read the position of Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish is as follows. Their ruling is not merely a function of what the person said or intended; it is a function of the limits of his actual power. A person\u8217's capacity to render something forbidden to another derives from his ownership and control over that thing. That power does not extend beyond the scope of his ownership. Therefore, once the fruit passes out of his domain \u8212- as happens with {\i shemitah} \u8212- the forbidden status he instilled in it cannot persist, because it was sustained only by his continued ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking conceptual claim: it is not about intent, not about language, not about the terms of the {\i neder}. It is about the ontological limits of a person\u8217's power to prohibit. The Gemara, as we will see, will ultimately not accept this framing as the fundamental principle at stake \u8212- but it is worth pausing to appreciate its force.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 This vs.\u160 ?My Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Proposal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proposes that this is indeed the crux of the dispute. Rav and Shmuel hold: {\i adam oser davar she\u8217'birshuso afilu l\u8217'khesheyeitzei mershuso} \u8212- a person can render something in his possession forbidden, and that prohibition persists even after the object leaves his possession. Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish hold: {\i ein adam oser davar she\u8217'birshuso l\u8217'khesheyeitzei mershuso} \u8212- a person\u8217's power to forbid is coextensive with his ownership; it does not extend beyond it. This would be a profound and fundamental disagreement about the nature of {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this framing: {\i v\u8217'sisbara} \u8212- but does this really hold together? If the debate were truly so fundamental, they should have stated their positions with respect to {\i nechasim elu}, \u8220"this property,\u8221" as well \u8212- for if even generic language without any possessive is insufficient to sustain a prohibition past the loss of ownership, one would expect Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish to say so. Yet their formulation specifically uses {\i nechasai} \u8212- \u8220"my property.\u8221" This already hints that the debate is not about the fundamental limits of one\u8217's power to forbid, but about the linguistic interpretation of the possessive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the Gemara raises a proof from a Mishnah that clearly establishes the ability to render something forbidden even beyond one\u8217's lifetime and ownership: {\i omer livno konam she\u8217'atah neheneh li} \u8212- a father says to his son, \u8220"Korban \u8212- all my property is forbidden to you as benefit from me.\u8221" If the father dies and the son inherits him: {\i yirshenu} \u8212- he inherits and may benefit. Why? Because inheritance does not constitute receiving something {\i from} the father. At the moment of death, the property passes automatically to the son\u8217's domain; the son is not receiving it {\i from} the deceased. Hence, if the father said only \u8220"from me,\u8221" the prohibition does not apply to the inherited property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the father said {\i bechayav uvemoto} \u8212- both in his lifetime and after his death \u8212- then {\i met lo yirshenu}: the son may not benefit from the property. The Ran here points out that this does not mean the son literally fails to inherit; the laws of inheritance operate automatically through {\i choshen mishpat} and cannot be stopped by a {\i neder}. What it means is that the son owns the property but may not derive benefit from it \u8212- he cannot sell it, use it, or profit from it in any way. This raises its own fascinating questions: can mere ownership, without benefit, itself constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah}? And what would the son ultimately be able to do with property he owns but cannot benefit from?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah just cited proves that one can render property forbidden {\i even after one\u8217's death} \u8212- even after one has entirely ceased to be the owner. This demonstrates conclusively that {\i adam oser davar she\u8217'birshuso l\u8217'khesheyeitzei mershuso} is not a contested position; it is universally accepted. But the Gemara notes: perhaps this requires explicit language \u8212- the father had to say {\i bechayav uvemoto}, explicitly extending the prohibition beyond his lifetime. Without such explicit language, one might still maintain that the default interpretation limits the prohibition to the period of actual ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites a further Mishnah that is decisive. The Mishnah teaches: if someone says {\i konam letoch beitecha she\u8217'ani nichnas} \u8212- \u8220"your house is forbidden to me, that I shall not enter it\u8221" \u8212- or {\i sadecha she\u8217'ani loke\u8217'ach} \u8212- \u8220"your field, that I shall not purchase it\u8221" \u8212- and then the owner dies or sells to another, the vow lapses: {\i mutar}, it is permitted. The prohibiting party said \u8220"your\u8221" house, \u8220"your\u8221" field \u8212- and once it is no longer {\i your} house, the prohibition dissolves. But if he said {\i le\u8217'bayit zeh she\u8217'ani nichnas}, {\i sadeh zo she\u8217'ani loke\u8217'ach} \u8212- \u8220"this house I shall not enter,\u8221" \u8220"this field I shall not purchase\u8221" \u8212- and the owner dies or sells to another, the prohibition persists: {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This Mishnah is extremely clear. The use of the possessive \u8212- \u8220"your house,\u8221" \u8220"your field\u8221" \u8212- limits the prohibition to the duration of that person\u8217's ownership. The use of the demonstrative \u8220"this house,\u8221" \u8220"this field\u8221" \u8212- generic reference to the object itself \u8212- creates a prohibition that follows the object regardless of ownership. And note: in that Mishnah, the person forbidding is {\i not the owner at all} \u8212- he is the one seeking to enter or purchase. Yet even his prohibition is interpreted according to this possessive/demonstrative distinction. If the possessive limits {\i his} prohibition when the property is someone else\u8217's entirely, then certainly when I use the possessive about {\i my own} property, the limitation applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Linguistic Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this evidence, the Gemara concludes that there is no fundamental dispute between the Amoraim at all. The debate between Rav and Shmuel on the one hand and Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish on the other is not about whether a person\u8217's power to forbid is ontologically bounded by ownership. Everyone agrees on the following: if one uses the demonstrative \u8212- {\i nechasim elu}, \u8220"these properties\u8221" \u8212- the prohibition persists even after one loses ownership. If one uses the possessive \u8212- {\i nechasai}, \u8220"my property\u8221" \u8212- the prohibition lapses once ownership ends.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav and Shmuel said the {\i neder} remains in force even when {\i shemitah} arrives, they were speaking of a case where the declarant said {\i nechasim elu} \u8212- \u8220"these properties.\u8221" When Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish said the fruit becomes permitted once {\i shemitah} arrives, they were speaking of a case where the declarant said {\i nechasai} \u8212- \u8220"my property.\u8221" {\i Velo pligi} \u8212- they do not actually disagree. They are describing two distinct scenarios, and the Mishnah\u8217's ambiguity allowed each pair of Amoraim to read it in accordance with their respective case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains is a striking {\i hava amina}: one might have thought that the power to forbid is inherently and always bounded by ownership, such that no {\i neder} could ever outlast one\u8217's control over the object \u8212- but at the end of the day we reject this and hold that the determinative factor is not metaphysical power but linguistic formulation. Generic, demonstrative language creates a prohibition that is object-bound; possessive language creates one that is relationship-bound and lapses with the relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possessive & Demonstrati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran at the close of this section cites a fascinating {\i Yerushalmi} addressing a case where someone uses {\i both} a possessive and a demonstrative: {\i beitecha zeh} \u8212- \u8220"your house, this one.\u8221" Which term governs the interpretation? Does \u8220"your house\u8221" limit the prohibition to the duration of the other person\u8217's ownership, or does \u8220"this\u8221" extend it to the object itself regardless of ownership? The {\i Yerushalmi} concludes: {\i mishum zeh} \u8212- you follow the final term. If he ended with the demonstrative \u8220"this,\u8221" that is the operative word, and the prohibition follows the object. The principle is {\i holchin achar lashon acharon} \u8212- we follow the last formulation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shemitah Rights & Access\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rights vs.\u160 ?Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the Mishnah\u8217's ruling that even on {\i shevi\u8217'it} the {\i neder} prevents the other party from entering the field: why should this be? On {\i shevi\u8217'it}, the poor have an inherent right of access to private fields in order to gather the ownerless fruit. If the Torah grants them this right of access, how can a {\i neder} override it? Indeed, one might ask more pointedly: if someone could simply declare {\i nechasim elu} over his field and thereby bar the poor from entering, this would effectively allow any landowner to protect his produce from the poor on {\i shemitah} simply by making a {\i neder} \u8212- which would seem to undermine the entire intent of the sabbatical year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla answers: {\i be\u8217'omdim ilanot al ha-gvulin} \u8212- the Mishnah is speaking of a case where the trees are situated right at the boundary of the property. In such a configuration, the poor do not actually need to enter the field in order to gather the fruit. They can position themselves outside the border, place a ladder against the boundary, and reach the fruit without setting foot on the owner\u8217's land. The Torah\u8217's grant of access to the poor is a right of {\i entry for the purpose of gathering}, not an unlimited right of access. Where gathering is possible without entry, the right of entry does not exist. Therefore, the {\i neder} can effectively prevent entry \u8212- because the poor have no independent right to enter in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely striking limitation on the {\i shemitah} access rights we might have assumed the poor possess. The right is functional and proportionate: they may enter to the extent necessary to gather the fruit, and no more. If the fruit is accessible from outside, entry is not required and cannot be demanded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elyakim offers a related but distinct answer. The poor do have a right to enter the field to gather fruit, but they do not have a right to {\i linger} there after gathering is complete. The owner retains the right to insist they leave once they have collected what is owed to them. Now, through his {\i neder}, the owner has made any extended presence in his yard {\i assur} \u8212- the poor person who stays longer than necessary to collect the fruit would transgress the {\i neder}. Since the {\i neder} creates a real prohibition against lingering, the rabbis enacted a {\i gezeirah}: the poor may not enter the field at all, lest they come to tarry after gathering and thereby transgress.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable rabbinic safeguard, and it invites reflection. The {\i gezeirah} has the practical effect of barring all access to the field \u8212- which, as noted, might seem to provide an effective loophole against the obligations of {\i shemitah}. The question of why the rabbis would institute such a {\i gezeirah}, knowing its potential consequences for the poor\u8217's access to {\i shemitah} produce, is a genuine difficulty. Presumably, the individual owner\u8217's ability to benefit from this arrangement is circumscribed by other aspects of {\i shemitah} law \u8212- including the prohibition on commercial harvesting \u8212- but exactly how these constraints interact is worth further study.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lending & Selling Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next Mishnah addresses a person who is {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah me\u8217'chaveiro} \u8212- forbidden from benefiting from his fellow. The Mishnah states: {\i lo yash\u8217'ileinu} \u8212- he may not lend him objects; {\i ve\u8217'lo yish\u8217'al mimenu} \u8212- and may not borrow objects from him; {\i lo yelvennu} \u8212- may not lend him money; {\i ve\u8217'lo yilveh mimenu} \u8212- and may not borrow money from him; {\i lo yimkor lo} \u8212- may not sell to him; {\i ve\u8217'lo yikach mimenu} \u8212- and may not purchase from him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an immediate difficulty. {\i Bishlama lo yelvennu} \u8212- we understand why he may not lend him money: lending is a benefit conferred on the borrower. But {\i lo yilveh himenu} \u8212- why may he not borrow {\i from} him? What benefit is the borrower giving to the lender? The same question applies across the categories: we understand why the person forbidden from {\i hana\u8217'ah} may not receive objects, money, or goods \u8212- but why may he not give objects, lend money, or sell?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's {\i girsa} makes the structure of the Gemara\u8217's question cleaner: we understand {\i lo yilveh mimenu} \u8212- he may not borrow money \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'lo yikach mimenu} \u8212- may not purchase \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'lo yish\u8217'al mimenu} \u8212- may not borrow objects, because in all these cases he is receiving benefit. But why {\i lo yash\u8217'ileinu} \u8212- may he not lend objects \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'lo yelvennu} \u8212- and lend money \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'lo yimkor lo} \u8212- and sell to him? What benefit is flowing in those directions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Chanina answers: the Mishnah is speaking of a case where both parties took mutual vows \u8212- {\i shenadaru hana\u8217'ah ze mize} \u8212- neither may benefit from the other. Under this reading, the Mishnah is not surprising: it is simply listing all the transactions that are prohibited when the {\i issur} runs in both directions. Each prohibition can be explained straightforwardly as preventing benefit to one or the other party. The difficulty with this reading is that it renders the Mishnah somewhat less interesting \u8212- it is merely applying the same principle twice, once in each direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a different answer: {\i gezera lish\u8217'ol mishum lehash\u8217'il vechein bekulhu} \u8212- it is a rabbinic {\i gezeirah}: even though in principle only one direction of each transaction is actually prohibited, the rabbis forbade the reverse direction as well, lest one come to do the forbidden direction. Don\u8217't borrow, lest you come to lend. Don\u8217't lend, lest you come to borrow. And so forth for all the categories. This reading is likely closer to the plain sense of the Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sale Benefit & Avoda Zara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of sales raises a related point from earlier in the tractate: in a standard commercial transaction, who is the beneficiary? The Gemara\u8217's reasoning here seems to assume that primarily it is the buyer who benefits \u8212- which is why we understand why the {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} cannot purchase, but need an explanation for why he cannot sell. The possibility that both buyer and seller mutually benefit from a transaction \u8212- an idea that surfaces elsewhere in the Gemara \u8212- would explain why the sale prohibition naturally runs in both directions without needing either Rabbi Yose\u8217's mutual-vow reading or Abaye\u8217's {\i gezeirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sugya has a striking parallel in the opening Mishnah of Avoda Zara, which lists a similar set of restrictions \u8212- no selling, no buying, no lending of money, no borrowing, no lending of objects, no borrowing \u8212- that apply to transactions with non-Jews in the three days before their holidays. The Gemara there similarly asks: we understand why one might not give benefit to a non-Jew before his holiday, but why may one not {\i receive} benefit from him? And Abaye there offers the same answer: it is a {\i gezeirah}, one direction is forbidden because of the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel is illuminating. The collection of prohibited transactions here \u8212- lending, borrowing, buying, selling \u8212- does not operate purely as a technical consequence of {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}. As we saw throughout the previous chapter, many interactions remain entirely permissible even when {\i hana\u8217'ah} is forbidden: one may pay off another\u8217's debts, support his wife, visit him when ill.\u160 ?Yet this specific set of commercial and financial transactions is {\i assur} in both directions. It creates, in effect, a wall of separation. In the context of {\i nedarim}, this barrier is a legal consequence of the vow; in {\i Avoda Zara}, it expresses the social and religious distance between the Jewish community and idolatrous practice. That both contexts generate the same formal structure, and that Abaye\u8217's {\i gezeirah} appears in both, is worth noting as a reflection of how the Talmud thinks about the social meaning of prohibited transactions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 44\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hefker & Nedarim Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Madir & Hefker Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin on daf 44a, though in order to organize the relevant background, we must first briefly revisit the mishnah that precedes it. The mishnah addresses a case of a {\i madir} \u8212- someone who has rendered another person ({\i mudar}) forbidden from deriving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from him. If the {\i madir} has made all of his possessions {\i assur} to the {\i mudar} and the two are traveling together, with only enough food for one, the question arises how the {\i mudar} might be permitted to eat. One solution suggested is that the {\i madir} declare his food {\i hefker} \u8212- he places it on a rock and announces that it is ownerless, available to whoever wishes to take it \u8212- thereby permitting the {\i mudar} to partake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose, however, rejects this solution. The {\i gemara} explains, through Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's analysis, why Rabbi Yose considers this approach ineffective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's Matana\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan explains that Rabbi Yose holds {\i hefker} to be analogous to a {\i matana}: a gift does not take legal effect until the recipient takes possession of it. Accordingly, until a third party actually comes and claims the {\i hefker} item, that item is still considered to be within the {\i reshut} of the original owner. Since it remains in the {\i reshut} of the {\i madir}, the {\i mudar} is still forbidden to benefit from it. Only after an intermediate party has claimed it and removed it from the {\i madir}\u8217's {\i reshut} could it then pass to the {\i mudar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baalut & Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position carries an interesting implication: it suggests that there is no such thing in the world of {\i metaltelin} \u8212- movable property \u8212- that belongs to absolutely no one. Everything, at any given moment, resides in the {\i reshut} of some person and carries some degree of {\i baalut}. Parallels can be found in the laws of {\i nezikin}, where one who declares his {\i kad} ownerless in the {\i reshut harabbim} may still bear responsibility if someone trips over it \u8212- indicating that even an act of {\i hefker} does not necessarily sever all legal connection between an owner and his property. The word {\i hefker} itself means \u8220"consciously abandoned\u8221" \u8212- it requires verbalization, either to oneself or to others \u8212- and is distinct from {\i yeush}, the mere mental resignation to loss, which is itself a {\i machloket rishonim} as to whether it operates {\i mishum hefker}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Although an earlier portion of the {\i sugya} appears to have raised a challenge to Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's explanation, the {\i gemara} continues to operate on the assumption that his position remains viable. That baseline \u8212- {\i hefker} is like a {\i matana} and requires {\i zechiyas} by another person before it exits the original owner\u8217's {\i reshut} \u8212- underlies everything that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Field Hefker & 3-Day Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Make Fields Hefker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the mishnah at the bottom of 43b, which serves as our primary text for the rest of the {\i sugya}. The mishnah states: one who declares his field {\i hefker} may retract within three days; thereafter he may not retract. If he declared it {\i hefker} for a limited period \u8212- one day, one week, one month, one year, or one {\i shavua} (a seven-year cycle; note that in the Talmud {\i shavua} means a {\i shemita} cycle, while {\i shabbat} denotes a seven-day period) \u8212- then as long as no one has taken possession, whether he himself or another, he may retract; once someone has taken possession, he may not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why would a person declare his field {\i hefker} in the first place? The reason is that {\i chiyuv} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} depends on ownership. A field that has been declared {\i hefker} and then repossessed from {\i hefker} is {\i patur} from {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}. So the scheme is: declare the field {\i hefker}, gamble that no one will immediately pounce on it, and then recollect it \u8212- now ostensibly exempt from {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} for that season. This is, to put it plainly, a sleazy practice, and the Talmud will have much to say about it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3-Day Retraction Period\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah\u8217's first clause tells us that for the first three days one may retract the {\i hefker} declaration. The Ran explains that even if someone has come along and claimed the field during those three days, the original owner may still retract. The logic here is that both parties are, in a sense, engaged in a kind of gamble: the owner hopes no one will take it, while an opportunistic third party \u8212- himself not entirely above suspicion \u8212- waits to claim it the moment it is declared {\i hefker}. During those first three days, the owner may say, \u8220"I withdraw; it was never really {\i hefker} after all.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Importantly, during those three days the field is also not {\i patur} from {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}. The owner does not get the benefit of {\i hefker} during the window when he retains the right to retract. After three days, however, he can no longer retract at all. If he wishes to recollect the field, he must do so as a fresh act of {\i zechiya} \u8212- claiming it from {\i hefker} just as anyone else would \u8212- not by retroactively voiding the {\i hefker} declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question naturally arises: why three days specifically? The answer offered is, essentially, that three days is the pragmatic middle ground \u8212- two days is too short, and four days is too long. The Rabbinic literature frequently uses three as a meaningful number, and common sense aligns with it as a period long enough to be meaningful but short enough to be definitive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limited-Time Hefker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second clause of the mishnah introduces a distinction. When a person declares his field {\i hefker} only for a limited period \u8212- a day, a week, a month, a year, or a seven-year cycle \u8212- the three-day retraction mechanism does not apply in the same way. Instead, the rule is: as long as no one has taken possession, whether the owner himself or another, he may retract; once someone has taken possession, he may not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possession & No Retracti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To clarify the practical distinction between retracting a {\i hefker} declaration and actively claiming the field from {\i hefker}: retracting means saying, \u8220"I withdraw the declaration; the field was never {\i hefker}.\u8221" Claiming means going out and physically taking possession \u8212- building a fence, walking the boundaries \u8212- which is the standard method of acquiring a field. One who claims it thereby obtains it as {\i hefker} property, and if the {\i hefker} was valid, that acquisition carries a {\i petur} from {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}. Retracting, by contrast, is asserting that the field never left one\u8217's possession at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether the owner may then claim the field for himself as an act of {\i zechiya} \u8212- distinct from retraction \u8212- is meaningful. Taking possession as {\i zocheh} from {\i hefker} is an act consistent with {\i hefker} being real; retraction treats it as though {\i hefker} never occurred. If the owner takes possession as {\i zocheh}, the field is {\i patur} from {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}. If he retracts, it was never {\i hefker} and remains {\i chayav}. This distinction will become central in the analysis that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reisha vs.\u160 ?Seifa Attribu\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Three-Day Solut\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having laid out the two clauses of the mishnah, the {\i gemara} now asks whether the {\i reisha} (the first clause, with the three-day rule) reflects the position of the {\i rabbanan}, while the {\i seifa} (the second clause, which ties retractability to possession rather than time) reflects the position of Rabbi Yose. The reasoning is as follows: the {\i seifa}, which allows retraction so long as no one has taken possession, fits naturally with Rabbi Yose\u8217's underlying view that {\i hefker} never truly exits the owner\u8217's {\i reshut} until someone claims it. The {\i reisha}, by contrast, which cuts off retraction after three days regardless of whether anyone has claimed it, seems to reflect the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's view that {\i hefker} is a real act of dispossession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Rammaim Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What motivates the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's position in the {\i reisha}? They are troubled by the practice of {\i rammaim} \u8212- those who declare fields {\i hefker} with the full intention of reclaiming them in order to avoid {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}. The Rabbis were unwilling to allow this scheme to proceed unchecked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Patience Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav explains the logic: the {\i rabbanan} imposed a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} {\i chiyuv} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} during the first three days \u8212- the period during which the {\i madir} retains the right to retract. Even though {\i de\u8217'oraita} the {\i hefker} may be valid and the field should be {\i patur}, the Rabbis said: as long as you can still retract, you remain {\i chayav de\u8217'rabbanan}. And, critically, Rav observes that the {\i rammaim} do not have the patience to wait longer than three days. They want their field back quickly. By making the three-day period a window of continued {\i chiyuv} \u8212- with freedom only arriving after three days \u8212- the Rabbis effectively neutralized the scheme: those who couldn\u8217't wait three days would be {\i chayav}, and those who did wait would find their {\i hefker} to be real and irrevocable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: how can the Rabbis empower the original owner to retract even after a third party has taken possession {\i de\u8217'oraita}? The answer invoked is {\i hefker beit din hefker}: the {\i beit din} possesses the authority to dispossess and transfer property. The Rabbis, acting through this principle, can give the owner the right to retract his {\i hefker} even in circumstances where {\i de\u8217'oraita} he could not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla: Both Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla takes a different approach to the {\i reisha}-{\i seifa} division. He maintains that both clauses of the mishnah reflect the position of the {\i rabbanan}, not a split between {\i rabbanan} and Rabbi Yose. The {\i gemara} challenges Ulla: if the {\i seifa} is also {\i rabbanan}, why does it condition retractability on whether someone has taken possession, rather than simply applying the three-day rule? Ulla\u8217's answer is: {\i shani shanah v\u8217'shavua de\u8217'la shechicha} \u8212- the cases of a limited-time {\i hefker} (for a day, a week, a month, etc.) are unusual enough that the {\i rabbanan} did not bother applying the three-day mechanism to them. The anti-{\i rammaim} legislation targets the standard {\i hefker} scenario, which is declaring the field fully ownerless without a time limit. The limited-time {\i hefker} is not the typical exploit the Rabbis were concerned about, and so they left it subject only to the basic rule: once someone has claimed it, there is no retraction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might press: if the scheme is to declare {\i hefker} for just one day, complete the harvest, and reclaim the field, isn\u8217't that even more efficient than waiting three days? The answer seems to be that the critical moment of {\i chiyuv} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} is connected to the land itself, not the harvested crop. Once produce has been brought into a {\i kri} \u8212- the storage pile \u8212- it becomes {\i chayav}, but the mechanism of {\i hefker} to avoid {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} operates at the level of the field\u8217's ownership at the time of harvest. Making the field {\i hefker} for a single day is simply too unusual a practice for the Rabbis to have specifically legislated against, and thus the limited-time {\i hefker} falls outside the three-day framework entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reish Lakish: Both Yose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\u8217's Attributi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish offers an entirely different reading of the mishnah. Rather than splitting the clauses between {\i rabbanan} and Rabbi Yose, or attributing both to {\i rabbanan}, Reish Lakish holds that both the {\i reisha} and the {\i seifa} reflect the position of Rabbi Yose. The {\i seifa} is identifiable as Rabbi Yose for the reasons already discussed \u8212- it conditions retractability on possession rather than time, consistent with Rabbi Yose\u8217's view that {\i hefker} remains in the owner\u8217's {\i reshut} until claimed. And since the {\i seifa} is Rabbi Yose, says Reish Lakish, the {\i reisha} must be as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then why does the {\i reisha} impose a three-day limit at all? If Rabbi Yose\u8217's fundamental position is that you may retract at any time until someone takes possession, why did he institute a cutoff at three days? This requires explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Confusion Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran offers what he considers the most coherent account of Reish Lakish\u8217's reading. Rabbi Yose, says the Ran, is concerned about a particular confusion that would arise if {\i hefker} were always revocable up to the moment of possession. If people knew that the owner could retract at literally any moment before someone claims the field \u8212- that the field is, in Rabbi Yose\u8217's view, still in the owner\u8217's {\i reshut} the entire time \u8212- they might begin to think that {\i hefker} is not really {\i hefker} at all, but simply a kind of deferred {\i matana}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker vs.\u160 ?Matana Confus\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran develops this concern. If the owner can retract up until the very moment someone takes possession, then from the perspective of the person who ultimately claims the field, what he received was not truly ownerless property \u8212- it was property that the original owner could have reclaimed at any instant. This makes the transaction look far more like a {\i matana} than a claim from {\i hefker}. And that confusion has practical consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The person who claimed the field might reason as follows: since the owner retained {\i reshut} over this property until the very moment I claimed it, perhaps this field is still {\i chayav} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}, just as property received as a gift is {\i chayav}. If he thinks that, he will then try to separate {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} from this field for other fields, or from other fields for this one \u8212- generating a problem of {\i min ha-chayav al ha-patur} or {\i min ha-patur al ha-chayav}. That is a serious {\i de\u8217'oraita} concern: separating tithes from produce that is actually {\i patur} on behalf of produce that is {\i chayav}, or vice versa, invalidates the separation entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make this vivid: the field was declared {\i hefker}, but the owner could have retracted at any moment. The person who ultimately claimed it is in a state of genuine uncertainty. Was this a real {\i hefker}? Or was this more like receiving a gift from someone who retained ownership throughout? That uncertainty is precisely what Rabbi Yose sought to prevent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumot U\u8217'Maasrot Confus\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran elaborates further on why this confusion is specifically dangerous in the context of {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}. He writes (at the top of 44b) that if we allow the owner to retract at any point before someone has claimed it, people will say: since the owner could have retracted even after a long period, that means the property must have been in his {\i reshut} the whole time, which in turn means that anyone who claims it is really receiving a {\i matana} \u8212- and therefore the field remains {\i chayav} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's language is precise: {\i atu le-meimar de-chi lo chazar bo nami lizchei bah acher lo me-hefker ka-zachei} \u8212- someone who claims the field will not believe they are claiming it from genuine {\i hefker}, but rather from the owner\u8217's continuing possession. And: {\i venimtza she-ein ze zocheh min ha-hefker ella mekabel matanah} \u8212- it turns out that this person is not taking from {\i hefker} at all, but receiving a {\i matana}. And therefore: {\i ve-chasvri de-michayav be-maaser} \u8212- they will think the field is {\i chayav} in {\i maaser}, and they will then proceed to treat it as such, mixing {\i chayav} and {\i patur} produce in their {\i tithe} separations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make this concrete with an analogy: if someone announces a field is {\i hefker} but it is common knowledge that the owner can retract at any moment, then anyone rushing to claim it understands they are racing against the clock of the owner\u8217's own will. The field is \u8220"available\u8221" only because the owner permits it to be, and could close that window at any instant. This feels structurally identical to receiving a gift, not to claiming genuinely ownerless property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is therefore to prevent this confusion \u8212- the collapsing of {\i hefker} into {\i matana} in the public\u8217's legal understanding \u8212- that Rabbi Yose institutes the three-day rule. For three days, the owner may retract. That is consistent with Rabbi Yose\u8217's {\i de\u8217'oraita} position, since the property is still in his {\i reshut}. But after three days, Rabbi Yose institutes a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} rule: you may no longer retract. This ensures that people understand there is a real {\i torat hefker} at work \u8212- a genuine legal category of ownerless property \u8212- and not merely an elaborate mechanism for making gifts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Challenge & Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Day Puzzle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a sharp question against Reish Lakish\u8217's reading. If the point of the three-day limit is to ensure that people understand {\i hefker} is real \u8212- i.e., that {\i torat hefker} should not be forgotten \u8212- then why impose a three-day limit at all? If the concern is that people will confuse {\i hefker} with {\i matana}, the solution should be to declare {\i hefker} fully effective from day one, not to give the owner three days of retraction and only then declare it irrevocable. Why not simply make it {\i hefker} immediately, so that there is never any confusion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Rammaim Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava provides the answer: there are two competing considerations. On one side is the concern that {\i torat hefker} will be forgotten \u8212- that people will not understand {\i hefker} as a genuine legal category distinct from {\i matana}. On the other side is the concern about the {\i rammaim}: those who exploit {\i hefker} declarations to escape {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}. These two values must be balanced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After 3 Days Fully Hefker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three-day period represents this balance. During the first three days, the owner retains the right to retract \u8212- consistent with Rabbi Yose\u8217's {\i de\u8217'oraita} position \u8212- and during this period the field remains {\i chayav de\u8217'rabbanan} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}, frustrating the {\i rammaim}. After three days, the {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} rule kicks in: the {\i hefker} becomes fully irrevocable, and the field is now genuinely {\i hefker de\u8217'rabbanan}, so that people understand it to be truly ownerless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Long-Term Reclaim Confus\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Without this three-day limit, a further problem would emerge. Suppose the owner declares {\i hefker} and simply waits \u8212- for weeks or months \u8212- until he is ready to reclaim the field. He then walks in and takes possession. According to Rabbi Yose\u8217's {\i de\u8217'oraita} position, that is entirely permissible: the field was in his {\i reshut} the whole time, and no one else has claimed it. But if the public observes this pattern \u8212- someone declares {\i hefker}, and then months later simply takes the field back \u8212- they will be deeply confused about the {\i chiyuv} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}. Did the person who eventually harvests this field acquire it from genuine {\i hefker}? Or is it still the original owner\u8217's property? That long-term ambiguity is even more dangerous than the short-term confusion the three-day rule is designed to address. By cutting off retractability at three days {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}, the system ensures that any claim made after three days is understood to be a fresh {\i zechiya} from {\i hefker}, carrying a {\i petur} from {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita: Hefker Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vineyard Hefker Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is now introduced that challenges the positions developed above. The {\i baraita} states: one who declares his vineyard {\i hefker} and then, in the morning, rises and harvests it himself is {\i chayav} in {\i peret}, {\i olelot}, {\i shichecha}, and {\i pe\u8217'ah} \u8212- all the agricultural obligations associated with those categories \u8212- but is {\i patur} from {\i maaser}. The reason for the {\i petur} from {\i maaser} is derived from a {\i midrash} on the relevant {\i pasuk}: the double language of {\i azov ta\u8217'azov} that appears in connection with certain agricultural gifts does not appear with {\i maaser}, and therefore {\i hefker} exempts from {\i maaser} but not from those other obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla can accommodate this {\i baraita} within his framework without difficulty. According to Ulla, both the {\i baraita} and the mishnah follow the {\i rabbanan}. The {\i baraita} is describing the situation {\i de\u8217'oraita}: when the owner harvests his field on the morning after declaring it {\i hefker}, this falls within the three-day window during which, {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}, the {\i hefker} has not yet taken full effect. But {\i de\u8217'oraita} the {\i hefker} is real, and therefore the field is {\i patur} from {\i maaser de\u8217'oraita}. {\i De\u8217'rabbanan} there may be additional obligations, but the {\i baraita} is speaking of the {\i de\u8217'oraita} exemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish, however, has a problem. If the {\i baraita} follows the {\i rabbanan}, then during the three-day period the field should be treated as though it is still the owner\u8217's \u8212- {\i chayav} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} \u8212- as that is precisely the Rabbinic mechanism designed to foil the {\i rammaim}. Why then is the {\i baraita} telling us that he is {\i patur} from {\i maaser}? If the three-day rule is meant to maintain {\i chiyuv de\u8217'rabbanan} during that period, the {\i baraita} should be saying he is {\i chayav}, not {\i patur}. The {\i baraita} seems to undercut the very logic of the three-day Rabbinic legislation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran: Implicit Retraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran offers a resolution. The key is in what the owner did on the morning after declaring {\i hefker}. He simply went down to the vineyard and started harvesting \u8212- he never made any explicit declaration that he was reclaiming the field as his own through {\i zechiya} from {\i hefker}. Since he did not explicitly state that he was being {\i zocheh} from the {\i hefker}, the Ran says: {\i kivan de-lo parish she-hu zocheh bo\u8230 ? neima de-mehadar} \u8212- we interpret his action not as a fresh act of {\i zechiya} from {\i hefker}, but as an implicit retraction of the {\i hefker} itself. He changed his mind and reverted to treating the field as his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this is a retraction, then according to Rabbi Yose \u8212- whose {\i de\u8217'oraita} position allows retraction at any point before someone has taken possession \u8212- the retraction is valid {\i de\u8217'oraita}, and the field was never {\i hefker}. If the field was never {\i hefker}, then it is {\i chayav} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} and not {\i patur}. But the {\i baraita} says {\i patur from maaser} \u8212- a result which, under this reading, fits precisely because the retraction is understood as a reversal within Rabbi Yose\u8217's framework, and the {\i baraita} is speaking to the resulting status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish: Attribute\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish sidesteps the difficulty entirely with a clean solution: he attributes the entire {\i baraita} to {\i rabbanan}, not to Rabbi Yose. In other words, Reish Lakish says: when I associated the mishnah with Rabbi Yose, that did not require me to associate this {\i baraita} with Rabbi Yose as well. The {\i baraita} can simply be understood as following the {\i rabbanan}, and the various technical details work themselves out under that attribution without generating the contradictions that plague the alternative reading.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two vs.\u160 ?Three Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?Three Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative explanation ({\i i ba\u8217'it eima}) is offered to resolve the tension between the {\i baraita} and the mishnah without attributing them to different {\i tanna\u8217'im}. The distinction is not between Rabbi Yose and {\i rabbanan} at all, but between two different circumstances of {\i hefker}: whether the declaration was made in the presence of two people ({\i bifnei terei}) or three people ({\i bifnei tlata}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i hefker} is declared before only two people, the complexities discussed throughout \u8212- the right to retract, the three-day period, the ambiguity about {\i chiyuv} in {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} \u8212- all apply. The declaration is not maximally effective, and the various Rabbinic and {\i de\u8217'oraita} rules come into play. The mishnah, with its three-day retraction rule and all its attendant complications, speaks to this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Witnesses Effective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rabbi Yochanan, citing Rabbi Shimon ben Yehotzadak, and Elazar ben Anas both hold that if {\i hefker} is declared before three people, it is fully and immediately effective. The declaration before three confers a qualitatively different legal status on the {\i hefker}: it is irrevocable from the outset, and its effect as genuine ownerless property \u8212- {\i patur} from {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} \u8212- is immediate. The {\i baraita}, which speaks of a {\i petur} from {\i maaser}, is therefore the case of {\i hefker} before three: the {\i hefker} took full effect immediately, and the owner who harvests the next morning is indeed {\i patur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Witnesses Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi adds a further nuance. He holds that {\i devar Torah} \u8212- as a matter of pure Torah law \u8212- even declaring {\i hefker} before a single person is sufficient to render the property ownerless. The Rabbinic requirement of three is not because three are needed for the {\i hefker} itself to be effective, but for a purely practical purpose: one person to take possession of the {\i hefker} property, and two to serve as witnesses to that act of possession. The three-person rule is thus a procedural safeguard for proof, not a substantive requirement for the {\i de\u8217'oraita} validity of the {\i hefker} declaration itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 45\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hefker & Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker as Speech Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous {\i perek} closed with a {\i machlokes} between Rabbi Yosi and the {\i Rabbanan} concerning how {\i hefker} operates, along with a dispute among the {\i Amora\u8217'im} about how to apply Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position, drawing on various {\i beraisos} and {\i mishnayos}. Before turning to the new {\i perek}, it is worth stepping back to examine the nature of {\i hefker} more broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case at the end of the prior {\i mishna} involved a person who was {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from another and placed food on a rock, declaring it {\i hefker}, so that the other person could freely take and benefit from it. The operative question is: what precisely does {\i hefker} accomplish? If {\i hefker} merely grants others permission to use an object without actually extinguishing the original owner\u8217's title \u8212- so that during the limbo period between the declaration and another person\u8217's acquisition the original ownership persists \u8212- then the {\i neder} would remain in effect wherever that {\i neder} depends on ownership. This would create a real difficulty for understanding how Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position functions in the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Hefker Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are essentially two ways to understand {\i hefker}. The first model holds that {\i hefker} is a grant of permission to others to use or acquire the object, but does not transfer ownership until a second party actually acquires it. Under this view, the original owner\u8217's title lingers in that interim period. The second model holds that {\i hefker} is a complete and immediate severance of ownership \u8212- the moment one declares an object {\i hefker}, it passes out of one\u8217's {\i reshut} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Which model the {\i mishna} presupposes is precisely the question. The tension between these two models runs through many of the discussions that follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker & Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam observes that {\i hefker} is, in a certain sense, a kind of {\i neder}. This is not to say they are identical, but that in both cases one\u8217's words change the status of an object. With a {\i neder}, the words render the object {\i assur} or {\i mutar}; with {\i hefker}, the words alter to whom the object belongs. The Rambam\u8217's comparison is illuminated by the {\i Gemara} on daf 7, which asks whether {\i yad} functions for {\i hefker} \u8212- that is, whether the rules of partial verbal formulations that govern {\i nedarim} apply equally to {\i hefker}. That the {\i Gemara} even entertains the question demonstrates that these two categories share significant structural overlap as {\i speech acts} that change the {\i halachic} status of an object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can observe a similar dynamic with the separation of {\i terumah} and {\i maaser}: there too one employs words to change the legal standing of produce. In each of these cases \u8212- {\i hefker}, {\i nedarim}, tithing \u8212- it is a verbal act directed at an object, a {\i cheftza}, that produces a {\i halachic} transformation. The {\i masechta} has touched on this underlying pattern repeatedly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker After Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The comparison to {\i nedarim} carries one further striking implication: what happens if a person declares something {\i hefker} and then dies before anyone has acquired it? If {\i hefker} is a genuine {\i kinyan} \u8212- a real transfer of title to no one, with the property suspended in ownerless limbo \u8212- then the declaration should remain effective even after the owner\u8217's death; anyone who comes along afterward may acquire it. But if {\i hefker} is more akin to a {\i neder}, then it is a personal obligation of the declarer, and just as one\u8217's heirs are not obligated to fulfill the {\i nedarim} of their ancestor, they would not be bound to treat the property as ownerless. The property would simply fall back into the estate, having never truly left the owner\u8217's {\i reshut} \u8212- it was merely an attempted verbal transformation that lapsed upon death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is thus another domain in which the structural similarity between {\i hefker} and {\i nedarim} has concrete {\i halachic} consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker} \u8212- that a rabbinic court possesses the authority to render property ownerless \u8212- also arose at the end of {\i amud alef}. Rabbi Shimon ben Hotzadak holds: {\i kol hamaphkir bifnei shlosha havi hefker} \u8212- declaring something {\i hefker} before three people constitutes valid {\i hefker}; before fewer than three it does not. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi disagrees and says that {\i devar Torah}, even one person suffices, and the rabbis required three for a pragmatic reason: {\i kedai sheyihiyeh echad zocheh ushnayim me\u8217'idim} \u8212- so that one person may acquire the property and two may serve as witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two positions suggest rather different theories of what the three-person requirement accomplishes. On Rabbi Shimon ben Hotzadak\u8217's view, one might read the requirement as analogous to a {\i beit din}, though it is equally possible he is not invoking formal judicial procedure at all. Rather, his concern may be with {\i publicity}: once three people know something, the matter becomes public \u8212- {\i avsha milta} \u8212- and acquires a standing in the communal sphere. Indeed, there is a known principle with {\i lashon hara} that once three people know of something it changes its status, because a certain {\i kol}, a communal voice, begins to form around that information. Under this reading, Rabbi Shimon ben Hotzadak\u8217's three-person requirement is about making the {\i hefker} publicly known, not about convening a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker} itself comes up in several critical contexts. One striking application, to be encountered in {\i Kiddushin}, is the {\i sugya} of {\i afkinu rabanan lekiddushei minei} \u8212- the notion that under very circumscribed circumstances the rabbis possess the power to annul a marriage retroactively. At least according to some {\i Rishonim}, the mechanism is that the {\i beit din} declares that the money used for {\i kiddushin} was never truly the groom\u8217's \u8212- it was {\i hefker} \u8212- and thus the {\i kiddushin} never took effect. This illustrates the extraordinary reach of {\i hefker beit din hefker}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goral & Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} in {\i Gittin} identifies two potential sources for {\i hefker beit din hefker}, and the two sources carry sharply divergent implications. The first source is the division of the land of Israel by {\i goral} \u8212- by lottery \u8212- when the Jewish people entered the land. At first glance this raises an obvious question: how did each person actually acquire his portion? The entire land was in some sense the collective inheritance of the Jewish people; no individual owned any of it; and yet through the {\i goral} each family received a defined tract. The mechanism by which this worked \u8212- by which the {\i beit din} could take something from the collective and vest it in an individual \u8212- is {\i hefker beit din hefker}. The {\i beit din} rendered the communal property ownerless with respect to all others and simultaneously designated it as belonging to a specific person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This first source implies something quite far-reaching: {\i hefker beit din hefker} is not merely the power to make property ownerless, but the power to transfer ownership directly. The {\i beit din} can take from the collective and give to an individual. This reflects a model of communal or governmental ownership: the land ultimately belongs to God \u8212- {\i l\u8217'Hashem ha-aretz umlo\u8217'ah} \u8212- and the {\i beit din} functions as its administrator, capable of directing ownership where it chooses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second source is from Ezra, who exercised the power to confiscate \u8212- to declare {\i cherem} \u8212- the property of those who refused to ascend to the land of Israel. This source tells a different story. Here the {\i beit din}\u8217's power is punitive and coercive: it is a policing authority, the capacity to fine or confiscate property from those who have violated communal norms. This is not a model of governmental ownership of everything, but a model of governmental enforcement power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two models \u8212- communal ownership with the {\i beit din} as administrator versus {\i beit din} as wielder of police power \u8212- represent genuinely different conceptions of what the Torah\u8217's governmental structure prescribes and what the principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker} means at its root.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related but distinct question is whether the government may seize private land for public use. There are indeed such rules, traceable to Yehoshua\u8217's conquest of the land. The {\i Rambam} and the {\i Gemara} in {\i Bava Batra} enumerate approximately eight basic public-property-law rules that Yehoshua established: public easements through fields, specifications for road widths, rights of pasturage, and similar matters. These public-land-use questions constitute their own complex domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bitul Chametz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One other significant application of the concept of {\i hefker} is {\i bitul chametz}. According to Tosafot on Pesachim daf 2, {\i bitul chametz} \u8212- the nullification of one\u8217's {\i chametz} before Passover \u8212- operates through a mechanism related to {\i hefker}. It may not be that the act of {\i bitul} is identical to {\i hefker}, but rather that {\i bitul} causes one\u8217's {\i chametz} to become {\i hefker}. Under this view, a person who nullifies all his {\i chametz}, declares it ownerless, and simply closes the door on whatever remains \u8212- the Cheerios on the shelf, the residue on the frying pan \u8212- has in principle fulfilled his biblical obligation. The rabbinic requirements of {\i bedikat chametz} (searching), active destruction, and other measures were layered on top because {\i bitul} alone seems too easy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ramban\u8217's Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ramban offers a sharp critique of Tosafot\u8217's position. He argues that {\i bitul chametz} cannot function through {\i hefker}, and that it operates through an entirely different mechanism \u8212- one closer to {\i bitul avodah zarah}. Just as a non-Jew who once worshipped an idol and then rejects it \u8212- smashing it, discarding it, or declaring it worthless \u8212- has performed a {\i bitul} that removes the idol\u8217's prohibited status, so too the person performing {\i bitul chametz} is not declaring the {\i chametz} ownerless but rather fundamentally reorienting his relationship to it: {\i eino ani rotzeh b\u8217'kiyumo} \u8212- I do not want this to exist for me any longer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the Ramban\u8217's model, the decisive factor is a psychological-legal relinquishment of attachment to the {\i chametz}, not a formal act of ownership transfer. A person who simply does not want his {\i chametz} \u8212- who has inwardly and verbally severed his connection to it \u8212- has accomplished something {\i mide\u8217'oraita}, and the rabbis then required additional acts because this inner relinquishment alone seems insufficiently concrete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might press: with {\i avodah zarah}, the act of physical destruction demonstrates that the object is no longer worshipped \u8212- the idol\u8217's status is defined entirely by its being an object of veneration, and the owner\u8217's rejection makes that plain. But with {\i chametz}, what does a verbal declaration actually accomplish? Why should words alone have the power to change the {\i halachic} status of the {\i chametz}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran on Chametz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran in {\i Psachim} \u8212- on daf 1 amud alef in the pages of the Rif \u8212- offers a distinctive answer. {\i Chametz} is categorically different from other property, the Ran explains, because the Torah itself declared {\i bal yera\u8217'eh u\u8217'val yimatzei} \u8212- two distinct prohibitions against owning or possessing {\i chametz} on Passover. These prohibitions have the effect of removing the {\i chametz} from one\u8217's {\i reshut} in a meaningful sense: the only dimension of \u8220"ownership\u8221" one retains over {\i chametz} on Passover is precisely the dimension that makes one liable for violating those two {\i mitzvot}. The {\i Gemara} itself uses language suggesting that the Torah places the {\i chametz} into one\u8217's ownership for the purpose of creating the violation. What remains of ordinary ownership is therefore only a residual sliver of connection, and to dissolve even that sliver requires only a much weaker mechanism than would be needed to declare ordinary property {\i hefker}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the counterintuitive point: because the Torah has rendered the {\i chametz} so nearly outside one\u8217's {\i reshut} already, the bar for completing the severance is lower than for real property. It is not that {\i bitul chametz} is a full {\i hefker}; rather, the ownership that needs to be dissolved is itself attenuated, and so an attenuated act suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of how all this fits with Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position \u8212- who holds that {\i hefker} does not fully remove ownership until another person acquires the property \u8212- is itself a subject the {\i Rishonim} address primarily from the perspective of the {\i Rabbanan}. Tosafot might resolve the difficulty by noting that {\i bitul chametz} is a different and weaker kind of {\i hefker} than ordinary {\i hefker}, one appropriate to the already-diminished ownership the Torah leaves one with respect to {\i chametz}. This would align, in spirit if not in precise mechanism, with the Ran\u8217's approach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of how many people are required for {\i hefker} to take effect: Rabbi Shimon ben Hotzadak holds that three people are required even {\i mide\u8217'oraita}, while Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi holds that one suffices {\i mide\u8217'oraita} and three are required only {\i miderabbanan} (so that one may acquire while two serve as witnesses). The {\i Mishna} in the previous {\i perek} apparently involved a case with only two people \u8212- the {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} and the one being {\i mafkir} \u8212- and the {\i Ritva} resolves this by invoking {\i sha\u8217'at hadachak}: because the person had nothing to eat, it was a pressing situation, and even if one follows Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi and requires three rabbinically, the rabbis relaxed their requirement in a case of genuine need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, the {\i Machaneh Ephraim} draws a distinction between land ({\i karka}) and movable property ({\i metaltelin}). Ownership of movable objects is, in a sense, constituted more immediately by physical possession \u8212- you hold it, you own it; you put it down, and someone else picks it up, and it is {\i hefker}. Land, by contrast, involves a much more formal and layered structure of ownership, requiring public establishment of rights \u8212- analogous to filing a deed with a municipal office in modern law. On this view, even according to Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, the rabbinic requirement of three applies only to land; for movable property, even {\i miderabbanan} one person suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solo Hefker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question is whether {\i hefker} declared entirely by oneself \u8212- {\i hefker beino levein atzmo}, with no one present at all \u8212- takes any effect. The {\i Rosh} holds that it does work, at least at some level, and the {\i Kesef Mishnah} notes this position. The Rambam, by contrast, appears to hold that {\i mide\u8217'oraita} at least one person must be present, and {\i miderabbanan} three are required. The logic behind the Rambam\u8217's position is pointed: if you declare something {\i hefker} with no one around, what have you accomplished? Everyone else will continue to regard the object as yours, and in practical terms it functions as if you still own it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation loops back directly to the question of {\i bitul chametz}: if the entire act of {\i bitul} is performed privately, with no one present and no external manifestation, does it accomplish anything? The debate between Tosafot and the Rambam about the nature of {\i hefker} bears directly on this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shulchan Aruch Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Interestingly, the {\i Shulchan Aruch} does not rule uniformly. In {\i Orach Chayim}, the {\i Shulchan Aruch} rules in accordance with the {\i Rosh} and accepts {\i hefker beino levein atzmo} in the context of {\i shevitat behemto} \u8212- the prohibition against allowing one\u8217's animal to work on {\i Shabbat}. If a person has lent his animal to a non-Jew who intends to plow with it on {\i Shabbat}, the owner may declare the animal {\i hefker} privately on {\i Shabbat} morning and thereby remove it from his ownership, dissolving the prohibition. In {\i Choshen Mishpat}, however, where the {\i Shulchan Aruch} addresses property law more directly, it rules like the Rambam \u8212- requiring at least one person present even {\i mide\u8217'oraita}, and three {\i miderabbanan}. The {\i Shulchan Aruch} thus applies different standards depending on the context: a leniency for {\i shevitat behemto} that it does not extend to ordinary property transactions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Perek Hashutfim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Breira Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to {\i amud bet} and the opening of {\i Perek Hashutfim}. The {\i perek} introduces one of the most far-reaching and technically demanding topics in all of {\i Shas}: {\i breira}. The concept of {\i breira} \u8212- {\i huvar hadavar lemafrea} \u8212- asks whether something that happens later can retroactively clarify the status of something that was ambiguous or unresolved at an earlier moment. Can a subsequent event cast its light backward in time and determine what was true at the outset?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question recurs across six or seven {\i sugyos} throughout {\i Shas}, with additional {\i gemaras} that echo those discussions \u8212- touching on {\i Gittin}, {\i Eruvin}, and the present {\i masechta}, among others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first classic case involves separating {\i terumah} and {\i maaser} from a barrel of wine on {\i Shabbat}, when such separation is rabbinically prohibited. The person wishes to drink from the barrel but cannot perform the separation formally. So he declares: whatever remains in the bottom of the barrel at the end will be the {\i terumah} and {\i maaser}, designated accordingly, and the rest is {\i chullin} and permitted. He makes sure to leave behind the correct proportion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is apparent: throughout the drinking, the wine in the barrel is all mixed together. No wine has actually been separated; one has merely designated {\i wine that has not yet been identified} as the future {\i terumah}. When the dregs finally remain at the bottom, the claim is that it has now been {\i huvar hadavar lemafrea} \u8212- retroactively clarified \u8212- that those dregs were always the {\i terumah}. This is {\i breira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yesh/Ein Breira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fundamental {\i machlokes} is between {\i yesh breira} \u8212- there is {\i breira}, we can employ this mechanism \u8212- and {\i ein breira} \u8212- we cannot. The {\i halacha} as established based on a principle derived from {\i Beitzah} is that {\i yesh breira} applies to rabbinic prohibitions and {\i ein breira} applies to biblical prohibitions. The {\i Gemara} in the barrel-of-wine case is therefore not straightforwardly problematic, since separating {\i terumah} and {\i maaser} on {\i Shabbat} is a rabbinic prohibition, and for rabbinic matters we say {\i yesh breira}. The difficulty comes when trying to reconcile individual {\i sugyos} with this settled rule, since not every application fits neatly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eruvin Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second classic case comes from {\i eruvei techumin}. On {\i Shabbat} one may not walk beyond the {\i techum}, the boundary outside a city. A person who wishes to walk to a {\i shiur} on {\i Shabbat} afternoon does not know which {\i shul} the {\i rebbe} will be speaking in. He therefore sets up two {\i eruvin} \u8212- one in each direction \u8212- so that he can walk in either direction and still reach his house afterward. Then, when the announcement is made on Friday night specifying where the {\i rebbe} will appear, he retroactively designates which of the two {\i eruvin} should have taken effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In some ways this resembles a {\i tnai}, a conditional statement: I make this {\i eruv} on condition that my {\i rebbe} comes to this {\i shul}. But the {\i Gemara} notes that {\i tnai} and {\i shaliach} apply only within the same category of act, and that {\i tnai} may not fit naturally here. The reliance is therefore not on {\i tnai} but on {\i breira}: the later event \u8212- the announcement of the {\i rebbe}\u8217's venue \u8212- retroactively clarifies which {\i eruv} was always the intended and operative one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partners Mutual Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Neder Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i ha-shutafin she-nadru hana\u8217'ah zeh mi-zeh, asurin likanes le-chatzer} \u8212- two partners who have each taken a {\i neder} against receiving any {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the other are forbidden from entering their shared {\i chatzer}. The scenario is two people, call them Reuven and Shimon, who own adjacent apartments and share a courtyard \u8212- something like the hallway of a modern apartment building. Each has declared a {\i neder} of {\i hana\u8217'ah} against the other, meaning each has sworn not to receive any benefit from the other. They intensely dislike one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that since the {\i chatzer} is jointly owned, neither party may enter it at all. The reasoning: every square inch of the courtyard is co-owned by both partners. If Reuven walks across the courtyard, he is deriving benefit from that portion of the property which belongs to Shimon \u8212- and vice versa. Since each has taken a {\i neder} against benefiting from the other, and since there is no identifiable spot in the courtyard that belongs exclusively to one of them rather than jointly to both, neither may enter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov disagrees: {\i zeh nichnas le-toch shelo ve-zeh nichnas le-toch shelo} \u8212- each one may enter into his own portion of the {\i chatzer}. The {\i Gemara} understands this disagreement as reflecting the {\i machlokes} on {\i breira}. The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds {\i ein breira}: one cannot retroactively determine which portion of the jointly owned courtyard was \u8220"yours,\u8221" and therefore the entire space remains problematic. Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov holds {\i yesh breira}: once you are walking on a particular patch, it becomes retroactively clarified that that patch is the portion you own, and you are therefore walking only on your own property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partnership Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This forces a deeper question: what does joint ownership of a property actually look like? There are several possible models. One model: the ownership is like alternating stripes \u8212- Reuven fully owns patch 1, Shimon fully owns patch 2, Reuven fully owns patch 3, and so on, in a kind of checkerboard or striped pattern, with full independent ownership of each distinct strip. A second model: ownership is a grey blur \u8212- every single molecule of the property is owned fractionally by both parties, with each holding a percentage interest in every infinitesimal piece. A third model: the property is divisible into two halves, each partner owning one half outright as a separate and distinct parcel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical stakes of this question are significant. Even according to {\i ein breira}, if the partnership arrangement can be understood as the third model \u8212- each person owns one clearly defined half \u8212- then perhaps each could technically tiptoe through his own half without ever setting foot on the other\u8217's. But if the ownership is the grey-blur model, there is no square inch that is unambiguously one partner\u8217's alone, and the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's blanket prohibition follows naturally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's discussion of this {\i mishna} is extensive. He offers multiple explanations for Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's position, and these explanations are important because, as one looks more carefully at the {\i mishna}, the invocation of {\i breira} seems puzzling. Normal {\i breira} involves a one-time retroactive clarification \u8212- at the end of the barrel, the dregs are designated as {\i terumah}. But in the case of the {\i chatzer}, the partners are not passing through once; they live there and use the courtyard continuously. If walking in one direction is retroactively clarified as being on Reuven\u8217's portion, but tomorrow Reuven walks the same path, and yesterday Shimon walked it, the \u8220"clarification\u8221" seems to shift back and forth. It is not obvious that {\i breira} in the ordinary sense can bear the weight of this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam, whose view is cited within the Ran, makes an observation along these lines: this case may not really be about {\i breira} in the standard sense at all. The Ramban suggests it is a {\i weaker} form of {\i breira} than usual because the situation is so anomalous. The Ran, interestingly, takes the opposite position: he argues this is actually a {\i stronger} form of {\i breira} than the ordinary case. In a barrel of mixed wine, there is no prior agreement that could have specified which wine would be the {\i terumah}. Here, by contrast, there was an original partnership agreement between two real people with real claims to defined portions of a real property. The terms of the agreement, however imprecisely articulated, always contained the seeds of a division. Retroactively clarifying who owned which portion, then, is not conjuring a clarification from nothing \u8212- it is drawing out what was implicit in the original agreement. That is a stronger and more legally grounded use of {\i breira} than the wine barrel case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Real Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first explanation the Ran entertains for why Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov permits entry is that walking across a courtyard may not constitute real {\i hana\u8217'ah} at all. The {\i neder} forbade {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the other person\u8217's property. But is the act of putting one\u8217's feet on shared property truly a benefit within the meaning of the {\i neder}? Traversing a space to get from point A to point B, with no enjoyment of the amenities of the space as such, may simply fall below the threshold of {\i hana\u8217'ah} that the {\i neder} was intended to prohibit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is the case, then the mechanism that allows entry is not {\i breira} at all, and the case does not really turn on {\i yesh breira} versus {\i ein breira}. Walking is categorically outside the {\i neder}\u8217's scope. This does not mean {\i breira} is irrelevant to the case \u8212- the {\i Gemara} still invokes {\i breira} as a factor in Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's position \u8212- but it would mean that {\i breira} provides an additional layer of reasoning rather than the primary one. There is tension here that the {\i Gemara} will need to work through in the coming days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Millstones/Ovens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second clause of the {\i mishna} states: {\i u-shneihem assurim le-ha\u8217'amid reichayim ve-tannur u-legadel tarnagolim} \u8212- both partners are forbidden from placing a millstone ({\i reichayim}) or an oven ({\i tannur}) in the {\i chatzer}, or from raising chickens there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i reichayim} referred to is not the great circular millstone but the hand-operated flat stone grinder \u8212- a large base stone with a smaller stone moved back and forth across it to grind flour. The Torah itself singles out the upper millstone as something that cannot be taken as collateral ({\i mashkon}) for a loan, precisely because a household grinds fresh flour regularly and confiscating the millstone would deprive the family of bread.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tannur}, the oven, presents a parallel issue. In the world of the {\i mishna}, ovens were clay tandoor-style structures kept not inside the home but in the {\i chatzer}, functioning as a communal resource for all tenants of the courtyard. If one of the partners now constructs a {\i tannur} in the {\i chatzer}, this becomes a permanent fixture of the shared space \u8212- and a fixture that the other partner would ordinarily have the right to use as co-owner of the {\i chatzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two distinct rationales could explain the prohibition. First, permanent installation: by placing a {\i reichayim} or {\i tannur} in the {\i chatzer}, the partner is occupying shared space permanently \u8212- and that occupancy itself constitutes a use of the {\i chatzer} in a way that touches on the co-ownership and implicates the {\i neder}. Second, communal-use introduction: by introducing a communal-use object into the {\i chatzer}, the partner creates something that the other partner, as co-owner, would naturally be entitled to use \u8212- but now cannot, because of the {\i neder}. This creates a structural conflict between the {\i neder} and the terms of the ownership agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third prohibited act, {\i legadel tarnagolim} \u8212- raising chickens \u8212- raises questions addressed in both the {\i Yerushalmi} and the {\i Bavli} here. One issue is the principle of {\i zeh neheneh ve-zeh lo chaseir}: if the chickens roam the courtyard but their presence does not actually diminish anyone else\u8217's use of the space, perhaps there is no real {\i hana\u8217'ah} problem. Chickens are property of one partner, but they occupy shared space the way any movable object might. The {\i Yerushalmi}\u8217's treatment of this question will need to be revisited in more detail. A further issue is that the chickens, unlike the millstone or oven, are not stationary \u8212- they range across the entire {\i chatzer}, and one cannot invoke {\i breira} to argue they were always only on the owner\u8217's portion of the space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unidirectional Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then shifts to a case where the {\i neder} is unidirectional: {\i haya echad mehem mudar hana\u8217'ah michaveiro, lo yikanes lachatzer} \u8212- if one of the partners has been forbidden from receiving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the other, he cannot enter the {\i chatzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is, on reflection, more difficult than the mutual-{\i neder} case. Here the person forbidden from the {\i chatzer} is a co-owner of that property. He owns fifty percent of it. How can the other partner\u8217's {\i neder} strip him of access to his own property? The principle {\i ein adam oser davar she\u8217'eino shelo} \u8212- a person cannot forbid something that does not belong to him \u8212- would seem to protect the co-owner\u8217's right of access, since the {\i chatzer} is also his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i Tanna Kamma} permits this situation to arise. The {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's position in the first clause \u8212- that joint ownership means every fleck of the {\i chatzer} is co-owned \u8212- creates this paradox: because every inch is partially his, every inch is also partially the other partner\u8217's, and so the forbidden party can find no spot in the {\i chatzer} that is uncontaminated by the other\u8217's ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov responds with two points. First: {\i yachol lomar l\u8217'toch sheli ani nichnas, v\u8217'eini nichnas l\u8217'toch shelcha} \u8212- the forbidden party can say, \u8220"I am entering into my portion, not yours.\u8221" Through the mechanism of {\i breira}, he can treat his movement through the {\i chatzer} as movement exclusively through his own portion of the property. Second \u8212- and this is the {\i mishna}\u8217's resolution of the impasse in the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's model:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forcing Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'kofin et hanoder limkor chelko} \u8212- we compel the partner who made the {\i neder} to sell his portion. The {\i neder} he made has created an impossible situation for the other co-owner: the other co-owner cannot access his own property because the {\i neder} has rendered every portion of the shared space potentially problematic. Since the situation is untenable, {\i beit din} forces the {\i noder} to sell his share to a third party. Once he has done so, the ownership structure changes, the {\i neder} no longer attaches to that person\u8217's property, and the formerly forbidden party may now freely enter the {\i chatzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: must the sale be to the person forbidden under the {\i neder}? No \u8212- that would itself constitute {\i hana\u8217'ah}, since the person would be acquiring property from one he is forbidden to benefit from. The sale is to a third party or to some other arrangement that simply removes the {\i noder}\u8217's ownership from the picture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A close structural parallel exists in {\i halacha}: if two partners jointly own a slave and one frees his half, the slave is left in a state of {\i chatzyo eved chatzyo ben chorin} \u8212- half slave, half free \u8212- unable to marry a slave woman (as a free person) and unable to marry a free woman (as a slave). The {\i halacha} there, as here, resolves the impasse by compelling the other master to free him as well, because leaving a person permanently in this legal limbo would {\i levatel lo mi\u8217'periyah v\u8217'reviyah} \u8212- deny him the ability to fulfill the commandment of procreation. The coercive resolution in our {\i mishna} follows the same logic: an unworkable {\i halachic} conflict demands a structural remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Outsider Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next clause of the {\i mishna} takes the case outward: {\i ha\u8217'echad min hashuk mudar be\u8217'echad mehen hana\u8217'ah, lo yikanes lachatzer}. An outsider \u8212- someone who neither lives in nor owns any part of the {\i chatzer} \u8212- has taken a {\i neder} forbidding himself from receiving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from one of the two partners. He, too, cannot enter the {\i chatzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is consistent with the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's model: since the {\i chatzer} is jointly owned, and since one of the two owners is the person from whom the outsider is forbidden to benefit, every portion of the {\i chatzer} contains an ownership stake belonging to that person. There is no spot the outsider can stand on without implicating the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov again offers a way out: {\i yachol lomar letoch shel chavercha ani nichnas ve\u8217'eini nichnas letoch shelcha} \u8212- the outsider can say he is entering into the other partner\u8217's portion, not the forbidden partner\u8217's. Whether a visitor or outsider may rely on this mechanism \u8212- essentially claiming to use only the \u8220"half\u8221" of the {\i chatzer} belonging to the person from whom he is {\i not} forbidden \u8212- raises its own questions about trespass and permissible use of jointly owned space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rented Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces a case involving rental: {\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro, veyesh lo merchatz uveit habad mushcharim ba\u8217'ir} \u8212- a person who is {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from another, where that other person owns a bathhouse and an olive press which are rented out to the public in the city. {\i Im yesh lo bahen tefisat yad, assur} \u8212- if the owner retains a {\i tefisat yad} in those properties, it is forbidden for the {\i mudar} to use them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefisat Yad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i tefisat yad} \u8212- literally \u8220"a hand\u8217's grasp\u8221" or residual grip \u8212- is the central analytical tool for this clause and will be the primary topic of the following day\u8217's discussion. The question is: once a property has been rented out, does the owner retain any meaningful rights over it? Clearly the owner retains legal title \u8212- ownership and rental are distinct \u8212- but during the rental period the renter holds exclusive use rights. Does the owner\u8217's residual connection to the property during the rental period constitute enough of a {\i reshut} that the {\i neder} extends to prohibit use of that property?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} offers two positions: if the owner has {\i tefisat yad} \u8212- some form of residual use-right or access during the rental period \u8212- then the {\i mudar} is forbidden from using the property ({\i assur}). If {\i ein lo bahen tefisat yad} \u8212- the owner has fully transferred operational control and retains no use-rights during the rental \u8212- then the {\i mudar} is permitted ({\i mutar}), because the property is effectively no longer within the owner\u8217's {\i reshut} in the relevant sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exact nature of {\i tefisat yad} \u8212- what kinds of residual rights during a rental qualify \u8212- is left for the {\i Gemara} to elaborate and will be the subject of the next day\u8217's learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House/Field Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces a {\i konam}-formulation: {\i ha\u8217'omer lachaveiro konam leveitcha she\u8217'ani nichnas, uvesadecha she\u8217'ani loke\u8217'ach} \u8212- one who says to his fellow: \u8220"Your house is forbidden to me as a {\i konam} that I shall not enter it,\u8221" or \u8220"Your field is forbidden to me as a {\i konam} that I shall not take from its produce.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death/Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Met o shemacharo le\u8217'acher, assur} \u8212- if the owner dies, or if he sells the property to someone else, the prohibition remains in effect. This clause was already employed as a proof-text approximately two weeks earlier in the discussion of what can dissolve a {\i neder}: specifically, whether making a {\i neder} against a person\u8217's property lapses when that property is transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The language here is deliberate. The clause that follows the present one uses the formulation {\i konam bayit ze she\u8217'ani nichnas} \u8212- \u8220"this house is forbidden to me\u8221" \u8212- rather than \u8220"your house.\u8221" The distinction is precise: saying \u8220"your house\u8221" implies that the prohibition is tied to your ownership, and if you sell it, the prohibition should lapse. Saying \u8220"this house\u8221" declares the object itself \u8212- the {\i cheftza} \u8212- forbidden, independent of who owns it. Therefore, even after death or sale, the prohibition adheres to the object and the person remains forbidden from entering or taking from it. This {\i mishna} was a critical proof-text in that earlier {\i machlokes}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modern Applications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Corporate Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The foregoing discussion of partnership, {\i hefker}, {\i breira}, and ownership raises a question with profound practical ramifications: how does {\i halacha} treat corporate ownership in the modern sense? Most publicly traded companies conduct business on {\i Shabbat}. Shareholders own fractional interests in those companies. Does {\i halachic} ownership attach to those fractional shares in a way that creates {\i Shabbat} liability or {\i chametz} problems on Passover?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Minchat Yitzchak} addresses questions along these lines. David Zvi Hoffman in his {\i Melamed LeHo\u8217'il} has a {\i teshuvah} dealing specifically with the question of corporate ownership of {\i chametz} on Passover, arising from the case of a beer brewery. The underlying analytical question is whether a corporation constitutes a distinct legal entity \u8212- in which case shareholders own interests in the corporation, not in its assets directly \u8212- or whether shareholders are simply partners in a human partnership arrangement and directly co-own all corporate assets. If corporations can have genuine {\i halachic} personhood distinct from their shareholders, many of the problems dissolve; if shareholders are merely partners in the classical {\i shutafim} model, the same questions about joint ownership, {\i hefker}, and {\i breira} that animate the {\i mishna} of {\i Perek Hashutfim} apply with full force to every publicly traded company.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These are genuinely open and important questions, and one that the classical sources, composed before the advent of corporate law, could not directly anticipate. The {\i poskim} of the last century and a half have had to work carefully with the conceptual tools the {\i Gemara} provides to address a commercial world the {\i Tannaim} and {\i Amoraim} could not have foreseen.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 46\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shutfin Mishnah Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro & Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's shiur covers {\i Nedarim} 46, the beginning of {\i Perek HaShutfin}. We begin by reviewing the {\i mishnah} that was introduced at the end of the previous {\i daf} before proceeding into the {\i gemara\u8217's} analysis. The {\i perek} concerns partners \u8212- {\i shutfin} \u8212- who hold joint ownership of a {\i chatzer}, perhaps a shared courtyard between two adjoining buildings or a communal hallway in a larger structure, and who have taken {\i nedarim} affecting their ability to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} from one another or from the jointly owned space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Partners Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i hashutfin shenadu hana\u8217'ah zeh mizeh, asurim lehikanes lachatzer} \u8212- if two business partners have each taken a {\i neder} against deriving benefit from the other, both are prohibited from entering the jointly owned {\i chatzer}. Since each of them has partial ownership of that space, and since neither can receive {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the other\u8217's portion, the entire {\i chatzer} becomes inaccessible to both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} disagrees: {\i zeh nichnas letoch shelo vezeh nichnas letoch shelo} \u8212- each one may enter into his own part. This ruling rests on the principle of {\i yesh breira}, the concept of retroactive clarification of the terms of an agreement. Even though the {\i chatzer} was held in undifferentiated joint ownership, {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} holds that it can become clear after the fact which portion belongs to which partner. Wherever a person walks at any given moment, he may claim retroactively that this was the portion designated for his use \u8212- so long as the {\i neder} against the other\u8217's property is not violated. On this view, each partner can navigate the entire {\i chatzer} by invoking {\i breira} to designate his current location as his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In addition to the prohibition on entering the {\i chatzer}, the {\i mishnah} states that {\i ushneihem asurim leha\u8217'amid reichaim vetannur ulegadel tarnigolim} \u8212- both are forbidden to place a millstone or an oven in the {\i chatzer} or to raise chickens there. Objects like a {\i reichaim} or a {\i tannur} are heavy, stationary, and permanently occupy a fixed portion of the space. Chickens, by contrast, range freely and effectively commandeer the entire {\i chatzer}. Both types of use therefore implicate the other partner\u8217's property in a way that triggers the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what meaningful {\i hana\u8217'ah} is at stake from merely walking through a communal {\i chatzer}? The {\i Ran} addresses this point based on the {\i gemara} in {\i Bava Batra}. Partners in a jointly owned {\i chatzer} each hold a right to protest certain encroachments \u8212- most notably the permanent installation of structures like the {\i tannur} and {\i reichaim}. The {\i Ran} suggests that the right not to exercise that veto \u8212- that is, refraining from lodging a protest \u8212- itself constitutes a form of {\i hana\u8217'ah}. It is the forbearance of the other partner, his tacit consent to one\u8217's use of the common space, that carries halachic weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Partner Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses a case where only one of the two partners has a {\i neder}: {\i haya echad mehem mudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro, lo yikanes lachatzer} \u8212- if one of them is {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from the other, he may not enter the {\i chatzer}. There is an important ambiguity here about how to read this case. One reading is that {\i Reuven} forbade {\i Shimon} from deriving benefit from his ({\i Reuven\u8217's}) property \u8212- that is, {\i Reuven} imposed the {\i neder} on {\i Shimon}. A second reading is that {\i Reuven} himself took a {\i neder} not to derive benefit from {\i Shimon}. This distinction matters greatly in light of the principle {\i ein adam oser davar she\u8217'eino shelo} \u8212- a person cannot render forbidden something that does not belong to him. If {\i Reuven} attempts to forbid {\i Shimon} from the {\i chatzer} despite the fact that {\i Shimon} holds partial ownership in it, the application of that principle becomes complicated. Some {\i Rishonim} therefore prefer the reading that it is {\i Reuven} who has taken the {\i neder} upon himself. Others hold that even the first reading is valid, because partial joint ownership creates sufficient intermingling of interests to allow one partner to restrict the other\u8217's access.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} again permits: {\i yachol hu lomar lo letoch sheli ani nichnas ve\u8217'eini nichnas letoch shelcha} \u8212- he can always say, \u8220"I am entering my portion, not yours.\u8221" The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i vekofin et hanoder limkor et chelko} \u8212- the {\i beit din} compels the {\i noder} to sell his share. This detail provides important support for reading the {\i mishnah} as the first case \u8212- where {\i Reuven} imposed the {\i neder} on {\i Shimon}. If the situation were merely that {\i Reuven} forbade himself from {\i Shimon\u8217's} property, it would be difficult to understand why {\i beit din} would force {\i Reuven} to divest his own holding. The compulsion to sell suggests that {\i Reuven} is the one who has created an unbearable situation for {\i Shimon}, and it is {\i Reuven\u8217's} portion that must be removed from the equation. Both a textual and a conceptual argument thus exist on either side of this interpretive dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Party Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} moves to a third scenario: {\i haya echad min hashuk mudar be\u8217'echad mehen hana\u8217'ah, harei zeh lo yikanes lachatzer} \u8212- a third party from outside the {\i chatzer}, from the {\i shuk}, has taken a {\i neder} not to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} from one of the partners. He may not enter the {\i chatzer} because every corner of it is jointly owned by both partners, and he cannot enter any portion without encountering the one from whom he is {\i mudar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} permits: {\i letoch shel chaverecha ani nichnas ve\u8217'eini nichnas letoch shelcha} \u8212- whenever this third party enters, he can invoke the principle that he is entering the other partner\u8217's portion, not the forbidden one\u8217's. Here again, {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} applies the same logic of retroactive designation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case raises the question of how a third party from outside acquires any right of access to the {\i chatzer} to begin with \u8212- given {\i ein adam oser davar she\u8217'eino shelo}, one cannot forbid what isn\u8217't his, and conversely, one does not ordinarily have rights in another\u8217's property. The {\i Ran} cites the {\i Rashba} and the {\i Ra\u8217'ah} on this point. The {\i Rashba} argues that the case applies {\i davka} when the outsider is entering {\i letzorekh hashutafut} \u8212- specifically in connection with business he is transacting with one of the partners. It is only because of an existing relationship with one of the residents that the {\i chatzer} is relevant to him at all; without such a relationship, his entry would be mere trespass and the {\i neder} would have no operative subject matter. The {\i Ra\u8217'avad} disagrees and permits more broadly, though the {\i Ran} finds the {\i Ra\u8217'avad\u8217's} reasoning unconvincing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bathhouse/Press Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth case in the {\i mishnah}: {\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro veyesh lo merchatz uveit habad muskarin ba\u8217'ir} \u8212- a person is {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from another, and that other person owns a bathhouse or an olive press that are leased out in the city. {\i Im yesh lo bahen tefisat yad, assur} \u8212- if the owner retains {\i tefisat yad} in them, it is forbidden. {\i Ein lo bahen tefisat yad, mutar} \u8212- if he does not, it is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Understanding who is {\i mudar} from whom requires careful reading. The {\i mishnah} states {\i hamudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro veyesh lo merchatz} \u8212- the one who owns the {\i merchatz} appears to be the person from whom the {\i mudar} is forbidden. To draw the picture: the owner of the {\i merchatz} \u8212- call him {\i Reuven} \u8212- has leased it to {\i Shimon}. A third party, {\i Levi}, is {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from {\i Reuven}. The question is whether {\i Levi} using the bathhouse constitutes receiving {\i hana\u8217'ah} from {\i Reuven}. This depends on the nature of {\i Reuven\u8217's} lease arrangement with {\i Shimon}. If {\i Reuven} leases on a percentage basis \u8212- receiving a share of the proceeds \u8212- then every additional customer, including {\i Levi}, directly increases {\i Reuven\u8217's} income. In that case {\i Levi} is forbidden from using the {\i merchatz} because his patronage benefits {\i Reuven}. If, however, {\i Shimon} pays {\i Reuven} a flat fee regardless of how many customers use the bathhouse, then {\i Levi\u8217's} use generates no {\i hana\u8217'ah} for {\i Reuven}, and {\i Levi} is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i tefisat yad} thus points to a residual ownership interest expressed through ongoing financial stake \u8212- a percentage of the proceeds that keeps {\i Reuven} invested in the day-to-day success of the enterprise. The {\i gemara} at 46b will develop this point further, so a fuller treatment is reserved for the discussion of {\i tefisat yad} below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House/Field Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes with two related formulations. {\i Ha\u8217'omer lachaveiro konam leveitecha she\u8217'ani nichnas} \u8212- if a person says \u8220"your house is {\i konam} that I should not enter it\u8221" \u8212- or {\i vesadcha she\u8217'ani loke\u8217'ach} \u8212- \u8220"your field that I will not buy\u8221" \u8212- then {\i met o shemecharah le\u8217'acher, mutar}: if the original owner dies or sells it to another, the {\i neder} is dissolved. This is because the formulation was {\i beitecha} or {\i sadcha} \u8212- {\b your} house, {\b your} field \u8212- making the prohibition contingent on the object\u8217's ownership by that particular person. Once ownership changes, the operative condition no longer obtains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast: {\i konam bayit zeh she\u8217'ani nichnas} \u8212- \u8220"this house that I should not enter\u8221" \u8212- or {\i sadeh zo she\u8217'ani loke\u8217'ach} \u8212- \u8220"this field that I will not acquire\u8221" \u8212- {\i met o shemecharo le\u8217'acher, assur}: even after the owner dies or sells, the prohibition remains. Here the {\i neder} was directed at the object itself, not at the ownership relationship. The {\i mudar} would need to appear before a {\i beit din} to have the {\i neder} dissolved; there is no other mechanism by which he could ever enter that field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction invites the further question of what happens if the house is demolished and rebuilt. If the formulation was {\i beitecha}, it is plausible that rebuilding on the same property would preserve the force of the {\i neder}, since it is still \u8220"your house\u8221" on that site \u8212- though a completely new structure might challenge even that reading. If the formulation was {\i bayit zeh}, the rebuilt structure is arguably not {\i zeh}, that same physical building, and the {\i neder} would not apply to it. This parallels the well-known philosophical puzzle about the Ship of Theseus \u8212- at what point does incremental replacement of components change the identity of an object?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} in {\i Hilchot Nedarim}, {\i Perek} 8, {\i Halacha} 2, addresses an analogous case: a person who vows not to marry a certain woman because her father is wicked, or not to enter a house because there is a dangerous dog inside. If the father repents or the dog dies \u8212- {\i metu o she\u8217'asu teshuva, harei zeh mutar} \u8212- the {\i neder} lapses, because the original prohibition was implicitly conditional on the continued existence of the harmful circumstance. It is as if the person stipulated at the outset: this {\i neder} applies only so long as the problem remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishneh Lamelech} notes a further question raised by {\i Rabbi Eliyahu ben Chaim} (the {\i Ranach}): if the father did {\i teshuva} and then returned to his former ways, does the {\i neder} re-activate? The {\i Ranach} holds that it does \u8212- likening it to the house being rebuilt. The {\i Mishneh Lamelech} disagrees, arguing that the lapse of the {\i neder} upon the first {\i teshuva} was complete, and the subsequent relapse is a new development that cannot retroactively restore the old prohibition. The underlying question \u8212- whether a person who sins again after {\i teshuva} has retroactively invalidated that {\i teshuva}, or whether each episode is treated as a fresh transgression by a genuinely renewed individual \u8212- is itself a profound topic that runs through the literature on {\i teshuva} and is discussed in other contexts across {\i Shas}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bidirectional vs Unidire\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bidirectional Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a formal {\i ibaya}: {\i benadru pligi?} \u8212- is the dispute in the {\i mishnah} about a case where each partner took a {\i neder} upon himself not to derive benefit from the other ({\i nadru}, they vowed), or is it about a case of {\i hediru ze et ze} \u8212- where each one imposed a {\i neder} on the other, forbidding the other from his property?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These are importantly different situations. In the {\i nadru} case, {\i Reuven} says \u8220"I will not derive benefit from {\i Shimon}\u8221" and {\i Shimon} says \u8220"I will not derive benefit from {\i Reuven}.\u8221" In the {\i hediru} case, {\i Reuven} says \u8220"{\i Shimon}, you are forbidden from deriving benefit from my portion,\u8221" and {\i Shimon} says \u8220"{\i Reuven}, you are forbidden from deriving benefit from my portion.\u8221" The question the {\i gemara} poses: {\i mi amrinan binadru hu depligi aval behediru ze et ze modu lei Rabbanan leRabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} \u8212- do we say that the dispute exists only in the {\i nadru} case, but in the {\i hediru ze et ze} case the {\i Rabbanan} concede to {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why would the {\i Rabbanan} concede in the {\i hediru} case? {\i Deki anusim damu} \u8212- because in that case each partner is {\i anus}, acting under duress. {\i Reuven} did not choose to make himself forbidden from the {\i chatzer}; {\i Shimon} unilaterally imposed that restriction on him. You cannot tell a person he is barred from his own home because his neighbor \u8212- who shares ownership of the entryway \u8212- chose to forbid him. The {\i ones} situation is foundational: the entire arrangement of joint ownership is now being weaponized against someone who had no say in it. This gives the {\i Rabbanan} grounds, perhaps, to agree with {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} that the partners must at minimum retain access to their own portions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, {\i o dilma afilu behediru ze et ze pligi Rabbanan} \u8212- perhaps the {\i Rabbanan} dispute {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} even in the {\i hediru ze et ze} case, and the fact of {\i ones} provides no relief. This is the {\i gemara\u8217's} open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Sale as Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to resolve this question from the {\i mishnah\u8217's} second case \u8212- the unidirectional {\i neder}. The language of the {\i mishnah} is {\i haya echad mehem mudar hana\u8217'ah mechaveiro} \u8212- one of them was {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from his partner. The {\i gemara} suggests: {\i tani nadur} \u8212- read it not as {\i mudar} (imposed upon him) but as {\i nadur} (he vowed himself). That is, perhaps the {\i mishnah\u8217's} second case involves a person who voluntarily took upon himself a {\i neder} not to benefit from his partner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} immediately notes: {\i hachi nami mistabra detani seifa vekofin et hanoder limkor chelko} \u8212- the stronger reading is supported by the end of that clause, which says {\i beit din} compels the {\i noder} to sell his portion. If it were a case where {\i Reuven} voluntarily forbade himself from {\i Shimon\u8217's} property, why would we compel {\i Reuven} to sell his own share? He made a personal choice about his own conduct; there is no obvious reason to force a divestiture. The compulsion to sell makes sense only if {\i Reuven} is the one who imposed the restriction on {\i Shimon} \u8212- because then {\i Reuven} has created an impossible situation for {\i Shimon}, and the {\i beit din} acts to remedy it by removing {\i Reuven\u8217's} share from the equation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading then implies that the {\i mishnah\u8217's} second case is precisely the {\i hediru} scenario \u8212- and the {\i Rabbanan} there still disagree with {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} despite the {\i ones} dimension. That would suggest the {\i ones} argument does not persuade the {\i Rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this produces a difficulty. If the {\i mishnah\u8217's} second case is one of {\i ones} \u8212- if {\i Shimon} was involuntarily subjected to {\i Reuven\u8217's} restriction \u8212- then why does the {\i gemara} need to force {\i Reuven} to sell? The {\i gemara} asks: {\i amai kofin oto, ha menanas anis} \u8212- if he is {\i anus}, why are we compelling him to divest his property? The very premise of {\i ones} is that the situation is against his will and not of his choosing \u8212- so it seems unjust to then impose the additional burden of forced sale on him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer implied by the {\i gemara} is that the {\i ones} argument cuts the other way: precisely because the situation is one of {\i ones}, precisely because the partnership agreement has been rendered unworkable against {\i Shimon\u8217's} will, the {\i beit din} must intervene. The only equitable resolution is to compel {\i Reuven} \u8212- who created this situation by imposing the {\i neder} \u8212- to sell his portion and thus restore {\i Shimon\u8217's} ability to function. It is not a punishment of the {\i mudar} but rather the logical remedy when the structure of the joint ownership has been undermined. With this the {\i gemara} transitions to the debate between {\i Rabba} and {\i Rav Yosef} about whether the physical size of the {\i chatzer} affects the analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Courtyard Size Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba: Small = Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Zeiri: machloket sheyesh bah kedei chalukah} \u8212- Rabba\u8217's position (cited here in the name of {\i Zeiri}) is that the dispute between the {\i Rabbanan} and {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} applies specifically to a {\i chatzer} that is large enough to be subdivided \u8212- {\i yesh bah kedei chalukah}. In such a case, the {\i Rabbanan} hold that even though each partner theoretically has his own portion, the undivided joint ownership renders entry by either forbidden partner problematic. {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} disagrees and permits entry on the basis of {\i zeh nichnas letoch shelo vezeh nichnas letoch shelo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in a {\i chatzer} that is too small to be meaningfully subdivided \u8212- {\i ein bah kedei chalukah} \u8212- {\i divrei hakol mutar}: everyone agrees, including the {\i Rabbanan}, that entry must be permitted. The reason is that in such a small space the \u8220"private\u8221" portions of the two partners would necessarily overlap; there is no way to allocate each of them a distinct private zone. Given this physical reality, the logic of {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} becomes irresistible: each partner\u8217's use of any part of the {\i chatzer} simply is use of his own portion, because the space cannot be divided otherwise. The {\i Rabbanan} therefore concede to {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} when the {\i chatzer} is too small to divide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef: BK Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} challenges {\i Rabba\u8217's} position with a pointed analogy: {\i harei beit haknesset dechimi she\u8217'ein bo kedei chalukah dami} \u8212- consider the case of a {\i beit knesset}. A {\i beit knesset} functions like a {\i chatzer} that cannot be divided: its communal purpose makes it inherently indivisible regardless of its physical size. No one can claim a personal section of the synagogue the way a householder might claim a corner of his courtyard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} in {\i Megillah} explores the corporate character of {\i beit knesset} ownership. For a large city, the {\i beit knesset} was built with the broader public \u8212- including travelers and visitors \u8212- in mind, and therefore its ownership is so diffuse that the local residents cannot even sell it. For a small town, the {\i beit knesset} belongs more exclusively to its residents, who function as its shareholders. In either case, the space cannot be portioned out to individuals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i mishnah} later rules that when two residents of the same city take {\i nedarim} against one another, {\i shneihem asurim bidvar shel otah ha\u8217'ir} \u8212- both are forbidden from communal assets of that city, including the {\i beit knesset}. The {\i beit knesset} thus parallels the small {\i chatzer} (which {\i Rabba} says should be {\i mutar}), yet the {\i mishnah} treats it as {\i assur}. If {\i Rabba} were correct that a space which cannot be divided defaults to {\i mutar}, the {\i beit knesset} case should also be {\i mutar}. The fact that it is {\i assur} defeats {\i Rabba\u8217's} position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef: Size Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} therefore proposes the inverse of {\i Rabba\u8217's} framework: {\i machloket she\u8217'ein bah kedei chalukah, aval yesh bah kedei chalukah divrei hakol assur} \u8212- the dispute between {\i Rabbanan} and {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} applies to the {\i small} courtyard that cannot be divided. There, {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} maintains that each partner can navigate by invoking {\i zeh nichnas letoch shelo}, while the {\i Rabbanan} hold that even this small space is entirely {\i assur}. In the {\i large} courtyard that is capable of division, however, everyone agrees it is {\i assur} \u8212- even {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} concedes that when the space is large enough to be genuinely subdivided, one cannot plausibly claim that wherever one happens to walk is automatically one\u8217's own designated portion. The discreteness of potential private zones undermines the blanket {\i breira} argument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking reversal. {\i Rabba} had argued that the small {\i chatzer} is the easy case where all agree it is {\i mutar}; {\i Rav Yosef} argues it is the hard case where the dispute lies. The large {\i chatzer}, rather than being the contested scenario, is actually the one where both authorities agree the entire space is {\i assur}. The {\i beit knesset} analogy supports {\i Rav Yosef}: the synagogue, as an inherently indivisible communal space, is precisely the paradigm case where the dispute plays out \u8212- and the {\i mishnah} rules it {\i assur}, consistent with the {\i Rabbanan\u8217's} position in the small-{\i chatzer} dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Huna: halacha keRabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} \u8212- Rav Huna rules that the {\i halacha} follows {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov}. {\i Vechein amar Rabbi Elazar: halacha keRabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar likewise rules accordingly. The operative principle of {\i zeh nichnas letoch shelo vezeh nichnas letoch shelo} is therefore accepted as {\i halacha}, and {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's} invocation of {\i yesh breira} in this context becomes one of the recognized sources for applying {\i breira} in practice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Breira & Ones Concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 How Breira Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i halacha} established, it is worth clarifying precisely how {\i breira} functions in this context. The principle of {\i yesh breira} holds that an ambiguous designation can be retroactively clarified by subsequent events. Here, the joint ownership agreement for the {\i chatzer} never specified which spatial portions belonged to which partner. When {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} rules {\i zeh nichnas letoch shelo}, he means that wherever a partner is standing at any given moment, that location is retroactively designated as the portion that was his \u8212- it was always going to be his portion; the current act of use simply brings that latent designation into focus. The {\i breira} is not a new legal act but a clarification of something that was, in a sense, always implicit in the original partnership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This variety of {\i breira} is arguably more tractable than other applications of the concept precisely because there was a pre-existing purchase agreement undergirding the entire arrangement. Unlike cases of {\i breira} involving {\i terumah} or {\i tithes}, where the status of a particular quantity was indeterminate from the outset, here the two partners genuinely own the {\i chatzer} together. The task is not creating a new status but identifying what the existing ownership already implied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Ones Justifies Breira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ones} dimension reinforces the availability of {\i breira} in this context. The entire structure of the joint partnership was premised on mutual benefit and shared use. When one partner imposes a {\i neder} that forbids the other from accessing any part of the {\i chatzer}, that act fundamentally undermines the operating premise of the arrangement \u8212- not by agreement but by unilateral imposition. The partner who finds himself suddenly barred is {\i anus}; the situation is not of his choosing. This involuntary character of the restriction is precisely what makes it necessary to invoke {\i breira}: the original partnership agreement is being coercively subverted, and the retroactive designation of each partner\u8217's portion is the mechanism by which the equity of that original arrangement can be at least partially preserved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partnership Assumptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of fifty-percent joint ownership is less straightforward than it initially appears. When two partners own a {\i chatzer} together, what exactly does each hold? Does each own fifty percent of every square {\i amah} simultaneously \u8212- a kind of gray undifferentiated co-ownership throughout the space? Or does the arrangement carry an implicit understanding that certain zones are more naturally associated with one partner, particularly the area directly in front of each partner\u8217's dwelling? Does \u8220"fifty percent\u8221" mean fifty percent of the space at all times, or does it contemplate alternating exclusive use \u8212- each partner enjoying full access on designated days?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These questions recur throughout {\i Shas} in contexts involving jointly owned animals, jointly owned tools, and jointly owned slaves. The fundamental ambiguity of what shared ownership operationally means is precisely the gap that {\i breira} is invoked to fill. In the context of the {\i chatzer}, the practical implication is that whatever portion a partner is actually using at a given moment may be understood as the portion that was always notionally his \u8212- at least when the alternative would leave him with no viable access to property he co-owns.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tefisat Yad Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman: 25% Thresho\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i tefisat yad} case from the {\i mishnah} and asks: {\i vekamah tefisat yad} \u8212- how large must the owner\u8217's percentage stake be in order to constitute {\i tefisat yad} and thereby render use of the bathhouse or press {\i assur} to the {\i mudar}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Nachman: lemechtza, leshlish, uleravia} \u8212- Rav Nachman rules that {\i tefisat yad} applies when the lessor retains a profit share of one-half, one-third, or one-quarter of the proceeds. {\i Aval babatzir lo} \u8212- but if the percentage falls below a quarter, it does not constitute {\i tefisat yad}. On this view, only profit-sharing arrangements at or above the twenty-five percent threshold preserve sufficient residual ownership interest to make the {\i mudar\u8217's} use of the facility a prohibited {\i hana\u8217'ah} for the lessor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: All Percentages C\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} disagrees: {\i afilu babatzir assur} \u8212- even a percentage below one-quarter creates {\i tefisat yad} and renders use {\i assur}. Abaye\u8217's logic is straightforward: if the arrangement is percentage-based at all, then each additional customer who uses the bathhouse or press directly increases the lessor\u8217's income. Even at ten percent, one more person using the {\i merchatz} means the original owner earns ten percent more from that visit. The lessor thus has a real and direct financial stake in every individual use of the facility, and the {\i mudar\u8217's} patronage therefore constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah} flowing to the {\i mudar-mimenu}. Abaye\u8217's position appears to be the logically stronger one \u8212- if Rav Nachman accepts that a quarter-share creates {\i tefisat yad}, it is difficult to see why a tenth-share would not, since the mechanism is identical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Percentage vs Fixed Rate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent tension by identifying the case in which {\i tefisat yad} definitively does not apply: {\i heichi dami dishru, hamikabel betaska} \u8212- the permitted case is where the lessee pays a fixed fee, a {\i taska}. A {\i taska} is a flat annual payment to the lessor, independent of how many customers use the facility or how much revenue the business generates. When the arrangement is structured as a {\i taska}, the number of people who use the {\i merchatz} has no effect whatsoever on the lessor\u8217's income; he has already received his fixed payment. The {\i mudar\u8217's} patronage therefore generates no {\i hana\u8217'ah} for him, and use is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tefisat yad} is thus defined precisely as a percentage-of-proceeds arrangement \u8212- any arrangement in which the lessor\u8217's income is tied to the ongoing use and revenue of the facility. A {\i taska} (fixed rate) excludes {\i tefisat yad} entirely. Both Rav Nachman and Abaye agree on this structural point; their dispute concerns only the minimum percentage threshold at which a profit-sharing arrangement triggers {\i tefisat yad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Market Practice Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} invokes the phrase {\i machtela shilash verivea} \u8212- a standard formulation well-known from elsewhere in {\i Shas} (it appears, for instance, in the context of {\i geneiva} in {\i Bava Kamma}) \u8212- to indicate that the relevant percentages are not technically precise cutoffs but rather reflect common market practice for this type of transaction in this locality. The fractions of half, third, and quarter are not arbitrary; they represent the standard terms at which these leasing arrangements were conventionally structured in the marketplace. {\i Tefisat yad} is therefore defined not by an abstract numerical floor but by reference to market norms: whatever share of proceeds is typical for this kind of arrangement in this time and place defines the threshold. Below those conventional market rates, the arrangement is so attenuated that it no longer reflects genuine ongoing ownership interest; at or above those rates, the lessor\u8217's stake in the enterprise remains real and operative.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 47\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Houses & Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a {\i mishnah} presenting two distinct formulations of a {\i neder} concerning a house. In the first case, a person says to his friend, {\i konam le-veitecha she-ani nichnas} \u8212- your house is forbidden to me by {\i konam}, that I will not enter it. Similarly, {\i sadecha she-ani lokei\u8217'ach} \u8212- your field is forbidden for me to acquire. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i meis o she-macharo le-acher mutar} \u8212- if the owner dies or sells the house to a third party, the prohibition is lifted. The {\i lashon} of the {\i neder} was precisely {\i le-veitecha}, your house; since it is no longer his house once he dies or sells it, the {\i neder} no longer applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the person instead says {\i konam bayit zeh she-ani nichnas} \u8212- this house is forbidden to me \u8212- then {\i meis o she-macharo le-acher asur}: even if the owner dies or sells it to someone else, it remains forbidden. This {\i mishnah} had already been quoted in the {\i gemara} last week; we now arrive at the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} on 47a notes a striking feature of these two formulations: each carries its own {\i chumrot} in different contexts. Our {\i mishnah} establishes that {\i bayit zeh} is stricter regarding change of ownership \u8212- since it is the object itself that was rendered {\i asur}, rather than the object in relation to its owner, a transfer of ownership does not dissolve the prohibition. However, the {\i Ran} observes that {\i beitecha chamur le-inyan nefilah} \u8212- the formulation \u8220"your house\u8221" is actually stricter when it comes to the case of the house collapsing and being rebuilt. Since the {\i neder} was framed in terms of the owner\u8217's house, a new house built in the same place and owned by the same person would still fall under the {\i neder}. By contrast, with {\i bayit zeh} \u8212- this specific house \u8212- {\i keivan de-nafal azla}: once it falls, it is gone. The prohibition attached to that particular structure, and once that structure no longer exists, the {\i neder} dissipates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shivrei keilim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question of how much the rebuilding or reconstitution of an object preserves continuity with the original connects to a parallel in the laws of {\i tumah}. When vessels that are {\i tamei} break, {\i shivrei keilim} \u8212- broken vessels \u8212- ordinarily become {\i tahor}. However, the Rabbis instituted for certain types of vessels, particularly metal ones, that if you reconstitute the broken vessel \u8212- by soldering or forging the pieces back together \u8212- the {\i tumah yeshanah}, the old {\i tumah}, is reinstated in the newly remade vessel. The concern was that people would deliberately break a {\i tamei} metal vessel, render it {\i tahor}, and then immediately smelt it back together, avoiding the proper processes of purification \u8212- either immersion in a {\i mikveh} or, in cases of {\i tumat meis}, sprinkling with the ashes of the {\i Parah Adumah}. The institution of {\i tumah yeshanah} thus reflects a question parallel to the one in our {\i mishnah}: when you remake something, to what degree is it a continuation of the original object?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sukkah parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further parallel emerges from a {\i gemara} in Sukkah 27a, in connection with the position of {\i Rabbi Eliezer}. {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds that the {\i sukkah} one uses on {\i Sukkot} must be {\i re\u8217'uyah lekol shivah} \u8212- fit for use throughout all seven days. This creates a difficulty: what happens if the {\i sukkah} collapses during {\i Sukkot} due to circumstances beyond one\u8217's control \u8212- an {\i onus} \u8212- and is then rebuilt? The {\i gemara} raises the objection {\i hai achriti hi ve-einah le-shivah}: the rebuilt {\i sukkah} is an entirely new {\i sukkah} and was not fit for all seven days from the outset. The {\i gemara} resolves that even according to {\i Rabbi Eliezer} it would still be valid, and {\i Tosfos} there suggests that the dispensation may require rebuilding with the same materials. Using the same wood might render the rebuilt structure sufficiently continuous with the original {\i sukkah} \u8212- analogous to the question here of whether rebuilding a house on the same plot with the same materials constitutes the same house for purposes of the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder & possession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avimi\u8217's question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a question attributed to {\i Avimi}. The case under discussion is {\i konam labayit zeh she-atah nichnas} \u8212- this house is forbidden for you to enter. The {\i gemara} notes that this must be referring to a house currently belonging to the person making the {\i neder}, because {\i ein adam oser davar she-eino shelo} \u8212- a person cannot render someone else\u8217's property forbidden to a third party. One can affect one\u8217's own status regarding one\u8217's own property, one\u8217's own status regarding another\u8217's property, or another person\u8217's status regarding one\u8217's own property, but one cannot alter another person\u8217's relationship to that person\u8217's own property. Therefore {\i konam labayit zeh} must mean: my house, which I am forbidding you to enter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i meis o she-mecharo le-acher mahu} \u8212- what is the law if that person, the owner who made the {\i neder}, subsequently dies or sells the property to someone else? The underlying question is formulated as: {\i adam oser davar she-birshuto lichshe-yeitzei me-reshuto o lo} \u8212- can a person forbid something that is currently in his possession even after it leaves his possession? The owner had legal control over the property at the moment of the {\i neder} and could thereby create the prohibition. But once the property changes hands \u8212- through death or sale \u8212- does the prohibition continue? Did the {\i neder} alter the intrinsic character of the house, or was the prohibition contingent on the ongoing ownership relationship, such that it dissipates once the connection is severed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} offers a proof from a {\i sugya} in {\i Bava Kamma}. The case is: {\i ha-omer livno konam she-atah neheneh li} \u8212- a father says to his son that the son is forbidden from deriving benefit from him. If the father subsequently dies, {\i yirashenu} \u8212- the son may inherit from him. The rationale is that at the moment of death the father is no longer a living person in the same sense, and so the inheritance, which passes at that moment, is no longer really \u8220"the father\u8217's property\u8221" in the operative sense. However, if the father added in his {\i lashon} the phrase {\i be-chayai u-ve-moti} \u8212- during my lifetime and after my death \u8212- then {\i bemet lo yirashenu}: the son cannot inherit, because the explicit language extended the prohibition beyond the father\u8217's lifetime. {\i Rava} draws the conclusion: {\i shema mina adam oser davar she-birshuto lichshe-yeitzei me-reshuto} \u8212- a person does indeed have the legal power to create a prohibition over property in his possession that will persist even after it leaves his possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit vs explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This proof raises a related point about the role of explicit versus implicit language in the {\i neder} formula. The proof from {\i Bava Kamma} works specifically because the father made explicit mention of {\i be-chayai u-ve-moti}, extending the prohibition beyond death. Without that explicit language, the son could inherit. This suggests that the legal power to forbid an object after it leaves one\u8217's possession exists in principle, but whether it is activated in any given case may depend on whether the {\i neder}\u8217's {\i lashon} makes that intention sufficiently clear \u8212- or whether, as in {\i Avimi}\u8217's original case, the formula used ({\i bayit zeh}) is itself enough to implicitly carry that force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's problems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran}, however, raises difficulties with {\i Rava}\u8217's proof. The first concerns the principle of {\i zechia b\u8217'issurei hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- whether one can acquire ownership of something that is {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah}, forbidden to derive benefit from. The {\i Ran} and the {\i Rashba} disagree on this point. The {\i Ran} maintains that yes, one may own property even if it is {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- one simply cannot derive benefit from it. Ownership and permissibility of benefit are distinct categories. The {\i Rashba} questions this. This becomes relevant in the {\i Bava Kamma} case because the question of whether the son can have any legal relationship with the inheritance turns on whether one can hold ownership of something forbidden to benefit from.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second difficulty the {\i Ran} raises is even sharper. In the {\i sugya} in {\i Bava Kamma}, it emerges that although the son cannot inherit the property \u8212- {\i lo yirashenu} \u8212- he can apparently pass it on to his own children, to the grandchildren of the original owner. But if the son truly cannot inherit, how can he pass anything on? One cannot transfer what one does not own. The {\i Ran}\u8217's position \u8212- that the son does own the property but simply cannot derive benefit from it \u8212- allows for this: he holds title, he simply cannot extract personal benefit. On that reading, he could convey the property to his children without himself benefiting from the transaction. The alternative reading, that he has no ownership at all, makes the mechanism difficult to explain. One might characterize the son\u8217's ability to direct the property elsewhere as a form of {\i tovat hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- a vestigial interest \u8212- but even that framing requires some analysis, since {\i tovat hana\u8217'ah} itself can constitute a form of benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh vs nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chaim} clarifies an important conceptual distinction that underlies this entire discussion: the difference between {\i hekdesh} and {\i nedarim}. {\i Hekdesh}, according to {\i Rav Chaim}, creates an absolute status within the object itself. It is entirely {\i cheftza}-based \u8212- the sanctified status inheres in the object regardless of who owns it, where it is, or what anyone\u8217's relationship to it may be. {\i Nedarim}, by contrast \u8212- even though we generally describe {\i nedarim} as operating on the {\i cheftza} \u8212- are in fact always sensitive to the relationship between the object and the person who declared the {\i neder}. This is part of what {\i ein adam oser davar she-eino shelo} reflects: the {\i neder} depends on the {\i noder}\u8217's connection to the object. The {\i sugyot} here are therefore exploring precisely what happens when that connection is conditioned on continued ownership. The distinction also explains why one cannot be {\i makdish} something that is not one\u8217's own \u8212- not because {\i hekdesh} is relational in the same way, but because one lacks the legal power to act on an object one does not control. Once one does have that power, {\i hekdesh} creates a permanent, unconditional status, while a {\i neder} may be contingent on an ongoing relationship.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fruits & proceeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i mishnah} is introduced from later in the tractate. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i konam peirot ha-eilu alai} \u8212- if a person declares these fruits forbidden to himself \u8212- whether phrased {\i al pi} or {\i le-fi} \u8212- then he is {\i assur be-chilufeihem u-ve-giduleihem}: forbidden not only from the fruits themselves but also from {\i chilufeihem}, the proceeds obtained by selling those fruits, and from {\i giduleihem}, the produce that grows from the seeds of those fruits if they are planted. This bears a structural resemblance to the world of {\i korbanos} and {\i hekdesh} \u8212- specifically {\i temura} \u8212- and also to {\i terumah}, where there is a concept of {\i gidulei terumah}: produce that grows from {\i terumah} fruit takes on {\i terumah} status as well, whether {\i mid\u8217'oraita} or {\i miderabanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami\u8217's question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rami Bar Chama} poses a question on this {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} describes a case where the person forbids the fruits to himself. But what if a person says {\i konam peirot ha-eilu al ploni} \u8212- these fruits are forbidden to another person? Does the {\i halacha} of {\i chilufeihem} \u8212- the prohibition extending to the proceeds \u8212- still apply in that case? Can this additional {\i chumra} operate when the {\i neder} is directed at someone else?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami\u8217's analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rami Bar Chama} builds his question on an analogy. He notes two parallel limitations on the power of the {\i noder}. First: just as a person can make someone else\u8217's property ({\i peirot chaveiro}) forbidden to himself, but {\i cannot} make someone else\u8217's property forbidden to that other person \u8212- {\i ein adam oser peirot chaveiro al chaveiro} \u8212- so too there may be an asymmetry regarding {\i chilufeihem}. Second: {\i chilufeihem} has a character of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} \u8212- something that does not yet exist. At the moment the {\i neder} is declared, the proceeds have not yet been generated; the money has not yet been spent for that purpose and has no connection to the {\i issur}. The purchase and the consequent designation of that money as {\i chilufei issur} will only occur later. Just as a person can make a {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} forbidden to himself \u8212- which the {\i mishnah} explicitly permits \u8212- but may not have the same power to make it forbidden to someone else, so too with {\i chilufeihem}. The analogy thus runs: one\u8217's power over another person\u8217's property and one\u8217's power over a {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} are parallel limitations \u8212- both represent things outside the {\i noder}\u8217's full control. For oneself, the {\i halacha} is lenient in both directions; for another person, both limitations may apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When it comes to making something {\i assur} to oneself, the {\i noder} has broad latitude \u8212- the principle is almost {\i ha-peh she-asar ha-peh she-hitir}, that one can create {\i issurim} for oneself. One can make someone else\u8217's property forbidden to oneself, and one can make a {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} forbidden to oneself. When making something {\i assur} to another person, however, one lacks the power to make that person\u8217's own property forbidden to him, and \u8212- by the same reasoning \u8212- one may equally lack the power to extend the prohibition to {\i chilufeihem}, which have no current connection to the {\i issur} and are in that sense analogous to a {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} under third-party ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two possibilities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus presents two possibilities. The first: {\i chilufeihem} should be treated like {\i peirot chaveiro} \u8212- as something subject to the asymmetry between {\i al atzmo} and {\i al chaveiro} \u8212- in which case the prohibition on {\i chilufeihem} would operate only when the person is making the fruits forbidden to himself, but not when he is making them forbidden to someone else. The second possibility: {\i o dilma mishum de-chilufin ke-gidulin dami} \u8212- perhaps {\i chilufin} are structurally comparable to {\i gidulin}. Just as the produce that grows from the forbidden fruit is forbidden universally \u8212- regardless of whether the original {\i neder} was {\i al atzmo} or {\i al chaveiro} \u8212- because {\i gidulin} and {\i chilufin} are both listed together in the same {\i mishnah} and both partake of the character of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}, perhaps {\i la shena hu ve-la shena chaveiro}: no distinction should be drawn between the case of the person himself and the case of his friend.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife & creditors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Acha Bar Minyumi} offers a proof toward resolving {\i Rami Bar Chama}\u8217's question. The case is: {\i ha-omer le-ishto konam she-ani neheneh lach} \u8212- a husband says to his wife that he cannot derive benefit from her. The {\i mishnah} then states that {\i lovin u-va\u8217'alei chovot ba\u8217'im ve-nifra\u8217'im} \u8212- creditors can come and collect the debt from the husband. The {\i gemara} asks: how does this work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding, the {\i Ran} notes that this {\i neder} must have been made before {\i nisu\u8217'in}, before the formal completion of the marriage. The reason is that after {\i nisu\u8217'in}, the husband already has obligations to his wife by virtue of the {\i ketubbah}, and a {\i neder} to the contrary would directly contradict those contractual obligations. If the {\i neder} was made before {\i nisu\u8217'in}, however, the husband could have created this prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama ba\u8217'alei chovot nifra\u8217'im} \u8212- on what basis can the creditors extract the debt from the husband, given that he took a {\i neder} not to benefit the wife? When the husband pays off his wife\u8217's debts, is that not benefiting her? And if so, would those funds not constitute {\i chilufei issur} \u8212- an exchange deriving from the forbidden {\i hana\u8217'ah}? The {\i gemara} asks: {\i lav mishum de-chilufin lav ke-gidulin dami} \u8212- doesn\u8217't this suggest that {\i chilufin} are not like {\i gidulin}? The argument is that if {\i chilufin} functioned like {\i gidulin} \u8212- extending the prohibition universally \u8212- then the husband\u8217's paying the wife\u8217's creditors would be {\i assur} to her just as the original {\i hana\u8217'ah} would be. The fact that it is permitted would then suggest that {\i chilufin} are not analogous to {\i gidulin} and do not extend the prohibition to the second party.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} rejects this as a conclusive proof. He says: {\i dilma lechatchila hu de-lo ve-im avad avad} \u8212- perhaps the distinction is only {\i lechatchila} and {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. That is, {\i lechatchila} the husband should not pay the wife\u8217's creditors in such a way that she benefits, but {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, if he did so, it stands. The reason is that when the husband pays off the creditor, the {\i hana\u8217'ah} to the wife is already two steps removed \u8212- the wife borrowed money, the creditor comes, and the husband pays the creditor. It is not a direct transfer of benefit from husband to wife, but something more triangular. Because of that remove, it is not fully captured by the {\i neder} in the same way \u8212- it is only {\i lechatchila} to be avoided, but not {\i assur bedi\u8217'avad}. Thus, the fact that the creditors can collect does not prove that {\i chilufin} are categorically different from {\i gidulin}; it may simply reflect the {\i bedi\u8217'avad} leniency of a two-step removal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's orlah proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since {\i Rav Acha Bar Minyumi}\u8217's case does not constitute a decisive proof, {\i Rava} brings a different one: {\i ha-mekadesh be-orlah eina mekudeshet} \u8212- if a man attempts to betroth a woman using fruits of {\i orlah} as {\i kiddushin}, the betrothal does not take effect. {\i Orlah} \u8212- the fruit of a tree during its first three years \u8212- is {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah}, and so although fruit generally has the value of {\i shaveh perutah} required for {\i kiddushin}, the woman cannot receive {\i hana\u8217'ah} from it. The analogy between {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} of {\i orlah} and the {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} created by {\i nedarim} makes this case instructive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orlah explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Getting married constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah}, as the tractate {\i Kiddushin} discusses extensively \u8212- cases range from a woman declaring that her {\i hana\u8217'ah} from watching a man dance shall serve as {\i kiddushin} money, to the extreme case of {\i adam chashuv}, where the woman\u8217's {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the distinguished man\u8217's willingness to accept a gift from her is itself the basis of the betrothal. Given that accepting {\i kiddushin} is {\i hana\u8217'ah}, and {\i orlah} is {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah}, even if the woman accepts the {\i orlah} fruit she receives no valid {\i shaveh perutah} and the {\i kiddushin} fails. However, {\i micharan ve-kidesh bi-dmeihem harei zo mekudeshet} \u8212- if he first sold the {\i orlah} \u8212- even though doing so was itself an illegal act \u8212- and then used the money received from that sale as {\i kiddushin}, the betrothal is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-step removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case functions as {\i Rava}\u8217's actual proof. The sale of the {\i orlah} and the use of the proceeds as {\i kiddushin} represents the same two-step removal seen in the wife-and-creditors case: it is not a direct transfer of the {\i assur} item but an indirect one. And yet {\i Rava}\u8217's proof also demonstrates that {\i lechatchila hu de-lo ve-im avad avad} \u8212- the same logic applies. Just as in the wife case, where the creditor payment is only {\i lechatchila} problematic but stands {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, so too here: selling the {\i orlah} was {\i lechatchila} forbidden, but the proceeds are valid for {\i kiddushin bedi\u8217'avad} because they are sufficiently removed from the {\i issur} itself. Both cases thus reflect the principle that a two-step removal from the original {\i issur} places the resulting benefit outside the full reach of the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legality discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether there is a meaningful distinction between an illegal act \u8212- such as selling {\i orlah}, which is {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- and the legal status of the proceeds. The answer seems to be that regardless of the illegality of the sale, the money received is simply money: it has attained a new status as currency and is not itself subject to the {\i issur} of {\i orlah}. The object has changed its form so thoroughly that the proceeds are no longer characterized as {\i orlah}, even if the transaction that generated them was impermissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orlah burning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further point emerges regarding {\i orlah}. Since {\i orlah} must be burned \u8212- the fruits are subject to destruction by fire \u8212- one might ask: what if a person gives a large quantity of {\i orlah} and the woman then burns all of it? Would the ashes, being worth a {\i perutah}, count for {\i kiddushin}? The answer is no, because items that are {\i nisrafim} \u8212- required to be burned \u8212- attain a new status through burning. The principle of {\i panim chadashos ba\u8217'u l\u8217'kan} \u8212- a new entity has arrived \u8212- applies: the ashes are no longer {\i orlah} and are indeed a new object. Therefore, giving {\i orlah} to a woman with the expectation that she will burn it and use the ashes as {\i kiddushin} would not work. The act of burning transforms the object into something new that is no longer connected to what the husband originally gave her, meaning it would not function as the consideration for {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rambam & inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question raised earlier concerning the son forbidden from deriving benefit from his father\u8217's estate, the {\i Rambam} in {\i Hilchos Nedarim}, Perek 5, {\i halacha} 7 states: {\i asar bno be-hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- a person renders his son forbidden from deriving benefit from him \u8212- and the {\i Rambam} introduces a case where it is stated that if the son is a {\i talmid chacham} a specified mechanism may operate to permit him access. Moving to {\i halacha} 8, the {\i Rambam} rules: {\i zeh ha-ben ha-asur be-yerushat aviv im natan yerushat aviv le-achav o le-vanav harei zeh mutar} \u8212- the son who is forbidden from inheriting from his father may give the inheritance to his brothers or to his own children, and this is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'avad objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ra\u8217'avad} raises a sharp objection. He asks: {\i u-mah tzarich litna\u8217'o} \u8212- why does the {\i Rambam} in {\i halacha} 7 need to introduce the condition regarding the {\i talmid chacham} at all? If {\i halacha} 8 already says the son can simply give the inheritance to his brothers or children, why is any special condition necessary? The next {\i halacha} seems to render the condition in the previous one superfluous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying tension in the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}\u8217's objection points to a deeper unresolved question: does the son actually own the property \u8212- and merely cannot derive benefit from it \u8212- or does he have no ownership at all? The {\i Ran}\u8217's position, as noted above, is that the son can hold ownership even over something that is {\i assur be-hana\u8217'ah} to him. On that view, the son owns the inheritance and can convey it to his brothers or children, even though he cannot himself benefit from it. The {\i Ra\u8217'avad}\u8217's objection implies that this is difficult to sustain: how can one give away something one does not own? And if one can give it away \u8212- as the {\i Rambam}\u8217's {\i halacha} 8 says \u8212- then why the need for any special condition in {\i halacha} 7? The question of whether the son possesses genuine ownership that is merely encumbered by a prohibition on benefit, or whether the prohibition on benefit entails a more fundamental absence of ownership, remains unresolved in this discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cherem & communal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cherem cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces cases involving the formula of {\i cherem}. {\i Hareini alecha cherem} \u8212- I am to you {\i cherem}, meaning my property is entirely forbidden to you \u8212- {\i ha-mudar asur}: the one upon whom the prohibition falls is forbidden. {\i Harei at alai cherem} \u8212- you are to me {\i cherem} \u8212- {\i ha-noder asur}: now the one who declared the {\i neder} is restricted. {\i Hareini alecha ve-at alai} \u8212- I am to you and you are to me \u8212- {\i shneihem asurim}, both are forbidden in their mutual restrictions, {\i ve-shneihem mutarim be-davar shel Olei Bavel} \u8212- yet both are permitted regarding things of {\i Olei Bavel} \u8212- {\i she-hefkirum Olei Bavel le-chol Yisrael}: because the returnees from Babylonia designated that property as belonging to all of Israel, it is essentially {\i hefker} for the entire Jewish people, and since neither party has individual ownership rights over it, neither has the power to forbid it to the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Olei bavel defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What constitutes property of {\i Olei Bavel}? The {\i mishnah} lists: {\i Har HaBayit, ha-azarot, ve-ha-bor she-be-emtza ha-derech} \u8212- the Temple Mount, the outer courtyards, and the wells along the road. {\i Har HaBayit} is owned by the entire Jewish people; no individual has the power to forbid it to another, since neither possesses individual proprietary rights. {\i Ha-azarot} \u8212- the extensions of {\i Har HaBayit} \u8212- represent a slightly distinct category, a point worth examining further. The {\i bor she-be-emtza ha-derech} refers to watering stations along the pilgrim routes \u8212- rest stops, {\i olei ha-regel} would pass through them and need to water their animals, and these too were established as communal property for all of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what {\i hana\u8217'ah} would one even derive from {\i Har HaBayit} that the {\i mishnah} is concerned about? Whether simply walking there constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah}, or whether it refers to bringing a sacrifice or observing the Temple service, requires further investigation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 City property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i ve-asurim be-davar shel otah ha\u8217'ir} \u8212- but both are forbidden regarding things belonging to the city. What counts as communal city property? The {\i mishnah} lists: {\i ha-rachavah} \u8212- the town square, {\i ha-merchatz} \u8212- the public bathhouse (specifically a publicly owned one, not a privately held bathhouse, a distinction that appears frequently in the literature), {\i beit ha-keneset} \u8212- the synagogue, {\i ha-teivah ve-ha-sfarim} \u8212- the ark and the books, meaning {\i Sifrei Torah} and also {\i Sifrei Nevi\u8217'im u-Ketuvim} (since {\i sfarim} in {\i mishnaic} language refers broadly to the sacred books). These items belong collectively to the residents of the city, who are in a sense shareholders in communal ownership of these facilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Radical implication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The radical implication of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling is striking. If two people declare mutual {\i cherem} on each other, and communal city property \u8212- including the streets \u8212- belongs to both of them as joint owners, then each has effectively forbidden the other from using the shared city infrastructure. This would include the streets themselves. A person who is subject to the other\u8217's {\i cherem} could not leave his house without using the streets, which now fall under the prohibition. The {\i mishnah} is therefore describing a situation in which mutual {\i cherem} renders both parties effectively housebound with respect to all shared municipal facilities. This is admittedly an extreme outcome, but it follows directly from taking seriously the logic of communal ownership: precisely because both parties are genuine shareholders in the city\u8217's public property, each has the power to impose a {\i neder}-based restriction on the other\u8217's use of their common share.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar consistency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a question about consistency with an earlier position in the {\i masechta}. {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov}, whose {\i breira}-based position was discussed in the opening {\i mishnah} of the {\i perek}, would argue that since each party also owns part of the shared property himself, he retains enough rights to pass through, and one person cannot fully forbid another\u8217's own portion to him. If that reasoning is correct, why does {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov} not register disagreement with the {\i mishnah} here, where the same logic of shared ownership is at work? That is an open question on the {\i mishnah} that warrants further examination.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Writing to Nasi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ktiva solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents a practical solution to the problem of mutual {\i cherem} locking both parties out of communal city property: {\i ha-kotev chelko la-nasi} \u8212- a person writes over his share in municipal property to the {\i Nasi}, the governor. By executing a {\i ktiva}, a document ceding his share in these public facilities to a designated third party, the person removes himself from the category of joint owner. Once he no longer holds a share, his {\i cherem} declaration cannot reach the other party\u8217's use of property in which he himself has no stake. The {\i Nasi} is identified as an ideal recipient for this transfer precisely because he functions as a natural representative figure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda view\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} rules: {\i echad ha-kotev la-nasi ve-echad ha-kotev la-hedyot} \u8212- whether writing to the {\i Nasi} or to a commoner, the mechanism works. However, {\i Rabbi Yehuda} draws a procedural distinction: {\i she-ha-kotev la-nasi ein tzarich lizkot} \u8212- when writing over one\u8217's share to the {\i Nasi}, no formal act of {\i zechiya}, of acquisition by the recipient, is required. But {\i la-hedyot tzarich lizkot} \u8212- when writing to an ordinary person, a {\i hedyot}, the transfer requires an actual act of acquisition on the recipient\u8217's part.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} disagree: {\i echad zeh ve-echad zeh tzrichim lizkot} \u8212- both the {\i Nasi} and the {\i hedyot} require an actual act of {\i zechiya} for the transfer to be valid. The {\i Chachamim} add: {\i lo dibru ba-nasi ela ba-hoveh} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} mentions the {\i Nasi} not because of any special legal status he holds but simply because it is the realistic and customary way of doing it. There is no substantive {\i halachic} distinction between writing to the {\i Nasi} and writing to any other person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Galil exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} further rules that {\i ein anshei Galil tzrichim lichtov} \u8212- the residents of the {\i Galil} do not need to execute this writing at all. The reason: {\i she-kevar katvu avoteihem al yadeihem} \u8212- their ancestors already signed over the relevant property in a similar manner generations ago. Since the land arrangements in the {\i Galil} were already transferred through prior generations\u8217' agreements, the matter is already settled for the inhabitants of that region, and no further document is needed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 49\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lashon & Cooking Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sixth {\i perek} of {\i Nedarim}, {\i Hanoder Min Hamvushal}, is organized around a central preoccupation: the role of {\i lashon} \u8212- word choice \u8212- in determining the scope of a {\i neder}. The {\i perek} demonstrates repeatedly that the precise term a person selects when taking a vow is not merely stylistic but legally determinative. Sometimes the difference lies between a noun and a verb, sometimes between a more general and a more specific term, and in each case the halakhic outcome differs accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mvushal Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens: {\i Hanoder min hamvushal} \u8212- if a person takes a {\i neder} not to eat cooked food \u8212- {\i mutar bitzali uvshaluk}, he is permitted to eat roasted or stewed food. The prohibition is specific: {\i mvushal} sounds precisely like boiled, and therefore no other method of preparation is included. Those familiar with the {\i mishna} in {\i Pesachim} at the tenth {\i perek} will recognize these cooking terms. The {\i mishna} there notes that on all other nights one may eat {\i basar tzali, shaluk, umvushal} \u8212- roasted, stewed, or boiled meat \u8212- whereas on the night of the {\i Korban Pesach} only roasted meat is permitted. That context will return shortly in the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tavshil Term\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces a second case: {\i amar konam tavshil she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- if a person says a {\i konam} that he will not taste a {\i tavshil}, a dish. The word {\i tavshil}, while derived from the same root as {\i mvushal}, refers to a broader category \u8212- a prepared dish, a side dish \u8212- rather than specifically boiled food. The {\i mishna} rules that such a person is {\i asur b\u8217'maaseh kederah rach}, forbidden to eat a soft, liquidy pot dish. However, he is {\i mutar b\u8217'aveh}, permitted to eat a very thick preparation of the same kind, since that would not qualify as a {\i tavshil}. The {\i gemara} will explain that the word {\i tavshil} denotes specifically something eaten together with bread; a thick {\i maaseh kederah} is eaten on its own and therefore falls outside the {\i neder}\u8217's scope. We also see that a person who says {\i konam tavshil she-eini to\u8217'em} is permitted {\i b\u8217'veitzat turmusa} \u8212- a type of prepared egg \u8212- and {\i b\u8217'idlat hamrimutza}, a variety of stewed squash or pumpkin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaseh Kederah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i Hanoder mimaaseh kederah} \u8212- if a person takes a {\i neder} specifically against {\i maaseh kederah} \u8212- {\i ein oser ela b\u8217'maaseh ritachta}, he is forbidden only from something actually boiled in a pot. {\i Tavshil} is a broader category that includes {\i maaseh kederah} as a subcategory; {\i maaseh kederah} is the narrower term. The practical hierarchy becomes clear: the more general term encompasses the more specific, and the person\u8217's chosen {\i lashon} determines which tier of the hierarchy is activated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Method vs.\u160 ?Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further case: {\i amar konam hayored lekederah she-eini to\u8217'em}, if a person says he will not eat anything that \u8220"goes into a pot.\u8221" Here the focus shifts from the type of dish to the method of preparation \u8212- {\i asur bechol hamitbashlim b\u8217'kederah}, he is forbidden from anything cooked in a pot. The distinction is between saying, for instance, that one will not eat stew (a category of food) versus saying one will not eat anything prepared in a pot (a method). These overlap substantially in vocabulary, which is precisely what the {\i mishna} is working through.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Nedarim Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader principle underlying this {\i perek} is {\i stam nedarim le\u8217'hachmir}: when a person takes a {\i neder} without specifying what it covers, we interpret it inclusively and stringently. Once a person does specify, however, we take their words seriously on their own terms. Importantly, the specification need not come immediately \u8212- even if someone later comes to ask whether the {\i neder} covers a particular food, the person\u8217's explanation at that point is credited. Both the rule of stringency in ambiguous vows and the weight given to the person\u8217's own stated intent reflect the fundamental principle that {\i nedarim} track what a person actually means by the words they say.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oshaya Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oshaya Forbids\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now brings a {\i baraita}: {\i Tanya Rabi Oshaya oser} \u8212- Rabbi Oshaya forbids what the {\i tana kamma} of the {\i mishna} permitted. Recall that the opening line of the {\i mishna} permitted roasted and stewed food to someone who took a {\i neder} against {\i mvushal}. Rabbi Oshaya disagrees and holds that roasted and stewed meat are included in the prohibition as well. He acknowledges, however, that {\i af al pi she-ein raya l\u8217'davar}, he does not have a definitive proof \u8212- only {\i zecher l\u8217'davar}, a mnemonic support or allusion, drawn from the thirty-fifth chapter of {\i Divrei HaYamim} (2 Chronicles).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse cited is {\i vayivashlu et ha-Pesach ba-esh kamishpat} \u8212- they \u8220"cooked\u8221" the {\i Pesach} offering in fire according to its law. This cannot mean they boiled it, since the {\i mitzvah} of the {\i Korban Pesach} requires roasting. The negative commandment is explicit: {\i lo tochlu mimenu na u-vashel mevushal ba-mayim} \u8212- do not eat it raw or boiled in water \u8212- and from this we derive {\i ki im tzali esh}, it must be roasted on fire. Yet the verse in {\i Divrei HaYamim} uses the word {\i vayivashlu}. The inference Rabbi Oshaya draws is that the root {\i b-sh-l} can function as a general term for cooking, encompassing roasting as well as boiling. Therefore, a {\i neder} against {\i mvushal} might include roasted food as well, since the word {\i mevushal} in its broader register means simply \u8220"cooked.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabi Akiva Eiger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A sharp question appears in the {\i Gilyon HaShas} of Rabbi Akiva Eiger. He notes that there is a verse from {\i Parshat Re\u8217'eh} in {\i Devarim} that uses similar language in connection with the {\i Korban Pesach} \u8212- {\i u-vishalta ve-achalta} \u8212- you shall \u8220"cook\u8221" and eat it. This verse is in the {\i Torah} itself and makes the same point as the verse from {\i Divrei HaYamim}. Rabbi Akiva Eiger asks: why did Rabbi Oshaya quote from {\i Divrei HaYamim} rather than from the {\i Torah} directly? This question has also attracted attention from those who approach {\i Tanach} with a source-critical eye, since the usage of {\i levashel} in {\i Devarim} in connection with the {\i Korban Pesach} \u8212- which elsewhere clearly requires roasting \u8212- has been flagged as an apparent contradiction. The resolution offered by later commentators is that {\i Devarim} there is addressing not only the {\i Korban Pesach} (which must be from {\i tzon}, sheep) but also the {\i Korban Chagigah} that accompanies it, which may include {\i bakar} (cattle) and is a {\i Korban Shelamim} that would be boiled. Alternatively, the word {\i levashel} in all these contexts simply functions as the general term for cooking, precisely the point Rabbi Oshaya is making.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah vs.\u160 ?Colloquial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially frames the dispute in terms of a fundamental question about {\i nedarim}: {\i leima b\u8217'ha kamiflagi} \u8212- perhaps their disagreement turns on whether {\i nedarim} follow {\i lashon Torah} or {\i lashon bnei adam}. On this reading, Rabbi Oshaya holds {\i halach achar lashon Torah} \u8212- the {\i halachic} meaning of a word is determined by how it is used in the {\i Torah} \u8212- and since the {\i Torah} uses {\i levashel} to encompass roasting, a {\i neder} against {\i mevushal} would include roasted food. The {\i tana kamma}, by contrast, holds {\i b\u8217'nedarim halach achar lashon bnei adam} \u8212- in {\i nedarim} we follow colloquial usage, and in ordinary speech {\i mevushal} means specifically boiled. This connects to an earlier debate in the {\i masechta} regarding {\i kinuyei nedarim} \u8212- whether words like {\i konam, konas}, and similar terms are artificial constructions invented by the rabbis so that people would not pronounce the sacred terms {\i neder} or {\i korban} lightly, or whether they are simply foreign loan words absorbed into ordinary speech. The deeper question is whether {\i nedarim} as a {\i halachic} category is created by the {\i Torah} and then applied to human behavior, or whether the {\i Torah} is recognizing and giving structure to something people already do naturally. If the latter, then what matters is what words mean to the person saying them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Everyone Agrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then reverses course: {\i la, dekulei alma b\u8217'nedarim halach achar lashon bnei adam} \u8212- in fact, everyone agrees that {\i nedarim} follow colloquial usage. Even Rabbi Oshaya does not hold {\i lashon Torah}. The true explanation is {\i mar ki atra umar ki atra} \u8212- each authority was following the way the word {\i mevushal} was actually used in his own locale. In Rabbi Oshaya\u8217's region, {\i mevushal} functioned as a general term for cooking \u8212- much as the word is used in modern Hebrew today \u8212- and therefore a {\i neder} against {\i mevushal} would include roasted food. In the locale of the {\i tana kamma}, the word {\i mevushal} meant specifically boiled, and so only boiling was prohibited. Both are right; they simply live in different places with different linguistic conventions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regional Variation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} spells this out: {\i be\u8217'atra de-Tana dilan, letzali karu lei tzali umevushal karu lei mevushal} \u8212- in the place of the {\i tana} of our {\i mishna}, roasted was called roasted and cooked was called cooked, each term having a distinct referent. {\i Be\u8217'atra de-Rabbi Oshaya, afilu tzali karu mevushal} \u8212- in Rabbi Oshaya\u8217's locale, even roasted food was called {\i mevushal}, cooked. The citation from {\i Divrei HaYamim} is therefore {\i asmachta be\u8217'alma} \u8212- nothing more than an {\i asmachta}. And indeed, Rabbi Oshaya himself signaled as much when he said {\i af al pi she-ein raya l\u8217'davar, zecher l\u8217'davar} \u8212- he explicitly disclaimed having a genuine proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asmachta Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a broader question: what is the function of {\i asmachta} in rabbinic literature? We know that some {\i mitzvot} are {\i d\u8217'oraita} and learned from {\i pesukim}, and others are {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, introduced by the rabbis themselves. Why, then, do the rabbis sometimes attach their own {\i halachot} to biblical verses by way of {\i asmachta}, as though providing a scriptural anchor for what is ultimately a rabbinic idea? A significant answer is offered by the {\i Ritva} in his commentary to {\i Masechet Rosh Hashanah}, where the question of {\i asmachta} comes up in connection with whether {\i zichronot, malchuyot, ve-shofarot} on {\i Rosh Hashanah} might be {\i d\u8217'oraita}. The {\i Ritva} explains that {\i asmachta} is not wordplay or a rhetorical game. Rather, it reflects the essential relationship between {\i Torah she-bi-khtav} and {\i Torah she-be-al peh}: God embedded in the words of the written {\i Torah} the capacity to give rise to the oral {\i Torah}. When the rabbis find a hook for a rabbinic concept in a biblical text, that {\i asmachta} demonstrates the {\i Torah she-bi-khtav}\u8217's {\i pluripotency} \u8212- its inherent capacity to generate and support the {\i Torah she-be-al peh} as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tavshil Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bread Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's second case \u8212- {\i konam tavshil she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- now receives direct attention from the {\i gemara}. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i ha mitavshil nadar}, didn\u8217't he take a {\i neder} against {\i tavshil} \u8212- why then does the ruling not simply prohibit all cooked foods? {\i Amar Abbaye: hai tanna kol midei de-mitachal b\u8217'rifta tavshil karu lei} \u8212- this {\i tanna} holds that the word {\i tavshil} denotes anything eaten together with bread. It does not mean any cooked food whatsoever; it means specifically a side dish consumed alongside bread.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamotzi Implications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This definition has direct {\i halakhic} implications. The accepted rule is that the {\i brachah} on {\i pat} \u8212- on bread \u8212- is {\i poteret ha-tavshil}, it exempts the recitation of a separate {\i brachah} on the side dishes. If {\i tavshil} means specifically something eaten together with and alongside the bread \u8212- not merely anything served at a meal where bread is also present \u8212- then the scope of the {\i hamotzi} exemption may be narrower than commonly assumed. The word {\i kol midei de-mitachal b\u8217'rifta} suggests something you actually eat the bread with or on \u8212- something you dip your bread into, spread on your bread, or eat bite by bite along with the bread. Questions follow naturally: does the {\i hamotzi} exemption extend to fruit served as a salad course, to dessert, to different kinds of drinks? These are questions the {\i poskim} address at length. It is worth noting that Rav Aharon Lichtenstein {\i zecher tzadik livrachah} was reportedly careful enough about this issue that on one occasion, when a melon was served as a salad course, he placed a piece of it on a roll specifically to bring it within the category of something eaten together with bread.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefta View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a contradiction from the {\i Tosefta}: {\i vehanoder min hatavshil asur bechol minei tavshil} \u8212- a person who takes a {\i neder} against {\i tavshil} is forbidden from every kind of {\i tavshil} \u8212- {\i veasur betzali uveshaluk umevushal}, he is forbidden from roasted, stewed, and boiled meat \u8212- {\i veasur behitriyot rakot}, and he is forbidden from soft gourds or squashes, {\i shehacholim ochlin bahen pitam}, which sick people eat with their bread. This {\i Tosefta} appears broadly inclusive, treating {\i tavshil} as a wide category that encompasses roasted meat and soft gourds alike \u8212- which sets up the discussion that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gourds & Health\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yirmiyah Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta}\u8217's final line \u8212- that sick people eat soft gourds with their bread \u8212- raises an immediate question: is this actually medically sound advice? {\i Eini, veha Rabbi Yirmiyah chalash} \u8212- is it not so that when Rabbi Yirmiyah fell ill, {\i al legabeih hahu asya leasuyei}, a certain doctor came to treat him? {\i Chaza kara demanach beveiteih, shakil venafak} \u8212- the doctor saw a gourd lying in the house, was alarmed, and left. The doctor\u8217's reaction seems to contradict the {\i Tosefta}\u8217's implication that gourds are appropriate food for the sick. One commentator \u8212- the {\i Rashash} \u8212- notes that this is a peculiar way to practice medicine: if a patient is eating something harmful, you ought to advise him rather than simply walk out. However that may be, the doctor\u8217's statement upon departing was: {\i amar malach hamavet it lei ledein beveiteih, veana eiul leasuyei yateih} \u8212- the angel of death is in this man\u8217's house, and I am supposed to heal him? The presence of the gourd in the house was so alarming to the physician that he regarded the healing effort as futile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Soft vs.\u160 ?Hard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i la kashya, ha berachichei, ha beashunei} \u8212- there is no difficulty. One case refers to the soft, tender flesh of the gourd, and the other to the hard outer shell or rind. Certain gourds have a very hard exterior that would be difficult and probably harmful for a sick person to eat, while the soft interior flesh is easily digestible and appropriate. {\i Rava bar Ulla} offers a variant formulation: {\i ha bekara gufa, ha beigabei} \u8212- one refers to the gourd\u8217's own flesh and the other to its shell. A further nuance emerges as well: younger gourds tend to be softer throughout, while older ones become tougher and thicker. Either way, the conclusion is that squash or gourd can be healthy for sick people to eat in certain forms but not in others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vegetable Pairings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Deamar Rav Yehuda: lulavei dekara besilka, lulavei dechitna bechucha} \u8212- it is good to eat the outer parts of the gourd with beets, and the stalks or seeds of flax with {\i kutakh}. {\i Kutakh} is the Babylonian condiment that the sages of {\i Eretz Yisrael} regarded with some revulsion \u8212- made from bread crusts, possibly moldy, and sour milk. It received attention in {\i Pesachim} in connection with the question of whether the {\i chametz} used in its production is halakhically significant or edible enough to matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Am Ha\u8217'aretz Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-davar ze asur le-omro bifnei am ha-aretz} \u8212- and this matter should not be said in front of an {\i am ha-aretz}. The warning is clear, but its rationale is debated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Warning Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three distinct explanations are offered. The {\i Ran} suggests that if an {\i am ha-aretz} hears that flax stalks or seeds are healthy to eat, he will pull up all the flax plants to consume them \u8212- damaging the flax crop. {\i Tosafot} offers a different explanation: an {\i am ha-aretz} will mock the {\i gemara} for including seemingly obscure and irrelevant medical advice about which varieties of squash are healthy, and this mockery is precisely the kind of objection heard even today from those who ask why the {\i gemara} contains such material. The {\i mefaresh} here advances a third interpretation. On his reading, {\i am ha-aretz} refers not to an uneducated person in the general sense but to the technically defined halakhic category enumerated in {\i Berachot} \u8212- people who deliberately avoid certain {\i mitzvot} such as {\i tefillin}, {\i tzitzit}, and regular {\i Torah} study. On this reading, the concern is that one should not share this kind of detailed practical guidance with someone who has chosen to be malicious in their religious conduct. Each of these three explanations reflects a different understanding of both the category of {\i am ha-aretz} and the nature of the concern.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cholim Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava amar: man kholin? Rabbanan} \u8212- Rava says: who are these \u8220"sick people\u8221" mentioned in the {\i Tosefta}? They are the rabbis, not physically ill people in the ordinary sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shemoneh Esrei Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava le-ta\u8217'ameih de-amar Rava: ke-man matzlinan ak-tzirei ve-amriei?} \u8212- This follows Rava\u8217's own stated position about for whom we pray when we pray for {\i ktzirei} and {\i mriei}, both of which denote types of sick people. Looking at the {\i mefaresh}, {\i matzlinan ak-tzirei} refers to the prayer for the physically ill \u8212- {\i rofeh kholei ammo Yisrael}, the {\i brachah} in {\i Shemoneh Esrei} that God should heal the sick. But {\i mriei}, also a term for the unwell, is associated in the {\i brachah} with {\i pleitat sofreichem} \u8212- the flourishing of their scholars. Since we have a {\i brachah} covering both the physically sick and Torah scholars, and since both groups are described using the language of illness, it follows that the rabbis themselves are called {\i cholim}. {\i Ke-man? Ke-Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- this follows the position of Rabbi Yosi. {\i Mi-de-amar ktzirei u-mriei, shema mina: ktzirei ktzirei mamash, mriei rabbanan} \u8212- from the fact that the prayer uses both {\i ktzirei} and {\i mriei}, we infer that {\i ktzirei} refers to the genuinely physically ill, while {\i mriei} refers to the rabbis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Study Weakness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why should rabbis be considered sick to begin with? There is a well-known {\i midrash}, found also in the {\i gemara} in {\i Shabbat}, connected to {\i Parshat Chukat}: {\i zot torat adam ki yamut be-ohel} \u8212- this is the {\i Torah} of a person who dies in a tent. The {\i gemara} reads this as teaching that {\i Torah} is acquired only by one who, metaphorically speaking, kills himself over it \u8212- pushing himself to the point of physical exhaustion, studying intensively for long hours, not eating adequately, not exercising, and spending his days and nights in the {\i beit midrash}. Such a person would naturally become weakened. One commentator connects this to the famous story of {\i Resh Lakish} and Rabbi Yochanan: {\i Resh Lakish} had been a physically powerful man, but once Rabbi Yochanan persuaded him to commit himself to {\i Torah} study, his physical strength left him \u8212- as though dedicating oneself entirely to the life of the {\i neshamah} necessarily diminishes the body. This dynamic has even been noted sociologically: in the {\i Yeshivish} world, the image of manliness and greatness is not the physically robust outdoor type but rather the pale, bespectacled scholar hunched over his seforim \u8212- someone who, by the world\u8217's standards, might appear weak, but who embodies the highest communal ideal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Thick Kederah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thick Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} had ruled that one who takes a {\i neder} against {\i tavshil} is {\i asur b\u8217'maaseh kederah rach} \u8212- forbidden from a thin, soft pot dish \u8212- but {\i mutar b\u8217'aveh}, permitted to eat a thick one. The reasoning, as we have established, is that {\i tavshil} means something eaten together with bread, and a thick {\i maaseh kederah} would ordinarily be eaten on its own rather than as an accompaniment to bread.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bread with Bread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes: {\i matni de-lo ke-Bavlai} \u8212- the {\i mishna} does not reflect Babylonian practice. {\i De-amar Rabbi Zeira: Bavlai tipshai, de-achli lachma be-lachma} \u8212- Rabbi Zeira said: the foolish Babylonians eat bread with bread. What they would do is take a thick porridge \u8212- a hot cereal made from wheat or barley meal \u8212- and spread it on bread, creating a preparation of carbs on carbs. The comparison to a potato knish is apt: a starchy filling inside a starchy dough. Rabbi Zeira, who was himself a Babylonian who had made {\i aliyah}, was in a position to observe and critique this practice with some authority. This is one of several moments in the {\i gemara} where the food cultures of {\i Eretz Yisrael} and {\i Bavel} are contrasted, sometimes with pointed humor \u8212- we have already seen the {\i Eretz Yisrael} rabbis\u8217' disdain for {\i kutakh}, and the Babylonian rabbis reciprocated in kind regarding certain Israeli dishes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kederah on Bread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence for {\i halakhah} is direct: if Babylonians eat even a thick {\i maaseh kederah} by spreading it on bread \u8212- using it as a side dish or condiment to bread \u8212- then in Babylonia, even the thick preparation qualifies as {\i tavshil} and would fall under the {\i neder}. The {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that the thick version is permitted does not apply in the Babylonian context, because the premise of the ruling \u8212- that thick {\i maaseh kederah} is not eaten with bread \u8212- is false in Babylonia.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Babylonian Foods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Pairings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Chisda} \u8212- Rav Chisda raised the question of {\i da\u8217'itsa}: {\i heichei ma\u8217'alei, demichla dechittei belachma dechittei, udesia\u8217'arei belachma desia\u8217'arei} \u8212- given that porridge is eaten on bread in Babylonia, which pairing is preferable? Should you eat wheat porridge on wheat bread and barley porridge on barley bread, {\i o dilma dechittei bedesia\u8217'arei udesia\u8217'arei bedechittei} \u8212- or should you cross them, eating wheat porridge on barley bread and barley porridge on wheat bread? It is worth noting that {\i Tosafot} there cites {\i Rabbeinu Chananel} to the effect that {\i sea\u8217'arei} in this context may refer not to barley but to lentils \u8212- which would give the porridge a protein component and make the \u8220"carbs on carbs\u8221" critique somewhat less damning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava achlei bechasissei} \u8212- Rava ate his porridge with {\i chasissei}, meaning he ate it with roasted or toasted grain rather than in the way others prepared it, dipping his bread into the porridge. The {\i gemara} in {\i Pesachim} explains this practice further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fingers & Taste\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabba bar Rav Huna ashkeichei leRav Huna deka\u8217'achil da\u8217'itsa be\u8217'etzba\u8217'ateih} \u8212- Rabba bar Rav Huna found Rav Huna eating porridge with his fingers. Rabba bar Rav Huna expressed surprise: {\i amai ka\u8217'achil mar beyadeih?} Why is my master eating with his hands? Rav Huna replied: {\i hachi amar Rav: da\u8217'itsa be\u8217'etzba\u8217'ateih bassim} \u8212- Rav taught that porridge eaten with the fingers tastes better, because you feel the texture and engage more fully with the eating experience. {\i Vechol doken bitartein} \u8212- and even more so with two fingers \u8212- {\i vechol doken bitlat} \u8212- and even more so with three. These were the Amoraim of Babylonia, who did not have forks and for whom the choice was essentially between eating with the hands and eating with a stick, as we shall see momentarily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Travel Advice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Rav leChiaya breih, vehachen amar lei Rav Huna leRabba breih} \u8212- Rav told his son Chiaya, and Rav Huna similarly told his son Rabba: {\i mezamnin lach lemachal da\u8217'itsa, ad parsa} \u8212- if people invite you to eat porridge, you should travel as much as a whole {\i parsa} to join them. {\i Lemachal bisra detora, ad tlata parsa\u8217'ei} \u8212- if people invite you for steak, you should go even three {\i parsa\u8217'ot}. Sound advice for any season.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spitting Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further teaching: {\i kol midam lo tiflot kammai rabbach} \u8212- do not spit out your food in front of your teacher \u8212- {\i levar mikara veda\u8217'itsa} \u8212- except for gourds and porridge. These two foods are compared to {\i ptila shel avar} \u8212- the lead wire used in executions by {\i sreifah}, where molten lead was poured down the throat. The image is of something extraordinarily heavy and oppressive sitting in one\u8217's gut, potentially very painful. Therefore, {\i va\u8217'afilu kamei Shavur Malka pelot} \u8212- even in front of a king, one is permitted to spit them out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fingers vs.\u160 ?Stick\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yose v\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- two of the great {\i tannaim} \u8212- are brought anonymously here, presumably because the dispute involves something somewhat uncivilized and the {\i gemara} preferred not to attribute the positions explicitly. {\i Chad achal daisa b\u8217'etzbaotei} \u8212- one ate porridge with his fingers \u8212- {\i v\u8217'chad achal b\u8217'hutza} \u8212- and one ate with a stick. The one eating with a stick challenged the one eating with his fingers: {\i ad matay ata ma\u8217'achileni tzo\u8217'atecha} \u8212- how long will you keep feeding me your filth? Without washing one\u8217's hands, eating with the fingers would contaminate the shared pot. The finger-eater replied: {\i ad matay ata ma\u8217'achileni roka\u8217'acha} \u8212- and how long will you keep feeding me your saliva? The stick, having been in one\u8217's mouth, goes back into the communal dish on each bite. Each man\u8217's method of eating was, from the other\u8217's perspective, a source of contamination.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehuda Tales\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Figs Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda v\u8217'Rabbi Shimon ayitu l\u8217'kameihu belusfin} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon were brought {\i belusfin}, which the {\i mefaresh} identifies as a kind of very overcooked or stewed figs. {\i Rabbi Yehuda achal, Rabbi Shimon lo achal} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda ate them; Rabbi Shimon did not. Rabbi Yehuda asked why. Rabbi Shimon explained: {\i elu ein yotzim mibnei me\u8217'ayim kol ikkar} \u8212- these will never fully pass through the digestive system; they are too dense and will leave one feeling blocked up. Rabbi Yehuda responded: {\i kol shekein shenishmoch aleihen l\u8217'machar} \u8212- all the more reason to eat them now, so that I can rely on them to keep me full until tomorrow. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's intention was not mere gluttony but economy: eating a satiating food now to carry him through the following morning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rutabagas Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda hava yativ kamei d\u8217'Rabbi Tarfon} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda was once sitting before Rabbi Tarfon when Rabbi Tarfon remarked: {\i hayom panecha tzehubim} \u8212- today your face is shining, or perhaps very yellow or ruddy. Rabbi Yehuda explained: {\i emesh yatz\u8217'u avadecha lasadeh v\u8217'hevi\u8217'u lanu tardin v\u8217'achalnum b\u8217'lo melach} \u8212- last evening we went out to the field and they brought us {\i tardin} \u8212- large beets or rutabagas \u8212- and we ate them without salt. {\i V\u8217'im achalnum b\u8217'melach, kol shekein shehayu paneinu tzehubim} \u8212- had we eaten them with salt, our faces would have been glowing even more. The commentators observe that this was a deliberate act of modesty on Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's part: his face shone because of his Torah greatness, as we will see momentarily, but he did not wish to embarrass Rabbi Tarfon by suggesting his student outshone him. He attributed the radiance to the rutabagas instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amra hi matronita le-Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- a Roman matron said to Rabbi Yehuda, addressing him with what amounts to a pun \u8212- {\i mora v\u8217'ravi}, which plays on {\i mori v\u8217'rabbi}, \u8220"my teacher.\u8221" She accused him: are you teaching Torah while drunk? His face was so bright and flushed that she suspected him of inebriation. {\i Amar lah: heimanuta b\u8217'yada d\u8217'hahi itata} \u8212- he invoked a kind of oath and replied: have I ever tasted wine except for {\i kiddush} and {\i havdalah} and {\i arba kasse de-Pischa} \u8212- the four cups on {\i Pesach}? Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's self-description as a near-teetotaler was not just personal modesty. He added that from {\i Pesach} to {\i Shavuot} his head aches \u8212- {\i min ha-Pesach ve-ad ha-Atzeret} \u8212- from having drunk the four cups. This passage is frequently cited in discussions of whether the wine used for {\i kiddush} and {\i havdalah} must be genuinely alcoholic. The position attributed to Rav Moshe Feinstein in the {\i Igrot Moshe} is that wine with some measure of alcohol is required, based in part on this very story: Rabbi Yehuda, who avoided wine at great personal cost, did not substitute grape juice even when he might easily have done so. This implies that grape juice does not suffice for these {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzeduki Insult\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei hahu Tzeduki le-Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- a {\i Tzeduki} said to Rabbi Yehuda: {\i panecha domim o ke-malvei ribit o ke-megadlei chazirim} \u8212- your face looks like either those who lend money at interest or those who raise pigs. The {\i mefaresh} suggests the {\i malvei ribit} comparison is because such people grow wealthy and therefore appear perpetually happy and flush. The image of Moses descending from Sinai with his face radiant is a relevant parallel, as is the verse from {\i Kohelet} that will appear momentarily. The {\i Tzeduki} seemed to be insinuating that Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's bright face reflected the contentment of someone engaged in prohibited activity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Latrine Visits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda replied: {\i bi-yehudai tarveihu asiran} \u8212- for Jews, both of those activities are forbidden. He then offered the true explanation for his shining face: {\i ella esrin ve-arba\u8217'ah batei ha-kisse it li min beita le-bei midrasha} \u8212- there are twenty-four latrines on the way from my house to the {\i Beit Midrash}, {\i de-khol sha\u8217'ah ve-sha\u8217'ah ani nikhnas le-khol echad ve-echad} \u8212- and every opportunity I have, as I walk to the {\i Beit Midrash} each day, I make use of every one of them. That is the source of his radiant wellbeing. The {\i Tzeduki}\u8217's challenge had also implicitly accused him of imitating the shining face that comes from ill-gotten ease; Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's answer \u8212- that {\i chokhmat adam ta\u8217'ir panav}, a person\u8217's wisdom brightens his face ({\i Kohelet}) \u8212- was the reply to the matron\u8217's accusation of drunkenness earlier. To the {\i Tzeduki} he gave a more earthly and pointed answer: proper bodily function, maintained rigorously on the walk to Torah study each day.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddush Wine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddush Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's wine consumption naturally raises the question of the {\i halachic} status of wine for {\i kiddush}. The {\i gemara} in {\i Pesachim} cites the {\i baraita}: {\i zakhor et yom ha-Shabbat le-kaddesho \u8212- zakhrehu al ha-yayin} \u8212- remember the Sabbath day to sanctify it; remember it over wine. This sounds like a {\i d\u8217'oraita} obligation derived directly from the verse of {\i zakhor}. Nevertheless, the consensus among the {\i Rishonim}, drawing on other {\i gemarot} as well, is that the requirement for wine at {\i kiddush} is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. {\i Tosafot} in {\i Pesachim} suggests a middle position: holding a cup of wine during {\i kiddush} may be {\i d\u8217'oraita}, but actually drinking it is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. The {\i mefaresh} in {\i Masechet Nazir} \u8212- who is not actually {\i Rashi} but a later author writing in that tradition \u8212- takes the more expansive view that having wine for {\i kiddush} is a {\i mitzvah d\u8217'oraita} in the full sense. The {\i daf} concludes here, at the bottom of 49b, and we will resume from that point at the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 50\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Radiance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three audiences respond\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning briefly to the series of three vignettes concerning Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's luminous complexion \u8212- described by the Aramaic word {\i tzahov}, which might suggest a yellowish tinge but almost certainly connotes a radiant, glowing countenance. Three different interlocutors posed the same question to him: his fellow {\i rabbanan}, a wealthy woman, and a {\i Tzeduki} (Sadducee). Each received a response calibrated to the questioner \u8212- more expansive and measured for some, brisk and dismissive for the heretic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Labor brings honor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this point the Gemara moves forward. We are told that Rabbi Yehuda, when he would go to the {\i beit midrash}, would carry a barrel or large jug on his shoulders \u8212- {\i shakil gufa al katfeih} \u8212- and he would declare, {\i gedola melacha she-mechabedet et be\u8217'aleha}: labor is a great thing because it brings honor to those who perform it. At first glance this seems puzzling. He is, after all, simply transporting something to sit on, a kind of personal shtender. It is not immediately obvious why this constitutes a significant act of {\i melacha} of the sort that confers great {\i kavod}. Nevertheless, this was Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's lived demonstration of the principle that honorable labor dignifies the worker, however modest the task.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shared cloak blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The narrative continues with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's wife, who went and acquired wool, {\i avda glima}, and fashioned a cloak. The arrangement was that when she would go out to the marketplace she wore it, and when Rabbi Yehuda would go to pray he wore it \u8212- {\i michasya umatzli}. On its surface, this passage conveys the remarkable austerity in which Rabbi Yehuda and his wife lived: a single cloak shared between husband and wife. When Rabbi Yehuda would wrap himself in it, he would recite a {\i bracha}: {\i Baruch she\u8217'atani me\u8217'il} \u8212- Blessed is God who has wrapped me in this cloak. Two points deserve attention here. First, this is a different {\i nusach ha-bracha} from the standard blessing recited upon wearing new garments. Second, the Rosh in Berachot cites this passage as proof that the threshold for reciting {\i Shehecheyanu} upon a new garment \u8212- or {\i Malbish Arumim} \u8212- is fundamentally subjective, determined by what brings genuine enjoyment and significance to the individual. For Rabbi Yehuda, who lived in poverty, this simple homespun cloak brought him real joy, and that was sufficient grounds for the blessing, regardless of how others might appraise its value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unisex garment law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The shared cloak also raises a pointed {\i halachic} question concerning {\i beged ish u-veged isha} \u8212- the prohibition against men wearing women\u8217's garments and vice versa. How could Rabbi Yehuda and his wife both wear the same cloak? The implication appears to be that a garment made without specific intention for either a man or a woman \u8212- a genuinely unisex article \u8212- is permitted for both to wear, and this episode serves as evidence for that position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehuda\u8217's treasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fast day refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces another story. {\i Zimna chada gazar Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel ta\u8217'anita} \u8212- one time Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, who served as {\i nasi} of the {\i Sanhedrin}, decreed a fast day, presumably on account of drought, as was standard practice familiar from Masechet Ta\u8217'anit. Rabbi Yehuda did not appear at the gathering. We know from Ta\u8217'anit that the public fast involved bringing the {\i Sefer Torah} into the town square and conducting communal prayer there, partly to accommodate greater numbers and partly to lend the occasion the character of public supplication. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's absence was noted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refuses garment gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The explanation offered was {\i leit lei kisuya} \u8212- he has no garment, no {\i ksut}, with which to appear. It is worth pausing on the terminology: {\i ksut} here refers to the outer wrap or cloak, as opposed to {\i beged}, which denotes more of an inner tunic. The distinction has relevance beyond this narrative \u8212- there are those who argue that the obligation of {\i tzitzit} as formulated in the {\i pasuk} in Devarim ({\i al arba kanfot ksutecha asher tichaseh bah}) attaches specifically to the outer wrap rather than to a smaller undergarment, which bears on the question of whether a {\i tallit katan} fulfills the {\i mitzvah} of {\i tzitzit} in the same sense as a {\i tallit gadol}, though this remains a matter of debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, himself of the wealthy {\i nasi} family \u8212- a family whose considerable resources, and whose occasional insensitivity to the poverty of {\i talmidei chachamim}, are documented elsewhere in {\i Shas}, most notably in the well-known episodes in Berachot where Rabban Gamliel embarrassed Rabbi Yehoshua until the extent of that sage\u8217's poverty became apparent \u8212- sent Rabbi Yehuda a new shirt. Rabbi Yehuda refused it. {\i Shadar lei glima velo kibel.} The refusal appears to reflect a kind of pride, or perhaps a principled self-sufficiency: his wife\u8217's cloak, however modest, was enough for him. He had said a {\i bracha} over it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gold discovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The story reaches its surprising conclusion at the top of daf 50a. Rabbi Yehuda lifted the mat he was sitting on and said to the messenger: {\i chazi mai ika} \u8212- look and see what is here. The {\i mefaresh} explains: beneath the mat, a miracle had occurred, and the entire space was filled with gold. {\i Na\u8217'asah li nes ve-nitmale kol oto makom zehuvim.} He was not, in other words, impoverished in any material sense. He simply chose not to avail himself of wealth. As the Gemara puts it: {\i lo nicha lei l\u8217'ithenuyei be-hadein alma} \u8212- it was not agreeable to him to derive {\i hana\u8217'ah} from this world. The refusal of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's shirt thus was not born of helplessness but of deliberate spiritual detachment from material comfort.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions to the famous story of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's marriage \u8212- a story that appears as well in Ketubot 62b, and it would be worth comparing the two versions for their differences. {\i Rabbi Akiva ikadsha lei barteh de-Kalba Savua} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva married the daughter of Kalba Savua. The name {\i Kalba Savua} means \u8220"the satisfied dog,\u8221" which is an unusual epithet. Some {\i mefarshim} suggest that people would come to him as hungry as a dog and leave fully satisfied \u8212- {\i savua} \u8212- because he was extraordinarily generous and wealthy. Whether this is the actual etymology or a folk explanation, it situates Kalba Savua as a man of great means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Kalba Savua learned of the marriage, he was furious. Rabbi Akiva at this point was poor, illiterate, and a simple shepherd \u8212- hardly what he envisioned for his daughter. He therefore made a {\i neder hana\u8217'ah} from all his property: Rabbi Akiva and perhaps his daughter as well would derive no {\i hana\u8217'ah} from his estate. The story thus arrives in Masechet Nedarim organically, though it is in substance an {\i agadata} about the {\i tanna\u8217'im}. The theme of {\i hana\u8217'ah} also resonates with the preceding episode: Rabbi Yehuda chose not to take {\i hana\u8217'ah} from this world; here, Kalba Savua weaponizes the {\i neder} to cut off {\i hana\u8217'ah} as a punitive measure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Straw house poverty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The young couple went to live together in a {\i bei tivna} \u8212- a straw house \u8212- sleeping on straw, {\i hava ganu be-itivna}. There, Rabbi Akiva would remove straw from her hair. There is a dispute among the {\i rishonim} here: the {\i mefaresh} understands that she would remove straw from his hair, but the Ran reads it as Rabbi Akiva removing the straw from his wife\u8217's hair \u8212- a small act of tenderness and care even within extreme deprivation, the two of them still tending to one another. Rabbi Akiva said to her: {\i ei hava lei ramina lach Yerushalayim de-dahava} \u8212- if I had the means, I would give you a {\i Yerushalayim shel zahav}, the golden Jerusalem circlet mentioned in the Mishnah in Shabbat \u8212- a tiara of gold, possibly engraved with the forms of Jerusalem\u8217's buildings or the Temple, a cherished piece of women\u8217's jewelry of that era. That this is the aspiration Rabbi Akiva articulates, even from within a straw house, speaks to his devotion to his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elijah straw story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then Elijah appears \u8212- as he must in any properly told {\i aggadic} tale \u8212- disguised as an ordinary person, knocking at the door and asking for a small amount of straw, because his wife had just given birth and they had nothing. Rabbi Akiva turns to his wife and says: {\i chazi gavra de-afilu tivna la it lei} \u8212- look, here is a person who does not even have straw. We, who have so little, still have something that another person lacks entirely. This moment of generosity and perspective from within utter poverty seems to be the catalyst. His wife immediately says to him: {\i zil havi be-rav} \u8212- go, study Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What connects the Elijah episode to her instruction to go learn? Perhaps she recognized in that instinctive act of generosity \u8212- in his immediate concern for someone even poorer than himself \u8212- a greatness of character that demanded cultivation through Torah. It is also worth noting that in other sources, most prominently in {\i Avot de-Rabbi Natan}, the account of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's awakening to Torah study includes the celebrated image of water wearing away a stone: Rabbi Akiva, at the age of forty and still illiterate, observed water dripping onto rock, and reasoned that if soft water could penetrate hard stone, Torah could surely penetrate his heart. Here in our Gemara, it is his wife\u8217's instruction that sends him on his way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twelve years learning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Azal tartesrei shenei kamei de-Rabbi Eliezer ve-Rabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- he went for twelve years to study before Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua. Rabbi Eliezer here is Rabbi Eliezer ben Hurcanus, and Rabbi Yehoshua is Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananiah \u8212- two of the greatest {\i tanna\u8217'im} of their generation, known for different intellectual strengths, and themselves frequently in dispute with one another. It is notable that Rabbi Akiva studied under both simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife permits departure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After twelve years, Rabbi Akiva returned home. As he approached, he overheard a conversation from behind the house: a {\i rasha} \u8212- a wicked man \u8212- was saying to his wife that her father had been right, that she had married someone unsuitable, that her husband had been gone for twelve years and left her as {\i armalut chayot} \u8212- a living widow. Her response was striking: she said she wished that he would go and study for another twelve years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva overheard this. {\i Ho\u8217'il ve-yahavat li reshu ahadar le-achorei} \u8212- since she has given me permission, I will turn back now and return to study. And he did. He did not stop to greet her. This troubles everyone who encounters the story. How could he not at least pause, spend even a short time at home, see how she was faring? The story is, in some sense, a parable \u8212- not necessarily a literal biographical record \u8212- but the message it may be conveying concerns the value of {\i siyum} without interruption, the irreplaceable quality of unbroken continuity in learning. Twenty-four consecutive years are not simply the arithmetic sum of two twelve-year periods with a break between them. What Rabbi Akiva represents, taken to an extreme, is the model of complete and undivided focus. The {\i rasha}\u8217's observation that she was a {\i armalut chayita} is not, the Gemara acknowledges implicitly, without some force; but the story insists that this was her choice, that she understood and permitted it. How she sustained herself materially during this period, the text does not address, and these are genuinely difficult questions to sit with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akiva returns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twenty-four thousand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadar azal hava tartesrei shenei achranita} \u8212- he went back for another twelve years. {\i Ata be-esrin ve-arba alfin zugei talmidei} \u8212- he returned with twenty-four thousand pairs of students. The number is not incidental: one thousand students for each of the twenty-four years he spent in study. Everyone came out to greet him, {\i nafku kulei alma le-apei}, and she too rose to go out to meet him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wicked man challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same {\i rasha} from before appeared and said to her: {\i ve-at le-heicha} \u8212- and where are you going, as if to say, you really think you are actually his wife? She responded: {\i yodea tzaddik nefesh behemto} \u8212- a righteous person knows the soul of his animal. The saying is cryptic. One reading: just as a {\i tzaddik} can understand even the inner life of his animal, how much more certainly does he know me, his wife. Another reading, more self-deprecating: I have been living like a {\i behema}, with almost nothing, but even so the {\i tzaddik} is aware of that. There is something unsettling about the comparison, and the text does not resolve it cleanly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife deserves credit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the crowd gathered around Rabbi Akiva and his wife was being pushed aside by the {\i rabbanan} pressing close, Rabbi Akiva said: {\i hanichu lah} \u8212- leave her be. {\i Sheli ve-shelachem shelah hu} \u8212- what is mine, and what is all of yours, belongs to her. Everything his students owed to him, he owed to her. The acknowledgment, rendered in a single sentence before the assembled thousands, is one of the more remarkable moments of public recognition in the Talmudic corpus. It comes at genuine cost to her \u8212- the sacrifice of a normal marriage, of family life, of presence \u8212- and Rabbi Akiva does not minimize that cost. The story of course raises larger questions about the tension between total commitment to {\i talmud Torah} and the obligations of married life, questions that have not become easier in subsequent generations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Six riches sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shama Kalba Savua} \u8212- Kalba Savua heard of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's prominence. Twenty-four years earlier he had made a {\i neder} to ensure his son-in-law derived nothing from his property. Now that son-in-law had become the Rabbi Akiva known throughout {\i Shas} and the {\i Mishnayot}. {\i Ata ve-isha\u8217'il al nidrei ve-ishtarei} \u8212- he came and asked to have his vow annulled, and it was annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important {\i halachic} difficulty. A {\i mishna} later in the tractate, at daf 64, rules {\i ein potchin be-nolad} \u8212- one cannot use a newly arisen circumstance as the {\i petach}, the opening, to undo a {\i neder}. If the rationale for annulling Kalba Savua\u8217's vow is that Rabbi Akiva turned out to be a great Torah scholar, that would seem to be precisely a {\i nolad} \u8212- a new fact that had not existed at the time of the vow. The resolution, apparently, is that the potential was always there. The greatness was {\i not} new; it was latent from the beginning. Kalba Savua could have known, and in some sense should have known, even at the time he made the vow, that this man had within him what was needed to become who he became. This is itself a striking claim: that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's genius and spiritual capacity were present even when he was an illiterate shepherd \u8212- that his wife saw what his father-in-law could not. The doubled expression {\i ishtarei ishtarei} may suggest something about the annulment\u8217's completeness or the emphasis with which it was granted; there is a suggestion from the Hagahos Ya\u8217'avetz that one instance of the word should perhaps be deleted, and the Ran and ArtScroll translations may reflect a reading where the annulment required more than a single process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 From Kalba Savua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Min shita milei i\u8217'atar Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- from six sources Rabbi Akiva became wealthy. The first is {\i min Kalba Savua}: once the {\i neder} was dissolved, his now proud father-in-law supported him generously. There is something almost ironic in this: Kalba Savua had always, it seems, wanted a great rabbinic son-in-law; he simply failed to see the potential standing before him. His daughter saw what he could not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shipwreck treasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second source: {\i min ila de-sfinta} \u8212- from the mast of a ship. The Gemara explains that every ship contains a hidden cavity in the mast where valuables are concealed. One time, Rabbi Akiva was walking along the beach \u8212- {\i inshuya al kef yama} \u8212- and he had previously paid four {\i zuz} to some sailors to bring him back whatever they might find. They found nothing except a {\i gavza}, a chest or coffer, washed up on the shore, which they brought to him without opening. It was found to be full of gold coins. The sailors are presented as notably honorable \u8212- they brought back exactly what they found, without helping themselves to the contents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether there is any obligation to return such found property. The question is in fact live. The doctrine of {\i ye\u8217'ush} \u8212- renunciation of ownership through despair of recovery \u8212- would arguably apply once a ship sinks to the sea floor; the original owner, having given up hope of recovery, loses legal title, and the finder acquires the object. What the modern law of salvage looks like, and how far back the practical insistence on retained ownership of sunken vessels extends, is a separate question: the history of maritime insurance, going back to the origins of Lloyd\u8217's of London, reflects a consistent expectation that ownership of lost cargo does not simply evaporate. Whether the full conditions for {\i ye\u8217'ush} are met in a given case, and what a {\i posek} would say about underwater salvage today, are questions that merit their own careful {\i teshuvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arabian traders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third source involves a ship belonging to traders identified as {\i Sarukita} people \u8212- understood here as Arabian merchants \u8212- whose ship was tossed about at sea, {\i tavat sfintayeh}. The entire commercial cargo was placed into a chest, and that same chest was later found and brought to Rabbi Akiva. The narratives about shipwrecks and sea-chests cluster together and likely reflect both independent traditions about Rabbi Akiva\u8217's wealth and the special association of maritime fortune with the blessing of Zevulun \u8212- {\i sefunei tmunei chol}, things hidden in the sand and sea that become a source of riches.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matronita\u8217's gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth source is {\i min Matronita} \u8212- from a wealthy Roman noblewoman. The Ran quotes the full story: Rabbi Akiva and his students found themselves in need of funds and went to borrow from this {\i matronita}. She agreed, but stipulated an unusual condition: God and the sea would serve as {\i arevim}, as guarantors. If Rabbi Akiva could not repay her, she would look to them. A time for repayment was fixed. When the date arrived, Rabbi Akiva was ill \u8212- the Rosh notes explicitly that he fell sick at precisely the time payment was due \u8212- and could not pay. The {\i matronita} went down to the beach and prayed: You know that Rabbi Akiva is sick and cannot repay. You are the guarantor; I am holding You to it. Immediately, the daughter of Caesar was struck with a sudden fit \u8212- {\i nishtatit bito shel Keisar} \u8212- and she seized a chest full of precious stones and gold coins and threw it into the sea. The waves carried it to the exact spot where the {\i matronita} was sitting. She took it and went home.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Later, when Rabbi Akiva recovered, he came to repay the loan in full. She told him: {\i hazarti etzel ha-arev} \u8212- I went to the guarantor, and he paid it all, and more. She returned to Rabbi Akiva the surplus beyond what was owed him, and from this he became wealthy. The narrative is remarkable not only as a story of miraculous divine payment but as a demonstration of Torah\u8217's claim that {\i tzaddikim} operate within a framework where God\u8217's involvement in human affairs is direct and responsive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Turnusrufus wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fifth source is {\i min ishto shel Turnusrufus} \u8212- from the wife of Turnusrufus, the notorious Roman general, one of the great {\i resha\u8217'im} of the Talmudic period, and \u8212- as is noted \u8212- the very man responsible for Rabbi Akiva\u8217's eventual martyrdom. The Ran quotes this story as well: Turnusrufus came home troubled, and his wife asked what was wrong. He said that Rabbi Akiva was constantly taunting him publicly. She said: the God of these people hates licentiousness \u8212- give me permission and I will cause him to stumble. She went to seduce Rabbi Akiva. When Rabbi Akiva first saw her, he did three things: he spat, he wept, and he laughed. She asked him to explain. He said he would explain two but not the third. He spat because she came from a {\i tipa sruha} \u8212- a putrid drop, as the {\i mishna} says of every human being. He wept because such beauty would in the end be consigned to the earth and come to nothing. The laugh he did not explain at the time \u8212- but the Ran notes that he had seen through {\i Ruach Ha-Kodesh} that she would eventually convert and become his wife. Rabbi Akiva resisted the seduction, and through some further sequence of events, derived wealth from the encounter. The end of this story \u8212- including her conversion, their marriage, and what that says about Rabbi Akiva\u8217's later life \u8212- would require tracing across multiple {\i sugyot} and raises genuinely difficult questions about how the various biographical traditions about Rabbi Akiva fit together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Monkey pearls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pearl-finding monkey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sixth source involves {\i Rav Gamda}, who similarly paid four {\i zuz} to sailors to bring him whatever they could find. They found nothing except a monkey, {\i kofa}, which they brought to him. The monkey ran off and began digging \u8212- {\i chafru batrei} \u8212- and was found to have been sitting atop a cache of pearls, {\i riva al marganita}. The honorable sailors brought all of the pearls back to him. Thus: a monkey led to discovery of precious gems, and this constitutes the sixth and final source of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's wealth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah vessel debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the Gemara introduces a new episode. The daughter of Caesar says to Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananiah: {\i Torah mefo\u8217'arah bi-chli mecho\u8217'ar} \u8212- such glorious Torah dwelling in such an ugly vessel. Rabbi Yehoshua was famously unbeautiful in appearance, and the Caesar\u8217's daughter poses the challenge with an almost philosophical directness: why should something so luminous be housed in such undistinguished physical form? That the daughter of a Roman emperor would regard the Torah as {\i mefo\u8217'arah} \u8212- magnificent \u8212- is itself noteworthy and the Gemara does not explain it further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine vessel analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua responded with a question: in your father\u8217's house, what do you store your wine in? She said: in earthenware jars, {\i manei de-pachra}. He said: everyone stores wine in earthenware \u8212- and even the imperial household does the same? Surely you should be storing it in vessels of silver and gold. She went and transferred the wine into silver and gold vessels \u8212- {\i ramat chamra be-manei de-chaspa ve-dahava} \u8212- and it went bad, {\i ve-sari}. Rabbi Yehoshua said: {\i af Oraita kein} \u8212- the Torah is just like this. Throughout {\i Shas}, Torah is compared to wine \u8212- something that begins well and deepens and improves with age. The {\i mishna} at the end of Kiddushin makes the parallel point that unlike physical crafts, which deteriorate as a person ages and loses strength and fine motor capacity, {\i talmud Torah} is a discipline in which one grows sharper and wiser with years of sustained effort.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She pressed further: but there are people who are both handsome and learned \u8212- {\i ve-ha\u8217'ika shapiran ve-gmiri}? He replied: if they were as ugly as I am, {\i havu gmiri tfei} \u8212- they would be even greater scholars.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel criticism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judgment questioned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A woman came before Rav Yehuda in Nehardea for a legal judgment, {\i de-atai le-kamei de-Rav Yehuda min Nehardea le-dina}, and was found liable \u8212- {\i ve-it-haivat min dina}. She then challenged Rav Yehuda by asking whether his teacher Shmuel would have ruled the same way. Shmuel was the {\i rosh yeshiva} of Nehardea, so a litigant in Nehardea invoking Shmuel\u8217's name carried a local resonance. Rav Yehuda had studied under both Rav and Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical criticism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Yehuda asked whether she had known Shmuel, she answered in the affirmative \u8212- and then described not his {\i Torah} or his method of judgment, but his physical appearance: short, obese, dark-complexioned, with prominent teeth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Excommunication death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said to her: {\i u-ve-vizui ka atit} \u8212- did you come here to disgrace him? The parallel elsewhere in {\i Shas} is instructive: when an Amora traveling from Bavel to Eretz Yisrael was asked about the great teacher of his yeshiva and referred to Rav by the name {\i Abba Aricha} rather than his honorific, he was rebuked for failing to appreciate and properly name his master. This woman had presumably seen Shmuel in the streets of Nehardea but had no perception of who he truly was \u8212- his depth of {\i Torah}, his authority as a judge and legal decisor. Her challenge to Rav Yehuda\u8217's ruling rested not on a substantive legal objection but on a dismissive, superficial impression of the teacher from whom that ruling ultimately derived. Rav Yehuda placed the woman in {\i nidui} \u8212- excommunication \u8212- and the Gemara reports starkly: {\i paka u-mita} \u8212- she exploded and died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Medicinal eggs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Turmaita definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to material from the Mishnah concerning the {\i beita turmaita}. The Mishnah had stated that even one who takes a vow not to eat {\i tavshil} \u8212- a cooked dish \u8212- may still eat {\i beita turmaita}. The Gemara now asks: {\i mai beita turmaita}? Shmuel explains that it is an egg prepared through an extraordinarily laborious process: it is placed alternately a thousand times in hot water and a thousand times in cold water \u8212- {\i alfa zimnei be-maya chamimei ve-alfa zimnei be-maya kerirei} \u8212- until it shrinks to very small size, {\i ad de-mizutra}, small enough to swallow whole. The reason it does not qualify as a {\i tavshil} may be that it is not something eaten with bread in the ordinary sense, or more precisely because it functions as a medicine rather than food. Its medicinal purpose is explicit: if one has intestinal trouble, the egg will absorb it on the way down and carry it out on the way up \u8212- or rather, on the way through. Because of the extraordinary difficulty of preparation, a skilled slave capable of making it was worth a thousand {\i dinarei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's medical use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that Shmuel himself would use the {\i kulcha} vegetable \u8212- a different plant \u8212- for the same diagnostic and therapeutic purposes, testing himself with it to assess his own medical condition. He pressed this practice to such lengths that the members of his household were tearing their hair out with worry over how much pain he was enduring in the process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fig jam dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jam comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then discusses a case from the Mishnah in Ma\u8217'asrot concerning {\i kluvsin} \u8212- a variety of figs from which a kind of {\i laftei}, a thick jam or spread, is made. One who vows not to eat {\i bana\u8217'ot sheva} (another variety) may not eat {\i kluvsin}, and vice versa, because these are distinct species.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's disinterest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A legal dispute is brought before Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi. A man had given his slave to another person on the representation that the slave could prepare a thousand kinds of fig jam. The slave produced only eight hundred kinds. The aggrieved party brought the matter to Rabbi for adjudication. Rabbi\u8217's reaction is memorable: {\i avoteinu amru nashinu tova, anu afilu be-eineinu lo ra\u8217'inu} \u8212- our ancestors said they had forgotten good things, and even by our own eyes we haven\u8217't seen them. The point being that Rabbi, a man of considerable wealth and standing, had absolutely no frame of reference for these eight hundred, let alone a thousand, varieties of fig jam. The entire dispute seemed to him bewildering in its premises. Why was anyone litigating over two hundred missing varieties of fig jam?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wedding feast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi avid lei hilula le-Rabbi Shimon be-Rabbi} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi made a wedding feast for his son Rabbi Shimon. The scale was prodigious: the {\i beit ganana}, the garden house or wedding canopy, alone cost 24,000 {\i ribvan dinarei} \u8212- an enormous figure. The celebration was lavish. And yet \u8212- {\i ve-lo azmanei le-Bar Kappara} \u8212- he did not invite Bar Kappara.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Kappara\u8217's rebuke\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara\u8217's response was pointed. He said: {\i im le-overei r\u8217'tzono kach, le-osei r\u8217'tzono al achat kama ve-kama} \u8212- if this is the kind of feast put on for those who merely transgress God\u8217's will, how much more so will it be for those who fulfill it. Then, after Rabbi invited him in response to this, Bar Kappara sharpened the point: {\i le-osei r\u8217'tzono ba-olam ha-zeh kach, le-olam ha-ba al achat kama ve-kama} \u8212- if those who do God\u8217's will in this world receive such celebration, how much more extraordinary will be the reward of {\i olam ha-ba}. The Gemara breaks off here, and we resume from this point in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 51\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi\u8217's Wedding & Bar Ka\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wedding Feast Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 50b concludes a narrative concerning a wedding feast that Rabbi \u8212- {\i Rabbeinu Ha-Kadosh}, Yehudah Ha-Nasi \u8212- made for his son, Rabbi Shimon Be-Rabbi. As the {\i meforesh} had already noted, Rabbi conspicuously did not invite Bar Kappara, one of the great {\i Tannaim} of the generation \u8212- a remarkable omission that the story itself helps explain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The text records that twenty-four million {\i dinarin} were spent on the wedding hall: {\i ktav a-beit ginana esrin ve-arba alfin ribo\u8217'in dinarin nafkan a-beit ginana dein} \u8212- an enormous sum, whether this was an inscription by Rabbi proclaiming his expenditure or graffiti of protest by Bar Kappara himself. Upon learning of the celebration from which he had been excluded, Bar Kappara remarked: {\i im le-ovrei retzono kach} \u8212- if even those who displease him merit such wealth and grandeur, then those who genuinely fulfill God\u8217's will should be rewarded all the more. The phrase {\i ovrei retzono}, those who transgress His will, is notable: Bar Kappara applies it almost ironically to himself, inverting the usual theological register in which it describes those who transgress God\u8217's commands, not merely those who have fallen out of favor with Rabbi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Eventually the two reconciled, and Bar Kappara was invited. At that point he shifted his language entirely: {\i osei retzono ba-olam ha-zeh kach, le-olam ha-ba al achat kama ve-khama} \u8212- those who truly do God\u8217's will in this world merit such blessing, and how much more so in the World to Come. The shift from {\i ovrei retzono} to {\i osei retzono} marks the resolution of the personal quarrel, though the underlying tension between the two figures becomes clearer as the story continues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suffering & Joking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains the deeper reason Rabbi had been reluctant to include Bar Kappara: {\i yoma de-michayach bei Rabbi ati pur\u8217'anuta le-alma} \u8212- any day that Rabbi would smile or take pleasure, suffering would enter the world. This startling statement is explained by the {\i Rishonim}, particularly with reference to a tradition cited in the {\i Ein Yaakov} (from {\i sof perek Yakin Yaakov}), that Rabbi had accepted upon himself personal physical suffering in order to shield others \u8212- apparently in the context of a prolonged drought \u8212- so that the broader world would be spared. On any day that Rabbi permitted himself to enjoy life, his self-imposed suffering was interrupted, and its protective effect correspondingly lapsed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concept is not without theological precedent in {\i Chazal}: the idea that great {\i tzadikim} absorb suffering on behalf of their generation appears in various places throughout {\i Shas}. It differs from the Christian theological framework of vicarious atonement in important structural ways \u8212- not least that in the rabbinic model it applies to specific {\i tzadikim} of the generation rather than to a single universal atoner \u8212- though the apparent resemblance is worth acknowledging as a point of theological interest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara, being something of a {\i badchan}, a comic and wit, was precisely the sort of person whose company Rabbi had reason to avoid. Knowing this, Rabbi made Bar Kappara an unusual offer: {\i la tivadchan vehavna lach arba\u8217'in grivey chittey} \u8212- if you do not make me laugh, I will pay you forty measures of wheat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wheat Joke Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara accepted this arrangement and then proceeded to collect. He took a very large basket, coated it with pitch, carried it upside down on his head, and came before Rabbi demanding the forty measures of wheat: {\i leiti li Mar arba\u8217'in grivey chittey dedarshina bach} \u8212- give me, Mar, the forty measures of wheat that I am owed on account of my dealings with you. Rabbi responded: {\i lav azhartach dela tivadchan} \u8212- did I not warn you not to make me laugh? Bar Kappara replied: {\i chittey dedarshina ka nasivna} \u8212- I am only here to take the wheat that was promised to me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exact nature of the humor here is somewhat opaque to modern sensibilities. The combination of the oversized basket, the pitch coating, and the upside-down carrying seems to have constituted a kind of visual practical joke \u8212- perhaps the point being that he had no intention of actually collecting the wheat and was doing the whole thing in a manner calculated to be absurd, while simultaneously protesting that he was acting in complete seriousness. The joke, like many pieces of ancient humor, may require having been there. In any event, Rabbi apparently found it amusing enough to react, which meant Bar Kappara had technically violated the terms of their agreement \u8212- a paradox Bar Kappara was exploiting with evident relish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayot Derashot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At some point proximate to the wedding \u8212- apparently while Ben Elasa, Rabbi\u8217's wealthy son-in-law, was also present \u8212- Bar Kappara turned to Rabbi\u8217's daughter and said: {\i lemachar shatina chamra berikuda deavuch uvekirkunei deimmach} \u8212- tomorrow I will drink wine while your father dances and your mother sings or cackles. This passage has been cited in discussions of {\i kol isha}, the prohibition on listening to a woman\u8217's voice in song: the {\i Ya\u8217'avetz} lists it among passages in {\i Shas} where {\i Tannaim} and {\i Amoraim} appear to conduct themselves in ways that stand in tension with norms accepted as normative by later authorities. The {\i Meforesh} attempts to read the passage as referring to pouring wine rather than singing, but the {\i Ran} understands it as singing \u8212- and the plain meaning of the language appears to support the {\i Ran}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara then proceeded to put his plan into effect. He approached Rabbi with a series of {\i drashot} on the terminology of {\i arayot} drawn from Vayikra chapters 18 and 20. The appropriateness of such a topic at a wedding has been questioned, though one possible context is the reading of the {\i parasha} of {\i arayot} on {\i Yom Kippur} afternoon \u8212- an occasion described in the last {\i Mishna} of Masechet Taanit as one of the two happiest days of the year, when young people would go out and engage in flirtatious behavior. Just as {\i Yom Kippur}, paradoxically a day of great joy, required awareness of {\i arayot} precisely because of the festive atmosphere, so too at a wedding the topic may have been deemed relevant as a kind of cautionary counterpoint to the celebratory mood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara asked Rabbi: {\i mai To\u8217'evah} \u8212- what does the word {\i to\u8217'evah} mean, as it appears in the context of {\i arayot}? Every explanation Rabbi offered, Bar Kappara shot down: {\i kol de-amar lei Rebbe de-hachin hu to\u8217'evah parcha Bar Kappara} \u8212- every interpretation Rabbi proposed, Bar Kappara rejected. Finally, Rabbi asked Bar Kappara to explain it himself. Bar Kappara then requested that Rabbi\u8217's wife bring him a cup of wine, and told Rabbi himself: {\i kum rakod li} \u8212- get up and dance for me. This was the fulfillment of the scenario he had pre-announced to Rabbi\u8217's daughter: the father dancing, the mother serving. Having established the stage, he delivered his {\i derasha}: {\i de-hachin amar Rachmana to\u8217'evah \u8212- to\u8217'eh ata bah} \u8212- the word {\i to\u8217'evah} means \u8220"you are making an error with this,\u8221" a {\i derashah} on the letters of the word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After a second cup of wine, Bar Kappara asked: {\i mai tevel} \u8212- what is the word {\i tevel}, used in the context of bestiality? He again deflected every answer Rabbi offered and insisted Rabbi do as he had done before \u8212- dance, and have the wine poured. His explanation: {\i tevel hu \u8212- tavlin yesh bah} \u8212- the word {\i tevel} asks rhetorically whether there are spices in this, whether this act is in some way more enjoyable than any other. The {\i derasha} frames the Torah\u8217's prohibition as a kind of ironic question directed at the would-be transgressor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third cup followed, and Bar Kappara asked: {\i u-mai zimah} \u8212- what is the word {\i zimah}, associated in the {\i pesukim} of Vayikra with promiscuity and harlotry, the verse {\i ve-lo tizneh ha-aretz u-malah ha-aretz zimah}. Again the same sequence played out, and Bar Kappara\u8217's answer was: {\i zo mah hi} \u8212- this one, who is she? The implication is that {\i zimah} evokes the consequence of widespread promiscuity \u8212- children of unknown parentage \u8212- a concern that forms the subject matter of the fourth {\i perek} of Kiddushin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Elasa Leaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notably, the Gemara does not say that Rabbi himself grew frustrated or offended. Some observe that Rabbi possessed such a deep love of Torah that he was genuinely engaged by the {\i derashot}, however oddly timed. It was his son-in-law Ben Elasa who could not tolerate the spectacle: {\i lo yachil ben alasa lemisbal} \u8212- Ben Elasa could not bear it, and so {\i kam ve-nafak hu ve-ittateih metaman} \u8212- he arose and left together with his wife, Rabbi\u8217's daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ben Elasa\u8217's Haircut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Elasa Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pauses to ask: who was this Ben Elasa, and what was he known for? The answer comes from a {\i baraita}: {\i lo le-chinam pizer ben alasa et ma\u8217'otav} \u8212- it was not for nothing that Ben Elasa spent his money lavishly. Rather: {\i ela lehar\u8217'ot bahen tisporet shel kohen gadol} \u8212- he spent his wealth in order to demonstrate to people what the haircut of the {\i kohen gadol} looked like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some background: there are three categories of figures obligated to maintain distinctive hairstyles. The king must have his hair cut every day, befitting his need to appear dignified and presentable \u8212- a rule connected to the verse {\i yofyecha techzenah einecha} and to the ruling in Yoma that a king may even wash his face on {\i Yom Kippur}. The {\i kohen gadol} must have his hair cut once a week, in a specific prescribed style. Regular {\i kohanim} must have their hair cut once a month.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Elasa, as the son-in-law of Rabbi \u8212- who flourished around the year 180 CE, well after the destruction of the {\i beit hamikdash} \u8212- was living in a world where the {\i kohen gadol\u8217's} haircut had not been seen in living memory. His motivation appears to have been a form of preservation and anticipation: by replicating that haircut at great personal expense, he was showing people what it had looked like, and implicitly affirming his expectation that the Temple service would one day be restored. Whether it is halachically proper to replicate the {\i keilim} or practices of the {\i beit hamikdash} under current conditions is itself a disputed question \u8212- some hold it constitutes an {\i aveira} \u8212- but Ben Elasa\u8217's gesture is at minimum a striking expression of {\i messianic} longing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} cites the verse: {\i kasom yichsemu et rosheihem} \u8212- they shall surely trim their heads \u8212- which describes the {\i kohanim\u8217's} distinctive manner of cutting their hair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lulianit Haircut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} teaches: {\i ke-ein lulinit} \u8212- it resembles the {\i lulinit} style. But what exactly is the {\i lulinit} haircut? {\i Mai lulinit?} Rav Yehuda \u8212- one of the {\i Amoraim} \u8212- did not understand it and asked for a description. Rava explains: {\i rosho shel zeh betzad ikaro shel zeh} \u8212- the tip of one hair lies alongside the root of the next. This appears to describe an extremely close, even cut \u8212- somewhat like a very precise buzz cut \u8212- in which each hair\u8217's tip meets the base of the one beside it, reminiscent of the way the {\i lulav} or {\i hadas} is ideally described as growing. Achieving such precision without modern instruments would have been extraordinarily difficult and consequently very expensive \u8212- which explains why Ben Elasa needed to spend so much money simply to replicate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rif} offers a different interpretation: something more akin to cornrows, where the hair lies in close parallel rows. The name {\i lulinit} may derive from the Latin word for wool, suggesting a texture or layered appearance \u8212- though this remains speculative. Whatever its precise appearance, it was clearly a haircut of elaborate and costly workmanship, and Ben Elasa\u8217's willingness to fund it for himself speaks to the depth of his commitment to honoring the memory of the Temple service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tavshil & Dilat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tavshil Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} had previously stated that among the things exempt from a {\i neder} against {\i tavshil} \u8212- a cooked dish \u8212- are the {\i beitza} (the very hard-boiled egg whose medicinal character was discussed on the previous daf) and the {\i dilat haramuza}, a type of gourd or squash. As a point of context, the {\i Geonim} issued a {\i cherem} prohibiting the application of medical remedies mentioned in the Gemara, on the grounds that what worked in that era may not work in ours \u8212- so the medicinal dimension of the {\i beitza} is of historical rather than practical interest. The question now before the Gemara is what exactly the {\i dilat haramuza} is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel vs.\u160 ?Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai dilat haramuza?} \u8212- what is this {\i ramuza} gourd? Shmuel says: {\i kara karkuzai} \u8212- it is the {\i karkuzai} type of squash, i.e., it is a particular species of gourd identified by that name. Rav Ashi disagrees: {\i dilat hatmuna beremetz} \u8212- it is a gourd buried and pit-roasted in hot coals, and the word {\i ramuza} refers not to a species but to a method of preparation. On Rav Ashi\u8217's reading, the {\i dilat haramuza} is distinguished from ordinary {\i tavshil} by its unusual preparation method \u8212- it is cooked in embers rather than in a pot \u8212- and that is why it is treated as an exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina Refutes Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina challenges Rav Ashi\u8217's position: {\i itvei Ravina le-Rav Ashi}. He cites a passage from Masechet Kilayim in which Rabbi Nechemia rules that the Aramean pumpkin ({\i dilat arammit}), which is identical to the Egyptian pumpkin ({\i dilat hamitzrit}), constitutes {\i kilayim} if cross-bred with the Greek pumpkin ({\i ha-yevanit}) \u8212- and similarly constitutes {\i kilayim} with the {\i ramuza} pumpkin. The logic of {\i kilayim} requires that the two varieties be sufficiently similar to count as the same species for cross-breeding purposes, yet distinct enough that mixing them is prohibited. The first {\i perek} of Masechet Kilayim catalogs precisely these relationships. The critical point is that {\i kilayim} terminology only makes sense if {\i ramuza} is a species name \u8212- you cannot have {\i kilayim} between a method of preparation and a type of squash. This proves that {\i dilat haramuza} refers to a distinct variety of gourd, not to a preparation method, and Rav Ashi\u8217's position is therefore refuted: {\i tiyuvta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By implication, Shmuel\u8217's position \u8212- that {\i dilat haramuza} is simply the {\i karkuzai} squash, another species \u8212- is confirmed. In the late {\i Amoraic} period, disputes between Ravina and Rav Ashi are common; here, unusually, it is Rav Ashi who loses, because Ravina brought a text from Kilayim that Rav Ashi had apparently not accounted for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Methods of Preparation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tavshil Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} being quoted here was actually stated earlier \u8212- it is not a fresh {\i Mishna} introduced at this point, which is itself somewhat unusual, as the Gemara normally would mark such a repetition with {\i gufa} or a similar indicator. The clause under discussion is: {\i ha-noder mimaseh kedeirah} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against {\i maseh kedeirah}, a dish prepared in a pot. The ruling is: {\i ein assur ela mimaseh retachto} \u8212- he is only forbidden to eat something that is the characteristic boiled-up, pot-liquidy result of cooking in a pot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if a person says {\i amar konam ha-yored likdeirah} \u8212- using the formulation \u8220"whatever descends into a pot\u8221" \u8212- then {\i assur be-khol ha-na\u8217'aseh ba-kedeirah}, everything prepared in a pot is forbidden. This distinction is fundamental to the entire {\i perek}: {\i maseh kedeirah} is the name of a dish, while {\i ha-yored la-kedeirah} describes a method of preparation. A {\i neder} against a dish type applies only to that dish in its characteristic form; a {\i neder} against a method of preparation applies to anything processed by that method.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pot vs.\u160 ?Frying Pan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} elaborates: if a person took a {\i neder} against {\i ha-yored la-kedeirah} \u8212- whatever goes into the pot \u8212- he is also forbidden to eat {\i yored la-ilpas}, whatever goes into a frying pan. Why? {\i Shekevar yarad la-kedeirah kodem sheyarad la-ilpas} \u8212- because the pot precedes the frying pan: the food is first parboiled in the pot, and only then finished in the {\i ilpas} to crisp the exterior. Since the food has already entered the pot in the course of its preparation, a {\i neder} against the pot catches it at an earlier stage of the process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse does not hold: if a person takes a {\i neder} against {\i ha-yored la-ilpas} \u8212- whatever goes into the frying pan \u8212- he is permitted to eat whatever was only boiled in a pot, because that preparation never passed through the frying pan. Similarly, {\i min ha-na\u8217'aseh ba-kedeirah} \u8212- a {\i neder} against whatever is {\i prepared} in a pot \u8212- permits eating {\i na\u8217'aseh ba-ilpas}, whatever is prepared in a frying pan, since the frying pan represents a later, distinct stage. And {\i min ha-na\u8217'aseh ba-ilpas} permits {\i na\u8217'aseh ba-kedeirah} for the same reason \u8212- the two methods, once separated, do not implicate each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The governing principle throughout this entire discussion is {\i lashon bnei adam} \u8212- we follow ordinary colloquial usage. This {\i perek} is built on the premise that {\i nedarim} are interpreted according to how people actually speak, not according to technical or textual precision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oven & Bread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} continues: {\i ha-noder min ha-yored la-tanur} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against whatever goes into an oven \u8212- {\i ein assur ela be-pat}, is forbidden only regarding bread. The {\i tanur} of Talmudic times was a conical or cylindrical clay oven, similar to a tandoor, into which flatbread was stuck against the inner walls to bake. While meat could also be roasted in such an oven \u8212- as we know from discussions in Pesachim and from the laws of {\i Shabbat} \u8212- the essential purpose and primary use of the oven was bread-baking. Roasting meat in the same oven was, in effect, a secondary use of the heat already generated for bread.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accordingly, when someone says {\i yored la-tanur}, what goes into the oven, the natural referent in {\i lashon bnei adam} is bread, and only bread is forbidden. But if a person says {\i kol maseh tanur alai} \u8212- everything prepared in an oven is forbidden to me \u8212- then {\i assur be-khol ha-na\u8217'asim ba-tanur}, he is forbidden everything prepared there, including anything else one might bake or roast in that oven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pickled & Prepared Foods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kavush & Shaluk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} on 51b opens with a series of cases about particular preparation methods. First: {\i min ha-kavush} \u8212- if a person takes a {\i neder} against {\i ha-kavush}, the pickled thing, using the definite article \u8212- {\i ein assur ela min ha-kavush shel yerak} \u8212- he is forbidden only from pickled vegetables. The {\i Ran} notes the significance of the {\i hei ha-yedi\u8217'ah}: because he said \u8220"the pickled,\u8221" he is referring to the thing that people characteristically pickle, which is vegetables. Had he said simply {\i kavush} without the article, the {\i neder} would be broader in scope. This points toward the principle of {\i stam nedarim le-hachmir, peirushan le-hakel}: where the language is ambiguous, we would need to ask what the person actually meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe that what counts as \u8220"the\u8221" pickled thing varies considerably by community \u8212- cucumbers in one setting, radishes, kimchi, or salted turnips in another. Corned beef, for instance, is itself a form of pickled meat. In communities where a wide range of foods are customarily pickled, the referent of {\i ha-kavush} would need to be determined by local usage. But if a person explicitly says {\i kavush she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- I will not taste any pickled thing \u8212- {\i assur be-khol ha-kevushim}, every form of pickle is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: {\i min ha-shaluk} \u8212- from {\i shaluk}, a preparation method for meat, perhaps best described as braising or prolonged stewing. Using the definite article again, the {\i neder} is {\i eino assur ela min ha-shaluk shel basar} \u8212- it applies only to red meat prepared in this manner. But if the person says {\i shaluk she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- braised I will not taste \u8212- then it applies to all braised foods, whether meat, poultry, fish, or vegetables.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Akha\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Akha son of Rav Avya raised a challenge to Rav Ashi: {\i amar dikhvish mai, deshalik mai, detzali mai, demaliakh mai} \u8212- what if someone used not the form {\i ha-kavush} or {\i kavush}, nor the possessive {\i neder} formulation, but rather the participial form {\i dikhvish} (that which has been pickled), {\i deshalik} (that which has been braised), {\i detzali} (that which has been roasted), or {\i demaliakh} (that which has been salted)? When someone uses this formulation \u8212- \u8220"the thing that was prepared in this manner\u8221" \u8212- does that mean specifically the item characteristically prepared that way, or does it mean anything prepared in that manner at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds: {\i tiba\u8217'ei} \u8212- the question stands unresolved. In the language of {\i nedarim}, this is effectively a {\i teiku} \u8212- a question left without a definitive ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzali & Roasted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} next addresses {\i tzali}: {\i min ha-tzali, eino assur ela min ha-tzali shel basar} \u8212- from the roasted, a person is forbidden only from roasted red meat. This ruling is attributed specifically to Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- {\i divrei Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- which is somewhat puzzling, since the parallel clauses about {\i kavush} and {\i shaluk} are stated anonymously. The attribution to Rabbi Yehuda suggests there may be a {\i machloket} about this somewhere else in the {\i sugyot}, though no explicit dispute is evident in this immediate context. As before, if the person says {\i tzali she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- roasted I will not taste \u8212- then {\i assur be-khol ha-tzluyim}, it applies to anything roasted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fish Nidrei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maliakh Fish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} continues: {\i min ha-maliakh} \u8212- from the salted thing. As is well known from other contexts throughout {\i Shas}, whenever {\i maliakh} is used without further qualification, it refers specifically to salted fish \u8212- the dried, salt-cured variety, something resembling salt cod or another hard-dried salted fish rather than a brined pickle. The distinction between {\i maliakh} and {\i kavush} is that {\i kavush} involves immersion in a brine solution, while {\i maliakh} involves dry-salting, producing a much harder and more concentrated product that requires soaking in changed water before eating. This is also why consuming very salty fish at certain times of year was considered a health concern. Accordingly: {\i eino assur ela min ha-maliakh shel dag} \u8212- the {\i neder} applies only to salt-cured fish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if a person says {\i maliakh she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- salted I will not taste \u8212- then {\i assur be-khol ha-melukhim}, it applies to anything salted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} then addresses a {\i neder} against fish more broadly: if a person says {\i dag she-eini to\u8217'em} or {\i dagim she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- fish, I will not taste \u8212- {\i assur bahen bein gedolim bein katanim bein melukhim bein tfelim bein chayim bein mevushalim} \u8212- it is forbidden in every form: large or small, salted or fresh, raw or cooked. The inclusion of raw fish is of some halakhic interest: the question of whether {\i bishul akum} \u8212- the rabbinic prohibition on food cooked by non-Jews \u8212- applies to sushi has been debated by contemporary {\i poskim}, since the {\i gezeirah} was not applied to foods normally eaten raw, and raw fish has in recent years become a normative food category. Some authorities, including material published by the Star-K, have addressed this question, though a range of opinions exists. Rice, which commonly accompanies sushi, has its own complicated status under {\i bishul akum}, further complicating the analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast: {\i umutar bit-rayta trufa u-vetzir} \u8212- one is permitted to eat the minced or chopped fish dish along with its brine. The precise character of this dish is not entirely clear, but it appears to resemble something like chopped herring \u8212- a minced preparation with accompanying liquid \u8212- distinct enough from ordinary {\i dag} to fall outside the {\i neder\u8217's} scope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dag vs.\u160 ?Dagah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} introduces the position of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar, which complicates the {\i Mishna\u8217's} apparent equivalence between {\i dag} and {\i dagim}. Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar rules: if a person says {\i dag she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- using the masculine singular \u8212- {\i assur ba-gedolim umuttar ba-katanim}, he is forbidden to eat large fish but permitted to eat small fish. Conversely, {\i daga she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- using the feminine or diminutive form \u8212- {\i assur ba-katanim umuttar ba-gedolim}, it is the small fish that are forbidden and the large fish that are permitted. And {\i dag daga she-eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- using both forms together \u8212- {\i assur bein ba-gedolim bein ba-katanim}, since the {\i neder} encompasses both forms, it applies to all fish regardless of size.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} thus creates a grammatical distinction between {\i dag} and {\i dagah} based on the size of the fish in question: {\i dag} refers to large fish, {\i dagah} to small.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jonah Fish Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa poses a challenge to Abaye: {\i mima\u8217'i de-dag she-eini to\u8217'em gadol hu} \u8212- what is the basis for saying that {\i dag} in the masculine form refers specifically to large fish? He points to the verse in Sefer Yonah: {\i vayeman Hashem dag gadol livloa et Yonah} \u8212- God prepared a large fish to swallow Yonah, which establishes {\i dag} as large. But then: {\i vayitpallel Yonah el Hashem Elohav mimi\u8217'ei ha-dagah} \u8212- Yonah prayed from within the innards of the fish, and there the word {\i dagah} is used. How can {\i dag} mean large and {\i dagah} mean small if the same fish is described by both words?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers that this apparent contradiction reflects a change in the fish itself: Yonah was initially swallowed by a large fish and remained there for three days without praying. When the fish then became more constricted \u8212- whether because God transferred him to a smaller fish or because the fish was otherwise diminished \u8212- Yonah found himself in a situation of {\i eit tzara}, genuine distress, and only then prayed. The {\i Maharsha} explains the transition differently: God transferred Yonah from a male fish ({\i dag}) to a female fish ({\i dagah}) that was filled with eggs, making it more crowded and prompting the prayer. Either reading explains why Yonah\u8217's prayer was elicited not by the initial swallowing but by a subsequent change in his situation \u8212- a point considered thematically relevant when the book of Yonah is read on {\i Yom Kippur} at {\i mincha}, evoking the image of approaching God from a place of constriction and need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dagah Inclusive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa presses further: {\i dilma paltei dag gadol uvalei dag katan} \u8212- perhaps there is no size change at all; rather the large fish simply expelled Yonah underwater, and immediately a smaller fish swallowed him. In this reading there are two distinct fish in the narrative, and the change in terminology reflects a change in the fish rather than a change in its size. This would undermine the proof that {\i dag} means large and {\i dagah} means small, since both words in the Yonah narrative would simply describe whichever fish happened to be present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a different challenge to the {\i dag/dagah} distinction: {\i ella ve-ha-dagah asher ba-ye\u8217'or meta} \u8212- the verse describing the plague of blood says that the fish in the Nile died, using the word {\i dagah}. If {\i dagah} means only small fish, were only the small fish of the Nile killed while the large fish survived? Clearly that is not what the {\i pasuk} intends. The conclusion therefore is: {\i dagah mashma gedolim u-mashma katanim} \u8212- {\i dagah} is inclusive of all fish, both large and small.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This does not necessarily mean that {\i dag} cannot refer specifically to large fish \u8212- the masculine singular could still carry that meaning. But for practical purposes, the discussion is ultimately resolved on a different basis: {\i u-venedarim halach achar lashon bnei adam} \u8212- in matters of {\i nedarim}, we follow colloquial usage, and this holds {\i le-kulei alma}, by all opinions. Whatever the precise biblical semantics of {\i dag} versus {\i dagah}, what governs is how ordinary people use these words in their everyday speech. The {\i pesukim} from Tanach thus serve as evidence only insofar as they shed light on colloquial usage, and when they fail to do so consistently, we simply set them aside in favor of {\i lashon bnei adam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzichna & Conclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzichna Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} states: {\i ha-noder min ha-tzachana} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against {\i tzachana} (a form of prepared or pickled fish) \u8212- {\i assur be-tirya trufa}, is also forbidden the chopped minced fish preparation, {\i umuttar be-tzir u-ve-muryas}, but is permitted the fish brine and the {\i muryas} (a word of Latin origin meaning fish brine) which is the separately prepared pickling liquid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this, Ravina raises a question to Rav Ashi: {\i amar harei alai tzichin mai} \u8212- if a person declared forbidden upon himself {\i tzichin}, what is the law? Is {\i tzichin} simply the singular form of {\i tzachana}, such that one who is forbidden {\i tzachana} would obviously also be forbidden {\i tzichin}? Or are they distinct enough that a separate {\i neder} formulation would yield a different result? The Gemara offers no resolution: {\i tiba\u8217'ei} \u8212- the question is left open, a {\i teiku} on which we close the {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 52\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dairy Products\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milk & Whey Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the bottom of 51b opens with the case of one who takes a {\i neder} against {\i chalav} \u8212- milk. The ruling is {\i muttar b\u8217'kum}: the {\i kum}, the whey produced during the cheese- or yogurt-making process, is not included in the {\i neder} and remains permitted. Whey is the liquid that separates out when milk curdles, distinct from the protein solids that become cheese. {\i Rabbi Yose oser} \u8212- Rabbi Yose forbids the whey as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The way the Bavli understands this {\i machloket} is not as a technical dispute over how to classify whey in relation to milk, but rather as a question of {\i lashon bnei adam} \u8212- the vernacular usage of ordinary people \u8212- which is the animating principle throughout {\i Nedarim}. What do people mean when they say \u8220"milk\u8221"? The Yerushalmi, by contrast, appears to treat this as a genuine {\i machloket} between the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Yose over the ontological nature of whey \u8212- whether it truly qualifies as milk. Worth noting as well: when a person says he is refraining from milk, he does not mean he is refraining from {\i milchik} broadly. The {\i neder} tracks the word, not the category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashon Bnei Adam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entire framework of {\i nedarim} follows {\i lashon bnei adam} \u8212- what the speaker\u8217's words mean in ordinary usage. Points that might otherwise appear to be technical classification disputes turn out, at least in the Bavli\u8217's reading, to be more superficial questions about how people actually use certain words in their locale. The {\i mishna} thus encodes a sensitivity to lived linguistic practice rather than formal taxonomic reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an interesting tension worth pausing on. The {\i gemara} in {\i Shabbos} makes clear that the cheese-making process involves {\i mechabetz} \u8212- the curdling that separates solids from liquid \u8212- which is a {\i toladah} of {\i borer}, a derivative of the {\i melacha} of separating a mixture. That framing suggests that {\i halacha} conceives of milk as already a mixture of cheese solids and whey together. Yet our {\i mishna} treats whey as a new substance, distinct from the milk out of which it came. Reconciling these two positions is a genuine question that deserves further attention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocal Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues with the reciprocal cases. If a person takes a {\i neder} against {\i kum} \u8212- whey \u8212- he is {\i muttar b\u8217'chalav}: even Rabbi Yose would agree that a {\i neder} against whey does not include milk. This is also noteworthy because it implies that the curdling process produces two genuinely new substances. Otherwise one might argue that since milk is simply a mixture of curds and whey, drinking milk always involves consuming whey, and a {\i neder} against whey would thus apply to milk as well. The {\i mishna}\u8217's permissive ruling reflects the view that whey, once separated, is a new and distinct entity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul & Cheese\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Shaul adds: {\i ha-noder min ha-gevina asur bah bein melucha u\u8217'tefeila} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against cheese is forbidden from eating it whether it is salted or unsalted. The novelty of the ruling is that one might have thought the term {\i gevina} in ordinary usage referred specifically to salted cheese, the more common form, and that unsalted cheese might not be included. Abba Shaul teaches that even unsalted cheese is called {\i gevina} \u8212- there is no other adequate word for it \u8212- and therefore both fall within the scope of the {\i neder}. This parallels other cases throughout the {\i mishna} where different preparation methods raise questions about which items are included in a given {\i neder} term.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meat & Flavor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meat/Gravy Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i ha-noder min ha-basar muttar b\u8217'rotev} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against meat is permitted to eat the gravy \u8212- {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'kippa}, and also the cracklings or drippings. The {\i rotev} is a gravy or sauce that carries the flavor of the meat and may contain small fragments of it, but is not itself a piece of meat. The {\i kippah}, according to the {\i mefaresh}, refers to a thick residue \u8212- drippings or compressed matter that accumulates during meat preparation, something like what falls to the bottom of the pan. Since the person\u8217's {\i neder} was against meat \u8212- a steak, a chicken leg, a discrete piece \u8212- the gravy and drippings are not included.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point opens onto a major discussion in the {\i Ran} \u8212- which we will return to shortly \u8212- concerning {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}, {\i bittul}, {\i min b\u8217'mino}, and {\i min b\u8217'she\u8217'eino mino}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yose oser} \u8212- Rabbi Yose forbids the gravy and cracklings as well. His reasoning is that the gravy contains pieces of meat and carries the {\i ta\u8217'am} \u8212- the flavor \u8212- of the meat, and therefore it falls within the scope of the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda adds a proof narrative: {\i ma\u8217'aseh v\u8217'asar aleinu Rabbi Tarfon b\u8217'veitzim she-nitbashlu imo} \u8212- there was an incident in which Rabbi Tarfon forbade eggs that had been cooked together with the meat to those who had taken a {\i neder} against meat. The scenario resembles a Sephardi-style stew where eggs are cooked directly in the meat pot and absorb its flavor. Rabbi Tarfon was {\i machmir} that even an egg cooked in a meat stew is considered meat for the purpose of {\i nedarim}. This serves as a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} proof for Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position that the gravy and {\i kippah} should similarly be forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The questioners responded: {\i amru lo kein hadavar \u8212- aval eimatai? Bizman she-yomar basar zeh alay} \u8212- granted, but under what circumstances does this apply? Only when the person specified {\i this} meat \u8212- {\i basar zeh}. The principle is: {\i she-ha-noder min ha-davar v\u8217'nitarev b\u8217'acher im yesh bo b\u8217'noten ta\u8217'am asur} \u8212- if one took a {\i neder} against something and it then became mixed with something else, it is forbidden only if there is enough of it to impart a {\i ta\u8217'am}, a flavor. The principle of {\i ta\u8217'am k\u8217'ikar} \u8212- that flavor carries the legal status of the substance itself \u8212- is a foundational concept in {\i kashrut} and {\i issur v\u8217'heter} broadly, and this passage is among its significant sources.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Noten Ta\u8217'am Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The later {\i tannaim} thus clarify Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's position: {\i noten ta\u8217'am} applies only when the {\i neder} was articulated with {\i zeh} \u8212- \u8220"this\u8221" \u8212- as in {\i basar zeh}, this specific piece of meat. If the person simply said {\i basar} \u8212- meat in general \u8212- then even if a dish carries the full flavor of meat, it is not included. The logic is that the {\i neder} against a specific item causes that item\u8217's status to travel into whatever it subsequently flavors. A {\i neder} against meat in general, by contrast, is about the substance \u8220"meat\u8221" as a category and does not extend to {\i noten ta\u8217'am}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same framework applies to the question of whether funds from items made {\i asur} by a {\i neder} are themselves {\i asur}. If a person declared his entire purse {\i asur} to someone, would every subsequent coin spent in the marketplace carry that {\i issur}? The {\i gemara} implies that for consumable foods, {\i bittul} may apply \u8212- the prohibited item can be nullified in a mixture \u8212- a point that will be developed through the {\i machloket} between Rashi and the {\i Ran}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine & Flavor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Dishes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha-noder min ha-yayin} \u8212- if one takes a {\i neder} against wine, {\i muttar b\u8217'tavshil she-yesh bo ta\u8217'am yayin} \u8212- he is permitted to eat a cooked dish prepared with wine, even one where the wine is a significant ingredient and its flavor is clearly detectable. Wine in a beef stew or a wine-braised dish is not \u8220"drinking wine\u8221" \u8212- it is eating a stew. The person who vowed against wine meant the wine itself, not every dish in which wine played a role. This stands in instructive contrast to the meat case: meat\u8217's {\i noten ta\u8217'am} is invoked only when the person said {\i basar zeh}, whereas with wine, ordinary {\i lashon} is already sufficient to exclude wine-flavored dishes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting as well the distinction between a {\i neder} against wine and a {\i neder neziroot}. A {\i nazir} acquires a comprehensive personal status \u8212- the prohibitions of {\i tameh meis}, grape products, and haircut are all bound together as dimensions of a single identity. Saying \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} from wine\u8221" would actually create a full {\i nezirut}. But a {\i neder} against wine is simply a {\i neder} against wine, with no further extensions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 This Wine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar konam yayin zeh she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- if one said, \u8220"this wine I will not taste,\u8221" and it subsequently fell into a dish, then {\i im yesh bo ta\u8217'am yayin harei zeh assur} \u8212- if it imparts a flavor to the dish, the dish is forbidden. The same {\i noten ta\u8217'am} principle that operates with {\i basar zeh} now operates with {\i yayin zeh}. Specifying {\i zeh} \u8212- this particular wine \u8212- causes that wine\u8217's prohibition to travel wherever its flavor travels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises regarding the precise role of the phrase {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- \u8220"I will not taste\u8221" \u8212- as distinct from {\i zeh}. Why does the {\i mishna} formulate the wine case as {\i yayin zeh she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} while the meat case uses {\i basar zeh alai}? One might distinguish between {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} and {\i issurei achilah}, but this thread will be resolved more precisely through the {\i sugya} of Rami bar Chama, to be addressed below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Yeinam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Stam yeinam} refers to wine produced or handled by non-Jews, which is not necessarily wine used for {\i avodah zarah} but which carries a rabbinic prohibition extending from the concern over {\i yayin nesech} \u8212- libation wine. This prohibition may also be related to broader concerns about social mingling and the avoidance of close relationships that could lead to intermarriage, placing {\i stam yeinam} in the same family of rabbinic enactments as {\i bishul akum} and {\i pas akum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For purposes of {\i bittul}, {\i stam yeinam} follows a lower threshold than standard prohibited substances: it is {\i batel b\u8217'shisha} \u8212- nullified one-to-six \u8212- rather than the standard {\i batel b\u8217'shishim}. At one-in-six, the wine would certainly still be {\i noten ta\u8217'am}, so the ratio itself is set at a level that includes the {\i ta\u8217'am} threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine Days Custom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related practical question concerns the {\i minhag} of {\i Ashkenazim} not to eat meat during the nine days before {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av}. What exactly does that {\i minhag} encompass? The {\i gemara} speaks of reducing {\i simcha} during {\i shavua she-chal bo} \u8212- the week in which {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} falls \u8212- but the {\i Ashkenazi} community has extended and formalized the practice. This raises several questions: Does the {\i minhag} apply to chicken as well as beef? The {\i Magen Avraham} in {\i Shulchan Aruch} takes a stringent position that extends the restriction broadly. What about a dish that is technically {\i fleishig} \u8212- prepared with chicken broth \u8212- but contains no pieces of meat and has no detectable {\i ta\u8217'am} of meat? And what about grape juice, which carries the full {\i halachic} status of wine? These questions flow naturally from the {\i sugya}\u8217's distinctions between the substance of a prohibited item and the flavor or derivative status it can impart.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meat & Flavor (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the {\i Ran}\u8217's full conceptual analysis, it is worth establishing the question he is responding to. The {\i mishna} appears to assume that items made {\i asur} through a {\i neder} can in principle become {\i batel} in a mixture \u8212- that the usual rules of {\i bittul} apply. This assumption is what the {\i Ran} examines, because it intersects with the principle of {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} and with the distinct phenomenon of {\i min b\u8217'mino}, each of which represents a case where {\i bittul} ordinarily does not operate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Davar She-Yesh Matirin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Davar she-yesh lo matirin} refers to something that can or will become permitted later. The general rule in {\i hilchot bittul} is that such an item cannot be nullified in a mixture. Even if the prohibited item is outnumbered sixty-to-one by the permitted substance it has fallen into, the fact that it will eventually become permitted on its own means we do not allow the mechanism of {\i bittul} to render it permitted now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam cites {\i chadash} \u8212- new grain \u8212- as a paradigmatic example. {\i Chadash} is prohibited {\i d\u8217'oraisa} until after the {\i omer} sacrifice is brought on the second day of {\i Pesach} (or, in the absence of the Temple, until that date passes). Since {\i chadash} will become permissible automatically after that date, it is {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}, and a piece of {\i chadash} bread that falls into a basket of {\i yashan} \u8212- old-grain \u8212- flour cannot be nullified by the majority. The same logic could theoretically extend to {\i chametz} on {\i Pesach}: since {\i chametz} becomes fully permitted once {\i Pesach} ends, it too would appear to qualify as {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi in {\i Beitzah} 3a explains the underlying rationale directly: since the prohibited item will eventually become permitted on its own, there is no need to rely on {\i bittul} \u8212- one should simply wait. {\i Bittul} is a mechanism for dealing with prohibitions that are inherently and permanently {\i asur}. Where the {\i issur} is time-bound, waiting is the appropriate response, and the Torah did not grant the mechanism of {\i bittul} for such cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mordechai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mordechai takes issue with applying {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} to {\i chametz} after {\i Pesach}. His argument is that {\i chametz} never truly becomes unconditionally {\i mutar}: every year it becomes {\i asur} again on {\i Pesach}. Its status is not simply \u8220"prohibited now, permitted later\u8221" but rather an ongoing cycle of alternating permissibility. Because of this vacillating nature, {\i chametz} does not fit the clean conceptual definition of {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}, and so it should not be governed by that principle. The rabbinic prohibition on {\i chametz she-avar alav ha-Pesach} \u8212- {\i chametz} owned by a Jew over {\i Pesach}, which remains forbidden even after {\i Pesach} as a {\i knas} \u8212- is a further complication. That prohibition is {\i miderabbanan}, imposed as a penalty for failing to eliminate {\i chametz} before {\i Pesach}; it does not reflect the intrinsic nature of the substance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} here mounts a fundamental challenge to Rashi\u8217's rationale. If the entire logic of {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} is simply that one should wait for it to become permitted, then {\i nedarim} should also qualify as {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} \u8212- one can always do {\i sheilah}, asking a {\i chacham} to be {\i matir} the {\i neder}. Yet our {\i mishna} clearly implies that items made {\i asur} through a {\i neder} can become {\i batel} in a mixture. This contradicts Rashi\u8217's explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} therefore offers a different conceptual framework, anchoring the principle of {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} in the distinct phenomenon of {\i min b\u8217'mino}. The {\i gemara} in {\i Menachos} establishes that {\i min b\u8217'mino} \u8212- a substance mixed with something of the same kind \u8212- is not subject to {\i bittul}, deriving this from the {\i Yom Kippur} Temple service: even though the blood of the {\i par} (bull) vastly outnumbers the blood of the {\i se\u8217'ir} (goat), the {\i Torah} continues to reference each separately after they are mixed. The reason {\i min b\u8217'mino} is not {\i batel} is conceptual: {\i bittul} works through conflict \u8212- an {\i asur} item and a {\i mutar} item are sufficiently different that one can override the other. But when two items are of the same type, there is no conflict; they are too similar to contradict each other, and therefore neither nullifies the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} extends this logic to {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}: an item that will become {\i mutar} is not genuinely \u8220"in conflict\u8221" with the {\i mutar} substance surrounding it. A truly {\i asur} item \u8212- one that is permanently prohibited \u8212- exists in genuine tension with its {\i mutar} surroundings, which is precisely what allows {\i bittul} to operate. But {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}, which is not a {\i davar ha-asur} in any absolute sense since it will become permitted, cannot be said to \u8220"conflict\u8221" with the permitted mixture in the way required for {\i bittul} to apply. The item\u8217's pending permissibility removes it from the category of genuine {\i issurim} that {\i bittul} is designed to address.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lentils Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lentil Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a {\i riminhu} \u8212- a contradiction \u8212- against what we have just seen in the {\i mishna}. A {\i braita} states: {\i min ha-adashim} \u8212- if one took a {\i neder} against lentils \u8212- {\i asur ba-ashishim} \u8212- he is also forbidden from {\i ashishim}. The {\i mefaresh} understands {\i ashishim} as some form of preparation made from lentil flour mixed with other flour and baked into bread, or perhaps lentils prepared with honey \u8212- a preparation that has undergone a meaningful transformation from plain lentils. The intuition that the transformation should matter \u8212- just as whey is permitted to one who took a {\i neder} against milk \u8212- would suggest {\i ashishim} should be permitted. Yet the {\i braita} forbids them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contradiction runs in both directions. The {\i Tanna Kamma} of our {\i mishna} permits whey to one who vows against milk, yet the {\i Tanna Kamma} of this {\i braita} forbids {\i ashishim} to one who vows against lentils. And Rabbi Yose, who forbids whey, here permits {\i ashishim}. Neither position is internally consistent on its face.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Ke-Atreih\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with a principle already encountered earlier in the tractate: {\i mar ke-atreih mar ke-atreih} \u8212- each follows the vernacular of his place. The resolution is deliberately anti-theoretical. Do not attempt to construct a comprehensive conceptual account of why Rabbi Yose holds as he does in one case and differently in another, or why the {\i Tanna Kamma} takes the positions it does. The disagreements are not about ontology or classification at all \u8212- they are entirely about local usage. In one locale, the word for a thing extends to cover its derivatives; in another locale, it does not. The {\i halakha} of {\i nedarim} follows what words mean to the people who speak them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milk Whey Terminology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} makes this concrete: {\i be-atra de-rabbanan} \u8212- in the place of the Rabbis, meaning the {\i Tanna Kamma} \u8212- people call milk {\i chalba} and whey {\i kuma}. They are simply different words for different things. {\i Be-atra de-Rabbi Yose} \u8212- in Rabbi Yose\u8217's locale \u8212- whey is not called {\i kuma} but {\i kuma de-chalba}, \u8220"milk-whey.\u8221" The word for milk is embedded in the very name of the whey. Consequently, when someone in Rabbi Yose\u8217's place takes a {\i neder} against {\i chalav}, the word {\i chalav} is heard in the name of the whey as well, and so it is included in the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is {\i lashon bnei adam} operating at a hyperlocal level. Not only do we follow how people generally speak \u8212- we follow how people speak {\i in a particular place}. The entire {\i machloket} dissolves into a question of regional terminology. The {\i braita} about lentils and {\i ashishim} follows the same logic: in the locale of the {\i Tanna Kamma} of that {\i braita}, {\i ashishim} is still called by a name close enough to lentils that it falls within the {\i neder}; in Rabbi Yose\u8217's locale, it does not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Grapes & Olives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grapes/Olives Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to fruit: {\i ha-noder min ha-anavim muttar b\u8217'yayin} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against grapes is permitted to drink wine. {\i Min ha-zeitim muttar b\u8217'shamen} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against olives is permitted to use olive oil. The transformation from grape to wine, and from olive to olive oil, is sufficiently complete that wine is not thought of as grapes and oil is not thought of as olives. The process of producing wine does not merely extract something that was already inside the grape; it creates a new substance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Mashkim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Halacha} singles out wine and olive oil as two of the seven {\i mashkim} \u8212- the seven liquids that carry {\i halachic} significance, particularly in the laws of {\i tumah v\u8217'taharah}. The seven are wine, oil, blood, water, milk, dew, and {\i dvash} (bee honey). Only these seven liquids can render food susceptible to {\i tumah} or become {\i tameh} in the relevant ways. Other fruit juices \u8212- orange juice, for instance \u8212- do not carry this status. They are not considered {\i mashkim} in the {\i halachic} sense; from the {\i Torah}\u8217's perspective they remain, as it were, extensions of the fruit itself rather than independent liquids.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This special status of wine and oil is also reflected in the distinct {\i brachot} recited over them. Wine receives {\i borei pri ha-gafen} and oil, in certain contexts, receives its own designation \u8212- in recognition of the transformation from raw fruit to processed liquid. Other juices, by contrast, take the same {\i bracha} as the fruit from which they came.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 These Grapes/Olives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar konam zeitim va-anavim eilu she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em asur ba-hem u-va-yotzei ba-hem} \u8212- if one said \u8220"these olives, these grapes, I will not taste them,\u8221" then not only the fruits themselves but also {\i kol ha-yotzei mei-hem} \u8212- whatever is produced from them \u8212- is forbidden. Just as {\i basar zeh} causes the meat\u8217's status to travel into whatever it flavors, and {\i yayin zeh} causes the wine\u8217's status to travel into whatever dish it enters, so too {\i eilu} \u8212- these specific fruits \u8212- causes the prohibition to extend to whatever emerges from them, including the wine pressed from them or the oil extracted from them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this applies only when one specifies {\i zeh} or {\i eilu} \u8212- \u8220"this\u8221" or \u8220"these.\u8221" If one simply said {\i anavim} \u8212- grapes \u8212- without specification, the wine remains permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami Bar Chama Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates a pointed question from Rami bar Chama: {\i eilu davka o she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em davka?} \u8212- Is it the word {\i eilu} \u8212- \u8220"these\u8221" \u8212- that is the operative element, or is it the phrase {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- \u8220"I will not taste them\u8221"? The two elements appear together in the {\i mishna}\u8217's formulation, and it is unclear which one is doing the legal work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If it is {\i eilu davka}, then {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} should be superfluous \u8212- one could simply say \u8220"these grapes are {\i asur} to me\u8221" without adding \u8220"I will not taste them.\u8221" Conversely, if it is {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} that is operative, then the word {\i eilu} is what requires explanation. Rami bar Chama\u8217's question sets up the {\i sugya} that follows, which will work through several attempted proofs before arriving at Rava\u8217's resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fish Brine Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} revisits the {\i baraita} of Rabbi Yehuda with an eye toward Rami bar Chama\u8217's question. As established earlier, Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's proof from Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's case was qualified by the response that {\i noten ta\u8217'am} applies only when one says {\i basar zeh alai} \u8212- \u8220"this meat is forbidden to me\u8221" \u8212- after which everything that meat flavors is also forbidden. The {\i sugya} now attempts to leverage this {\i baraita} to determine whether the key variable is {\i zeh} specifically or {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fish Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites the {\i mishna} concerning {\i dagim} \u8212- fish. One who says {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em dagim} \u8212- \u8220"I will not taste fish\u8221" \u8212- is forbidden from fish of all sizes and preparation methods, whether large or small, raw or cooked. The {\i mishna} also states {\i u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'tzir} \u8212- he is permitted the {\i tzir}, the brine or liquid that comes out of the fish. This appears to be a case involving {\i yotzei mehen} \u8212- the liquid produced by the prohibited item \u8212- and it is invoked here to try to answer Rami bar Chama\u8217's question. Notably, the {\i mishna} uses {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} without specifying {\i dag zeh} \u8212- \u8220"this fish\u8221" \u8212- which seems to suggest that {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} alone is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava intervenes to block this inference. The {\i tzir} that is {\i mutar}, he says, is only {\i kvar yatza mehen} \u8212- brine that had already come out of the fish before the {\i neder} was made. That pre-existing brine was never part of the fish at the moment of the {\i neder} and so was never included in its scope. But {\i tzir} produced from the fish {\i after} the {\i neder} \u8212- that is genuinely {\i yotzei mehen}, liquid emerging from a {\i davar ha-asur}, and would be prohibited. Rava\u8217's reading prevents the fish case from serving as direct proof one way or the other about the primacy of {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She\u8217'eini To\u8217'em\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite Rava\u8217's qualification, the {\i gemara} ultimately resolves that {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} \u8212- \u8220"I will not taste\u8221" \u8212- is what generates the {\i issur} on {\i yotzei mehen}, not the specification of {\i zeh}. The fish {\i mishna} uses {\i she\u8217'eini to\u8217'em} without {\i dag zeh}, yet the post-{\i neder tzir} is still included. This demonstrates that when a person frames the {\i neder} in terms of tasting \u8212- \u8220"I will not taste X\u8221" \u8212- the prohibition extends to whatever emerges from X afterward, because whatever comes out of it also carries the taste he has foresworn. The word {\i zeh}, by contrast, serves a different function: it ties the prohibition to a specific object and its subsequent {\i noten ta\u8217'am} in other foods, rather than extending to {\i yotzei mehen}. The two formulations thus generate distinct {\i halachic} consequences, and Rami bar Chama\u8217's question is answered accordingly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dates & Winter Grapes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dates/Date Honey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the top of 53a turns to dates. {\i Ha-noder min ha-temarim muttar b\u8217'dvash temarim} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against dates is permitted to eat date honey. The inverse is also stated: {\i min ha-dvash} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against date honey \u8212- is permitted to eat dates. This contrasts with the grapes and wine case in an instructive way. Wine and olive oil are among the seven {\i mashkim}, halakhically independent liquids; date honey is not. It does not constitute a {\i mashkeh} in the technical sense \u8212- it is more properly understood as the syrup or extracted juice of the date rather than a transformed independent substance. The {\i neder} against dates, then, does not include date honey because date honey is simply a separate food, not an extension of the date in the way wine is an extension of the grape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether {\i dvash} in the {\i Torah} refers to bee honey or date honey \u8212- with significant {\i nafka minas} for the prohibition on bringing honey as an offering and for the description of {\i Eretz Yisrael} as {\i zavat chalav u\u8217'dvash} \u8212- is a distinct and important topic. For the laws of the seven {\i mashkim}, the {\i dvash} that counts is {\i dvash devorim} \u8212- bee honey \u8212- not date honey, as established in {\i Masechet Machshirin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Winter Grapes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Istavnit} \u8212- winter grapes, or more precisely {\i istevimit} \u8212- are grapes that remain on the vine through autumn, either because they are too hard to ripen properly or because they are deliberately left late to develop a high acidity. The product made from them is something resembling vinegar \u8212- a very sour pressed liquid with a high content of malic and citric acids, similar to a {\i verjuice}. Alternatively, the reference may be to grapes left so long they become overly sweet, after which wine and then vinegar is made from them. One who takes a {\i neder} against winter grapes is {\i muttar b\u8217'chometz stavnit} \u8212- permitted to consume the vinegar made from them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edible Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar formulates a general principle: {\i kol she-darko le-ekhol ve-derekh ha-yotzeh mimenu le-ekhol} \u8212- anything that is itself customarily eaten and whose extracted liquid is also customarily eaten \u8212- such as dates and date honey \u8212- if one takes a {\i neder} against it, one is also forbidden from what comes out of it. And if one takes a {\i neder} against what comes out of it, one is forbidden from the item itself. The bidirectional inclusion follows from the fact that both the fruit and its liquid are regarded as edible foods in their own right, and neither is so distinct from the other as to escape the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inedible Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's principle has a second half: {\i kol she-ein darko le-ekhol ve-derekh ha-yotzeh mimenu le-ekhol} \u8212- anything not customarily eaten on its own, whose extracted liquid {\i is} customarily consumed \u8212- {\i nadar bo ein assur ela be-yotzeh mimenu}: if one takes a {\i neder} against such a thing, only what comes out of it is forbidden, because {\i she-lo nitkaven zeh ela le-yotzeh mimenu} \u8212- one\u8217's intention was only for the edible product it yields, not the inedible item itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Winter grapes appear to be precisely such a case, according to the Steinsaltz reading of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's position. Since the grapes themselves are not normally eaten, one who takes a {\i neder} against them had in mind only the vinegar or sour liquid they produce. Accordingly, the liquid is forbidden, but the grapes themselves remain {\i mutar}, even though the {\i lashon} of the {\i neder} named the grapes \u8212- because the person had no genuine intent toward the inedible fruit, only toward its edible derivative. This may place Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar in tension with the earlier {\i mishna} that stated {\i ha-noder min ha-anavim muttar b\u8217'yayin}, or it may be that he simply excludes grapes and olives from his principle, treating their wine and oil as categorically different by virtue of their status among the seven {\i mashkim}. The {\i sugya} will continue into 53a.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 53\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening & Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro & Schedule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We are presently in the middle of Nedarim 53a, having moved somewhat ahead in anticipation of the learning around Tisha B\u8217'Av. With that scheduling note aside, we turn directly to the substance of the {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine, Oil, Honey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} midway down 53a opens with a sequence of specific exclusions from {\i nedarim}. {\i Hanoder min ha-yayin mutar b\u8217'yein tapuchim} \u8212- one who vows against wine is still permitted to drink apple wine, something akin to hard cider. The {\i mishnah} then continues: {\i min ha-shemen} \u8212- one who vows against oil \u8212- {\i mutar b\u8217'shemen shumshumin}, is permitted to use sesame oil, since oil, in the primary sense, means olive oil. And {\i min ha-dvash} \u8212- one who vows against honey \u8212- {\i mutar b\u8217'dvash tmarim}, is permitted to use date syrup, since {\i dvash} in the language of the {\i mishnah} already refers to bee honey, {\i dvash dvorim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tapuach & Charoset\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i tapuach} merits a brief note. In Tanakh, {\i tapuach} may not refer to what we think of as an apple at all; it may denote some variety of citrus fruit, possibly an {\i etrog} or something related. This has practical implications, for instance regarding the preparation of {\i charoset}, since the {\i gemara} requires that {\i charoset} include {\i tapuchim}. Extensive research has been done on the range of ingredients \u8212- spices, wine, fruit \u8212- that appear in {\i charoset} traditions across Jewish communities, and the botanical identity of {\i tapuach} bears directly on that question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lashon Bnei Adam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Usage vs.\u160 ?Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overarching principle animating these {\i mishnayot} is {\i lashon bnei adam} \u8212- common, everyday speech. One might suppose that these exclusions rest on technical botanical or culinary definitions: apple wine is not truly wine, sesame oil is not truly oil. But the {\i bavli}\u8217's understanding, at least, is different. The debate is not about technical taxonomy but about what a person actually means when he utters a particular word. The power of human speech to create the {\i neder} is bounded by what the speaker intends, which in turn is determined by ordinary usage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's cases make this clear. When a person says {\i dvash} today, no one \u8212- even in the most health-conscious of communities \u8212- intends agave nectar or maple syrup. Everyone understands {\i dvash} to mean bee honey. The meaning of {\i dvash} in Tanakh is a separate question; there it likely referred in many contexts to fruit syrup rather than a bee product. But by {\i lashon chachamim}, by the time of the {\i mishnah}, {\i dvash} had come to mean bee honey, and that is the {\i lashon bnei adam} that governs the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vinegar & Sour Foods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i min ha-chometz mutar b\u8217'chometz stavniyot} \u8212- one who vows against vinegar is still permitted to use the vinegar produced from the winter grapes discussed in the previous {\i mishnah}. These are grapes left on the vine until winter, either unripened or overripened, which yield a sour condiment used in cooking. Since this is not the standard vinegar a person has in mind when he uses the word {\i chometz}, it falls outside the scope of the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leeks & Plants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Min ha-krishim mutar ba-kappalotot} \u8212- one who vows against {\i krishim} (leeks) is permitted to eat {\i kappalotot} (a different variety of leek). Many of the plants that appear throughout {\i mishnayot} \u8212- the {\i luf} plant in {\i Masechet Shevi\u8217'it} being a notable example \u8212- require specialized study to identify. There are dedicated works on the flora and fauna of the Talmud, and they are indispensable for understanding such passages with precision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field vs.\u160 ?Garden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses an important distinction: {\i hanoder min ha-yerek mutar b\u8217'yarkot ha-sadeh} \u8212- one who vows against {\i yerek} (vegetables) is permitted to eat field vegetables, {\i yarkot ha-sadeh}, because the {\i neder} is understood to apply only to garden vegetables, {\i yarkot ginah}, the ones cultivated and sold in markets. The reason given is {\i she-hu shem levai} \u8212- field vegetables carry a distinctive, qualifying name. The concept of {\i shem levai} appears in other areas of {\i halacha} as well; for instance, the {\i mishnah} in {\i Parah} rules that the {\i ezov} used for the {\i parah adumah} must be the plain variety, not one that carries a qualifying name such as {\i ezov yavan} or {\i ezov kochli}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One may ask whether the distinction between garden and field vegetables turns on species \u8212- whether certain species are inherently wild and uncultivated \u8212- or on the actual condition of the plant, whether a particular specimen happened to grow wild even if its species has been domesticated. This remains an interesting open question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oil & Rov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oil in EY & Bavel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i baraita} (introduced with {\i tanya}) from the {\i Tosefta} that elaborates on the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling about oil. {\i Ha-noder min ha-shemen b\u8217'eretz Yisrael} \u8212- one who vows against oil in the Land of Israel \u8212- {\i mutar b\u8217'shemen shumshumin ve\u8217'asur b\u8217'shemen zayit}: he is permitted to use sesame oil but prohibited from olive oil, because in Eretz Yisrael, where olive trees are plentiful, olive oil is the default oil. {\i U\u8217'v\u8217'Bavel asur b\u8217'shemen shumshumin u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'shemen zayit} \u8212- in Bavel, where sesame is the primary oil crop, the {\i neder} against oil prohibits sesame oil and permits olive oil.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Locales\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then addresses a place where both oils are in use: {\i asur ba-zeh u\u8217'va-zeh} \u8212- both are forbidden. This seems obvious \u8212- {\i pshita}! The {\i gemara} responds: {\i la tzricha} \u8212- no, we need this ruling. {\i D\u8217'rov mi-chad mistapkin mahu} \u8212- what is the law in a locale where the majority of oil use comes from one type? One might have thought we should follow the {\i rov} and only prohibit the majority oil. The {\i baraita} therefore teaches us that even in such a case, both are forbidden, because {\i safek issura l\u8217'chumra} \u8212- in a case of doubt regarding a prohibition, we rule stringently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a deeper question about the nature of this {\i rov}. The {\i Beis Yosef} (in {\i Siman} 208 on the {\i Tur}) notes a crucial distinction. The standard application of {\i rov} is a case where the majority of the population uses one oil exclusively and a minority uses the other exclusively. But what if everyone uses both oils, simply in different proportions? In that scenario, no one truly thinks of one oil as {\i the} oil to the exclusion of the other. As the {\i Beis Yosef} points out, when everyone in a locale uses both, the {\i rov} argument loses its force, because {\i b\u8217'lashon bnei adam} everyone there understands that \u8220"oil\u8221" refers to both. The {\i baraita}\u8217's statement that {\i asur ba-zeh u\u8217'va-zeh} may therefore be directed at a case where some people use only one and others use only the other \u8212- with a majority using one \u8212- and the ruling comes to teach that even the minority oil is included, because {\i safek issura l\u8217'chumra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Deoraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This application of {\i safek issura l\u8217'chumra} to {\i nedarim} raises a broader question: would we not simply apply {\i safek deoraita l\u8217'chumra} here? This gets into a significant dispute among {\i rishonim}. The {\i Rambam}, most prominently in {\i Hilchos Tumas Meis} but also elsewhere, maintains that the principle of {\i safek deoraita l\u8217'chumra} is itself rabbinic in origin. According to the {\i Rambam}, on a {\i deoraita} level every {\i safek} is technically permitted, since one cannot establish the prohibition conclusively. The one exception the {\i Rambam} acknowledges is the {\i asham talui}, which one brings for doubts about certain {\i aveiros} \u8212- and even there the {\i Rambam} reasons carefully. The {\i Rashba}, by contrast, holds that {\i safek deoraita l\u8217'chumra} is itself a {\i din deoraita}, built into the structure of Torah law itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against the backdrop of this dispute, the specific invocation of {\i safek issura l\u8217'chumra} for {\i nedarim} raises a further point. Even according to the {\i Rashba}, who makes {\i safek deoraita l\u8217'chumra} a biblical principle, one might have asked: why should a self-imposed {\i neder} \u8212- an {\i issur} that the individual created through his own speech \u8212- receive the same treatment? That requires its own {\i halacha}. And according to the {\i Rambam}, who holds the general principle is only rabbinic, perhaps {\i nedarim} could be treated with special stringency even at a {\i deoraita} level, given that a person has personally undertaken the obligation through his own words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Speech & Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This points to a broader dimension of {\i nedarim}: the authority of human speech to create prohibition must, in some measure, connect the speaker to the {\i Hakadosh Baruch Hu}. When a person vows, he is in some way placed in a position analogous to divine legislation \u8212- his words carry binding force. That parallel may be precisely why the {\i safek} in {\i nedarim} is treated with comparable gravity to a {\i safek} in Torah-ordained law. A proof for {\i safek deoraita l\u8217'chumra} as an explicit Torah principle may also be derivable from {\i Parshas Ki Seitzei}, where the verse concerning cutting down trees during a siege seems to indicate that a tree of doubtful status \u8212- neither clearly a fruit tree nor clearly otherwise \u8212- may not be cut down, which would constitute a direct textual grounding for treating {\i safek} in {\i deoraita} matters stringently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shemita & Vegetables\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shemita Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i hanoder min ha-yerek} \u8212- one who vows against vegetables \u8212- {\i b\u8217'she\u8217'ar shnei shavua asur b\u8217'yarkot ha-ginah u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'yarkot ha-sadeh} \u8212- during the six ordinary years of the seven-year cycle, the {\i neder} applies to garden vegetables and not to wild field vegetables, since the latter carry a {\i shem levai} and were presumably not in the person\u8217's mind. {\i U\u8217'va-shvi\u8217'it asur b\u8217'yarkot ha-sadeh u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'yarkot ha-ginah} \u8212- but during a {\i shemita} year, the ruling reverses: the {\i neder} applies to wild field vegetables, not to garden vegetables. Why? Because during {\i shemita}, one cannot ordinarily be cultivating annual garden vegetables; it would be impermissible to maintain such a garden in most configurations. So the vegetables one actually means when saying {\i yerek} during {\i shemita} are the wild, field varieties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An interesting question arises: if one vowed against {\i yerek} in the third year, does the meaning of that vow shift when the {\i shemita} year arrives, reflecting the changed {\i lashon bnei adam} of that time? Or does the {\i mishnah} apply only to a {\i neder} taken during the {\i shemita} year itself? The answer is not clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Imports & Abbahu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu, quoting Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, qualifies the ruling: {\i lo shanu ela bimkom she\u8217'ein mevi\u8217'in yerek mi-chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- this applies only to a place where vegetables are not imported from outside Eretz Yisrael. If there is no active import trade, then whatever vegetables are available during {\i shemita} must be the permissible field vegetables, and the {\i neder} is understood accordingly. But {\i aval bimkom she-mevi\u8217'in yerek mi-chutz la\u8217'aretz asur} \u8212- in a place where vegetables are imported from outside Eretz Yisrael, garden vegetables remain available even during {\i shemita}, and therefore the {\i neder} would apply to those as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that this is {\i ka-tanai} \u8212- analogous to a Tannaic dispute \u8212- and records the debate: one {\i tanna} holds that one may not import vegetables into Eretz Yisrael from outside at all (not only during {\i shemita} but at any time), while Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel permits the importation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedushat Shvi\u8217'it\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before moving on, it is worth noting that eating fruits and vegetables with {\i kedushat shvi\u8217'it} is not itself prohibited. On the contrary, the {\i Rambam} holds that there is a {\i mitzvah} to eat {\i peirot shvi\u8217'it}, derived from the phrase {\i lachem l\u8217'ochlah}. The various {\i heterim} employed by the {\i rabbanut} or other agencies to allow {\i shvi\u8217'it} produce into the marketplace are a separate and complex topic, but the underlying point is that one should not be anxious about eating such produce \u8212- that is precisely what it is there for. The {\i Rav Kook} reportedly suggested that one should wash one\u8217's hands before eating {\i kedushat shvi\u8217'it} fruits, not necessarily with a {\i bracha}, but as an expression of deference to the {\i kedusha} of those fruits, which carry the holiness of the Land of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumat Met & Soil\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the reason for the prohibition on importing vegetables into Eretz Yisrael according to the first opinion? {\i Amar Rabbi Yirmiya mishum gush} \u8212- Rabbi Yirmiya explains it on account of a clod of soil. Vegetables brought from outside Eretz Yisrael may carry soil from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, and the soil of foreign lands carries a rabbinic {\i tumah} known as {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} \u8212- {\i tumat meit m\u8217'drabbanan} \u8212- because of the concern that the soil of those lands may be mixed with fragments of bone from bodies buried throughout the terrain. Since one cannot know, the Rabbis applied a blanket {\i tumah} to the soil of lands outside Eretz Yisrael. This concern would be relevant primarily for those who maintain {\i chaverim} standards of purity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vegetable Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cabbage & Asparagus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with additional cases. {\i Min ha-keruv assur ba-isparagus} \u8212- one who vows against cabbage ({\i keruv}) is also prohibited from eating {\i isparagus}, which appears to be a subcategory of cabbage. The Steinsaltz edition describes {\i isparagus} as an energy drink prepared from a leafy green vegetable, possibly kale \u8212- a characterization that reflects the culinary discourse of our own era. Other {\i sugyot} in {\i shas} suggest it may be a prepared drink of some kind, though the precise preparation is unclear. In any case, {\i isparagus} is a sub-category of {\i keruv}, and therefore a {\i neder} against the more inclusive term encompasses it. {\i Min ha-isparagus mutar ba-keruv} \u8212- but if one vowed only against {\i isparagus}, the {\i neder} does not extend to cabbage generally, since {\i isparagus} is the more specific subcategory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Etymology & Modern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i keruv} also means the {\i keruvim}, the angelic figures on the {\i aron}. Our ancestors did not place two golden cabbages on the {\i aron}, so one wonders what the two words share. One possibility is that the way a cabbage\u8217's leaves fold tightly over each other resembles the way the {\i keruvim}\u8217's wings enclosed their bodies \u8212- the same quality of folding inward and covering. In modern Hebrew, {\i keruvit} means cauliflower, while Brussels sprouts, being simply small cabbages, are called {\i keruv katan}. Whether the etymological connection between {\i keruv} the vegetable and {\i keruvim} the angels is biblical or post-biblical is itself an interesting question, as {\i keruv} in the sense of cabbage may not appear in Tanakh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grits & Mikpeh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Min ha-grisin assur ba-mikpeh} \u8212- one who vows against {\i grisin} (some kind of coarsely ground grain or bean, prepared into a thick porridge \u8212- roughly analogous to grits) is also prohibited from eating {\i mikpeh}, a specific prepared dish, perhaps a bean soup or grain porridge. {\i Mikpeh} appears elsewhere in {\i shas} in {\i Masechet Sukkah}, where the question of {\i yardu geshamim} (rain falling into the {\i sukkah}) is resolved when {\i mi-she-tisrach ha-mikpeh} \u8212- when the {\i mikpeh} is ruined by the rain. {\i Mikpeh} is more inclusive than plain {\i grisin}; it is a dish made with {\i grisin} as a principal ingredient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Garlic & Mikpeh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Min ha-mikpeh assur ba-shum} \u8212- one who vows against {\i mikpeh} is even prohibited from eating garlic, because garlic was evidently a principal ingredient in {\i mikpeh}. When a person says {\i mikpeh}, the garlic is so central to that dish that even plain garlic falls within his intent. {\i Ve-Rabbi Yose matir} \u8212- Rabbi Yose disagrees and permits it, since he does not consider garlic to be encompassed by the word {\i mikpeh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This entire sequence of cases turns on the principle of {\i lashon bnei adam}. We are not living in the era of the {\i mishnah} and cannot know with certainty what each term evoked for its speakers. That is precisely why contemporary {\i poskim} who receive {\i shailot} about {\i nedarim} \u8212- and people do take {\i nedarim} inadvertently or with less than full appreciation of their severity \u8212- must carefully investigate what the person actually meant and whether grounds exist for {\i hatarah}, {\i petach}, or {\i charata}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Min ha-shum mutar ba-mikpeh} \u8212- but if one vowed only against garlic, he is permitted to eat {\i mikpeh}, because {\i mikpeh} is a complete dish in its own right, not reducible to garlic alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lentils & Bread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Min ha-adashim assur ba-ashishim} \u8212- one who vows against lentils is prohibited from eating {\i ashishim}, a kind of lentil cake or bread made from lentil flour, because lentil bread is included in the person\u8217's intent when he says {\i adashim}. {\i Rabbi Yose matir} \u8212- Rabbi Yose permits it, holding that {\i ashishim} carries its own distinct name and is therefore a separate food. {\i Min ha-ashishim mutar ba-adashim} \u8212- but one who vows against {\i ashishim} specifically is permitted to eat plain lentils, since {\i ashishim} is the more specific term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wheat Singular/Plural\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then discusses {\i chita chitin she-eini to\u8217'eim} \u8212- one who says \u8220"wheat I will not taste\u8221" \u8212- {\i asur bahen bein kemach bein ha-pat}: it is prohibited whether as flour or as baked bread. The word {\i chitin} in {\i mishnaic} Hebrew refers both to the grain and to wheat bread, since bread colloquially took its name from its primary ingredient. An interesting question arises about raw flour: since no one eats raw flour, one might argue a person never had raw flour in mind when saying {\i chitin}. Yesterday\u8217's {\i daf} touched on a related {\i machloket} about foods that are inedible in their natural state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} introduced with {\i tanya} records Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's view: {\i chita she-eini to\u8217'eim} \u8212- if one says \u8220"wheat\u8221" in the singular \u8212- {\i asur le\u8217'afot} \u8212- it is prohibited to bake it into bread \u8212- {\i u\u8217'mutar lachos} \u8212- but permitted to chew raw kernels. Because {\i chita} in the singular refers to {\i pat shel chita}, a loaf of bread from wheat flour. But {\i chitin she-eini to\u8217'eim} \u8212- if one uses the plural form {\i chitin} \u8212- {\i assur lachos u\u8217'mutar la\u8217'afot} \u8212- it is prohibited to chew on raw kernels but permitted to bake, because {\i chitin} refers to the wheat grains themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda had earlier made a similar distinction between the singular {\i gris} and the plural {\i grisin}. The same logic applies: {\i gris she-eini to\u8217'eim assur levashel u\u8217'mutar lachos} \u8212- a vow against a single {\i gris} prohibits preparing it as porridge but permits chewing the raw grain; {\i grisin she-eini to\u8217'eim assur lachos u\u8217'mutar levashel} \u8212- a vow against the plural {\i grisin} prohibits chewing and permits boiling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important methodological question about how to read {\i mishnah}. When a later {\i tanna} states a position that overlaps with an earlier one, is he disagreeing, or is he clarifying what the earlier {\i tanna} already meant? The tendency to minimize {\i machloket} as much as possible \u8212- reading later opinions as specifications or elaborations rather than as genuine disagreements \u8212- is a recognizable approach, and one that the Artscroll edition tends to favor. Alternatively, one can read the editorial history of the {\i mishnah} as genuinely incorporating later opinions that diverge from earlier ones, which is not a defect but simply how law develops. Neither approach is obviously wrong; they reflect different methodological commitments about how {\i mishnah} was composed and what its editors intended.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Perek 7: Yerek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gourds & Yerek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach ha-noder min ha-mevushal} \u8212- we have completed the sixth {\i perek} of {\i Nedarim} and begin the seventh. The seventh {\i perek} opens: {\i ha-noder min ha-yerek mutar b\u8217'diluim} \u8212- one who vows against {\i yerek} (vegetables) is permitted to eat {\i delu\u8217'im} (gourds, squash, pumpkins). One possible explanation: {\i yerek} may literally mean {\i greens}, referring specifically to leafy or green-colored produce, and {\i delu\u8217'im}, which come in shades of orange, yellow, and other colors, do not fit that description. Just as we speak of \u8220"getting some greens\u8221" and do not mean tomatoes or orange bell peppers, {\i yerek} may exclude gourds on coloristic or categorial grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-Rabbi Akiva oser} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva, however, prohibits {\i delu\u8217'im} as well. The {\i chakhamim} respond: {\i va-halo amar adam l\u8217'shlucho kach li yerek ve-hu omer lo matzasi ella diluim} \u8212- is it not the case that if a person sends a messenger ({\i shaliach}) to buy vegetables, and the {\i shaliach} returns saying \u8220"I\u8217'm sorry, they didn\u8217't have any {\i yerek}, they only had {\i delu\u8217'im},\u8221" the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} would be dissatisfied? The {\i shaliach} came back empty-handed because he concluded that gourds are not {\i yerek}. Does this not prove that {\i delu\u8217'im} are not included in {\i yerek}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legumes & Merchants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva responds: {\i kein ha-davar} \u8212- is that really what would happen? {\i O shema omer hu lo matzati ella kitnit} \u8212- would the {\i shaliach} not rather say, \u8220"They didn\u8217't have anything except beans\u8221"? That is, the {\i shaliach} would return empty-handed when there were only {\i kitniyot} available \u8212- because {\i kitniyot} are clearly not {\i yerek} \u8212- but if {\i delu\u8217'im} were available, the {\i shaliach} would simply buy them and bring them back. {\i Ela she-ha-diluim bichlal yerek ve-kitniyot eino bichlal yerek} \u8212- gourds are included in the category of {\i yerek}, while beans are not. According to Rabbi Akiva, then, the {\i chakhamim}\u8217's scenario is not realistic; a sensible {\i shaliach} would bring back the gourds. It is only beans that would provoke the response \u8220"they didn\u8217't have vegetables.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kitniyot Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the question of what {\i kitniyot} means as a category. We are familiar with the prevalent {\i Ashkenazi minhag} \u8212- and also the custom of some North African communities \u8212- to avoid certain foods on {\i Pesach}, including rice, various legumes, mustard, and possibly sesame. The word {\i kitniyot} reflects a broad taxonomic category already present in {\i mishnaic} law. The {\i Rambam} in {\i Hilchos Kilayim} recognizes a tripartite division of cultivated plants: {\i tevuah} (grain), {\i yerakot} (vegetables), and {\i kitniyot} \u8212- a broad intermediate category with its own {\i halachot} for {\i kilayim} and other areas. The {\i Ashkenazi Pesach minhag} of {\i kitniyot} does not necessarily encompass every plant in this rabbinic category, though the question of how broadly to apply it has generated ongoing debate. The rhetorical moves made by those who question the {\i minhag} \u8212- pointing to coffee beans or cocoa beans \u8212- reflect genuine engagement with where the category\u8217's boundaries lie.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ful Hamitzri\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes the {\i perek} with: {\i ve-assur b\u8217'ful ha-mitzri lach u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'yavesh} \u8212- one who vows against {\i yerek} is prohibited from eating fresh Egyptian beans ({\i ful ha-mitzri}) but permitted to eat them dried. The {\i ful} in Arabic-speaking cuisine of North Africa is a familiar staple, and this is almost certainly the same food. When fresh, {\i ful ha-mitzri} is a vegetable; when dried and hard, it becomes a {\i kitnit}. Green beans provide a useful analogy: fresh off the plant, with the pod still bright green and tender, they are a vegetable. Fully dried, the beans inside the pod become a legume.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction has practical {\i halachic} ramifications: many families are willing to eat fresh green beans on {\i Pesach} even while observing the {\i minhag} of {\i kitniyot}, since the fresh bean in its pod is a vegetable, not a dried legume. The same transition point applies to peas \u8212- fresh pea pods are a vegetable; split peas are a legume. Fresh corn on the cob occupies a similar conceptual space: fresh, it reads as a vegetable; dried and ground, it approaches the status of a grain. The moment of transition from fresh vegetable to dried grain or legume is itself a meaningful taxonomic and {\i halachic} threshold.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara: Shaliach Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yarkei Kedeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now enters into the dispute between the {\i rabbanan} and Rabbi Akiva more analytically. A {\i meitivei} presses: if one who vows {\i mimini yerek} \u8212- from types of vegetables \u8212- is permitted, what is the exact content of the {\i neder} and what does it tell us about the dispute? {\i Amar Ulla be\u8217'omer yarkei kedeira alai} \u8212- Ulla explains: we are dealing with a case where the person said not simply {\i yerek} but {\i yarkei kedeira} \u8212- pot vegetables \u8212- are forbidden to me. This introduces a specific subcategory. In this framing, the {\i neder} targets pot vegetables specifically, leaving open the possibility of sending the {\i shaliach} to purchase a different kind of {\i yerek} that does not go in a pot. The dispute between the {\i rabbanan} and Rabbi Akiva would then concern whether {\i delu\u8217'im} qualify as {\i yarkei kedeira} or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cooked in Pot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges Ulla\u8217's reading: {\i ve-dilma yerek ha-ne\u8217'echal ba-kedeira ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- perhaps the person said a vegetable {\i eaten} from a pot, or as the alternate formulation has it, {\i yerek ha-mitbashel ba-kedeira}, a vegetable {\i cooked} in a pot? In that case, it is not a named subcategory but a universal description \u8212- any vegetable cooked in a pot \u8212- and that would encompass essentially all vegetables. Under this reading, Ulla cannot use the {\i yarkei kedeira} framing to explain why a {\i shaliach} might be sent to purchase a different kind of {\i yerek}: if all cooked vegetables are included, there is no remainder for the {\i shaliach} to retrieve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Messenger Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Ulla\u8217's resolution set aside, the {\i gemara} proceeds directly to the underlying dispute: {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'mai kamiflagi?} \u8212- what exactly do the {\i rabbanan} and Rabbi Akiva disagree about? {\i Rabbanan savri: kol milta de-tzarikh shaliach le-imlukhei aleih, lav mineihu} \u8212- the {\i rabbanan} hold that if the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} would consider dismissing the {\i shaliach} for having made the purchase, that demonstrates it was not what the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} meant. When we say a person would send back the {\i shaliach} for bringing {\i delu\u8217'im} when asked for {\i yerek}, that proves {\i delu\u8217'im} are not {\i yerek}. The standard of interpretation is the {\i meshale\u8217'ach}\u8217's intent and expectation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-Rabbi Akiva savar: kol milta de-mamlikh shaliach aleih, mineihu} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva holds that whatever falls within the {\i shaliach}\u8217's range of reasonable judgment is included in the category. If the {\i shaliach} could plausibly have concluded that {\i delu\u8217'im} fulfill the request for {\i yerek}, then {\i delu\u8217'im} are indeed {\i yerek}. The {\i meshale\u8217'ach}\u8217's subjective displeasure is not the determining factor; what matters is whether the {\i shaliach} had a reasonable basis for his decision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This disagreement reflects a deeper question about the nature of {\i shelichut}. Is a {\i shaliach} an extension of the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} \u8212- {\i shaliach mi-din yad} \u8212- in which case the {\i shaliach} has no independent standing and every decision he makes is evaluated purely by reference to what the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} would have wanted? Under this model, the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's position is entirely natural: the {\i shaliach} is only an arm of the {\i meshale\u8217'ach}, and the {\i meshale\u8217'ach}\u8217's intent is the sole criterion. Alternatively, is the {\i shaliach} a newly empowered halakhic agent \u8212- someone to whom the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} has granted genuine authority to act? Under that model, the {\i shaliach} retains a measure of autonomous judgment, and the question shifts to what a reasonable agent in that role would determine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlucho Kemoso\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This second model, in its most radical form, implies that what the {\i shaliach} decides {\i is} the {\i meshale\u8217'ach}\u8217's will \u8212- legally, by definition. When the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} empowers a {\i shaliach} with full {\i shelichut}, he transfers his {\i ratzon} to the {\i shaliach}: whatever the {\i shaliach} does, the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} is bound by it, not because the {\i shaliach} guessed correctly, but because the {\i meshale\u8217'ach} empowered the {\i shaliach} to stand in his place. {\i Shlucho shel adam ke-moso} \u8212- a person\u8217's agent is as the person himself. This principle generates many further questions across {\i shas}, including whether {\i shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid} and whether {\i shaliach oseh shaliach}, and it remains a central topic in the {\i lomdishe sfarim} on {\i shelichut} broadly. We will continue from approximately the midpoint of 54a tomorrow morning, which will be {\i Rosh Chodesh Av}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 54\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pumpkins & Vegetables\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Pumpkin Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i hanoder min hayerek mutar badiluim} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} not to derive benefit from vegetables is permitted to eat pumpkin. The immediate question is why pumpkin should not be included in the category of {\i yerek} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} at the opening of his commentary offers two distinct explanations. The first is: {\i dela havei yerek ela pri} \u8212- pumpkin is not a vegetable at all; it is a fruit. It grows on a vine and falls into the {\i pri} category rather than the {\i yerek} category. The second explanation offered by others is: {\i desatam yerek ne\u8217'echal kemoto shehu chai} \u8212- the ordinary meaning of {\i yerek} refers to something that is edible while raw. {\i Diluim} do not meet that criterion, and therefore fall outside the scope of the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edible Raw Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether something is {\i ne\u8217'echal kemoto shehu chai} \u8212- edible in its raw state \u8212- carries significant halachic weight across a range of areas. One prominent application is {\i bishul} on {\i Shabbat}. The {\i rishonim} discuss whether cooking a food that is already edible raw constitutes the {\i melacha} of {\i bishul}, and the {\i halacha} is that it does. {\i Tosafot} and the {\i Mordechai} raise the case of baked apples and similar foods where the item remains edible throughout but undergoes a qualitative transformation \u8212- becoming soft, mushy, and differently flavored \u8212- and even so, the conclusion is that such cooking is still {\i chayav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A clarifying example: boiling water. Water is edible cold and edible hot; functionally nothing has changed. Yet it remains subject to the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The related question of partially cooked food generates a significant {\i machloket}. The {\i Rashba} holds that once a food has been cooked to the point of edibility, it is considered halachically cooked, and one is not {\i chayav mide\u8217'oraita} for cooking it further \u8212- for instance, returning a rare steak to the fire. The {\i Rambam} disagrees, holding that any improvement through further cooking, up to the point of full completion, continues to constitute the {\i melacha} of {\i bishul}. The {\i Shiltei Gibborim} goes even further, ruling that even fully cooked food, if further cooking would improve it \u8212- {\i mitztamek veyafeh lo} \u8212- remains subject to the {\i melacha de\u8217'oraita}, until the point at which it begins to deteriorate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second major application is {\i bishul akum}. A food that is edible in its raw state is exempt from the prohibition of {\i bishul akum}, since the non-Jew\u8217's involvement in its preparation is irrelevant \u8212- the food could have been eaten without any cooking at all. Carrots, for instance, are edible raw, and therefore cooked carrots present no {\i bishul akum} concern. The same logic has been applied to sushi, as eating raw fish has come to be regarded as normative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i v\u8217'Rabbi Akiva oser} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva prohibits eating pumpkin under such a {\i neder}. The {\i chachamim} challenge him: {\i vehalo omer adam lishlucho kach li yerek veomer lo matzati ela diluin} \u8212- is it not true that a person who sends a {\i shaliach} to buy vegetables, and the {\i shaliach} returns saying they had no vegetables but did have pumpkins, would report back in exactly those terms? The implication of the {\i chachamim} is that this return-and-report demonstrates pumpkin is {\i not} a vegetable \u8212- the {\i shaliach} came back empty-handed, distinguishing pumpkins from what was requested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva, however, responds differently. He draws a contrast: if a {\i shaliach} came back and said they had no vegetables but did have {\i kitnit} \u8212- beans \u8212- no one would say that, because beans are too far removed from the vegetable category. The very fact that the {\i shaliach} specifically returns to report that pumpkins were available, and to ask whether pumpkins would be acceptable, is what interests Rabbi Akiva.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also addresses the case of {\i ful hamitzri} \u8212- the Egyptian bean. {\i Vaasur beful hamitzri lach umutar beyavesh} \u8212- one is prohibited from eating it when fresh but permitted when dry. Fresh green beans are clearly a vegetable; dried beans are a different matter. This line supports Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position, demonstrating that even within the {\i kitnit} category, context determines whether a food falls within the scope of {\i yerek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machloket Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now articulates the underlying dispute. {\i Rabbanan savri kol milta d\u8217'tzarich shlicha l\u8217'amlokei alei lav minei hu} \u8212- the {\i rabbanan} hold that anything the {\i shaliach} feels the need to return and clarify is thereby shown to be {\i outside} the requested category. If he comes back and says \u8220"they had no vegetables but they did have pumpkins,\u8221" he is implicitly distinguishing pumpkin from vegetable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'Rabbi Akiva savar kol milta d\u8217'mimloch shlicha alei minei hu} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva holds the opposite: anything the {\i shaliach} returns to specify is shown to be {\i within} the category. The {\i shaliach} is saying: the only vegetable they had was pumpkin \u8212- do you want it? The act of asking the question itself indicates that pumpkin is understood to belong to the same general category as what was requested. The {\i machloket} thus turns on whether the need to specify is an act of inclusion or exclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ulla\u8217's Precision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} questions the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation from a different angle: {\i tanan hamudar min hayerek vehu min hayerek nadar} \u8212- but the {\i mishnah} simply says he took a {\i neder} from {\i yerek}, and {\i yerek} sounds inclusive enough that even Rabbi Akiva should concede that {\i diluim} would be included. The language seems too broad to generate the {\i machloket} described. {\i Amar Ulla beomer yarkei kedeirah alai} \u8212- Ulla therefore explains that the {\i mishnah} must be dealing with a case where the person used more precise language: \u8220"vegetables for the pot are forbidden to me.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back on Ulla\u8217's reading, suggesting alternative formulations: {\i vedilma yerek hane\u8217'echal bekedeirah ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- perhaps he said \u8220"vegetables that are eaten in a pot,\u8221" or {\i beomer yerek hamitbashel bekedeirah alai} \u8212- \u8220"vegetables that are cooked in a pot.\u8221" Any of these phrasings, while sounding more specific, would still be relatively inclusive, and Ulla\u8217's precision requirement would not meaningfully distinguish the case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashba\u8217's Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rashba} extends the question raised here \u8212- whether {\i diluim} falls within the category of {\i yerek} \u8212- beyond the laws of {\i nedarim} entirely. Drawing on the {\i Yerushalmi}, the {\i Rashba} notes that the same categorical question applies in commerce and in the {\i halachot} of {\i hefker}. If a person declares all of his vegetables {\i hefker}, did he intend to include his pumpkins? The {\i Rashba}\u8217's point is that this is a broadly applicable question about the scope of categorical language, not one confined to the specific domain of vows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Specification Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to articulate the positions more precisely. {\i Rabbanan savri kol milta d\u8217'tzarich shlicha l\u8217'amlokei alei lav minei hu} \u8212- the {\i tanna kamma} holds that anything requiring the {\i shaliach} to return and seek clarification is thereby shown to lie {\i outside} the category. The specification signals a distinction, not an inclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'Rabbi Akiva savar kol milta d\u8217'mimloch shlicha alei minei hu} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva maintains that the act of returning to specify is precisely what places something {\i within} the category. The {\i shaliach}\u8217's clarification is a statement that the item is the only representative of the requested category available; it is not a concession that the item falls outside it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Malkot vs.\u160 ?Issur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye modeh Rabbi Akiva l\u8217'inyan malkot she\u8217'eino lokeh} \u8212- Abaye introduces an important distinction: even Rabbi Akiva concedes that one does not receive {\i malkot} for eating pumpkin in violation of a {\i neder} against {\i yerek}. Rabbi Akiva holds that it is {\i assur}, but he would not say that the violation of {\i bal yachal} is sufficiently clear-cut to impose lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is connected to the principle of {\i safek nedarim l\u8217'hachmir} \u8212- that in cases of doubt regarding {\i nedarim}, we rule stringently. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is essentially one of {\i safek l\u8217'chumra}: the item is close enough to the prohibited category to be forbidden, but the doubt is real enough that punishment cannot follow. There is also a technical dimension relating to the rules of {\i hatra\u8217'ah} \u8212- the warning that witnesses must deliver before an {\i aveirah} can result in {\i malkot}. The warning must be specific and unambiguous, and where there is genuine uncertainty about whether the item falls within the {\i neder}, the conditions for {\i malkot} cannot be met. The {\i issur} remains; the {\i onesh} does not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlichut Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlichut Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's discussion of the {\i shaliach} who returns to report on the availability of certain items opens the door to a broader treatment of {\i shlichut}. The major {\i sugyot} on {\i shlichut} appear in {\i Kiddushin} and will be encountered there in due course, but several fundamental points are relevant here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The foundational principle is {\i shlucho shel adam k\u8217'moto} \u8212- a person\u8217's {\i shaliach} stands in his stead. There are, however, different models of {\i shlichut}: one in which the {\i shaliach} is an extension of the sender, and another in which the {\i shaliach} is an independent actor operating on the sender\u8217's behalf. The scope of {\i shlichut} varies depending on the type of act being performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One important application of this principle, touched on tangentially in this context, concerns {\i chamar medina} \u8212- the locally significant beverage that may substitute for wine for {\i kiddush} and {\i havdala}. The question of whether a {\i shaliach} sent for wine who returns with beer parallels the {\i diluim} case: does the return-and-report indicate that beer is within the category of wine, or does it mark beer as distinct? Rav Moshe Feinstein addresses a related question regarding seltzer water in a well-known {\i teshuvah}, suggesting that the operative criterion is whether a beverage is consumed for enjoyment beyond mere thirst. Other {\i poskim} apply a different standard, asking whether it is something one would serve to guests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Shaliach L\u8217'Aveira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A central principle of {\i shlichut} is {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveirah} \u8212- one cannot appoint an agent to commit a sin on one\u8217's behalf. There are three distinct ways to understand what this principle means in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first understanding focuses on the {\i meshaleach} \u8212- the sender. The reasoning is {\i divrei harav v\u8217'divrei hatalmid divrei mi shomim}: when there is a conflict between God\u8217's law and a human instruction, God\u8217's law prevails. The {\i shaliach} knows the act is prohibited and has no legitimate basis to comply; therefore, the act cannot be attributed back to the sender.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second understanding focuses on the {\i shaliach} himself. Since this is someone else\u8217's idea entirely \u8212- the {\i shaliach} did not conceive the act independently \u8212- one might argue that holding the {\i shaliach} liable requires some affirmative basis. The third understanding draws an analogy between {\i shlichut} and {\i tnai}: the capacity to appoint a {\i shaliach} is equivalent to the capacity to formulate a conditional \u8212- both require that one actually controls the act in question. Since a {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition is not within one\u8217's control to authorize or modify, one cannot make a {\i tnai} on an {\i aveirah}, and therefore cannot appoint a {\i shaliach} to perform one. Conversely, for a {\i mitzvah}, the analysis differs, with a further distinction between {\i mitzvah she\u8217'begufu} \u8212- a {\i mitzvah} performed with one\u8217's own body, such as wearing {\i tefillin}, which cannot be performed by an agent \u8212- and other {\i mitzvot} that can be fulfilled through an agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are three recognized exceptions to {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveirah}, derived from specific sources: {\i me\u8217'ilah}, where one sends a {\i shaliach} to spend {\i hekdesh} money; {\i shlichut yad}, where a {\i shomer} misappropriates a watched object; and the case of {\i dalet v\u8217'hei}, where slaughtering or selling a stolen animal can be accomplished through an agent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlichut Principles (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: Meila\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tnan hatam: hashaliach she\u8217'asah shelichuto, baal habayit maal} \u8212- a {\i mishnah} elsewhere states that if the {\i shaliach} performed the task he was assigned, the {\i baal habayit} \u8212- the owner who sent him \u8212- is liable for {\i me\u8217'ilah}. {\i Me\u8217'ilah} is the misappropriation of consecrated {\i hekdesh} property for personal use. This {\i mishnah} establishes {\i me\u8217'ilah} as one of the exceptions to {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveirah}, and the following analysis explains why.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meila Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Correct Performance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a person instructs his {\i shaliach} to take money \u8212- which turns out to be {\i hekdesh} \u8212- and purchase a watermelon, and the {\i shaliach} does exactly that, then {\i baal habayit maal} \u8212- the sender has committed {\i me\u8217'ilah}. The {\i shaliach} acted as a pure extension of the sender, executing the task precisely as assigned, and the liability flows back to the one who dispatched him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deviation from Task\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo asah shelichuto} \u8212- if, however, the {\i shaliach} deviates from his instructions and purchases something other than what was requested, then {\i shaliach maal} \u8212- it is the {\i shaliach} who bears the {\i me\u8217'ilah} liability. By departing from the {\i shelichut}, the {\i shaliach} acts on his own volition and severs the chain of agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Man tana?} \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks which {\i tanna} is the author of this {\i mishnah} on {\i me\u8217'ilah}. {\i Amar Rav Chisda matnitin lo ke-Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- Rav Chisda suggests it does not fit Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position, citing another {\i mishnah}: {\i keitzad amar lo ten basar le\u8217'orchin venatan lahem kaved} \u8212- if the householder instructed his steward to serve meat to guests and the steward served liver instead, or vice versa, {\i hashaliach maal} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} has violated the {\i shelichut}. Here \u8220"maal\u8221" may not strictly denote {\i me\u8217'ilah} in the technical sense; it means the {\i shaliach} has transgressed the terms of the agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case appears to cut against Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position. Under Rabbi Akiva\u8217's framework, the {\i shaliach}\u8217's act of returning to clarify \u8212- \u8220"they had no meat, but they did have liver\u8221" \u8212- would indicate that liver is {\i within} the category of meat, making the {\i shaliach}\u8217's independent substitution irrelevant. But here, serving liver when meat was requested is treated as a deviation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ve\u8217'i Rabbi Akiva, ha amar Rabbi Akiva kol milta d\u8217'mimlecha hashaliach minei} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva can still maintain his position, because his principle applies only when the {\i shaliach} actually returns to check. In this case, the {\i shaliach} did not go back and ask; he simply substituted on his own initiative. The act of seeking clarification is what does the classificatory work in Rabbi Akiva\u8217's system. Without that act, no inference about categorical inclusion can be drawn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye elaborates: {\i modeh Rabbi Akiva de-tzarich imlucha} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva concedes that the {\i shaliach} must actually have gone back and inquired. The {\i mishnah} therefore fits Rabbi Akiva\u8217's framework after all: since the steward substituted without checking, he bears the liability. Had he returned and asked \u8220"you asked for meat \u8212- shall I give them liver, since that is all available?\u8221" and that inquiry been made and addressed, the liver would have been classified as falling within the requested category. The inquiry itself is the operative act; its absence means the {\i shaliach} acted unilaterally. {\i Amar Rava: shapir amar Nachmani} \u8212- Rava affirmed Abaye\u8217's analysis, referring to him by his alternate name {\i Nachmani}, a name associated with Abaye\u8217's adoptive family connection to {\i Rabbah bar Nachmani}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meat vs.\u160 ?Poultry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Man tanna de\u8217'palig alei de\u8217'Rabbi Akiva?} \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks who is the {\i tanna} in our {\i mishnah} who disagrees with Rabbi Akiva, holding that {\i diluim} are excluded from a {\i neder} against {\i yerek}. The answer is Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, as established by a {\i baraita}: {\i hanoder min habasar, assur be\u8217'kol minei basar} \u8212- one who takes a {\i neder} against meat is prohibited from all types of meat. The {\i baraita} enumerates: the head, the legs, the trachea ({\i kaneh}), the liver, the heart, poultry \u8212- all are included. Fish and locusts, however, are permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's position is stricter in one respect and narrower in another: {\i hanoder min habasar, assur be\u8217'kol minei basar, umutar be\u8217'rosh u\u8217'be\u8217'raglayim u\u8217'be\u8217'kaneh u\u8217'be\u8217'kaved u\u8217'be\u8217'lev u\u8217'be\u8217'ofot, ve\u8217'ein tzarich lomar basar dagim vachagavim} \u8212- he holds that a {\i neder} against {\i basar} applies only to red meat proper, excluding offal, poultry, and obviously fish and locusts. The word {\i basar}, for Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, means beef or lamb \u8212- what one would colloquially call meat \u8212- and not the broader category of {\i fleishig}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} is discussed in the context of {\i Masechet Chullin} as well, in {\i perek kol habasar}, which treats the question of which meats are prohibited together with milk. The appearance of this material in that {\i masechet} alongside its treatment here in {\i Nedarim} is itself noteworthy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Offal Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Keravim lav basar} \u8212- Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel states that organ meats are not {\i basar} in the relevant sense. He goes further: {\i veochleihem lav bar inish} \u8212- those who eat them are not acting as civilized people. This reflects both a socioeconomic sensibility and a cultural one. In societies where resources are scarce, people consume organ meats out of necessity; those from more affluent backgrounds may view such consumption as beneath the standard of {\i bar inish}. The comment is worth reading in its social context \u8212- Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel came from a prominent family, and his characterization reflects the tastes and norms of that milieu. Nonetheless, the {\i halachic} point stands independent of the social commentary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds: {\i ochleihem kevasar leinyan zvini} \u8212- those who eat them are treated as having purchased meat {\i for purposes of commerce}. If a {\i shaliach} is sent to purchase meat and returns with offal, that constitutes a departure from the {\i shelichut} \u8212- it is not the meat that was intended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poultry vs.\u160 ?Fish Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai shna basar of?} \u8212- why should poultry be treated differently from fish regarding a {\i neder} against {\i basar}? According to the {\i tanna kamma}, poultry is included within such a {\i neder} while fish is not. But a {\i shaliach} sent for meat might just as plausibly return and report the availability of fish as of poultry. If the {\i shaliach}\u8217's report-back is the operative criterion for categorical inclusion, why does the {\i tanna kamma} draw the line between poultry and fish rather than including both or excluding both?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bloodletting Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Answer 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye kegon sheheikiz dam d\u8217'lo achil dagei} \u8212- Abaye\u8217's first answer is that the {\i mishnah} is addressing the specific context of {\i hakazat dam}, bloodletting. After a bloodletting, a person should not eat fish. Accordingly, if a {\i shaliach} were sent to procure food after a bloodletting, and he returned to report that only fish was available, no one would take that as a meaningful clarification \u8212- the person would not have wanted fish regardless. With poultry, the situation is different: a person who has had a bloodletting {\i can} eat poultry under certain conditions (as will be elaborated), and therefore a {\i shaliach}\u8217's return to specify that poultry is available does constitute a meaningful clarification within the category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i D\u8217'amar Shmuel d\u8217'misukar v\u8217'achil bisra d\u8217'tsafra parach libei k\u8217'tsafra} \u8212- Shmuel states that a person in a certain vulnerable medical state who eats meat in the morning will find that his heart flutters like a bird. This is understood as referring to heart palpitations \u8212- a warning against eating poultry after {\i hakazat dam}. The {\i gemara} then challenges Abaye: if Shmuel says that even poultry causes heart palpitations after bloodletting, what distinguishes poultry from fish in this context? If both are medically problematic, why would the {\i shaliach} clarify about poultry but not fish?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: Hakazat Dam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} provides a comprehensive list of foods to avoid after {\i hakazat dam}: {\i hekiz dam lo yochal lo chalav velo gevinah velo beitzim velo shachalayim velo ofot velo basar maliach} \u8212- after bloodletting, one should not eat milk, cheese, eggs, {\i shachalayim} (a small leafy vegetable, possibly cress), poultry, or salted meat. The {\i baraita} also teaches: {\i ein makizin lo al hadagim velo al haofot} \u8212- do not perform bloodletting [and then eat] fish or poultry. The general principle seems to be one of dietary restriction and care for the recovering body \u8212- eat simply, eat fresh, and avoid straining the system.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question has been raised whether a modern blood donation carries the same {\i halachic} status as the {\i hakazat dam} discussed in the {\i gemara}, and whether the dietary restrictions would therefore apply. One consideration pointing toward exclusion is that a modern blood donation is not a therapeutic procedure performed for the donor\u8217's own benefit, and unlike classical bloodletting, it does not leave the body in the same depleted medical state. There is also a practical consideration: one should not discourage people from donating blood, which is of vital communal importance. In any case, the {\i Rambam} discusses these dietary guidelines in {\i Hilchot De\u8217'ot}, situating them as medical advice within a broader framework of healthful living based on the humoral medicine of his era.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Answer 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now offers a second answer to the question of why {\i tanna kamma} distinguishes between poultry and fish. {\i Abaye amar kegon dechaivin lei eineih} \u8212- the context here is someone whose eyes are in pain. {\i Dagim kashim la\u8217'einayim} \u8212- fish is harmful for painful eyes. And therefore, if a {\i shaliach} is sent on behalf of someone with eye pain, and returns to report that fish is available, the person would have no interest in fish \u8212- so the clarification would be pointless. With poultry, there is no such constraint, and the {\i shaliach}\u8217's report-back remains meaningful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, the distinction between poultry and fish in the {\i hakazat dam} context resolves along a similar axis: {\i shani ofot de\u8217'efshar al yedei shelikah} \u8212- poultry is different from fish in that if it is prepared {\i shaluk}, stewed or braised, it may be consumed after bloodletting. Fish, by contrast, is entirely prohibited following {\i hakazat dam}. This distinction explains why the {\i tanna kamma} draws the line where he does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fish & Eyes Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds a striking counterpoint from Shmuel: {\i nuna sama l\u8217'eina} \u8212- fish is a remedy for the eyes. Shmuel derived this from the sequence of Hebrew letters {\i nun, samech, ayin} \u8212- which spell {\i nuna sama l\u8217'eina}, fish is medicine for the eye. The apparent contradiction \u8212- fish is both harmful and beneficial for the eyes \u8212- is resolved by distinguishing stages of the condition: fish is harmful when the eyes are actively in pain, but serves as a preventative or restorative once the acute phase has passed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chida} applies this {\i gemara} in a broader {\i kabbalistic} context, noting that {\i nuna sama l\u8217'eina} has been taken to mean that fish protects against {\i ayin hara}, the evil eye, and connects this to the widespread custom of eating fish on {\i Shabbat}. He also records a practice of reciting the eleven {\i pesukim} in {\i Tanakh} that both begin and end with the letter {\i nun}, as a protective measure against {\i ayin hara} \u8212- drawing directly on this {\i gemara}\u8217's equation of {\i nun} with protection for the eye. With this, we leave off and will continue from here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 57\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Perek Transition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Scope Shift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 57 opens with a significant shift in character from the preceding material. Much of the previous perek and a half was dedicated to examining what objects are included within the scope of particular vow-words: what falls under {\i bayis}, what is encompassed by {\i ir}, what is meant by {\i mevushal} or {\i tavshil}, or when something is considered to have been poured into a pot. All of that analysis revolved around specific objects and the linguistic reach of particular terms. Now we encounter a considerably more conceptual {\i mishna} \u8212- one whose key {\i chiddushim} were largely anticipated in earlier {\i gemara} discussions and will therefore not be rehearsed at length here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Konam & Korban Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the top of 57a opens with several formulations: {\i konam peiros ha\u8217'eileh alai} \u8212- these fruits are forbidden to me via {\i konam}; {\i konam hein al pi} \u8212- they are forbidden to my mouth; {\i konam hein lifi} \u8212- to my mouth. In all such cases, the person is not merely restricted from the specific fruits themselves. The {\i mishna} rules {\i assur b\u8217'chilufeihen u\u8217'v\u8217'giduleihen} \u8212- he is also forbidden from things those fruits have been exchanged for ({\i chilufim}), and from what grows from them ({\i gidulim}). If the fruit is still on the tree and continues to grow, that growth is included. And even if we are speaking of a second generation \u8212- say, a peach pit that was planted and produced a new tree \u8212- those fruits are likewise forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual foundation for this rule lies in the model upon which {\i nedarim} are built. The keyword {\i konam} is drawn from the world of {\i korbanot}: to declare something {\i konam} is to treat it as though it were a {\i korban}. In the domain of {\i hekdesh}, if one sells a {\i korban} or attempts to exchange it, the money received also becomes {\i kadosh} \u8212- the principle of {\i tofes es dam\u8217'ha}, that the sanctity adheres to what replaces it, is operative. This is most pronounced in {\i avodah zarah} and {\i shvi\u8217'it}, and applies to some degree in {\i hekdesh} as well \u8212- most clearly in the laws of {\i temura}, where an attempt to transfer {\i kedusha} from one animal to another results in both animals becoming {\i kadosh}. The concept of {\i gidulim} is similarly rooted in the {\i hekdesh} paradigm: if one consecrates a cow and it gives birth, the offspring \u8212- {\i vlad hekdesh} \u8212- is itself {\i kadosh}. This is precisely the concept of {\i giduleihem} in miniature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issurei Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} in an earlier section raised the question of how far these principles extend to {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} generally. If one possesses {\i orlah} \u8212- fruit forbidden during the first three years of a tree\u8217's growth \u8212- and sells it, does the money received also become forbidden? As a general rule, the answer is no: the principle that exchanges become forbidden applies specifically to {\i avodah zarah} and {\i shvi\u8217'it}, and in certain respects to {\i hekdesh}, but not to other categories of {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah}. The {\i gemara} there, as the Ran develops it, explored whether at minimum the seller himself might remain forbidden from the proceeds even if others could benefit \u8212- a notion arising from the intimacy between the transgressor and his own exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that {\i chilufim} and {\i gidulim} are forbidden in the context of {\i konamot} draws heavily, then, on the specific character of {\i konam} language. Because {\i konam} invokes the world of {\i hekdesh}, both exchanges and growths are treated as carrying the original forbidden status forward. One earlier {\i gemara} also raised the question of {\i yesh me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'konamot} \u8212- whether transgressing a {\i konam} vow carries with it a violation of {\i me\u8217'ilah}, the prohibition of trespass against {\i hekdesh}. This too derives from the {\i konam}-as-{\i korban} framework, though the {\i gemara} does not restrict this principle exclusively to cases where the word {\i konam} was used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that while the {\i konam} formulation seems to be the primary conceptual driver, the {\i Rishonim} \u8212- including the Ran \u8212- also pointed, in discussing the earlier {\i gemara}, to the {\i intent} of the person making the vow. One might argue that when a person says \u8220"this is forbidden to me,\u8221" they implicitly mean to forbid not just this object but everything that flows from it. This, however, is less clearly the {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna} than the {\i konam}-based explanation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Konam vs.\u160 ?Action Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ochel/To\u8217'em Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces a critical distinction. If instead of declaring the fruit {\i konam}, the person said {\i she\u8217'ani ochel} \u8212- I will not eat these fruits \u8212- or {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em} \u8212- I will not taste them \u8212- then the rule reverses: {\i mutar b\u8217'chilufeihen u\u8217'v\u8217'giduleihen}. In these formulations, the exchanges and growths are permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 State vs.\u160 ?Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two explanations converge here. The first concerns the absence of the word {\i konam}: without it, the vow does not invoke the {\i hekdesh} model and therefore does not carry forward the stringencies associated with that domain, including the extension of the prohibition to {\i chilufim} and {\i gidulim}. The second \u8212- and this is what Tosafot, the Ran, and the {\i Rishonim} generally emphasize \u8212- concerns not what was omitted but what was said. When one declares an object {\i assur}, that is a statement about the {\i state} of the object: it is forbidden. A state, by its nature, can transfer: the state of being forbidden inheres in this fruit, and that state can carry over to what replaces it or grows from it. But when one says {\i she\u8217'ani ochel} or {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em}, the prohibition is not about the object\u8217's state \u8212- it is about a specific {\i action} with respect to that object. The prohibition is on the act of eating or tasting {\i this} fruit. If one then eats or tastes what grew from the pit of that fruit, or benefits from the proceeds of an exchange, one is not performing that specific action on that specific object. The vow simply does not reach there. Even phrasing like {\i konam hein al pi} \u8212- forbidden to my mouth \u8212- though it might seem to approximate action-language, is still essentially a statement of the object\u8217's state vis-\u224 ?-vis oneself, not a specification of a concrete action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction also touches on the longstanding question about whether {\i she\u8217'ani ochel} implies an inadvertent blurring of {\i neder} and {\i shevuah}. A {\i shevuah} is an oath about one\u8217's own behavior, and only the person who swore can violate it. A {\i neder} is classically about the status of an object. The use of {\i she\u8217'ani ochel}, however, creates a {\i neder} about a specific action, a {\i neder} whose reach is therefore bounded by that action rather than by the object\u8217's general status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zaro Kalah Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zaro Kalah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then distinguishes between two categories of {\i gidulim} based on the physical character of the original object. {\i B\u8217'davar she\u8217'zaro kalah} \u8212- when the original thing goes out of existence \u8212- the growth that emerges is permissible. This applies even when the formulation used was {\i konam} rather than {\i she\u8217'ani ochel}. The classic example is planting wheat seeds: the seeds themselves cease to exist, and what grows is an entirely new generation. In such a case, even if one used {\i konam} language, the {\i gidulim} are permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Zaro Kalah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval b\u8217'davar she\u8217'ein zaro kalah} \u8212- but when the original thing does not cease to exist, when it continues to grow and expand \u8212- the rule is reversed: {\i afilu gidulei gidulim assurim}, even what grows from the growth is forbidden. The {\i gemara} will discuss the case of an onion as the paradigmatic example, since replanting an onion results in the original onion continuing to grow rather than ceasing to exist and being replaced by a new generation. Similarly, fruit continuing to grow on a tree represents {\i ein zaro kalah}: the original fruit is still there, merely expanding. In such cases, even if the person used {\i she\u8217'ani ochel} language, the prohibition remains in force. One reason this might be so is that when the original forbidden thing is still present and the new growth is still physically attached to and an extension of it, the new growth partakes of the same status as the original \u8212- there is no clear boundary between old and new, and the growth may itself be {\i assur} as a continuation of the original forbidden matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gidulei Gidulim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of {\i gidulei gidulim} \u8212- growth of growth, i.e., the third generation \u8212- requires a moment\u8217's attention. When we say that {\i afilu gidulei gidulim assurim}, this applies in the {\i ein zaro kalah} scenario: the original thing is still there and continues to grow, and even what grows from that growth carries the same status. But what scenario would produce a meaningful {\i gidulei gidulim} in the {\i ein zaro kalah} case, given that the original is always present? The {\i Rishonim} note that a true third-generation scenario really only makes structural sense in the {\i zaro kalah} case \u8212- take the wheat seed, plant it, harvest the new generation, take seeds from {\i that}, and plant again. In the {\i ein zaro kalah} case, the original is always there, making it difficult to identify a distinct third generation. Nevertheless, the {\i mishna}\u8217's language covers the case, even if the practical application in the {\i ein zaro kalah} context is less clear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife\u8217's Labor & Time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duration Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to a distinct and important set of cases involving the husband\u8217's forbidding of benefit from his wife\u8217's labor. The first formulation: {\i she\u8217'at osah eini ochel ad haPesach} \u8212- what you produce I will not eat until Pesach; or {\i she\u8217'at osah eini mitkaseh ad haPesach} \u8212- what you produce I will not clothe myself with until Pesach. In this formulation, {\i ad haPesach} \u8212- until Pesach \u8212- modifies the {\i duration} of the prohibition: he is forbidding himself from deriving benefit until a certain point in time. Accordingly, {\i asta lifnei haPesach mutar leechol ulehitkasot achar haPesach}: anything she produced before Pesach, if it is still available after Pesach, he may then eat it or wear it \u8212- because his restriction was temporal, not object-based.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Object Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then presents the contrasting case: {\i she\u8217'at osah ad haPesach eini ochel}, {\i she\u8217'at osah ad haPesach eini mitkaseh} \u8212- what you make until Pesach I will not eat; what you make until Pesach I will not wear. Here, {\i ad haPesach} shifts position and now modifies the {\i object}, not the duration: it specifies {\i which} things are forbidden \u8212- namely, everything produced between now and Pesach \u8212- with no temporal limit on the prohibition itself. Accordingly, {\i astah lifnei haPesach assur le\u8217'echol u\u8217'lehitkasot achar haPesach}: whatever she made before Pesach remains forbidden even after Pesach, because the restriction falls on those specific objects permanently. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishna} is precisely that the placement of the temporal modifier determines the entire structure of the prohibition. In the second formulation, the phrasing is somewhat ambiguous \u8212- one could theoretically read {\i she\u8217'at osah, ad haPesach eini ochel}, placing the comma differently and treating the time-limit as the modifier of the prohibition\u8217's duration rather than its object. The {\i mishna} reads it in the more stringent direction, which may itself be part of the {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Reread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point it is worth noting that the {\i mishna} contains a preliminary clause that was inadvertently passed over \u8212- a case of what scribes call {\i hashmata al yedei hadmiyot}, omission due to similarity of adjacent phrases. Returning to the proper beginning: {\i ha\u8217'omer le\u8217'ishto} \u8212- a man says to his wife \u8212- {\i konam ma\u8217'aseh yadayich alai}, {\i konam hein al pi}, {\i konam hein lefi}: what you produce with your hands is {\i konam} to me, forbidden to my mouth. The {\i mishna} rules {\i assur b\u8217'chilufeihen u\u8217'v\u8217'giduleihen} \u8212- he is forbidden even from the exchanges and the growths. If he instead says {\i she\u8217'ani ochel} or {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em} \u8212- I will not eat or taste \u8212- then {\i mutar b\u8217'chilufeihen u\u8217'v\u8217'giduleihen}: permissible. And as before, this last leniency applies only {\i b\u8217'davar she\u8217'zaro kalah}; if the original thing persists, {\i afilu gidulei gidulim assurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam Wife Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} accordingly establishes that when {\i konam} language is used regarding the wife\u8217's labor, the husband is forbidden from {\i chilufim} and {\i gidulim} as well. The Ran, citing Rabbeinu Yona, addresses a practical consequence: if the wife bakes bread and sells it in the marketplace, the husband may not benefit from the proceeds \u8212- except that he is entitled to recoup the cost of the raw materials. The reasoning is that the household runs on the husband\u8217's income, and the raw materials were purchased with his money. What he is forbidden from is the value of {\i her} labor, not the cost of inputs that were his to begin with. He may therefore deduct the cost of the raw materials from whatever she received and retain that portion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i mishna} introduces a considerably more complex structure: a conditional vow with two distinct time-frames. {\i She\u8217'at nehenes li ad haPesach} \u8212- you may not derive benefit from me until Pesach \u8212- {\i im holeches at l\u8217'veit avich ad hachag}: if you go to visit your father\u8217's house between now and {\i Succos}. The setting is assumed to be before Pesach \u8212- perhaps {\i Adar} \u8212- and the husband has set up a vow with a prohibition window of roughly one month (until Pesach) and a condition window of six months (until {\i Succos}). The condition can thus be triggered {\i after} the prohibition period has already ended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Triggered Before\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} first presents the straightforward case: {\i halcha lifnei haPesach asura b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah ad haPesach} \u8212- if she actually goes to her father\u8217's house before Pesach, during the period when both the condition window and the prohibition window are active, she is clearly forbidden from deriving benefit until Pesach. The condition has been triggered while the prohibition is in force; the vow operates without complication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Transgress\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more striking case follows. What if she derives benefit before Pesach \u8212- relying on the fact that the condition has not yet been triggered \u8212- and then, after Pesach, she goes to her father\u8217's house and triggers the condition? The {\i mishna} rules that she transgresses {\i lo yachel devaro} retroactively. At the moment she benefited, it might have seemed permissible, since the condition was not yet operative. But once she later triggered the condition, her earlier benefit is retroactively found to have violated the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechatchila Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} does not, however, address a question that the {\i gemara} discussed at length in an earlier context: was she permitted {\i lechatchila} to derive benefit before Pesach, relying on her intention not to trigger the condition? Or do we say she may not, because we are concerned that people do not take the triggering condition as seriously as the vow itself, and three months hence she may well visit her father without the weight of the original vow bearing upon her decision? This debate among the {\i Amoraim} was recorded earlier in the {\i gemara} and remains unresolved by the {\i mishna} itself; the {\i mishna} tells us only about the retroactive case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam vs.\u160 ?Ran\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's statement that she transgresses {\i lo yachel devaro} \u8212- he shall not void his word \u8212- raises another question discussed by the {\i Rishonim} in the earlier {\i gemara}: specifically, can she receive {\i lashes} for this transgression? The Rambam held that lashes for a vow-violation can only be administered to the person who actually took the vow and violated it. If the husband declares something forbidden to the wife, she may have transgressed a prohibition \u8212- but it was not {\i her} words she violated; it was his. Accordingly, the Rambam maintains that lashes for {\i lo yachel devaro} can only be given either when a person violates their own vow, or when the one who made the vow is the one who actively causes the transgression \u8212- e.g., if the husband made the vow and he is the one who fed her the forbidden item. If she herself benefited, she may have done something wrong, but she cannot be flogged, because it was not her {\i lo yachel devaro}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran disagrees fundamentally. For the Ran, a {\i neder} is not primarily about whose words were spoken; it is about the real {\i halachic} status that was created in the object. Once the husband declared something forbidden to his wife, that object or that benefit acquired a genuine prohibition-status. If she then violated that status, she has violated something real and tangible, not merely someone else\u8217's speech-act. The Ran\u8217's position draws directly on the distinction between {\i neder} and {\i shevuah}: a {\i shevuah} is categorically about the person\u8217's own words and can only be violated by the one who swore. A {\i neder}, by contrast \u8212- modeled as it is on {\i hekdesh} \u8212- creates an objective status in the world. If someone makes an object {\i hekdesh} and another person misappropriates it, that other person has committed {\i me\u8217'ilah} regardless of whose words consecrated the object. So too with a {\i neder}: the wife, by benefiting from what her husband forbade, violated the prohibition inherent in the object itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also illuminates the husband\u8217's leverage within this structure: because the object\u8217's status is what matters, and because he owns the things he produces and can declare them forbidden to her, he has created a genuine constraint on her behavior rooted in the {\i hekdesh} paradigm. She, for her part, can make her own vows and create obligations on her own productions \u8212- though he retains the power of {\i hafara}, annulment, in areas that affect him, which significantly limits her ability to create binding constraints that he cannot dissolve. He, by contrast, has no such vulnerability.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Condition/Prohibition Va\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Condition Expires First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final clause of the {\i mishna} reverses the time-frames: {\i ad manat li ad hachag im halachet l\u8217'veit avich ad haPesach} \u8212- you may not derive benefit from me until {\i Succos} if you go to your father\u8217's house between now and Pesach. Here the condition window is shorter than the prohibition window: the condition can only be triggered for approximately one month (until Pesach), while the prohibition lasts for six months (until {\i Succos}). Once Pesach arrives without the condition having been triggered, the condition has lapsed entirely and can no longer be activated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Visit Timing Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Halcha lifnei haPesach asura b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah ad hachag} \u8212- if she went before Pesach, the condition was triggered during its active window, and she is now forbidden from benefit until {\i Succos}. {\i U\u8217'muteret leilech achar haPesach} \u8212- but if she did not go before Pesach, once Pesach passes, she may go freely; the condition has expired and the vow never took effect. The vow itself runs until {\i Succos}, but the triggering condition was limited to the period before Pesach, and that window has now closed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gidulim & Bittul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onion Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the specific phenomenon of {\i gidulim} and introduces a striking question. Yishmael of Kfar Yama \u8212- or, some say, Kfar Dima \u8212- raised the following case: an onion whose root was uprooted during {\i shvi\u8217'it}, the {\i shemita} year, and which therefore carried {\i kedushat shvi\u8217'it} \u8212- or perhaps was even subject to the laws of {\i biyur} and fully forbidden \u8212- was then planted in the eighth year, the year following {\i shemita}. The new growth in the eighth year eventually exceeded the original quantity \u8212- the amount that grew was a majority relative to, or at least enough to create {\i bittul} of, the original core. What is the status of the whole?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The physical intuition behind the question is clear: the original core is {\i assur} by virtue of its {\i shvi\u8217'it} status; the stuff that grew in the eighth year, however, grew without any such status and is {\i heter}. If the {\i heter} growth sufficiently exceeds the original {\i assur} core, might we apply the laws of {\i bittul} \u8212- nullification \u8212- and declare the whole permissible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two preliminary points before the {\i gemara} proceeds. First, regarding the phrase {\i he\u8217'elah b\u8217'yado} \u8212- \u8220"he brought up in his hand\u8221" \u8212- the plain reading, followed by Tosafot, is that Yishmael literally took this onion and replanted it. This raises its own questions: does taking {\i shvi\u8217'it} produce from {\i Eretz Yisrael} to outside the Land raise issues? And if {\i shemita} does not apply in {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz}, why would he need to plant it specifically in the eighth year? The Ran, however, reads {\i he\u8217'elah b\u8217'yado} idiomatically, as \u8220"he raised in hand\u8221" \u8212- meaning he brought forward this as a theoretical question, not that he literally performed the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, regarding the word {\i rov} \u8212- \u8220"majority\u8221": normally {\i rov} is far from sufficient to create {\i bittul}; depending on the {\i issur}, one needs sixty times, one hundred times, or even two hundred times the original amount. The Ran therefore reads {\i rov} here as {\i lav davka} \u8212- imprecise \u8212- meaning simply that the growth was sufficient, in principle, to create {\i bittul}, whatever amount that might require in the specific case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bittul Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With those clarifications, the {\i gemara} formulates the question precisely: {\i gidulav heter v\u8217'ikaro assur} \u8212- what grew is {\i heter}, the core is {\i assur}. {\i Keivan d\u8217'rabu gidulav me\u8217'ikaro, otan gidulei heter ma\u8217'alin et ha\u8217'issur} \u8212- since what grew constitutes enough to ordinarily create {\i bittul} of the original amount, does that {\i heter} growth nullify the {\i issur}? {\i O lo} \u8212- or does it not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why might {\i bittul} fail to operate here? One consideration is that {\i bittul} ordinarily requires that the forbidden item be truly lost within a larger, independently existing body of {\i heter}. Here, the {\i heter} is not independently existing \u8212- it grew {\i from} the {\i assur} core. It might therefore be argued that growth derived from a forbidden object cannot turn around and negate that very object; the derived cannot negate the source from which it derives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran presses even further. The plain reading of the {\i gemara}\u8217's question is that the growth is genuinely {\i heter} but perhaps cannot perform {\i bittul}. The Ran, however, reads the {\i gemara} as leaving open the possibility that the {\i gidulim} are not really {\i heter} at all \u8212- that because they grew from an {\i assur} core, they themselves are {\i assur}. If that is so, there would be no {\i heter} growth to create {\i bittul} in the first place. These are two distinct conceptual positions: either the growth is {\i heter} but lacks the independent weight to negate what spawned it, or the growth is itself {\i assur} as an extension of the original forbidden matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Onion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo ha\u8217'biyadei} \u8212- Rabbi Ami had no answer. The questioner then came before Rabbi Yitzchak Naphcha, who attempted to resolve the question. He cited a statement of Rabbi Chanina Treita in the name of Rabbi Yannai: {\i batzal shel terumah sheneta\u8217'o v\u8217'rabu gidulav al ikaro, mutar} \u8212- a {\i terumah} onion that was planted, and whose growth exceeded the core, is {\i mutar}. This is precisely the same physical scenario transposed from {\i shvi\u8217'it} to {\i terumah}, and the ruling is permissibility. This seems to provide a clear {\i psak}: when growth sufficiently exceeds the original amount, {\i bittul} operates and the whole becomes permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orlah Grafting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiyah \u8212- and some say Rabbi Zereika \u8212- challenged Rabbi Yitzchak Naphcha\u8217's citation. His objection was pointed: {\i shavik mar trein v\u8217'avid ke\u8217'chad} \u8212- why are you setting aside the opinion of {\i two} authorities and ruling in accordance with {\i one}? He argued that there exist two rulings going the other way, establishing that in this type of scenario, {\i bittul} does not operate and the whole remains {\i assur}. The first of these rulings is attributed to Rabbi Abahu in the name of Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orlah Specifics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abahu in the name of Rabbi Yochanan taught: {\i yaldah she\u8217'sibkah b\u8217'zekeinah} \u8212- a young branch, grafted into an older tree. The governing {\i halacha} of grafting is that if a branch bearing no fruit is grafted onto an existing tree, it is absorbed into the tree and any subsequent fruit it produces takes on the status of the host tree, without {\i orlah} concerns. But if the young branch already has fruit growing on it when it is grafted \u8212- and that fruit is {\i orlah}, since the branch is from a tree within its first three years \u8212- then after grafting, the fruit continues to grow. The fruit that grew after grafting is itself not {\i orlah}, since it grew while incorporated into the older tree; but the portion of the fruit that existed at the moment of grafting carries {\i orlah} status. So we have a core of {\i orlah} and a growth of {\i heter}. The {\i halacha} requires two hundred times the original amount to create {\i bittul} for {\i orlah}. {\i Af al pi she\u8217'hosifu matayim, assur} \u8212- even if the {\i heter} growth reached two hundred times the amount of the original {\i orlah}, the fruit remains forbidden. The {\i gidulim} do not perform {\i bittul} on the original {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting in passing that some {\i poskim} have invoked this principle \u8212- a grafted branch with existing growth that becomes incorporated into a host \u8212- as an analogy for certain questions in the domain of surrogate motherhood, where the original and the subsequent growth are similarly intertwined. But that is a discussion unto itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kilayim Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second ruling comes from Rabbi Shmuel bar Rabbi Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yonatan: {\i batzal sheneta\u8217'o b\u8217'kerem} \u8212- an onion planted in a vineyard, which constitutes {\i kilayim} and renders the onion forbidden. The vineyard is subsequently uprooted \u8212- {\i ne\u8217'ekar hakerem} \u8212- so that the onion continues to grow without any ongoing {\i kilayim} violation, since the vineyard no longer exists. The portion that grew before the vineyard was uprooted is {\i assur} because of {\i kilayim}; the portion that grew afterward is {\i heter}. The ruling: {\i assur}. Here, then, is a fourth instance of the same physical scenario \u8212- we have now traversed {\i shemita}, {\i terumah}, {\i orlah}, and {\i kilayim}, four separate {\i halachic} domains \u8212- and in two of them, the ruling is {\i assur}. Rabbi Yirmiyah therefore pressed: why adopt the lone opinion of Rabbi Chanina Treita that the growth creates {\i bittul} when two authorities \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan in the {\i orlah} case and Rabbi Yonatan in the {\i kilayim} case \u8212- rule otherwise?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'asrot Bittul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'asrot Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The questioner returned, now thoroughly confused, to Rabbi Ami, who attempted to resolve the matter from a different direction. Rabbi Ami cited a ruling of Rabbi Yitzchak in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i litra betzalim she\u8217'tiknah ve\u8217'zar\u8217'ah, mitaseret lefi kullah} \u8212- if one had a {\i liter} of onions from which {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} had already been properly separated, planted them, and harvested them after they had grown substantially, those onions are subsequently tithed based on the {\i total} quantity \u8212- not merely on the newly grown portion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'asrot Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic behind Rabbi Ami\u8217's citation is as follows: since {\i ma\u8217'asrot} had already been taken from the original amount before planting, the original portion technically has no further tithing obligation. If we were to tithe based on the total harvest, that implies that the new growth has, in some sense, nullified the original \u8212- rendering the whole as if it were all new, untithed produce. This would suggest that {\i bittul} does operate: the {\i heter} growth absorbs the original, and we treat everything as subject to the new status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stringency Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The objection, however, is immediate: {\i dilma l\u8217'chumra shani} \u8212- perhaps the application of {\i bittul} in the {\i ma\u8217'asrot} case is categorically different because it operates in a {\i stringent} direction. In the {\i ma\u8217'asrot} scenario, saying that the new growth nullifies the original {\i results in greater obligation} \u8212- we must tithe the entire quantity, including the portion that was already tithed, rather than only the increment. This is a {\i chumra}. But the question before us \u8212- whether the {\i heter} growth can negate the {\i issur} core and render everything permissible \u8212- would require {\i bittul} to operate in a {\i lenient} direction. It is entirely coherent to say that {\i bittul} by growth may apply when it increases stringency, while declining to apply it when doing so would produce leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Tension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The matter therefore remains unresolved. On one side stand the {\i terumah} ruling of Rabbi Chanina Treita and the {\i ma\u8217'asrot} ruling of Rabbi Yitzchak in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's name \u8212- both of which, at least initially, seem to support the operation of {\i bittul} by growth. On the other stand the {\i orlah} ruling of Rabbi Yochanan himself and the {\i kilayim} ruling of Rabbi Yonatan, both of which deny it. And the {\i ma\u8217'asrot} case, which might have served as a resolution, is undermined by the asymmetry between applying {\i bittul} for stringency versus for leniency. The question of whether {\i heter} growth can negate an original {\i issur} through the mechanism of {\i bittul} \u8212- and whether such growth is truly {\i heter} at all or itself derives the {\i assur} status of its source \u8212- remains open, to be continued.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 58\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Core Negation Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Growth Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 58, and we pick up at the bottom of 57b, in the middle of a discussion that will carry through to the end of the tractate. The question the {\i gemara} is grappling with is this: if something is forbidden and continues to grow \u8212- a plant or produce that carries a certain {\i halachic} status \u8212- can the new growth negate the forbidden core?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cases are varied. An onion that is {\i kilayim}, or a fruit that is {\i orlah}, or produce that carries the status of {\i terumah} or {\i shemittah} \u8212- in each of these situations, one can imagine a scenario in which the original forbidden core continues to grow, but the new growth itself does not share that forbidden status. Perhaps the vineyard was uprooted, so the onion is no longer growing in a {\i kilayim} context. Perhaps it is now the eighth year, and the onion continues to grow after {\i shemittah} has ended. Perhaps seeds of {\i terumah} were planted, and the produce that sprouts is not itself {\i terumah}. In all of these scenarios, the question is the same: can the new growth \u8212- which is {\i hetter} \u8212- apply the principles of {\i bittul} and nullify the original forbidden core?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} never explicitly articulates the reason one might say no, but several possibilities present themselves. One is that because the new growth derives entirely from the forbidden source, it is not sufficiently distinct or neutral to carry the nullifying power of {\i bittul}. The argument would run: the whole thing is simply a continued growth of the core \u8212- it is always defined by the core, and how can what is merely an extension of the core turn around and declare the core nonexistent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} takes this one step further and proposes an alternative framing entirely. Perhaps the question is not really about {\i bittul} at all. Perhaps what grows from a forbidden source simply takes on the status of that source. If the original was {\i terumah}, what grows from it is {\i terumah}. If the original was {\i orlah}, what grows from it is {\i orlah}. In that case, the question is not whether the new growth can nullify the core \u8212- it is a question about what {\i halachic} status attaches to the new growth in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, seems to be asking a {\i bittul} question: can the new growth have the power to negate the core? That framing shapes everything that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraic Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} has already established that this is an {\i amoraic} dispute. {\i Rabbi Yitzchak} held that the new growth can negate the core \u8212- that the permissible growth can apply {\i bittul} and render the mixture permitted. {\i Rabbi Yochanan} and {\i Rabbi Yonasan}, by contrast, held that it cannot \u8212- the core remains forbidden regardless of how much grows around it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Davar Sheyesh Matirin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now pick up at the bottom of 57b, where the {\i gemara} introduces a {\i baraita} that will anchor the discussion. {\i Rabbi Shimon} states: {\i kol davar sheyesh lo matirin} \u8212- anything that has a path to permissibility \u8212- is never subject to {\i bittul}, regardless of the quantities involved. What does he mean by a path to permissibility?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He gives four examples. First, {\i tevel} \u8212- grain from which {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} have not yet been separated. It is forbidden, but it will become permitted the moment those separations are made. Second, {\i maaser sheni} \u8212- the second tithe, which carries a special status requiring it to be eaten in {\i Yerushalayim}, but which can very naturally be redeemed onto money and the money then brought to {\i Yerushalayim}. Third, {\i hekdesh} \u8212- property consecrated to the Temple treasury. This does not refer, according to most {\i rishonim}, to an animal designated as a {\i korban}, but rather to ordinary property \u8212- one\u8217's possessions donated to the Temple. The Temple treasury has no use for the object itself; it will sell it, and the {\i kedusha} will transfer to the proceeds. Thus the consecration will naturally be lifted in the ordinary course of things. Fourth, {\i chadash} \u8212- the new grain that grows before {\i Pesach}, which is forbidden but becomes permitted once the {\i omer} is brought on the second day of {\i Pesach}. All four of these things are currently forbidden, but all four have a natural or common path to becoming permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why No Bittul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding all of these, {\i Rabbi Shimon} rules that {\i lo nasnu bahem chachamim shiur} \u8212- the rabbis did not establish a quantity threshold. These things can never be nullified in a mixture. Even the tiniest amount renders the entire mixture problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The general logic is compelling: {\i bittul} is a mechanism for situations of true unavoidability \u8212- the forbidden item fell in, and discarding the entire mixture would impose an unacceptable loss. But here there is another option. You can simply wait until the second day of {\i Pesach}. You can redeem the {\i maaser sheni}. You can separate {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} from other grain and thereby fix the status of the {\i tevel}. {\i Bittul} is a last resort; when a preferable route exists, the rabbis declined to make that last resort available. This is the principle of {\i davar sheyesh lo matirin}: {\i afilu b\u8217'elef lo batel} \u8212- even one in a thousand, even the most infinitesimal presence, is not nullified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that while {\i Rabbi Shimon} presents this principle here as a point of dispute, it ultimately becomes accepted as the undisputed {\i halachic} ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe something philosophically striking about this principle. An item whose status can actually change through some formal process turns out to be, in a sense, more fixed and permanent than an item whose identity is simply forbidden with no exit. Counterintuitively, the more flexible item \u8212- the one that {\i can} become permitted \u8212- is less susceptible to dissolution through {\i bittul}. Its very capacity for transformation through a legitimate channel closes off the back door of nullification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Path to Permissibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon} continues: {\i v\u8217'chol davar she\u8217'ein lo matirin} \u8212- anything that has no path to permissibility. Here he lists {\i terumah}, {\i trumas maaser}, {\i challah}, {\i orlah}, and {\i kilay hakerem}. None of these has any mechanism by which it ceases to be what it is. {\i Terumah} will always be {\i terumah}; {\i orlah} will always be {\i orlah} from those trees; {\i kilayim} will always retain its status. In these cases, the rabbis {\i did} establish a quantity threshold \u8212- and it is worth noting that for agricultural forbidden items, the threshold is higher than the standard sixty-to-one ratio. For {\i terumah} the ratio is one hundred to one; for {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim} it is two hundred to one. Nevertheless, once the mixture crosses that threshold, the forbidden item is {\i batel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A brief technical footnote: all of the above applies when the mixture is {\i min b\u8217'mino} \u8212- the same species mixed with the same species, apples with apples, grain with grain. If the forbidden item falls into something of a different species \u8212- {\i eino mino} \u8212- then even a {\i davar sheyesh lo matirin} would be {\i batel} at the standard sixty-to-one ratio. But all of {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's analysis pertains to the case of like mixed with like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevi\u8217'it Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rabbis respond to {\i Rabbi Shimon}: {\i v\u8217'halo shevi\u8217'it ein la matirin} \u8212- {\i shevi\u8217'it} has no path to permissibility. Once produce carries the status of the seventh year, that status never goes away. You must eat it in accordance with its sanctity; you cannot treat it casually or dispose of it; and once the time of {\i bi\u8217'ur} arrives \u8212- when that species of produce is no longer available in the fields for animals to eat \u8212- you must actually destroy it. There is no mechanism by which {\i shevi\u8217'it} produce simply loses its status. And yet, says the {\i gemara}, {\i velo nasnu bah chachamim shiur} \u8212- even {\i shevi\u8217'it} is not subject to {\i bittul}. The {\i mishnah} in {\i shevi\u8217'it} states: {\i hashevi\u8217'it oseret kol shehu beminah} \u8212- produce with {\i shevi\u8217'it} status, when mixed with its own kind, renders the entire mixture forbidden even in the most minute quantity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So this seems to disprove half of {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's principle. He said that things with no path to permissibility {\i are} subject to {\i bittul} \u8212- yet here is {\i shevi\u8217'it}, which has no path to permissibility, and it is nevertheless not {\i batel}. At most, the rabbis have only disproven one half of his statement \u8212- the half that said {\i she\u8217'ein lo matirin} is always {\i batel}. They have not touched his claim that {\i sheyesh lo matirin} is never {\i batel}. But they have shown that his framework does not map neatly onto all cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon} replies: {\i af ani lo amarti ela levi\u8217'ur} \u8212- even I would only say that {\i shevi\u8217'it} is not {\i batel} with respect to the {\i halacha} of {\i bi\u8217'ur}. But {\i aval l\u8217'achilah} \u8212- regarding the obligation of eating it \u8212- the standard rule of {\i nosein ta\u8217'am} would apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requires explanation. {\i Shevi\u8217'it} produce carries two distinct {\i halachic} dimensions. During the period while the produce is still available in the fields and animals can still eat from it, there is an obligation to eat the produce and handle it with {\i kedushat shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- one cannot simply discard it or destroy it. After that period ends and the species is no longer available in the fields, the time of {\i bi\u8217'ur} arrives, and it becomes obligatory to destroy the produce rather than eat it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is that neither of these statuses seems to constitute {\i yesh lo matirin} in any obvious sense. Neither one provides a mechanism by which the {\i shevi\u8217'it} status simply dissolves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rishonim Reframe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where the {\i rishonim} significantly reinterpret what {\i Rabbi Shimon} is saying. The {\i Ran} reads the passage as follows. {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's answer is that the rabbis who challenged him were only correct with respect to {\i bi\u8217'ur} \u8212- that is, regarding the {\i halacha} of eating the produce before {\i bi\u8217'ur} arrives. During that period, even though the produce has {\i shevi\u8217'it} status and you cannot simply flush it away, you {\i can} eat it. It is, in a sense, workable \u8212- you can operate within its parameters. And that, says the {\i Ran}, constitutes a kind of {\i yesh lo matirin}: not that the status will disappear, but that there is a permissible way to engage with the item. That is why, during the eating period, {\i bittul} does not apply \u8212- since just as with classic {\i yesh lo matirin}, you should simply eat it in the proper manner rather than dissolve it into a mixture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the time of {\i bi\u8217'ur} arrives, however, when one is forbidden to eat the produce and obligated to destroy it, that is a genuine {\i ein lo matirin} situation \u8212- there is no permissible path. In that case, {\i Rabbi Shimon} would actually agree that {\i bittul} could apply, and the standard rule of {\i nosein ta\u8217'am} would govern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a notably flexible deployment of the concept of {\i davar sheyesh lo matirin}. Elsewhere one can find a similar approach: the {\i Rif} in a complicated passage discusses bread baked in an oven with meat, and invokes the category of {\i davar sheyesh lo matirin} \u8212- not in the sense that the bread will stop being {\i fleishig}, but in the sense that one can perfectly well eat it with {\i fleishig} food. The principle is being applied wherever there exists a workable path within the item\u8217's status, not only where the status itself will eventually dissolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the text: {\i amru lo: velo shevi\u8217'it ein lo matirin, detnan: hashevi\u8217'it oseret kol shehu beminah. Amar lei: af ani lo amarti ela levi\u8217'ur} \u8212- which the {\i rishonim} understand to mean: I would only say it is not {\i batel} with respect to the period in which you must eat it, because there you can simply eat it ({\i yesh lo matirin}). {\i Aval achilah benosen ta\u8217'am} \u8212- but after {\i bi\u8217'ur}, when eating is forbidden and destruction is required, in that case it follows the standard rule of {\i nosein ta\u8217'am} and would be {\i batel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} makes explicit what the words seem to invert: {\i af heim lo amru she\u8217'hashevi\u8217'it oseret b\u8217'chol shehu b\u8217'minah ella l\u8217'inyan she\u8217'tzarich le\u8217'echol hata\u8217'aroves kodem habi\u8217'ur} \u8212- they only said that {\i shevi\u8217'it} is not {\i batel} regarding the requirement to eat the mixture before {\i bi\u8217'ur}. {\i V\u8217'keivan she\u8217'efshar le\u8217'ochla, havei lei k\u8217'davar sheyesh lo matirin} \u8212- since one can eat it, it functions like {\i yesh lo matirin}, and therefore {\i bittul} does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To sharpen the logic: {\i bittul} functions by declaring the forbidden component nonexistent, effectively eliminating the need to comply with its {\i halachic} demands. But if the item can simply be eaten \u8212- if compliance is easy and available \u8212- then {\i bittul} does not dissolve the status; instead, the mixture must be treated with {\i kedushat shevi\u8217'it}, handled properly, and eaten before {\i bi\u8217'ur}. It cannot be discarded as if the {\i shevi\u8217'it} component were not there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the {\i gemara}\u8217's engagement with {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's principle is complete, and we turn to the central question of whether new growth can negate a forbidden core.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Onion Growth Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onion Color Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a {\i mishnah} to test the principle. {\i Betzalim sheyardu aleihem geshamim} \u8212- onions upon which rain fell. The scenario is as follows: onions that were growing in the sixth year continue into the seventh year. The question is whether any significant growth occurred during {\i shemittah} or whether the onion essentially completed its growth in the sixth year. {\i Im hayu alim shelahem shchorim, assurim} \u8212- if the leaves were dark or black-colored, the onion is forbidden, indicating that enough growth occurred during {\i shemittah} to give it {\i shevi\u8217'it} status. {\i Horiku, mutarim} \u8212- if they turned bright green, the onion is permitted, indicating that the growth had essentially concluded in the sixth year, or that any additional growth was driven by rainfall rather than by the {\i shemittah} ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The precise mechanism by which leaf color indicates the nature of growth is not fully transparent, and the {\i rishonim} offer various readings. The {\i mishnah} also works in the reverse direction \u8212- from the seventh year into the eighth \u8212- and the same indicators apply with opposite consequences: what was permitted in the sixth-to-seventh scenario is analogous to what would be forbidden in the seventh-to-eighth scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leaf Strength Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Chanina ben Antignos} offers an alternative diagnostic criterion: {\i im yecholim lehitaresh ba\u8217'alim shelahem, assurim} \u8212- if the leaves are strong enough that one could pull the onion up by them, the onion is forbidden, as this demonstrates that significant growth occurred during {\i shemittah} and that the leaves are robustly connected in a way that reflects continued growth during the seventh year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Growth Negates Core\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws an inference from the {\i mishnah}. In the case of an onion growing from the seventh year into the eighth: if the color of the leaves or their strength indicates that growth continued into the eighth year, the onion becomes {\i permitted}. But wait \u8212- the core of the onion grew during {\i shemittah}. How is it permitted? Does this not show that {\i gidulei heter ma\u8217'alin et ha\u8217'issur} \u8212- permissible new growth negates the forbidden core? This would appear to vindicate {\i Rabbi Yitzchak}\u8217's lenient position and contradict {\i Rabbi Yochanan} and {\i Rabbi Yonasan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ground-Up Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects: {\i vedilma bimduchanim} \u8212- perhaps the case of the {\i mishnah} involves onions that have been ground up. In other words, perhaps once the onion is processed into a ground form, the usual logic of {\i bittul} applies by virtue of the mixture\u8217's processed state, and this is not a case where the new growth in its whole form is negating the original whole core. It is not immediately obvious why being ground up changes the equation, but the {\i gemara}\u8217's suggestion seems to be that when the onion retains its original form \u8212- one continuous object, growing from the very core that was forbidden \u8212- it is difficult to say that the same onion that was forbidden is now permitted simply because it is larger. But if it has been ground up and turned into a processed mixture, then ordinary {\i bittul} ratios can apply without the conceptual difficulty of claiming that the same object has negated itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Samaritan Weeding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Worker Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} tries again from a {\i baraita}: {\i hamnakeish im hakuti bechasyos} \u8212- someone who is weeding together with a Samaritan among leafy vegetables. The underlying assumption is that the Samaritan does not observe the laws of {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros}, and therefore the produce he planted likely did not have those separations taken from it. Nevertheless, workers are permitted under certain conditions to snack on the produce they are working with \u8212- {\i achilat arai}. The ruling is that one may eat in that manner, but if one receives a portion of the vegetables as wages, those must be treated as definite {\i tevel} and separated accordingly, since we have no reason to assume the Samaritan separated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon ben Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} introduces the case directly relevant to our question. {\i Im Yisrael chashud al hashevi\u8217'it} \u8212- if instead of a Samaritan, one is working with a Jew who is suspect of not observing {\i shemittah} properly, such that the seeds or bulbs he planted may have been {\i shemittah} produce \u8212- {\i lemotzei shevi\u8217'it mutar} \u8212- once {\i shemittah} is over and it is the eighth year, one may still eat freely from the vegetables growing in his field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permits Mixture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws the inference: {\i lemimra degidulei heter ma\u8217'alin et ha\u8217'issur} \u8212- this seems to demonstrate conclusively that permissible new growth negates the forbidden core. Here you are, eating from onions in the eighth year, knowing that the core of those onions may have been {\i shemittah} produce. And yet it is permitted. The new growth in the eighth year appears to negate the original {\i shemittah} core.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wheat Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts a first deflection: {\i vedilma bedavar shezaro kalah} \u8212- perhaps we are not dealing with onions or similar vegetables at all. Perhaps the {\i baraita} concerns wheat or another grain in which the original seed disintegrates into the ground. If so, there is no surviving \u8220"core\u8221" to speak of \u8212- what grows is entirely new, and there is no issue of the new growth negating a present forbidden core, because the forbidden core has long dissolved and is gone. The question of {\i gidulei heter ma\u8217'alin et ha\u8217'issur} only arises when the original core remains \u8212- something like an onion, where the bulb is still there and continues to grow. In the case of wheat, the original kernel is gone, and what grows is simply new produce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vegetables Confirmation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this evasion: {\i ha tanya \u8212- elu hein chasayos: kegon haluf vehashum vehabetzalim} \u8212- the {\i baraita} itself defines what {\i chasayos} are: the {\i luf} (some type of onion-family vegetable, possibly a leek), garlic, and onions. These are all vegetables that do not dissolve when planted; the original bulb remains and continues to grow. So we are definitively in the category of {\i ayin zaro kalah} \u8212- the seed does not disintegrate \u8212- and the original core is present. The {\i baraita}\u8217's permissive ruling cannot be explained away by appealing to the disintegration of the original.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ground Again\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} tries the earlier deflection: {\i vedilma bimduchanim} \u8212- perhaps the scenario involves ground-up, processed vegetables rather than whole ones, and that is why {\i bittul} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Weeding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} refutes this as well: {\i hamnakeish katani} \u8212- the {\i baraita} explicitly describes someone who is {\i weeding} in the field. He is not eating from processed or ground vegetables; he is eating directly from vegetables growing in the ground as he works. There is no way to read a scenario of processed food into a description of field weeding. The {\i baraita} thus seems to present an unambiguous case: one eats from whole vegetables growing in the field, the core of which may have been {\i shemittah} produce, and it is nevertheless permitted. This appears to decisively demonstrate that the new growth negates the core.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shemittah Unique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shanya Shemittah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having apparently established a clear proof that {\i gidulei heter ma\u8217'alin et ha\u8217'issur}, the {\i gemara} turns to {\i Rabbi Yitzchak} for the resolution: {\i shanya shevi\u8217'it ho\u8217'il v\u8217'issurah al yedei karka bitulah nami al yedei karka} \u8212- {\i shemittah} is different. Since its prohibition operates by virtue of the land, its nullification likewise operates by virtue of the land. This is the {\i gemara}\u8217's decisive answer. What grows from {\i shemittah} produce in the eighth year can negate the {\i shemittah} core \u8212- but only because {\i shemittah} is a unique category. This does not establish a general principle that new growth negates a forbidden core in other domains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Year-Based Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does it mean that {\i shemittah}\u8217's prohibition operates through the land? The insight is that {\i shemittah} asks a fundamentally different question than other forbidden categories. When one encounters an {\i orlah} fruit, the question is: is this object forbidden? The focus is on the fruit as an object \u8212- it carries the {\i orlah} status and that is what defines it. When one encounters an onion that may or may not have grown during {\i shemittah}, the question one naturally asks is: {\i which year is this from?} It is a year-based, land-based question. Did this grow from the land of the seventh year or the land of the eighth year?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because the question is framed that way, one does not necessarily subdivide the onion itself and ask: how many grams grew in year seven versus how many grams grew in year eight, and does the latter quantity cross the threshold needed to nullify the former? That would be a standard {\i bittul} analysis applied to components of a single object. Instead, since {\i shemittah} is defined by which year the land belongs to, one can make a global assessment: if the majority of this onion grew in the eighth year, it is an eighth-year onion, and that single designation applies to the whole. The year-based framing allows for a holistic determination of status in a way that other {\i issurim} do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, an {\i orlah} fruit cannot be characterized holistically as \u8220"belonging to\u8221" a permitted year simply because more of it grew after the three-year {\i orlah} period. The analysis there focuses on the object and its components, not on a year-based label.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Active/Passive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} offers a different approach to understanding {\i shanya shevi\u8217'it}. Looking back at the cases cited by {\i Rabbi Yochanan} and {\i Rabbi Yonasan} \u8212- grafting a branch from an {\i orlah} tree onto a non-{\i orlah} tree, and uprooting the vineyard in which a {\i kilayim} onion was growing \u8212- {\i Tosafos} observes that in both of those cases, a person actively intervened to create the {\i hetter}. The {\i orlah} status was dissolved by the act of grafting; the {\i kilayim} status was dissolved by the act of uprooting the vineyard. This active human intervention, says {\i Tosafos}, is problematic: it resembles {\i mevatlin issur l\u8217'chatchila} \u8212- the prohibition against deliberately arranging for a forbidden item to be nullified. Since the person went out and actively did something to convert the {\i issur} into {\i hetter}, we do not credit the resulting {\i bittul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i shemittah} case, by contrast, nothing was done. The onion simply continued to grow naturally after {\i shemittah} ended. There was no deliberate manipulation to engineer permissibility. Therefore, the natural growth in the eighth year is credited and can define the onion\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading is not the straightforward {\i pshat} of the {\i gemara}, which grounds the distinction in the land-focused nature of {\i shemittah} rather than in the active-versus-passive distinction. But {\i Tosafos}\u8217's reading is worth noting as an alternative conceptual framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maaser Countercase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i shemittah} is exceptional because its prohibition focuses on the land, the {\i gemara} immediately raises a challenge: {\i harei maaser de\u8217'issura al yedei karka} \u8212- {\i maaser} is also a prohibition that arises from the land. Grain that grows from the land becomes obligated in {\i maaser} by virtue of having grown from that land. If the land-focus of {\i shemittah} is what allows the new growth to negate the core, then by the same logic, new growth of {\i maaser} grain should negate the original untithed core. But the {\i gemara} is about to show that it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Growth Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i litra maaser tevel} \u8212- a measure of {\i maaser} grain that is {\i tevel}. How does one have {\i maaser} grain that is {\i tevel}? When a {\i levi} receives his {\i maaser} from the {\i Yisrael}, the {\i levi} must in turn separate {\i trumas maaser} \u8212- a tenth of that portion \u8212- and give it to a {\i kohen}. If he fails to do so, his {\i maaser} grain retains the status of {\i tevel}: it is forbidden to eat. If this {\i levi} takes his untithed {\i maaser} grain \u8212- or onion bulbs, or whatever the produce may be \u8212- and plants them in the ground before separating {\i trumas maaser}, and they then grow tenfold, we have a case with a clear forbidden core surrounded by substantial new growth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Remains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling of the {\i baraita} is: {\i chayaves bemaaser uvishvi\u8217'it} \u8212- the new produce is fully obligated in {\i maaser}, and if the year is a {\i shemittah} year it carries {\i shevi\u8217'it} status, just like any produce that grows that year. So far this is unremarkable. But then: {\i ve\u8217'osah litra} \u8212- what of the original single unit that was {\i tevel}? It has not been negated. One must go and separate {\i trumas maaser} for that original portion from other, unambiguous {\i maaser} grain elsewhere. One cannot take the {\i trumas maaser} from the mixture itself \u8212- everything is commingled and indistinguishable \u8212- so one separates from a separate known source on behalf of that original unit. The original unit retains its status and requires independent rectification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is striking in light of what was just demonstrated about {\i shemittah}. There we said the new growth negates the core. Here we are saying it does not. And {\i maaser} grain also grows from the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Digun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i maaser digun hu dekagrim lah} \u8212- the defining moment of {\i maaser} obligation is not the land itself but the {\i digun}, the processing. Grain becomes fully obligated in {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} only after it has been threshed, winnowed, piled, and the pile smoothed over. That final act of processing is the trigger. Therefore, even though {\i maaser} grain grows from the land, the essential focus of {\i maaser} is not \u8220"which land did this come from\u8221" or \u8220"which year did this grow from\u8221" \u8212- it is \u8220"has this grain been processed in the manner that triggers the obligation?\u8221" Because the obligation is ultimately keyed to human processing rather than to the identity of the land or the year, the analysis of the {\i maaser} grain focuses on the grain as an object with its own processing history. And when the analysis focuses on the object rather than on the land-year question, the result is the same as with {\i terumah} or {\i orlah}: there is an original component with a problematic status, and new growth around it does not negate that original core.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 General Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pulling back from the details, the {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion can be stated as follows: the general rule is that new growth does not negate the original forbidden core. {\i Shemittah} is an exception to this general rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Shemittah Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shemittah} is exceptional because it poses a global, land-based, year-based question. When determining whether produce is {\i shevi\u8217'it}, one asks: which year is this from? If the majority grew in the eighth year, the holistic answer is that it is an eighth-year product. The framework is not about the components of the object and their individual {\i halachic} histories, but about a single characterization of the produce as a whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Others Fail\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All other categories \u8212- {\i terumah}, {\i orlah}, {\i kilayim}, and even {\i maaser} despite its connection to the land \u8212- ultimately require analysis that focuses on the object itself and on human processing components. For {\i maaser}, the trigger is {\i digun}, not the land. For {\i terumah} and {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim}, the question is whether a particular component carries a particular status. In none of these cases can one simply step back and ask \u8220"which category does this whole thing belong to?\u8221" \u8212- because the analysis invariably requires accounting for the original core as a distinct component. Therefore, in all of these cases, the original core is never negated by new growth, and one must always address it independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The long discussion that began at the bottom of 57b will be concluded in tomorrow\u8217's {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 59\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro: Growth & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sugya Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya occupying our attention spans the entirety of Nedarim 59a, and it addresses a question that sits at the intersection of several {\i issurim}: if an object possesses a {\i halachic} status \u8212- {\i terumah}, {\i orlah}, {\i shmita}, or an object rendered forbidden through a {\i neder} \u8212- and it continues to grow, does the additional growth alter or nullify the original status?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The particular case that has anchored the discussion is that of an onion. An onion begins with a forbidden status and then continues to grow. The original bulb is still present \u8212- it has not disintegrated \u8212- but there is now significantly more onion than there was originally. The {\i gemara} proceeds from the assumption that the new growth itself would be {\i heter}, since it grew after the original prohibition was in place. But since the original object has simply expanded rather than been replaced, the question is whether the laws of {\i bittul} \u8212- nullification \u8212- can even apply here. Is there any mechanism by which the expanded new growth could nullify the original forbidden portion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the question, consider that the {\i gemara} had already discussed the case of a {\i shmita} onion that continues to grow into the eighth year. The tentative conclusion reached in the previous session was that, at least by {\i shmita}, the continued growth during the eighth year can redefine the onion\u8217's identity, since the relevant question for {\i shmita} purposes is whether this is a seventh-year or an eighth-year onion. If the majority of its mass grew in the eighth year, it may be classified as an eighth-year onion. But whether this logic applies more broadly \u8212- to {\i orlah}, {\i terumah}, or {\i nedarim} \u8212- remained an open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam vs.\u160 ?Ochel/Toeim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a {\i baraita} cited by Rami bar Chama that introduces us to the {\i mishnah} at the heart of the tractate. If someone declares {\i konam peiros ha-eilu alai} \u8212- \u8220"these fruits are forbidden to me as a {\i korban}\u8221" \u8212- or formulates the prohibition in a generic, object-centered way, then not only are the fruits themselves forbidden but so too is any money exchanged for them ({\i chilofeihem}) and anything that grows from them ({\i giduleihem}). The {\i konam} formulation is a sweeping speech act that imparts a broad {\i halachic} status to the object and everything flowing from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if one uses a more restricted formulation \u8212- {\i she-ani ochel} or {\i she-ani toeim}, \u8220"that I will not eat\u8221" or \u8220"that I will not taste\u8221" \u8212- the prohibition is framed around a specific act rather than the object itself. In that case, the {\i mishnah} rules that {\i chilofeihem u-giduleihem mutarin}: proceeds and new growth are permitted. The reasoning is that eating or tasting the proceeds or the new grain is not the same act as eating or tasting the original fruit. These more limited formulations do not extend to what grows in the fruit\u8217's place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a critical qualification: {\i be-davar she-zaro kalah} \u8212- this leniency applies only where the original seed disintegrates in the ground. If one plants wheat and the original wheat seed ceases to exist as such, then the new wheat that grows is entirely new and unconnected to the original. But {\i aval be-davar she-ein zaro kalah} \u8212- if the original seed does not disintegrate, as in the case of the onion \u8212- then {\i afilu gidulei gidulim assurim}: even what grows from what has already grown remains forbidden, because the original forbidden entity is still physically present within the expanded object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bittul Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling of the {\i mishnah} raises a sharp conceptual question. If the onion has grown dramatically \u8212- if the original tiny bulb has expanded tenfold \u8212- why does {\i bittul} not apply? Normally, if a small quantity of a forbidden substance falls into a much larger quantity of permitted substance, the forbidden portion is nullified within the majority. Why, then, does the large new growth of the onion not nullify the small original bulb? One thing cannot be {\i mevatel} itself \u8212- the continued growth of the very same object cannot nullify the origin of that growth \u8212- and the {\i mishnah} seems to confirm this: the original onion remains forbidden regardless of how much it has grown. This appears to establish that {\i bittul} does not apply to a continuously growing object.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Abba: Matirin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nedarim & Bittul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records the response of Rav Abba. He concedes the point: {\i bittul} indeed does not apply in the case of the {\i mishnah}. But his reason is not that there is something uniquely impossible about growth-based {\i bittul} as a general matter. Rather, he argues, {\i shani konamot} \u8212- {\i nedarim} are categorically different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinctive feature of a {\i neder} is that it always has a pathway to becoming permissible: {\i ho-il ve-it bei mishala alai} \u8212- one can always approach a {\i chacham} and request release from the vow. Because this avenue of release always exists, a {\i neder}-based prohibition is classified as {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}, a thing that has a natural means of becoming permissible. And the established principle is that {\i ein batel be-rov} \u8212- {\i bittul} does not apply to such a category. Rather than rely on {\i bittul}, the proper course is to pursue the permissible avenue directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing significantly clarifies the conceptual picture. The reason {\i bittul} does not apply to the {\i neder}-onion case has nothing to do with the mechanics of growth or the fact that the forbidden and permitted portions are parts of the same onion. Even if one were to take a small piece of the forbidden onion and mix it into an entirely separate pile of onions from the market, {\i bittul} would still not apply \u8212- because the prohibition derives from a {\i neder}, and a {\i neder} is a {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Davar She\u8217'Yesh Lo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} is thus not about whether the forbidden item is growing or static, nor about whether there is any physical commingling. It is a categorical rule: whenever a prohibition can be removed through a legitimate {\i halachic} mechanism, the law declines to apply {\i bittul} and instead insists that the person utilize the available mechanism. The logic is that {\i bittul} is a tool of last resort for truly intractable prohibitions; where a doorway out exists, one should walk through it rather than rely on nullification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One technical refinement deserves mention: the principle that a {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} is not {\i batel} applies in the context of {\i min be-mino} \u8212- a like-with-like mixture. In {\i min she-eino mino} mixtures, the rules differ. But the essential conceptual point stands: the {\i neder}\u8217's status as reversible is what disqualifies it from the ordinary operation of {\i bittul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises an objection. {\i Ve-harei terumah} \u8212- consider {\i terumah}. Just as a {\i neder} can in principle be reversed by approaching a {\i chacham} who may find grounds to annul it retroactively, so too can {\i terumah} be reversed: if a {\i chacham} determines that the original designation was made in error or without proper intent, the speech act can be undone and the grain retroactively becomes {\i tevel}. Yet we know that {\i terumah} is subject to {\i bittul} \u8212- there is a well-established rule that {\i terumah} that falls into a mixture of {\i chullin} is nullified if the ratio is one part in a hundred. Why does {\i terumah} not qualify as a {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} and thereby escape {\i bittul}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Speech Acts & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Power of Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand both the question and its answer, one must appreciate the deeper {\i halachic} structure shared by {\i nedarim} and {\i terumah}. Both prohibitions are created by a speech act. The prototype of a {\i neder} is rooted in the world of {\i korbanot}: one says of one\u8217's animal \u8220"this is a {\i korban},\u8221" and the speech act transforms the animal\u8217's {\i halachic} status. The {\i konam} formulation mirrors this: one says \u8220"this fruit is forbidden to me like a {\i korban},\u8221" and the speech act renders it so. {\i Terumah} operates the same way \u8212- one does not need to physically separate a portion of grain; one simply declares \u8220"this grain is {\i terumah},\u8221" and the designation is effected through speech alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because both {\i nedarim} and {\i terumah} are rooted in speech, both are in principle reversible by undoing the speech act. A {\i chacham} can examine the intent and circumstances of the original declaration and, finding grounds, annul it \u8212- in effect, determining that the speech act never validly took effect. This is why both seem to qualify as {\i yesh lo matirin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theology & Derash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is something profound in this structure. The speech act is a non-physical act of remarkable power: it can create an entire {\i halachic} world, rendering objects sacred, forbidden, or obligated. Yet precisely because it is rooted in speech \u8212- which is in turn rooted in will and thought \u8212- it carries within it the possibility of being undone. A {\i chacham} who releases a vow is not, so to speak, overpowering the original declaration; rather, he is determining that the person\u8217's deepest will was never truly aligned with the words they spoke. This is the inner logic of {\i hataras nedarim}: the vow is not broken but revealed to have been imperfect from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe a certain theological resonance here. The power of divine speech to create the world, and the rabbinic enterprise of {\i Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh} as the interpretive unpacking of what divine speech truly means, parallel in some ways the structure of human vows and their release. Just as the {\i beis din} of scholars interprets what God\u8217's word actually requires, so the {\i chacham} interprets what the person\u8217's word actually meant. The analogy is not precise, but the structural similarity is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Batel Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i terumah} can also be reversed through a speech-act annulment, why is {\i terumah} not treated as a {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}? The {\i gemara}\u8217's first attempt at resolution is to restrict the scope of the question: perhaps {\i bittul} only applies to {\i terumah} that has already been given to a {\i kohen}. Once {\i terumah} has been transferred into the physical possession of a {\i kohen}, the speech act has had a concrete real-world effect: another person now owns the object and has relied on it. At that point, one cannot reverse the designation by approaching a {\i chacham} \u8212- the {\i kohen} would be deprived of property he has legitimately received. Since reversal is no longer possible, {\i terumah} in the hands of a {\i kohen} is no longer a {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}, and {\i bittul} can apply.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Pre-Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} tests this proposed limitation by examining the continuation of the {\i mishnah} it cited. The {\i mishnah} discusses {\i se\u8217'ah terumah tme\u8217'iah} \u8212- a unit of impure {\i terumah} \u8212- that fell into a mixture of fewer than a hundred units of {\i chullin}, which is insufficient for {\i bittul} since {\i terumah} requires a ratio of one in a hundred. The {\i mishnah} says: {\i tirakev} \u8212- let it rot. By implication, the {\i gemara} infers, if it had fallen into a hundred units, {\i ta\u8217'aleh} \u8212- it would be nullified and the mixture would be permitted. This establishes that {\i bittul} does apply to {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i mishnah}\u8217's continuation \u8212- {\i im hayeta tehorah}, the case where pure {\i terumah} became mixed \u8212- instructs that such a mixture should be sold to a {\i kohen}. This language, the {\i gemara} notes, indicates clearly that the {\i terumah} has not yet been given to a {\i kohen}; it is still in the possession of the Israelite who separated it. His only recourse is to find a {\i kohen} willing to purchase the entire mixture. Yet the {\i gemara} had proposed that {\i bittul} only applies after transfer to a {\i kohen}. Here we have {\i bittul} applying to {\i terumah} that has never been given to a {\i kohen}. The proposed limitation collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Charge Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} sharpens this point by examining a fine detail in the {\i mishnah}: {\i chutz midmei otah se\u8217'ah} \u8212- when selling the mixture to a {\i kohen}, the Israelite may charge for the ninety-nine units of {\i chullin} but may not charge for the one unit of {\i terumah} that is mixed in. He has an obligation to give the {\i kohen} the {\i terumah} portion; he is not entitled to sell it, only to give it. This rule confirms that the {\i terumah} was never previously given to a {\i kohen}, since if it had been received as a gift from a {\i kohen} grandfather \u8212- the scenario the {\i gemara} had been exploring \u8212- the Israelite owner would have full rights to resell it. The fact that he cannot charge for it proves we are dealing with {\i terumah} that originated with the Israelite himself and has never changed hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion is that {\i bittul} demonstrably applies to {\i terumah} that is still in the Israelite\u8217's possession and is entirely reversible. The simple restriction \u8212- that {\i bittul} only applies after transfer \u8212- fails. A different explanation is needed for why {\i terumah} is not a {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin} despite the theoretical possibility of its reversal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Natan Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Natan Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now arrives at the definitive answer. {\i Bishlama konamot} \u8212- for {\i nedarim}, it makes sense that they are classified as {\i yesh lo matirin} and therefore not subject to {\i bittul}. The reason is stated explicitly: {\i mitzvah le\u8217'itshulei aleihu} \u8212- there is a positive obligation to seek release from a vow. And the source for this obligation is Rabbi Natan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bamah Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan states: {\i kol ha-noder ke\u8217'ilu bana bamah} \u8212- anyone who makes a vow is as though he built a {\i bamah}, a private altar. The {\i bamah} is a resonant image. In an era when the {\i Beit HaMikdash} stands, constructing a private altar \u8212- even with sincere devotion to God \u8212- is a transgression. The worshipper may believe he is enhancing his religious experience; he is not. He is setting himself up as a private religious authority, creating his own sacred space outside the framework of communal, institutionalized worship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i neder} functions similarly. The person who makes a vow imagines he is adding to the religious world, expanding the domain of the sacred, mimicking God\u8217's capacity to render things forbidden \u8212- {\i chazir}, {\i basar be-chalav} \u8212- by adding his own categories of prohibition. This impulse, however sincere, represents a form of private religion, a {\i bamah} of the soul. The communal religious experience is not meant to be supplemented by each individual\u8217's personalized system of prohibitions and obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Fulfillment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan continues with a further escalation: {\i veha-mekayemo} \u8212- one who not only makes a vow but then actually fulfills it, rather than seeking release \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'ilu hikriv alav umaktir aleha}: it is as though he has offered a sacrifice on that private altar and burnt incense upon it. Building the {\i bamah} is problematic; actually worshipping at it is worse. The person had an opportunity \u8212- indeed, an obligation \u8212- to dismantle the {\i bamah} by going to a {\i chacham} and being released from the vow. Having failed to do so and having instead lived out the {\i neder}, he has taken the private religious act to its full troubling conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Critique Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkably sharp critique, and it is striking that the {\i gemara} returns to it repeatedly throughout {\i Masechet Nedarim}. The {\i masechet} does not offer much by way of a counterweight \u8212- a robust positive account of why {\i nedarim} might be a worthy religious practice \u8212- and the dominant tone, particularly as expressed through Rabbi Natan, is one of deep skepticism toward the entire enterprise of vow-making.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This makes best sense against the historical background in which {\i nedarim} were an extraordinarily common practice in the Greco-Roman world of late antiquity. Saul Lieberman documented extensively how vow-making had become trivialized and widespread, not only within the Jewish community but throughout the broader culture. People made vows loosely and casually, without appreciating their weight. What the rabbis appear to be doing in this tractate \u8212- through both the intricate legal details they develop and through the explicit critique voiced by Rabbi Natan \u8212- is to reassert control over this phenomenon: to make vow-making a serious, weighty act that people would think carefully before undertaking, and ideally to discourage the practice altogether.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mitzvah to Reverse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvah Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the legal question: since there is a {\i mitzvah} to seek release from a {\i neder}, the status of {\i neder}-based prohibition is categorically different from other {\i issurim}. The existence of a {\i mitzvah} to undo the {\i neder} means that the prohibition is, in a sense, already pointing toward its own dissolution. This is why it qualifies as a genuine {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}: not merely that reversal is theoretically possible, but that reversal is what one is {\i supposed} to do. Rather than relying on {\i bittul} to navigate the practical consequences of the {\i neder}, one should simply go to a {\i chacham}, have the vow released, and the problem resolves itself entirely. That is the {\i halachically} preferred path, and because it exists, the tool of {\i bittul} is withheld.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, {\i terumah} carries no corresponding obligation to reverse it. Quite the opposite: separating {\i terumah} and giving it to a {\i kohen} is itself a {\i mitzvah}. The fact that reversal is theoretically possible does not make {\i terumah} a {\i davar she-yesh lo matirin}, because the entire thrust of the {\i mitzvah} points toward preserving and fulfilling the {\i terumah} status, not undoing it. Accordingly, {\i terumah} is subject to ordinary {\i bittul}, while {\i nedarim} are not. The distinction is not about the mechanics of reversibility but about the normative direction the {\i halacha} points: toward reversal in the case of {\i nedarim}, toward fulfillment in the case of {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yochanan Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Replanted Tithes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a statement of Rabbi Yochanan that was cited earlier in the sugya and examines it directly. Rabbi Yochanan states: {\i litra betzalim she-tikna ve-zara} \u8212- if one had a {\i litra} of onions from which {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot} had already been taken, and one then planted those onions, the subsequent produce is subject to {\i ma\u8217'aser} based on the total quantity \u8212- {\i mit\u8217'aseret lefi kulah}. One does not subtract the original unit from the calculation on the grounds that it had already been tithed; the entire yield, including the original unit, is treated as new {\i tevel} produce subject to a fresh round of {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking ruling, and it raises the fundamental question about {\i bittul} in a new register. Evidently, when one actively plants already-tithed grain and it grows into a larger yield, the original unit\u8217's previously-fixed status is overridden: it becomes subsumed into the new {\i tevel} identity of the entire crop, and one must tithe from the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chisda Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda\u8217's reaction to this teaching is memorable in its directness: {\i man tsayeis lach u-le-Rabbi Yochanan rabach?} \u8212- who would listen to you or your teacher Rabbi Yochanan in saying such a thing? His objection is: {\i heter she-bahen le-heichan halach?} \u8212- where did the {\i heter} portion go? That original unit was already tithed; it had {\i heter} status. Where did that status disappear to?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the surface, this might seem like an ordinary {\i bittul} question \u8212- after all, {\i bittul} is precisely the mechanism by which items lose their distinct {\i halachic} status within a mixture. But Rav Chisda\u8217's question is more probing than that. {\i Bittul} works by erasing a problematic {\i halachic} status \u8212- a piece of {\i chazir} falls into a pot and its {\i issur} status is nullified by the majority. But here the dynamic runs in reverse: the original unit had {\i heter} status, and apparently, through the process of growth within the new crop, that {\i heter} status has been transformed into {\i issur} \u8212- the unit is now being treated as {\i tevel}, requiring a new round of tithing. Can {\i bittul} work not merely to erase {\i issur} but to impart a new {\i issur} status onto something that was {\i heter}? That is the genuinely counterintuitive question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bittul Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} engages this conceptual difficulty directly. He explains that the ordinary mechanism of {\i bittul} works as follows: when forbidden material is present in a mixture in a quantity too small to be tasted, the {\i issur} status of that forbidden material becomes inoperative, since {\i halachic} prohibition is tied to the presence of identifiable taste or substance. The physical material may still be there \u8212- the piece of {\i chazir} has not literally vanished \u8212- but because it is undetectable, it ceases to exert its {\i issur} status. The result is that its status is erased.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the standard growth case, the Ran frames the argument similarly: when the new growth of an onion dramatically exceeds the original quantity, the original forbidden portion is no longer detectable in the way that would warrant a distinct prohibition. The taste of the original {\i issur} is overwhelmed. Hence the new, larger onion may be permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transform Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this analysis, the Ran continues, creates a difficulty for Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling. When Rabbi Yochanan says that the whole expanded crop is subject to {\i ma\u8217'aser} including the original tithed unit, he is not erasing an {\i issur} \u8212- he is taking a unit that was {\i heter} and treating it as {\i tevel}. The {\i heter} status is being negated and replaced with a new obligatory status. This is not how {\i bittul} standardly operates. As the {\i gemara} frames it more simply: since when does {\i bittul} make {\i heter} into {\i issur}? {\i Bittul} removes {\i issur} \u8212- it does not create it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezuzah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the {\i acharonim} poses a related question that illuminates this conceptual point. If nine {\i mezuzot} written with proper intent are mixed with one {\i mezuzah} written without proper intent, does the one defective {\i mezuzah} become {\i kasher} through {\i bittul}? The intuitive answer is no \u8212- {\i bittul} does not work to impart a positive {\i halachic} status onto an object. It is a mechanism for eliminating {\i issur}, not for conferring {\i heter} or {\i kashrut}. This is the same structural question raised by Rav Chisda: can {\i bittul} go in the direction of imposing a status, rather than merely removing one?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Onion Growth Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six-to-Seven Year\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i mishnah} about the onion that grew from the sixth year into the seventh year of the {\i shmitta} cycle \u8212- what has been called throughout the {\i sugyya} the paradigmatic case. The {\i mishnah} addresses onions that began growing in the sixth year, continued through the transition into the seventh year, and were still in the ground during {\i shmitta}. The question is: what {\i halachic} status does the resulting onion bear?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Black Leaves Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} provides an observable criterion: {\i im hayu alin shelahen shchorin} \u8212- if the leaves of the onion are black, {\i assurin} \u8212- the onion is forbidden. {\i Horiku} \u8212- if the leaves have turned green, {\i mutarin} \u8212- it is permitted. The blackness or greenness of the leaves serves as an evidentiary indicator of when the decisive growth occurred. Black leaves suggest that the onion\u8217's significant growth happened during the {\i shmitta} year, while green leaves suggest it remained essentially a sixth-year product.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: if the leaves are black and the onion is therefore {\i assur}, why is the {\i whole} onion forbidden? Surely some part of the onion grew in the sixth year, when there was no {\i issur}. One would expect only the portion that grew during the seventh year to be forbidden. Why does the {\i mishnah} speak of the onion as simply {\i assur}, apparently as a whole?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This language raises the very question about {\i bittul} in reverse: if the larger seventh-year portion renders the entire onion forbidden, it would appear that the seventh-year {\i issur} has been imposed not merely on the new growth but on the original sixth-year portion as well, transforming {\i heter} into {\i issur}. If so, the {\i gemara} would have evidence that {\i bittul} can indeed work bidirectionally \u8212- not only to erase {\i issur} but to impart it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Growth Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the question by reinterpreting the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language. {\i Mi savrat a-ikar katani} \u8212- do you think the {\i mishnah} is saying that even the original part of the onion is forbidden? {\i A-tosefet katani} \u8212- it is speaking only about the new growth. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that the onion is {\i assur} refers specifically to the portion that grew during the seventh year. The original sixth-year core retains its {\i heter} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To be sure, since the two portions are thoroughly commingled within the same onion and cannot be physically separated, one must treat the entire onion with the stringency of {\i shmitta} produce as a practical matter \u8212- {\i agav orchiah}, as the {\i gemara} puts it. But as a matter of principle, only 80% (or whatever the actual proportion of seventh-year growth) is truly {\i assur}; the 20% that was sixth-year growth remains {\i heter}. The {\i mishnah} is not asserting that the original {\i heter} portion became {\i assur}. {\i Bittul} has not imposed a new status; the two portions simply coexist within the same physical onion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Gamliel Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this reading established, the {\i gemara} examines the {\i baraita} that features Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel commenting on the same case. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel states: {\i ha-gadel be-chiyuv chayav, ha-gadel be-fator patur} \u8212- that which grew during the period of obligation ({\i shmitta}) has the status of obligation; that which grew during the period of exemption (the sixth year) is exempt from {\i shmitta} restrictions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel is explicitly articulating the principle that different portions of the onion bear different statuses. He refuses to assign a single uniform status to the onion as a whole. The sixth-year growth remains sixth-year produce; the seventh-year growth is {\i shmitta} produce. They happen to share a physical onion, but their {\i halachic} identities remain distinct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} infers that this position of Rabban Gamliel is being offered as a clarification or qualification of what the {\i tanna kamma} seemed to say. The {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's language \u8212- that the onion is {\i assur} \u8212- appeared to attribute the {\i assur} status to the entire onion, potentially suggesting that the seventh-year growth transforms the sixth-year portion as well. Rabban Gamliel contests this reading and insists that the portions must be tracked independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes that our {\i mishnah} itself reflects the position of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel \u8212- and that the {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i baraita} may also be read consistently with this approach. On this reading, there is no actual dispute: everyone agrees that only the new seventh-year growth is {\i assur}, and the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's language is simply speaking about the new portion in shorthand. The {\i mishnah} and the {\i baraita}\u8217's first opinion are thus harmonized under Rabban Gamliel\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Effort & Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Effort Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that in the onion case, where growth simply continued naturally from year six into year seven without any human intervention, the old and new portions retain distinct statuses \u8212- the {\i gemara} now asks whether this analysis is universal. Is it always true that {\i bittul} cannot transform {\i heter} into {\i issur}, or are there circumstances in which the original portion\u8217's identity genuinely changes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer the {\i gemara} develops is that the key variable is {\i tarach} \u8212- effort or exertion. Where no effort is exerted, where the object simply continues to grow as part of an uninterrupted natural process, the original portion retains its independent {\i halachic} status. Old and new remain distinct. {\i Bittul} does not impose the new status on the old portion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Effort Exerted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But where effort is exerted \u8212- where the person actively intervenes and does something new to the object \u8212- the analysis may shift. The paradigmatic case of {\i tarach} in this context is planting: one takes the produce, makes a conscious decision to plant it in the ground, and the resulting growth is qualitatively different from mere continuation. The {\i gemara} suggests that this active intervention may be sufficient to redefine the object\u8217's identity entirely, such that even the original portion\u8217's status is transformed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why Rav Chisda\u8217's question arises specifically in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's case: the Israelite {\i actively planted} the already-tithed onions. That act of planting is the {\i tarach}, the deliberate exertion, that opens the possibility of identity transformation. When transformation occurs, the entire crop \u8212- including what derived from the original unit \u8212- takes on the new {\i tevel} status, and {\i ma\u8217'aser} must be taken on the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vegetable to Seed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} articulates this principle explicitly: {\i hani mili heicha de-lo tarach le-vatulei} \u8212- the rule that the original portion retains its status and only the new growth takes on the new status applies only where no transformative effort was made, as in the onion growing naturally from year six to year seven. But {\i aval heicha de-tarach} \u8212- where effort was invested, as when one took a unit of onions and planted them in the ground, {\i asu gidulim um-vatlin ikar le-gamrei} \u8212- the new growth comes up and completely transforms the original, to the point where even the original {\i heter} turns into {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual underpinning is this: when one takes produce and plants it, one has transformed the object from {\i vegetable} to {\i seed}. It is no longer the onion one had; it is a seed from which something new grows. The act of planting confers a new identity upon the original material. And once identity is transformed, the question of what the \u8220"original\u8221" status was becomes moot \u8212- there is no \u8220"original\u8221" in the relevant sense anymore. Everything that grows belongs to the new crop with its new status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Untithed Seed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Contrast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this framework with a countercase. {\i Ve-harei litra ma\u8217'aser tevel de-ka-tarach} \u8212- consider the case of a unit of {\i ma\u8217'aser} that was {\i tevel} (meaning the {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser} had not yet been taken from it), and one went ahead and planted it. Effort was clearly exerted \u8212- planting is the paradigmatic {\i tarach}. Yet the {\i mishnah} or {\i baraita} in question rules that one does not simply treat the entire yield as new {\i tevel} and tithe from the whole. Instead, one still separately addresses the original unit\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'aser tevel} status. The original {\i tevel} status of that planted unit is not erased and replaced by the new crop\u8217's status; it persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This appears to be the same scenario \u8212- active planting of a unit that then grows \u8212- yet the outcome is the opposite of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's case. In Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's case, actively planting already-tithed grain into a larger crop causes the original\u8217's {\i heter} to be absorbed into the new crop\u8217's {\i tevel} identity. Here, actively planting {\i tevel} grain apparently does not cause the original {\i tevel} unit\u8217's status to be absorbed into the new crop in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this tension by appeal to a {\i pasuk}: {\i aser ta\u8217'aser et kol tevuat zar\u8217'echa} \u8212- you shall tithe all the produce of your seed. The critical word, the {\i gemara} notes, is {\i zar\u8217'echa} \u8212- your seed. This verse is addressed to the person planting in the normal, standard manner: {\i de-hetra zari inshi} \u8212- normal people plant {\i heter} grain, grain from which they have already taken {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot}. The verse presupposes this normal scenario and instructs that everything that grows from such planting is assessed together as new produce for purposes of {\i ma\u8217'aser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natural Planting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key insight is that the Torah\u8217's mechanism of identity transformation \u8212- whereby planting causes the original to lose its prior status and become part of the new crop \u8212- only operates when one is doing the natural, expected thing. {\i Issura lo zari inshi} \u8212- people do not normally plant {\i tevel} grain, grain that has not been fully tithed. This is an aberrant act, outside the normative framework the verse addresses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When one plants {\i tevel} grain, one is doing something unnatural in a {\i halachic} sense. And because the Torah\u8217's rule of identity transformation is designed for the normal case of planting {\i heter} grain, it does not apply here. The transformation principle is, so to speak, a privilege: one earns the ability to redefine the planted material\u8217's identity by doing what people normally do. If one is doing something unusual \u8212- planting grain that ought not to be planted in that state \u8212- the transformative mechanism does not engage, and the original material retains its prior identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Tevel Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This produces a nuanced and somewhat complex result in the {\i ma\u8217'aser tevel} case. The new produce that grows from the planted {\i tevel} grain is indeed new {\i tevel} \u8212- it is produce that has not yet had its {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot} separated, just like any other new crop. But the original unit of {\i ma\u8217'aser tevel} also continues to exist as such, with its own distinct {\i halachic} status that was never dissolved. There are thus, in a sense, two concurrent {\i tevel} statuses present in the field: the {\i tevel} of the new produce, and the unresolved {\i tevel} of the original planted unit, which must be separately addressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the resolution the {\i gemara} offers. The apparent symmetry between Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's case and the {\i ma\u8217'aser tevel} case is broken by the distinction between natural planting ({\i hetera}) and aberrant planting ({\i issura}). When one plants {\i heter} grain, identity transforms and the whole yield is new; when one plants {\i issur} grain, identity does not transform, the old status persists alongside the new, and both must be addressed independently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 60\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Onion Bitul Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chanina\u8217's Onion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We open on daf 60 by returning to a statement introduced at the bottom of 59b. The Gemara cites {\i guffa} \u8212- returning to the matter itself \u8212- and quotes: {\i tzirtam Rav Yanai betzalim shel terumah sheneta\u8217'u verabu gidulav al ikaro mutar}. Rabbi Chanina reports in the name of Rav Yanai: if one plants an onion of {\i terumah} and the subsequent growth exceeds the original amount, it is {\i mutar} \u8212- permissible \u8212- because the original {\i terumah} onion is rendered {\i batel} by the new growth. This statement has been our primary focus throughout the preceding discussion: the question of whether {\i bitul} applies to this kind of growth at all, and whether {\i bitul} can even operate in a direction that converts {\i heter} into {\i issur} \u8212- the fascinating question raised in the prior session. Here, the Gemara accepts as its working premise that the growth does indeed effect {\i bitul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knockout Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks what it calls {\i le-meimra} \u8212- \u8220"does this suggest\u8221" \u8212- that {\i gidulei heter ma\u8217'alin et ha-issur}, that growth from permissible material nullifies the original forbidden quantity? And then comes the challenge: {\i ve-hatnan} \u8212- but we have already learned in a Mishna \u8212- {\i gidulei terumah, terumah}: growth from {\i terumah} is itself {\i terumah}. If whatever grows from a {\i terumah} onion is itself considered {\i terumah}, then the entire discussion of {\i bitul} seems beside the point. Before asking whether the new growth can nullify the original, one must ask whether the new growth is itself forbidden {\i terumah} by virtue of having grown from {\i terumah} in the first place. This is, as the Gemara essentially admits, a knockout question \u8212- and it is striking that it is posed only now, at the very end of the {\i sugya}, rather than at the outset where it would have reframed the entire discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gidulei Gidulin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's response is {\i begidulei gidulin amrinan} \u8212- Rabbi Chanina\u8217's statement was not referring to the second-generation growth, but to the third generation: growth from growth. When the Mishna says {\i gidulei terumah, terumah}, it means that the second-generation product retains {\i terumah} status. Rabbi Chanina\u8217's ruling about {\i bitul} concerns only the third generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate conceptual difficulty. With a crop like wheat, the notion of generational sequence is clear: one plants a seed, the seed disintegrates, new grain grows \u8212- that is the second generation. One then plants kernels of that harvested grain and a third generation of wheat emerges. But with an onion, which is a {\i davar she-ein zaro kalah} \u8212- something whose original seed does not disintegrate \u8212- how does one define a third generation? The onion simply continues to expand and produce shoots. One possibility is that a shoot is separated and replanted, akin to the horticultural technique of layering ({\i havrachah}) known from the laws of {\i Pe\u8217'ah}. The precise agricultural mechanics are not entirely clear from the text, but the conceptual point stands: the Gemara posits that a third generation of onion growth is analyzable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then anticipates an objection: we already know that {\i gidulei gidulin chullin} \u8212- that the third generation is {\i chulin} and no longer {\i terumah}. So what is the {\i chiddush} of Rabbi Chanina\u8217's statement? The Gemara answers: {\i afilu bedavar she-ein zaro kalah} \u8212- even in the case of an item whose seed does not disintegrate, such as an onion. By wheat, the ruling that the second generation is {\i terumah} and the third is not is comprehensible: each new generation involves the complete disappearance of the prior seed and a genuinely new plant. By the onion, one might have thought that since the original material is still physically present \u8212- {\i ein zaro kalah} \u8212- all subsequent growth, however remote, retains {\i terumah} status. The {\i chiddush} is that we do not reason this way: even for the onion, the third generation is {\i chulin}, and furthermore, it can effect {\i bitul} of the original.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah vs Tevel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a further complication from a Mishna concerning {\i tevel} \u8212- grain from which {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot} have not yet been separated. The Mishna states: {\i ha-tevel gidulav mutarin} \u8212- growth from {\i tevel} is permissible. So while {\i terumah} passes its status to the second generation, {\i tevel} does not. The Mishna qualifies this: {\i gidulav mutarin bedavar she-zaro kalah} \u8212- the second generation of {\i tevel} is permissible only if it is a crop whose seed disintegrates, such as wheat. {\i Aval bedavar she-ein zaro kalah, gidulei gidulav asurin} \u8212- but if the original seed does not disintegrate, even {\i tevel}, which is more lenient than {\i terumah} in not transmitting its status to the second generation, transmits its status through to the third generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a seemingly insurmountable difficulty for the entire preceding discussion. If we are dealing with an onion \u8212- a {\i davar she-ein zaro kalah} \u8212- then: by {\i terumah}, the second generation is {\i terumah}, and by {\i tevel}, which is more lenient, even the third generation is still {\i tevel}-status. Under what circumstances could {\i bitul} ever apply? The new growth should always carry the forbidden status. This truly is, as one might ask, a question that should have appeared at the very beginning of the {\i sugya} rather than at its conclusion. The Gemara\u8217's having deferred it to this point is at once rhetorically effective and analytically puzzling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sugya Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's response reads: {\i ribuya de-rabu gidulin al ikaro mutar kamashma lan} \u8212- the novelty concerns the case where the new growth exceeds the original. This phrase is difficult to parse on its own terms, and it is precisely because of this difficulty that the {\i Rishonim} propose alternative readings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} offers a different {\i girsa} \u8212- textual reading \u8212- which he signals with {\i hachi garsinan}, and which provides the clearest resolution. The {\i Ran} reads the Gemara as follows: {\i hatam delo rabu gidula al ikaro} \u8212- when we say that the third generation of an onion remains forbidden even in a case of {\i tevel}, that ruling applies only when the new growth has not become a majority over the original. In that scenario, the onion is forbidden not because the new growth itself has acquired {\i terumah} or {\i tevel} status, but because the original forbidden piece is still physically present and has not been nullified. The new growth has not outnumbered it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval hacha gabi bitul shel terumah mishum she-rabu gidulei gidulim al ha-gidulim} \u8212- but in our case concerning {\i bitul} of {\i terumah}, the third-generation growth has become the majority over what preceded it. Under these circumstances, two conclusions follow: first, the new third-generation growth does not itself carry {\i terumah} status; second, it constitutes a majority sufficient to effect {\i bitul} of the original {\i terumah} onion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The earlier Mishna\u8217's ruling that the onion remains forbidden through the third generation in the {\i tevel} case is thus reinterpreted: it was never saying that the growth itself acquires {\i tevel} status. It was simply saying that as long as the original {\i tevel} onion persists without having been nullified by a majority, the combined mass remains forbidden. Once the growth does constitute a majority \u8212- which is precisely the case Rabbi Chanina describes \u8212- {\i bitul} can and does apply. In this way, the {\i Ran} preserves the entirety of the {\i sugya}\u8217's analysis by arguing that what grew from an onion is not itself {\i terumah}, that {\i bitul} is operative once the growth forms a majority, and that the Mishna\u8217's apparently restrictive ruling about the onion concerned only cases where no such majority existed. The conclusion: everything discussed over the preceding two days stands, circumscribed to the context of third-generation growth rather than second-generation growth.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temporal Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Today & Nightfall\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded the lengthy discussion of the onion with {\i Hadran Alach Hanodea Min Hayarak}, the Gemara now opens a new {\i perek} focused on a different dimension of vows: not the object of the prohibition, but its temporal scope. If the preceding {\i perek} asked what is included in the noun specifying what is forbidden, this {\i perek} asks what is included in the time specification accompanying the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna opens with a case: {\i konam yayin she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em hayom} \u8212- \u8220"Wine that I taste is {\i konam} for me today.\u8221" The ruling is {\i eino asur ela ad shetechshach} \u8212- it is forbidden only until nightfall. The vow follows the halachic day, which ends at nightfall. Critically, this applies regardless of when during the day the vow was made: whether the person made the vow at noon or a minute before sunset, the prohibition ends when night falls. As a practical matter, this means the duration could be as brief as minutes or as long as nearly an entire calendar day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weeks & Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna proceeds to expand these principles across larger time units. {\i Shabbat zu} \u8212- \u8220"this Shabbat,\u8221" meaning this week \u8212- renders wine forbidden {\i bechol ha-Shabbat}, the entire week. And the Shabbat day itself, the Mishna notes, counts {\i le-she\u8217'avar} \u8212- it belongs to the prior week rather than inaugurating the next. If one makes such a vow on Sunday, the coming Shabbat is included within \u8220"this week.\u8221" This is treated as relatively obvious by the Gemara, with the {\i chiddush} being only that one not suppose the term {\i Shabbat} refers exclusively to the weekdays, excluding the Shabbat day itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i chodesh zeh} \u8212- \u8220"this month\u8221" \u8212- forbids wine for the entire month, with {\i Rosh Chodesh} belonging to the next month and not included in the prohibition. The Gemara notes that this requires clarification specifically in the case of a full thirty-day month, where the thirtieth day is simultaneously the last day of the current month and the first day of {\i Rosh Chodesh} of the next. Since people call that day {\i Rosh Chodesh} \u8212- {\i kari inish kari lei Rosh Yarcha} \u8212- it is conceptually associated with the coming month, and the vow does not extend to cover it, even though technically it is the thirtieth day of the month in which the vow was made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Years & Rosh Hashana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shanah zo} \u8212- \u8220"this year\u8221" \u8212- forbids wine for the entire year, with {\i Rosh Hashana} belonging to the coming year. The Gemara observes that the Mishna uses different phrasing for {\i Rosh Chodesh} and {\i Rosh Hashana}: {\i Rosh Chodesh} is described as {\i lehaba} \u8212- what is coming \u8212- while {\i Rosh Hashana} is described as {\i le\u8217'atid lavo} \u8212- the future that is yet to come \u8212- suggesting perhaps a sense of greater temporal distance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The seven-year {\i shemitah} cycle follows the same logic: {\i shavua zeh} forbids wine for the entire cycle, with the {\i shemitah} year itself belonging to the end of the prior cycle rather than beginning a new one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duration Specificity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna then introduces a crucial distinction. If instead of saying \u8220"this day,\u8221" \u8220"this week,\u8221" \u8220"this month,\u8221" or \u8220"this year,\u8221" one specifies an absolute duration \u8212- {\i yom echad}, one day; {\i shabbat achat}, one week; {\i chodesh echad}, one month; {\i shanah achat}, one year \u8212- then the vow operates differently: {\i asur miyom leyom}, it goes from the moment of the vow forward for a full measured unit of time. If one makes a vow at 1:00 PM on Monday saying {\i yom echad}, the prohibition extends until 1:00 PM on Tuesday \u8212- a full twenty-four hours \u8212- rather than ending at nightfall that same day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether such duration-based vows calculate to the precise minute or only to the day. The Mishna does not resolve this fully for longer units such as a week or a month, though the logic of the rule implies a degree of precision: if one is on the third of {\i Adar} and takes a one-month vow, the vow presumably extends until the third of {\i Nissan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Until Pesach Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna addresses the phrase {\i ad haPesach} \u8212- \u8220"until Pesach.\u8221" If one uses the formulation {\i ad sheyagia} \u8212- until it arrives \u8212- the vow lasts until Pesach begins, and the phrase is read as exclusive: {\i ad velo ad bichlal}, until but not including. If one says {\i ad sheyehei} \u8212- until it has been \u8212- the vow lasts until Pesach concludes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna then records a debate concerning the phrase {\i ad pnei haPesach} \u8212- \u8220"until the face of Pesach.\u8221" Rabbi Meir holds {\i asur ad sheyagia}: the face of Pesach means its beginning, so the vow expires when Pesach arrives. Rabbi Yossi holds {\i ad sheyetze}: {\i pnei} refers to the receding face, i.e., the end of Pesach, so the vow continues through the entire festival. The correct {\i girsa} here, as noted in the margin and as established by {\i Rashi}, the {\i Ran}, and the {\i Rosh}, includes the word {\i pnei} in the Mishna text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note the practical implication: a vow not to drink wine {\i ad pnei haPesach} could, depending on which position one follows, create an obligation to seek alternative means of fulfilling the {\i arba kosos} at the Seder \u8212- since {\i nedarim}, operating on objects rather than actions, can indirectly conflict with the performance of {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to explore the ruling of Rabbi Yirmiyah that even though a vow said {\i hayom} \u8212- \u8220"today\u8221" \u8212- expires at nightfall, one nonetheless requires {\i she\u8217'eila lechacham}, a formal release from a sage, once night falls. The vow has lapsed; why is {\i heter chacham} necessary?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef explains: {\i gezeira hayom mishum yom echad} \u8212- it is a rabbinic decree, lest one who has experienced a {\i hayom} vow expiring at nightfall come to treat a {\i yom echad} vow as though it too expires at nightfall, when in fact it runs for a full twenty-four hours. If we allow a {\i hayom} vow to conclude without any formal act, one might reason by analogy that a {\i yom echad} vow likewise expires at the end of the calendar day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges this: if so, should we not make the reverse decree as well \u8212- that a {\i yom echad} vow, even after twenty-four hours, requires {\i hatarat chacham} lest one mistakenly think that {\i hayom} means twenty-four hours too? Rav responds: {\i hayom beyom echad michlaf} \u8212- \u8220"today\u8221" could be confused with \u8220"one day,\u8221" but {\i yom echad behayom lo michlaf} \u8212- \u8220"one day\u8221" cannot be confused with \u8220"today.\u8221" Tosafot makes the underlying logic explicit. If one practices a {\i hayom} vow that expires at nightfall and then applies that experience to a {\i yom echad} vow, one would stop drinking too early. But if one practices a {\i yom echad} vow that runs twenty-four hours and tries to apply that experience backward to a {\i hayom} situation, the result is simply that one would abstain for longer \u8212- which causes no violation. The confusion is asymmetric: leniency in the {\i hayom} case could lead to improperly terminating a {\i yom echad} vow early, but leniency in the {\i yom echad} direction creates no practical error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a different framework through Ravina citing Ameimar in the name of Rav Yosef: this ruling of Rabbi Yirmiyah accords with the view of Rabbi Natan, who holds that anyone who makes a vow is as one who built a {\i bamah}, and one who fulfills it is as one who offered a sacrifice upon it. According to the {\i Ran}, the {\i gezeira} about {\i hatarat chacham} even after the vow has lapsed is not purely about preventing logical confusion between {\i hayom} and {\i yom echad}. Rather, the rabbinic antipathy toward vows as a category provides the underlying impetus: since vows are discouraged in principle, the rabbis imposed the requirement of {\i heter chacham} even for the most trivial of vows \u8212- the person who says \u8220"this item is forbidden to me until tonight\u8221" \u8212- precisely because such short, minor vows are perhaps the most prone to casual proliferation and need to be subjected to some procedural friction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vows vs Fasts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} addresses a practical corollary that flows naturally from this analysis. The custom of {\i ta\u8217'anit yachidim} \u8212- a private fast accepted at {\i mincha} time, observed from dawn until nightfall \u8212- follows the same temporal structure as a {\i hayom} vow: it expires at nightfall. Yet the {\i Ran} rules that at nightfall one does not require {\i hatarat chacham} to eat. {\i Velo gazru mishum yom echad} \u8212- no decree was made here lest one confuse it with a {\i yom echad} vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\i hadavar yadua shedin shel ta\u8217'anis kach hi} \u8212- everyone knows that the law of a {\i ta\u8217'anis} is precisely this: it ends at nightfall. The practice of the {\i ta\u8217'anis} is a recognized institution with well-understood parameters, and no one observing a {\i ta\u8217'anis} will confuse it with a {\i neder} forbidding food for a full twenty-four-hour period. Moreover, {\i ein issuro kish\u8217'ar nedarim shekhvar kav\u8217'u lo chachamim zman} \u8212- its prohibition is not structured like ordinary {\i nedarim}; the sages already established its duration as a fixed matter. Even though a {\i ta\u8217'anis} is technically accepted through verbal declaration, the declaration does not create a {\i neder} in the sense of rendering all food forbidden as an object; it is an acceptance of a recognized institution that concludes at nightfall by established law. No one will confuse being in a {\i ta\u8217'anis} with having taken a vow that lasts a full day.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ambiguous Specifications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh Chodesh Dating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a series of {\i ibayei lehu} \u8212- unresolved questions \u8212- that extend the Mishna\u8217's framework into more difficult territory. Before reaching these, however, the Gemara pauses to draw out the practical {\i halacha le-ma\u8217'aseh} implications of the ruling that {\i Rosh Chodesh} belongs to the coming month even when it falls on the thirtieth day of the preceding month \u8212- specifically, the question of how to date documents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this directly. When writing a {\i ketubah}, a {\i shtar}, or a {\i get} on the first day of a two-day {\i Rosh Chodesh}, what date does one record? The first day of a two-day {\i Rosh Chodesh} is technically the thirtieth day of the prior month \u8212- so today, for instance, might be the thirtieth of {\i Shevat} \u8212- yet it is universally called {\i Rosh Chodesh Adar}. Tosafot rules: {\i kosvin yom ploni shehu Rosh Chodesh} \u8212- one writes the day of the week together with {\i Rosh Chodesh} of the new month. One does not record it as the thirtieth of the prior month, since in common usage \u8212- {\i lashon benei adam} \u8212- the day is called {\i Rosh Chodesh Adar}, not the thirtieth of {\i Shevat}. Since the Gemara elsewhere demonstrates that the laws of documents follow vernacular language in the same manner as {\i nedarim}, the same principle applies: one writes Monday, {\i Rosh Chodesh Adar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Tosafot adds that by a {\i get}, where precision is critical and error could determine whether a woman is bound or free, it is preferable to write both: {\i Rosh Chodesh Adar shehu yom shloshim le-chodesh Shevat} \u8212- identifying the day by both its popular name and its technical calendar position. Tosafot then cites Rabbeinu Tam as having followed this practice in the {\i ketubah} of his own daughter, and notes that this dual identification has become the accepted practice in {\i ketubot} as well. The distinction Tosafot draws \u8212- that the dual formulation is strictly required only for a {\i get} but recommended for a {\i ketubah} \u8212- is worth noting, since in practice both documents receive the fuller formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved \u8216'Day\u8217'\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i ibayei lehu} concerns a person who says {\i konam yayin she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em yom} \u8212- not {\i hayom} (\u8220"today\u8221") and not {\i yom echad} (\u8220"one day\u8221"), but simply {\i yom}, \u8220"day,\u8221" without a demonstrative or numeral. The Mishna specifies outcomes for {\i hayom} (until nightfall) and for {\i yom echad} (twenty-four hours), but does not address the bare word {\i yom}. Which model does it follow?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to resolve this from the Mishna itself. One might reason: since the Mishna says {\i hayom} goes until nightfall, surely {\i yom} without the demonstrative {\i ha-} would be treated more like {\i yom echad} \u8212- a full day. The Gemara responds by pointing to the {\i seifa} of the Mishna, which rules that {\i yom echad} means twenty-four hours. Does not that imply by contrast that anything short of the explicit {\i yom echad} formulation \u8212- including bare {\i yom} \u8212- is treated like {\i hayom}? The Gemara rejects this reasoning: {\i ela meha lika le-mishma minah} \u8212- one cannot derive the answer from the Mishna, because the Mishna addresses only the two explicit cases and says nothing about what falls between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then attempts to bring a proof from a {\i Beraita}: {\i konam yayin she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em hashana nitabra hashana asur bah u\u8217've\u8217'iburah} \u8212- if one says \u8220"this year\u8221" and the year is subsequently declared a leap year with an added second {\i Adar}, the vow extends through the added month as well. Rav Ashi suggests that the {\i chiddush} of this {\i Beraita} is not the leap-year extension \u8212- since if the year ends at the conclusion of {\i Elul}, the inclusion of {\i Adar Sheni} is entirely obvious \u8212- but rather that the {\i Beraita} was formulated using the word {\i shana} alone, without {\i ha-}, and yet it treats that as equivalent to {\i hashana}, \u8220"this year.\u8221" By analogy, {\i yom} would be equivalent to {\i hayom}, ending at nightfall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this argument: {\i le\u8217'olam de\u8217'amar hashana} \u8212- the {\i Beraita} was in fact saying {\i hashana}, \u8220"this year,\u8221" all along, and its {\i chiddush} is precisely the leap-year point. The {\i hava amina} would have been: since most years are not leap years, one could follow the majority of years and hold that the vow ended with the ordinary {\i Adar} even in a leap year. The {\i Beraita} teaches that the vow follows the actual year in which it was taken, not the statistical norm. The word {\i shana} does not enter the analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the question remains unresolved \u8212- {\i teiku} \u8212- practical {\i halacha} defaults to the more stringent position: {\i yom} is treated as {\i yom echad}, a full twenty-four hours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yovel Cycle Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara next poses an {\i ibayei lehu} regarding the {\i Yovel} \u8212- the Jubilee year that concludes a fifty-year cycle. If one says wine is forbidden to him for \u8220"this {\i Yovel},\u8221" does the prohibition extend through the fiftieth year itself, or does the fiftieth year belong to the next cycle?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to the weekly and monthly cases is suggestive. Just as {\i Shabbat} was said to belong to the prior week rather than inaugurate the next, one might reason that the {\i Yovel} year, as the culmination of seven {\i shemitah} cycles, belongs to the period just concluded, and the vow therefore extends through it. But equally, one might argue as follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir vs Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings a {\i Beraita} recording a dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and the Rabbis derived from the verse {\i vekidashtem et shnat ha-hamishim shana} \u8212- \u8220"you shall sanctify the fiftieth year.\u8221" The {\i derashah} yields: {\i shnat hamishim ata moneh ve-ee ata moneh shnat hamishim ve-ahat} \u8212- you count year fifty as fifty, and you do not count it simultaneously as fifty and as one of the next cycle. The Rabbis derive from here that year fifty is the conclusion of the prior cycle; the following year begins a fresh cycle as year one. The fifty-year unit is a complete and closed unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: {\i yovel eino oleh le-minyan shavua} \u8212- the {\i Yovel} year does not count toward the seven-year cycle that follows it. Rather, {\i yovel oleh le-minyan shavua} \u8212- the {\i Yovel} year counts as year one of the next cycle. According to Rabbi Yehuda, the effective unit is forty-nine years, and what one calls the \u8220"fiftieth year\u8221" is simultaneously the first year of the subsequent cycle. This produces a practically significant consequence: the first cycle after a {\i Yovel} would have only five years of permitted planting, since the {\i Yovel} year itself is forbidden for agricultural work, and the immediately following year is {\i shemitah}, leaving only five intervening years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis challenge Rabbi Yehuda: the Torah says {\i shesh shanim tizra sadecha} \u8212- you shall plant your field for six years \u8212- yet according to his system, in the cycle immediately following a {\i Yovel}, there are only five plantable years. Rabbi Yehuda replies symmetrically: and according to you, the Torah says {\i ve-tziviti et birchati li-shlosh ha-shanim} \u8212- I will command My blessing for the three years \u8212- referring to the produce of year six sustaining the people through year seven (the {\i shemitah}), year eight (until the new harvest), and part of year six itself. But according to his interlocutors\u8217' system, at the boundary of a {\i Yovel} cycle, the produce of year six would need to sustain the people through year seven ({\i shemitah}), year eight ({\i Yovel}), and into year nine \u8212- four years, not three. The Gemara responds for both sides: in each case, the verse is speaking of the ordinary situation, not the boundary case involving {\i Yovel}. Each disputant\u8217's anomalous case is simply not the referent of that verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yovel & Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara closes by applying this dispute directly to the vow question: if someone says \u8220"this {\i Yovel} is forbidden to me,\u8221" the question of whether the {\i Yovel} year itself is included depends precisely on whether year fifty belongs to the prior cycle (the Rabbis\u8217' view, in which case the vow runs through the {\i Yovel} year) or inaugurates the next (Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, in which case by the time the {\i Yovel} arrives, the \u8220"this {\i Yovel}\u8221" period has already concluded and wine becomes permissible).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hermeneutic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguity Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to daf 60b, the Gemara addresses the broader interpretive principle underlying the debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yossi about {\i ad pnei haPesach}. The Gemara frames the dispute not merely as a question about the meaning of the word {\i pnei} but as a fundamental disagreement about how to interpret inherently ambiguous language in vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara formulates it thus: {\i Rabbi Meir savar lo ma\u8217'ayil inish nafshieh li-sfeika} \u8212- Rabbi Meir holds that a person does not wish to place himself in doubtful territory. Since the phrase {\i pnei haPesach} is ambiguous \u8212- it could mean the beginning of Pesach, the middle, or the end \u8212- Rabbi Meir assumes the speaker intended only the unambiguous, clear meaning: until Pesach begins, but not into the period of the festival itself, whose inclusion would be uncertain. {\i Rabbi Yossi savar ma\u8217'ayil inish nafshieh li-sfeika} \u8212- Rabbi Yossi holds that a person is willing to have his language carry ambiguous implications. The speaker using the word {\i pnei} may well have intended one of the possible meanings that extends into or through Pesach. Rabbi Yossi therefore rules that the prohibition extends until Pesach concludes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately raises a difficulty: the positions appear reversed in a different {\i sugya}. In a case where a man has two wives and from each wife has two daughters \u8212- an older daughter and a younger daughter \u8212- and he says {\i kidashti et biti ha-gedolah}, \u8220"I betrothed my older daughter,\u8221" the Gemara in Kiddushin records a dispute about which daughter is meant. The terms {\i gedolah she-ba-gedolot} (the older daughter of the older wife), {\i gedolah she-ba-ketanot} (the older daughter of the younger wife), and {\i ketanah she-ba-gedolot} (the younger daughter of the older wife, who is \u8220"older\u8221" only in the sense of coming from the first wife) are all plausible referents of the term {\i gedolah}. The only daughter clearly excluded is {\i ketanah she-ba-ketanot} \u8212- the younger daughter of the younger wife, who cannot by any reading be called \u8220"older.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir there rules {\i kulan asurot chutz min ha-ketanah she-ba-ketanot} \u8212- all daughters except the youngest of the younger wife are in a state of doubtful betrothal. Rabbi Yossi rules {\i kulan mutarot chutz min ha-gedolah she-ba-gedolot} \u8212- only the older daughter of the older wife is presumptively betrothed, since that is the narrowest and most definite meaning of {\i gedolah}. In Kiddushin, then, it is Rabbi Meir who extends the ambiguous possibilities and Rabbi Yossi who restricts interpretation to the unambiguous core \u8212- precisely the reverse of what the Gemara stated above regarding {\i pnei haPesach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversed Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the contradiction by citing Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa in the name of Rav: {\i muchlefa ha-shita} \u8212- the positions are transposed. In our Mishna \u8212- concerning {\i pnei haPesach} \u8212- Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yossi should be understood in reverse of how they were first presented. The version of the debate in Kiddushin, where Rabbi Meir expands to all ambiguous possibilities and Rabbi Yossi restricts to the most definite meaning, reflects their true and consistent positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Beraita} then resolves the matter by formulating a general principle: {\i zeh ha-kelal, kol she-zmano kavu\u8217'a, ve-amar ad pnei} \u8212- whenever something has a fixed, defined time, and one uses the phrase {\i ad pnei}, Rabbi Meir rules {\i ad sheyetze} \u8212- until it concludes \u8212- and Rabbi Yossi rules {\i ad sheyagia} \u8212- until it begins. This {\i Beraita} is consistent with the Kiddushin ruling and confirms that the {\i muchlefa} applies to our Mishna: in the final understanding, Rabbi Meir includes the ambiguous duration and Rabbi Yossi restricts interpretation to the clear and definite beginning point. The underlying principle, on which both cases rest, is precisely whether one assumes a speaker intends his ambiguous language to carry its full possible range of meaning or only its most restricted and uncontested sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 61\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Harvest Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Harvest Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna at the center of today\u8217's daf concerns vows that employ agricultural time-markers. If someone says {\i ad hakatzir} \u8212- until the time of the grain harvest \u8212- or {\i ad habatzir} \u8212- until the grape harvest \u8212- or {\i ad hamasik} \u8212- until the time of the olive pressing \u8212- the rule is {\i aino assur ela ad sheyagia}: the person is forbidden only until that time arrives, meaning until it begins. This in itself is not a novel ruling; it follows directly from what was established earlier regarding {\i ad hapesach}. The {\i chidush} of this mishna lies in what comes next.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna introduces its {\i klal} before its {\i prat}, which is somewhat unusual. The general principle is stated as follows: {\i kol shezmano kavua} \u8212- anything that has a fixed time \u8212- if one says {\i ad sheyagia}, one is forbidden until that time arrives. But if one says {\i ad sheyehei}, until it \u8220"becomes,\u8221" the term is interpreted to mean until it is over \u8212- {\i assur ad sheyeitzei}. This is the rule established for Pesach in the previous mishna. The expression {\i ad sheyehei} points to when the event becomes a {\i haya}, a thing of the past; only then is the prohibition lifted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast \u8212- and the {\i chidush} \u8212- is the second half: {\i vechol she\u8217'ein zmano kavua}, anything that does not have a fixed time. In such cases, whether one says {\i ad sheyehei} or {\i ad sheyagia}, the result is the same: {\i aino assur ela ad sheyagia}, one is forbidden only until the season begins. The practical illustration given is {\i kayitz} \u8212- the gathering in of summer fruit. Whether one says {\i ad hakayitz} or {\i ad sheyehei hakayitz}, the vow expires when people begin bringing the fruit in their baskets, {\i ad sheyatchilu ha\u8217'am lehachnis bekalkalot}. If, however, one explicitly says {\i ad sheya\u8217'avor hakayitz} \u8212- until the {\i kayitz} passes \u8212- then the end point is {\i ad sheyekaplu hamiktzo\u8217'ot}, until the harvesting knives are folded and put away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed vs.\u160 ?Unfixed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual core of this mishna is the distinction between {\i zmano kavua} and {\i ein zmano kavua}. The instinctive reading of {\i ein zmano kavua} is that one does not know when the season begins \u8212- every year the harvest starts at a different time, and so the phrase {\i ad sheyehei} cannot be pointing to a known endpoint. On this reading, when one says \u8220"until it happens\u8221" about something whose beginning is uncertain, the phrase naturally denotes the onset: it hasn\u8217't happened yet, and when it happens \u8212- meaning when it starts \u8212- that is when the vow expires. By contrast, when one says {\i ad sheyehei hapesach}, since everyone knows precisely when Pesach begins, \u8220"until it happens\u8221" cannot mean until it starts \u8212- that is already known. It must therefore point forward to when Pesach will have already occurred, will have become a thing of the past. This reading gives the principle considerable internal logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Zmano Kavua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran, however, offers a different explanation of {\i ein zmano kavua}. He understands it to mean not that the beginning is uncertain, but that the {\i duration} is uncertain \u8212- one does not know when it ends. On this basis, he argues that we are disinclined to interpret {\i ad sheyehei} as meaning \u8220"until it is over,\u8221" because when the endpoint is itself indeterminate, such a reading would leave the vow without any concrete terminus. We therefore fall back on the concrete beginning point rather than an open-ended conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might find this somewhat puzzling: after all, the beginning of the harvest is also variable from year to year, so the asymmetry is not immediately obvious. But part of the Ran\u8217's point may be that the beginning of the harvest is in fact a milestone \u8212- a recognizable moment when people go out to the fields with their baskets \u8212- while the end tapers off gradually and is never marked by a clear, collective act of cessation. The onset has a definite social character; the conclusion does not. So while both are uncertain in the abstract, the beginning is in practice more determinate than the end, and {\i ad sheyehei} therefore tracks the beginning rather than the indeterminate close.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kayitz & Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara elaborates on exactly what type of fruit defines the beginning of the {\i kayitz} season. A {\i tana} teaches: {\i kol kalkalah sheamru kol kalkalah shel teenim} \u8212- when the mishna speaks of people bringing in the baskets, it refers specifically to baskets of figs, not grapes. The reason, as a {\i braita} explains, is that {\i kayitz} as a word connotes hand-picking, and figs are picked by hand while grapes are cut with a knife. A {\i braita} further states: if someone takes a vow {\i miperot hakayitz} \u8212- from the fruits of the {\i kayitz} \u8212- he is forbidden only from figs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel disagrees: {\i anavim bekhlal teenim} \u8212- grapes are included in the same category as figs. His reasoning is that when grapes become very ripe and the vine\u8217's supporting tendrils dry out, they too can be picked by hand \u8212- {\i ki mirdedu miktetzim beyado} \u8212- and therefore they are not categorically excluded from the definition of {\i kayitz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that in the agricultural context of the Land of Israel, figs, dates, olives, and grapes were the primary produce. That the term {\i kayitz} attaches specifically to hand-picked fruits in this setting reflects the cultural and linguistic context in which these vows were made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 End of Kayitz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna states that the end of the {\i kayitz} season is marked by the folding away of the harvesting knives: {\i ad sheyachpilu hamiktzaot}. A {\i tana} specifies that the operative threshold is {\i rov hamiktzaot} \u8212- most of the knives, not all. This raises an immediate difficulty. If most of the knives in a given area have been put away, but a particular field-owner has not yet finished his harvest and his workers are still active, on what basis may anyone enter that field and take fruit?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Tosefta states: {\i hukplu rov hamiktzaot mutarot mishum gezel} \u8212- once most of the knives have been folded away, anything remaining in the field is no longer prohibited as theft. The reasoning is that the owner has effectively abandoned the remaining produce \u8212- he has been {\i mafkir} it \u8212- and it is therefore also exempt from {\i ma\u8217'aser}. But this logic applies cleanly only when one can say with confidence that this particular owner has relinquished his claim. The use of {\i rov} introduces genuine ambiguity: how does one know that this specific field falls within the majority whose owners have finished harvesting? The apparent answer embedded in the rule is that the communal practice \u8212- the fact that most workers in the region have put their tools away \u8212- effectively forces a social reality in which even those who have not formally concluded simply cannot any longer harvest. The rule seems to impose a collective endpoint even on individual holdouts, perhaps on the theory that once the season is effectively over for the region, the remaining fruit is treated as abandoned. This tension will be explored through the stories that follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rov Hamaktzaot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huflu Rov Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The halacha of {\i huflu rov hamaktzaot} has clear social-welfare dimensions. Its promulgation appears to have been driven in part by the rabbis themselves, who actively demonstrated its application. The field-owners, it emerges from the stories below, were not always sympathetic to the rule \u8212- some may not have fully accepted it, or may have accepted it in principle while resenting its application in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first story: Rabbi and Rabbi Yossi bar Rabbi Yehuda came to a certain place \u8212- {\i iklu l\u8217'hahu atra} \u8212- at the time when most of the knives had been folded away. Rabbi began eating from the fruit of the trees they passed. Rabbi Yossi bar Rabbi Yehuda did not eat. The owner of the field arrived and said to them: why are the rabbis not eating? The knives have been put away \u8212- {\i hukplu rov hamaktzaot} \u8212- go right ahead. And even so, Rabbi Yossi bar Rabbi Yehuda did not eat. The Gemara explains: {\i kasavar mishum sanyus milta hu deka\u8217'amar hai gavra} \u8212- he suspected that the field-owner was not sincere, that he was saying this out of spite or resentment rather than genuine permission. This is entirely plausible given what we saw above: if the field-owner genuinely felt that {\i rov hamaktzaot} obligated him to yield his fruit, he would not have said it with evident bitterness. Rabbi Yossi bar Rabbi Yehuda read the social cue correctly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chama Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second story: Rav Chama bar Rav Chanina came to a certain place at the same time \u8212- {\i bizman shehukplu rov hamaktzaot} \u8212- and he was eating. He gave some to his attendant, but the attendant would not eat. Rav Chama told him: eat. This is what Rabbi Yishmael bar Rabbi Yossi transmitted in the name of his father Rabbi Yossi \u8212- that once most of the knives have been put away, it is permitted. The attendant apparently felt uncertain about the halacha or its application; Rav Chama had to actively instruct him and cite the ruling with rabbinic authority. The point seems to be that the rabbis were not merely themselves aware of this rule but felt it important enough to actively promote, even to their own household staff. There is something almost like a public-welfare function being performed: the rule exists, and the rabbis are demonstrating it by personal example and explicit teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third story is the most famous and most ethically complex. A certain man found Rabbi Tarfon in his field at the time when most of the knives had been put away, eating from the produce. The man put Rabbi Tarfon into a sack, hoisted him up, and was dragging him toward the river to throw him in \u8212- evidently under the impression that he had finally caught the thief who had been stealing from his vineyard all year. Rabbi Tarfon, from inside the sack, called out: {\i oi lo l\u8217'Tarfon sheze horgo} \u8212- woe to Tarfon, for this man is going to kill him. Upon hearing the name, the man dropped the sack and fled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rest of Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's life was shadowed by anguish over this incident. As Rabbi Abahu reports in the name of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel: {\i kol yamav shel oto tzaddik haya mitzta\u8217'er al davar ze} \u8212- that righteous man was distressed about this matter for the rest of his days. His stated reason was: {\i oi li shenishtamashti b\u8217'kitra shel Torah} \u8212- woe to me that I used the crown of Torah. He felt he had no right to invoke his identity as Rabbi Tarfon to extricate himself, that some other means should have been found.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kitra Shel Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara buttresses this with a principle stated by Rabbah bar Bar Chana in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i kol hamishtamesh b\u8217'kitra shel Torah ne\u8217'ekar min ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- anyone who uses the crown of Torah for his own purposes is uprooted from the world. The proof is a {\i kal vachomer} from Belshatzar. Belshatzar used the sacred vessels of the Beit Hamikdash \u8212- yet those vessels, the Gemara notes, had already been de-sanctified, as derived from the verse {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'u vah pritzim v\u8217'chililuha}: once the Beit Hamikdash was breached, its vessels became {\i klei chol}. The principle {\i kivan she\u8217'partzu na\u8217'asu chol} \u8212- once they have been broken into, they become ordinary \u8212- is a significant halachic principle with implications in many areas, including the status of synagogues that have been seized. Nevertheless, even though the vessels Belshatzar used had already been de-sanctified, he was killed for it that very night \u8212- {\i babeliyo kteil Belshatzar}. The {\i kal vachomer}: if the Torah itself \u8212- which can {\i never} be de-sanctified, which is {\i shechai v\u8217'kayam l\u8217'olam} \u8212- is misused, {\i al achat kama v\u8217'kama}, how much more severe the consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a difficulty: given that Rabbi Tarfon was eating {\i during} the permissible period of {\i huflu rov hamaktzaot}, why was the man attacking him in the first place? The Gemara answers: {\i mishum dehu havei ganvi lei anveizi kol shatah} \u8212- that man had been having his grapes stolen throughout the year by someone, and when he stumbled upon Rabbi Tarfon, he was convinced he had finally caught his thief. The legality of {\i huflu rov hamaktzaot} was therefore irrelevant from the man\u8217's perspective \u8212- he thought he was dealing with a year-round criminal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sharpens the question of Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's anguish. If the man believed Rabbi Tarfon was a common thief and would not have been persuaded by a simple offer of payment or an invocation of the halachic rule, what alternative did Rabbi Tarfon have? The Gemara answers: {\i mishum de\u8217'Rabbi Tarfon ashir gadol havei, havei lei le-fayaso bi-damei} \u8212- Rabbi Tarfon was a very wealthy man. He could have offered substantial compensation, enough to satisfy the man for whatever had been stolen throughout the year. He was not compelled to identify himself as a great Torah sage. That, the Gemara concludes, is what he should have tried first, and his failure to explore that avenue \u8212- reaching instead for the announcement of his identity \u8212- is what caused him lasting remorse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status & Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning Motivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is now cited that addresses the proper motivation for Torah study, grounding the broader discussion in the {\i pasuk}: {\i le-ahavah et Hashem Elokecha lishmoa be-kolo u-le-davkah bo} \u8212- to love God, to hear His voice, to cleave to Him. A person should not say: I will study Torah so that people will call me a {\i chacham}. Nor: I will study Mishnah so that they will call me {\i rebbi}. Nor: I will study so that I will become an elder who sits in the {\i yeshivah} \u8212- though this motivation is somewhat different, since it may be more about the pleasure of learning than the honor it brings. Rather, one should {\i lemod me-ahavah} \u8212- learn out of love. And: {\i ve-sof ha-kavod lavo} \u8212- in the end, the honor will come. As the {\i pesukim} say: {\i koshrem al etzbe\u8217'otecha kotvem al luach libecha} \u8212- bind them to your fingers, inscribe them on the tablet of your heart \u8212- and {\i deracheiha darchei no\u8217'am}, its ways are ways of pleasantness, and {\i etz chayim hi lamachazikim bah}, it is a tree of life for those who hold fast to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The teaching does not deny the well-known principle that one should always learn even {\i shelo lishmah}, since {\i mitoch shelo lishmah ba lishmah} \u8212- from learning for ulterior motives, one arrives at learning for its own sake. But the ideal, clearly, is {\i lishmah} \u8212- learning for love of God and devotion to the Torah itself, not for the status it confers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elazar\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok adds: {\i aseh devarim leshem pa\u8217'alam} \u8212- do things for the sake of their Maker \u8212- {\i ve-daber bahem lishmam} \u8212- and speak of them for their own sake. {\i Al ta\u8217'asem atarah lehitgadel bahem} \u8212- do not make them a crown for self-aggrandizement \u8212- {\i ve-al ta\u8217'asem kardom lihyot oder bahem} \u8212- and do not make them an agricultural tool, a spade to dig with. The same {\i kal vachomer} from Belshatzar follows: Belshatzar used vessels that had already been de-sanctified and was destroyed. One who uses the crown of Torah \u8212- which is never de-sanctified \u8212- {\i al achat kama v\u8217'kama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this background, Rava introduces a seemingly divergent position: {\i shari leih le-inish le-odoei nafshieh be-atra de-la yad\u8217'ei leih} \u8212- it is permitted for a person, and specifically for a {\i tzurba meirabanan}, to make himself known in a place where people do not know who he is. The proof-text is the verse in which {\i Ovadyah} identified himself to {\i Eliyahu}, saying {\i ve-avdecha yere et Hashem mi-ne\u8217'urav} \u8212- your servant has feared God since his youth. Ovadyah was in a situation where his identity and history were directly relevant, and he made them known. So too, Rava holds, a Torah scholar who arrives in an unknown town may identify himself rather than remaining anonymous. The line between appropriate self-identification and misuse of the Torah\u8217's crown is real but not always obvious. If a scholar comes to an inn and no one knows who he is, he may let it be known. This seems qualitatively different from exploiting one\u8217's status to override a just claim against oneself \u8212- but exactly where the line falls is not immediately clear from Rava\u8217's formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Known vs.\u160 ?Unknown\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara itself then raises the tension with the Rabbi Tarfon story: but Rabbi Tarfon also identified himself in a place where the man did not know him \u8212- and he was anguished about it for the rest of his life. The Gemara\u8217's response returns to the argument already established: {\i inash gadol hayah va-hava leih le-payusei bi-dmei} \u8212- he was a great and wealthy man and could have offered compensation. But this still leaves Rava\u8217's position somewhat open-ended. Is it only permissible to identify oneself when there is no alternative available? That seems like an overly narrow reading of Rava\u8217's ruling, which appears to permit self-identification more broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava himself then addresses an apparent internal contradiction in his position. The verse says {\i yehalelucha zar velo picha} \u8212- let others praise you, not your own mouth. How does that square with permitting self-identification? The Gemara resolves this cleanly: {\i ha b\u8217'atra d\u8217'yad\u8217'i lei ha b\u8217'atra d\u8217'lo yad\u8217'i lei} \u8212- in a place where people know who you are, you need not say anything; others will establish your identity, and the information will spread on its own. But in a place where no one knows you, you may speak on your own behalf. Rava does not appear to feel fully constrained by the Gemara\u8217's treatment of the Rabbi Tarfon narrative; his position seems more permissive \u8212- that a Torah scholar is genuinely entitled to acknowledgment and to the honor that properly accrues to his station, and that making this known is not the same as {\i mishtamesh b\u8217'kitra shel Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran resolves the tension with a principled distinction. He explains: the problem with what Rabbi Tarfon did was not that he identified himself per se. In the Ran\u8217's reading, the man, upon hearing the name Rabbi Tarfon, did not let him go out of a conviction that he was innocent \u8212- he let him go because he was unwilling to harm so prominent a Torah figure, even though he still believed Rabbi Tarfon had been stealing from him all year. That is {\i mishtamesh b\u8217'kitra shel Torah}: using Torah status to escape the {\i consequences} of something one is actually being held responsible for, regardless of whether that responsibility is just or unjust.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran then draws the critical line: {\i liftor atzmo b\u8217'chvod Torah bamah she\u8217'eino chayav bo} \u8212- to use the honor of Torah to exempt oneself from something one is genuinely not obligated to do \u8212- that is permitted. And the examples that follow in the Gemara are all cases of legitimate entitlement. A {\i talmid chacham} may say he will not pay the poll tax \u8212- {\i lo yehavna karga} \u8212- because Torah scholars are genuinely exempt from that tax. He may say his case should be heard first at the court \u8212- {\i sharo li tigrai bereisha} \u8212- because that is an established right of Torah scholars, derived from {\i u-vnei David kohanim hayu}: just as a {\i kohen} receives priority \u8212- {\i kohen notel b\u8217'rosh} \u8212- derived from {\i v\u8217'kidashto} and developed by the school of Rabbi Yishmael to mean priority in reading Torah, in leading {\i Birkat Hamazon}, and in receiving the first and finest portion \u8212- so too does a {\i talmid chacham} receive priority. These are not instances of misusing the crown; they are the crown\u8217's proper exercise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran thus formulates the principle as follows: a {\i talmid chacham} may use his status for the benefits it genuinely entitles him to, just as {\i kohanim} and {\i leviyim} are entitled to {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser}. What he may not do is use that status to get out of something that is legitimately being claimed against him \u8212- to escape accountability under cover of Torah honor. That is the authentic misuse. This reading has an elegant coherence, though it inevitably leaves open the further question of precisely what the entitlements of a {\i talmid chacham} are \u8212- a question the Gemara does not fully resolve but whose general contours are suggested by the examples cited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tax Exemptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poll Tax Exempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having articulated the principle through the Ran\u8217's framework, the Gemara now turns explicitly to the material benefits to which {\i talmidei chachamim} are entitled. Rava states: {\i shari lei l\u8217'tzurba meirabanan l\u8217'meimar lo yehavna karga} \u8212- a Torah scholar may declare that he will not pay the poll tax. The scriptural basis is the verse from Ezra: {\i minda blo vahalach lo shalit lemirmei aleihon} \u8212- it is not permitted to impose upon them. Rav Yehuda identifies each term: {\i minda zu manat hamelech} \u8212- a tribute to the king; {\i blo zu kesef gulgaltah} \u8212- the poll tax, literally a head tax; {\i vehalach zu arnona} \u8212- a third category, variously interpreted. The Ran understands {\i arnona} as some form of obligation to provision the king and his armies when they pass through. Tosafot, drawing on Latin etymology, understands it as corv\u233 ?e \u8212- forced labor. The JPS translation renders the three terms as tribute, head tax, and land tax.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This exemption has ancient and widespread precedent. Religious authorities and institutions were routinely exempted from taxation by ruling powers throughout history \u8212- from the account in the Yosef narrative, through the Persian edict in Ezra, through medieval European practice in which the Church enjoyed tax-exempt status. The practical dynamic often involved negotiation: the Jewish community would establish before the governing authority that these individuals served a religious function, and the exemption would follow from the recognized status of the religious class.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Servant of Flame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds a further ruling: {\i shari lei l\u8217'tzurba meirabanan l\u8217'meimra avda d\u8217'nura ana} \u8212- it is permitted for a Torah scholar to say \u8220"I am a servant of the flame,\u8221" in the context of the Zoroastrian Babylonian environment where a sacred, maintained flame was central to religious worship. By saying this, one would be understood by the tax authorities as belonging to or serving the Zoroastrian priestly class, whose members were also exempt from the poll tax. The result: {\i lo yavina acharga} \u8212- I will not pay the poll tax. The Gemara explains: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama l\u8217'salukei aria d\u8217'minei ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- he is only saying this to escape the lion pressing him for money, to avoid a payment he is in principle not obligated to make.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking ruling. The declaration creates the impression in the minds of the tax collectors that the speaker is affiliated with {\i avodat zarah}. This is not a small or casual misrepresentation. Relevant to the broader halachic literature is the question of whether such ambiguous speech is permissible in more extreme circumstances \u8212- such as to conceal one\u8217's Jewish identity during persecution \u8212- and this Gemara has been cited in that context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Justification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran addresses the obvious difficulty. He offers two lines of reasoning. First, {\i de-mucha milsa de-amar hachi le-ashtomei mikarga} \u8212- it is transparent that the speaker is only saying this to evade the tax; the statement is not a sincere confession of religious identity, and its instrumental character is evident. But evident to whom? If it is evident to the non-Jewish tax collectors, why would the stratagem work? And if it is only evident to Jewish observers, the first justification is weaker than it appears.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran therefore adds: a scholar does not become suspect of {\i avodat kochavim} simply because tax collectors take his ambiguous statement at face value. The scholar knows he is referring to God, who is described as {\i eish ochlah} \u8212- a consuming fire. The phrase {\i avda d\u8217'nura} is technically true of every servant of God. The gap between what the speaker intends and what the hearer understands is what permits the statement. Nevertheless, the Ran also notes that the ruling is specifically about a {\i talmid chacham} who is in principle exempt from the tax \u8212- he is not cheating the system, only asserting an exemption through an indirect and ambiguous formulation. Whether someone who is not a {\i talmid chacham} could use such language to evade a tax obligation they genuinely bear is another question entirely and is not resolved here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi Forest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a case that moves from the question of tax evasion to the broader parameters of {\i lifnei iver} \u8212- the prohibition on placing a stumbling block before the blind, which extends to facilitating another\u8217's sin. Rav Ashi had a forest \u8212- {\i havah lei hahu appa} \u8212- and he sold it to {\i bei nura}, the house of fire, that is, to Zoroastrians, presumably the priests or the institution associated with the sacred flame. Ravina challenged him: {\i haika lifnei iver lo titein michshol} \u8212- does this not violate the prohibition of {\i lifnei iver}? You are providing them with the very material they will use in their religious worship, which constitutes {\i avodat zarah} \u8212- and facilitating a Noahide in committing one of the seven prohibitions of {\i bnei Noach} is itself forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi responded: {\i rov etzim le-haskata ninhu} \u8212- most wood is purchased for heating and cooking, not for religious ritual purposes. Even if the buyers are Zoroastrians whose religion centers on a sacred fire, one cannot assume that every piece of wood sold to them will be used for religious worship rather than for ordinary domestic needs. Since the majority of wood is used for mundane purposes, Rav Ashi held that he bore no halachic responsibility for how this particular batch would be used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of where exactly the sale was made \u8212- whether to a private buyer who happened to be Zoroastrian or to the religious institution itself \u8212- is worth noting. {\i Bei nura} as a designation suggests the institutional context, which might strengthen Ravina\u8217's concern. But even so, Rav Ashi maintained that {\i rov etzim le-haskata ninhu} provides sufficient cover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashkenazi Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This Gemara is directly relevant to a well-documented feature of medieval Ashkenazi Jewish economic life: Jewish merchants commonly sold goods to the Church and to Christian clergy, including clearly religious objects such as chalices, liturgical vestments, and other ritual items. How could this be squared with {\i lifnei iver} and the prohibition on facilitating {\i avodat zarah}? Several justifications were developed in the {\i rishonim} to address this reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One approach, which developed in the period of the Tosafot, was to argue that Christians are not truly sincere in their religious practice \u8212- that it is not genuine {\i avodat zarah} in the full sense. This is of course historically implausible, and the argument becomes even weaker when applied to sales made directly to clergy and to the institutional Church rather than to lay individuals. A later development, associated with the period of the Rema and drawing on a Tosafot, was the position that Christianity constitutes {\i avodat zarah} for Jews but not for non-Jews \u8212- a theologically controversial and legally significant distinction. Another argument was that since the Church could obtain the same goods from any number of other suppliers, the Jewish merchant\u8217's contribution was not the determining cause of the sin. As noted, however, this reasoning is difficult to sustain: the fact that someone else could supply the forbidden item does not generally exempt one from {\i lifnei iver}, and this Gemara itself seems to assume otherwise, since wood was presumably available from other sources and the concern for {\i lifnei iver} was raised nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Tosafot on this passage address the limits of the {\i rov etzim} principle directly. Tosafot states: {\i heicha de\u8217'ika lifnei iver} \u8212- in cases where {\i lifnei iver} genuinely applies \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ein lahem heter} \u8212- where there is no permissible use \u8212- the prohibition is real. Tosafot then derives: {\i u-mi-kan yesh lomar she\u8217'assur lehalvot ma\u8217'ot le-tzorech binyan avodat kochavim} \u8212- it is forbidden to lend money for the construction of a church \u8212- {\i o le-tzorech tachshiteha} \u8212- or for its ornamental furnishings. {\i Ve-chol sheken limkor lahem meshamshei ve-divrei tiflut} \u8212- and certainly one may not sell them actual implements of worship and what the Tosafot calls {\i divrei tiflut} (a pointed play on {\i tefillot}) \u8212- such as {\i gvi\u8217'im} (chalices), {\i machtot} (censers), and {\i sefarim} (books, meaning liturgical texts). Tosafot concludes: {\i veha-mone\u8217'a yatzliach} \u8212- one who is able to refrain will be rewarded. The phrasing is telling: Tosafot acknowledges that the practice was widespread and that calling for complete abstention was aspirational rather than enforceable \u8212- but the halachic position is unambiguous. Selling these items is {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geographic Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mountain vs.\u160 ?Valley\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final mishna of the daf addresses how the terms of a vow are interpreted when the person\u8217's location at the time of the vow differs from the location where the vow\u8217's term will be fulfilled. The mishna establishes: {\i hakol lefi mekom nidro} \u8212- everything follows the place of the vow. {\i Im hayah bahar bahar, im hayah bebikah bebikah} \u8212- if one was on a mountain, we follow the mountain; if in a valley, we follow the valley. The different elevations experience different agricultural rhythms, and the {\i neder} tracks the environmental context in which it was formulated. A {\i braita} applies this specifically to {\i kayitz}: if one vowed {\i ad hakayitz} while in the Galil, and then descended to the valleys, even though the {\i kayitz} season has arrived in the valleys, the prohibition continues \u8212- {\i assur ad sheyagia kayitz ba-Galil} \u8212- until the {\i kayitz} comes in the Galil.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Rain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna continues: {\i ad hageshamim}, until the rains \u8212- or {\i ad sheyehu geshamim}, until the rains occur \u8212- is interpreted as {\i ad shetered reviah shniyah}, until the second rainfall descends. The second rain is treated as the onset of the rainy season proper, the point at which one can say the rains have genuinely arrived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rain Season\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel qualifies this: {\i ad sheyagia zmanah shel reviah} \u8212- until the time of the second rain arrives, even if it has not actually fallen. This is a realistic concession to meteorological variability: in a dry year, the rains may be significantly delayed, and a vow \u8220"until the rains\u8221" should not remain binding indefinitely on the theoretical possibility that rain may still come.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Rain Ends\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna then addresses the other end of the rainy season: {\i ad sheyifseku geshamim} \u8212- until the rains end. Rabbi Meir holds: {\i ad sheyetzei Nissan kulo} \u8212- until the end of Nissan. Even though Pesach marks the practical end of the rainy season, rains can continue intermittently into Nissan, and Rabbi Meir calibrates the terminus at the end of that month. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: {\i ad sheya\u8217'avor ha-Pesach} \u8212- until Pesach has passed. We stop reciting {\i mashiv haruach umorid hageshem} before the end of Pesach, but actual rain can extend beyond that point; Rabbi Yehuda nonetheless sets the end of the rainy season at the conclusion of Pesach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Galil to Valley\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} cited in the Gemara makes the geographic principle concrete. A person who vowed {\i ad hakayitz baGalil} \u8212- until the {\i kayitz} in the Galil \u8212- and then descended to the valleys remains bound by his vow even when the {\i kayitz} arrives in the valleys, until {\i sheyagia kayitz baGalil}. One might equally ask the reverse: if someone vowed while in a valley and later went to the mountains where the {\i kayitz} comes later, would we apply stringency or leniency? The mishna\u8217's general principle \u8212- {\i hakol lefi mekom nidro} \u8212- would suggest that the place of the vow determines the reference point in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Singular/Plural\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira introduces a linguistic distinction that resolves the {\i machloket} between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel regarding the rain terms. The dispute, Rabbi Zeira explains, applies specifically when one says {\i ad hageshamim} \u8212- the plural form, referring to the rains. In that case, there is a question of whether the term means the actual rains must fall ({\i reviah shniyah}) or whether the rainy season\u8217's arrival is sufficient (Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel). But if one says {\i ad hageshem} \u8212- the singular, \u8220"the rain\u8221" \u8212- Rabbi Zeira holds that everyone would agree: {\i ad zman geshamim ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- this refers to the season of rain, not to an actual rainfall event. The singular form, in Rabbi Zeira\u8217's reading, evokes the concept of the rainy season more abstractly, and there is no dispute about its meaning. The plural {\i geshamim} is more ambiguous \u8212- it could be pointing to actual precipitation events \u8212- and that ambiguity is what generates the disagreement between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 64\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scope to Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Return & New Perek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Welcome back after the {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} break. Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 64, the opening of a new {\i perek} \u8212- the ninth {\i perek} \u8212- and with it we move into substantially different terrain. The preceding chapters were occupied with the scope of {\i nedarim}: what certain words mean, how broadly a vow extends in time, and so on. Now we transition to the subject of {\i hatarat nedarim}, the annulment of vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition to Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not, in fact, the first time {\i hatarat nedarim} has appeared in the {\i masechta}. Earlier we encountered the conceptual category of {\i nidrei ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- four types of vows that were not genuinely intended \u8212- which serves as the theoretical foundation for the entire institution of {\i hatarat nedarim}. The idea is that when a vow was not fully thought through, when there was some error in the person\u8217's understanding of the situation, there is room to revisit and undo it. Now, at the opening of this new {\i perek}, the {\i mishnayot} return to that institution and develop it further.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kavod Aviv V\u8217'Imo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i perek} opens at the top of daf 64 with the following position of Rabbi Eliezer: {\i potchin l\u8217'adam b\u8217'khvod aviv v\u8217'imo} \u8212- one may open for a person, when seeking to annul his {\i neder}, with the matter of the honor of his father and mother. In practice, this means that the {\i beit din} asks the petitioner: had you known how this vow would have impacted your parents, would you have taken it? If the answer is no, that serves as a {\i petach} \u8212- a grounds for annulment. The {\i chachamim}, however, forbid this approach, on the grounds that it would effectively license everyone to escape any vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran: Disgrace Parents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What exactly does the {\i beit din} say to the person? The {\i Ran} explains the formula this way: {\i ilu hayita yode\u8217'a she-mevazin avicha v\u8217'imecha al de-taka b\u8217'nedarim} \u8212- had you known that people would disgrace your father and mother, would speak ill of them on account of your behavior? The {\i Ran} elaborates: people call a wicked person {\i bar rashia} \u8212- the child of a wicked person \u8212- because a child\u8217's deeds reflect on the parents. Whatever people think of you, they will say the same of those who raised you. Had you realized that your vow would cause people to speak poorly of your parents, would you still have made it? A second formulation in the {\i Ran} is similar in thrust: {\i she-amarta me-hem she-af hem mezalzelin b\u8217'nedarim} \u8212- that people will say your parents must also be cavalier about vows, since clearly you learned this behavior from them. Both formulations point in the same direction: the shame accruing to the parents through what people say about them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Bad Things Heard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} offers a somewhat different framing. The formula, according to {\i Rashi}, is: {\i ilu hayita yode\u8217'a she-omrim al avicha, oy lo l\u8217'av she-gidal ben rasha kazeh she-parutz b\u8217'nedarim} \u8212- had you known that people would say to your father, \u8220"Woe to the father who raised such a wicked son, so dissolute in his vows\u8221" \u8212- would you have taken the vow? This is not quite the same as saying that people will think poorly of the parents\u8217' character. Rather, it is that the news will reach the parents and cause them anguish. It is less about the parents\u8217' reputation than about the grief that will come back to them when they hear what is being said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vows Proliferate in Prac\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One cannot help but note a certain tension in this entire framework. From everything we have read in {\i Nedarim}, it is clear that vows were extremely prevalent. People were invoking {\i neder} formulae constantly \u8212- in disputes, in negotiations, in everyday social interaction. Given that reality, one wonders whether the social stigma that the {\i Chachamim} are describing \u8212- that a person who takes a vow will be regarded poorly, and that this will reflect badly on his parents \u8212- was an accurate description of how society actually functioned, or rather an expression of how the rabbis wished society would respond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} treats this as a genuine sociological reality: yes, it really will reflect badly on your parents. But the disparity between the frequency of vowing and the severity of the social sanction is striking. It is possible that {\i Chazal} are reading into the social response to vows the attitude they aspired for society to have, rather than describing how people actually behaved. That said, this is not the direction the {\i Gemara} itself takes; it treats the social consequence as real and genuine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One important point is also worth raising: the framing of {\i kavod aviv v\u8217'imo} as a {\i petach} is notably different in kind from the more self-regarding concerns that typically motivate a vow. A {\i neder} is by nature an intensely self-centered act \u8212- it is about what I want, what I refuse, what I will or will not do. To ask the person to consider a third party \u8212- the parents, who were not part of the original vow \u8212- broadens the frame of inquiry considerably. It is entirely plausible that a person who had thought through every dimension of his own situation would still not have considered how it would rebound onto his parents. That, arguably, is precisely what gives this {\i petach} its force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Child\u8217's Shame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} offers yet another variation, closer in structure to {\i Rashi} but with a slightly different emphasis. {\i Reitem binchem kal b\u8217'nedarim v\u8217'yitbaishu} \u8212- people will say to your parents, \u8220"Have you seen what your son is doing? Have you seen how lightly he treats vows?\u8221" And they will be embarrassed. The focus here is on the anguish that comes back to the parents when they hear their child spoken of this way. It is not that people think poorly of the parents\u8217' character, nor quite that people are saying something harsh about them in the abstract; it is that it gets back to the parents directly, and causes them shame.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chachamim Reject Parents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Tzadok: Reductio\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tzadok enters the discussion and makes what is in effect a {\i reductio ad absurdum} against Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. His argument: {\i ad she-potchin lo b\u8217'khvod aviv v\u8217'imo, yiftchu lo b\u8217'khvod hamakom} \u8212- if you are already prepared to open with the honor of the parents, you might as well open with the honor of God. And if that is permitted \u8212- if we can always invoke the fact that taking a vow is religiously inappropriate, an affront to God \u8212- then {\i ein nedarim}, there will be no vows, because that argument applies universally to every single {\i neder} ever taken. It is therefore obviously impossible to permit this as a standard {\i petach}, and if we reject the {\i kavod hamakom} argument, consistency would seem to require rejecting {\i kavod aviv v\u8217'imo} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kavod HaMakom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What exactly is meant by {\i kavod hamakom} in this context? The {\i Ran} cites a {\i Sifri} that speaks of a distinction between {\i nozer b\u8217'chayei hamelech} and {\i nozer b\u8217'melech atzmo} \u8212- vowing by the life of the king versus vowing by the king himself \u8212- and somehow implicates God\u8217's honor in the realm of {\i nedarim}. The precise logic of this crossover is not entirely clear, especially since God\u8217's honor is far more immediately at stake in a {\i shvuah}, where God\u8217's name is explicitly invoked \u8212- {\i lo yenakeh Hashem et asher yisa shemo lashav}. A {\i neder}, by contrast, concerns the status of an object, not a direct invocation of the divine name. Even {\i konam}, which draws on the power of the {\i korban} formula, is not so directly about God\u8217's honor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} offers a simpler reading: {\i af she-keneged haKadosh Baruch Hu oseh shenoder, v\u8217'rasha hu lashamayim} \u8212- the one who takes a vow is acting against God, is considered wicked before Heaven. This reflects the rabbinic attitude, well attested elsewhere in {\i Nedarim}, that vows are in general discouraged and regarded as religiously problematic. The {\i kavod hamakom} argument, then, is simply: taking a vow is not a good thing religiously; had you thought about that, you wouldn\u8217't have done it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universal Petach Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The force of Rabbi Tzadok\u8217's objection becomes clear once we spell out what it would mean to permit {\i kavod hamakom} as a universal {\i petach}. Since every vow, by definition, falls short of the rabbinic ideal \u8212- since the rabbis consistently discourage vowing \u8212- every person who comes before a {\i beit din} could immediately invoke this argument. {\i Im kein ein nedarim} \u8212- if this were valid, there would simply be no vows that could not be undone. And this, of course, is untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what is so terrible about that? If we do not like vows, if {\i Chazal} consistently discourage them, and if it is genuinely true that people who reflected carefully would not have made them \u8212- why not make it easy for everyone to get out? The {\i Gemara} will address this, but two distinct policy considerations present themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Policy Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is a concern about dishonesty: if you frame the question in a way that no one could honestly say yes to \u8212- \u8220"had you known this was an offense to God, would you still have done it?\u8221" \u8212- you are not going to get a truthful response. People will say what they need to say to get out of the vow, not what they actually would have done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second is a concern about trivialization: if there is a universal escape route from all {\i nedarim}, people will stop taking vowing seriously. The institution of {\i hatarat nedarim} serves to create an appropriate gravity around vows \u8212- it should be hard enough to get out of them that people think carefully before making them in the first place. A blanket universal {\i petach} undermines that entire framework. Both of these considerations will be developed in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's analysis below.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concession on Direct Imp\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes at this point that there is, in fact, one case where the {\i chachamim} concede the validity of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position: {\i modim chachamim l\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'davar she-beino l\u8217'vein aviv v\u8217'imo she-potchin lo b\u8217'khvod aviv v\u8217'imo} \u8212- where the vow directly impacts the parents, the {\i chachamim} agree that the {\i beit din} may open with the matter of the parents\u8217' honor. If a person took a vow that he would receive no benefit from his parents, or that his parents would receive no benefit from him, the {\i beit din} may indeed ask: had you really thought through how this would affect your relationship with your parents? This concession by the {\i chachamim} will need to be explained in light of their broader objection, and the {\i Gemara} will return to this point in the next section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nolad Innovation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Nolad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i ve\u8217'od amar Rabbi Eliezer, potchin b\u8217'nolad} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer further rules that one may open with a {\i nolad}, a newly occurred event. This is an even more far-reaching {\i chiddush}. The claim is that even an event which took place {\i after} the vow was made, and which the person had no reason to anticipate at the time of the vow, may serve as the basis for undoing it. Had you known that this would come to pass, the {\i beit din} may ask, would you still have made the vow?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mega Millions Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate: suppose a person makes a {\i neder} that everything belonging to Michael is {\i assur} to him \u8212- that he will derive no benefit from Michael. The next day, Michael wins the Mega Millions lottery. Clearly, the person could say: had I realized that this would happen, I never would have made this vow. That is a {\i nolad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual difficulty with {\i nolad} is apparent. The standard logic of a {\i petach} runs as follows: we revisit what you were thinking at the time of the vow, and we try to establish that deep down, had you fully processed all the relevant considerations, you would not have made it \u8212- which means the vow was, in effect, a {\i ta\u8217'ut}, an error in understanding. But this logic only works if the person {\i could} have been thinking about the relevant consideration at the time. A future event that no one could have anticipated does not speak to the person\u8217's frame of mind when they made the vow. They were entirely sincere; they had thought it through to the best of their ability; they simply could not have known what the future held. Rabbi Eliezer, nonetheless, permits using such an event as a {\i petach}. The {\i chachamim} disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nolad Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} provides several examples of {\i nolad} in practice. Someone takes a vow that a certain person is {\i assur} to him \u8212- {\i konam she-ani neheneh l\u8217'ish peloni} \u8212- and that person subsequently becomes the {\i sofer} of the city. The {\i sofer} is not merely the repairman for {\i tefillin} and {\i mezuzot}; he is the scribe who writes all legal documents, and therefore the one to whom the entire community must inevitably turn for essential services. The petitioner says: {\i ilu hayiti yode\u8217'a she-na\u8217'asah sofer}, had I known he would become the city scribe, I would never have made this vow. Alternatively, the person was about to marry off his son \u8212- {\i mashia et beno} \u8212- and everyone in the community is expected to participate in the celebration. Or: someone makes a {\i konam} that a certain house is {\i assur} to him \u8212- {\i l\u8217'bayit zeh she-ani nichnas} \u8212- and the house is subsequently purchased and converted into a {\i beit haknesset}. Had I known, he says, that this building would become a {\i shul}, I would not have made the vow. In all these cases, Rabbi Eliezer {\i matir} \u8212- permits \u8212- and the {\i chachamim} forbid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Reject Nolad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The disagreement between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i chachamim} here appears to be more fundamental in character than the earlier dispute about {\i kavod aviv v\u8217'imo}. In that case, it was plausible to read the {\i chachamim} as making primarily a policy argument \u8212- we worry about dishonest answers, or about trivializing {\i nedarim} \u8212- while conceding that in principle the concern about parents\u8217' honor could be a genuine {\i ta\u8217'ut}. But in the case of {\i nolad}, the {\i chachamim} seem to be making a principled objection: an event that the person could not have anticipated at the time does not retroactively undermine the sincerity and conviction behind the vow. The person was completely sincere; they intended exactly what they said; no {\i ta\u8217'ut} occurred. That a future event later made the vow inconvenient or regrettable does not mean the vow was made in error. The question of {\i matir} and {\i osrim} in the {\i nolad} cases thus appears to concern not merely the proper {\i beit din} procedure but the very validity of the annulment {\i b\u8217'dieved} \u8212- whether it works at all even after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Expected vs.\u160 ?Unexpected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth being precise about the scope of the debate. Not all future events fall into the category of disputed {\i nolad}. There is an important distinction between events that, while they had not yet occurred at the time of the vow, were nevertheless reasonably foreseeable \u8212- things the person could and arguably should have been thinking about \u8212- and events that no one could have expected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For example: if someone makes a vow in winter that he will not enter a bathhouse, and the {\i beit din} asks, \u8220"had you thought about how hot the summer becomes and how much you would need to bathe?\u8221" \u8212- well, summer always comes. That is something one could reasonably have anticipated. Or if a child\u8217's admission to a particular institution was a genuinely realistic possibility that the person should have factored in \u8212- even though it had not yet occurred \u8212- that too might be something one could reasonably have expected. Everyone agrees that events which were reasonably foreseeable, even if they had not yet taken place at the moment of the vow, may be used as a basis for {\i hatarat nedarim}, since they were things the person plausibly could have thought about at the time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i chachamim} is specifically about events that the person had {\i no good reason} to expect \u8212- genuine surprises. Here the {\i chachamim} insist: this does not speak to your frame of mind at the time, and it cannot undo a sincere vow. Rabbi Eliezer disagrees and permits even this.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Dishonesty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the analytical question: what exactly is the reason behind the {\i chachamim}\u8217's ruling that you may not open with {\i kavod aviv v\u8217'imo} or {\i kavod hamakom}? The {\i Gemara} records a dispute between Abaye and Rava. {\i Amar Abaye: im kein ein nedarim nitarim yafeh} \u8212- Abaye says that the problem is that {\i nedarim} will not be properly released. What does this mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Explains Abaye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} explains Abaye\u8217's position clearly. Even in a case where a person in fact would not have been deterred by {\i kavod hamakom} \u8212- even someone who took the vow fully aware that the rabbis frowned upon vowing, and would have done so regardless \u8212- such a person, once he is standing before a {\i chacham} in {\i beit din}, will not have the {\i chutzpah} to say so. {\i Lo ya\u8217'iz panav b\u8217'khach lifnei chacham v\u8217'yeshaker} \u8212- he will not have the audacity to say outright, \u8220"Yes, I knew it was not religiously ideal, I knew it was an offense to God, and I would have done it anyway.\u8221" He will lie. {\i V\u8217'yomar ilu hayiti yode\u8217'a lo hayiti noder} \u8212- he will say he would not have made the vow, whether or not that is true. {\i Nimtza she-ein nedarim nitarim yafeh} \u8212- the result is that {\i nedarim} are not properly released, because the annulments are built on false pretenses. The person does not genuinely meet the conditions for {\i hatara}, and yet the {\i beit din} grants it. That is why we do not raise {\i kavod hamakom} or {\i kavod aviv v\u8217'imo} as a general {\i petach} \u8212- not because they would never be genuine, but because in practice you cannot trust the answer you will receive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Beit Din Undermined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, by contrast, offers a different explanation: {\i im kein ein nedarim nishalin l\u8217'chacham} \u8212- if such a universal {\i petach} were available, no one would bother coming to a {\i chacham} at all. Since {\i kavod hamakom} applies to every {\i neder} without exception, people would simply conclude that they do not need a {\i beit din} to release them from their vows. They would reason: I can invoke this on my own \u8212- I wasn\u8217't thinking about the religious impropriety of vowing, therefore I never really meant it, and I am hereby released. And so the institution of {\i hatarat nedarim} \u8212- which requires going before a {\i beit din} or a {\i chacham} \u8212- would collapse entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requires a brief clarification. There are indeed cases where a person does not need to appear before a {\i beit din} to be released from a {\i neder}. These include cases of {\i nidrei ta\u8217'ut} in the strict sense \u8212- where from the very beginning the {\i neder} was based on a clear factual error, or where the person was merely haggling and never genuinely intended the vow \u8212- or where the stated condition of the vow has been met, or where the object of the vow falls outside its proper scope. In all these cases, the {\i neder} either was never binding or ceased to be binding on its own terms. But when all the formal conditions are met \u8212- when the person was sincere, when the vow was genuinely intended, and when we are retroactively examining the depth of that intention through the process of {\i hatarat nedarim} \u8212- that process requires a {\i beit din}. It does not happen automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's concern, then, is that by making a universal {\i petach} available, we would lead people to believe they can bypass the required procedure. They would {\i matir} their own {\i nedarim} without ever appearing before a {\i beit din}, undermining the integrity of the institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Of the two positions, Rava\u8217's seems closer in language to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's own formulation \u8212- the concern that {\i im kein ein nedarim} points to the structural collapse of the institution, not merely to the problem of dishonest answers in individual cases. At the same time, both Abaye and Rava are, in their respective ways, concerned with the integrity of the judicial process and the authority of the {\i chachamim} within it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's concession \u8212- that the {\i chachamim} agree with Rabbi Eliezer when the vow directly impacts the parents \u8212- and asks how this fits with each of the two positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Abaye, who holds that the concern is dishonesty, the exception is readily explained. {\i Hacha, keivan d\u8217'achtzif lei, achtzif lei} \u8212- here, since the person had the audacity to take a vow that directly and knowingly affected his parents, we can expect an honest answer from him. If someone explicitly makes a {\i neder} that his parents shall not benefit from him \u8212- knowing full well that his parents are involved \u8212- and still goes ahead with it, then when the {\i beit din} subsequently asks him, \u8220"Had you truly thought through how this would affect your relationship with your parents, would you have done this?\u8221" we are not afraid that raising the parents will produce a dishonest response. This person demonstrated, by his very willingness to make the vow, that he is not someone who automatically backs down when his parents are invoked. If he now says he would not have made the vow, that answer is credible. The concern about dishonesty that applies in the general case does not apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rava, who holds that the concern is that people will feel they can bypass the {\i beit din}, the exception requires a different explanation. One might worry: once people hear that the {\i beit din} raises {\i kavod aviv v\u8217'imo} in cases where the vow impacts the parents, won\u8217't they start thinking they can use this as a general formula and release themselves on their own? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i keivan d\u8217'khol nidrei lo sagi lehu belo chacham, hacha nami potchin} \u8212- since for all other {\i nedarim} the person knows that you cannot get out of it without appearing before a {\i chacham}, the existence of this one narrow exception \u8212- specifically for cases where the vow directly involved the parents \u8212- will not give rise to the impression that there is a blanket license to invoke parents universally and bypass the {\i beit din}. The exception is too specific and too recognizable as singular to produce that effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe\u8217's Oath Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the prooftext that underlies Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position on {\i nolad}. {\i Amar Rav Chisda de\u8217'amar kra: ki meisu kol ha\u8217'anashim ham\u8217'vakshim et nafshecha} \u8212- Rav Chisda cites the verse in which God tells Moshe to return to Egypt, assuring him that all the men who had sought his life had died. The background is as follows. The {\i Gemara} earlier noted that the word {\i vayo\u8217'el} \u8212- describing Moshe\u8217's acceptance of Yisro\u8217's hospitality \u8212- carries the connotation of an oath, as in the term {\i alah}. Moshe had sworn to Yisro that he would remain with him and not return to Egypt. How, then, could he later return? God releases him from that oath by saying: the reason you took the oath is no longer operative. You swore to stay because there were people in Egypt who wanted to kill you; they have now died; therefore, had you known at the time of your oath that they would die, you never would have sworn to remain \u8212- and on that basis, the oath is annulled. This, says Rav Chisda, is the Scriptural source for Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling that {\i potchin b\u8217'nolad} \u8212- we may open with an event that occurred after the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabanan Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} challenge this reading. {\i Kasvari hanhu mi meisi?} \u8212- Who says those people actually died? The verse says they died, but perhaps that is not the plain meaning. And in fact, {\i Rav Chisda} is reading the verse as supporting {\i nolad}, but the {\i rabanan} who disagree with Rabbi Eliezer would need to explain the verse differently. The challenge is: did these people literally die, or is something else going on?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan ID\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} invokes a teaching of Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: {\i kol makom she-ne\u8217'emar nitzim v\u8217'nitzavim, einam ela Datan v\u8217'Aviram} \u8212- wherever the Torah uses the language {\i nitzim} (fighting/standing defiantly) or {\i nitzavim} (standing in confrontation), it refers to none other than Datan and Aviram. The connection runs as follows. When Moshe first fled Egypt, it was after he saw {\i shnei anashim Ivrim nitzim} \u8212- two Hebrews fighting \u8212- and one of them challenged him: {\i mi samcha l\u8217'ish sar v\u8217'shofet aleinu?} \u8212- who appointed you as a prince and judge over us? Later, when Moshe returns to Egypt and confronts Pharaoh, we read that the people were {\i nitzavim likratam b\u8217'tzetam me-Paroh}, standing to meet them as they emerged from Pharaoh\u8217's presence, accusing Moshe of making their situation worse. Both the confrontational posture and the language of standing in defiance are parallel. And since at the time of Korach\u8217's rebellion it says of Datan and Aviram {\i yatzu nitzavim}, Rabbi Yochanan identifies all these figures as one and the same: Datan and Aviram.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This identification creates a problem for the straightforward reading of {\i ki meisu kol ha\u8217'anashim}. If the people who were out to get Moshe at the beginning were Datan and Aviram, and Datan and Aviram are still very much alive at the time of the Korach episode \u8212- which occurs well after Moshe\u8217's return to Egypt \u8212- then it cannot be literally true that they died. One might naturally have thought that the {\i anashim} seeking Moshe\u8217's life were Pharaoh and his officials; but even if we accept this identification, the problem remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish: Poor = Dea\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish resolves the difficulty: {\i she-yardu mi-nichseihem} \u8212- they did not literally die; they became poor and lost their wealth and influence. And this, Reish Lakish explains, constitutes a kind of death \u8212- becoming poor is akin to dying. This resolution serves two purposes simultaneously: it explains why the verse says {\i meisu} when they did not literally die, and it introduces the broader rabbinic concept that certain conditions in life render a person as though dead. This becomes the point of departure for the {\i baraita} that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Like Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'tanya: arba\u8217'ah chashuvin k\u8217'meis} \u8212- a {\i baraita} teaches that four categories of people are considered as though dead: the poor ({\i ani}), the leper ({\i metzora}), the blind ({\i suma}), and the childless ({\i mi she-ein lo banim}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ani (Poor)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for the {\i ani} is the verse just cited: {\i ki meisu kol ha\u8217'anashim} \u8212- \u8220"all the men died,\u8221" referring, as Reish Lakish explained, to Datan and Aviram who became impoverished. The poor person becomes powerless in society \u8212- without means, without influence, without the respect that accompanies wealth and standing. This social death is real enough to be described in the language of actual death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora (Leper)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i metzora} is derived from the verse {\i al na tehi k\u8217'meis} \u8212- \u8220"let her not be like dead,\u8221" spoken in the context of {\i tzara\u8217'at}. The {\i metzora} is cast out of the community, expelled from the camp or city, cut off from normal social interaction. The physical affliction itself \u8212- the skin turned white and pallid \u8212- visually evokes the appearance of a corpse. And the communal isolation that {\i tzara\u8217'at} necessitates is, in effect, a social death: the afflicted individual is excluded from the living community of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suma (Blind)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The blind person ({\i suma}) is derived from the verse: {\i b\u8217'machshakim hoshivani k\u8217'meisei olam} \u8212- \u8220"He has made me dwell in darkness, like the eternally dead.\u8221" The association between death and darkness \u8212- the dead descending into the dark netherworld \u8212- is ancient and pervasive. To live in perpetual darkness is, in a profound sense, to share the condition of the dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blind vs.\u160 ?Other Disabili\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a striking question. Why does the {\i Gemara} identify specifically the {\i suma} as falling into this category, rather than, say, the {\i cheireish} \u8212- the one who cannot hear or speak \u8212- whose {\i halachic} status is in many ways even more restrictive, as such a person has historically not been considered a {\i bar da\u8217'at} in the full sense? Other disabilities might seem, from a practical standpoint, to have rendered life even more constrained. The likely answer is that the {\i Gemara}\u8217's identification here is driven primarily by the {\i pasuk} and its imagery: the verse explicitly links darkness and death, and blindness is the condition that most directly instantiates that imagery. It is the poetic and textual resonance of darkness-as-death that places the {\i suma} in this list, even if other conditions might seem to compete on practical grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Childless\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The childless person is derived from Rachel\u8217's cry to Yaakov: {\i hava li banim v\u8217'im ayin meisa anochi} \u8212- \u8220"Give me children, or else I am dead.\u8221" The death here is of a different character from the others \u8212- it is not social exclusion or physical affliction, but the absence of continuity. A person lives on through their children; children are the means by which one persists beyond one\u8217's own death. To have no children is to face a kind of absolute ending, a death with no continuation. The world one built, the life one lived, simply stops \u8212- there is no one to carry it forward. This sense of radical discontinuity is what earns the childless person a place among those considered {\i k\u8217'meis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} thus ties together the thread that began with Reish Lakish\u8217's observation about Datan and Aviram: {\i misa} can mean more than literal death. The four conditions enumerated here all represent forms of loss \u8212- of power, of community, of perception, of future \u8212- that the tradition regards as bearing the essential quality of death, even while the person yet lives.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 65\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vows for Another Person\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Benefit Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the top of 65a, continuing the discussion of the {\i mishnah} concerning {\i petach} \u8212- the \u8220"opening\u8221" used to release someone from a vow. The {\i gemara} had already addressed whether one may use {\i kvod ha-makom} or {\i kvod aviv ve-imo} as the basis for a {\i petach}, and whether a {\i nolad} \u8212- an unforeseen new circumstance \u8212- may serve as the grounds for retroactive {\i charata}. Before returning to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's treatment of {\i nolad}, the {\i gemara} introduces an important digression concerning {\i hatarat nedarim} and the role of a third party when a vow is made for another person\u8217's sake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: {\i ha-mudar hana\u8217'ah me-chaveiro} \u8212- one who has vowed not to derive benefit from a friend \u8212- {\i ein matirin lo ella be-fanav}: the vow cannot be released except in the presence of that friend. The {\i baraita} does not immediately explain the reason, and the {\i gemara} proceeds to illuminate it through scriptural analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe & Yitro Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i minani milei} \u8212- from where do we derive this rule? Rav Nachman answers that it is sourced from the verse: {\i va-yomer Hashem el Moshe be-Midyan lekh shuv Mitzrayim ki meitu kol ha-anashim ha-mevakshim et nafshekha} \u8212- \u8220"And God said to Moshe in Midyan: go, return to Egypt, for all the men who sought your life have died\u8221" (Shemot 4:19). The {\i gemara} had already discussed on the previous daf that this verse provided Moshe with the {\i petach} for the vow he had made to Yitro \u8212- more precisely a {\i shevua}, as indicated by the use of the word {\i va-yo\u8217'el}, which the {\i gemara} reads as an expression of oath. Now the focus turns to the phrase {\i be-Midyan}: God told Moshe that he had taken his vow {\i in Midyan}, and therefore must go and release his vow {\i in Midyan} \u8212- that is, before Yitro himself. The basis for the release already existed \u8212- {\i ki meitu kol ha-anashim ha-mevakshim et nafshekha} \u8212- but that release could not take effect unless Moshe returned to Yitro and appeared before him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic begins to emerge. When a vow is made as an expression of commitment to another person \u8212- as Moshe\u8217's {\i shevua} to Yitro was an undertaking made {\i for Yitro\u8217's benefit}, as a promise to remain with him \u8212- that other person is not merely background context. The vow is, in a meaningful sense, a commitment {\i to} that person, much as a {\i teshuvah} for a sin {\i bein adam le-chaveiro} requires not only reconciliation with God but with the person wronged. Even after the {\i beit din} provides a technical {\i heter}, the interpersonal dimension demands that the other party be brought into the process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nebuchadnezzar Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites the verse from Divrei Hayamim: {\i ve-gam be-melech Nevuchadnezzar marad asher hishbio be-Elohim} \u8212- \u8220"and he also rebelled against King Nevuchadnezzar, who had made him swear by God.\u8221" The Talmud here unfolds a dramatic narrative around this verse. Tzidkiyahu came upon Nevuchadnezzar eating a live rabbit \u8212- something deeply embarrassing \u8212- and Nevuchadnezzar compelled Tzidkiyahu to swear an oath not to reveal it. Tzidkiyahu took the oath, but later became tormented by his desire to publicize it: according to Rashi, he wanted to damage Nevuchadnezzar\u8217's reputation. He therefore went before a {\i beit din} and obtained a {\i heter} for his {\i shevua}. The {\i beit din} released him \u8212- perhaps on the grounds that he could not have anticipated how anguished he would be by the oath \u8212- and Tzidkiyahu proceeded to reveal the embarrassing episode, causing Nevuchadnezzar to be publicly denigrated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevuchadnezzar, upon hearing what had happened, summoned the {\i Sanhedrin} and Tzidkiyahu. He confronted them: had Tzidkiyahu not sworn in the name of Heaven not to reveal this? The {\i Sanhedrin} replied that he had obtained a {\i heter}. Nevuchadnezzar was stunned \u8212- he had not known that oaths could be released \u8212- and then asked the critical question: {\i be-fanav o afilu shelo be-fanav?} \u8212- must this release be done in the presence of the person to whom the oath was made, or may it be done even without his presence? When told {\i be-fanav} \u8212- in his presence \u8212- Nevuchadnezzar turned on the {\i Sanhedrin} and demanded: why did you not tell Tzidkiyahu that he could not obtain a release without appearing before {\i me}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mi-yad yashvu la-aretz yadmu ziknei bat Tziyon} \u8212- immediately the elders of Zion sat on the ground in silence. Rav Yitzchak explains that they removed the cushions from beneath them and sat on bare ground in a posture of mourning, recognizing that their failure had set catastrophic events in motion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} is genuinely striking. Until this point, the entire machinery of {\i heter nedarim} had been presented as a matter between a person and God \u8212- a technical halachic mechanism, like other rules of {\i Shabbos} or {\i issur ve-heter}, where the law has its own internal logic and the fact that one found a release was simply the operation of that system. But this narrative ruptures that framing. When a vow or {\i shevua} functions as a commitment to another person, the ease with which one can extract a release becomes morally troubling. How can one make a binding oath to another party and then simply slip away through a technical {\i heter}? The {\i gemara} insists that in such cases we must, at a minimum, require that the other person be present \u8212- both to force the one seeking release to confront the person to whom the commitment was made, and to impose a degree of social weight and embarrassment that puts the brakes on too easy an escape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran engages in a careful analysis of the rule and its underlying rationale. He begins by citing one view: the requirement of {\i be-fanav} applies only when the vow was made {\i le-tzorech chaveiro} \u8212- specifically for the sake of the other person, as in Moshe\u8217's vow to Yitro or Tzidkiyahu\u8217's oath to Nevuchadnezzar. A {\i neder} that was not made for another person\u8217's sake could, according to this position, be released even in that person\u8217's absence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran then notes that the question turns on a dispute found in the Yerushalmi regarding the reason for the rule. The first rationale offered there is {\i mipnei ha-busha} \u8212- on account of embarrassment. Since the vow was made for the other person\u8217's benefit and now the vow-maker wants to be released from it, he must appear before that person and be embarrassed. This embarrassment functions as a deterrent, adding gravity to any such release. Crucially, this rationale applies only when the vow was made for the sake of the other person \u8212- only then does embarrassment before {\i him specifically} make sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second rationale in the Yerushalmi is {\i mipnei ha-chashad} \u8212- on account of suspicion. If the other person, before whom I vowed not to derive benefit, later sees me deriving benefit from his property, he will suspect me of violating my vow, not knowing I obtained a {\i heter}. Under this rationale, the requirement has nothing to do with whether the vow was made for the other person\u8217's sake; it applies any time the vow concerns the other person\u8217's property or domain, simply because he might see the vow-maker acting in what appears to be a prohibited manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran draws out the practical differences. According to the {\i busha} rationale, {\i be-fanav} is strictly required \u8212- only genuine personal presence produces the embarrassment that the rule demands. According to the {\i chashad} rationale, the requirement can be satisfied in other ways: if the {\i beit din} sends word to the other party notifying him that the vow has been released, the suspicion is dispelled and physical presence is unnecessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran then turns to the question that lies at the heart of the matter: what is the other person\u8217's actual legal status? If the vow was made for his sake, does he have the power to {\i refuse} to release the vow-maker? This would mirror the structure of {\i teshuvah bein adam le-chaveiro}, where the wronged party must grant {\i mechila}. The Ran cites Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's position that where the {\i neder} was made for another person\u8217's benefit, a release obtained {\i shelo mi-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- without that person\u8217's consent \u8212- is in question, and he raises the possibility that {\i bedi\u8217'avad} it may not work at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran examines the Tzidkiyahu narrative for guidance. One reading is that since the {\i Sanhedrin} released Tzidkiyahu\u8217's oath and he was then able to reveal the secret, the {\i heter} must have worked {\i bedi\u8217'avad} \u8212- which would suggest the rule is only {\i lechatchila}. But the opposing view cites the very verse that introduces the story \u8212- {\i asher hishbio be-Elohim} \u8212- as implying that Tzidkiyahu was indeed punished for violating his oath, suggesting the {\i heter} did not work and the oath remained binding. The Ra\u8217'avad supports this stricter reading, pointing to the full catastrophe of the {\i churban} that followed as evidence of the gravity of what was done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran concludes without fully resolving the question, though his analysis tends toward the stricter position \u8212- that when a {\i neder} or {\i shevua} is made as a commitment to another person, that person becomes in some meaningful sense a party to it, and their consent may be required for any release to be effective even {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. This represents a remarkable extension: the ordinarily bilateral world of {\i nedarim}, which operates between a person and God, has been opened to admit a third party \u8212- the human being to whom the commitment was made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot adds a further observation on the mechanism. The {\i baraita} says {\i be-fanav} \u8212- in his presence \u8212- but does not say {\i bi-reshuto} \u8212- with his permission. This suggests that what is required is presence, not consent. Moreover, Tosafot raises a logical difficulty: if the vow-maker initiated the vow entirely on his own, on what basis does the other person acquire any legal power over its release? The mechanism does not easily support the conclusion that another person can hold a vow in place against the vow-maker\u8217's will. The distinction between {\i shevua} and {\i neder} is relevant here as well: a {\i shevua} is by its nature interpersonally oriented \u8212- one can be {\i mashbia} another, compel another to swear \u8212- whereas a {\i neder} is always self-initiated. To import the logic of one into the other is a significant conceptual step, and while the {\i gemara} does so, the Rishonim flag the tension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kenolad v\u8217'Einan Kenolad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i mishnah} and its treatment of {\i nolad}. The next {\i mishnah} introduces a category that Rabbi Meir calls things that are {\i ke-nolad ve-einam ke-nolad} \u8212- they resemble a {\i nolad} event but are not truly {\i nolad} \u8212- and may therefore serve as valid grounds for release even though the Sages disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} gives examples. A man says: {\i konam} \u8212- the fruit of the world is forbidden to me \u8212- were I to marry this woman, because her father is wicked. They then tell him: her father died, or he did {\i teshuvah}. Or: {\i konam} \u8212- I will not enter this house \u8212- because it contains a vicious dog, or a snake. They then tell him: the dog died, or the snake was killed. In all these cases, Rabbi Meir rules that release is permissible: {\i harei hen ke-nolad ve-einan ke-nolad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason these cases qualify under Rabbi Meir\u8217's view, despite resembling {\i nolad}, is that the vow-maker explicitly articulated his reason within the vow itself. He didn\u8217't simply make a general prohibition \u8212- he stated {\i why} he was making the vow. Since he embedded the reason in the vow\u8217's formulation, Rabbi Meir reads the vow as if it were {\i conditional}: he forbade the thing only insofar as the stated reason remained applicable. When the reason disappears \u8212- the father dies, the dog is killed \u8212- the {\i konam} lapses on its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages, however, refuse to read the vow as a {\i tenai} simply because a reason was articulated. In their view, the person stated his mindset at the time, but made an unconditional vow. The subsequent change of circumstances is a {\i nolad}, and if {\i nolad} is not a valid basis for release, this case cannot be permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nolad or Error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what is the precise nature of Rabbi Meir\u8217's ruling? Is this a case of {\i nolad} \u8212- the circumstances genuinely changed after the vow \u8212- or is it a case of {\i taut} \u8212- error \u8212- meaning the facts were already different from what the vow-maker believed at the time he made the vow? The distinction matters for how we understand the debate and for how the {\i mishnah} relates to a later {\i mishnah} that everyone agrees governs a case of clear {\i taut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna: Conditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna answers: {\i na\u8217'aseh ke-mi she-hisnah nidro be-davar} \u8212- the vow is treated as though he made it conditional on the stated circumstance. This means the case in our {\i mishnah} is indeed one of {\i nolad}: the father genuinely was wicked when the vow was made, the conditions truly changed afterward. Rabbi Meir\u8217's innovation is simply to read the vow {\i as if} he had formulated it as a {\i tenai}, because he articulated the reason explicitly. The Sages decline to read it that way. On Rav Huna\u8217's account, the later {\i mishnah} \u8212- which addresses a case of clear {\i taut} \u8212- represents an entirely different scenario and is not in tension with our {\i mishnah}, since each addresses its own category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan: Died Alr\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, however, reads the case differently: {\i u-kvar meis} \u8212- the father had {\i already} died before the vow was made. The vow-maker simply did not know it. Under this reading, the case is one of {\i taut} even in our {\i mishnah}, not {\i nolad}, since the facts were wrong from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an obvious difficulty: if it is a plain {\i taut}, why would the Sages disagree? A clear factual error should be uncontroversial grounds for release. Tosafot addresses this by offering a reading of Rabbi Meir\u8217's position under Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's interpretation: Rabbi Meir is not simply saying the case is {\i taut} and permissible. Rather, Rabbi Meir is responding to a concern that one might apply a {\i gezeirah} and prohibit even this case, lest people confuse it with a scenario where the father {\i died after} the vow \u8212- which would be a genuine {\i nolad} and should not serve as grounds for release. Rabbi Meir says: we need not make such a {\i gezeirah} here, because the difference between the two cases is clear enough that confusion is unlikely. The Sages, by contrast, hold that since the scenarios are superficially similar \u8212- in both cases the vow was made because of the father\u8217's wickedness and the father is now dead \u8212- people {\i will} confuse them, and we therefore prohibit even the {\i taut} case to prevent it from being mistaken for {\i nolad}. This is how Tosafot reconciles Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reading with the existence of a genuine Rabbinic dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Beauty Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a challenge from a later {\i mishnah}: {\i konam she-eini nosei le-plonis ke-urah} \u8212- a {\i konam} that I will not marry this woman, who is ugly \u8212- and it turns out she was beautiful; or he thought she was dark-skinned and she was fair-skinned; or thought she was short and she was tall. In such cases the {\i halacha} is {\i mutar bah} \u8212- he may marry her. The {\i mishnah} explicitly states: {\i ella she-ha-neder taut} \u8212- because the vow was an error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that on Rav Huna\u8217's reading, this works out neatly: our {\i mishnah} addresses cases where the vow-maker treated the articulated reason as a {\i tenai}, and the later {\i mishnah} addresses a straightforward case of {\i taut} \u8212- two distinct scenarios requiring two distinct rulings. But on Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reading, since both {\i mishnayot} address {\i taut}, the repetition becomes difficult: {\i lama li le-mitni tartei zimnei neder taut} \u8212- why state the rule of erroneous vows twice? The {\i gemara} marks this as a {\i kashya} and does not resolve it cleanly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One observation worth noting: according to Rabbi Yochanan, the {\i gezeirah} against {\i nolad} that the Sages invoke would presumably be limited to cases where confusion with a genuine {\i nolad} scenario is a realistic concern \u8212- for instance, where the stated condition could plausibly change over time. The later {\i mishnah}, by contrast, involves mistaking someone\u8217's appearance, which admits of no {\i nolad} analogue, and would therefore be {\i divrei ha-kol}. The {\i gemara} does not explicitly draw this inference, but it follows naturally from the logic of the dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Petach: Prohibited Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i ve-od amar Rabbi Meir} \u8212- Rabbi Meir further stated that one may open a {\i petach} for the release of a vow by invoking the subject\u8217's violation of a prohibition written in the {\i Torah}. The {\i gemara} cites the rule: {\i potchin lo min ha-katuv she-ba-Torah} \u8212- one opens based on what is written in the {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beit din} asks the person: had you known that by taking this vow you were transgressing {\i lo tikom ve-lo titor} \u8212- the prohibitions against vengeance and bearing a grudge (Vayikra 19:18) \u8212- or the prohibition {\i ve-ahavta le-rei\u8217'acha kamocha}, or {\i lo tisna et achicha bi-lvavecha}, or {\i ve-chai achicha imach} \u8212- the obligation to sustain one\u8217's impoverished fellow Jew \u8212- would you still have made this vow? If he answers that he would not, {\i harei ze mutar} \u8212- the vow is released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a contextually significant ruling. The {\i mishnah} earlier in the tractate had established that one may not use certain universal openings \u8212- {\i kvod ha-makom} or {\i kvod aviv ve-imo} \u8212- because they are so broad that everyone would be able to release their own vows, or because the framing would compel a dishonest answer. Rabbi Meir permits this category, however, because the focus on specific prohibitions that directly relate to the content and motivation of the vow is narrower and more honest. The person taking a vow of spite or revenge genuinely might not have considered that he was thereby violating {\i lo tikom} or {\i lo titor}, and the question is therefore a real one that can elicit a sincere response.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection to Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: is it truly plausible to claim ignorance of Torah prohibitions? Can a person genuinely say he would not have taken a vow had he known it constituted a transgression? This is a subtle point. The concern with certain earlier {\i petachim} was that the framing would essentially guarantee a particular answer, generating dishonest responses and turning the process into a formality. But here, the argument runs, people sometimes act impulsively in moments of anger or spite without consciously connecting their actions to the prohibitions they are violating. The question {\i ilu hayita yodea she-atah over} \u8212- had you known you were transgressing \u8212- is therefore genuinely informative and capable of receiving an honest answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might still ask: shouldn\u8217't anyone who fears heaven already know he is violating these prohibitions? And if he knows it and did it anyway, is the {\i petach} still legitimate? The {\i gemara} leaves the ruling in Rabbi Meir\u8217's name without explicitly indicating that the Sages dispute it here, though the attribution to Rabbi Meir alone may imply a dispute. The force of the ruling rests on the recognition that people act in moments of passion without deliberate weighing of halachic consequences, and the question invites them to step back and reflect honestly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gabbayim Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Avdimi raises a challenge against the use of {\i ve-chai achicha imach} as a {\i petach}. He suggests that the person seeking release from his vow might simply say: even granting that this person has become poor, my obligation does not fall to me alone. Whatever falls to me proportionally, along with the rest of the community, I will contribute through the communal {\i gabbayim}. I do not have a personal, individualized responsibility to support this particular person \u8212- let him receive his share from the general charitable distribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Newly Poor Person\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds to this argument: {\i ani omer kol ha-nofel eino nofel li-dei gabbai techilah} \u8212- a person who descends into poverty for the first time does not first turn to the communal {\i gabbay}. Someone already established in the charitable system may rely on it, but when a person first becomes poor, he turns to his friends and relatives \u8212- those who know him personally. In this case, given that there was clearly a prior relationship between the vow-maker and the person against whom the vow was directed, the newly impoverished individual would naturally have turned to him first. If the vow-maker is bound by his {\i neder} and cannot help him at that critical moment, he would thereby transgress {\i ve-chai achicha imach}. This closes the objection and restores the legitimacy of using that prohibition as a {\i petach}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening: Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Opening Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now states: {\i potchin le-adam bi-ktuvatat ishto} \u8212- one may open a release from a vow by confronting the vow-maker with the implications of his wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} obligation. When a person\u8217's vow leads toward divorce, the {\i beit din} may ask: had you known what you would be required to pay out under her {\i ketubah}, would you still have made this vow?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man\u8217's Vow Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents a concrete case: a man vowed that his wife could derive no benefit from him \u8212- {\i nadar me-ishto hana\u8217'ah}. Her {\i ketubah} was worth four hundred {\i dinarim}, an exceptionally large sum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He came before Rabbi Akiva, and Rabbi Akiva ruled: {\i chiyavo liten lah kesuvatah} \u8212- you are obligated to pay her full {\i ketubah}. The man protested: his father had left him only eight hundred {\i dinarim}, of which his brother took four hundred and he took four hundred. Was it not enough, he asked, that she should receive two hundred and he keep two hundred? Rabbi Akiva was unmoved: {\i afilu atah mocher se\u8217'ar roshecha} \u8212- even if you had to sell the hair of your head, {\i atah noten lah kesuvatah} \u8212- you must pay her full {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vower Released\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The man then said: {\i ilu hayiti yodea she-hu kein, lo hayiti noder} \u8212- had I known this would be the consequence, I would never have made this vow. Rabbi Akiva thereupon released him from his vow. The case is remarkable because the {\i petach} was not arranged artificially by the {\i beit din} in anticipation of seeking a release; it emerged organically from the man\u8217's direct encounter with the halachic ruling about his {\i ketubah} obligation, producing a genuinely sincere expression of regret.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chattels & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a technical difficulty with the narrative: {\i metaltelin mi mishta\u8217'abdei li-ktuva?} \u8212- are chattels encumbered for the {\i ketubah}? The standard rule is that a {\i ketubah}, like a debt, creates a lien only on {\i karka} \u8212- real property \u8212- and not on {\i metaltelin} \u8212- movable goods. If so, why did Rabbi Akiva say the man would have to sell the hair of his head? Hair is chattel, not real estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore suggests that the figure of eight hundred {\i dinarim} left by the father refers not to liquid cash but to {\i karka} worth eight hundred {\i dinarim}. The brother took land worth four hundred and the man retained land worth four hundred \u8212- exactly the value of the {\i ketubah}. This accounts for the {\i ketubah}\u8217's collection from real property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} then presses: the {\i mishnah} explicitly mentions selling the hair of one\u8217's head. Does this not imply that chattel must also go toward paying the {\i ketubah}? The {\i gemara} works through several approaches, including the general principle that {\i avadim hukshu le-karkaot} \u8212- servants are legally analogous to real property \u8212- but this does not directly resolve the question of hair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Sale Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty with a clarification offered by Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak: {\i lomar she-ein mekori\u8217'in shetar ketubah} \u8212- the purpose of the phrase is to convey that the {\i ketubah} must be paid in full without reduction, not that the man\u8217's hair itself would literally be sold to pay it. The expression {\i afilu atah mocher se\u8217'ar roshecha} means: even if full payment would leave you so impoverished that you would need to sell whatever you had \u8212- the hair off your head \u8212- just to put food on the table, the obligation to pay her complete {\i ketubah} nonetheless stands. Rabbi Akiva was not saying that chattel is encumbered for the {\i ketubah}; he was expressing the absoluteness of the obligation in vivid, hyperbolic terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also addresses a further inference the {\i gemara} considered: {\i shema mina ein mesadrin le-ba\u8217'al chov} \u8212- perhaps Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling implies that we do not provide the debtor with an installment arrangement, allowing him to retain enough to sustain himself while paying off the debt gradually. This principle, known as {\i mesadrin le-ba\u8217'al chov}, generally governs ordinary creditors. But here too, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's language is not establishing a rule about installments; he is emphasizing that the {\i ketubah} amount itself cannot be negotiated downward. The man\u8217's impoverishment after full payment is his own consequence to bear; what cannot happen is a reduction of the {\i ketubah} amount that she is owed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 66\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Petach Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro & Petach Theme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 66 continues the theme of {\i petach} \u8212- the opening or grounds through which a {\i chacham} releases a person from a {\i neder}. The preceding {\i mishnayos} have already established that the ideal {\i petach} is something highly personal, specific to the individual\u8217's circumstances. Nevertheless, the {\i mishna} here addresses circumstances that are more generic \u8212- framings broad enough to apply to many people \u8212- which could also afford a valid basis for saying that, had the person thought of those circumstances at the time, he would not have made his {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond practical advice, there is a conceptual {\i chiddush} embedded in these {\i mishnayos}. One might think that a {\i petach} so universal becomes self-defeating in two ways: first, that it renders nedarim trivially escapable, thereby undermining their weight; and second, that it creates conditions under which people will give untruthful responses to secure their release. These were precisely the concerns raised regarding {\i bichvod aviv ve-imo}, honor of one\u8217's father and mother \u8212- that concern being both the ubiquity of the circumstance and the embarrassment that would prevent anyone from honestly admitting they had been indifferent to parental honor. The {\i chiddush} of the upcoming {\i mishnayos} is therefore that the circumstances discussed, while genuinely broad, do not trigger those concerns.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir vs.\u160 ?Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim} over {\i bichvod aviv ve-imo} as a {\i petach} turned on precisely these two concerns: universality and the risk of dishonest responses. No one would brazenly admit to having been indifferent to parental honor, and so using it as a {\i petach} was feared to produce false attestations. The cases discussed in the present {\i mishna}, by contrast, pass that threshold \u8212- they are general enough to be genuinely useful but not so universal or weighted that they would produce dishonesty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universal Petachim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i petachim} that are too universal and those that are appropriately broad is thus a live one throughout this section of the {\i gemara}. A {\i petach} that applies to literally everyone in every situation risks making nedarim feel meaningless; a {\i petach} tailored to something genuinely relevant to this person\u8217's situation remains halakhically sound and does not invite false testimony.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yom Tov & Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yom Tov Petach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens: {\i potchin beyomim tovim u-vshabbatos} \u8212- one may open with the circumstance of {\i yom tov} and {\i Shabbos}. Since the paradigmatic {\i neder} involves forbidden benefit from food, the {\i chacham} asks the person: had you realized that this {\i neder} would interfere with your full enjoyment of {\i Shabbos} and {\i yom tov}, would you have made it? While it is admittedly difficult to claim one was unaware that the calendar contains a weekly {\i Shabbos}, many people making a {\i neder} in a moment of anger or distress are not thinking about an upcoming {\i yom tov}. If the person responds that he would not have made the {\i neder} had he thought about it, that constitutes a valid basis for release.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Addition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran adds a further dimension: beyond simply pointing out that {\i Shabbos} and {\i yom tov} would be impacted, one also emphasizes to the person that refraining from the forbidden item on those days would constitute a failure to fully discharge his {\i obligation} of {\i oneg Shabbos}. As the Ran frames it: {\i de-ilu haya yode\u8217'a she-assur le-hitzta\u8217'er be-Shabbos} \u8212- had he known it is forbidden to afflict himself on {\i Shabbos}. The dual emphasis \u8212- practical inconvenience and religious obligation \u8212- increases the likelihood of an honest response while also reflecting the genuine weight of what the person failed to consider.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Berishona Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now introduces a historically and halakhically significant point: {\i berishona hayu omrim} \u8212- originally, they used to say something different. In the earlier practice, if a {\i petach} was found through the circumstance of {\i Shabbos} and {\i yom tov}, the release applied only to those days: {\i otan hayamim mutarim, u-shear kol hayamim assurim} \u8212- those days were permitted and all other days remained forbidden. The underlying logic was that the person\u8217's {\i neder} was, in retrospect, made {\i be-ta\u8217'us} only insofar as it included {\i Shabbos} and {\i yom tov}; the remainder of the {\i neder} was fully intended and therefore binding. The practical result was a kind of virtual reconstruction: what the person would have said, had he been thinking, was \u8220"meat and wine are forbidden to me on all days {\i except} Shabbos and yom tov.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This earlier practice was revised by Rabbi Akiva, who taught: {\i neder she-hutar miktzato hutar kullo} \u8212- a {\i neder} from which part has been released, the whole of it is released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miktzato vs.\u160 ?Miklalo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two textual variants of this principle. One reads {\i miktzato} \u8212- if part of it has been permitted, the whole is permitted, since the {\i neder} is treated as a single unit. The other version reads {\i miklalo} \u8212- if something is excluded from the collective, from the totality of what was included, then the entire {\i neder} is rendered permissible. Both versions express the same underlying idea: once the integrity of the {\i neder} as a whole has been compromised by the discovery of a flaw in part of it, the whole falls.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this applies specifically to a {\i neder} or extends to a {\i shevu\u8217'ah} as well. The answer is that the case here remains a {\i neder} in the technical sense \u8212- {\i konam ha-basar alai} \u8212- since the prohibition focuses on the object rather than on the person\u8217's own activity. The same way a {\i neder} can be temporally limited ({\i konam basar alai kol sheloshim yom}), it can also have its prohibited status shift between weekdays and {\i Shabbos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran cites the Yerushalmi\u8217's derivation of this principle from the verse {\i ke-chol ha-yotzei mi-piv ya\u8217'aseh} \u8212- \u8220"according to all that issues from his mouth, he shall do.\u8221" The Yerushalmi formulates it: {\i shavya lah shakul kayama} \u8212- his obligation is to fulfill everything. Consequently, once a person is released from {\i part} of what he declared, he is no longer obligated to fulfill {\i everything} \u8212- and therefore he is not obligated to fulfill {\i anything}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple People Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Keitzad Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then asks {\i keitzad} \u8212- how does this work? It proceeds to illustrate the principle of {\i neder she-hutar miktzato hutar kullo} through the case of a {\i neder} involving multiple individuals collectively. If someone declares {\i konam she\u8217'eini nehene l\u8217'kulchem} \u8212- \u8220"it is {\i konam} that I derive benefit from all of you\u8221" \u8212- he has formulated a single, unified {\i neder} encompassing the entire group.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Released All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence: {\i hutar echad meihen, hutar l\u8217'kulan} \u8212- if he is released from one of them through a {\i petach}, he is released from all of them. Because the {\i neder} was framed as a collective unit, any breach of its integrity releases the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vav Ha\u8217'Hibbur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then presents a more nuanced case: {\i she\u8217'eini nehene la-zeh u\u8217'la-zeh} \u8212- \u8220"that I derive no benefit from this one {\i and} this one {\i and} this one,\u8221" using the conjunctive {\i vav}. The {\i vav ha-hibbur} connecting the individuals creates a chain: the prohibition assigned to the first person is transferred to the second, the second\u8217's to the third, and so on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual model here is one of status transfer and daisy-chaining. When I say {\i la-zeh u\u8217'la-zeh u\u8217'la-zeh}, I am not making five independent nedarim; rather, the forbidden status I have assigned to David is then also assigned to Dov, and Dov\u8217's to Jonathan, and so on. The status is derivative \u8212- each person\u8217's prohibition is built upon the previous one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First vs.\u160 ?Last\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This model generates precise consequences. {\i Hutar ha-rishon, hutar kullan} \u8212- if the first person in the chain is released, everyone is released, because all subsequent prohibitions are built upon his. By contrast, {\i hutar ha-acharon, mutar v\u8217'kullan asurin} \u8212- if only the last person is released, everyone else remains forbidden, since the last person\u8217's prohibition is built upon theirs, but theirs is not built upon his. Releasing the last link does not undo the earlier ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The intermediate case: {\i hutar ha-emtza\u8217'i} \u8212- if the middle person is released. The result is that those {\i after} him in the chain \u8212- {\i heimenu u\u8217'lemata mutarin} \u8212- are also released, since their prohibitions were built upon his. Those {\i before} him \u8212- {\i heimenu u\u8217'lemala asurin} \u8212- remain forbidden, since his prohibition was built on theirs, not the reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This structure is analogous to an earlier {\i gemara} discussion on 25a, where the placement of a qualifier \u8212- at the beginning or at the end \u8212- determines the scope of what it governs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceptual Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to appreciate the conceptual structure underlying all of this. If the {\i la-zeh u\u8217'la-zeh} formulation were treated as one large unified {\i neder}, the principle of {\i hutar miktzato hutar kullo} would apply straightforwardly and release one would release all. But the {\i mishna} demonstrates that releasing the last person does not release the first \u8212- meaning these are not simply one {\i neder}. The better conceptual model is that these are technically separate nedarim, but each subsequent {\i neder} is built upon the prior one, creating the daisy-chain effect. It is precisely this inter-dependence that produces the asymmetric results.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Independent Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Linked vs.\u160 ?Independent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then addresses the case where nedarim are fully independent. If someone says {\i konam la-zeh, konam la-zeh, konam la-zeh} \u8212- using the technical word {\i konam} (or {\i korban}, or {\i neder}) anew for each individual \u8212- he has created separate, self-contained nedarim. In this case, {\i tzrichim petach l\u8217'chol echad v\u8217'echad} \u8212- a distinct {\i petach} is required for each one individually, and releasing one has no effect on the others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the {\i mishna}\u8217's formulation, which uses the word {\i konam} to mark the case of independence, implies that {\i anything less} than repeating the operative word renders the nedarim linked. This raises the question: does the {\i vav ha-hibbur} create the link, or is even {\i la-zeh, la-zeh, la-zeh} without a {\i vav} sufficient to create the chain? The {\i gemara} attributes the view in the {\i mishna} to Rabbi Shimon, based on a parallel principle in the laws of {\i shevu\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine & Onion Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Old Wine Release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents: {\i konam yayin she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em, she-ha-yayin ra le-me\u8217'ai} \u8212- \u8220"Konam that I will not taste wine, because wine is bad for my digestion.\u8221" If they then told him: {\i ve-halo ha-meyushan yafeh le-me\u8217'ai} \u8212- \u8220"but aged wine is actually good for digestion\u8221" \u8212- and he did not know this, then {\i hutar ba-meyushan} \u8212- he is released from aged wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Wine Released\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And furthermore: {\i velo ba-meyushan bilvad hutar ella be-chol ha-yayin} \u8212- not only aged wine, but all wine is released. This is the application of {\i neder she-hutar miktzato hutar kullo}: since the {\i neder} was predicated on the assumption that wine is harmful, and part of that assumption has been shown to be false, the entire {\i neder} is undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ta\u8217'ut vs.\u160 ?Heter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a subtle but important distinction. What if, upon being told that aged wine is good for digestion, the person responds: \u8220"Of course I knew that \u8212- when I said \u8216'wine\u8217' I only meant new wine\u8221"? In that case, it is not a situation of {\i hutar miktzato} at all. Rather, the scope of what he declared in the first place is being interpreted according to his own stated intent at the time: he meant only new wine, so the word \u8220"wine\u8221" is understood accordingly, and no formal release is needed for aged wine because it was never included. The case of {\i hutar miktzato} \u8212- and its consequence that all wine is now permitted \u8212- applies specifically where he genuinely intended to include all wine, did not realize that aged wine was beneficial, and only in retrospect would not have included it. The distinction between a fundamental {\i ta\u8217'ut} in the scope of the {\i neder} and a subsequent {\i heter} through {\i hutar miktzato} is a fine but real one, and the {\i chacham} must assess which situation applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onion Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle plays out in the parallel case: {\i konam batzal she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em, she-ha-batzal ra la-lev} \u8212- \u8220"Konam that I will not taste onion, because onion is bad for the heart.\u8221" If told that the {\i kufari} onion is actually good for the heart, he is released from it \u8212- {\i hutar ba-kufari} \u8212- and consequently released from all onions: {\i velo ba-kufari bilvad hutar ella be-chol ha-betzalim}. Again, if he were to say he always knew the {\i kufari} was fine and he only meant regular onions, the scope of his {\i neder} would be interpreted accordingly and no {\i heter} would be needed for the {\i kufari}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir Permits All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} records: {\i ma\u8217'aseh hayah ve-hitir Rabbi Meir be-chol ha-betzalim} \u8212- there was an actual case and Rabbi Meir permitted all onions. This provides practical {\i ma\u8217'aseh} support for the principle of {\i hutar miktzato hutar kullo} as applied to these circumstances.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Independent Nedarim (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of when nedarim are treated as linked versus independent, the {\i gemara} identifies the author of the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling as Rabbi Shimon. The basis: {\i d\u8217'amar Rabbi Shimon, ad she-yomar shevu\u8217'ah l\u8217'chol echad ve-echad} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds, in the context of {\i shevu\u8217'ah}, that the oath-word must be explicitly repeated for each individual in order to create independent obligations. In the {\i shevu\u8217'ah} context: if six people each demand repayment and one takes a single false {\i shevu\u8217'ah} that he owes nothing to any of them, that is one {\i korban}. If he says \u8220"{\i shevu\u8217'ah} I owe nothing to you, {\i shevu\u8217'ah} I owe nothing to you\u8221" \u8212- six separate {\i korbanot}. If he says \u8220"{\i shevu\u8217'ah} I owe nothing to you or you or you\u8221" \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds that is still one, since the operative word {\i shevu\u8217'ah} was not repeated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Disagree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim}, however, disagree. In their view, saying {\i la-zeh, la-zeh, la-zeh} without the {\i vav} \u8212- that is, listing the individuals separately without the conjunctive \u8212- is itself sufficient to establish independent obligations, even without repeating the operative term for each one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine & Onion Cases (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the wine case. One might ask: why does the {\i mishna} state that aged wine is {\i good} for digestion \u8212- would it not have been sufficient to say merely that it is {\i not bad}? Rabbi Abba responds: {\i v\u8217'od yafeh katani} \u8212- the {\i mishna} teaches an even stronger formulation, but the minimum required for the {\i heter} is simply that it is not harmful. The person\u8217's stated reason for his {\i neder} was that wine damages digestion; it is enough to demonstrate that this assumption is untrue, without needing to show that wine is positively beneficial.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Honor Petachim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal Honor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} turns to a further category of {\i petach}: {\i potchin l\u8217'adam bichvod atzmo u-vichvod banav} \u8212- one may open with the person\u8217's own honor and the honor of his children. In the classic scenario driving this {\i mishna}, a husband has made a {\i neder} that his wife may not benefit from him, which halakhically compels him to divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vesto Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chacham} may say to him: {\i ilu hayita yode\u8217'a she-lemachar omrim alecha, kach hi vesto shel ploni, megoreshet ishto} \u8212- had you known that tomorrow people would say of you, \u8220"that is the practice of so-and-so, his wife is divorced,\u8221" would you have made your {\i neder}? The word {\i veset} is used here in its sense of habit or regular practice \u8212- noteworthy given how frequently the term appears in the {\i gemara} in the context of a woman\u8217's menstrual cycle, where it means the regularity of her cycle. Here it simply connotes: this is the kind of person he is, this is his pattern. The {\i chacham} may also invoke the person\u8217's financial interests \u8212- had you thought about the {\i ketubbah} payment? But if the husband responds that he is unconcerned about paying the {\i ketubbah}, the {\i chacham} shifts to a weightier consideration: had you thought about the social consequences \u8212- that no one will want to marry you again, given your reputation for making destructive nedarim?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters\u8217' Honor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} adds the dimension of {\i kvod banav} \u8212- the honor of his children: {\i ve-al bnotecha yomru, bnos grushos hein} \u8212- and regarding your daughters, people will say they are daughters of a divorc\u233 ?e. Already in the period of the {\i mishna}, the {\i shidduch}-implications of a mother\u8217's divorce were a recognized social reality. People would wonder: {\i mah ra\u8217'ah ima shel elu le-hisgareish} \u8212- what did the mother of these girls do to cause the divorce? They might suspect improper conduct on her part, or simply that she was not a suitable wife. And the daughters, having grown up in her household, would be viewed with wariness. The {\i mishna} is not creating this social reality; it is reflecting it, and using it as a lever to make the husband consider whether he would have made his {\i neder} had he appreciated all its consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the husband says {\i ilu hayiti yode\u8217'a she-kein lo hayiti noder} \u8212- had I known this I would not have made the {\i neder} \u8212- then {\i harei zeh mutar}, he is released.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Appearance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ugly Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to another case, already introduced in the prior {\i daf}: {\i konam she\u8217'ani nosei plonis ke\u8217'urah} \u8212- \u8220"Konam that I will not marry so-and-so, because she is ugly.\u8221" The {\i neder} was made on the basis of an assumed characteristic of the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 True Beauty Found\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If it then emerges that she is actually beautiful \u8212- {\i ve-harei hi na\u8217'ah} \u8212- or that he mistakenly thought she was dark-skinned and she is actually fair, or that she was short but is actually tall, the {\i neder} is permitted. The {\i mishna} clarifies the reason: this is not a case of {\i nolad} \u8212- a circumstance that arose after the fact \u8212- because the woman did not {\i become} beautiful; she {\i was} beautiful and he simply did not realize it. This is therefore not a change in circumstances but a fundamental error of fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Ta\u8217'us\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} makes explicit: {\i lo mipnei she-ke\u8217'urah ve-na\u8217'aseis na\u8217'ah} \u8212- it is not because she was ugly and became beautiful, which would be a {\i nolad} situation. Rather, {\i she-ha-neder ta\u8217'us} \u8212- the {\i neder} was fundamentally erroneous. Because the {\i neder} was based on a false assumption of fact, it arguably does not even require a formal {\i heter}; the {\i ta\u8217'ut} is sufficient to undo it at its root.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then recounts a {\i ma\u8217'aseh}: {\i uma\u8217'aseh be-echad she-nadar mi-bat achoto hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- a certain man took a {\i neder} against deriving benefit from his niece. It was customary in that era for men to be encouraged to marry their nieces, and this {\i neder} was evidently a mechanism to escape that social pressure. They brought the woman to the house of Rabbi Yishmael, and he and his household beautified her \u8212- {\i ve-ipe\u8217'uha}. Rabbi Yishmael then said to the man: {\i beni, mi-zu nadarta?} \u8212- \u8220"My son, was it about this woman that you made your {\i neder}?\u8221" The man replied: {\i lav} \u8212- no, he would never have made a {\i neder} about a woman like this. Rabbi Yishmael permitted her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that moment, Rabbi Yishmael wept and said: {\i bnos Yisrael na\u8217'os hein, ella she-ha-aniyus menaveltan} \u8212- the daughters of Israel are beautiful, but it is poverty that makes them appear unattractive. They are underfed, unable to bathe properly, without fine clothing. And when Rabbi Yishmael died, {\i hayu bnos Yisrael mekonenos kinah} \u8212- all the women of Israel would recite a lamentation, a formal {\i kinah} of poetic wailing. They declared: {\i bnos Yisrael al Rabbi Yishmael bechainah} \u8212- daughters of Israel, weep for Rabbi Yishmael. This is compared to the lament over King Shaul recorded in Scripture: {\i bnos Yisrael al Shaul bechainah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nolad Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presses on the story: {\i maiseh listor} \u8212- is the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} a contradiction? The {\i mishna} began by saying that the permissibility of the {\i neder} is not because the woman became beautiful \u8212- that would be {\i nolad}. Yet the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} describes the man being shown a woman who has been made beautiful through cosmetic intervention. It sounds precisely like a case of {\i nolad} \u8212- she changed after the {\i neder} was made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this through {\i chisorei mechsara} \u8212- there are missing words in the text, and the {\i mishna} must be read with an implicit addition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael Nolad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reconstruction is: Rabbi Yishmael holds that {\i afilu ke\u8217'urah ve-na\u8217'aseis na\u8217'ah} \u8212- even if she was ugly at the time of the {\i neder} and subsequently became beautiful, one may still open with this as a {\i petach}. Rabbi Yishmael disagrees with those who would restrict this form of release only to cases where the woman was always beautiful and the man was simply mistaken. For Rabbi Yishmael, {\i poschin be-nolad} \u8212- one may use a circumstance that arose later as a basis for release \u8212- even in a case like this where perhaps the change was not entirely foreseeable. This aligns him with the position associated with Rabbi Eliezer earlier in the {\i masechta}, who permitted {\i petach} through {\i nolad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gold Tooth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then specifies what the beautification actually consisted of: {\i shen shel cheves hayta lah} \u8212- she had a replacement tooth of some kind, presumably an unattractive prosthetic material available at the time. {\i Aseh lah Rabbi Yishmael shen shel zahav} \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael had it replaced with a gold tooth, and that single change transformed her appearance sufficiently that the man no longer recognized her as the woman about whom he had made his {\i neder}. This clarifies why the {\i gemara} reads the story as a {\i nolad} case: the woman genuinely changed after the {\i neder} was made, rather than always having been beautiful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's Eulogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabbi Yishmael died, the {\i gemara} records that a male eulogizer opened with the following {\i hesped}: {\i bnos Yisrael al Rabbi Yishmael bechainah} \u8212- daughters of Israel, weep for Rabbi Yishmael \u8212- {\i ha-malbishechem ve-chulei} \u8212- he who clothed you, and so forth. Interestingly, the {\i gemara} does not record the remainder of the verse; it trails off with {\i ve-chulei}. The eulogy echoes the words found in the book of Shmuel regarding the lament over Shaul \u8212- {\i bnos Yisrael al Shaul bechainah} \u8212- and the parallel is intentional: as Shaul provided material abundance for the women of Israel, so Rabbi Yishmael took a personal interest in the welfare and dignity of Jewish women.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bar Bavel Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dialect Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes the {\i masechta} with a famous and charming {\i ma\u8217'aseh} that, while seemingly a departure from the halakhic discussion, connects naturally to the overarching theme of {\i nedarim} \u8212- the interpretation of language, the gap between what one says and what one means, and the relationship between husband and wife. A man from Babylonia \u8212- {\i hahu bar Bavel} \u8212- went up to {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where they spoke Palestinian Aramaic rather than Babylonian Aramaic. The two dialects shared vocabulary but assigned different meanings to many terms. As one might say, the two communities were separated by a common language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Hooves/Lentils\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He married a local woman. He said to her: {\i basheli li trei talsi} \u8212- \u8220"cook me two.\u8221" In Babylonian Aramaic, this meant two legs or hooves of an animal \u8212- a substantial meat dish. In Palestinian Aramaic, the word could also mean lentil beans. She cooked him two lentils. Some explain that his \u8220"two\u8221" was simply a colloquial way of saying \u8220"some,\u8221" and she took him quite literally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Escalation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He became angry \u8212- {\i ratah alah}. The next day, {\i basheli li garriva} \u8212- \u8220"cook me a large measure of it.\u8221" She understood and cooked him an enormous quantity \u8212- taking his words literally once more. His frustration mounted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melon vs.\u160 ?Candle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then said: {\i zili ayiti li trei butzini} \u8212- \u8220"go get me two.\u8221" In one Aramaic dialect, {\i butzina} means a melon; in the other, it means a lamp or candle. She brought him two candles \u8212- {\i ayita lei trei shargi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Breaking on Head\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At his wit\u8217's end, he told her: {\i zili tavrinhu al reisha d\u8217'Baba} \u8212- \u8220"go break them on the head of Baba.\u8221" He meant: go break them at the gate, the {\i bava}, take them there. At that moment, Baba ben Buta happened to be sitting by the gate \u8212- {\i havai yativ Baba ben Buta ad d\u8217'baba} \u8212- adjudicating a court case. She went and broke the candles on his head.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The comic structure of the passage is unmistakable: {\i Baba} was sitting at the {\i baba}, and so the husband\u8217's instruction was fulfilled in the most literal and disastrous way possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pastoral Judgment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Baba ben Buta asked her: {\i mah deita avat} \u8212- what did you do? She replied: {\i kach tzivani ba\u8217'ali} \u8212- \u8220"that is what my husband commanded me.\u8221" Baba ben Buta did not react with indignation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Will\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Instead, he said to her: {\i asit retzon ba\u8217'alech} \u8212- you did your husband\u8217's will. This is the great irony of the episode: she demonstrably did {\i not} do what her husband intended, yet she did precisely what she understood him to say. Baba ben Buta chose to reframe the entire situation as one of good intent on all sides. He then blessed her: {\i ha-Makom yotzi mimcha shenei vanim k\u8217'Baba ben Buta} \u8212- may God bring forth from you two sons like Baba ben Buta.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes the {\i masechta} here with {\i Hadran alach ha-Ma\u8217'adir} \u8212- the concluding formula of the tractate. The placement of this story at the very end is deeply apt. The entire {\i masechta} of {\i Nedarim} has been occupied with the question of how language is interpreted \u8212- what a person means when he speaks, how we assess the scope and intent of his words, and how {\i beit din} navigates the gap between utterance and intention. This final {\i ma\u8217'aseh} distills that theme into a scene of domestic miscommunication, where no one is acting in bad faith, yet the gap between what is said and what is understood produces chaos. Baba ben Buta\u8217's response \u8212- affirming the wife\u8217's good intent, reframing the situation as one of genuine effort rather than failure, and blessing the household \u8212- models a pastoral wisdom that transcends strict textual interpretation. Everyone was trying to do the right thing; the work now is to communicate more carefully.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 67\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hafarah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transition to Hafarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf opens at the very last line of 66b, marking a shift in the tractate\u8217's focus. While the preceding {\i perek} was devoted largely to {\i hatarah} \u8212- the mechanism by which a {\i beis din} retroactively releases a vow \u8212- {\i hatarah} itself was never the subject of a direct, foundational exposition. The {\i gemara} never paused to ask: from where do we derive that {\i hataras nedarim} is possible at all? That question remains, in a sense, suspended. What the Torah does address explicitly is a related but distinct institution: {\i hafarah}, the annulment or voiding of vows. It is this phenomenon that forms the subject of the new {\i perek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Halachot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah establishes that a woman is always, conceptually, under the authority of some man. If she is in her father\u8217's house, her father has the power to annul her vows. If she is in her husband\u8217's house, her husband has that power. The Torah assumes no independent space for a woman between these two domains. Even if she never marries and remains in her father\u8217's home well into adulthood, she is conceptually under his authority. The passage from one domain to the other is precisely {\i nisuin} \u8212- the physical transfer from the father\u8217's house to the husband\u8217's house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exception the Torah carves out is explicit: {\i v\u8217'neder almanah u\u8217'grushah kol asher asra al nafsha yakum aleha} \u8212- the vow of a widow or divorcee stands, because she has left both domains and no man holds authority over her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several additional {\i halachos} govern this institution. First, annulment must occur {\i b\u8217'yom sham\u8217'o} \u8212- on the day the relevant party hears of the vow; the precise parameters of \u8220"that day\u8221" will be discussed as the {\i gemara} develops. Second, alongside the act of voiding, the Torah also recognizes {\i hakamah} \u8212- an affirmative declaration that the vow stands. If the husband or father actively establishes the vow, the opportunity for {\i hafarah} is lost even within that same day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It bears noting that Chazal, in their reading of the Torah, do introduce a concept the Torah itself does not explicitly formulate: that once a girl reaches twelve and a half ({\i bogeres}), she is conceptually independent of her father. The Torah knows no such temporal boundary \u8212- in the Torah\u8217's framework, a woman moves from father to husband with no intervening independent stage. That independent stage is a rabbinic conceptual development.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafarah vs.\u160 ?Hatarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third foundational point concerns the structural difference between {\i hafarah} and {\i hatarah}. {\i Hatarah} is retroactive. It is modeled on the concept of {\i ta\u8217'os} \u8212- the premise that had the person genuinely understood the implications of the vow, they would never have taken it. Undoing that misunderstanding retroactively dissolves the vow from its inception, as though it had never bound. {\i Hafarah}, by contrast, operates {\i me\u8217'kan u\u8217'laba} \u8212- from this point forward. It does not reach back. The mechanism is not intellectual dissolution but the assertion of authority: the husband or father exercises dominion over the woman\u8217's commitments and declares them void. The vow was binding until that moment; it is no longer binding from that moment onward. Accordingly, if a woman violated her vow prior to her husband\u8217's {\i hafarah}, she has genuinely transgressed \u8212- {\i hafarah} cannot reach back to undo that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphan Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: according to the Torah\u8217's framework, what of a girl whose father has died and who has not yet married? In principle, she would be independent, since she has no father to place her under authority. Chazal, however, open up an entirely different conceptual landscape. Once the category of {\i bogeres} is introduced \u8212- independent adult status beginning at twelve and a half \u8212- the question of the orphaned girl largely loses its urgency. A girl who has passed twelve and a half is already independent regardless of whether her father is living. The large gap that opens up between {\i bas mitzvah} and {\i nisuin}, especially as {\i kiddushei ketanah} became uncommon, creates an expansive zone of female independence that the Torah itself does not map.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Na\u8217'arah Me\u8217'orasah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i na\u8217'ara ham\u8217'urasa aviha u\u8217'va\u8217'alah mefirin nedareha} \u8212- the adolescent betrothed girl: her father and her husband annul her vows. This is a case of a woman occupying a genuinely intermediate status. She is a {\i na\u8217'ara} \u8212- between twelve and twelve and a half \u8212- and thus not yet a {\i bogeres} who has left her father\u8217's authority. At the same time, she is {\i m\u8217'urasa}, betrothed through {\i kiddushin}, though {\i nisuin} has not yet occurred. Critically, {\i nisuin} is the act of transition, the physical movement from the father\u8217's house to the husband\u8217's house. Until that transition occurs, she remains, in the most concrete sense, in her father\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's language \u8212- \u8220"her father and her husband\u8221" \u8212- is itself the subject of the entire legal and textual discussion that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran opens his commentary with a foundational observation: {\i afilu nedarim she-nadra b\u8217'odah penuyah} \u8212- even vows she took before the {\i kiddushin} can be annulled in this framework. The {\i arus} (groom) can participate in annulling vows that predated his entry into her life entirely \u8212- but only {\i b\u8217'shutfuta d\u8217'av}, in partnership with the father. Furthermore, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's use of the term {\i na\u8217'ara} is noted as excluding the {\i bogeres}: once she is twelve and a half, {\i d\u8217'ein aviha rishus bah} \u8212- her father no longer holds authority over her, and accordingly the {\i arus} too cannot annul, {\i b\u8217'lo shutfuta d\u8217'av lo matzi meifer} \u8212- without the partnership of the father he has no capacity to act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking conceptual point. One might have expected that the {\i arus}, now that the father is out of the picture, would simply step into that role. But no: the {\i arus}\u8217's capacity to annul is not an independent power. It is derivative of the father\u8217's authority. Where the father has no standing, neither does the {\i arus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom Annuls Pre-Betroth\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads directly to an important conceptual clarification. Normally, a husband who enters {\i nisuin} cannot annul vows his wife took before the marriage. The logic is clear: his domain begins at the moment of {\i nisuin}, and what preceded it was outside his purview. Why, then, can the {\i arus} annul vows from before the {\i erusin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer follows directly from the principle just established: because it is fundamentally the father who annuls. The father\u8217's authority over his daughter is not bounded by the moment of {\i erusin}. He was always in authority. Since the {\i arus} acts in partnership with the father, and since it is really the father\u8217's power that drives the {\i hafarah}, the temporal boundary that would limit a husband acting alone does not apply here. The {\i arus} is, as the Ran puts it, tagging onto the father\u8217's participation. The father is primary; the groom is secondary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is sharp: when the father is absent \u8212- whether because the girl is a {\i bogeres} or because the father has died \u8212- the {\i arus} has no role at all. His power exists only in the shadow of the father\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vows Age 11+1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The category is not limited to a {\i na\u8217'ara} in the strict sense. The Ran notes that the same {\i halacha} applies to a {\i ketanah} who is in her twelfth year \u8212- after eleven years and one day \u8212- because there is a separate principle in {\i hilchos nedarim} according to which vows taken in the year preceding {\i bar} or {\i bas mitzvah} are binding. For a girl who takes a vow in that year, she is still a {\i ketanah}, but her vow has force, and in that case, too, both the father and the {\i arus} together would annul it. This demonstrates, again, that the fundamental dynamic is not the girl\u8217's maturity but the structure of whose authority she is under.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joint Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i heifer ha\u8217'av v\u8217'lo heifer ha\u8217'baal, heifer ha\u8217'baal v\u8217'lo heifer ha\u8217'av, eino mufar} \u8212- if the father annulled without the husband, or the husband without the father, it is not annulled. Neither party acting alone is sufficient. The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i v\u8217'ein tzarich lomar she\u8217'kiyam echad mehem} \u8212- and it goes without saying that if one of them performed {\i hakamah}, actively affirmed the vow, the annulment fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Parsing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} parses this {\i mishnah} carefully. The statement that if the father annulled alone or the groom annulled alone it does not work is necessary, because one might have thought that either party acting alone suffices. The {\i gemara} therefore notes: {\i mahu d\u8217'teima o aviha o va\u8217'alah} \u8212- lest one read the {\i mishnah} disjunctively, thinking either the father or the husband may act. The {\i mishnah} teaches that both must act together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} questions why the {\i mishnah} needs to state that {\i hakamah} by one of them ruins the process. If neither can annul alone, how much more so when one of them actively affirms! {\i Hashet\u8217'a yesh lomar heifer zeh belo zeh belo klum} \u8212- if one annuls without the other it accomplishes nothing, then {\i kiyam echad mehem lamah li} \u8212- why state the {\i hakamah} case at all? The {\i gemara} acknowledges this and says {\i tzaricha} \u8212- it is necessary to teach \u8212- and proceeds to explain in what unusual case the {\i hakamah} provision adds something new.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Affirm vs.\u160 ?Void\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Voids, One Affirms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case the {\i gemara} constructs is this: party A performed {\i hafarah}, party B performed {\i hakamah}. Now, ordinarily, that is the end of the story \u8212- the {\i hakamah} has affirmed the vow and the prior {\i hafarah} of A is rendered ineffective, since the two must act together and B has instead gone the opposite direction. But the {\i gemara} introduces a twist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatarah on Hakamah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i hakamah} is a verbal act \u8212- a declaration that the vow stands. As such, it is subject to {\i hatarah}: just as a vow itself can be retroactively released through a {\i chacham} who demonstrates it was made in error, so too a {\i hakamah} made in error ({\i hakamah b\u8217'ta\u8217'us}) can be retroactively released. The mechanism of {\i hatarah} applies to the {\i hakamah} just as it applies to any other consequential verbal commitment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakamah Reversal Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the case is: A performed {\i hafarah}, B performed {\i hakamah}, and then B went to a {\i chacham} and had the {\i hakamah} released retroactively. The obvious intuition is: fine, the {\i hakamah} is undone as though it never happened, so A\u8217's {\i hafarah} still stands \u8212- now let B add his {\i hafarah} and it is complete. But the {\i gemara} rejects this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'chazar hamekayem v\u8217'nish\u8217'al al hakamaso} \u8212- and the one who affirmed went and sought release from his affirmation. {\i Mahu d\u8217'teima mai d\u8217'meifer me\u8217'ikara hashta v\u8217'eima} \u8212- one might think that what he voided initially, he has now reversed, so we are back to square one and can proceed. {\i Ka mashmalan d\u8217'ba\u8217'inan sh\u8217'neihem b\u8217'vas achas} \u8212- no, the {\i gemara} teaches that we need both parties to perform {\i hafarah} together, meaning A must now perform a new {\i hafarah}. The {\i hafarah} A performed before the {\i hakamah} cannot combine retroactively with B\u8217's new {\i hafarah} now that the {\i hakamah} has been undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic, though terse in the {\i gemara}, is roughly as follows. At the moment B performed {\i hakamah}, A\u8217's {\i hafarah} became effectively inert \u8212- not merely incomplete, but drained of any halachic potential. Even if that {\i hakamah} is later retroactively dissolved, the {\i hafarah} A performed had already passed through a period in real time during which it was entirely powerless. That passage is irreversible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam takes this even further: not only can A\u8217's prior {\i hafarah} not combine with B\u8217's subsequent one, but A cannot even now perform a new {\i hafarah}. Once A\u8217's initial {\i hafarah} lost its effectiveness \u8212- even if for a reason later retroactively undone \u8212- A has exhausted his single opportunity. The rationale is that you cannot perform {\i hafarah} a second time after the first was already done, and since the first is now spent, A is out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Logical Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran raises a sharp objection to the {\i gemara}\u8217's ruling. The entire power of {\i hatarah} is retroactivity. When we say the {\i hakamah} has been released retroactively, we are saying that in retrospect there was never a {\i hakamah}. It is as though it never happened. If so, why does A\u8217's {\i hafarah} suffer? Looking back, there was no {\i hakamah} intervening. The comparison the Ran invokes: if a man betroths a woman on condition that she has no outstanding vows, and she has vows at the time, and then she subsequently obtains a retroactive {\i hatarah} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} takes effect retroactively, because retroactively she had no vows at the moment of betrothal. Why cannot the same retroactive logic restore A\u8217's {\i hafarah} to full effectiveness?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran offers two lines of response. The first is conceptual: even when {\i hatarah} dissolves a verbal act retroactively, it does not undo the fact that the act occurred. What it undoes is the binding force of the words. The act of {\i hakamah} \u8212- the physical speaking of words of affirmation \u8212- remains a historical fact, even if its legal consequences are erased. That act, having occurred, may be sufficient to create an obstacle, even after its effects are dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second line of response reflects a structural asymmetry. {\i Hafarah} is a peculiarly fragile kind of act. Unlike {\i kiddushin}, which is a concrete act with immediate independent legal force, {\i hafarah} is purely verbal and, when only one party has acted, is inherently incomplete \u8212- it has no standing effect until the other party joins. It is waiting, in a state of suspended potential, for its partner. An act of such tenuous and inchoate character, which passed through a period in real time where it was not merely incomplete but actively foreclosed by the {\i hakamah}, loses that potential permanently. The Ran acknowledges he does not fully resolve the theoretical mechanism, but the {\i halacha} stands: even retroactively undoing the {\i hakamah} does not restore A\u8217's {\i hafarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mention Hakamah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This returns us to the {\i gemara}\u8217's question: why does the {\i mishnah} mention {\i hakamah} at all, given that it \u8220"goes without saying\u8221"? The Ran answers: the simple case \u8212- where one party does {\i hakamah} and that is the end of the story \u8212- indeed goes without saying. But the {\i mishnah} introduces {\i hakamah} specifically to teach that {\i hakamah} can create an obstacle even in more complex scenarios: specifically, the case where a {\i hakamah} is made and then retroactively dissolved, yet still manages to block the earlier {\i hafarah} from combining with a later one. That {\i chiddush} is not obvious, and it required the {\i mishnah} to mention {\i hakamah} in order to convey it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scriptural Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading the Pesukim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the scriptural grounding of {\i hafarah}, and we read through the relevant {\i pesukim} of Parashat Matot. The section opens: {\i vayedaber Moshe el rashei hamatot livnei yisrael lemor zeh hadavar asher tziva Hashem} \u8212- God spoke to Moses to tell the heads of the tribes. The phrase {\i rashei hamatot} is noted by Chazal as a small textual peg: normally the address would be {\i bnei yisrael}, so the mention of tribal heads, figures of authority, provides a hint in the direction of {\i hatarah} \u8212- though the text itself is entirely about {\i hafarah}. The textual basis for {\i hataras nedarim} itself is a separate discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i pasuk} addresses the general case of vowing: {\i ish ki yidor neder la-Hashem o hishava shevua le-esor issar al nafsho lo yachel devaro ke-chol ha-yotze mipiv ya\u8217'aseh} \u8212- a man who takes a vow or an oath to prohibit something upon himself may not violate his word; he must do as he said. This {\i pasuk} establishes both the positive obligation and the prohibition of {\i bal yachel} for vows in general. It is worth noting that both {\i neder} and {\i shevua} are mentioned here, though in the framing of making something forbidden \u8212- which has been the consistent assumption throughout the tractate, where {\i nedarim} are understood primarily as prohibitions placed on objects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The text then shifts: {\i v\u8217'isha ki tidor neder la-Hashem v\u8217'asra issar b\u8217'veit aviha b\u8217'neureiha} \u8212- a woman who takes a vow while in her father\u8217's house in her youth. The father hears the vow and the prohibition she has placed upon herself. Notably, from this point the {\i shevua} drops from the text entirely \u8212- a fact consistent with the tractate\u8217's sustained analysis that {\i nedarim} are fundamentally about making objects forbidden, which is the core function of a {\i neder} rather than a {\i shevua}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'hecherish lah aviha} \u8212- if the father is silent \u8212- {\i v\u8217'kamu kol nedareha v\u8217'chol issar asher asra al nafsha yakum} \u8212- all her vows and prohibitions stand. His silence functions as passive {\i hakamah}. {\i V\u8217'im hini aviha osa b\u8217'yom sham\u8217'o} \u8212- but if her father {\i hini} (annulled) it on the day he hears \u8212- {\i kol nedareha v\u8217'essareha asher asra al nafsha lo yakum} \u8212- they are not established. The word {\i hini} is an unusual term for annulment, but the content is clear: the father has the power to void his daughter\u8217's vows, and he must exercise it on the day he hears.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'Hashem yislach lah} \u8212- and God will forgive her \u8212- {\i ki hini aviha osa}. The phrase \u8220"God will forgive her\u8221" is unexpected: she has not necessarily violated anything, since the father annulled the vow. Chazal explain: the father annulled it without her knowledge, and she subsequently violated it, believing the vow to still be in force. On the level of objective action she committed no transgression \u8212- the vow was already void. But subjectively, in her own mind and intention, she experienced herself as transgressing. It is for that psychological act, that {\i kavanah} of transgression, that she requires divine forgiveness even in the absence of an objective {\i ma\u8217'aseh aveira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second paragraph begins: {\i v\u8217'im hayo tihye l\u8217'ish} \u8212- if she comes to be to a man. The plain sense of {\i tihye} in many contexts in the Torah is {\i nisuin}, full marriage. {\i V\u8217'nedareha aleha} \u8212- and her vows are upon her from before. The husband hears, is silent, and the vows stand. If he annuls on the day he hears, they are dissolved, and {\i V\u8217'Hashem yislach lah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow/Divorcee\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Torah inserts the case of the widow and divorcee: {\i v\u8217'neder almanah u\u8217'grushah kol asher asra al nafsha yakum aleha}. Having left her father\u8217's house and her husband\u8217's house, she stands under no man\u8217's authority, and her vows stand unconditionally. No one can annul on her behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vows During Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah then adds a third paragraph: {\i v\u8217'im beis isha nadra} \u8212- if she took the vow while already in her husband\u8217's house. This is a fresh vow, taken during the marriage itself. {\i O asra issar al nafsha b\u8217'shavua} \u8212- or she prohibited something upon herself by oath. The husband hears, is silent, and the vow stands. {\i V\u8217'im haifer yaifer osam isha b\u8217'yom sham\u8217'o} \u8212- if the husband annuls them on the day he hears \u8212- {\i kol motza sefaseha l\u8217'nedareha v\u8217'l\u8217'issar nafsha lo yakum} \u8212- all that issued from her lips will not stand. {\i Isha haiferam v\u8217'Hashem yislach lah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Redundancy Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Nisuin Cases?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now confronts a fundamental problem. We have two separate paragraphs about a husband annulling his wife\u8217's vows. The second paragraph explicitly concerns vows taken {\i b\u8217'veit isha}, during the marriage. The first paragraph, which says {\i v\u8217'nedareha aleha} \u8212- her vows were already upon her \u8212- seems to describe vows predating the marriage. If both paragraphs concern a {\i nesuah}, a fully married woman, and if the first establishes that the husband can annul even prior vows, then the second paragraph is entirely superfluous: the case it covers is less novel and less demanding than the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The redundancy is what forces Chazal to read the {\i pesukim} otherwise. If reading them both as {\i nisuin} creates an impossible redundancy, then one of them must be about something different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ramban, in his commentary on the {\i parashah}, takes note of this problem explicitly. His position is that the {\i peshat} of the text reads both paragraphs as referring to a {\i nesuah} \u8212- a fully married woman. The repetition, on a {\i peshat} level, is a genuine difficulty, but it is the plain sense of the words. Nevertheless, he acknowledges, Chazal have read it otherwise, and he concludes {\i v\u8217'hakabalah tachria} \u8212- the received tradition adjudicates. That closing phrase is telling: the Ramban is not fully satisfied, but accepts the rabbinic reading as authoritative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Nisuin Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} entertains the possibility that both paragraphs address {\i nisuin}. If so, why would the Torah need two paragraphs? Perhaps, the {\i gemara} suggests, the second paragraph \u8212- {\i v\u8217'im beis isha nadra} \u8212- comes to teach a {\i chiddush} about the first: specifically, {\i she\u8217'ein ha\u8217'baal meifer b\u8217'kodmin} \u8212- the husband cannot annul vows that predate the marriage. The first paragraph contains the phrase {\i v\u8217'nedareha aleha} \u8212- her vows were upon her \u8212- and one might have thought this means the husband can reach back to prior vows. The second paragraph, by emphasizing {\i beis isha}, \u8220"in her husband\u8217's house,\u8221" would then be a corrective: his power of annulment extends only to vows made within the marriage, not before it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading attempts to make both paragraphs work together around {\i nisuin} without redundancy. But the {\i gemara} refuses to accept it. Even if the second paragraph adds the {\i chiddush} that prior vows cannot be annulled, one is still left asking why the first paragraph is necessary at all. If the second paragraph already tells us everything \u8212- the husband can annul vows made during marriage, and cannot annul prior ones \u8212- what does the first paragraph add? {\i Sof sof lamah li} \u8212- ultimately, why do I need it? The redundancy is not dissolved; it is only shifted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Havayah = Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then proposes a different approach: {\i havayah k\u8217'kiddushin mashma} \u8212- the word {\i tihye} (becoming) in the phrase {\i v\u8217'im hayo tihye l\u8217'ish} connotes {\i kiddushin}, betrothal, not {\i nisuin}. The {\i gemara} in tractate {\i Kiddushin} draws interpretive connections between the language of {\i havayah} and the language of betrothal, and on that basis Chazal read the first paragraph as describing an {\i arusah}, a betrothed woman, not a {\i nesuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran notes that this reading is not entirely straightforward. {\i Havayah} can mean {\i nisuin} as well \u8212- it can mean both. But since it can also be read as {\i erusin}, and since reading it as {\i erusin} resolves the redundancy, Chazal adopt that reading. With the first paragraph now understood as the case of an {\i arusah}, and the second as the case of a {\i nesuah}, the two paragraphs address different situations and each is independently necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father-Only Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But even granting that the first paragraph addresses an {\i arusah}, a question remains: the paragraph mentions only the husband (or groom). How does Chazal\u8217's conclusion \u8212- that both father {\i and} groom must act \u8212- emerge from a text that seems to discuss only one party?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} entertains a position consistent with the principle that the groom has no real authority before {\i nisuin}: {\i eima av lechudei meifer} \u8212- perhaps the father alone annuls. The mention of the groom in the {\i pasuk} would then serve a different purpose: not to give him a positive role, but to signal that if he performs {\i hakamah} he can block the process. The groom\u8217's only function is as a potential obstacle, not as a necessary co-participant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading has a certain logical appeal. If we know that the father is the primary authority before {\i nisuin} and the groom has no genuine independent standing, why should we read the {\i pasuk} as requiring the groom\u8217's participation? Perhaps his mention is only cautionary \u8212- beware, if he affirms the vow, the father\u8217's annulment will fail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father-Only Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this reading, however, by noting that it makes the first paragraph of the {\i parashah} \u8212- the section about a girl in her father\u8217's house before she has any {\i arus} \u8212- completely unnecessary. If the father can annul alone even when there is an {\i arus}, how much more obviously can he annul when there is no {\i arus} at all. {\i Hashta yesh lomar bimkom arus meifer av lechudei} \u8212- if in the presence of a groom the father acts alone \u8212- {\i shelo bimkom arus mibaya} \u8212- is it necessary to tell me about the case without a groom? Of course the father\u8217's authority is established in the simpler case; you would never need to state it first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran explains what the {\i gemara} is driving at: the Torah clearly intends the opening section about the father to be a necessary teaching, not a redundant prelude. If the father-alone theory were correct, that opening section would be logically dependent on \u8212- and derivable from \u8212- the more complex {\i arus} case, which is backwards. You establish simple cases before complex ones; you do not require a complex case to be stated first in order to understand a simple one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partnership Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom-Only Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now reverses course entirely and tests the opposite extreme: {\i eima arus lechudei meifer} \u8212- perhaps the groom alone annuls, with no need for the father. This is actually far closer to the {\i peshat} of the {\i pesukim}. The text speaks of the husband\u8217's role; if we are reading the first paragraph as {\i erusin}, then the plain sense is that the groom holds the authority. Perhaps the mention of the father in the structure of the {\i parasha} is merely contextual, and the groom, now that she is {\i m\u8217'urasa}, is the sole relevant authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Case Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this reading too generates a redundancy problem. If the groom can annul entirely without the father, then the case of the {\i nesuah} \u8212- the fully married woman, {\i beis isha} \u8212- follows {\i kal vachomer}: if the groom can do it even when she is still conceptually in her father\u8217's house, then certainly the husband can do it after {\i nisuin} when she is fully under his authority. There would be no need for the Torah to state the husband\u8217's case separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'mah bimkom av arus meifer lechudei} \u8212- if in the presence of a father the groom annuls alone \u8212- {\i shelo bimkom av mibaya} \u8212- when the father is entirely out of the picture after {\i nisuin}, is it even a question? The {\i nesuah} paragraph would be entirely derivable by {\i kal vachomer} and would serve no independent purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to rescue the groom-only reading by suggesting that the {\i nesuah} paragraph adds a genuine {\i chiddush} even in this framework: {\i she\u8217'eino meifer b\u8217'kodmin} \u8212- the husband cannot annul prior vows, only those made during the marriage. This would distinguish the {\i nesuah} case from the {\i arus} case, since the {\i arus} \u8212- acting in the context of a woman still fundamentally in her father\u8217's house \u8212- can reach prior vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this response, the {\i gemara} observes, actually creates a new difficulty. If the {\i nesuah} paragraph\u8217's function is to teach that the husband cannot annul prior vows, then implicitly the {\i arus} paragraph teaches the opposite \u8212- that the {\i arus} can. But that implication of the first paragraph would then itself be informative in a way that makes the reader wonder: why can the {\i arus} do something the husband cannot? The answer, as we know, is {\i shutfuta d\u8217'av} \u8212- it is really the father doing it. But if that is the answer, the groom-only reading cannot be correct, because the father\u8217's involvement is the very explanation for the {\i arus}\u8217's extended capacity. {\i Ela lav mishum shutfuta d\u8217'av} \u8212- is this not precisely because of the father\u8217's partnership?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shutfuta Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only reading that resolves all the tensions simultaneously is the {\i shutfuta} model: neither the father alone nor the groom alone, but both together. Each paragraph then serves an independent purpose: the first establishes the unique joint requirement for the {\i na\u8217'ara me\u8217'urasa}, and the second establishes the husband\u8217's independent but limited authority after {\i nisuin}. Neither is redundant, and neither is derivable from the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Fundamentality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even within the {\i shutfuta} framework, it is essential to understand that the partnership is not between equals. The father remains the primary authority. The evidence is structural: when the father is absent \u8212- when the girl is a {\i bogeres} or the father has died \u8212- the {\i arus} can accomplish nothing at all. The {\i hafarah} is fundamentally the father\u8217's act; the groom participates but does not initiate. The reason the {\i arus} can annul vows predating the {\i erusin} is precisely this: since it is really the father\u8217's authority being exercised, temporal boundaries linked to the groom\u8217's entry into her life are irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final word of {\i b\u8217'yom sham\u8217'o} governs the entire structure. Anything heard more than a day before \u8212- whether heard by the father before the {\i kiddushin} or by both parties at different times \u8212- has already achieved its finalized status: either it was passively established by the father\u8217's silence or actively annulled. The groom\u8217's role pertains only to matters still within the window of {\i yom sham\u8217'o} at the time both parties are in a position to act. These complexities of timing \u8212- whose clock governs, what happens when the father hears on one day and the groom hears a week later \u8212- will be developed as the {\i perek} continues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What this {\i perek} establishes at its outset is a new halachic reality, one the {\i pesukim} do not state plainly and that required Chazal to work carefully through the verses\u8217' apparent redundancy to uncover. The {\i arus} is given any role at all only because of his connection to the father\u8217's authority \u8212- and that is why the {\i parasha} cannot be reduced either to the father alone or to the groom alone. It is the {\i shutfuta}, the partnership, that the Torah encodes \u8212- with the father as the fundamental and primary partner, and the groom as the necessary but secondary one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 68\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Review: Father-Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Na\u8217'arah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 68. As with yesterday, there is relatively little {\i gemara} text here \u8212- much of the page is occupied by {\i Ran} \u8212- and the substantive {\i halachic} discussion follows closely in the spirit of what we saw on the preceding daf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By way of review: the {\i mishnah} introduces the case of a girl who has not yet reached {\i bogeret} status, meaning she is still a {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- or in some circumstances even a {\i ketanah} whose {\i neder} is halakhically binding. Such a girl is considered, both physically and, more importantly, {\i halachically}, to be in her father\u8217's house. This remains true even after {\i kiddushin}, and continues until {\i nissu\u8217'in} \u8212- until the formal transfer at {\i chuppah} when she fully enters her husband\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Darsha Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, {\i Chazal} read one of the {\i parashiyos} in the {\i Torah} that discusses the husband or father annulling a {\i neder} in a striking way. That {\i parasha} is understood to describe a case where the father and husband are {\i partners} in the act of {\i hafara} \u8212- specifically when the girl is a {\i na\u8217'arah murasah}, a betrothed minor. One might have expected that in this period, since she is still legally in her father\u8217's house, the father alone would hold the power of {\i hafara}. Yet {\i Chazal} read the {\i Torah} to teach that the husband participates as well, and that the two together annul the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Counterintuitively, however, even though the {\i parasha} the {\i gemara} draws upon speaks primarily of the husband, the father remains the primary and more central figure. Two important consequences flow from this. First, the {\i hafara} applies even to vows that predate the {\i kiddushin}, because it is fundamentally the father\u8217's authority that is operative. Second, if the girl has no father \u8212- if she is a {\i yesomah} with {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the husband alone cannot annul the {\i neder}. The father is indispensable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the top of 68a, the {\i gemara} cites a {\i braita}: {\i tanya devei Rabbi Ishmael} \u8212- from the school of Rabbi Ishmael the following was taught. The {\i pasuk} reads: {\i bein ish l\u8217'ishto bein av l\u8217'vito b\u8217'neureha beis aviha} \u8212- \u8220"between a man and his wife, between a father and his daughter, in her youth in her father\u8217's house.\u8221" The question is posed: the {\i Torah} has already stated {\i av l\u8217'vito} \u8212- why add {\i b\u8217'neureha beis aviha}? The plain reading of the {\i pasuk} would be that the section about the father applies when she is in his house, and the section about the husband applies after she marries. What, then, is the additional phrase teaching?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} answers: {\i mikan l\u8217'na\u8217'arah murasah she\u8217'aviha u\u8217'va\u8217'alah mefirin nedareha} \u8212- from here we derive the case of the {\i na\u8217'arah murasah}, whose father and husband together annul her vows. The {\i pasuk} is read not as describing two mutually exclusive scenarios but as identifying a single case that is simultaneously {\i bein ish l\u8217'ishto} and {\i bein av l\u8217'vito} \u8212- when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah} in her father\u8217's house who also has a husband. That is the {\i na\u8217'arah murasah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: according to this {\i tanna} of the school of Rabbi Ishmael, what does he do with the other {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hayah tihyeh l\u8217'ish v\u8217'nedareha aleiha}? The answer is that he uses it for the other teaching of Rabbah, which we will encounter in approximately two {\i dafim}. That {\i drasha} addresses the case where one {\i arus} dies and she subsequently becomes betrothed to another: the double language of {\i v\u8217'hayah tihyeh} signals that the father\u8217's full authority over her \u8212- which he exercised before the first betrothal \u8212- is restored in the interval before the second.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inuy Nefesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to ask: and what does Rabbah himself do with the {\i pasuk} of {\i bein ish l\u8217'ishto bein av l\u8217'vito}? The answer is that he uses it to derive a crucial {\i halacha} about the scope of the husband\u8217's power of {\i hafara}: what categories of vows can a husband annul?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Torah} repeatedly employs the phrase {\i le\u8217'esor issar al nafshah} \u8212- to make something forbidden upon herself \u8212- and the related {\i issar le\u8217'annot nefesh}, which refers to a type of self-denial or self-affliction. This is the same root used on {\i Yom Kippur} \u8212- {\i te\u8217'anu et nafshoteichem}. This explicit language establishes {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- affliction-type vows \u8212- as one category the husband may annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Devarim Shebeino\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second category is not stated explicitly but is derived. There are vows that do not rise to the threshold of {\i inuy nefesh} but that directly affect the dynamics of the husband-wife relationship. For example, she takes a vow that forbids her certain garments or modes of personal appearance. That may not constitute {\i inuy nefesh} in the strict sense, yet it plainly affects the marital relationship. For this second category \u8212- {\i devarim she\u8217'beino l\u8217'veinah}, matters between them \u8212- the {\i Torah}\u8217's phrase {\i bein ish l\u8217'ishto} serves as the source. Thus the two categories the husband may annul are: {\i inuy nefesh} and {\i devarim she\u8217'beino l\u8217'veinah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran vs.\u160 ?Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An interesting question now arises: do these same two categories \u8212- and hence the same {\i limitations} \u8212- apply to the father as well? Or is the father\u8217's authority over his daughter absolute, unlimited by category?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} cites the {\i Yerushalmi} explicitly. The {\i Yerushalmi} asks: we know that {\i inuy nefesh} vows fall under the husband\u8217's power, and that {\i devarim she\u8217'beino l\u8217'veinah} are derived from {\i bein ish l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- but what is the source for the father? The {\i Yerushalmi}\u8217's answer is precise: just as the husband may only annul vows of {\i inuy nefesh} and {\i devarim she\u8217'beino l\u8217'veinah}, so too the father may only annul vows in those same two categories. The {\i Ran} states this explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam}, however, takes a different position. In his view, the father\u8217's authority over his daughter is categorical and unlimited \u8212- he may annul any {\i neder} she takes, without restriction to these two categories. This is not made explicit in the {\i Bavli}, and the {\i Rambam} understands the father\u8217's control to be absolute. As the {\i Ran} notes, this is a direct {\i machloket Rishonim}, and it presumably reflects a deeper question about the extent of parental and spousal authority as understood by {\i Chazal}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hafara Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a conceptual question about the mechanics of {\i hafara} in this partnership: {\i ba\u8217'al megez gayiz o miklesh kalesh?} When the husband \u8212- or, by extension, either partner \u8212- performs {\i hafara}, does he {\i cut off} half of the {\i neder}, or does he merely {\i weaken} it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i megez gayiz} position holds that each partner\u8217's act of {\i hafara} has an independent and concrete effect: each one cuts off his half. Under this view, if one partner does {\i hafara} and the other does not, something real has been accomplished \u8212- half the {\i neder} has been annulled, even if the other half remains. The {\i miklesh kalesh} position holds instead that the act of {\i hafara} by one party weakens the {\i neder} as a whole but does not sever a portion of it. The entire {\i neder} remains, just in a diminished state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Olives Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} illustrates the practical stakes with a concrete case. Suppose she took a vow not to eat two specific olives. The {\i arus} heard and performed {\i hafara}. She then ate both olives. If {\i megez gayiz} is correct \u8212- if he cut off half \u8212- then his {\i hafara} effectively released her from the prohibition on one of the two olives. She thus remained forbidden in one olive, ate it, and would be liable to {\i malkos}. But if {\i miklesh kalesh} is correct \u8212- if he only weakened the {\i neder} \u8212- then the entire prohibition remained in some attenuated form, and eating both olives would constitute a violation, but not one of sufficient weight to generate {\i malkos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Student Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might raise a more fundamental objection: why assume that either partner\u8217's {\i hafara} has any independent effect at all? The structure of the partnership might suggest that nothing occurs until {\i both} parties act \u8212- like a system that requires two keys to be turned simultaneously before anything happens. If one key is turned without the other, nothing changes. This challenge is a serious one, and the {\i gemara} does not address it conceptually. Instead, it brings a {\i braita} as a proof-text and resolves the question from there.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father as Anchor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Principle Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} opens by articulating the foundational principle: {\i met ha-ba\u8217'al nidrokna reshut la\u8217'av; met ha\u8217'av v\u8217'lo nidrokna reshut la\u8217'ba\u8217'al} \u8212- when the husband dies, his authority empties out to the father; when the father dies, his authority does not transfer to the husband. This is simply another formulation of the theme we have been developing: the father is the {\i anchor} of the {\i hafara} partnership. If the husband dies, full control reverts to the father \u8212- who was always the primary party anyway. But if the father dies, the husband is left without the essential partner, and his own rights cannot stand alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Dies Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} now unpacks when it is that the husband\u8217's death empties his authority to the father. The answer: {\i bizman she-lo shama ha-ba\u8217'al kodem she-yamut} \u8212- when the husband had not heard the {\i neder} before he died, or when he heard and was silent, or when he heard and actively performed {\i hafara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three scenarios are thus identified where the husband did nothing problematic prior to dying. First, he never heard the {\i neder} at all. Second, he heard but died within that same day \u8212- meaning his silence never extended to a full period that would constitute {\i kiyum}. Third, he heard and actively performed {\i hafara}. In all three cases, he either did nothing or did nothing harmful, and the father may now take full control and annul the {\i neder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiyum Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are, however, two bad scenarios. The first is that the husband heard and performed an explicit {\i kiyum} \u8212- he actively confirmed the {\i neder}. The second is that he heard, remained silent for a full day\u8217's period, and then died \u8212- making his silence effectively a {\i kiyum} by inaction, a kind of pocket {\i kiyum}. In either of these cases, {\i ein yachol le-hafer} \u8212- even though he has now died and the father is the anchor, the husband has already put a stop to the possibility of {\i hafara}. That cannot be undone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Dies Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Hafara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} now turns to the reverse scenario: the father dies and the husband cannot annul, even when the father had already performed active {\i hafara}. The case: the father heard the {\i neder} and performed {\i hafara}, but the husband did not hear about it before the father died. One might reason that this case is particularly strong for the {\i neder} being annulled \u8212- the husband was not yet in the picture at all, the father alone acted, and the father did everything he was supposed to do. Perhaps the husband can now simply finalize what was all but completed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} notes that the {\i chidush} here is precisely to reject this reasoning. Despite the father having performed his {\i hafara}, despite the husband being entirely absent and peripheral, {\i met ha\u8217'av lo nidrokna} \u8212- the rights do not pass to the husband. The father is the anchor, and the {\i hafara} requires the father\u8217's presence all the way through. If the father dies, there is no completion. The husband cannot finish what the father began.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she takes an entirely new {\i neder} after the father has died, the husband similarly cannot annul it \u8212- not until {\i nissu\u8217'in}. This is a point that underscores how thoroughly the husband\u8217's {\i hafara} authority is contingent on the father. Even if the two of them were the only parties involved, and she makes the {\i neder} after {\i kiddushin} but before {\i nissu\u8217'in}, he has no independent standing to annul. And even once they do marry and {\i nissu\u8217'in} occurs, he cannot annul retroactively a {\i neder} from before {\i nissu\u8217'in}, because retroactive {\i hafara} of prior vows is not within his power at that point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partnership Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues with a case that uses the explicit language of {\i shutafut}: {\i she\u8217'ein ha-ba\u8217'al meifer ela b\u8217'shutafut} \u8212- the husband can only annul as part of a {\i partnership} with the father. Here the father heard the {\i neder} and performed {\i hafara}, but the husband had not heard, and then the father died. Since the vow was taken while both the father and the husband were in the picture \u8212- even if the husband had not yet engaged with it \u8212- there remains a portion of the {\i hafara} that conceptually belongs to the husband. The father cannot supply that on his own after the husband\u8217's death, and certainly the husband cannot supply the father\u8217's portion. Without the {\i shutafut}, the {\i hafara} is incomplete.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Takeover\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} now reaches its final and most complex case. The father heard the {\i neder} and performed {\i hafara}. The husband, however, died without ever having heard the {\i neder} at all \u8212- not just without having responded to it, but without even knowing it existed. This is an extreme case of the husband\u8217's non-involvement. One might argue that since he was never in the picture at all, the father\u8217's {\i hafara} should simply operate as though there were no husband \u8212- as the dominant and essentially exclusive party.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai v. Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is disputed by {\i Beis Shammai} and {\i Beis Hillel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai} holds: {\i chozer ha-av u-meifer chelko shel ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the father goes back and annuls the husband\u8217's portion. Notably, even {\i Beis Shammai} does not say the {\i neder} is simply annulled automatically now that the husband is gone. Rather, there is still a distinct act required: the father must perform an additional {\i hafara} specifically for the portion that would have been the husband\u8217's. Since the {\i neder} was taken while there was a {\i arus}, there is a portion of it that had been under his purview. Even though he never acted on it, that portion was not dissolved by the father\u8217's initial {\i hafara}. Upon the husband\u8217's death, the father now assumes that right and can annul it with a further act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan clarifies: this is the position of {\i Beis Shammai}, not {\i Beis Hillel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Hillel} says: {\i ein yachol lehafer} \u8212- the father cannot annul the husband\u8217's portion. According to {\i Beis Hillel}, when the groom was in the picture and died, the right that was his does not simply pass to the father. Nobody now steps into the groom\u8217's role, and consequently the {\i neder} cannot be fully annulled. The girl remains bound by it \u8212- which, as one might note, may in some cases actually be her preference.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shma Mina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to its original question about {\i hafara} mechanics and draws the answer directly from this {\i machloket}. {\i Shma mina leBeis Shammai megez gayiz, leBeis Hillel miklesh kalesh} \u8212- we may conclude from this debate: {\i Beis Shammai} holds {\i megez gayiz}, that each party\u8217's {\i hafara} cuts off his half; {\i Beis Hillel} holds {\i miklesh kalesh}, that each party\u8217's {\i hafara} merely weakens the {\i neder} as a whole. {\i Shma mina} \u8212- this is the resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} explains what precisely is at stake in each position, and why the {\i macholoket} about {\i hafara} mechanics maps onto the {\i macholoket} about whether the father can take over the husband\u8217's portion. The key concept the {\i Ran} introduces is {\i yerusha} \u8212- inheritance or transfer of rights. The question of whether the father can step into the husband\u8217's role is modeled on whether a {\i right} can be transferred, which in turn depends on whether that right is sufficiently concrete and substantial to function like a transferable object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Beis Shammai}, the father\u8217's initial {\i hafara} annulled precisely his half \u8212- {\i chelko bilvad}. The husband\u8217's half was entirely unaffected \u8212- {\i lo nigrah klal} \u8212- it remained completely intact. That means there is something whole and substantial that was the husband\u8217's: the full right to annul the {\i neder} with respect to his portion, equivalent to one of the two olives in the earlier example. Because that right is intact and concrete \u8212- {\i chashuv hu} \u8212- it can be transferred and inherited by the father when the husband dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Beis Hillel}, when the father performed {\i hafara}, he did not sever a clean half. Rather, he weakened the {\i neder} as a whole \u8212- {\i miklesh kalesh} \u8212- affecting both portions together. What the husband is left with is therefore not a full, intact right to annul his half, but merely the right to act on a vow that has already been weakened. That is not substantial enough to constitute a transferable object \u8212- {\i eino chashuv lehorisho la\u8217'av}. Furthermore, since the father is still alive when this weakening occurs, his own portion of the {\i hafara} has not yet fully taken effect either; the {\i neder} sits in a weakened state. A weakened right is all the more incapable of being transferred, and thus the father cannot inherit it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} articulates the underlying model clearly: we are using the framework of {\i yerusha} \u8212- of transmissible rights \u8212- and for that framework to apply, the thing being transferred must be sufficiently object-like. A half-annulment that is whole and concrete can be conceptualized as a thing and passed on. An attenuation of the whole, with no clean half belonging to anyone, cannot.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 70\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father & Groom Partnersh\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present {\i mishna} returns to the fundamental dynamic of the partnership between a father and an {\i arus} in annulling vows, after the preceding discussion of {\i hafarah} and {\i hakamah} performed in sequence. The {\i mishna} states: {\i meit av, lo nisroknah reshut l\u8217'baal} \u8212- if the father dies, his authority does not transfer to the husband. The underlying principle is that the father is the anchor of the entire annulment process. Even if the father has already performed his portion of the {\i hafarah}, the {\i arus} cannot complete it on his own, because the {\i hafarah} is fundamentally grounded in the father\u8217's presence and participation. Without the father, the process cannot be concluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom\u8217's Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The converse, however, obtains in the opposite direction: {\i meis habaal, nisroknah reshut l\u8217'av} \u8212- if the {\i arus} dies, his authority transfers back to the father. Whether the {\i arus} had already annulled his portion or had not yet done so, the father may now either complete the annulment or perform it in its entirety. The one exception is if the {\i arus} had performed {\i hakamah} before dying \u8212- in that case, the vow is confirmed and nothing further can be done. The asymmetry is telling: the father can absorb the {\i arus}\u8217's role, but the {\i arus} cannot absorb the father\u8217's, because at bottom it is the father who is the principal actor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Power Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then draws the comparison explicitly: {\i b\u8217'zeh yafeh koach ha\u8217'av mikoach habaal} \u8212- in this respect, the power of the father exceeds that of the {\i arus}. But then the {\i mishna} turns in a surprising direction: {\i b\u8217'davar acher yafeh koach habaal mikoach ha\u8217'av} \u8212- in another matter, the power of the {\i arus} exceeds that of the father. In what respect? {\i Shehabaal meifer b\u8217'bogeres, v\u8217'av eino meifer b\u8217'bogeres} \u8212- the {\i arus} can annul the vow of a {\i bogeres} (a young woman who has passed twelve and a half years of age), whereas the father cannot. The father\u8217's inability is readily understandable: once she becomes a {\i bogeres}, she is no longer {\i bi\u8217'neureiha beis aviha}, no longer subject to her father\u8217's household authority. But why does the {\i arus} have this power? We have been emphasizing throughout that the {\i arus} has no independent authority of his own \u8212- his power derives entirely from piggybacking on the father. If there is no father in the picture, either because she is a {\i bogeres} or because she is a {\i yesomah}, the {\i arus} should have no standing to annul. This question will be addressed momentarily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} begins by asking the source for the rule that when the father dies, the {\i arus} cannot complete the annulment. The answer is the verse {\i bi\u8217'neureiha beis aviha} \u8212- in her youth, in her father\u8217's house. This phrase establishes that she is fundamentally located in her father\u8217's domain; the {\i arus} is an adjunct to that arrangement, not its foundation. Consequently, even if the father performed his part and then died, his absence renders the entire process incomplete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other side, the {\i gemara} asks: from where do we know that when the {\i arus} dies, the father may take over even the {\i arus}\u8217's share \u8212- not merely complete his own portion, but absorb the {\i arus}\u8217's role entirely? Rabba answers from the verse {\i ve\u8217'im hayo tihyeh l\u8217'ish u\u8217'nedareiha aleiha} \u8212- \u8220"and if she shall come to be married to a man, and her vows are upon her.\u8221" The doubling of {\i hayo tihyeh} \u8212- \u8220"having been\u8221" and \u8220"shall be\u8221" \u8212- is read as encompassing the case of successive marriages: she was betrothed to a first man, he died, and now she is to be betrothed to a second. The {\i gemara} derives from this that the father\u8217's rights before the second betrothal ({\i kodem havayah sheniyah}) are equivalent to his rights before the first betrothal ({\i kodem havayah rishonah}). Before groom number one appeared, the father had complete and sole authority to annul. So too, in the interval between groom number one\u8217's death and groom number two\u8217's arrival, the father\u8217's authority is once again complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rights Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately presses this derivation with an objection. The analogy holds cleanly for vows that were never under the {\i arus}\u8217's auspices \u8212- vows made after groom number one died, or vows he simply never heard of. Before groom number one entered the picture, the father\u8217's authority was unlimited and unencumbered. If the parallel is to vows equally free of prior entanglement, then of course the father\u8217's authority is complete in that parallel period. But what of vows that groom number one actually heard? In that situation, some residual dimension of the {\i arus}\u8217's authority had attached to those vows. How do we know that, upon his death, it is specifically the father who inherits that dimension of authority?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this elegantly. If the {\i pasuk} were needed only to tell us that vows the {\i arus} never heard revert to the father, it would be unnecessary \u8212- that ruling already follows from {\i bi\u8217'neureiha beis aviha} alone. A betrothed girl whose {\i arus} dies without having heard the vow remains {\i beis aviha} in every relevant sense, and the father\u8217's authority is self-evident. The very fact that the {\i pasuk} is needed to teach something implies it is teaching something non-obvious: namely, that even vows the {\i arus} had heard and over which he had authority revert to the father upon his death. The father is empowered to annul them in full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Groom Heard Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Analogy Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the principle now established that the father\u8217's authority is restored upon the {\i arus}\u8217's death even regarding vows the {\i arus} had heard, the {\i gemara} examines the analogy underpinning this ruling more carefully. The {\i hayo tihyeh} derivation equates the father\u8217's position before the second betrothal with his position before the first. But this analogy is most naturally read as applying to vows that were never under the first {\i arus}\u8217's authority \u8212- since before the first betrothal, no {\i arus} had ever been in the picture at all. Does the analogy genuinely extend to vows that passed through the first {\i arus}\u8217's {\i reshut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore entertains the possibility that the analogy should be read narrowly: {\i eima hanei milei bi-nedarim she-lo nir\u8217'u la-arus} \u8212- perhaps the father\u8217's restored authority applies only to vows that the first {\i arus} never heard. In those cases, there is a clean structural parallel: before groom number one, the father had full authority over vows made without any {\i arus} involvement; before groom number two, he has full authority over vows equally unrelated to groom number one. The cases are genuinely analogous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what of vows that the first {\i arus} did hear, over which he had some degree of control? One might argue that those vows carry a residual character different from vows that were always purely within the father\u8217's domain, and that the death of the first {\i arus} does not straightforwardly restore the father\u8217's authority over that peculiar category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom\u8217's Residual Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} dismisses this concern, and its reasoning is important. If the {\i pasuk} were only needed to cover vows that the {\i arus} never heard, it would be superfluous \u8212- that ruling is already derivable from {\i bi\u8217'neureiha beis aviha} without any additional verse. A girl whose {\i arus} dies and whose vows he never heard about is simply back to being fully in her father\u8217's house; no new derivation is required. The {\i pasuk} must therefore be addressing a case not already covered \u8212- namely, vows the {\i arus} had heard and over which he had exercised some authority. The ruling is that even for those vows, the father\u8217's authority is restored in full upon the {\i arus}\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reinforces the conceptual framework that has been building throughout: the {\i arus}\u8217's participation in the annulment process is not a free-standing right but a conditional one, wholly dependent on the father\u8217's continued presence and authority. When the {\i arus} dies, there is no independent {\i reshut} of his that persists or passes to another party independently \u8212- it simply dissolves back into the father\u8217's comprehensive authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bogeres Anomaly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom > Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We turn now to the second half of the {\i mishna}\u8217's comparison: the case in which the {\i arus} has greater power than the father. The {\i mishna} states that the {\i arus} can annul the vow of a {\i bogeres} while the father cannot. This is genuinely puzzling. We have established that the {\i arus} has no independent {\i reshut} \u8212- his power is entirely derivative of the father\u8217's. How, then, can he have any authority in a case where the father has none?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Father Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first considers the possibility that the case involves a woman who was betrothed while already a {\i bogeres} \u8212- that is, the father was never in the picture to begin with. If there was no father present at the time of the {\i erusin}, perhaps the {\i arus}\u8217's authority does not need to piggyback on anything and stands on its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom\u8217's Lack of Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this attempted resolution, and it should, because the logic does not hold. The absence of the father from the picture does not generate any new authority in the {\i arus}. We have established that even in cases where the father is gone \u8212- whether because he died or because she was always a {\i yesomah} \u8212- the {\i arus} as such has no power to annul vows. His authority arises only in conjunction with the father\u8217's. Simply removing the father does not fill the vacuum with {\i arus} authority; it leaves a vacuum. So the question remains: what is the source of the {\i arus}\u8217's power over a {\i bogeres}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Later Mishna Reference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 73b Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve the {\i bogeres} anomaly, the {\i gemara} points to a later {\i mishna} on 73b. The principle embedded in the current {\i mishna} is, in effect, borrowed from that later context, and understanding the later {\i mishna} is necessary to make sense of what appears here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} on 73b addresses the case of a {\i bogeres} who waited twelve months after betrothal, or a widow who waited thirty days. Rabbi Eliezer rules: {\i hoil u\u8217'baal chayav bi\u8217'mezonoseha, yafeir} \u8212- since the husband is already obligated to provide for her sustenance, he may annul her vows. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position is that the arrival of the time designated for {\i nisuin} \u8212- the point at which the husband becomes financially responsible for her even though the marriage has not yet technically been consummated \u8212- is sufficient to grant him the annulment authority of a husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Financial Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning underlying Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, as the {\i gemara} explains it, is that once the husband has begun bearing the financial obligations of marriage, and the expected time of {\i nisuin} has arrived, the woman\u8217's vows are implicitly made with an understanding that her husband\u8217's acceptance is a condition of their binding force. He is now in a relationship of financial responsibility toward her, she knows she is about to enter {\i nisuin}, and in that context her vows carry an implicit {\i tnai} of spousal consent. Rabbi Eliezer is therefore prepared to treat her as effectively {\i nesuah} for purposes of {\i hafarah} from the moment those conditions obtain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chakhamim Disagree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chakhamim} reject this position: {\i ein habaal meifer ad shetikanes lireshuto} \u8212- the husband does not have annulment power until she has actually entered into his domain through {\i nisuin}. Financial obligation alone, however significant, does not substitute for the formal entry into the marital relationship. On this view, the {\i arus} has no authority as {\i arus} regardless of how long the betrothal has persisted, and the arrival of the expected time of {\i nisuin} changes nothing legally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna\u8217's Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teaching Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question: why does our {\i mishna} on 70a mention the {\i bogeres} case at all, given that the fuller treatment \u8212- including the time periods, the case of the {\i almana}, and the debate between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i chakhamim} \u8212- belongs to the {\i mishna} on 73b? The {\i gemara} notes the apparent redundancy: {\i hasnina chada zimna} \u8212- we have taught this once already (or will teach it in its proper place), so why mention it here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before answering, the {\i gemara} first refines what the {\i bogeres} case in the later {\i mishna} actually means. The later {\i mishna} mentions a {\i bogeres} who waited twelve months. But the {\i gemara} objects: twelve months is the waiting period applicable to a {\i katana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- a girl under twelve and a half. By a {\i bogeres}, the time of {\i nisuin} is expected to arrive much sooner, within thirty days. Why, then, does the later {\i mishna} attach the twelve-month figure to the {\i bogeres}? The {\i gemara} resolves this by reading the two conditions disjunctively: either she is a {\i bogeres} \u8212- in which case thirty days suffice \u8212- or she is a {\i katana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} who has waited the twelve months. The {\i mishna} on 73b is listing two parallel paths to the same legal threshold, not applying twelve months to the {\i bogeres} specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then offers two possible answers to the question of why the {\i bogeres} is mentioned in our {\i mishna} here rather than only in its natural location. The first answer is {\i ee ba\u8217'is eima hacha davka}: here in our {\i mishna} the mention of the {\i bogeres} is in fact the primary teaching, offered in a compressed form, and the later {\i mishna} on 73b then elaborates with the full details \u8212- the time periods, the {\i almana}, the dispute between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i chakhamim} \u8212- that clarify precisely when this rule applies and according to whom. The second answer inverts this: {\i ve-ee ba\u8217'is eima}, the real and proper location for teaching the {\i bogeres} law is the later {\i mishna} on 73b, where the full discussion naturally belongs. Our {\i mishna} mentions it only incidentally \u8212- {\i ve-aydi de-nasiv reisha bazeh nasiv nami seifa bazeh} \u8212- because our {\i mishna} was structured around the phrase {\i bazeh yafeh koach ha\u8217'av mikoach habaal}, and the redactor wanted a parallel clause in the form {\i bazeh yafeh koach habaal mikoach ha\u8217'av}. To supply that parallel, our {\i mishna} borrowed the {\i bogeres} case from the later {\i mishna}, which is its true home.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceptual Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual upshot must not be lost in these structural observations. The {\i arus} by himself has no independent power whatsoever. Even if the father is dead, even if she is a {\i bogeres}, even if he is the only man in her life \u8212- as {\i arus}, he has no {\i reshut} to annul her vows. His authority exists only as a piggyback on the father\u8217's authority. The single exception \u8212- and it is Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, disputed by the {\i chakhamim} \u8212- is when the time of {\i nisuin} has arrived. At that point, Rabbi Eliezer is prepared to treat her as effectively {\i nesuah} and to grant the husband full annulment power. A question that will require attention when the {\i mishna} on 73b is reached directly: if a {\i katana} reaches the time of {\i nisuin} according to Rabbi Eliezer, does the father still participate? When it is a {\i bogeres} with no father in the picture, the husband\u8217's full power at the time of {\i nisuin} is clean and unencumbered. But where the father is still legally present \u8212- because she has not reached {\i bagrus} \u8212- does Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's grant of husband-like power to the {\i arus} operate alongside or in place of the father\u8217's role? That question remains open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Successive Betrothals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Divorces\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a new scenario that pushes the logic of the father\u8217's authority further. A woman makes a {\i neder}, is betrothed ({\i arusa}), then divorced and re-betrothed \u8212- and this happens multiple times, even up to a hundred times in a single day ({\i bo bayom}). The stipulation that everything occurs on the same day is not incidental. Someone \u8212- either the father or the first {\i arus} \u8212- has heard the vow, the twenty-four-hour window has begun, and all the subsequent betrothals and divorces occur within that same window before the clock expires on whoever has not yet performed the annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Last Groom Powers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halakha} stated in the {\i mishna} is: {\i afilu l\u8217'meiah, aviha u\u8217'va\u8217'alah ha\u8217'acharon mefirin nedareha} \u8212- even if there were a hundred such betrothals and divorces, it is the father and the last {\i arus} together who annul her vows. The last {\i arus} in the chain is the one who participates in the annulment process, not any of the intermediate ones. And crucially, the last {\i arus} participates in annulling vows over which he was never directly a party \u8212- vows that groom number one may well have heard, over which groom number one had some authority, and which passed through potentially dozens of subsequent betrothals before reaching him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father-Focused Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This result is, on reflection, powerful evidence that the entire framework is father-centered. What does groom number ten have to do with vows she made under groom number one? Nothing, in any direct sense. But viewed through the lens we have been developing, the answer becomes clear. Those vows were never really under groom number one\u8217's authority in any primary sense \u8212- they were always fundamentally under the father\u8217's authority. The {\i arus} at any stage is simply the individual who happens to be betrothed to her at the time of the annulment and who therefore joins the father in the process. Whether it is groom number one or groom number ten is irrelevant to the structure, because neither groom is the foundation; the father is the foundation. Groom number ten joins the father in the annulment the same way groom number one would have \u8212- not because he has any special connection to those vows, but because he is the current {\i arus} piggybacking on the father\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Continuous Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then articulates the governing principle: {\i zeh haklal, kol she-lo yatz\u8217'ah li-reshut atzmah sha\u8217'ah achat} \u8212- the rule is that as long as she has never attained independent status for even a moment, the father retains continuous control. What would constitute independence for such a moment? Entry into {\i nisuin} followed by divorce or the husband\u8217's death; the death of the father himself; or the attainment of {\i bagrus}. If none of these has occurred \u8212- if she has passed through an unbroken chain of betrothal, divorce, and re-betrothal without ever exiting the father\u8217's domain \u8212- then {\i aviha u\u8217'va\u8217'al acharon mefirin nedareha}: the father and the current {\i arus} together annul her vows, regardless of how many intermediate {\i arusim} there have been.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Verse Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derivation Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the {\i halakha}, the {\i gemara} asks the obvious question: what is the textual source for the rule that the last {\i arus} can annul vows that the first {\i arus} heard? There is a significant distinction to draw here. We know from the earlier discussion that after groom number one dies and the father\u8217's authority is restored, the father can annul even vows groom number one had heard \u8212- that was derived from {\i bi\u8217'neureiha beis aviha} and the {\i pasuk}\u8217's implicit teaching about the non-obvious case. But how do we know that a subsequent {\i arus} \u8212- not the father, but a new {\i arus} \u8212- gains the power to participate in annulling vows specifically heard by the first {\i arus}? The word {\i nir\u8217'u} (\u8220"were seen,\u8221" i.e., came under the authority of) is used deliberately: once vows were presented to and heard by the first {\i arus}, they carry a distinct character. How does the second {\i arus} acquire authority over them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel answers from the verse: {\i ve\u8217'im hayo tihyeh l\u8217'ish u\u8217'nedareiha aleiha} \u8212- \u8220"and if she shall have been married to a man, and her vows are upon her.\u8221" The phrase {\i nedareiha aleiha} \u8212- \u8220"her vows upon her\u8221" \u8212- refers to vows she already carries with her. The {\i hayo tihyeh} construction, as established earlier, encompasses the case of successive marriages. Shmuel reads {\i nedareiha aleiha} in conjunction with {\i tihyeh}: the vows that were upon her from the first marriage ({\i hayo}) follow her into the second ({\i tihyeh}), and the second husband acquires authority over them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Word \u8216'Aleha\u8217'\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the objection that perhaps this derivation applies only to vows that were never under the first {\i arus}\u8217's auspices \u8212- that is, the {\i hayo tihyeh} teaching addresses the transfer of vows the father controlled, not vows that had passed through the first {\i arus}\u8217's {\i reshut}. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i aleha kra yeseira} \u8212- the word {\i aleha} is superfluous and carries additional legal weight. {\i Nedareiha aleiha} means \u8220"her vows, whatever was upon her\u8221" \u8212- in whatever state they existed, regardless of whether the first {\i arus} had heard them or not. She carries those vows into the new marriage, and the new {\i arus} acquires full authority over them as part of the joint annulment process with the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Timing Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Clock Start\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now identifies several unresolved timing questions that the foregoing analysis leaves open. The first concerns the father\u8217's twenty-four-hour window. When groom number one hears the vow, his twenty-four-hour clock begins. If he then dies and the father\u8217's authority is restored \u8212- including over the vows groom number one heard \u8212- does the father\u8217's own clock begin from the moment groom number one first heard the vow, or does it begin only from the moment the father himself becomes aware of the vow, which may be some hours after the {\i arus}\u8217's death?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Groom\u8217's Clock\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel question arises for the later {\i arus}. When groom number two or groom number ten enters the picture, from what point does his twenty-four-hour window run? Does it begin from when groom number one originally heard the vow \u8212- since the {\i hayo tihyeh} derivation in effect equates the second {\i arus}\u8217's position with the first\u8217's \u8212- or does it begin only from when the later {\i arus} himself first hears about the vow?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 24-Hour Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also the prior definitional question of what exactly the {\i mishna} means by the annulment window. The verse speaks of {\i miyom el yom} \u8212- \u8220"from day to day\u8221" \u8212- which implies a twenty-four-hour period. But the precise parameters of this window, and whether \u8220"day\u8221" means a calendrical day ending at nightfall or a full twenty-four-hour unit measured from the time of hearing, remains to be defined as the {\i gemara} continues. These questions are left open for subsequent discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 71\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Erusin & Divorce Framewo\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 71a. We pick up in the middle of the page at {\i tanya kevatei deShmuel}, but since there is time available, it is worth starting from the very beginning of the daf to set the frame.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Multiple Divorc\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a {\i mishnah} presenting a striking scenario involving a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'urasah} \u8212- a betrothed maiden \u8212- whose vow was made during {\i erusin}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i nadra vehi arusa} \u8212- she made a {\i neder} while betrothed \u8212- {\i nisgaresh bo bayom} \u8212- and she was divorced that same day. We will return later to precisely why the same-day requirement matters; for now, the point is that at least the person who heard the vow has a twenty-four-hour window within which to act. {\i venisarsa bo bayom} \u8212- she then became betrothed again to a second man \u8212- {\i afilu leme\u8217'ah} \u8212- even if this sequence repeated a hundred times over on the same day \u8212- {\i aviha uba\u8217'al ha\u8217'acharon meferin nedareha} \u8212- the father and the last groom may nonetheless annul her vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is initially surprising. For grooms two through one hundred, the {\i neder} was one she had made while betrothed to a different man \u8212- or, in the case of the first groom, a {\i neder} she may have made before the {\i erusin} altogether. And yet the {\i halacha} is that an {\i arus} is {\i mefer bekodmin}: he can annul even vows that predate him. This is because the mechanism of annulment is fundamentally the father\u8217's prerogative, and the {\i arus} merely participates. Since the father is always the anchor, and the {\i neder} has never left the father\u8217's jurisdiction, the current {\i arus} \u8212- whoever he may be \u8212- can always join the father\u8217's annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father as Anchor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The governing principle is stated explicitly: {\i kol shelo yatza lireshut atzmah sha\u8217'ah achat} \u8212- as long as she has not gained independence for even a single moment. Independence is acquired through becoming a {\i bogeres}, through her father\u8217's death, or through {\i nisuin} \u8212- full marriage \u8212- any of which would remove her from the joint {\i erusin} framework. But as long as none of these have occurred, and she has always remained under her father\u8217's authority with grooms merely passing through, the rule is {\i aviha uba\u8217'al ha\u8217'acharon meferin nedareha} \u8212- it is the father and the last groom who perform the annulment together. The last groom piggybacks on what is fundamentally the father\u8217's right, and this is true regardless of how many prior grooms there may have been.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Last Groom & Prior Heari\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niru L\u8217'Arus Rishon Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now probes a question that the {\i mishnah} leaves unanswered. The {\i mishnah} tells us that the last groom can annul together with the father \u8212- but it does not tell us whether the first groom had already {\i heard} the {\i neder}. This distinction matters. One might reason that if the first groom had already heard the vow, he was already an active participant in the process; his right had been engaged. Perhaps in that case the later groom cannot simply step in and take over what had already come under the first groom\u8217's purview. The later groom might be able to substitute only when working from a blank slate \u8212- that is, when the first groom had no involvement at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} formulates the question precisely: {\i minalan de\u8217'arus acharon mafer nedarim sheniru le\u8217'arus rishon} \u8212- from where do we know that the last groom can annul vows that had already been {\i nir\u8217'u le\u8217'arus rishon}, heard by the first groom? It is worth noting, as the term suggests, that {\i niru} here carries the meaning of {\i nishme\u8217'u} \u8212- they were heard, they came to the first groom\u8217's attention and fell within his capacity to act upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Verse Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Shmuel de\u8217'amar kra} \u8212- Shmuel\u8217's answer is grounded in a verse. The Torah states {\i ve\u8217'im hayo tihyeh le\u8217'ish} \u8212- the double language {\i hayo tihyeh} implies a woman who has been married once ({\i hayo}) and then is married again ({\i tihyeh}) \u8212- {\i unedareha aleha} \u8212- and her vows remain upon her. The implication is that she carries her vows into the second marriage from the first: {\i nedarim she\u8217'hayu aleha kevar}, vows already upon her from the time of the first {\i hayo}, accompany her into the second {\i tihyeh}. Despite this carry-over, the final groom still participates in the annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately presses: {\i dilma hani mili shelo niru le\u8217'arus rishon} \u8212- perhaps the verse only applies when the first groom had not yet heard the vow, had not yet entered the process. If so, {\i aval nedarim sheniru le\u8217'arus rishon lo matzi mafer arus acharon} \u8212- but for vows that the first groom had already heard, perhaps the last groom cannot annul them. The word {\i aleha} in the verse is cited as a possible extra term pointing to this additional ruling, though its precise exegetical force is not entirely transparent from the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara\u8217's} resolution is that the {\i halacha} of the {\i mishnah} extends even to the case where the first {\i arus} had full capacity to act \u8212- that is, even when it was {\i nir\u8217'u le\u8217'arus rishon}. The Ran notes something that is not self-evident from the way the passage is phrased: what we might have read as the {\i Gemara\u8217's} own assertion was, according to the Ran, Shmuel\u8217's intended point all along. Shmuel was not merely providing a verse-source for the {\i mishnah}. Rather, by citing the {\i pasuk}, Shmuel was asserting a reading of the {\i mishnah} that extended it to cover the case of {\i nir\u8217'u le\u8217'arus rishon} \u8212- the case where the first groom had already heard the vow. The Ran understands the {\i Gemara} as attributing to Shmuel not just the scriptural derivation but the substantive interpretive claim: the {\i mishnah\u8217's pshat} includes this harder case. This is, admittedly, not how the passage reads on its surface, but that is the Ran\u8217's understanding of what the {\i Gemara} knows about Shmuel\u8217's intent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita on Groom Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Shmuel\u8217's position established \u8212- that even vows heard by the first groom can be annulled by the last \u8212- the {\i Gemara} proceeds to cite a {\i braita} that it claims supports this view: {\i tani kavasei deShmuel}, we have a tradition that accords with Shmuel. The {\i braita} is complex, and as the Ran acknowledges in a lengthy analysis, the {\i Gemara} itself provides little explicit commentary. The basic scenario: {\i na\u8217'arah hame\u8217'urasah aviha veba\u8217'alah meferin nedareha} \u8212- a betrothed maiden, whose father and groom together annul her vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Annuls, Groom Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first scenario in the {\i braita}: {\i shama aviha veheifer lah} \u8212- the father heard the vow and annulled it \u8212- {\i velo hispik haba\u8217'al lishmo\u8217'a ad shemeis} \u8212- and the groom did not manage to hear before he died. {\i Venisarsa bo bayom} \u8212- she became betrothed to another man that day \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'afilu me\u8217'ah pe\u8217'amim} \u8212- even a hundred times over \u8212- {\i aviha veba\u8217'ala ha\u8217'acharon meferin nedareha} \u8212- the father and the last groom annul her vows. As stated, at this point we have not yet advanced beyond the {\i mishnah\u8217's} basic case, and the scenario explicitly describes the first groom as having died {\i before} hearing the vow \u8212- that is, it is a case of {\i lo nir\u8217'u le\u8217'arus rishon}. This is not yet a proof for Shmuel\u8217's more far-reaching claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom Annuls, Father Fin\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario is more interesting: {\i shama ba\u8217'alah veheifer lah} \u8212- the groom heard and annulled his portion \u8212- {\i velo hispik ha\u8217'av lishmo\u8217'a ad shemeis haba\u8217'al} \u8212- and the father had not yet heard when the groom died. The father is the anchor of the entire process; we would therefore expect that since the father remains, he can complete the annulment. And indeed: {\i chozer ha\u8217'av u\u8217'meifer chelko shel ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the father goes back and annuls the groom\u8217's portion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This formulation is striking. One might have supposed the father need only annul his own portion, since the groom had already done his. But the {\i braita} says the father must annul {\i chelko shel ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the groom\u8217's portion \u8212- as well as his own. The explanation appears to be that when the groom died, the effect of his {\i hafara} was lost \u8212- the annulment does not survive him. Yet the fact that he had staked a role in the process means someone must still play that role. The father therefore steps in and performs a double {\i hafara}: one in his own capacity as father, and one in the capacity of the deceased groom. This is consistent with the principle encountered earlier in the tractate \u8212- the father is the anchor, and the groom\u8217's rights, when they lapse, revert in some fashion to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai vs Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Natan}: {\i hein hein divrei Beit Shammai} \u8212- this represents the position of Beit Shammai, not an uncontested ruling. {\i Aval Beit Hillel omrim ein yachol lehafer} \u8212- Beit Hillel says the father cannot annul at all in this situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At first glance this seems to echo an earlier discussion about whether the groom\u8217's right, upon his death, is {\i migaz gayiz} \u8212- fully cut off and therefore transferable \u8212- or {\i miklash kalosh} \u8212- weakened but not cleanly severed, making it harder to transfer. But the present case, taken alone, seems to concern only one father and one groom; what does it have to do with our {\i mishnah\u8217's} question about whether a later groom can step in when it was {\i nir\u8217'u le\u8217'arus rishon}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, worked out by the Ran at considerable length, is that the {\i Gemara} reads the {\i braita} as addressing a scenario with {\i two} grooms. The first groom heard the vow, performed {\i hafara}, and then died. A second groom has now stepped in. When Beit Hillel says {\i ein yachol lehafer} \u8212- he cannot annul \u8212- the referent is the father together with the second groom. Even with a new groom on the scene, the father cannot complete the annulment. This is the case of {\i nir\u8217'u le\u8217'arus rishon} \u8212- but with the additional complication that the first groom not only heard the vow but actually performed {\i hafara} before dying.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Debate Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Beit Shammai: {\i nedarim she\u8217'niru le\u8217'arus nisrokna reshuto la\u8217'av umageiz gayiz} \u8212- vows that had come under the first groom\u8217's authority revert to the father when the groom dies, and the transfer ({\i migaz gayiz}) is effective. Even though the first groom staked his role in the process by performing {\i hafara}, Beit Shammai holds that his portion can nonetheless be transferred \u8212- first back to the father and, by extension, to a subsequent groom. The annulment process can therefore be completed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel disagrees: {\i aviha uba\u8217'al ha\u8217'achron meifirin nedareha, v\u8217'lo migaz gayiz} \u8212- the father and the last groom could in principle annul, but {\i lo migaz gayiz} \u8212- the transfer does not work. Once the first groom has exercised his right and performed {\i hafara}, he has positioned himself as a genuine stakeholder in the {\i neder}. His act created a role that is now his and his alone. When he dies, the effect of his {\i hafara} is lost \u8212- but the role he staked is not forgotten. According to Beit Hillel, once a groom has asserted and exercised that role, nobody else can step into it. The father cannot reclaim it, and neither can a later groom substitute for him. The {\i neder} therefore cannot be annulled, and the woman is left bound by it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an irony worth noting: the outcome is actually worse when the first groom acted than when he did nothing. Had he merely heard the vow and then died without acting, his role would not have been staked, and the question of substitution would not arise. The father and later groom could simply proceed. But because he performed {\i hafara} \u8212- because he inserted himself as a participant in the annulment process \u8212- that act, though its effect dissipates with his death, leaves a residue that blocks anyone else from filling the void. He put his key in the lock, so to speak, and that act of engagement makes the role irreversibly his. This is {\i miklash kalosh} as opposed to {\i migaz gayiz}: the engagement weakened and transformed the {\i neder} in a way that entangles his portion with his identity as the one who acted, rather than cleanly cutting off a separable share.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafara as Key Factor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} draws the crucial distinction: this entire debate \u8212- Beit Shammai against Beit Hillel \u8212- arises specifically because the first groom performed {\i hafara}. If he had merely heard the vow and done nothing \u8212- if he had {\i not} exercised his right \u8212- then even Beit Hillel would agree that his potential right creates no obstacle. It would simply revert to the father, or pass to a later groom, without complication. Both schools would concur that the final groom and father may proceed. The debate is {\i davka} because the groom acted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} therefore does serve as a proof for Shmuel\u8217's broader claim, though not a direct one. The presumption underlying the debate of Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel is that if the first groom merely heard the vow without performing {\i hafara} \u8212- the case of {\i niru} without action \u8212- then a later groom unquestionably can step in. This assumption is shared by both schools, and it is precisely Shmuel\u8217's point about the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerushin & Hakama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerushin Status Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a new and important question: {\i gerushin k\u8217'shtika damya o k\u8217'hakama damya?} \u8212- does the act of divorce function halachically as {\i shtika} (silence, i.e., doing nothing), or does it function as {\i hakama} (affirmation of the vow)?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question has several layers. On one reading, divorce could be seen as {\i hakama} because it is causally related to the {\i neder}: when a husband divorces his wife rather than annulling the vow, his choice implies that he assumes the {\i neder} will remain in force. He had another option \u8212- {\i hafara} \u8212- and instead he divorced. This suggests that he accepts, or even endorses, the {\i neder\u8217's} continued existence. On another reading, the act of divorce itself \u8212- entirely apart from any inferred intention \u8212- constitutes an implicit {\i hakama} by virtue of the fact that the husband is formally relinquishing his right to perform {\i hafara}. The act of exiting the relationship, and with it the legal capacity to annul, might be categorized as an affirmation by conduct, regardless of what was going through his mind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, perhaps divorce is simply neutral \u8212- like {\i shtika} \u8212- a circumstantial exit from the marriage that says nothing about the {\i neder} one way or the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Right After Div\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most direct {\i nafka mina} \u8212- practical consequence \u8212- is whether the father retains the ability to be {\i meifer} after the {\i arus} has divorced the woman. If {\i gerushin} functions as {\i shtika}, then the {\i arus} has simply exited the picture without having done anything to the {\i neder}, and the father can proceed and annul his own portion (and, arguably, the groom\u8217's as well). But if {\i gerushin} functions as {\i hakama}, then the {\i arus} has effectively affirmed the {\i neder} by divorcing her, and the father\u8217's ability to annul is compromised just as it would be had the {\i arus} explicitly confirmed the vow verbally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also raises an underlying question that the {\i Gemara} has not yet fully confronted: does {\i hakama} require a verbal act? {\i Nedarim} in general operate through formal verbal declarations \u8212- that is virtually the first principle of the tractate. Does {\i hakama}, which the {\i Gemara} in earlier contexts compared to a new {\i neder}, require the same formality? Or can it be constituted by a demonstration of will \u8212- an act, a choice, a conduct \u8212- without any words at all? The case of {\i gerushin} pushes precisely on this question, since divorce is accomplished through a written document ({\i get}), not through speech, and even setting that aside, the question is whether the {\i act} of divorce, irrespective of formality, can operate as an affirmation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One example of {\i hakama} by conduct already encountered in the tractate: a woman says {\i hareini nezira} and her husband responds {\i va\u8217'ani} \u8212- \u8220"and so am I.\u8221" This is treated as {\i hakama}. But there, the husband\u8217's words directly engage the {\i neder} and affirm it. The case of {\i gerushin} is both stronger and weaker: stronger in that the divorce formally alters the husband\u8217's legal position with respect to the marriage; weaker in that the act does not directly or verbally address the {\i neder} at all, but only demonstrates an assumption about it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce & Remarriage Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific scenario the {\i Gemara} constructs for this question: {\i k\u8217'gon she-nadra v\u8217'shama ba\u8217'alah v\u8217'gershah v\u8217'hachzirah b\u8217'yomei} \u8212- she made a {\i neder}, her husband heard it, divorced her, and took her back that same day. {\i I amar mar k\u8217'shtika damya} \u8212- if {\i gerushin} is like {\i shtika} \u8212- {\i matzi meifer leh} \u8212- he can still annul. {\i V\u8217'i amar mar k\u8217'hakama damya} \u8212- but if {\i gerushin} is like {\i hakama} \u8212- {\i lo matzi meifer} \u8212- he cannot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran notes an important qualification about this scenario: it cannot be a case of {\i nesuah} \u8212- a fully married woman \u8212- because if a married woman is divorced and then remarried, the vow would now have the status of {\i kodmin} with respect to the second marriage, and a husband cannot be {\i meifer bekodmin}. The case therefore must involve an {\i arusa}. With an {\i arus}, there is no {\i kodmin} problem, since the {\i arus} is always {\i meifer bekodmin} by virtue of the father\u8217's role. So the question is posed in the context of {\i erusin}: the {\i arus} heard the vow, divorced her, and then became betrothed to her again on the same day \u8212- can he now annul?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First Proof Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logical Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now attempts to prove from the {\i mishnah} itself that {\i gerushin} does not constitute {\i hakama}. Recall that the {\i mishnah} presents a case of {\i nisgaresh bo bayom venisarsa bo bayom} \u8212- she was divorced and remarried, cycling through multiple {\i arusim}, yet the father and the last {\i arus} can annul. {\i Shema mina gerushin k\u8217'shtika dami} \u8212- learn from this that {\i gerushin} is like {\i shtika} \u8212- {\i de\u8217'ei k\u8217'hakama dami, mi matzi arus acharon} \u8212- because if {\i gerushin} were {\i hakama}, how could the last {\i arus} possibly annul {\i nidrei de\u8217'ika arus rishon} \u8212- a {\i neder} that the first {\i arus} had already affirmed through his act of divorce? If each divorce constitutes {\i hakama}, then by the time she reaches the last {\i arus}, the {\i neder} has been affirmed repeatedly, and no one can annul it. Yet the {\i mishnah} says the last {\i arus} and father may annul \u8212- seemingly demonstrating that {\i gerushin} carries no weight as {\i hakama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a compelling argument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Qualification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects it: {\i hacha bemai askinan \u8212- b\u8217'shelo shama arus rishon} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} is dealing with a case where the first {\i arus} never heard the vow at all. If none of the prior {\i arusim} ever heard the {\i neder}, then their divorces are meaningless with respect to {\i hakama}; you cannot affirm what you do not know. Accordingly, the {\i mishnah} proves nothing about whether {\i gerushin} functions as {\i hakama} when the {\i arus} actually heard the vow \u8212- which is the operative question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this response creates a further difficulty: {\i mai irya bo bayom} \u8212- why does the {\i mishnah} specify that the divorces and re-betrothals all occurred {\i on the same day}? If the prior {\i arusim} never heard the vow, there is no ticking clock as far as they are concerned. {\i Afilu achar me\u8217'ah yamim nami} \u8212- even if she remarried after a hundred days, the {\i arus} who never heard would pose no {\i hakama} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that the clock that is running belongs to the {\i father}, not to the prior {\i arusim}. It is the father who heard the {\i neder}, and the father who has a twenty-four-hour window. The {\i mishnah\u8217's} same-day requirement reflects the fact that the entire process \u8212- father and last {\i arus} annulling together \u8212- must be completed within the father\u8217's window. The scenario is constructed so that the father can act jointly with the last {\i arus} within the day on which the father heard. This also means, by implication, that presumably the father could in principle have annulled his own portion at any point within his window. But since the {\i arus} must also participate, and the {\i arus} can only act together with the father, it is structured as a joint same-day annulment timed to the father\u8217's hearing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerushin Braita Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Divorce Remarry Brai\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} tries once more: {\i ta shema} \u8212- {\i nadara bo bayom, girsha vehachzira bo bayom, ein yachol lehafer} \u8212- she made a vow that day, he divorced her and took her back that day, and he cannot annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerushin as Hakama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At first glance this is exactly the proof sought: the {\i arus} heard the vow, divorced her, and took her back \u8212- and he cannot annul. {\i Shema mina gerushin k\u8217'hakama dami} \u8212- this implies that the divorce functioned as an affirmation, thereby blocking the subsequent annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nesuah Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} immediately counters: {\i ami hacha benisua askinan} \u8212- this {\i braita} is discussing a {\i nesuah}, a fully married woman, not a betrothed maiden. {\i Vehai ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'ein yachol lehafer mishum de\u8217'ein haba\u8217'al meifer bekodmin} \u8212- and the reason he cannot annul is not because {\i gerushin} is {\i hakama}, but simply because a husband who has married a woman cannot be {\i meifer bekodmin} \u8212- he cannot annul vows that predate their marriage. After the divorce and remarriage, the vow now stands as {\i kodmin} relative to the new marriage, and that is what bars him from annulling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Nesuah Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran presses this further. One might try to salvage the proof by noting that two marriages are present \u8212- could one of them save the situation? The Ran explains why neither can. If the first marriage was {\i nisuin} and the husband then divorced and remarried as an {\i arus}, the new betrothal is an {\i erusin} without a father in the picture \u8212- because the {\i nisuin} removed her from her father\u8217's jurisdiction. An {\i arus} without a father has no capacity to annul at all. Conversely, if the second marriage is {\i nisuin}, there is no {\i kodmin} problem from the husband\u8217's side, but now the vow falls under the {\i nisuin} framework entirely, with no father. Either way, the {\i nesuah} scenario fails to isolate the question about {\i gerushin} and {\i hakama}, and no proof can be extracted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hakama Nature & Form\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerushin Status Unresolv\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is that the question \u8212- {\i gerushin k\u8217'shtika damya o k\u8217'hakama damya} \u8212- remains unresolved. No available source definitively settles whether the act of divorce, when a groom has heard the {\i neder} and then divorces the woman, constitutes an affirmation of the vow or merely a neutral exit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakama & Verbal Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the discussion does illuminate, however, is a broader and important question that underlies the entire inquiry: what does {\i hakama} require? Is {\i hakama} an act that must be performed verbally, in the same way that a {\i neder} requires a verbal declaration, or can it be constituted through conduct \u8212- through a demonstration of will or a change in legal status that implies an assumption about the {\i neder\u8217's} continuity?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We have seen from earlier in the tractate that {\i hakama} was in certain respects compared to a {\i neder}, such that it can itself be subject to a {\i hatara} \u8212- a release. That framing suggests a degree of formality. Yet the case of {\i gerushin} entertains the possibility that an act entirely unrelated to the {\i neder} in its formal verbal content \u8212- namely, the delivery of a {\i get} \u8212- might nonetheless count as {\i hakama} by virtue of what it implies about the husband\u8217's stance. The {\i Gemara} leaves this question open, and with it the larger question of whether {\i hakama} by conduct, without explicit speech directed at the {\i neder}, is halachically cognizable at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 72\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-Marital Vow Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Overview & Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up on 72b, {\i amud bet}, with a new {\i mishna}. The {\i mishna} continues in the theme of the father and groom who together annul vows in partnership, but in doing so it raises \u8212- somewhat in passing \u8212- a central question that the {\i gemara} will open up and explore in depth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that our {\i perek} in Nedarim follows a pattern common to many {\i masechtos} and {\i perakim}: it begins with the more obscure and in some ways more fascinating {\i halacha} \u8212- the intricate details of the partnership between father and groom, who is the {\i ikar}, how the combination functions, and so on \u8212- rather than starting from first principles. A more straightforward pedagogical approach would have first explained how {\i hafara} works, what the twenty-four-hour period entails, what {\i hakama} is, and so forth. Instead, all of that gets addressed along the way, as the {\i gemara} works through the more complex and nuanced cases. This is simply further confirmation that the {\i gemara} and the {\i mishnayos} were organized for those already in the conversation \u8212- not as Nedarim for beginners.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father & Groom Partnersh\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the top of 72b opens with a striking phrase: {\i derech talmidei chachamim} \u8212- it is the way of Torah sages. It is worth pausing on this, as it is unusual for a {\i mishna} to invoke the category of {\i talmidei chachamim} so directly. The {\i mishna} then describes the following practice: before a man\u8217's daughter is about to leave his home \u8212- and {\i yotzes} here means {\i nissu\u8217'in}, marriage proper, since {\i kiddushin} and {\i erusin} leave her still legally in her father\u8217's house \u8212- a conscientious father would ensure that any outstanding vows were addressed. Once she becomes {\i nesu\u8217'ah}, the father exits the picture entirely: he can no longer annul, and her husband cannot annul vows that predate the {\i nissu\u8217'in} because they are {\i kodmim} \u8212- they preceded his jurisdiction. The only window for annulling such prior vows is before the {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i mishna} tells us: {\i derech talmidei chachamim}, before his daughter departs his home, a father would say to her, {\i kol nedarim she-nadart betoch beisi harei hein mefurim} \u8212- any vows you have taken in my house are hereby annulled. This is a blanket annulment, reminiscent in structure of {\i hataras nedarim} on {\i erev Rosh Hashana}. Clearly, he does not know the specific content of the vows; if he did, the situation would have played out differently, and the urgency of doing it now, before the window closes, would not have the same character. The {\i mishna} then adds: {\i vechain haba\u8217'al}, and so too the groom, before she enters his domain \u8212- since once she is {\i mekudeshes} his participation is already required \u8212- would similarly say: {\i kol nedarim she-nadart ad shelo tikanesi lirshusi harei hein mufarim} \u8212- any vows you may have taken before the {\i chuppah} are annulled. Because once the {\i chuppah} occurs and she is {\i nesu\u8217'ah}, he can no longer address vows that preceded his jurisdiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blanket Annulment Practi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication of the {\i mishna} is immediately apparent: both the father and the groom are issuing blanket annulments of vows they have not heard. The framing could not be clearer \u8212- they say \u8220"any vows you may have taken,\u8221" which indicates they are unaware of any specific vow. And crucially, there is no sense of a clock running out that would require them to speak now rather than later. The only urgency is the impending {\i nissu\u8217'in} after which the window closes entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question rooted in the {\i pesukim} in {\i Matos}: the Torah says {\i min hayom shema\u8217'o} \u8212- from the day he heard. It sounds as though hearing is a prerequisite for annulment. And one might add: if a husband can issue a standing blanket annulment without hearing, then presumably he could do so every night as a matter of course, and his wife\u8217's vows would always be pre-annulled. These are precisely the questions the {\i gemara} will now explore.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hearing Requirement Deba\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami Bar Chama\u8217's Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'i Rami Bar Chama} \u8212- Rami Bar Chama poses the question: {\i ba\u8217'al mahu sheyafer belo shmiya?} Can a husband \u8212- and the same question applies equally to a father, there is no distinction here \u8212- annul a vow without having heard it? {\i V\u8217'shama isha gabeyhu} \u8212- does the requirement that the husband hear function as the trigger that empowers him to annul? {\i O lav davka hu} \u8212- or is that phrase not to be read literally as a strict requirement?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is almost striking to take a {\i pasuk} from the Torah and say {\i lav davka} \u8212- as if to say, the phrase doesn\u8217't really mean what it seems to say. But that is precisely what is at stake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lav Davka Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\i lav davka} must mean here requires some unpacking. It would not be a wholesale dismissal of the phrase as meaningless. Rather, the more precise reading would be: the husband has twenty-four hours from the time he heard. He need not hear in order to perform the act of annulment, but once he has heard, he has only twenty-four hours remaining. Failure to annul within twenty-four hours of hearing is treated as {\i hakama} \u8212- tacit confirmation of the vow \u8212- since his silence upon learning of it for an entire day is construed as acquiescence. So {\i lav davka} would not mean hearing is irrelevant; it would mean that hearing is not a precondition for annulling, but it is the event that starts the twenty-four-hour clock. The {\i gemara} does not state this explicitly, but it appears to be the necessary {\i pshat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We should not simply say {\i lav davka} without accounting for the word\u8217's role at all. Rather, {\i b\u8217'yom shama\u8217'o} informs us of the consequences of hearing \u8212- the twenty-four-hour window \u8212- without making hearing itself a precondition for the act of annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Conversation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava ta shma} \u8212- Rava says, come and hear from our {\i mishna} itself. The {\i mishna} says {\i derech talmidei chachamim} \u8212- and it is interesting to note that the {\i mishna} identifies this practice specifically with {\i talmidei chachamim}, perhaps because knowing that a blanket annulment of unknown vows is valid, and that hearing is not strictly required to perform it, itself requires significant {\i halachic} awareness. The {\i mishna} describes the father giving a comprehensive annulment of any vows the daughter may have taken \u8212- {\i ve-ha lo shama?} But he didn\u8217't hear them. So how does this work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i v\u8217'chi shama hu demifer} \u8212- no, what the {\i mishna} means is that he is not actually annulling now. Rather, he is opening the conversation with her. By saying \u8220"any vows you may have taken are hereby annulled,\u8221" he is prompting her to disclose whatever vows she may have made, so that he can then perform the actual annulment upon hearing. So the {\i mishna}\u8217's case involves a father who initiates the discussion precisely in order to get his daughter to articulate her vows \u8212- and only then does the formal annulment occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a significant reading into the {\i mishna}, which on its face sounds like a genuine blanket annulment of unspecified vows. The {\i gemara} then turns to the second half of the {\i mishna}: {\i vechain haba\u8217'al}, the groom likewise says this before the {\i nissu\u8217'in}. And here too the {\i gemara} applies the same logic \u8212- {\i hacha nami de-amar lah lechishe\u8217'eshma\u8217'enah} \u8212- he is saying that when he hears, the annulment will take effect. This is how the {\i gemara} reads the case: the act of annulment can be performed before hearing, but its effect is contingent on hearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now this is a genuinely interesting middle position. One might have thought: if hearing is required for the effect, surely the very act of annulment cannot be initiated before that threshold is met \u8212- much like the case of {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}, where one cannot perform a legal act regarding something that does not yet exist. And yet the {\i gemara} here seems to carve out an intermediate stance: the act can be initiated prior to hearing, but the effect can only take hold once hearing has occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father vs.\u160 ?Husband Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran raises a pointed question: why is this resolution stated specifically regarding the husband, and not the father? The distinction is significant. For the father, if he performs the act of annulment now and hearing only occurs later, that \u8220"later\u8221" might well be after the {\i chuppah} \u8212- after which the daughter is entirely outside his domain. Since neither the moment of the act nor the moment of the effect corresponds to a time when he is genuinely empowered to annul, it is difficult to see how the two could combine into a valid {\i hafara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the husband the situation is different. He performs the act now as an {\i arus} \u8212- he already holds the power to annul in partnership with the father. And when he later hears, he will hear as a {\i ba\u8217'al}, a fully married husband, at which point he has comprehensive authority over her vows. So the act performed at one valid stage and the effect triggered at another valid stage can indeed combine. The father, by contrast, must work within the {\i gemara}\u8217's first reading: he initiates conversation, she discloses, and he annuls before the {\i chuppah} while he still can.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Annulment Before Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Annulment of Future Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ta shma} \u8212- come and hear. The {\i gemara} now introduces a new {\i mishna} \u8212- which we find at 75a \u8212- that takes the question even further. The case: a man says to his wife before departing on a journey, {\i kol nedarim she-tidori mi-kan ad she-avo mi-makom ploni} \u8212- any vows that you make from now until I return from such-and-such a place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Existent Vows Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These are not vows that exist and have merely not been heard \u8212- these are vows that do not yet exist at all. The {\i mishna} addresses two scenarios. If the husband says {\i harei hen kayamin} \u8212- I am confirming them, I am being {\i mekayem} \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}, his declaration is null. One cannot strengthen something that does not yet exist; {\i kiyum} requires the vow to already have some footing before it can be anchored. On this everyone agrees.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if he says {\i harei hen mufarin} \u8212- I am annulling them \u8212- then Rabbi Eliezer says it works, while the {\i Chachamim} say it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prevention Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Eliezer}: why should pre-emptive annulment be less effective than after-the-fact annulment? {\i Im hefer nedarim she-bau li-khlal issur, lo yafer nedarim she-lo bau li-khlal issur?} If he can annul a vow once it has already entered the realm of prohibition, surely he can act to prevent one from entering that realm altogether. Prevention is easier than cure; an ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure. This is also why the cases are not symmetrical: {\i kiyum} means strengthening, and you cannot strengthen what is not yet there. But {\i hafara} means preventing or removing \u8212- and surely preventing is even more achievable than curing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Establishment Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} respond from the {\i pasuk} itself: {\i ishah yekimenu ve-ishah yefirenu} \u8212- her husband shall establish it and her husband shall annul it. {\i Kol she-ba li-khlal hakam ba li-khlal hafer} \u8212- only something that can be subject to {\i hakama} can be subject to {\i hafara}. If a vow cannot be established \u8212- because it does not yet exist \u8212- it cannot be annulled either. This may reflect the mechanics of the annulment process, or it may simply be a {\i gezeirat ha-katuv}. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's logic is conceptually appealing, but the {\i Chachamim} hold that the Torah\u8217's mechanism of annulment operates only on something already in existence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note the irony: Rabbi Eliezer concedes that {\i kiyum} of a non-existent vow is impossible \u8212- but this is precisely the assumption that undermines his own {\i kal va-chomer}, since if {\i kiyum} cannot apply, and {\i kiyum} and {\i hafara} are treated as parallel categories by the {\i Chachamim}, then the same limitation applies to both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any case, this debate concerns only whether the act of annulment can precede the vow\u8217's existence. Nobody in the {\i mishna} raises the objection that the husband failed to hear the vow \u8212- neither Rabbi Eliezer nor the {\i Chachamim} flag hearing as a problem. This silence is itself informative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Effect Upon Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-ha lo shama} \u8212- but he didn\u8217't hear these vows. This too doesn\u8217't seem to trouble either opinion in the {\i mishna}. The {\i gemara} once again resolves this: {\i hacha nami de-amar le-chi-ishmana} \u8212- here too, we read it as if he said the annulment would take effect when he hears about the vows. So again: the act is performed now, but the effect is held in abeyance until hearing occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if that is the case, why does he need to make the declaration before he leaves? {\i Lechi yishma yeifer lah} \u8212- when he gets back and hears about any vow she made, let him annul it then. The answer: {\i kasvar dilma matreeda ha-hee sha\u8217'ata} \u8212- he is worried that upon returning from his trip, in the bustle of arriving home, he might be distracted and forget to annul in time. The twenty-four-hour window could expire. So he sets things in motion beforehand to ensure the annulment will take effect automatically once he hears, without relying on his future composure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna} clearly reads as genuine pre-emptive annulment. But the {\i gemara}, in order to preserve the requirement of hearing, consistently reads all such cases as: the act is permitted before hearing, the effect requires hearing. This is the framework the {\i gemara} applies throughout this {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach (Agent) Authori\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Appointed Manager Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ta shma} \u8212- the {\i gemara} brings a further case. A man about to leave on a trip says to his {\i apotropus} \u8212- his household manager or appointed agent \u8212- {\i kol nedarim she-tidor ishti mi-kan ad she-avo mi-makom ploni}, hafer lah: any vows my wife makes from now until I return, go ahead and annul them. The manager does so \u8212- {\i yachol yihiyu mufarim?} Could such an annulment be valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Delegable Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Talmud lomar isha yekimenu ve-ishah yefirenu} \u8212- the verse specifies the husband. The power of {\i hafara}, says Rabbi Yoshiah, cannot be transferred to an agent. It belongs exclusively to the husband himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yonatan objects: {\i matzinu be-chol ha-Torah kulo shlucho shel adam k\u8217'moto} \u8212- throughout the entire Torah, an agent stands in the place of the one who appointed him. Why should {\i hafara} be any different? Why can the husband not empower a {\i shaliach} to annul on his behalf?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing vs.\u160 ?Legal Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes a deeper puzzle: even if the principle of {\i shaliach} could in theory apply, there is a conceptual difficulty. The {\i shaliach} would be the one who hears the vow \u8212- but the husband would not have heard it. If hearing is what empowers the husband to annul, then how could his {\i shaliach}\u8217's hearing translate into the husband\u8217's ability to act?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer \u8212- and this is spelled out more fully in the Ran and in Tosafot \u8212- is that hearing is not a legal act that can be delegated. One can transfer a legal power to a {\i shaliach}; one cannot make someone else do one\u8217's hearing. Hearing is circumstances, not agency. The circumstances of hearing create the empowerment; but those circumstances must attach to the person himself. As Tosafot states concisely: {\i inyan shmiah eino na\u8217'aseh shaliach} \u8212- the matter of hearing cannot be made into an act of agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Hears & Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given all of this, the {\i gemara} concludes: even Rabbi Yoshiah, who holds that the power of {\i hafara} cannot be transferred, acknowledges that the reason is specifically {\i gezeirat ha-katuv} \u8212- the verse {\i isha yekimenu ve-ishah yefirenu} specifies the husband. Were it not for that verse, everyone would agree the power could in principle be transferred. {\i Aval d\u8217'chulei alma shlucho shel adam k\u8217'moto} \u8212- the baseline principle of {\i shaliach} would apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then works out how this case would function if the {\i shaliach} mechanism were valid: {\i de-amar lah le-chi shamana meifer lah} \u8212- the husband says to the {\i shaliach}, when you hear, annul it, and it will take effect when I hear. {\i Savar dilma matridna} \u8212- the husband anticipates being distracted upon his return, so he sets the {\i shaliach} in motion ahead of time. The {\i shaliach} performs the act upon hearing; the effect activates once the husband himself hears. In this way the {\i gemara} accommodates the case within its consistent framework: the act of annulment can precede hearing, but the effect requires the husband personally to have heard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This entire discussion reinforces once more that the {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna} and {\i braita} is that hearing is not required to perform {\i hafara}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's repeated accommodation \u8212- reading every case as a deferred effect contingent on future hearing \u8212- is the only mechanism by which it preserves any residual role for {\i b\u8217'yom shama\u8217'o} at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deaf Person Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'ei Rami Bar Chama} \u8212- Rami Bar Chama now poses a second question: {\i cheresh mahu sheyafer le\u8217'ishto?} Can a deaf person annul the vows of his wife? The question presupposes the conclusion of the previous discussion: if we have established \u8212- or are at least working with the assumption \u8212- that one need not actually hear a vow in order to annul it, then a deaf person\u8217's disability does not categorically prevent him from performing {\i hafara}. So the question must be sharper than that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Category of Hearer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Im timtzei lomar ba\u8217'al meifer belo shemia} \u8212- if we say a hearing husband can annul without actually having heard a particular vow, perhaps the reason is {\i mishum debar shemia hu} \u8212- because he at least belongs to the category of one capable of hearing. {\i Aval cheresh de-lav bar shemia hu} \u8212- a deaf person, however, is not in that category at all. Perhaps that categorical exclusion disqualifies him from {\i hafara} altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Zeira\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question connects to a broader principle articulated by Rabbi Zeira: {\i kol ha-ra\u8217'ui le-vila ein bila me\u8217'akevet bo, ve-chol she-eino ra\u8217'ui le-vila bila me\u8217'akevet bo} \u8212- whatever is fit to be mixed, the mixing is not actually indispensable; but if it is not fit to be mixed, then the mixing is determinative. The reference is to a {\i mincha} offering: the Torah requires the flour and oil to be mixed ({\i bila}), yet if one failed to mix them in a case where mixing was possible, the offering remains valid. But if the offering is of such a size that mixing is physically impossible \u8212- once the quantity exceeds what can be practically mixed \u8212- then its lack of fitness for {\i bila} disqualifies it entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot flags the broader question this principle raises: how does one know when the {\i halacha} requires merely the potential for an act, and when it requires the act itself? The answer is not always clear. But the {\i gemara} here applies Rabbi Zeira\u8217's principle to suggest: perhaps {\i b\u8217'yom shama\u8217'o} is not eliminated entirely by our conclusion that hearing need not actually occur \u8212- perhaps it is reduced to the requirement of being in the {\i category} of a hearer. A hearing person who happens not to have heard a specific vow retains that potential; a deaf person has no such potential at all. {\i O dilma ve-shama isha lav me\u8217'akev} \u8212- or perhaps {\i ve-shama isha} plays no role whatsoever, not even as a categorical requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava ta shma} \u8212- Rava resolves the question by citing a {\i braita}. The {\i braita} expounds {\i ve-shama isha} \u8212- her husband shall hear \u8212- and adds: {\i prat le\u8217'eshet cheresh}, to exclude the wife of a deaf person. The exclusion is explicit. A deaf man cannot annul his wife\u8217's vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From here, many {\i rishonim} draw the further implication: since the {\i braita} finds it necessary to explicitly exclude the case of the {\i cheresh}, it implies that merely not having heard a particular vow is insufficient to constitute a problem. The only disqualifying circumstance is being fully outside the category of hearing. This reinforces the position \u8212- consistent with everything seen in this {\i sugya} \u8212- that a hearing person need not actually have heard the vow in order to perform {\i hafara}. As this is a striking conclusion given the Torah\u8217's explicit language of {\i b\u8217'yom shama\u8217'o}, it bears emphasis: according to most {\i rishonim} and {\i poskim}, the {\i pshat} of the {\i sugya} is that actual hearing of the specific vow is not required for annulment to be valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Wives Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Iba\u8217'ya lehu} \u8212- a further question is raised: {\i ba\u8217'al mahu sheyafer li-sh\u8217'tei nashim be-vat achat?} Can a husband annul the vows of two of his wives simultaneously? He has vows from two wives on the same day and declares {\i mufar lachem} \u8212- it is annulled for both of you. Is that valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question parallels the earlier inquiry into {\i ve-shama isha}: just as we asked whether {\i isha} \u8212- singular \u8212- is {\i davka}, implying the husband must address one wife at a time, so too we might ask about {\i yafer otah} \u8212- the annulment is of {\i her} vow. One might note that in the {\i pesukim} in {\i Matos}, the verb of {\i hafara} appears in the context of {\i kol nedareha ve-chol issar asher asra al nafsha} \u8212- her vows in their entirety \u8212- and the {\i hafara} there is on the {\i neder}, not on the person. The word {\i otah} in the sense of disallowing her personally ({\i yani otah}) is distinct from the {\i hafara} of the vow. So the textual basis for requiring individual treatment is not straightforwardly clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Aina ta shma} \u8212- come and hear from the laws of {\i sotah}. {\i Ein mashkin sh\u8217'tei sotos ke\u8217'achat} \u8212- one does not administer the {\i sotah} waters to two suspected wives at the same time. Even if a man suspects both his wives of infidelity, the {\i sotah} process must be conducted separately for each. The reason given by the {\i tanna kama} is {\i mipnei she-liba gas ba-chaverta} \u8212- because one woman\u8217's heart becomes emboldened by her companion. Seeing her fellow undergo the drinking process without immediate consequence, she may not take the matter seriously, failing to appreciate that her companion may be innocent while she is guilty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Individual Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Judah disagrees with this rationale and offers instead a textual basis: {\i ela mishum she-ne\u8217'emar ve-hishka otah levadah} \u8212- because the verse says \u8220"and he shall make her drink {\i alone},\u8221" {\i levadah}, implying individual treatment is mandated by the {\i pasuk} itself. There is some discussion among the {\i rishonim} as to whether the {\i tanna kama} disputes Rabbi Judah\u8217's {\i derashah} entirely, or whether he agrees with the {\i pasuk} but is merely supplying the underlying reason behind it \u8212- in the manner of Rabbi Shimon who {\i doresh ta\u8217'ama di-kra}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion on the question of simultaneous annulment for two wives appears to be that it must be done individually, though the precise resolution remains somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bogeret & Widow Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ready-for-Marriage Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now addresses a distinct set of cases: a {\i bogeret} \u8212- a woman who has reached full maturity \u8212- who was betrothed ({\i mekudeshes}) and is now in the period that would naturally precede {\i nissu\u8217'in}, and similarly a widow who is betrothed for a second marriage. These are women who are fully ready to be married but have not yet entered the {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer}: since the {\i Sages} have ruled that the husband is now obligated in her {\i mezonos} \u8212- her financial support \u8212- he may annul her vows unilaterally, without the father\u8217's partnership, as though she were already {\i nesu\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking ruling. {\i De\u8217'oraisa} she is not yet {\i nesu\u8217'ah}, and the annulment would be {\i le-kula} \u8212- a leniency. How can the husband be granted unilateral authority that normally requires full {\i nissu\u8217'in}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chachamim omrim}: the husband cannot perform full {\i hafara} until she has actually entered his domain \u8212- until there is a real {\i chuppah} and genuine {\i nissu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava}: Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i mishna rishona} \u8212- the earlier teaching cited in Ketubot \u8212- express the same position. As the {\i mishna} in Ketubot teaches: {\i notnin li-vtula yod bet chodesh le-farnes atzma} \u8212- a virgin bride is given twelve months to prepare her trousseau and arrange for the wedding. {\i Higia yod bet chodesh} and the husband has not yet married her \u8212- his financial obligations kick in. {\i Ocheles mi-shelo} \u8212- he bears her food expenses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Truma Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And further: {\i ocheles bi-truma} \u8212- if the husband is a {\i kohen}, she may even eat {\i truma}, despite the fact that ordinarily a woman must be genuinely {\i nesu\u8217'ah} to a {\i kohen} to be permitted {\i truma}. So there is a ritual-legal parallel: just as once twelve months have elapsed she is treated as {\i nesu\u8217'ah} for the purpose of eating {\i truma}, Rabbi Eliezer holds that she is treated as {\i nesu\u8217'ah} for the purpose of {\i hafara} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, the {\i mishna} in Ketubot distinguishes the case of {\i yibum} \u8212- the {\i yavam}, the brother-in-law awaiting {\i yibum}. Even if six months passed under the deceased husband and six more under the {\i yavam} \u8212- or all twelve months in either configuration \u8212- they do not combine, and she may not eat {\i truma} by virtue of the {\i yavam}\u8217's obligation. The later {\i beit din}, the {\i mishna rishona}\u8217's successor ruling, went further and said: {\i ein ha-isha ocheles bi-truma ad she-tikanes la-chuppah} \u8212- she does not eat {\i truma} until she actually enters the {\i chuppah}. So even the {\i truma} leniency was subsequently restricted, reflecting the debate about how far we are prepared to treat her as {\i nesu\u8217'ah} for {\i issur} purposes before she formally is.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Truma vs.\u160 ?Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanic vs.\u160 ?Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Abaye} \u8212- Abaye challenges Rava\u8217's equation. Perhaps, says Abaye, the parallel between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i mishna rishona} is not so simple. {\i Ad kan lo ashmian mishna rishona ela meichal bi-truma d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- the {\i mishna rishona} permitted her to eat {\i truma}, but {\i truma} in this context is a {\i rabbinic} matter. {\i Aval nedarim d\u8217'oraisa eima lo} \u8212- but {\i nedarim} is Torah law, and one might say Rabbi Eliezer would never have extended his position to allow a Torah-level {\i hafara} before actual {\i nissu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Truma Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does Abaye mean when he says {\i truma} here is {\i de-rabbanan}? This requires some background. {\i Mideoraita}, why may the wife of a {\i kohen} eat {\i truma} at all? Two potential {\i pesukim} are relevant. One is {\i anshei beiso heim yochlu be-lachmo} \u8212- the people of his household eat from his bread \u8212- pointing toward a {\i nesu\u8217'ah}, a woman established in his home. The other is {\i kinyan kaspo hu yochal be-lachmo} \u8212- that which he has acquired with his money, referring to {\i kiddushin} \u8212- pointing toward an {\i arusa}, a betrothed woman. The Yerushalmi understands the basis as {\i beiso}, requiring full {\i nissu\u8217'in} even {\i de\u8217'oraita}. The Bavli understands it as {\i kinyan kaspo}, so that {\i de\u8217'oraita} even an {\i arusa} may eat {\i truma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nesu\u8217'ah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, why did the {\i Sages} require {\i nissu\u8217'in} before she may eat {\i truma}? The reason is practical: she is still living in her father\u8217's house, among family members who are not {\i kohanim}, and would likely share the {\i truma} with them \u8212- which is forbidden. The {\i rabbinic} restriction is therefore a protective measure. Since her inability to eat {\i truma} before {\i nissu\u8217'in} is only {\i de-rabbanan}, it is not so significant to say: once twelve months have elapsed and it is truly time for the {\i nissu\u8217'in}, we will treat her as {\i nesu\u8217'ah} and allow her to eat. {\i Mah she-ein kein nedarim} \u8212- but for {\i nedarim}, which are {\i de\u8217'oraita}, how could the husband annul vows if she is not formally his {\i nesu\u8217'ah}? That is a much more serious claim, and Abaye holds that the parallel to {\i truma} therefore does not carry.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vows with Spousal Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spousal Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the position of Rav Pinchas in the name of Rava: {\i kol ha-noderet al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah hi noderet} \u8212- any woman who takes a vow does so with the presumption that her husband will agree. This is the conceptual key to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. The woman\u8217's vow is not a unilateral declaration issued in disregard of her husband; it is made against the backdrop of her relationship with him, implicitly conditional on his acceptance. Since that implicit conditionality is built into the vow itself, the husband\u8217's annulment does not need to await formal {\i nissu\u8217'in} in the same way as a standard {\i hafara} would \u8212- because the vow\u8217's very force depends on his concurrence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains why Rabbi Eliezer holds that once twelve months have elapsed and the husband is already obligated in her maintenance, the relationship has already reached a stage where her psychological orientation is effectively that of a {\i nesu\u8217'ah}. She is already thinking about him as her husband, and her vows are already being made {\i al da\u8217'at} him. The formal {\i nissu\u8217'in} has not yet occurred, but the relevant dynamic \u8212- the one the Torah was addressing when it invested the husband with the power of {\i hafara} \u8212- is already present. This is not merely a financial or technical marker; it reflects the actual state of the relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran is careful to note, however, that one should not conclude from this that all formal requirements of {\i hafara} are suspended in such cases. The principle that a woman vows {\i al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah} does not mean that the husband\u8217's silent non-objection constitutes {\i hafara}, or that any expression of reluctance will do. The Ran writes explicitly: even granting that she vows on the presumption of his agreement, the husband must still perform a formal act of {\i hafara} using the language designated by the Torah. This is {\i gezeirat ha-katuv} \u8212- the Torah prescribed a formal mechanism with specific rules: a twenty-four-hour window, particular language, and so on. {\i Al da\u8217'as} explains the {\i reason} the Torah invested the husband with this power; it does not dissolve the formal requirements of how that power must be exercised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think, for example, that if someone vows explicitly {\i al da\u8217'as} a specific individual \u8212- say, \u8220"I am taking this vow subject to So-and-so\u8217's approval\u8221" \u8212- that person could annul the vow without formal {\i hafara} language, simply by withholding consent. The Ran firmly rejects this: {\i vazu hi shegagas hora\u8217'ah} \u8212- this would be a genuine error in {\i psak}. The mechanism of {\i hafara} is formal and its rules are fixed; {\i al da\u8217'as} informs the underlying rationale but does not replace the formal act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Category Expansi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran develops the point further in a direction that is conceptually illuminating. If {\i al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah} is the {\i reason} the Torah gave the husband power over his wife\u8217's vows, then we are essentially engaged in {\i darshinan ta\u8217'ama di-kra} \u8212- deriving the law from the reasoning behind the {\i pasuk}. And if so, when the Torah says {\i ba\u8217'al} \u8212- husband \u8212- we need to ask: what does the Torah mean by husband in this context? The Torah means the person standing in that relationship of presumptive agreement with her vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once we frame it this way, Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's extension becomes intelligible. An {\i arus} who has been in a committed relationship with his betrothed for twelve months, who is already providing her with financial support, and in whose eyes she is already conducting herself as though married \u8212- such a person fits the Torah\u8217's category of {\i ba\u8217'al} for purposes of {\i hafara}, even though the formal {\i chuppah} has not yet occurred. The Torah\u8217's category of \u8220"husband\u8221" is not strictly limited to post-{\i chuppah} status; it encompasses whoever stands in the relevant relationship. At the twelve-month mark, with the {\i arus} already obligated in {\i mezonos}, the relationship has effectively reached that threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not an unlimited expansion. The Ran is explicit: it would not extend to a stranger, a neighbor, or even a close friend, regardless of how much the woman trusted him or made her vows with him in mind. It extends only to someone who already fits within some recognizable form of the {\i ba\u8217'al} category \u8212- an {\i arus} at an advanced stage of betrothal. For such a person, the Torah\u8217's language of {\i ba\u8217'al} already includes him, given the reasoning that underlies the entire institution of {\i hafarat nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 72b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Perek Structure & Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Perek Organization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at 72b with a new {\i mishnah}, still within the broader theme of the father and the groom who together annul a daughter\u8217's vows. Yet as we will see, this {\i mishnah} raises \u8212- almost in passing \u8212- a central and far-reaching question that the {\i Gemara} proceeds to explore at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing at the outset to note something characteristic about the organization of this {\i perek}, as indeed of many {\i masechtos} and {\i perakim} throughout the Talmud. Rather than beginning with the most straightforward {\i halachos} \u8212- explaining what the twenty-four-hour window for {\i hafarah} consists of, what {\i hakama} means, how the process works in its basic form \u8212- the {\i perek} opens instead with the more obscure and in many ways more fascinating cases: the precise nature of the partnership between father and groom, who is the {\i ikar}, how the combination functions. All of the more foundational material gets addressed along the way, as it becomes relevant. This is simply another demonstration that the {\i Gemara} and the {\i Mishnayos} were organized for those already in the conversation, not as a beginner\u8217's introduction to the laws of {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Blanket Annulme\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the top of 72b opens with a striking phrase: {\i derech talmidei chachamim} \u8212- it is the way of Torah scholars. It is notable that the {\i mishnah} directly identifies this practice with the category of {\i talmidei chachamim}; the term is common in the {\i Gemara} but relatively rare as a {\i mishnah}\u8217's own formulation. Perhaps it means that one must actually be a {\i talmid chacham} to know that this practice is both possible and unproblematic \u8212- that he may annul vows he has not yet heard about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} describes the following: before a man\u8217's daughter goes to {\i nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- and {\i yotzet me\u8217'etzlo}, leaving the father\u8217's house, conceptually means {\i nisu\u8217'in}, since during {\i eirusin} she remains in the father\u8217's domain \u8212- the father would say to her: {\i kol nedarim shenadart betoch beiti harei hem mufarim}, any vows you have taken in my house are hereby annulled. He gives her a blanket annulment, similar in spirit to the {\i hatarat nedarim} of {\i erev Rosh Hashanah}. The framing is deliberately open-ended: if she had already told him the specific vows, there would be no need for the general formula, and the clock would already be running. The phrasing makes clear that the father has not heard the specific vows; he is annulling whatever may exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father & Groom Roles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand why both father and groom must participate, it is necessary to recall the underlying structure. During {\i kiddushin}, the daughter remains conceptually in the father\u8217's house; {\i nisu\u8217'in} is when she leaves. The father alone can annul vows made before {\i kiddushin}, and in partnership with the {\i arus} he can annul vows made after {\i kiddushin} but before {\i nisu\u8217'in}. Once {\i nisu\u8217'in} occurs, the father is entirely out of the picture. If vows predate {\i nisu\u8217'in}, the husband cannot annul them unilaterally after {\i nisu\u8217'in} because they are {\i kodmin} \u8212- they precede his period of sole authority. The only window for addressing such vows is before {\i nisu\u8217'in}, which is precisely why the {\i mishnah} describes the father and groom each making their blanket declarations beforehand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i vechen haba\u8217'al} \u8212- so too the groom, before she enters his domain, would say: {\i kol nedarim shenadart ad shelo tikanesi lirshuti harei hem mufarim}, any vows you may have taken before the {\i chuppah} are hereby annulled. Once {\i nisu\u8217'in} occurs, {\i eino yachol lehafer}, he can no longer address those earlier vows, for they are {\i kodmin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The clear implication of the {\i mishnah} is that neither the father nor the groom has actually heard the specific vows they are annulling. The blanket language and the absence of any running clock make this evident. And it is precisely this implication that the {\i Gemara} will take as its opening question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmiea Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Q1: Annul Without Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'ei Rami Bar Chama} \u8212- Rami Bar Chama poses the question: {\i ba\u8217'al mahu sheyapir belo shemiah?} Can the husband \u8212- and the same question applies equally to the father \u8212- annul a vow without having heard it? Is {\i veshamah ishah} \u8212- and her husband heard \u8212- an absolute requirement, or is it {\i lav davka}, not strictly necessary?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is almost comical, in a certain sense, to take a {\i pasuk} from the {\i Torah} and simply declare it {\i lav davka}. The question, however, is more nuanced than simple dismissal of the phrase. What {\i lav davka} likely means in this context is not that {\i beyom shmo\u8217'o} carries no significance whatsoever, but rather that it is not a prerequisite for the act of annulment. Its operative role would be different: from the moment the husband hears, the clock begins running and he has twenty-four hours to act. Failing to annul within twenty-four hours of hearing is treated as {\i hakama} \u8212- acquiescence by silence. On this reading, hearing is not what enables the annulment; rather, it is what starts the countdown. The husband could, in principle, annul even before he has heard, and {\i beyom shmo\u8217'o} would simply mean that having heard, he must now act within the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} does not spell this out explicitly, but it seems the most coherent {\i pshat} if one is going to say {\i lav davka} at all \u8212- it is not a wholesale dismissal of the phrase, but a reframing of its function.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Conversation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} seeks a proof from our {\i mishnah}: {\i ta shema}, come and hear \u8212- {\i derech talmidei chachamim ad shelo yatza bito me\u8217'etzlo amar lah kol nedarim shenadart betoch beiti harei hen mufarim \u8212- vehu lo shama}, and he did not hear. How does this work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers an initial interpretation: {\i nehi d\u8217'chi shama hu d\u8217'mepar} \u8212- really, he only annuls when he hears. The declaration he makes now, before he has heard anything, is not itself an act of annulment. Rather, it is an opening to conversation \u8212- {\i orcha d\u8217'tzurba merabanan}, the way of a Torah scholar is {\i lehadurei}, to go back over it, to initiate a dialogue so that his daughter will articulate any vows she may have taken, and then he can properly annul them having heard. The blanket statement draws her into sharing, whereupon the real act of annulment can occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading is, admittedly, very difficult to square with the plain meaning of the {\i mishnah}. It imposes a great deal onto a text that reads simply as a completed act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Act vs.\u160 ?Effect Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns to the {\i seifa}: {\i vechen haba\u8217'al ad shelo tikanes l\u8217'rishuto amar lah} \u8212- the groom too says it before the {\i chuppah}. Here too, did he not act without hearing? The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i hacha nami d\u8217'amar lah l\u8217'chi shamana} \u8212- here too, he says to her: I will annul it when I hear. Some manuscript readings elaborate this in the same language as before; others give only the compressed formulation, {\i l\u8217'chi shamana}, meaning the annulment takes effect upon hearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading opens up a conceptually interesting middle position: perhaps hearing is required not for the {\i act} of annulment, but for the {\i effect} of annulment. The husband performs the act of {\i hafarah} now, but it takes hold only once he has actually heard. This is structurally somewhat reminiscent of debates around {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}: the puzzle of whether and when legal acts can be initiated regarding things that are not yet fully present. One might compare it to the case of a man who says {\i harei at mekudeshet li} before he has converted \u8212- not only is there an external impediment, but the very act seems to have no legal substrate. Yet the {\i Gemara} here is searching for a middle path: the act can be initiated now, and the effect will crystallize upon hearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran: Father vs.\u160 ?Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran raises a pointed question on this middle position: if this explanation works for the husband \u8212- he does the act now and it takes effect when he hears \u8212- why doesn\u8217't the {\i Gemara} give the same answer for the father? Why is the father\u8217's case handled differently, with the device of opening a conversation to get her to articulate the vow?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's answer turns on the difference in timing. For the father, the problem is that by the time he hears, it may already be after the {\i chuppah} \u8212- at which point his authority over her vows is entirely gone. At the moment of hearing, she is completely outside his domain. For that combination to work \u8212- the act done while she was still in his house, the effect crystallizing after {\i nisu\u8217'in} when he has no authority \u8212- neither moment is one in which he is actually in a position to annul. The two halves cannot meaningfully combine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the husband, the situation is different. He performs the act now as an {\i arus}, at which point he has at least partial authority. He will hear after {\i nisu\u8217'in}, when he is fully in the role of {\i ba\u8217'al} and unambiguously empowered to annul new vows. The act of annulment and the moment of hearing therefore both occur within legitimate windows of some authority, and it is coherent for them to combine. The effect taking hold upon hearing, at a time when he is fully in control, is a workable legal structure.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Vows Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Vows Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings another {\i mishnah} \u8212- located two pages later at 75a \u8212- as a further proof. The case: a man says to his wife, {\i kol hanedarim shetidderi mikan ad she\u8217'avo mimakom ploni} \u8212- any vows you make from now until I return from such-and-such place. He is about to leave on a trip and wishes to address whatever vows his wife may take in his absence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Kiyum Before Existence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he declares {\i harei hen kayamim} \u8212- I am establishing them \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}, he has accomplished nothing. There is a universal consensus on this: one cannot perform {\i kiyum}, the act of establishing or affirming a vow, on something that does not yet exist. {\i Kiyum} is about anchoring and strengthening an existing vow; there is nothing to anchor if the vow has not yet been taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Hafarah Befo\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if he says {\i harei hen mufarim} \u8212- I am annulling them \u8212- {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer mufar}: Rabbi Eliezer holds this works. His reasoning: {\i im hefer nedarim sheba\u8217'u l\u8217'chlal issur, ra\u8217'uy yafer nedarim shelo ba\u8217'u l\u8217'chlal issur} \u8212- if one can annul a vow once it has already taken effect as a prohibition, how much more so should one be able to prevent it from ever taking effect in the first place. {\i Hafarah} is fundamentally about eliminating a prohibition; a preventive measure, he argues, is at least as valid as a curative one. The vow that does not yet exist is in an even more accessible state for annulment \u8212- there is less there to dissolve. This also explains why Rabbi Eliezer distinguishes {\i hafarah} from {\i kiyum}: {\i kiyum} requires something present to strengthen, whereas {\i hafarah} is about removal or prevention, and prevention is easier than cure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim: Parallel Requ\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} respond from the verse: {\i ishah yekimenu ve\u8217'ishah yeferenu} \u8212- the husband shall establish it and the husband shall annul it. {\i Et sheba l\u8217'chlal hakeim ba l\u8217'chlal hafer, lo ba l\u8217'chlal hakeim lo ba l\u8217'chlal hafer} \u8212- only something that falls within the category of being establishable can be annulled; something that cannot be established cannot be annulled. This may be a {\i gezeirat hakatuv}, a formal Scriptural decree \u8212- the mechanism of annulment simply operates on existing vows \u8212- or it may reflect the logical symmetry that {\i ishah yekimenu ve\u8217'ishah yeferenu} presents {\i hakama} and {\i hafarah} as parallel operations on the same object. Either way, the {\i Chachamim} insist that the vow must exist before either operation can apply. Rabbi Eliezer concedes that {\i kiyum} requires existence, and the {\i Chachamim} argue that {\i hafarah} must be constrained in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Act & Effect Upon Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i Gemara} returns to the {\i shmiea} question. In this {\i mishnah}, nobody \u8212- neither Rabbi Eliezer nor the {\i Chachamim} \u8212- raises any objection about the fact that the husband has not heard the vows. The entire debate is whether {\i hafarah} can apply to vows not yet in existence; the absence of hearing goes unremarked. The {\i Gemara} notes: {\i v\u8217'ha lo shama} \u8212- he has not heard \u8212- and yet this concerns no one?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i hacha nami d\u8217'amar lich\u8217'she\u8217'eshamana} \u8212- here too, he says, I am annulling it, and it will take effect when I hear. Once again: the act is performed now; the effect waits upon hearing. The {\i Chachamim}\u8217's objection to Rabbi Eliezer is that the vow does not yet exist, not that hearing is missing. Both sides operate within the assumption that hearing is what the effect waits upon, but neither treats its absence as independently disqualifying the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why State It Now\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question: if the annulment only takes effect when he hears, why must he make the declaration before departing? Why not simply annul each vow as he hears about it upon his return?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i kasavar dilma matridna hi sha\u8217'ata} \u8212- he worries that he will be distracted at that later moment. Coming home from a trip, there are many demands on his attention. His wife might tell him about a vow she took, and by the time he has settled and remembered to formally address it, twenty-four hours will have passed and his silence will have been treated as {\i hakama}. He therefore initiates the process now, before he is distracted, setting things in motion so that the effect can crystallize upon hearing without risk of the window closing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Epitropos & Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Epitropos Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i mishnah} \u8212- also upcoming in the {\i masechta} \u8212- introduces the case of the {\i epitropos}: a man is about to leave on a trip and says to the steward running his household, {\i kol nedarim shenoderet ishti mikan ve\u8217'ad she\u8217'avo mimakom ploni} \u8212- any vows my wife takes from now until I return, {\i hafer}, annul them. The steward hears her take a vow and annuls it. Has the annulment taken effect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yoshiya vs.\u160 ?R. Yonatan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yoshiya says no: {\i ishah yekimenu ve\u8217'ishah yeferenu} \u8212- only the husband can annul; this is not a power that can be transferred. Rabbi Yonatan responds: {\i matzinu bechol hatorah kullaha shelucho shel adam kemoso} \u8212- throughout the entire Torah, an agent stands in the place of the one who appointed him. Why should this power be different?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transferability of Powers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that the debate between Rabbi Yoshiya and Rabbi Yonatan concerns whether this particular power can be delegated. Not all legal capacities are transferable. There is a category of acts that are not merely about being invested with a power but about personally and physically performing something. {\i Chalitza} is the classic example \u8212- one cannot appoint a {\i shaliach} to perform {\i chalitza} on one\u8217's behalf, because the act is constituted by the personal participation of the principals. Similarly, one cannot appoint a {\i shaliach} to perform a {\i mitzvah} in the purely personal sense. The conceptual framework, developed in the Brisker tradition, distinguishes between a power invested in the person that can be transferred, and a personal act that cannot. The debate here is precisely which category {\i hafarah} falls into.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adds a clarifying point: even Rabbi Yoshiya, who says the {\i epitropos} cannot annul, concedes that this is only {\i mishum gezeirat hakatuv} \u8212- because of the Scriptural decree of {\i ishah yekimenu ve\u8217'ishah yeferenu}. In principle, the act of annulment is of a type that {\i could} be transferred; it is the verse that restricts it to the husband. {\i Avald\u8217'chulei alma shelucho shel adam kemoso} \u8212- everyone agrees that absent that verse, transfer would be conceptually valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Hearing Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concession generates a striking problem. If the {\i shaliach} could in principle annul on the husband\u8217's behalf, how would that work given that the {\i shaliach} heard the vow but the husband did not? If hearing is what empowers the annulment, and the husband has not heard, then the husband is not in a position to annul \u8212- and an agent cannot possess authority that the principal himself lacks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran: Hearing as Circumst\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran resolves this by drawing a precise conceptual distinction. Hearing, he explains, is not itself a legal power \u8212- it is the {\i circumstances} under which the power to annul arises. One cannot transfer circumstances to a {\i shaliach}. What one can transfer is a legal power, once it has been activated by the relevant circumstances. The hearing itself \u8212- a passive, experiential state \u8212- is not the kind of act that can be delegated. The {\i shaliach} hearing is not equivalent to the husband hearing in any legally operative sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once this distinction is drawn, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's assumption becomes intelligible: if we could transfer the {\i power} of annulment (absent Rabbi Yoshiya\u8217's verse), the {\i shaliach}\u8217's hearing would serve as the circumstances activating that power, and the husband\u8217's prior delegation would constitute the transfer of the now-activated power. But there is no way to delegate the {\i hearing itself} so as to produce the same result as if the husband personally heard. Hearing is circumstance, not power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: No Shaliach for\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's answer, found in manuscript traditions added to this {\i perek}, states the point more directly: {\i de\u8217'inyan shemi\u8217'ah eino na\u8217'aseh shaliach} \u8212- in the matter of hearing, one cannot make a {\i shaliach}. The reasoning implicit in this formulation is the same: hearing is not a legal act, and therefore not the kind of thing for which agency can be constituted. You can transfer a legal power; you cannot make another person hear for you in any legally operative sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution in this case \u8212- {\i hacha nami de\u8217'amar lah lechish\u8217'shameina mefer lach} \u8212- is structurally the same as the previous cases. The husband tells the {\i shaliach}: annul when you hear, and it will take effect when I hear. The {\i shaliach} performs the act; the effect waits upon the husband\u8217's own hearing. Why use a {\i shaliach} at all if he can do it himself upon hearing? {\i Savar dilma mitradna} \u8212- he worries about distraction. The {\i shaliach} heard and acted; as soon as the husband hears, the effect crystallizes. The pattern is now entirely consistent across all the cases the {\i Gemara} has examined.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cheresh Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Can Cheresh Annul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami Bar Chama now poses his second question: {\i cheresh mahu she-yafer l\u8217'ishto?} Can a deaf man \u8212- a {\i cheresh} in the Talmudic sense of one who speaks but cannot hear \u8212- annul vows for his wife? This question is built on the assumption that the husband does not need to actually hear in order to annul. If hearing were literally required, the question would be moot. The question therefore presupposes at least some degree of {\i lav davka} in the {\i shmiea} requirement, and asks whether even the residual requirement \u8212- membership in the {\i category} of those capable of hearing \u8212- matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Shmiya Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The formulation: {\i im timtzei lomar ba\u8217'al mefer belo shemia} \u8212- if you say the husband can annul without hearing \u8212- {\i mishum de-bar nishma hu}, it is because he belongs to the category of hearing. {\i Aval cheresh de-lav bar nishma hu} \u8212- but a deaf person who is not in that category \u8212- can he annul at all? This would apply the principle of Rabbi Zeira.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Zeira\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira\u8217's principle: {\i kol ha-rauy l\u8217'villah ein billah me-akevet bo, vechol she-ein rauy l\u8217'villah billah me-akevet bo} \u8212- anything fit to be mixed, the mixing does not actually prevent validity even if not performed; but if it is not fit to be mixed, then mixing \u8212- had it been possible \u8212- would have been required, and its impossibility renders it invalid. The context is the {\i mincha} offering: the Torah requires the flour and oil to be mixed, but if the {\i mincha} is of such a large quantity that mixing is physically impossible, it is invalid \u8212- whereas if mixing is possible but simply not done, it remains valid. Potential, not actual performance, is what matters; but the potential must be real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied here: a normal husband is {\i bar shemia} \u8212- capable of hearing \u8212- and therefore the fact that he did not actually hear does not prevent annulment. But a {\i cheresh} lacks even the potential. The question is whether this disqualifies him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot and others note a broader theoretical question lurking here: how does one determine, across different {\i mitzvos} and legal acts, when the principle of {\i rauy} \u8212- fitness or potential \u8212- is sufficient in place of actual performance? The {\i Gemara} does not resolve this general question but applies the specific query to the {\i cheresh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beraita: Prat L\u8217'Eishet C\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava brings a {\i beraita} in response: {\i veshamah ishah \u8212- prat l\u8217'eishet cheresh} \u8212- \u8220"and her husband heard\u8221" \u8212- this comes to exclude the wife of a deaf man. The exclusion is explicit and unambiguous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Category Membership Matt\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this {\i beraita}, many {\i Rishonim} draw an important inference. The {\i beraita} specifically singles out the {\i cheresh} as excluded. If the mere failure to hear were itself a disqualification, there would be no need to identify the {\i cheresh} as a special case \u8212- anyone who did not hear would already be excluded. The fact that the {\i beraita} reserves its exclusion for the {\i cheresh} specifically implies that in ordinary cases, the husband\u8217's not having heard is not itself an obstacle. What disqualifies the {\i cheresh} is not that he failed to hear this particular vow, but that he is constitutively outside the category of hearing \u8212- he is {\i lav bar shemia}. A hearing husband who simply did not hear a particular vow remains {\i bar shemia}, and can therefore annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking conclusion. The {\i Torah} says {\i veshamah ishah}, and the {\i halacha} \u8212- as understood by most {\i Rishonim} \u8212- is that the husband need not actually hear. The {\i pshat} understanding of {\i beyom shmo\u8217'o} is perhaps best read as defining the window of {\i hakama}: from the moment of hearing, the husband has twenty-four hours before his silence becomes acquiescence. But the act of annulment itself is not contingent on prior hearing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Wives Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Wives Simultaneously\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami Bar Chama poses a third question: {\i ba\u8217'al mahu she-yafer l\u8217'shtei nashav b\u8217'vat achat?} Can a husband with two wives annul vows for both of them simultaneously? He has two wives who have both made vows, and he says {\i mufar lachem} \u8212- it is annulled for you \u8212- addressing both at once. Is that effective?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Singular \u8216'Otah\u8217' in Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question turns on the language of the {\i pesukim}. The relevant phrase is {\i yafer otah} \u8212- he annuls {\i her}, singular. The text consistently uses the singular form: {\i ve\u8217'im henia osah}, and her husband disallowed {\i her}; {\i ve\u8217'im bayom shmo\u8217'o isha yania osah}; {\i ve\u8217'hefer es nidrah}. The {\i hafarah} is grammatically directed at one woman at a time. Is this {\i davka} \u8212- meaning annulment must be performed individually for each wife \u8212- or {\i lav davka}, meaning the plural could be encompassed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that in these {\i pesukim} the verb of {\i hafarah} applies to the {\i neder}, while the verb {\i yania} \u8212- disallowing \u8212- applies to {\i her}, the woman. The singular forms run through both verbs, suggesting the entire process is conceived as an individual act directed at one wife and her vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sota Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina brings a proof from {\i sota}: {\i ein mashkin shtei sotos ke\u8217'achas} \u8212- one may not administer the {\i sota} waters to two {\i sotos} simultaneously. The ritual involving a wife suspected of adultery must be performed individually, not for two women at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reason for Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} in {\i sota} records a dispute about the reason. The {\i Tanna Kamma} says: {\i mipnei she-libbah gas ba-chavertah} \u8212- because one woman\u8217's heart becomes emboldened by her companion. Seeing the other woman undergo the ritual without visible consequence, she may lose her fear and not confess even if guilty. Rabbi Yehuda says the reason is simply from the verse: {\i ve\u8217'hishkah osah levaddah} \u8212- and he shall make {\i her} drink, {\i levaddah} \u8212- alone, individually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reason for Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rishonim} debate whether the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Yehuda are in substantive disagreement or whether the {\i Tanna Kamma} is simply explaining the rationale behind the verse, in the manner of Rabbi Shimon who is {\i doresh ta\u8217'ama dikra}. If the {\i Tanna Kamma} also relies on the verse fundamentally, then all opinions would require the procedure to be individual. This debate affects the parallel case of {\i hafarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Individual Annulment Req\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's apparent conclusion is that annulment must be performed for each wife individually. The consistent use of the singular throughout the {\i pesukim}, reinforced by the {\i sota} parallel, points in that direction. Precisely how the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's position in the {\i sota} case affects the {\i hafarah} parallel remains somewhat open, but the overall thrust of the {\i Gemara} is toward requiring individual annulment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bogeret & Almanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Bogeret & Alman\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf concludes with a {\i mishnah} concerning several cases in which a woman is technically still in the stage of {\i eirusin} but the time for {\i nisu\u8217'in} has effectively arrived. A {\i bogeret} \u8212- a fully adult woman \u8212- who has waited twelve months after {\i kiddushin}; or an {\i almanah} \u8212- a widow \u8212- for whom {\i chazal} set only a thirty-day waiting period between {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisu\u8217'in} when that period has elapsed. These are scenarios in which {\i nisu\u8217'in} has not yet occurred but is expected imminently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Payment = An\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer rules: {\i ho\u8217'il uba\u8217'alah chayav bimezonoseha} \u8212- since the husband is now obligated to provide for her food, this is sufficient to confer upon him the ability to annul her vows even without actual {\i nisu\u8217'in}. His financial obligation establishes his effective relationship to her as that of a husband, and therefore he can annul unilaterally \u8212- without the father\u8217's participation \u8212- even though {\i de\u8217'oraisa} she is technically still in the {\i eirusin} stage. This is puzzling: if she is not {\i halachically} a full wife, how can he have the annulment authority of a husband? The question presses especially given that this is a {\i leniency} \u8212- we are granting him a power he would not normally have.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim: Actual Nisu\u8217'in\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} disagree: {\i ein haba\u8217'al mefer ad shetikanes lirshuto} \u8212- the husband cannot annul until she genuinely enters his domain through actual {\i nisu\u8217'in}. The formal legal status must be achieved; the financial obligation does not substitute for the real thing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava observes that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position here aligns with the {\i Mishnah Rishonah} \u8212- an earlier ruling \u8212- on a parallel question. {\i Rabbi Eliezer u-Mishnah Rishonah amru davar echad} \u8212- they said the same thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah After 12 Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah Rishonah} in {\i Kesubot} ruled: {\i nosnin l\u8217'vsulah yod bet chodesh l\u8217'farnes atzmah} \u8212- a virgin is given twelve months to prepare her trousseau. If twelve months pass and the husband has not yet brought her to {\i nisu\u8217'in}, his financial responsibilities kick in \u8212- {\i ochelet mishelo}, she eats at his expense \u8212- and more strikingly: {\i ve\u8217'ochelet b\u8217'terumah} \u8212- if he is a {\i kohen}, she may even eat {\i terumah}, despite not yet being married. Normally a woman must be genuinely {\i nesuah} to a {\i kohen} to eat {\i terumah}. The {\i Mishnah Rishonah} extended this right to her based on the elapsed time, treating her effective status as already spousal even before {\i nisu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Disallowance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A later {\i beit din} subsequently retracted this position: {\i eina isha ochelet b\u8217'terumah ad shetikanes l\u8217'chuppah} \u8212- a woman may not eat {\i terumah} until she has actually entered the {\i chuppah}. The later ruling tightened the requirement, refusing to extend spousal status for {\i issur} purposes before {\i nisu\u8217'in} had actually occurred. Thus the {\i Mishnah Rishonah} was willing to treat the twelve-month threshold as conferring near-marital status for {\i terumah} consumption; the later ruling was not. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position in our {\i mishnah} \u8212- that the husband\u8217's food obligation translates into annulment authority \u8212- maps onto the {\i Mishnah Rishonah}\u8217's analogous expansion of spousal status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah vs.\u160 ?Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye objects to Rava\u8217's alignment: {\i dilma lo hi} \u8212- perhaps the two positions are not actually the same. The {\i Mishnah Rishonah} may have permitted {\i terumah} consumption only because {\i terumah bizman hazeh} is a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} concern; the rabbis had the flexibility to relax it when circumstances warranted. But {\i nedarim} are {\i d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- the Torah itself controls who may annul. Even if the {\i Mishnah Rishonah} was willing to stretch the concept of spousal status for a rabbinic prohibition like {\i terumah}, it would never have granted the husband power to annul vows, which is a Torah-level matter. {\i Aval nedarim d\u8217'oraita eima lo} \u8212- for vows, we would not have extended that power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic vs.\u160 ?Torah Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does it mean to say {\i terumah} is {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} in this context? The Talmud elsewhere discusses which {\i pasuk} establishes the wife\u8217's right to eat {\i terumah}: is it {\i anshei beito hem yochlu b\u8217'lachmo} \u8212- people of his household \u8212- which would require {\i nisu\u8217'in}; or is it {\i kinyan kaspo hu yochal b\u8217'lachmo} \u8212- that which he acquired with his money \u8212- which would apply from {\i kiddushin}? The {\i Yerushalmi} reads {\i beito} and holds that even {\i d\u8217'oraita} she requires {\i nisu\u8217'in}. The {\i Bavli} reads {\i kinyan kaspo} and holds that {\i d\u8217'oraita} even an {\i arusa} may eat {\i terumah}. On the Bavli\u8217's reading, the restriction of {\i terumah} to {\i nesuot} is entirely rabbinic, making the {\i Mishnah Rishonah}\u8217's leniency a relaxation of a rabbinic stringency \u8212- which is a very different matter from relaxing a Torah-level restriction on who may perform {\i hafarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Terumah Restricted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did the rabbis restrict {\i terumah} to {\i nesuot} in the first place if {\i d\u8217'oraita} an {\i arusa} is permitted? The most straightforward reason is practical: while still in her father\u8217's house during {\i eirusin}, she is likely to share the {\i terumah} with members of her family who are not {\i kohanim}, which would constitute a Torah violation. There is also a secondary concern \u8212- the possibility that the {\i kiddushin} will be undone by a {\i mekach ta\u8217'os}, retroactively rendering her never married, in which case her eating {\i terumah} would have been improper. Once the twelve months have passed and she is effectively standing at the threshold of {\i nisu\u8217'in}, the rabbis were willing \u8212- in the {\i Mishnah Rishonah} \u8212- to permit her to eat, since the sharing concern is less acute and the marriage is imminent. But this entire flexibility, Abaye argues, operates within the rabbinic sphere, and tells us nothing about the Torah-level rules of {\i hafarah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Al Da\u8217'as Ba\u8217'al\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Husband Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces the teaching of Rav Pinchas in the name of Rava: {\i kol hanoderet al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah hi noderet} \u8212- any woman who takes a vow, she vows with her husband\u8217's assent in mind. When a woman who is already in a marital relationship \u8212- or a relationship effectively equivalent to marriage \u8212- takes a vow, it is presumed that she intends the vow to be conditional upon her husband\u8217's acceptance. This is not an explicit {\i tnai} that she articulates, but a psychological reality about how she understands her own act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psychological Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning applies specifically to a woman who has been betrothed for twelve months, the stage at which Rabbi Eliezer permits the husband to annul. At that point, her sense of herself as bound to this man \u8212- as living within the framework of their upcoming marriage \u8212- is sufficiently developed that when she takes a vow, her implicit orientation is toward a life with him. She vows, as it were, on the understanding that he would agree. This psychological fact, Rav Pinchas argues, is what grounds the husband\u8217's ability to annul at that stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vows vs.\u160 ?Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning, however, applies to {\i nedarim} specifically. When a woman takes a vow, her subjective intent shapes the vow\u8217's structure. The concept of {\i al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah} captures something internal to the vow itself \u8212- it was taken with an implicit understanding. {\i Terumah} consumption, by contrast, is entirely an external legal status: she either has the right to eat {\i terumah} or she does not, and that depends on her formal legal standing, not on any psychological orientation she holds. The {\i Mishnah Rishonah} granted her {\i terumah} rights based on an analogous assessment of her practical status; the later {\i beit din} withdrew it. But for {\i nedarim}, the psychological dimension is different \u8212- the vow\u8217's own structure already incorporates the husband\u8217's hypothetical consent, and therefore {\i hafarah} is possible even before technical {\i nisu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ran: Redefining Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Al Da\u8217'as Non-Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran raises a sharp question against the {\i al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah} framework. If the mechanism is that she vowed with somebody else\u8217's assent in mind, can that person \u8212- whoever it is \u8212- now annul the vow? For instance, if someone says {\i al da\u8217'as} of a third party, not a husband at all, does that person acquire annulment power? And conversely, if {\i al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah hi noderet} is the operative principle, does the husband lose the twenty-four-hour window \u8212- can he annul at any time, since the vow was always implicitly conditional on his assent? The formal mechanics of {\i hafarah} \u8212- the specific language required, the twenty-four-hour limit \u8212- seem to sit in tension with a model that reduces {\i hafarah} to an implicit {\i tnai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Formal Hafarah Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran resolves this by insisting on the formal character of {\i hafarah}. {\i Hafarah} is not a {\i tnai} \u8212- it is not simply that the vow dissolves because a condition was not met. {\i Hafarah} is a formal Torah mechanism: it requires specific language, it operates within a twenty-four-hour window, it is a {\i gezeirat hakatuv} that the Torah invested in the husband. {\i U-gzeirat hakatuv hu, u-mishum hachi ein kol leshonot shavim bo} \u8212- proper language is required; not every formulation will do. Any view that reduces {\i hafarah} to the operation of an implicit {\i tnai} cannot account for why the husband must use the formal {\i lashon shel hafarah} or why a twenty-four-hour window applies at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah\u8217's Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So what role does {\i al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'alah} play? The Ran articulates it as follows: {\i hafarah} is indeed a formal Torah mechanism, complete with its own rules. But the Torah had a reason for investing this mechanism in the husband specifically. That reason is the presumption that the wife, when she takes a vow, does so with her husband in mind \u8212- {\i mishum d\u8217'al da\u8217'as ba\u8217'al hi noderet}. The Torah\u8217's decision to empower the husband is grounded in this psychological reality about the wife\u8217's mental state when she vows. This is a reading of the {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'kra} \u8212- the reason behind the verse \u8212- and it is a strikingly woman-centric framing. The power is not given to the husband because he is the dominant party in whose house she lives and whose authority overrides hers. Rather, the Torah empowers him because the vow itself was implicitly made with him in mind, and it is therefore appropriate that he should be the one who can address it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Expanding Husband Catego\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this understanding of the Torah\u8217's rationale, the Ran draws a conclusion about the scope of the category \u8220"husband\u8221" as the Torah uses it. If the reason the Torah empowers the {\i ba\u8217'al} is that the wife takes vows with him in mind, then the category of {\i ba\u8217'al} for purposes of {\i hafarah} should include any person who genuinely occupies that role in her psychological universe. After twelve months of {\i kiddushin}, when the relationship has matured to the point that she is practically living for the moment of {\i nisu\u8217'in}, when he is providing for her food, when she thinks of herself as functionally his wife \u8212- at that stage, an {\i arus} fits within the Torah\u8217's conception of {\i ba\u8217'al} for purposes of {\i hafarah}. The Torah says {\i ishah}, husband, and Rabbi Eliezer argues that the Torah would recognize the {\i arus} in this stage as falling within that category, precisely because the reason for the category is fully operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Role-Based Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran is careful to confine this reasoning. It is not an invitation to say that any person whom a woman holds in high regard, or with whose approval she takes vows in mind, can thereby annul those vows. The power of {\i hafarah} flows through the formal Torah mechanism. What the {\i al da\u8217'as} reasoning does is define who fits within the Torah\u8217's category of {\i ba\u8217'al} for purposes of that mechanism. That category can be extended to an {\i arus} who stands in the husband\u8217's role \u8212- who is, in every relevant sense, the person she is married to, albeit not yet under {\i chuppah}. It cannot be extended to an arbitrary third party who is not in that role at all. The formal structure of {\i hafarah} remains intact; what expands, carefully and on principled grounds, is the definition of who qualifies as the {\i ba\u8217'al} whom the Torah empowered to perform it. This, the Ran concludes, is the most coherent account of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position \u8212- and it explains how {\i hafarah} before {\i nisu\u8217'in} is not a departure from the Torah\u8217's framework but a faithful application of its underlying logic.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 74\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shomeret Yavam Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fifth {\i mishnah} of this {\i perek} continues the discussion of unusual cases \u8212- those that do not arise in the standard situation contemplated by the {\i Torah} \u8212- which the {\i perek} has chosen to focus upon: the case of the father and the {\i arus} acting in partnership in the annulment of vows, and now the case of the {\i shomeret yavam}, the woman who awaits {\i yibum}, levirate marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i yavam}\u8217's relationship with his {\i yevamah} is, on one hand, a carryover of the marriage of the deceased brother, which may itself have been at the level of {\i nesu\u8217'in}. The {\i yibum} relationship requires less deliberate intent to actualize into a full {\i nesu\u8217'in} \u8212- even an act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} performed without specific {\i kavanah} to create a marriage accomplishes it, because of the {\i zika}, the bond and remnant of the {\i kiddushin} that transfers from the dead husband to his brother. On the other hand, the relationship in its present, unrealized state is not equivalent to a full marriage: if the {\i yevamah} were to be with another man, it would not constitute {\i eshet ish}; and the release from the obligation can be achieved through {\i chalitzah} rather than a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam & Hafara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this ambiguous status, the question arises whether the {\i yavam} is needed and able to participate in {\i hafarat nedarim} together with the father. If the original marriage to the deceased husband had been at the level of {\i erusin}, the father remains in the picture entirely. The question then becomes whether the {\i yavam}\u8217's relationship resembles {\i erusin} \u8212- in which case his participation in {\i hafara} is necessary but must be jointly with the father \u8212- or whether it rises to the level of {\i nesu\u8217'in}, in which case he would act alone. The {\i mishnah} uses the word {\i yafer} (he may annul), and it is not immediately clear which of these two possibilities that word entails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents the following case: a {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibum} \u8212- whether she has one {\i yavam} or two. With two {\i yevamin}, the question is immediately more complex, since one cannot designate any particular one as being in a concrete relationship with her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer yafer} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer says the {\i yavam} may be {\i mefir}, whether there is one or two brothers-in-law. Whether this means he does so alone (as a {\i ba\u8217'al}) or together with the father (as an {\i arus}) remains to be clarified by the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua omer l\u8217'echad aval lo lishnayim} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua says this authority applies only when there is one {\i yavam}, not when there are two. The reason is not merely epistemic uncertainty about which brother is \u8220"really\u8221" the one in the relationship, but something more fundamental: the relationship is indeterminate at its base, and therefore neither brother genuinely occupies the role of husband. It follows that if there are two {\i yevamin} and neither has the status of husband, the father annuls the vow by himself \u8212- not that the parties are paralyzed, but that the indeterminacy removes the {\i yavam} from the equation and leaves the father as the sole authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva omer lo l\u8217'echad velo lishnayim} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva rules that even a single {\i yavam} has no such status. In his view, even with only one brother-in-law, the relationship does not carry sufficient halachic weight to confer authority over her vows, and everything reverts to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer challenges Rabbi Akiva with a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}. When a man marries a woman on his own initiative \u8212- {\i isha shekana hu l\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- he acquires the right to annul her vows. How much more so, then, in the case where heaven itself has designated the relationship \u8212- {\i isha shehiknu lo min hashamayim} \u8212- should he be in a position to be {\i mefir}? The very existence of a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, the concept of {\i yakum shem achiv hamet}, suggests this divinely ordained bond is stronger, not weaker, than one created by purely human initiative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva rejects the comparison. {\i Im amarta b\u8217'isha shekana hu l\u8217'atzmo, she\u8217'ein l\u8217'acherim bah reshus} \u8212- when a man marries a woman entirely on his own, no one else has any share or claim to her. {\i Tomar b\u8217'isha shehiknu lo min hashamayim, sheyesh l\u8217'acherim bah reshus} \u8212- in the case of {\i yibum}, another brother also has a connection to her, and even after one brother acts, the other is not categorically excluded. The relationship is thus not exclusive, and for that reason the {\i yavam} does not stand in the same position as a husband. Rabbi Yehoshua presses back \u8212- {\i Akiva, peratzta b\u8217'shnei yevamim, mah atah meshiv al yavam echad?} \u8212- conceding that the argument works for two brothers but asking what Rabbi Akiva would say about a single {\i yavam}, where Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} seems to apply in full force. Rabbi Akiva responds: {\i ein hayevamah gemurah layavam k\u8217'shem she\u8217'arusah gemurah l\u8217'isha} \u8212- the {\i yevamah} is not fully given over to the {\i yavam} the way an {\i arusah} is given over to her husband. The nature of the relationship is not as weighty; it is not marriage in the full sense, and therefore even a single {\i yavam} cannot be {\i mefir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zika & Relationship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva: No Zika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now provides a conceptual framework for the three-way dispute. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is explained as {\i ein zika} \u8212- he does not recognize any concrete bond. {\i Zika}, as familiar from {\i Masechet Yevamot}, refers to whether the potential relationship awaiting actualization is itself given real halachic weight, such that we speak of an actual existing bond rather than a mere future possibility. One might have thought that even Rabbi Akiva could acknowledge {\i zika} while nonetheless maintaining that it is not strong enough to reach the level of {\i eshet ish} or to function like the bond of a husband for purposes of {\i hafara}. However, the {\i Gemara} prefers to frame Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position as a denial of {\i zika} as a concrete relationship altogether. There is a {\i mitzvah}, there is an {\i issur} of {\i lo tihiyeh eishet hamet hachutza}, but none of that has yet defined a real, concrete relational status \u8212- it remains a potential and an obligation, not an actualized bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua/Eliezer: Yes Zi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Eliezer affirm {\i zika} as a real, concrete bond. The distinction between them is then straightforward: Rabbi Yehoshua holds that when there are two {\i yevamin}, the {\i zika} is fundamentally indeterminate \u8212- one cannot locate where it attaches \u8212- and so neither brother can invoke it. Rabbi Eliezer holds {\i ein b\u8217'reirah} is not a concern here, and even with two brothers some basis remains to recognize a {\i yavam}\u8217's authority. The {\i Gemara} presses: even granted {\i zika}, how can Rabbi Eliezer hold that any particular one of two brothers is in a position to annul?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami Ukeimta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Ami: k\u8217'gon she\u8217'asa ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- Rabbi Ami resolves the difficulty with an {\i ukeimta}, a recontextualization of the {\i mishnah}. He holds that the {\i mishnah} is not describing the baseline case of two brothers where neither has done anything. Rather, it speaks of a case where one of the brothers has performed {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- the rabbinic act of {\i kiddushin} performed by a {\i yavam} with his {\i yevamah}. As is well known from {\i Masechet Yevamot}, {\i ma\u8217'amar} has no {\i d\u8217'Oraita} effect; the only halachically effective routes to completion are {\i bi\u8217'ah} for {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah} for release. A {\i get} accomplishes nothing {\i d\u8217'Oraita}, and {\i kiddushin} on its own does nothing {\i d\u8217'Oraita}. Nevertheless, the Rabbis instituted {\i ma\u8217'amar} as a preparatory act \u8212- demonstrating intent, providing structure to the relationship, and in the case of multiple brothers, serving to designate which one is proceeding with the {\i yibum}. The Rabbis actually encouraged {\i ma\u8217'amar} even when there is only one brother, as a way of bringing formality and intention to the process before the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} that constitutes the {\i yibum} itself. Once one of two brothers performs {\i ma\u8217'amar}, he is thereby designated as the one proceeding, and on that basis Rabbi Eliezer holds he is in a position to be {\i mefir}. This is the {\i ukeimta}: the dispute between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua concerns a case where {\i ma\u8217'amar} has been performed \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer holding it is sufficient to designate one brother for {\i hafara}, and Rabbi Yehoshua holding it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar & Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately notes a difficulty: {\i ma\u8217'amar} is rabbinic; why should a rabbinic act create a concrete enough relationship to confer the authority to do {\i hafara}? The answer is that {\i Rabbi Eliezer savar lah k\u8217'Beis Shammai}, for Rabbi Eliezer holds in accordance with Beis Shammai, {\i de\u8217'amri ma\u8217'amar koneh kinyan gamur} \u8212- that {\i ma\u8217'amar} effects a complete acquisition, a {\i kinyan gamur}, with real {\i d\u8217'Oraita} consequences. The Ran elaborates: according to Beis Shammai, {\i ma\u8217'amar} achieves the status of {\i k\u8217'nusah} \u8212- as if she has been fully brought into the marriage \u8212- such that if the {\i yavam} subsequently changes his mind, he need not perform {\i chalitzah}; he can dissolve the relationship with a {\i get} instead. This is a significant halachic consequence that demonstrates the {\i ma\u8217'amar} has genuinely redefined the relationship, moving it from the track of {\i yibum} onto the track of regular marriage with its attendant modes of dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beis Shammai Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar Narrow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} introduces a complication. A certain {\i Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- distinct from Rabbi Eliezer of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- holds a narrower reading of Beis Shammai\u8217's position: {\i ma\u8217'amar l\u8217'Beis Shammai ein koneh ela lidchos b\u8217'tzara} \u8212- all {\i ma\u8217'amar} accomplishes according to Beis Shammai is to push aside the concern about the co-wife; it does not constitute a genuine {\i kinyan} in the broader sense. This narrow reading undercuts the premise that allowed Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position in the {\i mishnah} to make sense, since without a true {\i kinyan gamur} from {\i ma\u8217'amar}, one cannot explain how one brother among two is designated as holding a concrete relationship with the {\i yevamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Brother Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand what \u8220"pushing aside the co-wife\u8221" means, consider a case with three brothers \u8212- call them Reuven, Shimon, and Levi \u8212- where two of them are married to sisters. If Shimon dies, Reuven has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} with Shimon\u8217's wife. But if he does so and then Levi dies, he is already married to the wife\u8217's sister and cannot perform {\i yibum} with Levi\u8217's widow. Conversely, if he does not perform {\i yibum} and Levi also dies, he faces the problem of {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- each woman is the quasi-wife\u8217's sister of the other because the {\i zika} to each renders the other an {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- and he cannot perform {\i yibum} with either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now suppose Reuven performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with Shimon\u8217's widow before Levi dies. When Levi subsequently dies, can Reuven say: \u8220"I have already acted upon my {\i zika} to Shimon\u8217's widow; Levi\u8217's widow can go free, since she is not in a genuine {\i zika} relationship with me in the same way\u8221"? Beis Shammai says yes \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is sufficient to establish that Levi\u8217's widow need not concern him; she is the {\i tzarah} who is {\i nidchit}, pushed aside. Rabbi Elazar understands this narrowly: Beis Shammai is not saying {\i ma\u8217'amar} effects a full {\i kinyan}; it merely operates in this local context to remove the obligation with respect to the sister. Read this way, we are left without the broader {\i kinyan gamur} that would explain Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position in the {\i mishnah}. The {\i Gemara} therefore turns to an alternative explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mezonot Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the difficulty: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan \u8212- k\u8217'gon she\u8217'amad b\u8217'din v\u8217'nitchayev lah mezonot}. The case is not simply one of {\i ma\u8217'amar} alone; it is a case where one of the brothers was brought before a {\i beit din} \u8212- presumably the older brother, to whom the obligation of {\i yibum} is first directed \u8212- and the {\i beit din} obligated him to pay {\i mezonot} to the {\i yevamah}. The logic is that this woman has been left without a husband, and if one particular brother is expected to fulfill the obligation and has been deferring, once a full year has passed she should not be left without support. The {\i beit din} therefore makes him financially responsible for her maintenance for as long as she continues to wait.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to a principle the Ran articulated in the previous {\i sugya}, cited here in the name of Rabbi Pinchas in the name of Rava: {\i kol hanoderet al da\u8217'at ba\u8217'alah hi noderet} \u8212- a woman who makes a vow does so with her husband\u8217's understanding in mind. The Ran was careful to explain that this principle is not a blanket rule that any person paying someone\u8217's bills can annul her vows. The mechanism requires someone who is already in a quasi-husband relationship, and to that pre-existing relationship one adds the further reality that he is paying her food bills and she sees herself as living in a relationship with him and about to be fully married to him. Under those conditions, her vows are presumed to be made {\i al da\u8217'ato}, in accordance with his understanding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezonot = Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran draws out the practical consequence: paying {\i mezonot} combined with the existing {\i zika} relationship places this {\i yavam} into the {\i Torah}\u8217's category of {\i ba\u8217'al}. More specifically, the Ran holds that this reflects a {\i nesu\u8217'in}-type relationship \u8212- and therefore, according to those who say the {\i yavam} can annul in such a case, he does so alone, without the father, as a {\i ba\u8217'al} and not merely as an {\i arus}. This resolves the earlier question about what {\i yafer} in the {\i mishnah} means: in the case where {\i mezonot} have been imposed, the {\i yavam} functions as a {\i ba\u8217'al} and acts independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Textual Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now re-examines the text of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} in the {\i mishnah} in light of this reading. Rabbi Eliezer said: a woman he acquired for himself \u8212- {\i isha shekana hu l\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- he can annul her vows; how much more so a woman whom heaven designated for him. If, as Rabbi Ami holds, the {\i mishnah} is describing a case where {\i ma\u8217'amar} was performed, then this woman is not merely {\i min hashamayim} \u8212- she is {\i also} one whom he acquired himself through {\i ma\u8217'amar}, making her {\i kanah le\u8217'atzmo al yedei shamayim}. The {\i Gemara} notes this apparent over-description but dismisses it: it only strengthens the argument. He is saying, \u8220"This case is designated by heaven \u8212- and parenthetically he also acted himself on top of that \u8212- so surely this should be stronger than one who acted entirely alone.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then observes that if we accept Rabbi Ami\u8217's reading of the {\i mishnah} as a {\i ma\u8217'amar} case, we can potentially resolve a question posed by Rava: {\i ma\u8217'amar l\u8217'Beis Shammai erusin oseh o nisuin oseh?} \u8212- does {\i ma\u8217'amar} according to Beis Shammai produce a state of {\i erusin} or a state of {\i nisuin}? This question arises because even if Beis Shammai holds {\i ma\u8217'amar} is a {\i kinyan gamur}, it is formally an act of {\i kiddushin}. Yet the nature of the {\i yibum} relationship \u8212- where {\i bi\u8217'ah} without any deliberate intent still consummates the marriage \u8212- suggests the entire structure might be closer to {\i nisuin} from the outset, such that {\i ma\u8217'amar} elevates it all the way to {\i nisuin} rather than stopping at {\i erusin}. The practical implications include whether {\i mezonot} is owed after {\i ma\u8217'amar} and the full range of questions attending to {\i nesu\u8217'in} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Father Mentioned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proposes a resolution: since the {\i mishnah} states simply {\i yafer} \u8212- he annuls \u8212- without mentioning the father, it implies the {\i yavam} acts alone, as a {\i ba\u8217'al}, which would indicate {\i nisuin} status. The working assumption is that those who grant the {\i yavam} any authority do so because his relationship resembles {\i nisuin}, and therefore the {\i mishnah}\u8217's omission of the father is significant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yitzchak View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak, citing Rav Ami, rejects this inference: {\i yafer beshutfut} \u8212- {\i yafer} means together with the father. The {\i yavam}\u8217's status in that case is like an {\i arus}, not a {\i ba\u8217'al}, and his authority is not independent. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's {\i yafer} does not resolve whether it is {\i erusin} or {\i nisuin}, and the question remains open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braiyta Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefta Citation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a {\i braita} \u8212- a passage from the {\i Tosefta} \u8212- that parallels the {\i mishnah} debate. {\i Shomeret yavam bein yavam echad bein shnei yevamin}, Rabbi Eliezer says {\i yafer}, Rabbi Yehoshua says only for one and not for two, and Rabbi Akiva says neither one nor two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's argument in the {\i braita} is formulated more precisely than in the {\i mishnah}. He says: {\i u\u8217'mah im isha she\u8217'ein lo chelek bah ad shelo tavo lereshuto} \u8212- a completely unrelated woman, toward whom he had absolutely no prior connection, once she enters his domain through an act of {\i kiddushin}, she is fully his and he can annul her vows. {\i Isha sheyesh lo chelek bah ad shelo tavo lereshuto} \u8212- but the {\i yevamah} is a woman to whom he already has a connection \u8212- the {\i zika}, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} \u8212- even before he has taken any additional step. How much more so, {\i mishebat\u8217'a lereshuto}, once she has come further into his domain, should he have that power? The Ran notes that the phrase {\i mishebat\u8217'a lereshuto} is chosen rather than {\i nichnesah lereshuto}, and suggests this reflects the language of {\i kiddushin} rather than {\i nesu\u8217'in} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} reads {\i ba\u8217'ah lereshuto} as referring to an act of {\i ma\u8217'amar} rather than completed {\i yibum}, since completed {\i yibum} would make her fully his wife and no one would debate it. This confirms Rabbi Ami\u8217's {\i ukeimta}: the case is one where {\i ma\u8217'amar} has been performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's response in the {\i braita} is explicit. He distinguishes: {\i bekiddushin shekanah hu l\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- when a man performs {\i kiddushin} himself, {\i shekeshem she\u8217'ein lo chelek bah} before the marriage, {\i she\u8217'ein l\u8217'acherim chelek bah} either \u8212- nobody has any connection to her, and after {\i kiddushin} he closes out everyone else entirely. {\i Tomar b\u8217'isha shehiknu lo min hashamayim} \u8212- in the case of the {\i yevamah}, {\i shekeshem sheyesh lo imah chelek, ken yesh l\u8217'acher imah chelek} \u8212- just as he has a connection to her, so too another brother has a connection to her. Even after {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the other brother can theoretically perform {\i bi\u8217'ah} and complete the {\i yibum}; the exclusivity that characterizes an ordinary {\i kiddushin} is simply absent. This is the structural flaw in Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}: ordinary {\i kiddushin} excludes all others; the {\i yavam}\u8217's connection does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua again presses Rabbi Akiva: {\i Akiva, zu hi bishnei yevamin} \u8212- this reasoning about the lack of exclusivity holds when there are two brothers, but what do you say about a single {\i yavam}? When there is only one brother, there is no other claimant. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's argument seems fully applicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva Final\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's answer is deliberately terse: {\i u\u8217'kesha\u8217'ar devarim ken nedarim} \u8212- just as in all other laws, so too with vows. Ben Azzai famously lamented \u8212- {\i chavalo l\u8217'cha Ben Azzai shelo shimasht et Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- that he had not attended upon Rabbi Akiva, implying the answer was so compressed that one needed deep familiarity with Rabbi Akiva\u8217's thinking to grasp it fully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Key Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar Presupposed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now draws the conclusion that the {\i braita} confirms Rabbi Ami\u8217's {\i ukeimta}. The evidence: Rabbi Akiva himself says {\i bein asah ma\u8217'amar bein lo asah ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- whether or not he performed {\i ma\u8217'amar}, it makes no difference. This statement only makes sense if {\i ma\u8217'amar} was already on the table as a live option in the debate, which confirms that the scenario under discussion was one where {\i ma\u8217'amar} was part of the picture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'at Lereshuto\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Further confirmation comes from the {\i braita}\u8217's formulation. The phrase {\i isha sheyesh lo chelek bah\u8230 ? mishebat\u8217'a lereshuto} \u8212- referring to the {\i yevamah} who has come further into his domain \u8212- can only refer to a state after {\i ma\u8217'amar}. If {\i ba\u8217'at lereshuto} meant the consummation of {\i yibum} through {\i bi\u8217'ah}, she would already be his wife and the debate would be meaningless. The phrase therefore presupposes that something beyond mere {\i zika} has occurred but that full {\i yibum} has not yet been completed \u8212- which is precisely the state after {\i ma\u8217'amar}. This confirms {\i ka pashit mineih d\u8217'keshe\u8217'asah bah ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- one can derive from this passage that the entire debate is framed around the {\i ma\u8217'amar} scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Stoning = No Eshet Ish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's clinching argument is now spelled out by Rava: {\i ee atah modeh she\u8217'ein chayavin sekilah k\u8217'na\u8217'arah hame\u8217'urasah} \u8212- you would have to admit that if the {\i yevamah} were to sleep with another man after {\i ma\u8217'amar}, she would not be liable to stoning as would a {\i na\u8217'arah hame\u8217'urasah}, the betrothed maiden. She is not {\i eshet ish} in the fullest sense. {\i U\u8217'kesha\u8217'ar devarim ken nedarim} \u8212- therefore, just as she lacks the full status of {\i eshet ish} in all other areas of law, so too with respect to {\i nedarim} she is not fully under his authority. No matter how robust the {\i zika}, no matter that {\i ma\u8217'amar} was performed, no matter that only one brother is in the picture \u8212- unless the relationship rises to the level of genuine {\i eshet ish}, the {\i yavam} cannot be {\i mefir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Mishnah Phrase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes by returning to the closing words of the {\i mishnah} itself, as cited by Rav Ashi: {\i ein yevamah gemurah l\u8217'ish keshem she\u8217'arusah gemurah l\u8217'ish} \u8212- the {\i yevamah} is not fully this man\u8217's wife in the way that a betrothed woman is fully her husband\u8217's {\i arusah}. This formulation encapsulates Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position: whatever the weight of {\i zika}, whatever the effect of {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the {\i yevamah} remains in a categorically incomplete relationship \u8212- not a fully constituted {\i eshet ish} \u8212- and therefore the {\i yavam} cannot perform {\i hafara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 75\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-Annulment of Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this {\i daf} we move away from the extended discussion of the {\i aras} (groom) and the {\i yavam}, returning to what might be called more basic territory concerning the act of {\i hafarah} \u8212- though the cases examined remain anything but simple.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents the following case. A husband about to depart on a business trip says to his wife: {\i kol hanedarim she-tadori mi-kan v\u8217'ad she-avo mi-makom ploni, ha-rei hen kayamim} \u8212- \u8220"All the vows you make from the time I leave until I return, I hereby affirm.\u8221" The Ran notes that the husband says {\i mi-kan}, from here, i.e., from the moment of his departure, rather than {\i me-achshav}, from right now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i lo amar klum} \u8212- he has said nothing. If, however, he declares {\i ha-rei hen mufarim} \u8212- that they are hereby annulled \u8212- then Rabbi Eliezer rules {\i mufar}, they are validly annulled in advance. The {\i chachamim} rule {\i eino mufar}: pre-annulment of a vow that does not yet exist is ineffective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mekayem vs.\u160 ?Meifer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between these two rulings rests on the conceptual difference between {\i mekayem} (affirming a vow) and {\i meifer} (annulling it). To be {\i mekayem} a vow is to strengthen its reality as a binding {\i issur}, and one cannot strengthen what does not yet exist. But negating a vow \u8212- the {\i chachamim} and Rabbi Eliezer dispute whether annulment requires the prior existence of a vow in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Kal Vachom\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer presses his case with a {\i kal va-chomer}: {\i im heifer nedarim she-ba\u8217'u li-khlal issur, lo yafer nedarim she-lo ba\u8217'u li-khlal issur?} \u8212- If the husband can annul vows that have already taken effect and manifested as real-world prohibitions, how much more so should he be able to annul vows that have not yet come into existence? As the {\i gemara} will soon explore, the precise import of this argument \u8212- whether Rabbi Eliezer means the pre-annulment {\i prevents} the vow from ever taking effect, or whether it {\i annuls} the vow at the instant of its formation \u8212- is itself an open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Respond\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} respond by invoking a scriptural link: {\i ishah yekimennu ve-ishah yeferennu} \u8212- \u8220"the husband shall affirm it and the husband shall annul it.\u8221" From this verse they derive a parallel structure: {\i et she-ba li-khlal hakim, ba li-khlal hafer; lo ba li-khlal hakim, lo ba li-khlal hafer} \u8212- only something that is subject to affirmation can be subject to annulment. Since a vow that does not yet exist cannot be affirmed, it likewise cannot be annulled. The {\i kal va-chomer} may be logically appealing in principle, but the verse forecloses it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Act vs.\u160 ?Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} takes for granted that a husband may annul his wife\u8217's vows without having heard them \u8212- that question was addressed at length in the preceding discussion. The only issue here is whether annulment can precede the vow\u8217's very existence. One might ask whether the same conceptual move that was used earlier \u8212- splitting the act of {\i hafarah} from its effect, so that the act is performed now but takes hold only later \u8212- could be applied here to the {\i kiyum} side of the equation. The answer, however, is that such a maneuver cannot help the husband who wishes to be {\i mekayem}: since {\i kiyum} by definition requires a vow to strengthen, any attempted {\i kiyum} would necessarily await the vow\u8217's formation and would still be ineffective. The device of separating act from effect addressed only the {\i shemi\u8217'ah} problem; it has no traction here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prevent vs.\u160 ?Annul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a formal question \u8212- {\i iba\u8217'ya lehu} \u8212- concerning the nature of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's ruling. Granted that the husband\u8217's pre-issued {\i hafarah} is effective, what precisely does it accomplish?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Annul After Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first possibility: the vow does take effect, but simultaneously with its taking effect it is annulled. There is no moment in real time during which the wife stands bound \u8212- the annulment is instantaneous \u8212- yet conceptually the vow exists and is then negated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prevent Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possibility: the husband\u8217's prior {\i hafarah} is preventative. The pre-annulment acts as an ounce of prevention, blocking the vow from ever acquiring any force or reality whatsoever. It never takes effect at all \u8212- {\i lo chail klal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ve-ani Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference ({\i nafka mina}) between these two positions concerns the case of {\i hatfasah}. Suppose the wife declares {\i hareini nezirah}, and a bystander immediately says {\i ve-ani} \u8212- transferring the {\i nezirut} status to herself. The husband had, a week earlier, issued a blanket pre-annulment covering his wife\u8217's vows. If the vow took effect and was then annulled \u8212- the first interpretation \u8212- the bystander\u8217's {\i ve-ani} attaches to a real status that existed, if only for a conceptual instant, and her {\i nezirut} stands. If the vow never took effect at all \u8212- the second interpretation \u8212- there is no status to transfer, and the {\i ve-ani} is meaningless.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hafarah vs.\u160 ?Hatarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Example Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara\u8217's} example: the wife takes a vow, a companion says {\i ve-ani}, and the husband then annuls the wife\u8217's vow. The question is what consequences follow for the companion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatarah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a further question. What if, instead of the husband\u8217's {\i hafarah}, the original person who made the vow went to a {\i chacham} for {\i hatarah} \u8212- formal dissolution? Would the companion who said {\i ve-ani} also be released?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the Ran articulates a distinction that is central to this entire {\i perek} yet, somewhat surprisingly, has not been foregrounded until now. {\i Hatarah} \u8212- dissolution by a {\i chacham} \u8212- is {\i oker ha-neder me-ikaro}: it retroactively uproots the vow from its very source. The mechanism of {\i hatarah} is to reframe the original vow as a {\i neder ta\u8217'ut}, a vow made in error, which means the vow is deemed never to have taken effect. {\i Hafarah}, by contrast, operates {\i mikan u-le-ha\u8217'ba}: it cuts off the vow going forward but does not retroactively undo the past. The husband does not uproot the vow; he severs it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction has direct practical consequences for the {\i ve-ani} case. When the first person obtains {\i hatarah} from a {\i chacham}, the retroactive effect means the original vow never existed, so all subsequent {\i hatfasah} \u8212- the status-transfers effected by those who said {\i ve-ani} \u8212- collapses as well. When the husband performs {\i hafarah}, however, the vow genuinely did exist (even if only for a conceptual instant), and that real, if momentary, existence is what the companion\u8217's {\i ve-ani} latches onto. The {\i hafarah} stops the wife\u8217's {\i nezirut} going forward, but the companion who said {\i ve-ani} transferred the status at the moment it existed and therefore remains bound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not merely a {\i sevarah}-level inference; there is an explicit {\i gemara} in Nazir, 79b, that poses precisely this question: {\i ba\u8217'al me\u8217'akar akar, o migaz gayiz} \u8212- does the husband uproot the vow entirely, or does he merely cut it off from continuing? The {\i nafka mina} given there is identical: a woman vows {\i nezirut}, her companion says {\i ve-ani}, and the husband annuls the wife\u8217's vow. If the husband is {\i me\u8217'akar} \u8212- uprooting retroactively \u8212- then the companion too is released. If he is {\i migaz gayiz} \u8212- cutting it off going forward \u8212- the wife is released but the companion remains bound. The {\i gemara} in Nazir concludes: {\i ba\u8217'al migaz gayiz}; a {\i chacham} is {\i oker me-ikara}. This is an important and fundamental distinction that is, ironically, spelled out more explicitly in Nazir than in Nedarim itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ve-ani Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran raises a second question worth addressing. By the time the companion says {\i ve-ani}, the wife\u8217's vow has already been annulled \u8212- so the wife is no longer a {\i nezirah} at that moment. Why should {\i ve-ani} not mean \u8220"and I am like you are right now,\u8221" in which case the companion is equally free?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelamim Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran resolves this by reference to a {\i gemara} concerning meat of {\i zivchei shelamim}. Before the blood is dashed on the {\i mizbeach}, the meat is forbidden; once it has been dashed, it is permitted. If someone then declares {\i ve-zeh ka-zeh} \u8212- \u8220"and this like that\u8221" \u8212- using the {\i shelamim} meat as the referent, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i be-ikaro kamatfis} \u8212- does he mean to compare to the object\u8217's current status (now permitted) or to its earlier status (when it was forbidden)? The {\i gemara} concludes {\i de-hashta kamatfis} \u8212- he refers to its present status \u8212- and therefore no {\i hatfasah} results. The Ran acknowledges this parallel, but distinguishes the {\i ve-ani} case on two grounds. First, the verbal form {\i ve-ani}, without the explicit comparative {\i kamoah} (\u8220"like her\u8221"), does not naturally carry the nuance \u8220"like her as she is right now.\u8221" Second, and more substantially: what stands before the companion is the wife\u8217's act of declaration \u8212- {\i hareini nezirah} \u8212- and the {\i hafarah} itself is not present or visible. Given that backdrop, {\i ve-ani} most naturally refers to the {\i nezirut} status that that declaration created. Even if that status evaporated an instant later, it did exist, and it is to that status that {\i ve-ani} attaches.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the {\i nafka mina}: if the pre-issued {\i hafarah} means the vow took effect and was then immediately annulled, the companion\u8217's {\i ve-ani} latches onto the status that existed in that conceptual instant and the companion remains bound. If the pre-issued {\i hafarah} prevented the vow from ever taking effect, then there was never any status to transfer and the companion is free.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the precise language of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i kal va-chomer}: if the husband can annul vows {\i she-ba\u8217'u li-khlal issur} \u8212- that have already come into the category of prohibition \u8212- should he not {\i a fortiori} be able to annul vows {\i she-lo ba\u8217'u li-khlal issur} \u8212- that have not yet entered that category? The natural reading of this argument is that Rabbi Eliezer claims the pre-annulment should prevent the vow from ever taking effect. That reading would resolve the earlier {\i ibaya} in favor of {\i lo chaylin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelo Ba\u8217'u\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, resists this reading. The phrase {\i shelo ba\u8217'u} does not mean vows that will never come into existence \u8212- {\i she-einan ba\u8217'in}. It means vows that have not yet come into existence \u8212- {\i adayin lo ba\u8217'u}. Rabbi Eliezer is not claiming that the pre-annulment defeats vows that would otherwise never take effect; he is speaking about vows that exist in the future but have not yet arrived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenged Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran offers a refined reading of the {\i kal va-chomer} in light of this correction. The argument is not that pre-annulment {\i prevents} the vow from taking effect. Rather: if the husband can annul a vow after it has taken hold in an unchallenged state, how much more so should he be able to annul a vow that comes into existence already challenged \u8212- {\i mumo imo}, bearing its defect from the very moment of its birth. The vow never existed in a pristine, unchallenged state; from the instant it came into being, the {\i hafarah} was already present. According to the Ran, this is Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's actual position: the vow does take effect, but it arrives already carrying the seeds of its own annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own vs.\u160 ?Wife Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a further analogy. When a person makes a conditional pre-declaration \u8212- on the order of what is done on {\i Erev Rosh Hashanah} \u8212- that any future vows should be considered void, those vows do not take effect when subsequently made. Should we infer by {\i kal va-chomer} that a husband\u8217's pre-issued {\i hafarah} similarly prevents his wife\u8217's vows from taking effect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Mechanisms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects this inference: {\i ha ke-d\u8217'ita ve-ha ke-d\u8217'ita} \u8212- each case operates by its own mechanism. When a person pre-empts his own vows, the mechanism is {\i ta\u8217'ut}: the conditional declaration frames any future vow as an error, as something he never truly intended. The vow does not take effect because the {\i ta\u8217'ut} mechanism retroactively undermines the act of vowing. {\i Hafarah} of a wife\u8217's vow operates through an entirely different mechanism \u8212- annulment, not error \u8212- and the two cannot simply be mapped onto each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After to Before\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran, however, finds this answer unsatisfying if taken at face value as the {\i kal va-chomer\u8217's} foundation, and offers a brilliant reinterpretation. The {\i kal va-chomer} does not claim that what works for one\u8217's own vows must work identically for a wife\u8217's vows. Rather, the analogy is structural: for one\u8217's own vows, where the operative mechanism is {\i ta\u8217'ut}, the pre-emptive declaration works {\i before} (via a {\i tenai}) even though one cannot undo one\u8217's own vow {\i after} by oneself. The structural lesson is: what works {\i after} should certainly work {\i before}. Now transpose to the wife\u8217's vows. There, the operative mechanism is {\i hafarah}, which concededly works {\i after}. By the same structural logic \u8212- if it works after, it should work before \u8212- the {\i hafarah} should work before the vow comes into existence as well. The mechanisms are different, but the logical form of the {\i kal va-chomer} is the same in both cases, and that is the argument\u8217's real force.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mikveh Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikveh Cleans\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} respond to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i kal va-chomer} with a counter-example from the laws of {\i tumah}: {\i u-mah mikveh she-ma\u8217'aleh et ha-teme\u8217'im mi-tum\u8217'atan} \u8212- just as a {\i mikveh} restores a {\i tamei} person to {\i taharah} after the fact\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remove vs.\u160 ?Prevent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8230 ?nonetheless, {\i ein matzil et ha-tahor min ha-tumah} \u8212- being immersed in a {\i mikveh} does not prevent {\i tumah} from attaching. If a person is standing in a {\i mikveh} and simultaneously comes into contact with something {\i tamei}, or a bodily condition arises that would render him {\i tamei} \u8212- {\i tovel ve-sheretz be-yado} \u8212- he is {\i tamei}. The {\i mikveh} removes {\i tumah} after the fact but does not block {\i tumah} from taking hold in the first place. The {\i kal va-chomer} that \u8220"if it works after it works before\u8221" therefore does not hold as a general principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Person\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The text continues with an additional line, noting: a person \u8212- {\i adam} \u8212- who cannot make a {\i tamei} object {\i tahor} ({\i she-ein ma\u8217'aleh et ha-tamei mi-tum\u8217'ato}), is it not logical that he should be unable to prevent {\i tahor} things from becoming {\i tamei}? Yet consider the case of {\i tumah blu\u8217'ah}: an object swallowed inside a person\u8217's body is shielded from {\i tumah} \u8212- it cannot become {\i tamei} \u8212- even though the person himself cannot make {\i tamei} things {\i tahor}. In other words, the mechanisms run in exactly opposite directions: a {\i mikveh} removes {\i tumah} but does not prevent it; a person prevents {\i tumah} (when absorbed internally) but does not remove it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context-Dependent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran explains the double thrust of the {\i chachamim\u8217's} argument. Point one: the {\i mikveh} itself demonstrates that something can remove without preventing \u8212- {\i kal va-chomer} logic from \u8220"after\u8221" to \u8220"before\u8221" does not operate in a vacuum. Point two: the comparison between the {\i mikveh} and the {\i adam} case demonstrates that across {\i halakhah}, the direction in which a mechanism operates is context-determined and cannot be extrapolated by {\i kal va-chomer} alone. The {\i chachamim} are not necessarily claiming that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i kal va-chomer} is logically incoherent, but that this class of inference is not ironclad \u8212- its force depends on what the surrounding {\i halakhic} framework supports.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-dunking & Seeds (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-dunking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} offer a second counter-example: {\i im matbilin kli tamei le-taharo} \u8212- if you can immerse a {\i tamei} vessel to render it {\i tahor}, can you pre-immerse a {\i tahor} vessel so that when it later becomes {\i tamei} the problem will have been solved in advance? The answer is obviously not. This is analogous to the person who says {\i echta ve-ashuv} \u8212- I will sin and preemptively repent. The {\i mikveh} does not work prospectively in that fashion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suggests Haylan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Interestingly, the way the {\i chachamim} frame this counter-example \u8212- {\i yatbilu kli le-cheshe-yitma le-taharo}, \u8220"let them pre-immerse a vessel so that when it becomes {\i tamei} it will become {\i tahor}\u8221" \u8212- seems to presuppose that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's mechanism is one in which the vow {\i does} take effect and is then reversed. The phrase {\i le-cheshe-yitma} implies: once it becomes {\i tamei}, the pre-immersion will kick in and render it {\i tahor}. This is the {\i haylan} interpretation \u8212- the vow takes effect, and the pre-annulment then resolves it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conflicting Signals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} has two analogies pointing in opposite directions. The {\i mikveh}-in-use analogy \u8212- standing in a {\i mikveh} does not prevent {\i tumah} \u8212- suggests the {\i chachamim} are arguing against the {\i lo haylan} position: Rabbi Eliezer thinks pre-annulment prevents the vow from taking effect, and they counter that prevention does not work that way. The pre-dunking analogy, by contrast, suggests the {\i chachamim} are arguing against the {\i haylan} position: Rabbi Eliezer thinks the vow takes effect and is then reversed, and they counter that pre-applying a remedy for a future condition does not work. Two analogies; two different signals about what position they are attacking.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion & Scriptu (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Either Way\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this tension in a striking way. {\i Rabbanan lo kaymei lehu be-ta\u8217'ameih de-Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} themselves did not know precisely what Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position was. Accordingly, {\i ve-hachi kamre lei} \u8212- their response was structured to cover both possibilities. They said to him: what is your view? If you hold {\i de-chayil u-mivtal} \u8212- that the vow takes effect and is then annulled \u8212- then the pre-dunking of the vessel is your refutation; we do not say that pre-applying the remedy solves the problem after the {\i tumah} attaches. And if you hold {\i de-lo chayil} \u8212- that the pre-annulment prevents the vow from taking effect entirely \u8212- then the {\i mikveh} is your refutation; being immersed in a {\i mikveh} does not prevent {\i tumah} from attaching.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-dunking & Seeds (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seeds Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer then responds with his own analogy. {\i U-mah zera\u8217'im teme\u8217'im, keivan she-zera\u8217'an be-karka taherun} \u8212- impure seeds, when planted in the ground, lose their {\i tumah} and become {\i tahor}. And we further know: {\i zeru\u8217'in ve-omdin lo kol she-ken} \u8212- things already planted cannot become {\i tamei} at all. Here is a case in which both mechanisms operate in tandem: planting removes existing {\i tumah} and simultaneously prevents future {\i tumah} from taking hold.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-dunking & Seeds (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suggests Lo Haylan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's chosen analogy, it points strongly toward the {\i lo haylan} interpretation. The seeds, once planted, are entirely immune to {\i tumah}; they do not become {\i tamei} and then become {\i tahor} \u8212- they simply cannot become {\i tamei} at all. Rabbi Eliezer appears to be saying that the pre-issued {\i hafarah} operates like the planting of the seed: it shields the vow from ever taking hold.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion & Scriptu (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seeds Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i shema mina lo chaylin} \u8212- the seeds analogy supports the {\i lo haylan} interpretation, that according to Rabbi Eliezer the vow never takes effect at all. This resolves the {\i ibaya} in favor of the straightforward reading of the {\i kal va-chomer}: if {\i hafarah} can annul a vow that has taken effect, it should certainly be able to prevent a vow from taking effect in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncertainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the {\i gemara} has already observed: {\i rabbanan lo kaymei lehu be-ta\u8217'ameih de-Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} themselves were uncertain which of these two positions Rabbi Eliezer held, and hence they armed themselves with counter-arguments sufficient to rebut either possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scripture Over Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the {\i chachamim\u8217's} own ruling, the {\i gemara} notes that in the end their position does not rest solely on refuting the {\i kal va-chomer} but on a {\i gezerat ha-katuv}. The verse {\i ishah yekimennu ve-ishah yeferennu} establishes a parallel: only what can be established ({\i hakim}) can be annulled ({\i hafer}). A vow that does not yet exist cannot be established; therefore, it cannot be annulled. This is a scriptural limitation that operates independently of any logical inference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran raises what seems like an obvious difficulty: if the {\i chachamim} have a {\i gezerat ha-katuv}, why did they spend so much effort deploying {\i kal va-chomer} counter-arguments and analogies from {\i tumah}? Why not simply cite the verse and be done with it? And conversely, if the {\i chachamim} reject this type of {\i kal va-chomer} as a matter of principle, why do we find elsewhere \u8212- as in the case of selling a daughter into slavery, where the {\i gemara} uses precisely this form of reasoning ({\i u-mekhurah kevar yotze\u8217'ah\u8230 ? eino din she-lo timakher}) \u8212- that the {\i chachamim} do accept \u8220"if it works to reverse, it works to prevent\u8221" as a valid {\i kal va-chomer}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Integrated Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's answer weaves the two together. The {\i chachamim} do accept this class of {\i kal va-chomer} as a legitimate form of inference \u8212- it is not categorically invalid. What they are saying in the earlier analogies is not \u8220"your logic is incoherent\u8221" but rather \u8220"your logic is not ironclad.\u8221" Look at the breadth of {\i halakhah}: the {\i mikveh} works to remove but not to prevent; the {\i adam} prevents but does not remove; {\i tumah} and {\i taharah} operate according to their own principles and do not always follow intuitive inference. The {\i kal va-chomer} is a starting point, but {\i pesukim} frequently override it. That is the lesson the {\i chachamim} were teaching through all the earlier analogies: this type of inference is defeasible. And here, a {\i pasuk} does indeed override it \u8212- {\i ishah yekimennu ve-ishah yeferennu} establishes that the capacity for {\i kiyum} and the capacity for {\i hafarah} are coextensive, and no {\i kal va-chomer} can undo that. The earlier argumentation, understood in this light, was not a frontal assault on the {\i kal va-chomer\u8217's} logical form, but a preparation for the conclusion: even granting the {\i sevara}, a {\i pasuk} takes precedence, and here we have precisely such a {\i pasuk}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 76\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Annulling Throughout Day\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Whole Day Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on 76b opens with a foundational principle: {\i hafarat nedarim kol hayom} \u8212- the annulment of vows may be performed throughout the entire day. At first glance, one might read this as meaning twenty-four hours, a reading seemingly supported by the verse {\i miyom el yom}, \u8220"from day to day,\u8221" which naturally suggests from this moment today until the same moment tomorrow. As the discussion will make clear, however, the {\i mishnah} and the final {\i psak halacha} actually mean something more restricted: until the end of the halakhic day, not a full twenty-four-hour window. That this point requires clarification is itself noteworthy; the {\i pshat} of {\i miyom el yom} had drawn many toward the twenty-four-hour reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds that this rule carries within it both a {\i kulah} and a {\i chumrah} \u8212- or, as the {\i Ran} explains, it is lenient in some cases and strict in others. The leniency is that a husband or father may have many hours in which to perform {\i hafara}. The stringency is that in certain cases, the window may be extraordinarily brief.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Friday Night Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} illustrates with a case involving {\i Shabbat}: if a woman made a {\i neder} on Friday night after {\i Shabbat} had begun, the husband may perform {\i hafara} that same Friday night and throughout {\i Shabbat} day \u8212- until it gets dark at {\i Shabbat}\u8217's end ({\i ad shetecheshach}). The word {\i techeshach} ordinarily implies {\i tzet hakochavim}, nightfall proper, though the precise question of {\i bein hashmashos} remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse case is equally instructive: if she made the {\i neder} just before {\i Shabbat} ended \u8212- one minute before dark \u8212- then the husband has only that one minute to perform {\i hafara}. If darkness fell and he did not act, the opportunity is gone permanently. The {\i mishnah} underscores this with urgency: {\i she\u8217'im chashecha velo hefer, eino yachol lehafer} \u8212- once night falls and he has not annulled, he can no longer do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The choice of {\i Shabbat} as the {\i mishnah}\u8217's example is itself significant. {\i Shabbat} serves to illustrate that even when a {\i neder} is made on {\i Shabbat}, there is a pressing imperative to perform {\i hafara} on {\i Shabbat} itself \u8212- because that may be the husband\u8217's only opportunity. As the {\i Gemara} will clarify, this is what permits {\i hafarat nedarim} on {\i Shabbat} even when the {\i neder} has no bearing whatsoever on {\i Shabbat} observance \u8212- for instance, if she vowed that apples would be forbidden to her on Sundays. Even in such a case, if the {\i neder} was made on {\i Shabbat}, the husband may perform {\i hafara} on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records that this very principle is disputed among {\i Tannaim}. The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds {\i hafarat nedarim kol hayom} \u8212- annulment may be performed throughout the entire halakhic day. {\i Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} and {\i Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} dissent, ruling {\i me\u8217'eis le\u8217'eis} \u8212- a full twenty-four hours. A note on the side cites a {\i Tosefta} that attributes the {\i kol hayom} position explicitly to {\i Rabbi} (Rebbi); either way, the structure is a {\i Tanna Kamma} in favor of the halakhic day and two dissenting voices favoring twenty-four hours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks after the reasoning on each side. The {\i Tanna Kamma} derives from the verse {\i b\u8217'yom shom\u8217'o} \u8212- \u8220"on the day that he hears\u8221" \u8212- that {\i hafara} must be performed on that same day. {\i Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} and {\i Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} derive from the verse {\i miyom el yom}, \u8220"from day to day,\u8221" which sounds precisely like from now until the same time tomorrow \u8212- a full twenty-four hours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then reconciles each position with the verse it appears to discard. For the {\i Tanna Kamma}, who rules by the end of the halakhic day: why does the Torah also write {\i miyom el yom}? Because without that verse, one might have read {\i b\u8217'yom shom\u8217'o} as meaning only the daytime \u8212- excluding the nighttime hours. The verse {\i miyom el yom} comes to teach that the night is included within \u8220"the day,\u8221" so that if she took the vow on Friday night, he may perform {\i hafara} that same Friday night. Yet the outer limit remains the end of that halakhic day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the position of twenty-four hours: why does the Torah also write {\i b\u8217'yom shom\u8217'o}? Because without it, one might have read {\i miyom el yom} as meaning from this Sunday until next Sunday \u8212- from one occurrence of a day to the same day of the following week. The verse {\i b\u8217'yom shom\u8217'o} constrains the window to one day\u8217's time. However, \u8220"one day\u8217's time\u8221" for these {\i Tannaim} means twenty-four hours, not the end of the calendar day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both readings are thus internally coherent and genuine engagements with the verses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi} said in the name of {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi}: {\i ein halacha k\u8217'oso hazug} \u8212- we do not rule like that pair. The {\i halacha} follows the {\i Tanna Kamma} and the {\i mishnah}: annulment must be performed by the end of the halakhic day. {\i Levi} had been inclined to follow the twenty-four-hour position, but {\i Rav} said in the name of his uncle {\i Rabbi Chiya}: we do not rule like that pair. That is the {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shabbat Annulling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion now turns to a {\i mishnah} cited from elsewhere: {\i mefirin nedarim b\u8217'Shabbat}, one may perform {\i hafarat nedarim} on {\i Shabbat}. As for {\i hataras nedarim} \u8212- the process of releasing a vow before a {\i chacham} \u8212- one may seek that release on {\i Shabbat} only when it is {\i letzorech haShabbat}, necessary for {\i Shabbat} itself. These two forms of vow dissolution are treated quite differently, and the {\i Gemara} proceeds to explain why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Din Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hataras nedarim} is a {\i ma\u8217'aseh beis din} \u8212- an act of a court. It involves sitting judges who probe, inquire, and deliberate. Such an act is categorically problematic on {\i Shabbat}. The sole exception is when the {\i hatara} serves a {\i Shabbat} purpose: if one vowed not to eat meat and wishes to dissolve the vow in order to enjoy {\i cholent} on {\i Shabbat}, that qualifies as {\i letzorech Shabbat} and is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafara Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hafarat nedarim}, by contrast, is a fundamentally different act. It involves no inquiry, no deliberation, and no beis din process. The husband or father simply declares {\i mufar lach} \u8212- \u8220"it is annulled for you\u8221" \u8212- and the {\i neder} is dissolved. This is not a {\i ma\u8217'aseh beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet even {\i hafara} is not entirely without halachic complexity on {\i Shabbat}. Beyond the {\i beis din} concern, there is a separate issue: performing {\i hafara} effects a meaningful change in the world. One who takes {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} on {\i Shabbat} is prohibited because he is fixing the grain; one who immerses a vessel ({\i tovel a kli}) is prohibited because he is fixing the vessel. Similarly, dissolving a {\i neder} \u8212- even without the formality of a {\i beis din} \u8212- is some kind of significant, transformative act. That alone might be grounds for concern on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What overrides this concern is the urgency of the situation: {\i Shabbat} may be the husband\u8217's only opportunity to perform {\i hafara}. Because the {\i mishnah} rules that {\i hafara} must be performed within the halakhic day and cannot carry over to the next, the urgency of that last window is precisely what permits the act on {\i Shabbat} \u8212- even when it serves no {\i Shabbat} purpose whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One-Minute Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially entertained the possibility that just as {\i hatara} is limited to cases of {\i letzorech Shabbat}, perhaps {\i hafara} too is only permitted on {\i Shabbat} when it serves a {\i Shabbat} purpose. A {\i baraita} attributed to the school of {\i Rav Pappi} indeed stated: {\i ein meferin nedarim ela letzorech haShabbat} \u8212- one may perform {\i hafara} only for a {\i Shabbat} need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} refutes this from our {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} teaches that if she made her vow one minute before {\i Shabbat} ended, the husband must perform {\i hafara} before dark. Now: can any {\i Shabbat} purpose conceivably be served in that one final minute? Manifestly not \u8212- it is the last moment of {\i Shabbat}, and whatever the {\i neder} touches cannot be remedied for {\i Shabbat} at that point. Yet the {\i mishnah} does not say he is forbidden to perform {\i hafara} in that minute; on the contrary, it instructs him to do so. If {\i hafara} were limited to {\i Shabbat} purposes, it would be forbidden at that very moment \u8212- and indeed at any moment on {\i Shabbat} when the {\i neder} poses no {\i Shabbat} constraint. The {\i mishnah} would have had nothing to say about that final minute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Dependency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the tension by referring back to the {\i Tannaim} debate. The permissibility of {\i hafara} on {\i Shabbat} without a specific {\i Shabbat} purpose depends entirely on whether one rules like the {\i Tanna Kamma} (the end of the halakhic day) or like {\i Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} and {\i Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} (twenty-four hours).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the view that one has only until the end of the halakhic day \u8212- {\i kol hayom} \u8212- {\i hafara} is permitted on {\i Shabbat} even when it serves no {\i Shabbat} purpose, because {\i Shabbat} is the husband\u8217's only and final opportunity. Of course we allow it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the twenty-four-hour view, there is no such urgency. After {\i Shabbat} ends, time still remains \u8212- the full twenty-four hours has not elapsed. Therefore, under that view, {\i hafara} on {\i Shabbat} is permitted only when there is a specific {\i Shabbat} need; otherwise, one should simply wait until {\i Shabbat} is over. Since we rule like the {\i Tanna Kamma} \u8212- the end of the halakhic day \u8212- we also rule that {\i hafara} on {\i Shabbat} is permitted across the board, even without {\i letzorech Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Negligence Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a question about {\i hataras nedarim} specifically: when we permit {\i hatara} on {\i Shabbat} because it is {\i letzorech Shabbat}, does this apply even when the person had ample opportunity to perform {\i hatara} before {\i Shabbat} and simply failed to do so? Or do we deny the {\i Shabbat} leniency when the person was negligent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is vivid: a person vowed on Friday morning that he would not eat meat. He had all of Friday to find a {\i chacham} and dissolve the vow; he did not bother. Now it is {\i Shabbat} and he cannot eat his {\i cholent}. He wishes to seek {\i hataras nedarim} now. On one hand, this is genuinely {\i letzorech Shabbat}. On the other hand, he should have handled it beforehand. The question is whether his prior negligence forfeits his right to the {\i Shabbat} leniency. This parallels discussions among later {\i poskim} regarding {\i shvus d\u8217'shvus b\u8217'makom mitzvah}: some hold that if one was negligent and could have arranged things properly before {\i Shabbat}, no allowance is given on {\i Shabbat} itself; others hold that whatever the cause, the present need must be addressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Negligence Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the question from a real-life precedent: {\i izdakkaku lei rabanan livrei d\u8217'Rav Zutra brei d\u8217'Rav Zeira} \u8212- the rabbis responded to the needs of the son of {\i Rav Zutra}, son of {\i Rav Zeira}, and performed {\i hatara} even for {\i nedarim} that he could have dissolved before {\i Shabbat} but did not. They did not withhold the leniency on account of his prior negligence. The {\i Gemara} notes that this is in fact the dominant ruling among {\i poskim} in analogous cases \u8212- whatever the reason one finds oneself in the situation, the present halachic need must be addressed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hataras Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Expert Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now addresses the question of who may perform {\i hataras nedarim} and under what conditions. {\i Rav Yosef} proposes that when {\i hataras nedarim} is permitted on {\i Shabbat} \u8212- in a case of {\i letzorech Shabbat} \u8212- it may only be performed before a {\i yachid mumcheh}, a single qualified expert. It may not be performed before three {\i hedyotos}, three lay people who together constitute the alternative form of a valid {\i beis din} for {\i hatara} purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requires some background. The basic structure of {\i hataras nedarim} is that it may be performed before either a {\i yachid mumcheh} \u8212- a single recognized expert \u8212- or before three lay people. (The precise meaning of {\i mumcheh} \u8212- what qualifications it requires \u8212- is a question for another discussion.) Most of the cases in {\i aggadata} and {\i halacha} involve a single rabbi, because a qualified rabbi is a {\i mumcheh} and suffices alone. But the option of three lay people also exists. {\i Rav Yosef}\u8217's position is that on {\i Shabbat}, even where {\i hatara} is permitted, one may only use the {\i yachid mumcheh} option \u8212- not the three-{\i hedyotos} option.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Appearance Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason {\i Rav Yosef} gives is {\i mishum d\u8217'michzei k\u8217'dina} \u8212- because three people sitting together to perform {\i hatara} looks like a full {\i beis din}. Even if the underlying act is permitted, it creates the appearance of conducting formal legal proceedings on {\i Shabbat}, which is itself problematic. Therefore, when we extend the {\i Shabbat} leniency, we extend it only in its least court-like form: a single expert. Three people \u8212- even lay people \u8212- is too suggestive of a {\i beis din} sitting in judgment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Refutes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} rejects {\i Rav Yosef}\u8217's position. He argues that given what we know about the leniencies that apply to {\i hataras nedarim}, it simply cannot be said to look like a {\i beis din} at all \u8212- whether it involves one person or three. Since the very nature of {\i hataras nedarim} is manifestly not that of a formal court proceeding, the concern of {\i michzei k\u8217'dina} does not apply, and the three-{\i hedyotos} option remains available even on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Formal Features\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye}\u8217's proof rests on the accumulated leniencies that apply to {\i hataras nedarim}: it may be performed {\i me\u8217'umad} \u8212- while standing; it may be performed {\i balayla} \u8212- at night; and it may be performed {\i b\u8217'krovim} \u8212- by relatives. None of these features are consistent with a {\i beis din}. A {\i beis din} must sit; it must convene in the daytime; its members may not be relatives of the parties or of one another. The fact that {\i hataras nedarim} permits all of these departures from {\i beis din} norms means that no reasonable observer could mistake it for one. The appearance-of-{\i beis-din} concern therefore simply does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceptual Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads directly to a deeper conceptual question: is {\i hataras nedarim} fundamentally an act of a {\i beis din} \u8212- albeit with many procedural exceptions \u8212- or is it something categorically different from a {\i beis din} act altogether?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One could argue that it remains a {\i beis din} act in essence, with certain relaxed procedures. After all, even in {\i Sanhedrin}, a {\i yachid mumcheh} is treated as equivalent to a full {\i beis din} in certain respects. Under this view, the departures from standard {\i beis din} procedure (standing, night, relatives) are explicable as exceptions, and one would need to account for each one. The act itself, however, remains a form of judicial proceeding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively \u8212- and this is the more radical position \u8212- the cumulative weight of these exceptions may prove that {\i hataras nedarim} is not a {\i beis din} act at all. This is particularly compelling with respect to relatives: it is impossible to constitute a {\i beis din} of relatives. Standing and nighttime are merely procedural variations; one could argue those remain within the range of {\i beis din} flexibility. But relatives? A {\i beis din} composed of relatives is no {\i beis din}. Their inclusion in {\i hataras nedarim} suggests that the process belongs to an entirely different category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even if one concludes that it is not a {\i beis din} act, this does not mean it is entirely unrestricted on {\i Shabbat}. Even {\i hafarat nedarim} \u8212- which is certainly not a {\i beis din} act \u8212- is problematic on {\i Shabbat} for the separate reason that it effects a real change in status. {\i Hataras nedarim} similarly effects a change, and therefore remains something that requires specific {\i Shabbat} justification. The key insight is that once we recognize it is not a {\i beis din} act, the three-{\i hedyotos} option is no more problematic on {\i Shabbat} than the {\i yachid mumcheh} option, and {\i Abaye}\u8217's refutation of {\i Rav Yosef} stands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Night/Standing/Relatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now records a tradition in the name of {\i Rav Abba}, who said in the name of {\i Rav Huna} in the name of {\i Rav}: the {\i halacha} is that {\i hataras nedarim} may be performed at night. The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges: is that not already explicit in the {\i mishnah}? After all, the {\i mishnah} permits {\i hafara} on Friday night \u8212- surely it is obvious that vow dissolution can occur at night. The {\i Gemara} therefore corrects the tradition: what {\i Rav} meant was not {\i hafarat nedarim} at night \u8212- that requires no demonstration \u8212- but {\i hataras nedarim} at night. That is the genuine {\i chiddush}: the process before a {\i chacham}, which resembles a {\i beis din} proceeding, may be performed at night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once this correction is made, {\i Rav Abba} turns to {\i Rav Huna} and asks: did {\i Rav} actually say this? {\i Rav Huna} fell silent \u8212- {\i ishtik}. The {\i Gemara} then probes the nature of that silence: was it {\i ishtik} in the sense of \u8220"be quiet, I reject this,\u8221" meaning {\i Rav} did not agree? Or was it {\i shasik} \u8212- a silence of approval? The text leaves the question somewhat ambiguous. Different {\i girsaot} handle this differently, but the overall sense is that {\i Rav}\u8217's agreement with the ruling of nighttime {\i hatara} remains uncertain from this exchange alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standing Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second tradition resolves the question in practice. {\i Rav Ika bar Avin} reported that {\i Rav} himself had performed {\i hataras nedarim} for {\i Rabba} \u8212- in a small chamber of the {\i beis midrash} ({\i kituna d\u8217'vei Rav}), while standing, alone, and at night. Three features in a single act: standing, without the formal quorum of three, and at night. The \u8220"alone\u8221" element, it should be noted, is not necessarily problematic if {\i Rav} qualifies as a {\i yachid mumcheh}; the significant departures from {\i beis din} norms are the standing and the nighttime. This incident establishes that at least {\i Rav}\u8217's practice embodied these leniencies, even if his explicit ruling had been ambiguous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabba} then said in the name of {\i Rav Nachman}, consolidating the {\i halacha} explicitly: {\i nishalin nedarim omeid, yachidi, u\u8217'valayla, u\u8217'v\u8217'Shabbos, u\u8217'v\u8217'krovim} \u8212- {\i hataras nedarim} may be performed while standing, alone, at night, on {\i Shabbat}, and by relatives. The ruling on {\i Shabbat} still operates within the earlier framework \u8212- it appears to refer to cases of {\i letzorech Shabbat}, and the addition that this applies {\i va\u8217'afilu haya lahem pnai mib\u8217'od yom} \u8212- even if they had time before {\i Shabbat} \u8212- confirms that prior negligence does not forfeit the {\i Shabbat} leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam}\u8217's formulation is particularly clear and worth noting. He writes: {\i hakrovim matirin lahatir nedarim, u\u8217'matirin balayla u\u8217'me\u8217'umad, she\u8217'ein hatara zu din} \u8212- relatives may perform {\i hataras nedarim}, and it may be performed at night and while standing, {\i because this release is not din}. The {\i Rambam} does not frame these as exceptions to an otherwise {\i beis din}-like process; he states flatly that {\i hatara} is categorically not {\i din}. This makes the leniencies not anomalies requiring justification but natural consequences of the fundamental nature of {\i hataras nedarim}. The one remaining question \u8212- why then are three people involved at all, if it is not {\i din}? \u8212- is perhaps the easiest thing to ask and the hardest to answer simply, but the {\i Rambam}\u8217's framing makes clear that the {\i requirement} of three does not transform the act into a {\i beis din} proceeding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sitting vs Standing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a challenge from a famous story involving {\i Rabban Gamliel}. The {\i baraita} records that when {\i Rabban Gamliel} performed {\i hataras nedarim}, he dismounted from his donkey, wrapped himself in his {\i tallit}, and sat down \u8212- only then proceeding to release the {\i neder}. This appears to contradict the position that standing is permitted, since {\i Rabban Gamliel} took explicit care to sit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by invoking the dispute over {\i potchin b\u8217'charata}. The question is: what must the {\i chacham} do to release a {\i neder}? One view \u8212- {\i potchin b\u8217'charata} \u8212- holds that it suffices to open with a question of regret: \u8220"Do you regret having made this vow?\u8221" If the person says yes, the {\i chacham} declares {\i mutar lach} and the {\i neder} is dissolved. No elaborate probing of circumstances is required. Another reading \u8212- {\i ein potchin b\u8217'charata} \u8212- holds that simple regret is insufficient. The {\i chacham} must investigate the concrete circumstances surrounding the {\i neder}: what the person was thinking, what he failed to consider, whether had he taken those circumstances into account he would not have made the {\i neder}. This is a deliberate, probing process that uproots the {\i neder} ({\i me\u8217'aker nidra}) through careful analysis ({\i ba\u8217'i iyunei}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the latter view, real deliberation is required \u8212- not as a formal technical requirement for the {\i hatara} to take effect, but because the nature of the act demands that it be given proper weight and focus. {\i Rabban Gamliel}, who held this view, accordingly sat, wrapped himself in his {\i tallit}, and gave the matter his full and serious attention. Not because sitting is a formal {\i din}, but because proper deliberation practically requires it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simple Regret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman}, who ruled that standing is permitted, held the position that simple regret suffices. If all that is needed is for the person to express regret \u8212- {\i are you sorry you made this vow? yes, fine, mutar lach} \u8212- then there is no need for deep deliberation, and therefore no need to sit. One can stand, or even occupy oneself with other things, because the act is so simple and direct that it does not require focused concentration. This is what the {\i Rosh} suggests is the proper context for the earlier account of {\i Chiyya bar Rav}, who performed {\i hataras nedarim} while shooting arrows, and {\i Rabba bar Rav Huna}, who moved around the room without full concentration: those stories belong precisely here, illustrating what it looks like when the {\i potek b\u8217'charata} approach is taken to its logical conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might be troubled by such a picture. Does not trivializing the process of {\i hatara} also trivialize the gravity of making {\i nedarim}? This tension is real, and the subsequent discussion in the {\i Gemara} about the sinfulness of making vows \u8212- even fulfilled ones \u8212- may itself be understood as a corrective: even if the {\i hatara} process is straightforward, the act of having made the {\i neder} at all is not without moral consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashba Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rashba} offers a nuanced position. He acknowledges that {\i potchin b\u8217'charata} is the operative ruling: the {\i chacham} may begin by simply asking whether the person regrets the {\i neder}. If the answer is yes, {\i mutar lach} suffices and no further deliberation is needed. Suppose, however, the person says no \u8212- he does not regret it. The {\i chacham} must then probe for a {\i petach}, a grounds for release based on something the person had not considered at the time. This requires genuine deliberation. Even so, the {\i Rashba} holds that standing remains permitted even in this second phase, because the {\i opening} of the process \u8212- {\i ikar dina} \u8212- was begun while standing; therefore the {\i conclusion} of the process may also be concluded while standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} is unpersuaded. He writes explicitly: {\i v\u8217'lo yaradeti l\u8217'ta\u8217'amo} \u8212- I do not follow his reasoning, and since I do not understand it, I cannot rule accordingly. The {\i Ran}\u8217's position is that once actual deliberation becomes necessary \u8212- once one must truly probe and investigate \u8212- the practical requirement of sitting and focusing applies. Whatever was done standing before does not carry forward to justify standing through a phase of the process that genuinely requires concentration. The mode of the proceeding must match the demands of what is being done at each moment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vow as Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prayer Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} closes with a striking moral teaching occasioned by a real case. {\i Rava} reported to {\i Rav Nachman}: a rabbi who had come from {\i Eretz Yisrael} said that the rabbis had performed {\i hataras nedarim} for the son of {\i Rav Huna bar Avin}, dissolving his {\i neder}, and then said to him: {\i zil u\u8217'vei rachmei a\u8217'nafshecha de\u8217'chatat} \u8212- go and pray for your soul, for you have sinned. This is remarkable. The {\i hatara} was successful; the {\i neder} was dissolved and, retroactively speaking, never truly bound him. Yet the rabbis told him to pray for his soul for having sinned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sinner Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} grounds this in a {\i baraita} of {\i Rabbi Dimmi}, brother of {\i Rav Safra}: {\i kol ha\u8217'noder, af al pi she\u8217'mekayemo, nikra chotei} \u8212- anyone who makes a {\i neder}, even if he fulfills it, is called a sinner. One might note that the {\i Gemara} could have cited the more vivid formulation \u8212- {\i kol ha\u8217'noder ke\u8217'ilu banah bamah}, he who makes a vow is as one who built an {\i illicit altar} \u8212- where the {\i Ran} explains that fulfilling the vow is even worse, analogous to actually sacrificing upon it. But the formulation chosen here makes a distinct point: even the act of making the {\i neder}, independent of fulfillment or violation, is itself sinful. In the case at hand, the {\i neder} was dissolved \u8212- and yet the moral stain of having made it remained. The rabbis were right to tell the young man to pray.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cease Making Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Zvid} then adds a verse in support: {\i ve\u8217'ki techdal lindor lo yihyeh vecha cheit} \u8212- if you cease from making vows, there will be no sin in you. The logical inference is clear: if you do not cease, there is sin \u8212- {\i even if you fulfill} the vow. And with even greater force: if the {\i neder} is dissolved through {\i hatara} and technically never existed in a halachic sense, the underlying act of having made it is still a moral failing that requires {\i tefillah} and seeking forgiveness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qiyyum vs Hafara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} closes with a {\i baraita} that draws an important distinction between {\i qiyyum} \u8212- ratification \u8212- and {\i hafara} \u8212- annulment. If a husband says to his wife: \u8220"Any vows that you make, I do not want you to make them\u8221" \u8212- or \u8220"they are not vows\u8221" \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}: he has said nothing. This is not a valid {\i hafara}. Expressing displeasure is not annulment; declaring that the {\i neder} is not a {\i neder} does not change the fact that it is one. {\i Hafara} requires a formal, active act of annulment with precise language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Qiyyum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposite case yields the opposite conclusion. If the husband says: \u8220"Well done, there is no one like you \u8212- had you not made those vows, I would have made them for you,\u8221" then {\i devarav kayamim} \u8212- his words constitute a valid {\i qiyyum}, a ratification of the vow. For {\i qiyyum}, even implicit expressions of approval suffice. Indeed, as the {\i Ran} notes, we will see later in the {\i Gemara} that {\i qiyyum} does not even require verbal expression at all \u8212- if in his heart the husband desired the vows, that alone constitutes {\i qiyyum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Formal Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast between {\i qiyyum} and {\i hafara} thus reflects a deep structural asymmetry. {\i Qiyyum} requires no formal language; it follows from will and desire. {\i Hafara} requires specific, formal verbalization \u8212- not merely an expression of sentiment or wish, but the actual performative act of annulment. To say \u8220"I don\u8217't want these vows\u8221" or \u8220"these are not real vows\u8221" conveys a state of mind, but it does not perform the act. The language of {\i hafara} must be language {\i of} annulling, not language {\i about} annulling. This distinction \u8212- between performing an act through speech and describing one\u8217's attitude through speech \u8212- is what separates effective {\i hafara} from words that, whatever they may express, accomplish nothing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 78\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hafara & Hatara Basics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Judicial Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens by revisiting the foundational distinction between {\i hafara} \u8212- annulment of a vow by a husband \u8212- and {\i hatara} \u8212- annulment by a sage. Earlier discussion established that the panel performing {\i hatara} shares certain formal features with a {\i beit din}: it requires either three individuals or a single {\i mumche}, an expert of sufficient standing. Yet the {\i Gemara} makes clear that this body does not function as a proper {\i beit din} in the adjudicating sense. It may convene at night, its members may stand rather than sit, and relatives ({\i krovim}) are not disqualified \u8212- all departures from ordinary judicial procedure. This non-judicial character is not a peripheral detail; it is central to understanding several practical {\i halachos} that follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Permissibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One immediate implication of the non-judicial status of {\i hafara} and {\i hatara} concerns performance on {\i Shabbos}. {\i Hafara} by a husband may always be performed on {\i Shabbos}, because the husband has only until the end of the day to annul \u8212- there is therefore always an immediate need ({\i tzorech}), and when there is a need it does not matter by what formal arrangement it is performed. {\i Hatara} may similarly be performed on {\i Shabbos} when it is needed for the sake of {\i Shabbos} itself ({\i letzorech Shabbos}). Moreover, precisely because the act of {\i hatara} is not a {\i maaseh beit din} in the formal judicial sense, there is no prohibition on performing it with three individuals rather than one, since even when three people act together they are not constituting a formal {\i beit din}. The non-judicial character thus dispenses with the concern that would otherwise make such an act on {\i Shabbos} problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standard Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to the appropriate language for annulment, particularly on {\i Shabbos}. A man should not say to his wife on {\i Shabbos} the standard weekday formula \u8212- {\i mufar lichi}, {\i batul lichi}, \u8220"it is annulled to you, it is void to you\u8221" \u8212- as one would ordinarily say during the week. Rather, he should say to her: {\i tli ve-ochli}, {\i tli ve-shtei} \u8212- \u8220"take and eat, take and drink\u8221" \u8212- and the {\i neder} is automatically void ({\i veneder batel meilav}). This is instructive on two levels. First, it establishes what the standard, direct language of annulment is. Second, it demonstrates that even in cases where a more direct formulation would be appropriate, indirect language can suffice when circumstances call for it \u8212- in this case, the concern about performing an overtly legal verbal act on {\i Shabbos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan adds a critical qualification: {\i vetzarich sheyivatel belibo} \u8212- it is not sufficient to utter the indirect words alone; the husband must simultaneously hold in his heart an intention to annul ({\i bitul belibo}). The words spoken need not themselves be words of {\i bitul}, but the inner intention must be genuinely present. This formulation is reminiscent of the {\i bitul} of {\i chametz} before Pesach, where one who cannot perform {\i biur chametz} may instead annul it in the heart ({\i mevatel belibo vedaio}), rendering it ownerless. The parallel invites reflection, though the cases are distinct. Critically, as the {\i Ran} emphasizes and as the {\i Gemara} will clarify more explicitly, purely internal {\i bitul} without any accompanying verbal act would be insufficient. What is required is a combination: words that, while not explicit declarations of annulment, implicitly convey it \u8212- together with genuine inner intention. Neither element alone is adequate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} presents an explicit dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel on this question. Beit Shammai holds that on {\i Shabbos}, a husband should annul {\i belibo} \u8212- with inner intention accompanied by indirect language, as described above \u8212- but that on a weekday he must be more explicit, articulating the direct formula {\i mufar lichi}, {\i batul lichi}. The {\i Ran} clarifies that even Beit Shammai does not mean the husband annuls by thought alone on {\i Shabbos}; rather, the indirect verbal act combined with inner intention, as described in the previous passage, is what Beit Shammai permits when circumstances prevent direct articulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel holds that the same indirect approach suffices both on {\i Shabbos} and during the weekday: {\i echad ze ve-echad ze mevatel belibo ve-ein tzarich lehotzi bisfasav} \u8212- in both cases he annuls in his heart without needing to articulate the formula explicitly. On its face this sounds as though Beit Hillel requires no verbal act whatsoever, only internal intention. Yet as the {\i Ran} insists, and as a later passage in the {\i Gemara} makes clear, pure internal {\i bitul} without any verbal component would not be effective. Beit Hillel\u8217's position is therefore that the indirect verbal act is always sufficient \u8212- it is not a special dispensation limited to {\i Shabbos} \u8212- while Beit Shammai would prefer direct articulation when possible and treats the indirect form as an accommodation to {\i Shabbos} rather than an equally valid alternative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heart Intent Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question of whether Beit Shammai would validate indirect annulment on a weekday after the fact ({\i bedi\u8217'avad}). It is reasonable to conclude that Beit Shammai would \u8212- their position is that direct language is preferred when there is no obstacle, not that indirect language is categorically invalid. Beit Hillel, by contrast, simply does not distinguish between the two modes: if it works, it works, regardless of when and under what circumstances it is employed. What is clear from both positions is that there is an irreducible combination at work: implicit verbal expression and genuine inner intention operating together. And of course, if one does say {\i mufar lichi} explicitly, that simultaneously fulfills whatever inner intention is required \u8212- the explicit formula carries the inner meaning by definition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meifer vs.\u160 ?Mevatel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Checking the {\i Rambam} on this point reveals a striking distinction that requires closer examination. In {\i Hilchos Nedarim} 13:7 ({\i yud gimel}, {\i zayin}), the {\i Rambam} rules as follows: a husband who performs {\i hafara} of his wife\u8217's or daughter\u8217's vow must articulate it verbally \u8212- if he performs {\i hafara} only in his heart, it is not effective. But one who performs {\i bitul} \u8212- the annulment from a different angle \u8212- does not need to articulate it verbally; {\i bitul belibo} alone suffices, and the {\i neder} is thereby void. The {\i Rambam} in the passage there appears to treat {\i meifer} and {\i mevatel} as two distinct processes with differing requirements. The standard formula often cited \u8212- {\i mufar lichi}, {\i batul lichi} \u8212- may in fact be the {\i Rambam}\u8217's way of referring to two separate acts, one of {\i hafara} and one of {\i bitul}, with different formal requirements for each. This distinction deserves further analysis, but at minimum the {\i Rambam} agrees with the {\i Ran} that some combination of verbal act and inner intention is operative, even if he parses the components differently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Language Specificity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit vs.\u160 ?Implicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} raises a pointed question. Earlier in the {\i Gemara}, a case was discussed in which a husband says to his wife: {\i konam, nedarim shetiduri, i efshi shetiduri} \u8212- \u8220"I do not wish you to make such vows\u8221" \u8212- or {\i ein ze neder} \u8212- \u8220"this is not a vow.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} ruled that such language is ineffective: {\i lo amar klum}, he has said nothing of consequence. But if the {\i Ran} just established that indirect language is sufficient provided the husband has {\i bitul belibo}, why did that earlier formulation fail? After all, it too is a form of indirect expression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Negation Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution lies not in the directness of the language per se but in whether the language is {\i implicitly} an act of annulment at all. Consider the phrase \u8220"go ahead and eat it.\u8221" If a wife has made a vow forbidding herself from eating something and her husband tells her \u8220"go ahead and eat it,\u8221" there is only one coherent way to understand that instruction: the husband must have annulled the vow, because otherwise his instruction would be meaningless. The implication of annulment is thus inherent in and inseparable from the statement. By contrast, if the husband says \u8220"this is not a vow,\u8221" he is making a factual claim \u8212- and a false one at that, since it plainly is a vow. He is not saying \u8220"I am making it not a vow\u8221" or \u8220"I am annulling it\u8221"; he is asserting a state of affairs that does not exist. Similarly, \u8220"I do not wish you to make vows\u8221" is a statement about his preferences, not an act of annulment. It leaves entirely open whether he intends to annul or merely to express displeasure. A statement that communicates annulment implicitly must actually communicate annulment \u8212- not every indirect locution accomplishes that. The crucial requirement, therefore, is not merely that the language be indirect but that the indirect language unambiguously implies the act of annulment. A statement of factual negation or personal preference does not clear that bar.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wrong Language Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Swapping Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan teaches: {\i chacham she-amar bi-leshon ba\u8217'al u-va\u8217'al she-amar bi-leshon chacham lo amar klum} \u8212- if a sage employs the language appropriate to a husband, or a husband employs the language appropriate to a sage, neither has accomplished anything. The case contemplated is not one of confusion about which process to perform; rather, it is a case where the actor knows what process he intends but uses the wrong terminology. A sage conducting a {\i hatara} who says {\i mufar lach} instead of the appropriate language of the sage has not effected {\i hatara}. A husband performing {\i hafara} who says {\i mutar lach} has not effected {\i hafara}. This ruling emphasizes that verbal articulation is not merely instrumental \u8212- it is constitutive of the act. Wanting to do the right thing while saying the wrong thing is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder whether the principle of {\i yad la-neder} \u8212- where language that partially and ambiguously gestures toward a neder is nonetheless treated as binding \u8212- could apply here in reverse, validating an act whose language approximates but does not match the required formula. The answer appears to be no, precisely because the husband and the sage are performing fundamentally different processes. The {\i ba\u8217'al}\u8217's act of {\i hafara} operates going forward \u8212- it dissolves an existing vow from the moment of annulment onward. The {\i chacham}\u8217's act of {\i hatara} operates retroactively \u8212- it treats the vow as though it never existed. These are not merely formally different; they are conceptually opposed. Using the other\u8217's language does not create an approximate version of one\u8217's own process; it describes the other process entirely. There is no room for a {\i yad} construction when the language accurately describes something real, just the wrong thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} grounds this in the {\i pasuk} from {\i Mattos}: {\i zeh hadavar asher tzivah Hashem \u8212- ish ki yidor neder} \u8212- \u8220"this is the matter that God commanded \u8212- a man who takes a vow.\u8221" The phrase {\i zeh hadavar} \u8212- \u8220"this is the matter\u8221" \u8212- is read restrictively: {\i chacham matir ve-ein ba\u8217'al matir}, only the {\i chacham} performs {\i hatara}, not the husband. Now, one might have argued the contrary by a {\i kal va-chomer}: if a {\i chacham}, who lacks the power of {\i hafara}, can nevertheless perform {\i hatara}, then surely a {\i ba\u8217'al}, who possesses the greater power of {\i hafara}, should a fortiori be able to perform {\i hatara} as well. It is something of an irony \u8212- the husband\u8217's unique power is invoked as the basis for expanding his authority into the sage\u8217's domain. Nevertheless, {\i talmud lomar zeh hadavar}: no, only the {\i chacham} performs {\i hatara}, and not the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafara Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraisa} derives the mirror proposition. {\i Zeh hadavar} \u8212- again read restrictively \u8212- establishes that {\i ba\u8217'al meifer ve-ein chacham meifer}: only the husband can perform {\i hafara}, and the {\i chacham} cannot. Here too a {\i kal va-chomer} suggests itself in the other direction: if the husband, who lacks the power of {\i hatara}, can nevertheless perform {\i hafara}, then surely the {\i chacham}, who possesses the power of {\i hatara}, should a fortiori be able to perform {\i hafara}. Again, {\i talmud lomar zeh hadavar}: no. Each authority is confined to its own domain. The {\i Gemara} notes that these derivations do not prove quite as much as Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's rule might require \u8212- they establish that the actual {\i processes} are exclusive, not necessarily that using the other\u8217's language while performing one\u8217's own process would fail. But the broader point stands: since the two processes are conceptually distinct and the {\i devar} that defines each is specific to its actor, using the language that describes the other\u8217's process cannot constitute one\u8217's own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Language Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a practical question: what exactly is the language of the {\i chacham}? The language of the {\i ba\u8217'al} is explicit in the {\i Torah} \u8212- {\i va-im hafer yafer osah me-ishah}, the language of {\i hafara} belongs to the husband. But where does the {\i Torah} specify that a {\i chacham} says {\i mutar lach}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} addresses this at the end of 77b. His first answer draws from the verse {\i lo yachel devaro} \u8212- \u8220"he shall not profane his word.\u8221" From here we learn the role of the {\i chacham}: others can render his word {\i chullin}, ordinary and non-binding. Since {\i chullin} is the category of the non-sanctified and unrestricted, the term {\i mutar} \u8212- permissible \u8212- is the apt language for what the {\i chacham} does. This answer, as the {\i Ran} himself concedes, is not entirely persuasive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the very top of 78b, however, the {\i Ran} cites the {\i Yerushalmi} for a more compelling resolution. The {\i Yerushalmi} records: {\i ha-ba\u8217'al omer mufar lach, ve-ha-zaken omer ein kan neder ein kan shvu\u8217'a} \u8212- the husband declares \u8220"it is annulled to you,\u8221" while the elder declares \u8220"there is no vow here, there is no oath here.\u8221" This formulation clarifies the conceptual logic. {\i Mufar} \u8212- from {\i meifer} \u8212- means dissolved going forward: the vow existed and is now annulled from this point onward. {\i Mutar} \u8212- and equivalently {\i ein kan neder} \u8212- means it was always permissible; there never was a binding vow. This is the nature of the {\i chacham}\u8217's act: {\i hatara} renders the vow as though it never came into existence. And this is precisely why the husband cannot use that language \u8212- to say {\i ein kan neder} or {\i mutar lach} is to claim that the vow never existed, but the husband\u8217's role presupposes that a vow did exist and is now being dissolved prospectively. He cannot retroactively undo its existence; he can only cut off its future effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis casts light on the {\i nusach} of {\i hataras nedarim} recited on {\i erev Rosh Hashanah}. The formula includes {\i hakol mutar lach, hakol sharui lach} \u8212- {\i mutar} and its Aramaic equivalent {\i sharui}, both indicating that the thing was always permissible. It also includes {\i hakol mechulin lach}, which carries a somewhat different connotation \u8212- {\i mechila} suggests erasure of something that existed, somewhat closer in spirit to {\i hafara}. Whether {\i mechulin} was always part of the original {\i nusach} may be worth investigating. And crucially, the formula also includes {\i ein kan lo neder ve-lo shvu\u8217'a ve-lo nezirus ve-lo cherem ve-lo issur ve-lo konam} \u8212- there is no vow here, no oath, no {\i nezirus}, no {\i cherem}, no prohibition, no {\i konam}. All of these are the language of the {\i chacham}: retroactive annulment treating the original commitment as though it never took legal hold.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Laypeople Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeh HaDavar Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraisa} continues with a {\i gezeira shava} \u8212- a textual analogy based on a shared phrase. The phrase {\i zeh hadavar} appears in {\i parshas Mattos} in the context of {\i nedarim}, and it appears again elsewhere in the {\i Torah} in the context of {\i shchutei chutz} \u8212- the prohibition against slaughtering a consecrated animal outside the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. The {\i baraisa} proposes linking them: just as by {\i shchutei chutz} the {\i Torah} states {\i daber el Aharon ve-el banav ve-el kol b\u8217'nei Yisrael} \u8212- applying the law to Aharon, his sons, and all of Israel \u8212- so too, {\i parshas nedarim} applies to all of Israel, not only to {\i rashei ha-matos}. And conversely, just as {\i parshas nedarim} emphasizes {\i rashei ha-matos} \u8212- heads of the tribes \u8212- so too {\i shchutei chutz} must be understood to involve {\i rashei ha-matos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Hedyotos Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does it mean for {\i parshas nedarim} to extend to {\i kol Yisrael} beyond {\i rashei ha-matos}? Rav Acha bar Yaakov explains: {\i lehachshir shlosha hedyotos} \u8212- to validate three laypeople as a qualified panel for {\i hataras nedarim}. The {\i parsha} focuses on {\i rashei ha-matos}, which establishes that a {\i yachid mumche} \u8212- a single qualified individual of sufficient stature \u8212- may annul vows. The inclusion of {\i kol Yisrael} then signals a second, broader category: ordinary people, provided there are three of them. The logic of choosing three, as the {\i Ran} explains, is that once we know one individual is not enough in this second category, the next coherent institutional form is that of a {\i beit din}, which requires three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this conception of a {\i beit din} deserves careful definition. The body that performs {\i hataras nedarim} is not a {\i beit din} in the adjudicating sense \u8212- it may convene at night, it may include relatives, its members need not be seated. How then is it meaningful to call it a {\i beit din}? Rav Soloveitchik observed in a number of contexts that {\i beit din} does not always function as an adjudicating body. The {\i Sanhedrin} that goes out in the case of {\i eglah arufah} and declares {\i yadeinu lo shafchu es hadam hazeh} is not adjudicating anything \u8212- it is representing the Jewish people. Similarly, when {\i beit din} sanctifies the new month or proclaims a leap year, it acts not as a court but as a representative leadership body. {\i Rashei ha-matos} is itself a leadership role, not a juridical one. The institution of {\i beit din}, understood as a representative leadership body rather than a judicial one, is the natural parallel. This may also explain why it is three specifically: not because three {\i dayanim} are needed, but because three is the constitutive number for a recognized communal body, and the {\i beit din} in its representative \u8212- as distinct from its adjudicating \u8212- capacity provides that form. The {\i Torah} elsewhere refers to the {\i beit din} using different terms at different points \u8212- {\i zekenim}, {\i shoftim}, {\i Elohim} \u8212- and it is the {\i zekenim} formulation, connoting communal leadership rather than strict adjudication, that applies here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shchutei Chutz Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i gezeira shava}: what role do {\i rashei ha-matos} play in the context of {\i shchutei chutz}? Rav Sheshet explains: {\i lomar she-yesh she\u8217'eila b\u8217'hekdesh} \u8212- to teach that {\i rashei ha-matos} can perform {\i she\u8217'eila} regarding {\i hekdesh}, that is, a {\i chacham} can annul a consecration ({\i hekdesh}) just as he can annul a {\i neder}. This creates a significant practical opening: if someone slaughtered a consecrated animal outside the {\i Beis HaMikdash} and is thereby liable to {\i kareis}, he may go to a {\i chacham} and argue that had he been aware of certain circumstances he would never have consecrated the animal. The {\i chacham} performs {\i hatara} on the original act of {\i hekdesh}, which retroactively undoes the consecration, rendering the animal ordinary ({\i chullin}). The slaughter then turns out to have been of an unconsecrated animal, and no {\i aveira} was committed at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, however, is the position of Beit Hillel, which holds that {\i she\u8217'eila} \u8212- the process of retroactive annulment on grounds of error \u8212- applies to {\i hekdesh}. Beit Shammai disagrees: {\i ein she\u8217'eila b\u8217'hekdesh}. Even if one consecrated an animal in error, the {\i hekdesh} is binding. Since the entire mechanism of {\i hatara} works by reclassifying the original act as having been made in error ({\i b\u8217'to\u8217'us}), and Beit Shammai holds that even {\i hekdesh b\u8217'to\u8217'us} is fully valid, Beit Shammai denies that {\i rashei ha-matos} play any role by {\i shchutei chutz}. Consequently, for Beit Shammai the {\i gezeira shava} linking {\i parshas nedarim} and {\i shchutei chutz} does not hold \u8212- they do not derive the law of three {\i hedyotos} from this source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Beit Shammai, then, where do three {\i hedyotos} come from? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i nafka lehu mi-deRav Assi bar Nasn} \u8212- from the teaching of Rav Assi bar Nasn. The {\i pasuk} states: {\i vayedabeir Moshe es moadei Hashem el b\u8217'nei Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"and Moshe spoke the appointed times of God to the children of Israel.\u8221" A {\i baraisa} records a dispute. Rabbi Yosi HaGlili says: the {\i pasuk} speaks of the {\i moadim} and does not mention {\i Shabbos Bereishis} alongside them \u8212- which is puzzling, since in {\i parshas Emor} where the {\i moadim} are listed, {\i Shabbos} appears first, {\i sheshes yamim} and so on. Rebbi ({\i Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi}) says: the {\i pasuk} speaks of the {\i moadim} and does not mention {\i parshas nedarim} alongside them \u8212- which is equally puzzling, given that {\i parshas nedarim} in {\i Mattos} follows immediately after the {\i moadim} of {\i Pinchas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi bar Nasn was troubled by this {\i baraisa} and went to find Rav Sheshet to ask him about it. He traveled first to Naharda\u8217'ah and, not finding Rav Sheshet there, followed him to Mechuza.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moadim Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet resolved the difficulty. The {\i baraisa} is not making a claim about what appears adjacent in the text; it is drawing a {\i halachic} distinction. {\i Moadei Hashem tzrichim kiddush beit din} \u8212- the appointed times require sanctification by {\i beit din}. The {\i moadim} are proclaimed through {\i kiddush hachodesh}, the {\i beit din}\u8217's sanctification of the new moon. {\i Shabbos Bereishis ein tzricha kiddush beit din} \u8212- {\i Shabbos} requires no such sanctification; it has its sanctity independently, without any action by {\i beit din}. The verse {\i asher tikre\u8217'u osam bemo\u8217'adam} \u8212- \u8220"that you shall proclaim in their appointed time\u8221" \u8212- applies only to those occasions whose timing is declared by {\i beit din}, not to {\i Shabbos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly \u8212- and this is the key point for our topic \u8212- {\i moadei Hashem tzrichim mumche}: the {\i moadim} require an expert panel, specifically a {\i beit din} of {\i mumchim}, for the sanctification of the new moon. {\i Ve-ein parshas nedarim tzricha mumche}: but {\i parshas nedarim} \u8212- the process of {\i hataras nedarim} \u8212- does not require a {\i mumche}-level panel. The word {\i eileh} \u8212- \u8220"these\u8221" \u8212- in {\i eileh moadei Hashem asher tikre\u8217'u osam} is restrictive: only {\i these} require a {\i beit din} of {\i mumchim} for their proclamation. {\i Shabbos} does not, and {\i parshas nedarim} does not. From here Beit Shammai derives that {\i hataras nedarim} is valid even when performed by {\i hedyotos}, laypeople without expert credentials.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises the obvious difficulty: but {\i parshas nedarim} says {\i rashei ha-matos}, which implies people of stature and expertise \u8212- so how can laypeople suffice? The answer, attributed to Rav Chisda and alternatively to Rabbi Yochanan: {\i b\u8217'yachid mumche} \u8212- \u8203 ?{\i rashei ha-matos} refers to the case of a {\i yachid mumche}, a single individual of sufficient standing. When it is a {\i yachid}, he must be a {\i mumche}. But the separate teaching about {\i kol Yisrael} \u8212- and for Beit Shammai, the teaching from {\i eileh} \u8212- establishes the alternative: three {\i hedyotos} without individual expert status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mumche Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mumche Relativity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the critical question of who qualifies as a {\i mumche} for the purpose of a {\i yachid} performing {\i hataras nedarim}. One might suppose that the {\i mumche} required here is parallel to the {\i mumche} required for {\i kiddush hachodesh} \u8212- a member of a {\i beit din} whose authority derives from an unbroken chain of {\i semicha} going back to Moshe Rabbeinu. On that reading, no {\i yachid mumche} could perform {\i hataras nedarim} today, since the chain of {\i semicha} has been severed and such a {\i beit din} no longer exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam}, however, rejects this interpretation. In {\i perek} 12 of {\i Hilchos Shevuos}, the {\i Rambam} rules that any {\i rav muvhak} \u8212- a distinguished authority \u8212- may perform {\i hataras nedarim} as a {\i yachid}. The {\i mumche} required here is not the same {\i mumche} as the one required for {\i kiddush hachodesh}. The power to sanctify the new month derives from a specific authority transmitted from Moshe Rabbeinu through an unbroken chain; the power to annul vows as a {\i yachid} derives from something different \u8212- expertise, knowledge, standing in the realm of {\i halacha} \u8212- and that kind of {\i mumche} exists in every generation. There is no requirement of {\i semicha} in the technical genealogical sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knowledge Minimum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what exactly is the knowledge threshold, and does it apply differently to a panel of three than to a {\i yachid}? Here one must distinguish between the requirements for a {\i yachid mumche} and the minimum competence required even for a {\i beit din} of three. The {\i Ran} asks pointedly: if laypeople suffice, why can one not convene three people who are entirely ignorant of the laws of {\i nedarim}? The answer, which the {\i Ran} derives from other passages in the {\i Gemara} \u8212- discussed earlier in the tractate \u8212- is that some minimum knowledge is required even from a panel of three. This is not a formal credential requirement but a functional one: if the members of the panel do not know what they are doing, they are not performing {\i hataras nedarim} at all. The act of {\i hatara} requires analysis \u8212- identifying a {\i petach} or a {\i charata}, understanding why the vow would not have been made had certain circumstances been known, and declaring the vow dissolved on that basis. A panel that mechanically recites words without any comprehension of the process is not conducting {\i hatara}; it is performing a meaningless verbal exercise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical minimum for a panel of three is therefore that at least one member must genuinely understand the process and be capable of explaining it to the others. The other two must at minimum be able to understand the explanation when it is given \u8212- they need not be {\i gamir}, knowing the law independently, but they must be {\i savir}, capable of following reasoning and understanding the substance of what they are affirming. One must know and explain; the others must be able to understand. Below that threshold, the panel\u8217's declaration is without legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gadol HaDor Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} takes a stronger position regarding the {\i yachid mumche}. Citing {\i Rabbeinu Shmuel} in {\i perek Yayin Nesech}, the {\i Ran} notes that a {\i yachid mumche} must be {\i baki b\u8217'Gemara}, a thorough expert, {\i klomar rav muvhak} \u8212- that is, a distinguished rabbinic authority. But the {\i Ran} presses further. If being a {\i mumche} means simply possessing expert knowledge of the relevant laws, why does a {\i Gemara} elsewhere entertain the possibility that Rav Huna might not have the standing to annul a {\i neder} as a {\i yachid}? Rav Huna was certainly a great scholar; if expertise alone sufficed, he would plainly qualify. The {\i Ran} concludes from this that {\i mumche} for the purpose of {\i yachid} annulment requires more than expert knowledge. The {\i Torah} says {\i rashei ha-matos} \u8212- the heads of the tribes \u8212- and this implies that the annulment of vows was entrusted to those who are {\i yoshvim u-frukkim she-ba-dor}: the preeminent figures of their generation. It is not enough to be well-versed in the laws; one must be counted among the {\i gedolim} of the generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This produces a three-tiered analysis. The first possibility \u8212- that one must have {\i semicha} traceable to Moshe Rabbeinu, like the {\i beit din} of {\i kiddush hachodesh} \u8212- is rejected; that standard would make the role practically impossible and is not what the {\i Torah} intends here. The second possibility \u8212- that any expert scholar suffices \u8212- is also rejected by the {\i Ran}, since that would not explain the {\i Gemara}\u8217's doubt about Rav Huna. The correct standard, according to the {\i Ran}, is a relative one: the {\i yachid mumche} must be the preeminent rabbinic authority of his generation, the {\i gadol ha-dor} in a real sense. This is a demanding but not impossible standard. Every generation has its {\i gedolim}, and among them, those at the very top are the ones who possess the standing to annul vows as a {\i yachid}. It is not an absolute metric but a generational one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modern Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh Hashanah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis gives rise to a serious practical question about the widespread custom of {\i hataras nedarim} performed on {\i erev Rosh Hashanah}. The ceremony is conducted publicly, with groups of people serving as the panel before whom the petitioner declares nullification of vows made over the past year. Often the participants \u8212- both those serving as the {\i beit din} and those seeking {\i hatara} \u8212- have limited familiarity with the relevant laws and proceedings. Given the standards just established \u8212- that even a panel of three requires at minimum one member who genuinely understands the process, with the others capable of following the explanation \u8212- it is legitimate to ask whether the Rosh Hashanah ceremony typically meets halachic requirements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Petach vs.\u160 ?Charata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ceremony as commonly practiced does not proceed through the formal {\i petach} model of {\i hatara}, in which the {\i chacham} identifies a specific new consideration that, had the person known it at the time of making the vow, would have caused him not to take it on at all \u8212- thereby establishing that the vow was made in error. Instead, the Rosh Hashanah ceremony operates primarily on the model of {\i charata} \u8212- remorse \u8212- where the petitioner expresses regret over vows made, and the panel affirms the nullification on that basis. There is a question among {\i poskim} as to whether the Rosh Hashanah practice constitutes a genuine {\i hataras nedarim} according to the stricter understanding, or whether it functions primarily as a {\i mesirat moda\u8217'ah} \u8212- a declaration of intent for the future \u8212- signaling that vows one may inadvertently take in the coming year should not be considered binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i charata} alone suffices without going through the {\i petach} process, and if the {\i charata} model requires a less intensive analytical engagement, one might argue that the bar of {\i gamir ve-savir} is lower in that context, and that the ceremony thus has greater validity than the stricter analysis might suggest. Nevertheless, the concern remains that participants who do not understand the words being recited \u8212- or who recite them in a language they do not comprehend \u8212- are not meaningfully engaged in any process at all. The correct practice is for the words to be said in whatever language the participant understands. The broader question of whether the Rosh Hashanah ceremony reliably accomplishes genuine {\i hataras nedarim} is, by honest assessment, an open and serious one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 79\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mumcheh & Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatarat Nedarim Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin today\u8217's {\i daf} at the bottom of 78b, in the middle of a discussion concerning {\i hatarat nedarim}. The Gemara has established that {\i hatarat nedarim} \u8212- the annulment of vows \u8212- may be performed either by a {\i yachid mumcheh}, a single expert authority, or by a court of three laypeople.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defining Mumcheh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The precise definition of {\i mumcheh} is itself a matter of dispute. One view holds that it requires {\i semicha} from Moshe Rabbeinu \u8212- a chain of ordination reaching back to Sinai \u8212- a standard that effectively renders the category inapplicable in post-Talmudic times. A second view requires an extraordinarily high level of knowledge and brilliance, a threshold so demanding that even some {\i Amoraim} may not have attained it. The third approach, which appears to be the inclination of the Ran, is that {\i mumcheh} simply means {\i rashei hamatot} \u8212- someone who stands among the distinguished Torah authorities of his generation. On this view, every generation has its {\i mumchehim}; it is not an exclusive club of five, but neither is it open to all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shulchan Aruch Mumcheh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Shulchan Aruch}\u8217's position appears to set an even more accessible standard. It initially frames {\i mumcheh} in terms of {\i gimur vesavir} \u8212- full mastery and comprehension of the relevant {\i halachot} of {\i nedarim} \u8212- suggesting that genuine expertise in this area suffices. Interestingly, however, the {\i Shulchan Aruch} adds that in the present day, no one qualifies as a {\i mumcheh}. The apparent tension between these two statements \u8212- a relatively modest theoretical threshold paired with the practical conclusion that no one meets it \u8212- is not easily resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Members\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i hatarat nedarim} is performed by a court of three, the question arises as to the required qualifications of each member. All authorities agree that the members cannot be entirely ignorant of what is transpiring; each must be sufficiently engaged to meaningfully participate in the process. The intuitive position, therefore, is that at least one member must possess {\i gimur vesavir} \u8212- genuine mastery \u8212- while the other two need only {\i savir}, the capacity to understand what is explained to them. On this model, one person truly knows the material, explains it to the others, and they comprehend it well enough to function as legitimate members of the {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Savir Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here too, however, the {\i Shulchan Aruch} introduces a surprising ruling: it appears to hold that all three members need only meet the {\i savir} standard. This raises an obvious difficulty \u8212- if all three are merely {\i savir}, who is doing the explaining? Presumably some fourth party, not a formal member of the {\i beit din}, would need to provide the necessary background. The {\i Shulchan Aruch} does not resolve this, but the ruling reflects a notably permissive approach to the qualifications required for {\i hatarat nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Chanina Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence Indicates Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Picking up approximately five lines down on 78b, the Gemara records a striking interpretation attributed to Rabbi Chanina. The Torah states that if a husband remains silent for a day upon hearing his wife\u8217's vow, the vow is established \u8212- {\i yekimenu}, it becomes {\i hakamah}, and he loses the power to annul. Rabbi Chanina proposes a radical reinterpretation of this rule. The reason silence constitutes {\i hakamah}, he argues, is not the silence itself but rather what the silence indicates. If a husband is quiet for an entire day without annulling the vow, we infer that he was genuinely accepting of it \u8212- he was effectively ratifying the {\i neder} through his inaction. The {\i pasuk}, on this reading, is not establishing a categorical rule about silence but rather a presumption about what silence reveals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spite Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretive move opens the door to significant qualification. If the husband is silent not because he accepts the vow but for some other reason \u8212- say, to cause his wife anxiety, to manipulate her emotionally, to let her remain uncertain about whether the {\i neder} will stand \u8212- then his silence does not indicate acceptance at all. He has his own agenda, entirely unrelated to {\i hakamah}. In such a case, argues Rabbi Chanina, the silence should not be treated as {\i hakamah}, and the husband\u8217's ability to perform {\i hafarah} should remain intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heifer After 10 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina follows this reasoning to its conclusion: even after ten days have passed, if the silence was clearly motivated by spite or manipulation rather than genuine acceptance, the husband retains the right to perform {\i hafarah}. The operative concept is {\i yom shamo} \u8212- the day is defined by what the silence indicates. Where silence plainly does not indicate acceptance, the clock does not run on the husband\u8217's ability to annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this position from a Baraita that enumerates the circumstances under which a father regains control over his daughter\u8217's vows after the husband\u8217's involvement. The Baraita states: {\i eimatai amru met habaal nishkena reshut la\u8217'av} \u8212- when does the father regain authority? When the husband neither heard the vow, or heard it and remained silent ({\i shama veshatak}), or heard it and annulled it and then died on that very day. By contrast, if the husband was {\i mekayem} \u8212- actively affirming the vow \u8212- or was silent and then died after 24 hours, the father cannot annul. Rava notes that this Baraita appears to make no distinction based on the reason for the silence. Any silence sustained past the end of the {\i halachic} day appears to constitute {\i hakamah} regardless of intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 24 Hour Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's logic is pointed: the Baraita simply states that silence beyond the day is {\i hakamah}, without qualifying whether the silence was motivated by acceptance or by spite. If Rabbi Chanina were correct that spite-motivated silence never constitutes {\i hakamah}, one would expect the Baraita to distinguish between the two cases. Its failure to do so seems to imply that all silence, whatever its motivation, yields {\i hakamah} by the end of the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shatak Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds with a distinction. When the Baraita speaks of {\i shatak} \u8212- of silence \u8212- it is specifically referring to silence undertaken with the intent of {\i kiyum}, of ratifying the vow. That is the ordinary case: a husband who is making up his mind, uncertain whether to annul, and lets the day pass without acting. That silence constitutes {\i hakamah} by the end of the day. The Baraita was never addressing the exceptional case of silence motivated by spite. Rava\u8217's challenge therefore does not compel Rabbi Chanina\u8217's defeat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presses further. If {\i shatak al menat lekayem} \u8212- silence specifically intended as {\i kiyum} \u8212- is the case under discussion, that is effectively identical to an explicit verbal {\i kiyum}. Why distinguish it from the Baraita\u8217's earlier phrase {\i shama vekiyem}, where the husband actively ratified the vow? The Gemara\u8217's answer, as explained by the Ran, is that there are two meaningfully distinct scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Two Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran explains that {\i hakamah} does not require verbalization. A husband who, at a given moment, genuinely and actively desires to be {\i mekayem} the vow right now \u8212- even without saying a word \u8212- has performed a valid {\i kiyum} immediately. His silence in that instant, if it is apparent that this is his intent, is sufficient. By contrast, if the husband is in a state of deliberation \u8212- thinking about doing {\i kiyum} at some later point, not yet resolved \u8212- then we cannot credit him with an immediate {\i kiyum}, because his intent is not yet evident. In that case, it is only the passage of the full {\i halachic} day without {\i hafarah} that resolves the ambiguity and renders the silence meaningful as {\i hakamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus {\i shama vekiyem}, where the husband actively ratifies, and {\i shatak al menat lekayem stam} \u8212- neutral deliberative silence \u8212- are not identical: the first works immediately, the second requires the full day. The classic ruling that silence until the end of the day constitutes {\i hakamah} addresses only the intermediate {\i stam} case, where the husband is neither actively ratifying nor actively spiting, but simply undecided.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hakeimah vs Heifer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chomer in Each\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a Baraita cited by Rav Chisda asserting that there are respects in which {\i hakamah} is stronger than {\i hafarah}, and respects in which {\i hafarah} is stronger than {\i hakamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakeimah Non-Verbal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first area where {\i hakamah} is stronger: silence itself can produce {\i kiyum}. As established above, the passage of the full day without {\i hafarah} constitutes {\i hakamah} even without the husband ever uttering a word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heifer Verbal Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, silence never produces {\i hafarah}. {\i Hafarah} requires verbalization \u8212- some form of explicit speech \u8212- and no amount of silence, however prolonged, can serve as an annulment of the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiyum In Heart\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two points are, in fact, deeply connected. The Baraita states: {\i kiyem belibo kiyem; heifer belibo eino mufar} \u8212- if one ratified the vow in his heart, it is ratified; if one annulled the vow in his heart, it is not annulled. {\i Kiyum} does not require verbalization; it can be achieved through internal intention alone. {\i Hafarah} must be spoken aloud. The reason silence over the course of the day yields {\i hakamah} is precisely because we infer from that silence that the husband\u8217's internal disposition is one of acceptance \u8212- and since {\i kiyum} operates through internal disposition, the inference suffices. {\i Hafarah}, which operates exclusively through speech, cannot be accomplished in this indirect manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can see immediately how these two points reinforce each other: the effectiveness of silent {\i kiyum} explains why the passage of time without speech yields {\i hakamah}, and the requirement of speech for {\i hafarah} explains why no analogous inference can work in the opposite direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Baraita then notes one area of symmetry: {\i kiyem ein yakhol lehafer; heifer ein yakhol lekayem} \u8212- once one has performed {\i kiyum}, {\i hafarah} is no longer available; once one has performed {\i hafarah}, {\i kiyum} is no longer available. This mutual exclusivity applies in both directions equally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Baraita promised an area where {\i hafarah} is stronger than {\i hakamah}, yet so far only areas of {\i hakamah}\u8217's strength have been identified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Undoing Hakeimah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara supplies the answer in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i nish\u8217'alin al ha-hekem ve-ein nish\u8217'alin al ha-hefer} \u8212- one may seek release from {\i hakamah} through a {\i chakham}, but not from {\i hafarah}. Since {\i hakamah} operates by reinforcing the {\i neder} \u8212- by treating it as fully affirmed \u8212- it partakes of the character of a {\i neder} itself, and just as a {\i neder} may be undone through {\i hasharat nedarim} before a {\i chakham}, so too a {\i hakamah} may be unwound in that way. {\i Hafarah}, once performed, cannot be undone. In this respect, {\i hafarah} is definitively stronger.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Tyufot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tyufa 1: Spite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now marshals three successive Tannaitic challenges against Rabbi Chanina\u8217's position. The first is brought by Rav Kahana. The Baraita addresses a case of {\i shoteik al menat lemekat} \u8212- a husband who is silent specifically to cause his wife distress \u8212- and rules that by the end of the {\i halachic} day, this silence nonetheless constitutes {\i hakamah}. This appears to be a direct and explicit rejection of Rabbi Chanina, who held that spite-motivated silence should not yield {\i hakamah} regardless of how much time has passed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Silences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds by questioning whether the Baraita is indeed addressing spite-motivated silence, suggesting it might be referring to the more ordinary case of silence aimed at eventual {\i kiyum}. The Gemara then resolves the matter by noting that the word {\i hecherish} \u8212- \u8220"he was silent\u8221" \u8212- appears three times in the relevant {\i pasuk}. Each mention extends the ruling to an additional type of silence: one occurrence addresses silence where the husband intends immediate {\i kiyum}; a second addresses deliberative silence; and the third \u8212- the surplus mention \u8212- extends the law to even spite-motivated silence. The Torah\u8217's threefold use of the word of silence is thus read as a deliberate textual signal that all forms of silence, irrespective of motivation, yield {\i hakamah} by the end of the day. This constitutes a clear refutation of Rabbi Chanina.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tyufa 2: Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second challenge is raised by Rava. The Gemara elsewhere permits a husband to perform {\i hafarah} on {\i Shabbat} \u8212- an act that would otherwise seem to raise concerns \u8212- specifically because if the vow was taken as {\i Shabbat} was ending, he must have the opportunity to annul before the day closes. The reasoning is: if he cannot perform {\i hafarah} on {\i Shabbat}, then the day ends and the vow becomes established. The very fact that we permit {\i hafarah} on {\i Shabbat} to prevent this outcome is Rava\u8217's basis for a powerful argument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider the logic carefully. If a husband cannot perform {\i hafarah} on {\i Shabbat} \u8212- whether due to a rabbinic prohibition \u8212- then his silence on that day is not a free choice. He is silent because {\i halacha} prevents him from acting, not because he wishes to ratify the vow. According to Rabbi Chanina, such silence \u8212- silence whose reason is clearly not acceptance \u8212- should not become {\i hakamah}. If that were so, he would not lose the right to perform {\i hafarah} at the end of {\i Shabbat}, and we would have no reason to permit {\i hafarah} on {\i Shabbat} in the first place. The very existence of the dispensation to perform {\i hafarah} on {\i Shabbat} demonstrates that the reason for one\u8217's silence is legally irrelevant \u8212- any silence sustained through the end of the day becomes {\i hakamah}, period. This is another {\i tyufta} against Rabbi Chanina.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tyufa 3: Ignorance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third challenge comes from Rav Ashi. The Gemara addresses a case where a husband says: I knew that my wife took a vow, but I did not know that I had the power to perform {\i hafarah}. He was never taught the relevant laws. Three days have now passed. The ruling: {\i yaper} \u8212- he may still perform {\i hafarah}. The reasoning is that {\i beyom shamo} \u8212- \u8220"on the day that he heard\u8221" \u8212- is defined not from the moment he first heard the words of the {\i neder}, but from the moment he became aware that he was in a position to act on it. Hearing without the contextual knowledge that one has the ability to respond is not halachically meaningful hearing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling sets up a related debate about a parallel case. What if the husband says: I knew I had the power to perform {\i hafarah}, but I did not realize that what my wife said constituted a {\i neder}? Rabbi Meir rules that this still counts as hearing; the husband heard the words, knew he had the relevant legal power, and his failure to recognize the statement as a {\i neder} does not reset the clock. The {\i chakhamim} disagree and hold that this is analogous to the previous case \u8212- without realizing it is a {\i neder}, one has not truly \u8220"heard\u8221" it in the halachically operative sense, and the day begins only when full awareness is achieved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is notable for our purposes is what both Rabbi Meir and the {\i chakhamim} appear to agree upon: once the clock does begin \u8212- once hearing is established \u8212- the reason for the husband\u8217's subsequent silence does not matter. If a man is silent because he is clueless about {\i halacha}, that too becomes {\i hakamah} by the end of the day. The debate is only about when the clock starts, not about whether the motivation behind silence modulates the {\i hakamah} rule. This is the third and final {\i tyufta} against Rabbi Chanina\u8217's intent-based limitation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ran & R. Akiva Eiger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran On Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the Ran\u8217's discussion, the Gemara\u8217's principle that {\i kiyum} may be accomplished non-verbally \u8212- indeed, through the very mechanism of silence \u8212- requires explanation. The Ran addresses this directly: we learn from the law of silence on the day of hearing that {\i hakamah} does not require verbalization. The fact that sustained silence through the end of the day yields {\i hakamah} demonstrates that {\i kiyum} operates through internal disposition, not through speech.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why, then, does the silence need to last until the end of the day? The Ran explains: until sunset, it is simply not yet clear what the silence means. Perhaps the husband is planning to perform {\i hafarah} shortly. The ambiguity cannot be resolved before the day ends. Only once the full day has passed without any act of {\i hafarah} does the silence become unambiguous evidence that the husband\u8217's disposition is one of acceptance. As the Ran puts it: since we are going exclusively by what the husband intends, and we do not have direct access to his inner state, we require the accumulated weight of an entire day\u8217's silence to establish that his intent is indeed {\i kiyum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Active Desire\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this the Ran draws a further inference: if the husband at some earlier moment during the day genuinely and actively desires \u8212- not merely contemplates \u8212- {\i kiyum}, that intent is effective immediately, without needing to wait until sunset. The Ran describes this as analogous to crossing a finish line: if you have actually crossed it, you do not need to wait for the race to be over. In such a case it is as though the full day has already been discharged, because the husband\u8217's will is unambiguously directed toward {\i kiyum} at this very moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Eiger Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva Eiger raises a sharp challenge against this position. The Ran has argued, on the one hand, that since {\i kiyum} operates through internal intent, and since we cannot access a person\u8217's internal thoughts directly, we require the silence of a full day as evidence of that intent. But on the other hand, the Ran also holds that if the husband actively desires {\i kiyum} right now, his intent is immediately effective \u8212- without waiting for the day to end. The difficulty is this: if, as the Ran acknowledges, we normally cannot access what a person is thinking, and if we therefore require observable evidence (the passage of the full day) to establish {\i kiyum} \u8212- then how can an immediate unspoken desire for {\i kiyum} ever work? In the case of immediate intent, there is no equivalent evidence. The husband has said nothing and done nothing; we simply have no basis on which to credit his inner desire with halachic effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evidence Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gilyon HaShas} addresses this challenge. The underlying principle, he explains, is that internal thoughts \u8212- {\i devarim shebelev} \u8212- do not generally carry halachic significance. However, where those thoughts are {\i muchach uvalui lechol} \u8212- evident and manifest to observers \u8212- they do acquire halachic weight. This is the logic of the silent {\i hakamah} that accrues over the course of the day: the sustained silence renders the husband\u8217's inner acceptance evident to those around him. By the end of the day, {\i galui lanu kavanato} \u8212- his intent is disclosed to us \u8212- and that is why it is legally effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Observable Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applying this framework to Rabbi Akiva Eiger\u8217's challenge, the {\i Gilyon HaShas} resolves that even in the case of immediate {\i kiyum}, there would need to be some form of observable evidence \u8212- something visible to others \u8212- for the immediate intent to take legal effect. Merely thinking, with nothing to disclose the thought to any observer, would fall under the general rule that unmanifest internal states carry no halachic weight. Silence accumulated over the day provides precisely such evidence; a bare internal desire, unaccompanied by any observable act or signal, does not. The question of immediate {\i kiyum} is thus left with some difficulty, though the {\i Gilyon HaShas}\u8217's framework at least clarifies what form of evidence would theoretically be required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Heifer Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nedarim To Meifer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the beginning of the final chapter of the tractate, the Gemara turns from the question of {\i who} may perform {\i hafarah} and {\i when} to the question of {\i which} vows are subject to {\i hafarah} at all. The opening of the {\i perek} establishes: {\i ve-elu nedarim shehu mefer} \u8212- these are the vows that a husband annuls. Before proceeding, it is worth noting the Ran\u8217's observation that the same categories applicable to the husband \u8212- {\i inuy nefesh} and the relational category of {\i bein ish leishah} \u8212- apply equally to the father with respect to his daughter, at least according to the Ran\u8217's position. The Rambam disagrees and holds that the father\u8217's authority over his minor daughter\u8217's vows is unlimited; the more restricted categorization applies only to the husband, who is dealing with an emancipated adult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inuy Nefesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah frames the husband\u8217's authority over his wife\u8217's vows around the phrase {\i leanot nefesh} \u8212- to afflict the soul, or more precisely, to afflict the body. The first category of vows subject to {\i hafarah} is accordingly those involving {\i inuy nefesh}, vows whose effect constitutes a form of bodily affliction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inuy Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} offers two initial examples of {\i inuy nefesh}: a vow to refrain from bathing and a vow to refrain from wearing makeup. These are presented as paradigmatic cases of self-denial that rise to the level of {\i inuy nefesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bein Ish Leishah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second category is derived from the Torah\u8217's phrase {\i bein ish leishah} \u8212- between a man and his wife \u8212- and encompasses vows that, while perhaps not constituting bodily affliction, directly affect the marital relationship and the dynamics between husband and wife. This category opens a separate channel of authority for the husband beyond the {\i inuy nefesh} framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yosi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi disputes the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's classification. He holds that refraining from bathing and refraining from makeup do not rise to the level of {\i inuy nefesh}. This is a striking position, particularly given the Roman-era context in which the Talmud was composed, where public bathing was a regular and socially normative practice. Yet Rabbi Yosi maintains that {\i inuy nefesh} requires a higher threshold \u8212- something closer to a complete and significant deprivation \u8212- and that these examples fall short. Instead, Rabbi Yosi proposes his own paradigm cases of {\i inuy nefesh}. What Rabbi Yosi does with bathing and makeup, then, is presumably to place them in the {\i bein ish leishah} category rather than {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- an important distinction, as the Gemara will go on to explain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Inuy Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's conception of {\i inuy nefesh} is notably demanding. He illustrates with two cases where {\i hafarah} is {\i not} available: if a wife vows to abstain from the fruit of a particular country, that is not {\i inuy nefesh}, because fruit from other countries remains available \u8212- {\i yavi mimedina acheret}, go fetch it from another region. Similarly, if she vows to abstain from produce purchased at a particular storekeeper\u8217's shop, that too is not {\i inuy nefesh}, since she can simply shop elsewhere. These cases lack the quality of genuine, inescapable deprivation that {\i inuy nefesh} requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sole Supplier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, an exception to the storekeeper case. If the husband\u8217's only practical supplier of food was that particular storekeeper \u8212- for example, because the storekeeper was the only one who would extend him credit, while no other vendor would deal with him on those terms \u8212- then forbidding the produce of that storekeeper\u8217's shop does constitute {\i inuy nefesh}. Whatever the theoretical availability of alternatives, the practical reality is that this family\u8217's food supply depends entirely on that one source, and to eliminate it is genuine deprivation. The Ran further applies this principle to the country case: when the {\i Mishnah} says that forbidding fruit from a particular country is not {\i inuy nefesh} because one can obtain it from elsewhere, this presupposes that the alternative country is genuinely comparable \u8212- both are secondary sources. If the forbidden country is in fact the primary source of the household\u8217's food, the analysis would follow the storekeeper model and the vow would indeed constitute {\i inuy nefesh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Permanence Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Only Inuy Mufar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara initially reads the {\i Mishnah} as holding that only {\i inuy nefesh} vows are subject to {\i hafarah} by the husband. Vows lacking {\i inuy nefesh} cannot be annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Citation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Baraita immediately challenges this reading by citing the Torah\u8217's phrase {\i bein ish leishah bein av levito} \u8212- explicitly establishing that vows {\i bein ish leishah}, in the relational category, are also subject to {\i hafarah}. The Baraita confirms that both categories \u8212- {\i inuy nefesh} and {\i bein ish leishah} \u8212- lie within the husband\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Distinguish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a pointed question. If the husband can annul vows in both categories, why does Rabbi Yosi insist that bathing and makeup are {\i not inuy nefesh}? If they fall into the {\i bein ish leishah} category, the husband can annul them anyway. What halachic work is the categorical distinction doing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suggested Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proposes a theory: although the husband can perform {\i hafarah} on both categories, the categories differ in their duration of effect. {\i Inuy nefesh} vows, when annulled, are annulled permanently \u8212- {\i meifer le-olam}. But {\i bein ish leishah} vows, when annulled, are effective only during the marriage; upon divorce, the {\i neder} reasserts itself. On this theory, the mechanism of {\i hafarah} differs between the two categories: in the case of {\i inuy nefesh}, the husband possesses genuine annulment authority; in the case of {\i bein ish leishah}, his power is more like a right to suspend the vow\u8217's operation for as long as the marital relationship continues. This would explain why Rabbi Yosi needs to distinguish the categories \u8212- whether a vow falls into {\i inuy nefesh} or merely {\i bein ish leishah} has real consequences for the permanence of the {\i hafarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Nuri\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This theory is supported, at least prima facie, by the ruling of Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri in the {\i Mishnah}. If a wife takes a vow forbidding the product of her labor to her husband \u8212- {\i maaseh yadeha} \u8212- the husband is not technically required to annul it, since that labor belongs to him anyway. But Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri advises that he should annul it regardless. The reason: if they later divorce, he would no longer own her labor, and the vow \u8212- never having been formally annulled \u8212- would spring back into effect and forbid her labor to him. This would prevent them from remarrying, since no marriage can function if the wife\u8217's labor is forbidden to her husband. The advice to annul proactively demonstrates that {\i hafarah} of a {\i bein ish leishah} vow can have lasting effect \u8212- it will protect against the vow\u8217's reinstatement even after divorce. This seems to support the theory that {\i hafarah} of such vows does indeed carry some lasting force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theory Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately rejects the theory that {\i bein ish leishah hafarah} lapses upon divorce. Even after the couple divorces, a {\i hafarah} that was performed during the marriage remains effective. The case of Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri confirms this: the whole point of his advice is that the husband should perform {\i hafarah now} so that it will remain in effect even if they divorce \u8212- which would make no sense if {\i bein ish leishah hafarah} automatically lapsed upon divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refined Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore refines the distinction. Both {\i inuy nefesh} and {\i bein ish leishah} vows, when annulled, remain annulled even after divorce. The difference lies in what happens if the woman subsequently marries another man. For {\i inuy nefesh} vows, the original husband\u8217's {\i hafarah} is effective {\i bein le\u8217'atzmo bein le\u8217'acheirim} \u8212- both for himself and for others, meaning the vow remains annulled even with respect to a new husband. For {\i bein ish leishah} vows, the {\i hafarah} is effective {\i le\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- for himself \u8212- but not {\i le\u8217'acheirim} \u8212- not for a new husband. Once she marries another man, the vow reasserts itself in her new relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic is that {\i bein ish leishah hafarah} is not a free-standing annulment of the {\i neder} but rather a suspension rooted in the particular marital relationship. After divorce, the couple might still remarry, and in that potential relationship the {\i hafarah} retains its relevance \u8212- it remains {\i bein ish leishah} even in the divorce state, since the bond has not been fully severed beyond all prospect of reunion. But once she marries another man, the original marital context that grounded the {\i hafarah} is definitively gone, the old husband\u8217's sphere of authority over this {\i neder} is extinguished, and the {\i neder} reasserts itself in her new marriage. With {\i inuy nefesh}, by contrast, the husband\u8217's authority is absolute: he possesses genuine annulment power, and what he annuls stays annulled regardless of subsequent relationships.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 81\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wellspring Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Towns Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf picks up at the bottom of 80b, continuing the larger discussion of what constitutes a {\i neder inuy nefesh} \u8212- a vow of self-affliction that a husband is entitled to annul \u8212- alongside the category of {\i beino l\u8217'veina}, matters that pertain specifically to the dynamic between husband and wife. The Gemara introduces a {\i baraita} (found in the Tosefta to Bava Metzia) with the formula {\i v\u8217'rammi d\u8217'Rabbi Yosi ad d\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- a contradiction is raised between two rulings of Rabbi Yosi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} presents the case of a {\i ma\u8217'ayan shebnei ha\u8217'ir}: a natural wellspring that originates in one town and flows toward another. The question is how to adjudicate competing water needs between the two towns across a hierarchy of uses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Life & Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The hierarchy proceeds as follows. First: {\i chayeihem v\u8217'chayei acheirim} \u8212- the lives of the townspeople who possess the wellspring take precedence over the lives of the people in the other town. This is entirely unsurprising; it reflects the foundational halachic principle of {\i v\u8217'chai achicha imach} \u8212- {\i chayecha kodmin l\u8217'chayei achicha}, your own life takes priority over that of another. Second: {\i behemtam u\u8217'vehemat acheirim} \u8212- their animals take precedence over the other town\u8217's animals. Third: {\i kvisatan u\u8217'kvisat acheirim} \u8212- their laundry takes precedence over the other town\u8217's laundry. All three of these rulings follow naturally from the principle of self-priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Laundry Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The surprising ruling comes next. The {\i baraita} poses the case of {\i chayei acheirim u\u8217'kvisatan}: what if the other town\u8217's people need the water for basic sustenance \u8212- their lives \u8212- while the town with the wellspring needs it only for laundry? The anonymous first voice of the {\i baraita} rules {\i chayei acheirim kodmin l\u8217'kvisatan}: the other town\u8217's lives take precedence over laundry, which seems obvious. But then: {\i Rabbi Yosi omer, kvisatan kodemes l\u8217'chayei acheirim} \u8212- Rabbi Yosi rules that their laundry precedes the other town\u8217's lives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, on its face, a startling ruling. Someone in the next town needs water to survive, and Rabbi Yosi permits the first town to keep the water for laundry?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers an important qualification. He reads {\i kodemet} not as a categorical denial of water to the other town, but as a question of temporal priority. The wellspring flows naturally from town A to town B. Town A is permitted to first pause or dam the water in order to complete their laundry, after which the water continues downstream to town B. On this reading, nobody in the second town ultimately goes without \u8212- they simply have to wait. The {\i chiddush} is only about the order of access, not about a permanent withholding, and Tosafot presumably frames it this way because the assumption is that no one will actually die from the delay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ethics & Charity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran, by contrast, appears to favor a more literal reading, which creates genuine moral tension. One might try to defend the position with reference to the principle that even a limb of one\u8217's own body takes precedence over another person\u8217's life \u8212- the {\i psak halacha} being that one is not obligated to risk a limb to definitively save another\u8217's life, even though objectively a life outweighs a limb. If even one\u8217's own limb takes precedence, perhaps something central to one\u8217's bodily dignity and well-being \u8212- clothing one wears, the state of one\u8217's physical environment \u8212- might similarly precede a more distant person\u8217's claim on one\u8217's resources. This is admittedly harsh, and the lecturer does not endorse a literal reading. But it invites comparison to Peter Singer\u8217's well-known critique: every day people spend significant sums on dinners, consumer goods, and personal luxuries while lives elsewhere could be saved with those same funds. The distance and abstraction of the need makes it feel different \u8212- but morally, is it? Here, the context is less abstract: these are fellow Jews in a neighboring town. The tradition of {\i pidyon shvuyim} illustrates that when Jews are in acute need nearby, significant personal sacrifice is expected. A mitigating interpretation of {\i chayei acheirim} is also possible \u8212- perhaps it refers not to imminent death but to a lower level of well-being, a harder life rather than a life-threatening emergency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzedakah Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling opens onto a broader question about the competing metrics within {\i tzedakah} obligations. Halacha articulates two distinct hierarchies: one based on proximity ({\i aniucha ve\u8217'aniyei ircha, aniucha kodmin} \u8212- your own poor take precedence over those of another city) and one based on degree of need ({\i ani ve\u8217'ashir, ani kodem} \u8212- the poor take precedence over the wealthy even in loan contexts). What happens when proximity and need pull in opposite directions? The poor of one\u8217's own city need education, while the poor of a neighboring city need food \u8212- to whom does one\u8217's primary obligation run?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Chasam Sofer holds that need always governs first: one must ensure that basic needs everywhere are met before ascending to higher-level needs, beginning with those closest. The Aruch Hashulchan, by contrast, emphasizes that primary obligation always tracks proximity. Something of this tension plays out in the {\i baraita}: even granting that we should not imagine people literally dying, one can still ask whether one town\u8217's laundry precedes another town\u8217's general welfare. These are genuinely unsettled questions in the application of {\i tzedakah} priorities, and the Gemara surfaces them here without fully resolving them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Medical Dangers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Health\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that Rabbi Yosi elevates laundry to a remarkable degree of importance, the Gemara now presents a medical teaching from Shmuel that contextualizes this. {\i Arvuvita dereisha} \u8212- the tangling and filth of the hair from not washing \u8212- {\i mayti lidei avira}, leads to blindness. {\i Arvuvita demani} \u8212- the filth of one\u8217's garments from not doing laundry \u8212- {\i mayti lidei sha\u8217'amimuta}, leads to a kind of mental disturbance or psychological deterioration. {\i Arvuvita degufa} \u8212- bodily filth from not bathing \u8212- {\i mayti lidei shichney vekivi}, leads to sores and blisters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Resolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching sharpens the original contradiction. If not bathing causes sores and blisters, how can Rabbi Yosi say bathing is not {\i inuy nefesh}? And if not laundering causes psychological deterioration \u8212- seemingly worse \u8212- why does Rabbi Yosi use laundry to such great rhetorical effect while dismissing bathing? The Gemara\u8217's resolution is that Rabbi Yosi does not doubt Shmuel\u8217's medical claims. Rather, his position is that not laundering is genuinely weighty \u8212- enough that one may prioritize laundry over other competing claims \u8212- yet that still does not automatically make the {\i deprivation} of laundering the kind of {\i inuy nefesh} that rises to the level activating a husband\u8217's annulment power. The distinction is between what is medically serious and what crosses the formal halachic threshold of {\i nidrei inuy nefesh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Warnings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hygiene Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records that {\i shalchu mitam} \u8212- they sent from Eretz Yisrael \u8212- three warnings. The first: {\i hizahru b\u8217'arvuvsa} \u8212- be careful regarding filth, the neglect of bathing and laundering. Given Shmuel\u8217's medical teachings, the community has a responsibility to ensure that even those without means have access to the basic hygiene that protects health.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chavruta Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second warning: {\i hizahru b\u8217'chavura} \u8212- be attentive to community, which the {\i rishonim} explain as a reference to learning in {\i chavruta}, in partnership. Torah study is not meant to be a solitary enterprise; the maintenance of a learning community is presented as a communal priority on a par with the first warning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poor Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third and most extensively discussed warning: {\i hizahru bivnei aniyim} \u8212- take special care of the children of the poor, for {\i shemehem taitzei Torah} \u8212- from them Torah will ultimately go out. The verse cited is {\i yizal mayim midalyav} \u8212- water will flow from his lowly ones \u8212- where {\i dal} (lowly) is read as the source of {\i mayim} (water), and water is Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Transmission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the Gemara to pose a question: {\i umipnei mah einam metzuyin talmidei chachamim l\u8217'taitzei talmidei chachamim mibeineihen?} Why do we not consistently see the children of {\i talmidei chachamim} themselves becoming great {\i talmidei chachamim}? Logic would seem to favor it \u8212- Torah is spoken in the home, learning is prioritized, the culture is saturated with it. And indeed, one can point to dynasties like that of Rashi \u8212- Rashbam, Rabbeinu Tam, the Ri \u8212- where Torah did pass with remarkable depth across generations. Yet the Gemara treats the absence of such dynasties as the norm requiring explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three {\i amoraim} offer explanations. Rav Yosef says: {\i shelo yomru Torah yerusha hi lahem} \u8212- so that no family should come to feel that Torah is their {\i yerusha}, their inheritance. A {\i yerusha} is something received without effort; it arrives by default. The children of {\i talmidei chachamim}, if Torah passed automatically from father to son, would have no incentive to labor for it. The {\i bnei aniyim}, by contrast, receive nothing for free and must work for every opportunity. Their Torah therefore comes from genuine effort and urgent appreciation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet bar Rav Idi adds: {\i k\u8217'dei shelo yitgadru al hatzibur} \u8212- so that they should not become haughty over the community. If one family maintained a sustained monopoly on Torah learning across generations, the inevitable result would be a kind of aristocratic elevation \u8212- the family of Torah, set above and apart, a protected hierarchy. God arranges matters so this does not solidify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra frames the same point as consequence rather than prevention: {\i mishum shehen mitgabrim al hatzibur} \u8212- it is because such families, when Torah does concentrate in them, do become overbearing toward the community. The loss of the dynasty is recompense for that pattern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers a final reason: {\i mishum d\u8217'karu l\u8217'inshei chamrei} \u8212- because they call other people donkeys. When a family comes to regard itself as the sole bearers of Torah, they come to view laypeople as inherently inferior \u8212- {\i am ha\u8217'aretz hadomeh k\u8217'veheimah} \u8212- and that contempt, when rooted in a sense of innate superiority rather than earned distinction, generates its own punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Universality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a deeper point underlying all three explanations. The {\i Beit HaMikdash} was hierarchical by design: physical proximity to it mattered, access was stratified, and {\i kehunah} was exclusively hereditary \u8212- a genuine {\i yerusha} for the {\i bnei Aharon}. Torah, by contrast, is the one of the {\i shlosha k\u8217'tarim} of which the Gemara says {\i kol harotzeh yavo v\u8217'yitol} \u8212- whoever wishes may come and take it. Chazal created the institution of universal education. The warning to care for {\i bnei aniyim} is in part a reminder that the universality of Torah is not self-maintaining; it requires active communal protection. Even something that should be as radically open and non-hierarchical as Torah can drift toward becoming a protected, centralized institution if the community is not vigilant.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blessing Over Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse & Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now cites the verse: {\i mi ha\u8217'ish ha\u8217'chacham v\u8217'yaven et zot \u8212- al mah avdah ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- who is the wise person who will understand this: why was the land destroyed? The Gemara records that the sages could not answer, the prophets could not answer, until God himself answered: {\i al azvam et Torati asher nasati lifneihem velo sham\u8217'u bekoli velo halchu bah} \u8212- because they abandoned My Torah and did not listen to My voice and did not walk in it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voice & Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The apparent redundancy of {\i velo sham\u8217'u bekoli} and {\i velo halchu bah} \u8212- not listening to God\u8217's voice and not walking in the Torah seem to describe the same failure \u8212- occasions the teaching of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav: {\i she\u8217'ein mevarchin baTorah techila} \u8212- they did not make the {\i bracha} over Torah first. \u8220"Not listening to My voice\u8221" refers to the failure to make the blessing before learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Business\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the connection between not making a {\i bracha} and the problem of Torah becoming a family inheritance? A {\i bracha} is not merely a formality. {\i Asher kideshanu bemitzvotav vetzivanu} \u8212- it is an act of recognizing that one learns because God commanded it, that Torah is a gift received from outside oneself, a {\i mitzvah} being performed in response to divine demand. The danger for a family in which Torah has become the family business across generations is that this sense evaporates. Learning becomes what one\u8217's father did, what one\u8217's grandfather did, the professional substrate of one\u8217's life. When Torah is simply the family trade, one does not make a {\i bracha} over it \u8212- not because one is impious, but because it no longer registers as a treasure being encountered. One is not {\i listening to God\u8217's voice}; one is simply doing what one does. This loss of orientation \u8212- the failure to experience Torah as a commanded gift rather than a familial possession \u8212- is, according to Ravina, the deeper cause behind both the abandonment of Torah and the failure of dynasties to sustain genuine Torah scholarship.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vardimus Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vardimus Confused\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Isi bar Yehuda stopped attending Rabbi Yosi\u8217's {\i beit midrash} for three days. When Vardimus bar Rabbi Yosi \u8212- Rabbi Yosi\u8217's son, bearing a name apparently of Greek derivation \u8212- encountered him and asked why he had not come, Isi bar Yehuda replied: {\i ki ta\u8217'ameh de\u8217'avuch lo yad\u8217'ana heichi asina} \u8212- if I cannot understand the reasoning of your father, how can I keep attending? Vardimus asked him to state what he did not understand, saying perhaps he could explain it. Isi bar Yehuda cited the ruling: {\i Rabbi Yosi omer kvisatan kodemet lechayei acheirim} \u8212- that one\u8217's laundry precedes the other town\u8217's lives. This, he said, makes no sense, and he could not continue attending a {\i beit midrash} where he heard such things without comprehension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levite Suburbs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Vardimus then offers a textual derivation. The verse states: {\i umigrasheihem yihyu livhemtam ulechol chayasam} \u8212- the surrounding fields of the Levitical cities shall be for their animals and for all of their {\i chayasam}. The question is: what does {\i chayasam} mean? If it means wild animals \u8212- deer and the like \u8212- that is already included in {\i behemtam}. The verse would be redundant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chayasam Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, {\i chayasam} must refer to something that sustains life \u8212- {\i chayusa mamash}. But if it means literal life-sustaining activities, that too seems obvious and need not be stated. The derivation therefore pushes further: it must refer to something that comes next in the hierarchy of needs after {\i behema} \u8212- and consulting the order of the {\i baraita} (lives, then animals, then laundry), that next category is laundry. Laundry is thus included in the term {\i chayasam} \u8212- things that sustain life \u8212- because {\i de\u8217'ika tzaar degivshusa}, the neglect of laundry brings real physical harm (the filth, the associated ailments discussed by Shmuel). This is how Vardimus grounds his father\u8217's ruling: laundry is classified as life-sustaining activity and therefore may, under the framework of {\i chayecha kodmin}, be prioritized even against the competing needs of others.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bathing & Makeup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Inuy Nefesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the central case. {\i Amar Rabbi Yosi: ein elu nidrei inui nefesh} \u8212- Rabbi Yosi rules that vows involving the prohibition of bathing or the application of makeup do not fall within the category of {\i nidrei inuy nefesh}. The husband, therefore, cannot exercise his annulment power on that basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beino L\u8217'Veina Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question is then raised: even if these vows are not {\i inuy nefesh}, does Rabbi Yosi hold that they qualify as {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina} \u8212- matters pertaining to the direct relationship between husband and wife \u8212- for which the husband also has annulment authority? The Gemara attempts to answer from Rabbi Yosi\u8217's own phrasing: {\i ein elu nidrei inui nefesh} sounds as though he is making a categorical ruling about the classification, not simply saying he cannot annul them. This might imply that they are not even in the {\i beino l\u8217'veina} category. But the Gemara deflects: perhaps Rabbi Yosi is only addressing the Rabbis who placed these in the {\i inuy nefesh} category, and his statement is {\i l\u8217'didchu} \u8212- addressed to those who take that position. He is saying: at minimum concede they are not {\i inuy nefesh}. His own view may go further and remove them from {\i beino l\u8217'veina} as well. Whether they belong there is therefore left open by this exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Adda vs.\u160 ?Huna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two {\i amoraim} dispute the matter. Rav Adda bar Ahava holds that the husband can annul \u8212- these vows do fall within {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}. Rav Huna holds he cannot annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fox Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna\u8217's reasoning is captured by the saying: {\i shelo matzinu shual shemet b\u8217'afar pir} \u8212- we have never found a fox that died from the dust of its own burrow. A creature is not harmed by the familiar environment it has grown accustomed to. Applied here: if a husband\u8217's wife does not bathe or apply makeup, that is part of the physical reality of their shared life together. People adapt to and tolerate what becomes their normal environment. On this view, the husband has no genuine complaint, and therefore the vow does not rise to the level of {\i beino l\u8217'veina} in a way that would justify annulment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Annulment Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inuy Nefesh Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then sets out the governing categories systematically. Vows that carry {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- genuine self-affliction \u8212- the husband may annul {\i bein beino l\u8217'veina bein beino l\u8217'veina ve\u8217'acheirim}: whether the vow directly concerns their relationship or involves her deprivation by a third party. The operative question for {\i inuy nefesh} is entirely about the nature and degree of harm to her, not about who is the subject of the vow. If her being deprived of something constitutes real {\i inuy nefesh}, it does not matter whether that thing comes from her husband, from another person, or from the world at large.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Examples List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is illustrated concretely. If she says {\i konam peirot eilu alai} \u8212- all fruit is forbidden to me \u8212- he can annul it. It does not matter whose fruit. If she says she is forbidden from benefiting from a particular person \u8212- her father, her brother, his brother, his father \u8212- or says she will not give straw before his animals or water to his cattle, he {\i cannot} annul. The reason: even though such vows may cause him annoyance or inconvenience, and even though they affect his household and family relationships, they are not directly about the intimate dynamic between the two of them. Since they do not rise to {\i inuy nefesh} either, neither basis for annulment applies. The {\i chiddush} here is that vows which create indirect irritation or hardship for the husband \u8212- even regarding his animals, his family members \u8212- fall outside both categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beino L\u8217'Veina Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if she vows not to apply {\i kechol} or {\i afros} (the various forms of eye and face makeup), or not to have sexual relations \u8212- {\i shelo eshamesh mitatecha} \u8212- he can annul, {\i mishum devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}, because these directly concern their relationship. The vow implicates something between the two of them in a direct and personal way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligatory Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But certain acts she cannot effectively vow away at all, because they fall within her existing obligations to him: {\i shelo atzia lecha mitatecha} (making his bed), {\i shelo emzog lecha et hakos} (mixing his wine), {\i shelo erchatz lecha panecha yadecha veraglecha} (washing his face, hands, and feet). As established in Ketubot, even a wife who brings a hundred maidservants into the household is still obligated to perform these acts of intimate service for her husband personally. Since she lacks the halachic standing to vow away something she is already obligated to provide, such a vow is simply ineffective \u8212- {\i ein tzarich lehafer}, the husband does not even need to formally annul.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Yachel Devaro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That said, Rabban Gamliel holds that even with respect to vows that are technically ineffective \u8212- those covering acts she is already obligated to perform \u8212- the husband should nonetheless go through the process of annulment. The basis is {\i lo yachel devaro}: he should not let his word become violated. The Ran observes that the verse does not say {\i lo yachel nidro} or {\i shvuaso} \u8212- it says {\i devaro}, his {\i word}. The implication is that even speech that is not technically binding as a {\i neder} carries weight. Dismissing it entirely, treating it as though it never happened, is itself a form of disregard. The process of annulment, even where not strictly required, honors the gravity of speech and restores clarity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son\u8217's Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resonates practically. A son returned from camp having made some kind of conditional declaration \u8212- not technically a {\i neder} under strict halachic analysis \u8212- but he was distressed by it and wanted resolution. The technically correct answer that \u8220"it means nothing\u8221" was insufficient; he needed the formal process. The father said {\i mutar lach, mutar lach}, and only then did the son feel genuinely released. The words had weight, and the weight needed to be formally addressed. That is the thrust of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A digression is prompted by the verse {\i lo yachel devaro}: {\i mikan lechacham she\u8217'ein matir nidro atzmo} \u8212- a Torah scholar cannot annul his own vow. The verse implies that one can {\i violate} one\u8217's words \u8212- meaning the words retain binding force on the person who spoke them. Someone else can annul, but the person himself cannot. As the aphorism goes: a prisoner cannot release himself from jail.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bathing & Makeup (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i baraita}: who is the {\i tana} who placed the prohibition of applying {\i kechol} and {\i afros} in the category of {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}? The question matters because the {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i mishnah} places bathing and makeup in the {\i inuy nefesh} category. The {\i baraita} placing these in {\i beino l\u8217'veina} \u8212- a lesser category \u8212- can only reflect the view of Rabbi Yosi, who holds they are not {\i inuy nefesh}. This is therefore strong evidence that Rabbi Yosi, despite saying these do not qualify as {\i inuy nefesh}, does hold they belong in {\i beino l\u8217'veina} \u8212- resolving the earlier question in favor of Rav Adda bar Ahava.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sexual Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Pleasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the case of sexual relations. The {\i baraita} said that if she vows {\i shelo eshamesh mitatecha} \u8212- she will not have sexual relations with him \u8212- he can annul, {\i mishum devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}. But the Gemara presses: what exactly is the case? If she says {\i hana\u8217'at tashmishi alecha} \u8212- the pleasure of my sexual service is forbidden to you \u8212- why would annulment even be necessary? {\i Hameshubdedet leih} \u8212- she is obligated to him in this regard; it is not hers to forbid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kahana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case must therefore be, the Gemara concludes, {\i k\u8217'Rav Kahana}. Rav Kahana rules: if a woman says {\i hana\u8217'at tashmishi alecha} \u8212- my sexual service is forbidden to you \u8212- {\i kofin u\u8217'meshamshto}: she is compelled to fulfill her obligation regardless. The vow does not take effect, because it targets something that is not hers to withhold. But if she says {\i hana\u8217'at tashmishcha alai} \u8212- the pleasure of your sexual service is forbidden to me \u8212- then {\i yafer}: he must annul. This version of the vow does take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the second formulation takes effect while the first does not relates to the principle {\i she\u8217'ein ma\u8217'achilin et adam davar ha\u8217'asur lo} \u8212- one may not force a person to consume something forbidden to them. She did not say she would not fulfill her duty; she said she would not benefit from him. Nevertheless, when the time comes to fulfill her obligation, she cannot be placed in the position of performing a transgression. Her obligation is therefore effectively neutralized by the vow, even though it was structured indirectly. And since this creates a genuine disruption in the dynamic between them, it falls within {\i beino l\u8217'veina} and he has the power to annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara takes for granted that a wife is {\i meshubdedet} \u8212- obligated \u8212- to have sexual relations with her husband. A wife who refuses is classified in halacha as a {\i moredet}, a rebel. The Rashba grounds this obligation not in a specific biblical verse but in the implicit structure of the marital bond: when a couple stands under the {\i chuppah}, the commitment they undertake includes mutual sexual fidelity and availability. It is not merely a contractual arrangement but a fundamental dimension of what marriage is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam offers a different account, one rooted in the {\i kinyan} model of {\i kiddushin}. On the Rambam\u8217's view \u8212- as elaborated by the Netziv and implicit in the Rambam\u8217's own formulation \u8212- the act of {\i kiddushin} involves an acquisition of the woman for marital purposes; her body, in the context of sexual relations, is in a sense her husband\u8217's domain. Therefore, she cannot make forbidden to herself what does not, on this account, fully belong to her to make forbidden. This reasoning is halachically potent but ideologically fraught, and can be read as providing halachic cover for marital coercion. The meaning of {\i kofin} in this context \u8212- whether it means legal compulsion or physical force \u8212- is itself disputed, and there is a documented history of women bringing complaints to rabbinic authorities and receiving inadequate redress on exactly these grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Custom Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara closes with a teaching about the weight of established practice: {\i devarim hamutarim ve\u8217'acheirim nahagu bahem issur, ee ata rashai linhog bahem heiter kedei levatlam} \u8212- if something is genuinely permitted but a community has adopted a practice of treating it as forbidden, one may not simply act in defiance of that practice to demonstrate its technical permissibility. The basis is once again {\i lo yachel devaro} \u8212- even informal speech and practice carry a kind of moral weight that demands respect. This is in the same register as Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling: even when there is no binding {\i neder}, the words and practices that people have taken seriously are not to be dismissed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Annulling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matir vs.\u160 ?Meifer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the Ran raises a distinct and important question: can a husband serve as the one who is {\i matir} \u8212- who permits \u8212- his wife\u8217's vows, rather than simply being {\i meifer}, the one who annuls? The two roles are conceptually different. {\i Hafarah} is the husband\u8217's specific statutory power to annul within the marriage framework. {\i Hatarat nedarim} is the rabbinically prescribed process of releasing a vow through a sage or panel. The question is whether a husband can function in the latter capacity for his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vested Interest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran\u8217's analysis turns on the principle of {\i nogea badavar} \u8212- an interested party. If the husband has a personal stake in the vow being dissolved \u8212- because it is a {\i beino l\u8217'veina} matter, or because it implicates him in some way \u8212- he cannot serve as {\i matir}, since an interested party is disqualified from serving in that role. But if the vow does not affect him, the question becomes whether his status as her husband \u8212- governed by the principle of {\i ishto k\u8217'gufo}, that a wife is as one\u8217's own body \u8212- disqualifies him from serving as {\i matir} even when he has no direct interest. This is an open dispute between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Yehuda in a {\i mishnah}, and the question of how we {\i pasken} on it remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 82\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Inuy Nefesh vs.\u160 ?Beino L\u8217'\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bathing & Makeup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf picks up from the bottom of 81b, where the Gemara had been wrestling with the distinction between {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- vows of genuine self-denial over which a husband holds broad annulment power \u8212- and {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}, matters bearing on the intimate spousal relationship alone. The discussion established that according to the {\i Rabbanan}, a woman\u8217's vow not to bathe and not to wear makeup constitutes {\i inuy nefesh}. According to Rabbi Yosi, however, those same restrictions do not rise to the level of {\i inuy nefesh} but rather fall into the category of {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabanan vs.\u160 ?R. Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara had further examined whether even Rabbi Yosi\u8217's category of {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina} would encompass such vows, and concluded that it would. This distinction matters because the two categories carry different halachic weight: {\i inuy nefesh} allows for full annulment, while {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina} grants the husband only a more circumscribed power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Limited Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara underscored an important limitation: even where a vow qualifies as {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}, the husband can annul only that portion of the vow that directly affects the marital relationship between the two of them. He cannot, by virtue of that annulment, remove the vow\u8217's effect on her interactions with all other people. This sets the stage for the Gemara\u8217's next inquiry.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tashmish HaMita Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Question Posed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resuming two lines from the bottom of 81b, the Gemara records that Rava posed a question to Rav Nachman: {\i tashmish hamita l\u8217'Rabbanan} \u8212- the denial of marital relations according to the {\i Rabbanan} \u8212- does it constitute {\i inuy nefesh}, or does it fall instead under {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The framing of this question is significant. For Rabbi Yosi, we had asked whether the denial of bathing was even sufficient to qualify as {\i beino l\u8217'veina}. The {\i Rabbanan}, by contrast, operate with a considerably lower threshold for {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- they are more ready to classify deprivations as genuine self-affliction. The question is therefore whether the {\i Rabbanan} would regard the denial of sexual relations as crossing into {\i inuy nefesh}, or whether by its very nature it remains a matter fundamentally about the husband-wife bond and therefore categorized as {\i beino l\u8217'veina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman responded that the answer may be derived from a {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} teaches the case of a woman who declares {\i netulah ani min hayehudim} \u8212- that she is \u8220"removed from Jews\u8221" \u8212- meaning she has forbidden herself from receiving sexual benefit from any Jew. In such a case, the husband {\i yafar chelko}, he annuls his portion of the vow, with the result that {\i u\u8217'meshameshta}, she may have marital relations with him. However, should the couple ever divorce, she remains forbidden to all other Jewish men \u8212- an outcome that would make remarriage exceedingly difficult. What the {\i mishnah} thus demonstrates is that the husband\u8217's power extends only to that dimension of the vow that affects him personally. He cannot remove the vow\u8217's restriction vis-\u224 ?-vis the rest of the world. The implication of Rav Nachman\u8217's proof is that denial of sexual relations is treated as {\i beino l\u8217'veina} rather than as {\i inuy nefesh}, since if it were {\i inuy nefesh} the husband would possess full annulment power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava pushes back: {\i l\u8217'Rabbanan ki ba\u8217'ei lach} \u8212- that was the question I asked according to the {\i Rabbanan}. But the {\i mishnah} Rav Nachman cited is {\i Rabbi Yosi katani lah} \u8212- it reflects the view of Rabbi Yosi. And Rabbi Yosi, as established, holds a far more restrictive definition of {\i inuy nefesh}. For Rabbi Yosi, bathing and makeup do not constitute {\i inuy nefesh}, so it follows naturally that sexual relations, too, would fall outside {\i inuy nefesh} and be classified as {\i beino l\u8217'veina} at most. The question was what the {\i Rabbanan} hold, and citing a {\i mishnah} that reflects Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position provides no answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution to Rabbi Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a striking observation: {\i de\u8217'amar Rav Huna kulei pirkan Rabbi Yosi hi} \u8212- Rav Huna states that the entire remainder of this {\i perek} is authored by Rabbi Yosi. The basis for this claim is a textual cue in the {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} concludes with the phrase {\i divrei Rabbi Yosi}, explicitly attributing a statement to Rabbi Yosi. The Gemara reads this as not merely closing out one ruling but as introducing Rabbi Yosi\u8217's voice as the governing voice for all subsequent {\i mishnayos} in the {\i perek} \u8212- {\i mikan v\u8217'eilach Rabbi Yosi hi}. This is a remarkable hermeneutic move: the repetition of {\i divrei Rabbi Yosi} at the end of a {\i mishnah} is taken as signaling that Rabbi Yosi is the {\i stam} author of what follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence is that Rava\u8217's objection stands: the {\i mishnah} Rav Nachman cited, which shows that {\i tashmish} qualifies only as {\i beino l\u8217'veina} and not {\i inuy nefesh}, reflects Rabbi Yosi\u8217's narrower standard. It offers no guidance regarding the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nature of Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore leaves the question unresolved. The Ran, in the narrow columns, notes that since the question was not definitively answered \u8212- {\i baya v\u8217'lo ipashta} \u8212- we adopt the stringent position: {\i nekitinan l\u8217'chumra v\u8217'eino meifer ela devarim she\u8217'beino l\u8217'veina}. The husband cannot perform a blanket annulment of such a vow; his power extends only to its {\i beino l\u8217'veina} dimension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran, however, adds that some authorities infer from a later statement of Shmuel \u8212- which we will encounter shortly \u8212- that even without explicit Talmudic resolution, {\i tashmish} falls within the {\i inuy nefesh} category according to the {\i Rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ambiguity is genuinely striking. The {\i rishonim} note \u8212- in connection with other halachic discussions, such as the question of whether a woman in a dangerous medical situation may use certain forms of birth control \u8212- that the {\i halacha} cannot simply counsel a couple not to have marital relations. Sexual relations are regarded as constitutive of marriage itself. Given that, the notion that a permanent denial of sexual relations might not rise to the level of {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- while not bathing does \u8212- presents an intuitive challenge that the Gemara\u8217's inconclusiveness does not fully resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The classification of {\i inuy nefesh} also raises a related question of method. The Gemara appears to operate with formal, categorical judgments: once a category of deprivation \u8212- food, bathing, makeup \u8212- is deemed {\i inuy nefesh}, it applies as a class regardless of individual variation. The {\i mishnah} does not ask whether a particular woman cares greatly about makeup; it classifies makeup categorically. This formal approach raises questions about scope and duration that the Gemara gestures toward but does not resolve here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a statement of Shmuel transmitted in the name of Levi: {\i kol nedarim ba\u8217'al meifer l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- a husband may annul all of his wife\u8217's vows, with one exception. If she declares that a specific individual may not receive benefit from her, the husband cannot annul it, because {\i she\u8217'eino meifer} \u8212- such a declaration imposes no deprivation on her; another person\u8217's inability to benefit from her is not {\i inuy nefesh} for her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Source Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here is Shmuel\u8217's {\i chiddush}: {\i hana\u8217'as Ploni alai meifer} \u8212- if she says \u8220"I may not receive benefit from Reuven,\u8221" the husband {\i can} annul it. This is categorized as {\i inuy nefesh}. The innovation is that {\i inuy nefesh} is not assessed by asking: if this vow stands, will she actually be living in an afflicted state? The answer to that question might well be no \u8212- there are many other people who can provide her with fruit, assistance, or whatever benefit she has forsworn. Instead, Shmuel holds that the determination depends on whether the {\i type} of thing being denied is intrinsically a form of {\i inuy nefesh}. If fruit in general constitutes {\i inuy nefesh}, then denial of fruit from even a single source is sufficient to invoke the {\i inuy nefesh} category, and the husband may annul the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant {\i chiddush} \u8212- and, as the Gemara immediately observes, one that appears to collide directly with our {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i peirot medinah zo alai} \u8212- if a woman declares that the fruit of a particular country is forbidden to her, {\i yavi lah mimedinah acheret} \u8212- her husband should bring her fruit from a different country, and since she retains access to the same type of item through another source, the vow does not constitute {\i inuy nefesh}, and the husband cannot annul it. This is a direct challenge to Shmuel\u8217's position: the {\i mishnah} explicitly rules that if another source is available, there is no {\i inuy nefesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Difficult Reading Propos\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To salvage Shmuel\u8217's position, Rav Yosef \u8212- and the Gemara working through this \u8212- proposes a forced reading of the {\i mishnah}. The case, on this reading, is not that she simply forbade herself from a country\u8217's fruit; rather, she said: \u8220"I may not have the fruit of this country {\i that you, my husband, bring.}\u8221" On this interpretation, she has not really denied herself access to that fruit at all \u8212- she can still obtain it through anyone else. Because the deprivation is purely contingent on the husband\u8217's role as provider, and she retains full access through any other channel, it falls outside {\i inuy nefesh} and the husband cannot annul it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with this reading is not merely that it strains the plain meaning of the {\i mishnah}. It also requires reconciling it with a subsequent case in the same {\i mishnah}: {\i peirot Chonvoni ze alai} \u8212- if she says that the fruit of a specific storekeeper is forbidden to her, the {\i mishnah} rules that the husband likewise cannot annul it. The Gemara suggests the same forced reading: she is only denying herself the storekeeper\u8217's fruit as conveyed by her husband; anyone else may still bring it to her. This reading is admittedly far from {\i peshat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sole Provider Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The forced reading collapses under the {\i mishnah}\u8217's own internal evidence. The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i lo hayta parnasato ela mimenu} \u8212- if the husband\u8217's sole source of food provision is this very storekeeper, then {\i harei ze yafer}, the husband may annul the vow. The reason is that in such a circumstance, she is genuinely denied access to that fruit regardless of any alternative channel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the entire {\i mishnah} is about vows she made only contingent on the husband being the one who conveys the fruit \u8212- then even in the case of the sole-provider storekeeper, her access is not actually blocked: someone other than the husband could simply go to that same storekeeper and bring the fruit to her. Why, then, does the {\i mishnah} permit annulment in the sole-provider case? The forced reading cannot account for this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah\u8217's Plain Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes: {\i ela mide\u8217'seifa delo maisi ba\u8217'al} \u8212- since the end of the {\i mishnah} clearly envisions a case where the husband\u8217's conveyance is not the operative variable, it follows that the {\i reisha} likewise is not conditioned on who brings the fruit. She has forbidden the fruit to herself categorically, regardless of the source. {\i Harei reisha ein yachol lehafer} \u8212- the husband cannot annul in the opening case simply because she can always obtain the same fruit elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The plain reading of the {\i mishnah} thus stands as a straightforward rejection of Shmuel\u8217's position. The {\i mishnah} holds that so long as another source exists, the vow does not constitute {\i inuy nefesh}, and the husband has no power to annul it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Shmuel has a response: {\i u\u8217'matnitin Rabbi Yosi hi} \u8212- as Rav Huna established, the entire {\i mishnah} in this {\i perek} reflects Rabbi Yosi\u8217's view. For Rabbi Yosi, who operates with a narrow definition of {\i inuy nefesh}, it makes sense that deprivation must be total \u8212- from all sources everywhere \u8212- before {\i inuy nefesh} applies. The {\i Rabbanan}, who operate with a broader category, would hold that even denial from a single source suffices. Shmuel therefore aligns himself with the {\i Rabbanan} and is undisturbed by the {\i mishnah}\u8217's apparent contradiction, since that {\i mishnah} represents Rabbi Yosi\u8217's stricter position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The corollary noted parenthetically by the Gemara \u8212- {\i meifer nedarim she\u8217'beino l\u8217'veina} \u8212- is that even where Shmuel cannot establish full {\i inuy nefesh}, the {\i beino l\u8217'veina} avenue may yet be available, though the Ran notes this is not a logical continuum: something may simply not bear on the marital relationship at all and therefore not qualify on either ground.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Loaves Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Loaves Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a new case, transmitted by Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel. A woman takes a vow forbidding herself from two loaves of bread. One is fine white bread \u8212- {\i b\u8217'achat nit\u8217'aneh}, a genuine deprivation not to be permitted to eat it. The other is bran bread or a stale inferior loaf \u8212- {\i b\u8217'achat ein nit\u8217'aneh}, something whose loss imposes no meaningful affliction. Half of her single, unified vow falls within {\i inuy nefesh}; half does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note the underlying assumption: that even a vow about a single loaf of bread \u8212- a narrow scope \u8212- is treated categorically as {\i inuy nefesh} once food of that quality is in the {\i inuy nefesh} class. The Gemara does not evaluate the magnitude of the deprivation in context; it reasons formally from the category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel rules: {\i mitoch shehu meifer l\u8217'mit\u8217'aneh, meifer l\u8217'she\u8217'eino mit\u8217'aneh} \u8212- because the husband can annul the portion of the vow that involves genuine {\i inuy nefesh}, he can annul both portions of the vow. The vow was made as a single, unified utterance; and since the husband holds annulment power over part of it, Shmuel extends that power to the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran explains the logic carefully. The Gemara will establish later that the husband cannot perform a {\i hafara} on only {\i part} of a vow: {\i hafara} is all-or-nothing. He cannot say \u8220"I annul this vow for three days but not the rest,\u8221" or \u8220"I annul it for these fruits but not those.\u8221" The {\i pasuk} indicates that his act of {\i hafara} must operate on the vow as a whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this constraint, the husband confronts two options: either he has no power at all \u8212- because he cannot do a complete {\i hafara} of a vow over which he only has partial authority \u8212- or we must extend his authority to cover the whole vow, precisely because only a complete {\i hafara} is a valid act of {\i hafara}. Shmuel adopts the latter position: since the husband is entitled to annul the {\i inuy nefesh} portion, and since {\i hafara} can only be executed on the vow in its entirety, we conclude that he is therefore empowered to annul the whole. The Ran articulates this not merely as a consequence of {\i hutar miktzato hutar kulo} \u8212- the principle that once part of a prohibition is dissolved the rest dissolves with it \u8212- but as a direct requirement of how {\i hafara} functions: the act itself must engage the entire vow, and so the husband must be regarded as authorized to perform it completely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beino L\u8217'Veina Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder how this all-or-nothing logic squares with the earlier case of {\i netulah ani min hayehudim}, where the husband annuls only {\i chelko} \u8212- his portion \u8212- leaving the prohibition with respect to all other men intact. The Ran explains that {\i beino l\u8217'veina} is categorically different in kind from {\i inuy nefesh} annulment. When a husband annuls a vow on grounds of {\i beino l\u8217'veina}, he is not undoing the {\i neder} wholesale; he is exercising an override rooted in the fact that the vow impinges upon {\i their} relationship. The annulment is inherently relational and partial in nature \u8212- it removes the vow\u8217's force to the degree that it affects him, while leaving the rest untouched. This is why, for instance, if the couple divorces, the prohibition reasserts itself: the {\i hafara} never dissolved the underlying {\i neder}, it only suspended it within the bounds of the marriage. {\i Beino l\u8217'veina} annulment is by its very nature targeted and relational, not a complete removal of the {\i neder}. A {\i hafara} grounded in {\i inuy nefesh}, by contrast, is a substantive abrogation of the {\i neder} itself \u8212- and therefore must be complete.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yochanan\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Annulment Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- or in one version, Rav Assi in the name of Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- disagrees with Shmuel\u8217's position on the two-loaves case. His ruling is {\i meifer l\u8217'mit\u8217'aneh v\u8217'eino meifer l\u8217'she\u8217'eino mit\u8217'aneh}: the husband annuls only that portion of the vow that constitutes {\i inuy nefesh}, and the part that is not {\i inuy nefesh} remains in force. Rabbi Yochanan does not accept the all-or-nothing logic that Shmuel invokes. In his view, the husband\u8217's authority is precisely calibrated to his domain: he has complete power over {\i inuy nefesh} and no power beyond it, so the non-{\i inuy nefesh} component of the vow stands unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection against Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position from a {\i mishnah} concerning {\i nezirus}. The {\i mishnah} teaches: if a woman took a vow of {\i nezirus} and was going around drinking wine and contracting corpse-impurity \u8212- thereby violating her {\i nezirus} \u8212- she receives lashes. If, however, her husband annulled the {\i nezirus} without her knowledge, and she continued drinking wine and contracting impurity thinking she was violating it, she does not receive lashes \u8212- because objectively, the {\i nezirus} had already been dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's challenge focuses on this case. The objection asks: if Rabbi Yochanan is correct that a husband can only annul the {\i inuy nefesh} portion of a vow, perhaps when the husband annulled the {\i nezirus} he only annulled the wine prohibition \u8212- which is the aspect that causes genuine deprivation, {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- while leaving intact the prohibition against grape skins and grape pits, and possibly the {\i tumas meis} prohibition. If so, might she still be liable for violating those remaining prohibitions, even after his annulment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite is Package Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef resolves the objection: {\i ein nezirus l\u8217'chatzoim} \u8212- there is no such thing as a partial {\i nezirus}. A {\i nezirus} is a unified, indivisible package. One cannot be a {\i nazir} only with respect to wine, or only with respect to {\i tumas meis}. The obligations of {\i nezirus} \u8212- the prohibition of wine and all grape products, the prohibition of corpse-impurity, and the prohibition of cutting one\u8217's hair \u8212- constitute an all-or-nothing commitment. Therefore, when the husband annulled his wife\u8217's {\i nezirus} by virtue of his power over the {\i inuy nefesh} aspect \u8212- the wine prohibition \u8212- the entire {\i nezirus} necessarily dissolved, because there is no intermediate state of a partial {\i nezirus} that could remain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is what distinguishes the {\i nezirus} case from the two-loaves case. In the two-loaves case, it is entirely possible for the vow to stand as to one loaf and be annulled as to the other \u8212- there is a coherent residual state. In {\i nezirus}, no such residual state exists. And because {\i ein nezirus l\u8217'chatzoim}, the husband\u8217's annulment of the wine aspect brings the entire edifice down. There is therefore no contradiction with Rabbi Yochanan: his {\i hafara} works precisely as he describes \u8212- it annuls the {\i inuy nefesh} component \u8212- and as a consequence, because of the indivisible nature of {\i nezirus}, the whole vow dissolves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye accepts Rav Yosef\u8217's resolution but sharpens the formulation. Rav Yosef said {\i ein nezirus l\u8217'chatzoim} \u8212- there is no half-{\i nezirus} \u8212- which speaks to the qualitative indivisibility of {\i nezirus}: one cannot be a {\i nazir} for only some of the prohibited categories. Abaye adds the parallel point about duration: {\i ein nezirus l\u8217'chatzoim bein korban l\u8217'chatzi nezirus} \u8212- there is no half-{\i nezirus} in duration either, meaning that if a woman began a {\i nezirus} of, say, thirty days and her husband annulled it after fifteen days, she cannot bring the {\i korban} associated with completing a {\i nezirus}, because a {\i korban} requires the full term to have been fulfilled. Abaye wished to make explicit that the indivisibility of {\i nezirus} applies both to its qualitative scope and to its temporal duration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader debate between Shmuel and Rabbi Yochanan regarding the two loaves thus remains intact. Shmuel holds that once the husband possesses authority over the {\i inuy nefesh} component of a unified vow, he must be understood as empowered to annul the whole \u8212- because {\i hafara} is by nature an act on the complete {\i neder}, and empowering him to act on part necessarily requires empowering him to act on the whole. Rabbi Yochanan holds that the husband\u8217's authority is precisely and exclusively coextensive with {\i inuy nefesh}: he annuls everything within that domain and leaves everything outside it entirely untouched.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 83\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Annulling Partial Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda/Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To situate today\u8217's discussion, we begin by recalling the debate introduced earlier on this daf. The gemara had presented the position of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel: when a woman takes a vow encompassing two objects \u8212- one of which constitutes {\i inuy nefesh} (self-affliction) and one of which does not \u8212- and the husband annuls the vow with respect to the part that is {\i inuy nefesh}, the entire vow is dissolved. The question was how to understand the mechanism. One approach would invoke the principle of {\i neder shehufar miktsato hufar kulo} \u8212- a vow annulled in part is annulled in its entirety. The Ran, however, frames it somewhat differently: the Torah empowers the husband to annul the whole precisely because, in order to annul the part to which he is entitled, the annulment must necessarily encompass the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disputes this. In his view, the husband annuls the part that is {\i inuy nefesh} and that part alone; the remainder of the vow stays intact. The gemara had noted that {\i devarim shebeino leveinah} \u8212- matters pertaining specifically to the dynamics between husband and wife \u8212- may operate under a different principle, where the husband annuls his portion while the rest remains. The present debate, however, concerns vows that fall into the category of {\i inuy nefesh}, and it is there that Rabbi Yochanan and Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel diverge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirus Package\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara then challenged Rabbi Yochanan from the case of {\i nezirus}. When a husband annuls his wife\u8217's Nazirite vow, the entire {\i nezirus} falls away, even though strictly speaking the only element of {\i nezirus} that qualifies as {\i inuy nefesh} is the prohibition on wine. The gemara resolved this by invoking the principle of {\i ein nezirus b\u8217'chatzayin} \u8212- there is no such thing as a half-Nazirite. {\i Nezirus} is an indivisible package: one cannot be a {\i nazir} with respect to some of its components while exempt from others. Therefore, even Rabbi Yochanan would agree that when the wine prohibition is annulled, the entire {\i nezirus} collapses \u8212- not because of the general rule about partial annulment, but because {\i nezirus} by its nature admits of no partial status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Korban Clarif.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye sharpens this point further. It is not merely that there is no partial Nazirite status \u8212- there is also {\i ein korban nezirus lachatziyin}: no Nazirite offering can be brought for an incomplete {\i nezirus}. The practical implication is this: even if a woman observed her {\i nezirus} faithfully for, say, fifteen days before her husband annulled it, she was fully a {\i nezirah} throughout that period and would even incur lashes had she willfully violated the {\i nezirus} with proper warning. Nevertheless, since the thirty-day period was never completed \u8212- or whatever term she accepted upon herself \u8212- no {\i korban nezirus} is ever brought. The {\i korban} attaches only to the completion of the Nazirite term, and without completion there is nothing to which the offering can attach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question naturally arises: if a woman accepted {\i nezirus} for sixty days and her husband annulled it after thirty days \u8212- a complete standard term \u8212- does she bring the {\i korban nezirus} then? This is left open; the answer is not clear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tamei in Nezirus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Tamei Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara now introduces a {\i baraita} to challenge the principle that no {\i korban} is brought for an incomplete {\i nezirus}. The case: a woman vowed {\i nezirus}, became {\i tamei} in the course of her {\i nezirus}, separated out a {\i behemah} for the {\i asham} that a {\i nazir} who becomes {\i tamei} is required to bring, and thereafter her husband annulled her vow. What happens?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the structure of the {\i korbanos} brought when a {\i nazir} becomes {\i tamei}. Three offerings are required: an {\i olas ha-of}, a {\i chatas ha-of}, and an animal for the {\i asham}. The baraita tells us the woman separated out the animal for the {\i asham} and then the husband annulled. It then states: she brings the {\i chatas ha-of} but not the {\i olas ha-of}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot flag a striking textual difficulty here: the baraita mentions that she separated out the {\i behemah} for the {\i asham} and then says nothing further about it. It does not tell us whether she brings it or what becomes of it. Tosafot leave this as an unresolved question, and there is no obvious answer beyond what they themselves acknowledge. In any event, the focal point for the gemara is the {\i chatas ha-of}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas Ha-Of Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara presses: if the principle of {\i ein korban nezirus lachatziyin} is correct and no {\i korban} is brought for an incomplete {\i nezirus}, why is this woman bringing a {\i chatas ha-of} at all? Surely the fact that she is bringing even one of the Nazirite {\i korbanos} demonstrates that some obligation persists even when the {\i nezirus} is cut short.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think, before getting to the answer, that the {\i korbanos} associated with {\i tum\u8217'ah} are a categorically distinct matter. A {\i nazir} who becomes {\i tamei} brings those offerings in the middle of the Nazirite term, not at its conclusion \u8212- their purpose is to restart the {\i nezirus}, not to mark its completion. Still, one could reason that even if she is no longer a {\i nezirah} by virtue of the husband\u8217's annulment, she had become {\i tamei} and was obligated in those {\i korbanos} at the time of her {\i tum\u8217'ah}, and perhaps that obligation persists. So the gemara\u8217's challenge has force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not General Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara deflects by turning the challenge back on itself: if bringing a {\i korban} for an incomplete {\i nezirus} were in fact the rule, the baraita should have simply said that. It should have told us straightforwardly that a woman whose {\i nezirus} is annulled mid-term brings her standard three {\i korbanos} \u8212- {\i chatas}, {\i olah}, and {\i shelamim}. The fact that the baraita instead presents a narrow, specific case \u8212- {\i tum\u8217'ah} in the middle of {\i nezirus}, with only one of the three {\i korbanos} brought \u8212- is itself the proof. Clearly, the general rule is that a woman whose {\i nezirus} is terminated before completion brings no standard {\i korbanos}. This case is exceptional on multiple fronts: it arises specifically because of {\i tum\u8217'ah}, and it involves only the {\i chatas ha-of} rather than the full complement of offerings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas Ha-Of Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara then provides the positive account of why the {\i chatas ha-of} is uniquely suited to this situation. It draws on a principle from an entirely different context. The gemara elsewhere discusses the case of a {\i yoledes} \u8212- a woman who gave birth or suffered a miscarriage \u8212- where there is doubt as to whether her circumstances genuinely created {\i tumas leida}. Was the miscarriage after forty days of conception or before? Such a woman finds herself in a bind: if she is indeed {\i tamei}, she cannot eat {\i korbanos} or enter the {\i Beis Hamikdash} until she brings her {\i korban leida}, which is a {\i chatas ha-of}. But if she is not {\i tamei}, she cannot bring a {\i chatas} to which she has no genuine obligation. The Torah resolves this through a {\i mesorah} \u8212- a tradition derived from {\i pesukim} \u8212- that she may bring a {\i chatas ha-of al hasafek}, a {\i chatas ha-of} in a state of doubt. The blood is sprinkled, the bird is not eaten, and she becomes {\i tehorah}. This establishes that the {\i chatas ha-of} is a category of {\i korban} capable of functioning in inherently ambiguous and indeterminate situations, unlike most {\i korbanos} which demand a definite, concrete obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chatas ha-of} is thus not merely a {\i korban} for a known {\i chet}; it is a {\i korban} designed to address a gray zone \u8212- a situation that is {\i nisht ahin, nisht aher}, neither fully resolved one way nor the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that foundation in place, the resolution becomes clear. This woman \u8212- who became {\i tamei} during her {\i nezirus} and then had the {\i nezirus} annulled by her husband \u8212- finds herself in precisely such a gray zone. She genuinely did become {\i tamei} and incurred an obligation in {\i korbanos} at that time. Yet she is no longer a {\i nezirah}, so the purpose of those {\i korbanos} \u8212- to restart the {\i nezirus} \u8212- no longer applies. She is thus in an inherently indeterminate state. Conveniently, the {\i korban} she needs is a {\i chatas ha-of}, which is the very type of offering the Torah designates for such ambiguous situations. She therefore brings the {\i chatas ha-of}, not because it represents a general rule that {\i korbanos} are brought for incomplete {\i nezirus}, but because the {\i chatas ha-of} is precisely the instrument for addressing this kind of gray-area obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rishonim add a further dimension from another sugya dealing with this same baraita: there is an opinion of Rabbi Shimon that a {\i nazir} is considered a {\i chotei} \u8212- one who has sinned \u8212- because he deprived himself of wine, {\i shetzi\u8217'er atzmo min hayayin}. For this reason as well, there is value in the {\i chatas ha-of} even once the {\i nezirus} is no longer in force, as an element of atonement for the very essence of the Nazirite deprivation. The bottom line: the {\i chatas ha-of} in this case proves nothing about a general obligation to bring {\i korbanos} for an incomplete {\i nezirus}. The rule of {\i ein korban nezirus lachatziyin} stands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine vs.\u160 ?Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restate Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara now returns to the central debate \u8212- whether a husband can selectively annul only the {\i inuy nefesh} portion of a vow \u8212- and brings the same baraita again: a woman vowed {\i nezirus}, became {\i tamei}, and the husband then annulled. She brings the {\i chatas ha-of} and not the {\i olas ha-of}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hypothetical Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara now poses a pointed question. Recall that Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position is that the husband annuls only the {\i inuy nefesh} portion of a vow and not the rest. Applied to {\i nezirus}: the {\i inuy nefesh} of {\i nezirus} is the wine prohibition. The prohibition against becoming {\i tamei l\u8217'meis} \u8212- the prohibition on contracting corpse-impurity \u8212- is not obviously {\i inuy nefesh}. So suppose the husband\u8217's {\i hafarah} annulled only the wine element of the {\i nezirus}. What happens to the {\i tum\u8217'ah} element?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the annulment left intact the prohibition against becoming {\i tamei}, then she is still a {\i nezirah} with respect to {\i tum\u8217'ah}. And if that is so, then the {\i korban} she must bring upon becoming {\i tamei} is not merely the limited {\i chatas ha-of} of a gray zone \u8212- it is the full complement of {\i korbanos} appropriate to a {\i nazir} who became {\i tamei} and must restart the {\i nezirus}, because she still has an active {\i nezirus} to restart (at least regarding {\i tum\u8217'ah}). The fact that the baraita says she brings only the {\i chatas ha-of} and not the {\i olas ha-of} would then be difficult to explain: why not the full set of {\i korbanos} for a {\i nazir} who became {\i tamei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran explains the {\i hava amina} underlying this challenge. It is known that there exists a form of {\i nezirus} called {\i Nazir Shimshon} \u8212- modeled on Samson, who avoided cutting his hair and abstaining from wine but regularly contracted {\i tumas meis} in the course of killing Philistines. The gemara derives from this that the prohibition on {\i tumas meis} is in principle separable from the other elements of {\i nezirus}. If so, one might entertain the idea \u8212- at least as a {\i hava amina} \u8212- that the husband\u8217's annulment of the wine prohibition left in place a {\i nezirus} limited to the {\i tumas meis} prohibition, and that she therefore has an ongoing {\i nezirus} to complete and a full {\i korban tamei nazir} to bring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran notes that this is a difficult position; we have never before heard of a regular {\i nezirus} that covers only {\i tumas meis} without wine and hair. The only exception is {\i Nazir Shimshon}, which is explicitly labeled as such. Nevertheless, the gemara entertains it for the sake of the answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah = Inuy Nefesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara responds: {\i tumas meis} is also {\i inuy nefesh}. Not being permitted to become {\i tamei l\u8217'meis} \u8212- not being able to attend funerals, visit a {\i beis kvoros}, go to a {\i beis avel} \u8212- is itself a form of self-denial. The proof is from the verse {\i v\u8217'hachai yiten el libo} \u8212- the living shall take it to heart. The baraita expounds in the name of Rabbi Meir: one who eulogizes others will be eulogized when he dies; one who weeps for others will be wept over; one who buries others will be buried. The message is that participating in communal mourning and burial is a fundamental social responsibility and act of belonging. Being barred from that \u8212- being unable to go to a funeral, to enter a {\i beis kvoros}, to fulfill one\u8217's obligations as a member of the community \u8212- is a genuine {\i inuy nefesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This carries a significant practical implication beyond the Nazirite context. If a woman were to vow that she will not become {\i tamei} or visit a cemetery or attend a funeral, the husband would be able to annul that vow, since it qualifies as {\i inuy nefesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Package Deal Affirmed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i tumas meis} established as {\i inuy nefesh}, both the wine prohibition and the {\i tum\u8217'ah} prohibition fall within the domain of {\i inuy nefesh}. When the husband\u8217's {\i hafarah} operates on the {\i nezirus}, it annuls the entire {\i nezirus} at once \u8212- wine and {\i tum\u8217'ah} together. Since the {\i nezirus} is entirely terminated, there is no ongoing Nazirite status of any kind, and therefore no obligation to bring the standard {\i korbanos} of a {\i nazir} who became {\i tamei}. The only {\i korban} that persists is the {\i chatas ha-of}, for the reasons articulated above. The principle that {\i nezirus} is an indivisible package is thus reaffirmed: the husband cannot annul only the wine element and leave the {\i tum\u8217'ah} element standing. The entire {\i nezirus} falls away at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran notes that even if the gemara could have relied solely on the package-deal principle \u8212- {\i ein nezirus b\u8217'chatzayin} \u8212- to explain why the husband cannot annul only one element, by adding the point that {\i tumas meis} is itself {\i inuy nefesh} the gemara achieves a {\i nafka mina}: it establishes that a standalone vow against contracting {\i tumas meis} or attending funerals is annullable on its own merits, not merely as a consequence of the indivisibility of {\i nezirus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel and Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- whether the husband who annuls the {\i inuy nefesh} portion of a mixed vow thereby annuls the whole \u8212- thus remains unresolved by the Nazirite case, precisely because in {\i nezirus} every element qualifies as {\i inuy nefesh} and the entire structure collapses together.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Benefit from All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Stated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara moves to the next mishnah. A woman declares {\i konam she-ani nehenis l\u8217'vriyos} \u8212- \u8220"let it be {\i konam} that I receive benefit from people,\u8221" meaning she forbids herself all benefit from other human beings. The mishnah rules: the husband cannot annul this vow. This is striking. If any vow is {\i inuy nefesh}, one would think it is this one \u8212- forbidding oneself benefit from all of humanity. Why can he not annul it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, which the gemara will develop, is that when a woman says \u8220"all people,\u8221" she implicitly excludes her husband. The husband can still provide for her, sustain her, and serve as her conduit to the world. Since she is not wholly cut off \u8212- her husband remains available as a source of support \u8212- it is not considered {\i inuy nefesh} at the level that would trigger the husband\u8217's right of annulment. This is admittedly a counterintuitive result, and the gemara will need to work through several layers of analysis to make it coherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should also be borne in mind that the entire chapter follows the position of Rabbi Yossi, who maintains a particularly narrow definition of {\i inuy nefesh}. That narrowness will inform the answers that follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran anticipates an obvious difficulty. Earlier in the tractate there is a case involving a {\i chanvani} \u8212- a storekeeper. If a woman vows not to benefit from a particular storekeeper, and that storekeeper is the only one who will sell to her household, the husband can annul the vow, because sealing off that single source of supply constitutes {\i inuy nefesh}. But then: in the present case the woman has sealed off the entire world of people. Why is that not {\i inuy nefesh}, if blocking one storekeeper is?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran resolves this with a precise distinction. In the storekeeper case, the woman vowed not to benefit from {\i the fruit of that storekeeper}. This is a prohibition on the object \u8212- those specific goods. Even if her husband goes and purchases the fruit from that storekeeper and brings it home to her, she is still receiving the fruit of that storekeeper; the husband\u8217's role as intermediary does not change the identity of the forbidden object. If that store is the only available source of food, the vow completely seals off all food, regardless of who physically retrieves it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intermediary Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the present case, however, the woman did not say \u8220"the objects of all people are forbidden to me.\u8221" She said she will not receive {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- benefit \u8212- from people. {\i Hana\u8217'ah} here means direct benefit. The prohibition attaches to the act of direct receipt, not to the objects themselves. This creates a crucial aperture: the husband can go to any person in the world, receive benefit from them on his own account, and then convey it to her. When she receives it from his hand, she is receiving benefit from her husband, not from those other people. Since the objects themselves are not the forbidden item \u8212- only the direct interaction is \u8212- and since the husband can serve as an intermediary, she retains access to the entire world\u8217's resources through him. Therefore, this does not rise to the level of {\i inuy nefesh} under Rabbi Yossi\u8217's stringent standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah adds that she may still benefit from {\i leket}, {\i shichachah}, and {\i pe\u8217'ah} \u8212- the agricultural gifts left for the poor \u8212- since these have been rendered {\i hefker} (ownerless) by their original owners. Collecting them is not receiving benefit from a person; it is taking from what has become ownerless. This further moderates her situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah then addresses a related case: if a woman vows that {\i Kohanim u\u8217'Levi\u8217'im} shall not benefit from her, they may take {\i trumah u\u8217'ma\u8217'aser} against her will, because the obligation to give those gifts is not in her control \u8212- it is an entitlement of the {\i Kohanim} and {\i Levi\u8217'im} independent of her will. If she specifies {\i these particular Kohanim and Levi\u8217'im}, she may direct her {\i trumah u\u8217'ma\u8217'aser} to others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara then presses the logic of the mishnah. If the husband cannot annul because she is still being sustained by him \u8212- {\i efshar d\u8217'mitazna midilei} \u8212- this implies {\i michlal d\u8217'ba\u8217'al lav b\u8217'chlal briyot hu}: the husband is not included in the category of {\i briyot} when she makes this vow. The gemara then reads the end of the mishnah \u8212- {\i v\u8217'yachola l\u8217'ehanot b\u8217'leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah} \u8212- and observes that if she is still being sustained by the husband, she is not poor, and would not be entitled to collect {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah} under ordinary agricultural law. The mention of {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah} thus implies she is not being supported by the husband, which seems to contradict the premise that the husband is excluded from her vow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Benefit from All (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara presents three distinct positions in response to this difficulty. Each takes a different stance on whether the husband is included within the term {\i briyot} when a woman declares {\i konam she-ani nehenis l\u8217'vriyos}, and each reads the mishnah accordingly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ulla: Husband Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla answers: the husband is fundamentally not included in the category of {\i briyot} when she says \u8220"all people.\u8221" When a woman uses this formulation, she means all other people \u8212- not her husband, who stands in an entirely different relationship to her. It is therefore not {\i inuy nefesh}, because her husband can still provide for her directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leket Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mention of {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah} in the mishnah, according to Ulla, is a supplementary point: even setting aside the husband\u8217's support, she has an additional avenue \u8212- the ownerless agricultural gifts. The mishnah is telling us both reasons she is not sealed off from the world, and the primary reason the husband cannot annul is the husband\u8217's own continued availability.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava: Hefker Solves It\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees. In his view, the husband is included within {\i briyot} \u8212- she has genuinely forbidden herself benefit from everyone, including him. The reason the husband cannot annul is not that she can still receive from him, but solely that she can still be provided for from {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah}, which is {\i hefker} and therefore not benefit from any person. Under Rabbi Yossi\u8217's exceedingly narrow conception of {\i inuy nefesh}, the mere availability of ownerless food is sufficient to prevent this from rising to the level of {\i inuy nefesh} that would empower the husband to annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran finds this position deeply difficult. The very reason the {\i chanvani} case constitutes {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- even according to Rabbi Yossi \u8212- is that sealing off a primary food source is real deprivation. But in the {\i chanvani} case, the woman could in principle collect {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah}. Why didn\u8217't Rabbi Yossi say there too that it is not {\i inuy nefesh} because she can still get food from {\i hefker}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seasonal Leket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ran proposes a resolution that is itself startling: perhaps the {\i chanvani} case is set in winter, when {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah} is not available because there is no harvest. In summer, when gleaning is possible, even the {\i chanvani} case might not constitute {\i inuy nefesh} under Rava\u8217's reading of Rabbi Yossi. This is an extraordinarily narrow reading of {\i inuy nefesh}, but it follows from Rava\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Nachman: Conditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman returns to the position that the husband is not included in {\i briyot} \u8212- when she says \u8220"all people\u8221" she implicitly means everyone other than him. The reason: the husband occupies a categorically different position relative to her while they are married, and it is natural to assume that the vow, in its ordinary meaning, did not encompass him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where Rav Nachman diverges from Ulla becomes clear in how he reads the mishnah\u8217's mention of {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah}. Rav Nachman reads the mishnah as saying: {\i if she is divorced} \u8212- {\i nitgarshah} \u8212- then she is left with {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah}. Once she is no longer married, the husband reverts to the {\i briyot} category, and she can no longer benefit even from him. At that point, her only recourse is the ownerless agricultural gifts. This reading makes the {\i leket shikchah u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah} clause make sense: it describes her situation post-divorce, when the \u8220"exception\u8221" for the husband no longer operates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difference between Ulla and Rav Nachman is thus: according to Ulla, the husband is categorically and permanently outside the scope of the vow \u8212- even after divorce, presumably, the vow never encompassed him. According to Rav Nachman, the husband was always in principle included in {\i briyot}, but the marital relationship created a temporary inapplicability. Once that relationship ends, he falls back into the category to which he naturally belongs, and the vow operates against him as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the Ran into an extended discussion of a broader question: what happens when the circumstances relevant to a vow change during its duration? The gemara elsewhere discussed a case where a woman vowed not to benefit from {\i yordei hayam} \u8212- sailors \u8212- and someone\u8217's status as a sailor changed after the vow was taken. Does the vow\u8217's scope reassess itself as circumstances change?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rav Nachman\u8217's reading, it seems we do reassess: the husband begins as effectively excluded from the vow and then, upon divorce, becomes included. The Ran notes, however, that this may be a special case. The husband was never fundamentally outside the vow \u8212- he was always within the category of {\i briyot} in principle; the marriage simply created an override. When the marriage ends, he does not switch categories; he returns to the category he was always in. This is different from the {\i yordei hayam} case, where the question is about a person genuinely moving from one category to another. Here the husband\u8217's fundamental category never changed \u8212- only the practical applicability of the vow shifted with the marital status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nituah Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nituah Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now challenges Rav Nachman with a proof from another mishnah. That mishnah concerns a woman who declares that she is {\i netuah} \u8212- removed, meaning she forbids herself sexual pleasure from all people. This is a vow of sexual estrangement that encompasses the entire world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava argues: if the husband were truly not included in the category of {\i briyot} when a woman says \u8220"all people,\u8221" then this vow would not touch him at all, and it would already be {\i inuy nefesh} by virtue of excluding everyone else. There would be no need for any further action on the husband\u8217's part. Yet the mishnah states explicitly that the husband annuls his portion \u8212- {\i yafer chelko} \u8212- and then has conjugal relations with her. The necessity of the husband\u8217's annulment demonstrates that he was included in the vow to begin with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Annuls Share\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is that the husband annuls the part of the vow that pertains to him, and she remains forbidden to all other men. This confirms that he was included in the original vow \u8212- otherwise, there would be nothing for him to annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Determines\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gemara resolves the apparent contradiction between this case and the {\i konam she-ani nehenis l\u8217'vriyos} case as follows: {\i shani hacha demuchach miltha} \u8212- here the matter speaks for itself. In the {\i nituah} case, the woman says she is forbidden sexual pleasure from {\i Yehudim} \u8212- from all Jews. But she already has no permitted sexual relationship with anyone other than her husband. If the vow means \u8220"everybody except my husband,\u8221" it is entirely meaningless: she already cannot have relations with anyone else. A vow that says nothing is not a vow. Therefore, in this context, the husband must be included \u8212- otherwise the declaration has no content at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meaningfulness Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The governing principle that emerges is this: whether the husband is included in a formulation of \u8220"all people\u8221" depends on whether the vow has meaningful content without him. In the {\i nituah} case, including only the rest of the world would render the vow pointless \u8212- the husband must be included for it to say anything at all. In the {\i konam she-ani nehenis l\u8217'vriyos} case, by contrast, a vow not to benefit from all other people is entirely meaningful on its own, even without the husband. In such a case, we read the vow according to its natural implication: \u8220"all people other than you,\u8221" and the husband is not within its scope.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 85\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah & Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benefit Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} with which we open Daf 85 is one we have encountered before, still situated within the broader discussion of which {\i nedarim} a husband may annul on behalf of his wife \u8212- specifically, what qualifies as {\i d\u8217'varim shebeino l\u8217'veina} (matters between them) or {\i d\u8217'varim shel inui nefesh} (matters of self-denial). The case the {\i mishnah} presents is the following: a woman declares \u8220"{\i konam she\u8217'ani osa al pi abba, v\u8217'al pi avicha, v\u8217'al pi achi, v\u8217'al pi achicha}\u8221" \u8212- that anything she produces shall be forbidden in benefit to her father, her husband\u8217's father, her brother, or her husband\u8217's brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna Kamma} rules that the husband cannot annul this {\i neder}, and on two distinct grounds. First, it does not qualify as {\i inui nefesh}, since she is not forbidding any pleasure to herself. Second, it does not qualify as {\i d\u8217'varim shebeino l\u8217'veina}: although the practical consequences may certainly annoy the husband \u8212- the couple cannot host her father or his brother for a meal \u8212- the {\i neder} itself does not directly forbid anything as between the two of them. The further ruling of the {\i mishnah} is that if she were instead to say \u8220"{\i she\u8217'ani osa al picha}\u8221" \u8212- what I produce is forbidden to you, the husband \u8212- he does not need to annul even that, because the husband already holds a lien on his wife\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} (the products of her labor). Since it is not fully hers to forbid, the {\i neder} cannot effectively take hold on something that is already his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} disagrees: \u8220"{\i yafer, shema ta\u8217'adif alav yoter min hara\u8217'uy lo}\u8221" \u8212- he should annul the {\i neder}, lest she produce more than she is strictly obligated to provide. Recall the {\i mishnah} in {\i Ketubot} that enumerates the minimum labor a wife is required to perform. If she exceeds that minimum, the surplus \u8212- the {\i hadafah} \u8212- is something the husband has no strict legal entitlement to, even if in practice he would benefit from it. The {\i neder} would therefore take effect on that excess. {\i Rabbi Akiva} thus provides two reasons for annulment: first, it qualifies as {\i d\u8217'varim shebeino l\u8217'veina}; and second, he risks transgressing inadvertently if he benefits from her extra production.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri} takes a different position: \u8220"{\i yafer, shema yigarshena v\u8217'tei\u8217'asur alav}\u8221" \u8212- he should annul it lest he divorce her and she become forbidden to him. His concern is not the {\i hadafah} at all; he may well accept that the husband is entitled to her extra production. His worry is forward-looking: right now the {\i neder} does not take effect because the husband holds a lien on her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}. But if they were to divorce, that lien dissolves, the labor returns to her ownership, and the {\i neder} \u8212- held in a kind of abeyance \u8212- would then kick in and take effect. The consequence would be that they could not remarry, since it would be untenable to live together with him unable to benefit from her work. Better, therefore, to annul it now and remove it entirely, rather than leave it suspended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position of {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri} implicitly establishes something important: an annulment grounded in {\i d\u8217'varim shebeino l\u8217'veina} remains effective even after divorce, at least for the period in which she is not yet remarried. If the annulment would evaporate upon divorce, the entire concern of {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri} would be pointless \u8212- for the annulment would need to be operative precisely in that post-divorce moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the {\i Gemara} is now going to focus on, however, is a more fundamental question: before we even get to the husband\u8217's annulment, how does this {\i neder} manage to remain in abeyance at all? If her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} are not hers at the time she makes the vow, should the {\i neder} not be null and void on the spot? Why is it not simply extinguished the moment it is uttered?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel & Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"{\i Amar Shmuel, halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri}\u8221" \u8212- Shmuel rules in accordance with {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri}, affirming both that the annulment is effective in the post-divorce period and that we must be concerned about what happens if they divorce, even though the {\i neder} has not taken effect at the time of its utterance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately draws a comparison to {\i hekdesh}: \u8220"{\i l\u8217'meimra d\u8217'kasavar Shmuel adam makdish davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam?}\u8221" \u8212- is Shmuel thereby implying that a person can sanctify something that does not yet exist? The parallel is direct: when she declares her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim konam}, those products are not yet hers \u8212- they belong to the husband by right. Yet {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri} holds that the {\i neder} will take effect once she reclaims them upon divorce, just as if someone were to declare an animal he does not yet own as {\i hekdesh}, expecting it to take effect once he acquires it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this creates a contradiction, for the {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: \u8220"{\i hamakdish ma\u8217'asei yedei ishto, harei zu osa v\u8217'ocheles}\u8221" \u8212- if a husband himself sanctifies his wife\u8217's labor as {\i hekdesh}, she may continue working and consuming the proceeds without concern. The reason: at minimum in one reading of the case, the husband is not providing her food, and therefore he holds no entitlement to her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}. Since his rights to her labor depend on his maintenance of her, absent that maintenance his {\i hekdesh} cannot take hold on something he does not own. She also has the recourse of declaring \u8220"{\i eini nizonis v\u8217'eini osa}\u8221" \u8212- I will not be supported by you and I will not work for you \u8212- thereby freeing herself from the arrangement and leaving him with no claim to her labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues with the {\i mosar} \u8212- the surplus beyond her basic obligation. Here, curiously, the text seems to assume the husband {\i is} entitled to it, with {\i Rabbi Meir} declaring it {\i hekdesh} and {\i Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar} declaring it {\i chullin}, non-sanctified. The underlying dispute, as the {\i Gemara} in {\i Ketubot} works out through a rather forced {\i ukimta}, appears to concern whether the husband\u8217's {\i hekdesh} can take effect on something not yet produced \u8212- a {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}. {\i Rabbi Meir} holds it can; {\i Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar} holds it cannot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"{\i V\u8217'amar Shmuel halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar}\u8221" \u8212- and Shmuel rules like {\i Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar}: one cannot sanctify a {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}. This is striking. In one place Shmuel rules like {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri}, apparently validating a {\i neder} on something not yet in the woman\u8217's possession, even potentially years in the future. In another, he rules like {\i Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar}, explicitly rejecting the capacity to make {\i hekdesh} on something not yet existing. How are these two rulings consistent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might attempt to resolve this by reading Shmuel\u8217's ruling in favor of {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri} narrowly \u8212- perhaps he was only endorsing {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri}\u8217's dismissal of {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's concern about the {\i hadafah}, affirming that the husband is entitled to the surplus as well and that there is therefore no danger of inadvertent transgression. But the {\i Gemara} rejects this reading: if that were all Shmuel intended, he should have simply ruled like the {\i Tanna Kamma} \u8212- who says the same thing \u8212- or stated explicitly that the law does not follow {\i Rabbi Akiva} regarding the {\i hadafah}. Saying \u8220"the law follows {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri}\u8221" plainly implies endorsement of that opinion\u8217's central and distinctive claim: that the husband must annul lest the {\i neder} take effect upon divorce. And that claim presupposes that such a {\i neder} can remain in abeyance and later take effect \u8212- which looks very much like sanctifying a {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konamos Different\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} offers a resolution that will be familiar from an earlier {\i sugya}: \u8220"{\i shani konamos}\u8221" \u8212- vows expressed through the {\i konam} formula are different. The principle he invokes is that a person can make another\u8217's fruit forbidden to himself, even without owning it. Since {\i nedarim} can operate on objects one does not own \u8212- making them forbidden {\i to oneself} \u8212- they can also apply to things that do not yet exist. This is in contrast to {\i hekdesh}, where the act of sanctification is fundamentally one of ownership transfer, which requires that one actually possess the object being transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hands as Entity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} presses back on {\i Rav Yosef}\u8217's solution with precision: \u8220"{\i bishlama adam oser peiros chaveiro alav} \u8212- granted, I can make your fruit forbidden to me, {\i sheharei adam oser peirosav al chaveiro} \u8212- because you can also make your own fruit forbidden to someone else.\u8221" The logic requires that at least one of two relationships hold: either the person making the {\i neder} controls the object (forbidding his own fruit to others), or he controls himself (forbidding himself from another\u8217's fruit). But there must be a genuine hook somewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the woman is attempting, however, is something different: she is trying to make her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} \u8212- which (a) does not yet exist, and (b) she will not be entitled to until some indefinite future point if and when she is divorced \u8212- forbidden {\i to her husband}. That is doubly problematic. She neither controls the object (it is not yet produced and belongs to the husband by lien) nor is she making it forbidden to herself. The rule that {\i nedarim} work on things one doesn\u8217't own applies only when the one making the {\i neder} is making it forbidden to himself. But forbidding something to another person \u8212- \u8220"{\i eino oser peiros chaveiro al chaveiro}\u8221" \u8212- that she cannot do. So we are back to the fundamental question: how does {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri}\u8217's position work at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Hands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: \u8220"{\i ela amar Rav Huna brei d\u8217'Rav Yehoshua b\u8217'omeres yikdeshu yadai l\u8217'oseihem}\u8221" \u8212- the resolution lies in the formulation of the vow itself. When the woman declares her hands forbidden, she is not making a vow about an abstract future object that does not exist. She is making a vow about her hands \u8212- the physical, present, tangible hands \u8212- declaring them forbidden for what they produce, {\i l\u8217'ma\u8217'aseihem}. The hands are here, in the world, right now. Whatever the precise phrasing \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} notes that this phrase may have originated in the context of actual {\i hekdesh} sanctification, where {\i l\u8217'oseihem} could refer to the Creator, but is here being repurposed or applied by analogy \u8212- the operative point is \u8220"{\i yadai isneihu b\u8217'olam}\u8221": the hands exist in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer addresses one dimension of the {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam} problem \u8212- that the product has not yet been produced. By re-framing the object of the {\i neder} as the hands themselves rather than their future output, the {\i Gemara} resolves the question of non-existence. But the second dimension of the problem immediately reasserts itself: \u8220"{\i meshubada yadehah l\u8217'ba\u8217'al}\u8221" \u8212- her hands are subject to the husband\u8217's lien. If the {\i neder} is to take effect only {\i lich\u8217'she-tisgaresh}, upon divorce, we are once again dealing with a future contingency that she does not presently control. How, given Shmuel\u8217's position that one cannot sanctify a {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}, does this work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Analogies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Ila} attempts to demonstrate through analogy that the concept should be intuitively workable. He envisions someone saying to a friend: \u8220"{\i sadeh zu she-ani mocher lach, lech-she-ekachena mimmeka takdish}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"this field I am about to sell you, when I buy it back from you I will sanctify it.\u8221" {\i Rabbi Ila} assumes that this pre-sanctification would indeed take effect at the moment of repurchase. The intuitive basis is that at the time of the declaration, the field is fully in the owner\u8217's control; he could sanctify it right now if he wished. He is therefore performing a genuine act of sanctification in the present, with the effect deferred to the moment the field returns to his control after the intervening sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirmiyah Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} challenges this analogy sharply: \u8220"{\i mi dami?}\u8221" \u8212- how can you compare the two cases? In the field case, at the moment the declaration is made, the field is entirely in the owner\u8217's control \u8212- \u8220"{\i hashta be-yadeih}\u8221" \u8212- and he could sanctify it immediately if he chose. But is a married woman similarly positioned with respect to her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}? She is not. The moment she is married, she no longer has the capacity to freely sanctify her labor. The more apt analogy, {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} argues, would be: \u8220"{\i sadeh zu she-macharti lach, lech-she-ekachena mimmeka takdish}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"this field I have already sold you, when I buy it back it will be sanctified.\u8221" But this formulation exposes the problem rather than solving it: if you have already sold the field, you cannot now sanctify it. The past ownership is irrelevant; what matters is the present, and right now you have no rights to it. The same is true for the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collateral Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} takes a different approach, arguing that {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah}\u8217's counter-analogy is too extreme in the other direction. When one sells a field outright, \u8220"{\i pesika miltayhu}\u8221" \u8212- the matter is entirely settled, the field is completely out of the seller\u8217's hands. But the woman\u8217's situation is not like that. The husband has a lien on her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}, but it is not the case that her hands have been fully alienated from her. She retains title; it is just that the husband\u8217's lien encumbers all benefits flowing from that title. A better analogy, {\i Rav Pappa} proposes, is: \u8220"{\i sadeh zu she-mashkanti lach, lech-she-efdenah mimmeka takdish}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"this field that I have given you as collateral, when I redeem it from you it will be sanctified.\u8221" In this case the field has not been sold; the owner retains title, but the holder of the collateral is entitled to all its produce. This maps neatly onto the wife\u8217's situation: she retains title to her hands, but the husband, by virtue of the marital arrangement, is entitled to all their productive output. {\i Rav Pappa} assumes intuitively that since title remains with the owner, one has sufficient grounds to make a declaration today that will take effect upon redemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Control Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet bar Rav Idi} objects: \u8220"{\i mi dami? sadeh beyado l\u8217'fdotah}\u8221" \u8212- the field analogy still fails because in the collateral case, it is in the owner\u8217's power to redeem the field. If he gathers enough money, he can pay off the lender and reclaim the field. The decision to redeem is his. But \u8220"{\i ishah beyadah l\u8217'garesh?}\u8221" \u8212- is it in the woman\u8217's power to bring about a divorce? It is not. The husband decides whether to divorce her. She cannot compel the situation. Since she is not in a position to reclaim her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} through her own action, how can her present declaration have any operative force?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} then attempts a refined analogy: a field given as collateral for a period of ten years, during which redemption is contractually prohibited. If the owner declares \u8220"{\i lichi afdena mimcha titkadesh}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"when I do redeem it after the ten years it will be sanctified\u8221" \u8212- that should work, since after the ten-year period has elapsed the right to redemption automatically comes into effect. At least there is a fixed endpoint. But this only sharpens the problem with the woman\u8217's case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No End-Point\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} presses further: \u8220"{\i hatam kayitz}\u8221" \u8212- in the field case, there is a defined terminus. After ten years, the period of encumbrance ends by its own terms. But \u8220"{\i ishah mi it lah kaitza?}\u8221" \u8212- by the woman, there is no fixed end-point at all. The marriage could last indefinitely. There is no moment that is structurally guaranteed to arrive at which she will regain her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim}. If the woman lacks the capacity to redeem her labor and there is no fixed point at which the situation must resolve in her favor, what possible basis does she have for making a declaration today that will take effect in that indeterminate future?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kedushat Haguf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity Transform\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having exhausted the analogies, {\i Rav Ashi} now provides the genuine resolution: \u8220"{\i shani konamos, d\u8217'hachi kedushat haguf dami}\u8221" \u8212- {\i konamos} are different, because they operate like {\i kedushat haguf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand this, one must distinguish between two modes of sanctification. {\i Kedushat damim} \u8212- sanctification of monetary value \u8212- is what occurs when one donates a field or animal to {\i bedek habayit}, Temple maintenance. The object is not fundamentally transformed; it simply changes ownership, in effect entering {\i hekdesh}\u8217's account, to be sold and converted into funds. Because this is a transaction in the domain of ownership, all the standard rules governing ownership apply: one can only sanctify what one owns, liens can block the transfer, and so forth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kedushat haguf} \u8212- sanctification of the body or substance of the object \u8212- is categorically different. When one consecrates an animal as a {\i korban}, its very identity is transformed. It is no longer merely a cow that belongs to {\i hekdesh}; it has become an {\i olah}, a sacrifice. The {\i kedushah} has redefined what it is, not merely who holds title to it. Because the sanctification is operating on the plane of identity rather than ownership, the ordinary rules of ownership and lien do not apply in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} invokes the principle stated by Rava: \u8220"{\i hekdesh chametz veshichrur mafki\u8217'a midei shi\u8217'abud}\u8221" \u8212- sanctification, {\i chametz}, and the freeing of a slave override existing liens. A {\i konam}, {\i Rav Ashi} argues, functions like {\i kedushat haguf} \u8212- it transforms the identity of the object rather than simply transferring its ownership. And because the woman retains title to her hands even though the husband holds a lien on their output, she has just enough of a foothold to invoke this identity-transforming power. The husband\u8217's monetary lien cannot block a process that is not operating in the monetary domain of ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the slave provides the most vivid illustration. Suppose a creditor holds a lien on a slave owned by his debtor. If the debtor were to attempt to sell that slave to a third party, the creditor could intervene: the lien encumbers the slave, the debtor lacks the freedom to transfer ownership against the creditor\u8217's interest. But if the debtor frees the slave, the result is different. Freeing a slave is not a transfer of ownership from one party to another \u8212- it is a transformation of the slave\u8217's fundamental status. He is no longer a slave; he is now a free man. The creditor cannot assert that his monetary lien gives him rights over a free person. The process of changing the slave\u8217's status operates on a different plane from the monetary arrangements, and because the owner still retains title despite the lien, the transformation takes effect. The creditor may still be owed money, and in principle he retains a claim, but he may well lose the practical ability to enforce it through that particular asset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Chametz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic applies when an encumbered animal is consecrated as a {\i korban}. The creditor cannot interpose his monetary lien to prevent the animal from achieving its new identity as a sacrifice. And similarly with {\i chametz}: if someone failed to destroy his {\i chametz} before {\i Pesach} and the {\i chametz} is now {\i assur behana\u8217'ah}, a non-Jewish creditor operating within the halachic framework cannot claim his lien on the {\i chametz} remains actionable, because the {\i chametz}\u8217's identity has fundamentally changed. Its transformation into something forbidden overrides prior monetary claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In each of these cases, the key feature is that the one effecting the transformation retains some form of title even if a lien encumbers the object\u8217's use. That residual title is sufficient to activate the identity-transforming power, and once activated, monetary liens fall away as irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied to the {\i mishnah}: the woman\u8217's declaration of {\i konam} on her hands is, by the mechanism {\i Rav Ashi} describes, not merely a declaration of ownership-based prohibition but an invocation of {\i kedushat haguf}-like power through the {\i konam} formula. Since she retains title to her hands even during the marriage \u8212- it is only the {\i benefit} of her labor that the husband has a lien on \u8212- that title is sufficient for the {\i konam} to take hold and transform their status. The husband\u8217's lien, being a monetary arrangement, cannot block a process of identity transformation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 When Does It Work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution, however, immediately generates a new difficulty. The {\i Gemara} asks: \u8220"{\i ee hachi, lama lee shema yigarshena?}\u8221" \u8212- if {\i konamos} carry this identity-transforming power that can override liens, why does {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri} frame his entire concern around the scenario of divorce? If the {\i konam} works by {\i kedushat haguf} and thereby mafki\u8217'a the husband\u8217's lien, it should take effect right now, not only upon divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds by emending the reading of {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri}: \u8220"{\i tani ve\u8217'od shema yigarshena}\u8221" \u8212- read the text as saying \u8220"and furthermore, lest he divorce her,\u8221" meaning that the {\i konam} does take effect immediately, and the concern about divorce is an additional consideration layered on top, not the sole or primary mechanism. The {\i Ran} grapples with this interpretation, acknowledging that it sits somewhat uneasily in context. For if one accepts that the {\i konam} takes effect right now \u8212- which {\i Rav Ashi}\u8217's principle would support \u8212- then the {\i Tanna Kamma} and {\i Rabbi Akiva} simply do not accept that the {\i konam} has this power. The {\i Tanna Kamma}, in particular, likely holds that while {\i kedushat haguf} does {\i mafki\u8217'a midei shi\u8217'abud} in cases of actual {\i korban}, {\i hekdesh}, or {\i shichrur}, a mere {\i neder} \u8212- even one formulated as a {\i konam} \u8212- does not carry that same transformative capacity, and therefore the husband\u8217's lien blocks it entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri}\u8217's response, as rendered after the {\i Gemara}\u8217's emendation, is that the {\i konam} does work now by virtue of this principle, and {\i uve\u8217'od} \u8212- and even if one does not accept the full philosophical argument that it takes effect immediately \u8212- one should at the very least be concerned that it will become operative upon divorce. The circularity of that position is not lost on the {\i Gemara}: if one rejects the underlying principle, one would presumably reject the post-divorce concern as well, since both rest on the same foundation. How the {\i Ran} resolves this is its own discussion, but it is a notably striking way for the {\i sugya} to conclude \u8212- with the logical tension between the two positions left in some degree of productive suspension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 86\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Specificity in Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mistaken Identity Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on 86b continues the discussion of a husband\u8217's power of {\i hafarah}, shifting focus from the content of vows \u8212- what constitutes {\i inuy nefesh} or {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina} \u8212- to the mechanics of the act of annulment itself. The {\i mishnah} presents a series of cases in which a husband attempted to annul or affirm a vow under some form of mistaken identity or misclassification. {\i Nedar ishto vesavar shenadar bito} \u8212- his wife took a vow but he thought it was his daughter, or {\i nedar bito vesavar shenadar ishto} \u8212- his daughter took a vow but he thought it was his wife. {\i Nadra benazir vesavar shenadra bekorban} \u8212- she vowed to be a {\i nazir} but he thought she used {\i korban} language, or the reverse. {\i Nadra mite\u8217'einim vesavar shenadra min ha\u8217'anavim} \u8212- she vowed not to eat figs but he thought the vow was about grapes, or vice versa.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In each case, the husband was mistaken either about the identity of the woman whose vow he was acting upon, the subject matter of the vow, or the type of the vow. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i harei zeh yachzor veyafer} \u8212- he must go back and annul properly if he wants the annulment to take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle emerging from the {\i mishnah} is that for an act of {\i hafarah} \u8212- and as the {\i gemara} will clarify, {\i kiyum} as well \u8212- to be valid, the husband must know for whom he is acting, what object the vow concerns, and what type of vow it is. The {\i mishnah} is not primarily concerned with the subjective reasons a husband might have for preferring to annul one vow over another, though one could argue that the reason he might annul a {\i nazir} vow differs from his reason for annulling a {\i korban} vow, and similarly that what he wishes to annul for his wife may differ from what he wishes to annul for his daughter. The {\i mishnah} treats these as formal requirements of knowledge rather than merely motivational ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing from Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether a mistaken annulment resets the clock of {\i beyom shamo} \u8212- the requirement that the husband act on the day he hears the vow. The answer appears to be no. Since in all these cases the husband did know he had the power to annul and did know he was dealing with a {\i neder} he could address, the original time period is not reset. The clock runs from the day he actually heard the vow. The {\i mishnah} does not treat this category of error as analogous to cases \u8212- discussed in upcoming {\i mishnayot} \u8212- where the husband did not realize he had the power to annul or did not recognize the vow as one he could address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mode of Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One question that arises is how a husband could mistake his wife for his daughter or vice versa. The simplest reading is that he heard from behind a door and confused their voices. The {\i Ran}, however, writes that {\i she\u8217'amru lo shebito nadra umitoch kach hefer o kiyem} \u8212- it was reported to him incorrectly, and on the basis of that report he annulled or affirmed. This is somewhat puzzling, since an earlier discussion established that the husband must hear the vow personally, which was the basis for ruling that he cannot appoint a {\i shaliach} to hear on his behalf. How the {\i Ran}\u8217's formulation fits that requirement is a question that deserves further attention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Otah Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} takes up the {\i mishnah} with the observation: {\i l\u8217'memra d\u8217'otah davka} \u8212- the word {\i otah} (\u8220"her\u8221") in the relevant verse is specific. He must know precisely who the woman is whose vow he is annulling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knowing the Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} here explains: {\i dichtiv v\u8217'ini otah d\u8217'mashma she-makkir ha-ishah ha-noderes} \u8212- the word {\i otah} implies recognition of the woman who made the vow, {\i umashma nami hadichtiv v\u8217'heifer et nidrah} \u8212- and the phrase \u8220"her vow\u8221" implies that he must also recognize the specific vow. {\i Tosafot}, by contrast, understands the word {\i otah} in {\i v\u8217'ini otah} to refer not to the woman but to the vow itself. We noted earlier that the plain meaning of the verse is that {\i v\u8217'ini otah} means \u8220"prevent her\u8221" \u8212- the woman \u8212- while {\i nidrah} refers to the vow. Thus the {\i Rosh} draws on both to derive both requirements, while {\i Tosafot} reads {\i otah} as referring to the vow directly. Either way, the conclusion is the same: the husband must know both the woman and the vow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Explicit vs.\u160 ?Interna (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Keriah Wrong Person\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the husband must have specific knowledge, the {\i gemara} raises a challenge from {\i keriah} \u8212- the rending of garments in mourning. The verse speaks of David rending his garments {\i al Shaul v\u8217'al Yehonatan beno}, explicitly naming those for whom the rending was performed. One would therefore expect that rending garments for the wrong person ought not to count, just as annulling the wrong person\u8217's vow does not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraisa} is cited: {\i amru lo met aviv ve-kara} \u8212- someone was told his father had died and he rent his garments, {\i ve-achar kach nimtza beno} \u8212- and it turned out it was actually his son who had died. The {\i beraisa} rules {\i yatza yedei keriah} \u8212- he has fulfilled his obligation of {\i keriah}, even though the act was performed under a mistaken identity. This seems directly analogous to our case, and yet the ruling goes the other way. The verse contains {\i al} \u8212- just as specific as {\i v\u8217'ini otah} \u8212- and still we do not require that the {\i keriah} be performed for the correct person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i beraisa} appears to contradict the first: {\i amru lo met aviv ve-kara, ve-achar kach nimtza beno, lo yatza yedei keriah} \u8212- he did not fulfill the obligation. The {\i gemara} resolves this apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya, ha bistam ve-ha bemeforesh} \u8212- it is not difficult; the two {\i baraitot} address different cases. The distinction turns on whether the act was explicitly labeled as being for a particular person or whether the mistaken identity was only internal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Mislabel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} explains the resolution more precisely. The first {\i beraisa}, which rules that {\i keriah} counts even though it was done for the wrong person, is not describing a case where they explicitly told him his father had died. Rather, {\i be-she\u8217'amru lo met} \u8212- they told him only that someone had died, {\i ve-savar aviv ve-nimtza beno} \u8212- and he assumed it was his father, when in fact it was his son. Since the act was not explicitly labeled as a rending for the father, {\i keriah} performed in the context of a family member\u8217's death is valid even though his internal assumption was wrong. The second {\i beraisa}, which rules that {\i keriah} does not count, describes the case where they told him explicitly that his father died \u8212- that is, the act was explicitly labeled as being for the father \u8212- and then it emerged that it was the son. An explicitly mislabeled act cannot be reassigned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle \u8212- that what matters is not internal intention but external, explicit labeling \u8212- is striking. One might have thought that {\i keriah} could tolerate internal error because it is an outward, physical demonstration in a real-world context of loss, whereas an act of {\i hafarah} or {\i hakamah} requires much more specific engagement with the substance of the vow, and {\i kiyum} in particular requires no verbal act at all. That the same principle of external labeling would govern all of these is somewhat counterintuitive, though the {\i gemara}\u8217's development of the {\i keriah} analogy pushes in precisely that direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apply to Annul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Translating this principle back to {\i hafarah} and {\i hakamah}: as long as the husband has not explicitly labeled his act as an annulment of a specific person\u8217's specific vow \u8212- as long as he simply heard a vow and declared {\i meifer ani} \u8212- the act is not mislabeled. Even if internally he was thinking about the wrong woman or the wrong vow, the act stands: there was a vow, he had the power to annul, and he performed the act of {\i hafarah}. It is only where he explicitly designated the act \u8212- \u8220"I am annulling my daughter\u8217's vow\u8221" \u8212- and it turned out to be his wife\u8217's vow, that the {\i hafarah} fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a surprising extension of the {\i keriah} analogy. The sense in which an act of mourning has objective meaning in the context of a real death \u8212- regardless of what was in the mourner\u8217's mind \u8212- is not identical to the sense in which a husband\u8217's act of annulment has meaning regardless of whom he was thinking of. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's logic treats the two as parallel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Toch Kedei Dibur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} goes further: {\i kan betoch kedei dibbur kan le\u8217'achar kedei dibbur} \u8212- the difference between the two {\i baraitot} on {\i keriah} is not a question of explicit versus internal labeling, but of timing. Even if the act was explicitly mislabeled \u8212- even if they told him his father had died and he rent his garments \u8212- if the discovery that it was actually his son occurred {\i betoch kedei dibbur}, within the time window of {\i toch kedei dibbur}, the act can be redefined and the {\i keriah} counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneity Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i toch kedei dibbur} \u8212- roughly, \u8220"within the time of speech,\u8221" understood as approximately the time it takes to greet a teacher ({\i shalom alecha rabbi}) \u8212- normally applies to verbal acts, where a speaker can reconsider and amend a declaration within an immediate window. It is less intuitive when applied to a physical act like {\i keriah}, where there is no speech to extend or amend. What {\i Rav Ashi} seems to be saying is that the discovery itself, occurring {\i toch kedei dibbur}, allows the earlier act to be retrospectively redefined \u8212- not by redoing anything, but simply by virtue of the temporal proximity of the new awareness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Keriah Discovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} supports this with a {\i beraisa}: {\i mi sheyesh lo choleh b\u8217'toch beito} \u8212- someone has a gravely ill person at home, {\i ve-chedumeh shemet} \u8212- and the person appeared to have died (stopped breathing), {\i ve-kara} \u8212- and he rent his garments, {\i ve-achar met} \u8212- and then the person actually died shortly afterward. {\i Lo yatza yedei keriah} \u8212- he did not fulfill the obligation. Then comes a gloss attributed to {\i Rav Shimon ben Pazi} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} in the name of {\i Bar Kappara}: {\i lo shanu ela shemet achar k\u8217'dei dibbur} \u8212- this ruling applies only if the person died after {\i k\u8217'dei dibbur} had elapsed; {\i aval b\u8217'toch k\u8217'dei dibbur k\u8217'dibbur dami} \u8212- but if the person died within {\i toch kedei dibbur}, it counts, because within that window, the moment of the act and the moment of the death are treated as contemporaneous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The combined teaching of this section is therefore: first, {\i keriah} need not be intentionally directed at the correct person \u8212- it must only not be explicitly mislabeled; second, even explicit mislabeling can be corrected if the correction or discovery occurs {\i toch kedei dibbur}; third, the same principles apply by analogy to {\i hafarah} and {\i hakamah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Explicit vs.\u160 ?Interna (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Keriah Before Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most extreme and philosophically difficult implication of {\i Rav Ashi}\u8217's ruling is the {\i beraisa} just cited: the man rent his garments before the person had actually died, and yet it counts as valid {\i keriah} provided the death followed {\i toch kedei dibbur}. This is a genuinely difficult case. It is one thing to say that an act performed in the correct context \u8212- someone has died, a family member rends garments \u8212- is valid even if the mourner\u8217's internal assumptions were wrong. But here the precondition for {\i keriah} \u8212- actual death \u8212- had not yet obtained at the moment the act was performed. How can an act be valid for an event that had not yet occurred?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Toch Kedei Dibur (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Condition Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question is genuinely difficult. For {\i toch kedei dibbur} to apply to a matter of intent or a verbal act, one can say that the speaker\u8217's mind or declaration remains open and continuous within that window, allowing a later moment within it to redefine an earlier one. But here the difficulty is ontological: you are trying to pull a future event \u8212- the actual death \u8212- backward to give meaning to a prior act. It is not merely a question of redefining one\u8217's frame of mind; the very condition that would make the act halachically meaningful did not exist yet.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time Granularity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One possible resolution is that {\i halachah} measures time in {\i toch kedei dibbur} increments, not in seconds. The clock of {\i halachah}, so to speak, does not tick in units finer than {\i toch kedei dibbur}. Just as the principle of {\i i efshar letzamtzem} addresses the impossibility of perfect precision, one could suggest that {\i halachah} treats the entire span of {\i toch kedei dibbur} as a single temporal unit \u8212- anything occurring within it is halachically simultaneous with anything else in that window. Under this reading, the {\i keriah} and the death occur in the same halachic moment, even if, measured by an ordinary clock, the death came fractionally later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretation does have limits. The {\i gemara} rules elsewhere that certain acts cannot be remedied even within {\i toch kedei dibbur}. For example, if a husband betroths a woman who at that same moment receives a {\i get} from a previous husband, the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect. So the principle of halachic simultaneity does not resolve every case. The temporal-granularity approach may explain the {\i keriah} case \u8212- where one is merely relabeling an act in the context of a real death \u8212- but the full philosophical implications of {\i toch kedei dibbur} as applied to preconditions that do not yet exist remain genuinely challenging.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exceptions to Toch\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes with the operative {\i halachah}: {\i v\u8217'hilchasa, toch kedei dibbur k\u8217'dibbur dami} \u8212- the rule of {\i toch kedei dibbur} applies as a matter of law, {\i chutz mimigadef v\u8217'oved avodas kochavim u\u8217'mekadesh u\u8217'mgaresh} \u8212- except for blasphemy ({\i megadef}), idolatry ({\i oved avodah zarah}), betrothal ({\i kiddushin}), and divorce ({\i gerushin}). In these four cases, a retraction or correction within {\i toch kedei dibbur} has no legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All the {\i rishonim} note that this entire framework \u8212- the general principle and its exceptions \u8212- appears to be {\i d\u8217'oraisa} in its application. The {\i mishnah} we are analyzing concerns {\i hafarah} and {\i hakamah}, which are themselves {\i d\u8217'oraisa} matters. This creates a significant difficulty for the view of {\i Rabbeinu Tam}, who held that {\i toch kedei dibbur} is a rabbinic {\i takkanah} designed to ease commercial transactions by giving parties a brief window to reconsider. The {\i gemara} here applies {\i toch kedei dibbur} across a range of {\i d\u8217'oraisa} contexts without any indication that it is a rabbinic overlay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weighty Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 No explicit verse is cited as the source for excluding the four cases. The {\i rishonim} explain it on the basis of {\i sevara} \u8212- logic. The {\i Ran} writes: {\i bishar mili d\u8217'lav chamiri kulei hai, k\u8217'she-adam oseh otam lo b\u8217'gemar da\u8217'ato hu oseh} \u8212- in ordinary matters of life, a person acts without having fully made up his mind, always holding a small reservation, {\i da\u8217'ato she-yuchal lachazor bo toch k\u8217'dei dibbur} \u8212- expecting that he can still reconsider within that brief window. The act is undertaken with a kind of built-in tentativeness, which is what {\i toch kedei dibbur} accommodates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Finality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i hani kivan d\u8217'chamiri kulei hai, ein adam oseh otam ela b\u8217'haskamah gemurah} \u8212- since these four acts are so weighty, a person never undertakes them without complete and unreserved intention. There is no residual tentativeness to be accommodated. Therefore, {\i mishum hachi chazarah afilu toch k\u8217'dei dibbur lo mahani} \u8212- any attempt to retract is legally null, because the {\i da\u8217'as} at the moment of the act is considered to have been full and final.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One could alternatively formulate the principle as follows: rather than asking about the actor\u8217's internal state, one could say that an act of such weight is itself final by virtue of what it is. The act speaks for itself and cannot be relabeled or retracted, regardless of whether the actor\u8217's frame of mind was technically tentative or not. Whether the focus is on the finality of {\i da\u8217'as} or the finality of the act itself, the functional conclusion is the same: for {\i megadef}, {\i oved avodah zarah}, {\i kiddushin}, and {\i gerushin}, {\i toch kedei dibbur} provides no relief.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Acts Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Figs Grapes Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} introduces a new scenario. A woman declared: {\i konam te\u8217'einim v\u8217'anavim eilu she-ani to\u8217'emet} \u8212- these figs and grapes are forbidden to me regarding my tasting of them. The husband then acted on only one part of the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i kiyam le\u8217'te\u8217'einim, kulo kiyam} \u8212- if he affirmed the vow regarding figs, the entire vow is affirmed. {\i Heifer le\u8217'te\u8217'einim, eino mufar ad she-yafer af la-anavim} \u8212- but if he annulled regarding figs, it is not annulled at all until he annuls the grapes as well. The principle: a partial {\i hakamah} works for the whole {\i neder}, but a partial {\i hafarah} works for nothing. The {\i neder} already exists as a reality; to affirm what is already there, even a partial affirmation is sufficient to root the entire {\i neder} in place. To annul, however, requires rejecting the whole, and a partial annulment accomplishes nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} suggests a different reading of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- namely, that {\i hafarah} on part does work for that part, and the phrase {\i eino mufar} until he annuls the grapes means only that the grapes remain bound until he addresses them separately. But this is not the {\i pshat} of the {\i mishnah}, and the {\i Ran} himself appears to acknowledge as much. The plain reading is all-or-nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separate Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second case in the {\i mishnah}: {\i amar konam te\u8217'einah she-ani to\u8217'emet ve-anavah she-ani to\u8217'emet} \u8212- she used the phrase {\i she-ani to\u8217'emet} separately for each item, applying it to the fig and to the grape individually. {\i Harei hu shenei nedarim} \u8212- this constitutes two separate vows. The consequence is that a {\i kiyum} on one does not establish the other, and a {\i hafarah} on one is effective for that one. The repetition of the verbal formula {\i she-ani to\u8217'emet} suffices to break the single vow into two independent vows, even without repeating the word {\i konam} for each item.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael All/Nothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies the {\i mishnah} with {\i Rabbi Yishmael}. A {\i beraisa} is cited to spell out his position: {\i isha yekimenu ve-isha yeferenu} \u8212- the husband shall establish it and the husband shall annul it. If she said {\i konam te\u8217'einim v\u8217'anavim eilu she-ani to\u8217'emet} \u8212- figs and grapes that I shall not taste \u8212- {\i kiyem le\u8217'te\u8217'einim, kulo kiyem} \u8212- if he affirmed for figs, it is all affirmed; {\i heifer le\u8217'te\u8217'einim, eino mufar ad she-yafer af la-anavim} \u8212- if he annulled for figs only, it is nothing until he annuls the grapes as well. These are the words of {\i Rabbi Yishmael}. The {\i mishnah} is thus fully aligned with his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Linked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} disagrees. He reads the juxtaposition in the verse \u8212- {\i isha yekimenu ve-isha yeferenu} \u8212- as linking {\i hakamah} and {\i hafarah} so that they operate with equal efficacy. The {\i gemara} then asks: where does the concept of partial {\i hakamah} producing full effect come from? The {\i drasha} is from the word {\i yekimenu}: the {\i mem} suffix is read as {\i mimmenu} \u8212- \u8220"from it\u8221" or \u8220"of it\u8221" \u8212- yielding {\i yakim mimmenu}, meaning that affirming even a part of the vow constitutes a full {\i hakamah}. From this, {\i Rabbi Akiva} derives by {\i hekesh} \u8212- analogy \u8212- that {\i af yesirenu mimmenu}: just as a partial {\i hakamah} is a full one, a partial {\i hafarah} is also effective. Both work partially and completely in parallel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael} would counter: {\i mi ketiv yafer mimmenu?} \u8212- the word {\i mimmenu} appears only by {\i yekimenu}, not by {\i yeferenu}. You cannot derive the partial {\i hafarah} from the partial {\i hakamah} without a specific textual basis. {\i Rabbi Akiva} responds that the verse {\i hekesh}\u8217's the two \u8212- they are explicitly linked \u8212- so {\i ma hakamah mimmenu af hafarah mimmenu}. The debate is thus clear: both agree that partial {\i hakamah} operates fully; they disagree on whether the same follows for {\i hafarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Hiyya bar Abba} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan} introduces a third opinion, that of the {\i Chachamim}. According to the standard {\i girsa}: {\i ma hafarah ma sheheifer heifer af hakamah ma shekiyam kiyam} \u8212- just as {\i hafarah} works for what was annulled (and only for that), so too {\i hakamah} works only for what was affirmed (and only for that). This reading rejects the all-or-nothing framework entirely: partial {\i hafarah} is effective for the part addressed, and partial {\i hakamah} is effective only for the part affirmed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran}, however, presents an alternative {\i girsa} found in {\i k\u8217'tzat sefarim}: {\i aval chachamim omrim mah hafarah mah shehefir lo hefir af hakama mah shekiyam lo kiyam} \u8212- just as a partial {\i hafarah} does nothing, so too a partial {\i hakamah} does nothing. The {\i Ran} further notes that this is the {\i girsa} of {\i Rabbeinu Moshe bar Nachman} in his {\i halachot}. Under this reading, the {\i Chachamim} represent the third logical possibility in an all-or-nothing framework: Rabbi Yishmael holds that partial {\i hakamah} is everything and partial {\i hafarah} is nothing; Rabbi Akiva holds that both are everything; and the {\i Chachamim} hold that both are nothing. Three logically coherent positions given an all-or-nothing premise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam}, in chapter 13 of {\i Hilchot Nedarim}, halachah 10, rules as follows: {\i asah te\u8217'einim va\u8217'anavim bein bishvuah bein she\u8217'asar atzmo bechol hamin vekiyam lite\u8217'einim vehefir la\u8217'anavim o shekiyam la\u8217'anavim vehefir lite\u8217'einim mah shekiyam kiyam mah shehufar mufar} \u8212- if the figs and grapes were treated as separate matters, and he affirmed the figs and annulled the grapes, or vice versa, what he affirmed is affirmed and what he annulled is annulled. The {\i Rambam} thus follows the standard {\i girsa} of the {\i Chachamim}, ruling that partial {\i hafarah} and partial {\i hakamah} each take effect for their respective objects.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oaths Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} observes: {\i masnitin Rabbi Shimon hi} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} reflects the position of {\i Rabbi Shimon}. The reference is to the debate regarding multiple oaths: {\i de\u8217'amar ad sheyomar shevuah lechol echad ve\u8217'echad} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds that separate liability for each oath requires that each be designated separately. In the case of one who borrowed from five people and denied each debt, a single blanket denial-oath produces only one {\i korban}, whereas separate declarations of {\i shevuah} for each person produce separate obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separation Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to our {\i mishnah} is that combining the figs and grapes in a single vow creates a single {\i neder}, just as combining the oaths in a single declaration creates a single oath. The repetition of {\i she-ani to\u8217'emet} for each item functions analogously to repeating the word {\i shevuah} for each creditor \u8212- it creates the necessary separation. {\i Rava}\u8217's point is that our {\i mishnah}\u8217's requirement of explicit separate language to divide a compound vow into independent {\i nedarim} mirrors {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's requirement in the realm of oaths.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Next Mishnah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Know Vows Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with the opening of the next {\i mishnah}: {\i yodeia ani sheyesh nedarim} \u8212- \u8220"I know that there are vows.\u8221" This sets up the question of when the husband\u8217's time window for annulment begins in a case where he knew vows existed but lacked full knowledge of their specifics \u8212- the timing question that the upcoming discussion will develop further.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 86b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Specificity in Hafara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife vs.\u160 ?Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} on 86b continues the discussion of {\i hafara} by a husband, shifting focus from the categories of {\i inui nefesh} and {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veinah} to the act of {\i hafara} itself. The {\i mishna} presents several cases of confusion: a man\u8217's wife took a vow but he thought it was his daughter, or his daughter took a vow but he thought it was his wife; a woman made a vow using {\i nazir} terminology but he thought she used {\i korban} language, or vice versa; she made a vow not to eat figs and he thought she made a vow not to eat grapes, or the reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These cases span three distinct categories of confusion: the identity of the person whose vow is being acted upon, the object of the vow, and the type of vow made. The distinction between a {\i neder} made using {\i nazir} language and one using {\i korban} language is not merely terminological \u8212- these represent genuinely different types of {\i nedarim}, and the basis on which a husband might wish to annul one could differ substantially from his basis for annulling the other. Of course, one could argue the same for wife versus daughter: what he wishes to annul for his wife may not be what he wishes to annul for his daughter. But it seems the {\i mishna} is not primarily concerned with the substantive motivation in each case. The operative point is structural: you must know whom you are annulling for and what exactly you are annulling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In all these cases of confusion, the {\i mishna} rules: {\i harei ze yachzor veyafer} \u8212- he must go back and be {\i mefer} if he wishes the annulment to take effect. One natural reading is that he attempted {\i hafara} while mistaken, and so he must try again. But the {\i Ran} suggests \u8212- and both readings may in fact be true simultaneously \u8212- that the {\i mishna} also contemplates the case where he was {\i mekayem} the vow under a mistaken impression. Since a {\i kiyum} made in error is not a binding {\i kiyum}, he retains the ability to go back and be {\i mefer}. Whether the act was {\i kiyum} or {\i hafara}, the {\i gemara} will make clear that one must know for whom the vow was made, on what object, and of what type.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reattempt Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a timing question: does the confusion reset the clock, giving him until the end of a new day from when he realized his error? The answer appears to be no. The {\i mishna} seems to assume that the husband knew he was empowered to annul, knew he was dealing with a type of {\i neder} he could annul, and knew all the general circumstances \u8212- he simply had the details wrong. Because he did have that fundamental awareness when he first heard the vow, the operative day remains {\i beyom sham\u8217'o}, the day he heard it. The discovery of his error does not restart the twenty-four-hour window.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 How Husband Hears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A separate question arises: how did the husband confuse his wife for his daughter, or vice versa? One natural answer is that he heard through a door, the voices were similar, and he simply mistook one for the other. However, the {\i Ran} offers a surprising reading \u8212- that the case involves someone reporting the vow to him incorrectly: {\i sha\u8217'amru lo shebito nadra umitoch kach hefer o kiyem}, \u8220"they told him his daughter had vowed, and on that basis he annulled or affirmed.\u8221" This is striking, because earlier in the tractate there was an extensive discussion establishing that the husband must personally hear the vow \u8212- he cannot appoint an agent to hear it on his behalf. The {\i Ran}\u8217's formulation seems to imply that being told about a vow suffices for {\i hafara} to take effect, which sits in tension with that earlier position. This point is worth noting even if a full resolution is not readily at hand.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Explicit Mislabeling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Otah Davka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens its analysis with the phrase {\i lema\u8217'imra di\u8217'otah davka}: the word {\i otah} \u8212- \u8220"her\u8221" \u8212- in the verse {\i yani otah} is precise and exclusive. He must know exactly which woman\u8217's vow he is annulling. Not only is there specificity required regarding the person, but the {\i gemara} draws the same inference from the phrase {\i v\u8217'heifer et nidrah} \u8212- \u8220"he shall annul her vow\u8221" \u8212- meaning he must know the specific {\i neder} he is annulling: {\i she-yakir ha-neder}. The {\i Rosh} here draws both requirements from the two components of the verse: the {\i otah} specifying the woman, and {\i nidrah} specifying the vow. Interestingly, {\i Tosafot} takes a different approach and understands the word {\i otah} by {\i yani otah} as referring not to the woman but to the vow itself. Either way, both {\i Rosh} and {\i Tosafot} arrive at the same practical conclusion: the husband must know both the person and the vow he is annulling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Keriah Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises what appears to be a strong counterexample from the laws of {\i aveilus}. The verses regarding {\i keriah} use the formulation {\i al Shaul v\u8217'al Yehonatan beno} \u8212- explicitly naming for whom the rending is performed \u8212- suggesting that {\i keriah} must be done with a specific person in mind. Yet a {\i brayta} teaches: {\i amru lo meit aviv v\u8217'kara} \u8212- they told him his father died and he rent his garments \u8212- {\i v\u8217'achar kach nimtza b\u8217'no}, and it turned out it was actually his son who had died. Despite this, {\i yatza yedei kriya} \u8212- he fulfilled his obligation. On its face, this seems to contradict the principle that the act must be specifically directed, since the {\i al al} formulation in the {\i pesukim} appears at least as demanding as {\i otah davka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mind vs.\u160 ?Labeling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i ha b\u8217'stam v\u8217'ha b\u8217'meforesh} \u8212- the cases differ based on whether the misdirection was merely internal or was made explicit. What renders an act as being \u8220"for X\u8221" is not what is silently passing through one\u8217's mind, but rather explicit labeling. If a person performs {\i hafara} or {\i kiyum} \u8212- or {\i keriah} \u8212- while thinking of the wrong person, but without that mistake being verbally or explicitly declared, it does not disqualify the act. Only if the act is explicitly labeled as being for the wrong person does it fail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied to the {\i brayta} about {\i keriah}: the case where {\i yatza} is ruled, the {\i Ran} explains, is not actually where they explicitly told him his {\i father} had died \u8212- for then it would be labeled as a {\i keriah} for the father. Rather, the case is where they said to him only that someone died ({\i amru lo meit}), he assumed it was his father ({\i v\u8217'savar aviv}), and then it emerged it was actually his son. Since his assumption was entirely internal and was never made explicit, the {\i keriah} succeeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Keriah Efficacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then brings a second {\i brayta} that distinguishes the two scenarios precisely. Where someone was explicitly told his father had died and rent his garments, and it later emerged it was his son \u8212- {\i lo yatza yedei kriya}, he did not fulfill the obligation. But where they told him only that someone had died ({\i amru lo meit lo meit}), he assumed it was his father, rent his garments, and it turned out it was his son \u8212- {\i yatza yedei keriah}, he fulfilled it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is notable and somewhat counterintuitive. For {\i hafara} and {\i kiyum}, one might expect internal awareness of the correct person to be especially critical \u8212- after all, {\i kiyum} need not even be verbal; the {\i rishonim} earlier in the tractate established that {\i kiyum be-lev} suffices. An act of annulment or affirmation is, by its nature, an expression of approval or disapproval, and it seems to require knowing the actual circumstances, not merely performing the external act in the right general context. {\i Keriah}, by contrast, is a physical act that derives some of its meaning from the objective reality of rupture and mourning, regardless of what is precisely in one\u8217's head. Yet the {\i gemara} draws the analogy, and apparently {\i hafara} and {\i kiyum} similarly do not require specific internal intention toward the correct person \u8212- they require only that the act not be explicitly mislabeled for the wrong person.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Toch Kedei Dibbur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} goes further: {\i kan be-toch kedei dibbur, kan le-achar kedei dibbur} \u8212- the distinction between the two {\i braitot} is not about explicit versus implicit mislabeling at all. Rather, even explicit mislabeling can be corrected, provided the correction occurs within {\i toch kedei dibbur}. This is a significant extension. Normally, {\i toch kedei dibbur} is associated with acts of speech \u8212- the idea that within the immediately subsequent moment one can still define or redefine what one has said. It is more difficult to apply it to a physical act like {\i keriah} where no further speech follows, and where there is no continuing act being performed that one\u8217's subsequent thoughts might redirect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Later Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Rav Ashi}, then, the first {\i brayta} \u8212- where {\i yatza} is ruled \u8212- applies to a case where the discovery was made {\i toch kedei dibbur}, even if the act was explicitly labeled for the wrong person. The second {\i brayta} \u8212- where {\i lo yatza} is ruled \u8212- applies to a case where the discovery came only {\i le\u8217'achar kedei dibbur}, after the window had closed. A further {\i brayta} is adduced in support: someone had an ill person in his house who appeared to have died, and in that belief rent his garments, but it then emerged the person had actually died only a short time afterward. {\i Lo yatza yedei kriya} \u8212- he did not fulfill the obligation. To this, {\i Rav Shimon ben Pazi} in the name of {\i Rav Yehoshua ben Levi} in the name of {\i Bar Kappara} adds the qualification: {\i lo shanu ela shemet le\u8217'achar kedei dibbur} \u8212- this ruling applies only where the actual death followed the {\i keriah} by more than {\i kedei dibbur}. But {\i aval betoch kedei dibbur k\u8217'dibbur dami} \u8212- if the death occurred within {\i kedei dibbur} of the {\i keriah}, it is treated as simultaneous and the {\i keriah} counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Discovery Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last case is perhaps the most difficult in the entire discussion of {\i toch kedei dibbur}. It is one thing to say that an explicit mislabeling can be corrected within {\i toch kedei dibbur} \u8212- one could argue that within that window the act remains in a process of definition, and a corrective realization somehow relabels it. But in the case of the {\i brayta}, the problem is not mislabeling at all. The problem is that at the moment of {\i keriah}, the person had not yet died. The {\i keriah} was performed before the triggering circumstance even existed. For {\i toch kedei dibbur} to salvage this is not a question of relabeling \u8212- it seems to require treating {\i keriah} performed before a death as valid for a death that occurs afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Circumstance Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a fundamental conceptual puzzle. {\i Toch kedei dibbur} is normally understood as an opportunity to correct or redefine an act while still in its immediate orbit. But one cannot straightforwardly correct an act done before the circumstances that would validate it even came into being. This seems categorically different from, say, correcting a mistaken {\i beracha} by immediately saying the right one. The person did {\i keriah} for someone not yet dead, then the person died within {\i kedei dibbur} \u8212- and the {\i gemara} says the {\i keriah} counts. How can one perform an act of mourning before there is anything to mourn?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneity Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most coherent way to understand this is that {\i toch kedei dibbur} is here being deployed as a concept of legal simultaneity rather than as an opportunity for relabeling or correction. {\i Halacha} does not measure time in seconds. It measures in {\i toch kedei dibbur} increments. Anything occurring within the same {\i toch kedei dibbur} window is treated as occupying the same legal moment \u8212- the same \u8220"tick\u8221" of the halakhic clock. On this understanding, the {\i keriah} and the death are legally simultaneous because they fall within the same {\i kedei dibbur} unit, and therefore the {\i keriah} validly marks the death. This is a striking and demanding application of the concept, and it does not obviously generalize. One would not say, for instance, that betrothing a woman takes effect retroactively because she received her {\i get} from a prior husband within {\i kedei dibbur} \u8212- yet the logic of simultaneity would seem to suggest that. Accordingly, it seems more precise to say that the function of {\i toch kedei dibbur} here is specifically about whether the act and the triggering event can be treated as occurring within the same legally indivisible unit of time, in a way that {\i halacha} demands but does not extend indiscriminately to all acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the structure of the {\i sugya}: first, specificity of intent is required \u8212- the act must not be explicitly labeled for the wrong person. Second, even if it is explicitly mislabeled, correction within {\i toch kedei dibbur} remedies this. And the same principles apply, by parallel, to {\i hafara} and {\i hakama}, where \u8212- despite the fact that {\i kiyum} is internal and one would expect much more is demanded by way of specific awareness \u8212- the same permissive framework operates.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exclusions to TKD\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes with a {\i hilchasa}, a ruling on the practical {\i halacha}: {\i toch kedei dibbur k\u8217'dibbur dami} \u8212- within {\i kedei dibbur} is treated as the statement itself. However, there are five categories excluded from this rule: {\i chutz mi-megadef v\u8217'oved avodas kochavim u-mekadesh u-megaresh} \u8212- blasphemy, idolatry, {\i kiddushin}, and {\i geirushin}. If someone uttered a blasphemy and immediately retracted, saying he did not intend God, or someone bowed in worship and then claimed he intended the {\i davening} posture, or someone betrothed a woman and {\i toch kedei dibbur} said he takes it back, or delivered a {\i get} and immediately claimed he did not mean it \u8212- in none of these cases does {\i toch kedei dibbur} provide any remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining the reasoning, an important structural point must be noted. All the {\i rishonim} observe that the framework of {\i toch kedei dibbur} being applied here \u8212- including to {\i hafara} and {\i hakama}, which are {\i d\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- is evidently a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} concept. This creates a serious difficulty for the position of {\i Rabeinu Tam}, who held that {\i toch kedei dibbur} is a rabbinic {\i takkanah} instituted to ease commerce, allowing parties to back out of a transaction within the first few moments. The application of {\i toch kedei dibbur} throughout this passage to biblical-level obligations makes {\i Rabeinu Tam}\u8217's characterization very difficult to sustain, and all the {\i rishonim} raise this objection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} addresses the rationale for excluding the five cases. He writes: {\i u\u8217'l\u8217'chol milei d\u8217'lav chamiri kulei hai} \u8212- for ordinary matters that are not especially weighty, {\i k\u8217'she\u8217'adam oseh osam lo b\u8217'gmar da\u8217'ato hu oseh} \u8212- when a person does them, he never acts with fully resolved intention. There always remains a degree of tentativeness, an implicit reservation, a sense that within {\i kedei dibbur} he can still reconsider. Therefore {\i toch kedei dibbur} allows for retraction. But {\i hani keivan d\u8217'chamiri kulei hai ein adam oseh osam ella b\u8217'haskama gmura} \u8212- these five matters, being of such gravity, are never undertaken unless one\u8217's intention is completely unreserved and final. There is simply no subjective reservation being held. Therefore {\i chazara afilu toch kedei dibbur lo mahani} \u8212- even a retraction within {\i kedei dibbur} cannot undo them, because the {\i da\u8217'as} at the time of the act was already complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acts Speak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative explanation does not focus on the internal state of the actor but on the objective weight of the act itself. Acts of such significance \u8212- idolatry, blasphemy, {\i kiddushin}, {\i geirushin} \u8212- are so weighty that {\i halacha} treats them as speaking for themselves and as inherently final. Even if one were to argue that subjectively the person maintained some reservation, the act itself is of a category that cannot be redefined by subsequent second thoughts. The act\u8217's weight forecloses the possibility of relabeling it through any later awareness or retraction, however immediate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial vs.\u160 ?Whole\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} introduces a new scenario: {\i amrah konam te\u8217'einim va\u8217'anavim elu she\u8217'ani to\u8217'emes} \u8212- a woman vowed that these figs and grapes are forbidden to her in terms of her eating them. {\i Kayam l\u8217'te\u8217'enim kulo kayam} \u8212- if the husband affirmed the vow with respect to the figs, the entire {\i neder} is affirmed. {\i Heifer l\u8217'te\u8217'enim} \u8212- if however he annulled only the figs \u8212- {\i eino mufar ad sheyafer af l\u8217'anavim} \u8212- it is not annulled until he also annuls the grapes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The straightforward reading of this {\i mishna} is that {\i hakama} and {\i hafara} operate on an all-or-nothing basis, but asymmetrically. Since the {\i neder} already exists and is a real, established thing, even a partial affirmation is enough to root and confirm it in its entirety. Annulment, however, must contend with the full {\i neder}: a partial {\i hafara} accomplishes nothing. To erase the vow, the husband must reject it in its entirety. An ounce of {\i kiyum} goes a long way; a partial {\i hafara} does nothing at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} proposes an alternative reading of the {\i mishna} \u8212- that {\i eino mufar ad sheyafer af l\u8217'anavim} means the grapes are not annulled until he annuls them, but the figs {\i are} annulled by the partial {\i hafara}. On this reading, partial {\i hafara} works for the part it covers. This reading is, however, not the {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna}, and the {\i Ran} himself acknowledges as much. The plain sense remains: partial {\i kiyum} affirms everything; partial {\i hafara} accomplishes nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separate Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces a limiting case: {\i amar konam te\u8217'enah she\u8217'ani to\u8217'emes v\u8217'anavah she\u8217'ani to\u8217'emes} \u8212- if she used the word {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'emes} separately for each item, {\i harei elu shenei nedarim} \u8212- these constitute two separate {\i nedarim}. Once the two items are treated as separate {\i nedarim}, the all-or-nothing framework applies independently to each one. Performing {\i kiyum} or {\i hafara} on one does not affect the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The natural question is what makes language sufficiently separating. If she had separately said {\i konam} before each item, they would clearly be two vows. Here she did not repeat the word {\i konam}, but she did repeat the verbal phrase {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'emes} for each item individually. Is that sufficient? The {\i mishna} says it is, though the {\i gemara} will examine how this connects to a broader debate about what constitutes the separation of {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mani matnitin} \u8212- whose view does our {\i mishna} represent? {\i Rabbi Yishmael}. This is established from a {\i brayta} that reproduces the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling almost verbatim: {\i ishah yekimenu v\u8217'ishah yeferenu} \u8212- her husband shall affirm it and her husband shall annul it. If he affirmed the figs, {\i kulo kayam} \u8212- the whole thing is affirmed. If he annulled the figs, it remains fully in force {\i ad sheyafer af l\u8217'anavim} \u8212- until he also annuls the grapes. {\i Divrei Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- these are the words of {\i Rabbi Yishmael}. The {\i mishna} as we have it straightforwardly reflects {\i Rabbi Yishmael}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} disagrees. He begins from the same verse: {\i ishah yekimenu v\u8217'ishah yeferenu}. The linkage of {\i hakama} and {\i hafara} in a single verse implies they must operate with comparable efficacy. But what is the {\i drasha} that produces the rule that even a partial {\i hakama} works? {\i Rabbi Akiva} reads {\i yekimenu} as {\i yakim mimenu} \u8212- the {\i mem} in the word becomes the preposition {\i min}, yielding: he affirms even {\i from} it, meaning from a portion of it. A partial {\i hakama} of even one component affirms the entire vow. And since {\i hafara} is {\i hekesh}-ed to {\i hakama} by the same verse \u8212- {\i mah hakama mimenu af hafara mimenu} \u8212- partial {\i hafara} likewise works as a full {\i hafara}. On {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's view, both {\i hakama} and {\i hafara} operate symmetrically: partial works as complete for both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael} would respond that the {\i drasha} of {\i mimenu} applies only to {\i yekimenu} \u8212- there is no such formulation by {\i yesirenu}. {\i Rabbi Akiva} would counter that the {\i hekesh} between them in the verse bridges that gap. The debate thus has a clear formal structure: {\i Rabbi Yishmael} reads partial {\i hakama} as complete by {\i drasha}, with no such extension to {\i hafara} \u8212- partial {\i hafara} is nothing. {\i Rabbi Akiva} uses the same {\i drasha} but extends it via {\i hekesh} \u8212- partial works as complete for both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba} said in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: those are the positions of {\i Rabbi Yishmael} and {\i Rabbi Akiva}. But the {\i chachamim} say: {\i makish hakama le-hafara} \u8212- they compare {\i hakama} to {\i hafara} in the opposite direction. {\i Mah hafara ma she-heifer heifer, af hakama ma she-kiyam kiyam} \u8212- just as {\i hafara} works on what is annulled, so {\i hakama} works on what is affirmed. The apparent meaning is that partial works for both \u8212- what is affirmed is affirmed, what is annulled is annulled \u8212- and neither sweeps the whole {\i neder} in its wake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If we map out the logical possibilities, assuming an all-or-nothing framework, there are three: partial {\i hakama} is everything and partial {\i hafara} is nothing ({\i Rabbi Yishmael}); partial is everything for both ({\i Rabbi Akiva}); and partial is nothing for both. The {\i chachamim} as rendered here seem to represent a departure from all-or-nothing entirely, saying that partial works for the part it covers \u8212- a genuinely distinct fourth possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Girsa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} reports a different {\i girsa} found in several manuscripts and attributed to {\i Rabbeinu Moshe bar Nachman} in his {\i hilchos}: {\i aval chachamim omrim mah hafara ma she-heifer lo hufar af hakama ma she-kiyam lo kiyam} \u8212- what he annulled is not annulled, and what he affirmed is not affirmed. On this {\i girsa}, the {\i chachamim} hold that partial {\i hafara} is nothing {\i and} partial {\i hakama} is nothing. This preserves a clean all-or-nothing structure across all three opinions: {\i Rabbi Yishmael} (partial {\i hakama} is everything, partial {\i hafara} is nothing), {\i Rabbi Akiva} (partial is everything for both), and {\i chachamim} (partial is nothing for both). On this reading, all three positions remain within the all-or-nothing framework, distributed across the logical possibilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} in {\i Hilchos Nedarim} 13 rules in accordance with our {\i girsa} of the {\i chachamim}, holding that partial works for both: if he affirmed the figs and annulled the grapes, {\i mah she-kiyam kiyam u\u8217'mah she-hufar mufar} \u8212- what he affirmed is affirmed and what he annulled is annulled. Each part is governed independently by whatever act was performed upon it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava/Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the question of what language is needed to separate a single compound vow into two distinct {\i nedarim}, {\i Rava} invokes {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's position from the laws of oaths. The case there involves someone who denies debts owed to multiple creditors with a single oath. If he says \u8220"I swear I owe none of you anything,\u8221" that is one oath and incurs one {\i korban} if he lied. If he says \u8220"I swear I don\u8217't owe you, and I swear I don\u8217't owe you,\u8221" addressing each creditor separately with the word {\i shvuah}, those are five separate oaths and incur five {\i korbanos}. The debate is what happens when he uses a form like \u8220"I swear I don\u8217't owe you or you or you\u8221" \u8212- one use of the oath-word covering multiple people. {\i Rabbi Shimon} holds that the oath-word must be repeated for each person individually \u8212- {\i ad sheyomar shvuah l\u8217'chol echad v\u8217'echad} \u8212- to produce separate oaths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} says our {\i mishna} reflects this same position applied to {\i nedarim}. When the woman says {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'emes} separately for each item \u8212- figs and grapes \u8212- this is analogous to, though not perfectly identical with, reusing the oath-word. It is not as complete a separation as repeating {\i konam} before each item would be, but it is sufficient to constitute two {\i nedarim} according to the view that requires more explicit separation to break a compound declaration into its components.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Next Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} opens the topic of {\i yodeia ani sheyesh nedarim} \u8212- the husband\u8217's knowledge regarding which types of {\i nedarim} he has the ability to annul and how that affects the timing of the {\i hafara}. This is a topic we had touched on earlier in the context of when the {\i yom sham\u8217'o} clock begins to run, and it will be explored there in detail.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 88\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unawareness of Hafara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Existence of Hafara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} for today is 88, and we pick up at the bottom of 87b with a new {\i mishna}. We remain in the domain of {\i hafara} \u8212- the husband\u8217's power to annul his wife\u8217's vows \u8212- and the {\i mishna} we now encounter introduces a nuanced question about what it means to truly \u8220"hear\u8221" a vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents the following case: a husband says, {\i yodea ani sheyesh nedarim} \u8212- I know that there is such a thing as a halachic {\i neder} \u8212- but {\i eini yodea sheyesh mefirim} \u8212- I did not know that there exists such a thing as {\i hafara}, or that anyone is empowered to act as a {\i meifer}, an annulling authority. Perhaps he assumed that {\i hafara} was an ancient biblical institution no longer in practice. Perhaps he simply never considered that such a mechanism was available to him at all. In such a case, the {\i mishna} rules {\i yafeh} \u8212- he may still annul the vow. The reason is that the day on which the vow was made is not considered {\i yom shamo}, the halachically operative \u8220"day of hearing.\u8221" Since he was entirely unaware that {\i hafara} was relevant to his situation, he was not truly \u8220"hearing\u8221" the {\i neder} in any meaningful sense. It is only the day he realizes that he possesses this power that becomes his {\i yom shamo}, and that is the day he may perform {\i hafara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yom Shamo Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important conceptual question: one might argue that {\i yom shamo} should be defined purely in terms of the physical act of hearing \u8212- one either heard or did not. If we begin to sever the physical act of hearing from its practical significance and meaning, perhaps neither day qualifies as {\i yom shamo} and the husband finds himself unable to annul at all. The {\i mishna} rejects this approach, at least in the case where a person was entirely outside the {\i parsha} \u8212- entirely unaware that {\i hafara} existed or applied to him. In such a case, the day genuine awareness arrives is the operative {\i yom shamo}, and {\i hafara} is valid on that day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relevance Anecdote\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is that if something is not perceived as relevant to you, it does not register \u8212- even if it is technically within your sensory range. If a person does not know that commercial vehicles are prohibited on certain roadways, a sign reading \u8220"no commercial vehicles\u8221" simply does not register as one passes it. And if one does not know that a mechanism of {\i hafara} exists or applies to him, the words of the {\i neder} he overhears do not truly constitute hearing in the sense the Torah has in mind. {\i Shmi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the kind of hearing that activates the husband\u8217's right and obligation to respond \u8212- means a meaningful, cognizant apprehension of what one has heard and its implications.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Hearing Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific Neder Unknown\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more complex and analytically rich case appears at the end of the {\i mishna}: {\i yodea ani sheyesh mefirim aval eini yodea shezeh neder} \u8212- I know that I possess the power of {\i hafara}, and I know that the world of {\i nedarim} is in principle my concern, but I did not realize that this particular utterance of my wife\u8217's constituted a {\i neder}. Perhaps he thought a certain key word was required and it was absent; perhaps he believed this topic fell outside the category of {\i devarim shebeino leveinah}. In any case, he was not entirely outside the {\i parsha} \u8212- he knew he was someone who could be a {\i meifer} \u8212- but he misjudged the specific details that would make this vow operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Is this sufficient to constitute {\i yom shamo}? The {\i mishna} records a dispute: {\i Rabbi Meir omer lo yafer} \u8212- Rabbi Meir says he cannot annul \u8212- while {\i Chachamim omrim yafer} \u8212- the Sages say he may.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} offers two distinct explanations of this dispute, and they represent genuinely different readings of the underlying halachic positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Realization as Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The simpler explanation \u8212- which the {\i Ran} presents second but which flows most naturally from the {\i reisha} of the {\i mishna} \u8212- begins with the position of the {\i Chachamim}. The {\i Chachamim} hold that the husband may perform {\i hafara}, meaning: on the day he realizes that this utterance was in fact a halachic {\i neder}, that day becomes his {\i yom shamo}, and he may annul just as in the first case of the {\i mishna}. When the pieces come together and full awareness is achieved, that constitutes {\i yom shamo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir, on this reading, disagrees. He holds that since the husband already knew he was within the {\i parsha} \u8212- he knew {\i hafara} was relevant to him as a general matter \u8212- that initial day of hearing already constituted {\i yom shamo}. One could therefore not retroactively designate the later day of fuller comprehension as {\i yom shamo}. The question then becomes whether Rabbi Meir would permit {\i hafara} on the original day of hearing itself. In principle, yes \u8212- if, for example, his wife urged him to perform {\i hafara} even though he himself was uncertain whether it was relevant, and he complied, that would presumably be valid according to Rabbi Meir. The operative point is that the day one knew one was in the {\i parsha} is {\i yom shamo}, and no later day can serve as a substitute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Half-Hearings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first and more striking explanation the {\i Ran} offers presents Rabbi Meir as holding a far more restrictive position: the husband cannot annul either that day or the next. The reasoning is as follows. On the day of hearing, he knew he was within the {\i parsha}, but he lacked the specific knowledge that this was a {\i neder}. On the later day when he realizes it was a {\i neder}, too much time has passed \u8212- the clock of {\i yom shamo} already began ticking when he first heard and recognized his general capacity. Neither half constitutes a complete {\i shmi\u8217'ah}: the first half is sufficient to preclude the later day from serving as {\i yom shamo}, but it is not itself sufficient to permit {\i hafara}, because the hearing was incomplete. The second half completes the knowledge but falls outside the operative window. Rabbi Meir thus splits the hearing into two partial {\i shmi\u8217'ahs}, neither of which individually suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to this reading, the {\i Chachamim} disagree and hold that on the day the husband first heard \u8212- even with incomplete knowledge of the specifics \u8212- that constitutes adequate {\i yom shamo}, and he may perform {\i hafara} on that day if he chooses. The partial initial hearing, according to the {\i Chachamim}, is sufficient. In summary, under the second {\i Ran} explanation: Rabbi Meir holds that partial hearing is never sufficient \u8212- two halves do not make a whole \u8212- while the {\i Chachamim} hold that the initial partial hearing is adequate for {\i hafara} on that day itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blind Person Paralle (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 B\u8217'lo Ra\u8217'ot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having laid out the {\i mishna}\u8217's debate, the {\i Gemara} now poses a challenge from an entirely different area of halacha: the laws of accidental manslaughter and {\i galus}, exile to a city of refuge. The verse describes a case where one person kills another {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot} \u8212- without seeing. The scenario is the standard one: a person is chopping wood in a forest, the blade flies off the handle, and someone is killed. The verse\u8217's phrase {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot} generates a dispute about the status of a {\i suma}, a blind person, who by definition always acts without seeing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the following: {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot prat l\u8217'suma}, divrei Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda holds that {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot} excludes a blind person. The logic is that {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot} means one who could have seen but did not; a blind person, who can never see, is in a categorically different situation and is exempt from {\i galus} entirely. Rabbi Meir, by contrast, holds {\i l\u8217'rabot et hasuma} \u8212- that {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot} includes the blind person. Now, the {\i Gemara} perceives a structural parallel to our {\i mishna}: the debate about the blind person concerns whether partial awareness \u8212- a kind of awareness-without-seeing \u8212- suffices to place someone within the legal category at hand. This maps, the {\i Gemara} suggests, onto the debate about partial hearing in the context of {\i hafara}. Yet something striking emerges: the positions appear to be reversed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's reasoning as follows. The verse in the context of {\i galus} also contains the phrase {\i v\u8217'asher yavo et re\u8217'ehu baya\u8217'ar} \u8212- and one who comes upon his friend in the forest \u8212- regarding which the {\i Gemara} observes: {\i kol d\u8217'var me\u8217'ilei laya\u8217'ar hu} \u8212- any person who enters a forest is included. A blind person is perfectly capable of entering a forest, so the verse begins by assuming a blind person is included in the law of {\i galus}. When we then encounter {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot}, there is no need to use it to include the blind person \u8212- that has already been accomplished. The only remaining function of {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot} is therefore to exclude the blind person. This is the principle of {\i ein ribui achar ribui ela lema\u8217'et}: a second inclusion, when the item is already included, must function as an exclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir proceeds from the opposite starting point. A different verse in the passage uses the phrase {\i b\u8217'li da\u8217'at} \u8212- without awareness \u8212- and Rabbi Meir reads this as referring to someone who is {\i bar meda}, capable of full awareness. A blind person, he argues, is not fully capable of such awareness \u8212- {\i suma lav bar meda hu} \u8212- and so the passage, read from the outset, implies that a blind person is excluded. When the verse then says {\i b\u8217'lo ra\u8217'ot}, there is nothing left for it to do by way of exclusion \u8212- the blind person is already excluded. Therefore, this phrase must function to include the blind person. This is the principle of {\i ein miyut achar miyut ela lerabos}: a second exclusion, when the item is already excluded, must function as an inclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Awareness Drive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore concludes with {\i Rava}\u8217's statement: {\i hacha me\u8217'inyana de\u8217'kra v\u8217'hacha me\u8217'inyana de\u8217'kra} \u8212- in each case, the outcome is driven by the specific language of the verse, and therefore the positions in the {\i galus} dispute need not be consistent with one\u8217's general view on partial awareness. What appears to be a reversal of positions is in fact the result of each {\i tanna} being driven by different textual starting points.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran}, however, goes further and offers a deeper reading of how positions on partial awareness actually {\i drive} the textual interpretations rather than being independent of them. The {\i Ran} aligns the {\i galus} dispute with the second explanation he gave of our {\i mishna}. According to that reading, Rabbi Meir holds that partial awareness \u8212- a half-hearing \u8212- is never sufficient. This is his general orienting principle. When Rabbi Meir approaches the {\i parsha} of {\i galus}, he therefore begins by assuming that a blind person, who has only partial awareness, is excluded, since his default approach is that the partial does not count. The extra verse then has no exclusionary work to do and must therefore include the blind person. Rabbi Yehuda, who is presumably the position of the {\i Chachamim} in our {\i mishna}, begins from the opposite principle: partial awareness is meaningful and sufficient. Accordingly, he reads the {\i parsha} of {\i galus} as initially including the blind person, since his default is that partial suffices. The extra verse then has no inclusionary work left and must therefore exclude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the positions are not merely reversed by textual accident. Rather, the deeper halachic intuition about whether partial awareness suffices shapes how each {\i tanna} reads the verse, and the resulting textual conclusion comes out in the opposite direction from where each would land in the context of {\i hafara}. The {\i Ran} is careful to observe that this is specifically true under the second reading of the {\i mishna}\u8217's dispute \u8212- the reading in which Rabbi Meir holds that two half-hearings, each insufficient alone, leave the husband unable to perform {\i hafara} at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder/Son-in-Law Pr (III)operty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the next {\i mishna}. The case involves {\i hamadir hana\u8217'ah mechasno} \u8212- a father-in-law who takes a vow prohibiting his son-in-law from deriving any benefit from him. Having done so, he nonetheless wishes to give money to his daughter. The question the {\i mishna} grapples with is how the father-in-law can give property to his daughter in a way that does not benefit his son-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Automatic Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is rooted in the principle of {\i kol shekansah isha kanah ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- whatever a wife acquires, her husband acquires. If the father simply gives money to his daughter, the husband immediately acquires it, and the father-in-law has thereby indirectly caused his son-in-law to benefit from his property, in violation of his vow. The father therefore faces a challenge: how does one give a gift to a married daughter without the benefit flowing through to the son-in-law?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blind Person Paralle (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution through {\i Rava} \u8212- that in each domain the verse operates according to its own logic \u8212- applies specifically to the question of how the blind person debate in the laws of manslaughter maps onto the partial hearing debate in the laws of {\i hafara}, and the analysis belongs with the {\i galus} discussion above rather than being reintroduced here. What the context of the son-in-law {\i mishna} requires is a separate analysis of how the wife\u8217's acquisition operates and whether the father\u8217's stipulation can interrupt the automatic transfer to the husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder/Son-in-Law Pr (III)ope\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Noseies V\u8217'Notenes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} provides a solution: the father must give the money with the stipulation {\i mah she\u8217'at noseies v\u8217'notenet l\u8217'ficha} \u8212- \u8220"whatever you take and give over into your mouth.\u8221" This phrasing goes beyond a simple declaration that the husband has no rights; it defines the moment of the daughter\u8217's acquisition in a specific and constrained way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stipulation Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} explains why a simple stipulation \u8212- saying merely that the husband shall have no rights over the gift \u8212- is insufficient according to the {\i mishna}. The reason lies in the principle of {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah}, which the {\i mishna} is understood to follow in accordance with the position of Rabbi Meir. If the wife\u8217's hand is halachically equivalent to her husband\u8217's hand \u8212- that is, if she is not a legally independent acquisitional entity but rather an extension of him \u8212- then a stipulation excluding the husband\u8217's rights is logically incoherent. One cannot give something to a person\u8217's hand while simultaneously excluding that person from ownership. If her hand {\i is} his hand, the transfer goes to him directly and there is no mechanism to interrupt it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's solution, as the {\i Ran} explains it, is that the father is not truly transferring ownership to his daughter at all. The money remains, in effect, the father\u8217's. What he is granting is something far more limited: the right to consume. As she is in the act of placing the food or its equivalent into her mouth \u8212- and only at that moment \u8212- he permits her to benefit from it. At that instant of consumption, the benefit accrues to her directly, and there is no opportunity for it to pass through her hand into her husband\u8217's. Whether this applies only to consumables or extends to non-consumable goods such as clothing is a question the {\i Ran} considers separately: one might argue that a non-consumable is possessed at the moment she takes hold of it and could then pass to the husband; alternatively, one could argue that even then the item remains the father\u8217's, which she is merely permitted to use, and it never enters the chain of acquisition that leads to the husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yad Isha Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} sets out the conceptual distinction underlying the dispute between Rav and Shmuel, who debate whether the {\i mishna}\u8217's stringent formulation is in fact required as a matter of {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual foundation of the entire discussion turns on the precise meaning of {\i kol shekansah isha kanah ba\u8217'alah} versus the stronger formulation of {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah}. These two principles are not equivalent. The first formulation describes an automatic transfer: the wife possesses property, but it immediately flows to the husband. One can visualize this as automatic email forwarding \u8212- the item enters her account and is instantly redirected to his. Under this model, she does have her own {\i yad}, her own acquisitional capacity; it is simply that her account is configured to forward everything to her husband\u8217's. If this is correct, perhaps one could interrupt the forwarding \u8212- perhaps the father can stipulate that what he deposits in her account shall not be forwarded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second and stronger formulation \u8212- {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- holds something more radical. Her hand is not merely connected to his; it {\i is} his. She has no independent acquisitional standing at all. There is no separate account that then forwards; there is only his account. Under this model, no stipulation can change the outcome, because there is no interstitial stage of her ownership in which to intervene. The only approach that can work is the one the {\i mishna} describes: eliminating the transfer entirely by ensuring she never \u8220"acquires\u8221" in the standard sense, but only benefits at the precise moment of consumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav holds in accordance with this stronger model \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} will note that this aligns with Rabbi Meir\u8217's position \u8212- and therefore requires the full formulation of {\i noseies v\u8217'notenet l\u8217'ficha}. Shmuel holds the more moderate position and therefore maintains that a simple stipulation against the husband\u8217's rights suffices.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder/Son-in-Law Pr (III)ope\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money vs Food\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further question requires attention: why does the {\i mishna} specify that the father gives his daughter {\i money} rather than food directly? The {\i Ran} explains that food is a categorically different case. The husband is obligated to feed his wife. If a third party directly provides food to the wife, that provision satisfies the husband\u8217's existing obligation to feed her. The property, in other words, is discharging what is already his debt. Since what he owes her is her food, that food belongs to her by virtue of his obligation and does not carry with it any residual right for him to claim benefit. The father\u8217's gift of food would therefore be free of the problem entirely \u8212- the husband never acquires rights over what satisfies his own obligation to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The indirect benefit to the husband from not having to spend money on food himself is, the {\i Ran} notes, an instance of {\i mavriach ari} \u8212- warding off a lion, preventing a loss \u8212- which is not halachically treated as conferring direct benefit on the person in question. Therefore the father wishing to give food would face no difficulty at all. It is specifically money \u8212- and, by extension, non-food property \u8212- that presents the problem addressed by the {\i mishna}, because money given to the wife flows through her hand into the husband\u8217's in a way that food designated for her sustenance does not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav vs Shmuel (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Both Phrases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents Rav\u8217's position directly. {\i Lo shanu ela de-amar lah mah she-at noset ve-notenet be-ficha} \u8212- Rav taught that the {\i mishna}\u8217's permission applies only if the father explicitly uses the formulation of \u8220"what you place into your mouth.\u8221" {\i Aval amar mah she-tirtsi asi} \u8212- but if he says \u8220"do with it as you wish,\u8221" even though this is functionally equivalent to saying \u8220"your husband has no rights over it,\u8221" in that case {\i kana bahen baal} \u8212- the husband acquires it nonetheless. According to Rav, a simple declaration that the husband has no rights is legally ineffective. The only mechanism that prevents the husband from acquiring is the construction in the {\i mishna} whereby the wife does not fully acquire until the moment of consumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Stipulation Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees. {\i Afilu amar mah she-tirtsi asi} \u8212- even if the father merely says \u8220"do with it as you wish,\u8221" conveying that it is entirely hers and the husband has no rights, {\i lo kana bahen baal} \u8212- the husband does not acquire it. Shmuel holds the more moderate view of {\i kol shekansah isha kanah ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- the automatic transfer model \u8212- and maintains that since the wife does possess her own acquisitional capacity, it is possible to interrupt the forwarding mechanism through explicit stipulation. Under Shmuel\u8217's position, the {\i mishna}\u8217's formulation of {\i noseies v\u8217'notenet l\u8217'ficha} is not legally required; a straightforward condition that the husband shall have no rights is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} is careful to note that Shmuel is not disputing the internal logic of the {\i mishna} itself. Rather, Shmuel holds that the {\i mishna} reflects the position of Rabbi Meir \u8212- who applies {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah} in its stronger sense \u8212- but that as a matter of practical {\i halacha}, we do not rule like Rabbi Meir on this point. Rav, by contrast, holds that the {\i mishna}\u8217's stringent position is the operative {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shitur Mevo\u8217'ot Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shitur Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a challenge to Rav\u8217's position from a different context entirely: the laws of {\i shituf mevo\u8217'ot}. To understand the challenge, a brief description of the institution is necessary. In a shared alleyway \u8212- a {\i mavoi} \u8212- all the courtyards whose residents use that alleyway must establish a form of joint ownership in a single food item in order for the alleyway to be treated as a unified domain and carrying within it to be permitted on Shabbat. This is the {\i shituf mevo\u8217'ot}. Each courtyard is independently populated by residents of separate households, and each household is legally distinct. In order to unify all of these distinct entities into a single corporate-like domain, they all become joint owners of a single communal food item \u8212- typically a barrel of wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical question is how to effect this joint ownership efficiently. Rather than going door to door collecting a contribution from each household, a simpler method is available: one person takes the barrel and declares {\i harei zeh lechol bnei hamavoi} \u8212- this is hereby transferred to all the residents of the {\i mavoi}. This person confers ownership on everyone. But this act of conferring ownership requires a third party \u8212- someone who is not the owner \u8212- to take possession of the barrel and hold it on behalf of all the other residents. The owner cannot transfer something to himself; the acquisition must pass through an independent hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife as Third Party\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} governing this process \u8212- a {\i stam mishna}, presumed to reflect the position of Rabbi Meir \u8212- lists the parties who may serve in this role. {\i Umezakeh lahen al yedei avdo veshifchato ha\u8217'ivrim} \u8212- one may effect the transfer through one\u8217's Hebrew male or female slaves. {\i Ve\u8217'al yedei beno uvito hagedolim} \u8212- through one\u8217's adult sons or daughters. {\i Ve\u8217'al yedei ishto} \u8212- and through one\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale for including adult children and Hebrew slaves is that even though they exist in a close relationship with the owner, they are halachically recognized as distinct legal persons capable of independent acquisition. The mention of the wife in this list is what creates the difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The community\u8217's {\i eruv} is handled in practice in a manner consistent with this principle: a rabbi takes a box of matzahs \u8212- which remain usable over an extended period \u8212- gives them to a designated individual, and says that this person is taking possession on behalf of all members of the community who reside in the relevant domain. The wife is listed as one who can perform this function for the {\i shituf mevo\u8217'ot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here lies the tension. If Rabbi Meir holds {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- that the wife\u8217's acquisitional capacity is simply an extension of the husband\u8217's \u8212- then giving the barrel to one\u8217's wife is no different from giving it from one\u8217's right hand to one\u8217's left. There is no independent legal entity receiving it; it simply remains in the husband\u8217's domain. {\i Ve\u8217'i amart kneyah shon ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- and if you say that the husband invariably acquires whatever comes into his wife\u8217's hand, with no possibility of stipulating otherwise \u8212- then {\i eruv lo nafik mereshuteih deba\u8217'al}: the {\i eruv} barrel never leaves the husband\u8217's domain, and he cannot use her to effect the transfer to the rest of the {\i mavoi}. How then can the {\i stam mishna} \u8212- which follows Rabbi Meir \u8212- include the wife as a valid agent for this {\i kinyan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i af al gav de\u8217'amar Rabbi Meir yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah, modeh Rabbi Meir le\u8217'inyan shituf, dekeivan d\u8217'lezakot l\u8217'acherim hu, miyad ba\u8217'alah zachyah} \u8212- even though Rabbi Meir holds that the wife\u8217's hand is like her husband\u8217's hand, he concedes in the case of {\i shituf} that she can serve as the acquisitional agent, because here the transfer is from the husband\u8217's hand and is intended to confer ownership upon others. In this circumstance, the wife successfully represents an entity distinct from the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} and {\i Tosafos} both emphasize that the critical element here is {\i miyad ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- that it is the husband himself who is initiating the transfer. The reasoning is as follows. The principle of {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah} is not a metaphysical description of what a wife is; it is an imposed legal structure. Unlike the case of a non-Hebrew slave \u8212- whose status as the master\u8217's property is fundamental and cannot be severed by act of will \u8212- a wife retains full legal personhood within {\i halacha}. She can be a litigant, own property under certain conditions, and is in no way a chattel. The characterization of her {\i yad} as equivalent to her husband\u8217's is therefore a legal construct that was instituted and that, importantly, can be removed. The only party empowered to sever that connection is the husband himself. He can, by an act of explicit intention, declare that he is releasing his claim and that she is to function as a genuinely independent entity for purposes of this transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third party could not accomplish this. If someone other than the husband places an item in the wife\u8217's hands and says \u8220"this is for other people,\u8221" the {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah} structure is not disrupted \u8212- it takes what is going into her hand and redirects it to the husband. Only the husband, as the one whose relationship with her generates that legal structure, can choose to step outside it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is to be distinguished from reading the {\i Gemara}\u8217's language as emphasizing {\i lezakot l\u8217'acherim} \u8212- the fact that the transfer is destined for others. On an alternative reading, one might argue that when a wife takes possession for the sake of parties other than herself, we view her as standing in for those third parties rather than as an extension of her husband, and therefore the {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah} structure simply does not apply. Under this reading, the critical variable would be the destination of the property rather than the identity of the giver. However, the {\i Ran} and {\i Tosafos} both reject this reading and emphasize that it is specifically {\i miyad ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- the husband\u8217's active, explicit participation as the giver \u8212- that breaks the structural equivalence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife Standing Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now identifies a further complication. {\i Aesivay Ravina l\u8217'Rav Ashi} \u8212- Ravina posed to Rav Ashi a challenge from another Tannaic source: a {\i baraita} that explicitly enumerates the parties who can and cannot serve as the acquisitional agent for the {\i shituf mevo\u8217'ot}. On the permissible side, it lists adult children and Hebrew slaves \u8212- precisely as in the {\i stam mishna}. On the impermissible side \u8212- {\i elu she-ein zachin lahen} \u8212- it lists minor children, Canaanite slaves, and {\i v\u8217'ishto} \u8212- the wife. This creates a direct contradiction: one Tannaic source explicitly permits the wife to serve in this role and another explicitly prohibits it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Interest Requir\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi resolves the contradiction. The {\i stam mishna} that permits use of the wife does not apply across the board. {\i Matnitin besheyesh lah chatzer be\u8217'oto mavoi askinan} \u8212- the {\i mishna} refers specifically to a case where the wife herself owns a house in that very {\i mavoi}. {\i Demigo dezachyah l\u8217'nafshah zachyah l\u8217'achrinei} \u8212- since she has independent standing to acquire for herself in this context, she can also acquire on behalf of others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of Rav Ashi\u8217's answer is as follows. Even when the husband is willing to sever the {\i yad isha k\u8217'yad ba\u8217'alah} structure, and even when he is the one giving explicitly \u8212- as Rava established \u8212- Rabbi Meir still requires that the wife have some form of genuine independent legal standing in the relevant context in order for her acquisition to be recognized as belonging to a distinct entity. If she is simply his wife with no independent connection to the {\i mavoi}, she remains, even in the best of circumstances, primarily an extension of him and cannot serve as a genuinely independent {\i kinyan} vessel. But if she independently owns a house in that {\i mavoi}, she is already recognized as her own legal presence within the shared domain. That pre-existing standing \u8212- {\i miggo dezachyah l\u8217'nafshah}, since she is capable of acquiring for herself in this setting \u8212- enables her to acquire on behalf of others as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It bears noting that for the wife to independently own a house in the {\i mavoi} in a manner that is not subject to the husband\u8217's rights, it would likely have to have been brought into the marriage under conditions that explicitly excluded the husband\u8217's claims \u8212- perhaps as a prenuptial arrangement. Once inside the marriage, any newly acquired property would, according to Rabbi Meir\u8217's strong position, pass immediately to the husband\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further observation emerges from this entire discussion: the transfers being analyzed here appear to concern not merely the husband\u8217's right of {\i ochel peiros} \u8212- his entitlement to the usufruct of his wife\u8217's property while she retains title \u8212- but full transfer of ownership itself. The {\i shituf mevo\u8217'ot} question is about who holds title to the barrel of wine, not about who profits from it. This suggests that the halachic principle under discussion operates at the level of full ownership, a point that would require further elaboration beyond what this {\i Gemara} explores directly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes with the question of practical {\i halacha}: do we rule like Rav or like Shmuel? The general principle is {\i halacha k\u8217'Shmuel b\u8217'dinei mamonot} \u8212- we follow Shmuel in monetary law. Although the dispute arises here in the context of {\i issur} \u8212- the prohibitions of {\i nedarim} \u8212- the {\i Ran} makes clear that the underlying debate is fundamentally one of {\i dinei mamonot}, concerning the nature of the wife\u8217's acquisitional relationship with her husband. Accordingly, the {\i Ran} cites the ruling of the {\i Nagid} Rabbeinu Shmuel HaLevi in the name of Rav Amram Gaon that the {\i halacha} follows Shmuel: {\i halacha k\u8217'Shmuel}. Furthermore, the {\i Ran} observes that Rav aligns with Rabbi Meir and Shmuel with the {\i Rabbanan} \u8212- the {\i Chachamim} \u8212- and that the general tendency of the {\i sugya} runs in Shmuel\u8217's direction. The practical consequence is that a father who gives his daughter a gift with an explicit condition that her husband shall have no rights over it \u8212- {\i al menat she\u8217'ein l\u8217'ba\u8217'alech reshut bahen} \u8212- that stipulation alone is halachically sufficient, and the elaborate formulation of {\i noseies v\u8217'notenet l\u8217'ficha} is not required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} notes, however, that not all {\i poskim} accept this conclusion. There are authorities who rule like Rav and require the stricter formulation of the {\i mishna}. The precise scope of the debate \u8212- including how to read exactly what \u8220"do with it as you wish\u8221" implies and whether there are intermediate positions \u8212- is subject to further discussion among the {\i rishonim}. The {\i Ran} acknowledges these alternative views without fully resolving every dimension of the question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 89\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow/Divorc\u233 ?e Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i perek} of {\i Nedarim} arrives at the end of a sustained treatment of vow annulment that began with the father\u8217's authority over his daughter, moved through the complex case of the {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} who stands under the joint control of father and {\i arus}, and then devoted considerable attention to the husband\u8217's power of {\i hafara} over his wife. Now, three {\i dapim} from the end of the tractate, the {\i mishnah} turns to the case that the Torah addresses in what appears to be the most straightforward fashion: the woman who is entirely her own person, subject to no man\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} in question is {\i v\u8217'neder almanah u\u8217'grushah kol asher asra al nafshah yakum aleha} \u8212- the vow of a widow or divorc\u233 ?e, whatever she has forbidden upon herself, shall stand. At first glance, this seems to require no derivation at all: if there is no father and no husband, there is obviously no one to annul her vow. The {\i mishnah} therefore signals, by opening with this verse, that the {\i pasuk} is coming to teach something less obvious. The {\i chiddush} is not the bare case but a more nuanced one \u8212- and it is in unpacking that nuance that the {\i perek} begins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delayed Vow Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case the {\i mishnah} presents is the following. A woman who is independent \u8212- a {\i bogeret}, an {\i almanah}, a {\i gerushah}, any woman not under a man\u8217's control \u8212- declares: {\i hareini nezirah achar shloshim yom}, I will become a {\i nezirah} after thirty days. Before those thirty days elapse, she gets married. The vow then takes effect when she is already married to a husband who, in principle, has the power to annul her vows. Can he annul it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} answers: {\i af al pi shenisset b\u8217'toch shloshim yom eino yachol l\u8217'hafer} \u8212- even though she married within the thirty days, he cannot annul it. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i pasuk} is precisely this: the husband\u8217's authority to annul is not determined by when the vow takes effect but by when the vow was made. Since she was an {\i almanah} or {\i gerushah} at the moment of utterance, the vow is established as hers alone, and no subsequent marriage can strip it of that character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse case follows naturally. A married woman \u8212- {\i nadrah v\u8217'hi b\u8217'reshut habaal} \u8212- declares while under her husband\u8217's authority: {\i hareini nezirah achar shloshim yom}. He has the power to annul her vow. The {\i mishnah} rules that he may in fact annul it, {\i af al pi shenit\u8217'almanah o nitgarshah b\u8217'toch shloshim yom} \u8212- even if she is widowed or divorced within those thirty days, before the vow ever takes effect. He need only perform the annulment during the period of their marriage; the fact that when the thirty days finally expire she is no longer his wife is irrelevant. He cannot, of course, annul her vows after she is widowed or divorced, but he can annul before it takes effect during the marriage, and that annulment holds. Again, the controlling moment is the time of utterance, not the time of effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same-Day Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents a compressed and striking scenario: {\i nadrah bo bayom v\u8217'nitgarshah bo bayom v\u8217'hechezirah bo bayom} \u8212- she took a vow, was divorced, and was then remarried to the same man, all on the same day. Can he annul it, given that it was made while she was his wife and she is his wife again at the moment he seeks to annul? The {\i halachah} is {\i eino yachol l\u8217'hafer} \u8212- he cannot. The reasoning draws on two related principles. First, a husband cannot be {\i meifer} for vows that predate him \u8212- {\i ein ba\u8217'al meifer b\u8217'kodmin} \u8212- and the second marriage, even to the same man, is a new marriage. From the perspective of that second marriage, the vow was made prior to it and is therefore beyond his reach. Second, the divorce, even for a moment, granted her independence, and that moment is legally transformative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Governing Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} closes with the formulation {\i zeh haklal: kol sheyatzah l\u8217'reshut atzmah sha\u8217'ah achat eino yachol l\u8217'hafer} \u8212- the governing rule is that once she enters her own domain, even for a single moment, he loses the power to annul. This serves as the practical articulation of the {\i pasuk}\u8217's teaching. The independence of the {\i almanah} and {\i gerushah} is not merely a biographical state but a legal threshold: once crossed, it cannot be erased by subsequent remarriage. The possibility raised in discussion \u8212- that his act of divorcing her might itself function as a kind of {\i kiyum}, a ratification of the vow through the very act that created her independence \u8212- is worth considering, even if the {\i halachah} does not depend on it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yishmael vs.\u160 ?Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Case 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} now presents the underlying {\i machloket tanna\u8217'im} in explicit form. The first case: an {\i almanah} or {\i gerushah} \u8212- a woman fully independent \u8212- declares {\i hareini nezirah l\u8217'cheshe\u8217'inasei}, I will become a {\i nezirah} when I get married. She then does get married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael: Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael omer yafer} \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael holds that the husband can annul. His reasoning: the determinative moment is when the vow takes effect, i.e., when it becomes operative. The vow takes effect upon marriage, which means it takes effect precisely when she is under his authority. The fact that it was formulated before the marriage is irrelevant; what matters is the moment at which the prohibition becomes real. Rabbi Yishmael thus focuses on the {\i sha\u8217'at chal} \u8212- the time of effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva: Utterance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva omer lo yafer} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva holds that the husband cannot annul it, because the controlling moment is {\i sha\u8217'at amira} \u8212- the time of utterance. She uttered the vow when she was independent, and that character attaches to the vow permanently. The {\i seiman} introduced here \u8212- {\i yelali} \u8212- encodes the alternating pattern of {\i yafer / lo yafer} in Case 1 and {\i lo yafer / yafer} in Case 2, since the positions reverse in the second case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Case 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case is the mirror image: a married woman declares {\i hareini nezirah l\u8217'cheshe\u8217'etgaresh}, I will become a {\i nezirah} when I get divorced. The husband, cooperatively or otherwise, then divorces her, and the vow takes effect at the moment of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Positions Reversed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The positions now reverse. {\i Rabbi Yishmael omer lo yafer}: since the vow takes effect when she is divorced and no longer under his authority, his annulment is meaningless \u8212- he had no power over it at the moment it became operative. {\i Rabbi Akiva omer yafer}: since the vow was made during the marriage, the husband had the power to annul it on the day she made it, and it does not matter that the effect only came later. The reversal is not surprising once the underlying logic is clear: each {\i tanna} is applying a single consistent principle across both cases, and the two cases simply instantiate the tension between the two principles in opposite directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Exegesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Talmud then presents the scriptural foundations for each position. Rabbi Yishmael derives his view from the phrase {\i veneder almanah u\u8217'grushah} \u8212- the word {\i veneder}, he argues, refers not to the act of vowing but to the actual standing reality of the vow itself. The focus of {\i veneder almanah u\u8217'grushah} is on when the vow {\i exists} as an operative prohibition: is it in existence when she is an {\i almanah u\u8217'grushah}, i.e., independent, or when she is married? That is the question. {\i Ad she-hi neder besha\u8217'at almanut v\u8217'gerushin} \u8212- we look to the state she is in when the vow is in effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva derives his view from the phrase {\i kol asher asra al nafshah} \u8212- the word {\i asra}, she forbade, focuses attention not on the standing reality of the prohibition but on the act of prohibiting. {\i Ad she-isur neder besha\u8217'at almanut v\u8217'gerushin} \u8212- was she independent at the moment she performed the act of forbidding? That is the question, and it is answered by looking at the {\i sha\u8217'at amira}, the moment of utterance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda states the obvious conclusion: {\i matnitin Rabbi Akiva hi} \u8212- our {\i mishnah}, which consistently governs by the time of utterance, reflects Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thirty-Day Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, challenges this. He argues: {\i afilu teima Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- one might say the {\i mishnah} is consistent even with Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's position. The key distinction lies in how the vow is formulated. In the {\i mishnah}, the vow is time-indexed: the woman says it will take effect after thirty days. The date of effect is fixed independently of her marital status. It is possible that over the course of those thirty days, nothing changes: she remains in exactly the same legal position she was in when she uttered the vow. The change in her status \u8212- the marriage or divorce \u8212- happens to occur incidentally within that window, but it is not built into the structure of the vow itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Event-Based Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i baraita}, by contrast, the vow is status-indexed: {\i l\u8217'cheshe\u8217'inasei}, when I get married; {\i l\u8217'cheshe\u8217'etgaresh}, when I get divorced. The event of taking effect is definitionally tied to a change in legal status. By the very structure of the vow, the act of utterance and the moment of effect are categorically different moments \u8212- the vow was designed to span a transition from one legal domain to another. It is this intrinsic opposition between the two moments \u8212- utterance in one status, effect in another status \u8212- that Rabbi Yishmael focuses on and that triggers his rule of {\i sha\u8217'at chal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incidental Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's thirty-day case, Abaye argues, even Rabbi Yishmael might agree that the {\i sha\u8217'at amira} governs. The vow was expressed as taking effect after a certain number of days \u8212- {\i shalmu yomei velo nitgarsha, shalmu yomei velo mitnasba} \u8212- and it was entirely possible that those thirty days would run out without any change in her status at all. The change that happened to occur was adventitious, not intrinsic to the vow. Since Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's principle focuses on cases where the two moments are by design in opposition, when the status change is merely incidental he may concede that the original status, the one in which the vow was made, is the one that counts. This is the {\i ra\u8217'n}\u8217's formulation: {\i keivan shebitchilato haya raui haneder lachul beoto reshut shehaya beshaat amira} \u8212- since at the outset the vow could have taken effect within the same legal domain as the utterance, the original domain defines it even in Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's view. The {\i halachah} in any case follows Rabbi Akiva, both because {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Akiva mechavero} and because the {\i mishnah} is a {\i stam mishna} \u8212- though whether it is in fact a {\i stam mishna} depends on whether one accepts Abaye\u8217's resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Leaving Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubot Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the closing line of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i zeh haklal kol sheyatzah l\u8217'reshut atzmah sha\u8217'ah achat eino yachol l\u8217'hafer} \u8212- and reads it alongside a parallel {\i zeh haklal} from tractate {\i Ketubot}, which dealt with the {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}. That {\i klal} in {\i Ketubot} establishes the following: as long as the daughter has not fully left her father\u8217's authority, the father retains his right to participate in the annulment of her vows jointly with the {\i arus}. The formulation {\i zeh haklal kol shelo yatzah mereshut ha\u8217'av aviah u\u8217'vaalah mefirin nedareha} is broad: even if her departure from the father\u8217's domain is partial or ambiguous \u8212- for example, if the father or his messengers accompany the husband\u8217's messengers on the way \u8212- she is still considered within the father\u8217's authority, and the father may still annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i leatuyei} \u8212- the case the {\i klal} in {\i Ketubot} comes to include \u8212- is the scenario where the father accompanies, or sends his representatives to accompany, the husband\u8217's emissaries. Even in that borderline situation, since she has not fully exited her father\u8217's control, he remains in the picture. The {\i klal} reaches broadly in favor of maintaining the father\u8217's presence so long as any connection remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Our Perek Klal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i zeh haklal} of our {\i perek} \u8212- {\i Elu Naarot} \u8212- operates in the opposite direction and addresses the threshold from the other side. Once she has been handed over to the husband\u8217's messengers without the father or his representatives present \u8212- {\i masar ha\u8217'av lishluchei habaal o shemasru shluchei av lishluchei habaal} \u8212- she has left her father\u8217's control, and any prior vows can no longer be annulled by the husband under the rule of {\i ein ba\u8217'al meifer b\u8217'kodmin}. Interestingly, at this precise moment she has not yet entered {\i reshut habaal} in the full sense \u8212- she has not seen her husband, there has been no {\i chuppah}, she is simply in transit with his messengers. Yet since the father has stepped back, the opportunity for the husband to annul vows from the betrothal period is now gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's language of {\i yatzah l\u8217'reshut atzmah} is therefore somewhat forced in this reading, since she has not become independent in any straightforward sense \u8212- she is on her way to {\i reshut habaal}. The better reading seems to be {\i yotzet mereshut ha\u8217'av}: she is leaving the father\u8217's domain, not entering her own. But the practical consequence is the same: once the father is no longer present, the window for annulling prior vows closes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betrothal Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question this raises is worth pressing: vows she made during {\i erusin} \u8212- the betrothal period \u8212- were subject to annulment by the father and the {\i arus} jointly. Once she transitions to full {\i nisuin}, those vows from the betrothal period become prior to the husband\u8217's status as full husband and cannot be annulled. The moment she is handed over to his messengers without the father functions, in this analysis, as a kind of threshold event equivalent to {\i chuppah} for purposes of the annulment window. The husband had the opportunity to annul betrothal-period vows while she was still betrothed and still partially in her father\u8217's domain; once that threshold is crossed, the father\u8217's authority is gone, and from the husband\u8217's vantage point, those vows preceded his authority and are beyond his reach.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nine Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three by Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now presents what appears to be an elaborate multiplication of cases, though the underlying structure is simple: there are three ways a woman becomes {\i yatzah l\u8217'reshut atzmah} \u8212- independent \u8212- and the {\i mishnah} takes each of those three and shows that various other circumstances that might seem to add complexity are in fact irrelevant. The {\i Yerushalmi} is cited to note that Rabbi Yehuda taught this {\i mishnah} {\i l\u8217'chahed bo et hatalmidim} \u8212- to sharpen the students \u8212- and indeed the exercise has the quality of a deliberate puzzle rather than a necessary elaboration of the law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three fundamental categories are: a woman who has been married and divorced ({\i yetomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av}, an emancipated minor despite her father\u8217's being alive); a true orphan ({\i yetomah}, whose father has died); and a {\i bogeret}, a woman who has reached adulthood. Within each of these categories, the {\i mishnah} varies circumstances that make no legal difference \u8212- whether she is a {\i bogeret} or a {\i na\u8217'arah}, whether the father is alive or dead \u8212- to arrive at nine cases. The key point is that once the essential condition is met, the additional factors are legally superfluous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important clarification: for a girl to achieve independence through marriage and divorce, the marriage must have been {\i nisuin}, full marriage, not merely {\i erusin}. If she became widowed or divorced during {\i erusin}, she never left her father\u8217's authority in the first place. Only genuine {\i nisuin} \u8212- {\i masi bito} \u8212- fully removes her from the father\u8217's domain, such that a subsequent divorce or widowhood renders her truly independent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorced Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first group focuses on the {\i yetomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av} \u8212- the woman who has been married and divorced while her father is still alive. Within this group, three variations are presented: she is a {\i bogeret} and also happens to be a {\i yetomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av} (emancipated through divorce); or she was a {\i na\u8217'arah}, became a {\i bogeret}, and is a {\i yetomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av}; or she is a {\i na\u8217'arah} who has not yet become a {\i bogeret} but is a {\i yetomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av}. In all three cases, the controlling factor is simply that she has been married and divorced. The other circumstances are irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphan Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second group focuses on the woman whose father has actually died \u8212- the true {\i yetomah}. Again, three variations: she is a {\i bogeret} and her father has died; she was a {\i na\u8217'arah}, became a {\i bogeret}, and her father died; or she is a {\i na\u8217'arah} who has not become a {\i bogeret} and whose father has died. In each case, what renders her vows binding is simply that there is no father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third group focuses on the {\i bogeret} \u8212- the woman who has attained adulthood. Here the variation is somewhat different, since by definition this group is about the {\i bogeret} status itself. The cases are: she is a {\i bogeret} and her father is alive; she was a {\i na\u8217'arah} who became a {\i bogeret} and her father is alive. One might note that one could have generated a third variation \u8212- adding whether she was married and divorced \u8212- to produce a full twenty-seven case matrix, but the {\i mishnah} (and {\i Rabbi Yehuda}) stops at nine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After presenting the nine cases, the {\i mishnah} adds a ruling attributed to Rabbi Yehuda: {\i af hamasi bito haktana v\u8217'nitalmana v\u8217'nitgarsha v\u8217'chazra etzlo} \u8212- even a man who marries off his minor daughter, and she becomes widowed or divorced and returns to him, {\i adayin hi na\u8217'arah} \u8212- even if she is still a {\i na\u8217'arah} and not a {\i bogeret}, since she has had {\i nisuin} and is now divorced, she is independent. Her vows are binding. This is not adding a new principle but rather illustrating what {\i yetomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av} means \u8212- the father is alive, she is still a minor, but the experience of full {\i nisuin} followed by divorce has emancipated her in the eyes of the law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Simplify\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda cites Rav: {\i zo divrei Rabbi Yehuda, aval Chachamim omrim shalosh naarot nidreihen kayamim} \u8212- this elaborate enumeration is Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's teaching, but the Sages state the matter simply: three categories of women have binding vows \u8212- a {\i bogeret}, a {\i yetomah} (in the literal sense), and a {\i yetomah b\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'av} (divorced after {\i nisuin}). The {\i ra\u8217'n} quotes the {\i Yerushalmi} explicitly: {\i she\u8217'af Rabbi Yehuda lo amar ella l\u8217'chahed et hatalmidim} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's nine cases were pedagogical, not substantive. The Sages\u8217' three categories contain all nine within them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Konam Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The following {\i mishnah} presents two {\i konam} formulations that test the edges of the husband\u8217's annulment authority. The first: {\i konam she\u8217'eini nehenet l\u8217'abba u\u8217'l\u8217'avicha im ose ani al picha} \u8212- a vow that the wife will receive no benefit from her own father or from her father-in-law, conditional on her husband benefiting from her. The two parts of the package work in opposite directions: if the husband eats what she produces, the {\i tnai} is triggered and she cannot benefit from either father \u8212- which constitutes {\i inui nefesh}; but if he does not eat, the {\i tnai} is not triggered, yet the vow sits over him as a reason not to eat from what she makes, which affects {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Package Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One way or the other, this vow touches on something within the husband\u8217's domain: either it will produce {\i inui nefesh} for his wife, or it will constrain the marital dynamic between them. The first {\i mishnah} rules that he can annul it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second part of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's first case raises a harder question. The wife says: {\i she\u8217'eini nehenet l\u8217'abba u\u8217'l\u8217'avicha im ose ani al pi abba u\u8217'al pi avicha} \u8212- I will receive no benefit from you (my husband) if my father or your father benefit from anything I do. Now, if the {\i tnai} is triggered, the result is clearly {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina} and {\i inui nefesh} \u8212- she cannot benefit from her husband. But one might ask: can she simply go through life ensuring that neither father benefits from her, thereby never triggering the vow? If so, there is no {\i inui nefesh} and no {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina} in the untriggered state \u8212- the husband cannot derive marital benefit from whatever she gives to her father or father-in-law, and they do not benefit from her. Why should he be able to annul?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ra\u8217'n} explains that since it is so highly likely that the fathers will at some point benefit from her activity, the vow is treated as effectively anticipating a result that is {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina} and {\i inui nefesh}, and therefore he may annul it. One might add \u8212- building on what we already saw in the previous {\i mishnah} \u8212- that the {\i mishnah} there already established that a husband can annul a vow before it takes effect. If we know that once the {\i tnai} is triggered the vow would be squarely within the categories he may annul, then the question of whether it is certain to be triggered may not be the threshold question at all; it is enough that if it takes effect it would be his to annul, and he may act on that potential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Other {\i Rishonim} \u8212- the {\i Rosh} among them \u8212- prefer a different reading: the package as a whole, even in its untriggered state, is {\i devarim shebeino l\u8217'veina}. The {\i tnai} itself is structurally tied to the marital relationship \u8212- the triggering event is him benefiting from her, and the consequence is her being forbidden to benefit from him \u8212- and this interconnection places the entire construct within the scope of {\i beino l\u8217'veina}, not merely as an indirect annoyance but as something that directly configures the relationship between the two of them. On this reading, the {\i mishnah} is not relying on probabilistic anticipation but on a structural analysis of the vow itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A previous {\i mishnah} established that if she makes a vow about her father or his father\u8217's not benefiting from her, that is not {\i beino l\u8217'veina} \u8212- he may be indirectly upset, but it does not directly touch the marital relationship. Here, the difference is that her inability to give her father benefit is linked by {\i tnai} to a vow that is explicitly about the two of them. That linkage transforms the package into something {\i beino l\u8217'veina}, at least according to these {\i Rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i konam} is the {\i mishnah}\u8217's bolder case: {\i menu\u8217'ah ani min haYehudim im meshameshetcha} \u8212- the benefit of marital relations with any Jewish man is forbidden to me if I have marital relations with you. This is clearly {\i beino l\u8217'veina} and {\i inui nefesh}. The {\i mishnah} rules he may annul it. The mechanism is worth noting: the vow does not directly say she will not have marital relations with her husband; it says the {\i hana\u8217'at tashmish} of all men \u8212- which, since it excludes no one, includes him \u8212- is forbidden to her. This formulation is permitted, whereas directly forbidding marital relations with him would be {\i batel} outright. As a vow of {\i hana\u8217'at tashmish}, the husband is included among those she may not be with if the {\i tnai} is triggered. So one way or another, the vow affects {\i tashmish} with her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records a debate. The case is the first {\i konam} \u8212- the wife will not benefit from her father or father-in-law if the husband benefits from her. {\i Rabbi Natan omer lo yafer} \u8212- Rabbi Natan says the husband cannot annul it. {\i Chachamim omrim yafer} \u8212- the Sages say he can.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ra\u8217'n} explains Rabbi Natan\u8217's position strikingly: Rabbi Natan holds that {\i hafara} \u8212- the annulment of a vow \u8212- can only be performed after the vow has actually taken effect. {\i Eina ba\u8217'al meifer ela im kein chal haneder} \u8212- the husband cannot be {\i meifer} unless and until the {\i neder} has {\i chal}. Therefore, in the first {\i konam} case, where the {\i tnai} has not yet been triggered and the vow has not yet taken effect, Rabbi Natan says he cannot annul it. The {\i chachamim} disagree and hold that the husband may be {\i meifer} even before the {\i neder} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position of Rabbi Natan is striking in context, because it seems to echo the view of Rabbi Yishmael that annulment timing should track the time of effect. Yet Rabbi Natan was not drawn into that earlier discussion. The two principles are related but not identical: Rabbi Yishmael was speaking about which party has the {\i power} to annul (father vs.\u160 ?husband) based on the woman\u8217's status at the time of effect; Rabbi Natan is speaking about whether annulment can occur at all before the vow takes effect. They are parallel intuitions operating in different analytical registers.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Worldly Deprivation Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a story that illuminates the debate between Rabbi Natan and the {\i chachamim} and then leads into a broader question about {\i hatarat nedarim}. A certain man forbade himself all benefit from the world \u8212- {\i asar hana\u8217'at d\u8217'alma alei} \u8212- conditional on his getting married before he had had the opportunity to learn {\i halachah}. His intent was to motivate himself: he wanted to spend a period of genuine study in {\i yeshiva} before marrying, and he took this vow as a spur to his own commitment. He was not merely saying he would try; he set a substantive goal of {\i taninan hilcheta} \u8212- mastering the essential corpus of legal learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temptation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He tried with every possible effort \u8212- {\i rehat b\u8217'agpei v\u8217'tuvlei}, running with his staff and flask, or a ladder and pulley, depending on the reading; all idioms for maximum exertion \u8212- but he was unable to accomplish the learning he had set out to do. He could not find the right teacher, or could not succeed in understanding, or the circumstances simply did not permit it. He remained unmarried, bound by the social and personal pressures of not having fulfilled his vow\u8217's condition. Then Rav Acha bar Rav Huna came and, as {\i piteihu} \u8212- he misled him \u8212- told him that since he had tried as hard as he could, he had satisfied his vow and could proceed to marry. The man did so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Subsequently \u8212- and this is where the tar or mud comes in \u8212- Rav Acha bar Rav Huna apparently caused the man to realize, through some kind of practical intervention, that he might in fact be bound by his vow after all. The exact nature of the intervention is debated among the {\i Rishonim}: one reading has him throwing dirt on the man\u8217's garments, making him unable to get them cleaned without deriving benefit from the world; another reading has him applying tar to make the man unrecognizable. One plausible reading is that having gotten the man to marry, Rav Acha bar Rav Huna then made him feel that his vow had actually taken effect \u8212- i.e., that the marriage had triggered it \u8212- so that the man would now be in a position to seek a formal {\i hatarat nedarim}. The man\u8217's embarrassment in coming to Rav Chisda may explain why he concealed his identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Appeal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The man came before Rav Chisda and laid out the situation: he had tried as hard as he could, he had taken a wife, and he was now uncertain whether his vow had taken effect. He asked to be released from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then reflects on what Rav Acha bar Rav Huna had done and praises it: {\i man hakim le-mevad ki hai milta i lav Rav Aha bar Rav Huna} \u8212- who but Rav Acha bar Rav Huna would have been clever enough to set this up? The genius of the strategy, Rava explains, was that Rav Acha bar Rav Huna understood that the same {\i machloket} between Rabbi Natan and the {\i chachamim} that applies to {\i hafara} \u8212- whether the vow must take effect before annulment \u8212- {\i hacha nami paligi b\u8217'she\u8217'eila}, might also apply to {\i she\u8217'eilat chacham}, the process of a sage releasing someone from a vow. Rabbi Natan might hold that just as a husband cannot be {\i meifer} until the {\i neder} takes effect, so too a {\i chacham} cannot release a vow until it has taken effect. Rather than leave the man in the position of seeking release from a vow that had not yet definitively triggered, Rav Acha bar Rav Huna arranged for the vow to take effect \u8212- by having the man get married and thereby trigger the condition \u8212- so that the release could proceed cleanly even according to Rabbi Natan\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatarat Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the explicit question: is the debate between Rabbi Natan and the {\i chachamim} limited to {\i hafara} by the husband, or does it extend to {\i hatarat nedarim} by a {\i chacham}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa answers: {\i mahloket b\u8217'hafara} \u8212- the debate between Rabbi Natan and the {\i chachamim} is only about {\i hafara}. Rabbi Natan holds {\i eina ba\u8217'al meifer ela im kein chal haneder}, and derives this from the verse {\i ve-hafrah ha-levanah} \u8212- playing on the similarity between {\i ve-hafrah} and {\i hafara}, and reading {\i ha-levanah} as {\i chal} the {\i bamah} (since {\i kol ha-noder ke-ilu boneh bamah}, one who vows is as if he builds a {\i bamah}): {\i hafara} operates when {\i chal} the vow, i.e., when the vow has taken effect. The {\i rabanan}, for their part, derive that {\i hafara} may occur even before the vow takes effect from the verse {\i mefer mahshevot arumim} \u8212- God annuls the schemes of the devious \u8212- meaning one can annul even a thought, even something not yet actualized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universal Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, when it comes to {\i she\u8217'eilah} \u8212- releasing through a {\i chacham} \u8212- {\i divrei ha-kol ein chacham matir klum ela im kein chal haneder}: everyone agrees, both Rabbi Natan and the {\i chachamim}, that a sage cannot release a vow until it has taken effect. The source is the verse {\i lo yahel devaro} \u8212- he may not violate his word \u8212- from which we derive that {\i others} may release his word for him; but the word {\i yahel} contains within it the word {\i chal}, teaching that the release operates only once there is {\i chal devaro}, once the vow has taken effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of this is admittedly difficult. The mechanism of {\i hatarat nedarim} is that the {\i chacham} retrospectively identifies the vow as having been made {\i b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- in error \u8212- as though the person would not have wanted to take it had he foreseen the circumstances. Retroactive recharacterization of this kind should not, intuitively, require the vow to have taken effect. Yet the formal requirement stands: the process of {\i hatarat nedarim} has an independent formal structure, and that structure, according to all opinions, requires {\i chal} as a prerequisite. This is why Rav Acha bar Rav Huna\u8217's strategy was not merely playing it safe with respect to Rabbi Natan\u8217's position on {\i hafara} but was in fact necessary from the perspective of {\i hatarat nedarim} according to everyone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 90\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hatarat Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up in the middle of daf 90, approximately eleven lines down, at the words {\i leima mesayei lei}. The preceding discussion had introduced a complicated case from our {\i mishnah} involving the prohibition of {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- benefit \u8212- and a {\i braita} that recorded the position of Rabbi Dosa concerning a woman who said, in effect, \u8220"I am forbidden to have sex with you if I have sex with you.\u8221" The question raised was: why cannot the husband be {\i meifer} such a vow? After all, it seems clearly to fall within the categories of {\i inuy nefesh} or {\i devarim shebeino leveinah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Dosa\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} established that Rabbi Dosa\u8217's position is that a husband cannot be {\i meifer} a {\i neder} until it has actually taken effect \u8212- {\i ad sheyachol haneder}. The {\i chachamim} disagree with this requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially find this surprising. Intuitively, {\i hafara} \u8212- the husband\u8217's annulment \u8212- seems to be precisely the kind of power that operates on an existing vow: the husband confronts a {\i neder} already in force and overrides it. It is {\i hatara} \u8212- annulment by a {\i chacham} \u8212- that seems to operate retroactively, going back to the original speech act and declaring it to have been made in error. One might therefore have expected the requirement of prior effectuation to apply more naturally to {\i hafara}, while {\i hatara} could plausibly function even before the vow has taken hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha\u8217's Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This tension is illustrated by a story about Rav Acha bar Rav Huna, who tricked someone into triggering the {\i tenai} \u8212- the condition \u8212- of his vow so that it would become {\i chal}, and only then sought {\i hatara} from a {\i chacham}. The {\i Gemara} explained that Rav Acha bar Rav Huna believed the same debate that governs {\i hafara} applies to {\i hatara} as well, and he therefore wanted to satisfy the position of Rabbi Dosa by ensuring the {\i neder} had taken effect before seeking its dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then advances an even more far-reaching position, attributed to Rav Pappi: the debate between Rabbi Dosa and the {\i chachamim} applies only to {\i hafara}, but when it comes to {\i hatara}, there is no debate at all \u8212- even the {\i chachamim} agree that a {\i chacham} cannot be {\i matir} a {\i neder} until it has taken effect. This is striking precisely because it runs counter to the intuition outlined above. One would expect {\i hatara}, with its retroactive character, to be the procedure more capable of operating before effectuation, yet Rav Pappi places the stricter requirement there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Yachel Devaro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappi derives this from the verse {\i lo yachel devaro} \u8212- \u8220"he shall not profane his word\u8221" \u8212- the very source from which we learn that a {\i chacham} has the power to annul a {\i neder}. The word {\i yachel} shares its root with {\i chal}, to take effect. The implication, on this reading, is that the vow can only be voided after it has first been {\i chal}: it can only be un-{\i chal} once it has been {\i chal}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Konam She\u8217'eini\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Dual Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now attempts to bring a supporting proof for Rav Pappi\u8217's position. The {\i braita} reads: a person says {\i konam she\u8217'eini ne\u8217'eneh li-Ploni} \u8212- a {\i konam} prohibiting benefit between himself and Ploni \u8212- and adds that whoever serves as the {\i chacham} to grant {\i hatara} on this {\i neder} will also be forbidden to him. The Ran reads the case as Ploni being prohibited from benefiting from the person making the vow, though the grammar of the vernacular formulation remains ambiguous even at this late stage of {\i Maseches Nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What results is a kind of M\u246 ?bius {\i neder}: two people are rendered {\i assur} \u8212- Reuven and the as-yet-unidentified {\i chacham} who will grant the {\i heter}. The {\i braita} then rules that one must first obtain {\i hatara} for the first prohibition \u8212- the one involving Reuven \u8212- and only afterward obtain {\i hatara} for the prohibition that thereby fell upon the {\i chacham} who granted it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism is this: the first {\i chacham}, say Rabbi Cohen, is {\i matir} the {\i neder} as it pertains to Reuven. At the moment he does so, the condition is met and the prohibition against him personally takes effect. A second {\i chacham} must then be {\i matir} that prohibition. The two must be sequential, not simultaneous, because a {\i chacham} who is himself a party to the {\i neder} \u8212- a {\i nogea ba-davar} \u8212- cannot serve as its {\i matir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} argues from this {\i braita} in favor of Rav Pappi: the {\i braita} instructs that one must first resolve the prohibition against Reuven, allowing the prohibition against the {\i chacham} to take effect, and only then seek {\i hatara} for that second prohibition. This ordering proves that {\i hatara} requires the {\i neder} to have taken effect first. If {\i hatara} could work before a {\i neder} takes effect, one could simply approach a second {\i chacham} and preemptively undo the prohibition against the first {\i chacham} before that first {\i chacham} even renders his ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rishon Reinterpreted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects the proof by arguing that {\i rishon} in the {\i braita} need not mean \u8220"the first one you took\u8221" \u8212- it may simply mean \u8220"do one and then do the other.\u8221" The {\i braita} need not be teaching that the first {\i neder} chronologically must be dissolved before the second; it may merely be instructing that the two dissolutions must be sequential rather than simultaneous, without specifying which comes first. This is, however, an unsatisfying reading of the {\i braita}, both because such a banal instruction scarcely requires a {\i braita} to communicate it, and because the word {\i rishon} in context naturally implies chronological priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yesh Breira Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a further objection that the {\i Gemara} does not raise, though it seems compelling in its own right. Even setting aside whether the {\i neder} against the {\i chacham} has yet taken effect, there is a {\i yesh breira} type of difficulty: at the time one seeks to preemptively undo the prohibition against the {\i chacham}, we do not yet know who that {\i chacham} will be. One might intend to go to Rabbi Cohen, but then change one\u8217's mind and go to a different {\i chacham}. How can one be {\i matir} a {\i neder} whose subject has not yet been determined? The parameters of the {\i neder} are themselves unknown. Why the {\i Gemara} does not invoke this argument \u8212- which seems to be the more obvious objection \u8212- and instead resorts to the weaker reinterpretation of {\i rishon}, is unclear. Nevertheless, the {\i Gemara} proceeds as it does.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Triggered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Plus Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts a cleaner proof from a second {\i braita}: one declares {\i konam she\u8217'eini ne\u8217'eneh le-Ploni} \u8212- a {\i konam} prohibiting benefit from Reuven \u8212- and adds that if this {\i neder} is ever dissolved, he will become a {\i nazir}. The {\i braita} rules that one must first be {\i nish\u8217'al} \u8212- seek {\i hatara} \u8212- on the {\i neder}, and only afterward seek {\i hatara} on the {\i nezirut}. This case is far less ambiguous than the previous one. The two objects are clearly distinct: the {\i neder} and the {\i nezirut}. There is no question of which is {\i rishon} and which is {\i sheini}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why must they be resolved in that order? Because the {\i nezirut} does not yet exist when the {\i neder} is first made \u8212- it is triggered only when the {\i neder} is dissolved. The {\i braita}\u8217's instruction to first obtain {\i hatara} on the {\i neder} before obtaining {\i hatara} on the {\i nezirut} presupposes that the {\i nezirut} must actually have come into existence before it can be dissolved. This appears to be a straightforward proof that {\i hatara} requires the {\i neder} to be in effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: according to the view that {\i hatara} can work before a {\i neder} takes effect, why not dissolve the {\i nezirut} first? One could approach a {\i chacham} and say: I undertook {\i nezirut} contingent upon a certain condition being met, I regret having done so, and I wish it not to take effect when that condition is triggered. The fact that the {\i braita} insists on the opposite order seems to prove that the condition must be triggered \u8212- the {\i nezirut} must become {\i chal} \u8212- before {\i hatara} is possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: this {\i braita} may represent only the position of Rabbi Natan, not a consensus ruling. The {\i braita} is admittedly taught anonymously, which ordinarily suggests broader agreement. Nevertheless the {\i Gemara} maintains that the proof is limited \u8212- the {\i braita} establishes clearly that this one position requires effectuation before {\i hatara}, but it does not demonstrate that the view is universally held, as Rav Pappi claimed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Pappi Alt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mareimar Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces an entirely different version of Rav Pappi\u8217's position. Ravina reports in the name of Mareimar, who transmitted it in the name of Rav Pappi: the debate between Rabbi Dosa and the {\i chachamim} applies only to {\i hafara}, but when it comes to {\i hatara}, everyone agrees that a {\i chacham} can be {\i matir} the {\i neder} even before it takes effect. This is the exact inverse of the previous version of Rav Pappi. Both versions agree that the dispute is limited to {\i hafara}; they disagree only on which direction the consensus regarding {\i hatara} runs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This second version actually accords more naturally with the intuition developed at the outset. Since {\i hatara} operates by retroactively identifying an error in the original speech act, there is no obvious reason it cannot be applied to a speech act that has not yet produced a binding effect. If anything, the retroactive character of {\i hatara} makes it more suited to early intervention, not less.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Emphasis on Divro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This version of Rav Pappi also draws from {\i lo yachel devaro}, but with a different emphasis. Where the first version stressed the word {\i yachel} \u8212- implying that the {\i neder} must first be {\i chal} before it can be undone \u8212- this version stresses {\i devaro}, \u8220"his word.\u8221" The moment a person utters the vow, the speech act itself exists and is available for a {\i chacham} to address. The effectuation of the {\i neder} is not what renders it subject to {\i hatara}; the act of speaking is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counterargument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this version by reinstating the same {\i braitot} considered earlier. First, the {\i braita} about the dual {\i konam} affecting Reuven and the dissolving {\i chacham}: if {\i hatara} is available before effectuation, why must the dissolutions occur in sequence at all? The {\i Gemara} repeats its answer \u8212- {\i rishon} simply means one before the other, not necessarily the first chronologically \u8212- which is equally unsatisfying here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i braita} about the {\i neder} and the conditional {\i nezirut} poses the real difficulty. The {\i Gemara} asks: according to this version of Rav Pappi, why cannot one dissolve the {\i nezirut} first, before the {\i neder} is even dissolved and the {\i nezirut} triggered? The {\i Gemara} pronounces {\i tiyuvta} \u8212- a refutation. This version of Rav Pappi appears to be defeated by the {\i braita}, which requires that the {\i neder} be dissolved and the {\i nezirut} triggered before the {\i nezirut} itself can be dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional vs Postponed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here a conceptually important distinction emerges. The {\i braita} about the {\i nazir} case involves a genuinely conditional {\i neder} \u8212- one whose effectuation is contingent upon the occurrence of a specific event that may or may not come to pass. This is categorically different from a {\i neder} whose effect has been postponed \u8212- for example, a vow stated to take effect in thirty days. In the postponed case, effectuation is certain; the {\i neder} will definitely become {\i chal}, it simply has not done so yet. In the conditional case, there remains a genuine question whether the condition will ever be triggered and whether the {\i neder} will ever take effect at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument of Rabbi Natan and the supporting {\i braitot} may be specifically addressing the conditional case. If a {\i neder} is conditional and might never take effect, it is unclear what a {\i chacham} is dissolving \u8212- there may be nothing there to dissolve. By contrast, a postponed {\i neder} that will certainly take effect at a future date represents a genuine vow from the moment of its utterance, and the question of whether {\i hatara} can be performed before the appointed date may remain genuinely open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakhic Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} at the top of 90b states the {\i halakha} explicitly: {\i le-inyan she\u8217'eila kaima lan k\u8217'Rav Pappa d\u8217'lo machlokes b\u8217'hafara aval b\u8217'she\u8217'ela divrei hakol ein chacham matir ad sheyachol haneder} \u8212- when it comes to {\i hatara}, we rule like the first version of Rav Pappi that everyone agrees a {\i chacham} cannot dissolve a {\i neder} until it has taken effect. The {\i Ran} notes that the great authorities ruled this way {\i l\u8217'chumra}, as the strict position, though he acknowledges that another position exists maintaining the debate applies equally to {\i hatara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With respect to {\i hafara}, the case is clearer. The earlier {\i sugya} \u8212- involving a woman who took a vow while married but whose vow took effect thirty days later after her divorce, with Rabbi Akiva ruling that the husband could annul the vow at the time it was taken \u8212- already implicitly established that we rule like the {\i chachamim} that {\i hafara} can occur before the {\i neder} takes effect. The story of the man tricked into triggering his {\i tenai} might seem to complicate this \u8212- the {\i chacham} there was concerned to satisfy the position of Rabbi Natan \u8212- but the {\i Ran} notes that the overwhelming consensus of the {\i Gemara} is that we do not rule like Rabbi Natan even by {\i hafara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Postponed Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} also addresses the question of postponed, as opposed to conditional, vows for purposes of {\i hatara}. He records two views. One position holds that a {\i chacham} may dissolve a postponed {\i neder} even before its appointed time arrives, reasoning that since the {\i neder} will certainly take effect, there is effectively already a {\i neder} in existence \u8212- it has merely been deferred. The other position holds that even in such a case, since the person has not yet become {\i assur}, a {\i chacham} cannot yet be {\i matir}. The {\i Ran} states his preference for the first view: {\i sevara kama tritza tefei} \u8212- there is a meaningful conceptual difference between a conditional {\i neder}, which might never materialize, and a postponed {\i neder}, which will certainly take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical distinction turns on how we understand Rav Pappi\u8217's ruling. If the prohibition on {\i hatara} before effectuation is grounded in the uncertainty of the conditional case \u8212- we cannot treat something as a {\i neder} when we do not know whether it will ever function as one \u8212- then once the outcome is certain, even a pre-effectuation {\i hatara} may be permissible. If, however, the ruling is understood more literally \u8212- a {\i chacham} simply cannot act until the {\i assur} actually exists \u8212- then the certainty of future effectuation is irrelevant, and {\i hatara} must wait in all cases. The general ruling follows Rav Pappi: {\i hatara} requires effectuation, with the question of postponed vows remaining subject to the views just described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that, we arrive at the final {\i mishnah} of the tractate \u8212- a {\i mishnah} whose {\i halakhic} implications extend far beyond the narrow domain of {\i nedarim} and whose reach into questions of marriage, divorce, belief, and human dignity makes it among the most consequential texts in the entire {\i masekhet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women\u8217's Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Stage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i barishona hayu omrim, shalosh nashim yotz\u8217'ot ve-notlot ketubbah} \u8212- they used to say that three categories of women are granted divorce and collect their {\i ketubbah}. This opening formula signals what will follow: a two-stage ruling in which an initial policy is reversed. The three women are those who bring claims that, under the initial ruling, were believed without requiring independent corroboration. Not only are they forced to divorce, but the divorce is treated as no fault of their own \u8212- hence they receive their {\i ketubbah} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first category is {\i ha-omeret teme\u8217'a ani lach} \u8212- a woman who tells her husband \u8220"I am {\i tame\u8217'ah} to you.\u8221" This means either that she was violated \u8212- which would be directly relevant if her husband is a {\i kohen}, since even a raped woman becomes forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband \u8212- or that she committed adultery. But if the woman committed adultery, why would she receive her {\i ketubbah}? This prompts the {\i Gemara} to conclude that the receipt of the {\i ketubbah} applies specifically to the case of an {\i eshet kohen} who was raped: she was the victim, bears no culpability, and is therefore entitled to her {\i ketubbah}. The initial policy of believing her, however, presumably extends to both cases \u8212- {\i ketubbah} or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Underlying this policy is the principle of {\i shavya nafshah chatikhah de-issura}: by making this declaration, the woman effectively defines herself as forbidden. One might also argue that the initial policy was simply grounded in the assumption that a person would not make such a claim without it being true. The husband may insist he does not believe her, but if in practice he does believe her, that becomes a separate problem we will return to. At this first stage, the {\i mishnah} says: we believe her, we compel divorce, and \u8212- in the {\i kohen} case \u8212- we require the {\i ketubbah} to be paid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shamayim Beini\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second category is {\i shamayim beini u-veinecha} \u8212- \u8220"heaven is between us.\u8221" This phrase signals a claim that only God can attest to, meaning it pertains to some aspect of their intimate life that no external witness could observe. The {\i Gemara} understands this as a claim that the husband is incapable of {\i yoreh ke-chetz} \u8212- of normal ejaculation \u8212- and consequently cannot give the wife children. She is therefore demanding a divorce on the grounds that she cannot have children with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might object: since a woman is not commanded in {\i pru u-rvu}, why should her inability to have children constitute grounds for a forced divorce? The {\i Gemara} in {\i Ketubot} addresses this and concludes that a woman has a legitimate human interest in having children \u8212- framed in part as a concern for who will care for her in old age \u8212- and that this constitutes a sufficient claim. It is worth noting that the claim here is not about the {\i mitzvah} of {\i onah}, of marital intimacy itself. Even if the husband cannot produce children, the couple can presumably have marital relations; it is specifically the inability to give her children that drives the claim. That the failure to fulfill {\i onah} is not itself raised as grounds for divorce is an interesting implication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the initial policy, the wife is believed on this claim as well, the divorce is compelled, and the {\i ketubbah} is paid \u8212- again on the assumption that she has been wronged and is not at fault.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estrangement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} raises the possibility that {\i shamayim beini u-veinecha} carries a broader meaning than the {\i Gemara}\u8217's technical reading. He cites the {\i Yerushalmi}, which reads the phrase as a claim about marital distance: {\i k\u8217'ma d\u8217'Shamayim rechuka min ar\u8217'a, kach hi iteta rechika m\u8217'ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- just as the heaven is distant from the earth, so is this wife distant from her husband. On this reading, the claim need not be specifically about the husband\u8217's physiological incapacity; a general assertion of profound marital estrangement would itself constitute a cognizable basis for divorce, and the wife would be believed on that claim alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} acknowledges that the Babylonian {\i Gemara} understands the phrase as referring specifically to {\i yoreh ke-chetz}, and supports this with a paraphrase: {\i Elokim eid beini u-veinecha d\u8217'kushta kamina she-davar zeh eino yadua bo ela la-Shem} \u8212- God alone is witness to this, for this matter is known to none but God. On the {\i Gemara}\u8217's reading, the claim must be about something that only God can testify to, and something specific \u8212- namely, the inability to have children. Nevertheless, the {\i Ran} notes that according to the {\i Yerushalmi}, the language {\i efshar d\u8217'lashon Hashamayim beini l\u8217'veinecha kolel hakol} \u8212- perhaps the phrase encompasses both ideas: the physiological claim and the claim of estrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} bridges these positions in a different way. He frames the woman\u8217's claim in terms of marital intimacy itself: she declares that she finds her husband so intolerable that she cannot bear to have sex with him. This formulation makes the claim not about whether children are possible but about whether marital life is sustainable. The {\i Rambam} holds that no person can be compelled to remain in a marriage in which intimate relations have become impossible to tolerate, and that principle grounds the entitlement to divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezurah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third category is {\i netulah ani min ha-Yehudim} \u8212- she has taken a vow prohibiting herself from having relations with any Jew. This is why the case appears in {\i Maseches Nedarim}. But the logic requires unpacking. If she has forbidden herself relations with all Jews, she will be unable to have relations even after the divorce \u8212- so why does the marriage need to end?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that if a woman has reached the point of taking a vow of this extremity, we assume she cannot tolerate marital relations. Whether the cause is physiological, psychological, or otherwise, the assumption is that something has driven her to this drastic declaration, and it would be unconscionable to compel her to remain in the marriage under such circumstances. Why, one might ask, cannot the husband simply annul the vow through {\i hafara} \u8212- since it is a {\i devarim shebeino leveinah} \u8212- and continue the marriage? And in fact, as we will see, under the revised ruling, that is precisely what happens with respect to his portion. But under the initial ruling, we believe her claim as to the circumstances that led to the vow and accordingly compel the divorce.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reversal & Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazru Lomar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i chazru lomar} \u8212- they reconsidered and revised the ruling. This formula signals a deliberate reversal of the initial policy. The same three categories of women who had been believed and given both divorce and {\i ketubbah} are now treated differently across the board.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natna Eineha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stated reason for the reversal is {\i shelo tehei ishah notenet einehah be-acher u-mekaklelet al ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- that a woman should not set her eye on another man and thereby ruin her marriage. The concern is that the original policy, however well-intentioned, created a mechanism for abuse: a woman who wished to leave her husband for another relationship could simply manufacture one of the three qualifying claims, obtain a divorce together with her {\i ketubbah}, and face no adverse consequences for doing so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the revised ruling, {\i ha-omeret teme\u8217'a ani lach tavi ra\u8217'ayah li-devareha} \u8212- a woman who claims she is forbidden to her husband must now bring proof. If no proof is forthcoming, the couple remains married. For the claim of {\i shamayim beini u-veinecha}, the ruling is {\i ya\u8217'aseh derekh bakashah} \u8212- the husband is asked to make efforts to placate her or address her grievance. The {\i Yerushalmi} suggests something as concrete as making her a fine meal; the {\i Gemara} understands this as an attempt at reconciliation, whether through marital counseling or similar means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Divorce/Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the case of {\i ha-nezurah min ha-Yehudim}, the ruling under revision is that the husband annuls his portion of the {\i neder} \u8212- {\i yafer le-chelko} \u8212- since it pertains to {\i devarim shebeino leveinah}, and they continue marital relations. If she is eventually divorced for other reasons, she remains bound by the vow as it applies to all other Jews, since the husband\u8217's annulment covers only his own portion. The practical result is that we are in effect saying we do not believe her \u8212- or at least that we will not act as if we believe her. If we are not going to force a divorce based on her claim, then we will require her to continue in the marriage as though the underlying concern did not exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is notable is how binary this treatment is. One might have expected a middle position: believe her enough to compel a divorce, but not enough to grant a {\i ketubbah}. That would impose a financial cost on making the claim while still providing relief to a woman in a genuinely intolerable situation. This is indeed the position of the {\i Rambam} when a woman says she finds her husband physically intolerable: he rules that she receives a divorce but forfeits her {\i ketubbah}, the financial disincentive serving as a filter against bad-faith claims while preserving a genuine avenue of relief. That middle position is not adopted here. Under the revised ruling it is all or nothing: either we believe her fully, or we do not believe her at all, and there is no divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The real-world consequences of this reversal are far-reaching. A woman who genuinely cannot tolerate her marriage \u8212- whether because of estrangement, because her husband has treated her terribly, or because the marriage has broken down in every meaningful sense \u8212- finds herself without recourse. This is the dynamic that produces the {\i agunah} situation in its various forms. Courts invoking {\i shalom bayit} as a response to such claims are in fact carrying forward the logic of {\i chazru lomar} \u8212- the reversal itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is crucial to be precise here: what the {\i mishnah} is actually saying, in its initial ruling, is that these conditions are genuinely intolerable and warrant divorce. The only thing that shifted in the revision is the question of proof and the fear of abuse. But the downstream consequence is that the framework of \u8220"try to work it out\u8221" and \u8220"we are not going to believe you\u8221" becomes the default response to women\u8217's claims of marital breakdown \u8212- even in cases where evidence eventually becomes available, or where the claims are clearly substantiated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry is also worth noting. A man who wishes to leave his marriage may simply do so, within the limits of {\i beit din} authority; even {\i beit Shammai} requires cause, but the threshold for the husband is structurally lower. A woman who wishes to leave must overcome the presumption created by {\i chazru lomar}, no matter how compelling her situation. That asymmetry, rooted in a polygamous social structure in which a man can have multiple wives and a woman can have only one husband, is the foundation upon which all of this rests.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shavya Nafsha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Belief Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to what is perhaps the deepest and most philosophically challenging problem raised by the {\i mishnah}. The revised ruling says we do not believe the woman. But what does it mean for {\i halakha} to officially refuse to believe something that may be true \u8212- or that the husband himself believes to be true?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i shavya nafshah chatikhah de-issura} holds that a person\u8217's own declaration can create a binding prohibition for themselves, regardless of whether witnesses can corroborate the claim. One hundred witnesses may testify that a piece of meat is {\i kosher}; if I declare it to be {\i chazir}, I am bound by my own declaration and may not eat it. My self-assessment creates a binding legal reality for me, independent of the broader legal determination. So here: if a woman declares herself forbidden to her husband \u8212- whether through a claim of adultery, rape, or a vow \u8212- why does {\i chazru lomar} dissolve the force of {\i shavya nafsha}? And if the husband himself believes his wife and says so, does not his belief create a {\i shavya} for him as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafkat Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One answer proposed by the {\i rishonim} is {\i afkinu rabbanan le-kiddushin mineih} \u8212- the rabbis retroactively annul the {\i kiddushin}. On this approach, in those cases where the woman\u8217's claim is in fact true, the rabbis exercise their authority to undo the marriage from the outset, which means the {\i kiddushin} never had legal force, and accordingly neither the prohibition that would normally result from adultery \u8212- nor the concern about {\i shavya nafsha} \u8212- ever attaches. The husband and wife continue living together because they are, retroactively, as though never halakhically married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a radical position. And the {\i rishonim} themselves note its limits: it does not resolve the case of an {\i eshet kohen} who slept with a non-Jew while married, since the prohibition on her is not derived from the marriage itself but from a separate status that {\i hafkat kiddushin} cannot undo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second, somewhat less radical answer focuses on the specific textual source of the {\i kohen} prohibition. The verse forbidding a {\i kohen} from living with a woman who has been defiled speaks to the {\i kohen} \u8212- it is his prohibition. The woman herself has no separate Torah prohibition against living with her {\i kohen} husband as a result of her status. So even if she knows she has become forbidden to him according to his {\i issur}, she is not herself in violation by continuing to live with him. His prohibition and hers are asymmetric. This answer, however, applies only to the {\i kohen} case and does nothing to resolve the adultery scenario involving a {\i yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What appears ultimately to be the underlying position \u8212- even if the {\i rishonim} do not state it so starkly \u8212- is that {\i halakha} defines reality. If the legal system has determined as a matter of policy that this class of claims is not to be believed, then the legal effect of those claims is suspended. The {\i shavya nafsha} principle may itself be overridden when the relevant declaration falls into a category that {\i halakha} has ruled non-cognizable. The declaration simply has no legal traction within the system that has decided not to credit it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses the problem of the husband directly and practically. At the end of the {\i Tosafot}, in the widest lines, {\i Tosafot} states: {\i d\u8217'af le-mishnah achronah im omeret ishah teme\u8217'ah ani lakh v\u8217'amar ha-ba\u8217'al she-ma\u8217'amina} \u8212- even under the revised ruling, if the wife says \u8220"I am {\i tame\u8217'ah} to you\u8221" and the husband says that he believes her, she becomes forbidden to him through his {\i shavya}. And if subsequently the wife reverses her claim and says it was in fact under duress \u8212- {\i b\u8217'ones} \u8212- so that perhaps his {\i shavya} should dissolve along with the underlying claim, {\i Tosafot} rules that once he is bound by {\i shavya}, he cannot undo it through her retraction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then records a further position: {\i v\u8217'yesh omrim d\u8217'mshamtinan lei al she-garam she-tei\u8217'aser ishto alav} \u8212- some authorities say we place the husband in {\i cherem} if he chooses to believe his wife, because by doing so he effectively forces a divorce upon her against her will, in violation of {\i cherem de-Rabbeinu Gershom}. The wife, under the revised ruling, has a legal right to remain married; she is believed when she says she wants to continue the marriage, even if she made a contrary claim previously. If the husband invokes {\i shavya nafsha} based on her prior claim, he is unilaterally imposing a divorce on a woman who does not wish to be divorced, which is precisely what {\i Rabbeinu Gershom} forbade.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication is that the husband is counseled: do not say you believe her. Not because the law prohibits him from having private beliefs, but because if he articulates that belief, he will find himself in {\i cherem}. The threat of {\i cherem} operates as a kind of legal prophylactic against the problem of {\i shavya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Official vs Real\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\i Tosafot}\u8217's ruling reveals is an attempt at communal policy-making that navigates a genuinely impossible tension. The law officially does not believe the woman. The husband, who lives with her, who knows her, may believe her completely. But the legal system says: as long as you do not officially say you believe her, {\i shavya} does not engage and the marriage can legally continue. And the community reinforces this by threatening {\i cherem} against anyone who invokes {\i shavya nafsha} in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the point at which {\i halakha} and lived reality come into sharpest conflict. A husband who privately believes his wife was raped, or that she committed adultery, or that their marriage is genuinely broken, is told: for the legal system to function as it has been designed to function, you may not say this publicly, and if you do, you bear the consequences. The pastoral and psychological dimensions of this \u8212- how a couple is supposed to rebuild or continue a marriage under such conditions, how a woman is supposed to process her own experience within a framework that officially denies its legal relevance \u8212- are largely left unaddressed by the formal {\i halakhic} discussion. That silence is itself significant.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nedarim 91\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Belief & Disbelief\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf before us is 91, the last daf of Nedarim, and we pick up at 91b at the {\i gemara} following the {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} presents a powerful set of cases: a woman who claims she has been raped, or who claims that she committed adultery, or who claims that she is otherwise unable to remain with her husband \u8212- and the question is whether she is believed. The {\i mishnah} wrestles with the consequences of that belief, particularly regarding the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opening position of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the {\i mishnah rishona} \u8212- holds that the woman is believed, and that her belief extends even to the {\i ketubah}. There is no concern that she might be fabricating her claim in order to force a divorce; she is credited fully, even though believing her provides her with no financial disincentive to lie.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The later position \u8212- the {\i mishnah acharona} \u8212- reverses course. There, the {\i rabbanan} express concern that the woman is inventing the entire account in order to extract a {\i get}, and therefore she is not believed in any of these cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Middle Path\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Between these two poles lies the Rambam\u8217's middle position, highly significant in its practical implications: if a woman declares that she finds her husband so intolerable that she cannot bear to live with him and cannot maintain intimate relations, the Rambam holds that she is believed \u8212- but she does not collect her {\i ketubah}. Critically, the Rambam rules that the husband is compelled to grant her a {\i get}. This position, which has largely been abandoned in the {\i Ashkenazi} world, bears directly on contemporary {\i agunah} discussions as a path that has been foregone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah & Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a {\i she\u8217'elah}: in the case of a wife of a {\i kohen} who claims she was raped \u8212- which is the paradigm case for the {\i mishnah}, since rape renders such a woman forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband while it would not render the wife of a {\i Yisrael} forbidden \u8212- and who is not believed according to the {\i mishnah acharona}, what is her status with respect to {\i terumah}? Can she continue eating {\i terumah}? How thoroughly do we disbelieve her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} rules that she continues to eat {\i terumah}. To act otherwise \u8212- to tell her to refrain from {\i terumah} \u8212- would be to communicate to the world that she has a doubtful status, that there is a {\i safek} regarding her. We cannot adopt a position that formally says we do not believe her and simultaneously signal to the outside world that we half-believe her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelo Totzi La\u8217'az\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative concern is {\i shelo totzi la\u8217'az al baneha} \u8212- that casting doubt on her status will raise questions about her children\u8217's legitimacy as {\i kohanim}. If she is actually forbidden to her husband and subsequently bears children, those children would be {\i chalalim}. Any public signal of ambiguity regarding her status would therefore taint the status of children born during the marriage. So {\i Rav Sheshet} insists: we must project a complete, unambiguous position that she is fully permitted to her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} disagrees. He rules that she should not eat {\i terumah}. His reasoning is that abstaining from {\i terumah} does not necessarily communicate a problematic status to the outside world \u8212- {\i efshar d\u8217'achla chullin}, she might simply be eating the {\i chulin} food in the house rather than the {\i terumah}. The practical oddity of this logic is readily apparent: it is hardly inconspicuous when someone consistently avoids a particular category of food, and those who visit would certainly notice. Nevertheless, that is {\i Rava}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Qualification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} adds, however, that even {\i Rav Sheshet} would agree that if she becomes widowed or divorced, she should stop eating {\i terumah}. Why? Because while her husband was alive and she was eating {\i terumah}, any children born during that period will be assumed by observers to have been born under a period of her uncontested status. If, after her husband\u8217's death, people notice she has stopped eating {\i terumah}, they will naturally assume something occurred {\i after} the husband died \u8212- a subsequent rape or other incident \u8212- and that will not retroactively cast doubt on the children born during the marriage. Thus, {\i Rav Sheshet}\u8217's policy of protecting her status for the sake of the children can, in {\i Rava}\u8217's view, be suspended once the marriage has ended, since the temporal gap protects the children\u8217's status regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet this is a deeply strange position. {\i Rava} is saying that we should instruct this woman to begin adopting a practice that will broadcast to the community that she has a problematic status. The underlying tension is significant: {\i Rava} is unable to fully set aside his discomfort with entirely disbelieving her. He cannot fully let go of the possibility that her claim might be true, and that unease resurfaces once she leaves the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is most striking is that both {\i Rav Sheshet} and {\i Rava}, despite their disagreement on practical policy, are each reflecting a residual uncertainty. Neither has fully made peace with the position that she simply is not believed. {\i Rav Sheshet} sets aside his discomfort for reasons of public policy; {\i Rava} cannot. But both are signaling that the doubts have not been fully extinguished.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Standards of Belief\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheskas Tahara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Setting aside the question \u8212- which arose in the previous discussion \u8212- of {\i shavya nafshah chaticha de\u8217'issura}, of what she herself knows in her own mind, it is worth stepping back to recall that it is not so straightforward that a woman\u8217's self-report should be automatically credited to change her status. Consider the analogy: if a pile of grain had an established {\i chezkas tahara} and a single witness reported that a {\i tamei} person touched it, there would be a genuine question about {\i eid echad ne\u8217'eman be\u8217'issurin} versus the force of an existing {\i chazaka}. The question of whether testimony is effective when the subject is {\i beyado} \u8212- within the person\u8217's own control or knowledge \u8212- versus when it is not, adds further complexity. Personal status raises these questions with even greater intensity. It is simply not self-evident that a woman\u8217's own testimony about herself is sufficient, for all purposes, to change her status vis-\u224 ?-vis the rest of the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof Threshold\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the deeper threshold question: what standard of proof is actually required to establish that a woman is forbidden to her husband, or to compel a husband to divorce his wife? If one were asked {\i ab initio}, the answer would not be \u8220"whatever she says is sufficient.\u8221" So when the {\i mishnah rishona} declares that she is believed and the {\i mishnah acharona} declares she is not, the shift may be less dramatic than it appears. The bigger structural problem, as noted previously, is that according to her own words she is claiming to be engaged in an {\i aveira} by continuing to live with her husband \u8212- which makes it difficult to understand how we can compel her to remain in the marriage regardless. That tension does not disappear simply because we formally decline to believe her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takana Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One way to understand the shift from the {\i mishnah rishona} to the {\i mishnah acharona} is as a {\i takana} \u8212- a rabbinic enactment that simply overrides the default rule and refuses to credit her testimony regardless of what the {\i halacha} would otherwise require. The alternative understanding is that once the possibility of ulterior motive has been raised \u8212- once the concern of {\i shema einah natna be\u8217'acher} has been introduced \u8212- this is enough to reassess her believability altogether, not as an override, but as a genuine recalculation that brings us back to the status quo of her being permitted to her husband. Under this reading, there is no {\i takana} at all; it is simply a recognition that once motive and veracity are called into question, she no longer meets the threshold of credibility needed to change her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both {\i Rav Sheshet} and {\i Rava}, as noted, suggest that whatever the mechanism, the doubts have not fully dissipated \u8212- and that lingering uncertainty is what animates their respective positions on {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ones & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Quiz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} reports that {\i Rava} posed a question to them as a kind of test: if the wife of a {\i kohen} was raped and is therefore forbidden to continue living with her {\i kohen} husband and must be divorced \u8212- does she collect her {\i ketubah} or not? The intuitive answer would seem to be yes, since it is not her fault. But the {\i hava amina} runs as follows: since {\i ones} \u8212- rape \u8212- in the case of the wife of a {\i kohen} is equivalent in its legal consequences to willful adultery in the case of the wife of a {\i Yisrael}, in the sense that both require a divorce, perhaps she should receive no {\i ketubah}, just as the willfully adulterous wife of a {\i Yisrael} receives none.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames the counter-argument in a striking way. Rather than resting simply on the absence of fault, it has the woman say, in effect: {\i ana hazina} \u8212- I am actually fit. My personal status has not been compromised. Even after being raped, I remain fully eligible to marry a {\i Yisrael}. {\i Degavra hu denistachfa sadeh} \u8212- it is you, the husband, whose field has been flooded. The disability that requires the divorce is a function of your status as a {\i kohen}, not of any fault of mine. The {\i gemara} is not suggesting the woman bears any moral blame \u8212- the {\i Torah} itself declares {\i velanaarah lo ta\u8217'aseh davar}. Rather, the question being probed is: when divorce is required and neither party is at fault, does the {\i ketubah} attach? And the answer the {\i gemara} works toward is that since the cause of the divorce is rooted in the husband\u8217's status as a {\i kohen}, it is, to some degree, attributable to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution comes from the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} discusses a woman who says {\i tme\u8217'ah ani lach} \u8212- \u8220"I am forbidden to you\u8221" \u8212- and rules that she has a {\i ketubah}. The {\i gemara} works through the possible cases: if she is the wife of a {\i Yisrael} claiming willful adultery, she would not receive a {\i ketubah}; if she is the wife of a {\i Yisrael} claiming rape, she is not forbidden to her husband at all, so there is no question. The only case that makes sense of the {\i mishnah} is the wife of a {\i kohen} claiming rape. And the {\i mishnah} rules she receives her {\i ketubah}. {\i Rav Pappa} and the other students informed {\i Rava} that they could prove the answer from the {\i mishnah} itself \u8212- and they passed the test.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerashtani Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new question: {\i amra leh lebaal gerashtani} \u8212- a woman says directly to her husband\u8217's face, \u8220"You divorced me,\u8221" without producing a {\i get}. She claims the {\i get} was lost, but insists the divorce occurred. What is her status?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hamnuna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Hamnuna} reasons from the {\i mishnah}. Even according to the {\i mishnah acharona}, which rules that a woman is not believed when she claims rape or adultery, the reason for the disbelief is that {\i hatam hu demeshakra} \u8212- there we suspect her of lying. What enables her to lie in those cases? {\i Deyad\u8217'a debaa\u8217'la lo yada bah} \u8212- she knows that her husband has no way of independently verifying her claim. She knows he cannot contradict her. This, combined with the possibility that she harbors ulterior motives \u8212- {\i shema einah natna be\u8217'acher} \u8212- is what makes her statement insufficiently credible. But in the case of {\i gerashtani}, even if she has motivations to be free of the marriage, {\i hayad\u8217'a bah} \u8212- the husband would know whether or not he gave her a {\i get}. A man does not forget whether he divorced his wife. And therefore, {\i chazaka ein isha me\u8217'iza paneha bifnei ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- there is a presumption that a woman will not brazenly lie to her husband\u8217's face about something he knows to be false. In such a case, {\i Rav Hamnuna} rules, she is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Knows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper implication of {\i Rav Hamnuna}\u8217's position is significant: the {\i mishnah acharona}\u8217's refusal to believe the woman is not an absolute, overriding {\i takana} that silences her in every conceivable circumstance. Rather, the {\i mishnah acharona} has raised doubts and questions about her credibility in specific types of cases. Those doubts can be overcome where there are stronger countervailing reasons to believe her. The {\i chazaka} of {\i ein isha me\u8217'iza paneha bifnei ba\u8217'alah} supplies such a reason.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shame Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} challenges this analysis. He argues that even the {\i mishnah rishona}\u8217's decision to believe the woman did not reflect a general disposition to credit whatever she says. Rather, it rested on specific circumstances that made her claim particularly credible \u8212- namely, that nobody would make a claim of rape or adultery if it were false, because even for the victim, even in a case where she bears no moral blame, such a disclosure carries an element of shame and vulnerability. The {\i mishnah rishona} believed her {\i davka} because of that logic \u8212- and even then, the {\i mishnah acharona} ultimately chose not to believe her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Motivation Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the case of {\i gerashtani}, {\i Rava} disagrees with {\i Rav Hamnuna}. He acknowledges the force of {\i chazaka ein isha me\u8217'iza paneha}, but argues that when a woman is sufficiently motivated to exit the marriage \u8212- {\i dezimnin detakif lah min gavra} \u8212- she can summon the resolve to confront her husband directly and lie to his face. The desire to leave the marriage, when powerful enough, overrides the {\i chazaka} that she would be too ashamed to lie in his presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Absolute Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the further question of whether {\i Rava}\u8217's position amounts to saying that the woman can never be believed once we have reached the {\i mishnah acharona}. It is possible that {\i Rava}\u8217's view has a categorical dimension: given that even in the {\i mishnah rishona}, where the case for believing her was at its strongest \u8212- where there was genuine {\i davka} reason to credit her \u8212- the {\i mishnah acharona} nevertheless refused to do so, {\i Rava} may be unwilling to permit any exceptions. Having established a regime of non-belief, he may hold that it admits no case-by-case reappraisal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Testing Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hashamayim Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Mesharshya} raises a challenge to {\i Rava} from the {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} includes the case of a woman who says {\i hashamayim beini u\u8217'veinecha} \u8212- \u8220"heaven is between you and me.\u8221" As the {\i Bavli} reads this phrase, it is a claim about the husband\u8217's inability to father children \u8212- specifically, a claim that he cannot ejaculate with sufficient force. The {\i mishnah rishona} believes her in this case. But if {\i Rava} is correct that the {\i mishnah rishona} only believed the woman in cases where her claim was intrinsically credible due to the shame involved in making it, how does the {\i hashamayim} case fit? Here she is saying something embarrassing about {\i him}, not about herself. There is no shame to her in making this claim, and yet the {\i mishnah rishona} credits her. This seems to undercut {\i Rava}\u8217's reading of the {\i mishnah rishona}\u8217's rationale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fertility Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} responds that the case is more specific than it appears. For the woman to invoke {\i hashamayim beini u\u8217'veinecha} in the relevant sense, she cannot simply speak in vague terms \u8212- she must articulate explicitly the nature of the deficiency, specifically that he {\i ein yorek ke-\u7717 ?etz}, that he cannot ejaculate with the force of an arrow. And {\i Rava} argues that this explicit formulation \u8212- describing intimate physiological detail about her husband in front of a {\i beit din} \u8212- is itself something that she would not say if it were not true. Even though the embarrassment attaches to him rather than to her, the sheer intimacy and specificity of the statement, made in a public judicial forum, is such that she would not articulate it unless it were accurate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Reasserts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus {\i Rava} reasserts his position: the {\i mishnah rishona} believed her across all its cases {\i davka} because each case presented a specific and identifiable reason to credit the claim. In no case did the {\i mishnah rishona} operate from a general presumption of female credibility. And in each case, notwithstanding those reasons, the {\i mishnah acharona} declined to believe her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acharona Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then runs the test in the other direction: if {\i Rav Hamnuna} is correct that the {\i mishnah acharona}\u8217's regime of disbelief admits exceptions when the husband would know the truth of the matter, should the {\i hashamayim} case also constitute an exception under the {\i mishnah acharona}? After all, the husband knows whether or not {\i ein yorek ke-\u7717 ?etz} applies to him \u8212- he is the one to whom it is happening. If she is willing to say that to his face, and he would know whether she was lying, should she not be believed even under the {\i mishnah acharona}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Conviction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Hamnuna}\u8217's position here is that she would not be believed even in this case under the {\i mishnah acharona}, because {\i michezi debe\u8217'iah yada} \u8212- while the husband may be aware of what occurs during the act of intercourse itself, {\i beyoreh ke-\u7717 ?etz lo yada} \u8212- he does not have independent clarity about the precise nature of how he ejaculates in the technical sense relevant to the claim. More to the point: it is not what the husband actually knows that determines whether the woman will lie, but what she can convince {\i herself} that he does not know. If she can persuade herself that he might have doubts, or that he won\u8217't be able to definitively refute her, that is enough for her to be willing to make the claim. {\i Umishum hachi meshakra} \u8212- and for that reason she will lie. The decisive variable is her own psychology of rationalization, not the objective state of the husband\u8217's knowledge.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakhic Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Follow Hamnuna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halakhic} ruling is that we follow {\i Rav Hamnuna} against {\i Rava}. The principle of {\i chazaka ein isha me\u8217'iza paneha bifnei ba\u8217'alah} is accepted broadly throughout the {\i gemara}, and the ruling here is in line with that general acceptance. The implication is significant: it is not only that we believe a woman when she says {\i gerashtani} \u8212- it is that the {\i mishnah acharona}\u8217's regime is not absolute, and there are circumstances in which a woman will be believed even within it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran on Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} draws out a further consequence. Once we accept {\i Rav Hamnuna}\u8217's principle \u8212- that where the husband would know the truth of the matter, she will be believed even under the {\i mishnah acharona} \u8212- it follows that a woman who says her husband refuses to have intimate relations with her altogether would also be believed. {\i She\u8217'omeret eino nizkak imi klal} \u8212- she claims he will not be with her at all. Since the husband knows whether this is true, and since she would not lie to his face about something he knows, she is believed \u8212- {\i keivan deba\u8217'ala yada bah afilu lemishna acharona meheimna} \u8212- and he is compelled to give her a {\i get} and pay her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} notes the broader {\i halakhic} implication: a man who refuses to have relations with his wife is failing to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of {\i onah}, and the woman\u8217's testimony about that refusal is sufficient to compel divorce and payment of {\i ketubah}. Similarly, the {\i Ran} cites the principle that if a man refuses to have relations unless they remain clothed \u8212- if he will not fulfill {\i onah} in the manner {\i halakhically} required \u8212- he must also {\i yotzi ve-yitein ketubah}, divorce her and pay her {\i ketubah}. In all these cases, the woman\u8217's claim is credited precisely because the husband would know its truth, and the {\i chazaka} of {\i ein isha me\u8217'iza paneha} therefore applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran\u8217's Caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} adds an important qualification, attributed to the {\i Ri}: the woman is believed and receives her {\i get} and {\i ketubah} only when her expressed demand is for the {\i get} itself \u8212- for the divorce. If she draws attention to the {\i ketubah}, if she emphasizes the financial dimension of her claim, this introduces a new motive and weakens her credibility. The focus on money raises fresh concerns of the same kind as {\i shema einah natna be\u8217'acher}. But if she says simply that she wants to be free of the marriage and demands a {\i get}, without foregrounding the monetary claim, she will receive both the {\i get} and the {\i ketubah}. In practice this points to the need for careful framing \u8212- which, as {\i Tosafot} noted somewhat wryly, is precisely why one needs competent legal counsel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Story One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daily Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a series of narrative cases that conclude the {\i perek}. The first involves a woman who, as a regular intimate gesture following relations with her husband, would wash his hands. The {\i Ran} suggests she may have done this for reasons connected to {\i tumah} \u8212- perhaps her husband was a {\i kohen} \u8212- though what precisely this would address is not entirely clear, since more significant {\i tumah} concerns require immersion in a {\i mikveh}. In any case, it functioned as a gesture of intimacy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Surprised\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One day, the husband noticed she had brought water to wash his hands, even though, as he told her, they had not had relations that day. Why was she performing this ritual?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Her response was striking: if it wasn\u8217't you, then it must have been one of those non-Jewish fragrance merchants \u8212- {\i ahaloyei}, aloe merchants \u8212- who had been present in the area. It must have been dark, she must have heard someone she took to be her husband, and she went to him. It was essentially the same logic as the classic case of mistaken identity in the dark, familiar from other contexts in the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i gemara} specifies that the other man was a non-Jew is relevant to the question of her status: for the wife of a {\i kohen}, even a mistaken or coerced act renders her forbidden, so the identification of the man as a non-Jewish stranger is the legally operative detail that would create her prohibition. For the wife of a {\i Yisrael}, even if her account were believed, she would not be forbidden to her husband since she acted under the belief that it was her husband \u8212- a case of {\i shogeg} that would not render her forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} ruled: {\i ein anu mashgichin bah} \u8212- pay no attention to her. {\i V\u8217'leit bei mashasha b\u8217'mila} \u8212- there is no substance to her words. She is seeking a divorce and has constructed an ingenious account \u8212- one designed to appear as if the disclosure was involuntary, drawn out by the husband\u8217's question rather than volunteered \u8212- precisely in order to make it look credible. Despite how convincing the circumstantial presentation might seem, {\i Rav Nachman} rules categorically that we do not believe her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is noteworthy: the ruling is categorical, not case-by-case. {\i Rav Nachman} does not undertake a granular assessment of the plausibility of her story. This connects to the earlier discussion about whether the {\i mishnah acharona}\u8217's disbelief is absolute or contextual. Here, the ruling appears to favor the categorical approach \u8212- and, as we noted, this actually serves the interests of a woman who does not want to be divorced, since by not believing her we allow the marriage to continue.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Story Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Painful Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case involves a woman who was visibly not in a good mood with her husband. He asked her why. She replied that she had never experienced such pain during relations as she had that day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Her husband told her: we did not have relations today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Merchants Again\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She responded: if that is so, then it must have been those non-Jewish fragrance merchants who had been present in the house that day \u8212- {\i dilma chad minhon}, perhaps one of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Another Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} again ruled: do not pay attention to her \u8212- {\i natna einayha be\u8217'acher hi hi}, she has set her eyes on another man. Once more, despite the apparent circumstantial weight of the evidence, the ruling is categorical. The couple\u8217's ability to remain married is preserved precisely through that categorical disbelief.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Story Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caught & Run\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is different in kind. A man was hiding behind closed doors with a married woman \u8212- not his wife \u8212- when the husband came home. The man broke through the wicker gate and fled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hiding Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} ruled: {\i itteta sharya} \u8212- the woman is permitted. His reasoning: {\i im itta de-avar issura}, had he actually committed adultery with her, {\i mikhse hava mirchas} \u8212- he would have hidden more carefully. He would have taken greater care to ensure that he was not seen fleeing. The very brazenness of his visible escape suggests that there was nothing he needed to conceal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ran Clarifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} addresses a question that lurks beneath the surface of this ruling: does this mean that without {\i Rava}\u8217's reasoning, the circumstantial evidence of a man found hiding with a married woman would be sufficient to render her forbidden to her husband? The {\i Ran} is explicit that it does not. {\i She-ein itteta niaseret al ba\u8217'alah} except through {\i kinnuy ve-stira} \u8212- the formal {\i sotah} procedure involving a witnessed warning followed by witnessed seclusion \u8212- {\i o be-eidei davar barur}, or through witnesses who observe the act itself with substantial clarity. Circumstantial evidence alone, however suggestive, is insufficient to render a woman forbidden to her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reassurance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so, what is {\i Rava} accomplishing with his reasoning? The {\i Ran} explains: {\i hayu romi b\u8217'chei hai gavna v\u8217'chol bar nafesh d\u8217'chush al atzmo k\u8217'dei la\u8217'asot yedei shamayim} \u8212- the husband, even without a {\i halachic} obligation to divorce his wife, might nevertheless feel that morally or personally he cannot remain in the marriage given what he witnessed. He suspects that something occurred even if he cannot prove it. {\i Rava}\u8217's reasoning is directed at {\i him} \u8212- at calming his fears and providing him with a rational basis for concluding that his suspicions are unfounded. The rabbinic authority here operates not only on the level of formal {\i halacha} but as a form of pastoral reassurance, requiring considerable confidence and leadership on the rabbi\u8217's part.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Story Four\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cress & Snake\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final case is the most vivid. An adulterer \u8212- described as such, though the irony of that label will become apparent \u8212- had entered a woman\u8217's home and was hiding behind the door or in the closet when the husband arrived. On the table was cress that a snake had bitten from. The concern about uncovered food bitten by a snake \u8212- {\i gilu\u8217'i}, the category of leaving food exposed \u8212- is that snake venom may be left behind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Life-Saving Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The husband, unaware of the danger, was about to eat from the poisoned cress. {\i B\u8217'lo da\u8217'ata de-itteta} \u8212- and the wife, who may have known about the snake but had not noticed her husband was about to eat, was also unaware of what was happening in that moment. The man hiding in the closet, seeing the husband about to eat the poisoned food, called out: {\i lo teichul mihanech d\u8217'tam\u8217'inhu chivya} \u8212- do not eat from that; the snake has bitten from it. He exposed himself in order to save the husband\u8217's life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} ruled: {\i itteta sharya} \u8212- the woman is permitted. {\i Ei ita de-avad issura} \u8212- had he actually committed adultery with her, {\i nicha lei de-leichul ve-leimut} \u8212- he would have wanted the husband to eat the poisoned cress and die. The {\i gemara} supports this from a verse: {\i ki nafu ve-dam biyadeihem} \u8212- they committed adultery and blood was on their hands. An adulterer, the logic runs, would not recoil from the husband\u8217's death; indeed he would welcome it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secret Affair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} anticipates the counter-argument: perhaps he {\i did} commit adultery, and yet {\i davka} wanted the husband to live \u8212- {\i ke-dei de-tehavi itteta leh}, so that the woman would remain available to him as a secret lover. For as the verse says, {\i mayim genuvim yimtaku ve-lechem setarim yinam} \u8212- stolen waters are sweet and hidden bread is tasty. He prefers the clandestine intrigue of adultery over marriage; he has no interest in the husband\u8217's death and every interest in his continued existence. The {\i gemara} rejects this counter-argument and maintains {\i Rava}\u8217's ruling: the presumption is that an adulterer would want the husband out of the way, and the man\u8217's life-saving warning is therefore proof that he did not commit adultery. The woman is permitted to her husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shalom Bayis Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to observe where these stories appear. They close an entire {\i perek} whose central subject is a husband\u8217's power to annul his wife\u8217's vows. Whatever dimension of power that dynamic reflects, it also represents a {\i halakhic} mechanism aimed at reducing sources of marital tension \u8212- an instrument for preventing or dissolving vows that generate discord between husband and wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concluding stories operate with a dual method. On the formal {\i halakhic} level, the {\i gemara} consistently refuses to believe the woman\u8217's claim that she committed adultery or was violated \u8212- a categorical disbelief that, paradoxically, protects the integrity of marriages in which neither party actually wants a divorce. On the informal level, the {\i gedolei Yisrael} appearing in these stories \u8212- {\i Rav Nachman}, {\i Rava} \u8212- employ circumstantial reasoning and pastoral reassurance to address the husband\u8217's fears and suspicions, providing him with a rational and emotional basis for remaining in the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dual method \u8212- formal disbelief combined with circumstantial reassurance \u8212- achieves the goal of reducing marital discord while maintaining {\i halakhic} integrity. The {\i chazal} are not simply dismissing the woman\u8217's words; they are navigating an intricate balance between the demands of {\i din}, the welfare of the marriage, and the dignity of both parties. The final image of the {\i gemara} \u8212- the man hiding in the closet calling out to warn the husband not to eat the poisoned food \u8212- is almost cinematically resonant: the would-be adulterer becomes, in the end, the husband\u8217's unlikely rescuer, and {\i itteta sharya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro to Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Dedication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin today {\i Maseches Nazir}. Today\u8217's daf is Nazir 2a. Today is also the {\i yahrtzeit} of my mother, {\i Chana bas Mordechai Eliezer}, Anat Linzer, and this learning is dedicated to her memory. A few brief words in her honor appear at the end of this shiur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Maseches Nazir} logically continues {\i Maseches Nedarim}. The first word to say before jumping in is that {\i nazir} is a type of {\i neder} \u8212- a status created through an act of speech, just as we learned regarding {\i neder} in general. A {\i neder}, as distinct from a {\i shavuah}, is not an oath to do or refrain from doing something; it is a speech act that effects the status of an object, renders an object or a person forbidden to another. A {\i nazir}, similarly, is constituted through a speech act that does not focus on what is being prohibited, but rather creates a status and defines the person as a {\i nazir}. As the Torah states, {\i ish o ishah ki yafli lindor neder nazir} \u8212- a man or a woman can undertake {\i nezirut}. We encountered several cases of a woman taking a vow of {\i nezirut} and her husband annulling it in the preceding {\i masechet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequences of {\i nazir} status are threefold: the person may not drink wine or consume any grape products, may not become {\i tamei} to the dead, and must allow his or her hair to grow wild. This is not an oath; it is not a focus on forbidding a particular act. It is a status \u8212- analogous to designating an animal as a {\i korban}, which generates a host of {\i halachos} governing what may or may not be done with that animal. So too here, the speech act instills a status, and from that status flow the attendant obligations and prohibitions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir vs.\u160 ?Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i mishnah} of {\i Nazir} very closely parallels the first {\i mishnah} of {\i Nedarim}, in that both address the types of speech acts that are legally sufficient to effect the relevant status. The question in both cases is: what precisely must one say, and how formal or precise does that language need to be?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the text, a word about the structure of the page and the available commentaries. Unlike {\i Nedarim}, where the {\i Tosfos} running along the side was not the standard genre \u8212- more a running commentary than the dialectical, critical engagement we associate with {\i Tosfos} proper \u8212- here in {\i Nazir} the {\i Tosfos} is a fully standard {\i Tosfos}, asking pointed questions from other sugyos and working toward resolutions. There is no {\i Ran}, as the need for that kind of running commentary is less acute here. However, the pseudo-{\i Rashi} \u8212- which is not the authentic {\i Rashi}, though the identity of its true author remains a matter of scholarly discussion \u8212- will likely be consulted more frequently precisely because {\i Tosfos} is now functioning in its standard role rather than as a running commentary. The {\i Rosh} also provides something of a running commentary here. We will refer to all of these as occasion demands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One should also note that while the page looks more standard than {\i Nedarim}, the external {\i rishonim} on {\i Nazir} are sparser. On {\i Nedarim} one finds the {\i Ramban}\u8217's {\i Hilchos Nedarim} paralleling the {\i Rif}, as well as a {\i Ritva}, {\i Nemukei Yosef}, and {\i Rashba}. For {\i Nazir}, the broader corpus of {\i rishonim} simply does not exist to the same degree \u8212- a curious fact, perhaps partly explained by the limited practical relevance of the laws of {\i nezirut} in the absence of the {\i Beis Hamikdash} (since {\i nezirut} cannot be concluded without the requisite {\i korbanot}), though since when has practical relevance determined whether {\i rishonim} write on a {\i masechet}? It is also worth noting that both {\i Nedarim} and {\i Nazir} appear to reflect a somewhat different editorial tradition than most of the Bavli \u8212- current scholarly consensus suggests a different {\i yeshiva} center in {\i Bavel} rather than the main academies, which accounts for certain differences in terminology and idiom, though nothing radical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seder Nashim Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} will address momentarily why {\i Nazir} belongs in {\i Seder Nashim}. The simplest answer \u8212- discussed by {\i Tosfos} and the {\i Rosh} \u8212- is that it follows {\i Nedarim}, and {\i Nedarim} belongs in {\i Seder Nashim} because the primary locus of {\i nedarim} in the Torah concerns the husband\u8217's and father\u8217's right to annul the vows of a wife or daughter. That familial, marital context is what anchors {\i Nedarim} in {\i Nashim}, and {\i Nazir} follows naturally in its wake. The {\i gemara}, however, will offer a more substantive reason, to which we now turn.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinuyim & Yadot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinuyim Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i kol kinuyei nezirut ke-nezirut} \u8212- all substitute expressions for {\i nezirut} carry the same legal force as {\i nezirut} itself. Even non-standard terminology, if it functions as a stand-in for the formal term, suffices to make one a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elliptical Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then states: {\i ha-omer ahei, harei zeh nazir} \u8212- one who says \u8220"I will be,\u8221" without completing the sentence, is a {\i nazir}. This is striking: the sentence is entirely open-ended. One could fill in that blank with any number of things. The {\i gemara} will explain that this case applies specifically where a {\i nazir} is passing in front of the speaker, such that the context fills in the ellipsis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i o ahei na\u8217'eh} \u8212- \u8220"or I will be beautiful.\u8221" Here too, the meaning is not self-evident. The word {\i na\u8217'eh} means beautiful, not {\i nazir}. Yet either because a {\i nazir} was passing before the speaker and the phrase means \u8220"I will be beautiful like that {\i nazir},\u8221" or because the speaker is understood to be gesturing toward his own hair, the phrase carries legal force. {\i Tosfos} and the {\i Rosh} place {\i ahei na\u8217'eh} in the category of {\i yad} \u8212- an elliptical or incomplete expression \u8212- though it occupies an interesting intermediate space between a pure {\i yad} (an unfinished sentence) and a {\i kinuy} (a substitute term that carries meaning through convention). A pure {\i kinuy} involves a substitute word standing in for the term {\i nazir} itself. {\i Ahei na\u8217'eh} is a complete sentence, but its meaning is carried by context rather than by the word\u8217's own semantic content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Substitute Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses: {\i nazik, naziach, paziach} \u8212- words that are clearly variations of the word {\i nazir}, with slight phonetic distortions. {\i Harei zeh nazir} \u8212- such a person is a {\i nazir}. These are the classic {\i kinuyim}: substitute terms that stand in for the formal word. One can see how {\i nazik} and {\i paziach} are distortions of {\i nazir} \u8212- take the {\i nun} and deform it slightly into a {\i pei}, draw a line through the {\i reish} to arrive at {\i paziach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a debate in the {\i gemara} in {\i Nedarim} as to whether these words constitute {\i lashon shebadu chachamim} \u8212- a language invented by the rabbis \u8212- or {\i lashon umos}, the vernacular of other nations. The latter option seems difficult to sustain: it would be rather remarkable if foreign languages had their own terminology for {\i nezirut} that happened to closely mirror the Hebrew, and these words sound more like inventions than genuine foreign vocabulary. Yet the question has real {\i halachic} bite: if the rabbis simply invented these terms, does that make them a less-than-formal language? Does using them create a full-fledged {\i nezirut d\u8217'Oraita}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} in {\i Nedarim} argued that the entire concept of language is conventional \u8212- there is no objective standard for what constitutes a \u8220"real\u8221" language. Once a convention is established and people understand what the terms mean, it becomes a valid language for the purposes of {\i nezirut}, regardless of whether the rabbis originated it. The analogous case in {\i Nedarim} concerns {\i konam} and related terms. The {\i gemara} there explains that the substitute terminology was instituted so that a person would not begin by saying {\i korban} and complete the utterance with {\i l\u8217'Hashem}, thereby invoking God\u8217's name in a potentially frivolous context. That logic applies more naturally to {\i nedarim} involving the word {\i korban} than to {\i nezirut}, where the concern is less obvious \u8212- though the general desire to signal the weightiness of the act through substitute language may still apply.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Allusions & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Allusive Phrases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with further examples: {\i harei ka-ze} \u8212- \u8220"I am like this one,\u8221" presumably said while looking at a {\i nazir}; {\i harei alai mesalsel} \u8212- \u8220"I will twirl my hair\u8221"; {\i harei alai mechalkel} \u8212- similarly referring to attending to one\u8217's hair; and {\i harei alai l\u8217'shaleach pera} \u8212- \u8220"I will let my hair grow wild.\u8221" In all these cases, {\i harei zeh nazir} \u8212- the person is a {\i nazir}. These are what one might call allusions with legal force: the words do not literally mean {\i nazir}, but the phrase, taken in context, communicates an unambiguous intent to accept {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bird Offerings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A more ambiguous case follows: {\i harei alai tziporin} \u8212- \u8220"I hereby undertake [an obligation of] birds.\u8221" Bird offerings are indeed part of the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korbanot} at the conclusion of {\i nezirut}, but they are also the offering of a {\i metzora}. The statement thus does not unambiguously point to {\i nezirut} specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir omer Nazir} \u8212- Rabbi Meir holds that such a person is a {\i nazir}. {\i Chachamim omrim eino Nazir} \u8212- the Sages disagree. The {\i gemara} will elaborate on this dispute, but the underlying question is whether the allusion to bird offerings alone is sufficient to constitute an act of accepting {\i nezirut}, or whether the ambiguity of the reference defeats the speech act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinuyim & Yadot (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosfos Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} raises a pointed question about the {\i chiddush} of {\i kinuyim}. If these are simply words in another language \u8212- or even in a made-up language that has become conventionally understood \u8212- what is the novelty? You can obviously become a {\i nazir} by saying so in any language. So the {\i mishnah}\u8217's listing of {\i nazik, naziach, paziach} would be trivially obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} answers: {\i ein hachi nami d\u8217'gam b\u8217'sha\u8217'ar lishonot im hikkir v\u8217'hevin v\u8217'nitkaven lekabel alav nezirut havei nazir} \u8212- it is certainly true that in any other language, if one understood and intended to accept {\i nezirut}, one becomes a {\i nazir}. The {\i chiddush} of these particular terms, says {\i Tosfos}, is that {\i mishum d\u8217'dami tefai v\u8217'lashon haTorah} \u8212- because they closely resemble the formal Torah language of the word {\i nazir}, they bind a person even without specific intent, in a way that ordinary foreign-language equivalents do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent & Convention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking position, because the entire foundation of {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut} is {\i piv v\u8217'libo shavin} \u8212- one\u8217's words and one\u8217's heart must be aligned. One might object: if you say {\i harei ani nazir} without intending it, the words alone do not make you a {\i nazir}. So how can {\i Tosfos} say that saying {\i nazik} or {\i paziach} binds you even without intent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most reasonable reading is that {\i Tosfos} does not mean \u8220"zero intent.\u8221" Rather, there must be some general intent to undertake something significant \u8212- but the speaker may not have focused specifically on {\i nezirut} as opposed to some other form of {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, some other verbal act of self-imposition. The argument would then be: when the words used are so close to the formal Torah language of {\i nazir}, that proximity carries its own interpretive weight and locks in the meaning of {\i nezirut} even if the speaker had not consciously specified it. Whether spoken in French or English or any other language, only precise, specific intent would suffice. But when the words are near-formal Torah language, that specificity is supplied by the formal register of the words themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} further elaborates on the position of {\i Reish Lakish}, who holds that these are {\i lashon shebadu chachamim} \u8212- invented words. {\i Tosfos} asks: according to {\i Reish Lakish}, how can one bring a {\i korban} on the basis of invented language? Would this not be {\i chullin ba-azarah} \u8212- bringing non-consecrated animals into the Temple precincts? {\i Tosfos} answers: {\i keivan de-mitkavein lindor be-nazir ve-yodeia she-lashon zeh badu chachamim lindor bahem be-nazir, kabbalah gemurah hu ke\u8217'ilu amar bi-lashon ha-katuv ba-Torah} \u8212- since the person intends to accept {\i nezirut} and knows that this is the language the rabbis instituted for that purpose, it constitutes a complete acceptance, as though one had spoken in the formal language of the Torah itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is interesting here is the phrase {\i ke\u8217'ilu amar bi-lashon ha-katuv ba-Torah}. There is, strictly speaking, no single prescribed verbal formula in the Torah for accepting {\i nezirut}. Yet {\i Tosfos} writes as though such a formal text exists. Both parts of the {\i Tosfos} \u8212- the first part, which suggests near-formal language binds even without full intent, and this second part, which describes the invented language as equivalent to the Torah\u8217's formal language \u8212- seem to presuppose a kind of formal register of sacred speech that occupies a different normative space than ordinary language, even intentional ordinary language. One might not resolve this tension entirely, but it reflects a coherent underlying intuition: the closer one\u8217's words are to the formal idiom of Torah obligation, the more fully they function as legal speech acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes vs.\u160 ?Korbanot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} cites one final possibility, attributed to {\i Ravya bar Chiya}, who explains {\i Reish Lakish}\u8217's position as applying specifically to {\i malkut} \u8212- lashes \u8212- rather than to {\i korbanot}. That is: one who violates {\i nezirut} accepted through these invented terms receives lashes, but does not bring a {\i korban}. This is a curious intermediate category, positing a {\i nezirut} that is weighty enough to generate corporal liability upon transgression, yet not substantial enough to obligate the full complement of Temple offerings. The question of how such a middle tier is conceptually coherent is left open, but the possibility itself reflects the deeper uncertainty about whether invented convention, even when widely accepted and intentionally employed, creates a fully {\i d\u8217'Oraita} legal status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Seder Nashim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens by asking: {\i tana be-seder nashim ka-ei} \u8212- we are in {\i Seder Nashim}. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama tani Nazir?} \u8212- what is the reason {\i Nazir} is taught here? As noted above, the simplest answer is that it follows {\i Nedarim}, and {\i Nedarim} belongs in {\i Nashim} because the Torah\u8217's primary treatment of {\i nedarim} concerns the husband\u8217's and father\u8217's power to annul. {\i Nazir} follows naturally in that wake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah & Wine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, offers a substantive reason of its own: {\i tana kra ka-ei} \u8212- the {\i tanna} is anchored in a biblical context. The verse discusses a husband divorcing his wife because of {\i ervat davar} \u8212- presumed adultery. {\i Mi garam lah la-aveirah?} \u8212- what caused her to sin? {\i Yayin} \u8212- wine. And therefore: {\i kol ha-ro\u8217'eh sotah be-kilkulah} \u8212- anyone who sees a {\i sotah} in her disgrace, as her hair is disheveled and she undergoes the ordeal, should recognize that her downfall was caused by wine and the pursuit of sensual pleasure. The appropriate response is {\i yazir atzmo min ha-yayin} \u8212- to disavow wine, that is, to become a {\i nazir}. It is worth noting the irony that this reasoning is addressed entirely to a male audience \u8212- it is the man who sees the {\i sotah} and resolves to become a {\i nazir}. One might have expected the reasoning to apply symmetrically, but the {\i gemara}\u8217's framing reflects how completely the lens here is male.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir-Sotah Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because {\i nazir} is thus connected to {\i sotah}, and {\i sotah} is placed in {\i Seder Nashim}, {\i nazir} belongs there as well \u8212- and indeed, {\i Maseches Sotah} follows {\i Maseches Nazir} directly in the ordering of the {\i seder}. Moreover, in the Torah itself, {\i nazir} and {\i sotah} are juxtaposed in the very same section of {\i Bamidbar}. The placement in {\i Seder Nashim} is therefore not merely a structural afterthought; it reflects a deliberate thematic and scriptural linkage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 A-B-B-A Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Yadot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established why {\i Nazir} belongs in {\i Seder Nashim}, the {\i gemara} turns to an internal structural question about the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} opened by stating {\i kol kinuyei nezirut ke-nezirut} \u8212- the principle of {\i kinuyim} \u8212- but its first concrete example was {\i ahei}, which is not a {\i kinuy} at all, but a {\i yad}, an elliptical sentence. So the {\i mishnah} seems to announce one principle and immediately illustrate a different one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} (and some say it was stated anonymously) resolves this by the principle of {\i hisurei mihasra ve-hachi katani} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} is missing text and must be read as follows: {\i kol kinuyei nezirut ke-nezirut, ve-yadot nezirut ke-nezirut} \u8212- both {\i kinuyim} and {\i yadot} are effective. The {\i mishnah} implicitly includes {\i yadot} alongside {\i kinuyim}. The examples of {\i ahei} and {\i ahei na\u8217'eh} are then the illustrations of {\i yadot}, while {\i nazik, naziach, paziach} are the illustrations of {\i kinuyim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises a further question: if we are inserting both categories, and the {\i mishnah} announced {\i kinuyim} first, why does it illustrate {\i yadot} first? The {\i gemara} notes that the {\i mishnah} appears to follow an A-B-B-A structure \u8212- announcing two categories and then discussing them in reverse order. Why would it do so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precedent Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adduces parallel examples from other {\i mishnayot}. {\i Bameh madlikin u-vameh ein madlikin} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} in {\i Shabbos} discusses what one may and may not use as a wick, and it discusses what one may {\i not} use first. {\i Bameh tomnin u-vameh ein tomnin} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} about insulating food discusses the prohibited cases first. {\i Bameh ishah yotza u-vameh eino yotza} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} about what a woman may carry on {\i Shabbos} similarly starts with the prohibited. In all these cases, the {\i mishnah} names both options (A and B) and then discusses B before A \u8212- a classic A-B-B-A structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} notes that this is not a universal rule. {\i Bameh behemah yotza u-vameh eino yotza} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} about what an animal may wear on {\i Shabbos} starts with the permitted cases, not the prohibited. And {\i yesh nochalin u-manhilin} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} about inheritance begins with the primary case (those who both inherit and bequeath), not the last category mentioned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So neither A-B-B-A nor A-B-A-B is a fixed rule. The {\i gemara} therefore articulates the underlying logic case by case. When the subject concerns personal prohibitions \u8212- what {\i you} may or may not do \u8212- the {\i mishnah} leads with the prohibited, because that is what demands attention and care. When the subject concerns the animal rather than the person directly ({\i behemah}), there is less urgency to foreground the prohibition, and the {\i mishnah} begins with the permitted. When the topic is inheritance ({\i nochalin}), the {\i mishnah} starts with the primary, normative case before moving to exceptions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yadot Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then is the logic here in {\i Nazir}? Why illustrate {\i yadot} before {\i kinuyim}? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i yadot hava de-atu leh midrashah chavivi leh} \u8212- the concept of {\i yad} is derived from a {\i pasuk} through {\i derasha}. Things derived through rabbinic {\i derasha} from scriptural texts occupy a beloved place; the rabbis cherish what they have derived from close reading of {\i pesukim} and wish to discuss it first as the concrete illustrative case. Hence, even though {\i kinuyim} was announced first, the {\i yadot} are illustrated first because they carry the prestige of scriptural derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet if {\i yadot} are so beloved, one might ask: why not open the {\i mishnah} itself with {\i yadot} rather than {\i kinuyim}? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ki matchil, matchil be-ikar korban} \u8212- when beginning, one begins with the more primary act. The use of substitute terms ({\i kinuyim}), even though these may in some cases be conventional or invented language, is a more basic, self-evident concept: you use a different word that means the same thing, and of course it counts. The concept of {\i yad} \u8212- that an {\i incomplete} sentence, never even articulating what you are accepting, can nonetheless constitute a binding speech act \u8212- is a far greater {\i chiddush}. Indeed, the very fact that a {\i derasha} was necessary to establish {\i yadot} proves that {\i kinuyim} is the more fundamental concept; {\i kinuyim} needs no scriptural derivation because it is simply the obvious principle that language works by meaning, not by arbitrary form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the structure is: the {\i mishnah} opens by announcing {\i kinuyim} as primary, then (via the implicit insertion of {\i yadot}) introduces the concept of {\i yadot} as well. When it comes to concrete illustration, it illustrates {\i yadot} first \u8212- because they are derived from {\i derasha} and cherished \u8212- and then returns to illustrate {\i kinuyim}. This produces the A-B-B-A pattern: announce {\i kinuyim}, introduce {\i yadot}, illustrate {\i yadot}, illustrate {\i kinuyim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ahei Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now homes in on the specific case of {\i ahei} \u8212- \u8220"I will be.\u8221" The difficulty is obvious: how does an incomplete sentence become legally binding? The person has not said what he will be. {\i Dilma ahei be-ta\u8217'anis kamar} \u8212- perhaps he means \u8220"I will undertake a fast,\u8221" or any of a thousand other possibilities. Shmuel\u8217's answer is: {\i kegon she-hayah Nazir over lefanav} \u8212- the case is where a {\i nazir} was walking in front of him. That context fills the ellipsis and makes the meaning clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yad Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the {\i gemara} to examine Shmuel\u8217's position through a three-tier conceptual framework. There is a {\i yad mokhiach} \u8212- a contextually obvious ellipsis. There is a {\i yad she-eino mokhiach} \u8212- an ellipsis that probably points to a certain meaning but is not entirely unambiguous. And there is something that is not a {\i yad} at all \u8212- so open-ended that it cannot even be treated as a truncated speech act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The clearest example of a {\i yad mokhiach} comes from a parallel discussion in {\i Kiddushin}: a man standing under the {\i chuppah} says {\i harei at mekudeshes} without adding {\i li} \u8212- \u8220"to me.\u8221" Strictly speaking, he might be acting as an agent for someone else. But given that he is present at his own wedding ceremony, surrounded by family, the omission of {\i li} is transparently a {\i yad mokhiach} \u8212- so obvious from context that there is no real ambiguity. He did not complete the sentence, but no reasonable person would doubt what he meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Before Him\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since Shmuel specifies that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case requires a {\i nazir} to be passing before the speaker, the {\i gemara} infers that Shmuel may hold that only a {\i yad mokhiach} suffices \u8212- a fully transparent contextual ellipsis. A {\i yad she-eino mokhiach} would then not be adequate. The {\i gemara} raises this as a question about Shmuel\u8217's position, noting that the same issue arises in the {\i Kiddushin} context where Shmuel also opines.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then qualifies: even with a {\i nazir} walking past, the case is not so simple. When there is a {\i nazir over lefanav}, {\i leika le-safukei be-milsa achrina} \u8212- there is no room to suspect that the speaker meant anything other than accepting {\i nezirut} upon himself. Without a {\i nazir} present, however, {\i amrina dilma ahei be-ta\u8217'anis kamar} \u8212- we would simply say he might have meant a fast, and the statement would not be a {\i yad} at all. The point is that bare {\i ahei} with no context is not even on the spectrum of {\i yad} \u8212- it is too indeterminate. Shmuel\u8217's example of {\i nazir over lefanav} establishes the minimum context required, but does not resolve what Shmuel holds regarding intermediate cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further complication: even with a {\i nazir} walking in front, the {\i gemara} raises the possibility that {\i ahei} does not mean \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir}\u8221" at all, but rather \u8220"let it be upon me\u8221" \u8212- meaning the speaker intends to assume responsibility for that {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korbanot} at the conclusion of his {\i nezirut}. Strange as it sounds, one person can indeed undertake to finance another\u8217's {\i korbanot}, relieving the {\i nazir} of that obligation. So even the most natural contextual reading \u8212- a {\i nazir} before you, you say \u8220"I will be\u8221" \u8212- admits of an alternative interpretation: not {\i nezirut} itself, but commitment to the other\u8217's offerings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clarification Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by noting that the case is one where the person subsequently clarified his intention \u8212- {\i de-kamar be-libo}, he said (or it became clear) that he meant {\i nezirut}. The {\i gemara} then asks: if he clarified it afterward, is that not obvious? Why does the {\i mishnah} need to tell us anything? The answer is: {\i mahu de-teima be-inan piv u-libo shavin kamashma lan} \u8212- one might have thought that we require a complete alignment between words and intent at the moment of the speech act itself, such that the words must be fully self-explanatory. The {\i mishnah} teaches that this is not required: subsequent clarification of intent can work together with the original words to constitute a valid acceptance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yad Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the discussion to its conclusion. What has been illustrated here is precisely a {\i yad she-eino mokhiach}. The words {\i ahei}, even with a {\i nazir} passing by, are not entirely transparent \u8212- there is the alternative reading of assuming the {\i korban} obligation. The words in context \u8220"probably\u8221" point to {\i nezirut}, but not with full certainty. The {\i chiddush} is that this is sufficient: a {\i yad she-eino mokhiach}, joined with subsequent clarification of intent, constitutes a valid speech act. Since the person intended {\i nezirut} and subsequently clarified that intention, we can say that that is indeed what the words expressed. The principle of {\i piv u-libo shavin} does not require that the words be maximally explicit at the time of utterance; it requires that the words and the intent be aligned \u8212- and that alignment can be established in conjunction with subsequent clarification.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Ethics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Sinner?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tomorrow\u8217's discussion \u8212- which we had hoped to reach today \u8212- concerns the question of whether the {\i nazir} is a {\i chotei}, a sinner. The Talmud\u8217's treatment of this question is nuanced and important, and we will address it in full then.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By way of preview, one might note that the standard understanding in the {\i gemara} of why the {\i nazir} might be considered a {\i chotei} is that he deprives himself of the pleasures of this world. But this opens onto a deeper question about what constitutes the primary form of religious engagement. One model of {\i kedushah} holds that a person reaches spiritual heights through separation \u8212- growing one\u8217's hair wild as a mark of social estrangement, avoiding contact with the dead, refraining from any engagement with the physical in order to transcend it. That is one vision of holiness. But there is a very different conception of {\i kedushah}, expressed in {\i Rabbi Yisrael Salanter}\u8217's famous principle: {\i yenems gashmiyus iz dayn ruchnius} \u8212- the other person\u8217's physical needs are your spiritual obligation. On this understanding, genuine holiness is not found in withdrawal from the world but in full engagement with it, in understanding and attending to the real human needs of those around us.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother\u8217's Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This daf is dedicated to the memory of my mother, {\i Chana bas Mordechai Eliezer}, {\i aleha ha-shalom}. If I were to identify the image that most captures who she was, it would be this: answering calls in the middle of the night \u8212- midnight, later \u8212- to arrange a {\i taharah}, and going immediately to perform it. The {\i nazir} achieves holiness by distancing himself from contact with the dead. My mother achieved {\i kedushah} by running toward it, by being {\i metamei le-meitim} in the deepest and most selfless sense, extending herself at the moments when no one else was there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also the other dimension \u8212- what people shared at the {\i shiva}: that when families moved into the community, she was the one who welcomed them, who made them feel invited and at home, who remembered, years later, what a friend\u8217's favorite dish was and made sure it appeared on the {\i Shabbos} table when that friend returned after a long absence. What it does to a person \u8212- to know that they are remembered, that they matter, that someone has held them in mind across years \u8212- is not a small thing. That kind of {\i kedushah}, the {\i kedushah} of attentiveness to other human beings and their real needs, is the alternative model to the {\i nazir}\u8217's separation. It is the model my mother lived. May her memory be a blessing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 I will be beautiful\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ehei Na\u8217'eh classification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with the phrase {\i ehei na\u8217'eh nazir} \u8212- \u8220"I will be beautiful, [I will be] a {\i nazir}.\u8221" If someone declares \u8220"I will be beautiful,\u8221" that person is considered to have accepted {\i nezirut}. The question is how to classify this statement. A {\i yad} is typically a truncated, elliptical sentence \u8212- \u8220"I will be\u8221" \u8212- where one must fill in the blank. Here, by contrast, a complete sentence has been uttered: \u8220"I will be beautiful.\u8221" The issue is not incompleteness but rather what the word \u8220"beautiful\u8221" signifies in this context. One could therefore suggest that this functions more as a {\i kinuy} than as a {\i yad}, though it does not fit the standard definition of {\i kinuy} either. A {\i kinuy} normally involves a technical substitute word \u8212- {\i nazik}, {\i paziyach}, {\i naziyach} \u8212- standing in for the word {\i nazir}. The word {\i na\u8217'eh} does not mean {\i nazir}; it means beautiful. It is only by contextual interpretation that it is understood to refer to {\i nezirut}. The categorization therefore remains somewhat ambiguous, somewhere between {\i yad} and {\i kinuy}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately presses: how do we know that {\i na\u8217'eh} refers to being a {\i nazir}? Perhaps {\i ehei na\u8217'eh lefanav bamitzvot} \u8212- \u8220"I will make myself beautiful before God in {\i mitzvot}\u8221" \u8212- as the {\i Braisa} in the {\i Mekhilta} expounds the verse {\i zeh Eli ve\u8217'anveihu}: \u8220"This is my God and I will glorify Him.\u8221" The {\i Braisa} explains {\i anveihu} as {\i ehei na\u8217'eh lefanav bamitzvot} \u8212- I will glorify myself before God through the performance of {\i mitzvot} \u8212- and gives examples: {\i sukkah na\u8217'eh}, {\i lulav na\u8217'eh}, {\i tzitzis na\u8217'eh}, {\i sefer Torah na\u8217'eh}. This is the concept of {\i noi mitzvah}, beautifying the objects of the commandment. Even within a religious register, then, the phrase {\i ehei na\u8217'eh} need not refer to {\i nezirut}. The challenge stands: even setting aside entirely secular interpretations, the phrase could carry a meaningful religious sense without triggering any vow of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samuel: hair context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel resolves the difficulty by appeal to context: {\i she-tefus be-sa\u8217'aro ve\u8217'amar ena\u8217'eh} \u8212- the speaker is grabbing hold of his hair while saying \u8220"I will be beautiful.\u8221" With that physical gesture providing the frame, what else could he mean? The long hair of a {\i nazir} is being contemplated as something beautiful, and the gesture of seizing one\u8217's hair gives the necessary context to interpret the declaration as an acceptance of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir as sinner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution, however, raises its own difficulty. The Gemara objects: {\i nezira milsa de\u8217'aveira} \u8212- being a {\i nazir} is a matter associated with sin. If {\i nezirut} is sinful, how can it be described as \u8220"beautiful,\u8221" and how can we halakhically anchor that description? The Gemara acknowledges the point but deflects it: {\i de\u8217'afilu le-Rabbi Elazar HaKappar de\u8217'amar nazir chote, hani mili gabbei nazir tamei} \u8212- even according to Rabbi Elazar HaKappar, who holds that the {\i nazir} is a sinner, that characterization applies specifically to a {\i nazir tamei}, a {\i nazir} who has become ritually impure. That is the context of the Torah\u8217's language {\i asher chata al hanefesh} \u8212- \u8220"who sinned against the soul\u8221" \u8212- which appears in the portion dealing with a {\i nazir} who became {\i tamei}. Why is the {\i nazir tamei} a sinner in a way that goes beyond the mere fact of impurity? Because {\i de\u8217'aydi deba\u8217'ei mistar} \u8212- since he must now reverse his entire count and begin again, as the Torah says {\i vehayamim harishonim yipplu} \u8212- the first days fall away \u8212- there is a concern that {\i dilma ati leme\u8217'evar al nezirutei} \u8212- he will come to transgress his {\i nezirut} altogether. As the {\i Rishonim} note, drawing on a {\i gemara} encountered in {\i Nedarim}, a {\i nazir tamei} whose {\i nezirut} drags on indefinitely may come to regret having undertaken it at all, thereby undermining the {\i leshem shamayim} dimension of the vow. That compromised sincerity is what earns the designation {\i chote}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tahor nazir not sinner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then states: {\i aval nazir tahor lav chote kari bei} \u8212- a {\i nazir tahor}, one who has successfully maintained purity throughout, is not called a sinner. Consequently, according to everyone, including the more skeptical voices regarding {\i nezirut}, a {\i nazir tahor} can genuinely be described as {\i na\u8217'eh}. The statement \u8220"I will be beautiful,\u8221" uttered while seizing one\u8217's hair, is therefore an entirely coherent acceptance of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on mixed nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, however, raises a pointed difficulty. In every other context where Rabbi Elazar HaKappar is cited, his position is precisely the opposite: his {\i chiddush} is that the term {\i chote} \u8212- which the Torah applies textually to the {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- extends equally to a {\i nazir tahor}. That is the whole force of his ruling. It is therefore surprising that this Gemara restricts him to the {\i nazir tamei} case. Tosafot resolves this tension through a nuanced reading: Rabbi Elazar HaKappar holds that {\i nezirut} is always a mixed phenomenon. The {\i perishat atzmo min hayayin} \u8212- the self-denial of wine \u8212- or more broadly the aspiration to attain holiness through separating from society rather than engaging it, is genuinely problematic. Yet there is also something worthy in the gesture: a form of sincere religious striving, a personal acceptance of discipline. If the {\i nazir} managed to remain {\i tahor}, the positive element dominated, and we can say the overall experience was good \u8212- {\i hein na\u8217'eh}. But the {\i cheit} element was always present; the negative was not eliminated, merely outweighed. If the {\i nazir} became {\i tamei}, the negative dominates entirely and the designation {\i chote} is unambiguous. This reading allows Rabbi Elazar HaKappar\u8217's position to be consistently one of complexity: the {\i chote} element always applies even to the {\i nazir tahor}, but when purity is maintained, the balance tips toward the positive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This picture of {\i nezirut} as an inherently ambivalent institution \u8212- neither straightforwardly praiseworthy nor simply condemned \u8212- is worth holding in mind as the tractate unfolds. In {\i Nedarim}, the dominant voice was decidedly negative toward vow-making: one who makes a {\i neder} is likened to one who builds a {\i bamah}, and the presumption was that {\i resha\u8217'im} make {\i nedarim}. Whether {\i Nazir} will sustain that register or modulate toward something more ambivalent remains to be seen.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Incomplete expressions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harei kazeh context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the phrase {\i hareini kazeh} \u8212- \u8220"I am like this.\u8221" Does this constitute a declaration of {\i nezirut}? The Gemara immediately asks the same question it posed before: how do we know what \u8220"like this\u8221" refers to? The answer given is {\i she-tefus be-sa\u8217'aro} \u8212- the speaker was holding his hair, the same contextualizing gesture used earlier. Without some such framing, the phrase is too open-ended to anchor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete vs incomplete\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot draws an important structural distinction arising from these cases. When a sentence is left incomplete \u8212- \u8220"I will be\u8221" \u8212- we look to the external surroundings to fill it out. If a {\i nazir} is passing by, that context supplies the missing content: \u8220"I will be like that.\u8221" However, when a complete sentence has been uttered, the interpretive logic changes. \u8220"I will be beautiful\u8221" is a full declaration about oneself; one does not naturally look outward to nearby figures to supply its meaning. The necessary context must therefore be intrinsic \u8212- the speaker holding his own hair \u8212- rather than external. Similarly, if one said \u8220"I will be\u8221" while holding his hair, Tosafot suggests that would not necessarily produce an acceptance of {\i nezirut}: an incomplete sentence looks to the passing {\i nazir} for completion, not to a gesture the speaker is making to himself. Conversely, \u8220"I will be beautiful\u8221" with a {\i nazir} walking past \u8212- but without the speaker touching his own hair \u8212- would likewise be insufficient, since the completed sentence points inward and should be interpreted in relation to the speaker\u8217's own words and actions. The asymmetry follows from the structural difference between an incomplete sentence reaching outward for completion and a complete sentence that is self-contained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silsul discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara next considers {\i hareini mesalsel} \u8212- \u8220"I will {\i salsel}.\u8221" What does {\i silsul} mean, and why should it refer to {\i nezirut}? The Gemara cites the maidservant of Rabbi, who appears in several contexts in {\i Megillah} and {\i Rosh Hashanah} as a repository of authentic biblical Hebrew \u8212- a language increasingly distant from the spoken vernacular of the period, which had shifted toward Aramaic. She said to a certain man, {\i ad matai ata mesalsel be-se\u8217'arekha} \u8212- \u8220"How long will you keep twirling your hair?\u8221" \u8212- thereby establishing that {\i silsul} can refer to playing with or twirling one\u8217's hair. However, the Gemara counters: perhaps {\i mesalsel} means one intends to devote himself to Torah study, as the verse says {\i salseleha u-teromemeka} \u8212- \u8220"delve into it and it will raise you up\u8221" \u8212- where {\i silsul} connotes deep engagement and immersion. Shmuel\u8217's answer is consistent with what he has said throughout: {\i hacha nami she-tefas be-sa\u8217'aro} \u8212- here too, the speaker must be holding his hair. With that gesture, {\i silsul} clearly refers to the hair and not to Torah study.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether {\i silsul} \u8212- twirling or playing with one\u8217's hair \u8212- creates any tension with the {\i nazir}\u8217's obligation {\i gadel pera se\u8217'ar rosho} \u8212- to let his hair grow wild and unruly. The underlying verse describes hair that grows loose and untamed, whereas {\i silsul} implies active handling. The Gemara does not raise this as a formal objection, and the discussion of {\i psik reisha} and intent would apply, but the juxtaposition is worth noting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth pausing over the contextual framings the Gemara has consistently provided. Of the two standard contexts that render these ambiguous declarations meaningful, one involves seeing another person and wishing to emulate them \u8212- a motivation that is, at best, externally driven \u8212- and the other emphasizes the beauty or length of the hair. Neither framing represents the most elevated motivation for undertaking {\i nezirut}. The Gemara may not have had any other practical choice, but both the mimetic impulse and the focus on personal appearance are less than ideal as foundations for a sacred vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then considers {\i hareini mechalkel} \u8212- a declaration apparently referring to some manner of tending the hair. The Gemara cites a {\i mishnah} about the minimum quantity of plaster carried into the public domain, where Rabbi Yehudah specifies {\i kedei la-sud kalkal} \u8212- enough to plaster down the temple hair. Rav Papa explains {\i bastidan} (which Rashi glosses {\i templa be-la\u8217'az} \u8212- \u8220"temple\u8221" in the vernacular) as the hair by the temples that one folds or flattens down in order to secure a covering over it. However, the Gemara counters that {\i kilkul} can also mean sustaining or feeding the poor, as in the verse {\i va-yechalkel Yosef es aviv ve-es echav} \u8212- \u8220"and Yosef sustained his father and brothers.\u8221" Once again, Shmuel provides the same answer: {\i she-tefas be-sa\u8217'aro} \u8212- he was holding his hair. The consistency of Shmuel\u8217's approach throughout this passage underscores what is at stake in his position. Recall that the Gemara earlier asked what Shmuel holds regarding a {\i yad she-einah mochiachat} \u8212- an unclear {\i yad}. The systematic provision of a contextualizing condition for every case in the {\i mishnah}, conditions that the {\i mishnah} itself does not mention, reflects Shmuel\u8217's insistence on a higher threshold of clarity. The {\i mishnah} appears to accept these phrases as self-sufficient; Shmuel requires either the holding of hair or a {\i nazir} passing by to make them evidently meaningful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shalach pera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara arrives at what seems the most obvious case: {\i hareini meshalleach pera} \u8212- \u8220"I will send forth my wild hair.\u8221" This should be unambiguous. Nevertheless, the Gemara asks: {\i mimai de-hadin shiluach ribuya hi} \u8212- how do we know {\i shiluach} means to let grow? Perhaps it means to remove, as in {\i ve-shilach mayim al pnei chutzos} \u8212- \u8220"to cast the water outward\u8221" \u8212- meaning one intends to cut off and discard one\u8217's hair. The Gemara resolves this by invoking the {\i gezeira shava} of {\i pera-pera}: the {\i nazir}\u8217's verse reads {\i kadosh yihyeh gadel pera se\u8217'ar rosho} \u8212- \u8220"holy shall be the growing of the wildness of his hair\u8221" \u8212- and the verse in {\i Yechezkel} regarding ordinary {\i kohanim} reads {\i u-pera lo yishalechu} \u8212- \u8220"they shall not send forth wild hair\u8221" \u8212- i.e., {\i kohanim} must cut their hair every thirty days. The {\i Tosafot Rosh} notes that the primary function of this {\i gezeira shava} is in fact to define the {\i kohen}\u8217's restriction: just as a {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i pera} is defined by the standard term of {\i nezirut}, which is thirty days, so too a {\i kohen} who allows his hair to grow for thirty days has transgressed. But the link also establishes that {\i shilu\u8217'ach pera} by the {\i kohanim} \u8212- letting the hair grow wild \u8212- is the same {\i pera} as the {\i nazir}\u8217's. Therefore {\i shiluach pera} means the growing of wild hair, not its removal. The Gemara adds an alternative: {\i v\u8217'iba\u8217'is eima, ha\u8217'i meshale\u8217'ach mayim nami ribuya hu} \u8212- even the verse about casting water can be read as referring to growth, since {\i d\u8217'chimaski lehu maya l\u8217'peirei} \u8212- when one waters fruits, they grow. For {\i hareini meshalleach pera}, no additional contextual gesture is required; the phrase is sufficiently clear on its own.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birds dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir birds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces a significant dispute: {\i harei alai tzipporim} \u8212- \u8220"I accept upon myself birds.\u8221" Rabbi Meir holds this constitutes {\i nezirut}; the Sages disagree. What is Rabbi Meir\u8217's reasoning? Reish Lakish explains: {\i tzipporim semuchim le-se\u8217'ar kibel alav} \u8212- birds appear in a verse juxtaposed to hair. The verse in Daniel reads: {\i ad di se\u8217'areh k\u8217'neshrin raba v\u8217'tiprohi k\u8217'tziporin} \u8212- \u8220"until his hair grew like an eagle\u8217's and his nails like birds\u8217'.\u8221" The word {\i tzipporin} appears in the context of {\i se\u8217'ar}, hair, and therefore Rabbi Meir holds that birds carries the meaning of its adjacent term, referring to the {\i nazir}\u8217's characteristic hair. It is a considerable interpretive leap \u8212- the more directly juxtaposed word in the verse is {\i neshrin}, eagle, not {\i tzipporin} \u8212- but one can also observe a linguistic connection: the Aramaic {\i tiprohi} shares resonance with {\i tzipornav}, fingernails, which in turn is connected to {\i tzipor}, bird. In any case, Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle is explicit: {\i matpisin b\u8217'davar ha-samuch lo} \u8212- one can perform a {\i hatpasa}, borrowing the meaning of a nearby word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The term {\i hatpasa} carries technical weight from {\i Masechet Nedarim}, where it describes the transfer of status: if item A has been rendered {\i assur} by a {\i neder konam}, and one then says {\i zeh kazeh}, one is {\i matpis} the second item with the status of the first. Here the usage is somewhat different \u8212- one is not transferring a status but borrowing a semantic meaning from an adjacent word in a verse \u8212- yet the technical vocabulary is deliberately introduced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages reject borrowing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages hold {\i lo matpis inish bemidi d\u8217'samich leh} \u8212- one does not borrow meaning from a nearby word. Their more straightforward understanding of the declaration {\i harei alai tzipporim} would be that the speaker intends to bring bird sacrifices in the context of {\i nezirut}: since a {\i nazir tamei} brings bird offerings as part of his {\i korban}, saying \u8220"I accept birds upon myself\u8221" might mean \u8220"I accept the type of {\i nezirut} that involves bringing birds.\u8221" That is the more direct semantic connection, and notably it reflects somewhat negatively on the speaker\u8217's framing \u8212- accepting {\i nezirut} with anticipated impurity built into the conception is a troubling starting point, given everything the earlier discussion established about the {\i nazir tamei}. But that is the Sages\u8217' reading of what Rabbi Meir himself must mean. The Sages say that even this more direct connection is insufficient: you cannot anchor a declaration of {\i nezirut} in a term that is itself neutral simply because a related term appears nearby.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan view\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan offers a different account entirely. He holds {\i dekuli alma lo matpis} \u8212- everyone agrees that one cannot borrow meaning from a nearby word. The disagreement between Rabbi Meir and the Sages is not about the {\i hatpasa} principle at all. Rabbi Meir\u8217's reasoning, according to Rabbi Yochanan, is {\i de-chayshinan shema tzipporei nazir tamei kibel alav} \u8212- we are concerned that perhaps the speaker meant to accept upon himself the bird-sacrifices of a {\i nazir tamei}. That is the practical connection: a {\i nazir tamei} brings bird sacrifices, and saying \u8220"I accept birds\u8221" in that context may be understood as a declaration of {\i nezirut} with the attendant {\i korbanot} in mind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses: if we are operating on \u8220"perhaps that is what he means,\u8221" why not say {\i dilma tzipporei nedavah kibel alav} \u8212- perhaps he simply meant to bring a voluntary bird offering as an {\i olah}? The Gemara responds: if that were his intent, he would have said {\i harei alai kan} \u8212- the standard idiom for a pair of bird offerings. Since he said {\i tzipporim} without the standard term {\i kan}, the voluntary-offering reading is less natural. The Gemara continues: perhaps he meant to donate birds for a {\i metzora}\u8217's purification ritual, where the verse uses the word {\i tzippor} repeatedly \u8212- {\i ve-shachat et ha-tzippor ha-chayah}, {\i ve-et ha-tzippor}, {\i be-dam ha-tzippor ha-shechutah}. Tosafot explains this as meaning: perhaps he intended that if any {\i metzora} in the world requires birds for his {\i tahara} process, he accepts upon himself to donate them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish differs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These challenges reveal that even Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's explanation requires an additional condition to eliminate alternative readings. The Gemara therefore concludes: {\i kegon shehaya nazir tahor over lefanav} \u8212- the case is one in which a {\i nazir tahor} was walking in front of the speaker. With that context, the declaration \u8220"I accept birds upon myself\u8221" is understood as relating to {\i nezirut}, and not to any of the alternative possibilities. This answer applies equally to Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reasoning and to Reish Lakish\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir context required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If both Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish ultimately require a {\i nazir} walking in front of the speaker, the question arises: {\i mai beinaiahu} \u8212- what is the practical difference between their positions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot reconciles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: {\i ika beinaiahu kegon d\u8217'amar tzipporim ha-semuchim le-se\u8217'ar alai} \u8212- the difference emerges in a case where there was no {\i nazir} passing by, but the speaker explicitly said \u8220"I accept upon myself birds that are written next to hair.\u8221" According to Rabbi Yochanan, even this explicit formulation is insufficient without a {\i nazir} present; the contextual anchor is required regardless. According to Reish Lakish, this explicit invocation of the verse\u8217's juxtaposition is itself sufficient: by referencing birds in the specific context of that {\i pasuk} in Daniel, the speaker has made clear that \u8220"birds\u8221" carries the meaning of the adjacent \u8220"hair,\u8221" and therefore of {\i nezirut} \u8212- without any need for a {\i nazir} to be passing by.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This allows Reish Lakish\u8217's account to explain the underlying debate between Rabbi Meir and the Sages in the {\i mishnah} as stated, where no special external context is mentioned at all. Rabbi Meir holds that the invocation of a word whose neighboring term in a verse has the desired meaning is sufficient; the Sages hold it is not. On that reading, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation makes sense: Rabbi Meir requires no passing {\i nazir}; he relies on the semantic borrowing from the text. The Sages reject that borrowing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a challenge to the Sages\u8217' position from a {\i Braisa}: {\i ha-omer yamin harei zo shevu\u8217'ah} \u8212- one who says \u8220"by my right hand\u8221" has made a valid oath. How can the mere utterance of \u8220"right hand\u8221" constitute a {\i shevu\u8217'ah}? Is that not precisely borrowing the meaning of a neighboring term \u8212- since the verse says {\i va-yarem yemino u-smolo el ha-shamayim va-yishava b\u8217'chai ha-olam}, where {\i yamin} appears alongside {\i shevu\u8217'ah}? The Gemara rejects this comparison: {\i mishum d\u8217'yamin gufei ikrei shevu\u8217'ah} \u8212- because the word {\i yamin} itself has acquired the independent meaning of oath, as the verse {\i nishba Hashem bi-yemino} demonstrates: God swore \u8220"by His right hand,\u8221" meaning God took an oath in the form of \u8220"By my right hand I will do X.\u8221" The meaning of {\i yamin} as oath is therefore intrinsic to the word through usage, not merely borrowed from an adjacent term. This distinction vindicates the Sages\u8217' position: they reject the borrowing of neighboring meaning, but acknowledge that words can organically develop new meanings through the contexts in which they consistently appear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All-or-nothing rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} introduces a new and conceptually important case: {\i harei ani nazir min ha-chartzanim} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} from the grape-skins,\u8221" or {\i min ha-zagim} \u8212- from the seeds, or {\i min ha-tiglachat} \u8212- from shaving, or {\i min ha-tumah} \u8212- from impurity. The person has declared {\i nezirut} but attempted to limit it to only one of its constituent restrictions. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i harei zeh nazir ve-chol dikdukei nezirut alav} \u8212- that person is nonetheless a {\i nazir}, and all the detailed laws of {\i nezirut} apply. It is an all-or-nothing institution: one cannot accept the status of {\i nazir} piecemeal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately notes: {\i masnisin d\u8217'lo k\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} does not reflect the position of Rabbi Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon view\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Braisa} is cited: Rabbi Shimon holds {\i eino chayav ad she-yidom mikulam} \u8212- one is not bound by {\i nezirut} unless one vows regarding all of its restrictions. If someone attempts only a partial acceptance, he has done nothing at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages hold the opposite: {\i afilu nazar ela be-echad min ha-nehon havei nazir} \u8212- even if one accepts only one restriction, one becomes a {\i nazir} subject to all of them. The logic is that {\i nezirut} is a unified package; a partial acceptance drags the whole structure along with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What are the biblical sources for these positions? Rabbi Shimon derives his view from {\i mikkol asher ye\u8217'aseh mi-gefen ha-yayyin me-chartzannim ve-ad zag} \u8212- \u8220"from everything that is made from the grapevine, from the skin to the pit.\u8221" The word {\i mikkol} \u8212- from everything \u8212- implies that one must accept the full scope; a partial acceptance falls short of the whole and is therefore nothing. The Sages derive theirs from {\i mi-yayin ve-shachar yazir} \u8212- \u8220"from wine and intoxicating drink he shall become a {\i nazir}.\u8221" The verse names only wine, yet we know full well that a {\i nazir} is bound by far more than wine alone. Evidently, naming only wine \u8212- only part of the obligation \u8212- is sufficient to constitute {\i nezirut} for everything. Even a partial declaration triggers the full status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir and wine mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine mizvot issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: since the extra language {\i mi-yayin ve-shachar yazir} is not needed to tell us about the all-or-nothing rule (the Sages deriving their position from elsewhere), what does this verse come to teach? The answer: {\i la-shavyei mitzvah ke-reshut} \u8212- to equate even a {\i mitzvah} involving wine with an ordinary permitted act. That is, the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition on wine applies even when drinking wine would fulfill a {\i mitzvah}. The verse\u8217's inclusive language covers wine consumed in the performance of commandments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddush problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\i mitzvah} would be implicated? {\i Kiddushta ve-avdalta} \u8212- the wine of {\i kiddush} and {\i havdala}. Even for those, the {\i nazir} is forbidden. This seems unremarkable. But the Gemara then produces what is, by any measure, a striking statement: {\i harei mushba ve-omed hu me-Har Sinai} \u8212- but is he not already sworn and obligated from Sinai? The implication appears to be that since one has a {\i Sinai}-level obligation to make {\i kiddush}, that obligation pre-empts the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition: {\i mushba ve-omed} \u8212- a pre-existing oath or obligation cannot be overridden by a subsequent vow. The {\i Rishonim} are confounded. Wine for {\i kiddush} is not {\i d\u8217'Oraita} in the first place \u8212- there is no verse in the Torah requiring {\i kiddush} on wine specifically; one may make {\i kiddush} on bread. And even if there were a Torah-level obligation to make {\i kiddush} on wine, the principle of {\i mushba ve-omed} would not straightforwardly apply, because accepting {\i nezirut} does not constitute a direct declaration against the {\i kiddush} obligation. One could compare it to declaring a {\i sukkah} forbidden to oneself: such a declaration does not directly negate the obligation to dwell in a {\i sukkah}, but focuses on the object; and yet it effectively prevents fulfillment of the {\i mitzvah} when the time comes. The same logic applies to {\i nezirut}: by accepting the status of {\i nazir} one does not say \u8220"I will not make {\i kiddush}\u8221" \u8212- one merely takes on a status that renders wine {\i assur}, which then gets in the way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddush not d\u8217'Oraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rishonim} therefore struggle to make sense of this {\i gemara} as a straightforward statement. The basic analytic objections are twofold: first, wine for {\i kiddush} is {\i de-Rabbanan}, not {\i d\u8217'Oraita}; second, even for a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} obligation, the {\i mushba ve-omed} principle does not apply when the newer vow operates obliquely rather than directly against the standing obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbeinu Tam rhetorical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam cuts through the difficulty with characteristic boldness: the Gemara\u8217's statement is not a declarative assertion but a rhetorical question expressed with astonishment \u8212- {\i bitmiha}. The Gemara is not saying that {\i mushba ve-omed} prevents {\i nezirut} from taking effect regarding {\i kiddush} wine. It is asking incredulously: do you really think there is a Sinai-level obligation to drink wine for {\i kiddush}? Of course there is not. The whole premise of the question collapses, and the verse\u8217's teaching that {\i nezirut} applies even to wine connected with {\i mitzvot} stands uncontested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presents Rava\u8217's formulation of a related case: {\i shavu\u8217'ah she-eshteh} \u8212- if one takes an oath that he will drink, {\i ve-chazar ve-amar hareini nazir} \u8212- and then declares himself a {\i nazir}, {\i chalah nezirut al shavu\u8217'ah} \u8212- the {\i nezirut} takes effect notwithstanding the oath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirut vs shevua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is the clean illustration of the principle at stake. A person has sworn an oath to drink wine and subsequently accepted {\i nezirut}. His {\i nezirut} is fully operative, and he is now forbidden to drink. But this does not mean the {\i nazir} simply overrides his {\i shevua}: it means the {\i nezirut} takes effect as a status, and when the time arrives to fulfill the oath, the {\i nazir} status stands in the way \u8212- just as making all {\i sukkot} forbidden to oneself does not directly violate the obligation to sit in a {\i sukkah} but renders fulfillment impossible when {\i Sukkot} arrives. The {\i nezirut} does not go head-to-head against the {\i shevua}; it operates laterally, focusing on the person\u8217's status rather than directly contradicting his prior commitment. That is why {\i nezirut} takes effect \u8212- it is not {\i mushba ve-omed} in the direct sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overwhelming consensus of the {\i Rishonim}, including the extended discussion in the {\i Tosafot Rid} in {\i Arvei Pesachim}, is to follow Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's reading: the {\i gemara}\u8217's {\i mushba ve-omed} statement is rhetorical, wine for {\i kiddush} is {\i de-Rabbanan}, and {\i nezirut} can take effect even in the face of a {\i shevua} because the two operate on different planes. A small number of {\i Rishonim} do attempt to read the {\i gemara} literally \u8212- concluding, for instance, that a {\i nazir} would be permitted to drink {\i kiddush} wine, or entertaining related anomalies \u8212- but these readings are widely regarded as untenable. The {\i sugya} resolves cleanly on Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's account, and with that the daf reaches its conclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 4\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimon vs.\u160 ?Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirut Indivisible\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} establishes that {\i nezirut} is an all-or-nothing commitment: one cannot accept upon oneself only a partial form of {\i nezirut} \u8212- only from grapes, only from hair-cutting, or only from {\i tumah}. It is a complete package. The {\i gemara} then engages Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position, which likewise treats {\i nezirut} as indivisible, but with an additional and sharper implication: if one attempted to accept {\i nezirut} from only one aspect of its obligations, one has done nothing at all. There is a conceivable third position \u8212- that partial {\i nezirut} is in fact valid \u8212- but this is a position the {\i gemara} rejects. The one exception, which will emerge in the coming {\i mishna}, is {\i nazir Shimshon}, since Shimshon himself became {\i tamei} regularly. Beyond that, there is no phenomenon of partial {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon: All or Nothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, as it often does, seeks to identify the {\i pasuk} from which each side derives its position, and then to ask what the opposing side does with that same verse. In this context, the central {\i pasuk} under analysis is {\i miyayin veshechar yazir} \u8212- that a {\i nazir} will abstain from wine and intoxicating liquid. As discussed previously, the Sages understand that in the context of {\i nezirut}, {\i shechar} does not carry its ordinary meaning of non-wine intoxicants such as beer or mead, but refers only to wine-based mixtures \u8212- a reading that is admittedly not the straightforward {\i pshat} of the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} had explained that the {\i rabbanan} use this verse to teach that even an attempt to accept {\i nezirut} from only one element draws in the entire package. If so, what does Rabbi Shimon do with that verse? He holds that the verse comes to tell us that even a type of wine one might have thought should be excluded remains forbidden to the {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miyayin Veshechar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially suggests that the excluded wine might be the wine of {\i kiddush} and {\i havdalah}, but this is rejected. The {\i gemara}\u8217's language \u8212- {\i mushba ve\u8217'omed me\u8217'har sinai} \u8212- is read by most {\i rishonim}, following Rabbeinu Tam, as ironic or rhetorical: obviously a {\i nazir} cannot drink the wine of {\i kiddush} and {\i havdalah}. No verse is needed to establish that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore identifies a more meaningful case, introduced by Rava: suppose someone takes a {\i shevuah} that he will drink wine, and immediately afterward declares himself a {\i nazir}. One might have thought that the prior oath to drink wine cannot be overridden by the subsequent {\i nezirut}, and that he must therefore drink the wine. The verse {\i miyayin veshechar yazir} comes to teach that the {\i nezirut} does indeed take hold, and he is forbidden from that wine as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yayin Mitzvah Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle here is that one cannot take a {\i shevuah} that directly countermands an existing obligation \u8212- {\i mushba ve\u8217'omed}. I cannot swear not to sit in a {\i sukkah} on {\i Sukkot}, for instance, since I am already obligated. Nor can I swear to drink wine and then swear not to drink wine; the second oath would directly contradict the first obligation. However, a {\i neder} operates differently. A {\i neder} works indirectly: if I declare all {\i sukkahs} forbidden to me, I am not directly countermanding my personal obligation. I am making the physical object of the {\i sukkah} forbidden, and then when the {\i mitzvah} arrives, I simply cannot fulfill it \u8212- the object is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, when one declares oneself a {\i nazir}, one is not saying \u8220"I will not drink wine.\u8221" One is changing one\u8217's status, one\u8217's identity. The natural consequence of having assumed the status of {\i nazir} is that wine becomes forbidden, but this is not a direct countermand of the prior {\i shevuah} to drink. One relates to the object \u8212- or in the case of {\i nezirut}, one becomes the object \u8212- and everything flows from that new status. This is why {\i nezirut} can override even an existing oath, because it is a change of personal status rather than a direct acceptance of individual obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirut as Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is crucial: when a person says {\i harei ani nazir}, he is not directly obligating himself not to drink wine, not to cut his hair, not to become {\i tamei}. He is imparting upon himself the status of {\i nazir}, and from that status all the individual {\i issurim} flow by Torah law. He himself becomes, in a sense, the object \u8212- the bearer of the status \u8212- and the prohibitions attach to him through that status, not through direct vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i gemara}\u8217's give-and-take: the {\i rabbanan} counter that they too need the verse {\i miyayin veshechar yazir} to teach the case of the wine that was the subject of a prior {\i shevuah}. If so, they cannot use that verse for the all-or-nothing teaching. But the {\i gemara} responds: if the only purpose of the verse were to teach that this particular wine remains forbidden, it would have sufficed to say \u8220"from wine.\u8221" The fact that it adds {\i veshechar} \u8212- the extra element \u8212- must come to teach something additional: namely, that even if one attempted {\i nezirut} only from {\i shechar}, one becomes a {\i nazir} with respect to everything. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's purpose for the word {\i shechar} is then asked: he holds that {\i shechar} in this verse comes to establish a {\i hekesh}, a comparison, between the law of the {\i nazir} and the law of the {\i Beit HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shechar Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tziruf Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse in the context of the {\i Kohanim} \u8212- {\i yayin veshechar al teisht ata u-vanecha itach} \u8212- prohibits a {\i kohein} from entering the {\i Beit HaMikdash} in a state of intoxication. That verse likewise uses the phrase {\i yayin veshechar}. Since by {\i nazir} we establish that {\i shechar} cannot mean non-wine intoxicants but must refer to wine-based mixtures, the {\i hekesh} between {\i nazir} and {\i Mikdash} teaches that the same is true for a {\i kohein}: only wine, not other intoxicating liquids, would trigger the prohibition of entering the {\i Mikdash}. In both contexts, the prohibition extends specifically to wine-based drinks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The technical distinction matters: for a {\i kohein} and wine, there is a formal measure \u8212- a {\i revi\u8217'is}, approximately three and a half ounces, which is less than the size of most {\i kiddush} cups. For {\i shechar} in the ordinary sense, the prohibition requires actual intoxication. The {\i gemara} reasons that since {\i shechar} cannot carry its plain meaning in the context of {\i nazir}, and the verse in {\i Mikdash} uses the identical phrase, it too must be restricted to wine-based drinks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda: Broad Shec\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a rejection of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position. A {\i baraita} records that Rabbi Yehuda holds: if a person ate pressed figs from Ke\u8217'ilis, or drank honey and milk, and then entered the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, he is liable \u8212- because all of these substances, even some that are solid foods rather than drinks, can cause a state of intoxication and are therefore encompassed within {\i shechar}. For Rabbi Yehuda, {\i shechar} extends to anything that produces this state of being intoxicated or somewhat elevated, regardless of whether it is a beverage at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechar Wine-Based\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} reject this broad reading. Only wine-based substances are encompassed by {\i shechar}; other intoxicating substances \u8212- {\i sha\u8217'ar devarim hamishkarin} \u8212- are not included. There is a significant gap between these positions. Rabbi Yehuda takes the word {\i shechar}, which refers to a specific type of intoxicating beverage, and extends it to anything that produces this physiological state. A reasonable position might have rejected Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's extension to solids while still reading {\i shechar} literally as beer or mead. Yet the {\i gemara} here implies that even for entering the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, only wine-based substances are included. It should be noted that this is not the way we rule in practice: the {\i halacha} does in fact hold that {\i shechar} is included, and we tend to rule like Rabbi Yehuda that even other intoxicating substances are prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hora\u8217'ah Digression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion naturally gives rise to a related practical question: if a person consumed a substance that caused intoxication \u8212- whether a food like fermented figs or a medication taken for serious pain that produced an altered state \u8212- would that disqualify him from rendering a {\i halachic} ruling or sitting on a {\i beit din}? The prohibition against {\i hora\u8217'ah} while intoxicated is not limited to wine; if we rule like Rabbi Yehuda that other intoxicating substances are included, then medication that produces a high might equally disqualify a {\i dayan}. One practical distinction found in the {\i Shulchan Aruch} is that if a person is merely relating what is already written explicitly in the books \u8212- not rendering a novel ruling \u8212- that is not considered {\i paskening} and would be permitted even in an intoxicated state. As was wryly observed: if one can remember the {\i siman} in {\i Shulchan Aruch}, perhaps one is not genuinely incapacitated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Isur Chal Al Isur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Double Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues. The {\i rabbanan} need the verse {\i miyayin veshechar yazir} for the case of the {\i shevuah} to drink wine; they use it to teach that even that wine remains forbidden after one accepts {\i nezirut}. But there is an alternative explanation for what Rabbi Shimon does with this verse. The {\i gemara} suggests: Rabbi Shimon does not hold the principle of {\i isur chal al isur} \u8212- that one forbidden status cannot take hold atop another. Because he rejects this principle generally, he needs a verse to establish that {\i nezirut} does indeed create a prohibition even where a prior {\i isur} already exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neveila/YK Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} brings a {\i baraita} to illustrate Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position: Rabbi Shimon says that one who eats a {\i neveila} on {\i Yom Kippur} is exempt from violating {\i Yom Kippur}, because there is no {\i isur chal al isur}. This is a striking {\i gemara}. One can understand the principle when it applies to the status of an object: if a piece of meat is already a {\i neveila} and one then cooks it in milk, one might argue that the object cannot acquire the additional status of {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} \u8212- it already bears a forbidden status, and the second prohibition does not attach. That logic is coherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Yom Kippur} case is far more puzzling. The prohibition of {\i Yom Kippur} does not rest on the status of the object; it rests on me \u8212- on the person\u8217's obligation of {\i inuy}, self-affliction. What does the forbidden status of the {\i neveila} have to do with whether I have violated my personal obligation of {\i Yom Kippur}? I have still engaged in an act of eating on {\i Yom Kippur}. Why should I be exempt? This is the primary case from which Rabbi Shimon derives his principle, and yet it is the most difficult to understand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath vs.\u160 ?Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} applies this to {\i nezirut}: suppose one took a {\i shevuah} not to drink wine \u8212- the mirror image of the earlier case \u8212- and then declared himself a {\i nazir}, and then drank wine. According to Rabbi Shimon, the verse {\i miyayin veshechar yazir} establishes that even in this case he is forbidden on the wine through {\i nezirut} as well \u8212- not only through his prior {\i shevuah}. This is a case where {\i isur chal al isur} does apply even according to Rabbi Shimon, because the verse teaches it explicitly. The principle applies whether the prior {\i shevuah} was to drink the wine or not to drink it; in either case, the {\i nezirut} remains operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This again connects to the insight that {\i nezirut} functions as a change of status rather than a direct assumption of obligations. When one declares {\i harei ani nazir}, one is not directly confronting the prior oath at all; one is transforming one\u8217's identity, and the prohibitions follow from that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimon: No Shiur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combining Products\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the verse the {\i rabbanan} use for their all-or-nothing teaching. Rabbi Shimon had used {\i mikkol asher ye\u8217'aseh migefen ha-yayin} to mean that one must accept {\i nezirut} from everything, and that accepting only part produces nothing. What do the {\i rabbanan} do with this verse? They read it as teaching the law of {\i tziruf} \u8212- that the various grape-derived products forbidden to a {\i nazir} combine with one another. If one ate a small amount of grape skin and a small amount of seeds and some raisins, and together these combine to constitute a {\i kezayit}, one has violated the prohibition. The word {\i mikkol} \u8212- from all \u8212- indicates that all the different categories of forbidden grape products join together to form the minimum measure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Shehu L\u8217'Malkos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then does Rabbi Shimon do without this teaching of {\i tziruf}? The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Shimon holds {\i kol shehu l\u8217'malkot} \u8212- even the smallest amount is sufficient for lashes. This is a general principle Rabbi Shimon advances throughout {\i Shas}, argued most fully in {\i Masechet Makkot}: wherever the Sages require a minimum measure, Rabbi Shimon holds that one violates the prohibition even with the smallest amount and is liable even to lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shiur for Korban Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon does allow for a minimum measure \u8212- but only in one specific context: {\i lo amru kezayit ela l\u8217'inyan korban} \u8212- the {\i kezayit} measure was stated only with respect to the {\i korban}. If one acted unintentionally and the question is whether one must bring an atonement offering, a minimum threshold applies. But for purposes of punishment \u8212- including lashes \u8212- even the smallest amount constitutes a full violation. This is a fascinating idea: willful transgression, even of a minute quantity, is a complete violation. Unintentional transgression triggers a {\i korban} only once it reaches a certain degree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By {\i nezirut}, Rabbi Shimon therefore has no need for {\i tziruf}. For lashes, he does not need a {\i kezayit} of any single type, let alone a combination \u8212- even the tiniest amount suffices. For a {\i korban}, one would need a minimum measure, but by {\i nezirut} one does not bring a {\i korban} for violating the prohibition on grapes and wine. The {\i korban} is brought either upon the completion of {\i nezirut} or when one becomes {\i tamei}. There is therefore no context in which {\i tziruf} would be necessary according to Rabbi Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i kezayit} measure does remain relevant according to most authorities, with Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling that {\i chatzi shiur} \u8212- less than the minimum amount \u8212- constitutes an {\i issur de\u8217'oraita} (unlike Resh Lakish\u8217's position), though not at the full degree of violation. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position goes further: he holds there is no minimum at all, even for purposes of punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Shimshon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identifying Shimshon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} introduces the institution of {\i nazir Shimshon}. If someone says {\i harei ani k\u8217'Shimshon} \u8212- behold I am like Shimshon \u8212- or {\i k\u8217'ven Manoach}, like the son of Manoach, or {\i k\u8217'ba\u8217'al Delilah}, like the husband of Delilah, or {\i k\u8217'mi she\u8217'akar daltot Azza}, like the one who tore down the doors of Gaza, or {\i k\u8217'mi shenakru Pelishtim et einav}, like the one whose eyes the Philistines gouged out \u8212- in any of these cases one becomes a {\i nazir Shimshon}. The larger {\i chiddush} here is not merely which phrases work, but that there exists this distinct institution of {\i nazir Shimshon}, in which one does not violate \u8212- or does not violate to the same degree \u8212- through becoming {\i tamei}, since Shimshon himself became {\i tamei l\u8217'met} routinely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clarification Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why does the {\i mishna} need to list all of these formulations? The answer is that each one is necessary. If one said only {\i harei ani k\u8217'Shimshon}, one might be referring to another Shimshon entirely \u8212- there are many people by that name. The {\i mishna} therefore adds {\i k\u8217'ven Manoach}. But if one said only {\i k\u8217'ven Manoach}, there might be other people called ben Manoach; the {\i mishna} therefore includes {\i k\u8217'ba\u8217'al Delilah}. And so on, each phrase clarifying and building upon the previous ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an interesting question about how to read the {\i mishna}. On the surface, the {\i mishna} appears to offer these phrases as alternatives \u8212- any one of them suffices. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's reasoning seems to suggest that they are cumulative: one must say {\i k\u8217'Shimshon}, then {\i k\u8217'ven Manoach}, then {\i k\u8217'ba\u8217'al Delilah}, each phrase required to remove ambiguity introduced by the prior one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses this tension. They hold that the first set of phrases \u8212- {\i k\u8217'Shimshon k\u8217'ven Manoach k\u8217'ba\u8217'al Delilah} \u8212- do indeed require one another, meaning all must be said together before {\i nazir Shimshon} status is established. However, {\i Tosafot} maintains that the final phrases \u8212- {\i k\u8217'mi she\u8217'akar daltot Azza} and {\i k\u8217'mi shenakru Pelishtim et einav} \u8212- are sufficiently explicit on their own. Even so, this reading does not align naturally with the straightforward {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna}, which seems to present each phrase as independently sufficient, nor does the {\i gemara} state this distinction explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading is in fact consistent with a theme we have encountered in the previous two days: the {\i mishna} appears to permit relatively vague formulations to be effective, while the {\i gemara} requires far greater specificity \u8212- as we saw with Shmuel\u8217's ruling that one must have a {\i nazir} standing in front of him and be holding one\u8217's hair. The {\i gemara} here similarly tightens the requirements of the {\i mishna}, which is fully consistent with what we have seen throughout the opening of this tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Olam vs.\u160 ?Shimshon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Olam Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} asks: {\i mah bein nazir olam l\u8217'nazir Shimshon} \u8212- what is the difference between a {\i nazir olam} and a {\i nazir Shimshon}? Both are permanent forms of {\i nezirut}, but they are mirror images of one another. A {\i nazir olam} is one who accepts {\i nezirut} upon himself forever, with no intention to terminate it. The reader will be aware that Rav Kook had a student known as \u8220"the Nazir\u8221" who accepted {\i nezirut} and remained a {\i nazir} for his entire life, since there is no {\i Beit HaMikdash} in which to bring the {\i korban} that would terminate {\i nezirut}. It is worth noting, however, that {\i nazir olam} is a specific institution: one who declares himself a {\i nazir} but never brings the concluding {\i korban} is not thereby a {\i nazir olam} \u8212- he is simply a {\i nazir} who has chosen not to end his {\i nezirut}. A {\i nazir olam} is one who by the very nature of his declaration intends it to be permanent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the {\i mishna} has introduced the comparison without having first established what {\i nazir olam} is. It therefore fills in: {\i michsarei michsara vehachi katani} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is incomplete and should be read as having stated: one who says {\i harei ani nazir olam}, behold I am a {\i nazir} forever, is a {\i nazir olam}. Now, what is the difference between a {\i nazir olam} and a {\i nazir Shimshon}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i nazir olam hichbid se\u8217'aro meikel beta\u8217'ar umevi shalosh behemot} \u8212- a {\i nazir olam}, when his hair becomes too heavy, is permitted to trim it with a razor, and when he does so he must bring three animals as offerings. This is remarkable: by all appearances, cutting one\u8217's hair violates a biblical prohibition for a {\i nazir}, yet the Sages derive from the case of Avshalom that this allowance exists for a {\i nazir olam}. We are learning from actual cases in {\i Nach} \u8212- Shimshon and Avshalom \u8212- that variant forms of {\i nezirut} exist which carry leniencies not found in the {\i Torah}\u8217's presentation of {\i nezirut}. A {\i nazir Shimshon} may become {\i tamei lemet}; a {\i nazir olam} may trim his hair when it becomes excessively burdensome. Both seemingly violate biblical prohibitions, yet both are learned out from these later figures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avshalom Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} in the last {\i tosafot} of the {\i daf} explains: the requirement to bring three animals when cutting one\u8217's hair is a {\i halacha} transmitted to Moshe at Sinai, with support derived from the verse concerning Avshalom, who declared {\i ashaleim nidri} \u8212- I will fulfill my vow. The vow is understood as the same three-animal sacrifice \u8212- {\i chatat}, {\i olah}, and {\i shelamim} \u8212- that a {\i nazir tahor} brings upon completing his {\i nezirut}. In a certain sense, bringing these offerings constitutes a virtual completion of the {\i nezirut}, opening a window that permits the hair to be cut, even though the {\i nezirut} itself continues forever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Korbanot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the {\i nazir olam} brings these three offerings and cuts his hair, he has not ended his {\i nezirut}. He must continue observing all the laws of {\i nezirut} going forward. The {\i korbanot} serve as a kind of partial punctuation \u8212- a moment of release from the burden of accumulated hair \u8212- while the fundamental status of {\i nazir} remains intact. The laws of {\i tumah} for a {\i nazir olam}, by contrast, are standard: {\i ve\u8217'im nitma meivi korban tumah} \u8212- if he becomes {\i tamei}, he must bring the {\i korban tumah} just as any other {\i nazir} would. There is no leniency by {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimshon Leniencies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nazir Shimshon} is precisely the reverse. {\i Hichbid se\u8217'aro eino meikel} \u8212- even if his hair grows impossibly heavy, he is not permitted to trim it. There is no allowance regarding hair. This, of course, reflects what we know of Shimshon himself: the relationship between scissors and his hair was not a favorable one. But {\i ve\u8217'im nitma eino meivi korban tumah} \u8212- if he becomes {\i tamei}, he is not obligated to bring a {\i korban tumah}. There is an allowance by {\i tumah}. The two institutions are thus mirror images, each reflecting the actual reality of the figure from whom they take their name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that {\i Tosafot} adds another difference between the two institutions that the {\i mishna} does not mention: a {\i nazir olam} can have his {\i nezirut} revoked by a {\i chacham} through {\i she\u8217'eila}, whereas a {\i nazir Shimshon} cannot. Since the {\i mishna} wished to enumerate differences in the practical observance of {\i nezirut} rather than in the manner of its dissolution, this distinction was not included.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nezirut on Shimshon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a sharp question about the {\i mishna}\u8217's formulation. The {\i mishna} says that a {\i nazir Shimshon} does not bring a {\i korban tumah} \u8212- implying that becoming {\i tamei} is still prohibited but does not trigger a {\i korban}. But this creates an apparent paradox: if {\i nezirut} applies fully to a {\i nazir Shimshon} in all other respects, why would he not be obligated to bring a {\i korban tumah} upon becoming {\i tamei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mani matnitin?} the {\i gemara} asks \u8212- who is our {\i mishna} following? The answer is: neither Rabbi Yehuda nor Rabbi Shimon, as their positions will be shown to be more extreme than the {\i mishna} implies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Permits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records that Rabbi Yehuda holds: {\i nazir Shimshon mutar letamei lametim} \u8212- a {\i nazir Shimshon} is permitted {\i lechatchila} to become {\i tamei} to corpses, since that is precisely what we find that Shimshon himself did. His becoming {\i tamei} was not a violation at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon, on the other hand, holds: {\i ha\u8217'omer harei ani nazir Shimshon lo amar klum} \u8212- one who says \u8220"behold I am a {\i nazir Shimshon}\u8221" has said nothing whatsoever. The reason: {\i shelo matzinu b\u8217'Shimshon sheyatza nezirut mipiyu} \u8212- we do not find that Shimshon ever verbally accepted {\i nezirut} upon himself. The angel declared it; the status was conferred from birth. Shimshon\u8217's father did not accept it for him, and Shimshon himself never articulated an acceptance. There was no {\i halachic} act of vowing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then resolves who the {\i mishna} follows: it must go like Rabbi Yehuda. The reason the {\i mishna} used the conditional formulation {\i im nitma} \u8212- \u8220"if he became {\i tamei}\u8221" \u8212- is only because the parallel structure required it: since by {\i nazir olam} the {\i mishna} stated {\i im nitma meivi korban tumah}, it mirrored that formulation for {\i nazir Shimshon}. But the true content of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, as the {\i gemara} clarifies, is that a {\i nazir Shimshon} is {\i lechatchila} permitted to become {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vow vs.\u160 ?Automatic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Becoming Nazir Shimshon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now explores how exactly one becomes a {\i nazir Shimshon} according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's understanding. All the phrases listed in the {\i mishna} \u8212- {\i k\u8217'Shimshon}, {\i k\u8217'ven Manoach}, {\i k\u8217'ba\u8217'al Delilah}, and so on \u8212- are formulations in which one says \u8220"I wish to be like Shimshon.\u8221" This is not, on reflection, the acceptance of an independent institution called {\i nazir Shimshon}. What one is actually saying is: I want to take Shimshon\u8217's {\i nezirut} status and transfer it to myself. This mechanism of transferring a status from one object or person to another is called {\i hatfasah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatfasah Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hatfasah} \u8212- the act of \u8220"grabbing,\u8221" of connecting one thing to another that already bears a status in order to transfer that status \u8212- is a recognized mechanism within the world of {\i nedarim}. If a sanctified animal is standing before me, and I point to a loaf of bread and say {\i zeh ka-zeh} \u8212- this is like that \u8212- I have transferred the {\i kedushah} of the first object to the second. This works because both the original object and the new one are operating within the same world of {\i nedarim}, the world in which status is imparted through speech acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what if the first object\u8217's status was never imparted through words? What if it became {\i kadosh} automatically, without a human vow? Can one then use {\i hatfasah} to transfer that status to another object? This is the crux of the debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Davar Hanidur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon dispute a principle called {\i davar hanidur} \u8212- whether {\i hatfasah} is effective only when the original status came into being through a {\i neder}, a verbal act of consecration. Rabbi Shimon holds that {\i hatfasah} requires a {\i davar hanidur}: the original status must have been created through words. A status that came about automatically \u8212- like the status of {\i nazir} conferred upon Shimshon from the moment of his birth through the angel\u8217's declaration, without any human speech act \u8212- cannot be transferred through {\i hatfasah}. Since every formulation by which one becomes a {\i nazir Shimshon} is an act of {\i hatfasah} \u8212- I want what Shimshon had \u8212- and since Shimshon\u8217's status was not a {\i davar hanidur}, the declaration is meaningless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bekhor Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bekhor Transferability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} tests this principle through the parallel case of {\i bekhor}. A {\i baraita} records a dispute: if one says {\i harei alai k\u8217'bechor} \u8212- this loaf of bread shall be like this firstborn animal \u8212- does the {\i hatfasah} work? A {\i bekhor} is indeed {\i kadosh} and forbidden in benefit, but it became {\i kadosh} automatically, through birth, without any human {\i neder}. Rabbi Yaakov holds that the {\i hatfasah} works \u8212- the loaf is forbidden. Rabbi Yossi holds that it does not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially suggests that Rabbi Yehuda follows Rabbi Yaakov \u8212- holding that {\i hatfasah} is effective even when the original status was not imparted through words \u8212- and that Rabbi Shimon follows Rabbi Yossi. But the {\i gemara} then retracts: in fact, everyone agrees that {\i hatfasah} fundamentally requires a {\i davar hanidur}. The entire dispute between Rabbi Yaakov and Rabbi Yossi concerns only whether {\i bekhor} can be considered a {\i davar hanidur} despite the automatic nature of its sanctity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How so? The verse says {\i ki tidor neder laHashem} \u8212- when you vow a vow to God. The extra phrase {\i laHashem} is taken to extend the law to include certain cases one might not have expected. Rabbi Yaakov reads {\i laHashem} as including the {\i bekhor}: even sanctity that began automatically as belonging to God can be part of the {\i hatfasah} system. Rabbi Yossi disagrees: {\i laHashem} is needed to include {\i chatat} and {\i asham}, which one might have thought are excluded from {\i hatfasah} because they are not voluntary offerings but obligatory ones. Their sanctity was imparted through speech \u8212- one does sanctify the animal \u8212- but they are not classic {\i nedarim} in the motivational sense; one was compelled to bring them. The {\i laHashem} establishes that even obligatory offerings are part of the {\i hatfasah} world. Having used the verse for that purpose, one cannot further stretch it to include {\i bekhor}, whose sanctity was never imparted through any speech act at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yossi: No Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yossi\u8217's position is thus: the {\i bekhor} is categorically different from {\i chatat} and {\i asham}. A {\i chatat} and {\i asham}, even though they are obligatory, have their sanctity imparted through the owner\u8217's speech \u8212- one goes and sanctifies the animal. The status came through words. Therefore there is reason to include them in the {\i hatfasah} world: {\i matfisan bineder}, their status was grabbed through a {\i neder}-type act. But a {\i bekhor}: {\i ein matfiso bineder} \u8212- its status does not come through words at all. It is automatically sacred from birth. There is therefore no basis to include it in the {\i hatfasah} world even through the extension of {\i laHashem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yaakov: Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's response is that a {\i bekhor} does in fact have a dimension of status imparted through words. A {\i baraita} from the school of Rebbe teaches: from where do we know that one who has a {\i bekhor} born in his flock has a {\i mitzvah} to actively sanctify it \u8212- even though it is already automatically {\i kadosh}? From the verse {\i hazachar takdish} \u8212- the male you shall sanctify. Even though the {\i bekhor} is already sacred, there is a positive commandment to perform an act of sanctification upon it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov argues that this obligatory act of sanctification means that the {\i bekhor}\u8217's status does have a dimension that comes through words, through a speech act of {\i hakdashah}. Therefore it can participate in the {\i hatfasah} world. Rabbi Yossi\u8217's counter is that this {\i mitzvah} of sanctification is ultimately superfluous \u8212- {\i i-lo makdish lei mi lo kadosh}? \u8212- even if one does not perform this act, the {\i bekhor} is already sacred. If one\u8217's words do not actually affect the {\i bekhor}\u8217's status \u8212- since it would have been sacred regardless \u8212- then one has not imparted status through a {\i neder}, and it cannot serve as the basis for {\i hatfasah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rav offers a beautiful application of this dispute. When we recite {\i kiddush} on {\i Shabbat} \u8212- {\i zachor et yom hashabbat l\u8217'kadesho} \u8212- one might ask: does our {\i kiddush} actually add anything to {\i Shabbat}\u8217's sanctity, which exists independently of us? Following Rabbi Yossi\u8217's logic, one might say that {\i kiddush} is merely an act of acknowledgment \u8212- we are recognizing a holiness that exists without us, and our words change nothing. But following Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's logic, one might say that our act of {\i kiddush} does add a human dimension of {\i kedushah} to a {\i Shabbat} that already possesses its own {\i kedushah}. The parallel is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bekhor Debate (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimshon Mystery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that everyone agrees {\i hatfasah} requires a {\i davar hanidur}, the {\i gemara} leaves unresolved an important question: if this is indeed the rule, how does Rabbi Yehuda permit a person to become a {\i nazir Shimshon}? If Shimshon never verbally accepted {\i nezirut}, his status was not a {\i davar hanidur}, and one cannot transfer it to oneself through {\i hatfasah}. The {\i gemara} does not provide a direct answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} offers three possible resolutions. First: perhaps the verse by {\i nazir} uses the phrase {\i l\u8217'hazir laHashem}, and this {\i laHashem} extends to include {\i nazir Shimshon} \u8212- the same way some hold that {\i laHashem} by {\i neder} includes {\i bekhor}, so too it might include the {\i nezirut} of Shimshon. Second: Rabbi Yehuda may hold that Shimshon in fact did verbally accept {\i nezirut} upon himself when he came of age and became {\i bar mitzvah} \u8212- this is what the angel\u8217's instruction pointed toward, and naturally Shimshon fulfilled this obligation through an act of acceptance. Third: Shimshon\u8217's father may have made him a {\i nazir} when he was a child, as a father has the authority to do \u8212- {\i ish madir et beno b\u8217'nazir} \u8212- making the {\i nezirut} rest on a human verbal act after all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to these resolutions, everyone agrees that {\i nazir Shimshon} works by the mechanism of {\i hatfasah} \u8212- by transferring Shimshon\u8217's status \u8212- not by accepting an independent institution. The question is only how to establish that Shimshon\u8217's original status was itself a {\i davar hanidur}. The debate about Shimshon\u8217's {\i bar mitzvah} acceptance is, as one participant put it, an imaginative but halachically serious reconstruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimon HaTzadik\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refusing Asham\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} uses the phrase {\i l\u8217'hazir laHashem} in the verse for a different teaching entirely. A {\i baraita} reports: Shimon HaTzadik \u8212- from the era of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- said: {\i miyamai lo achalti asham nazir tamei} \u8212- in all my days I never ate the {\i asham} brought by a {\i nazir} who had become {\i tamei}. He would eat other {\i ashamos}, which also arise from sin \u8212- unintentional sin \u8212- and therefore could theoretically raise similar concerns. But a {\i tamei nazir} was different in his eyes. Becoming {\i tamei} during {\i nezirut} means one must start the entire process again from the beginning. The concern the {\i gemara} had expressed was that such a person might retrospectively regret having ever accepted {\i nezirut}, rendering the entire {\i nezirut} retroactively invalid \u8212- something like a {\i neder} made in error \u8212- making the animal effectively {\i chullin ba\u8217'azarah}. Therefore, Shimon HaTzadik refused to eat the {\i asham} of such a person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shepherd Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He makes one exception. A man came to him from the south, {\i yafeh einayim v\u8217'tov roi} \u8212- beautiful eyes, a fine appearance \u8212- and his hair was extraordinary, flowing in layered locks. Shimon HaTzadik asked him: my son, what moved you to become a {\i nazir}? For at the end of his {\i nezirut}, this man would have to shave off this magnificent hair. This is an interesting irony: we have encountered earlier the suggestion that a {\i nazir}\u8217's growing hair might itself be a source of attraction and pride, yet here the conclusion of {\i nezirut} demands that it all be cut off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repentance Motivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The man replied: I was a shepherd for my father in my village. I went to draw water from the well, and I saw my reflection. My {\i yetzer hara} was stirred up within me and sought to draw me down from this world into a life of self-indulgence and hedonism. I said to it: {\i reika} \u8212- empty one \u8212- why do you take such pride in a world that is not yours, a passing temporal world whose end is worms and maggots? {\i Ha\u8217'avodah} \u8212- I swear \u8212- I will shave this hair for heaven\u8217's sake. And so he became a {\i nazir} in order to redirect his life toward holiness and away from the pull of vanity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Right Motivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shimon HaTzadik stood and kissed him on his head, and said: {\i kamosecha yirbu nezirim b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- may there be many {\i nezirim} in Israel like you. And about you, the verse says: {\i ish ki yafla lindor neder nazir lehazir laHashem} \u8212- a man who makes his vow as a {\i nazir} to be separated for God\u8217's sake. The {\i laHashem} of the verse, the {\i gemara} thus teaches, is not a {\i halachic} point about the mechanism of {\i hatfasah}. It is a moral and religious point: {\i nezirut} must be truly and genuinely for God. A {\i nezirut} arising from the right motivations \u8212- redirecting one\u8217's life from the pull of the self toward dedication to the divine \u8212- is the {\i nezirut} that the verse envisions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reflects the {\i gemara}\u8217's ongoing ambivalence about {\i nezirut}. Throughout {\i Masechet Nedarim}, the {\i gemara} was deeply skeptical of {\i nedarim} \u8212- one who vows is called a {\i chotei}, a sinner. {\i Nazir} is more nuanced. On one hand, a {\i nazir} is someone striving for holiness; on the other hand, the {\i gemara} worries about wrong motivations, about separation from society, about the {\i nazir tamei} who probably regrets everything. The ending of this {\i daf} captures that ambivalence beautifully: yes, {\i yirbu nezirim b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- there should be many {\i nezirim} \u8212- but only, and precisely only, when the motivation is truly {\i laHashem}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 5\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Shimshon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimshon Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on daf 4b picks up in the middle of a discussion about {\i Nazir Shimshon} and {\i Nazir Olam}. The background question concerns whether Shimshon accepted his {\i nezirut} himself, through a {\i neder}, or whether the {\i nezirut} was externally imposed. This is halachically significant because the mechanism for one person to declare himself a {\i Nazir Shimshon} \u8212- to transfer Shimshon\u8217's status onto oneself \u8212- depends on whether Shimshon\u8217's own {\i nezirut} originated through a vow. This was the subject of a {\i tannaitic} dispute reviewed previously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Angel\u8217's Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: {\i v\u8217'Shimshon lav nazir havae} \u8212- and was Shimshon not a {\i nazir}? The question seems strange at first glance, since nobody denied that he was a {\i nazir}. The debate was specifically about whether his {\i nezirut} came about through a {\i neder}. The {\i gemara} presses: {\i v\u8217'ha-ksiv} \u8212- does the verse not say {\i ki nezir elohim yihye hana\u8217'ar min habaten}, that the child would be a {\i nazir} to God from the womb? To this, the {\i gemara} responds: {\i hasam malach d\u8217'ka-amar} \u8212- there it was only the angel saying so, not Shimshon himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fitting this into the preceding discussion, the objection would be: didn\u8217't he accept his {\i nezirut} through a {\i neder}? The angelic declaration would not demonstrate that Shimshon undertook his {\i nezirut} in the normal way. The answer is that it was only the angel\u8217's directive, not a formal vow. More broadly, though, one may read this passage as addressing a simpler version of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. Rabbi Shimon said {\i lo amar klum shelo matzinu b\u8217'Shimshon sheyatza nezirut mipiv} \u8212- he never uttered a declaration of {\i nezirut}. This can be read in two ways: either he was a {\i nazir} but not through a vow, and therefore the status cannot be transferred; or, more radically, that according to Rabbi Shimon, Shimshon never formally held the halachic status of {\i nazir} at all. The {\i gemara}\u8217's question and answer may be hinting at this second reading \u8212- the angelic announcement did not itself confer a halachic {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contact with Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues: {\i u\u8217'minah d\u8217'itmei l\u8217'meisim} \u8212- how do we know that a {\i Nazir Shimshon} carries an allowance to become {\i tamei meis}? Shimshon certainly killed many people, but how do we know he directly touched them? The {\i gemara} first suggests a proof from the verse {\i lechi hachamor hikeisi elef ish} \u8212- with the jawbone of an ass I slew a thousand men. Since he was wielding that instrument at such close range, presumably he came into contact with the bodies. The {\i gemara} pushes back: {\i dilma giruy garu beih} \u8212- perhaps he threw it at them, never touching them directly. Questions of {\i tuma b\u8217'chiburin} \u8212- {\i tumah} transmitted through an intermediate object \u8212- are at play, but if he threw the jawbone as a projectile, no direct contact need be assumed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then proposes a different verse: {\i vayach mehem shloshim ish vayikach es chalitzosam} \u8212- he smote thirty men and took their garments. If he removed their clothing after killing them, he must have touched them. The {\i gemara} responds: perhaps he stripped them first and then killed them. To this, the {\i gemara} answers {\i vayach vayikach ksiv} \u8212- the verse says \u8220"he smote and he took,\u8221" in that order: he struck them first and took their garments afterward. Yet the {\i gemara} still resists: {\i v\u8217'dilma gosesin shavinahu} \u8212- perhaps he only rendered them mortally wounded but not yet dead when he took the garments, so that he still did not touch full corpses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tradition on Contact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With all textual proofs exhausted, the {\i gemara} concludes: {\i ela fayn, gemara gemira lah} \u8212- this is simply a received tradition that Shimshon did in fact come into contact with some of those he killed. That closes the discussion of {\i Nazir Shimshon}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Absalom Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the concept of {\i Nazir Olam} and asks: {\i v\u8217'nazir olam heicha ktiv} \u8212- where in Scripture is the idea of a {\i Nazir Olam} sourced? This refers not simply to someone who chooses indefinitely to remain a {\i nazir}, but to a formal status in which the {\i nezirut} is permanently constitutive \u8212- and which paradoxically permits periodic hair-cutting. A {\i braita} answers: {\i Rabbi omer: Avshalom Nazir Olam hayah} \u8212- just as {\i Nazir Shimshon} is derived from a narrative in {\i Tanach}, so too {\i Nazir Olam} is derived from the story of Avshalom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Absalom Narrative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevant verse is {\i vayehi miketz arba\u8217'im shanah} \u8212- and it came to pass at the end of forty years \u8212- at the point when Avshalom was plotting his rebellion against David. He says to the king: {\i elchah na va\u8217'ashalem et nedarai asher nadarti l\u8217'Hashem b\u8217'Chevron} \u8212- let me go and I will fulfill my vows that I made to God in Chevron. We also know that he would cut his hair {\i echad l\u8217'shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- once every twelve months, as Rabbi derives from the phrase {\i vayehi miketz yamim l\u8217'yamim} \u8212- and it was at the end of days upon days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is being read into these {\i pesukim} is that Avshalom\u8217's declaration \u8220"let me fulfill my vows\u8221" means he needed to bring the three {\i korbanot} that normally conclude a {\i nezirut}. For a {\i Nazir Olam}, these offerings do not terminate the {\i nezirut} \u8212- they open a halachic aperture allowing the {\i nazir} to cut his hair. Thus Avshalom would periodically bring these {\i korbanot} and trim his hair, yet his {\i nezirut} persisted permanently. The phrase {\i yamim l\u8217'yamim} is understood to indicate that he did this on a yearly basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting an irony here that connects to the previous {\i sugya}. The discussion concluded with the case of a man who became a {\i nazir} for the purpose of eventually cutting his hair, and there was a contrast with the {\i mishnah} as Shmuel reads it \u8212- a person who grabs his hair and says \u8220"I will be beautiful, therefore I will be a {\i nazir}.\u8221" The question of whether the {\i nazir}\u8217's long hair represents beauty or social displacement is genuinely complex. The {\i pshat} of {\i pera} suggests the hair grows wild and unkempt, marking the {\i nazir} as outside normal society. Yet Avshalom\u8217's hair is described in {\i Tanach} precisely as something strikingly beautiful \u8212- which makes the {\i Nazir Olam} example an interesting counterpoint.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Walled City Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi derives that Avshalom\u8217's yearly hair-cutting cycle comes through a {\i gezeirah shavah} between the word {\i yamim} as it appears in the Avshalom narrative and the word {\i yamim} as it appears in the laws of houses in a walled city. The relevant {\i pasuk} there reads {\i yamim tihyeh ge\u8217'ulato} \u8212- his right of redemption shall be for {\i yamim} \u8212- and then subsequently, {\i ad melot lo shanah temimah} \u8212- until a full year has been completed. The {\i gemara} derives: {\i yamim} there means twelve months, a full year. Therefore {\i yamim l\u8217'yamim} by Avshalom also means a full year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'yalif yamim yamim mibatei arei chomah, ma hasham yud-bet chodesh, af kan yud-bet chodesh} \u8212- just as {\i yamim} there means twelve months, so too here. This is how Rabbi concludes that a {\i Nazir Olam} cuts his hair once a year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a challenge. Rabbi himself holds elsewhere that {\i yamim} in that very {\i pasuk} about the walled city means literally two days \u8212- {\i ein yamim pachus mishnayim} \u8212- so that the seller must wait at least two days before exercising his right of redemption. How then can Rabbi use {\i yamim} in that {\i pasuk} to mean a full year?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hai gezeirah shavah mishum koved gamir, uvishenei yamim leika koved} \u8212- the {\i gezeirah shavah} is established on account of the weight of the hair becoming burdensome, and the hair certainly does not become burdensome after two days. Since the {\i gezeirah shavah} for the {\i Nazir Olam} case is premised on the notion of hair growing heavy, the {\i yamim} of the {\i gezeirah shavah} must refer to a period long enough for hair to become burdensome \u8212- which brings us to the fuller reading of that {\i pasuk}, identifying {\i yamim} with a complete year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qualified Yamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that Rabbi links {\i yamim} to a full year via {\i batei arei chomah}, the {\i gemara} considers whether Avshalom\u8217's {\i yamim} could be linked to some other verse that carries {\i yamim} with a different duration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First: {\i ve\u8217'eima beis shana} \u8212- perhaps it means two years? There is a verse that reads {\i vayehi mikeitz shenasayim yamim} \u8212- and it came to pass at the end of two years of days \u8212- where {\i yamim} appears in the context of two years. The answer: {\i danin yamim she\u8217'ein imahem shanim miyamim she\u8217'ein imahem shanim} \u8212- we derive {\i yamim} (unqualified) from {\i yamim} (unqualified). The verse by Avshalom says {\i yamim l\u8217'yamim} with no attached qualifier, and the verse by {\i batei arei chomah} says {\i yamim tihyeh ge\u8217'ulato} also without a qualifier. But {\i shenasayim yamim} has the word {\i yamim} immediately juxtaposed with {\i shenasayim}, making it a qualified usage. One may not learn from a qualified {\i yamim} to an unqualified one. {\i Al yochiach zeh sheyeish imo shanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: {\i ve\u8217'eima sheloshim yom} \u8212- perhaps thirty days? There is a verse that reads {\i ad chodesh yamim} \u8212- until a month of days \u8212- where {\i yamim} appears alongside the word for month. The same answer applies: {\i danin yamim she\u8217'ein imahem chodashim miyamim she\u8217'ein imahem chodashim} \u8212- {\i yamim} without a month-qualifier is learned from {\i yamim} without a month-qualifier. {\i Ve\u8217'al yochiach zeh sheyeish imo chodashim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third: {\i ve\u8217'eima meihacha miyamim yamimah} \u8212- perhaps we should learn from the verse about the daughters of Israel who would go to commemorate the daughter of Yiftach, described as coming {\i miyamim yamimah}, four times a year. If they came four times a year, perhaps the unit of {\i yamim} represents a three-month interval? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i danin yamim miyamim ve\u8217'ein danin yamim miyamimah} \u8212- we derive {\i yamim} from {\i yamim}, not from {\i yamimah}, which is a slightly different morphological form of the word. A {\i gezeirah shavah} requires the closest available verbal match.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i umai nafka mina} \u8212- why should the morphological form matter in a {\i gezeirah shavah}? Does a {\i gezeirah shavah} require identical words? The {\i gemara} invokes the well-known {\i braita} of the school of Rabbi Yishmael regarding {\i tzara\u8217'at} in a house, where the {\i kohen} \u8220"returns\u8221" ({\i veshav}) and \u8220"comes\u8221" ({\i uva}) \u8212- two different words used to describe the same act \u8212- and the {\i gemara} uses them interchangeably: {\i zo hi shivah zo hi biah}. This seems to suggest that {\i gezeirah shavah} tolerates links of meaning even without identical words. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hani mili heicha deleika dedami leih} \u8212- that principle applies only when there is no closer match available. {\i Aval heicha de\u8217'ika dedami leih, midedami leih yalpinan} \u8212- but where there is an exact verbal match, the {\i gezeirah shavah} must follow the more precise link. Since {\i yamim} unqualified is available as a match, we do not resort to {\i yamimah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further reason not to learn from the Yiftach narrative: {\i mena yade\u8217'ah dechol tlata yarchi chad zimna} \u8212- who says the four annual visits were evenly spaced at three-month intervals? {\i Dilma arba zimnei beshata}, four times a year, but irregularly distributed \u8212- perhaps {\i arba yarchi chad zimna}, then {\i trei yarchi bechad zimna}, and so on. Since the visits need not have followed a fixed interval, one cannot extract any standard unit of time from {\i miyamim yamimah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A student raised the structural observation that the {\i braita} of Rabbi Yishmael \u8212- who is associated with the school of {\i dibra Torah k\u8217'lashon b\u8217'nei adam} and generally tends toward meaning over precise morphology \u8212- is being read here as if it were making a point about {\i gezeirah shavah} as a formal technique, when in fact that {\i braita} may not be using {\i gezeirah shavah} at all. Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's comparison of {\i shivah} and {\i biah} may simply be a recognition that two words are synonymous in context, not a {\i gezeirah shavah} in the technical sense. This touches on the broader question of what {\i gezeirah shavah} originally meant. There is an early {\i mishnah} in which Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel invoke a {\i gezeirah shavah} in the context of two rabbinic laws \u8212- possibly the {\i eiruv techumin} case \u8212- suggesting that in its earliest form, {\i gezeirah shavah} may have meant something more like a logical analogy between comparable concepts, rather than the formal technique of word-linking that automatically transfers an entire body of law from one domain to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of all of this is that Rabbi\u8217's derivation stands: Avshalom is understood to have been a {\i nazir}, periodically bringing his {\i korbanot} and trimming his hair on a yearly basis ({\i miyamim l\u8217'yamim} = once a year), and this is how Rabbi establishes that a {\i Nazir Olam} may trim his hair annually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nehorai: 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nehorai disagrees and says {\i megale\u8217'ach achas lishloshim yom} \u8212- a {\i Nazir Olam} may cut his hair once every thirty days. This is a substantial allowance: patterned after the standard unit of {\i nezirut}, after one has completed an equivalent period, one may get a trim. The basis is {\i gabei kohanim} \u8212- derived from the laws of {\i kohanim}, who may not allow their hair to become {\i gadel pera}, wild growth. {\i Mishum d\u8217'ika koved} \u8212- after thirty days, hair is considered to have become heavy and burdensome. {\i Hacha nami ika koved} \u8212- the same applies here: once the hair reaches the thirty-day threshold, it is considered burdensome and the allowance to trim kicks in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes the circularity in this reasoning: the only reason we know that the {\i kohen} prohibition of {\i gadel pera} means more than thirty days is that we learn it from the standard {\i nezirut}, where thirty days\u8217' hair growth is defined as wild. So the chain of reasoning goes: a standard {\i nazir} has thirty-day {\i nezirut}, during which the hair must be {\i gadel pera}; this means thirty days defines wild hair; a {\i kohen} may not have {\i gadel pera} hair, so thirty days is the threshold; and now we use that threshold to tell us when a {\i Nazir Olam} may trim. Tosafot suggests the reasoning may prefer {\i kohanim} as the intermediate reference rather than deriving {\i Nazir Olam} directly from ordinary {\i nezirut}, since it may be preferable not to derive a {\i Nazir Olam}\u8217's law directly from the temporary {\i nazir} ({\i Nazir Sha\u8217'ah}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One genuinely puzzling question that this raises: when a {\i Nazir Olam} receives his trim, must his hair still maintain the {\i pera} state? If he trims every thirty-one days \u8212- cutting off only the hair that grew after the previous trim \u8212- his hair would cycle in and out of the {\i pera} threshold. On day twenty-nine it would not yet qualify as {\i pera}; on day thirty or thirty-one it would; then he trims and returns below the threshold. Is the {\i Nazir Olam} then living most of his life with hair that does not qualify as {\i pera}? This seems strange, since one would expect the condition of {\i pera} to be a permanent characteristic of the {\i nazir}\u8217's state, not merely a momentary threshold he crosses before trimming back down. The {\i gemara} does not resolve this explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi: Weekly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi is more radical still: {\i megale\u8217'ach me\u8217'erev Shabbos le\u8217'erev Shabbos} \u8212- a {\i Nazir Olam} may cut his hair every {\i erev Shabbos}. The reasoning is {\i shekein matzinu bivnei melachim shemegalchin me\u8217'erev Shabbos le\u8217'erev Shabbos} \u8212- this is the grooming standard of princes, who cut their hair weekly, and since Avshalom was a prince, he presumably maintained this standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position is striking on multiple levels. First, if Avshalom trimmed his hair weekly, how did his hair become so famously long that it caught in the tree? One answer offered is that his weekly trim was minimal \u8212- perhaps only split ends \u8212- and the hair overall remained long. Second, and more fundamentally, if a {\i Nazir Olam} gets a weekly haircut, his hair may never truly reach the state of {\i pera} at all after the first month. He would look entirely presentable at all times \u8212- essentially indistinguishable from anyone else. This seems to suggest that the {\i Nazir Olam}\u8217's outward appearance is qualitatively different from that of a standard {\i nazir}, which is itself quite strange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i mai ika beinei v\u8217'sha\u8217'ar echav} \u8212- how would Avshalom look different from his non-{\i nazir} brothers, the other princes, who also cut their hair weekly? What is the practical {\i nafka mina} between Avshalom\u8217's regimen and theirs? Two answers are given. First, {\i yom tov shechal lihayos b\u8217'emtza Shabbas} \u8212- if a {\i yom tov} fell mid-week, the other princes could get haircuts for {\i kavod yom tov}, but Avshalom, being a {\i nazir}, could not cut mid-week. Second, {\i ur l\u8217'giluychi mitzafra d\u8217'ma\u8217'alei Shabata} \u8212- his brothers could cut their hair Friday morning, whereas Avshalom, who had to wait out the full permitted interval, could not cut until {\i panya}, just before sunset on Friday. Tosafot adds that this may also reflect the requirement that Avshalom could not cut without first bringing his {\i korbanot} \u8212- so the distinction between him and his brothers was visible in the timing, even if not in the length of his hair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forty Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a chronological difficulty: {\i hanei arbayim shana mai avidteihu} \u8212- what are these forty years counting from? David\u8217's entire reign lasted only thirty-seven years, so \u8220"forty years after\u8221" the beginning of David\u8217's kingship is impossible. Rav Nehorai says in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua: {\i l\u8217'ketz mem shana she\u8217'sha\u8217'alu lahem melech} \u8212- the forty years count from when the Jewish people first asked for a king from Shmuel. The rebellion of Avshalom against his father mirrors the original rebellion of {\i Bnei Yisrael} against divine sovereignty when they demanded a human king \u8212- an act that Shmuel regarded as a rejection of God\u8217's kingship. Forty years later, Avshalom rebels against his father\u8217's earthly kingship. A {\i tanna} adds: {\i osa shana she\u8217'sha\u8217'alu lahem melech hi shenas eser l\u8217'Shmuel HaRamasi} \u8212- the year of that request was the tenth year of Shmuel\u8217's career, a chronological anchor that explains how all the dates in the narrative align.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Standard 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states simply: {\i stam nezirut sheloshim yom} \u8212- standard, unexplicated {\i nezirut} lasts thirty days. If a person says \u8220"I am a {\i nazir}\u8221" without specifying a duration, the assumed period is thirty days, after which he brings his {\i korbanot}. We return here to ordinary {\i nezirut}, setting aside the special cases of {\i Nazir Shimshon} and {\i Nazir Olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gematria Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mani milei} \u8212- what is the source for thirty days? Rav Matna derives it from the verse {\i kadosh yihiyeh, gadel pera se\u8217'ar rosho} \u8212- holy shall it be, the growing wild of his hair. The word {\i yihiyeh} in {\i gematria}: {\i yud} (10) + {\i heh} (5) + {\i yud} (10) + {\i heh} (5) = 30. {\i Yihiyeh be-gematria tlasin havu} \u8212- {\i yihiyeh} in {\i gematria} equals thirty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this is a case where the {\i gemara} deploys {\i gematria} in service of an actual {\i halacha}. It seems fairly clear, however \u8212- given that Bar Pada immediately offers an entirely different derivation \u8212- that both authorities likely began with a received tradition that {\i stam nezirut} is thirty days, and each then sought to connect that tradition back to the {\i pesukim}. When everyone assumes a law and the debate is only about which verse supports it, that pattern typically indicates a tradition in hand rather than a derivation in process. This dynamic recurs frequently in the {\i sugyot} of {\i Chazal}: sometimes they derive the law from the verse, and sometimes they start with the tradition and find the verse afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pada Counts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Pada offers a different derivation: {\i k\u8217'neged nazir neziru ha\u8217'amurim ba\u8217'torah lamed chasar achas} \u8212- count the occurrences of the words {\i nazir} and {\i neziru} in the Torah and you will find twenty-nine. Interesting: if one actually counts all occurrences of {\i nazir} and related forms in the Torah, the total comes to thirty. Bar Pada\u8217's position is that one occurrence must be excluded \u8212- either because it is needed to introduce the concept and cannot count, or because, as the {\i rishonim} suggest, the phrase {\i ki nezer elohav al rosho} uses {\i nezer} to mean a crown rather than {\i nezirut}, and therefore does not count toward the tally. This leaves twenty-nine \u8212- and that, as we shall see, leads Bar Pada to conclude that {\i stam nezirut} is in principle twenty-nine days, not thirty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Word Usage Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges: why does Rav Matna not use Bar Pada\u8217's counting method? Rav Matna replies: {\i hahu ledrasha} \u8212- each extra occurrence of the word {\i nazir} in the Torah is there to teach a specific law. As we have seen, for example, {\i miyayin veshechar yazir} with its doubled form teaches {\i le\u8217'esor yayin mitzvah keyayin reshut} \u8212- that even wine consumed in fulfillment of a {\i mitzvah} is prohibited to the {\i nazir}; and the doubled expression {\i ki yafuli lindor neder Nazir lehazir l\u8217'Hashem} teaches {\i melamed she\u8217'nezirut chala al nezirut} \u8212- that one {\i nezirut} can take effect while another is still running, hovering in abeyance until the first concludes. Since every occurrence is pressed into service for an independent law, Rav Matna cannot simply count them up as a calendar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pada Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Pada responds: {\i leit chad min\u8217'hon d\u8217'lav ledrasha} \u8212- there is not a single occurrence that does not teach something. His point is that even those occurrences used for counting are not simply superfluous; rather, their very presence is meaningful. Since it is impossible that all twenty-nine (or thirty) instances teach independent {\i drashot} \u8212- there are not that many independent laws of {\i nezirut} to be derived \u8212- at least some of them must be there for counting purposes. {\i Ela mide\u8217'hahu leminyana, kulhu nami leminyana} \u8212- since one is established as being for counting, all may be treated as counting toward the total. The ones that do teach specific laws serve a dual function: they teach the law and they count. The key principle is that once any of the occurrences cannot be assigned an independent {\i drasha}, it establishes that the function of enumeration is operative across all of them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matna vs.\u160 ?Pada (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matna: 30 True\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the two methods of derivation, the {\i gemara} now draws out their substantive legal disagreement. For Rav Matna, the true duration of {\i stam nezirut} is thirty days. The {\i korbanot} ending the {\i nezirut} are brought on day thirty-one, after the thirty days are complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pada: 29 True\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Bar Pada, the true duration is twenty-nine days in principle. However, since the {\i korbanot} cannot be brought at night, and one reaches the end of day twenty-nine at nightfall, one must wait until day thirty to bring the {\i korbanot} in practice. So the {\i nezirut} ends functionally on day thirty \u8212- but this is merely a practical constraint, not the halachically defined duration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now tests these positions against the {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} states {\i stam nezirut sheloshim yom} \u8212- thirty days. For Rav Matna this is straightforward. For Bar Pada it is a difficulty: he says twenty-nine. Bar Pada responds: {\i idi ve\u8217'idi yom sheloshim d\u8217'megaleach umeiti korbanotav nami} \u8212- in all cases, it is on day thirty that one shaves and brings {\i korbanot}, because one can never end it before day thirty even on the twenty-nine-day theory. Hence the {\i mishnah} says \u8220"thirty days\u8221" as the practical day of completion, even though the underlying duration is twenty-nine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} next examines the {\i mishnah}: {\i mi she\u8217'amar hareini Nazir megaleach yom sheloshim ve\u8217'echad} \u8212- one who simply says \u8220"I am a {\i nazir}\u8221" cuts his hair on day thirty-one. For Rav Matna this is straightforward: thirty-day duration, {\i korbanot} on day thirty-one. For Bar Pada it is a difficulty: he should be able to bring his {\i korbanot} on day thirty. Bar Pada responds: {\i eima seifa} \u8212- look at the end of that {\i mishnah}: {\i im gilach beyom sheloshim yatza} \u8212- if he cut on day thirty, he has fulfilled his obligation {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. Bar Pada can explain this: the {\i nezirut} was twenty-nine days, and day thirty is the proper day of completion. The {\i seifa} is actually in his favor. The {\i reisha}, which says to wait until day thirty-one {\i lechatchila}, Bar Pada explains as a precautionary measure: {\i na\u8217'aseh ke\u8217'omer shleimim} \u8212- we are concerned that one may have said \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} for a full month,\u8221" in which case full thirty days are required. Since the difference between twenty-nine days and a month is not substantial in people\u8217's minds, we require everyone to wait until day thirty-one {\i lechatchila} to avoid confusion. In principle, for {\i stam nezirut}, day thirty is the correct and sufficient day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matna Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rav Matna, the {\i reisha} is straightforward \u8212- thirty days, bring {\i korbanot} on day thirty-one \u8212- but the {\i seifa} is the difficulty. How does day thirty work {\i bedi\u8217'avad} if the duration is thirty days? {\i Kasavar miktzas hayom kekulo} \u8212- Rav Matna holds the principle that part of a day counts as the whole day. One need not complete all of day thirty; having entered day thirty, the thirty-day requirement is satisfied, and the {\i korbanot} may be brought. The reason we nonetheless say {\i lechatchila} to wait until day thirty-one is that we prefer not to rely on {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} as a first resort. But {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, bringing the {\i korbanot} on day thirty itself is valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Day Princip (III)le\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Principle Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} \u8212- that part of a day counts as the whole \u8212- is familiar from other areas of {\i halacha}. In {\i aveilus}, the seven days of mourning: one rises at the beginning of the seventh day, with that partial day counting as the full seventh. Similarly, a {\i zavah} who counts seven clean days may immerse at the beginning of the seventh. Importantly, this principle does not always operate as a {\i lechatchila} standard; in some contexts, such as {\i niddah}, one must wait until the full end of the seventh day. Whether and when one may rely on {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} is itself context-dependent, which is part of why Rav Matna treats it as a {\i bedi\u8217'avad} resource rather than a first-choice option.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matna vs.\u160 ?Pada (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consecutive Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines back-to-back {\i neziriyot}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i mi shenazar shtei neziriyot megaleach et harishona yom shloshim ve\u8217'echad vehashniya leyom shishim ve\u8217'echad} \u8212- one who takes on two consecutive {\i neziriyot} cuts for the first on day thirty-one and for the second on day sixty-one. The same questions arise. For Rav Matna: straightforward, thirty-day duration each, so day thirty-one ends the first and day sixty-one ends the second. For Bar Pada: why wait until day thirty-one for each? On his view the {\i nezirut} is twenty-nine days.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Day Princip (III)le\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Days Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then examines the {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement: {\i hareini nazir shloshim yom} \u8212- if one explicitly says \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} for thirty days\u8221" and then cuts on day thirty, {\i lo yatza} \u8212- one has not fulfilled the obligation. This holds for both opinions. For Bar Pada, who does not invoke {\i miktzat hayom kekulo}, this is simple: once one has specified full days, the full count must be satisfied. For Rav Matna, the question is sharper: why doesn\u8217't {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} apply here? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i be\u8217'omer shleimim} \u8212- because one said \u8220"full days.\u8221" When one explicitly articulates a full thirty-day {\i nezirut}, the {\i miktzat hayom} principle is preempted; the statement of intent requires complete days, not a partial final day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 30 Hinge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of back-to-back {\i neziriyot}, day thirty-one ends the first and begins the second. But if one went ahead and ended the first {\i nezirut} on day thirty \u8212- either through {\i miktzat hayom} (Rav Matna) or because it was a twenty-nine-day {\i nezirut} (Bar Pada) \u8212- then day thirty itself serves as the pivot: it ends the first and begins the second. Accordingly, the second {\i nezirut} started on day thirty, and adding thirty days (with the same {\i lechatchila} logic) brings one to day sixty for the second shaving. This is exactly what the {\i mishnah} says: {\i ve\u8217'im gilach et harishona leyom shloshim, megaleach et hashniya leyom shishim} \u8212- if one shaved for the first on day thirty, one shaves for the second on day sixty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matna vs.\u160 ?Pada (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pada Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to Bar Pada\u8217's resolution of the back-to-back case: the {\i seifa} of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- that shaving the first on day thirty means shaving the second on day sixty \u8212- works for Bar Pada because on his view the first {\i nezirut} was twenty-nine days, properly concluded on day thirty, and the second likewise. The {\i reisha}, which says to wait until day thirty-one and day sixty-one {\i lechatchila}, Bar Pada again explains as {\i be\u8217'omer shleimim} \u8212- or as a precautionary measure to account for cases in which one has articulated a full month, so that everyone is asked {\i lechatchila} to wait the extra day in order to avoid confusion between {\i stam nezirut} and an explicitly month-long {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Day Princip (III)le\u8230 ?\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double-Dip Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rav Matna, the {\i seifa} of the back-to-back {\i mishnah} requires that {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} operate in two directions simultaneously on day thirty. On Rav Matna\u8217's view, the first {\i nezirut} was thirty days; ending it on day thirty requires invoking {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} \u8212- a portion of day thirty counts as the full thirtieth day of the first {\i nezirut}. But then day thirty also counts as day one of the second {\i nezirut}, which in turn requires invoking {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} in the other direction \u8212- that entering day thirty is treated as a full first day of the new {\i nezirut}. The {\i gemara} presses: can we truly double-dip in this way?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i mahu detaima, hani mili le\u8217'inyan chada nezirut, aval lishtei nezirut lo} \u8212- one might have thought that {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} can be invoked for one {\i nezirut} but not to simultaneously serve two {\i neziriyot}. If the logic of the principle is that we treat the portion of day thirty as if it were the full final day of the first {\i nezirut}, then that day is, conceptually, fully consumed by the first. How can that same partial day also be treated as a fresh opening day of the second? The {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution is that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's explicit ruling \u8212- {\i yom shloshim oleh lekan ulekan}, day thirty counts in both directions \u8212- establishes this as a genuine {\i chiddush}: the same partial day can simultaneously close one {\i nezirut} and open another, applying {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} in both directions at once.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 6\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Masna vs Bar Pada\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 30 vs 29 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 6a, picking up a dispute introduced in prior sessions. {\i Rav Masna} and {\i Bar Pada} disagree about the fundamental structure of {\i stam nezirus} \u8212- standard, unspecified {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Masna} holds that {\i nezirut} is, in principle, thirty days. Since a series of {\i mishnayot} makes clear that a {\i nazir} may bring his {\i korban} on day thirty \u8212- thereby completing his {\i nezirut} on that day \u8212- {\i Rav Masna} must account for this by invoking the principle of {\i miktzas hayom kekulo}: a partial day counts as the whole. Just as a mourner rises from {\i aveilus} on the morning of the seventh day without completing a full seventh day, so too the {\i nazir} who enters day thirty has, in principle, satisfied his thirty-day obligation. This also explains why the {\i mishna} specifies that {\i lechatchila} \u8212- as the preferred initial practice \u8212- one should wait until day thirty-one to bring the {\i korban}. If {\i miktzas hayom kekulo} applies, there is still some practical reason to wait, which will become clearer as the discussion unfolds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bar Pada}, by contrast, rejects {\i miktzas hayom kekulo} entirely. In his view, {\i stam nezirus} means twenty-nine days \u8212- not thirty \u8212- but twenty-nine {\i complete} days, with no partial-day principle at play. The {\i nazir} brings his {\i korban} on day thirty as a matter of course, because by that point twenty-nine full days have already elapsed. Why {\i lechatchila} one should still wait until day thirty-one on his account is less clear \u8212- perhaps to accommodate cases where the {\i nazir} explicitly stipulated thirty days \u8212- but his fundamental position is twenty-nine full days, no {\i miktzas hayom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miktzas Hayom Kekulo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what precisely is the conceptual basis for {\i miktzas hayom kekulo}, and when does it apply? There is a related but distinct principle, {\i rubo kekulo} \u8212- the majority counts as the whole \u8212- but these are not equivalent. The principle of {\i rubo kekulo} operates when the goal is to achieve a particular state: for instance, in {\i shechita}, one need not sever the trachea and esophagus entirely; cutting the majority of each is sufficient, because the required state \u8212- severance \u8212- is considered achieved. Similarly, in {\i eiruv}, a wall need not be continuous along the entire perimeter; as long as the majority is enclosed, the area is considered walled off. In each case, what matters is achieving a recognized condition, not completing a precise physical act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Miktzas hayom kekulo} is better understood as a principle specific to durations \u8212- periods of time framed as units rather than as counts. When one asks whether a person \u8220"completed a week\u8221" or \u8220"completed a month,\u8221" the focus is on the conceptual unit, not on the literal number of hours. Once a person has entered the final day of that unit, one can say that the unit has, in a meaningful sense, been achieved \u8212- because the unit, not its constituent days, is what matters. This is why the principle is most naturally applied to things like mourning periods, {\i nezirut}, or any framework structured around temporal units rather than discrete counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rubo Kekulo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction has real {\i halachic} consequences. {\i Rubo kekulo} and {\i miktzas hayom kekulo} each have their domain. When a goal is to achieve a state, the majority suffices. When a goal is to complete a duration understood as a unit, entering the final day may suffice. But where one is literally counting discrete actions \u8212- say, eating a {\i kezayis} or completing all eighteen blessings of {\i shemoneh esrei} \u8212- neither principle straightforwardly applies. The appropriate application of each principle depends on how one analyzes the underlying obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Consecutive Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 30 Double Count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now cites a {\i mishna} with a striking implication. The case involves a person who has accepted two consecutive {\i neziruyot}, one immediately following the other. The rule is that {\i lechatchila} one should bring the {\i korban} for the first {\i nezirut} on day thirty-one. If, however, one did so {\i bedieved} \u8212- after the fact \u8212- on day thirty, that too is valid. Day thirty then becomes day one of the second {\i nezirut}, so the second {\i korban} should {\i lechatchila} be brought on day sixty \u8212- which is day thirty-one counting from that new starting point. And if {\i bedieved} one brought the second {\i korban} on day fifty-nine, that is also valid, for the same reason: day fifty-nine of the combined count is day thirty of the second {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical principle the {\i mishna} articulates is {\i oleh lakan ulakan} \u8212- that day thirty counts toward both the ending period and the beginning period. The arithmetic makes the point starkly: two units of thirty days should require sixty days, yet the {\i mishna} validates completion in fifty-nine. The only way to reconcile this is if the middle day \u8212- the day of the {\i korban} \u8212- is counted in both directions simultaneously. This is the {\i chidush} implicit in {\i oleh lakan ulakan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Pada\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i bishlama} according to {\i Rav Masna}, this makes sense. His position is that {\i nezirut} is fundamentally thirty days, and {\i miktzas hayom kekulo} allows day thirty to function as the completion of the first unit and \u8212- simultaneously \u8212- the beginning of the next. One can say the partial day counted as a full thirtieth day for the first {\i nezirut}, and also as day one for the second. But according to {\i Bar Pada}, what is the {\i chidush}? If {\i nezirut} is twenty-nine full days, then by the end of day twenty-nine the obligation is already complete. Of course day thirty will work \u8212- there is nothing surprising about it counting toward the next {\i nezirut}, because it was never required for the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oleh Lakan Ulakan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bar Pada} responds: the {\i mishna}\u8217's language of {\i oleh lakan ulakan} is actually where he derives his position. The reason day thirty can count toward the beginning of the next period is precisely because it was not needed for the previous one. This is, admittedly, a somewhat counterintuitive reading of the phrase \u8212- {\i oleh lakan ulakan} sounds like double-counting in both directions, yet {\i Bar Pada} reads it as conveying the opposite: that the day is available for the next period because it was never part of the prior one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From {\i Rav Masna}\u8217's perspective, the {\i chidush} of {\i miktzas hayom kekulo} is that bringing the {\i korban} midway through day thirty counts as completing the first {\i nezirut} and simultaneously initiates the second \u8212- a double application of the principle in a single day. {\i Bar Pada} finds this conceptually untenable. How can one day be counted twice? His answer is that the {\i mishna} is teaching something different: the principle is that {\i nezirut} is truly only twenty-nine days, and therefore there is no double-counting. Day thirty was never in play for the first unit, and it simply initiates the next.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that {\i Tosafot} here \u8212- whose commentary on this {\i daf} is unusually extensive and reads more like sustained running exposition than the familiar dialectical give-and-take \u8212- takes up much of the analytical burden that the {\i Ran} typically bears in {\i Nedarim}, precisely because the standard {\i Rashi} on {\i Nedarim} is not authentic {\i Rashi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} at this point does not resolve the dispute by ruling definitively. The {\i gemara}\u8217's primary goal is to explore and articulate each position rather than always to reach a {\i halachic} conclusion, and both positions remain alive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumah on Day 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Soter Hakol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now cites another {\i mishna}: if someone said {\i hareini nazir} and became {\i tamei} on day thirty, {\i soter et hakol} \u8212- the entire {\i nezirut} is undermined and must be restarted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Rav Masna} this is immediately intelligible. The {\i nazir} may bring his {\i korban} on day thirty by virtue of {\i miktzas hayom kekulo}, but that same principle carries a vulnerability: if something happens later in day thirty that is fundamentally incompatible with {\i nezirut}, we cannot retroactively affirm that the partial day counted as a complete and valid thirtieth day. Drinking wine or getting a haircut after the {\i korban} is brought does not undo anything \u8212- those are acts made permissible by the {\i korban} itself. But {\i tumah} is categorically different: it does not merely violate the {\i nazir}\u8217's restrictions; it retroactively impugns the integrity of the entire period, requiring him to start over. Since we relied on {\i miktzas hayom kekulo} to say the beginning of day thirty counted as a complete thirtieth day, we can only maintain that position if the remainder of the day is consistent with it. If {\i tumah} occurred later that day, we must step back and say: we cannot call that a {\i yom tahara} \u8212- a day of purity \u8212- and therefore cannot count it as the thirtieth day at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Negation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking idea: the {\i halachic} status of the beginning of the day is, in a sense, conditional on what unfolds in the remainder. When we say {\i miktzas hayom kekulo}, we are making a legal determination that the partial day counts as the whole \u8212- but that determination is open to reassessment if subsequent events within that day fundamentally contradict the status we assigned it. The {\i korban} does not lock in the result irrevocably; what happens for the rest of day thirty can redefine what the beginning of that day meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A useful analogy: in {\i shechita}, if one cuts the majority of the trachea and esophagus and then, in attempting to complete the cut, performs the remaining portion in a disqualifying manner, there is a serious opinion that the entire {\i shechita} is invalidated. One could have stopped while ahead \u8212- the majority was sufficient \u8212- but once the problematic cut was made in the remaining portion, it retroactively undermined the whole. The same logic applies here: once the day has run its full course and {\i tumah} occurred within it, we cannot sustain the fiction that the earlier portion of the day was equivalent to a complete, untainted thirtieth day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zava Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An illuminating parallel comes from the laws of {\i zava}. A woman who experiences three consecutive days of non-menstrual bleeding must count seven consecutive clean days. On the seventh day, she need not wait until nightfall \u8212- she may immerse in the {\i mikvah} during the day itself, invoking a form of {\i miktzas hayom kekulo}. However, if she immerses during day seven, then has relations with her husband, and then bleeds again before nightfall, the entire count is retroactively undermined \u8212- she was always in the status of {\i zava}, the immersion was void, and she must begin a new seven-day count. This is precisely why {\i halacha} prescribes that even after immersing on day seven, she should wait until nightfall before resuming marital relations \u8212- or better yet, wait to immerse at all until nightfall \u8212- so as not to create the conditions for this retroactive collapse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechatchila Day 31\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is exactly the reason why {\i lechatchila} one should wait until day thirty-one to bring the {\i korban}. If the {\i korban} is brought on day thirty and the {\i nazir} then becomes {\i tamei} before nightfall, the entire {\i nezirut} is invalidated \u8212- every day of it \u8212- because day thirty cannot be counted as a valid conclusion. By waiting until day thirty-one, the {\i nazir} ensures that even if {\i tumah} occurs on that day, it falls entirely outside the completed thirty-day unit, which by then is unambiguously in the past. The {\i lechatchila} is thus a safeguard: it protects against a scenario in which {\i tumah} on the final day would force a complete restart.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 7 vs 30 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues by examining the end of the {\i mishna}, where {\i Rabbi Eliezer} disagrees with the ruling that {\i tumah} on day thirty undermines everything: {\i Rabbi Eliezer} says it undermines only seven days. This makes sense if {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds that day thirty is already beyond the completed {\i nezirut} \u8212- whatever happens there cannot retroactively erase what preceded. But now the {\i gemara} presents a second case in the same {\i mishna}: a person explicitly vowed a hundred days of {\i nezirut} and became {\i tamei} on day 100. Here {\i Rabbi Eliezer} says the {\i tumah} undermines thirty days, not seven and not a hundred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is deeply puzzling. According to {\i Rav Masna}\u8217's framework, {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds {\i miktzas hayom kekulo} in its most absolute form \u8212- an iron wall descends at the moment the partial day begins, making everything that follows completely irrelevant. On that account, {\i tumah} on day 100 should undermine only seven days, not thirty. And according to {\i Bar Pada}\u8217's framework, {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds there is no {\i miktzas hayom kekulo} at all \u8212- which would mean that on day 100 of an explicitly stated hundred-day {\i nezirut}, the {\i nazir} has not yet completed his obligation, so {\i tumah} would require him to do the entire hundred days over again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Neither framework produces thirty days. {\i Rabbi Eliezer} seems to contradict himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Pada Sides Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bar Pada} now clarifies his position. He states that regarding the earlier {\i mishna} \u8212- the standard thirty-day case where {\i Rabbi Eliezer} said only seven days need be redone \u8212- he concedes the {\i chachamim}\u8217's theoretical framework. The {\i chachamim} hold that thirty days are required and debate with {\i Rabbi Eliezer} only over how to conceptualize {\i miktzas hayom kekulo}: whether the partial day leaves the remainder of the day still consequential (the {\i chachamim}\u8217's view) or closes everything off completely (what {\i Rav Masna} attributes to {\i Rabbi Eliezer}). {\i Bar Pada} concedes that the {\i chachamim}\u8217's position on thirty days is internally coherent, but aligns himself with {\i Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- whom he understands as holding twenty-nine full days and no {\i miktzas hayom kekulo}. On that account, when someone becomes {\i tamei} on day thirty of a standard {\i nezirut}, it is already past the completion point, so {\i tumah} there cannot impinge on the main unit \u8212- hence only seven days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this still leaves {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's ruling of thirty days in the hundred-day case unexplained, on any account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Last Day Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Reish Lakish} provides the answer: there is a special {\i halacha} \u8212- not derived from logical extension of either {\i miktzas hayom} or the twenty-nine-day principle, but from a specific {\i drashah} on a {\i pasuk} \u8212- that applies uniquely when one becomes {\i tamei} on the very last day of a {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Verse Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Reish Lakish} cites the verse: {\i zot tihiyeh torat ha-Nazir b\u8217'yom melot yemei nizro} \u8212- \u8220"this shall be the law of the {\i nazir} on the day that the days of his {\i nezirut} are completed.\u8221" The {\i Torah} is teaching: if one becomes {\i tamei} specifically on the {\i yom melot} \u8212- the final day of the {\i nezirut} \u8212- then {\i ten lo torat nazir}, give him the law of the {\i nazir}, meaning a standard {\i nezirut} of thirty days. This is not derived from any general principle about {\i miktzas hayom}; it is a discrete {\i halacha} extracted from the verse. If you become {\i tamei} on your final day, whatever the duration of your {\i nezirut}, you do not redo the entire {\i nezirut} \u8212- you do a standard one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's ruling of thirty days in the hundred-day case. A hundred days were explicitly stipulated; {\i miktzas hayom} is irrelevant for {\i Bar Pada} who rejects it. The {\i nazir} became {\i tamei} on day 100 \u8212- the final day. The verse\u8217's special {\i halacha} kicks in: he does a standard {\i nezirut} of thirty days, not a hundred. And in the earlier case \u8212- {\i tumah} on day thirty of a standard {\i nezirut} \u8212- according to {\i Bar Pada}, day thirty is already past the twenty-nine-day completion point, so day thirty is not the \u8220"last day\u8221" in the relevant sense; day twenty-nine was. Therefore the special {\i halacha} of {\i tumah} on the last day does not apply, and {\i tumah} on day thirty is simply {\i tumah} that occurs after the {\i nezirut} concluded, requiring only the standard seven-day purification process. {\i Reish Lakish} concedes there is no logical derivation for the thirty-day ruling; it is a {\i halacha} extracted from the {\i Torah}\u8217's formulation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Melot Hayamim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yoshiya vs Yonatan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks whether the dispute of {\i Rav Masna} and {\i Bar Pada} mirrors a prior {\i tannaitic} debate, cited in a {\i braita}. The relevant verse reads: {\i ad melot hayamim asher yazir l\u8217'Adonai\u8230 ? kadosh yihye gadel pera sear rosho} \u8212- \u8220"until the fullness of the days that he consecrated to God\u8230 ? he shall be holy; he shall let the hair of his head grow wild.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} derives from this that there is no {\i gidul pera} \u8212- the required hair growth that is the hallmark of {\i nezirut} \u8212- in fewer than thirty days: {\i ein gidul pera sear pachos mishloshim}. His proof-text is the phrase {\i kadosh yihye gadel pera} \u8212- specifically the word {\i yihye}, whose {\i gematria} is thirty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yonatan} says the thirty-day requirement needs no derivation from {\i yihye}; it follows from the phrase {\i ad melot hayamim} itself. The concept of \u8220"days that need to be filled\u8221" implies a unit that can be either full or less than full \u8212- and the only temporal unit that admits of such variability is a month. A week is always seven days; it admits no partial version. A year is counted by months, not by days (as the {\i Rabbanan of Kisari} will clarify below), so a year does not yield a number of days in the relevant sense. A month, however, can be twenty-nine or thirty days \u8212- it can be full or deficient \u8212- and the notion of \u8220"filling\u8221" days points precisely to that structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gematria Yihye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gematria} of {\i yihye} \u8212- {\i yud} (10), {\i heh} (5), {\i yud} (10), {\i heh} (5) \u8212- equals thirty. {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} uses this {\i gematria} to establish the thirty-day minimum for {\i nezirut} from the word {\i yihye} in the verse. This is the hermeneutic {\i Rav Masna} is associated with in the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Masna\u8217's Unity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Masna} argues that the two {\i tannaitic} positions \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} and {\i Rabbi Yonatan} \u8212- are not actually in dispute about the number of days. Both agree that thirty days are required. Their disagreement concerns the interpretive question of {\i ad v\u8217'ad bichlal}: does the word {\i ad} (until) mean \u8220"up to and including\u8221" or \u8220"up to but not including\u8221"? {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} reads {\i ad} as exclusive \u8212- \u8220"until but not including\u8221" \u8212- and therefore cannot derive thirty days from {\i ad melot hayamim} alone; he supplements with the {\i gematria} of {\i yihye} to reach thirty. {\i Rabbi Yonatan} reads {\i ad} as inclusive \u8212- \u8220"until and including\u8221" \u8212- and therefore {\i ad melot hayamim} by itself implies thirty full days, the full month inclusive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, {\i Rav Masna} can claim that both {\i tannaim} support his view: everyone agrees that thirty days are required. The interpretive dispute about {\i ad} is purely internal to the verse and does not affect the substantive ruling. This is the stronger posture for an {\i amora}: demonstrating that one\u8217's position is consistent with all {\i tannaitic} opinions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ad Inclusivity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bar Pada}\u8217's position, by contrast, requires conceding that {\i Rabbi Yoshiya}\u8217's view does not support him. {\i Bar Pada} can align himself only with {\i Rabbi Yonatan}, and specifically with a particular reading of {\i Rabbi Yonatan}: that {\i ad melot hayamim} means \u8220"until the fullness of the days,\u8221" with {\i ad} understood as exclusive \u8212- up to but {\i not} including the thirtieth day. On that reading, the verse points to twenty-nine days. This is, admittedly, a somewhat strained reading of {\i Rabbi Yonatan}, since {\i Rabbi Yonatan}\u8217's own stated logic seems to lead to thirty days, but {\i Bar Pada} exploits the ambiguity in the word {\i ad} to extract a twenty-nine-day reading from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Kisari\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why not interpret {\i melot hayamim} as referring to a year rather than a month? A year also admits of variability \u8212- Hebrew years can be shorter or longer depending on whether months are full or deficient and whether an extra month is intercalated. The {\i gemara} answers by citing the {\i Rabbanan of Kisari}, who teach: {\i minayin she\u8217'ein monin yamin leshanim?} \u8212- how do we know that years are not counted by days? From the verse {\i lechodeshei hashana} \u8212- \u8220"for the months of the year.\u8221" Years are reckoned by months, not by days. Since {\i melot hayamim} invokes the counting of {\i yamim} \u8212- days \u8212- it cannot refer to a unit measured by months. Only a month, whose duration is itself measured in days and which can genuinely be full (thirty days) or deficient (twenty-nine), fits the category of a unit whose \u8220"days need to be filled.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Indefinite Expressions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Big/Small Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i mishna} now establishes the practical scope of {\i stam nezirut}. If someone says {\i hareini nazir achat gedola} \u8212- \u8220"behold I am a {\i nazir}, a single big {\i nezirut}\u8221" \u8212- or {\i hareini nazir achat ketana} \u8212- \u8220"a single small {\i nezirut}\u8221" \u8212- he is a {\i nazir} for thirty days. The qualifiers \u8220"big\u8221" and \u8220"small\u8221" are meaningless without a specified duration. A person who says \u8220"big\u8221" or \u8220"small\u8221" has still said only that he is a {\i nazir}; and {\i stam nezirut} is thirty days. Since no number was given, no number can be derived. \u8220"Big\u8221" might mean it will feel big, or require great effort \u8212- but it does not communicate a time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distance Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More striking is the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling: even if someone says {\i hareini nazir mikan ad sof ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- \u8220"behold I am a {\i nazir} from here to the end of the world\u8221" \u8212- he is nonetheless a {\i nazir} for thirty days. The expression {\i ad sof ha\u8217'olam} here is locative, not temporal \u8212- it describes a physical distance (\u8220"from here to China,\u8221" as it were), not a duration of time. Since {\i nezirut} is measured in days, not in distance, distance language does not translate into an extended {\i nezirut}. The {\i gemara} explains: when someone says this, the intent is hyperbolic \u8212- this {\i nezirut} is going to feel as long as the journey from here to the end of the world. But since no time duration was specified, only the default {\i stam nezirut} of thirty days applies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna-Braita Reconcilia\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Distance-Time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita}, however, seems to rule differently. If someone says {\i hareini nazir mikan ve\u8217'ad makom ploni} \u8212- \u8220"behold I am a {\i nazir} from here until such-and-such place\u8221" \u8212- we calculate how many days it takes to travel from here to that place. If the travel time is less than thirty days, he is a {\i nazir} for thirty days (since {\i nezirut} cannot be less than thirty). If the travel time is more than thirty days, he is a {\i nazir} for the actual number of travel days. Here, distance is explicitly being translated into time duration, in apparent contradiction to the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava on Journey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} resolves the contradiction: the {\i braita} is discussing a case where the person {\i hechzik baderech} \u8212- has already set out on the journey. Once he is already traveling, saying \u8220"from here until that place\u8221" naturally expresses duration rather than mere spatial distance. When you are on the road and say \u8220"until we get there,\u8221" you are measuring in time \u8212- thinking about how long until we arrive, not how many miles remain. In that context, translating distance into travel time is perfectly natural. But if a person is sitting at home and says he will be a {\i nazir} from here to the end of the world, there is no journey underway, no natural conversion of distance to duration, and the expression is best understood as rhetorical emphasis, not as a specification of time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} adds a further nuance: when one is on a journey, the vow may carry an element of seeking divine protection for the duration of the travel, which further explains why distance would be understood as defining the time frame of the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miles Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: even in the {\i mishna}\u8217's case, where the person is not on a journey, could the distance at least yield a number \u8212- and could that number then indicate {\i how many} sequential {\i neziruyot} the person has accepted? After all, if the world is a certain number of {\i parsa\u8217'ot} across, then \u8220"from here to the end of the world\u8221" might mean that many sequential units of thirty-day {\i nezirut}. The same question applies to {\i Rava}\u8217's case: why not say that each {\i parsa} of the journey represents one separate {\i nezirut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa No Units\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} answers: {\i be-atra de-lo manu parsei} \u8212- in a locale where distances are not measured in {\i parsa\u8217'ot}. Without a fixed, conventional unit of measurement, no determinate number emerges from the distance expression. \u8220"From here to that place\u8221" does not yield a number if there is no standard way of measuring the intervening distance. And since no number can be extracted, no count of sequential {\i neziruyot} can be established. The same logic disposes of the question of counting by overnight lodgings along the route: even if one tried to count nightly stops, that still runs into the underlying problem that the vow was framed in spatial terms and there is no fixed unit that converts cleanly into a number of {\i neziruyot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Countable vs Uncountable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Olam Forms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final {\i mishna} presents cases that at first appear similar but yield dramatically different results. If someone says {\i hareini nazir ke-afar ha-aretz} \u8212- \u8220"like the dust of the earth\u8221" \u8212- or {\i ke-sear roshi} \u8212- \u8220"like the hairs of my head\u8221" \u8212- or {\i ke-chol ha-yam} \u8212- \u8220"like the sand of the sea\u8221" \u8212- he is a {\i nazir olam}, a {\i nazir} forever, and cuts his hair every thirty days. The mechanism here is conceptually distinct from a single extended {\i nezirut}: the person has accepted upon himself an effectively infinite sequence of sequential thirty-day {\i neziruyot}, each terminating with a {\i korban} and immediately followed by the next. In practice he will be a {\i nazir} for his entire life, bringing a {\i korban} at the end of each thirty-day unit and immediately beginning the next.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question: why does \u8220"like the dust of the earth\u8221" \u8212- clearly a spatial or quantity reference \u8212- translate into an infinite number of sequential {\i neziruyot}, while \u8220"from here to the end of the world\u8221" \u8212- also a spatial reference \u8212- translates into only one thirty-day {\i nezirut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketzisusa Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} articulates the principle: {\i kol milta d\u8217'is bei ketzisusa lo k\u8217'tani} \u8212- anything that has a fixed, determinate number is not what was taught. That is: expressions like \u8220"the hairs of my head\u8221" or \u8220"the sand of the sea\u8221" are understood as conveying an uncountable, practically infinite quantity. No one seriously believes they can enumerate every grain of sand on the sea or every hair on a head. The expression is understood as meaning an unlimited, indeterminate number \u8212- and therefore it is taken as expressing an intention to accept an unlimited series of {\i neziruyot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, \u8220"from here to the end of the world\u8221" \u8212- despite sounding equally hyperbolic \u8212- could in principle yield a concrete number depending on what unit of measurement is applied. The circumference of the world, as the {\i gemara} elsewhere records, is a specific figure (five hundred {\i parsa\u8217'ot}). Because a determinate number {\i could} be extracted, even if we do not know which unit the speaker has in mind, the expression does not clearly convey an infinite sequence. Since no definite time duration is specified and no definite number of sequential {\i neziruyot} is clearly implied, the vow defaults to a single standard {\i nezirut} of thirty days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle, in short: where a spatial or quantity expression is so vast that no number can concretely emerge from it \u8212- sand of the sea, hairs of the head \u8212- the vow is taken as infinite sequential {\i neziruyot}. Where the expression, however expansive it sounds, could theoretically be resolved into a concrete number, it is treated as mere emphasis and yields only the default single {\i nezirut}. The remainder of this complex sugya will be taken up in the following session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Default & Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Nezirut Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The eighth daf of Nazir continues the discussion of {\i stam nezirut} \u8212- the default, unqualified acceptance of {\i nezirut} \u8212- which carries a duration of thirty days. The previous daf dealt with complicating cases: what happens when someone says something like \u8220"from here, a large one\u8221" or \u8220"from here until the end of the world\u8221"? In those cases, the rule is that since no particular quantity, time, or unit was specified, the vow is interpreted as a single, standard {\i nezirut} of thirty days. The contrast is with a case where the person does specify time \u8212- as in the earlier {\i mishnah} where someone says \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} for thirty days and one hour.\u8221" Since there is no such thing as an hour-long {\i nezirut}, that statement is interpreted to mean thirty-one days. The organizing principle, then, is threefold: if someone speaks abstractly (big, far, great), that does not translate into additional time or units; if someone specifies time, that redefines duration; and if someone specifies units, that generates multiple {\i neziruyot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Singular vs.\u160 ?Plural\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One subtle point worth noting is that the {\i mishnah} employs the feminine form in the phrase {\i hareini nazir achat gedolah}, which is grammatically curious. The {\i nazir} is masculine, yet {\i achat gedolah} is feminine, suggesting the underlying referent is {\i nezirut achat} \u8212- one unit of {\i nezirut} \u8212- rather than a masculine {\i nazir gadol}. This linguistic observation anticipates a theme the {\i gemara} will develop at length: the difference between using the word {\i nazir} in the singular versus using the word {\i nezirut} or {\i neziruyot}, and whether that grammatical distinction carries legal weight.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fill of Box\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair/Dust/Sand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens with a series of cases: {\i hareini nazir kisa\u8217'ar roshi} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} like the hairs of my head\u8221"; {\i ukhe\u8217'afar ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- \u8220"or like the dust of the earth\u8221"; {\i ukhechol hayam} \u8212- \u8220"or like the sand of the sea.\u8221" The ruling is {\i harei zeh nazir olam} \u8212- this person is a {\i nazir} forever. This does not mean a {\i nazir olam} in the technical sense of an uninterrupted, permanent {\i nezirut}, but rather that the person has accepted upon himself an effectively infinite number of discrete {\i neziruyot} in sequence. Because hairs, dust, and grains of sand are all discrete countable units, the vow is interpreted as: \u8220"I accept upon myself as many separate {\i neziruyot} as the number of hairs on my head\u8221" \u8212- or grains of sand, or specks of dust. Since that number is functionally infinite, the person will forever be cycling through thirty-day {\i neziruyot}, each ending with the full complement of {\i korbanot} and shaving, followed immediately by the next.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This must be distinguished from a true {\i nazir olam}, who accepts a single uninterrupted {\i nezirut} with no end date, and who is permitted to trim his hair periodically \u8212- some hold once a week, some once a month, some once a year \u8212- precisely because otherwise the hair would become unmanageable. The person who says \u8220"like the hairs of my head\u8221" is a {\i nazir le\u8217'olam} in a colloquial sense: he will be a {\i nazir} forever as a practical matter, cycling through one thirty-day term after another, but he completes each one with a full shaving and {\i korbanot}. He is not in the legal category of {\i nazir olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi} disagrees with the {\i tanna kamma}. He says: {\i ein zeh megale\u8217'ach achat lishloshim yom} \u8212- this person does not shave every thirty days. {\i Rebbi}\u8217's position is that since the speaker used the word {\i nazir} in the singular \u8212- not {\i neziruyot} in the plural \u8212- he did not accept multiple units upon himself. The reference to hairs on his head does not translate into a specific number of {\i neziruyot}, because units only become significant if the speaker has framed the vow in terms of multiple units. If he used the singular {\i nazir}, then no matter how vast the comparison, he is simply one {\i nazir} for thirty days. The number of hairs on his head tells us only that he felt the vow was enormously significant \u8212- it does not redefine the scope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi} then clarifies what {\i would} mean multiple {\i neziruyot} with shaving every thirty days: {\i ha\u8217'omer harei alai neziruyot kisa\u8217'ar roshi} \u8212- \u8220"upon me are {\i neziruyot} like the hairs of my head.\u8221" Only the use of the plural {\i neziruyot} transforms the comparison into a multiplicity of units. Without that plural, the singular {\i nazir} limits the vow to one thirty-day term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House/Basket Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents the case of {\i hareini nazir melo habayis o melo hakupa} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} like the fill of the house\u8221" or \u8220"like the fill of this basket.\u8221" Here, the rule is {\i bodkin oso} \u8212- we examine him, we ask him what he meant. If he says {\i achas gedolah nazarti} \u8212- \u8220"I accepted one large {\i nezirut}\u8221" \u8212- meaning the comparison to the fill of the house was meant only to convey magnitude, not to specify a number of units, then he is simply a {\i nazir} for thirty days. He did not give us time, he did not give us units, he just expressed that it felt like a very large thing. Fine \u8212- one {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if he says {\i stam nazarti} \u8212- \u8220"I declared {\i nezirut} without any particular intention\u8221" \u8212- then the words themselves must be interpreted. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i ro\u8217'in es hakupa ke\u8217'ilu hi mele\u8217'ah chardal} \u8212- we view the basket as if it were filled with mustard seeds, and the person is a {\i nazir} for all the days of his life, since the number of mustard seeds that fill a basket is functionally uncountable. This is striking. The very premise requires examination: why does saying \u8220"like the fill of the box\u8221" translate into a number of discrete units at all? The person used the word {\i nazir} in the singular. And even granting that the phrase somehow implies units, why interpret it as the most numerous possible units \u8212- mustard seeds \u8212- rather than the fewest, such as watermelons or gourds?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distance Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also addresses {\i hareini nazir mikan ad makom ploni} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} from here to such-and-such a place.\u8221" The rule is {\i omdin kama yamim mikan ad makom ploni} \u8212- we estimate how many days of travel it takes to get from here to there. If the distance is less than thirty days\u8217' travel, the person is a {\i nazir} for thirty days, since {\i nezirut} cannot be less than thirty days. If the distance is more than thirty days\u8217' travel, then {\i keminyan hayamim} \u8212- the number of travel days defines the duration of the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This was discussed earlier in the context of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement that saying \u8220"from here to the end of the world\u8221" does not lengthen the {\i nezirut}. The question there was: if \u8220"from here to Chicago\u8221" translates into a duration, why does \u8220"from here to the end of the world\u8221" not do the same? Tosafot raises the possibility that people do have a fixed assumption about the distance to the end of the world, which would make it no different from any other measurable journey. Rabbah\u8217's resolution was that this case applies when the person is already on the trip \u8212- when someone is traveling and declares {\i nezirut} for the duration of the journey, the distance naturally converts into time. Tosafot adds another dimension: the person may be accepting {\i nezirut} as a {\i neder} seeking divine protection for the journey, in which case \u8220"until I get there\u8221" is the natural scope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solar Year\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case: {\i hareini nazir keminyan yemot hachama} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} like the number of days of the solar year.\u8221" The {\i mishnah} rules {\i moneh neziros keminyan yemot hachama} \u8212- the person must fulfill 365 {\i neziruyot}, one after another. Critically, the formula is \u8220"the {\i number} of days of the solar year,\u8221" not \u8220"for the {\i duration} of a solar year.\u8221" The word {\i keminyan} \u8212- \u8220"like the number of\u8221" \u8212- signals that we are counting discrete units, not measuring a span of time. Accordingly, we count 365 separate thirty-day {\i neziruyot}, each requiring its own completion and {\i korbanot}. At thirty days per {\i nezirut}, that amounts to approximately thirty years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehudah} reports that there was an actual such case, and by the time the man finished all 365 {\i neziruyot}, he had died. This anecdote is cited not merely as a curiosity but as proof of the legal ruling: we do not interpret \u8220"the number of days of the year\u8221" as one year-long {\i nezirut}, but as 365 discrete {\i neziruyot}. The man spent the better part of his life fulfilling them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mustard vs.\u160 ?Watermelon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} addresses the obvious question raised by the fill-of-the-basket ruling. It asks: {\i b\u8217'mai} \u8212- why mustard seeds? {\i Delachzei ke\u8217'ilu mele\u8217'ah kishuin vedilu\u8217'in} \u8212- why not view the basket as filled with cucumbers and gourds? Tosafot sharpens the question: why not view it as filled with dough? A single mass of dough, no matter how large the container, could constitute one piece filling the entire basket, which would yield a single {\i nezirut} of thirty days. The {\i gemara} is pressing the question of whether ambiguous language should be resolved strictly or leniently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that there is a {\i mishnah} in {\i Taharos} that states {\i safek nezirus lehakeil} \u8212- in cases of doubt regarding {\i nezirut}, we rule leniently. That principle would point toward interpreting the basket as filled with the fewest, largest units possible. The {\i gemara} then reveals that this is in fact a dispute between {\i Rebbi Shimon} and {\i Rabbi Yehudah}, and that the stringent interpretation of mustard seeds reflects the position of {\i Rebbi Shimon}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimon vs.\u160 ?Yehudah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the broader principle: what do we do when there is doubt about {\i nezirut}? {\i Rebbi Shimon} holds {\i safek nezirus lehachmir} \u8212- in cases of doubt, we rule strictly. {\i Rabbi Yehudah} holds {\i safek nezirus lehakeil} \u8212- in cases of doubt, we rule leniently. This dispute is directly cited by the {\i gemara} as the backdrop for the fill-of-the-basket case. {\i Rebbi Shimon} is strict and therefore interprets the basket as filled with mustard seeds, yielding an uncountable number of {\i neziruyot}. {\i Rabbi Yehudah} is lenient and would presumably interpret it more narrowly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that the two cases under this debate are not entirely parallel. One is a question of language: how do we interpret ambiguous words? The other is a question of whether a condition was actually met. These are conceptually distinct \u8212- one concerns the meaning of the vow, the other concerns whether the vow took effect at all. The {\i gemara} treats them together, but the difference matters, as we will see in {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's subsequent clarification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is introduced: {\i hareini nazir al m\u8217'nas sheyehei becheri zeh me\u8217'ah kor} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} on the condition that there will be in this pile of grain one hundred {\i kor}.\u8221" The person then went to measure the pile and found it gone \u8212- either stolen or lost. The question is whether the {\i nezirut} took effect. {\i Rebbi Shimon} rules that the person is forbidden \u8212- {\i safek nezirus lehachmir}, we treat the uncertainty strictly and assume the {\i nezirut} applies. {\i Rabbi Yehudah} rules that the person is permitted \u8212- {\i safek nezirus lehakeil}, and since we do not know whether the condition was met, the {\i nezirut} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One way to frame {\i Rabbi Yehudah}\u8217's position: when the person said \u8220"on condition that there are one hundred {\i kor} in the pile,\u8221" what he really meant is \u8220"on condition that we {\i find} one hundred {\i kor} in the pile.\u8221" Since one went and the pile was gone, one never found one hundred {\i kor} \u8212- regardless of whether the hundred {\i kor} were there before being stolen. On that reading, the condition was demonstrably not met. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's broader framing is that the dispute is simply about whether uncertainty triggers leniency or stringency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Worse Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces a striking and counterintuitive claim: {\i adam machnis es atzmo ledavar shesefeko chamur mivado} \u8212- a person can put himself into a situation where the {\i safek} (doubt) is actually more stringent than the {\i vadai} (certainty). How is being a {\i safek nazir} worse than being a definite {\i nazir}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: if one is a definite {\i nazir}, one completes thirty days, brings the {\i korbanot} \u8212- {\i olah}, {\i shelamim}, {\i chatat}, and {\i asham} \u8212- and is released. But if one is a {\i safek nazir}, one must observe the prohibitions of {\i nezirut} since one might be a {\i nazir}. Yet at the end of thirty days, one cannot bring the {\i korbanot} that complete and terminate the {\i nezirut}, because those {\i korbanot} \u8212- particularly the {\i chatat} and {\i asham} \u8212- may only be brought by a definite {\i nazir}. They cannot be brought as voluntary offerings by someone who may not be a {\i nazir} at all. The result is that the {\i safek nazir} is trapped: obligated to maintain the restrictions indefinitely because the {\i nezirut} has not been formally closed, yet unable to close it because doing so requires {\i korbanot} one cannot bring under uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solution Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a way out of this trap. The {\i safek nazir} can make the following declaration: \u8220"If I am already a {\i nazir} from my earlier statement, I am doing nothing now. But if I am not yet a {\i nazir}, then I hereby accept {\i nezirut} upon myself right now.\u8221" This conditional statement converts the ambiguous situation into a {\i vadai} either way. If the first acceptance worked, nothing changes. If it did not, the new declaration is unconditional and definitive. Either way the person is now a certain {\i nazir}, can complete thirty days, bring the {\i korbanot}, and be released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes, however, that even this solution does not always work, and that is precisely why {\i safek} remains worse than {\i vadai} in principle. Consider the following scenario: the person is a {\i safek nazir} and is about to recite the conditional formula to resolve his status \u8212- but before he can do so, he becomes {\i tamei} (ritually impure). Now the problem compounds. If he was already a {\i nazir} before becoming {\i tamei}, he brings the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir tamei}, which include a {\i chatat} ha-of and other specific offerings, and he restarts his {\i nezirut} from the beginning. But if he was not yet a {\i nazir} when he became {\i tamei}, and he now accepts {\i nezirut} upon himself, he does {\i not} bring the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- because those are only for someone who was already a {\i nazir} when the impurity occurred. The conditional formula therefore does not resolve the problem in this scenario; one remains stuck in genuine uncertainty with no clean exit. This demonstrates that being a {\i safek nazir} can be structurally worse than being a {\i vadai nazir}, even accounting for the partial solution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is clear: {\i Rebbi Shimon} is fully aware that his stringency places the person in a worse position than the stringency of a definite obligation \u8212- and he is willing to accept that consequence, because his view is that whenever there is doubt in {\i nezirut}, we go strict. {\i Rabbi Yehudah} disagrees and refuses to impose {\i nezirut} under uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudah Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Quo Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} intervenes with a crucial distinction: {\i afilu teima Rabbi Yehudah} \u8212- one can say even {\i Rabbi Yehudah} would agree with the strict ruling in the fill-of-the-basket case. The key is {\i hasam lo nachis lei l\u8217'nezirus, hacha nachis lei l\u8217'nezirus} \u8212- there, in the conditional case of the pile of grain, the person never definitively entered into {\i nezirut} in the first place. Since we do not know whether the condition was met, we do not know whether {\i nezirut} ever began. {\i Rabbi Yehudah}\u8217's leniency operates precisely in this space: the person\u8217's current status is non-{\i nazir}, and without certainty that the condition was fulfilled, we preserve that status quo. We do not impose {\i nezirut} on the basis of doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in the fill-of-the-basket case, the person has certainly entered {\i nezirut} \u8212- at minimum, even the most lenient interpretation (one watermelon) means he became a {\i nazir}. Once {\i nezirut} has definitely begun, {\i Rabbi Yehudah} might agree that we do not allow doubt to terminate or limit it. The question is no longer whether {\i nezirut} begins; it has already begun. The question is whether it continues and extends. And on that question, perhaps even {\i Rabbi Yehudah} would say: {\i b\u8217'mai islukey minei?} \u8212- what force would remove him from his {\i nezirut}? Since he is already a {\i nazir}, doubt does not extricate him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Ends Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot complicates {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's proposed distinction. One might think that after the first thirty-day {\i nezirut} is completed, the person has fully exited {\i nazir} status \u8212- and therefore when the question arises whether a second {\i nezirut} begins, we are back to the same situation as the conditional case: the person is currently not a {\i nazir}, and doubt should not create one. In other words, Tosafot is pointing out that {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's logic \u8212- that the person already entered {\i nezirut} \u8212- applies only during the first term. Once that term ends, the person\u8217's status reverts to non-{\i nazir}, and the rationale for stringency dissolves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Term Start\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the {\i gemara}\u8217's sharper formulation of the difficulty. After the first thirty days conclude, the person\u8217's first {\i nezirut} is definitively complete. The question becomes: does a second {\i nezirut} begin? At this moment, the person is not a {\i nazir}. If {\i Rabbi Yehudah} holds that we do not impose {\i nezirut} under doubt when the person\u8217's status quo is non-{\i nazir}, then at the end of each thirty-day cycle, he should be permitted to stop \u8212- because the question of whether the next term begins is itself uncertain, and the status quo is not-{\i nazir}. The {\i gemara} raises this as a genuine challenge to {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's proposed reconciliation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's response is that perhaps the entire fill-of-the-basket case is treated as one {\i nezirut} whose length is uncertain \u8212- rather than multiple {\i neziruyot} of uncertain number. If {\i Rabbi Yehudah} holds like {\i Rebbi} (as the {\i gemara} will now develop), then saying {\i nazir} in the singular with a vague quantifier might mean one very long {\i nezirut} of indefinite duration, not thousands of discrete separate {\i neziruyot}. In that framing, the question is never \u8220"does the next {\i nezirut} begin?\u8221" but rather \u8220"when does this one end?\u8221" \u8212- and since it has already begun, the uncertainty about its endpoint keeps it running, consistent with {\i Rabbi Yehudah}\u8217's logic.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudah = Rabbi?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Singular Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To establish whether {\i Rabbi Yehudah} could hold like {\i Rebbi}, the {\i gemara} must first determine whether {\i Rabbi Yehudah} actually agrees with {\i Rebbi}\u8217's core principle. {\i Rebbi}\u8217's ruling, as stated in the {\i mishnah}, is that using the word {\i nazir} in the singular \u8212- even combined with \u8220"like the hairs of my head\u8221" \u8212- means only one {\i nezirut} of thirty days, because the plural {\i neziruyot} is required to generate multiple units. The {\i gemara} proposes that perhaps {\i Rabbi Yehudah} holds this same view, which would explain how our {\i mishnah} (the fill-of-the-basket case with its stringent ruling) could be attributed to {\i Rabbi Yehudah}: he agrees it is one {\i nezirut}, but since it definitely began and we don\u8217't know when it ends, it continues indefinitely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 365 Days Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this hypothesis. It cites the very case discussed in the {\i mishnah}: {\i hareini nazir keminyan yemot hachama} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} like the number of days of the solar year.\u8221" The {\i mishnah} rules that the person must fulfill 365 {\i neziruyot}. And {\i Rabbi Yehudah} himself endorses this ruling by citing the story of the man who died before completing all 365. The verb {\i hishlim} \u8212- \u8220"completed\u8221" or \u8220"finished them all\u8221" \u8212- implies a plurality of obligations the man had to discharge one by one. Clearly, {\i Rabbi Yehudah} holds that when you say {\i nazir} in the singular combined with \u8220"like the number of days of the year,\u8221" you mean 365 {\i discrete} {\i neziruyot}, not one long one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This directly contradicts the hypothesis that {\i Rabbi Yehudah} holds like {\i Rebbi}, since {\i Rebbi} would say: you used the word {\i nazir} in the singular, therefore you mean one {\i nezirut}. {\i Rabbi Yehudah}, by contrast, treats the reference to number as generating multiple units even when the word {\i nazir} is in the singular. So on the surface, {\i Rabbi Yehudah} does not hold like {\i Rebbi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Number Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i minyan shani} \u8212- the use of the word {\i minyan} (number, quantity) is different. When someone explicitly says {\i keminyan} \u8212- \u8220"like the {\i number} of\u8221" \u8212- everyone may agree that this generates discrete units, regardless of whether the word {\i nazir} or {\i neziruyot} was used. The word {\i keminyan} is inherently about counting, about discrete enumeration, and its presence in the formula is sufficient to signal that the person intends multiple units. This is not the same as saying {\i nazir} in the singular combined with a vague comparison like \u8220"like the hairs of my head\u8221" \u8212- which contains no explicit number language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To verify whether {\i Rebbi} himself holds this distinction, the {\i gemara} introduces a {\i baraita}. The {\i baraita} presents the case of {\i hareini nazir keminyan yemot hachama} \u8212- just as in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- and the {\i tanna kamma} rules 365 {\i neziruyot}, and a parallel case of {\i keminyan yemot halevana} (like the number of days of the lunar month), yielding 29 or 30 {\i neziruyot}. {\i Rebbi} then says: {\i ad sheyomar neziruyot alai keminyan yemot hachama} \u8212- the person must use the plural {\i neziruyot} combined with the number language before we count multiple units. According to {\i Rebbi}, even the phrase \u8220"like the {\i number} of days of the year\u8221" is insufficient if the word used is the singular {\i nazir}. Only the plural {\i neziruyot} creates multiple units, regardless of what else is said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This proves that {\i Rebbi} does not distinguish between cases with and without {\i minyan} language. For {\i Rebbi}, the singular {\i nazir} always means one \u8212- full stop. Therefore, the {\i gemara}\u8217's attempt to reconcile {\i Rabbi Yehudah} and {\i Rebbi} by appealing to the {\i minyan} distinction runs into trouble: that distinction may be {\i Rabbi Yehudah}\u8217's but is not {\i Rebbi}\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Figs/Paths Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i baraita} is brought to confirm {\i Rabbi Yehudah}\u8217's independent position. {\i Rabbi Yehudah} himself says in a {\i baraita}: {\i hareini nazir minyan helktei kayitz} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} according to the number of mounds of drying figs,\u8221" or {\i minyan shvilei shemitah} \u8212- \u8220"the number of paths cut through the field during {\i shemitah}.\u8221" In both cases, {\i Rabbi Yehudah} rules {\i moneh neziros keminyan helktei kayitz, keminyan shvilei shemitah} \u8212- the person must fulfill that many {\i neziruyot}. This confirms that {\i Rabbi Yehudah} holds that when {\i minyan} language is used, even with the singular {\i nazir}, the result is multiple discrete {\i neziruyot}. He clearly does not hold like {\i Rebbi}, who would require the plural {\i neziruyot} in any event.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudah = Rabbi? (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension with a nuanced formulation: {\i Rabbi Yehudah savar lah kavatei bichada upaliga alei bichada} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yehudah} agrees with {\i Rebbi} in one respect and disagrees in another. The {\i gemara} has now established that {\i Rabbi Yehudah}\u8217's position is a middle ground between {\i Rebbi} and the {\i chachamim}, and the specifics of that middle ground are worked out in the following section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Middle Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alone = One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehudah} agrees with {\i Rebbi} on the following: if someone says the word {\i nazir} in the singular \u8212- without any {\i minyan} language, without any explicit quantifier \u8212- then even if he adds a comparison that involves vast quantities (like the hairs of my head, like the sand of the sea), that comparison does not transform the vow into multiple {\i neziruyot}. The singular {\i nazir} without a number word attached to it means one {\i nezirut}. On this point, {\i Rabbi Yehudah} concurs with {\i Rebbi}: the word {\i nazir} alone, in the singular, limits the vow to one term. The vast comparison expresses only the subjective weight of the acceptance, not the number of units.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plus Minyan = Many\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where {\i Rabbi Yehudah} disagrees with {\i Rebbi} is when {\i minyan} language \u8212- explicit number-counting \u8212- is introduced. When someone says {\i hareini nazir keminyan yemot hachama}, or {\i keminyan helktei kayitz}, the word {\i keminyan} is an explicit quantifier that converts the vow into discrete units. For {\i Rabbi Yehudah}, that number language overrides the singular form of {\i nazir} and generates multiple {\i neziruyot} equal to the stated count. For {\i Rebbi}, by contrast, even this does not suffice \u8212- only the plural form {\i neziruyot} creates multiple terms, and nothing else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This middle position elegantly accounts for the {\i baraita}ic rulings cited in {\i Rabbi Yehudah}\u8217's name. In every case where he rules multiple {\i neziruyot}, explicit {\i minyan} language was present. In cases without {\i minyan} language \u8212- the vague fill-of-the-basket case, the hairs-on-the-head case without the number word \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yehudah} would agree with {\i Rebbi} that the singular {\i nazir} means one term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution also clarifies {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's original argument. The fill-of-the-basket case, where the {\i mishnah} rules that {\i stam} (no declared intent) means an uncountable number of {\i neziruyot}, uses the construction {\i hareini nazir melo hakupa} \u8212- the word {\i nazir} in the singular, with no explicit {\i keminyan}. If {\i Rabbi Yehudah} agrees with {\i Rebbi} in such cases that the singular {\i nazir} means one, then the fill-of-the-basket case could indeed go like {\i Rabbi Yehudah} \u8212- interpreted as one indefinitely long {\i nezirut} rather than multiple discrete ones. The stringency is not about multiplying {\i neziruyot} but about the ambiguous endpoint of a single, already-begun {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further observation complicates the picture, raised toward the end of the discussion. The grammatical point is well taken that {\i nezirut} in the singular is itself an abstract noun \u8212- it is not simply the singular of {\i neziruyot}. If {\i nezirut} denotes an abstract, endless state, then {\i Rebbi}\u8217's use of {\i neziruyot} to denote multiple units is linguistically precise: {\i neziruyot} as a true plural of a count noun implies discrete enumerable instances, while {\i nezirut} as an abstract noun implies a single undifferentiated state. This grammatical substructure runs beneath the entire {\i sugya} and lends precision to what might otherwise seem like a merely formal distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} toward the end of the chapter summarizes by presenting the full range of cases \u8212- {\i hareini nazir} plus one ({\i v\u8217'achat}), plus more ({\i v\u8217'od}), plus again ({\i v\u8217'shuv}) \u8212- yielding two, three, and four {\i neziruyot} respectively. The {\i gemara} briefly entertains whether {\i v\u8217'shuv} could be interpreted as retroactively doubling everything that came before, potentially reaching six. It concludes that {\i v\u8217'shuv} simply means one additional unit, so the total is four.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Closing Greek Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hen/Digon/etc.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i perek} concludes with a {\i baraita} attributed to Sumchos, a {\i tanna} whose name reflects Hellenistic cultural influence. Sumchos enumerates Greek numerical terms as they apply to {\i nezirut}: {\i hen} (one) means one {\i nezirut}; {\i digon} (two) means two; {\i trigon} (three) means three; {\i tetragon} (four) means four; and {\i pentagon} (five) means five. If someone declares {\i nezirut} using these Greek number-words, the {\i halacha} recognizes them as valid specifications of quantity, and the person must fulfill the corresponding number of {\i neziruyot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The terms {\i trigon}, {\i tetragon}, and {\i pentagon} are cognates of the Greek words for three-sided, four-sided, and five-sided figures \u8212- the same roots as \u8220"triangle,\u8221" \u8220"tetragon,\u8221" and \u8220"pentagon\u8221" in English. The word {\i digon} is less immediately obvious but refers to a two-sided figure. These terms appear in the Talmud as evidence that Greek numerical and geometric vocabulary had penetrated the everyday speech of Jews in the Talmudic period, sufficiently so that {\i halacha} had to address vows made using that vocabulary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Shapes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraita} follows, also employing these geometric terms, this time in the context of {\i nega\u8217'im} \u8212- the laws of {\i tzara\u8217'at} affecting houses. The {\i baraita} states: {\i bayis agul} \u8212- a circular house \u8212- or a house shaped as a {\i digon}, {\i trigon}, or {\i pentagon} does not become {\i tamei} with {\i nega\u8217'im}. Only a {\i tetragon} \u8212- a four-sided, rectangular house \u8212- can become {\i tamei}. The reason derives from the verse\u8217's repeated use of the word {\i kir} (wall): the Torah mentions walls multiple times in describing a house afflicted with {\i nega\u8217'im}, and the {\i gemara} derives from these repetitions that four walls are required. A house without four distinct walls \u8212- whether round, triangular, or pentagonal \u8212- falls outside the category of house subject to {\i tum\u8217'at nega\u8217'im} altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the {\i perek} concludes: {\i hadran alach kol kinuyei}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 9\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Grogrot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Sheini Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the opening of the second perek, {\i Hareini Nazir}, we enter terrain that overlaps with themes from the first perek, though with a distinctly new focus. Much of {\i perek} 1, as in the parallel tractate Nedarim, addressed the different formulations of language a person might use to declare {\i nezirut} \u8212- not only whether a particular declaration makes one a {\i nazir}, but also how many units of {\i nezirut} are created, whether we interpret the vow as binding for the standard duration or a longer period, and the special categories of {\i Nazir Olam} and {\i Nazir Shimshon}. The second perek now takes up a different kind of question: what happens when the language of the declaration is internally incoherent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opening {\i mishna} presents the following case: {\i Hareini nazir min hagrogros umin hadvela} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} from dried figs and pressed dates.\u8221" Neither {\i grogros} (dried figs) nor {\i dvela} (pressed dates) are grapes or wine. They have no connection to the world of {\i nezirut}, whose prohibitions, as the Torah specifies, all derive from {\i kol asher ye\u8217'aseh migefen hayayin} \u8212- everything produced from the grapevine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai/Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai} rules that such a person is indeed a {\i nazir}. {\i Beis Hillel} rules that he is not. The immediate difficulty, of course, bears on {\i Beis Shammai}: how can declaring oneself a {\i nazir} from something entirely outside the domain of {\i nezirut} \u8212- figs and dates \u8212- create {\i nezirut} at all?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yehuda Reframe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} attempts to reframe {\i Beis Shammai}\u8217's position: {\i ad kan she\u8217'omru Beis Shammai, lo amru ela be\u8217'omer harei hen alai korban} \u8212- \u8220"Even when {\i Beis Shammai} said it, they only said it in the case where one says \u8216'these are upon me like a {\i korban}.\u8217'\u8221" That is, {\i Rav Yehuda} reads {\i Beis Shammai} as holding that the declaration {\i Hareini nazir min hagrogros} is not interpreted as {\i nezirut} at all, but rather as a {\i neder} \u8212- a vow rendering those figs forbidden. On this reading, {\i Beis Shammai} never actually says the person becomes a {\i nazir} in the conventional sense; rather, the statement is recast as a prohibition on the figs themselves, as though one said {\i harei hen alai ke\u8217'korban}. Alternatively, the word {\i nazir}, as Tosafot notes, can itself carry the meaning of {\i lehazir} \u8212- to separate \u8212- and thus the phrase {\i Hareini nazir min hagrogros} is read simply as \u8220"I hereby separate myself from these figs,\u8221" a declaration of personal prohibition rather than a {\i kabbalat nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir = Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether the reframing proceeds through the language of {\i lehazir} or through the analogy to {\i korban}, {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's point is the same: {\i Beis Shammai} does not hold that uttering the word {\i nazir} in conjunction with figs actually triggers {\i nezirut}. The statement is translated into its most plausible halakhic meaning \u8212- a self-imposed prohibition on those items.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder/Shvua Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretive move is familiar from the opening discussions in Nedarim. The {\i gemara} there explored at length the question of {\i neder belashon shvua, shvua belashon neder} \u8212- using the technical term for one type of vow when one clearly intended the other. Since a {\i neder} applies to objects and a {\i shvua} to actions, a person who says \u8220"a {\i neder} that I will not enter this house\u8221" is understood by many {\i rishonim} to have actually made a {\i shvua}, because that is the only form that fits the content. The wrong term is treated as a {\i yad} \u8212- a handle or indicator \u8212- pointing toward the actual intent. So too here: the word {\i nazir}, under {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's reading of {\i Beis Shammai}, functions as a {\i yad} for a {\i neder} forbidding the figs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem, however, is that the {\i Tanna Kamma} of the {\i mishna} does not read {\i Beis Shammai} as {\i Rav Yehuda} does. On the straightforward reading of the {\i mishna}, {\i Beis Shammai} holds that the person is genuinely a {\i nazir} \u8212- not merely forbidden from eating figs, but a full {\i nazir} in all its dimensions. How can a declaration that mentions only dates and figs \u8212- items wholly foreign to {\i nezirut} \u8212- create actual {\i nezirut}? This is the question the {\i gemara} now addresses directly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Adam Motzi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i Beis Shammai savrei ke\u8217'Rabbi Meir, de\u8217'amar ein adam motzi devarav levatala} \u8212- {\i Beis Shammai} holds like Rabbi Meir, who says that a person does not utter his words for naught. A meaningful halakhic principle operates here: when someone makes a formal declaration in a halakhic context, we do not simply dismiss the statement as nonsense. We seek a legally coherent interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erech/Damim Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} grounds this in Rabbi Meir\u8217's classic case. The Torah provides a system of {\i arachin} \u8212- fixed valuations based on a person\u8217's age and sex \u8212- distinct from {\i dmei}, the market value of something. If someone says {\i erech kli alai} \u8212- \u8220"the {\i erech} of this vessel is upon me\u8221" \u8212- this is a problematic formulation, since {\i erech} as defined by the Torah applies only to human beings, not to objects. Rabbi Yehuda would say the person is not liable for anything, because the statement is simply incoherent. Rabbi Meir, invoking {\i ein adam motzi devarav levatala}, says we reinterpret the word {\i erech} as {\i dmei} \u8212- the person meant to say \u8220"the {\i value} of this vessel is upon me\u8221" \u8212- and they are obligated accordingly. The declaration, though technically misphrased, is given a coherent halakhic reading rather than being discarded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Different?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applying this to our case, however, reveals a significant tension. When someone says {\i erech kli alai}, the fix is clean: substitute {\i dmei} for {\i erech}, and the entire statement makes sense. But {\i Hareini nazir min hagrogros} cannot be saved by a simple terminological substitution. Even if we accept {\i ein adam motzi devarav levatala} and refuse to dismiss the statement outright, we are still left with a nonsensical clause: {\i min hagrogros}. Under the {\i nazir} framework, figs are irrelevant. So {\i Beis Shammai}, in applying Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle here, is doing something more aggressive than Rabbi Meir\u8217's original example: not just reinterpreting a term, but actually discarding the second half of the statement entirely, retaining only the first word \u8212- {\i nazir} \u8212- as binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Student Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question: why not do both? If the first part of the statement ({\i Hareini nazir}) is binding and the second part ({\i min hagrogros}) represents some additional intent, why not say the person is both a {\i nazir} and also forbidden from figs \u8212- treating the two halves as independent halakhic acts? The {\i mishna} offers no such intermediate position. {\i Beis Shammai}, on the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's reading, says the person is a {\i nazir} \u8212- full stop. Nobody says the person is both. The {\i gemara} will develop why, but the question is well-taken: logically, a \u8220"both\u8221" position is conceivable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Begmar Devarav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Hillel}, the {\i gemara} explains, holds {\i ke\u8217'Rabbi Yosi, de\u8217'amar begmar devarav adam nitpas} \u8212- a person is \u8220"caught\u8221" \u8212- meaning bound \u8212- by the conclusion of his words. We do not freeze the analysis at the first clause of a statement; we read the statement as a whole, and the final portion of what was said carries interpretive weight for what preceded it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder Pitcho Imo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative concept here is {\i neder upitcho imo} \u8212- a vow that carries its own \u8220"opening\u8221" for dissolution within itself. Normally, dissolving a vow requires presenting a {\i chacham} with a {\i petach}, an opening \u8212- some basis for regret. Here, the very terms of the declaration themselves constitute that opening: by saying {\i min hagrogros}, the person has revealed that his entire concept of {\i nezirut} was built around abstaining from figs, which is not {\i nezirut} at all. The statement as a whole is self-undermining. {\i Beis Hillel} does not stop at {\i Hareini nazir} and declare it binding; they allow the speaker to finish, and what emerges is a statement that never coherently constitutes {\i kabbalat nezirut}. Notably, the {\i gemara} could have explained {\i Beis Hillel} more simply \u8212- as merely rejecting {\i ein adam motzi devarav levatala} \u8212- but instead it grounds {\i Beis Hillel} within Rabbi Yosi\u8217's framework, affirming that even if we accept the principle that one\u8217's words must mean something, the complete statement here, taken as a whole, produces a self-canceling result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot elaborates on how this works. The key passage, several lines into Tosafot\u8217's discussion, explains: since {\i begmar devarav adam nitpas}, we are \u8220"caught\u8221" by the conclusion \u8212- which is the mention of {\i grogros} \u8212- and since there is no {\i nezirut} with respect to {\i grogros}, the person is not a {\i nazir}. Even though at the outset he said {\i Hareini nazir}, by adding {\i min hagrogros} he effectively reversed himself {\i toch k\u8217'dei dibbur} \u8212- within the halakhic window of speech that is treated as continuous. It is as though he found a {\i petach} for his vow, a ground for dissolution, immediately upon completing the utterance. Since {\i nidro upitcho imo} \u8212- his vow and its opening came together \u8212- nothing takes effect at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot then raises the precise question that arose above: the {\i gemara} in the context of {\i tmura} records that Rabbi Yosi holds that when someone says {\i harei zeh tmurath olah tmurath shelamim} \u8212- this animal is an exchange for both an {\i olah} and a {\i shelamim} \u8212- Rabbi Yosi does not declare the whole statement void. Rather, he says the animal has both {\i kedushas} and it cannot be brought as a single {\i korban}, but can be redeemed, with half the proceeds going to each. There, even though the two parts of the statement are logically contradictory, Rabbi Yosi says both are operative. Why, then, in our case, don\u8217't we say both parts of the statement are operative \u8212- the person is a {\i nazir} and also a {\i nadur} from figs?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Not Both?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safa Explains Reisha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's resolution is precise. In the {\i tmura} case, {\i tmurath olah} and {\i tmurath shelamim} are two independent, equal-weight clauses. Neither explains or qualifies the other; they simply sit side by side as separate declarations. When two parts of a statement are genuinely independent and non-overlapping, we say both are meant and both take effect. But in our case, the situation is different: {\i min hagrogros} is not an independent second clause. It is the speaker\u8217's own explanation of what he means by {\i nazir} \u8212- his conception of {\i nezirut} is that it consists of abstaining from {\i grogros}. The {\i safa}, the end of the statement, comes {\i lifaresh} the {\i reisha} \u8212- to explain the opening. The speaker is not making two separate declarations; he is elaborating on the one declaration, and that elaboration reveals it to be incoherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 End Contradicts Start\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a structural problem. Once we read the second half as explaining the first, the whole sentence effectively says: \u8220"I am a {\i nazir}, and by {\i nazir} I mean someone who abstains from figs.\u8221" But that is not {\i nezirut}. The {\i safa} has thus not merely added a second layer; it has retroactively undermined the entire content of the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One or Two Parts?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying hermeneutic question is whether we are dealing with one statement or two. If they are two independent parts \u8212- as in {\i tmurath olah tmurath shelamim} \u8212- we say both. If the second part is an interpretation or elaboration of the first \u8212- as in {\i nazir min hagrogros}, where the {\i grogros} clause explains what the speaker thought {\i nazir} meant \u8212- then the {\i safa} is the key to the whole utterance, and since the {\i safa} reveals a fundamental misunderstanding, the statement as a whole produces nothing. That is what {\i begmar devarav adam nitpas} means in this context: we wait until the speaker is finished, and only then assess the whole.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Version 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the main conceptual framework, the {\i gemara} notes that the {\i mishna} does not align with a certain {\i tanna}: {\i matnitin delo ke\u8217'hai tanna}. A {\i braita} records Rabbi Natan citing a different version of the dispute. According to Rabbi Natan, {\i Beis Shammai} says the person is {\i nadur ve\u8217'nazir} \u8212- both forbidden from the figs (a {\i neder}) and a {\i nazir}. {\i Beis Hillel} says {\i nadur ve\u8217'ein nazir} \u8212- he is forbidden from the figs but is not a {\i nazir}. This version is notably different from the {\i mishna}. Here, everyone salvages the entire statement in some way. {\i Beis Shammai}, by saying both parts are independently binding, preserves the full declaration \u8212- the word {\i nazir} creates {\i nezirut}, and {\i min hagrogros} creates a {\i neder} on the figs. {\i Beis Hillel}, rather than voiding the whole statement, gives it a coherent unified meaning: {\i nazir min hagrogros} is read as {\i nadur min hagrogros} \u8212- the word {\i nazir} is interpreted as {\i neder} language, and the person is simply forbidden from figs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Positions Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains the theoretical underpinnings of this version as well. {\i Beis Shammai savrei ke\u8217'Rabbi Meir uke\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}. {\i Beis Shammai} here applies {\i ein adam motzi devarav levatala} (the principle of Rabbi Meir) to both halves of the statement: {\i nazir} means {\i nazir} and {\i min hagrogros} means {\i nadur min hagrogros} (as {\i Rav Yehuda} had originally read {\i Beis Shammai}). Nothing in the sentence is wasted; both parts carry independent halakhic force. {\i Beis Hillel savrei ke\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- applying {\i begmar devarav adam nitpas} to read the statement as one coherent whole, they give the entire declaration the meaning of {\i nadur min hagrogros}, the only reading that renders the complete sentence meaningful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Version 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second textual tradition ({\i lishna achrina}) within Rabbi Natan\u8217's account presents yet another variation: {\i Beis Shammai omrim nadur ve\u8217'eino nazir} \u8212- the person is forbidden from the figs but is not a {\i nazir}. This is precisely the reading {\i Rav Yehuda} attributed to {\i Beis Shammai} at the outset of the discussion. {\i Beis Hillel} says {\i lo nadur velo nazir} \u8212- neither; he is nothing at all. This version maps neatly onto {\i Beis Shammai} following {\i Rav Yehuda}\u8217's interpretation and {\i Beis Hillel} following Rabbi Shimon\u8217's approach of {\i lo hitnadev k\u8217'derech hamitnadvim} (to which we return shortly).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Options\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Surveying all the material, there are three distinct positions one could occupy when applying {\i ein adam motzi devarav levatala} to this case. First: the person is a {\i nazir} and we discard the {\i min hagrogros} clause \u8212- the first part of the statement was binding, the second part is treated as either irrelevant or as a failed attempt at reversal. Second: the person is {\i nadur min hagrogros} but not a {\i nazir} \u8212- the whole sentence is given a single coherent meaning by translating {\i nazir} as {\i nadur}. Third: the person is both a {\i nazir} and {\i nadur min hagrogros} \u8212- both halves of the statement take independent effect. All three positions stem from the shared refusal to read the statement as meaningless, but they differ in how they distribute that meaning across the two clauses. {\i Beis Hillel}, for its part, is willing to look at the complete statement and conclude that it never coherently achieved anything halakhically binding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mincha Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mincha Variations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a parallel set of cases from a {\i mishna} elsewhere: {\i Ha\u8217'omer harei alai mincha lehavi min hase\u8217'orim} \u8212- someone who says \u8220"I accept upon myself to bring a {\i mincha} from barley.\u8221" Since there is no voluntary {\i mincha} brought from barley (barley {\i minchot} are restricted to specific obligatory cases such as {\i minchat sotah}), the declaration is internally inconsistent. Nevertheless, the {\i tanna} rules: {\i yavi min hachittim} \u8212- he brings it from wheat. Similarly: one who said he would bring a {\i mincha} from {\i kemach} (bran or coarse flour) must bring it from fine flour ({\i solet}), since there is no {\i mincha} from {\i kemach}; one who said he would bring it without oil and {\i levona} (frankincense) must include them; one who said {\i chatzi issaron} (a half-measure, which has no standing as a {\i mincha}) must bring a full {\i issaron}; and one who said one and a half {\i issaronot} must bring two, rounding up to the nearest valid unit. In all these cases, the {\i tanna kamma} holds: we strip away the invalid qualifier, identify the core intent as a {\i mincha}, and obligate accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon} exempts in all these cases: {\i shelo hitnadev k\u8217'derech hamitnadvim} \u8212- this person did not make a vow in the manner of those who normally vow. If the only halakhically coherent interpretation of the whole statement requires discarding a significant portion of what was actually said, {\i Rabbi Shimon} would rather declare the entire declaration non-binding than artificially reconstruct it. This is a direct and close parallel to the {\i Hareini nazir min hagrogros} case: just as there is no {\i nezirut} from {\i grogros}, there is no {\i mincha} from barley as a freewill offering, and {\i Rabbi Shimon} would treat both declarations identically \u8212- as generating no obligation at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma ID\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chizkiyah} is the first to address the question of who the {\i tanna kamma} is in the {\i mincha} case. He identifies the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's position as equivalent to that of {\i Beis Shammai}: just as {\i Beis Shammai} says {\i Hareini nazir min hagrogros} creates {\i nezirut} by ignoring the invalid {\i min hagrogros} clause, so too the {\i tanna kamma} says {\i mincha min hase\u8217'orim} creates an obligation to bring a {\i mincha} by ignoring the invalid {\i min hase\u8217'orim} clause. The structure of the reasoning is identical in both cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Understandable Mistake\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} disagrees with {\i Chizkiyah}\u8217's identification. The {\i tanna kamma} in the {\i mincha} case can be understood even according to {\i Beis Hillel} \u8212- it need not reflect the position of {\i Beis Shammai} at all. Why? {\i Be\u8217'omer ilu hayiti yode\u8217'a she\u8217'ein nodrim kach, lo nadarti kach ella kach} \u8212- because the case is one where the person can be presumed to say: \u8220"Had I known that one cannot vow this way, I would not have said {\i min hase\u8217'orim}, I would have said {\i min hachittim}.\u8221" The declaration is treated as binding not because we mechanically ignore its invalid clause, but because the mistake was an understandable one. Most people do not carefully distinguish between barley and wheat when thinking about bringing a {\i mincha}; the commitment to bring a {\i mincha} is genuine and the specification of {\i se\u8217'orim} was simply an error that, if caught at the time, the person would immediately have corrected. In such a case, we say the fundamental intent was to bring a {\i mincha}, the qualifier was mistaken but not misconceived, and therefore the obligation stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Barley vs Lentils\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle, {\i Rabbi Yochanan} implies, has a sharp limit. An \u8220"understandable mistake\u8221" is one within the normal range of confusion \u8212- where the gap between what was said and what could have been said is relatively small. {\i Min hase\u8217'orim} vs.\u160 ?{\i min hachittim} is a modest error; barley and wheat are both grains, both familiar, and easily confused in the mind of someone unfamiliar with the precise laws of {\i minchot}. But if someone says {\i mincha min ha\u8217'adashim} \u8212- a {\i mincha} from lentil beans \u8212- that is a categorically different kind of error. No one making a sincere vow about a {\i mincha} confuses lentil beans with wheat. Such a statement reflects not a small mistake but a fundamental misunderstanding of what a {\i mincha} is, analogous to someone who thinks {\i nezirut} means not eating figs. In that case, the speaker is not simply misspecifying a detail; the whole concept they are operating with is wrong, and we cannot say their \u8220"fundamental\u8221" intent was to bring a valid {\i mincha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chizkiyah Initial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chizkiyah} initially argues that the {\i mincha} dispute is parallel to the {\i Beis Shammai/Beis Hillel} dispute, implying that even a radical departure from the norm \u8212- like lentil beans \u8212- should be treated consistently: we drop the invalid qualifier and obligate the {\i mincha}. On his view, there is no halakhically meaningful distinction between small and large mistakes in this context. The principle of ignoring the nonsensical second clause operates uniformly, regardless of how far outside the realm of {\i minchot} the stated qualifier falls.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges {\i Chizkiyah}\u8217's position by pressing the parallel he himself established. {\i Chizkiyah} had identified the {\i tanna kamma} by the {\i mincha} case as following {\i Beis Shammai}. But {\i Beis Shammai} holds \u8212- even in the case of {\i Hareini nazir min hagrogros} \u8212- that the person becomes a {\i nazir}, despite the radical absurdity of the qualifier. {\i Ve\u8217'adashim legabei mincha kegrogros legabei nazir damu} \u8212- lentil beans in the context of a {\i mincha} are like {\i grogros} in the context of {\i nezirut}: both are wildly off-base. But {\i Beis Shammai} rules the person is a {\i nazir} even in the {\i grogros} case. By his own analogy, {\i Chizkiyah} should conclude that even lentil beans would obligate a {\i mincha}. Yet {\i Chizkiyah} himself had said that the {\i adashim} case produces nothing. This is internally inconsistent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chizkiyah Reverses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadar bei Chizkiyah} \u8212- {\i Chizkiyah} reversed himself. Faced with this challenge, he conceded that the distinction between small and large mistakes is indeed operative, and came around to the position of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: the {\i mincha} obligation in the case of {\i se\u8217'orim} rests on the fact that it was an understandable mistake, not on a blanket rule of ignoring all invalid qualifiers. Large, radical mistakes \u8212- like {\i adashim} \u8212- do not generate a {\i mincha} obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Only Barley?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} explains why {\i Chizkiyah} ultimately capitulated: {\i matnisin kashya lei} \u8212- he was troubled by the {\i mishna} itself. {\i Mai irya d\u8217'tani min hase\u8217'orim} \u8212- why did the {\i mishna} give specifically the example of {\i barley}? If the rule were that we always strip away the invalid qualifier and require a valid {\i mincha} regardless of how outlandish the qualifier was, the {\i mishna} could have \u8212- and should have \u8212- made the point more powerfully by giving an extreme case: {\i lisni min ha\u8217'adashim}, \u8220"even if you said lentil beans, you still bring a {\i mincha}.\u8221" The fact that the {\i mishna} chose {\i barley} as its example demonstrates that the case is specifically calibrated to the range of understandable mistakes. {\i Se\u8217'orim} is at the boundary \u8212- close enough to a real {\i mincha} that we can say the person genuinely intended a {\i mincha} and simply got the grain wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Small Mistake Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When someone says {\i mincha min hase\u8217'orim}, the underlying intent is clear: this person wants to bring a {\i mincha}. The specification of barley was a detail they got wrong, but it does not transform their core commitment. We therefore ignore the mistaken qualifier and obligate them in a normal {\i mincha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Big Mistake Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When someone says {\i mincha min ha\u8217'adashim}, the situation is qualitatively different. Lentil beans have nothing to do with the {\i mincha} as an institution. Someone who thinks they can bring a {\i mincha} of lentils is not merely confused about a detail; they are operating with a fundamentally different conception of what they are doing. We cannot say they \u8220"fundamentally wanted to bring a {\i mincha}\u8221" and the lentils were an incidental error. They were trying to do something outside the framework of {\i minchot} entirely, and that attempt generates no halakhic obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yochanan Refined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Final\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After {\i Chizkiyah} reverses himself and accepts {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's position, the {\i gemara} presents {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's own response to the challenge about lentil beans. Remarkably, {\i Rabbi Yochanan} now asserts: {\i afilu min ha\u8217'adashim} \u8212- even if someone says {\i mincha min ha\u8217'adashim}, they are still obligated to bring a {\i mincha}. This appears to contradict his earlier stated position. The {\i gemara} clarifies: {\i Rabbi Yochanan} is not abandoning his reasoning but rather arguing {\i lidvarav de\u8217'Chizkiyah} \u8212- speaking according to {\i Chizkiyah}\u8217's own framework. {\i Rabbi Yochanan} is pressing {\i Chizkiyah}: you, {\i Chizkiyah}, should have stayed with your original position that we always drop the nonsensical qualifier. Your reason for reversing was that the {\i mishna} only mentioned {\i se\u8217'orim}; but that proves nothing, because there are other explanations for why the {\i mishna} chose that example.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversal Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One such explanation: {\i dilma lo mibaya ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- perhaps the {\i mishna} is making its point {\i a fortiori} in the opposite direction. If someone says {\i min ha\u8217'adashim}, that is such an obviously impossible qualifier that it must be read as a {\i chazara} \u8212- a reversal. The person is effectively saying, \u8220"I\u8217'm not really committing to a {\i mincha}.\u8221" But {\i tefos lashon rishon} \u8212- we hold them to the first statement. Since the second clause is so clearly absurd, we treat it as an attempted (and ineffective) retraction rather than as a genuine explanation, which means the first statement stands and the {\i mincha} obligation is even more obviously binding. On this reading, {\i adashim} is {\i kal vachomer} \u8212- if anything, it makes the case for obligation more compelling, not less.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Genuine Intent Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second alternative explanation for why the {\i mishna} used {\i se\u8217'orim}: when someone says {\i mincha min hase\u8217'orim}, it is genuinely ambiguous whether they are making a mistake or expressing a genuine and specific preference. Perhaps they specifically want to bring something like a {\i minchat omer} or a {\i minchat sotah}, which are indeed barley-based. If such a thing exists, I want to bring it; if not, I don\u8217't want to bring anything at all. If that is the case, the person has not \u8220"fundamentally wanted to bring a {\i mincha}\u8221" with barley as a mistaken qualifier \u8212- rather, they specifically wanted {\i that} type of {\i mincha} and nothing else. The {\i mishna} therefore has to affirmatively teach us that even in this ambiguous case we say they are obligated to bring a regular {\i mincha} from wheat, because we interpret the qualifier as a mistake rather than a precise condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus surfaces a deeper conceptual question running beneath the entire discussion: what is the nature of the mistake being made? If the error is small and within the normal range of confusion, we can confidently say the person\u8217's fundamental intent was to perform the {\i mitzvah} in question, and the qualifier reflects a genuine misunderstanding of the details. But if the error is large \u8212- if the qualifier reflects a radically different conception of the act itself \u8212- the question becomes whether this represents an attempted reversal, a completely different undertaking, or simply a bigger version of the same kind of mistake. These three framings lead to different conclusions, and the {\i amoraim} are divided on which framing correctly captures the logic of the {\i tanna kamma}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai Lock\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the core of the entire sugya is a fundamental disagreement about how to evaluate a declaration that contains an incoherent element. {\i Beis Shammai}\u8217's approach, as it emerges across all the versions of the debate, is essentially one of {\i tefisah} \u8212- locking in the first meaningful word that was uttered. Once someone says {\i nazir}, that word carries halakhic force, and it is exceedingly difficult to walk it back or requalify it. The subsequent clause \u8212- {\i min hagrogros} \u8212- does not redefine what was said; it arrives too late to undo what the first word already accomplished. Whether one explains this as an application of {\i ein adam motzi devarav levatala} (giving the statement force by focusing on its first viable clause), or as {\i ein she\u8217'eila b\u8217'hekdesh} (the principle that {\i nezirut}, like {\i hekdesh}, cannot be dissolved), or as a refusal to permit {\i chazara} once the declaration has taken effect, the result is the same: {\i Beis Shammai} is highly resistant to allowing a speaker to escape a statement they have already made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Hillel Whole\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Hillel}, by contrast, insists on reading the declaration as a whole before assigning it any binding force. {\i Begmar devarav adam nitpas} \u8212- a person is assessed by the entirety of what they said. When we look at the complete statement {\i Hareini nazir min hagrogros}, we see that the speaker\u8217's own conception of {\i nezirut} is built on something that has nothing to do with {\i nezirut}. The statement is not two parts in tension; it is one coherent (if confused) statement that, taken whole, fails to constitute any recognized halakhic act. In the {\i mincha} parallel, this translates to: when the qualifier is radically off, it shows that the speaker was not, at bottom, trying to bring a valid {\i mincha} \u8212- they were trying to do something else, something that does not exist within the halakhic framework, and therefore nothing is binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limits of Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i ein adam motzi devarav levatala} is thus powerful but bounded. It operates most cleanly where the statement is almost right \u8212- where a small terminological error can be corrected by reinterpretation, as in {\i erech kli alai} being read as {\i dmei kli alai}. It becomes strained when the invalid portion of the statement is not a minor terminological error but a fundamental reconception of what the {\i mitzvah} is \u8212- {\i grogros} instead of {\i yayin}, lentil beans instead of {\i solet}. In those cases, {\i Beis Hillel} (and ultimately {\i Chizkiyah} as well) concludes that the speaker simply was not doing what they thought they were doing, and no binding obligation results. {\i Beis Shammai}, unwilling to leave any utterance without halakhic consequence, finds a way to impose one regardless \u8212- but at the cost of effectively ignoring everything the speaker said except the very first word.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 10\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Talking Cow Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cow\u8217's Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 10 opens with what will likely be one of the most memorable {\i mishnayot} encountered in the course of learning {\i Nazir}. The {\i Mishnah} presents the following scenario: a man reports that a cow declared, {\i hareini nezira im omedet ani} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazira} if I stand up.\u8221" Or alternatively, that a door declared, {\i hareini nezira im niftach ani} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nezira} if I am opened.\u8221" The man comes before us reporting, in apparent seriousness, that he witnessed these declarations by a cow and a door.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai/Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai} rules that the man is a {\i nazir} \u8212- not the cow or the door, but the man himself is a {\i nazir}, presumably contingent on whether the stated condition was fulfilled. {\i Beis Hillel} rules that he is not a {\i nazir}. The {\i Gemara} will clarify the precise nature of this dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} adds a qualification: even when {\i Beis Shammai} said the man is a {\i nazir}, they did not mean he becomes a full {\i nazir}. Rather, their intent was only that whoever says {\i harei para zo alai korban im omedet hi} \u8212- \u8220"this cow is upon me as a {\i korban} if it stands\u8221" \u8212- the cow becomes forbidden to him. This is analogous to the earlier {\i Mishnah} where a man says he is a {\i nazir} from dates ({\i develah}): {\i Rabbi Yehuda} held there that the dates become forbidden to him rather than that he actually assumes full {\i nezirut}. So too here, the cow becomes forbidden, not that a full {\i nezirut} takes effect. How this reasoning extends to a talking door is left somewhat open, but presumably the door would become forbidden to him in some analogous sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The natural reaction to all of this is: of all the things one might ask about this {\i Mishnah}, the debate over whether this constitutes {\i nezirut} or a {\i korban}-like prohibition seems almost beside the point. What on earth is going on with a cow that declares itself a {\i nezira}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami bar Chama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks the obvious question: {\i para mi ka mishtaya} \u8212- since when does a cow talk? {\i Rami bar Chama} proposes the following reading: {\i kegon shehayesa para revutza lefanav} \u8212- we are dealing with a case in which a cow was crouched down before the man. He observes it and says, {\i kisvura para zo eina omedet} \u8212- this cow must be thinking that it is not going to be able to stand up. It appears so thoroughly crouched that one would say it has given up on rising.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meat Nazirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that as the backdrop, {\i Rami bar Chama} explains the man\u8217's actual statement as {\i hareini nazir mibesara im amda me\u8217'aleha} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} from its flesh if it stands up on its own. And then the cow did in fact stand up on its own. The condition was thus met. This immediately reduces the {\i Mishnah} to a parallel of the preceding one, since the question now becomes: what does it mean to declare oneself a {\i nazir} from the meat of a cow?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate Repetition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that {\i Beis Shammai} and {\i Beis Hillel} simply follow their respective positions \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'azlu Beis Shammai leshitasan veBeis Hillel leshitasan} \u8212- the same positions they took in the earlier {\i Mishnah} regarding dates and {\i develah}. {\i Beis Shammai} holds that if someone says he is a {\i nazir} from dates or from meat, he is indeed a {\i nazir}, because we look past the odd object named and recognize the underlying intent to accept {\i nezirut}. {\i Beis Hillel} holds that such a declaration is meaningless, since one cannot be a {\i nazir} \u8220"from\u8221" meat any more than one can be a {\i nazir} \u8220"from\u8221" dates, and so he is not a {\i nazir} at all. The immediate question the {\i Gemara} raises is: why must this debate be repeated? {\i Beis Shammai} already stated this position once in the earlier {\i Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two/Three Times\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} answers: {\i trei telas} \u8212- two or three times we are shown variations of the same principle. And so teaches {\i Rabbi Chiya}, and so says {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} as well: there are multiple illustrations of the same debate, and that is intentional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dates/Grapes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} goes further and insists that each case is necessary \u8212- {\i u\u8217'tzricha}. Had the {\i Mishnah} only taught the case of {\i grogros u\u8217'develah}, dates and dried figs, one might have thought that {\i Beis Shammai}\u8217's ruling applies only there. Why? {\i Mishum demichalfin ba\u8217'anavim} \u8212- because dates and grapes are genuinely similar. They are both smallish, darkish fruits. It is not entirely unreasonable for someone to confuse the two, to think that what the {\i Torah} prohibits to a {\i nazir} under the category of grapes somehow extends to or includes dates. And so {\i Beis Shammai} could be saying: you clearly wanted to be a {\i nazir} and simply got confused about which fruit was meant, and therefore your declaration takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meat Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But meat and grapes \u8212- {\i lo michalef} \u8212- nobody confuses those. So if the {\i Mishnah} only taught the case of meat, one might assume the opposite: that the case of dates would not work for {\i Beis Shammai}, since the confusion between dates and grapes is more plausible and {\i Beis Shammai} might be relying on that confusion to impose {\i nezirut}. The case of meat would then need its own logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine/Meat Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That logic runs as follows: {\i bevishra vechamra} \u8212- wine and meat go together. A good meal, a life of sensual indulgence, is classically represented by meat and wine. The institution of {\i nezirut} is understood by many to represent a form of asceticism, a withdrawal from physical pleasure. It is therefore not at all unreasonable to suppose that when a person says \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} from meat,\u8221" they are thinking: a {\i nazir} abstains from the pleasures of meat and wine together. As the {\i Gemara} notes, {\i de\u8217'amar mar}, there is a teaching that an {\i onen} \u8212- a mourner in the acute stage of mourning \u8212- is forbidden from wine and meat. These two are linked as the classic expression of physical indulgence. Indeed, it is worth pausing over this association, because the prohibition of wine for a {\i nazir} is not solely about hedonism. The {\i pasuk} in {\i Yirmiyahu} \u8212- {\i vatashku es hanezirim yayin ve\u8217'al hanevi\u8217'im tzivisem leimor lo tinavu} \u8212- speaks of giving the {\i nezirim} wine to drink and commanding the prophets not to prophesy, suggesting that wine is problematic not merely because of physical pleasure but because it clouds the mind and distracts from devotion to God. Nonetheless, the popular association of {\i nezirut} with abstaining from both wine and meat is understandable, and {\i Beis Shammai} might be saying: when you declared yourself a {\i nazir} from meat, you were really expressing a desire to be a {\i nazir} in the full sense. Had the {\i Mishnah} only told us the meat case, however, I might have thought the dates case is too far-fetched for {\i Beis Shammai} to accept \u8212- dates have no association with {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Door Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And then there is the door. Had the {\i Mishnah} only given us the cases of dates and meat, one might have concluded that {\i Beis Hillel} concedes in those cases \u8212- that even {\i Beis Hillel} agrees that when there is some basis for the confusion or association, the declaration works. The door case eliminates that possibility. By including a case of complete absurdity \u8212- {\i aval deles}, but a door \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} shows that {\i Beis Shammai} maintains its position even when the appended object is total nonsense, and {\i Beis Hillel} maintains its position even in cases where the association might seem coherent. All three cases together establish the full scope of the dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mapik Hey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having understood the {\i Gemara}\u8217's reduction of the {\i Mishnah} to a straightforward debate about declaring oneself a {\i nazir} from meat or from a door, {\i Tosafot} now attempts the harder task: making sense of the actual language of the {\i Mishnah} on its own terms. The {\i Mishnah} says {\i amra para zu} \u8212- this cow said \u8212- and then quotes the cow as saying {\i hareini nezira im omedet ani}. How does one read those words in a way that produces the {\i Gemara}\u8217's conclusion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}\u8217's first approach invokes anthropomorphism. The idea is that we project human intention onto an animal. The cow is crouched and straining \u8212- it {\i looks} as though it is telling us something. Just as the verse in {\i Yonah} says {\i veha\u8217'oniyah chishvah lehishaver} \u8212- the ship {\i thought} it would break \u8212- the ship of course was not literally thinking; the language is anthropomorphic, conveying that the ship was on the verge of breaking. So too here, when the {\i Mishnah} says the cow {\i said} it would be a {\i nezira}, it means the cow\u8217's posture and behavior are {\i telling} us that it cannot stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grammar Fix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That handles the first part of the {\i Mishnah}. But the second part presents a grammatical challenge. The word {\i nezira} is feminine. How does a man declare himself a feminine {\i nezira}? {\i Tosafot} offers a brilliant grammatical observation: the {\i hei} at the end of {\i nezira} is a {\i mapik hei} \u8212- a suffix meaning \u8220"from it.\u8221" So {\i hareini nezira} does not mean \u8220"I will be a {\i nezira}\u8221" in the feminine sense; rather, it means {\i hareini nazir heimena} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} from it, from the cow. This transforms the declaration into exactly what the {\i Gemara} requires: a statement that the man will be a {\i nazir} from the cow\u8217's meat if it stands. The only remaining difficulty is the word {\i ani} at the end \u8212- {\i im omedet ani}, \u8220"if I stand\u8221" \u8212- which does not fit naturally, since it is the cow that would be standing, not the man. {\i Tosafot} acknowledges this difficulty but regards the rest of the reading as compelling despite that loose end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Literal Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}\u8217's second approach takes the {\i Mishnah} more at face value. According to this reading, the man is genuinely reporting a story \u8212- he came and told us: I saw this cow, and it appeared to say that it would be a {\i nezira} if it stood up. The {\i Mishnah} records this as a straightforward narrative of what the man reported.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Story/Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since what the man reports is complete nonsense \u8212- a cow cannot be a {\i nezira} \u8212- we interpret the telling of this absurd story as a {\i de facto} declaration of {\i nezirut} on the man\u8217's part. As {\i Tosafot} puts it: {\i heichah kesheadam mesaper lefaneinu kach bedaito lekayem alav sheyehe nazir mivesara} \u8212- when a person comes and tells us this strange story about a cow, what he is really communicating is that he intends to accept upon himself {\i nezirut} from the cow\u8217's meat. The story about the cow is the vehicle; the real content is his declaration of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that according to this second approach, the primary {\i chiddush} of the {\i Mishnah} shifts. It is no longer simply another illustration of the {\i Beis Shammai}/{\i Beis Hillel} debate about declaring {\i nezirut} from non-grape objects. Rather, the novel ruling would be that we interpret an absurd story about a cow as a declaration of {\i nezirut} altogether \u8212- that we translate bizarre narrative into legally operative speech. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's focus on the {\i Beis Shammai}/{\i Beis Hillel} debate then becomes a secondary point following from that primary {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} now raises an objection to the entire framework of {\i Rami bar Chama}. {\i Mi ktani im amda me\u8217'aleha} \u8212- does the {\i Mishnah} actually say \u8220"if it stood up on its own\u8221"? It does not. And if that is the {\i Gemara}\u8217's complaint, one might ask: of all the liberties one could take with a {\i Mishnah} about a talking cow, why is the insertion of the words \u8220"on its own\u8221" the sticking point? {\i Tosafot} essentially reads {\i Rava}\u8217's objection as a more general dissatisfaction with the entire reading \u8212- a way of saying that the explanation of {\i Rami bar Chama} simply does not reflect what is actually happening in the {\i Mishnah}, and the missing words \u8220"on its own\u8221" are the marker of that problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inverted Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} then offers his own reframing. The cow is still crouched down in front of the man, but {\i Rava} changes the nature of the condition. Rather than the man saying {\i im amda} \u8212- if it stands \u8212- {\i Rava} reads the condition as {\i im lo amda} \u8212- if it does not stand. The man declares {\i hareini nazir miyayin} \u8212- I will be a standard {\i nazir} from wine \u8212- {\i im lo amda} \u8212- if it does not stand. And then the cow stood up on its own \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'amda me\u8217'aleha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might immediately note that {\i Rava} himself is inserting a word \u8212- {\i lo} \u8212- that does not appear in the {\i Mishnah}, having just criticized {\i Rami bar Chama} for inserting the phrase \u8220"on its own.\u8221" {\i Tosafot} acknowledges this asymmetry but suggests that a missing {\i lo} is a more tolerable emendation than a missing qualifying phrase, though the reasoning is admittedly strained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ability to Raise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute between {\i Beis Shammai} and {\i Beis Hillel} now takes on an entirely different character. The man said he would be a {\i nazir} if the cow does not stand. The cow did stand. So why should he be a {\i nazir} at all? {\i Beis Shammai savri tursa de-hai gavra mishum okmei biyadei hu} \u8212- {\i Beis Shammai} holds that the man\u8217's focus, his {\i tursa}, was on whether he himself would be able to get the cow to stand. His statement \u8220"if it does not stand\u8221" was not a neutral observation about the cow\u8217's posture; it was a wager or a declaration of intent: \u8220"If I cannot get it to stand, I will be a {\i nazir}.\u8221" The cow ultimately stood up on its own, but the man was not the one who caused it to stand. The condition \u8212- {\i im lo ukma biyadei}, if I cannot raise it \u8212- was therefore met, and he is a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} asks: where does the {\i Gemara} derive this reading of the man\u8217's psychology? The answer, {\i Tosafot} explains, is that the {\i Gemara} is trying to understand why a person would ever make such a condition. It makes sense if the man was trying to demonstrate his own ability to get the cow up \u8212- essentially making a deal: \u8220"I bet I can get it to stand; if I can\u8217't, I\u8217'll be a {\i nazir}.\u8221" That is an intelligible psychological motivation. The cow\u8217's crouching is what prompts this, and the man\u8217's declaration is really about his own agency in resolving the situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standing Fact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Hillel} disagrees about the {\i tursa}. {\i U-Beis Hillel savri turpei d\u8217'hai gavra mishum terviahu} \u8212- {\i Beis Hillel} holds that the man\u8217's focus was simply on the crouching. He was upset that the cow was crouched down, and his declaration was: \u8220"If this cow doesn\u8217't get up, I\u8217'll be a {\i nazir}.\u8221" It does not matter who or what caused it to stand \u8212- the relevant fact is simply whether the cow ended up on its feet. {\i Ha kama} \u8212- it stood. The condition was met in the most straightforward sense, and he is not a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava}\u8217's reframing now runs into difficulty with {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position. If, as {\i Rava} explains, the man explicitly declared {\i hareini nazir miyayin} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} from wine \u8212- then why does {\i Rabbi Yehuda} limit even {\i Beis Shammai}\u8217's ruling to saying that merely the cow becomes forbidden to him? If the man said outright that he would be a {\i nazir} from wine, there is no basis for {\i Rabbi Yehuda} to reduce the outcome to a mere prohibition on the cow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Must Be Meat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by reverting to the formulation of {\i Rami bar Chama}: the case must be one in which the man said {\i hareini nazir mibesara} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} from its meat \u8212- not from wine. We return, in other words, to the same debate we have seen before: the man declared {\i nezirut} from a non-standard object \u8212- meat \u8212- and the three-way dispute of {\i Beis Shammai}, {\i Beis Hillel}, and {\i Rabbi Yehuda} plays out exactly as it did in the case of dates. {\i Beis Shammai} holds that he is a {\i nazir}, {\i Beis Hillel} holds that he is not, and {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds that the meat becomes forbidden to him. The {\i tna\u8217'i} \u8212- the condition about the cow standing \u8212- is now layered on top of this debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then stages an exchange between the two schools. {\i Beis Shammai} addresses {\i Beis Hillel}: even according to your own logic, concede to us \u8212- {\i odu lan mei\u8217'ha} \u8212- that in this case the man\u8217's {\i tursa} was on the crouching, and the cow stood, so the condition was not met, and he should not be a {\i nazir} even according to you. {\i Beis Shammai} is saying: whatever you think about {\i nezirut} from meat in general, at least agree that in this specific case the condition was fulfilled \u8212- the cow stood \u8212- and therefore {\i Beis Hillel} should not impose {\i nezirut} here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Hillel} responds: {\i lav turpei d\u8217'hai gavra mishum okmei biyadei} \u8212- no, we read the man\u8217's focus as being on {\i his ability} to get the cow to stand, not merely on whether it stood. He was not the one who got it to stand, so the condition \u8212- from {\i Beis Hillel}\u8217's perspective \u8212- was in fact not met, and the man is not a {\i nazir}. {\i Beis Shammai} in turn insists: {\i turpei d\u8217'hai gavra mishum okmei biyadei v\u8217'halo ukme} \u8212- his focus was indeed on whether he could get it to stand, and he did not, so the condition was met and he is a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simple Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stepping back, the simplest way to characterize what the {\i Gemara} has done is this: the talking cow is, in the end, a distraction. Whatever elaborate story the {\i Mishnah} constructs about a cow declaring {\i nezirut} from its crouched position, the operative legal content reduces to a man saying {\i hareini nazir mibesara} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} from its meat \u8212- with some kind of condition attached about whether the cow stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Illustrations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The multiple illustrations \u8212- dates, meat, door, and now the cow \u8212- serve the purpose identified earlier: to demonstrate that the dispute between {\i Beis Shammai} and {\i Beis Hillel} applies across the full range of cases, from the marginally understandable to the completely absurd.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} of the talking cow thus runs in exact parallel to the earlier {\i Mishnah} about dates. {\i Beis Shammai} says the man is a {\i nazir}, {\i Beis Hillel} says he is not, and {\i Rabbi Yehuda} says the cow \u8212- or the dates, or the door \u8212- is forbidden to him. {\i Rava}\u8217's version adds one further layer: a debate about whether the condition embedded in the declaration was actually met, turning on whether the man\u8217's intent was simply that the cow would stand, or specifically that he himself would succeed in raising it. What the talking cow and the talking door have to do with this precise dispute about the nature of a conditional vow remains, to say the least, an interesting question \u8212- but there it is, laid out in the {\i Gemara} of {\i Nazir} daf 10.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 11\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Nezirus Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Hillel/Shammai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 11 opens by returning to the terrain of ordinary {\i nezirus} after the extended discussion of unusual formulations \u8212- including the debate of {\i Beis Hillel}, {\i Beis Shammai}, and {\i Rebbi Yehuda}\u8217's interpretation of {\i Beis Shammai} \u8212- regarding what it means to declare oneself a {\i nazir} from something that is not itself one of the prohibited categories of {\i nezirus}. The question there was whether such a declaration constitutes full {\i nezirus}, no {\i nezirus} at all, or merely a prohibition on the specific item named. With that backdrop in place, the {\i Mishnah} now turns to more standard cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cup of Wine Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i Mishnah} on this daf presents a person who is offered a cup of wine \u8212- {\i mazgu lo es hakos} \u8212- and declares, {\i hareini nazir mimenu}, \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} from it.\u8221" The word {\i mimenu} is somewhat ambiguous: it could mean \u8220"from wine\u8221" generically, or it could mean \u8220"from this particular cup.\u8221" One might therefore have thought that the person was merely declining this specific serving rather than genuinely accepting {\i nezirus}. Nevertheless, the ruling is that such a person is a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial = Full\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling reflects a principle established in an earlier {\i Mishnah}: accepting {\i nezirus} with respect to any one component of {\i nezirus} \u8212- wine, haircuts, or {\i tumas meis} \u8212- constitutes full {\i nezirus}. The position of the {\i Chachamim} is that {\i nezirus} is a package deal; if you accept any piece of it, you have accepted all of it. Since this declaration concerns wine, which is a genuine element of {\i nezirus}, even {\i Beis Hillel} agrees that the person becomes a {\i nazir} in full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that had the person said {\i hareini nazir min hakos hazeh} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} from this cup\u8221" \u8212- the result might well have been different, since that formulation makes explicit that the reference is to a particular vessel rather than to wine as such. The formulation {\i mimenu} is general enough to be read as referring to wine, which is why the ruling applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Drunk Woman Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then presents a contrasting case: {\i maaseh be-isha achas she-haysah shikorah} \u8212- there was a woman who was drunk, and people were offering her another cup of wine. She declared, {\i hareini nezirah mimenu}, \u8220"I am a {\i nezirah} from it\u8221" \u8212- the very same formulation used in the previous case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limited Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite the identical language, the {\i Chachamim} rule that she did not become a {\i nezirah}. Their reasoning is that {\i lo nis-kavnah zo ela lomar harei hu alai k\u8217'korban} \u8212- she merely intended to forbid this specific cup to herself as a {\i neder}, as a {\i konam}. She was not accepting {\i nezirus} from wine generally; she meant only to refuse this particular cup.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately raises a sharp question: why is the context of the second case any different from the first? In both instances, the identical phrase {\i mimenu} was used. The {\i Gemara} presses this directly: {\i maaseh listor}? Is the story being brought in order to contradict the ruling just stated? You declared at the outset that {\i harei zeh nazir}, and then you tell us of a case where the same words yield the opposite result?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining the {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution, it is worth pausing on a broader question raised by this passage. One might invoke the principle of {\i perushan le-hakel}, familiar from {\i Nedarim}, according to which ambiguous {\i nedarim} are read leniently. If {\i nezirus} is a species of {\i neder}, should the same principle not apply here \u8212- permitting the person to explain what he or she actually meant? The {\i Gemara} throughout this tractate has largely not pursued that avenue; rather than asking the declarant what was intended, the discussion proceeds to determine meaning objectively from context and language. The principle of {\i piv v\u8217'libo shavin} \u8212- that words and intent should align \u8212- is one the {\i Gemara} is aware of, but its actual deployment in these {\i sugyos} is more limited than one might expect. The question of when subjective intent can override the plain import of words, and when we say {\i ein piv v\u8217'libo shavin} renders a declaration non-binding, will resurface explicitly later in this {\i daf}. For now, the {\i Gemara} proceeds by asking not what she meant to say, but what, objectively, her situation tells us about what she was saying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chasorei Mechasra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction with the technique of {\i chasorei mechasra vehachi katani} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} is missing a clause and must be read as though it contained an explanatory transition. The missing clause establishes that when an ordinary person, in an ordinary social setting, is offered a cup of wine and says {\i hareini nazir mimenu}, that person is a {\i nazir}; but if the person was drunk at the time and made the same declaration, the result is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Drunk Woman Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strong Language Tactic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then explains why the drunk person\u8217's declaration is treated differently: {\i keman de\u8217'amar harei alai korban hu} \u8212- it is as if the person said this cup is forbidden to me like a {\i korban}. But one might ask: if that is what the person meant, why not simply say those words? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i savar meitina achrina umitza\u8217'aru li} \u8212- the drunk person is thinking that if he or she says merely \u8220"this cup is forbidden to me,\u8221" those around will simply continue offering wine. The person wants to communicate something categorical and absolute, something that will make clear that no further pressure should be applied. By invoking the language of a {\i nazir}, the person is deploying a rhetorical tactic: {\i eima lehu hamiltha}, saying something that signals total abstention, {\i depaska lehu}, cutting off the attempt entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Social Pressure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} elaborates on this reasoning helpfully. The passage describes a social reality: {\i derech b\u8217'nei adam l\u8217'haftzir b\u8217'shikor she\u8217'yishteh} \u8212- it is common practice to pressure a drunk person to keep drinking. People find it amusing to encourage further intoxication, and the drunk person, who genuinely does not wish to drink more, cannot make that refusal stick by merely declining a particular cup. The only language powerful enough to end the pressure is the language of {\i nezirus}, which conveys an absolute and apparently binding commitment. {\i Tosafos} concludes: {\i aval k\u8217'she\u8217'eino shikor ein derech l\u8217'haftziro} \u8212- but when a person is not drunk, there is no social pressure of this kind. If a sober person declines a cup of wine and no one is pressing the matter, there is no need to invoke the language of {\i nezirus} merely to get people to back off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sober Contrast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This contrast explains the ruling in the first part of the {\i Mishnah}. When a sober person, who is under no special pressure to drink, uses the language of {\i nezirus} in response to being offered a cup of wine, that person is genuinely invoking the concept. There is no ulterior communicative purpose being served. The heavy word carries its actual weight, and since the person is declaring {\i nezirus} from wine \u8212- a genuine component of {\i nezirus} \u8212- the full status applies. {\i Tosafos} states this explicitly: {\i v\u8217'l\u8217'kach omer d\u8217'nazir mikulehu} \u8212- when you use the strong word {\i nazir} without any social pressure compelling you toward theatrical language, you really mean to be a {\i nazir} from wine, and being a {\i nazir} from wine pulls along the rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditions on Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i Mishnah} presents a new category of case. A person declares: {\i hareini nazir al mnas she\u8217'ehei shoteh yayin umitamei lameitim} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} on the condition that I may still drink wine\u8221" or \u8220"on the condition that I may still become {\i tamei} through contact with the dead.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditions Void\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is unambiguous: {\i harei zeh nazir v\u8217'assur bekulan} \u8212- this person is a {\i nazir} and is forbidden in all the relevant respects. None of the conditions are honored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two ways to understand why. The first approach, and the one the {\i Gemara} will confirm in the name of Ravina, is that the person has accepted full {\i nezirus} and then attempted to attach conditions to a Torah-mandated status. This triggers the principle of {\i matneh al mah shekatuv ba-Torah}: any stipulation that contradicts what the Torah requires is void. The person has genuinely accepted the {\i nezirus}; we simply discard the conditions. The second approach reads {\i al mnas} not as \u8220"with a condition upon\u8221" the whole of {\i nezirus} but as equivalent to {\i chutz} \u8212- \u8220"except for.\u8221" Under this reading, the person was attempting to accept partial {\i nezirus}, which brings us back to the earlier debate about whether partial acceptance constitutes full {\i nezirus}. The {\i Gemara} will return to both of these frameworks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mistaken Understanding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unaware of Wine Ban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then introduces two further cases involving mistaken understanding. In the first, the person declares {\i nezirus} but states: {\i yodea ani sheyesh nezirot aval eini yodea shehanazir assur beyayin} \u8212- \u8220"I know that the institution of {\i nezirus} exists, but I did not know that a {\i nazir} is forbidden wine.\u8221" Perhaps, he imagined, {\i nezirus} is simply about growing one\u8217's hair long and nothing more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim vs.\u160 ?Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling of the {\i Chachamim} is {\i harei zeh assur} \u8212- this person is bound as a {\i nazir}. Ignorance of one of the {\i halachos} does not void the acceptance. {\i Rabbi Shimon}, however, rules that the person is not a {\i nazir} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Expected Dispensation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case involves a different kind of mistake. Here the person knew full well that a {\i nazir} is forbidden wine, but declares: {\i savur hayiti she-Chachamim matirin li} \u8212- \u8220"I assumed that the rabbis would grant me a dispensation,\u8221" either because he is someone who cannot function without wine, or because his livelihood as a gravedigger requires him to become {\i tamei} regularly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Positions Reversed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Remarkably, the positions of the {\i Chachamim} and {\i Rabbi Shimon} are now reversed. In this third case, the {\i Chachamim} rule {\i muttar} \u8212- this person is not bound as a {\i nazir} \u8212- while {\i Rabbi Shimon} rules {\i assur}. And lest there be any confusion: {\i muttar} here means the person does not become a {\i nazir} at all. A partial {\i nezirus} is not an option except in the case of {\i Shimshon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reversal is genuinely striking, and the {\i Gemara} devotes considerable attention to explaining it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Acceptance L (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Nezirus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before addressing the reversal directly, the {\i Gemara} first asks why there is apparently no debate regarding the first case \u8212- the person who says {\i hareini nazir al mnas she\u8217'ehei shoteh yayin}. If {\i Rabbi Shimon} holds that when a person did not realize wine was included, the {\i nezirus} is not binding \u8212- because the person was in effect only accepting a partial {\i nezirus} \u8212- why does {\i Rabbi Shimon} not say the same when the person explicitly stipulates that wine should be excluded?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer given by {\i Rebbi Yehoshua ben Levi} is that {\i Rabbi Shimon} in fact does dispute that first case as well; it simply was not recorded separately in the {\i Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditions on Torah (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matneh al Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina, however, takes a different position: {\i Rabbi Shimon} genuinely concedes in the first case. The reason is {\i matneh al mah shekatuv ba-Torah}: the person has accepted the full {\i nezirus} and then attempted to impose conditions on a Torah-defined status. The conditions are simply voided, and the full {\i nezirus} stands. This is distinct from the second case, where the person never understood or accepted the full package in the first place. As {\i Tosafos} puts it: {\i d\u8217'k\u8217'sh\u8217'amar hareini nazir chal alav nezirus l\u8217'chol mili} \u8212- when you say {\i hareini nazir}, full {\i nezirus} takes effect immediately on all its components; {\i v\u8217'ki amar al mnas matil tnai al mah shekatuv ba-Torah u\u8217'vatel ha-tnai} \u8212- and when you then say \u8220"on condition,\u8221" you are attempting to make a stipulation against what the Torah prescribes, and that stipulation is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, in the second case, what the person was internally accepting was a {\i nezirus} that did not include the wine prohibition \u8212- because that is all they knew {\i nezirus} to be. That is not a case of full acceptance plus a void condition; it is a case of genuinely partial acceptance. And the question of what partial acceptance amounts to is the subject of the ongoing debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Acceptance L (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that foundation in place, the {\i Gemara} turns to explaining why {\i Rabbi Shimon} and the {\i Chachamim} reverse their positions between the second and third cases. The key is to appreciate that the second case \u8212- where the person did not know wine was prohibited \u8212- is a case of partial {\i nezirus}. In the person\u8217's own conception, {\i nezirus} meant only the hair-related practices; in accepting {\i nezirus}, that is all they were accepting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us back to the debate that was introduced in an earlier {\i Mishnah}. If a person says {\i hareini nazir min ha-yayin}, or any formulation indicating acceptance of only one component of {\i nezirus}, the {\i Chachamim} hold that accepting any piece of {\i nezirus} constitutes accepting the whole thing \u8212- it is a package deal. {\i Rabbi Shimon}, by contrast, holds {\i ad sheyidor mikulan} \u8212- unless you explicitly accept all components, you have accepted nothing; a partial package counts as nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So in the second case, the person was in their mind accepting partial {\i nezirus}. The {\i Chachamim} say partial acceptance equals full {\i nezirus}, and therefore the person is bound. {\i Rabbi Shimon} says partial acceptance equals nothing, and therefore the person goes free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hitur Request\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the third case. Here the person knew exactly what {\i nezirus} entailed. What they believed was that they would receive a {\i heter} \u8212- a dispensation \u8212- because of their particular circumstances. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's most elegant explanation is that this case involves not the process of {\i kabbalah} \u8212- acceptance \u8212- but the process of {\i hatara} \u8212- dissolution. The person in fact accepted full {\i nezirus}, and now, in effect, is coming before the {\i Chachamim} and seeking to be released from one component of it: the prohibition on {\i tumas meis}, because they are a gravedigger.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then becomes: if someone seeks {\i hatara} \u8212- dissolution of a {\i neder} \u8212- on the basis of {\i charata} regarding only one element of the {\i neder}, does that partial {\i charata} dissolve the entire {\i neder}, or must {\i charata} attach to the whole?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Processes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where the elegance of the mirror-image explanation becomes clear. The process of {\i kabbalah} and the process of {\i hatara} are structurally parallel. The {\i Chachamim} hold that just as accepting {\i nezirus} for one component sweeps in the whole, so too being {\i mitcharet} \u8212- expressing regret \u8212- for one component dissolves the whole. {\i Neder she-hutar miktzato hutar kulo}: a {\i neder} that is dissolved in part is dissolved entirely. Therefore, when this person comes with {\i charata} over the gravedigger element, the {\i Chachamim} say that dissolves everything, and the person is {\i muttar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon}, maintaining his consistent position, holds that both processes require totality. Just as you must accept the full package to become a {\i nazir}, you must be {\i mitcharet} over the full package to be released. Partial {\i charata}, focusing only on the {\i tumas meis} component, does not constitute a valid {\i hatara}, and therefore the person remains bound \u8212- {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explanation makes sense at the level of process rather than end-state. One might wonder: if the {\i Chachamim} say that accepting the wine component alone makes one a {\i nazir} for everything, why should expressing {\i charata} over the {\i tumas meis} component dissolve the wine component as well? Shouldn\u8217't the wine component remain in force, and a {\i nazir} who is still obligated on wine is still a {\i nazir} in full? The answer is that we are looking not at what formal obligations happen to remain, but at the act itself: the act of {\i kabbalah} can be partial and still be valid, and the act of {\i hatara} can likewise be partial and still be valid, because the two processes are structurally analogous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Alt Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nidrei Onesim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers an alternative framework for explaining the third case: the dispute concerns {\i nidrei onsin} \u8212- coerced or circumstantially compelled vows. The {\i Mishnah} in {\i Nedarim} records four categories of {\i nedarim} that the {\i Chachamim} released: {\i nidrei ziruzin} (vows of encouragement or haggling), {\i nidrei havai} (exaggerated or nonsensical vows), {\i nidrei shegagot} (vows made under mistaken beliefs about conditions), and {\i nidrei onsin}. A {\i neder ones} is illustrated by a case where one says \u8220"my possessions shall be forbidden to you if you do not come to my celebration,\u8221" and the other person does not come \u8212- but only because of circumstances beyond their control, such as an unavoidable delay. The vow-maker never intended the prohibition to take effect under those conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} applies this framework to the third case: the person who accepted {\i nezirus} thinking they would receive a dispensation is, in some sense, in a position analogous to {\i nidrei onsin} \u8212- they would never have accepted {\i nezirus} had they known it would interfere with their livelihood as a gravedigger, and that interference is a kind of {\i ones}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hatara Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between the {\i Chachamim} and {\i Rabbi Shimon} in this framework tracks the dispute between {\i Shmuel} and {\i Rav Assi}. {\i Rav Yehuda} cites {\i Rav Assi} to the effect that even these four categories of {\i nedarim}, though fundamentally non-binding, still require a formal process of {\i hatarat nedarim} \u8212- the {\i Chachamim} should formally pronounce {\i muttar lach} \u8212- and this is {\i derabbanan}. When this position was reported before {\i Shmuel}, he responded: the {\i Mishnah} says {\i hatiru Chachamim} \u8212- the {\i Chachamim} released them \u8212- meaning they are simply not binding; no formal process is required. The {\i Chachamim} in the {\i Mishnah} on this {\i daf} follow {\i Shmuel}: the {\i neder} is simply not binding, hence {\i muttar}. {\i Rabbi Shimon} follows {\i Rav Assi}: the {\i neder} is not binding in a fundamental sense, but one must still go through a process of dissolution \u8212- meaning it is {\i assur} in the sense that it is presently binding until formally undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Piv V\u8217'libo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} offers a further alternative. Looking at a line in {\i Tosafos} that begins with the word {\i nami}, {\i Tosafos} argues that the third case is not really a matter of {\i nidrei onsin} at all. Rather, it is a case of {\i ein piv v\u8217'libo shavin} \u8212- the person\u8217's words do not match their inner intent. The person said {\i hareini nazir}, but their mental state at the time of declaration was that {\i nezirus} would not require them to give up their gravedigging work. What they said and what they intended diverged fundamentally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate then maps onto the same {\i Amoraic} dispute about {\i ein piv v\u8217'libo shavin}: does this situation require no formal process at all, because the declaration was never truly binding, or does it require going through {\i hatarat nedarim} even though the {\i neder} was fundamentally defective? One position says the {\i neder} is simply not binding and no process is needed; the other says it is presently binding until a formal {\i hatara} is performed, if only to avoid trivializing the whole institution of {\i nedarim}. When we say {\i assur} in this context, we mean not that it is permanently binding, but that it requires formal dissolution \u8212- as in the story of a son who returned from camp troubled by a vow he had made, where the {\i halachic} response of {\i muttar lach, muttar lach, muttar lach} provided the needed closure, whether for his psychological ease or to preserve the gravity of the institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korbanot Responsibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i Mishnah} of the {\i daf} introduces a new scenario. A person declares: {\i hareini nazir v\u8217'alai l\u8217'galeach nazir} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir}, and I accept upon myself the responsibility to shave another {\i nazir},\u8221" which means taking on the financial obligation to bring the {\i korbanot} for another {\i nazir} at the conclusion of that person\u8217's {\i nezirus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Help\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then describes the optimal practical arrangement. If both parties are clever \u8212- {\i im hayu pikchin} \u8212- they will each bring the other\u8217's {\i korbanot}. This way, each person fulfills only one set of {\i korbanot} rather than two, and neither needs to search for a stranger whose {\i korbanot} they have obligated themselves to bring. If they cannot arrange this mutual arrangement, each must go and find another {\i nazir} whose {\i korbanot} they can bring. The {\i Mishnah} notes that the second person declared {\i v\u8217'ani v\u8217'alai l\u8217'galeach nazir} \u8212- \u8220"and me, and I also take responsibility to shave a {\i nazir}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 V\u8217'ani Coverage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This formulation raises a question for the {\i Gemara}: if the second person had said only {\i v\u8217'ani} \u8212- \u8220"and me\u8221" \u8212- without the additional clause {\i v\u8217'alai l\u8217'galeach nazir}, would that abbreviated statement have covered both obligations, the {\i nezirus} itself and the responsibility to bring another\u8217's {\i korbanot}? Or does {\i v\u8217'ani} alone cover only part of what the first person said?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Statement Analys\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full vs.\u160 ?Partial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the {\i Gemara} formulates is precise: does {\i v\u8217'ani} go on {\i kulei dibura} \u8212- covering the full statement made by the first person \u8212- or only {\i palga d\u8217'dibura} \u8212- covering part of it? And if only part, which part: the {\i reisha}, the first element of the declaration (the {\i nezirus} itself), or the {\i seifa}, the concluding element (the responsibility to bring another\u8217's {\i korbanot})?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Text Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings an initial proof from the {\i Mishnah} text itself. Since the {\i Mishnah} had the second person say both {\i v\u8217'ani} and {\i v\u8217'alai l\u8217'galeach nazir}, it appears that both clauses were necessary. If {\i v\u8217'ani} alone had covered everything, why would the {\i Mishnah} have required the additional clause? The inference follows: {\i shma mina v\u8217'ani al techilat dibura mashma} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ani} alone picks up only the first element of the statement, the {\i nezirus}, while the {\i v\u8217'alai l\u8217'galeach} clause is necessary to cover the second obligation. This reading seems natural: one would expect \u8220"and me\u8221" to echo the most immediate referent \u8212- the {\i nezirus} \u8212- leaving the additional responsibility to be stated explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 V\u8217'ani Omnibus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua} challenges this proof before Rava. He argues that {\i v\u8217'ani} could in fact cover everything \u8212- both the {\i nezirus} and the obligation to bring another\u8217's {\i korbanot}. If so, why does the {\i Mishnah} have the second person also say {\i v\u8217'alai l\u8217'galeach}? The answer is that this added phrase is not adding halachic content; it is simply explanatory \u8212- {\i alai be-ha milta}, \u8220"upon me, this thing,\u8221" meaning the speaker is spelling out what {\i v\u8217'ani} already implied. The {\i Mishnah} gives us a full sentence not because the sentence was required to create the halachic effect, but for the sake of clarity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In support of this reading, {\i Rav Huna} points to the {\i Mishnah} that follows in the text: {\i harei alai l\u8217'galeach chatzi nazir}, where a person accepts responsibility to bring the {\i korbanot} for half of another {\i nazir}\u8217's obligations. There too, the second person says a full sentence \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ani alai l\u8217'galeach chatzi nazir} \u8212- even though in that case only one thing is at stake, namely the responsibility for another\u8217's {\i korbanot}, with no personal {\i nezirus} involved. If {\i v\u8217'ani} were truly insufficient and required supplementation, why does the {\i Mishnah} not simply record that the second person said {\i v\u8217'ani} in a case where there is only one thing to echo? The fact that the {\i Mishnah} still has that person say a full sentence suggests that the full sentence is the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's way of giving an example of what was said, not a halachic requirement. And if so, in our case as well, {\i v\u8217'ani} alone would have sufficed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejects this argument. His reasoning is that the two {\i Mishnayos} can be read together in a way that avoids making both of their full-sentence formulations superfluous. If in the first {\i Mishnah} \u8212- our case \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ani} was insufficient and the additional clause was genuinely necessary, then we can account for the second {\i Mishnah}\u8217's full sentence by saying: {\i tani seifa d\u8217'lo tzricha mishum reisha d\u8217'tzricha} \u8212- the {\i seifa} includes a clause that was not technically required, but it does so in order to parallel the {\i reisha} where the clause was required. This is an acceptable halachic literary technique: one case is formulated in full because it needed to be, and the parallel case adopts the same formulation for consistency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Supplementation Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rava argues that the reverse reading creates an insupportable result. If {\i v\u8217'ani} already covers everything, then in both {\i Mishnayos} the additional phrases are purely superfluous. Having two independent cases each containing a legally unnecessary clause \u8212- purely for stylistic reasons \u8212- is not how a {\i halacha} text operates. One can accept superfluity in the {\i seifa} where it mirrors necessary language in the {\i reisha}, but one cannot accept gratuitous superfluity in both. Therefore Rava concludes: {\i v\u8217'ani} alone, without supplementation, is insufficient to create the full halachic effect of both obligations. The first {\i Mishnah} required both clauses because both clauses do real work: {\i v\u8217'ani} picks up the {\i nezirus}, and {\i v\u8217'alai l\u8217'galeach} adds the obligation to bring the other\u8217's {\i korbanot}. Here the {\i daf} concludes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 12\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agent & Unknown Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the bottom of 11b, introducing a {\i sugya} that, at first glance, appears to have little connection to the topic at hand. To recall the context: the {\i mishnah} dealt with two men, each of whom declared, \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} and I accept upon myself to shave a {\i nazir}\u8221" \u8212- meaning, to bring another {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korbanot}. The {\i mishnah} observes that if they are clever, they will simply take care of each other\u8217's {\i korbanot}, a kind of mutual arrangement not unlike the practice of two poor people exchanging {\i Mishloach Manot} so that each fulfills his obligation for {\i Seudas Purim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unspecified Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that {\i mishnah} in mind, the {\i gemara} cites a ruling of Rav Yosef bar Yosef in the name of Rabbi Yochanan. If a man says to his {\i shaliach}, \u8220"{\i Tze ve-kadesh li isha stam}\u8221" \u8212- go out and marry any woman for me, whoever says yes first \u8212- and the {\i shaliach} departs but never returns with any report, then the man who sent him is forbidden to marry any woman in the entire world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning proceeds in two steps. First, there is a presumption \u8212- {\i chazaka shaliach oseh shelichuto} \u8212- that an agent carries out his mission. We therefore assume the {\i shaliach} did in fact betroth someone. Second, since the man gave no specification as to which woman the {\i shaliach} should approach, he has no idea who his {\i shaliach} married on his behalf. Any woman he might now wish to marry could be a sister, mother, or daughter of the woman who is already, unknowingly, his wife. He is therefore forbidden to everyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling calls to mind, in a somewhat whimsical vein, the {\i vort} that {\i Lavan} sought to destroy everything ({\i bi-kesh Lavan avinu la-akor et hakol}) \u8212- for if {\i Avraham}\u8217's servant had married a woman without specification and never returned, {\i Yitzchak} would have been unable to marry anyone, since every woman in the world might be a relative of that unknown wife. The joke collapses, of course, since {\i Avraham} did in fact give {\i Eliezer} specificity \u8212- \u8220"{\i lo sikach isha livni mibnos Kena\u8217'ani}\u8221" \u8212- but the {\i gemara} here serves as its basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Fine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot immediately raises an obvious difficulty: if this man is forbidden to every woman because any one of them might be his wife\u8217's sister or mother, then by the same logic, how can any man in the entire world marry any woman? Perhaps the woman he wishes to marry is the very woman the {\i shaliach} betrothed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot resolves this by invoking the position of Rabbeinu Tam that women are believed when they testify that they were not the ones who accepted {\i kiddushin}. So any adult woman can simply testify on her own behalf. But this does not help with {\i ketanot} \u8212- minor girls who would not know whether their father accepted {\i kiddushin} on their behalf. How, then, can anyone marry a minor?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot answers that on a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} level, the man can actually marry any woman. We follow the majority \u8212- {\i azlinan batar ruba} \u8212- since most women are not relatives of the woman the {\i shaliach} married. The prohibition on the man himself is not a genuine {\i issur} but rather a {\i knas b\u8217'alma} \u8212- a fine \u8212- imposed upon him for having sent a {\i shaliach} with no specification at all, handing him a blank check without identifying even a general pool of eligible women. Other people in the world are not subject to this fine; it is targeted solely at the one who acted irresponsibly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kavua Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot presses further: even granting the majority principle, why should this not constitute a {\i kavua} situation and therefore trigger a fifty-fifty {\i safek} rather than a lenient {\i rov}? The principle {\i kol kavua k\u8217'mechtza al mechtza dami} holds that when a forbidden thing occupies a fixed place, we cannot simply dissolve it into percentage probabilities; we must treat it as an even doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The classic illustration: if one finds a piece of meat on the ground and does not know which butcher shop it came from, and the majority of shops in the neighborhood sell kosher meat, one may follow the majority and assume it is kosher \u8212- {\i kol d\u8217'parish meruba parish}, what has separated itself has separated from the majority. But if one walked into a store, purchased meat, and later cannot remember which store one entered, the analysis changes. One is not simply asking about the identity of the meat in the abstract; one is asking which specific store \u8212- each standing in its fixed place \u8212- one walked into. Since each store is concrete and recognizable, not dissolved into an abstract mixture, we treat the question as fifty-fifty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is difficult to articulate precisely, and the {\i gemara} cites a {\i pasuk} as its basis. One way to approach it: when the forbidden option has a fixed, concrete identity that stands before you, you cannot simply think away from it by invoking percentages. It is not lost in a mixture. The forbidden store is right there; you cannot pretend it is only abstractly possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the question of whether the women in this case should constitute {\i kavua} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} went to wherever the woman was, she was in her home, that is a fixed place, analogous to the store he entered. Why should we not apply {\i kavua} and treat every woman as a fifty-fifty doubt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's answer is that {\i kavua} applies only when the forbidden thing is distinctly recognizable as itself \u8212- {\i nikkar l\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- and the permitted thing is equally recognizable as itself. You know which store sells {\i treif}, you know which sells {\i kosher}; you simply do not know which one you entered. That is the scenario where you cannot ignore the concrete possibility. But in the case of the woman, it is not that you have a woman standing before you whom you know to be your wife\u8217's sister but are unsure whether you entered \u8220"that house.\u8221" It is simply a question of identity in the abstract: you do not know who the {\i shaliach} married, and you do not know whether this woman has any relationship to that unknown person. That is closer in structure to finding meat on the floor \u8212- a question of abstract identity \u8212- not to walking into a fixed store. {\i Kavua}, therefore, does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reish Lakish Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bird Pairs Lost\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish poses a challenge to Rabbi Yochanan from a related domain. There is a category of {\i korban} \u8212- brought by a woman who has given birth, a {\i zavah}, or a {\i zav} \u8212- that consists of a pair of birds, one to serve as a {\i chatas} and one as an {\i olah}. One may purchase these as a unit, a {\i kan}, and both birds are immediately sanctified, but their respective identities \u8212- which is the {\i chatas} and which the {\i olah} \u8212- remain unspecified until a later point. This is called a {\i kan stuma}, an unexplicated pair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one bird from a {\i kan stuma} flies away, or flies into a group of birds that must be put down and is lost among them, or dies, the remaining bird can simply be paired with a new replacement, since no identity has yet been fixed. This is the advantage of the {\i stuma} arrangement. Had the pair been explicit \u8212- {\i kan meforeshas} \u8212- the loss of one bird would have been far more problematic, since one would not know whether the {\i chatas} or the {\i olah} had been lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge Reish Lakish raises concerns all the {\i kinim} in the world. One of these birds has flown somewhere out there, carrying a sanctity \u8212- an unresolved {\i kedushat korban} \u8212- as it wanders. How can anyone ever bring a bird as a {\i korban} again? Any bird one now brings might be that escaped bird, already designated for someone else\u8217's {\i korban}. The parallel to Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling is exact: just as any woman might be the {\i shaliach}\u8217's unknown wife, any bird might be the escaped {\i korban} bird.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that this challenge is not simply about other people. Even the person who lost the bird faces the question: when he goes to bring a new {\i korban} using birds, how does he know that none of these birds is his own escaped one?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed Places\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's response draws precisely the distinction Tosafot had articulated. {\i Kamina ana isha d\u8217'lo nayda} \u8212- I am speaking of a woman, who does not simply fly off. People have fixed places; they live somewhere, they can be found. Even if a woman goes to the marketplace, {\i hadar lenichusa} \u8212- she returns home. Women are therefore more analogous to the stores standing in their fixed places than to things dissolved into an anonymous mixture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Birds, by contrast, are fundamentally mobile. A bird that has flown out into the world is genuinely lost in the mass of birds everywhere. That is a question of abstract identity \u8212- like the piece of meat found on the floor \u8212- and for such cases one simply follows the majority. There is no {\i kavua} concern because there is no fixed place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognizable Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following on from Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's response, the {\i gemara} cites Rava, who clarifies the limits of the position. Rabbi Yochanan concedes that if the woman one wishes to marry has no living relatives whatsoever \u8212- no daughter, granddaughter, mother, grandmother, or sister \u8212- one may marry her even under the shadow of the unknown {\i shaliach} marriage, since there is no relative whose identity could create a problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more striking ruling is this: even if the woman did have a sister \u8212- and that sister was married to another man at the time the {\i shaliach} was appointed \u8212- one may marry this woman. The reasoning is {\i d\u8217'ba\u8217'ei sha\u8217'ata d\u8217'kamar lei havah nasuy l\u8217'gavrei}: at the moment the {\i shaliach} was appointed, the sister was already married to someone else. She was, at that moment, unavailable. And the principle is {\i ki mashvi shaliach bemilta d\u8217'kaima kamei} \u8212- one only empowers a {\i shaliach} in matters that stand before one as currently available options. {\i Bemilta d\u8217'lo kaima kamei lo mashvi shaliach} \u8212- for things not currently available, one simply does not grant the {\i shaliach} power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking and counterintuitive {\i halacha}. The implication is that even if the sister subsequently became divorced \u8212- after the {\i shaliach} was appointed but before he carried out the marriage \u8212- and even if the {\i shaliach} then married her, it would not be binding, because he was never empowered to take that action. His authority was fixed at the moment of appointment, limited to what was then accessible. The {\i gemara} will develop the implications of this principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agent Empowerment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Divorced Later\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule that a {\i shaliach} is empowered only in currently available matters now connects directly to the {\i mishnah}. But before reaching that connection, the {\i gemara} pauses to clarify what the underlying principle actually is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case in question: I sent a {\i shaliach} to marry a woman for me. This woman has a sister who, at the time I appointed him, was married. Subsequently \u8212- sometime during the year I have not heard from the {\i shaliach} \u8212- the sister gets divorced. Can I now marry this sister?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is yes, and the reason is that the {\i shaliach} was never empowered to marry the sister in the first place, because she was unavailable at the time of appointment. Since she could not have been the woman he married on my behalf, she poses no danger of being my wife or my wife\u8217's sister. I may therefore marry her freely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario that creates the genuine difficulty is the following: the {\i shaliach} departs, the sister subsequently gets divorced, and the {\i shaliach} \u8212- now encountering the sister in her state of availability \u8212- goes ahead and betroths her. Why should this not be binding? He is marrying a perfectly available woman, on behalf of a man who has authorized him to marry anyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thought vs.\u160 ?Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might understand the limitation as a matter of {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- the man\u8217's intention at the time of appointment. When he sent the {\i shaliach}, he was only thinking about women who were then available; the sister simply was not in his mind. This reading suggests the limitation is cognitive: if he had explicitly said \u8220"and for any woman who becomes available later,\u8221" it would work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} moves toward a different and more fundamental understanding: this is not merely a question of what was in the man\u8217's mind. It is a structural limitation on the power of {\i shelichut} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Current Availability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} articulates the principle explicitly: {\i lo mashvei inish shaliach ela bemidi demati avid hashta} \u8212- a person can only empower a {\i shaliach} in something that he himself is currently capable of doing. {\i Bemidi delo mati avid lei hashta, lo mashvei} \u8212- for something outside his current capability, he simply cannot grant the power of agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable limitation. It seems to say that the scope of {\i shelichut} is not merely what one intends or instructs, but is bounded by what one could actually have executed at the moment of appointment. If I cannot do a given act right now \u8212- even though I will be able to do it later, and even though by the time the {\i shaliach} acts the situation will have changed \u8212- I never succeeded in granting the {\i shaliach} that power in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Nazirite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to the {\i mishnah} now becomes clear. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i bishlama batra} \u8212- it is understandable that the second man\u8217's vow to care for a {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korbanot} can include the first man, who was already a {\i nazir} when the second man made his declaration. {\i Ela kamai} \u8212- but what about the first man? When he declared that he would take care of a {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korbanot}, the second man had not yet become a {\i nazir}. How can his vow cover someone who did not yet exist as a {\i nazir}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by reading the vow\u8217's intent: {\i im eshkachna dehavi nazir} \u8212- what the man meant was, whoever I find to be a {\i nazir} when the time comes for me to fulfill my vow. He is not committing to care for a specific, currently existing {\i nazir}; he is committing to care for whoever will be a {\i nazir} at the relevant time. This reading suggests that one\u8217's commitments naturally extend to future contingencies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the same logic, one might argue that when I send a {\i shaliach} to marry a woman, I implicitly mean: whoever is available when you get around to it, including women who become available after I send you. {\i Im eshkachna demigaresha} \u8212- if you find a woman who has since been divorced, go ahead and marry her for me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i lo mashvei inish shaliach ela bemidi demati avid hashta}. Even if this is within my {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- even if this is what I intended \u8212- the structural rule of {\i shelichut} prevents it. I cannot grant power for something I could not have done at the moment of appointment. The {\i mishnah} case works for a different reason: the man is not appointing a {\i shaliach} at all. He is making a personal vow about his own future action, and personal vows can certainly be directed at future contingencies. The limit applies to {\i shelichut}, not to personal undertakings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vow Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guardian Annuls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of {\i shelichut} and current availability raises a further question, tested against a case from the laws of vow annulment. If a man, about to depart on a journey, tells his {\i epitropos} \u8212- his estate manager, the one who holds his power of attorney \u8212- to annul any vow his wife makes during his absence, and the {\i epitropos} does indeed annul such vows upon hearing them, can those annulments stand?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yoshiya vs.\u160 ?Yonatan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yoshiya says no. He derives this from the verse {\i isha yekimenu v\u8217'isha yiferenu} \u8212- the husband himself must confirm or annul; there is a specific textual requirement that the husband perform this act personally. An agent cannot substitute for the husband here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yonatan disagrees: {\i matzinu bechol makom she-shlucho shel adam kemoto} \u8212- we find everywhere that one\u8217's agent is as oneself. The one place in the {\i mishnah} where this principle is stated explicitly is in the context of a {\i shaliach tzibur} for {\i Shemoneh Esrei}, as cited in Berachot 34b. Why should the principle not apply here as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Obstacle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now uses this debate to test the principle of {\i shelichut} and current availability. The husband who appoints the {\i epitropos} cannot himself annul his wife\u8217's future vows at the moment of appointment, because those vows do not yet exist. The question is whether this constitutes an argument against appointing the {\i epitropos} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} reasons: if we assume that the husband himself is incapable of pre-annulling vows \u8212- that is, if we follow the {\i chachamim} who say in a related context that {\i lo matzi mefer} \u8212- then the very act of appointing an {\i epitropos} is a case of empowering a {\i shaliach} for something one cannot currently do. And yet Rabbi Yonatan says it works. This seems to directly contradict the principle that one can only empower a {\i shaliach} in currently available matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by suggesting that perhaps both Rabbi Yoshiya and Rabbi Yonatan are operating according to the view of Rabbi Eliezer, who holds that a husband {\i can} pre-annul his wife\u8217's future vows. Under Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view, there is no contradiction: the husband already has the current capability to annul future vows himself, and he therefore has the full power to delegate that capability to an {\i epitropos}. The reason he is appointing an {\i epitropos} rather than simply pre-annulling the vows himself is not inability but practicality \u8212- he might forget when he returns, or he might be angry, or distracted, and the annulment window will close.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-annul Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this only pushes the question back: if Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position allows the husband to pre-annul his wife\u8217's vows right now, why bother with the {\i epitropos} at all? Just do it yourself before you leave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this. Looking at the {\i tosafot} discussion of {\i dilma mishtalyana}, he explains that a husband who pre-annuls his wife\u8217's vows explicitly \u8212- saying to her directly, \u8220"any vow you make while I am away, I hereby void\u8221" \u8212- creates a confrontation. {\i Tipul ketata beineihem}: it leads to a fight. It is disempowering to a wife to be told before any vow is even made that her vows mean nothing. But if the {\i epitropos} handles it quietly in the husband\u8217's absence, reacting to each vow as it arises, the wife\u8217's dignity is preserved and a direct confrontation is avoided. The husband prefers not to raise the issue at home before he leaves \u8212- and certainly not face-to-face \u8212- which is precisely why he makes the arrangement quietly with the {\i epitropos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's own answer to this question is somewhat unsatisfying, and Tosafot\u8217's response fills the gap. In any case, the {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is that the limitation on {\i shelichut} \u8212- that one can only empower an agent in currently available matters \u8212- remains intact. The {\i epitropos} case does not contradict it, because under Rabbi Eliezer, the husband does have current capability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half Nazirite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Men Half\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i mishnah} at hand. The next {\i mishnah} presents the following case: one man says {\i harei alai legaleiach chatzi nazir} \u8212- I accept upon myself to shave half a {\i nazir}, meaning to bring the {\i korbanot} for half a {\i nazir}. Another man hears and says the same thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir vs.\u160 ?Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir says: each of them is obligated to bring a full set of {\i korbanot} for a {\i nazir shaleim}. The {\i chachamim} say: each is only obligated to bring half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that the second man is structurally superfluous to the core debate; the real question is simply what the phrase {\i alai legaleiach chatzi nazir} means for a single individual. The {\i mishnah} introduces two men only to parallel the structure of the previous {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grammar of Half\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava clarifies the terms of the dispute. Everyone would agree on the following: if one explicitly says {\i chatzi korbanot nazir alai} \u8212- half of the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir} I accept upon myself \u8212- one brings half the {\i korbanot}. The word {\i chatzi} is clearly modifying {\i korbanot}, and half an offering is what one owes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if one says {\i korbanot chatzi nazir alai} \u8212- the {\i korbanot} of a half-{\i nazir} I accept upon myself \u8212- one brings a full set of {\i korbanot}. Why? Because {\i d\u8217'ha lo askan nezirut le-palga} \u8212- there is no such thing as a half-{\i nazir}. A person who is half a {\i nazir} is simply a {\i nazir}. \u8220"Half a {\i nazir}\u8221" is simply a {\i nazir}, and whoever is such a person has a full complement of {\i korbanot}. When one says {\i chatzi nazir}, the word {\i chatzi} attaches to the person, and that person\u8217's {\i korbanot} are full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute in the {\i mishnah} concerns the language {\i alai legaleiach chatzi nazir}, which reads as though {\i chatzi} is modifying {\i nazir} \u8212- in which case, following the above logic, one would owe a full {\i korban}. This is, on its face, Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. What requires explanation is why the {\i chachamim} read this differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's reasoning: {\i keivan de\u8217'amar harei alai}, once one has said {\i harei alai}, one has obligated oneself in the full {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut}. When one then says {\i chatzi nazir}, that addition is simply vacuous \u8212- {\i lav kol kemeineih} \u8212- it cannot undo what {\i harei alai} already established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim}\u8217's reasoning: {\i neder u-fetach imo hu} \u8212- there is a vow and its opening together. The latter part of the phrase explains and limits the earlier part. The entire declaration must be read as a unit, with the end clarifying the scope of the beginning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Grammar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains puzzling is why, if the phrase {\i alai legaleiach chatzi nazir} looks structurally parallel to {\i korbanot chatzi nazir}, it should produce a different result for the {\i chachamim}. In both cases, {\i chatzi} seems to be modifying the word {\i nazir}, which should yield a full {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot grapples with this. The conclusion Tosafot reaches is that the presence of the word {\i alai} up front, before {\i legaleiach}, makes a structural difference. When one says {\i harei alai legaleiach chatzi nazir}, the {\i alai} \u8212- the acceptance of obligation \u8212- comes first, and then {\i chatzi} follows. According to the {\i chachamim}, {\i chatzi} is thus understood as modifying the {\i alai}, the scope of what one is accepting: I am accepting upon myself half of the obligations involved. This is equivalent in import to saying {\i chatzi korbanot nazir alai}, with {\i chatzi} clearly modifying the quantum of obligation rather than the identity of the {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It must be admitted that this distinction is subtle, and the grammatical argument does not yield itself to clean exposition. The fundamental point, however, is that the debate turns on whether the word {\i chatzi} in this construction limits the person \u8212- a half-{\i nazir}, who is a full {\i nazir} \u8212- or limits the obligation \u8212- half the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Upon Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the daf introduces a new type of conditional {\i nazir} vow. A man declares: {\i hareini nazir liksheyihyeh li ben} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} when I have a son. If a son is subsequently born to him, he becomes a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumtum Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What if the child born is a daughter, or a {\i tumtum} \u8212- a child whose genitalia are covered, making the sex indeterminate \u8212- or an {\i androgynos}, a child with both male and female genitalia? In all these cases, {\i eino nazir} \u8212- he is not a {\i nazir}. The vow specified a {\i ben}, a definite male child. Even a {\i tumtum}, who represents a {\i safek} as to sex, does not satisfy the condition, because when one says {\i ben} one means a certain, unambiguous male.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the man had said {\i kesheyihyeh li valad} \u8212- when I have a child \u8212- then even a daughter, a {\i tumtum}, or an {\i androgynos} triggers the {\i nezirus}, since the word {\i valad} encompasses all offspring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If his wife miscarried, {\i eino nazir} \u8212- he is not a {\i nazir}, because the vow specified {\i nolad}, the child being born. A miscarriage does not satisfy {\i nolad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doubtful Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon, however, takes a different position. He holds that if the fetus miscarried but might have been viable \u8212- {\i ben kayama} \u8212- and might have been male, then the man is in a state of {\i safek nezirus}. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's reasoning is twofold: first, he considers a miscarriage of a potentially viable fetus to qualify as {\i leidah} for the purposes of this vow; second, he treats even a {\i safek} outcome as sufficient to create a {\i safek} vow obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Being a {\i safek nazir} is a deeply problematic position. One must observe {\i nezirus} because one might be a {\i nazir}, but one cannot bring the concluding {\i korbanot} because one might not be a {\i nazir} at all. The situation is entirely unresolvable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon therefore prescribes a declaration to escape the bind. The man should say: if the first child was a viable male \u8212- {\i im haya ben kayama} \u8212- then {\i harei ani nazir hova}, I am now completing the obligatory {\i nezirus} already triggered. And if it was not \u8212- {\i ve-im lav} \u8212- then {\i harei ani nazir nedava}, I am now taking on {\i nezirus} as a voluntary act. By this formula, the man commits himself either way to observing {\i nezirus} and may bring his {\i korbanot}, since regardless of which scenario obtains, he is now definitively a {\i nazir} under one heading or the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Child\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the wife then gives birth a second time \u8212- a viable child \u8212- the {\i chachamim}\u8217's analysis is straightforward: the second birth is what triggers the {\i nezirus}, and {\i harei zeh nazir} \u8212- he is a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rabbi Shimon, however, the situation is more layered. Since the first child already placed the man in a state of {\i safek nezirus}, the second child now creates a new {\i safek}: perhaps the first child was the triggering event, in which case the {\i nezirus} has already been completed (or is in process), and the second child is irrelevant. Or perhaps the first was not triggering, and the second is the true trigger \u8212- creating yet another {\i safek nezirus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon again prescribes a two-part declaration. The man should say: if the first child was viable, {\i harishon hova} \u8212- the first period of {\i nezirus} I am now completing was the obligatory one, and {\i vezu nedava} \u8212- the {\i nezirus} I now take on for the second child is voluntary. If the first was not viable, {\i harishon nedava} \u8212- the first period was voluntary, and {\i vezu hova} \u8212- this one, for the second child, is the obligatory fulfillment of my vow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 13\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 13. We begin by revisiting the {\i mishnah} at the bottom of 12b, which was briefly introduced at the close of the previous session but requires a full reading before proceeding to the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son/Daughter Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i hareini nazir l\u8217'khsheyihyeh li ven} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if I have a son.\u8221" If a son is indeed born, {\i harey zeh nazir} \u8212- he becomes a {\i nazir}. But what if the child born is a daughter, a {\i tumtum}, or an {\i androgynous}? A {\i tumtum} is a person whose genitalia is concealed, so one cannot determine whether the child is male or female. An {\i androgynous} child possesses both sets of genitalia, raising its own {\i halachic} classification questions. In all such cases, the {\i mishnah} rules {\i eino nazir} \u8212- he does not become a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implied {\i chiddush} here is that when a man vows {\i nezirut} contingent on having a {\i ben}, a {\i tumtum} does not suffice to trigger the vow even as a {\i safek}, because {\i ben} is understood to mean a definite son. If, however, he had said {\i l\u8217'khsheyihyeh li vlad} \u8212- \u8220"when I have a child\u8221" \u8212- then even if the child born is a daughter, a {\i tumtum}, or an {\i androgynous}, he becomes a {\i nazir}, since he used the more inclusive term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miscarriage Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i hipilah ishto eino nazir} \u8212- if his wife miscarried, he is not a {\i nazir}. The underlying assumption of the {\i mishnah} appears to be that if the miscarried child had been considered viable \u8212- {\i kalu lo chadashav}, having reached full term \u8212- it would in principle fall within the category of {\i vlad} and potentially trigger the vow. Nonetheless, the {\i tanna kamma} rules that the person is not a {\i nazir}, and the {\i gemara} will explain why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an important distinction worth noting here between a {\i nefel} \u8212- a miscarriage of a non-viable fetus \u8212- and what might be termed {\i nolad met}, a stillbirth of a child that had been viable until the moment of death. The {\i mishnah} uses the term {\i hipilah} precisely because the status of the child is uncertain, and that uncertainty is what drives the {\i halachic} discussion that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma vs R. Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The position of the {\i tanna kamma} is identified by the {\i gemara} as that of Rabbi Yehuda, who holds {\i lo nachis inish nafshai l\u8217'safeka} \u8212- a person who makes a conditional vow does not intend to be bound when the condition is in doubt. The reasoning is that being a {\i safek nazir} is worse than being a {\i vadai nazir}: you must observe all the restrictions of {\i nezirut} but have no means of ending it, since you cannot bring the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir} without certainty that you are one. Rabbi Yehuda therefore concludes that the person intends his vow to take effect only if the condition is definitely fulfilled. Since there is doubt whether the miscarried child was viable, the vow does not take effect at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees and holds that the person is indeed a {\i safek nazir} and must grapple with that status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek to Vadai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon provides a practical resolution. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i yomar} \u8212- the person should declare \u8212- {\i im hayah ben kayama, hareini nazir chovah} \u8212- \u8220"if that child was viable, I am a {\i nazir} by virtue of my original vow.\u8221" {\i V\u8217'im lav, hareini nazir nedavah} \u8212- \u8220"and if it was not viable, I now accept {\i nezirut} as a freewill gift.\u8221" By conditioning the current {\i nezirut} on both possibilities, the person transforms himself from a {\i safek nazir} into a {\i vadai nazir} in either case. He can then complete his {\i nezirut}, bring his {\i korbanot}, and be done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Child Born\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses the scenario where {\i chazrah v\u8217'yaldah} \u8212- his wife subsequently gives birth to a living child. According to the {\i tanna kamma}, the first miscarriage did not satisfy the condition, so this birth does. The person is unambiguously a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Shimon, however, the situation is once again uncertain. It is possible that the first child was viable and the first {\i nezirut} was obligatory, making the current birth trigger only a new freewill consideration. Or the first child was not viable, in which case the first {\i nezirut} was freewill, and now the obligatory {\i nezirut} has been triggered. Rabbi Shimon applies the same resolution: {\i yomar, im harishon ben kayama, harishon chovah v\u8217'zo nedavah} \u8212- \u8220"if the first was viable, my first {\i nezirut} was obligatory and this new one I accept as freewill.\u8221" {\i V\u8217'im lav, harishon nedavah v\u8217'zo chovah} \u8212- \u8220"if the first was not viable, my first was freewill and this one is my obligatory {\i nezirut}.\u8221" For each ambiguous birth, the person resolves {\i safek nezirut} into {\i vadai nezirut} through conditional declaration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with {\i hai mai l\u8217'memra} \u8212- what is the point? The opening clause of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- that one who vows {\i nezirut} contingent on having a son, and has a son, becomes a {\i nazir} \u8212- seems self-evident. The {\i gemara} answers that the {\i chiddush} lies in the subsequent clause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben vs Child\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first addresses why it was necessary to state the case of {\i ben}. Perhaps, the {\i gemara} suggests, one might have thought that {\i ben} is not strictly \u8220"son\u8221" but rather derives from the root {\i l\u8217'hibanot}, meaning \u8220"to be built\u8221" \u8212- and thus {\i ben} functions as a generic term for child. The {\i mishnah} therefore teaches {\i kamashma lan d\u8217'lo} \u8212- we do not interpret it that way. {\i Ben} means son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is admittedly not fully satisfying. There are many other words in biblical Hebrew that function as generic terms for offspring. The more interesting {\i chiddush}, one might argue, concerns the {\i tumtum}: a {\i tumtum} is a {\i safek} \u8212- either a son or daughter \u8212- so why does the presence of a {\i tumtum} not trigger even a {\i safek} obligation? Moreover, what happens if some time later the membrane covering the {\i tumtum}\u8217's genitalia is removed and the child is discovered to be male \u8212- does one retroactively become a {\i nazir}? The {\i gemara} does not pursue this line of inquiry, but it is a striking question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumtum Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} similarly questions the {\i chiddush} of the {\i vlad} case. If he said \u8220"when I have a child,\u8221" it seems obvious that even a daughter, {\i tumtum}, or {\i androgynous} would qualify. The {\i gemara} responds that one might have thought {\i vlad d\u8217'michshav beinei inshi ba\u8217'inan} \u8212- that \u8220"child\u8221" means a child considered significant in the eyes of people, which is to say, a son. Society\u8217's historical preference for male children is the subtext of the {\i gemara}\u8217's observation. The {\i mishnah} therefore teaches that we take his words literally: he said {\i vlad}, and any child qualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the {\i chiddush} of the miscarriage case, which is identified as the real crux of the {\i mishnah}. The question of who holds this position leads the {\i gemara} to identify the {\i tanna kamma} as Rabbi Yehuda, based on an earlier ruling involving a conditional vow about a threshing floor. The principle is the same: {\i lo nachis inish nafshai l\u8217'safeka} \u8212- one who makes a conditional vow intends certainty, not doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Viability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this framework established, the {\i gemara} considers whether and how the birth of a subsequent child might retroactively resolve the doubtful status of a previously miscarried child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'i minei Rav Abba mi\u8217'Rav Huna} \u8212- Rav Abba posed a question to Rav Huna. The case: a man says {\i hareini nazir l\u8217'khsheyihyeh li ven} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if I have a son\u8221" \u8212- and his wife miscarried. Operating under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's principle, he is definitively not a {\i nazir}. Nonetheless, he separated out an animal as a {\i korban}. His wife then gives birth to a living child. The question is: was he a {\i nazir} from the time of the first child, and is the animal already sanctified?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} is practical: if one were to use the animal for shearing or labor \u8212- {\i legizato ul\u8217'avoda bo} \u8212- would one be in violation of {\i me\u8217'ilah}? If the animal was sanctified when he set it aside, it is {\i asur}; if not, it is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately clarifies: according to Rabbi Shimon, there is no question. From the moment of the miscarriage, the man was already a {\i safek nazir} under Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling. The interesting question arises specifically according to Rabbi Yehuda, who would normally have said the person is not a {\i nazir} at all. Does the subsequent birth of a living child retroactively establish that the miscarried child was in fact {\i ben kayama} \u8212- viable \u8212- and that the man was therefore a {\i nazir} from the earlier point?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is probing a nuanced middle position: perhaps the evidence supplied by the second living child is sufficient to move Rabbi Yehuda from his default position of non-obligation. It might not render the man a {\i vadai nazir} from that earlier moment \u8212- which could make him {\i chayav me\u8217'ila} \u8212- but perhaps enough to impose some form of obligation. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i kadosh o lo kadosh} \u8212- is the animal sanctified or not? The {\i gemara} concludes {\i teiku} \u8212- the question is left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twin Viability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether the birth of the second child can retroactively establish the viability of the first is complicated by a further consideration. {\i Tosafot} observes that the {\i gemara}\u8217's implicit assumption \u8212- that the second child\u8217's viability would prove the first\u8217's \u8212- may not be warranted. The key variable is whether these are identical or fraternal twins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the twins are identical, they derive from the same fertilized egg split in two, and they are biologically the same age. In that case, the viability of the second child would indeed establish that the first was also viable. If, however, they are fraternal twins, two separate eggs may have been fertilized at different times \u8212- perhaps one egg fertilized on the first day after intercourse and another on the third. This could mean that the twins differ by as much as two days in gestational age, which in turn could place one at the threshold of viability and the other just below it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} makes this explicit, citing the language {\i deshema miktzat hatipah nikletah miyad umiktzat hatipah hashniyah lo nikletah ad achar shney yamim} \u8212- that perhaps part of the semen was absorbed immediately and part only two days later. If that is the case, then the twin born alive may actually have been conceived slightly earlier, and the one who was miscarried, though appearing to be the same age, was in fact two days younger and had not yet crossed the threshold of {\i ben kayama}. This explains why the {\i gemara} leaves the question unresolved as {\i teiku}, rather than simply concluding that the second birth settles the matter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Nezirut Declarations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 And Me Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'i minei Ben Rechumi me\u8217'Abaye} \u8212- Ben Rechumi posed a question to Abaye. The case: a man declares {\i hareini nazir l\u8217'khsheyihyeh li ven} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} when I have a son\u8221" \u8212- and a companion overhears him and says {\i ve\u8217'alai} \u8212- \u8220"and upon me.\u8221" What does this mean? Is the companion saying he will be a {\i nazir} when he himself has a son, or when the original speaker has a son?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alai vs Ani\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to a parallel case. What if rather than saying {\i ve\u8217'alai}, the companion says {\i va\u8217'ani} \u8212- \u8220"and I\u8221"? At first glance, both seem to convey the same meaning. Yet the {\i gemara} probes whether {\i va\u8217'ani} might carry a different intent. One possibility is {\i anfshah ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- he is speaking about himself, meaning \u8220"and when I have a son, I too will be a {\i nazir}.\u8221" The other possibility is {\i ve\u8217'rachimna lach kvatich} \u8212- \u8220"and I care for you as I care for it,\u8221" expressing not a personal vow inspired by the other\u8217's example, but rather an expression of solidarity with the speaker. In this reading, {\i va\u8217'ani} means: \u8220"Because of my bond with you, if you have a son, I too will be a {\i nazir}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i alai} and {\i ani} is subtle. {\i Alai} \u8212- \u8220"upon me\u8221" \u8212- places the obligation directly on oneself, stepping away, so to speak, from the original speaker. {\i Ani} \u8212- \u8220"and I\u8221" \u8212- may instead be oriented toward the relationship: \u8220"I am with you in this,\u8221" suggesting that the {\i nezirut} is conditioned on the original speaker\u8217's son rather than one\u8217's own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative version of the text, noted in the margin ({\i samech alef}, denoting {\i sfarim acherim}), has the original vow formulated as {\i l\u8217'khsheyihyeh lecha ven} \u8212- \u8220"when you have a son\u8221" \u8212- making the question even more pointed. If Michael says \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} when you have a son\u8221" and Dov says \u8220"and me,\u8221" does \u8220"and me\u8221" follow Michael\u8217's formulation (conditioned on Michael\u8217's son) or does the principle of {\i adam karov eitzel atzmo} \u8212- one is closest to oneself \u8212- pull the declaration toward Dov\u8217's own son?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ksifa Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces the concept of {\i ksifa lei milta} \u8212- embarrassment in another\u8217's presence \u8212- as a factor shaping intent. {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reading is as follows: when the companion says {\i va\u8217'ani} in the presence of the original speaker, there is a social pressure not to appear indifferent to the other\u8217's situation. Accordingly, {\i va\u8217'ani} in the presence of the speaker would naturally be understood as \u8220"and I am here for you \u8212- if you have a son, I too will be a {\i nazir},\u8221" because to say \u8220"and I, when I have a son\u8221" in that moment would be to ignore the person standing before you. The embarrassment of appearing self-focused in front of someone who has just expressed so much significance to having a son pulls the declaration toward the other person\u8217's condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Party Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises the case of a third party: {\i amar hareini nazir l\u8217'khsheyihyeh l\u8217'ploni ven} \u8212- Michael says \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if Charlie has a son.\u8221" Charlie is not present. If Dov then says {\i va\u8217'ani}, there is no direct social pressure to express loyalty to Charlie, since Charlie is not in the room.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loyalty Motivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether the dynamic of loyalty still operates indirectly. Even though Charlie is absent, Dov has witnessed Michael\u8217's deep commitment to Charlie\u8217's welfare \u8212- so much so that Michael is prepared to accept {\i nezirut} on Charlie\u8217's behalf. Dov may therefore feel a pull not of loyalty to Charlie, but of loyalty to Michael. The reasoning would be: Michael, I see how much you care for Charlie. Because I care for you, I will also be a {\i nazir} if Charlie has a son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis reveals two distinct psychological models at work in these declarations. In the first model, someone observes a {\i nazir} or hears a vow and says \u8220"me too\u8221" \u8212- inspired by the idea and adopting it for themselves. In the second model, the \u8220"me too\u8221" is not about inspiration but about solidarity \u8212- not \u8220"you gave me a good idea\u8221" but \u8220"I am with you in this.\u8221" The two models yield different {\i halachic} conclusions about whether the companion\u8217's vow is conditioned on his own child or on the original speaker\u8217's. The {\i gemara} leaves this as {\i tiku} \u8212- unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Immediate/Delayed Start\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Nezirut Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} presents the following case: {\i hareini nazir v\u8217'nazir k\u8217'sheyihyeh li ven} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} [starting now] and a {\i nazir} when I have a son.\u8221" The {\i mishnah} is working through the interaction between a {\i nezirut} that begins immediately and one conditioned on a future event.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The subtlety lies in the order of the declarations. When the man says \u8220"and a {\i nazir}\u8221" without specifying a future time, even though it was his second statement, it is understood to take effect immediately. What he has in effect said is: my second {\i nezirut} begins now, and my first {\i nezirut} \u8212- the one conditioned on having a son \u8212- will begin when the condition is met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an unusual inversion: the person is currently practicing his {\i second} {\i nezirut} while his {\i first} {\i nezirut} has not yet taken effect. When his son is born and the first {\i nezirut} comes due, it preempts the second: {\i maniach et shelo u\u8217'moneh shel beno} \u8212- he sets aside his own ongoing {\i nezirut} and counts the thirty days of the son-{\i nezirut}. Then, {\i achar kach mashlim et shelo} \u8212- he completes the remaining days of his own {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's use of the word {\i mashlim} \u8212- completing or making up \u8212- carries an important implication. {\i Tosafot} makes explicit what the {\i mishnah} leaves implicit: it is impossible that the man would have shaved at the conclusion of the son-{\i nezirut} before completing his own, because the remaining days of his own {\i nezirut} would then be fewer than thirty. The requirement of {\i giddel pera} \u8212- the growth of hair for the requisite period \u8212- means that one cannot validly conclude a {\i nezirut} without at least thirty days of uninterrupted hair growth since the last shaving. Accordingly, there is no shaving between the two periods; the man does a straight continuous run and brings his {\i korbanot} only at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields a significant practical consequence: because of the unusual order in which these {\i neziruyot} took effect, the man avoids bringing two separate sets of {\i korbanot}. Normally, two accepted {\i neziruyot} require the first to conclude \u8212- with shaving and {\i korbanot} \u8212- before the second begins. Here, because the second was already underway before the first came due, the first is entitled to preempt the second without requiring the second to formally conclude first. And since the second was never formally concluded, it does not need to restart from scratch \u8212- only the remaining days need to be completed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hundred Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the full scope of the issue, it is helpful to look ahead to the {\i mishnah} on 15a, which presents a variant: {\i hareini nazir l\u8217'khsheyihyeh li ven v\u8217'nazir mei\u8217'hayom} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} when I have a son and a {\i nazir} starting now for a hundred days.\u8221" Here the immediately-effective {\i nezirut} is one of a hundred days rather than the standard thirty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} establishes a threshold: {\i nolad lo ven ad shivim lo hifsid klum} \u8212- if the son is born within the first seventy days, he loses nothing. Why seventy? Because if the son is born by day seventy, there will be at least thirty days remaining in the hundred-day {\i nezirut} after the thirty-day son-{\i nezirut} concludes. That means the son-{\i nezirut} is entirely encompassed within the time frame of the hundred-day {\i nezirut}, and the man gets to \u8220"double-dip\u8221": both {\i neziruyot} are satisfied by the same period of observance, and no days beyond the hundred need be added.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the son is born after day seventy, the son-{\i nezirut} will push beyond the hundred-day framework. The {\i mishnah} then employs the term {\i soter} \u8212- the later birth undermines the double-dipping. {\i Tosafot} clarifies that {\i soter} here does not mean that all prior days are invalidated entirely, but rather that {\i ein tiglachat pachos mishloshim yom} \u8212- a valid conclusion of {\i nezirut} cannot occur with fewer than thirty days of hair growth. The man is therefore required to add a full thirty days at the end rather than simply the remaining balance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are thus three distinct structural scenarios for overlapping {\i neziruyot}. In the first, the man declares a conditional {\i nezirut} first and the immediate {\i nezirut} second, meaning his immediate {\i nezirut} is number one and he simply performs it, brings his {\i korbanot}, and then waits for the condition to be met before performing the second. This is the straightforward case with no unusual interactions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second scenario \u8212- the {\i mishnah} currently before us \u8212- the man starts what is structurally his second {\i nezirut} before his first has taken effect. When the first comes due, it preempts the second; when it concludes, the man completes the second. No intermediate shaving occurs, and only one set of {\i korbanot} is brought at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the third scenario, the immediately-effective {\i nezirut} is long enough (a hundred days) that the son-{\i nezirut} falls entirely within its span. If the birth occurs early enough, the man double-dips entirely and needs no additional days at all. If the birth occurs too late, the double-dipping is lost and additional days must be added.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Post-Dated Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba\u8217'i Rava} \u8212- Rava posed a question. The case: {\i amar hareini nazir l\u8217'achar esrim yom u\u8217'me\u8217'akhshav me\u8217'ah yom} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} after twenty days, and starting now, a {\i nazir} for a hundred days.\u8221" Unlike the previous {\i mishnah}, where the timing of the conditional {\i nezirut} was determined by an uncertain future event (the birth of a son), here the man has explicitly post-dated his first {\i nezirut} to begin after twenty days. The question is whether the hundred-day {\i nezirut}, declared to start immediately, actually takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Growth Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames the question as follows: {\i keivan dehani me\u8217'ah be\u8217'esrim lo shalmi, lo chayilin} \u8212- since the hundred days will not be completed by the time the twenty-day {\i nezirut} begins, perhaps the hundred-day {\i nezirut} should not take effect at all. The logic is: why begin something you cannot finish before being interrupted?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other hand, {\i keivan d\u8217'it lei giddel sei\u8217'ar l\u8217'vasof, shapir dami} \u8212- since there will still be an opportunity for {\i giddel sei\u8217'ar} \u8212- the required hair growth \u8212- after the conclusion of the entire arrangement, perhaps the hundred-day {\i nezirut} should take effect and the man will simply complete the remaining days afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mention of {\i giddel sei\u8217'ar} implies that a {\i korban} is being brought at the juncture between the two {\i neziruyot} \u8212- the twenty-day {\i nezirut} concludes with shaving, and then the question is whether the hundred days must start fresh or whether the days already counted are somehow preserved. Unlike the prior case, where the unusual ordering arose spontaneously from an unpredictable birth, here the man has deliberately structured the arrangement from the outset. {\i Tosafot} notes that this deliberate pre-arrangement may change the analysis: when a person consciously lays out the sequence in advance, the expectation may be that each {\i nezirut} must conclude properly before the next begins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Small vs Large\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why Rava needed to pose the question specifically with a hundred-day {\i nezirut}. Why not ask the question with a standard {\i nezirut mu\u8217'etat} \u8212- a short, thirty-day {\i nezirut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that the two questions are nested: {\i chada migu chada kamiba\u8217'ya lei} \u8212- one question is built upon the other. If we are uncertain about a small {\i nezirut}, we would certainly wonder about a large one; if we resolve the large one, the small one may or may not follow. The cases are therefore not interchangeable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sufficient Remainder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of a short {\i nezirut}, if the man began the hundred days immediately and then had to stop for the twenty-day {\i nezirut}, he would have only ten days remaining at the end. {\i Keivan d\u8217'asara yomin hu d\u8217'yasiran, lo salik lehu hanin asara} \u8212- since those ten days are not sufficient to stand on their own as a {\i nezirut}, perhaps the hundred-day {\i nezirut} should not have begun at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in the case of the hundred-day {\i nezirut}, the remaining days after the twenty-day interruption would be substantial \u8212- perhaps eighty days. {\i Keivan d\u8217'isya tmanin yomin l\u8217'vasof, salik lei} \u8212- since eighty days remain at the end, that is certainly sufficient to warrant the original hundred-day {\i nezirut} taking effect. Whether this logic prevails, or whether the deliberate post-dating of the twenty-day {\i nezirut} imposes a different framework entirely, is left as an open question \u8212- and the discussion continues on the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 15\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Son Born Before Day 70\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Nazirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah on this daf continues the themes developed over the preceding days. The case is one of conditional and sequential {\i nezirut}: a person declares {\i hareini nazir k\u8217'sheyihiyeh li ben} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} when I have a son \u8212- and simultaneously declares a hundred-day {\i nezirut}. Because the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} cannot yet take effect (the son has not been born), the person begins observing the hundred-day {\i nezirut} immediately. When the son is subsequently born, as we learned previously, the person interrupts the {\i nezirut} he is currently observing and shifts into the {\i nezirut} vowed for the son, since that vow takes precedence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Counting Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then states: {\i im ad shivim lo hifsid klum} \u8212- if the son is born up to and including day seventy, the person loses nothing. As Tosafot understands this, the operative principle is that if the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} \u8212- a thirty-day {\i nezirut} at minimum \u8212- will conclude before or by the end of the hundred-day {\i nezirut} already underway, and is thus completely contained within it, the person is permitted to double-count the two periods. This is a striking idea: rather than interrupting, pausing, and resuming, the two periods of {\i nezirut} simply overlap and are counted simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 70 Boundary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the son is born {\i l\u8217'achar shivim} \u8212- after day seventy \u8212- the Mishnah states {\i soter shivim}. Tosafot brackets the word {\i shivim} as not carrying its usual meaning here, reading {\i soter} not as an outright nullification of the preceding seventy days but as an interruption: since the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} will now extend beyond the hundred-day period and therefore possesses a distinct identity, it creates a suspension. The person must complete the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} and then resume the hundred-day {\i nezirut} from where he left off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah adds the phrase {\i ein tiglachas pachos mishloshim yom} \u8212- there is no shaving in fewer than thirty days. Tosafot explains that the word {\i tiglachas} (shaving) is not itself the point; what the Mishnah is conveying is that {\i ein nezirut pachos mishloshim yom} \u8212- no {\i nezirut} is fewer than thirty days. Since the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} is at minimum thirty days, it will inevitably extend beyond the hundred-day period if begun after day seventy, thereby acquiring a distinct identity and triggering the suspension. This requires considerable reading into the Mishnah\u8217's language \u8212- bracketing {\i shivim} in one place and reinterpreting {\i tiglachas} in another \u8212- but Tosafot\u8217's reading provides the coherent framework within which the subsequent Gemara is discussed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fundamental principle that emerges is: if the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} is fully contained within the hundred-day {\i nezirut}, double-counting is permitted and nothing is lost; if it is not contained, suspension and resumption are required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav\u8217's Double Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces Rav\u8217's position: {\i yom shivim oleh l\u8217'kan ul\u8217'kan} \u8212- day seventy itself double-counts. If the son is born on day seventy, that day counts simultaneously as the seventieth day of the father\u8217's hundred-day {\i nezirut} and as day one of the son\u8217's thirty-day {\i nezirut}. The son\u8217's {\i nezirut} then concludes on day ninety-nine, and the two periods together account for the full hundred days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what practical difference does this double-counting make? After all, if you simply count from day seventy as day one for the son, the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} still concludes by day ninety-nine, and one still needs to complete the remaining day of the hundred-day period. The Gemara does not spell out the significance explicitly, but Tosafot addresses this directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bringing Korban Early\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains that the double-counting would, in theory, allow the person to bring the {\i korban} on day one hundred rather than having to wait until day one hundred and one. The logic runs as follows: the person completed seventy days of {\i nezirut} up through day seventy; starting from that same day, thirty days were counted for the son, concluding on day ninety-nine; the total is therefore seventy plus thirty, equaling one hundred. The hundred-day {\i nezirut} is complete, and the {\i korban} may be brought on the following day \u8212- day one hundred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot immediately flags how strange this is: not only are we permitting double-counting in general, but by virtue of the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} beginning on day seventy, the father somehow gets to count an extra day toward his own hundred. Tosafot resolves this by noting that, in principle, one could always bring a {\i korban} on the last day itself due to the principle of {\i miktzas hayom k\u8217'kulo} \u8212- a partial day counts as a full day, much as a mourner rises from {\i shiva} on the seventh day. The practical benefit of Rav\u8217's position is therefore not that one gains an entirely new day, but that one may bring the {\i korban} on day one hundred rather than waiting for day one hundred and one. Tosafot notes that this leniency is in any case {\i derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Rav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more pointed implication of Rav\u8217's position \u8212- and the one the Gemara really targets \u8212- concerns day seventy-one. If day seventy double-counts, then by the same logic, even if the son is born on day seventy-one, that day should count as day one for the son, and the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} would conclude by day one hundred. If so, the two {\i neziruyot} would still be coterminous, and the Mishnah\u8217's distinction between \u8220"up to seventy\u8221" and \u8220"after seventy\u8221" would be inexplicable. Why should day seventy-one be treated any differently than day seventy? That is the question the Gemara now turns to press.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Refutation via Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gains vs.\u160 ?Loses Nothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges Rav from the Mishnah\u8217's own language. The Mishnah says that if the son is born by day seventy, {\i lo hifsid klum} \u8212- the person loses nothing. But according to Rav\u8217's position, this formulation is not merely accurate \u8212- it actually understates the case. If day seventy double-counts and allows the {\i korban} to be brought a day earlier, then the person has not merely \u8220"lost nothing\u8221" \u8212- he has actually {\i gained} something. The Gemara captures this with the idiom {\i itgurei mitgar} \u8212- he gains. The Mishnah, by saying only that nothing is lost, implies that there is no gain, which directly contradicts Rav\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 \u8216'Achar\u8217' Means Day 72\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then turns to the end of the Mishnah: {\i nolad achar shivim soter} \u8212- if born after seventy, it causes a hiatus. According to Rav, even day seventy-one should not be problematic, since counting from day seventy-one as day one, the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} concludes by day one hundred. The Gemara therefore proposes: perhaps {\i achar shivim} does not mean \u8220"after day seventy\u8221" (i.e., day seventy-one) but rather \u8220"after the after of seventy\u8221" \u8212- meaning day seventy-two. The Mishnah would then be teaching that up through day seventy-one nothing is lost, and only from day seventy-two onward is there a problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara immediately rejects this reading: if that were the Mishnah\u8217's intent, it should simply have said \u8220"up to day seventy-one\u8221" rather than \u8220"up to day seventy.\u8221" The precision of the Mishnah\u8217's language demonstrates that day seventy-one is genuinely a problem, which means the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} does not begin on the day he is born. It must begin on the following day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day of Birth Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes: {\i achar mamash} \u8212- \u8220"after seventy\u8221" means literally day seventy-one, and day seventy-one is indeed a problem. The son\u8217's first day of {\i nezirut} is not the day of his birth but the day after. Consequently, if born on day seventy-one, his thirty-day {\i nezirut} begins on day seventy-two and concludes after day one hundred \u8212- it is not contained within the hundred-day period, a suspension is triggered, and the Mishnah\u8217's ruling is fully coherent. The Gemara thus demonstrates that the Mishnah does not support Rav\u8217's principle of {\i oleh l\u8217'kan ul\u8217'kan}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Day Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion requires clarification, lest it be misread as a rejection of the general principle that a partial day counts in {\i nezirut}. Under ordinary circumstances, if a person declares {\i nezirut} today with only an hour remaining in the day, that hour counts as day one. The partial day suffices. This is not the principle being rejected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Day 71 Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is being rejected is something more specific: the double-counting of a day that has already begun as part of a different counting. When day seventy-one breaks \u8212- at the start of that calendar day \u8212- it is already day seventy-one of the person\u8217's own hundred-day {\i nezirut}. The day does not begin as a neutral day; it begins already assigned to a pre-existing {\i nezirut}. When the son is subsequently born in the middle of that day, the day cannot be reassigned to serve simultaneously as the son\u8217's day one. The problem is not that a partial day is insufficient \u8212- a partial day is generally sufficient. The problem is specifically {\i oleh l\u8217'kan ul\u8217'kan}: double-counting a day that belongs to one {\i nezirut} so that it also serves as the first day of another. The Mishnah\u8217's ruling that day seventy-one creates a problem proves that this kind of double-counting is not permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav & Abba Shaul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miktzas Hayom Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having demonstrated that the Mishnah does not support Rav\u8217's view, the Gemara asks: {\i v\u8217'Rav keman amar l\u8217'shmaisa} \u8212- according to whom does Rav hold? The Gemara suggests: {\i eima k\u8217'Abba Shaul} \u8212- perhaps Rav follows the view of Abba Shaul. This introduces the principle of {\i miktzas hayom k\u8217'kulo} \u8212- a partial day counts as a whole \u8212- in its most familiar context: the laws of {\i aveilus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moed Katan Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites the Mishnah from Moed Katan. One who buries a close relative fewer than three days before {\i Yom Tov} \u8212- the arrival of the festival does not end the {\i shiva}. The festival can only interrupt {\i shiva} if the mourner has observed at minimum three days. But if the burial occurred eight days before {\i Yom Tov}, such that {\i erev Yom Tov} already falls within the {\i shloshim} period rather than the {\i shiva} period, then the festival ends the {\i shloshim}. Furthermore, in that case, because {\i shloshim} will be interrupted by {\i Yom Tov}, the person is permitted to take a haircut on {\i erev Yom Tov} in deference to {\i kevod ha-regel}. However \u8212- and here the Mishnah adds an important caveat \u8212- {\i v\u8217'im lo siper b\u8217'erev ha-regel, asur l\u8217'saper achar ha-regel}: if the person did not actually take the haircut before {\i Yom Tov}, the interruption is deemed not to have occurred and the {\i shloshim} must be completed after the festival.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This Mishnah is not the ruling we follow, but it provides the framework within which Abba Shaul\u8217's view is presented.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Shaul disagrees on two points. First, he maintains that even if the mourner did not take the haircut before {\i Yom Tov}, the festival still interrupts \u8212- {\i mutar l\u8217'saper achar ha-regel}. The haircut is not a condition for the interruption. Second, and more fundamentally, Abba Shaul holds that a mourner need not wait until {\i the eighth day} to be considered in {\i shloshim} at the onset of {\i Yom Tov}; it suffices to have completed {\i seven days}. His ruling is expressed as a parallelism: just as the obligation of three days terminates {\i shiva} when the festival arrives, so the obligation of seven days terminates {\i shloshim} when the festival arrives. The key question is what Abba Shaul means by \u8220"seven days.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in the principle of {\i miktzas hayom k\u8217'kulo}. On the morning of the seventh day of {\i shiva}, the mourner rises \u8212- that partial day counts as the completion of the seventh day. From that morning onward, the mourner is no longer in {\i shiva}; he has already entered the {\i shloshim} period. When {\i Yom Tov} then arrives the following day, it encounters a mourner who is observing {\i shloshim}, not {\i shiva}, and the festival is therefore {\i mevatel} the {\i shloshim}. Abba Shaul\u8217's principle is thus precisely analogous to Rav\u8217's: day seven double-counts \u8212- it is simultaneously the last day of {\i shiva} and, from the morning onward, the first day of {\i shloshim}. The seventh day is {\i oleh l\u8217'kan ul\u8217'kan}. This is exactly the structure of Rav\u8217's position regarding the son\u8217's {\i nezirut}: the day functions as the conclusion of one period and simultaneously as the beginning of another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbinic vs.\u160 ?Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite the conceptual elegance of the parallel, the Gemara raises an objection. {\i Dilma} \u8212- perhaps Abba Shaul\u8217's principle of double-counting applies only {\i b\u8217'aveilus shiva derabbanan} \u8212- in the context of {\i aveilus}, which is rabbinic (at least from the second day onward, and certainly with respect to the {\i shloshim} period and the festival\u8217's power to interrupt it). In rabbinic law there may be greater flexibility to permit such double-counting. But {\i b\u8217'nazir de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- in the context of {\i nezirut}, which is a Torah-level obligation \u8212- who says the same leniency would apply? The parallelism is intellectually compelling but legally inconclusive: one cannot automatically extend a principle developed in the context of a rabbinic institution to a biblical one. The Gemara therefore sets aside Abba Shaul and looks for a {\i tanna} who applies the equivalent of {\i miktzas hayom k\u8217'kulo} in a double-counting sense within a biblical context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's Zava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proposes: {\i ella Rav d\u8217'amar k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- Rav holds in accordance with Rabbi Yosi. The relevant {\i baraita} concerns the laws of {\i zivah}. The Gemara will now develop Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position in detail through the case of the {\i shomeret yom keneged yom}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yosi Zava Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zava Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a woman bleeds outside her normal menstrual cycle, she is governed by the laws of {\i zivah}. If she bleeds on a single day, the {\i halacha} requires her to observe the following day in {\i tahara} \u8212- she becomes a {\i shomeret yom keneged yom}, observing one clean day corresponding to the day she saw blood. If she observes that day without any bleeding, she immerses in the {\i mikvah} and becomes {\i tehora}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case Rabbi Yosi addresses is as follows. A woman saw blood on Sunday and is therefore {\i shomeret yom} on Monday \u8212- she must observe Monday in {\i tahara}. It happens that Monday is {\i erev Pesach}. The hope is that Monday will pass without bleeding, she will immerse in the {\i mikvah} during the day (relying on {\i miktzas hayom k\u8217'kulo}), and she will be {\i tehora} in time to partake of the {\i korban Pesach} that evening. Because of this expectation, they go ahead and bring a {\i korban Pesach} on her behalf on Monday afternoon, at which time she has not yet bled and is {\i tehora} \u8212- having already immersed in the {\i mikvah} earlier in the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactivity Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At 3:00 PM on Monday \u8212- after the {\i korban Pesach} has been slaughtered and its blood sprinkled on her behalf \u8212- the woman unfortunately bleeds again. She will clearly be unable to eat the {\i korban Pesach} that evening, as she is now {\i teme\u8217'ah}. The more serious question is whether the {\i korban Pesach} itself counts. The natural analysis would be: the only reason she was permitted to immerse in the {\i mikvah} mid-day was because we applied {\i miktzas hayom k\u8217'kulo} \u8212- we treated the partial day as though the remainder of the day had already passed cleanly. If it turns out that by the end of the day she was not clean, then retroactively we must say she was {\i teme\u8217'ah} all along, the {\i mikvah} immersion was invalid, and the {\i korban Pesach} did not count. This is the problematic implication of understanding {\i miktzas hayom} as working in anticipation of the rest of the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi rules otherwise. The {\i korban Pesach} counts \u8212- she is {\i petur} from {\i Pesach Sheni} \u8212- because she was genuinely {\i tehora} when it was brought. The bleeding at 3:00 PM is a new {\i tumah}, not a retroactive disqualification of the {\i tahara} she enjoyed earlier. This ruling reflects a fundamentally different conception of {\i miktzas hayom}: not that the partial day works because we fold the remainder of the day into it, but rather that a partial day is {\i sufficient in itself}. The woman\u8217's {\i tahara} from the morning until the time of the {\i korban} is real and complete. What happens afterward is independent. This is directly analogous to Rav\u8217's principle: if a partial day is sufficient in itself, then the first hours of a day can count for one purpose and the remaining hours for an entirely different one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Models Miktzas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 71 Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now makes explicit the connection between Rabbi Yosi\u8217's ruling and Rav\u8217's position in {\i nezirut}. The structural parallel is as follows. On day seventy-one, the son is born at 10:00 AM. According to Rav\u8217's principle of {\i oleh l\u8217'kan ul\u8217'kan}, the early hours of that day count toward the father\u8217's hundred-day {\i nezirut}, and the hours following the son\u8217's birth count as day one of the son\u8217's {\i nezirut}. How can a single day be split between two different {\i neziruyot}? Only if {\i miktzas hayom} means that a partial period is independently sufficient \u8212- not that the partial period works because we project the remainder of the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sufficiency Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields Model Two: {\i miktzas hayom} means a partial day is sufficient in itself. Under this model, what happens later in the day is entirely separate from and independent of what occurred earlier. The woman immerses in the {\i mikvah} mid-day: the hours of {\i tahara} up to that point constitute a complete, self-contained unit. The bleeding at 3:00 PM is a new {\i tumah} because the day is not a monolithic unit \u8212- it can be divided, with earlier portions standing on their own. Similarly, the first hours of day seventy-one belong to the father\u8217's {\i nezirut}, and the later hours, from the moment of the son\u8217's birth, begin a new and independent counting for the son. Neither half requires the other to be valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anticipation Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Model One operates entirely differently: a partial day counts only in anticipation that the rest of the day will unfold as expected. Under this model, the {\i mikvah} immersion mid-Monday works only because we are projecting forward and treating the whole of Monday as already having passed without incident. If bleeding occurs later in the day, it retroactively reveals that this anticipatory projection was incorrect, and the {\i tahara} of the morning is undone. By the same logic, under Model One, if day seventy-one begins as day seventy-one of the father\u8217's {\i nezirut}, the entire day belongs to that {\i nezirut} \u8212- we cannot split it, because the day was already \u8220"claimed\u8221" from the moment it began. The son\u8217's {\i nezirut} cannot begin until the following day, which is why day seventy-one creates a problem in the Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Retroactive Impurity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactivity Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now examines Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position more carefully, because a complication arises. A different {\i baraita} appears to attribute to Rabbi Yosi the view that when the {\i zava} bleeds later in the day, she is retroactively {\i teme\u8217'ah} \u8212- and that if she sat on an object earlier in the day (even after her {\i mikvah} immersion), that object is rendered {\i tamei}. This seems to contradict the ruling that the {\i korban Pesach} counts: if she was retroactively {\i teme\u8217'ah} the whole time, how can the {\i korban} have been brought validly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's first resolution: {\i le\u8217'mafrea miderabbanan} \u8212- the retroactive {\i tumah} is only rabbinic. Biblically, her {\i tahara} at the time of the {\i korban} was real, and the later bleeding constitutes a new {\i tumah}. Rabbinically, we treat the bleeding as retroactively disqualifying the day, which is why objects she sat upon are rendered {\i tamei} \u8212- but this is a rabbinic stringency overlaid on a biblical status of {\i tahara}. The {\i korban Pesach}, which operates at the biblical level, is therefore unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers an alternative resolution: {\i le\u8217'olam eima lach} \u8212- perhaps Rabbi Yosi genuinely holds that the retroactive {\i tumah} is biblical. Even so, the {\i korban Pesach} counts for a separate reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah of Depths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle at work is {\i tumas tehom} \u8212- the {\i tumah} of the depths, meaning an {\i tumah} that was unknowable at the time the offering was brought. The classical example is a person who brings a {\i korban Pesach} while unknowingly standing above a buried grave, rendering him biblically {\i tamei} \u8212- yet because the {\i tumah} was entirely hidden and unknowable to all parties at the time, the {\i korban} counts. The Gemara applies the same logic here: when the {\i korban Pesach} was brought for this woman, she had observed part of the day in {\i tahara}, immersed in the {\i mikvah}, and all evidence indicated she was {\i tehora}. The subsequent bleeding was not foreseeable or knowable at that moment. This constitutes a {\i tumah} of the depths with respect to {\i zivah}, and the {\i korban} therefore counts despite the fact that she was, retroactively, biblically {\i teme\u8217'ah}. Under this second approach, Rabbi Yosi does not rely on the {\i sufficiency model} of {\i miktzas hayom}, and consequently cannot serve as support for Rav\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yoshiya View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zav Day 7 Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues by citing Rabbi Yoshiya\u8217's view in a {\i baraita}: a {\i zav} \u8212- a man who has experienced two or more abnormal seminal discharges \u8212- must count seven clean days before immersing in the {\i mikvah} and becoming {\i tahor}. Rabbi Yoshiya rules: if on day seven the {\i zav} has another discharge, {\i soter et shelefanav} \u8212- he loses not merely that day but all seven preceding days and must begin the count again from scratch. This is the {\i anticipation model} operating to its most extreme conclusion: the partial day seven was counted only in anticipation of the rest of the day passing cleanly; if a discharge occurs, retroactively the entire preceding period is undermined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, upon hearing Rabbi Yoshiya teach this, responded: {\i lo nistar ela yomo} \u8212- he loses only that day, not the full seven. The discharge on day seven does not retroactively destroy the days that preceded it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logical Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately presses: {\i mah nafshach} \u8212- either way you look at it, Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position is logically unstable. If we apply the {\i anticipation model} \u8212- if the partial day seven counted only because we projected the rest of the day passing cleanly \u8212- then when the discharge occurs, {\i kulu sater}: all seven days should be lost, because day seven itself is retroactively disqualified, and the count cannot be complete without it. If, on the other hand, we apply the {\i sufficiency model} \u8212- if the partial day seven counted independently of what happens later \u8212- then {\i lo nistar ela yomo} doesn\u8217't even go far enough: the discharge on day seven shouldn\u8217't even cost him {\i one} day, because a single {\i re\u8217'iyah} on its own does not destroy a day of {\i tahara} for a {\i zav}. How then can Rabbi Yochanan say he loses exactly one day?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yochanan Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Retroactive Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by clarifying what Rabbi Yochanan actually means. The formulation {\i lo nistar ela yomo} should be read as: there is no disruption at all beyond that day itself \u8212- meaning the discharge later on day seven has no retroactive effect whatsoever on the days that preceded it. The man immersed in the {\i mikvah} in the morning of day seven; the later discharge does not undo that immersion or undermine the previous six days. He is {\i tahor} from the {\i mikvah} forward; the discharge constitutes a new {\i re\u8217'iyah} from this point onward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 From Now Onward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely the principle of {\i mikan ul\u8217'haba metamei} \u8212- from now onward he is {\i tamei}, not retroactively. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position aligns entirely with the {\i sufficiency model} of {\i miktzas hayom}: the partial day is complete in itself, and what happens later is independent. The {\i zav} who went to the {\i mikvah} on the morning of day seven is genuinely {\i tahor} at that moment. The later discharge begins a new count of {\i zivah} and does not reach backward to disqualify what was already established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oshaya Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Oshaya draws the explicit conclusion: {\i Reb Yosi kai ke-vathakh} \u8212- Rabbi Yosi stands together with Rabbi Yochanan. Rabbi Yosi\u8217's ruling that the {\i korban Pesach} counts for the {\i shomeres yom} who bleeds later in the day is grounded in the same {\i sufficiency model} of {\i miktzas hayom}: the earlier hours stand on their own, the later bleeding is {\i mikan ul\u8217'haba}, and the two portions of the day are independent. The Gemara\u8217's note that Rabbi Yosi also says the retroactive {\i tumah} applies \u8212- {\i ve-ha Reb Yosi le-mafrea hu de-amar} \u8212- is resolved, as before, by limiting that ruling to the rabbinic level: {\i u-mai le-mafrea miderabbanan}. Biblically, Rabbi Yosi holds with Rabbi Yochanan that {\i miktzas hayom} is sufficient in itself, and the later events do not reflect back on the earlier portion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Full Zava Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consecutive Days Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final difficulty arises from Rabbi Yosi\u8217's {\i sufficiency model} of {\i miktzas hayom}. A woman becomes a {\i zavah gemurah} \u8212- a full {\i zava} requiring seven clean days and a {\i korban} \u8212- only by bleeding on three consecutive days. Under the normal understanding, \u8220"three consecutive days\u8221" means she bled on Sunday, Monday, and Tuesday without interruption. But under Rabbi Yosi\u8217's model, since a partial period at the start of any day is independently sufficient as a unit of {\i tahara}, it would seem that any day on which she does not bleed {\i at the very beginning} would constitute a break \u8212- a {\i shomeret yom keneged yom} interlude \u8212- that resets the count. The question is therefore: how does a woman ever accumulate three truly consecutive days of {\i zivah} bleeding under Rabbi Yosi\u8217's framework? Every day that begins without bleeding would, it seems, be counted as a clean unit and interrupt the sequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Continuous Bleeding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers two answers. The first is direct: {\i dechazya tlata yomei behadi hadadi} \u8212- she bled continuously for three full days without any break whatsoever, not even for a moment. Every minute of all three days involved active bleeding, leaving no opening for {\i miktzas hayom} to establish a partial unit of {\i tahara}. This is the straightforward solution, however biologically demanding it may be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sunset Straddling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second answer is more conceptually precise. The three consecutive days of bleeding need not be entirely continuous; rather, the bleeding straddles {\i shkiah} \u8212- sunset \u8212- each day. That is, each day she begins bleeding just before sunset and the bleeding continues past sunset into the next day. Consider the structure: the bleeding begins Sunday just before 6:00 PM and continues past 6:00 PM into Monday; it continues into Monday and again extends past 6:00 PM into Tuesday; and so through Tuesday into the night. Under this pattern, each calendar day {\i begins} with the bleeding already in progress (since it carried over from the previous evening) and {\i ends} with the bleeding still ongoing (since it will carry past sunset). There is therefore no window \u8212- neither at the beginning of the day nor at its end \u8212- that could serve as a clean partial unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miktzas Hayom Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This second answer clarifies the precise scope of {\i miktzas hayom}: it requires that either the beginning or the end of the day be clean. A clean period sandwiched in the middle of the day \u8212- with bleeding before and bleeding after \u8212- does not constitute a valid unit of {\i tahara} under the {\i sufficiency model}. The phrase {\i delo hava shehus lesalukei leminyana} \u8212- there is no time that counts toward the clean-day count \u8212- captures this precisely. {\i Miktzas hayom k\u8217'kulo}, in its most sophisticated form, means that as long as a day genuinely opens or closes with a period of {\i tahara}, that period stands independently and is sufficient. But a clean interlude embedded within a day that begins and ends in {\i tumah} cannot serve this function.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the Gemara concludes its analysis of the interlocking themes of {\i nezirut}, {\i miktzas hayom}, and {\i zivah} that run through daf 15. {\i Hadran alach hareini nazir}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 16\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Implicit vs Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 31 vs Day 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i perek} of Masechet Nazir opens with a {\i mishnah} that in large part recapitulates ground covered on 5b, so the discussion here moves quickly, drawing on analysis already developed there. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i mi she-amar hareini nazir megaleiach leyom shloshim ve-echad} \u8212- a person who declares {\i stam} that he will be a {\i nazir} brings his {\i korbanos} and shaves on the 31st day. The reasoning is straightforward: the {\i nezirut} must encompass 30 days, and the morning of the 31st day is only a partial day, so in order to have a full 30 days for the {\i nezirut} one waits until the 31st. {\i Ve-im gileach leyom shloshim yatza} \u8212- but if such a person shaved and brought {\i korbanos} on the 30th day, he has nonetheless fulfilled his obligation {\i bedi\u8217'eved}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if a person was explicit \u8212- {\i hareini nazir shloshim yom}, I will be a {\i nazir} for 30 days \u8212- then {\i im gileach leyom shloshim lo yatza}: shaving on the 30th day does not suffice even {\i bedi\u8217'eved}, because once the person explicitly stipulated 30 days, we cannot rely on {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} to substitute a partial day for a full one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on De\u8217'oraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot on 5b provides a crucial framing for why the {\i stam} case works {\i bedi\u8217'eved}. The {\i machlokes} between Bar Pada and Rav Matna \u8212- which concerned how extensively to apply {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} \u8212- is, according to Tosafot, a dispute only at the rabbinic level. At the {\i de\u8217'oraita} level, all agree that 30 days is sufficient for a {\i stam nazir}, and the 30th day itself is acceptable. The rabbinic extension then requires waiting until the 31st day {\i lechatchila}. This explains why {\i bedi\u8217'eved}, when a person has already shaved on the 30th day, he is {\i yotzei}: {\i dino de\u8217'oraita de-stam nazir yegaleiach beyom shloshim} \u8212- the Torah-level obligation has been met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miktzat Hayom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying question threading through this section is how {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} operates when applied at both ends of a time period simultaneously. When a person accepts {\i nezirut}, the very day of acceptance counts as a full day even if only a small portion of the day remains before {\i shki\u8217'ah}. The question is whether we can then also count a partial day at the conclusion of the period. This same issue arises in {\i aveilus}: the first day and the final day of mourning each count through {\i miktzat hayom kekulo}. One notable exception is the 12-month period of mourning for a parent, where the year cannot be shortened through {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} at its close \u8212- because years are composed of months, not days, and one cannot invoke {\i miktzat hachodesh kekulo} to treat a partial month as though it were complete. The practical implication is that the 12-month period of {\i aveilus} extends until {\i shki\u8217'ah} of its final day. The {\i mishnah} here reflects this same structural concern about whether a partial day at both endpoints can legitimately stand in for full days.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Naziriyos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 L\u8217'chatchila Bedieved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses the case of {\i mi she-nazar shtei neziriyos} \u8212- a person who took two consecutive {\i neziriyos}. {\i Lechatchila}, the person should shave and bring {\i korbanos} for the first {\i nezirut} on the 31st day, and for the second on the 61st day. The 31st day is split: its beginning counts toward the conclusion of the first {\i nezirut}, and its end counts as the opening of the second. {\i Bedi\u8217'eved}, if the person shaved on the 30th day, the second {\i nezirut} would then end on the 60th day \u8212- {\i im gilach yom shishim chasar echad yatza}, and if the person shaved on the 59th day that would be {\i yotzei} as well, returning to the calculations established in the first {\i perek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Day Two Periods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ability to count a single day simultaneously toward the close of one {\i nezirut} and the opening of another raises the deeper question of how {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} operates. One might compare this to {\i rubo kekulo} \u8212- the principle that a majority functions as the whole. But the two principles may work differently. When we say {\i rubo kekulo}, one understands the majority as expanding to fill the entirety; the minority is not ignored but treated as subsumed. {\i Miktzat hayom kekulo}, however, seems capable of operating in both directions at once: a partial day at the start of one period and a partial day at the close of another can each be counted, and thus the same calendar day serves double duty for two distinct {\i neziriyos}. This is a striking application of the principle and points to its distinct logical character.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 40 Minus 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 40 Structural\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrasing \u8212- {\i yom shishim chasar echad yatza}, \u8220"60 minus one\u8221" \u8212- reflects a recurring rhetorical pattern in {\i mishnayos} of expressing a number as a round figure minus one. The most prominent instances of {\i arba\u8217'im chasar achat} appear in two contexts: the {\i melachos} of {\i Shabbos}, which are enumerated as 40 minus one, and the {\i makkos} administered for various {\i aveiros}, which are similarly given as 40 minus one. The Torah states explicitly {\i arba\u8217'im yakenu lo yosif}, and {\i Torah she-be\u8217'al peh} derives from this that 40 itself represents an extreme maximum never actually to be reached, so that in practice the count is always 39.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 40 Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The number 40 throughout {\i halacha} functions as a marker of completeness or transition. A {\i mikvah} requires 40 {\i se\u8217'ah} of water; Moshe Rabbeinu ascended Har Sinai for 40 days on at least two, and possibly three, occasions; Israel wandered 40 years in the desert. To say 40 minus one is therefore to gesture at something that falls just short of a complete unit \u8212- as though something is still missing from the full reckoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melachot Count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With respect to {\i hilchos Shabbos}, the {\i Tannaim} and {\i Amoraim} themselves debated whether the exact enumeration of {\i melachos} is precisely 39 or whether additional {\i melachos} were inadvertently excluded from the canonical list. The {\i machlokes} on this point further underscores that \u8220"40 minus one\u8221" is not a mechanical count but a structural statement about the relationship between the number and its parent category.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Naziriyos (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Papyas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} records a specific testimony: {\i v\u8217'zu eidus he\u8217'id Rabbi Papyas al mi she-nazar shtei neziriyos} \u8212- Rabbi Papyas testified regarding one who took two consecutive {\i neziriyos}: {\i she-im gilach es harishona yom shloshim megaleiach es hashniya l\u8217'yom shishim} \u8212- if he shaved for the first {\i nezirut} on the 30th day, he shaves for the second on the 60th day, still counting a full 30 days {\i lechatchila} for the second even though the first was completed {\i bedi\u8217'eved}. {\i V\u8217'im gilach yom shishim chasar echad yatza} \u8212- and if he shaved on the 59th day for the second, he is nonetheless {\i yotzei}. The formulation recalls {\i Masechet Eduyos}, where {\i Tannaim} came before the {\i beit din} to transmit recollections of earlier rulings; here too Rabbi Papyas brings an older tradition, the substance of which confirms {\i sheyom shel shloshim oleh lo min haminyan} \u8212- that the 30th day counts toward the reckoning, in this case serving both {\i neziriyos} simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumah Cancels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Day 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to the case of a {\i nazir} who becomes {\i tamei}. {\i Mi she-amar hareini nazir ve-nitma yom shloshim soter es hakol} \u8212- a person who declared {\i stam nezirut} and became {\i tamei meis} on the 30th day: the {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that this cancels everything. Not only must the person undergo the seven-day {\i taharah} process for {\i tumas meis}, but the entire {\i nezirut} is nullified and must be restarted with a full 30-day count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer eino soter ela shiva} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer disagrees. Importantly, his use of the word {\i soter} is somewhat misleading: he is not saying that seven days of the {\i nezirut} are cancelled. Rather, he holds that only seven days of {\i taharah} are required, after which the person brings his {\i korbanos} and the {\i nezirut} stands. Nothing of the {\i nezirut} itself is undone; the person simply must complete the {\i taharah} process and then proceed. During those seven days the person remains bound by the prohibitions of {\i nezirut} \u8212- he may not drink wine or shave \u8212- since those prohibitions remain until the {\i korbanos} are brought. Whether a {\i nazir} who is {\i tamei meis} is prohibited from becoming further {\i tamei meis} is an interesting separate question that touches on a well-known dispute between the Rambam and the Ra\u8217'avad regarding {\i kohanim} today, but that discussion lies beyond the scope of the present {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Day 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then takes up the case of {\i hareini nazir shloshim yom} \u8212- an explicit 30-day {\i nezirut}. {\i Nitma yom shloshim soter es hakol} \u8212- both the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Eliezer agree that becoming {\i tamei} on the 30th day cancels the {\i nezirut} entirely and requires restarting. The {\i Gemara} will explain that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's leniency does not extend to this case because when a person specifies 30 days explicitly, the implication is {\i shloshim shlemim} \u8212- 30 complete days \u8212- which changes the halakhic calculus even for Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 100-day Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a person takes an explicitly longer {\i nezirut} \u8212- {\i hareini nazir me\u8217'ah yom}, 100 days \u8212- and becomes {\i tamei} on the 100th day, {\i nitma yom me\u8217'ah soter es hakol} according to the {\i Tanna Kamma}. Rabbi Eliezer, however, says {\i eino soter ela shloshim} \u8212- only 30 days are cancelled. This initially appears to contradict Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's own position in the earlier case. In the {\i stam} 30-day case, Rabbi Eliezer said the {\i nezirut} is not cancelled at all on the 30th day; here, with 100 days, he says 30 days are cancelled. The resolution, which the {\i Gemara} will develop, turns on the {\i drasha} of {\i zot toras ha-nazir beyom melos} \u8212- that even on the {\i yom melos}, the day of completion, one may be required to treat the {\i nazir} as though he needs a full restart. Rabbi Eliezer holds that even when the person has kept 99 consecutive days of pure {\i nezirut}, the minimum {\i nezirut} required to fulfill the {\i neder} is 30 days, and therefore a new 30-day {\i nezirut} suffices. The person need not repeat the entire 100-day period, because Rabbi Eliezer understands the {\i neder} as fundamentally satisfied by a complete 30-day {\i nezirut} undertaken in fulfillment of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 101\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the person becomes {\i tamei} on the 101st day \u8212- the day planned for bringing {\i korbanos} {\i lechatchila}, after the 100-day {\i nezirut} is already complete \u8212- both the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Eliezer agree that only 30 days are cancelled, corresponding to the 30 days needed for {\i gidul se\u8217'ar}, the required hair growth. The logic is consistent: once the {\i nezirut} period itself has fully elapsed, whatever happens afterward cannot undo the substance of the {\i neder} already kept; what remains is only the practical requirement of re-establishing sufficient hair growth before shaving and bringing {\i korbanos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yom Milat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's governing principle is stated explicitly in the {\i Gemara}: {\i kol she-achar milot shiva soter} \u8212- anything that occurs after {\i yom milot}, the day of completion of the {\i nezirut}, cancels only seven days, the seven days needed to become {\i tahor}, but does not cancel the {\i nezirut} itself. The conceptual distinction underlying this principle is important. Taking a {\i nezirut} is a {\i neder} for a specific block of time; if a person becomes {\i tamei} during that period, the {\i tumah} destroys the ability to have kept the {\i neder} for that time and requires restarting. But once the period has concluded, the person has already fulfilled the {\i neder}. Whatever happens afterward \u8212- even while the person is technically still a {\i nazir} awaiting the bringing of {\i korbanos} \u8212- falls outside the scope of the original {\i neder}. Becoming {\i tamei} at that stage triggers an obligation to become {\i tahor} but does not reach back to nullify what has already been kept.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reconcile R. Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 30-day Puzzle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presses on the apparent inconsistency within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's positions. For the {\i stam} 30-day {\i nazir}, Rabbi Eliezer holds that becoming {\i tamei} on the 30th day does not cancel the {\i nezirut} \u8212- only seven days of {\i taharah} are required. For the explicit 30-day {\i nazir}, Rabbi Eliezer agrees with the {\i Tanna Kamma} that it cancels everything. If Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's principle is {\i kol she-achar milot shiva soter} \u8212- that anything after the day of completion cancels only seven days \u8212- why should an explicit 30-day {\i nazir} be treated differently from a {\i stam} 30-day {\i nazir}? In both cases the 30th day is the final day of the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelemim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution is that the explicit declaration of 30 days necessarily implies {\i shloshim shlemim} \u8212- 30 complete, full days. When a person says {\i hareini nazir shloshim yom} explicitly, the additional specification transforms the {\i neder} into one requiring whole days, not days counted through {\i miktzat hayom kekulo}. Under that reading, the 30th day is not yet {\i yom milot} \u8212- the {\i nezirut} is not yet complete \u8212- because complete days are required, and the 30th day has not yet finished. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's leniency therefore does not apply: it is not \u8220"after {\i milot}\u8221" because {\i milot} has not yet arrived. The {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's rule \u8212- that {\i tumah} on the 30th day cancels everything \u8212- follows as a matter of course.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Pada Matna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution directly connects back to the {\i machlokes} between Bar Pada and Rav Matna on 5b. The {\i Gemara} states explicitly: {\i kulah ke-dahavinan bah aliba de-Bar Pada ve-Rav Matna} \u8212- the entire set of disputes in this {\i mishnah} flows from that earlier {\i machlokes} about how to count {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} for {\i nezirut}. Bar Pada, who held that 29 days suffice at the {\i de\u8217'oraita} level, can read the {\i reisha} of the {\i mishnah} as dealing with a case of {\i shlemim}; Rav Matna, who requires 30 days, has his own {\i okimta} to resolve the apparent contradictions. The {\i Gemara} on the current {\i daf} returns to 5b\u8217's language \u8212- {\i reisha na\u8217'asit ke-omer shlemim} \u8212- to show that both {\i Amoraim} can accommodate the {\i mishnah} by reading the cases as instances where {\i shlemim} was intended, whether explicitly stated or implied. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position is then aligned with Rav Matna: since the 30th day is not yet {\i achar milot} if {\i shlemim} is required, Rabbi Eliezer agrees that {\i tumah} on that day cancels the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's discussion of what Rabbi Eliezer requires for the 100-day {\i nezirut} follows the same logic. For a {\i nezirut meruba}, Rabbi Eliezer says {\i eino soter ela shloshim} \u8212- even when the person must restart, it is sufficient to do a 30-day {\i nezirut} rather than repeat the entire 100 days. This is because {\i zot toras ha-nazir beyom melos} teaches that a {\i nazir} who becomes {\i tamei} on {\i yom melos} is still treated as a {\i nazir} who must fulfill a complete period \u8212- but that complete period, according to Rabbi Eliezer, need only be 30 days. The person has kept 99 consecutive days of valid {\i nezirut}; it would be impossible at that point to repeat 100 days and maintain the integrity of the {\i neder} as originally conceived. Rabbi Eliezer therefore reads the requirement of a new {\i nezirut} as the minimum: 30 days of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} and clean {\i nezirut} to bring the {\i neder} to its proper conclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Cemtery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cemetery Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens with a striking case: {\i mi she-nazar ve-hu be-veit ha-kevaros} \u8212- a person who takes a {\i neder} to become a {\i nazir} while standing in a cemetery. The person is presumably already {\i tamei meis}, either because the {\i meis} is present and providing {\i tumah} or because being within the {\i ohel} of a grave imparts {\i tumas meis} in any event. {\i Afilu haya sham shloshim yom ein olin lo min haminyan} \u8212- even if the person remained in the cemetery for 30 days straight, those days do not count toward the 30-day {\i nezirut}. {\i Ve-eino meivi korban tumah} \u8212- and such a person does not bring a {\i korban tumah}. The {\i korban tumah} is required only of a {\i nazir} who was {\i tahor} and then became {\i tamei}; a person who was already {\i tamei} at the moment {\i nezirut} was accepted cannot satisfy that prerequisite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a {\i machlokes} among the {\i Rishonim} as to whether the case of the cemetery is the only configuration that triggers this {\i halacha}, or whether it applies equally to any person who is {\i tamei meis} when accepting {\i nezirut} \u8212- including someone who is simply walking in the street while {\i tamei}. Tosafot and the Rosh suggest the {\i mishnah}\u8217's specification of the cemetery is illustrative rather than exclusive, and the same {\i halacha} would apply to any {\i tamei meis} who accepted {\i nezirut}. Other {\i Rishonim} may have required specifically the {\i beit ha-kevaros}, perhaps because there the {\i avi avot ha-tumah} is actively and continuously present, creating a distinct relational dynamic, or perhaps because being in a cemetery at the moment of the vow reveals that the person cannot genuinely have intended a {\i nezirut} to take effect immediately, since a {\i nazir} patently may not be {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Left Returned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i yatza ve-nichnas} \u8212- what if the person left the cemetery and re-entered? {\i Olin lo min haminyan u-meivi korban tumah} \u8212- in that case the days do count and the {\i korban tumah} applies. The {\i Mefareish} explains that {\i yatza ve-nichnas} must involve a meaningful interruption: {\i kegon she-hiza ve-shana ve-taval} \u8212- the person received both {\i haza\u8217'os} of {\i mei chatas} from the {\i parah adumah} on the seventh day, immersed in a {\i mikveh}, and thus became {\i tahor}, and only afterward re-entered the cemetery. In other words, the person became {\i tahor} and then became {\i tamei} again \u8212- the precondition for the {\i korban tumah} obligation. The phrasing {\i yatza ve-nichnas} in the {\i mishnah} does not seem to require this on its face, but it is the only logically coherent reading: one cannot owe a {\i korban tumah} without having first been {\i tahor} as a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer 7th Day\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer adds a caveat: {\i lo bo ba-yom} \u8212- not on that very day. If the person re-entered the cemetery on the seventh day of his {\i taharah} process \u8212- the day on which he receives the {\i haza\u8217'ah}, immerses, and becomes {\i tahor} \u8212- the days do not yet count. The source is {\i ve-hayamim ha-rishonim yippolu} \u8212- \u8220"the prior days will fall\u8221" \u8212- with the plural {\i rishonim} understood as requiring at least two prior days of established {\i nezirut} before {\i tumah} cancels them. {\i Ad she-yihyu lo yamim rishonim} \u8212- there must be multiple days. A person who becomes {\i tahor} and immediately re-enters the cemetery the same day has never established even a single full day of {\i tahor nezirut} before the interruption, and therefore the condition of {\i yamim rishonim} \u8212- plural prior days \u8212- is not met. Absent that, there is no {\i korban tumah} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question arises in the {\i machlokes} between Tosafot and the Rambam regarding how those two days are counted. The seventh day of the {\i taharah} process itself begins already as a partial day \u8212- the {\i haza\u8217'ah} and {\i tevilah} occur at some point during the day, and the person then waits until nightfall. That first day of {\i taharah} therefore already invokes {\i miktzat hayom kekulo}. Can the second day also be counted through {\i miktzat hayom kekulo}? If so, the person might establish {\i yamim rishonim} with only a brief span of actual time \u8212- perhaps 12 to 14 hours total. Tosafot appear to accept this, while the Rambam may require that the second day be a more complete day, reflecting a stricter view of when {\i miktzat hayom kekulo} applies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yochanan Reish L.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matla Talya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a direct dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish: {\i mi she-nazar ve-hu be-veit ha-kevaros} \u8212- what is the status of a person who accepts {\i nezirut} while in a cemetery? {\i Rabbi Yochanan amar nezirus chalah alav} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan holds that the {\i nezirut} takes effect. His reasoning: {\i savar matla talya ve-kayma} \u8212- he holds that the {\i nezirut} is a {\i matla talya}, a hanging or suspended thing. The {\i neder} attaches itself to the person and hovers, waiting to take hold once {\i taharah} is achieved. {\i Keivan de-mishkachat taharah chaila} \u8212- as soon as it finds the person {\i tahor}, it takes effect. Under this view, the person does not need to re-declare {\i nezirut} after becoming {\i tahor}; the original acceptance already binds him, suspended in place until the moment of {\i taharah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish disagrees: {\i ein nezirus chalah alav} \u8212- the {\i nezirut} does not take effect at all. {\i Ei hadar ve-amar chaila aleih} \u8212- only if the person goes back and re-accepts {\i nezirut} after becoming {\i tahor} does it take effect; {\i ve-ei lo lo} \u8212- otherwise it means nothing. The logic underlying Reish Lakish\u8217's position is that {\i nezirut}, as a {\i neder}, cannot take hold under conditions where it is impossible to fulfill. A person who is {\i tamei meis} cannot be a {\i nazir} who observes the prohibitions of {\i nezirut} in their fullest sense. Accepting {\i nezirut} while {\i tamei} is thus analogous to {\i matneh al mah she-kasuv ba-Torah} \u8212- conditioning a {\i neder} on circumstances that the Torah itself precludes. The {\i mitzvos ha-Torah} prevent the {\i neder} from taking root, and it simply fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan challenges Reish Lakish from the {\i mishnah} itself: the {\i mishnah} states that a person in a cemetery for 30 days does not bring a {\i korban tumah} \u8212- {\i eino meivi korban tumah}. Rabbi Yochanan reads this as a pointed specification: the {\i mishnah} is telling us that this person is indeed a {\i nazir} \u8212- one who has certain exemptions \u8212- but that the exemptions apply. The very need to inform us {\i eino meivi korban tumah} implies that there is a {\i nazir} present who might otherwise be subject to that obligation. The clause would be superfluous if the person were not a {\i nazir} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defense 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish deflects: {\i eino be-toras tumah} \u8212- the person is not in the category of {\i tumah} at all, meaning he is not a {\i nazir} with exemptions but simply not a {\i nazir}. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement that he does not bring a {\i korban tumah} and that the days do not count is, according to Reish Lakish, simply a comprehensive description of someone who has no {\i nezirut} \u8212- {\i eino be-toras tumah ve-eino be-toras korban}. One can grant that this reading is somewhat strained \u8212- it is an odd way to describe a non-{\i nazir} \u8212- and perhaps that awkwardness itself provides some support for Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan presses further with a {\i Tosefta}: {\i mi she-hayah tamei ve-nazar asur le-galei\u8217'ach ve-lishtot yayin ve-tamei le-meisim} \u8212- a person who was {\i tamei} and accepted {\i nezirut} is prohibited from shaving, drinking wine, and becoming {\i tamei le-meisim}. Moreover, {\i ve-im gilach ve-shasah yayin ve-nitma le-meisim harei zeh sofeg et ha-arba\u8217'im} \u8212- if he shaved, drank wine, or became {\i tamei le-meisim}, he receives {\i malkos}. {\i Ei amrat bishlama chaila} \u8212- if the {\i nezirut} takes effect, that explains why violations carry {\i malkos}. But if Reish Lakish holds the {\i nezirut} does not take effect at all, why should there be any punishment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defense 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish responds: {\i hacha be-mai askinan be-yotzei ve-nichnas} \u8212- the {\i Tosefta} must be dealing with the case of {\i yatza ve-nichnas}, the person who left the cemetery, became {\i tahor}, and thereby re-affirmed the {\i nezirut}. There is a dispute between the {\i Mefareish} and the Meiri about how much of an affirmation is needed at that stage. According to the {\i Mefareish}, the person must verbalize a re-acceptance of the {\i nezirut} \u8212- stating explicitly that he upholds what he declared in the cemetery. According to the Meiri, even a mental re-acceptance upon becoming {\i tahor} \u8212- a recognition that \u8220"now that I am {\i tahor} I am a {\i nazir}\u8221" \u8212- may suffice. Either way, Reish Lakish maintains that the original declaration in the cemetery was inoperative; it is only the post-{\i taharah} affirmation, explicit or implicit, that creates the binding {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third challenge, which arrives at the top of 17a, comes from a {\i baraita}: {\i ein bein tamei she-nazar le-nazir tahor she-nitma ela tamei she-nazar shevi\u8217'i shelo oleh lo min ha-minyan ve-nazir tahor she-nitma ein shevi\u8217'i shelo oleh lo min ha-minyan} \u8212- there is no difference between a {\i tamei} person who became a {\i nazir} and a {\i tahor nazir} who became {\i tamei}, except in one respect: for the {\i tamei} person who became a {\i nazir}, the seventh day of his {\i taharah} process counts toward the 30-day {\i nezirut}; but for the {\i tahor nazir} who became {\i tamei}, the seventh day of his {\i taharah} does not count toward the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} presents a strong challenge to Reish Lakish: {\i ve-ei salka da\u8217'atach lo chaila, amai oleh lo min ha-minyan?} \u8212- if the {\i nezirut} of the {\i tamei} person who accepted it while {\i tamei} does not take effect at all, why should his seventh day of {\i taharah} count toward his 30-day {\i nezirut}? If nothing attached at the time of the vow, the commencement of the 30-day count should only begin after full {\i taharah}, not on the seventh day itself. Yet the {\i baraita} explicitly grants him credit for that seventh day. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view explains this naturally: the {\i nezirut} was already hovering \u8212- {\i matla talya ve-kayma} \u8212- and {\i keivan de-mishkachat taharah chaila}, as soon as it finds {\i taharah}, even a partial day of {\i taharah} kicks the {\i nezirut} into counting through {\i miktzat hayom kekulo}. The continuing discussion of this exchange, and Mar bar Rav Ashi\u8217's contribution, carries over to 17a.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intentional Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam in {\i perek} 5 of {\i Hilchos Nezirus} addresses several related cases. A {\i nazir tahor} who intentionally made himself {\i tamei} \u8212- {\i nazir tahor she-timei atzmo} \u8212- receives {\i malkos}. The Rambam specifies that such a person in fact incurs four separate sets of lashes: {\i mishum lo yitma} \u8212- for violating the prohibition on a {\i nazir} becoming {\i tamei}; {\i mishum lo yachel devaro} \u8212- for profaning his word; {\i mishum lo te\u8217'acher leshalmo} \u8212- for violating {\i bal tacher}, the prohibition on delaying fulfillment of a {\i neder}; and a fourth prohibition related to certain conditions of simultaneous violation. The {\i bal tacher} analysis is significant: since {\i nezirut} is a {\i neder}, intentionally becoming {\i tamei} delays the ability to fulfill the {\i neder} on schedule \u8212- the person now must wait seven days to become {\i tahor}, bring a {\i korban tumah}, and only then complete a fresh period. This postponement constitutes a violation of {\i bal tacher le-shalmo}, and the Rambam holds it independently actionable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bal Tacher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam then addresses our case \u8212- a person who was in a {\i beit ha-kevaros} and accepted {\i nezirut} there. Such a person, the Rambam rules, still violates {\i bal yachel devaro} and {\i bal tacher}. This ruling presupposes that the {\i nezirut} takes effect \u8212- consistent with the {\i halacha} as we rule, following Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- and that the person is therefore bound by the {\i neder} from the moment of acceptance. Remaining in the cemetery, unable to begin counting the 30-day period, constitutes a delay in fulfilling the {\i neder}, and that delay triggers {\i bal tacher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lav She\u8217'ein Bo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Lechem Mishnah raises a challenging question about the Rambam\u8217's ruling: awarding {\i malkos} to the person who stood in the cemetery and took {\i nezirut} there seems to run afoul of the principle of {\i lav she\u8217'ein bo maise} \u8212- a prohibition that does not involve a physical act does not ordinarily carry {\i malkos}. Standing in a cemetery while accepting {\i nezirut} does not appear to involve any active {\i maise}. The Lechem Mishnah\u8217's lengthy discussion explores how the act of remaining stationary in the cemetery \u8212- deliberately staying put \u8212- might constitute a sufficient {\i maise} to sustain a {\i malkos} penalty. The analysis is admittedly a stretch: remaining in place is at the outer boundary of what we ordinarily call an {\i action}. Nevertheless, the Lechem Mishnah entertains the possibility that intentional stationary presence, as a deliberate choice, qualifies as a {\i maise} for this purpose. Other prominent examples of {\i lav she\u8217'ein bo maise} include believing in a false god without any act of worship, and emotional prohibitions such as {\i lo sisna et achicha bilvavecha} \u8212- these carry no {\i malkos} precisely because they involve no outward action whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Days vs Prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A fundamental conceptual distinction emerges from the foregoing analysis. A person who accepts {\i nezirut} in a {\i beit ha-kevaros} is, according to Rabbi Yochanan and the ruling {\i halacha}, a {\i nazir} from the moment of acceptance \u8212- but the 30-day count cannot begin until after {\i taharah}. These are two separable dimensions of {\i nezirut}: the status of being a {\i nazir}, with its attendant prohibitions, and the counting of the prescribed time period, which demands consecutive {\i tahor} days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder-like\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibitions of {\i nezirut} \u8212- abstaining from wine, refraining from shaving, and avoiding {\i tumah le-meisim} \u8212- function much like any other {\i neder} and extend throughout the person\u8217's {\i nezirut}, regardless of {\i taharah} status. A {\i neder} binds from the moment of its utterance. The counting of the 30 days, by contrast, is a requirement specifically of the {\i nezirut} and can only be satisfied by days lived as a {\i tahor nazir} in sequence. The two aspects thus operate on different tracks: the prohibitions engage immediately, the count awaits {\i taharah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence is illustrated by the following scenario. A person accepts {\i nezirut} in a cemetery, remains there for 20 days, leaves, and then undergoes the seven-day {\i taharah} process. On day 27 from the original acceptance, the person is fully {\i tahor}. At that point \u8212- day 27 \u8212- the 30-day {\i nezirut} count begins. The count will conclude on day 56, at which point the person shaves and brings {\i korbanos}. Throughout the entire period from day one, the prohibitions of {\i nezirut} have applied; but the days in the cemetery, and the seven days of {\i taharah}, do not count toward the 30. Only the 30 consecutive {\i tahor} days beginning on day 27 constitute the {\i nezirut} period proper.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 17\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Tamei: Yochanan vs\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir in Cemetery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opening discussion of this {\i daf} centers on a case that the {\i mishna} introduces: a {\i nazir} who accepts {\i nezirut} while standing in a cemetery ({\i beis hakevarot}). This scenario raises several interrelated questions, and before proceeding to the {\i gemara}\u8217's dialectic, it is worth placing the broader issue on the table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If someone is {\i tamei} and accepts {\i nezirut}, what are the implications? When does the {\i nezirut} take effect? And there is an additional question lurking beneath the surface, one relevant not only to a {\i nazir} but to a {\i kohen} as well: if a person is already {\i tamei}, can they become {\i tamei} again? Practically speaking, this question bears on {\i kohanim} today, given the widespread assumption that people are born in hospitals containing morgues, such that nearly everyone is considered {\i tamei meis} from birth. If a {\i kohen} is already {\i tamei}, what is the status of becoming {\i tamei} a second time?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The real difficulty in this question arises only on the very day the original {\i tumah} began. Any subsequent day, one day later or more, there is a nearly universal assumption that further {\i tumah} adds a layer and is therefore equally prohibited. But on the very first day, the question is genuinely interesting and has practical relevance for {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} addresses the case of someone who accepts {\i nezirut} while in a cemetery. The principle established is as follows: the person\u8217's {\i nezirut} does not begin while they remain {\i tamei}. They must first become {\i tahor}, which requires going through the {\i taharah} process \u8212- receiving the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} on day three and day seven \u8212- and their thirty-day count of {\i nezirut} begins only once they emerge as {\i tahor}. Crucially, however, such a person does {\i not} bring a {\i korban tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is structural: the {\i korban tumah} is brought only when a {\i nazir} began in a state of {\i taharah} and subsequently became {\i tamei}. Here, the person began their {\i nezirut} already in a state of {\i tumah}, so the specific {\i korban} associated with violating a previously clean {\i nezirut} does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} adds a further qualification regarding the {\i korban}. He holds that even in the standard case \u8212- a {\i nazir tahor} who becomes {\i tamei} \u8212- a {\i korban} is required only if the person became {\i tamei} on the {\i second} day of their {\i nezirut}, not on the very first day. The {\i torahitic} phrase about \u8220"earlier days falling away\u8221" implies that there must be earlier days to fall away in the first place. If someone became {\i tamei} on the very day they accepted {\i nezirut}, {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds they would not bring a {\i korban}. However, this debate does not directly affect the case of the person who began as {\i tamei} in the cemetery, who does not bring a {\i korban} for an entirely independent reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirut Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially frames the dispute between {\i Rabbi Yochanan} and {\i Reish Lakish} as a question about {\i when} the {\i nezirut} takes effect. On one reading, the core question is whether the {\i nezirut} begins at all while the person is still {\i tamei}, or whether it is entirely suspended until after the person achieves {\i taharah}. This framing would have sweeping consequences for what obligations and prohibitions apply during the {\i tumah} period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Reish Lakish}\u8217's initial position, as the {\i gemara} first presents it, is that the {\i nezirut} is entirely suspended while the person is {\i tamei}. Since the {\i nezirut} did not get a chance to begin in a state of {\i taharah}, it is in abeyance, and therefore none of the prohibitions of {\i nezirut} apply during this period. The striking consequence: if the person were to drink wine, get a haircut, or incur further {\i tumah} while remaining in the cemetery, they would receive no {\i malkus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is counterintuitive when contrasted with the case of a {\i nazir tahor} who subsequently becomes {\i tamei}. That person, who started their {\i nezirut} properly, remains a full {\i nazir} throughout \u8212- even while {\i tamei}, they are prohibited from wine, shaving, and contracting {\i tumah}, and violations are punishable with {\i malkus}. The asymmetry that {\i Reish Lakish} is apparently proposing is that when the {\i nezirut} never began in {\i taharah}, it does not rise to the level of full force even to impose {\i malkus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Re-Acceptance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} introduces an important additional inference on 16b. The passage there reads: {\i Reish Lakish amar ein neziros chalah alav be-odo tamei ad she-yit-hare ve-az yit-starech lachazor u-lekabel alav neziros} \u8212- that according to {\i Reish Lakish}, the {\i nezirut} does not take effect while the person is {\i tamei}, and when they become {\i tahor}, they would need to re-accept the {\i nezirut} explicitly. {\i Tosafot} cites {\i Rashi}\u8217's view that a minimal re-acceptance ({\i kabalah mu\u8217'etes}) suffices \u8212- one need not perform a full acceptance anew, merely indicate that the {\i nezirut} should now begin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does {\i Tosafot} introduce this? Because it helps explain the {\i gemara}\u8217's otherwise puzzling question. The {\i gemara} asks: if {\i Reish Lakish} holds that the {\i nezirut} is suspended while the person is {\i tamei}, why does the {\i mishna} say that \u8220"day seven counts\u8221" as the beginning of the thirty-day period? The force of the question depends on the assumption that, since it was suspended, it should not begin automatically \u8212- it should require active re-triggering. Without that assumption, the question is weak; once {\i tahor}, why {\i wouldn\u8217't} it begin? So {\i Tosafot} reads into {\i Reish Lakish}\u8217's position the assumption that automatic resumption is insufficient, which is what gives the {\i gemara}\u8217's challenge its teeth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Self-Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then fundamentally recasts the dispute. {\i Mar bar Rav Ashi} clarifies: {\i meheicha kaamrat lo pligi d\u8217'chayil} \u8212- actually, {\i Reish Lakish} does not disagree that the {\i nezirut} takes effect even while the person is {\i tamei}. Everyone agrees that accepting {\i nezirut} in a state of {\i tumah} creates {\i nezirut} immediately. {\i Ela ki pligi l\u8217'milki} \u8212- the dispute is only about whether the transgressions carry {\i malkus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} holds that since the {\i nezirut} took effect, it carries full force and violations are punishable with {\i malkus}. {\i Reish Lakish} holds that while it took effect, because it was accepted in a compromised state \u8212- a {\i tumah} state \u8212- the full force of {\i nezirut} has not yet been reached, and therefore violations during this period do not carry {\i malkus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This revised framing is important: it is not that the {\i nezirut} does not exist. It is that the {\i nezirut} that began in {\i tumah} has not yet attained full legal force, and therefore the question is whether all of its prohibitions come with the consequence of {\i malkus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Full Effect While Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} challenges {\i Reish Lakish} from the {\i mishna} itself. The {\i mishna} states that someone who accepts {\i nezirut} in a cemetery \u8212- even if they remained there for thirty days \u8212- does not begin counting their days and does not bring a {\i korban tumah}. {\i Rabbi Yochanan} reads this as listing only a single difference: the {\i korban}. If the {\i nezirut} had another significant distinction \u8212- namely, that violations carry no {\i malkus} \u8212- the {\i mishna} should have mentioned it. The silence of the {\i mishna} about {\i malkus} implies that there is no difference in that domain; the person is a full {\i nazir} in every sense except for the {\i korban} and the timing of the count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Reish Lakish} responds: {\i b\u8217'din hu d\u8217'litni eino lokei} \u8212- you are correct that it should have said there is no {\i malkus}. But the {\i mishna}\u8217's focus is entirely elsewhere. The {\i mishna} is structured around the {\i sof}, the later case, which discusses what happens if the person left the cemetery, became {\i tahor}, and then re-entered. In that case \u8212- {\i yatza v\u8217'nichnas oleh lo min haminyan u\u8217'meivi korban tumah} \u8212- they do bring a {\i korban tumah}. This is the ruling the {\i mishna} is building toward, against the position of {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, who requires the {\i tumah} to occur on the second day. Since the {\i mishna}\u8217's entire interest is in the {\i korban} question, it mentions nothing about {\i malkus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This defense, while not without force, is strained. The gap the {\i mishna} leaves unmentioned \u8212- namely that a person who is fully prohibited from wine, shaving, and {\i tumah} would receive no {\i malkus} for any of these \u8212- is no small omission. If that were truly the {\i halacha}, one would expect it to be taught.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion then turns to {\i Tosafot}\u8217's analysis of a phrase that appears in the {\i baraita} on 17a: {\i v\u8217'tiglachas zeh v\u8217'zeh shavin} \u8212- \u8220"and for shaving, they are the same.\u8221" {\i Tosafot} offers two interpretations. The first, cited in the name of {\i yesh mefarshim}, reads this as meaning that both persons \u8212- the one who started {\i tamei} and the one who began {\i tahor} and became {\i tamei} \u8212- would receive {\i malkus} for shaving. On this reading, {\i Reish Lakish}\u8217's limitation of \u8220"no {\i malkus}\u8221" applies only to the specific act of becoming {\i tamei}, since that is the particular aspect compromised by the way the {\i nezirut} began. For all other prohibitions \u8212- wine, shaving \u8212- {\i malkus} would apply even to the {\i nazir tamei} who started that way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} rejects this interpretation: {\i ve-lo nehira} \u8212- it does not seem correct. If the {\i gemara} meant that {\i Reish Lakish} was distinguishing only {\i tumah} from the other prohibitions, it should have said \u8220"for shaving {\i and} wine, they are the same.\u8221" Since it mentions only shaving, a different reading must be intended. {\i Tosafot} therefore concludes that the phrase about shaving refers not to {\i malkus} for the prohibition of shaving, but to the {\i mitzvah} of {\i tiglachat} \u8212- the shaving done to restart the {\i nezirut}. The {\i gemara} is saying that both persons, whether they started {\i tamei} or became {\i tamei}, must shave to restart their counting. But when it comes to {\i malkus} for transgressions, {\i Reish Lakish} would say there is a difference, and that difference might extend to all prohibitions, not just {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On {\i Reish Lakish}\u8217's view, therefore, even wine and shaving would not carry {\i malkus} during the period the {\i nezirut} has not fully taken force. The {\i nezirut} is not {\i muttar} \u8212- it is forbidden \u8212- but it is in the category of {\i patur aval assur}: prohibited but not punishable with {\i malkus}. The {\i gemara} is quite clear on this: even under {\i Reish Lakish}\u8217's reading, the acts are transgressions; they simply do not carry corporeal punishment until the {\i nezirut} attains full force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then resolves the question definitively against {\i Reish Lakish}: {\i mi she-haya tamei ve-nazar, assur legaleiach velishtos yayin, ve-im gilach ve-shata yayin ve-nitma, harei zeh sofeig arba\u8217'im} \u8212- a person who was {\i tamei} and accepted {\i nezirut} is forbidden to shave, drink wine, or become {\i tamei}, and if they did any of these, they receive forty {\i malkus}. This is a direct, explicit statement that the full {\i nezirut} applies with full {\i malkus} even when it was accepted in a state of {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tiyufta} \u8212- a complete refutation. Though the {\i gemara} had previously attempted a weak answer to earlier challenges, {\i Tosafot} notes that the same answer cannot rescue {\i Reish Lakish} here. The simple meaning of this {\i baraita} stands as a conclusive proof that the {\i nezirut} takes full effect from the moment of acceptance, even in {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Nezirut Applies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus concludes: accepting {\i nezirut} while {\i tamei} creates a full {\i nezirut} immediately, with all its prohibitions and consequences, including {\i malkus}. The {\i only} difference between such a person and a {\i nazir tahor} who became {\i tamei} is structural: the former begins counting on day seven, the latter on day eight, and the {\i korban tumah} applies only to the latter. Everything else \u8212- the prohibitions, the {\i malkus}, the full range of {\i nezirut} obligations \u8212- is identical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tarrying Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unintentional Tumah Fram\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i nezirut} question settled, the {\i gemara} turns to a new and subtle inquiry raised by {\i Rava}: when a {\i nazir} is in a cemetery, is {\i shehiya} \u8212- continued tarrying \u8212- necessary in order to become liable for {\i malkus}, or does liability attach immediately?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question requires some setup. The general framework involves situations where a person incurs {\i tumah} unintentionally, through no fault of their own. In such cases, no {\i malkus} attaches to the initial act since it was not willful. The question is whether the {\i act of remaining} \u8212- after becoming aware of one\u8217's situation \u8212- can serve as the operative transgression that generates liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hamikdash Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The model {\i Rava} has in mind comes from the laws of {\i tumah} in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. It is forbidden for a {\i tamei} person to be present in the {\i Azara}. If someone was in the {\i Beis Hamikdash} and accidentally became {\i tamei} \u8212- for example, an animal was slaughtered improperly and became a {\i neveila} and the person touched it inadvertently \u8212- no {\i malkus} attaches to the initial act of becoming {\i tamei}, since it was entirely unintentional. But if someone then warns this person that they are {\i tamei} and cannot remain, and the person stays anyway, the {\i act of staying} is treated as an active, willful transgression equivalent to entering the {\i Beis Hamikdash} in a state of {\i tumah}. The tarrying becomes the operative act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava}\u8217's question is whether this same principle applies to a {\i nazir} in a cemetery. The case he envisions: someone in a cemetery accepts {\i nezirut}, or is in a cemetery and becomes a {\i nazir} \u8212- and the question is whether {\i shehiya} can serve as the qualifying act of transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 With Warning Given\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately clarifies that if someone warned the person \u8212- \u8220"do not accept {\i nezirut} here, you are in a cemetery\u8221" \u8212- and the person did so anyway, {\i shehiya} is entirely irrelevant. The willful act of accepting {\i nezirut} in the cemetery, done deliberately after receiving a warning, is itself the transgression. There is no need for any subsequent tarrying to create liability. Just as a {\i nazir} who deliberately enters a cemetery incurs {\i malkus} through the act of entering (not through staying), here the act of accepting {\i nezirut} while warned is the operative act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The genuine case where the question of {\i shehiya} arises is when the initial situation was {\i ones} \u8212- entirely beyond the person\u8217's control. The {\i gemara} gives a striking example: a person being transported in a sealed container \u8212- {\i shidah, teivah, u\u8217'migdal}, a large enclosed box \u8212- through a cemetery. Since the container is large enough not to become {\i tamei} itself and is sealed above and below, the person inside is protected from {\i tumat meis} by its {\i ohel}-like enclosure. But then a companion comes along and removes the covering \u8212- {\i para me\u8217'alav maziva} \u8212- exposing the person to {\i tumah} against their will. This is the paradigmatic {\i ones}: the person became {\i tamei} in the cemetery through no fault of their own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now someone warns them: \u8220"You are {\i tamei} and you are a {\i nazir} \u8212- you must leave.\u8221" Do they incur {\i malkus} by remaining? Is the act of staying the operative transgression, as it would be in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames this as a straight question: {\i ki gamrinan shehiya b\u8217'veis hamikdash, aval a\u8217'barai lo} \u8212- perhaps the principle of {\i shehiya} as the operative act was taught specifically for the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, and does not apply outside it, as in the case of a {\i nazir} in a cemetery. Or perhaps \u8212- {\i o dilma lo shna} \u8212- it makes no difference, and {\i shehiya} can constitute the transgression anywhere. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i teiku}. The question remains undecided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sacred Space vs Sacred P\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The unresolved status of this question points to something conceptually significant, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's hesitation is not merely technical. There is a profound structural difference between the two cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, the problem with {\i tumah} is that a {\i tamei} person is defiling a sacred {\i space}. The {\i Beis Hamikdash} retains its {\i kedushah} regardless \u8212- its sanctity is not diminished by the presence of {\i tumah}, it simply does not belong there. The continued presence of a {\i tamei} person in that space is an ongoing, active violation of its {\i kedushah}. Each moment of tarrying is another moment of maintaining {\i tumah} in a place that cannot tolerate it. The {\i shehiya} is therefore a natural extension of the initial violation, and treating it as the operative act makes intuitive sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With a {\i nazir} in a cemetery, however, the dynamic is inverted. The {\i nazir} is the {\i kadosh} \u8212- the sacred person. The cemetery is not a place whose {\i kedushah} is being violated; it is a profane space that the {\i nazir}, as a sanctified person, should not be in. But once the {\i nazir} is {\i tamei}, their own {\i kedushah} has already been compromised. The {\i nazir tamei} is not the same as the {\i Beis Hamikdash} with {\i tumah} inside it \u8212- the {\i Beis Hamikdash} does not itself become {\i tamei}, whereas the {\i nazir} has already become {\i tamei}. One might therefore argue that remaining in the cemetery does not continue to violate anything at an escalating rate, since the {\i kedushah} of the {\i nazir} has already been compromised. It is less clear that staying, rather than leaving, constitutes a fresh or ongoing act of transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- between a sacred {\i space} being violated by an impure {\i object}, and a sacred {\i person} whose sanctity has been compromised in a profane space \u8212- is what the {\i gemara} leaves as {\i teiku}. It is a genuinely difficult conceptual question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaving Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaving Package Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} raises yet another question about the {\i nazir} who began in a state of {\i tumah}: {\i teun giluach o lav} \u8212- is such a person required to shave on day seven (when they become {\i tahor} and begin counting) or not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question requires some background. Normally, when a {\i nazir tahor} becomes {\i tamei} and must restart their {\i nezirut}, the restart involves two things: bringing a {\i korban} and shaving. Both are part of the ritual by which the {\i nazir} transitions from the {\i tumah} state back to the {\i taharah} state and begins counting again. We have already established that a {\i nazir} who began as {\i tamei} does not bring a {\i korban}. But does that mean the {\i giluach} \u8212- the shaving \u8212- also does not apply? Or is the shaving independently required to restart the {\i nezirut}, even without a {\i korban}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first attempt to answer comes from our {\i mishna} itself: {\i korban tumah hu d\u8217'lo maity} \u8212- the {\i mishna} says only that such a person does not bring a {\i korban tumah}. It does not explicitly say there is no shaving. One might infer from the {\i mishna}\u8217's silence on shaving that shaving is still required. Perhaps the {\i mishna} only addressed the {\i korban} because that was the {\i halacha} under discussion, and the shaving \u8212- which is also part of restarting \u8212- still applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Day Seven vs Eig\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} returns: {\i ein bein tamei nazir l\u8217'nazir tahor shenitma} \u8212- there is no difference between a person who was {\i tamei} and accepted {\i nezirut} and a person who was {\i tahor} and became {\i tamei}, except that the {\i tamei} person starts counting on day seven while the {\i tahor} person starts on day eight. The {\i baraita} enumerates this as the {\i only} difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} presses: if there is also a difference regarding shaving \u8212- one must shave and the other need not \u8212- why does the {\i baraita} not mention it? The {\i baraita} seems to say the {\i only} difference is day seven versus day eight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Package Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ma ta\u8217'am eino meivi korban tumah? Mishum dela ba\u8217'ei giluach} \u8212- why does the person not bring a {\i korban tumah}? Because they do not need to shave. The reasoning works both ways and establishes that the two \u8212- {\i korban} and {\i giluach} \u8212- are a single package. If you are not bringing the {\i korban}, you are not doing the associated shaving. The {\i baraita}\u8217's statement that the {\i only} difference is the timing (day seven versus day eight) implicitly conveys that this person does not have the whole ritual of day eight: no {\i korban} and no shaving. They simply become {\i tahor} on day seven and begin counting. The {\i mishna}\u8217's mention of the {\i korban} alone is sufficient to establish both, since {\i giluach} is bundled into the {\i korban} ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metzora vs Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumat Meit Destroys\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i baraita} relating to a {\i nazir} who becomes a {\i metzora} \u8212- a person with {\i tzaraat}. The {\i tannaitic} rule is as follows: only {\i tumat meis} (corpse-{\i tumah}) destroys the earlier days a {\i nazir} has already counted. If someone was ten days into their {\i nezirut} and became {\i tamei meis}, those ten days are lost and they must begin again from zero. But if that same {\i nazir} became a {\i metzora}, those ten days are {\i not} lost. Being a {\i metzora} puts the {\i nezirut} on hiatus \u8212- the days of {\i metzora} status do not count toward the thirty \u8212- but the earlier days already banked remain intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale is straightforward: the prohibition that destroys earlier days is specifically {\i tumat meis}. Since the {\i nazir} did not contract {\i tumat meis}, the earlier days survive. The {\i nazir} who becomes a {\i metzora} finishes the {\i metzora} process and then counts however many remaining days are needed to complete thirty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One complicating factor is worth noting: becoming {\i tahor} from {\i tzaraat} requires shaving. If the {\i nazir-metzora} must shave to exit {\i metzora} status, the hair that was growing is lost, raising the question of whether the {\i nazir} can finish their thirty days without thirty days of continuous hair growth. {\i Tosafot} raises this but the {\i gemara} does not resolve it here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaving Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} attempts to establish this {\i halacha} through a {\i kal va-chomer} argument. First it asks: how do I know that the days of being a {\i metzora muchlat} (sealed off) do not count toward a {\i nazir}\u8217's thirty days? Taking it for granted that those days do not {\i destroy} the earlier period (since there was no {\i tumat meis}), perhaps they {\i do} count, since the {\i metzora} period is not inherently antithetical to {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument from analogy proceeds: just as when a {\i nazir} is {\i tamei}, the {\i tumah} ends by shaving and bringing a {\i korban} \u8212- {\i megalei\u8217'ach u\u8217'meivi korban} \u8212- and those days do not count; so too when the {\i nazir} is a {\i metzora}, the {\i metzora} period also ends with shaving and bringing a {\i korban} \u8212- {\i af yimei chaluto megalei\u8217'ach u\u8217'meivi korban} \u8212- and therefore those days should similarly not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this analogy. {\i Im amarta b\u8217'imei tumah} \u8212- the case of {\i tumat meis} is fundamentally different. When a {\i nazir} becomes {\i tamei meis}, those days destroy the earlier period: {\i she\u8217'ken mevatel bahen es hakodmin}. The {\i tumah} was so antithetical to {\i nezirut} that it wiped out everything preceding it. Of course those days do not count. But {\i tomar bimei chaluto} \u8212- can you say the same of the {\i metzora} period? {\i She\u8217'eino mevatel es hakedomim} \u8212- being a {\i metzora} does not destroy the earlier days. Since the earlier days survive, the argument runs the other direction: the {\i metzora} period is not so antithetical to {\i nezirut}, and therefore one might say the days {\i can} count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then introduces a new comparison involving the {\i nazir} in the cemetery \u8212- the case of {\i nazir bakever} \u8212- and the phrase {\i she\u8217'searo raui letiglachas}: \u8220"his hair is fit for shaving.\u8221" {\i Rav Pappa} interprets this as referring to {\i tiglachat tumah} \u8212- the shaving done by a {\i nazir tamei} to restart their {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the {\i baraita} is establishing the following comparison: the {\i nazir} in the cemetery, even though he began as {\i tamei} and brings no {\i korban}, is still required to shave his hair (on this reading, to restart his {\i nezirut}). He is therefore still operating within the framework of {\i nezirut} law in a meaningful sense. His hair is \u8220"fit for shaving\u8221" because the {\i nezirut} laws still actively govern him. A {\i metzora nazir}, by contrast, has no shaving of the hair specifically in the context of {\i nezirut}; the shaving that occurs is about exiting {\i metzora} status, not about {\i nezirut}. Since the {\i nazir} in the cemetery remains more squarely within the {\i nezirut} mode \u8212- his days still do not count \u8212- a {\i metzora nazir}, who is less connected to active {\i nezirut} governance, should certainly have his days not count either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Safety Interpretati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second interpretation rejects this reading: {\i lo, tiglachat taharah} \u8212- no, the phrase refers not to shaving at the {\i beginning} of the restart, but to shaving at the {\i end} of the {\i nezirut} period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the {\i baraita} is not assuming that the {\i nazir bakever} shaves to restart. Rather: the {\i nazir} in the cemetery does {\i not} shave to restart (as the {\i gemara} concluded earlier \u8212- the shaving is part of the {\i korban} package and this person does not shave). His hair, therefore, remains intact as he begins counting on day seven. That hair is \u8220"fit for shaving\u8221" in the sense that it will eventually be shaved at the {\i end} of the thirty-day {\i nezirut} \u8212- it is preserved for the final {\i tiglachat} of {\i tahara}. The hair is safe; his {\i nezirut} is fully on track.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i metzora nazir}, by contrast, will have to shave his hair midway through \u8212- to exit {\i metzora} status \u8212- and that hair will not be present at the conclusion of the {\i nezirut}. His hair is {\i not} safe for the ultimate {\i tiglachat}. This makes his {\i metzora} period a more significant contradiction to the {\i nezirut} \u8212- the hair, which is central to the identity and process of {\i nezirut}, will be lost before the period concludes. If the {\i nazir} whose hair remains intact and whose {\i nezirut} is comparatively less disrupted still cannot count those days, the {\i metzora nazir} whose hair will be lost before the end of the period certainly cannot count those days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} supports this second interpretation: {\i hachi nami mistabra} \u8212- this is the more sensible reading \u8212- {\i de-i salka daitach tiglachat tumah}: if the {\i baraita} were talking about {\i tiglachat tumah}, how could it contrast the {\i nazir tamei} (who shaves for {\i tumah}) with the {\i metzora} (who does not shave for {\i tumah}), since a {\i metzora} also shaves as part of the {\i tumah}-{\i taharah} process? {\i Yemei chaluto mi lo ba\u8217'ei tiglachat} \u8212- the days of being a {\i metzora muchlat} also require shaving. So the contrast cannot be about which type of {\i tumah} requires shaving, since both do. Therefore: {\i lo tiglachat di-nezirut katani} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is comparing the shaving done as part of {\i nezirut} itself, not the general shaving associated with {\i tumah} processes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} reaches the end of this analysis in a state of unresolved ambiguity. The {\i baraita} attempted to prove that the days of {\i metzora} do not count toward a {\i nazir}\u8217's thirty days, and the comparison it draws involves the shaving of the {\i nazir bakever}. But as the two interpretations show, the {\i baraita} can be read either as assuming that the {\i nazir bakever} does shave to restart (the {\i Rav Pappa} reading), or as assuming that he does not shave and his hair remains safe for the final {\i tiglachat} (the second interpretation).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means the original question of {\i Rav Ashi} \u8212- does a {\i nazir} who began as {\i tamei} require shaving on day seven to restart his {\i nezirut}? \u8212- remains unanswered by this {\i baraita}. The {\i baraita} is genuinely ambiguous on the point, and the {\i halacha} is not yet determined.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 18\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Tamei - Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Tamei Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a {\i tashma} two lines from the top of 18a, continuing the discussion of a {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- a {\i nazir} who accepted his {\i nezirut} while in a state of {\i tumah}, specifically {\i tumas meis}. Such a person cannot begin counting his thirty days of {\i nezirut} until he becomes {\i tahor}, a process that takes seven days. The question that has been driving this discussion concerns what happens on day seven: must this {\i nazir} shave his head, even though he does not bring {\i korbanot} on day eight?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the {\i nazir tamei} does not bring {\i korbanot} on day eight is already established \u8212- the {\i mishna} and the {\i gemara} have consistently maintained that the {\i korbanot} associated with {\i tumas nazir} apply only when someone began {\i tahor} and subsequently became {\i tamei}. But that conclusion regarding {\i korbanot} does not automatically resolve the question of shaving. Shaving and the {\i korbanot} are performed on different days: the shaving is on day seven, explicitly connected in the {\i pesukim} to the {\i taharah} process, while the {\i korbanot} are on day eight. Perhaps the shaving of a {\i nazir} who is completing a {\i taharah} process serves an independent function \u8212- enabling the resumption of {\i nezirut} \u8212- and would therefore be required even when no {\i korbanot} are brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesukim - Days 7 & 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevant {\i pesukim} from {\i Parshas Naso} are worth reading carefully, as they clarify the structure of the inquiry. The Torah states: {\i v\u8217'chi yamut meis alav befesa pitom v\u8217'timei rosh nizro} \u8212- if a corpse dies upon him suddenly and the head of his {\i nezirut} becomes {\i tamei} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'gilach rosho b\u8217'yom taharato b\u8217'yom hashvi\u8217'i yegalhenu}, he shall shave his head on the day he becomes pure, which is day seven. Then, {\i u\u8217'vayom hashmini}, on day eight, he brings two turtledoves or two young pigeons to the {\i kohen} at the entrance of the {\i ohel moed}. One is a {\i chatas} and one is an {\i olah}. The {\i kohen} atones for him, {\i v\u8217'kidesh et rosho bayom hahu}, he sanctifies his head on that day \u8212- presumably day eight \u8212- and {\i v\u8217'hizir l\u8217'Hashem et yemei nizro}, he dedicates to God the days of his {\i nezirut}. Finally, {\i v\u8217'hevi keves ben shnato l\u8217'asham}, he brings a lamb as an {\i asham}, and {\i v\u8217'hayomim harishonim yiplu ki tamei nizro} \u8212- the earlier days are lost because his {\i nezirut} became {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure thus shows clearly that the shaving is on day seven, tied directly to the {\i taharah} process, while the {\i korbanot} are on day eight. The {\i gemara}\u8217's question therefore has genuine textual grounding: since the shaving is connected to {\i taharah} and not explicitly to the {\i korbanot}, perhaps it is required independently, even in the case of someone who began {\i tamei} and whose {\i taharah} process on day seven marks the beginning of his {\i nezirut} of {\i tahara}, even without any {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita - Tahor/Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with a decisive {\i baraita}. The verse states {\i v\u8217'timei rosh nizro} \u8212- the head of his {\i nezirut} became {\i tamei} \u8212- and the {\i baraita} explains: {\i b\u8217'tahor she\u8217'nitma hakatuv midaber}, the verse is speaking about a {\i nazir tahor} who became {\i tamei}. It is precisely such a person who requires {\i ha\u8217'avarat se\u8217'ar v\u8217'hava\u8217'as tziporin} \u8212- the removal of hair and the bringing of the bird-offerings. And it concludes: {\i u\u8217'fatar et haNazir bakever} \u8212- this formulation exempts the {\i nazir} who accepted his {\i nezirut} in a graveyard, who does not require the shaving of the hair or the bringing of the birds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then acknowledges that one might have constructed a {\i kal vachomer} in the other direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Refuted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} would run as follows: if a {\i nazir tahor} who subsequently became {\i tamei} \u8212- someone who at least began on the right foot \u8212- requires the full process of shaving and bird-offerings, then how much more so should someone who accepted {\i nezirut} from the outset as {\i tamei}, having never properly begun, be required to undergo this process before commencing a {\i nezirut} of {\i tahara}? This is not a frivolous argument. The logic is that the harder it is to begin, the more rigorous the entry requirements should be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To this the {\i baraita} responds: {\i talmud lomar v\u8217'timei rosh nizro} \u8212- the verse\u8217's very phrasing, describing someone whose head {\i became} {\i tamei} (implying it was previously {\i tahor}), confines the law to {\i b\u8217'mi shehayah tahor v\u8217'nitma hakatuv midaber}, one who was {\i tahor} and became {\i tamei}. The Torah is not making a logical point; it is limiting the law by description. Only that person requires the shaving and the birds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a classic form of {\i midrash halacha}: the {\i derashah} is stated, and then the text steps back to explain why the {\i derashah} was necessary \u8212- because without it, one might have reasoned logically toward the opposite conclusion. Sometimes {\i midreshei halacha} present arguments that could cut in either direction, and the {\i pasuk} resolves the ambiguity. Here, only one direction of the {\i kal vachomer} is presented, but the structure is the same: logic might have led one way, and the {\i pasuk} redirects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei - No Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion is clear: the entire complex of rituals \u8212- {\i korbanot} and shaving alike \u8212- applies exclusively to someone who began {\i tahor}. A person who accepted {\i nezirut} while {\i tamei} simply undergoes the normal {\i taharah} process, and on day seven begins his {\i nezirut} of {\i tahara} without any shaving of the hair. {\i Shema mina.}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 When Restart Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei vs Tahor Alike\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to a related question. It cites a teaching that distinguishes between two types of {\i nazir}: one who accepted {\i nezirut} while {\i tamei} ({\i tamei she\u8217'nazar}), and a {\i nazir tahor} who subsequently became {\i tamei} ({\i nazir she\u8217'nitma}). The only difference between them, according to this formulation, is that for the {\i tamei she\u8217'nazar}, his seventh day counts toward the new tally \u8212- since his {\i taharah} is complete on day seven, he begins counting from that point. By contrast, for the {\i nazir tahor she\u8217'nitma}, day seven does not count, because he is obligated to bring {\i korbanot} on day eight, and his new period of {\i nezirut} only begins on day eight, after the {\i korban} is brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Or so we have assumed throughout. The {\i gemara} now reveals that this position \u8212- that the {\i nazir tahor she\u8217'nitma} restarts only on day eight \u8212- is not unanimous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Tanaim Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attributes the position we have been working with to {\i Rebbi} specifically. {\i Amar Rav Chisda Rebbi hi} \u8212- this is the opinion of {\i Rebbi}. The reason for saying so is that if the source were {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda}, a different and previously unintroduced {\i tanna}, the picture would look quite different. We are now introduced to this dispute for the first time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose - Day 7\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda} holds a fundamentally different position: {\i amar nezirut detahara mishvi\u8217'i chayla} \u8212- the {\i nezirut} of {\i tahara} takes effect from day seven, the very day the {\i nazir} becomes {\i tahor}. Even in the classic case of a {\i nazir tahor} who became {\i tamei}, even though the {\i korbanot} are brought on day eight, the resumption of his {\i nezirut} of {\i tahara} begins immediately upon becoming {\i tahor} on day seven. The {\i korban} obligation exists independently; the restarting of the count is tied to {\i taharah}, not to the {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this dispute is a {\i baraita} interpreting the phrase {\i v\u8217'kidesh et rosho bayom hahu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesuk - Day 7 Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i pesukim}: the Torah describes the shaving on day seven ({\i v\u8217'gilach rosho b\u8217'yom taharato b\u8217'yom hashvi\u8217'i yegalhenu}), then on day eight the {\i korbanot} are brought ({\i u\u8217'vayom hashmini yavi shetei torim}), after which the {\i kohen} atones, and then: {\i v\u8217'kidesh et rosho bayom hahu} \u8212- he sanctifies his head on that day. At first read, \u8220"that day\u8221" plainly refers to day eight, the day being discussed. {\i Rebbi} reads it this way: {\i divrei Rebbi} \u8212- the day he brings his {\i korbanot}. {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda} reads it differently: {\i beyom tiglachto} \u8212- no, \u8220"that day\u8221" refers back to the day spoken of earlier, namely day seven, the day of shaving. He reads {\i bayom hahu} as a reference to the previously mentioned day, not the one currently under discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly not the {\i pshat} of the {\i pasuk}, and the {\i gemara} will address this. But before unpacking the precise derivation, it ties the dispute into a well-known {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Tumos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i nazir shenitma tumos harbe} \u8212- a {\i nazir} who became {\i tamei} many times \u8212- {\i eino mevi ela korban echad}, brings only one {\i korban}. This requires explanation, because intuitively, each separate act of becoming {\i tamei} after having been {\i tahor} ought to generate its own {\i korban} obligation. The question is: under what circumstances is it possible to have multiple episodes of {\i tumah} and {\i tahara} and yet incur only one {\i korban}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} says: this {\i mishnah} reflects the opinion of {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda}, for whom {\i nezirut detahara} begins on day seven. The scenario is constructed as follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 7 Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nazir} became {\i tamei}, underwent the seven-day {\i taharah} process with the sprinkling of {\i parah adumah} ashes on days three and seven, became {\i tahor} on day seven, and then became {\i tamei} again \u8212- all on day seven itself. According to {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda}, the moment he became {\i tahor} on day seven, his {\i nezirut detahara} restarted. He then became {\i tamei} again, meaning all the conditions for a second {\i korban} obligation would seem to be in place: he was genuinely a {\i nazir tahor} who became {\i tamei}. Yet the {\i mishnah} says only one {\i korban}. The explanation: {\i keivan de\u8217'lo yatza sha\u8217'ah re\u8217'uyah l\u8217'havi korban} \u8212- since the time fit for bringing a {\i korban} has not yet arrived. The {\i korban} is brought on day eight. On day seven, even after restarting {\i nezirut}, there is no {\i chiyuv korban} yet. Therefore, regardless of how many times he became {\i tahor} and {\i tamei} on day seven, only one {\i korban} is ever incurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario is necessarily one where the {\i taharah} and subsequent {\i tumah} occur on day seven of one cycle, followed by another day-seven repetition of the same pattern. It is not literally the identical calendar day repeated, but successive cycles in which the action occurs on the seventh day before day eight\u8217's {\i korban} time arrives. The key structural point is: he entered a genuine state of {\i nezirut detahara}, became {\i tamei}, yet never reached day eight, and so the {\i korban} obligation from the first cycle was never yet triggered. Under this account, multiple such cycles yield only one {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi - Day 8 View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why RY Yose Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: why must we say this {\i mishnah} goes like {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda}? Why not say it goes like {\i Rebbi}? To make it work for {\i Rebbi}, one would need to construct a scenario where the {\i nazir} genuinely restarted his {\i nezirut detahara} \u8212- which for {\i Rebbi} requires entering day eight \u8212- and then became {\i tamei} again, yet still without having yet incurred a {\i korban} obligation. Is there such a window?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The suggestion is: day eight has two parts \u8212- night and day. {\i Korbanot} are brought only during the day. But for {\i Rebbi}, who says the restart occurs simply when the time of the {\i korban} arrives, not when the {\i korban} is actually brought \u8212- perhaps entering the nighttime of day eight suffices to restart the counting, even though the {\i korban} itself cannot be brought until daybreak. If so, one could construct a scenario where the {\i nazir} enters night of day eight, restarts his {\i nezirut detahara}, and then becomes {\i tamei} before sunrise \u8212- before the time when he would be obligated in a {\i korban}. That would be multiple {\i tumot} and one {\i korban}, even for {\i Rebbi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses on this: since {\i Rav Chisda} did not offer this solution and instead attributed the {\i mishnah} to {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda}, it would appear he holds that the nighttime does not function as a separate window in the way this suggestion assumes. There is a deeper principle at stake here, which {\i Rav Adda bar Ahava} will articulate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle bears some unpacking. According to {\i Rebbi}, the reason the {\i nazir} restarts his count on day eight is that day eight is the {\i zman} \u8212- the time designated for bringing the {\i korban}. It is not that the act of bringing the {\i korban} itself triggers the restart; it is that day eight is recognized as the moment when this transition becomes operative, because it is the time of the {\i korban}. Now, what is the relationship between the {\i nezirut detahara} restart and the {\i korban} obligation? For {\i Rebbi}, they are tied to the same conceptual anchor: the {\i zman korban}, the time designated for the {\i korban}. This is the key insight that {\i Rav Adda bar Ahava} makes explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entire thrust of the proposed scenario \u8212- that night and day might be \u8220"out of sync\u8221" with respect to restart of {\i nezirut} and {\i chiyuv korban} \u8212- rests on the assumption that these two things could come apart. But they cannot, because for {\i Rebbi} both are functions of the same underlying concept: the time of the {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Night/Day Sync\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Adda bar Ahava ha b\u8217'ha talya} \u8212- these two matters are bound together. The reason {\i nezirut detahara} restarts on day eight for {\i Rebbi} is precisely because day eight is considered the time of the {\i korban}. It follows inescapably that the two \u8212- restart of {\i nezirut} and {\i chiyuv korban} \u8212- track each other identically. They move together; they cannot come apart.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one holds that nighttime is {\i mechusar zman} \u8212- that it is still considered lacking in time, meaning the {\i korban} time has not yet truly arrived \u8212- then on that view, neither the {\i korban} obligation nor the {\i nezirut} restart has kicked in at night. Both are deferred to the daytime. And if one holds that nighttime is not {\i mechusar zman} \u8212- that the time of the {\i korban} is already considered present at night \u8212- then both the {\i korban} obligation and the {\i nezirut} restart take effect at night. Either way, they are synchronized. The attempt to exploit a gap between them \u8212- where the {\i nezirut} restarts at night but the {\i korban} obligation only kicks in by day \u8212- is structurally impossible. {\i Ha b\u8217'ha talya}: they are interdependent. No arbitrage is available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Day 8 Refined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings {\i Rebbi}\u8217's position into sharper focus. {\i Rebbi} holds that the {\i nazir} restarts his count on day eight \u8212- not because the {\i korban} was actually brought, but because day eight is the time designated for bringing the {\i korban}. Whether that time begins at nightfall or at sunrise depends on one\u8217's general position regarding the status of the night preceding a daytime {\i korban} \u8212- but in either case, the restart and the {\i chiyuv} are always in step with each other. The {\i nazir} can never find himself in a state where his {\i nezirut detahara} has restarted but he has not yet incurred the {\i chiyuv korban}. Consequently, for {\i Rebbi}, it is impossible to construct a scenario on day eight where multiple {\i tumot} yield only a single {\i korban} obligation in an interesting way. Any scenario where the {\i nazir} is {\i tamei} again on day eight is either trivial (he never truly restarted) or incorrect (he has already incurred the {\i korban} obligation and the second {\i tumah} generates a second obligation). Only {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda}, for whom the restart occurs on day seven while the {\i korban} time is not until day eight, can generate the genuine gap that makes the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling non-trivial.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korban Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Tumos Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i baraita} itself \u8212- introduced with the word {\i gufa} \u8212- and examines the full spectrum of opinions on when exactly the {\i nazir} restarts his count after becoming {\i tamei}. The text under discussion: {\i nitma beshvii v\u8217'chazar v\u8217'nitma beshvii}, became {\i tamei} on the seventh and again on the seventh \u8212- one {\i korban} only. {\i Nitma bishmini v\u8217'chazar v\u8217'nitma bishmini} \u8212- became {\i tamei} on the eighth and again on the eighth \u8212- a {\i korban} for each one. The debate within this {\i baraita} centers on the following: when does the counting restart?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Matchil u\u8217'moneh miyad} \u8212- he begins counting immediately. This, following the {\i gra}\u8217's emendation which removes the word {\i Eliezer}, is {\i divrei Rebbi}. And we now encounter the full formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim - Chatat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} take a different position: {\i korban echad al hakol ad sheyavi chatato} \u8212- one {\i korban} covers everything, until he brings his {\i chatas}. Only once the {\i chatas} is brought does the counting restart. If he becomes {\i tamei} again after bringing his {\i chatas}, even before bringing the {\i olah} or the {\i asham}, that generates a new {\i korban} obligation \u8212- because the {\i chatas} already restarted the count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the structure of this debate: there are three {\i korbanot} involved \u8212- two birds ({\i chatas} and {\i olah}) and a lamb ({\i asham}). The {\i chachamim} maintain that the {\i chatas} is the pivotal {\i korban} for purposes of restarting the {\i nezirut}. The {\i olah}, as will be explained, is not central to any {\i taharah} or status-change process; it is universally understood to be a voluntary gift-offering ({\i doron be\u8217'alma}) and requires no special verse to exclude it from being a necessary prerequisite. The {\i chatas}, by contrast, is precisely the type of {\i korban} that marks a change of status, and the {\i chachamim} hold that without it, the {\i nezirut detahara} does not resume.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical formulation makes this clear. {\i Heivi chataso v\u8217'nitma} \u8212- he brought his {\i chatas} and became {\i tamei} \u8212- {\i heivi chataso v\u8217'nitma} \u8212- brought his {\i chatas} and became {\i tamei} again \u8212- {\i meivi korban al kol echad v\u8217'echad}. Each {\i tumah} after a {\i chatas} has been brought generates its own full obligation. Before the {\i chatas} is brought, no matter how many times the cycle recurs, it remains a single process with a single {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize {\i Rebbi} versus {\i chachamim} cleanly: {\i Rebbi} says the restart occurs on day eight regardless of whether any {\i korban} was actually brought \u8212- the arrival of day eight\u8217's {\i zman korban} is what matters. The {\i chachamim} say the restart occurs only when the {\i chatas} is actually brought. A {\i nazir} who reached day eight but did not yet bring his {\i chatas} has not yet restarted his count. Additionally: {\i heivi chataso v\u8217'lo heivi ashamo moneh} \u8212- even if the {\i nazir} brought only his {\i chatas} and has not yet brought his {\i asham}, he begins counting. The {\i chatas} suffices; the {\i asham} is not required to trigger the restart.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael - Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael beno shel Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka} disputes this final point. {\i Keshem shechatas\u1086 ? ikvato, kach ashamo me\u8217'akvo} \u8212- just as the {\i chatas} holds him back from restarting, so too does the {\i asham} hold him back. Both the {\i chatas} and the {\i asham} are the central {\i korbanot} of {\i taharah}-change processes \u8212- appearing in the {\i taharah} of the {\i metzora}, the {\i zavah}, and similar cases \u8212- and {\i Rabbi Yishmael} holds that both must be brought before the counting resumes. Only the {\i olah} is dispensable, because an {\i olah} is never structurally central to any transition of status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus {\i Rabbi Yishmael}\u8217's position is that the {\i nazir} must bring both the {\i chatas} and the {\i asham} before his {\i nezirut detahara} restarts. The {\i olah} he may defer; the other two he may not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Pesuk Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now seeks the {\i pesuk}-based grounding for each of these positions. For {\i Rebbi}: {\i amar kra v\u8217'kidesh et rosho bayom hahu} \u8212- the verse says \u8220"on that day,\u8221" which implies the day itself, independent of the {\i korbanot} being brought. {\i Af al pi shelo heivi korbanotav} \u8212- even without having brought the {\i korbanot}, the day itself is the trigger.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i chachamim}, the verse {\i bayom hahu} is also relevant, but it functions differently. They agree that the day has significance \u8212- specifically that it exempts the {\i olah} from being necessary. The phrase \u8220"on that day\u8221" tells them that the day matters, which they interpret as meaning: the {\i asham}, which comes later in the sequence, is not required. But the {\i chatas} \u8212- which is the essential {\i taharah korban} \u8212- is still required. {\i Olah lo baei miuta} \u8212- you need no special verse to exclude the {\i olah}; it is self-evidently non-central. So the {\i chachamim} read {\i bayom hahu} as limiting the requirement to exclude the {\i asham}, but insisting on the {\i chatas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} provides the {\i chachamim}\u8217's derivation: the verse states {\i v\u8217'hizir l\u8217'Hashem et yemei nizro} \u8212- he shall re-dedicate the days of his {\i nezirut} \u8212- and then, {\i v\u8217'hevi keves ben shnato l\u8217'asham} \u8212- and bring the lamb as an {\i asham}. The sequence is: rededicate, then bring the {\i asham}. This teaches that normally one might have assumed the {\i asham} was also a prerequisite, since {\i ashamot} throughout the Torah are generally central to {\i taharah} processes. Yet the order here \u8212- rededication first, {\i asham} afterward \u8212- shows that the {\i asham} is not a {\i me\u8217'akev}: {\i af al pi shelo hevi, hizir} \u8212- even without bringing the {\i asham}, the rededication of {\i nezirut} has already taken effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael Pesuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael ben Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka} reads the same phrase differently. {\i V\u8217'hizir v\u8217'hevi} \u8212- \u8220"he shall rededicate and bring.\u8221" {\i Eimatai hizir? Bi\u8217'zman shehevi} \u8212- when does he rededicate? When he has brought. The conjunction is not sequential ({\i and then bring}) but conditional ({\i when he has brought}). The rededication of {\i nezirut} occurs precisely at the moment that the full {\i korban} process \u8212- {\i chatas} and {\i asham} \u8212- is completed. The {\i olah} he concedes is not required, since {\i olah} is never central. But the two {\i korbanot} that define the transition of status must both be brought before counting may resume.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i Rabbi Yishmael}, the phrase {\i bayom hahu} in the earlier verse serves only to exempt the {\i olah} \u8212- not to permit restarting before the {\i asham} is brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses: if {\i Rabbi Yishmael} reads the sequence in this way, what does he do with the implication of {\i bayom hahu}, which seems to emphasize the day itself? The answer is that {\i bayom hahu} tells him even without the {\i olah} one may restart \u8212- but not without the {\i chatas} and {\i asham}. That is the work the phrase does for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is four distinct positions on when {\i nezirut detahara} restarts: {\i Rabbi Yose ben Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- day seven, when {\i taharah} is achieved; {\i Rebbi} \u8212- day eight, when the {\i zman korban} arrives, regardless of whether the {\i korban} is brought; {\i chachamim} \u8212- when the {\i chatas} is brought; and {\i Rabbi Yishmael ben Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka} \u8212- when both {\i chatas} and {\i asham} are brought.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafos on Gufa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gufa Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the {\i baraita} above was analyzed in depth, the {\i gemara} introduced it with the word {\i gufa} \u8212- \u8220"the body [of the text].\u8221" {\i Tosafos} on 18b notes a textual issue with this usage and takes the opportunity to explain precisely what {\i gufa} means and when it is legitimately used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} states: {\i lo garsinan gufa} \u8212- we do not read {\i gufa} here \u8212- because throughout {\i Shas}, {\i gufa} is only used in a specific context: when the {\i gemara} has previously cited a {\i memra} or a {\i baraita} in passing \u8212- {\i agav grira} \u8212- as part of another discussion, in order to extract a particular point from it, and then returns to that same text to analyze it in greater depth. The word {\i gufa} signals: let us now examine the body of that text properly, rather than just the piece we used earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here, by contrast, the {\i gemara} is not returning to a previously cited {\i baraita} or {\i memra} of an {\i amora}. It is referring to its own {\i stam} language \u8212- the {\i shakla v\u8217'tarya} of the {\i gemara} itself. For that, {\i gufa} is not the appropriate term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gufa vs Amar Mar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} further distinguishes {\i gufa} from {\i amar mar}. Both serve the function of returning to an earlier text, but they reflect different situations. {\i Gufa} applies when the full {\i baraita} or {\i memra} was already quoted in its entirety, but only in passing \u8212- to extract a small point \u8212- and the {\i gemara} now wants to engage with the whole text in depth. {\i Amar mar} applies when only a single line of a text was quoted in the earlier discussion, and the {\i gemara} now quotes the fuller text in order to address it systematically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction, then, is not merely grammatical but structural: {\i gufa} returns to a complete text that was cited instrumentally; {\i amar mar} returns to a text of which only a fragment was previously cited. In both cases the {\i gemara} is returning to source material it has touched earlier; the difference lies in how much of that material was already on the table.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 19\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Tamei - Restart\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Positions Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of an ongoing discussion about a {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- a {\i nazir} who began his {\i nezirut} in a state of {\i taharah}, subsequently became {\i tamei}, underwent the full purification process (sprinkling on days three and seven, shaving on day seven, bringing {\i korbanot} on day eight), and is now required by the {\i Torah} to restart his {\i nezirut}. The simple question is: when exactly does that restart occur?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day 7 vs Day 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Four {\i tannaitic} opinions address this question. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda holds that the {\i nezirut} restarts on day seven \u8212- the day the {\i nazir} achieves {\i taharah}. Rabbi holds that it restarts on day eight, the moment the time arrives that is fit for bringing the {\i korban}, whether that is technically the nighttime or the daytime of day eight, depending on one\u8217's broader position regarding whether nighttime counts as the appropriate time vis-\u224 ?-vis {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two further opinions appear in a {\i braisa}. The {\i Chachamim} hold that the {\i nazir} only restarts after bringing the {\i chatat}. Rabbi Yishmael b\u8217'no shel Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka goes further, requiring not only the {\i chatat} but also the {\i asham} \u8212- the one {\i korban} he excludes from that requirement is the {\i olah}. The {\i Chachamim} and Rabbi derive their positions from the verse {\i vikiddash et rosho bayom hahu} \u8212- \u8220"that day\u8221" carries halachic weight, allowing a restart without all the {\i korbanot}. Rabbi requires none of the {\i korbanot} at all; the {\i Chachamim} require only the {\i chatat}; and Rabbi Yishmael b\u8217'no shel Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka requires the {\i chatat} and the {\i asham}, but not the {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Nazira - Husband A\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i man tanna leha detanu rabbanan} \u8212- who is the {\i tanna} behind a particular {\i braisa} that the Rabbis taught? The {\i braisa} presents the following case: a woman takes a vow of {\i nezirut}, becomes {\i tamei}, and \u8212- as {\i Tosafot} reads the case \u8212- has already sanctified two birds, one as a {\i chatat} and one as an {\i olah}, which she would offer once her {\i tumah} process is complete. After this, her husband performs {\i hafara} \u8212- annulment \u8212- on the day he heard of her vow ({\i beyom shamo}). He need not have heard her take the vow in person; it suffices that he learned of it. The annulment might be on the same day as everything else or several days later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is a peculiar legal situation: this woman is no longer a {\i nezirah}, yet she had been a {\i nezirat tamei} while she was a {\i nezirah} and was therefore obligated in {\i korbanot}. Ordinarily, a {\i nazir} cannot cease being a {\i nazir} without undergoing the full process. But this woman has managed to exit {\i nezirut} while still technically obligated in the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir tamei}. The question is: what does she bring?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braisa: Chatas Not Olah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} rules: {\i mavia chatat ha\u8217'of ve\u8217'eina mavia olat ha\u8217'of} \u8212- she brings the bird {\i chatat} but not the bird {\i olah}. This ruling is striking in several ways. First, it confirms that she is still obligated in at least some of the {\i korbanot} even though she is no longer a {\i nezirah} \u8212- meaning the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir tamei} are not exclusively future-oriented. They address the past violation of {\i nezirut} status, regardless of whether a new {\i nezirut} will follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, and more puzzling, is the split: she brings the {\i chatat} but not the {\i olah}. One might try to frame this as: the {\i chatat} addresses the past while the {\i olah} is future-oriented \u8212- but that framing is difficult, since the {\i olah} is ordinarily the least consequential {\i korban} with respect to starting a new status. As the {\i Gemara} itself notes elsewhere, the {\i olah} seems to function as a kind of {\i doron b\u8217'alma} \u8212- a gift offering \u8212- perhaps meant to get the renewed {\i nezirut} started on a good footing after the {\i chatat} and {\i asham} have addressed the past violation. Why the {\i olah} of all things would be the one excluded here is what the {\i Gemara} will need to explain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further ask: why doesn\u8217't she simply bring the {\i olah} as a voluntary {\i olah}? {\i Tosafot} addresses this directly. The bird had already been sanctified specifically as an {\i olat nazir}. A {\i stam olah} she could bring, but this bird, labeled as an {\i olat nazir}, cannot be brought by someone who is no longer a {\i nazir}. For all other {\i tannaim}, who see the {\i olah} as having no independent functional role in the {\i nezirut} process, this labeling would be irrelevant \u8212- the bird is just an {\i olah} and she could bring it regardless. But according to the {\i tanna} of this {\i braisa}, the {\i olah} does play some role in the world of {\i nezirut}, and that label therefore matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael Exclude\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda identifies the author of this {\i braisa} as Rabbi Yishmael b\u8217'no shel Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka. The connection, as {\i Tosafot} explains, lies precisely in his treatment of the {\i olah}. All other {\i tannaim} held that the {\i olah} is simply irrelevant to the {\i nezirut} process \u8212- it requires no {\i pasuk} to exclude it from the conditions for restarting {\i nezirut}, because it plays no functional role. Rabbi Yishmael b\u8217'no shel Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka, by contrast, needed a {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i bayom hahu} \u8212- specifically to exclude the {\i olah}. The implication is that he held the {\i olah} might otherwise have been considered necessary to begin the new count. He is the one {\i tanna} who sees the {\i olah} as potentially playing some functional role in the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir}. Therefore, when the bird has been sanctified as an {\i olat nazir} and the woman is no longer a {\i nezirah}, she cannot bring it \u8212- precisely because according to him, it was not merely an {\i olah} but something embedded in the {\i nezirut} framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the central difficulty the {\i Gemara} now poses. {\i Mai kasavar} \u8212- what exactly does this {\i tanna} hold about the nature of {\i hafara}? If he holds {\i baal meaker aker} \u8212- that the husband\u8217's {\i hafara} retroactively uproots the vow entirely, as if she were never a {\i nezirah} \u8212- then how could she bring a {\i chatat} at all? Retroactively she was never a {\i nezirah} who became {\i tamei}, so there is no {\i chatat} obligation. On the other hand, if he holds {\i baal meigez gaiaz} \u8212- that the husband merely cuts the {\i nezirut} from this point forward while leaving intact everything that preceded \u8212- then she was fully a {\i nezirah} who became {\i tamei}, and she should bring the {\i olah} just as she brings the {\i chatat}. The {\i Gemara} cannot find a clean middle ground: either both or neither.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer is striking: {\i le\u8217'olam kasavar baal meaker aker} \u8212- he holds that {\i hafara} is indeed retroactive. This is a significant position, running contrary to the standard assumption throughout Masechet Nedarim that a husband only cuts the vow forward and does not retroactively undo it. But the {\i Gemara} is not saying this is {\i halacha}; it is working within this {\i braisa}. If the {\i hafara} is retroactive and she was never a {\i nezirah}, how can she bring a {\i chatat}? The {\i Gemara} answers: this {\i tanna} holds like Rabbi Elazar HaKappar \u8212- and here the {\i Gemara} transitions into a broader discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elazar HaKappar Sinner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Soul/Nefesh Interpretati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i braisa}: Rabbi Elazar HaKappar beRabbi says \u8212- {\i mah talmud lomar, vechiper alav measher chata al hanefesh} \u8212- what does it mean that the verse says the {\i nazir tamei} is atoned because he sinned {\i al hanefesh}, against the soul? Rabbi Elazar HaKappar asks: against which {\i nefesh} did he sin? His answer: {\i ela shetzier atzmo min hayayin} \u8212- he sinned in that he denied himself wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a creative and multilayered interpretation. The verse is ostensibly about the {\i nazir tamei} who brings {\i korbanot} after becoming impure. Rabbi Elazar HaKappar reads it instead as a general statement about the {\i nazir}\u8217's act of self-denial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nefesh Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i nefesh} itself rewards attention here. The verses about fasting on Yom Kippur \u8212- {\i ki kol hanefesh asher teuneh}, {\i veinithem es nafshoseichem} \u8212- use the word {\i nefesh} in the context of affliction and self-denial. We typically think of {\i nefesh} as something spiritual, yet in the {\i Torah} it more commonly refers to life and living beings: {\i kol nefesh asher hu chai}, {\i shivim nefesh}. Scholars of the ancient Near East have noted that the Akkadian cognate of the word {\i nefesh} means \u8220"throat\u8221" \u8212- connecting it to appetite and the physical experience of eating. This linguistic background makes the connection to fasting and self-denial through food and drink feel more direct and {\i pshat}-oriented than it might at first appear. Rashi understands the {\i nefesh} in the verse as relating specifically to pleasure and appetite \u8212- the {\i nazir} sinned against his {\i nefesh} by not drinking wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asceticism Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader point Rabbi Elazar HaKappar is making is that {\i nezirut} is problematic because of its ascetic dimension, the self-denial it imposes. Yet one must pause here: a {\i nazir} can eat fatty meat, drink other beverages, have relations, and engage in most of life\u8217's pleasures \u8212- the restriction is only wine. Is wine alone what makes {\i nezirut} an act of asceticism? It would seem more {\i pshat} to say that wine\u8217's intoxicating properties, not its pleasure value, are what make it the subject of {\i nezirut}. Rabbi Elazar HaKappar is reframing the entire institution: he emphasizes the denial aspect of the wine prohibition as representative of what is problematic about {\i nezirut} as a path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This stands in a broader rabbinic context. The {\i Gemara} elsewhere, and even more prominently in Masechet Nedarim, contains sharp voices condemning the taking of {\i nedarim}. Interestingly, those voices seem somewhat less prominent and dominant with respect to {\i nezirut} specifically \u8212- as if the {\i Gemara} is slightly more tolerant of {\i nezirut} than of random {\i nedarim}. But Rabbi Elazar HaKappar\u8217's voice is critical nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then concludes: {\i umah zeh shelo tzi\u8217'er atzmo ela min hayayin nikra chotei} \u8212- this person who only denied himself wine is called a sinner; {\i hametza\u8217'er atzmo mikol davar al achas kama vekama} \u8212- someone who denies himself everything, such as one who undertakes an unnecessary {\i ta\u8217'anit}, is all the more so a sinner. This connects to the broader rabbinic debate about whether someone who fasts unnecessarily is called {\i kadosh} or {\i chotei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moral Sin Justification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks how any of this actually solves the problem. If the {\i hafara} was retroactive and she was never a {\i nezirah}, how does her bringing a {\i chatat} make sense? Calling the vow of {\i nezirut} a \u8220"sin\u8221" is all very well, but moral or religious censure does not ordinarily create a halachic obligation to bring a {\i chatat}. One cannot bring a {\i chatat} simply because one did something considered blameworthy \u8212- a {\i chatat} requires a specific, concrete {\i aveirah} of the sort for which one is liable to {\i karet}, and so on. A vague sense that something was religiously problematic is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} explains that this is why the {\i braisa} is read as a case where she had already sanctified the bird. The bird exists; it has the sanctity of a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of nazir}. The question is not whether to sanctify it but whether it may be offered. Here {\i Tosafot} invokes an important principle: unlike an animal {\i chatat}, which may never be brought unless the obligation is certain, a bird {\i chatat} may sometimes be brought in a case of doubt. The classic example is a woman who miscarried and cannot determine whether the fetus was viable \u8212- is she a {\i yoleded} obligated in {\i korbanot} or not? She cannot remain in permanent limbo, unable to enter the {\i Beit HaMikdash} for the rest of her life. The tradition \u8212- {\i mesorah} \u8212- permits her to bring a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of al hasafek}, on the basis of uncertainty. A {\i beheima chatat} carries no such latitude; only a bird {\i chatat} can be brought under conditions of doubt or marginal obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that principle in place, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's reasoning can be reconstructed: retroactively, she was never a {\i nezirah}; but the bird has already been sanctified as a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of}. Since there was an element of sin involved in her having accepted {\i nezirut} and denied herself wine \u8212- even if the {\i nezirut} itself was retroactively annulled \u8212- that is sufficient justification to offer the bird, given the additional leniency that attaches to a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of}. The {\i Gemara} readily acknowledges that this is a strange conclusion. It would have been simpler and more intuitive to say that {\i hafara} is only {\i mikan ulehaba} \u8212- prospective rather than retroactive \u8212- and then analyze which {\i korbanot} relate to the past violation and which to the future {\i nezirut}. But that is not the path the {\i Gemara} took.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} also addresses the obvious question: if this justification works, why couldn\u8217't a woman who took {\i nezirut}, never became {\i tamei}, had an animal sanctified as a {\i chatat}, and had her vow annulled also bring the {\i chatat}? The answer is that in the case of a {\i nezirah tehorah}, the {\i chatat} would be a {\i beheima}, an animal {\i chatat}, which categorically cannot be brought under conditions of doubt or marginal justification. Only the {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} of a {\i nazir tamei} benefits from this leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the {\i olah}: here the logic runs in the opposite direction. An {\i olah} is normally the easiest {\i korban} to bring \u8212- anyone can bring one voluntarily at any time. But because according to Rabbi Yishmael b\u8217'no shel Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka the {\i olah} does carry some functional role in the {\i nezirut} framework, this bird labeled as an {\i olat nazir} cannot be brought by someone who is no longer a {\i nezirah}. There is no basis to justify it: the {\i chatat} could be justified by the element of {\i chet} involved in the {\i nezirut}, but the {\i olah} of a {\i nazir} has no anchor in sin \u8212- and a pure voluntary {\i olah} this bird is not, since it was designated as an {\i olat nazir}. It falls into an unresolvable middle ground, and therefore she does not bring it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises one final question against Rabbi Elazar HaKappar: {\i ve-ha be-nazir tamei kesiv} \u8212- the verse he is interpreting, which speaks of sinning {\i al hanefesh}, is written specifically in the context of a {\i nazir tamei}. How can he derive from it a general principle that {\i nezirut} itself constitutes a sin, applying even to a {\i nazir tahor}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i kasavar Rabbi Elazar HaKappar, nazir tahor nami chotei hu} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar HaKappar holds that even a {\i nazir tahor} is called a sinner. The reason the verse specifically mentions the {\i nazir tamei} is {\i de-hainu ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- because this one is {\i hoveh ve-shoneh be-cheit}, he compounded his sin. Either he extended his {\i nezirut} beyond its intended term by allowing himself to become {\i tamei} and thereby prolonging the denial, or, more pointedly, the very fact that he became {\i tamei} reveals that his commitment to the {\i nezirut} was insufficiently serious \u8212- he did not guard himself carefully enough. As the saying goes, the more one practices, the luckier one gets; accidents happen, but careful people have fewer of them. So the {\i nazir tamei} is not just a sinner for the self-denial of wine; he is doubly a sinner because his {\i tumah} demonstrates the superficiality of his commitment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} prefers the more textual reading: the {\i nazir tamei} is called a sinner specifically because he stretched out the suffering unnecessarily \u8212- {\i she\u8217'lo nizhar mitumah veherich tzar al tza\u8217'aro milehanot beyayin} \u8212- he was not careful to avoid {\i tumah} and thereby extended the period during which he denied himself wine. Either way, the principle Rabbi Elazar HaKappar derives is broader than the verse\u8217's context: all {\i nezirut} involves an element of sin.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Leaves & Re-enters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Yatza Venichnas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the next {\i mishnah}: {\i yatza venichnas oleh lo min haminyan} \u8212- if a {\i nazir} who accepted {\i nezirut} in a cemetery leaves and re-enters, it counts toward his {\i minyan}. The phrase {\i oleh lo min haminyan} is potentially confusing here, since it cannot mean he is accumulating days toward his thirty. Every time he re-enters the cemetery, he begins again. What the {\i mishnah} means is clarified by its continuation: {\i ve\u8217'eino mevi korban, oleh lo min haminyan mevi korban tumah} \u8212- he does not bring a {\i korban tumah} from simply having started his {\i nezirut} in the cemetery; but once he left, became {\i tahor}, and then re-entered \u8212- now violating an established {\i nezirut of taharah} \u8212- he is obligated in a {\i korban tumah}. The {\i chiddush} is that we consider his {\i nezirut} to have genuinely begun.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: simply walking out of a cemetery does not make one {\i tahor} \u8212- one is still {\i tamei}. So how can leaving the cemetery trigger the beginning of a new {\i nezirut}? Shmuel explains: {\i kegon sheyatza vehiza beshlishi uvishvii vetaval} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} does not mean he simply stepped out. It means he left, underwent the full purification process \u8212- the sprinklings on days three and seven, and immersion in a {\i mikvah} \u8212- and thereby became {\i tahor}. Only then, when he is genuinely {\i tahor}, does his {\i nezirut} of {\i taharah} begin. If he subsequently re-enters the cemetery, he has violated that {\i nezirut} and is obligated in a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Even Re-entering Counts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then sharpens the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation. One might have thought: surely leaving and becoming {\i tahor} is enough to begin the {\i nezirut} \u8212- why does the {\i mishnah} need to specify that re-entering counts? The answer is that the {\i chiddush} lies in the following scenario: what if he became {\i tahor} and re-entered on the very same day he completed his purification? He was {\i tamei} right up until that point; there is only the briefest sliver of {\i taharah} separating his old {\i tumah} from his new violation. One might argue that this thin interval, with no true establishment as a {\i nazir tahor}, cannot count as the start of a genuine {\i nezirut} \u8212- that the entire period should be seen as one continuous state of {\i tumah}. The {\i mishnah} teaches otherwise: {\i afilu nichnas oleh lo min haminyan} \u8212- even if he re-entered on that very same day he became {\i tahor}, we treat his {\i nezirut} as having genuinely begun. He has therefore violated it and is obligated in a {\i korban}. This is the genuine {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Rav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana and Rav Assi said to Rav: {\i mai taama lo mefarshet lan kulhu hanei milei} \u8212- why did you not explain all of this to us? Shmuel provided a careful reading of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- clarifying that {\i yatza} means he left and became {\i tahor}, and that {\i venichnas} teaches a {\i chiddush} even about re-entry on the very same day. These students had apparently been present in Rav\u8217's {\i yeshiva} without receiving this explanation. Rav replied: {\i amina dela tzerichitu} \u8212- I assumed you did not need it, that you understood it yourselves. Why did you not simply ask?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Eliezer Days Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Same Day\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer now introduces a further qualification: {\i lo bo bayom} \u8212- not on the same day. If a {\i nazir} became {\i tamei} on the same day he became {\i tahor}, he does not bring a {\i korban} and is not considered to have fully violated his {\i nezirut} in the sense that triggers the {\i korban} obligation. For that, he must have been established as a {\i tahor nazir} for at least a second day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earlier Days Fall\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source is the verse: {\i vehayamim harishonim yipulu, ki tamei nizro} \u8212- the earlier days shall fall away, because his {\i nezirut} was made {\i tamei}. The word {\i yamim} \u8212- days, in the plural \u8212- indicates that there must be multiple days that are falling away. A single day is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla qualifies Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position: {\i lo amar Rabbi Eliezer ela betamei shenitnazar} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's requirement of multiple days applies only to the case of one who began his {\i nezirut} in a state of {\i tumah}, i.e., the {\i nazir} who accepted {\i nezirut} in a cemetery. But if one began as a {\i nazir tahor} and subsequently became {\i tamei}, {\i afilu yom echad soter} \u8212- even one day is sufficient, and the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's rule applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava explains why Rabbi Eliezer limits his ruling to the cemetery case. The verse says {\i ki tamei nizro} \u8212- and Rava reads this phrase not as \u8220"because he made his {\i nezirut} {\i tamei}\u8221" (i.e., violated it by becoming {\i tamei}) but rather as {\i mishum debetumah nazar} \u8212- because his {\i nezirut} itself began in {\i tumah}. The verse connects the falling away of days ({\i yamim harishonim yipolu}) specifically to the scenario where the {\i nezirut} was established in {\i tumah} from the outset. In that case, Rabbi Eliezer requires a more thorough establishment as a {\i nazir tahor} \u8212- two days \u8212- before the violation triggers a {\i korban} obligation. The logic is comprehensible: a {\i nazir} who began in {\i tumah} is still in some sense in the shadow of that original {\i tumah}; he needs to be more solidly grounded as a {\i tahor nazir} before a new violation becomes fully operative. A {\i nazir} who began {\i betaharah}, by contrast, is clearly violated from the first day, and even one day triggers the obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye: 100-Day Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Day One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges this reading with a {\i braisa}: {\i hareini nazir meah yom} \u8212- one declares a hundred-day {\i nezirut} \u8212- {\i venitma betechilat meah} \u8212- and becomes {\i tamei} at the beginning of that hundred, on day one. The {\i braisa} asks: might this be {\i soter}, destroying the days and requiring him to bring a {\i korban}? The answer: {\i talmud lomar, vehayamim harishonim yipolu} \u8212- the verse requires that there be multiple earlier days. {\i Ad sheyihyu lo yamim rishonim} \u8212- until there are actually earlier days, plural, to fall away. This person has only day one, so there are no {\i yamim rishonim} \u8212- no multiple days \u8212- and therefore no {\i korban} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} directly challenges the attempt to limit Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position to the cemetery case. Here the person began his {\i nezirut betaharah} \u8212- it says he declared a hundred-day {\i nezirut} and became {\i tamei} at the start \u8212- and yet Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's requirement of multiple days still applies. This is a {\i tiyuvta}, a refutation: Rabbi Eliezer holds his position even for a standard {\i nazir tahor} who becomes {\i tamei} on day one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Last Day\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} continues: what if he became {\i tamei besof meah}, on the last day? Might one think he would have to restart the entire hundred days? The verse says {\i vehayamim harishonim yipolu} \u8212- the earlier days fall away. The implication of {\i rishonim} \u8212- \u8220"earlier\u8221" \u8212- is that there are also {\i acharonim}, later days, days yet to come. If there are earlier days, there must also be later ones. On the very last day, there are no later days remaining \u8212- {\i ein lo acharonim} \u8212- and therefore this case is not one where all the days fall away. He brings a {\i korban} but does not restart the full hundred; he need only add thirty more days, in keeping with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's general position about becoming {\i tamei} on the final day of a {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Day 99\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} then addresses day 99 \u8212- {\i yom meah chasar achat}. Here there are both {\i yamim rishonim} \u8212- the first 98 days \u8212- and {\i acharonim} \u8212- day 100 still remains. Accordingly, this case falls squarely within the verse\u8217's description: {\i vehayamim harishonim yipolu}, and he does bring a {\i korban} and the earlier days are lost. This is why the {\i braisa} chose a hundred-day {\i nezirut} as its example: the hundred-day structure makes visible the distinctions between day one (no {\i rishonim}), the last day (no {\i acharonim}), and the middle days (both present), in a way that a standard thirty-day {\i nezirut} would not, since becoming {\i tamei} on day thirty of a standard {\i nezirut} already triggers a thirty-day restart and thus produces no practical difference from any other middle day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether {\i yamim} requires two full days, such that becoming {\i tamei} on day 99 should be borderline. {\i Tosafot} raises this question, but the {\i braisa} explicitly says {\i meah chasar achat} \u8212- the remainder of day 99 plus the entirety of day 100 together constitute sufficient {\i yamim}. This connects to a further question the {\i Gemara} will address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i soter} language \u8212- and the word {\i yipolu} \u8212- are worth noting here. The metaphor embedded in {\i yipolu} (they fall away) and {\i soter} (destroying) evokes the image of building a wall: each day is like a brick, and for the structure to fall it must have achieved a certain height. This is not merely poetic; it frames the legal question of when accumulated {\i nezirut} days are vulnerable to being swept away.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yipolu & Yamim Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full vs Partial Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa asks Abaye: when Rabbi Eliezer requires {\i yamim}, does he mean two full days? Specifically, must the {\i nazir} have completed all of day one and entered day two before a violation on that second day triggers the {\i korban} obligation? Or does even a partial day one followed by a partial day two \u8212- where he became {\i tamei} partway through the second day \u8212- satisfy the requirement? Abaye did not have an answer. They went and asked Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Terms Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava answers: {\i yipolu ketiv} \u8212- the verse writes {\i yipolu}. The word {\i yipolu} somehow signals that the falling away itself is relatively minor \u8212- an action of falling, not a deliberate established loss \u8212- and therefore partial days suffice. Each of the two terms in the verse \u8212- {\i yamim} and {\i yipolu} \u8212- contributes something irreducible, and both are necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Only Yamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the verse had written only {\i yamim} without {\i yipolu}, one would have thought: {\i ad denafki tarein umatchil tlata} \u8212- you would need two complete days to have passed before becoming {\i tamei} on the beginning of the third day. The word {\i yamim} alone implies full days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Only Yipolu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if the verse had written only {\i yipolu} without {\i yamim}, one might have concluded: {\i afilu chad} \u8212- even one day (or part of a day) would suffice. The plural implication would be lost, and any period of {\i taharah} might trigger the obligation upon violation. Therefore both are needed: {\i yamim} establishes that there must be at least two periods (even if partial), and {\i yipolu} establishes that those periods need not be full days. All of this operates within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework requiring multiple days before a {\i korban} obligation is triggered.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Outside EY - Beit S/H\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aliyah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces a new case: {\i mi shenazar nezirot harbe} \u8212- one who took upon himself a large {\i nezirut}, meaning a single extended {\i nezirut} rather than multiple separate ones. He completed his {\i nezirut} outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael} and then came to {\i Eretz Yisrael} in order to bring his {\i korbanot} and conclude the process. What must he now do?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai: 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai rules: {\i nazir shloshim yom} \u8212- he must be a {\i nazir} for another thirty days. Arriving in {\i Eretz Yisrael} with a completed {\i nezirut} in hand, he is still required to do thirty additional days. This is hardly an incentive for {\i aliyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel: Start Over\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel goes further: {\i nazir batchila} \u8212- he must start all over again, as if his {\i nezirut} never happened. Whatever years he invested in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} count for nothing; he must begin from the beginning. Beit Hillel is not always the more lenient opinion, and this is a striking example.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumat Outside EY\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying rationale connects directly to the preceding discussion of a {\i nazir} in a cemetery. There is a concept of {\i tumat eretz ha\u8217'amim} \u8212- the {\i tumah} of the lands of non-Jewish peoples. A {\i nazir} who spent years in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is, in a sense, analogous to a {\i nazir} who spent years in a {\i beit hakevarot}. The juxtaposition in the {\i Gemara} seems deliberate: the {\i tumah} of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is at least partly rooted in the practice of non-Jews burying their dead in all manner of places, not necessarily in formal cemeteries, leaving the ground of the {\i diaspora} in a state of pervasive {\i tumah}. This {\i tumah} is rabbinic \u8212- {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- which is why the Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel dispute operates on a {\i m\u8217'drabbanan} level. The person who completed his {\i nezirut} outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael} has fulfilled his {\i d\u8217'oraita} obligations; it is only the rabbinically imposed treatment of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} as {\i tamei} territory that requires him to do something upon arrival in {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tumah} of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is not a simple technical {\i tumah}. It is a mixture of actual concern about corpse {\i tumah} from bones near the surface and an attitudinal dimension \u8212- a rabbinic expression of the sanctity of {\i Eretz Yisrael} relative to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. This is why it is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. For Beit Hillel, only when one arrives in {\i Eretz Yisrael} can one truly say: {\i now} you are beginning your genuine {\i nezirut shel taharah}. The entire {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} period, whatever its length, is treated as if it were conducted in a state of {\i tumah}, and must be redone entirely. For Beit Shammai, thirty days is a symbolic acknowledgment of the distinction, without requiring a full restart.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask about the reverse case: does a {\i nazir} who begins in {\i Eretz Yisrael} and then leaves for {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} and returns face a similar obligation? The {\i Gemara} on this {\i mishnah} does not address that question directly. It also bears noting that a {\i nazir} who observes his {\i nezirut} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} has not sinned {\i d\u8217'oraita} in doing so \u8212- he simply cannot conclude the process until he arrives in {\i Eretz Yisrael} where the {\i korbanot} can be brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir = Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important parallel emerges here: the {\i nazir} and the {\i kohen} share several defining characteristics \u8212- both are forbidden wine when in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, both are forbidden to become {\i tamei laMeit}, and both are not supposed to leave {\i Eretz Yisrael} for {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. The {\i nazir} is, in a sense, someone striving for the {\i kedushah} of a {\i kohen} even outside the {\i Beit HaMikdash} context. Both prohibitions against leaving {\i Eretz Yisrael} operate on a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} level \u8212- violating them does not invalidate one\u8217's status {\i d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- but the parallel is recognized and legally significant.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Queen Helni Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Helni Background\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} brings a famous narrative: {\i maaseh be\u8217'Helni HaMalka} \u8212- the story of Queen Helni. Queen Helni was the ruler of Adiabene, a tributary kingdom in what is today northwestern Iraq, within the orbit of the Parthian Empire. She converted to Judaism along with her family, and her descendants became notable figures in the Jewish world. Her son Munbaz is mentioned in the sources. She was a woman of considerable wealth, and her family\u8217's contributions to the Jewish community in Jerusalem are documented.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven-Year Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When her son went off to war, Queen Helni took a vow: {\i im yavo beni min hamilchamah beshalom, harei nezirah sheva shanim} \u8212- if my son returns safely from war, I will be a {\i nezirah} for seven years. This is a striking example of the phenomenon of vowing in exchange for divine protection \u8212- bargaining with God \u8212- a theme that reaches back to {\i Yaakov Avinu}: {\i im Elokim imadi, ushmarani baderech hazos, kol asher titein li}. The {\i mishnah} makes this motivation explicit in a way few other texts do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aliyah After 7 Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Her son returned safely. She fulfilled her vow and was a {\i nezirah} for seven years. At the end of those seven years \u8212- {\i uvesof sheva shanim} \u8212- she made {\i aliyah} to {\i Eretz Yisrael}, presumably in order to bring her {\i korbanot} and conclude the {\i nezirut} formally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Another 7 Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel ruled: {\i shetihi nezirah od sheva shanim acheros} \u8212- she must be a {\i nezirah} for another seven years. The entire seven years in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} counted for nothing under Beit Hillel\u8217's ruling. She had to start from the beginning. One might observe wryly that this is not the most encouraging {\i kiruv} strategy, but the {\i halacha} is as it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Becomes Tamei Day 14\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She observed the second seven years. Then \u8212- {\i uvesof sheva shanim nitmeis} \u8212- at the end of that second seven years, she became {\i tamei}. She therefore had to do another seven years \u8212- bringing her total to 21 years as a {\i nezirah}: the first seven in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} (retroactively not counted), the second seven required by Beit Hillel upon arriving in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and the third seven required because of {\i tumah} at the end of the second. This is {\i 21} years of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda: 14 Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda offers an alternative account: {\i lo haysah nezirah ela arba esreh shanah} \u8212- she was a {\i nezirah} for only 14 years, not 21. This raises two interpretive possibilities. Either Rabbi Yehuda agrees with Beit Hillel that she had to redo the {\i nezirut} upon {\i aliyah}, but holds that she did not become {\i tamei} at the end of the second seven years \u8212- and the total is therefore 14. Or Rabbi Yehuda disagrees with Beit Hillel\u8217's ruling entirely, holding that the {\i aliyah} did not require a full restart (perhaps he follows Beit Shammai with thirty additional days, or some other position), and the 14 years represent the original seven plus seven more for having become {\i tamei} at some point \u8212- with no additional seven required for {\i aliyah} itself. The {\i Gemara} will explore both possibilities in the continuation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 20\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chutz Laretz Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Long Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} at the bottom of 19b presents the case of someone who accepted an extended {\i nezirut} and subsequently arrived in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The juxtaposition is deliberate and significant: the preceding {\i Mishnah} dealt with someone who accepted {\i nezirut} in a {\i beit hakevarot}, who then begins a {\i nezirut de-tahara} upon leaving the cemetery. Here, by contrast, the {\i tuma} of {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} is rabbinic in character, and no one seriously argues that it {\i soter} \u8212- interrupts \u8212- the {\i nezirut} outright. Nevertheless, there is a symbolic dimension: one arrives in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, the place where the {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut} must ultimately be brought, emerging from what had been deemed an {\i eretz tmei\u8217'ah}. The question is how to respond halachically to that transition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai holds that the {\i nazir} must observe an additional symbolic thirty days of {\i nezirut} upon entering {\i Eretz Yisrael}. Beit Hillel goes further: it is as though the {\i nazir} is transitioning from {\i tuma} to {\i tahara}, and must now observe the full original duration of the {\i nezirut} anew \u8212- not bringing a {\i korban} at the midpoint, since the first {\i nezirut} has not yet concluded, but extending the period so that only at the end of the combined duration does one bring the {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gussha vs Avira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens by asking what underlies this dispute. {\i Lema b\u8217'ha kamipalgi} \u8212- what is the substantive disagreement that produces these two different results?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proposes that Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel differ on the precise nature of the rabbinic decree rendering {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz tamei}. Beit Shammai holds {\i mishum gusha gazru aleha} \u8212- the decree was directed at the {\i gusha}, the soil or dirt of {\i eretz ammim}. The concern is the ground itself, which may contain buried bodies. Beit Hillel holds {\i mishum avira gazru aleha} \u8212- the decree extended even to the very air of {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical distinction: if someone enters {\i eretz ammim} suspended above the ground \u8212- carried in a box, for instance, without touching the soil \u8212- then according to Beit Shammai, who treats only the {\i gusha} as {\i tamei}, that person remains {\i tahor}. According to Beit Hillel, even the {\i avira} of {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} conveys {\i tuma}, and such a person would be {\i tamei} regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask how this differs from ordinary {\i tumat ohel}. Two answers emerge. First, non-Jews may not generate {\i tumat ohel} even if they are buried there, in which case the concern would only arise through direct contact with the ground. Second, even if {\i tumat ohel} were a factor, it could theoretically be avoided by certain configurations \u8212- a person suspended in a box would, under normal {\i tumat ohel} logic, be protected from the spread of {\i tuma} rising through an enclosure \u8212- whereas the {\i avira} decree of Beit Hillel admits of no such escape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does this map onto the {\i machlokes} about the duration of the additional {\i nezirut}? The most natural reading is one of proportionality: Beit Shammai, who limits the decree to the {\i gusha} and thus treats the {\i tuma} of {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} with relative restraint, requires only a symbolic thirty days in response. Beit Hillel, who extends the decree even to the air and thus treats the {\i tuma} with greater weight, requires the {\i nazir} to redo the entire period, since they afford no credit for {\i nezirut} observed in so heavily {\i tamei} an environment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Girsa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, however, is dissatisfied with this reading. Tosafot presses: even if Beit Shammai limits the decree to the {\i gusha}, the {\i tuma} is still treated as a form of {\i tumat meit} \u8212- rabbinic, yes, but real. In the case of a {\i beit hakevarot}, the {\i nazir} must redo everything upon leaving, even though {\i tumat ohel} there also does not apply in the identical manner to the air. So why should Beit Shammai\u8217's position of only thirty days follow from treating the {\i gusha} as {\i tamei}? The analogy to a cemetery, where even a {\i tuma} of relatively defined scope requires full restart, seems to tell against Beit Shammai\u8217's leniency. This is what Tosafot phrases as {\i d\u8217'mai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Beit Shammai} \u8212- the logic of Beit Shammai remains unexplained under the standard {\i girsa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot therefore proposes a reversed {\i girsa}: Beit Shammai is the one who holds {\i mishum avira gazru} \u8212- that even the air of {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} is {\i tamei} \u8212- and Beit Hillel holds {\i mishum gusha gazru} \u8212- only the ground. This reversal is, admittedly, counterintuitive, since one generally expects Beit Shammai to be the stricter school. But Tosafot\u8217's logic is as follows: if Beit Shammai extends the decree so far as to declare even the {\i avira tamei}, it becomes obvious to all observers that this is a purely rabbinic construct \u8212- no {\i tuma} from the Torah is contracted merely by breathing the air of a place. Precisely because everyone recognizes the decree as rabbinic and artificial in character, Beit Shammai requires only thirty symbolic days, so that the rabbinic {\i tuma} of {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} not be confused with a genuine biblical {\i tumat meit}. Beit Hillel, by contrast, limits the decree to the {\i gusha}, which mimics the more familiar pattern of {\i tumat kever} \u8212- actual ground containing bodies. Because the decree resembles biblical {\i tuma} in its parameters, Beit Hillel requires the {\i nazir} to treat it with full seriousness and redo the entire period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So Tosafot presents two coherent but opposing frameworks. In the standard {\i girsa}: the more one extends the reach of the decree (Beit Hillel, {\i avira}), the more one demands of the {\i nazir} when leaving {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz}, since the weight assigned to that {\i tuma} is greater. In Tosafot\u8217's reversed {\i girsa}: the more one extends the reach of the decree (Beit Shammai, {\i avira}), the more clearly everyone understands it as a rabbinic fiction, and therefore a lighter response suffices. It is worth noting that Tosafot elsewhere criticizes those who emend a {\i girsa} on the basis of {\i svara} alone \u8212- and yet Tosafot here does precisely that, which is not without irony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then steps back from the {\i gusha/avira} framework entirely and suggests an alternative: {\i lo, d\u8217'chulei alma mishum gusha gazru} \u8212- in fact, everyone would agree that the original decree was directed at the {\i gusha} and that {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} functions as a standard rabbinic {\i tuma} analogous to {\i tumat meit}. The dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel is not about the nature of the decree but about what response to demand of a {\i nazir} who observed {\i nezirut} in {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz}. Beit Shammai holds {\i b\u8217'stam nezirut kansinan} \u8212- we impose a single standard {\i nezirut} as a fine, a symbolic penalty. Beit Hillel holds {\i k\u8217'techilat nezirut kansinan} \u8212- we treat it as though the {\i nezirut} never began and impose the full original duration again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This second explanation is, as the lecturer notes, largely descriptive rather than explanatory. It tells us what each school requires without illuminating the underlying reasoning. But it at least establishes that the {\i machlokes} is not fundamentally about the nature of {\i tumas Chutz La\u8217'aretz} but rather about the appropriate rabbinic response to one who has observed {\i nezirut} in that condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Queen Helena\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To probe the practical application of the dispute, the {\i Gemara} introduces the story of Queen Helena ({\i Helni HaMalka}). She had been a {\i nezira} for seven years while in {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz}, then came to {\i Eretz Yisrael}. Beit Hillel instructed her to observe an additional seven years. Before that second period concluded, she became {\i tamei}, which required yet another seven-year cycle, yielding twenty-one years in total. Rabbi Yehuda, however, says she was a {\i nezira} for only fourteen years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: how do we get to fourteen? There are two possibilities. Either Rabbi Yehuda holds like Beit Shammai \u8212- that upon entering {\i Eretz Yisrael} one adds only thirty days rather than seven years \u8212- and then she became {\i tamei} before the end, requiring another seven years, producing seven plus thirty days plus seven, which does not yield a clean fourteen. Or Rabbi Yehuda holds like Beit Hillel \u8212- seven plus seven \u8212- but disputes the claim that she became {\i tamei} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} demonstrates that the first option is untenable. If Rabbi Yehuda held like Beit Shammai and she did become {\i tamei}, he would have said fourteen years and thirty days, not simply fourteen years. The round number of fourteen points to a reading where the thirty days play no role \u8212- which means he holds like Beit Hillel but disagrees that she became {\i tamei} during the second period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} adds the view of Rabbi Yehuda in the name of Rabbi Eliezer, who derives from the verse {\i zot torat hanazir} that if a {\i nazir} becomes {\i tamei} on the very day the {\i nezirut} was to be completed, the full law of {\i nezirut} restarts. This principle, applied to the final-day scenario, would yield yet another inconsistency with the twenty-one year count, reinforcing that Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position denies that she became {\i tamei} during the second period at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Pairs Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conflicting Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i Mishnah} introduces a case in which two sets of witnesses offer conflicting testimony about the duration of a man\u8217's {\i nezirut}. One pair testifies that he vowed to be a {\i nazir} for two years; the other pair testifies that he vowed for five years. The question is what the {\i beit din} does with this contradictory testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the dispute itself, a preliminary observation is worth making. The introduction of witnesses into {\i nezirut} is striking. Until this point, the entire tractate has been occupied with the {\i nazir}\u8217's personal relationship to his vow \u8212- the precise words he used, his inner intent, the possibility of seeking {\i hatarah} from a {\i chakham}. Now the {\i Gemara} establishes that there is an external juridical dimension: {\i beit din} has a stake in determining whether someone is a {\i nazir}, and witnesses can impose that status even against a person\u8217's own denial, at least with respect to consequences like {\i malkot} for violating the prohibitions of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a genuine question about the relationship between {\i nezirut} and {\i nedarim} in the domain of communal enforcement. One might argue that since {\i nezirut} involves ongoing and publicly observable prohibitions whose violation carries lashes, it falls more squarely within {\i beit din}\u8217's purview than an ordinary {\i neder}, even though {\i neder} violations can in principle also generate lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overlap Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai holds {\i nechleka ha\u8217'edut} \u8212- the testimony is divided and contradictory, and therefore neither pair has established anything. The result is that no {\i nezirut} is binding at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel holds {\i yesh bichlal chamesh shtayim} \u8212- within the claim of five is the claim of two. The pair testifying to five years is not contradicting the pair testifying to two years; they are saying something larger that subsumes what the other pair says. Therefore, on the point of two years, both sets of witnesses are in agreement. The {\i nazir} must observe two periods of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the question of why the subject does not simply admit the vow by his silence when the first pair testifies, given the principle of {\i shtika kehoda\u8217'ah} \u8212- silence as admission. Tosafot resolves this with two possible scenarios: either the subject actively denies everything, or both pairs came before the {\i beit din} simultaneously, in which case the internal contradiction between them means the subject has no reason to respond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a question about intention: since so much of the law of {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut} concerns the alignment of {\i piv velibo} \u8212- one\u8217's words and one\u8217's heart \u8212- one might wonder whether a {\i beit din} may compel {\i nezirut} based on testimony if the subject claims he did not mean the words. The {\i Gemara} does not address this directly, but the implicit logic of the {\i Mishnah} seems to be that once witnesses have testified to the words and there is no overriding context that would redefine them, the external court proceeds on the basis of the statement as made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's framing of the dispute \u8212- as between two competing pairs of witnesses \u8212- is not universally accepted. A {\i baraita} in the name of Rabbi Yishmael ben Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka states that Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel did not actually disagree in the case of two separate pairs of witnesses. In that case, everyone would agree with the principle of {\i yesh bichlal chamesh shtayim} \u8212- the overlap is clear enough, and the {\i nazir} must observe two periods. The genuine dispute, Rabbi Yishmael argues, concerned a single pair of witnesses whose members contradict each other internally \u8212- one witness says two years and the other witness says five years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In that scenario, Beit Shammai says {\i nechleka ha\u8217'edut} \u8212- the testimony is divided, and a pair whose members cannot agree on what they witnessed cannot stand as valid {\i edut}. Beit Hillel says even here {\i yesh bichlal chamesh shtayim} applies: the witness who says five implicitly includes the claim of two, so the two witnesses share common ground on the two-year period and combine to form valid testimony for that duration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reframing is illuminating. In the case of two separate pairs, the intuitive force of the overlap argument is relatively strong: it is hard to say that a pair testifying to five years {\i contradicts} a pair testifying to two, since five is simply a larger claim. The disagreement is about whether two pairs saying different things can generate a combined testimony \u8212- a higher standard, but not an inherently unreasonable one. In the case of a single internally divided pair, however, the question is sharper: to what extent can two witnesses who disagree with each other be said to be testifying to the same thing and thus join to create unified {\i edut}? That is where the real debate lies, and Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's {\i baraita} locates the dispute there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Discrete Units\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav introduces an important qualification: {\i hakol modim b\u8217'moneh} \u8212- everyone agrees in the case where the witnesses are counting discrete units. If the first pair testified that the subject said \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} one time and two times,\u8221" and the second pair testified that he said \u8220"three times, four times, and five times,\u8221" there is no genuine contradiction between them, and everyone would agree that the testimonies combine. The distinction turns on how the {\i nezirut} is described. When the subject is reported to have declared a single block \u8212- \u8220"two years\u8221" or \u8220"five years\u8221" \u8212- the two testimonies describe conceptually different units, and the question of overlap is genuinely contested. When the subject is reported to have declared individual discrete units one by one, the testimonies address individual instances and can be seen to agree on the specific units each covers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explicates the case of {\i moneh} as follows: the first pair said he declared one time and two times; the second pair said he declared three times, four times, and five times. Alternatively, one could construct the case so that the overlap is even more literal \u8212- the first pair reports one and two, the second reports one through five \u8212- but either way, when the claims are atomized into individual units, the logic of combination is considerably more intuitive than when they are expressed as aggregates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's exchange between Rav Chama and Rav Chisda clarifies the precise scope of the {\i moneh} principle. If one witness says five and explicitly not two \u8212- that is, he positively excludes the lower number \u8212- then the testimonies actively contradict each other and cannot combine. The {\i moneh} principle applies only where the witnesses enumerate units sequentially and no such exclusion is implied.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Va\u8217'ani Declarations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cascading Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next chapter opens with a {\i Mishnah} presenting the case of cascading {\i nezirut} declarations. One person says {\i hareini nazir} \u8212- I am a {\i nazir} \u8212- and his companion hears and says {\i va\u8217'ani} \u8212- and I as well. A third person hears the second and says {\i va\u8217'ani}. The {\i Mishnah} rules that {\i kulam nezirim} \u8212- all of them are {\i nezirim} \u8212- because each piggybacks on the one before.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then addresses what happens when the chain is disrupted. If the first person obtains a {\i heter} \u8212- retroactive annulment \u8212- they are all released, {\i hutru kulam}. The logic is that each subsequent person\u8217's {\i va\u8217'ani} is not merely an independent declaration of {\i nezirut} made in a context created by the first. Rather, it functions as {\i hatfasa} \u8212- a transfer of status. The later declarant is saying, in effect: whatever status applies to you applies to me. This is structurally analogous to {\i ze k\u8217'ze} in the laws of {\i nedarim}, where the {\i kedusha} of one object is transferred to another. If the first person\u8217's {\i nezirut} never truly took effect \u8212- retroactively voided by the {\i heter} \u8212- there was no status to transfer, and the subsequent declarations are vacuous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if the last person in the chain obtains a {\i heter}, only he is released. The first person\u8217's status is independent and self-standing; the last person\u8217's annulment has no upstream effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related {\i Mishnah} addresses the husband-wife scenario. A husband declares {\i hareini nazir}, and his wife, upon hearing, says {\i va\u8217'ani}. In this case, the husband may annul his wife\u8217's {\i nezirut} \u8212- which is his prerogative as a husband \u8212- and his own {\i nezirut} remains standing. Her {\i nezirut} derived from his and is therefore subject to his {\i hafera}; his does not depend on hers and is unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now consider the inverse: the wife declares {\i hareini nezira}, and the husband, upon hearing, says {\i va\u8217'ani}. The {\i Mishnah} rules {\i eino yachol lehafer} \u8212- he cannot annul it. There are two ways to understand this ruling. One reading is that annulling his wife\u8217's {\i nezirut} would, as a practical matter, undo his own, since his {\i va\u8217'ani} piggybacked on her status. Since {\i lo yachel devaro} \u8212- a person cannot annul his own vow \u8212- this indirect route of annulment is also closed. A second reading, which the {\i Gemara} favors, is that the moment the husband said {\i va\u8217'ani} in response to his wife\u8217's declaration, he performed an act of {\i kiyum} \u8212- ratification. Having ratified the wife\u8217's {\i nezirut}, he can no longer perform {\i hafera} upon it, since {\i kiyum} and {\i hafera} are mutually exclusive. On this reading, the reason he cannot annul is not that it would affect him, but that he has already forfeited the right to annul through the {\i kiyum} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} continues with further variations. If a husband says {\i hareini nazir v\u8217'at} \u8212- I am a {\i nazir}, and how about you \u8212- and the wife responds {\i amen}, he may annul her vow while his own remains standing, since her {\i nezirut} derived from his. If the wife says {\i hareini nezira v\u8217'ata} \u8212- I am a {\i nezira} and you as well \u8212- and he responds {\i amen}, he again cannot annul, for the same reason as above: his {\i amen} constitutes {\i kiyum} of her vow. Whether the mechanism is {\i lo yachel devaro} or {\i kiyum}, the practical result is the same \u8212- {\i hafera} is unavailable to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is raised whether, in the case where the husband performed {\i kiyum} through his {\i va\u8217'ani} or {\i amen}, he could subsequently seek {\i hatara} from a {\i chakham} for his own {\i va\u8217'ani}, thereby undoing his own {\i kiyum} and then annulling his wife\u8217's {\i nezirut} from beneath. The {\i Gemara} leaves this as a live question. It is worth noting that the earlier discussion in the tractate of whether a {\i chakham} can be {\i matir} a {\i kiyum} is directly relevant here, though no final resolution is offered at this stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish, sitting before Rabbi Yehuda Nesia \u8212- the grandson of Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi \u8212- articulates a crucial limiting principle: {\i vehu she-hitpisu kulam betoch kedei dibbur} \u8212- the entire sequence of {\i va\u8217'ani} declarations is valid only if all of them occurred within {\i tokh kedei dibbur} of the first person\u8217's statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The assumption underlying Reish Lakish\u8217's formulation at this stage is that all subsequent declarants are piggybacking directly on person number one \u8212- not on the person immediately before them, but on the original {\i nazir}. This assumption will be revisited, but it drives the {\i tokh kedei dibbur} requirement: since each later declarant is extending and participating in the first person\u8217's speech act, the temporal connection must be to that original act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant point. In the classic case of {\i yad} \u8212- where a {\i nazir} passes before someone who then says {\i va\u8217'ani} \u8212- the {\i va\u8217'ani} works because the passing {\i nazir} creates a context that gives meaning to the word. That is a different mechanism: the context frames the later utterance. Here, however, what is happening is not merely context-creation but actual {\i hatfasa} \u8212- the later declarant is attaching to and extending the earlier one\u8217's speech act itself. That is why {\i tokh kedei dibbur} is required: one can only be said to participate in another\u8217's speech act if one does so within the window during which that speech act is still, legally speaking, live.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tokh Kedei Dibbur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: how long is {\i tokh kedei dibbur}? The answer given is {\i kedei she\u8217'eilat shalom} \u8212- the time it takes to extend a greeting, as in {\i shalom alecha}. But then a further question arises: is it the greeting from a student to a teacher \u8212- {\i shalom alecha rebbi} \u8212- or from a teacher to a student? The difference is not merely ceremonial. A student greeting a teacher would say something like {\i shalom alecha rebbi u\u8217'mori}, which is a somewhat longer phrase than a teacher\u8217's brief {\i shalom alecha}. Whether {\i tokh kedei dibbur} spans two words or three words has practical significance for how many people can realistically fit their {\i va\u8217'ani} declarations within that window.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A response is offered to Reish Lakish: {\i lo shavkat ravcha le-talmidah} \u8212- you have not left room for the student. The implication, as Tosafot understands it, is that Reish Lakish\u8217's strict application of {\i tokh kedei dibbur} \u8212- measured from the original speaker\u8217's statement \u8212- creates problems in cases where a natural interruption intervenes, such as a student pausing to greet his arriving {\i rebbi} before saying {\i va\u8217'ani}. The push-back suggests that the window may be slightly more generous than Reish Lakish implies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is in fact a conceptual distinction worth drawing here. The rule of {\i tokh kedei dibbur} is classically invoked when a {\i speaker} wishes to retract or amend something they themselves said. In that context, the window is measured from the end of one\u8217's own speech. Here, however, a different party is joining in. One might therefore argue that the window for the subsequent declarant need not be identical to the classical {\i tokh kedei dibbur} of self-correction. Perhaps the second person\u8217's {\i va\u8217'ani} merely needs to occur in close proximity to the first person\u8217's statement \u8212- not necessarily within the exact same three-word window, but immediately in its wake. This would mean that even after the first person\u8217's {\i tokh kedei dibbur} has closed \u8212- after their own window for self-correction has elapsed \u8212- a second party could still validly say {\i va\u8217'ani} in the brief moment immediately following. The analogy to other cases of context-driven legal acts supports this reading: in {\i kiddushin}, for instance, if a couple has been discussing marriage at length, the handing of a ring without explicit words can suffice because {\i asukin b\u8217'oto inyan} \u8212- they are still engaged in that matter \u8212- and this extends well beyond any {\i tokh kedei dibbur} of the last spoken word. Here, by contrast, we are not merely saying the context gives meaning to the second person\u8217's words; we are saying the second person is actively participating in the first person\u8217's speech act. That participation must therefore be temporally proximate to the speech act itself, even if the precise measure of that proximity may differ from the self-correction model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i mi she\u8217'amar hareini nazir v\u8217'shama chaveiro v\u8217'shaha kedei dibbur v\u8217'amar va\u8217'ani, hu assur v\u8217'chaveiro mutar} \u8212- if the companion waited a {\i kedei dibbur} before saying {\i va\u8217'ani}, only the first person is a {\i nazir}, not the second. This supports Reish Lakish\u8217's position that {\i tokh kedei dibbur} is the operative standard, not something more generous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Piggyback Chain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i tokh kedei dibbur} framework established, the {\i Gemara} turns to the question of whose statement each subsequent declarant is piggybacking on. Reish Lakish assumed they all connect back to person number one. But the {\i Mishnah} describes a scenario with persons two and three saying {\i va\u8217'ani} sequentially, and the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i chad batar chad matpis} \u8212- does each person grab onto the one immediately before him \u8212- {\i o dilma bekamma matpis} \u8212- or do they all grab onto person number one?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} is considerable. If each person piggybacks on the one immediately before, the chain can theoretically extend indefinitely: person two connects to person one, person three connects to person two, person four connects to person three, and so on, each within {\i tokh kedei dibbur} of the prior speaker. If they all connect to person number one, the chain is limited to however many people can fit their {\i va\u8217'ani} declarations within {\i tokh kedei dibbur} of that first statement \u8212- only a handful at most.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i nafka mina} follows from this regarding the effect of a middle person\u8217's {\i heter}. If each person connects to the one before, then annulling person two releases persons three and four and everyone below, while leaving person one unaffected. If everyone connects to person one, then annulling anyone other than person one affects no one else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to resolve the question from the {\i Mishnah} itself. The {\i Mishnah} says: {\i hutar ha-rishon hutru kullan} \u8212- if the first is released, all are released. The inference ({\i ha emtzai lo}) would be that only the first person\u8217's annulment has this cascading effect; a middle person\u8217's annulment would affect no one. This would support the view that everyone connects to person one. But the {\i Gemara} deflects: perhaps the {\i Mishnah} focused on the first person not because he is the only one whose annulment matters, but because using the first person allows one to say {\i hutru kullan} \u8212- all are released \u8212- which is the most dramatic formulation. A middle person\u8217's annulment would release only those below, making it a less useful teaching vehicle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the {\i Mishnah} says: {\i hutar ha-achron, ha-achron mutar ve-kullan asurin} \u8212- if the last person is released, he is permitted and all before him remain forbidden. The inference would be that an intermediate person would affect only those who follow. But the {\i Gemara} resists: the {\i Mishnah} may be using {\i achron} loosely to mean anyone other than the first person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Sequential\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution comes from an explicit {\i baraita}: if the first person is released, all are released; if the last is released, only he is released; if a middle person is released, {\i heimenu u-lemata mutar, heimenu u-lemala assur} \u8212- from him downward is permissible, from him upward is forbidden. This explicitly distinguishes the middle person\u8217's annulment from the last person\u8217's and from the first person\u8217's. {\i Shema mina chad batar chad matpis} \u8212- this demonstrates conclusively that each person piggybacks on the one immediately before him, not all on person one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion has a natural quality to it. When person three says {\i va\u8217'ani}, the most immediate referent for those words is person two, who just spoke. The chain is sequential. The practical implication \u8212- that the chain can extend indefinitely, each link connecting within {\i tokh kedei dibbur} of the prior link \u8212- follows directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's conclusion also returns us to a question that runs beneath the entire discussion: to what extent is this the default legal interpretation of {\i va\u8217'ani}, and to what extent does it depend on the specific circumstances? One can readily imagine cases where person three was facing person one and clearly intending to connect to that original declaration, not to person two. Whether halacha would honor that stated intent, or whether the sequential {\i hatfasa} model operates objectively regardless of subjective direction, is a question the {\i Gemara} does not definitively resolve. As a default in the absence of clarifying context, the rule is {\i chad batar chad matpis} \u8212- each connects to the one immediately before.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 25\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reish Lakish vs.\u160 ?R. Yoch\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Death/Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a debate concerning a foundational halacha of {\i nazir}: when a {\i nazir} dies or a husband annuls his wife\u8217's vow of {\i nezirut}, and money has been sanctified and set aside for the {\i korbanot} without specification as to which {\i korban} each sum was designated for, the entire pile is assigned to an {\i olah}. The Gemara understands this case to involve a pile that would have contributed to all of the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korbanot}, including a {\i chatat} \u8212- making the ruling a genuine {\i chiddush}. It is not as if the entire pile could have gone to an {\i olah} in the ordinary course of events; some portion of it was destined for a {\i chatat}. And yet, the halacha is that once the funds can no longer be used for their {\i nazir} purpose, the entire pile is directed toward an {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan holds that this ruling is a {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} \u8212- a tradition received from Sinai with no textual derivation. On his view, the halacha distinguishes between {\i stumim} (undesignated funds) and {\i meforashim} (designated funds): if the pile was non-designated, this special dispensation applies; if individual sums were explicitly designated \u8212- this pile for the {\i chatat}, this for the {\i olah}, this for the {\i shelamim} \u8212- then the halacha was not said, and each sum follows its own designation. Since it is a {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, no logical questions can be pressed against it. It operates as an arbitrary rule confined to its precise parameters, and one cannot ask why similar scenarios are treated differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\u8217's Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish, by contrast, derives this halacha from a {\i pasuk}: {\i le-chol nidreihem u-le-chol nidvosem} \u8212- \u8220"for all their vows and for all their free-will offerings.\u8221" He understands {\i nezirut} as something that begins as a {\i neder} (a vow), and leftover or unusable funds therefrom can be directed toward a {\i nedava}, which paradigmatically refers to an {\i olah} \u8212- the quintessential free-will offering that is entirely given over. The Gemara, however, presses Reish Lakish: if the derivation is from this {\i pasuk}, why should the halacha be limited to undesignated funds? Even if the money was explicitly designated, it originated from a {\i neder} and now becomes a {\i nedava} \u8212- so let it go to an {\i olah} regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: designated funds cannot be repurposed. This principle is already established from the {\i braita} of the school of Rabbi Yishmael. Once funds carry a concrete designation, they remain within that category \u8212- just as a {\i tmura} or {\i vlad} (derivative sanctities) retain the identity of their parent {\i kedusha}. Accordingly, since we already know that anything with a designated {\i kedusha} cannot be redirected, the {\i pasuk} learned by Reish Lakish can only be speaking to a pile of undesignated funds. That is precisely where the {\i chiddush} lies: even though it was clear that {\i chatat} money was present in that pile, since it had not been formally designated, the {\i pasuk} permits the entire pile to be used as an {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vlad & Tmura\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rak Kodashecha Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to unpack the {\i braita} of Rabbi Yishmael, which grounds the broader principle about derivative sanctities. The {\i pasuk} reads: {\i rak kodashecha asher yihyu lecha u-nedarecha tisa u-vata el hamakom asher yivchar Hashem} \u8212- \u8220"Only your holy things that you possess, and your vows, shall you lift up and come to the place that God shall choose.\u8221" The {\i pasuk} concludes: {\i ve-asita olotecha habasar vehadam al mizbe\u8217'ach Hashem Elokecha vedam zevachecha yishapech al yesod mizbe\u8217'ach Hashem v\u8217'habasar tochel} \u8212- bring your {\i olot}, and the blood and flesh of your {\i shelamim} upon the altar, and eat the meat. Since there are ample other {\i pesukim} instructing one to bring {\i korbanot} to the {\i Beit Hamikdash}, this {\i pasuk} must be communicating something additional. The {\i midrash halacha} identifies that additional teaching: {\i bevladei kodashim u-vitmurasam hakatuv medaber} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} is speaking of the offspring of consecrated animals ({\i vlad}) and substituted animals ({\i tmura}), both of which represent derivative {\i kedusha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bringing Derivatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have supposed that such derivative sanctities \u8212- since they are not the original consecration \u8212- should be left to graze until they develop a blemish and are redeemed. The {\i pasuk} of {\i tisa u-vata} instructs that they be brought to the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. But perhaps the intent is merely to bring them there and withhold food and water until they die \u8212- {\i yimna mehem mayim u-mazon bi-shvil she-yamusu}. Therefore the {\i pasuk} continues: {\i ve-asita olotecha habasar vehadam} \u8212- you shall actually bring them as sacrifices. The principle derived is: {\i ke-derech she-ata noheg ba-olah noheg bi-tmurata} \u8212- treat the {\i tmura} of an {\i olah} as an {\i olah} is treated; and {\i ke-derech she-ata noheg bi-shlamim noheg bi-vladeihem} \u8212- treat the offspring of a {\i shelamim} as a {\i shelamim} is treated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A subtle point underlies the pairing of examples. An {\i olah} must be male \u8212- {\i im olah korbano min ha-zachar} \u8212- and therefore cannot produce a {\i vlad}. Accordingly, the example of a derivative {\i olah} is a {\i tmura}, not a {\i vlad}. A {\i shelamim}, by contrast, may be female, so it can produce offspring; hence the example of a derivative {\i shelamim} is a {\i vlad}. The {\i pasuk} thus instructs that each type of derivative is brought in the manner appropriate to its original {\i kedusha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further ask: {\i yachol af vlad chatat u-tmurat asham kein} \u8212- perhaps the same applies to the offspring of a {\i chatat} or the {\i tmura} of an {\i asham}, and those too should be brought as their respective {\i korbanot}? To this, the {\i pasuk} responds with the word {\i rak} \u8212- \u8220"only\u8221" \u8212- which excludes the {\i chatat} and {\i asham}. This is the position of Rabbi Yishmael. The rationale is well-established: a {\i chatat} that cannot be brought for its original purpose \u8212- whether it is the offspring of a {\i chatat}, the {\i chatat} whose owner died, the {\i chatat} whose owner was already atoned for by another offering, the {\i chatat} that passed its year, or a {\i tmurat chatat} \u8212- must be let to die. This rule encompasses five cases, recalled by the mnemonic {\i vitmanah} (\u1493 ?-\u1514 ?-\u1502 ?-\u1504 ?-\u1506 ?): {\i vlad chatat}, {\i tmurat chatat}, {\i metu ba\u8217'aleha}, {\i nitkapru be-acher}, and {\i avrah shnatah}. For all five, the animal is starved until death, and this is a {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva holds that one need not derive the exclusion of the {\i chatat} and {\i asham} from the word {\i rak}. Rather, a separate {\i pasuk} states {\i asham hu} \u8212- \u8220"it is a guilt-offering\u8221" \u8212- from which the Gemara derives {\i ba-havayato yehei}: the {\i asham} retains its identity and cannot be redirected to serve any other function. Implicitly, the same applies to the {\i chatat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva is not immediately transparent, since both are learning from ancillary textual indicators. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's preference for {\i asham hu} over {\i rak} may stem from the fact that {\i rak} is a generic exclusion whose precise scope is uncertain, whereas {\i asham hu} speaks specifically to the {\i asham} and communicates directly that its identity is fixed and cannot be overridden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Learn from Pasuk?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a pressing question that the Gemara raises at multiple junctures: {\i lama li kra, hilchata g\u8217'miri lah} \u8212- why do we need a {\i pasuk} at all? We already have the {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} that the five {\i chatot} die and that whatever would require a {\i chatat} to die by an {\i asham} instead requires it to graze ({\i kol she-be-chatat metah be-asham ro\u8217'eh}). Why, then, does the {\i braita} appear to be deriving these very rulings from {\i pesukim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution differs for Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva. For Rabbi Yishmael, the {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} establishes that the animal cannot be validly brought ({\i lo kasher}), but the {\i pasuk} of {\i rak kodashecha} adds a further dimension: if one were to bring such a {\i chatat} in these circumstances, one would violate a {\i lav ha-ba mi-klal aseh} \u8212- a prohibition implicit in a positive command. The positive command {\i ve-asita olotecha} covers only {\i olot} and {\i shelamim} in their derivative forms, implicitly excluding the {\i chatat}. Bringing the {\i chatat} in this state thus constitutes a transgression of an {\i aseh}. The {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} tells us the act is invalid; the {\i pasuk} tells us it is additionally a transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rabbi Akiva, who does not connect the matter to the {\i pasuk} of {\i rak kodashecha}, this resolution is unavailable. The Gemara therefore says the {\i pasuk} of {\i asham hu} comes to teach the ruling articulated by Rav Huna in the name of Rav \u8212- a ruling about the {\i asham}\u8217's capacity for transformation, discussed in the following section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes, moreover, the broader methodological point that this entire {\i midrash halacha} presupposes the {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}. Tosafot observes that the plain sense of the {\i braita} suggests Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva are both deriving the basic rule about the {\i chatat} dying \u8212- but the Gemara refuses to read it that way. Since it is committed to the view that this halacha is already known from {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, it assigns each {\i tanna} a distinct teaching derived from the {\i pasuk}, even at the cost of departing from the surface reading of the {\i braita}. Tosafot candidly acknowledges this tension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hava Amina: Olah/Shelamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hava Amina Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before reaching the question of why the {\i pasuk} is needed, the Gemara addresses the {\i hava amina} \u8212- the initial assumption \u8212- that the {\i pasuk} must overcome in the first place. The initial assumption is not that one would starve derivative {\i olot} and {\i shelamim} everywhere; rather, it is something more peculiar: {\i yachol ya\u8217'aleh le-veit habechira ve-yimna mehem mayim u-mazon bi-shvil she-yamusu} \u8212- one might have brought them up to the {\i Beit Hamikdash} and let them die there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i hava amina} arises from the structure of the {\i pasuk} itself. The first part, {\i tisa u-vata}, instructs bringing these animals to the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. One might read this as: bring them there, and then \u8212- since they are not eligible to be brought as original consecrations \u8212- leave them there to die. The {\i pasuk} then adds {\i ve-asita olotecha habasar vehadam}, teaching that they must actually be offered as sacrifices. The {\i hava amina} is therefore a perfectly logical reading of the sequence of the {\i pasuk}, and the conclusion ({\i ka mashma lan d\u8217'lo}) dispels it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bring as Sacrifices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} thus teaches: bring derivative {\i olot} and {\i shelamim} \u8212- whether {\i tmura} or {\i vlad} \u8212- and offer them as the {\i korban} that their original {\i kedusha} represents. The derivative {\i olah} is brought as an {\i olah}; the derivative {\i shelamim} is brought as a {\i shelamim}. The word {\i rak} then comes to limit this dispensation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatat Must Die\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i rak} excludes the {\i vlad chatat} and {\i tmurat asham} from this permission. The {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} of the five {\i chatot} that die is well established, and the Gemara is resolute that it not be displaced by a {\i pasuk}-derived rule. This is why, for Rabbi Yishmael, the {\i pasuk}\u8217's purpose is re-understood: rather than establishing that these animals cannot be brought (which is known from {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}), the {\i pasuk} establishes the additional {\i issur aseh} one incurs by bringing them improperly. For Rabbi Akiva, the {\i pasuk} of {\i asham hu} teaches the more nuanced halacha of Rav Huna \u8212- that the {\i asham}\u8217's identity can transform, but only once it has been formally handed over to pasture.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asham\u8217's Identity Transfo\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asham Given to Pasture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the question of why Rabbi Akiva needs the {\i pasuk} of {\i asham hu} by invoking the teaching of Rav Huna in the name of Rav: {\i asham she-nitak le-re\u8217'iya u-shechato le-shem ola kasher} \u8212- an {\i asham} that has been handed over to graze, if one then {\i shechts} it with the intent that it be an {\i olah}, it is valid as an {\i olah}. Once the {\i asham} has been set into the process of grazing \u8212- the process by which it will eventually develop a blemish, be redeemed, and have its funds applied to an {\i olah} \u8212- its {\i kedusha} has already functionally transformed. Even though the animal itself has not yet been redeemed, the act of handing it over to the shepherd initiates a shift in its identity such that a {\i shechita} performed {\i le-shem olah} at that point is effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This halacha applies specifically to an {\i asham} that cannot be used for its original purpose \u8212- a {\i tmurat asham}, an {\i asham} whose owner has died, or whose owner has already been atoned for by another offering. For such an {\i asham}, the {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} prescribes that it graze until it develops a blemish, at which point the animal is redeemed for its monetary value and that money is brought as an {\i olah}. The transformation is not of the money to another {\i asham} or {\i chatat} \u8212- since the original purpose can no longer be served \u8212- but specifically to an {\i olah}. The {\i pasuk} of {\i asham hu} comes to teach, according to Rabbi Akiva, that this process of identity transformation is real and operative \u8212- and that it is only once the {\i asham} is handed over to the shepherd that its {\i kedusha} begins to shift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transformation Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The condition is precise: {\i taima d\u8217'nitak} \u8212- the reason for the transformation is that the {\i asham} was actually handed over for pasture. {\i Ha lo nitak lo} \u8212- if it was not yet handed over, even if the owner knows full well that this {\i asham} can no longer serve its original purpose, the animal\u8217's identity has not yet shifted. {\i D\u8217'amar kra hu, asham hu, ba-havayato yehei} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} affirms that it retains its status as an {\i asham} unless and until something actually initiates the transformative process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a significant Tosafot on this sugya, since Rabbeinu Tam takes the position that the requirement of handing over to pasture is only {\i derabbanan}, not {\i de\u8217'oraisa}. On his reading, what truly shifts the animal\u8217's identity at the {\i de\u8217'oraisa} level is the {\i kavana} at the time of {\i shechita}: if one {\i shechts} it {\i le-shem olah}, it is valid as an {\i olah}, even without having first handed it to a shepherd; if one {\i shechts} it {\i le-shem asham}, it does not work. The plain sense of the Gemara, however, appears to establish the handing-over-to-pasture as the operative {\i de\u8217'oraisa} criterion, and Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's reinterpretation departs significantly from that reading in order to reconcile it with another {\i sugya} that seems to undercut the {\i de\u8217'oraisa} status of the pasturing requirement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beyond Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Obligation, Multi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's formulation, which described the rule as a {\i halacha} specifically in {\i nazir}. The Gemara asks: is this truly unique to {\i nazir}? Surely the same halacha applies in other analogous cases. The Gemara answers: {\i tana nazir ve-chayvei kinim d\u8217'damu lei} \u8212- the {\i tanna} taught {\i nazir} because it is the paradigm, but the halacha extends to all cases that resemble {\i nazir} \u8212- namely, any situation in which a single obligation generates multiple {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yoledes Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} establishes the parallel case: those obligated to bring {\i kinim} (pairs of bird-offerings) \u8212- of whom the {\i yoledes} is a classic example \u8212- who set aside money for their birds, and subsequently come into greater wealth such that they are now obligated to bring animal sacrifices rather than birds. Since their pile was set aside for an open-ended {\i chovah} (obligation), without specifying how much goes to the {\i chatat}-bird and how much to the {\i olah}-bird, the same principles apply: {\i rotzeh le-havi bahen chatat behemah yavi, olat behemah yavi} \u8212- one may direct the whole pile toward the animal {\i chatat} or toward the animal {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot engages carefully with the language used in setting aside such a pile. If one says \u8220"this is for my {\i korbanot},\u8221" Tosafot understands this to mean that both {\i korbanot} must come from this pile, even though it remains unspecified which sum is for which. If one says merely \u8220"this is for my obligation,\u8221" the language is open-ended enough that the whole pile could go toward a single {\i korban}. This distinction matters because the {\i chiddush} of the halacha \u8212- that after the person\u8217's death the pile can go entirely to an {\i olah} \u8212- is greatest when the pile was understood to contain both {\i chatat} and {\i olah} money, yet the halacha redirects the whole to an {\i olah}. If the pile was open-ended enough that even while the person lived it could be assigned entirely to one {\i korban}, there is less novelty in the post-death ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i yoledes} case: if she dies and the pile is {\i stumim} (unspecified), {\i yiplu le-nedavah} \u8212- it falls to an {\i olah}. This is precisely the same ruling as in {\i nazir}, confirming that the halacha is not limited to {\i nazir} but extends to any single obligation that generates multiple {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Independent Obligati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast case is introduced to delineate the scope of this principle precisely. The {\i braita} describes a person who is obligated in a {\i chatat} (arising from one cause) and who separately accepts upon himself an obligation of an {\i olah} (a completely unrelated {\i neder}). He sets aside money and says {\i harei eilu le-chovotai} \u8212- this is for my obligations. Since he has two entirely independent obligations, the pile contains both {\i chatat} money and {\i olah} money: {\i rotzeh le-havi bahen chatat behemah lo yavi, olat behemah lo yavi} \u8212- he may not use the entire pile for either the animal {\i chatat} or the animal {\i olah} alone. Each obligation\u8217's funds must be distinguished and directed appropriately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When such a person dies with this unspecified pile, {\i yelchu le-yam ha-melach} \u8212- the money must be thrown into the Dead Sea. The reason is that this is not a case of one obligation generating multiple {\i korbanot}; it is two separate, independent obligations that happen to share a single pool of funds. Since {\i chatat} money is present and cannot be repurposed, and since the {\i chatat} money cannot be separated out with precision, the entire pile is lost. One cannot extract the {\i olah} money from the mixture because no individual coin can be identified as specifically belonging to one obligation or the other. The sanctification of a coin is concrete \u8212- it is not merely a symbolic representation of general monetary value that can be reassigned \u8212- and thus the intermingling of {\i chatat} funds with {\i olah} funds, where the former cannot be redirected, disqualifies the entire pile from any further use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This stands in contrast to the {\i nazir} or {\i yoledes} case, where the single origin of the obligation means that even a pile containing identifiable {\i chatat} and {\i olah} funds can, upon the person\u8217's death, be directed entirely toward an {\i olah} through the special halacha derived either from {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} or from the {\i pasuk} of {\i le-chol nidreihem u-le-chol nidvosem}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unspecified vs.\u160 ?Designat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi clarifies the precise meaning of {\i meforashim} \u8212- explicitly designated \u8212- which serves as the cutoff beyond which the special halacha of redirecting funds to an {\i olah} no longer applies. His teaching refines what \u8220"unspecified\u8221" must mean in order for the halacha to operate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Naming Korbanos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might assume that \u8220"designated\u8221" refers only to the clearest case: where the {\i nazir} said explicitly {\i elu le-chatasi ve-elu le-olasi ve-elu le-shalmi} \u8212- \u8220"these coins are for my {\i chatat}, these for my {\i olah}, these for my {\i shelamim}.\u8221" Rav Ashi teaches that this is not the only case. Even if the person said {\i elu le-chatasi u-le-olasi u-le-shalmi} \u8212- \u8220"this pile is for my {\i chatat}, my {\i olah}, and my {\i shelamim}\u8221" \u8212- without further specifying which sum is for which, this is already considered {\i meforashim}. The explicit naming of the {\i chatat} as one of the intended recipients of this pile sufficiently concretizes the {\i chatat}\u8217's identity within that pile, and therefore the pile can no longer be redirected to an {\i olah} after the {\i nazirut} is annulled or the {\i nazir} dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obligation vs.\u160 ?Korbanos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second formulation attributed to Rav Ashi extends the principle further: {\i lo teima ad d\u8217'amar elu le-chatasi u-le-olasi u-le-shalmi} \u8212- do not say that only when one names the specific {\i korbanot} is it considered designated. Rather, {\i afilu amar elu le-chovati harei hein ke-meforashim} \u8212- even if one says merely \u8220"this pile is for my obligation,\u8221" that too is treated as designated, and the {\i chatat} money it contains cannot be redirected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a subtle question: if even saying \u8220"for my obligation\u8221" is considered sufficiently explicit to constitute {\i meforashim}, what formulation remains genuinely {\i stumim} and qualifies for the special halacha? Tosafot wrestles with this directly. He argues that saying {\i le-korbanot neziruti} \u8212- \u8220"for my {\i nazir}-offerings\u8221" \u8212- is marginally less concrete than {\i le-chovati}, because while both imply that all the {\i korbanot} are to come from the pile, the formulation {\i le-korbanot neziruti} does not explicitly invoke a {\i chatat} by name or category. Nevertheless, Tosafot acknowledges that even this formulation refers to all the {\i korbanot}, and therefore he is not entirely satisfied that this represents a true functional difference. The Gemara\u8217's {\i chiddush} \u8212- that a {\i stumim} pile containing {\i chatat} funds can go entirely to an {\i olah} \u8212- thus operates in an increasingly narrow band of cases, and the exact dividing line between {\i stumim} and {\i meforashim} remains genuinely difficult to pin down.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Separating Chatat Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pile Becomes Concrete\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava teaches a further principle that extends the concretization logic in a striking direction: {\i im hifrish d\u8217'mei chatat mibeineihem harei hen ke-meforashim} \u8212- if one separates out the {\i chatat} funds from an otherwise unspecified pile, then even the remaining funds \u8212- which consist only of {\i olah} and {\i shelamim} money, with no {\i chatat} money remaining \u8212- are treated as though the entire pile has become designated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The intuition one might bring to this case would be exactly the opposite: now that the {\i chatat} money has been cleanly removed, there is no longer any admixture of {\i chatat} funds. Surely the remaining pile should now qualify for the special halacha and go entirely to an {\i olah}? Rava rules otherwise. The act of separating out the {\i chatat} funds draws the entire pile out of the indeterminate, amorphous state in which the special halacha operates. Once something about the pile has been made concrete and real \u8212- even if it is only the {\i chatat} portion being segregated \u8212- the pile as a whole crosses from that indeterminate state into a more determinate one. Its identity has begun to solidify, and the special mechanism by which an unspecified pile is redirected to an {\i olah} is no longer available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Must Divide Remaining Mo\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence is that after the separation, the remaining {\i olah} and {\i shelamim} funds must be divided between an {\i olah} and a {\i shelamim} in the appropriate proportions; the entire remainder cannot simply be assigned to an {\i olah}. The pile has, in Rava\u8217's framing, entered the real world of determinate identity. Just as the Gemara\u8217's broader analysis draws on the image of sanctified funds as concretely attached to specific purposes \u8212- each coin, once designated, is not merely symbolic monetary value but carries a fixed {\i kedusha} \u8212- so too the act of beginning to apportion that pile triggers a kind of irreversible concretization. From that point forward, the remaining funds must be treated according to their actual proportional designations, and the special halacha of {\i stumim} no longer applies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maot Stumot Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Money to Olah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya on 26b opens with a ruling transmitted in the name of Rav Huna citing Rav: {\i lo shanu ela maot} \u8212- this principle applies only to money. The principle in question is the rule of {\i maot stumot}: if a {\i nazir} set aside an unspecified pile of money intended for his {\i chatat}, {\i olah}, and {\i shelamim}, and then died or had his {\i nezirut} annulled, that money is used entirely toward an {\i olah}. This is what we have been discussing. But Rav\u8217's ruling now introduces a critical limitation: {\i aval beavehemah, harei hi kimefureshet} \u8212- if instead of money it was animals, those animals are treated as if they were already explicitly designated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animals vs Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is: what exactly is the case of animals? If the animals in question were the precise three offerings a {\i nazir} is required to bring \u8212- a male lamb, a female lamb, and a ram \u8212- then the ruling would be self-evident. Each animal is inherently self-identifying, and of course each would be brought for its appropriate sacrifice. The case must therefore involve something less obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} works out that the {\i gemara} is discussing a situation in which someone set aside three cows \u8212- animals that are within the category of valid sacrifices but are not themselves the required offerings for this particular {\i korban nazir}, which must come from sheep. The owner declares that these animals are sanctified for the purpose of his sacrifices, intending that they will be sold, the proceeds redeemed, and the money then used to purchase the required animals. The question is why this situation is treated differently from an unspecified pile of money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Cow Kedushah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}\u8217's analysis turns on a careful distinction regarding {\i kedushah} and the ability to transfer it. To appreciate the problem, consider three scenarios. First: if one sanctifies a cow for {\i bedek habayit} \u8212- for the upkeep and operating expenses of the {\i Beit Hamikdash} \u8212- it may be sold immediately, just as one might donate a car or any object of value. Second: if one sanctifies a cow to be brought itself as an {\i olah}, then that {\i kedushah} cannot be transferred and the cow cannot be sold until it develops a {\i mum} \u8212- a blemish \u8212- since only the onset of a blemish can release it from the sanctity that had designated it as the animal itself to be offered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The interesting middle case \u8212- the one {\i Tosafot} grapples with \u8212- is where one sanctifies a cow with the {\i kedushah} of an {\i olah}, but not because the cow itself will be brought as that {\i olah}. Rather, since the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i olah} must be a sheep, the cow is being sanctified solely as a funding mechanism: sell the cow, take the proceeds, buy the sheep. One might have thought that because the cow itself was never going to be brought, the {\i kedushah} could simply be transferred through a sale without waiting for a {\i mum}. But {\i Tosafot} concludes, drawing on the logic of this very {\i sugya}, that the answer is otherwise. Since the cow belongs to the world of valid {\i korbanot} \u8212- since in theory a cow could be brought as an {\i olah}, even though that is not the {\i kedushah} designated here \u8212- the {\i kedushah} clings to it with full force, and cannot be removed without a {\i mum}. By contrast, if one were to sanctify a chicken \u8212- which is not a valid candidate for an {\i olah} of an animal \u8212- that could simply be sold outright without waiting for any blemish, because the chicken does not inhabit the world of animal {\i korbanot} and therefore the {\i kedushah} does not attach to it in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion has direct implications for our {\i halakha}. The question of whether animals are treated as {\i mefureshet} or as {\i maot stumot} depends heavily on whether they can be freely liquidated, which in turn depends on whether a {\i mum} is required before sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman: Tmimah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now provides the ruling of Rav Nachman, which clarifies the scope of Rav\u8217's statement: {\i ha de-amrinan be-behemah harei hi ke-mefureshet \u8212- lo shanu ela bitmimah}. When we say that animals are treated as explicitly designated, that applies only to an unblemished animal, a {\i tmimah}. Such animals, because of the {\i kedushah} problem just described, cannot be sold until they develop a {\i mum}, and this waiting period gives them a different legal character. {\i Aval ba\u8217'alat mum} \u8212- but if the animal already has a blemish \u8212- {\i harei hi kistumah} \u8212- it is treated like unspecified funds, like {\i maot stumot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the obvious question: why should the presence or absence of a {\i mum} matter to our {\i halakha} about whether the funds ultimately go toward an {\i olah}? Even animals without a {\i mum} will eventually develop one, or be sold, and the proceeds will be divided among the required {\i korbanot}. Why should the ease or difficulty of liquidation determine whether the pile counts as specified or unspecified?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezuman Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To answer this, the {\i gemara} offers two approaches. The first is formal: the {\i halakha} of {\i maot stumot} is a {\i halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, and it was transmitted specifically regarding money \u8212- {\i maot} \u8212- not regarding other things. If one has non-cash items with {\i kedushah} mixed in \u8212- including potential {\i chatat} funds \u8212- and the case does not fall within the ambit of this {\i halakha}, then the proper resolution is to cast the money into the {\i Yam HaMelach}, because the {\i chatat} component cannot be used and the entire pile must be destroyed. On this formal approach, the {\i halakha} simply was not said about anything other than cash, and that is the end of the matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then what is the line between cash and not cash? Here the concept of {\i mezuman} becomes operative. The Hebrew word for cash in modern parlance is {\i mezuman} \u8212- literally, \u8220"something ready.\u8221" The underlying idea is that something readily and immediately convertible to money, with minimal delay or friction, shares the legal character of money. An animal with a {\i mum} has no {\i kedushah} impediment to its sale \u8212- one simply brings it to market and sells it. That is cash. An unblemished animal cannot be sold until it develops a blemish; it must sit, grazing, for an indeterminate period. That is not cash. Similarly, a {\i niska} \u8212- a bar of precious metal \u8212- was regarded by some as sufficiently difficult to sell at the right price that it too falls outside the {\i mezuman} category, while others, as Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak rules, disagree and consider even metal bars to be cash-equivalent. A {\i shura d\u8217'korei} \u8212- a pile of lumber or beams \u8212- is clearly not cash, since finding a buyer willing to inspect and purchase raw timber requires locating someone building a house and negotiating terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second approach offers a logic beyond the formal rule. When one has three unblemished cows that must wait for a {\i mum} before they can be sold, one may, over time, mentally begin to pre-designate: \u8220"Bessie will go toward the {\i olah}, that other cow toward the {\i chatat}.\u8221" Even if this mental designation was never verbalized, the extended waiting period creates the conditions for this kind of private assignment, and the pile accordingly begins to take on the character of {\i mefureshet} \u8212- as if it were already explicitly divided. This is, admittedly, a somewhat rabbinic-sounding concern, and {\i Tosafot} grapples with whether an unspoken mental designation can actually alter the legal status of the pile. But as a framework for understanding the distinction, it at least provides some logic, even if the cleaner approach remains the formal rule: the {\i halakha} was said only about {\i mezuman}, about things that are readily liquidated, and an animal without a {\i mum} simply does not qualify.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakha Scope Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimi: Metal & Wood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shimi bar Ashi poses a direct question to Rav Papa: {\i mai tama d\u8217'Rabbanan d\u8217'amri d\u8217'maot v\u8217'lo behema v\u8217'lo naska} \u8212- what is the reason that the Rabbis said this {\i halakha} applies to money and not to animals, not to metal bars, {\i v\u8217'lo shura} \u8212- and not to a pile of beams? He has just been told that the rule is formally limited to {\i maot} \u8212- cash or cash-like items \u8212- and he is challenging the basis of that limitation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birds Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rav Shimi presses further with what turns out to be the most interesting extension of this question: {\i maot v\u8217'lo ofot} \u8212- money, and not birds? To understand what he means, one must recall a distinct {\i halakha} involving {\i kinei yoledes} \u8212- the two birds that a woman who has given birth must bring, one as an {\i olah} and one as a {\i chatat}. There, the {\i halakha} is that she need not specify at the time of purchase which bird is which; the specification can be made either by the owner at the time of purchase or by the {\i kohen} at the time of bringing. What would happen, then, if such a woman purchased two birds without specifying, and then died before either stage of specification occurred?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One would naturally assume that since one bird is an {\i olah} and one is a {\i chatat}, the {\i kohen} should simply bring whichever one he designates as the {\i olah} and let the {\i chatat} die \u8212- since a {\i chatat} cannot be brought on behalf of someone who has died. But the question arises: should we treat those two unspecified birds the way we treat {\i maot stumot}? The birds have the same problem \u8212- there is a {\i chatat} and an {\i olah} mixed together in an unspecified pile, the owner has died, and perhaps we should simply bring both as an {\i olah}, just as we do with unspecified money. On the other hand, given that the {\i gemara} has been consistently narrowing the scope of the {\i maot stumot} rule \u8212- excluding animals, excluding metal bars, excluding lumber \u8212- perhaps birds too fall outside its ambit, and in that case both birds could not be brought as an {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the tension Rav Shimi is articulating. The initial assumption of the {\i gemara} is that these birds, having not yet been specified \u8212- neither by the owner at purchase nor by the {\i kohen} at the time of the offering \u8212- retain the status of {\i stumim}, unspecified. And if they are {\i stumim}, and the {\i halakha} of {\i maot stumot} applies broadly, then both should go toward an {\i olah}. But given how sharply the {\i gemara} has been limiting the rule, maybe not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} here raises three possible readings of this passage, and the one that emerges as most coherent is the following: the {\i gemara} is not merely asking a hypothetical about whether the rule should apply to birds, but rather grounding its reasoning in the language Rav Chisda used \u8212- {\i mitparshot} \u8212- which will now become the pivot of the discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda: Birds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} invokes the teaching of Rav Chisda: {\i ein hakinim mitparshot ela o belakichat bealim o ba\u8217'asiyat hakohen} \u8212- birds are not explicated, are not rendered {\i mefureshet}, except either at the moment the owner purchases them or at the moment the {\i kohen} performs the offering. The word Rav Chisda chose is significant. He did not say that the birds\u8217' identity is not {\i established} or not {\i fixed} \u8212- he said they are not {\i mitparshot}, not rendered explicit. And {\i mitparshot} is the precise opposite of {\i satum}. By using this language, Rav Chisda implicitly assigned the birds the status of {\i stumim} whenever neither the owner nor the {\i kohen} has yet acted. If the woman dies before either stage, the birds remain {\i satum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And if their status is {\i satum}, what follows? The {\i gemara} reads Rav Chisda\u8217's language as implying that such unspecified birds follow the {\i halakha} of {\i maot stumot} \u8212- if the owner dies, they are both brought as an {\i olah}, as a {\i nedava}. But then Rav Shimi\u8217's question bites: you have just been told the rule only applies to money, not to animals, not to metal, not to lumber. So why should birds be different? Why would the {\i halakha} of {\i maot stumot} apply here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i v\u8217'litameich} \u8212- according to your counter-reasoning, that we should not limit the rule to money at all and should apply it across the board to anything unspecified. If that is so, there is an immediate problem from the {\i Mishnah} cited by Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel: {\i heivi shalosh behemot velo peireish} \u8212- if one brought three animals without specifying which is which, and those three animals happen to be the precise animals required \u8212- a male lamb, a female lamb, and a ram \u8212- then {\i harauya lechatas tikarev chatas, le-olah tikarev olah, ulishlamim tikarev shelamim} \u8212- each is brought for its appropriate sacrifice. The {\i kedushah} is apportioned correctly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if one wishes to apply the {\i maot stumot} rule to everything that is unspecified, one should be troubled by this ruling. The {\i gemara} says these animals were not specified \u8212- {\i behemah lav kimeforeshet dami} \u8212- animals are not like {\i mefureshet}. If animals truly share the status of {\i stumim}, then even these three animals \u8212- the exact right animals for the sacrifice \u8212- should follow the rule of {\i stumim} and we should bring them all as an {\i olah}. Why instead does each animal go to its appropriate sacrifice? This seems to directly contradict the position that {\i stumim} applies broadly to all unspecified items regardless of their form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Response: Gender\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response is sharp \u8212- almost dismissive. {\i Hacha nekeiva, hacha zachar} \u8212- here, the {\i chatat} is female and the {\i olah} is male. These animals are not unspecified in any meaningful sense. A male sheep and a female sheep are self-identifying. Of course they cannot have the status of {\i stumim} \u8212- it is perfectly clear which is which. The question was, frankly, absurd. {\i Stumim} describes a situation where it genuinely cannot be determined which item belongs to which category. If you have the exact right animals and their sex alone tells you which goes where, there is nothing unspecified about the situation. It is as though one had three piles of money with signs reading {\i olah}, {\i chatat}, and {\i shelamim} \u8212- that is not {\i stumim} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the broader debate remains: is the {\i maot stumot} rule formally limited to money and cash-equivalent items, or does it apply broadly to any genuinely unspecified constellation of {\i kodesh} items? The case of birds sharpens this question because birds present a genuine case of {\i stumim} \u8212- identical in appearance, capable of being brought as either {\i olah} or {\i chatat}, and unspecified until the act of the owner or {\i kohen}. If the rule is broad, both birds go toward an {\i olah} when the owner dies. If the rule is narrow and limited to money, the birds must be handled differently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita: Son & Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Father Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i braita} found in full on 30b, which the text here cites in abbreviated form. The {\i braita} poses the question: {\i keitzad amru ish megaleiach al nezirus aviv} \u8212- when does a person get to shave \u8212- meaning, when does a son get to use his deceased father\u8217's money to bring the {\i korbanot} for his own {\i nezirut}? The {\i braita} sets up the scenario: a father was a {\i nazir}, set aside unspecified money for his {\i nezirut}, and then died. Without the son\u8217's involvement, that money would simply go toward an {\i olah}, as we have established. The question is whether the son can step in and use that money for his own {\i korbanot} instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 1: After Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i v\u8217'amar hareini nazir al m\u8217'nas she\u8217'agaleach al ma\u8217'os abba} \u8212- the son declares himself a {\i nazir} on condition that he gets to use his father\u8217's money to bring his {\i korbanot}. This declaration was made after his father died. In this case, the son succeeds. The money was standing there, its original purpose no longer achievable since the father had died, and its ultimate destination had not yet been determined. The son stepped in at precisely the right moment, accepted {\i nezirut} explicitly tied to the use of that money, and is therefore entitled to use it. {\i Zehu she-megaleiach al ma\u8217'os aviv} \u8212- this is the case where the son actually does bring his {\i korbanot} with his father\u8217's money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 2: Simultaneous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: {\i aval mi shehaya hu v\u8217'aviv nezirim} \u8212- he and his father were both {\i nezirim} at the same time, meaning the son had already accepted {\i nezirut} while his father was still alive. The father subsequently set aside money for his {\i nezirut} and then died. The son now says he wants to use that money for his own {\i korbanot}. {\i Harei elu yiplu l\u8217'nedavah} \u8212- in this case, he cannot do so. The money falls to {\i nedava}, following the standard rule of {\i maot stumot}. The son cannot claim it because he was already a {\i nazir} before his father died; there was no moment where the money was available and his {\i nezirut} was formed with the intent to use it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specified Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then addresses the parallel case involving animals. {\i Haysa lo behemah mefureshet} \u8212- if the father had specifically designated particular animals \u8212- {\i chatat tamus} \u8212- the {\i chatat} animal dies since it cannot be brought, {\i olah tikarev olah} \u8212- the {\i olah} is brought as an {\i olah}, {\i shelamim yikrevu shelamim} \u8212- the {\i shelamim} is brought as {\i shelamim}. Each specified animal follows its designated {\i kedushah}, and there is nothing unusual about this outcome.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mum Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inference: Unspecified M\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now draws an inference from the {\i braita}. When the {\i braita} contrasts unspecified money \u8212- which goes toward an {\i olah} as {\i nedava} \u8212- with specified animals \u8212- where each goes to its designated sacrifice \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai lav afilu ba\u8217'alat mum} \u8212- does this not apply even to animals with a blemish? That is, the {\i braita} seems to be drawing a contrast between money and all forms of animals. If so, even animals with a {\i mum}, which we have suggested are cash-equivalent, would still fall on the \u8220"animal\u8221" side of the ledger and would be treated as {\i mefureshet} rather than as {\i maot stumot}. This would undermine what Rav Nachman had ruled earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this inference. {\i Lo, tmimah} \u8212- no, the {\i braita} is speaking of unblemished animals, or more precisely, it is speaking of clearly specified animals, where each animal\u8217's identity as {\i chatat}, {\i olah}, or {\i shelamim} was already made explicit by the owner. The {\i braita}\u8217's contrast is not between money and all animals, but between unspecified money and explicitly designated animals. {\i Aval ba\u8217'alat mum k\u8217'stumah dami} \u8212- but an animal with a blemish retains the status of {\i stumim} and is treated like money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates a further question from the {\i gemara}: if animals with a {\i mum} are truly cash-equivalent, then why does the {\i braita} not make this clear? Why contrast unspecified money with specified animals, when there is an intermediate category \u8212- unspecified blemished animals \u8212- that behaves just like money? Why not illustrate the {\i halakha} using that intermediate case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mum as Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i hachi nami} \u8212- in fact, animals with a {\i mum} are simply included in the category of money. {\i Ba\u8217'alat mum l\u8217'mai kadeshah? Lidmei} \u8212- when an animal with a blemish is sanctified, what is its sanctity directed toward? To its monetary proceeds. It will be sold, and the {\i kedushah} will transfer to the money received. {\i D\u8217'mei haynu maot} \u8212- proceeds are money. When the {\i braita} says \u8220"money,\u8221" it means anything whose {\i kedushah} operates through its monetary value, including blemished animals. The {\i braita} was not being incomplete; it was using \u8220"money\u8221" in a sense broad enough to encompass everything that is sold for its value and whose {\i kedushah} transfers to the proceeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus arrives at its final principle. The {\i halakha} of {\i maot stumot} \u8212- that an unspecified pile of funds intended for a {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korbanot} goes toward an {\i olah} when the owner dies \u8212- applies to anything whose {\i kedushah} operates through its monetary value: actual coins, blemished animals, and according to Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak, even metal bars. It does not apply to unblemished animals that cannot be sold until they develop a {\i mum}, and it does not apply to things like lumber that require significant effort to liquidate. The line is {\i mezuman} \u8212- readiness, liquidity, the practical ability to convert immediately into cash. Anything on the {\i mezuman} side of that line follows the {\i halakha} of {\i maot stumot}; anything that requires waiting or exceptional circumstances to convert falls outside it and is treated as {\i mefureshet}, as already designated, because the extended holding period creates the conditions for mental pre-designation or simply because the formal {\i halakha} was never transmitted for such cases in the first place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 26b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maot Setumot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unspecified Money Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 26b opens with a statement of Rav Huna in the name of Rav: {\i lo shanu ela ma\u8217'ot} \u8212- the rule we have been discussing, whereby unspecified funds set aside for the {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir} (the {\i chatat}, {\i olah}, and {\i shelamim}) are directed toward an {\i olah} when the {\i nazirut} is annulled or the {\i nazir} dies, applies only when those funds are literally money. {\i Aval behemah harei hi k\u8217'mefureshet} \u8212- but if instead of money, animals were set aside, those animals are treated as already specified, as though each animal\u8217's identity has been designated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animals vs.\u160 ?Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What exactly is the case under discussion? The Gemara is terse here, and Tosafot works through the possibilities at some length in order to establish the basic {\i pshat}. One might have thought the case involved the precise animals a {\i nazir} is obligated to bring \u8212- a female lamb for the {\i chatat}, a male lamb for the {\i olah}, and a ram for the {\i shelamim}. But if those are the animals in question, it is self-evident that each is already identified: they are three distinct species, and there is no ambiguity whatsoever. The {\i olah} animal, the {\i chatat} animal, and the {\i shelamim} animal identify themselves. In that case it would be trivially obvious that the animals are {\i mefurashim} \u8212- specified \u8212- and there would be nothing to teach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot therefore concludes that what the Gemara has in mind is a different scenario: the person sanctified three cows \u8212- animals that are within the world of {\i korbanot} in some sense but are not themselves the animals required for the {\i korban nazir}, which demands a sheep for the {\i olah}. The intent would be to sell the cows, take the proceeds, and use the money to purchase the required animals. Now the question becomes: when three cows are sanctified with the {\i kedusha} of {\i korbanot}, and they will eventually be sold and their proceeds divided among the various sacrifices, why should they not be treated like an unspecified pile of money? Which cow\u8217's proceeds are going to which sacrifice? Surely this should be {\i stumah} \u8212- unspecified \u8212- in exactly the same way as an indistinct pile of coins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cows Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand why animals are not treated like unspecified money, it is necessary to work through an important distinction that Tosafot develops and that will prove central to the Gemara\u8217's entire discussion. The question is what happens when one sanctifies an object with the {\i kedusha} of a {\i korban} but the object itself is not of the type that could be brought as that {\i korban}. For example, if one were to sanctify a digital recorder with the {\i kedusha} of an {\i olah}, the recorder cannot itself be placed on the {\i mizbei\u8217'ach}; what happens is that the {\i kedusha} transfers to the proceeds of its sale, and those proceeds are then used to purchase the required animal. This is a normal and unproblematic transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now consider a different case: one sanctifies a cow with the {\i kedusha} of an {\i olah} for a {\i nazir}, which must be a sheep. The cow is within the world of {\i korbanot} \u8212- it is the kind of animal that could, in the right circumstances, be brought as a {\i korban}. Does that make a difference? According to Tosafot, it does, and it makes a critical one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedusha Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle at work is this: if an object is sanctified for {\i bedek ha-bayis} \u8212- for the maintenance fund of the {\i Beis HaMikdash} \u8212- it has no identity as a sacrificial animal and can simply be sold at any time. If an animal is sanctified to be brought itself as a {\i korban olah}, its {\i kedusha} cannot be removed and the animal cannot be sold until it develops a {\i mum} \u8212- a disqualifying blemish \u8212- because only a blemish releases it from the realm of {\i korbanot} and permits its sale. The intermediate case is when an animal is sanctified with the {\i kedusha} of an {\i olah} but is not itself going to be brought as one \u8212- as with our cow, whose proceeds will fund a sheep {\i olah}. Does this middle case follow the rule of {\i bedek ha-bayis}, permitting immediate sale, or does it follow the rule of a {\i korban} animal, requiring a {\i mum} before sale?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot concludes from the present {\i sugya} that because the cow is intrinsically relevant to the world of {\i korbanot} \u8212- it is, after all, the kind of animal that could in principle serve as a {\i korban} \u8212- its {\i kedusha} cannot simply be transferred through a sale. The {\i kedusha} remains stuck to the cow until the cow develops a {\i mum} and thereby exits the world of {\i korbanot}. By contrast, if one were to sanctify a chicken with the {\i kedusha} of an {\i olah}, since a chicken is wholly outside the world of animal {\i korbanot}, the {\i kedusha} presents no obstacle to an immediate sale. It is this distinction \u8212- whether the object is {\i relevant} to the world of {\i korbanot} or not \u8212- that gives meaning to the distinction between animals with a {\i mum} and animals without one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temimah vs.\u160 ?Mum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Tmimah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this background, Rav Nachman adds a crucial qualification to Rav\u8217's statement: {\i lo shanu ela temimah} \u8212- the rule that animals are treated like {\i mefureshet} (specified) applies only to an unblemished animal. The reason is now clear: an unblemished cow that has been sanctified with the {\i kedusha} of a {\i korban} cannot be sold until it develops a {\i mum}. It will sit in the owner\u8217's field for an extended and indeterminate period. During that time, as will be developed shortly, there is reason to think its identity may become fixed in the owner\u8217's mind in a way that distinguishes it from an undifferentiated pile of money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'alat Mum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval ba\u8217'alat mum harei k\u8217'stumah} \u8212- but if the animal already has a blemish, it is treated like unspecified funds. An animal with a {\i mum} is not subject to the {\i kedusha} restriction that prevents sale; it can be readily sold and converted to cash. That ready convertibility places it, for halachic purposes, in the same category as cash \u8212- and therefore it follows the {\i halacha} of {\i ma\u8217'ot setumot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maot Setumot (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cows Unspecified?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This still leaves a question unanswered. Even granting that an unblemished cow presents complications around {\i kedusha} and sale, why should those cows ultimately be treated as {\i mefureshet} \u8212- specified? When the question was posed at the outset \u8212- three cows, no specification of which one\u8217's proceeds go to which sacrifice \u8212- surely this should be treated as {\i stumah}, unspecified, just like a pile of money. Why does the Gemara say otherwise?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Limits of Maot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two ways to understand the limits of {\i maot setumot}. The first approach is that the entire {\i halacha} is a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} \u8212- a received tradition from Sinai \u8212- and that tradition was stated specifically with respect to {\i ma\u8217'ot}, cash, and not with respect to other forms of property. Under this view, even if one\u8217's three cows are genuinely unspecified \u8212- there is no clarity about which animal\u8217's proceeds will fund which sacrifice \u8212- it simply does not matter, because the {\i halacha} was never said for anything other than money. The cows remain non-{\i stumah} not because they are in fact specified, but because the special {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} never applied to them in the first place. The practical consequence is that the money cannot be directed toward an {\i olah}; since {\i chatat} funds are mixed in with everything else, the entire amount would have to be cast into the {\i Yam HaMelach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if this is purely a formal rule limited to money, one might wonder: what about animals with a {\i mum}, which Rav Nachman just said are treated like {\i setumah}? Animals with a {\i mum} are also not cash. So the question becomes: where exactly is the dividing line?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezuman Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word that illuminates this is {\i mezuman} \u8212- the modern Hebrew word for cash. The root meaning is something like \u8220"ready\u8221" or \u8220"at hand,\u8221" and the concept it captures is immediate transferability. The {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} was stated for things that are readily liquidable \u8212- readily converted into money for the purpose of purchasing the required {\i korbanot}. Something that can be sold quickly and easily in the normal course of commerce is functionally equivalent to cash and falls within the scope of the {\i halacha}. Something that requires time, negotiation, or waiting for the right buyer does not. On this reading, animals with a {\i mum} \u8212- which, since they bear no {\i kedusha} restriction, can be taken to market and sold without delay \u8212- are considered {\i mezuman}, cash-equivalent, and therefore fall within the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim}. Animals without a {\i mum}, which cannot be sold until a blemish appears, are not {\i mezuman}, not cash-equivalent, and therefore fall outside the {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Designation Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second approach attempts to supply an internal logic for why unblemished animals are treated as {\i mefureshet} \u8212- specified \u8212- rather than simply excluded by a formal rule. The reasoning is as follows: when an unblemished animal has been sanctified and cannot be sold until it develops a {\i mum}, it may sit in the owner\u8217's possession for a long time. Over the course of that extended waiting period, the owner is likely to begin mentally allocating the animals \u8212- thinking to himself that the proceeds from this cow will fund the {\i olah}, and the proceeds from that cow will fund the {\i chatat}. If such mental pre-designation occurs, the animals are no longer genuinely unspecified; they have been informally assigned in the owner\u8217's mind, even if no formal declaration has been made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning has an obvious difficulty, which Tosafot acknowledges: a mental assignment that has not been verbalized ordinarily has no halachic standing. Thoughts in the mind, without an act or declaration, do not usually alter the status of {\i kodshim}. For this reason, the cleaner and more defensible approach is simply the formal one: the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} is limited to things that are readily liquidable \u8212- to {\i mezuman}, cash or cash-equivalents. Anything that requires extended time to convert to money falls outside the {\i halacha}, and the question of whether its identity has been mentally pre-designated is beside the point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Shimi Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metal vs.\u160 ?Wood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The foregoing discussion raises a further question about exactly where to draw the line between what counts as {\i mezuman} and what does not. One position implies that a {\i nascha} \u8212- a bar of precious metal, silver or gold \u8212- does not qualify as cash-equivalent, because selling a bar of precious metal requires waiting for the right price and is not as immediate as selling a blemished cow in an agricultural market. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak disagrees: {\i afilu nascha} \u8212- even a bar of metal is {\i mezuman} for this purpose, and the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} applies to it. What is excluded, on his view, is {\i sovi d\u8217'karei} \u8212- beams of lumber. Wood requires finding a buyer who is in the market to build a house and who must inspect the material; it is simply not liquid. The precise line between metal and lumber as measures of liquidity may be somewhat surprising \u8212- one might have thought precious metal, which sells by weight, would be at least as liquid as livestock \u8212- but such is the Gemara\u8217's assumption about the relative ease of these markets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Not Birds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shimi bar Ashi poses a sharp question to Rav Pappa: the {\i rabbanan} say the {\i halacha} was stated for {\i damim} \u8212- money \u8212- and not for {\i behemah}, not for {\i nascha}, not for {\i sovi}. Very well. Then it should equally be: {\i damim v\u8217'lo ofot} \u8212- money and not birds. What does he mean by birds?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Birds Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unspecified Birds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case Rav Shimi has in mind is not simply birds that someone happened to sanctify for a vague purpose. The classic {\i halacha} is that a {\i yoledes} \u8212- a woman who has given birth \u8212- is obligated to bring two birds, one as an {\i olah} and one as a {\i chatat}. She need not specify at the time she acquires the birds which is which; specification can happen either when the owner herself declares it at the time of purchase, or when the {\i kohen} designates the birds at the time he performs the service. This is a well-established {\i halacha} governing {\i kinnim} \u8212- pairs of birds brought for such obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now consider what happens if the woman acquires the two birds without specifying their identities and then dies before bringing them. She has two birds, one of which is a {\i chatat} and one an {\i olah}, but no one has yet declared which is which. One natural approach would be to bring the matter to the {\i kohen} and let the {\i kohen} make the designation \u8212- declaring one an {\i olah} and bringing it, and disposing of the other, since the {\i chatat} cannot be brought after the owner\u8217's death. But there is another possibility: could we treat those two unspecified birds like a pile of {\i ma\u8217'ot setumim}? In an unspecified pile of money containing both {\i chatat} funds and {\i olah} funds, the {\i halacha} is that the entire pile goes toward an {\i olah}. Why not apply the same {\i halacha} here and bring both birds as an {\i olah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shimi\u8217's challenge is essentially this: the question of whether the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} extends to birds is not academic. Either the {\i halacha} is limited to money \u8212- in which case it should not apply to birds, and bringing both birds as an {\i olah} would be incorrect \u8212- or the {\i halacha} is not so limited, in which case why was it ever restricted to money in the first place?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counter Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The counter-position is that the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} should not be artificially restricted. Whether one has an unspecified pile of money, unspecified animals, unspecified bars of metal, or unspecified birds \u8212- if the {\i kedusha} is genuinely undifferentiated and the owner has died, the property follows the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} and is directed toward a {\i nedava}, an {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Birds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda taught: {\i ein ha-kinnim mitparshot ella im be-likichat ba\u8217'alim o be-asiyat ha-kohen} \u8212- pairs of birds do not become specified except either at the time the owner purchases and designates them or at the time the {\i kohen} performs the service. The significance of Rav Chisda\u8217's formulation, as Tosafot explains, lies in his choice of the word {\i mitparshot}. He did not simply say that the birds\u8217' identity is not {\i nikva\u8217'im} \u8212- not established. He said they are not {\i mitparshot} \u8212- not explicated. {\i Mitparshot} is the precise opposite of {\i satum}. By framing it this way, Rav Chisda implicitly communicates that until one of those two events occurs \u8212- owner\u8217's designation at purchase or {\i kohen}\u8217's designation at service \u8212- the birds\u8217' halachic status is {\i satum}, unspecified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitparshot Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the birds have the status of {\i setumim} prior to designation, then when the owner dies before designation occurs, the normal {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} should apply: both birds go toward an {\i olah}, offered as a {\i nedava}. The inference from Rav Chisda\u8217's use of {\i mitparshot} is thus that birds do fall within the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} \u8212- which challenges the position that the {\i halacha} is limited strictly to money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birds Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specified Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds to Rav Shimi\u8217's challenge with a counter-question. If one wishes to say the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} applies equally to everything \u8212- money, animals, metal, birds, without distinction \u8212- then one must contend with what Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel teaches in the Mishnah: {\i heivi shalosh behemot v\u8217'lo peresh} \u8212- if someone brought three animals for his {\i korban nazir} without specifying which is which, and it happens that he brought precisely the three required animals (a female lamb, a male lamb, and a ram), then {\i harau\u8217'ya le-chatat tikarev chatat, le-olah tikarev olah, ve-li-shelamim tikarev shelamim} \u8212- each animal is assigned to its appropriate identity. The female goes as the {\i chatat}, the male lamb as the {\i olah}, the ram as the {\i shelamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The counter-position maintains that animals too should follow the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} and are not treated as specified. If so, how does the {\i kedusha} of this unspecified pile of three animals divide itself so neatly? The answer is that this is not at all a difficult case: these three animals, being the exact required animals, are self-identifying. They are not genuinely unspecified in any meaningful sense. Asking why they are not treated as {\i stumim} is like asking why a pile of money with a large sign reading \u8220"{\i chatat}\u8221" on one portion and \u8220"{\i olah}\u8221" on another should be treated as unspecified \u8212- the question answers itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What actually distinguishes the bird case and explains why birds can be {\i setumim} is that the Torah itself specifies the process by which birds receive their identity: {\i ve-lakach ve-asah} \u8212- the owner takes and the {\i kohen} does. The birds\u8217' identity is assigned at one of precisely two moments \u8212- acquisition or service \u8212- and until one of those moments arrives, the Torah itself leaves them undesignated. There is no analogous Torah-mandated process in the case of animals; an animal\u8217's identity as {\i chatat}, {\i olah}, or {\i shelamim} is determined at the moment of designation, and when the owner brings three distinct and self-identifying animals, the designation is obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point is further underscored by the observation that the {\i chatat} of a {\i nazir} is a female animal and the {\i olah} is a male animal \u8212- {\i hacha nekeva hacha zachar}. When you have a female sheep and a male sheep in front of you, it is simply not possible to speak of genuine non-specification. The {\i chatat} is the female; the {\i olah} is the male. There is nothing {\i satum} about it. By contrast, two birds of the same species brought for a {\i yoledes}, neither yet designated, are genuinely indistinguishable. That is why the Torah\u8217's process \u8212- designation at purchase or by the {\i kohen} \u8212- is both necessary and meaningful in the bird case, and not applicable in the case of clearly self-differentiating animals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mum Equivalence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamnuna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna raises a challenge against the position that an animal with a {\i mum} is treated like {\i setumah} \u8212- like unspecified funds. The argument in favor of that position was that a blemished animal, which bears no {\i kedusha} restriction and can be sold immediately, is {\i mezuman}, cash-equivalent, and therefore falls within the {\i halacha} of {\i setumim}. But one might question whether this is correct. After all, an animal with a {\i mum} is still an animal; it is not money. Even on the view that certain things are treated as money-equivalents, does a blemished animal really belong in that category? Rav Hamnuna brings a {\i braita} to probe this question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} \u8212- which is quoted here in abbreviated form, with the full version appearing at 30b \u8212- addresses the {\i halacha} of a son who shaves (i.e., brings the concluding {\i korbanot}) for his father\u8217's {\i nezirut}. The case is: the father was a {\i nazir}, set aside money for his {\i korbanot} ({\i hefrish maot le-neziruto}), and then died. Normally, when a {\i nazir} dies, unspecified money goes toward an {\i olah}. But if the son then declares: {\i hareini nazir al menat she-agalei\u8217'ach al maot Abba} \u8212- I will take upon myself {\i nezirut} on condition that I may use my father\u8217's money for my {\i korbanot} \u8212- and he makes this declaration after his father\u8217's death, then the money was set aside for the father\u8217's {\i nezirut}, the father has died, the money is available and not yet redirected, and the son becomes a {\i nazir} specifically to use those funds. In this case the son does get to use the father\u8217's money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Nezirim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case in the {\i braita} is where the son does not wait for his father to die. Both the father and the son are {\i nezirim} simultaneously. The father sets aside money for his own {\i nezirut} and then dies. The son then says he will use his father\u8217's money. Here he cannot, because when the father died with money set aside for his own {\i nezirut}, and the son was already a {\i nazir} at that time, the son is not entitled to redirect those funds. The money falls to {\i nedava} \u8212- it follows the normal {\i halacha} of unspecified funds and goes toward an {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specified Unspecified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i haitah lo behemah meforeshet} \u8212- if the father had animals that were already specified, {\i chatat tamut} \u8212- the {\i chatat} animal is left to die \u8212- {\i olah tikarev olah} \u8212- the {\i olah} animal is brought as an {\i olah} \u8212- {\i shelamim yiplu li-shelamim} \u8212- the {\i shelamim} animals are brought as {\i shelamim}. Unspecified money follows one rule; specified animals follow their own designations individually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mum Equivalence (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animals Not Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now draws an inference from this {\i braita} relevant to Rav Hamnuna\u8217's challenge. The {\i braita} contrasts unspecified money, which goes to {\i nedava}, with specified animals, which go to their respective purposes. The Gemara asks: {\i mai lav afilu ba\u8217'alat mum} \u8212- is the contrast not saying that even animals with a {\i mum} do not have the {\i halacha} of {\i setumah}? That is, even blemished animals are treated as specified (and thus go to their own designated purposes), not like unspecified money? This would undermine the position that a blemished animal is equivalent to {\i ma\u8217'ot setumot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara immediately notes that this inference is strained: the {\i braita} is explicitly speaking of {\i behemah meforeshet} \u8212- clearly specified animals. It is not making any statement at all about unspecified animals with blemishes. The contrast is between unspecified money and clearly specified animals, not between unspecified money and every possible type of animal. One might respond that the {\i braita} should have made its point more directly \u8212- if blemished animals are truly cash-equivalent, why not contrast unspecified money against unspecified blemished animals, which would be a more illuminating comparison?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dmei Hayinu Maot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is clean and decisive: {\i ba\u8217'alat mum le-mai kedishah? Li-dmei} \u8212- a blemished animal\u8217's {\i kedusha} is oriented toward what? Toward its monetary value. It is going to be sold; its {\i kedusha} lives in its sale price. {\i Demei hayinu ma\u8217'ot} \u8212- and proceeds are themselves money. When the {\i braita} speaks of {\i ma\u8217'ot setumot}, unspecified money, this already encompasses blemished animals, because a blemished animal\u8217's {\i kedusha} is money-in-waiting. There was never a category distinction to begin with; the {\i braita}\u8217's reference to money naturally includes anything whose {\i kedusha} is expressed through monetary proceeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution clarifies the overall shape of the {\i sugya}. The {\i halacha} of {\i setumim} \u8212- the rule that unspecified funds go toward an {\i olah} \u8212- applies to money and to anything functionally equivalent to money: things that are readily liquidable and whose {\i kedusha} is carried by their monetary value. A blemished animal fits squarely within that category. An unblemished animal that cannot be sold pending the appearance of a {\i mum} does not. And the specific {\i halacha} of birds is governed not by the money-equivalence analysis but by the Torah\u8217's own process of designation through {\i likichat ba\u8217'alim} or {\i asiyat ha-kohen}, which leaves the birds genuinely {\i setumim} until one of those moments arrives.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 28\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unspecified Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Pile of Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up in the middle of 27b, at the point where {\i masiv Rava} \u8212- Rava raises a challenge. We are finally wrapping up the {\i sugya} concerning the pile of unspecified funds that goes toward an {\i olah} in the case of a {\i nazir}. Recall the background: the three {\i korbanot} of the {\i nazir} \u8212- the {\i shelamim}, the {\i chatas}, and the {\i olah} \u8212- could in principle come from a single pile of unspecified funds, and the {\i halacha} directs such funds toward the {\i olah}. We had been working through a long discussion about whether that {\i halacha} is limited to money or whether it applies to other objects as well \u8212- things like beams of wood, bars of precious metal, or animals with a blemish. Even within the position that it applies only to money, we did not take \u8220"money\u8221" entirely literally. The argument was that anything capable of being quickly and easily liquidated should be treated as equivalent to money \u8212- including, perhaps, blemished animals, which on account of their {\i kedusha} would presumably find a ready market and would not require an extended valuation process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money vs.\u160 ?Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That is the backdrop for the current discussion. The question is whether blemished animals properly belong in the \u8220"money\u8221" category \u8212- that is, whether the {\i halacha} that unspecified funds go toward the {\i olah} would apply to them as well. If a person sanctified three blemished animals as non-specified funds for his {\i nezirut} and the {\i nezirut} was subsequently nullified, would those animals fall under this rule?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blemished Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider further: if one sanctified three cows \u8212- animals that cannot themselves be used as {\i korbanot} of a {\i nazir} \u8212- and then the person died or the {\i nezirut} was annulled, does the {\i halacha} that the unspecified {\i kedusha} devolves upon an {\i olah} apply? The answer would depend on whether those cows, blemished or otherwise inappropriate for sacrifice, are treated as equivalent to money or to specified sacrificial animals. This is the question one more attempt will try to resolve, before we proceed to the {\i mishna} on the following {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban - Personal Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a challenge from a {\i braita} that bears directly on this entire discussion. The {\i braita} draws on the {\i pasuk} describing the {\i chatas}: {\i nefesh ki techeta} \u8212- if a soul sins \u8212- {\i v\u8217'heivi korbano seirat izim al chatato asher chata} \u8212- he shall bring his {\i korban}, a she-goat, for the sin that he has sinned. The language of the verse is highly specific: it is his {\i korban}, for his sin. The {\i braita} proceeds to unpack that specificity at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bekorbano hu yotzei v\u8217'eino yotzei b\u8217'korban aviv} \u8212- a person fulfills his obligation with his own {\i korban}, not with his father\u8217's. This is the governing principle: a {\i chatas} is bound absolutely to the person who committed the transgression and to the specific sin for which it was designated. This principle, as we have been consistently noting, is why an animal sanctified as a {\i chatas} that cannot be used must be left to die \u8212- it cannot be redirected elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, of course, a striking counterpoint in the law of {\i nezirut}: if a father sets aside an unspecified pile of money for his {\i nezirut} and then dies, a son who subsequently accepts {\i nezirut} with the express intention of using that money may in fact do so. The money may thus be redirected from father to son. But that is a special application of the rule of unspecified funds \u8212- and, crucially, it is only possible when the funds are {\i stumim}, unspecified, not when they have been designated for a particular {\i korban}. That distinction is central to everything that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Chatas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} begins by positing the most apparently extreme case and then walking toward what might seem more lenient. Perhaps one cannot use his father\u8217's {\i korban} when the animal was set aside for a lighter category of sin and he wishes to apply it to a weightier category \u8212- {\i min hakal al hachamur} \u8212- or from a weightier to a lighter \u8212- {\i min hachamur al hakal}. In those cases the identity of the {\i korban} has been substantially altered, and clearly it cannot serve. But perhaps, the {\i braita} suggests, if one keeps it within the same category of sin \u8212- light for light, weighty for weighty \u8212- maybe using the father\u8217's {\i korban} for the son\u8217's equivalent obligation would be permissible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Talmud lomar: korbano} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} says \u8220"his,\u8221" explicitly and deliberately. It is his {\i korban}, not his father\u8217's \u8212- even if the category of sin is identical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas Specificity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then introduces the next layer: {\i yachol lo yetze b\u8217'korban aviv uvehemah she-hifrish afilu min hakal al hakal min hachamur al hachamur} \u8212- perhaps it is clear that one cannot use his father\u8217's sacrifice even if it is within the same category, when the father actually sanctified a specific animal. That we understand. But what about a case where the father only set aside money, not a designated animal?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The justification for this concern is drawn from the {\i halacha} of {\i nezirut} itself. {\i She-harei adam megalayach al maot aviv b\u8217'nezirot bizman she-hen stumim v\u8217'lo bizman she-hen meforashim} \u8212- a person may use his father\u8217's money for his own {\i nezirut}, but only when those funds are {\i stumim}, unspecified. Once the father has designated a particular pile for a particular {\i korban} \u8212- this pile for the {\i chatas}, this pile for the {\i olah} \u8212- the son may not reassign it. The capacity to reassign exists only when the funds remain amorphous and undirected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the {\i braita} presses the question: since we do observe at least some flexibility with money in the context of {\i nezirut} \u8212- a son can use a father\u8217's unspecified funds \u8212- perhaps that flexibility extends to {\i chatas} money as well. If the father set aside money specifically designated for his {\i chatas} and died, perhaps the son can use it for the son\u8217's own {\i chatas}, on the theory that money is inherently more reassignable than a designated animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Talmud lomar: korbano} \u8212- again the verse says \u8220"his.\u8221" Even money that the father sanctified for a {\i chatas} cannot be used by the son. The {\i halacha} of unspecified funds permitting a son to use his father\u8217's money for {\i nezirut} does not extend to {\i chatas}. A {\i chatas} must be specifically his \u8212- from the outset, not inherited or redirected from someone else \u8212- whether the sanctified object is an animal or a pile of money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Own Money Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} now advances to a further question: what about reassigning one\u8217's own {\i chatas} funds? We have established that a person cannot use his father\u8217's {\i chatas} {\i korban} \u8212- not an animal, not money. But what about using his own money, sanctified for one {\i chatas}, for a different {\i chatas}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yachol lo yetze b\u8217'behemah she-hifrish l\u8217'atzmo min hakal al hakal u-min hachamur al hachamur} \u8212- perhaps even for oneself, one cannot reassign an animal from one sin to another, even keeping it within the same category. And indeed the {\i braita} offers a demonstration of why not: if one set aside an animal for {\i chelev} and then slaughtered it with the intention of bringing it for {\i dam}, or vice versa \u8212- {\i sheharei lo ma\u8217'al v\u8217'lo kiper} \u8212- he has neither committed {\i me\u8217'ila} nor achieved atonement. He has not achieved atonement, because the animal cannot be reassigned from one sin to another; and because he has not succeeded in reassigning it, he has not technically misused {\i hekdesh} in the full sense. The attempted reassignment simply failed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that this argument is somewhat circular \u8212- it more assumes than proves the conclusion \u8212- but the {\i braita} takes it as established that an animal designated as a {\i chatas} for a specific sin cannot be reassigned even to a different sin of one\u8217's own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an interesting question: if I sanctified an animal for {\i chelev} and slaughtered it intending to bring it for {\i dam}, am I still {\i yotzei} for {\i chelev}? I have certainly not fulfilled my obligation for {\i dam}. But the animal was a {\i chatas} \u8212- just brought {\i shelo lishma}, for the wrong sin. The question of whether it might still satisfy the original designation is genuinely interesting, though we set it aside here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i aval yotzei b\u8217'maot} \u8212- perhaps money, though, is more freely reassignable. If the father set aside money for {\i chelev} and the son wishes to use it for {\i dam}, or if a person set aside his own money for one {\i chatas} and wishes to redirect it to another \u8212- perhaps that works. The logic would be: {\i sheken im hifrish l\u8217'atzmo ma\u8217'ot min hachelev v\u8217'heivi al hadam\u8230 ? sheharei ma\u8217'al v\u8217'kiper} \u8212- because with money, if one were to redirect his {\i chatas} funds from one sin to another, he would actually have committed {\i me\u8217'ila} and achieved atonement. Unlike the animal case, where the reassignment simply fails, with money something seems to happen: an act of {\i me\u8217'ila} has occurred, and its effect may be to discharge the {\i kedusha} and allow the funds to be reapplied. The {\i talmud lomar: al chatato} \u8212- the verse says \u8220"for his sin,\u8221" meaning {\i ad she-yehei korbano l\u8217'shum chatato} \u8212- his {\i korban} must be for the specific sin. Even money designated for one {\i chatas} cannot be redirected to another. The verse leaves no opening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money & Me\u8217'ila\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, {\i Tosafot} provides an illuminating explanation of the logic underlying the {\i braita}\u8217's argument about money and {\i me\u8217'ila}. {\i Tosafot} notes the correct textual reading: {\i she\u8217'ein mo\u8217'al v\u8217'kiper she\u8217'hame\u8217'ot ein bahem kedushat haguf} \u8212- the money does not possess {\i kedushat haguf}, intrinsic sanctity. This distinction is essential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two levels of {\i kedusha} for objects dedicated to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. {\i Kedushat haguf} refers to an object that is itself intrinsically sanctified \u8212- an animal designated as an {\i olah}, for instance, whose very body is consecrated for use. {\i Kedushat damim} refers to an object whose sanctity consists only in its monetary value \u8212- it is destined to be sold, with the proceeds used to acquire whatever is actually needed. Money set aside for a {\i korban} is {\i kedushat damim}: the money itself is not going to be burned on the altar; it will be used to purchase an animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction bears directly on the laws of {\i me\u8217'ila}. When one misuses {\i hekdesh} in error \u8212- {\i be\u8217'shogeg} \u8212- the effect of that {\i me\u8217'ila} is to transfer the object out of {\i hekdesh}. But this desanctification operates differently for {\i kedushat haguf} versus {\i kedushat damim}. An object with {\i kedushat haguf}, even if misused, retains its intrinsic sanctity \u8212- one violation does not strip an {\i olah} of its identity as an {\i olah}. But an object with only {\i kedushat damim} is more loosely bound to the domain of {\i hekdesh}; it is essentially property of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} waiting to be liquidated. If such an object is misused {\i be\u8217'shogeg}, the act of {\i me\u8217'ila} effectively removes it from {\i hekdesh}, much as an act of theft removes something from its owner\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two conditions, then, govern whether {\i me\u8217'ila} results in desanctification: the misuse must be {\i be\u8217'shogeg} \u8212- not intentional \u8212- and the object must have {\i kedushat damim}, not {\i kedushat haguf}. Money designated for a {\i chatas} satisfies both: it has only {\i kedushat damim}, and if misused accidentally it would constitute {\i me\u8217'ila} that strips the {\i kedusha}. {\i Tosafot} articulates the inference: {\i dehazi lemifak l\u8217'chulin al yedei me\u8217'ila} \u8212- the money is susceptible to losing its {\i kedusha} through {\i me\u8217'ila}, which demonstrates that its {\i kedusha} is less permanently affixed to its identity. One might therefore argue that the {\i kedusha} is more fungible, and that a conscious reassignment to a different {\i chatas} should similarly be effective \u8212- since {\i be\u8217'shogeg} the act of reassignment would have worked, perhaps {\i be\u8217'mezid} it can work as well: {\i ho\u8217'il v\u8217'shayeich bo shinui b\u8217'shogeg}. This is the {\i braita}\u8217's argument for why money {\i should} be reassignable to a different {\i chatas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i talmud lomar: al chatato} closes the door even on this reasoning. Whatever the theoretical flexibility of money\u8217's {\i kedusha}, the verse insists that the {\i korban} must be designated for this specific sin. There is no room to reassign \u8212- not from father to son, not from one sin to another, whether the sanctified object is an animal or money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the parallel this discussion bears to the concept of {\i shinui} in the laws of {\i geneiva}. When a thief effects a qualitative change \u8212- {\i shinui ma\u8217'aseh} or {\i shinui haguf} \u8212- in a stolen object, this can vest ownership in the thief under certain conditions. Here, the very act of {\i me\u8217'ila be\u8217'shogeg} functions as a kind of {\i shinui reshut}, a change of domain, that removes the object from {\i hekdesh}. There are {\i gemarot} that draw precisely this parallel between {\i ye\u8217'ush} and {\i shinui reshut} in the laws of theft and certain cases of {\i me\u8217'ila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One should also note that this {\i braita} is stylistically unmistakably {\i midrash halacha} \u8212- it moves verse by verse through the {\i pasuk}, deriving each successive rule from a subtle textual reading. While the {\i Gemara} does not signal whether a given {\i braita} derives from a {\i tosefta} or a {\i midrash halacha}, the sustained focus on the {\i pesukim} makes the origin here transparent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Relevance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder what the relevance of this lengthy {\i braita} is to our original question about blemished animals and unspecified funds. The connection lies in one of the {\i braita}\u8217's early moves: when considering whether money might be reassignable, the {\i braita} notes that a person can use his father\u8217's money for his own {\i nezirut} \u8212- but only when those funds are {\i stumim}, unspecified, {\i v\u8217'lo bizman she-hen meforashim}. The {\i braita} drew a contrast between money and animals: even in {\i nezirut}, a son cannot use his father\u8217's sanctified animal. It is only an unspecified pile of money that carries this reassignability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That contrast \u8212- between money and animals in the context of what a son may use for his own {\i nezirut} \u8212- is what our {\i sugya} wants to analyze. The question is: when the {\i braita} says a son cannot use his father\u8217's animal, does {\i behemah} mean only the specifically designated unblemished sacrificial animals? Or does it also include blemished animals that cannot themselves be offered? The answer to that question determines whether blemished animals fall in the \u8220"money\u8221" category or the \u8220"animal\u8221" category for purposes of the {\i halacha} of unspecified funds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Objects = Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blemished Animals?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now draws on that line in the {\i braita} directly. {\i Katani miha} \u8212- at least the {\i braita} teaches: a son cannot reuse his father\u8217's animal for {\i nezirut}, but he can reuse his father\u8217's money. When the {\i braita} says \u8220"animal,\u8221" does it mean {\i afilu ba\u8217'alet mum} \u8212- even a blemished animal? If so, then even blemished animals are not in the money category and cannot be reassigned \u8212- which would mean they do not fall under the {\i halacha} of unspecified funds going toward the {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rejects this reading: {\i lo temimah} \u8212- no. When the {\i braita} said \u8220"animal\u8221" in the context of things a son cannot reassign for his own {\i nezirut}, it was referring to unblemished animals, the properly designated sacrificial animals. Those are the ones whose identity is so concretely fixed that they cannot be reassigned even in {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All = Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presses further: {\i aval ba\u8217'alet mum mai?} \u8212- but what do you want to say about blemished animals? Would you say {\i kistuma damya} \u8212- that they are treated like an unspecified pile of money and may be reassigned? If so, then why does the {\i braita} mention only money \u8212- {\i maot she-hifrish aviv} \u8212- as the category of things a son may use? Why not also mention blemished animals, or beams of wood, or any other non-sacrificial object?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is straightforward: {\i hacha nami} \u8212- those other objects are equally included. {\i D\u8217'lamai khazya? Lidmai} \u8212- for what are they fit? For their monetary value, their proceeds. Anything that is not itself going to be used as the sacrifice \u8212- whether it is cash, a blemished animal, a beam of wood, or any other object \u8212- is fit only to be sold, with the proceeds going toward the ultimate purpose. In that sense, everything in this category is equivalent to money. {\i Demei hani maos} \u8212- it is all in the same category as the pile of money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's method here is instructive. The difficulty throughout this long {\i sugya} arose because the {\i mishnayot} and {\i braitot} dealt with two poles \u8212- an amorphous pile of money on one side, and a concretely specified sanctified animal on the other \u8212- leaving ambiguous everything that falls between. The {\i Gemara} now resolves that ambiguity definitively: the critical distinction is not between money and animals in the abstract, but between objects that will themselves be used as {\i korbanot} and everything else. Everything else \u8212- regardless of its form \u8212- is treated as money, because its {\i kedusha} is {\i kedushat damim}, value-based rather than identity-based. The mention of money in the {\i braita} was representative, not exhaustive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that, the {\i Gemara} brings this extended {\i sugya} to a close, and we turn to a new {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Annulment at Sacrifice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Sprinkled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishna} of this section addresses the annulment of a woman\u8217's {\i nezirut} by her husband at an extremely inopportune moment \u8212- not merely after she has set aside funds or animals, but while the {\i korbanot} are actually being brought. The husband has been away; he returns, finds his wife concluding her {\i nezirut} at the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, and seeks to annul her vow on the spot. The day of his return is the {\i yom shmoa} \u8212- the day he first learned of the vow \u8212- and so according to the laws of annulment, this is in principle the day he may act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nizrak aleha echad min hadamim eino yachol lehafer} \u8212- if the blood of one of the sacrifices has already been sprinkled on her behalf, the husband can no longer annul. The reason is not procedural inconvenience. After even one {\i korban} has been completed, the restrictions of {\i nezirut} fall away from her. She may immediately drink wine. Since the husband\u8217's power to annul is grounded in the {\i nazir} vow\u8217's character as {\i inuy nefesh} \u8212- an affliction of the soul, a category encompassing vows that restrict the woman\u8217's conduct or impact their relationship \u8212- once the restrictions are lifted, that footing is gone. He no longer has any basis on which to annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva: Slaughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva omer afilu nishchata aleha achas mikol habehemah eino yachol lehafer} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva holds that he cannot annul even earlier, immediately after one of the animals has been slaughtered but before the blood has been sprinkled. At this moment, the woman is technically still bound by the restrictions of {\i nezirut} \u8212- the {\i korban} is not yet complete \u8212- and therefore the {\i Tanna Kamma} would permit the husband to annul. Rabbi Akiva disagrees.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is {\i hefsed kodashim}. If the husband annuls after the animal has been slaughtered but before the blood is sprinkled, the {\i korban} \u8212- specifically, as the {\i Gemara} will clarify, the {\i chatas} \u8212- cannot be completed, since the woman is no longer a {\i nezirah}. The blood cannot be sprinkled for a {\i chatas} that no longer belongs to an obligated {\i nazir}, and the entire animal must be destroyed. This represents a devastating waste of a sacred object. Rabbi Akiva holds that the trivial benefit of escaping one final minute of the wine restriction does not justify this destruction. What would she even do \u8212- drink wine in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}? The disproportion between gain and loss is so stark that Rabbi Akiva forbids the annulment at this stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a fascinating question: if the husband ignores this ruling and annuls anyway, does the annulment take effect? The question is whether {\i yesh koach beyad chachamim la\u8217'akor davar min haTorah} \u8212- whether the Sages can void what would otherwise be a biblically valid annulment on policy grounds. {\i Tosafot} leaves this unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Annul Ends\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The positions of the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Akiva thus represent different thresholds. The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that once the blood of even one {\i korban} has been sprinkled, the husband can no longer annul \u8212- since the restrictions have lapsed. Rabbi Akiva extends the prohibition one step earlier: even after slaughter, before sprinkling, if annulling would destroy the {\i chatas}, he cannot annul. Both positions turn on the interplay between the husband\u8217's residual standing to annul and the cost \u8212- in wasted {\i kodashim} \u8212- of exercising that standing at this late stage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tamei & Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} draws a distinction between {\i tiglachat tahara} \u8212- the normal shaving that concludes {\i nezirut} after a complete, uninterrupted term \u8212- and {\i tiglachat tumah}, the shaving that follows after the {\i nezirah} has become ritually impure. In the {\i tumah} case, the woman must begin her {\i nezirut} anew \u8212- an additional full month stretches ahead of her. In such a case, {\i yafeh} \u8212- even if the {\i chatas} from the {\i tumah} process has already been slaughtered and the blood has not yet been sprinkled, or even after the blood has been sprinkled, the {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that the husband can still annul. Why? {\i Shehu yachol lomar: ee efshi b\u8217'isha menuvelat} \u8212- because he can say, I do not want a wife whose appearance is disorderly. But more substantively, since there is a full month of {\i nezirut} still ahead of her, the {\i nezirut} is far from over. The reason annulment was barred in the {\i tahara} case was that after one {\i korban} the restrictions were effectively finished. Here, with a full month remaining, there is ample basis for the husband to annul, regardless of what stage the {\i korbanot} have reached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir: Final Stage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir omer: af betiglachat tahara yafer mipnei she-yachol lomar ee efshi b\u8217'isha megulachat} \u8212- Rabbi Meir holds that even in a normal {\i tahara} process, even after all three animals have been brought and only the final shaving remains, the husband may annul. His justification: he can declare that he does not want his wife to shave her head. Even at the final moment, that declared preference constitutes sufficient grounds for annulment, and the {\i halacha} entitles him to prevent that last stage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatas & Shelo Lishma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Olah/Shelamim OK\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira challenges the ruling of Rabbi Akiva. Rabbi Akiva had held that after the animal is slaughtered but before the blood is sprinkled, the husband cannot annul because the {\i chatas} would be destroyed. But Rabbi Zeira asks: {\i v\u8217'amai? V\u8217'lizrok daman shelo lishman v\u8217'yishtarei b\u8217'chamra} \u8212- why is there any problem at all? Even after the annulment, simply sprinkle the blood \u8212- it will be brought {\i shelo lishma}, for an unintended purpose, since the woman is no longer a {\i nezirah}, but an {\i olah} or {\i shelamim} brought {\i shelo lishma} is still {\i kasher}. The meat will be permissible; the {\i korban} is not wasted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelo Lishma Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} supports this from a {\i braita} concerning {\i kivsei atzeret} \u8212- the lambs brought on {\i Shavuot} in conjunction with the {\i shtei halechem}. If those lambs were slaughtered {\i shelo lishman}, or before or after {\i Shavuot}, the ruling is: {\i hadam yizarek v\u8217'basar ye\u8217'achel} \u8212- sprinkle the blood and eat the meat. Even brought at the wrong time or for the wrong purpose, they remain valid. If it fell on {\i Shabbat}, the sprinkling should not be done {\i ab initio} \u8212- since the wrong purpose removes the basis for overriding {\i Shabbat} \u8212- but if done, it is valid, and the {\i imurin} are burned that evening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita}\u8217's relevance is that it demonstrates the general principle: {\i korbanot} other than a {\i chatas}, brought {\i shelo lishma}, remain valid. The case of {\i kivsei atzeret} is particularly instructive because it also encompasses situations where the intent may be correct but the context is wrong \u8212- bringing at the wrong time, for instance, is itself a form of {\i shelo lishma}. The annulment case is analogous: the person slaughtering may intend correctly, but by virtue of the woman\u8217's no longer being a {\i nezirah}, the {\i korban} is by definition not being brought for its intended purpose. Nevertheless, it remains valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus, if we are dealing with an {\i olah} or {\i shelamim}, Rabbi Akiva would have no objection to annulling \u8212- simply complete the {\i korban} as a voluntary offering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas Unique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval hacha b\u8217'mai askinan? K\u8217'gon she-shachat chatas b\u8217'reisha} \u8212- the case Rabbi Akiva is addressing is specifically where the {\i chatas} was slaughtered first. The {\i halacha} is {\i gilach al achat mishaloshan yatza} \u8212- shaving over any one of the three {\i korbanot} concludes the {\i nezirut}, so the {\i chatas} need not be first in order \u8212- but Rabbi Akiva\u8217's ruling applies to the scenario where the {\i chatas} is the first animal slaughtered. A {\i chatas} brought {\i shelo lishma} is invalid \u8212- unlike an {\i olah} or {\i shelamim}, a {\i chatas} for the wrong person or wrong purpose does not merely fail to satisfy its obligation; it is an entirely invalid {\i korban} and must be destroyed. Therefore, once the {\i chatas} has been slaughtered, annulling the {\i nezirut} before the blood is sprinkled would leave an unusable carcass. There is no option of simply completing it as a valid offering. This is why Rabbi Akiva says: we will not allow the annulment, even though technically the woman is still bound by the restrictions for one more moment \u8212- because the cost, the total destruction of the {\i chatas}, is wildly disproportionate to the benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the first animal slaughtered had been the {\i olah} or the {\i shelamim}, Rabbi Akiva would agree with the {\i Tanna Kamma}: the husband may annul, since those animals can still be validly offered even after the annulment, {\i shelo lishma}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tamei & Shaving (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wig Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We return to complete the {\i mishna}. The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that in a normal {\i tahara} conclusion, once the blood of one {\i korban} has been sprinkled, the husband can no longer annul. Rabbi Meir holds that he may annul even at the final stage \u8212- even after all three {\i korbanot} have been brought \u8212- to prevent her from shaving, declaring {\i ee efshi b\u8217'isha megulachat}: I do not want a bald wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna Kamma} responds: {\i efshar b\u8217'pe\u8217'ah nachris} \u8212- she can wear a wig. The husband\u8217's complaint that he does not want a shaved-headed wife is not a compelling objection if the condition is remediable through a wig. Since she can cover the shaved head, the claimed hardship is not real, and the husband has no standing to annul merely to prevent the shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir, however, is not persuaded: {\i depei nachris idi d\u8217'zuama lo nicha lei} \u8212- a wig, being foreign hair, has an unfamiliar smell. It does not smell like her own hair. The husband may legitimately object to that difference, and therefore his stated preference not to have her undergo the shaving remains a genuine and valid basis for annulment, even at this final stage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 29\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Making Son Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Limits Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with the suggestion that the Amoraic debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish \u8212- concerning whether a father\u8217's power to impose {\i nezirut} on his son derives from a special {\i halacha} or from the rabbinic concept of {\i chinuch} \u8212- may be rooted in an earlier Tannaitic dispute. The Gemara introduces a {\i baraita} with the question: {\i ad matai madzir beno benazir} \u8212- until what age can a father make his son a {\i nazir}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Halacha View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi answers: {\i ad sheyavi shtei se\u8217'arot} \u8212- until the son brings two pubic hairs, i.e., until the son reaches full halachic adulthood at thirteen. In Rabbi\u8217's view, the father retains the power to impose {\i nezirut} on his son as long as the son remains a minor in the complete sense. Even if the son may have already acquired a certain degree of independence in the realm of vows, the son is still fundamentally under the father\u8217's jurisdiction as long as he has not reached full maturity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose Chinuch View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda takes a different position: {\i ad sheyagia le\u8217'onat nedarim} \u8212- until the son arrives at the age of vow-making. This refers to the year preceding {\i bar mitzvah}, during which a child who sufficiently understands the meaning and gravity of a vow may make one that is halachically binding. This status is known as {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} \u8212- literally, one who can enunciate vows and is close to adulthood. The capacity to make independent vows, according to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, removes the son from his father\u8217's sphere of authority in this domain. The underlying rationale is that {\i chinuch} \u8212- the father\u8217's obligation to train his son in {\i mitzvot} \u8212- is the conceptual basis for the father\u8217's power here. Once the son can himself function as an agent in the area of {\i nedarim}, the justification for {\i chinuch} falls away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pauses before concluding that this Tannaitic debate straightforwardly maps onto the Amoraic one. The positions could align, but it is not self-evident that they must. The debate between thirteen and twelve does not automatically imply a debate about {\i halacha} versus {\i chinuch}, because each of those two conceptual positions could potentially lead to either age cutoff. The Gemara therefore explores two distinct approaches to reading the Tannaitic dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first approach posits that {\i dechulei alma halacha hi benader} \u8212- both Rabbi and Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda agree that the father\u8217's power to impose {\i nezirut} on his son is a {\i halacha}, not a rabbinic device of {\i chinuch}. The point of contention is a different one: {\i vehacha bemufla samuch le\u8217'ish kamiflagi} \u8212- they disagree about the legal status of {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi holds that {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish d\u8217'rabanan} \u8212- the son\u8217's independent vow-making capacity in the year before {\i bar mitzvah} is only a rabbinic institution. Since the father\u8217's power derives from a {\i halacha} \u8212- and this Gemara clearly assumes that {\i halacha} here means {\i d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'ata de\u8217'oraita dachi d\u8217'rabanan}: a biblical rule overrides a rabbinic one. Therefore, the father retains the power to impose {\i nezirut} on his son even during the year of {\i onat nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, holds that {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- the son\u8217's independent capacity for vows during that year is a biblical status. And since {\i nezirut} is itself a form of vow, even a {\i halacha}-based power in the father cannot override the son\u8217's biblically recognized independence in that very domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha L\u8217'Moshe Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important question about the nature of {\i halacha} itself. The Gemara never actually uses the phrase {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} \u8212- it says only {\i halacha}. The assumption that {\i halacha} means {\i d\u8217'oraita} is operative in this sugya, but deserves scrutiny. There are cases where {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} is used rhetorically or metaphorically rather than literally \u8212- a notable example appears in a {\i mishnah} in Eduyot, which uses the phrase {\i halacha} to describe what is clearly a rabbinic obligation regarding {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} in lands outside of Israel. Scholars have traced how the concept evolved across the Tannaitic and Amoraic periods, and the phrase does not invariably signal {\i d\u8217'oraita} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second consideration: there is a view, associated with the Netziv, that the word {\i halacha} in certain contexts \u8212- particularly as Rashi uses it \u8212- may mean simply an ancient tradition, without carrying the full force of {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}. These caveats notwithstanding, the Gemara\u8217's own logic in this passage clearly treats {\i halacha} as {\i d\u8217'oraita}. The critical point is that the {\i halacha} here is not an entirely independent law but rather a detail within the existing {\i d\u8217'oraita} framework of {\i nezirut}. When a {\i halacha} supplies a parameter internal to a {\i d\u8217'oraita} category, it is naturally folded into that category\u8217's biblical standing. That is presumably why {\i halacha} here is treated as carrying {\i d\u8217'oraita} weight: it tells us something about how the {\i d\u8217'oraita} laws of {\i nezirut} operate, not about some independent, externally-derived rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chinuch Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second approach argues in precisely the opposite direction: {\i dechulei alma k\u8217'dei l\u8217'chancho b\u8217'mitzvot} \u8212- everyone agrees the father\u8217's power is based on {\i chinuch}, and the entire institution is rabbinic. Here too, {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} is {\i d\u8217'rabanan}. The question becomes which of two competing rabbinic institutions prevails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi: {\i asi chinuch d\u8217'rabanan v\u8217'dachi mufla samuch l\u8217'ish d\u8217'rabanan} \u8212- the father\u8217's rabbinic obligation of {\i chinuch} overrides the son\u8217's rabbinic independence in vow-making. The father\u8217's role as trainer retains priority; the son\u8217's ability to make his own vows does not terminate the father\u8217's {\i chinuch} jurisdiction in this area. This is striking given that this framework already requires overriding numerous other halachic principles \u8212- {\i chullin b\u8217'azarah}, considerations of {\i shechitah}, and so forth. Adding one more override is consistent with the broader latitude the rabbis extended to the father\u8217's {\i chinuch} role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, once the rabbis established that a son of twelve has recognized independence in the realm of {\i nedarim}, that recognition itself removes him from the category of one requiring {\i chinuch} in this domain. The rabbis\u8217' acknowledgment of the son\u8217's capacity is not merely an incidental permission to make vows; it is a statement that the son is no longer a subject of the father\u8217's {\i chinuch} in this particular area. Therefore, {\i lo asi chinuch d\u8217'rabanan v\u8217'dachi mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} \u8212- the father\u8217's {\i chinuch} authority cannot override that recognized independence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Chanina Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanina Story Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the theoretical Tannaitic background, the Gemara now transitions to a concrete narrative. A {\i baraita} records: {\i ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'Rebbi Chanina she\u8217'hadiro aviv b\u8217'nazir} \u8212- there was an incident involving Rabbi Chanina whose father had made him a {\i nazir}. When the {\i nezirut} was completed, he was brought before Rabban Gamliel. {\i V\u8217'haya Rabban Gamliel bodek bo leida im hevi shtei se\u8217'arot im lav hevi} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel examined him to determine whether he had brought two pubic hairs. The question was whether the father had validly imposed {\i nezirut} on the son, or whether the son had already been an adult at the time \u8212- in which case the father\u8217's act would have had no effect. Rabbi Yose, for his part, holds that what Rabban Gamliel was checking was not {\i shtei se\u8217'arot} but whether the boy {\i higia l\u8217'onat nedarim} \u8212- had reached the age of vow-making \u8212- meaning that the decisive question was whether the child was twelve and sufficiently understood the nature of vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanina\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before Rabban Gamliel could complete his inquiry, Chanina himself spoke up: {\i Rabbi, al tista\u8217'er livdok kani} \u8212- do not trouble yourself to examine me. {\i Im katan ani, eyei bishvil abba} \u8212- if I was a minor when my father imposed {\i nezirut} on me, then I am a {\i nazir} by virtue of my father\u8217's act. {\i Im gadol ani, eyei bishvil atzmi} \u8212- and if I was an adult, I hereby accept {\i nezirut} upon myself. Either way, I am a {\i nazir}. There is no need for the examination because the outcome is the same regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel\u8217's Praise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel was moved by this response. {\i Amar Rabban Gamliel venashko al rosho} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel kissed him on the head and declared: {\i muvtach ani be-zeh she-moreh halacha be-Yisrael} \u8212- I am certain this young man will grow up to render halachic decisions in Israel. The {\i baraita} records that it was not long before this prediction was fulfilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Adulthood Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presses harder. For Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- who holds that the cutoff is {\i onat nedarim} \u8212- the case is relatively straightforward. If the boy had reached the age of vow-making, the father could not have imposed {\i nezirut}; if he had not, the father\u8217's imposition stands. Either way, Chanina\u8217's statement works: if he was still within the father\u8217's authority, the father\u8217's {\i nezirut} holds; if he was independent enough to make vows himself, he can accept {\i nezirut} on himself at that point. The analysis proceeds fluently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's challenge is directed primarily at Rabbi, who holds the threshold is {\i shtei se\u8217'arot} \u8212- full adulthood. There are three possible scenarios for when Chanina became an adult: before the father imposed {\i nezirut}, after the completion of the thirty-day period, or somewhere in the middle. The first two cases are manageable. If he was already an adult when the father acted, his silent acquiescence \u8212- or perhaps his verbal acceptance \u8212- makes him a {\i nazir} on his own terms. If he only became an adult after the thirty days were complete, the father\u8217's {\i nezirut} runs its course uninterrupted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem is the third scenario. It emerges \u8212- explicitly in the {\i Tosefta}, and implicitly from the Gemara\u8217's reasoning \u8212- that if a son becomes a full adult {\i in the middle} of the thirty-day period his father imposed, that transition of legal status terminates the father\u8217's {\i nezirut} even without any active protest. The son\u8217's transition to independent legal standing simply dissolves the earlier imposition. If so, Chanina\u8217's statement \u8212- \u8220"either way I am a {\i nazir}\u8221" \u8212- is not accurate for every scenario. If Chanina became an adult on, say, day ten, the period that preceded his adulthood would be invalidated, and he could not simply count backward from the start and claim he completed a full {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult Status Interrupts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara probes the asymmetry between {\i onat nedarim} and full adulthood more carefully. If it is the age of vow-making that matters, the fact that a boy arrives at {\i onat nedarim} mid-{\i nezirut} does not terminate the father\u8217's imposition. The father was entitled to impose the {\i nezirut} at the outset; the son\u8217's newly acquired capacity to make his own vows does not retroactively disrupt an already-operative process. The son\u8217's basic legal status has not fundamentally changed. He is still a minor in the general sense; he has merely acquired a limited, specific independence in the realm of vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Full adulthood, however \u8212- marked by {\i shtei se\u8217'arot} \u8212- is categorically different. Becoming a {\i bar mitzvah} represents a total transformation of legal status. It is not a narrow expansion of one competency; it is the complete emergence of the person as an independent legal subject. Therefore, according to Rabbi, who holds that the threshold is {\i shtei se\u8217'arot}, adulthood reached in the middle of the thirty-day period would genuinely rupture the father\u8217's {\i nezirut}. This is what makes Chanina\u8217's statement potentially problematic under Rabbi\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Acceptance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution involves a careful rereading of what Chanina actually said. Chanina\u8217's statement was not simply binary. It can be understood as an acceptance of {\i nezirut} from that very moment forward, regardless of when he became an adult. The question becomes whether this simultaneous declaration can cover all scenarios. For the scenario where Chanina became an adult precisely at the end of thirty days, the father\u8217's {\i nezirut} is intact and complete. For the scenario where he became an adult at the very beginning, his own verbal acceptance covered the {\i nezirut} from the start. The difficult case \u8212- adulthood in the middle \u8212- requires that Chanina\u8217's declaration on that day be treated as an independent acceptance of {\i nezirut} effective from that moment, with a full thirty days running from that point. By accepting {\i nezirut} upon himself regardless of circumstances, Chanina ensured that {\i mimah nafshach} \u8212- in any event \u8212- he would emerge as a valid {\i nazir} who completed a full thirty-day period: either his father\u8217's from the outset, or his own from whatever point adulthood arrived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation about the difference between what terminates paternal {\i nezirut} and what merely precluded the father from imposing it in the first place is worth underscoring. Even Rabbi, who holds that the father retains authority until {\i shtei se\u8217'arot}, concedes that once {\i shtei se\u8217'arot} arrive, the father\u8217's {\i nezirut} ends \u8212- even mid-process and even without any active objection from the son. It is a structural termination, not a volitional one. The son\u8217's ability to object, mentioned in an earlier {\i mishnah}, operates on a different axis: even as a minor, a son can terminate the father\u8217's imposition through protest. That is a separate mechanism. The present case addresses the automatic legal effect of reaching adulthood itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Using Father\u8217's Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} introduces a distinct but related topic: the ability of a son to make use of funds his father had set aside for his own {\i nazir} sacrifices. The rule stated is that a son may use such funds, but a daughter may not: {\i ha\u8217'ish megalei\u8217'ach al nezirut aviv ve\u8217'ein ha\u8217'ishah megalei\u8217'achat al nezirut aviha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Death Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents one configuration for this law: {\i mi shehaya aviv nazir vehifrish ma\u8217'ot stumim al neziruto umet} \u8212- a father who was a {\i nazir} and set aside undesignated funds for his {\i nezirut} and then died. Under normal circumstances, unspecified funds dedicated for {\i nezirut} go to an {\i olah}. But if the son thereafter declares: {\i hareini nazir al menat she\u8217'agalei\u8217'ach al me\u8217'ot abba} \u8212- I become a {\i nazir} on the condition that I may use my father\u8217's set-aside funds \u8212- the {\i mishnah} addresses whether this stipulation is effective. The {\i chiddush} is that funds which would otherwise be channeled to a communal {\i olah} can instead be redirected to the son\u8217's own {\i korbanot}, provided the son explicitly stipulates this at the time he accepts {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Nazirs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second configuration involves a case where father and son are {\i nezirim} simultaneously. The father sets aside funds for his own {\i nezirut} and then dies mid-process. May the son use those funds for his own {\i korbanot}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The text of the {\i mishnah} and an accompanying {\i baraita} present the opinions of Rabbi Yosi and others, and there is a notable textual difficulty: {\i Tosafot} cites a {\i girsa} of the {\i mishnah} that is the exact reverse of what appears in the standard printed text. In the {\i girsa} consistent with the {\i baraita} that the Gemara quotes, Rabbi Yosi holds that the son may use the funds only if he accepted {\i nezirut} after the father\u8217's death, stipulating at that moment that he would use the father\u8217's funds. If the son and father were {\i nezirim} simultaneously, Rabbi Yosi holds {\i harei elu yiplu li-nedavah} \u8212- those funds go to a communal contribution and may not be used by the son. Rabbi Eliezer, Rabbi Meir, and Rabbi Yehuda appear to hold the opposite on the simultaneous case \u8212- that when son and father are {\i nezirim} at the same time, the son may use the father\u8217's funds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The intuition behind the simultaneous case is suggestive: when father and son undertake {\i nezirut} together, it has the character of a shared family enterprise. The funds the father set aside are not entirely his alone in some moral sense; they belong to a joint endeavor. Accordingly, when the father dies, the funds pass naturally to the son who is continuing the same communal {\i nezirut}. In the after-death case, by contrast, the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} is an entirely independent undertaking \u8212- he has no special claim to the father\u8217's funds without an explicit stipulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask what halachic basis underlies any of this. The Gemara poses precisely this question \u8212- {\i mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- and Rabbi Yochanan responds simply: {\i halacha b\u8217'nazir hi}. There is no derivable source, no {\i pasuk}, no extension of {\i chinuch}. It is, as Rabbi Yochanan characteristically states in this tractate, simply a received tradition pertaining to {\i nezirut}. Reish Lakish, who elsewhere offers alternative reasoning, is silent here \u8212- presumably because there is no alternative to offer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son-Only Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: is it not obvious that only a son and not a daughter is entitled to use the funds, given that a son inherits from his father and a daughter does not? The Gemara answers that this is not at all obvious. Consider a case where the deceased father left only a daughter \u8212- {\i de-leit lei ella barta} \u8212- and she stands to inherit all his other property. One might argue that since we learn the son\u8217's entitlement from the concept of {\i yerusha}, and she inherits just as he does, she should also be entitled to use the funds. The Gemara therefore must address this: {\i mahu de-teima yorshin gamrinan lah} \u8212- lest one say we derive her entitlement from the laws of inheritance. To this, Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's answer stands \u8212- {\i halacha b\u8217'nazir hi}, and the {\i halacha} specifies sons, not daughters. Even a daughter who would inherit all the father\u8217's ordinary property cannot use the dedicated {\i nazir} funds for her own {\i korbanot}. The {\i halacha} carries its own internal definition of who qualifies, independent of the general laws of {\i yerusha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point illuminates a broader feature of {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} in this context. Not only does the {\i halacha} establish the basic rule that a son may use the father\u8217's funds \u8212- it also defines the precise contours of that rule: only sons, not daughters, even in a case where the daughter would be the sole heir. Without that component of the {\i halacha}, one would be tempted to extend the rule through reasoning from {\i yerusha}. The {\i halacha} forecloses that extension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks whether the {\i rabanan} who disagree with Rabbi Yosi dispute his ruling at the first point \u8212- the case where the son accepts {\i nezirut} after the father died \u8212- or at the second point, the simultaneous case, or both. The {\i baraita} quoted in response makes Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position explicit: the son may use the funds when he stipulates this after the father\u8217's death, but not when they were {\i nezirim} simultaneously. Rabbi Eliezer, Rabbi Meir, and Rabbi Yehuda are cited in the {\i baraita} as holding that the simultaneous case also works \u8212- but the {\i baraita} does not make entirely clear whether they dispute the first case as well or only add the second. What does emerge is a spectrum of positions: Rabbi Yosi endorses only the after-death stipulation; others extend the permission to the simultaneous case; and whether any authority holds that the simultaneous case works while the after-death case does not remains to be resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Sons Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Splitting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a question that pushes at the limits of the {\i halacha}: what if a father had two sons, both of whom are {\i nezirim} and both of whom wish to use the father\u8217's dedicated funds? {\i Mahu} \u8212- what is the rule? Is this {\i halacha} a purely independent tradition within {\i nezirut} \u8212- one that operates without reference to {\i yerusha} \u8212- such that we say {\i kol dekadim galach galach}, whoever gets there first and uses the funds gets them, first come first served? Or does the {\i halacha}, despite its independent status, nonetheless draw upon the framework of {\i yerusha} in certain respects, such that the funds are divided equally between the two sons?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerusha Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether the {\i halacha} is fully self-contained or whether it rides atop a {\i yerusha} infrastructure. If it is {\i yerusha}-governed in its details, then just as two sons would split the father\u8217's ordinary estate equally, each would receive an equal share of the {\i nezirut} funds to apply toward his {\i korbanot}. If it is purely independent, there is no particular reason why the funds should not simply go to whichever son acts first. The tension is meaningful: we have already seen that this {\i halacha} does not follow {\i yerusha} in every respect \u8212- daughters are excluded even when they are the only heir. But exclusion of daughters does not tell us how the funds are divided among multiple qualifying sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Double Share\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's second question sharpens this further: what if one of the sons is a {\i bechor} \u8212- a firstborn son \u8212- and the other is a {\i pashut}, an ordinary son? In the normal laws of inheritance, the {\i bechor} receives a double portion. Does that double portion apply here as well? {\i Hilkheta gamrinan lah} \u8212- do we say this is purely a matter of {\i halacha} and therefore the {\i bechor} does not receive a double portion? Or do we say {\i yerusha hi} \u8212- the {\i yerusha} framework applies, and just as the {\i bechor} takes two portions of the father\u8217's ordinary estate, {\i hacha nami megale\u8217'ach} \u8212- so too he receives double the funds toward his {\i korbanot}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chulin vs Hekdesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a further distinction within the question about the {\i bechor}. Even if we say the {\i yerusha} framework applies, perhaps it applies only to {\i chulin} \u8212- ordinary property \u8212- and not to {\i hekdesh} \u8212- consecrated property. To understand this, the Rosh offers a more precise reading of the Gemara\u8217's language. He points out that the {\i gemara\u8217's} question about {\i chulin} and {\i hekdesh} is not a freestanding inquiry; it is a refinement of the {\i bechor} question specifically. According to the Rosh, the Gemara\u8217's formulation should be read as follows: even if we conclude that the {\i yerusha} framework governs how the funds are divided among the sons \u8212- so that each son receives a proportionate share \u8212- it might still be true that the {\i bechor}\u8217's double portion does not apply, because the {\i bechor}\u8217's entitlement to a double share may be categorically limited to {\i chulin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Inapplicable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for this distinction lies in the scriptural language used for {\i bechor} \u8212- {\i bechol asher yimatze lo} \u8212- the firstborn gets a double portion of all that is {\i found}, meaning all that is available. The term implies accessible, ordinary wealth. {\i Hekdesh} funds, however, are pre-designated and consecrated; they are not simply {\i found} in the estate as available resources. Therefore, even if the general {\i yerusha} framework extends to dividing {\i nezirut} funds between sons, the specific entitlement of the {\i bechor} to a double portion may not apply, because {\i hekdesh} property does not satisfy the criterion of {\i bechol asher yimatze lo}. The Rosh emphasizes that the {\i chulin}-{\i hekdesh} distinction is not a separate question the Gemara entertains alongside the {\i bechor} question \u8212- it is part of the {\i bechor} question itself, clarifying why the {\i bechor}\u8217's double portion might not apply even under a {\i yerusha}-governed model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Nazir Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then asks: what if the father and son were different types of {\i nezirim}? For example, the father was a {\i nazir olam} \u8212- a perpetual {\i nazir} \u8212- while the son is a {\i nazir stam}, a standard thirty-day {\i nazir}. (A {\i nazir olam} does periodically bring {\i korbanot} \u8212- the {\i nezirut} of a {\i nazir olam} permits a haircut once every thirty days or annually, accompanied by the appropriate {\i korbanot} \u8212- so the dedicated funds are not without purpose.) The question is whether the {\i halacha} that permits the son to use the father\u8217's funds operates only when both are the same type of {\i nazir}, or whether it functions even across different categories of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes that one could further ask whether the {\i halacha} applies even when both are {\i nazir olam} \u8212- perhaps the rule was only ever formulated for standard {\i nezirut}, not for {\i nezirut} of the {\i olam} variety. And even if both {\i nazir olam} types can access the funds, perhaps that only holds when father and son share the same type, not when one is {\i olam} and the other {\i stam}. In any event, if the different types of {\i nezirut} are both {\i nezirut tahara} \u8212- both involving purity-related sacrifices \u8212- there is an argument that the funds can be redirected, since they are fundamentally the same category of {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei-Tahor Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final question is posed by Rav Ashi and concerns a more fundamental divergence. What if the father\u8217's funds were dedicated not for {\i nezirut tahara} but for {\i nezirut} in a state of {\i tumah} \u8212- the father had become {\i tamei} and set aside funds for the {\i korbanot} a {\i tamei} {\i nazir} must bring, which involve birds rather than the standard animals? Can the son, who is a {\i nazir tahor}, use those {\i korbanot tumah} funds for his own {\i korbanot tahara}? Conversely: {\i aviu nazir tahor vehu nazir tamei mai} \u8212- what if the father was {\i tahor} and the son is {\i tamei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These are not merely different {\i types} of {\i nezirut} \u8212- they represent categorically different {\i korbanot}, with different animals and different ritual contexts. The question is whether the {\i halacha} that transfers the father\u8217's dedicated funds to the son operates even across this kind of disparity. The Gemara leaves both versions of the question unresolved \u8212- {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that, the daf concludes: {\i hadran alach Mi She\u8217'amar}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 29b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Making Son Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan vs.\u160 ?Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} opens by asking whether the debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish \u8212- regarding whether a father\u8217's power to impose {\i nezirut} on his son is a special {\i halacha} particular to {\i nezirut}, or whether it derives from the rabbinic concept of {\i chinuch} \u8212- maps onto a parallel dispute among {\i Tannaim}. The {\i Gemara} introduces a {\i braita}: {\i ad masai madzir beno benazir} \u8212- until what age may a father make his son a {\i nazir}? Rabbi holds: until the son brings two pubic hairs, i.e., until he becomes a full adult at the age of thirteen. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rebbi Yehuda holds: until he reaches {\i onat nedarim} \u8212- the stage at which a minor may make binding vows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concept of {\i onat nedarim} requires some elaboration. A child \u8212- whether boy or girl \u8212- reaches {\i onat nedarim} one year before the age of majority: a year before a girl\u8217's {\i bat mitzvah} and a year before a boy\u8217's {\i bar mitzvah}. At this point, provided the child understands that he or she is making a vow to God and has basic comprehension of the act, the vow is considered binding. This status is known as {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} \u8212- the ability to enunciate a vow while not yet a full adult. Whether this category is {\i d\u8217'oraisa} or {\i derabbanan} is itself a subject of considerable debate, as we will see.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim: 13 vs.\u160 ?12\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks whether this {\i Tannaitic} dispute mirrors the {\i Amoraic} one. The initial reading suggests that Rabbi\u8217's position \u8212- that the father retains power until the son becomes a full adult with two pubic hairs \u8212- is consistent with viewing the father\u8217's authority as a {\i halacha}, an intrinsic legal power over his minor son. Even if the son has already reached {\i onat nedarim} and can make his own vows to some degree, the father\u8217's power is conceived broadly: the son is still a minor in the overall legal sense, and the father retains dominion over him until the son becomes a full adult. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rebbi Yehuda\u8217's position, by contrast \u8212- that the father\u8217's power ends already at {\i onat nedarim} \u8212- seems consistent with the {\i chinuch} framework: once the son has some independent standing in the domain of vows, the father\u8217's role as educator in that area becomes inapplicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Debate: Mufla\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rejects this initial alignment, however. It offers a first alternative resolution: {\i d\u8217'chulei alma halacha hi b\u8217'nazir} \u8212- perhaps all agree that the father\u8217's power is a {\i halacha}, not merely {\i chinuch}. The debate concerns a different question entirely: what is the legal status of {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi, {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} is {\i derabbanan}. The son\u8217's independent standing in vows at age twelve is only a rabbinic enactment. Since the father\u8217's authority to impose {\i nezirut} is a {\i halacha} \u8212- operating at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level \u8212- it overrides the son\u8217's merely rabbinic independence. Hence the father retains his power even past {\i onat nedarim}, all the way until the son is a full adult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rebbi Yehuda, {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} is {\i d\u8217'oraisa}. If the son possesses biblically recognized independence in the domain of vows, then even a {\i halacha} granting the father power over his son should not extend into an area where the son is biblically autonomous. Since {\i nezirut} is itself a form of {\i neder}, and the son is independently recognized by the {\i Torah} as a party to vows, the father cannot impose {\i nezirut} on him once he reaches {\i onat nedarim}. The dividing line is therefore not {\i chinuch} versus {\i halacha}, but rather {\i d\u8217'oraisa} versus {\i derabbanan} status of the son\u8217's independence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises: does the {\i Gemara} actually use the phrase {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}, or merely {\i halacha}? In fact, the {\i Gemara} uses only the word {\i halacha}. The assumption that {\i halacha} implies {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} and therefore {\i d\u8217'oraisa} status is standard, but two qualifications deserve mention. First, there are well-known instances where {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} is used rhetorically rather than literally \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} in {\i Eduyos} speaks of {\i halacha} that Ammon and Moav tithe {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} in the seventh year, a ruling that is clearly {\i derabbanan}, yet the phrase is used. Second, some authorities, including the {\i Netziv}, suggest that {\i halacha} in certain contexts may simply denote an ancient tradition without carrying the full technical weight of {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}. Nevertheless, the {\i Gemara} here clearly operates on the assumption that {\i halacha} functions at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level, since the entire dynamic depends on the father\u8217's authority being able to override the son\u8217's {\i derabbanan} independence \u8212- not his {\i d\u8217'oraisa} independence. What may further support treating it as {\i d\u8217'oraisa} here is that we are not dealing with a wholly new independent ruling, but rather with a parameter internal to the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} laws of {\i nezirut} themselves \u8212- a detail about the scope of the {\i Torah}\u8217's framework already in place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chinuch Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presents a second approach: {\i d\u8217'chulei alma k\u8217'dei l\u8217'chancho b\u8217'mitzvot} \u8212- perhaps all agree the father\u8217's authority is based on {\i chinuch}, and the entire institution is {\i derabbanan}. Moreover, {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} is also {\i derabbanan} on all views. We therefore have two rabbinic forces in tension: the father\u8217's {\i chinuch} obligation on one side, and the son\u8217's rabbinic independence in vows on the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi holds that in this collision, we tip in favor of {\i chinuch}: {\i ati chinuch d\u8217'rabbanan v\u8217'dachi mufla samuch l\u8217'ish d\u8217'rabbanan}. The father\u8217's {\i chinuch} role prevails, and the son\u8217's limited rabbinic independence in vows does not terminate the father\u8217's power. This approach also helps explain why {\i chinuch} in the context of {\i nezirut} can override other principles \u8212- such as {\i chullin ba\u8217'azarah} and {\i shechitah l\u8217'of} \u8212- that are similarly {\i derabbanan}: we are already in a domain of rabbinic accommodation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rebbi Yehuda holds the reverse: once the rabbis recognized the son\u8217's independent standing in the area of {\i nedarim}, that recognition itself removes him from the {\i chinuch} framework in this domain. {\i Chinuch} is predicated on the child being a non-agent in the relevant area. Once the child can act independently \u8212- even rabbinically \u8212- the father\u8217's {\i chinuch} role falls away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes that the {\i Tannaitic} dispute cannot be cleanly mapped onto a single underlying question. Either it is about the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} versus {\i derabbanan} status of {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} within an overall {\i halacha} framework, or it is about which rabbinic principle prevails when two {\i derabbanan} institutions conflict. The lines do not divide neatly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Chanina Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanina\u8217's Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a {\i braita} containing a real-life illustration of this debate: {\i maaseh b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina} \u8212- there was a case involving Rabbi Chanina whose father had made him a {\i nazir}. He was brought before Rabban Gamliel, who wished to investigate the matter. Rabban Gamliel sought to determine whether the boy had already reached adult status when his father imposed the {\i nezirut} \u8212- because if the son had already been a full adult at that point, the father\u8217's act would have been ineffective. The question, as framed by the two opinions, was either whether the boy had two pubic hairs (Rabbi\u8217's position \u8212- the test for full adulthood), or whether he had already reached {\i onat nedarim} (Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rebbi Yehuda\u8217's position \u8212- the earlier threshold).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The young Chanina, demonstrating remarkable presence of mind, said to Rabban Gamliel: do not trouble yourself with the examination. If I was a minor when my father imposed {\i nezirut} on me, then I am a {\i nazir} on account of my father \u8212- {\i eheye bishvil aba}. And if I was already an adult, then I accept {\i nezirut} upon myself now regardless \u8212- {\i eheye bishvil atzmi}. Either way, I am a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel rose and kissed him on his head, and declared: {\i muvtach ani beze sheyoreh halacha beyisrael} \u8212- I am confident that this young man will one day teach {\i halacha} in Israel. And indeed, we are told, it was not long before that prediction came true.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mid-Period Majority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially poses what the {\i Rosh} characterizes as a somewhat throwaway question \u8212- noting that it is easy to understand Chanina\u8217's reasoning according to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rebbi Yehuda, since reaching {\i onat nedarim} is that opinion\u8217's threshold, and Chanina\u8217's statement that he would accept {\i nezirut} himself if he had reached {\i onat nedarim} fits naturally. The harder question, according to Rabbi, is this: if the test is two pubic hairs and full adulthood, then when Chanina said \u8220"if I am an adult, I accept it for myself,\u8221" he was presumably speaking of that same standard. But then the {\i Gemara} quickly moves to what is the real question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} identifies three scenarios: the son was already an adult when his father made him a {\i nazir}; he became an adult after the thirty days of {\i nezirut} concluded; or he became an adult in the middle of the thirty-day period. The first two cases are straightforward. If he was already an adult at the outset, his acquiescence \u8212- whether silent or verbal \u8212- renders it his own {\i nezirut}, and it concludes after thirty days. If he became an adult only after the thirty days ended, the {\i nezirut} was entirely that of the father and it concludes naturally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the middle case is genuinely difficult. The {\i Tosefta} makes explicit what the {\i Gemara} assumes: if a son becomes a full adult in the middle of the {\i nezirut} his father imposed, becoming an adult in itself terminates whatever his father had begun \u8212- just as the {\i Mishnah} earlier discussed how the son\u8217's protest or the relatives\u8217' shaving terminates the {\i nezirut}. Reaching full legal adulthood functions as an automatic termination, even without any explicit act of protest. This creates a significant problem for Chanina\u8217's declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Termination Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks why the analysis differs depending on whether the dividing line is {\i shtei se\u8217'arot} (two pubic hairs, full adulthood) or {\i onat nedarim} (the ability to make vows). The answer turns on a fundamental distinction between these two thresholds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reaching {\i onat nedarim} grants the son a limited form of independence in the specific domain of vows. But it does not change his overall legal status. He is still a minor in every other sense. Consequently, while {\i onat nedarim} would be strong enough to prevent the father from imposing {\i nezirut} at the outset \u8212- since in the area of {\i nedarim} the son is now an independent actor \u8212- it is not strong enough to terminate a {\i nezirut} already underway. The father was fully empowered to impose {\i nezirut} before {\i onat nedarim} arrived. The fact that the son now has some limited independence in the realm of vows does not retroactively undo what was validly imposed. His fundamental legal status remains that of a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, {\i shtei se\u8217'arot} \u8212- full adulthood \u8212- represents a complete transformation of legal status. The son is now a new legal person in every domain, not merely in one limited area. That complete transformation is powerful enough to terminate whatever the father had begun, even mid-period, without any explicit protest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counting Periods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This asymmetry resolves the {\i Gemara}\u8217's problem according to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rebbi Yehuda. Under that opinion, reaching {\i onat nedarim} mid-period does not interrupt the {\i nezirut} at all. The thirty days run continuously from when the father imposed it. So Chanina\u8217's declaration \u8212- \u8220"either way I am a {\i nazir}\u8221" \u8212- works perfectly: whether the father\u8217's {\i nezirut} was valid from the start or not, thirty days from the outset suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under Rabbi\u8217's opinion, however, things are more complicated. If Chanina became an adult in the middle of the thirty days, the {\i nezirut} imposed by his father ended at that point. He would need to begin a fresh thirty-day count from the moment of adulthood, and bring his {\i korbanot} at the end of that period. So he cannot simply say \u8220"either way thirty days from the outset.\u8221" He would need to account for the possibility that he became an adult partway through.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this: according to Rabbi, one would have to be more precise. If it became clear that Chanina became an adult mid-period, the appropriate approach would be to complete the father\u8217's thirty days, and then \u8212- if adulthood had not been reached at the very outset \u8212- accept a new {\i nezirut} from that moment forward, bringing {\i korbanot} only at the conclusion of an additional thirty days. In practical terms, since the precise moment of adulthood cannot always be established, one might need to accept {\i nezirut} and bring {\i korbanot} in a way that covers both possibilities. The {\i Gemara} leaves the precise resolution open but establishes that unlike Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rebbi Yehuda\u8217's clean answer, Rabbi\u8217's position requires two separate counting periods depending on when adulthood was reached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might raise a comparison to {\i giur katan} \u8212- a minor who converts and upon reaching majority has the option to repudiate the conversion. There are genuine structural similarities: in both cases, a status was imposed on someone who was a minor, and full adulthood triggers a reassessment. Similarly, the {\i Minchas Chinuch} raises the celebrated question of a child who becomes a {\i bar mitzvah} in the middle of {\i sefiras ha\u8217'omer} \u8212- does the counting before adulthood count toward the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} obligation of counting fifty days, or was it entirely without legal meaning? The same logic applies here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from this {\i sugya} is a subtle distinction: the threshold that prevents imposition going forward ({\i onat nedarim}) is not necessarily the same threshold that terminates an ongoing {\i nezirut} ({\i shtei se\u8217'arot}). Nor is a minor\u8217's right to protest \u8212- which can end the {\i nezirut} even without adulthood \u8212- identical to the automatic termination triggered by becoming an adult. Each operates by its own logic.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Son Using Father\u8217's Funds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the next {\i Mishnah}, which addresses a distinct but related father-son question: may a son use funds that his father had designated for his own {\i nezirut} sacrifices? The {\i Mishnah} opens with the ruling that a son may do so \u8212- but a daughter may not. The details of how and when this applies are then spelled out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} distinguishes two scenarios. In the first, which Rabbi Yosi presents as the case where the mechanism does {\i not} apply, a son becomes a {\i nazir} after his father has already died, and declares: {\i hareini nazir al menas she\u8217'agale\u8217'ach al ma\u8217'os abba} \u8212- I accept {\i nezirut} on condition that I use my father\u8217's funds. In the second scenario, which Rabbi Yosi identifies as the case where it does apply: the son and the father were both {\i nezirim} simultaneously, the father set aside unspecified funds \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'os setumim} \u8212- for his own {\i nezirut}, and then the father died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that this is the {\i girsa} supported by the {\i braita} quoted shortly thereafter in the {\i Gemara}, which is the reverse of what appears in some printed versions of the {\i Mishnah}. Relying on that {\i braita}, where Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position is stated explicitly, we read the {\i Mishnah} as well with Rabbi Yosi holding that only the simultaneous case works \u8212- not the post-death acceptance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What underlies the difference between these two scenarios? When father and son are {\i nezirim} at the same time, there is something that might be described as a family religious undertaking \u8212- a shared {\i nezirut} enterprise. In that context, when the father dies, the funds he designated flow to the son as a natural extension of what had already been a shared process. When the son only accepts {\i nezirut} after the father\u8217's death, he is an entirely independent actor, and the father\u8217's designated funds \u8212- which are {\i hekdesh}, consecrated property \u8212- cannot simply be redirected for someone else\u8217's {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a first version of this mechanism, which in the {\i Mishnah} as initially read seems to be the case that works: the son stipulates from the outset that he will use the father\u8217's money. In that version, the logic is different. Unspecified funds \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'os setumim} \u8212- have a certain indeterminacy about their destination; they normally go to an {\i olah}. If the son at the moment of accepting his own {\i nezirut} declares that his {\i nezirut} is predicated on using those funds, he creates a link between his {\i nezirut} and those funds from the beginning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha: Son Not Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks for the rationale underlying the son\u8217's ability to use his father\u8217's funds. Rabbi Yochanan answers simply: {\i halacha hi b\u8217'Nazir} \u8212- it is a {\i halacha} specific to {\i nezirut}. There is no {\i pasuk}, no {\i chinuch} argument, no logical derivation. This is a traditional ruling. Notably, unlike other places where Rabbi Yochanan offers {\i halacha} as one possible explanation alongside others, here it appears to be the only available answer, and Reish Lakish offers no alternative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: is it not obvious that only a son and not a daughter may use the father\u8217's funds? A son inherits his father; a daughter does not, except in the absence of sons. Since these are {\i hekdesh} funds, the ability to use them follows an inheritance-like logic, and of course a daughter inherits differently. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i lo tzricha d\u8217'leis lei ela barta} \u8212- the {\i chiddush} is needed for the case where the father had only a daughter, who would in that case inherit all his other property. One might think that since the {\i halacha} is framed in terms of inheritance and she is the heir, she should be entitled. The {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} \u8212- or the {\i halacha} itself \u8212- tells us this is not so. The right belongs specifically to a son, not to a daughter, even when she is the sole heir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking ruling. The {\i halacha} is acknowledged to be somewhat arbitrary in its parameters \u8212- there is no broader principle that straightforwardly generates these details. That is precisely what makes it a {\i halacha}: it is an ancient tradition whose contours are simply received rather than derived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises the question: do the {\i rabbanan} actually disagree with Rabbi Yosi, and if so, on which point? As noted, Rabbi Yosi (working with the {\i girsa} supported by the {\i braita}) holds that the mechanism works when the son accepts {\i nezirut} after the father\u8217's death with explicit stipulation. The rabbis might agree with this and add that it also works when they were {\i nezirim} simultaneously \u8212- a broader position. Or the rabbis might hold the exact mirror image of Rabbi Yosi, saying it works only when they were {\i nezirim} simultaneously and not after death. Or there may be an even broader position that both scenarios work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} is then cited: it presents Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position explicitly \u8212- the post-death stipulation works; simultaneous {\i nezirut} does not. It then records that Rabbi Eliezer, Rabbi Meir, and Rabbi Yehuda all hold that the simultaneous case also works \u8212- {\i zehu shemegalei\u8217'ach al maos aviv}. What remains unclear is whether these latter authorities also include the post-death case or limit themselves to the simultaneous scenario. The {\i Gemara} notes this ambiguity and does not fully resolve which cases the {\i rabbanan} include or exclude. What is visible is the range: Rabbi Yosi anchors one position; Rabbi Eliezer, Rabbi Meir, and Rabbi Yehuda take a broader view regarding simultaneous {\i nezirut}; and the exact scope of their disagreement with Rabbi Yosi remains partially open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Sons & Yerusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Sons Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the basic framework established, the {\i Gemara} turns to a series of questions probing how far the analogy to {\i yerusha} \u8212- inheritance law \u8212- governs the operation of this {\i halacha}. These questions are raised as {\i ba\u8217'yot} by {\i Rabbah} and {\i Rava}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first: what if the father left two sons, both of whom are {\i nezirim} and both of whom wish to use their father\u8217's designated funds? Do we say that this entire institution has nothing to do with {\i yerusha} \u8212- in which case it is simply first come, first served: {\i kol de-kadim gille\u8217'ach gille\u8217'ach}, whoever brings his {\i korbanot} first gets to use the funds? Or do we say that {\i yerushah gemiri lah} \u8212- this {\i halacha} is nonetheless shaped by the principles of inheritance \u8212- in which case each son receives half?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further ask: at what point must both sons be {\i nezirim} in order for both to assert a claim? If one son becomes a {\i nazir} twenty days into the other\u8217's {\i nezirut}, or after the first son\u8217's thirty days are already complete but before the first son has brought his {\i korbanot}, does the second son have standing to claim half? The {\i Gemara} does not explore these complications here, focusing on the simpler case where both sons are {\i nezirim} simultaneously. The question of principle remains: is this a pure {\i halacha} with no {\i yerusha} logic embedded in it, or does it import {\i yerusha} principles?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Double Portion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} sharpens the question: what if one son is a {\i bekhor} \u8212- a firstborn \u8212- and the other is a {\i pashut}, an ordinary son? By the laws of {\i yerusha}, the {\i bekhor} receives a double portion: {\i pi shenayim}. Does that double portion apply here as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If we say {\i halakhah gemiri lah} \u8212- this is a pure {\i halacha} with no {\i yerusha} logic \u8212- then perhaps the sons divide equally, and the {\i bekhor} has no special claim. If we say {\i yerushah hi} \u8212- it follows {\i yerusha} principles \u8212- then the {\i bekhor} should receive twice what his brother receives of the father\u8217's {\i hekdesh} funds, just as he would receive a double portion of ordinary estate property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} then adds a further refinement, reflecting the analysis of the {\i Rosh}. Even if we generally apply {\i yerusha} principles, perhaps the {\i bekhor}\u8217's double portion is a special case that does not extend to {\i hekdesh}. The {\i bekhor}\u8217's entitlement to {\i pi shenayim} is derived from the verse {\i b\u8217'chol asher yimatzei lo} \u8212- from whatever is found, whatever is available to him. The word implies accessible, available assets. {\i Hekdesh} funds, however, are not \u8220"available\u8221" in the ordinary sense \u8212- they are pre-consecrated property. One might argue that the {\i bekhor}\u8217's special double portion, grounded in this language of availability, simply does not apply to {\i hekdesh} funds, even if the general {\i yerusha} principle of division between sons does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} argues that the phrase {\i u-ve-chullin hu de-it leh pi shenayim aval be-hekdesh lo} \u8212- that the {\i bekhor} gets a double portion from {\i chullin} but not from {\i hekdesh} \u8212- is not raising an entirely new question but rather clarifying the question about the {\i bekhor}. It is not, as {\i Tosafos} suggests, a matter of how the father verbally designated the funds (whether he said {\i nezirusi} versus {\i korbanos nezirusi}). The {\i Rosh} dismisses that reading as {\i pirush zar} \u8212- a strained explanation \u8212- since there is no meaningful legal difference between those formulations. Rather, the question is structural: even granting that {\i yerusha} principles govern the division of funds between sons, does the {\i bekhor}\u8217's specific entitlement to {\i pi shenayim}, which is textually anchored in the concept of available assets, extend into the realm of {\i hekdesh}? That is the unresolved question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks a further question: what if father and son are different {\i types} of {\i nezirim}? The case: the father is a {\i nazir olam} \u8212- a lifelong {\i nazir} \u8212- and the son is a {\i nazir stam}, a standard thirty-day {\i nazir}. Or the reverse. Can funds designated for one type of {\i nezirut} be used by someone observing a different type?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A brief clarification: a {\i nazir olam} does not bring {\i korbanot} at the conclusion of a fixed term, since there is no fixed term. Rather, a {\i nazir olam} takes a haircut periodically \u8212- once every thirty days by some views, once a year by others \u8212- and brings {\i korbanot} at that time. So the question of designated funds is still relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether the {\i halacha} allowing the son to use the father\u8217's funds was formulated specifically for standard {\i nezirut} \u8212- {\i stam nezirut} \u8212- or whether it extends to any combination of {\i nezirut} types. {\i Tosafos} further notes that one might even question whether this {\i halacha} applies when both are {\i nazir olam} \u8212- perhaps it was specifically calibrated for standard {\i nezirut} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei/Tahor Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, {\i Rav Ashi} poses the most acute version of the question: what if the father\u8217's funds were not merely designated for a different {\i type} of {\i nezirut}, but were designated for a qualitatively different sacrificial purpose? Specifically, if the father had become {\i tamei} \u8212- ritually impure \u8212- and set aside funds for {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut tumah} (which involve different animals, including birds), and the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} is {\i nezirut tahara} \u8212- pure {\i nezirut} \u8212- may the son use those funds? The {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut tumah} and {\i nezirut tahara} are not merely different in magnitude or proportion; they are categorically distinct offerings. And the reverse: if the father\u8217's funds were for {\i nezirut tahara} and the son\u8217's {\i nezirut} is {\i tamei} \u8212- what then?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} leaves this unresolved: {\i teiku}. The questions about two sons, the {\i bekhor}, the different types of {\i nezirut}, and the {\i tamei/tahor} transfer remain open, marking the close of the tractate\u8217's final discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehadran alach Mi She\u8217'amar.}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 31\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hekdesh Ta\u8217'us Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai/Hillel Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Perek Hamishi opens with a dispute that, at first glance, seems to belong more naturally to the laws of {\i hekdesh} than to {\i nezirut}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i Beis Shammai omrim hekdesh ta\u8217'us hekdesh} \u8212- Beis Shammai holds that a mistaken consecration is nonetheless a valid consecration. Beis Hillel disagrees: {\i ein hekdesh} \u8212- the consecration does not take effect. Taking the {\i mishnah} at face value, the question is whether an act of {\i hekdesh} grounded in a mistaken premise has any legal force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Link to Perek Sheini\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dispute does not arise in isolation. All the way back in Perek Sheini (daf 9), the {\i mishnah} records a parallel controversy: if someone declares {\i hareini nazir min ha-gerogerot u\u8217'min ha-devilah} \u8212- \u8220"I am a {\i nazir} from figs and pressed dates\u8221" \u8212- Beis Shammai says he is a {\i nazir} while Beis Hillel says he is not. The Gemara there explained: {\i Beis Shammai leta\u8217'myahu d\u8217'amri ein she\u8217'elah b\u8217'hekdesh, u\u8217'cheivan d\u8217'ein she\u8217'elah b\u8217'hekdesh ein she\u8217'elah b\u8217'nezirut} \u8212- Beis Shammai reasons that just as one cannot obtain release from {\i hekdesh}, so too one cannot obtain release from {\i nezirut}, since the Torah applies the word {\i kodesh} to the {\i nazir}, as in the {\i pasuk}: {\i kadosh yihyeh gadal peras se\u8217'ar rosho}. The stickiness of {\i hekdesh} thus anchors Beis Shammai\u8217's broader position on the irrevocability of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Shammai\u8217's reasoning, then, is that {\i hekdesh} is an unusually durable legal category \u8212- once something is consecrated, it cannot simply be undone. That same quality of durability is what Beis Shammai invokes here: if {\i hekdesh} cannot be released, it follows that even a mistaken {\i hekdesh} should take hold, since the underlying legal mechanism admits no exceptions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} provides three concrete illustrations. First: one declares that the first black ox to emerge from his house shall be {\i hekdesh}, but a white ox emerges instead \u8212- Beis Shammai says it is {\i hekdesh}, Beis Hillel says it is not. Second: one declares that the first gold coin to come up from his pocket shall be {\i hekdesh}, but a silver coin comes up \u8212- again Beis Shammai says {\i hekdesh}, Beis Hillel disagrees. Third: one declares that the first barrel of wine that comes to hand shall be {\i hekdesh}, but it turns out to be oil \u8212- same dispute. Three cases, seemingly making the same point. The reason the {\i mishnah} requires all three will become clear as the Gemara proceeds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temurah Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beginning/End Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens its analysis by asking: what is Beis Shammai\u8217's rationale? The first answer offered is {\i yalfinan techilat hekdesh mi-sof hekdesh} \u8212- we derive the rule governing the inception of {\i hekdesh} from the rule governing a later stage of {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temurah Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific comparison is to {\i temurah} \u8212- the act of attempting to substitute one animal for a consecrated one. Once an animal is {\i hekdesh}, it cannot be redeemed simply by designating a replacement; instead, both animals become consecrated. Crucially, {\i temurah} operates even when the substitution is mistaken: if one said \u8220"this black ox in exchange for my {\i korban}\u8221" and it turned out to be white, the {\i temurah} is still valid. The argument is therefore: {\i ma temurah afilu b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut af hekdesh afilu b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- just as {\i temurah} works even with a mistaken identification, so too the initial act of {\i hekdesh} should work even when mistaken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Hillel rejects this comparison on two grounds, both noted by Rashi. First, {\i temurah} is a {\i gezerat hakatuv} \u8212- a specific biblical decree \u8212- and one cannot extrapolate from a unique legal rule to a general principle. Second, and more substantively, there is a conceptual difference between making a mistake {\i within the realm of hekdesh} \u8212- as in {\i temurah}, where {\i kedushah} is already present \u8212- and mistakenly {\i bringing something into} the realm of {\i hekdesh} in the first place. Beis Hillel\u8217's formulation is: {\i hani milei temurah, u\u8217'le-kho de-hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut lo machtinan} \u8212- that rule applies to {\i temurah}, but I will not allow an item to enter {\i hekdesh} through a mistake at the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses Beis Shammai further. Consider a case where someone says, \u8220"Let this animal be a {\i temurah} as of noon today\u8221" \u8212- the {\i temurah} would not take effect at the moment of speech, but only when noon arrives. The condition must be fulfilled before the legal change occurs. By the same logic, in our {\i mishnah}, the person said the {\i black} ox \u8212- so why should anything happen before a black ox actually emerges? The white ox that came out is not what was specified, and the {\i temurah} analogy would seem to support waiting until the specified condition is met rather than allowing the wrong item to be consecrated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Pappa Reinterpret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Black Ox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, Rav Pappa offers a different approach entirely. Rather than defending the {\i temurah} comparison, he reframes the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case: {\i lekach ne\u8217'emar rishon le-khesheyetze rishon, ve-hashor shachor ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- what the person actually meant was \u8220"the first black ox to come out.\u8221" The {\i mishnah} is not describing a scenario with only two oxen \u8212- one black, one white \u8212- where the wrong one emerged. Rather, the person owned multiple oxen, including several black ones, and designated \u8220"the first of the black oxen to come out of my house.\u8221" When the white ox walked out, that was simply irrelevant to his declaration. The first black ox to subsequently emerge is then consecrated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Actually Mistake\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reinterpretation fundamentally changes the character of the case: it is no longer, strictly speaking, a {\i ta\u8217'us} at all. The speaker was deliberate and specific; there was no error in the premise of his declaration. So why does the {\i mishnah} frame this as a case of {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ta\u8217'us Redefined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains that {\i ta\u8217'us} here does not mean an outright mistake but rather an ambiguous or non-definitive statement \u8212- as distinct from a clear, unambiguous act of {\i hekdesh}. The declaration \u8220"the first one to come out of my house shall be {\i hekdesh}\u8221" has an element of imprecision that invites reinterpretation, and it is this quality that the {\i mishnah} calls {\i ta\u8217'us}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges Rav Pappa: the {\i mishnah} says \u8220"the first to come out of my house,\u8221" not \u8220"the first black one.\u8221" If the person truly meant \u8220"the first of the black ones,\u8221" the phrasing of the {\i mishnah} seems to point in a different direction. One could answer that \u8220"from my house\u8221" is simply specifying the location as opposed to another outbuilding, and that the primary criterion was the color \u8212- but the textual difficulty remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pappa Responds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa of Barnish responds to Rav Ashi: {\i mishum de-ati le-diburei kama} \u8212- because the declaration as stated comes across as somewhat misleading or ambiguous relative to what was actually intended. The face value of the words suggests one thing, while the speaker\u8217's real intent was another. It is precisely this gap between surface meaning and actual intent that requires reinterpretation and that justifies applying the label of {\i ta\u8217'us} to what is, in reality, a deliberate but loosely worded declaration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shammai True Ta\u8217'us\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established Rav Pappa\u8217's model \u8212- in which the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case is not a genuine mistake but a deliberately ambiguous formulation \u8212- a natural question emerges: does Beis Shammai actually hold that a true {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us}, a real mistake in the underlying premise, would be valid? The reinterpretation implies that a genuine {\i ta\u8217'us} might in fact not work even according to Beis Shammai, since the whole effort was to show that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case is not a {\i ta\u8217'us} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder/Chacham Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a {\i mishnah} that bears directly on this question: {\i mi she-nadar be-nazir ve-nishal le-chakham ve-hittiro, ve-hayetah lo behemah meforeshet, tetzei ve-tir\u8217'eh ba-eder} \u8212- if someone vowed to be a {\i nazir}, consulted a {\i chakham} who released him, and had already set aside a specific animal for his {\i korban}, that animal goes out to graze freely among the flock. The reason is that the {\i chakham} is {\i oker ha-neder me-ikaro} \u8212- he uproots the vow retroactively, as if it never existed. Since the {\i nezirut} never legally occurred, the animal designated for its {\i chatat} was never truly consecrated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Hillel invokes this case against Beis Shammai: {\i i attem modim be-she-hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut ve-tetzei ve-tir\u8217'eh ba-eder?} \u8212- do you not concede that in this case, which involves a {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us}, the animal is not consecrated and simply grazes freely? If Beis Shammai truly holds that {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us} is valid {\i hekdesh}, the animal should remain consecrated even after the {\i chakham} releases the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer \u8212- which both Rav Pappa\u8217's framework and, as Tosafot notes, straightforward analysis would supply \u8212- is that this is not a {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us} case at all. The person fully intended to be a {\i nazir} and deliberately designated the animal; there was no mistake in the premise. What subsequently changed was not the discovery of an error but a conscious legal act: the {\i chakham}\u8217's retroactive release of the vow. There is a fundamental difference between a {\i ta\u8217'us} \u8212- where the very premise of the consecration was mistaken from the outset \u8212- and a deliberate act that is later undone through a recognized legal mechanism. Beis Shammai can therefore concede this case entirely without any concession regarding genuine {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us}. According to Rav Pappa, moreover, our {\i mishnah}\u8217's case is itself not a genuine {\i ta\u8217'us}, so the entire exchange is operating on a separate track from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's core ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Past Tense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vocalization Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a third explanation of the {\i mishnah}, one that depends on a subtle difference in vocalization. The letters {\i shin-yud-tzadi-alef} can be read either as {\i she-yeitzei} \u8212- \u8220"that will come out,\u8221" future tense \u8212- or as {\i she-yatza} \u8212- \u8220"that came out,\u8221" past tense. The {\i mishnah}, as typically read, seems to use the future: one declares in advance that whatever black ox will emerge from his house shall be {\i hekdesh}. But the letters themselves are equally consistent with a past-tense reading, and Abaye proposes that this is precisely how the {\i mishnah} should be understood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Past Tense Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Abaye\u8217's reading, the {\i mishnah} describes not a prospective declaration but a retrospective one: the speaker is standing in the afternoon or evening, after an animal has already come out of his house. He declares, \u8220"The black ox that came out of my house \u8212- let it be {\i hekdesh}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Animal Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case then proceeds as follows: it becomes known \u8212- or is pointed out \u8212- that the animal which actually emerged was white, not black. The speaker responds: had I known it was a white ox that came out, I would have said \u8220"the white ox.\u8221" My intention was simply to consecrate the first animal, whatever it was. The color was incidental to the declaration; what mattered was that it was the first to come out. Beis Shammai holds this consecration valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extension Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes Abaye\u8217's explanation elegant for the first case creates a difficulty for the other two. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's second case uses {\i she-ya\u8217'aleh} \u8212- \u8220"that will come up\u8221" \u8212- in connection with the gold coin, and the third uses {\i she-ta\u8217'aleh} in connection with the barrel of wine. These forms are clearly future tense and do not naturally admit the same vocalic shift that worked for {\i she-yatza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vocalization Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by adjusting the vocalizations of the other two cases as well. For the coin, read {\i she-ala} instead of {\i she-ya\u8217'aleh}; for the barrel, read {\i she-altah} instead of {\i she-ta\u8217'aleh} \u8212- both past-tense forms. On this reading, all three cases follow the same model: the speaker designated something after the fact, mistakenly identifying it by a secondary attribute (color, material, contents), when his actual intent was simply to consecrate whatever had come to hand first.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eye Framework Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the three explanations of the {\i mishnah} laid out, the Gemara shifts to a related analysis introduced by Rav Chisda. He states: {\i ukma b\u8217'chivrei lakya, chivrei b\u8217'ukmei lakya} \u8212- a black ox among white ones is inferior, and a white ox among black ones is also inferior. In a white herd, a black ox stands out unfavorably; in a black herd, a white ox is at a disadvantage. This principle of relative quality among livestock now becomes the lens through which the three {\i mishnah} cases are examined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Case Quality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case \u8212- the black ox \u8212- thus involves an item of lesser quality, given that the herd is presumed to be white. If Beis Shammai holds that this consecration is valid, one might infer that a person can consecrate {\i b\u8217'ayin ra\u8217'ah} \u8212- in a \u8220"bad eye,\u8221" with a less generous disposition, giving {\i hekdesh} an inferior item while keeping the better one. The difficulty is determining whether Beis Shammai\u8217's ruling reflects that standard or a more generous one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Case Quality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case complicates the picture. The person said {\i zahav} \u8212- gold \u8212- but a silver coin emerged. Gold is more valuable than silver, so the person had intended to give the better item and ended up giving the lesser one. This would suggest he was consecrating {\i b\u8217'ayin yafah} \u8212- with a \u8220"good eye,\u8221" intending generosity toward {\i hekdesh} \u8212- yet Beis Shammai says even the lesser silver coin is consecrated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Case Quality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case adds further complexity. Wine was declared, oil emerged. Whether wine or oil is the superior commodity depends on region: in Galilee, wine is more valuable, making this a case of giving the lesser item; in other places, oil is more valuable, reversing the quality calculus entirely. The three cases thus seem to move back and forth between {\i ayin yafah} and {\i ayin ra\u8217'ah} without a consistent pattern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution by Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda resolves the difficulty by introducing a use-based framework: {\i ukma le-mashcha, sumka le-visra, chivra le-radya} \u8212- a black ox is best for leather (the hide), a red ox is best for eating, and a white ox is best for plowing. The quality of an animal is not absolute but relative to its intended purpose. With this principle, each of the three cases can be assigned a consistent value \u8212- whether the consecrated item represents a more or a less desirable choice depends on what the item is being used for. As for the first case, Rav Chisda adds: when I said that a black ox in a white herd is inferior, I was speaking of a particular type of ox \u8212- {\i turei d\u8217'Karmonaye}, oxen of that specific province \u8212- not of oxen in general. In ordinary circumstances, the black ox can in fact be the more valuable one, depending on its intended use. This allows all three cases to be read consistently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maaser Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miscount Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Shammai now presses its case for the validity of {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us} by mounting a counter-proof from the laws of {\i maaser behemah}. The argument runs as follows. When counting one\u8217's animals to designate the tithe, the tenth animal to pass under the rod becomes {\i maaser}. But what happens if the count goes wrong? The {\i mishnah} addresses a case where someone miscounted and called the ninth animal the tenth, the tenth animal the ninth, and the eleventh animal the tenth \u8212- thereby producing three animals, each called \u8220"tenth\u8221" at some point in the counting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Three Sanctified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite the error, all three animals acquire the sanctity of {\i maaser}. The biblical verse states: {\i kol asher ya\u8217'avor tachat ha-shevet ha-asiri yihyeh kodesh l-Hashem} \u8212- whatever passes under the rod as the tenth shall be holy. The law sanctifies the tenth animal through the act of the rod passing over it, and the miscounting does not undo the consecration that attached to each animal at the moment it was designated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, says Beis Shammai, is a clear demonstration that {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us} is valid: the person made an obvious mistake \u8212- one cannot have three different animals each genuinely be the tenth \u8212- and yet all three become {\i maaser}. If the Torah permits {\i hekdesh} through {\i ta\u8217'us} in the case of {\i maaser}, the same should apply to {\i hekdesh} generally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Rejects Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Hillel rejects the proof: {\i lo ha-shevet kidsho} \u8212- it is not the person\u8217's speech that consecrates the animal; it is the rod. The {\i kedushah} of {\i maaser behemah} is a {\i gezerat hakatuv}, a specific biblical decree, and the mechanism of consecration is the physical act of the rod touching the animal, not the verbal designation. Since the source of the {\i kedushah} is the rod rather than the spoken word, one cannot draw conclusions about the validity of verbal {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'us} from this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Hillel reinforces the point with a reductio: {\i u-mah ilu ta\u8217'ah be-hanahat ha-shevet al shemini ve-al shenim asar shema asah klum} \u8212- if someone mistakenly placed the rod on the eighth animal or the twelfth, nothing would happen; no {\i kedushah} would result. This proves that it is not the person\u8217's declaration or intent that drives the consecration in the {\i maaser} case. Rather, as Beis Hillel concludes: {\i ela ha-katuv she-kiddesh asiri} \u8212- it is the scriptural verse itself that consecrates the tenth, making this an entirely sui generis mechanism. The {\i maaser} case therefore provides no basis for the broader principle Beis Shammai seeks to establish.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir/Status Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Status Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through Beis Shammai\u8217's proof from {\i maaser} and Beis Hillel\u8217's rebuttal, the Gemara returns to the case introduced earlier: why is the animal designated for the {\i chatat} of a {\i nazir} not consecrated once the {\i chakham} releases the vow? If Beis Shammai holds that {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us} is valid, and the designation of the animal was made under the assumption of a {\i nezirut} that turned out never to have legally existed, should that not be a {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us} that Beis Shammai would validate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conscious Act Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that this is simply not a case of {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us}. The person who designated the animal did so consciously, deliberately, and with full intent. There was no mistake in the premise at the moment of the act. What happened subsequently \u8212- the {\i chakham}\u8217's retroactive release of the vow \u8212- is an entirely different legal phenomenon. An intentional act that is later reversed by a recognized legal mechanism is categorically distinct from an act that was mistaken in its very premise from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction that emerges from the entire sugya is therefore this: {\i hekdesh ta\u8217'us}, as the term is used in the {\i mishnah} and debated by Beis Shammai and Beis Hillel, refers to a situation where the premise underlying the consecration was erroneous at the moment of the declaration. It does not encompass a case where a person consciously and correctly performed an act of {\i hekdesh} and subsequently undid it through legitimate means. Beis Shammai and Beis Hillel agree on the latter \u8212- the animal goes out to graze \u8212- and their dispute is confined to the former.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 32\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nezirut B\u8217'Taaut Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the bottom of 31b continues the theme of {\i ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- error \u8212- that has occupied the preceding discussion. The previous {\i mishna} established that {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} is {\i hekdesh} according to {\i Beit Shammai}, and the current {\i mishna} builds on that foundation by turning to the intertwined questions of {\i nezirut b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} and {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refused Heter Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens: {\i mi shenadar b\u8217'nazir venishal l\u8217'chacham ve\u8217'asaro} \u8212- someone took a vow of {\i nezirut} and went to a {\i chacham} to obtain a {\i heter}, but the {\i chacham} was unable to release him. One might ask how such a case is to be understood according to {\i Beit Shammai}, who does not generally accept the framework of {\i nezirut b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} as grounds for release. {\i Tosafot} addresses this directly: for the {\i mishna} to function according to {\i Beit Shammai}, what the person asked the {\i chacham} must not have been a standard {\i heter} based on {\i ta\u8217'ut} or {\i petach} \u8212- which {\i Beit Shammai} would not recognize \u8212- but rather a claim that the formula of the vow was insufficiently precise. The {\i masechet} itself opened with the various formulations that constitute valid or invalid acceptance of {\i nezirut}, and there are cases where one might legitimately ask whether the precise formula was ever uttered. That type of challenge is one even {\i Beit Shammai} would entertain. According to {\i Beit Hillel}, of course, a broader claim of {\i ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- had I thought it through I would not have accepted \u8212- is itself sufficient grounds for a {\i chacham} to release the vow. In this {\i mishna}, whatever the basis of the request, the {\i chacham} ruled that he could not help, and the person remains bound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} teaches that in such a case the person counts his {\i nezirut} from the time he originally accepted the vow \u8212- {\i monei mishaa shenadar}. On its face this seems obvious: of course he counts from the onset. The {\i gemara} will therefore assume that the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishna} is that this is true even if, in the interim, the {\i nazir} violated his {\i nezirut}. Perhaps the very reason he was going to the {\i chacham} in the first place is that he had forgotten himself and drunk wine, or violated the {\i nezirut} in some other way, and was now seeking retroactive release. Very appropriately, the {\i chacham} refused. The teaching of the {\i mishna}, then, is that he does not lose his accumulated days. Being made {\i tamei} triggers a {\i korban} and a fresh start, but other violations \u8212- drinking wine, shaving \u8212- do not restart the count; they incur punishment, but the duration of the {\i nezirut} remains anchored to the day it was originally accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counting from Onset\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} will now explore exactly what this counting entails and whether any days need to be made up for violations \u8212- but before proceeding to that analysis, the {\i mishna} introduces the second case, which concerns the animal that was sanctified in anticipation of the {\i korban nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hekdesh Ta\u8217'us Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Goes Free\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i nishal l\u8217'chacham vehitiro} \u8212- now suppose the {\i chacham} did grant a {\i heter}. {\i Hayta lo behema mufreshet} \u8212- the person had already sanctified an animal as a {\i korban} of his {\i nezirut}. {\i Tetzei v\u8217'tir\u8217'eh ba\u8217'eder} \u8212- it goes free and grazes with the flock. Since retroactively he was never a {\i nazir}, there was never a valid context for sanctifying an animal as a {\i korban nezirut}. As {\i Tosafot} explains, this is analogous to a person who sanctifies an animal as a {\i chatat} without ever having the sin that obligates a {\i chatat}: the act of sanctification is void, not merely erroneous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amru Beit Hillel l\u8217'Beit Shammai} \u8212- {\i Beit Hillel} said to {\i Beit Shammai}: do you not concede this very point? There is something striking about the structure of this exchange. {\i Beit Hillel} is appealing to an earlier {\i mishna}, universally accepted, and using it to make an argument against {\i Beit Shammai}. This phenomenon \u8212- where {\i Beit Hillel} and {\i Beit Shammai} refer to a {\i mishna rishona}, an earlier ruling that predates their dispute \u8212- offers a window into the historical development of the {\i mishna} itself. The argument is: {\i ee atem modim b\u8217'ze she\u8217'hu hekdesh ta\u8217'ut sheteitzei v\u8217'tir\u8217'eh ba\u8217'eder} \u8212- do you not concede that this is a case of {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} and that the animal goes free? If so, this earlier {\i mishna} demonstrates that {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} is not {\i hekdesh}, and how can {\i Beit Shammai} maintain otherwise?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Behema Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} responds with a counter-example from {\i ma\u8217'aser behema}. The process is well known: animals born that year are counted one by one as they emerge from the pen, and every tenth one is struck with a marked staff and consecrated as {\i ma\u8217'aser behema}. If one miscounts and calls the ninth animal {\i ten}, or calls the eleventh {\i ten}, the {\i halacha} is that all three \u8212- nine, ten, and eleven \u8212- are consecrated. {\i Beit Shammai} presses: {\i ee atem modim b\u8217'mi she\u8217'ta\u8217'ah v\u8217'kara l\u8217'tshi\u8217'i asiri} \u8212- do you not concede that when you mistakenly call nine {\i ten}, or ten {\i nine}, or eleven {\i ten}, all three acquire {\i kedushah}? This, says {\i Beit Shammai}, is proof that {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} is {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel} responds: {\i lo hashevet kidshah} \u8212- it is not the staff that consecrates, not the act of designation driven by intent. The proof is that if one were to accidentally call the eighth animal {\i ten}, or the twelfth, it would not be consecrated at all. The rule of nine and eleven is not a general principle about the power of erroneous sanctification. Rather, {\i ela katuv shekidesh asiri hu kidesh tshi\u8217'i v\u8217'et achad asar} \u8212- the Torah itself defines the tenth in a way that encompasses a margin of error: nine and eleven called {\i ten} through an honest mistake fall within the category of what the Torah designates as {\i ma\u8217'aser}. But this is a specific {\i halacha} in {\i ma\u8217'aser behema} about how the Torah defines {\i ten} \u8212- it is not a general principle of {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut}. The proof is that even within {\i ma\u8217'aser behema}, calling the eighth {\i ten} produces nothing, demonstrating that the mechanism is the Torah\u8217's definition of the tenth, not the general power of erroneous consecration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Criteria Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then ask: why does {\i Beit Shammai} not respond to {\i Beit Hillel\u8217's} original argument \u8212- that the animal of the person who retroactively was never a {\i nazir} goes free? {\i Tosafot} explains that {\i Beit Shammai} did not need to respond because that case is not {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} at all in the operative sense. That case involves missing the fundamental criteria that would enable the sanctification in the first place. If one is not a {\i nazir}, one cannot consecrate an animal as a {\i korban nezirut}. If one has not eaten {\i chelev} and thus bears no obligation of a {\i chatat}, one cannot consecrate an animal as a {\i chatat}. The absence of the basic legal context is not a case of {\i ta\u8217'ut} in the relevant sense \u8212- it is a case where the very precondition for the {\i hekdesh} never existed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} phrases it precisely: {\i vechan hanezirus leisa hekdesh nami leisa vegarua zeh mehekdesh ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- here, since the {\i nezirut} itself does not exist, the {\i hekdesh} likewise does not exist, and this is something even weaker than {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut}. And therefore: {\i afilu Beit Shammai modu detivtal le\u8217'alter keivan dene\u8217'ekar haneder legamrei} \u8212- even {\i Beit Shammai} concedes that it is retroactively void, since the entire {\i neder} is uprooted completely. It is as if one who has no obligation declares an animal to be {\i lechatas} \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}, it is as though nothing was said, even according to {\i Beit Shammai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then draws this principle out symmetrically, noting that the same logic applies on {\i Beit Hillel\u8217's} side with respect to {\i temurah}. {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} observes: {\i mi-deBeit Shammai nishma leBeit Hillel} \u8212- from {\i Beit Shammai} we can derive the position of {\i Beit Hillel} as well. {\i Beit Shammai} holds that {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} is {\i hekdesh}, and yet they would concede that if the {\i nazir} obtains a {\i heter} and retroactively was never a {\i nazir}, the animal is not consecrated \u8212- {\i keivan de-iglei milta d\u8217'lav shapir nazar}, once it emerges that he was never properly a {\i nazir}, the fundamental basis for the {\i hekdesh} is gone. By the same token, {\i Beit Hillel}, who hold that {\i temurah b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} is {\i temurah} \u8212- {\i hani milei heicha d\u8217'ika ikar hekdesh}, that applies when the original animal is indeed consecrated. But if the original {\i hekdesh} itself is undermined \u8212- {\i itakar ikar hekdesh, itakar nami temurah} \u8212- the {\i temurah} falls away as well. For {\i Beit Hillel}, the original animal must be {\i hekdesh} before {\i ta\u8217'ut} can operate; for {\i Beit Shammai}, one must actually be a {\i nazir} before an animal can be sanctified as a {\i korban nezirut}. In both cases, {\i ta\u8217'ut} is a principle that can apply within a valid framework \u8212- but it cannot substitute for the framework itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Making Up Days Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Violation Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the first clause of the {\i mishna} \u8212- {\i moneh mishaa shenadar}, that one counts from the day one accepted the {\i nezirut} \u8212- and asks: {\i mani masnisin?} Whose view does this {\i mishna} represent? And the {\i gemara} answers, surprisingly: {\i lo Rabbi Yosei velo Rabbanan} \u8212- it fits neither {\i Rabbi Yosei} nor the {\i Rabbanan} as they appear in a {\i braita}. The {\i gemara} is assuming throughout that the {\i mishna} is addressing a case where the {\i nazir} violated his {\i nezirut} \u8212- by drinking wine, for example \u8212- and that the {\i chiddush} is that despite the violation, one does not lose the days already counted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Days Makeup Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} teaches: {\i mi shenadar ve\u8217'avar al neziruso, ein nizkakin lo ela im kein moneh bahen issur keyamim shenahag bahen hetter} \u8212- one who vowed and violated his {\i nezirut} is not received, meaning we do not accept his {\i korbanos}, unless he first makes up, through days of prohibited conduct, as many days as he conducted himself permissibly. So if he accepted a {\i nezirut} of a hundred days and drank wine for forty-five, he must complete a full hundred days of abstention before the {\i korbanos} can be brought. {\i Rabbi Yosei} disagrees: {\i dayo shloshim yom} \u8212- thirty days is sufficient, a complete standard unit of {\i nezirut}, even if many more were violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Either way, according to both {\i Rabbi Yosei} and the {\i Rabbanan}, some makeup is required. Yet the {\i mishna} appears to say simply that one counts from the onset, with no mention of additional days. The {\i gemara} notes that if this is the {\i Rabbanan}, the {\i mishna} is difficult in a case of {\i nezirut mu\u8217'etes} \u8212- a short {\i nezirut} \u8212- because the full amount of violated days must be made up. And if it is {\i Rabbi Yosei}, it is difficult in a case of {\i nezirut meruba} \u8212- a long {\i nezirut} \u8212- because even thirty additional days must be made up, and the {\i mishna} implies none are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Views Possible\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by offering a dual reading: {\i ee ba\u8217'is eima Rabbi Yosei, ee ba\u8217'is eima Rabbanan} \u8212- one can read the {\i mishna} as consistent with either position, provided one specifies the length of the {\i nezirut} involved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reading the {\i mishna} according to {\i Rabbi Yosei} is possible in one of two ways. One bold reading is that {\i dayo shloshim yom} does not mean {\i make up thirty days} but rather {\i thirty days is enough} \u8212- meaning {\i Rabbi Yosei} fundamentally rejects the requirement to make up violated days. The {\i braita} addresses a {\i nezirut mu\u8217'etes}, and {\i Rabbi Yosei\u8217's} statement means: he has served his thirty days, I do not require anything further. Under this reading, {\i Rabbi Yosei} and the {\i mishna} align perfectly \u8212- neither requires makeup days \u8212- and the {\i mishna} can be speaking of a long {\i nezirut}. A second reading takes {\i dayo shloshim yom} to mean that thirty additional days must indeed be made up when many were violated, but that for a short {\i nezirut} with few violations, {\i Rabbi Yosei} may not require makeup at all. The {\i mishna} would then address a {\i nezirut mu\u8217'etes} with few violations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reading the {\i mishna} according to the {\i Rabbanan} requires a more radical re-reading of the word {\i mishaa} itself. Rather than {\i moneh mishaa shenadar} \u8212- he counts from the moment he accepted \u8212- the {\i gemara} suggests reading it as {\i moneh ke-mishaa shenadar}, meaning he must recount all the days that have elapsed since he accepted the {\i nezirut}, because those are precisely the days he spent in violation. By inserting a {\i chaf} of comparison, the {\i mishna} is no longer saying he counts forward from the start but that he must reckon and make up every day since the start. Though no violation is explicitly mentioned in the {\i mishna}, the {\i gemara} takes for granted that this is the operative assumption \u8212- otherwise the {\i mishna} would be stating something entirely obvious.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stolen Animal Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Stolen Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The following {\i mishna} addresses a different form of {\i nezirut b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut}, this one based on a mistaken factual premise at the time the vow was accepted. {\i Mi shenadar b\u8217'nazir ve-halach le-havi et behemto, matza she-nigneva} \u8212- someone accepted {\i nezirut} and went to retrieve his animal in anticipation of bringing his {\i korbanos}, only to discover it had been stolen. This person is coming to a {\i chacham} and claiming: I am a poor man; I only ever intended to become a {\i nazir} on the assumption that this animal was available to me. Had I known it was stolen, I never would have accepted {\i nezirut}. So what is the {\i halacha}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the stolen animal unfolds across what follows, but the essential analytical framework is introduced here. It involves the principle known as {\i ein potchin b\u8217'nolad} \u8212- one cannot use as the basis for a {\i heter} something that did not yet exist or was not yet known at the time the vow was made. The {\i nazir\u8217's} claim depends on the animal\u8217's status at the time of his vow, and the {\i mishna} will draw a precise line depending on exactly when the theft occurred relative to when the vow was taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before/After Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Im ad she-lo nigneva behemto nadar, harei zeh nazir} \u8212- if the theft occurred only after he had already accepted the {\i nezirut}, then he is a {\i nazir}. At the time he took the vow, his assumption was correct: the animal was his. The subsequent theft could not have been anticipated and cannot retroactively define his state of mind as having been mistaken. {\i Ve\u8217'im mishe-nigneva behemto nadar, eino nazir} \u8212- but if the animal had already been stolen at the moment he accepted the {\i nezirut}, even though he was unaware of this, then he is not a {\i nazir}. In this case, the factual premise underlying his acceptance was false at the very moment of the vow. A {\i chacham} can question him: had you known at the time you accepted {\i nezirut} that your animal had already been stolen, would you have done so? He says no. That constitutes a classic {\i ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- the vow was accepted under a mistaken factual assumption, and under {\i Beit Hillel\u8217's} framework (since {\i Beit Shammai} does not really accept {\i nezirut b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut}), the {\i chacham} can release him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The release in such a case works through the process of {\i hatara} as it operates in {\i hilchot nedarim} generally. When a vow is taken on the basis of an unexplicated assumption that turns out to be false, a {\i chacham} can serve as the mechanism for release. This is not explicit in the {\i mishna} itself \u8212- we are reading in what we know from {\i Masechet Nedarim} \u8212- but that is the operative framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Destruction E (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Potchin B\u8217'Nolad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle at stake in the next case is {\i ein potchin b\u8217'nolad}: one may not open the door for a {\i heter} by invoking something that had not yet come into being at the time the vow was taken. Had the theft not yet occurred, it was not a fact in the world and not something the person could have anticipated. We are not fortune tellers. One cannot retroactively say: had I known what the future held, I would not have taken the vow \u8212- because there was no way to know the future. However, if the thing in question was something reasonably foreseeable \u8212- a {\i nolad hashachiach}, something likely to occur \u8212- then it may be used as a {\i petach} even if it had not yet occurred at the time of the vow. The precise line between what could and could not have been anticipated will be central to the discussions that follow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stolen Animal Case (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachum HaMedai Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} introduces the following arresting case: {\i keshe\u8217'alu nezirim min ha-golah} \u8212- when {\i nezirim} came up from the exile to bring their {\i korbanos} in Jerusalem \u8212- {\i u\u8217'matzu Beit HaMikdash charev} \u8212- and found that the {\i Beit HaMikdash} had been destroyed. This is a remarkable historical vignette: people who had accepted {\i nezirut} in the diaspora, who had not yet heard of the destruction, arriving in Jerusalem with their animals ready to bring {\i korbanos}, only to encounter the ruins. {\i Nachum HaMedai} addressed them and asked: {\i ilu heyissem yodim sheBeit HaMikdash charev, hayissem nozrim?} \u8212- had you known that the {\i Beit HaMikdash} would be destroyed when you accepted your {\i nezirut}, would you have become {\i nezirim}? They said no. And {\i Nachum HaMedai} released them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Destruction E (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachum\u8217's Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nachum HaMedai\u8217's} reasoning follows the logic of {\i nezirut b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut}: since the purpose of {\i nezirut} is ultimately to bring a {\i korban} in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, and had they known the {\i Beit HaMikdash} would be destroyed they would not have accepted {\i nezirut}, their original vow was made under a false premise. This is precisely the structure of a {\i ta\u8217'ut} that a {\i chacham} can address. The difficulty, however, is that what {\i Nachum HaMedai} used as the basis for the {\i heter} was the future destruction of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- something that had not yet occurred at the time they accepted {\i nezirut} and indeed something they could not have anticipated. This is a textbook application of {\i ein potchin b\u8217'nolad}, which is exactly why the {\i chachamim} overturned his ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-cheshe\u8217'va davar etzel chachamim amru} \u8212- when the matter came before the sages, they ruled: {\i kol she\u8217'nazar ad she-lo charav Beit HaMikdash, nazir} \u8212- anyone who accepted {\i nezirut} before the destruction of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} remains a {\i nazir}. The fact that the {\i Beit HaMikdash} was subsequently destroyed cannot retroactively release a vow accepted when it was still standing. {\i U-meshecharav Beit HaMikdash, eino nazir} \u8212- but if someone accepted {\i nezirut} after the destruction, having not yet heard of it, then he is not a {\i nazir}. In that case, the destruction was already a fact in the world at the moment of the vow; he simply was unaware of it. That is genuine {\i ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- the vow was predicated on a state of affairs that had already ceased to exist. But using a future event \u8212- the anticipated destruction \u8212- as the basis for releasing someone who accepted {\i nezirut} while the {\i Beit HaMikdash} still stood: that is {\i ein potchin b\u8217'nolad}, and {\i Nachum HaMedai} erred.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Potchin B\u8217'Nolad Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Silenced\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabba: shetaflu Rabbanan l\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer v\u8217'okmu b\u8217'shitasaihu} \u8212- the Rabbis overwhelmed {\i Rabbi Eliezer} and brought him into line with their position. {\i Rabbi Eliezer} in fact holds {\i potchin b\u8217'nolad} \u8212- as the {\i mishna} elsewhere records, {\i d\u8217'tannan: potchin b\u8217'nolad, divrei Rabbi Eliezer, va-chachamim ossrin} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds that one may open a door for a {\i heter} based on something that came into being after the vow, while the {\i chachamim} forbid it. {\i Rabbi Eliezer} was active at the time of the {\i churban ha-Bayit} and might have been expected to defend {\i Nachum HaMedai\u8217's} ruling. Yet his voice does not appear in the {\i mishna} challenging the {\i chachamim}. {\i Rabba\u8217's} explanation is that the {\i Rabbanan} simply overwhelmed him \u8212- pressed him into silence, whether by persuasion or by sheer weight of consensus. This is a striking {\i gemara}: it does not record {\i Rabbi Eliezer} being convinced, only that he was silenced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Future\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Va\u8217'amar Rava} \u8212- and {\i Rava} introduces a subtle distinction: {\i af al pi d\u8217'amri Rabbanan ein potchin b\u8217'nolad, aval potchin b\u8217'tnai nolad} \u8212- even though the {\i Rabbanan} say one may not open with a {\i nolad}, one may open with a {\i conditional nolad}. The {\i gemara} explains: {\i ami lehon, ilu ata inish ve\u8217'ami l\u8217'chon de\u8217'charav Beit HaMikdash, mi haveisa nadrisah?} \u8212- you could say to the {\i nezirim}: not {\i had you known the Beit HaMikdash would be destroyed} \u8212- that would be {\i ein potchin b\u8217'nolad} \u8212- but rather: {\i had people come to you and told you that the Beit HaMikdash might be destroyed any day now, would you still have accepted your nezirut?}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The initial reading of this distinction is that it resembles {\i nolad hashachiach} \u8212- invoking a likely future event rather than an unknowable one. Perhaps the {\i nezirim}, had they stopped to take honest stock of the political realities and the military situation, might have recognized that destruction was a serious possibility. Had someone they trusted confronted them with this reality, would they have proceeded? And had they answered no \u8212- well, that is not asking them about something unknowable; it is asking whether, had they thought clearly and been properly informed, they would have acted differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh Advice Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} reads this differently, emphasizing the phrase {\i ilu ata inish ve\u8217'ami l\u8217'chon} \u8212- had {\i people} come and {\i told} you. The point is not merely about the objective probability of destruction but about what the {\i nezirim} would have done had they actually been pressured by advisers. The {\i Rosh} elaborates on this reading at length: {\i ilu hayu baim anashim v\u8217'hayu nosnim lachem eitzah she\u8217'lo tidru ulai yecharav Beit HaMikdash terem klos neziroschem, klum hayitem nozrim? Ve\u8217'omru ken hayinu nimlachim v\u8217'shomim l\u8217'atzasam} \u8212- had people come and counseled you not to take the vow, because the {\i Beit HaMikdash} might be destroyed before your {\i nezirut} concluded, would you have accepted? And they would say: yes, had they advised us, we would have listened to their advice. The {\i Rosh} states that this constitutes a valid {\i petach}: {\i pesach havei af al pi she\u8217'haeitzah tluyah b\u8217'nolad} \u8212- it is a valid opening even though the advice itself concerned a future {\i nolad}, because the {\i charat} is rooted in what people would have told them and what they would have listened to, not in the objective occurrence of the {\i nolad} itself. {\i Kivan she\u8217'machmas atzasam hayah chozer} \u8212- since their turning back would have been on account of the advice of others, this does not fall under the prohibition of {\i potchin b\u8217'nolad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with this reading is genuine: the people never actually received such advice. Why should it matter that they would have listened to advisers who never came? Perhaps what this is really capturing is something like: had you been thinking clearly, had you had access to good judgment, would you have done this? The {\i petach} is not about the social pressure of friends but about what a reasonable, well-informed person in their position would have done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prophetic Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yosef: ei havai hatam, hava amina lehon: ha-ketiv, Heichal Hashem Heichal Hashem Heichal Hashem heima} \u8212- {\i Rav Yosef} says: had I been there, I would have released them by citing the {\i pasuk} from {\i Yirmiyahu}, where the people foolishly declare {\i Heichal Hashem} \u8212- the Temple of God will protect itself \u8212- and {\i Yirmiyahu} is warning them of their false confidence. The implication, as {\i Tosafot} notes, is that the {\i pasuk} speaks implicitly of both a first and a second {\i Beit HaMikdash}: people trusted that God would protect the {\i mikdash}, and {\i Yirmiyahu} prophesied otherwise. {\i Rav Yosef\u8217's} argument would be that the {\i nezirim} should have known \u8212- from {\i Yirmiyahu\u8217's} warning and from the historical precedent of the first {\i churban} \u8212- that destruction was a real possibility. This would function as a {\i nolad hashachiach}: something one could and should have anticipated. Had you genuinely internalized what {\i Yirmiyahu} was telling you, would you have taken {\i nezirut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye: nehi de-yadeitun lehu de-yicharav, mi yadeitun le-eimas?} \u8212- {\i Abaye} objects: granted, perhaps they should have known in some broad sense that the {\i Beit HaMikdash} was vulnerable to destruction; but did they know {\i when}? Even if they had absorbed {\i Yirmiyahu\u8217's} warning and the lessons of history, there was no reason to believe it would happen within the span of their {\i nezirut}. {\i Va-achti mi yadeina be-hai yoma?} \u8212- and even the additional {\i pasuk} from {\i Daniel} about seventy {\i shavuim} does not help, because even if one were to derive that destruction was four hundred and ninety years away, the precise timing was not knowable. To say that they should have foreseen not just that destruction would eventually come but that it would come {\i now}, within their {\i nezirut}, is to demand too much. The {\i gemara} accepts {\i Abaye\u8217's} objection and leaves {\i Rav Yosef\u8217's} approach unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion also prompted a broader exchange about the human capacity for self-deception in precisely such circumstances. Even after the {\i churban} of the first {\i Beit HaMikdash}, people clung to the faith that God would not allow it to happen again \u8212- that the precedent was irrelevant, that this time was different. The historical parallels noted in the {\i Rosh\u8217's} comment are sharp: {\i she\u8217'hayu misyaashim min ha-puranus} \u8212- they despaired of punishment; {\i im yeicharev Heichal Hashem od yibaneh lanu bayit sheni, im yeicharev gam oso od yibaneh lanu shelishi} \u8212- if it falls, God will build another; if that falls too, another will come for the days of {\i Mashiach}. The capacity to rationalize confidence in the continuation of what one loves is not a failure unique to any generation. The very fact that {\i Yirmiyahu} was right once does not, as one might observe, persuade the next generation that he is still right.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Six People Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishna} on the {\i daf} presents an intricate series of conditional vows. Six people are walking on a road when an unidentified man approaches. Each of the six makes a conditional {\i neder} of {\i nezirut} tied to the identity of the approaching person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Person one says {\i hareini nazir shezeh ploni} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} if that is Reuven. Person two says {\i hareini nazir she-ein zeh ploni} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} if that is {\i not} Reuven. Person three says {\i hareini nazir she-echad mikem nazir} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} if one of you is a {\i nazir}. Since one of the first two must necessarily be correct \u8212- either it is Reuven or it is not \u8212- person three seems by all appearances destined to be a {\i nazir}. Person four says {\i hareini nazir she-ein echad mikem nazir} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} if none of you is a {\i nazir}. And also one of them may not be \u8212- since one of the first two must be wrong \u8212- so person four\u8217's condition is also not obviously unmet. Person five says {\i hareini nazir sheshneichem nezirim} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} if both of you are {\i nezirim}. Person six says {\i hareini nazir she-kulchem nezirim} \u8212- I will be a {\i nazir} if all of you are {\i nezirim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai: All Nezirim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai omrim kulam nezirim} \u8212- all six are {\i nezirim}. This is the position of {\i Beit Shammai}, who holds that {\i nezirut b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} is {\i nezirut}. Everyone in this group wanted to become a {\i nazir}, and everyone thought their condition was being met, even if some were factually mistaken. The desire to accept {\i nezirut} was present; the error concerned the factual condition, not the intention. Under {\i Beit Shammai\u8217's} framework, that error does not undo the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel: Condition M\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel omrim: eino nazir ela mi shelo nitkaymu devarav} \u8212- only those whose conditions were met become {\i nezirim}. The {\i gemara} notes that this formulation is puzzling on its face, since it seems to say only those who were {\i wrong} become {\i nezirim}. The {\i gemara} immediately corrects this to mean the opposite: only those who were {\i right}, whose conditions were fulfilled by the objective facts, are {\i nezirim}. Under {\i Beit Hillel\u8217's} understanding, if your condition was not met, you are simply not a {\i nazir} \u8212- there is no vow to be retroactively affirmed by the fact that you thought you were right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon: None Nezir\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Tarfon omer: ein echad mehem nazir} \u8212- not one of them is a {\i nazir}. {\i Rabbi Tarfon} takes the opposite extreme from {\i Beit Shammai}: {\i lo nitna nezirut ela lehafla\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i nezirut} was given only for {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, clear articulation, drawn from the verse {\i ki yafli lindor neder nazir}. Since at the time each person accepted {\i nezirut} there was genuine uncertainty about whether the condition would be met, the vow lacked the clarity required. Even persons three and four, who might seem to have had their conditions certainly met, are not {\i nezirim} according to {\i Rabbi Tarfon}: since persons one and two lacked the requisite clarity and are thus not {\i nezirim}, person three\u8217's condition \u8212- {\i one of you is a nazir} \u8212- was not met, and person four\u8217's condition \u8212- {\i one of you is not a nazir} \u8212- must be read narrowly, as {\i Tosafot} explains, to mean one is not and one is, which also fails if neither one nor two is a {\i nazir}. Persons five and six, who conditioned their {\i nezirut} on the {\i nezirut} of others, are therefore likewise released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Person Turns Away\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hechezir panav le\u8217'achorav} \u8212- now suppose the approaching person turned away and his identity was never established. Within the framework of {\i Beit Hillel}, who holds that {\i nezirut} depends on whether the condition is met, what happens when the condition can never be evaluated? The {\i mishna} rules: {\i eino nazir} \u8212- he is not a {\i nazir}. If there is unresolvable doubt about whether your condition was met, you do not become a {\i nazir} under {\i Beit Hillel\u8217's} approach. This stands in contrast to {\i Beit Shammai}, under whose view everyone is a {\i nazir} regardless, and to the question {\i Rabbi Shimon} will now address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon: Safek Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon omer} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Shimon} says the logical position in the case of the person who turned away is that one is a {\i safek nazir}. The status is genuinely doubtful, and a {\i safek nazir} faces a particular practical problem: how does the {\i nezirut} ever conclude? One cannot bring a {\i korban} if one is in doubt about whether one is a {\i nazir} at all. {\i Rabbi Shimon\u8217's} solution is that the {\i safek nazir} must say: {\i im haya kidevarai, hareini nazir chovah} \u8212- if my condition was met, then I am a {\i nazir} by virtue of what I originally accepted; {\i ve\u8217'im lav, hareini nazir nedavah} \u8212- and if not, then I hereby accept {\i nezirut} as a free-will {\i neder} in any case. By accepting an optional {\i nezirut} in this conditional form, the person ensures that whatever his original status, he is now definitively a {\i nazir}, and can bring his {\i korbanos} and conclude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from this final {\i mishna} is a clean spectrum of positions. {\i Beit Shammai} holds that {\i nezirut b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} is {\i nezirut} \u8212- intending to be a {\i nazir} is enough, regardless of whether the condition was met. {\i Rabbi Tarfon} requires {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, absolute clarity at the moment of acceptance, and rejects {\i nezirut} whenever doubt was present at that moment. {\i Beit Hillel} stakes out the middle ground: {\i nezirut} depends on whether the condition is objectively satisfied. And {\i Rabbi Shimon}, working within the {\i Beit Hillel} framework, provides the practical resolution for the case of permanent uncertainty \u8212- the {\i safek nazir} accepts an additional, unconditional {\i nezirut nedavah} to bring the matter to a definitive close.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 33\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Six Vows Re: Reuven\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Text & Translation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the bottom of 32b opens with a vivid scene: six people are traveling together when a stranger approaches from the opposite direction. One of the six declares, {\i hareini nazir she-zeh Ploni} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if that is Reuven.\u8221" A second, disagreeing, says {\i hareini nazir she-ein zeh Ploni} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if that man is not Reuven.\u8221" So far these two are clearly in direct opposition to one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Each Condition Analyzed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Person three says {\i hareini nazir she-echad mikem nazir} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if one of you is a {\i nazir}.\u8221" This condition seems logically guaranteed: either the stranger is or is not Reuven, so one of the first two must have their condition met. Person four says {\i hareini nazir she-ein echad mikem nazir} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if one of you is not a {\i nazir}.\u8221" The {\i gemara} understands this not as \u8220"none of you is a {\i nazir}\u8221" but rather as asserting that one of them is not, which mirrors what person three said in reverse. Person five says {\i hareini nazir she-shneichem nezirim} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if both of you are {\i nezirim},\u8221" understood as referring to the first two. Person six says {\i hareini nazir she-kulchem nezirim} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if all of you are {\i nezirim},\u8221" which seems plainly impossible, since at most only some of the conditions can be met. Prima facie, only a subset of the group should become {\i nezirim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai: All Nezirim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} says {\i kulam nezirim} \u8212- they are all {\i nezirim}. This is a striking ruling, and the principle behind it is that {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- a dedication made in error \u8212- is nonetheless valid {\i hekdesh}, and similarly a {\i nazir b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} is nonetheless a {\i nazir}. The parallel is to the case discussed earlier of one who says \u8220"the first black cow that comes out of my house,\u8221" where the underlying intent is simply to consecrate the first cow. The stipulation reflects an assumption about reality, not a genuine open condition. So too here: each person is not merely rolling the dice. Each is assuming they know the truth and expressing that assumption. Since they fundamentally want to be a {\i nazir} and are simply mistaken about the facts, {\i Beit Shammai} holds that the {\i nezirut} takes effect regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh B\u8217'Taout Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why the concept of {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} applies to {\i nezirut} at all, given that it does not apply to {\i nedarim}. Two considerations emerge. First, {\i nezirut} involves {\i korbanot}, so there is a dimension of {\i hekdesh} inherent in it, and the principle of {\i hekdesh b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} operates within that framework. Second, and independently, the {\i nazir} is described as {\i kadosh yihiyeh gadel perah sear rosho} \u8212- there is a status of sanctity attached to the {\i nazir} himself that {\i nedarim} lack. This holiness-status is precisely what distinguishes {\i nezirut} from {\i nedarim} in {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also the question of whether {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's logic would extend even to a genuine {\i tnai} about the future \u8212- where you truly do not know what will happen \u8212- as opposed to a {\i ta\u8217'ut}, where you mistakenly assumed you knew the present reality. This may depend on how one reads the earlier {\i mishna} about the black and white cow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel: Conditional\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel} rules {\i eino nazir ela mi she-lo nitkaymu devarav} \u8212- the only one who is a {\i nazir} is the one whose condition was not met. The {\i gemara} will address the obvious anomaly in this formulation; the intended meaning is that only those whose conditions were met become {\i nezirim}. In short, {\i Beit Hillel} applies the ordinary logic of conditional vows: you become a {\i nazir} when and only when your stated condition is fulfilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon: No Nezirim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Tarfon} takes the opposite extreme from {\i Beit Shammai}: {\i ein echad mehem nazir} \u8212- none of them is a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Nitna Nezirut Princip\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains that {\i Rabbi Tarfon} holds the principle of {\i lo nitna nezirut ela l\u8217'hafla\u8217'ah}, derived from the verse {\i ish o isha ki yafli lindor neder nazir} \u8212- {\i nezirut} must be enunciated in a clear, unambiguous, black-and-white manner. Any element of stipulation or doubt at the moment of declaration undermines the foundational requirement. This would seem to imply that even a {\i tnai} about the future would be insufficient for {\i nezirut} according to {\i Rabbi Tarfon}, since the declaration must be fully resolved at the time it is made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the language of {\i hafla\u8217'ah} appears in the verse about {\i nezirut} but not in the parallel verse about {\i nedarim}, where the formulation is simply {\i ish ki yidor neder} without {\i hafla\u8217'ah}. This linguistic distinction is what grounds the {\i lo nitna nezirut ela l\u8217'hafla\u8217'ah} principle specifically in {\i nezirut} rather than in {\i nedarim} generally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can understand {\i Rabbi Tarfon}\u8217's position as reflecting the gravity of {\i nezirut}: because it is such a weighty commitment \u8212- and because unlike a physical act, it exists entirely as a speech act and therefore must be fully real on its own terms \u8212- it cannot be contingent or stipulated. {\i Nezirut} demands absolute clarity, a quality that a conditional vow by definition lacks.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stranger Turns Back\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stranger\u8217's Departure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces a complication: {\i hirtiah leachorav} \u8212- the stranger turned back and went away before anyone could determine his identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel: Condition U\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Rabbi Tarfon}, this development changes nothing \u8212- their positions are not contingent on whether the condition was ultimately verifiable. The discussion that follows concerns {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's position. According to {\i Beit Hillel}, since there is no way to determine whether the condition was met, the {\i tanna kamma} holds that no one becomes a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Nazir Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion might seem surprising: surely each person should be a {\i safek nazir}, a doubtful {\i nazir}. Indeed, that is precisely what {\i Rabbi Shimon} says.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's Dual Acce\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon} rules that everyone becomes a {\i safek nazir}, and he prescribes a specific formulation to resolve the legal difficulty. Each person must declare: {\i im haya kidvarai harei ni nazir chova} \u8212- if my condition was indeed met, then I am obligated in {\i nezirut} by virtue of my original declaration; {\i veim lav, harei ni nazir nedava} \u8212- and if my condition was not met and I am therefore not a {\i nazir} by that vow, I now accept {\i nezirut} upon myself as a voluntary gift. This two-pronged acceptance ensures that one way or another, the person is a genuine {\i nazir} and is not trapped in the impossible status of {\i safek nazir}, which would require observing all the stringencies of {\i nezirut} with no mechanism to conclude it through a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara on Conditions Met\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel\u8217's Anomaly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} homes in on {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's formulation: {\i eino nazir ela mi she-lo nitkaymu devarav} \u8212- the one whose condition was {\i not} met is the {\i nazir}? Why would that person be a {\i nazir}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Textual Fix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} proposes the simplest solution: simply remove the word {\i lo} from the {\i mishna}. The correct reading should be {\i mi she-nitkaymu devarav} \u8212- the one whose condition {\i was} met is the {\i nazir}. This is what {\i Beit Hillel} intends, and the word {\i lo} is a scribal or transmission error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Self-Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye}, at the top of 33b, offers an alternative reading that preserves the {\i mishna}\u8217's text. The case, he says, concerns a person who reversed himself {\i toch kedei dibbur} \u8212- within the unit of speech. For example, a person said \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if this man is Reuven \u8212- actually, if he is {\i not} Reuven.\u8221" He rescinded and reversed his initial condition within the same utterance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earlier vs.\u160 ?Later Statem\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, {\i lo nitkaymu devarav} \u8212- \u8220"his conditions were not fulfilled\u8221" \u8212- refers specifically to his {\i earlier} statement, {\i devarav harishonim}, even though his {\i later} statement, {\i devarav ha\u8217'acharonim}, was fulfilled. The {\i mishna} is saying: the person whose first condition was not met but whose second condition was, that person is a {\i nazir}. This is admittedly a strange way to transmit the ruling \u8212- requiring the reader to understand that {\i lo nitkaymu devarav} means only the first part of a self-contradicting utterance \u8212- but it is what the {\i mishna} requires on this interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identifying the Tanna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the final section of the {\i mishna} (where the stranger turns back) must reflect {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's framework \u8212- since for {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Rabbi Tarfon} the stranger\u8217's departure is irrelevant \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks whose position within the {\i Beit Hillel} camp is being represented. Specifically, the {\i mishna} indicates that {\i if} the condition could have been verified as met, the person would be a {\i nazir}, but if it cannot be verified, he is not even a {\i safek nazir} (according to the {\i tanna kamma}). Whose opinion is that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Tarfon} can be ruled out immediately. If it were {\i Rabbi Tarfon}, then even when the condition is verifiably met, the person would not be a {\i nazir}, since at the moment of declaration he did not know whether the condition was met \u8212- {\i keivan d\u8217'bishata d\u8217'kanazar lo yada i Ploni hu v\u8217'ilo} \u8212- and so the {\i nezirut} never took effect. But the {\i mishna} clearly implies that the status of the condition matters, meaning that if verified, the person would indeed be a {\i nazir}. This is incompatible with {\i Rabbi Tarfon}\u8217's position. Through this analysis of the end of the {\i mishna}, the {\i gemara} is simultaneously clarifying the logic that underlies {\i Rabbi Tarfon}\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita: Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Pile Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having ruled out {\i Rabbi Tarfon}, the {\i gemara} identifies the relevant authority through a {\i braita}: {\i hareini nazir al menas she-yehei b\u8217'kri zeh meah kor} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} on condition that this grain pile contains one hundred {\i kor}.\u8221" The person then goes to measure the pile and finds it has been stolen or lost, making it impossible to verify whether the condition was met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon: Safek Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon} says this person is {\i oser} \u8212- he is in a state of prohibition, meaning he is a {\i safek nazir}. This is the same {\i Rabbi Shimon} who appears in our {\i mishna}, and the parallel is precise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda: No Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} says {\i matir} \u8212- the person is entirely permitted, meaning he is not a {\i nazir} at all. When the condition cannot be verified, there is no {\i nezirut}. The {\i gemara} identifies this as the opinion matching the {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i mishna}: if the stranger turns back without being identified, no one becomes a {\i nazir}. Since the {\i mishna} does hold that the person would be a {\i nazir} if the condition were verified as met, this is not {\i Rabbi Tarfon}\u8217's position but rather {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's reasoning: {\i keivan d\u8217'ilu lo nignav dilma hava bei meah kor v\u8217'hava nazir} \u8212- since had it not been stolen, the pile might well have contained a hundred {\i kor}, and the person would have been a {\i nazir}, therefore even now, in doubt, he is a {\i nazir} \u8212- {\i misafek}. The same logic applies to the case of the stranger: since had he come before us and been identified as Reuven, the person who vowed on that condition would have been a {\i nazir}, therefore even when identity is uncertain, he is a {\i safek nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's reasoning is articulated by {\i Tosafot} on the phrase {\i ela Rabbi Yehuda dikree hu}: {\i d\u8217'lo machis inish nafshei l\u8217'safeika} \u8212- a person does not intend to include cases of doubt within his condition. When one says \u8220"if this pile contains a hundred {\i kor},\u8221" the implicit meaning is: if it is definitively proven that the condition is met. A case of doubt is not something the person consciously included in his vow. Therefore, {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds that the {\i tnai} applies only when its fulfillment is unambiguous and proven; in cases of doubt, the person has not activated his vow and is not a {\i nazir}. This concludes the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis of the first {\i mishna}, having clarified along the way both {\i Rabbi Tarfon}\u8217's principle of {\i lo nitna nezirut ela l\u8217'hafla\u8217'ah} and {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's principle that a {\i tnai} does not extend to {\i safek} cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seven Vows Re: Koy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What Is Koy?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} introduces a new case, structured similarly to the first but involving a very different subject: the {\i koy}. The {\i koy} is an animal of uncertain zoological classification \u8212- some type of hybrid, perhaps between a deer and another species \u8212- whose halachic status the rabbis were unable to determine. The essential question is whether the {\i koy} is a {\i chayah} (a wild animal) or a {\i behemah} (a domesticated animal), or perhaps belongs to some third category altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Koy\u8217's Halachic Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i chayah} and {\i behemah} carries significant halachic consequences in two directions. First, regarding blood covering: the verse {\i ish ki yatzud tzeid chayah o of asher yeiachel veshafachta et damo vechiso be\u8217'afar} \u8212- one who slaughters a {\i chayah} or a bird must cover the blood. A {\i behemah} is not subject to this requirement. Second, regarding {\i chelev} (forbidden fat): the {\i chelev} of a {\i behemah} is forbidden because the {\i behemah} is a species brought on the {\i mizbeiach}, whereas the {\i chelev} of a {\i chayah} is permitted since the {\i chayah} is not a sacrificial species. Because the {\i koy}\u8217's category is unknown, the rabbis imposed both stringencies: its blood must be covered and its {\i chelev} may not be eaten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i koy} also invites a broader reflection. The question of whether reality must be binary \u8212- whether every animal must be either {\i chayah} or {\i behemah} \u8212- parallels discussions about the {\i androgynous}, who has both male and female features. Did the rabbis consider the possibility that such an individual constitutes a third category rather than being forced into either existing one? These questions carry obvious resonance with contemporary discussions, but they arise organically from the rabbinic texts themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna\u8217's Seven Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then describes a group that sees a {\i koy} and makes conditional {\i nezirut} vows. Person one says {\i hareini nazir she-zeh chayah} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if this is a {\i chayah}.\u8221" Person two says {\i hareini nazir she-zeh eino chayah}. Person three says {\i hareini nazir she-zeh behemah}. Person four says {\i hareini nazir she-ein zeh behemah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both/Neither Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Person five says {\i hareini nazir she-zeh chayah uvehemah} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} only if this animal falls into both categories simultaneously.\u8221" Person six says {\i hareini nazir she-ein zeh lo chayah velo behemah} \u8212- \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} only if it falls into neither category.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collective Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Person seven says {\i hareini nazir she-echad mikem nazir} \u8212- \u8220"if one of you is a {\i nazir}.\u8221" Person eight says {\i hareini nazir she-ein echad mikem nazir} \u8212- \u8220"if one of you is not a {\i nazir}.\u8221" Person nine says {\i hareini nazir she-kulchem nezirim} \u8212- \u8220"if all of you are {\i nezirim}.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Nine: Nezirim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} rules: {\i harei kulam nezirim} \u8212- they are all {\i nezirim}. This is striking. If one takes the {\i koy} as either a {\i chayah} or a {\i behemah}, then only some of the conditions could be met and some could not. How can all nine become {\i nezirim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} explains that this need not be {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's position. It can be read according to {\i Rabbi Shimon}, who holds that in cases of doubt they are all {\i safek nezirim}. What {\i harei kulam nezirim} means, on this reading, is that they are all in the status of {\i safek nazir}. There remains some ambiguity about whether persons seven through nine might be {\i vadai nezirim} rather than {\i safek nezirim} \u8212- for example, if {\i she-ein echad mikem nazir} means \u8220"one of you is definitely not a {\i nazir} (and I will be one if that\u8217's true),\u8221" then that condition may be definitively met, making person eight a {\i vadai nazir}. Alternatively, {\i she-kulchem nezirim} might mean \u8220"all of you are {\i safek nezirim},\u8221" in which case the condition is met and person nine is a {\i vadai nazir}. {\i Tosafot} leaves these questions open but finds it possible to read the entire {\i mishna} as consistent with {\i Rabbi Shimon} and {\i Beit Hillel}, with all nine being {\i safek nezirim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nine Nezirim or Nezirut?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: Nine People\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes a discrepancy between two {\i braitot}. The first {\i braita} describes the {\i mishna}\u8217's case as involving {\i tisha nezirim} \u8212- nine {\i nezirim}, meaning nine separate individuals who each made one conditional vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: One Person\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i braita} describes it as {\i tisha nezirot} \u8212- nine {\i nezirut} statuses, implying that one person made all nine conditional declarations and consequently bears nine distinct obligations of {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cancellation Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second reading raises an immediate difficulty. If one person said both \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if this is a {\i chayah}\u8221" and \u8220"I will be a {\i nazir} if this is not a {\i chayah},\u8221" only one of those two conditions can logically be met. One might think the two should cancel each other out or that together they produce at most one {\i nezirut}, not two. Similarly with all the paired conditions. So how does one person accumulate nine separate {\i nezirut} statuses?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also presses on the final three conditions, which reference \u8220"you\u8221" \u8212- the other people \u8212- and cannot easily be said by a single individual about himself. {\i Rav Sheshet} resolves this by positing a two-stage process: nine people each made the declarations corresponding to the first portion of the {\i mishna}, giving rise to nine {\i nezirim}. Then one of them additionally declared {\i hareini nazir unezirot hakol alai} \u8212- \u8220"I accept upon myself all the {\i nezirut} statuses that you have all undertaken.\u8221" This one person thereby accumulates all nine {\i nezirut} obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Cancellation Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper {\i chidush} of the {\i tisha nezirot} reading \u8212- whichever mechanism produces it \u8212- is that contradictory conditions do not cancel each other out. When a person declares {\i nezirut} conditionally on the {\i koy} being a {\i chayah}, that {\i nezirut} takes effect {\i misafek}. When the same person declares {\i nezirut} conditionally on the {\i koy} not being a {\i chayah}, that {\i nezirut} also takes effect {\i misafek}. We do not step back and say: since logically only one can be true, only one counts. Each declaration stands independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legal Indeterminacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion connects to a deeper point. The reason the contradictory conditions do not cancel is not simply a procedural rule about how vows are processed. It reflects the underlying nature of the {\i koy}\u8217's status. Whether an animal is a {\i chayah} or a {\i behemah} is not a brute empirical fact that we happen not to know; it is a {\i legal} determination, and in the {\i koy}\u8217's case, that legal determination is genuinely indeterminate. There is no objective fact of the matter that resolves it. This is analogous to what {\i Rav Soloveitchik} said about {\i bein hashmashot}: one view holds that {\i bein hashmashot} is simply a period within which we cannot identify the precise moment night begins; but the alternative view holds that {\i bein hashmashot} is by definition a liminal period, neither day nor night, not merely an unknown but an ontological gray zone. On the second view, it makes no sense to say {\i mi-manafshach} (one way or the other). The period simply is what it is. So too with the {\i koy}: since its classification is legally indeterminate \u8212- it may be both, neither, or a third category \u8212- all the conditional possibilities remain perpetually open, none cancels the others, and all the {\i nezirut} statuses coexist.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Prohibitions Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new {\i mishna} that opens the following chapter. {\i Shloshah minim asurim b\u8217'nazir} \u8212- there are three categories of prohibition for a {\i nazir}: {\i hatumah}, contracting ritual impurity through contact with the dead; {\i hadiglachas}, shaving or cutting the hair; and {\i hayotze min hagefen}, consuming products of the vine. The subsequent discussion focuses primarily on the third category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grape Products Combine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states that {\i hayotze min hagefen mitztarefin zeh im zeh} \u8212- all products of the vine combine with one another toward the minimum required amount. So if a {\i nazir} consumes a small quantity of wine, a small quantity of grapes, and a small quantity of grape skins, these amounts aggregate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kezayit Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The minimum measure for violation is a {\i kezayit} \u8212- the volume of an olive \u8212- of grape matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Rishona: Revi\u8217'it\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} notes explicitly that there was an earlier {\i mishna} \u8212- a {\i mishna rishona} \u8212- which stated the measure as a {\i revi\u8217'it} of wine ({\i ad she-yishte revi\u8217'it yayin}), roughly three to three-and-a-quarter ounces. The relationship between these two measures requires clarification: the {\i mishna rishona} may have applied specifically to wine as a liquid, measured in the standard liquid unit of a {\i revi\u8217'it}, while the current ruling of {\i kezayit} applies to solid grape matter. Or it may be that originally even solid matter was conceptually measured against the liquid standard of wine \u8212- since the prohibition of grape products is conceptually derivative of the prohibition of wine \u8212- and the current position departed from that approach. The {\i gemara} will elaborate on this distinction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Individual Grape Parts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva: Soaked Bread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} extends the prohibition to {\i mishrat anavim}: even if a person did not consume a {\i kezayit} of actual grape matter, if he ate a piece of bread that was soaked in wine, and the total volume of that bread is a {\i kezayit}, he is liable. The wine need not constitute a {\i kezayit} itself; it is the soaked bread that reaches the measure. {\i Rabbi Akiva} derives this from the verse {\i v\u8217'chol mishrat anavim lo yochal} \u8212- anything soaked in wine shall not be eaten. This verse creates an independent prohibition based on the totality of the soaked item, not solely on the quantity of wine absorbed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle will lead into a broader discussion applicable to {\i kashrus} generally \u8212- specifically the question of how forbidden and permitted substances combine when mixed, whether liability is measured by the {\i issur} alone or by the composite. This {\i sugya} is studied in the context of {\i Yoreh De\u8217'ah} and the laws of {\i kashrus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Each Part Separately\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} holds that each of the four vine-derived categories \u8212- wine, grapes, {\i chartzanim} (which, as we will see, are the seeds), and {\i zagim} (the outer skin) \u8212- individually triggers liability if consumed in a {\i kezayit} quantity. The basis is the verse {\i meichartzanim v\u8217'ad zag lo yochal} \u8212- \u8220"from the seeds to the skin he shall not eat\u8221" \u8212- which enumerates each component separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eleazar: Two & One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eleazar ben Azariah} introduces a stringency specific to the {\i chartzanim} and {\i zag}. He derives from the verse\u8217's plural and singular forms that liability for these components requires consuming two {\i chartzanim} and one {\i zag}. It is not enough to have a {\i kezayit} of seeds alone; one needs at least two seeds and one skin (or the reverse, depending on which term refers to which). Presumably the {\i kezayit} requirement remains in force alongside this, raising the question of how these two criteria interact when the quantities partially overlap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chartzanim & Zag\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Which term refers to the seeds and which to the skin? The {\i gemara} quotes the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's identification: {\i hachartzanim eilu hachitzonim} \u8212- the {\i chartzanim} are the outer part, meaning the skin; {\i hazagim eilu hapnimiyim} \u8212- the {\i zagim} are the inner part, meaning the seeds. That is the view of {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi Corrects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yosi} disagrees and offers a mnemonic to prevent confusion: {\i kezug she-beveheimah} \u8212- like the bell worn by an animal. A bell ({\i zug}) is the outer casing, while the internal clapper that strikes it is called the {\i inval}. Therefore, {\i zag} \u8212- related to {\i zug} \u8212- denotes the outer skin, and {\i chartzanim} \u8212- being plural \u8212- denotes the seeds, since each grape contains multiple seeds but only one skin. This makes intuitive sense. {\i Rabbi Yosi}\u8217's identification is the accepted ruling: {\i chartzanim} are the seeds (as confirmed by modern Hebrew usage, where {\i beli chartzanim} means \u8220"seedless\u8221") and {\i zag} is the outer skin.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yotzei Min HaGefen Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gefen Itself Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now interrogates the scope of {\i hayotze min hagefen}. The phrase {\i hayotze min hagefen} \u8212- that which issues from the vine \u8212- but not the {\i gefen} itself. The actual wood of the vine is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer: Leaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds a much broader position: {\i afilu alim velulavim bemashma} \u8212- even the leaves and the vine tendrils are included in the prohibition. The {\i mishna}, which does not enumerate these, is therefore inconsistent with {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's view. The {\i lulavim} here are not the palm branches of {\i Sukkot} but rather the vine tendrils, the climbing appendages of the vine that wrap around supports.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hermeneutic Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute between {\i Rabbi Eliezer} and the {\i rabbanan} flows from a fundamental difference in hermeneutic methodology. {\i Rabbi Eliezer} employs the method of {\i ribui u\u8217'miyut}, associated with the school of {\i Rabbi Akiva}, while the {\i rabbanan} employ {\i klal u\u8217'prat}, associated with the school of {\i Rabbi Yishmael}. This is the same distinction articulated in the thirteen hermeneutic principles recited at the conclusion of the morning blessings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ribui U\u8217'Miyut Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ribui u\u8217'miyut} method operates as follows. If the Torah states a broad inclusive expression and then narrows it with a specific limiting expression, the \u1512 ?\u1497 ?\u1489 ?\u1493 ?\u1497 ? {\i ribui} has already broadened the scope to everything, and the {\i miyut} then narrows it by excluding only the category most unlike the items specified. If there is a further {\i ribui}, we are already at the stage of maximum inclusion and only the most dissimilar item is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klal U\u8217'Prat Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i klal u\u8217'prat} method works differently. A general statement ({\i klal}) followed by a specific statement ({\i prat}) limits the scope entirely to the items specified \u8212- {\i ein baklal ela mah shebaprat}. If there is a subsequent {\i klal}, giving a structure of {\i klal prat u\u8217'klal}, then the scope expands again to anything similar to the items in the {\i prat}, but only things similar to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse concerning the {\i nazir} reads: {\i miyayin v\u8217'shechar yazir} \u8212- a specification (wine and strong drink); {\i mikol asher ye\u8217'aseh mi-gefen hayayin} \u8212- a general inclusion (anything made from the vine); {\i meichartzanim v\u8217'ad zag lo yochal} \u8212- a return to specifics (from seeds to skin). This is the structure of {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}, which is the reverse of the more common {\i klal prat u\u8217'klal}. The {\i gemara} assumes that the reverse structure yields the same type of conclusion as {\i klal prat u\u8217'klal}: anything similar to the {\i pratim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This seems counterintuitive \u8212- a structure where the specifics frame the general, double-narrowing it, might appear to be more restrictive than {\i klal prat u\u8217'klal}. Yet the {\i gemara} treats the mirror image as generating the same result. The final {\i prat} ({\i meichartzanim v\u8217'ad zag}) is understood as limiting what would otherwise be a completely open-ended inclusion, rather than doubly restricting it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pri & Psolet Pri\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i rabbanan} operating under {\i klal u\u8217'prat}, the scope of {\i hayotze min hagefen} is determined by what is similar to the {\i pratim} enumerated. The {\i gemara} identifies the criterion: {\i mah prat meforash pri u\u8217'psolet pri} \u8212- the {\i pratim} include examples of {\i pri} (fruit) and {\i psolet pri} (fruit remnant or inferior fruit). Anything that is a {\i pri} or a {\i psolet pri} from the vine is included.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unripe & Spoiled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Pri} includes the grape itself. Does it require a fully ripened grape, {\i pri gamur}? The {\i gemara} rejects this, since it would include nothing not already specified in the verse. The extension of the {\i ribui} must include something additional, which is the {\i boser} \u8212- the unripe grape. Even unripe grapes are subsumed within the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for {\i psolet pri}, the {\i gemara} identifies this as {\i chometz} \u8212- vinegar, the product of spoiled wine. {\i Rav Kahana} adds that the extension on this side includes {\i invei dichrin} \u8212- blighted or otherwise spoiled grapes, perhaps wormy or otherwise damaged before harvest. So according to the {\i rabbanan}, the scope of {\i hayotze min hagefen} encompasses unripe grapes ({\i boser}) and various forms of spoiled or inferior grape matter, in addition to the items explicitly enumerated in the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, by contrast, applying {\i ribui u\u8217'miyut}, the only exclusion is the actual wood of the vine itself ({\i shibshta}). Anything else that grows out of or issues from the vine \u8212- including leaves and tendrils \u8212- falls within the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 35\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Klal U\u8217'Prat & Ribuy U\u8217'Mi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Hermeneutics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entirety of today\u8217's daf \u8212- Nazir 35a \u8212- is devoted to the hermeneutic rules of {\i klal u\u8217'prat} and {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut}, and it is worth pausing at the outset to review what was established the previous day, including a correction to one point that was misstated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klal U\u8217'Prat Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The foundational rules of the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} school \u8212- the school of Rabbi Yishmael \u8212- are as follows. When a verse presents a {\i klal} (a broad, general statement) followed by a {\i prat} (a specific, limiting statement), the operative principle is {\i klal u\u8217'prat}: we are left with only what is in the {\i prat}. Conversely, when a {\i prat} precedes a {\i klal}, the {\i klal} expands upon the {\i prat}, yielding everything \u8212- that structure is {\i prat u\u8217'klal}. When all three elements appear \u8212- {\i klal}, {\i prat}, and {\i klal} again \u8212- the result is anything similar to the {\i prat}. The same obtains for a {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} structure: the final {\i prat} pulls back from the universality that a bare {\i prat u\u8217'klal} would have yielded, and the result is again anything similar to the {\i prat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can observe an underlying logic: if one ended with {\i prat u\u8217'klal}, the {\i klal} would swallow everything. The final {\i prat}, coming after the {\i klal}, contracts the scope back to similarity with the {\i prat}. Thus in a {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal}, the second {\i klal} broadens; in a {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}, the second {\i prat} narrows. Both structures resolve to the same conclusion \u8212- anything similar to the {\i prat} \u8212- though as the {\i gemara} will note toward the end of the daf, there is a subtle difference in how closely the similarity must obtain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ribuy U\u8217'Miut Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} approach \u8212- associated with Rabbi Akiva \u8212- operates on a different logic. When a verse begins with a broad statement ({\i ribuy}) and then narrows ({\i miut}), the two terms together already generate an abstraction: we derive not the literal scope of the narrow term, but anything similar to it. This is because the reader is already prompted to ask: why did the verse begin broadly if it only meant the narrow? The answer is that the {\i miut} functions as an illustrative example, and the {\i ribuy} signals that the category is broader than the example alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the order is reversed \u8212- {\i miut} followed by {\i ribuy} \u8212- the verse ends on the broad term. According to this school, that broad term would, standing alone, have included everything. But the initial {\i miut} exercises some limiting force, pulling back from true universality. The result is: everything except that which is most dissimilar. This is a distinct category, parallel to {\i prat u\u8217'klal} in structure but not equivalent in outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important observation follows from this: the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} school already generates two types of abstraction from a two-term structure \u8212- one yielding anything like the {\i miut}, one yielding everything except what is most unlike. The function of a third term in this system is therefore not self-evident, and the {\i gemara} will address this directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miut V\u8217'Ribuy Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The correction from the previous shiur concerns {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy}. When a verse starts with the narrow and ends with the broad, the ending broad term does not produce simple universality. The initial {\i miut} exercises a limiting force, and the result \u8212- everything except that which is most dissimilar \u8212- is a category without a precise parallel in the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} system. The {\i klal u\u8217'prat} school\u8217's {\i prat u\u8217'klal} yields truly everything; {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} does not. That distinction will become critical later in the daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Vine Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens its analysis with the question: {\i be-mai kamiflagi} \u8212- in what do Rabbi Eliezer and the Rabbanan fundamentally disagree? The answer is that they employ different hermeneutic schools entirely. Rabbi Eliezer applies {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut}; the Rabbanan apply {\i klal u\u8217'prat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer reads the Nazir passage as follows. The verse opens with {\i miyayin v\u8217'shechar yazir} \u8212- a Nazir shall abstain from wine and {\i shechar} \u8212- which is a {\i miut}, a narrow term. It then continues {\i mikol asher ye\u8217'aseh migefen hayayin} \u8212- from anything made of the grapevine \u8212- which is a {\i ribuy}, a broad term. Rabbi Eliezer focuses on these two terms and treats the structure as {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy}. The {\i ribuy} at the end \u8212- {\i riba kol milei} \u8212- includes virtually everything. The {\i miut} at the opening excludes only that which is most dissimilar: {\i miut shevishta}, the wood of the vine. As a result, Rabbi Eliezer holds that grape leaves are included in the Nazir\u8217's prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbanan read the same passage through the lens of {\i klal u\u8217'prat}. They identify three structural elements: {\i miyayin v\u8217'shechar yazir} is a {\i prat}; {\i mikol asher ye\u8217'aseh migefen hayayin} is a {\i klal}; and {\i mechartzanim v\u8217'ad zag lo yochal} \u8212- from the pits to the skin he shall not eat \u8212- is again a {\i prat}. This yields a {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} structure, and the operative rule is {\i i attah dan ela k\u8217'ein haprat} \u8212- you may only derive what is similar to the {\i prat}. The question then becomes: what is the {\i prat}? What does it teach us?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pri U\u8217'Psolet Pri\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The abstraction extracted from the {\i prat} is: {\i pri u\u8217'psolet pri} \u8212- fruit and spoiled fruit. The grape exemplifies {\i pri}: it is a fruit. Even an unripe grape qualifies as {\i pri}, because the verse explicitly mentions {\i anavim lachim v\u8217'yeveshim} \u8212- fresh and dried grapes \u8212- elsewhere. Vinegar ({\i chometz}) exemplifies {\i psolet pri} \u8212- a degraded or spoiled form of the fruit. From these two poles, the Rabbanan derive that the prohibition encompasses anything that is either a grape-type fruit (even unripe) or a spoiled grape product. What it does not encompass are leaves, which bear no resemblance to {\i pri} or {\i psolet pri}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: should the {\i prat} not require a fully ripened, perfect fruit \u8212- {\i pri gamur} \u8212- and limit the prohibition to that? The {\i gemara} rejects this: if the prohibition extended only to fully developed grapes and their products, the hermeneutic would yield nothing beyond what is already explicit in the verses. Since {\i anavim lachim v\u8217'yeveshim} and {\i yayin v\u8217'chometz} are already stated explicitly, a {\i prat} that teaches us only those things contributes nothing. The point of the hermeneutic is to broaden the category by abstraction \u8212- to the general type, including its marginal cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Term Function\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a pointed question: why did the verse need the final {\i prat} \u8212- {\i mechartzanim v\u8217'ad zag} \u8212- at all? If the verse had ended with {\i prat u\u8217'klal}, the {\i klal} would have expanded the scope to include everything, and no limiting principle would have applied. The final {\i prat} is structurally necessary: it creates the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} formation and thereby prevents the {\i klal} from being read as universal. Without it, the {\i klal} would have been {\i mosif al haprat} \u8212- simply additive upon the {\i prat} \u8212- and the Nazir\u8217's prohibition would have extended to everything derived from the vine. The third term teaches us not only its own content, but the governing hermeneutic of the entire passage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Prat Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} articulates this explicitly: {\i kol makom she-atta motzei prat u\u8217'klal she-hayah besofo prat, i attah rashai le-moshcho u-leduno k\u8217'ein ha-prat} \u8212- wherever you find a {\i prat}, a {\i klal}, and then again a {\i prat} at the end, you may not simply follow the {\i klal} as though it were all-inclusive; you must draw your conclusions in the manner of the {\i prat}. The verse {\i ad sheyifrot lecha ha-katuv k\u8217'derech she-parat lecha be-Nazir mechartzanim v\u8217'ad zag} \u8212- until the text gives you a second {\i prat} in the manner it did in the Nazir passage \u8212- demonstrates this principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 V\u8217'Ad Zag Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond its structural role in the hermeneutic, the phrase {\i v\u8217'ad zag} carries independent content, as Ravina explains: {\i l\u8217'atuyei de-beini beinei} \u8212- to include what is in between. Tosafot offers two readings of this. The straightforward reading is that {\i chartzanim} are the pits and {\i zag} is the skin, and what lies between them is the flesh of the grape itself \u8212- the {\i beini beinei}. Tosafot notes, however, that this is somewhat puzzling as a novel teaching, since the flesh of the grape is the most obvious part of the fruit. The {\i hava amina} would be that eating the flesh alone, having scraped it out while leaving the pits and skin, might constitute a {\i shinuy} \u8212- an unusual mode of eating \u8212- that could exempt one from liability. The phrase comes to negate this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's second reading is more inventive: {\i beini beinei} refers to the small, undeveloped grapes that grow between the larger, mature grapes on the cluster. These grapes are shaded, never develop properly, and will never ripen \u8212- unlike ordinary unripe grapes, which are merely premature, these are constitutionally incapable of ripening. One might have thought they fall outside the prohibition; the phrase {\i v\u8217'ad zag} comes to include them. Of the two explanations, the first is simpler, though it requires a somewhat strained {\i hava amina}. The second requires a more precise reading of {\i beini beinei} as referring to a specific viticultural reality.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Third Term Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established how the Rabbanan\u8217's {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} hermeneutic operates, the {\i gemara} raises a difficulty involving Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah. Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah, as we know from the Mishnah, darshens the phrase {\i mechartzanim v\u8217'ad zag} to teach that a Nazir is not liable for eating pits or skin alone unless he has consumed two pits or one skin \u8212- a specific {\i halacha} about the minimum quantities that constitute liability when eating these parts of the grape individually. The difficulty is this: for those in the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} camp, the phrase {\i mechartzanim v\u8217'ad zag} is indispensable as the final {\i prat} in the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} structure. But Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah has already deployed the phrase for a different {\i drasha} entirely. Where, then, does he obtain the final {\i prat} needed to construct the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Answer 1: Dependent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's first answer is elegant: {\i savar lah k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer de-darish mi\u8217'ut v\u8217'ribuy} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah holds like Rabbi Eliezer, who applies the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} hermeneutic. For Rabbi Eliezer, recall, the operative terms are only the first two \u8212- {\i miyayin v\u8217'shechar} and {\i mikol asher ye\u8217'aseh} \u8212- and he derives his conclusion from a {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} analysis without needing a third term. Rabbi Eliezer has no structural use for {\i mechartzanim v\u8217'ad zag} in his hermeneutic, leaving it available for a separate {\i halacha}. Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah steps in to supply exactly that: the phrase teaches the {\i halacha} of two pits and one skin. The two opinions thus complement each other perfectly. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's hermeneutic leaves the third term unoccupied; Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah puts it to use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cost of this answer, however, is that it renders the two disputes interdependent. Accepting it means that Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah holds like Rabbi Eliezer, and that the question of how many pits and skins one must eat is linked to the question of which hermeneutic school one follows. We would prefer not to tie these two debates together in that way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Answer 2: Dual Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's second answer is more nuanced: {\i ve-iba\u8217'it eima k\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- one could equally say that Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah holds like the Rabbanan, maintaining the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} hermeneutic. The key insight is that the phrase {\i mechartzanim v\u8217'ad zag} can simultaneously serve two distinct functions without being double-counted, because the two functions operate on different levels: its content yields the {\i halacha} of two pits and one skin, while its placement \u8212- appearing after the {\i klal} \u8212- signals that it functions as the final {\i prat} in the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Syntax & Semantics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction between placement and content is what allows the {\i gemara} to defend the dual function. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i l\u8217'mai hilchata katveih batar klala} \u8212- why is this phrase written after the {\i klal}? The only purpose its placement serves is to generate the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} hermeneutic. Its content \u8212- the mention of pits and skin \u8212- teaches the {\i halacha} of two pits and one skin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} presses further: could we not say the entire phrase is there only for the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} hermeneutic \u8212- that it mentions pits and skin simply because those needed to be prohibited, and the {\i drasha} of Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah is illegitimate? The {\i gemara} answers by pointing to a grammatical anomaly in the text: {\i chartzanim} is written in the plural, while {\i zag} is written in the singular \u8212- {\i shnei chartzanim v\u8217'zag echad}. If the phrase existed solely to prohibit pits and skin and to anchor the hermeneutic, there would be no reason for this asymmetry; one would have expected either both in the plural or both in the singular. The fact that one appears in the plural and the other in the singular signals a further {\i drasha}: two pits and one skin. Placement teaches the hermeneutic; singular-versus-plural usage teaches the {\i halacha}. The two functions are thus derived from distinct features of the text and do not compete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Linguistic Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis prompted a natural concern: is it not an act of interpretive violence to the text to simultaneously read the placement of a phrase as hermeneutically significant and its internal grammar as halakhically significant, while treating these as independent layers of meaning? The concern is legitimate, and it resonates with broader questions about the {\i gemara}\u8217's hermeneutic freedom. It is worth noting that this is, after all, a principle of Rabbi Yishmael \u8212- {\i klal u\u8217'prat} \u8212- and Rabbi Yishmael is also the one who holds {\i dibra Torah k\u8217'lashon bnei adam}, that the Torah speaks in ordinary human language. Whether these two commitments sit easily together is itself a question, and the {\i gemara} does not pause to resolve it. The two functions of the phrase \u8212- structural and lexical \u8212- are defended on their own terms, and the analysis moves forward.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ribuy U\u8217'Miut Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Terms Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having clarified how Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah can hold like either Rabbi Eliezer or the Rabbanan without contradiction, the {\i gemara} turns to a broader theoretical question. Rabbi Elazar \u8212- the {\i amora} who applies {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} \u8212- is asked: {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat minalei} \u8212- where does he, as a member of the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} camp, get the principle of {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}? In other words, when does the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} school ever make use of the three-term hermeneutic of {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}? The question is pointed: if the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} camp operates on two-term structures and achieves all necessary abstractions with just {\i ribuy} and {\i miut}, what role does a third term ever play for them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Empirical Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot frames the issue in an illuminating way. The {\i drasha} of {\i klal prat u\u8217'klal} \u8212- or its mirror {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} \u8212- appears constantly throughout the {\i gemara}. Yet we essentially never find a dissenting voice from the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} camp saying: wait, I read this passage differently; using {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} I reach a broader conclusion, and I have a superfluous third term on my hands. The silence is conspicuous. If the two camps truly operated in parallel with strict consistency, we would expect robust disputes wherever a three-term structure appears. Instead, the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} analysis dominates almost without challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concession Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's inference from this silence is that the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} camp concedes the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} hermeneutic when circumstances make their own approach impractical or inapplicable. When a three-term structure appears and the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} analysis cannot be cleanly performed \u8212- whether because a third term has been assigned to another {\i drasha}, or because the structure does not lend itself to a two-term {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} reading \u8212- the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} camp accepts the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} method without disputing it. This is an assumption of practical flexibility rather than doctrinal rigidity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Schools Consistency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation connects to a broader scholarly point. There is an important book on this subject \u8212- Y. J. Harris, {\i How Do We Know This?} \u8212- which examines the different hermeneutic schools and their consistency of application. In the {\i Midreshei Halacha} and in the Yerushalmi, the schools are applied with considerable discipline. A {\i tanna} affiliated with the school of Rabbi Yishmael consistently applies {\i klal u\u8217'prat}; one affiliated with the school of Rabbi Akiva consistently applies {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut}. Crossing between schools is not attested in those sources. The Bavli, by contrast, applies different methodologies at different times, seemingly without concern for consistency. The same {\i amora} may be cited as applying {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} in one tractate and {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal} in another, and the Bavli does not experience this as a contradiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consistency Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This pragmatism does at times drive Tosafot to considerable effort. In some cases, Tosafot notes that Rabbi Eliezer appears to employ {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} in one passage and {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal} in another, and attempts to resolve the inconsistency \u8212- sometimes proposing that the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} hermeneutic is used when it can be, and {\i klal u\u8217'prat} is used when the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} approach is unavailable for technical reasons. Whether these resolutions are fully satisfying is another question, but they reflect the Bavli\u8217's characteristic willingness to allow a single authority\u8217's hermeneutic identity to remain somewhat fluid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prat Klal Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guardian Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this assumption that the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} camp does at times concede the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} hermeneutic, the {\i gemara} asks: from where does such a camp know this principle at all? Where is the {\i pasuk} that exemplifies or grounds it? Rabbi Abahu answers that the principle is derived from the passage concerning the obligations of guardians ({\i shomrim}): {\i v\u8217'chi yiten ish el rei\u8217'ehu chamor o shor o seh} \u8212- if a man gives his fellow a donkey, an ox, or a sheep \u8212- which is a {\i prat}; {\i v\u8217'chol behemah} \u8212- and any animal \u8212- which is a {\i klal}; {\i lishmor} \u8212- to watch \u8212- which Tosafot, working from a different textual reading, identifies as a second {\i prat}. The structure would then be {\i prat u\u8217'klal u\u8217'prat}, and the principle {\i i attah dan ela k\u8217'ein ha-prat} is derived from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a different {\i pasuk}, drawn from the laws of sacrifices: {\i v\u8217'im min hatzon korbano} \u8212- if from the flock his offering \u8212- where {\i min}, meaning \u8220"from,\u8221" functions as a {\i prat} (indicating a subset, not the whole); {\i hatzon} \u8212- the flock \u8212- is a {\i klal}; and {\i k\u8217'vasim v\u8217'izim} \u8212- lambs and goats \u8212- is again a {\i prat}. This yields {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}, and from it the principle is derived: {\i i attah dan ela k\u8217'ein ha-prat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Critique of Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both of these examples are, frankly, strained. In the guardian passage, it is not entirely clear how {\i lishmor} functions as a {\i prat} in any natural reading of the text, and Tosafot\u8217's reliance on an alternate textual tradition ({\i girsa}) to make it work signals the difficulty. In Rava\u8217's example from the sacrifices passage, one can ask: what exactly is the hermeneutic teaching us here? Sheep and goats are already mentioned explicitly. What is the {\i klal} \u8212- the word {\i hatzon} \u8212- adding that is then pulled back by the final {\i prat}? Tosafot suggests that one might have thought {\i k\u8217'vasim v\u8217'izim} was limited to one-year-old animals, and that {\i hatzon} comes to broaden the category to include older animals as well. But this is a somewhat forced reading, and the broader difficulty remains: demonstrating that a {\i pasuk} can be parsed as {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} does not prove that this hermeneutic principle was learned from that {\i pasuk}, since one could equally have applied {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} to the same text. The {\i gemara} seems to be illustrating rather than proving, and the examples serve as concrete anchors for the principle rather than logically decisive demonstrations of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection & Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda midiskarta challenges Rava\u8217's choice of the {\i pasuk} from the flock: why not derive the principle instead from the earlier passage concerning burnt offerings ({\i olah}) from cattle, which reads {\i min} \u8212- a {\i prat}; {\i ha-behemah} \u8212- animals in general \u8212- a {\i klal}; {\i bakar v\u8217'tzon} \u8212- cattle and sheep \u8212- a second {\i prat}? Rava responds that this earlier {\i pasuk} could not serve as the source, because it is defective as an illustration of the principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Behemah vs.\u160 ?Chayah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the cattle-offering {\i pasuk} fails is that the word {\i behemah} \u8212- animals \u8212- when read as a {\i klal}, might be understood to include {\i chayah}, wild animals. If that were so, then the initial {\i min} \u8212- the {\i miut} \u8212- would not have the power to exclude {\i chayah} from the scope of the {\i klal}, because the {\i klal} would have already encompassed it explicitly. One would then be unable to demonstrate how the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} structure limits the category to items similar to the {\i prat}. Rava\u8217's response to this objection is that the {\i pratim} \u8212- {\i bakar v\u8217'tzon}, cattle and sheep \u8212- make clear through the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} logic itself that {\i behemah} here means domesticated animals only, not wild ones. The {\i pratim} define the scope of the {\i klal}, and by the time one has completed the hermeneutic, it is evident that {\i behemah} does not include {\i chayah}. This, however, amounts to saying that the {\i pasuk} is self-referential in a circular way, and the overall impression is that these sourcing attempts are acknowledged to be weak. The {\i gemara} asserts the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} principle\u8217's validity through these examples without providing a clean, transparent demonstration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maaser Sheni Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Passage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to a passage that far more naturally and clearly exemplifies the {\i klal prat u\u8217'klal} structure: the laws of {\i Maaser Sheni}, the second tithe, in Devarim. This passage is taught in a {\i braisa} and is transparently organized as {\i klal}, {\i prat}, {\i klal} \u8212- making it an ideal illustration of the hermeneutic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Klal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse opens: {\i v\u8217'nasata ha-kesef b\u8217'chol asher te\u8217'aveh nafshekha} \u8212- you shall spend the money on whatever your heart desires. This sweeping statement is the {\i klal}: it is broad, open-ended, and inclusive of anything one might want.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prat Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse then specifies: {\i ba-bakar u-va-tzon u-va-yayin u-va-sheichar} \u8212- cattle, sheep, wine, and mead. These specific items are the {\i prat}: they are concrete, particular examples drawn from distinct categories of food and drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Klal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse closes: {\i u-v\u8217'chol asher tish\u8217'alekha nafshekha} \u8212- and whatever your soul requests. This is the second {\i klal}, restoring the broad framing with which the verse began.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abstraction Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The hermeneutic yields: {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal, i attah dan ela k\u8217'ein ha-prat} \u8212- anything similar to the {\i prat}. The {\i braisa} specifies the abstracted characteristics: {\i pri m\u8217'pri} \u8212- something that is itself born or produced from another organism (live-born animals, which reproduce from one another) \u8212- and {\i gidulei karka} \u8212- produce that grows from the ground (wine, grain, fruit). These two characteristics capture what the examples in the {\i prat} have in common.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From these two criteria, certain items are excluded. Water and salt, though fundamental to sustenance, do not grow from the ground in the relevant sense; they are extracted, not cultivated. Fish raise a more complex case: they do not give live birth in the same manner as land animals, and whether they qualify as {\i pri m\u8217'pri} is questionable. The {\i gemara}\u8217's application of the {\i pri m\u8217'pri} criterion appears to track live birth as the operative feature, which is why birds \u8212- whose eggs are fertilized internally before being laid \u8212- are included, while fish \u8212- which typically fertilize externally \u8212- present a harder case. The {\i gidulei karka} requirement excludes items like water and salt still more clearly. The {\i Maaser Sheni} passage, with its natural and transparent three-part structure, serves as the paradigmatic demonstration of {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal} and the abstraction it yields.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Klal vs.\u160 ?Prat Outer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Outer Klal Function\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the mechanics of both structures established, the {\i gemara} turns to a comparative analysis that clarifies the precise function of the outer terms in each structure. In {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal}, the question is: what does the second {\i klal} actually accomplish? If the {\i prat} defines the category and the hermeneutic yields anything similar to the {\i prat}, one might think the initial {\i klal} would have been sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Broadening Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that the second {\i klal} \u8212- the outer {\i klal} \u8212- broadens the scope of inclusion beyond the literal items of the {\i prat}. Without it, the structure would be {\i klal u\u8217'prat} alone, yielding only the {\i prat} itself. The second {\i klal} signals that the {\i prat} is illustrative rather than exhaustive and that the category extends to anything resembling it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Outer Prat Function\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel question arises for {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}: what does the second {\i prat} accomplish? Without it, the structure would be {\i prat u\u8217'klal} \u8212- which yields everything, since a {\i klal} following a {\i prat} is universally expansive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limiting Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i prat} \u8212- the outer {\i prat} in this structure \u8212- performs the opposite function: it contracts the scope. It prevents the {\i klal} from being read as universally inclusive and requires that whatever is derived must bear similarity to the {\i prat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Result\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notably, both structures \u8212- {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal} and {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} \u8212- arrive at the same conclusion: anything similar to the {\i prat}. Yet they reach it by opposite paths. In {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal}, the outer term is a {\i klal} that broadens; in {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}, the outer term is a {\i prat} that narrows. The outer term\u8217's role is always to modify the tendency of the preceding two-term structure, whether by expanding or contracting.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Similarity Requireme (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Aspect Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that both three-term structures yield \u8220"anything similar to the {\i prat},\u8221" the {\i gemara} now asks whether there is any practical difference between them. The answer is yes, and it concerns the degree of similarity required. When the majority of terms in the structure are broad terms \u8212- as in {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal}, where two {\i klalim} surround one {\i prat} \u8212- the broadening tendency dominates. In such a structure, it is sufficient for an item to resemble the {\i prat} in even a single characteristic in order to be included. The similarity threshold is low.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Aspect Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when the majority of terms are limiting terms \u8212- as in {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}, where two {\i pratim} surround one {\i klal} \u8212- the narrowing tendency dominates. In such a structure, for an item to be included, it must resemble the {\i prat} in two characteristics. A single point of similarity does not suffice. Tosafot attempts to reconcile this rule with some of the earlier applications in the {\i gemara}, where the {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat} analysis of the Nazir passage seemed to require only a single dimension of similarity \u8212- grape-type fruit \u8212- rather than two. The reconciliation is not fully satisfying, and the tension is noted without full resolution. The broad principle, however, stands: more {\i pratim} means a higher threshold of similarity required; more {\i klalim} means a lower one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prat U\u8217'Klal vs.\u160 ?Ribuy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Seem Universal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses a final comparative question: {\i prat u\u8217'klal} \u8212- a two-term structure ending with a {\i klal} \u8212- yields everything, since the {\i klal} swallows the {\i prat} and expands without limit. But {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} \u8212- the corresponding two-term structure in the {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} school, also ending with a broad term \u8212- likewise appears to yield everything: {\i ribuy ha-kol}. If both structures produce universal inclusion, what distinguishes them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dissimilarity Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical difference is that {\i prat u\u8217'klal} is genuinely and completely universal: it includes everything without exception, because the {\i klal} at the end is read as absolute. {\i Miut v\u8217'ribuy}, by contrast, does not include everything. Even though it ends with a {\i ribuy}, the initial {\i miut} retains limiting force. The result is: everything except that which is most dissimilar to the {\i miut}. The most unlike item is excluded. The {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut} school does not simply fold the {\i miut} into the {\i ribuy} and discard it; the {\i miut} informs the boundaries of the {\i ribuy} by identifying a class of items too unlike to qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Application Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied to the laws of the Nazir: under {\i prat u\u8217'klal}, one would include even vine leaves and the woody branches of the vine \u8212- everything connected to the vine in any way. Under {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy}, the most dissimilar item \u8212- the wood of the vine \u8212- is excluded. Leaves ({\i alim}) and vine tendrils ({\i lulafim}) fall in between: they are close enough to the vine\u8217's fruit-bearing parts to qualify under {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy}, but the wood is too dissimilar and is excluded. This contrast between near-universal {\i prat u\u8217'klal} and near-but-not-quite-universal {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} defines the practical difference between the two systems at their broadest reach.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Similarity Requireme (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vine Products\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the Nazir context with this framework in place, the {\i gemara} now works through the specific vine products. Under {\i ribuy u\u8217'miut}, vine leaves and tendrils ({\i lulafim}) are included \u8212- they are similar enough to the fruit-bearing parts of the vine \u8212- while the wood is excluded as most dissimilar. Under {\i prat klal u\u8217'prat}, by contrast, both leaves and tendrils are excluded, because they do not share the relevant characteristic of being {\i pri} or {\i psolet pri}. The two hermeneutic methods thus produce meaningfully different practical outcomes for items at the margins of the vine\u8217's components.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leaves Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates a tension that is worth flagging directly. Earlier in the {\i gemara}, Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} analysis was presented as including grape leaves in the Nazir\u8217's prohibition. Now the {\i gemara} states that {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} excludes the leaves ({\i alim lo}) while including the vine tendrils ({\i lulafim in}). Tosafot attempts to resolve this by distinguishing between edible and inedible leaves: edible leaves, being more similar to fruit, are included under {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy}, while inedible leaves are excluded as sufficiently dissimilar. The wood is the most dissimilar of all and is always excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with Tosafot\u8217's resolution is that the {\i gemara} uses the same term \u8212- {\i alim}, leaves \u8212- throughout, without distinguishing between edible and inedible varieties. The same word that was used earlier to exemplify what {\i miut v\u8217'ribuy} includes is now used to exemplify what it excludes. The terminology is not internally consistent, and Tosafot\u8217's distinction, while logically available, is imposed onto the text rather than drawn from it. This tension is noted and left unresolved. The broad outlines of the two systems remain clear even if this specific application resists clean harmonization.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Klal Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A fundamental question arises about the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} hermeneutic itself. If the rule of {\i klal u\u8217'prat} is {\i ein ba-klal ela mah sheba-prat} \u8212- the {\i klal} yields only what is in the {\i prat} \u8212- then what purpose does the {\i klal} serve at all? Why should the Torah write a broad, general statement if the {\i prat} that follows it immediately overrides it and reduces the scope to the specific items listed? The {\i klal} adds nothing to the legal outcome. Could the Torah not have simply stated the {\i pratim} directly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rhetorical Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this directly: {\i dibra Torah k\u8217'lashon bnei adam} \u8212- the Torah speaks in the manner of ordinary human speech. People naturally speak in generalities before specifying particulars. One begins with a broad category and then identifies the examples. This is how human language works, and the Torah accommodates that natural rhetorical pattern even when, technically, the {\i klal} adds no independent legal content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer is especially fitting given that the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} hermeneutic is associated with Rabbi Yishmael, who is also the author of the principle {\i dibra Torah k\u8217'lashon bnei adam}. The two principles cohere: Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's school reads the Torah as a text that employs the conventions of human rhetoric, and the presence of a {\i klal} before a {\i prat} is simply the Torah following the natural human pattern of general-to-specific speech. That the {\i klal} carries no independent legal weight is not a flaw or superfluity; it reflects the Torah\u8217's use of ordinary language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Structural Signal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a further function that the {\i klal} performs, beyond mere stylistic convention. The presence of the {\i klal} signals to the reader that a hermeneutic is in play. Without the {\i klal}, one would have only a list of {\i pratim} \u8212- specific items stated without any categorical framing. When the {\i klal} appears, it announces that these {\i pratim} are examples of a broader category and that the relationship between the general and the specific must be interpreted. The {\i klal} thus teaches hermeneutics through structure: it marks the text as requiring interpretive engagement, tells the reader that the items listed are not exhaustive but illustrative, and invites the application of the {\i klal u\u8217'prat} rules to determine what else falls within or outside the category. Without the {\i klal}, the {\i prat} would be read as a closed list. With it, the {\i prat} becomes the defining example of an analytically open category, governed by the rules of similarity and abstraction that the hermeneutic tradition provides.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 36\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Heter Mitztaref & K\u8217'zayis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heter Mitztaref Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya we are engaged with on this daf is one of the foundational texts for the study of {\i kashrut}, and it introduces in sequence three major principles governing the law of mixtures. The first two were treated in the previous session; today we turn to the third, which is the most fundamental of all. But it is worth briefly recalling what came before.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first principle is {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} \u8212- the permissible food combines with the forbidden food to reach the minimum quantity required for a transgression. The specific application is to the {\i nazir}, where the verse in the Torah speaks of {\i mishrat anavim} \u8212- something soaked in grapes \u8212- which we understand as referring to bread soaked in wine. According to this reading, if the bread and wine together reach a {\i k\u8217'zayis}, the {\i nazir} incurs lashes even if the wine alone does not constitute a full {\i k\u8217'zayis}. Whether this is because the {\i heter} genuinely combines with the {\i issur} toward the minimum, or because the soaking creates an independent prohibition \u8212- a prohibition of the {\i mixture} of wine \u8212- is a question that can be read either way from the language of {\i mishrat}. What the Gemara makes clear, at least according to Rabbi Akiva, is that {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} is not a general principle; it applies to {\i nazir} and, as we will see, perhaps to one or two other cases as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixture Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related but distinct principle involves cases where forbidden food is recognizably present \u8212- {\i nikar}, physically distinct \u8212- within a mixture of permitted food. The example is {\i kutach habavli}, the Babylonian dipping sauce made with sour milk and bread crumbs, which contains {\i chametz} on Pesach. There the {\i issur} does not transform the {\i heter}; rather, even within the mixture, one remains obligated with respect to the forbidden component. The {\i heter} does not lend its neutral status to the {\i issur}. Forbidden things that are {\i nikar} in a mixture retain their identity \u8212- they have not been {\i batel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 K\u8217'zayis B\u8217'chdei Pras\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second major principle established yesterday is {\i k\u8217'zayis bichdei achilas pras}: one transgresses if one has consumed a {\i k\u8217'zayis} of forbidden food within the time it takes to eat half a loaf of bread \u8212- roughly eight or nine {\i beitzim} \u8212- perhaps ten minutes. This applies even when the forbidden food is embedded within permitted food in a mixture, and even if one is not eating the {\i issur} directly. If one eats enough of the mixture to have ingested a {\i k\u8217'zayis} of the {\i issur} within that time span, one transgresses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bittul & Taste Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What was striking about how the Gemara concluded that discussion was that it extended the principle even to a case of {\i min b\u8217'mino} \u8212- the same species mixed with the same species \u8212- where the forbidden food is not visually distinct and where {\i bittul} would ordinarily be expected to apply. The implication seems to be that this proportion \u8212- enough to yield a {\i k\u8217'zayis bichdei achilas pras} \u8212- is itself sufficient to prevent {\i bittul}. However, as a matter of {\i halacha} we do not in fact apply that conclusion to {\i min b\u8217'mino}. The operative rule is that {\i bittul} is prevented only where the forbidden food is {\i min b\u8217'eino mino} \u8212- unlike species mixed together \u8212- and where the forbidden food gives off its taste into the mixture. It is precisely that transfer of taste which prevents {\i bittul}. The practical upshot is that {\i k\u8217'zayis bichdei achilas pras} governs one\u8217's liability for consuming the {\i issur} even when dispersed in a mixture, but it is the impartation of taste that prevents the {\i bittul} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Taam K\u8217'Ikar Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Taste Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now arrive at the third and central principle of this {\i sugya}: {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} \u8212- the taste of a forbidden thing counts like the thing itself. This is arguably the most consequential concept in practical {\i kashrut}, and it requires careful unpacking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two distinct scenarios in which {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} is invoked. The first is what is called {\i taamo ve-lo mamasho} \u8212- the taste without the physical substance. Consider a piece of meat that falls into a pot of stew and is then removed. From a human experiential standpoint, we do not perceive that meat as having dissolved into the stew; we experience it as having imparted flavor. Or consider the Gemara\u8217's own example: grapes soaked in water. After sitting long enough, the water acquires a wine-like taste. The grapes have imparted taste without leaving any discernible physical residue in the water. This is {\i taamo ve-lo mamasho} \u8212- pure taste, the physical substance absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario is {\i taamo u-mamasho} \u8212- taste accompanied by the actual substance, though the substance is no longer distinguishable. If one pours milk into chicken soup, the milk does not disappear; it is physically present throughout the pot, yet it has become indistinguishable. One would still invoke the {\i chiddush} of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} here, because one might otherwise argue that since the milk cannot be identified and pointed to, it loses its halakhic identity and is {\i batel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taam K\u8217'Ikar Ideas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two scenarios point to two distinct underlying ideas driving the principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}. The first idea addresses {\i taamo ve-lo mamasho}: one might have supposed that what remains after the meat is removed from the stew is simply \u8220"flavor\u8221" \u8212- a sensory impression, not a halakhically cognizable substance. The analogy to {\i reiach} is instructive: if a loaf of bread sits near frying forbidden meat and absorbs its smell, no one suggests the bread is forbidden. So too one might say that the residual taste in the soup is nothing more than a sensory impression, halakhically inert. The first {\i chiddush} of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} counters this: no, that taste represents actual minute particles of the forbidden food that have dispersed into the soup. Even if one saw the meat go in whole and come out whole, tiny fragments of it remain. There is real {\i chazir} in that soup, halakhically speaking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second idea addresses {\i taamo u-mamasho}: even where the physical substance of the {\i issur} is undeniably present \u8212- as when liquid {\i chazir} dissolves into a soup \u8212- one might still argue for {\i bittul} on the grounds that the {\i chazir} has lost its distinct identity, that it is no longer a recognizable entity. Here {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} teaches that the very fact one can taste the {\i chazir} in every spoonful demonstrates that the {\i chazir} has not ceased to exist. Its persistent perceptibility prevents its disappearance. {\i Bittul} does not occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quantity vs Taste Issur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both of these ideas converge on a shared conclusion: the taste represents the thing itself, and one transgresses for consuming it. And this is what distinguishes {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} sharply from {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}. Under {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}, no one is suggesting that the soup becomes {\i chazir}. The claim is only that the dispersed particles of {\i chazir} remain present and have not been annulled. One transgresses for the quantity of {\i chazir} actually present, not for the quantity of soup consumed. The soup does not become forbidden in its entirety; only the {\i chazir} embedded within it remains forbidden, and one\u8217's liability corresponds to the actual amount of {\i chazir} ingested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the position of virtually all the {\i rishonim} \u8212- with the notable exception of the Rosh, which we will not pursue here. One transgresses for the actual quantity of the {\i issur} present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, an alternative approach found in some {\i rishonim}, which holds that {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} gives rise to an independent prohibition \u8212- an {\i issur ta\u8217'am}. On this view, the underlying {\i issur} substance may in fact be {\i batel}, or in the case of {\i taamo ve-lo mamasho} may not be physically present at all. Nevertheless, there is a separate prohibition: just as it is forbidden to eat {\i chazir}, so too is it forbidden to eat something that carries the taste of {\i chazir} that originated from actual {\i chazir}. Critically, this is not a prohibition of {\i chazir} itself; it is a new category, an {\i issur ta\u8217'am}. The taste must genuinely have originated from the forbidden food \u8212- imitation flavoring carries no such prohibition even on this view.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishrat Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this conceptual backdrop established, the Gemara turns to a sharp question posed by Abaye to Rav Dimi. The question is straightforward: {\i mimai d\u8217'hai mishrat l\u8217'aitu\u8217'ei heter mitztaref le-issur} \u8212- how do you know that the verse {\i mishrat anavim} comes to teach the principle of {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}? {\i Dilma l\u8217'taam k\u8217'ikar hu de\u8217'ata} \u8212- perhaps it comes to teach the broader and more fundamental principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains the history of Abaye\u8217's challenge. Initially, Abaye had been troubled by Rav Dimi\u8217's limitation of {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} exclusively to {\i nazir}, and had pressed him with a series of questions aiming to apply the principle more broadly. After Rav Dimi provided answers that successfully confined {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} to {\i nazir}, Abaye shifted direction entirely: rather than arguing for the broader application of {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}, he now questions whether {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} is the correct reading of {\i mishrat} at all. Perhaps the verse teaches not this narrow and powerful combination-principle, but rather the universal principle that taste counts as the thing itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishrat Anavim Derash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishrat Text & Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now brings the textual basis for deriving {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} from {\i mishrat anavim}. The {\i braita} reads: {\i mishrat anavim bamayim} \u8212- if grapes are immersed in water and the water takes on the taste of wine, one is liable. {\i U-mikan atah dan l\u8217'chol issurin she-ba-Torah} \u8212- from here you extend this principle to all prohibitions in the Torah. The word {\i mishrat} points to a situation in which there is pure taste \u8212- the water has acquired the flavor of wine \u8212- and the {\i nazir} is nonetheless liable. This establishes that taste is treated as the thing itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal V\u8217'Chomer Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The extension to all other prohibitions in the Torah is accomplished through a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}. The {\i nazir} prohibition is a lenient one in multiple respects: {\i ein issuro issur olam} \u8212- it is not permanent, {\i nezirut} expires after thirty days; {\i ein issuro issur hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- a {\i nazir} is not prohibited from deriving benefit from wine; and {\i yeish heter le-issuro} \u8212- there is a legitimate path to becoming a {\i nazir} (one is permitted to place oneself in that situation, and furthermore one can have the vow dissolved by a {\i chacham}). Despite all these leniencies, the Torah treats the taste of wine like wine itself for the {\i nazir}. How much more so, then, should {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} apply to {\i kilayim} of the vine, which is an {\i issur olam}, an {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}, and \u8212- on the attractive reading \u8212- an {\i issur} one may not create: one is prohibited from planting {\i kilayim} in the first place. {\i Eino din she-ye\u8217'aseh bo taam k\u8217'ikar} \u8212- is it not a matter of logical inference that {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} would apply there as well? And the same argument extends to virtually every other prohibition in the Torah, which is at minimum an {\i issur olam}, unlike {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that {\i orlah} shares two of the three features \u8212- it is an {\i issur olam} and an {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- though on the reading that {\i yeish heter le-issuro} means one is permitted to create the prohibition, {\i orlah} also shares that leniency, since planting a tree is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taam Not Combination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to appreciate what this {\i derasha} is and is not teaching. The verse is not being read as combining the bread and the wine together to reach a minimum {\i k\u8217'zayis} of the combined mixture. That would be {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}. Rather, the verse is teaching that when the taste of grapes is present in the water, the water-with-grape-taste is treated as though it contains the grapes themselves. The transgression is for having consumed something carrying that taste \u8212- not for having combined two quantities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction also clarifies what the liability attaches to. One does not transgress for every {\i k\u8217'zayis} of bread or every {\i revi\u8217'is} of water one consumes; one transgresses when one has ingested enough of the absorbed taste \u8212- enough of what represents the actual {\i issur} \u8212- to constitute the minimum quantity for liability. In that respect, {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} remains anchored to the actual quantity of the {\i issur} present, rather than transforming the {\i heter} into {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question arises: when grapes soak in water and the water changes color, does not that visible change make the {\i issur} {\i nikar}, such that we need no special principle to establish its presence? The answer requires precision about what {\i nikar} means in this context. {\i Nikar} in the halakhic sense does not merely mean that one can detect that something was mixed in. It means the forbidden item is physically distinct \u8212- identifiable, separable, possessed of its own independent existence. If I can take it out, point to it, and remove it, it has not been {\i batel} because it never lost its identity. But when taste or color disperses through a medium, the forbidden substance is no longer distinct in that sense. It has lost its separate identity; what remains is flavored or colored water. That is precisely why {\i bittul} becomes a conceptual possibility in the first place, and precisely why {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} is needed as a {\i chiddush} to counter it. As for whether a change in color, rather than taste, would serve the same function as taste in preventing {\i bittul} for food prohibitions \u8212- the general rule is that color governs in non-food contexts (as in the case of blood mixed with water to be sprinkled on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, where the criterion is whether the water retains the color of the blood), while for food, the operative criterion is taste, since it is taste that constitutes the primary experience of eating.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Challenge (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After Rav Dimi\u8217's position has been explained and the {\i braita} establishing {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} from {\i mishrat anavim} has been quoted, the Gemara addresses the coherence of Abaye\u8217's challenge. His question was: why read {\i mishrat} as {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} when it appears to be the source for the universal principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}? The answer is that when Rav Dimi attributed {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} to {\i mishrat anavim}, he was presenting that reading only {\i according to Rabbi Akiva}. Rabbi Akiva reads {\i mishrat} as the narrow but powerful principle of {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}, applicable to {\i nazir} alone. The {\i braita} that reads {\i mishrat} as {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} and extends it to {\i kol issurin she-ba-Torah} reflects the position of the Rabbis ({\i rabbanan}), who understand the verse differently. There is in fact textual evidence that both {\i derashot} appear in the {\i midreshei halacha} on the verse of {\i mishrat anavim} \u8212- the {\i derasha} for {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} and the {\i derasha} for {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} \u8212- and the division between Rabbi Akiva and the Rabbis determines which reading is operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution prompts the natural follow-up: if Rabbi Akiva uses {\i mishrat} for {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} rather than {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}, where does Rabbi Akiva derive the principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}? This is the next question the Gemara takes up.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akiva\u8217's Taam Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basar B\u8217'Chalav Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha the son of Rav Avya asks Rav Ashi: according to Rabbi Akiva, who uses {\i v\u8217'chol mishrat} for the narrow principle of {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}, where does he know {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} from? The Gemara\u8217's first answer is that Rabbi Akiva derives it from {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} \u8212- meat cooked with milk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is straightforward. When meat is cooked in milk, all that the milk acquires from the meat \u8212- or that the meat acquires from the milk \u8212- is flavor. If you cook meat in milk and then remove the meat, and you drink the remaining milk, you are consuming nothing but the taste of meat. There is no meat physically present. And yet it is forbidden. Does this not demonstrate that taste counts as the thing itself? Similarly, if you remove the milk from consideration and focus on the meat, the meat has absorbed the taste of milk without there being milk {\i per se} remaining in it \u8212- yet it is forbidden. The classic case of {\i taamo ve-lo mamasho} appears to be vividly illustrated by {\i basar b\u8217'chalav}, and one would think the prohibition proves that {\i taam} counts as the {\i ikar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis push back: {\i basar b\u8217'chalav lo gamrinan d\u8217'chiddush hu} \u8212- we cannot derive general halakhic principles from {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} because it is an anomaly. Its foundational structure is paradoxical: meat alone is permitted, milk alone is permitted, and combining them creates a prohibition. That is deeply unusual. The Gemara tests whether this claimed anomalousness is truly distinctive by raising {\i kilayim}: there too, grapes are permitted, wheat is permitted, and planting them together creates a prohibition. The Gemara responds that {\i kilayim} is indeed unusual in that sense, but {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} carries a second, more pointed anomaly: {\i detara lei kulei yoma be-chalva, sharei} \u8212- if you soak meat in milk all day, so that by the end the milk has fully permeated the meat and you can taste milk throughout the flesh, it is nonetheless biblically permitted. Only when you cook them together does the prohibition apply. This is the decisive peculiarity: two things can be combined with full interpenetration of taste and remain {\i muttar}, and become {\i assur} only through the specific act of cooking. That cooking-requirement detaches {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} from any general principle about taste.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a sharper way to articulate the Gemara\u8217's point. The prohibition of {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} is not defined as \u8220"eating meat commingled with milk.\u8221" It is defined as eating meat and milk that have been cooked together. The verse says {\i lo tevashel gedi ba-chalev imo} \u8212- do not cook a kid in its mother\u8217's milk. The forbidden act generates a forbidden product, but the product is defined by the cooking, not by the presence of mixed taste. If one could somehow cook meat and milk together without any transfer of taste occurring \u8212- which the Gemara addresses by noting that cooking without taste-transfer would not constitute {\i derech bishul} \u8212- one might still have {\i basar b\u8217'chalav de-oraita}. In any case, the fact that soaking without cooking produces no biblical prohibition demonstrates that the {\i issur} is not tracking the mixture of tastes. The milk does not become forbidden because it absorbed the flavor of meat; it becomes forbidden because it was cooked with meat. One cannot therefore derive from {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} that taste-as-such triggers the prohibition of the underlying substance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also has consequences for how one understands the prohibitions of benefit and consumption derivable from {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} \u8212- a topic that properly belongs to the beginning of {\i Chullin} \u8212- but the core point here is that {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} cannot serve as the source for a general principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kelei Midian Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore says: {\i ela yalif mi-gi\u8217'ulei ovdei kochavim} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva derives {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} from the vessels of Midian. When the Jewish people took vessels from the Midianites, the Torah required those vessels to undergo a process \u8212- understood halakhically as a kashering process \u8212- before they could be used. The underlying reason is that those vessels had been used to cook non-kosher food, and the absorbed taste of that food would transfer into anything subsequently cooked in them. The verse states {\i kol davar asher yavo ba-esh} \u8212- anything that had been brought over fire must be kashered through fire. {\i Gi\u8217'ulei ovdei kochavim lav ta\u8217'ama be-alma hu, ve-assur} \u8212- what those vessels impart is nothing but taste, absorbed flavor from non-kosher food, and yet that taste renders the subsequently cooked food forbidden. This is precisely {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}: the taste of the forbidden food counts as the forbidden food itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kelei Midian Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis respond that {\i gi\u8217'ulei ovdei kochavim} cannot serve as the basis for the general principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}, because it too is a {\i chiddush} \u8212- a special halakhic category that does not follow the normal rules of taste. The reason: {\i be-chol ha-Torah kulah, noten ta\u8217'am li-fgam mutar} \u8212- throughout the entire Torah, if the taste imparted by a forbidden substance worsens the food into which it is imparted, the food remains permitted. This is the principle of {\i ta\u8217'am le-fgam}. But when taste is absorbed into the walls of a vessel and then re-released into subsequently cooked food, the reasonable assumption is that the re-released taste is by definition somewhat degraded \u8212- it has been absorbed, sat in walls, and been released again \u8212- and thus constitutes {\i ta\u8217'am le-fgam}. Yet the {\i kelim} remain forbidden despite this. The Torah apparently forbids the use of these vessels even when the taste they impart should logically be {\i ta\u8217'am le-fgam}. Since {\i kelei Midian} deviates from the standard rules of taste, it cannot be the basis for deriving those standard rules.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Yomo & Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's response to this is the concept of {\i ben yomo}. Rav Huna bar Chiyya states: {\i lo asra Torah ela kdeira bat yoma de-lav noten ta\u8217'am li-fgam hu} \u8212- the Torah forbade use of the vessel only on the same day it was used, because only on that day is the absorbed taste still fresh and therefore {\i not} a bad taste. This is why we rule that {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, if one cooked in a vessel that had not been used for non-kosher within the last twenty-four hours \u8212- {\i eino ben yomo} \u8212- the food is permitted: because the taste that transfers is {\i ta\u8217'am le-fgam}, a degraded taste. The {\i rishonim} ask the obvious question: if waiting twenty-four hours renders it permitted, why did the soldiers at Midian go through the elaborate kashering process rather than simply waiting? The answer is practical: they wanted to use the vessels immediately. The prohibition of {\i lecha\u8217'tchila} \u8212- which is {\i miderabbanan} \u8212- prevents one from cooking in an {\i eino ben yomo} vessel on purpose, but if one waited, the {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition would not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Akiva, then, the vessels of Midian were forbidden because they were {\i ben yomo} \u8212- their absorbed taste was still good \u8212- and therefore the taste they would impart would be a genuine, flavorful taste, not {\i ta\u8217'am le-fgam}. This fits squarely within the normal rules of {\i ta\u8217'am}, and the principle derived from them \u8212- that taste counts as the thing itself \u8212- is a genuine general principle, not a special law applicable only to vessels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis disagree: {\i kdeira bat yoma nami ee efshar de-la pagma purta} \u8212- even on the very same day, a vessel cannot absorb and re-release taste without that taste being at least slightly degraded. The transfer through walls inevitably introduces some diminution of quality. Therefore, even the {\i ben yomo} case falls under {\i ta\u8217'am le-fgam}, and {\i kelei Midian} cannot ground a general principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This debate also opens a deeper conceptual question about the nature of the laws of {\i kashering kelim} altogether. Are they fundamentally about absorbed taste that will transfer into food \u8212- a concern with what the food becomes \u8212- or are they about the identity of the vessel itself? A vessel that was used to cook {\i treif} acquires an identity as a {\i treif} vessel, and the prohibition is not to use {\i treif} vessels. This second framework would explain a {\i nafka mina} that arises in {\i kashrut} practice: when kashering a vessel, the method of kashering is determined by the vessel\u8217's predominant use, not by the most intensive use it ever experienced. If kashering were purely about extracting absorbed taste, every modality of use would need to be addressed at the highest level. But if the concern is the vessel\u8217's identity, then the identity is defined by its predominant function, and kashering restores that identity accordingly. The concept of {\i ben yomo} fits neatly into an identity framework as well: the vessel retains its clear and fresh identity on the day it was used; once past, that identity fades.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbanan vs Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kelei Midian Special\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that each proposed source has been challenged as a {\i chiddush} \u8212- {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} because its prohibition is generated by cooking rather than by commingled taste, and {\i kelei Midian} because the taste transfer from vessel walls is arguably always somewhat degraded \u8212- the Gemara now draws out the positions of Rabbi Akiva and the Rabbis in full. The Rabbis hold that {\i kelei Midian} indeed cannot serve as the basis for the general principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}, precisely because even {\i ben yomo} involves some degree of degraded taste. It is a special halakhic category, isolated from the normal operation of taste rules, and one cannot derive the general principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol HaTorah Kulah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis therefore derive {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} from {\i mishrat anavim}, reading the verse as teaching that the taste of wine in water is treated as wine itself, and extending this principle \u8212- via the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} from {\i nazir} as a lenient prohibition to all more stringent prohibitions \u8212- to {\i kol ha-Torah kulah}, the entirety of Torah law. This is the phrase that appears in the {\i braita}: {\i mikan atah dan le-chol issurin she-ba-Torah} \u8212- from here you reason to all the prohibitions in the Torah. The phrase {\i kol ha-Torah kulah} does not indicate that the principle applies only at a {\i de\u8217'oraita} level; it means that the principle is not to be confined to the specific case from which it is derived, whether {\i nazir} or {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} or {\i kelim}. It becomes a cross-categorical rule.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir vs Chatas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shnei Ketuvim Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now reach the question of why Rabbi Akiva cannot extend {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} \u8212- which he derives from {\i mishrat anavim} in {\i nazir} \u8212- to the rest of the Torah, the same way the Rabbis extend {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} from {\i mishrat anavim} everywhere. The Gemara\u8217's answer invokes the rule of {\i shnei ketuvim ha-ba\u8217'im ke-echad ein melamdin} \u8212- when an identical rule appears in two separate places in the Torah, that redundancy indicates that the rule is exceptional rather than general. Had the Torah intended the rule to apply everywhere, it would have stated it once and we would derive the general principle. The fact that it is stated twice suggests instead that the two cases are precisely the exceptions, not illustrations of a rule. The rule as such does not exist; only those two cases carry that {\i din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second locus of {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}, alongside {\i nazir}, is the {\i korban chatas} \u8212- the sin-offering. The verse states {\i kol asher yiga bivsara yikdash} \u8212- anything that touches the flesh of the {\i chatas} becomes sanctified. The Gemara understands this through the word {\i bivsara}: the contact must result in actual absorption, {\i ad she-yivla bivsarato} \u8212- until it is absorbed into its flesh. Once absorbed, the absorbing object takes on the status of the {\i chatas}: if the {\i chatas} was invalid, the other object becomes invalid; if it was valid, the other object must be eaten under the stringencies of the {\i chatas} \u8212- by {\i kohanim} only, within one day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not yet transparently {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} \u8212- it might appear to be no more than {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} applied within {\i kodashim}. Tosafot clarifies the connection. Since we already know {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} as a general principle from elsewhere, the Torah\u8217's reiteration of the principle in the context of {\i chatas} cannot be teaching {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} as such \u8212- we already know that. It must therefore be teaching something beyond {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}: namely, that if half a {\i zayit} of {\i chatas} fat is absorbed into half a {\i zayit} of {\i shlamim} flesh, one who eats a {\i k\u8217'zayis} of that combined mixture is liable as though having eaten a {\i k\u8217'zayis} of {\i chatas}. The {\i heter} \u8212- the {\i shlamim} portion \u8212- combines with the {\i issur} \u8212- the {\i chatas} portion \u8212- to constitute the minimum quantity. That is {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva vs Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's reading, then, is that both {\i mishrat} in {\i nazir} and the {\i chatas} verse teach {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}. Since the rule appears in two places, the principle of {\i shnei ketuvim ha-ba\u8217'im ke-echad} applies, and we cannot generalize: {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} applies only in {\i nazir} and in {\i chatas}, nowhere else. The Rabbis, by contrast, read {\i mishrat} in {\i nazir} as teaching {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}, which is a general principle applicable everywhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodashim Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis\u8217' counter-argument is that the two cases are not truly redundant, because {\i tzrichi} \u8212- both were necessary. Had the Torah written the {\i chatas} rule alone, one could never have derived {\i nazir} from it: {\i mi-kodashim lo yafinan} \u8212- we do not apply stringencies of {\i kodashim} to ordinary prohibitions outside the Temple domain. So the {\i chatas} case, standing alone, would be understood as a unique law of {\i kodashim} inapplicable beyond that domain. Conversely, had the Torah written it only in {\i nazir}, one could not have derived {\i chatas} from it \u8212- because {\i nazir} carries its own distinctive stringency, in that even the grape skin ({\i chartzan}) and pit are forbidden to the {\i nazir}, which might lead us to say that this combination-principle is unique to {\i nazir}\u8217's unusually comprehensive prohibitions and does not apply even to {\i chatas}. Since both cases were necessary, there is no redundancy, and the rule of {\i shnei ketuvim ha-ba\u8217'im ke-echad} does not apply; one can in principle derive a general rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes with the full positions of both sides articulated precisely. The Rabbis hold that what is written in {\i nazir} \u8212- specifically {\i mishrat anavim} \u8212- teaches {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}, a general principle extending to all prohibitions in the Torah. The {\i chatas} verse teaches {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}, but that is a {\i chumra} unique to {\i kodashim}, not derivable elsewhere: {\i chumra mi-kodashim lo gamrinan}. And {\i mishrat} in {\i nazir} extends everywhere \u8212- {\i mikan atah dan le-chol issurin she-ba-Torah} \u8212- because what is written in the domain of {\i nazir}, a lenient prohibition, certainly applies to all more stringent prohibitions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva, by contrast, takes {\i mishrat} in {\i nazir} as teaching {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}. He already knows {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} from {\i kelei Midian} \u8212- specifically from the {\i ben yomo} case, in which the taste transferred from the vessel was still fresh and full, fitting normal taste rules. Since he derives {\i taam k\u8217'ikar} independently from {\i kelei Midian}, he is free to use {\i mishrat} for the different and more powerful principle of {\i heter mitztaref le-issur}. And since {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} then appears in two places \u8212- {\i nazir} and {\i chatas} \u8212- the rule of {\i shnei ketuvim ha-ba\u8217'im ke-echad} applies, and Rabbi Akiva limits {\i heter mitztaref le-issur} to precisely those two cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is thus elegant and symmetrical. One way or another, there exists a Torah-level principle of {\i taam k\u8217'ikar}: for the Rabbis, it derives from {\i mishrat anavim} and applies universally; for Rabbi Akiva, it derives from {\i kelei Midian} and applies equally universally. The difference is only in what one does with {\i mishrat anavim} in {\i nazir} \u8212- whether it teaches the general principle of taste, or the narrower and more intense principle of combination.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 40\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shiurim: Hairs & Impleme\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda: Shiurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of the discussion concerning the prohibition against a {\i nazir} cutting his hair. The Torah states {\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho}\u8212-a razor shall not pass over his head\u8212-and the preceding sugya examined whether this prohibition encompasses implements other than a literal razor. By way of summary: one {\i beraita} derived from the {\i pasuk} of {\i kadosh yihyeh gadel perah se\u8217'ar rosho}, a {\i mitzvat aseh}, that even methods falling short of a razor are included in some form of prohibition. Tosafot understood this to cover even a trim that is not {\i ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar}\u8212-not close to the root\u8212-constituting a violation of the {\i aseh} but not of the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}. A second {\i beraita} extended the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} to anything resembling a razor, reading {\i lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho} as encompassing any {\i ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar}. Rabbi Yonatan, by contrast, held that only a literal razor triggers any biblical prohibition at all; anything else is entirely exempt. Rebbe read {\i ta\u8217'ar} literally, yet the Gemara\u8217's conclusion is that even Rebbe would agree that something resembling a razor entails a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, while using a razor itself produces two {\i lavin}\u8212-{\i ta\u8217'ar} and {\i lo ya\u8217'avor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that framework in place, the Gemara turns from the question of which implements are prohibited to the question of how many hairs must be involved for various legal consequences. Rav Chisda teaches: to incur lashes ({\i lalkot}), even cutting a single hair suffices\u8212-{\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho} does not require removing all of one\u8217's hair. To prevent fulfillment of the {\i mitzvah} ({\i le\u8217'akev})\u8212-that is, when a {\i nazir} shaves at the conclusion of his {\i nezirut} in order to bring his {\i korbanot}\u8212-he must remove virtually all his hair, and leaving two hairs means the {\i mitzvah} has not been fulfilled, whereas leaving only one hair is close enough that it counts as having shaved the entirety. Tosafot notes that these {\i shiurim} are {\i halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}: they are transmitted measures without a derivable textual reason, though there is a certain internal logic to each.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Soter: Rov Rosho\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third category is {\i listor}\u8212-interfering with the count of the {\i nezirut}. As discussed previously, the Rambam holds that shaving obligates the {\i nazir} to wait thirty days, while Tosafot holds that the {\i nazir} must ensure thirty days of growth before the conclusion of his {\i nezirut}. Taking Tosafot\u8217's approach, the question of {\i soter} is essentially whether a given cutting disrupts the growth record sufficiently to require additional days. The Gemara teaches: {\i eino soter ela be-rov rosho}\u8212-it only constitutes {\i soter} if the majority of the head is shaved. This makes sense within Tosafot\u8217's framework: if only a minority of hairs were removed, the majority still carries the requisite thirty days of growth, and the shaving of the minority does not undermine the {\i nezirut} as a whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Soter: Razors Only?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i u-ve-ta\u8217'ar}\u8212-\u8220"and with a razor\u8221"\u8212-appears at the end of the {\i beraita}. The question arises: to which of the three cases does this word attach? It clearly applies to the {\i mitzvah} of shaving at the conclusion of {\i nezirut}, and presumably applies as well to the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}. But what of {\i soter}? Is it only when the {\i nazir} shaves with a razor that his count is disrupted, or does even shaving by other means\u8212-a depilatory, for example\u8212-produce {\i soter}? Tosafot argues that the razor requirement attaches only to the prohibition that results in lashes, not to {\i soter}. However, the plain sense of the {\i beraita} appears to condition all three categories on the razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges the implication that only a razor can produce a violation: {\i be-ta\u8217'ar in, be-midi achrini lo?}\u8212-only with a razor and with nothing else? The Gemara then cites a {\i beraita}: {\i minyan le-rabbot et kol ha-ma\u8217'avirin}\u8212-how do we know to include anything that removes the hair from the prohibition of {\i lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ke\u8217'ein Ta\u8217'ar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves: {\i ela eima ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar}\u8212-rather, read it as anything resembling a razor. This is the category invoked by the earlier {\i beraitot} when discussing {\i tolesh}, {\i miret}, {\i sifsif}, and similar methods. Tosafot understands all of these prior discussions as converging on this point: the {\i lav} extends to anything functionally analogous to a razor, while a mere trim not at the root would be an {\i aseh} only.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites a second {\i beraita}: {\i ve-tanya nami hachi u-ve-ta\u8217'ar\u8212-tolesh, miret, ve-sifsif kol she-hu}, and if a {\i nazir} pulled out, plucked, or otherwise removed even the smallest amount of hair\u8212-even one hair\u8212-he is {\i chayav}, and {\i eino soter ela be-rov rosho}: it only constitutes {\i soter} with the majority of his head. The word {\i u-ve-ta\u8217'ar} here appears attached to the condition of {\i soter}, which would suggest on the plain reading that {\i soter} requires specifically a razor. Tosafot reads otherwise, but acknowledges the plain sense favors the other interpretation. Notably, the plain sense of this {\i beraita} establishes that the threshold for transgression is lower than the threshold for {\i soter}: one hair suffices to transgress, while {\i soter} requires the majority of the head\u8212-and perhaps a razor as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon ben Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda, citing Rabbi Shimon, disputes the majority-of-the-head requirement for {\i soter}. He reasons: just as leaving two hairs prevents the {\i nazir} from being considered to have fulfilled the {\i mitzvah} of shaving\u8212-because two hairs still represent meaningful remaining hair\u8212-so too does removing two hairs constitute something significant enough to be {\i soter}, without requiring the majority of the head. The analogy is admittedly strained: leaving two hairs means the {\i mitzvah} is not yet complete because almost everything remains, whereas Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda\u8217's conclusion is that removing two hairs is a significant act. Yet this is the position he maintains.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Shavings: Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions to examine the three instances in the Torah where shaving is a {\i mitzvah}: the {\i nazir}, the {\i metzora}, and the {\i levi\u8217'im}. Each warrants separate examination. By the {\i levi\u8217'im}, the obligation is most explicit: {\i ve-he\u8217'eviru ta\u8217'ar al kol besaram}\u8212-they passed a razor over their entire body. By the {\i nazir}, the shaving at conclusion is expressed as {\i ve-gilach ha-nazir}, without an explicit mention of a razor at that point, though the earlier prohibition of {\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho ad melot ha-yamim} implies that the razor is the implement used at the conclusion. By the {\i metzora}, the Torah states {\i ve-hayah ba-yom ha-shevi\u8217'i yigaleach et kol se\u8217'aro\u8212-et rosho ve-et zekano ve-et gabot einav ve-et kol se\u8217'aro yigaleach}\u8212-all of his hair, including beard and eyebrows, but without any mention of a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: All Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a Mishnah from {\i Nega\u8217'im}: {\i shlosha megalchin ve-tiglachtan mitzvah}\u8212-three categories of persons shave and their shaving is a {\i mitzvah}: a {\i nazir}, a {\i metzora}, and the {\i levi\u8217'im}. The Mishnah continues: {\i ve-chulan she-gilchu she-lo be-ta\u8217'ar o she-shiyaru shtei se\u8217'arot lo asu ve-lo klum}\u8212-if any of them shaved without a razor or left two hairs remaining, they accomplished nothing. From this Mishnah we derive two halakhot: first, all three require a razor; second, leaving two hairs is enough to invalidate the shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mention Mitzvah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i peshita}\u8212-is it not obvious that the shaving of these three is a {\i mitzvah}? Why state it explicitly? The Gemara answers: one might have thought that the essential requirement is merely the {\i end result}\u8212-that the person be rendered hairless\u8212-such that even applying a depilatory lotion ({\i nasha}) would suffice. The Mishnah therefore teaches that it is not the end result that matters but the act itself, and that act must constitute proper shaving. The distinction between {\i act} and {\i result} is fundamental here: one might have supposed the Torah\u8217's concern is simply that hair be absent, but the Mishnah establishes that the act of shaving is itself the {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir: Ta\u8217'ar Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then returns to the razor requirement and begins sourcing it for each of the three cases. By the {\i nazir}, the Gemara notes: {\i bishlama gabei Nazir ketiv ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho}\u8212-the Torah explicitly mentions a razor in the context of the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition, and from this we derive that the {\i mitzvah} of shaving at the conclusion is also with a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi\u8217'im: Ta\u8217'ar Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the {\i levi\u8217'im}, the derivation is even more straightforward: {\i ve-gabei Levi\u8217'im ketiv ve-he\u8217'eviru ta\u8217'ar al kol besaram}\u8212-the Torah explicitly states they passed a razor over their entire body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora: Ta\u8217'ar Source?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, by the {\i metzora}, the Torah never mentions {\i ta\u8217'ar} at all. {\i Ela metzora be-ta\u8217'ar me-nalan}\u8212-how do we know the {\i metzora} uses a razor? This is the question that will occupy much of the remainder of the sugya. It is worth noting that the {\i beraita} cited earlier in the tractate, which we studied previously, simply took it for granted that a {\i metzora} uses a razor and therefore ruled that you cannot learn the {\i nazir}\u8217's razor requirement from the {\i metzora} since the {\i metzora} is a stricter case, covering the whole body. The Gemara now probes where that assumption came from.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzad Hashaveh: Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 From Levi\u8217'im?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara first attempts to derive the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement from the {\i levi\u8217'im} by analogy: just as {\i levi\u8217'im} are required to shave and that shaving requires a razor, so too the {\i metzora} who is required to shave should also use a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi\u8217'im Distinct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately refutes this attempt: {\i mah Levi\u8217'im she-kein te\u8217'unim tenufa be-gufam}\u8212-the {\i levi\u8217'im} undergo the unique rite of having their own bodies waved ({\i tenufa}), a distinctive element of their initiation that marks a considerably more demanding ritual context. Since {\i metzora} involves no such {\i tenufa}, the analogy fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 From Nazir?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then attempts to learn from the {\i nazir}: if even a {\i nazir}, whose shaving obligation covers only his head, requires a razor, then surely a {\i metzora}, whose obligation covers the entire body and is therefore seemingly more stringent, should certainly require a razor as well. This appears to be a strong {\i kal va-chomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Distinct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara refutes this as well: {\i mah le-Nazir she-kein korbano ta\u8217'un lechem}\u8212-the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korban} includes bread ({\i lechem}, i.e., the {\i matzah} accompaniment), which is a feature absent from the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i korban}. While this distinction may seem tangential to the question of shaving, the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i korban} is in some respect more demanding, which means the cases are not sufficiently analogous to permit a direct derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Denominator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then attempts a {\i tzad ha-shaveh}\u8212-a derivation from the common denominator of both cases: both the {\i nazir} and the {\i levi\u8217'im} require shaving and both require a razor; therefore, any obligation to shave should require a razor, and the {\i metzora} should be included. The argument is structured as the classic back-and-forth of Talmudic derivation: begin with {\i levi\u8217'im}, note that their {\i tenufa} distinguishes them, invoke {\i nazir} to show that {\i tenufa} is not the critical factor since {\i nazir} also uses a razor without {\i tenufa}, note that {\i nazir} has bread in his {\i korban} which {\i levi\u8217'im} do not, and conclude that {\i lo re\u8217'i zeh ke-re\u8217'i zeh}\u8212-neither case is exactly like the other. The {\i tzad ha-shaveh}\u8212-the common denominator\u8212-is that {\i sh\u8217'neihem te\u8217'unim tiglachat ve-tiglachtan be-ta\u8217'ar}: both require shaving and both require a razor. Apply this to the {\i metzora}, who also requires shaving, and conclude that his shaving too must be with a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora: Sliding Scale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava of Barnish raises an objection to Rav Ashi: even this {\i tzad ha-shaveh} can be refuted. What do both {\i nazir} and {\i levi\u8217'im} share that the {\i metzora} does not? {\i She-kein ein korbanam be-dalut}\u8212-their {\i korbanot} have no sliding scale. A poor {\i nazir} and a poor {\i levi} must still bring the same {\i korban} as a wealthy one. A {\i metzora}, however, may bring birds if he cannot afford the standard {\i korban}\u8212-there is a {\i korban} adjusted for poverty. This leniency in the {\i korban} of a {\i metzora} arguably indicates a more flexible or lenient framework for the {\i metzora}\u8217's obligations, and one might therefore extend that flexibility to the shaving requirement as well, permitting non-razor alternatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking argument in the Gemara, because the connection between the sliding-scale {\i korban} and the implement used for shaving seems logically remote. One might raise a far more compelling distinction: the shavings of the {\i nazir} and the {\i levi\u8217'im} were performed in the context of the {\i Beit ha-Mikdash}, whereas the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving is not\u8212-a distinction that would seem directly relevant to the stringency of the ritual. Nevertheless, this is the objection the Gemara itself advances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Learn Metzora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion at this stage is that the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement cannot be derived from the {\i nazir}, from the {\i levi\u8217'im}, or from their common denominator. The earlier {\i beraita} had assumed it by {\i metzora} and could not apply it to {\i nazir}; today\u8217's {\i beraita} assumes it by {\i nazir} and cannot derive it for {\i metzora}. Rava bar Masharsheya explains to Rava that these two {\i beraitot} are not in conflict\u8212-they reflect different positions: the first follows the {\i Rabbanan} and the second follows Rabbi Eliezer. Understanding how those two positions differ is what the Gemara now turns to.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbanan: Razor Req\u8217'd\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peah Zakenim Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces the Mishnah from {\i Makkot}: {\i ve-eino chayav ad she-yelaktenu be-ta\u8217'ar}\u8212-one is not liable until he shaves the beard with a razor. This is the standard prohibition against shaving the beard, {\i pe\u8217'at ha-zakan}. The {\i Rabbanan} hold that this prohibition is triggered only by a razor; plucking with tweezers ({\i malket} and {\i rehitni}) does not incur liability. Rabbi Eliezer disagrees, holding that even plucking triggers liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position is derived from a {\i beraita} that reads two {\i pesukim} in conjunction. The verse by {\i kohanim} states {\i u-fe\u8217'at zekanam lo yegalechu}\u8212-they shall not shave the corners of their beards. The verse by {\i Yisrael} states {\i ve-lo tashchit et pe\u8217'at zekanecha}. The word {\i yegalechu} points to an act of shaving with a blade; the word {\i tashchit} points to destruction at the root. Either verse alone would be insufficient: {\i yegalechu} alone might have included even scissors; {\i tashchit} alone might have included even plucking to the root. Taking the intersection\u8212-what satisfies both {\i yegalechu} and {\i tashchit}\u8212-yields the answer: {\i eizeh giluach she-yesh bo hashchata, hevei omer zeh ta\u8217'ar}\u8212-what shaving involves destruction? That is a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Metzora: Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position that the only prohibited implement is a razor, the Gemara now connects this back to the {\i metzora}. The Torah in the context of the {\i metzora} states not merely {\i rosho} but explicitly {\i zekano}\u8212-his beard. Since the Torah already says {\i kol se\u8217'aro}, all of his hair, why mention {\i zekano} separately? The Gemara explains: {\i lefi she-ne\u8217'emar u-fe\u8217'at zekanam lo yegalechu}\u8212-because there is a prohibition against shaving the beard, one might have thought a {\i kohen} who is a {\i metzora} cannot shave his beard, since the prohibition encompasses both a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} (from {\i peat zekanam}) and a {\i mitzvat aseh} (from {\i kedoshim tihyu la\u8217'Eloheihem}, as Tosafot explains, which encompasses the specific {\i kedushah} obligations of {\i kohanim}). Since a {\i kohen} faces both a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} and an {\i aseh} prohibiting beard-shaving, the rule of {\i ein aseh docheh lo ta\u8217'aseh va-aseh} would prevent the {\i aseh} of {\i metzora}-shaving from overriding this combination. Therefore, the explicit mention of {\i zekano} tells us that even a {\i kohen metzora} must and may shave his beard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yegalechu & Tashchit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita} then establishes the derivation of the razor requirement for the prohibition of beard-shaving by working through two stages. First: {\i yachol afilu gilach be-misparayim yehei chayav?}\u8212-one might think even scissors would be prohibited, since the verse says {\i lo yegalechu}. Therefore, {\i talmud lomar lo tashchit}: it has to involve {\i hashchata}, destruction at the root, which scissors do not achieve. Second: {\i yachol yikto be-malket u-ve-rehitni yehei chayav?}\u8212-one might then think even tweezers, which destroy at the root. Therefore, {\i talmud lomar u-fe\u8217'at zekanam lo yegalechu}: it must be an act of {\i giluach}, shaving. What shaving involves destruction? A razor. This convergence of {\i giluach} and {\i hashchata} is the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's source that the prohibition of beard-shaving is limited to a {\i ta\u8217'ar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora: Implicitly Ta\u8217'ar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the connection to {\i metzora} becomes clear. The {\i Rabbanan} have established that the only prohibited implement for beard-shaving is a razor. The Torah then needs to explicitly permit a {\i kohen metzora} to shave his beard. That permission\u8212-derived from the word {\i zekano}\u8212-only makes sense if the shaving of a {\i metzora} is done with a razor. If the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving could be accomplished with tweezers or some other non-razor implement, there would be no conflict with the {\i kohen}\u8217's prohibition, since the {\i kohen}\u8217's prohibition is razor-specific. The Torah would have had no need to explicitly permit it. The very fact that the Torah grants explicit permission proves that the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving is with a razor\u8212-because that is the only scenario in which permission was needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Mere Permission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara anticipates a counterargument: perhaps the Torah is not establishing an obligation to use a razor, but merely a permission\u8212-telling us that even if a {\i kohen metzora} wishes to use a razor, he may do so, even though it would otherwise be prohibited. On this reading, the {\i mitzvah} itself could still be fulfilled with a non-razor implement, and the {\i pasuk} simply extends a leniency for those who prefer the razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Alternatives Exist\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this interpretation in two ways. The first argument\u8212-that a {\i nazir}\u8217's shaving already demonstrates we can override prohibitions when a {\i mitzvah} of shaving is present, so the {\i pasuk} could have been teaching us something else\u8212-is structurally complex. The stronger and clearer answer, articulated by Reish Lakish\u8217's principle, is this: {\i kol makom she-atah motzei aseh ve-lo ta\u8217'aseh, im atah yakhol le-kayeim sheneihem mutav}\u8212-wherever an {\i aseh} confronts a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, if you can fulfill both without conflict, you must do so. If the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i mitzvah} could be fulfilled with a non-razor implement, then the Torah would never have authorized overriding the prohibitions for a {\i kohen metzora} by using a razor\u8212-it would have directed him to use tweezers instead, thereby avoiding the conflict entirely. The very fact that the Torah found it necessary to permit the use of a razor\u8212-overriding the prohibition\u8212-demonstrates conclusively that there is no alternative: the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving must be with a razor and nothing else. This is the foundation upon which the earlier {\i beraita} assumed the {\i metzora} requires a {\i ta\u8217'ar}: it followed the {\i Rabbanan}, who locate the beard-shaving prohibition exclusively in the razor, and therefore could derive from the Torah\u8217's explicit permission for a {\i kohen metzora} that the {\i mitzvah} must be performed with that very implement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Eliezer: Non-Razors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Plucking Too\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, as stated in the Mishnah in {\i Makkot}, is that one transgresses the prohibition of beard-shaving even through {\i malket} and {\i rehitni}\u8212-tweezers and related plucking implements\u8212-without using a razor. The prohibition for Rabbi Eliezer is not limited to the {\i ta\u8217'ar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source: Rosho\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer derives his understanding of the {\i metzora}\u8217's obligation from the word {\i rosho} in the verse about the {\i metzora}: {\i yigaleach et kol se\u8217'aro et rosho}. Since the Torah already said {\i kol se\u8217'aro}, all of his hair, the explicit mention of {\i rosho} must come to teach something additional\u8212-specifically, that even the {\i nazir} who becomes a {\i metzora} must shave, though we will return to this below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Metzora: Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel question for Rabbi Eliezer is: why does the Torah need to explicitly permit the shaving of a {\i nazir} who becomes a {\i metzora}? The word {\i rosho} in the context of the {\i metzora} is being used to teach that even a {\i nazir}, who is prohibited from having a razor pass over his head, must nonetheless shave when he becomes a {\i metzora}. For Rabbi Eliezer, who holds that the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition includes not only a razor but also plucking and similar methods\u8212-since the {\i aseh} of {\i kadosh yihyeh gadel perah se\u8217'ar rosho} encompasses all forms of hair removal\u8212-it would be impossible to suggest that the {\i nazir metzora} could simply use tweezers and thereby avoid the conflict. Rabbi Eliezer must therefore derive from {\i rosho} that the shaving overrides even those broader prohibitions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer Cannot Assume\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely why Rabbi Eliezer cannot employ the reasoning the {\i Rabbanan} use to establish the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement. The {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's logic was: the Torah had to permit the {\i kohen metzora} to use a razor; the only beard-shaving prohibition is razor-specific; therefore, the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving must be with a razor. But for Rabbi Eliezer, the prohibition of beard-shaving extends beyond the razor to include plucking. Consequently, even if the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving could be done with tweezers, there would still be a conflict\u8212-and the Torah would still need to authorize it. Rabbi Eliezer therefore cannot infer from the Torah\u8217's permission that the {\i metzora} must use a razor; the permission is required regardless. He cannot start from the assumption that the {\i metzora} uses a razor, which is precisely why the {\i beraita} that follows Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position begins instead from the assumption that the {\i nazir} uses a razor and attempts\u8212-ultimately without success\u8212-to derive the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement by other means. The full resolution of that difficulty is left for the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 41\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metzora Shaving Requirem\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Categories Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by situating the current discussion within a broader framework: there are three categories of people who are obligated to perform {\i giluach} \u8212- ritual shaving. The first is the {\i Levi\u8217'im} at the time of their original initiation in the wilderness, where the Torah states explicitly {\i vehe\u8217'eviru ta\u8217'ar al kol besaram}, \u8220"they shall pass a razor over all their flesh\u8221" \u8212- a formulation that leaves no doubt that a razor is required. The second category is the {\i metzora}, the person afflicted with the skin condition described in the Torah, where the verse instructs {\i veyigalach et kol se\u8217'aro}, \u8220"he shall shave all his hair,\u8221" but makes no mention of a {\i ta\u8217'ar}, a razor. The third is the {\i nazir}, the nazirite, where the Torah says both {\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho}, \u8220"no razor shall pass over his head\u8221" \u8212- establishing a prohibition \u8212- and separately legislates the positive {\i mitzvah} of shaving, {\i vegilach hanazir petach ohel mo\u8217'ed et rosh nizro}, without again specifying the razor by name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying problem the {\i gemara} is working through is this: how do we know that the {\i mitzvah} shaving of the {\i metzora} and the {\i nazir} must be performed specifically with a razor, given that neither verse says so explicitly? Two {\i braisas} approach this question from opposite directions \u8212- one takes the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement as a given and tries (without success) to extend it to the {\i nazir}, while the other takes the {\i nazir}\u8217's razor requirement as a given and tries to extend it to the {\i metzora}. The {\i gemara} now explains why each {\i braisa} started where it did, and that explanation turns out to depend on the broader dispute between the {\i Rabbanan} and Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Beard Prohibiti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan} hold that the prohibition against shaving the beard applies only with a {\i ta\u8217'ar mamash} \u8212- literally a razor and nothing else. They derive this from the combination of two verses. The verse concerning {\i kohanim} states {\i ufat zekanam lo yegalechu}, which could encompass scissors \u8212- that is, any blade that cuts. The verse concerning Israelites states {\i velo tashchit et pe\u8217'at zekanecha}, where {\i tashchit} implies destruction at the root, which could encompass plucking but not necessarily a blade. Taken together, the prohibition requires both qualities simultaneously: a blade that destroys at the root. This defines the prohibited implement as, essentially, a razor. It is for this reason that many observe the practice of using electric shavers \u8212- on the view that an electric shaver operates through a scissor-like action and thus does not constitute a {\i ta\u8217'ar mamash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Learning Metzora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position of the {\i Rabbanan} on the beard prohibition is precisely what allows them to derive that the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving must be performed with a razor. The verse concerning the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i giluach} states {\i veyigalach et kol se\u8217'aro et rosho ve\u8217'et zekano} \u8212- it includes the beard in what the {\i metzora} must shave. Now, if the {\i metzora} is a {\i kohen}, shaving the beard would transgress both a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, the prohibition {\i lo yegalechu}, and an {\i aseh}, the injunction {\i kedoshim tihyu}. For an ordinary Israelite, too, shaving the beard is prohibited. The Torah\u8217's inclusion of {\i et zekano} teaches that the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i mitzvah} overrides even these prohibitions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical inference for the {\i Rabbanan} is this: since according to them the prohibition of shaving the beard applies {\i only} with a razor, the Torah could only need to declare an override if the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving is itself performed with a razor. Were the {\i metzora} permitted to shave the beard by some other means \u8212- scissors, for instance \u8212- the prohibition would not be triggered and no override would be necessary. The very fact that a {\i derashah} is required to permit it demonstrates that this shaving is done with a razor. Thus the {\i Rabbanan} learn the {\i metzora}\u8217's requirement of a razor from {\i et zekano}, without any recourse to the laws of {\i nazir} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Beard View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer disputes the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's understanding of the beard prohibition. In his view, the prohibition of shaving the beard is broader than the {\i Rabbanan} acknowledge \u8212- it is not limited to a razor alone. Exactly how broad Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position extends is the subject of the surrounding discussion, but the key point for the present {\i sugyah} is that for Rabbi Eliezer the beard prohibition applies in a wider range of cases than just the literal razor. This means the inference the {\i Rabbanan} draw from {\i et zekano} \u8212- that the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving overrides the beard prohibition, and therefore the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving must be with a razor \u8212- does not work for Rabbi Eliezer. Yes, the verse tells us the {\i metzora} overrides the beard prohibition, but since that prohibition is broader than just the razor, the override would need to apply across a wider range of implements. The fact that the {\i metzora} overrides the beard shaving does not, on Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view, prove that the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving is specifically and exclusively with a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Learning Metz\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer must therefore find a different source to establish that the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i giluach} requires a razor, and he does so by using {\i et rosho} rather than {\i et zekano}. A {\i braisa} asks: what does the word {\i rosho} \u8212- \u8220"his head\u8221" \u8212- in the {\i metzora} verse come to teach? It answers: since the {\i nazir} is told {\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho}, the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition of shaving is explicitly and exclusively a prohibition of the {\i ta\u8217'ar}. One might have thought that if a {\i nazir} becomes a {\i metzora}, he cannot fulfill the {\i metzora mitzvah} with respect to his head, since the razor is precisely what is prohibited to the {\i nazir}. {\i Talmud lomar rosho} \u8212- the word {\i rosho} teaches us that even the head of a {\i metzora} who is also a {\i nazir} must be shaved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the same logical structure applies here as applied for the {\i Rabbanan} regarding the beard. Rabbi Eliezer knows that the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition is specifically and only a prohibition of the razor. If the verse tells us that the {\i metzora} overrides even that razor prohibition \u8212- that even the {\i nazir}\u8217's head must be shaved \u8212- then, applying the principle that an override is only invoked when there is no alternative means of fulfilling the obligation, the {\i metzora mitzvah} must require a razor. If the {\i metzora} could have used scissors or tweezers and thereby avoided the conflict with the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition entirely, the Torah would never have needed to say that the {\i metzora} overrides this prohibition. The {\i gemara} makes this argument explicit: since the general rule \u8212- articulated by Reish Lakish \u8212- is that an {\i aseh} overrides a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} only when the two must inevitably conflict, the Torah\u8217's announcement that the {\i metzora} overrides the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i ta\u8217'ar} prohibition proves that the {\i metzora mitzvah} can only be fulfilled with a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus the two {\i braisas} map onto the two positions. The {\i braisa} that begins by assuming the {\i metzora} requires a razor and then attempts (unsuccessfully) to extend this to {\i nazir} reflects the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position \u8212- they already know the {\i metzora} requires a razor from {\i et zekano}, without involving {\i nazir} at all. The {\i braisa} that begins by assuming the {\i nazir} requires a razor and then attempts to extend this to {\i metzora} reflects Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position \u8212- he cannot learn the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement from the beard, so he learns it instead from {\i rosho}, i.e., from the fact that the {\i metzora} overrides the razor prohibition of the {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this reasoning proves only that the {\i metzora} is {\i permitted} to use a razor even against a {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition, rather than proving that the {\i metzora} is {\i required} to use a razor. The {\i gemara} addresses this directly. One could have imagined that the verse is merely telling us something like {\i hutrah} \u8212- once you are engaged in the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving, the razor prohibition no longer applies at all, even though alternative methods exist. The {\i gemara} rejects this precisely by invoking Reish Lakish\u8217's principle: we never say an {\i aseh} overrides a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} when the two can be reconciled. Therefore, if the {\i metzora} could have avoided the conflict, we would never allow him to use a razor. Since the verse does allow it \u8212- indeed requires it \u8212- the conclusion is that the {\i metzora mitzvah} must be performed with a razor and with nothing else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aseh Docheh Lo Ta\u8217'aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the question of how the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement is derived now resolved, the {\i gemara} turns to a broader structural question: what is the source for the general principle of {\i aseh docheh lo ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- that a positive commandment overrides a prohibition \u8212- which the entire preceding discussion has been relying upon?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer comes from the juxtaposition of two verses concerning {\i tzitzit} and {\i shatnez}. The Torah states {\i lo tilbash sha\u8217'atnez} \u8212- one may not wear a garment of mixed wool and linen \u8212- immediately followed by {\i gedilim ta\u8217'aseh lach} \u8212- you shall make for yourself twisted fringes. The {\i techeiles}, the blue thread of {\i tzitzit}, must be spun from wool, and if one is wearing a linen garment, attaching {\i techeiles} means placing wool on linen \u8212- the very combination prohibited as {\i shatnez}. Yet the Torah permits it; the {\i aseh} of {\i tzitzit} overrides the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} of {\i shatnez}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} observes that this instance is, in fact, a case of {\i hutrah} \u8212- a complete release from the prohibition rather than a mere {\i dechiyah}, a forced override \u8212- and that as a consequence one may wear the {\i tzitzit} garment even at night, when the {\i mitzvah} of {\i tzitzit} does not apply, without transgressing the {\i shatnez} prohibition. This illustrates that there are indeed cases where the {\i parsha} of one {\i mitzvah} so fundamentally reconstitutes the act that the prohibition is simply inapplicable to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the present discussion, however, the more immediately operative point is this: the {\i gemara} notes that for Rabbi Eliezer, both the override of the beard prohibition \u8212- which involves the {\i kohanim}\u8217's {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} of {\i lo yegalechu} together with the {\i aseh} of {\i kedoshim yihyu} \u8212- and the override of the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition \u8212- which involves the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} of {\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor} together with the {\i aseh} of {\i kadosh yihyeh gadel pera} \u8212- are cases of overriding a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} reinforced by an {\i aseh}, which represents a more difficult obstacle than a bare {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} alone. {\i Tosafot} points out that precisely because Rabbi Eliezer uses both of his {\i drashos} to overcome the harder case, he must already know the basic principle from {\i tzitzit} and {\i shatnez} that an {\i aseh} overrides even a simple {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}. The {\i drashos} by {\i rosho} and {\i zekano} then extend that principle to the harder cases \u8212- where both a prohibition and a positive commandment stand on the other side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rosho and Zekano Ana (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosho Sideburns Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established what each camp learns from {\i zekano} \u8212- that the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i giluach} overrides the prohibition of shaving the beard \u8212- the {\i gemara} turns to the question of what the {\i Rabbanan} do with {\i rosho}. For Rabbi Eliezer, {\i rosho} is already accounted for: it teaches that the {\i metzora} overrides the {\i nazir}\u8217's razor prohibition applicable to the head. But the {\i Rabbanan} do not need {\i rosho} for that purpose, since they learn the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement from {\i zekano} alone. So what does {\i rosho} come to teach them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: the {\i Rabbanan} need {\i rosho} to tell them that the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i giluach} overrides the prohibition of {\i lo takifu pe\u8217'at roshchem} \u8212- the prohibition of shaving the sideburns. Shaving all the hair of the head necessarily includes the area of the {\i pe\u8217'ot}, the sideburns, and without an explicit override, one might have thought that even a {\i metzora} could not remove the sideburns. A {\i braisa} states this explicitly: {\i lo takifu pe\u8217'at roshchem} \u8212- could one think this applies even to a {\i metzora}? {\i Talmud lomar rosho} \u8212- the verse says {\i rosho}, to teach that even the {\i metzora}\u8217's sideburns must be shaved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zekano Override Lesson\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines why both {\i rosho} and {\i zekano} are needed. Once the {\i metzora} is told to shave his beard, overriding the prohibitions incumbent upon {\i kohanim} and ordinary Israelites alike, would it not follow automatically that the {\i metzora} also overrides the sideburn prohibition? The sideburn prohibition is, after all, less severe in certain respects \u8212- it applies to all Israelites but is not specifically reinforced by an additional {\i aseh} the way the {\i kohen}\u8217's beard prohibition is. If {\i zekano} already teaches us that the {\i metzora} overrides the harder obstacles, surely we could infer that it also overrides the easier ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i zekano} is introduced to override the beard prohibition with respect to the {\i metzora}. A {\i braisa} makes this explicit: {\i ufat zekanam lo yegalechu} \u8212- could one think this applies even to a {\i metzora}? {\i Talmud lomar zekano} \u8212- the verse includes his beard. And then the {\i gemara} asks: given that we have {\i zekano}, why do we also need {\i rosho}? And given that we have {\i rosho}, why do we also need {\i zekano}? The answer is {\i tzrichi} \u8212- both are necessary \u8212- and the {\i gemara} proceeds to explain precisely why each is indispensable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beard vs.\u160 ?Sideburns Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through the {\i tzrichi} argument with care. Suppose the Torah had written only {\i zekano} and not {\i rosho}. In that case, I know the {\i metzora} overrides the beard prohibition, but I might have thought that the prohibition of {\i hakafat kol harosh} \u8212- shaving all the hair of the head including the sideburns \u8212- is not actually prohibited at all in the general case. The argument would run as follows: the verse says {\i lo takifu}, from the root of {\i hakafah}, going around in a circle. The primary understanding of this prohibition is that one may not create a straight line from in front of the ear to behind it, leaving the sideburns cut. But if one shaves off {\i all} the hair of the head, including the {\i pe\u8217'ot} \u8212- perhaps that is not called {\i hakafah} at all, since one never created the configuration of a circle with the remaining hair stripped away. This is the question of {\i hakafat kol harosh shemah hakafah} \u8212- does shaving the entire head count as {\i hakafah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had the Torah written only {\i zekano}, I would have concluded that {\i hakafat kol harosh lav shemah hakafah} \u8212- it is not called {\i hakafah}, and there is therefore no prohibition to override in the first place. The {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving of the head would then be self-evidently permitted, but only because it was never prohibited. Therefore the Torah writes {\i rosho} to establish an override, and from the fact that it requires an override, I can infer that as a general rule {\i hakafat kol harosh shemah hakafah} \u8212- shaving the entire head is included in the prohibition, and the {\i metzora} therefore requires an explicit {\i derashah} to permit it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Two Verses Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now suppose the reverse: the Torah had written only {\i rosho} and not {\i zekano}. From {\i rosho}, I would have learned two things: first, that the {\i metzora} overrides the sideburn prohibition, and second \u8212- from the very fact that an override is needed \u8212- that {\i hakafat kol harosh shemah hakafah}. I might have then assumed that by the same logic, once the {\i metzora} overrides the prohibition of the sideburns, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i metzora} overrides the beard prohibition as well, simply by inference. But there is a third thing I would not have learned: that the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i giluach} must be performed specifically with a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the crucial asymmetry. For the {\i Rabbanan}, the reason that {\i zekano} proves the {\i metzora} must use a razor is that the beard prohibition is specifically and exclusively a razor prohibition. When the Torah declares that the {\i metzora} overrides the beard prohibition, and we know that prohibition exists only in the case of a razor, then the override can only be invoked if there is no alternative \u8212- which means the {\i metzora mitzvah} must be performed with a razor. But the sideburn prohibition, as we shall see, is broader than just the razor. If I had only {\i rosho} and tried to infer the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement from the override of the sideburns, I would fail \u8212- because the sideburn prohibition encompasses more than the razor, the override of it does not prove that the {\i metzora} must use a razor. Hence {\i zekano} is indispensable for establishing the razor requirement. And {\i rosho} is indispensable for establishing both that the sideburns themselves must be shaved and that {\i hakafat kol harosh} is a real prohibition. {\i Tzrichi} \u8212- both are needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rosho and Zekano Ana (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sideburns Broader Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Embedded in the {\i gemara}\u8217's argument is a striking implicit claim: the prohibition of shaving the sideburns is broader in scope than the prohibition of shaving the beard. The beard prohibition, as the {\i Rabbanan} hold, is limited exclusively to a {\i ta\u8217'ar mamash} \u8212- a literal razor. The sideburn prohibition, however, applies across a wider range of implements. This is precisely why the override of the sideburn prohibition by the {\i metzora} cannot serve to establish that the {\i metzora mitzvah} requires a razor: an override of a prohibition that applies even without a razor does not prove that the act overriding it must be done with a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sideburns Practical Hala\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Head Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This implicit {\i gemara} is the starting point for {\i Tosafot}\u8217's analysis of the practical {\i halakhah} of sideburns. {\i Tosafot}, in the {\i dibbur hamatchil} beginning {\i hashta}, notes explicitly that our {\i sugyah} implies that the prohibition of {\i hakafat harosh} \u8212- shaving the sideburns \u8212- applies even without a razor. The {\i gemara}\u8217's logic compels this conclusion: if the prohibition were limited to a razor just like the beard, then the override of sideburns would have been just as informative about the {\i metzora}\u8217's razor requirement as the override of the beard, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's distinction between the two overrides would collapse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Sources Sideburns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} proceeds to marshal three sources that bear on the scope of the sideburn prohibition, and the picture they present is not perfectly harmonious. The first source is our {\i gemara} itself, which, as just described, implies that sideburns are prohibited with a broader range than the razor alone. The second source is the {\i Toras Kohanim} \u8212- the {\i Sifra} \u8212- which discusses the {\i metzora} verse and states explicitly: {\i sheharosh assur bemisparayim vehazakan muttar} \u8212- the head is prohibited even with scissors, while the beard is permitted with scissors. This sounds unambiguous: ordinary scissors applied to the sideburns are prohibited. The third source is the {\i Tosefta}, which discusses the sideburn prohibition and states that one is {\i chayav} only if one uses a {\i ta\u8217'ar} \u8212- a razor. This appears to pull in the opposite direction from the {\i Sifra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot K\u8217'ein Ta\u8217'ar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} resolves the tension between the {\i Sifra} and the {\i Tosefta} by proposing that {\i ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar} \u8212- scissors that function like a razor \u8212- is what is prohibited. The {\i Tosefta}\u8217's phrase \u8220"razor\u8221" is to be read as encompassing scissors that cut as close to the skin as a razor would; the {\i Sifra}\u8217's mention of scissors refers to precisely this type of close-cutting scissors. The prohibition of sideburns thus extends beyond the {\i ta\u8217'ar mamash} to include {\i misparayim ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar} \u8212- scissors that approximate the effect of a razor. In practical terms, this would include an electric shaver, which is understood to operate through a scissor-like mechanism that can cut very close to the skin. An instrument that cuts hair close to the root but through a scissor action \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar} \u8212- is prohibited at the sideburns even according to those who permit it at the beard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then adds a further caution: {\i tzarich lehizaher shelo yitol pe\u8217'as harosh afilu bemisparayim} \u8212- one must be careful not to remove the sideburns even with ordinary scissors, since scissors generally can be characterized as {\i ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar}. And the practice, {\i Tosafot} notes, is that when children get haircuts, one leaves a significant amount of hair at the temples \u8212- {\i leshayer betziddehen harbe se\u8217'ar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shulchan Aruch Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Shulchan Aruch} in {\i Yoreh De\u8217'ah}, siman 181, addresses all these prohibitions together. On the sideburns, the {\i Shulchan Aruch} begins by stating {\i eino chayav ela bataar} \u8212- one is only {\i chayav} with a razor \u8212- but immediately adds {\i veyesh osrim bemisparayim ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar veyesh lachush ledivrehem} \u8212- there are those who prohibit with scissors that are like a razor, and one should be concerned for their opinion. The ruling is thus not definitive in one direction; it leans toward the {\i ta\u8217'ar}-only position while acknowledging the stricter view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when the {\i Shulchan Aruch} addresses the beard prohibition, it states unambiguously: {\i eino chayav al hashchasas pe\u8217'as hazakan ela bataar aval bemisparayim muttar afilu ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar} \u8212- one is only {\i chayav} for destroying the beard with a razor; scissors, even if they cut like a razor, are permitted for the beard. The asymmetry is complete: for the beard, the prohibition is limited to the literal razor, and even {\i ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar} is permitted. For the sideburns, the {\i Shulchan Aruch} at minimum acknowledges a significant opinion that {\i ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar} is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contemporary Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of this analysis is familiar to anyone who has observed traditional Jewish practice: one may use an electric shaver on the beard \u8212- relying on the view that the beard prohibition is limited to a {\i ta\u8217'ar mamash} and that a shaver operates through a scissor-like mechanism \u8212- while leaving the sideburns entirely unshaved by anything that could be characterized as {\i ke\u8217'ein ta\u8217'ar}. The implicit teaching of the {\i gemara}, developed by {\i Tosafot} and reflected in the {\i Shulchan Aruch}, is that these are not the same prohibition with the same scope, and they should not be treated identically in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Complete Hair Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalid Shaving Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling is stated plainly: {\i vekhulan shegilchu shelo beta\u8217'ar o sheshiyeru shtei se\u8217'aros lo asu velo klum} \u8212- if any of these three categories \u8212- {\i nazir}, {\i metzora}, or {\i Levi\u8217'im} \u8212- shaved without using a razor, or if they left two hairs unshaved, they have not fulfilled the {\i mitzvah} of {\i giluach} at all. Both conditions must be met: the instrument must be a razor, and the shaving must be essentially complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rubo Kekulo Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha son of Rav Ika draws a broader principle from this ruling: {\i zos omeres rubo k\u8217'kulo m\u8217'deoraisa} \u8212- this tells us that as a general Torah principle, a majority counts as the whole. His reasoning is that since the {\i gemara} will show that the requirement of complete shaving is derived from a specific {\i drasha} \u8212- with one hair remaining still counting as \u8220"all\u8221" but two hairs remaining not counting \u8212- the {\i gemara} treats this stringency as an exception that proves the rule. In the ordinary case, {\i rubo k\u8217'kulo} would apply and a majority would suffice. Here, the Torah explicitly demands totality, and that explicit demand is itself evidence that the default would have been otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yigalchenu Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha son of Rav Ika points to the source for the requirement of complete hair removal: {\i deichtiv uvayom hashevii yigalchenu} \u8212- \u8220"on the seventh day he shall shave it entirely.\u8221" The word {\i yigalchenu}, with its third-person object suffix, implies the full entirety of the hair, not merely a majority. This {\i pasuk}, however, appears in the context of the {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- the nazirite who has contracted ritual impurity \u8212- and his shaving on the seventh day of purification. The {\i gemara} will return to the implications of this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Hair Tolerance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even within the requirement of complete shaving, the {\i gemara} acknowledges a kind of definitional tolerance: leaving one hair is still considered \u8220"all,\u8221" but leaving two hairs is not. One hair is apparently within the margin of what can be characterized as complete; two hairs cross a threshold into something that cannot be so characterized. This is reminiscent of a point made elsewhere about precision in {\i halakhah} \u8212- that even when the Torah demands exactness, the {\i halakhah} operates with a working definition of exactness that does not demand mathematical perfection. Just as the {\i gemara}\u8217's measurement of the diagonal of a square uses 1.4 rather than the precise irrational value, so too here: one remaining hair does not defeat the characterization of \u8220"complete.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Tamei vs.\u160 ?Tahor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef son of Rabbi Chanina challenges the derivation: the verse {\i yigalchenu} is written with respect to a {\i nazir tamei}, an impure nazirite \u8212- how can one use it as a source for the requirement of complete shaving that applies to a {\i nazir tahor}, a pure nazirite completing his term? This challenge meets with what the {\i gemara} describes as laughter from the scholars of {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- and the reason, as {\i Machaku alah bema\u8217'arava} implies, is that the question collapses under examination. The {\i gemara} responds: we know that the {\i nazir tamei}\u8217's shaving must use a razor because we learn it from the {\i nazir tahor}. Given that we learn the {\i nazir tamei} from the {\i nazir tahor} in one direction, we can equally well learn from the {\i nazir tamei} back to the {\i nazir tahor} in the other direction. {\i Mah tamei ki shayar shtei se\u8217'arot velo klum avid} \u8212- just as by the {\i tamei} shaving, {\i yigalchenu} means total shaving and leaving two hairs invalidates it, {\i af hacha nami} \u8212- the same applies here. The two cases of shaving illuminate each other bidirectionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes a curious irony: in {\i Sanhedrin}, it is Rav Yosef son of Rabbi Chanina who is described as one of those who {\i machaku} \u8212- who laughed \u8212- at a question posed in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. Here, the laughter is directed at his own question. As the {\i gemara} might put it: if you laugh at other people\u8217's questions, other people will laugh at yours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Edge Cases Giluach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} moves from the establishment of the requirement to a series of edge cases designed to test the outer limits of the principle. Rava poses the following question: a {\i nazir} who shaved at the conclusion of his {\i nezirut} but left two hairs. He has not yet fulfilled the {\i mitzvah}. Now hair grows back, and after a period his head is once again full of hair \u8212- but the original two hairs that he left are still identifiable. He then shaves those two specific hairs while his head remains full of all the regrown hair. Has he fulfilled the {\i mitzvah} of {\i giluach}? This is something like the philosophical puzzle of whether an action that targets only the two remaining original hairs constitutes a fulfillment, given that the totality of hair on his head is nowhere near completely shaved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Hair Not Signific\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more refined version of Rava\u8217's question \u8212- as clarified in the text \u8212- is actually different: a {\i nazir} who shaved and left two hairs, then one of those hairs fell out on its own and he deliberately shaved the remaining one. Has he fulfilled the {\i mitzvah}? At first glance, one might say that since the final remaining hair has now been removed, the {\i giluach} is complete. The question, however, is whether the deliberate cutting of a single hair when no other hairs remain to be cut constitutes a meaningful act of {\i giluach} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giluach as Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer given is {\i giluach ein kan se\u8217'ar ein kan} \u8212- there is no {\i giluach} here and there is no hair here. The {\i gemara} initially takes this to mean: if there is no hair, how can there be {\i giluach}? But it clarifies: the point is that even though the person is rendered bald \u8212- {\i af al pi shese\u8217'ar ein kan} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of {\i giluach} has not been fulfilled \u8212- {\i mitzvas giluach ein kan}. The act of cutting one hair when there is no second hair to accompany it is not defined as a significant act of shaving. {\i Giluach}, as a {\i mitzvah}, is understood to be a process involving multiple hairs; cutting a single hair in isolation, without others being cut alongside it, does not rise to the level of an act of {\i giluach} in the halakhic sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baldness Without Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final inference is stark: the person ends up completely bald \u8212- the last hair has been removed \u8212- yet the {\i mitzvah} has not been fulfilled. The act of rendering someone bald and the act of performing {\i mitzvas giluach} are not identical. Baldness is a physical outcome; {\i giluach} as a {\i mitzvah} requires a meaningful act of shaving involving multiple hairs as part of a coherent process. When the final act consists of cutting only one hair with no others present to be cut, the result may be baldness, but not the fulfillment of the {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hair Care Permitted Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand Straightening Allow\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} introduces a series of rulings about what a {\i nazir} may and may not do with respect to his hair during the period of {\i nezirut}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i nazir chofef umefaspes aval lo sorek} \u8212- a {\i nazir} may run his fingers through his hair and use his hands to straighten and separate the hairs, but he may not comb. The prohibition on pulling out hair during {\i nezirut} applies not only to a razor but \u8212- as the {\i gemara} has noted \u8212- to any deliberate act of hair removal, including by hand. The question, then, is why using the hands to straighten hair is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unintentional Loss Allow\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer the {\i gemara} provides is that this {\i mishnah} reflects the position of Rabbi Shimon, who holds that {\i davar she\u8217'eino miskaven mutar} \u8212- an action that is not intended to achieve the prohibited outcome is permitted. When a {\i nazir} runs his fingers through his hair or uses his hands to smooth and separate the hairs, his intention is to straighten them, not to pull them out. If a hair comes loose incidentally in the process, Rabbi Shimon regards this as {\i davar she\u8217'eino miskaven} and therefore permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combing Prohibited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the first part of the {\i mishnah} reflects Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency, why does the {\i mishnah} then prohibit combing? If unintended hair removal is permitted, why should combing be any different? The {\i gemara} answers through Rabbah: {\i kulla Rabbi Shimon hi} \u8212- the entire {\i mishnah} reflects Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view \u8212- but combing is different because {\i kol hasorek lehassir nimi meduldlos miskaven} \u8212- anyone who combs their hair is, in the process of combing, intending to remove tangled, loosened, or knotted hairs. A comb encounters stuck or matted hairs and the act of drawing it through is inherently directed at removing those obstructed hairs. It is not merely an incidental consequence \u8212- it is part of the purpose of the combing action itself. Therefore, even Rabbi Shimon would agree that combing is prohibited: it is not a pure case of {\i davar she\u8217'eino miskaven}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combing Shabbat Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gemara} carries significant implications beyond {\i nezirut}. The {\i Shulchan Aruch} and the {\i Beis Yosef} discuss the prohibition of combing hair on {\i Shabbos}, particularly in the context of women preparing for the {\i mikvah} on {\i Shabbos}. The concern is that combing on {\i Shabbos} involves the inevitable removal of hairs, either as a {\i pesik reisha} \u8212- an inevitable consequence \u8212- or, per this {\i gemara}, as an act of partial {\i kavanah}. Notably, the {\i gemara} here does not frame the prohibition of combing in terms of {\i pesik reisha} \u8212- it could have done so, relying on the principle that Abaye and Rava introduce limiting Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency. Instead, the {\i gemara} frames it as {\i miskaven}, as intentional removal, which is arguably a more fundamental objection. This distinction is itself noteworthy: {\i pesik reisha} as a concept limiting {\i davar she\u8217'eino miskaven} is associated with Abaye and Rava and is not something that appears explicitly in Rabbi Shimon\u8217's own formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what exactly constitutes {\i pesik reisha} in the context of combing is itself complex. If in the course of a three-minute combing session one will inevitably remove three hairs, but no single stroke of the comb is individually a {\i pesik reisha}, does the overall act qualify? The analogy to dragging a bench through a garden is instructive: each inch of movement may not be a {\i pesik reisha} for creating a furrow, but the full traversal of the garden is. Whether one aggregates across a process or evaluates each discrete act independently is a genuine question in the {\i poskim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Ovadia Modern Combing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A striking position on this question is attributed to Rav Ovadia Yosef, who is reported to have ruled that modern combing does not necessarily constitute {\i pesik reisha}. His reasoning apparently turns on the character of modern combs, modern hair care, or some other relevant variable \u8212- though one should consult the {\i teshuvah} directly rather than relying on a summary. The ruling is noted here precisely because it is exceptional: the {\i gemara}\u8217's reasoning, and the assumption of the {\i Shulchan Aruch} and the {\i poskim}, has generally been that combing inevitably involves hair removal, whether framed as {\i pesik reisha} or as {\i kavanah}. The suggestion that contemporary conditions alter this analysis is a significant departure, whatever its ultimate {\i halakhic} standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hair Cleansing Agents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mineral Cleanser Ban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concluding section of the daf introduces a ruling of Rabbi Yishmael: {\i lo yachof b\u8217'adama} \u8212- a {\i nazir} may not wash his hair with a type of earth-based mineral substance. The reason given is {\i mipnei she\u8217'hi mesharet et hase\u8217'ar} \u8212- because this substance loosens and ultimately causes the hair to fall out. In the ancient world, mineral substances such as natron (a naturally occurring sodium carbonate), bentonite clay, and related compounds served as cleansing agents, some of which could weaken the hair and cause it to shed. Rabbi Yishmael prohibits these substances to a {\i nazir} because using them would lead to hair loss, implicating the prohibition against removing hair during {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific vs.\u160 ?General Ban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a question about the precise scope of this prohibition: does the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrase {\i mipnei she\u8217'hi mesharet} \u8212- \u8220"because it loosens\u8221" \u8212- mean that Rabbi Yishmael is prohibiting specifically this type of mineral substance because of its hair-loosening properties? Or does it mean that the category of mineral cleansing agents is prohibited because such agents characteristically cause hair to loosen? The question turns on whether the prohibition targets the particular causal mechanism \u8212- the {\i mesharetut}, the loosening \u8212- of this specific agent, or whether it targets the broader category of mineral cleansing agents as a {\i gezeirah} on account of those within the category that do loosen hair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harmless Mineral Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference emerges clearly: suppose one identifies a mineral substance that is entirely harmless to hair \u8212- one that cleanses without loosening or causing any hair to fall out. If the prohibition is targeted at the specific harmful properties of the prohibited agent \u8212- {\i mipnei she\u8217'hi mesharet} in the narrow sense \u8212- then a harmless mineral would be permitted. If, on the other hand, the prohibition is a categorical {\i gezeirah} against all mineral cleansing agents, enacted because of those within the category that do cause hair loss, then even a demonstrably harmless mineral would be prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiku Unresolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} leaves this question unresolved \u8212- {\i teiku}. This is more than a technical loose end: it reflects a genuinely open question about the structure of Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's prohibition and whether it is grounded in the specific mechanism of harm or in the categorical identity of the substance. The practical implication \u8212- whether a {\i nazir} may use a mineral-based cleansing agent if it is known not to cause hair loss \u8212- remains without a definitive answer in the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 42\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna: Multiple Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 42 opens the Gemara\u8217's treatment of the third prohibition of {\i nazir}: becoming {\i tamei l\u8217'meitim}. Having addressed the prohibitions of wine and shaving, the sugya now turns to {\i tumas meis}, and as we shall see, the discussion carries consequences far beyond the laws of {\i nezirus} \u8212- extending to the prohibitions of {\i kehunah} in every generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single violation in day\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna at the bottom of 42a states: {\i nazir she-hayah shoteh yayin kol hayom eino chayav ela achat} \u8212- a {\i nazir} who was drinking wine the whole day receives only one set of lashes. This formulation itself raises a conceptual question worth noting. Some {\i Rishonim} suggest that the {\i nazir} may indeed be {\i chayav lishmayim} for each individual act of drinking, each one constituting a separate prohibition in some sense, yet he receives only one set of lashes because there was only one {\i hatra\u8217'ah}. The question is whether we conceptualize the day\u8217's drinking as multiple discrete acts for which the structure of {\i malkus} simply cannot be applied without separate warnings, or whether the entire episode is viewed as one continuous act of transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Act definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction matters because there is something genuinely puzzling here. On the one hand, drinking wine throughout the day could be seen as a process \u8212- an ongoing single act of violation. Getting a haircut, too, unfolds over time and could be conceptualized as one extended prohibition. But {\i tumas meis} is different in character: becoming {\i tamei} is not obviously a process. Each contact with a corpse seems, almost by definition, to be a discrete act. This observation cuts in two directions, as the Gemara will explore.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Role of warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna resolves the question of separate {\i malkus} through the mechanism of {\i hatra\u8217'ah}. If they said to him {\i al tishteh, al tishteh} \u8212- warning him before each act of drinking \u8212- and he drank each time, then {\i chayav al kol achat v\u8217'achat}: he is liable for each separately. The warning functions to define and divide what might otherwise be treated as a single continuous violation into separate acts, each carrying its own liability. Crucially, this appears to apply even if the individual drinkings occurred within a span that might otherwise be halachically combined as a single act of drinking. Conversely, even acts that are clearly separate \u8212- say, drinking in the morning and again days later \u8212- do not generate separate {\i malkus} without a separate {\i hatra\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel case of shaving follows the same structure: {\i hayah megaleyach kol hayom eino chayav ela achat}, but with separate warnings before each hair, {\i chayav al kol achat v\u8217'achat}. And the same applies, at least formally, to {\i tumas meis}. The Mishna presents the three cases as parallel. Yet as the Gemara will demonstrate, the {\i tumah} case carries a crucial complication that the wine and hair cases do not share.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine, Hair, Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three parallel cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna presents three parallel cases: drinking wine all day, cutting hair all day, and becoming {\i tamei l\u8217'meitim} all day. In each case, absent separate warnings, the {\i nazir} receives only one set of lashes; with separate warnings before each act, he receives a separate set for each. On the surface, these three cases are symmetric.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah vs.\u160 ?process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet a closer look reveals that {\i tumah} differs fundamentally from the other two categories. When a {\i nazir} drinks more wine, more of the prohibited substance enters his body \u8212- he has done something additional. When he cuts another hair, he has physically removed something additional from his head. In both cases, there is an incremental act with a concrete result. But when a person who is already {\i tamei} touches another dead body, what has he accomplished? His status was already {\i tamei}. The prohibition, as the Gemara will make clear, is {\i lo yitama} \u8212- a prohibition framed in terms of achieving a state of {\i tumah}. If the state was already present, the question arises whether a further contact constitutes a separate prohibited act at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repeat impurity question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates the central question that will drive the entire sugya: once a {\i nazir} (or {\i kohen}) is already {\i tamei}, does touching another dead body on the same day constitute an additional violation? The answer to this question has enormous practical consequences, not only for {\i nezirus} but for the laws of {\i kehunah} in our time. The {\i Rishonim} developed the position that all {\i kohanim} nowadays are presumed {\i tamei meis} \u8212- having buried a relative at some point and been unable to purify through the {\i efer ha-parah}. If a {\i kohen} who is already {\i tamei} does not transgress by further contact with the dead, the halachic implications are significant. This is the question to which the Gemara now turns.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further nuance deserves mention before entering the Gemara. If a person touches a dead body on Sunday and then touches another dead body on Monday, there is a real-world effect: his {\i parah adumah} purification timeline has been reset. The ashes would have been sprinkled on him Tuesday had he not touched again on Monday; now he must wait anew. But if he touches a second dead body on Sunday \u8212- the same day \u8212- his {\i tumah} level has not meaningfully changed, and no practical consequence follows. The Gemara will build on this distinction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava: Tumah status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Itmar}. The Gemara introduces a dispute through the formulation {\i amar Rava amar Rav Huna: mikra malei diber ha-katuv} \u8212- the Torah speaks fully, meaning that two distinct prohibitions are stated explicitly. The relevant verses for the {\i kohen} and {\i nazir} include both {\i lo yitama} and {\i lo yavo al kol nafshot meis}. The {\i lo yitama} verse prohibits the state of becoming {\i tamei}; the {\i lo yavo} verse prohibits the act of entering under the same roof as a corpse. The Gemara\u8217's point is that these are conceptually distinct {\i lavin}: a {\i nazir} who enters a house containing a corpse, having been warned separately about each prohibition, transgresses both and receives two sets of lashes. Touching a corpse without entering a house would yield only one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 State vs.\u160 ?act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava explains the structure: {\i l\u8217'hazhir al ha-tuma l\u8217'hazhir al ha-bia} \u8212- one prohibition targets the state of {\i tumah}, the other targets the act of entry. If a {\i nazir} was warned about both before entering a house with a corpse, and he entered, he transgresses {\i lo yitama} and {\i lo yavo} simultaneously \u8212- two {\i lavin}, two sets of lashes for one physical act. This is because there is no way to enter such a house without violating both. The two prohibitions are conceptually distinguishable even when they are practically inseparable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: No repeat lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the critical ruling of Rava concerns the case of {\i tumah} following {\i tumah}: {\i aval tuma v\u8217'tuma lo}. If a {\i nazir} is already {\i tamei} and he touches another dead body \u8212- whether simultaneously with the first contact or after removing his hand and touching again \u8212- Rava holds that he does not receive an additional set of lashes. The reasoning follows from the framing of {\i lo yitama}: the prohibition concerns achieving the state of {\i tumah}. If the state was already present, the subsequent contact has not changed the halachic reality. However actively he may have touched a second corpse, he has not become {\i tamei} in any new sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yosef: Repeat acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef: separate acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef disagrees sharply, invoking an exclamation \u8212- {\i ha Elokim!} \u8212- before citing Rav Huna\u8217's actual ruling: {\i afilu tumah v\u8217'tumah}, even {\i tumah} that follows {\i tumah}, incurs separate lashes. This applies not merely when a {\i nazir} removes his hand from the first corpse and then touches a second, but even while he remains in contact with the first body. For Rav Yosef, each distinct act of touching \u8212- accompanied by a separate warning \u8212- constitutes a separately punishable transgression, regardless of the actor\u8217's ongoing {\i tumah} status. The prohibition is not only about the resulting state; it is also, in some sense, about the act itself. Just as a separate warning before cutting another hair generates separate {\i malkus}, so too a separate warning before touching another corpse should generate separate {\i malkus}, even if the person is already {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An analogy illustrates the point: imagine a person in the process of cutting hair from one side of his head, and while still engaged in that act, he is warned before cutting hair from another section and proceeds. One might well say that those are separate acts generating separate {\i malkus}. Rav Yosef reasons similarly about {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cemetery case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna\u8217's concrete formulation of this principle is presented: {\i nazir she-hayah omed b\u8217'veit ha-kevarot v\u8217'hoshitu lo met acher v\u8217'naga bo, chayav} \u8212- a {\i nazir} standing in the cemetery, already {\i tamei}, who is handed another corpse and touches it, is nevertheless liable for a separate set of lashes. Note that by a {\i nazir}, unlike a {\i kohen}, even touching an immediate relative is prohibited \u8212- a {\i nazir} has the stringency of a {\i kohen gadol} in this regard. The case of the relative is mentioned only because it describes a common scenario; the halachic point is the same regardless of who the deceased is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rav Yosef, the {\i nazir} in the cemetery who is already {\i tamei} and touches an additional corpse has performed a new, separately punishable act. The fact that his {\i tumah} status did not change is irrelevant to his liability for the act itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sources conflict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents sources that appear to cut in opposite directions. {\i Abiaye} challenges Rav Yosef from a {\i braita}: a {\i kohen} who had a corpse on his shoulder \u8212- already {\i tamei} \u8212- and was handed another corpse and touched it, is not liable, because {\i lo yechalel} applies only {\i bemi she-eino mechuhlal}, to one whose status has not yet been violated. One who is {\i mechuhal v\u8217'omed}, already in a state of violated status, is excluded. This appears to support Rava\u8217's position against Rav Yosef.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds: {\i v\u8217'tifshut lach matnitin} \u8212- the Mishna supports me. The Mishna states explicitly that a {\i nazir} who became {\i tamei l\u8217'meitim} all day, with separate warnings before each contact, is {\i chayav al kol achat v\u8217'achat}. And the Gemara presses: {\i v\u8217'amai? Ha-mitamei ve-ka\u8217'ei!} Why should he be liable? He is already {\i tamei}! This is Rav Yosef\u8217's proof that even while already {\i tamei}, separate acts with separate warnings incur separate liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is a direct contradiction between sources: the {\i braita} supports Rava, the Mishna supports Rav Yosef. {\i Ella kashya hadadei} \u8212- they stand in tension with each other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Active vs.\u160 ?removed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Av hatumah levels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution hinges on a distinction in {\i tumah} levels. To understand it, some background is necessary. A corpse has the status of {\i avi avot ha-tumah} \u8212- the source of sources of impurity. Normally, an {\i av ha-tumah} (such as a {\i zav} or {\i niddah}) conveys seven-day {\i tumah} to objects but only one-day {\i tumah} to persons who touch it (who become {\i rishon}). By a {\i meis}, the {\i tumah} is more severe: the person who touches it becomes an {\i av ha-tumah} himself \u8212- a seven-day {\i tamei} requiring {\i parah adumah} purification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah bechiburin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the concept of {\i tumah bechiburin}: chain impurity while actively in physical contact. If person A is touching the corpse, and person B is touching person A while A remains in contact with the corpse, then person B, by virtue of this daisy-chain of contact, also attains a status of seven-day {\i tumah} as though he himself were touching the corpse. This elevation of B\u8217's status \u8212- from what would ordinarily be one-day {\i tumah} to seven-day {\i tumah} \u8212- is called {\i tumah bechiburin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, this elevated status exists only {\i while the contact is maintained}. The moment person A removes his hand from the corpse, person B reverts to his ordinary {\i rishon} status. The resolution now becomes clear: {\i lo kashya \u8212- kan bechiburin, kan she-lo bechiburin}. When the {\i braita} says the {\i kohen} is not liable for touching a second corpse while already holding the first, that is the case {\i bechiburin} \u8212- he is actively in contact with the first body, his {\i tumah} level is at its maximum, and touching another body changes nothing about his status. When the Mishna says he {\i is} liable for separate contacts with separate warnings, that is the case {\i she-lo bechiburin} \u8212- he has removed his hand, his {\i tumah} level has dropped slightly (from something approaching {\i avi avot} back to {\i av}), and touching again represents an actual change in his status. Hence in that case, separate {\i malkus} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goses proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately asks: {\i v\u8217'tumah bechiburin d\u8217'Oraita?} Is the elevated status during {\i bechiburin} a biblical phenomenon? If so, the resolution above works cleanly. But there is a proof that it may be only rabbinic. The Gemara cites a ruling: {\i lo amru tumah bechiburin ela l\u8217'terumah v\u8217'kodashim, aval l\u8217'nazir v\u8217'oseh pesach lo}. The elevated seven-day status that person B acquires by touching A while A touches the corpse applies for purposes of {\i terumah} and {\i kodashim}, but not for {\i nezirut} or {\i korban pesach}. If B is a {\i nazir} or needs to bring a {\i korban pesach}, we tell him he is only a {\i rishon} \u8212- one-day {\i tamei} \u8212- and he need not restart his {\i nezirut} or forfeit his {\i pesach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concession strongly implies that the elevated {\i bechiburin} status is not biblical, since we are willing to set it aside in high-stakes situations. If it were {\i d\u8217'Oraita}, we could not do so. The Gemara presses: {\i ve\u8217'amarta d\u8217'Oraita \u8212- mai shena?} If you say it is biblical, why distinguish? Clearly it is not fully {\i d\u8217'Oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's response: {\i kan bechiburei adam b\u8217'adam, kan bechiburei adam b\u8217'meis}. There is a difference between the case of person B touching person A who touches the corpse ({\i chiburei adam b\u8217'adam}) and the case of person A directly touching the corpse ({\i chiburei adam b\u8217'meis}). Person A\u8217's seven-day status when directly touching the corpse is {\i d\u8217'Oraita}. Person B\u8217's derived seven-day status, one step removed, is {\i d\u8217'Rabbanan} \u8212- and therefore we set it aside for {\i nazir} and {\i pesach}. It is only the third person in the chain (person C touching person B who touches person A who touches the corpse) whose elevated status is purely {\i d\u8217'Rabbanan}. This is the reading of {\i Tosafot}; the {\i Rambam} reads the Gemara\u8217's conclusion differently, maintaining that even person B\u8217's elevated status is ultimately {\i d\u8217'Rabbanan}, though he nonetheless agrees that touching another corpse while actively in contact with the first does not incur additional {\i malkus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution: contact vs.\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the resolution as Rava frames it: during active contact with a corpse, the person\u8217's {\i tumah} level is at its maximum \u8212- essentially elevated to a status approaching {\i avi avot} for purposes of biblical law. Touching another corpse at that moment does not change his status and therefore does not trigger additional liability. But once he removes his hand, his {\i tumah} level diminishes (he is now an {\i av} rather than the elevated status of {\i bechiburin}), and subsequently touching another corpse represents a genuine change \u8212- a new act of {\i tumah} in the {\i d\u8217'Oraita} sense \u8212- for which he incurs a separate set of lashes with a separate warning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, according to the simple reading of the Gemara, is how Rava and Rav Yosef divide: Rava says that after removing his hand and touching again, he {\i is} liable; while actively touching he is not. Rav Yosef says he is liable even while actively touching.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lo yavo analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a sharp question about the parallel between {\i tumah} and {\i bi\u8217'ah} (entering a house containing a corpse). We established that according to Rava, a {\i nazir} who is actively touching one dead body and then touches another is not liable \u8212- because his {\i tumah} level was already at its maximum and touching again does not change it. The Gemara asks: {\i tumah u-vi\u8217'ah nami \u8212- ha-mitamei ve-ka\u8217'ei?} By the same logic, why should a {\i nazir} who is carrying a corpse on his shoulder and then walks into a house be liable for {\i lo yavo}? He is already {\i tamei}! If the rationale for exempting the person who touches twice is that his {\i tumah} status has not changed, should not the same rationale exempt him when he walks into a house while already actively {\i tamei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} frames the question carefully: the case parallel to {\i tumah-tumah} for the {\i bi\u8217'ah} category is a person who has a corpse on his shoulder and then enters a house containing another corpse. He was already {\i tamei} from the corpse on his shoulder, and now encounters {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- why should he be liable for {\i lo yavo} when he has not changed his {\i tumah} status?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think the answer lies in the distinction between the two {\i lavin}: {\i lo yitama} is framed as a prohibition of achieving a status, whereas {\i lo yavo} is framed as a prohibition of performing an act of entering. But the Gemara does not rest with that answer; it probes more carefully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand vs.\u160 ?body\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's first approach cites Rabbi Yochanan: {\i kan ba-bayit, kan ba-sadeh}. The cases are distinguishable by context: \u8220"in the house\u8221" versus \u8220"in the field.\u8221" {\i Tosafot} reads this as follows. When we said that entering a house generates two sets of lashes \u8212- one for {\i lo yitama} and one for {\i lo yavo} \u8212- we meant a case where the person was not already {\i tamei} through active contact; both prohibitions struck simultaneously as he entered. The analogous case for {\i tumah-tumah} in the field is touching two corpses at the same instant \u8212- which yields only one set of lashes. And conversely, if a person is actively carrying a corpse on his shoulder and then enters a house containing another corpse, he would indeed not be liable for a second set, because his {\i tumah} was already present and active.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara presses further: {\i be-vayit nami} \u8212- even by the house, once any part of a person\u8217's body enters the space under the roof, he becomes {\i tamei} from the corpse inside. And the prohibition of {\i lo yavo} is not complete until the majority of his body has entered. So the moment his hand or foot crosses the threshold, he is {\i tamei}, and by the time his whole body is inside \u8212- which is when {\i lo yavo} is transgressed \u8212- he is already in an active state of {\i tumah}. By Rava\u8217's logic, this should exempt him from liability for {\i lo yavo}! Why does he get two sets of lashes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Box case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar offers one resolution: {\i tzireif yadav} \u8212- if the person entered with his hand pressed flat against his body, and {\i tzireif gufo} \u8212- the entire body entered at once as a single unit, then {\i tumah u-vi\u8217'ah be-hadi hadadi ka\u8217'atyan} \u8212- the {\i tumah} and the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} arrive simultaneously. In this case, neither precedes the other, and both {\i lavin} are transgressed together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara objects: {\i i efshar d\u8217'lo ayikhu khotmo be-reisha} \u8212- it is impossible for the nose not to enter first! As soon as the nose crosses the threshold, {\i tumah} has been contracted, and the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} is not yet complete. So there is always a tiny interval between the {\i tumah} and the {\i bi\u8217'ah}, and by the time {\i lo yavo} is transgressed, the person is already actively {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes that in their reading, entering backwards would be {\i she-lo ke-darko} \u8212- an abnormal mode of entry \u8212- and {\i lo yavo} requires normal entry to be transgressed, so walking backwards would not solve the problem halachically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa then offers the celebrated case: {\i kegon she-nichnas be-shida, teiva u-migdal} \u8212- a person who climbed into a box or trunk, and a friend then ripped off the roof. In this case, when the roof is removed, the corpse\u8217's {\i tumah} is exposed simultaneously throughout the space, and the person inside is considered to have \u8220"entered\u8221" the space of the corpse at the same instant he contracts {\i tumah}. Thus {\i tumah u-vi\u8217'ah be-hadi hadadi ka\u8217'atyan} \u8212- they truly arrive together. {\i Tosafot} notes that for this case to generate {\i malkus}, the person inside must have been warned and must have been actively participating in breaking out \u8212- if his friend acted unilaterally, the person inside cannot be blamed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goses case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi presents another scenario in which the two violations can arrive simultaneously: {\i kegon de-ayel ke-she-hu goses} \u8212- the person entered the room while the occupant was {\i goses}, on the threshold of death, and the soul departed while he was remaining there. Since a {\i goses} does not yet transmit {\i tumah} \u8212- {\i tumah} requires the actual departure of the soul \u8212- when the person entered the room, he was not yet {\i tamei}. But the moment death arrived, both the {\i tumah} from the newly-created {\i meis} and the violation of {\i bi\u8217'ah} (by remaining present under the same roof) came simultaneously. This case requires, of course, that the {\i hatra\u8217'ah} be meaningful \u8212- hence the use of a {\i goses}, where death is anticipated imminently, lending substance to the warning.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Goses impurity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lehachalo dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Braita} is now cited: {\i lo yitama ba\u8217'al be\u8217'amav le-hachalo} \u8212- a {\i kohen} does not violate {\i until the person dies}, {\i ad she-yamut}. This is the view of the first {\i Tanna}: the violation is triggered only at death, not before. {\i Rebbi} cites {\i bemotam} \u8212- in their death \u8212- from the verse concerning the {\i nazir}, and derives the same conclusion: only at actual death does the prohibition take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goses included\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish argues that there {\i is} a difference between the two {\i darshanim}: for the one who derives the rule from {\i le-hachalo}, even a {\i goses} is included in the prohibition. The phrase {\i le-hachalo} \u8212- \u8220"to desecrate\u8221" \u8212- is future-oriented, suggesting that putting oneself in a situation that will result in desecration is itself prohibited. It may also carry the sense of entering a situation of {\i chillul} even before death has occurred. For the one who derives it from {\i bemotam}, however, the rule is stricter in its semantic limitation: {\i ad she-yamut} \u8212- until he actually dies. The {\i goses} is not yet a {\i meis} and does not yet convey {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yochanan disputes Reish Lakish\u8217's reading, arguing that there is no practical difference between the two derivations: both exclude the {\i goses} from triggering {\i tumah}, and the difference is only in which verse provides the source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites a {\i braita} that appears to resolve the matter: {\i adam eino mitamei ella ad she-teitzei nafsho, va\u8217'afilu meguyad va\u8217'afilu goses} \u8212- a person does not cause {\i tumah} until the soul departs, even if he has been critically wounded or is in his death throes. The {\i goses} is simply not yet a {\i meis} for purposes of {\i tumah}. This appears to refute any reading of {\i le-hachalo} that would include an actual {\i tumah} violation for the {\i goses}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lehachalo vs tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent contradiction. {\i Le-inyan tum\u8217'ei} \u8212- for the purpose of {\i tumah} transmission \u8212- yes, the person must actually die. But {\i le-inyan achlolei} \u8212- for the purpose of violating the prohibition of {\i chillul} of one\u8217's sacred status \u8212- {\i ha itchallei}: that violation begins when one enters the space of a {\i goses}. The two categories are distinct: the act of {\i tumah} and the act of {\i chillul} can be triggered at different moments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes the uncertainty about how we {\i pasken} in practice. The {\i Shulchan Aruch} rules that a {\i goses} does not transmit {\i tumah} until the soul departs, but nonetheless rules that it is {\i assur} for a {\i kohen} to enter a house containing a {\i goses} \u8212- while noting that some permit it, and the {\i Rema} adds that it is good to be stringent. The precise level of this prohibition \u8212- whether {\i d\u8217'Oraita} or {\i d\u8217'Rabbanan}, and on which model \u8212- remains ambiguous in the {\i poskim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohanim practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: no repeat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} draws a direct practical conclusion from the Gemara\u8217's resolution. Even according to Rava\u8217's more lenient position \u8212- that one who is already {\i tamei} does not incur additional {\i malkus} for touching another corpse while actively in contact \u8212- this applies only during that active contact. Once a {\i kohen} has finished burying his relative and is no longer directly touching the body, he has reverted to a standard {\i av ha-tumah} status. Any further contact with the dead \u8212- touching another body, walking under a tree that encompasses a grave \u8212- constitutes a new and separately prohibited act. The {\i kohen} may not say: \u8220"I am already {\i tamei}, so I may as well visit other graves in the cemetery.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exit problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an immediate practical problem: how does a {\i kohen} exit a cemetery after burying a relative without violating further prohibitions? Walking out through a cemetery filled with graves, trees, and overhead obstructions creates endless opportunities for {\i ohel}-based {\i tumah}. The technical exemption of {\i bechiburin} \u8212- being actively in contact with one body \u8212- ceases the moment burial is complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edge burial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} records having heard from great authorities that this is precisely why it was the practice to bury the dead at the edge of the cemetery \u8212- so that the {\i kohen} who buried his relative could exit immediately without passing over additional graves or through areas that would constitute new violations. This is not merely a custom of convenience but a halachic accommodation to the constraints established by the Gemara\u8217's analysis of {\i tumah bechiburin} and its limits.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ra\u8217'avad reinterprets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef: active only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ra\u8217'avad, in his glosses to the {\i Rambam}\u8217's {\i Hilchos Nezirus}, announces: {\i amar Avraham \u8212- iyanti bi-shmu\u8217'ah be-Maseches Nazir}, I have carefully examined this sugya, and what I find there is not what everyone has assumed. The Ra\u8217'avad offers a revolutionary reinterpretation: the position that a {\i nazir} is exempt from additional lashes only {\i while actively touching} \u8212- the {\i bechiburin} exemption \u8212- is not the lenient position of Rava. It is the {\i strict} position of Rav Yosef. Rav Yosef, who said that even {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah} incurs separate lashes, only meant that this applies after one removes his hand and touches again. While actively in contact, even Rav Yosef concedes that no additional violation occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: never repeat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the Ra\u8217'avad, Rava\u8217's position is the genuinely lenient one: Rava holds that even after removing one\u8217's hand and touching again \u8212- even many days later, when the {\i bechiburin} factor is long gone \u8212- no additional {\i malkus} is incurred. Because the person is already {\i tamei}, the prohibition of {\i lo yitama} simply does not apply to him again, regardless of how much time has passed or how many additional contacts he makes. The focus of {\i lo yitama} on {\i status} means that once the status is present, it cannot be re-violated \u8212- full stop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'avad ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ra\u8217'avad then draws his halachic conclusion: {\i ve-khivan de-kaima lan Rava ve-Rav Yosef hilchesa ke-Rava} \u8212- since we rule like Rava against Rav Yosef, the ruling is that {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah, afilu peirish ve-chazar ve-naga, patur}. Even if a person removed his hand, waited, and touched again \u8212- he is exempt from {\i malkus}. He then applies this to the contemporary situation: {\i ve-ha-kohanim bi-zman ha-zeh temei mes hein} \u8212- {\i kohanim} in our time are all {\i tamei meis} \u8212- {\i ve-od ein aleihem chiyuv tumah} \u8212- and therefore they bear no further obligation of {\i tumah} prohibition with respect to additional contacts with the dead. {\i Ve-ha-mechayyev otam alav le-havi re\u8217'ayah} \u8212- whoever wishes to hold them liable bears the burden of proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ra\u8217'avad\u8217's position has obvious and significant implications. A word of caution is appropriate: some authorities read the Ra\u8217'avad as establishing {\i patur} \u8212- exemption from {\i malkus} \u8212- rather than {\i muttar} \u8212- full permission. On this reading, there may remain an {\i issur d\u8217'Oraita} or at least an {\i issur asei} even if lashes cannot be administered; the Ra\u8217'avad may only be saying that the person cannot be {\i mekhayyev} for lashes, not that the act is entirely permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the Ra\u8217'avad\u8217's reading has been invoked in discussions of whether a {\i kohen} may become a physician and thereby come into regular contact with deceased patients. The question of {\i kohen} doctors involves many additional considerations \u8212- questions of {\i pikuach nefesh}, of the nature of the prohibition in the absence of the {\i parah adumah} today, and of other grounds for leniency \u8212- but the Ra\u8217'avad\u8217's reinterpretation of this sugya remains a significant factor in that broader halachic conversation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 42b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf deals centrally with the transition to the third prohibition of the {\i nazir}\u8212-that of becoming {\i tamei l\u8217'meitim}\u8212-after the extended treatment of wine and shaving. The {\i Mishnah} at the bottom of 42a provides the framework, and the {\i Gemara} on 42b uses it as a springboard for what turns out to be one of the most consequential discussions in the tractate, with direct implications for the laws governing {\i kohanim} today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} presents three parallel cases. First: if a {\i nazir} was drinking wine the whole day, he receives only one set of lashes. This formulation is striking. Some {\i rishonim}\u8212-it may be the Rosh\u8212-hold that he is {\i chayav l\u8217'shamayim} for each separate act of drinking, each one constituting a distinct violation, yet receives only one set of lashes in practice if there was only a single {\i hatra\u8217'ah}. The question of whether to view this as multiple discrete acts or as one extended process is left open by the Mishnah itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if witnesses warned him before each drink\u8212-{\i al tishteh}, {\i al tishteh}\u8212-and he drank each time, he is {\i chayav al kol achat v\u8217'achat}: liable for each instance separately. Notably, the repeated warning is sufficient to define each drinking as a separate violation even if the drinks occurred within the halakhic unit of {\i toch k\u8217'dei shtiyat revi\u8217'it}, a timeframe that would ordinarily collapse multiple acts into one. In this direction, the warning functions not merely to satisfy a procedural requirement for {\i malkot}, but to categorically separate the acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same structure applies to shaving: a {\i nazir} who spent the entire day having his hair cut receives only one set of lashes; if warned before each hair\u8212-and as we established, even a single hair constitutes a violation\u8212-he is {\i chayav} for each one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel case for {\i tumah} follows the same pattern: if he was becoming {\i tamei l\u8217'meitim} all day without separate warnings, only one set of lashes; if warned {\i al titamei}, {\i al titamei} before each incident, {\i chayav al kol achat v\u8217'achat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Role of hatra\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The role of {\i hatra\u8217'ah} here is twofold. On one hand, in the absence of a separate warning, even clearly separate acts\u8212-a drinking in the morning and another days later\u8212-do not generate multiple sets of lashes. On the other hand, with repeated warnings, even acts that might halakhically be considered a single continuous event are divided into separately punishable transgressions. The warning, in other words, both activates and individuates the violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask about cases involving separate {\i lavin}: if a {\i nazir} was warned about drinking wine and about eating grapes\u8212-each a distinct prohibition\u8212-and did both simultaneously, he would receive multiple sets of lashes even for a single moment\u8217's act, provided the warnings addressed each prohibition distinctly. This point was developed earlier in the {\i Gemara}; the {\i Mishnah} here does not dwell on it, but it provides important background.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}, however, largely sets aside the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's primary teaching about {\i hatra\u8217'ah} and instead focuses on the specific case of {\i mitamei l\u8217'meitim}, a prohibition that raises conceptual questions not present in the same way for wine and hair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumah v\u8217'Tumah debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceptual framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's debate, it is worth noting the conceptual asymmetry among the three prohibitions. Drinking wine and getting a haircut can be understood as processes that unfold over time; there is at least a conceptual basis for viewing multiple acts of drinking or cutting as a single extended process. {\i Tumah}, by contrast, is not a process in the same sense. Each act of coming into contact with a {\i meit} is, almost by definition, discrete. On that basis, one might expect multiple acts of {\i tumah} to be treated as clearly separate violations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i Gemara} identifies a countervailing consideration: the prohibition of {\i tumah} may be oriented not toward the act of touching but toward the state of being {\i tamei}. The Torah states {\i lo yitama}\u8212-do not become {\i tamei}\u8212-which might be read as targeting the resulting condition rather than the physical contact. If so, once a {\i nazir} or {\i kohen} is already {\i tamei}, one can ask whether touching another corpse constitutes a new violation at all, since no new state of {\i tumah} is being acquired. This is the question the {\i Gemara} presses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens the dispute with a statement attributed to Rabbah in the name of Rav Huna. The Torah\u8217's fullness of expression\u8212-{\i mikra malei diber kasuv}\u8212-accounts for two distinct prohibitions. The verse {\i al kol nefesh meis lo yavo} addresses the act of entering under the same roof as a corpse ({\i bi\u8217'ah}), while {\i lo yitama} addresses the state of becoming {\i tamei}. These are conceptually separate {\i lavin}, and consequently, a {\i nazir} who enters a house containing a corpse violates both simultaneously and, with appropriate warnings, receives two sets of lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, draws a firm line: {\i aval tumah v\u8217'tumah lo}. If a {\i nazir} or {\i kohen} is already {\i tamei} and then touches another corpse\u8212-whether immediately upon removing his hand from the first or even while still in contact with the first\u8212-he does not incur an additional set of lashes. The prohibition {\i lo yitama} focuses on achieving the {\i state} of {\i tumah}. Once that state is present, no new violation is incurred through additional contact. Rava allows for the case of {\i tumah} combined with {\i bi\u8217'ah} as generating two violations, but {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might push back: is not touching a second corpse analogous to taking an additional sip of wine, where additional prohibited substance enters the body? The distinction is precisely that additional wine constitutes an additional quantity of the prohibited substance, whereas additional contact with a corpse does not change the person\u8217's {\i status} of {\i tumah}\u8212-which is what the prohibition targets. Indeed, touching a corpse on a later day does have a practical consequence, namely resetting the count toward the sprinkling of {\i parah adumah} ashes, but touching again on the same day when already {\i tamei} may have no such effect on the person\u8217's status at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds sharply: {\i hai elokim}, by God\u8217's name! He insists, citing Rav Huna, that even {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah} generates a separate set of lashes\u8212-{\i afilu tumah v\u8217'tumah}. The principle he articulates is illustrated by the case of a {\i nazir} standing in a cemetery who has been handed his deceased relative and then has contact with another corpse. Even while still actively {\i tamei} and still in proximity to the first body, he is {\i chayav} for the second contact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is that a separate act of touching, accompanied by a separate warning, produces a separate liability regardless of the person\u8217's prior state. The analogy would be a {\i nazir} in the midst of having his hair cut: if warned before each hair and he continues, each hair cut generates its own liability even though the process is continuous. Similarly here, each discrete act of touching a dead body, accompanied by its own {\i hatra\u8217'ah}, is independently actionable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position is more intuitive when one focuses on the act rather than the state: whatever the {\i nazir}\u8217's current condition, he performed another prohibited act of contact. The {\i Mishnah} appears to support Rav Yosef: it explicitly states that if warned {\i al titamei}, {\i al titamei}, becoming {\i tamei l\u8217'meitim} multiple times on the same day makes one {\i chayav al kol achat v\u8217'achat}. The {\i nazir} in the {\i Mishnah} is already {\i tamei} from the first contact; yet the {\i Mishnah} holds him liable for each subsequent one with a separate warning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: kohen case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rav Yosef from a {\i braita}. The case: a {\i kohen} had a corpse resting on his shoulder\u8212-already {\i tamei}\u8212-and then touched or was handed another corpse. The {\i braita} derives from the phrase {\i v\u8217'lo yichalel} that this prohibition applies only {\i b\u8217'mi she\u8217'eino mechulal}, to one whose sacred status has not yet been violated. It explicitly excludes {\i zeh she\u8217'mechulal v\u8217'omed}\u8212-one whose status is already violated. This appears to be a direct proof that {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah} does not generate additional liability, at least in the case of a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} acknowledges the potential to distinguish between a {\i kohen} and a {\i nazir}\u8212-the {\i braita} uses the language of {\i chillul}, desecration of sacred status, which is specific to the {\i kohen}\u8212-but the {\i Gemara}\u8217's framing proceeds on the assumption that the principles are shared, and does not develop this distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef defends\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef\u8217's response is striking in its directness: {\i v\u8217'tivshu lach masnitin}\u8212-you have a {\i braita} supporting you, but I have a {\i Mishnah} supporting me. The {\i Mishnah} explicitly holds a {\i nazir} liable for becoming {\i tamei} multiple times on the same day with separate warnings. The {\i Gemara} notes the rhetorical force of this: {\i v\u8217'amai hamitamei v\u8217'kai}\u8212-why should he be liable if he is already {\i tamei}? Yet the {\i Mishnah} says he is. This constitutes, in Rav Yosef\u8217's reading, a decisive counter to the {\i braita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then notes the apparent contradiction between the two sources: {\i ella kashya hadadi}, they contradict each other. Something must resolve the conflict between a {\i braita} that exempts and a {\i Mishnah} that obligates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chiburim resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiburim distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya kan b\u8217'chiburin kan shelo b\u8217'chiburin}\u8212-there is no difficulty; one source speaks of the case where the person is still in active physical contact ({\i b\u8217'chiburin}), and the other where he has separated ({\i shelo b\u8217'chiburin}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita}\u8217's case of exemption\u8212-the {\i kohen} with the corpse on his shoulder\u8212-is a case of {\i b\u8217'chiburin}: he remained in continuous contact with the first corpse. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's case of liability is {\i shelo b\u8217'chiburin}: he separated from the first corpse and then touched another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution requires explanation, since the distinction between remaining in contact and separating and returning might seem arbitrary. If touching a second corpse is a distinct act either way, why should the act of separation make a difference? The answer lies in the levels of {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avi avot tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While actively touching a {\i meit}, a person\u8217's {\i tumah} status is elevated. The {\i meit} is an {\i avi avot hatumah}\u8212-a \u8220"grandparent of impurity\u8221"\u8212-the highest category. Under normal circumstances, a person who is an {\i av hatumah} (such as a {\i zav} or {\i niddah}) transmits a one-day {\i tumah} to those who touch them; they become a {\i rishon}. But the {\i meit}, as {\i avi avot}, makes even the person who directly touches it into an {\i av}\u8212-a seven-day {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The further phenomenon is {\i tumah b\u8217'chiburin}, which operates through daisy-chain contact: if person A is touching the {\i meit} and person B is simultaneously touching person A, then B is considered, for the duration of the contact, as having the elevated seven-day {\i tumah} status normally reserved for one who directly touched the {\i meit}. This is the principle sometimes referenced as {\i dikrav ledikrav}\u8212-one who touches one who touches the dead body while they are all in contact simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus, while a {\i nazir} or {\i kohen} is actively touching a corpse, his {\i tumah} is at its absolute maximum. Touching a second corpse in that state does not change his level in any way. The {\i Gemara} therefore rules: when actively touching, no new violation is incurred. But once he removes his hand, his {\i tumah} level decreases\u8212-he is no longer at the {\i avi avot} functional level but reverts to the ordinary {\i av} status. Touching a new corpse then raises his status again, constituting a meaningful new act with real consequences, and that is when a second violation is incurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derabbanan limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a pointed question: is {\i tumah b\u8217'chiburin}\u8212-the principle that transmitted {\i tumah} is elevated during active daisy-chain contact\u8212-a {\i d\u8217'oraita} rule, or is it rabbinic? The {\i Gemara} cites Rav Yitzchak bar Yosef in the name of Rav Yanai: {\i lo amru tumah b\u8217'chiburin ela l\u8217'trumah ul\u8217'kodashim aval l\u8217'nazir v\u8217'oseh pesach lo}. The elevated status in the daisy-chain scenario applies, as a matter of stringency, to {\i terumah} and {\i kodashim}, but not to a {\i nazir} or one who needs to bring a {\i korban pesach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case: Shimon touched Reuven while Reuven was touching the {\i meit} on Sunday. Were this a seven-day {\i tumah d\u8217'oraita}, Shimon would be prohibited from eating his {\i korban pesach} on the night following the third day. But the {\i Gemara} indicates that if Shimon comes and says he needs to eat his {\i korban pesach}, or that his {\i nezirut} is at stake, we tell him: in your situation, you are only a one-day {\i tamei}\u8212-immerse in the {\i mikveh} and proceed. The willingness to set aside the seven-day status in these high-stakes situations is evidence that the elevated status derived from {\i chiburin} is only {\i derabbanan}. Were it {\i d\u8217'oraita}, we could not simply waive it for {\i pesach} or {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} refines this analysis with a critical distinction: {\i kan b\u8217'chiburei adam b\u8217'adam, kan b\u8217'chiburei adam b\u8217'meis}\u8212-one case involves the chain between a person and the {\i meit} directly ({\i adam b\u8217'meis}), and the other involves a person connected to another person who is connected to the {\i meit} ({\i adam b\u8217'adam}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reading, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's conclusion is: the elevated {\i tumah} of person B who directly touches person A while A is touching the {\i meit}\u8212-this is {\i d\u8217'oraita}, and applies even for {\i pesach} and {\i nazir}. It is the third link\u8212-person C touching person B while B touches A while A touches the {\i meit}\u8212-that is {\i derabbanan}, and it is this third-link status that we are willing to set aside when {\i pesach} or {\i nezirut} are at stake. Thus leniency for {\i nazir v\u8217'oseh pesach} applies only from the third link outward. The second link, {\i chiburei adam b\u8217'meis}, remains a full {\i d\u8217'oraita} seven-day {\i tumah} under all circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} reads the {\i Gemara}\u8217's conclusion differently\u8212-he understands the {\i maskana} to be that even the second-link {\i chiburin} is {\i derabbanan}, such that even Shimon\u8217's elevated status would be waived for {\i pesach} and {\i nezirut}. This yields a broader leniency but is difficult to reconcile with the plain reading of the {\i Gemara} that the {\i Rambam} himself cites.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any case, the structural resolution of the original contradiction stands: while actively touching, the person is at maximum {\i tumah} status and touching another body changes nothing; once separated, his status diminishes and renewed contact constitutes a fresh transgression. This is the principle of {\i kan b\u8217'chiburin, kan shelo b\u8217'chiburin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bia v\u8217'tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 State vs.\u160 ?act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah} question resolved, the {\i Gemara} turns to the interaction between the prohibition of {\i tumah} and the separate prohibition of {\i bi\u8217'ah}\u8212-entering a space under the same roof as a corpse. As Rav Huna established, these are two conceptually distinct {\i lavin}: {\i lo yitama} targets the state of becoming {\i tamei}, while {\i lo yavo} targets the act of entering. A {\i nazir} who walks into a house containing a corpse violates both simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks a sharp question: {\i tumah u\u8217'vi\u8217'ah nami hamitamei v\u8217'ka\u8217'ei}\u8212-is this not the same problem we just resolved with {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah}? If we say that a person who is actively {\i tamei} and then touches another body does not incur a second violation because he is already at full {\i tumah} status, why should he incur a second violation for {\i bi\u8217'ah} when he is walking in while already in contact with a body? He is already {\i tamei} to the fullest degree; entering under the roof changes nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} provides a careful reading of this question and its resolution. The scenario being analyzed: when we said that {\i tumah v\u8217'bi\u8217'ah} produces two sets of lashes, we meant a case where the person was not yet {\i tamei}\u8212-he walked into a house containing a corpse and thereby incurred both prohibitions simultaneously through a single act. The parallel case in the field would be touching two corpses at once: also one act, but only one set of lashes, since there is only one relevant {\i lav} in play outdoors ({\i lo yitama} without a {\i bi\u8217'ah} dimension). The point is {\i kan babayis, kan basadeh}\u8212-the {\i bi\u8217'ah} prohibition is architecturally distinct and adds a second violation only in the house context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The follow-on question is whether carrying a corpse into a house\u8212-actively touching a {\i meit} and then entering under a roof\u8212-generates both violations. Based on the {\i chiburin} analysis, the answer should be no: since you are already at maximum {\i tumah} status while touching, entering the house adds nothing to your {\i tumah} state, and therefore, by analogy to {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah b\u8217'chiburin}, you should not incur the second prohibition either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} reads the {\i Gemara}\u8217's reply\u8212-{\i kan babayis, kan basadeh}\u8212-to mean precisely this: if you are carrying a corpse and walk into a house, you do {\i not} receive two sets of lashes. The two-set liability applies only when you were not already touching anything and you entered the house. The parallel to the field (two corpses at once, not {\i b\u8217'chiburin}) also yields only one set of lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa\u8217's box case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presses toward a case where {\i tumah} and {\i bi\u8217'ah} occur with perfect simultaneity. The difficulty is that under normal circumstances, entering a building is not truly instantaneous: some part of the body\u8212-a hand, a nose, a toe\u8212-enters before the rest, and at that moment the person becomes {\i tamei} while not yet having completed the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah}. By the time he has fully entered, he is already {\i tamei} and the additional violation of {\i lo yavo} arrives fractionally later, when he is already actively {\i tamei}\u8212-again raising the {\i chiburin} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar attempts a solution: if one enters with the arm flattened against the body\u8212-entering in a single configuration\u8212-perhaps {\i tumah} and {\i bi\u8217'ah} arrive together. The {\i Gemara} objects: the nose goes in first ({\i efshar d\u8217'lo ayel hotmo b\u8217'reisha}), generating {\i tumah} before the act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} is complete. The same problem afflicts entry with toes first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa\u8217's resolution: the case is one where the person entered inside a {\i shida}, {\i teiva u\u8217'migdal}\u8212-a sealed box or cabinet\u8212-and a companion came and removed the roof covering ({\i para alav et ham\u8217'aziva}). At that moment, both {\i tumah} and {\i bi\u8217'ah} arrive at once, since the person was sealed off from the corpse and its {\i ohel} until the roof was removed. {\i Tumah} and {\i bi\u8217'ah} arrive simultaneously: {\i tumah u\u8217'vi\u8217'ah bahada hadadi ka\u8217'atyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes that for this to generate two sets of lashes, the person must have been warned inside the box and must have been the active agent in breaking out, not simply passive as a companion does the work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goses case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi offers a further case: {\i k\u8217'gon d\u8217'ayal k\u8217'shehu goses}\u8212-a person entered a room while its occupant was in the final death throes ({\i goses}). Since a {\i goses} is presumed to be {\i rov gosesim l\u8217'misa}\u8212-the majority of those in this state die\u8212-and the person was warned and chose to remain, at the moment the {\i goses} dies, the person simultaneously commits {\i bi\u8217'ah} (remaining present under the same roof) and incurs {\i tumah}: {\i tumah u\u8217'vi\u8217'ah bahada hadadi ka\u8217'atyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case raises a question not addressed earlier: since the person was already in the room before death, there was no act of entering ({\i bo}). The {\i Gemara} may be reading {\i lo yavo} as including willful remaining present at the moment the house acquires a {\i meit}, but this requires further examination, which leads naturally to the next major discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Goses violation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen vs.\u160 ?nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} addresses directly whether a {\i kohen} or {\i nazir} violates by being present with a {\i goses}. The {\i braita} brings two formulations. One position\u8212-derived from the phrase {\i l\u8217'hechalo}, referring to desecration of status\u8212-holds that a {\i kohen} does not violate until the person has actually died ({\i ad she\u8217'yamus}), meaning a {\i goses} does not yet generate a violation. Another position\u8212-attributed to Rabbi, derived from {\i b\u8217'mosam}, \u8220"in their death\u8221"\u8212-similarly requires actual death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interpretation debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks what distinguishes the two derivations. Rabbi Yochanan holds that there is no practical difference; both formulations simply establish that violation requires death. Reish Lakish identifies the {\i goses} as the point of contention: the view derived from {\i l\u8217'hechalo} would include even a {\i goses}, while the view derived from {\i b\u8217'mosam} explicitly excludes it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning for including a {\i goses} under {\i l\u8217'hechalo} is that the word can be read as future-oriented and anticipatory, or as referring to an abstract act of {\i chillul} of one\u8217's sacred status\u8212-placing oneself in a situation of risk may itself constitute a form of desecration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goses inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} traces the implications of each position through the textual surplus. The word {\i b\u8217'mosam} (\u8220"in their death\u8221") rather than simply {\i b\u8217'mot} carries a possessive, which allows the {\i Gemara} to derive two teachings simultaneously: (1) the prohibition applies only to {\i tumah} of corpses, not to other forms of {\i tumah} such as {\i tzara\u8217'at} or {\i zivah}\u8212-a {\i nazir} who touches a {\i zav} does not violate the prohibition of {\i tumah l\u8217'meitim}; and (2) the violation requires actual death ({\i ad she\u8217'yamus}). The word {\i lehachelo} similarly yields two teachings: (1) the violation applies only {\i b\u8217'mi she\u8217'eino mechulal}\u8212-to one whose status has not yet been violated\u8212-and (2) a {\i goses} may be included.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Soul departure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is then cited that appears to challenge the inclusion of a {\i goses}: {\i adam eino mitamei ela ad she\u8217'teitzei nafsho}\u8212-a person does not transmit {\i tumah} until the soul actually departs. Even someone who has been grievously wounded, even a {\i goses}, does not yet transmit {\i tumah}. This appears to directly contradict the position that includes a {\i goses} in the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status vs.\u160 ?desecration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this tension with a distinction of categories. The {\i braita} is correct that a {\i goses} does not cause {\i tumah} in the halakhic sense\u8212-that requires death. However, for purposes of {\i chillul}\u8212-violation or desecration of status\u8212-one transgresses by entering the presence of a {\i goses}: {\i l\u8217'inyan achluyei ha ichluyei}. The violation is not that one has become {\i tamei} through the {\i goses}, but that one has placed oneself in a situation that constitutes a form of desecration of one\u8217's sacred status as {\i kohen} or {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shulchan Aruch ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} at the end of the page notes that we rule like Rabbi Yochanan\u8212-and depending on the reading of the text, if the positions are reassigned to Abaye and Rava respectively, we rule like Rava. The practical question is whether a {\i kohen} is prohibited from entering the room of a {\i goses} who is not an immediate relative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Shulchan Aruch}\u8217's ruling is notably ambiguous. It states that a {\i goses} does not transmit {\i tumah} until the soul departs, even if the person is {\i meguyad} (gravely mutilated) or in extremis. However, it then adds: {\i umikol makom assur lehikanes l\u8217'bayis sheyesh bo goses}\u8212-it is nonetheless forbidden to enter a house where a {\i goses} lies. The {\i Rema} adds that it is good to be stringent. The language of {\i assur} without further qualification leaves unclear whether this is a {\i d\u8217'oraita} violation, a {\i derabbanan} enactment, or a stringency\u8212-precisely the ambiguity that {\i Tosafot} flagged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Practical consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot kohen rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} draws a clear practical conclusion from the {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution: a {\i kohen} who is already {\i tamei} because he has been burying a relative cannot on that basis permit himself to become {\i tamei} to other corpses on the same day. Even though he is currently {\i tamei}, the moment he is no longer in direct physical contact with the relative he was burying, he is no longer {\i b\u8217'chiburin}, and any subsequent contact with another corpse constitutes a fresh violation for which he would be liable to {\i malkot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kohen} cannot reason that since he is already {\i tamei} there is nothing further to lose. The leniency of {\i zeh she\u8217'mechulal v\u8217'omed} is limited to the precise moment of active contact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cemetery exit problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates a significant practical problem. A {\i kohen} who has been burying a relative in a cemetery must eventually leave. But as soon as he concludes the burial and separates from the body, he is no longer {\i b\u8217'chiburin} with the first corpse. Walking out of the cemetery now risks becoming {\i tamei} to other graves\u8212-whether by {\i ohel} under a shared tree or by passing over a grave. Each such encounter would constitute a separate transgression, punishable by {\i malkot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kohen} must essentially run out of the cemetery trying to avoid being {\i ma\u8217'ahil} under the same tree or standing over other graves. The problem is real and pressing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edge burial solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} provides a historical and practical resolution, citing it in the name of great authorities: the practice was to bury the {\i meit} at the very edge of the cemetery, so that the {\i kohen} could complete the burial and exit immediately without traversing the rest of the cemetery grounds. This minimizes\u8212-though cannot entirely eliminate\u8212-the risk of subsequent {\i tumah} encounters. Additionally, the practice of not planting trees in cemeteries is connected to this concern: without trees providing a shared {\i ohel} over multiple graves, the risk of inadvertent {\i tumah} transmission through {\i ohel} is reduced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ra\u8217'avad reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'avad\u8217's reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ra\u8217'avad}, in his critique of the {\i Rambam}\u8217's {\i Hilchot Nazir}, offers a radical reinterpretation of the entire {\i sugya}. He states: {\i i\u8217'yanti b\u8217'shmuah b\u8217'Maseches Nazir}\u8212-I have studied this {\i sugya} carefully. His reading inverts the positions of Rava and Rav Yosef as they have been understood above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}, the entire discussion of {\i b\u8217'chiburin} and {\i shelo b\u8217'chiburin}\u8212-the principle that while actively touching one body, contact with another does not constitute a new violation, while separation followed by renewed contact does\u8212-was Rav Yosef\u8217's position, not Rava\u8217's. That is, the {\i strict} position is that of Rav Yosef: you are liable when you separate and touch again ({\i shelo b\u8217'chiburin}), but not when you are actively touching ({\i b\u8217'chiburin}). This is more restrictive than the conventional reading, which took {\i shelo b\u8217'chiburin} liability as the lenient ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lenient position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}\u8217's reading, Rava\u8217's position is significantly more lenient: even {\i shelo b\u8217'chiburin}\u8212-even after separating from the first corpse and touching another\u8212-a person who was already {\i tamei} does not incur a new set of lashes. Rava holds that once you are {\i tamei}, no subsequent act of becoming {\i tamei} generates liability, regardless of whether you remained in contact or not. The focus remains on the state of {\i tumah}: if you were already in that state, you have not achieved anything new.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'avad conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this reading, the {\i Ra\u8217'avad} draws a far-reaching halakhic conclusion: {\i d\u8217'keivan d\u8217'kaima lan Rava v\u8217'Rav Yosef hilchasa k\u8217'Rava}\u8212-since we rule like Rava against Rav Yosef in their disputes, and Rava\u8217's position is that even {\i shelo b\u8217'chiburin} generates no new liability, it follows that {\i tumah v\u8217'tumah afilu peirash v\u8217'hazar v\u8217'naga patur}\u8212-even if a person separated and returned and touched again, he is exempt from {\i malkot}. He then explicitly applies this to {\i kohanim} in the present era: {\i v\u8217'hakohanim bizman hazeh tmei meisim hem v\u8217'od ein aleihem hiyuv tumah}\u8212-{\i kohanim} today are all presumed to be {\i tamei meis}, and under the ruling like Rava, they bear no further obligation of {\i tumah}. Anyone who holds them liable bears the burden of proof: {\i v\u8217'hamihayev osam alav l\u8217'havi raya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Muttar vs.\u160 ?patur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A significant qualification must be noted: it is not clear that the {\i Ra\u8217'avad} declares such contact {\i muttar}\u8212-permitted\u8212-as opposed to merely {\i patur}\u8212-exempt from {\i malkot}. The distinction is consequential. One can be free from lashes while still subject to a {\i lav} or even a {\i mitzvas aseh}. Some authorities read the {\i Ra\u8217'avad} as saying only that there are no lashes, leaving open the question of whether an {\i issur d\u8217'oraita} or {\i issur asei} remains in place. Others understand him more broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question bears directly on the contemporary discussion of whether a {\i kohen} may become a physician, where contact with deceased patients is frequent and often unavoidable. Some authorities have sought to incorporate the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}\u8217's reinterpretation as one factor within a broader analysis involving {\i pikuach nefesh}. Whether the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}\u8217's position can bear that weight\u8212-and whether it says {\i muttar} or only {\i patur}\u8212-remains a matter of halakhic debate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 44\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tiglachat Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 44b opens as the tractate transitions away from the previous discussions of the prohibitions of {\i tumah}, {\i yayin}, and {\i tiglachat} that defined the earlier sugyot. Each of those three topics, it is worth pausing to note, served as a springboard into broader areas of {\i halacha}: {\i tumah} opened the discussion of {\i tumat kohanim}, {\i tiglachat} shed light on the general laws of shaving, and {\i yayin} became a paradigm for the principle of {\i ta\u8217'am k\u8217'ikar} in the world of {\i kashrut}. Having covered those three prohibitions, the Mishnah now turns to the rituals associated with the conclusion of a Nazir\u8217's period of impurity \u8212- the {\i tiglachat tumah} \u8212- and then to the shaving at the conclusion of the period of purity, the {\i tiglachat taharah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah opens with the question: {\i tiglachat tumah keitzad} \u8212- how is the shaving of impurity performed? The Mishnah addresses a very specific question, as will become clear from what follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day Seven Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah describes the process: {\i hayah mazeh bashlishi u\u8217'vashvi\u8217'i} \u8212- the sprinkling of the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} takes place on day three and day seven. Then {\i u\u8217'megalei\u8217'ach bashvi\u8217'i} \u8212- the Nazir shaves on day seven. Finally, {\i u\u8217'meivi korbanotav bashmini} \u8212- the {\i korbanot} are brought on day eight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This structure follows directly from the Torah\u8217's language: {\i v\u8217'chi yamut meit alav b\u8217'feta pit\u8217'om v\u8217'tame rosh nizro v\u8217'gilach rosho b\u8217'yom taharato b\u8217'yom hashvi\u8217'i yigalchennu} \u8212- and on day seven he shaves. {\i U\u8217'v\u8217'yom hashmini yavi shtei torim o shnei bnei yonah} \u8212- and on day eight he brings the {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then teaches: {\i v\u8217'im gilach bashmini meivi korbanotav bo vayom divrei Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- if for some reason he postponed the shaving until day eight, he may still bring the {\i korbanot} on that same day eight. The {\i halacha} is not that the {\i korbanot} must be brought the day after the shaving; the {\i halacha} fixes the {\i korbanot} on day eight. If he shaved on day seven as intended, the {\i korbanot} come on day eight. If he postponed the shaving to day eight itself, the {\i korbanot} are still brought that same day eight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva vs Tarfon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarfon\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon poses a challenge: {\i mah bein zeh l\u8217'metzora} \u8212- how is the Nazir\u8217's case different from that of the {\i metzora}? Rabbi Tarfon takes it as given that if a {\i metzora} waits to perform his shaving until day eight, the {\i korban} is deferred to day nine. On that premise, he argues that the same logic should apply to the Nazir: if the shaving was delayed to day eight, the {\i korban} should only come on day nine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that for the {\i metzora} the delay is not precisely the same mechanism as for the Nazir \u8212- it involves a seven-day wait until the {\i kohen} revisits him, rather than a seven-day sprinkling with {\i parah adumah} ashes \u8212- but the structural parallel is what Rabbi Tarfon is drawing on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva responds: {\i zeh taharato tluyah b\u8217'yamav u\u8217'metzora taharato tluyah b\u8217'tiglachto} \u8212- by the Nazir, becoming {\i tahor} is dependent on the days, whereas by the {\i metzora}, the shaving itself is part of the {\i tahara} process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Tahara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does it mean that the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving is part of the {\i tahara} process, and how do we know it? Tosafot points out that the textual evidence lies in a careful comparison of the respective {\i pesukim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the Nazir, the Torah simply says {\i v\u8217'gilach rosho} \u8212- he shaves his head \u8212- and then {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'yom hashmini yavi shtei torim} \u8212- on day eight he brings the {\i korbanot}. The shaving is mentioned, but there is no subsequent {\i mikvah} immersion required as part of the Nazir\u8217's {\i tahara}; the {\i tahara} from {\i tumei meit} was accomplished through the sprinkling of the {\i parah adumah} ashes on days three and seven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i metzora}, by contrast, undergoes a different sequence. After the Kohen confirms the healing and the bird ritual is performed, the Torah states: {\i v\u8217'kibbes hamit-taher b\u8217'gadav v\u8217'gilach et kol se\u8217'aro v\u8217'rachatz bamayim v\u8217'taher v\u8217'achar yavo el hamachaneh} \u8212- the one being purified launders his garments, shaves all his hair, immerses in the waters, and becomes {\i tahor}, and thereafter he may return to the camp. There follows a seven-day wait. Then on day seven the Torah specifies again: {\i yegale\u8217'ach et kol se\u8217'aro et rosho v\u8217'et zkano v\u8217'et gabot einav v\u8217'et kol se\u8217'aro yegale\u8217'ach v\u8217'kibes et bgadav v\u8217'rachatz et bsaro bamayim v\u8217'taher} \u8212- he shaves all his hair and then immerses in water and becomes {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This second shaving of the {\i metzora} on day seven is thus explicitly followed by a {\i mikvah} immersion, making that shaving an integral component of the {\i tahara} process. After immersing in the {\i mikvah}, the {\i metzora} is a {\i tevul yom} \u8212- someone who has immersed but for whom sunset has not yet arrived. Since it is clear that the Torah requires waiting until the {\i tevul yom} status has resolved before bringing the {\i korban} \u8212- the {\i korban} being on day eight \u8212- it follows that if the shaving was delayed to day eight, he would need to immerse again on day eight, become a {\i tevul yom} again on day eight, and therefore only be able to bring his {\i korban} on day nine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's reading sharpens Rabbi Akiva\u8217's distinction through a precise analysis of the {\i pesukim}. By the {\i metzora}, the shaving is part of the {\i tahara} process: it is an act that must precede {\i mikvah} immersion, and it is only after that immersion and the subsequent nightfall that the {\i metzora} exits {\i tevul yom} status and is fully {\i tahor}. Therefore, if the shaving was delayed by a day, the entire {\i tahara} sequence is pushed forward, and with it, the {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the Nazir who became {\i tamei meit}, no such chain exists. His {\i tahara} is accomplished through the days-long sprinkling process. The shaving is a ritual of {\i nezirut}, not a step within the {\i tahara} framework. There is no {\i mikvah} immersion mandated after the Nazir\u8217's shaving. Consequently, by day seven, when the sprinkling has been completed and the Nazir goes to the {\i mikvah} as required for {\i tumei meit}, he is already a {\i tevul yom}, and by day eight he is fully {\i tahor} regardless of whether he has shaved yet. The shaving can therefore take place on day eight without any consequence for the {\i korban}, which remains fixed on day eight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Comparative Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize Rabbi Akiva\u8217's principle: {\i metzora taharato tluyah b\u8217'tiglachto} \u8212- the shaving is part of the {\i tahara} process, it is followed by {\i mikvah} immersion, and the {\i tevul yom} status that results requires waiting until the next day. {\i V\u8217'ein meivi korban ela im kein he\u8217'eriv shimsho} \u8212- one may only bring the {\i korban} after nightfall concludes the {\i tevul yom} status. If the {\i metzora} waits to shave until day eight, the {\i mikvah} immersion on day eight makes him a {\i tevul yom} on day eight, and the {\i korban} can only come on day nine. For the Nazir who is {\i tamei meit}, the {\i tahara} process is the sprinkling on days three and seven; the shaving is a separate ritual unconnected to the {\i tahara} mechanism, and postponing it to day eight leaves the {\i korban} unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel\u8217's Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i kiblah mineih o lo} \u8212- did Rabbi Tarfon accept Rabbi Akiva\u8217's answer? The Mishnah gives us no indication. The Gemara therefore brings a {\i braita}: {\i detani Hillel} \u8212- Hillel (the Amora, not Hillel HaZaken) taught: {\i gilach bashmini meivi korbanotav batshi\u8217'i} \u8212- if he shaved on day eight, he brings his {\i korbanot} on day nine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: who is the subject of this {\i braita} \u8212- the Nazir or the {\i metzora}? Tosafot explains that there was a tradition ({\i masorah}) that this {\i braita} refers to the Nazir, since the ruling regarding the {\i metzora} is undisputed \u8212- if a {\i metzora} postpones his shaving to day eight, the {\i korban} is on day nine, and that was never in question. The {\i braita} is therefore addressing the Nazir\u8217's case, and the only authority who would maintain that a Nazir who postponed shaving to day eight must bring his {\i korban} on day nine is Rabbi Tarfon \u8212- implying that Rabbi Tarfon did not concede to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's argument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers an alternative reading: {\i lo kashya} \u8212- the {\i braita} need not be read as a disagreement with Rabbi Akiva at all. The reason the {\i korban} is deferred to day nine in the {\i braita}\u8217's case is not that the shaving was postponed, but rather that the {\i mikvah} immersion was postponed. {\i Ha de\u8217'taval bashvi\u8217'i ha delo taval bashvi\u8217'i} \u8212- one case is where the Nazir immersed in the {\i mikvah} on day seven; the other case is where he did not immerse until day eight. Even for a Nazir who is {\i tamei meit}, the {\i mikvah} immersion produces {\i tevul yom} status, and if the immersion occurred on day eight, the {\i korban} can only come on day nine. On this reading, even Rabbi Tarfon agrees with Rabbi Akiva, and the {\i braita} is simply describing a case of delayed immersion rather than delayed shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Critical Evaluation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's reading, however, is problematic. If the {\i braita} intended to say that the immersion was delayed, it should have said {\i taval bashmini} \u8212- he immersed on day eight. Instead, it says {\i gilach bashmini} \u8212- he shaved on day eight. Rava has introduced a reference to delayed immersion that does not appear in the text of the {\i braita}, and the plain meaning of the {\i braita} clearly points to shaving as the operative act. The straightforward reading of the {\i braita}, assuming it refers to a Nazir, supports the position that Rabbi Tarfon did not concede.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tevul Yom Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises: is it really so clear that a {\i tevul yom} cannot have {\i korbanot} brought on his behalf? What is the {\i halacha} regarding a {\i tamei} person\u8217's ability to have his {\i korbanot} offered?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} is that {\i erev ve-tamei hamishalchin korbanoseihem} \u8212- both an {\i erev} and a {\i tamei} may send their {\i korbanot} to be brought by a proxy. However, this applies only to milder forms of {\i tumah} such as {\i sheretz} or {\i neveila}. If the person is {\i tamei meit}, a {\i zav}, a {\i zavah}, or a {\i metzora}, they may not have a {\i korban} brought on their behalf. This is a basic point of {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Tahora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even setting aside that rule, Tosafot makes a more fundamental point: it is implicit from the {\i pesukim} themselves that, for these specific {\i korbanot}, the Torah requires the person to be fully {\i tahor} before bringing them. By the Nazir the Torah specifies day seven for the sprinkling and shaving, and day eight for the {\i korban} \u8212- why would the Torah not simply have him bring the {\i korban} on day seven itself? By the {\i metzora}, the Torah similarly separates the {\i tahara} process from the {\i korban} by an explicit wait. The plain sense of the {\i pesukim} is that the Torah demands that the person exit the state of {\i tumah} entirely \u8212- including exiting {\i tevul yom} status \u8212- before offering the relevant {\i korban}. Whatever may be debated about other {\i korbanot} such as {\i shlamim}, for the particular {\i korbanot} of the Nazir\u8217's restart and the {\i metzora}\u8217's purification, the Torah clearly anticipates full {\i tahara} as a prerequisite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zav Entry Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zav\u8217's Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye reports that he encountered the colleagues of Rav Nassan bar Oshaya who were discussing the following {\i braita}. A {\i zav} undergoes a seven-day {\i tahara} process: {\i v\u8217'chi yithar hazav mizovo v\u8217'safar lo shivat yamim letaharato v\u8217'kibes bgadav v\u8217'rachatz besaro b\u8217'mayim chayim v\u8217'taher} \u8212- the {\i zav} counts seven days, launders his garments, immerses in living waters, and becomes {\i tahor}. On day eight he comes to bring his {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Entry Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} asks: {\i eimatai hu ba} \u8212- when may he enter? {\i Bizman shehu taval v\u8217'asah ha\u8217'erev shemesh} \u8212- only when he has immersed in the {\i mikvah} and nightfall has passed, meaning he is no longer a {\i tevul yom}. {\i Lo taval v\u8217'asah ha\u8217'erev shemesh} \u8212- if he had not done so, {\i lo} \u8212- he may not enter the {\i Mishkan} grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevul Yom Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this {\i braita}, the colleagues of Rav Nassan bar Oshaya sought to infer: {\i alma kasavar tevul yom shel zav ke-zav dami} \u8212- the {\i braita} apparently holds that even the {\i tevul yom} status of a {\i zav} retains the full status of a {\i zav} with respect to access to sacred ground. The {\i zav} is forbidden from entering {\i Machaneh Leviyah}, and the {\i braita} suggests this restriction persists even after the {\i mikvah} immersion, as long as he remains a {\i tevul yom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Har Habayit Access\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machaneh Leviyah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question has concrete practical relevance. {\i Machaneh Leviyah}, the camp of the Levites in the wilderness, corresponds in the present era to {\i Har HaBayit}. A {\i zav} was prohibited from entering {\i Machaneh Leviyah} and would thereby transgress by entering {\i Har HaBayit}. The question, then, is whether a {\i tevul yom} of a {\i zav} retains this restriction after immersion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical issue arises in the context of those who lead groups on tours of {\i Har HaBayit}. Those tours deliberately avoid the {\i makom hamikdash} itself, proceeding only through the parts of {\i Har HaBayit} that correspond to {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. The assumption underlying such tours is that even {\i tamei meit} \u8212- which we must presume applies to everyone today \u8212- does not disqualify a person from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. However, one who carries a {\i tumah yotzei migufo} \u8212- a {\i tumah} that originates from within the body, such as a {\i zav} or a woman who is a {\i niddah} \u8212- is in a categorically different position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baal Keri Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific problem arises with a man who has had a seminal emission \u8212- a {\i ba\u8217'al keri}. Even if he immerses in the {\i mikvah} before ascending {\i Har HaBayit}, he is a {\i tevul yom} during daylight hours. The question is whether a {\i tevul yom} of a {\i ba\u8217'al keri}, analogous to a {\i tevul yom} of a {\i zav}, carries the same entry restrictions as the original {\i tumah} \u8212- in which case ascending {\i Har HaBayit} as a {\i tevul yom} would be prohibited. Whether tour organizers routinely address this scenario is unclear, but the halakhic question is a genuine one that flows directly from this sugya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One should note that the situation of a woman who is a {\i niddah} is structurally different: she would not achieve {\i tevul yom} status during the day in the same way. But for a man who had a seminal emission and immerses in the morning before a daytime tour, the {\i tevul yom} status is directly operative and the question directly applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zav-Nazir Compare\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye reports that he responded to the colleagues of Rav Nassan bar Oshaya. He acknowledged the inference that {\i tevul yom shel zav ke-zav dami}, and then pressed the logic further: if the {\i pesukim} by the {\i zav} teach that he cannot enter the sacred precinct until he is no longer a {\i tevul yom}, then apply the same reasoning to the Nazir. The {\i pesukim} by the Nazir who became {\i tamei} also describe his coming {\i el petach ohel moed} on day eight to bring his {\i korbanot}. By the same logic, one could infer that on day seven, as a {\i tevul yom}, the Nazir was prohibited from entering. But this cannot be right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason it cannot be right is that the Nazir is {\i tamei meit}, and {\i tamei meit} is permitted to enter {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. Not only that, but even on day six when he was still fully {\i tamei meit} \u8212- before any sprinkling, before any immersion \u8212- he could freely enter {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. On day seven, as a {\i tevul yom} of {\i tumei meit}, he is categorically less {\i tamei} than he was on day six. If on day six he was permitted in {\i Machaneh Leviyah}, how could day seven\u8217's {\i tevul yom} status be read as creating an entry restriction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dead Body Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point is reinforced by a {\i braita}: not only is a person who is {\i tamei meit} permitted to enter {\i Machaneh Leviyah}, but {\i afilu meis atzmo} \u8212- even a corpse itself may be present in {\i Machaneh Leviyah}, as we learn from the verse {\i vayikach Moshe et atzmot Yosef imo} \u8212- Moshe took the bones of Yosef with him. {\i Mai imo \u8212- imo b\u8217'machaneh leviyah} \u8212- the bones of Yosef were present within {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. Since even a corpse is permitted there, the {\i pesukim} regarding the Nazir\u8217's day eight entry cannot possibly be read as implying that his {\i tevul yom} status on day seven barred him from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. Those {\i pesukim} must be read differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevul Yom Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye proceeds to distinguish between different categories of {\i tevul yom}. According to Tosafot and the Rosh, whose {\i girsa} includes the word {\i lav}, the correct reading of Abaye\u8217's conclusion is: {\i lav ke-zav dami} \u8212- a {\i tevul yom shel zav} is ordinarily not treated as a {\i zav} for purposes of entry restrictions. A {\i ba\u8217'al keri} who immerses in the {\i mikvah} and is now a {\i tevul yom}, or a {\i zav} who saw an emission only twice and therefore does not need to bring a {\i korban}, would fall into this category of an ordinary {\i tevul yom} \u8212- and such a person\u8217's {\i tevul yom} status would not carry the entry restriction of the original {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the case of the {\i zav} in the {\i braita} is different. This {\i zav} has seen an emission three times and is therefore obligated to bring a {\i korban} on day eight. After immersing in the {\i mikvah} on day seven, he is not merely a {\i tevul yom} waiting for nightfall. He is a {\i tevul yom} who is also {\i mechussar kippurim} \u8212- someone who still requires a {\i korban} to complete his {\i tahara} process. His {\i tevul yom} status is therefore not the naturally transitory state of someone who simply needs to wait for nightfall; there remains a further affirmative act \u8212- the bringing of the {\i korban} \u8212- without which he will not be fully {\i tahor}. This more complex status is treated more severely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question arises: where does one find a textual basis for restricting this special category of {\i tevul yom shel zav} from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}? The {\i pesukim} describing the {\i zav}\u8217's day eight entry use the language {\i u\u8217'va lifnei Hashem el pesach ohel moed}. As established earlier, the {\i pesukim} that say he comes on day eight are not teaching that he was prohibited beforehand \u8212- as demonstrated by the Nazir\u8217's case, where such an inference would be untenable. Rather, those {\i pesukim} are fixing the day on which the {\i korban} may be brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Circular Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's reasoning has an acknowledged circularity at this point. If the {\i pesukim} describing entry to {\i pesach ohel moed} are not proving an entry restriction, one cannot derive from them that the {\i tevul yom shel zav} was restricted from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. Abaye appears to concede the thrust of his own earlier challenge and then to reframe the analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Entry Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reframing proceeds as follows: while those {\i pesukim} do not demonstrate that the {\i zav} was prohibited from {\i Machaneh Leviyah} during his {\i tevul yom} period, they do describe him as entering on day eight, and since on day eight he is in {\i Machaneh Leviyah} before entering the inner precincts, the {\i pesukim} place him in {\i Machaneh Leviyah} using the language {\i pesach ohel moed}. The question then becomes: why does the Torah describe {\i Machaneh Leviyah} using the terminology of {\i pesach ohel moed}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Language Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Pesach?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The use of the phrase {\i pesach ohel moed} to describe what is actually {\i Machaneh Leviyah} is the interpretive key for Abaye. If it simply meant {\i Machaneh Leviyah}, why invoke the language of the entrance to the {\i Mishkan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kapparah Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in drawing an analogy. We know that a {\i mechusar kippurim} \u8212- even after immersing in the {\i mikvah} and after nightfall, when his {\i tevul yom} status has ended, but before bringing his {\i korban} \u8212- is forbidden from entering {\i ohel moed}, the inner sacred precincts. This is derived from the verse {\i tamei yiheyeh od tumato vo} \u8212- his {\i tumah} remains with him \u8212- which extends to include {\i tevul yom} and {\i mechusar kapparah} as categories that retain a residual disqualifying status for entry into the sacred zones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extension Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By describing {\i Machaneh Leviyah} with the language {\i pesach ohel moed}, the Torah signals that the same logic of exclusion applies here. {\i Mah hatam mechusar kapparah lo ayyil, af lemachaneh leviyah nami mechusar kapparah lo ayyil} \u8212- just as a {\i mechusar kippurim} cannot enter {\i ohel moed}, so too cannot a {\i mechusar kippurim} who is also a {\i tevul yom} enter even {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. This restriction does not apply to an ordinary {\i mechusar kippurim} \u8212- such a person on day eight traverses {\i Machaneh Leviyah} to present his {\i korban} at the entrance to the inner precinct. The restriction is specific to a {\i tevul yom} who is simultaneously {\i mechusar kippurim}, a status more burdened than either alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sacred Zones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geography\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three camps of the {\i Mishkan} map onto the geography of the {\i Mikdash} as follows. {\i Machaneh Shechinah} corresponds to the inner precincts \u8212- the {\i azarah}, comprising {\i Ezrat Kohanim}, {\i Ezrat Yisrael}, and the {\i Mishkan} structure itself with the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}. {\i Machaneh Leviyah} encompasses {\i Har HaBayit}, including the {\i Ezrat Nashim}. The camp of Israel corresponds to the city of Jerusalem itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevul Entry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question that arises from Abaye\u8217's analysis is whether an ordinary {\i tevul yom} \u8212- one who has immersed in the {\i mikvah} and is simply waiting for nightfall, with no further obligation \u8212- is permitted to enter {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. A full {\i tamei} of most categories may not enter. But a {\i tevul yom} is significantly less {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's analysis concludes that a {\i tevul yom} who is also {\i mechusar kippurim} \u8212- specifically a {\i zav} who has immersed but still must bring a {\i korban} the next day \u8212- is prohibited even from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. The textual derivation, as noted, is the use of {\i pesach ohel moed} to describe a location that is technically {\i Machaneh Leviyah}, teaching that the restrictions associated with the inner sanctum extend, in this specific case, to the outer camp as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ordinary {\i mechusar kippurim} \u8212- who has completed his {\i tevul yom} status and awaits only the bringing of his {\i korban} \u8212- is of course permitted in {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. That is precisely how he gets to the entrance to present his {\i korban}. What is restricted is the combined status of {\i tevul yom} and {\i mechusar kippurim} simultaneously. This is the nuanced distinction at the heart of Abaye\u8217's analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Practical Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's resolution is complex. Ordinarily, a {\i tevul yom shel zav} \u8212- according to Tosafot and the Rosh\u8217's {\i girsa} \u8212- is {\i lav ke-zav dami}, meaning his entry restrictions do not carry over from the original {\i tumah}. But when that same {\i tevul yom} is also {\i mechusar kippurim}, the combined status creates a heightened restriction that bars him even from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncertainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara does not arrive at a clean resolution regarding the final {\i halacha} on the question of {\i tevul yom shel zav} and {\i Har HaBayit} access. Whether the {\i girsa} reads {\i ke-zav dami} or {\i lav ke-zav dami} affects the ultimate ruling, and the {\i maskana} of the sugya is not entirely clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In practice, the contemporary approach to {\i Har HaBayit} access \u8212- for those {\i poskim} who permit it \u8212- appears to proceed on the assumption that a {\i tevul yom} of a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} is not treated as equivalent to the original {\i tumah} for purposes of {\i Machaneh Leviyah} entry. But the question is not trivially resolved, and the present sugya is precisely the {\i makom} in the Gemara where it is worked through. Anyone seeking to act in this area should be aware that the {\i gemara}\u8217's discussion here bears directly on the {\i halacha}, and that clarity on this point depends significantly on how one resolves the textual and conceptual questions raised by Abaye\u8217's analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tiglachat Taharah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah now turns to {\i tiglachat hataharah} \u8212- the shaving at the conclusion of a Nazir\u8217's full thirty-day {\i nezirut} period. The Mishnah describes the process: {\i haya meivi shalosh behemot} \u8212- he would bring three animals \u8212- {\i chatat, olah, u\u8217'shlamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah states {\i v\u8217'shachat et hashlamim u\u8217'megalei\u8217'ach aleihem} \u8212- and he shall slaughter the {\i shlamim} and shave upon them. He takes the hair and places it {\i al ha\u8217'eish asher tachat zevach hashlamim} \u8212- on the fire beneath the pot of the {\i shlamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the Mishnah addresses is: to which {\i korban} is the act of shaving conceptually connected? The Torah does not explicitly sequence the shaving as following immediately from any particular offering; rather, it presents the sequence of {\i chatat}, {\i olah}, and {\i shlamim}, and then separately describes the shaving and the placing of the hair on the fire. The Mishnah therefore asks which {\i korban} one is conceptually {\i megalei\u8217'ach al} \u8212- shaving in connection with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatas vs Shlamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first opinion holds that the shaving is connected to the {\i shlamim}. The textual basis is the phrase {\i pesach ohel moed} \u8212- the same language used in connection with the {\i shlamim} in the beginning of {\i Vayikra} ({\i v\u8217'shachato pesach ohel moed}) \u8212- linking the shaving of the Nazir to the ritual of the {\i shlamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer disagrees: {\i lo haya megalei\u8217'ach ela al hachatas, shehachatas kodemes b\u8217'chol makom} \u8212- the shaving is connected to the {\i chatat}, because the {\i chatat} is always brought first. Wherever a list of {\i korbanot} appears, the {\i chatat} takes precedence. Therefore, since the shaving is associated with the first {\i korban} brought, it is associated with the {\i chatat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes an interesting implication of this dispute. According to the opinion that the shaving is connected to the {\i shlamim}, and given that {\i shlamim} is always {\i lashon rabbim} (the word has no singular form, like pants in English), the word {\i aleihem} in the verse would naturally refer only to the {\i shlamim}. Tosafot also understands that the first opinion would agree with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's logic that the {\i korban} with which one shaves is brought first, and therefore the first opinion holds that the {\i shlamim} is brought first. However, this is not stated explicitly in the Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel introduces a third position: {\i heivi shalosh behemot v\u8217'lo peirash} \u8212- he brought three animals without specifying which was designated for which offering. In such a case, {\i hara\u8217'uya lachatatat tikrav chatatat, l\u8217'olah tikrav olah, u\u8217'lishlamim tikrav shlamim} \u8212- the animal suited to be a {\i chatat} is offered as a {\i chatat}, the one suited for an {\i olah} as an {\i olah}, and the one suited for {\i shlamim} as {\i shlamim}. Tosafot specifies the practical identification: the female sheep is suited for the {\i chatat}, the male sheep for the {\i olah}, and the ram for the {\i shlamim}. The {\i kedusha} thus defines itself according to the nature of each animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlamim Location (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes \u8212- and it is confirmed by other sources \u8212- that there was in fact a physical chamber in the {\i Beit HaMikdash} designated for this purpose: the {\i Lishkat Nezirim}, located in the {\i Ezrat Nashim}. This was the room where Nazirites would shave and where the {\i shlamim} were cooked. The practical setting of the {\i tiglachat taharah} thus took place in the {\i Ezrat Nashim}, which falls within {\i Machaneh Leviyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Location Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is cited: {\i v\u8217'gilach hanazir pesach ohel moed} \u8212- and the Nazir shaves at {\i pesach ohel moed}. The {\i braita} then asks: does this phrase indicate the actual location of the shaving, or is it a metaphorical expression meaning that the shaving takes place in conjunction with the {\i shlamim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Literal vs Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} presents both possibilities. Perhaps {\i pesach ohel moed} literally designates the place where the shaving occurs \u8212- namely, within the {\i makom hamikdash} itself. On this reading, the Nazir would shave in the {\i Ezrat Yisrael} or similar space within the inner precinct. Alternatively, {\i pesach ohel moed} is a way of saying that the shaving is conceptually linked to the {\i shlamim}, which are described elsewhere as being brought {\i pesach ohel moed}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Respect Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} rejects the literal reading on grounds of {\i derekh bizayon} \u8212- disrespect for the {\i makom hamikdash}. Performing a shaving on the Temple grounds themselves would be degrading to the sacred space. Therefore, {\i pesach ohel moed} in this context must be read as a reference to the {\i shlamim} and not as a literal geographic designation. The shaving takes place wherever the {\i shlamim} are being prepared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pesach Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Door Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Chanan cites a teaching in the name of Rabbi Eliezer: {\i v\u8217'gilach hanazir pesach ohel moed, kol zman she-ein pesach ohel moed pasuach eino megalei\u8217'ach} \u8212- as long as the doors of the {\i Ohel Moed} are not open, the Nazir may not shave. This reading does not treat {\i pesach ohel moed} as a purely metaphorical reference to the {\i shlamim}. Rather, it understands the phrase to mean that the shaving must occur while the {\i Ohel Moed} is open \u8212- whenever and wherever the shaving takes place, even in the {\i Ezrat Nashim}, the doors must be open. This is analogous to the rule governing {\i shlamim}: if the doors were closed when the {\i shlamim} was slaughtered, the {\i korban} is {\i pasul}. The shaving of the Nazir is similarly rendered invalid if the doors were not open at the time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Distinction (IV)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon Shezuri draws a different inference from the phrase {\i v\u8217'gilach hanazir pesach ohel moed}: the verse specifies {\i hanazir} \u8212- the Nazir \u8212- {\i v\u8217'lo nezirah} \u8212- but not the {\i nezirah}, the female Nazir. A male Nazir performs the shaving at or near {\i pesach ohel moed}; a female {\i nezirah} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pesach Timing (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Offering Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for Abba Chanan\u8217's position is the parallel to the {\i shlamim}: just as the {\i shlamim} must be offered while the doors of the {\i Ohel Moed} are open, and if the doors were closed the offering is {\i pasul}, so too the shaving of the Nazir requires that the doors be open. A shaving performed while the doors were closed would be halakhically invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Distinction (IV) (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason Rabbi Shimon Shezuri distinguishes between the male Nazir and the female {\i nezirah} is {\i shema yisgaru bah pirchei kehunah} \u8212- lest the young {\i Kohanim} be aroused by her, since her hair would be uncovered during the shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlamim Location (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Placement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak offers a further derivation. The Torah states {\i v\u8217'lakach et se\u8217'ar rosh nizro v\u8217'nasan al ha\u8217'eish} \u8212- he takes the hair of his head and places it on the fire. This act of taking and immediately placing the hair on the fire implies that the shaving occurs at the very location where the fire is burning. Since the fire is beneath the pot cooking the {\i shlamim}, the shaving must take place at the same location where the {\i shlamim} are being cooked. {\i Mi she-eino mechusar ela lekichah u\u8217'nesinah} \u8212- the process requires nothing more than taking and placing; {\i yatza zeh she-mechusar lekichah, hava\u8217'ah u\u8217'nesinah} \u8212- this rules out a scenario where the shaving occurs in one location and the hair must then be transported elsewhere, which would require an additional act of bringing. The Torah\u8217's formulation implies a single, immediate sequence: shave and place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Distinction (IV) (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's reading of Rabbi Shimon Shezuri\u8217's position clarifies the geography. Since the concern is specifically about {\i pirchei kehunah} \u8212- the young {\i Kohanim} \u8212- who would be present in and around the {\i Ezrat Nashim} area, Rabbi Shimon Shezuri is saying that while a male Nazir shaves in the {\i Ezrat Nashim} at the {\i Lishkat Nezirim} (or in its vicinity, which is {\i pesach ohel moed} in the relevant sense), a {\i nezirah} shaves in a more private location somewhere in Jerusalem, away from the {\i Mikdash} precincts altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlamim Location (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishkat Nezirim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From Rabbi Yitzchak\u8217's derivation it follows that the shaving does not take place in the {\i Mikdash} proper but in the {\i Lishkat Nezirim}, the chamber located in the {\i Ezrat Nashim}. It is there that the {\i shlamim} of the Nazir are cooked, and it is therefore there that the shaving takes place, allowing the hair to be placed directly on the fire beneath the {\i shlamim}. The {\i Lishkat Nezirim} in the {\i Ezrat Nashim} is thus the historical location of the {\i tiglachat taharah} in Temple times.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Distinction (IV) (III)\u8230 ?\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An objection is raised to Rabbi Shimon Shezuri\u8217's distinction: {\i amru lo \u8212- lidvarecha sotah tochiah} \u8212- according to your reasoning, the case of the {\i sotah} should disprove your point. By the {\i sotah}, the Torah writes {\i v\u8217'he\u8217'emidah lifnei Hashem} \u8212- she is stood before God, in the {\i Mikdash}, with her hair uncovered \u8212- and we do not find any concern raised about {\i shema yisgaru bah pirchei kehunah}. If uncovered hair in the {\i Mikdash} were truly a problem, it would have been addressed there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon Shezuri responds: {\i zu kochelet u\u8217'fokeset} \u8212- the {\i nezirah} has her makeup on, she is groomed and attractive in appearance. {\i Zu einah kochelet u\u8217'fokeset} \u8212- the {\i sotah}, by contrast, is not in that state; she is disheveled and is undergoing a degrading public ordeal. The two cases are not comparable. The {\i nezirah}, with her hair freshly uncovered and her normal appearance intact, would more likely attract the attention and urges of the young {\i Kohanim} than the {\i sotah}, who presents a very different appearance. The Gemara will continue on the following day with the remaining discussion of where exactly the shaving may take place within or outside {\i Makom HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 44b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Greeting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our hearts and {\i tefillot} go out to the injured and to all of Israel. May we know only security and peace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah to Taharah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin on 44b with the {\i mishnah}. We are moving from the section dealing with {\i tumah} \u8212- which covered the {\i issurim} of {\i tumah}, {\i yayin}, and {\i tiglachat} \u8212- into the rituals of the {\i nazir} associated with shaving at the end of the {\i tumah} process, and then, in the next {\i mishnah}, the shaving at the end of the {\i taharah} process. It is worth noting in passing that each of those three earlier topics turns out to serve as a basis for important areas of {\i halacha} elsewhere: {\i tumah} for {\i tumas kohanim}, {\i tiglachat} for the general issue of shaving, and {\i yayin} as a paradigm for the principle of {\i taam ke\u8217'ikkar} in {\i kashrus}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tiglachat Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tiglachat tumah keitzad?} \u8212- How is the shaving of {\i tumah} performed? The {\i mishnah} describes the procedure: one sprinkles the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} on days three and seven, shaves on day seven, and brings the {\i korbanos} on day eight. This is precisely what the {\i Torah} prescribes. The relevant verse in {\i Bamidbar} reads: {\i v\u8217'chi yamut meis alav b\u8217'fesa pitoam v\u8217'timei rosh nizro v\u8217'gilach rosho b\u8217'yom taharato b\u8217'yom hashvii yegalchenu} \u8212- he shaves on the seventh day, the day of his purification \u8212- {\i u\u8217'vayom hashmini yavi shtei torim o shnei b\u8217'nei yonah} \u8212- and on the eighth day he brings the {\i korbanos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purification Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The purification here follows the standard {\i parah adumah} process from {\i Bamidbar}: sprinkling on days three and seven renders the {\i nazir} {\i tahor}, and on the eighth day he brings his {\i korbanos} in order to restart the {\i nezirus taharah}. This is not the conclusion of the {\i nezirus}; it is the beginning of a fresh count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i ve\u8217'im gilach bashmini mevi korbanosav bo bayom} \u8212- if for some reason he postponed the shaving until day eight, he may nonetheless bring the {\i korbanos} on that same day eight. The {\i halacha} is not that the {\i korbanos} are brought the day after shaving; the {\i halacha} is that they are brought on day eight. If shaving was delayed, that delay does not push the {\i korbanos} to day nine. This is the position of Rabbi Akiva.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon challenged: {\i u\u8217'mah ma bein zeh l\u8217'metzora?} \u8212- how is the {\i nazir} different from the {\i metzora}? Rabbi Tarfon takes it as given that a {\i metzora} who delays shaving until a day later than prescribed must bring his {\i korban} one day later still \u8212- in that case on day nine rather than day eight. By the same logic, he argues, a {\i nazir} who postpones shaving until day eight should have to wait until day nine to bring his {\i korbanos}. (In the {\i metzora}\u8217's case the relevant delay is not about day eight per se, but about waiting the full seven days until the {\i kohen} revisits and the subsequent shaving cycle is completed \u8212- the structure is parallel even if the precise day count differs.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva replied with a fundamental distinction: {\i zeh taharato teluyah b\u8217'yamav} \u8212- by the {\i nazir}, his becoming {\i tahor} is dependent on the days themselves; {\i umetzora taharato teluyah b\u8217'tiglachto} \u8212- by the {\i metzora}, the shaving itself is part of the {\i taharah} process. What is the basis for this distinction? {\i Tosafos} points out that it derives from a close reading of the respective {\i pesukim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Process Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the {\i nazir}, the {\i Torah} simply says {\i v\u8217'gilach rosho b\u8217'yom hashvii} \u8212- he shaves on day seven \u8212- and then {\i u\u8217'vayom hashmini yavi}. The shaving is mentioned, but there is no requirement to immerse in a {\i mikvah} afterward. By contrast, the {\i metzora}\u8217's purification is described in detail in {\i Parshas Tazria}. First the {\i kohen} observes that the affliction has healed \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ra\u8217'ah hakohen v\u8217'hineh nirpa nega hatzaraat min hatzarua} \u8212- and the two-bird ritual is performed. Then: {\i v\u8217'chibes hamitaher begadav v\u8217'gilach kol searo v\u8217'rachatz bamayim v\u8217'taher v\u8217'achar yavo el hamachaneh} \u8212- he launders his garments, shaves all his hair, immerses in water ({\i mikvah}), becomes {\i tahor}, and only then may return to the camp. After a seven-day wait, on day seven he shaves again \u8212- {\i yegalach et kol searo et rosho ve\u8217'et zekano ve\u8217'et gabot einav ve\u8217'et kol searo yegaleach} \u8212- and then again: {\i vechibes et begadav verachatz et besaro bamayim vetaher}. Once more, shaving is followed by immersion in the {\i mikvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikvah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical difference is that by the {\i metzora}, the shaving is structurally integrated into the {\i taharah} process, and it is followed by a required immersion in the {\i mikvah}. This means that if the {\i metzora} postpones shaving until day eight, even if he had gone to the {\i mikvah} on day seven, that {\i mikvah} immersion is ineffective \u8212- it must come after the shaving. He therefore must shave on day eight and immerse again, rendering him a {\i tevul yom} on day eight. Since the {\i Torah} clearly intends for him to be fully out of his {\i tumah} status before bringing his {\i korban}, he must wait until day nine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i taharah} process consists entirely of the sprinkling of the {\i parah adumah} ashes on days three and seven. He is {\i tamei meis} like any person who has come into contact with a corpse \u8212- no unique {\i taharah} process beyond the standard one. There is no {\i mikvah} requirement following the shaving. He immerses on day seven after the sprinkling, becomes a {\i tevul yom}, and by day eight he is fully {\i tahor}. The shaving is a ritual of {\i nezirus}, not a component of {\i taharah}. Postponing the shaving changes nothing about when he is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva thus concludes: {\i zeh taharato teluyah b\u8217'yamav umetzora taharato teluyah b\u8217'tiglachto} \u8212- the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i taharah} is tied to days; the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i taharah} is tied to his shaving. And since the {\i metzora} must immerse after shaving: {\i ve\u8217'ein meivi korban ela im kein he\u8217'eriv shimsho} \u8212- one can only bring a {\i korban} after {\i ha\u8217'arev shemesh}, meaning after the {\i tevul yom} period has passed. If shaving was postponed to day eight, the subsequent {\i mikvah} makes him a {\i tevul yom}, and the {\i korban} cannot be brought until day nine. The {\i nazir} faces no such constraint \u8212- shaving is a ritual act of {\i nezirus}, entirely separate from the {\i taharah} mechanism, and its postponement does not delay the {\i korbanos}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tarfon & Akiva Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Did Tarfon Concede?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a pointed question: {\i kiblah minei o lo?} \u8212- did Rabbi Tarfon ultimately accept Rabbi Akiva\u8217's answer, or did he not? The text of the {\i mishnah} leaves this unresolved. To address it, the {\i Gemara} introduces a {\i braisa} attributed to the {\i Amora} Hillel (not Hillel Hazaken).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel\u8217's Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} states: {\i gilach bashmini mevi korbanosav batshi\u8217'i} \u8212- if he shaved on day eight, he brings his {\i korbanos} on day nine. {\i Tosafos} notes that there was a {\i masorah} that this {\i braisa} refers to a {\i nazir}, not a {\i metzora} \u8212- since everyone already agrees that a {\i metzora} who postpones shaving to day eight must wait until day nine. If the {\i braisa} is indeed discussing a {\i nazir}, then it asserts that even the {\i nazir} who delays shaving must bring his {\i korbanos} one day later. The only authority who holds that position is Rabbi Tarfon. Thus the {\i Gemara} infers that Rabbi Tarfon did not concede to Rabbi Akiva \u8212- this {\i braisa} reflects his view. Or at minimum, it reflects his position as it stood before the dispute was resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava proposes a different resolution: {\i ha detaval bashvii ha delo taval bashvii} \u8212- the distinction in the {\i braisa} is not about whether shaving was postponed, but about whether the {\i nazir} immersed in the {\i mikvah} on day seven or day eight. Even for a {\i nazir tamei meis}, going to the {\i mikvah} is required, and immersion renders one a {\i tevul yom} who cannot bring a {\i korban} until after {\i ha\u8217'arev shemesh}. If the {\i mikvah} immersion was delayed until day eight, one is not {\i tahor} until day nine \u8212- and that, says Rava, is what the {\i braisa} is describing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Critique of Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is difficult to sustain. If the {\i braisa}\u8217's case were about delaying the {\i mikvah} immersion, it should have said {\i taval bashmini} \u8212- he immersed on day eight. Instead it says {\i gilach bashmini} \u8212- he shaved on day eight. The very choice of language indicates that the shaving is the operative issue, not the timing of immersion. The simple reading of the {\i braisa}, then, supports the inference that Rabbi Tarfon did not accept Rabbi Akiva\u8217's distinction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tevul Yom Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei & Korbanos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a broader question: can a {\i tamei} person have {\i korbanos} brought on his behalf? The general {\i halacha} \u8212- {\i arel v\u8217'tamei hameshalachin korbonoseihem} \u8212- is that a {\i tamei} person may indeed have someone else bring his {\i korbanos} for him, even though he cannot enter the relevant areas of the {\i Beis Hamikdash} himself. However, this rule applies only to lighter forms of {\i tumah}, such as {\i sheretz} or {\i neveilah}. A {\i tamei meis}, {\i zav}, {\i zava}, or {\i metzora} cannot have another person bring a {\i korban} on his behalf \u8212- that is the {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond this general rule, {\i Tosafos} argues that even if the precise {\i halacha} about {\i tamei} persons sending {\i korbanos} were in some degree of dispute, it is implicit from the {\i pesukim} themselves that for the specific {\i korbanos} of the {\i metzora}\u8217's purification process \u8212- and for the {\i korbanos} of the {\i nazir} restarting his {\i nezirus taharah} \u8212- the {\i Torah} requires the person to be fully out of his state of {\i tumah} before bringing the relevant {\i korban}. This is evident from the structure of the verses: the {\i Torah} prescribes shaving and {\i mikvah} on day seven and bringing the {\i korban} the following day. Why not bring it that same day? Clearly because the {\i Torah} requires the person to no longer be a {\i tevul yom}. The specific {\i korbanos} tied to the {\i taharah} process demand full {\i taharah} \u8212- not merely {\i tevul yom} status \u8212- before being brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether a {\i tevul yom} cannot in principle have a {\i korban} brought for him at all. The answer is that {\i lehalacha} this is probably true, but {\i Tosafos}\u8217's point is a more focused one: even setting aside that broader question, the {\i pesukim} governing these specific {\i korbanos} make clear that the {\i Torah} demands the person be completely free of his {\i tumah} state before proceeding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Quote\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to a direct discussion of {\i tevul yom} status. Abaye reports: {\i ashkechinhu l\u8217'chavrei d\u8217'Rav Nosson bar Oshaya d\u8217'yasvin v\u8217'kamrin} \u8212- I found the colleagues of Rav Nosson bar Oshaya sitting and discussing the following {\i braisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zav\u8217's Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} derives from the {\i pesukim} concerning a {\i zav}. The {\i Torah} describes the {\i zav}\u8217's purification in {\i Vayikra}: {\i v\u8217'chi yithar hazav mizovo v\u8217'safar lo shivas yamim l\u8217'taharato v\u8217'chibes begadav v\u8217'rachatz b\u8217'saro b\u8217'mayim chayim v\u8217'taher} \u8212- he counts seven days, launders his garments, immerses in living water, and becomes {\i tahor}. On day eight he comes to the {\i mishkan}. The {\i braisa} asks: when may he come? {\i Bizman shehu taval v\u8217'asah ha\u8217'erev shemesh} \u8212- only after he has immersed in the {\i mikvah} and {\i ha\u8217'arev shemesh} has passed, meaning he is no longer a {\i tevul yom}. If he has not done so \u8212- {\i lo taval v\u8217'asah ha\u8217'arev shemesh} \u8212- he may not enter the {\i mishkan} grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zav Status Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this {\i braisa}, the colleagues of Rav Nosson bar Oshaya inferred: {\i alma kasavar tevul yom shel zav kezav dami} \u8212- this {\i braisa} holds that a {\i tevul yom} of a {\i zav} retains the status of a {\i zav}. The significance of this inference is considerable. A {\i zav} is prohibited from entering {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. The {\i halakhic} translation of {\i Machaneh Leviyah} in post-{\i mishkan} terms is {\i Har HaBayis}. The question therefore is: if the {\i zav} has completed his seven days, immersed in the {\i mikvah}, and needs only nightfall to be fully {\i tahor} \u8212- does his status as a {\i tevul yom} still carry the prohibitions of a {\i zav}, including the prohibition against entering {\i Machaneh Leviyah}/{\i Har HaBayis}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modern Har Habayit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Har Habayit Tours\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question has direct practical relevance. There are tours conducted on {\i Har HaBayis} that are carefully routed to avoid the {\i Makom HaMikdash} proper, where one would be prohibited regardless of {\i tumah} status. The justification for such tours is that {\i Tamei Meis} \u8212- which we must assume describes virtually everyone today \u8212- is permitted in {\i Machaneh Leviyah}, i.e., on {\i Har HaBayis}, and only prohibited within the {\i Makom HaMikdash} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Yotzei Migufo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, a more stringent category of {\i tumah} is that of {\i tamei yotzei migufo} \u8212- {\i tumah} that originates from within one\u8217's own body. This includes a woman who is a {\i niddah} and a man who has had a seminal emission ({\i ba\u8217'al keri}). These individuals are restricted from {\i Har HaBayis} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevul Yom & Har Habayit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical difficulty arises. Suppose such a man goes to the {\i mikvah} before arriving at {\i Har HaBayis} \u8212- he is now a {\i tevul yom} of a {\i ba\u8217'al keri}. Is a {\i tevul yom} of a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} treated like a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} for purposes of {\i Har HaBayis} access? Is he still prohibited? This is precisely the question that turns on whether {\i tevul yom shel zav kezav dami} \u8212- whether a {\i tevul yom} who derives from one of these more severe {\i tumah} categories retains that category\u8217's restrictions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye pushes back against the colleagues\u8217' inference. He argues: if {\i tevul yom shel zav kezav dami} is truly the principle, then the same logic should apply to the {\i nazir tamei}. The verse concerning the {\i nazir} who has become {\i tamei meis} similarly says he comes to {\i petach ohel moed} on day eight \u8212- {\i yavish shtei torim el hakohen el petach ohel moed}. One should be able to make the same inference: {\i eimatai hu ba? bizman shetaval v\u8217'asah ha\u8217'erev shemesh} \u8212- he may only come after having immersed and {\i ha\u8217'arev shemesh} has passed. This would imply that until day eight, the {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- as a {\i tevul yom} of {\i tamei meis} \u8212- was prohibited from entering those grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this inference cannot be correct. The {\i nazir tamei} is {\i tamei meis} \u8212- not a {\i zav}. And {\i tumah} from contact with a corpse does not restrict one from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Meis Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i braisa} that makes this explicit: {\i tamei meis mutar l\u8217'hikaenes l\u8217'Machaneh Leviyah} \u8212- one who is {\i tamei meis} is permitted to enter {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. And not only that \u8212- {\i afilu meis atzmo} \u8212- even a corpse itself may be present in {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. The proof: {\i vayikach Moshe es atzmos Yosef imo} \u8212- Moshe took the bones of Yosef {\i imo}, meaning with him, {\i imo b\u8217'Machaneh Leviyah}, in the very camp of the {\i Leviyim}. Since the {\i nazir tamei} was permitted in {\i Machaneh Leviyah} even at the peak of his {\i tumah}, the {\i pesukim} that speak of waiting until day eight cannot be about permission to enter the grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Timing Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore the {\i pesukim} are addressing something else entirely: the timing of the {\i korban}, not the permission to enter the space. The day-eight designation tells us that the {\i korban} cannot be brought until the {\i nazir} is no longer a {\i tevul yom} \u8212- but it says nothing about when he may enter {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. This undermines the inference the colleagues sought to draw from the {\i zav} passage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zav vs.\u160 ?Metzora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechusar Kapara Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now offers a crucial distinction. It is not that {\i tevul yom shel zav} is simply {\i kezav dami} across the board. Rather, the specific {\i tevul yom} discussed in the {\i braisa} is one who is also {\i mechusar kapara} \u8212- who must still bring a {\i korban} the following day. This {\i tevul yom} is categorically different from an ordinary {\i tevul yom}. An ordinary {\i tevul yom} has completed everything; he need only wait for nightfall and he will be fully {\i tahor}. But a {\i tevul yom} who is also {\i mechusar kapara} is not in a merely transitory state. His {\i taharah} is not complete until he brings the {\i korban}. His waiting is not simply the passive passage of time \u8212- it is waiting to perform a remaining act of {\i kapara}. This more substantial residual status is what gives him a {\i zav}-like character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction within the {\i zav} category itself clarifies this. A {\i zav} who has seen a flux twice does not need to bring a {\i korban}; a {\i zav} who has seen three times must bring one. The {\i tevul yom} of the {\i zav} who saw twice \u8212- or analogously, a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} who has simply immersed and needs only nightfall \u8212- is {\i lav kezav dami} according to {\i Tosafos} and the {\i Rosh}\u8217's reading. That is, his {\i tevul yom} status does not carry the {\i zav}\u8217's restrictions on {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. But the {\i tevul yom} who must still bring a {\i korban} the next day occupies a different position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Yihyeh Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: where do we derive that a {\i mechusar kapara} who is also a {\i tevul yom} is prohibited from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}? The source is the verse {\i tamei yihyeh od tumato vo} \u8212- he shall remain {\i tamei}, his {\i tumah} still upon him. The {\i drashah}: {\i tamei yihyeh} comes to include a {\i tevul yom}, and {\i od tumato vo} comes to include a {\i mechusar kapara}. From this verse we know that both statuses carry continuing restrictions. It is taken as axiomatic that any residual {\i tumah} \u8212- even the weakest, even simply being a {\i tevul yom} waiting for nightfall \u8212- bars one from {\i Makom HaMikdash}. The question unique to {\i Machaneh Leviyah} is whether {\i tevul yom} of a {\i zav} carries {\i zav}-like restrictions there as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Petach Ohel Moed Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why do the {\i pesukim} describe the {\i zav}\u8217's entry as coming to {\i petach ohel moed}? The {\i Gemara} explains: since a {\i zav} as a full {\i zav} is already prohibited not just from {\i Makom HaMikdash} but from {\i Machaneh Leviyah} itself, the phrase {\i petach ohel moed} cannot be referring to {\i Makom HaMikdash} proper \u8212- he could not have been there before day eight simply on account of {\i Machaneh Leviyah} restrictions. So when the {\i Torah} says he comes on day eight to {\i petach ohel moed}, it is describing his entry into {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. The use of the phrase {\i petach ohel moed} to describe what is functionally {\i Machaneh Leviyah} access teaches us by analogy: just as one who is {\i mechusar kapara} cannot enter {\i Makom HaMikdash} (the {\i ohel moed} proper), so too a {\i zav} who is {\i mechusar kapara} \u8212- still a {\i tevul yom} with a {\i korban} obligation pending \u8212- cannot enter {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. The language of {\i petach ohel moed} applied to {\i Machaneh Leviyah} teaches that the restriction on a {\i mechusar kapara} vis-\u224 ?-vis {\i Makom HaMikdash} finds a parallel restriction on a {\i tevul yom mechusar kapara} vis-\u224 ?-vis {\i Machaneh Leviyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is admittedly somewhat circular, as Abaye\u8217's own challenge forces a concession. But the conclusion is that the {\i pesukim} about waiting until day eight are about bringing the {\i korban} \u8212- not about entering the grounds \u8212- and the {\i zav mechusar kapara} who is a {\i tevul yom} is restricted from {\i Machaneh Leviyah} not because the {\i pesukim} imply entry restrictions per se, but because the description of entry as {\i petach ohel moed} teaches a parallel {\i din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion on Tevul Yom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot, according to {\i Tosafos} and the {\i Rosh}, is: a normal {\i tevul yom} of a {\i zav} \u8212- one who has no outstanding {\i korban} obligation \u8212- is {\i lav kezav dami} and is not restricted from {\i Machaneh Leviyah} on that account. Only a {\i tevul yom} who is simultaneously {\i mechusar kapara}, who still has a {\i korban} to bring the next day, retains a {\i zav}-like status that prohibits him from {\i Machaneh Leviyah}. A {\i mechusar kapara} who has passed through {\i ha\u8217'arev shemesh} and is simply waiting to bring his {\i korban} the following day is of course permitted in {\i Machaneh Leviyah} \u8212- that is how he goes to bring the {\i korban} in the first place, approaching the entrance of {\i Makom HaMikdash}. The restriction on a {\i tevul yom mechusar kapara} applies specifically while he is still a {\i tevul yom}, not after.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modern Har Habayit (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Significance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution of this {\i sugya} has direct bearing on the question of {\i Har HaBayis} access in contemporary times. The key question was whether a {\i tevul yom} of a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} retains the status of a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} \u8212- and by extension, whether going to the {\i mikvah} before ascending {\i Har HaBayis} is sufficient to resolve the restriction, or whether daytime {\i tevul yom} status still carries a prohibition. According to the reading of {\i Tosafos} and the {\i Rosh}, a {\i tevul yom} who is not {\i mechusar kapara} is not {\i kezav dami} and does not carry the {\i ba\u8217'al keri}\u8217's restrictions. However, it should be noted that this is not the unambiguous {\i psak}, and other {\i sugyos} address the same question, with the {\i halacha} not entirely settled. Those who permit ascent to {\i Har HaBayis} under careful conditions are presumably relying on the position that {\i tevul yom} of a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} is not like a {\i ba\u8217'al keri}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tiglachat Taharah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the next {\i mishnah}: {\i tiglachat hataharah keitzad?} \u8212- what is the shaving of {\i taharah}? This is the shaving that concludes the full thirty-day {\i nezirus}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i haya meivi gimel behemot} \u8212- he brings three animals \u8212- {\i chatat, olah, u\u8217'shelamim}. The {\i Torah} in {\i Bamidbar} then says: {\i veshachat et hashelamim umigaleach aleihem} \u8212- he slaughters the {\i shelamim} and shaves in connection with them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Which Korban?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} asks: to which {\i korban} is the shaving conceptually linked? The {\i pesukim} list all three {\i korbanos} and then describe the shaving, without explicitly tying the shaving to any one of them. The {\i mishnah} therefore debates this point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelamim Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first opinion holds that the shaving is linked to the {\i shelamim}, based on the juxtaposition in the verse: the {\i Torah} says {\i veshachat et hashelamim} immediately before the description of shaving. Additionally, the hair is placed under the pot cooking the {\i shelamim}, which further connects the act of shaving to the {\i shelamim} ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer disagrees: {\i lo haya migaleach ela al hachatat shehachatat kodemet bechol makom} \u8212- the shaving is linked to the {\i chatat}, because the {\i chatat} is brought first in all contexts involving multiple {\i korbanos}, and therefore the shaving is conceptually tied to the first {\i korban} brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bedieved Validation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds that {\i b\u8217'diavad}, if one shaved in connection with any of the three {\i korbanos}, one has fulfilled the obligation. The dispute between the opinions is only {\i lechatchila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's view is brought as a practical illustration: if one brought three animals without specifying which is which \u8212- simply designating them as his {\i korbanos} for {\i nezirus} \u8212- then {\i ha\u8217'ra\u8217'uya l\u8217'chatat tikarev chatat, l\u8217'olah tikarev olah, l\u8217'shelamim tikarev shelamim}. Since the three animals appropriate for the respective {\i korbanos} are physically different \u8212- {\i Tosafos} notes: a female lamb ({\i kisbah}) for the {\i chatat}, a male lamb ({\i keves}) for the {\i olah}, and a ram ({\i ayil}) for the {\i shelamim} \u8212- the {\i kedushah} of each animal sorts itself out accordingly. Each animal is inherently suited to one of the three {\i korbanos}, so even without explicit designation the {\i korbanos} are valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Where Shaving Occurs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i braisa}: {\i v\u8217'gilach hanazir pesach ohel moed} \u8212- the {\i nazir} shaves at {\i petach ohel moed}. The {\i braisa} connects this with the {\i shelamim}: since the {\i shelamim} is described as being brought {\i pesach ohel moed} \u8212- {\i u\u8217'shchato pesach ohel moed} \u8212- and the {\i nazir}\u8217's shaving is described with the same language, the shaving is being conceptually linked to the ritual of the {\i shelamim}. Indeed, beyond the {\i nazir} context, there is a {\i halacha} that if the doors of the {\i ohel moed} are not yet open when the {\i shelamim} is slaughtered, it is {\i pasul} \u8212- the phrase {\i pesach ohel moed} carries substantive weight in the {\i shelamim} ritual specifically. Applying that same phrase to the {\i nazir}\u8217's shaving therefore connects the two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Literal vs.\u160 ?Metaphoric\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: perhaps {\i pesach ohel moed} is not a metaphoric association but a literal geographic description \u8212- that the {\i nazir} performs his shaving literally inside {\i Makom HaMikdash}, in {\i Ezras Yisrael}. For a {\i nazir tahor} completing his thirty days, this is not a {\i taharah} problem \u8212- he is fully {\i tahor} and permitted in {\i Makom HaMikdash}. So conceivably the {\i Torah} is directing that the shaving literally take place at {\i pesach ohel moed}. The {\i Gemara} addresses and rejects this possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yoshaya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yoshaya argues that the literal reading cannot be correct, on grounds of {\i bizayon} \u8212- disrespect to the sanctity of {\i Makom HaMikdash}. He derives this from a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}: the {\i Torah} says {\i lo ta\u8217'aleh b\u8217'ma\u8217'alos al mizbachi} \u8212- do not ascend the altar by stairs \u8212- because even the legitimate {\i avodah} of a {\i kohen} walking up steps might involve some slight exposure that is considered disrespectful. If even the {\i avodah} itself must be performed with such care regarding dignity, then surely the act of shaving \u8212- which is not an act of {\i avodah} \u8212- must not be performed within {\i Makom HaMikdash} itself, where it would constitute a {\i bizayon}. Therefore the phrase {\i pesach ohel moed} cannot mean literally there; it must be describing the connection to the {\i shelamim}. A parallel version of this {\i drashah} is cited from a {\i Midrash} in {\i Parshas Naso}, with slightly different wording but the same conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yitzchak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak offers a different textual argument for the same conclusion. The {\i Torah} says {\i v\u8217'lakach et se\u8217'ar rosh nizro v\u8217'natan al ha\u8217'esh} \u8212- he takes the hair of his head and places it on the fire. The implication is {\i mi she\u8217'eino mechusar ela lekicha u\u8217'netina} \u8212- that all that intervenes between taking and placing is the act of taking and placing themselves. If the shaving took place in {\i Makom HaMikdash}, the {\i nazir} would then have to carry his hair out to where his {\i shelamim} is cooking \u8212- creating additional steps of {\i hava\u8217'ah} between the taking and the placing. The {\i Torah}\u8217's language implies they occur in the same location. Therefore the shaving must take place where the {\i shelamim} is being cooked \u8212- which is outside {\i Makom HaMikdash}, in {\i Ezras Nashim} or even elsewhere in {\i Yerushalayim}. (Indeed, {\i lehalacha} there was an actual {\i lishkas nezirim} \u8212- a chamber in the {\i Ezras Nashim} \u8212- where the {\i nazir} would shave and cook his {\i shelamim} together.) A parallel formulation of this argument is cited in an alternative version: {\i makom shehu mevashel sham hayah megaleach} \u8212- where he cooks, there he shaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Chanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Chanan, in the name of Rabbi Eliezer, offers a position somewhat closer to the literal reading: {\i v\u8217'gilach hanazir pesach ohel moed kol zman she\u8217'ein pesach ohel moed pasuach eino megaleach} \u8212- the {\i nazir} shaves at {\i pesach ohel moed}, and if the doors of the {\i ohel moed} are not open, he may not shave. This takes the phrase {\i pesach ohel moed} not as a pure metaphor but as imposing a real condition: the shaving is only valid when the doors are open, wherever the shaving physically occurs. This is analogous to the rule about {\i shelamim} \u8212- a {\i shelamim} slaughtered when the doors are not yet open is {\i pasul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Shizuri\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon Shizuri offers a distinct {\i drashah} that moves in a different direction entirely. He reads: {\i v\u8217'gilach hanazir pesach ohel moed v\u8217'lo nezira} \u8212- a male {\i nazir} shaves at {\i pesach ohel moed}, but a female {\i nazirah} does not. According to {\i Tosafos}, what this means practically is that the {\i nazir} shaves in the vicinity of the grounds \u8212- in {\i Ezras Nashim} or wherever the {\i shelamim} is being cooked \u8212- an area that is frequented by {\i kohanim}, while the {\i nazirah} shaves in a private, modest location ({\i makom tzanua}) elsewhere in {\i Yerushalayim}. The reason: {\i shema yisgaru ba pirchei kehuna} \u8212- lest the young {\i kohanim} be aroused by her, since her hair is uncovered during the shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises an objection: the {\i sotah} also stands before the {\i kohanim} with her hair uncovered \u8212- {\i v\u8217'he\u8217'emidah lifnei Hashem} \u8212- and we do not invoke any concern of {\i shema yisgaru ba pirchei kehuna}. Why should the {\i nazirah} be treated differently?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon Shizuri responds: {\i zu kocheles u\u8217'pokeses} \u8212- the {\i nazirah} wears cosmetics and presents herself attractively, and it is in that state that she is shaving and her hair is uncovered. {\i Zu eina kocheles u\u8217'pokeses} \u8212- the {\i sotah}, by contrast, is being subjected to a public, degrading process; she is disheveled, not adorned. A woman in an attractive state with uncovered hair raises a concern of arousal that does not apply to a woman undergoing the humiliating {\i sotah} ordeal. It is for this reason that the {\i nazirah}, unlike the {\i nazir}, must shave in a modest, private location.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 47\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening Mishnah & Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Daf Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We open today with daf 47, amud alef, picking up with the {\i mishnah} at the very top of the page. We are concluding one {\i perek} and beginning the next.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beard Shaving Q&A\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the text, one clarification worth noting: a {\i nazir} is only prohibited from cutting the hair of the head. Shaving the beard presents no problem whatsoever, since the prohibition of {\i nezirut} concerns only the long hair of the head.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Blood & Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} reads: {\i mi shenizrak alav echad min hadamim venitma} \u8212- one upon whom one of the blood-sprinklings has been performed and then became {\i tamei}. To set the context: we have just concluded the extended discussion in which the {\i chachamim} hold that even a single {\i korban} completes one\u8217's {\i nezirut}. The {\i mishnah} now addresses the case where, after one {\i korban} has already been brought, the {\i nazir} becomes {\i tamei}. What is the consequence?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer soter et hakol} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer says it undoes everything. This is a natural extension of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position from earlier, that {\i achar ma\u8217'aseh kulam} \u8212- one requires all the {\i korbanot} to complete {\i nezirut}. Since the {\i nezirut} was not yet concluded, the subsequent {\i tumah} destroys whatever had been accomplished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vachachamim omrim yavi shear korbanotav veyit\u8217'har} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} say that the {\i nezirut} is indeed finished. The {\i tumah} does not undermine anything; the {\i korban} already brought remains valid. All that remains is the obligation to bring the remaining {\i korbanot}, which she completes after becoming {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot: Tahara Before K\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Veyit\u8217'har Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The language of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i yavi shear korbanotav veyit\u8217'har} \u8212- might seem to suggest that one brings the {\i korbanot} first and only afterwards becomes {\i tahor}. Tosafot addresses this directly, explaining that {\i veyit\u8217'har} in this context means: {\i lichsheyit\u8217'har} \u8212- only once one has become {\i tahor} may the remaining {\i korbanot} be brought. The order in the {\i mishnah} should not be read literally as \u8220"bring and then purify,\u8221" but rather \u8220"bring [once purified].\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sources: Yerushalmi & Pe\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot cites the {\i Yerushalmi}, which reads the {\i mishnah} straightforwardly as requiring {\i taharah} first and only then bringing the {\i korbanot}: {\i kach mefaresh biyerushalmi stam matnita d\u8217'tahara v\u8217'achar kach yavi korbanotav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for this reading lies in the {\i pesukim} themselves. The {\i Torah}, when addressing a {\i nazir} who became {\i tamei meit}, describes a process in which he first achieves full {\i taharah} and only on the eighth day brings his {\i korban}. This reflects an implicit {\i Torah} demand: a {\i nazir} may not have his {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut} brought while in a state of {\i tumah}. There is a broader {\i halachic} discussion about {\i tamei meshalayach korbanotav} \u8212- whether a {\i tamei} person may have {\i korbanot} brought on his behalf \u8212- and the ruling is that only certain light levels of {\i tumah} permit this. One who is {\i tamei meit}, or one who is a {\i metzora} or {\i zav}, certainly cannot be {\i meshalayach korbanotav}. But Tosafot\u8217's point is more specific: even if one might debate the general rule, in the particular context of a {\i nazir} who is {\i tamei meit}, the {\i pesukim} make clear that the {\i Torah} insists on full {\i taharah} before any {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut} are brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Tumah Can\u8217't Destroy\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once we understand this, Tosafot explains why the {\i tumah} cannot destroy what has already been accomplished. The {\i korban} that was brought while the {\i nazir} was still {\i tahor} was valid \u8212- {\i harei hu kodem shenitma ra\u8217'uy litiglachas}, before he became {\i tamei} he was fit for the {\i tiglachat}, and the {\i korban} was brought in a state of {\i hechsher}. There is therefore no basis to disqualify it retroactively. However, any {\i korban} brought after the {\i tumah} but before achieving {\i taharah} would indeed be invalid: {\i aval hazevachim shehikriv mishenitma yistor} \u8212- whatever he brought after becoming {\i tamei} and before becoming {\i tahor} does not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle Tosafot articulates is: {\i dekapid kra sheyavi korbanot neziroot betaharah} \u8212- the {\i Torah} insists that the {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut} be brought in a state of {\i taharah}. After the {\i nazir} undergoes {\i taharah}, including the {\i hazayah} of days three and seven, he may then bring the remaining {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eliezer: Soter Korba (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads naturally to a clarification of what Rabbi Eliezer actually means when he says {\i soter et hakol}. The phrase sounds sweeping \u8212- it destroys everything \u8212- but we already know from an earlier {\i mishnah} that Rabbi Eliezer ruled that {\i tumah} after the full thirty days of {\i nezirut} requires only seven additional days, not the full thirty. So {\i soteret hakol} cannot literally mean all the days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot makes this explicit in his very first comment here: {\i v\u8217'nitma Rabbi Eliezer omer soter hakol v\u8217'lo kol hayamim k\u8217'mforesh ba-Gemara d\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer hu d\u8217'amar l\u8217'eil nitma l\u8217'achar milo soter zayin ela soter korbanot kama} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer says it destroys everything, but not all the days, as the {\i Gemara} clarifies; Rabbi Eliezer is the same one who said earlier that becoming {\i tamei} after the full thirty days requires only seven additional days. What is destroyed is not the count of days but the {\i korbanot} \u8212- the prior {\i korbanot} are nullified, and any subsequent {\i korbanot} are also invalid until the {\i nazir} properly completes his {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The context of the {\i mishnayot} throughout this section has been precisely this: whether the {\i korbanot} already brought remain valid or are destroyed. This is the {\i machloket} between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i chachamim}, and the relationship to the number of days is secondary \u8212- a natural consequence of whether the {\i nezirut} itself was complete, but not the primary focus.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miriam from Tarmod Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miriam\u8217's Daughter Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} bolster their position with a practical precedent: {\i ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'Miriam haTarmodit} \u8212- there was a story involving Miriam from Tarmod. She had taken upon herself a {\i neder} of {\i nezirut} and one of the blood-sprinklings had already been performed on her behalf \u8212- {\i shenizrak aleha echad min hadamim}. At that moment, she was informed that her daughter was in mortal danger. She ran to her daughter and found that she had died \u8212- {\i vehalcha umatza shemeta}. She thus became {\i tamei meit} after one {\i korban} had already been brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} ruled: {\i tavi shear korbanoteha v\u8217'tit-har} \u8212- let her bring the remaining {\i korbanot} once she becomes {\i tahor}. Her {\i nezirut} was complete after that first {\i korban}; the subsequent {\i tumah} did nothing to undermine it. The {\i korban} that had been brought stood, and her obligation was only to complete the remaining {\i korbanot} after achieving {\i taharah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling stands as a direct practical refutation of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. Rabbi Eliezer holds that the {\i tumah} destroys the {\i korbanot} and effectively nullifies what was accomplished; the {\i chachamim} demonstrated through the case of Miriam that this is not so.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eliezer: Soter Korba (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara: Soter Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now steps back and presses the question: the {\i mishnah} states that Rabbi Eliezer says {\i soter et hakol}, and elsewhere Rabbi Eliezer ruled that {\i tumah} after the full thirty days requires only seven additional days \u8212- so what does {\i soter} mean here? {\i Amar Rav mai soter d\u8217'ka\u8217'amar Rabbi Eliezer soter korbanotav} \u8212- Rav states: the {\i soter} Rabbi Eliezer refers to is that it destroys the {\i korbanot}. Any {\i korban} previously brought is nullified, and any {\i korban} brought in an improper state is likewise invalid. The {\i chachamim}, by contrast, hold that the first {\i korban} remains fully valid \u8212- {\i hacha nami savarta d\u8217'chachamim im tavi shear korbanoteha v\u8217'tit-har, shma mina} \u8212- as the case of Miriam from Tarmod confirms: let her bring the remaining {\i korbanot} once she becomes {\i tahor}, and this resolves the question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The focus, then, in both the {\i mishnah} and the story of Miriam, is on the {\i korbanot} \u8212- not on the number of days. Days are implicated insofar as destroying {\i nezirut} means more days are required, but what is immediately at stake is whether the {\i korbanot} already brought retain their validity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Transition to Kohanic Tu\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Summary & Tumah Fo\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the declaration of {\i hadran alach sheloshah minim}, we conclude the {\i perek} of {\i sheloshah minim}. That {\i perek} was structured around the three core prohibitions of a {\i nazir}: wine, shaving, and {\i tumah}. These three areas opened broader {\i halachic} discussions with general relevance. The wine prohibition led into the discussion of {\i ta\u8217'am ke\u8217'ikar} \u8212- the principle that taste functions like substance in matters of forbidden mixtures. The prohibition on shaving led to an examination of what constitutes shaving with respect to the beard and the {\i pe\u8217'ot}. And the prohibition on {\i tumah} opened the discussion of {\i tumat kohanim} \u8212- whether a {\i kohen} or {\i nazir} who is already {\i tamei meit} transgresses again by becoming {\i tamei} again, and the laws of {\i meit mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Preview: Nazir-Kohen Pri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, as we enter the new {\i perek}, we continue and deepen the discussion of {\i tumah}, because there is much more to learn from the relationship between {\i nazir} and {\i kohen} with respect to the restrictions on {\i tumah}. The central organizing principle of the coming {\i perek} is the rule known as {\i kol tumah she\u8217'ein haNazir megale\u8217'ach aleha ein Kohen muzhar aleha} \u8212- any {\i tumah} for which a {\i nazir} does not shave, a {\i kohen} is not prohibited against.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle might seem to refer only to {\i tumat sheretz}, {\i tumat neveilah}, {\i zivah}, {\i tzara\u8217'at}, and the like \u8212- types of {\i tumah} that are simply outside the scope of what {\i nezirut} addresses. But in fact it reaches further: even within the domain of {\i tumat meit} itself, there are categories that, by received tradition, do not trigger the obligation for a {\i nazir} to shave or to restart his {\i nezirut}, and therefore a {\i kohen} is likewise not prohibited against them. This creates an important area of inquiry \u8212- things that are technically {\i tumat meit} and yet fall outside the prohibitions both of {\i nezirut} and of the {\i kohen}\u8217's restrictions against {\i tumah}. This has direct practical relevance for contemporary questions involving {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Gadol, Nazir & Mei\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priority: Kohen Gadol vs\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i Kohen Gadol veNazir ein mitamim likroveihem} \u8212- a {\i kohen gadol} and a {\i nazir} do not become {\i tamei} even to their immediate relatives. This is a striking parallel. A normal {\i kohen} may become {\i tamei} to his seven close relatives; a {\i kohen gadol} cannot. Conceptually, the {\i kohen gadol} is always in a state of readiness to serve \u8212- {\i min hamikdash lo yeitzei} \u8212- he is never permitted to remove himself from that role, even for the death of an immediate family member. The {\i nazir}, interestingly, shares this same restriction according to the {\i pesukim}: he too cannot become {\i tamei} even to an immediate relative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both, however, do become {\i tamei} for a {\i meit mitzvah}: {\i aval mitamim lemeit mitzvah}. This is not stated explicitly in the {\i Torah} but is understood by the {\i rabbis} from the {\i pesukim}, as the {\i Gemara} will explain. The {\i mishnah} then addresses the question of priority: {\i hayu mehalchim baderech u-matzu meit mitzvah} \u8212- if a {\i kohen gadol} and a {\i nazir} are traveling together and encounter a {\i meit mitzvah}, who should become {\i tamei} first?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying issue here touches on the concept of {\i hutra} versus {\i dichuya} \u8212- whether in situations like {\i meit mitzvah} or {\i pikuach nefesh}, the prohibition is entirely suspended ({\i hutra}), or merely overridden by a competing value ({\i dichuya}). The {\i dichuya} perspective leads naturally to the effort to minimize the degree of override: if only one person needs to become {\i tamei} to fulfill the obligation, we try to determine which person\u8217's {\i tumah} is least consequential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer: Kohen Gadol Pri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer says: {\i yitma Kohen she\u8217'eino meivi korban al tumato v\u8217'al yitma Nazir shemeivi korban al tumato} \u8212- let the {\i kohen} become {\i tamei}, because a {\i kohen}\u8217's {\i tumah} (under normal circumstances) does not require him to bring a {\i korban} as part of the consequences. The {\i nazir}, on the other hand, whose becoming {\i tamei} destroys his {\i nezirut} and requires him to bring a {\i korban}, suffers far more grievous consequences. Even though under normal circumstances both would be violating a {\i lav}, the practical fallout for the {\i nazir} is far weightier. Therefore, protect the {\i nazir} and let the {\i kohen gadol} become {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask about a {\i nazir Shimshon} \u8212- such a {\i nazir} does not bring a {\i korban} for {\i tumah} to begin with, since a {\i nazir Shimshon mitamei lemeitim} in any case. But that would be a moot point, as the {\i nazir Shimshon}\u8217's status is sui generis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim: Nazir Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} respond with a fundamentally different framework: {\i yitma Nazir she\u8217'ein kedushato kedushat olam v\u8217'al yitma Kohen shekedushato kedushat olam} \u8212- let the {\i nazir} become {\i tamei}, because the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i kedushah} is only a {\i kedushah} of time, a temporary status, while the {\i kohen}\u8217's {\i kedushah} is permanent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument is not about the practical consequences of becoming {\i tamei}, but about the quality of the violation. When a {\i kohen}\u8217's {\i kedushah} is breached, something essential to his very identity is transgressed. A {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i kedushah}, while real and meaningful, is a status he accepted upon himself, not an intrinsic identity. It may even be a temporary status \u8212- though Tosafot notes that even a {\i nazir olam} does not change this calculus, because the fundamental character of {\i nezirut} as a {\i kedushah sha\u8217'ah} remains. The {\i chachamim} go further: they maintain this principle even as against a {\i kohen hedyot}, not just a {\i kohen gadol}. For Rabbi Eliezer, even a {\i kohen gadol} becomes {\i tamei} before a {\i nazir}; for the {\i chachamim}, even a {\i nazir} becomes {\i tamei} before a {\i kohen hedyot}. This explains why the {\i girsa} shifts in the text when recording the {\i chachamim}\u8217's position \u8212- they ground the priority in {\i kedushat olam}, which belongs to any {\i kohen}, not only the {\i kohen gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tension between the two positions is illuminating: Rabbi Eliezer measures by {\i halachic} consequence, the {\i chachamim} by the ontological weight of the violation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Kohen Gadolim Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anointed vs.\u160 ?Garmented\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now proceeds through a series of thought experiments involving cases where two figures of comparable but not identical status encounter a {\i meit mitzvah} together. The first case: {\i Mashuach beshemen hamishcha u\u8217'merubeh bivgadim} \u8212- one {\i kohen gadol} who was anointed with the anointing oil, and another who attained the office of {\i kohen gadol} by having worn the eight priestly garments ({\i merubeh begadim}). The anointing oil was secreted away in the time of King Yoshiyahu, so the question concerns what would have been the rule had such a scenario existed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rules: {\i mashuach beshemen hamishcha adif} \u8212- the one anointed with the anointing oil has the higher status and is therefore protected from {\i tumah}. The reason: {\i de\u8217'illu mashuach beshemen hamishcha meivi par al kol hamitzvot} \u8212- there is a special {\i halacha} in Masechet Horayot that if a {\i kohen gadol} who was {\i nimshach beshemen hamishcha} issued an incorrect ruling and then followed his own ruling, he brings a special {\i par} as an {\i atonement korban}. This privilege and the elevated status it reflects belongs only to the {\i mashuach beshemen hamishcha}, not to the {\i merubeh begadim}. Therefore, the anointed one has an objectively higher status and is accordingly protected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of how such a scenario could realistically arise \u8212- with both simultaneously alive and both either equally active or equally retired \u8212- is something Tosafot addresses, though the precise reconstruction is complex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Off-Duty vs.\u160 ?Active\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: {\i mashuach she\u8217'avar u\u8217'merubeh begadim} \u8212- a {\i mashuach beshemen hamishcha} who is no longer in active service, alongside one who is {\i merubeh begadim} and currently serving. The ruling here reverses the first: {\i merubeh begadim adif}, {\i de\u8217'merubeh begadim avid avodah} \u8212- the one currently in active service takes priority and is protected. The reason is straightforward: what overrides the inherent prestige of having been anointed with the oil is the immediate practical reality that this person is actively performing {\i avodah}, and {\i tumah} would interfere with that. {\i Ve\u8217'ilu mashuach she\u8217'avar lav bar avodah hu} \u8212- the one who has been permanently removed from service cannot perform {\i avodah} regardless; there is no comparable disruption if he becomes {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One-Day vs.\u160 ?Permanent Is\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case: {\i avar machmas kiryo ve\u8217'avar machmas muma} \u8212- two {\i kohanim gedolim} who are both currently off duty. One was temporarily removed because he was a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} (a seminal emission), and the other because of a bodily blemish. The ruling: {\i avar machmas kiryo adif} \u8212- the one temporarily off duty due to {\i keri} is protected, {\i de\u8217'hu chazi le\u8217'avodah lemachar} \u8212- because he will be able to return to {\i avodah} the following day after immersing in the {\i mikveh}. The one removed due to a blemish, {\i ve\u8217'ilu avar machmas muma lo chazi le\u8217'avodah} \u8212- cannot currently serve, and Tosafot adds that even if the blemish is potentially temporary, one does not know when it will heal, whereas the {\i ba\u8217'al keri}\u8217's disqualification is precisely one day. All three of these cases follow a clear logical structure: the one whose active relationship with the {\i avodah} is more immediate and more restorable is always the one whose {\i taharah} is more protected.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mashuach Milchamah vs.\u160 ?S\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Posed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i Gemara} raises a question that is less immediately obvious: {\i iba\u8217'ya lehu mashuach milchama ve\u8217'sgan hai minaihu adif} \u8212- which has higher priority, the {\i mashuach milchama} or the {\i sgan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mashuach milchama} is the {\i kohen} anointed to lead the people into battle \u8212- the one who addresses the people before war with the words {\i mi ha\u8217'ish hayarei ve\u8217'rach halevav yelech ve\u8217'yashuv leveiso}. He holds a specially anointed role of considerable stature. The {\i sgan} is the vice {\i kohen gadol}, whose role is to stand ready to substitute for the {\i kohen gadol} should he become {\i tamei}, particularly on {\i yom kippur} when a {\i kohen gadol} is absolutely indispensable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both are a rank below the {\i kohen gadol}, functioning as executive vice-presidents of sorts. The question: if they both encounter a {\i meit mitzvah}, which one is protected and which one must become {\i tamei}? The {\i mashuach milchama} has an active, weighty role \u8212- he is not merely waiting in the wings, he is on stage. The {\i sgan}, one might think, is essentially defined by his readiness to step in, which only rarely becomes relevant. So perhaps the {\i mashuach milchama}\u8217's status warrants protection?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita} in response: {\i tashma detanya, ein bein mashuach milchama lesgan ela she\u8217'im hayu mehalchim baderech u\u8217'matzu meit mitzvah, yitamei mashuach milchama ve\u8217'al yitamei sgan} \u8212- the only difference between the {\i mashuach milchama} and the {\i sgan} is this: if they are going on the way and find a {\i meit mitzvah}, the {\i mashuach milchama} becomes {\i tamei} and not the {\i sgan}. So the {\i sgan} is the one who is protected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rationale: Sgan\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale is precisely what makes this a striking ruling. The {\i sgan}\u8217's entire job description is to remain {\i tahor} and ready. {\i Rebbi Chanina ben Antigonus omer: lama tiknu sgan lekohen gadol? she\u8217'im ye\u8217'arei bo psul harei nichnas umeshamesh tachtav} \u8212- why did they institute a {\i sgan} for the {\i kohen gadol}? So that if the {\i kohen gadol} becomes disqualified, the {\i sgan} enters and serves in his place. His primary responsibility is not some active task he is currently performing \u8212- it is the maintenance of his {\i taharah} and readiness. Becoming {\i tamei} would be a fundamental violation of the {\i sgan}\u8217's core function in a way that the {\i mashuach milchama}\u8217's becoming {\i tamei} is not. The {\i mashuach milchama}\u8217's role does not require him to preserve his {\i taharah} as its central purpose; the {\i sgan}\u8217's role does, almost by definition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Different Priorities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Noted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this ruling seems to contradict another {\i baraita}: {\i mashuach milchama kodem lesgan} \u8212- the {\i mashuach milchama} comes before the {\i sgan}, apparently suggesting that the {\i mashuach milchama} has higher priority. Does this not contradict the rule that the {\i sgan} is protected from {\i tumah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Contexts, Two Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves: {\i le\u8217'inyan hachayoso mashuach milchama adif} \u8212- when it comes to saving a life, the {\i mashuach milchama} takes priority. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama? detali beih rabim} \u8212- because the welfare of many depends on him. If one must choose whose life to save, the {\i mashuach milchama}\u8217's role in protecting the entire people gives him priority, much as one would prioritize saving the Secretary of Defense over a deputy official whose role is primarily one of readiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ule\u8217'inyan tumah sgan adif} \u8212- but when it comes to {\i tumah}, the {\i sgan} is given priority and is protected. {\i Detanya Rabbi Chanina ben Antigonus omer: lama tiknu sgan lekohen gadol} \u8212- because the entire rationale for the {\i sgan}\u8217's existence is to remain {\i tahor} and ready to serve if needed. These are two different questions yielding two different answers, and there is no contradiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Hierarchy Clari\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an important point here. The {\i baraita} states {\i ein bein mashuach milchama lesgan} \u8212- there is no difference between them except for this matter of {\i tumah}. But this seems plainly incorrect: there are in fact many significant differences between the two. The {\i mashuach milchama} wears the garments of the {\i kohen gadol}, while the {\i sgan} wears the garments of a {\i kohen hedyot} \u8212- even when performing his role, and even in the context of {\i yom kippur}, when the {\i kohen gadol} himself wears {\i bigdei lavan}. Moreover, the five restrictions enumerated in the {\i parasha} for the {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- not disheveling his hair, not rending his garments, not becoming {\i tamei} to the dead, not marrying a widow, and being required to marry only a {\i betulah} \u8212- all of these apply to the {\i mashuach milchama} but not to the {\i sgan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means the {\i mashuach milchama} is effectively a quasi-{\i kohen gadol} in a different sphere: not in the {\i Beit HaMikdash} but in the domain of the battlefield and the camp. So what does the {\i baraita} mean by {\i ein bein}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains: the {\i ein bein} means that there is no area in which we give greater weight to the {\i sgan} over the {\i mashuach milchama}, except for this one matter \u8212- that the {\i sgan} does not become {\i tamei} when they encounter a {\i meit mitzvah} together. In every other domain, the {\i mashuach milchama} has equal or greater status. But in this one instance, because the {\i sgan}\u8217's entire identity is bound up with maintaining {\i taharah}, that is the single exception where the {\i sgan} is protected and the {\i mashuach milchama} is not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Individual Override for\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Individual Permission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having completed the analysis of who takes priority when a {\i kohen gadol} and {\i nazir} travel together, the {\i Gemara} steps back to address the foundational premise. {\i Ad kan lo peligi ela be-Kohen Gadol ve-Nazir ki ka azli bahadi hadadi} \u8212- all the debate has been about who takes priority when they are traveling together. {\i Aval chad chad le-chudei bartzit meiyei inun} \u8212- but if each of them is alone, it is taken for granted that each one individually is permitted to become {\i tamei} to a {\i meit mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a fundamental question: {\i minani milei} \u8212- where does this come from? The {\i Torah} never states explicitly that a {\i meit mitzvah} overrides the prohibitions on {\i tumah} for a {\i kohen gadol} or a {\i nazir}. What is the textual source?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pesukkim: Al Kol Nafshot\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesuk: Le\u8217'aviv U\u8217'le\u8217'imo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the relevant {\i pesukim}. Regarding the {\i kohen gadol} it is written: {\i al kol nafshot met lo yavo} \u8212- he shall not approach any dead person, and separately: {\i le\u8217'aviv u\u8217'le\u8217'imo lo yitama} \u8212- not even to his father and mother shall he become {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Va\u8217'chomer Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: who is the subject of {\i al kol nafshot met lo yavo}? If it is addressing non-relatives, this would be redundant \u8212- {\i kal va-chomer hu}. A normal {\i kohen} who may become {\i tamei} to his relatives may not become {\i tamei} to non-relatives; how much more so a {\i kohen gadol} who cannot even become {\i tamei} to relatives \u8212- surely he cannot become {\i tamei} to non-relatives. There would be no need for the {\i Torah} to state this explicitly. Tosafot frames the same point more directly, without the circularity of a formal {\i kal va-chomer}: a {\i kohen gadol} is already a {\i kohen}, and as such is already prohibited against {\i tumah} to non-relatives in his basic capacity as a {\i kohen}. Therefore {\i al kol nafshot met} cannot be teaching us about non-relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Phrases Extra?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It follows that {\i al kol nafshot met lo yavo} must be referring to relatives \u8212- the {\i Torah} is telling us that a {\i kohen gadol} may not become {\i tamei} even to his own family. If that is so, then the subsequent phrase {\i le\u8217'aviv u\u8217'le\u8217'imo lo yitama} \u8212- not to his father and mother \u8212- appears to be entirely redundant. We have already been told he cannot become {\i tamei} to any relative. Why repeat it for father and mother specifically?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extra Words Teach New Ru\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because both phrases are extra, each can be expounded to teach something new. {\i Le\u8217'aviv} \u8212- \u8220"to his father\u8221" \u8212- is cited as an example: {\i ha mitamei hu le-met mitzvah} \u8212- but he does become {\i tamei} to a {\i meit mitzvah}. The specific mention of {\i le\u8217'aviv} signals that there is someone to whom he may become {\i tamei} \u8212- and this is the {\i meit mitzvah}. The logic follows the principle of {\i miyut achar miyut ela lerabbot}: two restrictions in sequence imply that within those restrictions there is a permitted category. The {\i Torah} has already said he cannot become {\i tamei} to relatives; adding \u8220"not to the father\u8221" implies \u8212- but there is one case where he can, namely a {\i meit mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezeira Shava: Le\u8217'imo Co\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir\u8217's Le\u8217'imo Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to {\i le\u8217'imo} \u8212- \u8220"to his mother\u8221" \u8212- and brings a {\i braisa} in the name of {\i Rebbi} that opens with the {\i nazir}. A {\i nazir} is told {\i lo yitama lahem bemotam} \u8212- he shall not become {\i tamei} to them in their deaths. The word {\i bemotam} \u8212- \u8220"in their deaths\u8221" \u8212- is significant: it specifies {\i tumat meit} as the prohibited {\i tumah}, thereby implying that other types of {\i tumah} \u8212- such as {\i tzara\u8217'at} or {\i zivah} \u8212- do not fall under the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition. {\i Gabi nazir bemotam eino mitamei aval mitamei hu lenigam ulezivatam} \u8212- for a {\i nazir}, the prohibition is specifically against becoming {\i tamei} through death-related {\i tumah}; contact with a {\i metzora} or a {\i zav} is not prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This might seem obvious, since the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition is textually framed around corpse-{\i tumah}. But it sets up the crucial {\i gezeira shava}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Applied to Kohen Gadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With respect to the {\i kohen gadol}, the question is different. The {\i pesukim} say {\i le\u8217'aviv u\u8217'le\u8217'imo lo yitama} \u8212- not to his father and mother shall he become {\i tamei}. These words do not specify {\i bemotam}; they simply say he shall not become {\i tamei}. One might therefore think the prohibition encompasses any {\i tumah} his father or mother might have \u8212- whether {\i tumat meit}, {\i tzara\u8217'at}, or {\i zivah}. {\i Bekohen gadol minalan} \u8212- how do we know that for a {\i kohen gadol} as well, the restriction is limited to {\i tumat meit}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is a {\i gezeira shava}: {\i ne\u8217'emar imo benazir vene\u8217'emar imo bekohen gadol} \u8212- the word {\i imo} (\u8220"his mother\u8221") appears both by the {\i nazir} and by the {\i kohen gadol}. This verbal parallel licenses a {\i halachic} connection: {\i mah imo ha\u8217'amur benazir bemotam eino mitamei aval mitamei hu lenigam ulezivatam} \u8212- just as by the {\i nazir}, the prohibition is limited to {\i tumat meit} (as established by {\i bemotam}), {\i af imo ha\u8217'amur bekohen gadol bemotam eino mitamei aval mitamei hu lenigam ulezivatam} \u8212- so too by the {\i kohen gadol}, the restriction is only against {\i tumat meit}, and contact with a {\i metzora} or {\i zav} is not prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redundancy Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall structure of the exposition is now complete. Since {\i al kol nafshot met lo yavo} must refer to relatives (as argued above), both {\i le\u8217'aviv} and {\i le\u8217'imo} are redundant within the {\i pesuk}. Each extra phrase comes to teach a distinct {\i halacha}: {\i le\u8217'aviv} teaches that there is a case \u8212- {\i meit mitzvah} \u8212- where even a {\i kohen gadol} becomes {\i tamei}; and {\i le\u8217'imo}, through the {\i gezeira shava} with the {\i nazir}, teaches that the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's prohibition is restricted to {\i tumat meit} and does not extend to other forms of {\i tumah}. These are not references to the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's specific father or mother \u8212- once {\i al kol nafshot met} encompasses all relatives, {\i le\u8217'aviv} and {\i le\u8217'imo} become free to carry new independent {\i halachic} content, as extra words in the {\i Torah} are never without purpose.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 48\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Gadol & Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meis Mitzvah Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 48a, approximately fifteen lines down, at the margin notation {\i Bamidbar} 6. The sugya is concerned with establishing the source for a striking principle: the prohibitions against {\i tumah} incumbent upon a {\i kohen gadol} and a {\i nazir} are each exceptionally severe and categorical \u8212- neither may become {\i tamei} even to his closest relatives \u8212- and yet both prohibitions are overridden when a {\i meis mitzvah} is at stake. The question the {\i gemara} sets out to answer is: what is the textual and logical basis for this override?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the {\i kohen gadol}, the Torah states: {\i al kol nafshos meis lo yavo, l\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo lo yitama} \u8212- he shall not enter upon any dead soul, and shall not become {\i tamei} for his father or mother. The {\i gemara}, as framed by {\i Tosafos}, reasons as follows: since a {\i kohen gadol} is already a standard {\i kohen}, he is already forbidden to become {\i tamei} to non-relatives by virtue of the general {\i kohen} prohibition. The phrase {\i al kol nafshos meis lo yavo} need not therefore be read as restricting him from non-relatives \u8212- that is already known. Rather, it must be addressing relatives. And if relatives are already implicit in the opening clause, what purpose does the separate phrase {\i l\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo lo yitama} serve? Since a prohibition followed by a further limiting phrase \u8212- a {\i miyut achar miyut} \u8212- implies an implicit permission, we derive that while for his father he is prohibited, a {\i meis mitzvah} overrides even this. The word {\i aviv} teaches no to relatives but yes to {\i meis mitzvah}. And the word {\i imo} is used to link \u8212- through a {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- to the corresponding passage in the laws of {\i nazir}, establishing that the prohibition applies only to {\i tumat meis} and not to other forms of {\i tumah} such as {\i tzara\u8217'as} or {\i zivah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's exception established, the {\i gemara} turns to the {\i nazir}: how do we know that a {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition is similarly overridden for a {\i meis mitzvah}? One might have proposed a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}: if a {\i kohen gadol}, whose sanctity is permanent and eternal, may become {\i tamei} for a {\i meis mitzvah}, then a {\i nazir}, whose sanctity is limited in time and assumed voluntarily, should certainly be overridden for a {\i meis mitzvah}. However, the {\i gemara} rejects this inference. The {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} can be reversed: a {\i kohen gadol} who becomes {\i tamei} intentionally violates a prohibition and receives lashes, but there is no {\i korban} associated with his violation. A {\i nazir} who becomes {\i tamei}, by contrast, must bring a {\i korban} and restart his {\i nezirut}. The {\i tumah} carries far heavier practical consequences for the {\i nazir}, and so one might argue that his {\i kedushah} is more precarious rather than less severe, and that a {\i meis mitzvah} therefore would not override it. The {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} fails, and a {\i pasuk} is needed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Braita Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nefesh Meis Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} transitions to the laws of {\i nazir} and introduces a {\i braita}. The relevant verse states: {\i al nefesh meis lo yavo} \u8212- the {\i nazir} shall not enter upon a dead soul \u8212- followed by {\i l\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo l\u8217'achiv u\u8217'l\u8217'achoso lo yitama lahem b\u8217'mosam}. The {\i braita} begins by asking whether the phrase {\i nefesh meis} might include even an animal carcass, since an animal is also called a {\i nefesh}, as in the verse {\i makeh nefesh behemah}. The {\i braita} resolves this by noting that a dead animal is called a {\i neveilah}, not a {\i nefesh meis} \u8212- {\i nefesh meis} denotes a human corpse specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma vs Yishmael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} derives this restriction from the word {\i meis} itself: the verse speaks of {\i nefesh adam}, a human being. Rabbi Yishmael disagrees and says there is no need to derive it from the word {\i meis}. The verse says {\i lo yavo} \u8212- he shall not enter the same space \u8212- not merely {\i lo yiga}, he shall not touch. The only form of {\i tumah} that operates through {\i biah}, through being under the same roof without direct contact, is {\i tumat meis} of a human corpse. All other forms of {\i tumah} operate only through touch or carrying. Since the verse prohibits {\i lo yavo}, it is implicitly addressing only human corpses: {\i benefashot hamitamot b\u8217'viah hakatuv medaber} \u8212- the verse speaks of those corpses that convey {\i tumah} through entry into the same space. One who enters the same room as the corpse, even without touching, becomes {\i tamei} \u8212- and this is true only of human corpses. Whether this extends to non-Jewish corpses with respect to {\i tumat ohel} is itself a matter of {\i machloket} among the {\i tannaim}, but for Jewish corpses there is no question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aviv v\u8217'Imo Signal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this established, the {\i braita} proceeds: {\i l\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo eino mitamei aval mitamei hu l\u8217'meis mitzvah} \u8212- for his father and mother he does not become {\i tamei}, but for a {\i meis mitzvah} he does. The words {\i aviv} and {\i imo} are understood as carrying a surplus meaning. Before explaining the precise function of each word, the {\i braita} notes that one might have thought the exception for {\i meis mitzvah} could be derived by logic alone: {\i ad shelo yomar yesh li badin} \u8212- even before the verse says so, I could have reasoned it out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic would be a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}: a {\i kohen gadol} whose sanctity is permanent \u8212- {\i kedushato kedushat olam} \u8212- may nonetheless become {\i tamei} for a {\i meis mitzvah}. A {\i nazir} whose sanctity is inherently time-limited \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ein kedushato kedushat olam} \u8212- should certainly be overridden for a {\i meis mitzvah}. But as already noted, the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} can be deflected: a {\i kohen gadol} does not bring a {\i korban} for his {\i tumah}, whereas a {\i nazir} brings a {\i korban} and must restart entirely. This counter-argument \u8212- {\i lo im amarta b\u8217'kohen gadol she\u8217'kein eino meivi korban al tumato} \u8212- is sufficient to defeat the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} next considers another possible interpretation of {\i l\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo}. Perhaps these words are not teaching that relatives are forbidden while {\i meis mitzvah} is permitted. Perhaps the reverse is intended: for his father and mother he is forbidden, but for other corpses \u8212- non-relatives \u8212- he is permitted? This {\i hava amina} has a certain logic: with relatives, the emotional pull is so strong that any boundary might dissolve. With strangers, there is less risk of overreach, so perhaps only relatives are restricted. However, the {\i gemara} dismisses this reading via a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} from the ordinary {\i kohen}: even a standard {\i kohen hedyot}, whose level of {\i kedushah} is far lower, is {\i tamei} to relatives but not to non-relatives \u8212- relatives are the easier case for a {\i kohen}, not the harder one. If a lower-grade {\i kohen} may become {\i tamei} for relatives but not for non-relatives, then certainly a {\i nazir}, who may not become {\i tamei} even for relatives, cannot be {\i tamei} for non-relatives. So {\i l\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo} is not telling us non-relatives are permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then is the function of these words? {\i L\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo she\u8217'eino mitamei aval mitamei hu l\u8217'meis mitzvah} \u8212- they are extra, and their surplus signals the permission of {\i meis mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A difficulty remains, however: for the {\i kohen gadol}, the opening clause was already understood as referring to relatives because he is already a standard {\i kohen} and is obviously forbidden to non-relatives. The extra words therefore pointed to {\i meis mitzvah}. But for a {\i nazir}, there is no such prior prohibition against non-relatives. A {\i nazir} does not begin from a baseline of being forbidden to any corpse. Therefore, the opening clause {\i al nefesh meis lo yavo} might itself be needed to establish the prohibition against non-relatives, leaving no surplus at all. If the opening clause addresses non-relatives, then {\i aviv} is needed to teach relatives, and there is nothing left over for {\i meis mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Shavah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through this carefully. Ultimately it concludes: {\i aviv} teaches that relatives are forbidden \u8212- that is its function for the {\i nazir}, in contrast to the {\i kohen gadol} where the same role was played by the opening clause. And {\i imo} is reserved for the {\i gezeirah shavah} connecting the {\i nazir}\u8217's verse to the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's verse on the matter of {\i b\u8217'mosam} \u8212- teaching that the prohibition applies only to {\i tumat meis} and not to other {\i tumot}. That leaves {\i achiv} \u8212- \u8220"his brother\u8221" \u8212- as the word that teaches {\i meis mitzvah} is permitted. And {\i achoso} remains to teach something further, which we will reach shortly. The {\i gemara} thus maps the words as follows for the {\i tanna kamma} (Chachamim) approach: the opening {\i al nefesh meis} establishes non-relatives; {\i aviv} establishes relatives; {\i imo} functions in the {\i gezeirah shavah} establishing that only {\i tumat meis} is covered; {\i achiv} teaches yes to {\i meis mitzvah}; and {\i achoso} teaches something additional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the entire structure of this {\i sugya} is built around an interplay between the authority of the text and the authority of human reasoning. The very phrase {\i ad shelo yomar yesh li badin} \u8212- I could have reasoned it out even without the verse \u8212- reflects a principle found elsewhere in the {\i gemara}: {\i lama li kra, sevara hu} \u8212- why do I need a verse, this is logical. The verse is needed only where the logic is genuinely ambiguous. Where the conclusion follows clearly from reason, a verse would be superfluous.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meis Mitzvah Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach & Milah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} now takes up the function of {\i achoso} \u8212- \u8220"his sister\u8221" \u8212- and what that word comes to teach. {\i U\u8217'l\u8217'achoso mah talmud lomar?} A case is presented: someone is on his way to slaughter his {\i korban Pesach} or to perform a {\i bris milah} for his son \u8212- both of which are {\i mitzvos aseh} whose violation carries {\i kares} \u8212- and he hears that a relative has died. Must he interrupt what he is doing and become {\i tamei} to attend to the burial? The instinct might be that yes, the obligation to bury relatives is powerful, perhaps powerful enough to override these other {\i mitzvos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Weighing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} in the discussion of {\i u\u8217'l\u8217'achoso mah talmud lomar} clarifies the scope of this rule: it applies even to a regular person \u8212- not a {\i kohen gadol} and not a {\i nazir} \u8212- who is in the process of performing {\i Pesach} or {\i milah}. {\i Tosafos} explains: {\i lo ati aseh d\u8217'tumah she\u8217'ein bah kares, d\u8217'dachi aseh d\u8217'Pesach u\u8217'milah she\u8217'yesh bahem kares} \u8212- a positive commandment to become {\i tamei} for a relative, which is a {\i mitzvah} without {\i kares}, does not override a weightier positive commandment associated with {\i kares}. One might have thought that the obligation to attend to a deceased relative, given how seriously the {\i gemara} treats {\i meis mitzvah}, would override even these powerful {\i mitzvos}. The {\i chidush} here is that it does not \u8212- at least in a situation where others are available to attend to the deceased, so that the relative does not become a {\i meis mitzvah}. If the person himself abandons {\i Pesach} or {\i milah} to attend to the burial, he violates the principle. This is a significant ruling given the general weight we assign to caring for the deceased.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achoso Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the verse then continues: {\i yachol lo yitamei l\u8217'meis mitzvah} \u8212- perhaps even in the case of a {\i meis mitzvah} he should not become {\i tamei}, even while performing other {\i mitzvos}? The answer: {\i talmud lomar achoso} \u8212- the word {\i achoso} teaches that by a {\i meis mitzvah}, he does become {\i tamei}, and moreover, {\i meis mitzvah} overrides even his being actively engaged ({\i osek}) in the {\i mitzvos} of {\i korban Pesach} and {\i milah}. {\i Achoso} therefore teaches the outer limit of {\i meis mitzvah}\u8217's power: not only does it override the {\i kedushah} of a {\i nazir} and a {\i kohen gadol}, it overrides one\u8217's active engagement in these other critical {\i mitzvos}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tanna Kamma & Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's (Chachamim\u8217's) {\i drasha}: {\i aviv} establishes that relatives are forbidden; {\i imo} serves the {\i gezeirah shavah} to establish that only {\i tumat meis} is at issue (not other {\i tumot}); {\i achiv} teaches that {\i meis mitzvah} overrides; {\i achoso} teaches that {\i meis mitzvah} overrides even when one is {\i osek} in {\i Pesach} and {\i milah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva offers an alternative {\i drasha}. He says: {\i nefesh ilu ha\u8217'rechokim, meis ilu ha\u8217'kerovim} \u8212- within the opening clause {\i al nefesh meis lo yavo}, the word {\i nefesh} already addresses non-relatives, and the word {\i meis} addresses relatives. Two separate words, two separate categories. This means that by the time we reach the subsequent listing of {\i aviv, imo, achiv, achoso}, we have already excluded both non-relatives and relatives from any permission, and the words that follow are entirely free to teach other things. Consequently, Rabbi Akiva does not need {\i aviv} to prohibit relatives \u8212- he has already accomplished that from the opening clause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Aviv, Imo, Achiv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rabbi Akiva, {\i l\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo eino mitamei aval mitamei hu l\u8217'meis mitzvah} \u8212- both {\i aviv} and {\i imo} together teach that {\i meis mitzvah} is permitted. This immediately raises a question: why are both words needed to convey a single ruling? The {\i gemara} will address this. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's use of both {\i aviv} and {\i imo} for {\i meis mitzvah} also creates a problem for the {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- if {\i imo} is occupied here, how can it simultaneously serve as the connecting word for the {\i gezeirah shavah} establishing that the prohibition applies only to {\i tumat meis}? That too will be addressed. {\i Achiv} and {\i achoso}, now freed from their earlier roles, are available for further teachings.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akiva Achiv & Achoso\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Achiv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rabbi Akiva, {\i achiv} teaches the following: {\i she\u8217'yehei kohen gadol v\u8217'hu nazir, l\u8217'achiv eino mitamei aval mitamei hu l\u8217'meis mitzvah} \u8212- this refers to a person who is both a {\i kohen gadol} and a {\i nazir} simultaneously. We already know from Rabbi Akiva\u8217's system that {\i meis mitzvah} overrides a {\i kohen gadol} alone, and that it overrides a {\i nazir} alone. But what of a person carrying both statuses at once, a double accumulation of prohibitions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achiv Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought that although a {\i meis mitzvah} overrides a single {\i lav}, it would not override two simultaneous {\i lavin}. Perhaps the quantitative increase in the number of prohibitions tips the scales. {\i Achiv} therefore comes to teach that {\i meis mitzvah} overrides even this double status: {\i mah li chad lav, mah li trei lavin} \u8212- whether there is one {\i lav} or two {\i lavin}, the {\i Torah}\u8217's dispensation for {\i meis mitzvah} applies equally.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezeirah Shavah Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problem Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an apparent difficulty for Rabbi Akiva. If both {\i aviv} and {\i imo} are together needed to teach that {\i meis mitzvah} is permitted for a {\i nazir}, then neither word is free at either end of the analogy, since they are both actively employed in the verse about the {\i nazir}. Equally, up at the verse of the {\i kohen gadol}, both words would presumably also be needed to teach {\i meis mitzvah} there. If so, {\i imo} is not free at either end, and the {\i gezeirah shavah} between the two passages \u8212- which requires at least one word to be free \u8212- cannot be constructed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solution: Already Known\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers concisely, and {\i Tosafos} elaborates: {\i kivan d\u8217'amar mar im hayah kohen gadol v\u8217'nazir l\u8217'achiv eino mitamei aval mitamei hu l\u8217'meis mitzvah, mah li kohen gadol lachudei, mah li nazir v\u8217'kohen gadol} \u8212- since Rabbi Akiva\u8217's own {\i drasha} on {\i achiv} already teaches that {\i meis mitzvah} overrides a person who is simultaneously a {\i kohen gadol} and a {\i nazir}, we have thereby already established that {\i meis mitzvah} overrides the {\i kohen gadol} as well. Once {\i meis mitzvah}\u8217's override of the {\i kohen gadol} is already known from {\i achiv}, the word {\i imo} in the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's verse no longer needs to convey that ruling \u8212- it is freed. And once it is free at that end, the {\i gezeirah shavah} can be constructed even without the word being free at both ends. {\i Imo} is therefore available to serve the {\i gezeirah shavah} linking the {\i nazir} and the {\i kohen gadol} on the restriction of {\i tumat meis} exclusively.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akiva Achiv & Achoso (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Achoso\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the function of {\i achiv} in Rabbi Akiva\u8217's system, the remaining word is {\i achoso}. For Rabbi Akiva, where {\i achoso} is no longer needed to teach the double-status case (already derived from {\i achiv}), {\i achoso} retains the role it plays in the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's system: teaching that {\i meis mitzvah} overrides even when one is actively engaged in other {\i mitzvos}, namely {\i korban Pesach} and {\i milah}. As the {\i braita} had stated: even if one is already on the way to perform these time-sensitive {\i mitzvos aseh she\u8217'yesh bahem kares}, if a {\i meis mitzvah} arises, he must stop and attend to it. And by way of parallel \u8212- for the {\i tanna kamma} ({\i Chachamim}), this same ruling was derived from {\i achoso} as well, but they needed {\i achiv} first for the basic {\i meis mitzvah} exception, having no other available word. Rabbi Akiva, having derived {\i meis mitzvah} from {\i aviv} and {\i imo} and double-status from {\i achiv}, uses {\i achoso} for this final teaching about overriding active engagement in other {\i mitzvos}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aviv & Imo Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aviv Alone Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We must now address the question of why Rabbi Akiva needs both {\i aviv} and {\i imo} to teach {\i meis mitzvah} \u8212- why would one of them alone not suffice? The {\i gemara} explains with two distinct {\i hava aminas}. Had the verse stated only {\i aviv} \u8212- the father \u8212- one might have thought: the reason the {\i nazir} is restricted from becoming {\i tamei} to his father is specifically because paternity is established by {\i chazakah} alone. You never know with absolute certainty who one\u8217's father is. Perhaps that very uncertainty is what generates the stringency \u8212- there is a measure of doubt about the relationship. But if so, the {\i tumah} restriction to the father might reflect that doubt rather than the general principle of {\i kedushah}. By implication, the mother \u8212- {\i deyadi\u8217'an deyaldasei}, whose identity as the birth mother is certain \u8212- might be permissible. So {\i aviv} alone could have been misread as indicating that only relationships characterized by paternal uncertainty are restricted, not maternal relationships.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Imo Alone Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, had the verse stated only {\i imo} \u8212- the mother \u8212- one might have thought the opposite. The reason the {\i nazir} is restricted from becoming {\i tamei} to his mother might be because she is not part of the patriarchal family identity. The Torah everywhere speaks of family in terms of the father\u8217's line: {\i l\u8217'mishpechotam l\u8217'beit avotam}. The mother\u8217's side is not the primary identifier of lineage or family in the Torah\u8217's structure. So if the verse said only {\i imo}, one might have understood: yes, the mother\u8217's side is restricted precisely because it falls outside the primary family definition, but the father\u8217's side \u8212- which {\i is} the family \u8212- should certainly be permissible. {\i Imo} alone could have been misread as limiting the prohibition to the non-patriarchal line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Combined Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, the verse states both {\i aviv} and {\i imo}. When read together, they convey nothing that the opening clause had not already established \u8212- all relatives are prohibited. The combination adds nothing new in terms of scope, and this is precisely the point. Because the combination does not limit the prohibition to one parental line over the other, it does not subtract from what the opening clause established. The words are therefore truly surplus, and a {\i miyut achar miyut} \u8212- a repeated restriction after an already-complete restriction \u8212- serves to {\i permit} rather than to further restrict. The surplus of both words {\i combined} is what signals the permission for {\i meis mitzvah}. Had only one been present, it might have been read as a limiting clarification rather than a permissive signal. With both present, neither misinterpretation is possible, and the {\i drasha} emerges cleanly: they are extra, and their surplus message is that {\i meis mitzvah} is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is what the lengthy {\i Tosafos} on this passage ultimately establishes. It navigates carefully through the apparent circularity \u8212- the words were used to teach {\i meis mitzvah}, yet the {\i gemara} seems to focus on why you need both to maintain the prohibition on relatives \u8212- and concludes that the necessity of both words is precisely what ensures the {\i drasha} for {\i meis mitzvah} is not susceptible to misinterpretation. You must say both to prevent the reader from thinking the verse is narrowing the prohibition rather than opening an exception.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol & Nefesh Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Nafshos Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the opening clause of the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's verse \u8212- {\i al kol nafshos meis lo yavo} \u8212- and asks, given everything Rabbi Akiva has now derived from subsequent words, what does {\i kol nafshos} teach? The answer is that it establishes the general prohibition against {\i tumah} from non-relatives: {\i l\u8217'afukei rechokim} \u8212- to exclude the thought that any leniency might apply even to distant persons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nafshot Plural\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The plural form {\i nafshos} serves a distinct function: {\i l\u8217'afukei revi\u8217'is dam sheyatza mishnei meitim} \u8212- to exclude a quarter-{\i log} ({\i revi\u8217'is}) of blood drawn from two separate corpses from the principle that a {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood from a single corpse conveys {\i tumah} through {\i ohel}. Normally, a {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood from one body represents a sufficient quantity to convey {\i tumat ohel}, and a {\i kohen gadol} or {\i nazir} would be prohibited. One might have thought that if the same volume is drawn from two separate corpses, it does not represent a complete single {\i meis} and therefore ought not convey {\i tumat ohel}. The plural {\i nafshos} \u8212- {\i kol nafshos} \u8212- teaches that even blood from two separate corpses combining to form a {\i revi\u8217'is} does convey {\i tumat ohel}, and is therefore prohibited for a {\i kohen gadol} and a {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the {\i sugya} of 48a reaches its conclusion. The bottom line of the entire discussion, shared by both the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Akiva despite their different methods of {\i drasha}, is consistent: both a {\i kohen gadol} and a {\i nazir} are prohibited from {\i tumat meis} for all relatives, but both are obligated to attend to a {\i meis mitzvah}, and that obligation supersedes even their heightened {\i kedushah} \u8212- and in fact supersedes active engagement in other weighty {\i mitzvos} as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 49\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's shiur picks up on 49b and will cover, God willing, through daf 50 as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Focus & Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i perek} has been focused on the prohibition against a {\i nazir} becoming {\i tamei}. We have spent considerable time understanding how {\i meis mitzvah} overrides the restrictions of both a {\i nazir} and a {\i kohen gadol}, and the question of priorities when those statuses are combined. Now the {\i mishnah} turns to the actual parameters of the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i tumah} restrictions \u8212- specifically, the minimum sizes and types of materials derived from a corpse that render a {\i nazir tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To some degree this brings us into territory that touches on broad sections of {\i Meseches Taharos}, but we will not be entering all of that. What makes this {\i perek} particularly interesting is a handful of cases where something causes {\i tumas meis} and yet the {\i nazir} does not shave as a result. There are a few such exceptions, learned from tradition \u8212- {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe m\u8217'Sinai} \u8212- where even though {\i tumas meis} technically obtains, the {\i nazir} is somehow excluded from the full consequence. This matters beyond the narrow context of {\i nezirus}, because the parameters of {\i tumas kohanim} are learned from the restrictions of the {\i nazir}. So these exceptions, though they may initially seem obscure, carry genuine practical weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Relevance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: how relevant is any of this today? The answer is that {\i tumah} for a {\i kohen} remains practically relevant. Beyond that, these {\i halachos} apply only to someone who actually takes a {\i nazir} vow \u8212- which, while uncommon, is halachically available.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First Impurity List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First List Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i al eilu tumos hanazir megale\u8217'ach} \u8212- for the following {\i tumot} does a {\i nazir} shave his head, destroying his current count of {\i nezirus}, obligating him to bring a {\i korban} and begin again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first item is {\i al hameis} \u8212- an entire corpse. Then {\i al kezayis min hameis} \u8212- even just an olive-sized piece of flesh from the corpse. The {\i kezayis} measure is notable here: it is normally associated with food, but it functions as a kind of default minimum in many contexts. The tradition is that even an olive-sized portion of the flesh of a corpse causes {\i tumas meis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that all the items in this first list \u8212- until we reach the final item \u8212- cause {\i tumas meis} even through {\i ohel}, that is, even if one is merely under the same roof. There are three modes of {\i tumah} conduction from a {\i meis}: {\i maga} (touching), {\i massa} (carrying), and {\i ohel} (tenting \u8212- being under the same roof, or being over the {\i meis}, or having the {\i meis} over you). The {\i Torah} emphasizes {\i ohel} particularly in the context of a full human corpse \u8212- {\i adam ki yamut b\u8217'ohel} \u8212- and one can conceptually understand that what {\i tumat ohel} is doing is identifying something sufficiently representative of the whole. This is why the {\i shiurim} for {\i ohel} are somewhat different: some very small items cause {\i tumah} through touch but not through {\i ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third item in the list is {\i al kezayis netzel} \u8212- a {\i kezayis} of {\i netzel}, a substance we will examine closely in the {\i Gemara}. In brief, it refers to flesh that has dissolved and then recongealed, or to juices that ran off the flesh and then congealed through boiling. It retains the status of flesh for purposes of {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Next is {\i melo torvad rekev} \u8212- a ladle-full of {\i rekev}, the decomposed matter of a body that has turned to dust. This is not flesh that liquefied and resolidified; it is the natural dusty decomposition of the body. Several of these {\i shiurim}, as we will see, are {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe m\u8217'Sinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then lists {\i al shidra v\u8217'al gulgoles} \u8212- the spinal cord and the skull, whether that means either one alone or the combination will be clarified. Then {\i al ever min hameis} \u8212- a complete limb of the dead body \u8212- and {\i al ever min hachai} \u8212- a limb that fell off from a living person. This last item is striking. Until now one might have thought the list is about things that representatively stand in for the whole corpse. But here the person is not dead. A limb severed from a living person is itself treated as a kind of {\i meis}, independent of whether the person survives. The {\i ever} must have upon it {\i basar karuy} \u8212- enough flesh that, were it still attached to the body, the limb could heal over and scar. It cannot be bone alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Kav Bones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i v\u8217'al chatzi kav atzamos} \u8212- a half of a {\i kav} of bones. A {\i kav} is a volume measure; a half-{\i kav} is roughly one and a half to two and a half quarts in the range of contemporary estimates. Tosafot notes that this is not referring to just any random collection of bones. The bones must come from {\i rov minyano o rov binyano} \u8212- either a majority of the number of bones in the body, or a majority of the body\u8217's structural bones. That quantity itself, {\i rov minyan v\u8217'rov binyan}, causes {\i tumat ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot continues: {\i rov binyan} refers to the two hips, or one thigh, or the equivalent \u8212- the major structural bones of the body. And then Tosafot introduces the key point: even though {\i tumat ohel} is caused by a {\i rova} (a quarter of a {\i kav}) of bones that come from {\i rov minyan} or {\i rov binyan}, the {\i nazir} does not shave unless there is a full half-{\i kav}. This is stated explicitly in the first chapter of {\i Ohalos}. So here we encounter the first of the exceptions: a quarter-{\i kav} of bones from {\i rov minyan} or {\i rov binyan} causes {\i tumas meis d\u8217'Oraisa}, yet the {\i nazir} does not shave on account of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quarter-Kav Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} that a {\i nazir} shaves only on a half-{\i kav} and not a quarter-{\i kav} is itself a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe m\u8217'Sinai}. This may seem like a minor technical distinction, but the principle is important: there exist cases of {\i tumas meis} that do not trigger {\i nazir megaleach}. These exceptions also bear on {\i tumas kohanim}, since the {\i nazir}\u8217's laws govern those parameters. A {\i kohen} who would otherwise be prohibited from contracting {\i tumah} is likewise not bound by this prohibition ({\i ein kohen muzhar alav}) in cases where the {\i nazir} does not shave \u8212- such as {\i tumat ohel} from a quarter-{\i kav} of bones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Modes of Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first list concludes: {\i v\u8217'al magaan v\u8217'al masaan v\u8217'al ahilan} \u8212- touching them, carrying them, or being in their {\i ohel}. Everything on this list causes {\i tumah} through all three modes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Second Impurity List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Barley-Grain Bone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then introduces a second, shorter list: {\i v\u8217'al etzem k\u8217'se\u8217'ora} \u8212- even a tiny chip of bone the size of a barley grain. This causes {\i tumah}, but only through {\i al maga\u8217'o v\u8217'al masa\u8217'o} \u8212- touching and carrying \u8212- not through {\i ohel}. The key distinction between the two lists is therefore this: most things that cause {\i tumas meis} through contact also cause it through {\i ohel}. But bones are different. For {\i tumat ohel}, you need either the skull or the spinal cord, or {\i rov minyan} or {\i rov binyan} expressed in the minimum volume. For {\i tumah} through contact, even a tiny sliver of bone the size of a barley grain suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences of Impurity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a {\i nazir} contracts {\i tumah} from any of these sources, the consequences are: {\i u\u8217'mazeh bashlishi u\u8217'va\u8217'shvi\u8217'i} \u8212- sprinkling of the purification-water on days three and seven; {\i soter es ha\u8217'kodmin} \u8212- all prior days of the {\i nezirus} count are destroyed; and he does not begin counting again until he becomes {\i tahor}, brings his {\i korbanot}, and only then begins the new count. The chip of bone the size of a barley grain, though it causes {\i tumah} only through touch and not through {\i ohel}, still triggers this full set of consequences for the {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sumchos Incident\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens this section with a {\i baraisa}: after the passing of Rabbi Meir \u8212- {\i achar petiraso shel Rabbi Meir}, literally \u8220"after his departure\u8221" \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda said to his students: do not allow Rabbi Meir\u8217's students into our {\i beis medrash}, because they are {\i kantranim} \u8212- troublemakers. They do not come with a sincere desire to learn {\i Torah}; they come {\i lekapacheini bahalachos} \u8212- to harass through {\i halachic} argumentation, treating {\i halacha} as an intellectual exercise and mind game rather than as a genuine search for truth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sumchos Forces Entry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite this, {\i dachak Sumchos v\u8217'nichnas} \u8212- Sumchos forced his way in. Sumchos was one of Rabbi Meir\u8217's most distinguished students. He came and reported: {\i kach shana li Rabbi Meir} \u8212- this is how Rabbi Meir taught me this {\i mishnah} \u8212- listing both {\i al hameis} and {\i al kezayis min hameis} explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda grew angry and said to his students: did I not warn you? Don\u8217't let Rabbi Meir\u8217's students in here \u8212- they are troublemakers. His objection was: {\i al kezayis min hameis megaleach, al hameis lo kol shekein?} If you shave for a {\i kezayis} of flesh from the corpse, is it not obviously true all the more so for the entire corpse? Why state {\i al hameis} at all? It is entirely implicit. The irritation is understandable: it is not merely that Rabbi Meir\u8217's version includes an extra phrase. It is that Sumchos is insisting on it, presenting it as halachically significant. \u8220"What are you doing?\u8221" is the implied complaint. \u8220"You\u8217're just making trouble \u8212- it\u8217's obvious.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi\u8217's Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's response is itself worth pausing over. He says: {\i yomru Meir shachav, Yehuda ka\u8217'as, Yosi shatak \u8212- Torah mah tehei aleha?} They will say: Meir passed away ({\i shachav} \u8212- he went to lie down), Yehuda grew angry, and Yosi was silent \u8212- what will be of the {\i Torah}? Rabbi Yosi is saying: I must defend Rabbi Meir\u8217's honor, and more importantly, his {\i Torah}, even though I am Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's colleague. The personalities are getting in the way. The {\i Torah} has to be defended regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So Rabbi Yosi offers a justification: {\i lo nitzrecha ela l\u8217'meis she\u8217'ein alav kezayis basar} \u8212- you need the explicit statement of {\i al hameis} for the case of a corpse that has no {\i kezayis} of flesh remaining. Imagine a body that has decomposed so that only bones remain \u8212- there is no {\i kezayis} of flesh. Yet it is a complete corpse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nefel Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} objects: but that is also covered by the other items in the list \u8212- limbs, or {\i rov binyan}, and so on. If you shave even for a single limb, surely you shave for the whole body?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then brings Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's formulation: {\i lo nitzrecha ela l\u8217'nefel shelo nitkashru evarav begidin} \u8212- it is needed for the case of a {\i nefel}, a stillborn or miscarried fetus, whose sinews have not yet developed. Because there are no {\i gidin}, the halachic concept of an {\i ever} does not apply. Such a {\i nefel} could be very underdeveloped \u8212- possibly as early as day forty \u8212- and might have neither a {\i kezayis} of flesh nor a developed skeletal structure sufficient to trigger the other items on the list. In that case, only {\i al hameis} covers it. Why the absence of {\i gidin} also excludes the {\i shidra} and {\i gulgoles} categories is not fully explored, but there seems to be an assumption that if the fetus is so small and undeveloped, those thresholds are also not met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers yet another resolution: {\i lo nitzrecha ela l\u8217'rov binyano o l\u8217'rov minyano she\u8217'ein bahen rov atzamos} \u8212- it is needed for a case where you have {\i rov minyan} and {\i rov binyan}, but not the minimum volume. If the corpse is very small \u8212- such as a very tiny skeletal structure \u8212- you could theoretically have a majority of the number of bones and a majority of the body\u8217's structure, and yet not reach the minimum volume threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an important clarification: {\i rov minyan} and {\i rov binyan} are not independent of volume. As Tosafot demonstrated, the requirement is {\i rov minyan} or {\i rov binyan} expressed in at least a {\i rova} (for ordinary {\i tumas ohel}) or a half-{\i kav} (for {\i nazir megaleach}). Even a full {\i rov minyan} and {\i rov binyan}, whole and uncrushed, without that minimum volume, does not cause {\i tumat ohel}. So a very small corpse could have {\i rov minyan} and {\i rov binyan} \u8212- every structural bone present \u8212- and yet fall below the minimum volume. In that case, the explicit {\i al hameis} is what covers it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah vs.\u160 ?Personalities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One student made a beautiful observation worth noting. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's language \u8212- {\i Meir shachav, Yehuda ka\u8217'as, Yosi shatak} \u8212- is not merely narrative background. It captures something deeper. When all the focus falls on personalities \u8212- Meir passed away, Yehuda grew angry, Yosi stayed silent \u8212- the {\i Torah} is forgotten. That is precisely what Rabbi Yosi was responding to. His statement, {\i Torah mah tehei aleha}, is a reorientation: stop thinking about the people, start thinking about the {\i Torah} being taught. It is also worth noting that the {\i Gemara}\u8217's terminology of death is notably dignified \u8212- {\i petira} (he took his leave), {\i shachav} (he went to lie down) \u8212- language that preserves a sense of the soul continuing and of departing to another realm, rather than reducing death to mere bodily dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same {\i sugya} appears, in a more developed literary form, in Kiddushin 52b. There, the {\i mishnah} in question concerns whether a {\i kohen} can effect {\i kiddushin} with {\i basar kodshei kodashim} \u8212- meat of the innermost sanctified offerings, which has monetary value even if the woman cannot eat it herself. The absurdity Rabbi Yehuda objects to is: what is a woman doing in the {\i Azara} conducting a {\i kiddushin} ceremony? That makes the nature of his complaint clearer \u8212- Rabbi Meir\u8217's students are not pursuing practical {\i halacha} but are constructing fanciful, practically impossible scenarios as intellectual exercises. And the {\i Gemara} there provides a charming series of answers about how a woman could legitimately be in the {\i Azara} \u8212- her father is there as the recipient, or she sent a {\i shaliach} \u8212- and the final answer plays directly off {\i dachak Sumchos v\u8217'nichnas}: {\i dachka venichnesa} \u8212- perhaps she simply forced her way in. So the literary structure of that {\i sugya} illuminates our own and explains why the troublemaker framing resonates so strongly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Netzel Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Netzel Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} lists {\i kezayis netzel} among the things for which a {\i nazir} shaves. The {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i ve\u8217'eizu netzel} \u8212- what exactly is {\i netzel}? Two answers are given: (1) {\i basar hameis shekarash} \u8212- flesh of the dead that had liquefied and then recongealed; and (2) {\i mochal she\u8217'hirtiach} \u8212- juices that ran off the flesh and then were boiled and congealed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identifying Netzel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: what is the case? If we simply do not know whether this congealed substance came from the flesh or from some other liquid of the body \u8212- like saliva or mucus \u8212- then the act of congealing tells us nothing about its identity. Those other bodily fluids are not {\i metamei} as {\i tumas meis} in their own right; they may carry indirect {\i tumah} from contact with the dead, but they are not themselves the {\i meis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i Gemara} clarifies: {\i ella diyaadina dedidahu} \u8212- we know that it came from the flesh. And then the question becomes: even so, does congealing matter? Even in dissolved or liquid form, since we know it came from the flesh, it should cause the same {\i tumah}. What does congealing add?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiyah answers: {\i bistam} \u8212- in an ambiguous case, where we do not know whether this liquid came from the flesh or from {\i keicho v\u8217'ni\u8217'o} (phlegm and mucus), congealing is the identifying factor. {\i I karash} \u8212- if it congeals \u8212- {\i mochal hu} \u8212- then we know it is from the juices of the flesh, since those other bodily fluids would not congeal in the same way. {\i Lo karash} \u8212- if it does not congeal \u8212- then it may well be mucus or saliva, and we cannot be certain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i chiddush} of the {\i Gemara} is that the {\i tumah} status of the dissolved flesh does not actually depend on it being solid \u8212- even in liquid form, flesh-derived {\i netzel} is {\i metamei}. The congealing or the boiling is not a condition for {\i tumah} but rather a diagnostic tool to establish identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot extends this reasoning to the {\i hirtiach} case: we know the substance came from the body, but we do not know whether it is {\i mochal} from the flesh or from {\i keicho v\u8217'ni\u8217'o}. If it boils and bubbles in a characteristic way ({\i de\u8217'avar ritchus}), then it is identifiably from the flesh. If not, it may be mucus. The precise logic of why boiling identifies it as such \u8212- rather than simply being another test of viscosity \u8212- is not entirely clear, but Tosafot is fitting it into the same structural framework as the congealing case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Netzel for Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye poses a question to Rava: {\i yesh netzel livheimah o ein netzel livheimah} \u8212- does the principle of {\i netzel} apply to animals and {\i tumas neveilah}, or is it unique to {\i tumas meis}? The question is whether this is a {\i halacha} about the definition of flesh as such \u8212- in which case it should apply broadly \u8212- or whether it is a tradition specific to human corpse {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two possible positions frame the question: perhaps the tradition of {\i netzel} is specifically {\i d\u8217'ati mi-adam} \u8212- derived from human remains, as part of {\i tumas meis} \u8212- and does not extend to {\i tumas neveilah}. Or perhaps there is no distinction, and liquefied animal flesh counts as flesh for {\i tumas neveilah} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumat Chamurah Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether this question is even practically relevant, however, depends on another dispute. The {\i Gemara} introduces the principle: {\i tumah chamurah ad lager, v\u8217'tumah kalah ad lekelev}. The heavier {\i tumah} \u8212- specifically {\i tumas neveilah}, the carcass of an animal that is itself a primary source of {\i tumah} \u8212- is measured by fitness for human consumption. This is derived from the verse {\i lager asher bish\u8217'arecha titnenah ve\u8217'achalcha}: the carcass may be given to the {\i ger} to eat; from here we learn {\i ha-raui lager karui neveilah she-eino raui lager eino karui neveilah} \u8212- only what is fit for human consumption bears the status of {\i neveilah} and its accompanying {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is itself somewhat counterintuitive, since most {\i avot hatumah} have nothing to do with edibility \u8212- a {\i zav}, a {\i niddah}, a {\i metzora} \u8212- none of those derive their {\i tumah} status from being food. Nevertheless, for {\i tumas neveilah}, which inherently concerns the flesh of what would normally be edible, the tradition is that the {\i tumah} dissipates once the flesh is no longer fit for human consumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tumah kalah ad lekelev} is parenthetical to our discussion: it refers to foods that conduct {\i tumah} from elsewhere \u8212- the threshold for something to be considered \u8220"food\u8221" in that context may be as low as being fit for consumption by a dog. This is a familiar distinction from {\i hilchos Pesach} and elsewhere, where different areas of {\i halacha} operate with different definitions of food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Moot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, if {\i tumah chamurah ad lager} is correct \u8212- that {\i tumas neveilah} requires fitness for human consumption \u8212- then the question of whether {\i netzel} from an animal is considered {\i neveilah} is moot. The juices of decomposing flesh are not fit for human consumption, and therefore they are simply {\i tahor} regardless of whether they conceptually count as \u8220"flesh.\u8221" The question only has force if {\i tumah chamurah ad lekelev} \u8212- if even {\i tumas neveilah} requires only fitness for dog consumption, and one holds that decomposing animal juices remain fit for a dog.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then brings a {\i tosefta}: if you melt {\i neveilah} fat over fire, it remains {\i tamei}; if you leave it to decompose in the sun and it liquefies, it is {\i tahor}. One might think this proves the {\i lekelev} opinion wrong \u8212- even liquefied flesh is {\i tahor} \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} deflects this. When left out in the sun, the flesh first putrefies ({\i asrach}) before it liquefies; at that point it has become like dust, no longer fit even for a dog. So the {\i tosefta} is {\i tahor} not because liquefied flesh cannot qualify as {\i neveilah}, but because the particular process of sun-rotting renders it unfit even for {\i lekelev}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion, therefore, is that if one holds {\i tumah chamurah ad lekelev}, and one imagines a scenario where the liquefied animal flesh remains fit for a dog, the question of whether {\i netzel} applies to {\i neveilah} is genuinely unresolved. The {\i Gemara} leaves it as a {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nitzok Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pouring Not Connected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now transitions to a {\i mishnah} from {\i Machshirin}: {\i kol hanitzok tahor} \u8212- all instances of pouring are {\i tahor}. The scenario is this: I have a {\i tamei} carcass, and I am pouring water from a vessel onto it. The water that actually contacts the carcass becomes {\i tamei} \u8212- it is a {\i rishon}. But what of the water still in the vessel above? Is the stream of water connecting them treated as one unit, rendering the water in the vessel {\i tamei} as well? The answer is no: the poured liquid does not constitute a single connected unit with the source. Only what actually makes contact becomes {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Honey Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} provides exceptions: {\i chutz midevash hazifim} \u8212- except for a very thick type of honey \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hatzapichit} \u8212- and the waxy cakes of honey that come directly from the beehive. These substances are so thick that they are considered more like a solid, and the normal rule of {\i nitzok} does not apply to them. They are treated as sufficiently solid \u8212- or sufficiently connected \u8212- to constitute a single unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai add: {\i af hamikpah shel gerisin v\u8217'shel pol} \u8212- even a thick porridge of oat-groats or lentils can constitute a connected unit, {\i mipnei shehi soledet leachoreha} \u8212- because it \u8220"pulls back.\u8221" What this means is: if you invert the vessel mid-pour, a watery liquid will simply fall away entirely, while something sufficiently viscous will partially retract back into the vessel. That pulling back demonstrates connectedness and coherence. Notably, the term {\i soledet}, meaning to recoil or pull back, echoes the {\i shiur} of {\i yad soledes bo} \u8212- the threshold of heat in the laws of cooking on Shabbos, where one\u8217's hand recoils from contact. Both uses of the root share the idea of a reactive withdrawal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nitzok for Foods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nitzok Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Building on this {\i mishnah}, the {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i yesh nitzok le\u8217'ochlin o ein nitzok le\u8217'ochlin} \u8212- does the principle of {\i nitzok} apply to foods? Specifically: if something that began as solid food is liquefied \u8212- say, ground down or cooked into a thick flowing mass \u8212- and then poured onto a source of {\i tumah}, is the remaining portion in the vessel considered connected to what flows out, or not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question turns on which of two principles underlies the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling. The first possibility: {\i mishum d\u8217'is behu riri} \u8212- the reason thick honey and similar substances are considered connected is that they possess {\i riri}, sticky strands that cling and cohere. If that is the criterion, then even a very thick liquefied food that lacks these clinging strands would not be considered connected. The second possibility: {\i mishum d\u8217'smichi} \u8212- the reason is simply thickness itself, not the quality of stickiness. If that is the criterion, then any sufficiently thick liquefied food would be connected regardless of whether it forms such strands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Beit Shammai, the criterion is clearly {\i riri}: they explicitly speak of the substance {\i soldin leachoreha} \u8212- pulling back \u8212- which is precisely the behavior of something sticky and cohesive. But the question is what the {\i Tanna Kamma} holds. When the {\i Tanna Kamma} mentions only the very thick honey, is it because honey has {\i riri}, or because honey is thick? It would seem intuitive that honey also has {\i riri}, and so both could be true of honey. Beit Shammai\u8217's contribution would then be extending the principle to things that have {\i riri} but are less thick than honey. But the {\i Gemara} does not resolve this definitively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fat of Corpse Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava brings a proof from a {\i mishnah}: {\i cheilev hameis she\u8217'hu shaleim, v\u8217'hisicho \u8212- tamei}. If the fat of a corpse is whole \u8212- meaning present in the minimum quantity sufficient for {\i tumah} \u8212- and then liquefied, it remains {\i tamei}. However, {\i hayah mefurad v\u8217'hisicho \u8212- tahor}: if the fat was already separated into pieces individually below the minimum, and then melted together, it is {\i tahor}. The logic of that second ruling concerns a broader question: can something that lost its {\i tumah} status by falling below the minimum regain it once the volume is reconstituted? The {\i mishnah} assumes it cannot, at least for {\i tumah} through contact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liquefied Fat Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What concerns us is the first clause: once the fat is liquefied, it remains {\i tamei}. But here a paradox emerges. The {\i Gemara} is exploring the possibility that {\i ein nitzok le\u8217'ochlin} \u8212- that liquefied food is {\i not} considered one connected unit. If that is so, then even the fat sitting at the bottom of the vessel is not connected to the fat at the top. The fat, once liquefied, is simply a large collection of microscopic droplets, no more united than any poured liquid. So how can one say the entire quantity is {\i tamei}? One is not touching all of it simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This challenge is more striking than it may first appear, because the question of {\i nitzok} is not limited to the act of pouring. Even if the liquid is stable within a vessel, it may not constitute a single halachic unit. The {\i Gemara} is suggesting that liquid is not \u8220"connected\u8221" in the halachic sense even when it is sitting undisturbed in a container \u8212- and this makes the case of the liquefied fat puzzling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Zeira Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira, together with Mar the son of Rav Avina, offers an answer: {\i kegon debehadei demartach lei, salik amuda denura lefuma demana, vekarash} \u8212- we are speaking of a case where, in the very process of liquefying the fat over a fire, a column of flame reached the opening of the vessel and caused the fat to recongeal at the surface. In other words, the fat never became fully and stably liquid; in the same moment that the fire melted it, the heat at the opening caused it to solidify again. The whole quantity is therefore treated as congealed and unified. This is admittedly a forced reading of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's plain language, which speaks straightforwardly of the fat being liquefied, but Rav Zeira is constraining the case to preserve coherence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina-Ashi Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina said to Rav Ashi: come and hear a proof from Beit Shammai \u8212- {\i Beit Shammai omrim af hamikpah shel gerisin v\u8217'shel pol mi-she-hen soladin leachoreha} \u8212- you can see that for Beit Shammai, the criterion is {\i riri}, stickiness, not mere thickness. The fact that the porridge pulls back is what makes it connected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects: {\i midi irya?} Is that relevant? {\i Hasam mishum desmichin, hacha mishum riri} \u8212- there, perhaps the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's honey is about thickness; here, Beit Shammai are about {\i riri}. In other words: Beit Shammai\u8217's criterion is clearly {\i riri}, but we do not know whether the {\i Tanna Kamma} agrees with that criterion or operates on thickness alone. The case of honey is compatible with both \u8212- honey is both thick and sticky \u8212- so it cannot disambiguate. The {\i Gemara} therefore leaves the original question unresolved: whether for the {\i Tanna Kamma}, a very thick but non-sticky liquefied food constitutes a connected unit for {\i tumah} purposes remains a {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 49b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Overview & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf continues our forward pace through the masechta, picking up on 49b, with the expectation of covering through daf 50 as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i perek} has been centrally concerned with the prohibition against a {\i nazir} becoming {\i tamei}. The earlier material established that a {\i nazir} may become {\i tamei} for a {\i meit mitzvah}, and we devoted considerable time to understanding how that exception is derived from the relevant verses \u8212- including how {\i meit mitzvah} overrides the restrictions of both a {\i nazir} and a {\i kohen gadol}, even when those sanctities are compounded. The question of priority \u8212- whose {\i kedushah} one must protect first when there is some element of choice \u8212- also occupied us. The present {\i mishnah} turns to the actual parameters of a {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i tumah} restrictions: specifically, what types and quantities of a dead body trigger the {\i nazir}\u8217's obligation to shave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Parameters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To some degree, this {\i mishnah} covers territory one might expect to find in {\i masechta Taharot}, cataloguing the minimum sizes of a dead body and its parts that cause {\i tumat meit}. We will not be immersing ourselves in every corner of those laws, but the {\i mishnah} does give us the basic framework: what objects, in what quantities, cause {\i tumat meit}, and in particular which of those require the {\i nazir} to shave his head, nullifying his prior count, requiring a {\i korban}, and necessitating a fresh start.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha L\u8217'Moshe Exceptio\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important nuance emerges: in a few cases, something causes {\i tumat meit} as a matter of {\i d\u8217'oraita} law, and yet the {\i nazir} is nonetheless not obligated to shave. These exceptions are derived from {\i halacha l\u8217'moshe misinai} \u8212- received tradition without explicit scriptural derivation. This distinction is not merely theoretical. Since the parameters of {\i tumat kohanim} are learned from the restrictions of the {\i nazir}, these exceptions carry practical relevance for {\i kohanim} as well, and one should keep that in mind as the discussion unfolds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah: Tumah Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i al elu tumos hanazir megalei\u8217'ach} \u8212- on the following {\i tumot} does a {\i nazir} shave his head, destroying his prior count of {\i nezirut}, requiring the relevant {\i korbanot}, and beginning the count again only after becoming {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Corpse & Flesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first item is {\i al hameit} \u8212- an entire corpse \u8212- and {\i al kezayit min hameit} \u8212- even a {\i kezayit}-sized piece of the flesh of the corpse. The choice of {\i kezayit} is notable. In most contexts, {\i kezayit} is a standard measure for foods; here it functions as a kind of default minimum size, the received tradition being that even an olive-sized piece of a corpse\u8217's flesh causes {\i tumat meit}. It is worth emphasizing from the outset that the items on this first list all cause {\i tumat meit ba\u8217'ohel}: that is, not only through direct contact or carrying, but through being under the same roof \u8212- through {\i ahel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition of Ahel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ahel} \u8212- tenting \u8212- encompasses several configurations: you may be beneath the same roof as the {\i meit}, the {\i meit} may be above you, or you may be above it. The Torah\u8217's language of {\i adam ki yamut ba\u8217'ohel} provides the scriptural anchor for this concept. Conceptually, what {\i tumat ohel} is capturing is the idea of something representative of the whole \u8212- a part of the dead body significant enough to stand in for the complete person. That is why the {\i shi\u8217'urim} for {\i tumat ohel} tend to be somewhat larger or more specific than those for {\i maga} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder about more complex spatial configurations \u8212- for instance, whether a {\i meit} on an upper floor causes {\i tumah} to someone in a basement with a floor separating them. The short answer is that this is precisely what {\i masechta Oholot} is about, with the key principle being that a roof with a {\i tefach} of airspace beneath it can, under most circumstances, interrupt the {\i tumah} and prevent it from passing through. The reason that flying over a cemetery remains a problem is that there is no {\i tefach} cavity \u8212- the coffin itself is treated as an extension of the {\i meit} rather than a barrier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Netzeil Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i v\u8217'al kezayit netzeil} \u8212- on a {\i kezayit} of {\i netzeil}. The {\i gemara} will define this precisely, but the basic concept is flesh that has liquefied and subsequently recongealed, which carries the same {\i halachic} status as the flesh itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rekev Amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'al melo tarvad rekav} \u8212- on a ladle-full of {\i rekav}, the decomposed matter of the body. This is not flesh that liquefied and resolidified; this is the body reduced to dust, the biological endpoint of decay. The {\i gemara} will have more to say about these distinctions. Some of these categories are rooted in {\i halacha l\u8217'moshe misinai} without explicit derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spine & Skull\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i al hashidra v\u8217'al hagulgollet} \u8212- the spinal column and the skull, whether they operate jointly or severally will become clearer from context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limb Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'al eiver min hameit} \u8212- a complete limb from a dead body \u8212- and, perhaps more surprisingly, {\i v\u8217'al eiver min hachai} \u8212- a limb severed from a living person. This second case is striking. Until this point, one might have understood the principle as synecdoche: the part represents the whole person who has died. But here the person is alive. The limb itself, when severed, takes on an independent status akin to a {\i meit}. It is not representing a corpse; it is itself treated as a kind of {\i meit} in its own right. This has practical implications \u8212- a limb lost to amputation, for example, would require burial. For a limb from a corpse or from a living person to carry this status, there must be adequate flesh on it: {\i sheyesh alav basar karaui}, sufficient flesh that, were the limb still attached to the body, it would be capable of healing over.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bones Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'al chatzi kav atzamot} \u8212- on a half-{\i kav} of bones. Roughly speaking, this is a volume of a few ounces to perhaps a liter or more, a not entirely trivial quantity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Bone Amounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} here makes an important observation that deserves careful attention. The {\i mishnah} specifies a half-{\i kav}, but {\i Tosafot} notes that it is not simply any random collection of bones. The bones must derive from either {\i rov minyan} \u8212- a majority of the count of the bones in the body \u8212- or {\i rov binyan} \u8212- a majority of the structural mass of the body. Once one has bones satisfying that criterion, and they are crushed, and one scoops out a half-{\i kav} from that pile, that quantity triggers the {\i nazir}\u8217's obligation to shave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} further clarifies {\i rov binyan}: it is exemplified by the two hip-bones and one thigh-bone, or the reverse \u8212- the largest structural bones of the body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key distinction {\i Tosafot} draws, however, concerns the relationship between {\i tumat ohel} and the {\i nazir}\u8217's shaving obligation. The {\i halacha} for {\i tumat ohel} in general is a quarter-{\i kav} \u8212- {\i rova hakav} \u8212- as stated explicitly in {\i perek} 2 of {\i masechta Oholot}. Yet the {\i halacha l\u8217'moshe misinai} for the {\i nazir} requires twice that amount: a half-{\i kav}. This is one of the cases where an amount that genuinely causes {\i tumat meit d\u8217'oraita} does not obligate the {\i nazir} to shave. A quarter-{\i kav} of bones from {\i rov minyan} or {\i rov binyan} causes {\i tumat ohel}, but the {\i nazir} does not shave until the quantity reaches a half-{\i kav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i kohen} in such a case would technically not be in violation of the {\i lav} \u8212- just as we say {\i ein kohen muzhar alav} in such a context \u8212- and could theoretically become {\i tamei} through {\i tumat ohel} from this quarter-{\i kav} without violating the prohibition. These fine distinctions, though they may seem remote, matter for practical {\i hilchot kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'al chatzi log dam} \u8212- on a half-{\i log} of blood. Here again, the same principle applies. A {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood from a corpse causes {\i tumat ohel} \u8212- this was established at the end of the previous daf\u8217's discussion. Yet once more, for the {\i nazir} to be obligated to shave, the quantity must be double: a half-{\i log}. So both for bones and for blood, the threshold for {\i tumat ohel} in general is half the quantity required to trigger the {\i nazir}\u8217's shaving obligation \u8212- another instance of {\i halacha l\u8217'moshe misinai} creating a distinction between causing {\i tumah} and obligating the {\i nazir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Types Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Everything on this first list causes {\i tumah} through all three modes: {\i maga} \u8212- touching, {\i masa} \u8212- carrying, and {\i ahel} \u8212- tenting. The {\i mishnah} states this explicitly: {\i v\u8217'al maga\u8217'an v\u8217'al masa\u8217'an v\u8217'al ohilan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second List: Bones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then pivots to a second, shorter list: {\i v\u8217'al etzem k\u8217'se\u8217'ora} \u8212- even a chip of bone as small as a barleycorn. This tiny fragment causes {\i tumat meit}, but only through {\i al maga\u8217'o v\u8217'al masa\u8217'o} \u8212- touching and carrying \u8212- not through {\i ahel}. This is the principal asymmetry in the laws of {\i tumat atzamot}: bone fragments of a certain mass cause {\i tumat ohel} \u8212- either {\i rov minyan} or {\i rov binyan} with the requisite volume, or the skull or spine \u8212- but for {\i tumat maga}, even the most minuscule fragment suffices. A barleycorn-sized chip of bone, therefore, causes {\i tumat meit} through contact, obligating the {\i nazir} to shave, even though it would never cause {\i tumat ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When any of these {\i tumot} apply, the {\i nazir} shaves his head, has the {\i mei chatat} \u8212- the waters mixed with the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} \u8212- sprinkled upon him on the third and seventh days, and all prior days of {\i nezirut} are nullified: {\i soter es hakodmin}. He does not begin counting anew until he becomes {\i tahor} and brings his {\i korbanot}; only the following day does his fresh count commence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sumchus Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Entry Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i baraita} that establishes the context for the ensuing discussion. After Rabbi Meir passed away \u8212- the {\i gemara} uses the term {\i petirah}, \u8220"he took his leave,\u8221" which already signals a certain dignity of expression \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda addressed his students: {\i al yikansu talmidei Rabbi Meir l\u8217'chan} \u8212- do not permit Rabbi Meir\u8217's students to enter this {\i beit midrash}. His reasoning: {\i mipnei she\u8217'kantarin hem} \u8212- they are provocateurs \u8212- {\i v\u8217'lo lilmod Torah hen ba\u8217'in} \u8212- they do not come with a sincere desire to learn Torah \u8212- {\i ella l\u8217'kapechani bahalachot hen ba\u8217'in} \u8212- they come only to best me in {\i halachic} debate, using the learning as an intellectual exercise rather than as a pursuit of {\i pesak} and truth. We have all encountered students whose motivation seems to be winning rather than learning; Rabbi Yehuda evidently felt Rabbi Meir\u8217's disciples embodied this tendency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sumchus Enters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nonetheless, {\i dachak Sumchus v\u8217'nichnas} \u8212- Sumchus forced his way in. Sumchus was among Rabbi Meir\u8217's most distinguished students. Once inside, he reported that Rabbi Meir had taught the {\i mishnah} in the following form: {\i al elu tumat hanazir megalei\u8217'ach al hameit v\u8217'al kezayit min hameit} \u8212- explicitly listing both the entire corpse and a {\i kezayit} of the corpse, rather than mentioning only the {\i kezayit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda was incensed. He turned to his students: did I not tell you not to let Rabbi Meir\u8217's students enter? Then he pressed the substantive objection: {\i al kezayit min hameit megalei\u8217'ach al hameit lo kol sheken?} \u8212- if one shaves for a {\i kezayit}, is it not self-evidently true that one shaves for an entire corpse? Why does Rabbi Meir\u8217's version of the {\i mishnah} need to state the entire corpse separately? The redundancy seemed pointless, and the insistence on the fuller formulation struck Rabbi Yehuda as precisely the kind of mind-game he had warned against \u8212- not pure Torah learning but intellectual provocation dressed up as precision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi Defends Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi would not remain silent. He framed his intervention memorably: {\i yomru Meir shachav} \u8212- they will say Meir has gone to his rest \u8212- {\i Yehuda ka\u8217'as} \u8212- Yehuda grew angry \u8212- {\i Yosi shatak} \u8212- and Yosi was silent? {\i Torah mah tehei aleha?} \u8212- what then will become of the Torah? Here we see Rabbi Yosi standing not merely as a defender of Rabbi Meir\u8217's reputation but as a defender of Torah itself. Rabbi Meir\u8217's teaching cannot be dismissed as mere provocation; it must be understood and vindicated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi offers the first explanation: {\i lo nitzrecha ella meit she\u8217'ein alav kezayit basar} \u8212- the separate mention of the entire corpse is necessary for the case of a dead body that has no remaining flesh. A skeleton stripped entirely of flesh does not have a {\i kezayit} of {\i basar}; yet the entire body, the full corpse, still causes {\i tumah}. Were the {\i mishnah} to list only the {\i kezayit} of flesh, this case would be left without explicit coverage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back: but the {\i mishnah} also mentions a limb from the corpse, and we know that {\i eiver} causes {\i tumah} \u8212- so {\i kal vachomer}, if even for a single limb one shaves, surely for an entire body? Rabbi Yosi\u8217's answer does not fully resolve the redundancy on its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Stillbirth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan provides a more precisely targeted answer: {\i lo nitzrecha ella nefel shelo nitkashu evarav begidin} \u8212- the separate listing of the entire corpse is needed for a {\i nefel}, a stillborn or miscarried fetus, whose sinews have not yet developed. Without {\i gidin}, the {\i halacha} of {\i eiver} \u8212- limb \u8212- does not apply, since a {\i halachic} limb requires the connective structure of sinews. And such a {\i nefel} may be so small and undeveloped that it also lacks a {\i kezayit} of flesh. It may furthermore lack the skeletal volume needed for {\i rov minyan} or {\i rov binyan}. Yet it is a complete {\i meit}, and as such the separate mention of the entire corpse is necessary to include it. It remains somewhat unclear why the {\i shidra} and {\i gulgollet} \u8212- the spine and skull \u8212- also fail to apply in this case, though perhaps the assumption is that at such early stages of development these structures have not yet formed adequately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Skeleton Volume\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers yet another explanation: {\i lo nitzrecha ela lerov binyano ulerov minyano she\u8217'ein bahen rova atzamot} \u8212- the listing is needed for a case where one has {\i rov minyan} and {\i rov binyan} \u8212- a majority of the skeletal structure in both number and form \u8212- but the total volume does not reach the minimum of {\i rova kav} (or, more precisely for the {\i nazir}, {\i chatzi kav}) required to impose the shaving obligation. Even with a complete skeletal majority, if the body is so small \u8212- perhaps a very young fetus \u8212- that its bones lack the minimum volume, the {\i nazir} would not otherwise be obligated from any of the other categories. The separate mention of the entire corpse thus covers this gap. Rava\u8217's mention of {\i rova atzamot} here is likely not a precise reference to the {\i nazir}\u8217's threshold of {\i chatzi kav} but rather the general minimum for {\i tumat meit}, since his point is about {\i tumat meit} in general. The underlying principle, however, confirmed by {\i Tosafot}\u8217's earlier analysis, is that {\i rov minyan} and {\i rov binyan} are not independent of volume requirements \u8212- even a complete majority of bones, uncrushed and whole, fails to cause {\i tumat ohel} if it lacks the minimum quantity. This intersection of structural majority and volumetric threshold is fundamental.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Terminology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing over an aspect of the language in this {\i gemara} that is easy to overlook. The discussion began with the word {\i petirah} \u8212- \u8220"he took his leave\u8221" \u8212- and Rabbi Yosi\u8217's protest employed {\i shachav} \u8212- \u8220"he has gone to lie down.\u8221" Both are deliberate euphemisms, dignified circumlocutions that resist the objectifying language that necessarily dominates the surrounding {\i halachic} discussion of body parts and minimum quantities. There is something instructive in the contrast: the {\i halacha} demands precision about flesh and bone and volume, but the {\i gemara} simultaneously insists on a more humane register when speaking of the person who died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One participant made a perceptive observation about Rabbi Yosi\u8217's formulation \u8212- {\i Meir shachav, Yehuda ka\u8217'as, Yosi shatak} \u8212- pointing out that by framing everything in terms of what people will say about the named personalities, Rabbi Yosi is actually critiquing the very dynamic he is interrupting: everyone has become focused on the individuals and their reputations rather than on the Torah being taught. His exclamation {\i Torah mah tehei aleha} is a deliberate reorientation, pulling the discussion back from personalities to the substance of what matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same {\i gemara} also appears in {\i Kiddushin} 52b, where its original literary context may be clearer. There the {\i mishnah} discusses what objects a man may use to effect {\i kiddushin}, including {\i basar kodshei kodashim} \u8212- meat of the most sacred offerings, which has monetary value even if the woman cannot personally consume it. Rabbi Meir\u8217's version of that {\i mishnah} prompted Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's complaint in strikingly similar terms; the scenario of performing {\i kiddushin} with meat while standing in the {\i Azarah} struck Rabbi Yehuda as an absurd theoretical exercise rather than practical {\i halacha}. The {\i gemara} there then offers a series of answers for how a woman might legitimately be in the {\i Azarah} \u8212- through a {\i shliach}, through her father accepting on her behalf, and finally in a flourish that mirrors the opening narrative, {\i dachakah v\u8217'nichnesah mai} \u8212- perhaps she simply forced her way in, just as Sumchus had forced his way into the {\i beit midrash}. The literary symmetry is unmistakable, and that context illuminates what it means to be a {\i kantran}: not a troublemaker in the crude sense, but one who treats {\i halacha} as a vehicle for intellectual jousting rather than for arriving at the truth.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Netzeil Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Netzeil Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's mention of {\i netzeil} to define it precisely. {\i Netzeil} is flesh of a corpse that has liquefied and subsequently congealed: {\i basar hameit shekarash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} offers two variations. The first is flesh that liquefied naturally and recongealed on its own. The second is {\i mohel she\u8217'hirtiach} \u8212- some type of liquid that ran off from the flesh and then was boiled and congealed. These may represent two distinct physical processes: in the first, it is the flesh itself that dissolved; in the second, it is more the juices or fluids that separated from the flesh and then, through heat, solidified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} poses a fundamental question about the first case. {\i Ilima d\u8217'lo yadiana d\u8217'didei hu} \u8212- if we simply find some congealed mass and do not know its origin, does the fact of its congealing tell us anything? At first glance, one might ask: if we do not know whether this material came from the flesh or from some other bodily liquid, then congealing alone proves nothing. But the {\i gemara} probes further: suppose we do know it came from the body \u8212- {\i ela dyadinan d\u8217'dideihu} \u8212- why then should it matter whether it congealed or not? Once we know the material is dissolved flesh, it should cause the same {\i tumah} as the flesh itself regardless of its physical state. Dissolved flesh, one might think, is still flesh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Congealing Indicator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiyah resolves the matter: {\i bistam} \u8212- in a case of uncertainty about the liquid\u8217's identity. The {\i halacha} regarding {\i netzeil} applies where we do not know whether the liquid before us derived from the flesh or from other bodily secretions such as saliva or mucus, {\i keicho v\u8217'nie\u8217'o}, which are not themselves sources of {\i tumat meit}. In such a case, congealing serves as an indicator of identity: {\i ei karash, mohel hu} \u8212- if it congeals, it derives from the flesh and carries the {\i tumah} of a {\i kezayit} of the corpse; {\i lo karash, dilma keicho v\u8217'nie\u8217'o hu} \u8212- if it does not congeal, it may be spit or mucus and does not carry that {\i tumah}. The congealing, then, is not a condition of the {\i tumah} itself but a diagnostic tool for determining the substance\u8217's origin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Tests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} extends this analysis to the second variant \u8212- {\i mohel she\u8217'hirtiach}, the liquid that boiled. There too, {\i Tosafot} explains, we are dealing with a case of uncertain identity: {\i dyadinan d\u8217'ba mizeh hameit aval lo yadinan mohel zeh mibasaro o mikeicho v\u8217'nie\u8217'o} \u8212- we know the liquid came from the body but not whether it originated from the flesh or from other secretions. If upon heating the liquid it boils vigorously \u8212- {\i hirtiach v\u8217'hadar rasach} \u8212- that indicates it derives from the flesh and is therefore {\i tamei}. If it does not exhibit this behavior, it is presumably saliva or mucus and is {\i tahor}. The underlying logic of the two versions is thus parallel: whether congealing or boiling, these are diagnostic tests to establish identity, not conditions of the {\i tumah} itself. One might note that the {\i gemara} does not itself address the {\i hirtiach} case directly; it is {\i Tosafot} who fills in the parallel reasoning.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Animal Neveilah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Netzeil for Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye poses a question to Rava: {\i yeish netzeil l\u8217'beheimah o ein netzeil l\u8217'beheimah} \u8212- does the principle of {\i netzeil} extend to animal carcasses for the purposes of {\i tumat neveilah}? In the preceding discussion, {\i netzeil} functioned within the framework of {\i tumat meit} \u8212- the unique {\i tumah} associated with human corpses, relevant to the {\i nazir} and to {\i kohanim}. But {\i netzeil} might alternatively be understood as a general definitional statement about flesh: dissolved flesh retains the {\i halachic} identity of flesh. If so, that principle could apply equally to {\i tumat neveilah}, the {\i tumah} conveyed by the carcass of an animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Human vs.\u160 ?General\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two approaches present themselves. One holds that the {\i netzeil} principle was received as a {\i halacha} specifically for {\i tumat meit} \u8212- {\i gemiri netzeil d\u8217'asi me\u8217'adam} \u8212- and does not extend beyond that domain. The other holds that it reflects a general definition of flesh that applies wherever flesh is the operative {\i halachic} category, including {\i tumat neveilah} \u8212- {\i lo shena}, it makes no difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consumption Fitness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that whether this question has practical significance depends on a prior dispute. {\i Hanicha leman d\u8217'amar tumah chamura ad lager v\u8217'tumah kala ad lekelev shapir} \u8212- the question is only live according to the view that distinguishes between a {\i tumah chamura}, whose continued force depends on the substance remaining fit for human consumption, and {\i tumah kala}, whose continued force requires only that the substance remain fit for a dog.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle for {\i tumat neveilah} is derived from the verse {\i lager asher bish\u8217'arecha titnenah v\u8217'achalah} \u8212- you may not eat this {\i neveilah}, give it to the stranger in your gates and let him eat it. From here the tradition derives that {\i haraui lager karui neveilah v\u8217'she\u8217'eino raui lager eino karui neveilah} \u8212- what is fit for human consumption is called {\i neveilah}; what is not fit for human consumption is not called {\i neveilah} and loses its {\i tumah} status. This is {\i tumah chamura ad lager}: the weighty {\i tumah} of the {\i neveilah} as an {\i av hatumah} endures only so long as the carcass is fit for a human being to eat. The mention of {\i tumah kala ad lekelev} \u8212- that a lesser {\i tumah}, such as the capacity of a food substance to conduct {\i tumah} from another source, may persist even when the substance is only fit for dogs \u8212- is parenthetical to the main question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i tumah chamura} requires fitness for human consumption, then the liquefied juices running off a decomposing animal carcass are presumably not fit for human consumption, and the question of whether they retain the {\i halachic} identity of flesh is moot \u8212- they have already lost their status as {\i neveilah} regardless. But if one holds that {\i tumah chamura} requires only fitness for a dog \u8212- {\i tumah chamura ad lekelev} \u8212- then the juices, which might well be consumable by a dog, could retain their status. In that case, Abaye\u8217's question becomes pressing: do they retain the identity of flesh for {\i neveilah} purposes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liquefied vs.\u160 ?Decomposed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is introduced as potential evidence: {\i himchehu ba\u8217'or tamei bachama tahor} \u8212- if a {\i neveilah} was liquefied by placing it over fire, it remains {\i tamei}; if it decomposed by being left in the sun, it becomes {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Decomposition Results\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: if {\i tumah chamura} extends to whatever is fit for a dog, then even the sun-decomposed matter should remain {\i tamei}, since surely it is still fit for a dog? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i eimat matchilei, basar de\u8217'astrich} \u8212- when does the {\i neveilah} liquefy in the sun? Only after it has putrefied. And once it has putrefied, {\i bechama kivan de\u8217'astrich havei lei afar} \u8212- it becomes like dust, no longer fit even for a dog. The sun-decomposition case therefore does not resolve the question about juices that remain fit for a dog, because the {\i baraita}\u8217's case involves matter that has crossed the threshold into unfitness for any consumption whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion, then, is that whether the liquefied juices of an animal carcass carry the {\i tumah} of {\i neveilah} remains unresolved. If one holds that {\i tumah chamura} requires human consumption fitness, the question is moot \u8212- the juices fail that test. If one holds that fitness for a dog suffices, the question turns on whether the liquids carry the definitional identity of flesh, and on that the {\i gemara} leaves us without a definitive answer.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Niztok Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pouring Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i mishnah} from {\i masechta Machshirin}: {\i kol hanitzok tahor} \u8212- all instances of pouring liquids remain {\i tahor}. The operative question is whether a liquid being poured constitutes a single halachic unit connecting the source vessel to the point of contact. If, for example, one pours water from a vessel onto a {\i neveilah}, the water that physically contacts the carcass becomes {\i tamei}. Does the act of pouring render the water still in the vessel a single object with the water that touched the {\i neveilah}, thereby making the contents of the vessel {\i tamei} as well? The answer of the {\i mishnah} is no \u8212- {\i kol hanitzok tahor} \u8212- the flow of liquid does not create a halachic connection. Only what actually contacts the {\i tamei} object becomes {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exceptions involve extremely thick substances: {\i midvash haziphim} \u8212- the very thick honey of the {\i ziphim} \u8212- and {\i hatzapichit} \u8212- what may be the dense cakes of honey from the honeycomb \u8212- substances so viscous they function more like solids than liquids, and which are therefore treated as connected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} extends the category further: {\i af hamikpa shel gerisin v\u8217'shel pol} \u8212- even a very thick porridge of crushed legumes is considered connected, {\i mipnei shehi soledet leachoreha} \u8212- because it pulls back. The criterion articulated by {\i Beit Shammai} is {\i soledet}: when you tilt or interrupt the flow of such a viscous substance, it does not simply fall away completely \u8212- some of it adheres and pulls back toward the source. This quality of adhesion or retraction is the basis for treating it as a single unit. The term {\i soledet} appears also in the context of {\i yad soledet bo} \u8212- the threshold temperature for {\i halachic} cooking \u8212- where the sense of recoiling from heat is similarly the governing intuition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niztok & Foods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} uses this {\i mishnah} as a springboard to ask: {\i yeish nitzok le\u8217'ochlin o ein nitzok le\u8217'ochlin} \u8212- does the concept of {\i nitzok} apply to foods as well as to liquids? That is, if one takes a food substance that has been liquefied \u8212- pulverized or processed into a thick, flowing mass \u8212- and pours it onto a {\i tamei} source, is the material in the vessel considered connected to what has touched the {\i tamei} object?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thick Foods Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question turns on why the thick honey qualifies as connected according to the {\i chachamim} and why {\i Beit Shammai} extends the principle to thick porridge. Two distinct rationales are possible. The first \u8212- {\i mishum de\u8217'it behu riri} \u8212- is that the relevant property is the presence of {\i ririn}, sticky stringy components that adhere and pull back, creating the physical connection that underlies the {\i halachic} unity. The second \u8212- {\i mishum desmichin} \u8212- is that sheer thickness alone is sufficient, even without the adhesive quality of {\i ririn}. For {\i Beit Shammai} the answer is clear: the criterion is {\i soledet}, the pulling-back quality, which is precisely the {\i riri} factor. But the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's inclusion of honey could reflect either view \u8212- honey is both thick and sticky, so it is consistent with either rationale.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fat Liquefaction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fat Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is now introduced: {\i chelev hameit shehu shalem, vehiticho, tamei} \u8212- the fat of a corpse, when whole \u8212- meaning present in the minimum quantity required for {\i tumah} \u8212- and then liquefied over fire, remains {\i tamei}. {\i Haya meforad vehiticho, tahor} \u8212- if the fat was fragmented below the minimum and was then liquefied and reassembled, it is {\i tahor}. The second ruling reflects the general question of whether {\i tumah} can be reacquired once a substance has fallen below the minimum threshold through physical separation; the {\i baraita} assumes it cannot, at least for purposes of {\i tumat maga}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liquids Not Combined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first ruling, however, raises a pointed question. We have just established that {\i kol hanitzok tahor} \u8212- liquid is not considered a unified connected object. If so, when one liquefies the fat of a corpse, the result is a liquid that, by this principle, is not a single unit. The person who touches part of the liquefied fat is not touching the full minimum quantity. Why, then, does it remain {\i tamei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Container Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely surprising implication. The {\i nitzok} principle is typically discussed in the context of liquid being actively poured, and one\u8217's intuition might be that liquid sitting stably in a container constitutes a single unit. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis here suggests that the principle of liquid not being combined applies even to liquid at rest in a vessel \u8212- the liquid at the bottom of the container and the liquid at the top are not, in {\i halachic} terms, a single object. That being so, how does liquefied fat retain its {\i tumah} status?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recongealing Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira, together with the son of Ravina, offers a resolution: {\i anah u\u8217'mar brai d\u8217'Ravina targumina} \u8212- we explained this together. {\i Kegon d\u8217'bahadi d\u8217'martach lei, salik amuda d\u8217'nura l\u8217'puma d\u8217'mana, v\u8217'karash} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is speaking of a case where, in the very process of liquefying the fat over fire, the flame reached the opening of the vessel and caused the contents to recongeal partially. Thus the fat was never fully in a liquid, uncombined state \u8212- at the moment of its liquefaction, heat simultaneously caused recongealing. This allows the fat to remain {\i tamei} because it retained something of its solid, unified character even during the cooking process. This answer departs from the {\i p\u8217'shat} of the {\i baraita}, which simply describes the fat as having been liquefied, but it is the explanation offered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stickiness Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina raises the question to Rav Ashi, returning to the unresolved matter of {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's criterion. {\i Beit Shammai} says: {\i af hamikpa shel grisin v\u8217'shel pul, mimeshehen sollin leachoreih} \u8212- the porridge is connected {\i because} it pulls back. This clearly makes the {\i soledet} quality \u8212- the {\i riri} factor \u8212- the operative criterion. But the question about the {\i tanna kamma} remains: when the {\i tanna kamma} includes honey, is that because honey has {\i ririn}, or simply because it is thick? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i midi irya \u8212- hasam mishum desmichin, hacha mishum riri} \u8212- these are two different considerations; {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's criterion is {\i riri}, but the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's criterion may be {\i smichut}, thickness alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus concludes without resolving the question. For {\i Beit Shammai}, the stickiness factor \u8212- {\i riri}, the adhesive pulling-back quality \u8212- is clearly the determinative criterion. But for the {\i tanna kamma}, whose exemplar is honey (which is both thick and sticky), it cannot be determined whether thickness alone suffices without the {\i riri} factor. The case of a substance that is very thick but not sticky \u8212- lacking {\i ririn} \u8212- therefore remains a {\i teiku} in the {\i gemara}: an unresolved question as to whether such a substance is considered combined through {\i nitzok} or not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 52\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Incomplete Animals & Bir\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Ant Limb\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} for today is 52. We pick up at the bottom of 51b, where the {\i Gemara} has been engaged with the topic of {\i rekev} \u8212- the dust produced by a decomposed corpse \u8212- and the narrow circumstances under which it generates {\i tumah}: primarily, a naked body in a marble casket, along with any additional criteria the {\i Gemara} may impose. Three lines from the bottom of 51b, the {\i Gemara} introduces a question posed by Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bei Rava} \u8212- Rava asked: if you have an ant ({\i nemalah}) that is missing a limb, yet remains viable, what is the {\i halacha}? This question arises from the concept of {\i biryah} \u8212- the principle that consuming an entire creature, even one smaller than a {\i kezayit}, carries the same legal weight as consuming a {\i kezayit} of a forbidden substance, because the integrity of a complete entity constitutes a qualitative substitute for the quantitative {\i shiur}. The question is whether this qualitative status holds when the ant is missing a leg but can still survive, or whether the {\i biryah} status requires, in the end, translation into a quantitative measure, such that any deficiency renders it incomplete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More precisely, Rava\u8217's question is: {\i shiura gmarina lei} \u8212- do we ultimately translate the {\i biryah} into a fixed quantity (the volume of the whole ant), so that anything less than that full volume is insufficient? Or do we say {\i biryah gmarina lei} \u8212- that as long as the creature remains a viable, living entity, it retains its qualitative {\i biryah} status regardless of a missing limb?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biryah & Quantity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that the law of {\i biryah} applies to prohibitions of eating, not to the {\i shiurim} for {\i tumah}. Nevertheless, the underlying conceptual question \u8212- whether {\i biryah} is an irreducibly qualitative standard or whether it ultimately resolves into a quantitative measure \u8212- is what Rava is probing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proceeds to seek a resolution. {\i Amar Rav Yehuda miDiskharta} \u8212- Rav Yehuda of Diskharta says: {\i ta shema}, come and hear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheretz & Lentil\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the domain of {\i tumah}, specifically to the {\i sheretz} \u8212- a creeping creature whose carcass conveys impurity. Two {\i pesukim} are at play: one says {\i kol asher yiga bahem} (\u8220"whatever touches them\u8221"), suggesting contact with the entire creature; the other says {\i kol asher yipol mehem} (\u8220"whatever falls from them\u8221"), suggesting that even a part suffices. The {\i Gemara} resolves this tension: {\i bahem} implies the whole, {\i mehem} implies a part \u8212- but the resolution is that one must touch {\i miktzatan shehu kekulan}, a portion that is representative of the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How is a part deemed representative of the whole? {\i Veshi\u8217'eru hachamim ke\u8217'adasha} \u8212- the sages established the measure as the size of a lentil bean. The reasoning: {\i shekein hachomet techilat briato ke\u8217'adasha} \u8212- the {\i chomet} (identified variously as a snail or a type of limbless lizard) is the size of a lentil bean at the very beginning of its formation. This smallest of the {\i shratzim}, at the moment of its initial creation, is the size of a lentil. Therefore, the minimum quantity that can represent an entire {\i sheretz} is the size of a lentil bean \u8212- a piece of a dead mouse of that size renders one {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then draws a conclusion: {\i shema mina shiura gemiri lah} \u8212- we see that it is a matter of {\i shiurim}, not of {\i biryah}. Even where one begins with the concept of something that represents the whole, one ultimately arrives at a fixed quantitative number. The conceptual point is significant: even though the {\i sheretz} analysis does not work precisely the same way as the {\i biryah} analysis for eating (which is case-by-case, ant by ant, rather than a universal lentil-bean standard), the {\i Gemara}\u8217's direction is clear \u8212- we seek to quantify. The {\i biryah} status, rather than remaining an open-ended qualitative assessment, gets translated into a number: the volume of the complete creature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Full Creature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} reads the proof somewhat differently. Two lines into the narrow commentary at the top of the {\i daf}, {\i Tosafot} writes: {\i bishlama shiura gemiri midba\u8217'inan ke\u8217'adasha} \u8212- since we require the size of a lentil bean, we see it is about {\i shiur}. Why? Because when we derived the lentil-bean measure, we derived it from the {\i chomet} in its full form, with its legs and horns intact. Even though one could theoretically cut a small piece of the {\i chomet} that would leave it lacking a leg and still capable of surviving, we do not say the {\i shiur} is the minimum size at which a {\i chomet} could live. Rather, {\i ba\u8217'inan ke\u8217'adasha mamash keviyata} \u8212- we require the full size of a complete {\i chomet}. This shows that the operative standard is the full creature, not a reduced creature that might still be viable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In contrast to {\i Tosafot}\u8217's emphasis, one might stress the more conceptual point: the {\i Gemara} shows that we consistently seek to translate the {\i biryah} concept into a fixed quantity, and that once we do so, any deficiency from that quantity is disqualifying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Soul & Shiur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} is not yet finished. {\i Amar Rav Shemaya}: {\i ki ba\u8217'inan shiura} \u8212- when do we need a {\i shiur} in the case of the {\i chomet} and the lentil-bean measure? {\i Devalo hachi ke\u8217'adasha lo metamei dela nafla beh neshama} \u8212- because without the lentil-bean size, the {\i chomet} would not yet convey {\i tumah}, since a soul has not yet entered into it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is somewhat cryptic, but {\i Tosafot} explains as follows: even though it is true that an animal can be missing a limb and still survive, this is only the case after it has been fully born. If the baby {\i chomet} were to be born without a leg, it would be born smaller than the size of a lentil \u8212- too small to be viable at all. Therefore, the reason we do not consider a sub-lentil-size {\i chomet} \u8212- one that might theoretically be missing a leg \u8212- is that no such viable creature exists before the soul has entered into it. However, if a {\i chomet} or ant was already born, already living, and then lost a limb, it remains viable. Whether such a living creature with a missing limb retains its {\i biryah} status for the purposes of the prohibition of eating \u8212- that question, Rav Shemaya implies, remains open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper conceptual point is worth appreciating: normally we think of a quantitative {\i shiur} as what lends qualitative significance to an act. But here the {\i Gemara} is working from the opposite direction \u8212- {\i biryah} is a qualitative substitute for the quantitative significance of a {\i kezayit}, and the {\i Gemara} asks whether that qualitative status is always anchored to a fixed quantity, or whether it can float contextually depending on the individual creature in front of us.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Backbone & Skull\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro Backbone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We turn now to a related but distinct domain: the backbone ({\i hashidra}) and the skull ({\i hagulgoles}) as sources of {\i tumah}. These are bone-based sources, and bone operates differently from flesh in the laws of {\i tumah}. A small piece of bone \u8212- the size of a barley grain \u8212- conveys {\i tumah} through touch, but to convey {\i tumat ohel} (impurity under a shared roof), one needs enough bone to represent the body in a more substantial way. Two primary {\i shiurim} govern {\i tumat ohel}: either {\i rov binyano} (majority of the body\u8217's structure) or {\i rov minyano} (majority of the body\u8217's bone count), and even without meeting those thresholds, a {\i rova} or {\i chatzi kav} of bones from such a significant collection will cause {\i tumat ohel}. The backbone and skull constitute a separate category \u8212- the question before us is whether you need both together or whether either one alone suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Backbone No Ribs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Iba\u8217'ya lehu} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} raises the question: when the {\i mishna} says {\i shidra ve-gulgoles}, does it mean the backbone and the skull together, or the backbone or the skull, either one independently? This is not merely a linguistic question about the {\i vav} in Hebrew; it is a substantive {\i halachic} question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava ta shema} \u8212- come and hear. {\i Shidra shegirer rov ila\u8217'in sheba, tehora} \u8212- a backbone from which most of the ribs have been broken off is {\i tahor}; it does not convey {\i tumat ohel}. {\i Uvakever, afilu meshuberes o meforekes temea mipnei hakever} \u8212- but if it is in a grave, even if it is broken or dismembered, it remains {\i tamei} because of the {\i kever} itself. This is a {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} \u8212- the grave is treated as combining the fragments into a single unified object, even when they are broken, and rendering the contents {\i tamei}. The {\i chiddush} is twofold: first, that a backbone without most of its ribs is no longer deemed a backbone for purposes of {\i tumah}; second, that the grave overrides this and causes {\i tumah} nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both or Either?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i Gemara} asks what this teaches about our question. {\i Ta\u8217'ama mishum degirei} \u8212- the reason the backbone without ribs is {\i tahor} is because the ribs are broken off. {\i Ha lo girei tamei} \u8212- if not, it would be {\i tamei}. This implies that the backbone alone \u8212- with its ribs intact \u8212- suffices to cause {\i tumat ohel}, even without the skull.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} deflects: {\i halo katani} \u8212- did we actually say that? Perhaps the passage is only telling us what happens when the ribs are broken. {\i Dechi girei tahara itztreicha lach} \u8212- what the passage needed to tell us is that when the ribs are broken, it is {\i tahor}. Whether this means the backbone alone (with ribs) is enough, or whether the skull is also needed, is not determined by this ruling. Perhaps even if you need both the backbone and the skull, once the backbone loses most of its ribs it is no longer halachically a backbone, and so the {\i tumah} fails \u8212- but that does not tell us whether an intact backbone alone suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion then turns to the story of Dustai the doctor ({\i Todos HaRofei}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dustai Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabi Yehuda omer: shisha devarim Rabbi Akiva metamei ve-Chachamim mitaherin} \u8212- there were six matters in which Rabbi Akiva held something to be {\i tamei} and the {\i chachamim} held it {\i tahor} with respect to {\i tumat meis}, and Rabbi Akiva eventually reversed himself on most of them. The occasion for identifying these six was a famous episode:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Boxes filled with bones were brought to the {\i beit knesset} of the metalworkers \u8212- the craftsmen did not want to bring them inside, lest the {\i kohanim} present become {\i tamei}, so they placed the boxes in the open air of the courtyard. This episode itself illustrates an important feature of the {\i beit knesset} in the {\i Tannaic} period: it served as a communal gathering space ({\i beit am}), not merely as a place of prayer \u8212- which is why the doctors and rabbis convened there. {\i Venichnas Todos HaRofei vekhol harofim imo} \u8212- Todos the doctor came with all the other physicians, and they declared: {\i ein kan shidra mimeit echad} \u8212- there is not here a backbone coming from a single corpse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point of the doctors\u8217' testimony was that even if the bones collectively constituted a quantity sufficient for {\i tumah}, they came from multiple bodies. This is directly relevant to the debate between Rabbi Akiva and the {\i chachamim} about whether bones from multiple corpses can be combined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Bone Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially reads this episode as resolving our question about backbone and skull: {\i ta\u8217'ama deleka shidra michada} \u8212- the reason it was {\i tahor} is that there was no backbone from a single body. {\i Ha ika o shidra o gulgoles michada} \u8212- if there had been a single backbone or a single skull from one body, {\i denazir megale\u8217'ach alei} \u8212- it would have been sufficient for a {\i nazir} to shave, and would have caused {\i tumat ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This would seem to confirm: {\i shema mina o shidra o gulgoles} \u8212- it is either one or the other that suffices, not both together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} again deflects with the formula of {\i lo miba\u8217'ya ka\u8217'amar}: not only was there no backbone-and-skull together from one corpse, there was not even a backbone alone from one corpse, nor a skull alone. The case was simply one of total absence. We cannot infer from the doctors\u8217' statement what would have been ruled had there been an intact backbone \u8212- whether that alone would have sufficed, or whether the skull would also have been required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The simple reading of all these {\i braitot} supports the \u8220"either one\u8221" reading, but the {\i Gemara} consistently rebuts each proof, maintaining that the question remains open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Bodies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Cases Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now quotes the passage that lists the six matters in dispute. {\i U-ma hen shisha devarim she-Rabbi Akiva metamei ve-Chachamim metaherin} \u8212- what are the six things that Rabbi Akiva declared {\i tamei} and the {\i chachamim} declared {\i tahor}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limbs & Blood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first: {\i al ever min ha-mes she-ba mi-shnei mesim} \u8212- a limb assembled from parts of two separate corpses (e.g., the top of a finger from one body and the bottom from another). This is the most striking case \u8212- a limb would seem to derive its significance from its integrity as an entity, and combining parts from two bodies would seem to undercut that integrity entirely. Yet Rabbi Akiva held it {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second: {\i al ever min ha-chai she-ba mi-shnei bnei adam} \u8212- a limb from a living person composed from two separate living individuals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third: {\i al chatzi kav atzamot she-ba mi-shnei mesim} \u8212- a half-{\i kav} of bones from two separate corpses. Rabbi Akiva held that such bones, even from two different bodies, combine to cause {\i tumah}; the {\i chachamim} disagreed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth: {\i al reviet dam ha-ba\u8217'ah mi-shnayim} \u8212- a {\i reviet} (approximately three to four ounces, the same measure used for {\i kiddush}) of blood from two corpses. Here {\i Tosafot} notes a subtle distinction: the standard for a {\i nazir}\u8217's obligation to shave is {\i chatzi kav} of bones and {\i chatzi log} of blood, while the standard for {\i tumat meis} more generally is {\i reviet kav} of bones and {\i reviet log} of blood. The {\i braita} mentions {\i chatzi kav} of bones (the {\i nazir} standard) but then mentions {\i reviet} of blood (the {\i tumat meis} standard) \u8212- a slight asymmetry worth noting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Split Barley Bone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fifth: {\i al etzem k\u8217'se\u8217'orah she-nechelak li-shnayim} \u8212- a bone the size of a barley grain that has been split into two pieces. This case is notably different from the others: it does not involve bones from two separate bodies, but rather a single bone divided into two. It also differs in type \u8212- this is a case of {\i tumat maga} (impurity through contact) rather than {\i tumat ohel}. One might understand the ruling: a barley-grain-sized bone is already the minimum for contact-based {\i tumah}, and dividing it into two might cause each piece to fall below any meaningful threshold, even if they are placed back together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Count Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question of enumeration: listing both backbone-and-skull and the above cases would produce seven, not six. The {\i Gemara} offers several answers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First: {\i kol heicha de-palgi alei rabbim} \u8212- when we count six, we mean six cases in which {\i rabbim}, many sages, argue against Rabbi Akiva. {\i L\u8217'afukei etzem ka\u8217'siora} \u8212- excluding the split barley bone, because there it is only Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri who disagrees with Rabbi Akiva, not the {\i chachamim} collectively. The core dispute of Rabbi Akiva with the {\i chachamim} is about combining from two separate bodies \u8212- and the barley-bone case, involving division of a single bone, does not fit that pattern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second alternative: {\i ki tani eiver min hamet eiver min hachai lo katani} \u8212- when we count six cases related to corpses, {\i ever min ha-chai} (a limb from a living person) was not being counted, since it is not a matter of {\i tumat meis} per se.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third alternative: perhaps {\i ever min ha-mes} and {\i ever min ha-chai} are treated as a single combined case, since they are closely related.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fourth alternative: {\i ki tani kol heicha denazir migale\u8217'ach al achila} \u8212- we are counting cases that involve {\i tumat ohel} and {\i nazir megale\u8217'ach}, which excludes {\i etzem ka\u8217'siora} since that is only {\i tumat maga}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fifth: {\i ki tani kol heicha de-hadar beih} \u8212- the six refer to those cases on which Rabbi Akiva ultimately reversed himself. Though seven are listed, he reversed himself on only six \u8212- not on the {\i reviet} of blood. That brings us to the next discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-hadar bo Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva reversed himself on most of these positions. But on the {\i reviet} of blood from two bodies, he did not reverse himself. One can appreciate why: blood from different bodies is naturally indistinguishable. Todos the doctor could look at bones and determine that they came from different individuals, but blood \u8212- before blood typing was possible \u8212- would have appeared as a single homogeneous mass. Rabbi Akiva therefore held firm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De\u8217'amar lei Rebbi le-Bar Kappara: lo tish\u8217'na reviet dam be-chazara} \u8212- Rebbi told Bar Kappara not to include {\i reviet dam} in the list of reversals. {\i She-hi limuda she-Rabbi Akiva beyado} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva kept his teaching firmly in hand. {\i Ve-od hamikra mesaye\u8217'o} \u8212- and furthermore, a {\i pasuk} supports him: {\i ve-al kol nafshot met lo yavo} \u8212- the word {\i nafshot} is in the plural, and given that {\i ki hadam hu hanafesh} \u8212- blood is the life \u8212- the plural implies that blood from multiple persons of the dead (in the plural) still generates {\i tumah}. This {\i drasha} grounds Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position in a {\i pasuk}, which is also why the {\i braita} mentions {\i reviet dam} (the {\i tumat ohel} measure) rather than {\i chatzi log} (the {\i nazir} measure): Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i limud} begins with {\i reviet} as the primary quantity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabi Shimon omer: ad yamav haya metame} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon testified that his entire life, Rabbi Akiva held his position on the blood. {\i Im mishemet hazar bo ani lo yodea} \u8212- if after Rabbi Akiva died he reversed himself, Rabbi Shimon says he does not know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then cites a {\i braita}: {\i hushcharu shinav mipnei taniyotav} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon\u8217's teeth turned black from the many fasts he undertook in repentance for having made this irreverent remark (implying that Rabbi Akiva might reverse himself post mortem). The humor of the remark was not lost on the {\i Gemara} itself, which also repeated it \u8212- and apparently both the {\i Gemara} and the audience understood the joke. Nonetheless, Rabbi Shimon recognized it as disrespectful toward his {\i rebbi} and took it seriously enough to fast in penance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Majority Bones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Majority Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a dispute between {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} regarding the {\i rov atzamot} requirement for {\i tumat ohel}. {\i Beit Shammai omrim: rov atzamot min ha\u8217'atzamim o mishnayim u\u8217'mishlosha} \u8212- Beit Shammai holds that the requisite {\i chatzi kav} (or {\i rova}) of bones may come from as few as two or three bones; they need not come from a broad representation of the body\u8217's full skeletal structure. {\i Beit Hillel omrim: rova min hageviya, mi-rov binyano o mi-rov minyano} \u8212- Beit Hillel requires that the bones represent the majority of the body\u8217's structure ({\i rov binyan}) or the majority of its bone count ({\i rov minyan}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Yehoshua: yachol ani la\u8217'asot divrei Beit Shammai ve-divrei Beit Hillel ke-echad} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua claimed he could reconcile the two positions. The apparent contradiction between \u8220"two or three bones\u8221" and \u8220"{\i rov binyan} or {\i rov minyan}\u8221" is not a genuine dispute, he argued.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anatomy Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua explains: when Beit Shammai said \u8220"two or three bones,\u8221" they specifically meant {\i mishtei shokaim ve-yerech echad o mishtei yerechayim ve-shok echad} \u8212- either two calves and one thigh, or two thighs and one calf. This combination of three bones \u8212- taken from the lower limbs \u8212- constitutes {\i rov govho shel adam}, the majority of a person\u8217's height. And this, Rabbi Yehoshua argues, is precisely what Beit Hillel means by {\i rov binyan}: the structural majority of the body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The anatomical reasoning is that the thighs and calves together account for more than half of a person\u8217's standing height. (The pelvis sits slightly higher than the midpoint of the body, so where the thigh attaches is approximately at the midpoint; the combined leg bones below that represent the majority of the height.) Furthermore, not only do the thigh-and-calf bones represent the majority of the body\u8217's height, but taking a majority of those four bones (two thighs and two calves) \u8212- i.e., any three of the four \u8212- represents the majority of the body\u8217's primary structural bones. This is what Beit Shammai meant: not that any random two or three bones suffice, but that these specific two or three from the legs are the bones that constitute {\i rov binyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfot} notes here that the {\i Gemara} in Ohalot assumes {\i rmach evarim} \u8212- 248 limbs \u8212- in a person, citing the principle that {\i kuf kaf hey hem rov minyan}, meaning 125 bones constitute a majority. This figure of 248 {\i evarim} (which likely here means bones rather than full limbs, since 248 discrete limbs would be anatomically impossible) underlies the {\i rov minyan} calculation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for {\i rov minyan}, Beit Hillel\u8217's position is that the majority of the bone count may actually be found concentrated in the small bones of the hands and feet \u8212- the {\i mifarkei yadayim ve-raglayim}. Thus one could have {\i rov minyan} without having {\i rov binyan}, if all the tiny bones of the extremities were present. Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's reconciliation is that Beit Shammai was articulating {\i rov binyan} in anatomical terms, while Beit Hillel was stating the principle more broadly (encompassing both {\i rov binyan} and {\i rov minyan}) \u8212- but both agree that some form of body-representation is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Bone Sammai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now quotes the line that is directly relevant to the backbone-and-skull question: {\i Shammai omer afilu etzem min ha-shidra o min ha-gulgoles} \u8212- Shammai himself (not Beit Shammai collectively, but Shammai) held that even a single bone from the backbone or the skull suffices to cause {\i tumat ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the leniency of Shammai in the context of bone {\i tumah}: whereas Beit Shammai required two or three specific bones ({\i rov binyan}), Shammai went further and said even one bone from the skull or backbone is enough. What then is the opposing position? Presumably, Beit Hillel or the {\i chachamim} would require the whole backbone or skull. But Shammai says even one bone from either of those structures is enough \u8212- an extremely stringent position as to what qualifies, even if the single bone is minimal in size.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to infer what the opposing {\i Rabbinic} position must be: {\i u-lishmeih d\u8217'ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Beit Shammai d\u8217'machmir, hanei Rabbanan ad d\u8217'ika shidra ve-gulgoles} \u8212- if the {\i rabbinic} position were simply requiring both together (the whole backbone and the whole skull), we could read the debate that way. But the {\i Gemara} pushes back: one cannot determine from Shammai\u8217's extreme stringency (accepting even one bone from either structure) what exactly the opposing position demands. The {\i Rabbanan} only disagree about the single bone; where Shammai says one bone is enough, they say you need the whole thing. But do they mean the whole skull {\i or} the whole backbone (either one), or the whole skull {\i and} the whole backbone (both together)? This ambiguity remains unresolved. The evidence consistently leans toward \u8220"either one\u8221" \u8212- all the cases discussed, including the Todos the doctor episode, speak only of finding a single backbone, with no mention of needing a skull as well. But the {\i Gemara} repeatedly declines to accept these as conclusive proofs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, one may consult the {\i Rambam} in {\i Hilchos Tumat Meis}, Perek 8. The {\i Rambam} rules: {\i hashidra ve-hagulgoles, rov binyano ve-rov minyano} \u8212- these are the bones that convey {\i tumah} for a corpse. {\i Shidra sheshleimah harei hi ke-meis shalem} \u8212- a complete backbone is treated like a whole corpse. {\i Gulgoles sheshleimah harei hi ke-meis shalem} \u8212- a complete skull is likewise treated as a whole corpse. The {\i Rambam} thus rules that either one alone suffices \u8212- which is clearly where all the evidence in the {\i Gemara} was pointing, even if the {\i Gemara} itself declined to resolve the question with formal proof.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Backbone Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quarter Kav Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a new question posed by Rami bar Chama. {\i Ba\u8217'ei Rami bar Chama: rov atzamot min ha-shidra ve-ha-gulgoles mai?} \u8212- what is the law if one does not have a whole backbone or skull, but has a quantity of bones derived from the backbone or skull?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand this question properly, one must recall the framework. For {\i tumat ohel}, bones convey {\i tumah} either when they constitute {\i rov binyan} or {\i rov minyan} of the body (or a {\i chatzi kav} / {\i rova kav} coming from such a significant collection), or when one has an entire backbone or skull. Now Rami bar Chama asks: what if one has only a partial backbone \u8212- not the whole thing \u8212- but enough of it to constitute the relevant {\i shiur} of bones? Since the backbone and skull are themselves {\i significant} bone structures (indeed, they are treated as equivalent to a whole corpse when complete), perhaps a {\i shiur} derived from them should carry special weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More specifically, Rami bar Chama frames the question in terms of {\i nazir megale\u8217'ach}: when is a {\i chatzi kav} of bones required? {\i Heicha de-ika mish\u8217'ar evarav} \u8212- normally when the bones come from the general skeletal remains of the body \u8212- its ordinary bones. But since the backbone and skull are of heightened significance ({\i de-chamiri}), such that even a single one causes {\i tumat ohel}, perhaps {\i afilu rova atzamot} \u8212- even a {\i rova kav} (quarter-{\i kav}) of bones derived from the backbone or skull \u8212- should be sufficient for a {\i nazir} to shave, equivalent in weight to the {\i chatzi kav} required from ordinary bones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In other words, Rami bar Chama assumes as his premise that a {\i shiur} of bones from the backbone or skull does convey {\i tumah} even if the backbone or skull is not whole. His question is only whether the {\i shiur} in such a case is reduced to a {\i rova} (due to the enhanced significance of these bones) rather than the standard {\i chatzi kav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava ta shema} \u8212- come and hear. The {\i mishna} that governs the {\i nazir} discusses {\i chatzi kav atzamot} and separately lists {\i shidra ve-gulgoles}. If a {\i rova atzamot} from the backbone or skull were also a distinct operative measure for {\i nazir megale\u8217'ach}, it should have been mentioned in the {\i mishna} alongside these other categories. Its absence suggests that no such reduced {\i shiur} exists: the only quantity-based measure is the {\i chatzi kav}, and the only backbone/skull-based measure is the complete structure. A partial backbone or skull generating {\i tumah} at a reduced {\i shiur} finds no place in the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a complication. Rava himself is reported to have stated in another context: {\i lo nitzrecha ela le-shidra ve-gulgoles she-ein bahem rova atzamot} \u8212- that a ruling was necessary specifically for a case of backbone or skull that does not contain a {\i rova atzamot}. The implication is that even without the requisite {\i rova} quantity, the intact backbone or skull alone causes {\i tumah}. Conversely, if the backbone or skull were large enough to contain a {\i rova}, then the {\i rova} itself would be the operative measure \u8212- not the completeness of the structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 (This statement is formulated in the {\i Gemara} as referring to {\i rova atzamot}, not {\i chatzi kav atzamot} \u8212- which is intriguing, since it seems to align with Rami bar Chama\u8217's suggestion that by backbone and skull, the {\i shiur} is a {\i rova} rather than a {\i chatzi kav}.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an internal tension: Rava used the {\i mishna} to refute the idea of a reduced {\i shiur}, yet his own statement in another context seems to presuppose exactly that reduced {\i shiur}. The resolution proposed is that Rava\u8217's earlier statement was made before he had fully considered Rami bar Chama\u8217's question; after hearing the challenge, he reconsidered and took the position that the {\i mishna\u8217's} silence on the {\i rova} measure from backbone/skull is dispositive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ultimately, the question is left unresolved. The {\i Gemara} cannot confirm whether a partial backbone or skull generates {\i tumah} as a quantity measure, and if so, what that quantity is. Rava\u8217's proof from the {\i mishna} \u8212- that the absence of a {\i rova}-from-backbone category implies no such category exists \u8212- stands as the strongest argument. But Rava\u8217's own earlier statement, which the {\i Gemara} here attributes to a pre-reconsideration position, muddles the picture. The sugya thus concludes in an unresolved state, to be continued.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 53\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Skull & Backbone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Individual Bones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We are in the middle of a discussion concerning the portion of the {\i mishnah} that focuses on the skull and the backbone \u8212- {\i shidra v\u8217'gulgollet}. The first question to establish was whether it is only the two of them combined that are {\i metamei b\u8217'ohel}, or whether each one alone suffices. All the evidence pointed to each alone, and that is the way the Rambam rules.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quantity Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next question concerns cases where one does not have the whole skull or backbone, but has a sufficient quantity of them. We have already learned that a quarter of a {\i kav} \u8212- {\i revi\u8217'it kav} \u8212- of the majority of bones by number ({\i rov minyan}) or by structure ({\i rov binyan}) causes {\i tuma b\u8217'ohel}, and that double that amount, a half-{\i kav}, is the threshold for {\i nazir megaleach}: the {\i nazir} must shave his head and reverse the entire process. These two cases thus present a different threshold for the {\i nazir} to shave versus what causes {\i tuma b\u8217'ohel} in the ordinary sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The assumption, then, is that the same structure applies to {\i shidra v\u8217'gulgollet}: a {\i revi\u8217'it kav} drawn from the skull or backbone \u8212- even if one does not have their entirety \u8212- causes {\i tumas ohel}, and a half-{\i kav} would be the threshold for {\i nazir megaleach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami bar Chama\u8217's Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this assumption, Rami bar Chama raised the following question: since {\i shidra v\u8217'gulgollet} are far weightier than ordinary bones \u8212- there are only two of them and each one alone represents the entire body \u8212- perhaps even a smaller quantity of them should trigger {\i nazir megaleach}. That is, perhaps for {\i nazir megaleach} one does not need the standard half-{\i kav}; perhaps a {\i revi\u8217'it kav} \u8212- the same amount that normally causes {\i tuma} from ordinary bones \u8212- would suffice. This question remains before us as we enter the top of 53a.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shammai\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai: One Bone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a potential proof: {\i ta shema} \u8212- come and hear. Shammai says: {\i etzem echad min hashidra u\u8217'min hagulgollet} \u8212- even one bone from the backbone or from the skull causes {\i tuma} and triggers the {\i nazir}\u8217's obligation to shave. The question is whether we can extrapolate from Shammai\u8217's ruling what the opposing opinion holds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Infer Opposition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} dismisses this attempt: {\i shani Shammai d\u8217'machmir tfei} \u8212- Shammai is simply exceptionally stringent. From his stringency in one direction, one cannot infer the precise contours of the opposing view. The opposing position might hold, first, that you need the whole skull and backbone, and second, that if you have a certain quantity, you need a standard half-{\i kav} \u8212- not conceding any reduced threshold for {\i shidra v\u8217'gulgollet}. Just because Shammai is so strict does not mean the other school agrees to his degree of stringency; they might simply apply the ordinary rule of a half-{\i kav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then attempts to reason in the other direction: perhaps Shammai\u8217's strictness implies that the opposing opinion is as lenient as possible \u8212- requiring the full half-{\i kav}. But the {\i Gemara} rejects this too: {\i dilma ad kan lo pligi rabanan alei d\u8217'Shammai ela b\u8217'etzem echad aval b\u8217'rov atzamot revi\u8217'it kav afilu rabanan modei} \u8212- perhaps the Rabbis disagree with Shammai only regarding a single bone being sufficient, but would agree that a {\i revi\u8217'it kav} of the majority of bones suffices. We simply cannot know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same point was made previously in a different discussion: from Shammai\u8217's position alone, one cannot infer how wide the {\i machloket} is. Shammai is very strict \u8212- even one bone from the skull or backbone. The opposing view might require both skull and backbone together, or might require skull or backbone individually but demand a full half-{\i kav} of their substance, or might concede that a {\i revi\u8217'it kav} suffices. All remain open. The question of whether, for the {\i tuma} relevant to the {\i nazir}, a half-{\i kav} or a {\i revi\u8217'it kav} from {\i shidra v\u8217'gulgollet} is required therefore remains unresolved. The broader lesson is that from one position in a {\i machloket} one cannot infer how wide that {\i machloket} extends.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Historical Shiurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Elders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established this unresolved question, the {\i Gemara} introduces a statement by Rabbi Eliezer that provides historical and conceptual background to the entire doubling of the {\i shiur} for {\i nazir megaleach}. The doubling is admittedly quite strange: why should the threshold for the {\i nazir} shaving be specifically double the amount that causes ordinary {\i tumas ohel}? If this were a universal rule it would be one thing \u8212- but why is it only with bones and blood that the {\i shiur} for {\i nazir megaleach} is double the ordinary {\i shiur} for {\i tumas ohel}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer explains: {\i zekeinim ha-rishonim} \u8212- the original early elders \u8212- were themselves divided. One group among them held {\i chatzi kav atzamot ve-chatzi log dam le-kol}: a half-{\i kav} of bones and a half-{\i log} of blood was the universal {\i shiur} for all purposes \u8212- {\i tuma}, {\i teruma}, {\i nezirut}, {\i Pesach}, {\i kodashim}. This was their single undifferentiated {\i shiur} for {\i tumas ohel}. The quarter-{\i kav} and quarter-{\i log} played no role in their system.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Elders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second group among the early elders took the opposite position: {\i af rova atzamot ve-revi\u8217'it dam le-kol} \u8212- the quarter-{\i kav} of bones and the quarter-{\i log} of blood suffice for everything. They likewise drew no distinction between {\i nazir}, {\i oseh Pesach}, {\i teruma}, and {\i kodashim}. The debate between these two groups was simply: half-{\i kav} or quarter-{\i kav}, half-{\i log} or quarter-{\i log} \u8212- with no internal differentiation by category. One notes that the existence of this debate already complicates any simple claim that all {\i shiurim} are {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, a point the {\i Gemara} itself will address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beit din} that came after them \u8212- {\i beit din shel achareihem} \u8212- established the following ruling: {\i chatzi kav atzamot ve-chatzi log dam le-kol} \u8212- a half-{\i kav} of bones and a half-{\i log} of blood is the {\i shiur} for all purposes. This is because if one has double the standard {\i shiur}, obviously everyone is rendered {\i tamei} under any category. But \u8212- and this is the essential point that must be read alongside the first part \u8212- {\i rova atzamot ve-revi\u8217'it dam le-teruma ve-kodashim aval lo le-Nazir ve-oseh Pesach}: the quarter-{\i kav} of bones and the quarter-{\i log} of blood constitute {\i tuma} only with respect to {\i teruma} and {\i kodashim}, not for the purposes of the {\i nazir} (triggering the obligation to shave and bring {\i korbanot}) and not for the {\i oseh Pesach} (affecting one\u8217's ability to bring the Passover offering).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite vs Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is this: if one has only the quarter-{\i kav} or quarter-{\i log}, one is considered {\i tamei met} for the purposes of {\i teruma} and {\i kodashim} \u8212- one would need to become {\i tahor} \u8212- but this would not destroy one\u8217's {\i nezirut} and force the full shaving and {\i korban} process. Similarly, it would not prevent one from bringing a {\i korban Pesach} \u8212- though the {\i Gemara} does not spell out explicitly whether such a person would then be permitted to eat the {\i korban Pesach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most natural analytical framework for this distinction would be to say that the half-{\i kav} is the {\i de-oraita} threshold, while the quarter-{\i kav} is a {\i mi-de-rabbanan} stringency \u8212- one we apply for {\i teruma} and {\i kodashim} but do not apply when the consequences are particularly weighty: we would not on account of a merely {\i de-rabbanan} status of {\i tamei} prevent someone from bringing a {\i korban Pesach}, nor would we force a {\i nazir} to bring a {\i korban} he is not truly {\i de-oraita} obligated to bring. This exact structure was applied earlier on 42b in the context of {\i tumat chiburin}, where Rav Yannai said: {\i lo amru tuma be-chiburin ela le-teruma ve-kodashim, aval le-Nazir u-le-oseh Pesach lo}. The {\i Gemara} there also drew the same line between {\i teruma} and {\i kodashim} on one side, and {\i nazir} and {\i oseh Pesach} on the other \u8212- suggesting a {\i de-oraita}/{\i de-rabbanan} framework. However, that is not where our {\i Gemara} ultimately lands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Third Opinion Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not a Compromise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a pointed challenge: {\i michdi ein hachra\u8217'ah shelishit machra\u8217'at} \u8212- a third opinion does not qualify as a compromise position that decides the {\i halacha}. What does this mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a third opinion occupies the middle ground between two extremes, we might be inclined to rule according to it \u8212- we treat it as a {\i hakhra\u8217'ah}, a deciding compromise. But that inclination applies only when the third opinion is genuinely a midpoint within the same conceptual universe as the other two. If the third opinion is not really a compromise but rather introduces an entirely new concept that neither of the first two positions acknowledges as a legitimate axis of distinction, then it is simply a third independent view \u8212- not a {\i hakhra\u8217'ah}. In that case, the three opinions are simply three equal positions, and the fact that one appears numerically \u8220"in the middle\u8221" does not give it any special decisional weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here: the first group of elders said the {\i shiur} is a half-{\i kav}, full stop, for everything. The second group said the {\i shiur} is a quarter-{\i kav}, full stop, for everything. Neither group drew any distinction between {\i nazir} and {\i oseh Pesach} on the one hand and {\i teruma} and {\i kodashim} on the other. The third position \u8212- the {\i beit din shel achareihem} \u8212- says: for {\i nazir} and {\i oseh Pesach} the {\i shiur} is half-{\i kav}, for {\i teruma} and {\i kodashim} the {\i shiur} is quarter-{\i kav}. This is not a compromise; it is an entirely new conceptual category that the first two groups never acknowledged. To use an analogy: if one party insists on living in New York and the other insists on Los Angeles, declaring that they will live in Ohio is not a {\i hakhra\u8217'ah} \u8212- it is a completely independent position of its own. Kansas City, by contrast, would be a genuine {\i hakhra\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since both earlier groups spoke in absolute, undifferentiated terms \u8212- half-{\i kav} for everything, or quarter-{\i kav} for everything \u8212- the final {\i beit din}\u8217's categorical distinction between types of {\i tuma} purposes is not a compromise between them. It is a third opinion that neither side prepared the conceptual ground for. And precisely because it is not a {\i hakhra\u8217'ah}, the question becomes: on what authority do we adopt it? Why does this third position carry the day?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prophetic Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers through a statement of Rabbi Yaakov bar Idi: {\i mipi shemu\u8217'ah amru} \u8212- this ruling was transmitted as a received tradition. Specifically, it was a tradition going back to {\i mipi Chagai Zechariah u-Malachi} \u8212- Chaggai, Zechariah, and Malachi, the last of the prophets, at the beginning of the Second Temple period. The ruling is not the independent reasoning of the later {\i beit din}; they had an ancient tradition supporting this very distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes \u8212- and the Rambam states explicitly \u8212- that this is {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}. Yet it is also worth noting that {\i mipi shemu\u8217'ah} and {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} may not be identical. As the Netziv explains, when the {\i Gemara} uses the term {\i halacha}, Rashi often glosses it as {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, but the Netziv argues that it may simply mean an exceedingly ancient tradition whose original rationale has been forgotten \u8212- a tradition of great antiquity, but not necessarily one with the formal claim of Sinaitic origin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why the tradition is attributed specifically to Chaggai, Zechariah, and Malachi. The {\i Gemara} does not explain this attribution. Nor does it fully clarify whether they transmitted this distinction by virtue of their status as {\i nevi\u8217'im} \u8212- which would raise complex questions about the role of prophecy in the {\i halachic} process \u8212- or whether they transmitted it simply as the last links in the chain of tradition as members of the {\i Anshei Knesset HaGedolah}, in which case the authority is that of transmission rather than of prophetic revelation. These questions are not resolved here. What is clear is that the distinction between {\i nazir} and {\i oseh Pesach} on one side and {\i teruma} and {\i kodashim} on the other is, in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's final conclusion, a genuine {\i ikar halacha} \u8212- not merely a rabbinic stringency applied selectively, but a substantive distinction grounded in received tradition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah List Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} teaches: {\i al elu nazir megaleach} \u8212- over these does the {\i nazir} shave. The list comprises the backbone, the skull, a limb from a corpse or from a living person bearing sufficient flesh ({\i basar ka-rauy}), a half-{\i kav} of bones, a half-{\i log} of blood, and finally \u8212- in a separate category \u8212- a bone the size of a barley grain ({\i etzem ke-se\u8217'orah}), which causes {\i tuma} through touching and carrying ({\i maga} and {\i masa}) but not through {\i ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Al Elu Function\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains that the phrase {\i al elu} \u8212- \u8220"over these\u8221" \u8212- serves an exclusionary function. It comes to exclude the {\i etzem ke-se\u8217'orah} from the {\i ohel} category: {\i de\u8217'al maga ve\u8217'al masa in, ve\u8217'al ohel lo} \u8212- for touching and carrying, yes; for being under the same {\i ohel}, no. The {\i mishnah} is thus organized into two sub-lists: one pertaining to {\i tumas ohel}, and one pertaining to {\i tumas maga u-masa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking is that the {\i Gemara} here takes something of a {\i Rabbi Yishmael}-like approach \u8212- {\i dibra Torah kileshon bnei adam} \u8212- to the {\i mishnah} itself, treating each additional word as carrying halachic weight rather than as ordinary linguistic usage. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's first {\i al elu} divides the two internal lists (those causing {\i tumas ohel} from those causing only {\i tumas maga}), while the {\i ve\u8217'elu} at the {\i mishnah}\u8217's close divides it from the {\i mishnah} that follows on 54a, which lists cases where the {\i nazir} does {\i not} shave \u8212- including, as we will see, cases such as an overhanging stone ({\i even ha-sochech}).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half-Kav Bones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now doubles back to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling on {\i chatzi kav atzamot} \u8212- a half-{\i kav} of bones causes {\i tumas ohel} and {\i nazir megaleach} \u8212- as opposed to {\i rov atzamot}, the majority of bones, which is not stated here. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what is the actual scenario? If the bones are all individually the size of a barley grain, then even without a half-{\i kav}, a single bone suffices \u8212- {\i teipok lei mishum etzem ke-se\u8217'orah}, it comes out from the rule of {\i etzem ke-se\u8217'orah}. There is no need for a half-{\i kav} in that case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dust Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore resolves: {\i ela de-ikmach chamuchei} \u8212- the bones must have been ground into dust, pulverized to such a degree that there is not even a single {\i etzem ke-se\u8217'orah} among them. In that case, no individual bone meets the {\i shiur} for {\i tumas maga}, yet the aggregate quantity of a half-{\i kav} still causes {\i tumas ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes the difficulty here: {\i tzarich iyun, mai ka parich} \u8212- why is the {\i Gemara} asking this question at all? We are discussing {\i tumas ohel}, and an {\i etzem ke-se\u8217'orah} does not cause {\i tumas ohel} regardless. What prompted the {\i Gemara}\u8217's challenge? Tosafot offers a complicated answer: the {\i Gemara} understood that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling on {\i chatzi kav atzamot} implicitly excludes scenarios involving {\i less} than a half-{\i kav} \u8212- not merely from causing {\i tumas ohel}, but from causing {\i tuma} at all. What scenario would that be? One where the bones have been entirely pulverized to dust. In that condition, less than a half-{\i kav} of dust would not be {\i metamei} at all, not even through touching. The half-{\i kav} threshold, when reduced to dust, is what preserves the {\i tuma} even without any individual bone meeting the {\i shiur}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Limb w/o Flesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flesh Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then turns to {\i ever min ha-met ve\u8217'ever min ha-chai she-yesh alav basar ka-rauy} \u8212- a limb from a corpse or from a living person that has sufficient flesh upon it. {\i Basar ka-rauy} means flesh of an amount sufficient to heal if the limb were reattached to a living person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proceeds with what reads as a somewhat unusual reverse order through the {\i mishnah}, having already addressed the bones, the {\i rov minyan} and {\i rov binyan}, and the skull and backbone. Now it turns to the {\i ever}. Before examining the {\i ever} with sufficient flesh, the {\i Gemara} raises a prior question:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan: No Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eiver she-ein alav basar ka-rauy mai} \u8212- what is the law when a limb does not have sufficient flesh upon it? The answer is obviously that it does not cause {\i tumas ohel}. The real question is: does it still cause {\i tuma} through {\i maga} \u8212- through touching? Could one argue that a limb retains halachic significance as an {\i ever}, even when lacking sufficient flesh, such that touching it renders the {\i nazir} obligated to shave?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan rules: {\i ein ha-nazir megaleach aleihem} \u8212- the {\i nazir} does not shave over them. His reasoning: {\i de-katani b\u8217'reisha} \u8212- the first {\i mishnah} explicitly requires {\i she-yesh alav ke-zayit basar}, and the implicit reading is that without that amount of flesh, it does not cause {\i tuma}. Furthermore, if a limb lacking sufficient flesh could cause {\i tumas maga}, it should have appeared on the {\i maga} list in the {\i mishnah}. It is on neither list, and therefore the {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish: Tumah via\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish disagrees: {\i megaleach}. He derives this from the fact that the second {\i mishnah} \u8212- the one listing cases where the {\i nazir} does {\i not} shave \u8212- does not include {\i ever she-ein alav basar ka-rauy} in its list. Since it is absent from that negative list, Reish Lakish infers that the {\i nazir does} shave upon touching it \u8212- it is a case of {\i tumas maga}, not {\i tumas ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the force of this debate, it is essential to appreciate the structure of the two {\i mishnayot}. The first {\i mishnah} (on 49b) lists cases of {\i nazir megaleach}; its list includes {\i ever min ha-met ve-ever min ha-chai she-yesh alav basar ka-rauy}, along with the {\i etzem ke-se\u8217'orah} for {\i maga}. The second {\i mishnah} (on 54a) lists cases of {\i ein hanazir megaleach}: places like a field of graves ({\i beit ha-pras}) and {\i eretz ha-amim}, and physical categories such as the tombstone ({\i golel} and {\i dofek}), {\i revi\u8217'it dam}, and {\i rov ha-atzamot} \u8212- this last corresponding to the {\i revi\u8217'it ha-kav}. Critically, {\i ever she-ein alav basar ka-rauy} does not appear in the second {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit vs Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presses Rabbi Yochanan: if the logic is that anything not appearing in the first list is excluded, why does the second {\i mishnah} go to the trouble of listing {\i reva\u8217'it atzamot} \u8212- the quarter-{\i kav} of bones \u8212- which is already implicitly excluded by the first {\i mishnah}\u8217's requirement of a half-{\i kav}? The second {\i mishnah} apparently does mention things that are already implicit exclusions from the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan answers: {\i kol heicha d\u8217'mashma miklala lo tana b\u8217'seifa} \u8212- wherever the exclusion is genuinely implicit in the {\i klal} of the first {\i mishnah}, the second {\i mishnah} does not need to repeat it. The second {\i mishnah} only mentions something when, without the explicit statement, one might have drawn an erroneous inference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of {\i reva\u8217'it atzamot}, had the second {\i mishnah} not stated it explicitly, one might have thought that less than a half-{\i kav} is not {\i metamei} even through {\i maga} or {\i masa}. The explicit statement therefore comes to clarify: the quarter-{\i kav} is excluded only from {\i ohel}, not from {\i maga}. Even less than a half-{\i kav} of bones still causes {\i tuma} through touching. That clarification was necessary and thus warranted explicit mention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the {\i revi\u8217'it dam} appearing in the second {\i mishnah}, Rabbi Yochanan explains: {\i l\u8217'afukei midivrei Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- it is stated there to exclude Rabbi Akiva\u8217's persistent position that {\i revi\u8217'it dam} drawn from two corpses causes {\i tumas ohel}. The {\i mishnah} wishes to explicitly reject that view and therefore states it. In principle, the second {\i mishnah} does not need to restate things already implicit in the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to {\i ever she-ein alav basar ka-rauy}: since the first {\i mishnah} explicitly conditions the {\i ever} on {\i she-yesh alav basar ka-rauy}, the implication that a limb without sufficient flesh is excluded is fully embedded in the first {\i mishnah}. There is no need to repeat it in the second {\i mishnah}. Thus, the {\i nazir} does not shave for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish, by contrast, reads the two lists differently: the first {\i mishnah} organizes by modality \u8212- {\i ohel} cases and {\i maga} cases \u8212- and the second {\i mishnah} enumerates everything that does not trigger {\i nazir megaleach}. If a scenario is absent from the second {\i mishnah}, it is {\i megaleach}. The {\i she-yesh alav basar ka-rauy} of the first {\i mishnah} implicitly suggests the case of {\i she-ein alav basar ka-rauy}, and since that case is not listed in the second {\i mishnah} among the non-{\i megaleach} categories, Reish Lakish concludes that it is {\i megaleach} through {\i maga}. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position seems the stronger reading of the {\i mishnah} as a whole, but as we will see, Reish Lakish draws support from {\i midrashei halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Midrashic Derivations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i braita} that provides scriptural grounding for the categories of {\i tumas ohel} and {\i tumas maga}. The {\i pasuk} read for {\i ohel} is: {\i ve-chol asher yiga al pnei ha-sadeh u-ve-chalal cherev o ve-meit o ve-etzem adam o ve-kaver} (Bamidbar 19:16). The {\i Gemara} reads {\i al pnei ha-sadeh} as referring to {\i ha-me\u8217'ahil} \u8212- one who is an {\i ohel} over these sources of {\i tuma}. The phrase {\i al pnei} \u8212- literally \u8220"upon the face of\u8221" \u8212- is understood as superfluous given the word {\i yiga} (\u8220"touch\u8221"), and from this superfluity the {\i drashah} derives that the {\i pasuk} speaks of {\i tumas ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Cherev\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the word {\i be-chalal} the {\i braita} derives {\i eiver min ha-chai} \u8212- specifically, a limb from a living person that {\i yesh lo le-haalot arucha}, has enough flesh that it could heal if reattached. The word {\i chalal} suggests something that has been struck or wounded but is not yet fully dead \u8212- the limb, so to speak, is \u8220"the dead thing\u8221" while the person remains alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cherev & Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Cherev} gives rise to the famous principle {\i cherev harei hu ke-chalal} \u8212- a metal vessel is halachically equivalent to a {\i chalal}. This means that metal {\i keilim} that come into contact with a {\i meit}, or are present in the same {\i ohel} as a {\i meit}, acquire the {\i tuma} of the {\i meit} itself and become {\i avi avot ha-tuma} rather than merely {\i av ha-tuma}. They are not merely secondary sources of {\i tuma} but are elevated to the primary level of the corpse itself. This principle will be discussed further in the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i O ve-meit} \u8212- from {\i meit} the {\i braita} derives {\i eiver ha-nechlal min ha-meit}, a limb that is part of a corpse \u8212- that is, {\i ever min ha-meit}, where the whole body is the {\i meit} and the limb is extracted from it, as opposed to the {\i chalal} case where only the limb itself is the \u8220"dead thing.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i O ve-etzem adam} \u8212- from {\i etzem adam} the {\i braita} derives {\i rov atzamot}, the majority of bones. Though {\i etzem} sounds like a single bone, the {\i drashah} understands it as referring to the bone that represents the entire person \u8212- {\i rov atzamot}. The list from this {\i pasuk} thus covers {\i eiver min ha-chai}, {\i eiver min ha-meit}, and {\i rov atzamot} \u8212- most of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's {\i ohel} categories \u8212- though notably not the blood or {\i ke-zayit basar} categories as yet.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ohel vs Touching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} on {\i tumas ohel} is then set alongside the parallel {\i braita} on {\i tumas maga}, derived from a different {\i pasuk}: {\i ve-al ha-nogei\u8217'a be-etzem o be-chalal o be-meit o be-kaver} (Bamidbar 19:18). Rav Yehuda introduces this {\i braita} as a {\i tena} \u8212- a tannaitic source \u8212- dealing with touching. The two {\i beraitot} share much of the same language but serve different halachic functions: the first defines sources of {\i tumas ohel}; the second defines sources of {\i tumas maga}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From {\i be-etzem} in the touching {\i pasuk}, the {\i braita} derives {\i etzem ke-se\u8217'orah} \u8212- a bone the size of a barley grain causes {\i tuma} through touching. This is the distinctive {\i shiur} for {\i maga} that does not apply to {\i ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i O ve-kaver} in the {\i ohel} list gives rise to {\i kever satum} \u8212- a sealed grave. The {\i halacha} of {\i kever satum} is that if a body is enclosed in a sealed coffin or burial chamber, the entire enclosure is treated as the {\i meit} itself: the {\i tuma} spreads across the full dimensions of the casket and bursts upward ({\i tumah boka\u8217'at ve-olah boka\u8217'at ve-yoredet}), passing through the covering and continuing upward, because the sealed grave is treated as an extension of the corpse. This is why overflight of cemeteries is a concern. If the burial chamber were not sealed \u8212- lacking full sides \u8212- the {\i tuma} would stop within a {\i tefach} of the body at the covering above and would not proceed further upward, since the covering would function as an {\i ohel} to block it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RL: Flesh-Deficient Limbs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key contribution from the {\i maga} braita for the Reish Lakish dispute is the following. Where the {\i ohel} braita derived from {\i be-chalal} the case of {\i eiver min ha-chai she-yesh lo le-haalot arucha} \u8212- a limb with sufficient flesh \u8212- Reish Lakish reads the same word {\i chalal} in the {\i maga} braita to yield a different derivation: {\i zeh eiver ha-nichleh min ha-chai ve-ein bo le-haalot arucha} \u8212- a limb from a living person that does {\i not} have enough flesh to heal. Similarly, {\i o be-meit} in the {\i maga} braita yields {\i eiver ha-nichleh min ha-meit} without sufficient flesh. The two {\i beraitot} thus present a parallel structure: the {\i ohel pasuk} gives us {\i eiver min ha-chai} and {\i eiver min ha-meit} with sufficient flesh; the {\i maga pasuk} gives us the same two categories without sufficient flesh \u8212- and these latter cause {\i tuma} only through touching, not through {\i ohel}. This is the explicit scriptural support for Reish Lakish\u8217's position: a limb from a corpse or a living person lacking sufficient flesh is {\i metamei} through {\i maga}, even if it does not cause {\i tumas ohel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel structure between the two {\i beraitot} is also visible in the bone categories: {\i etzem adam} in the {\i ohel pasuk} refers to {\i rov atzamot}, while {\i be-etzem} in the {\i maga pasuk} refers to the smaller {\i etzem ke-se\u8217'orah}. So too with {\i chalal} and {\i meit} \u8212- each appears in both {\i pesukim} but yields a different level of {\i tuma} depending on context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final word in the {\i maga pasuk} \u8212- {\i o ve-kaver} \u8212- receives a striking interpretation from Reish Lakish: {\i zeh kever she-lifnei ha-dibbur} \u8212- the grave before the giving of the Torah at Sinai. What this mysterious phrase means, and its full halachic significance, will be taken up in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 54\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumat Met Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ohel vs.\u160 ?Touch\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to a foundational question in the laws of {\i tumat met}: which objects generate {\i tumah} through {\i ohel} \u8212- that is, by being under the same roof as a {\i met} \u8212- and which generate {\i tumah} only through direct contact? The two {\i pesukim} that govern {\i tumat met} both appear, at the level of {\i peshat}, to be discussing touch: {\i vechol asher yigah al p\u8217'nei hasadeh} \u8212- \u8220"and anyone who touches on the face of the field\u8221" \u8212- refers specifically to the open field, meaning not under a roof, so it is not {\i tumat ohel}. The second {\i pasuk}, {\i ve\u8217'al hanogea}, similarly seems to address touch. Yet the {\i midreshei halakha} read {\i al p\u8217'nei hasadeh} as describing a situation where a person is {\i over} the face of the {\i met} \u8212- leaning above it without making direct contact \u8212- and thereby interpret this {\i pasuk} as speaking about {\i ohel}. From this reading, the first {\i pasuk} yields the items that convey {\i tumah} through {\i ohel}, and the second yields the items that convey {\i tumah} through touch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesukic Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the first {\i pasuk}, the {\i midreshei halakha} derive that {\i chalal}, {\i cherev}, {\i met}, {\i etzem adam}, and {\i kever} all cause {\i tumah} through {\i ohel}. These five categories are not identical in their natures, and unpacking what each term signifies is essential to understanding how the system operates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cherev Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chalal} in this context is understood to mean something that was once an {\i eiver min hachai} \u8212- a limb severed from a living person \u8212- that is now dead in isolation, even without a complete corpse. {\i Cherev} introduces what is perhaps the most striking principle: {\i cherev harei hu kechalal} \u8212- the sword is like the corpse itself. The significance of this principle is considerable. A {\i met} is an {\i avi avot hatumah}, meaning that whoever touches it is rendered an {\i av hatumah} and is {\i tamei} for seven days. The rule of {\i cherev harei hu kechalal} extends this status to the sword \u8212- or more broadly, as we will see, to certain metal vessels \u8212- such that whoever touches it also becomes an {\i av}, not merely a {\i rishon}, and is {\i tamei} for seven days. The sword does not go down a level of {\i tumah} by virtue of having been in contact with the {\i met}; it retains the full status of the {\i met} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishkav Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is conceptually parallel to another domain of {\i halakha}: the {\i mishkav u\u8217'moshav} of a {\i zav}. A {\i zav} is an {\i av hatumah}. When a {\i zav} sits or lies upon something, that object \u8212- the {\i mishkav} or {\i moshav} \u8212- does not become a {\i rishon} as an ordinary vessel would through contact. It becomes an {\i av hatumah} by virtue of its identity as the seat of the {\i zav}. Famously, this applies even if the {\i zav} is separated from his seat by many cushions \u8212- the image invoked is of a princess and the pea \u8212- because what defines the {\i mishkav} is not physical contact but a status relationship. In a similar conceptual register, {\i cherev} acquires the full status of the {\i met} not by simple contact but by something more like identity-level association. Although we will arrive later in today\u8217's daf at a broader definition of what counts as {\i cherev} \u8212- potentially extending to all metal vessels that shared an {\i ohel} with a {\i met} or touched it \u8212- the basic principle is that these objects are conceived as {\i avi avot hatumah}, just as the {\i met} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumat Ohel Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The five items that cause {\i tumat ohel} are thus: {\i chalal}, {\i cherev}, {\i met}, {\i etzem adam}, and {\i kever satum}. The last of these, {\i kever satum} \u8212- a sealed coffin \u8212- requires explanation. One might have thought that a coffin would function as an {\i ohel} and thereby {\i contain} the {\i tumah}, preventing it from spreading upward. But the {\i halakha} of {\i kever satum} holds that if the coffin is sealed on all sides \u8212- walls and top \u8212- then the entire coffin is treated as the {\i met} itself, and the {\i tumah} is {\i metamei} not only in line with the {\i met} but in line with the entire coffin. The coffin does not serve as an {\i ohel}; it is, in legal terms, a {\i met}. This is a critical principle underlying the {\i halakha} of graveyards: one cannot simply argue that the coffin contains the {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bone Amount Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The category of {\i etzem adam} introduces the most significant difference between {\i tumat ohel} and {\i tumat maga}. For {\i tumat ohel}, the bone must be of sufficient quantity to represent the person \u8212- {\i rov minyan}, {\i rov binyan}, a {\i shidrah}, a {\i gulgolet}, or a corresponding quantity of bones. This is {\i etzem adam}. For {\i tumat maga}, however, even a bone the size of a barley grain \u8212- {\i etzem kese\u8217'orah} \u8212- causes {\i tumah} through touch. This distinction between {\i etzem adam} and {\i etzem} is the principal point of divergence between the two {\i pesukim}, and it will become the pivot of the Talmudic debate immediately to follow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of the second {\i pasuk}, which the {\i midreshei halakha} read as governing {\i tumat maga}, provides the occasion for a major dispute between Reish Lakish and Rabbi Yochanan. Reish Lakish contends that there exists a category of {\i eiver min hachai} and {\i eiver min hameis} \u8212- severed limbs \u8212- that lacks sufficient flesh to survive if reattached, yet still causes {\i tumah} through touch. His proof comes from a {\i braita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda introduces the relevant {\i braita} with the word {\i tanya}. The {\i braita} expounds the {\i pasuk} {\i ve\u8217'al hanogea be\u8217'etzem o vechalal}: \u8220"touching a bone\u8221" refers to {\i etzem kese\u8217'orah}; \u8220"or a {\i chalal}\u8221" refers to an {\i eiver} that was severed from a living person and does not have sufficient flesh to heal \u8212- {\i ein bo lehaalos arucha}; \u8220"or a {\i meis}\u8221" refers to {\i eiver min hameis}; \u8220"or a {\i kever}\u8221" \u8212- and here Reish Lakish interjects that this {\i kever} is {\i kever shelifnei hadibbur}, the grave before the divine speech, a phrase we will return to shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eiver Definitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within the {\i braita}, the distinction between the two types of severed limbs is as follows. An {\i eiver min hachai} or {\i eiver min hameis} that has sufficient flesh \u8212- enough that the limb could survive if reattached \u8212- is the standard case that causes {\i tumah} through {\i ohel}. An {\i eiver min hachai} or {\i eiver min hameis} that does not have sufficient flesh is the more unusual case, and it is this case that Reish Lakish says the {\i braita} addresses in the context of {\i tumat maga}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limb Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses: what exactly is the case of {\i eiver min hameis} in this {\i braita}? If the limb contains an {\i etzem kese\u8217'orah}, then it already causes {\i tumah} as a bone \u8212- {\i hainu hanogea be\u8217'etzem}. So the {\i braita} must be speaking of a case where the bone within the limb is insufficient to qualify independently. There is, then, a limb with insufficient bone to cause {\i tumah} as a bone, insufficient flesh to heal, and yet it causes {\i tumah} through contact. This, says Reish Lakish, demonstrates that a limb without sufficient flesh retains independent {\i tumah}-causing status by virtue of being a limb, and the {\i Torah} includes it explicitly \u8212- {\i rachmana rabyei}. The relevance to our {\i masechta} is that Reish Lakish infers that a {\i nazir} should also be {\i megaleach} upon touching such a limb, even though the {\i mishnah} listing the causes of {\i nazir megaleach} mentioned only limbs with sufficient flesh. The {\i mishnah} may have omitted it, but the {\i midrash halacha} makes clear that the {\i tumah} exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yochanan Rebuttal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan responds: {\i le\u8217'olam de\u8217'is beih} \u8212- the limb in the {\i braita} does in fact contain an {\i etzem kese\u8217'orah}. The apparent redundancy of the {\i braita} mentioning this limb separately from \u8220"bone\u8221" is not because it is teaching a new category; it is teaching a different mode of {\i tumah}-transmission. {\i V\u8217'ei eino inyan lemaga, tanehu inyan lemassa} \u8212- if the text is not needed to teach about {\i touching}, apply it to teach about {\i carrying}. This is the standard {\i talmudic} principle of {\i im eino inyan l\u8217'zeh, tnehu inyan l\u8217'zeh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Masa Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure of the two {\i pesukim} establishes two modes of {\i tumah}: {\i ohel} and {\i maga}. But there is a third mode \u8212- {\i massa}, carrying \u8212- and it needs a Scriptural source. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position is that there is no independent category of {\i eiver} without sufficient flesh causing {\i tumah} through touch; there is simply a second application of {\i etzem kese\u8217'orah}, and the redundancy derives {\i tumat massa}. The {\i halakhic} rule that carrying is equivalent to touching \u8212- as stated in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- still needs a textual basis, and that is what the apparent duplication in the {\i braita} provides. This is the substance of the Reish Lakish\u8211-Rabbi Yochanan dispute as it stands on this {\i amud}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kever Lifnei HaDibbur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now return to the striking phrase that Reish Lakish appended to his reading of the {\i braita}: the {\i kever} mentioned in the {\i pasuk} governing {\i tumat maga} is {\i kever shelifnei hadibbur} \u8212- the grave before the divine speech. {\i Tosafot} identifies two possible interpretations of this phrase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first interpretation understands {\i lifnei hadibbur} as referring to the period before {\i Matan Torah}. The underlying question is: what was the status of graves and the {\i tumah} they generate before the Torah was given? {\i Tosafot} links this to the broader question of when the people of Israel shifted from the status of {\i bnei Noach} to the status of {\i Yisrael}. Was it from the moment Avraham received {\i brit milah}? From {\i ma\u8217'amad Har Sinai}? There is an entire work \u8212- attributed to the {\i Mishneh Lamelech}, entitled {\i Parshat Drachim} \u8212- that works through the {\i midrashim} through this lens. One famous illustration: the debate between Yosef and his brothers, in which he accused them of eating {\i eiver min hachai}, turned precisely on this question. Yosef believed they already had the status of {\i bnei Yisrael} and were subject to more stringent obligations; they believed they retained the status of {\i bnei Noach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes, however, a difficulty with this first interpretation. If one accepts the view of {\i Rabbanan} and Rabbi Shimon that non-Jews are {\i metamei} through {\i ohel} \u8212- that is, if the rule {\i atem atem keruyim adam} limiting {\i tumat ohel} to Jews is not accepted \u8212- then there would be no need for a special {\i pasuk} to teach that a grave from before {\i Matan Torah} conveys {\i tumah} through {\i maga}. Everyone already concedes that non-Jews convey {\i tumah} through touch; that is uncontested even by Rabbi Shimon. So what novel teaching is {\i kever shelifnei hadibbur} adding? If the point were about the status of the person \u8212- Jewish versus non-Jewish \u8212- and if touching a non-Jew\u8217's grave already conveys {\i tumah}, why do we need a {\i drasha} about the period before the Torah was given?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Legislation Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads {\i Tosafot} to offer a second interpretation, which is, one might argue, the more natural reading. The {\i dibbur} in question is not the moment of {\i Matan Torah} as a whole, but the specific moment when the particular {\i parasha} of {\i tumat met} was legislated. The {\i parasha} of {\i adam ki yamut b\u8217'ohel} appears in {\i Chukat}, far into the forty years in the wilderness \u8212- not at Sinai. It is entirely possible that between {\i Matan Torah} and the giving of that specific {\i parasha}, people came into contact with the dead and entered a state of {\i tumah} before those laws existed. What is the status of such a person once the {\i parasha} is given?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ritva Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ritzba} \u8212- cited within {\i Tosafot} \u8212- addresses this question by distinguishing between the metaphysical reality of {\i tumah} and the legal legislation that addresses it. The {\i Ritzba} holds that Jews possess {\i tumah} status even before the law is given; what {\i kever shelifnei hadibbur} teaches is that the laws, once legislated, apply retroactively to those who entered that status beforehand. This is not a punitive or retroactive legislation in the ordinary legal sense; rather, it reflects a presumption that {\i tumah} is a metaphysical condition that exists independently of whether {\i halakha} has yet defined its consequences. Once the {\i halakha} is given, it addresses the person\u8217's actual state, whatever the origin of that state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} draws a parallel to other areas of {\i tumah}. A {\i pasuk} in {\i Perek Haba al Ha\u8217'arayot} similarly derives the status of a {\i zav shelifnei hadibbur} \u8212- a man who experienced a {\i zov} before {\i Parshat Zavim} was given. The same question applies to a woman who had her period before {\i Parshat Niddah} was legislated: is she a {\i niddah} from the moment that {\i parasha} takes effect? The {\i drasha} answers that yes, even if the condition arose before the legislation, these laws apply. What this implies is that these {\i tumot} are not created by the law; the condition exists, and the law governs it once it is given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metaphysics & Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a genuinely profound question: to what degree is {\i tumah v\u8217'tahara} a metaphysical reality, and to what degree is it purely a legal construct? If {\i tumah} is only a legal category, then it cannot exist before the law is enacted, and any attempt to apply it retroactively would be incoherent. But if {\i tumah} has an independent metaphysical reality \u8212- if it is a condition that attaches to a person regardless of legislation \u8212- then the law, when given, simply addresses a pre-existing state. One practical formulation: it is not that the person is held liable for having entered the {\i Mikdash} or otherwise violated a prohibition before the law was given \u8212- they violated nothing, since no prohibition existed. But from the moment the {\i parasha} is given, the person\u8217's current state is what it is: they are {\i tamei}, they must wait seven days, they must undergo the sprinkling of {\i parah adumah}, and all the attendant {\i halakhot} now apply to their actual condition. The law did not create the condition; it chose to address it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Non-Megaleach List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sprinkling Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes with the phrase {\i umaza bashlishi uvisvi\u8217'i vesoteis} \u8212- he sprinkles on day three and day seven, and it destroys the earlier days. A {\i nazir} who contracts one of the qualifying forms of {\i tumat met} must undergo the full {\i tahara} process, including {\i hazayah} on days three and seven, and the days of {\i nezirut} already completed are invalidated. The {\i nazir} may not begin counting anew until he becomes {\i tahor}, and he brings his {\i korbanos} as required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counting Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a question about the precise reading of the {\i mishnah\u8217's} phrase {\i ad she-yit-har} \u8212- until he becomes {\i tahor}. Does this mean he begins his new count on day seven, once {\i tahara} is technically achieved, or only on day eight, after the {\i korbanos} are brought? {\i Tosafot} notes that the version of the {\i mishnah} available to the {\i gemara} did not include the phrase {\i umeivi es korbanosav} \u8212- \u8220"and he brings his {\i korbanos}\u8221" \u8212- so the question is genuinely open from the text alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames the dispute as follows. If {\i ad she-yit-har} means until the end of day seven \u8212- at which point {\i ha\u8217'erev shemesh} has passed and {\i tahara} is complete \u8212- then the {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Eliezer, who holds that the {\i nazir} begins counting his {\i nezirut taharah} on day seven, since he is fully {\i tahor} by nightfall. If {\i ad she-yit-har} means until after the {\i korbanos} are brought, which occurs on day eight, then the {\i mishnah} follows the Rabbanan, who hold that {\i nezirut taharah} does not take effect until day eight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the question by cross-referencing the {\i mishnah} immediately to follow \u8212- the list of things for which a {\i nazir} is {\i not} {\i megaleach}. That {\i mishnah} states: {\i umaza bashlishi uvisvi\u8217'i umatchil umoneh miyad} \u8212- he sprinkles on day three and seven and begins counting immediately. In this case, since the {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach}, he does not bring {\i korbanos}, and {\i miyad} naturally refers to the moment he becomes {\i tahor} on day seven after the sprinkling. If \u8220"immediately\u8221" in the non-{\i megaleach} case means right after day seven, then \u8220"until he becomes {\i tahor}\u8221" in the {\i megaleach} case must refer to something later \u8212- namely, day eight after the {\i korbanos}. The contrast between the two formulations reveals that {\i miyad} is the earlier start point and {\i ad she-yit-har} is the later one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day Eight Start\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i manu Rabbanan hi d\u8217'amri nezirut tahara ad shemini lo chayila} \u8212- this accords with the Rabbanan, who hold that {\i nezirut tahara} does not take effect until day eight. The {\i nazir} who must bring {\i korbanos} cannot begin counting toward his new {\i nezirut} until after those {\i korbanos} are offered. The comparison with the non-{\i megaleach} case, where counting resumes immediately after day seven, establishes that the requirement to bring {\i korbanos} is what delays the start in the standard {\i megaleach} case, and that delay continues until day eight.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumat But Not Megaleach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit HaPras\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now presents a list of things for which a {\i nazir} is {\i not megaleach}. The first items are {\i beit hapras} and {\i eretz amim}. A {\i beit hapras} is a field over which a grave was plowed. The rabbis decreed that one who passes through such a field is {\i tamei}, out of concern that tiny fragments of bone may have been scattered across a wide area. Biblically, however, this decree does not apply: the area is too large, the fragments too small, and the concern too attenuated to rise to the level of {\i de\u8217'oraita tumah}. Since a {\i nazir} is only {\i megaleach} for qualified {\i tumat met de\u8217'oraita}, {\i beit hapras} does not trigger {\i megaleach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eretz Amim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eretz amim} \u8212- the land outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- was declared {\i tamei} by rabbinic decree. The reasons given are multiple. One is a concern that Jewish bodies are buried throughout the diaspora, though the practical force of this reasoning is complicated. A more compelling explanation, which {\i Tosafot} states explicitly and which we will revisit, is that this decree was enacted as a social and national measure to encourage Jews to remain in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. There may also be a theological dimension \u8212- {\i Eretz Yisrael} as the uniquely sanctified land, and the outside as {\i eretz hatzmei\u8217'ah}. In any case, the {\i tumah} of {\i eretz amim} is rabbinic, and a {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach} for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Golel ve\u8217'Dofek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Golel ve\u8217'dofek} is a category subject to a dispute between Rashi and {\i Tosafot} regarding its physical referent. Rashi, in {\i Perek Kama} of {\i Ketubot}, explains that the {\i golel} is the cover of the coffin and the {\i dofek} refers to the side panels. {\i Tosafot} disagrees, citing the {\i gemara} in {\i Perek Bameh Madlikin} which derives {\i golel ve\u8217'dofek} from the phrase {\i al p\u8217'nei hasadeh}, which implies objects that are exposed above ground, not buried. Rabbeinu Tam therefore explains that {\i golel} is the tombstone \u8212- the {\i matzeiva} \u8212- and {\i dofek} refers to the stones upon which the tombstone leans for support. A third possibility, raised in discussion, is that these terms refer to the stone sealing a catacomb, which accords with archaeological evidence that both above-ground and below-ground burial practices coexisted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Golel Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is significant about {\i golel ve\u8217'dofek} is that it is {\i de\u8217'oraita tumah} \u8212- specifically {\i tumat meis de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- and yet a {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach} for it. The {\i Torah} derives from {\i al p\u8217'nei hasadeh} that the tombstone and its supporting stones carry biblical {\i tumah}, yet they are not included in the list of {\i nazir megaleach}. This is a striking principle: {\i de\u8217'oraita} status does not automatically entail {\i nazir megaleach}. The previous {\i mishnah} establishing when a {\i nazir} is {\i megaleach} never mentioned touching a {\i kever satum} on a part not directly opposite the {\i met}; the present {\i mishnah} clarifies that {\i golel ve\u8217'dofek}, though biblically {\i tamei}, is excluded from the {\i megaleach} requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical corollary emerges from {\i Masechet Smachot}, which states: {\i kol meis she\u8217'ein hanazir megaleach alav, ein kohen muzhar alav} \u8212- any {\i tumah} for which a {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach} is also one for which a {\i kohen} is not prohibited. This principle extends the significance of the {\i megaleach} list well beyond {\i nezirut} into {\i kohen} law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Revi\u8217'is Dam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Revi\u8217'is dam} \u8212- a quarter-{\i log} of blood from a corpse \u8212- causes {\i tumah de\u8217'oraita}, whether through touch or {\i ohel}. Yet the {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach} for it, because the qualifying quantity for {\i nazir megaleach} by blood is {\i chatzi log}, not {\i revi\u8217'is}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ohel Rova Atzmos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i ohel rova atzmos} \u8212- being under the same roof as a quarter-{\i kav} of bones \u8212- constitutes {\i tumat meis de\u8217'oraita} through {\i ohel}, yet it does not trigger {\i megaleach}. The threshold for {\i nazir megaleach} by bones in an {\i ohel} context is {\i chatzi kav}, not {\i rova kav}. These cases represent the other face of the distinctions that dominated the previous days of the {\i daf}: smaller quantities of blood and bone are {\i de\u8217'oraita tamei}, but they fall below the threshold at which {\i nazir megaleach} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels Touching Corpse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next category is {\i keilim hanogaim b\u8217'meis} \u8212- vessels that touched the corpse. The more interesting reading of this case, and the one with the most {\i halakhic} consequence, is that these are vessels falling into the category of {\i cherev harei hu kechalal}. Such vessels do not merely become an {\i av} through contact; they acquire the status of {\i avi avot hatumah}, like the {\i met} itself. A {\i nazir} who touches such a vessel would become an {\i av} and be {\i tamei} for seven days \u8212- a full {\i tumat meis} by all accounts. Yet the {\i mishnah} teaches that a {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach} for this. These vessels are {\i de\u8217'oraita tumat meis} at the level of {\i avi avot}, and still not {\i megaleach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Goren Innovation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling has a remarkable practical application in the {\i halakhot} of {\i kohanim}. We noted earlier the principle from {\i Masechet Smachot}: {\i kol meis she\u8217'ein hanazir megaleach alav, ein kohen muzhar alav}. If a vessel that has acquired the status of {\i cherev harei hu kechalal} is one for which a {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach}, it follows that a {\i kohen} is not prohibited from touching such a vessel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Goren \u8212- not Rav Herzog, as initially misstated \u8212- proposed an innovative {\i halakhic} solution to the question of whether a {\i kohen} may study medicine and thereby come into repeated contact with corpses. The background is as follows: even granting the position that a {\i kohen} who is already {\i tamei meis} cannot become {\i re-tamei} simply by touching the {\i met} again, there remains the concern that active contact with the {\i met} raises the level of the {\i kohen\u8217's tumah}. Rav Goren\u8217's solution: bring metal rings into the room where the {\i met} is located. These rings thereby acquire the status of {\i cherev harei hu kechalal} \u8212- they become {\i avi avot hatumah}, as {\i tamei} as the {\i met} itself. Since a {\i kohen} is permitted to touch these rings (a {\i nazir} would not be {\i megaleach} for them, and accordingly a {\i kohen} is not {\i muzhar} on them), the {\i kohen} can then wear these rings while handling the {\i met}. By wearing an object that is already at the {\i avi avot} level, the {\i kohen} is not raising his level of {\i tumah} through direct contact with the {\i met} \u8212- he is already in contact with something of equivalent status. The logical and {\i halakhic} elegance of this approach generated significant controversy; Rav Moshe Feinstein reportedly had a strong reaction against it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then turns to entirely different categories of {\i tumah} \u8212- those that are not {\i tumat meis} at all. A {\i metzora} in the stage of {\i yimei gmaro} \u8212- the second week of quarantine, when the spot has already spread and the {\i kohen} has sealed a full determination \u8212- or in {\i yimei seforo} \u8212- the subsequent seven-day counting period during which the {\i metzora} undergoes {\i tahara} before bringing {\i korbanos} on day eight \u8212- is a full {\i metzora} in the {\i halachic} sense. Tzaraas {\i tumah} is its own {\i de\u8217'oraita} category. Yet since it is not {\i tumat meis}, there is no {\i megaleach}. For these cases, the {\i mishnah} states: he is not {\i megaleach}, he sprinkles on days three and seven because there is {\i tumat meis} as well (since whatever {\i tumat meis} he may have contracted still requires {\i hazayah}), earlier days are not destroyed, and he begins counting {\i miyad} \u8212- immediately \u8212- once he becomes {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also clarifies that he does not bring the {\i korban} associated with {\i nazir megaleach}, since no {\i megaleach} has occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Light Tumot Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Umbe\u8217'emes amru} \u8212- in truth they said \u8212- the {\i mishnah} adds a further distinction. {\i Yimei hazav v\u8217'hazavah} \u8212- the days in which a man is a {\i zav} or a woman is a {\i zavah} \u8212- and {\i yimei hesgeiro shel metzora} \u8212- the days of the first week of a {\i metzora\u8217's} quarantine, before a definitive diagnosis \u8212- not only do not trigger {\i megaleach}, they can actually count positively toward the {\i nazir\u8217's} total count of days. Since none of these constitute {\i tumat meis}, they do not prevent {\i nezirut} days from accumulating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think this is obvious \u8212- what {\i tumah} other than {\i tumat meis} would interrupt {\i nezirut}? The real {\i chiddush} is what this implies by contrast with the first case. The {\i mishnah} contrasting these lighter cases with the case of the {\i metzora} in {\i yimei gmaro} or {\i yimei seforo} reveals something important: even for the heavier {\i metzora}, it is not {\i tumat meis} that prevents counting \u8212- it is the {\i tzaraas} state itself. The {\i nazir} in that state cannot count those days toward his {\i nezirut}, and must wait until he exits the {\i metzora} status entirely before resuming. The reason is not yet fully explained by the {\i gemara} and will require further examination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sakkot & Peraot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now defines the terms {\i sakkot} and {\i peraot} from the {\i mishnah}. {\i Sakkot} are trees that overhang the ground \u8212- {\i ilan hamaysach al ha\u8217'aretz}. {\i Peravot} are stones protruding from a wall \u8212- {\i avanim hayotzos min hageder} \u8212- adjacent to a cemetery. Walking under either constitutes only a rabbinic {\i tumah}, and therefore a {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach} for either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sakkot Non-Ohel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} explains, citing {\i Perek Kama d\u8217'Niddah}, that a tree is not a valid {\i ohel} from a {\i de\u8217'oraita} perspective because the branches are spread apart and do not constitute a solid {\i tefach} of coverage. The leaves and branches vary in density, and there is no reliable {\i tefach-by-tefach} roof. Even if there is certainly a {\i kezayit min hamet} buried beneath one of these trees, one who walks under it is {\i tahor mi-de\u8217'oraita}. Even walking under all of them would not create {\i de\u8217'oraita} liability, because one was never under a proper {\i ohel}. The rabbinic decree imposes {\i tumah} on one who passes beneath such trees, but since it is rabbinic, {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peraot Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i peraot} is different in its rationale. A stone overhang can in principle constitute a proper {\i ohel} \u8212- that is not the issue. The issue is uncertainty about whether there is a {\i meis} beneath any given stone. {\i Tosafot} explains: this is a wall adjacent to a cemetery, and there may be a {\i nefel} \u8212- an infant who did not survive \u8212- buried beneath one of the protruding stones. The scenario of a protruding stone above a cemetery wall raises the question of how the {\i tumah} passes around or through the wall to the other side, which is why the case is framed in terms of a burial near or beneath the stone itself rather than across from a grave. In any case, one does not know with certainty whether there is a {\i meis} beneath the specific stone under which one walks. This is {\i sefeik tumah b\u8217'reshut harabbim} \u8212- a doubtful {\i tumah} in a public domain \u8212- which the rule of the {\i Torah} renders {\i tahor}. It is only by rabbinic decree that one is considered {\i tamei} for passing beneath such a stone. Since the {\i tumah} is rabbinic, {\i nazir} is not {\i megaleach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between the two cases is thus: by {\i sakkot}, the {\i ohel} itself is not a valid {\i de\u8217'oraita ohel} \u8212- the branches are insufficiently solid. By {\i peraot}, the {\i ohel} is potentially valid, but there is {\i sefeik} regarding the existence of a {\i meis} beneath it. In both cases the result is the same \u8212- only rabbinic {\i tumah}, and therefore no {\i nazir megaleach}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 54b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin on 54b, five lines down in the {\i Gemara}. The {\i Mishnah} presented a list of categories of {\i tumah} for which a {\i Nazir} is not {\i megale\u8217'ach} \u8212- does not shave his head, does not lose the earlier days of his {\i nezirus}, and does not bring a {\i korban}. Some of these exclusions apply because the {\i tumah} in question is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}; others apply even to {\i tumah} that is nominally {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, such as {\i tumas meis}, but that nevertheless fall outside the category that triggers {\i nazir megale\u8217'ach}. An important parallel principle, found not in our {\i Gemara} but in {\i Masechet Smachot}, states that whatever does not cause a {\i Nazir} to shave also does not fall within the prohibitions incumbent upon a {\i Kohen} \u8212- a point of great significance to which we will return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Categories Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's list of exclusions from {\i nazir megale\u8217'ach} divides into two broad categories. The first consists of cases where there is no doubt that {\i tumah} is present, but the mechanism by which it is transmitted is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. The second consists of cases where the {\i tumah} itself may be {\i d\u8217'oraisa} in origin but is nonetheless excluded from the operative category. The upcoming discussion focuses primarily on the first type: cases where a genuine dead body may well be present, but the manner of transmission does not rise to the biblical standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trees & Walls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two examples of rabbinically-based {\i tumah} appear in the {\i Mishnah}: overhanging trees and outcropping walls. In the case of the trees \u8212- most plausibly trees growing over a cemetery \u8212- {\i Tosafot} explains that while there is indeed a corpse buried underneath, the branches and leaves in any given span do not constitute a {\i tefach} by {\i tefach} surface and therefore cannot form a {\i d\u8217'oraisa ohel}. In the case of the outcropping stone walls, the scenario involves something buried on the outside of the cemetery wall \u8212- sometimes fetuses, buried there due to uncertainty about their status. Because the burial is uncertain, the {\i tumah} is a {\i safek tumah b\u8217'reshut harabbim}, and the person remains biblically {\i tahor}. Both cases thus represent categories where the {\i Nazir} does not shave, and where \u8212- by the parallel principle of {\i Masechet Smachot} \u8212- the {\i Kohen} is not formally prohibited.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beis Hapras\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now take up a new category in the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's list: {\i beis hapras} and {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim}. {\i Beis hapras} refers to a field through which a grave was accidentally plowed, scattering fragments of bone throughout. Biblically, there would ordinarily be no sufficient basis for {\i tumah} without actual contact with the bone itself, but rabbinically one may not walk through such a field. The {\i Gemara} gives this only brief treatment here; it is {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} \u8212- the lands outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, which are deemed {\i tamei} \u8212- that will occupy the bulk of our discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Air vs.\u160 ?Ground\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} poses a pointed question about the nature of {\i tumas eretz ha\u8217'amim}: {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim ibaya lehu} \u8212- they raise the question: {\i mishum avira gazru aleha} \u8212- was the decree directed at the airspace itself? Or {\i mishum gusha gazru aleha} \u8212- was it directed at the ground?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the stakes: if the concern is the ground ({\i gusha}), then the {\i tumah} operates within the standard framework of {\i tumas meis} and {\i ohel}. Just as one who stands over a corpse is {\i tamei} by virtue of being in the same {\i ohel}, so too one who walks or stands over the ground of {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} would be {\i tamei} if we assume the ground harbors the dead. Critically, however, if one were inside a structure or on an elevated platform that effectively forms an {\i ohel} between oneself and the ground, one would be protected \u8212- the floor below serving as an {\i ohel} separating the person from whatever lies beneath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avira & Gusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Avira}, by contrast, means that even this protection is unavailing. Even if one is entirely shielded from what is beneath \u8212- sealed above an {\i ohel}, standing on the second floor of a building \u8212- one remains {\i tamei} by virtue of being within the airspace of {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim}. There are, after all, no dead bodies floating in the air; there is no physical rationale for {\i tumas avira}. This observation itself points toward something important about the nature of this decree: it cannot be explained as a straightforward extension of ordinary {\i tumas meis} and {\i ohel}. It must be motivated by something else.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Airspace\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transport Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} introduces precisely the cases the {\i Gemara} will shortly bring: {\i k\u8217'gon mahalach b\u8217'shidah tevah u\u8217'migdal} \u8212- one who is transported through {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} inside a large box, wagon, or tower. The box must be larger than forty {\i se\u8217'ah} \u8212- the size of a {\i mikveh} \u8212- because below that volume it qualifies as a {\i kli}, a vessel, and as a vessel it could not serve as an {\i ohel} and would itself be susceptible to {\i tumah}. Assume instead a massive box, like the crate a large appliance comes in, with an open top. If you are standing inside it, elevated on a platform within it, then the floor of the box constitutes an {\i ohel} between you and the ground of {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim}. You should, by normal {\i ohel} logic, be protected. Similarly, if you travel through {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} by wagon or boat, the structure of the vessel stands between you and the ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stricter Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet {\i Tosafot} states: {\i she\u8217'hechmiru vo yoter mib\u8217'ohel hameis} \u8212- the Sages were more strict regarding the airspace of {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} than the ordinary rules of {\i ohel} and corpse-{\i tumah} would require. Even though one is above an {\i ohel} that would normally be fully protective, the mere fact of being within the airspace renders one {\i tamei}. This is a stringency that has no parallel in standard {\i halachot} of {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Policy Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key phrase in {\i Tosafot} is: {\i shelo yeitzei me\u8217'eretz l\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- so that people should not leave {\i Eretz Yisrael} for {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. This is a remarkable statement. The {\i tumah} of {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} is not grounded in a practical concern about contact with the dead. It is a policy instrument \u8212- an application of {\i halacha} as a form of social legislation designed to discourage emigration from {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The immediate practical effect falls most heavily on {\i Kohanim}, for whom {\i tumah} carries formal prohibitions, but the designation of the outside world as {\i eretz tmei\u8217'ah} presumably carries a broader cultural weight as well, reinforcing the attitude that {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is categorically inferior \u8212- a place one should not wish to inhabit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is already a separate prohibition, {\i assur latzeit me\u8217'eretz Yisrael l\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz}, applicable to all Jews. One might ask: is that not sufficient? Perhaps. But {\i Kohanim}, it seems, were especially responsive to the framework of {\i tumah v\u8217'taharah}, and the designation of {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} as {\i tamei} would have had particular force for them. Beyond the formal prohibition, the very label of {\i eretz tmei\u8217'ah} shapes how one relates to the outside world. This stands in interesting tension with the recognition \u8212- which we noted based on a student\u8217's observation \u8212- that {\i Eretz Yisrael} itself has, through the millennia of invasion and massacre, become saturated with the dead. Every building project today in Israel uncovers old graves. That irony only reinforces the conclusion that the fundamental motivation of {\i tumas eretz ha\u8217'amim} must be this {\i kedei shelo yeitzu} rationale, whatever form it ultimately takes in {\i halachic} application.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gusha Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dead Bodies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} also considers the {\i gusha} position: that the decree operates only through the ground and the normal laws of {\i ohel}, without the additional stringency of {\i avira}. On this reading, one would be {\i tamei} by walking over or being within a normal {\i ohel} relationship with the ground of {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim}, but protected if one stands above an {\i ohel} that intervenes. The question is what bodies would ground this {\i gusha}-based {\i tumah}. {\i Tosafot} offers two answers: {\i mipnei metei mabul} \u8212- because of the dead of the Flood, who, according to certain {\i gemarot}, were swept into {\i Bavel} (identified with {\i Shin\u8217'ar}) and are buried beneath its ground; and {\i mipnei rov Yisrael shenehergu b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- because the majority of Jews who were killed throughout history died outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both answers face serious difficulties. The {\i mabul} dead were not Jewish, and the standard ruling is that only Jewish corpses convey {\i tumas ohel}. One might argue that the first answer assumes we rule that non-Jews also convey {\i tumas ohel} \u8212- but that is far from settled. Moreover, {\i Bavel} is not all of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. As for the second answer: even granting the tragic historical claim that many Jews perished outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, the major mass deaths of the {\i Beis HaMikdash} period occurred predominantly within {\i Eretz Yisrael} itself. There is no clear empirical basis for assuming that Jewish dead are uniformly distributed throughout {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} in quantities sufficient to ground a {\i gusha}-based {\i tumah} with the force of {\i tumas ohel}. Rightly understood, even the {\i gusha} account does not rest on solid physical reasoning \u8212- it cannot be a straightforward application of ordinary {\i tumah} law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Policy Underlying\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why {\i Tosafot}\u8217's policy rationale \u8212- {\i kedei shelo yeitzu} \u8212- is likely the underlying motivating force regardless of which account one adopts. The question between {\i avira} and {\i gusha} is then not whether the decree is policy-driven, but rather how far the Sages extended it: did they apply ordinary {\i tumas ohel} logic with the ground as the source ({\i gusha}), or did they go further and declare the very airspace {\i tamei} even when one is otherwise protected by an {\i ohel} ({\i avira})? Both represent departures from purely physical {\i tumah} reasoning; {\i avira} simply departs further.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir-Kohen Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the broader principle connecting {\i nazir megale\u8217'ach} and {\i kohen muzhar} comes into focus. The principle, as formulated in {\i Masechet Makkos} and echoed here, is: {\i kol tumah she\u8217'ein ha\u8217'nazir megale\u8217'ach aleha, ein ha\u8217'kohen muzhar aleha} \u8212- any {\i tumah} that does not cause a {\i Nazir} to shave does not trigger the formal biblical prohibition for a {\i Kohen}. This linkage is structurally important: it means that the entire question of what constitutes operationally significant {\i tumah} \u8212- the {\i tumah} that the Torah treats as the most serious kind \u8212- is defined by the {\i Nazir}\u8217's shaving requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application to {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} and {\i beis hapras} follows directly. Because these are {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} forms of {\i tumah} that do not cause a {\i Nazir} to shave, a {\i Kohen} entering {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} or {\i beis hapras} does not incur {\i malkus d\u8217'oraisa} and is not in violation of a biblical prohibition. There remain {\i issurei d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- the rabbinic prohibitions are real \u8212- but the {\i Kohen} is not {\i muzhar} in the full biblical sense, does not receive lashes, and is not subject to a {\i d\u8217'oraisa issur}. This distinction between the biblical and rabbinic levels of prohibition will prove critical in the discussions that follow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sprinkling Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now attempts to resolve the {\i avira}-versus-{\i gusha} question from a ruling found in the {\i Mishnah} itself: {\i u\u8217'mazei bashlishi uvashvi\u8217'i} \u8212- one who entered {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} must be sprinkled with the purification waters of the {\i parah adumah} on days three and seven of the purification process. The {\i Gemara} reasons: {\i v\u8217'ei amart mishum avira} \u8212- if the {\i tumah} is based purely on the airspace, then this is a novel, free-standing form of {\i tumah} with no basis in the framework of {\i tumas meis}. If it is not grounded in {\i tumas meis}, there is no logical basis for applying the {\i haza\u8217'ah} on {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii} \u8212- the sprinkling cycle is inherently part of the {\i tumas meis} purification process. On the {\i avira} account, the Sages invented a new {\i tumah}; they cannot then borrow the ritual mechanics of an entirely different {\i tumah} to define its purification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially appears to conclude that the sprinkling requirement points to {\i gusha} \u8212- the {\i tumah} operates within the {\i tumas meis} framework, and therefore the standard purification ritual applies. But the {\i Gemara} immediately rejects this inference: {\i le\u8217'olam eima lach mishum avira} \u8212- one can still say it is {\i avira}-based. The sprinkling mentioned in the {\i Mishnah} does not apply to every item on the list. The {\i Mishnah} mentions {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii} with respect to some categories, such as {\i sechachos}, {\i pera\u8217'os}, and {\i beis hapras}, which are extensions of {\i tumas meis} and therefore appropriately purified through {\i haza\u8217'ah}. But other items on the list \u8212- {\i metzora} being the clearest example, since the {\i parah adumah} sprinkling is obviously irrelevant to {\i tzara\u8217'as} \u8212- are not subject to {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii}. On the {\i avira} reading, {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} falls into the second category: it is a distinct {\i tumah} to which {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii} does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then adds that this conclusion is supported by another consideration: {\i mideka tanni v\u8217'keilim hanog\u8217'im b\u8217'meis} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} also includes vessels that touched a dead body among the categories for which a {\i Nazir} does not shave. {\i Hani keilim benei haza\u8217'ah nin?} \u8212- Are such vessels subject to sprinkling on days three and seven? Clearly not, in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's assumption. This demonstrates that the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's mention of {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii} cannot be read as applying to every item enumerated. Therefore, {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim}\u8217's exemption from {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii} is consistent with the {\i avira} interpretation and does not refute it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cherev Ke\u8217'Chalal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a significant question. The {\i Gemara} assumes without explanation that {\i keilim} that touched a dead body would not require {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii} sprinkling. Let us examine why. Under ordinary {\i halachot} of {\i tumah}, a vessel that touches a {\i meis} becomes an {\i av hatumah}. A person who then touches that vessel becomes a {\i rishon l\u8217'tumah} \u8212- a first-level derivative \u8212- and is {\i tamei} for only one day. The one-day {\i tumah} has no connection to the seven-day purification process and {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii}; those apply only to {\i tumas shivah}, the full seven-day {\i tumah} associated with direct contact with a corpse or its equivalent. So if we are talking about ordinary vessels, the logic is clear: no {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii}. But then a different question arises: if it is such a routine form of {\i tumah}, why does the {\i Mishnah} bother mentioning it at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely {\i Tosafot}\u8217's question. {\i Tosafot} asks: {\i v\u8217'im tomar} \u8212- why not read the {\i Mishnah} as referring to metal vessels under the principle of {\i cherev harei hu k\u8217'chalal} \u8212- a sword is like a corpse? Under {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal}, a metal vessel that was in contact with a corpse is treated as if it were the corpse itself \u8212- an {\i av hatumah} in the fullest sense. Anyone who touches it becomes {\i tamei} with a seven-day {\i tumah} and requires {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii} sprinkling. If that is the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's intent, then two things follow: first, it is a genuine {\i chiddush} worthy of mention, and second, the person {\i would} require {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii}, so the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's reference to sprinkling would apply here too, undercutting the {\i Gemara}\u8217's proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why, then, does the {\i Gemara} assume the {\i Mishnah} is not talking about metal vessels under {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbeinu Tam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbeinu Tam}\u8217's answer is direct: {\i de\u8217'al karchach lo miktamah masnisin b\u8217'klei matechos} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} cannot be speaking of metal vessels under {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal}, because {\i de\u8217'im kein hanazir megale\u8217'ach, d\u8217'cherev harei hu k\u8217'chalal} \u8212- if it were, the {\i Nazir} would be {\i megale\u8217'ach}. If the vessel is treated like the corpse itself and the {\i Nazir} who touches it is thereby {\i tamei tumas shivah}, there is no known {\i Gemara} that exempts such a {\i Nazir} from shaving. {\i Rabbeinu Tam}\u8217's position is that {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} would indeed trigger {\i nazir megale\u8217'ach}, and therefore the {\i Mishnah}, which teaches that the {\i Nazir} does {\i not} shave for {\i keilim hanog\u8217'im b\u8217'meis}, cannot be discussing {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} at all. The {\i Mishnah} must be speaking of ordinary vessels under ordinary {\i tumah} rules.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbeinu Chaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then records the famous exchange between {\i Rabbeinu Tam} and {\i Rabbeinu Chaim HaKohen}. {\i Rabbeinu Chaim} sent {\i Rabbeinu Tam} a letter, opening with the verse {\i aizeh bayis asher tivnu li, v\u8217'aizeh makom menuchati} \u8212- \u8220"What house can you build for Me, and what place shall be My resting place?\u8221" \u8212- invoking the divine rhetorical question to pose one of his own: he is a {\i Kohen}, and {\i Rabbeinu Tam} is telling him that any metal vessel that was ever in a house with a {\i meis} is now itself like the {\i meis} \u8212- and the {\i Kohen} is biblically prohibited from being in the same space as it. {\i Aizeh bayis asher tivnu li} \u8212- what house can you build for me in which I could ever live?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metal Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument is a {\i reductio ad absurdum}. {\i Rabbeinu Chaim} invokes the principle {\i kol tumah she\u8217'hanazir megale\u8217'ach aleha kohen muzhar aleha} \u8212- everything that requires {\i nazir megale\u8217'ach} also triggers the {\i Kohen}\u8217's prohibition. He observes: {\i v\u8217'ein lecha bayis she\u8217'ein bo shum klei machos she\u8217'hayah b\u8217'ohel hameis o shum masmer} \u8212- there is no house that does not contain some metal vessel or nail that was at some point under the same {\i ohel} as a dead body. In the Middle Ages nails were not manufactured fresh from a factory; they were reused from house to house, passed through markets, carried across generations. Any such nail, if {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} applied in full force, would render the {\i Kohen} biblically prohibited from entering any ordinary home. The implication is patently impossible as a practical matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbeinu Chaim HaKohen} therefore concludes that even metal vessels \u8212- even when {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} applies and the person who touches them is {\i tamei tumas shivah} \u8212- do not cause a {\i Nazir} to shave and do not trigger the formal biblical prohibition for a {\i Kohen}. This is what the {\i Mishnah} is teaching when it lists {\i keilim hanog\u8217'im b\u8217'meis} among the cases where a {\i Nazir} does not shave: the {\i chiddush} is specifically about metal vessels under {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal}, and the ruling is that even there, the {\i Nazir} is not {\i megale\u8217'ach} and the {\i Kohen} is not {\i muzhar}. This position represents the accepted {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rambam Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} takes the discussion in a striking direction. Not only does he affirm {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal}, but he extends its scope beyond metal vessels. In {\i Hilchos Tumas Meis} 5:3, he writes: {\i keilim she\u8217'nitme\u8217'u b\u8217'meis bein b\u8217'maga bein b\u8217'ohel, harei hanogei\u8217'a bahen k\u8217'nogei\u8217'a b\u8217'meis atzmo} \u8212- vessels that became {\i tamei} through a corpse, whether through direct contact or through {\i ohel}, render one who touches them as if one had touched the corpse itself. Just as a corpse conveys seven-day {\i tumah} whether to a person or to vessels, so too do vessels that became {\i tamei} through the corpse \u8212- whoever or whatever subsequently touches them is {\i tamei tumas shivah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And the {\i Rambam} derives this not only from tradition ({\i mi\u8217'pi hashmu\u8217'ah}) but from a novel reading of the verse. He asks: {\i v\u8217'chi ya\u8217'aleh al da\u8217'atcha} \u8212- would it enter your mind that someone who killed another person would be {\i tamei} for seven days even if he had no physical contact with the body? Surely the verse {\i kol horeg nefesh} cannot mean one who shot an arrow from a distance. It must refer to killing with a sword \u8212- {\i horeg nefesh b\u8217'cherev} \u8212- in which case the verse teaches that the sword itself becomes like the corpse, and its bearer is {\i tamei tumas shivah} through the contact with the sword. From there the {\i Rambam} derives that all vessels that touched a {\i meis} \u8212- {\i bein klei matechos, bein klei shetef uvgadim}, whether metal, cloth, or other materials \u8212- share this status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion thus yields three distinct positions. The first restricts {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} to metal vessels ({\i klei matechos}) and holds that touching them creates {\i tumas shivah}; the question then is what follows for a {\i Nazir} and a {\i Kohen}. The second position \u8212- {\i Rabbeinu Tam}\u8217's, which is rejected {\i l\u8217'halacha} \u8212- holds that if {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} applies to metal vessels, the {\i Nazir} is {\i megale\u8217'ach} and the {\i Kohen} is {\i muzhar}; therefore the {\i Mishnah} cannot be speaking of metal at all. The third position \u8212- {\i Rabbeinu Chaim HaKohen}\u8217's, representing the accepted ruling \u8212- holds that {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} does apply to metal vessels and does create {\i tumas shivah}, but the {\i Nazir} does not shave and the {\i Kohen} is not {\i muzhar} even for metal. And the {\i Rambam} goes furthest of all: {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} applies to all vessels regardless of material, but here too the {\i Nazir} does not shave. The {\i Rambam}\u8217's additional ruling \u8212- that even the obligation of {\i shlishi v\u8217'shvii} sprinkling does not apply to a {\i Nazir} in this case, despite the seven-day {\i tumah} \u8212- is, frankly, a striking {\i halacha} that demands further attention of its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The common thread across all positions that have halachic standing is the impossibility of the alternative. If touching any vessel or any metal vessel ever in contact with a {\i meis} were to impose biblical prohibitions on {\i Kohanim} with the full force of {\i tumas meis}, no {\i Kohen} could function in ordinary life. The {\i reductio ad absurdum} of {\i Rabbeinu Chaim HaKohen} \u8212- what house can you build for me? \u8212- captures the underlying principle: {\i halacha} cannot be read in a way that makes normative life for a {\i Kohen} simply impossible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaitic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Box Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now proposes to resolve the underlying {\i avira}-versus-{\i gusha} question by reference to a {\i tannaitic} dispute: {\i lema katannai} \u8212- let us say this is a debate among {\i Tanna\u8217'im}. The case is: {\i haniknas l\u8217'eretz ha\u8217'amim b\u8217'shidah tevah u\u8217'migdal} \u8212- one who enters {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} while enclosed in a large box, crate, or tower. {\i Rabbi} (Judah HaNasi) says he is {\i tamei}; {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda} says he is {\i tahor}. On its face, this looks like an exact parallel to the {\i avira}-versus-{\i gusha} question. The box, as we have noted, would constitute an {\i ohel} between the person and the ground. If one holds {\i gusha}, the person should be protected and {\i tahor}. If one holds {\i avira}, the open-topped box leaves the person exposed to the airspace and {\i tamei}. {\i Rabbi}\u8217's position that he is {\i tamei} would thus reflect {\i avira}, and {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position that he is {\i tahor} would reflect {\i gusha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the straightforward reading \u8212- {\i mai lav}, the {\i Gemara} asks, is {\i Rabbi} not holding {\i mishum avira}, and {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda} holding {\i mishum gusha}? If so, we have our resolution: the {\i tannaitic} debate maps directly onto the {\i avira}-versus-{\i gusha} question, and the debate remains unresolved at the {\i tannaitic} level. But the {\i Gemara} does not rest with this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ohel Zaruk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De\u8217'chulei alma mishum gusha} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} proposes that in fact both {\i Tanna\u8217'im} agree the issue is the ground, not the airspace. The disagreement is instead about whether the box, which is being transported, constitutes a valid {\i ohel}. A moving {\i ohel} \u8212- one that is being carried rather than standing stationary \u8212- is called an {\i ohel zaruk}, a \u8220"thrown\u8221" or \u8220"projected\u8221" {\i ohel}. The question is: {\i mar savar ohel zaruk shemei ohel u\u8217'mar savar lo shemei ohel} \u8212- one {\i Tanna} holds that a moving structure constitutes a valid {\i ohel} and therefore protects the person inside; the other holds it does not. On this reading, the dispute is not about the source of {\i tumas eretz ha\u8217'amim} at all, but about a general principle in {\i hilchos ohel} \u8212- one that has implications far beyond {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim}, including, as was noted, the question of aircraft today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited in which {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda} himself teaches that if a box filled with {\i keilim} is thrown in front of a {\i meis} so that it flies over it as an {\i ohel}, the {\i keilim} inside are {\i tamei} \u8212- because the flying box does not constitute an {\i ohel}. If the same box were stationary, suspended on poles above the {\i meis}, the {\i keilim} inside would be {\i tahor}, because then it is a genuine {\i ohel}. This {\i baraita} appears to demonstrate that {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda} himself holds {\i ohel zaruk lo shemei ohel} \u8212- and that would seemingly contradict his position that the person in the transported box in {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} resolves this by distinguishing between degrees of {\i zaruk}. A box that is literally thrown through the air \u8212- or a {\i talis} flapping in the wind with no support from the ground \u8212- is a classic {\i ohel zaruk} and is {\i lo shemei ohel} by everyone\u8217's agreement. A box being carried by bearers who stand on the ground is a more ambiguous case: it is moving, and thus not fully stationary, but it is anchored to the ground through the bodies of those carrying it. The debate between {\i Rabbi} and {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda} may then concern precisely this intermediate case of a transported, but ground-anchored, box \u8212- not the extreme case of a box in free flight.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Transport Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then advances to a third possible framework for the debate, and before presenting the {\i baraita} that supports it, it is worth flagging a conceptual difficulty in the second framework (the {\i lo shechiach} account). The principle {\i davar lo shchiach lo gazru beih rabbanan} \u8212- that the Sages did not apply a rabbinic decree to unusual, rare cases \u8212- is a rule about prohibiting {\i actions}. When the Sages prohibit a category of conduct, they intend their prohibition for the normal range of cases; if a situation is so unusual that it was simply not contemplated, the prohibition may not apply. But applying this principle to the {\i status} of {\i tumas avira} is conceptually jarring. If the Sages declared the airspace of {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} to be {\i tamei}, that is a categorical assignment of {\i tumah} to a space \u8212- it is not a prohibition of a particular action. To then say that one can be within that airspace through an unusual method and thereby escape the {\i tumah} is a strange application of the {\i lo shechiach} principle: it is not prohibiting a rare action, it is selectively withdrawing a status based on the rarity of the method of contact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transport Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} reads: {\i haniknas l\u8217'eretz ha\u8217'amim b\u8217'shidah tevah u\u8217'migdal, tahor} \u8212- one who enters {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} in a box or tower is {\i tahor}. {\i U\u8217'vakaron u\u8217'visfinah u\u8217'veis\u8217'karya, tamei} \u8212- but one who enters by wagon, boat, or {\i eis\u8217'karya} (apparently the mast or rigging of a ship) is {\i tamei}. The distinction the {\i baraita} draws between these two groups is the central question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Normalcy Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially think the difference relates to {\i ohel} status \u8212- perhaps a wagon or boat does not constitute an {\i ohel} while the box does. But this analysis does not hold. Both a box and a wagon can provide the same physical separation from the ground. Both can, in principle, be analyzed under the {\i ohel zaruk} framework. The only consistent difference between the two groups is the normalcy of the mode of transport. How does one normally enter {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim}? By wagon, by boat, by ship. These are the standard means of travel. Being carried through {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim} enclosed in a large box is by contrast an entirely unusual scenario. The {\i baraita}\u8217's distinction, then, supports the interpretation that the {\i tumah} is {\i avira}-based, but was decreed only for normal modes of entry into the airspace \u8212- not for the unusual case of the box.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Leaning Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers yet a third possibility: {\i ve\u8217'iba\u8217'it eima, hacha shema yotzi rosho verubo lesham pligi} \u8212- if you wish, say that the debate concerns a different issue altogether. On this reading, everyone agrees that {\i eretz ha\u8217'amim}\u8217's {\i tumah} is {\i gusha}-based, and everyone agrees that the box constitutes an {\i ohel} that would normally protect its occupant. Nevertheless, one might say the person in the box is {\i tamei} because of a presumption that he will lean out \u8212- that he will extend his head or the majority of his body outside the box, at which point even the {\i gusha}-based {\i tumah} would reach him through normal {\i ohel} logic. The {\i Hachamim} who rule him {\i tamei} immediately may be acting on this presumption: we are sufficiently confident he will lean out that we treat him as {\i tamei} from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosei Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} brings a {\i baraita} in which {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda} explicitly addresses this: {\i hanichnas l\u8217'eretz ha\u8217'amim b\u8217'shidah tevah u\u8217'migdal tahor, ad sheyotzi lesham rosho o rubo} \u8212- one who enters in a box is {\i tahor} until he actually extends his head or the majority of his body outside, at which point he becomes {\i tamei}. This formulation strongly suggests that the operative concern is indeed the act of leaning out, and that {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda} refuses to presume it in advance \u8212- he requires it to actually occur. The {\i Hachamim} who rule {\i tamei} immediately presumably accept the presumption and act on it preemptively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if this is really about {\i gusha} and {\i ohel} logic, why does it require specifically {\i rosho verubo} \u8212- the head or majority of the body \u8212- to trigger {\i tumah}? Even a small part of the body extending beyond the {\i ohel} might seem sufficient. The {\i Gemara} does not fully resolve this tension, and it remains a difficulty with the {\i gusha}-leaning interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We thus have a range of interpretive possibilities for the {\i tannaitic} debate and for the underlying question of {\i tumas eretz ha\u8217'amim}. The first possibility: the debate is directly about {\i avira} versus {\i gusha} \u8212- {\i Rabbi} holds {\i avira} and therefore rules the person in the box {\i tamei} despite the {\i ohel}, while {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Rabbi Yehuda} holds {\i gusha} and rules him {\i tahor} because the {\i ohel} protects him. The second possibility: both agree the issue is {\i gusha}, but disagree whether the transported box constitutes an {\i ohel zaruk} that fails to protect. The third, supported by the {\i baraita} about wagon, boat, and mast: both agree the issue is {\i avira}, but the decree applies only to normal modes of entry \u8212- the box is too unusual a method to have been included. The fourth: both agree the issue is {\i gusha} and the box is a valid {\i ohel}, but one {\i Tanna} presumes the occupant will lean out and rules him {\i tamei} preemptively, while the other requires the leaning to actually occur. Each of these frameworks carries different implications for how broadly or narrowly {\i tumas eretz ha\u8217'amim} applies \u8212- and each illuminates a different dimension of what the Sages were trying to accomplish with this remarkable decree.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 55\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metzora Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens at the bottom of 55a, five lines from the bottom, with the words {\i umatchil umoneh}. The {\i mishnah} presents two halachic situations involving a {\i nazir} whose days do not count toward his {\i nezirut}. The first concerns someone who is {\i tamei tumas meis} only {\i derabbanan} \u8212- or subject to one of those forms of {\i tumas meis} that does not require the {\i nazir} to shave, such as {\i golel}, {\i dofek}, or {\i kelim} \u8212- and the second concerns a {\i metzora} who is in the process of becoming {\i tahor}. In both cases, the earlier days of {\i nezirut} are not destroyed, but the days during the period of {\i tumah} do not count toward the {\i nezirut}. This makes sense for {\i tumas meis}, since {\i tumah} of that kind is fundamentally antithetical to {\i nezirut}. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's inclusion of the {\i metzora} requires elaboration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Metzora Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are three stages a {\i metzora} passes through, and it is important to understand each one clearly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A person develops a suspicious mark and must present himself to a {\i kohen}. The {\i kohen} examines the mark and, if uncertain, declares a waiting period of one week. During this week the person is called a {\i musgar} \u8212- quarantined, under observation \u8212- and is considered {\i tamei} with a full level of {\i tumah}. At the end of the week, the {\i kohen} returns. If the mark has cleared, the person simply immerses in a {\i mikveh} and is {\i tahor}; he does not become {\i tamei} retroactively. If the mark has not cleared, the {\i kohen} declares him a {\i muchlat} \u8212- a definite, fully confirmed {\i metzora}. The word {\i muchlat} conveys absoluteness, in contrast to the tentative status of {\i musgar}. Once declared {\i muchlat}, the person remains in this state indefinitely, until the mark clears.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tahara Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the mark clears, the {\i kohen} comes and examines the {\i metzora} and confirms the healing: {\i vehineh nirpah nega hatzara\u8217'as min hatzarua}. At that point an elaborate {\i tahara} process begins. Two birds are taken, one is slaughtered over a clay vessel containing spring water, and the living bird together with cedar wood, crimson thread, and hyssop is dipped in the blood and water and used to sprinkle the {\i metzora} seven times. The {\i metzora} then launders his garments, shaves all his hair \u8212- {\i kol se\u8217'aro}, not only the hair of the head but all bodily hair \u8212- immerses in water, and returns to the camp. At this point, however, the process is not yet complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamro vs.\u160 ?Sipro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The period during which the {\i metzora} is {\i muchlat} \u8212- that is, the indefinite quarantine from the time he is declared a confirmed {\i metzora} until the mark clears \u8212- is called {\i yemei gamro}. The stage that follows the initial {\i tahara} ritual is called {\i yemei sipro}, the seven-day counting period. During {\i yemei sipro}, the {\i metzora} dwells outside his tent but inside the camp. After those seven days, he shaves all his hair a second time, launders his garments, and immerses. Only then is he ready for the final stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Purification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the eighth day following {\i yemei sipro}, the {\i metzora} brings a {\i korban}. The Torah specifies: {\i uv\u8217'yom hashemini yikach shtei torim o shenei venei yona} \u8212- two doves or two pigeons \u8212- along with additional offerings, completing the {\i tahara} process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the full arc: first, the {\i musgar} stage, an uncertain quarantine; if confirmed, the {\i muchlat} period ({\i yemei gamro}), indefinite until the mark clears; then the {\i tahara} ritual of birds, sprinkling, first shaving, and return to the camp; then {\i yemei sipro}, seven days of counting; then a second shaving, immersion, and finally the {\i korban}. Our {\i mishnah} stated that during {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sipro}, the days do not count toward the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i nezirut}, though they do not destroy the earlier days either.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Chisda\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Short vs.\u160 ?Long\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a statement of Rav Chisda: {\i lo shanu ela bi\u8217'nezirut mu\u8217'etet} \u8212- the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that the days of {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sipro} do not count toward the {\i nezirut} applies only to a short {\i nezirut}. But {\i aval bi\u8217'nezirut merubah masak nami salikan} \u8212- in the case of a long {\i nezirut}, those days do indeed count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Growth Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What lies behind this distinction? Rav Chisda\u8217's actual position is that fundamentally those days {\i do} count. After all, a {\i metzora} is not {\i tamei meis} \u8212- why should his status affect the counting of {\i nezirut}? The reason the {\i mishnah} says they don\u8217't count is not a matter of principle but of practical necessity: the {\i nazir} will always require thirty days of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} \u8212- hair growth \u8212- before his {\i nezirut} can conclude, and the {\i metzora} process requires shaving twice, leaving him with no hair. So if a {\i nazir} had completed five days of a thirty-day {\i nezirut} and then became a {\i metzora}, there is no point in saying those middle days count, because no matter how many days he had banked, he will need a full thirty days of hair growth after the shaving. In practice, therefore, saying the days count would never help him \u8212- it is effectively as though they do not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, in a {\i nezirut merubah} \u8212- a long {\i nezirut}, say of one hundred days or, as the {\i Gemara} will illustrate, fifty days \u8212- the situation differs. Suppose the {\i nazir} had completed twenty days before becoming a {\i metzora}, and the entire {\i metzora} process takes another fifteen days or so. He will then have thirty days remaining anyway, providing the required {\i gidul se\u8217'ar}. In such a case, those middle days can in principle count, since there is no practical obstacle. This is what Rav Chisda means: the real {\i halacha} is that the days count; the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that they do not applies only where, practically speaking, it makes no difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sharvaya\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sharvaya challenges this. He points to the language of the {\i mishnah} itself: {\i nitdakah lo manin miyad} \u8212- as soon as he becomes {\i tahor} he begins counting immediately. This phrasing implies that he does not lose his earlier days. But if Rav Chisda\u8217's reasoning is merely about the practical need for thirty days of hair growth, then in the case of a short {\i nezirut}, the {\i metzora} would not merely fail to count his middle days \u8212- he would actually lose his earlier days as well, because he is going to need a full thirty days regardless. The {\i mishnah}, however, says the earlier days are not lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consequently, the {\i mishnah} must be speaking of a case where those earlier days are genuinely preserved \u8212- which means it cannot be speaking of a simple thirty-day {\i nezirut}. It must be speaking of a long {\i nezirut}. And even so \u8212- even in that long {\i nezirut} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} says the middle days do not count. This directly contradicts Rav Chisda, who said they do count in a long {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fifty-Day Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hypothetical Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda attempts to resolve this. He responds: {\i hu mative lah ve-hu mefarek lah} \u8212- he raises the objection and he answers it himself. The {\i mishnah}, he suggests, is describing a fifty-day {\i nezirut}. The {\i nazir} completed twenty days before becoming a {\i metzora}, then went through the shaving process, and afterward would do another thirty days \u8212- {\i de-nazir de-it lei gidul se\u8217'ar}. In this configuration, Rav Chisda can maintain his position: the earlier twenty days are not lost (which is why the {\i mishnah} says he starts counting immediately), and yet the middle days do not count \u8212- not as a matter of principle, but because they would not help him anyway, since he still needs thirty days at the end. The {\i mishnah} thus describes a case where the days are neither {\i soter} (destroying earlier days) nor {\i oleh} (counting toward the total), but only because of the {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Real Halacha Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem with this approach is that it requires an oddly narrow reading of the {\i mishnah}, tailored specifically to a fifty-day {\i nezirut}. More importantly, Rav Chisda\u8217's assertion is that fundamentally the days {\i are} counted toward the {\i nezirut} \u8212- it is only the practical constraint of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} that prevents them from helping. But the {\i Gemara} is about to demonstrate from a {\i baraita} and from a complex case involving double uncertainty that fundamentally {\i ein olim lo} \u8212- those days simply do not count, irrespective of whether the {\i nazir} will or will not have enough days of hair growth at the end.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Double Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a striking case: {\i nazir shehayah tamei besafek umuchlat besafek} \u8212- a {\i nazir} who may have contracted {\i tumas meis} (in the manner that would require shaving and nullify earlier days), and who may also be a {\i muchlat metzora}. These two conditions of uncertainty overlap, creating a complex halachic problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rishonim} note a conceptual difficulty: can one be a {\i muchlat} in a state of {\i safek}? {\i Tzara\u8217'at} is unique in that a person only acquires the status of {\i metzora} through the ruling of a {\i kohen}. The {\i kohen} examines the mark and either declares the person {\i tamei} or {\i tahor}. There are prescribed rules governing how the {\i kohen} rules in cases of doubt, but once those rules are applied, the person\u8217's status is definite. So there is no true {\i safek muchlat} in the ordinary sense. The {\i rishonim} understand the case as involving, for example, uncertainty about whether the {\i kohen} who ruled was a valid {\i kohen} \u8212- some suggest the {\i kohen} may have been a {\i safek challal} \u8212- or some other meta-level doubt about the validity of the ruling itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timeline: 60 & 120 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} in this double-{\i safek} case is: {\i ochel be-kodashim le-achar shishim yom} \u8212- he may eat {\i kodashim} after sixty days; {\i ve-shoseh yayin u-mitamei le-meitim le-achar me\u8217'ah ve-esrim yom} \u8212- he may drink wine and contract {\i tumas meis} (that is, he may complete and conclude his {\i nezirut}) after one hundred and twenty days. These numbers require explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First, note that the sixty-day threshold is about removing any doubt of {\i metzora} status, since a {\i metzora} cannot eat {\i kodashim}. Being {\i tahor} of {\i tumas meis} is a separate matter \u8212- the sprinkling on days three and seven of the {\i parah adumah} ashes resolves that. A {\i nazir tamei} who has completed the sprinkling is {\i tahor} for the purpose of eating {\i kodashim}, even if he has not yet brought his {\i korban} or done the {\i giluach} of {\i nezirut tumah} and restarted his {\i nezirut tahara}. The sixty days are therefore needed to resolve the {\i metzora} doubt, not the {\i tumas meis} doubt. The remaining sixty days \u8212- from day sixty to day one hundred and twenty \u8212- are then needed to fully conclude the {\i nezirut} under conditions of uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Triple Shaving Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand how these numbers are derived, one must trace the three required {\i giluachim} through the double-{\i safek} case. The {\i nazir} might be a {\i nazir tahor} who cannot shave until the end of his {\i nezirut}. At the same time, if he is a {\i metzora}, the {\i mitzvah} of {\i giluach} for {\i metzora} overrides the prohibition against shaving during {\i nezirut} \u8212- but only if he is definitely a {\i metzora}. Since he is only a {\i safek metzora}, there is no overriding justification. Therefore, he must wait thirty days before the first {\i giluach}, so that if he is a {\i nazir tahor}, those thirty days will suffice for his {\i nezirut} to conclude and he may shave. He brings an {\i olah} with a {\i tenai} \u8212- if he is a {\i nazir tahor}, this is his {\i korban olah}; if not, it is a mere {\i nedavah} \u8212- and shaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After thirty days and the first {\i giluach}, if he is a {\i nazir tamei}, he still needs thirty more days before the second {\i giluach} (which would serve for {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i yemei sipro} or for the {\i nazir tamei} shaving). He cannot assume those thirty days counted, since he might have been a {\i nazir tahor} and those days were {\i yemei gamro} or {\i yemei sipro} \u8212- {\i ein olim lo}. Thus, another thirty days pass and a second {\i giluach} is performed. At sixty days, he is free of any {\i metzora} status doubt and may eat {\i kodashim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaving Non-Doubling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But he cannot declare his {\i nezirut} concluded at sixty days, because the shaving done for {\i metzora} does not simultaneously count as the shaving for {\i nezirut}. This is the critical {\i halacha}: if a person was genuinely both a {\i metzora} and a {\i nazir}, the {\i giluach} performed as a {\i metzora} cannot be credited as the {\i giluach} for the {\i nazir}. They are two separate requirements and the same act cannot discharge both. Consequently, even if the first two shavings resolved his {\i metzora} status, if he was truly a {\i metzora} then those shavings were for {\i metzora} and did not serve the {\i nazir}. And since he might have been a {\i metzora}, we cannot credit those shavings toward his {\i nezirut}. He must therefore wait yet another thirty days and perform a third {\i giluach} \u8212- bringing the total to one hundred and twenty days \u8212- before concluding his {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} confirms: if it were a year-long {\i nezirut} and the same double {\i safek} applied, the numbers scale accordingly. {\i Ochel be-kodashim le-achar shtei shanim} \u8212- it would take two years to resolve the {\i metzora} doubt; {\i ve-shohai et ha-metama le-meitim le-achar arba shanim} \u8212- and four years to conclude the {\i nezirut}. This last figure is telling. If {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sipro} fundamentally counted toward the {\i nezirut}, then once the {\i metzora} doubt is resolved after two years, the {\i nazir} would need only thirty additional days to conclude his {\i nazir tahor} status, and another year for {\i nazir tamei}, yielding three years and thirty days \u8212- not four. The fact that four full years are required demonstrates that none of the days during {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sipro} count toward the {\i nezirut} at all. This directly refutes Rav Chisda\u8217's position that the days fundamentally count and the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling is merely a practical consequence of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises the question: {\i ein li ela yemei tumah she-ein olim lo min ha-minyan} \u8212- I know only that the days of {\i tumas meis} (in the case that is {\i soter}, i.e., that destroys earlier days) do not count. {\i Yemei chaluto minayin?} \u8212- from where do I know that {\i yemei chaluto}, the days when the {\i nazir} is a {\i muchlat metzora}, do not count? What is the biblical source for this rule?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Graveyard Nazir Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts a {\i kal va-chomer}. At the end of the {\i tumas meis} period, the {\i nazir} shaves and brings a {\i korban}; similarly, at the end of the {\i metzora} period, there is shaving and a {\i korban}. By analogy, just as {\i yemei tumah} do not count, {\i yemei chaluto} should not count either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the analogy is challenged: {\i tumas meis} is weightier \u8212- it destroys earlier days \u8212- and that is why it does not count toward new days. {\i Tzara\u8217'at}, by contrast, does not destroy earlier days and is therefore less weighty. Perhaps then {\i yemei chaluto} do count?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds with a {\i kal va-chomer} in the other direction: consider the {\i nazir be-kever} \u8212- a person who accepts {\i nezirut} while standing in a graveyard. Since he was already {\i tamei meis} when he began his {\i nezirut}, he never had a period as a {\i nazir tahor} who subsequently became {\i tamei}, and therefore he is not required to undergo the shaving and {\i korban} of {\i nezirut tumah}. His hair, the very hair on his head when he accepted the {\i nezirut}, is preserved \u8212- {\i se\u8217'aro ra\u8217'ui le-tiglachat nezirut} \u8212- it will be the hair shaved at the conclusion of his {\i nezirut tahara}. And yet, {\i ein olim lo min ha-minyan} \u8212- while he remains {\i tamei meis}, those days do not count. This is despite the fact that his hair is fully intact and will serve the final {\i nezirut} shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now compare: a {\i nazir} who is a {\i muchlat metzora} \u8212- his hair will definitely be shaved off before his {\i nezirut} concludes. The hair does not survive to the final {\i giluach} of {\i nezirut}. If even the {\i nazir be-kever}, whose hair is preserved, has days that do not count \u8212- how much more so ({\i lo kol she-ken}) should {\i yemei chaluto} not count, where the hair itself is going to be shaved away, representing a fundamental challenge to the {\i nazir}\u8217's hair-preserving {\i nezirut} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaving & Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} identifies the distinguishing features that render {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sipro} as {\i ein olim lo}: the requirement for {\i tiglachat} (shaving) and for bringing a {\i korban}. These parallel the defining characteristics of a {\i nazir tamei} whose days do not count \u8212- shaving and a {\i korban}. It is this dual requirement that marks the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i muchlat} and {\i sipro} periods as analogous to {\i tumas meis} for purposes of {\i nezirut} counting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Musgar Stage Counts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then asks: {\i yachol af yemei hasgiro?} \u8212- perhaps the same rule applies to the first stage, when the {\i nazir} is a {\i musgar}? The answer is no. {\i Im amarta bi\u8217'yemei chaluto shekein chaluto ta\u8217'un tiglachat u-meivi korban} \u8212- in the {\i muchlat} stage there is shaving and a {\i korban}, and that is why those days do not count. {\i Tomar bi\u8217'yemei hasgiro she-ein ta\u8217'un tiglachat ve-eino meivi korban} \u8212- in the {\i musgar} stage there is no shaving and no {\i korban}. The {\i musgar} who is cleared will simply go to the {\i mikveh} and be done. Therefore, {\i l\u8217'fichach ya\u8217'alu le-minyan} \u8212- those days do count toward the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fundamental Non-Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} concludes: {\i yemei sipro ve-yemei gomro ein olim lo min ha-minyan} \u8212- these two stages do not count, because of the shaving and {\i korban}. But {\i yemei hazav ve-hazavah} and {\i hesgeiro shel metzora} \u8212- {\i harei eilu olim lo} \u8212- these do count, because they lack the shaving requirement (or both shaving and {\i korban}) that characterizes the non-counting periods.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i Gemara} deploys this {\i baraita} against Rav Chisda with full force. When the {\i baraita} deliberated whether {\i yemei chaluto} might be {\i oleh min ha-minyan}, it was plainly contemplating a case in which the {\i nazir} would have his thirty days of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} at the end regardless \u8212- that is, a {\i nezirut merubah}. There would be no reason to consider the possibility of {\i oleh} if we were dealing with a short {\i nezirut}, because in that case the {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} problem alone would answer the question. The {\i baraita}\u8217's discussion thus assumes a long {\i nezirut} \u8212- and yet it concludes {\i ein olim lo min ha-minyan}. {\i Alma lo silik lei} \u8212- clearly, in principle they do not count. The {\i baraita} is not concerned with {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} as the operative reason; it assumes the {\i nazir} will get his {\i gidul se\u8217'ar}, and still rules that the days do not count. {\i Shema mina} \u8212- this is a definitive proof against Rav Chisda.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 55b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metzora & Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} on this {\i daf} discusses the status of a {\i nazir} who finds himself simultaneously subject to another form of ritual impurity or process that prevents him from completing his {\i nezirut}. Before engaging with the {\i Gemara\u8217's} analysis, it is worth clarifying the three distinct stages of a {\i metzora\u8217's} progression, since the {\i mishna\u8217's} ruling depends critically on understanding them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a person develops a suspicious skin condition, a {\i kohen} must examine it. If the {\i kohen} cannot render an immediate determination, he declares the individual a {\i musgar} \u8212- quarantined for observation \u8212- and returns after a week to reassess. During this first stage, the individual is {\i tamei} at a full level of {\i tumah}, yet the situation remains tentative: the {\i kohen} has not rendered a definitive verdict. If upon the second inspection the condition has not resolved, the {\i kohen} declares him a {\i muchlat} \u8212- a confirmed, definitive {\i metzora}. The term {\i muchlat} conveys the absolute, locked-in nature of the status: there is no longer any ambiguity about his condition. This period of confirmed {\i metzora} status, which continues until the condition clears, is referred to in the {\i mishna} as {\i yemei gamro}. During {\i yemei gamro} the {\i metzora} is sent outside the camp entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the condition clears, the {\i kohen} examines the individual and initiates the {\i tahara} process. On the day the condition is found to have healed, the {\i kohen} performs the bird ritual \u8212- taking two living, {\i tahor} birds, cedar wood, crimson thread, and hyssop; slaughtering one bird over an earthenware vessel containing spring water; and sprinkling the blood seven times upon the person being purified. The individual then shaves all his hair, immerses in a {\i mikveh}, and is permitted to re-enter the camp, but must remain outside his tent for seven days. This seven-day counting period that follows the initial ritual is called {\i yemei sifro}. On the seventh day of {\i yemei sifro}, he shaves all his hair a second time, immerses again, and on the eighth day brings his {\i korbanot}. Only then is he fully {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure, then, is as follows: {\i musgar} \u8212- the first tentative quarantine stage; {\i muchlat} and {\i yemei gamro} \u8212- the confirmed indefinite period of {\i metzora} status until the condition clears; and {\i yemei sifro} \u8212- the seven-day counting period following the initial {\i tahara} ritual, culminating in a second shaving and the offering of {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our {\i mishna} establishes that during {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sifro}, the days do not count toward a {\i nazir\u8217's} tally of {\i nezirut}, though they do not destroy ({\i soter}) the days already counted beforehand. This parallels the rule for a {\i nazir} who is {\i tamei meis} in a way that does not trigger {\i giluch} and {\i korban} \u8212- such as one who contracted a rabbinic-level {\i tumas meis}, or who touched a {\i golel}, {\i dofek}, or {\i kelim} that had been in contact with a corpse. In those cases, the days of {\i tumah} do not count, but neither do they nullify the earlier days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if the {\i metzora\u8217's} condition was caught at the {\i musgar} stage and cleared without advancing to {\i muchlat}, the episode is treated like a {\i zav} or {\i zava} \u8212- it has no effect whatsoever on the {\i nazirut}. Similarly, a {\i nazir} who becomes a {\i zav} or {\i zava} experiences no interruption to his {\i nezirut}. The {\i mishna\u8217's} ruling of {\i ein olim lo} \u8212- the days do not count \u8212- applies specifically to {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sifro}, the stages characterized by {\i giluach} and {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Chisda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda\u8217's Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records the opinion of Rav Chisda, who distinguishes between a short and a long {\i nezirut}: {\i lo shanu ela binezirut mu\u8217'etet, aval binezirut merubah misak nami salikan lei} \u8212- the rule that those days do not count was stated only regarding a short {\i nezirut}, but in the case of a long {\i nezirut}, those days do in fact count. This is a striking position, because it essentially challenges the plain reading of the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda\u8217's reasoning is rooted in the requirement of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} \u8212- thirty days of hair growth \u8212- that must precede the final {\i giluach} of a {\i nazir tahor}. A {\i nazir} who goes through the {\i metzora} process will have his hair shaved not once but twice before emerging from that process. By the time he is {\i tahor}, his head is bare. He will therefore require a full thirty days of hair growth regardless of how many days he counted beforehand. In a standard thirty-day {\i nezirut}, then, this practical reality means that the days spent as a {\i metzora} cannot contribute anything meaningful \u8212- even if they \u8220"count\u8221" in principle, you will still need a full thirty days at the end. Hence the {\i mishna\u8217's} language of {\i ein olim lo} is, in Rav Chisda\u8217's view, not a statement of fundamental law but a practical consequence of the {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a long {\i nezirut}, however \u8212- say, one of fifty or one hundred days \u8212- if a {\i nazir} had already completed twenty days before becoming a {\i metzora}, and the {\i metzora} process occupied an additional stretch of time, he may still have enough days remaining that, even accounting for thirty days of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} at the end, those middle days can effectively be credited. In such a case, Rav Chisda holds that they fundamentally do count, and the {\i mishna} simply does not address this scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Mesharsheya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharsheya raises a challenge to Rav Chisda\u8217's position. He observes that the {\i mishna} states {\i ve\u8217'eino mevatel bahen et hakodmin} \u8212- these days do not destroy the earlier ones. He argues: if the {\i mishna} were speaking of a short {\i nezirut}, then it should have said the opposite \u8212- that the earlier days {\i are} destroyed \u8212- because in practice, a {\i nazir} with a thirty-day {\i nezirut} who undergoes this entire process and has his hair shaved twice will need the full thirty days thereafter regardless of what he accumulated beforehand. Saying merely that the middle days {\i do not count} without nullifying the earlier ones presupposes a scenario where one already has days that are worth preserving. That is only meaningful, Rav Mesharsheya argues, in the context of a long {\i nezirut} \u8212- {\i nezirut merubah} \u8212- yet even there, the {\i mishna} rules {\i ein olim lo}. This directly contradicts Rav Chisda, who held that in a {\i nezirut merubah} the days should count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fifty-Day Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda responds by proposing a specific scenario that fits the {\i mishna\u8217's} language of neither {\i soter} nor {\i oleh}: a fifty-day {\i nezirut} in which the {\i nazir} had completed twenty days before becoming a {\i metzora}, then undergoes the {\i metzora} process \u8212- involving two shavings \u8212- and afterward must count another thirty days of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} before the final {\i giluach}. In this case, the twenty days beforehand are preserved and not destroyed, but the middle days of the {\i metzora} process cannot be used either, because the {\i nazir} will need those thirty days at the end regardless. This accounts for the {\i mishna\u8217's} formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda concedes that in a truly long {\i nezirut} \u8212- one where enough days remain that the middle days could be credited and still leave thirty days for {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} at the end \u8212- those middle days would in principle count. He maintains that the {\i mishna\u8217's ein olim lo} is not a fundamental halakhic statement but rather a practical limitation born of the {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} requirement. The rest of the {\i Gemara} will proceed to demonstrate that this position is untenable, and that the days fundamentally do not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Double Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Safek Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a complex case: a {\i nazir} who may have become {\i tamei meis} in a way that would require {\i giluach} and {\i korban} \u8212- that is, a {\i tumah} of the {\i soter} variety \u8212- and who is also a {\i muchlat b\u8217'safek}, a doubtful confirmed {\i metzora}. The intersection of these two {\i safekot} creates a situation where multiple {\i giluchim} may be required under different conditions, but performing any one of them without justification could violate the {\i nezirut} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask how a person can be a {\i muchlat b\u8217'safek}, since the {\i halacha} of {\i tzaraas} is that the status of {\i metzora} is determined entirely by the {\i kohen\u8217's} declaration \u8212- once the {\i kohen} renders a ruling according to the established procedures, the status is definitive. The {\i rishonim} acknowledge this difficulty and note that a precise scenario is hard to identify; one possible construction is that there was doubt about the {\i kohen\u8217's} own qualifications. Regardless of the exact framing, the {\i Gemara} treats the double {\i safek} as a valid case and proceeds to work through its consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sixty to 120 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rules that such a {\i nazir} may eat {\i kodshim} only after sixty days, and may drink wine and contract {\i tumas meis} \u8212- that is, fully complete his {\i nezirut} \u8212- only after one hundred and twenty days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is as follows. In order to eat {\i kodshim}, the {\i nazir} need not yet fully resolve his {\i nazir tamei} status \u8212- from a standpoint of {\i tumah v\u8217'taharah}, once he has undergone the sprinkling of {\i parah adumah} on days three and seven, he is fully {\i tahor} and may eat {\i kodashim} even without yet bringing his {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut}. What must be resolved first is his possible {\i metzora} status, since a {\i metzora} cannot eat {\i kodshim}. It takes sixty days to exit the {\i metzora} status in light of the {\i safek}, for reasons the {\i Gemara} will explain. Completing the {\i nezirut} entirely, however \u8212- which requires resolving the {\i nazir tamei} and {\i nazir tahor} obligations \u8212- takes a full one hundred and twenty days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Korbanos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} and {\i Tosafot} work through the mechanics in considerable detail. The procedure unfolds across multiple thirty-day intervals, with each {\i giluach} separated by thirty days because of the need for {\i gidul se\u8217'ar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Initially, the individual cannot shave immediately upon the clearing of the condition, because he may be a {\i nazir tahor} \u8212- and a {\i nazir tahor} may not have his hair cut before the completion of his {\i nezirut}. Therefore one must wait thirty days before performing the first {\i giluach}, ensuring that even if he were a {\i nazir tahor}, the minimal period required has elapsed. At that point, a conditional {\i korban olah} is brought: if he is a {\i nazir tahor}, this serves as his {\i korban} for {\i nezirut}; if not, it is a voluntary {\i nedava}. The {\i halacha} that {\i gilach al echad min hadamim yatza} \u8212- even a single {\i korban} can serve {\i bedi\u8217'eved} to complete the {\i giluach} of a {\i nazir tahor} \u8212- allows this to work. After thirty days, the first {\i giluach} is performed, covering both the possible {\i metzora giluach} and the possible {\i nazir tahor} or {\i nazir tamei giluach} as applicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the matter is not resolved. One cannot proceed to the second {\i metzora giluach} \u8212- which would normally take place seven days after the first \u8212- because the {\i nazir} may actually have been a {\i nazir tamei} rather than a {\i nazir tahor}. If so, the first {\i giluach} served only the {\i nazir tamei} role, and thirty more days are needed before the second {\i giluach} can take place for the {\i nazir tahor}. Thus a second {\i giluach} with its own conditional {\i korban} occurs at day sixty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After sixty days and two {\i giluchim}, the {\i metzora} process \u8212- if it was ever real \u8212- is fully complete, and the individual can eat {\i kodshim}. But the {\i nezirut} is not yet done. If the individual was actually both a {\i metzora} and a {\i nazir tamei}, then the {\i halacha} is that the {\i giluach} of a {\i metzora} does not count toward the {\i giluach} of a {\i nazir}; they cannot serve double duty. This means the first sixty days of {\i giluachim} may not have counted for the {\i nazir} at all in such a scenario. The individual must therefore wait another thirty days and perform a third {\i giluach}, and then yet another thirty days with a fourth {\i giluach}, to cover all possible configurations. In each interval, conditional {\i korbanot} are brought with the appropriate {\i tnai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key principle underlying this entire structure, and the one the {\i Gemara} intends to prove through it, is {\i ein olim lo} \u8212- the days of {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sifro} do not count toward the {\i nezirut}. The fact that the concluding period requires not a mere thirty days but a full doubling of the {\i nezirut} period demonstrates that those middle days contribute nothing. This point is pressed against Rav Chisda\u8217's position in the next section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a further proof \u8212- and, more fundamentally, the textual source for the entire principle \u8212- when Rav Ashi poses the question: {\i ein li ela yemei tumah she\u8217'ein olim lo min haminyan} \u8212- I know only that the days of {\i tumah} do not count; {\i yemei chaluto minayin} \u8212- what is the source that the days of {\i yemei gamro} do not count?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to clarify what is meant here. The baseline case that does not count is a full {\i tumas meis} with {\i giluch} \u8212- and that case is actually {\i soter}, not merely {\i ein olim lo}. The question Rav Ashi is asking is grounded in a {\i Midrash Halacha} from the {\i Sifrei}: given that we have a source for the {\i nazir tamei} not counting days, what is the derivation that the {\i metzora\u8217's} days in {\i yemei gamro} also do not count?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i kal vachomer} is proposed. A {\i nazir} who accepts {\i nezirut} upon himself while standing in a graveyard \u8212- a full {\i tamei meis} condition \u8212- does not need to perform the {\i giluach} and {\i korban} of a {\i nazir tamei}, because he never transitioned from {\i tahor} to {\i tamei} during the {\i nezirut}. Crucially, his hair at that point is preserved: it will not need to be shaved as a result of his {\i tumah}, and it remains available for the final {\i giluach} of his {\i nezirut taharah}. Yet even this person, whose hair is intact and whose case appears less disruptive than a {\i metzora\u8217's}, has his days of {\i tumah} not count. How much more so, then, should the days of a {\i nazir muchlat} not count \u8212- for the {\i metzora\u8217's} hair will inevitably be shaved off before the {\i nezirut} is complete, representing a direct contradiction to the integrity of the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument runs: {\i kal vachomer} \u8212- just as the {\i nazir bakever} whose hair is {\i raui letiglachat nezirut} (fit for the final shaving of {\i nezirut}) nonetheless has his days not count, the {\i muchlat} whose hair is {\i she\u8217'ein saro raui letiglachat nezirut} (not fit, because it will be shaved off prematurely) should {\i a fortiori} have his days not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i kal vachomer} is then challenged: {\i tumas meis} is weightier because it destroys the earlier days \u8212- that is why it doesn\u8217't count. But {\i yemei gamro} does not destroy the earlier days, suggesting it is less disruptive and perhaps its days should count. The {\i braita} responds: {\i lo, kal vachomer} \u8212- and reframes the {\i kal vachomer} to rest on the comparison of {\i giluach} and {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} then organizes the categories systematically. {\i Yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sifro} \u8212- the periods characterized by {\i giluach} and {\i korban} obligation \u8212- do not count toward the {\i nezirut}. The analogy to {\i nazir tamei} holds: both involve shaving and {\i korban}, and in both cases those days are {\i ein olim lo min haminyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, the days of a {\i zav}, a {\i zava}, and the {\i musgar} stage of a {\i metzora} all count toward the {\i nezirut}. The distinguishing criterion is the absence of {\i giluach} and {\i korban}. A {\i zav} and {\i zava} do bring a {\i korban} at the conclusion of their status, but they do not shave. The {\i musgar} has neither {\i giluach} nor {\i korban} \u8212- if the condition clears at that stage, he simply immerses in a {\i mikveh} and is {\i tahor}. Therefore, {\i harei elu olim lo} \u8212- those days count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} addresses the question of whether {\i yemei hasgiro} \u8212- the {\i musgar} stage \u8212- should also not count, given that it too involves the same level of {\i tumah} as {\i yemei gamro}. The answer is decisive: it is the {\i giluach} and {\i korban} that render {\i yemei gamro} and {\i yemei sifro} analogous to {\i nazir tamei} and generate the rule of {\i ein olim lo}. Without them, there is no basis to exclude those days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} thus provides the {\i midrash halacha} foundation for the {\i mishna\u8217's} rule: {\i yemei sifro viyemei gamro ein olim lo min haminyan} \u8212- because they bear the halakhic signature of {\i giluach} and {\i korban} that marks days of significant ritual disruption to the {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fundamental Non-Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i braita} in hand, the {\i Gemara} now delivers its decisive refutation of Rav Chisda. The {\i braita} had considered the possibility that {\i yemei gamro} might be {\i oleh min haminyan}. At what point would such a position be coherent? Clearly not in the case of a {\i nezirut mu\u8217'etet} \u8212- a standard thirty-day {\i nezirut} \u8212- because there one would need thirty days of {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} regardless, and the days could never practically count. The only scenario in which one could meaningfully {\i consider} those days as counting is in the case of a {\i nezirut merubah}, where there would be more than thirty days remaining and the {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} requirement could still be satisfied at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i braita} entertains precisely this scenario and then concludes: {\i ein olim lo min haminyan} \u8212- they do not count. {\i Alma lo salik leh} \u8212- the {\i braita\u8217's} conclusion is that fundamentally, as a matter of principle, those days do not count. This has nothing to do with {\i gidul se\u8217'ar}. The {\i braita} is operating in a {\i nezirut merubah} context \u8212- the very scenario Rav Chisda claimed would allow the days to be credited \u8212- and rules that they do not count regardless. {\i Shema mina}: this is a conclusive proof that the {\i halacha} of {\i ein olim lo} is not a practical consequence of the {\i gidul se\u8217'ar} requirement, as Rav Chisda maintained, but a fundamental halakhic principle in its own right.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 56\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nezir & Mikdash Entry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final section of this {\i perek} opens with a {\i mishnah} that draws a striking parallel between two distinct bodies of law: the rules governing when a {\i nazir} must shave his head as a result of {\i tumas hameis}, and the rules governing liability for entering the {\i Beit Hamikdash} in a state of {\i tumah}. Throughout the preceding {\i perek}, we encountered a series of cases in which a {\i nazir} who contracted a form of {\i tumas hameis} was nonetheless exempt from the obligation of {\i nezir megaleach} \u8212- cases such as the {\i metzora} and certain other categories where, despite the {\i tumah} being biblical in nature, the {\i mesorah} indicates that it does not trigger the shaving requirement or nullify the {\i nezirut}. The {\i mishnah} now establishes a general principle: these two domains \u8212- {\i nezir megaleach} and {\i biat mikdash} \u8212- track each other precisely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule, stated by Rabbi Eliezer in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua, runs as follows: {\i kol tumah min hameis shehanazir megaleach aleha chayavim aleha al biat mikdash} \u8212- any {\i tumah} from the dead that causes a {\i nazir} to shave also renders one liable for entering the {\i Beit Hamikdash} while bearing that {\i tumah}. Conversely, {\i kol tumah min hameis she\u8217'ein hanazir megaleach aleha ein chayavim aleha al biat mikdash} \u8212- any {\i tumah} from the dead that does not obligate a {\i nazir} to shave similarly carries no liability for {\i biat mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the practical scope of this rule. {\i Tumas meis} ordinarily permits entry into the {\i Machaneh Leviyah} and even into the {\i Ezrat Nashim}, right up to the {\i Azarah} itself. What is at stake here is therefore entry into the temple precinct proper. The {\i mishnah} thus tells us that those categories of {\i tumas meis} that are exceptional with respect to {\i nezir megaleach} are equally exceptional with respect to {\i biat mikdash} \u8212- they carry no liability even at the biblical level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir registers a sharp objection: {\i lo tehei zu kallah min hasheretz} \u8212- surely this {\i tumah} should not be treated more leniently than touching a dead rodent. His point is intuitive. Even if a particular variety of {\i tumas meis} does not activate the {\i nazir}\u8217's obligation to shave \u8212- perhaps because it is categorized as a lesser or anomalous form \u8212- it is still {\i tumas meis}. And any {\i tumah} whatsoever, even touching a {\i sheretz}, renders a person liable for entering the {\i Beit Hamikdash} in that state. The cases under discussion are not rabbinic enactments like {\i sechochot u\u8217'praot} or {\i Beit Hapras}, where no one would argue the point. These are assumed to be {\i d\u8217'oraita tumot}. How, then, can one say that a person bearing such {\i tumah} is exempt from liability for {\i biat mikdash}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is not primarily interested in the substance of this dispute, but in the transmission of the tradition itself. It notes that the principle attributed in the {\i mishnah} to Rabbi Eliezer in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- the {\i stam} Rabbi Yehoshua being Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananya \u8212- was in fact received through an intermediate link: Rabbi Yehoshua bar Mama. A {\i beraita} fills in the background.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer relates that he once traveled to Ardaskaya, where he encountered Rabbi Yehoshua ben Peter Rosh seated and presenting his analysis before Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Peter Rosh articulated the same rule: {\i tumah} from the dead that causes a {\i nazir} to shave also entails liability for {\i biat mikdash}, and {\i tumah} that does not obligate shaving likewise entails no such liability. Rabbi Meir pushed back with precisely the argument recorded in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- that this {\i tumah} should at minimum be no lighter than {\i tumas sheretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that point, Rabbi Eliezer intervened, asking Rabbi Meir whether he was acquainted with the authority of Rabbi Yehoshua bar Mama. Rabbi Meir confirmed that he was. Rabbi Eliezer then reported: {\i kach amar li Rabbi Yehoshua bar Mama mishum Rabbi Yehoshua} \u8212- this is what Rabbi Yehoshua bar Mama told me in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua, that he had received the tradition {\i shamati} \u8212- I heard \u8212- that any {\i tumah} from the dead that obligates a {\i nazir} to shave also carries liability for {\i biat mikdash}, and any that does not obligate shaving carries no such liability. Rabbi Eliezer thereby backed the position of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Peter Rosh, establishing that it was a received tradition from Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananya himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chain of Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This background raises an obvious question: if Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's immediate source was Rabbi Yehoshua bar Mama, who in turn received it from Rabbi Yehoshua, why does our {\i mishnah} cite Rabbi Eliezer in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua directly, skipping the intermediate link?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} derives from this a general principle about the reporting of traditions: {\i kol shma\u8217'teta d\u8217'mitamra b\u8217'bei tlata} \u8212- any tradition that was transmitted through three stages, {\i kadmaya uvatraya amrinan} \u8212- we cite the first and the last, {\i metzaya la amrinan} \u8212- but the middle link need not always be mentioned. It is not required to enumerate every step in the chain of transmission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak illustrates this with a further example. Nachum HaLavlur declares his tradition going back {\i miRabbi Mayasha shekibel me\u8217'abba shekibel min hazugot shekiblu min hanevi\u8217'im} \u8212- from Rabbi Mayasha who received from his father who received from the {\i zugot} who received from the prophets \u8212- a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} concerning one who plants dill and mustard seed in two or three different locations in a field, that {\i pe\u8217'ah} is given from each separate location. What is instructive here is not the content of this {\i halacha} but the structure of its citation: it proceeds from Nachum HaLavlur all the way back to the prophets, yet it omits Yehoshua and the {\i zekenim} who, according to {\i Pirkei Avot}, stood between Moshe and the prophets in the chain of tradition. The {\i gemara} concludes {\i shema mina}: it is indeed not necessary to cite every intermediate link.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might reasonably ask \u8212- and the {\i gemara} invites this observation \u8212- that Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua were in fact contemporaries, both teachers of Rabbi Akiva. How peculiar, then, that Rabbi Eliezer would receive this tradition three removes away rather than directly from his colleague. Perhaps this reflects circumstances surrounding Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's {\i cherem}, or some other reason they did not communicate directly on this matter. The question is a natural one, and it was noted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same {\i halacha} has a parallel formulation in {\i Masechet Smachot} that is considerably more relevant in practical terms: {\i kol tumah she\u8217'ein hanazir megaleach aleha ein kohein muzhar alav} \u8212- any {\i tumah} that does not obligate a {\i nazir} to shave likewise does not constitute a biblical violation for a {\i kohein}. This raises the broader question \u8212- touched upon throughout the {\i perek} \u8212- of whether certain anomalous categories of {\i tumas meis}, those associated with {\i cherev k\u8217'chalal} or {\i keilim hanoge\u8217'im b\u8217'meit}, also create exceptions in the laws of {\i tumas kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Logic vs.\u160 ?Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} introduces a fascinating exchange between Rabbi Akiva and his teacher Rabbi Eliezer, followed by a contrasting encounter with Rabbi Yehoshua. The discussion touches on a theme that runs through much of the {\i perek}: many of the laws of {\i nezirut} resist logical explanation and appear almost arbitrary. Rabbi Yochanan, as we have seen elsewhere, would often say simply {\i halacha hi b\u8217'Nazir} \u8212- it is a law that applies to the {\i nazir}, with no further derivation offered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva opens by presenting an argument before Rabbi Eliezer. He begins with two established facts. First, a bone the size of a barley grain \u8212- {\i etzem k\u8217'se\u8217'orah} \u8212- does not cause {\i tumah} through {\i ohel}, i.e., being in the same enclosed space as a corpse. It lacks the minimum mass to produce {\i tumas ohel}. And yet, a {\i nazir} who touches or carries such a bone must shave and loses his accumulated {\i nezirut}. Second, a {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood \u8212- {\i revi\u8217'it dam} \u8212- does cause {\i tumas ohel} at the biblical level. A person who is in the same space as a {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood from a corpse becomes {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From these two facts, Rabbi Akiva constructs a {\i kal vachomer}. If a tiny chip of bone that cannot even produce {\i tumas ohel} is nonetheless sufficient to obligate a {\i nazir} to shave when he touches or carries it, then certainly a {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood \u8212- which does produce {\i tumas ohel} \u8212- should at minimum obligate a {\i nazir} to shave when he touches or carries it. It is important to note that Rabbi Akiva carefully limits his argument to {\i maga} and {\i masa} \u8212- touch and carrying \u8212- not {\i ohel}, because the bone only causes liability through those two modes, and a {\i kal vachomer} cannot extend beyond its premise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication is significant. The established rule requires a {\i chatzi log} of blood \u8212- half a {\i log} \u8212- before a {\i nazir} is obligated to shave due to blood. Rabbi Akiva argues this is too high a threshold, at least for touch and carrying. A {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood should suffice for those modes, given that a {\i revi\u8217'it} produces {\i tumas ohel} while the tiny bone does not produce {\i tumas ohel} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer Dismisses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's response is blunt: {\i ma zeh Akiva, ein danim kan mi-kal vachomer} \u8212- what are you doing, Akiva? There is no place for a {\i kal vachomer} here. This amounts to a flat dismissal: these laws are arbitrary, they do not respond to logic, and Rabbi Akiva\u8217's reasoning, however clever, is simply beside the point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua Validates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Unsatisfied with this response, Rabbi Akiva brought his argument to his other teacher, Rabbi Yehoshua. Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's reaction was strikingly different: {\i yafeh amarta} \u8212- you argued well. The argument is valid. And yet, he continued, {\i ela kach amru halacha} \u8212- nonetheless, this is the received tradition, and the tradition stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difference in approach between the two teachers is pedagogically significant. Rabbi Eliezer simply rejected the argument without engaging it. Rabbi Yehoshua validated the argument intellectually while explaining why it ultimately cannot prevail: the domain in question is governed not by logical inference but by {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} \u8212- a tradition transmitted from Sinai. A good argument does not override a received tradition; it simply illuminates why logic alone is insufficient here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This exchange is not merely a disagreement about a specific {\i shiur} of blood. It reflects a deeper tension about the nature of {\i Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh}. Rabbi Eliezer, by his own example and by the famous characterization of him in rabbinic literature, never transmitted anything that he had not received from his teacher. His entire orientation is toward received tradition. Rabbi Akiva, by contrast, represents the power of human intellect and logical derivation \u8212- the idea that law can and should be reasoned out from principles, not merely inherited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This tension has an older precedent. When the question arose whether a {\i korban Pesach} overrides {\i Shabbat} when {\i Erev Pesach} falls on {\i Shabbat}, Hillel HaBavli spent the entire day presenting {\i kal vachomers} and analogies to the assembled authorities, and they would not accept his conclusion. It was only when he said {\i kach mikublani miShemaya v\u8217'Avtalyon} \u8212- I have received this as a tradition from Shemaya and Avtalyon \u8212- that they accepted the ruling. The implication is that tradition remains the ultimate authority, even when logic independently arrives at the same conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is in this spirit that Rabbi Eliezer was more dismissive of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i kal vachomer}. For Rabbi Eliezer, the very act of deploying a logical argument in a domain governed by tradition was a category error. Rabbi Yehoshua, perhaps more generous in temperament, acknowledged the logical force of the argument before setting it aside. And Rabbi Akiva \u8212- who elsewhere is identified as the one who first established that a {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood constitutes the minimum {\i shiur} for a {\i nazir} with respect to touch and carrying \u8212- evidently continued to press this position based on his {\i kal vachomer}, a position grounded in his distinctive approach to legal derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halacha Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Which is Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a precise question: {\i ibaya lehu} \u8212- the following problem was raised. When Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua said that the {\i kal vachomer} is inapplicable because this domain is governed by {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}, which rule exactly is the {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}? Is it the rule governing the bone \u8212- that even a tiny {\i etzem k\u8217'se\u8217'orah} is sufficient to obligate a {\i nazir} to shave through touch and carrying \u8212- or is it the rule governing the blood \u8212- that even a {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood causes {\i tumas ohel}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question is not as simple as it first appears. One might have assumed that anything constituting {\i tumas meis} would obviously obligate a {\i nazir} to shave, and that the exceptions are what require {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}. But as {\i Tosafot} notes, the {\i pesukim} concerning the {\i nazir} emphasize {\i tumas ohel}: {\i al kol nafshos meis lo yavo} \u8212- he shall not come near any dead person. The straightforward reading might suggest that a {\i nazir} is only obligated when exposed to something that produces {\i tumas ohel}, or at minimum to something powerful enough to produce that category of {\i tumah}. If so, the rule that even a tiny chip of bone \u8212- which lacks the mass for {\i tumas ohel} \u8212- suffices to obligate a {\i nazir} to shave is itself a {\i chiddush}, a departure from the plain meaning of the {\i pesukim}. That departure could be the {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, the {\i chiddush} could be on the side of the blood. The {\i pesukim} concerning the {\i kohein} use the phrase {\i al kol nafshos meis} as well, and {\i Tosafot} suggests that reading {\i nafshos} in the plural implies the minimum blood of two people, which would amount to a {\i chatzi log}. On that reading, the {\i pesukim} would support requiring a {\i chatzi log} of blood to produce {\i tumas ohel}, making the rule that a {\i revi\u8217'it} is sufficient the {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} \u8212- a transmitted tradition that goes beyond what the {\i pesukim} alone would support. If so, it is the {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood that is the anomalous exception, and Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua were telling Rabbi Akiva: do not build a {\i kal vachomer} beginning from the {\i revi\u8217'it}, because the {\i revi\u8217'it} itself is the exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beraita Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this through a {\i beraita} that states the matter explicitly: {\i etzem k\u8217'se\u8217'orah halacha v\u8217'revi\u8217'it dam kal vachomer} \u8212- the rule governing the tiny bone is the {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}, while the rule that a {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood causes {\i tumas ohel} is derived through {\i kal vachomer}. In other words, the anomaly is on the side of the bone; the {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood is the logically derived rule, and the bone is the exceptional tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No KV from Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately clarifies why Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i kal vachomer} cannot stand. He sought to argue from the bone \u8212- which obligates a {\i nazir} to shave through touch and carrying despite producing no {\i tumas ohel} \u8212- to the {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood, which does produce {\i tumas ohel}. But the bone is itself an exception, a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} that departs from what logic would dictate. {\i Ein danim kal vachomer me-halacha} \u8212- one cannot build a {\i kal vachomer} from a premise that is itself a received anomaly. A {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} is {\i sui generis}; it stands on its own and is not meant to serve as a logical premise from which further laws are inferred. The bone\u8217's rule is true, but it tells us nothing about what the law should be elsewhere, precisely because it exists outside the domain of rational derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a broader conceptual point. One might have thought that a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}, being the most authoritative form of tradition, would constitute the strongest possible foundation for a {\i kal vachomer}. In fact, the opposite is the case. The force of logical inference depends on its premises being part of a coherent, rationally accessible system. A {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} that represents an exception to what logic would otherwise dictate is precisely not such a premise; it is a standalone received tradition, valid within its own scope, but incapable of generating further inferences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Nzirim Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to an entirely different topic, though one that touches on realities of {\i tumas meis} in a very concrete way. The {\i mishnah} presents the following scenario: {\i shnei nezirim} \u8212- two {\i nezirim} \u8212- to whom one person says: {\i ra\u8217'isi echad mikem she-nitma} \u8212- I saw one of you become {\i tamei}, perhaps by entering an area with a grave, {\i v\u8217'eini yodeia eizeh mikem} \u8212- but I do not know which of you it was. Each {\i nazir} is now a {\i safek nazir tamei}. Neither knows with certainty whether he is the one who contracted {\i tumas meis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a complex halachic predicament. A {\i nazir tamei} must go through the process of bringing a {\i korban tumah} and then counting new days of {\i nezirut}, culminating in the {\i korban tahara}. But one cannot simply bring a {\i korban} if one is not certain one is obligated. Nor can one simply shave without being certain of one\u8217's status. The solution for a single individual in this situation would involve waiting out the minimum period of {\i nezirut} \u8212- thirty days \u8212- then shaving and bringing minimal {\i korbanot} with appropriate stipulations. The {\i olah}, for instance, could be brought with the condition that if one is obligated as a {\i nazir tamei} it counts as the obligatory {\i olah}, and if not it is a voluntary {\i olat nedavah}. The {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} \u8212- a bird offering \u8212- may be brought even in a state of doubt, this being a special {\i halacha}. The {\i asham}, however, cannot be brought conditionally or on the basis of doubt. So one never fully satisfies that component of the {\i korban tumah} in a state of {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Nzirim Safek (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Tumah Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before addressing the practical solution for the two {\i nezirim}, the {\i gemara} raises a fundamental question: why are they {\i safek tamei} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle governing doubtful {\i tumah} is derived from the laws of {\i sotah}. The Torah describes the {\i sotah} as having gone into seclusion \u8212- {\i venistara} \u8212- in a private space. From this, the {\i gemara} derives the general principle: {\i safek tumah birshut hayachid sefeko tamei} \u8212- doubtful {\i tumah} that occurred in a private domain is treated as {\i tamei}, while {\i safek tumah birshut harabim sefeko tahor} \u8212- doubtful {\i tumah} in a public domain is treated as {\i tahor}. This applies even at the biblical level, {\i d\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Domain Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes, however, that the analogy to {\i sotah} requires that only two people be present in the private space \u8212- the woman and the man with whom she secluded herself. The rule of {\i safek tumah birshut hayachid} applies when there were only two people in the relevant space, paralleling the {\i sotah} scenario. Here, however, there are three people: the two {\i nezirim} and the witness who observed them. With three people present, this should constitute a {\i reshut harabim}, and {\i safek tumah birshut harabim sefeko tahor}. If so, both {\i nezirim} should simply be presumed {\i tahor} \u8212- yet the {\i mishnah} treats them as {\i safek tamei} and requires them to act accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reshut Hayachid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this through Rabbah bar bar Chana, who explains that the case involves a witness who saw the two {\i nezirim} from a distance \u8212- {\i be\u8217'omer ra\u8217'isi tumah shenizreka beineichem} \u8212- who observed from afar and could not make out which of them was involved. The witness was not physically present in the same space as the two {\i nezirim}; he observed from outside. The space the two {\i nezirim} occupied was therefore a {\i reshut hayachid}, and the witness\u8217's presence at a distance does not convert it into a {\i reshut harabim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi adds that this reading is supported by the language of the {\i mishnah} itself: {\i v\u8217'eini yodeia eizeh mikem} \u8212- I do not know which of you \u8212- implies not that the witness forgot, but that he never knew, which fits precisely the scenario of someone watching at a distance who could not identify which individual was which.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Importantly, throughout this entire analysis the {\i gemara} assumes that the {\i safek} retains its status as a genuine doubt, treating it as {\i safek tamei} rather than {\i vadai tamei}. {\i Tosafot} raises the point that when we say {\i safek tumah birshut hayachid} is {\i tamei}, the normal rule is that we treat the person as {\i vadai tamei}, not merely as {\i safek tamei} \u8212- mirroring the {\i sotah}, where the presumption becomes essentially a certainty for legal purposes. However, since it is impossible for both {\i nezirim} to be {\i vadai tamei} \u8212- only one of them actually became {\i tamei} \u8212- the {\i gemara} here retains the language and substance of {\i safek}, operating within a framework where both individuals carry genuine uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paired Offerings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's solution for the two {\i nezirim} is elegant. Between the two of them, there must be one {\i korban tumah} and one {\i korban tahara}. Each {\i nazir} shaves and they bring together both a complete {\i korban tumah} and a complete {\i korban tahara}. The stipulation each makes is: {\i im ani hu hatamei korban tumah sheli v\u8217'korban tahara shelcha} \u8212- if I am the one who was {\i tamei}, the {\i korban tumah} is mine and the {\i korban tahara} is yours; {\i v\u8217'im ani hu hatahor korban tahara sheli v\u8217'korban tumah shelcha} \u8212- and if I am the one who was {\i tahor}, the {\i korban tahara} is mine and the {\i korban tumah} is yours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the first thirty days, the {\i nezirim} count an additional thirty days. At the end of those sixty days, one of them has definitively completed a full {\i nezirut taharah} following an episode of {\i tumah}, and the other has completed a standard {\i nezirut taharah}. Each brings a complete set of {\i korbanot}, again with appropriate stipulation: the {\i korban} of the second thirty days belongs to whichever of them was indeed the one who was {\i tamei} and has now completed his {\i taharah}. The attribution flows to the person for whom it is obligatory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Person Advantage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The advantage of having two {\i nezirim} in this situation rather than one is substantial. When a single {\i nazir} faces this uncertainty alone, he is limited to bringing minimal {\i korbanot} \u8212- primarily an {\i olah} on a conditional basis \u8212- because he cannot bring the full {\i korban tumah} when he does not know himself to be obligated. He can never bring the {\i asham} at all. With two {\i nezirim}, however, the situation is different: since one of them is definitively obligated in the {\i korban tumah} and the other is definitively obligated in the {\i korban tahara}, both complete sets may be brought without any conditional framing. The only stipulation required is to direct each set toward the person for whom it applies. {\i Tosafot} notes that in this configuration the stipulation is almost formulaic \u8212- it is simply an attribution, not a genuine conditional offering. No {\i olat nedavah} is necessary, no incomplete {\i korban} is brought; each person brings a full set and declares to whom it belongs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Double Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakafah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having described the practical solution for the two {\i nezirim}, the {\i gemara} identifies a difficulty that had been lurking beneath the surface. The {\i mishnah} envisions each {\i nazir} shaving twice: once at the end of the first thirty-day period and once at the end of the second. But if one of them was in fact {\i tahor} all along, then his second shaving \u8212- undertaken so that he can fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of {\i nazir megaleach} in the event that he was {\i tamei} \u8212- is a shaving that he does not need and that he may not need halachically to perform. And the act of shaving all the hair of one\u8217's head implicates the prohibition of {\i hakafat rosh} \u8212- the prohibition of {\i lo takifu}, shaving the sideburns in a manner that makes a circuit of the head.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is thus asking: even granting that the shaving itself is not {\i me\u8217'akev} \u8212- that {\i nezirut} is formally complete without it \u8212- we still have to account for the fact that we are permitting a {\i nazir} to shave in circumstances where the shaving may constitute a violation of {\i hakafat rosh}. Why do we permit that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Woman/Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel answers that the {\i mishnah} is speaking of a woman or a minor. A woman is excluded from the prohibition of {\i lo takifu} because the prohibition against shaving the beard \u8212- {\i lo yashchitu pe\u8217'at zekanam} \u8212- does not apply to women, who ordinarily have no beard. And since there is an assumption that whoever falls outside the prohibition of shaving the beard also falls outside the prohibition of shaving the sideburns, a woman is entirely exempt from {\i hakafat rosh}. A minor likewise falls outside these prohibitions in a way that eliminates the concern. Therefore the {\i mishnah} is addressing a case where the two {\i nezirim} are women, or one of them is a minor, such that the double shaving creates no violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inference: Hakafah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately draws an inference from Shmuel\u8217's answer. One might have suggested a far simpler resolution to the problem: leave the {\i nezirim} as adult males and simply hold that {\i hakafat kol harosh lo shemah hakafah} \u8212- that shaving all the hair of the head does not constitute the prohibited form of {\i hakafah}. The prohibition of {\i lo takifu}, on this reading, refers specifically to making a defined perimeter cut around the head, leaving a circle of hair \u8212- like shaving around a circuit so that the remaining hair describes a line. When one shaves the entire head smooth, there is no circling, no defined sideburn \u8212- there is simply baldness. Perhaps that does not implicate the prohibition at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had Shmuel accepted this position, he could have resolved the {\i gemara}\u8217's difficulty without invoking the special status of women and minors. He did not. {\i Midela mokim lah hachi} \u8212- since he does not explain it this way \u8212- {\i shema mina kasavar Shmuel hakafat kol harosh shemah hakafah} \u8212- we infer that Shmuel holds that shaving all the hair of the head does constitute the prohibited {\i hakafah}. This is a clear inference. For Shmuel, an adult male who shaves his entire head violates the prohibition of {\i lo takifu} just as surely as one who makes a circular perimeter cut. The only way to permit the double shaving of the {\i mishnah} is therefore to situate the case among those who are outside the prohibition entirely \u8212- women and minors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaver/Shavee\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna: Shaver Independent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites a further teaching: {\i amar Rav Huna hamakif et hakatan harei hu chayav} \u8212- Rav Huna rules that one who shaves a minor is liable. This challenges the apparent implication of Shmuel\u8217's answer, which seemed to suggest that when the {\i shavee} \u8212- the person whose hair is being cut \u8212- is a minor, the act is entirely permitted. Rav Huna disagrees, or at least introduces a critical distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying conceptual question is how to read the {\i Torah}\u8217's formulation of the prohibition: {\i lo takifu pe\u8217'at roshchem}. To whom does the {\i Torah} speak? {\i Pe\u8217'at roshchem} \u8212- the sideburns of your head \u8212- seems addressed to the person whose sideburns are being cut, i.e., the {\i shavee}. But {\i lo takifu} \u8212- do not make a circuit \u8212- seems addressed to the one who performs the cutting, i.e., the {\i shaver}. Are these two aspects of a single integrated prohibition, or are they independent obligations that can be separated?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna holds that the {\i shaver} bears an independent obligation. If the {\i shaver} is an adult male, he is liable regardless of who the {\i shavee} is. Even if the {\i shavee} is a minor \u8212- who is himself outside the prohibition \u8212- the adult male performing the shaving violates the prohibition from the {\i shaver}\u8217's side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adda Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava challenged Rav Huna directly: {\i vedidach man megaleach lehu} \u8212- your own children, who do you have shaving them? Presumably Rav Huna\u8217's children had their sideburns cut, yet Rav Huna had just ruled that an adult male shaver is liable regardless of the shavee\u8217's status. If an adult male shaved Rav Huna\u8217's children, that adult male would be violating the prohibition. Who, then, was doing the cutting?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna answered simply: {\i Chovah} \u8212- his wife. Since a woman is exempt from the prohibition \u8212- as Shmuel established, and as the logic of the {\i Torah}\u8217's formulation confirms \u8212- a woman acting as the {\i shaver} violates nothing. The shavee is a minor, and the shaver is a woman. Neither occupies the position of an adult male in the chain of the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates a coherent model. The prohibition operates along two independent axes: the {\i shaver} axis and the {\i shavee} axis. An adult male cannot occupy either position without violating the prohibition \u8212- he cannot be the one who cuts and he cannot be the one whose sideburns are cut. But women and minors are outside the prohibition on both axes. A woman can serve as the {\i shaver}, and a minor can serve as the {\i shavee}, without creating any violation. The only constraint is that an adult male must not appear in either role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Curse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exchange, however, did not end peacefully. Rav Adda bar Ahava responded to the name {\i Chovah} \u8212- Rav Huna\u8217's wife \u8212- by invoking it ominously: {\i tekavrinehu Chovah livneih} \u8212- may Chovah bury her children. Whether this was intended as a genuine curse or as an exclamation of disbelief \u8212- the {\i Rosh} understands it as rhetorical, something like \u8220"does she want to bury her children? How can you allow this?\u8221" \u8212- the words carried consequence. The {\i gemara} records that for all the years of Rav Adda bar Ahava\u8217's life, Rav Huna had no further children. The {\i gemara} does not say that Chovah had no further children \u8212- pointedly, it focuses the consequence on Rav Huna himself. The power of such utterances, even when not intended as a curse, appears repeatedly in the {\i gemara}, and this episode is a striking illustration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna\u8217's Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize Rav Huna\u8217's position as it emerges from this discussion: the prohibition of {\i lo takifu} operates independently on the {\i shaver} and the {\i shavee}. An adult male cannot serve as the {\i shaver} \u8212- even if the {\i shavee} is a minor or a woman \u8212- and an adult male cannot serve as the {\i shavee} \u8212- even if the {\i shaver} is a woman or a minor. Women and minors, however, may occupy either role freely. Thus a woman shaving a minor creates no violation on either side, and Rav Huna\u8217's own practice \u8212- having his wife Chovah cut his children\u8217's sideburns \u8212- was entirely consistent with his ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 56b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumah & Mikdash Entry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i perek} concludes with two {\i mishnayot} that are considerably less intense than the intricate debates we have been navigating throughout this chapter regarding the various forms of {\i tumas meis} and their consequences for the {\i nazir}. The first of these opens with a sweeping principle attributed to Rabbi Eliezer in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua: any {\i tumah min hameis} for which a {\i nazir} shaves his head \u8212- thereby nullifying his earlier {\i nezirut} \u8212- is also the category of {\i tumah} for which one incurs liability upon entering the {\i Beit Hamikdash} while in that state of {\i tumah}. Conversely, any {\i tumah min hameis} for which the {\i nazir} does not shave carries with it no liability for {\i bias mikdash}. The two categories mirror each other precisely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth recalling the context: {\i tumas meis} is unusual in that it is tolerated even within {\i Machaneh Leviyah} \u8212- one may proceed as far as {\i Ezras Nashim} \u8212- and the liability we are discussing applies only upon actual entry into the {\i Azarah} itself. With that in mind, the principle the {\i mishnah} is establishing becomes clear: whatever constitutes a {\i nazir}-invalidating {\i tumah} is also the threshold for {\i Mikdash} liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir pushes back sharply. His argument is straightforward: even granting that there exist forms of {\i tumas meis} that do not require the {\i nazir} to shave \u8212- and we spent much of this {\i perek} cataloguing those cases \u8212- these are still forms of {\i tumah} derived from contact with the dead. How can they possibly be less severe than touching a {\i sheretz}, a dead rodent? Any {\i tumah} whatsoever generates liability upon entering the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. The {\i mishnah} is not discussing the rabbinically derived categories such as {\i skachos}, {\i praos}, or {\i Beis Hapras}, but rather cases that are assumed to be biblically rooted \u8212- cases that, despite their biblical status, are nonetheless exceptions to the rule of {\i nazir megalayach}. Rabbi Meir insists that the {\i nazir} exception cannot translate into an exception for {\i bias mikdash}; the person is still {\i tamei meis}, and that is enough to be liable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beraitah Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} is less interested in the substance of this debate than in a secondary observation: how did Rabbi Eliezer come to state this principle in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua? A {\i braisa} is introduced in which Rabbi Eliezer recounts that when he traveled to Ardaskaya, he found Rabbi Yehoshua ben Peser Rosh sitting and presenting his analysis before Rabbi Meir \u8212- and the content of that teaching was precisely the principle articulated in our {\i mishnah}: the categories of {\i tumah} that trigger {\i nazir megalayach} are the same that trigger liability for {\i bias mikdash}, and those that do not trigger the former do not trigger the latter. When Rabbi Meir responded with his challenge \u8212- again invoking the comparison to {\i sheretz} \u8212- it was Rabbi Eliezer who intervened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer turned to Rabbi Meir and asked: are you familiar with Rabbi Yehoshua ben Mamo, and do you know how weighty an authority he is? Rabbi Meir replied that he was. Rabbi Eliezer then stated: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Mamo transmitted this ruling to me in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- the {\i stam} Rabbi Yehoshua, that is, Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananya \u8212- and the content of that tradition is precisely this principle. With that citation, Rabbi Eliezer gave backing to the position of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Peser Rosh and confirmed the ruling in our {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question: if Rabbi Eliezer received the tradition through Rabbi Yehoshua ben Mamo, why does our {\i mishnah} present it simply as Rabbi Eliezer saying it in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua, skipping the intermediate link entirely?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tradition Transmission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kadmaya and Batraye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} draws from this episode a principle about how chains of tradition are reported. From the fact that Rabbi Eliezer states a tradition as going directly from himself to Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- omitting Rabbi Yehoshua ben Mamo, the authority from whom he actually received it \u8212- we learn that when a tradition passes through multiple links, it is sufficient and indeed standard practice to cite only the {\i kadmaya} and the {\i batraye}, the originator and the final transmitter. The intermediate authorities \u8212- the {\i metziaye} \u8212- need not always be enumerated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak adds a parallel illustration. Nachman haLavlur is quoted as saying: this is what I received from Rabbi Measha, who received it from his father, who received it from the {\i zugot} \u8212- the early paired authorities of the Second Temple era, such as Hillel and Shammai and those before them \u8212- who received it from the {\i neviim}, and this constitutes a {\i halakha leMoshe miSinai} going back to Moshe Rabbeinu. The specific content concerns planting dill and mustard seed in two or three separate locations in one\u8217's field, and the requirement to leave {\i peah} \u8212- a corner portion \u8212- independently for each section rather than one combined corner for the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The precise details belong to the laws of {\i peah}, but the point the {\i Gemara} draws on is the structure of the transmission: Nachman haLavlur gives a chain that proceeds from himself through several generations back to the {\i neviim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Omitted Authorities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: but where in this chain are Yehoshua and Chalev? We know from {\i Pirkei Avot} that Moshe transmitted the Torah to Yehoshua, Yehoshua to the {\i zekenim}, and the {\i zekenim} to the {\i neviim} \u8212- yet Nachman haLavlur\u8217's chain jumps directly from the {\i neviim} to earlier links without specifying Yehoshua and the {\i zekenim}. This confirms, {\i shema mina}, that one need not enumerate every intermediate step in a chain of tradition. Some steps may be skipped while still faithfully representing the lineage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} shifts to a debate between Rabbi Akiva and his teacher Rabbi Eliezer, and it is notable that the discussion is introduced with the same formula used earlier: {\i danti lifnei}, I laid out my argument before him. Rabbi Akiva formulates the following {\i kal vachomer}: a bone the size of a barley grain does not cause {\i tumah} through {\i ohel} \u8212- it lacks the power to render someone {\i tamei} by being in the same covered space \u8212- and yet a {\i nazir} must shave if he touches or carries such a bone. Now, a {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood \u8212- a quarter-{\i log} \u8212- does cause {\i tumah be\u8217'ohel}. Should it not follow, by an inference {\i a fortiori}, that a {\i nazir} who touches or carries a {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood should certainly be obligated to shave?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The elegance of the argument is worth pausing over. Rabbi Akiva is careful not to argue that the {\i revi\u8217'is} should cause {\i tumah be\u8217'ohel} for the {\i nazir} \u8212- that would require a different {\i kal vachomer}. He argues only about {\i maga} and {\i masa}, touch and carrying. If the little bone \u8212- which cannot even cause {\i ohel tumah} \u8212- is sufficient to obligate a {\i nazir} to shave when touched, then a {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood, which can cause {\i ohel tumah}, should certainly be sufficient to obligate shaving when touched. The existing rule held that shaving was triggered only from a half-{\i log} of blood, and Rabbi Akiva was pressing: why demand a half-{\i log} even for {\i maga}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's response was terse: {\i mah zeh Akiva, ein danim mi-kal vachomer} \u8212- what are you doing, Akiva? Logical inference has no place here. These are arbitrary rules, and this is not the arena for your {\i kal vachomers}. He neither validated the argument nor explained why it was inapplicable; he simply dismissed it. This reaction reflects something fundamental about Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's orientation toward Torah: he famously never stated anything he had not received from his teacher, and the application of human logic to these received rules struck him as fundamentally misplaced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua Validates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva was unsatisfied with this response and brought the argument to Rabbi Yehoshua. Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's reply was strikingly different in tone. He said: {\i yafeh amarta} \u8212- you have argued well. The argument is sound. But: {\i ella kach amru halacha} \u8212- nevertheless, this is the received tradition, and it stands as it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction in approach is instructive. Both Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua arrive at the same bottom line \u8212- the {\i kal vachomer} does not change the {\i halacha}. But Rabbi Yehoshua validated the argument\u8217's internal logic while explaining why it nonetheless cannot override the tradition. Rabbi Eliezer simply rejected the enterprise of reasoning altogether. This dynamic reflects a broader tension running through the tradition between those who see {\i Torah she-be\u8217'al peh} primarily as the custodianship and transmission of received law, and those who see it as an ongoing intellectual engagement with text and logic. Rabbi Eliezer was paradigmatically the former; Rabbi Akiva \u8212- who brought unprecedented creativity and derivation to {\i halacha} \u8212- represented the latter. It is no coincidence that Rabbi Eliezer was more dismissive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakha L\u8217'Moshe Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Which Exception?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a pointed question: when Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua say that the {\i kal vachomer} is inappropriate because this is a {\i halacha}, what exactly is the {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai} being referenced? The {\i Gemara} presents two possibilities. One: the {\i halakha leMoshe miSinai} is the rule that even a bone the size of a barley grain \u8212- which lacks the power of {\i tumas ohel} \u8212- is sufficient to obligate a {\i nazir} to shave. Under this reading, the bone is the anomaly, and you cannot build a {\i kal vachomer} on an anomaly. Two: the {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai} is the rule that a {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood causes {\i tumas ohel} at all. Under this reading, the {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood is the anomaly, and it cannot be invoked as the stronger horn of a {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning behind the second possibility draws on the verse: {\i al kol nafshot meis lo yavo}. As {\i Tosafot} explains, a plain reading of the {\i psukim} might imply that the minimum for {\i tumas meis} is a half-{\i log} of blood \u8212- because {\i nafshot} in the plural suggests an amount sufficient for two lives, which would be two {\i revi\u8217'is}, totaling a half-{\i log}. On this reading, the {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood causing {\i tumas ohel} is itself a {\i chiddush}, a departure from what the verses suggest, and therefore cannot serve as the stronger premise of a {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bone as Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the question by citing a {\i braisa} explicitly: the rule that a bone the size of a barley grain obligates a {\i nazir} to shave is the {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai}. The {\i revi\u8217'is} of blood is not a {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai} in this context; Rabbi Akiva was attempting to derive it by {\i kal vachomer} from the bone. But since the bone is itself a designated exception \u8212- a {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai} \u8212- it cannot serve as the basis of derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to a broader principle established here: {\i ein danin kal vachomer me-halacha} \u8212- one cannot construct a {\i kal vachomer} using a case that is itself labeled as a {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai}. The reason is conceptual. A {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai} is, by its nature, a sui generis received tradition \u8212- it is not intended to convey a larger principle from which further law may be derived. The entire enterprise of {\i kal vachomer} depends on the premise that the stronger case is stronger because of some underlying logic. When a rule is categorized as a {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai}, it stands outside that logical framework. It is simply tradition, not a proposition whose implications can be extended by human reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why this should be so \u8212- surely a tradition received at Sinai carries the highest possible authority, and if anything one might expect it to be the most reliable premise from which to derive. The answer seems to lie in the nature of the two modes of {\i halacha}. When we engage {\i pesukim} through the thirteen hermeneutical principles, we are working within a system of intellectual derivation where each ruling participates in a larger logical discourse. {\i Halacha leMoshe miSinai}, by contrast, is pure received tradition, standing apart from that discourse. To borrow its specific ruling as a logical stepping stone would be to misappropriate it \u8212- treating as a logical proposition something that is, by definition, a tradition unto itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Nazirims Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} presents a new scenario. There are two {\i nezirim}, and a third person informs them: I saw one of you become {\i tamei} \u8212- perhaps he walked unknowingly into a burial ground \u8212- but I do not know which one of you it was. Each of the two {\i nezirim} is now a {\i safek nazir tamei}, a {\i nazir} of doubtful {\i tumah} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solution with Stipulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} provides a practical resolution for this predicament. Since one of the two is definitely obligated in a {\i korban tumah} and the other is definitely obligated in a {\i korban tahara}, they do not each need to bring both sets independently with stipulations. Rather, they bring their respective sets together and stipulate: if I am the one who was {\i tamei}, then my {\i korban} is the {\i korban tumah} and yours is the {\i korban tahara}; and if I am the one who was {\i tahor}, then mine is the {\i korban tahara} and yours is the {\i korban tumah}. Because one of each is definitively owed, the full set of obligatory {\i korbanot} \u8212- including the {\i chatat ha-of} for {\i tumah} \u8212- can be brought without resorting to a {\i nedava} framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This approach is considerably more efficient than what a single {\i safek tamei nazir} would face. A single individual in that state cannot bring a full {\i korban tumah} set, since he may not be obligated, and faces additional complications around the {\i asham}, which cannot be brought either {\i misafek} or {\i bitnai}. Having a partner in the same predicament allows the full sets to be brought and directed appropriately.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Nazirims Safek (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Periods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following the initial {\i korban} sequence, the two {\i nezirim} count an additional thirty days. One of them has just concluded \u8212- if he was the {\i nazir tahor}, he is now finished. But the other \u8212- if he was the {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- must now complete his reconstituted {\i nezirut}. At the conclusion of the second thirty-day period, again one of them is definitively obligated in a full {\i korban tahara}, and again a stipulation is issued: whoever is the {\i tamei} one, the earlier set thirty days ago was his {\i korban tumah}; whoever is the {\i tahor} one, the earlier set was his {\i korban tahara}. And now, the {\i korban tahara} brought at day sixty belongs to whichever of them still needs it. In this way, the full halakhic obligations of both possible scenarios are discharged without either person needing to know his own status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Tumah Domains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises an unexpected question on the entire framework of the {\i mishnah}: why are the two {\i nezirim} treated as {\i safek tamei} at all? The principle governing {\i safek tumah} \u8212- learned from the case of the {\i Sotah} \u8212- is: {\i safek tumah birshut hayachid, safeiko tamei; safek tumah birshut harabbim, safeiko tahor}. Doubtful {\i tumah} in a private domain is treated as {\i tamei}; doubtful {\i tumah} in a public domain is treated as {\i tahor}. This principle, derived conceptually from the {\i Sotah} case \u8212- where {\i v\u8217'nistra}, she went into seclusion, implies that only two parties were present \u8212- applies whenever a doubt arises in a setting of two people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Make Public\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If there were two {\i nezirim} plus the witness who observed the incident, that is three people. Three people constitute a {\i reshut harabbim} for the purposes of this rule. And if it is a {\i reshut harabbim}, then {\i safeiko tahor} \u8212- both {\i nezirim} should be ruled {\i tahor}, and the entire elaborate procedure of the {\i mishnah} would be unnecessary. This is a striking problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a further complication from the other direction. Even if we were to apply the {\i reshut hayachid} principle, the {\i halacha} in that case does not merely treat the person as {\i safek tamei} \u8212- it treats him as {\i vadai tamei}, definitively {\i tamei}, until cleared. Yet the entire {\i mishnah} operates within a framework of {\i safek}, not {\i vadai}. {\i Tosafot} resolves this by noting that since it is impossible for both of them to be {\i vadai tamei} \u8212- only one actually is \u8212- we cannot apply the {\i vadai} consequence to both simultaneously. The situation therefore remains in the realm of {\i safek}, even in a {\i reshut hayachid}, because the absolute ruling cannot be consistently applied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distance Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the domain question through Rabba bar bar Huna: the case concerns a witness who was standing at a distance from the two {\i nezirim}. He observed them from afar \u8212- perhaps he saw a piece of a corpse thrown in their direction, or simply could not make out who was who \u8212- and did not share the same space. The two {\i nezirim} were therefore in a {\i reshut hayachid} unto themselves, since the witness was not with them in that enclosed space. This reading is reinforced by Rav Ashi, who notes that the precise language of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i eini yodeia eizeh mikem}, I do not know which one of you \u8212- implies not that the witness forgot what he saw, but that he never knew in the first place, which fits naturally with the scenario of observing at a distance and being unable to identify which individual was involved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaving Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakafa Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a difficulty with the mechanics of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's solution. Both {\i nezirim} shave at the end of each thirty-day period. But if one of them is {\i tahor}, he has no obligation to shave the second time. He shaves anyway \u8212- to discharge the possible obligation of the one who is {\i tamei}. Yet in doing so, he shaves all the hair of his head. This raises the concern of {\i hakafa}: the prohibition of {\i lo takifu pe\u8217'at rosheichem}, not to cut the sidelocks in a circular manner. Even setting aside the question of whether this person\u8217's {\i nezirut} is over, does shaving all the hair of one\u8217's head violate this prohibition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that the {\i giluach} itself is not {\i me\u8217'akeiv} the completion of {\i nezirut} \u8212- one\u8217's {\i nezirut} ends regardless of whether one shaves. So one might argue: why not simply have both {\i nezirim} shave only once at day thirty, and forgo the second shaving, thereby avoiding any possible transgression? The {\i mishnah} nevertheless prescribes shaving twice. The question is how that is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel resolves the difficulty: the {\i mishnah} is dealing with a case of a woman or a minor. A woman is excluded from the prohibition of {\i hakafa} because that prohibition is linked to the prohibition against shaving the beard \u8212- and since women do not typically have beards, they are outside the category entirely. Anyone excluded from the prohibition of shaving the beard is correspondingly excluded from the prohibition of {\i hakafa} of the sideburns. Thus, if the two {\i nezirim} are women, there is no {\i hakafa} concern, and both may shave freely at both junctures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Head Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges this: why did Shmuel resort to the case of a woman or minor at all? Another solution was available: perhaps shaving the entire head does not constitute a violation of {\i hakafa} in the first place. {\i Lo takifu} means to make a circular cut \u8212- to create a hairline that encircles the head. If one shaves everything and is left entirely bald, there is no hairline at all, and therefore no {\i hakafa} in any meaningful sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since Shmuel does not offer this answer and instead specifies the case of a woman or minor, we may infer \u8212- {\i shema mina} \u8212- that Shmuel holds {\i hakafat kol harosh shemah hakafa}: shaving the entire head does constitute the prohibited {\i hakafa}. Under ordinary circumstances, an adult male who shaves all the hair of his head without halakhic justification violates this prohibition. The only way to permit the second shaving, therefore, is to situate the case where the individuals are not subject to the prohibition at all \u8212- hence women or minors.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Shavings Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra reports that Shmuel\u8217's discussion is presented not in connection with our {\i mishnah} but with a subsequent one, which we encountered in a recent {\i daf}: a {\i nazir} who is {\i safek tamei} and {\i safek metzora}. Such a person must spread out his obligations over sixty days and performs four separate shavings across that period. As a {\i metzora}, he shaves at the onset of his {\i tahara} process and again a week later at its conclusion; as a {\i nazir tamei}, he shaves at the end of his {\i tumah} period and again at the completion of his reconstituted {\i nezirut}. If he is {\i safek} in both categories, the four shavings are spaced across sixty days to accommodate all possibilities. Yet in each case, the possibility that a given shaving is unnecessary raises the same {\i hakafa} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same Question Applied\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question and resolution follow the same logic. The {\i Gemara} asks why we permit an individual who may not be obligated to shave repeatedly, given the prohibition of {\i hakafa}, and Shmuel\u8217's answer is the same: {\i be-ishah ve-katan}, a woman or a minor. Since Shmuel requires this qualification rather than relying on the argument that shaving the full head is not {\i hakafa}, the inference stands: Shmuel maintains that shaving the entire head does constitute {\i hakafa} and is prohibited for an adult male under ordinary circumstances.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaver Independence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult Shaver Liable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point Rav Huna introduces a position that cuts against the implicit assumption in Shmuel\u8217's answer. Shmuel resolved the {\i hakafa} concern by making the {\i nazir} a woman or minor \u8212- implying that if the {\i shavee} is exempt from the prohibition, the shaving is permissible. Rav Huna challenges this framing: {\i ha-makif et ha-katan harei hu chayav} \u8212- one who shaves a minor is nonetheless liable. The prohibition of {\i lo takifu} operates independently for the {\i shaver}: if the shaver is an adult male, he transgresses regardless of who the {\i shavee} is, even if the {\i shavee} is a minor or a woman entirely outside the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Shaving Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava immediately challenges Rav Huna: if an adult male transgresses as a shaver regardless of who the {\i shavee} is, how does your wife shave your children? Their sidelocks are evidently being cut. Who is doing it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Exempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna replies: {\i Chova} \u8212- his wife\u8217's name \u8212- shaves them. Since a woman is excluded from the prohibition of shaving the beard, she is correspondingly excluded from the prohibition of {\i hakafa} as the shaver. The shaver\u8217's liability tracks whether the shaver is in the prohibited category, not whether the {\i shavee} is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Role Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields a clear structure. An adult male cannot occupy either position: he may not be the shaver, and he may not be the {\i shavee}. Women and minors may occupy either position \u8212- shaver or {\i shavee} \u8212- without transgressing. The key restriction is that both parties in the act must be free of the prohibition: an adult male as shaver violates the prohibition regardless of who sits in the chair; an adult male as {\i shavee} similarly implicates a transgression. But a woman may shave a minor, a minor may shave a woman, and any combination that excludes an adult male in either role is permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Adda\u8217's Curse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava\u8217's response to Rav Huna\u8217's answer was, to say the least, pointed. He exclaimed: {\i tikberinehu Chova le-vaneia} \u8212- may Chova bury her children. The {\i Rosh} softens this, reading it as a rhetorical expression of outrage: does she want to bury her children? Is this what she is doing to them? Yet whether spoken as a curse or an exclamation, the {\i Gemara} records its effect. For all the years of Rav Adda bar Ahava\u8217's lifetime, Rav Huna had no surviving children. The words, however they were intended, carried force \u8212- a theme the {\i Gemara} returns to repeatedly in its understanding of how the utterances of scholars reverberate beyond their speaker\u8217's intention.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 59\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partner Dies: Stranger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Two Partners\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the top of 59b returns to a scenario whose basic architecture we have encountered before: two people, one of whom has become {\i tamei}, though it is unknown which one. To resolve this uncertainty, they entered into a partnership, bringing their various {\i korbanos} with the stipulation that each offering should work for whichever partner truly needs it. This was the arrangement of the earlier {\i mishnah}: the {\i korban tumah} works for whoever is {\i tamei}, and the {\i korban tahara} works for whoever is {\i tahor}, with the process playing out over two sets of rounds separated by thirty days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partnership & Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To review the mechanics of that earlier arrangement: a wait of thirty days is required before bringing the {\i korban tumah}, because the person who is {\i tahor} cannot shave within thirty days of his {\i nezirut}, and shaving accompanies the {\i korbanos}. After thirty days, the partnership brings the {\i korban tumah} \u8212- which works for whichever one is {\i tamei} \u8212- and the {\i korban tahara} works for whichever one is {\i tahor}. After another thirty days, a final {\i korban tahara} is brought, and the process is complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partner Death Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces a complication: one of the two partners died. The surviving partner no longer has anyone with whom to make these joint stipulations. What is he to do?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The instinctive answer would be that he reverts to the situation of any ordinary {\i nazir} who has become {\i safek tamei}: he waits thirty days \u8212- for the person who is {\i tahor} cannot shave before thirty days \u8212- and then brings what he can bring {\i besafek}. For his {\i korban tumah} he brings a {\i chatas ha\u8217'of besafek}, since the Sages permitted bringing a {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} even under conditions of doubt in a way that a standard animal {\i chatas} may not be brought. For his {\i korban tahara} he brings an {\i olat behemah bitnai}: if he is {\i tahor}, this counts as his {\i korban tahara}; if he turns out to be {\i tamei}, it is simply a {\i nedavah}, a voluntary gift. Then after another thirty days he brings the remainder of the {\i korban tahara}, with similar conditional stipulations governing each component.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that there is also a {\i shelamim} among the {\i korbanos} of {\i korban tahara} \u8212- this is indeed the case and should be included in the reckoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua: Stranger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the {\i mishnah} actually records, however, is the position of Rabbi Yehoshua, who prescribes a more elaborate solution. He says: {\i yevakesh she\u8217'chamino shuk} \u8212- go find a stranger in the marketplace, perhaps offer him something for his trouble, and ask him to take on {\i nezirut} alongside you. The purpose is to reconstitute a two-person framework so that the full complement of {\i korbanos} can be brought with appropriate stipulations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Stipulations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stipulations work as follows. At the time of entering the arrangement with the stranger, the surviving partner declares: if I am the {\i tamei} one, then you \u8212- the stranger \u8212- become a {\i nazir} immediately ({\i harei atah nazir miyad}). If I am the {\i tahor} one, then you become a {\i nazir} after thirty days ({\i harei atah nazir acharei shloshim yom}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They then count thirty days together and bring both a {\i korban tumah} and a {\i korban tahara}. At this point the surviving partner says: if I was the {\i tamei} one, the {\i korban tumah} is mine and the {\i korban tahara} is yours \u8212- you have completed your {\i nezirut tahara}. If I was the {\i tahor} one, the {\i korban tahara} is mine and the {\i korban tumah} was brought {\i besafek} \u8212- it does not belong definitively to anyone. He then adds: after another thirty days we bring a further {\i korban tahara}, and if I was the {\i tamei} one, that first {\i korban tumah} was mine, that {\i korban tahara} was yours, and this final {\i korban tahara} is mine. If I was the {\i tahor} one, the earlier {\i korban tahara} was mine, and this final {\i korban tahara} is yours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Wait Thirty Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder: why must the stranger wait thirty days at all? If the surviving partner may have to act as a {\i nazir} for the entire sixty-day period anyway, what is accomplished by deferring the stranger\u8217's {\i nezirut} until thirty days in, in the scenario where the survivor turns out to be {\i tahor}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer concerns the cutting of hair. At the thirty-day mark, both parties will shave. In scenario one \u8212- the survivor is {\i tamei} \u8212- the stranger completed his {\i nezirut tahara} in thirty days and is entitled to shave. That works smoothly. But if the survivor is {\i tahor}, then the {\i korban tahara} at thirty days belongs to the survivor, and the stranger has not yet brought his own {\i korban tahara}. If the stranger\u8217's {\i nezirut} had already begun at the outset, he would have no valid {\i korban tahara} at the thirty-day mark and could not shave. We would then need to introduce yet another animal \u8212- an {\i olah bitnai} \u8212- just to permit him to shave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Tamei Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In scenario one, where the survivor is {\i tamei}: the {\i korban tumah} at thirty days is his, the {\i korban tahara} at thirty days belongs to the stranger (whose {\i nezirut} began immediately), and the stranger shaves and concludes his {\i nezirut}. After another thirty days the survivor brings his own {\i korban tahara} and shaves. The mechanism works cleanly \u8212- both people bring a full complement of {\i korbanos} and neither is splitting his {\i korban tahara} across two periods.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Tahor Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In scenario two, where the survivor is {\i tahor}: at thirty days, the {\i korban tahara} belongs to the survivor. The {\i korban tumah} was brought {\i besafek} \u8212- it counts as nobody\u8217's obligatory offering and was conditional. The stranger\u8217's {\i nezirut} has not yet begun, which means he has not violated his {\i nezirut} by shaving. He cuts his hair not as the ritual shaving of a concluding {\i nazir}, but simply as a person who has not yet taken on {\i nezirut} \u8212- there is no violation. His {\i nezirut} then begins after this shaving. Thirty days later, the stranger brings his {\i korban tahara} and completes his {\i nezirut} properly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism for permitting the shaving, then, is not that the haircut carries positive ritual significance for the stranger. It is purely preventive: by stipulating that his {\i nezirut} does not begin until after thirty days in this scenario, we ensure that when he does cut his hair at the thirty-day mark, he is not violating a {\i nezirut} that has not yet commenced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Cutting Justificati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of why cutting the stranger\u8217's hair is permissible at all \u8212- given that he is not yet completing a {\i nezirut} \u8212- connects to the broader discussion of {\i hakafas kol harosh} treated in the previous day\u8217's {\i sugya}, and the conclusion there governs here as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper question underlying Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's entire scheme remains pointed: if the survivor is, at bottom, simply a {\i safek tamei nazir}, why does he need to recruit anyone from the marketplace at all? He could simply do what any {\i safek tamei nazir} does. The advantage Rabbi Yehoshua claims is that at the end of the process each party has brought a complete, undivided {\i korban tahara}. By the single-person method, the {\i olat behemah} is brought in the first period as part of the {\i korban tahara} and the {\i chatas} and {\i shelamim} only later, splitting the {\i korban tahara} across two stages. Under Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's scheme, each {\i korban tahara} is brought whole. That, at minimum, is the gain \u8212- though as we shall see, this advantage came under challenge.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ben Zoma Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zoma: Who Agrees?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Zoma registered a pointed practical objection to Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's scheme: {\i umi she\u8217'shomea lo she\u8217'yidor kenegdo benazir?} \u8212- who in the world is going to agree to become a {\i nazir} together with this fellow and submit to this entire complicated arrangement of stipulations? One might offer payment, but as a practical matter the mechanism is deeply unwieldy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zoma: Bird & Animal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Zoma therefore proposed: forget the dead partner entirely. The survivor is simply a {\i safek tamei nazir}. Treat him as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Period: Besafek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first period, the survivor brings a {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} and an {\i olat behemah}, and declares: if I was {\i tamei}, the {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} counts toward my obligation and the {\i olah} is a {\i nedavah}; if I was {\i tahor}, the {\i olah} counts toward my obligation and the {\i chatas} was brought {\i besafek} \u8212- which is permitted. This is the standard mechanism available to a {\i safek tamei nazir}: a {\i chatas ha\u8217'of besafek} and an {\i olah bitnai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Period: Complete\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After thirty days, the survivor brings a full {\i korban tahara}, declaring: if I was {\i tamei} earlier, that first {\i olah} was a {\i nedavah} and this {\i olah} now counts toward my obligation of {\i korban tahara}, together with the {\i chatas} and {\i shelamim}. If I was {\i tahor} earlier, that first {\i olah} was already part of my obligation, and this {\i olah} is a {\i nedavah}, while the {\i chatas} and {\i shelamim} complete my {\i korban tahara}. The thirty-day wait before bringing the final {\i korban tahara} is required because, since one cannot know whether the person is {\i tamei}, one must wait out the minimum period of {\i nezirut tahara} before the {\i korban tahara} can be valid \u8212- one must first permit the possibility that the full period has elapsed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua: No Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's response to Ben Zoma was: {\i nimtza zeh mevi korbonosav lachatza\u8217'in} \u8212- according to your method, this person\u8217's {\i korban tahara} is split in two. The {\i olah} comes in the first period and the {\i chatas} and {\i shelamim} come in the second. Under my scheme, each person brings his full {\i korban tahara} intact and undivided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages Agree with Zoma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} records that the Sages agreed with Ben Zoma. That objection about splitting the {\i korban} was apparently not compelling enough to justify the extraordinary complexity of recruiting a stranger. The {\i Gemara} reinforces this: it asks, in effect, {\i ve\u8217'leisei} \u8212- so what? Let him bring his {\i korban} in two stages. What is really so terrible about that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda: Sharpening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's explanation: {\i amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel, lo amar Rabbi Yehoshua ella lechaded bo es hatalmidim} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua only said this in order to sharpen his students, to provoke them to think and respond. This explanation is itself somewhat puzzling. Normally one might invoke the principle of {\i lechaded es hatalmidim} for a position that the teacher does not actually hold, floated as a pedagogical provocation. But Rabbi Yehoshua here articulated a full {\i psak} position with detailed working-out of stipulations. It is not at all clear what the {\i Gemara} means when it says he said it only to sharpen his students.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman: Fat Problems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman offered an additional critique from a different angle. The {\i Gemara} records: {\i amar Rav Nachman mai avid lei Rabbi Yehoshua ledakei dlo yisru} \u8212- what should Rabbi Yehoshua do that his fat should not go off? The precise meaning of this remark is not entirely clear. {\i Tosafos} notes that the phrase is unexplained ({\i lo uparash}) and attempts an interpretation not based on a figurative expression. Both {\i Tosafos} and the {\i Rosh} understand the remark as referring to the fats of the {\i korbanos} themselves: because the two {\i nezir\u1080 ?\u1084 ?} are sharing {\i korbanos} and must perform {\i tenufah} together, there will be an unavoidable delay in burning the fats and other parts of the offerings, leading to practical problems with those sacrificial portions. Whatever benefit Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's method achieves by keeping each {\i korban tahara} undivided is offset by the problems that arise from the delays the shared arrangement necessarily introduces. In any case, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's overall assessment appears to be that Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's elaborate partnership mechanism is unnecessary and that Ben Zoma\u8217's straightforward approach is both simpler and equally valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Tamei & Metzora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complex Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} introduces a substantially more intricate case. A {\i nazir} finds himself in a double state of uncertainty: he may have contracted {\i tumah} from a corpse ({\i tamei meis}) and he may also be a {\i metzora}. These are two independent doubts, giving rise to four possible actual statuses: he might be purely {\i tahor}, he might be only {\i tamei meis}, he might be only a {\i metzora}, or he might be both {\i tamei meis} and a {\i metzora}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single-Status Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate why the combined doubt creates such a protracted ordeal, it helps to trace what each individual certainty would have required. If he were definitively {\i tahor}, he would complete his thirty-day {\i nezirut}, shave, and bring his {\i korban tahara}. If he were definitively {\i tamei meis}, he would undergo the seven-day purification with the {\i eifer parah adumah}, shave after seven days, wait thirty days of {\i nezirut tahara}, shave again, and bring his {\i korban tahara} \u8212- a total of two shavings spread across roughly thirty-seven days. If he were definitively a {\i metzora}, the {\i metzora} purification process involves a first shaving (accompanied by the bird ritual outside the {\i Beis ha\u8217'mikdash}) followed by a seven-day wait and a second shaving, after which the {\i korban tahara} of {\i nezirut} would follow. If he were both {\i tamei meis} and a {\i metzora}, all of these processes would need to run in sequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to note the distinction between the bird ritual of {\i metzora} stage one \u8212- the dipping of birds in blood, the sprinkling, and the sending-free \u8212- and the {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} brought later. The first is not a {\i korban} at all; it is a purification ritual performed outside the {\i Beis ha\u8217'mikdash}, comparable in character to the {\i eifer parah adumah}. The {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} of stage two is a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Wait: Tahor Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that he does not know which status he is in, the {\i mishnah} rules that he cannot shave at the outset. The reason is that he might be a {\i nazir tahor} \u8212- a completely pure {\i nazir} who has no license to shave before thirty days have elapsed. This possibility alone forces every other possible scenario to wait. No shaving can occur before the first thirty days are complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Stages Mapped\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This produces four sequential thirty-day periods, each corresponding to one of the four possible statuses. The logic of why there must be four periods, rather than fewer, follows from what might be happening at each stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At stage one, three possibilities converge: he might be {\i tamei meis} (whose first shaving is now available after the thirty-day wait, since the {\i tamei meis} process is effectively complete after seven days of purification but had to wait here because of the {\i nazir tahor} possibility); he might be at {\i metzora} stage one (the bird ritual); and he might be a {\i nazir tahor} (completing his {\i nezirut tahara} of thirty days). Accordingly, at the first shaving he brings: the {\i tzipporim} (bird ritual) for the possibility of {\i metzora} stage one, a {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} for the possibility of {\i tamei meis} (which can be brought {\i besafek}), and an {\i olat behemah} for the possibility of {\i nazir tahor} (which can be brought {\i bitnai}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the first shaving, one might think: surely we can now proceed quickly to the second shaving after seven days, since a {\i metzora} needs only seven days between shavings. The problem is that if the person was {\i tamei meis}, his first shaving was only the beginning of his {\i nezirut tahara}. A {\i nazir tamei} who shaves must then observe thirty days of {\i nezirut tahara} before his final shaving. That possibility forces everyone to wait another thirty days before the second shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the second shaving, the relevant possibilities are: {\i metzora} stage two (the {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} of {\i metzora} and the full set of {\i korban taharah} for that stage) and the {\i nazir tahor} of thirty days who might now be completing his {\i nezirut tahara} as a continuation from after the first shaving. And so the pattern continues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the second shaving, if the person were a definite {\i metzora} and nothing else, one might think he is finished. But the rule is that time spent as a {\i metzora} does not count toward one\u8217's {\i nezirut}. When the {\i metzora} purification concludes, the {\i nezirut} resumes from where it left off \u8212- or, in certain configurations, begins fresh. This means that after the {\i metzora} process, a full thirty-day {\i nezirut tahara} must still be observed. That generates the third stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the third shaving, the {\i metzora}-plus-{\i tahor} possibility may conclude its {\i nezirut tahara}, and the {\i metzora}-plus-{\i tamei meis} possibility brings its remaining offerings. After the third shaving, only the {\i metzora}-plus-{\i tamei meis} possibility remains, requiring one final thirty-day period of {\i nezirut tahara}. This produces the fourth and final stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Acts Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical results the {\i mishnah} enumerates follow from this structure. After sixty days \u8212- two complete thirty-day periods \u8212- the person is definitively out of {\i metzora} status. The {\i metzora} purification process, however lengthy it became due to the uncertainties, is complete by day sixty. Therefore, from day sixty onward, he may eat {\i kodshim} and engage in activities that {\i tumah} would prohibit, since his {\i tumah} from {\i meis} would have concluded much earlier (after seven days, once the {\i eifer parah adumah} was applied). After one hundred and twenty days \u8212- four complete thirty-day periods \u8212- he is definitively out of his {\i nezirut} and may drink wine and contract {\i tumah} from the dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Year-Long Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} extends this framework to the case of a year-long {\i nezirut}. The principle is stated concisely: {\i bameh devarim amurim benezirut muetet} \u8212- the sixty-day and one-hundred-twenty-day figures apply to a standard thirty-day {\i nezirut}. But if the person took on a year-long {\i nezirut}, then he may eat {\i kodshim} only after two years and may drink wine and contract {\i tumah} only after four years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Each Stage: 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The arithmetic is direct. The entire reason each stage required a thirty-day wait was that at each possible juncture, there might begin a fresh {\i nezirut tahara} whose minimum duration is thirty days. If the {\i nezirut tahara} is a year in length, then each of those four stages becomes a year-long wait. The four possible statuses \u8212- purely {\i tahor}, {\i tamei meis} alone, {\i metzora} alone, {\i tamei meis} and {\i metzora} together \u8212- each give rise to a {\i nezirut tahara} that begins at a different stage, and it is the length of that {\i nezirut tahara} that determines the interval between shavings. With a year-long {\i nezirut}, each interval is a year, and four such intervals total four years.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Shavers Korbanos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Shavings Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} following the {\i mishnah} specifies the {\i korbanos} brought at each of the four shavings in the year-long complex case. The underlying logic at each stage is the same: one brings whatever {\i korbanos} can be validly offered given the set of statuses that remain possible at that juncture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the first shaving \u8212- after the initial thirty-day period \u8212- three possibilities remain open. The person might be at {\i metzora} stage one, in which case the {\i tzipporim} ritual is performed. He might be a {\i nazir tamei}, in which case a {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} is brought (permitted {\i besafek}). He might be a {\i nazir tahor} completing his {\i nezirut tahara}, in which case an {\i olat behemah} is brought {\i bitnai}. Accordingly, the first shaving involves: {\i tzipporim}, {\i chatas ha\u8217'of}, and {\i olat behemah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the second shaving \u8212- after another thirty-day period \u8212- the possible statuses are: {\i metzora} stage two (bringing a {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} as one of the {\i metzora} stage two {\i korbanos}) and {\i nazir tahor} completing a {\i nezirut tahara} that began after the first shaving (bringing an {\i olat behemah bitnai}). The second shaving thus involves {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} and {\i olat behemah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the third shaving, the remaining possibilities again include those whose {\i nezirut tahara} would begin at this stage \u8212- specifically the configurations involving {\i metzora} whose {\i nezirut} has now resumed and the remaining possibilities not yet concluded. The {\i korbanos} at the third shaving are again {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} and {\i olat behemah}, corresponding to the combinations of statuses still in play.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fourth Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the fourth and final shaving, only one possibility remains: the person was both a {\i metzora} and a {\i nazir tamei}, and this final thirty-day period was his {\i nezirut tahara}. At this stage there is no remaining doubt requiring conditional offerings \u8212- the full {\i korban tahara} is brought. This is the {\i korban tahara} in its complete form, with {\i olah}, {\i chatas}, and {\i shelamim}, concluding the entire four-stage process. The full analysis of precisely which {\i korbanos} at the third and fourth shavings correspond to which possible statuses will be taken up in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 59b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partner Dies\u8212-Marketplace\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: One Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the top of 59b introduces a scenario that bears a family resemblance to cases we have encountered before, though with a crucial new complication. Previously, the {\i mishnah} dealt with two {\i nezirim} who did not know which of them had become {\i tamei}. Their solution was to enter a kind of partnership, pool their offerings, and stipulate that each {\i korban} should count for whoever genuinely required it. They would proceed through two rounds of offerings \u8212- first bringing a {\i korban tumah} and a {\i korban tahara} with the appropriate stipulations, then, after another thirty days, completing the remaining {\i korban tahara}. The reason for the thirty-day wait between rounds was that the {\i tahor} partner could not shave before completing thirty days of {\i nezirus}, and so that constraint governed the entire sequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The new {\i mishnah} asks: what happens when one of the two partners dies? The surviving partner is left without anyone to stipulate with, and the question becomes what he must do on his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Nazir Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining the {\i mishnah}\u8217's answer, it is worth establishing what the straightforward expectation would be. A single {\i nazir} who is uncertain whether he became {\i tamei} \u8212- a {\i safek tamei} \u8212- follows a well-defined procedure. Because he cannot shave in fewer than thirty days (lest he turn out to have been {\i tahor} all along and thereby cut short a valid {\i nezirus tahara}), he waits the full thirty days. At that point, he must bring something that could serve as his {\i korban tumah} if he was indeed {\i tamei}, and something that could serve as his {\i korban tahara} if he was {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i tumah} side, he brings a {\i chatas ha-of b\u8217'safek} \u8212- the bird {\i chatas} \u8212- since halacha permits bringing a {\i chatas ha-of} under conditions of doubt. For the {\i tahara} side, he brings an {\i olas behemah b\u8217'tenai}: an animal {\i olah} with a conditional stipulation, because an {\i olah} can be offered as a voluntary {\i nedavah} even by someone who is {\i tamei} and therefore cannot validly complete a full {\i korban tahara}. He stipulates: if I am {\i tahor}, this {\i olah} fulfills part of my {\i korban tahara} obligation; if I am {\i tamei}, it is merely a {\i nedavah}. He cannot bring a full {\i chatas} of an animal on a conditional basis because an animal {\i chatas} may not be brought under conditions of doubt \u8212- only the bird {\i chatas} has that dispensation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After this initial round, he waits another thirty days and brings the remainder. If he was {\i tamei}, his {\i olah} at the first stage was a {\i nedavah} and he has not yet brought any component of his {\i korban tahara}, so he must now bring the complete set \u8212- {\i olah}, {\i chatas}, and {\i shelamim} (the {\i korban tahara} does include a {\i shelamim}). If he was {\i tahor}, he already brought his {\i olah} at the first stage, so he need only bring the {\i chatas} and {\i shelamim} now, with the second {\i olah} offered {\i b\u8217'tenai}: if I was {\i tamei} previously, this {\i olah} is now obligatory as part of my {\i korban tahara}; if I was {\i tahor}, then my obligation has already been discharged and this one is a {\i nedavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is what one would expect the {\i mishnah} to say about a person left alone after his partner dies. Yet the {\i mishnah} takes a more surprising path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marketplace Partner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Instead of treating the surviving {\i nazir} as a simple {\i safek tamei}, Rabbi Yehoshua proposes that he go out and find a stranger in the marketplace \u8212- {\i yevakesh lo echad min hashuk} \u8212- and recruit him to enter into the same kind of stipulated {\i nezirus} partnership that would have existed with the original partner. The practical mechanics of this, however, are considerably more intricate than what the original two partners had to do, precisely because the stranger is not himself {\i tamei} in any way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If I Am Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After thirty days of counting together, the original {\i nazir} declares to the stranger: {\i im tamei hayiti} \u8212- if I was the {\i tamei} one, then your {\i nezirus} began immediately from the moment we made this arrangement. We are now at the end of thirty days. In that scenario, the {\i korban tumah} brought now is mine, and the {\i korban tahara} is yours. The stranger, having completed thirty days of {\i nezirus tahara}, may shave and is done. The original {\i nazir}, having now brought his {\i korban tumah}, will then count thirty more days of {\i nezirus tahara} and bring his {\i korban tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If I Am Tahor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stipulation continues: {\i v\u8217'im tahor hayisi} \u8212- if I was the {\i tahor} one, then your {\i nezirus} only began at this moment, not thirty days ago. In that case, the {\i korban tahara} being brought now is mine \u8212- I was {\i tahor} all along and have completed my thirty days. The {\i korban tumah} in this scenario is brought in a state of doubt; it counts for nobody definitively, but since we bring only what can be validly offered {\i b\u8217'safek}, we proceed accordingly. The stranger\u8217's {\i nezirus} having just started, he will wait another thirty days and bring his own {\i korban tahara} at that point. And in that second round, the original {\i nazir} makes a further declaration: if I was the {\i tamei} one, then the earlier {\i korban tumah} was mine and the earlier {\i korban tahara} was yours \u8212- and this final {\i korban tahara} is mine. If I was the {\i tahor} one, then the earlier {\i korban tahara} was mine, the {\i korban tumah} was a {\i safek} offering belonging to no one, and this {\i korban tahara} now concludes your {\i nezirus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Wait 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might well ask: why does the original {\i nazir} wait thirty days before even approaching the stranger with this arrangement? Why not find the stranger immediately and stipulate from the outset?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer becomes clear when one considers what happens at the end of thirty days. Not only must the {\i korbanos} be brought, but both partners must shave. And the shaving is the crux of the problem. If I was {\i tamei}, then the stranger completed thirty days of {\i nezirus tahara} and may shave legitimately following his {\i korban tahara}. But if I was {\i tahor}, then in the scenario where the stranger\u8217's {\i nezirus} had already begun thirty days earlier, the stranger now needs to shave as well \u8212- yet in that scenario, the offering brought was my {\i korban tahara}, not his. His {\i korban tahara} is only coming in the next round. How, then, can he shave now?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One solution would be to introduce yet another animal \u8212- an {\i olah b\u8217'tenai} \u8212- specifically to cover the stranger\u8217's potential need for a {\i korban tahara} at this stage. But Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's approach avoids this complication elegantly: by stipulating that if I was {\i tahor}, your {\i nezirus} did not begin until this very moment, the stranger\u8217's {\i nezirus tahara} has not yet run its full course when the first shaving takes place. He is therefore not shaving at the conclusion of a {\i nezirus} at all \u8212- his {\i nezirus} has not yet ended \u8212- and there is no violation. The shaving at the thirty-day mark does not terminate a {\i nezirus} he is still in; it simply is a haircut that precedes the start of the relevant thirty-day period of his {\i nezirus tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To be precise about the function of this hair-cutting: it is not a positive ritual act concluding a {\i nezirus}; rather, the entire purpose of the stipulation is to ensure that the haircut does not {\i violate} a {\i nezirus}. If the stranger\u8217's {\i nezirus} had already started and he shaved without a valid {\i korban}, it would be a violation. By deferring the start of his {\i nezirus} in that scenario, the haircut remains permissible even without a {\i korban} \u8212- and then his thirty-day {\i nezirus tahara} begins fresh afterward. This is why the stipulation must be constructed so carefully, and why the full thirty-day waiting period before approaching the stranger is an integral part of Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's design.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ben Zoma Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection: Who Agrees?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Zoma challenges Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's proposal at its most basic level: {\i u\u8217'mi sheshomeia lo sheyidor kenegdo b\u8217'nazir} \u8212- who in the world is going to agree to this arrangement? Who will volunteer to take on {\i nezirus} and go through this elaborate conditional protocol simply as a favor to a stranger? The practical objection is decisive on its own. But Ben Zoma goes further and argues that the entire mechanism is unnecessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simpler Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Forget about finding a partner, says Ben Zoma. This person is simply a {\i safek tamei nazir}. Treat him as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He brings a {\i chatas ha-of} and an {\i olas behemah}, just as we described above for the standard {\i safek tamei nazir}. He stipulates: {\i im tamei hayisi}, the {\i chatas} is my obligatory {\i korban tumah} and the {\i olah} is a {\i nedavah}; {\i v\u8217'im tahor hayisi}, the {\i olah} is my obligatory {\i korban tahara} offering and the {\i chatas} is a {\i safek} \u8212- I was not obligated, so we dispose of it accordingly. This allows him to shave at the end of thirty days in either scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then waits another thirty days and brings the full {\i korban tahara} \u8212- {\i olah}, {\i chatas}, and {\i shelamim}. He stipulates again: if I was {\i tamei}, then the first {\i olah} was a {\i nedavah} and this entire set is now my obligatory {\i korban tahara}; if I was {\i tahor}, then the first {\i olah} was my obligatory offering, and this final {\i olah} is a {\i nedavah} while the {\i chatas} and {\i shelamim} complete my {\i korban tahara} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is straightforward, it avoids the need for any stranger from the marketplace, and it achieves the same result.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Splitting Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Split Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua responds to Ben Zoma\u8217's simpler method with the objection: {\i nimtza ze mevi korbanotav lachatza\u8217'in} \u8212- you are causing this person to split his {\i korban tahara} into two stages. In Ben Zoma\u8217's scheme, the {\i olah} of the {\i korban tahara} comes at thirty days, while the {\i chatas} and {\i shelamim} come only at sixty days. The {\i korban tahara} is thus delivered in two installments rather than as a complete unit. Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's marketplace arrangement ensures that each person brings a full, undivided {\i korban tahara} at their respective stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, is unimpressed with this response. The objection that one\u8217's {\i korban} is split into two stages does not seem like a compelling enough reason to impose the entire complicated apparatus of finding a stranger, negotiating a conditional {\i nezirus}, and drafting elaborate stipulations. The sages agreed with Ben Zoma \u8212- {\i hodu lo chachamim l\u8217'ven Zoma} \u8212- and this is not a close call. The Gemara treats Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's objection as weak.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sharpen Students\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel to offer a remarkable interpretation: {\i lo amar Rabbi Yehoshua ela lechaded bo et hatalmidim} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua only said it to sharpen his students. He was not actually advocating for the marketplace-stranger approach as normative halacha; he presented it as an intellectual exercise to stimulate discussion and sharpen the analytical capacities of his disciples.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, as the Gemara itself implies, a somewhat strange use of that phrase. Typically, {\i lechaded et hatalmidim} refers to a rhetorical device \u8212- presenting a position to see how students respond, not actually ruling that way. But here Rabbi Yehoshua laid out a detailed, fully-articulated halachic ruling with specific stipulations and scenarios. It is not obvious how an elaborate {\i psak} constitutes merely sharpening the students, and the Gemara leaves the precise meaning somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fat Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman then adds a further criticism of Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position with the cryptic remark: {\i mai avidei leh Rabbi Yehoshua ledakei delo lissrach} \u8212- what should Rabbi Yehoshua do for his intestines that they should not spoil?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This phrase is opaque. {\i Tosafot} notes that it is unexplained \u8212- {\i lo ipparesh} \u8212- and then attempts an interpretation involving the fat of the offerings. The idea, as the {\i Rishonim} develop it, is that when two {\i nezirim} share a {\i korban} and must perform {\i tenufa} together, they must synchronize their shavings, and the delays involved in waiting for both partners to complete their respective stages could cause the fat of the offerings to sit unburned for an inappropriately long time, creating its own set of problems. Whatever advantage Rabbi Yehoshua thought he gained by avoiding the splitting of the {\i korban tahara}, he loses because the shared arrangement between the original {\i nazir} and the marketplace stranger generates delays that compromise the timely burning of the fat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Others read the phrase as simply a colorful expression of dismissal \u8212- something to the effect that Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position is so untenable that one cannot even fathom what motivated it. The precise intent of the remark remains genuinely unclear among the {\i Rishonim}, and one should not pretend otherwise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Tumah & Metzora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} introduces a compound case of uncertainty: a {\i nazir} who does not know whether he became {\i tamei meis} \u8212- ritually impure through contact with a corpse \u8212- or whether he is a {\i metzora}, a person afflicted with the skin condition whose {\i tumah} and subsequent purification process follow an entirely different track.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states that such a person may eat {\i kodashim} after sixty days, may drink wine after sixty days, and may contract impurity from the dead after one hundred and twenty days. These numbers emerge from a careful analysis of all the possible scenarios he might be in and the constraints each one imposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key insight driving the entire analysis is the following: regardless of which status this person actually has, the possibility that he might be a standard {\i nazir tahor} \u8212- one who is neither {\i tamei} nor a {\i metzora} \u8212- prevents him from shaving until he has completed at least thirty days of {\i nezirus}. A {\i nazir tahor} may not cut his hair until his {\i nezirus} is finished, and his {\i nezirus} requires at minimum thirty days. This thirty-day constraint, imposed by the mere possibility that he might be a {\i nazir tahor}, cascades through the entire sequence and forces the entire analysis into thirty-day (or year-long, as we will see) increments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tahor Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this person turns out to be purely {\i tahor} \u8212- neither {\i tamei} nor a {\i metzora} \u8212- he simply waits thirty days, shaves, and brings the full {\i korban tahara}. That is his complete {\i nezirus} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he turns out to be a {\i nazir tamei}, the process is: seven days of purification through the sprinkling of the {\i parah adumah} ashes, then shaving at the conclusion of that purification, and then thirty days of {\i nezirus tahara} leading to a second shaving and the {\i korban tahara}. His {\i tumah} ceased at the halachic level after seven days regardless of offerings; the {\i korbanos} are what trigger the start of the {\i nezirus tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he turns out to be a definite {\i metzora} who is also a {\i nazir}, the procedure for a {\i nazir metzora} would involve shaving immediately upon confirmation of the {\i metzora} status, then a seven-day wait, then a second shaving as part of the {\i metzora} purification, followed by the resumption of the {\i nezirus} with its own waiting period and final shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 30-Day Constraint\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since we do not know which of these scenarios he is actually in, and since the {\i nazir tahor} possibility prevents any shaving before thirty days, the entire analysis gets shifted. We cannot shave immediately even if he might be a {\i metzora}, because we must account for the possibility that he is a {\i nazir tahor}, for whom a premature haircut would constitute a violation of his {\i nezirus}. This single constraint \u8212- the {\i nazir tahor} possibility \u8212- forces the first shaving to be deferred to day thirty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four 30-Day Periods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here is how the full timeline unfolds. Because we must wait until day thirty for the first shaving, that first shaving at day thirty can serve double duty: it counts as the shaving of a {\i nazir tamei} (if that is his status) and simultaneously as the first shaving of a {\i metzora} (if that is his status). After that first shaving, one might think we could proceed to the second shaving after just seven more days \u8212- seven days being the waiting period between the first and second shaving of a {\i metzora}, and also sufficient time for the {\i tamei meis} purification. But we cannot: if he was a {\i nazir tamei}, then his first shaving now obligates him to begin thirty days of {\i nezirus tahara}, and he cannot shave again until that thirty-day period is complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the second shaving is deferred to day sixty. At day sixty, a person who was purely {\i tahor} has long since completed his {\i nezirus} \u8212- he finished at day thirty. A person who was purely {\i tamei meis} finished his {\i nezirus tahara} at day sixty and is now done. A person who was a {\i metzora} but not {\i tamei meis} has now completed both his {\i metzora} shavings (the first at day thirty, the second at day sixty), and his {\i nezirus}, which was suspended during his {\i metzora} status, now resumes. Since the resumed {\i nezirus} might itself be a {\i nezirus tahara} of thirty days (corresponding to the scenario where he was a {\i tahor nazir} who also happened to be a {\i metzora}), he must wait yet another thirty days before the third shaving. And finally, if he was both {\i tamei meis} and a {\i metzora}, then his {\i nezirus tahara} only begins after all the {\i metzora} processing is complete \u8212- which means it only begins at day sixty, and he will not finish until day ninety.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields four consecutive thirty-day periods and, effectively, four shavings or stages at days thirty, sixty, ninety, and one hundred and twenty. By day sixty he is definitively clear of {\i metzora} status and may therefore eat {\i kodashim} (since the {\i kodashim} prohibition flows from his {\i tumah} statuses, not his {\i nezirus} obligations). And by day one hundred and twenty he has definitively completed his {\i nezirus} in all possible scenarios and may drink wine and contract impurity from the dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The driving logic of the four stages corresponds to four possible scenarios: (1) purely {\i tahor} \u8212- {\i nezirus tahara} concludes at stage one; (2) purely {\i tamei meis} \u8212- {\i nezirus tahara} concludes at stage two; (3) {\i tahor meis} but {\i metzora} \u8212- {\i nezirus tahara} concludes at stage three; (4) {\i tamei meis} and {\i metzora} \u8212- {\i nezirus tahara} concludes at stage four. Each scenario places the genuine {\i nezirus tahara} at a different stage, and the uncertainty forces us to wait through all four.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Year-Long Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then notes, and a {\i baraita} elaborates: all of the above applies when the {\i nezirus} is of the standard minimum duration \u8212- thirty days. But if this {\i nazir} had accepted upon himself a {\i nezirus} of one year, then each thirty-day unit in the above analysis expands to a full year. He may eat {\i kodashim} after two years, and may drink wine and become {\i tamei} for the dead after four years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is intrinsic to the structure: the reason each unit had to be thirty days was that the possible start of a {\i nezirus tahara} at each stage required waiting out its full length. If the {\i nezirus tahara} is one year long rather than thirty days, then each waiting period becomes one year. A {\i nazir tamei} must wait, after his first shaving, the full length of his {\i nezirus} before his next shaving; if his {\i nezirus} is a year long, he waits a year. A {\i metzora nazir} whose {\i metzora} processing concludes at a given stage and who then resumes his {\i nezirus tahara} must wait the full year before his next shaving. Thus each of the four stages now spans one year, yielding a total timeline of four years.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Haircuts & Offers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the structure of the four stages, the Gemara now presents a {\i baraita} that specifies precisely which {\i korbanos} are brought at each of the four shavings. This requires care, because at each stage the person might be in a different halachic status, and only offerings that can be validly brought under conditions of uncertainty are permissible. The {\i baraita} states: at the first shaving, one brings {\i tzipporim}, a {\i chatas ha-of}, and an {\i olas behemah}; at the second shaving, a {\i chatas ha-of} and an {\i olas behemah}; at the third shaving, a {\i chatas ha-of} and an {\i olas behemah}; and at the fourth shaving, the full {\i korban tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Haircut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the first shaving \u8212- which occurs at day thirty \u8212- the person might be in any of three possible halachic states. He might be a {\i nazir tahor} completing his {\i nezirus}, for which the {\i olah} component of the {\i korban tahara} applies; an {\i olas behemah b\u8217'tenai} can be brought because even if he turns out to be {\i tamei}, the {\i olah} is valid as a {\i nedavah}. He might be a {\i nazir tamei} who has just concluded his purification and is initiating his {\i nezirus tahara}, for which the {\i chatas ha-of} applies; a {\i chatas ha-of} may be brought under conditions of doubt, and it serves as the {\i korban tumah} of a {\i nazir tamei}. And he might be a {\i metzora} at stage one of his purification process, for which the {\i tzipporim} \u8212- the birds used in the {\i metzora} purification ritual \u8212- apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birds & Chatas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to distinguish carefully between the {\i tzipporim} and the {\i chatas ha-of}, as they can be confused. The {\i tzipporim} of the {\i metzora} purification are not a {\i korban} at all. They are a purification ritual performed outside the {\i beit hamikdash}: one bird is slaughtered, its blood collected in a vessel, the living bird is dipped into the blood, and the living bird is released into the field. It is analogous, in its nature as a non-{\i korban} purification act, to the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} \u8212- it is a {\i tahara} process, not a sacrificial offering. The {\i chatas ha-of}, by contrast, is an actual {\i korban} brought within the {\i beit hamikdash}, offered on the altar. It is one of the {\i korbanos} required of a {\i metzora} at the second stage of his purification, and it is also one of the {\i korbanos} of a {\i nazir tamei}. The halachic dispensation permitting a {\i chatas ha-of} to be brought under conditions of {\i safek} makes it valid for both purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tzipporim} are brought at the first shaving because at that stage one might be a {\i metzora} at stage one of purification, and that is when the {\i tzipporim} ritual takes place. The {\i chatas ha-of} is not a {\i metzora} stage-one requirement but is brought at the first shaving because of the possibility of being a {\i nazir tamei}, for whom it is required as part of the {\i korban tumah} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Haircut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the second shaving \u8212- day sixty \u8212- the {\i baraita} states that one brings a {\i chatas ha-of} and an {\i olas behemah}, but not {\i tzipporim}. The {\i tzipporim} are absent because, by day sixty, any {\i metzora} scenario has advanced beyond stage one \u8212- the {\i tzipporim} ritual was already performed at day thirty. The {\i chatas ha-of} and {\i olas behemah} continue to be brought because at the second shaving there are still multiple possible statuses the person might be in, and these two offerings cover the relevant possibilities in the same way they did at the first stage. The full analysis of what each offering covers at the second and subsequent shavings will be taken up in the continuation of the {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 60\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Case Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tani ala} with which this daf opens presents a case examined previously: a person who is a {\i safek nazir tamei} \u8212- perhaps he contracted {\i tumat meit} \u8212- and simultaneously a {\i safek metzora}. The Gemara\u8217's ruling is that he must wait 120 days in total: 60 days to exit the status of {\i metzora} and 60 days to exit {\i nezirut tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Shavings Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure of that 120-day period flows from the fact that such a person will ultimately require four shavings ({\i giluachim}). As a {\i nazir metzora}, one shaves upon becoming {\i tamei} and again at the completion of the {\i tahara} process. As a {\i metzora}, one shaves when the {\i tzara\u8217'at} begins to clear and again seven days later at the end of the {\i tahara} process. As a {\i nazir tamei}, one shaves upon the onset of {\i tumah} and once more upon completing the {\i nezirut} of {\i tahara}. Taken together, four distinct shavings emerge. Under ordinary circumstances these could occur in rapid succession, but our case demands substantial gaps between each one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 30-Day Gap Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason 30-day gaps are required is that at every stage, the individual might be a {\i nazir tahor} \u8212- in the midst of a standard, uninterrupted {\i nezirut} of {\i tahara} \u8212- and shaving in the middle of one\u8217's {\i nezirut} is prohibited by {\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho}. Consequently, at each juncture where a shaving might be called for, he must first wait out a full 30 days, since only then will even a {\i nazir tahor} have completed his term and be permitted to shave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Board Walkthrough\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To walk through this concretely: a normal {\i nazir} completes 30 days and then shaves and brings a {\i korban}. A {\i nazir tamei} waits seven days, shaves and brings a {\i korban}, and then does 30 days of {\i tahara} before a second shaving and {\i korban} \u8212- technically day 37. A {\i metzora}, when the condition clears, performs the bird ritual ({\i tziporim}), shaves after a waiting period, and then seven days later shaves again and brings a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, for our {\i safek} individual: he cannot begin by immediately performing any shaving, because he might be a {\i nazir tahor} who cannot shave mid-{\i nezirut}. He must first wait 30 days. After those 30 days, each possible status is satisfied: even a {\i nazir tamei} has long since passed his seven-day threshold; a {\i metzora} was always ready to shave; and a {\i nazir tahor} has now completed his requisite 30 days. So the first {\i giluach} takes place at day 30.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following that first shaving, the {\i nazir tamei} scenario would ordinarily call for 30 days of {\i tahara} followed by a second shaving. The {\i metzora} scenario would ordinarily call for shaving again after just seven more days. But because our individual might be a {\i nazir tahor} who has just begun a new 30-day term, he cannot shave again after a mere seven days \u8212- doing so would violate a possible {\i nezirut tahara}. He must wait another 30 days. At day 60, the second {\i giluach} occurs. At this point, the {\i nazir tamei}-turned-{\i nazir tahor} scenario is complete, and the {\i metzora} who had his bird ritual at day 30 and his second shaving now at day 60 is also done with his {\i metzora} process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the person who was both a {\i nazir tamei} and a {\i metzora} is not yet finished, because \u8212- and this is an important principle the Gemara will develop \u8212- the {\i giluach} of {\i metzora} does not count toward the {\i giluach} of {\i nezirut tumah}. These shavings do not double-count. So after day 60, this individual\u8217's scenario of being a {\i nazir tamei metzora} requires restarting: he still needs a {\i giluach} for {\i tumah} and a {\i giluach} for {\i tahara}. But he cannot immediately perform the {\i giluach} for {\i tumah} because, at this point, he might be entering a fresh {\i nezirut tahara} \u8212- and shaving would again violate it. So he waits yet another 30 days, arriving at day 90 for the third {\i giluach}, at which point he can address his {\i nezirut tumah}. Then, yet another 30 days must pass \u8212- since the {\i nazir tahor} term might be running again \u8212- until the fourth and final {\i giluach} at day 120, completing his {\i nezirut tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle woven throughout is that each waiting period is designed so that no possible {\i nezirut tahara} is violated. It is worth noting that being a {\i metzora} does not count toward one\u8217's {\i nezirut}: even a {\i vadai nazir metzora} must still complete a full 30 days of {\i nezirut} after the {\i metzora} period concludes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Year-Long Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same structure applies if the {\i nezirut} in question was a year-long {\i nezirut}. Since all of the 30-day gaps exist to avoid violating a possible {\i nezirut tahara}, if the {\i nezirut} is one of a full year, those gaps must correspondingly expand to year-long intervals. The Gemara states: {\i binezirut bas shanah}, for a year-long {\i nezirut}, he may eat {\i kodshim} only after two years and 36 days, and may become {\i tamei} for the dead only after an even longer period.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korbonot Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permissible Offerings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning from the timeline to the {\i korbanot} that accompany each stage, the question is: in a {\i safek} situation, which offerings can actually be brought? It is helpful to survey the relevant {\i korbanot} for each possible status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a {\i metzora} at stage one, the process consists of the bird ritual ({\i tziporim}) \u8212- one bird is slaughtered, the other is dipped and released. This is performed outside the {\i beit hamikdash} entirely, and since it does not constitute a formal {\i korban}, anyone may technically perform this ritual regardless of his status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At stage two of {\i metzora}, the full {\i korban} normally consists of two lambs, a {\i chatat}, and the like. However, the Torah makes an exception for a poor person ({\i dal}): {\i v\u8217'im dal hu v\u8217'ein yado maseget}, he brings instead a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} (bird {\i chatat}) and an {\i olat ha\u8217'of} (bird {\i olah}), along with an {\i asham}. The {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} is significant here because a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} may be brought {\i b\u8217'safek} \u8212- the Torah permits a doubtful {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} in a way that is not permitted for an ordinary animal {\i chatat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a {\i nazir tamei}, he too brings a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} and an {\i olat ha\u8217'of} (again, this applies specifically if he is a {\i dal}, though the details may require further verification). This {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} can likewise be brought {\i b\u8217'safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a {\i nazir tahor} completing his {\i nezirut}, the standard {\i korban} consists of an {\i olah}, a {\i chatat}, and a {\i shelamim}. The {\i olah} can be brought conditionally ({\i b\u8217'tenai}): it is either his obligatory {\i olah} or, if it is not needed as an obligation, it counts as a {\i olat nedavah} \u8212- a free-will offering. The {\i tziporim}, as noted, are simply a ritual performed outside and carry no prohibition for anyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek and Tenai Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic is as follows. A standard {\i chatat} cannot be brought in a state of doubt \u8212- the rule of {\i safek chatat} is well established. But the Torah treats a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} differently, and it may be brought even when one is uncertain whether the obligation applies. An {\i olah}, on the other hand, is inherently flexible: it can be either a fulfillment of an obligation or a gift \u8212- {\i olat nedavah}. Therefore, in any {\i safek} situation, an {\i olah} can be brought {\i b\u8217'tenai}: if this is my obligatory {\i olah}, it counts as such; if not, it is a free-will sacrifice. The {\i tziporim} ritual, occurring entirely outside the {\i mikdash}, raises no issues of bringing {\i kodshim} in vain \u8212- it is simply a ritual that may be performed without harm regardless of whether the obligation applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason it is necessary to bring these {\i korbanot} at each stage at all is that in order to advance from one stage to the next \u8212- whether from {\i tumah} to beginning {\i tahara}, or from {\i metzora} status to the next phase \u8212- a minimal {\i korban} must be brought. A {\i nazir tamei} cannot begin his {\i nezirut tahara} without bringing his minimal {\i korban}. A {\i metzora} cannot advance to the next stage without a minimal {\i korban}. And a {\i nazir tahor} cannot complete his {\i nezirut} without bringing his {\i korban} either. Since progression at each stage requires some minimal set of offerings, the {\i halacha} requires bringing whatever subset of {\i korbanot} can be legitimately offered in a {\i safek} context.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korbonot Overview (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Stages Korbonot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} itself states: {\i umegalei\u8217'ach arba tiglachyot} \u8212- he shaves four shavings. At the first shaving, he brings {\i tziporim}, a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of}, and an {\i olat behema}. At the second shaving, a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} and an {\i olat behema}. At the third shaving, again a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} and an {\i olat behema}. At the fourth shaving, he brings the full {\i korban tahara}, with a conditional stipulation regarding the {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale at each stage follows directly from which statuses are still plausible. At the first shaving, the individual might be at {\i metzora} stage one (requiring {\i tziporim}), or a {\i nazir tamei} (requiring a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of}), or a {\i nazir tahor} completing his {\i nezirut} (requiring an {\i olat behema}). Hence all three categories of offerings are brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the second shaving, having already performed the {\i tziporim} at the first shaving, stage one of {\i metzora} is concluded \u8212- there is no longer any need to repeat that ritual. The remaining possibilities are: completing {\i metzora} stage two (requiring a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of}), or completing a {\i nezirut tahara} (requiring an {\i olat behema}). So two types of {\i korban} suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the third shaving, {\i metzora} as a category is entirely off the table \u8212- it was disposed of by the second shaving. Now the possibilities are: the individual was a {\i nazir tamei} who has arrived at the end of his {\i nezirut tumah} (requiring a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of}), or he is a {\i nazir tahor} completing his term (requiring an {\i olat behema}). Again, two types.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the fourth shaving, only one status remains: {\i nezirut tahara}. He brings the full {\i korban tahara} \u8212- {\i olah}, {\i chatat}, and {\i shelamim} \u8212- and makes the conditional stipulation described below regarding the {\i olah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asham Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One glaring omission stands out from this analysis: the {\i braita} makes no mention of an {\i asham}. This is puzzling, because the {\i asham} is critical for the {\i metzora} \u8212- the Gemara elsewhere establishes that the {\i asham} is the central vehicle of {\i kapara} for a {\i metzora}, and without it his {\i tahara} is not complete; it is {\i me\u8217'akev}. An {\i asham} for {\i nazir tamei} exists as well, though it is not {\i me\u8217'akev} in the same way. The {\i braita}\u8217's silence on the {\i asham} is therefore striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this difficulty by invoking the position of {\i Rabbi Shimon}, who holds that one may bring an {\i asham b\u8217'tenai}: the animal is brought conditionally \u8212- if it is indeed needed as an {\i asham}, it functions as one; if not, it becomes a {\i shelamim}. This works because the {\i halacha} is that the remainder of an {\i asham} ({\i mosar asham}) goes to {\i shelamim} \u8212- the same animal that would serve as an {\i asham} can, under certain conditions, be redirected as a {\i shelamim}. Accordingly, the {\i braita} follows {\i Rabbi Shimon\u8217's} position, which allows the {\i asham} to be brought conditionally even in a {\i safek} context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara does not directly address what becomes of this individual if one does not rule in accordance with {\i Rabbi Shimon}. That question is left without a clear resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further ask \u8212- as {\i Tosafot} does \u8212- why the {\i braita} does not also mention an {\i olat ha\u8217'of} at various stages (which could be brought {\i b\u8217'tenai} just like an {\i olah behema}), or a {\i shelamim} with an appropriate stipulation. The answer given by {\i Tosafot} is that the goal throughout is to do the bare minimum that is {\i halachically} required to be {\i yotze}. There is no benefit to bringing additional {\i korbanot} whose obligation is uncertain, and the {\i braita} accordingly confines itself to the minimal set that is both necessary and permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita Four Shavings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaving One: All Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the first shaving, the individual might be any one of three things: at {\i metzora} stage one, a {\i nazir tamei}, or a {\i nazir tahor}. The {\i braita} therefore prescribes three categories of {\i korban}: {\i tziporim} for {\i metzora} stage one, a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} for {\i nazir tamei}, and an {\i olat behema} for {\i nazir tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaving Two: Two Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the second shaving, the two remaining possibilities are completing {\i metzora} stage two or completing a {\i nezirut tahara}. A {\i nazir tahor} who finished at stage one is done; a {\i nazir tamei} who shaved at stage one has now transitioned and is completing {\i nezirut tahara} at stage two. The {\i braita} thus prescribes only a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} and an {\i olat behema}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaving Three: Two Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the third shaving, {\i metzora} has been fully resolved. What remains are the possibilities of ending a {\i nezirut tumah} (requiring {\i chatat ha\u8217'of}) or ending a {\i nezirut tahara} (requiring {\i olat behema}). Again, the {\i braita} prescribes a {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} and an {\i olat behema}, though for different underlying reasons than at the second shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaving Four: Full Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the fourth and final shaving, only {\i nezirut tahara} remains. He brings the full {\i korban tahara} \u8212- the complete set of {\i korbanot} owed by a {\i nazir tahor} at the end of his term \u8212- and stipulates regarding the {\i olah}: if I was already a {\i nazir tahor} at one of the earlier stages and brought my obligatory {\i olah} then, that earlier {\i olah} was my {\i chovah} and this one is a {\i nedava}; if only now am I completing my {\i nezirut tahara}, then any {\i olah} I brought earlier was a {\i nedavah} and this present one is my {\i chovah}. Either way, the {\i chatat} and {\i shelamim} are brought in full as the remainder of his {\i korban}, since even if he discharged his {\i olah} obligation earlier, the rest of the {\i korban} was never brought.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Shaving Justified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now works through each stage in detail, confirming that the offerings prescribed at each shaving cover every possible scenario without violating any prohibition. At the first shaving, the Gemara says: {\i mima nafshach} \u8212- whichever way you look at it, everything brought at stage one is appropriate. {\i Shapir ka mayti} \u8212- it is well brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he was a {\i vadai metzora} and not a {\i nazir tamei}, then {\i tziporim chovato} \u8212- the bird ritual is his obligation. The {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} that he brought because he might be a {\i nazir tamei} is brought {\i b\u8217'safek} and accordingly goes to burial ({\i likvurah}) \u8212- that is what is done when a doubtful {\i chatat} is set aside. And the {\i olat behema} he brought in case he was a {\i nazir tahor} counts as a free-will {\i nedavah}. No prohibition has been violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he was not a {\i metzora} but was a {\i nazir tamei}, then the {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} is his obligatory {\i korban}. The {\i tziporim} ritual that was performed \u8212- {\i avra\u8217'i ka mita\u8217'avdin} \u8212- was done entirely outside the {\i beit hamikdash} and constitutes no {\i korban} at all, so it is harmless; it is not a case of {\i chulin ba\u8217'azarah}. The {\i olat behema} is a {\i nedavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And if he was neither a {\i metzora} nor a {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- meaning he was simply a {\i nazir tahor} \u8212- then the {\i tziporim} were done outside with no consequence, the {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} is buried as a doubtful offering, and the {\i olat behema} is his {\i chovah}. All three are fully accounted for. Additionally, why not shave immediately within seven days rather than waiting 30? Because {\i dilma lav metzora hu} \u8212- perhaps he is not a {\i metzora} at all, and the Torah says {\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho}; as a possible {\i nazir tahor}, shaving is forbidden until {\i ad melot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shavings Two-Three Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the second and third shavings, the Gemara notes that the {\i tziporim} are no longer required \u8212- {\i tziporin lo tzarich}. The bird ritual was only relevant at stage one of {\i metzora}, and since that was already done at the first shaving, {\i deha avid} \u8212- it has been discharged. What then remains at stage two? The Gemara puts it neatly: {\i mai ikka dilma vadai muchlat} \u8212- you might say he is definitively a {\i muchlat}, a confirmed {\i metzora}. At that stage, one {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} covers {\i safek sipro} \u8212- the doubt as to whether he is completing {\i metzora} stage two \u8212- and the {\i olat behema} covers his possible {\i nezirut tahara}. At stage three, one {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} covers {\i safek tumato} \u8212- the doubt as to whether he is ending his {\i nezirut tumah} \u8212- and again the {\i olat behema} covers {\i nezirut tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double-Function Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara identifies a particularly elegant convergence at the second shaving. At that point, two scenarios simultaneously find resolution in a single {\i giluach}: the {\i metzora} stage two shaving and the completion of a {\i nazir tahor}\u8217's term. The two are not the same act performed for two different reasons in a single moment \u8212- rather, each {\i korban} brought at that shaving maps precisely onto one of the two possible statuses. The {\i chatat ha\u8217'of} addresses the {\i safek} of being a {\i muchlat} completing his {\i metzora} cycle ({\i safek sipro}), while the {\i olat behema} addresses the {\i safek} of being a {\i nazir tahor} completing his {\i nezirut}. They are distinct {\i korbanot} for distinct obligations \u8212- and the shaving, occurring once, technically serves each in its own right. In a {\i safek} situation, each individual {\i korban} is justified by the relevant possible status, and the {\i giluach} itself is the one act that all scenarios require at that juncture.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixed Certainty Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Tamei Vadai Muchlat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through the fully {\i safek} case, the Gemara turns to mixed scenarios where one of the two statuses is certain. The first such case is {\i tamei safek u\u8217'muchlat vadai} \u8212- he is definitively a {\i metzora} ({\i vadai muchlat}), but only possibly a {\i nazir tamei}. The Gemara rules that he may eat {\i kodshim} after eight days, and may become {\i tamei} for the dead after 76 days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning runs as follows. Because he is {\i vadai metzora}, the {\i halacha} is that the shaving of a definite {\i metzora} takes precedence even over the prohibition against a {\i nazir} shaving. Therefore, he shaves immediately at the outset of his {\i metzora} process, and again after seven days \u8212- the standard {\i metzora} timeline. The {\i korban} is then brought on day eight, allowing him to eat {\i kodshim}. At this point, the {\i metzora} process is complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He is now left with the {\i safek} of being a {\i nazir tamei}. Since he is only {\i possibly} tamei, he cannot immediately shave for {\i tumah} \u8212- he must wait lest he be a {\i nazir tahor} who cannot shave mid-{\i nezirut}. He therefore waits 30 days and shaves, and then waits another 30 days for his {\i nezirut tahara} and shaves again. That yields an additional 67 days beyond day eight, totaling 76 days until he is fully out of his {\i nezirut} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vadai Tamei Safek Muchlat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second mixed case is {\i muchlat safek v\u8217'tamei vadai} \u8212- he is definitively a {\i nazir tamei} but only possibly a {\i metzora}. The Gemara rules that he may eat {\i kodshim} after 37 days and may become {\i tamei} for the dead after 74 days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the logic is more nuanced. He cannot shave immediately, even as a {\i nazir tamei}, because if he is also a {\i metzora}, then the {\i metzora} process must be addressed first \u8212- {\i tzara\u8217'at} takes precedence. However, since he is {\i vadai nazir tamei}, he may shave after seven days, as the seven-day {\i tahara} process for {\i tumat meit} is complete. Crucially, that shaving \u8212- mandated by his definite {\i nazir tamei} status \u8212- can simultaneously serve as {\i giluach} number one for his possible {\i metzora} status, since he is shaving anyway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After that first shaving at day seven, the next shaving in the {\i metzora} sequence would ordinarily follow after another seven days. But because he may now be beginning his {\i nezirut tahara} as a {\i nazir tamei}, he cannot shave again within that time without potentially violating a {\i nezirut tahor}. He must therefore wait 30 days \u8212- arriving at day 37 \u8212- where he performs the second {\i metzora} shaving. At this point, his {\i safek metzora} status is fully resolved, and he may eat {\i kodshim} at day 37 (strictly speaking, after the day-37 {\i korban}, which the Gemara counts as eating {\i kodshim l\u8217'achar shloshim v\u8217'shivah yamim}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Only now, with the {\i metzora} process behind him, can he address his {\i nazir tamei} status as its own matter. The shaving at day seven cannot double-count for both {\i metzora} and {\i nazir tamei} simultaneously \u8212- that is the principle the Gemara will elaborate on extensively. He must now wait seven days (for {\i tumat meit} purification) and then 30 days (for {\i nezirut tahara}), adding another 37 days. Seven plus 30 plus 37 equals 74 days total until he is fully clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vadai Both Definite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The simplest case is {\i tamei vadai u\u8217'muchlat vadai} \u8212- he is definitively a {\i nazir tamei} and definitively a {\i metzora}. The Gemara rules that he may eat {\i kodshim} after eight days and may become {\i tamei} for the dead after 44 days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since both statuses are certain, the {\i vadai metzora} shaving proceeds immediately: shaving one on day one, shaving two on day seven, {\i korban} on day eight \u8212- he eats {\i kodshim} on day eight. Then, because the {\i metzora} shavings do not count for {\i nazir tamei}, he must now do the full {\i nazir tamei} sequence: seven days for {\i tumah}, followed by 30 days of {\i nezirut tahara} \u8212- 37 additional days. Eight plus 37 gives 44 days until he may become {\i tamei} for the dead again.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Double-Counting Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Student Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concluding section of the {\i daf} moves to a {\i tosefta} that addresses what has been implicit throughout: why cannot any single shaving serve simultaneously for two purposes? The students of {\i Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai} pose this directly: {\i nazir tahor u\u8217'metzora mahu she\u8217'yigale\u8217'ach tiglachat achat v\u8217'olah lo l\u8217'kan u\u8217'l\u8217'kan} \u8212- if a person is a {\i nazir tahor} and also a {\i metzora}, why cannot one shaving count for both? To sharpen the case: suppose a man\u8217's 30 days of {\i nezirut} were complete, and immediately before he could bring his {\i korban} and shave, he contracted {\i tzara\u8217'at}. When his {\i metzora} period concludes and that final shaving takes place, why should it not simultaneously discharge his obligation as a {\i nazir tahor}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Functions Differ\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai} answers: {\i eino megale\u8217'ach} \u8212- he may not. The reason, he explains, is that the shavings are not functionally identical. {\i Ilu zeh l\u8217'gadel v\u8217'zeh l\u8217'gadel, v\u8217'zeh l\u8217'ha\u8217'avir v\u8217'zeh l\u8217'ha\u8217'avir, yafeh atem omrim} \u8212- if both shavings were performing the same function, either both serving to grow the hair back afterward ({\i l\u8217'gadel}) or both serving to remove the hair as a terminal act ({\i l\u8217'ha\u8217'avir}), then the students\u8217' argument would be compelling. But {\i achshav nazir l\u8217'ha\u8217'avir u\u8217'metzora l\u8217'gadel} \u8212- the {\i nazir}\u8217's shaving is {\i l\u8217'ha\u8217'avir}, a terminal removal, while the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving is {\i l\u8217'gadel}, the beginning of a process after which the hair grows back and there will be another shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case One: Tahor Metzora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of {\i nazir tahor} combined with {\i metzora}: the {\i nazir tahor}\u8217's shaving at the end of his {\i nezirut} is {\i l\u8217'ha\u8217'avir} \u8212- he shaves and he is done, the hair grows back naturally and there is no further obligation tied to it. The {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving, by contrast, is {\i l\u8217'gadel} \u8212- it is the first of a two-stage shaving sequence, and the whole point is that after this shaving the hair grows back so that it can be shaved again on the seventh day. These are structurally different acts, and they therefore cannot collapse into one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Two: Tamei Metzora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The students then press further: even granting that point, what about the {\i metzora} stage two shaving \u8212- the one on day seven of the {\i metzora} process \u8212- and the shaving of a {\i nazir tamei} who is also a {\i metzora}? Here both shavings come {\i before} a {\i korban} is brought, so perhaps they can double-count? {\i Rabbi Shimon} responds: {\i ilu zeh lifnei zrikat damim v\u8217'zeh lifnei zrikat damim, yafeh atem omrim} \u8212- if both came before the sprinkling of blood ({\i zrikat damim}), the argument would hold. But {\i metzora lifnei zrikat damim} \u8212- the {\i metzora}\u8217's day-seven shaving takes place before the {\i korban} is brought (and the blood is sprinkled) on day eight \u8212- while {\i nazir l\u8217'achar zrikat damim} \u8212- the {\i nazir tamei}\u8217's shaving takes place after the blood of his {\i korban} has been sprinkled. They occupy different positions in the ritual sequence relative to the {\i korban}, and they are therefore doing functionally different things.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The students raise one further question: what about the case of {\i metzora} stage two combined with {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- both of which, it might be argued, precede the {\i korban} on one reading \u8212- and compare it with a case where both shavings come before {\i bi\u8217'at mayim} (immersion in the {\i mikvah})? {\i Rabbi Shimon} answers: {\i ilu zeh lifnei bi\u8217'at mayim v\u8217'zeh lifnei bi\u8217'at mayim, yafeh atem omrim} \u8212- if both came before {\i bi\u8217'at mayim}, fine. But the shaving of the {\i nazir tamei} comes {\i after} the {\i tahara} process involving the {\i efer parah adumah} and immersion in the {\i mikvah} \u8212- it is the conclusion of the {\i tahara} process. The {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving on day seven, however, comes {\i before} that immersion, which follows the shaving. They are therefore on opposite sides of the {\i mikvah} in the ritual sequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chiya Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Chiya} distills all of these distinctions into a compact formulation: {\i zeh lifnei bi\u8217'at mayim v\u8217'zeh l\u8217'achar bi\u8217'at mayim} \u8212- one comes before immersion and one comes after; {\i zeh lifnei zrikat damim v\u8217'zeh l\u8217'achar zrikat damim} \u8212- one comes before the sprinkling of blood and one comes after; and there is a third distinction, that of stage number \u8212- {\i chaluto} versus {\i sipro}, first phase versus second phase. Examining these three variables \u8212- position relative to {\i mikvah}, position relative to {\i korban}, and stage within the process \u8212- one finds that no pair of shavings from different obligations ever matches on all three. It is this triple divergence that establishes, systematically, that no two shavings can ever double-count for one another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 61\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaving Ritual: Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora/Nazir Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 61a opens a new {\i perek} on the heels of a fascinating discussion about one who is a {\i safek nazir, safek metzora} \u8212- a person uncertain whether he is a {\i nazir} or a {\i metzora} \u8212- who must bring all the various {\i korbanot} associated with each status. The {\i mishna} at the top of the daf turns to a case of certainty: where a person is definitively both a {\i metzora} and a {\i nazir}, the shaving required for {\i tzaraat} is performed even though he is a {\i nazir}. This is the {\i chidush} learned from the {\i pesukim} \u8212- the phrase {\i rosho}, appearing in connection with the {\i nazir}, teaches that the {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving is performed even if he currently bears the {\i nazir}\u8217's hair. The elaborate thirty-day waiting gaps encountered in previous discussions arose only because of uncertainty regarding the {\i metzora} status; once that status is certain, the shaving proceeds without complication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a question posed by Rami bar Chama: regarding the four shavings \u8212- the two belonging to the {\i metzora} and the two belonging to the {\i nazir} ({\i nazir tamei} and the regular {\i nazir} completing his term) \u8212- {\i tanei arba tiglahat}, are these shavings required {\i mishum mitzvah}, as a formal ritual act, or {\i mishum avorei se\u8217'ar}, merely for the purpose of removing the hair?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a classic {\i chakira}: is the requirement about the process, or is it about the end result? The question is framed around whether a particular ritual must be carried out in a prescribed manner, or whether all that matters is achieving the outcome \u8212- getting the hair removed \u8212- regardless of how one gets there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechita/Milah Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot Rid} raises a parallel question that illuminates the distinction. The {\i gemara} entertains the possibility that a non-Jew could serve as a {\i mohel}, yet takes it as obvious that if a non-Jew performs {\i shechitah}, the animal is a {\i neveilah}. Why the asymmetry between two acts of cutting?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, as {\i Tosafot Rid} explains, is that {\i shechitah} is fundamentally about the {\i process}. There are five distinct laws governing how the cut must be performed \u8212- no pressure, no notch in the knife, and so forth \u8212- such that if any of these conditions is violated, the {\i shechitah} is invalid even if the trachea and esophagus are severed. {\i Milah}, by contrast, is about the {\i end result}. Nowhere in the {\i gemara} is there a description of {\i how} circumcision must be performed \u8212- what instrument must be used, what procedures must be followed. What is specified is what must be {\i achieved}: the {\i orlah} must be removed, and {\i priah} \u8212- the pulling back of the thin membrane beneath the {\i orlah} \u8212- must be performed. But the process itself carries no formal specifications. If the requirement is about process, then who performs it and how matters greatly. If it is only about achieving the end result, there is considerably more flexibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Depilatory vs.\u160 ?Razor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical {\i nafka minah} of Rami bar Chama\u8217's question is whether the hair could be removed using a {\i sam} \u8212- a depilatory \u8212- rather than a razor. If the shaving is a formal ritual, {\i l\u8217'avorei benashta lo}: a depilatory does not suffice. If it is merely about hair removal, {\i afilu sachi nashta nami}: even a depilatory would fulfill the requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that earlier {\i gemaras} in the tractate explicitly derived from {\i pesukim} that a {\i ta\u8217'ar} \u8212- a razor \u8212- is required for the other shavings. {\i Tosafot} points out, however, that the derivation was not equally clear regarding the shaving of the {\i nazir tamei}. The {\i ta\u8217'ar} was not learned out there with the same force. It is therefore the shaving of the {\i nazir tamei} that is the real locus of the question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can understand why the doubt arises specifically there. The {\i metzora}\u8217's shaving takes place before {\i tevilah} in the {\i mikvah} and has the character of a ritual associated with {\i taharah}. The shaving at the conclusion of a regular {\i nezirut}, following the {\i korban}, similarly carries the character of a formal ritual act. But the {\i nazir tamei} has already become {\i tahor} the day prior; the shaving that follows is not part of a {\i taharah} process. One might therefore reason that this shaving serves a different purpose altogether \u8212- not a ritual, but simply the removal of hair that became defiled during the {\i tumah}, so that the {\i nazir} can begin growing new, {\i tahor} hair. Perhaps no razor is required; perhaps any means of removal will do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves the question by invoking the {\i mishna} itself: {\i ta sha\u8217'ma, umigaleach arba tiglahat} \u8212- four shavings. If it were merely a matter of removing {\i tamei} hair, {\i afilu beshalosh nami sagi lei} \u8212- three shavings would suffice. The {\i mishna} groups all four shavings together in a single category. Since three of those four are unambiguously ritual shavings requiring a razor, the fact that the {\i mishna} speaks of {\i four} \u8212- categorizing all of them together \u8212- demonstrates that the fourth is no different. As {\i Tosafot} explains, the {\i mishna}\u8217's decision to count them together as a unified set of four, rather than treating three as ritual shavings and the fourth as mere hair removal, is itself the {\i limud} that all are equally ritual acts requiring a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus concludes: all four shavings constitute a {\i mitzvah}, requiring a razor as a formal ritual instrument. Even the shaving of the {\i nazir tamei} is not merely {\i avorei se\u8217'ar} \u8212- it is not about removing hair by whatever means \u8212- but is a ritual act in its own right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that conclusion, the {\i perek} of {\i Shenei Nezirim} draws to a close.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jews/Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakutim Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tractate now moves into its final {\i perek}, titled in many printed editions {\i Hakutim} \u8212- the Samaritans. The Samaritans ({\i Kutim}) were a population settled in the land of Israel by the Babylonian king following the exile, replacing the indigenous Jewish population. As the biblical narrative recounts, these settlers initially continued worshipping their own gods, until lions attacked them; they were then instructed that the land belonged to the God of Israel and that they must worship Him. They proceeded to adopt some form of Jewish practice, maintaining a {\i Sefer Torah} and observing certain {\i mitzvot}. By the period of the {\i Bayis Sheni}, when Jews returned from {\i Bavel}, the Samaritans were an established presence, and the question of their status \u8212- whether their conversion was genuine or merely motivated by fear, making them {\i geirei arayos} rather than {\i geirei emes} \u8212- is debated throughout the Talmud among the {\i Tannaim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, a student\u8217's observation here is apt and important: in many manuscripts and printed editions, the word {\i Kutim} in this tractate is a product of medieval censorship. The original text uses {\i ha-goyim} \u8212- non-Jews. This is confirmed upon examining the {\i gemara} itself, where the discussion concerns non-Jews generally, with no analysis of the particular status of Samaritans. The self-censorship of the {\i Talmud} by Jewish communities in the Middle Ages \u8212- undertaken to preempt the Church\u8217's censorship and the burning of {\i Talmud} volumes \u8212- operated on several levels. The mildest substitution was {\i akum} ({\i ovdei kochavim}, star-worshippers) in place of {\i goyim}, presenting the text as referring to ancient pagans rather than contemporary Christians. More sensitive passages employed {\i Kuti}, and the most charged formulations were replaced with {\i Kena\u8217'ani}. An example of the latter is the phrase {\i gazel Kena\u8217'ani} in {\i Bava Kama}, which in the {\i Rishonim} reads {\i gazel goy}. Here in the opening of this {\i perek}, the heading {\i Hakutim} is indeed a substitution for {\i ha-goyim}, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's internal discussion consistently refers to non-Jews as {\i ovdei kochavim} \u8212- itself another layer of the same self-censorship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One student offered an intriguing additional observation: the Church may have found the denial of {\i nezirut} to non-Jews particularly offensive given Christianity\u8217's own tradition of asceticism and monastic self-denial, which bears a resemblance to {\i nezirut}. The suggestion that the Church might have occluded any discussion denying non-Jews a share in this form of sanctity adds an interesting dimension to the censorship question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i ha-goyim ein lahem nezirut} \u8212- non-Jews do not have {\i nezirut}. {\i Nashim v\u8217'avadim yeish lahem nezirut} \u8212- women and slaves, however, do have {\i nezirut}. This is worth pausing over. Throughout the tractate, the {\i mishnayot} and {\i beraitot} have repeatedly assumed that women can be {\i nezirot}, as the {\i pasuk} itself states {\i ish o isha ki yafli lindor neder nazir} \u8212- \u8220"a man or a woman who takes a {\i nazir} vow.\u8221" Women are not in the category typically excluded; they are explicitly included in the {\i parashah}. It is non-Jews who are singled out here as excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women/Slaves Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that both women and slaves have {\i nezirut}, the {\i mishna} notes a stringency that applies to women but not to slaves: {\i she\u8217'hu kofeis avdo} \u8212- a master can force his slave in the matter of his {\i nezirut}, meaning he can effectively override his slave\u8217's {\i nezirut} and prevent him from maintaining it. {\i V\u8217'eino kofeis ishto} \u8212- a husband, however, cannot force his wife. This is notable because one might expect a husband to have the right to annul his wife\u8217's {\i nedarim}, particularly those touching on their relationship. Whether the {\i nezirut} restrictions \u8212- abstention from wine, prohibition from becoming {\i tamei} to the dead \u8212- constitute {\i devarim she\u8217'beino l\u8217'veina} such that the husband could annul them is an open question; the prohibition on cutting hair might theoretically fall within that category since it affects appearance, yet the {\i mishna} says he cannot override it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pseudo-Rashi on this passage clarifies: if a slave took a {\i nazir} vow, his master may force him to drink wine \u8212- i.e., to violate the {\i nezirut}. But if a wife took a {\i nazir} vow and the husband did not annul it in time, he can no longer nullify it, and he certainly cannot force her to violate it. The slave\u8217's {\i nezirut} remains revocable at the master\u8217's discretion; the wife\u8217's, once the window for annulment passes, becomes fully binding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bnei Yisrael Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnei Yisrael Exegesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i minan hani mili} \u8212- from where do we know that non-Jews have no {\i nezirut}? The {\i baraita} answers: {\i Daber el Bnei Yisrael} \u8212- the Torah says \u8220"speak to the {\i Bnei Yisrael},\u8221" excluding non-Jews. Before returning to the exclusion of non-Jews, the {\i gemara} asks why the {\i baraita} also needs a verse to {\i include} slaves, since ordinarily slaves are obligated in all {\i mitzvot} that women are obligated in. Rava answers: one might have thought that the phrase {\i le-esor issar al nafsho} \u8212- \u8220"to bind a restriction upon oneself\u8221" \u8212- applies only to {\i b\u8217'mi she-nafsho kenuyah lo}, one who fully owns himself and can direct his own conduct. A slave, whose person is legally the property of his master, might not be in a position to impose binding restrictions upon himself. Therefore the {\i baraita} needs {\i ve\u8217'amarta aleihem} to include slaves \u8212- expanding beyond the {\i Bnei Yisrael} framework to encompass them. Curiously, this framing suggests that while a slave may not be capable of making an ordinary {\i neder} binding, his {\i nezirut} can nonetheless take effect \u8212- though his master may override it \u8212- which is itself a remarkable position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the exclusion of non-Jews, the {\i gemara} notes that the phrase is {\i Daber el Bnei Yisrael}, excluding non-Jews. But {\i Daber el Bnei Yisrael ve\u8217'amarta aleihem ish o isha ki yafli lindor neder nazir} \u8212- the same passage also says {\i ish}, \u8220"a person,\u8221" which is a broader inclusive term. By {\i erchin}, the {\i gemara} observes a parallel dynamic: {\i Daber el Bnei Yisrael} excludes non-Jews from being {\i ma\u8217'arich} \u8212- the one who declares another\u8217's value \u8212- yet {\i talmud lomar ish} comes back to include non-Jews in the category of {\i ne\u8217'erach}, the one whose value is declared. There is thus a \u8220"middle position\u8221" in {\i erchin}: non-Jews can be {\i ne\u8217'erach} but not {\i ma\u8217'arich}. One might ask whether the same logic applies to {\i nezirut}: {\i Bnei Yisrael} partially excludes non-Jews, but {\i ish o isha} partially includes them, yielding some middle status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erchin Half-Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is therefore sharpened: by {\i erchin}, we have a verse that generates a genuine half-status \u8212- non-Jews are inside the {\i parashah} as objects of valuation ({\i ne\u8217'erach}) even though they are outside it as active participants ({\i ma\u8217'arich}). Why can\u8217't we say the same about {\i nezirut}? {\i Bnei Yisrael} restricts their full participation; {\i ish o isha} might still grant them some partial entry into the {\i parashah}. What that middle position would look like is not immediately obvious \u8212- perhaps they observe {\i nezirut} without bringing a {\i korban}, or perhaps they have some attenuated form of the status \u8212- but the structural question is whether any partial inclusion is possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i shani hacha d\u8217'amar kra} \u8212- this case is different, because the verse says {\i l\u8217'aviv ul\u8217'imo lo yitama} \u8212- the {\i nazir} may not become {\i tamei} to his father and mother. This locates the {\i nazir}\u8217's laws within a framework of someone who has {\i tumah} \u8212- someone who {\i can} become {\i tamei}. From here the {\i gemara} begins its analysis of whether non-Jews fall within that framework at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumah/Tahara Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first attempt at reading {\i l\u8217'aviv ul\u8217'imo lo yitama} as an exclusion of non-Jews focuses on the word {\i aviv} \u8212- \u8220"his father.\u8221" Perhaps non-Jews have no legally recognized father, such that the verse simply cannot apply to them. This is rejected immediately: {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Avin said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan} that a non-Jew does inherit from his father by Torah law, as the verse states {\i ki yerusha l\u8217'Esav natati et Har Se\u8217'ir} \u8212- \u8220"for I gave Mount {\i Se\u8217'ir} as an inheritance to {\i Esav}.\u8221" The Torah itself recognizes the paternal line among non-Jews as legally meaningful for purposes of inheritance. The claim that non-Jews have no legal father is therefore untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks whether the exclusion might derive from the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kibbud av} \u8212- perhaps the verse means to speak only about someone who has an obligation of honoring a father. Non-Jews are not obligated in {\i kibbud av}. The {\i gemara} rejects this as well: {\i mi ketiv kibbud avicha, kaveid avicha} by {\i nazir}? The {\i nazir} verses say nothing about {\i kibbud av}; they simply say father and mother. Moreover, the logic of being {\i mitamei} to relatives is not limited to parents \u8212- a regular {\i kohen} becomes {\i tamei} to siblings and children as well, where there is no {\i kibbud} relationship involved. Whatever the conceptual foundation of this category, it is not {\i kibbud av} per se.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the idea of being {\i mitamei} to relatives is conceptually linked to {\i avelut} \u8212- it is from the {\i kohen}\u8217's obligation to become {\i tamei} to the seven {\i krovim} that the obligation of {\i avelut} itself is derived, at least according to those who hold {\i avelut} is {\i d\u8217'Oraita}. So while {\i kibbud av} may be the most concrete expression of a child\u8217's responsibility toward a parent, the {\i gemara}\u8217's category here is the broader one of relational obligation to {\i krovim}, and that does not map neatly onto {\i kibbud} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Tumah = No Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then arrives at its key insight: {\i l\u8217'aviv ul\u8217'imo lo yitama} speaks only {\i b\u8217'mi she-yesh lo tumah} \u8212- about someone who has the capacity to become {\i tamei}. {\i Yatza akum she-ein lo tumah} \u8212- a non-Jew is excluded because a non-Jew does not become {\i tamei}. Since {\i tumah} is the organizing concept of the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition to his parents \u8212- you cannot be prohibited from contracting {\i tumah} if {\i tumah} does not apply to you \u8212- non-Jews are categorically outside this {\i parashah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking {\i gemara}. It is not that the {\i halacha} lacks practical implications regarding non-Jews and {\i tumah}; quite the contrary. If a non-Jew touches a dead body and a Jew then touches him, determining the {\i Jew\u8217's} status requires knowing whether the non-Jew contracted {\i tumah} in the first place. These statuses matter even when they are not part of the non-Jew\u8217's own legal obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parah Adumah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i minayan dileis lehu tumah} \u8212- from where do we know that non-Jews have no {\i tumah}? The answer is a verse concerning the {\i parah adumah}: {\i veha\u8217'ish asher yitma velo yitchata venichreta hanefesh ha\u8217'hi mitoch hakahal} \u8212- a person who becomes {\i tamei} and does not undergo the {\i chitat} (sprinkling of the {\i parah adumah} ashes) and then enters the {\i Mikdash} or eats {\i kodshim} will be cut off {\i mitoch hakahal}, from the community. The word {\i kahal} refers specifically to {\i Klal Yisrael}. {\i Yatza zeh she\u8217'ein lo kahal} \u8212- one who has no share in {\i kahal}, i.e., a non-Jew, is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tahara-Tumah Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} anticipates an objection: perhaps all this verse teaches is that a non-Jew is not subject to {\i karet} for entering the {\i Mikdash} while {\i tamei} \u8212- {\i dilma kares hu d\u8217'lo mechayev} \u8212- but perhaps a non-Jew nonetheless {\i does} contract {\i tumah}. The {\i gemara} responds by citing a second verse: {\i vehizah hatahor al hatamei} \u8212- the {\i tahor} person sprinkles upon the {\i tamei} person. From here: {\i kol she\u8217'yesh lo taharah yesh lo tumah, vekol she\u8217'ein lo taharah ein lo tumah} \u8212- whoever has {\i taharah} has {\i tumah}, and whoever lacks {\i taharah} lacks {\i tumah}. Since a non-Jew lacks {\i taharah} \u8212- specifically the {\i taharah} of {\i parah adumah}, which the verse in {\i Parshat Chukat} restricts with {\i vehaysa la\u8217'adat Bnei Yisrael lemishmeret} \u8212- he therefore lacks {\i tumah} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes a further question here: perhaps the linkage between {\i taharah} and {\i tumah} via {\i vehizah hatahor al hatamei} is drawn for a different {\i drasha} entirely, or perhaps the exclusion from {\i parah adumah} covers only that particular form of {\i taharah}, not all {\i taharah} generally \u8212- perhaps a non-Jew has {\i taharah} via {\i mikvah}, for instance. The {\i gemara} answers by citing {\i veha\u8217'ish asher yitma velo yitchata}, which explicitly connects {\i tumah} to the {\i chittui} of {\i parah adumah} through the phrase {\i velo yitchata}. That extra phrase, which would be unnecessary if the verse simply said {\i veha\u8217'ish asher yitma}, underscores that {\i tumah} and the {\i taharah} of {\i parah adumah} are inextricably linked. Since non-Jews are excluded from that {\i taharah} by {\i Bnei Yisrael} language in {\i Parshat Chukat}, and since {\i tumah} follows {\i taharah}, non-Jews have no {\i tumah} of any kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of this chain: non-Jews are excluded from {\i nezirut} because {\i l\u8217'aviv ul\u8217'imo lo yitama} requires the capacity for {\i tumah}; non-Jews lack {\i tumah} because they lack {\i taharah}; they lack {\i taharah} because the {\i parah adumah} is restricted to {\i Bnei Yisrael}; and the link between {\i taharah} and {\i tumah} is derived from {\i vehizah hatahor al hatamei} and {\i veha\u8217'ish asher yitma velo yitchata}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachala Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha bar Yaakov offers an alternative derivation. Rather than proceeding through {\i tumah} and {\i taharah}, he returns to the phrase {\i l\u8217'aviv ul\u8217'imo lo yitama} and argues that the word {\i aviv} is not fully applicable to non-Jews for a different reason. The verse in the context of slaves reads {\i vehenchaltem otam livneichem achareichem} \u8212- \u8220"you shall bequeath them to your children after you.\u8221" From this: {\i kol she\u8217'yesh lo nachala yesh lo tumah} \u8212- whoever participates in this category of {\i nachala} (specifically, the bequeathing of slaves from one generation to the next) is within the {\i parashah} of {\i nazir}. {\i Kol she\u8217'ein lo nachala ein lo tumah} \u8212- whoever lacks this {\i nachala} is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Non-Jews, while they do inherit property from their fathers \u8212- as established earlier from {\i ki yerusha l\u8217'Esav} \u8212- do not legally bequeath slaves to their children in the manner recognized by {\i halacha}. The {\i halacha} recognizes that Jews own {\i avadim kena\u8217'anim} as legal property passed down through generations; it does not recognize the equivalent institution among non-Jews. Therefore, the {\i avir} of the non-Jew is not the full {\i aviv} the {\i pasuk} contemplates, and this excludes them from the {\i parashah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i i hachi avadim nami lo} \u8212- if the criterion is {\i nachala} of slaves, then slaves themselves would also be excluded from {\i nezirut}, since slaves obviously do not own and bequeath other slaves. Since we know slaves {\i do} have {\i nezirut}, this derivation fails. Accordingly, the {\i gemara} abandons Rav Acha bar Yaakov\u8217's approach and returns to Rava\u8217's formulation, which we take up next.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korban Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having set aside Rav Acha bar Yaakov\u8217's alternative, Rava presents a broader logical argument for why non-Jews are {\i categorically} excluded from {\i nezirut} \u8212- not merely partially excluded \u8212- and why the analogy to {\i erchin} (where a half-status exists) does not apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question, recall, is why {\i Bnei Yisrael} and {\i ish o isha} cannot generate the same middle position for {\i nezirut} that {\i Bnei Yisrael} and {\i ish} generate for {\i erchin}. Rava argues: {\i im mishum korban lo mehacha nafka leh} \u8212- if you were to say the partial exclusion would concern the {\i korban} \u8212- that non-Jews are excluded from {\i Bnei Yisrael} when it comes to bringing a {\i korban nezirut} but included via {\i ish o isha} in the status of {\i nezirut} itself \u8212- we don\u8217't need the {\i Bnei Yisrael} verse to tell us that. We already know from elsewhere that non-Jews cannot bring a {\i korban nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source is a verse in {\i Vayikra} 22:18: {\i daber el Aharon ve-el banav ve-amarta aleihem ish ish mi-beit Yisrael u-min hager be-Yisrael asher yakriv korbano le-chol nidreihem u-le-chol nidvotam asher yakrivu l-Adonai le-ola} \u8212- despite the phrase {\i mi-beit Yisrael}, the repeated {\i ish ish} expands the scope to include non-Jews, who may vow and bring {\i korbanot}. However, the verse concludes {\i le-ola} \u8212- only an {\i olah}. We accept an {\i olah} from a non-Jew; we do not accept other types of {\i korbanot}. Rabbi Yossi HaGlili derives explicitly: {\i le-ola perat le-nezirot} \u8212- the word {\i le-ola} excludes a {\i korban nezirut}, which comprises an {\i olah}, a {\i chatat}, and a {\i shelamim}. Since a non-Jew may only bring a {\i nedavah} {\i olah} and not the distinctive {\i korbanot} of {\i nezirut}, they cannot bring a {\i korban nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, when we arrive at the {\i parashah} of {\i nezirut} and read {\i Bnei Yisrael}, the partial exclusion \u8212- the {\i korban} \u8212- is already accomplished. The only work left for {\i Bnei Yisrael} to do is {\i complete} exclusion. There is no room for a middle status, because the middle was already occupied by the earlier derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Status Hypotheticals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} examines whether other potential half-statuses might nonetheless have been entertained, such that we would need {\i Bnei Yisrael} to exclude them specifically and {\i ish o isha} to include them in some residual way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first suggestion: perhaps {\i Bnei Yisrael} excludes non-Jews from {\i nezirut olam} \u8212- permanent {\i nezirut} \u8212- but {\i ish} still includes them in ordinary, time-limited {\i nezirut}. Rabbi Yochanan dismisses this: {\i mi ketiv nazir olam}? Nowhere in the {\i pesukim} is {\i nezirut olam} even mentioned. If the {\i pesukim} do not address {\i nezirut olam} as a category, one cannot construct a partial exclusion based on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second suggestion: perhaps {\i Bnei Yisrael} excludes non-Jews from the ability to impose {\i nezirut} on their children, while {\i ish} still includes them in their own personal {\i nezirut}. Again, Rabbi Yochanan answers: {\i halacha hi be-nazir} \u8212- the ability to make one\u8217's child a {\i nazir} is not derived from the {\i pesukim} at all. It is a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}, a tradition entirely outside the textual framework of the {\i pesukim}. Since it is not in the {\i pesukim}, the {\i pesukim} cannot be read as splitting on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third suggestion parallels the second: perhaps {\i Bnei Yisrael} excludes non-Jews from the {\i halacha} of {\i megalchim al nezirut avihem} \u8212- the practice whereby a son may shave, or bring a {\i korban}, in connection with his father\u8217's {\i nezirut} (a case discussed elsewhere in the tractate where a father and son accepted {\i nezirut} simultaneously, or where the son carries out the {\i nezirut} on behalf of the father in some capacity). Again, Rabbi Yochanan responds: {\i halacha hi be-nazir} \u8212- this too is {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}, not derived from the {\i pesukim}, and therefore the {\i pesukim} cannot be generating a partial inclusion or exclusion based on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure of Rava\u8217's argument thus emerges clearly: any partial exclusion of non-Jews from {\i nezirut} that might have been entertained is either already accomplished through other {\i pesukim} (the {\i korban} via {\i le-ola}), not actually contained in the {\i pesukim} of {\i nezirut} (the derivative {\i halachot} of child {\i nezirut} being {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai}), or simply not referenced in the {\i pesukim} at all ({\i nezirut olam}). Since no coherent half-status remains possible, {\i Bnei Yisrael} in the {\i parashah} of {\i nezirut} can only serve as a complete exclusion \u8212- and the earlier derivation from {\i l\u8217'aviv ul\u8217'imo lo yitama} through {\i tumah} and {\i taharah} establishes the categorical ground for that exclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 62\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jews Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prior Discussion Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens in the middle of a sustained discussion about who is included in and excluded from {\i nezirut}. The {\i mishnah} includes women and slaves, and excludes non-Jews. The {\i gemara} had established that non-Jews are excluded from {\i nezirut} based on the {\i pasuk} of {\i daber el bnei Yisrael} \u8212- the commandment is addressed specifically to the Children of Israel. The {\i gemara} had then explored whether non-Jews might be partially included, following the model of {\i erchin}, where the phrase {\i ish ki yafli neder be-erkecha} creates a balance between {\i bnei Yisrael} and {\i ish}, yielding a middle position: non-Jews cannot make an {\i erech} vow, but they can be the subject of one \u8212- that is, a Jew who vows to pay the {\i erech} valuation of a non-Jew fulfills his vow according to the {\i Torah}\u8217's fixed tables of valuation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} had considered whether a parallel middle position might exist for {\i nezirut}, and had rejected it on two grounds. First, the {\i pasuk} of {\i l\u8217'aviv u\u8217'l\u8217'imo lo yitama} \u8212- the {\i nazir}\u8217's prohibition against contracting {\i tumah} for a parent \u8212- is inapplicable to non-Jews, who are not governed by the {\i Torah}\u8217's {\i tumah} laws. Second, and more fundamentally, there is no logical middle position available for {\i nezirut}. The proposed middle position \u8212- that non-Jews would not bring a {\i korban nazir} but would remain bound by the other restrictions of {\i nezirut} \u8212- collapses, because their exclusion from the {\i korban nazir} is already established from a separate {\i pasuk} indicating that non-Jews may bring {\i korbanot} only as a {\i nedavah}, a free-will {\i olah} offering, not as a {\i korban nezirut}. With no middle position available, the {\i psukim} excluding non-Jews from {\i nezirut} must be understood as a total exclusion. The {\i gemara} had further considered other partial categories \u8212- {\i nazir Shimshon}, {\i nazir olam}, {\i madir et beno b\u8217'nazir} \u8212- and dismissed each, since those categories derive from {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} or from principles not dependent on the relevant {\i psukim}. The conclusion reached was that non-Jews are entirely excluded from {\i nezirut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erchin vs Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that discussion resolved, the {\i gemara} pivots back to examine the {\i erchin} discussion more carefully. On 62a, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i ei hachi, ish ki yafli neder be-erkecha be-erchin lama li?} \u8212- given what was just established, why does the {\i Torah} need the word {\i ish} to include non-Jews partially in {\i erchin}? The {\i gemara} notes that {\i erchin} is explicitly linked to {\i nedarim}: the very {\i pasuk} that establishes {\i erchin} \u8212- {\i ish ki yafli neder be-erkecha} \u8212- frames an {\i erech} vow as a type of {\i neder}. And regarding {\i nedarim} generally, a separate principle already establishes the inclusion of non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews as Subject/Obje\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} cited is from Vayikra 22, where the {\i pasuk} reads: {\i ish ish mibeit Yisrael u\u8217'min hager b\u8217'Yisrael asher yakriv korbano l\u8217'chol nidreihem u\u8217'l\u8217'chol nidvotam asher yakrivu l\u8217'Hashem l\u8217'olah}. The double expression {\i ish ish} is interpreted as {\i lerabbot et ha-ovdei kochavim she-nodrim nedarim u-nedavot ke-Yisrael} \u8212- to include non-Jews who take vows and make free-will offerings like Israel, with the scope of that inclusion being {\i l\u8217'olah}, that is, {\i korbanot} of the free-will type. This encompasses not only animal offerings but vows of monetary gifts to the {\i beit hamikdash} and, by extension, {\i erchin} vows. Since non-Jews are already included in the {\i parsha} of {\i nedarim}, and {\i erchin} is a type of {\i neder}, they are already implicitly included in {\i erchin} \u8212- making the word {\i ish} in the {\i erchin pasuk} apparently redundant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a subtle double question embedded here. The prior discussion had concluded that non-Jews can be the {\i object} of an {\i erech} vow but not the {\i subject} \u8212- they can be {\i ne\u8217'erach} but cannot be the {\i maarich}. Yet the present discussion, which derives their inclusion from the fact that they can make {\i nedarim}, seems to imply that they can be the one making the {\i erech} vow. The tension between being the subject and being the object of {\i erchin} will be addressed shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ish Ki Yafli Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Ish in Erchin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the redundancy of {\i ish} in the {\i erchin pasuk} as follows: {\i ela hai ish miba\u8217'ei leih le-atuyei mufla samuch le-ish} \u8212- the word {\i ish} by {\i erchin} is needed not to include non-Jews in the {\i parsha} of {\i erchin} (which we already know), but rather to establish the principle of {\i mufla samuch le-ish}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erchin Linked to Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before developing this principle, it is worth pausing to appreciate the structure of the {\i gemara}\u8217's reasoning. Since {\i erchin} is linked to {\i nedarim} \u8212- and the inclusion of non-Jews in {\i nedarim} is independently established \u8212- the word {\i ish} in the {\i erchin pasuk} must be doing something else. The {\i gemara} identifies that something else as the principle of {\i mufla samuch le-ish}, and once we understand that principle, we understand that {\i ish ki yafli} in the {\i erchin pasuk} (and, by extension, by {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut}) teaches us something specifically about the maturity threshold for vow-making.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mufla Samuch L\u8217'Ish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i mufla samuch le-ish} is discussed in a {\i mishnah} in {\i Niddah} and related contexts. The idea is that a boy or girl within the year before reaching {\i bar} or {\i bat mitzvah} respectively \u8212- within the year before their age of majority \u8212- may have their vows tested. The question posed is: {\i nidrehem nivdakim} \u8212- do they understand what they are vowing? Specifically, do they appreciate the weight and significance of making a vow to God? If they demonstrate that understanding \u8212- {\i leshem mi nadaru} \u8212- then their vow is binding even during that year before majority. This principle applies not only to {\i nedarim} but to all categories that fall under {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, speech acts that create a change of status: {\i nezirut}, {\i hekdesh}, {\i korbanot}, and all the related categories discussed throughout {\i masechet Nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an interesting question about whether this principle is {\i de\u8217'oraita} or {\i derabbanan}, and that question is now introduced. If it is {\i de\u8217'oraita}, then the {\i pasuk} of {\i ish ki yafli} is its source. If it is only {\i derabbanan}, then the {\i pasuk} must be doing something else \u8212- and the {\i gemara} proposes that in that case, {\i ish ki yafli} comes to teach the parallel principle as applied to non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mufla Samuch L\u8217'Ish Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now articulates the apparent anomaly: {\i ha nicha le-man de-amar mufla samuch le-ish de-oraita} \u8212- that works well if this is a biblical principle derivable from the {\i pasuk} of {\i ish ki yafli}. {\i Ela le-man de-amar derabbanan}, but if it is only rabbinic for Jews, then the {\i pasuk} is freed up and {\i ish ki yafli} in the {\i erchin} context comes to teach {\i mufla samuch le-ish} for non-Jews: {\i le-atuyei mufla samuch le-ish de-ovdei kochavim}. This is a striking and counterintuitive result: the principle of {\i mufla samuch le-ish} would be only {\i derabbanan} for Jews but {\i de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- having a biblical source \u8212- for non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} on 61b raises this very question and offers an answer. The relevant {\i Tosafot} explains: {\i d\u8217'vadai Yisrael d\u8217'lo havei bichlal bal yachel ad d\u8217'havei gadol} \u8212- a Jew is not yet subject to the prohibition of {\i bal yachel devaro} (do not violate your word) until reaching adulthood. And once he does become an adult, he is immediately included in {\i bal yachel}. The principle derived from the {\i gemara} in the {\i perek} of {\i yotzei dofen} is: {\i kol deleis b\u8217'bal yachel leis b\u8217'hafla\u8217'ah} \u8212- anyone who is not yet in the category of {\i bal yachel} is not yet capable of binding {\i hafla\u8217'ah}. Since the {\i mitzvot} governing vows do not apply to a minor, the speech act of vowing has no legal reality for him yet \u8212- he is not yet fully in the {\i parsha}, and therefore the vow cannot bind. This is why {\i mufla samuch le-ish} requires {\i derabbanan} reinforcement for Jews: without it, the minor before his year of {\i mufla samuch le-ish} would simply not be in the category at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bal Yachel & Formalism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A non-Jew, however, is never subject to {\i bal yachel} \u8212- that {\i mitzvah} is not part of {\i sheva mitzvot b\u8217'nei Noach}. Since the formal halachic category of {\i bal yachel} never applies to a non-Jew at any age, the logic of {\i kol deleis b\u8217'bal yachel leis b\u8217'hafla\u8217'ah} cannot be used to exclude a young non-Jew from making a binding vow. There is no formal line saying: until this age, you are not yet in the category. Hence the principle of {\i mufla samuch le-ish} can apply to non-Jews directly from the {\i pasuk}, without the same logical obstacle that necessitates rabbinic reinforcement for Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This can be elaborated further. For Jews, what fundamentally matters is the formal halachic category: you are either in the category of {\i bar mitzvah} or you are not. The formal rule is absolute and does not yield to contextual assessment. By contrast, {\i sheva mitzvot b\u8217'nei Noach} does not operate with the same level of rigid formalism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheva Mitzvos Flexibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This difference between Jewish law and {\i sheva mitzvot b\u8217'nei Noach} in terms of formalism and defined thresholds appears to be a broader structural feature. Consider {\i gezeila}: for Jews, there is no violation for stealing less than a {\i shaveh prutah}, because the {\i Torah} establishes that minimum threshold as the legal definition of meaningful money. For non-Jews, the prohibition of theft applies even to {\i pachos mishaveh prutah}. Similarly, consider abortion: for Jews, a fetus in utero is not legally classified as a {\i nefesh}, and the act is not subject to the full category of {\i lo tirtzach}. For non-Jews, the prohibition of {\i lo tirtzach} extends to include a fetus, as derived from the {\i pasuk} of {\i shofech dam ha\u8217'adam ba\u8217'adam damo yishafech} \u8212- {\i eizehu adam ba\u8217'adam}, what is a person within a person, that is a fetus. Notably, this is not a distinction based on the identity of the fetus but on the applicable law: a Jew aborting a non-Jew\u8217's fetus is not subject to {\i lo tirtzach}, and a non-Jew aborting a Jew\u8217's fetus is. The governing variable is the law applicable to the actor, not the status of the fetus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What seems to emerge is that {\i sheva mitzvot b\u8217'nei Noach} does not draw formal lines \u8212- minimum monetary amounts, specific developmental thresholds, precise ages \u8212- in the same way that Jewish law does. Jewish law creates clear, observable, quantifiable criteria: this side of the line yes, the other side no. For non-Jews, the law operates in a less formalized mode, more sensitive to the concrete reality of each situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This structural difference illuminates the {\i mufla samuch le-ish} question in a new way. For Jews, the entire system of vow-making operates through formal categories: are you {\i bar mitzvah}? If yes, binding; if no, not binding; and perhaps there is a year before that has its own status \u8212- but even that is a formal rule, debated as to whether it is {\i de\u8217'oraita} or {\i derabbanan}. There is no room for a case-by-case assessment of individual cognitive maturity once you are past the threshold. For non-Jews, who is to say there is a formal age of majority at all? And even if there is, does it operate with the same categorical rigidity? The {\i gemara}\u8217's approach \u8212- that non-Jews are assessed based on whether they understand what they are doing \u8212- reflects precisely this less-formalized framework. The question of whether a non-Jew has an age of majority, and what that age would be, remains genuinely open; but what is clear is that the assessment proceeds differently, without the hard formal lines of Jewish law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yodea L\u8217'haflot Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Yafli Inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now reconsiders the relationship between the debate about who is the subject and who is the object of {\i erchin} by non-Jews, and how {\i ish ki yafli} functions within each position. The {\i gemara} presents both sides of the debate. According to the view that {\i bnei Yisrael ne\u8217'erchin ve-ein ovdei kochavim ne\u8217'erchin} \u8212- Jews can be the object of an {\i erech} vow but non-Jews cannot be the object \u8212- the word {\i ish} comes to teach that non-Jews can be the subject, the {\i maarich}, which is already established through the {\i nedarim} connection, and {\i ki yafli} teaches the principle of {\i mufla samuch le-ish} for either Jews or non-Jews. That approach fits naturally with everything discussed so far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the other view \u8212- {\i bnei Yisrael maarichin ve-ein ovdei kochavim maarichin} \u8212- Jews are the subject of the {\i erech} vow but non-Jews cannot make the vow, though they can be the object \u8212- the word {\i ish} comes to include non-Jews as valid objects of {\i erchin}, which the {\i gemara} notes applies even to an infant: {\i afilu tinok ben chodesh barucheihu}, even a month-old child can be the {\i ne\u8217'erach} since the tables of valuation in the {\i Torah} already account for that age. This is admittedly an odd exclusion: a non-Jew who can make all other types of {\i nedarim} and {\i korban} vows, who can be {\i makdish} property to the {\i beit hamikdash}, is nonetheless excluded specifically from making the {\i erech} vow. The {\i pasuk} excludes him from this particular formalized category, even while leaving him in every adjacent category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is the case, then {\i ki yafli} is no longer needed to establish {\i mufla samuch le-ish} in the direction of including under-age individuals who understand what they are doing. Instead, {\i ki yafli} now functions in the other direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews Case-by-Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Adda bar Ahava}: {\i le-atuyei oved kochavim gadol d\u8217'af al gav d\u8217'gadol hu im eino yodea lehaflot} \u8212- {\i ki yafli} comes to teach that even an adult non-Jew, if he does not know what he is doing, if he does not understand the act of {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, his vow is not binding. This is exclusively applicable to non-Jews. For a Jew who is an adult, there is no case-by-case investigation into whether he understood the nature of his vow; he is an adult, and the vow is binding. Unless there is a clearly established and severe cognitive disability, the formal category of adulthood overrides any claim of ignorance. But for a non-Jew, even adulthood does not suffice \u8212- the question is whether he was {\i yodea lehaflot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a perfectly symmetrical picture. For Jews: formal age of majority determines binding status; {\i mufla samuch le-ish} may be applied in the year before majority, but this is at most {\i derabbanan}, and the framework is categorical. For non-Jews: there is no formal line; a minor who understands what he is doing can be bound, and an adult who does not understand is not bound. The assessment is contextual and individual, not categorical and formal \u8212- precisely consistent with the broader structural distinction between Jewish law and {\i sheva mitzvot b\u8217'nei Noach} described above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes that according to the opinion that non-Jews cannot be the {\i maarich} in {\i erchin}, the principle of {\i yodea lehaflot} derived from {\i ish ki yafli} is applied not to {\i erchin} itself but to all the other {\i nedarim} that non-Jews can make \u8212- {\i korbanot}, {\i hekdesh}, and similar categories. Even though they are excluded from the specific {\i parsha} of {\i erchin}, the {\i pasuk} speaks in the context of {\i erchin} but teaches a principle applicable to all their {\i hafla\u8217'ah} acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ki Yafli & Yad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Yafli by Nezirut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established what {\i ish ki yafli} teaches by {\i erchin}, the {\i gemara} now asks the parallel question about {\i nezirut}: {\i ela ki yafli d\u8217'chtav rachmana gabei nezirut lama li?} \u8212- why does the {\i Torah} also write {\i ki yafli} by {\i nezirut}? The relevant {\i pasuk} there is {\i ki yafli lindor neder nazir}. Since {\i nezirut} is explicitly linked to {\i nedarim} \u8212- {\i itkash nezirut l\u8217'nedarim} \u8212- anything derived from {\i ki yafli} by {\i nedarim} regarding {\i mufla samuch le-ish}, whether for Jews or non-Jews, automatically applies to {\i nezirut} as well. So what additional teaching does {\i ki yafli} provide in the {\i nezirut} context?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i le-atuyei yadayim she\u8217'einan mochiachot} \u8212- to include the case of {\i yadayim} (handles, shorthand formulations of vows) that are not self-evident. A {\i yad} is an incomplete or elliptical expression that implies the rest of a vow \u8212- for example, a person walking in front of a {\i nazir} says {\i ehei}, \u8220"I will be,\u8221" without completing the sentence. If the meaning is implied, it is a {\i yad mokhiach}, an evident handle, and it is binding. If it is not clear what was implied \u8212- a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mokhiach}, a non-evident handle \u8212- the question is whether such an expression is nonetheless binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs Rava Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i D\u8217'itmar yadayim she\u8217'einan mochiachot: Abaye amar havyan yadayim}, Abaye holds that even a non-evident handle is binding; {\i Rava amar lo havyan yadayim}, Rava holds it is not binding. According to Abaye, {\i ki yafli} by {\i nezirut} includes even the case of a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mokhiach} \u8212- even an ambiguous handle can constitute a valid {\i hafla\u8217'ah}. But for Rava, who holds that a non-evident handle is not binding, what is {\i ki yafli} teaching?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that there is a well-known textual difficulty here: in other {\i gemarot} Abaye and Rava appear to hold the reverse positions from those attributed to them here, and {\i Tosafot} discusses various versions of the text and attempts to harmonize them with the positions found elsewhere. The ambiguity turns on how one understands the word {\i yafli} itself. If {\i yafli} connotes clear, articulate expression, then {\i ki yafli} could be read as demanding a high standard of explicitness \u8212- which would {\i exclude} a non-evident handle rather than include it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rava, {\i ki yafli} by {\i nezirut} comes to support the position of Rabbi Tarfon: {\i ve-khid-Rabbi Tarfon, d\u8217'tanya Rabbi Yehuda omer mishum Rabbi Tarfon: ein echad me-hem nazir le-fi she-lo nitnah nezirot ela le-hafla\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i nezirut} is valid only when it is made with complete and clear articulation. Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's position arises from the {\i mishnah} involving a chain of conditional {\i nezirut} vows, where various people vow to be a {\i nazir} depending on whether another person is or is not a {\i nazir}, with circular or indeterminate conditions. Rabbi Tarfon declares that none of them is a {\i nazir}, because the vow was never explicitly and unambiguously articulated at the moment it was made. According to this reading, {\i ki yafli} demands a degree of clarity and articulation that a {\i yad she\u8217'einah mokhiach} fails to provide. One could add that even a {\i yad mokhiach} involves incomplete articulation, though it is completed by inference \u8212- and {\i ki yafli} may come to include that case as well, while excluding the purely non-evident handle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i rabbanan} who do not accept Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's stringent position, {\i ki yafli} by {\i nezirut} must be teaching something else entirely. And here the {\i gemara} arrives at a striking conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hattarat Nedarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chagigah Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites: {\i hattarat nedarim porkhin ba-avir ve-ein la-hem al mah she-yismokhu} \u8212- the institution of {\i hattarat nedarim}, the annulment of vows, is as if floating in the air, with no biblical text to support it. This is in fact a {\i mishnah} in {\i Chagigah}, and it is striking that we have gone through the entirety of {\i masechet Nedarim} and all of {\i Nazir} without this {\i mishnah} having been placed more centrally in the discussion. There is a distinction worth drawing: the {\i mishnah} in {\i Chagigah} elsewhere describes certain areas of law as {\i harim ha-teluyin be-sa\u8217'arah} \u8212- mountains hanging by a hair, meaning there is at least {\i some} textual peg, however slender. {\i Hattarat nedarim}, by contrast, is described as floating entirely without support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer yesh la-hem al mah she-yismokhu, she-ne\u8217'emar ish ki yafli, ki yafli shtei fe\u8217'amim} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer disagrees and holds that {\i hattarat nedarim} does have a biblical basis: the fact that {\i ki yafli} appears twice, once by {\i nezirut} and once by {\i nedarim}. One mention of {\i ki yafli} establishes the {\i hafla\u8217'ah} of creating a vow; the other establishes the {\i hafla\u8217'ah} of annulling a vow: {\i echad hafla\u8217'ah le-issur ve-echad hafla\u8217'ah le-hetter} \u8212- one speech act of {\i hafla\u8217'ah} binds, and another speech act of {\i hafla\u8217'ah} releases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafla\u8217'ah Creates & Annuls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a conceptually significant framing. Until now, the understanding of {\i hattarat nedarim} has generally been rooted in the concept of {\i ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- the {\i chacham} identifies some element of error or mistaken assumption that was present when the vow was made, and on that basis declares it void retroactively. Even when {\i poskim} were lenient and did not require the person to identify a specific error he had at the time of the vow, the conceptual framework remained oriented around the idea of retrospective invalidity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's derivation from {\i ki yafli shtei pe\u8217'amim} frames {\i hattarat nedarim} differently: it is itself an act of {\i hafla\u8217'ah}, a formal speech act by the {\i beit din} that produces a change of status. Just as the individual\u8217's {\i hafla\u8217'ah} creates the obligation, the {\i beit din}\u8217's {\i hafla\u8217'ah} annuls it. This is not necessarily rooted in identifying a {\i ta\u8217'ut}; it is the exercise of a formal power. This is analogous to the husband\u8217's {\i hafarah} of his wife\u8217's vow, which works {\i mikan u-le-haba} \u8212- going forward, not retroactively \u8212- and is also understood as an annulment rather than a declaration that the vow was never made. {\i Heter neder} works retroactively, but under this conceptualization it may be understood as an exercise of a formal {\i hafla\u8217'ah} power rather than as a discovery of antecedent error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe that this framework has implications for the broader question of the power of {\i beit din} to act in the domain of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the question of {\i hafka\u8217'at kiddushin} \u8212- where similarly the debate is whether {\i beit din}\u8217's power rests on {\i ta\u8217'ut} (the marriage was always implicitly conditional on {\i beit din}\u8217's approval) or on an independent formal power of annulment. The present {\i gemara} offers a suggestive parallel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife vs Slave Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Annulment Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} moves to the next {\i mishnah} (62b): {\i chomer be-avadim mibanashim she-hu meifer nidrei ishto ve-eino meifer nidrei avdo} \u8212- there is a respect in which vows are more binding for slaves than for women, in that a husband can annul his wife\u8217's vow but cannot annul his slave\u8217's vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry runs in both directions. A husband who annuls his wife\u8217's vow on the day he hears it annuls it permanently \u8212- {\i heifer olamit}. Even if he subsequently divorces her, or dies, the vow has been removed entirely. Whatever happens afterward, the {\i nezirut} is gone. This is a true {\i hafarah}, an annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Override Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, a master has a power over his slave\u8217's vow that he does not have over his wife\u8217's vow: he can {\i kofe} \u8212- compel \u8212- his slave to violate his {\i nezirut}, requiring him to perform tasks that are incompatible with {\i nezirut} even if that means incurring {\i tumah} or drinking wine. He cannot do this to his wife. This is not an annulment but an override \u8212- the master\u8217's authority over his slave\u8217's labor and body allows him to demand behavior that conflicts with the slave\u8217's self-imposed restrictions, but it does not dissolve the {\i nezirut} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is important: what the master exercises is a power of override grounded in his authority, not a power of annulment grounded in a spousal relationship. The slave, who is bound by {\i mitzvot} as a partial Jew, would \u8212- if compelled to incur {\i tumah} \u8212- need to undergo the relevant purification process and restart his {\i nezirut} count, even if the master\u8217's demand was halachically legitimate as an exercise of his authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Freed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the slave is subsequently freed, the full {\i nezirut} becomes binding upon him: {\i yatza le-cherut mashlim neziruto}. The {\i mishnah} states this, and the {\i gemara} will develop it further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Override Nezirut, Not Ne\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Comparative Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a question that was implicit in the {\i mishnah}: {\i lama ravo kofe le-nezirut aval lo le-nedarim u-le-erchin} \u8212- why does the master have the power to override his slave\u8217's {\i nezirut} specifically, but not to override other binding vows ({\i nedarim}) or {\i erchin} commitments? (Some versions of the text substitute {\i shevua} for {\i erchin}.) This seems puzzling, because the {\i pasuk} that would logically support the master\u8217's override \u8212- {\i le-esor isar al nafsho}, restricting oneself \u8212- appears in the context of {\i nedarim}, not {\i nezirut}. The {\i pasuk} continues: {\i b\u8217'mi she-nafsho knuya lo}, in the case of one who owns himself; {\i yatza eved she-ein nafsho knuya lo} \u8212- this excludes a slave, whose self is not his own. If this exclusion is read as rendering the slave\u8217's vow fundamentally non-binding, it would seem to apply to {\i nedarim} just as much as to {\i nezirut}. So why is the master\u8217's power limited to {\i nezirut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Sheshes}: The answer is circumstantial. The case under discussion by {\i nedarim} is one where a slave said {\i konam} on a specific cluster of grapes in front of him \u8212- {\i kegon she-hayah eshkol shel anavim munach lefanav}. By {\i nedarim}, forbidding that particular cluster does not significantly impede his service to his master: {\i deki nami mitzar be-hai lo mitzar be-achrinim} \u8212- even though he is restricted regarding this item, he is not restricted regarding other grapes. The master can simply direct him to eat other food. {\i Lo matzi kapi lei} \u8212- the master cannot compel him to eat that cluster, since there are alternatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the slave undertakes {\i nezirut}, the situation is categorically different: {\i deki mitzar be-hai mitzar be-khulhu} \u8212- the restriction on grapes applies to all grapes without exception. If the master requires him to eat grapes, or requires him to work in a vineyard and consume the produce, there is no alternative. {\i Matzi kapi lei} \u8212- the master can therefore override the {\i nezirut} and compel him to drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this answer as overly circumstantial. The same situation could arise by {\i nedarim}: if the slave made a {\i neder} on the only available food and would grow weak without eating it, {\i d\u8217'ilo achil lei chalish}, the same logic would seem to apply. Moreover, one might argue that {\i nedarim} are actually more likely than {\i nezirut} to interfere with a slave\u8217's service, since {\i nezirut} restricts only grape products, {\i tumah}, and haircuts \u8212- none of which arise in most ordinary work situations \u8212- while a {\i neder} could be made on any food, any activity, anything at all. If the criterion is whether the vow gets in the way of serving the master, {\i nedarim} would seem to pose at least as great a risk as {\i nezirut}. Rav Sheshet\u8217's answer, while constructing a case where {\i nezirut} does interfere, does not establish a categorical difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haroa O Haitiv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela amar Rava}: Rava reformulates the case: imagine there were grape seeds \u8212- {\i chartzan} \u8212- in front of the slave. His {\i neder} applies only to these seeds; {\i be-hai hu de-mitzar lo matzi kapi lei} \u8212- the master cannot force him to eat them, since grape seeds are not essential nourishment and the slave can manage without them. But if he is a {\i nazir}, {\i d-mitzar nami be-achrinimi}, his restriction extends to all grape products. If the master needs him to eat grape seeds \u8212- however implausible \u8212- he can compel him. The {\i gemara} raises the same objection: {\i u\u8217'gabei nedarim mi lo askinan d\u8217'leika ela hai chartzan d\u8217'ilo achil lei chalish?} \u8212- what if the grape seeds are literally all that is available and the slave would grow weak without them? Again the distinction is purely circumstantial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela amar Abaye}: Abaye offers a fundamentally different answer. The question is not whether the vow happens to interfere with the slave\u8217's labor in a given situation, but rather what the law says about the binding nature of such vows in the first place. The {\i pasuk} of {\i leharei o lehaitiv} \u8212- said by the context of {\i shevua} \u8212- establishes that an oath is binding only when it restricts you from something you are permitted to do: {\i ma hatava reshus af hara\u8217'a reshus} \u8212- just as doing good is volitional, so too the bad one swears to must be volitional. This excludes an oath to harm others \u8212- {\i yatza leharei le-acherim she-ein reshus be-yado} \u8212- since one does not have the right to harm another person, an oath to do so is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye extends this principle: when a slave takes a {\i neder} or {\i shevua} that restricts his service to his master, he is in effect doing harm to his master \u8212- {\i leharei le-acherim}. Since he has no right to harm his master in this way, the {\i neder} or {\i shevua} is simply not binding in the first place. This is why the master does not need to override it: there is nothing to override. This is a striking reversal of expectations. One might have thought the {\i neder} is binding but the master has power to override it; Abaye concludes that the {\i neder} is not binding at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i nezirut}, the situation is different. The {\i pasuk} of {\i le-esor isar al nafsho b\u8217'mi she-nafsho knuya lo} applies, and the {\i gemara} had previously derived from this that the slave\u8217's {\i nezirut} is binding \u8212- but subject to the master\u8217's override. The exclusion there is categorical-sounding: {\i yatza eved she-ein nafsho knuya lo}. Yet the {\i halacha} treats it not as a total invalidation but as a situation where the {\i nezirut} is binding and the master has the power to override when necessary. The result is that for {\i nedarim} and {\i shevuot}, the slave\u8217's restricting vow is not binding at all, while for {\i nezirut} it is binding but overridable \u8212- which is, as the {\i gemara} notes, somewhat ironic, since the {\i pasuk} excluding the slave reads as a more categorical exclusion by {\i nezirut} than the {\i leharei o lehaitiv} reasoning does by {\i nedarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave Ran Away\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir vs Yose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents the case of a slave who ran away from his master. Rabbi Meir says {\i lo yishteh} \u8212- the slave may not drink wine; his {\i nezirut} is binding even when he is not in his master\u8217's presence. Rabbi Yose says {\i yishteh} \u8212- he may drink; the {\i nezirut} is overridden even when the slave has run away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mafkir Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first asks whether this dispute reflects the ruling of Shmuel. Shmuel holds: {\i ha-mafkir avdo yatza le-cherut ve-ein tzarich get shichrur} \u8212- if a master renounces monetary ownership of his slave, the slave automatically acquires free personal status without needing a formal {\i get shichrur}, a writ of manumission. The reasoning is that a slave\u8217's monetary status and his personal status are linked: loss of the former automatically produces the latter. If the slave ran away and the master has effectively abandoned his claim, perhaps this constitutes {\i mafkir}, and the slave is now free \u8212- in which case, according to Rabbi Meir who holds like Shmuel, the slave would be fully free and therefore fully bound by his {\i nezirut}; while Rabbi Yose, who does not hold like Shmuel, would say the slave is still a slave and his {\i nezirut} remains overridable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this framing: {\i d\u8217'kulei alma isleih d\u8217'Shmuel} \u8212- everyone could hold like Shmuel. The case here is not one where the master has truly renounced ownership; he expects the slave to return or to be recaptured. The master\u8217's monetary claim is still in effect. The question is therefore not about personal status at all but about the master\u8217's interest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Master\u8217's Interest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entire rationale for the master\u8217's power to override the slave\u8217's {\i nezirut} is that the master needs the slave to serve him effectively, and the {\i nezirut} gets in the way of that service. The override is justified by \u8212- and limited to \u8212- the master\u8217's practical interest. The question when the slave has run away is therefore: what does the master\u8217's interest dictate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the position that says {\i yishteh} \u8212- Rabbi Yose \u8212- the master still wants the slave to drink wine and remain strong: {\i savar sof sof mehadar hadar ve-ati gabei} \u8212- the master expects ultimately to recapture the slave or have him return. Therefore, {\i lishki chamra ke-heichi d\u8217'lo likhush} \u8212- the master wants him to drink now so he stays strong for when he is recaptured. The same interest that justifies the override when the slave is present \u8212- keeping him strong and capable of service \u8212- applies even when he is away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the position that says {\i lo yishteh} \u8212- Rabbi Meir \u8212- the master\u8217's interest when the slave has run away is precisely the opposite: {\i savar lihavy leh tzara ke-heichi d\u8217'lahadar gabei} \u8212- the master wants the {\i nezirut} to remain binding and the slave to be restricted, so that the slave will be motivated to return in order to escape the discomfort of his restrictions. On this view, the override does not apply when the slave is away, because the master\u8217's interest is now served by {\i allowing} the {\i nezirut} to bind, not by overriding it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both opinions agree that the master has not renounced ownership and the question turns entirely on how we assess the master\u8217's interest in the slave\u8217's absence \u8212- a purely circumstantial determination with no categorical resolution, dependent on the specific relationship between the master\u8217's authority and the slave\u8217's situation in each case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 63\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazir Discovers Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Topic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah at the top of daf 63 introduces a substantially new topic. The preceding discussion concerned which categories of persons are subject to the laws of {\i nezirut} \u8212- concluding, for instance, that a Canaanite slave ({\i eved}) is included while non-Jews are not. Now the mishnah turns to an entirely different question: the case of a {\i nazir she-gilach ve-noda lo she-hu tamei} \u8212- a {\i nazir} who completed his thirty-day term, brought his {\i korbanot}, shaved, and only then discovered that at some point during his {\i nezirut} he had been {\i tamei}. Perhaps someone has just informed him that the house he entered a week earlier contained a corpse. He now confronts the realization that his {\i nezirut} was retroactively compromised, that he brought his {\i korbanot} in a state of {\i tumah}, and that seemingly his entire {\i nezirut} was invalidated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Yeduah vs.\u160 ?Tehom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah distinguishes between two categories of {\i tumah}. If it was {\i tumah yeduah} \u8212- a known {\i tumah}, meaning one that was known to others even if not to the {\i nazir} himself \u8212- then {\i soter}, his entire {\i nezirut} is retroactively demolished. He must begin the thirty days again, bring new {\i korbanot}, and shave again, just as if he had discovered the {\i tumah} at the moment it occurred. However \u8212- and here lies the {\i chiddush} of the mishnah \u8212- if it is {\i tumat ha-tehom}, {\i tumah} of the depths, a particular category of hidden {\i tumah}, then {\i eino soter}: his {\i nezirut} is not invalidated, and he has fulfilled his obligation through his {\i korbanot} and his shaving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumat Tehom Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i tumat ha-tehom} is at first striking, because in both cases the {\i nazir} was, in fact, {\i tamei} in hindsight. In neither scenario did he know about it at the time, and yet only one category \u8212- {\i tumah yeduah} \u8212- destroys his {\i nezirut} while the other does not. The {\i chiddush} of the mishnah, then, is this exception: a certain class of hidden {\i tumah}, if discovered only after the {\i nezirut} is complete, does not retroactively undermine it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before/After Shaving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah adds a critical qualification: {\i im ad she-lo gilach, bein kach u-vein kach soter} \u8212- if he discovered the {\i tumah} before he shaved, then regardless of whether it was {\i tumat ha-tehom} or {\i tumah yeduah}, his {\i nezirut} is destroyed and he must begin again. The {\i chiddush} of {\i tumat ha-tehom} applies only when he discovers the {\i tumah} after he has finished \u8212- that is, after he has shaved. The mishnah\u8217's emphasis on shaving ({\i gilach}) as the operative endpoint raises a question about the structure of the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Does Nezirut End\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah\u8217's emphasis on {\i gilach} as the critical boundary invites a question: does this formulation assume that {\i nezirut} is not complete until the {\i nazir} has shaved? This would align with the position of Rabbi Eliezer, who holds that {\i tiglachat me\u8217'akevet} \u8212- the shaving is indispensable and the {\i nezirut} is not concluded until all the {\i korbanot} and the shaving have been performed. By contrast, the Rabbis hold that {\i keivan she-nizrak alecha echad min ha-damim yatza} \u8212- once one blood-offering has been sprinkled on the {\i nazir}\u8217's behalf, his {\i nezirut} is concluded. One might therefore wonder whether the mishnah\u8217's reference to shaving is specifically tied to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, or whether the rule of {\i tumat ha-tehom} independently creates a requirement to wait until shaving. The {\i gemara} will resolve this: the operative principle is whether the {\i nezirut} is over, whatever that point is determined to be. If we adopt Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view, then \u8220"before he shaved\u8221" is simply the way of saying \u8220"before his {\i nezirut} concluded.\u8221" If we adopt the Rabbis\u8217' view, then safety comes once he has brought his first {\i korban} \u8212- the shaving is simply the marker of completion under Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework, not an independent requirement of {\i tumat ha-tehom}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cave Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immersion Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah proceeds to illustrate the concept of {\i tumat ha-tehom} with a concrete case. {\i Yarad litbol be-mearah} \u8212- he descended to immerse himself in a cave, and afterward it was discovered that there was a {\i met}, a corpse, {\i tzaf al pi ha-mearah}, at the mouth or opening of the cave. The precise topography here requires some interpretation. Based on Tosafot, it appears that the body was located at or near the entrance of the cave, in the same body of water in which the {\i nazir} immersed, so that if the body was there at the time of his immersion, he would have been beneath the same roof ({\i ohel}) as the corpse. The question is whether the body was there when he immersed \u8212- he did not see it, but it has now been discovered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Floating Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i tzaf}, ordinarily translated as \u8220"floating,\u8221" generates some puzzlement in context. What does it mean for a corpse to be floating at the mouth of a cave? The most coherent reading, aided by Tosafot, is that the body was floating in the water at the entrance \u8212- that is, the body of water in the cave extended to the mouth, and the corpse was found floating in that same water. If the corpse was there when the {\i nazir} immersed, he would have been in {\i ohel} relationship with it \u8212- beneath the same enclosed roof of the cave. The question of whether he was {\i tamei} turns on whether the corpse was actually present during the immersion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Floating vs.\u160 ?Buried\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah then draws a distinction that is the heart of the {\i tumat ha-tehom} rule. {\i Nimtza tzaf} \u8212- if the corpse was found floating, exposed to the open air, then {\i tamei}: this is not {\i tumat ha-tehom}, and the {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i nezirut} is destroyed. {\i Nimtza meshuka be-karka ha-mearah} \u8212- if the corpse was found buried in the floor of the cave, and the {\i nazir} went down {\i le-hakir}, to cool himself, then {\i tahor}: this is {\i tumat ha-tehom}, and his {\i nezirut} stands. The core distinction is between {\i exposed} and {\i buried}. Something that was out in the open \u8212- even if neither the {\i nazir} nor anyone else happened to see it \u8212- is categorically not {\i tumat ha-tehom}. Something buried beneath the ground is. Neither scenario involved the {\i nazir} knowing about the {\i tumah} at the time, yet only the buried case qualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why the mishnah switches its language mid-case \u8212- using {\i tovel} (immersing) in the floating scenario and {\i le-hakir} (cooling oneself) in the buried scenario \u8212- since the reason one entered the cave would seem irrelevant to the {\i halakha}. Tosafot explains that the switch is deliberate and reflects a double {\i chiddush}. When a person enters water for the purpose of {\i tevila}, he is being scrupulous about his {\i tahara} and presumably attentive to any potential source of {\i tumah}; yet even such a careful person is considered to have encountered {\i tumat ha-tehom} if the body was buried \u8212- while still being considered {\i tamei} if the body was exposed, even though he missed it. In the reverse direction, a person who entered simply to cool off is presumably less vigilant; yet even for such a person, if the body was buried, it is {\i tumat ha-tehom}. Both directions of the switch carry a {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Burial + Unknowable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah focuses on the distinction between buried and exposed, but it leaves implicit a second requirement that the {\i gemara} will make explicit: the {\i tumah} must not only be buried but must also have been entirely unknown \u8212- {\i ein echad makirah be-sof ha-olam}, not known to anyone anywhere in the world. If a body is buried but the local community is aware of the grave \u8212- even if the {\i nazir} himself did not know \u8212- that is not {\i tumat ha-tehom}. A {\i beit ha-pras}, for example, would not qualify, since people are generally aware of it. The mishnah does not spell this out, but the {\i gemara} will.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Purification Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah During Purification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah introduces a further limitation on {\i tumat ha-tehom}. {\i Yarad litaher mi-tumat met} \u8212- if he descended to immerse not simply as a general act of purification but specifically to complete his purification from a prior {\i tumat met}: he had already been in contact with a corpse, undergone the sprinkling of the ashes of the {\i parah adumah}, and was now going to the {\i mikveh} to finalize his {\i tahara}. During this immersion, it is subsequently discovered that there was a buried, unknown corpse \u8212- the very type of buried, unknowable {\i tumah} that in the earlier case would have qualified as {\i tumat ha-tehom}. Nevertheless, the mishnah rules: {\i tamei}, his {\i nezirut} is {\i soter}. The principle of {\i tumat ha-tehom} does not apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazakah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishnah explains the ruling with the principle: {\i shechazkat tamei tamei, shechazkat tahor tahor, she-raglayim la-davar} \u8212- the presumptive status ({\i chazaka}) of a {\i tamei} person is {\i tamei}, and the presumptive status of a {\i tahor} person is {\i tahor}, and there is a {\i raglayim la-davar}. The operative logic is {\i chazaka}: this person entered the {\i mikveh} with the legal status of {\i tamei}. His status was already {\i tamei}, and the discovery of a buried corpse would have, if anything, extended or compounded that {\i tumah}. Since his {\i chazaka} was {\i tamei}, we do not apply the benefit of {\i tumat ha-tehom} to spare him \u8212- his starting status was already compromised. The {\i tumat ha-tehom} exception was designed for a person who began from a {\i chazaka} of {\i tahor} and encountered a wholly hidden {\i tumah}; it does not extend to one whose legal status was already {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazakah vs.\u160 ?Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth distinguishing between {\i chazaka} and what the mishnah calls {\i raglayim la-davar}, which the mishnah uses in conjunction with the {\i chazaka} principle. {\i Chazaka} is not a probabilistic claim. It does not assert that a person is {\i more likely} to have retained a certain status; it is a legal operating principle by which, for {\i halachic} purposes, we treat a person\u8217's status as unchanged until we have affirmative information otherwise. Whether I touched a {\i tamei} object or not is a factual matter about which {\i chazaka} says nothing in terms of likelihood; it simply instructs the {\i halacha} to presume status quo. {\i Raglayim la-davar}, by contrast, normally denotes circumstantial evidence \u8212- factual grounds that give some support to a conclusion. Used here alongside {\i chazaka}, the phrase perhaps means that the {\i chazaka} itself gives the status \u8220"legs,\u8221" a kind of permanence and solidity, rather than referring to independent circumstantial evidence. Alternatively, {\i raglayim la-davar} might be used loosely. The distinction between these two modes of legal reasoning \u8212- presumptive status versus circumstantial support \u8212- is worth bearing in mind throughout the discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also observe that the mishnah\u8217's phrase {\i tamei} vs.\u160 ?{\i tahor} in describing the outcomes of these cases is itself somewhat imprecise. As the Rambam\u8217's language makes clear, the real question in all of these cases is {\i soter} vs.\u160 ?{\i lo soter} \u8212- whether the {\i nezirut} is retroactively invalidated. Even when the mishnah says {\i tahor} in the {\i tumat ha-tehom} case, the {\i nazir} is not literally {\i tahor}: he still must go through the process of becoming {\i tahor} (sprinkling of {\i parah adumah} ashes, {\i mikveh}, etc.). What the {\i halakha} means is that his {\i nezirut} and his {\i korbanot} were not destroyed. The Rambam consistently frames these cases in terms of {\i soter} and {\i eino soter}, and that is the more precise formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the ultimate source of {\i tumat ha-tehom}, one might anticipate that such a counterintuitive principle \u8212- that a person retroactively known to have been {\i tamei} is nonetheless treated as having fulfilled his {\i nezirut} \u8212- must derive from a specific {\i pasuk}, and the {\i gemara} will indeed explore textual sources. But it is worth noting now that the mishnah itself offers no derivation, and the {\i gemara} will ultimately conclude that {\i tumat ha-tehom} is a {\i halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, or at minimum a very ancient tradition ({\i gemara gemiri lah}) whose source cannot be traced through standard {\i drashah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sources Drashah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesuk Nazir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i mina hanei mili} \u8212- from where do we derive this extraordinary rule? Rabbi Elazar cites a {\i pasuk} from the laws of {\i nazir}: {\i ve-chi yamut met alav be-feta pitom} \u8212- \u8220"and if a dead body dies upon him suddenly\u8221" (Numbers 6:9). The phrase {\i alav} \u8212- \u8220"upon him,\u8221" meaning in his clear awareness \u8212- is read to indicate {\i be-mechuveret lo}, that which is manifest to him. When the death is sudden and plain ({\i pitom}), the {\i nazir} is {\i tamei}. The implication is that when the {\i tumah} was {\i not} manifest to him \u8212- not sudden, not in his direct awareness \u8212- a different rule applies, creating the category we are calling {\i tumat ha-tehom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesuk Pesach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish derives the principle from a different {\i pasuk}, one concerning the {\i korban Pesach}: {\i ki yihyeh tamei la-nefesh o be-derech rechoka} \u8212- \u8220"if a man is {\i tamei} for a dead body or on a far road\u8221" (Numbers 9:10), which describes when a person brings {\i Pesach Sheni}. The key word Resh Lakish focuses on is {\i derech}, a road or path. {\i Mah derech be-galuy} \u8212- just as a road is exposed and open to public view, {\i af kol be-galuy} \u8212- so too any {\i tumah} that qualifies under this verse must be \u8220"out in the open.\u8221" By implication, the criterion of {\i tumat ha-tehom} for {\i Pesach} is that the {\i tumah} was {\i not} in the open \u8212- it was covered and buried. Thus from this {\i pasuk} we derive the \u8220"exposed vs.\u160 ?buried\u8221" element of the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combining Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Together, these two {\i pesukim} could in principle yield both criteria of {\i tumat ha-tehom}: from {\i alav} (\u8220"upon him\u8221") we derive the requirement of unknownness \u8212- the {\i tumah} must not have been in the awareness of the person or, as we will see, of anyone; and from {\i derech} (\u8220"road\u8221") we derive the requirement of being covered and buried, not exposed. One might thus synthesize the two and construct the full definition of {\i tumat ha-tehom} from these verses. However, the {\i gemara} raises a difficulty. The {\i baraita} on {\i tumat ha-tehom} requires not merely that the {\i nazir} was unaware, but that {\i ein echad makirah be-sof ha-olam} \u8212- not a single person in the world knew of the {\i tumah}, even someone at the farthest end of the earth. If one uses the {\i pasuk} of {\i alav} as the source, one might conclude only that {\i the nazir himself} was unaware, and that if anyone at the far end of the world happened to know, it would still be {\i tumat ha-tehom}, since it was still not \u8220"upon him.\u8221" The {\i gemara} observes that the {\i pasuk} of {\i derech} might support the notion that the {\i tumah} must be unknown to all \u8212- since a path implies public awareness \u8212- but the derivation is strained. Ultimately, the {\i gemara} concludes that these {\i pesukim} cannot satisfactorily yield both criteria.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Dead Body\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} quotes a {\i baraita} that articulates the critical case: {\i ha-motze met mushkav le-rochbah shel derech} \u8212- if one finds that a corpse was laid out spanning the full width of a road. Here there is no {\i safek} about whether the traveler passed over the {\i met}: since the corpse covered the entire width of the path, anyone who walked that road definitively passed over it and was under {\i ohel} with it. For ordinary {\i tumah} purposes, {\i be-tumat ha-met} \u8212- the person is {\i tamei}. But {\i benazir ve-oseh Pesach tahor} \u8212- for the {\i nazir} and the one performing the {\i Pesach} offering, the {\i baraita} declares them {\i tahor}, meaning their obligations were not invalidated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking case because it is not a {\i safek} \u8212- it is a certainty, in hindsight, that the person was {\i tamei}. Yet {\i tumat ha-tehom} saves him for purposes of {\i nezirut} and {\i Pesach}. This will be important later when the {\i gemara} considers whether {\i tumat ha-tehom} requires an element of doubt ({\i safek}) or operates even in cases of retrospective certainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given the difficulties in deriving {\i tumat ha-tehom} from {\i pesukim}, the {\i gemara} concludes: {\i tumat ha-tehom gemara gemiri lah} \u8212- it is a received tradition. The Rambam characterizes it as {\i davar zeh halakha mi-pi ha-kabalah}. Whether {\i gemara gemiri lah} is precisely synonymous with {\i halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} is a matter of some discussion. Rashi in several places treats them as identical. The Netziv, drawing on the Rambam and other {\i rishonim}, suggests that {\i halakhata gemiri lah} may refer to an ancient, enshrined tradition of very early {\i batei din} whose source is no longer known \u8212- not necessarily a tradition transmitted orally from Sinai in the technical sense, though it carries full {\i de-oraita} authority. The phrase {\i halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} is itself sometimes used loosely in {\i tannaitic} literature, as the gemara\u8217's preference for the Aramaic formulation here may simply reflect linguistic register rather than a substantive distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the practical scope of {\i tumat ha-tehom}: the {\i halakha} applies specifically to {\i nazir} and {\i oseh Pesach}, not to {\i terumah} or other categories of {\i tahara}. If someone ate {\i terumah} and subsequently discovered he had been subject to a {\i tumat ha-tehom}, he does not benefit from any exemption \u8212- he violated the prohibition, albeit {\i be-shogeg}. The {\i halakha} of {\i tumat ha-tehom} is a specific leniency carved out for {\i nezirut} and {\i Pesach}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rami\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i tumat ha-tehom} was defined as a {\i halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, and that the mishnah\u8217's emphasis on shaving reflects the position of Rabbi Eliezer, the {\i gemara} turns to a question posed by Rami bar Chama. This is distinct from the {\i tumat ha-tehom} context \u8212- Rami bar Chama is asking about an ordinary {\i tumah yeduah}. His question: {\i nitma be-toch melot ve-noda lo le-achar melot mahu} \u8212- if the {\i nazir} became {\i tamei} during his thirty days, but the discovery occurred only after the thirty days were complete, what is the {\i halakha}? Does the timing of discovery matter? Specifically: {\i batar yedi\u8217'ah azlinan} \u8212- do we follow the moment of discovery? And since the discovery was after the thirty days, is it treated more leniently, analogously to a case where {\i tumah} occurred after the thirty days?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To frame the question precisely: Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i tumah} occurring {\i after} the thirty-day term does not destroy the entire {\i nezirut} \u8212- the {\i nazir} need not restart thirty days, but merely waits seven days to become {\i tahor} before bringing his {\i korbanot} ({\i soter shiva}). The question Rami bar Chama raises is whether {\i tumah} that occurred {\i during} the thirty days but was only {\i discovered} afterward might be treated similarly, on the grounds that the discovery came late \u8212- or whether the factual reality that {\i tumah} occurred during the {\i nezirut} is determinative, regardless of when it was discovered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava First Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds by directing attention to the mishnah: {\i ta shema, ad she-lo gilach, bein kach u-vein kach soter} \u8212- even in a case of {\i tumat ha-tehom}, if he discovered the {\i tumah} before he shaved, the {\i nezirut} is destroyed either way. The force of this proof: the phrase {\i bein kach u-vein kach} (\u8220"either way\u8221") implies that the mishnah is addressing a case where the discovery came after the thirty days but before the shaving \u8212- since if he discovered it {\i during} the thirty days, the ruling would be obvious and not worth stating. Therefore, the mishnah teaches: even when discovery occurs after thirty days have elapsed, if it was before shaving (and thus, per Rabbi Eliezer, before {\i nezirut} was concluded), the {\i nezirut} is {\i soter}. This would appear to resolve Rami bar Chama\u8217's question: we do not follow the moment of discovery; we follow the moment of {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All vs.\u160 ?Seven Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does not rest here, however. Even granting that it is {\i soter}, a further question emerges: {\i kulo soter o shiv\u8217'ah soter mahu} \u8212- does the {\i tumah} destroy the entire thirty days, requiring the {\i nazir} to recommence from the beginning, or does it only obligate a seven-day waiting period ({\i soter shiva})? This question becomes interesting specifically in the context of Rabbi Eliezer. The Rabbis hold that {\i tumah} requiring a restart is always thirty days; under their view, whether the {\i tumah} occurred during or after the term, the outcome is thirty days. But Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position is more nuanced: he holds that {\i tumah} occurring after thirty days is {\i soter shiva} only. The mishnah under discussion is, as established, the position of Rabbi Eliezer. So now: under Rabbi Eliezer, if the {\i nazir} became {\i tamei} during the thirty days but discovered it only afterward \u8212- is that treated like {\i tumah} after thirty days ({\i soter shiva}), or like a standard mid-{\i nezirut} {\i tumah} ({\i soter hakol})?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the possibility that {\i soter shiva} might apply here, reasoning that since the {\i yedi\u8217'ah} came after {\i melot}, perhaps Rabbi Eliezer would treat it similarly to {\i tumah} occurring after the term. Conversely, since the {\i tumah} itself occurred during the thirty days, perhaps it destroys the full {\i nezirut} even for Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes by observing that the mishnah employs the language {\i bein kach u-vein kach soter} \u8212- \u8220"either way it is {\i soter}\u8221" \u8212- without distinguishing between the case of becoming {\i tamei} during the term and discovering it during the term, on the one hand, and becoming {\i tamei} during the term and discovering it afterward, on the other. Tosafot, the Rosh, and the pseudo-Rosh on this {\i daf} all read the {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion as follows: since Rabbi Eliezer rules that {\i tumah} after thirty days is {\i soter shiva}, and since the mishnah draws no distinction between that case and the case of {\i tumah} during thirty days discovered afterward, the {\i gemara} equates them \u8212- and the conclusion is that even {\i tumah} occurring {\i during} the thirty days, if discovered only afterward, is {\i soter shiva} under Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view. This reading is, as must be acknowledged, not immediately transparent from the {\i gemara}\u8217's language. The argument is that since the mishnah does not differentiate, we infer that the case of late discovery is treated identically to the post-term {\i tumah} scenario \u8212- which Rabbi Eliezer rules is {\i soter shiva} rather than {\i soter shloshim}. This is a striking conclusion, and the reading requires some care.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Full Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Road Span Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now quotes the full {\i baraita} on {\i tumat ha-tehom}. The first case: {\i ha-motzi met mutal le-rochbah shel derech} \u8212- a corpse discovered lying the full width of a road, buried and unknown ({\i tumat ha-tehom}). The ruling: {\i le-trumah tamei} \u8212- for {\i terumah} purposes the person is {\i tamei}; {\i u-venazir u-ve-oseh Pesach tahor} \u8212- but for {\i nazir} and {\i oseh Pesach}, the principle of {\i tumat ha-tehom} applies and his obligations were fulfilled. The use of {\i tahor} here is somewhat loose, as noted above: he is not literally {\i tahor} and must still undergo the purification process; the meaning is that his {\i nezirut} and {\i Pesach} obligation are not invalidated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Escape Route\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} immediately qualifies this ruling: {\i bameh devarim amurim she-ein lo makom la-avor} \u8212- when is this true? When there was no space to pass around the body \u8212- the corpse genuinely spanned the entire width, leaving no room for the traveler to have avoided it. {\i Aval yesh lo makom la-avor} \u8212- but if there was a small strip of the path, even a foot\u8217's width at the edge, that the corpse did not reach, such that a person walking that extreme edge would have avoided the {\i met}, then even for {\i terumah} the person is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Domain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is the principle of {\i safek tumah be-reshut ha-rabim}: a public road is a {\i reshut ha-rabim}, and a {\i safek} about {\i tumah} in a {\i reshut ha-rabim} is ruled {\i tahor}. Even if the probability of having walked in that narrow strip is very small \u8212- 5% of the road\u8217's width was clear, 95% covered \u8212- the existence of any possibility that one avoided the corpse is sufficient in a {\i reshut ha-rabim} to declare the person {\i tahor} for all purposes, including {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Body Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} adds a further qualification: {\i bameh devarim amurim she-matza\u8217'o shalem} \u8212- when is the ruling about a clear path being sufficient to be {\i tahor} applicable? Only when the body was whole. {\i Aval meshubar o mefurak} \u8212- if the body was broken apart or dismembered, even if there was a clear path, {\i chayshinan bein perakim avar} \u8212- we are concerned that the traveler may have woven through the gaps between the limbs, which means he could have passed through the space occupied by the body without walking over any single part. The logic is that a dismembered body presents multiple gaps, and it is possible that the traveler threaded his way through them rather than walking over any portion. In such a case, even the possibility of having avoided the corpse does not save him, because we are concerned that he may in fact have passed between the limbs and thus been in {\i ohel} with the body through the gaps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 In Coffin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However: {\i u-ve-kever} \u8212- if all the limbs, even if dismembered, were enclosed in a coffin that spanned the full width of the path, then {\i afilu nishbar u-meforak tamei}, even if dismembered, the person is {\i tamei}. The reason is {\i mipnei she-ha-kever mitztarfo} \u8212- the coffin unites all the contents as a single legal unit. One may recall that a coffin ({\i kever}) itself has the status of the {\i met} it contains; everything within the coffin is treated as a single entity. Therefore there are no \u8220"gaps\u8221" through which the traveler could have passed \u8212- the entire coffin is the {\i met}, and one cannot weave between its contents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Walking vs.\u160 ?Carrying\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bameh devarim amurim} \u8212- all the above leniencies concerning the possibility of having avoided the body apply {\i be-mahalech be-raglav}, when the traveler was walking on foot. {\i Aval ta\u8217'un o rachuv} \u8212- if he was carrying a burden or riding on an animal, then {\i tamei} even where there might have been a narrow escape route. The reason: {\i lefi she-ha-mahalech be-raglav efshar lo she-lo yiga ve-shelo yasit ve-shelo yahil} \u8212- a person walking on his feet can be precise enough in his movements to potentially avoid touching, shifting, or creating an {\i ohel} over the corpse. But {\i ee efshar she-lo yiga ve-shelo yasit ve-shelo yahil} \u8212- a person burdened with a load or mounted on an animal cannot control his movements to the same degree; he will inevitably have leaned, swayed, or extended over the space in a way that creates {\i tumah}. The {\i halakha} here is calibrated to circumstantial reality: not every claimed scenario is evaluated individually, but a general assessment is made that a mounted or burdened traveler cannot achieve the precision of avoidance that a careful walker might.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After all these cases, the {\i baraita} provides the formal definition: {\i ve-eizehu tumat ha-tehom, kol she-ein makkirin otah be-sof ha-olam} \u8212- {\i tumat ha-tehom} is any {\i tumah} that is unknown to anyone, even at the far end of the world. {\i Makkira echad be-sof ha-olam, ein zo tumat ha-tehom} \u8212- if even one person anywhere knew of it, it does not qualify. This is the unknowability criterion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Straw vs.\u160 ?Crevices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The burial criterion is also specified precisely. {\i Hayah tamun be-teven o bi-tzrorot} \u8212- if the body was buried under straw or under pebbles, {\i harei zu tumat ha-tehom} \u8212- this is {\i tumat ha-tehom}. The {\i chiddush} is that even covering as light as straw is sufficient to qualify as \u8220"buried\u8221" for this purpose. On the other hand, {\i be-yamim u-va-afelah u-venekikei ha-sla\u8217'im} \u8212- if it was in water, in darkness, or in a crevice of a rock, {\i ein zo tumat ha-tehom} \u8212- these do not qualify as \u8220"buried.\u8221" The apparent paradox is that a body in a rock crevice \u8212- which might be very difficult to see \u8212- is considered exposed, while a body under straw is considered buried. The best understanding is that \u8220"buried\u8221" in this context means covered {\i in a manner that the ground conceals it}, whereas a crevice, even a deep one, is part of the open landscape and thus \u8220"exposed\u8221" in the relevant legal sense. The precise limits of this distinction are not fully spelled out by the {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Met Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the {\i baraita} closes: {\i ve-lo amru tumat ha-tehom ella met bilvad} \u8212- the entire principle of {\i tumat ha-tehom} applies only to {\i tumat met}, the {\i tumah} of a corpse. This makes sense: {\i tumat ha-tehom} is the specific exception carved out for {\i nazir} and {\i oseh Pesach}, and the relevant {\i tumah} for both of those contexts is {\i tumat met}. Other forms of {\i tumah} (such as {\i tumah} from a {\i sheretz}, etc.) are not subject to this exception.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Floating Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheretz Floating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} uses the case in the mishnah \u8212- which involved a {\i met} that was described as {\i tzaf}, floating \u8212- as an occasion to examine an entirely separate {\i halakha} regarding floating and {\i safek tumah}. The principle to be established: a floating {\i tumah} \u8212- not related to {\i tumat ha-tehom}, but a floating {\i sheretz} (one of the eight creeping creatures whose carcass conveys {\i tumah}) \u8212- creates a {\i safek} that is treated more leniently than a standard {\i safek tumah be-reshut ha-yachid}. The {\i baraita} states: {\i safek tumah safa, bein bi-kelim bein bi-karka, tehorah} \u8212- a doubt about {\i tumah} involving a floating object, whether floating in a vessel or floating in the ground, is ruled {\i tahor} according to the {\i tanna kamma}, even in a {\i reshut ha-yachid}, where ordinarily a {\i safek tumah} would be ruled {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 On Earth Drashah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for this ruling: Rabbi Yitzchak bar Avdimi derives it from a {\i pasuk}. The verse states {\i be-khol ha-sheretz ha-shoretz} \u8212- \u8220"all creeping things that creep,\u8221" which would imply that contact with any {\i sheretz} anywhere renders one {\i tamei}. But the verse also says {\i al ha-aretz} \u8212- \u8220"on the ground.\u8221" Rabbi Yitzchak bar Avdimi reconciles these by distinguishing between {\i vadai maga\u8217'o} and {\i safek maga\u8217'o}. If one {\i certainly} touched the {\i sheretz}, one is {\i tamei} wherever the {\i sheretz} is \u8212- on the ground, in water, floating anywhere. But if there is a {\i safek} whether one touched it, then {\i al ha-aretz} \u8212- one is only {\i tamei} when the {\i sheretz} was on the ground. If it was floating, and there is a {\i safek} about the contact, one is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Floating Tahor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic, on reflection, is that floating objects are in some sense less \u8220"present\u8221" in a space than objects resting on the ground. The principle of {\i safek tumah be-reshut ha-yachid tamei} operates on the premise that the {\i tumah} source is fixed and present in a private domain; the {\i safek} is purely about whether contact occurred. But a floating object is not anchored \u8212- it is less definitively \u8220"there\u8221" \u8212- and the verse {\i al ha-aretz} limits the {\i safek tumah} rule to cases where the {\i tumah} source is resting on solid ground. In a {\i reshut ha-rabim}, this distinction is academic since {\i safek tumah be-reshut ha-rabim} is always {\i tahor}; the ruling matters only in a {\i reshut ha-yachid}, where the floating exemption overrides what would otherwise be a presumption of {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees with the {\i tanna kamma} in one respect: {\i bi-kelim tamei, bi-karka tahor} \u8212- if the {\i sheretz} was floating in a vessel, a {\i safek} about contact renders one {\i tamei}; if floating in the ground (i.e., in a body of water naturally in the ground), one is {\i tahor}. Ulla explains Rabbi Shimon\u8217's source: the verse {\i akh mayan u-vor mikveh mayim yihyeh tahor} \u8212- \u8220"but a spring, a pit, or a collection of water shall be {\i tahor}\u8221" \u8212- establishes that water naturally contained in the ground has a special status of {\i tahara}. But the verse also states {\i yitma} \u8212- implying that in other contexts {\i tumah} does apply. Rabbi Shimon reads this as distinguishing between floating in a natural body of water in the ground ({\i tahor} for a {\i safek}) versus floating in a vessel ({\i tamei} for a {\i safek}). Tosafot raises the question of whether the same principle would apply to an object flying through the air rather than floating in water \u8212- if a {\i sheretz} was thrown and one is uncertain whether it struck him, does the \u8220"not on the ground\u8221" exemption similarly apply? The question is raised but not resolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 65\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Single Unknown Burial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Body in Normal Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the bottom of 64b opens with a case that has little direct connection to {\i nazir} per se, but picks up on thematic associations with the opening {\i mishnah} of the tractate \u8212- particularly the principle of {\i tumat hatehom} and the phrase {\i raglayim ladavar}. The {\i mishnah} begins: {\i hamotzei meit techila} \u8212- if someone discovers a dead body that was previously unknown, {\i mushkav kedarko}, lying in the normal burial position. As the {\i gemara} will clarify, this specification is not incidental; it comes to exclude certain other cases, as we will see.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario presupposes a body that has been discovered, perhaps in the middle of a road or a field \u8212- somewhere it does not belong in the public sphere. In ancient times, if someone died while traveling, the practical exigencies of the moment might have compelled companions to bury the body wherever they happened to be, with the intention perhaps of returning later to relocate it properly. The paradigmatic case from {\i Tanach} is that of {\i Rachel}, about whom it is written {\i vatikaver sham bederech Ephrat} \u8212- she was buried right there on the road. Such a burial was understood not to confer a permanent right on that location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Removal & Reburial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that such a burial was understood as temporary and not conferring a permanent claim to the site, the {\i mishnah} rules: {\i notlo v\u8217'et tfusato} \u8212- one removes the body together with its {\i tfusa}. The word {\i tfusa} derives from {\i l\u8217'tpos}, to grab or seize, and refers to the surrounding earth that is taken up along with the body. The body is then to be reburied in a proper cemetery. The rationale for the relocation is to ensure that {\i kohanim}, {\i nezirim}, and others obligated to avoid {\i tumat meit} are not prevented from using the road or area.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purpose of Dirt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is the surrounding dirt taken as well? At first glance this might seem puzzling. After all, bodily fluids in themselves, once the person has died, do not necessarily constitute a primary source of {\i tumah} \u8212- the classic categories of {\i tumat meit} are flesh, bone, and blood in specific quantities. One might ask, then, what is the concern that drives the requirement to remove the surrounding earth?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer seems to be that the concern is not purely about {\i tumah} in the technical sense but about {\i kevura} \u8212- the dignity and integrity of the burial. One wants all the remains of the {\i meis} to be reinterred together. There is also a possibility, as the {\i gemara} will elaborate, that some material in the surrounding earth genuinely could contribute to the halakhic quantities of {\i rekev} \u8212- the decomposed matter of a corpse \u8212- that do cause {\i tumah}. The {\i mishnah} continues: if two bodies are found, {\i shnayim notlan v\u8217'et tfusatan} \u8212- you take both and the surrounding dirt of each and rebury them. But the critical ruling comes next.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shechunat Kvarot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Bodies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discovery of a single body, or even two bodies, does not by itself trigger any assumption of a graveyard. Each is treated individually: you remove it with its surrounding earth and rebury it elsewhere. No special investigation of the surrounding area is required. It is only when three bodies are found that the analysis shifts fundamentally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Bodies Threshold\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Matza shlosha} \u8212- if three bodies are discovered \u8212- {\i im yeish bein zeh l\u8217'zeh arba amot ad shmoneh}, if there is between them a spacing within a range of four to eight {\i amot}, then {\i harei zeh shechunat kvarot} \u8212- this constitutes a {\i shechunat kvarot}, a neighborhood of graves, that is to say, a graveyard. The logic of the four-to-eight {\i amot} range is that this corresponds to the spacing one would expect in a crypt or cemetery. If the bodies were too close together, one might suspect a mass burial under some tragic circumstance rather than a planned cemetery. If they were too spread out, they would simply appear to be isolated individual graves with no relation to one another. Within this particular range, the arrangement reflects what one would expect of a deliberately organized burial site.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What precisely the four-to-eight {\i amot} range means in terms of the layout of the three bodies is itself a matter of detailed discussion. {\i Tosafos} engages at length with this question in light of a {\i gemara} in {\i Bava Basra} concerning how bodies were placed in {\i kuchim} \u8212- burial niches or crypts. {\i Tosafos} offers the possibility that two of the bodies are within four {\i amot} of each other, while the full range extends to eight {\i amot} for all three, though the precise geometric arrangement remains somewhat unclear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twenty-Cubit Radius\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once three bodies are found within this range and the location is declared a {\i shechunat kvarot}, the {\i mishnah} mandates a further investigation: {\i bodeik mehemenu u\u8217'lehalan esrim ama} \u8212- one must check outward from this point twenty {\i amot}. The presumption is that if this is indeed a graveyard, there may be further burials beyond those three initially discovered, and one must determine the full extent of the graveyard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If within that twenty-{\i ama} radius another grave is found, then {\i bodeik mehemenu u\u8217'lehalan esrim ama} \u8212- you must check again, another twenty {\i amot} from that new discovery. The graveyard is assumed to be continuing, and the investigation must keep pace with each new find. The phrase the {\i mishnah} invokes for this cascading investigation is {\i raglayim ladavar} \u8212- there is good circumstantial evidence warranting continued suspicion that the graveyard extends further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is one of the two reasons this {\i mishnah} appears here rather than simply in a tractate dealing with purity law. First, the subject matter involves buried {\i tumah} of unknown provenance \u8212- {\i tumat hatehom} \u8212- which connects to the opening {\i mishnah} of the tractate. Second, the phrase {\i raglayim ladavar} appears here and connects textually to the {\i mishnah} that preceded it, though in that earlier context the phrase carried a somewhat different nuance: there it referred to a pre-existing basis \u8212- the person had already been {\i tamei}, and that prior status formed the foundation upon which a new {\i tumah} was assessed. Here, {\i raglayim ladavar} carries its more common meaning of circumstantial evidence justifying ongoing investigation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes with an important interpretive point: {\i she\u8217'ilu techila matza\u8217'o notel et tfusato} \u8212- had you found that one body alone, in isolation, you would simply have removed it with its surrounding dirt and reburied it elsewhere. You would not have been required to conduct any twenty-{\i amot} investigation, and even had you proactively done so and found another grave twenty {\i amot} away, you would not have declared the area a graveyard. You would have treated each grave as a separate isolated burial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But now, because you began with a cluster of three graves, and this additional grave falls within the twenty {\i amot} radius of that cluster, the interpretive context is entirely different. The same grave that in isolation would have been treated as a lone burial is now read as part of the graveyard. This is a striking illustration of how the identical physical fact \u8212- one grave \u8212- can carry entirely different legal significance depending on what has already been established about the surrounding context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Implicit in the {\i mishnah}, though not stated explicitly, is a principle the {\i gemara} spells out: once a location is determined to be a {\i shechunat kvarot}, the bodies there are not to be disinterred. They have a right to remain where they are. The original permission to disinter a lone grave rests on the assumption that the burial there was incidental \u8212- no one plants a permanent cemetery in the middle of a road. But if there is a graveyard, the graveyard predates the road, the road was built over the graveyard, and the bodies have every right to their place. In that case, you do not disinter the graves; you reroute the road.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exceptions & Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Discovered vs.\u160 ?Known\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to interpret each qualifying term in the {\i mishnah} as excluding a category of case. The method is one of {\i prat lezeh} \u8212- each word of the {\i mishnah} functions to exclude something, though the {\i gemara} notes somewhat wryly that this sounds like the style of {\i derashah} applied to a biblical verse, whereas here it is being applied to a {\i mishnah}. Nevertheless, the {\i mishnah} clearly articulates criteria, and those criteria function to exclude cases that do not meet them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Matza} \u8212- discovered \u8212- {\i prat lematzuy}: this applies specifically to a body whose existence was previously unknown. If a grave was always known to exist in a particular place, that changes the analysis entirely. A known grave is not subject to disinterment; its location was established and has always been recognized. The permission to move a grave depends on the assumption that it was an incidental or emergency burial; a known grave carries with it the presumption that it was intentionally placed there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Murdered Bodies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meit} \u8212- if it is a dead body \u8212- {\i prat le\u8217'harug}, as opposed to someone who was murdered. The {\i gemara} is about to cite the principle that these rules apply only when the body is whole, and a murdered or dismembered body would by definition not be whole. This explains the exclusion, at least in part, though {\i Tosafos} acknowledges that the rationale for excluding a murdered body is not made fully explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Position Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mushkav kedarko} \u8212- lying in the normal way \u8212- {\i prat leyoshev}, as opposed to sitting upright, and {\i prat leroshon munach bein yarchosav}, as opposed to a fetal position with the head placed between the knees. These exclusions address cases where the burial posture suggests the body may be that of a non-Jew. The normal Jewish burial practice was to lay the body horizontally; sitting or fetal positions were associated with non-Jewish burial customs. Notably, Bronze Age burials in the land were often in a sitting position, so this is not merely a theoretical concern. If the body is non-Jewish, then {\i tumat ohel} does not apply according to the ruling that a non-Jew does not convey {\i tumat ohel}, and therefore the entire apparatus of investigation becomes irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incomplete Bodies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tani Ulla bar Chanina}: a {\i braita} teaches that if the body is incomplete \u8212- {\i meit she\u8217'chasar ein lo tfisah} \u8212- there is no requirement to take the surrounding dirt, {\i velo shechunat kvarot} \u8212- and if three such incomplete bodies are found, one does not establish a {\i shechunat kvarot} from them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling raises a genuine question. The consequence in all these exclusion cases is not that one is prohibited from disinterring \u8212- it is, if anything, more lenient: one {\i can} disinter without the additional stringencies. One need not take the surrounding earth, and one need not worry that three such bodies establish a graveyard. The only case where disinterment is clearly prohibited is the case of a {\i known} grave \u8212- {\i prat lematzuy} \u8212- where the presumption is that the grave has a right to remain in place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i Tosafos} raises the question: what is the logic for saying that an incomplete body does not establish {\i shechunat kvarot}? The answer given is {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai} \u8212- which {\i Tosafos} acknowledges is, formally speaking, something of a last resort in terms of explanations, though it acknowledges that the tractate has had occasion to invoke {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai} repeatedly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the cases where the exclusion is based on the possibility that the bodies are non-Jewish \u8212- there are two possible consequences: either you are still allowed to disinter but without the stringencies of {\i tfusa} and {\i shechunat kvarot}, or, if we assume the bodies are non-Jewish, you may simply move on entirely, since a non-Jewish body does not convey {\i tumat ohel} and there is no {\i tumah} concern driving the investigation in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head-to-Foot Burial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Matza shnayim, rosho shel zeh b\u8217'tzad marglotav shel zeh, v\u8217'rosho shel zeh b\u8217'tzad marglotav shel zeh} \u8212- if two bodies are found buried in a head-to-foot, head-to-foot arrangement, lying parallel to each other in alternating orientation \u8212- {\i ein lahem tfusa v\u8217'lo shechunat kvarot}. Here too one does not take the surrounding dirt, and three such burials do not establish a {\i shechunat kvarot}. This type of arrangement is not consistent with normal Jewish burial practice, suggesting once again that these may be non-Jewish graves, or at least that something atypical is occurring that should not trigger the full apparatus of the graveyard rules.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Known/Unknown\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Matza shlosha, echad yadua u\u8217'shnayim techila} \u8212- if three bodies are found, one of which was always known and two of which are newly discovered \u8212- or, switching to a group of four, two known and two newly discovered \u8212- {\i ein lahem tfusa v\u8217'ein lahem shechunat kvarot}. Even though three or four bodies are present, if the group is a mixture of known and newly discovered graves, neither {\i tfusa} nor {\i shechunat kvarot} applies in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} explains the logic for the {\i shechunat kvarot} exclusion, which at least makes a certain amount of sense. The category of {\i shechunat kvarot} applies either to three bodies that were all previously known \u8212- in which case they constitute a recognized graveyard \u8212- or to three bodies all newly discovered \u8212- in which case we infer that this must be a forgotten graveyard. But a mixture of the two does not fit either category. If it were a recognized graveyard, one would have known about all three. If it were a forgotten graveyard, one would have found all three newly. The mixed case falls between the categories; one can simply assume that the known graves were always there and the newly discovered ones are coincidental additions. There is no basis to declare the entire area a graveyard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for why {\i tfusa} also does not apply in the mixed case, {\i Tosafos} quotes the {\i Rashbam} who invokes {\i halacha leMoshe miSinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yeshevav & Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeshevav\u8217's Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records a historical incident: {\i maaseh b\u8217'Rabbi Yeshevav she\u8217'badak u\u8217'matza shnayim yeduyim v\u8217'echad techila} \u8212- there was a case involving Rabbi Yeshevav who investigated and found two graves that were previously known and one that was newly discovered. He sought to declare this combination a {\i shechunat kvarot} and to cordon off the surrounding twenty {\i amot} for investigation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Rebuke\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva said to him: {\i kol she\u8217'yagata l\u8217'rik yagata} \u8212- all the effort you have put in is for nothing. The phrase is striking and evokes something of the sensibility of {\i Kohelet} \u8212- all our efforts amount to naught. But the legal point is sharp: {\i lo amru shechunat kvarot ela l\u8217'gimel yedua\u8217'im o l\u8217'shlosha techila} \u8212- the category of {\i shechunat kvarot} was said only when all three are previously known or all three are newly discovered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pure Graveyard Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The requirement is, in other words, categorical uniformity. Either three known graves \u8212- a recognized graveyard \u8212- or three newly discovered graves \u8212- a forgotten graveyard. Any mixture of the two, and one does not have grounds to declare the area a {\i shechunat kvarot}. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's rebuke also carries a practical dimension: declaring large areas to be graveyards has significant consequences for the public, foreclosing movement through those areas for {\i kohanim} and others who must avoid {\i tumat meit}. There is therefore good reason to be precise about when the threshold is met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tfusa & Soil\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tfusa Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} says {\i notlan v\u8217'et tfusatan} \u8212- one takes the bodies and their {\i tfusa}. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i heichi dami t\u8217'vusa} \u8212- what exactly constitutes the {\i tfusa}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egypt Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar kra} \u8212- Rav Yehuda derives it from a biblical verse: {\i amitatni miMitzrayim} \u8212- \u8220"take me up from Egypt,\u8221" the words of Jacob to Joseph regarding his burial. The {\i drasha} reads into the phrase the idea that Jacob was asking that some of Egypt \u8212- some of the earth \u8212- come with him. Rashi explains this as connected to the notion that Jacob did not want to remain in {\i adama tme\u8217'ia}, impure soil, and wished to be brought back to the land of Israel along with some of the surrounding earth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Fingers Earth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar bar Rabbi Tzadok specifies: {\i notel afar tichuach} \u8212- one takes the loosened, dug earth, {\i v\u8217'chofer b\u8217'vetula} \u8212- and one also digs into the virgin, undisturbed earth beneath the body, {\i gimmel etzba\u8217'ot} \u8212- three finger-widths deep. The rationale is that bodily fluids are assumed to have seeped even into the undisturbed earth beneath the corpse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coffin & Shards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a {\i braita} that appears to give a different account of the {\i tfusa}: {\i v\u8217'kama hi tfusa \u8212- peirash Rabi Elazar b\u8217'Rabi Tzadok: notel et hakismin v\u8217'et hatzrorot}. Here the {\i tfusa} is described in terms of taking {\i kismin} \u8212- splinters, presumably from the coffin \u8212- and {\i tzrorot}. {\i Tosafos} explains {\i kismin} as material rubbed off from a marble coffin, and as for {\i tzrorot}, Rashi explains them as clumps of earth that have absorbed blood and brain matter from the body \u8212- {\i tzrorot adama shenit\u8217'havu min hadam u\u8217'min hamuach} \u8212- so that they may contain substance that contributes to the halakhic quantities of the {\i meis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sifting Remains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is significant about this {\i braita} is that it does not simply say one takes all the surrounding earth indiscriminately. Rather, there is a process of sifting and discrimination: {\i v\u8217'zorek et havada\u8217'in} \u8212- you discard that which is certainly not part of the {\i meis}; {\i u\u8217'maniach et hasfeikot} \u8212- you set aside in a secluded place that which may possibly contain part of the {\i meis}, so that people engaged in matters of {\i tahara} should not come into contact with it; {\i v\u8217'hashar mitztaref l\u8217'rov binyano shel meis u\u8217'l\u8217'rov minyano u\u8217'l\u8217'mlo tirvad rekav} \u8212- and what is clearly identified as part of the body itself combines toward the threshold quantities: the majority of the body\u8217's structure ({\i rov binyano}), the majority of the body\u8217's count of bones ({\i rov minyano}), or the {\i melo tirvad rekav} \u8212- a full ladle\u8217's worth of the decomposed matter of the corpse \u8212- all of which are the halakhic thresholds for {\i tumat meis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is notable because it addresses the question raised earlier about {\i why} the surrounding dirt is removed. It is not a blanket concern about any fluid; it is because specific identifiable material \u8212- material that genuinely constitutes part of the {\i meis} or its decomposition \u8212- may have mingled with the earth, and that material contributes to the {\i shiurim} that convey {\i tumah}. One does not remove everything; one tries to identify what genuinely matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the earlier formulation \u8212- simply taking three finger-widths of dug and virgin earth \u8212- reflects the view of a different {\i tanna}, and that {\i Rav Yehuda amar Rav} follows that position. The {\i braita} of sifting and selecting follows a different approach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rekev Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises a difficulty connected to this passage. For material to count as {\i rekev} \u8212- decomposed matter of a corpse \u8212- and to contribute to the {\i shiur} of {\i melo tirvad rekav}, it must be pure, uncontaminated by foreign matter. But if the body was buried with fragrances, spices, or other materials placed in the {\i aron}, as was apparently common practice, then any decomposed matter would be contaminated by these foreign substances and would not qualify as {\i rekev}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} addresses this by suggesting that the {\i kismin} in the {\i braita} refer to ground-off fragments of a {\i marble} coffin \u8212- {\i aron shel shayish shenichtat} \u8212- rather than a wooden coffin. The reason is that a wooden coffin would itself decompose along with the body and potentially disqualify the {\i rekev}. If the coffin is marble, this problem is avoided. But this solution in turn raises its own difficulty: if the coffin is marble, how would bodily fluids seep out far enough into the surrounding earth to be a concern? The discussion illustrates how intricate these questions become once one tries to reconcile the {\i braita}\u8217's details with the underlying halakhic requirements for {\i rekev}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sequential Discovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Body Found\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava}: suppose one checks and disinters, then checks again and disinters, and then on a third occasion finds another body \u8212- {\i badak ufina, badak ufina, badak ve\u8217'ashkach, mahu} \u8212- what is the law? The question is this: if a person had already removed the first two bodies and then discovered a third, the three together would constitute a {\i shechunat kvarot}. But the two have already been disinterred. What now?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Graveyard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tension is between two considerations. On one hand, retroactively one can say this was a {\i shechunat kvarot} all along, and therefore the bodies had a right to remain where they were and should not have been disinterred. On the other hand, the disinterment of the first two had already been completed before the third was found; at that point in time there was no reason to suspect a {\i shechunat kvarot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Compromise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: {\i lo hai mafni leh legabi hanach terei, velo hani terei legabi hai chad} \u8212- you do not move the third to join the two that were disinterred, since you now know it was part of a graveyard; but you do not need to reinter the two alongside the third either. Each situation is left as it currently stands. The two that were already moved remain where they were reinterred, and the third remains in its original location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Continue Disinterring\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ika d\u8217'amri} \u8212- there is an alternative formulation of Rava\u8217's ruling: {\i keivan she\u8217'nasna reshut lefanot, mefaneh lehawn} \u8212- once permission to disinter was granted, one may continue and disinter the third as well. Since the process was already legitimately begun \u8212- two were already moved \u8212- and since practically speaking there is a communal interest in having all bodies in proper cemeteries rather than scattered about fields and roads, once one has started one may continue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ilu Matzu Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i velishavinhu shechunat kvarot} \u8212- why not simply declare this a {\i shechunat kvarot} and leave all three in place? Reish Lakish answers: {\i ilu matzu v\u8217'tiharu Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- they found an excuse and thereby purified {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The idea is that there is a broader interest in keeping {\i Eretz Yisrael} accessible and navigable for {\i kohanim}, for those engaged in {\i taharos}, and for the public generally. When any excuse presents itself to treat a site as not a {\i shechunat kvarot} \u8212- thus permitting the bodies to be removed to a proper cemetery \u8212- one avails oneself of it. In the sequential discovery case, the excuse is that when each body was disinterred, there was no known graveyard; the third body was found only afterward. Therefore one is permitted to use this as the {\i eila} \u8212- the legal basis \u8212- to avoid declaring the site a {\i shechunat kvarot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle, it should be noted, has a somewhat ironic dimension. The formal halakhic posture is that {\i Eretz Yisrael} is {\i tahora} and {\i Eretz Ha\u8217'amim} is {\i tamei}. Yet here the {\i gemara} reveals that this presumption of purity for {\i Eretz Yisrael} was maintained precisely {\i because} the rabbis used any available excuse to clear areas from {\i tumah} suspicion. The presumption of purity is not simply a given; it is actively constructed through halakhic creativity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} reads the {\i ilu matzu} principle more broadly. He comments that Reish Lakish\u8217's statement reflects a general operating principle: {\i ba\u8217'alila muetes uvisvara katana tiharu Eretz Yisrael mishum kohanim v\u8217'osei taharos} \u8212- with a small excuse and a modest argument, they purified {\i Eretz Yisrael} for the sake of {\i kohanim} and those engaged in {\i taharos}. This is not merely a rule for the specific sequential-discovery case; it is a general stance that a low threshold of reasoning suffices to establish purity of a place, because practical life in {\i Eretz Yisrael} requires it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} adds: {\i v\u8217'lo dami l\u8217'kever yadua d\u8217'asur lifnos d\u8217'ein kan yedia vada\u8217'is} \u8212- and this is not comparable to a definitively known grave, which cannot be disinterred, because in the case of sequential discovery there is no truly certain knowledge that this was a graveyard. The discovery was piecemeal, and when each disinterment occurred, the graveyard status had not yet been established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 No Find in Radius\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twenty-Cubit Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} poses a further question: {\i badak kaf ama v\u8217'lo matza mahu} \u8212- if one checked twenty {\i amot} and found nothing, what is the status? The question is somewhat ambiguous. {\i Tosafos} understands it as asking about checking in one direction and finding nothing \u8212- but as {\i Tosafos} notes, one does not understand why finding nothing in one direction should mean anything, since the graveyard might extend in the other direction. Nevertheless, that is how {\i Tosafos} reads the question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Still a Graveyard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Nachman bar Yirmya amar Rav: shechunat kvaros} \u8212- it remains a {\i shechunat kvarot}. Now, one would expect {\i shechunat kvarot} to be the stringent ruling \u8212- the one that prevents disinterment. But {\i Tosafos} understands this as a {\i kula}, a leniency: the answer is that having found nothing, you may assume that you have reached the end of the graveyard and need not continue checking further in that direction. This reading, as {\i Tosafos} acknowledges, does not sit perfectly with the words of the {\i gemara}, but that is how {\i Tosafos} resolves it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intentional Burial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} offers a fundamentally different reading of the question and answer. According to the {\i Rosh}, the question is: after finding three bodies and checking all twenty {\i amot} around in all directions \u8212- finding nothing \u8212- may you now disinter those original three? Perhaps the failure to find anything else in the radius suggests it is not really a {\i shechunat kvarot} after all, just three coincidental graves; and if not a {\i shechunat kvarot}, then they can be disinterred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer, says the {\i Rosh}, is no: {\i harei zeh shechunat kvarot} \u8212- it remains a {\i shechunat kvarot}. {\i U\u8217'pshita lei keivan d\u8217'gimmel hem muchzak lei shechunat kvarot af al pi shelo matza} \u8212- once three bodies are found, the presumption of {\i shechunat kvarot} is established even if the twenty-{\i amot} investigation yields nothing further. Three bodies in the appropriate configuration is sufficient to establish that this was an intentional burial site. Perhaps it was a small graveyard with only three graves; that is still a graveyard, and those bodies may not be removed. According to this reading, the {\i ilu matzu} line that appears in some manuscripts is a copyist\u8217's error transferred from the preceding discussion, and the {\i Rosh} removes it from his text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzaraat Doubts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Purity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} has no connection to {\i nazirut} at all, except that it employs structural principles parallel to those seen in the earlier {\i mishnayot} \u8212- particularly the principle of {\i raglayim ladavar} and the concept of {\i chazaka}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i kol safek negaim bit\u8217'chilatah tahor} \u8212- all doubts regarding {\i negaim} before the person has been declared {\i tamei} are resolved in favor of {\i tahara}. {\i Ad shelo nizkak l\u8217'tumah} \u8212- as long as the person has not yet been formally established as {\i tamei}, any doubt as to whether a skin lesion qualifies as {\i nega tzara\u8217'at} is resolved leniently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is {\i chazaka} \u8212- status quo. The person was {\i tahor} before; absent a definitive determination that the {\i nega} meets all requirements for {\i tumah}, the prior status is maintained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Established Impurity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishenizkak l\u8217'tumah} \u8212- once the person has been formally established as {\i tamei}, {\i sfeko tamei} \u8212- any subsequent doubt is resolved in favor of {\i tumah}. If there is a question whether the {\i nega} has cleared sufficiently to render the person {\i tahor}, the status quo of {\i tumah} is maintained. This mirrors the principle from the opening {\i mishnah} of the tractate: a person already {\i tamei} is not saved by a subsequent {\i tumat hatehom} being retroactively unknown, because {\i raglayim ladavar} \u8212- there was already a pre-existing basis of {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} invokes the phrase {\i mipnei she\u8217'chezkatam tamei} \u8212- because the presumptive status is {\i tamei}. The mechanism is pure {\i chazaka}: whatever the prior status was, it is maintained in the face of doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two People Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} provides a vivid illustration of how this {\i mishnah} operates in practice, drawing on cases from tractate {\i Negaim}. Two people come before the {\i kohen} at the same time. One has a lesion of a certain smaller size; the other has a lesion the size of a {\i sela} \u8212- a coin, larger than a {\i gris}. Neither is yet declared {\i tamei}; both are examined and locked up for a week of assessment. When they return after the week, both lesions are now the size of a {\i sela}. One of them has clearly spread \u8212- having started smaller, it grew \u8212- and is therefore {\i tamei}. The other, which started at {\i sela} size and remained at {\i sela} size, did not spread and was never even the minimum size, so it is {\i tahor}. But the {\i kohen} has forgotten which person had which initial lesion size, and neither person remembers either. The ruling: both are {\i tahor}. Even though one of them is objectively {\i tamei}, since neither can be identified as the {\i tamei} one, both retain their initial {\i chezkat tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lesion Progression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} then presents the reverse scenario. Two people come, one with a lesion smaller than a {\i gris}, one with a lesion the size of a {\i sela}. Both are examined and locked up. They return and both have lesions the size of {\i sela va\u8217'od} \u8212- larger than a {\i sela}. Both have spread; both are declared {\i tamei}. They are locked up again. They return once more, and now both lesions have diminished, returning toward a {\i sela} in size. The question is: when does each of them become {\i tahor}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baseline Return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is: each person must return to the size they had {\i initially}, before any spread. For the person who began with a {\i sela}-sized lesion, returning to a {\i sela} suffices to establish {\i tahara} \u8212- that is the original baseline. For the person who began with a smaller lesion, a return to {\i sela} size is not enough; the lesion must return all the way to its original smaller size. Now if both return to {\i sela} size simultaneously and the {\i kohen} cannot recall who started with which size, both remain {\i tamei}. The one who started smaller has not yet returned to baseline; the one who started at {\i sela} has. But since one cannot determine which is which, both retain their {\i chezkat tumah} until the matter can be clarified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nega Appearance Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse on Purity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mena hani milei} \u8212- what is the source for the principle of initial {\i tahara} in cases of doubt? {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav amar kra: letaharo o letame\u8217'o} \u8212- the verse says \u8220"to purify or to make {\i tamei}.\u8221" The fact that the verse mentions {\i tahara} first \u8212- {\i ho\u8217'il ufatah bo hakatuv betahara techila} \u8212- since the Torah opened with {\i tahara}, this indicates a disposition toward {\i tahara} as the starting point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purity Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately objects: {\i ei hachi, afilu mishenizkak l\u8217'tumah nami sfeko tahor} \u8212- if the verse teaches a general inclination toward {\i tahara}, then even once a person has been declared {\i tamei}, any subsequent doubt should also be resolved in favor of {\i tahara}. That would contradict the second half of the {\i mishnah}. So the verse cannot be the source of the principle stated in the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Whitening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by relocating the verse\u8217's teaching: {\i ela ki itmar deRav Yehuda amar Rav aha itmar} \u8212- this verse was cited by Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav for a different {\i halacha} entirely. The verse is not the source for the status quo principle; that principle rests on the general rule of {\i chazaka}, which operates independently. The verse instead addresses a specific case: {\i im beheret kadma lese\u8217'ar lavan tamei, ve\u8217'im se\u8217'ar lavan kodem labeheret tahor} \u8212- if the {\i baheret} lesion appeared first and then the hair within it turned white, the person is {\i tamei}; but if the hair was always white before the lesion appeared, the person is {\i tahor}, since the {\i baheret} did not cause the hair to whiten. {\i Safek} \u8212- if one does not know which occurred first \u8212- the person is {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is structurally different from the ordinary doubt: it is not a question of two separate individuals whose identities are confused. It is a question about the order in which two events occurred in the same person. The general principle of {\i chazaka} does not straightforwardly resolve this, since one knows the person previously had naturally white hair but also knows the lesion is present. It is in this specific case that the verse supplies a rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unknown Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua comments {\i kehe} \u8212- which Rav Yehuda explains as meaning {\i tahor}. The {\i Rosh} understands Rabbi Yehoshua as providing the rationale for why the order-of-events doubt resolves to {\i tahara}: the verse opens with {\i letaharo}, establishing that when the order is unknown, one presumes {\i tahara} based on the textual priority given to purity. This is the unique contribution of the verse in this specific context, as distinct from the {\i chazaka}-based principle governing the general cases in the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zav Checks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Factors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The penultimate {\i mishnah} \u8212- again unrelated to {\i nazirut} but brought here for the phrase {\i raglayim ladavar} \u8212- deals with the law of {\i zav}. {\i Beshiv\u8217'ah derachim bodkim et hazav} \u8212- in seven ways one examines the {\i zav}, the man with an abnormal penile discharge. The context requires some clarification. A man can have two types of emission: seminal emission ({\i keri}), which conveys a lighter degree of {\i tumah}, or {\i zivah}, an abnormal non-seminal discharge, which conveys a heavier degree. The law of {\i zav} applies to the latter. A man becomes a {\i zav} after two such discharges; a third requires him to bring a {\i korban} upon purification. The seven-way check applies {\i ad shelo nizkak le\u8217'zivah} \u8212- before he has yet been established as a {\i zav} \u8212- meaning before he has seen two such discharges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The seven factors are potential external causes that, if present, would explain the discharge as something other than {\i zivah}, thus exempting the emission from counting toward {\i zav} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 External Causes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The seven factors are: {\i bemachal uv\u8217'mishteh} \u8212- something he ate or drank that may have caused the discharge; {\i bemassa} \u8212- carrying a heavy load, physical exertion; {\i uv\u8217'kfitzah} \u8212- jumping, again exertion; {\i uv\u8217'choli} \u8212- illness. If any of these factors were present, and the man has not yet seen two discharges to establish {\i zav} status, the emission is attributed to the external cause and does not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sight & Thoughts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remaining factors are: {\i uv\u8217'mareh uv\u8217'hirhur} \u8212- something he saw and thoughts that arose from it, referencing visual stimulation and sexual thoughts that might have prompted the discharge through a pathway analogous to arousal. Though counted separately, these two are understood as connected \u8212- something seen leading to {\i hirhur}, mental dwelling, which in turn might cause the emission through something akin to seminal stimulation even if the discharge itself is not semen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Zav Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before {\i zav} status is established \u8212- {\i ad shelo nizkak le\u8217'zivah} \u8212- if any of these seven factors is present, the emission is attributed to the external cause rather than to {\i zivah}, and the man does not advance toward {\i zav} status. The logic is that until the pattern is established, any available explanation for the emission is taken seriously and used leniently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zav Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishenizkak le\u8217'ziva} \u8212- once the man has been established as a {\i zav} by having two qualifying emissions, the calculus changes entirely. {\i Ein bodkin oto} \u8212- one does not investigate these seven factors anymore. Even if exertion, illness, or other factors are present, a subsequent discharge is treated as {\i zivah} regardless. The established condition is presumed to be the cause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones & Semen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Onso vesfeko veshichvat zaro tme\u8217'im} \u8212- once he is a {\i zav}, discharges that would otherwise be attributed to {\i ones} \u8212- external compulsion \u8212- are {\i tamei}; discharges that are in doubt as to their nature are {\i tamei}; even an emission of semen from a {\i zav} is treated as {\i tamei}, on the possibility that it contains {\i zivah} mixed within it. All categories of discharge, once {\i zav} status is established, are presumed to be manifestations of the underlying condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attributed to Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason given is {\i sheraglayim ladavar} \u8212- because there is now a pre-existing basis, a {\i raglayim ladavar}, that all emissions are attributable to his condition. Once the condition is established, there is no need to search for alternative explanations; everything is attributed to the {\i zivah}. This is the third application of {\i raglayim ladavar} in these {\i mishnayot}, and here it carries its clearest meaning: a pre-established factual basis that governs the interpretation of all subsequent ambiguous events.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fatal Wound\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mortal Wound\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} brought in this cluster \u8212- again for {\i raglayim ladavar} \u8212- addresses a capital case: {\i hamakeh chavero} \u8212- one who strikes another, {\i ve\u8217'amduhu lemitah} \u8212- and the victim is assessed as likely to die from the wound. The striker faces potential execution for murder if the victim dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recovery & Relapse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehekel mimah shehaya} \u8212- but then the victim improves. The wound appeared mortal, the prognosis seemed clear, but the person begins to recover. Does this improvement change the legal analysis? Then: {\i v\u8217'le\u8217'achar mikan hichbid umet} \u8212- after the initial improvement, the victim deteriorates and ultimately dies. Does the death still trace back to the original wound?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Attributed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first opinion: {\i chayav} \u8212- the striker is liable. The fact that the victim temporarily improved does not break the causal chain. The injury was declared mortal at the outset, and the victim ultimately died. The improvement is simply part of the natural and unpredictable course of a fatal wound, and the death is attributed to the original act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Cause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi Nechemiah omer patur} \u8212- Rabbi Nechemiah says he is exempt. The improvement, in Rabbi Nechemiah\u8217's view, is itself legally significant evidence. It is {\i raglayim ladavar} \u8212- circumstantial evidence \u8212- that the original wound was not in fact fatal; something else intervened to cause the ultimate death, and one cannot attribute the death to the original striker.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Circumstantial Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This use of {\i raglayim ladavar} is distinct from the earlier applications. In the graveyard case, {\i raglayim ladavar} referred to positive evidence justifying ongoing suspicion. In the {\i zav} case, it referred to an established condition that governed interpretation of subsequent ambiguous events. Here, Rabbi Nechemiah uses {\i raglayim ladavar} to mean that the circumstantial evidence \u8212- the fact of improvement \u8212- points away from the original wound as the cause of death. The improvement gives {\i raglayim} \u8212- legs, grounds \u8212- to the inference that the original wound was survivable, and therefore the striker is exempt. The {\i tanna kamma}, by contrast, reads the circumstances as confirming the original assessment: the victim was fatally wounded and ultimately died, with the improvement being an interlude that does not change the fundamental trajectory.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Nazir 66\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Raglayim L\u8217'Davar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 External Causes Dismissed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} on daf 66 opens at the bottom of 65b, picking up from a {\i Mishnah} that, while having little direct connection to {\i Nazir}, introduced the phrase {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar} \u8212- the same phrase that appeared at the beginning of the {\i perek}. The {\i Mishnah} concerned a {\i zav}, a man who has experienced the abnormal genital discharge designated as {\i zivah} by the Torah. Before a man becomes a full {\i zav}, we check whether each sighting of the discharge might be attributable to some external cause \u8212- {\i ones} \u8212- such as exertion, illness, or certain foods. If so, that sighting is discounted. Once he has had two flows, however \u8212- making him a {\i zav} in the preliminary sense \u8212- any subsequent flows are relevant either because a third flow obligates him to bring a {\i korban} or because any flow during the seven clean days he must count will reset that count entirely. The principle of {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar} teaches that for these subsequent flows, we no longer concern ourselves with the possibility of external causes: since the man is already a {\i zav}, we assume the continued flow stems from his condition, not from something incidental.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 V\u8217'Hazav Et Zovo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i minah hanei milei} \u8212- what is the scriptural source for this distinction? Rabbi Natan answers by citing the verse {\i v\u8217'hazav et zovo} \u8212- \u8220"and the one who flows, his flow\u8221" (Leviticus 15:3) \u8212- and notes that the verse concludes {\i l\u8217'zachar ul\u8217'nekeivah}, \u8220"whether male or female,\u8221" drawing an analogy between the male {\i zav} and the female {\i zavah}. The difference between the two is instructive. For a woman, the operative distinction in the Torah is between {\i niddah} \u8212- menstrual bleeding occurring during the expected cycle \u8212- and {\i zavah} \u8212- bleeding outside the menstrual period. The question for her is entirely about {\i when} the blood flows, not {\i what} is flowing. For a man, by contrast, the distinction is between {\i shichvat zera}, a seminal emission, which is natural and its own category of {\i tumah}, and {\i zivah}, which is an abnormal discharge distinguishable from semen by its consistency, as described in the {\i Mishnah}. For a woman, external causes are entirely irrelevant \u8212- the {\i tumah} applies regardless. The verse {\i v\u8217'hazav et zovo l\u8217'zachar ul\u8217'nekeivah} is therefore read as establishing when the male {\i zav} is treated like the female: after the double language of {\i zovo} \u8212- implying two prior flows \u8212- external causes cease to matter for him as well. From the third sighting onward, he is analogized to the woman for whom {\i ones} is never a consideration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Rabban\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited that appears to complicate this picture. Rabbi Eliezer holds that even for the third flow \u8212- the one that would obligate a {\i korban} \u8212- we still check for external causes. According to him, it is only the fourth flow and beyond (those that could interrupt the seven clean days) for which we no longer check. The {\i tanna kamma}, by contrast, holds that from the third sighting onward, {\i ones} is already irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Et Particle Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} traces this disagreement to a hermeneutical dispute over the word {\i et} in {\i v\u8217'hazav et zovo}. The {\i rabbanan} do not treat the particle {\i et} as expanding the scope of the verse, and so they read {\i v\u8217'hazav zovo} \u8212- two flows \u8212- as the threshold after which external causes are dismissed; the third flow is therefore already governed by the rule of {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar}. Rabbi Eliezer, by contrast, applies the standard exegetical principle associated with Rav Nachum Ish Gamzu and does {\i darish et} \u8212- reads the particle as adding to the count \u8212- yielding three flows as the threshold, so that only the fourth flow is beyond scrutiny. All authorities agree, in any event, that from a certain point onward, {\i ones} is simply not a consideration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Regarding Emission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity of Emission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then lists additional categories treated as {\i tamei}: {\i ones}, {\i safek}, and {\i shichvat zera}. Having addressed {\i ones}, the {\i Gemara} now turns to {\i safek}. Rava clarifies that the {\i safek} in the {\i Mishnah} does not refer to a case where one is uncertain whether anything came out at all. Such a doubt would not be governed by {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar} \u8212- the mere fact that a man is already a {\i zav} does not allow us to assume that a new flow occurred when there is no evidence of one. Rather, Rava explains: {\i vadai chaza safek machmat shichvat zera safek machmat re\u8217'iyah} \u8212- something definitely emerged, but its identity is unknown. The doubt is whether what came out was the result of a seminal emission or whether it was {\i zivah} flowing on its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zivah with Semen Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does this distinction matter? {\i Tosafot} clarifies by reference to a rule taught elsewhere: {\i zivah} that is mixed together with semen \u8212- {\i depeticha bei shichvat zera} \u8212- destroys only one day of the man\u8217's clean count, whereas {\i zivah} that flows on its own \u8212- {\i delo peticha bei shichvat zera} \u8212- destroys all seven days and requires him to start again from the beginning. The practical stakes are therefore significant. If a man is, say, on day three of his seven clean days, and something occurs: if it was a seminal emission, there is no interruption at all. If it was a mixed emission \u8212- {\i zivah} together with semen \u8212- he cannot count that one day but may continue counting the rest. If it was {\i zivah} alone, he must begin the entire count anew. The {\i safek} of the {\i Mishnah} addresses the case where one finds evidence of both substances \u8212- stains of each \u8212- and cannot determine whether they emerged together or separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strict Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava concludes: {\i keivan shenizkak l\u8217'tumah, sfeko tamei} \u8212- since the man has already established himself as a {\i zav}, we invoke {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar} and adopt the stricter reading. We assume that the {\i zivah} came out on its own, not mixed with the semen, and the man must restart all seven days.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zav\u8217's Semen Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Semen Tumah by Masa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} includes {\i shichvat zera} \u8212- semen \u8212- as {\i tamei}. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i l\u8217'mai hilchata} \u8212- for what practical {\i halachah}? If the point is merely that touching the {\i zav}\u8217's semen renders one {\i tamei}, that would hardly be remarkable: even the semen of any ordinary man who is {\i tahor} is itself an {\i av hatumah} and conveys {\i tumah} by contact. The {\i Gemara} therefore concludes that the {\i Mishnah} must be establishing something more: {\i zaro shel zav metamei b\u8217'masa} \u8212- the semen of a {\i zav} conveys {\i tumah} even through carrying ({\i masa}), not merely through contact. This would place it in the same category as the {\i zivah} flow itself, which is {\i metamei b\u8217'masa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Yehosh\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then identifies which {\i tanna} holds this position, by citing a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Eliezer says that the semen of a {\i zav} is {\i not metamei b\u8217'masa}; Rabbi Yehoshua says it {\i is metamei b\u8217'masa}. Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's reasoning is not that semen inherently achieves this status \u8212- rather, he reasons that since the man is a {\i zav}, it is impossible that any penile emission would not contain within it at least some droplets of {\i zivah} \u8212- {\i tzikhtzukhei zivah}. It is those droplets, not the semen itself, that account for the stringent {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Droplets Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This seems a perfectly serviceable reading of the {\i Mishnah}: our case involves a known {\i zav}, and {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar} allows us to assume that any emission will be contaminated with {\i zivah} \u8212- this is precisely why we treat even his semen strictly. The {\i Gemara}, however, pushes back: {\i afilu Rabbi Yehoshua lo amar ella mishum tzikhtzukhei zivah, aval b\u8217'inei lo amar} \u8212- even Rabbi Yehoshua does not say that pure semen alone, entirely free of any {\i zivah}, would be {\i metamei b\u8217'masa}. This is an abstract distinction \u8212- in practice there is indeed no way to separate the emissions \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} treats it as meaningful in principle, and so will not read the {\i Mishnah} as making a statement solely about the droplets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ada Bar Ahava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Instead, the {\i Gemara} cites Rav Ada bar Ahava: {\i lomar she\u8217'ein tolin bah} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's reference to {\i shichvat zera} is teaching that once a man is a {\i zav}, we do not attribute a subsequent {\i zivah} flow to the seminal emission that preceded it. This interpretation connects back to the laws of {\i ones}: among the external causes that would ordinarily discount a {\i zivah} is a prior seminal emission. The reasoning is that the body may be weakened or affected by the ejaculation, and a flow following it closely may therefore be attributed to that cause rather than to the {\i zivah} condition itself. But once this man is already an established {\i zav}, the principle of {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar} operates: we no longer attribute the flow to the {\i ba\u8217'al keri} that preceded it. {\i She\u8217'ein tolin bah} \u8212- we do not chalk it up to the seminal emission. That is the law the {\i Mishnah} is teaching when it says {\i shichvat zera} is {\i tamei} \u8212- not that the semen conveys elevated {\i tumah}, but that being a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} no longer serves as a discounting factor for a man who is already a {\i zav}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Weakness Theory Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pappa\u8217's Weakness Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now steps back to examine the underlying rationale for why a seminal emission ordinarily exempts from {\i zivah}. Rav Pappa, reasoning before Rava, proposed that it is a straightforwardly physical explanation: the body is weakened by the ejaculation \u8212- {\i aiday khulsha hu d\u8217'chazei} \u8212- and any subsequent discharge is therefore attributable to that depleted physical state, much as a flow following illness or exertion would be attributed to those conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Conversion Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenged this with a {\i Mishnah}: {\i ger shenitgayer metamei miyad b\u8217'zivah} \u8212- a convert who converts and then experiences a {\i zivah} flow immediately becomes a {\i zav}, and we do not take into account anything that may have happened to his body immediately before his conversion. If the exemption of {\i ba\u8217'al keri} were genuinely based on the physical condition of the body, then a seminal emission occurring moments before immersion in the {\i mikveh} should matter just as much as one occurring after conversion \u8212- the body\u8217's state is unchanged by the conversion itself. {\i Tosafot}, in the name of the Ri, reconstructs Rava\u8217's argument as follows: {\i ein lecha choli gadol mizeh} \u8212- meaning that this case of the convert presents exactly the same physical reality, yet we disregard what occurred before his conversion. The contrast with the other causes listed in the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- illness, certain foods, exertion \u8212- is striking: those are real physical conditions that presumably affect the body regardless of one\u8217's legal status, yet for the {\i ba\u8217'al keri} exemption, a pre-conversion occurrence is treated as entirely irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirat HaKatuv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes: {\i ela tanai hi} \u8212- it is a dispute among {\i tanna\u8217'im}. More precisely, the underlying conclusion is that the exemption of {\i zivah} following {\i ba\u8217'al keri} is not grounded in the physical state of the body at all, but is rather a {\i gezeirat hakatuv} \u8212- a Scriptural decree, derived from elsewhere in the {\i midrash halachah}, that this specific combination does not count as {\i zivah}. Because it is a legal decree tied to the person\u8217's obligations under the Torah, it applies only from the moment of conversion onward. Whatever occurred before the man was Jewish falls outside the scope of the decree entirely. This also resolves the question that now re-emerges about the {\i zav}: if the {\i ba\u8217'al keri} exemption is a {\i gezeirat hakatuv} rather than a description of physical reality, does it potentially continue to operate even after a man becomes a {\i zav}? Rav Ada bar Ahava\u8217's reading of the {\i Mishnah} addressed precisely this \u8212- once he is a {\i zav}, {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar} removes the basis for applying that exemption in the first place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Semen & Scripture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Yihyeh Tahor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the scriptural grounding for the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's statement that the semen of a {\i zav} is {\i tamei}. The {\i pasuk} in question is drawn from {\i Devarim} \u8212- entirely removed from the {\i parshiyot} of {\i Vayikra} that deal with {\i zav} \u8212- and reads: {\i ki yihyeh vecha ish asher lo yihyeh tahor mikreh layla}, \u8220"if there is a man among you who is not {\i tahor} because of an occurrence of the night.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} reads this as referring to a man who is already not {\i tahor} \u8212- already a {\i zav} \u8212- and who then experiences a nocturnal emission, a {\i mikreh layla}. That emission, in his case, is to be treated as {\i zivah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duration of Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this basis, a {\i tannaitic} dispute is cited. One view holds that the semen of a {\i zav} conveys {\i tumah} for a full twenty-four hour period \u8212- {\i kol me\u8217'et le\u8217'et} \u8212- following the {\i zivah} flow. Rabbi Yose holds that it applies only for {\i yomo} \u8212- that same period of the day, roughly a twelve-hour segment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verses Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} identifies the source of this dispute in a contradiction raised by Shmuel between two expressions in the same passage. One phrase is {\i mikreh layla} \u8212- \u8220"an occurrence of the night\u8221" \u8212- implying that the {\i tumah} pertains only to the night of the emission. The other phrase, later in the verse, is {\i lifnot erev yirchatz bamayim} \u8212- \u8220"towards evening he shall bathe in the water\u8221" \u8212- implying that the {\i tumah} extends all the way until the following evening, a full twenty-four hours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The view that holds {\i me\u8217'et le\u8217'et} derives from {\i lifnot erev}: the man experiences the event at night, and the semen\u8217's status as {\i zivah} persists until the next evening, a period of approximately twenty-four hours. The view of Rabbi Yose that holds {\i yomo} derives from {\i mikreh layla}: only that same nocturnal period carries the stringency. The {\i Gemara} then asks: for the view that holds {\i lifnot erev} \u8212- what does he do with {\i mikreh layla}? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i orchah de\u8217'keri} \u8212- nocturnal emissions typically occur at night; that phrase simply reflects the normal circumstance, while the {\i tumah} itself extends until the following evening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of this entire discussion is to establish from a {\i pasuk} that the semen of a {\i zav}, flowing in the wake of {\i zivah}, is treated as {\i zivah} itself \u8212- and is therefore {\i metamei b\u8217'masa}. This scriptural grounding supersedes the earlier discussion about whether the {\i tumah} is only because of the admixed droplets of {\i zivah}, or whether the semen itself achieves this status during the defined window following a {\i zivah} flow. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's statement that {\i shichvat zera} is {\i tamei} is thus substantiated on a principled, textual basis, not merely through the practical inference of {\i raglayim l\u8217'davar}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Closing Aggadot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samuel Nazir Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes the tractate with a series of {\i aggadic} passages. The final {\i Mishnah} states: {\i nazir hayah Shmuel k\u8217'divrei Rav Nehorai} \u8212- Samuel was a {\i nazir}, according to Rav Nehorai. The scriptural basis is the phrase spoken before Samuel\u8217's birth: {\i u\u8217'mora lo ya\u8217'aleh al rosho} \u8212- \u8220"and a razor shall not come upon his head\u8221" (1 Samuel 1:11). The verse says nothing about wine or contact with the dead, the other {\i nezirut} prohibitions, but the language of the razor is itself decisive. Moreover, the same word {\i mora} appears in the verse describing Shimshon: {\i nazir Elokim yiheyeh hana\u8217'ar min habaten} \u8212- \u8220"the youth shall be a {\i nazir} of God from the womb.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} reasons by means of a {\i gezeirah shavah}: the same term {\i mora} used for Shimshon \u8212- who is explicitly called a {\i nazir} \u8212- appears also for Samuel, and therefore Samuel too was a {\i nazir}. One might note the irony that in both cases it is neither the father nor the subject himself who initiates the {\i nezirut}, but rather a mother or an angel speaking to a mother \u8212- recalling earlier discussions in the tractate about whether a father may impose {\i nezirut} on a son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mora Meaning Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose challenges Rav Nehorai: {\i v\u8217'halo ein mora ela shel basar vadam} \u8212- surely {\i mora} means fear, not razor! The word, he argues, means that no human being shall instill fear in Samuel. Rav Nehorai deflects this reading by invoking the plain narrative of Shmuel itself: {\i vayomer Shmuel eich eileich v\u8217'shama Shaul va\u8217'haragani} \u8212- \u8220"Samuel said: how shall I go? If Saul hears, he will kill me!\u8221" Samuel was clearly afraid of a human being. The interpretation of {\i mora} as \u8220"fear of mortals\u8221" therefore cannot stand, and the reference must be to the razor. One might have thought the most straightforward counter would be to point out that {\i mora} with the meaning of razor is spelled with a {\i heh}, while {\i mora} meaning fear is spelled with an {\i alef} \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} pursues the narrative refutation instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chiyya Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following the close of the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's material, Rav said to his son Chiyya: {\i chatof u\u8217'varech} \u8212- seize the opportunity and make the {\i bracha}. Rashi explains that this refers to being the one to lead {\i birkat zimun} or to recite {\i kiddush} or similar communal blessings. Rav Huna similarly told his son Rabbah: {\i chatof u\u8217'varech}. The implicit teaching is that one should eagerly pursue the opportunity to recite a blessing on behalf of others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amen Response Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises a difficulty: does this not imply that the one who recites the {\i bracha} has the greater merit? But a {\i baraita} teaches explicitly \u8212- {\i gadol ha\u8217'oneh Amen yoser min hamevarech} \u8212- responding {\i Amen} is greater than making the blessing. Rav Nehorai confirms this with an oath: {\i hashamayim kach hu} \u8212- by heaven, this is so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He offers a vivid military illustration: {\i she\u8217'rei gulyarim misgarim b\u8217'milchama v\u8217'giborim notzchim} \u8212- the lighter front forces go out and engage first, but it is the mighty warriors who follow who ultimately prevail. So too in the structure of prayer: the one who recites the {\i bracha} goes first and initiates, but the one who answers {\i Amen} provides the force that carries it through. The image recalls a contemporary insight: a lone person acting publicly is easily dismissed, but the first follower who joins in is what transforms an individual gesture into a movement. The one who answers {\i Amen} is that first and decisive responder \u8212- lending weight and communal reality to the blessing that preceded it. It is also worth noting that the Sephardic custom of responding {\i hashamayim} when one is about to recite a blessing on behalf of the community \u8212- such as for {\i sefirat ha\u8217'omer} or {\i zimun} \u8212- resonates precisely with this passage, since {\i hashamayim} is itself the response of the {\i oneh} preceding the {\i bracha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mevarech vs.\u160 ?Oneh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that this is in fact a {\i tannaitic} dispute: one view holds {\i echad hamevarech v\u8217'echad ha\u8217'oneh Amen bamashma} \u8212- whether one recites the {\i bracha} or answers {\i Amen}, both are equally reckoned. The {\i Gemara} seems to conclude that while the two are equal in ultimate weight, the counsel to {\i chatof u\u8217'varech} was nevertheless practical guidance \u8212- encouraging one to take initiative \u8212- even if the {\i oneh Amen} carries no lesser merit. The passage is presumably cited here in part because Rav Nehorai, the unusual {\i tanna} whose name appeared in the final {\i Mishnah}, also appears in this {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peace Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tractate closes with the words of Rabbi Elazar in the name of Rabbi Chanina: {\i talmidei chachamim marbim shalom ba\u8217'olam shene\u8217'emar v\u8217'chol banayich limudei Hashem v\u8217'rav shalom banayich} \u8212- Torah scholars increase peace in the world, as it is said: \u8220"And all your children shall be students of God, and great shall be the peace of your children.\u8221" With these words, {\i masechet Nazir} reaches its conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach masechet Nazir.}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro to Sotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi & Commentary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin a new {\i masechet} today: {\i Sotah}, daf 2. A few preliminary remarks about the tractate are in order. After the extended journey through {\i Nazir}, we are grateful to have genuine {\i Rashi} restored to us \u8212- and one comes to appreciate {\i Rashi} all the more having gone without him for a time. His commentary is distilled, clear, and remarkably terse, and that terseness has cascading effects on the shape of the {\i daf} as a whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In {\i Nazir}, the pseudo-{\i Rashi} had to do double duty: both explain the flow of the {\i gemara} in the manner of {\i Rashi} and take on a broader expository role \u8212- and he did so at considerable length. {\i Tosafos}, in turn, was largely occupied with the work of basic clarification rather than the broader analytical and comparative questions that are {\i Tosafos}\u8217's hallmark. Here in {\i Sotah}, by contrast, genuine {\i Rashi} handles the baseline explanation with characteristic economy, freeing {\i Tosafos} to focus on precisely those sharper, cross-tractate analytical questions that define it at its best. The result is that both commentaries are leaner, and there is correspondingly more {\i gemara} per {\i daf}. Add to this the substantial amount of {\i aggadeta} woven throughout {\i Sotah} \u8212- one can glance ahead and see it already taking over entire {\i dappin} \u8212- and the conclusion is that we will need to move at a somewhat brisker pace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Content Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As advertised by its placement in {\i Seder Nashim}, {\i Masechet Sotah} deals with {\i parshat Sotah} in the {\i Torah} and follows a remarkably sequential logical structure. The first six or so {\i perakim} work through the procedure systematically: under what circumstances is a woman declared a {\i sotah} \u8212- a woman suspected of having committed adultery \u8212- such that she must be brought to the {\i Beit Hamikdash} to undergo the ritual of the bitter waters? Once those circumstances are established, the {\i masechet} moves to the procedure itself: the bringing of the waters, the ritual of drinking, the offering of sacrifices, the possible consequences, and then the treatment of special cases. For instance, what is the {\i halakha} if the woman is a {\i shomeres yabam} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibbum} \u8212- who is suspected of adultery? What if she is an {\i arusah} rather than a {\i nesuah}? The structure is almost entirely logical and sequential, proceeding from initial suspicion through to the ritual and its ramifications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Chapters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final chapters of the {\i masechet} are particularly rich. The seventh {\i perek} opens with the question of {\i eilu ne\u8217'emarim bechol lashon} \u8212- which ritual recitations may be said in any language, rather than exclusively in Hebrew. Since the recitation of {\i parshat sotah} itself is among those discussed, this becomes an occasion for a wide-ranging survey of ritual speech: {\i Kriat Shema}, {\i tefillah}, {\i Birkat Kohanim}, and many others. The {\i masechet} uses the question of language as a lens through which to explore other dimensions of these {\i mitzvot} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i perek} concerns {\i eglah arufah} \u8212- the breaking of the neck of the heifer in a case of unresolved murder \u8212- which enters through the same hook of whether its declaration is said {\i bechol lashon}. But the {\i masechet} then takes the opportunity to treat the full parameters of {\i eglah arufah}: when it applies, how one measures the distance to the nearest city, and all its other details. This is, in fact, the one place in {\i Shas} where {\i eglah arufah} receives a sustained treatment; {\i Sanhedrin} handles the prosecution of a murderer once he is found, but {\i Sotah} is the natural home for the case of not knowing. And that, one might argue, is not merely a coincidence of where the hook of language happened to land. Both {\i sotah} and {\i eglah arufah} are rituals performed under conditions of uncertainty \u8212- in one case, uncertainty about whether a grave offense was committed at all; in the other, uncertainty about who committed a grave offense that may itself be known. The pairing seems deliberate and conceptually coherent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 From Jealousy to Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Makane Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens: {\i hamekane le\u8217'ishto} \u8212- if a man warns his wife. Here we immediately encounter a striking transformation of a verb. In the {\i Torah}, the operative verb is {\i ve-kina et ishto} \u8212- and he was jealous of his wife, or he harbored jealousy toward his wife. The root is {\i kaneh}, connoting jealousy, a spirit of {\i kinah}, something entirely internal and subjective. The {\i Torah} itself presents the matter in terms of {\i ruach kinah} \u8212- a spirit of jealousy passed over him \u8212- and then the same act is described again whether or not his suspicion proved correct: {\i ve-kina et ishto ve-hi nitme\u8217'ah, o avar alav ruach kinah ve-kina et ishto ve-hi lo nitme\u8217'ah}. He is jealous; she may or may not have been {\i tmeiah}. The whole structure in the {\i pesukim} is personal, subjective, unverified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\i Chazal} do with {\i hamekane le\u8217'ishto} is something remarkable. Notice even the preposition shifts: in the {\i Torah} it is {\i ve-kina et ishto} \u8212- jealousy directed {\i at} his wife \u8212- whereas the {\i mishna} has {\i mekane le\u8217'ishto}, warning or admonishing {\i his wife}. The entire act is transformed from an expression of private jealousy into a formal legal act: a specific warning, directed at a specific named man, delivered in the presence of witnesses. The subjective emotional state becomes an objective judicial foundation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This transformation is all the more striking when one reads the {\i pesukim} carefully, as they introduce the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah}. The {\i Torah} begins: {\i ish ish ki tisteh ishto u\u8217'ma\u8217'alah bo ma\u8217'al, ve\u8217'shachav ish otah shichvat zera, ve\u8217'ne\u8217'elam me\u8217'einei ishah, ve\u8217'nistarah ve\u8217'hi nitme\u8217'ah, ve\u8217'ed ein bah ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasah} \u8212- a man\u8217's wife has gone astray and was unfaithful; another man lay with her, it was hidden from her husband\u8217's eyes, she was secretive, she became {\i tmeiah}, and there is no witness, and she was not caught. Only afterward does the {\i Torah} describe the husband\u8217's jealousy and the possibility that perhaps she did not sin after all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue \u8212- and it is a compelling reading \u8212- that the {\i pshat} of the {\i pesukim} carries a built-in presumption of guilt. The opening verses do not present an uncertain situation from the outset; they describe what seems to have occurred and then note only the absence of witnesses. From this reading, the ritual of the bitter waters functions not so much to resolve genuine doubt as to clear a woman who is already presumed guilty but against whom the evidentiary threshold for prosecution has not been met. The {\i pasuk} at the conclusion of the {\i parsha} \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'nikah ha\u8217'ish me\u8217'avon ve\u8217'ha\u8217'isha ha\u8217'hi tisa et avonah} \u8212- reinforces this: the husband is explicitly exonerated, implying he did something right in pursuing this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another possibility worth considering is that the opening verses \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'shachav ish otah} and what follows \u8212- describe not objective events but the images filling the husband\u8217's imagination. They render his inner world of suspicion, not a factual account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any case, {\i halakha} reads the situation differently than the {\i pshat} of the {\i pesukim} might suggest. Once a woman achieves {\i sotah} status, she carries the status of {\i tmeiah}: she is forbidden to continue having relations with her husband, who is likewise forbidden from continuing to be with her. She is treated, in effect, as though she committed adultery \u8212- until the ritual in the {\i Beit Hamikdash} clarifies her status. This is the {\i gemara}\u8217's framing: not that she is presumed guilty absent any foundation, but that the circumstantial evidence \u8212- the formal warning, the disregard of that warning, the subsequent {\i stirah} \u8212- creates a sufficient legal foundation. The {\i gemara} will articulate this as {\i raglayim la-davar}: circumstantial evidence, a legitimate basis for assuming.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that {\i Masechet Nazir} closed with a sequence of {\i mishnayot} about {\i raglayim la-davar} \u8212- when circumstantial evidence provides sufficient basis to proceed \u8212- and this may well have been an intentional segue from {\i Nazir} to {\i Sotah}. That phrase becomes central to understanding the entire structure of {\i sotah} as {\i Chazal} reframe it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reframing also calls to mind the {\i Torah}\u8217's treatment of the {\i go\u8217'el ha-dam}, the blood avenger. There too, the {\i Torah} begins from an existing social reality \u8212- the avenger of blood, acting on tribal impulse \u8212- and then introduces a judicial structure around it: the {\i arei miklat}, the role of the {\i beit din}, the return of the killer after the death of the {\i kohen gadol}. The {\i Torah} does not eliminate the institution entirely but channels and constrains it. Something similar is happening with {\i sotah}: the raw jealousy of a husband that might, in a pre-legal context, have resulted in vigilante honor killing is brought under judicial auspices. This point deserves particular emphasis. Already within the {\i pesukim}, the {\i Torah} is controlling what could otherwise become unchecked violence against women, directing it instead into a structured process. The {\i Torah she\u8217'ba\u8217'al peh} goes further still, requiring objective circumstantial foundations before the procedure may even begin.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 From Jealousy to Law (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halakha} as {\i Chazal} develop it thus requires three distinct elements before a woman can acquire {\i sotah} status. First, the husband must formally warn her \u8212- {\i kinuy} \u8212- in the presence of witnesses, specifying a particular man with whom she is not to seclude herself: {\i al tidberi im ish peloni}, do not speak with so-and-so. Second, she must subsequently seclude herself with that man \u8212- {\i stirah} \u8212- in a private location. Third, that seclusion must be of sufficient duration: {\i kedei tumah}, the amount of time it would take to engage in sexual intercourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It bears noting that the {\i kinuy} need not be about private seclusion specifically. A husband\u8217's jealousy might lead him to warn his wife even against speaking with a particular man, well beyond any concern about actual seclusion. That level of warning is sufficient. The {\i mishna} makes explicit, however, that merely violating the warning by speaking with the man does not confer {\i sotah} status and does not affect her permissibility to her husband or \u8212- if she is married to a {\i kohen} \u8212- her eligibility to eat {\i terumah}. What is required is the actual private seclusion of sufficient duration. The word {\i tidberi} in the warning is clearly not a euphemism for sexual relations; the {\i mishna} underscores this by noting that speech alone leaves her fully permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Eliezer vs R. Yeh (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seclusion Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents the dispute between {\i Rabbi Eliezer} and {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} regarding the evidentiary requirements. On the question of {\i kinuy} \u8212- the warning \u8212- both agree: it must be delivered in the presence of two witnesses. The dispute is about {\i stirah}, the testimony of seclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds that {\i mekane lah al pi shenayim u\u8217'mashkah al pi ed echad o al pi atzmo} \u8212- the warning requires two witnesses, but to establish the seclusion and thereby compel her to drink, even one witness suffices, or even the husband\u8217's own testimony. This is a striking position: the husband himself \u8212- who cannot ordinarily serve as a witness in matters concerning his wife, let alone in a case where he is manifestly interested \u8212- is believed to testify to the {\i stirah}. He is not merely expressing suspicion; he is making a positive claim that he directly observed it. But it remains his testimony, which in ordinary jurisprudence would carry no weight whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua}, by contrast, maximizes objectivity: {\i mekane lah al pi shenayim u\u8217'mashkah al pi shenayim} \u8212- both the warning and the seclusion must be established by two witnesses, and the husband cannot be counted among them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asur LeKanot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding to the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis of these positions, the {\i gemara} makes a striking opening move. The {\i mishna}\u8217's use of {\i hamekane} rather than {\i mekane adam le\u8217'ishto} is read as a {\i di\u8217'eved} formulation \u8212- describing what happens when this procedure occurs, not prescribing that a man ought to undertake it. {\i Tosafos} raises the objection that the {\i ha-X} construction does not always signal {\i di\u8217'eved} \u8212- the phrase {\i haholech lishchot et hapesach}, for example, is not read as a {\i di\u8217'eved} \u8212- and there may well be a genuine grammatical or contextual argument embedded in the {\i gemara}\u8217's reading here. But what follows makes the {\i gemara}\u8217's inclination clear. {\i Kasavar tana didan asur lekanot} \u8212- the {\i tanna} of our {\i mishna} holds that it is {\i forbidden} to initiate this procedure. This is the first substantive claim one encounters upon opening {\i Masechet Sotah}: not a technical rule about witnesses, not a procedural requirement, but a moral-legal prohibition. The juxtaposition is powerful and deliberate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This posture is entirely consistent with an ambivalence toward these institutions that runs through {\i Seder Nashim}. Just as {\i Masechet Nedarim} and {\i Masechet Nazir} presented {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut} with persistent ambivalence \u8212- not simply as neutral legal categories but as practices to be approached with caution and even disapproval \u8212- so too {\i Sotah} opens by registering disapproval of the husband\u8217's act. The {\i Torah}\u8217's own framing, recall, concluded with {\i ve\u8217'nikah ha\u8217'ish me\u8217'avon} \u8212- the husband is exonerated. The {\i gemara} will now say something quite different about that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resh Lakish Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites a tradition about {\i Resh Lakish}: {\i amar Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak, ki hava patach Resh Lakish beSota} \u8212- when {\i Resh Lakish} would open his teaching on {\i Sotah}, he would begin with a {\i drasha}, the kind of {\i aggadic} opening one would give in the context of a public discourse. And indeed what he says is {\i aggadic} in register but pointed in implication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matching by Deeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Resh Lakish} would say: {\i ein mezavgin lo le\u8217'adam isha ela lefi ma\u8217'asav} \u8212- a person is only matched with a wife in accordance with his deeds, as it says, {\i ki lo yanuach shevet ha\u8217'rasha al goral ha\u8217'tzaddikim} \u8212- the staff of the wicked shall not rest upon the lot of the righteous. The implication: if a righteous man would not be given a wicked portion, then a man who finds himself with an unfaithful wife must ask what it says about him. If your wife has reached the point of this procedure, what kind of person were you, what did you do, that you deserved a wife capable of this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a direct counterweight to {\i ve\u8217'nikah ha\u8217'ish me\u8217'avon}. The {\i Torah} said the husband is clear of sin. {\i Resh Lakish} is saying: think very carefully before you conclude that. Your wife\u8217's behavior reflects on you. And if that is the theological context, then {\i asur lekanot} gains additional force: going ahead with this procedure is not only halachically disfavored \u8212- it is a public announcement about what kind of person you are. There is also a practical dimension: the waters only work against the wife if the husband himself has been faithful. So this is not merely a theological observation; it has direct ritual consequences.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bashert Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Difficulty of Match\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites {\i Rabbah bar Bar Hana} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: {\i kashah mezavgam ki\u8217'kriat Yam Suf} \u8212- matching two people [in marriage] is as difficult as the splitting of the sea, as it says, {\i Elokim moshiv yechidim baitah} \u8212- God causes isolated individuals to dwell in a home together. The {\i gemara} derives from the juxtaposition with {\i motzi asirim bakosharot} \u8212- bringing out those who are bound \u8212- that God\u8217's work in matching spouses is compared to the great miracle of {\i yetziat Mitzrayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Kol Midrash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} challenges: {\i aini?} Is it really that difficult? But {\i Rav Yehuda} said in the name of {\i Rav}: {\i arba\u8217'im yom kodem yetziras ha-vlad bat kol yotzei ve\u8217'omeret} \u8212- forty days before the formation of the fetus, a heavenly voice goes out and declares: the daughter of so-and-so is destined for so-and-so, the house of so-and-so for so-and-so, the field of so-and-so for so-and-so. The forty-day reference marks the moment of conception \u8212- the period before which the embryo is considered {\i maya be\u8217'alma}, mere fluid \u8212- meaning at the very instant of conception, the match is already declared. If that is so, then the match is not {\i kashah} at all; it is simply pre-determined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty this creates is serious. If the match is declared at conception, how can it be {\i lefi ma\u8217'asav} \u8212- determined by one\u8217's deeds \u8212- when those deeds have not yet occurred, and indeed the person has not yet been born? The principle of {\i bechirah chofshit}, free will, is fundamental: {\i hakol bidei shamayim chutz miyirat shamayim} \u8212- everything is in heaven\u8217's hands except the fear of heaven. Indeed, a parallel {\i midrash} that enumerates what is declared at conception \u8212- will the child be wise or foolish, rich or poor, righteous or wicked \u8212- is itself challenged by the {\i gemara} elsewhere: {\i tzadik} or {\i rasha} must be removed from the list, since righteousness is precisely the domain of free will. How, then, can we say the match is fated?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing over what this {\i midrash} reflects about reality even beyond its theological dimensions. So much about a person\u8217's life is already set before birth: one\u8217's genetic constitution, family of origin, social position, the education one will receive, the community one will inhabit. All of this can be imagined as the content of that {\i bat kol} at conception. That the {\i midrash} includes one\u8217's spouse in this list reflects how deeply pre-determined marriage was in the ancient world \u8212- and raises profound questions about how to hold that together with the notion that {\i Resh Lakish} has asserted, that the match reflects the husband\u8217's moral worth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First vs Second\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya, ha be\u8217'zivug rishon, ha be\u8217'zivug sheni} \u8212- there is no difficulty. The {\i bat kol} at conception, the idea of {\i bashert}, applies to the first marriage. {\i Lefi ma\u8217'asav} \u8212- matching by deeds \u8212- applies to the second marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is not without its difficulties. {\i Tosafos} \u8212- specifically {\i Tosafos Shantz}, from the school of the {\i Rash mi\u8217'Shantz} \u8212- raises the question of perspective: if the matching is from the man\u8217's perspective, then a second marriage for the man might still be a first marriage for the woman, and vice versa, which complicates the picture considerably.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More significantly: if the first marriage is {\i bashert} and not {\i lefi ma\u8217'asav}, then the entire force of {\i Resh Lakish}\u8217's {\i drasha} about {\i sotah} is undermined for the vast majority of cases. Since most men in that era were in first marriages \u8212- divorce was less common, and while death in childbirth did occur, second marriages were far from universal \u8212- {\i Resh Lakish} could hardly have intended his statement to apply only to those on second marriages. Clearly {\i Resh Lakish} meant {\i lefi ma\u8217'asav} as a general principle applicable to all marriages. The {\i gemara}\u8217's reconciliation of the two {\i midrashim} is doing a specific technical job \u8212- resolving a contradiction between two sources \u8212- but it should not be read as undermining the moral-theological thrust of {\i Resh Lakish}\u8217's opening teaching. When we speak colloquially of {\i bashert}, we typically mean a fated match that is also the {\i right} match. The {\i gemara} here seems to leave the first marriage as fated, not necessarily on account of the husband\u8217's merit, and reserves {\i lefi ma\u8217'asav} for the second. That is a rather unsettling picture of what {\i bashert} means.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Eliezer vs R. Yeh (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the {\i mishna}\u8217's dispute: {\i Rabbi Eliezer} holds that the {\i kinuy} \u8212- the warning \u8212- requires two witnesses, but the {\i stirah} \u8212- the seclusion \u8212- can be established by even one witness, or by the husband\u8217's own testimony. His own testimony: {\i al pi atzmo}. This is indeed exceptional. The husband cannot ordinarily testify in matters involving his wife; he is a party with the clearest possible interest. Yet {\i Rabbi Eliezer} permits it here. He is not merely expressing suspicion \u8212- he is making a factual claim of direct observation \u8212- but by any standard of normal jurisprudence, his testimony would be inadmissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} maintains that both {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} require two witnesses, and the husband cannot be counted among those two. This is the most objective standard possible: every step in establishing {\i sotah} status must rest on independently verified evidence, and the husband\u8217's personal interest entirely disqualifies him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One Witness Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now moves to a related but distinct question. The dispute between {\i Rabbi Eliezer} and {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} concerns the {\i stirah} \u8212- the testimony of seclusion. But what about {\i tumah} directly \u8212- that is, what if a witness testifies not merely that the woman secluded herself with the man but that the witness directly observed adultery? The {\i gemara} states that in matters of {\i tumah} \u8212- direct testimony of the act itself \u8212- {\i ed echad meheman}, one witness is believed. This is stated as common ground, not as part of the {\i Rabbi Eliezer}-{\i Rabbi Yehoshua} dispute. Importantly, as {\i Rashi} makes explicit \u8212- {\i betuma shel stira achar kinuy} \u8212- this applies only when the background of {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} has already been established. A single witness appearing out of nowhere to accuse a woman of adultery, without that foundation, is not taken seriously; indeed, the {\i gemara} elsewhere notes that such a claim risks the territory of {\i motzi shem ra}. The single witness\u8217's testimony is only credible when it builds upon the circumstantial foundation of warning and seclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises a sharp question here: why assume that one witness is sufficient even in the case of direct testimony? Given the severity of the consequence \u8212- the woman would be divorced and lose her {\i ketubah} \u8212- perhaps the {\i gemara} should have demanded two witnesses. Indeed, the {\i mishna} itself does not make this explicit; it is the {\i gemara} that introduces the assumption. {\i Tosafos} concludes that we proceed on this assumption because a {\i baraita} is about to confirm it, and the {\i gemara} is using the {\i mishna} as a lead-in to that {\i baraita}. But logically, given the stakes, one could have argued for a higher standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Linguistic Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks {\i mid\u8217'oraita minalan de\u8217'meheman ed echad} \u8212- what is the {\i Torah}-level source for believing a single witness in this context? The derivation proceeds from two {\i pesukim}. The {\i Torah} says in {\i parshat sotah}, {\i ve\u8217'ed ein bah ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasah} \u8212- there is no witness against her and she was not taken by force. And the {\i Torah} elsewhere says, {\i lo yakum ed echad be\u8217'ish} \u8212- one witness shall not rise against a man. Now the {\i gemara} constructs the following: {\i mimashma shene\u8217'emar lo yakum ed be\u8217'ish}, one could have simply written {\i ed} \u8212- a witness \u8212- shall not rise against a man; we would have understood this to mean even a single witness. Why then does the {\i Torah} add the word {\i echad} \u8212- one? This superfluous word teaches a universal principle: {\i kol makom shene\u8217'emar ed harei kan shenayim} \u8212- wherever the {\i Torah} uses the word {\i ed} without qualification, it means two witnesses. The word {\i ed} on its own always implies two; {\i echad} is what explicitly signals that only one is meant. This is a foundational hermeneutical principle in the {\i gemara}\u8217's reading of testimony passages throughout {\i Shas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Witness Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Armed with this principle, the {\i gemara} now returns to {\i ve\u8217'ed ein bah}. Since {\i ed} by itself means two witnesses, the phrase {\i ve\u8217'ed ein bah} \u8212- parsed as {\i ve\u8217'ed} alone, without {\i echad} \u8212- means: there are not {\i two} witnesses against her. There is, however, one. {\i Ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasah} \u8212- and she was not taken by force \u8212- adds that the act, if it occurred, was consensual. From this combination \u8212- one witness testifying to the act, no claim of coercion \u8212- the {\i gemara} derives that the woman is {\i asura}, forbidden to continue living with her husband. The single witness is believed to the degree that she is divorced without a {\i ketubah} and does not undergo the ritual of drinking the waters; that ritual is reserved for cases of genuine unresolved doubt, not for cases where a witness has established what occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking read of the {\i pesukim}. The straightforward {\i pshat} reading of {\i ve\u8217'ed ein bah ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasah ve\u8217'hevi ha\u8217'ish et ishto} is: because there is no witness, because she was not caught, the husband must bring her to the {\i Beit Hamikdash} for the ritual. That is the simple sequence: absence of witnesses \u8594 ? proceed to ritual. The {\i gemara} reads it instead as: there are not two witnesses, but there is one, and the woman has not been raped, therefore she is forbidden and does not undergo the ritual at all. Not only is the {\i gemara} deriving the presence of a single witness from a phrase that literally says \u8220"there is no witness,\u8221" but it is also reading the {\i pasuk} as describing a case where the ritual does {\i not} take place \u8212- the exact opposite of what the {\i pshat} of the sequence suggests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raglayim L\u8217'Davar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then works through what might have been thought \u8212- {\i salka daitach amina} \u8212- had this derivation not existed. One might have thought that in the case of {\i sotah}, a single witness ought to be believed even without a specific {\i Torah} source, and therefore the {\i Torah} would have needed to tell us the {\i opposite}: that a single witness is {\i not} sufficient, that two are required. Why would one naturally have expected a single witness to suffice here? {\i Sotah shani} \u8212- {\i sotah} is different. {\i De\u8217'raglayim la-davar} \u8212- because there is circumstantial evidence. The combination of the formal {\i kinuy} \u8212- the husband\u8217's warning in the presence of witnesses \u8212- and the {\i stirah} \u8212- her subsequent entry into private seclusion with the very man against whom she was warned \u8212- creates a backdrop of substantial circumstantial grounds for suspicion. With that foundation already in place, one might naturally have thought that a single witness testifying to the act would be believed, precisely because his testimony is not standing alone but is corroborating what the circumstantial evidence already suggests. In that case, we would have needed the {\i Torah} to explicitly tell us that one witness is {\i not} enough and two are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hypothetical Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} dismisses this hypothetical reading. How could one have thought that reading the {\i pasuk} to mean \u8220"one witness is not believed, two are required\u8221" and she is then forbidden only if there are two witnesses \u8212- how could one have read {\i ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasah}, that she was not taken by force, as an {\i additional} condition for guilt only when there are two witnesses? The phrase {\i miklal de\u8217'asura} \u8212- the logical implication of the {\i pasuk} is that she is forbidden \u8212- shows that the {\i pasuk} cannot be read as describing a scenario of doubt or permissibility. {\i Ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasah} is an aggravating factor, establishing that what occurred was consensual. It does not introduce a new evidentiary threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, {\i kamashma lan}: read the {\i pasuk} as the {\i gemara} reads it. {\i Ve\u8217'ed ein bah} means there are not two witnesses but one. {\i Ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasah} confirms that the act, if it occurred, was consensual. Together they establish that one witness of adultery \u8212- following {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} \u8212- is sufficient to treat her as forbidden: she is divorced, she loses her {\i ketubah}, and she does not drink the waters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's treatment of this first {\i daf} thus establishes two fundamental orienting principles for the entire {\i masechet}. First, {\i asur lekanot}: the {\i tanna} views the husband\u8217's initiating this procedure with disfavor, and the opening {\i aggadeta} of {\i Resh Lakish} \u8212- {\i ein mezavgin lo le\u8217'adam isha ela lefi ma\u8217'asav} \u8212- gives that disfavor theological grounding. Second, the entire structure of {\i sotah} halakha, including the exceptional credibility granted to a single witness, is justified by {\i raglayim la-davar}: the accumulating circumstantial evidence that the formal structure of {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} creates. Without that foundation, nothing proceeds. With it, the {\i halakha} can extend credibility in ways it would never do in ordinary jurisprudence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kina\u8217'ut: Mitzvah/Reshut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Kina\u8217'ut Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 3a opens by returning to a topic introduced earlier in the {\i masechet}: the question of {\i kina\u8217'ut} \u8212- whether a husband\u8217's act of warning or expressing jealousy toward his wife is a {\i mitzvah}, a {\i reshut}, or perhaps even an {\i issur}. The underlying tension is real: on one hand, if there is genuine cause for suspicion, allowing a problematic situation to go entirely unchecked could itself be deeply corrosive to the marriage. On the other hand, introducing unfounded suspicion into the home risks sowing hatred and conflict. These competing considerations reflect not merely technical {\i halachic} categories but genuine value tensions, and they will animate the discussion throughout this section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva vs.\u160 ?R. Yishmael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to this debate in the form of a {\i gufa} \u8212- going back to the original statements to examine them more closely. The {\i gufa} presents a series of three paired disputes between Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva. First: {\i v\u8217'kina et ishto} \u8212- a husband who warns his wife \u8212- is a {\i reshut}, according to Rabbi Yishmael, and a {\i chova} (obligation), according to Rabbi Akiva. Second: {\i l\u8217'hitama}, a {\i kohen}\u8217's becoming {\i tamei} for his deceased sister, is a {\i reshut} according to Rabbi Yishmael and a {\i chova} according to Rabbi Akiva. Third: {\i le\u8217'olam bahem ta\u8217'avodu} \u8212- the verse permitting the permanent enslavement of Canaanite slaves \u8212- is a {\i reshut} according to Rabbi Yishmael and a {\i chova} according to Rabbi Akiva. Rav Pappa said to Abaye \u8212- and some versions have Rav Mesharshiya saying this to Rava \u8212- a pointed question: {\i leima Rabbi Ishmael v\u8217'Rabbi Akiva b\u8217'chol haTorah kullah hachi paligi} \u8212- does this debate extend across the entire Torah? Is it a general methodological dispute between these two schools?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Methodology: Universal v\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's question \u8212- whether Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva disagree universally or only in these specific cases \u8212- can be read in two ways. Rashi reads it rhetorically, as if to say: surely Rabbi Yishmael does not read every commandment in the Torah as a mere {\i reshut}? {\i V\u8217'asu lahem tzitzit} \u8212- permission? {\i V\u8217'sukkot teshvu} \u8212- permission? Clearly not. An alternative reading, however, takes the question as genuinely open. There are some {\i psukim} that are clearly imperative in form or context, and others that are grammatically or contextually ambiguous. {\i V\u8217'kina et ishto}, for instance, is narrative in form, not imperative. {\i L\u8217'hitama} could be read as permissive. The question thus might be: wherever a {\i pasuk} is genuinely ambiguous as to whether it conveys permission or obligation, does Rabbi Yishmael tend systematically toward {\i reshut} and Rabbi Akiva toward {\i chova}? The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer \u8212- {\i hacha d\u8217'krayi paligi}, that there is something specific in each {\i pasuk} driving them in their respective directions \u8212- supports the view that it is the particular features of these verses, rather than a blanket methodological rule, that generate the dispute. Still, as the analysis of each case will show, underlying interpretive commitments are very much at work.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rashi\u8217's Rhetorical Readi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Rhetorical Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's position is that the question {\i leima Rabbi Ishmael v\u8217'Rabbi Akiva b\u8217'chol haTorah kullah hachi paligi} is posed {\i b\u8217'timyah} \u8212- rhetorically, with an element of disbelief. The force of the question is: does Rabbi Yishmael really interpret every commandment as a mere {\i reshut}? The obvious answer is no, and the rhetorical edge makes that point sharply. {\i V\u8217'asu lahem tzitzit} \u8212- is that a {\i reshut}? {\i V\u8217'sukkot teshvu} \u8212- optional? The absurdity of that reading shows that the question cannot possibly be asking whether Rabbi Yishmael has a global preference for {\i reshut}. According to Rashi, the {\i gemara} is using the rhetorical form to foreclose the possibility of reading these debates as reflecting a sweeping general principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alt Reading: Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative reading of the {\i gemara}\u8217's question takes it as non-rhetorical and genuine. On this reading, the {\i gemara} is asking whether, in cases of genuine textual ambiguity \u8212- whether arising from the grammatical form of the verb, as with {\i v\u8217'kina et ishto} (a narrative rather than imperative construction), or from contextual uncertainty, as with {\i l\u8217'hitama} \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael consistently tends toward {\i reshut} while Rabbi Akiva tends toward {\i chova}. This is not a claim that Rabbi Yishmael reads all of Torah as optional; it is a claim that each {\i tanna} has an interpretive disposition that shapes how he resolves ambiguous cases. The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer \u8212- {\i hacha d\u8217'krayi paligi}, here the specific features of the verses drive them \u8212- does not necessarily refute this reading. It may mean that something in each {\i pasuk} specifically pushes Rabbi Yishmael one way and Rabbi Akiva another, but that on a different {\i pasuk} the same dispositions might yield different results. In other words, it is not a universal rule but neither is it random: the underlying hermeneutical commitments of each school are consistently operative wherever ambiguity exists.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Case Studies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 1: Vekina et Ishto\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines each of the three cases individually. Beginning with {\i v\u8217'kina et ishto}, what is Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's reasoning for reading this as a {\i reshut}? The {\i gemara} answers that Rabbi Yishmael holds in accordance with the position of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, who reasons as follows: since the Torah elsewhere commands {\i lo tishnah et achicha bilvavecha} \u8212- do not harbor hatred toward your fellow in your heart \u8212- one might have thought that a husband who suspects his wife should suppress his concerns and remain silent rather than raise them. By speaking up, he risks introducing open hatred into the marriage. Given this concern, one might have thought it was actually {\i assur} to warn the wife. Since the {\i pasuk} says {\i v\u8217'avar alav ruach kinah v\u8217'kina et ishto}, teaching that he is allowed to act on his jealousy, the most natural first reading is that this constitutes a {\i reshut} \u8212- permission to do something that might otherwise have been forbidden. This context illuminates the genuine value tension embedded in {\i kina\u8217'ut}: the concern about addressing a real problem on one side, and the concern about generating hatred and conflict in the home on the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva Responds: Repet\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva has a compelling counter-argument. The verse {\i v\u8217'kina et ishto} appears twice in the relevant passage \u8212- once in the case of {\i v\u8217'hi nitma\u8217'ah} (she became defiled) and once in the case of {\i v\u8217'hi lo nitma\u8217'ah} (she did not become defiled). The Torah could have simply stated {\i v\u8217'hi nitma\u8217'ah v\u8217'hi lo nitma\u8217'ah} without repeating {\i v\u8217'kina et ishto} a second time. The repetition, Rabbi Akiva argues, is not incidental: it is there to teach that the act of warning is not merely permitted but obligatory \u8212- a {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael: Parasha Pri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael responds by invoking a fundamental hermeneutical principle attributed to his school: {\i kol parasha she-ne\u8217'emra ve-nishnita lo nishnita ela bishvil davar she-nitchadesh bah} \u8212- any passage that was stated and then repeated was repeated only in order to convey one new point. Once the Torah needed to address the two possible outcomes \u8212- {\i nitma\u8217'ah} and {\i lo nitma\u8217'ah} \u8212- it was natural and indeed necessary to repeat the framing context of {\i v\u8217'kina et ishto}. But that repetition was compelled by the structure of the passage, not introduced to add a new level of obligation. One cannot therefore {\i darshan} the repeated phrase to yield a {\i chova}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Philosophy: Omnisignific\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This exchange crystallizes the deep methodological divide between the two schools. Rabbi Yishmael operates on the principle of {\i dibra Torah ki-leshon bnei adam} \u8212- the Torah speaks in the language of human beings. He will certainly interpret verses, but he will not insist that every small redundancy or apparent superfluity carries an independent {\i halachic} lesson. By contrast, Rabbi Akiva is associated with what scholars have termed \u8220"omni-significance\u8221": every detail, every repetition, every seemingly extra letter or phrase is there to teach something. He will {\i darshan} even {\i kotzo shel yud} \u8212- the tip of the letter {\i yud}. With this difference in mind, the pattern across all three cases becomes clear: it is not that Rabbi Yishmael is committed to {\i reshut} as a conclusion, and it is not that Rabbi Akiva is committed to {\i chova} as a conclusion. Rather, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's hermeneutic naturally drives him further \u8212- wherever there is something extra in a {\i pasuk}, he will extract an additional level of instruction from it, reaching {\i chova} where Rabbi Yishmael, declining to {\i darshan} the apparent surplus, remains at {\i reshut}. Both schools begin from the same starting point: without the {\i pasuk}, one might have thought the act was {\i assur}. The {\i pasuk} establishes permission. The question is only whether something within the {\i pasuk} then elevates that permission to an obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 2: Letamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case concerns the {\i kohen} who becomes {\i tamei} for a deceased relative, specifically the case of a sister. The verse in {\i Emor} reads: {\i ki im l\u8217'she\u8217'ero hakrov eilav l\u8217'imo ul\u8217'aviv ul\u8217'vno ul\u8217'vito ul\u8217'achiv} \u8212- for his close relative, his mother, father, son, daughter, and brother. It then continues with the sister. The question of {\i reshut} versus {\i chova} here has concrete {\i halachic} stakes. If it is a {\i reshut}, then a {\i kohen} who, for example, is {\i osek b\u8217'mitzvah} \u8212- engaged in fulfilling another commandment \u8212- need not interrupt his activity to become {\i tamei} for his sister. Likewise, if he wishes to protect his {\i kehunah} status and there is another person available to attend to the burial, he is exempt. That is the {\i reshut} position of Rabbi Yishmael. Rabbi Akiva, by contrast, holds that a {\i kohen} has an obligation to become {\i tamei} for his close relatives, regardless of competing considerations: it is a {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael: Presumption\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's reasoning proceeds from the structure of the relevant passage. The Torah begins with the general prohibition: {\i l\u8217'nefesh lo yitama b\u8217'amav} \u8212- a {\i kohen} shall not defile himself for any of his people. This establishes a baseline presumption that {\i tum\u8217'ah} is forbidden. Given this presumption, when the Torah carves out exceptions for close relatives, the natural reading is that it is granting permission \u8212- overriding the prohibition for those specific cases. The {\i pasuk}, on this reading, lifts the {\i issur} and grants {\i reshut}, but does not go further to create an affirmative obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva: Extra Phrase A\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva responds with a textual argument. Before it ever said {\i lo yitama}, the verse had already enumerated the relatives for whom the {\i kohen} may become {\i tamei}: {\i ki im l\u8217'she\u8217'ero hakrov eilav l\u8217'imo ul\u8217'aviv ul\u8217'vno ul\u8217'vito ul\u8217'achiv}. The list already established permission; it could then simply have said {\i ul\u8217'achoto} (and for his sister) without any additional verb. Why does it then say {\i lah yitama} \u8212- {\i to her} shall he become {\i tamei} \u8212- adding an explicit verbal form? That extra phrase, Rabbi Akiva argues, elevates permission to obligation: it is a {\i chova}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael: Body vs.\u160 ?Li\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's response is sharp: the word {\i lah} (to her) is not extra in the way Rabbi Akiva supposes \u8212- it is present to teach a specific and independent {\i halacha}: {\i lama tama ve-eino mitamei le-evareiha} \u8212- he may become {\i tamei} for her as a whole body, but not for her individual limbs. If a limb falls off during her lifetime, the {\i kohen} may not become {\i tamei} for the burial of that limb. Once the Torah needed to write {\i lah} to convey this distinction, it naturally wrote the associated verb {\i yitama} as well \u8212- not to teach an obligation, but simply because the phrase would read awkwardly without it. Rabbi Akiva presses further: if it was only {\i lah} that was needed, the Torah could have written {\i lah} alone. Why include the full verb {\i yitama}? Rabbi Yishmael replies by citing the principle of {\i parashat she-ne\u8217'emra ve-nishnita}: the phrase was already needed to convey the body-versus-limbs distinction, so the verb accompanying it carries no additional weight. The pattern of the debate is thus entirely consistent with the first case: Rabbi Akiva extracts obligation from what appears extra; Rabbi Yishmael explains that apparent surplus is structurally necessary for another purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case 3: Le\u8217'olam Bahem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is the most challenging from a values perspective. The verse {\i le\u8217'olam bahem ta\u8217'avodu} \u8212- \u8220"you shall work them forever\u8221" \u8212- refers to Canaanite slaves. Rabbi Yishmael reads it as a {\i reshut}: one is permitted to keep them enslaved permanently. Rabbi Akiva reads it as a {\i chova}: one is obligated to do so and is not permitted to free them. The {\i reshut}/{\i chova} distinction in the context of {\i sotah} already raises significant questions of competing values \u8212- the concern about addressing genuine wrongdoing on one hand, and the potential for injustice or strife on the other. But this third case is harder still. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position of {\i chova} seems to say not only that slavery is permitted but that it is mandated \u8212- that to free a Canaanite slave is {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Values: Slavery Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth dwelling on the values dimension here. A compelling and textually grounded reading of the Torah\u8217's treatment of slavery is not that the Torah endorses or celebrates the institution, but that it accepted the reality of a world in which slavery had existed for millennia, and sought to regulate and humanize it \u8212- to put Israel on a path toward something better. This reading gains support from the very structure of {\i Sefer Shemot}: immediately following the {\i Aseret ha-Dibrot}, with the declaration {\i anochi Hashem elokecha asher hotzeiticha me-eretz mitzrayim mi-beit avadim}, the first laws in {\i Mishpatim} concern the treatment of slaves. The Torah leads with those laws precisely because humanizing slavery was the immediate moral work of that moment. The {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} was also, in a sense, someone in process \u8212- not yet a full member of the Jewish people, but not entirely outside either. One might argue that precisely this liminal status would make freeing him and converting him {\i more} desirable, not less. Yet Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position, as understood in the {\i gemara}, is that freeing a Canaanite slave is {\i assur}. Other {\i gemaras} invoke {\i le\u8217'olam bahem ta\u8217'avodu} as the source for the ruling that {\i assur leshachrer avdo} \u8212- it is forbidden to free one\u8217's slave \u8212- though whether those sources specifically invoke Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position warrants independent examination. The {\i gemara} does note that there are circumstances in which freeing a slave is permitted when there is a specific purpose \u8212- such as completing a {\i minyan} \u8212- suggesting that even the {\i chova} position is not absolute in practice. Nevertheless, reconciling the {\i chova} reading with the view that the Torah is fundamentally opposed to the institution of slavery remains genuinely difficult.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Canaanite Slaves Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Canaanite vs.\u160 ?Non-Canaan\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses a crucial question: what is the reasoning behind Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's {\i reshut} position in the case of {\i le\u8217'olam bahem ta\u8217'avodu}? Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's reasoning is as follows: since the Torah elsewhere commands {\i lo techayeh kol neshamah} \u8212- do not leave any of the seven Canaanite nations alive \u8212- there would be a reason to think that enslaving a Canaanite is {\i assur}. One would be obligated to kill him, not enslave him. The verse {\i le\u8217'olam bahem ta\u8217'avodu} is therefore necessary to teach that a certain category of person \u8212- not a full Canaanite \u8212- may be enslaved rather than killed. Specifically, the {\i gemara} identifies this as the case of {\i echad mi-kol ha-umot she-ba al canaanit ve-holid mimena ben} \u8212- a man from a non-Canaanite nation who fathered a child with a Canaanite woman. The child of such a union may be purchased as a slave; it is not subject to the obligation of {\i lo techayeh kol neshamah}. The {\i pasuk} establishes permission for this case \u8212- {\i reshut} \u8212- but does not mandate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Patrilineal Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The determining factor in this analysis is national identity, which the {\i gemara} treats as patrilineal. In the general framework of Jewish law, tribal identity ({\i shevet}) is patrilineal \u8212- one\u8217's {\i shevet} follows the father. Jewishness itself follows the mother, which is an exception to an otherwise thoroughly patriarchal structure. But for purposes of national identity among the nations \u8212- and specifically for the category of the seven Canaanite peoples \u8212- identity follows the father. A father from a non-Canaanite nation, even if he lived among Canaanites and fathered children with a Canaanite woman, transmits his own national identity to his children. Those children are therefore not Canaanites and not subject to the mandate of {\i lo techayeh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} is cited in support: {\i minyan le-echad min ha-umot she-ba al canaanit ve-holid mimena ben she-ata rashai liknoto be-eved} \u8212- how do we know that a child born of a non-Canaanite father and a Canaanite mother may be purchased as a slave? The verse says {\i vegam mivnei hatoshavim hagarim imachem mehem tiknu} \u8212- from those who dwell among you as temporary residents, you may purchase. The father, being of another nation, is a temporary resident; his children may be acquired as slaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Parentage: Non-Can\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws the {\i halacha} specifically from the scenario of a non-Canaanite father and a Canaanite mother. Because national identity follows the father, the child in this case is not Canaanite \u8212- the father, though residing among Canaanites, belongs to another nation and is a temporary resident ({\i toshav}). The children he fathered are thus included in the category of {\i mivnei hatoshavim hagarim imachem} \u8212- the children of those temporarily settled among you \u8212- and may be purchased as slaves. The verse {\i mehem tiknu} establishes this as {\i reshut}: permission, not obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse: Canaanite Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What of the reverse scenario? Perhaps if a Canaanite father fathered a child with a non-Canaanite woman, that child \u8212- having a non-Canaanite mother \u8212- would also not be Canaanite, and could similarly be enslaved rather than killed? The {\i gemara} rejects this. The verse {\i asher holidu be\u8217'artzechem} \u8212- who were born in your land \u8212- refers to those born in the land but who do not truly {\i belong} there, in the sense that their father is from elsewhere. The underlying logic is that a person belongs to where their father belongs. If the father is Canaanite, he truly belongs in the land; he is not a {\i toshav} or temporary resident. His children, born there of a father who belongs there, are themselves truly settled \u8212- {\i hagarim be\u8217'artzechem} \u8212- and are not in the category of those who may be purchased. Thus, for both the permission to enslave and the exemption from {\i lo techayeh}, the determining factor is the national identity of the father.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Chisda: Home Destruc\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zenuta & Tukpa Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a statement of Rav Chisda that returns to the theme of {\i kina\u8217'ut} and its consequences, though from a different angle. Rav Chisda says: {\i zenuta be-beita ke-kira le-shumshamei} \u8212- adultery or sexual immorality in the home is like a worm in sesame seeds. Just as a worm silently destroys the seeds from within, so too does unchecked sexual sin rot away at the fabric of the household \u8212- not only through the gravity of the transgression itself, but through its corrosive effect on the {\i shalom bayit}. This statement thus provides a rationale for why a husband who has genuine cause for suspicion cannot simply ignore the situation in the name of domestic peace: the unaddressed wrong itself destroys that peace. Rav Chisda adds a second observation: {\i tukpa be-beita ke-kira le-shumshamei} \u8212- anger in the home is equally corrosive, like that same worm in the sesame. The implication is striking: both the sin itself and the jealousy or anger it generates are destructive to the home. One might think this creates an impossible bind \u8212- damned if you address it, damned if you do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Asymmetry Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces a qualification that is difficult to read without discomfort: {\i idei ve-idei be-itata} \u8212- both of these (the {\i zenuta} and the {\i tukpa}) apply when it is the {\i woman} committing the adultery or generating the anger, {\i aval be-gavra leit lan bah} \u8212- but if it is the man doing it, it does not carry the same consequence for the household. Rashi explains the distinction pragmatically: if the woman is engaged in an affair, she is not attending to the children, not managing the household; therefore, the practical fabric of the home disintegrates. If the man is unfaithful, he is, in Rashi\u8217's framing, rarely home anyway, and so the household functioning is not similarly disrupted. The {\i tukpa} parallel is presumably explained on the same basis: if the wife is angry, she will not cook, clean, or manage the home properly. If the husband is angry, the home\u8217's basic functioning is less immediately affected. Whatever one makes of this reasoning, it effectively places the analysis entirely within the framework of the woman\u8217's role and responsibility. The {\i gemara} does not address the moral equivalence of the sin; it focuses on the real-world impact on the household. The result, however, is that the analysis tends strongly toward the {\i mitzvah le-kanaot} position: since neither the man\u8217's anger nor his infidelity is treated as equally corrosive, the framing places the burden and the obligation squarely on the husband\u8217's act of addressing the wife\u8217's behavior.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechina Presence & Depa\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda continues with a further statement: {\i batchilah kodem shechatu Yisroel} \u8212- before Israel sinned (here referring specifically to sexual sin rather than the incident of the {\i egel}) \u8212- {\i haysa Shechina shora im kol echad ve\u8217'echad}. The divine presence rested with each individual \u8212- or, as Rashi reads it, within each household. The verse cited is {\i ki Hashem elokecha misalech b\u8217'kerev machanecha} \u8212- for God walks in the midst of your camp, present in the very midst of your dwelling place. {\i Keivan shechatu} \u8212- once they sinned sexually \u8212- {\i nistalka Shechina mehem}: the {\i Shechina} departed from them. The verse supporting this is {\i v\u8217'lo yireh becha ervat davar v\u8217'shav me\u8217'acharecha} \u8212- should God see a matter of indecency among you, He will recoil and distance Himself. When sexual sin or discord enters the home, the sanctity of the marriage is undermined and the divine presence can no longer dwell within it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aggadic: Mitzvah & Aveir\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvah\u8217's Path Forward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani said in the name of Rabbi Yonasan: {\i kol oseh mitzvah achas ba\u8217'olam hazeh makdimato v\u8217'holeches lefanav le\u8217'olam haba} \u8212- whoever performs even a single {\i mitzvah} in this world, that {\i mitzvah} goes before him and accompanies him into the World to Come. The verse cited is {\i v\u8217'halach lefanecha tzidkecha} \u8212- your righteousness will walk before you. The {\i mitzvah} precedes the person into {\i olam haba} and proclaims his merit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aveirah\u8217's Wrapping Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The counterpart is sobering: {\i v\u8217'kol ha\u8217'over aveirah achas ba\u8217'olam hazeh melafafto v\u8217'holeches lefanav le\u8217'yom hadin} \u8212- whoever commits even a single {\i aveirah} in this world, that sin wraps itself around him and walks before him to the {\i yom hadin}. Significantly, the {\i aveirah} is said to walk before him to the {\i day of judgment}, not to {\i olam haba} \u8212- because if it is there at the {\i yom hadin}, the person will not make it to {\i olam haba}. The sin clings to him at the moment of judgment and testifies against him. The verse cited is {\i yilafsu orchot darkam ya\u8217'alu batohu v\u8217'yovedu} \u8212- their paths will entangle themselves, they will rise into nothingness and be lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Dog Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer adds a vivid image: {\i keshura bo ke-kelev} \u8212- the {\i aveirah} ties itself to the person like a dog. The prooftext is taken from the encounter between Yosef and the wife of Potifar: {\i v\u8217'lo shama eleiha lishkav etzlah lihyot imah} \u8212- he did not listen to her to lie with her, to be with her. Rabbi Eliezer reads {\i lishkav etzlah} as referring to this world, and {\i lihyot imah} as referring to the future \u8212- had Yosef succumbed, that sin would have been with him always, clinging like a dog, following him into whatever came after. It is worth noting that while the earlier teaching of Rav Chisda focused predominantly on the woman\u8217's adultery and its effects on the household, this {\i aggadic} section shifts the focus: the language of {\i kol ha\u8217'over aveirah} addresses any person, and the story of Yosef is paradigmatically a story of a man who resisted sexual sin. The {\i gemara} thus broadens the consequences of sexual transgression beyond the gendered framing of the earlier teaching.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakha Transition: One\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions back to {\i halacha} with a formal {\i mishnah} reference: {\i tnan hasam}, \u8220"we learned elsewhere.\u8221" The topic is the evidentiary structure of the {\i sotah} procedure, specifically the question of how many witnesses are required at different stages. The {\i gemara} presents what initially appears to be a logical tension: if the first testimony \u8212- {\i kinuy} (the warning) and {\i stirah} (the seclusion) \u8212- which does not definitively forbid the wife, requires two witnesses, then by logic the final testimony, which does definitively forbid her forever, should certainly require two witnesses as well. Yet the Torah teaches that one witness suffices for the final testimony. How does one make sense of this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} articulates the evidentiary framework precisely. Two witnesses are required for {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} \u8212- the groundwork of the {\i sotah} procedure. These establish the conditions under which the wife must undergo the test of the {\i sotah} water. If she does not drink \u8212- or if something prevents it \u8212- she is divorced without her {\i ketubah} and loses the right to the {\i sotah} test. Now, one witness who testifies after {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} that she actually committed adultery is sufficient to make her definitively forbidden to her husband, to eliminate the option of the {\i sotah} water, and to cause her to be divorced without a {\i ketubah} \u8212- this despite the testimony being final and irreversible. The {\i chiddush} is that one witness suffices for this most consequential determination, specifically because the groundwork of {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} has already been laid by two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse cited is {\i v\u8217'ed ein bah} \u8212- and there is no witness against her. The logic of the {\i gemara} is: {\i kol she-yesh bah} \u8212- whenever there {\i is} a witness \u8212- even one \u8212- that is sufficient. This has been read in multiple ways earlier in the {\i masechet}, but here it yields the rule that a single witness after {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} finalizes the matter. Had there been two witnesses testifying to the actual act of adultery with prior warning ({\i hasra\u8217'ah}), she would have been subject to capital punishment. The {\i chiddush} of one witness is specifically operative in the {\i sotah} context following {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah}, not as a pathway to prosecution but as a pathway to final {\i halachic} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezerah Shavah: Ervat Da\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: from where is this derived? The {\i beraita} had cited {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} \u8212- \u8220"for he finds in her a matter of indecency\u8221" \u8212- and connected it via a {\i gezerah shavah} of the word {\i davar} to {\i al pi shenei edim o al pi sheloshah edim yakum davar} \u8212- \u8220"by the testimony of two or three witnesses shall a matter be established.\u8221" The {\i gezerah shavah} teaches that {\i ervat davar} \u8212- the \u8220"nakedness of a matter\u8221" relevant to {\i sotah} \u8212- is subject to the same requirement of two witnesses as the general rule of testimony. This is the source for requiring two witnesses for {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah}: the word {\i davar} in {\i ervat davar} is read through the {\i gezerah shavah} to import the standard of {\i shenei edim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness Absent Groun\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then poses a further question: we now understand why one witness suffices after {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} \u8212- the groundwork was laid by two. But how do we know that one witness who testifies to adultery {\i without} any prior {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} would not be believed at all? Why wouldn\u8217't such a witness suffice to establish something, even if not the full consequence of the {\i sotah} procedure? The answer comes from the same {\i gezerah shavah}: {\i ne\u8217'emar kan ki matza bah ervat davar v\u8217'ne\u8217'emar le-halan al pi shenei edim} \u8212- the word {\i davar} here and there links the {\i sotah} testimony to the general requirement of two witnesses. {\i Mah davar ha-amur le-halan edim shenayim af kan edim shenayim}: just as in the general case two witnesses are required to establish a matter, so too for testimony about adultery in general, two are required. It is only in the specific case where {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} have already been established by two witnesses that the Torah makes an exception and allows one witness to finalize matters \u8212- and this is derived from the word {\i bah} in {\i v\u8217'ed ein bah}: {\i bah v\u8217'lo b\u8217'kinnuy bah v\u8217'lo b\u8217'stirah} \u8212- it is specifically for the act of adultery itself that one witness suffices; for the preliminary stages of {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah}, two are always required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rishona & Achrona\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definitions: Rishona/Ach\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} provides formal definitions for the terms used in this evidentiary framework. {\i Edut rishona} \u8212- the \u8220"first testimony\u8221" \u8212- refers to {\i edut stirah}, testimony of seclusion, which encompasses both {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah}. {\i Edut achrona} \u8212- the \u8220"last testimony\u8221" \u8212- refers to {\i edut tumah}, testimony of actual adultery. These definitions align with the framework just analyzed: the \u8220"first\u8221" testimony requires two witnesses, the \u8220"last\u8221" requires only one when the \u8220"first\u8221" has already been properly established.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Measure of Stirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definitional Chain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i stirah} is significant because it creates the conditions for potential {\i tumah}, the {\i gemara} now asks: {\i ve-kama shiur stirah} \u8212- how long must the seclusion last to constitute legally significant {\i stirah}? The answer is given through a chain of definitional steps: the duration of {\i stirah} is {\i kedei tumah} \u8212- the time sufficient for {\i tumah} to occur. What is the time sufficient for {\i tumah}? {\i Kedei bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the time sufficient for intercourse to take place. What is the minimal definition of intercourse? {\i Kedei ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} \u8212- the time sufficient for {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ha\u8217'arah Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} is defined as the insertion of the {\i atarah} \u8212- the crown or tip \u8212- of the male organ, and not the completion of the full act of intercourse ({\i gmar bi\u8217'ah}). Halachically, {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} constitutes a legally significant sexual act even without full penetration. The {\i gemara} also notes \u8212- addressing what one might reasonably wonder \u8212- that this definition does not require an erection to have occurred beforehand. The assumption underlying all the time-measurements that follow is that the seduction has already taken place and the relevant clothing has already been removed (the {\i Yerushalmi} specifies that at minimum the belt has been undone). Given all that, the question of how much time the seclusion must last reduces to: how long does {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} itself take? This also has practical relevance beyond {\i sotah} \u8212- for instance, in determining the minimal duration of {\i yichud} after {\i chuppah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Competing Time Measures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael: Palm Tree W\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's measure for the duration of {\i stirah} \u8212- that is, the time sufficient for {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} \u8212- is {\i kedei hakafat dekel}: the time it takes to walk around a palm tree. The question of how long this is in absolute terms is not specified; it is evidently understood to be quite short, consistent with the minimal definition of {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Mixing Wine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer gives a different measure: {\i kedei mezigat hakos} \u8212- the time it takes to mix a cup of wine (diluting wine with water, as was the practice).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua: Drinking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua says: {\i kedei lishtoto} \u8212- the time it takes to drink the cup.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai: Frying Egg\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Azzai says: {\i kedei litzlot beitzah} \u8212- the time it takes to fry an egg.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva: Swallowing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva says: {\i kedei legomah} \u8212- the time it takes to swallow the egg just fried.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda ben Beteira: T\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira says: {\i kedei ligmoa shalosh beitzim zo achar zo} \u8212- the time it takes to swallow three eggs in sequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar: String Tying\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Yirmiyah says: {\i kedei likshor girdei nima} \u8212- the time it takes to tie the ends of a thread or string together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Chanin: Splinter Rem\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Chanin ben Pinchas says: {\i kedei she-toshit yadah le-toch piha litol kaisam} \u8212- the time it takes for her to insert her hand into her mouth and remove a splinter or sliver of wood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pelimo: Bread from Basket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pelimo says: {\i kedei she-toshit yadah la-sal litol kikar} \u8212- the time it takes for her to reach her hand into a basket and take out a loaf of bread. The {\i beraita} adds a scriptural allusion: {\i af al pi she-ein raya la-davar zecher la-davar}, {\i ki be\u8217'ad isha zona ad kikar lechem} \u8212- for the price of a harlot woman is even a loaf of bread. Though the full exegesis of this verse will be addressed later in the {\i masechet}, the association of a woman, sexual sin, and a loaf of bread makes the imagery of Pelimo\u8217's measure thematically apt.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Measure of Stirah (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enumeration Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why does the {\i beraita} enumerate the full chain \u8212- {\i stirah}, {\i tumah}, {\i bi\u8217'ah}, {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} \u8212- rather than simply stating {\i kedei ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} directly? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i tzrichei} \u8212- each step in the chain is necessary. Had the {\i beraita} said only {\i kedei tumah}, one might have thought it means the time for {\i tumah} including the preceding seduction \u8212- {\i kedei tumah ve-artzota}. By saying {\i kedei bi\u8217'ah}, it makes clear that the seduction is excluded, and we are measuring only the act itself. Had the {\i beraita} then said only {\i kedei bi\u8217'ah}, one might have thought it means the time for full intercourse \u8212- {\i kedei gmar bi\u8217'ah}. By saying {\i kedei ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah}, it specifies that even the minimum of {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} suffices. And had it said only {\i kedei ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} without the preceding steps, one might have thought it means {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} including the preceding desire or seduction. The phrase {\i kedei tumah} \u8212- derived from the {\i pasuk} {\i v\u8217'hi nitma\u8217'ah} \u8212- tells us that we measure only the act, not what came before it. Each step of the definitional chain thus functions to narrow and clarify the measure, ruling out broader readings at each stage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reconciliation of Measur\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakafa vs.\u160 ?Chazara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now notes a difficulty: a second {\i beraita} presents what appears to be a parallel set of positions, but the attributions are shifted by one \u8212- each {\i tanna} seems to say in this second source what the {\i tanna} one position later says in the first. Rather than simply acknowledging a dispute about who said what, the {\i gemara} attempts to reconcile all the positions as consistent. The first point of reconciliation concerns the opening measures. In the first {\i beraita}, Rabbi Yishmael said {\i kedei hakafat dekel} \u8212- the time to walk around a palm tree. In the second {\i beraita}, Rabbi Eliezer says {\i kedei chazarat dekel} \u8212- the time for the palm tree to return or sway back. The {\i gemara} initially assumes these are the same: {\i haynu hakafat dekel haynu chazarat dekel}. But Abaye corrects this: {\i hakafa beragel chazara beruach} \u8212- {\i hakafat dekel} means walking around the tree on foot, while {\i chazarat dekel} means the swaying of the tree blown by the wind and then returning to its upright position. These are two genuinely different actions and potentially different durations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi: Palm Return Ti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises a further ambiguity regarding {\i chazarat dekel}: when exactly does the palm tree finish its return? Is the relevant endpoint when the tree reaches its upright position again \u8212- {\i ki heichi de-azal ve-hadar asi} \u8212- or does it include the time the tree continues to sway gently after returning upright until it fully settles \u8212- {\i ki heichi de-azal ve-asi ve-hadar kai be-duchtei}? The {\i gemara} leaves this unresolved: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combining Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then reconciles Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's position across the two {\i baraitot}. In the first, Rabbi Eliezer said {\i kedei mezigat hakos}. In the second, Rabbi Yishmael says {\i kedei mezigat hakos}. How can both be right? They are simply the same position stated by different {\i tannaim} in different traditions. Moving to Rabbi Yehoshua: in the first {\i beraita} he said {\i kedei lishtoto} (drinking the cup), while in the second he said {\i kedei mezigat hakos} (mixing the cup). The {\i gemara} reconciles these: {\i eima kedei limzog velishtot} \u8212- combine the two; Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's full measure is the time to both mix and drink. One might ask why the {\i gemara} does not instead say that mixing and drinking are the same amount of time and thus the two {\i baraitot} are simply using different language for the same thing. The answer is that we have already assigned the single measure of {\i mezigat hakos} to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position (via the first {\i beraita}), so we cannot also make it equal to {\i lishtoto} and thereby conflate Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Frying & Swallowing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Azzai said {\i kedei litzlot beitzah} (frying an egg) in the first {\i beraita}, and {\i kedei lishtoto} (drinking the cup) in the second. The {\i gemara} resolves this by saying {\i idi ve-idi chad shiura hu} \u8212- frying an egg and drinking a cup take the same amount of time. For Rabbi Akiva: in the first {\i beraita} he said {\i kedei legomah} (swallowing), and in the second he said {\i kedei litzlot beitzah} (frying). The {\i gemara} reconciles: {\i eima kedei litzlot beitzah ve-ligmoa} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva\u8217's measure is the time to both fry and swallow. Again, one might ask why not say frying and swallowing are the same \u8212- but that would make Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position identical to Ben Azzai\u8217's, which we have already assigned. For Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira: in the first {\i beraita} he said swallowing three eggs, in the second he said swallowing one egg. The {\i gemara} explains that in the second {\i beraita}, Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira is responding to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's combined measure of frying-and-swallowing, telling him: you don\u8217't need both frying and swallowing to describe the measure \u8212- just say {\i kedei ligmoa shalosh beitzim}, swallowing three eggs, and that captures the same duration as frying one and swallowing it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Ambiguities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even after all these reconciliations, the {\i gemara} leaves two positions with unresolved internal ambiguities. Regarding Rabbi Elazar ben Yirmiyah\u8217's measure of tying string ends: Rav Ashi asks whether the string ends are close together ({\i d\u8217'mikrava}) or far apart ({\i d\u8217'mirchaka}), since the time to tie them would differ significantly. {\i Teiku} \u8212- unresolved. Regarding Ben Chanin ben Pinchas\u8217's measure of removing a splinter from one\u8217's mouth: Rav Ashi asks whether the splinter is tightly lodged ({\i d\u8217'mahadka}) or loose ({\i d\u8217'la mahadka}). {\i Teiku}. And for Pelimo\u8217's measure of reaching into a basket for a loaf of bread, Rav Ashi raises a cascade of variables: Is the loaf tightly packed in the basket or loose? Is it a new basket (with rough, gripping edges) or an old one? Is the bread warm or cold? Is it made of wheat flour or barley flour? Is it soft bread or hard? All {\i teiku} \u8212- none of these ambiguities are resolved. The multiplication of unresolved questions here is striking, given how carefully the {\i gemara} reconciled the other positions. The {\i gemara} simply acknowledges that the precise duration of these measures cannot be determined without knowing the relevant physical parameters.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Personal Measurement Bas\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal Measurement Pri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak bar Rabbi Yosef said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i kol echad ve\u8217'echad be\u8217'atzmo shi\u8217'er} \u8212- each of these {\i tannaim} measured the duration from his own experience. That is, each {\i tanna} reflected on how long a given activity took him personally, and offered that duration as his measure for {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah}. This is a remarkable observation: the entire array of differing positions is not a dispute about an abstract unit of time but a record of individual experience \u8212- each {\i rabbi} consulting, as it were, his own life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation immediately raises a well-known difficulty. Ben Azzai famously never married \u8212- or at least famously did not maintain a marriage. He explained that his love of Torah was so consuming that he could not devote himself to a wife. How, then, could he have measured {\i kedei litzlot beitzah} as the time for {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} from his own experience, as Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's principle would require?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers three possible resolutions. {\i Ibba\u8217'it eima nasiv u-ferash}: perhaps Ben Azzai did marry but subsequently separated from his wife \u8212- he left the marriage to devote himself to Torah study, and this tradition records that episode. {\i Ibba\u8217'it eima me-rabeih shemia leih}: alternatively, he heard this measure from his {\i rabbi} and reported it as authoritative tradition, without it being derived from personal experience. {\i Ve-ibba\u8217'it eima}: and if you prefer \u8212- {\i Torah hi ve-lilmod ani tzarich} \u8212- this is Torah and I must learn it; Ben Azzai was, in the well-known tradition, one of those who studied the most intimate dimensions of human life as pure Torah, possessing knowledge through sacred learning rather than personal encounter. Finally, {\i sod Hashem liyreiav} \u8212- the secret of God is with those who fear Him: perhaps Ben Azzai simply knew, in the manner of one whose fear of Heaven grants him a form of direct apprehension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 5\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bread & Prostitution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bread without washing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} continues from where we left off on 4b, where the {\i Gemara} had surveyed a range of opinions regarding the {\i shiur bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the minimum duration of the act of intercourse \u8212- and one of those opinions had invoked a metaphor involving reaching a hand into a basket of loaves of bread, citing a {\i pasuk} in the process. The {\i Gemara} now builds upon that {\i pasuk}, offering a series of {\i drashot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avira taught \u8212- sometimes transmitting the teaching in the name of Rav Ammi, and sometimes in the name of Rav Assi \u8212- that whoever eats bread without {\i netilat yadayim} is as if he had intercourse with a woman who is a prostitute ({\i zonah}), as it is stated (Mishlei 6:26): {\i ki be\u8217'ad ishah zonah ad kikar lachem} \u8212- \u8220"for on account of a woman who is a prostitute, a man is reduced to a loaf of bread.\u8221" The homiletic connection is drawn between one\u8217's treatment of a loaf of bread and the domain of sexuality: just as eating can be invested with {\i kedushah} \u8212- through handwashing, blessings, and intentionality \u8212- or can be reduced to mere self-gratification, so too with sexuality. The eating of bread with {\i kedushah} parallels sexuality within marriage, while eating without proper preparation parallels the indulgence of appetite without sanctity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's reread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, characteristically attentive to the precise wording of a {\i pasuk}, raises a grammatical objection to Rav Avira\u8217's reading. The verse opens with the {\i zonah} and concludes with the loaf of bread; if the intent were to compare the improper eating of bread to illicit relations, the verse should have been phrased in the reverse order. Rather, Rava reads the {\i pasuk} according to what is likely its {\i pshat}: {\i kol haba al ishah zonah lesof mevakesh kikar lachem} \u8212- whoever habitually consorts with a prostitute will in the end become impoverished and go begging for a loaf of bread. That is the natural and primary meaning of the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mayim rishonim/acharonim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whatever the precise interpretation of the {\i pasuk}, the underlying concern with {\i netilat yadayim} generates further discussion. Rav Zreika in the name of Rabbi Elazar states that whoever treats {\i netilat yadayim} lightly \u8212- {\i hamizalzel binetilat yadayim} \u8212- will be uprooted from the world. This is a striking formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiya bar Ashi in the name of Rav specifies the proper technique: for {\i mayim rishonim}, the first washing before eating bread, one must hold one\u8217's fingers upward; for {\i mayim acharonim}, the final washing after the meal, one must hold the fingers downward. A {\i baraita} confirms this: {\i hanotel yadav tzarich sheyagbi\u8217'ah yadav lemalah} \u8212- one who washes hands must raise them upward \u8212- lest the water flow below the wrist and then run back over the fingers, thereby rendering them {\i tamei} once again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic requires some background. The practice of washing hands before eating bread derives from the {\i kohanim}, who would eat {\i terumah} \u8212- bread made from {\i terumah} flour \u8212- and for whom ritual purity was therefore essential. The rabbis established that unwashed hands carry the status of a {\i sheni letumah}, a second-degree derivative of impurity, sufficient to render {\i terumah} {\i tamei}. Rather than requiring full immersion in a {\i mikvah} to purify the hands, the rabbis instituted the washing of hands as a means of restoring {\i taharah}. Ordinary Jews adopted this practice in parallel, treating their eating as if they were {\i kohanim} consuming {\i terumah}, thereby elevating the sanctity of their meals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanics of the washing require two pourings. The first water \u8212- {\i mayim rishonim} \u8212- purifies the hands, but in doing so becomes {\i tamei} itself; indeed, liquids that come into contact with impurity acquire a special status and themselves become {\i tamei}. The second water then purifies the first water. The practical concern is this: if during the first pouring the water flows past the wrist onto the forearm, it becomes {\i tamei} there, but the second pouring reaches only up to the wrist. The water sitting on the forearm has thus not been rendered {\i tahor} by the second pouring. If one then lowers the fingers, that {\i tamei} water may run back down onto the hands, rendering them {\i tamei} once more. Hence the requirement to keep the fingers elevated during {\i mayim rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that there is a dispute among the {\i rishonim} as to precisely which joint serves as the boundary \u8212- whether the relevant threshold is the wrist or the knuckle where the fingers attach to the palm. The normative practice is to ensure the entire hand is washed; in pressing circumstances, {\i besha\u8217'as hadechak}, one may rely on the more lenient position that requires only the fingers. In any case, the essential rule is clear: fingers up for {\i mayim rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the fingers must be held {\i downward} for {\i mayim acharonim} is different in nature. {\i Mayim acharonim} serve to remove {\i melach Sedomit} \u8212- a particularly harsh salt associated with Sodom \u8212- and other unpleasant residues from the fingers. The purpose is to push the undesirable material away from oneself, hence the fingers point downward so that the water and residue drain away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drying hands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abahu adds a further dimension: {\i kol ha\u8217'ochel pat belo niguv yadayim ke\u8217'ilu ochel lechem tamei} \u8212- whoever eats bread without drying one\u8217's hands afterward is as if eating {\i tamei} bread, as the verse states (Yechezkel 4:13): {\i kacha yochlu venei Yisrael et lachmam tamei} \u8212- \u8220"so shall the children of Israel eat their bread in a state of impurity.\u8221" The hands at that point are technically {\i tahor}, but Rashi explains that the act is {\i ma\u8217'us} \u8212- disgusting \u8212- and therefore treated rhetorically as equivalent to ritual impurity. There is something instructive here about the dual register of religious motivation: for some, the sense of ritual significance ({\i tamei}) is more compelling; for others, it is the aesthetic and moral dimension ({\i ma\u8217'us}) that resonates.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gasut haruach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haughty soul & adultery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the same {\i pasuk} in Mishlei (6:26) and focuses on its concluding phrase: {\i ve\u8217'eshet ish nefesh yekarah tatzud} \u8212- \u8220"and a married woman ensnares a precious soul.\u8221" Rabbi Chiya bar Abba said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i kol adam sheyesh bo gasut haruach lesof nichshal be\u8217'eshet ish} \u8212- whoever possesses haughtiness of spirit will ultimately stumble in the sin of adultery with a married woman, because the very haughtiness that leads one to feel entitled to anything will lead one to transgress even in the domain of another man\u8217's wife. The {\i pasuk} is read as: a {\i nefesh yekarah}, a precious soul \u8212- understood here as a haughty soul, one that considers itself supremely important \u8212- will be \u8220"captured\u8221" by a married woman. This resonates with the narrative at the end of {\i Parashat Bereishit}, where the {\i benei Elokim} seized {\i benot ha\u8217'adam} as they pleased \u8212- taking married women directly from the {\i chuppah} \u8212- reflecting a complete breakdown of boundaries born of a sense of absolute entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, again scrutinizes the {\i pasuk}. If the intent is to speak of a haughty soul, the verse should have said {\i nefesh gevoha}, a high soul, rather than {\i nefesh yekarah}, a precious soul. Furthermore, if the married woman is to be the active subject ensnaring someone, the verse should have read {\i hi tatzud} \u8212- \u8220"she will ensnare\u8221" \u8212- making the married woman the grammatical subject. Since the verse does not align with the desired reading, Rava offers an alternative: {\i kol haba al eshet ish} \u8212- whoever has intercourse with a married woman \u8212- {\i afilu lamad Torah} \u8212- even if he is a {\i nefesh yekarah}, a precious soul, who has learned {\i Torah}, which is described as more precious than jewels ({\i yekarim mipninim}) and whose bearer is even greater than a {\i Kohen Gadol} who enters the innermost chambers \u8212- {\i hi titzudennu ledina shel Gehennom} \u8212- the married woman\u8217's sin will ensnare him all the way to the judgment of {\i Gehennom}. Here it is the {\i sin}, not the woman, that ensnares; the sinner is not ensnaring her but is himself being drawn in to the gravest punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haughtiness = idolatry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because the topic of {\i gasut haruach} \u8212- haughtiness of spirit \u8212- has now been introduced, the {\i Gemara} embarks on an extended exposition of its gravity. It is worth pausing to note that the term is {\i gasut ruach}, a heaviness or thickness of spirit \u8212- and one cannot help but observe the resonance with the {\i pasuk} at the heart of {\i Sotah}: {\i ve\u8217'avar alav ruach kinah}, a spirit of jealousy came over him. This juxtaposition invites reflection on whether the spirit of jealousy driving the husband may itself be entangled with a spirit of haughtiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan said in the name of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: {\i kol adam sheyesh bo gasut ruach ke\u8217'ilu oved avodat kochavim} \u8212- whoever has haughtiness of spirit is as if he worshipped {\i avodah zarah}. The one who is haughty recognizes no authority above themselves and is effectively a law unto themselves. The connection is drawn through the shared term {\i toavah}: the verse states {\i toavat Hashem kol geva lev} (Mishlei 16:5) \u8212- an abomination to God is all who are of haughty heart \u8212- and separately the verse states {\i ve\u8217'lo tavi toavah el beitecha} (Devarim 7:26) \u8212- do not bring an abomination into your home \u8212- referring to {\i avodah zarah}. The same term {\i toavah} links them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haughtiness = denial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan himself, speaking in his own name rather than in the name of his teacher, goes further: {\i ke\u8217'ilu kafar be\u8217'ikar} \u8212- it is as if one denied the very existence of God. The verse cited is {\i ve\u8217'ram levavecha ve\u8217'shachachta et Hashem Elokecha} (Devarim 8:14) \u8212- \u8220"your heart will be lifted up and you will forget the Lord your God.\u8221" There is a subtle distinction in the progression here. Worshipping {\i avodah zarah} involves rejecting one authority in favor of another; outright {\i kefira} is the rejection of divine authority altogether, with oneself as the sole authority. Haughtiness, Rabbi Yochanan argues, belongs to this latter and more radical category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences of pride\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with further characterizations of haughtiness. Rabbi Chama bar Chanina says it is as if one had relations with all the {\i arayot}, all the forbidden sexual relations: {\i ke\u8217'ilu ba al kol ha\u8217'arayot}. The shared word {\i toavah} links the haughty heart ({\i toavat Hashem kol geva lev}) with the entire category of {\i arayot}, which is collectively described as {\i kol ha\u8217'toavot eleh} (Vayikra 18:27). The logic is that the unrestrained ego will ultimately give free rein to all desires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla offers yet another formulation: {\i ke\u8217'ilu bana bamah} \u8212- it is as if he built a {\i bamah}, a private altar outside the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. The verse cited is {\i chadlu lachem min ha\u8217'adam asher neshamah be\u8217'apo ki bameh nechshav} (Yeshayahu 2:22) \u8212- \u8220"cease from the man whose breath is in his nostrils, for what is he worth?\u8221" The homiletic reading takes {\i bameh nechshav} \u8212- \u8220"what is he worth?\u8221" \u8212- and vocalizes it as {\i bamah}, reading it as: such a person {\i is} a {\i bamah}. Rashi notes that a {\i bamah} was characteristically built on elevated ground, on high mountains and prominent places \u8212- an image perfectly fitting of one who elevates himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This comparison to a {\i bamah} is instructive in the way it complements the two previous comparisons. {\i Avodah zarah} represents a total substitution of divine authority with another. {\i Kefira} is an outright rejection of God. But a person who is haughty may still be observant and God-fearing. Such a person is not worshipping another god and is not denying God; yet they are nonetheless a law unto themselves, bypassing communal norms and institutions. That is the {\i bamah}: still worshipping God, perhaps, but doing so on one\u8217's own terms, outside the sanctioned framework \u8212- as though saying, everyone else goes to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, but I will do it my own way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Charity despite pride\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then takes up the phrase from the same {\i pasuk} that introduced the abomination of the haughty heart: {\i yad leyad lo yinakeh} \u8212- \u8220"hand to hand will not go unpunished\u8221" (Mishlei 11:21). The question is what \u8220"hand to hand\u8221" alludes to in relation to haughtiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav interprets it as referring to intercourse with a married woman \u8212- though the {\i Bach} notes that the correct reading, as attested by Rashi and others, is that the subject remains the haughty person. Even if such a person has recognized God\u8217's mastery over heaven and earth, as Avraham Avinu declared {\i harimosi yadi el Hashem El Elyon koneh shamayim va\u8217'aretz} \u8212- \u8220"I have lifted my hand to God, Master of heaven and earth\u8221" \u8212- {\i lo yinakeh midinah shel Gehennom}, he will not be cleansed from the punishment of {\i Gehennom}. The haughty person may sincerely affirm that God is the ultimate authority, and yet the practical effect of his haughtiness \u8212- his sense of entitlement and the way he treats others \u8212- remains a grave transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Devei Rabi Sheila} objects to this reading: if the verse intends to allude to Avraham\u8217's declaration, it should have said {\i yadi} rather than {\i yad leyad}, echoing {\i harimosi yadi}. They therefore offer an alternative: even if such a person received {\i Torah} at the level of Moshe Rabbeinu, of whom it is written {\i miyimino esh dat lamo} \u8212- the fiery law was given from God\u8217's right hand \u8212- {\i lo yinakeh midinah shel Gehennom}. Even the greatest {\i Torah} scholarship does not neutralize the haughtiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan in turn objects to {\i devei Rabi Sheila}\u8217's reading: if the verse meant to allude to God giving the {\i Torah} to Moshe\u8217's hand, it should have said {\i yad miyad} \u8212- from one hand to another \u8212- not {\i yad leyad}. Rather, Rabbi Yochanan interprets {\i yad leyad} as referring to the act of giving: hand extended to another\u8217's hand \u8212- that is, {\i tzedakah}. Even if such a person performs {\i tzedakah baseser} \u8212- gives charity anonymously, in a way that preserves the recipient\u8217's dignity, with no public recognition \u8212- and even though we know from the verse {\i matan baseser yichaper af} (Mishlei 21:14) that giving in secret appeases divine anger \u8212- nevertheless, because of the haughtiness, {\i lo yinakeh midinah shel Gehennom}. The sin of haughtiness is not neutralized even by this. The cumulative force of these three readings is striking: a haughty person may acknowledge God as Master of all, may have acquired enormous {\i Torah} knowledge, and may give {\i tzedakah} with complete anonymity and discretion \u8212- and yet none of this is sufficient to offset the fundamental wrong of haughtiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prohibition source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i azhara legasut haruach minayin} \u8212- what is the source of a prohibition against haughtiness in the {\i Torah}? Rava, or some say Rabbi Zeira, cites the verse {\i shimu veha\u8217'azinu al tigbahu} (Yirmiyahu 13:15) \u8212- \u8220"hear and give ear, do not be haughty.\u8221" Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak cites instead the verse in Devarim: {\i ve\u8217'ram levavecha ve\u8217'shachachta et Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- \u8220"your heart will become lifted and you will forget God\u8221" \u8212- in conjunction with {\i hishamer lecha pen tishkach et Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- \u8220"guard yourself lest you forget the Lord your God.\u8221" Rabbi Avin, transmitting from Rabbi Ila, establishes the principle that wherever the {\i Torah} uses the language of {\i hishamer}, {\i pen}, or {\i al}, these constitute negative prohibitions \u8212- {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}. Since the verse warns {\i hishamer lecha pen tishkach}, and since allowing one\u8217's heart to grow proud leads directly to forgetting God, the prohibition against forgetting God encompasses within it a prohibition against the pride that causes it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Consequences & rewards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Diminishment & repentance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avira taught \u8212- again sometimes in the name of Rav Assi and sometimes in the name of Rav Ammi \u8212- that whoever possesses haughtiness will in the end be diminished: {\i kol adam sheyesh bo gasut haruach lisof mitma\u8217'et}. The verse cited is {\i romu me\u8217'at} \u8212- \u8220"lift up a little\u8221" \u8212- read homiletically as: if you lift yourself up, you will become {\i me\u8217'at}, diminished. And lest one think the consequence is merely a slight reduction in stature while one continues to exist and have an impact in the world, the verse continues {\i ve\u8217'einenu} \u8212- and he is no more \u8212- meaning his memory will be obliterated entirely, as if he had never existed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the person returns in {\i teshuva}, {\i ne\u8217'esaf bizmano ke\u8217'Avraham Avinu} \u8212- he will be gathered in his time, that is, he will live a full life, like Avraham Avinu. This suggests that the punishment for unrepented haughtiness is not merely diminishment but premature death, since the alternative to repentance is dying before one\u8217's time. The verse concludes {\i vehumchu kechol yikfatzun} \u8212- they will be brought low and gathered in like all \u8212- and if one does make oneself humble through {\i teshuva}, they will be gathered in like {\i bakol mikol kol}: like Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov, each of whom the {\i Torah} describes with that phrase, indicating a life of complete fullness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head of husk debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse under discussion concludes: {\i uchrosh shibolet yimalu} \u8212- \u8220"like the head of the husk they will wither.\u8221" Rav Huna and Rav Chisda debate what this image means. One says it refers to the {\i kisa d\u8217'shivalta} \u8212- the hairs that crown the top of the husk of wheat, the thin tendrils that extend upward. The other says it refers to the {\i shibolet atzmah} \u8212- the husk itself, and its top specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If it refers to the hairs at the crown, the verse is straightforward: just as those hairs are cut away, so too will the haughty be cut down. The objection arises for the one who says it refers to the husk itself: what then does {\i kerosh} \u8212- the head \u8212- add to the image? Rav Assi, and likewise the school of Rabbi Yishmael, offer a parable: a person entering his field will pick the tallest stalks first. The tallest ones are gathered first and removed. So too the one who raises himself highest draws attention and is cut down first. The first image \u8212- the hairs being snipped from the crown \u8212- is perhaps more vivid, but both convey the same essential point: it is precisely what is most elevated that is most vulnerable to being cut down.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God with lowly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} moves to a {\i pasuk} in Yeshayahu (57:15) that speaks of God dwelling {\i et daka ushefal ruach} \u8212- with the downtrodden and the lowly of spirit. Again Rav Huna and Rav Chisda disagree. One interprets the verse as: {\i iti daka} \u8212- God says \u8220"bring the downtrodden to me; I will raise them up.\u8221" The other interprets it as: {\i ani et daka} \u8212- \u8220"I will go down and be with the downtrodden where they are.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} concludes that the second interpretation is the more compelling, {\i umistavra}, because we know that God passed over all the great mountains and rested the {\i Shechinah} on {\i Har Sinai} \u8212- the lowest of the mountains \u8212- and did not raise {\i Har Sinai} up to the level of the others but came down to where it was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not merely a logical point but a deeply pastoral one. When a person is in a state of bleakness, depression, or despair, the message that God will {\i eventually} lift them up may feel irrelevant or unbelievable. What is needed \u8212- and what the {\i Gemara} is affirming \u8212- is the knowledge that God is present {\i there}, in the darkness, in the lowliness itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning from God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef draws a practical lesson: {\i le\u8217'olam yilmad adam mida\u8217'as kono} \u8212- a person should always learn from the conduct of his Creator. Just as God passed over all the great mountains and chose to rest the {\i Shechinah} on the lowly {\i Har Sinai}, so too a person should cultivate humility. A variant of this teaching adds: just as God rested the {\i Shechinah} not in grand trees but in the humble thorn bush ({\i sneh}), so too human beings should model themselves on this divine preference for the lowly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deserve cutting down\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar states: {\i kol adam sheyesh bo gasut haruach ra\u8217'uy legado ke\u8217'asheirah} \u8212- whoever has haughtiness of spirit is deserving of being cut down like an {\i asheirah}, an idolatrous tree. The verse states {\i veramei hakoma gedu\u8217'im} (Yeshayahu 10:33) \u8212- \u8220"the tall ones will be cut down\u8221" \u8212- and the verse regarding {\i asherot} states {\i va\u8217'asheihem tigde\u8217'un} (Devarim 7:5) \u8212- \u8220"their {\i asherot} you shall chop down.\u8221" The combination of the two verses, sharing the root {\i gimel-dalet-ayin}, associates the fate of the haughty with the command to destroy idolatrous trees. The language is stark. One might note that the verse about {\i asherot} carries the force of a commandment \u8212- {\i you shall cut them down} \u8212- which is somewhat unsettling when applied to haughty people, and the practical implications of such rhetoric require care in interpretation. But at minimum, the {\i Gemara} is saying that the haughty are in their spiritual character akin to something that is an abomination and fit to be eliminated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar adds a second teaching: {\i kol adam sheyesh bo gasut haruach ein afro ninar} \u8212- whoever has haughtiness of spirit, his dust will not be stirred at the time of {\i techiyat hameitim}. The verse states (Yeshayahu 26:19): {\i hakitzu veranenu shochnei afar} \u8212- \u8220"awaken and rejoice, those who dwell among the dust.\u8221" The verse does not say {\i shochvei be\u8217'afar} \u8212- those who have lain down in the dust, which would refer to all the dead \u8212- but {\i shochnei afar} \u8212- those who have made their dwelling among the dust, meaning those who were humble and low in their lifetimes. Only they will be roused; the haughty, who disdained such lowliness in life, will not merit {\i techiyat hameitim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God cannot dwell\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda, or some say Mar Ukva, states: {\i kol adam sheyesh bo gasut haruach amar Hakadosh Baruch Hu: ein ani vehu yecholim lador ba\u8217'olam} \u8212- God says of the haughty: \u8220"I and he cannot dwell in the same world.\u8221" If the haughty person fills all the space and acknowledges no authority, there is no room for God. The verse states (Tehillim 101:5): {\i malshni baseser re\u8217'ehu oto atzmit; gevah einayim urechav levav oto lo uchal} \u8212- \u8220"one who slanders his friend in secret I will cut down; one of haughty eyes and broad heart, him I cannot endure.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} reads the final phrase not as {\i oto lo uchal} \u8212- \u8220"him I cannot endure\u8221" \u8212- but as {\i ito lo uchal} \u8212- \u8220"with him I cannot be.\u8221" God and the haughty cannot coexist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that some teach this statement in the context of those who speak {\i lashon hara}, since the first half of the verse concerns slander; on this reading, it may be the {\i lashon hara} born of haughtiness \u8212- rather than the haughtiness itself \u8212- that is the primary focus of the verse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Extended homilies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Little wind disturbs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Alexandri teaches: {\i kol adam sheyesh bo gasut haruach afilu ruach kima ocharto} \u8212- whoever has haughtiness, even a small wind will disturb him. The haughty person is hypersensitive to any perceived slight, constantly alert to whether others are sufficiently recognizing their status and authority. The verse states (Yeshayahu 57:20): {\i vehareshaim kayam nigrash} \u8212- \u8220"and the wicked are like the driven sea.\u8221" The comparison: the sea contains vast quantities of water, and yet even a small wind disturbs its surface. {\i Adam she\u8217'ein bo ela revit} \u8212- a human being has only a {\i revi\u8217'it} of blood, which is the {\i Gemara}\u8217's understanding of the minimal vital fluid \u8212- and yet the haughty person, driven by {\i gasut haruach}, is as easily agitated as the ocean. The {\i reshaim} here are those identified with haughtiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmid chacham arrogance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Ashi in the name of Rav states: {\i talmid chacham tzarich sheyehe bo echad mishmini shebishminit} \u8212- a {\i talmid chacham} requires within himself one sixty-fourth of haughtiness. A {\i talmid chacham} represents the {\i Torah} and carries its dignity. If someone slights him, they are slighting not merely a private individual but the {\i Torah} he embodies. Therefore a minimal measure of self-respect \u8212- a derivative of a derivative, an eighth of an eighth \u8212- is appropriate. The formulation is deliberately attenuated: not one-eighth, but one sixty-fourth, as though to say that even this is only barely tolerable and only at the greatest remove from real {\i gaivah}. Yet the {\i Gemara} has also already stated that even one who received {\i Torah} at the level of Moshe will not be cleansed from {\i Gehennom} if he has haughtiness. {\i Torah} scholarship can become a source of precisely this dangerous pride \u8212- the same thing it is meant to guard against.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crowns like husk hairs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua adds: {\i uma\u8217'atra lei ke-sasa l\u8217'shibulta} \u8212- and this minimal amount of pride crowns the {\i talmid chacham} like the hairs on top of the husk of wheat. This, of course, deliberately echoes the earlier image of those same hairs being cut off as a punishment for haughtiness. The image is inherently ambivalent: the very thing that adorns can, in excess, become the thing that is severed. A tiny amount is a crown; any more and it becomes a liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava concludes that this is an impossibly fine line: {\i beshamta de\u8217'is bei uv\u8217'shamta de\u8217'leis bei} \u8212- a {\i talmid chacham} is deserving of {\i cherem} if he has [too much] pride, and is deserving of {\i cherem} if he does not have [any]. Too much, and the {\i Torah} has made him insufferable and contemptuous of others; too little, and he fails to uphold the dignity of the {\i Torah} he represents. The balance is real, delicate, and in practice extraordinarily difficult to maintain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Total rejection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak cuts through the complexity with a unilateral ruling: {\i lo minah velo miktzatah} \u8212- I want none of it, not a drop, not even the smallest fraction. His reasoning: is it a trivial matter when the verse states {\i toavat Hashem kol geva lev} \u8212- an abomination to God is all who are of a haughty heart? If the {\i Torah} calls it an abomination, one must keep far away from it entirely. The tension between Rav Nachman\u8217's total rejection and the recognized need for a {\i talmid chacham} to maintain some measure of dignity is left standing. The {\i Gemara} does not resolve it so much as present the full range of the challenge. A well-worn {\i yeshiva} joke captures it: the {\i Gemara} says a {\i talmid chacham} should have one sixty-fourth of arrogance, but some seem to be very {\i machmir} on the {\i shiur}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Value of humility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heart like flesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Chizkiyah teaches: {\i ein tefilaso shel adam nishma\u8217'at ela im kein mesim libo k\u8217'vasar} \u8212- a person\u8217's prayer is heard only if he makes his heart like flesh ({\i basar}) \u8212- not like stone, not like a haughty elevated spirit, but soft, flexible, impressionable. The verse cited is (Yeshayahu 66:23): {\i vehayah midei chodesh bechodsho yavo kol basar lehishtachavot} \u8212- \u8220"all flesh shall come to bow down.\u8221" It is {\i basar} \u8212- flesh \u8212- that comes to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} to prostrate. The one who approaches God must approach as {\i basar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flesh vs.\u160 ?adam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira observes a distinction within the laws of {\i tzara\u8217'at}. When {\i tzara\u8217'at} appears on {\i basar} \u8212- on flesh \u8212- the verse indicates {\i venerpa}, it will heal. When the verse speaks of {\i adam} \u8212- a person \u8212- who has {\i tzara\u8217'at}, this phrase of healing does not appear. The point is not that {\i tzara\u8217'at} on a person cannot clear up, but that one who thinks of himself primarily as {\i adam} \u8212- a person of stature, a human being in his full dignity \u8212- finds healing and change more difficult. The one who regards himself as {\i basar} \u8212- humble flesh \u8212- is more open to the possibility of healing and transformation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adam & basar acronyms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan expands this further, offering {\i gematric} and acronymic readings of both words as a reminder of human frailty. {\i Adam} may be read as an acronym for {\i eifer, dam, marah} \u8212- ashes, blood, and bile. {\i Basar} may be read as {\i bushah, sruchah, rimah} \u8212- shame, putrefaction, and worms. An alternative tradition reads the middle letter as {\i shin} rather than {\i sin}, yielding {\i Sheol} \u8212- the netherworld \u8212- in place of {\i srucha}. These readings are not intended to produce despair but to provide an antidote to haughtiness: whatever one\u8217's achievements, the body decomposes into these elements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Se\u8217'et & sapachat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi applies this to haughtiness directly: {\i kol adam sheyesh bo gasut haruach lisof nifchat} \u8212- whoever has haughtiness will in the end be brought low. The verse cited is {\i ulaseit velasfachat} (Vayikra 13:2), referring to types of {\i tzara\u8217'at}. The {\i derasha} reads {\i se\u8217'eit} as related to the language of elevation \u8212- {\i se\u8217'eit} means lifting up, as in {\i ve\u8217'al kol heharim haramim ve\u8217'al kol hageva\u8217'ot hanisa\u8217'ot} \u8212- and {\i sapachat} as something secondary and adhesive, something that clings to and is subordinate to another. The haughty person who is {\i se\u8217'eit} \u8212- elevated \u8212- will be brought down to the status of {\i sapachat} \u8212- a mere addendum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Greatness of lowly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi teaches: {\i bo ur\u8217'ei kama gdolim nemuchei haruach lifnei Hakadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- come and see how great are those of lowly spirit before God. When the {\i Beit HaMikdash} stood, one who brought an {\i olah} received the merit of an {\i olah}; one who brought a {\i minchah} received the merit of a {\i minchah}. But one who possessed a lowly spirit, it is as if he brought all the sacrifices together. The lowly spirit surpasses any individual sacrifice that one could bring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Broken spirit greatest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse confirms this: {\i zivchei Elokim ruach nishbarah} (Tehillim 51:19) \u8212- \u8220"the sacrifices of God are a broken spirit.\u8221" The plural {\i zivchei} \u8212- sacrifices \u8212- refers to all sacrifices collectively, and the broken spirit encompasses them all. And furthermore, {\i ein tefilato nima\u8217'eset} \u8212- his prayer will not be despised, as the verse concludes: {\i lev nishbar venidkeh Elokim lo tivzeh} \u8212- \u8220"a broken and crushed heart, God will not despise.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi adds one final teaching: {\i kol hasham orchotav ba\u8217'olam hazeh zocheh vero\u8217'eh bishu\u8217'ato shel Hakadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- whoever weighs and assesses his paths in this world will merit seeing God\u8217's salvation. The {\i Rambam} cites this teaching as foundational, grounding in it the principle that all one\u8217's actions should be directed and evaluated {\i leshem shamayim}. The word {\i vesham} in the verse (Tehillim 50:23) is read not as \u8220"there\u8221" but as a form of {\i shuma} \u8212- assessment and evaluation. Whoever consistently weighs his direction in this world will behold the salvation of God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This extended passage on pride and humility is not a mere digression. The {\i ruach} \u8212- spirit \u8212- that animates {\i gasut haruach} stands in pointed juxtaposition to the {\i ruach kinah} \u8212- spirit of jealousy \u8212- with which {\i masechet Sotah} opens. The question of what kind of {\i ruach} is driving the jealous husband is one the {\i Gemara} seems deliberately to be placing before us.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinui & forbidden acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Warning contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the {\i halacha} of {\i Sotah} and the mechanics of the {\i kinui} \u8212- the formal warning that a husband must give his wife. The process has been substantially legalized and limited by {\i Chazal}: witnesses, {\i beit din}, and a carefully defined sequence of acts are required. The {\i Gemara} addresses an apparent internal contradiction in the {\i Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states that the husband warns his wife in front of two witnesses not to speak with a particular man \u8212- {\i al tedabri im ish ploni zeh}. The implication would seem to be that {\i dibbur}, speech, constitutes the legally significant act, equivalent to {\i stirah}, seclusion. Yet the {\i Mishnah} immediately thereafter states that if she spoke with the man she remains {\i muteret le-veitah} \u8212- permitted to her husband \u8212- and {\i muteret le\u8217'echol bitrumah} \u8212- permitted to eat {\i terumah} if her husband is a {\i kohen}. The internal contradiction is stark: the first clause implies that speaking is the relevant act, while the second clause implies that it is not. {\i Ha gupa kashya} \u8212- this passage is self-contradictory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the contradiction by explaining how the {\i Mishnah} must be read. The {\i Mishnah} is enumerating several scenarios in which the woman remains permitted: whether he warned her not to speak and she spoke; whether he warned her not to speak and she went into {\i seclusion} ({\i nistera}); whether he warned her not to go into seclusion and she spoke \u8212- in all these cases, {\i adayin muteret le-veitah muteret le\u8217'echol bitrumah}. She remains permitted in all of them, because in each case either the warning does not match the act, or the act alone is insufficient. The warning and the forbidden act must align: the warning must be against the act that generates the legal consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final forbidden state\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The one case in which she becomes forbidden is specified: {\i nichnesa imo leveit haseser veshahat kedei tumah} \u8212- she entered with him into a private place and remained there for sufficient time to have intercourse ({\i kedei tumah}) \u8212- after having been warned specifically against seclusion. In that case alone, {\i asura le-veitah va\u8217'asura le\u8217'echol bitrumah} \u8212- she is forbidden to her husband and forbidden to eat {\i terumah}. The warning must be against seclusion, the act must be seclusion, and there must be sufficient time. All three elements together constitute the forbidden state. Here the {\i daf} concludes, and the shiur will continue from {\i amud bet} at the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 6\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sotah Status & Yibum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Suspected Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin on 6b, approximately ten lines from the bottom, with the words {\i umet choletzet}. The subject is an interesting application of {\i yibum} law \u8212- a topic familiar to those who have studied Maseches Yevamos \u8212- arising from the case of the {\i sotah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To review the basic framework: a woman who has been warned ({\i kinui}) and subsequently secluded herself with the man against whom she was warned now carries the status of a {\i sotah}. Halakhically, she is a {\i safek} \u8212- a doubt \u8212- but she is treated as a {\i vaday}, a certainty, because the language of the {\i Torah} declares {\i venistarah vehi nitma\u8217'ah}. Based on the evidence accumulated at this stage, the {\i Torah} treats her as though {\i tumah} has been established, even though the ultimate clarification awaits the ritual at the {\i Beit HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of this {\i safek}-treated-as-{\i vaday} status is that she is forbidden from having marital relations with her husband, and her husband is forbidden from having relations with her. Furthermore, if she is married to a {\i kohen}, she may not eat {\i terumah}. There is something deeper here than a mere technical prohibition: the bond of marriage has been, in some sense, weakened or compromised, which is why the {\i Torah}\u8217's declaration of {\i v\u8217'nitma\u8217'ah} reaches beyond the bedroom and touches even her right to eat {\i terumah}. This will be developed further when we encounter the word {\i v\u8217'nitma\u8217'ah} appearing multiple times in the relevant {\i pesukim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Yibum Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this background, the {\i Mishnah} teaches that if the husband dies before the {\i sotah}\u8217's status has been clarified \u8212- before she has had the opportunity to drink the bitter waters \u8212- the brother, in a case where the couple has no children, may not perform {\i yibum}. The reasoning is almost self-evident: {\i yibum} is an act of sexual intimacy, it establishes a sexual relationship with this woman, and it does so precisely because the brother steps into the shoes of the deceased husband. But the husband himself was forbidden from having relations with her. How, then, could the {\i Torah} mandate the brother to do what the husband could not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, because the matter remains a {\i safek} \u8212- she has not been definitively proven guilty \u8212- she is not simply released. The brother must still perform {\i chalitzah} to sever the {\i zika}, the levirate bond that has transferred to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder: what if the man against whom she was warned and with whom she secluded herself was, in fact, the brother? In that case, the calculus would shift \u8212- but that is a separate discussion. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's case is the standard one, and the {\i Gemara} now asks the obvious opening question: {\i amay}? Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peshat Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} first asks: why indeed can the brother not perform {\i yibum}? And then: if he cannot perform {\i yibum}, why must {\i chalitzah} be done at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef provides a scriptural basis: {\i v\u8217'yatz\u8217'ah mibeito v\u8217'halchah v\u8217'haytah l\u8217'ish acher} \u8212- \u8220"she shall leave his house and go to be with another man\u8221" (Devarim 24:2). This verse describes a woman who is divorced in a context of {\i matza bah ervat davar}, the discovery of some act of nakedness. The {\i drasha} extends the verse: we read it as applying not only to divorce but to any scenario in which a {\i sotah} is no longer with her first husband \u8212- whether through divorce or death \u8212- and the phrase {\i l\u8217'ish acher}, \u8220"to another man,\u8221" is expounded as {\i ish acher v\u8217'lo yavam}: she may go to a completely different man, but not to the {\i yavam}, the brother-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a narrow and unusual reading of the {\i pesukim}. Rashi notes that the {\i drasha} treats {\i v\u8217'yatz\u8217'ah mibeito} as encompassing any manner by which she leaves the marriage \u8212- not just divorce \u8212- and then focuses on the next phrase: {\i v\u8217'halchah v\u8217'haytah l\u8217'ish acher}, her subsequent relationship must be with someone entirely unrelated to her first husband, not with someone who is essentially a continuation of that husband\u8217's legal persona. As a student rightly noted, this is clearly not the {\i peshat} of the verse. The verse describes a divorced woman, not a widow, and {\i yibum} applies in cases of death, not divorce. This is, in every sense, a {\i drasha} \u8212- a hermeneutical reading that reads particular significance into precise language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum Prohibition Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Refutation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately presses the internal logic. Abaye raises a sharp objection: {\i ella me\u8217'ata chalitzah nami lo tiba\u8217'i} \u8212- if the {\i sotah} is excluded from {\i yibum} by this {\i pasuk}, she should be excluded from {\i chalitzah} as well. If the verse categorically removes her from the domain of levirate law, why should the brother have any obligation toward her at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds: {\i ilu itei leva\u8217'al mi lo ba\u8217'ei get} \u8212- if the husband were still alive, would he not need to sever the bond with a {\i get}? The relationship does not dissolve on its own, even in cases of adultery or suspicion. The husband would still need to formally sever the marital bond through a {\i get}. Similarly, the {\i zika} that transfers to the brother upon the husband\u8217's death does not dissolve automatically. Even if {\i yibum} is unavailable, the bond must be formally severed, and that severance takes the form of {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, however, points out a problem with this logic. There are many situations in which a brother cannot perform {\i yibum} and yet is equally exempt from {\i chalitzah}, even though a husband in those same cases would still need to give a {\i get}. For example, if the widow is an {\i ervah} to the brother \u8212- a forbidden relative \u8212- the brother has no obligation of {\i chalitzah}, even though had the husband been alive, a {\i get} would have been necessary to dissolve the marriage. The principle that \u8220"the husband needs a {\i get}\u8221" does not automatically entail that \u8220"the brother needs {\i chalitzah}.\u8221" So the argument does not quite hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voluntary vs Mandated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef offers a second version of the derivation. The {\i Torah} says {\i v\u8217'yatz\u8217'ah mibeito v\u8217'halchah v\u8217'haytah l\u8217'ish acher} \u8212- she goes to another man, to someone who, the {\i Gemara} explains, is {\i acher} in a particular sense: {\i acharei desani lah}, because she has become problematic for the household. The first husband did the right thing \u8212- he rid himself of a woman whose conduct had undermined the marriage. The language of {\i ish acher} carries a negative valence: the man who subsequently marries such a woman occupies a lower moral standing than the first husband who had the clarity to end the relationship. He was prepared to marry a woman suspected of adultery, described as a {\i rishah} in the context of {\i matza bah ervat davar}, and he {\i hichnis rishah l\u8217'toch beito} \u8212- he brought this problematic woman into his house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks pointedly: {\i v\u8217'at amrat tityabem nami yibumei?} \u8212- and you are proposing that the {\i Torah} would now mandate the brother to marry this woman under compulsion? The whole point of the verse was that removing her from this household was the right and proper response. How could the {\i Torah} turn around and require another man to take her on involuntarily?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye pushes back: {\i ella me\u8217'ata le\u8217'acher lo tinasei} \u8212- if the logic is that she is the kind of woman no one should be entering into a relationship with, then she should be forbidden to everyone, not just to the brother. Every subsequent marriage should be prohibited. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i mi ka ramina lah aleih be\u8217'al korchei?} \u8212- no one is forcing anyone to marry her. A third party who chooses to marry such a woman does so of his own free will; caveat emptor. But the {\i Torah} cannot mandate \u8212- cannot compel \u8212- a man to enter a relationship with a woman about whom these serious questions have been raised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As noted above, Tosafot acknowledges that neither of these derivations is fully satisfying as a formal logical proof. The real logic, Tosafot concludes, is more fundamental: if sexual relations are forbidden between this woman and her husband, then how can the {\i Torah} mandate {\i yibum}, which is a direct continuation of the husband\u8217's marital relationship \u8212- in effect, mandating the very act that was forbidden? That is the core of the argument. Because the suspicion is not definitive \u8212- it remains a {\i safek} \u8212- she cannot be fully excluded, and {\i chalitzah} is still required. But the mandate of {\i yibum} cannot stand where sexual relations were themselves prohibited with the man whose place the {\i yavam} is taking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House-Wrecker Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot here cites a beautiful elaboration from Rabbeinu Chananel. Where does the phrase {\i listore l\u8217'beito}, \u8220"to wreck the household,\u8221" come from? Rabbeinu Chananel \u8212- and Tosafot says {\i hit\u8217'im devarav}, he sweetened the words, he gave them flavor \u8212- notes that it is the precise inverse of {\i asher lo yivneh et beit achiv}, \u8220"that he shall build his brother\u8217's house,\u8221" which is the defining obligation of {\i yibum}. This woman, suspected of adultery, is in the category of a home-wrecker \u8212- {\i listore l\u8217'beito}. How could she be brought into the {\i mitzvah} of building a house? The very language of {\i yibum} contradicts the nature of what is being proposed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage to Others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then tests the second version of Rav Yosef\u8217's argument \u8212- the {\i ish acher} reading \u8212- from a different angle. Abaye raises the following case: she was suspected of adultery while married to her first husband. Her first husband dies, or divorces her, and she subsequently marries a second man \u8212- an {\i ish acher}, in the language of the {\i pasuk}. They have a happy marriage, undisturbed by any suspicion of adultery. The second husband then dies without children, leaving his brothers obligated in {\i yibum}. Should the brothers of the second husband now be prohibited from performing {\i yibum} with this woman, on the grounds that the {\i pasuk} calls her second husband an {\i ish acher} \u8212- implying that marrying her was a poor choice, and no one should be mandated to do so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i gavi d\u8217'hai mi b\u8217'shem tov hu ka\u8217'i} \u8212- but in the context of the second husband, she carries a good reputation. The successful second marriage has changed the picture. Whether it indicates she did {\i teshuva}, or that the problem lay with the first relationship rather than with her as a person, the point is that we now assess her relationship status through the lens of the second marriage. It is that relationship \u8212- the second husband\u8217's household \u8212- whose continuity the {\i yibum} obligation is meant to serve. The {\i ish acher} label applied to the second husband was appropriate in hindsight \u8212- he took a risk by marrying a woman with a questionable past \u8212- but by the time we reach the question of {\i yibum}, the relevant relationship is the second marriage, in which she was unimpeachable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a different approach: a {\i kal vachomer}. The act that caused her to become a {\i sotah} made her forbidden to her husband \u8212- the man she was {\i muttar} to, the man she was married to. If this compromised status is strong enough to forbid her to the person who had the most legitimate relationship with her, how much more so should it create a barrier to the person who, while her husband was alive, was {\i arayos} to her \u8212- her husband\u8217's brother, who was doubly forbidden to her as both a married woman\u8217's relative and her husband\u8217's brother. If the suspicion is potent enough to sever the permitted relationship, it should certainly be potent enough to prevent the creation of what had previously been a forbidden one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} tests this {\i kal vachomer} with a counterexample. Consider a {\i kohen hedyot} \u8212- an ordinary priest \u8212- who dies, leaving his widow to the {\i yibum} obligation of his brother. But the brother in this case is a {\i kohen gadol}, and a {\i kohen gadol} is forbidden to marry a widow ({\i almanah}). Does the {\i kal vachomer} apply here? Could we say: since she is forbidden to the {\i kohen gadol} who was muttar to her \u8212- wait, no \u8212- he was never {\i muttar} to her. The {\i Gemara} dismisses this case: {\i ha asirah ve-kaima} \u8212- she was always forbidden to him. The {\i kal vachomer} requires a situation in which some new development turned a previously permitted relationship into a forbidden one. The {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's prohibition against marrying a widow predated everything; it is not the result of any new status affecting her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape Case Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} tries a better counterexample. A woman married to a {\i kohen} was raped. The rape, tragically, renders her forbidden to her husband \u8212- a {\i kohen} may not remain married to a woman who has been violated, even involuntarily. The husband dies, and the brother \u8212- who is a {\i challal}, the son of a {\i kohen} who married a {\i gerushah}, making the offspring a {\i challal} \u8212- is obligated in {\i yibum}. According to the {\i kal vachomer}, one might reason: the act of rape made her forbidden to the husband she was {\i muttar} to; how much more so should it make her forbidden to the brother! But this would prohibit the {\i challal} from performing {\i yibum}, which seems incorrect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i ones be-yisrael mishra sharei} \u8212- rape by a non-{\i kohen} does not create any prohibition. The {\i challal} occupies the status of a non-{\i kohen} with respect to marriage restrictions. And {\i gavi d\u8217'hai leika issura} \u8212- there is no prohibition applicable to the brother in this scenario. The {\i kal vachomer} fails here because the prohibition created by rape is {\i kedushah}-specific: it is a restriction that applies only within the domain of {\i kehunah} and does not carry over into relationships where {\i kehunah} is not a factor. The {\i sotah}\u8217's case is fundamentally different: the suspicion of adultery creates a prohibition that is relevant in any intimate relationship, not one limited to a particular domain. Therefore, it does transfer, so to speak, and the {\i kal vachomer} does hold: if it forecloses the permitted relationship with the husband, it certainly forecloses the continuation of that relationship through {\i yibum} with the brother. {\i Chalitzah} alone is what remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden to Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enumeration Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} now turns to enumerate the women who, while carrying their unresolved {\i sotah} status, remain forbidden to eat {\i terumah}. The underlying structure of this list is important: each case represents a scenario in which the {\i sotah} process will never reach its completion through the actual drinking of the waters. The only way to lift the prohibition on {\i terumah} eating is to resolve the question \u8212- either by the ritual working or by some other definitive clarification. When that resolution becomes impossible, the prohibition remains in place. It is therefore somewhat pointed that the {\i Mishnah} frames these cases as {\i asurot milachol biterumah} \u8212- forbidden to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- since that framing emphasizes not just what happened, but what can never now be undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guilt & Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} lists first: {\i ha\u8217'omeret temei\u8217'a ani lach} \u8212- if she herself admits that she has committed adultery, the process stops immediately. Similarly, if witnesses arrive and testify that she committed adultery \u8212- {\i v\u8217'shavu eidim she-hi temei\u8217'ah} \u8212- the process is halted. In both cases, the {\i sotah} ritual is superfluous. The matter has been resolved by direct evidence. These cases may seem obvious \u8212- if she is unquestionably guilty, of course she cannot eat {\i terumah} \u8212- but the {\i Mishnah} is making clear that the {\i terumah} prohibition, having attached, does not lift simply because the ritual is no longer going to take place. She must receive a {\i get} and the prohibition persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refusal Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} continues: {\i ve\u8217'omeret eini shotah} \u8212- if she refuses to drink, the process ends. And: {\i o she-ha-ba\u8217'al eino rotzeh le-hashkotah} \u8212- if the husband refuses to go through with giving her the waters, the process equally ends. In all of these cases, the ritual will never take place. The {\i Mishnah} is teaching us what the practical consequence is when the process is interrupted at this stage: she gets divorced \u8212- without a {\i ketubah}, if it was her own refusal; possibly with a {\i ketubah}, if it was the husband who chose not to proceed \u8212- and she remains in her state of prohibition, since the situation will never be resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Violation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the {\i Mishnah} lists: {\i ve-shavah ba\u8217'alah baderech} \u8212- if the husband had marital relations with her while she was in the process of being brought to undergo the ritual. By doing so, he violated the restrictions that the {\i sotah} status imposed on both of them. Once this violation occurs, one does not go through with the waters. Again the process is terminated, and again the prohibition on {\i terumah} eating remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A brief but important structural observation: the {\i Torah} grounds the entire {\i sotah} process in the husband\u8217's jealousy \u8212- {\i ruach kin\u8217'ah} \u8212- which is fundamentally subjective. Chazal sought to objectify the process, placing it under {\i beit din} supervision, requiring objective evidence of warning and seclusion, and so forth. But this created a tension: once the process is formalized and removed from the husband\u8217's unilateral control, a husband can no longer simply retract his suspicion and end the matter. The machinery, once set in motion, follows its own juridical path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses Overseas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\u8217's Point\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Amram reports in the name of Rav Sheshet: {\i sotah she-yesh lah eidim bimdinat hayam ein ha-mayim bodkin otah} \u8212- if there is a {\i sotah} whose guilt is known to witnesses somewhere overseas ({\i medinas hayam}), in a distant place, the waters will not work on her. The scenario is this: those witnesses are unknown to the {\i beit din}; the woman and her husband proceed through the entire {\i sotah} ritual; the waters appear to have no effect; everyone concludes she is innocent. Rav Sheshet says: no. The reason the waters had no effect is not that she was innocent \u8212- it is that there existed objective evidence of her guilt elsewhere in the world, and the ritual is designed for the domain of unresolvable doubt. When concrete evidence exists \u8212- even unknown, even far away \u8212- the ritual is neutralized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately notes the epistemic problem this creates: how do we ever know {\i why} she came out appearing clean? The {\i Gemara} will address this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet adds: {\i ve-anhir lah einan mimatnisin} \u8212- and he illuminated his teaching from the {\i Mishnah} itself. The {\i chiddush} was not new; it was embedded in the {\i Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the scriptural basis for Rav Sheshet\u8217's ruling? The {\i Gemara} cites: {\i venistarah vehi nitma\u8217'ah ve-eid ein bah} \u8212- \u8220"and she was secluded and became {\i nitma\u8217'ah} and there is no witness\u8221" (Bamidbar 5:13). The phrase {\i ve-eid ein bah}, there is no witness who knows, is read precisely: {\i deleka deyadah bah}, there is nobody who knows of her guilt. {\i Le\u8217'apukei ha de-ika deyadah bah} \u8212- this excludes the case where someone does know, even from afar. In that case, the waters cannot function as a clarifying mechanism, because the truth is not hidden \u8212- it is merely inaccessible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises: does {\i ve-eid ein bah} require specifically two witnesses \u8212- {\i eidim} \u8212- or does even a single witness ({\i ed echad}) neutralize the ritual? This is a genuine question, since we have a principle that a single witness is believed after {\i kinui} and {\i stirah}, and can prevent the drinking from proceeding. Would a single distant witness similarly neutralize the waters? Tosafot is not certain. The question extends further: does a single witness who appears {\i after} the ritual has been performed have the same effect retroactively?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Post-Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states that witnesses who arrive to testify she is {\i temei\u8217'ah} result in her not eating {\i terumah}, and the {\i Gemara} asks the diagnostic question: {\i de-atu eidim eimat} \u8212- when exactly do these witnesses arrive? If they arrive before she drinks, the case is trivial \u8212- she is proven guilty and of course does not eat {\i terumah}. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's formulation would then be unremarkable. The {\i Mishnah} must therefore be referring to a case where the witnesses arrive {\i after} she drank. Having drunk, and having appeared to emerge unharmed, she would seem to have been vindicated by the waters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i Mishnah} says we still believe the witnesses. This, says the {\i Gemara}, makes perfect sense {\i bishlama} if we accept Rav Sheshet\u8217's principle \u8212- that when witnesses exist, the waters do not function as a truth-revealing mechanism. The waters\u8217' apparent silence proves nothing in that case; we now understand why the ritual seemed inconclusive. We therefore believe the witnesses, and her {\i terumah} prohibition remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if, on the contrary, we maintain that the waters function even when witnesses are present elsewhere, then the waters\u8217' silence would constitute strong proof of her innocence \u8212- and the subsequent arrival of witnesses should retroactively render them liars. {\i Tiggalei milta le-mafrea de-sahadei shikrei ninhu} \u8212- it would become retrospectively clear that these witnesses were lying. Here the {\i Gemara} makes a remarkable implicit move: the {\i sotah} ritual, embedded within a juridical framework of {\i beit din}, objective evidence, and formal procedure, is treated as a category of valid evidentiary proof \u8212- strong enough, in principle, to override witness testimony. The fact that the {\i Mishnah} clearly trusts the witnesses even after the ritual therefore proves Rav Sheshet\u8217's point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Merit & Efficacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Merit View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenges Rav Sheshet\u8217's proof. {\i Le-olam eima lach mayim bodkin otah} \u8212- the waters do function even when overseas witnesses exist. How then do we explain the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's ruling that we believe the witnesses who arrive after the ritual? {\i Ve-ha eimur zekhut tolah lah} \u8212- because there is another possible explanation for why the ritual had no apparent effect: the woman possessed {\i zechut}, merit, which suspended the waters\u8217' action. The witnesses are believed not because witnesses neutralize the ritual, but because there are other reasons the ritual might not have worked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then identifies what the two positions are debating: {\i be-minvella de-Rabbi} \u8212- the question of deterioration, as articulated by Rabbi in a {\i baraita}. Rabbi says: {\i zekhut tolah ba-mayim ha-marim} \u8212- merit suspends the effect of the bitter waters. But it does not suspend it indefinitely without consequence. She does not give birth normally, and she does not improve in her health: {\i ela minvella ve-holechet} \u8212- rather, she progressively deteriorates, until ultimately she dies in the manner the {\i Torah} described. It is a delayed reaction: the merit purchases time, but the end is the same.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis disagree with Rabbi. According to the Rabbis, {\i zechut} is fully {\i tolah} \u8212- it fully suspends the effect of the waters, and she does not die at all. The {\i zechut} is complete. The question then becomes: do the Rabbis agree that she at least visibly deteriorates, even if she does not die? Or do they hold that there is no observable effect whatsoever \u8212- the waters simply do not work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheshet Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet reads the debate as follows: {\i bein le-Rabbi bein le-Rabbanan hi misnavna} \u8212- whether according to Rabbi or according to the Rabbis, she deteriorates. The Rabbis may disagree with Rabbi about whether she ultimately dies, but they agree that some visible deterioration occurs. If that is correct, then the apparent complete absence of effect \u8212- the ritual seemingly working perfectly with no ill consequence \u8212- cannot be attributed to {\i zechut}. If {\i zechut} always produces at least observable deterioration, then when there is no deterioration at all, the only explanation is either that she is genuinely innocent, or that the waters were neutralized by the existence of overseas witnesses. And since the {\i Mishnah} credits the witnesses who arrive afterward, Rav Sheshet concludes that his position stands proven: the witnesses neutralize the waters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef reads the debate differently: {\i le-Rabbi hi misnavna, le-Rabbanan lo havya misnavna} \u8212- according to Rabbi, she deteriorates; according to the Rabbis, she does not deteriorate. Nor, for that matter, does she die \u8212- the Rabbis hold that with sufficient {\i zechut}, the waters have no effect whatsoever, and she lives her life undisturbed. If that is a possible outcome, then the waters\u8217' complete silence \u8212- no deterioration, no ill effect \u8212- could simply reflect that she had {\i zechut}. There is therefore no need to invoke the principle that witnesses neutralize the waters. The witnesses who arrive after the ritual are believed because they establish actual adultery; the ritual\u8217's silence is explained by {\i zechut}. The two explanations coexist, and neither requires the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimon\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Policy Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon bar Ashi raises a fundamental objection to the entire framework of {\i zechut} suspending the waters\u8217' effect. {\i Ein zechut tolah ba-mayim ha-marim} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon categorically rejects the idea. His argument is one of policy and systemic legitimacy. If {\i zechut} can suspend or neutralize the waters\u8217' effect, then the entire ritual is undermined. {\i Madche ata et ha-mayim bifnei kol ha-nashim ha-shotot} \u8212- you have effectively discredited the waters in the eyes of all women who undergo this ritual. Anyone can point to a case where a woman who seems guilty emerged apparently unscathed, and attribute it to {\i zechut} rather than innocence. {\i Ve-ata motzi shem ra al ha-tehorot she-shatu} \u8212- you have cast aspersions on all the women who legitimately emerged innocent, because people will suspect that they too were actually guilty and simply benefited from their merit. The guilty will think they can get away with it; the innocent will be suspected of guilt. The entire mechanism is corrupted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's argument is striking precisely because it grounds a halakhic conclusion not in textual derivation but in policy reasoning. The logic of what a well-functioning institution would look like determines what the {\i halacha} must be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then extends Rabbi Shimon\u8217's critique directly to Rav Sheshet\u8217's principle. {\i Ve-im ita yesh eidim bimdinat hayam nami ata motzi shem ra al ha-tehorot she-shatu} \u8212- if we accept that overseas witnesses neutralize the waters, then exactly the same systemic problem arises. Every woman who emerges apparently clean will be suspected of having gotten away with it because there are witnesses far away who know the truth. The same logic that Rabbi Shimon deploys against {\i zechut} applies with equal force to the principle of overseas witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Rejects Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does the {\i Gemara} respond? {\i Rabbi Shimon ka-amrat?} \u8212- you are asking about Rabbi Shimon? {\i Rabbi Shimon midei de-zechut lo tali eidim nami lo talu} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon rejects both. He does not accept that {\i zechut} interferes, and he equally does not accept that witnesses interfere. But the {\i Gemara} is operating within the position that does accept these interferences, and within that position, both {\i zechut} and overseas witnesses play parallel roles. The argument is: if you accept one form of interference, you must accept that the same systemic concern applies to both, and yet both remain valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minchah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minchah Sequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i sotah}\u8217's {\i minchah} offering and what becomes of it in various interrupted scenarios. The sequence of the {\i minchah} process is key to understanding the analysis. The {\i minchah} is brought; it is placed in a {\i kli sharet} \u8212- a consecrated vessel \u8212- at which point it acquires {\i kedushat ha-guf}: the body of the offering is sanctified, and it can no longer be redeemed. The {\i kometz} \u8212- the prescribed handful \u8212- is then separated and offered on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}; the remainder ({\i shirayim}) would normally be eaten by the {\i kohanim}. The question is what happens to the {\i minchah} at each stage if the {\i sotah} process is interrupted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} connects this to the debate about overseas witnesses. The {\i Mishnah} taught that among the cases where the {\i minchah} is burned is the case where witnesses arrive to testify she is guilty \u8212- {\i ushev\u8217'u lah eidim she-hi temei\u8217'ah}. The {\i Gemara} asks the diagnostic question: when do the witnesses arrive? If before the {\i minchah} was placed in the {\i kli sharet}, it could simply be redeemed \u8212- {\i tifrok ve-teichulin}. The case in the {\i Mishnah} must therefore be where the witnesses arrive after the {\i minchah} was already placed in the {\i kli sharet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, says the {\i Gemara}, if we accept Rav Sheshet\u8217's position \u8212- that the waters function when there are no witnesses, but are neutralized when witnesses exist \u8212- then at the time of sanctification, there were no witnesses (or they were unknown), the process was valid, the {\i minchah} was properly sanctified, and its {\i kedushah} stands. When the witnesses subsequently arrive, the process is terminated, but the {\i minchah} retains its sanctity and must be burned. This is coherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if we say the waters function even with overseas witnesses \u8212- meaning the waters are not neutralized by their existence \u8212- then we are forced to say that even had the witnesses not come, the process would have been ineffectual from the outset. The sanctification was therefore in error from the very beginning: {\i de-kadosh me-ikara be-ta\u8217'ut kadosh}. If so, the {\i minchah} was never truly sanctified, and rather than burning it, one should be able to take it out as ordinary {\i chulin} \u8212- {\i tipok le-chulin}. The fact that the {\i Mishnah} says it must be burned, and not simply redeemed or eaten as {\i chulin}, thus appears to support Rav Sheshet.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda mi-Diskarta offers a solution that limits the scope of the problem. He says: {\i kegon she-zinta ba-azara} \u8212- the scenario in the {\i Mishnah} is one where the witnesses testify not that she committed adultery a year ago in some distant city, but that she committed adultery {\i right now}, in the {\i azarah} itself, after the {\i minchah} was already placed in the {\i kli sharet}. In that case, at the time of sanctification, there were genuinely no witnesses \u8212- none existed anywhere. The sanctification was valid. The witnesses then come and testify about a brand new act of adultery, which independently terminates the process. The {\i minchah} was properly sanctified, but the process is now terminated, so it must be burned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} playfully notes the practical difficulties with this scenario \u8212- she has an escort of young priests ({\i pirchei kehunah}). The objection is answered in several ways: she could have committed adultery with one of the escorting priests themselves, or she may have needed to use the bathroom and took advantage of that moment of relative privacy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer, though technically valid, applies only to a narrow subset of cases. It does not resolve the general principle for the more common case of witnesses who arrive with testimony about prior adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Papa\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa offers the definitive resolution, one that operates without restricting the case. {\i Le-olam ke-da-amrinan me-ikara} \u8212- maintain the original scenario: witnesses arrive with testimony about prior adultery, long before and far away. Technically, one could argue the sanctification was retroactively in error and the {\i minchah} is simply {\i chulin}. But this cannot be permitted in practice. {\i Mi-de-rabbanan gezeira shema yomru motzi\u8217'in mi-kli sharet le-chol} \u8212- by rabbinic decree, we do not permit what appears to everyone to be a {\i kli sharet}-sanctified offering to be simply treated as ordinary food. The appearance of sanctity, once established in the public eye, cannot be undone without communal confusion and the undermining of {\i kedushah} as a category. Therefore, even if technically the sanctification was in error, we burn the {\i minchah} \u8212- not as a genuine {\i korban}, not on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, but in the {\i beit ha-deshen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minchah Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Sanctity Contamination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Rav Mari enumerates the various scenarios systematically. If the {\i minchah} became {\i tamei} before it was placed in the {\i kli sharet}, it has not yet acquired {\i kedushat ha-guf}: {\i harei hi ke-chol ha-menachot ve-tipade} \u8212- it is treated like any ordinary {\i minchah} in that state, and may be redeemed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death After Sanctity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i minchah} was already placed in the {\i kli sharet} and then became {\i tamei}, it has {\i kedushat ha-guf} and cannot be redeemed. The process is terminated, and: {\i harei hi ke-chol ha-menachot ve-tisaref} \u8212- it must be burned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kometz Offered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i kometz} was already separated and offered on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} but the {\i shirayim} had not yet been eaten \u8212- and in the meantime either the husband or the wife died, terminating the process \u8212- the {\i kometz} offering was brought legitimately, at a time when everything was in proper order. In that case: {\i harei ke-chol ha-menachot ve-tei\u8217'achel} \u8212- the {\i shirayim} are eaten normally. The offering was brought in proper context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doubtful Minchah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i shel safeik ba\u8217'ah mitchila} \u8212- a {\i minchah} that from the very outset was brought in circumstances of doubt \u8212- is resolved by the principle of {\i kippurah s\u8217'fika v\u8217'halcha la}. Rashi clarifies that {\i kippurah} here does not mean {\i atonement} in its technical sense, but rather that the offering accomplished its purpose: it was brought as it was meant to be brought, in a context of genuine doubt, and that context was legitimate. The fact that retrospectively the doubt was never going to be resolved \u8212- because, say, one of the parties was going to die \u8212- is irrelevant, since at the time of bringing, everything was appropriate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Post-Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When witnesses arrive after the ritual: {\i minchasah tisaref} \u8212- the {\i minchah} is burned. As explained by Rav Papa, this is by rabbinic decree to preserve the appearance of {\i kedushah}. It is burned in the {\i beit ha-deshen}, not as an offering on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zomemin Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, if the original witnesses \u8212- those whose testimony established the {\i kinui} and {\i stirah} \u8212- were subsequently proven to be {\i eidim zomemin}, false witnesses contradicted by other witnesses, the entire basis for the {\i sotah} process collapses. {\i Minchasah chulin} \u8212- the {\i minchah} reverts to {\i chulin} and may simply be eaten. There is no obligation to burn it and no need to redeem it; it was never sanctified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might object: did we not just say that even when sanctification is retroactively in error, we burn for appearance\u8217's sake? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i eidim zomemin kala is lehu} \u8212- the exposure of false witnesses is a public event. When witnesses are contradicted and proven to be liars by additional witnesses, the entire community becomes aware that the original testimony was fabricated. It becomes broadly understood that the {\i sotah} process was initiated on false grounds, and therefore the {\i minchah} was never sanctified. There is no danger of anyone thinking that a legitimately sanctified offering is being desecrated; the public knowledge of the {\i eidim zomemin} provides full transparency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimon Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Text vs Policy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established, in Rav Papa\u8217's framework, that the {\i minchah} in the witnesses-after-the-ritual case is burned by rabbinic decree, the {\i Gemara} returns to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's policy objection. Rabbi Shimon had argued that accepting any interference with the waters \u8212- whether from {\i zechut} or from overseas witnesses \u8212- systematically undermines the legitimacy of the entire {\i sotah} process. One might therefore think that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's objection strikes at the very source from which Rav Sheshet derived his principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} clarifies that this is not so. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's policy argument is powerful, but it cannot override a textual derivation ({\i drasha}). The question is: is there a textual basis that survives Rabbi Shimon\u8217's critique?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vetohorah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the derivation from the word {\i vetohorah} \u8212- \u8220"and she will be pure\u8221" (Bamidbar 5:28). The verse states that if she is innocent, the waters will prove her pure and she will conceive. The word {\i vetohorah} is read as implying a limiting condition: the waters function as a mechanism of {\i taharah} only under specific conditions. {\i V\u8217'lo she-yesh lah eidim bimdinat hayam} \u8212- not when there are witnesses overseas who know she is guilty. And similarly, {\i v\u8217'lo she-tola lah zechus} \u8212- not when {\i zechut} is operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gossiping Peshat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse also yields a third case: {\i v\u8217'lo she-yisu v\u8217'yitnu bah mozeros b\u8217'levana} \u8212- not when she is the subject of widespread gossip among women knitting in the moonlight. This is a striking {\i drasha}: if her alleged adultery has become so publicly known that it is the talk of the community, the waters will not function as a revelation of hidden truth, because the matter is no longer truly hidden. The textual basis is the same word {\i vetohorah} \u8212- the waters are effective only when her status is genuinely unresolved and concealed; not when it has entered the domain of public knowledge, however informal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rarity Mitigation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position is then reconciled with these derivations as follows. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's policy concern \u8212- that exceptions undermine legitimacy \u8212- is not without force, but it does not reach every possible case. The case of {\i zechut} was his primary target, and he rejects it entirely as too corrosive. The case of overseas witnesses is different. {\i Lo shchicha} \u8212- it is not common. Cases where witnesses exist in distant lands, unknown to the {\i beit din}, who could testify to her adultery, are genuinely rare. The textual derivation from {\i vetohorah} compels us to acknowledge this as a valid limiting condition, but its rarity means that it will not, in practice, generate the systemic suspicion that Rabbi Shimon feared. The waters\u8217' legitimacy is not meaningfully undermined by an exception that almost never arises. Rabbi Shimon can therefore concede the textual point while maintaining his broader objection to {\i zechut}, whose pervasive applicability would indeed corrode the entire institution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 7\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Private to Judicial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous {\i mishna} dealt with circumstances that would prevent the {\i sotah} procedure from going forward \u8212- cases where the woman remained forbidden to eat {\i terumah}, including situations involving {\i edim} in {\i medinat hayam} and cases of admission. That {\i mishna} serves as a necessary background, because the present {\i mishna} now addresses what happens once none of those impediments apply: the procedure is cleared to proceed, and we turn to a description of how it would actually unfold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman to Beis Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens with the question {\i keitzad oso lah} \u8212- what would you do with her once she has acquired the status of a {\i sotah}, having been warned and having gone into seclusion? The answer: {\i molicho l\u8217'veit din she\u8217'be\u8217'oto makom} \u8212- you bring her to the {\i beit din} of that locale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is already a striking departure from the {\i Torah}\u8217's presentation of the {\i sotah} procedure. In the {\i Torah}, the husband brings his wife directly to the {\i Beis HaMikdash} for the ritual. Here, however, the first stop is the {\i beit din}. That choice is not incidental \u8212- it is a statement about what this process fundamentally is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazal\u8217's Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's framing makes a clear declaration: this is a matter of {\i din}. What had begun as a husband\u8217's jealousy, his private suspicion of his wife, has been converted into a judicial proceeding. The witnesses, the circumstantial evidence, the formal warning \u8212- all of it transforms the dynamic from one of passion and individual initiative into one of institutional supervision and objective adjudication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This move is characteristic of {\i Chazal} more broadly. The authority of {\i Chazal} and {\i beit din} is grounded in the {\i pasuk} of {\i al pi hatorah asher yorucha v\u8217'al hamishpat asher yomru lecha ta\u8217'aseh lo tasur yamin u\u8217'smol} \u8212- but that {\i pasuk} in the {\i Torah} is addressed {\i el hakohanim v\u8217'el hashofet asher yihiyeh bayamim hahem}, with the {\i kohanim} far more prominent than the singular {\i shofet}. At the time of the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, institutional authority resided far more with the {\i kohanim} than with the rabbis. Yet {\i Chazal}, in describing the reality of how these procedures were conducted, consistently assert their own authority. We find this, for example, in the way the {\i beit din} would determine whether a {\i kohen} was fit for {\i avodah} when questions arose about his {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sotah} procedure illustrates both dimensions of this assertion simultaneously. First, {\i Chazal} are taking something rooted in the husband\u8217's passion and making it objective \u8212- grounded in testimony and evidence rather than in one man\u8217's unilateral discretion. Second, they are taking something that belonged to the {\i kohanim} and the {\i Beis HaMikdash} and placing it under the authority of {\i beit din}. Both moves are reflected in the single word {\i keitzad}: what do you do? You bring her to a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shema Yavo Aleha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the local {\i beit din} has received the case, the {\i mishna} continues: {\i u\u8217'mosru lo shnei talmidei chachamim} \u8212- they hand over two Torah sages to the husband, who will serve as escorts, accompanying the couple on their journey to {\i Yerushalayim}. The stated reason is {\i shema yavo aleha baderech} \u8212- lest he have relations with her along the way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This detail is layered with significance. Once a woman has acquired the status of a {\i sotah}, husband and wife are forbidden to have relations with each other until the matter is resolved. The concern is not merely one of general propriety but of practical efficacy: we learn that the {\i sotah} waters function only when the husband himself is free of sin. If he were to violate the prohibition en route, the entire proceeding would be undermined. The waters would no longer be effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The presence of the two escorts therefore serves a concrete halachic purpose. But notice what it also does to the character of the whole process. At no point from here on is this the husband dragging his wife to judgment. The {\i beit din} has dispatched its agents; they are the ones accompanying this woman to the next stage. The husband is no longer the driver of events \u8212- the {\i beit din} is. This interestingly mirrors the description in {\i Yoma} of the {\i beit din}\u8217's oversight of the {\i Kohen Gadol} before {\i Yom Kippur}, where the {\i mishna} uses the very same language: {\i mosru lo shnei talmidei chachamim}, the {\i beit din} hands over two sages to accompany the {\i Kohen Gadol} as he prepares for the {\i avodat hayom}. In that context too, a {\i Beis HaMikdash} ritual of the highest order is being overseen and structured by the {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks why two representatives were needed rather than one. The fact that there are two escorts plus the husband makes three men in total, and the {\i Gemara} suggests this might support a teaching of Rav. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: {\i lo shanu ela be\u8217'ir} \u8212- the rule that two men and a woman do not constitute {\i yichud} applies only when they are in a city, but {\i aval be\u8217'derech}, on a journey, {\i ad de\u8217'ika tlasa}, you need three men to avoid concern of {\i yichud}. The reason: {\i shema yitztarech echad me\u8217'hem le\u8217'nekavav} \u8212- one of the men may need to attend to his bodily needs. In a city, stepping away for that purpose does not create meaningful seclusion; but on a journey in open terrain, the man who steps away from the group to find some privacy in the brush or forest will leave the remaining man and the woman alone in what would constitute {\i yichud}. Hence the need for a third person to ensure someone is always present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the requirement for two escorts rather than one follows directly from Rav\u8217's principle: because they are traveling, three people are required to prevent any situation of {\i yichud} arising along the route.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} does not ultimately accept that as the explanation, however. {\i Lo}, it says \u8212- {\i hacha hainu ta\u8217'ama ki heichi de\u8217'lehavu alah sahadei}: here the reason is so that they should serve as witnesses. What would they be witnessing? As Rashi explains, if the husband were to go ahead and have relations with his wife during the journey, these two escorts would be positioned to testify to that fact, which would then disqualify the proceeding from going forward. A single witness would not suffice to establish that the husband and wife had relations on the journey \u8212- that requires the standard two-witness evidentiary threshold. Hence two escorts are needed to function as a proper {\i beit din}-style testimony mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmidei Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This still leaves a question open: why must the escorts specifically be {\i talmidei chachamim}? If the purpose is simply to provide two witnesses, any two competent witnesses would do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially considers whether this supports another teaching of Rav \u8212- {\i lo shanu ela ke\u8217'sherim aval prutzim afilu asara nami lo} \u8212- that the principle allowing two men to be alone with a woman without concern for {\i yichud} applies only to upstanding individuals, but if the men are {\i prutzim}, licentious in sexual matters, even ten such men with a woman cannot be trusted. The concern is not that those other men will exercise moral restraint; it is that a man known to be {\i parutz} will not be inhibited by their presence. The {\i Gemara} cites a case in which ten men actually smuggled a woman out of a city concealed in a bed for illicit purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} again sets this aside: {\i lo, hacha hainu ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'yad\u8217'i l\u8217'atruyei bei} \u8212- the reason we need {\i talmidei chachamim} is that they will know to give him {\i hatra\u8217'ah}, a formal halachic warning. Rashi explains: if the husband is on the verge of violating the prohibition of having relations with his {\i sotah}, they will recognize what is happening and be positioned to issue a valid {\i hatra\u8217'ah} before the act, which is necessary for him to incur {\i malkos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, admittedly, a somewhat surprising rationale. It is one thing to want the proceeding to go forward without being compromised \u8212- but why does it matter to us that this man specifically receives a warning so that he can then be liable for {\i malkos}? In isolation, the concern for ensuring a particular transgressor receives his due punishment is not typically something we go out of our way to engineer proactively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the logic becomes clear when you recognize what this does to the character of the procedure. To send {\i talmidei chachamim} who are capable of giving {\i hatra\u8217'ah}, who function in effect as a small traveling {\i beit din} throughout the journey, is to make the most emphatic possible statement that this entire proceeding is framed within the world of {\i din}. Witnesses, warning, liability for lashes \u8212- these are the very structural elements of rabbinic judicial process. By foregrounding all of them, {\i Chazal} are foregrounding {\i din} itself. The {\i talmidei chachamim} are not incidental chaperones; they are representatives of the {\i beit din} whose presence at every stage of the journey makes clear that this is, above all, a judicial matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arei Miklat Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} notes that this same language and structure appear in a different context \u8212- the case of one who kills accidentally and is sent to an {\i ir miklat}. There too, the {\i Gemara} records that two {\i talmidei chachamim} accompany the exile, {\i shema ye\u8217'hargenu baderech} \u8212- lest the {\i go\u8217'el hadam} kill him on the way. The {\i Gemara} there similarly explains that the two are needed so that if the {\i go\u8217'el hadam} is about to kill this man, they can issue a proper warning and, if necessary, intervene to stop him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} observes that in both cases \u8212- the {\i sotah} and the accidental murderer \u8212- the {\i Gemara} grounds the requirement for {\i talmidei chachamim} in the need for {\i hatra\u8217'ah}. The parallel is striking and deliberate. At the beginning of the study of {\i sotah}, it was observed that several scenarios in the {\i Torah} involve something that begins as vigilante justice, as taking the law into one\u8217's own hands, and the process by which {\i Chazal} and sometimes the {\i Torah} itself bring these situations under institutional control. The {\i go\u8217'el hadam} is precisely the paradigm case: the {\i Torah} already legislates {\i arei miklat} as a structured response to the otherwise unbounded right of the blood-avenger. {\i Chazal}\u8217's treatment of the {\i sotah} follows the same trajectory \u8212- taking what was a private act of jealousy and passion, placing the {\i beit din} at the center of the proceedings, and asserting its supervisory presence at every stage, from departure to arrival. The presence of two {\i talmidei chachamim} accompanying the accused, just as two {\i talmidei chachamim} accompany the accidental murderer, makes the parallel explicit and structural.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'ala Ne\u8217'eman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the position of the {\i Tanna Kamma} \u8212- that the {\i beit din} dispatches two {\i talmidei chachamim} to accompany the couple \u8212- the {\i mishna} records a dissenting view: {\i Rabbi Yehuda omer ba\u8217'alah ne\u8217'eman aleha}, Rabbi Yehuda holds that the husband is trusted, and no escorts are needed. If he is trusted not to have relations with her en route, there is no reason to send two {\i talmidei chachamim} along. And this difference is not merely logistical \u8212- it fundamentally changes the character of the journey. Is the experience one of the {\i beit din}\u8217's agents bringing this woman toward judgment, or is it the jealous husband bringing his wife? Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position restores something of the {\i Torah}\u8217's original dynamic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal V\u8217'Chomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} records Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's argument in the form of a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}: {\i u\u8217'mah niddah she\u8217'hi b\u8217'kares ba\u8217'ala ne\u8217'eman aleha} \u8212- in the case of a {\i niddah}, whose prohibition carries the penalty of {\i kares}, a very serious transgression, we trust a husband and wife to sleep in the same room without having relations. {\i Sotah she\u8217'hi b\u8217'lav} \u8212- a {\i sotah}, whose prohibition is merely a negative commandment, {\i lo kol sheken} \u8212- how much more so can we trust him not to transgress?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis respond with the phrase {\i hi hanosenes} \u8212- that very argument is the reason we {\i don\u8217't} trust him. {\i Niddah d\u8217'kares chamira lei u\u8217'meheiman} \u8212- a {\i niddah} prohibition, being so weighty, will restrain him; we can rely on him. {\i Sotah b\u8217'lav lo chamira lei v\u8217'lo meheiman} \u8212- a {\i sotah} prohibition, being only a simple negative commandment, is not weighty enough to serve as a reliable deterrent, and we cannot trust him to observe it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i beraita} records the same dispute, but with different attribution. There, Rabbi Yosei presents the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} argument, and the Rabbis push back with two objections. The first: {\i im amarta b\u8217'niddah she\u8217'yesh lah heter} \u8212- with a {\i niddah}, there is an eventual release; after she immerses in the {\i mikveh}, she becomes permissible again, and the prohibition has a cyclical, expected quality. {\i Tomar b\u8217'sotah she\u8217'ein lah heter} \u8212- with a {\i sotah}, if she is found guilty, she will never be permissible to him again. This finality might make him feel that relations during the journey are his last opportunity, creating a far more powerful temptation. The second objection adds: {\i mayim genuvim yimtaku} \u8212- stolen waters are sweet. Now that she is forbidden to him, the very fact of her prohibition may itself heighten the temptation. {\i Tosafos} explains why this second argument was necessary: one might have argued that since he has been with her before, there is no special urgency \u8212- but the very novelty of her forbidden status can make her more tempting, not less.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In that second {\i beraita}, Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position is stated not as a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} but as a direct appeal to the {\i pasuk}: {\i v\u8217'heivi ha\u8217'ish es ishto el hakohen} \u8212- the man brings his wife. This is the {\i Torah}\u8217's own description of who initiates and executes this procedure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazal\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction between the two {\i beraisos} by explaining that Rabbi Yehuda first presented the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} argument, which the Rabbis refuted. He then fell back on the {\i pasuk} itself: regardless of whether the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} holds, the {\i Torah} says the man brings his wife, and that should be sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i Rabbi Yehuda haynu Tanna Kamma} \u8212- isn\u8217't Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position the same as the first {\i tanna}\u8217's? Both concede that from the {\i Torah}\u8217's perspective, it is the man who brings his wife. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i ika beinaihu aval amru} \u8212- the difference is entirely in what {\i Chazal} added. Everyone agrees that on the {\i Torah}\u8217's own terms, the husband brings his wife. The dispute is whether the Rabbis are authorized to change that procedure by adding their own supervisory layer. The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that they did so and appropriately so. Rabbi Yehuda holds that we should not alter what the {\i Torah} prescribed. He is not necessarily denying that there is some basis for concern \u8212- but he does not want to transform the fundamental nature of the process to the degree that the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's position implies. He wants to keep it closer to its biblical form, where the husband is the driving force, rather than converting it into an exercise in institutional supervision.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beis Din Hagadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 To Jerusalem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} describes the subsequent stages. {\i Hayu maalin osah le-Beis Din Hagadol she-bi-Yerushalayim} \u8212- they bring her to the Supreme Court in {\i Yerushalayim}. Again, notice the framing: not directly to the {\i Beis HaMikdash} for the ritual, but to the {\i Beis Din HaGadol}. It is true that the {\i Beis Din HaGadol} was situated within the {\i Beis HaMikdash} complex, in the {\i Lishkat HaGazit}, but that is not the point. The {\i mishna} is presenting the journey as proceeding from one {\i beit din} to the next \u8212- from the local {\i beit din} to the Supreme Court \u8212- and from there only then to the {\i kohanim} for the ritual itself. At no stage does the {\i mishna} describe this as simply a husband bringing his wife to the {\i Beis HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intimidation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'me\u8217'ayemin aleha ke\u8217'derech she\u8217'me\u8217'ayemin al edei nefashot} \u8212- they frighten her in the manner in which witnesses in capital cases are frightened. In capital cases, the concern is that a witness might fill in gaps in his knowledge, might testify to something he is not fully certain of, and so the {\i beit din} would impress upon such witnesses the enormous weight of their testimony \u8212- that if an innocent man dies based on their words, his blood and the blood of his descendants would be on their hands. This cuts in both directions: if the witness truly knows, he must testify; but if there is any uncertainty, he must stand down. The purpose is to ensure that only reliable testimony enters the proceedings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here, the {\i beit din} applies analogous pressure to the {\i sotah} herself. The explicit parallel to capital cases is drawn \u8212- and the {\i mishna} makes no apology for it. The {\i sotah} proceeding is being framed within the same institutional framework as the gravest matters of {\i din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Appeals to Admit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then describes what the {\i beit din} says to her: {\i biti, harbeh yayin osah} \u8212- my daughter, wine often causes people to act in ways they would not otherwise act. They invite her to confess by offering her mitigating circumstances: {\i harbeh se\u8217'chok osah}, excessive frivolity, {\i harbeh yaldus osah}, the passions and poor judgment of youth, {\i harbeh shechenim ra\u8217'im osim}, the corrupting influence of bad company and bad neighbors. They are, in effect, making it possible for her to admit by reducing the apparent weight of moral responsibility. They do not require her to present herself as a fully deliberate sinner \u8212- they offer her a range of explanations that would allow her to acknowledge what happened without bearing its full shame.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelo Yimacheh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The culminating appeal is: {\i asi lishmo hagadol she\u8217'nichtav bi\u8217'kedushah shelo yimacheh al hamayim} \u8212- act for the sake of God\u8217's great Name, which is written in holiness, so that it should not be dissolved into the water. This is a striking inversion. The purpose of the {\i sotah} procedure is precisely to dissolve the Divine Name into the water so that it can be administered to the woman. But here, the {\i beit din} presents that prospect as something to be avoided. The purpose, they are saying, is {\i shelo yimacheh} \u8212- not to erase it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a genuine question: if we trust that the ritual works, why are we working so hard to prevent it from being used? One might argue it is better for the sinner to confess of her own accord; or one might invoke the various reasons explored in earlier discussion about why the ritual\u8217's efficacy might be unreliable in practice. But the {\i mishna}\u8217's focus here is revealing: it does not frame the goal as arriving at the truth, or even as vindicating the innocent. The goal, as stated, is to avoid erasing God\u8217's Name. Everything is oriented toward obtaining an admission and thereby bypassing the ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admission Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she responds {\i tme\u8217'ah ani} \u8212- I am impure, I am guilty \u8212- the consequences are clear: {\i shoveret ktuvatah}, she loses her {\i ketubah}, {\i v\u8217'yotzah}, and she is divorced. Admission ends the proceeding. She leaves without the financial protection of her {\i ketubah} and without her marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maintains Innocence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she says {\i tehorah ani} \u8212- I am pure, I am innocent \u8212- the proceedings advance. {\i Maalin otah leshaar hamizrach} \u8212- they bring her to the eastern gate, which is directly opposite the {\i shaar Nikanor}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eastern Gate Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eastern Gate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The eastern gate of the {\i Beis HaMikdash} is the entry point into the {\i ezrat nashim}. Proceeding westward through the {\i ezrat nashim}, one reaches the top of a series of steps leading into the {\i azarah} proper, where the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} stands. At the top of those steps is the {\i shaar Nikanor}, the gate that transitions from the {\i ezrat nashim} into the {\i azarah}. The {\i mishna} tells us that the {\i sotah} is brought to the eastern gate, facing this {\i shaar Nikanor}, because that is where the {\i sotot} drink, where newly delivered mothers come for their purification, and where {\i metzora\u8217'im} undergo parts of their purification ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gate Theology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i shaar Nikanor} is a gate in a wall, and like ancient city gates it has physical thickness \u8212- a substantial passage of stone. That liminal space within the gate\u8217's thickness did not carry the {\i kedushah} of the {\i azarah}; it had only the {\i kedushah} of the {\i ezrat nashim}. This made it a legal threshold available to those who could not yet enter the {\i azarah} but whose ritual required some form of proximity to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i yoledet}, a woman who had given birth, needed to bring a {\i korban} as part of her purification, but was not yet {\i tahor} in a way that allowed her into the {\i azarah}. By standing in the {\i shaar Nikanor}, she could be present for the bringing of her {\i korban} without entering the prohibited space. A {\i metzora} needed the blood of his {\i korban} applied to his thumbs and toes, but similarly could not yet enter the {\i azarah}. He would stand in the thickness of the gate and extend his thumb and toe into the {\i azarah}, allowing the {\i kohen} to apply the blood \u8212- though this raises the separate halachic question of {\i bi\u8217'ah b\u8217'miktzas shemah bi\u8217'ah}, whether partial entry into the {\i Beis HaMikdash} while {\i tamei} constitutes a prohibited entry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sotah}, too, would stand in this liminal space. And what is striking is the company she is placed in. The {\i mishna} describes her alongside the {\i yoledet} and the {\i metzora} \u8212- people undergoing processes of {\i tum\u8217'ah} and {\i taharah}. This is no accident. The {\i Torah}\u8217's own language for the outcome of the {\i sotah} procedure is {\i v\u8217'nitme\u8217'ah} and {\i hi lo nitme\u8217'ah} \u8212- she is {\i tamei} or she is not {\i tamei}. The procedure is being reframed not only as a matter of {\i din} but also as a process of {\i taharah}, of determining and rectifying a state of spiritual impurity. This is a different kind of framing than the trial-by-ordeal model, and perhaps a more accessible one \u8212- we know there are {\i taharah} processes that involve unusual rituals, birds, blood on fingers, strange mixtures. A {\i minchah} offering, words dissolved in water, a woman drinking \u8212- situated within that framework, the ritual becomes less anomalous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Garment Tearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hakohen ochez bi\u8217'vgadeha} \u8212- the {\i kohen} takes hold of her garments. {\i Im nikre\u8217'u nikre\u8217'u v\u8217'im nifremu nifremu} \u8212- if they tear, they tear; if they split entirely, they split. The {\i kohen} holds her garments and does not release them regardless of how extensively they rend, continuing until {\i ad she\u8217'hu megaleh libah} \u8212- he has exposed her chest. He also undoes her hair, {\i v\u8217'soter et se\u8217'arah}, which echoes the {\i Torah}\u8217's own language for this stage of the procedure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modesty Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda dissents in a specific case: {\i im haya libah na\u8217'eh lo haya megalehu} \u8212- if her chest was particularly attractive, he would not expose it, and {\i im haya se\u8217'arah na\u8217'eh lo haya sotro} \u8212- if her hair was beautiful, he would not undo it. The underlying tension is obvious: this degradation ritual involves uncovering parts of the body that are normally covered, and in doing so risks transforming what is meant to be a shameful and sobering spectacle into something erotically charged for the onlookers. Rabbi Yehuda attempts to set limits precisely at the point where the degradation threatens to tip into something else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Degradation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If she had been wearing white garments, she is dressed in black. If she wore golden jewelry \u8212- {\i k\u8217'lei zahav, ve\u8217'katliyot, ve\u8217'nezamim ve\u8217'tabba\u8217'ot} \u8212- various chokers, earrings, and rings \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'avirin mimena}, they are removed from her, {\i kedei lena\u8217'velah}, in order to dishevel her and render her repulsive. This is a systematic process of degradation, stripping away the markers of status and beauty and replacing them with their opposites. Then an Egyptian rope is taken and bound above her breasts to hold up the garment after it has been torn, so that it does not fall entirely. The sequence likely proceeds as follows: first the black garments are substituted and the jewelry removed; then the {\i kohen} tears the garments; then the rope is tied to hold them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Spectacle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'chol ha\u8217'rotze lir\u8217'ot ba v\u8217'ro\u8217'eh} \u8212- and whoever wishes to come and see may come and see. This is a public spectacle, and notably it is one that has no direct source in the {\i Torah}. The {\i Torah}\u8217's {\i sotah} procedure is not a public event. {\i Chazal} have made it one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This transformation is worth pausing over. The movement from a private matter between a jealous husband and his wife to a public spectacle before all of {\i Yerushalayim} mirrors a broader pattern in {\i Chazal}\u8217's treatment of {\i Beis HaMikdash} rituals generally. The {\i Yom Kippur} service, for instance, is explicitly marked in the {\i Torah} as a moment of singular divine intimacy: {\i v\u8217'chol adam lo yihyeh b\u8217'ohel mo\u8217'ed b\u8217'vo\u8217'o l\u8217'chaper bakodesh}. Yet in the {\i mishna}\u8217's description of {\i Yom Kippur}, the service becomes a public ritual \u8212- the people gather to see the lot selected, to watch the red string, to hear the reading of the {\i Torah}. {\i Chazal} consistently push these rituals into the public sphere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And there is a further dimension here, drawn from the work of Yishai Rosen-Zvi, who wrote on {\i Masechet Sotah} in his book {\i Ha-tekes she-lo hayah} \u8212- the ritual that never was. Rosen-Zvi argues that the {\i mishna} proceeds throughout with an assumption of the woman\u8217's guilt. The entire procedure is oriented not toward clarifying her status but toward punishing her. One way to see this is in the scaring: when the {\i beit din} frightened the {\i sotah}, they appealed only in one direction \u8212- please confess, spare us the need to erase God\u8217's Name. When {\i beit din} frightens witnesses in capital cases, by contrast, they press in both directions: testify if you truly know, but do not testify if you are uncertain. Here, the {\i beit din} does not say: if you are truly innocent, go forward so that the waters can vindicate you and restore you to your husband. The assumption is that she did it, and the {\i beit din}\u8217's appeals are structured accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This presumption of guilt also helps explain the public spectacle. A neutral judicial proceeding aimed at determining guilt or innocence does not need an audience. But if the outcome is already understood \u8212- if this is, in effect, a public punishment \u8212- then gathering the people makes perfect sense, both as a form of communal shaming and as a deterrent. The {\i Torah} itself provides the underlying principle: {\i v\u8217'chol ha\u8217'am yishme\u8217'u v\u8217'yira\u8217'u}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who May Watch\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chutz me\u8217'avadeha u\u8217'shifchoteha} \u8212- with one exception: her servants may not be present. The reason given is {\i mipnei she\u8217'libah gas bahen} \u8212- their presence would bolster her sense of confidence and importance. She might be less willing to confess with her servants watching; she might feel less the weight of the proceedings if surrounded by people over whom she has authority. The goal of obtaining a confession or at least ensuring the degradation achieves its full psychological effect requires that she be stripped of those familiar sources of comfort and status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'chol ha\u8217'nashim mutarot lir\u8217'otah} \u8212- but all women are permitted and indeed encouraged to see her. The {\i mishna} quotes a {\i pasuk} from {\i Yechezkel}: {\i v\u8217'niv\u8217'ssru kol ha\u8217'nashim v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'asena k\u8217'zimatchena} \u8212- all the women shall take heed and not do as she did in her lewdness. The {\i sotah} proceeding, in its public form, serves the same function as {\i kol ha\u8217'am yishme\u8217'u v\u8217'yira\u8217'u} in capital cases: punishment as public instruction and deterrent, the logic of {\i din} made visible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beis Din Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deriving Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mena hani millei} \u8212- from where is this derived? How do we know that the {\i sotah} must be brought before the {\i Beis Din HaGadol} in {\i Yerushalayim}? Rabbi Chiyya bar Gamda said in the name of Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina: the derivation comes from a {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i Torah-Torah}. It is written here, by the {\i sotah}: {\i v\u8217'asah lah ha\u8217'kohen et kol ha\u8217'Torah ha\u8217'zot} \u8212- the {\i kohen} shall do all of this {\i Torah}, this ritual. And it is written there, by the {\i zaken mamre}: {\i al pi ha\u8217'Torah asher yorucha} \u8212- in accordance with the {\i Torah} they teach you. {\i Mah le\u8217'halan b\u8217'shivim v\u8217'echad} \u8212- just as a {\i zaken mamre} who rebels against the ruling of the {\i Sanhedrin} requires a court of seventy-one, {\i af kan b\u8217'shivim v\u8217'echad} \u8212- so too here, the {\i sotah} procedure requires the oversight of a {\i beit din} of seventy-one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zaken Mamre\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} from {\i zaken mamre} is an instructive choice of parallel. The {\i zaken mamre} is a Torah sage who rules contrary to the {\i Sanhedrin HaGadol}, the supreme authority, and whose defiance carries the penalty of death. The {\i Sanhedrin}\u8217's authority in that case is total and essential \u8212- without their involvement the case cannot proceed. The {\i Gemara} is drawing on that model to establish the {\i Beis Din HaGadol}\u8217's centrality to the {\i sotah} process as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chronology Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises a powerful difficulty with this derivation. The {\i halacha} of {\i zaken mamre} applies only when the {\i Sanhedrin} is sitting in its designated place within the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. Historically, we know that the {\i Sanhedrin} left the {\i Beis HaMikdash} \u8212- they moved out of the {\i Lishkat HaGazit} \u8212- before the {\i churban}, because they did not wish to bear responsibility for adjudicating capital cases. Yet the evidence indicates that {\i sotah} rituals continued even after the {\i Sanhedrin} had left the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. If the {\i sotah} procedure truly required a {\i Beis Din HaGadol} sitting in the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, on the model of {\i zaken mamre}, then {\i sotah} should have ceased the moment the {\i Sanhedrin} relocated. But that does not appear to be what happened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Warning Not Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} resolves the difficulty as follows: {\i d\u8217'iyum lo haya ma\u8217'akev} \u8212- the intimidation, the warning administered by the {\i Beis Din HaGadol}, was not {\i me\u8217'akev}. It was not a blocking condition. {\i D\u8217'im lo hayu me\u8217'ayimin klal, af al pi chen haysa shosah} \u8212- even if the {\i beit din} had not issued the warning at all, she could still have gone through with the drinking of the waters. The {\i sotah} ritual would still function. {\i Hilchach haysa nohages afilu achar hagalus} \u8212- therefore the practice continued even after the {\i Sanhedrin}\u8217's departure from the {\i Beis HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazal Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is, on reflection, remarkable. {\i Tosafos} is telling us that for all the elaborate {\i beit din} scaffolding that has been constructed around the {\i sotah} procedure \u8212- the local {\i beit din} receiving the case, the two {\i talmidei chachamim} accompanying the couple, the {\i Beis Din HaGadol} in {\i Yerushalayim} issuing its solemn warning \u8212- none of it is ultimately {\i me\u8217'akev}. None of it is strictly necessary. At its core, the {\i sotah} procedure is a {\i Beis HaMikdash} ritual: the man brings his wife to the {\i kohen}, and the {\i kohen} administers the waters. The entire {\i beit din} superstructure could theoretically be dispensed with, and the procedure would still be valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This paradox runs in both directions. {\i Chazal} foreground {\i beit din} authority at every stage, insist on its presence, draw explicit parallels to {\i din}, and embed witnesses and {\i hatra\u8217'ah} into the very journey to {\i Yerushalayim} \u8212- and then the sources reveal that it was all optional. This also provides an answer to the earlier question that the first {\i Tosafos} implicitly raised: why, if {\i sotah} requires a {\i beit din} of seventy-one on the model of {\i zaken mamre}, is it not listed in {\i Perek Kamma} of {\i Sanhedrin} among the cases requiring a {\i beit din} of seventy-one? The answer is precisely because the {\i sotah} proceeding is not, at its base, something that requires a {\i beit din} of seventy-one. It is a {\i Beis HaMikdash} ritual. The {\i beit din}\u8217's role, however elaborate and however prominently {\i Chazal} present it, is ultimately not constitutive of the ritual\u8217's validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What we are left with is a picture of extraordinary complexity: a ritual that the {\i Torah} conceived as a husband\u8217's private action in the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, which {\i Chazal} systematically transformed into a judicial proceeding saturated with the language and structure of {\i din} \u8212- while simultaneously acknowledging, when pressed, that the transformation was always optional and the underlying reality remained a pure {\i Beis HaMikdash} ritual.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 7b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Warnings & Severity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Name Erased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with a discussion of the foundational concern underlying the {\i sotah} ritual: the erasure of God\u8217's name. The {\i mishnah} had established that the {\i beit din} impresses upon the woman the severity of what she is about to undergo \u8212- just as witnesses in capital cases are solemnly warned \u8212- because of the prohibition against erasing God\u8217's name unnecessarily. The relevant phrase is {\i assur lishmo hagadol shenichtav bikdushah shelo yimcha al hamayim}: the great name, written in holiness, must not be erased over the water without cause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concern is central to understanding the {\i sotah} ritual\u8217's inner logic. Part of the ritual\u8217's profound beauty, as reflected in {\i aggadata}, is that God is willing to have His name erased in order to restore {\i shalom bayit} between a husband and wife. Nevertheless, the prohibition against erasing God\u8217's name remains in force, and so the {\i beit din} works to prevent that erasure wherever possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paradox of Warnings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises what appears to be a sharp contradiction. A {\i baraita} from the {\i Tosefta} expands on the notion of warning the woman and states: {\i kederech shema\u8217'amin aleha shelo tishteh kach ma\u8217'amin aleha shetishteh} \u8212- just as we warn her not to drink, so too we warn her to drink. This seems paradoxical. If the entire purpose of warning her is so that she will not go through with the ritual and thereby cause God\u8217's name to be erased, what could it possibly mean to warn her to drink?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} itself elaborates: we tell her, \u8220"If it is clear to you that you are innocent, stand firm and drink, because the bitter water is like a dry medicine placed on raw flesh \u8212- if there is a wound, it dissolves into it and takes effect; if there is no wound, it does nothing.\u8221" In other words, if she is innocent, the water will have no effect and it is actually beneficial for her to proceed with the ritual. The destruction of God\u8217's name will then serve a purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before/After Erasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya kan kodem shenimchakah hamegillah kan le\u8217'achar shenimchakah hamegillah} \u8212- there is no difficulty, because these are two distinct stages. Before the {\i Megillat Sotah} has been erased into the water, the {\i beit din} tries to encourage the woman to admit guilt, so that the erasure of God\u8217's name is avoided altogether. Once the {\i megillah} has already been erased, however, we do not want that erasure to have been in vain, and so we now encourage her to drink and affirm her innocence, telling her the water will benefit rather than harm her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} had stated that they say before her {\i devarim she\u8217'einam kedai lishoman} \u8212- things that are not suitable to be heard, yet are said to her anyway. The Gemara clarifies that these are {\i devarim shel aggadah}, aggadic narratives, stories that will move her emotionally. Why are they described as {\i einam kedai lishoman}? That becomes clear as the Gemara provides its examples.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judah & Reuben\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's {\i baraita} specifies the content of these narratives. We tell her stories from the earlier writings, from the lives of the founding figures of Israel, citing a verse from Iyov: {\i asher chakhamim yagidu velo kichadu me\u8217'avotam} \u8212- the wise ones have spoken of these things and have not hidden them from their ancestors. Two paradigmatic examples are then given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yehudah hodah velo bosh} \u8212- Yehuda admitted his sin and was not embarrassed. {\i Mah hayah sofo? Nachal chayei ha\u8217'olam haba} \u8212- his end was that he inherited the life of the World to Come. Similarly, {\i Reuven hodeh velo bosh mah hayah sofo nachal chayei ha\u8217'olam haba} \u8212- Reuven admitted his sin and was not embarrassed, and he too inherited the World to Come.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the rabbis are making use of the {\i pshat} reading of narratives that, from the perspective of {\i Torah she-be-al peh}, are generally understood more carefully. We are told in {\i Megillah} that the story of Reuven is not translated into {\i Targum} during public reading, precisely so that people should not be misled by the {\i pshat} understanding \u8212- which sounds as though Reuven literally slept with his stepmother, while {\i Chazal} maintain the incident was something less severe. Yet here, the Gemara is explicitly drawing on the {\i pshat} reading of these stories as emotionally powerful examples of repentance and admission of guilt. This reflects a broader tension in rabbinic thought about how to read {\i Tanach} and how much to engage with the personalities of its protagonists as real, flawed human beings capable of sin and {\i teshuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason these narratives are {\i einam kedai lishoman} \u8212- not suitable to be heard \u8212- is precisely because they reflect badly on great {\i tzaddikim} or on figures who appear prominently in the {\i Torah}. Yet they are told to the {\i sotah} anyway, because inspiring her to confess and repent overrides that concern. The {\i Rema} cites this principle in one of his {\i teshuvot}, noting that the concern of embarrassing a {\i talmid chakham} can be overridden in the context of a process aimed at eliciting confession and restoring {\i shalom bayit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judah\u8217's Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: we said both Yehuda and Reuven inherited {\i Olam Haba} \u8212- what further reward is there to discuss? {\i Ela mah secharan ba\u8217'olam hazeh} \u8212- what was their reward even in this world?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Reuven, the {\i pasuk lahem levadam nitnah ha\u8217'aretz} hints at his being among the tribes who received the first inheritance on the east side of the {\i Yarden}, a share in {\i Eretz Yisrael} that was his own. For Yehuda, we know he admitted his sin because the {\i pasuk} says explicitly: {\i vayakker Yehuda vayomer tzadkah mimmeni} \u8212- Yehuda recognized and said, \u8220"She is more righteous than I.\u8221" But how do we know that Reuven admitted his sin? The Gemara cites Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: what does it mean when Moshe blesses Reuven at the end of {\i Sefer Devarim}, saying {\i yechi Reuven ve\u8217'al yamot}, \u8220"Reuven shall live and not die\u8221"? And immediately following is the blessing for Yehuda, introduced with the unusual word {\i vezot} \u8212- a connection that the Gemara draws upon to link the two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presents a striking teaching: {\i kol otan shanim shehayu Yisrael bamidbar hayu atzmotav shel Yehuda mitgalgelin ba\u8217'aron} \u8212- throughout the forty years that the Jewish people were in the desert, the bones of Yehuda were rolling around in the ark, dislocated, until Moshe prayed on his behalf. The specific punishment was {\i eivarei deshapa} \u8212- his limbs were disjointed from their sockets \u8212- an image that Rashi connects to the {\i treifah} discussed in {\i Chullin} on daf 42b, {\i buka d\u8217'atma deshaph midukhtei}, the dislocated hip joint. Even in death, Yehuda was punished for his role in the episode with Tamar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What prompted Moshe\u8217's prayer? {\i Amar lefanav Ribbono shel Olam mi garam leReuven shehodeh} \u8212- who was it that caused Reuven to do {\i teshuvah}? It was Yehuda. Yehuda\u8217's character, his willingness to admit wrongdoing publicly, inspired Reuven\u8217's own {\i teshuvah}. And so it was not even Yehuda\u8217's own {\i teshuvah} directly that earned him this redemption, but the fact that his example had inspired another person to repent. This is a striking teaching about the ripple effects of genuine admission of sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then parses the blessing of Yehuda in {\i parashat v\u8217'zot habracha} as a series of remedies for the punishments Yehuda had suffered. {\i Shema Adonai kol Yehuda} \u8212- God heard Yehuda\u8217's cry, releasing him from his dislocated bones. {\i V\u8217'el amo tevi\u8217'enu} \u8212- bring him back to his people, meaning they finally allowed him into the {\i yeshivah} of heaven, the {\i metivta d\u8217'rakiya}, from which he had been excluded. {\i Yadav rav lo} \u8212- his hands were strengthened, corresponding to his now being permitted to participate in the {\i shakla v\u8217'tarya}, the back-and-forth of debate in the heavenly {\i beit midrash}, from which he had previously been barred even after gaining entry. And {\i v\u8217'ezer mizarav tihyeh} \u8212- let there be help against his enemies, meaning he could finally arrive at halakhic conclusions, {\i aliba d\u8217'hilkhata}, in his learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reuven\u8217's Public Apology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then returns to Reuven and poses a question. {\i Bishlama biYehuda ashkechan de\u8217'odei} \u8212- we understand why Yehuda had to make a public admission of his sin: {\i ki heichi d\u8217'lo tisoraf Tamar}, so that Tamar would not be burned. Yehuda had no choice but to speak publicly to save her life. But why did Reuven need to make a public admission? {\i Lama lei de\u8217'odei} \u8212- there was seemingly no practical purpose; no one\u8217's life was at stake. Moreover, does not Rav Sheshet say that it is brazen to publicize one\u8217's own sins, {\i chatzip alai diprat chataei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i ki heichi d\u8217'lo lyechshadu achvohi} \u8212- Reuven was concerned that if he did not publicly acknowledge what he had done, his brothers would fall under suspicion. Something had become known, but without knowing who was responsible, people might have suspected any of his brothers. By making a public declaration, he ensured that no one else would be considered {\i chashud} for that sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reflects the broader principle, noted especially in the {\i Rambam}, that for sins {\i bein adam lachavero}, there is an obligation to publicly acknowledge and apologize, because another person has been hurt and public rectification is part of the repair. For sins {\i bein adam lamakom}, by contrast, it is generally preferable to keep the matter private, between the person and God, without unnecessary public disclosure. Reuven\u8217's case was exceptional: the public nature of his admission was not self-serving, but was required specifically to protect his brothers from wrongful suspicion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Confession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Receipt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then transitions to the case in which the woman admits to having committed adultery. {\i Im amra tmea ani} \u8212- if she says she is impure, {\i shovrim ktubatah} \u8212- we break her {\i ketubah}. The question is what exactly this means. {\i Shmaat mina kotvim shovar} \u8212- does it mean we write a receipt acknowledging that the {\i ketubah} has been settled, leaving the original document in place? Or does it mean something else?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye proposes emending the reading: {\i tni mikrat}, we should read it as {\i koraat}, she tears up the {\i ketubah}. If you tear up the {\i shtar} itself, the instrument of debt is destroyed and cannot be claimed. Rava objects: {\i veha shoveret katani} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} uses the word {\i shoveret}, which implies breaking, not tearing. You cannot simply change the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore offers a different resolution: {\i bemakom she\u8217'ein kotvim ktubah askinan} \u8212- we are discussing a locale where it was not the practice to write a physical document for the {\i ketubah}. The {\i ketubah} obligation existed, but was conducted entirely orally. In such a case, there is no document to tear. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's language of {\i shovrim} then means that we write a receipt \u8212- a {\i shovar} \u8212- to formally dissolve her claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a noteworthy window into the historical development of the {\i ketubah} as an institution. The {\i sugyot} across {\i Shas} present a complex picture: in some places the {\i ketubah} appears {\i d\u8217'Oraita}, in others {\i d\u8217'Rabbanan}. The {\i Rambam} at the beginning of {\i Hilchot Ishut} formulates it strikingly, stating that the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kiddushin} requires marriage {\i bekiddushin uvektubah} \u8212- the {\i ketubah} is part of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kiddushin} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi at the bottom of the page notes two alternatives for how, in a place without a written {\i ketubah}, a woman would collect her entitlement. If divorced, {\i mishenitgarsha motzia gita vegova ba} \u8212- she brings the {\i get} itself as proof of her status and collects on the basis of that. If widowed, {\i u\u8217'chshenitalmena mevia edei mita vegova} \u8212- she must bring witnesses who testify to her husband\u8217's death before she can claim from his estate. This latter process is considerably more burdensome, requiring {\i edei mita} rather than simply producing a document. In a certain sense, one can trace in this the practical impetus for institutionalizing the written {\i ketubah}: the difficulty for a widow of having to produce testimony made it far simpler, ultimately, to give the woman a {\i shtar} she could hold in her hand.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eastern Gate Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nikanor Gate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i de-in amarta tehorah ani ma\u8217'alin ota le-sha\u8217'ar hamizrach} \u8212- if she says she is innocent, they bring her up to the eastern gate, which is the {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nikanor}, the great gate at the boundary between the {\i Ezrat Nashim} and the main {\i Azarah} of the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. This gate had fifteen steps ascending to it and two smaller doors on either side. It was the central point for those who had no reason to enter the {\i Azarah} itself \u8212- {\i Yisraelim}, women, and others \u8212- who nevertheless came to the {\i Beit Hamikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i ma\u8217'alin ota} \u8212- what does it mean they bring her up? {\i Hatam ka\u8217'ima} \u8212- she was already there, since the earlier stages of the ritual had already taken place at the {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nikanor}. The Gemara clarifies: {\i ma\u8217'alin ota} refers to bringing her up from the {\i Beit Din Hagadol she-biYerushalayim}, the {\i Lishkat Hagazit}, which straddles the boundary between the {\i Azarah} and the {\i Ezrat Nashim} \u8212- half {\i kodesh} and half {\i chol} \u8212- but which is also adjacent to the {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nikanor}. The Gemara notes that it is precisely here, {\i lifnei Hashem}, that the woman must be made to drink, as the {\i pasuk} requires: {\i ve-he\u8217'emid ha-kohen et ha-ishah lifnei Hashem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same location requirement applies to the {\i metzora}, whose purification process also requires the {\i kohen} to stand him at the entrance of the {\i Mishkan} or {\i Beit Hamikdash}: {\i ve-he\u8217'emid ha-kohen ha-mitaher}. The symbolic power of this is notable \u8212- the {\i metzora} is excluded even from walled cities, and then for his {\i taharah} he is brought back inside, to the very threshold of the sacred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Making Her Weary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then states: {\i ma\u8217'akimim ota u-mekifim ota kedei le-yage\u8217'ah} \u8212- they make her walk all around the {\i Har Habayit} in order to tire her out. The purpose is to wear her down so that, if she is guilty, she will be more likely to admit it rather than proceed to drink the water. Conversely, if she is genuinely innocent, the fact that she is willing to drink even after this exhausting ordeal demonstrates the sincerity of her claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tanna} draws an explicit comparison to the procedure in capital cases. Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar states: {\i Beit Din mesa\u8217'in et ha-edim mi-makom le-makom kedei she-titaref da\u8217'atan aleihem ve-yahzeru ba-hen} \u8212- the {\i beit din} makes witnesses in capital cases schlep from place to place in order to tire them out and induce them to reconsider whether they really wish to testify. The parallel is precise: in capital cases the witnesses may be putting an innocent person to death if they are lying, and we create disincentives for false testimony. At the same time, if they are telling the truth, they are absolutely obligated to testify \u8212- indeed, it is a great {\i mitzvah} to do so. The same dual dynamic applies to the {\i sotah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara also notes the broader question of who else must present themselves at the {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nikanor}. A {\i yoledet}, a woman who has given birth, must bring her {\i korban} there as well, by the rule that {\i ein korbano shel adam karev ela im ken omed al gabav} \u8212- a person\u8217's sacrifice cannot be offered unless that person is present. The Gemara raises {\i zavim} and {\i zavot} as additional examples of {\i mechusei kapparah}, and concludes that the {\i mishnah} simply listed one representative case \u8212- {\i tana chada mineihu nakat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Sotahs Together\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: {\i ein mashkin shtei sotos ke\u8217'achas} \u8212- one may not administer the {\i sotah} water to two women simultaneously. The concern expressed by the first opinion is {\i kedei shelo yehei libah gas bechavertah} \u8212- one woman should not be emboldened by the presence of the other. If two women are undergoing the ritual together and both are stubborn, each one may be emboldened by the other\u8217's stubbornness into proceeding to drink the water even if guilty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees not with the ruling but with its basis: {\i lo min hashem hu zeh ela amar kra osah levadah} \u8212- this is not derived from {\i sevara}, from logical reasoning, but directly from the {\i pasuk}, which says {\i osah levadah}, \u8220"her alone.\u8221" This is a {\i gezeirat hakatuv}. This constitutes a good example of a {\i machloket} in which both {\i tanna\u8217'im} agree on the {\i halakhah} but differ on whether its source is logical or scriptural. Notably, the Gemara treats a compelling {\i sevara} as carrying {\i d\u8217'Oraita} force \u8212- {\i tana kamma}, identified as Rabbi Shimon, holds that we {\i darshin ta\u8217'ama dekra}, we interpret the {\i pasuk} in light of its reasoning, so the {\i gezeirat hakatuv} is bounded by the logic behind it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} between the two opinions concerns a woman who is {\i rotetet} \u8212- particularly fearful and visibly trembling. According to Rabbi Shimon, such a woman could theoretically be made to drink alongside another {\i sotah}, since there is no concern that she would be emboldened by the other woman\u8217's presence. According to Rabbi Yehuda, the {\i gezeirat hakatuv} is absolute regardless of the circumstances. The {\i Tosafot HaRosh} adds that one would still need to account for the case where one woman is fearful but the other is not \u8212- the latter could still embolden the former, requiring the prohibition to cover both scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a broader principle: {\i ein osin mitzvos chavilos chavilos} \u8212- one may not bundle {\i mitzvot} together. Rashi explains that this is because doing so gives the appearance of treating {\i mitzvot} as a heavy burden one is trying to rush through, as though eager to discharge one\u8217's obligations and move on \u8212- {\i nire\u8217'eh kemo shehayah alav lemassoi u-memaher leforok masa\u8217'o}. {\i Tosafot} in {\i Moed Katan} offers a different and more positive framing: just as we do not combine one celebration with another \u8212- {\i ein me\u8217'arvin simcha besimcha} \u8212- so too each {\i mitzvah} deserves its own separate focus and its own {\i simcha shel mitzvah}. One of the {\i acharonim}, Rav Yosef Engel, notes an additional technical dimension: the principle of {\i osek bemitzvah patur min hamitzvah} \u8212- one who is occupied with a {\i mitzvah} is exempt from another {\i mitzvah} \u8212- may itself create a structural barrier to performing two {\i mitzvot} simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} lists further applications of {\i ein osin mitzvos chavilos chavilos}: two {\i metzoraim} cannot undergo the purification ritual simultaneously; two {\i avadim Ivrim} cannot have their ears pierced at the same time; two {\i agalot arufot} cannot be performed together. Notably, all of these examples involve performing two instances of the {\i same mitzvah} rather than two different {\i mitzvot} in parallel \u8212- an important distinction, since no one would suggest that one cannot {\i daven minchah} while simultaneously shaking a {\i lulav} simply because they are two different {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i machloket} over whether the piercing of a slave\u8217's ear is a {\i mitzvah} at all \u8212- and therefore subject to {\i ein osin mitzvos chavilos chavilos} \u8212- is itself debated by later authorities. The {\i Chazon Ish} holds it is a {\i reshut}, entirely optional, and the master could refuse even if the slave requests to remain; the {\i Magen Avraham} holds that once the slave requests to be {\i nirtza}, it becomes obligatory for the master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately resolves the apparent tension between the principle of {\i ein osin mitzvos chavilos chavilos} and the possibility of having two {\i sotahs} drink on the same day: {\i lo kashya kan bekohen echad kan beshnei kohanim} \u8212- there is no contradiction, since the prohibition applies to a single {\i kohen} who would be performing the ritual for two women. Two different {\i kohanim} could each administer the water to a different {\i sotah}, even at the same time, since neither {\i kohen} individually is bundling {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exposure & Humiliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncovering Hair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} cites the {\i pasuk} {\i u-farah et rosh ha-ishah} \u8212- the {\i kohen} unbraids or uncovers the woman\u8217's hair. The Gemara asks: from {\i roshah} alone, we know only about her head. From where do we derive that her body itself must also be exposed? The Gemara answers: the {\i pasuk} does not say merely {\i u-farah et roshah} but rather {\i u-farah et rosh ha-ishah} \u8212- the full word {\i ha-ishah}, \u8220"the woman,\u8221" is included, extending the obligation to expose her body as well. And if so, what does the specific mention of {\i roshah} teach? {\i Melamed shehakohen soter et se\u8217'arah} \u8212- it teaches that the {\i kohen} undoes and loosens her hair specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hirhurim Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda raises a consideration: what if the woman is particularly attractive? This brings into focus a broader question: how concerned are we about {\i hirhurim}, inappropriate sexual thoughts, among those witnessing the {\i sotah} ritual? {\i L\u8217'meimra de\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda chaish l\u8217'hirhur} \u8212- it would appear that Rabbi Yehuda is concerned about {\i hirhurim} arising from the public exposure of the {\i sotah}. And {\i v\u8217'rabbanan lo chaishi} \u8212- the Rabbanan are less concerned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Execution Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the Gemara notes that this apparent alignment seems to be precisely the reverse of what these same authorities hold elsewhere. In the context of execution by {\i skilah}, a {\i baraita} states that a man is stripped of all clothing except {\i perek echad milfanav} \u8212- one piece of cloth in front. A woman, by contrast, receives two pieces \u8212- {\i echad milfaneha v\u8217'echad milachareha} \u8212- because, as Rashi explains, {\i she\u8217'beit haboshet ne\u8217'erat mishnei tzadim}, a woman\u8217's private area is exposed from both sides and therefore requires coverage on both sides. But notably, even according to this {\i baraita}, a woman\u8217's entire torso would be uncovered during execution by {\i skilah} \u8212- indicating that the Rabbanan are not especially concerned about {\i hirhurim} in that context. Yet that is described as {\i divrei Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position. Meanwhile, {\i v\u8217'chachamim omrim ha\u8217'ish niskal arom, v\u8217'ein ha\u8217'ishah niskalet aroma} \u8212- the Sages say a man is stoned completely naked, but a woman is not stoned naked at all; she must be clothed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This presents a puzzling inversion: in the context of {\i sotah}, Rabbi Yehuda appears more protective of the woman from {\i hirhurim}, while in the context of {\i skilah} the Rabbanan are the ones who require that a woman not be exposed naked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raba\u8217's Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves both the internal contradiction within Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position and that within the Rabbanan\u8217's position simultaneously: {\i de-Rabbi Yehuda ad-Rabbi Yehuda lo kashya}, and {\i de-rabanan ad-rabanan nami lo kashya}. The concern in the {\i sotah} case is not {\i hirhurim} per se \u8212- it is {\i mishum venissaru kol hanashim}, because all women must witness the {\i sotah}\u8217's fate and be warned by her example. The humiliation of the {\i sotah} serves a broader deterrent and educational purpose. In the case of execution, however, {\i ein lecha yisur gadol mizeh} \u8212- there is no humiliation greater than death itself, and there is no need to add further degradation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava identifies a more specific concern driving the {\i sotah} case: {\i sh\u8217'ma totzi mibeit din zakai v\u8217'yitgaru bah pirchei kehunah} \u8212- perhaps she will leave the {\i sotah} ritual having been found innocent after drinking the water, and the young {\i kohanim} who are present in the {\i Azarah} ({\i metzuyin ba\u8217'azara}, as Rashi notes) will be stirred by inappropriate thoughts about her. If she has been exposed in public in the course of the ritual, these {\i kohanim} will have seen her, and that sight will provoke {\i hirhurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why not apply that concern to execution as well? {\i Hatam mistalka} \u8212- in the case of execution, she is gone; she has died. The {\i kohanim} will not see her again. {\i Gemiri d\u8217'ein yetzer hara sholet ela b\u8217'ma she\u8217'einav ro\u8217'ot} \u8212- it is a received principle that the {\i yetzer hara} only takes hold with respect to what a person\u8217's eyes have actually seen. Once she is dead, there is no ongoing exposure to provoke ongoing {\i hirhurim}. But in the {\i sotah} case, if she is found innocent, she returns to her husband and continues to live in the community, and the {\i kohanim} who saw her exposed will encounter her again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bru Lo Mitah Yafah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises the question of whether we should administer both punishments \u8212- expose her publicly {\i and} execute her \u8212- in the case of a guilty {\i sotah}. The answer draws on one of the most fundamental principles of rabbinic capital law: {\i ve\u8217'ahavta lere\u8217'acha kamocha beru lo mitah yafah} \u8212- from the verse \u8220"love your neighbor as yourself,\u8221" the rabbis derive that one must provide an appropriate death, a {\i mitah yafah}, even to one who is executed. This {\i drashah} is invoked repeatedly throughout {\i Sanhedrin} in connection with all four {\i mitot beit din}, consistently functioning to pull back from what the {\i Torah} might appear to mandate explicitly, in order to preserve the physical integrity of the human body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Professor Lorberbaum has argued, the underlying principle is {\i tzelem Elokim} \u8212- the human being possesses a physical form that reflects the divine image, and capital punishment must therefore be carried out in a manner that does not deform or disfigure the body. {\i Beru lo mitah yafah} does not necessarily mean the most pleasant death, but the death that leaves the body most intact and unspoiled. By this standard, adding public humiliation on top of execution would violate the principle \u8212- we cannot require someone to be both publicly exposed and put to death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pain vs.\u160 ?Humiliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses a specific {\i machloket} regarding what {\i mitah yafah} means in practice, directly relevant to the question of execution: {\i mar savar bizyona adif lah tfei mitza\u8217'ara d\u8217'gufa} \u8212- one opinion holds that emotional humiliation is worse than physical pain, and therefore being executed naked (which accelerates death by removing cushioning from the stones but is more humiliating) is actually the {\i better} death \u8212- more pain, less humiliation. {\i U-mar savar tza\u8217'ara d\u8217'gufa adif lei tfei mibizyonei} \u8212- the other opinion holds that physical pain is worse, and therefore being clothed at execution (which cushions the impact but prolongs death) is preferable \u8212- more humiliation, but less physical suffering. Both opinions apply the principle of {\i mitah yafah}, but they disagree about which factor is the dominant consideration in determining the most appropriate death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Removing Jewelry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states that if the woman was dressed beautifully \u8212- wearing white garments or fine jewelry \u8212- she is dressed in ugly or worn clothing for the ritual. The Gemara initially treats it as obvious ({\i pshita}) that her jewelry must be removed, since the entire purpose of the process is {\i nivvul}, to make her look degraded. But the Gemara raises a counterintuitive possibility: {\i behanei it lah bizyuna tfei} \u8212- perhaps leaving her jewelry on is actually {\i more} humiliating, because of the common proverb about a person who walks around naked but wearing shoes. That is, a figure who retains tokens of elegance and status while being otherwise degraded is in some respects more pitiable and embarrassing than one who is stripped completely. The Gemara concludes \u8212- {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- we do not apply this reasoning; the jewelry is removed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egyptian Rope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the tearing of the garment, the {\i kohen} brings a {\i chevel hamitzri}, an Egyptian rope, and ties it above her breasts to prevent her clothing from falling down entirely. The {\i Yerushalmi} offers two explanations for why specifically an Egyptian rope: either because her conduct recalled the licentiousness of Egypt, {\i k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh eretz mitzrayim}, or alternatively the term is not {\i chevel mitzri} but {\i chevel matzui} \u8212- a common rope woven from palm fronds, rough and coarse, which was the standard material of the time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records a question posed by Rav Abba to Rav Huna: is the {\i chevel hamitzri} {\i me\u8217'akev} \u8212- is it an indispensable part of the {\i sotah} ritual, without which the process is invalid? Two possible explanations for its use are considered. If the reason is simply {\i shelo yishm\u8217'tu bgadeha me\u8217'aleha}, so that her clothes do not fall down, then even a small cord would suffice and the specific Egyptian rope would not be essential. But if the reason is rooted in {\i middah keneged middah}, corresponding to her having provocatively girded herself, then the specific rope carries substantive significance and might be {\i me\u8217'akev}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the question by citing a more expanded version of the {\i mishnah}, a {\i baraita}, which makes explicit that the reason for the rope is purely functional \u8212- {\i k\u8217'dei shelo yishm\u8217'tu bgadeha me\u8217'aleha} \u8212- so that her garments do not fall. Since the reason is purely practical, the specific Egyptian rope is not {\i me\u8217'akev}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} at the top of the following page raises an additional question prompted by the {\i Yerushalmi}: the rope is purchased from {\i mishirei halishkah}, public temple funds. This raises a parallel question about other instruments of capital punishment \u8212- the sword used for beheading, the cord used for {\i chenek} \u8212- whether those too are purchased with public funds, and how far the parallel between {\i sotah} and {\i mitat beit din} extends.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses to Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Resolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that the {\i mishnah} appears to contradict itself. On one hand it states {\i ve-chol ha-rotzeh lir\u8217'ot bah yera\u8217'eh} \u8212- whoever wishes to see may come and see, with no distinction between men and women. On the other hand, the {\i mishnah} also states {\i kol ha-nashim mutarot lir\u8217'otah} \u8212- all women are permitted to see her, implying that women yes, men no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye attempts to resolve the contradiction by suggesting {\i targemah anashim} \u8212- that even the first clause refers only to women. Rava objects: {\i ve-ha kol ha-rotzeh lir\u8217'ot bah yera\u8217'eh} \u8212- but the first clause clearly says \u8220"whoever wishes,\u8221" which is inclusive language, not restricted to women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Obligated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a different resolution: {\i kol ha-rotzeh lir\u8217'ot bah ro\u8217'eh lo shena gavrei ve-lo shena nashei} \u8212- whoever wishes to come and see, whether men or women, may come and see. Both are permitted. The second clause, however, conveys something more: {\i nashim chayavot lir\u8217'otah} \u8212- women are not merely permitted but {\i obligated} to attend.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Lesson\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obligation for women to witness the {\i sotah} ritual is derived from the {\i pasuk}: {\i ve-nissaru kol ha-nashim ve-lo ta\u8217'aseina ke-zimatchanah} \u8212- all women are to be chastened and warned so that they do not engage in the same {\i zimah}, the same prohibited sexual behavior, that the {\i sotah} may have committed. Women must be present specifically so that they will internalize the lesson and be deterred from similar conduct. Men are permitted to attend but are not obligated; women are not merely permitted but are required to come and witness.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Middah Keneged Middah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Measure for Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the chapter introduces the principle of {\i middah keneged middah} \u8212- the manner in which a person conducts himself or herself is precisely the measure by which God responds. {\i Be-middah she-adam moded bah modedin lo.} The {\i sotah}\u8217's punishment corresponds exactly to her sin. {\i Hi kishtah atzmah la-aveirah, ha-Makom nivvelah} \u8212- she adorned herself with jewelry for the sake of her sin; God therefore takes away her beauty. {\i Hi giltah et atzmah la-aveirah, ha-Makom gilah aleiha} \u8212- she revealed herself for her sin; God therefore reveals her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order of Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} specifies the sequence of the {\i sotah}\u8217's physical punishment: {\i be-yarech hitchilah ba-aveirah} \u8212- she began her sin with her thigh, a euphemism for her genitalia. {\i Tcilah ve-achar kach ha-beten} \u8212- and then her womb, corresponding to the concern about pregnancy from the adulterous relationship. Therefore, {\i lokah ha-yarech tcilah ve-achar kach ha-beten} \u8212- the punishment begins with the thigh and only then affects the womb. {\i Ve-sha\u8217'ar kol ha-guf lo palat} \u8212- the rest of the body is entirely unaffected by the {\i sotah} water; only those specific parts of the body that were implicated in the sin are punished. The Gemara will elaborate on these {\i pesukim} and their implications in the discussion that continues on daf 8b.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Warning & God\u8217's Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Warnings & Erasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens on 7b with the phrase {\i uma\u8217'amin aleha} \u8212- the {\i beit din} impresses upon the {\i sotah} the gravity of what she is about to undergo. The {\i mishnah} had already stated that they warn her just as they warn witnesses in capital cases, and the reason given is {\i asur lishmo hagadol shenikhtav bikdusha shelo yimacheh al hamayim} \u8212- it is forbidden to erase God\u8217's name, which has been written in holiness, upon the water for no reason. The {\i megillas sotah} is inscribed with God\u8217's name, and the ink is then scraped into the bitter water. This is, in one sense, one of the most striking features of the {\i sotah} ritual: God is willing to have His name erased in order to restore {\i shalom bayit} to a couple. Nevertheless, erasing God\u8217's name carries a genuine {\i issur}, and so the {\i beit din} works strenuously to avoid bringing about that erasure unnecessarily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drinking Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises what appears to be a contradiction. A {\i baraita} from the {\i Tosefta} states that just as we scare her out of drinking, so too we scare her into drinking \u8212- {\i kederech shema\u8217'amin aleha shelo tishteh kach ma\u8217'amin aleha shetishteh}. This seems paradoxical: if the entire purpose of the warnings is to prevent the erasure of God\u8217's name, how can it also be our goal to encourage her to drink?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water as Medicine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} resolves this by explaining that if she is certain of her innocence, she is told: {\i omrim lah bitti im barur lach hadavar shetehorab at omdi al boryich ushti} \u8212- if it is entirely clear to you that you are pure, stand firm and drink. The water, the {\i baraita} explains, is like a dry medicine placed upon raw flesh: {\i lefi she\u8217'ein mayim hame\u8217'arerim domim ela lesam yavesh shemunach al basar chai}. If there is a wound, the medicine dissolves into it and takes effect; if there is no wound, it does nothing at all. The implication is clear: if she is innocent, the water will have no effect and the ritual will demonstrate her innocence. If she is guilty, the ritual will run its course as designed. The {\i gemara} thus resolves the contradiction: there are two distinct stages of the ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before/After Megillah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the {\i megillas sotah} has been erased, the goal is to encourage the woman to confess so that God\u8217's name need not be effaced at all. After the {\i megillah} has already been erased, that concern is moot \u8212- God\u8217's name is already gone \u8212- and it would now be wasteful and purposeless for her to refuse to drink. At that point, she is instead encouraged to drink: the water will prove her innocent if she is indeed innocent, and the erasure of God\u8217's name will have served its purpose. These are different encouragements suited to different moments in the proceeding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Judah & Reuben\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aggadic Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} had said that they tell her {\i devarim she\u8217'einam kedai lishmo\u8217'an} \u8212- things not fit to be heard \u8212- in order to move her emotionally. The {\i gemara} explains: {\i tanu rabbanan omer lefaneha devarim shel aggada uma\u8217'asim she\u8217'iru baksuvim harishonim} \u8212- they tell her {\i aggadot} and stories that occurred in earlier generations. The specific source cited is from {\i Iyov}: {\i asher chachamim yaggidu velo kichadu me\u8217'avosam} \u8212- that the wise of earlier times would tell of these things and not conceal them from their ancestors. As {\i Rashi} explains, these are things written in the {\i Torah} about events of ancient times \u8212- stories in which great and wise people were able to acknowledge their sins openly. The phrase {\i devarim she\u8217'einam kedai lishmo\u8217'an} refers to the fact that, under normal circumstances, one would not publicize these stories, as they could be embarrassing to figures of great stature whose full standing the tradition wishes to protect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judah & Reuben\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two figures are given as examples. {\i Yehuda hoda velo bosh} \u8212- Judah admitted his sin and was not embarrassed. {\i Mah haya sofo? Nachal chayei olam haba} \u8212- and what came of it? He inherited a share in the {\i olam haba}. Similarly, {\i Reuven hoda velo bosh, mah haya sofo? Nachal chayei olam haba} \u8212- Reuben admitted his sin and was not embarrassed, and he too inherited the {\i olam haba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peshat & Derash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage is striking because the {\i gemara} here goes against what the tradition generally maintains in other contexts. As we know from Tractate {\i Megilla}, the rabbinic view \u8212- at least from the perspective of {\i Torah she-be\u8217'al peh} \u8212- tends to be that Judah and Reuben did not actually commit the transgressions that the {\i peshat} of the {\i Torah} seems to imply. When the {\i parshiyot} dealing with Reuben are read publicly, the {\i targum} is withheld, so that listeners will not be misled by the surface reading \u8212- for the {\i peshat} of the story of Reuben suggests that he literally violated his stepmother, while {\i Chazal} understand it as something considerably less severe. Yet here the {\i gemara} reads these stories according to their {\i peshat} in order to draw a moral lesson \u8212- that great people can admit wrongdoing. The reason these words are {\i she\u8217'einam kedai lishmo\u8217'an} is precisely because they are potentially embarrassing to figures who are elsewhere treated as towering {\i tzaddikim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to the broader contemporary debate about approaching figures in {\i Tanach begova ha-einayim} \u8212- relating to biblical personalities as fully realized human beings \u8212- and how much that is permissible from the rabbinic perspective. The story of Judah and Tamar raises precisely this question: in the {\i peshat}, Judah says {\i tzadka mimeni}, acknowledging that Tamar was right and he was wrong. The {\i gemara} here appears to authorize reading the story as it reads, even if other contexts demand a more interpretive layer, in order to achieve the goal of inspiring the {\i sotah} to confess. The {\i Rema} in one of his {\i teshuvot} cites this passage as a proof that even embarrassing a {\i talmid chacham} can be part of what is legitimately permitted in the broader context of the {\i sotah} process \u8212- bringing people to confession and restoring {\i shalom bayit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reuben\u8217's Repentance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that Judah admitted his sin, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i ela Reuven menalan de\u8217'hodi} \u8212- how do we know that Reuben admitted to having sinned? The proof comes from {\i Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani} citing {\i Rabbi Yochanan}, who reads the connection between {\i yechi Reuven ve\u8217'al yamot} in Moses\u8217's blessing to the tribes at the end of {\i Devarim} and {\i vezot liYehudah}, which follows immediately. {\i Rashi} notes that the blessing of Judah is the only one that opens with the unusual word {\i vezot}, suggesting a textual linkage between Reuben\u8217's life-and-death fate and Judah\u8217's admission. The {\i gemara} understands this connection to mean that Reuben\u8217's fate depended upon Judah\u8217's own repentance and what it inspired.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judah\u8217's Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then describes what Judah suffered in the {\i midbar}. All forty years that {\i Bnei Yisrael} wandered in the desert, Judah\u8217's bones were rolling around in his coffin \u8212- {\i kol otan shanim she\u8217'hayu Yisrael bamidbar hayu atzmotav shel Yehuda mitgalglim ba\u8217'aron} \u8212- until Moses stood and prayed for him. Moses argued before God: {\i mi garam l\u8217'Reuven she\u8217'hoda} \u8212- who was it that inspired Reuben to do {\i teshuvah}? It was Judah. Thus Judah was rewarded not because of his own {\i teshuvah} alone, but because his character and his admission inspired Reuben to do {\i teshuvah} as well. That is why the next phrase in the blessing is {\i shema Adonai kol Yehudah} \u8212- God hears Judah\u8217's prayer and responds, releasing him from his posthumous suffering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What was the precise nature of that suffering? {\i Rashi} here connects {\i evarai deshaffa} \u8212- his limbs were dislocated \u8212- to the {\i treifah} discussed in {\i Chullin} 52b, {\i buka de\u8217'atma deshaf biduchteh}, the hip socket displaced from the hip joint. The punishment of forty years of rolling bones was understood as a consequence of whatever connection existed between Judah and his conduct regarding Tamar \u8212- even as he had acknowledged his failing and been forgiven. {\i Tosafot} in the {\i gemara} raises the question of why Judah was still being punished for forty years, and {\i Rashi} connects it to a {\i niduy al tenai} \u8212- some form of conditional {\i cherem} \u8212- though this requires further investigation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the rolling bones, Judah suffered additional penalties. {\i Velo hava ka\u8217'aylin lei lemtivta dirakia} \u8212- they would not admit him into the heavenly {\i yeshiva}. Even once he was admitted, {\i velo hava ka yada mishkal bishmayta bahadei rabanan} \u8212- he could not participate in the {\i shakla vetarya}, the back-and-forth of halakhic debate with the {\i rabbanim} in heaven. And then, {\i velo hava kasalik lei shemayta aliba dehilkhata} \u8212- he could not bring his {\i sugyot} to their proper halakhic conclusion. Moses\u8217's blessing {\i ve\u8217'ezer mitsarav tihyeh} \u8212- that the {\i Torah} should be a protection from his enemies \u8212- is read as the remedy for this final deficiency, enabling Judah to learn {\i sugyot aliba dehilkhata} at last.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reuben\u8217's Motivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then distinguishes between Judah\u8217's case and Reuben\u8217's. {\i Bishlama Yehuda de\u8217'odi kehikhi delo tishraf Tamar} \u8212- Judah had an obvious reason to admit publicly: if he had not done so, Tamar would have been burned. But what motivated Reuben to confess? {\i Ve\u8217'amar Rav Sheshet}: {\i chatsifa alai demafrat chatatei kehikhi delo tishdu achohi} \u8212- he was concerned that if he did not publicly admit what he had done, his brothers would be suspected. Under normal circumstances, he might have gone to Jacob privately, but since others who knew something had happened would not know who was responsible, Reuben had to make a public declaration to clear his brothers\u8217' names.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public vs.\u160 ?Private\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a broader principle. The {\i Rambam}, drawing on the {\i gemara}, distinguishes between {\i mitzvot bein adam lachaveiro} and {\i mitzvot bein adam laMakom} when it comes to the question of public versus private apology. For sins between a person and another person, one must publicize the apology, because another person was genuinely hurt and public acknowledgment is part of making it right. For sins between a person and God, it is preferable to keep the matter private \u8212- there is no reason to publicize a transgression of that kind, and doing so only adds another dimension of exposure without any remedial benefit. The case of Reuben illustrates that even when a public apology might magnify the problem in some ways, the concern of allowing others to remain under unjust suspicion can override that consideration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Breaking Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the practical consequences if the woman admits to adultery during the {\i sotah} proceeding. {\i Im amart tameia ani} \u8212- if she says she is impure, {\i shomein sheovrim ketubatah} \u8212- the {\i gemara} infers that we break her {\i ketubah}. But what does {\i shovrim} mean here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shma mina kotvim shovar} \u8212- one might think it means we write a receipt canceling the obligation of the {\i ketubah}. {\i Amar Abaye tani mikra} \u8212- Abaye says we should emend the {\i mishnah} to read {\i korei\u8217'a}, meaning we simply tear up the {\i ketubah} document itself. If a physical {\i shtar} is torn up, there is no way for the holder to claim the debt it represents, and the matter is closed. The question of which approach is better turns on where the burden lies: does the husband need a receipt proving the {\i ketubah} was discharged, or is it sufficient to simply destroy the document? {\i Amar lei Rava} \u8212- Rava objects that the {\i mishnah} says {\i shoveret}, not {\i koriat}, and one cannot simply amend a {\i mishnah}. Therefore the {\i mishnah} must be read as written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela amar Rava bimkom she\u8217'ein kotvim ketubah askinan} \u8212- Rava resolves the issue by saying the {\i mishnah} is discussing a case where no physical {\i ketubah} document was written at all. The {\i ketubah} obligation was undertaken orally. In such a case, there is no document to tear up, and so {\i shovrim} \u8212- breaking the {\i ketubah} \u8212- means writing some form of receipt or acknowledgment that the obligation has been discharged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Document Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} at the end of this passage offers two related scenarios in which a woman might claim her {\i ketubah} without a physical document. In a case of divorce, {\i mishenitagresha motzi\u8217'ah gittah ve\u8217'govah bah} \u8212- she produces the {\i get} itself as proof of the divorce and uses it to collect her {\i ketubah}. In a case of widowhood, {\i mevi\u8217'ah edei mitah ve\u8217'govah} \u8212- she brings witnesses who can testify to her husband\u8217's death, and on that basis she collects from the estate. The latter is a considerably more burdensome process, reflecting the general principle of {\i hamotzi mechaveiro alav hara\u8217'ayah} \u8212- that the one seeking to extract money from another bears the burden of proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can trace in these cases the historical development of the {\i ketubah} document itself. At an earlier stage, the {\i ketubah} was apparently an oral obligation, and the woman had to marshal independent evidence \u8212- witnesses to the divorce or death \u8212- in order to collect. The rabbis subsequently instituted the practice of writing a physical {\i ketubah} document precisely to ease this burden, enabling the woman to collect simply by presenting the {\i shtar} she held in her hand.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ritual Locations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eastern Gate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i de\u8217'imar amart tehorah ani ma\u8217'alin osah lisha\u8217'arei mizrach} \u8212- if she says she is pure, they bring her up to the eastern gate, which is the {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nicanor}, the great gate standing between the {\i Ezrat Nashim} and the main {\i Azarah} of the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. The {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nicanor} featured two smaller doors on either side and fifteen steps ascending to it, and it constituted the central meeting point for those who had reason to be in the {\i Beit Hamikdash} but not in the {\i Azarah} proper \u8212- including women and other {\i Yisraelim} who gathered in the {\i Ezrat Nashim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what does {\i ma\u8217'alin osah} mean \u8212- she was already there? The answer is that she had first been brought to the {\i Beit Din Hagadol}, the {\i Lishkat Hagazit}, which straddles the border between the {\i Azarah} and the {\i Ezrat Nashim}. From there she is brought up to the {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nicanor} for the actual drinking of the water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Walking Around\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Umakkifin osah kedei l\u8217'yage\u8217'ah} \u8212- they make her walk all around the {\i Har Habayit} to tire her out. A weary woman, if guilty, will be more inclined to admit it; and a woman who drinks the water despite exhaustion demonstrates more convincingly that she genuinely believes herself innocent. The {\i gemara} draws a parallel to a {\i baraita}: {\i Rebbe Shimon ben Elazar omer beit din masin es ha\u8217'edim mimakom lemakom kedei shetitaref da\u8217'atan aleihem veyachzeru bahen} \u8212- the {\i beit din} also makes witnesses in capital cases travel from place to place in order to wear them down. The goal is to create a genuine disincentive to false testimony \u8212- if you are lying, make it hard enough that you will reconsider \u8212- while simultaneously affirming that if you are telling the truth, it is a great {\i mitzvah} to do so regardless of the difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Where She Drinks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} establishes that the {\i sotah} drinks at the {\i Sha\u8217'ar Hamizrach}. The {\i gemara} asks: is this rule also applied to the {\i metzora} and the {\i yoledet}? For the {\i sotah}, the {\i pasuk} is explicit: {\i veha\u8217'emid hakohen es ha\u8217'ishah lifnei Adonai} \u8212- it must be done before God, meaning at the entrance of the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. For the {\i metzora}, the {\i pasuk} similarly says {\i veha\u8217'emid hakohen hamitaher} \u8212- the {\i kohen} must stand the {\i metzora} at the entrance of the {\i Mishkan}, and so the same location applies. The symbolism here is powerful: the {\i metzora} is excluded even from walled cities during the period of impurity and must dwell outside, and then the {\i taharah} process brings him back inside, to the very threshold of the sacred precincts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yoledet & Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What about the {\i yoledet}? The {\i gemara} asks: if the reason a {\i yoledet} must appear at the {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nicanor} is simply {\i de\u8217'ika mikaima akorbaneihu} \u8212- that anyone bringing a {\i korban} must be present for its offering \u8212- then by the same logic, {\i zavin vezavot} should also be listed. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hachi nami vetana chada mineihu nakat} \u8212- indeed the same rule applies to them as well; the {\i mishnah} simply listed one representative example, the {\i yoledet}, but intended all {\i mechusarei kaparah}. {\i Tosafot} in multiple places in {\i Shas} grapples with the general problem of {\i mishnayot} that appear to make sweeping inclusive or exclusive statements but in practice admit of many exceptions. The principle of {\i tana veshayer} \u8212- the {\i tanna} left out cases \u8212- is one of the standard resolutions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Sotahs Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Two Together\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i baraita}: {\i ein mashkim shtei sotot k\u8217'achat} \u8212- one cannot give two {\i sotot} the water simultaneously. The stated reason is {\i kedei shelo yehei libah gas bachaverta} \u8212- so that she should not become emboldened by the presence of another {\i sotah} alongside her. If two women who are both guilty were made to drink together, each might draw a kind of stubborn courage from the other and refuse to confess, even to the point of drinking the water despite her guilt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe from this rule that the {\i sotah} ritual is not deferred until the {\i regalim}. If it were, one would likely accumulate multiple {\i sotot} and be tempted to process them together \u8212- which is precisely what this rule prohibits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Judah\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehudah omer lo min hashem hu zeh ela amar kra otah levadah} \u8212- Rabbi Judah disagrees with the reason. He says the rule is not grounded in the {\i sevara} of {\i libah gas bachaverta} at all. Rather, it is a {\i gezeirat hakatuv}: the {\i pasuk} simply says {\i otah levadah}, just her alone, and that suffices. This is a meaningful {\i machloket}: both {\i tanna\u8217'im} agree on the {\i halacha}, but they disagree about its source. The {\i tanna kama} treats the rule as a {\i sevara} \u8212- a logical inference \u8212- and Rabbi Judah treats it as a direct {\i pasuk}. As the {\i gemara} then clarifies, {\i vetana kama hinu Rabbi Shimon dedarish ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'kra} \u8212- the {\i tanna kama} is Rabbi Shimon, who agrees the {\i pasuk} says {\i otah levadah} but reads the {\i pasuk\u8217's} limitation as deriving from and coextensive with the underlying logic. This reflects a broader {\i machloket} between Rabbi Judah and Rabbi Shimon throughout {\i Shas} regarding {\i darshinan ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'kra} \u8212- whether understanding the reason behind a {\i pasuk} can expand or contract the parameters of the {\i halacha} derived from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fearful Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai beinaihu? Ika beinaihu rotetet} \u8212- what is the practical difference between the two positions? The case of a woman who is particularly fearful and trembling about the {\i sotah} water. According to Rabbi Shimon, whose reason is {\i libah gas bachaverta}, a {\i rotetet} woman would not be emboldened by the presence of another {\i sotah} \u8212- her extreme fear would override any such effect \u8212- and so two {\i sotot} could theoretically be processed together in such a case. According to Rabbi Judah, the {\i pasuk} says {\i otah levadah} regardless, and the prohibition is absolute. {\i Tosafot HaRosh} raises the question of what happens when one of the pair is {\i rotetet} but the other is not: even then, one might worry that the more composed woman will embolden the other, and therefore we remain {\i choshesh} in that case as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bundling Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: and can one really make a {\i rotetet} woman drink at all? And then it challenges even more fundamentally: {\i vaha ein osin mitzvot chavilot chavilot} \u8212- we have a principle that one may not bundle {\i mitzvot} together. Even without the specific concern of {\i libah gas bachaverta}, performing two {\i sotah} rituals simultaneously would violate this broader rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} explains the reason for {\i ein osin mitzvot chavilot chavilot}: it appears as though the {\i mitzvot} are a heavy burden that one rushes to discharge. {\i Shenireh k\u8217'mo shehayah alav l\u8217'masoy umemaher l\u8217'farek masao} \u8212- it looks like someone hurrying to unload a heavy pack. Performing {\i mitzvot} hastily and in bulk conveys that they are merely obligations to be disposed of, rather than things of genuine value to be cherished. {\i Tosafot} in {\i Moed Katan} offers a different rationale: just as {\i ein me\u8217'arvin simcha b\u8217'simcha} \u8212- we do not hold a wedding on {\i Chol HaMoed} because we want each {\i simcha} to receive its own full attention \u8212- so too each {\i mitzvah} deserves its own dedicated focus and its own {\i simcha shel mitzvah}. One of the {\i Achronim}, {\i Rav Yosef Engel}, adds yet another conceptual lens: the principle of {\i osek b\u8217'mitzvah patur min hamitzvah} means that performing one {\i mitzvah} technically exempts one from another, and therefore bundling two {\i mitzvot} together may be not merely inadvisable but technically impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bundling Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} lists additional cases: {\i ein metaherin shtei metzoraim k\u8217'achas} \u8212- one cannot perform the purification ritual for two {\i metzoraim} simultaneously. {\i Ein rotzin shtei avadim k\u8217'achas} \u8212- one cannot pierce the ears of two {\i eved Ivri} together at the same ceremony. An {\i eved Ivri} who, after six years of service, declares {\i ahavti es adoni} and wishes to continue as a slave undergoes a formal ear-piercing, with the {\i pasuk} stating {\i va\u8217'avado l\u8217'olam}. A {\i machloket} exists between the {\i Chazon Ish} and the {\i Magen Avraham} about the nature of this: the {\i Chazon Ish} holds that even when the slave seeks to remain, the master may refuse, making it entirely a {\i reshut}; the {\i Magen Avraham} holds that once the slave actively wishes to be {\i nirtza}, it becomes obligatory for the master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ein orfin shtei eglos k\u8217'achas} \u8212- one cannot perform two {\i egla arufa} rituals simultaneously. While it would be tragic indeed for a city to require two such rituals at once, if it did, each would have to be performed separately. The {\i baraita} concludes: {\i lefi she\u8217'ein osin mitzvot chavilot chavilot}. It is worth noting that all the examples in this {\i baraita} involve doing two instances of the {\i same} mitzvah at once \u8212- not two different {\i mitzvot} performed together. The concern is not that one cannot daven and shake a {\i lulav} simultaneously; it is the ceremonial repetition, the bundling of identical solemn procedures, each of which demands its own dedicated attention and process. The {\i eved Ivri} must be brought before the court, the {\i egla arufa} requires traveling out to a remote location \u8212- these are elaborate, ceremonial proceedings that cannot be rushed together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye ve\u8217'isayma Rav Kahana: lo kashya kan b\u8217'kohen echad kan bishnei kohanim} \u8212- the resolution is that a single {\i kohen} cannot administer the water to two {\i sotot}, but two {\i sotot} could theoretically be processed on the same day \u8212- perhaps even simultaneously \u8212- by two different {\i kohanim}, since in that case each {\i kohen} is performing a single, undivided act and there is no violation of {\i mitzvot chavilot chavilot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sotah Clothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tearing Clothes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} states: {\i vekohen ochez b\u8217'vgadeha} \u8212- the {\i kohen} holds onto her clothing. The procedure described in the {\i mishnah} involves tearing open her garments and then securing them with an Egyptian rope above her breasts. The {\i gemara} asks about this in relation to concerns of {\i hirhurin} \u8212- inappropriate sexual thoughts \u8212- that the ritual might occasion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stoning Clothes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lemimra d\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda chaish l\u8217'hirhur} \u8212- is Rabbi Judah concerned about {\i hirhur} while the {\i Rabbanan} are not? The {\i gemara} notes that this seems to be inverted from what we know elsewhere. A {\i baraita} states: when a man is executed by {\i skilah}, he is covered with {\i perek echad milfanav} \u8212- a single cloth in front. A woman, however, receives {\i shenai perakim}, one in front and one behind, {\i mipnei shekulah ervah} \u8212- and as {\i Rashi} explains, this is because {\i beit haboshet nireh mishenei tzedadin}, a woman\u8217's genitalia are exposed from two directions, requiring coverage on both sides. But in that ruling, the woman\u8217's entire torso would still be uncovered \u8212- and that is the position attributed to {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. {\i Vachachamim omrim ha\u8217'ish niskal arom ve\u8217'ein ha\u8217'ishah niskalet aromah} \u8212- the {\i Rabbis}, by contrast, hold that a man is stoned completely naked while a woman may not be stoned naked at all. So it appears that Rabbi Judah is {\i less} concerned about exposure and the {\i Rabbanan} are {\i more} concerned \u8212- the opposite of what one might have expected from the {\i sotah} context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priestly Thoughts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava hacha ta\u8217'ama mai shema teitzi mibeit din zakai veyigaru bah pirchei kehuna} \u8212- Rava explains the concern in the {\i sotah} case differently. The worry is not about inappropriate thoughts during the ritual itself, but about afterward: if she leaves the {\i beit din} proven innocent, the young {\i kohanim} who are {\i metzuyin ba\u8217'azarah} \u8212- present in the {\i azarah} \u8212- will have seen her in a state of exposure and may develop inappropriate thoughts about her. An interesting related issue in later {\i sugyot} concerns whether a {\i kohen hedyot} who is not actively performing the {\i avodah} may remain in the {\i azarah} wearing {\i bigdei kehuna} if the {\i avneit} is woven of {\i shatnez}, and various {\i Achronim} address how to navigate the ensuing questions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yetzer Hara Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vachi teima asi l\u8217'itgaru bah acharinsa} \u8212- but one might argue that even if she leaves fully clothed, these {\i kohanim} might pursue her anyway, so what is accomplished by keeping her clothed? {\i Amar Rava gemiri de\u8217'ein yetzer hara sholeit ela bamah she\u8217'einav ro\u8217'os} \u8212- Rava answers with a received principle: the {\i yetzer hara} has power only over what one\u8217's eyes have actually seen. A person does not develop inappropriate thoughts about someone they have not seen. {\i Rashi} explains the concern more precisely: the worry is that seeing her could cause these {\i kohanim} to entertain inappropriate thoughts about other women \u8212- thoughts kindled by what they have witnessed. {\i Rava} responds that this is not how the {\i yetzer hara} operates: one\u8217's illicit impulse is directed toward the specific person one has seen, not transferred toward others not seen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beautiful Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vachi teima la\u8217'avi bei tartei} \u8212- the {\i gemara} then asks: why not do both, that is, expose her in public {\i and} execute her if she is found guilty? {\i Amar Rav Nachman amar Rabba bar Avuha amar kra veahavta lere\u8217'acha kamocha beror lo mita yafa} \u8212- the principle of {\i mita yafa}, choosing the most appropriate death, derived from the verse {\i veahavta lere\u8217'acha kamocha}, governs all four {\i mitot beit din}. The {\i Rabbis} apply this {\i derasha} throughout {\i Sanhedrin} to scale back what the {\i Torah\u8217's} language might seem to imply and to ensure that capital punishment is carried out in a manner that preserves the physical integrity of the human body. Professor Lorberbaum has argued that the core rabbinic principle underlying capital punishment is the concept of {\i tzelem Elokim}: since the human form bears some representation of the divine, execution must be performed in a way that does not deform or despoil the body. {\i Mita yafa} thus means not necessarily the most pleasant death, but the death that leaves the body most intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: even granting that the woman who drinks the water and is found guilty deserves her punishment, does she deserve additional public humiliation? This is genuinely difficult. One consideration is that even a woman ultimately innocent of adultery has still violated her husband\u8217's explicit instructions by entering into {\i yichud} with the suspected man, with witnesses able to testify to it \u8212- so in some sense she has betrayed a responsibility of her marriage. But for a woman who is guilty, there remains the question of {\i mita yafa} placing a limit on how much additional shame can be imposed alongside the physical punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pain vs.\u160 ?Shame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lima deRav Nachman tannai hi} \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks whether {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's principle is itself subject to a {\i tannaic} dispute. {\i Mar savar bizyona adif lah tfei mitza\u8217'ara degufa} \u8212- one position holds that public embarrassment is preferable to physical pain, so a woman being executed should be unclothed, which causes the stones to strike more directly and kill her more quickly, reducing physical suffering even at the cost of greater shame. {\i Umar savar tza\u8217'ara degufa adif lei tfei mibizyonei} \u8212- the other position holds that physical pain is the greater burden, and therefore it is better to clothe the condemned person, reducing the velocity of death even if that means greater physical suffering, in order to spare them the humiliation of nakedness. Both positions invoke {\i mita yafa}; they simply disagree about what that requires when shame and pain pull in opposite directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rope & Clothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then addresses what happens if her clothes are particularly striking. {\i Im hayu shechorim na\u8217'in la mechassin ota bivgadim mecho\u8217'arim} \u8212- if she looks particularly attractive in black garments, they dress her in ugly or coarse clothing instead, so as not to attract attention or inspire {\i hirhurim} in those who see her. Similarly, {\i hayu aleha klei zahav} \u8212- if she is wearing gold jewelry, is that removed? One might think that jewelry would make it even {\i more} embarrassing for her \u8212- as the folk saying goes, {\i shlach artil uremi masanei}, a person walking around naked but wearing shoes is somehow more embarrassing than being entirely undressed, because the incongruity itself is humiliating. A woman going through the {\i sotah} ritual in her finest jewelry, stripped of her dignity but still wearing her gold, might be even more shamed by that contrast. The {\i gemara} therefore teaches that we do not apply that reasoning, and the jewelry is removed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then, {\i achar kach mevi chevel hamitzri} \u8212- the {\i kohen} brings the Egyptian rope and ties it above her breasts so that her clothing, though torn open, does not fall down entirely. The {\i Yerushalmi} explains the term {\i chevel mitzri} either because her conduct resembled {\i ke-ma\u8217'aseh eretz Mitzrayim}, or alternatively because {\i chevel matzui} \u8212- a common rope woven from palm fronds \u8212- was the standard coarse rope of the region.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egyptian Rope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question is posed by {\i Rav Abba} to {\i Rav Huna}: is the {\i chevel hamitzri} indispensable to the {\i sotah} ritual \u8212- {\i me\u8217'akev} \u8212- or is it merely one way to accomplish the necessary task? If its purpose is simply {\i shelo yishmetu vgadeha me\u8217'aleha} \u8212- to prevent her clothes from falling completely off \u8212- then any cord would suffice, even a small one. But if the specific Egyptian rope is required because {\i hi chagra lo bitziltzul lefichach kohen mevi chevel hamitzri} \u8212- she girded herself with a fancy belt to entice her lover, and therefore the {\i kohen} must bring a qualitatively different material in its place \u8212- then it would be {\i me\u8217'akev}. The {\i gemara} resolves this from the {\i baraita} itself, which explains the reason as {\i kedei shelo yishmetu vgadeha me\u8217'aleha} \u8212- purely functional, preventing the clothing from falling \u8212- and therefore the specific Egyptian rope is not {\i me\u8217'akev}, and any comparable cord would do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises the question of where the Egyptian rope is purchased from \u8212- the {\i Yerushalmi} indicates it comes from {\i misheyarei halishka}, purchased from public communal funds. {\i Tosafot} then extends the question: by the same token, are the sword used for {\i sayif} and the cloth used for {\i chenek} also purchased from public funds? In other words, how far does the parallel between {\i sotah} and capital punishment extend when it comes to the provision of materials?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses to Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All vs.\u160 ?Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} appears to contradict itself. First it states {\i vechol harotzeh liros ba yireh} \u8212- anyone who wishes to see may come and see, implying no distinction between men and women. Then it states {\i kol hanashim mutarot lirosa} \u8212- women are permitted to see, implying that men are excluded. {\i Abbaye} tries to resolve this by saying {\i targama anashim} \u8212- read the first clause as referring to men: even the first inclusive-sounding statement is actually restricted to men who choose to attend. But {\i Rava} objects: the {\i mishnah} says {\i kol harotzeh liros ba yireh} \u8212- {\i whoever} wishes, which is broad language that cannot be limited to men alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Required Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela amar Rava} \u8212- Rava resolves the contradiction differently. {\i Kol harotzeh liros ba yireh lo shena gavrei velo shena nashei} \u8212- anyone, male or female, who wishes to attend may do so. But {\i venashim chayavot lirosa} \u8212- women are {\i obligated} to come and witness the ritual. The source is {\i shene\u8217'emar venisru kol hanashim velo taasena kezimaschena} \u8212- from {\i Yechezkel}, the {\i pasuk} states that all women must be chastened and not act in the manner of {\i zimah}, inappropriate sexual conduct. The {\i sotah} ritual thus serves an educational and deterrent function for women specifically: they are required to witness it so that they will not engage in similar behavior. Men may attend if they choose; women must attend. The {\i kohanim} present, as discussed earlier, present a distinct consideration given the potential for {\i hirhurin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Measure for Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midah K\u8217'neged\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} at the close of the {\i daf} articulates the principle of {\i midah keneged midah} in the {\i sotah} ritual: {\i bemidah she\u8217'adam moded bah modedin lo} \u8212- the measure by which a person acts is the measure that is applied back to them. {\i Hi kishtah atzmah la\u8217'aveirah, hamakom nivalah} \u8212- she adorned herself for her sin, and God degrades her. {\i Hi giltah es atzmah la\u8217'aveirah, hamakom gilah aleiha} \u8212- she revealed herself for her transgression, and God reveals her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order of Harm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beyerach hitchilah ba\u8217'aveirah techilah ve\u8217'achar kach habeten} \u8212- she began her sin with her thigh \u8212- understood as a reference to the genitalia \u8212- and then her womb became implicated, as the ultimate concern is the possibility of pregnancy from the adulterous relationship. {\i Lefichach palkah hayerech techilah ve\u8217'achar kach habeten} \u8212- and therefore the punishment follows the same order: the thigh first, then the womb. {\i Veshear kol haguf lo palat} \u8212- the rest of the body is unaffected; the {\i sotah} water acts only upon those parts of the body that were instruments of the sin. The {\i gemara} will go on from here to examine the precise relationship between the {\i psukim} and this ordering in considerable detail, but that belongs to the {\i sugyot} that follow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 9\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Midah K\u8217'neged Midah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ritual to Theology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ninth daf of Sotah opens with a return to the {\i mishna}, which we transition from describing the procedural mechanics of the {\i sotah} ritual to something of greater theological depth: the principle that everything done to the {\i sotah} parallels the acts she performed in her seduction and adultery. This framing is captured in the broader motto of {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} \u8212- measure for measure \u8212- a concept so fundamental to the Gemara\u8217's worldview that one might note, with some amusement, that Shakespeare borrowed it from precisely here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The core claim is that God brings about events in this world that constitute fitting payback for a person\u8217's deeds. Divine justice is not abstract or deferred indefinitely; it operates through a precise correspondence between sin and consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption of Guilt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing at a striking feature of this framing. The entire ritual leading up to the drinking of the water \u8212- the public exposure, the disheveling of hair, the rending of garments \u8212- occurs before any determination of guilt. The {\i mishna} and the Gemara\u8217's elaboration of {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} package all of this as punishment and divine payback, yet at the time these acts are performed, it is not yet known whether the woman is guilty. The framing thus carries within it a pronounced presumption of guilt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Garment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation is reinforced by a comment of Tosafot regarding the rending of the {\i sotah}\u8217's garments. Tosafot notes that a similar garment-rending occurs before administering lashes to a condemned person \u8212- a procedure connected to the requirement that lashes be administered directly to the body. But Tosafot goes further, understanding this act as a form of punishment imposed by {\i beis din}. The difficulty is obvious: the person receiving lashes is known to be guilty, whereas the {\i sotah} has not yet been proven so. Framing the garment-rending as {\i beis din} punishment applied here only deepens the implicit presumption of guilt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ordeal vs.\u160 ?Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a further tension worth noting. The {\i sotah} ritual is at its core a trial by ordeal \u8212- a miraculous divine intervention meant to determine guilt or innocence. At various points the Gemara tends to downplay this miraculous dimension: it works to have her confess early, treats the water\u8217's effect as a form of evidence that could theoretically override testimony, and provides explanations for why the water might not work even when she is guilty (perhaps she or her husband had accumulated merit, or perhaps she had committed other sins). This is one approach \u8212- containing and naturalizing the ordeal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} framing takes the opposite approach, heightening the miraculous dimension. Rather than minimizing divine intervention, it foregrounds it: God is actively punishing the {\i sotah} in precise correspondence to her sins. The more one heightens this sense of direct divine action, the more the entire ritual \u8212- and indeed the {\i kohen} himself \u8212- becomes merely an instrument of God\u8217's purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Human & Divine Agents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the final framing point. Half of the {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} is accomplished through human agents \u8212- the {\i kohen} performing the ritual \u8212- while the other half is accomplished through a miracle, the water\u8217's effect on the guilty woman. When the Gemara later extends {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} to divine punishments in the broader world \u8212- a man falling off a roof, being bitten by a snake \u8212- it is entirely God\u8217's doing, not the result of any human being consciously imposing punishment. By heightening the sense that God is acting directly, the {\i kohen} and the entire ritual are reconceived as agents and vehicles through which God operates. This reframing also carries moral weight: it becomes easier to perform this ritual \u8212- which occurs before guilt is established \u8212- when one understands the {\i kohen} not as an autonomous agent of punishment but as an instrument of divine justice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens with the general formulation: {\i bemida she\u8217'adam moded kach modedin lo} \u8212- in the measure that a person measures out, so it is measured out for him. It then proceeds to apply this principle specifically to the {\i sotah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beauty Reversed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi kishta et atzma la\u8217'avera, HaMakom nivla} \u8212- she beautified herself for sin, God disfigured her. The disfigurement, as will become clear, operates through the {\i kohen} as God\u8217's agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Revelation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi gilta et atzma la\u8217'avera, HaMakom gila aleha} \u8212- she revealed herself for sin, God revealed her. Here too the {\i kohen}, through the rending of her garments, is the vehicle through which God\u8217's exposure of her takes place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thigh & Belly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beyerech hitchila ba\u8217'aveira techila ve\u8217'achar kach habeten} \u8212- she began her sin with the thigh and then the belly. The term {\i beten} here may serve, depending on context, as a euphemism for the sexual organ, though it may also refer more literally to the physical position of the man\u8217's body weight. This will be examined further in the Gemara. The {\i mishna} states that therefore the punishment begins with the thigh \u8212- {\i tilakeh hayerech techilah ve\u8217'achar kach habeten} \u8212- as the verse states: {\i veyiten Hashem es yerecheich nofeles ve\u8217'es bitneich tzavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rest of Body\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Veshar kol haguf lo palat} \u8212- the rest of the body will not escape punishment either. The {\i mishna} thus assures that what begins with the thigh and belly will extend to every limb. This is a compact and well-structured presentation: the beginning of the ritual, its theological underpinning, and its ultimate scope are all packaged together. Notably, however, this neat packaging remains inseparable from the assumption of guilt that pervades the entire discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yosef: af al gav d\u8217'midah batlah, b\u8217'midah lo batlah.} Even though formal court-imposed punishments are no longer operative \u8212- now that there is no longer a {\i beis din} with jurisdiction over capital cases, and {\i dinei nefashos} no longer function \u8212- the principle of {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} has not ceased. God continues to ensure that fitting punishment is meted out. The heavenly dimension of justice persists even when its earthly counterpart has lapsed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Executions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef elaborates, citing also a teaching of Rabbi Chiya: {\i miyom shecharav Beis HaMikdash af al pi shebatlah Sanhedrin, arba misos lo batlu} \u8212- from the day the {\i Beis HaMikdash} was destroyed, even though the {\i Sanhedrin} ceased to function, the four modes of execution did not cease. The Gemara immediately challenges this: surely they did cease? The resolution: the court-imposed executions ceased, but {\i din arba misos lo batlu} \u8212- the divine justice corresponding to those four modes continues. As Rashi explains, this refers to {\i din shamayim she\u8217'hidugmato} \u8212- the heavenly justice that mirrors each of the four modes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Punishments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to enumerate the divine analogs to each of the four modes of execution. One who would have been liable to {\i sekilah} \u8212- stoning \u8212- will either fall from a roof or be trampled by an animal. Rashi notes that stoning involved pushing the condemned from a height before placing a stone upon him, and that when an animal attacks, it similarly knocks the person to the ground. One deserving {\i sereifah} \u8212- burning, which in {\i beis din} involved pouring molten lead down the throat \u8212- will either be consumed in a fire or bitten by a snake, whose venom burns like fire. One deserving {\i harigah} \u8212- execution by sword \u8212- will either be handed over to the Roman authorities, who executed by sword, or attacked by bandits armed with swords. And one deserving {\i chanikah} \u8212- strangulation \u8212- will either drown in a river, dying by asphyxiation, or die of a choking respiratory death. One participant noted the historical observation that Roman crucifixion itself killed through asphyxiation \u8212- a striking parallel to {\i chanikah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites Rabbi\u8217's derivation of the scriptural source for {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah}: {\i b\u8217'se\u8217'ase\u8217'ah b\u8217'shalchah terivenah} \u8212- in its measure, in its sending, it will be smitten. The word {\i se\u8217'ase\u8217'ah} is understood as pointing to the {\i se\u8217'ah}, a large measure, and thus seems to apply the principle only to significant sins that merit divine intervention. This raises the question: what about smaller sins?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Small Measures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ein li ela se\u8217'ah} \u8212- I might only know a large measure. What of smaller measures \u8212- a {\i kav}, a quarter-{\i kav}, a {\i rova}, or even smaller fractions? The Gemara expands the principle by reading {\i ki kol seon soen bera\u8217'ash} as \u8220"every measure\u8221" \u8212- even the smallest \u8212- is counted and paid back. And the principle that {\i kol pruta upruta mitztarefet lecheshbon gadol} \u8212- every tiny infraction accumulates into a large accounting \u8212- is derived from the verse in Kohelet: {\i achat le\u8217'achat limtzo cheshbon}, one to one until a significant reckoning is reached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This accumulation principle carries a dual implication. On one hand, nothing escapes notice; even the smallest sin is recorded. On the other hand, the punishment may not come immediately for each small act \u8212- rather, everything is collected until the sum demands a response.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vechen matzinu besotah} \u8212- and we similarly found by the {\i sotah}, that in the measure she measured out, it was measured out against her. It is worth noting the plural formulation {\i madedu lah} \u8212- \u8220"they measured out for her.\u8221" This is fitting: the {\i sotah}\u8217's punishment is administered through multiple agents \u8212- the {\i kohen}, the heavenly court \u8212- working together, evoking that language of {\i na\u8217'aseh adam}, as if a plurality of actors is involved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Detailed Sotah Examp (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doorway Exposure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi amdah al petach beitah leraot lo} \u8212- she stood by the doorway of her house to be visible to the man, to beckon him or to be seen when he arrived. Therefore {\i velifichach kohen maamidah al shaar nikanor umareh klonah lakol} \u8212- the {\i kohen} makes a public spectacle of her at the gate of Nikanor, displaying her shame to all. Again, this occurs before guilt is established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adornment Reversed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi parsah lo sudarin naim al roshah} \u8212- she spread beautiful silks over her head. Therefore the {\i kohen} removes her head covering and places it beneath her feet, disheveling her hair. {\i Hi kachalah lo paneiha} \u8212- she made up her face. Therefore, as we reach daf 9a, {\i paneiha morikot} \u8212- her face turns jaundiced. {\i Hi kachalah lo eineiha} \u8212- she applied eye makeup. Therefore {\i eineiha boltos} \u8212- her eyes bulge outward. {\i Hi kelaah lo et searah} \u8212- she braided her hair to attract him. Therefore the {\i kohen soter et searah} \u8212- not merely removing the hair covering but actively disheveling the hair, unbraiding whatever had been braided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beckoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi heratah lo be\u8217'etzba} \u8212- she beckoned him with her finger. Therefore {\i tziporneiha noshrot} \u8212- her fingernails fall out. This is the first mention of this detail, and it corresponds to the {\i mishna}\u8217's general assurance that {\i shear kol haguf lo palat} \u8212- no part of the body escapes punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Garment Binding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi chagraah lo betziltzul} \u8212- she wore a fine decorated belt. Therefore the {\i kohen mevi chevel mitzri vekosher lah lemalah midadeiha} \u8212- the {\i kohen} brings an Egyptian rope and ties it above her breasts to hold her garment up. The contrast is pointed: the refined belt exchanged for a crude utilitarian rope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi pashtah lo et yerechah} \u8212- she spread open her thighs for him. Therefore {\i yerechah nofelet} \u8212- her thighs collapse. {\i Hi kiblatu al kersah} \u8212- she received him upon her belly. The word {\i kersah} here may refer to his physical weight resting on her, or depending on the precise act described, may refer to penetration itself; the latter reading is reinforced by the corresponding punishment \u8212- {\i bitnah tzavah}, her belly swells from within.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Food & Drink\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi heechilat\u8217'hu maadanei olam} \u8212- she fed him the delicacies of the world. Therefore {\i korbanah maachal behemah} \u8212- her {\i korban} is made of barley, animal fodder. {\i Hi hishkata oto yayin meshubach bekosot meshubachin} \u8212- she gave him fine wine in beautiful goblets. Therefore {\i kohen mashke mayim hamarim bekeda shel cheres} \u8212- the {\i kohen} makes her drink bitter water from a crude earthenware shard.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point a crucial observation arises: the entire apparatus of {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} is applied exclusively to the woman. What of the male adulterer?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Patriarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Part of the answer lies in the structural realities of a patriarchal society. If a wife suspects her husband of committing adultery, what practical power does she have to act on that suspicion? The {\i sotah} procedure exists because the husband suspects his wife, and he is the one with legal standing to initiate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adultery Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a deeper halakhic dimension. In the framework of {\i halacha}, adultery as a transgression is defined relative to a married {\i woman}, not a married {\i man}. In a polygamous framework, a husband sleeping with another woman is not, technically, a betrayal of his wife, because the prohibition of adultery concerns violating another man\u8217's wife \u8212- the married man is the injured party. This explains the entire dynamic: the husband initiates the {\i sotah} procedure because he is the injured party; there is no parallel mechanism directed against the male adulterer, whose victim would nominally be the other woman\u8217's husband, who himself lacks practical recourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Victim Blaming\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This structural explanation, however, does not resolve a more pointed moral concern, which needs to be explicitly named: all of the blame is being placed on the woman. The {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} catalog \u8212- she wore this, she beckoned this way, she dressed thus \u8212- carries an implicit logic that she was inviting it, that her presentation was responsible for the outcome. This is the same logic that has historically been used to shift blame onto victims of assault or coercion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitigating Factors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara imagines this encounter in terms of deliberate romantic seduction \u8212- {\i she} fed him delicacies, {\i she} gave him fine wine. But as is pointed out, the reality of such situations can be far more complicated: coercion, economic pressure, or outright force may all be operative. The Gemara\u8217's stylized account, influenced by the imagery of {\i Mishlei} with its powerful archetype of the seductive woman (itself a metaphor), does not capture the full range of human situations that might have brought a woman to this point. This too requires acknowledgment alongside the legal and theological analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Detailed Sotah Examp (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secrecy to Exposure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hi asta baseter} \u8212- she performed her deeds in secret, in hiding, so that no one else would know. {\i Yoshev beseter elyon sam panim} \u8212- the One who dwells on high in concealment will turn His face toward her, attending to her case. Rashi understands {\i sam panim} as an expression of directing attention toward her in order to punish; but {\i panim} also carries the connotation of bringing something into the open \u8212- making it public.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse offered as support is: {\i ve\u8217'ein noef shamra neshef, lemor lo teshureni ayin} \u8212- the eye of the adulterer watches in the dark, saying, \u8220"No eye will see me.\u8221" The sinner believes secrecy is assured; God will publicize the sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Davar acher}: {\i Hi asta baseter, HaMakom pirsemah bagalui} \u8212- she did it in private; God publicized it openly. The verse: {\i techaseh sinah bemashaon, tigaleh raato bekahal} \u8212- one who conceals a hateful deed in darkness, his evil will be revealed in the community. Rashi interprets the prior formulation of {\i sam panim} more neutrally \u8212- God focused His attention \u8212- and reserves the theme of public exposure for this second formulation. Both formulations together explain why the {\i sotah} ritual is conducted as a public spectacle: the exposure before the nation is itself the {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} for the secrecy of the sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The narrative then doubles back briefly, noting that the {\i korban} the {\i sotah} brings actually precedes the water in the sequence of the ritual, though the water has been discussed first. The Gemara packages the full {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} together without strict attention to procedural chronology.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 National Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nations Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this juncture the Gemara addresses an apparent redundancy. We have derived {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} from multiple verses; the verse {\i besasa\u8217'ah beshalchah terivenah} seemed to add only the principle of the {\i se\u8217'ah} \u8212- a large measure. But this was already subsumed under the broader principle. What does that verse contribute independently?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i ukiderabi chanina bar papa} \u8212- it teaches the principle of Rabbi Chanina bar Papa. {\i De\u8217'amar Rabbi Chanina bar Papa: ein HaKadosh Baruch Hu nifra min ha\u8217'umah ad she\u8217'at shiluchah} \u8212- God does not punish a nation until the moment of its expulsion or destruction. All of the punishment owed to a nation is held back and concentrated at the ultimate moment of that nation\u8217's destruction or exile. As Rashi notes, {\i shiluchah} here encompasses not only exile but destruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle addresses a pressing theological problem. The Gemara does not here raise explicitly the classic difficulty of {\i tzaddik vera lo, rasha vetov lo} \u8212- the righteous who suffer, the wicked who prosper \u8212- but the application to nations makes the challenge acute. A nation like Rome could be oppressive and evil for centuries while remaining successful and unchallenged. The response offered here is: their punishment is not being waived, only deferred. Everything accumulates toward the final {\i yom hadin} when God will bring it all down upon them at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egypt Three Cups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A challenge is immediately raised from a teaching of Rava. The three cups of {\i Paroh} mentioned in Yosef\u8217's interpretation of the {\i sar hamashkim}\u8217's dream \u8212- {\i kos Paroh} repeated three times \u8212- are understood as a metaphor for Egypt being made to drink the cup of punishment. Why three times? Because Egypt received its punishment in three distinct historical periods: once at the time of Moshe, through the plagues and {\i Kriat Yam Suf}; once at the time of {\i Paroh Necho}, when Nevuchadnezzar conquered Egypt in the course of his broader campaigns; and once in the future, together with all the other nations at the time of {\i mashiach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This seems to contradict Rabbi Chanina bar Papa\u8217's principle: Egypt was punished in installments, not all at once at the moment of destruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nation Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before resolving this, the Gemara raises a preliminary question: perhaps the Egyptians being punished in later periods are not the same people as those of Moshe\u8217's time? Nations change composition over centuries; perhaps the {\i se\u8217'ah} verse speaks to the historical {\i Mitzrim} who have long since been replaced by a different population.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this by citing the testimony of Rabbi Yehuda in the name of Manimin, a convert of Egyptian descent who was a student of Rabbi Akiva. Manimin described his own genealogical situation: he was a first-generation {\i ger Mitzri}, had married a first-generation {\i Mitzrit}, and planned to have his son marry a second-generation {\i Mitzrit} \u8212- so that his grandchildren, as third-generation {\i Mitzrim}, would be {\i mutar lavo bekahal}, permitted to marry natural-born Jews, in accordance with the rule {\i beyuchsin holech achar hapsul} \u8212- the lineage follows the more disqualified parent \u8212- requiring that both parents of each successive generation carry the {\i Mitzri} status. The fact that in the era of the {\i tannaim} one could still identify as a first-generation Egyptian {\i ger} in a meaningful genealogical sense demonstrates that the Egyptian nation retains a continuous identity traceable to the {\i Mitzrim} of the Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nation Identity (continued)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers a resolution to the contradiction with Rabbi Chanina bar Papa\u8217's principle. {\i Ela ee itmar hachi itmar} \u8212- the statement was not made about a nation but about a king. {\i Amar Rabbi Chinana bar Pappa: ein HaKadosh Baruch Hu nifra min hamelech ad sheshlucha} \u8212- God does not punish a {\i king} until he is deposed or removed from power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara further records that {\i Amemar} transmitted Rabbi Chinana bar Papa\u8217's teaching about the {\i nation} (not the king) but grounded it in a different verse: {\i ki ani Hashem lo shanisi ve\u8217'atem benei Yaakov lo chilitem} \u8212- I, God, have not changed, and you, children of Yaakov, have not been destroyed. {\i Lo shanisi le\u8217'uma v\u8217'shanisi lah} \u8212- I never smote a nation twice; when I smite a nation, it is a complete annihilation. By contrast, {\i ve\u8217'atem benei Yaakov lo chilitem} \u8212- Israel was smitten and survived, because {\i chitzai kalin ve\u8217'hein einam kalin} \u8212- God\u8217's arrows are exhausted against them and yet they persist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing does not reassert the claim that a nation\u8217's punishment is all stored until one single moment, since that version was already rejected by the case of Egypt. Here the emphasis is rather on the totality and finality of punishment when it does come to other nations, in contrast to Israel\u8217's ongoing survival through repeated national sufferings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parenthetical note: a long Tosafot and Rashi both address the {\i tosefta} that underlies the Manimin passage, where Rabbi Akiva reportedly corrected Manimin\u8217's assumption \u8212- {\i manim paita}, you are making a mistake \u8212- arguing that {\i Sancheriv ubilbel es ha\u8217'umos}, Sancheriv had scrambled the nations, so the people living in Egypt in his day were not the descendants of the biblical {\i Mitzrim} at all. It is striking that the Gemara here accepts Manimin\u8217's testimony and the continuity of Egyptian national identity, while elsewhere the Gemara appears to accept Rabbi Akiva\u8217's contrary position. The tension between these two treatments of national continuity is noted by the {\i rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A technical question was also raised: what is the difference between {\i ela ee itmar hachi itmar} \u8212- the formulation used here to correct an {\i amora}\u8217's statement \u8212- and {\i chisuri michsra hachi kasani}, which emends the text of a {\i tannaic} source? The answer is that {\i chisuri michsra} applies to {\i tannaic} sources, whereas here, since Rabbi Chanina bar Papa is an {\i amora}, the Gemara uses the less drastic formulation that the statement \u8220"was transmitted thus\u8221" \u8212- not that the text is deficient, but that the report of the statement was incomplete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Individual Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the main theme: {\i amar Rabbi Chamnuna: ein HaKadosh Baruch Hu nifra min ha\u8217'adam ad shetimale se\u8217'ato} \u8212- God does not punish a person until his measure is filled. The {\i se\u8217'ah} is a large measure. Here the principle is applied not to a nation but to an individual, and the resulting picture is more nuanced than the {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} framework might suggest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Earlier the Gemara insisted that even the smallest infraction \u8212- a thimbleful, a single {\i pruta} \u8212- is counted and accumulated. Now the Gemara acknowledges that punishment may not come for each small act in isolation; rather, everything accumulates until the {\i se\u8217'ah} \u8212- the large measure \u8212- is filled, and only then does punishment arrive. The verse: {\i bimlot sifko yetzer lo} \u8212- only when his desires are fully satisfied, then will his anguish come.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an implicit acknowledgment of the same problem that opens {\i Berachot} \u8212- the difficulty of {\i tzaddik vera lo, rasha vetov lo}. The Gemara does not raise that problem explicitly here; it simply asserts {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} while offering this qualification: a {\i rasha} who appears to flourish may simply not yet have filled his {\i se\u8217'ah}. One participant observed that this might even cast a new light on the {\i sotah} ritual: the {\i kinui v\u8217'stira} procedure \u8212- the warning and seclusion \u8212- could be seen as a kind of accumulated pattern that brings the {\i sotah}\u8217's measure to fullness, which would reframe the presumption of guilt not as entirely arbitrary but as based on a pattern of behavior. That reading was noted as a possible insight, though the primary intent of the passage appears to be addressing the broader question of why {\i resha\u8217'im} appear to prosper.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tangential Aggada\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temple Treasures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara introduces an apparently tangential teaching, connected only by the fact that it comes from Rabbi Chanina bar Papa. {\i Darash Rabbi Chanina bar Papa: mah d\u8217'ichtiv ranenu tzaddikim ba\u8217'Hashem, layisharim nava tehila.} The verse says that to the righteous, praise is a {\i naveh} \u8212- dwelling place. On the reading {\i al tikrei nava tehila ela naveh tehila}, the verse is understood as saying that the righteous have a {\i naveh}, a domicile, which was not taken over by their enemies. This refers to Moshe and David, whose dwelling places for God \u8212- the {\i Mishkan} and the {\i Beis HaMikdash} respectively \u8212- were never captured by enemies and used against them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding David: the verse in {\i Eicha}, {\i tave\u8217'u va\u8217'aretz she\u8217'areha} \u8212- the gates sank into the earth \u8212- is understood as describing the golden gates of the {\i Beis HaMikdash} that were caused to sink into the ground before the enemy could seize them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidden Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding Moshe: the Gemara cites the principle that when the first {\i Beis HaMikdash} was built, the {\i Ohel Moed} \u8212- the Tabernacle \u8212- along with all its components, its beams, clasps, bars, pillars, and sockets, was hidden away. It was secreted so that it would not fall into enemy hands when the {\i Beis HaMikdash} was ultimately destroyed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i heicha} \u8212- where exactly were these things hidden? {\i Amar Rav Chisda amar Avimi: tachat mechilot shel heichal} \u8212- beneath the tunnels that run underneath the {\i heichal}, the sanctuary building, on the {\i Har HaBayis}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sotah & Desire\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Desire Lost\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the {\i sotah} with a new {\i baraita}. {\i Natna eineha bemi she\u8217'eino ra\u8217'ui lah} \u8212- she set her eyes upon one who was not appropriate for her, namely, a man who was not her husband. The consequence: {\i mah shebiksha lo nitan lah u\u8217'mah shebeyada natlu heimena} \u8212- what she desired she did not receive, and what she already had \u8212- her marriage, her relationship with her husband \u8212- was taken from her. This formulation offers a different moral register than the {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} framework. Rather than simply calibrating punishment to crime, it carries the message of {\i dayenu} \u8212- be content with what you have. Desire for the inappropriate ultimately destroys what was already in one\u8217's possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lust Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is then generalized: {\i kol hanotenk einav bema she\u8217'eino shelo} \u8212- anyone who gives his eyes and lusts after something that is not his and is not fitting for him to desire \u8212- {\i mah shemevakesh ein notnin lo, uma she\u8217'beyado notlin heimenu} \u8212- he does not receive what he desires, and what he already has is taken from him. This pattern, it must be acknowledged, does not always hold empirically. Yet it captures a real dynamic that many people recognize: that the relentless pursuit of the inappropriate or unattainable often costs a person what they already possess.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to trace this pattern through a series of biblical figures. {\i Vechen matzinu be\u8217'nachash ha\u8217'kadmoni} \u8212- and so we find in the case of the primordial serpent, who gave his eyes to something not fitting for him. The understanding of {\i chazal} here is that the serpent desired {\i Chava} sexually \u8212- she was not only married but a human being. {\i Ma shebikesh lo nitan lo} \u8212- what he desired was not given to him \u8212- and what he had was taken: presumably having previously stood upright, he was cursed to crawl on his belly. God had intended him to be the king of all animals, the most cunning; now he is cursed below all animals. He sought to kill Adam and take Chava; instead eternal enmity was placed between him and the woman and between his offspring and hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pattern continues with a long list: {\i Kayin, Korach, Bilam, Doeg, Achitofel, Geichazi, Avshalom, Adoniahu, Yoav, and Haman}. In each case, the principle holds: they desired what was not theirs, they did not receive it, and they lost what they had. Rashi on Kayin understands the object of his inappropriate desire as sexual \u8212- Hevel\u8217's twin sister, in the tradition that each of the brothers was born with twin sisters. One might wonder about Kayin\u8217's motive differently \u8212- the Midrash describes Kayin and Hevel\u8217's dispute as concerning wealth, land, and even the location of the {\i Beis HaMikdash} \u8212- but Rashi reads the sin as desire for the other\u8217's woman. Regarding Korach, he desired the {\i kehunah}; Bilam desired wealth; Avshalom desired the {\i malchut} \u8212- kingship \u8212- and so on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'ui Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i ra\u8217'ui} \u8212- what is fitting or appropriate to desire \u8212- is worth examining. The Gemara does not prohibit desire itself; to deny the basic human drive of wanting would be unrealistic. The concept of {\i ra\u8217'ui} captures both the sense of proportionality \u8212- a desire that is not a crazed, disproportionate lust \u8212- and the dimension of permissibility, since in many of these cases the desired object was one that was simply forbidden to the person in question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Desire vs.\u160 ?Acting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One participant astutely observed that the list above includes people who did not merely covet but actively acted on their desire \u8212- Avshalom, Bilam, and others pursued their goals with deliberate effort. The Gemara is thus describing not passive wishful thinking but the active pursuit of inappropriate objects of desire. At the same time, it is worth noting the tension with another kind of story \u8212- the inspiring narrative of a person who has an apparently impossible dream and, precisely by fixing their gaze on it and taking step after step, ultimately realizes it. The Gemara would presumably respond that such cases involve {\i ra\u8217'ui lahem} \u8212- desires that are fitting, however seemingly distant. The classification of what counts as {\i ra\u8217'ui} versus {\i eino ra\u8217'ui} is where the moral weight falls, and that determination is not always straightforward.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Order of Afflictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now takes up a technical question about the sequence of the {\i sotah}\u8217's afflictions. The {\i mishna} states that the thigh is punished first and the belly second, because she began her sin with the thigh. But is this actually what the verses say?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye}: when the {\i kohen} pronounces the curse, he curses the thigh first and then the belly, following the order of the sin. But when the waters actually do their work in the body, they proceed {\i ki orchaihu badkei} \u8212- in accordance with the body\u8217's natural path \u8212- first the belly and then the thigh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this. Even within the curse, does the verse not say {\i latzbot beten velanpil yerech} \u8212- to bloat the belly and cause the thigh to fall \u8212- i.e., the belly before the thigh?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen\u8217's Description\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i hahu demoda lah kohen debeten bereisha vehadar yerech} \u8212- that verse is the {\i kohen} describing to the woman what the water will do inside her body, in the order that the water will naturally proceed. He does this {\i shelo lehotzi la\u8217'az al mayim hamarerim} \u8212- so that no aspersion will be cast upon the bitter waters. If the {\i kohen} had described the effects in the wrong order and the water then affected the belly before the thigh, people might question the reliability of the waters. So when the {\i kohen} tells her what will happen, he states it in the physiological order \u8212- belly first, then thigh. But his actual curse is thigh first, then belly, as {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi adds that the initial curse of the thigh \u8212- even before the water has taken effect \u8212- is itself part of the {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah}: the thigh is singled out first in the curse as a direct parallel to its role in the sin.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Good Rewarded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miriam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now extends the {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} principle from punishment to reward: {\i vechen lemidah tovah}. The first example is Miriam. She stood watch over the infant Moshe for a brief hour \u8212- {\i vatitatzav achoto merachok}, her sister stood at a distance. Therefore, when Miriam was struck with {\i tzaraat} and quarantined outside the camp, {\i vehaam lo nasa ad he\u8217'asof Miriam} \u8212- the entire nation waited seven days before traveling, until Miriam was brought back in. One hour of watching yielded seven days of national waiting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yosef zachah likvor et aviv} \u8212- Yosef merited to bury his father, bringing him from Egypt back to Canaan. And {\i ein be\u8217'achav gadol mimenu} \u8212- there was none greater than him among his brothers. The verse: {\i vaya\u8217'al Yosef likvor et aviv, vaya\u8217'alu imo gam rechev gam parashim} \u8212- Yosef went up to bury his father, with chariots and horsemen accompanying him. Therefore, {\i mi lanu gadol miYosef shelo nitaseik bo ela Moshe} \u8212- who was greater than Yosef such that only Moshe would take care of his burial? Moshe \u8212- the greatest person of his generation \u8212- took charge of the bones of Yosef.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Moshe zachah be\u8217'atzmot Yosef, ve\u8217'ein beYisrael gadol mimenu} \u8212- Moshe merited to carry the bones of Yosef, and there was none greater than Moshe in Israel. The verse: {\i vayikach Moshe et atzmot Yosef imo}. Therefore, {\i mi gadol miMoshe shelo nitaseik bo ela HaMakom} \u8212- who was greater than Moshe such that only God took care of his burial? The verse: {\i vayikbor oto bagai}. And {\i lo al Moshe bilvad amru ela al kol hatzadikim} \u8212- this is not said only of Moshe; all the righteous are buried by God. The verse: {\i vehalach lefanecha tzidkecha, kevod Hashem ya\u8217'asfecha} \u8212- the glory of God will gather you in at the time of death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Good Exceeds Bad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The chain \u8212- Miriam\u8217's one hour yielding seven days; Yosef caring for his father being paid back by Moshe; Moshe caring for Yosef being paid back by God \u8212- reflects not merely parallel {\i midah k\u8217'neged midah} but an escalating one. In the case of Miriam, one hour becomes seven days. This resonates with the broader principle articulated repeatedly in the Gemara: {\i midat tovah merubah mimiddat pur\u8217'anut} \u8212- the measure of goodness that God repays exceeds the measure of punishment. Good is rewarded at a higher multiple than evil is punished.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimshon Eyes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eyes Poked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues with Shimshon. {\i Shimshon halach achar einav} \u8212- Shimshon went after his eyes. {\i Lefichach nikru Plishtim et einav} \u8212- therefore the Philistines pierced out his eyes. The verse: {\i vayochezu Plishtim vayenakru et einav}. The case of Shimshon continues the theme of desiring what is not appropriate \u8212- in this instance, non-Jewish women. But here it is explicitly a man pursuing his desires, and it is his eyes that become both the instrument of sin and the focus of punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Free Choice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges: {\i vehatichtiv ve\u8217'aviv ve\u8217'imo lo yadu ki meHashem hu} \u8212- but the verse states that his father and mother did not know that this was from God! Shimshon\u8217's marriage to the Timnite woman was directed by divine providence as a means through which God would act against the Philistines. If his behavior was all part of God\u8217's plan, in what sense was he punished for it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds: {\i ki azal miha batar yashruta azal} \u8212- in going, he was following his own desires. The fact that God simultaneously operated through those same events does not relieve Shimshon of responsibility for his own motivations and choices. This is a fundamental statement about {\i bechirah chofshit} \u8212- free choice. A parallel challenge arises from the case of the Egyptians, whom God had decreed would enslave Israel: why were they punished? One answer is that they were pursuing their own desires and choices, not consciously fulfilling divine decree. God\u8217's plan operating through human events does not erase those humans\u8217' moral agency or the accountability that flows from it. One participant observed a counterpoint in Yosef\u8217's words to his brothers \u8212- where the same principle of divine operation through human events is invoked in a way that mitigates the brothers\u8217' guilt. The Gemara does not resolve that tension here; it is content to apply the principle of free choice to Shimshon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gaza Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records: {\i Rebbe omer: techilas kilkulo b\u8217'Aza} \u8212- Rabbi taught: Shimshon\u8217's first sin was in Gaza. {\i Lefichach laka b\u8217'Aza} \u8212- therefore he was smitten in Gaza. The verse: {\i vayelech Shimshon Azasa vayar sham isha zona} \u8212- Shimshon went to Gaza and saw there a prostitute. And the verse recording his punishment: {\i vayoridu oso Azasa} \u8212- they brought him down to Gaza in chains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timnah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges: but the verse records that {\i vayered Shimshon Timnasa} \u8212- Shimshon descended to Timnah and took a woman there, and this incident preceded the episode with the prostitute in Gaza. So how can Rabbi say that Gaza was the beginning of his sin?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Real Sin Gaza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves: {\i techilas kilkulo miha b\u8217'Aza} \u8212- yes, Timnah came first, but that was a marriage. Rashi explains that the woman in Timnah, even though she was a non-Jew, was at least a woman Shimshon married. The real beginning of his moral corruption was in Gaza, where he went to a prostitute. Thus the starting point of his {\i kilkul} \u8212- his moral undoing \u8212- was specifically the episode of sexual licentiousness in Gaza, and that is why it is there that his punishment was ultimately administered. This introduces a moral distinction between sleeping with a non-Jewish woman in the framework of marriage versus visiting a prostitute \u8212- the latter being the more severe infraction that marks the true beginning of his downfall.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimshon Delilah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delilah Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i vayehi acharei chen vaye\u8217'ehav isha b\u8217'nachal Sorek, u\u8217'shma Delilah.} A {\i baraita} records Rabbi\u8217's observation: {\i ilmalei nikra shma Delilah, re\u8217'uya haysa shetikarei Delilah} \u8212- even if her name had not been Delilah, she was worthy of being called Delilah. The name captures her essence: {\i dildela es kocho} \u8212- she drained his strength; {\i dildela es libo} \u8212- she drained his heart; {\i dildela es ma\u8217'asav} \u8212- she diminished his deeds, causing the {\i Shechina} to depart from him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each element is grounded in a verse. {\i Dildela es kocho}: {\i vayasar kocho me\u8217'alav} \u8212- his strength was removed from him. {\i Dildela es libo}: {\i vatere Delilah ki higid lah es kol libo} \u8212- Delilah saw that he had disclosed his entire heart to her. {\i Dildela es ma\u8217'asav d\u8217'istalek Shechina minei}: {\i v\u8217'hu lo yada ki Hashem sar me\u8217'alav} \u8212- he did not know that God had departed from him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 True Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now examines how Delilah knew that Shimshon had finally told her the truth about the source of his strength. He had lied to her multiple times previously, giving false explanations, each of which she tested by summoning the Philistines while he slept \u8212- and each time he woke and overpowered them. When he finally revealed the secret of his hair, how did she know it was not another lie?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Chanin amar Rav: nikarin divrei emes} \u8212- true words can be recognized. These three words carry great force. Perhaps the story hung together, the evidence corroborated it \u8212- Rashi notes that she could see he did not drink wine and could observe his hair \u8212- and the truth manifested in a way that distinguished it from his prior fabrications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzaddik Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a different explanation: {\i yad\u8217'a b\u8217'oso tzaddik d\u8217'lo mafik shem shamayim l\u8217'vatala} \u8212- she knew this {\i tzaddik} would never utter God\u8217's name in vain. Notice the remarkable characterization: despite everything we have heard about Shimshon\u8217's sins, Abaye calls him a {\i tzaddik}. When Shimshon finally said {\i nezir elohim ani} \u8212- I am a {\i nazir} of God \u8212- invoking God\u8217's name in his declaration, she recognized that this was a man who would not say such a thing falsely. {\i Amra hashta vadai kushta ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- now, she said, he must surely be telling the truth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pressure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vayehi ki hitzika lo bidvareha kol hayamim vate\u8217'altzehu} \u8212- and it was when she pressed him with her words all the days that she pressured him. {\i Mai vate\u8217'altzehu?} \u8212- what does \u8220"pressured\u8221" mean here? {\i Amar Rabbi Yitzchak d\u8217'vei Rabbi Ami: besha\u8217'at gmar bi\u8217'ah nishmeta mitachtav} \u8212- at the moment of achieving climax, she withdrew. She withheld intimacy at the critical moment, repeatedly, until he was finally broken and told her the truth.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nazirite Laws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Angel Instruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes with a brief discussion about the instructions given by the {\i malach} to the mother of Shimshon, {\i eshet Manoach}: {\i ve\u8217'ata hishamri na al tishti yayin veshachar ve\u8217'al tochli kol tamei} \u8212- now be careful, do not drink wine or strong drink, and do not eat anything impure. The plain reading might suggest {\i tamei} refers to non-{\i kosher} food. But this raises an obvious difficulty: {\i vechi ad hashta devarim tme\u8217'im achla?} \u8212- had she been eating non-{\i kosher} food until now? What is the instruction adding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite Impurity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Yitzchak d\u8217'vei Rabbi Ami}: the instruction refers not to non-{\i kosher} foods in general but to {\i devarim ha\u8217'asurim benazir} \u8212- the things forbidden to a {\i nazir}. Since Shimshon was to be a {\i nazir} from birth, his mother was instructed to observe, during her pregnancy, the full range of {\i nazirite} restrictions \u8212- not only wine itself but grapes, grape juice, grape seeds, grape skins, and all the related prohibitions that we learned in {\i masechet Nazir}. The term {\i tamei} in this context means {\i tamei} from the perspective of a {\i nazir}, i.e., forbidden to a {\i nazir}, not ritually impure in the ordinary sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The shiur concludes here, with the Shimshon narrative to be continued in tomorrow\u8217's session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 10\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shimshon narrative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vayivka intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf picks up at the bottom of 10b, in the midst of the story of Shimshon \u8212- a narrative that the Gemara introduces as part of its broader {\i mida k\u8217'neged mida} expansion from the {\i sotah} theme, though notably the focus here shifts to a man\u8217's sexual sins rather than a woman\u8217's. The Gemara quotes the verse {\i Vayivka Elokim et hamachtesh asher balechi} \u8212- God cracked open the hollow within the jawbone, from which water flowed to relieve Shimshon\u8217's thirst.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lust & punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak of the school of Rabbi Ami comments: {\i hu ivah l\u8217'davar tamei} \u8212- he lusted after an impure thing, namely the non-Jewish women he pursued \u8212- {\i lefichach nitlu chayav b\u8217'davar tamei} \u8212- therefore his very life was made dependent on an impure thing, the jaw of a donkey from which the water emerged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dan prophecy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i Vatachel ruach Hashem l\u8217'pa\u8217'amo b\u8217'machane Dan} \u8212- the spirit of God began to ring out in the camp of Dan \u8212- prompts Rabbi Chama the son of Rabbi Chanina to observe: {\i chalah nevuaso shel Yaakov Avinu} \u8212- the prophecy of Yaakov Avinu took effect here. The reference is to {\i yehi Dan nachash alei derech}, that Dan would be a serpent upon the road. Now at last, with Shimshon arising from the tribe of Dan, that ancient blessing begins to manifest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechina as bell\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak of the school of Rabbi Ami adds a second teaching on {\i l\u8217'pa\u8217'amo}: {\i melamed she\u8217'haysa Shechina mekashkeshes lifanav k\u8217'zug} \u8212- the {\i shechina} was knocking before him like a bell. The verbal parallel is drawn between {\i l\u8217'pa\u8217'amo} here and {\i pa\u8217'amon v\u8217'rimon} \u8212- a bell and a pomegranate \u8212- as the {\i shechina} rang out, driving him forward between Tzor\u8217'ah and Eshta\u8217'ol.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mountains crushed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Assi teaches that Tzor\u8217'ah and Eshta\u8217'ol were {\i shnei harim gedolim} \u8212- two enormous mountains \u8212- {\i v\u8217'akran Shimshon v\u8217'tachnan zeh bazeh} \u8212- and Shimshon uprooted them both and ground them together with his great strength. This is offered as an elaboration on the magnitude of that same divine spirit resting upon him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ahimeleach oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then doubles back to the verse {\i v\u8217'hu yachel l\u8217'hoshia et Yisrael} \u8212- he will begin to bring salvation to Israel. Rabbi Chama the son of Rabbi Chanina reads the word {\i yachel} here not as {\i chalah} \u8212- taking effect \u8212- but as {\i chullin}, in the sense of {\i lo yachel devaro} from {\i Nedarim}: the oath of Avimelech has been voided. The reference is to Avimelech\u8217's oath \u8212- {\i im tishkor li u\u8217'lnini u\u8217'l\u8217'nechdi} \u8212- that Israel would not betray him or his children or grandchildren. Rashi explains that since the Philistines had already violated that covenant by oppressing Israel, the oath was annulled \u8212- {\i batlah l\u8217'shon chabalah lefi shehem avru al shvuas techila} \u8212- and Shimshon was therefore free to attack them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sexual blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i vayigdal hana\u8217'ar vayivarchehu Hashem} \u8212- the lad grew and God blessed him \u8212- raises the question: with what did God bless him? Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: {\i shebarcho b\u8217'amaso} \u8212- with his sexual organ. The Gemara immediately clarifies: not that it was unusually large, but {\i amaso k\u8217'vnei adam v\u8217'zaro k\u8217'nachal shotef} \u8212- its form was normal, but his seed was like a rushing river. Rashi explains: {\i l\u8217'mai shenisava nisbarech} \u8212- he was blessed in the very thing he desired.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about this teaching is that the verse in question precedes the episodes of Shimshon\u8217's pursuit of women. The Gemara\u8217's reading implies that the desire came first, then the blessing in response to it, and then the acting upon it \u8212- a sequence that anticipates the larger theme the Gemara will develop shortly: that individuals are blessed with extraordinary capacities which, if not governed properly, ultimately become their undoing. The connection to the message of {\i sotah} may also be relevant here \u8212- the desire for certain things does not justify pursuing them without restraint.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foxes & torches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then jumps back to an earlier episode: {\i vayelech Shimshon vayilkod shalosh meot shualim} \u8212- Shimshon caught three hundred foxes. Why specifically foxes? Rabbi Abbahu bar Rabbi Nagdi, citing Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba, explains that Shimshon said: {\i yavo mi shechozer le\u8217'achorav} \u8212- let the creature that walks backwards on its own path come to take vengeance on the Philistines, who similarly reversed themselves on their oath. The fox, which retreats backwards rather than turning to run, becomes the fitting symbol of those who betrayed the covenant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimshon\u8217's width\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Braitah} is cited in the name of Rabbi Shimon HaChasid: the distance across Shimshon\u8217's shoulders was {\i shishim amah} \u8212- sixty cubits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gates on back\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this measurement is derived from the later episode where Shimshon, having slept through half the night in Gaza, arose at midnight, seized the doors and lintels and bolt of the city gate, and carried them on his shoulders. A received tradition states that the gates of Azza were never less than sixty {\i amah} across \u8212- and he bore them all on his back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grinding metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i vayehi tochen b\u8217'veit ha\u8217'asurim} \u8212- he was grinding in the prison house \u8212- is interpreted by Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ein tchinah ela lashon aveirah} \u8212- {\i tchinah}, grinding, is here a euphemism for sexual intercourse. He supports this from the verse in Job: {\i titchan l\u8217'acher ishti} \u8212- then another man will grind after my wife. Proceeding from this, Rabbi Yochanan teaches: {\i melamed shekol echad ve\u8217'echad hevi lo et ishto l\u8217'veit ha\u8217'asurim k\u8217'dei shetita\u8217'ber heimenu} \u8212- every man brought his wife to the prison so that she might conceive from Shimshon, using him essentially as a means of obtaining powerful offspring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony within the context of {\i masechet Sotah} is sharp. Throughout the tractate, the framing has been one of a woman suspected of adultery, a jealous husband bringing her before the {\i beit hamikdash} for judgment. Here the image is inverted: it is the husband himself who is forcing his wife into sexual contact with another man. The woman is treated entirely as an object subject to her husband\u8217's will. This only deepens the tractate\u8217's implicit critique of a legal framework that places the full burden of suspicion on the woman while leaving the man\u8217's conduct largely unexamined. Rashi, notably circumspect, writes only: {\i Raboteinu peirshu mah shepeirshu} \u8212- the Sages explained what they explained \u8212- declining to elaborate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Desire fulfilled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa adds a proverbial gloss: {\i hainu d\u8217'amri inshi} \u8212- this is what people say \u8212- {\i kamei d\u8217'shatei chamra chamra}, before one who drinks wine, you bring wine; {\i kamei d\u8217'rafuka, gerida duvla}, before a farmer you bring a basket of vegetables. People receive what they are drawn to. The point, as applied to Shimshon, is that even when such a desire does a person no ultimate good, it is what they get \u8212- because it is what they sought.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reciprocal adultery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mida k\u8217'neged mida\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan teaches: {\i kol hamizaneh ishto mezaneh alav} \u8212- any man who commits acts of {\i znut}, his wife will do the same against him. This is a striking statement in the context of a tractate formally concerned with the wife\u8217's guilt. The Gemara does not limit the concept of sexual sin to its narrow {\i halachic} definition. Earlier, the Gemara noted that Shimshon\u8217's moral failing began not merely when he married non-Jewish women but when he went to prostitutes \u8212- a {\i musar} framing that goes well beyond the technical {\i halacha}. Similarly here, {\i hamizaneh} presumably refers not only to sleeping with another man\u8217's wife (which would be {\i halachic} adultery) but to sleeping around more broadly. The passage thus meaningfully shifts the lens: if a man\u8217's wife is sinning, one must ask what role he played in creating that dynamic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Job proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara brings a proof from the book of Job: {\i im niftah libi al ishah v\u8217'al pesach re\u8217'ei aravti} \u8212- if my heart has been seduced toward a woman, and I lay in wait at the door of my neighbor \u8212- then {\i titchan l\u8217'acher ishti} \u8212- another man will grind after my wife, and {\i v\u8217'aleha yichre\u8217'un acharin} \u8212- men will prostrate themselves after her. Here the adultery is indeed technical \u8212- it concerns another man\u8217's wife \u8212- but the parallel and the principle are clear: one who chases after his neighbor\u8217's wife will have men chasing after his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gourd & gander\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara closes this section with a popular saying: {\i ihu bevei kari v\u8217'it\u8217'teih bevei butzini} \u8212- he eats a small gourd while his wife eats a large one. The image captures the {\i mida k\u8217'neged mida} dynamic colloquially: what you do will be done to you in kind.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Righteous judge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimshon judges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to Shimshon, Rabbi Yochanan teaches: {\i Shimshon dan et Yisrael k\u8217'avihem she-bashamayim} \u8212- Shimshon judged Israel like their Father in Heaven. This is derived from {\i Dan yadin amo ke-achad} \u8212- like the One, like God. The verse\u8217's reference to the tribe of Dan is read as a reference to Shimshon specifically. The context matters: the Gemara is simultaneously depicting Shimshon\u8217's lust and his extraordinary righteousness as a judge \u8212- a portrait of genuine human complexity, resisting the easy categorization of a figure as wholly righteous or wholly wicked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimshon & God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan goes further: {\i Shimshon al shemo shel HaKadosh Baruch Hu nikra} \u8212- the very name Shimshon was a reference to God. The proof is from {\i ki shemesh u-magen Hashem Elokim} \u8212- for God is a sun and a shield. The name \u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1513 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ? shares its root with {\i shemesh}, a protector and shield.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name clarified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate question: if the name \u1513 ?\u1502 ?\u1513 ?\u1493 ?\u1503 ? is a divine name, may it be erased? The Gemara resolves: {\i ela me-ein shemo shel HaKadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- it is not a proper name of God but a descriptor, a name {\i me-ein}, in the manner of. Tosafot sharpens the analysis: names like {\i chanun} and {\i rachum} and even {\i shalom} are not among the seven divine names that may not be erased, because they are descriptions rather than proper names. The word {\i shemesh} \u8212- a shield, a protector \u8212- similarly functions as a descriptor. Many proper names incorporate divine descriptors: {\i Yeshayahu}, {\i Eliezer}, and others contain a divine element without constituting a {\i shem kodesh} in the formal sense. The answer of {\i me-ein shemo} in the Gemara therefore resolves the question: the name may be erased because it is a descriptor, not a proper name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eternal protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes the point: {\i ma HaKadosh Baruch Hu magen al kol ha-olam kulo, af Shimshon magen be-doro al Yisrael} \u8212- just as God protects the entire world eternally, so Shimshon was the protector of Israel in his generation. The contrast is telling: God\u8217's protection is universal and unending; Shimshon\u8217's was particular and temporal \u8212- but within those limits, it partook of something of the divine quality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then adds that Rabbi Yochanan also said Bilam was lame in one leg \u8212- {\i va-yelech shefi} contains an Aramaic word suggesting dislocation \u8212- while Shimshon was lame in both legs: {\i shefifon alei orach}, the doubled form. This is notable: Shimshon, for all his extraordinary physical power, carried a physical disability. The Gemara here refuses to flatten him into a simple hero. He could be the greatest judge of his generation, physically powerful beyond all human measure, morally serious in his conduct of the people \u8212- and simultaneously lustful, a pursuer of women, and physically lame. That complexity is itself part of what makes this {\i aggadata} so rich.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five blessed ones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a {\i braitah}: {\i chamisha nivre\u8217'u me-ein dugma shel ma\u8217'ala} \u8212- five were created bearing something resembling \u8212- with deliberate distancing language \u8212- the image of what exists above. That is, five individuals possessed qualities so extraordinary that they partook of something almost divine. {\i Ve-chulam laku bahen} \u8212- and for all of them, those very qualities became their undoing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimshon strength\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shimshon in his strength \u8212- {\i Shimshon be-kocho}. The verse cited is {\i vayaser kocho me-alav} \u8212- his strength was taken from him at the end. The moral logic here is somewhat different from the earlier {\i mida k\u8217'neged mida} formulation: it is not precisely that Shimshon\u8217's strength was his sin and therefore led to punishment of the same kind. Rather, the thing he was most extraordinarily gifted with was ultimately the thing that did not save him \u8212- it was taken. The strength was divine in quality; ultimately it was not his to keep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaul\u8217's neck\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shaul in his neck \u8212- {\i Shaul be-tzavaro} \u8212- because he stood {\i mi-shichmo va-ma\u8217'ala} above everyone else. The verse brought is {\i vayikach Shaul et hacherev vayipel aleha} \u8212- he fell upon his sword. Rashi suggests that the typical mode of execution by sword is decapitation, connecting the manner of his death to the neck. As with Shimshon, however, the connection is not that his height caused pride or sin \u8212- the moral message here is not about a fatal flaw in the usual sense. The Gemara\u8217's point seems to be rather that however blessed a person is, however divine the quality, it is not ultimately theirs. Nothing exempts them from their fate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Absalom\u8217's hair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Avshalom in his hair \u8212- {\i Avshalom be-se\u8217'aro} \u8212- and this is the case that is most explicitly a {\i mida k\u8217'neged mida}, as the Gemara notes briefly, deferring a fuller treatment to a later discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zidkiyahu eyes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tzidkiyahu in his eyes \u8212- {\i Tzidkiyahu be-einav} \u8212- and the verse is direct: {\i v\u8217'et einei Tzidkiyahu iver} \u8212- his eyes were blinded. Rashi acknowledges that there is no explicit verse praising the beauty of Tzidkiyahu\u8217's eyes, and concludes it must have been a received tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asa gout\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Asa in his legs \u8212- {\i Asa be-raglav} \u8212- the verse being {\i rak le-et ziknato chala et raglav} \u8212- in his old age his legs became ill.\u160 ?Again, no explicit verse establishes that his legs were a special blessing; that too must have been tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav that the disease which afflicted his legs was {\i podagra} \u8212- gout. Mar Zutra the son of Rav Nachman asked his father: what does {\i podagra} feel like? He replied: {\i kemachata be-vasar hachai} \u8212- like a needle in raw flesh. Samuel Johnson described it as a rat gnawing at one\u8217's toe. The Gemara then asks how Rav Nachman knew this. Three answers are offered: some say he had experienced it himself; some say he received it as a tradition from his teacher; and some say {\i sod Hashem lirei\u8217'av u\u8217'vrito le-hodiam} \u8212- God\u8217's secret is with those who fear Him \u8212- an inspired knowledge, reminiscent of a similar discussion elsewhere regarding the {\i shir habia}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asa draft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava asks: why was Asa punished? Since the punishment is not straightforwardly a {\i mida k\u8217'neged mida} \u8212- it is not that his legs caused sin \u8212- why did he suffer? {\i Mipnei she\u8217'asa angarya be-talmidei chachamim} \u8212- because he conscripted Torah scholars into military service. The verse is {\i ve-hamelech Asa hishmia et kol Yehuda ein naki} \u8212- King Asa mobilized all of Judah, none exempt. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: not only Torah scholars, but even {\i chatan me-chedro ve-chala me-chupata} \u8212- a groom from his chamber and a bride from her wedding canopy, whom the Torah itself exempts. The {\i Aruch} adds: {\i ve-chol sheken talmidei chachamim} \u8212- and certainly Torah scholars. This Gemara is, needless to say, extensively cited in contemporary debates over the drafting of {\i kollel} students in Israel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Timnah geography\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timnah verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes an apparent contradiction in the Torah: one verse says {\i vayered Shimshon Timnata} \u8212- Shimshon went {\i down} to Timnah \u8212- while another verse says {\i hinei chamich oleh Timnata} \u8212- your father-in-law is going {\i up} to Timnah. Does one ascend or descend to Timnah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moral reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar resolves the contrast not geographically but morally: {\i Shimshon shenisganeh bah, ksiv beih yeridah} \u8212- for Shimshon, who was morally degraded by the experience, the language of descent is used. {\i Yehudah shenisaleh bah, ksiv beih aliyah} \u8212- for Yehudah, who was ultimately elevated by the experience \u8212- through his confession and his role in the messianic line \u8212- the language of ascent is used. The same place, two contrasting spiritual trajectories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single vs.\u160 ?two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two further positions are offered. Rav Shmuel bar Nachmani says {\i shtei Timnos hava} \u8212- there were actually two separate places called Timnah, one topographically lower and one higher, each appearing in a different verse. This may well be {\i pshat}: the use of {\i aliyah} and {\i yeridah} in biblical Hebrew is not always strictly geographic, as {\i aliyah la-aretz} makes plain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa maintains that there was only {\i chada Timnah} \u8212- a single Timnah \u8212- and resolves the contradiction simply: {\i de-asi me-hai gisa yeridah u-de-asi me-hai gisa aliyah} \u8212- depending on from which direction one approached, the road went either up or down. He compares this to several known villages \u8212- Vardunya, Bei Bari, and Shuka de-Narsh \u8212- each situated on a hillside such that one approach descends and another ascends. The single-Timnah reading carries its own literary power: one place, two stories of men and women and sexual encounter, one degrading and one elevating.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tamar crossroads\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Einayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to Tamar and the phrase {\i vatashev be-pesach einayim} \u8212- she sat at the opening of Enaim, or at the crossroads. The phrase {\i pesach einayim} receives multiple interpretations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abraham\u8217's tent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Alexandri teaches: {\i melamed she-halcha ve-yashva be-fischo shel Avraham Avinu} \u8212- she went and sat at the doorway of Avraham Avinu \u8212- the place toward which all eyes turn, {\i makom she-kol einayim tzofot li-roso}, because Avraham kept {\i pesach ha-ohel} and was the paradigmatic welcoming host. It is a {\i drash}, of course; the geographical connection between Timnah and Avraham\u8217's tent remains unexplained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enaim place\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina in the name of Rav says: {\i makom hu she-shemo Enaim} \u8212- it is simply a place name, as confirmed by the verse {\i Tapuach ve-haEnam}, where Enam is listed as a city.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eyes to words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani offers a third reading: {\i she-natna einayim li-dvareha} \u8212- she gave eyes to her words, meaning she positioned herself so as to enable Yehudah to act upon his desire. The precise implication of this reading will become clearer as the encounter unfolds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudah\u8217's questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Yehudah approaches and requests to sleep with her, the Gemara presents a remarkable sequence of questions. First: {\i shem nachris at?} \u8212- perhaps you are a non-Jewish woman and I am forbidden from you? She replies: {\i giyoret ani} \u8212- I am a convert, do not worry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Married status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: {\i shem eshet ish at?} \u8212- perhaps you are a married woman? She replies: {\i pnuyah ani} \u8212- I am unattached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father married\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third: {\i shem kibel bach avich kiddushin?} \u8212- perhaps your father betrothed you while you were still a minor? She replies: {\i yetomah ani} \u8212- I am an orphan, and a convert at that \u8212- she would have no father to have arranged such a betrothal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fourth: {\i shem tme\u8217'ah at?} \u8212- perhaps you are in a state of {\i niddah}? She replies: {\i tehorah ani} \u8212- I am pure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi truth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi attempts to read each of Tamar\u8217's answers as technically true. She is indeed a {\i giyoret} by virtue of her marriage into Yehudah\u8217's family. She is indeed {\i pnuyah} in the sense the Gemara will explore. She is indeed an orphan, and her answer regarding {\i kiddushin} by her father serves a further {\i halachic} purpose for Rashi: since she was married not by her father but by her mother \u8212- and since the Rabbis had not yet legislated that maternal arrangements carry full {\i halachic} validity \u8212- she was not truly Yehudah\u8217's daughter-in-law in the strict sense, which is why Yehudah was not transgressing {\i arayot} by lying with her. One might immediately object that this undermines Tamar\u8217's entire purpose: if she was never really his daughter-in-law, then the {\i yibum} claim that motivated her entire scheme collapses. This tension is real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Family levirate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ramban addresses this directly. He argues that in the ancient world, before the Torah formalized {\i yibum} as a duty incumbent on the brother specifically, the obligation was understood more broadly as a family responsibility \u8212- anyone in the {\i mishpacha} could fulfill it. One sees a trace of this in the story of Rut and Boaz, where Boaz is not the dead man\u8217's brother yet the entire framework of redemption and quasi-{\i yibum} applies. What Tamar was doing, on this reading, was asserting her claim to {\i yibum} from the patriarch of the family itself \u8212- a claim that made perfect sense within the pre-Sinaitic understanding of the institution. The questions about {\i niddah} and {\i kiddushin} reflect the Gemara reading its own {\i halachic} categories back onto that earlier world, but the essential structure of the story remains coherent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abraham eshel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eshel meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having mentioned Avraham\u8217's doorway in connection with Tamar, the Gemara pauses to discuss the verse {\i vayita eshel be-Be\u8217'er Sheva} \u8212- and he planted an {\i eshel} in Be\u8217'er Sheva. What is an {\i eshel}? A dispute is offered: one opinion says {\i pardes} \u8212- a garden orchard \u8212- {\i she-nata bo kol minei migadim}, planted with all manner of delicacies; another says {\i pundak} \u8212- an inn. The word {\i vayita} presents a difficulty for the inn interpretation, since one plants a tree, not a building. But the Gemara resolves this from a parallel usage: {\i vayita ohalei apadano} \u8212- he pitched his palatial tents \u8212- where the same verb means to erect a structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eshel acrostic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish teaches: {\i al tikrei vayikra, ela vayakrei} \u8212- do not read it as \u8220"he called out,\u8221" but as \u8220"he caused others to call out.\u8221" The word {\i eshel} is further read as an {\i acrostic}: {\i achila, shtiya, levaya} \u8212- eating, drinking, and being accompanied on one\u8217's way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abraham method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Avraham\u8217's method of {\i kiruv} was as follows: he welcomed travelers in, fed them and gave them drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blessing redirect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the guests wished to thank him for the hospitality, he redirected them: {\i v\u8217'chi misheli achaltem? mishel Elohei olam achaltem} \u8212- you did not eat my food; you ate the food of the God of the world. {\i Hodu ve-shabchu u-varchu l\u8217'mi she-amar ve-haya ha-olam} \u8212- give thanks and praise and bless the One who spoke the world into being. That is the deeper meaning of {\i vayikra b\u8217'shem Hashem El Olam} \u8212- he caused God\u8217's name to be on the lips of all who passed by. The practical lesson is timeless: you bring people in with hospitality, and through that opening you direct their gratitude upward.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mistaken identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Covered face\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah says {\i vayachsheveha l\u8217'zonah ki kistah paneha} \u8212- he thought her to be a prostitute because she had covered her face. The Gemara is puzzled: {\i mishum d\u8217'kistah paneha chashvah l\u8217'zonah?} \u8212- is it from {\i covering} her face that he concluded she was a prostitute? This seems to assume a social context in which a brazen woman is uncovered and a modest woman is veiled. But in that case, a covered face should suggest modesty, not {\i znut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Veil meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The puzzle reflects a genuine cultural tension. In some societies modesty demands veiling; in others, the veil serves the opposite function \u8212- it enables anonymity and thus facilitates illicit encounters. A person entirely concealed cannot be identified, which may be precisely the point for certain purposes. The question the Gemara is wrestling with is which convention operates here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognition failure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar resolves it differently: {\i she-kistah paneha b\u8217'veit chamia} \u8212- she had always covered her face in Yehudah\u8217's household. Therefore he did not recognize her. He had never seen her clearly as his daughter-in-law because she was so modest in his presence \u8212- and since he had never seen her face, he could not now identify her. The implication is not that the veil signaled {\i znut} but rather that the veil prevented recognition, given her habitual modesty in his home.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modest bride reward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yonatan: {\i kol kallah she-tznuah b\u8217'veit chamia zochah v\u8217'yotzim mimena melachim u-nevi\u8217'im} \u8212- any bride who is modest in her father-in-law\u8217's house merits that kings and prophets descend from her. The principle is intuitive: a married woman, living under the same roof as her father-in-law, takes particular care to maintain her {\i tzniut} precisely because there is another man present who is not her husband. That careful modesty carries with it a great reward.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tamar proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amotz lineage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i menalan?} \u8212- from where do we know this is so? {\i Mi-Tamar} \u8212- from Tamar herself. For the prophets, the Gemara cites {\i chazon Yeshayahu ben Amotz}. Rabbi Levi adds: {\i davar zeh masoret b\u8217'yadeinu me-avoteinu} \u8212- we have this as a received tradition \u8212- {\i Amotz ve-Amatzia achim havu} \u8212- Amotz and Amatzia were brothers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isaiah descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since Amatzia was {\i melech Yehuda} \u8212- king of Judah \u8212- Yeshayahu\u8217's father Amotz was of the royal family, descended from David, and thus from Yehudah and Tamar. From Tamar therefore came both kings \u8212- through David \u8212- and prophets \u8212- through Yeshayahu. Her modesty in Yehudah\u8217's house was the seed of that extraordinary legacy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Public shame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Burning threat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah says {\i hi mutzeit} \u8212- she was being taken out to be burned. Rashi reads {\i mutzeit} as meaning she was discovered, i.e., found and apprehended. Rabbi Elazar teaches: {\i l\u8217'achar she-nimtze\u8217'u simanim} \u8212- after the evidence \u8212- the signet, cord, and staff \u8212- had been found, she was being brought to judgment with them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samael removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that moment, {\i ba Samael} \u8212- the angel Samael came. Samael, associated with Esau and the forces of impurity, {\i ba ve-richakon} \u8212- came and distanced those items from her. The motivation is clear: Samael does not want this story to succeed, because from Tamar and Yehudah will ultimately come David and the messianic line \u8212- which spells the undoing of Esau\u8217's dominion. He therefore tried to remove the evidence that would save her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gabriel restore\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ba Gavriel ve-kirvan} \u8212- Gavriel came and restored them to her. This is the deeper reading of {\i hi mutzeit}: the items were found again, restored by divine intervention, enabling her to present her case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara connects this to the Psalm title {\i la-menatze\u8217'ach al yonat eilem rechokim l\u8217'David} \u8212- a {\i michtam} of David. Rabbi Yochanan interprets: {\i mi-sha\u8217'ah she-nitrachaku simanim, na\u8217'aseit k\u8217'yonah ilemet} \u8212- at the moment the evidence was distanced from her, she became like a mute dove, with nothing to say in court and facing execution. David, who descended from Tamar, is the {\i michtam} \u8212- the one who emerged from that moment of divine rescue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fire principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same chain of authorities \u8212- Rav Zutra bar Tuvia in the name of Rav, and some say Rabbi Chanin bar Bizna in the name of Rabbi Shimon Chasida, and some say Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai \u8212- teaches: {\i noach lo le-adam she-yapil atzmo le-toch kivshan ha-esh ve-al yalbin pnei chaveiro be-rabbim} \u8212- it is better for a person to throw himself into a burning furnace than to shame his fellow in public.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof is from Tamar: rather than directly confront Yehudah and shame him, she sent the evidence obliquely and allowed him to come forward himself. The {\i peshat} dimension is compelling on its own terms: a direct confrontation would likely have been denied or deflected; by giving him the opening, she allowed him to own the truth. But the {\i midrash} draws from this the deeper principle about the gravity of public shaming \u8212- {\i libun panim}, whitening the face, draining the blood from someone\u8217's cheeks \u8212- which is so severe that one should be prepared for personal suffering rather than inflict it on another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David humility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers several interpretations of {\i michtam}. One: {\i she-haya mach ve-tam} \u8212- David was humble and pure. Another: {\i shechazazto tama} \u8212- his wound was complete, in the sense that {\i she-hu mahul} \u8212- he was born already circumcised. A third: just as in his youth David humbled himself before those greater than him for the purpose of learning Torah, so too in his greatness he maintained that same humility. The connection between David\u8217's {\i michtam} and Tamar\u8217's modesty is thus sustained: the quality that saved Tamar \u8212- her {\i tzniyut} in Yehudah\u8217's house \u8212- becomes an inheritance for the Davidic line, expressed as the humility and purity that characterize David himself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudah admits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognition items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Haker na} \u8212- do you recognize these? Yehudah is presented with the signet, cord, and staff.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haker circularity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chama the son of Rabbi Chanina observes: {\i be-haker biser le-aviv} \u8212- with the word {\i haker} he informed his father. The brothers had earlier sent Yosef\u8217's coat dipped in blood to Yaakov, saying {\i haker na ha-ketonet bincha hi im lo} \u8212- recognize now, is this your son\u8217's coat? And immediately after that episode, the Torah records {\i va-yered Yehudah me-eit echav} \u8212- Yehudah went down from his brothers \u8212- and the story with Tamar begins. The same word {\i haker} now returns to measure out Yehudah\u8217's reckoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jacob echo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The literary and moral parallel is precise. By Yaakov, the sons kept their distance and presented the evidence indirectly \u8212- they let the father recognize the coat himself rather than confront him with what they had done. Here too, Tamar does not confront Yehudah directly but sends the evidence and lets him recognize it. In both cases, the person given the {\i haker} moment is allowed to arrive at the truth through their own recognition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teshuva opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This structure is what makes Yehudah\u8217's {\i teshuva} genuine. Had Tamar accused him directly, the dynamic would have been defensive \u8212- denial, deflection, dismissal. By presenting the evidence and giving him an opening, she allowed him to come forward and claim the truth himself, to own it. That is the moment of authentic {\i teshuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognition theme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mida k\u8217'neged mida} dimension here reaches further back still. The pattern of {\i vayaker} and {\i haker} runs from Yaakov and Esav \u8212- where Yaakov used the {\i gedei izim} and Yitzchak {\i lo hikiro} \u8212- through the brothers using {\i gedei izim} dipped in blood to make Yaakov recognize something false \u8212- through Yehudah\u8217's reckoning here \u8212- and further still to {\i vayaker Yosef et echav v\u8217'hem lo hikiruhu} by the Yosef story. The word {\i haker} is a thread stitched through the entire family narrative, and it is always about recognition, concealment, and the moment of moral accounting.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehudah\u8217's confession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzaddka statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vayaker Yehudah vayomer tzadka mimeni} \u8212- Yehudah recognized and said: she is more righteous than I.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joseph vs Yehudah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanin bar Bizna said in the name of Rabbi Shimon Chasida: Yosef, who sanctified God\u8217's name {\i ba-seter} \u8212- in private \u8212- by withstanding the advances of Potifar\u8217's wife, {\i zacha ve-hosifu lo ot achat mishmo shel HaKadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- merited that one letter of God\u8217's name was added to his: {\i eydut bi-Yehosef shemo}, the extra {\i heh} making his name \u1497 ?\u1492 ?\u1493 ?\u1505 ?\u1507 ?. Yehudah, who sanctified God\u8217's name {\i be-farhesia} \u8212- in public \u8212- by openly acknowledging his sin, {\i zacha ve-nikra kulo al shemo shel HaKadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- merited that his entire name contains the letters of God\u8217's name: \u1497 ?\u1492 ?\u1493 ?\u1491 ?\u1492 ? contains a {\i yud}, a {\i heh}, a {\i vav}, and a {\i heh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat kol promise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the moment Yehudah said {\i tzadka mimeni}, a {\i bat kol} went forth: {\i ata hitzalta Tamar u-shnei baneha min ha-or} \u8212- you have saved Tamar and her two children from the fire.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chayecha she-ani matzil bi-zchutcha gimel mi-banecha min ha-or} \u8212- in your merit I will save three of your descendants from the fire. {\i Man ninhu? Chananya, Mishael, ve-Azarya} \u8212- those thrown into Nevuchadnezzar\u8217's furnace and saved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine origin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then reads {\i tzadka mimeni} not only as \u8220"she is more righteous than I\u8221" but as a divine utterance: {\i mimeni yatzu kevushim} \u8212- from Me went forth these hidden decrees. The entire chain of events, the {\i bat kol} declares, was God\u8217's design.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Union resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separation read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-lo yasaf od le-da\u8217'atah} \u8212- he no longer knew her. The plain reading is that after this episode Yehudah and Tamar had no further sexual contact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel Saba, the father-in-law of Rav Shmuel bar Ami, citing Rav Shmuel bar Ami himself, offers a different interpretation: {\i keivan she-yad\u8217'ah} \u8212- once he knew her, reading {\i da\u8217'atah} not as sexual knowledge but as recognition of her righteousness and her {\i l\u8217'shem shamayim} motivation, {\i shuv lo pasak mimena} \u8212- he did not cease from her. They began to live together as husband and wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual variant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof is a {\i gezeirah shavah}: {\i ve-lo yasaf od le-da\u8217'atah} is read in parallel with {\i kol gadol ve-lo yasaf} \u8212- the great voice that did not cease. Just as that voice continued without interruption, so too the relationship continued. The word {\i yasaf} in both contexts is read as cessation that did not occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Happy ending\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This alternative ending is remarkable in the context of a tractate about sexual sin and its consequences. Here the narrative that began with lust and moral failure \u8212- Yehudah mistaking his daughter-in-law for a prostitute \u8212- resolves into a proper marital union. Because he did the right thing, because he admitted, the story ends not in separation and shame but in legitimate relationship. The framework of {\i mida k\u8217'neged mida} is fulfilled in the positive direction: genuine {\i teshuva} and honest reckoning restore what was broken.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zonah vs kedesha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cult prostitute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Yehudah wished to send payment, he dispatched his friend the Adullamite, who was told to ask for {\i ha-kedeshah hi be-Einayim al ha-derech} \u8212- the {\i kedeshah} who sat by the road at Enaim. {\i Kedeshah} technically refers not to an ordinary prostitute but to a cult prostitute \u8212- a woman who served in pagan temples as part of idolatrous practice, a phenomenon attested widely across the ancient Near East.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Face-saving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Robert Alter notes the discrepancy: when Yehudah first encountered her, the Torah says he thought her a {\i zonah} \u8212- an ordinary prostitute. But when he sends his friend to make payment, he instructs him to ask for a {\i kedeshah}. Alter\u8217's insight is that Yehudah was attempting to save face among the local population. Asking a neighbor to help locate a {\i zonah} you had slept with is frankly embarrassing. Asking for a {\i kedeshah} carries a different connotation \u8212- something more exotic, perhaps quasi-religious \u8212- and sounds more dignified than having to admit you visited a common streetwalker. It is, in short, a face-saving euphemism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No cult here\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The neighbors\u8217' response is pointed: {\i lo hayta bizeh kedeshah} \u8212- there has been no {\i kedeshah} here. {\i Zonahs} we may know about; {\i kedeshas} we do not. The very attempt at euphemism fails, and the word the neighbors use brings Yehudah right back to where he started.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 11\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Absalom\u8217's Hair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Absalom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 11. We pick up at the bottom of 10b, in the middle of a story about Absalom ({\i Avshalom}) within the larger context of {\i midah keneged midah} that began with the {\i sotah} and her punishments. Now we turn to Absalom: his hair was the most beautiful, almost divine thing about him, and ultimately it proved to be his downfall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a {\i baraita}: {\i Tanu Rabbanan} \u8212- Absalom rebelled through his hair, as it says, \u8220"And like Absalom there was no man as beautiful\u8230 ? and when he shaved his head \u8212- for it was heavy upon him and he shaved it \u8212- he weighed the hair of his head at two hundred {\i shekalim} by the king\u8217's weight.\u8221" {\i Rashi} reminds us, in case we have forgotten what we learned in {\i Masechet Nazir}, that the {\i Gemara}\u8217's understanding is that Absalom was a {\i nazir olam} and was therefore permitted to shave his hair once every twelve months. The hair provided him a pretext \u8212- gathering people around him and leading an insurrection \u8212- and presumably filled him with the sense that he was deserving of the kingship, though the {\i Gemara} does not say that explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair & Pride\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then offers a {\i derasha} on the phrase \u8220"the king\u8217's weight,\u8221" identifying it as a standard measure used by the people of Tiberias and Tzipori. From this flows the moral consequence: {\i lefichach nitlah besearo} \u8212- therefore he was caught by his hair. He was, as it were, hoisted by his own petard. As it says, \u8220"And Absalom rode before the servants of David, and Absalom rode upon the mule, and the mule went under the thick branches of the great terebinth, and his head caught in the terebinth, and he was suspended between heaven and earth, and the mule that was under him passed on.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tree & Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses a natural question: why did he not simply cut himself free? It says he took a sword and wanted to cut his hair \u8212- but at that very moment, {\i tanna d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael} teaches, the depths of {\i Sheol} opened up beneath him. The insight offered in discussion resolves the difficulty elegantly: Absalom could not cut his hair because he now faced a second peril \u8212- if he cut himself free, he would fall into the abyss that had opened below him. That explains his paralysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following Joab\u8217's killing of Absalom, the report of his death was brought to King David, who went up to the gate and wept, crying out: {\i bni Avshalom, bni bni Avshalom, mi yiten muti ani tachtecha Avshalom bni bni} \u8212- \u8220"My son Absalom, my son, my son Absalom, would that I had died in your place, Absalom my son, my son.\u8221" And then again: {\i Avshalom bni bni}. It is difficult to overstate the pathos of these verses \u8212- arguably the saddest passage in all of {\i Tanakh}. No matter what Absalom had done, David\u8217's anguish is complete and unconditional. That is precisely what makes it so unbearably poignant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David\u8217's Lament\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i hanei tmanya bni lama} \u8212- why are there eight utterances of \u8220"my son\u8221"? Seven of them, it explains, correspond to the seven chambers of {\i Gehinnom}: each one of David\u8217's cries raised Absalom up from one level of the depths, each one lifting him a little closer to the surface. The eighth utterance \u8212- according to one opinion \u8212- served to reconnect Absalom\u8217's severed head to his body (though this detail does not appear explicitly in the biblical text); according to another opinion, David\u8217's prayer actually brought Absalom to {\i Olam Haba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gehinnom & Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last point raises an important question taken up by {\i Tosafot}: the principle stated in {\i Chelek} is {\i bra mezakei aba} \u8212- a son\u8217's merits can benefit his deceased father \u8212- but not {\i aba mezakei bra}, a father cannot bring benefit to his son in this way. How, then, did David\u8217's prayer avail Absalom?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} offers two responses. First, Absalom had not worshipped idols, and therefore the case may be different from those situations in which a sinner is wholly beyond redemption. Second, and more directly: the principle that {\i aba lo mezakei bra} means only that out of respect for the father, we do not ordinarily count the son\u8217's deeds as accruing to the father\u8217's merit \u8212- but that is a matter of formal accounting. Prayer, however, is different: {\i aval tefila mo\u8217'eles} \u8212- prayer is effective. And David prayed for Absalom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a deeper conceptual issue. {\i Kaddish} operates on a somewhat different logic: the son\u8217's recitation of {\i kaddish} sanctifies God\u8217's name in the world, and since the deceased father brought into the world a child who now performs this act, the merit redounds indirectly to him \u8212- {\i bra mezakei aba} in the classic sense. But praying directly for the welfare of another\u8217's soul is a distinct concept from performing good deeds whose merit flows back through a causal chain. One is asking God to act; the other is creating a new meritorious act attributable to the deceased through the child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is interesting, in passing, to note the parallels with the legends that grew up around the death of Shabtai Tzvi \u8212- the myths about prayer campaigns to elevate him from {\i Gehinnom} and bring him to {\i Olam Haba}. He remained so powerful a figure in the popular imagination that people could not accept that his sin was the final word. Something similar seems to be at work in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's wrestling with whether David\u8217's prayer could ultimately bring Absalom to {\i Olam Haba}. On the question of gradations: once a soul has been removed from punishment, it is one further step to {\i Olam Haba} \u8212- not to the highest level, but to some entry-level portion. The {\i Gemara} in {\i Rosh Hashanah} does speak of the {\i reshaim} dwelling in a kind of temporary state for twelve months before their final judgment, something not entirely unlike the concept of purgatory in other traditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: \u8220"And Absalom had taken and set up for himself a monument in his lifetime.\u8221" The {\i Tanakh} reports that after his death it was recalled that Absalom had already erected this monument, calling it {\i Yad Avshalom}, because he said, \u8220"I have no son to keep my name in remembrance.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai lakach} \u8212- what does \u8220"he had taken\u8221" mean? {\i Reish Lakish} says: just as {\i Vayikach Korach} means that Korach made a bad transaction, so too here \u8212- {\i shelakach mekach ra le\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- he had made a bad investment for himself. Whether this refers to his foreseeing his own death by erecting a monument, or to the entire disastrous enterprise of the rebellion, is left somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa} adds: {\i be\u8217'etzah amukah shel malko shel olam} \u8212- this was all part of the deep counsel of the King of the universe. {\i Rashi} explains this with reference to the punishment decreed upon David after the sin of Bathsheba \u8212- God had told him that evil would arise from within his own house \u8212- {\i hineni mekim alecha ra\u8217'ah mi\u8217'beitecha}. Without diminishing Absalom\u8217's own {\i bechirat chofshit}, the episode unfolded in accordance with a divine plan. The {\i Gemara} draws a parallel to the story of Joseph: {\i Vayishla\u8217'chehu me\u8217'emek Chevron} \u8212- he was sent from the valley of Hebron. {\i Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa} reads {\i emek} here as \u8220"deep counsel,\u8221" the deep strategy of that righteous one buried in Hebron, namely Abraham, and connects this to the divine decree of {\i yado\u8217'a teda ki ger yihyeh zar\u8217'acha} \u8212- \u8220"Know well that your descendants will be strangers.\u8221" Joseph\u8217's being sent to his brothers was all part of the mechanism by which that divine promise would come to be fulfilled. The question of {\i hashgacha pratit} and {\i bechirah chofshit} operating simultaneously is, as the {\i Gemara} itself seems to acknowledge, a formidable one \u8212- and the text moves on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children & Wheat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now addresses the monument\u8217's stated rationale: {\i ki amar ein li ben} \u8212- Absalom said he had no son. But this is puzzling, since the {\i Tanakh} earlier reports that he had three sons and one daughter. {\i Rabbi Yitzchak bar Avdimi} explains: he had no son {\i hagun lemalkhut} \u8212- no son fit to inherit a kingdom. This detail itself implies that even at the time he erected the monument, Absalom was already anticipating his bid for the throne \u8212- for why else would fitness for kingship matter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} adds a {\i gemiri} \u8212- a received tradition: whoever burns another\u8217's grain ({\i tevuato shel chavero}) will leave no son to inherit him. This explains why Absalom\u8217's sons predeceased him, though {\i Rav Chisda} acknowledges the tradition is transmitted as a general principle. The underlying logic is a form of {\i midah keneged midah}: just as one destroys the fruit of another\u8217's labor \u8212- that which was planted and nurtured \u8212- so one\u8217's own progeny, the fruit of one\u8217's own life, will be cut down.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The episode being referenced is Absalom\u8217's burning of Joab\u8217's wheat field. After Absalom had been permitted to return to Jerusalem but the king still refused to receive him, Absalom needed to compel Joab to intercede on his behalf. Since Joab was not responding to his messages, Absalom instructed his servants: \u8220"Go and set fire to Joab\u8217's field.\u8221" And so they burned the field. That act of destruction ultimately led to his restoration in the eyes of the king \u8212- and set in motion the chain of events that would culminate in the rebellion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miriam & Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miriam Hovers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the {\i Mishnah}, which teaches that just as there is {\i midah keneged midah} for punishment, so there is {\i midah keneged midah} for good \u8212- and the example is Miriam, who waited over Moses for one hour, and in the end the entire nation of Israel waited for her seven days when she was stricken. But {\i Abaye} raises an objection: can this really be called {\i midah keneged midah}? She waited one hour \u8212- {\i hatam chada sha\u8217'ata} \u8212- while Israel waited seven days. That is not measure for measure; it is vastly disproportionate. {\i Abaye} therefore proposes reading the {\i Mishnah} as saying that for {\i midah tovah} it is specifically not so \u8212- the reward is greater.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} objects: the {\i Mishnah} says {\i vechen linyan hatovah} \u8212- \u8220"and likewise for good.\u8221" You cannot simply change the {\i girsa}. The concept is the same; what differs is only the quantity. The resolution is that {\i midah keneged midah} applies to both directions, but {\i mida tovah} is always {\i merubah} \u8212- greater \u8212- than {\i midat puranut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Math: Hour & Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} picks up the mathematical precision of this point. {\i Tosafot} notes that when the {\i Mishnah} says Miriam waited \u8220"one hour,\u8221" it cannot be taken literally as a full hour. For the {\i Tosefta} teaches that {\i mida tovah merubbah mimiddat puranut al achat chamesh me\u8217'ot} \u8212- the measure of good exceeds the measure of punishment by five hundred times. Calculating: seven days contain 168 hours. If the ratio is 500:1, then Miriam must have waited only approximately one-third of an hour \u8212- roughly twenty minutes \u8212- not a full hour. {\i Tosafot} says \u8220"a third or a quarter,\u8221" though the arithmetic points more precisely to a third.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then notes a further difficulty: regarding the spies, the punishment was {\i yom lashanah} \u8212- a year for each day they spent scouting, which yields a ratio of 365:1. That is {\i midat puranut}, not {\i mida tovah}. But 365:1 is less than 500:1, yet the {\i Tosefta} claims that {\i mida tovah} exceeds {\i midat puranut} fivefold. Is then the {\i midat puranut} of the spies greater than the {\i mida tovah} of Miriam? The {\i Gemara} in the first chapter of {\i Chagigah} complicates things further: {\i Rabbi Yochanan} expounds the verse \u8220"they seek Me daily\u8221" to mean that whoever engages in Torah even one day is regarded as if he engaged all year \u8212- again a ratio of 365:1 \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} there explicitly equates this with the punishment of the spies. So are the two measures actually equal after all? The {\i Gemara} leaves the precise arithmetic to God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the verse {\i vatitatzav achoto merachok} \u8212- \u8220"and his sister stood from afar\u8221" \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yitzchak} teaches: this entire verse was said in the name of the {\i Shechina}; it was the divine presence that was hovering there to protect Moses, not merely Miriam standing watch. He demonstrates this by showing that each word of the verse is elsewhere associated with God: {\i vatitatzav} \u8212- to stand \u8212- appears in {\i vayavo Hashem vayityatzav}; {\i achoto} \u8212- sister \u8212- in {\i emor lechochma achoti at}, where wisdom, which is close to God, is called \u8220"sister\u8221"; {\i merachok} \u8212- from afar \u8212- in {\i merachok Hashem nirah li}; {\i lada\u8217'at} \u8212- to know \u8212- in {\i ki el de\u8217'ot Hashem}; {\i mah ya\u8217'aseh} \u8212- what will be done \u8212- in {\i ki lo ya\u8217'aseh Hashem Elokim davar}; and {\i lo} \u8212- to him \u8212- in {\i vayikra lo Hashem shalom}. Every word in the verse, on its own, connects back to God.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pharaoh\u8217's Plan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Pharaoh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this the {\i Gemara} opens into the broader narrative of Moses and the Israelites in Egypt \u8212- a backdrop that will occupy the remainder of the daf. {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} disagree about {\i vayakam melech chadash al Mitzrayim} \u8212- \u8220"and a new king arose over Egypt.\u8221" One says he was literally a new king; the other says that in fact his decrees were renewed \u8212- his entire approach toward Israel changed. The support for the first view is simply the word {\i chadash} \u8212- it says \u8220"new.\u8221" The support for the second view is that the text never says the old king died ({\i vayamot vayimloch}), implying continuity of the person with a radical change in policy. As for {\i asher lo yada et Yosef} \u8212- \u8220"who did not know Joseph\u8221" \u8212- the one who holds the policy-change view explains: he acted {\i keman dilo yadalei klal}, as if he had never known Joseph at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes: {\i hu hitchil be\u8217'eitzah techila} \u8212- Pharaoh was the one who initiated the plot against Israel first, and therefore {\i lefichach lakah techila} \u8212- he was struck first, as it says: \u8220"and upon you and upon your nation and upon all your servants the frogs shall come up\u8221" \u8212- he was the first one hit by the plagues. This is the {\i midah keneged midah} of the initiator.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water Plan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i hava nitchachma lo} \u8212- \u8220"come, let us deal wisely with them\u8221" \u8212- uses the singular {\i lo} where the plural {\i lahem} would be expected. {\i Rabbi Chama b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina} understands this as deliberate: {\i bo\u8217'u venitchacham lemoshi\u8217'an shel Yisrael} \u8212- let us outwit the Savior of Israel. The {\i lo} refers not to the Israelites but to their divine protector. And the plan was calibrated precisely around the principle of {\i midah keneged midah}: Pharaoh knew that God avenges measure for measure, and so he sought a method of oppression that God could not repay in kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Should we destroy them by fire? But Scripture says \u8220"Behold, God comes with fire\u8221" \u8212- God will repay us with fire. By the sword? But \u8220"with His sword against all flesh\u8221" \u8212- repayment in kind. Therefore: {\i ela bo\u8217'u venidonam bamayim} \u8212- let us cast them into the water. For God had already sworn never to bring another flood upon the world, as it says, \u8220"for this is as the waters of Noah to me, for I have sworn that the waters of Noah shall not again pass over the earth.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pot Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Their miscalculation, the {\i Gemara} explains, was twofold. First, {\i she\u8217'al kol ha\u8217'olam kulo eino meivi} \u8212- God swore He would not bring a flood upon the entire world, but He said nothing about destroying one nation by water. Second \u8212- and more pointedly \u8212- {\i aval hem ba\u8217'im venoflim betocho} \u8212- when the Egyptians pursued Israel, they ran into the sea themselves. God did not drown them; they came and fell into it of their own accord, as it says {\i uMitzrayim nasim likrato} \u8212- \u8220"and the Egyptians were fleeing toward it.\u8221" {\i Rabbi Elazar} crystallizes this: the verse says {\i ki vadavar asher zadu aleihem} \u8212- in the very matter in which they schemed against them. And the word {\i zadu}, the {\i Gemara} notes, is the language of cooking \u8212- as in {\i vayazed Yaakov nazid}, \u8220"and Jacob cooked a stew.\u8221" {\i Bekdeira shebishlu bah nitbashlu} \u8212- in the very pot in which they cooked, they themselves were cooked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether this entire passage \u8212- attributing to Pharaoh an awareness of {\i midah keneged midah} and a calculated effort to circumvent it \u8212- is consistent with his statement elsewhere that he does not recognize God\u8217's authority at all. It is a genuine tension: a {\i Midrash} that quotes verses from Isaiah anachronistically and posits a Pharaoh who is theologically sophisticated enough to scheme against the God of Israel sits uneasily alongside the Pharaoh who says \u8220"I do not know the Lord.\u8221" This is a place where {\i midrash} and {\i peshat} do not align \u8212- and where the {\i Midrash} transforms the entire dynamic of the narrative in a profound way, making Pharaoh from the very outset a figure who knows and fears God, rather than one who simply refuses to recognize His authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Advisors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Simai} teaches: three were in that council who advised Pharaoh on how to deal with Israel \u8212- Balaam, Job, and Jethro. Balaam was the one who gave the advice; he is, after all, the figure who throughout the tradition counsels other nations on how to harm Israel \u8212- {\i ba v\u8217'iyaatzcha}, \u8220"come and I will advise you,\u8221" and later it is Balaam\u8217's counsel that lies behind the incident of the daughters of Midian. For this, {\i neherag} \u8212- he was ultimately killed in the war against Midian.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Job was silent. He neither agreed nor protested \u8212- he lacked the courage to oppose Pharaoh. For this, {\i nidon b\u8217'yisurin} \u8212- he was afflicted with suffering. This is a striking passage, for it presents us with an answer to the question that the entire book of Job never explicitly resolves: why did Job suffer? Here the answer is that he was a silent enabler. There is a powerful parallel here to the famous {\i baraita} at the opening of {\i Masechet Avodah Zarah}, where the three sages before the Romans \u8212- one who praised Rome, one who was silent, and one who criticized \u8212- are treated not as morally equivalent but in terms of the degree to which their conduct constituted collaboration. Silence in the face of evil is itself a form of complicity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Jethro fled. Whether he also protested before fleeing is implicit in his departure \u8212- his running away was itself a form of refusal, even if incomplete. For this, {\i zachu mibnei banav sheyeshvu b\u8217'lishkat hagazit} \u8212- his descendants merited to sit in the Chamber of Hewn Stone, the seat of the Sanhedrin. As the verse says, \u8220"the families of scribes who dwell in Yavetz\u8221" \u8212- {\i Tirathites, Shimeathites, Sucathites, these are the Kenites who came from Chamath, the father of the house of Rechab} \u8212- and the Kenites are identified as the descendants of Jethro, the father-in-law of Moses. They became the scribes and members of the Sanhedrin, serving in the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. The {\i midah keneged midah} here is of counsel: because Jethro\u8217's response was ultimately the right one, his descendants came to be the very advisors of the nation of Israel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Treasure Cities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brick Ruse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to {\i vayasimu alav sarei misim} \u8212- \u8220"and they placed upon him taskmasters.\u8221" The use of the singular {\i alav} rather than the plural {\i aleihem} is noted, and a {\i baraita} from the school of {\i Rabbi Eliezer} and {\i Rabbi Shimon} explains: {\i she\u8217'hevi\u8217'u malben vetalu lo l\u8217'Paroh betzavaro} \u8212- they brought a form of a brick and hung it around Pharaoh\u8217's own neck. The ruse was to announce a great communal building project and have Pharaoh himself participate on the first day, so that no Israelite could claim to be too refined for the work. And indeed: if any Israelite said {\i istinis ani} \u8212- \u8220"I am too delicate for this\u8221" \u8212- they were told: \u8220"Are you more refined than Pharaoh himself?\u8221" In this way, everyone was pressed into labor on that opening day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with another grammatical tension: {\i lema\u8217'an anoto besivlotam} \u8212- the verse mixes singular and plural. The resolution offered is that {\i lema\u8217'an anoto} refers to Pharaoh \u8212- they oppressed that one for that single day \u8212- while {\i besivlotam} refers to Israel \u8212- all of this was for the sake of impressing the Israelites into their burdens. It was, in other words, a one-day ruse that served as the instrument of a far larger enslavement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pithom/Raamses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} addresses the word {\i miskenot} in {\i arei miskenot} \u8212- \u8220"treasure cities.\u8221" {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} disagree: one says {\i she\u8217'mesaknot et ba\u8217'aleihen} \u8212- these buildings endanger their builders ({\i Rashi} understands this as a general observation about construction projects, not limited to this specific case); the other says {\i she\u8217'memaskenot et ba\u8217'aleihen} \u8212- they impoverish their owners. As the tradition goes: {\i kol ha\u8217'osek bevinyan mitmasken} \u8212- anyone involved in building projects will come to poverty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding Pithom and Raamses themselves, again {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} disagree. One says: Pithom was the city\u8217's actual name, and it was called Raamses descriptively \u8212- {\i she\u8217'rishon rishon mitroses} \u8212- because as soon as they would build it, each new course of construction would crumble and shatter. The other says: Raamses was the actual name, and the name Pithom came from {\i pi tehom bola\u8217'o} \u8212- the mouth of the abyss swallowed everything they built, since the ground was swampy and each construction sank immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purposeless Work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This detail inspired a beautiful observation about the nature of {\i avodas parech}: beyond the physical brutality of the labor, what was most demoralizing was its utter purposelessness. Everything they built collapsed or sank \u8212- like Sisyphus, condemned to roll the stone up the hill only to watch it roll back down. The practice of making men do women\u8217's work and women do men\u8217's work \u8212- which the {\i Gemara} will shortly address \u8212- partakes of the same logic. {\i Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani} citing {\i Rabbi Yonatan} explains {\i befarech} as {\i shehaya machlifim melachet anashim lenashim umelachet nashim la\u8217'anashim} \u8212- they would interchange the work of men and women and the work of women and men. {\i Rashi} notes: {\i vezu kashah shelo hayu regilim bekach} \u8212- this was especially harsh because they were completely unaccustomed to it. It was demoralizing on two levels: not only the backbreaking nature of the work but the stripping of dignity that comes with being forced to do that which is alien to your nature and training.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiplying Promise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i veka\u8217'asher ya\u8217'anu oto ken yirbeh veken yifrotz} \u8212- \u8220"as they afflict him, so shall he increase and so shall he spread\u8221" \u8212- uses the future tense where past tense ({\i rabu vefaretzu}, \u8220"they increased and they spread\u8221") would be expected. {\i Reish Lakish} explains: {\i ruach hakodesh mevasertan} \u8212- the divine spirit is announcing it. The oppressors say \u8220"afflict him,\u8221" and God\u8217's voice comes back: \u8220"so shall he increase and so shall he spread.\u8221" There is also a formulation that is even sharper: {\i atah amarta pen yirbeh, ani omer ken yirbeh} \u8212- you said \u8220"lest they multiply,\u8221" I say \u8220"so they shall multiply.\u8221" The echo of {\i pen yirbeh} and {\i ken yirbeh} makes the {\i midah keneged midah} almost structural: the exact same word, with the negative particle of fear replaced by the affirmative particle of divine promise. Conceptually, punishment operates like contraction, while blessing is expansive \u8212- which is precisely why the multiplier for {\i mida tovah} exceeds the multiplier for {\i midat puranut}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Righteous Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Sustenance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Avira} expounds: {\i bisechar nashim tzidkaniyot shehayu beoto hador nigalu Yisrael miMitzrayim} \u8212- it was in the merit of the righteous women of that generation that Israel was ultimately redeemed from Egypt. The account is this: when the women would go to draw water, God would arrange that small fish would enter their pails alongside the water \u8212- {\i mesaknot lahem dagim ketanim bekadeihen} \u8212- so that their buckets came up half water and half fish. They would then set two pots on the fire, one to heat the water and one to cook the fish, and bring both out to their husbands in the field. There they would wash them, anoint them, feed them, and give them drink \u8212- and through this process restore their strength and their spirit until desire returned, and they would lie together {\i bein shefatayim} \u8212- between the folds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One need not try to resolve every detail of this account with others. The broader theme here is important: it is the woman who is the agent of redemption, through acts of sustenance and intimacy \u8212- an echo of the {\i Yehudah} and {\i Tamar} story, where again it was the woman who was the initiator, again through an act of intimacy, and again it was ultimately connected to the chain that leads to {\i David Hamelech}. The drive that can lead to destruction when it crosses the boundaries of what is permitted \u8212- as with the {\i sotah} with whom this tractate began \u8212- can equally be the force that brings about the {\i geulah} when directed toward what is right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse invoked is {\i im tishkevun bein shefatayim} \u8212- \u8220"if you lie down between the folds\u8221" \u8212- and through the merit of that lying down, {\i zachu Yisrael levizat Mitzrayim} \u8212- Israel merited the spoils of Egypt, as the verse continues: \u8220"the wings of a dove covered in silver and her feathers in a filigree of gold.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apple Trees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once pregnant, the women would return home, and when the time of birth came, they would go out to give birth in the field \u8212- {\i tachat hatapuach}, under the apple tree \u8212- away from the eyes of the Egyptians. What exactly a {\i tapuach} is remains uncertain: apples are not indigenous to the Middle East, and various commentators have suggested the {\i etrog} or other fruits. Notably, the word \u8220"apple\u8221" in many languages originally carried the generic meaning of \u8220"fruit\u8221" \u8212- hence {\i tapuach adamah} for potato. The {\i Shir Hashirim} verse {\i tachat hatapuach orarticha} \u8212- \u8220"under the tapuach I aroused you\u8221" \u8212- is applied here to this context of birth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God Himself, the {\i Gemara} continues, would send something from the heavenly realms to care for the newborns \u8212- cleansing them, preparing them, as a skilled midwife prepares a newborn infant, and providing them nourishment. The verse from {\i Yechezkel} is invoked: \u8220"on the day that you were born, your navel cord was not cut, and you were not washed with water\u8221" \u8212- the text of the {\i Haggadah}\u8217's {\i derasha} connects here, where the entire metaphor of {\i Yechezkel} 16 about the founding of the nation is read literally as describing these miraculous births. God supplied them with {\i devash misela veshemen mechalmish tzur} \u8212- honey from the rock and oil from the flinty stone \u8212- as the verse in {\i Devarim} says: {\i vayenikehu devash misela} \u8212- He suckled them with honey from the rock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ground & Grass\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the Egyptians became aware that births were occurring, they came to kill the infants. At that moment, a miracle occurred: {\i venivla\u8217'in bakarka} \u8212- the babies were swallowed into the ground. The Egyptians then brought oxen and plowed over the places where the babies lay buried, but they were too deep and were protected. After the Egyptians left, {\i hayu mevatzbetzin veyotzin} \u8212- the babies would sprout out from the ground {\i ke\u8217'esev hasadeh}, like the grass of the field. The verse {\i revava ketzamach hasadeh nesatich} is cited \u8212- the very verse from the {\i Haggadah} that forms part of the {\i derasha} on that passage in {\i Yechezkel}: \u8220"I caused you to increase like the plant of the field.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} is reading that verse with full literalness. The metaphor of the birthing of the nation and the literal birth of these children are collapsed into one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When these children grew up, {\i ba\u8217'in adarim adarim levateihem} \u8212- they would come home in flocks, like flocks of animals from the field. The verse is read {\i al tikrei ba\u8217'adi adayim ela be\u8217'edrei adarim} \u8212- do not read \u8220"in jewels\u8221" but \u8220"in flocks.\u8221" The image is striking: born in nature, swallowed by the earth, sprouting like grass, and returning home as flocks \u8212- they are almost animals of the field, untamed, raised by the earth itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When God revealed Himself at the sea, {\i hem hikiruhu techila} \u8212- they were the first to recognize Him. And why? Because they had already encountered Him in the field when they were infants \u8212- it was through God\u8217's care that they had been bathed, nourished, and protected. And so at the sea they were able to cry out {\i zeh Keili ve\u8217'anveihu} \u8212- \u8220"this is my God and I will glorify Him.\u8221" They recognized Him because they had known Him before.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Midwives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shifra & Puah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} disagree about the identity of the two midwives. One says they were a mother and daughter ({\i isha uvita}), the other says a daughter-in-law and mother-in-law ({\i kala vechamotah}). The one who holds they were a mother and daughter identifies them as Yocheved and Miriam. The one who holds they were a daughter-in-law and mother-in-law identifies them as Yocheved and Elisheva, Aaron\u8217's wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} sides with the mother-daughter view. {\i Shifra zu Yocheved} \u8212- Shifra was Yocheved. Why was she called Shifra? {\i Shemeshaperet et havelad} \u8212- she would beautify and care for the newborn; and additionally, {\i sheparu veravu Yisrael beyameha} \u8212- Israel was fruitful and multiplied in her days. {\i Puah zu Miriam} \u8212- Puah was Miriam. Why was she called Puah? {\i She\u8217'hayeta po\u8217'ah} \u8212- she would coo in order to help the child emerge, using soothing sounds to help the infant along. An alternate explanation: {\i she\u8217'hayeta po\u8217'ah beruach hakodesh} \u8212- she would speak with the divine spirit, saying: \u8220"My mother is destined to give birth to a son who will be the savior of Israel.\u8221" And indeed, {\i moshi\u8217'an shel Yisrael} \u8212- the savior of Israel \u8212- is the very phrase we encountered earlier in Pharaoh\u8217's council, where the plan was to outwit {\i moshi\u8217'an shel Yisrael}. There, one might have taken it as referring to God; here, retrospectively, it becomes clear it refers to Moses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} cites the explanation of {\i Rabbeinu Chananel} regarding {\i po\u8217'ah}: it refers to an incantation whispered to ease childbirth \u8212- {\i lo chashot lichisha} \u8212- and the practice of whispering such incantations to a woman in labor was common even in Tosafot\u8217's own time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birth Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pharaoh instructs the midwives: {\i beyaledchem et ha\u8217'ivriyot} \u8212- when you deliver the Hebrew women \u8212- and he specifies the {\i avnayim}, the birthing stones. {\i Rabbi Chanan} explains: Pharaoh gave them a sign by which to know that birth was imminent. When a woman crouches to give birth, {\i yarchoteiha mitztanenot ka\u8217'avanim} \u8212- her thighs become cold as stone \u8212- and that is when the midwives would need to be present to intercept the newborn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative explanation connects {\i avnayim} to the image of the potter\u8217's wheel: {\i va\u8217'ered beit hayotzer vehinei hu oseh melachah al ha\u8217'avnayim} \u8212- the potter works the stone, with one side here and one side there and the pedestal in the middle. Similarly, in childbirth: one thigh on this side, one thigh on the other, and the child coming out in the middle. The word {\i avnayim} thus becomes a metaphor for the birthing position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refusal & Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Chanina} teaches that Pharaoh also gave the midwives a sign by which to distinguish boy from girl even before an examination: {\i ben panav lematah} \u8212- a boy comes out face down; {\i bat paneha lema\u8217'alah} \u8212- a girl face up. This connects to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's broader understanding of conception: since the {\i Gemara} assumes the male is above and the female below during intercourse, the child is born in the position corresponding to its parent \u8212- a boy in the father\u8217's position, a girl in the mother\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse says the midwives feared God and did not do as the king of Egypt had commanded them {\i aleihen}. The use of {\i aleihen} rather than the expected {\i lahen} prompts {\i Rabbi Yose bar Chanina} to suggest that Pharaoh had also tried to seduce them to sin sexually \u8212- {\i she\u8217'tav\u8217'an lidvar aveirah} \u8212- using {\i alav} in the sense of {\i lavo eleiha}, which the {\i Gemara} reads as a euphemism for sexual approach. They refused on that account as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women as Beasts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatechayena et hayeladim} \u8212- \u8220"and they gave life to the children.\u8221" A {\i baraita} teaches: not only did they not kill the children, they actively provided them with water and food to sustain them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The midwives\u8217' excuse to Pharaoh \u8212- {\i ki lo ka\u8217'nashim hamitzriot ha\u8217'ivriyot ki chayot hena} \u8212- \u8220"for the Hebrew women are not like the Egyptian women, for they are {\i chayot}\u8221" \u8212- is read carefully by the {\i Gemara}. Does {\i chayot} mean midwives? The {\i Gemara} objects: {\i atu chaya mi lo tzaricha chaya achrita} \u8212- surely a midwife when she herself gives birth needs another midwife! A barber needs a barber to cut his own hair. So {\i chayot mamash} cannot be the meaning here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, {\i ela amru lo: uma zu kechaya nimshala} \u8212- they told him: this nation is compared to an animal. They give birth without help, as animals of the field do. And this is supported by the very tribal blessings of Jacob: Judah is a lion cub, Dan is a serpent, Naphtali a swift deer, Issachar a burdened donkey, Joseph a firstborn ox, Benjamin a wolf. For those tribes where the comparison is explicit in the text, it is written; and for those where it is not, the verse {\i mah imcha laviya bein arayot} \u8212- \u8220"your mother was a lioness among lions\u8221" \u8212- covers the rest. All of Israel, in some sense, is likened to animals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, in fact, a deeply layered {\i peshat}. The women who gave birth under the apple trees in the field, whose infants were swallowed into the earth and sprouted like grass, who came home in flocks \u8212- they are, in the most literal sense of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's narrative, being born as creatures of the natural world, beyond the reach of Pharaoh\u8217's machinery. But there is another dimension as well. Part of the process of enslaving a people is dehumanizing them \u8212- reducing them in the eyes of the oppressor to something less than human. Pharaoh could believe the midwives\u8217' claim precisely because he had already begun that process of dehumanization. Yet the {\i Gemara} turns that very trope on its head: yes, they are like animals \u8212- and that is their glory, their resilience, their source of redemption.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 12\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Midwives & Batim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro & Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up seven lines from the bottom of 11b, in the midst of the {\i aggadata} concerning the enslavement in Egypt \u8212- particularly fitting for {\i Chol HaMoed Pesach}. The broader context is the {\i mishnah}\u8217's principle of {\i midah keneged midah}, measure for measure: just as God repays the {\i sotah} according to what she did, so too does the divine calculus operate throughout history.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} states: {\i vayehi ki yar\u8217'u hameyaldot et haElohim vaya\u8217'as lahem batim} \u8212- when the midwives, identified with Yocheved and Miriam, feared God, He made for them {\i batim}, houses. A dispute between Rav and Shmuel turns on the meaning of these {\i batim}. One opinion holds {\i batei kehunah veleviyah}: houses of priesthood and Levitical service, referring to Aharon and Moshe who descended from Yocheved. The other holds {\i batei malchut}: houses of kingship. The first interpretation is relatively straightforward \u8212- Aharon stands at the head of the {\i kehunah} and Moshe is the preeminent of the Levites. The second requires more unpacking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Calev & Miriam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does kingship emerge from Yocheved and Miriam? The Gemara answers: {\i David nami miMiriam ka\u8217'ati} \u8212- David himself descends from Miriam. The relevant {\i pesukim} are in Divrei HaYamim, where Calev marries a woman named Azuvah, and then takes Efrat, who bears him Chur. Separately, David is called {\i ben ish Efrati}. The Gemara links these together: Calev ben Yefuneh married Miriam, who is identified with both Azuvah and Efrat. David descended from Calev and therefore, ultimately, from Miriam herself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Miriam\u8217's Genealogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Calev Marries Miriam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} in Divrei HaYamim reads: {\i veCalev ben Chetzron holid et Azuvah isha ve\u8217'et Yeriyot} \u8212- Calev son of Chetzron \u8220"begot\u8221" Azuvah the wife and Yeriyot. Several difficulties immediately arise. First, the {\i pasuk} says {\i ben Chetzron}, but we know him as Calev {\i ben Yefuneh}. The Gemara resolves this: {\i ben Yefuneh} is a descriptive epithet \u8212- {\i she\u8217'pinah me\u8217'atzat hameraglim}, he turned himself away from the counsel of the spies \u8212- while Chetzron is his father\u8217's proper name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further complication: Calev is also called {\i haKenizi}, associated with Kenaz, since the {\i pasuk} in Shoftim describes Osniel ben Kenaz as {\i achi Calev}, the brother of Calev, implying they share a father. Rava resolves this: Calev was Kenaz\u8217's stepson, not his biological child. The designation {\i haKenizi} reflects that he was raised in Kenaz\u8217's household without being his natural son. {\i Shema mina} \u8212- this resolves all identifications, and the Calev in the {\i pasuk} is Calev ben Yefuneh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, {\i zo Miriam} \u8212- Azuvah is Miriam. Why was she called Azuvah? {\i Shekol azvuha mitchilatah} \u8212- everyone had abandoned her, for she was sickly and no one wished to marry her. As for the word {\i holid} \u8212- \u8220"he begot\u8221" \u8212- the Gemara asks the obvious: surely he married her, he did not beget her? {\i Vehalo minsav hava nasiv lah} \u8212- of course he married her. This sets up the next teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage L\u8217'Shem Shamayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan derives from here: {\i kol hanos\u8217'e isha l\u8217'shem shamayim ma\u8217'aleh alav hakatuv ke\u8217'ilu yeladah} \u8212- whoever marries a woman {\i l\u8217'shem shamayim}, for the sake of Heaven, Scripture accounts it as though he had fathered her. Here, Calev married Miriam not out of desire or attraction \u8212- she was sick and abandoned, with no one to care for her \u8212- but purely for her benefit and for the sake of Heaven. Therefore the Torah uses the language of {\i holid}, as if he had given birth to her. The image of a husband as father-figure is striking to modern sensibilities, but reflects the reality of a society where such a marriage was genuinely an act of protection and selfless provision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} also mentions {\i Yeriyot} alongside Azuvah. Rather than reading this as a second wife, the Gemara interprets it as another name for Miriam: {\i she\u8217'hayu paneha domin liyeriyot} \u8212- her face resembled {\i yeriyot}, curtains, drawn and pale from illness. The following phrase, {\i ve\u8217'eileh baneha}, \u8220"these are her children,\u8221" is read {\i al tikri baneha ella boneha} \u8212- not her children but that which built her, describing how Calev\u8217's marriage restored and strengthened her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons\u8217' Names Interpreted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three sons listed \u8212- Yashar, Shovav, and Ardon \u8212- are interpreted not as literal children but as descriptions of Calev himself and what he accomplished. {\i Yashar}: {\i she\u8217'yishar et atzmo} \u8212- he made himself straight, whether by resisting the {\i meraglim} or by taking the upright path of marrying Miriam when others would not. {\i Shovav}: {\i she\u8217'shovev et yitzro} \u8212- he subjugated his {\i yetzer}, his inclination, again in resisting the {\i meraglim} or in choosing Miriam over someone more conventionally attractive. {\i Ardon}: {\i she\u8217'rada et yitzro} \u8212- he subdued his desire similarly. An alternative tradition reads {\i Ardon} as {\i she\u8217'panav domin levered} \u8212- his face was ruddy as a rose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashur & Tekoa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} continues: {\i veAshur avi Tekoa hayu shtei nashim Hel\u8217'ah veNa\u8217'arah} \u8212- Ashur the father of Tekoa had two wives, Hel\u8217'ah and Na\u8217'arah. The Gemara identifies Ashur with Calev once more. Why is he called Ashur? {\i She\u8217'hush\u8217'cheru panav beta\u8217'aniyot} \u8212- his face grew dark from the fasts he undertook, praying for the strength to withstand the pressure of the {\i meraglim}. This is a remarkable detail: the same Calev who stood before all of Bnei Yisrael with seemingly unshakeable courage could not rely on his own fortitude alone, but had to pour out his heart to God through fasting and prayer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Avi} \u8212- father: {\i she\u8217'nasa lah ke\u8217'av}, he became like a father to Miriam. {\i Tekoa}: {\i she\u8217'taka libo le\u8217'aviv she\u8217'bashamayim} \u8212- he drove his heart like a stake into his Father in Heaven, whether in the context of the {\i meraglim} or in his decision to marry {\i l\u8217'shem shamayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the \u8220"two wives,\u8221" Hel\u8217'ah and Na\u8217'arah: {\i na\u8217'asit Miriam k\u8217'shtei nashim} \u8212- Miriam became like two wives. {\i Lo Hel\u8217'ah veNa\u8217'arah hava} \u8212- these are not two distinct women. Rather, originally she was {\i Hel\u8217'ah}, sickly, and ultimately she became {\i Na\u8217'arah}, as strong and healthy as a young woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The children listed as {\i bnei Hel\u8217'ah} \u8212- Tzereth, Tzohar, and Etnan \u8212- are likewise interpreted in terms of this transformation. {\i Tzereth}: {\i she\u8217'na\u8217'asit tzarah lechaveroteha} \u8212- she became a rival to all the women around her, so stunning had she become. {\i Tzohar}: {\i she\u8217'hayu paneha domim ketzohorayim} \u8212- her face shone like midday. {\i Etnan} \u8212- which ordinarily refers to a harlot\u8217's payment \u8212- is interpreted here as: {\i shekol haro\u8217'eh otah molich etnan le\u8217'ishto}, that any man who saw her was so stirred that he would go and seek intimacy with his own wife, even offering her something akin to payment. The image is jarring, but conveys the idea of Miriam\u8217's remarkable transformation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Path to David\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With all this established, the {\i batei malchut} interpretation is complete. Miriam married Calev ben Yefuneh of the tribe of Yehudah, and David descended from that union. Thus the houses God made for the midwives include the house of kingship that ultimately produced David HaMelech.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pharaoh\u8217's Decrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drowning Edict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah states: {\i vayetzav Paroh lechol amo} \u8212- Pharaoh commanded his entire nation that every son born should be thrown into the Nile. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina notes that the phrase {\i lechol amo} indicates that the edict extended even to Pharaoh\u8217's own people \u8212- Egyptian children as well. Rashi, drawing on the Midrash Tanchuma, explains why: Pharaoh\u8217's astrologers had seen that the savior of Israel would be born on that day, but could not determine whether he was Egyptian or Hebrew. Since the identity was ambiguous \u8212- and indeed Moshe grew up in Pharaoh\u8217's palace and was even called {\i ish Mitzri} by the daughters of Yisro \u8212- Pharaoh cast the net wide and commanded that all male children born that day be drowned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Decrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina also enumerates three distinct decrees that Pharaoh issued. First, the instruction to the midwives: if it is a boy, kill him. Second, the broader command to throw every Hebrew male newborn into the Nile. Third, the final sweeping edict to his own people to cast even their own sons born on that day into the river. The decrees thus escalated in scope and cruelty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amram Divorces\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah then says: {\i vayelech ish mibeit Levi} \u8212- a man went from the house of Levi. This is Amram taking Yocheved. But the word {\i vayelech}, \u8220"he went,\u8221" requires explanation. Rav Yehuda bar Zvina says: {\i she\u8217'halach be\u8217'atzat bito} \u8212- he followed the advice of his daughter. This is the famous {\i midrash} connecting back to Miriam.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amram gadol hador hayah} \u8212- Amram was the preeminent figure of his generation. Once Pharaoh\u8217's edict to throw every newborn son into the Nile was issued, Amram reasoned: {\i l\u8217'shav anu ameilim} \u8212- it is for nothing that we labor, meaning that the effort of having children was futile if the boys would immediately be murdered. The word {\i ameilim} is slightly unusual here \u8212- it is the women who bear the burdens of pregnancy and birth \u8212- but the sense is clear. He then rose and divorced his wife. The purpose of the public divorce, rather than simply ceasing marital relations, seems to have been to make a statement that would prompt others to follow: {\i amdu kulam vegeirshu et neshoseihem} \u8212- all the men rose and divorced their wives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miriam\u8217's Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Miriam confronted her father: {\i abba, kasha gzeiratecha yoser mishel Paroh} \u8212- Father, your decree is harsher than Pharaoh\u8217's. The argument unfolds in three parts. First: Pharaoh decreed only against the males, but your act ensures that no female children will be born either. Second: Pharaoh decreed only for this world; by not bringing children into existence, you deny even those potential souls their share in {\i olam haba} \u8212- for even a child who is killed immediately has a soul that merits {\i olam haba}. Third \u8212- and this is the most striking element: {\i Paroh harasha, safek miskayemet gezeiraso safek einah miskayemet} \u8212- Pharaoh is a {\i rasha}, and it is uncertain whether his decrees will be fulfilled. But you, {\i tzaddik}, your decrees will certainly be fulfilled: {\i she\u8217'ne\u8217'emar vetigzar omer veyakom lach} \u8212- for it is said that when the righteous decree, it is established. Therefore, what you are doing is actually more harmful than what the {\i rasha} is doing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage Celebration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amad vehechezir et ishto} \u8212- Amram listened to Miriam and remarried Yocheved. {\i Amdu kulam vehecheziru et nesheihem} \u8212- and all the men followed suit. The {\i pasuk}\u8217's language of {\i vayelech ish} and {\i vayikach et bat Levi} sounds as though this is a first marriage. This is precisely why the Gemara had to construct this backstory: Aharon and Miriam are clearly older than Moshe, as the {\i pasuk} later says {\i vatitatzev achoto merachok} \u8212- his sister stood at a distance \u8212- so there must have been earlier children. The divorce and remarriage account explains the {\i pasuk}\u8217's language while preserving the chronology.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda bar Bizna adds: {\i she\u8217'asah lah ma\u8217'aseh likuchin} \u8212- he made of this remarriage a grand first-wedding celebration. {\i Hoshivah b\u8217'apiryon} \u8212- he seated her on a throne; {\i ve\u8217'Aharon veMiryam merakedin lefaneha} \u8212- Aharon and Miriam danced before her; {\i malachei hashareit amru} \u8212- the ministering angels proclaimed: {\i eim habanim semeichah} \u8212- the mother of children is rejoicing. Whether this refers to Yocheved\u8217's existing children or anticipates the birth of Moshe, the setting is one of great celebration and joy \u8212- which provides the backdrop for the conception and birth of Moshe.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yocheved\u8217's Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} calls Yocheved {\i bat Levi}, daughter of Levi, which implies youthfulness. The Gemara raises the difficulty: {\i efshar bat me\u8217'ah ushloshim shanah hava\u8217'i vekari lah bat?} \u8212- is it possible to call her a {\i bat}, a daughter, when she was 130 years old at the time of Moshe\u8217's birth? The tradition establishing her age is as follows: if she was born when Yaakov\u8217's family descended to Egypt and she was then 130 years old when Moshe was born, that aligns with Moshe being 80 years old at the time of the Exodus, having been born 130 years into the Egyptian sojourn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yocheved Born in Egypt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chama bar Rabbi Chanina addresses this through a textual reading: {\i zo Yocheved she\u8217'horatah baderech veleIdatah bein hachomot} \u8212- Yocheved\u8217's conception occurred while her mother was traveling down to Egypt, and she was born at the very moment of entry. The proof is the {\i pasuk}: {\i asher yaldah otah le-Levi beMitzrayim} \u8212- which is read as: her birth ({\i leidatah}) was in Egypt, but her conception ({\i horatah}) was not in Egypt. This could only be the case if she was born at the precise instant of entry into Egypt \u8212- between the walls, {\i bein hachomot}, at the city gates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 70 Souls Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretation resolves a well-known difficulty in the {\i pesukim}. When the Torah enumerates the souls who descended to Egypt, the count comes to 69, yet the Torah states there were 70. The answer is Yocheved: she was only being gestated during the journey, so there were 69 persons traveling, but the moment they entered Egypt she was born, making 70. This is the interpretive motivation for Rabbi Chama\u8217's reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Yocheved was born upon the entry to Egypt and the sojourn lasted 210 years, with Moshe being born 130 years in and living to 80 at the time of the Exodus, then Yocheved was indeed 130 years old at Moshe\u8217's birth. The question of how she can be called {\i bat} remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yocheved\u8217's Youth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda answers: the signs of her youth returned to her, as they had to Sarah \u8212- she regained the physiological markers of youthfulness, including the ability to conceive. The {\i Ibn Ezra} raises a sharp objection: by Sarah, the Torah makes an enormous point of the miraculous nature of her conceiving at 90, yet here at 130 the Torah passes over it without comment. The {\i Ramban} responds at length, noting that there are many hidden miracles the Torah does not record explicitly. In any case, this is the Gemara\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Month Pregnancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda bar Zvina adds a further detail that will become important shortly: Yocheved was already three months pregnant at the time of Amram\u8217's remarriage. The great celebratory {\i vayikach} \u8212- all that fanfare of the wedding, the dancing, the angels singing \u8212- occurred when she had already been carrying Moshe for three months. We will see momentarily why the Gemara requires this detail.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe\u8217's Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Painless Birth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} says {\i vatahar ha\u8217'isha vateled ben} \u8212- the woman became pregnant and gave birth to a son. If she was already pregnant at the time of the remarriage, why does the Torah speak of her pregnancy as beginning now? Rav Yehuda bar Zvina answers: {\i makish leidatah le-horatah} \u8212- the Torah juxtaposes her giving birth to her conceiving in order to teach something. {\i Mah horatah she\u8217'lo betza\u8217'ar af leidatah she\u8217'lo betza\u8217'ar} \u8212- just as her conception was without pain, so too her birth was without pain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection between conception and birth here teaches the principle: {\i mikan l\u8217'nashim tzidkaniyot she\u8217'lo hayu befitko shel Chava} \u8212- righteous women are not subject to the curse of Chava, who was told she would give birth in pain. It is worth noting that when the epidural became available and voices in some religious communities raised objections on theological grounds \u8212- citing the curse of Chava \u8212- there was never any corresponding objection from the Jewish community. For us, a curse is not something to be preserved but to be escaped, and here the Gemara makes clear that righteous women stand outside it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House of Light\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah says {\i vatere oso ki tov hu} \u8212- she saw that he was good. The question is what this means. Every mother finds her child beautiful; what does this observation tell us that is exceptional? The sages offer multiple answers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The view of the Sages is that when Moshe was born, {\i nitmalei habayis kulo or} \u8212- the entire house filled with light. This is the image most are familiar with, as Rashi quotes it on the {\i pasuk} in the Torah. The connection to {\i ki tov} is drawn from a {\i gezerah shavah}: the same phrase appears at the creation of light \u8212- {\i vayar Elokim et ha\u8217'or ki tov} \u8212- and just as God saw the light and called it good, Yocheved saw Moshe and perceived the divine light radiating from him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir says: {\i tov shemo} \u8212- {\i ki tov hu} simply means his name was Tov. This gives Moshe a Hebrew name bestowed by his mother, which is meaningful since {\i Moshe} is in fact an Egyptian name \u8212- present, for example, in {\i Rameses}, which means {\i Ra-Moshe}, born of Ra. {\i Ki min hamayim meshisihu} \u8212- \u8220"for I drew him from the water\u8221" \u8212- uses a Hebrew root that in context carries an Egyptian connotation of birth from water. Rabbi Meir\u8217's reading restores a Hebrew name to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda says his name was Tuvia \u8212- reading {\i ki tov hu} as {\i tov} plus {\i Yah}, the divine name: God is good. Rabbi Nechemia says: {\i hogun lenevu\u8217'ah} \u8212- she perceived that he possessed the potential for prophecy, though the text does not indicate how she perceived this. {\i Acheirim} say he was {\i nolad keshe\u8217'hu mahul} \u8212- born already circumcised, without a foreskin, which was itself a visible sign of his singular nature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Month Hiding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatispenehu shlosha yerachim} \u8212- she hid him for three months. The Gemara asks: why only three months? Why could she not simply continue hiding him? The answer requires understanding how the Egyptians were tracking the situation. When the Egyptians saw that the Hebrew men had remarried their wives, they began counting nine months forward from that date, anticipating when births would occur. They then sent informants to locate hidden babies through a disturbing method: {\i kol heichah de\u8217'hava shamei Mitzrayim demisyalid yanuka, mamtu yanuka hatam} \u8212- wherever Egyptians heard that a baby was expected, they would bring an infant of their own, slap it to make it cry, and the hidden baby would cry in response, revealing its location. Based on a {\i pasuk} in Shir HaShirim \u8212- {\i achazo lanu shualim shualim ketanim} \u8212- the small foxes, little babies used to ferret out other babies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the three-month detail becomes crucial. {\i Delo manu Mitzrayim ela mish\u8217'as hadra} \u8212- the Egyptians counted nine months from the date of the remarriage. But Yocheved was already three months pregnant at that point. So they expected birth nine months from the remarriage, while Moshe was actually born six months after the remarriage \u8212- three months earlier than the Egyptians anticipated. This bought Yocheved three months of undetected concealment. Once that window closed and the Egyptians began actively searching, she could no longer hide him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The Basket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wicker Material\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatikach lo teivat gome} \u8212- she took a basket of wicker reeds. Why specifically wicker? Rabbi Elazar says: {\i mikan l\u8217'tzadikim shemamounam chaviv aleihem yoter migufam} \u8212- from here we learn that the righteous value their money more than their bodies, choosing inexpensive materials. And why are they so careful with money? {\i Lefi she\u8217'ein poshtin yedeihem begazel} \u8212- because they scrupulously avoid theft, they are equally careful with what they spend. This is a striking interpretation, and the Gemara immediately senses the difficulty: being economical is one thing, but applying that principle to a basket meant to save your child\u8217's life seems misplaced. Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani therefore offers a more functional explanation: wicker was chosen because it is flexible \u8212- {\i davar rach she\u8217'yachol la\u8217'amod bifnei davar rach uvifnei davar kasheh} \u8212- it can absorb impact whether it encounters something soft or something hard. A rigid material would risk puncturing and sinking; a flexible one could bend without breaking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plastering Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatachmira vachamer uvazafet} \u8212- she plastered it with slime and pitch. A {\i tanna} teaches: {\i chamer mibifnim vezafet mibachutz} \u8212- the slime was on the inside and the pitch was on the outside. The reason: {\i kedei shelo yariach oso tzaddik rei\u8217'ach ra} \u8212- so that the {\i tzaddik}, the infant Moshe, should not have to smell the foul odor of the pitch. The plaster layer on the interior served to insulate him from the smell of the tar on the outside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Placement in Reeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatasem basuf} \u8212- she placed it among the {\i suf}. Rabbi Elazar says this refers to {\i Yam Suf}; Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani says {\i suf} simply means a swamp or reed bed, as the {\i pasuk} elsewhere uses {\i kaneh vasuf} to refer to reeds, making {\i suf} a species of aquatic plant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pharaoh\u8217's Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vaterd bat Paroh lirchotz al haYe\u8217'or} \u8212- Pharaoh\u8217's daughter went down to bathe at the Nile. Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai explains: {\i melamed sheyardah lirchotz migilulei aviha} \u8212- she went to cleanse herself from the excrement, meaning the idolatry of her father. The washing was a conversion ritual. The proof: the {\i pasuk} in Yeshayahu uses the image of washing to describe the removal of spiritual filth \u8212- {\i im rachatz Hashem et tzoa\u8217'at bnot Tzion} \u8212- establishing that washing can represent purification from sin rather than physical bathing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maidservants Killed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vena\u8217'aroteha holchot} \u8212- her maidservants were walking alongside her. Rabbi Yochanan reads {\i holchot} here as a euphemism for death, in the same way the {\i pasuk} says {\i hineh anochi holech lamut} \u8212- I am going to die. When the maidservants saw that Pharaoh\u8217's daughter intended to rescue Moshe, they protested: even when the whole world does not obey a king\u8217's decree, his own household should. How could she violate her father\u8217's {\i gezeirah}? {\i Ba Gavriel vechavatam bakarka} \u8212- the angel Gavriel came and struck them into the ground, killing them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to the earlier narrative is worth noting. Amram issued a {\i gezeirah} and his daughter Miriam rose to undermine it through persuasion. Here, Pharaoh issued a {\i gezeirah} and his daughter bat Paroh rose to violate it through action. A {\i tzaddik}\u8217's decree is certain to stand; a {\i rasha}\u8217's is not. The same principle echoes through both episodes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One maidservant was left alive. {\i Delav orcha de-bas malka lemeikam lechudeih} \u8212- it would not be proper for the king\u8217's daughter to stand entirely alone without an attendant. Gavriel spared one for the sake of decorum.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Discovery & Rescue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extended Arm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatishlach et amatah vatikacheha} \u8212- she sent her {\i amah} and took the basket. A dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Nechemia: one says {\i amah} means her hand \u8212- from {\i amah}, the cubit measure, referring to her arm \u8212- and the other says it means her maidservant, the standard meaning of {\i amah}. The one who reads {\i shifchah}, maidservant, asks: if you mean hand, why not use the clearer word {\i yad}? The one who reads {\i amah} as arm asks: if you mean maidservant, it should have said {\i shifchah}; the word {\i amah} is ambiguous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i amah} means her arm, then what does the text convey by using this unusual word? {\i De\u8217'ishtarbovei ishtarbuba} \u8212- her arm miraculously extended, stretching out an {\i amah} beyond its natural reach to grasp the basket. This is compared to Achashveirosh extending the scepter to Esther, and to the {\i pasuk}: {\i shinei resha\u8217'im shibarta} \u8212- Resh Lakish reads not {\i shibarta}, \u8220"you broke their teeth,\u8221" but {\i sherivavta} \u8212- \u8220"you caused them to elongate,\u8221" another miraculous extension in a different context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seeing Divine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatiftach vatireihu et hayeled} \u8212- she opened the basket and saw the child. The Hebrew uses {\i vatireihu} \u8212- she saw {\i him} \u8212- when we might have expected simply {\i vatire}, she saw the child. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina explains: {\i she\u8217'ra\u8217'atah Shechinah imo} \u8212- she saw the divine presence accompanying the child. {\i Vatireihu} \u8212- she saw God {\i et hayeled}, with the child. This connects directly to the earlier teaching that the house filled with light at Moshe\u8217's birth: here too, bat Paroh perceives something supernatural.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child Crying\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehinei naar bocheh} \u8212- there was a child crying. The text uses two different words: {\i yeled}, an infant, and {\i naar}, a young man. A {\i tanna} offers: {\i hu yeled vekolo ke\u8217'naar} \u8212- he was an infant in body but his voice was that of a young man, a powerful, adult cry. This is the view of Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voice & Blemish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nechemia objects: {\i im kein asita lo l\u8217'Moshe Rabbeinu b\u8217'mum} \u8212- you have made Moshe Rabbeinu into a person with a defect. For an infant to have so deep and powerful a voice would itself be a physical anomaly, a blemish. Rashi adds an additional dimension: since the Levites served as singers in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, such an aberrant voice in childhood would, somewhat anachronistically, invalidate someone from that service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nechemia therefore offers an alternative reading: {\i melamed she\u8217'asta lo imo chuppat ne\u8217'urim} \u8212- his mother had made him a small {\i chuppah}, a wedding canopy, inside the basket. {\i Bateivah amrah: shema lo ezkeh l\u8217'chuppato} \u8212- in placing him in the basket she thought, perhaps I will never live to see his wedding, so let me make him one now. The word {\i naar} thus refers not to his voice but to this miniature {\i chuppah}, recalling the wedding celebration that had marked his parents\u8217' remarriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Circumcision Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatomer miyaldei ha\u8217'ivrim zeh} \u8212- she said, this is one of the Hebrew children. How did she know? Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina: {\i she\u8217'ra\u8217'atah oso mahul} \u8212- she saw that he was circumcised. This is presumably distinct from the earlier tradition that he was born without a foreskin \u8212- here the implication is that he had been circumcised in the ordinary manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unwitting Prophecy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i zeh} \u8212- \u8220"this one\u8221" \u8212- receives close attention. Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i melamed she\u8217'nisnaveit shelo mida\u8217'atah} \u8212- she prophesied without knowing what she was saying. {\i Zeh nofel, ve\u8217'ein acher nofel} \u8212- this one was cast into the Nile, and no other would be. The moment Moshe was placed in the Nile, the astrological sign that had prompted the edict was read as fulfilled, and the decree ceased. Bat Paroh\u8217's words were thus an unconscious prophecy: {\i this} child, and no other, was \u8220"the one cast into the Nile\u8221" in the sense the edict had in mind.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Astrologers Confused\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saw by Water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar now brings a {\i pasuk} from Yeshayahu that mocks those who practice necromancy and divination: {\i hamitzpitzpim vehamehaggim}, those who chirp and mutter. The Gemara reads this as a description of astrologers generally: {\i tzofim} \u8212- they see something \u8212- {\i mitzpitzpim} \u8212- they read signs \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'einam yodim mah tzofim} \u8212- but they do not fully understand what they are seeing; {\i mahagim} \u8212- they speak \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'einam yodim mah mahagim} \u8212- but they do not fully understand what they are saying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What did Pharaoh\u8217's astrologers see? {\i Ra\u8217'u shemoshi\u8217'an shel Yisrael bamayim hu lokeh} \u8212- they saw that the savior of Israel would be punished by water. On this basis they issued the decree to cast all male children into the Nile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edict Ceased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Keivan deshadyuhu le-Moshe} \u8212- once Moshe was thrown into the Nile, {\i amru: tu lo chazina ki hai simana} \u8212- the astrologers said, we no longer see that sign in the heavens. {\i Batlu legezerataihu} \u8212- and they annulled the decree. Their reasoning: the savior has been cast into the water, punished, and is presumably dead. There is no longer cause for alarm. {\i Vehem einam yodim} \u8212- but they did not understand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mei Merivah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki al Mei Merivah hu lokeh} \u8212- the savior would not be punished by the Nile but by the waters of Merivah. The sign the astrologers saw was real; their interpretation was wrong. Rabbi Chama bar Rabbi Chanina derives this from the {\i pasuk}: {\i hemah Mei Merivah asher ravu} \u8212- these are the waters of Merivah, the very same waters that the astrologers of Pharaoh had seen {\i vetau}, and about which they were confused. They saw water, they thought the Nile; the true referent was Merivah, where Moshe would later strike the rock rather than speak to it, and for that he would be denied entry into the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Credit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally: {\i shesh me\u8217'ot elef ragli} \u8212- the word {\i ragli} is read by the Gemara not as \u8220"foot soldiers\u8221" but as {\i bishvili}, \u8220"because of me.\u8221" Moshe says to Israel: {\i bishvili nitzaltem kulchem} \u8212- all 600,000 of you were saved because of me. When I was cast into the Nile, the astrological sign was read as fulfilled, the edict ceased, and you were spared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the obvious objection: the entire edict was made in the first place because of Moshe, since it was his birth that the astrologers feared. How can he take credit for ending a decree that he caused? Tosafot answers: once Moshe was cast into the Nile and the astrologers declared the sign fulfilled, not only did the decree targeting Moshe\u8217's generation end \u8212- even the older, pre-astrological impulse to persecute and destroy the Israelites, which had manifested even in the initial orders to the midwives, ceased entirely. In that broader sense, Moshe brought about the complete cessation of the murderous decrees, and it is for this that he takes credit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 13\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe\u8217's Birth & Miriam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 21st Nissan Dating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the midst of a series of {\i midrashim} surrounding {\i yetziat mitzrayim}, specifically the birth of Moshe Rabbeinu. We pick up with Rabbi Hanina bar Pappa, who derives from the word {\i zeh} in the {\i pasuk} a precise date for when Moshe was discovered by {\i Bat Pharaoh}. His position is that {\i oto yom esrim ve\u8217'echad b\u8217'Nissan haya} \u8212- that day was the 21st of Nissan. On that basis, the heavenly angels said before God: {\i Ribbono shel olam}, the one who in the future will sing the {\i shira al hayam} on this very day \u8212- should he be smitten on this very same day?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 6th Sivan Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva bar Rabbi Hanina disagrees. According to him, {\i oto yom shisha b\u8217'Sivan haya} \u8212- the day Moshe was discovered was not the 21st of Nissan but the 6th of Sivan, the very day the Torah would later be given at Har Sinai. The angels\u8217' protestation is framed accordingly: the one who in the future will receive Torah from Har Sinai on this day \u8212- should he be smitten on this day? Both opinions derive their respective dates from the emphatic use of {\i zeh} in the {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i miyaldei ha\u8217'ivrim zeh} \u8212- understanding the word as pointing toward a day of particular cosmic significance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 7th Adar Birth/Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now examines which opinion is internally coherent with the tradition that Moshe was hidden for three months before his discovery. The underlying datum is the teaching: {\i beshiva b\u8217'Adar met uveshiva b\u8217'Adar nolad} \u8212- Moshe died on the 7th of Adar and was born on the 7th of Adar. His death date on the 7th of Adar is derived from the account in {\i Sefer Yehoshua}: Bnei Yisrael mourned Moshe for thirty days, after which God told Yehoshua to cross the Jordan, and that crossing, as the {\i pesukim} in Yehoshua make clear, occurred on the 10th of Nissan \u8212- counting back thirty days yields the 7th of Adar. The birth date on the same day is derived from the principle that {\i tzaddikim} live out complete years, dying on the anniversary of their birth, along with Moshe\u8217's own declaration {\i ben me\u8217'ah ve\u8217'esrim shana anochi hayom} \u8212- \u8220"today I am exactly 120 years old\u8221" \u8212- implying that the day of his death was precisely the day of his birth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leap Year Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i bishlamah} for the opinion that Moshe was discovered on the 6th of Sivan, the arithmetic works out elegantly \u8212- three months from the 7th of Adar yields Adar, Nissan, Iyar, Sivan, arriving at the 6th or 7th of Sivan, aligning with {\i Matan Torah}. But for the opinion that he was discovered on the 21st of Nissan, how can three months from the 7th of Adar reach only the 21st of Nissan? The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i osa shana me\u8217'uberet hayita} \u8212- that year was a leap year. Moshe was born on the 7th of Adar Rishon; he had the remainder of Adar Rishon, the entirety of Adar Sheni, and 21 days of Nissan \u8212- most of the first month, all of the middle month, and most of the third month, satisfying the requirement of {\i rubo shel rishon}, {\i rubo shel acharon}, and {\i emtza\u8217'ei shalem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns to the verse {\i ha\u8217'elech vekarati lach isha meneiket min ha\u8217'ivriyot} \u8212- shall I go and call a nursemaid from the Hebrew women? Why specifically Hebrew women? The {\i Gemara} teaches: {\i melamed she-hechziru l\u8217'Moshe al kol hamitzriyot} \u8212- they had already attempted to have Moshe nurse from Egyptian women, and he refused from every one of them. The reasoning given is: {\i peh she\u8217'atid ledaber im haShechina yanik davar tamei} \u8212- the mouth that would one day speak directly with the Divine Presence, should it suckle something impure? The impurity referred to is not the person of the Egyptian woman herself, but the non-kosher food she consumed, the effects of which pass into a nursing mother\u8217's milk. This {\i aggadic} passage carries real halakhic resonances among the {\i poskim} regarding whether the food of a nursing mother must meet kosher standards, given that the infant, who is below the age of {\i chinuch}, would still be receiving some spiritual or physical effect of {\i metamtem et halev} \u8212- the heart-dulling quality that the {\i Gemara} elsewhere attributes to non-kosher food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knowledge Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} connects Moshe\u8217's refusal to nurse from Egyptian women to the verse: {\i l\u8217'mi yoreh de\u8217'ah v\u8217'et mi yavin shemu\u8217'ah gemulei mechalav atikei mishadayim} \u8212- \u8220"To whom shall one teach knowledge, and who shall understand the tradition? One who weans himself from milk and removes himself from the breast.\u8221" This verse is now understood as a reference to Moshe, who as an infant refused the milk of Egyptian women, thereby preserving the sanctity of the mouth destined to speak before God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses the verse {\i vattomer lah Bat Pharaoh lechi}, in which {\i Bat Pharaoh} tells Miriam to go. Rabbi Elazar derives from the subsequent {\i vattelech ha\u8217'almah} \u8212- \u8220"and the young woman went\u8221" \u8212- that she went {\i bizrizut k\u8217'almah}, with the alacrity of a young woman. The {\i Gemara} then notes {\i vattelech ha\u8217'almah vatikra et em hayeled}, and Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani explains why she is called {\i ha\u8217'almah}: {\i she-he\u8217'elimah et dvareha} \u8212- she concealed her words, presenting Yocheved as merely a Hebrew nursing woman without revealing that she was Moshe\u8217's own mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse then continues: {\i vattomer lah Bat Pharaoh heichi et hayeled hazeh} \u8212- here, take this child. Rabbi Chama bar Rabbi Chanina observes: {\i misnave\u8217'ah ve\u8217'einah yoda\u8217'at mah misnave\u8217'ah} \u8212- she prophesied without knowing what she was prophesying. The word {\i heichi}, parsed as {\i hei} and {\i sheleichi}, carries the meaning \u8220"here is what is yours\u8221" \u8212- {\i Bat Pharaoh} unknowingly declared that this was Yocheved\u8217's own child. Rabbi Chama bar Rabbi Chanina adds a broader principle: {\i lo dayam latzaddikim she-machzirim lahem avedatan} \u8212- it is not sufficient for the righteous that their lost objects are returned to them; {\i ela she-notnim lahem scharam} \u8212- they are also paid for retrieving what was already theirs to begin with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miriam\u8217's Prophecy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} transitions to the verse at {\i Shirat Hayam}: {\i vattikach Miriam hanevia achot Aharon}. The question is posed: why does it say {\i achot Aharon} \u8212- Aaron\u8217's sister \u8212- and not Moshe\u8217's sister? The answer, cited in the name of Rav Amram citing Rav, and some say Rav Nachman citing Rav: {\i melamed she-nitnave\u8217'ah k\u8217'she-hi achot Aharon} \u8212- this teaches that she had already attained the status of {\i nevia} while she was only Aaron\u8217's sister, before Moshe was born. This implies that her prophecy concerned Moshe\u8217's birth: {\i atida imi sheteleid ben} \u8212- my mother will give birth to a son. Her father kissed her on her head and said, {\i bitti, nitkayma nevuatecha} \u8212- my daughter, your prophecy has been fulfilled \u8212- and, as a result, he restored his wife, bringing Yocheved back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miriam\u8217's Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet once Moshe was placed into the Nile, the father struck Miriam on her head and said: {\i bitti, heichan nevuatecha} \u8212- my daughter, where is your prophecy now? This explains the verse {\i vatitatzav achoto merachok lada\u8217'at mah yei\u8217'aseh lo}: Miriam stood at a distance not merely to watch over her brother, but {\i lada\u8217'at mah yihyeh b\u8217'sof nevuatah} \u8212- to see what would ultimately become of her prophecy, whether it would in fact be fulfilled. The contrast between Miriam\u8217's long-term prophetic perspective and Amram\u8217's immediate, reactive responses \u8212- first to Pharaoh\u8217's decree by divorcing Yocheved, then to Moshe\u8217's placement in the Nile by striking Miriam \u8212- runs as a recurring thread through this section of the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosef Buries Yaakov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Entourage Precedence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now moves to the burial of Yaakov, examining the {\i pasuk}: {\i vaya\u8217'al Yosef likbor et aviv vaya\u8217'alu ito kol avdei Pharaoh} \u8212- Yosef went up to bury his father, accompanied by all the servants of Pharaoh. The {\i Gemara} notes that on the way out, Pharaoh\u8217's entourage preceded Yosef\u8217's own family \u8212- {\i kol beit Yosef ve\u8217'echav u-veit aviv} \u8212- yet on the return journey, the order was reversed: {\i vayashov Yosef Mitzraima hu ve\u8217'echav v\u8217'hadar kol ha-olim ito likbor et aviv}, where Yosef and his family are mentioned first. Rabbi Yochanan explains: {\i batchila shelo ra\u8217'u bikhvodan shel Yisrael} \u8212- initially the Egyptians had not yet witnessed the honor afforded to Israel, so they did not show them honor; {\i ul\u8217'vasof she-ra\u8217'u bikhvodan, nahagu bahem kavod} \u8212- in the end, once they witnessed that honor firsthand, they treated them accordingly. The {\i Gemara} now turns to where this transformation of honor occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Atad & Crowns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the verse {\i vayavo\u8217'u ad goren ha-Atad} \u8212- they came to the threshing floor of the Atad, meaning thornbushes or thistles. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i v\u8217'chi goren yesh l\u8217'atad} \u8212- who makes a threshing floor for thorns? Rabbi Abbahu explains: {\i melamed she-hikifuhu ktarim l\u8217'arono shel Yaakov} \u8212- what the verse is describing is that they surrounded Yaakov\u8217's coffin with crowns, the various kings who came to pay their respects each placing a crown there. The word {\i atad}, with its connotation of sharp thorns, thus evokes the image of a {\i goren} \u8212- a threshing floor \u8212- surrounded by a fence of thorns, which Rashi understands as a barrier to keep animals out. But the deeper image is of Yaakov\u8217's coffin encircled by the pointed crowns of kings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Descendants\u8217' Arrival\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Braitah} is cited: {\i kulam lemilchamah ba\u8217'u} \u8212- they had not come to pay honor but to make war. Bnei Esav, Bnei Yishmael, and Bnei Keturah \u8212- all the descendants of Avraham outside of the line of Yaakov \u8212- came with the intention of attacking the Israelite family as they returned with Yaakov\u8217's coffin into the land they had been occupying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef\u8217's Crown\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet when they saw Yosef\u8217's crown suspended over Yaakov\u8217's coffin \u8212- {\i kivan she-ra\u8217'u kitro shel Yosef taluy be\u8217'arono shel Yaakov} \u8212- Yosef being the viceroy of Egypt and the most powerful figure present \u8212- their intention shifted. {\i Natlu kulam kitreihem u-tla\u8217'um be\u8217'arono shel Yaakov} \u8212- they all took their own crowns and hung them on Yaakov\u8217's coffin as well. The irony is apparent: the Egyptians in Pharaoh\u8217's entourage came to show honor to Yosef as an Egyptian dignitary, not specifically to honor Bnei Yisrael \u8212- and yet the net effect was a massive public demonstration of honor to Yaakov.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 36 Crowns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Braitah} teaches: {\i sheloshim ve-shishah ktarim nitlu be\u8217'arono shel Yaakov} \u8212- thirty-six crowns were ultimately hung on Yaakov\u8217's coffin. Rashi notes that one can roughly arrive at this number by tallying the princes listed in the genealogies of Esav and Yishmael, arriving close to thirty-six even if the count does not come out exactly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Esav\u8217's Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When they arrived at {\i Me\u8217'arat haMachpelah}, Esav came to obstruct the burial. The {\i Gemara} provides background via the teaching of Rav Yitzchak: {\i Kiryat Arba, arba zugot hayu} \u8212- the name Kiryat Arba, the city of the four, refers to the four pairs buried there: Adam and Chavah, Avraham and Sarah, Yitzchak and Rivkah, and Yaakov and Leah. Three and a half pairs were already interred. Esav argued: Yaakov buried Leah in his portion, and the remaining niche is mine. Even if I sold the privileges of the firstborn, {\i peshitosah mi zvini} \u8212- I did not sell my basic rights as a regular son, and therefore my share of the cave remains mine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bnei Yaakov responded that he had in fact sold all rights, citing Yaakov\u8217's own declaration {\i bekivri asher kariti li} \u8212- \u8220"my grave that I have acquired for myself.\u8221" Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yehotzadak explains: {\i ein kirah ela leshon mekhirah} \u8212- the word {\i kira} in this {\i pasuk} means not to dig but to purchase, as on the islands ({\i karkei hayam}) the word for purchase is {\i kira}. Thus Yaakov\u8217's insistence on {\i kariti li} \u8212- I acquired this for myself \u8212- strongly implies that his ownership of the specific niche was not merely ancestral but was actively established and transferred to him. Esav demanded proof: {\i havu li igarta} \u8212- show me the document. Bnei Yaakov replied that the document had been left behind in Egypt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Naftali\u8217's Mission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There was no difficulty in retrieving it quickly: {\i nizil Naftali} \u8212- let us send Naftali, {\i dekal ke\u8217'ayalta} \u8212- who is as swift as a hind, as the {\i pasuk} says {\i Naftali ayalah shelucha ha-noten imrei shefer} \u8212- Naftali is a swift deer who delivers beautiful words. Rabbi Abbahu applies {\i al tikri} here: {\i al tikri imrei shefer ela imrei sefer} \u8212- do not read \u8220"beautiful words\u8221" but \u8220"words of the document,\u8221" for Naftali was being sent to fetch the written proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chushim Kills Esav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While everyone waited for Naftali to return from Egypt, Chushim son of Dan was present. He was {\i yakiran leh udneih} \u8212- hard of hearing, literally his ears were heavy. He asked what was happening, and was told that Esav was holding up the burial until Naftali returned from Egypt with the document. Chushim\u8217's reaction was immediate: {\i ve\u8217'ad de\u8217'ati Naftali me\u8217'ara demitzrayim yehei avi abba mutal be-vizayon?} \u8212- Until Naftali returns from Egypt, my father\u8217's father should lie there in disgrace? He took a rod, struck Esav on the head, and Esav\u8217's eyes fell out and landed on Yaakov\u8217's lap \u8212- the coffin being evidently open. {\i Patchinhu Yaakov le\u8217'einei} \u8212- Yaakov opened his eyes and smiled. The {\i Gemara} cites the verse {\i yismach tzaddik ki chaza nakam} \u8212- the righteous will rejoice when he sees retribution done, {\i pe\u8217'amav yirchatz bedam harasha} \u8212- and will wash his feet in the blood of the wicked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note a difficulty: the {\i pesukim} seem to record a genuine reconciliation between Yaakov and Esav. Why is this narrative, with its violent resolution, the satisfying endpoint? The {\i Gemara}\u8217's presentation here likely reflects the perspective of {\i Chazal} who, living under Roman \u8212- and later other \u8212- oppressive powers, understood Esav as a recurring historical antagonist to Bnei Yisrael. What read as personal reconciliation in the {\i pesukim} becomes, in the broader sweep of history, an unresolved tension that demands ultimate resolution. There is something genuinely comforting, from within that framework, in the image of the righteous smiling at the sight of justice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yaakov Laughs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Be\u8217'otah sha\u8217'ah nitkayma nevo\u8217'atah shel Rivkah} \u8212- at that moment the prophecy of Rivkah was fulfilled. She had said {\i lamah eshkal gam shneichem yom echad} \u8212- why should I be bereaved of both of you in one day? Although Yaakov and Esav did not die on precisely the same day, {\i kevuratam miha beyom echad hava} \u8212- their burials occurred on the same day, and in that sense her prophecy found its fulfillment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosef-Moshe Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef\u8217's Leadership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a question about the earlier {\i pasuk}: if Yosef\u8217's brothers were also involved in carrying Yaakov up to Canaan \u8212- as the verse {\i vayisu oto vanav artza Kena\u8217'an} indicates \u8212- why does the primary {\i pasuk} credit Yosef alone? The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i amru hanichu lo} \u8212- the brothers said, let Yosef take the lead, {\i kevodo bi-mlachim yoter mibedyotot} \u8212- it is greater honor for Yaakov to be buried by kings than by ordinary people. They did not remove themselves from the burial; they deferred to Yosef\u8217's royal prominence to maximize the honor afforded to their father.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosef-Moshe Parallel (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Care\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Just as Yosef was the most prominent figure of his generation and took the lead in burying Yaakov, so too Moshe \u8212- the greatest of his generation \u8212- took it upon himself to carry Yosef\u8217's bones out of Egypt. The {\i Gemara} draws this as an explicit and intentional parallel, underscoring a principle of reciprocal care for the dead across generations. The narrative of Yosef carrying Yaakov\u8217's bones from Egypt to Canaan is thus mirrored in Moshe carrying Yosef\u8217's bones from Egypt to Canaan. There is moreover a deep sense of closure in this: the long arc of Yosef\u8217's oath to the brothers, the period of enslavement during which the hope of redemption may have seemed forgotten, and the ultimate fulfillment of the return to the land \u8212- all are gathered together in the act of Moshe lifting Yosef\u8217's bones.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe Gets Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe & Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu Rabbanan}: come and see how precious the {\i mitzvot} were to Moshe \u8212- {\i shechol Yisrael kulan nit\u8217'asku beviza} \u8212- all of Israel was occupied with the spoils of Egypt, the gold and silver and borrowed vessels, {\i vehu nit\u8217'asek bemitzvot} \u8212- while he devoted himself to the {\i mitzvah} of carrying Yosef\u8217's bones. This is what is meant by {\i chacham lev yikach mitzvot} \u8212- the wise of heart will take on {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Serach bat Asher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How did Moshe know where Yosef was buried? {\i Amru: Serach bat Asher nishtayra me\u8217'oto hador} \u8212- Serach daughter of Asher was the sole survivor still living from the generation of Yosef. Moshe went to her and asked: {\i klum at yoda\u8217'at heichan Yosef kavur?} The {\i Gemara} frames this detail with quiet pathos: if Bnei Yisrael had maintained the hope of redemption alive during the years of enslavement, one would expect them to have preserved the tradition of Yosef\u8217's burial place. That only this one elderly woman knew suggests how completely {\i yisrael} had despaired. It is also notable, as has been observed throughout this {\i sugya}, that it is women \u8212- Miriam, Serach \u8212- who serve as the keepers of tradition and the carriers of hope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metal Casket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Serach told Moshe: {\i Aron shel matechet asu lo Mitzrayim} \u8212- the Egyptians had made Yosef a metal casket, {\i ukeva\u8217'uhu baNilus} \u8212- and they had sunk it in the Nile, {\i kedei sheyitbarchu meimav} \u8212- so that the waters of the Nile would be blessed by Yosef\u8217's presence. Yosef\u8217's capacity for sustaining Egypt through the famine had left such an impression that even in death the Egyptians sought to harness his blessing for their river.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe Calls Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Halach Moshe ve\u8217'amad al sfat Nilus} \u8212- Moshe went and stood at the bank of the Nile and called out: {\i Yosef, Yosef, higa zman she-nishba HaKadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- the time has come that God swore to redeem, {\i ve-higi\u8217'ah ha-shvu\u8217'ah she-hishbata Yisrael} \u8212- and the oath that you made Israel swear has also arrived. {\i Im ata mareh atzmacha mutav; ve\u8217'im lav, harei anu menukim mi-shvu\u8217'atecha} \u8212- if you show yourself, good; if not, we are absolved of your oath. We have done everything we can. Immediately, {\i miyad tzaf arono shel Yosef} \u8212- Yosef\u8217's coffin floated to the surface.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elisha Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} anticipates the obvious question: how does iron float? It cites the episode from {\i Sefer Melachim} in which Elisha caused a borrowed iron axe-head that had fallen into the water to float by throwing a piece of wood into the spot where it had sunk. From this a {\i kal vachomer} is constructed: if the iron floated for Elisha, who was a student of Eliyahu who was a student of Moshe, {\i bifnei Moshe Rabbeinu al achat kamah vechamah} \u8212- how much more readily would iron float for Moshe himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crypt Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan offers an alternative tradition: Yosef was not buried in the Nile at all but {\i bikvarnit shel melachim} \u8212- in the royal crypt, the catacombs of the kings of Egypt. According to this view, Moshe went to the royal crypt, made the same declaration \u8212- {\i Yosef, higi\u8217'a et she-nishba HaKadosh Baruch Hu she\u8217'ani go\u8217'el etchem} \u8212- and at that moment {\i nizdaze\u8217'ah arono shel Yosef}, Yosef\u8217's coffin shook free from its niche, and Moshe removed it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Arks Travel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vechol otan shanim she-hayu Yisrael bamidbar hayu shnei aronot halalu mehalchim zeh im zeh} \u8212- all those years that Israel traveled in the wilderness, these two arks traveled together: {\i echad shel met} \u8212- one containing a dead body, Yosef, {\i ve\u8217'echad shel Shechinah} \u8212- and the other the {\i aron} of God\u8217's presence containing the {\i Luchot HaBrit}. They were not side by side in the strict sense \u8212- the {\i Gemara} elsewhere derives from Yosef that a corpse may enter the {\i machaneh Leviyah} but not the {\i machaneh Shechinah} \u8212- but they traveled in close proximity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Passersby Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehayu ovrim u-shavim omrim: mah tivan shel shnei aronot halalu} \u8212- passersby would ask: what are these two {\i aronot}? They would be told: one contains a dead body and the other contains the divine presence. {\i Vechi mah darko shel met le-halech im Shechinah} \u8212- what right does a dead body have to travel alongside the divine presence? The image is genuinely startling \u8212- and, as one might observe, carries a visual resonance that would have seemed deeply disturbing from the outside. The {\i Gemara} does not shy away from this tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef Fulfilled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer given: {\i kiyem zeh kol mah she-katuv bazeh} \u8212- this one, Yosef, fulfilled everything written in that one, the {\i luchot}. The {\i Gemara} connects this to the practice, known from other contexts, of placing a {\i sefer Torah} in the {\i aron} of a great {\i talmid chacham} during his {\i levayah}, and saying {\i ki ein zeh mah she-katuv bazeh} \u8212- for this one fulfilled what is written in that one. Yosef is thus presented as the original paradigm of one whose life so completely embodied the Torah that his {\i aron} properly accompanies the {\i aron} of the {\i Shechinah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Israel\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then poses the same structural question it raised earlier regarding Yosef and Yaakov: {\i ve-illu asik beh Moshe, Yisrael lo havu ma\u8217'aski beh?} If Moshe had not taken the initiative, would Israel not have done it themselves? Indeed, the {\i pasuk} says {\i et atzmot Yosef asher he\u8217'elu Bnei Yisrael mi-Mitzrayim kavru be-Shechem} \u8212- and Bnei Yisrael buried the bones that {\i they} brought up. A further question: and if all of Israel would not have done it, would not Yosef\u8217's own children have seen to it? For another {\i pasuk} links the land of Shechem specifically to {\i bnei Yosef} as an inheritance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer follows the same logic as before: {\i amru hanichu lo}. Bnei Yosef said, let Bnei Yisrael do it, {\i kevodo bi-mrubeh yotze mi-mi\u8217'ut} \u8212- the honor is greater when the multitude carries it out than when only a few do. And Bnei Yisrael in turn said, let Moshe do it, {\i kevodo bigdolim yoter mibiketanim} \u8212- it is greater honor when a great one does it than when lesser ones do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Completion Gets Credit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then there is a direct contradiction in the {\i pesukim} \u8212- one verse says {\i vayikach Moshe et atzmot Yosef imo}, Moshe took the bones, and another says {\i asher he\u8217'elu Bnei Yisrael} \u8212- which Bnei Yisrael brought up. Rabbi Chama bar Rabbi Chanina resolves this with a general principle: {\i kol oseh davar velo gmaro, u-va acher u-gmaro, ma\u8217'aleh alav ha-katuv al she-gmaro ke-illu asao} \u8212- whoever begins something and does not complete it, and another comes and completes it, the credit goes entirely to the one who finishes. A {\i mitzvah} is called by the name of the one who completes it \u8212- {\i ein ha-mitzvah nikret ela al shem gomrah}. Since it was Bnei Yisrael who ultimately buried Yosef in Shechem, the {\i pasuk} attributes the entire act to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar adds a sobering corollary: {\i af moridin oto migdulato} \u8212- one who begins and fails to complete is not merely passed over but is brought down from his greatness. The {\i pasuk} records {\i vayehi ba\u8217'eit vayered Yehuda} \u8212- Yehuda went down \u8212- because he set in motion the effort to save Yosef but did not see it through. Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani extends this even further: such a person may even bury his wife and children, as indeed Yehuda did \u8212- {\i vatamat bat Shua eishet Yehuda}, and {\i vayehi vayamat Er ve-Onan}. The {\i Gemara} presents this as {\i mussar} \u8212- pointed moral instruction \u8212- rather than strict legal analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bones Self-Reference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav asks: why did Yosef refer to himself with the word {\i atzmotai} \u8212- my bones \u8212- during his own lifetime, saying {\i ve-ha\u8217'alitem et atzmotai mizeh itchem}? The answer: {\i mipnei she-lo micha bikhvod aviv} \u8212- because he failed to protest on behalf of his father\u8217's honor. When his brothers stood before him without yet knowing who he was, they referred to Yaakov as {\i avdecha avinu} \u8212- your servant our father \u8212- and Yosef said nothing to correct the disrespectful formulation. Because he did not defend his father\u8217's dignity, his own honor was diminished, and he spoke of himself as {\i bones} \u8212- a term of lowered status \u8212- even in life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Early Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda citing Rav \u8212- and some say Rav Chama bar Rabbi Chanina \u8212- asks further: {\i mipnei mah meit Yosef kodem le\u8217'echav} \u8212- why did Yosef die before his brothers? {\i Mipnei she-inhig atzmo be-rabbanus} \u8212- because he conducted himself with an air of authority. The word {\i hinhig atzmo} implies that he gave himself superior airs, throwing his considerable weight around, asserting his status and position. This \u8212- rather than simply holding authority, which was unavoidable given his position as viceroy \u8212- is the fault attributed to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brought Down Egypt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the verse {\i Yosef hu hurad Mitzraima} \u8212- Yosef was brought down to Egypt. Rabbi Elazar applies {\i al tikri}: {\i al tikri hurad ela horid} \u8212- do not read \u8220"was brought down\u8221" but \u8220"brought down,\u8221" for Yosef ultimately brought down {\i itztigninei Pharaoh migdulatan} \u8212- the astrologers of Pharaoh from their greatness. He alone could interpret the dreams that confounded them all, and in doing so he effectively displaced them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Potiphar & Castration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i vayiknehu Potifar saris Pharaoh} raises a question: what is a {\i saris} \u8212- a eunuch \u8212- doing with a wife? Rav explains: {\i sheknaho le\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- he bought Yosef for his own personal use, meaning his intentions toward Yosef were sexual in nature, and he was not yet a {\i saris} at the time of purchase. Thereupon {\i ba Gavriel ve-sirso} \u8212- the angel Gavriel came and castrated him. The {\i Gemara} also notes a variant reading {\i ufara\u8217'o}, which Rashi explains as referring to the {\i peri\u8217'ah} \u8212- a more complete form of mutilation, analogous to the second stage of {\i milah} in which the thin membrane beneath the foreskin is pulled back. The linguistic evidence is the shift in the text: the name first appears as {\i Potifar} and subsequently as {\i Potifera}, the added syllable reflecting the act of {\i peri\u8217'ah} performed upon him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe\u8217's Final Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enough for You\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to God\u8217's response to Moshe\u8217's repeated entreaties to be permitted to enter the land: {\i rav lach} \u8212- it is enough for you, or more precisely, {\i rav lach} \u8212- it is too much for you. Rav Levi observes a kind of measure-for-measure quality in the language: {\i be-rav bisar, be-rav bisru} \u8212- Moshe informed others with the word {\i rav} \u8212- {\i rav lachem bnei Levi} \u8212- and he himself was informed with the word {\i rav}: {\i rav lach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Lach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers multiple interpretations of {\i rav lach}. One: {\i rav yesh lach} \u8212- Moshe, there is already a {\i rav} here, a leader in the wings, and that is Yehoshua. Rashi adds that this also carries the implication that it is no longer Moshe\u8217's turn \u8212- Yehoshua\u8217's time has come. Another interpretation: {\i she-lo yomru ha-rav kama kasheh ve-hatalmid kama sarvan} \u8212- if Moshe continues petitioning, people will say: how intransigent is the master, and how pressuring is the student. Neither God\u8217's reputation nor Moshe\u8217's is served by the continued back-and-forth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 High Standards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why was God so exacting with Moshe to begin with? {\i Tana devei Rabbi Yishmael: lefum gamla shichna} \u8212- based on the camel is the load. The load placed on a camel corresponds to its capacity to bear it. Because Moshe was on such an elevated level, he was held to account for things that would not be reckoned against a lesser person. The great are measured by a higher standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 120 Years Old\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moshe declared to Israel: {\i ben me\u8217'ah ve\u8217'esrim shana anochi hayom} \u8212- I am 120 years old today. The {\i Gemara} asks: why does it say {\i hayom} \u8212- today \u8212- unnecessarily? {\i Hayom malu yamai u-shnotai} \u8212- to tell you that today, this very day, my years are complete. This is also the proof that {\i tzaddikim} die on the anniversary of their birth: {\i God} fulfills {\i mispar yamecha amalei} \u8212- the number of your days I will fill \u8212- precisely, from day to day and from month to month.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gates of Wisdom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moshe then says {\i lo uchal od latzet velavo} \u8212- I can no longer go out and come in. One might assume this refers to physical movement, but the {\i Gemara} objects: {\i u-Moshe ben me\u8217'ah ve\u8217'esrim shana bemoto lo chahat eino velo nas lecho} \u8212- at his death his eyes had not dimmed and his strength had not left him. Indeed, a {\i Braitah} teaches that when God told Moshe to ascend Har Nevo, {\i shtem esrei ma\u8217'alot hayu sham, u-psa\u8217'an Moshe be-psi\u8217'ah achat} \u8212- there were twelve elevations from the plains of Moav to the mountain, and Moshe traversed them all in a single leap. He clearly retained his full physical vigor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leap to Nebo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yonatan therefore explains: {\i latzet velavo be-divrei Torah} \u8212- I can no longer go out and come in through words of Torah. {\i Melamed she-nistatmu mimenu sha\u8217'arei chochmah} \u8212- the gates of wisdom had been closed to him. He had lost his capacity for the kind of vigorous intellectual engagement with Torah \u8212- the going in and out, the argumentation and analysis \u8212- that had defined his entire life. There is something at once poignant and, perhaps, merciful in this: Moshe may have found it more possible to accept his death once he recognized that the faculty most precious to him was already departing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 God Buries Moshe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shared Sabbath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes the verse {\i vayeilech Moshe ve-Yehoshua ve-yityatzevu be-Ohel Moed} \u8212- Moshe and Yehoshua went and stood in the {\i Ohel Moed}. A {\i Tanna} teaches: {\i otah Shabbat shel dyo-zugi hayta} \u8212- that {\i Shabbat}, that particular period, was one of shared pair-leadership. {\i Nitlah me-zeh ve-nitnah le-zeh} \u8212- authority was being taken from Moshe and transferred to Yehoshua. The use of the word {\i Shabbat} here deliberately echoes the rabbinic institution of {\i Shabbat shel mi hayta} \u8212- whose week was it? \u8212- referring to the practice of rotating authority between two leaders, a phenomenon the rabbis were familiar with in their own institutional life. The {\i Gemara} uses that resonance to say that here, uniquely, the two were briefly able to share the moment of transition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death & Burial Sites\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Yehuda: ilmalei mikra katuv iy efshar le-omro} \u8212- if it had not been written in the verse, one could not say such a thing. The verse {\i vaya\u8217'al Moshe me-arvot Moav el Har Nevo} places Moshe\u8217's death at Mount Nevo, which lies in the portion of Reuven, as Bnei Reuven are recorded as having built {\i et Nevo}. Yet Moshe was buried in Gad\u8217's portion, as the verse {\i vayar reshit lo ki sham chelkat mechokek safun} \u8212- \u8220"for there is the hidden portion of the lawgiver\u8221" \u8212- is understood to refer to Moshe\u8217's burial place in Gad\u8217's territory. Between the site of his death in Reuven and his burial in Gad, the distance was four {\i mil} \u8212- approximately four kilometers. A considerable distance to traverse, and the question is: who transported him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Transport\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Melamed she-hayah Moshe mutal be-chanfei Shechinah} \u8212- Moshe lay cradled in the wings of the divine presence, which carried him the four {\i mil} to his burial place. This image closes the circle opened by our {\i Mishnah}: Moshe carried Yosef\u8217's {\i aron}, and God carried Moshe\u8217's body. The parallel is exact and intentional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Angels\u8217' Eulogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The heavenly angels accompanied Moshe and declared: {\i tzidkat Hashem asah u-mishpatav im Yisrael} \u8212- he performed the righteousness of God and God\u8217's laws with Israel. This verse from the end of {\i Devarim} serves as the angelic eulogy at Moshe\u8217's burial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Lament\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God\u8217's own expression of mourning: {\i mi yakum li im mere\u8217'im, mi yityatzeiv li im po\u8217'alei aven} \u8212- who will rise with me against the wicked? Who will stand firm with me against those who do iniquity? The death of Moshe is experienced, as it were, as a loss of an irreplaceable ally in the ongoing work of justice in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' Responses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then records several Amoraic responses, each apparently citing a verse that captures what was lost. Shmuel cites {\i mi ke-he-chacham u-mi yodea pesher davar} \u8212- who is the wise one who knows the explanation of things? Rabbi Yochanan cites {\i ve-hachokhmah me-ayin timatzei} \u8212- and from where now shall wisdom be found? Rav Nachman simply states {\i vayamot sham Moshe} \u8212- and there Moshe died. A further voice, {\i Samayon}, declares: {\i vayamot sham Moshe safra rabba de-Yisrael} \u8212- the great scribe of Israel has died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether these Amoraim are understood as voicing what the angels said, what God said, or as themselves participating virtually in the {\i levayah} of Moshe \u8212- the {\i vav} connecting them ({\i ve-Shmuel amar}, {\i ve-Rav Nachman amar}) gives the passage something of the quality of a gathering of mourners, each adding their eulogy in turn, until the last voice simply names the fact: the great scribe of Israel is gone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 14\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe\u8217's Death & Burial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Transition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 14 brings us to the close of the first {\i perek} and the opening of the second, situated within a sustained aggadic passage surrounding the death of Moshe. The thematic thread has been the chain of burial: Yosef buried his father Yaakov, Moshe buried Yosef, and God Himself buried Moshe. The preceding material contained a vivid description of Moshe\u8217's {\i levayah}, carried on divine wings and accompanied by the angels. We now pick up at the bottom of 13b.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Meis Moshe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Four lines from the bottom of 13b, the Gemara introduces a striking teaching: {\i V\u8217'yesh omrim lo meis Moshe} \u8212- and some say Moshe did not die. The famous parallel is of course {\i Yaakov Avinu lo meis}, but the case of Moshe is harder. By Yaakov, the verse omits any explicit statement of death \u8212- it says {\i vayei\u8217'asef el amav} but never says {\i vayamas}. By Moshe, however, the Torah states explicitly {\i vayamas sham}. How, then, can the Gemara maintain that he did not die?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is a {\i gezeirah shavah}: {\i kesiv hacha vayamas sham} \u8212- it says he died there \u8212- {\i ukesiv hasam vayehi sham im Hashem} \u8212- and it says there he was with God. Just as when Moshe was {\i sham} with God at Sinai he was in attendance before God, so too here he is in attendance before God. The implicit reasoning is worth dwelling on: Moshe spent forty days and forty nights at Sinai without eating or drinking, existing in some manner beyond normal physicality. Perhaps during that time something fundamental shifted in the nature of his body, and perhaps what occurred at his death was similarly an apparent departure \u8212- his body lies there, but his spirit remains in attendance before God, connected as it was during those forty days. It is not that all souls are with God after death, but rather that Moshe had not completely departed; his connection to God mirrors what occurred at Sinai. In this reading, the death is real in some external sense but not in the ultimate spiritual sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching arises naturally in the wake of Moshe\u8217's {\i levayah} and {\i hesped}. There is a profound difficulty in accepting the finality of Moshe\u8217's death \u8212- he is the lawgiver, the supreme {\i navi}, the figure through whom all Torah flows. The Torah\u8217's own language wraps his end in mystery: {\i velo yada ish et kevurato}. Once the Torah signals that mystery, once God is the sole witness to the burial, it becomes possible \u8212- even understandable \u8212- that a tradition would develop holding that he never truly died at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidden Grave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah provides three identifying markers for Moshe\u8217's burial place: in the valley, in the land of Moav, opposite Beis Peor. The Gemara notes that Rabbi Brachya observed that there is a {\i siman b\u8217'soch siman} \u8212- an identifier nested within an identifier. And yet, {\i va\u8217'afilu hachi velo yada ish et kevuraso}: even with all these markers, no one has ever been able to find it. The text proceeds to explain why this is not accidental.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The evil kingdom \u8212- Rome \u8212- sent to the leader of Beis Peor demanding: {\i hareinu heichan Moshe kavor} \u8212- show us where Moshe is buried. When the searchers stood at a high elevation, the site appeared to be below them. When they descended, it appeared to be above. They divided into two groups to cover both possibilities, but those above still saw it below, and those below still saw it above. A miracle prevented discovery regardless of approach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The meaning of this is clear. If Moshe\u8217's burial place were known, it would become a site of pilgrimage and veneration, and Moshe would be at risk of being deified. The entire point of the transition into the Land of Canaan was to happen without Moshe \u8212- indeed, the Torah closes with the words {\i lo kam navi od beYisrael}, and specifically because he was the greatest, the people had to be capable of living their lives without him. Thus: {\i lekayem ma shene\u8217'emar velo yada ish et kevuraso}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chama b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina adds a further dimension: even Moshe himself did not know where he was buried. The textual basis is a {\i gezeirah shavah} on the word {\i ish}: {\i velo yada ish et kevuraso} \u8212- no {\i ish} knew his burial \u8212- and {\i vezot haberachah asher berach Moshe ish ha\u8217'Elokim} \u8212- Moshe is called {\i ish}. Thus the verse itself tells us that even Moshe the {\i ish} did not know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chama b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina then asks why Moshe was buried specifically near Beis Peor. The answer: {\i kedai lechaper al ma\u8217'aseh Peor} \u8212- to atone for the sin of Peor, the sin of Bnos Moav, which occurred in that very location.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Imitatio Dei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Walking After God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same Rabbi Chama b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina continues with a teaching that, while not directly linked to what precedes it, is introduced at this point because it is his. He asks: what does it mean when the Torah says {\i acharei Hashem Elokeichem telechu} \u8212- you shall walk after Hashem your God? Is it literally possible for a person to walk after the {\i Shechinah}? The Torah itself declares {\i ki Hashem Elokecha esh ochlah hu} \u8212- God is a consuming fire. How can one aspire to closeness with or imitation of such a God?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Acts Enumerated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i leilech achar midotav} \u8212- to follow after God\u8217's {\i attributes}, the concrete ways in which God acts in the world. The Gemara enumerates these:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God clothed the naked, as it is written that He made for Adam and his wife garments of leather and dressed them \u8212- so too you should clothe the naked. God visited the sick, as it is written that God appeared to Avraham at the plains of Mamre after his {\i brit milah} \u8212- so too you should visit the sick. God consoled mourners, as it is written that after Avraham\u8217's death God blessed Yitzchak his son \u8212- and that blessing is understood as a visit of consolation \u8212- so too you should console mourners. God buried the dead, as it is written {\i vayikbor oto bagai} \u8212- and this ties directly back to the preceding material \u8212- so too you should bury the dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Character vs.\u160 ?Actions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i drasha} is worth holding up against another classical formulation of the same obligation: {\i mah hu rachum af ata rachum, mah hu chanun af ata chanun} \u8212- as God is merciful so you should be merciful, as God is gracious so you should be gracious. The two formulations represent fundamentally different approaches to ethics. One focuses on character traits \u8212- {\i rachum}, {\i chanun} \u8212- asking the person to cultivate an inner disposition that mirrors God\u8217's. The other, as in the Gemara before us, focuses entirely on concrete actions: it does not matter whether you are naturally inclined toward compassion; what matters is what you actually do in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This maps onto a broader debate in ethical thought. One tradition, exemplified by the Rambam in his {\i Peirush Hamishnayos} on {\i Pirkei Avot}, holds that the perfection of character is the ultimate goal, and that actions serve primarily as a means to that end. This is the consistent thread of the {\i Shemonah Perakim}: every ethical deed is described as a means toward perfecting one\u8217's character rather than as an end good in itself. A famous illustration is the Rambam\u8217's reading of the dictum {\i hakol lefi rov hama\u8217'aseh velo lefi godel hama\u8217'aseh} \u8212- it is all according to the quantity of the deed, not the size. Rambam explains that it is better to give one dollar of {\i tzedakah} a hundred times than a hundred dollars at once, because the repeated act cultivates the character of generosity more effectively. The goal is the internal transformation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, the formulation in our Gemara emphasizes what you actually do. In a Kantian register, one might even argue that doing a good deed against one\u8217's inclination, out of sheer sense of obligation, reflects a purer ethical commitment \u8212- what matters is the good accomplished, not the personality of the one accomplishing it. The two approaches are genuinely in tension: is {\i imitatio Dei} a program for self-cultivation, or a program for making the world better through concrete action?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note an additional dimension here: the concretization itself is significant. The general commandment of {\i ve\u8217'ahavta lere\u8217'acha kamocha} encompasses all of these obligations in principle, but left at that level of abstraction, no one would reliably fulfill them. Because {\i Chazal} speak with great concreteness about {\i nichum aveilim}, {\i bikkur cholim}, {\i hachnasat orchim}, and {\i halvayat hames}, these obligations become identifiable, actionable, and deeply embedded in communal practice. There is a real power in the concrete over the abstract, and in that sense the emphasis on actions rather than character traits also has a practical pedagogical valence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to the surrounding material is suggestive as well. The passage follows the account of Moshe\u8217's burial \u8212- an event at which the impulse to worship and deify Moshe had to be actively prevented. The response is not to explain Moshe philosophically but to say: figure out how to act like him. You cannot be God, you cannot be Moshe, but you can do what God and Moshe did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Bookended\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Simlai continues the theme. His {\i drasha}: {\i Torah techilasah gemilus chasadim vesofah gemilus chasadim} \u8212- the Torah begins with acts of loving-kindness and ends with acts of loving-kindness. At the beginning: {\i vaya\u8217'as Hashem Elokim le\u8217'adam ule\u8217'ishto katnos or vayalbishem} \u8212- God clothed Adam and his wife. At the end: {\i vayikbor oto bagai} \u8212- God buried Moshe. Rabbi Simlai is not listing these as two examples among many; he is pointing to the structural architecture of the Torah itself. Whatever else the Torah contains \u8212- all its commandments, all its narratives \u8212- it opens and closes with {\i gemilus chasadim}. This should tell us something essential about what the Torah is fundamentally about.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshe & Eretz Yisrael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvot in Land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Simlai continues, now turning to a different question: {\i mipnei mah nisaveh Moshe Rabbeinu lehikanes le\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- why did Moshe so desperately desire to enter the Land of Israel? Was it to eat its fruit? To enjoy its goodness? The Gemara answers in Moshe\u8217's own words: {\i harbeh mitzvos nitzvu ve\u8217'ein mitkaymim ella be\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- many {\i mitzvot} were commanded and can only be fulfilled in the Land of Israel. {\i Ekanes ani la\u8217'aretz kedei sheyitkaymu kulan alai} \u8212- let me enter so that all of them may be fulfilled through me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God\u8217's response is striking: {\i kelum atah mevakesh ella lekabel sachar?} \u8212- is it nothing but reward that you seek? And then, immediately: {\i ma\u8217'aleh ani alecha ke\u8217'ilu asitam} \u8212- I will credit you as if you had done them. The Gemara then expounds a passage from Isaiah 53 to support this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isaiah 53 Exegesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i lachen achalek lo varabbim ve\u8217'et atzumim yachalek shalal} \u8212- therefore I will divide his portion among the many and with the mighty he will divide the spoil \u8212- is parsed in detail. {\i Lachen achalek lo varabbim}: one might think this means Moshe\u8217's reward will be like that of later great figures, but not like the earliest great ones. {\i Talmud lomar ve\u8217'et atzumim yachalek shalal}: no, with the mighty \u8212- meaning Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov, who were mighty in Torah and {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tachat asher he\u8217'erah lamavet nafsho}: because he exposed himself to death \u8212- {\i she\u8217'masar atzmo lemitah} \u8212- referencing his declaration {\i ve\u8217'im ayin mecheni na misifrecha}, his readiness to be destroyed along with Israel rather than see them destroyed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'et poshim nimnah}: he was counted among sinners \u8212- {\i she\u8217'nimnah im metei midbar} \u8212- he was reckoned among those who died in the wilderness, like a captain who does not abandon his ship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehu chet rabbim nasa}: he carried the sin of the many \u8212- {\i she\u8217'kiper al ma\u8217'aseh ha\u8217'egel} \u8212- he atoned for the sin of the golden calf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Velafoshim yapgia}: for sinners he would entreat \u8212- {\i she\u8217'bikesh rachamim al poshei Yisrael she\u8217'yachzeru biteshuvah}, he prayed for God\u8217's mercy that sinners should repent. The word {\i pegiah} means entreaty, as in the verse {\i ve\u8217'al tifga bi} \u8212- do not entreat me \u8212- directed at Moshe when God wished him to stop praying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reward & Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exchange raises an immediate question. The Mishnah in {\i Pirkei Avot} states {\i al tihyu ka\u8217'avadim hameshameshim et harav al menat lekabel peras} \u8212- do not be like servants who serve the master in order to receive a reward. How, then, does God\u8217's response \u8212- {\i kelum atah mevakesh ella lekabel sachar?} \u8212- make sense as a critique of Moshe? Who said Moshe was only in it for the {\i sachar}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A famous story about the Vilna Gaon is relevant here. The Gaon once saw someone on {\i Sukkot} with an exceptionally beautiful {\i etrog} and asked to use it. The owner agreed on the condition that he receive all of the Gaon\u8217's {\i sachar} for the {\i mitzvah}. The Gaon agreed, took the {\i etrog}, and performed the {\i mitzvah} \u8212- and his students had never seen him so joyful. When they asked why, he said: every other time he could not be certain that some part of his motivation was the {\i sachar}; this time he knew with certainty he was doing it purely {\i leshem hamitzvah}, because there was no {\i sachar} to be had.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper question is what this passage suggests about the value of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The Gemara reduces Moshe\u8217's desire to enter the Land entirely to the issue of fulfilling {\i mitzvot hateluyot ba\u8217'aretz} \u8212- the commandments dependent on the land. But one might have expected something grander: the actualization of Israel as a national entity under God, defined by Torah, building a civilization with a {\i beit hamikdash}, realizing the whole vision of what it means to be the people of God in their land. Is the entire point really just to accumulate more {\i mitzvot}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects to Rabbi Simlai\u8217's teaching at the end of Masechet Makkos \u8212- the same Rabbi Simlai who authored the teaching here. There it says {\i darash Rav Simlai: sheish me\u8217'os ushlosh esrei mitzvos ne\u8217'emru lo leMoshe mi\u8217'Sinai}, and the preceding {\i mishnah} states {\i ratza HaKadosh Baruch Hu lezakot et Yisrael lefichach hirbah lahem Torah umitzvot}. The abundance of {\i mitzvot} is itself the mechanism of {\i zekhut}; the more you do, the greater the {\i sachar}. This framework \u8212- the quantity of {\i mitzvot} as a measure of spiritual standing \u8212- pervades both contexts, and it is possible that Rabbi Simlai is deploying it consistently as part of a larger theological program emphasizing the {\i ribbu\u8217'i mitzvot} as the core of Jewish identity and divine favor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some scholars have suggested that the statement {\i hirbah lahem Torah umitzvot lezakot et Yisrael} is a polemical response to Christian theology, which characterized the law as reflecting bondage rather than liberation \u8212- {\i the covenant from Mount Sinai bears children who are slaves}. The counter-claim is precisely that the abundance of {\i mitzvot} is not bondage but the vehicle of merit and spiritual elevation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also read God\u8217's response to Moshe \u8212- that he will receive credit as though he had performed the {\i mitzvot} without having entered the land \u8212- as a post-{\i churban} consolation of sorts. Jewish identity and the {\i kiyum mitzvot} do not depend on actually being in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. Moshe himself, who could not enter, receives the full credit. This reading understands the passage as addressing the exilic condition: one need not be in the Land to realize one\u8217's full spiritual potential through {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sotah\u8217's Minchah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egyptian Basket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the second {\i perek}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i haya mevi et minchatah besoch kefifah mitzrit} \u8212- the husband would bring her {\i minchah} inside an Egyptian basket. Note that it is the husband who is the actor here; looking at the {\i pesukim}, it is the husband who brings the woman to the {\i beit hamikdash} and brings her {\i minchah}. {\i Venosan al yadeha} \u8212- and he places it on her hands \u8212- {\i kedei leyaga\u8217'ah} \u8212- in order to weary her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then notes the contrast with all other {\i menachot}: {\i kol menachot techilatan vesofan bichli sharet} \u8212- all {\i menachot} begin and end with a {\i kli sharet}, a sanctified vessel. But this {\i minchah} begins not in a {\i kli sharet} but in this lowly, mundane vessel \u8212- {\i vezo techilasah bekefifah mitzrit} \u8212- and only at the end, {\i vesofah bichli sharet}, when it must actually be offered, is it placed in a {\i kli sharet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, all {\i menachot} require oil and frankincense, while this {\i minchah} requires neither. (The Gemara will note that the {\i minchat chotei}, the sinner\u8217's meal-offering, also lacks oil and frankincense, and will address this.) All {\i menachot} come from wheat; this one comes from barley. There is one other {\i minchah} that uses barley \u8212- the {\i minchat ha\u8217'omer} \u8212- but even that barley is ground into {\i geresh}, a very fine and refined barley flour comparable in quality to the {\i soles} of wheat offerings. The {\i sotah\u8217's} offering, by contrast, comes as a coarse bran \u8212- {\i kemach} \u8212- not refined at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel supplies the interpretive key: {\i kesheim shema\u8217'aseha ma\u8217'aseh behemah kach korbanah ma\u8217'achal behemah} \u8212- just as her actions were the actions of an animal, so her sacrifice is the food of an animal. This is a striking formulation. The {\i mishnah} throughout has been conveying degradation through ritual means \u8212- the lowly vessel, the coarse grain, the absence of oil and frankincense \u8212- but it is Rabban Gamliel who connects the dots explicitly: the deliberate lowliness of her offering mirrors a moral judgment about her conduct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also reinforces the posture of presumed guilt that pervades the {\i sotah} procedure. The repeated emphasis on {\i kedei leyaga\u8217'ah} \u8212- wearying her \u8212- operates on the assumption that she sinned and should be induced to confess. And the {\i middah keneged middah} logic that ran through the earlier material in the {\i masechet} continues here: each degrading element of the procedure corresponds to her presumed transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reordering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed question on the {\i mishnah\u8217's} organization. {\i Ein seder hamishnah keseder haparshah mechuvonim} \u8212- the order in the {\i mishnah} is not aligned with the order of the {\i parshah} in the Torah. Tosafot then reconstructs the Torah\u8217's sequence:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First comes {\i kinuy} and {\i setirah}. Then the husband brings her and brings her {\i minchah}. Then the {\i kohen} prepares the water \u8212- the earthenware vessel, the half-{\i log} of water from the {\i kiyor}, the dust from the floor. Then the woman is brought to the Sha\u8217'ar Nikanor and her garments are disheveled. Then the {\i minchah} is placed on her hands while the {\i kohen} holds the bitter waters. Then the oath is administered. Then the {\i megillah} is written, the name erased into the water, and she drinks. Then the {\i minchah} is transferred from the Egyptian basket to a {\i kli sharet}, the {\i kometz} is taken, and it is offered on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in our {\i mishnah}, the disheveling of the woman\u8217's garments and the rending of her clothing \u8212- discussed in the previous {\i perek} \u8212- came before the {\i minchah} is introduced. So the {\i mishnah} has reordered the material. Tosafot raises this without fully resolving it: either there is some literary or pedagogical reason for the {\i tanna}\u8217's reorganization, or we should assume that the {\i halakhic} sequence in practice follows the Torah\u8217's order, and the {\i mishnah} has organized things differently for its own expository purposes. What seems untenable is that the {\i tanna} would silently invert the Torah\u8217's sequence and expect us to understand that the Torah\u8217's own order should be disregarded \u8212- without explicitly signaling that, as the Gemara does in other contexts, such as the {\i Kohen Gadol\u8217's avodah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wearying Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i tanya Abba Chanin omer mishum Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- why does the procedure use the Egyptian basket and place the {\i minchah} on her hands? {\i Kedei leyaga\u8217'ah} \u8212- to weary her \u8212- {\i kedei she\u8217'tachzor bah} \u8212- so that she should repent and confess her sin. And from this Abba Chanin draws a {\i kal va\u8217'chomer}: if God shows such concern for those who transgress His will \u8212- wanting her to confess and be spared rather than suffer the punishment of the {\i mei hamarim} \u8212- how much more so does God care for those who do His will.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water & Minchah Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this interpretation. {\i Umimai mishum de\u8217'chasah hu?} \u8212- how do you know the reason for wearying her is concern for her welfare? {\i Dilma ki heichi de\u8217'lo timachak megillah?} \u8212- perhaps the intent is to prevent the unnecessary erasure of God\u8217's name? This is a very different concern. Other {\i mishnayot} make clear that there is a strong prohibition against erasing God\u8217's name unnecessarily; perhaps the entire effort to induce confession is motivated by that concern, not by concern for the woman herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: the {\i tanna} who attributes the motivation to concern for the woman must hold the position that {\i mashke ve\u8217'achar kach makriv et minchatah} \u8212- she drinks the water first, and only afterward is the {\i minchah} offered. If that is so, then by the time we are still urging her to confess and spare herself, God\u8217's name has already been erased \u8212- the {\i megillah} has already been scraped into the water she has drunk. There is no longer any protective purpose regarding God\u8217's name. The only remaining reason to continue urging her to confess would be concern for her life and well-being.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the mechanics: she drinks, and then the {\i minchah} is brought \u8212- and critically, the physical effects of the water are suspended until the {\i minchah} is offered. The {\i minchah}, in this view, is the final trigger. She has drunk the water, but nothing will happen until the {\i kometz} is placed on the fire. So even after drinking, she can confess, the {\i minchah} will not be offered, and she will be spared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sounds counterintuitive \u8212- we naturally imagine the drinking as the climactic act \u8212- but as Tosafot\u8217's careful reconstruction of the {\i pesukim} confirms, this is precisely what the Torah\u8217's own sequence suggests: {\i ve\u8217'hishkah\u8230 ? venatan al yadeha} \u8212- she drinks, and then the {\i minchah} is waved and offered. The Gemara is drawing out an opinion that takes that sequence seriously, however strange it appears to us.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Menachot Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standard Procedure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} stated that all other {\i menachot} begin in a {\i kli sharet} while the {\i sotah\u8217's} begins in an Egyptian basket. The Gemara challenges this from a {\i mishnah} in Menachot describing the standard procedure for bringing a {\i minchah}: a person brings it from home {\i bekaltot shel kesef veshel zahav} \u8212- in vessels of gold and silver \u8212- and only upon arriving at the Temple does he transfer it into a {\i kli sharet}. This suggests that even ordinary {\i menachot} do not begin in a {\i kli sharet} but rather in mundane home vessels. So what is distinctive about the {\i sotah\u8217's} basket?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kli Sharet Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa resolves the apparent contradiction: the {\i mishnah\u8217's} claim should be read as saying that all other {\i menachot} begin in vessels that are {\i fit} to serve as a {\i kli sharet} \u8212- meaning gold and silver vessels \u8212- even if they are not formally sanctified as such. The {\i kefifah mitzrit}, the Egyptian wicker basket, is not merely unsanctified but is categorically unfit to serve as a {\i kli sharet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This implies that a wicker vessel is disqualified from being a {\i kli sharet} altogether. The Gemara asks: like whom is this position? It aligns with Rebbi (Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi) against Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, as a {\i baraita} teaches: if {\i klei sharet} were made of wood, Rebbi disqualifies them while Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda validates them. Since wicker is treated as a form of wood, the {\i mishnah\u8217's} assumption that the {\i kefifah mitzrit} is disqualified from {\i kli sharet} status follows Rebbi\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then attempts to reconcile the {\i mishnah} even with the position of Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda: even he would only validate wooden {\i klei sharet} that are {\i chashuvim} \u8212- finely crafted and respectable. He would not validate lowly, crude wicker vessels. And why not? Because of the principle {\i hakrivehu na le\u8217'pechatecha} \u8212- would you offer this to a governor? \u8212- meaning that what is not suitable to bring before a human authority is certainly unfit as a vessel for divine service. This principle is not merely an ethical consideration; it has operative {\i halakhic} force, actually disqualifying an otherwise potentially valid vessel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Respect Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle {\i hakrivehu na le\u8217'pechatecha} operates as a genuine standard. What is fit to be presented to a person of authority is fit for the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}; what would be embarrassing to offer a governor is certainly unfit for God\u8217's altar. This raises a question of application: who determines whether a given vessel meets this standard? If a gold vessel were mangled or degraded, would it be disqualified despite being made of precious metal? The principle seems to address both the material and the manner of craftsmanship together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a notable tension here with the principle of {\i ein aniyut bemakom ashirut} \u8212- there is no poverty in a place of wealth \u8212- which governs the {\i beit hamikdash} and precludes the use of cheap or inadequate materials in divine service. The {\i sotah\u8217's} basket is thus doubly notable: not only is it not a {\i kli sharet}, it is the kind of vessel that would be disqualified from ever becoming one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanctification Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} describing the standard {\i minchah} procedure states that once placed in a {\i kli sharet}, you sanctify it \u8212- {\i umekadsho bichli sharet}. The Gemara notes that this language implies an act of sanctification distinct from the mere placing into the vessel. Does the {\i kli sharet} automatically sanctify whatever is placed in it, or must there be a specific intention to sanctify?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara suggests that the language {\i notna bichli sharet lekadsho bichli sharet} \u8212- placing it in the {\i kli sharet} in order to sanctify it \u8212- could reflect intention, but equally could simply mean that the result of placing is sanctification. The question remains open as to whether intentional sanctification is a prerequisite. It is noted that the owner\u8217's earlier verbal declaration \u8212- {\i harei zu minchas} \u8212- constitutes {\i kedushat peh}, verbal sanctity, which does not yet confer the full {\i kedushat ha-guf}, intrinsic sanctity, that determines whether the object can be redeemed, whether removal from the Temple precincts is consequential, and so forth. That full {\i kedushat ha-guf} comes only through placement in the {\i kli sharet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Altar Placement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The standard {\i minchah} procedure includes the act of {\i hagashah}: {\i magishah bekeren dromit ma\u8217'aravit keneged chudah shel keren vedayo} \u8212- you touch it at the southwest corner of the altar, at the very tip of the corner, and that suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand why the southwest corner, the Gemara draws on two verses. One verse says {\i lifnei Hashem} \u8212- before God \u8212- which directs us toward the west side of the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, the side facing the {\i Heichal}. The other verse says {\i el pnei hamizbeach} \u8212- at the face of the altar \u8212- which directs us toward the south, where the ramp ascends and the altar faces when you approach it. Neither verse alone is sufficient:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} articulates the tension precisely: {\i lifnei Hashem yachol bema\u8217'arav} \u8212- perhaps in the west? {\i Talmud lomar el pnei hamizbeach} \u8212- the face of the altar, meaning the south. {\i I el pnei hamizbeach yachol badarom} \u8212- perhaps in the south? {\i Talmud lomar lifnei Hashem} \u8212- before God, meaning the west. {\i Ha keitzad?} \u8212- how then? {\i Magishah bekeren dromit ma\u8217'aravit} \u8212- you place it at the southwest corner, satisfying both directions simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To visualize this: the {\i Heichal} stood on the west end of the {\i Azarah}, opening eastward. The {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} stood in the courtyard east of the {\i Heichal}. The {\i ramp} of the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} ascended from the south, making the south face the {\i pnei hamizbeach}. The {\i Heichal} was to the west, making the west side of the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} the side facing {\i lifnei Hashem}. The southwest corner is the precise geometric point that satisfies both demands simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hermeneutic Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer offers a different approach, though one that reaches the same conclusion through a broader hermeneutic principle. Rather than looking for a compromise position that satisfies both verses simultaneously, he articulates a general rule: {\i kol makom she\u8217'atah motzeh shenei mikra\u8217'ot} \u8212- wherever you find two verses \u8212- {\i echad mekayem atzmo umekayem divrei chaveiro ve\u8217'echad mekayem atzmo umevatel divrei chaveiro} \u8212- one of which can satisfy itself and also satisfy the other, while the other can only satisfy itself at the expense of the first \u8212- {\i menichim et zeh shemekayem atzmo umevatel et chaveiro vetofsim et shemekayem atzmo umekayem chaveiro} \u8212- you set aside the exclusive one and follow the inclusive one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In this case: if you follow {\i lifnei Hashem} in the west literally, you are on the west side of the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} and have no element at all of {\i pnei hamizbeach} in the south \u8212- the two are mutually exclusive. But if you follow {\i el pnei hamizbeach} \u8212- the south face \u8212- and specifically at the southern end, the question is whether that position can still be described as {\i lifnei Hashem}, before God. According to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view, it can, because even from the south face of the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} one stands facing the {\i Heichal}. Thus the south position satisfies itself and also preserves something of {\i lifnei Hashem}, while the west position satisfies only itself and sacrifices the south entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vedayo & Parallels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i vedayo} \u8212- and that is sufficient \u8212- requires explanation. One might think that beyond touching the vessel at the corner, the {\i minchah} itself must physically touch the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}. {\i Vedayo} tells us: no, touching the vessel is sufficient; the {\i minchah} within the vessel need not itself contact the altar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi draws a parallel: just as when the {\i kohen} sanctifies the blood of a {\i korban}, the sanctification comes through the vessel that catches the blood \u8212- the {\i kli sharet} of the {\i shechitah} being the knife itself \u8212- and not through the blood physically touching the altar, so too by the {\i minchah}, contact of the vessel with the altar suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Altar Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers yet another resolution to the two verses, independent of the southwest-corner solution. He argues that the {\i tanna} who generated this {\i drasha} held that {\i kulo mizbe\u8217'ach batzafon kai} \u8212- the entire altar was situated in the northern portion of the {\i Azarah}. If the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} was shifted entirely northward, then its southern face would align precisely with the midpoint of the {\i Azarah}, directly opposite the opening of the {\i Heichal}. In that configuration, standing at the south face of the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} places you squarely {\i lifnei Hashem} \u8212- directly before the {\i Heichal} \u8212- while also satisfying {\i el pnei hamizbeach} by being at the face of the altar. Both verses are satisfied without recourse to the corner at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kometz Preparation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Frankincense Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on the standard {\i minchah} procedure states {\i umesalek et halevonah letzad echad} \u8212- the {\i kohen} moves the frankincense to one side before taking the {\i kometz}. The reason is straightforward: the {\i kometz} must consist solely of the flour mixed with oil. {\i Ki heichi dela tikmotz behadi mincha} \u8212- so that when he scoops the handful, he does not inadvertently include any frankincense. As the Gemara elsewhere teaches, if when the {\i kohen} took the {\i kometz} he found in his hand a pebble, a grain of salt, or a single granule of {\i levonah}, the {\i kometz} is {\i pasul} \u8212- because his handful must be entirely of the {\i minchah} itself, nothing else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i vekometz mimakom shenitrabah shamanah} \u8212- the {\i kometz} is taken from the portion of the {\i minchah} where the oil is most thoroughly mixed in. The Scriptural basis is {\i misaltah umishmanah} \u8212- from its flour and from its oil \u8212- which the Gemara reads as mandating that the {\i kometz} come from a portion rich in oil.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the {\i kohen} has taken the {\i kometz}, {\i venotno letoch kli sharet umekadsho bichli sharet} \u8212- he places the {\i kometz} into a {\i kli sharet} and sanctifies it therein. The Gemara asks: the {\i minchah} was already sanctified in the {\i kli sharet} when it was first transferred from the home vessels \u8212- why does the {\i kometz} require yet another act of sanctification in a second {\i kli sharet}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers by drawing a parallel to the blood of a {\i korban}: {\i dam af al gav dekadisheih sakin betzavar behemah} \u8212- the blood is already sanctified by the knife, which is a {\i kli sharet} and which sanctifies the blood at the moment of {\i shechitah} \u8212- {\i hadar mikadshei leih bichli sharet} \u8212- and yet the blood is caught again in a {\i kli sharet}, which constitutes a second act of sanctification. The first sanctification establishes the sacred status of the blood; the second elevates it to the level required for application to the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}. So too by the {\i minchah}: the first placement in the {\i kli sharet} sanctifies the {\i minchah} as a whole; placing the {\i kometz} in a second {\i kli sharet} brings it to the level of sanctity required for offering on the altar. {\i Hacha nami lo shana} \u8212- here too, it is no different.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 15\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mincha Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Frankincense Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the bottom of 14b, where the Gemara resumes its account of the process of bringing the {\i mincha} of the {\i sotah}. Before returning specifically to that ritual, the Gemara completes its citation of a {\i braita} that describes the standard procedure for bringing any {\i mincha} \u8212- useful background for the discussion that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} states: {\i umalket et levonata venotnah al gabav} \u8212- the {\i kohen} gathers the {\i levona} (frankincense) from the original vessel in which the {\i mincha} was presented and transfers it to the separate {\i kli sharet} (sacred vessel) into which the {\i kometz} (handful) has already been placed. The verse cited is {\i ve-et kol ha-levona asher al ha-mincha} \u8212- \u8220"and all the frankincense that is upon the {\i mincha}\u8221" \u8212- which he then offers upon the {\i mizbeach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Handful Offering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The top of 15a states: {\i umaale umaktir bichli sharet} \u8212- he brings it up and offers it in the {\i kli sharet}. This formulation raises a question: is he actually burning up the {\i kli sharet} itself? Rashi notes a different {\i girsa} of the text: {\i maktirah} cannot be read as \u8220"he has already burnt it,\u8221" since the next line states that he first salts it. Rather, the reading should be {\i maale bichli sharet lehaktirou} \u8212- he brings it up in the {\i kli sharet} for the purpose of offering it, which both clarifies that the {\i kli sharet} is not itself being consumed and that the burning has not yet taken place. First, he salts it \u8212- {\i umolcha} \u8212- in fulfillment of the verse {\i vechol korban minchatcha bamelach timlach}, that all {\i korbanot} must be salted. Only then does he place it on the fire: {\i venotnah al gabei ha-ishim}. Afterward, {\i karev ha-kometz sheyarayim ne\u8217'echalin} \u8212- once the handful has been offered, the remainder ({\i sheyarayim}) becomes permissible to be eaten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Handful Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tannaitic} debate addresses the precise moment at which the {\i kometz} is considered to have been offered, thereby permitting the remainder. Rabbi Chanina holds that it is once the fire has taken hold: {\i mishe-tishlot bo ha-ur}. Rabbi Yochanan holds a stricter standard: {\i mishe-tatzit ha-ur berubo} \u8212- once the fire has caught the majority of it. This is a more advanced stage, requiring that the fire has not merely ignited the {\i kometz} but has actually taken hold of most of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixing Ingredients\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: the {\i kohanim} may then mix into the remainder wine, oil, and honey \u8212- anything they wish \u8212- provided they do not allow it to become {\i hametz}. The reason: the verse says {\i lemoshcha} \u8212- the remainder is to be treated in a manner befitting dignity, {\i kederech shehamelachim ochlim}, in the way that kings eat. One might have thought that mixing in additional foodstuffs diminishes the sanctity of the {\i korban}; the {\i braita} teaches that on the contrary, eating in a dignified, refined manner is not only permitted but appropriate. The sole limitation is that one may not allow the remainder to become leavened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leavening Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this prohibition is the verse {\i lo teafeh hametz chelkam} \u8212- do not make even their portion into {\i hametz}. Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish derives from the word {\i chelkam} that even the priests\u8217' portion \u8212- the remainder left after the {\i kometz} is offered \u8212- may not be allowed to become {\i hametz}. One might wonder how leavening is even possible once a {\i mincha} has already been cooked or placed on fire, a question that belongs more properly to Masechet Menachot, where an entire chapter is devoted to the {\i issur} of {\i hametz} in the context of {\i menachot}. Different {\i menachot} involve different modes of preparation \u8212- some are placed directly on coals, some are made on a flat pan ({\i machavat}) or deep pan ({\i meracheshet}), and there is even a {\i minchat ma\u8217'afeh tanur} baked in an oven \u8212- which is part of why Masechet Menachot is so extensive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Menachot Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sinner\u8217's Mincha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having completed the background {\i braita}, the Gemara now returns to the {\i minchat sotah} and opens with the statement from the Mishnah that all {\i menachot} require oil and frankincense, with the exception of the {\i mincha} of the {\i sotah}. The Gemara immediately challenges this: {\i vehaika minchat chotei} \u8212- what about the {\i minchat chotei}, the meal offering of a sinner? At the end of Parashat Vayikra, a category of {\i korbanot} called {\i oleh veyored} (variable offerings, scaled to one\u8217's means) provides that for certain specified sins, a person of limited means may bring a {\i mincha} in lieu of an animal {\i chatat}. That offering \u8212- the {\i minchat chotei} \u8212- is also explicitly stated in the Torah to require neither oil nor frankincense: {\i lo yasim aleha shemen velo yitein aleha levonah}, followed by {\i ki chatati} \u8212- because it is a sin offering. So the {\i sotah}\u8217's {\i mincha} is not unique in lacking oil and frankincense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Qualities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by reframing the Mishnah\u8217's claim. {\i Hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- what the Mishnah is actually saying is not that all other {\i menachot} have both oil and frankincense, but rather that all other {\i menachot} possess at least one of three positive qualities: they contain {\i shemen} (oil), {\i levona} (frankincense), or they are made from refined wheat flour ({\i soles}) that comes from wheat ({\i chitin}). Every {\i mincha}, without exception, has at least one of these distinguishing positive characteristics \u8212- though some have only one, while others have all three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sota Mincha Qualities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate: the {\i minchat chotei}, the sinner\u8217's meal offering, lacks oil and frankincense, yet it is made from refined wheat flour ({\i soles min ha-chitin}) \u8212- one redeeming quality remains. The {\i minchat ha-omer}, though it comes from barley rather than wheat, still requires oil and frankincense and is prepared from refined barley flour ({\i geres}) \u8212- it retains two of the three positive qualities. By contrast, {\i ve-zo} \u8212- the {\i sotah}\u8217's {\i mincha} \u8212- has none: it has no oil, no frankincense, it is made from barley ({\i se\u8217'orim}) rather than wheat, and it is made from coarsely ground flour ({\i kemach}) rather than refined flour ({\i soles}) \u8212- that is, whole grain with the bran still present, the opposite of {\i soles}. It is thus uniquely deficient across all three dimensions, a point the Gemara emphasizes as expressive of its degraded symbolic character. And yet, as an aside, the {\i kohanim} are still obligated to eat it \u8212- they receive no prize for that particular assignment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sota Mincha Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is cited presenting Rabbi Shimon\u8217's analysis of the underlying logic. He argues that by strict reasoning, the {\i minchat chotei} \u8212- the standard sinner\u8217's meal offering \u8212- {\i should} have required oil and frankincense: {\i she-lo yehei chotei niskar} \u8212- so that the sinner should not benefit by having a less costly or less elaborate offering. Why should he be let off easy? Yet the Torah does not impose this additional expense. Why? {\i She-lo yehei korbano mehurdar} \u8212- because it is more important that the offering not be beautiful or embellished, since its symbolic function \u8212- communicating the gravity of sin \u8212- outweighs the concern about the sinner\u8217's financial ease. The symbolic message takes precedence over the punitive economic logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sin Offering Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon extends this reasoning. He argues that by the same logic, a {\i chatat} for a standard sin (conventionally referred to as a {\i chatat cheilev}, since the passage establishing the {\i chatat} in the Torah is juxtaposed to the prohibition of eating forbidden fat) {\i should} have required {\i nesachim} (libations \u8212- the accompanying wine, oil, and flour offerings that come with many animal sacrifices). Rashi notes that the juxtaposition is not incidental: the laws of {\i chatat} in the Torah are written directly adjacent to the passage about {\i cheilev} and {\i dam} being offered on the {\i mizbeach}, making {\i chatat cheilev} a natural reference point. The same logic applies: the sinner should not benefit from the absence of {\i nesachim}. But they are not required \u8212- {\i she-lo yehei korbano mehurdar} \u8212- so that the offering not be embellished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Importantly, Rabbi Shimon is here giving a {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'kra} \u8212- a reason-based interpretation of the Torah\u8217's ruling. The question raised is whether he is merely {\i describing} the known law by offering an explanation, or whether he is actually {\i deriving} the law from his interpretive principle and using it to determine halacha in cases we do not yet know. The first possibility is significant because Rabbi Shimon is generally one who {\i darshinan ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'kra} \u8212- who applies the reason of a verse to determine legal conclusions in other cases. The Gemara will test which of these readings is correct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon continues: the {\i chatat} and {\i asham} of a {\i metzora} (a person afflicted with {\i tzara\u8217'at}) {\i do} bring {\i nesachim} \u8212- and this is because they are not brought on account of sin. The {\i metzora}\u8217's purification offerings are not {\i kaparah} for transgression but rather part of a {\i taharah} process. Rashi notes that this is not derived by Rabbi Shimon alone from logical inference \u8212- the Gemara elsewhere (Masechet Menachot, chapter {\i Shtei Middot}) actually has a specific {\i drasha} to include the {\i metzora}\u8217's offerings in the requirement of {\i nesachim}. So according to Rashi, Rabbi Shimon\u8217's statement here is {\i descriptive}: he knows the law and is explaining {\i why} these offerings are the exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then objects: is it really true that the {\i metzora}\u8217's offerings have nothing to do with sin? Does not the Gemara elsewhere (Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani citing Rabbi Yonatan) state that {\i nega\u8217'im} come for seven sins? The Gemara answers: there, the {\i nega} itself serves as the atonement for the sin \u8212- {\i hu d\u8217'ichpar lei}. By the time the person brings the {\i korban}, it is not to atone for sin but simply to be reinstated in the world of {\i kodshim}: {\i l\u8217'ishtarei b\u8217'kodshim hu d\u8217'ka-mayti}. The {\i korban} is purely a part of the {\i taharah} process, not an act of {\i kaparah} for the original transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses further: according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's logic, a {\i chatat} of a {\i nazir} should also require {\i nesachim}, since it too is not brought on account of sin. We know that in practice a {\i nazir}\u8217's {\i chatat} does not have {\i nesachim} \u8212- so the challenge is: does this refute Rabbi Shimon\u8217's explanatory principle entirely, or does it merely reveal that we are working with established {\i halacha} and testing whether his explanation is consistent with the data?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a subtle and important question about the nature of the argument. If the Gemara is actually using Rabbi Shimon\u8217's principle to determine what the law {\i should} be, it would be proposing a real change in {\i halacha}. But that seems not to be the case \u8212- the Gemara appears to know the {\i halacha} and is asking whether Rabbi Shimon\u8217's explanation is coherent given the data. Either way, the challenge stands: the {\i chatat nazir} does not bring {\i nesachim}, yet according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's framework it should, since it is not brought for sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes a more fundamental formulation available: the underlying rule is simply that only a {\i korban} that can be brought {\i beneder uvenidava} \u8212- as a freewill offering \u8212- brings {\i nesachim}. An {\i olah} and {\i shelamim} fall into that category and therefore always bring {\i nesachim}, even when obligatory (as in {\i musaf} offerings). A {\i chatat} and {\i asham}, which are by nature always obligatory and never voluntary, do not bring {\i nesachim} \u8212- with the single exception of the {\i metzora}\u8217's {\i chatat} and {\i asham}, which the Torah explicitly associates with {\i nesachim}. This is the real rule. Rabbi Shimon is offering a {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'kra} \u8212- an explanatory rationale \u8212- for why the rule works out the way it does, not a derivation of the rule itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the challenge: {\i savar lah keRabbi Eliezer haKappar} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds in accordance with Rabbi Eliezer haKappar, who maintains that a {\i nazir} is also considered a sinner. Therefore, even the {\i chatat} of a {\i nazir} is brought in the context of sin, and the data remains consistent with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a difficulty by noting that other passages in the Gemara discuss the extent of Rabbi Eliezer haKappar\u8217's position. Some {\i Gemarot} suggest that only a {\i nazir tamei} \u8212- one who became ritually impure and thereby failed to complete his {\i nezirut} \u8212- is considered a sinner by Rabbi Eliezer haKappar, because through impurity he undermined his commitment. Other {\i Gemarot} suggest that even a {\i nazir tahor} \u8212- one who completed his {\i nezirut} without any impurity \u8212- is considered a sinner simply because he denied himself the permitted pleasure of wine; even without regret, there is an element of improper self-denial. The present Gemara, Tosafot observes, seems to require that even the {\i nazir tahor} be considered a sinner according to Rabbi Eliezer haKappar \u8212- because it is specifically the {\i nazir tahor} whose {\i chatat} is an animal offering of the type that could theoretically require {\i nesachim}. The {\i nazir tamei} brings a different type of sacrifice. So the challenge and its resolution only cohere if Rabbi Eliezer haKappar\u8217's category of \u8220"sinner\u8221" encompasses the {\i nazir tahor}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal-Like Actions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a {\i braita} in which Rabban Gamliel offers an independent explanation for why the {\i sotah}\u8217's {\i mincha} is made from barley. He addresses the sages \u8212- referred to here as {\i sofrim} \u8212- with a certain formality, saying {\i hanichu li ve\u8217'edreshenu kemin chomer} \u8212- \u8220"leave me and I will expound it beautifully.\u8221" The phrase {\i kemin chomer} is interpreted by Rashi as something golden and beautiful, like a fine clasp; Tosafot suggests it may mean a pearl or jewel. Either way, Rabban Gamliel signals that he has a particularly elegant formulation to offer. The dynamic is intriguing: one wonders whether the designation {\i sofrim} here reflects a distinction between those whose specialty is {\i halacha} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel\u8217's primary domain \u8212- and those whose specialty is {\i drash} and symbolic interpretation, prompting his somewhat pointed request to be allowed to speak. A similar dynamic appears elsewhere in the literature, as when Rabbi Akiva is told that his area is the hardest legal tractates like {\i nega\u8217'im} and {\i ohalot}, not {\i aggadah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's explanation: {\i kesheim shema\u8217'aseha ma\u8217'aseh beheimah kach korbanah ma\u8217'achal beheimah} \u8212- just as her actions were like those of an animal \u8212- following her desires without human moral restraint \u8212- so too her offering is the food of an animal. This is, of course, a deeply dehumanizing framing, and, as has been noted throughout these pages, it presumes her guilt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wealth Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preceding explanation, attributed to Rabbi Meir, was that since she fed her lover the delicacies of the world ({\i ma\u8217'adanei olam}), her offering is the food of animals ({\i ma\u8217'achal beheimah}). Rabban Gamliel\u8217's objection to Rabbi Meir is that this reasoning works only for a wealthy woman who could actually feed her lover delicacies. What about a poor woman? The explanation simply does not account for all cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universal Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel\u8217's explanation, by contrast, applies universally. It does not depend on what she fed her lover or what her financial means were. Regardless of her economic situation, the characterization of her actions as animal-like \u8212- as driven by appetite rather than moral agency \u8212- is the same. Therefore, the offering that symbolizes those actions is uniformly the food of animals.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Water Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessel and Water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents the next Mishnah, which describes the preparation of the bitter waters. The {\i kohen} would bring a {\i pili shel cheres} \u8212- a pottery vial or vessel \u8212- and place into it {\i chatzi log mayim min ha-kiyor}, half a {\i log} of water from the {\i kiyor} (the laver in the Temple). A half-{\i log} is approximately double a {\i revi\u8217'it}, corresponding to something like six or seven ounces \u8212- roughly the size of a standard cup.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quarter-Log Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees, holding that only a {\i revi\u8217'it} of water is required. His reasoning will be explained later: since Rabbi Yehuda holds that less text was written on the scroll that is erased into the water ({\i keshem shemi\u8217'at bichtav}), less water is needed to dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heichal Procedure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kohen} would then enter the {\i Heichal} \u8212- the inner sanctum of the Temple, where the {\i Shulchan} and {\i Menorah} were located \u8212- and turn to his right. This rightward turn reflects the general principle in the Temple that all turns are made to the right, the direction associated with primacy and strength. (The same principle governs, for instance, the direction in which the {\i Sefer Torah} is carried around, which results in a counter-clockwise procession, since one always moves to one\u8217's right. Similarly, the {\i kallah} circles the {\i chatan} in this direction.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marble Tile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At a specific location in the {\i Heichal}, there was a space of one cubit by one cubit ({\i ama al ama}) covered by a marble tile ({\i tavla shel shayish} \u8212- {\i tavla} being a Latin-derived term). Set into this tile was an iron ring, allowing the tile to be lifted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retrieving Dirt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kohen} would lift the tile \u8212- presumably recessed into the floor so as not to be a hazard \u8212- and retrieve dirt from the natural ground beneath it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dirt in Water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He would then place this dirt into the water, enough so that it was visible floating on the surface: {\i kedei sheyeira\u8217'eh al hamayim}. The verse cited is: {\i min ha\u8217'afar asher yihyeh bekarka hamishkan yikach hakohen venatan el ha-mayim} \u8212- \u8220"from the dust that shall be on the floor of the {\i Mishkan}, the {\i kohen} shall take and place into the water.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 New Vessel Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Vessel Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a {\i tanna} who states that the pottery vessel must be new \u8212- {\i pili shel cheres chadasha} \u8212- in the view of Rabbi Yishmael. His reasoning is a {\i gezerah shavah}: he derives {\i kli kli} \u8212- from the shared term \u8220"vessel\u8221" \u8212- connecting the {\i sotah} ritual to the {\i metzora} ritual, where the Torah also specifies {\i kli cheres}. Just as by the {\i metzora} the vessel must be new, so too here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know that the {\i metzora}\u8217's vessel must be new? The verse states: {\i veshachat et hatzippor ha\u8217'achat el kli cheres al mayim chayim} \u8212- the bird is slaughtered over a pottery vessel containing living water. The Gemara derives: just as the {\i mayim chayim} (living water \u8212- water drawn directly from a natural spring, never processed or used) has never been worked with, so too the vessel must never have been used \u8212- {\i kli shelo na\u8217'asta bo melacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates a further question: if we are connecting the {\i sotah} ritual to the {\i metzora} ritual through this {\i gezerah shavah}, should we also require {\i mayim chayim} for the {\i sotah}? The Gemara notes that Rabbi Yishmael himself holds this position \u8212- he rules that the water of the {\i kiyor} comes from an underground spring and therefore qualifies as {\i mayim chayim}. The {\i Chachamim}, however, hold that the water of the {\i kiyor} need not be {\i mayim chayim} and can be drawn from other collected sources.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then challenges the derivation itself: {\i ika l\u8217'mifrach} \u8212- one can challenge the extension from {\i metzora} to {\i sotah}. The {\i metzora} ritual involves additional requirements \u8212- cedar wood, hyssop, and crimson thread ({\i etz erez, ezov, ushni tola\u8217'at}) \u8212- which impose multiple demands on the ritual. Perhaps those additional requirements justify the further demand that the vessel be new. By the {\i sotah}, where no such parallel demands exist, we might not extend that conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blackening Invalidation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves the matter in a way that applies to the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's position \u8212- that the vessel need not be entirely new. He states: {\i lo shanu} \u8212- the teaching that the vessel need not be new applies only when it has not become blackened. {\i Aval nitachmu panav, psulin} \u8212- if its surface has blackened from use, the vessel is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unchanged Vessel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\i dumya d\u8217'mayim} \u8212- the vessel must parallel the water. Just as the water must be {\i shelo nishtanu} \u8212- unchanged from its natural state \u8212- so too the vessel must be {\i shelo nishtaneh} \u8212- unchanged from its original condition. A vessel that has blackened through use has undergone a fundamental transformation and therefore no longer meets this standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a difficulty here, noting that another Gemara in Avoda Zara makes a similar comparison \u8212- {\i kemayim, shelo nishtaneh} \u8212- in the context of {\i bishul akum}, yet that application does not map cleanly onto all the parameters of that law. Tosafot therefore concludes that the derivation here is an {\i asmachta} \u8212- a rabbinic enactment with scriptural support \u8212- and that the requirement that the vessel be in its unchanged condition is {\i derabbanan}, not {\i d\u8217'oraita}. By implication, Tosafot is similarly suggesting that {\i bishul akum} is rabbinic in nature, though that is not his primary point here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nidcha Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refiring Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava poses a question: if the vessel had become blackened \u8212- and is therefore invalid \u8212- and was then refired in a kiln until it became white again, what is the law? {\i Mi amrinan kivan de\u8217'idchu idchu} \u8212- do we say that once it was pushed away ({\i nidcheh}) from eligibility, it can never return, even after being restored? This is the principle of {\i dichui} \u8212- that once an object has been rejected for sacred use, it cannot be reaccepted even if the defect is remedied. Or do we say {\i kivan de\u8217'hadar hadar} \u8212- since it has returned to a fit state, it is once again valid? Alternatively, one might argue that since the blackening was a transitory condition that has now been reversed, it was perhaps never a true {\i dichui} in the first place \u8212- perhaps comparable to a {\i mum over} (a passing blemish) that does not permanently disqualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Items Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To shed light on this question, the Gemara cites Rabbi Eliezer: the cedar, hyssop, and crimson string used in the {\i metzora} ritual \u8212- if they were used to form a strap to carry a pack on one\u8217's back \u8212- are {\i psulin}, invalid for subsequent use in the ritual. Presumably the concern is that they were bent or otherwise altered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permanent Damage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to derive from this that even if such items could theoretically be straightened out, they remain invalid. It responds: {\i hatam de\u8217'iklaf iklufei} \u8212- there, the bending may have caused actual peeling or permanent structural damage that cannot truly be undone. Therefore the {\i pesul} is permanent not because of {\i dichui} per se, but because the physical object cannot be fully restored. This response limits the case\u8217's applicability to the {\i nidcha} question about the vessel: since the vessel {\i can} be fully restored through refiring, perhaps it does return to valid status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Melacha Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a difficulty with the Gemara\u8217's framing. In a {\i Tosefta} to Nega\u8217'im (chapter {\i Ein Metzora}), Rabbi Eliezer himself states that the cedar, hyssop, and crimson thread used in the {\i metzora} ritual must not have had {\i melacha} performed with them. This suggests that the invalidation in that case is not primarily about the bending at all, but about the prohibition of {\i melacha} with these items \u8212- yet the Gemara focuses entirely on the bending. Tosafot does not resolve this difficulty, but it highlights a real tension: if {\i melacha} is the operative concern, the Gemara\u8217's analysis of whether the bending is correctable seems beside the point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question also connects to the broader discussion of {\i dichui} in contexts beyond {\i korbanot}. The classic principle of {\i nidcheh} applies most clearly to sanctified {\i korbanot}. It becomes less clear how it applies to objects used in rituals associated with the Temple but not themselves sanctified \u8212- like the items for the {\i metzora} or the vessel for the {\i sotah}. There is also a distinct question about {\i bigdei kehunah}: Tosafot notes that a Gemara in Zevachim holds that priestly garments which became soiled or torn invalidate the Temple service if worn in that state, yet if laundered they are restored to valid status. Priestly garments are sanctified objects, yet a correctable defect does not permanently disqualify them. One might contrast this with the blackened vessel, where the restoration requires refiring \u8212- perhaps a more drastic intervention \u8212- and ask whether that more fundamental transformation constitutes permanent {\i dichui}. These various permutations \u8212- {\i korban}, {\i mitzvah} object, Temple ritual object that is not a {\i korban}, and sanctified garments \u8212- each raise distinct questions about whether and how the concept of {\i dichui} applies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dirt Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishkan Ground\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to a {\i braita} analyzing the verse: {\i min ha\u8217'afar asher yihyeh bekarka haMishkan yikach hakohen venatan el ha-mayim}. One might have thought that the {\i kohen} could simply bring dirt from outside and carry it in. The {\i Talmud lomar} \u8212- the teaching that counters this \u8212- is {\i bekarka haMishkan}: the dirt must come from the floor of the {\i Mishkan} itself, not from an external source. On the other hand, one might have thought that {\i bekarka haMishkan} requires the {\i kohen} to actually dig up the floor with a shovel \u8212- and this too is rejected. {\i Asher yihyeh} \u8212- \u8220"that shall be\u8221" \u8212- indicates dirt that simply happens to be on the floor, not dirt that must be actively excavated. The practical upshot: {\i yesh sham havei, ein sham ten sham} \u8212- if there is already loose dirt there, use it; if not, pre-lay some dirt on the floor so that it will be available when the time comes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} takes an entirely different approach. Reading {\i asher yihyeh} as the operative phrase rather than {\i bekarka haMishkan}, this opinion holds that any dirt will do \u8212- dirt can even be brought from outside. The word {\i bekarka haMishkan} is then available to teach something else entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Locations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to this reading, {\i bekarka haMishkan} serves to include other locations beyond the original {\i Mishkan} in the wilderness. Issi ben Yehuda teaches: the verse comes to include the {\i karka} of Shilo, Nov, Givon, and even {\i Beis Olamim} \u8212- the permanent Temple. Dirt from the floors of any of these sacred locations would be valid for the {\i sotah} ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nov Givon Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi is deeply troubled by the inclusion of Nov and Givon and therefore maintains a different {\i girsa} of the text, excluding them and limiting the {\i ribbuy} to {\i Beis Olamim}. His reasoning: Shilo requires no {\i ribbuy} at all, since it was the actual {\i Mishkan} relocated from the wilderness \u8212- obviously it is included in the plain meaning of the verse. Nov and Givon, by contrast, had a diminished status: they functioned as a {\i bamah}, a high place, without the {\i Aron} and without the full status of a central sanctuary, and crucially, the {\i sotah} ritual would not have been performed there \u8212- it was not a place where individual communal rituals of this kind were conducted. Therefore Rashi considers it a corruption of the text to include Nov and Givon, and restricts the expansion to {\i Beis Olamim} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot disagrees with Rashi\u8217's emendation of the {\i girsa} and defends the inclusion of Nov and Givon on the grounds that Issi ben Yehuda may follow the opinion of Rabbi Yehuda (cited in chapter {\i Ba Siman}) that individual ritual offerings performed at the {\i Ohel Mo\u8217'ed} in the wilderness could also be performed at the {\i Ohel Mo\u8217'ed} at Gilgal \u8212- suggesting there is a position according to which individual rituals like {\i sotah} could indeed be performed at Nov and Givon. To emend the text entirely on the basis of a difficulty, Tosafot says, is going too far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal V\u8217'Chomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Issi ben Menachem holds that no {\i ribbuy} is needed to include {\i Beis Olamim}. A {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} suffices: if, regarding the lesser {\i tumah} of ordinary ritual impurity, the Torah makes no distinction between the {\i Mishkan} and the permanent {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- one may not enter either while {\i tamei} \u8212- then certainly the weightier moral {\i tumah} of {\i eshet ish} (suspected adultery, referred to in the Torah as {\i venitme\u8217'ah}) should equally apply to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. Therefore {\i Beis Olamim} requires no special {\i ribbuy}. What, then, does {\i bekarka haMishkan} teach according to Issi ben Menachem? Simply that one cannot bring dirt from a basket \u8212- it must come from the floor, as explained earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} is, frankly, a striking and puzzling one. The comparison being drawn is between two very different senses of {\i tumah}. The {\i tumah} of ordinary ritual impurity is a specific halachic status governed by the laws of entering sacred precincts. The {\i tumah} of {\i eshet ish} is a moral and spiritual designation \u8212- the Torah says {\i venitme\u8217'ah}, meaning she has become defiled through her actions \u8212- but this never generates a {\i halacha} that a known adulteress is forbidden to enter the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. Furthermore, the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} itself runs in a counterintuitive direction: if ordinary {\i tumah} is kept {\i out} of the Temple, then the weighty {\i tumah} of {\i eshet ish} should\u8230 ? be processed inside it? The inference does not flow in an obvious way. Nevertheless, the analogy between these two distinct domains of {\i tumah} \u8212- the halachic-ritual and the moral-spiritual \u8212- is a fascinating one, and the {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}, however structurally unusual, serves to ground the {\i sotah} ritual\u8217's connection to the permanent {\i Beit HaMikdash} in a principle rather than a mere {\i ribbuy} of the text.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 16\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dirt Source & Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-laid vs.\u160 ?Fresh Dirt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a question about the source of the dirt used in the {\i sotah} ritual. The verse states {\i asher yihyeh be-karka ha-mishkan} \u8212- the dirt that will be on the ground of the {\i mishkan} \u8212- which raises the question of whether the {\i kohen} must actually dig the dirt from the ground of the {\i mishkan}, or whether it suffices to bring dirt in and pre-lay it there. As we saw at the close of the previous daf, the possibility of pre-laying dirt \u8212- placing it beneath the floor tiles rather than digging it afresh \u8212- is on the table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Tannaitic Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two tannaitic positions emerge from the {\i braisa} at the top of the daf. Issi ben Menachem holds that {\i be-karka ha-mishkan} establishes a requirement that the dirt be placed on the ground \u8212- that is, the dirt must rest on the floor of the {\i mishkan} \u8212- but does not require it to be dug from there. Issi ben Yehudah takes a broader interpretive step: he understands {\i be-karka ha-mishkan} not as a procedural requirement about the dirt\u8217's placement, but as a geographic marker whose function is to exclude what might otherwise have been excluded, thereby teaching that the {\i sotah} ritual is valid not only in the {\i mishkan} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geographic Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to one version of Issi ben Yehudah\u8217's position, the verse extends the validity of the ritual to Shilo, Nov, and Givon \u8212- the intermediate sites of the {\i mishkan} before the permanent Temple was built. Rashi is uncomfortable with this extension and crosses it out, since the {\i sotah} procedure resembles the offering of an individual \u8212- a {\i korban yachid} \u8212- and there is a question whether such offerings could be brought at Nov and Givon, which had the status of a {\i bamah} and permitted individual altars. Tosafot, however, defends the inclusion, explaining that this reflects the view that a {\i korban yachid} could in fact be brought at Nov and Givon. In any case, the verse also extends the ritual to the {\i beit olamim} \u8212- the permanent {\i beit hamikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Interpretive Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning briefly to the discussion from the previous daf, it is worth noting that the {\i gemara}\u8217's treatment of the {\i sotah} ritual reflects three distinct conceptual frameworks. The plain sense of the {\i pesukim} frames the ritual as a mechanism for determining guilt or innocence. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's emphasis, by contrast, presumes guilt and frames the ritual as punishment. A third lens \u8212- and the one particularly relevant here \u8212- is that of purification: the {\i sotah} brings a {\i korban}, she stands in the section of the Temple where {\i teme\u8217'im} stood (at the Sha\u8217'ar Nicanor, the place where those afflicted with {\i tzara\u8217'at} and women after childbirth would stand), and she undergoes a rite at the {\i beit hamikdash}. This lens of {\i tumah} and purification runs through the discussion in ways that are not immediately obvious from the text, and the {\i gemara} itself draws on the {\i sotah}\u8217's category of {\i tumah} as a precedent for laws of {\i safek tumah} in entirely unrelated contexts \u8212- such as the rules of {\i safek tumah be-reshus ha-rabim} and {\i safek tumah be-reshus ha-yachid}, which are derived from the {\i sotah}\u8217's act of secluding herself in a {\i reshus ha-yachid}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit HaMikdash Inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why does the {\i torah} need a verse to include the {\i beit hamikdash}? Is the permanent Temple not always presumed to be included wherever the {\i torah} says {\i mishkan}? Tosafot addresses this directly and offers an illuminating answer: the difficulty is that the verse says {\i be-karka ha-mishkan} \u8212- the ground of the {\i mishkan} \u8212- and the {\i beit hamikdash}, unlike the {\i mishkan}, has a paved floor. One might therefore have thought that the {\i beit hamikdash} is specifically excluded, since the verse emphasizes {\i karka} \u8212- ground \u8212- and the Temple\u8217's interior is covered with stone flooring. The special inclusion is therefore necessary to override that potential exclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ashes vs.\u160 ?Dirt Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Dirt Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a new question: {\i ein sham afar, mahu sheyiten efer?} \u8212- what if there is no dirt available? May ashes be used in its place? This question arises within the context already established: we have concluded that the dirt need not be dug from the ground; at most, it must be placed upon the ground. Given that latitude, might ashes \u8212- which the {\i torah} itself sometimes calls {\i afar} \u8212- also qualify?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aliba de-Beit Shammai lo tivaei lach} \u8212- according to Beit Shammai, there is no question at all. Beit Shammai holds {\i lo matzinu efer she-karui afar}: ashes are never called {\i afar}. The two words \u8212- {\i afar} (dirt) and {\i efer} (ashes) \u8212- are categorically distinct, and no verse establishes that {\i efer} falls under the rubric of {\i afar}. The question therefore simply does not arise for Beit Shammai.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question becomes pressing according to Beit Hillel, who holds {\i matzinu efer she-karui afar} \u8212- there is indeed a case where ashes are called {\i afar}. The source is the verse {\i ve-lakach me-afar sereifat ha-parah} \u8212- \u8220"he shall take from the {\i afar} of the burning of the {\i parah}\u8221" (Numbers 19:17). The {\i parah adumah} leaves ashes, yet the verse calls them {\i afar}. Beit Hillel reads this as evidence that the word {\i afar} can encompass {\i efer}. Beit Shammai responds that {\i afar sereifa} is a compound term meaning \u8220"the dust of burning,\u8221" which is a way of referring to ashes \u8212- but the word {\i afar} standing alone does not mean ashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood-Covering Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical application of this dispute extends to {\i kisui ha-dam} \u8212- the commandment to cover blood after {\i shechita}, as the verse says {\i ve-kissehu be-afar} (Leviticus 17:13). May ashes be used? Beit Shammai says no, since ashes are not called {\i afar}. Beit Hillel says yes, since {\i afar sereifat ha-parah} demonstrates that {\i efer} can be called {\i afar}. According to Beit Hillel, one may fulfill {\i kisui ha-dam} with ashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dirt Type Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks whether the reasoning that allows ashes for {\i kisui ha-dam} according to Beit Hillel would also permit them for the {\i sotah}. One might argue that it should, since in both cases the operative word is {\i afar}. But the {\i gemara} raises a counterargument: {\i af al gav de-ikarei afar, hacha be-karka ha-mishkan ktiv} \u8212- even granting that {\i efer} can be called {\i afar}, the {\i sotah} verse specifically says {\i be-karka ha-mishkan}, which implies a connection to the nature of {\i karka}. Perhaps the contextual emphasis on {\i karka} restricts the {\i afar} here to something that genuinely resembles ground, and ashes \u8212- however legitimately called {\i afar} in another context \u8212- do not qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Readings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains {\i be-karka ha-mishkan} to mean that even if you may take dirt out of a basket without digging, it must still be the type of substance that {\i could} be the {\i karka ha-mishkan} \u8212- it must genuinely resemble ground. Alternatively, one may read {\i be-karka ha-mishkan} as conveying the territorial teaching of Issi ben Yehudah and Issi ben Menachem \u8212- not a qualitative requirement about the nature of the dirt itself \u8212- in which case the word {\i afar} might remain open enough to include {\i efer} according to Beit Hillel. Whether {\i be-karka ha-mishkan} is a qualitative restriction on the substance or merely a territorial and procedural teaching is thus the crux of the question left open at this point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halacha Okevet HaMikra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's Princip\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots to a celebrated ruling: {\i ta shema, de-amar Rabbi Yochanan mishum Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- come and hear, as Rabbi Yochanan says in the name of Rabbi Yishmael. {\i Be-shelosha mekomot halacha okevet mikra} \u8212- in three places the {\i halacha} undermines the plain sense of scripture. The word {\i okevet} \u8212- which may be translated as \u8220"undermines\u8221" or \u8220"overrides\u8221" \u8212- captures the idea that in these three cases, if one were to follow the simple reading of the verse, one would arrive at a {\i halacha} different from what the tradition mandates. Rashi explains that {\i halacha} here means {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} \u8212- an ancient received tradition \u8212- and it is this received tradition that stands in apparent tension with the plain sense of the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kissui HaDam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case is {\i kisui ha-dam}. The {\i torah} says {\i be-afar} \u8212- with dirt \u8212- yet the {\i halacha} is {\i be-chol davar} \u8212- with anything that can grow seeds. This is not a limitless expansion; one cannot, for example, cover blood with a book. But the tradition permits substances that resemble dirt in its seed-growing capacity, such as ground pottery shards, even though they are not literally {\i afar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Hair Cutting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case as quoted in the {\i gemara} is the {\i nazir}. The verse states {\i ta\u8217'ar lo ya\u8217'avor al rosho} \u8212- a razor shall not pass over his head \u8212- yet the {\i halacha} extends the prohibition to any instrument that removes hair. The {\i nazir} is forbidden from cutting his hair with anything, not only a razor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Writing Surface\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is the {\i get}. The verse uses the word {\i sefer} \u8212- a document or book \u8212- yet the {\i halacha} permits writing a {\i get} on any surface that is detached from the ground: a leaf, an animal\u8217's horn, the arm of a slave. The received tradition does not confine the {\i get} to parchment or any particular material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Girsa Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi takes issue with the second case as presented \u8212- the {\i ta\u8217'ar} of the {\i nazir} \u8212- and argues that this is actually not a case of the {\i halacha} undermining scripture but of the {\i halacha} expanding and intensifying scripture. The {\i torah} says a razor is forbidden; the {\i halacha} adds that all instruments are forbidden. This is being more {\i machmir} than the verse, not contradicting it. Rashi acknowledges a somewhat forced explanation \u8212- that it \u8220"undermines\u8221" in the sense that lashes would be administered for violations involving other instruments even though the verse does not explicitly require them, thereby raising the specter of lashing an innocent person \u8212- but Rashi finds this unsatisfying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi therefore prefers a different {\i girsa} (textual reading), citing the {\i Yerushalmi} in Tractate Kiddushin, where the three cases are: {\i sefer} \u8212- the {\i get}; {\i afar} \u8212- {\i kisui ha-dam}; and {\i martzea} \u8212- the awl used to pierce the ear of the slave who refuses his freedom (Exodus 21:6). As quoted by Rashi: {\i be-masechet Kiddushin Yerushalmi matzati mishna zu} \u8212- the three cases are the {\i sefer}, the {\i afar}, and the {\i martzea}. The {\i halacha} by the slave\u8217's ear is that any piercing instrument works \u8212- {\i afilu sul ve-sira} \u8212- even a thorn or similar implement. Rashi adds: {\i vehu nireh be-einai me\u8217'od de-hach de-ta\u8217'ar tosefet hi} \u8212- it seems very clear to him that the {\i ta\u8217'ar} version is an addition that does not belong, since it adds to rather than contradicts the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why These Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi raises a deeper question: given that {\i midrash halacha} regularly reinterprets verses \u8212- sometimes expanding them, sometimes narrowing them \u8212- what makes these three cases distinctive enough to be labeled {\i halacha okevet mikra}? The answer Rashi provides is that in all the other cases where the {\i halacha} diverges from the plain sense, there is a {\i drasha} \u8212- a hermeneutical derivation embedded in the verse \u8212- that allows one to say the alternate reading is present within the {\i mikra} itself. One may disagree about whether a given {\i drasha} is compelling, but there is a method that links the {\i halacha} back to the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael, however, holds that for these three cases, no such {\i drasha} exists. Other {\i tanna\u8217'im} do offer {\i drashot} for these laws \u8212- they find ways to derive {\i be-chol davar} for {\i kisui ha-dam} from the verse, ways to read the {\i sefer} of {\i get} as including any surface, and so on. But Rabbi Yishmael does not accept those {\i drashot}. As Rashi writes: {\i de-Rabbi Yishmael lo mashme\u8217' lei kra\u8217'i de-vechasav lah de-marbina minei le-rabot kol davar} \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael does not read the verse \u8220"and he shall write for her\u8221" as yielding an expansion to include all surfaces. {\i Ella halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai samchina} \u8212- the only basis is the received tradition. And any verse that appears to support it is merely an {\i asmachta}. {\i Hilkach, she\u8217'ar midrashim\u8230 ? lo chashiv lei Rabbi Yishmael ke-halacha okevet} \u8212- all other {\i drashot} throughout the {\i torah} do not constitute {\i halacha okevet} in Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's view, because in those cases there is a legitimate hermeneutical derivation that accounts for the {\i halacha}. But these three he cannot derive. The tradition stands and the simple sense of the verse is genuinely overridden. Rabbi Yishmael is prepared to live with that disparity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Midrash HaLacha Origins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tradition vs.\u160 ?Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion opens a broader question that is genuinely debated among {\i rishonim} and modern scholars alike: in {\i midrash halacha}, did {\i Chazal} begin with a received tradition about what the {\i halacha} is and then return to the {\i pesukim} to locate support for it, or did they begin with the text and derive the {\i halacha} through hermeneutics? The answer is almost certainly not uniform \u8212- sometimes one approach, sometimes the other \u8212- and the evidence internal to the {\i gemara} often points toward which method was operative in a given case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Drasha Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a {\i drasha} appears highly forced \u8212- when one wonders whether any independent reader of the verse would have arrived at this interpretation \u8212- it is a strong signal that {\i Chazal} started with the practice and worked backward. The classic example is the {\i gemara}\u8217's discussion of {\i pikuach nefesh docheh Shabbos}: it is manifest that the {\i halacha} was known and established, and the {\i gemara} is proposing various possible pesukim to serve as its textual anchor. When the {\i drasha} seems genuinely embedded in the verse, one may more plausibly conclude they started with the text and reasoned forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's Stance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is remarkable about Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's position is that it goes even further. Most situations where {\i Chazal} start with the tradition can still, in the end, produce a {\i drasha} \u8212- perhaps forced, perhaps creative, but one that anchors the {\i halacha} in the {\i pasuk} retroactively. Rabbi Yishmael is saying something more striking: even after the fact, even knowing the {\i halacha}, he cannot in good conscience say that the {\i pasuk} contains it. It is pure tradition, and the verses remain in apparent contradiction to it. This is why the term {\i okevet} \u8212- undermining \u8212- is apt for Rabbi Yishmael in a way it would not be for others: he is the one for whom the {\i halacha} genuinely overrides and does not resolve with the {\i mikra}. Other {\i tanna\u8217'im} have {\i drashot} that resolve the tension; for Rabbi Yishmael, the tension remains.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ashes Omission Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Ashes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the question of ashes in the {\i sotah} ritual and poses a pointed challenge. If ashes can in fact serve as {\i afar} for the {\i sotah} according to Beit Hillel \u8212- and if this represents a case where the {\i halacha} expands beyond the plain sense of {\i afar} \u8212- why is this not on Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's list of three places where {\i halacha okevet mikra}? This is, as Tosafot notes, a genuinely puzzling question for multiple reasons. First, Rabbi Yishmael presumably holds like Beit Shammai that {\i efer} is never called {\i afar}, and therefore there would be no expansion at all \u8212- no {\i okevet} to record. Second, if one follows Beit Hillel and {\i efer} is legitimately called {\i afar}, then the {\i halacha} is actually consistent with the {\i mikra}, not overriding it \u8212- and so again there is nothing to list. Tosafot struggles with the question, yet the {\i gemara} poses it and proceeds to answer it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbreviated List Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's first answer is {\i tana ve-shayer} \u8212- the list is incomplete; items were left off. The list says \u8220"three\u8221" but is not exhaustive; there are additional cases that also exemplify {\i halacha okevet mikra} beyond the three recorded. Ashes for {\i sotah} may be one such omission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i u-mai shayer de-hai shayer?} \u8212- if you claim the list is abbreviated, there must be more than one omitted item, because one would never compile a list and leave off only a single case. If you\u8217're going to say things were left off, show us another omission as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes: {\i shayer metzora} \u8212- the shaving of the {\i metzora} was also left off. The relevant verse, from the seventh day of the {\i metzora}\u8217's purification process, reads: {\i ve-haya ba-yom ha-shvi\u8217'i yegalach et kol se\u8217'aro} \u8212- and on the seventh day he shall shave all his hair. The verse then specifies: {\i et rosho ve-et zakano ve-et givot einav} \u8212- his head, his beard, and his eyebrows. It then returns to a general formulation: {\i ve-et kol se\u8217'aro yigalach} \u8212- all his hair shall he shave. This is a classic structure of {\i klal u-frat u-klal} \u8212- general, specific, and general \u8212- from which we derive {\i ein atah dan ella ke-ein ha-frat}: we include anything similar to what was specified. The {\i frat} describes places where hair is gathered and visible ({\i mekom kinus se\u8217'ar ve-nireh}), so we include the pubic hair ({\i se\u8217'ar ha-raglayim}, as Rabbi explains), while excluding underarm hair ({\i beit ha-shachi}) \u8212- which, even when naked, is not visible unless one raises one\u8217's arms \u8212- as well as individual stray hairs elsewhere on the body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The plain sense of the verse, following this {\i klal u-frat u-klal}, would limit the shaving on day seven to the gathered, visible areas. Yet the {\i halacha} \u8212- {\i megalcho ke-dala\u8217'at} \u8212- requires shaving the {\i metzora} completely bald, like a gourd. This appears to be another case of {\i halacha okevet mikra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Addition vs.\u160 ?Contradicti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} rejects this reasoning. {\i Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak} objects: {\i ki ka chashiv halacha okevet mikra} \u8212- when Rabbi Yishmael lists cases of {\i halacha okevet}, he means cases where the {\i halacha} genuinely contradicts and displaces the plain sense of the verse. {\i Ha okevet midirabanan hi} \u8212- this case undermines not the plain sense of the {\i mikra} but the {\i drasha} of the {\i mikra}. The {\i mikra} itself says {\i kol se\u8217'aro} \u8212- all his hair \u8212- which on its face sounds comprehensive. It is only because of the {\i klal u-frat u-klal} derivation that we would narrow it to gathered areas. The {\i halacha} of shaving like a gourd contradicts the narrowed {\i drasha}, not the plain verse. This is therefore not the same category as the three cases Rabbi Yishmael listed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa offers a second reason the {\i metzora} case does not belong on the list: {\i ki chashiv halacha okevet ve-okaret} \u8212- the three listed cases involve situations where following the {\i halacha} means you would {\i not} be fulfilling the {\i mikra}\u8217's instruction. Writing a {\i get} on a leaf means you are not writing it on a {\i sefer}; covering blood with pottery shards means you have not covered it with {\i afar} in the simple sense. The {\i halacha} displaces what the verse required. But by the {\i metzora}, shaving the entire body {\i includes} the areas the verse specified \u8212- one is doing the bunches of hair {\i and more}. The {\i halacha} is {\i okevet u-mosefet} \u8212- it overrides but adds \u8212- and one who shaves the {\i metzora} completely has certainly also shaved his head, beard, and eyebrows. It does not undermine the {\i mikra}; it fulfills it and exceeds it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Derasha Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records the full {\i drasha} for both hermeneutical approaches. According to {\i klal u-frat u-klal} (Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's method): {\i klal} \u8212- {\i ve-et kol se\u8217'aro}; {\i frat} \u8212- {\i et rosho ve-et zakano ve-et givot einav}; {\i klal} \u8212- {\i ve-et kol se\u8217'aro yigalach}. {\i Ain atah dan ella ke-ein ha-frat}: we include {\i mekom kinus se\u8217'ar ve-nireh} \u8212- places where hair gathers and is visible. Rabbi rules that this includes {\i se\u8217'ar ha-raglayim} \u8212- the pubic hair \u8212- because it is visible when unclothed. It excludes {\i beit ha-shachi} (underarm hair) and individual strands across the body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i ribbuy u-miut u-ribbuy} (Rabbi Akiva\u8217's method): {\i riba} \u8212- {\i ve-et kol se\u8217'aro} (expansion); {\i mi\u8217'et} \u8212- the listed body parts (limitation); {\i riba} \u8212- {\i ve-et kol se\u8217'aro yigalach} (expansion again). When you have {\i ribbuy, miut, ve-ribbuy}, you include virtually everything, excluding only the most categorically different thing. {\i Ma mi\u8217'et} \u8212- what is excluded? {\i Mi\u8217'et se\u8217'ar she-betoch ha-chotem} \u8212- the nose hair. Everything else is included, and one shaves him like a gourd.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva vs.\u160 ?Rabbi Yi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis generates Rav Ashi\u8217's third and cleanest answer to why the {\i metzora} shaving does not belong on Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's list. {\i Ha matnita mani? Rabbi Yishmael hi} \u8212- the {\i braisa} listing the three cases of {\i halacha okevet} is the position of Rabbi Yishmael himself. {\i De-darash klal u-frat} \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael applies {\i klal u-frat u-klal}, which yields on day seven only the shaving of bunches of hair. According to Rabbi Yishmael, that is precisely what the {\i halacha} of day seven {\i is}: you do not shave the {\i metzora} like a gourd on day seven. The position that requires shaving like a gourd on day seven belongs to Rabbi Akiva, {\i de-darash ribbuy u-miut} \u8212- who reads {\i ribbuy, miut, ve-ribbuy} and therefore includes the whole body. This is not {\i halacha} against {\i drasha}; it is simply Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's {\i drasha} against Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i drasha}. There is no {\i okevet} at all from Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's perspective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We thus have three separate reasons why the {\i metzora} shaving is not on Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's list: first, it contradicts only the {\i drasha} and not the plain verse; second, it adds to rather than displaces the {\i mikra}\u8217's requirement; and third, it is not Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's {\i halacha} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With all three answers in place, we return to the original question: why are ashes not on the list? No fully satisfying resolution emerges. The {\i gemara} offers the partial answer of Rav Huna bar Ashi quoting Rav: {\i ein sham afar, mevi rakvuvit yerek} \u8212- if there is no dirt, one may bring decomposed vegetable matter and use it to sanctify the water. This is not ashes; it is something that grew from the ground and naturally decomposed, and it retains enough of the character of {\i karka} to be considered a form of {\i afar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} immediately qualifies: {\i ve-lo hi} \u8212- that is not a true equivalence. {\i Rikvuvit yerek hu de-havei afar} \u8212- decomposed vegetable can be considered {\i afar}. {\i Efer lo havei afar} \u8212- ashes cannot. So even the partial resolution expands {\i afar} only modestly \u8212- to naturally decomposed organic matter \u8212- and explicitly stops short of including ashes. The question of ashes remains without a clean resolution, and the question of why they do not appear on Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's list remains similarly open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Visibility Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Visible Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i braisa}: {\i shelosha tzerichim she-yeira\u8217'u} \u8212- three things must be visible. These are: the {\i afar} of the {\i sotah} when placed in the water; the {\i efer parah} \u8212- ashes of the {\i parah adumah} \u8212- when placed in the water; and the {\i rok yevamah} \u8212- the spit of the {\i yevamah} in the {\i chalitzah} ceremony. The verse by {\i chalitzah} states {\i ve-yarkah be-fanav} and {\i le-einei ha-zekenim}, requiring that the {\i zekenim} actually see the spit. In each of these cases, the relevant substance must be visible when combined with the water or present before the observers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bird Blood Addition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishum Rabbi Yishmael amru} \u8212- in the name of Rabbi Yishmael, they added a fourth: {\i af dam tzippor} \u8212- the blood of the bird used in the {\i metzora}\u8217's purification. When the bird is slaughtered and its blood falls into the water, that blood must be visible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bird Blood Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} provides the derivation. The verse states: {\i ve-taval otam be-dam ha-tzippor ha-shechutah u-va-mayim ha-chayim} \u8212- he shall dip them in the blood of the slaughtered bird and in the living water. A {\i braisa} is then cited: one might have thought you dip only in the blood, without water \u8212- therefore the verse says {\i ba-mayim} (in the water). One might have thought you dip only in the water, without blood \u8212- therefore the verse says {\i be-dam} (in the blood). {\i Ha keitzad?} \u8212- how is this resolved? {\i Mevi mayim she-dam tzippor nikar bahem} \u8212- you bring water in which the blood of the bird will remain recognizable. The blood and the water are combined, but the blood must remain {\i nikar} \u8212- recognizable, visible \u8212- within the mixture. Rabbi Yishmael accordingly includes this among the things that must be seen.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blood-Water Mixture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Prominence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's requirement that the blood be {\i nikar} in the water is not merely a technical formality. The verse says {\i be-dam ha-tzippor ha-shechutah} \u8212- in the blood of the slaughtered bird \u8212- rather than simply \u8220"in them,\u8221" and Rabbi Yishmael reads this as establishing that the blood must retain its essential character. You are fundamentally dipping into {\i blood} \u8212- water is added to it, but blood is what the dipping is about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Revi\u8217'it Measure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of this position is a limit on the amount of water: {\i revi\u8217'it}. If more than a {\i revi\u8217'it} of water is added, the blood will be so diffused that it can no longer be said to be {\i nikar}. At a {\i revi\u8217'it} or less, the blood remains visibly and substantively present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamshut Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot deepens this analysis. The word {\i nikar} does not mean merely that one can detect that blood was once present, the way a forensic trace might indicate a prior substance. Tosafot explains that the blood must retain its {\i mamashus} \u8212- its essential, physical substantiality. The question is framed this way: are we looking at blood that has had some water added, or at water that has had some blood added? Rabbi Yishmael requires the former. There must be enough blood relative to the water that the mixture can still be characterized as {\i dam} \u8212- blood \u8212- into which one is dipping. The verse {\i ve-taval otam be-dam ha-tzippor} obligates one to dip into {\i blood}, and therefore the {\i mamashus} of the blood must remain. This is why the water is limited to a {\i revi\u8217'it}: at that volume, one can still say the blood is present as blood, not merely as a colorant dissolved into water. This point parallels broader questions of {\i bittul} \u8212- at what point has a substance ceased to be present in its essential form and become merely a trace?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simple Mixture View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis who do not share Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's position \u8212- and who accordingly did not include the bird\u8217's blood in their list of items requiring visibility \u8212- understand the verse differently. {\i Ha-hu le-gufei} \u8212- the separate mention of blood and water is not to establish that the blood must predominate; rather, it teaches the simple fact that blood and water must be combined. The instruction {\i be-dam u-va-mayim} means: dip in a mixture of both. The blood need not retain its {\i mamashus}; what matters is that both components are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael objects: if the verse only intended to say \u8220"combine them,\u8221" it should have written {\i ve-taval bahem} \u8212- dip in them \u8212- rather than specifying {\i be-dam} and then {\i ba-mayim} separately. The separate specification of {\i be-dam} teaches that the blood must be prominent \u8212- {\i le-nikar}. The Rabbis counter: if the verse had only written {\i ve-taval bahem} \u8212- in them \u8212- one might have thought the dipping in blood and the dipping in water are done separately in sequence, first one and then the other. The explicit mention of both together teaches that they must be combined. Rabbi Yishmael responds that this teaching \u8212- the need to combine them \u8212- is already derived from a different verse: the verse {\i ve-shachatu et ha-tzippor ha-achat el keli cheres al mayim chayim} \u8212- the bird is slaughtered over a vessel of water, so the blood naturally falls into the water, producing an automatic combination. The Rabbis reply that one could technically comply with {\i shachatu al mayim chayim} while preventing the blood from mixing \u8212- for instance, by slaughtering near the vessel and diverting the blood into a separate container. Therefore a verse is still needed to teach the requirement of combination.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bird Size Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extreme Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yirmiya poses a challenge to Rabbi Zeira targeting the {\i revi\u8217'it} measure. {\i Gedola u-madachat et ha-mayim} \u8212- consider a very large bird: its blood will be so abundant that a {\i revi\u8217'it} of water will be entirely overwhelmed, with no meaningful presence of water remaining. {\i Ktana ve-nimchit mipnei ha-mayim} \u8212- consider a very small bird: its blood will be so scant that even a {\i revi\u8217'it} of water will overwhelm it, and no meaningful presence of blood will remain. How can a single fixed measure of a {\i revi\u8217'it} work across the full range of bird sizes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sparrow Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira\u8217's response carries an implicit rebuke: {\i lav amina lach lo teippek nafshach levar me-hilchata} \u8212- haven\u8217't I told you not to try to outsmart the {\i halacha} in this way? The {\i revi\u8217'it} measure was calibrated specifically for the {\i tzippor dror} \u8212- the sparrow \u8212- which is the standard bird used for this ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Normal Range Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within the range of sparrows, Rabbi Zeira explains, the measure holds: {\i ein lach gedola she-madachat et ha-mayim} \u8212- there is no sparrow large enough that its blood will overwhelm a {\i revi\u8217'it} of water \u8212- {\i ve-ein lach ktana she-nimchit mipnei ha-mayim} \u8212- and there is no sparrow small enough that its blood will be overwhelmed by a {\i revi\u8217'it} of water. The {\i shiur} of a {\i revi\u8217'it} was established with the normal range of sparrows in mind, and within that range it is an accurate and workable measure. Extreme hypotheticals about oversized or undersized birds are not the target audience of the {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixing Order Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dirt-First Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the {\i sotah} ritual and the question of sequence. A {\i braisa} states: {\i hikdim afar le-mayim \u8212- pasul} \u8212- if the dirt was placed in the vessel before the water, the mixture is invalid. The verse {\i ve-natan el ha-mayim} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall put into the water\u8221" \u8212- implies that the water must already be present when the dirt is added.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disagrees: {\i Rabbi Shimon machshir} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon validates the mixture even if the dirt was placed first. His reasoning is derived from a comparison to the {\i parah adumah}, where, as we will see, both orderings have textual support, and he reads the two rituals as linked by a {\i gezeira shava}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection between the two rituals rests on the shared word {\i afar}. The {\i sotah} verse speaks of {\i afar}, and the {\i parah adumah} verse speaks of {\i afar sereifat ha-parah} \u8212- also called {\i afar} by the verse, as Beit Hillel established. Rabbi Shimon draws a {\i gezeira shava} between the two uses of {\i afar} to allow each ritual to teach something about the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Direction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does the {\i sotah} teach about the {\i parah adumah}? That the substance must be visible on top of the water \u8212- {\i afar al gabei mayim}. The {\i sotah} ritual implies that the dirt or its equivalent rests visibly upon the water\u8217's surface. Rabbi Shimon applies this teaching to the {\i parah adumah} as well: when the ashes are placed in the water, they too must be {\i nikar} \u8212- visible \u8212- on the water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Flexibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And in the reverse direction: what does the {\i parah adumah} teach the {\i sotah}? That the order of mixing can be reversed. Since the {\i parah adumah} has textual support for both orderings \u8212- as the {\i gemara} now explains \u8212- the {\i gezeira shava} extends that flexibility to the {\i sotah} as well. Even if the dirt is placed before the water in the {\i sotah} ritual, Rabbi Shimon holds it remains valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Red Heifer Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alav - Ashes First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the two verses in the {\i parah adumah} passage that seem to point in opposite directions regarding the order of mixing. The first verse is {\i ve-natan alav mayim chayim el keli} \u8212- \u8220"he shall put upon it living water into a vessel.\u8221" The word {\i alav} \u8212- \u8220"upon it\u8221" \u8212- implies that the ashes are already present when the water is added, meaning the ashes go first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 El Keli - Water First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second reading emerges from the phrase {\i mayim chayim el keli} \u8212- \u8220"living water into a vessel.\u8221" If one reads this phrase as indicating that the living water is placed into the vessel at the outset, the implication is that the water is in the vessel first, and the ashes are added afterward. Thus the same verse seems to support both sequences: {\i alav} implies ashes first; {\i el keli} implies water first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flexible Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon reconciles the two: {\i ratza zeh noten, ratza zeh noten} \u8212- one may do it either way. The two formulations in the verse are not contradictory but complementary, each validating a different order. By the {\i parah adumah} both sequences are valid, and through the {\i gezeira shava}, both sequences become valid for the {\i sotah} as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbis\u8217' Order Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machshir on Top\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis disagree with Rabbi Shimon in both cases. Their principle is: {\i mimah matzinu be-chol makom machshir le-mala} \u8212- we find universally that the {\i machshir} \u8212- the potent, active ingredient \u8212- goes on top. The water is the base solution, a passive medium. The special substance \u8212- whether dirt, ashes, or blood \u8212- is the {\i machshir}, the component that actually activates the ritual. And that component must be placed second, on top of the water already in the vessel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this principle, the Rabbis reinterpret {\i ve-natan alav} to mean something other than \u8220"the ashes are placed first.\u8221" Once the water is already in the vessel, the ashes are added, and then {\i alav} \u8212- upon it \u8212- the water is stirred over and mixed through the ashes. The word {\i alav} describes not an initial placement of ashes but a subsequent folding of the water over the ashes after they have been added. The {\i machshir} (the ashes) still goes second \u8212- after the water \u8212- and then the mixing is described by {\i alav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis find support for water-first in three parallel cases. By {\i sotah}, the plain sense of the verse requires water first: {\i ve-natan el ha-mayim} means you put the dirt into the water already present. By the {\i parah adumah}, the Rabbis read {\i mayim chayim el keli} as mandating water first in the vessel. And by the blood of the {\i metzora}\u8217's bird, {\i al mayim chayim} \u8212- upon living water \u8212- similarly implies the water is there first and the blood descends into it. In all three, the water precedes the special substance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water as Base\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic is consistent: the non-water element \u8212- dirt, ashes, blood \u8212- is in each case the special, ritual-defining component, the {\i machshir}. The water is simply the medium. The ritual logic therefore demands that the medium be in place first, and the defining ingredient be introduced into it. This ordering is {\i me\u8217'akev} according to the Rabbis \u8212- it is not merely a preferred sequence but a requirement, and reversing it invalidates the mixture. Rabbi Shimon, by contrast, drawing on the {\i gezeira shava} and the flexibility encoded in both verses of the {\i parah adumah}, holds that either order is valid in both rituals.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 17\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Writing the Megillah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Mezuzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 17 opens with a new {\i mishnah} that continues the procedure for the {\i sotah}. The preceding {\i mishnayot} had addressed the collection of the dirt and its placement into the vessel \u8212- the questions of where the dirt should come from, whether it should be visible on the surface of the water, and so on. Now, having established that the woman has already brought her {\i minchah}, the {\i mishnah} turns to the next stage: writing the {\i megillah} and scraping its contents into the water. The {\i kohen} comes to write the {\i megillah} \u8212- {\i ba lo lichtov et ha-megillah} \u8212- and the question immediately arises: from which point in the relevant passage does he begin writing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Pesukim Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining the {\i mishnah}\u8217's answer, it is worth pausing to review the order of the {\i pesukim} in {\i Parshat ha-Sotah}, since the {\i mishnah}\u8217's presentation does not straightforwardly follow that order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The passage begins with the husband bringing his wife to the {\i kohen} along with her offering of a tenth of an {\i eifah} of barley flour \u8212- {\i lo yitzok alav shemen v\u8217'lo yitein alav levonah} \u8212- no oil or frankincense, for it is a {\i minchah} of jealousy, a memorial offering that recalls iniquity. That is where our {\i perek} began. The {\i kohen} then presents her before God, takes holy water in an earthen vessel, and adds dust from the floor of the {\i Mishkan} to the water \u8212- {\i v\u8217'natan el ha-mayim} \u8212- which is what the previous {\i mishnah} covered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then comes: the {\i kohen} stands the woman before God, dishevels her hair, places the {\i minchah} in her hands \u8212- and in the {\i kohen}\u8217's hands are the bitter, curse-inducing waters. This passage already mentions disheveling her hair, which in fact appeared much earlier in the {\i mishnayot}\u8217' treatment. Next: {\i v\u8217'hishbia otah ha-kohen} \u8212- the {\i kohen} makes her take an oath, saying: \u8220"If no man has lain with you, and if you have not gone astray in impurity under your husband, {\i hinaki mi\u8217'mei ha-marerim ha-eileh} \u8212- be cleansed of these bitter, curse-inducing waters.\u8221" Then the conditional curse: \u8220"And you, if you have gone astray under your husband, and if you have become impure, and a man has given his seed in you apart from your husband \u8212- {\i yitein Hashem otach l\u8217'alah v\u8217'lishvuah b\u8217'toch amech} \u8212- may the Lord make you a curse and an oath among your people, may the Lord cause your thigh to fall and your belly to swell. {\i Uva\u8217'u ha-mayim ha-marerim ha-eileh b\u8217'me\u8217'ayich} \u8212- and let these bitter waters enter your innards to swell the belly and cause the thigh to fall.\u8221" {\i V\u8217'amrah ha-ishah amen amen} \u8212- and the woman shall say: amen, amen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then: {\i v\u8217'katav et ha-alot ha-eileh ha-kohen ba-sefer u-machah el mei ha-marim} \u8212- the {\i kohen} writes these curses in a book and scrapes them into the bitter water \u8212- and then {\i v\u8217'hishkah et ha-ishah}, he makes her drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Importantly, the {\i mishnah} skips entirely over the {\i kohen}\u8217's oral administration of the oath and proceeds directly to the writing. This omission will prove significant later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Writing Point\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} asks: from where does the {\i kohen} begin writing? The answer is: from {\i v\u8217'im lo shachav ish otach} \u8212- \u8220"if no man has lain with you.\u8221" That is the opening of the oath formula proper, beginning with the conditional declaration of innocence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then adds that the {\i kohen} does not write the instructional text embedded in the {\i pesukim} \u8212- {\i eino kotev v\u8217'hishbia ha-kohen et ha-ishah}, the narration that \u8220"the {\i kohen} shall make her swear.\u8221" The text to be written contains, as it were, quotation marks: the quoted speech of the oath, not the stage directions. And at the conclusion, the {\i mishnah} states {\i eino kotev v\u8217'amrah ha-ishah amen amen} \u8212- the woman\u8217's response of {\i amen amen} is likewise not written, for that is her reaction to the oath, not the oath itself. This, the {\i mishnah} implies, is the position of the {\i tanna kamma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's approach, then, is to write the full quoted text of the oath \u8212- both the conditional declaration of innocence (\u8220"if no man has lain with you, be cleansed\u8221") and the curse that follows \u8212- while omitting the narrative instructions embedded in the middle of the {\i pesukim} and the woman\u8217's concluding response of {\i amen amen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether it is problematic to omit the middle section of the {\i pesukim}. This is precisely the concern raised by the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's formulation: you are not cutting {\i within} any one {\i pasuk}, but rather excising what is, in context, parenthetical instruction. The quoted speech continues uninterrupted; only the interposed narrative direction is dropped. That is a natural reading \u8212- you write the words in quote marks, so to speak, and skip the words outside them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} takes a far more restrictive position. {\i Kol atzmo eino kotev ela} \u8212- his entire point is to write nothing other than the curse formula itself: {\i v\u8217'yitein Hashem otach l\u8217'alah u\u8217'lishvuah b\u8217'toch amech, uva\u8217'u ha-mayim ha-marerim ha-eileh b\u8217'me\u8217'ayich} \u8212- may the Lord make you a curse and an oath, and let the waters enter your innards. {\i Rashi} explains {\i kol atzmo} as an idiom indicating that one must be scrupulously careful to write only the curse phrase and nothing beyond it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} agrees with the {\i tanna kamma} that {\i v\u8217'amrah ha-ishah amen amen} is not written. But he goes far further: he also omits the opening conditional \u8212- the clause about being cleansed if innocent \u8212- and writes nothing of the conditional framing of guilt. Only the curse itself goes into the {\i megillah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position is striking in light of a theme that has run throughout the {\i mishnayot}: the {\i sotah} procedure is presented throughout with a presumption of guilt. The process has consistently been framed not as a clarification of her innocence but as the administration of punishment for her transgression. {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's view reflects this most sharply \u8212- the {\i megillah} contains only the curse; there is no trace in the written text of the possibility that she is innocent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue, as was raised in discussion, that {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position is not necessarily about a presumption of guilt, but about a more precise understanding of the {\i mechanics} of the water\u8217's power. The water is the {\i me\u8217'arerim} \u8212- the cursing water. What makes it cursed is the written curse scraped into it. The curse-producing element is specifically the curse text. If she is innocent, the water will not harm her regardless \u8212- so perhaps the rationale is about the function of the {\i megillah} rather than any presumption about her guilt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That said, the objection remains: if one thinks about the {\i power of the verses entering the water}, then the water itself is also conditional \u8212- because the full {\i pasuk} presents a conditional reality. If she is innocent, {\i v\u8217'niktah v\u8217'nizre\u8217'ah zara} \u8212- she will be cleansed and will bear seed. Not including those words means, in a sense, that only the curse element is being placed into the water. This tension is not fully resolved in the {\i mishnah} and awaits the {\i Gemara}\u8217's analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir: Implicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i b\u8217'mai kamifligi} \u8212- what is the source of the disagreement? {\i B\u8217'hai kra kamifligi} \u8212- they dispute the reading of the following {\i pasuk}: {\i v\u8217'katav et ha-alot ha-eileh ha-kohen ba-sefer} \u8212- he shall write these curses in the book.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} \u8212- who is identified as the {\i tanna kamma} \u8212- holds that {\i alot}, the curses, means {\i alot mamash}, the actual curse text. The additional {\i hei} at the beginning \u8212- {\i ha-alot} \u8212- is a {\i ribui}, an expansion: {\i l\u8217'rabot klalot ha-ba\u8217'ot machamat brachot} \u8212- to include the curses that come by way of the blessing. What does this mean? The phrase {\i hinaki mi\u8217'mei ha-marerim} \u8212- be cleansed from the bitter waters \u8212- is ostensibly a blessing. But it implies a curse: if you are not innocent, you will not be cleansed. The {\i hei} of {\i ha-alot} therefore includes even this implicit curse \u8212- the conditional statement of innocence \u8212- within what must be written, because it is the flip side of the curse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then {\i eileh} \u8212- \u8220"these\u8221" \u8212- serves to limit: {\i l\u8217'ma\u8217'utei klalot she\u8217'be-Mishneh Torah} \u8212- to exclude the curses that appear in {\i Parshat Ki Tavo}, which uses the same language of {\i alah}. Those {\i klalot} are not to be included. And {\i ha-eileh}, with the additional {\i hei}, further limits \u8212- {\i l\u8217'ma\u8217'utei tzava\u8217'ot v\u8217'kabalot amen} \u8212- to exclude the instructional portions and the woman\u8217's {\i amen amen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose: No Cutting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yose} accepts the overall framework of the {\i derashah} but extends it in a different direction. He holds that {\i et ha-alot ha-eileh} \u8212- the word {\i et} \u8212- comes to include the instructional text of the {\i pesukim} and the woman\u8217's response of {\i amen amen}. That is, {\i et l\u8217'rabot tzava\u8217'ot v\u8217'kabalot} \u8212- the {\i et} expands what is written to include the entire {\i pasuk} without interruption. His concern, in effect, is that one does not chop a {\i pasuk} in the middle. Starting from the beginning of the oath and including the conclusion \u8212- the woman\u8217's {\i amen amen} \u8212- he writes the entire passage without cutting any portion out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yose}\u8217's position is thus actually broader than the {\i tanna kamma} on the question of the instructional text embedded in the middle. The {\i tanna kamma} cuts out the narrative directions; {\i Rabbi Yose} does not cut anything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Responds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir}, by contrast, is {\i esin lo darish} \u8212- he does not accept {\i et} as a {\i ribui}. He is simply not impressed with the word {\i et} as a basis for including additional content. Therefore, unlike {\i Rabbi Yose}, he does omit the narrative instructions and the {\i amen amen} response.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehudah: Minimal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} takes the same words \u8212- {\i alot}, {\i ha-alot}, {\i eileh}, {\i ha-eileh} \u8212- but reads all of them as {\i mi\u8217'utim}, limitations, rather than expansions. {\i Alot mamash} \u8212- curses, yes, but only the actual curse text. {\i Ha-alot} \u8212- the {\i hei} is not a {\i ribui} but a {\i mi\u8217'ut}: {\i l\u8217'ma\u8217'utei klalot ha-ba\u8217'ot machamat brachot} \u8212- specifically to exclude the conditional innocence clause at the beginning, which is not itself a curse. {\i Eileh} \u8212- to exclude the curses of {\i Mishneh Torah}. {\i Ha-eileh} \u8212- to agree that the narrative instruction and the {\i amen amen} are excluded. Thus {\i Rabbi Yehuda} uses every extra letter to narrow, narrowing the written text to only the curse formula at the end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus presents a spectrum: write everything ({\i Rabbi Yose}), write the full oath but omit the instructions and {\i amen amen} ({\i tanna kamma / Rabbi Meir}), or write only the curse ({\i Rabbi Yehuda}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to consider whether these {\i derashot} reflect genuine hermeneutic differences \u8212- about when a {\i hei} expands versus when it limits, or whether {\i et} carries {\i ribui} \u8212- or whether each {\i tanna} began with a different intuition about what makes sense, and then found the hermeneutic language to support it. One position finds it logically indispensable to include both sides of the conditional; another feels that only the curse need enter the water; a third insists that one does not interrupt a {\i pasuk}. It is an open question whether the hermeneutic generated the conclusion or the conclusion generated the hermeneutic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hei Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} itself asks a sharp question: both {\i ha-alot} and {\i ha-eileh} contain an extra {\i hei} \u8212- yet {\i Rabbi Meir} treats {\i ha-alot} as expansive and {\i ha-eileh} as restrictive. Why does the same letter do opposite work in each location?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: it depends on context. {\i Hei de-gabei de-ribuiya hi} \u8212- when the {\i hei} attaches to a word that is inherently expansive, as {\i alot} is (since you are being told to write the {\i alot}, which implies writing what belongs to them), the {\i hei} expands further, including even the implicit curses at the beginning. {\i Hei de-gabei de-mi\u8217'uta} \u8212- when the {\i hei} attaches to a word that is inherently limiting, as {\i eileh} (\u8220"these\u8221" already restricts), the {\i hei} reinforces the limitation, excluding even the narrative instruction and the {\i amen amen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the end, the dispute distills to four positions about what gets written: the {\i pasuk} in full without excision ({\i Rabbi Yose}); the full oath text minus embedded instruction and the woman\u8217's response ({\i tanna kamma}); the full oath text including the innocence clause, minus instruction and {\i amen amen} ({\i Rabbi Meir}, which on one reading is the same as {\i tanna kamma}); or only the bare curse formula ({\i Rabbi Yehuda}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Inverse Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir & Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a pointed challenge to {\i Rabbi Meir}. {\i Rabbi Meir} holds that the opening conditional clause \u8212- \u8220"if no man has lain with you, be cleansed\u8221" \u8212- is an {\i implicit curse}, and that is why the {\i hei} of {\i ha-alot} includes it. But {\i Rabbi Meir} is famously of the opinion that {\i mi-klal lav attah shomea hen} \u8212- from a negative formulation you cannot infer a positive, and from a positive you cannot infer a negative. In other words, if the {\i pasuk} says \u8220"if you are innocent, be cleansed,\u8221" one cannot automatically infer the inverse: \u8220"if you are guilty, you will not be cleansed.\u8221" Yet that inference is precisely what makes the innocence clause an {\i implicit curse} according to {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is established in the context of {\i tena\u8217'im} \u8212- the formal conditions that must be stated when making stipulations. {\i Rabbi Meir} holds that a {\i tenai kaful} \u8212- a double condition \u8212- is required: you must spell out explicitly both sides of the stipulation. The model is Moshe\u8217's agreement with {\i Bnei Gad u-Bnei Reuven}: {\i im ya\u8217'avru chalutzim} \u8212- if they cross in arms before their brothers and the land is subdued, they receive their portion {\i me-ever la-Yarden}; {\i v\u8217'im lo ya\u8217'avru} \u8212- and if they do not cross, they will settle among you in Canaan. Both sides must be stated, because you cannot logically derive the inverse from a single conditional statement alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, as anyone who has studied formal logic knows, if P then Q does not entail if not-P then not-Q. The statement \u8220"if it rains, we will not play ball\u8221" does not mean \u8220"if it does not rain, we will play ball\u8221" \u8212- it only means that if we did play ball, it cannot have rained. So \u8220"if you are innocent, be cleansed\u8221" does not strictly imply \u8220"if you are guilty, you will be punished.\u8221" You would need that to be stated explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's principle that one cannot infer the inverse applies broadly and universally, or whether it is limited to formal {\i tena\u8217'im} \u8212- where the concern is about the strict legal sufficiency of a stipulation\u8217's formulation. If it is a broad principle, then {\i Rabbi Meir} cannot treat \u8220"be cleansed if innocent\u8221" as implying an inverse curse for guilt, and the whole basis of his {\i derashah} unravels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanchum: Chinaki\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Tanchum} resolves this difficulty with a linguistic observation: {\i chinaki ktiv} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} reads {\i hinaki}, which can be parsed as {\i chinaki}, meaning \u8220"be strangled.\u8221" If you read the conditional clause with that double valence \u8212- if you are innocent, {\i hinaki}, be cleansed; but embedded in the very word is also {\i chinaki}, be strangled \u8212- then the clause is not merely a statement of potential innocence from which a curse must be inferred inversely. The curse is, in a sense, already there within the word itself, as a play on its letters. This makes the clause less {\i implicitly} cursed and more directly so, giving {\i Rabbi Meir} firmer ground to include it as part of the {\i alot} that must be written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva: Fire\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} introduces a celebrated {\i derashah} of {\i Rabbi Akiva}. {\i Ish v\u8217'ishah} \u8212- man and woman. Take the letters of {\i ish}, aleph-yud-shin, and {\i ishah}, aleph-shin-hei. God\u8217's name, {\i yud-hei}, is distributed within the words of man and woman: the {\i yud} in {\i ish}, the {\i hei} in {\i ishah}. {\i Zachu} \u8212- if they merit \u8212- {\i shechina beineihem} \u8212- the divine presence is between them. If they do not merit \u8212- {\i lo zachu} \u8212- {\i esh ochaltan} \u8212- fire consumes them. Remove the {\i yud} and the {\i hei} and what remains in both words is {\i aleph-shin}, {\i esh}, fire.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} explains {\i zachu} in concrete moral terms: {\i lalechet be-derech yesharah} \u8212- to walk on the straight path \u8212- {\i shelo yehei hu no\u8217'ef v\u8217'lo hi no\u8217'efet} \u8212- that he should not be an adulterer and she not an adulteress. This is a striking formulation, because it reintroduces the man as a potential offender on equal terms with the woman. It is worth noting that technically, under {\i halacha}, a man commits adultery only when he sleeps with a {\i married woman}; sleeping with an unmarried woman, while prohibited, is not the technical offense of {\i ni\u8217'uf} against his own wife. Yet {\i Rashi} here seems to read the moral register more broadly: cheating on one\u8217's spouse \u8212- regardless of the technical legal category \u8212- tears the marriage, and then {\i esh ochaltan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: {\i amar Rava, v\u8217'de-ishah adifa mi-de-ish} \u8212- {\i Rava} observes that the woman\u8217's fire is greater than the man\u8217's. In the word {\i ish}, the {\i aleph} and {\i shin} of {\i esh} are separated by the {\i yud} \u8212- God\u8217's letter stands between the fire-letters and keeps them in check. In {\i ishah}, the {\i aleph} and {\i shin} are adjacent \u8212- {\i mitzarfis} \u8212- the fire letters are already joined. {\i Rashi} glosses: {\i esh shel ishah kashah u-mi-maharet le-echol} \u8212- the woman\u8217's fire is fierce and swift to consume. Whether this reflects something about the nature of temptation, the social dynamics of the household when infidelity occurs, or some other concern, the {\i Gemara} does not spell out fully, and the plain meaning remains somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection of this {\i derashah} to the {\i sotah} procedure is worth appreciating. The entire process takes place in the {\i Beit ha-Mikdash}, where God\u8217's presence dwells. God\u8217's name is being written on the parchment and then scraped into the water. If the couple\u8217's relationship has deteriorated to the point that God\u8217's name is not sufficiently present in their marriage to avert this crisis \u8212- that the fire has not been kept in check \u8212- then the procedure is an attempt to bring God\u8217's name more directly into the situation and, through it, resolve the conflict. If she is innocent, the water \u8212- containing the divine name \u8212- douses the fire. If she is guilty, the fire consumes. It is a fitting thematic arc.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dust & Divine Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Merit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava} \u8212- why does the {\i Torah} prescribe bringing {\i afar}, dust, for the {\i sotah}? {\i Zachtah, yotzei mimenah ben k\u8217'Avraham Avinu} \u8212- if she merits, she will bring forth a son like {\i Avraham Avinu}, of whom it is written {\i afar va-efer} \u8212- dust and ashes. {\i Lo zachtah, tachzor la-afarah} \u8212- if she does not merit, she returns to her dust.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to {\i v\u8217'niktah v\u8217'nizre\u8217'ah zara} \u8212- she will be cleansed and bear seed \u8212- is thus given a more specific content: the child she will bear is compared to {\i Avraham Avinu}. And if not, she reverts to dust \u8212- a fitting end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abraham: Merit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with a {\i derashah} of {\i Rava}: because {\i Avraham Avinu} said {\i v\u8217'anochi afar va-efer} \u8212- and I am but dust and ashes \u8212- his descendants merited two {\i mitzvot} connected to {\i afar} and {\i efer}: {\i efer parah} \u8212- the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} \u8212- and {\i afar sotah} \u8212- the dust of the {\i sotah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: but there is also {\i afar kisuy ha-dam} \u8212- dust used to cover blood after {\i shechita} \u8212- which is also a {\i mitzvah} involving dust. The answer: {\i hasam hachsher mitzvah ika, hana\u8217'ah leka} \u8212- there it is merely the performance of a {\i mitzvah}, but there is no concrete benefit. Here, both the {\i efer parah} and the {\i afar sotah} produce tangible benefit \u8212- the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} restore {\i taharah}, and the dust of the {\i sotah} resolves strife between husband and wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derasha: Efer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then records a second {\i derashah} of {\i Rava}: because {\i Avraham} said {\i im mi-chut v\u8217'ad s\u8217'roch na\u8217'al} \u8212- from a thread to a sandal strap, I will take nothing of the spoils of {\i Sdom} \u8212- his descendants merited two {\i mitzvot}: {\i chut shel techelet} \u8212- the thread of {\i techelet} in {\i tzitzit} \u8212- and {\i retzuah shel tefillin} \u8212- the strap of {\i tefillin}, which, like a shoelace, is made of leather.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again the {\i Gemara} requires that these be {\i mitzvot} with concrete this-worldly benefit. {\i Retzuah shel tefillin} \u8212- this is established by the {\i pasuk}: {\i v\u8217'ra\u8217'u kol amei ha-aretz ki shem Hashem nikra alecha v\u8217'yare\u8217'u mimeka} \u8212- all the peoples of the earth will see that God\u8217's name is called upon you, and they will be in awe of you. A {\i baraita} identifies this with the {\i tefillin she-ba-rosh} \u8212- and {\i Rabbi Eliezer ha-Gadol} confirms it. Wearing {\i tefillin} on one\u8217's head causes others to perceive one\u8217's connection to God and generates protective awe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Techelet: Throne\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i ela chut shel techelet mai hi} \u8212- in what way does wearing {\i techelet} produce concrete benefit? {\i Detanya, Rabbi Meir omer}: {\i mah nishtanah techelet mi-kol minei tzivonin} \u8212- what distinguishes {\i techelet} from all other colors? Because {\i techelet} resembles the sea, the sea resembles the sky, and the sky resembles the {\i kise ha-kavod} \u8212- the divine throne. This is supported by two {\i pesukim}: {\i v\u8217'tachtat raglav k\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh livnat ha-sapir u\u8217'che\u8217'etzem ha-shamayim la-tohar} \u8212- and under His feet was like the work of a sapphire brick, like the very essence of the sky in its purity \u8212- and {\i k\u8217'mareh even sapir dmut kise} \u8212- like the appearance of a sapphire stone is the form of the throne. The {\i techelet} thus draws one\u8217's gaze upward \u8212- from sea to sky to throne \u8212- and creates a genuine connection to God, a benefit of a different order than financial gain but concrete and real nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pattern in all these {\i derashot} is consistent: {\i Avraham}\u8217's merit produced {\i mitzvot} for his descendants that carry not merely the reward of performance but a palpable this-worldly effect \u8212- purification, domestic peace, divine awe, and connection to God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Writing Materials\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Surfaces\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i mishnah} addresses the physical requirements for writing the {\i megillah}. The {\i kohen} may not write on a {\i luach} \u8212- {\i Rashi} explains this as a wooden tablet \u8212- nor on {\i neyar}, paper, which {\i Rashi} describes as made from a fibrous material, presumably a papyrus-like substance. Nor may he write on {\i diphera}, which is untreated or incompletely treated hide. Rather he must write on the {\i megillah}, that is, on properly prepared parchment \u8212- {\i klaf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is interesting that the {\i mishnah} uses the word {\i megillah} here rather than {\i klaf}, since the {\i Torah} itself uses the word {\i sefer}. The {\i mishnah} contrasts {\i diphera} with {\i megillah} rather than with {\i klaf} \u8212- the constructed {\i megillah} as an object, not merely the raw parchment. The {\i pasuk} cited is {\i she-ne\u8217'emar ba-sefer} \u8212- for so the {\i Torah} says: in a {\i sefer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ink Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: one may not write using {\i komos} \u8212- {\i Rashi} identifies this as a tree sap \u8212- nor {\i kankantom}, which is copper sulphate, nor with any substance that leaves a permanent mark \u8212- {\i kol davar she-roshem}. The concern is indelibility: if the ink is absorbed into the parchment such that even scraping leaves a residue, the substance is invalid. The ink must sit on the surface of the parchment without being absorbed, so that it can be fully scraped off and dissolved into the water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The valid substance is {\i deyo} \u8212- the standard ink used for {\i Sifrei Torah} and the like, which sits on the surface and can be scraped off cleanly. The {\i pasuk} cited is {\i she-ne\u8217'emar u-machah} \u8212- the {\i Torah} says \u8220"and he erased\u8221" \u8212- requiring {\i ketav she-yachol le-himachot}, writing that can be erased. This implies not merely that the ink can be removed, but that when scraped it leaves nothing behind \u8212- the words must be fully dissolved into the water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Validity Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nighttime Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} rules: {\i megillat sotah she-ketavah ba-laylah pesulah} \u8212- if the {\i megillah} was written at night, it is invalid. The source is a {\i gezerah shavah}: {\i v\u8217'asah lah ha-kohen et kol ha-Torah ha-zot} \u8212- the {\i kohen} shall perform for her all this instruction \u8212- is linked to {\i al pi ha-Torah asher yoru-cha v\u8217'al ha-mishpat} \u8212- according to the instruction they teach you and according to the judgment. Just as judgment must take place during the day \u8212- {\i mishpat ba-yom} \u8212- so too {\i megillat sotah} must be written during the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Backward Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i megillah} was written {\i le-mafrea} \u8212- in reverse order, writing from the end backwards even if the final product reads correctly \u8212- it is invalid. The source: {\i v\u8217'katav et ha-alot ha-eileh} \u8212- write these curses \u8212- meaning write them {\i ke-heichi de-chtivei}, in the order they are written. The {\i pasuk} implies forward sequential writing, not writing that arrives at the correct text by working backwards.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Oath Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i megillah} was written before the woman accepted the oath \u8212- {\i kodem she-tikabel aleha ha-shevuah} \u8212- it is invalid. The source: {\i v\u8217'hishbia} \u8212- he made her swear \u8212- {\i v\u8217'achar kach v\u8217'katav} \u8212- and afterwards he wrote. This is the explicit sequence of the {\i pesukim}, and it must be followed. Notably, the {\i mishnah} itself skipped over the oath-taking entirely and moved directly to the writing, yet the actual {\i halacha} requires that the oath precede the writing. This underscores what {\i Tosafot} will observe: the order of the {\i mishnah} does not determine the {\i halachic} order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Scoring Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ketavah iggeret pesulah} \u8212- if it was written as a letter, it is invalid. {\i Rashi} explains this as writing without {\i shirtut} \u8212- scoring. {\i Sifrei kodesh} are written with scored lines on the parchment; if the {\i megillah} was written without this scoring, it is considered a mere letter and not a {\i sefer}, and therefore invalid. The derivation: {\i ba-sefer amrah Rachmana} \u8212- the {\i Torah} said it must be in a {\i sefer}, meaning with the formal requirements of {\i sifrei kodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Parchments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i megillah} was written on two separate pieces of parchment \u8212- say, the text did not fit on one piece \u8212- it is invalid: {\i sefer echad amrah Rachmana, v\u8217'lo shenayim u-sheloshah sefarim} \u8212- the {\i Torah} required one book, not two or three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intermittent Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, if the {\i kohen} wrote one letter and scraped it off, wrote the next letter and scraped it off \u8212- so that the entire text was never present on the parchment at one time \u8212- this is invalid: {\i d\u8217'chtiv v\u8217'asah lah ha-kohen et kol ha-Torah ha-zot} \u8212- the entire text must be present at once before it is scraped into the water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefer Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises an extensive question about the ruling that writing on a {\i luach} or {\i neyar} is invalid. In other halachic contexts \u8212- for example, a {\i get} \u8212- the {\i Torah} uses the language of {\i v\u8217'katav lah sefer keritut}, and the rabbis derive from the word {\i sefer} there that one may write a {\i get} on virtually any surface, even a cow\u8217's horn. Why, then, is the {\i megillat sotah} restricted to parchment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction {\i Tosafot} draws is the preposition {\i bet}. By the {\i sotah}, the {\i Torah} says {\i ba-sefer} \u8212- in a book \u8212- which places the emphasis on the physical object itself. The {\i megillah} must be a {\i sefer} in the full, physically realized sense. By the {\i get}, the {\i Torah} says {\i sefer keritut} \u8212- a document of severance \u8212- and the word {\i sefer} there is taken by the rabbis as {\i sefirat devarim be\u8217'alma}, a mere recounting of separation, emphasizing the content rather than the physical form. Hence the broad permissiveness for writing surfaces by a {\i get} but strict limitation to {\i klaf} by the {\i megillat sotah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Separately, the {\i Yerushalmi} raises the question of whether parchment from a non-kosher animal may be used. One consideration there is: if the woman refuses to drink and the {\i megillah} is not erased, you would be leaving God\u8217's name written on parchment from a non-kosher animal, which is unseemly. The {\i Yerushalmi} also asks whether the {\i megillah}, before being erased, has {\i kedushah} \u8212- does it convey impurity to the hands the way sacred {\i sifrei kodesh} do? The tentative answer is that it may not, because it was written for the purpose of being erased, which may deprive it of the standing of a sacred {\i sefer}. These remain interesting questions about the status of an object whose nature is fundamentally transitional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the matter of the nighttime invalidation, {\i Tosafot} addresses what type of judgment {\i sotah} resembles. {\i Dinei mamonot} \u8212- monetary cases \u8212- must begin during the day but may conclude at night. {\i Dinei nefashot} \u8212- capital cases \u8212- must be conducted entirely during the day, beginning and end. {\i Tosafot} rules that {\i sotah} resembles {\i dinei nefashot}: {\i kol din sotah, bein gmar bein techilah, pasul ba-laylah} \u8212- every aspect of the {\i sotah} process, whether beginning or completion, is invalid if performed at night. This framing of {\i sotah} as analogous to {\i dinei nefashot} is significant: it reflects the understanding that what is being adjudicated is, in effect, a capital matter. If found guilty, the woman will die \u8212- and {\i Chazal} understand this even though the {\i pesukim} do not state death explicitly. Treating it as {\i dinei nefashot} is therefore consistent with that thematic reading of the entire procedure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scoring Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then turns to the requirement of {\i shirtut}. Why does the {\i pasuk} need to tell us that writing without scoring invalidates the {\i megillah}? There is already a rule elsewhere \u8212- derived at the opening of {\i Gittin} \u8212- that one may not write even three words from a {\i pasuk} without scoring, even in the context of ordinary correspondence. Given that this is already a general prohibition, surely the {\i megillat sotah} must also be written with scoring. What novel information does the {\i pasuk} add?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}\u8217's answer: the existing requirement establishes a prohibition on writing without scoring, but a prohibition does not automatically mean that an object produced in violation of that prohibition is halachically invalid. One might have violated the rule and yet produced a valid {\i megillah}. The {\i pasuk} comes to tell us that here, the absence of scoring is not merely a procedural violation \u8212- it renders the {\i megillah} itself {\i pesulah} as an object. The formality of {\i ba-sefer} is {\i me\u8217'akev}, not merely {\i l\u8217'chatchilah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then develops an extended analysis of when {\i shirtut} is required and when it is not, working through the case of {\i tefillin}, where the {\i pesukim} are written without the full scoring applied to a {\i Sefer Torah}. {\i Tosafot} proposes distinguishing between two types of scoring: a perimeter score around the entire section, which satisfies the general prohibition against writing three words of a {\i pasuk} without any scored border, and the line-by-line scoring required for full formal {\i sifrei kodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the conclusion, {\i Tosafot} cites a tradition in the name of {\i Tosafot Rabbeinu} from {\i Masechet Gittin}, transmitted in the name of {\i Rabbi Eliyahu ha-Tzarfati}: when one quotes the language of a {\i pasuk} in correspondence not as a direct citation of a {\i pasuk} but as flowery Hebrew expression \u8212- {\i amira be\u8217'alma lifrosh b\u8217'shalom} \u8212- using the words as literary idiom rather than as scripture, there is no obligation of {\i shirtut}. But if one is writing a letter and explicitly cites a {\i pasuk} as such \u8212- \u8220"as the {\i pasuk} says\u8230 ?\u8221" followed by the quoted text \u8212- then {\i ki hai gavna assur le-michtav belo shirtut}, one may not write it without scoring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then adds its own question \u8212- and notably does not resolve it: {\i v\u8217'lo yadana minan li-michtav be-peirushin pesukim belo shirtut} \u8212- we do not know the source permitting the writing of {\i pesukim} in commentaries without {\i shirtut}, in cases where the {\i pasuk} is being brought as an explicit citation. Commentaries on {\i Torah} and {\i Gemara} routinely cite {\i pesukim} explicitly as {\i pesukim} without performing {\i shirtut}. How is this permitted? {\i Tosafot} leaves the question open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel question concerns quoting partial {\i pesukim} \u8212- whether citing only a portion of a {\i pasuk}, particularly when that portion includes {\i shem Hashem}, is permissible. {\i Rav Ovadia Yosef} addresses this in a {\i teshuvah} and concludes that the {\i Gemara} itself quotes partial {\i pesukim} containing {\i shem Hashem} without concern, demonstrating that the rule of {\i kol pasuk de-lo paskei Moshe} \u8212- not dividing a {\i pasuk} \u8212- applies specifically to a ritual recitation context, not to learning, discussion, or citation in analysis. The {\i megillat sotah} itself, as a ritual act, might be governed by that stricter standard, while ordinary Torah discourse is not. But the question of {\i shirtut} in correspondence and commentary remains, as {\i Tosafot} acknowledges, unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 18\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketivah & Mechika Lishmah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Scrolls, One Vessel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a question posed by Rava ({\i ba\u8217'ei Rava}) concerning the practical mechanics of the {\i sotah} ritual. Suppose a {\i kohen} is processing two {\i sotot} simultaneously and writes two {\i megillot} \u8212- one for each woman \u8212- and then scrapes them both into a single vessel. May both women drink from that one vessel?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Individual Process Requi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying issue is the requirement of {\i ketivah lishmah} \u8212- that the scroll must be written specifically for her, as the verse states {\i vechatav lah hakohen}, \u8220"and the priest shall write for her.\u8221" This requirement has already been established: the scroll cannot be pre-written, and it must be composed after she takes the oath. The {\i ketivah lishmah} requirement is clearly satisfied here, since each scroll was written individually for its respective woman. But the Gemara raises a further possibility: perhaps {\i mechika lishmah} is also required \u8212- that the act of scraping must itself be individualized for each woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern with simultaneous scraping is that even if one\u8217's knife moved across one woman\u8217's parchment for her sake and across the other woman\u8217's parchment for hers, the overall {\i process} of producing the bitter waters was not focused on one individual woman. Rather, it was a single process being conducted for two women at once. Whether {\i lishmah} requires that the action be understood as distinctly oriented toward a single individual \u8212- not merely labeled in one\u8217's mind \u8212- is the substance of the question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Waters Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then extends the question through a series of progressive scenarios. Suppose one concedes that {\i mechika lishmah} is required, and proceeds correctly: each woman\u8217's scroll is scraped into its own separate vessel. Now, however, the contents of those two vessels are poured together into one. The {\i ketivah} and {\i mechika} were each performed properly and individually, but the resulting waters are now commingled. Is that a problem?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara formulates this concern sharply: {\i hai lav didah ka shatya v\u8217'hai lav didah ka shatya} \u8212- each woman is drinking water that is not exclusively hers. The question is whether drinking requires that the water be precisely the water prepared for that individual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Breira & Re-division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara carries the inquiry one step further: suppose after mixing the waters, one then re-divides them into two separate vessels. Is this valid? The Gemara invokes the concept of {\i breira} \u8212- the legal principle of retroactive determination. Do we say that upon re-division, each vessel is deemed to contain the water that was originally hers? Or do we say {\i ein breira}, and the re-division accomplishes nothing, since the waters cannot magically self-separate along their original lines?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes all three sub-questions with {\i teiku} \u8212- the matter remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note how peculiar it would be to invoke {\i breira} in the conventional sense here. Normally, {\i breira} concerns situations where something was never distinctly identified at the outset and we ask retroactively which portion was always meant to belong to a particular party \u8212- such as brothers dividing an inherited estate, or grain designated as {\i terumah} only after the fact. In those cases, nothing was mixed; rather, something existed in an undifferentiated state and was eventually assigned. But here, two separately prepared, distinguishable waters were fully merged. Saying that upon re-division each woman receives precisely her original water seems to invoke not {\i breira} in the usual halakhic sense but something approaching outright magic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Breira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this difficulty directly and at length. Tosafot begins by noting that ordinary {\i breira} cannot mean that a fully diffused mixture retroactively resolves back into its original components. If that were possible, one could solve any case of {\i taarovet} \u8212- say, {\i terumah} wine mixed into {\i chullin} wine \u8212- simply by pouring into two vessels and declaring that the {\i terumah} went into one of them. Clearly that is untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot therefore reframes the Gemara\u8217's question. The real concern with commingling the waters, Tosafot argues, is not that each woman happens to drink water that belongs to another woman. Drinking someone else\u8217's water is not itself the problem. Rather, when the two separately prepared waters are merged into a single vessel from which both women drink, the two distinct ritual processes are effectively collapsed into one. The individuality of each woman\u8217's {\i sotah} ordeal is dissolved when everything ends in a single common cup.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is the correct framing, then what the Gemara means when it asks about {\i yesh breira} after re-division is not whether the waters physically self-separate along their original lines \u8212- that is impossible and Tosafot explicitly rejects it \u8212- but rather whether re-dividing into separate vessels restores the appearance and legal reality of two distinct processes. Once each woman is drinking from her own cup \u8212- a cup dedicated exclusively to her, in which only she is drinking \u8212- the {\i mechika} performed for her and the {\i ketivah} written for her can again be understood as a complete, individualized ritual, even if some of the other woman\u8217's water happens to be admixed within. The re-division may be sufficient to reconstitute the distinctiveness of the process in each woman\u8217's case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot acknowledges that this reading is difficult and the full formulation remains complex, but the thrust is: {\i yesh breira} in this context means not physical self-selection but rather that re-division re-establishes the ritual distinctiveness that the commingling had disrupted. The Gemara, notably, frames all of this in purely halakhic terms \u8212- whether the process was conducted {\i for her} \u8212- rather than in terms of any metaphysical power inhering in the waters themselves.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Drinking Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Straw/Sieve Drinking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava poses an additional question: {\i hashka besiv} \u8212- what is the law if the woman drank through a fiber, or through a straw ({\i bishfoferet})? Is that a valid mode of drinking? The operative question is {\i miderech shtiah bekach o ein derech shtiah bekach} \u8212- is this a normal manner of drinking or not? The Gemara concludes {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes a parallel case discussed elsewhere in the Talmud regarding eating {\i matzah} when it is wrapped in fiber ({\i karchah besiv}) \u8212- whether that constitutes valid {\i achilah}. However, Tosafot points out that the cases are distinguishable: when food is encased in something, the casing interposes between the food and the throat, raising a question of {\i chatzitzah}. By contrast, a straw or fiber tube does not actually surround the liquid as it passes down the throat; it merely conveys the liquid from cup to mouth. The question here is therefore more genuinely whether drinking via a conveyance is {\i derech shtiah} \u8212- the normal manner of drinking as envisioned by the Torah \u8212- rather than whether there is an interposition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Aruch} is cited with an interpretation that sheds further light. According to the {\i Aruch}, the fiber ({\i siv}) was placed into the water, absorbed it like a sponge, and then the saturated fiber was brought to her mouth, from which she sucked the water out. This makes the question considerably more compelling, since it is far less obvious that sucking water from an absorbent material constitutes {\i derech shtiah} in the manner of the verse {\i vehishka et hamayim} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall make her drink the water.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spilled Water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises a final question: {\i nishpechu mehen venishtayru mehen} \u8212- what if some of the water spilled and she drank only what remained? The Gemara again concludes {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Oaths & Curse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repeated Oath in Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira, citing Rav, raises the question: {\i shtei shevuot ha\u8217'amurot beSotah lamah} \u8212- why does the Torah mention the making of an oath twice in the {\i sotah} passage? The structure of the relevant verses is as follows: the first instance of {\i vehishbia otah hakohen} introduces the conditional formulation \u8212- if you have not gone astray you will be cleared, and if you have gone astray\u8230 ? \u8212- without completing the consequence. The second instance of {\i vehishbia hakohen et ha\u8217'ishah bishvuat ha\u8217'alah} then introduces the explicit curse: God shall make you an {\i alah} and an oath among your people, the water shall enter your belly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought that one oath precedes the scraping of the scroll and the other follows it. But Rava objects: both oaths as they appear in the text clearly precede the scraping. That interpretation is therefore untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath with/without Alah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore offers an alternative: one instance refers to a {\i shvuah she\u8217'yesh imah alah} \u8212- an oath accompanied by a curse \u8212- and the other to a {\i shvuah she\u8217'ein imah alah} \u8212- an oath without a curse. The first, more conditional presentation is an oath without an explicit curse, while the second invokes the full force of the {\i alah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defining Shvuah Sheyesh\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then struggles to define precisely what constitutes a {\i shvuah she\u8217'yesh imah alah}. Rav Abba citing Rav offers: {\i mashpiani alayich she-lo nitmeis she-im nitmeis yavo\u8217'u bach} \u8212- I am making you swear that you did not sin, because if you sinned the water will come upon you. The curse functions as the motivating consequence of the oath \u8212- swear truthfully and understand that if you are lying, this is the result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects that this construction leaves the curse as a separate, parallel element: the {\i alah} stands on its own and the {\i shvuah} stands on its own, but the oath does not actually incorporate the curse. Rava therefore proposes: {\i mashpiani alayich she-im nitmeis yavo\u8217'u bach} \u8212- I am making you swear that if you have sinned the waters will come upon you. But Rav Ashi counters: that formulation contains a curse but where is the oath? She is merely being told what will happen; she is not attesting to anything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi ultimately proposes: {\i mashpiani alayich she-lo nitmeis v\u8217'im nitmeis yavo\u8217'u bach} \u8212- I am making you swear that you did not sin, and if you sinned let the waters come upon you. The precise distinction between this and Rav Abba\u8217's original formulation is not entirely transparent, and the Gemara acknowledges the difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Two Oaths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi offers a clarifying framework. In Rashi\u8217's reading, the {\i shvuah} of the woman is exclusively her attestation to the truth of her declaration: {\i mashpiani alayich she-yehai emes mah she-amart lefaneinu she-lo nitmeis} \u8212- I am making you swear that what you have said before us \u8212- that you did not sin \u8212- is true. That is the {\i shvuah} in the strict sense: the woman swears to the veracity of her own statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second element \u8212- {\i v\u8217'im nitmeis yavo\u8217'u bach mayim} \u8212- is the {\i alah}, the curse, which the {\i kohen} calls upon to take effect upon the woman if she is lying. Rashi understands this as the {\i kohen} forswearing the curse to do its duty \u8212- invoking the {\i alah} as one might invoke witnesses or heavenly forces \u8212- in the manner of {\i ha\u8217'azinu hashamayim va\u8217'adabera}, calling upon heaven and earth as witnesses. The {\i kohen} is not administering the curse as an addendum; he is actively summoning it into its role. Thus there are genuinely two distinct acts: the woman\u8217's oath attesting to her truthfulness, and the {\i kohen}\u8217's invocation of the curse to operate upon her if her oath is false. The {\i shvuah} and the {\i alah} are separate in nature but bound together in the ceremony.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Amen Amen & Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Amen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states that the woman responds {\i amen amen} \u8212- the double affirmation. The Gemara explains: {\i amen al ha-alah, amen al ha-shvuah} \u8212- one {\i amen} accepts the {\i alah} and one {\i amen} affirms the {\i shvuah}. That is, with one {\i amen} she declares: yes, I accept the consequence \u8212- let the water come upon me if I have lied. With the other she declares: yes, I am swearing that I am telling the truth. Rashi accordingly reads {\i amen shelo nitmesi} as {\i kabbalas shvuah} \u8212- acceptance of the oath \u8212- and {\i amen v\u8217'im nitmesi yavo\u8217'u vi} as {\i kabbalas alah} \u8212- acceptance of the curse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i gilgul shvuah} is also operative here. Once a party has been validly brought before the court and compelled to take an oath on some matter, additional oaths may be attached. Consequently, since the husband is entitled to compel her oath regarding the specific man of suspicion, the court can also extend the oath to cover any man \u8212- she is swearing to her fidelity broadly, not only with respect to the particular individual named in the {\i kinui}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Men & Periods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah further specifies: {\i amen me\u8217'ish zeh, amen me\u8217'ish acher} \u8212- one {\i amen} affirming she did not sin with this man, and one {\i amen} affirming she did not sin with any other man. The {\i sotah} ritual thus functions as an occasion to extend the scope of the oath well beyond the specific suspected incident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusah & Nesuah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states: {\i amen shelo satisi arusah unesua\u8217'ah} \u8212- she says {\i amen} that she did not commit adultery when she was an {\i arusah} and when she was a {\i nesuah}. The husband can ordinarily initiate the {\i sotah} process only during the period of {\i nisuin}, but since the oath is being administered, its scope may extend retroactively to her period of {\i eirusin} as well. Were she to have committed adultery during {\i eirusin}, she would have been forbidden to him; therefore the oath may encompass that period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomeret Yevam Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah adds: {\i veshomeret yevama mekunesah} \u8212- the oath may also extend to conduct during her period as a {\i shomeret yavam}, after her first husband died and before the {\i yavam} performed {\i yibbum}. This raises an immediate difficulty: it is not at all obvious that a {\i shomeret yavam} who sleeps with another man is thereby forbidden to the {\i yavam}. The prohibition of {\i lo tihiyeh eshes hameit hachutzah le\u8217'ish zar} is an {\i issur lav}, a negative prohibition, but that does not automatically mean that such conduct renders her {\i asurah} to the brother-in-law in the way that adultery renders a woman forbidden to her husband. The Mishnah\u8217's inclusion of this case implies that it does \u8212- and the Gemara will interrogate that implication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah concludes with the principle: {\i zeh klal, kol she-bi-ybeah lo haytah asurah lo, lo hayah matneh imah} \u8212- any circumstance in which intercourse with another man would not render her forbidden to him is outside the scope of the oath. The converse likewise holds: wherever such intercourse would render her forbidden, that period falls within the scope of the oath.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shomeret Yevam Forbidden?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hamnuna Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna draws a significant halakhic conclusion from the structure of the Mishnah: {\i shomeret yavam she-zintah asurah li-yvamah} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibbum} who commits adultery is forbidden to the {\i yavam}. His argument is straightforward: the Mishnah allows the {\i yavam} to extend the oath to cover her conduct as a {\i shomeret yavam}. But the Mishnah also states explicitly that oath extension is only possible where intercourse would have rendered her forbidden. The only way both clauses can coexist is if sleeping with another man as a {\i shomeret yavam} does in fact render her forbidden to the {\i yavam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 EY Disagreement & Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages of Eretz Yisrael, however, rule otherwise: {\i leis hilkheta ke-Rav Hamnuna}. Despite acknowledging that Rav Hamnuna\u8217's reading of the Mishnah is compelling as {\i peshat}, they reject his conclusion. Their reasoning: the Mishnah\u8217's inclusion of the {\i shomeret yavam} case reflects a minority opinion. {\i Ha manni Rabbi Akiva hi} \u8212- this is the position of Rabbi Akiva.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Rav Hamnuna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva holds a sweeping general principle: {\i ein kiddushin topsin be-chayavei lavin} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} is not binding when contracted with one who is prohibited by a negative commandment. Moreover, the offspring of such a union is a {\i mamzer}. Under Rabbi Akiva\u8217's framework, any prohibition at the level of {\i issur lav} is treated with extraordinary legal weight: not merely does one violate the prohibition, but the relationship itself is legally unrecognized, the {\i kiddushin} is void, and the child bears the status of {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied to {\i shomeret yavam}: the prohibition {\i lo tihiyeh eshes hameit hachutzah le\u8217'ish zar} is an {\i issur lav}, and under Rabbi Akiva\u8217's general approach any relationship entered into in violation of a {\i lav} is treated as if it were a prohibited union in the full sense \u8212- {\i kiddushin} does not bind, and the offspring is a {\i mamzer}. It is this general position of Rabbi Akiva that explains why, in his view, a {\i shomeret yavam} who sleeps with another man would be forbidden to the {\i yavam}: not because {\i yibbum} constitutes a quasi-marriage in some special sense, but because Rabbi Akiva weights every {\i issur lav} so heavily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna\u8217's approach, by contrast, was not premised on Rabbi Akiva\u8217's general principle. He argued from the distinctive nature of {\i yibbum} itself: the {\i zikah} \u8212- the halakhic bond connecting the widow to the brother \u8212- is a form of quasi-marriage, and betrayal of that bond is akin to adultery. That is why, in his view, she is forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages of Eretz Yisrael make a crucial distinction: the Mishnah\u8217's extension of the oath to the {\i shomeret yavam} case is not a universally held position. It reflects Rabbi Akiva\u8217's particular view. We do not rule like Rabbi Akiva here, and therefore a {\i shomeret yavam} who sleeps with another man is not, in the view of the majority, rendered forbidden to the {\i yavam}. The {\i kiddushin} of another man with her would be binding, she does not produce a {\i mamzer}, and she remains eligible for {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that there is no other case of an {\i issur lav} that creates the kind of relational context in which \u8220"sleeping with another man\u8221" could constitute something analogous to adultery. The only two legal frameworks in which a woman\u8217's sexual conduct with another man impinges upon an existing relational obligation are {\i nisuin} and {\i shomeret yavam}. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position about {\i chayavei lavin} is thus distinctively operative precisely in the {\i shomeret yavam} case \u8212- not because {\i yibbum} is uniquely quasi-marital, but because Rabbi Akiva\u8217's principle is general, and {\i shomeret yavam} happens to be the only {\i issur lav} context in which the question of such betrayal can even arise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath Scope Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous & Later Marriag\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiah raises a further pair of questions regarding the extension of the {\i sotah} oath. First: {\i mahu she-yatne adam al nisuin harishonim} \u8212- can a man stipulate that the oath cover conduct during a previous marriage between them? That is, if a man divorced his wife and then remarried her, and now in the second marriage she is undergoing the {\i sotah} process, can he extend the oath to encompass any adultery she may have committed during the first marriage? Second: {\i al nisuei achiv} \u8212- can a {\i yavam} who performed {\i yibbum} extend the oath to cover conduct during the {\i arusah} period of the deceased brother\u8217's marriage, when that deceased brother was the husband?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These questions should be understood in light of the Mishnah\u8217's concluding principle: oath extension is permissible only where intercourse would have rendered her forbidden. Both scenarios above would satisfy that condition. Were she to have committed adultery during their first marriage, she would currently be forbidden to him in the second marriage. Were she to have committed adultery during the period when she was married to the deceased brother, she would have been ineligible for {\i yibbum}; the {\i yavam} could not have married her at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden = Oath Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves Rabbi Yirmiah\u8217's questions by citing the Mishnah\u8217's general rule: {\i kol shetiba\u8217'el velo te\u8217'aser lo, lo hayah matneh imah} \u8212- wherever intercourse would not forbid her to him, he cannot stipulate; the logical contrapositive confirms that wherever it would forbid her, he may stipulate. Since in both of Rabbi Yirmiah\u8217's cases the conduct in question would render her forbidden in the relevant relational context, the oath extension is permitted. {\i Shema mina} \u8212- this may be derived from the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One may also note a subtle point regarding the word {\i matneh}. Throughout this discussion, when the Gemara refers to the extension of the oath beyond its immediate default scope, it consistently uses the language of {\i matneh} \u8212- stipulation, active extension. This implies that such extensions are not automatic. The {\i yavam} or husband must explicitly extend the oath if he wishes it to apply to these additional circumstances; they do not take effect without such stipulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Marriage Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi poses the converse case: {\i mahu she-yatne adam al nisuin ha\u8217'achronim} \u8212- can a man, in the context of Rabbi Meir\u8217's position that the oath can be future-oriented, stipulate that the oath cover conduct during a potential second marriage between them, if they were to divorce and remarry? The argument in favor: if she were to commit adultery after they remarried, she would be forbidden to him in that second marriage. The argument against: in the context of the current marriage, that future adultery does not render her presently forbidden. {\i Hashta meiha lo asira lei} \u8212- right now she is not forbidden to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this from the Mishnah\u8217's language as well. The Mishnah specifies that he cannot stipulate about the period of divorce, but does not say he cannot stipulate about a future remarriage. By implication, stipulating about conduct during a potential remarriage is permissible \u8212- just as he may stipulate about conduct during a prior marriage \u8212- since in both cases, were she to sin at those times, she would be forbidden to him in that marital context. The symmetry is exact: past first marriage and future second marriage are treated equivalently. {\i Shema mina}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Double Jeopardy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Jeopardy Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} is introduced: {\i zot torat hakanot} \u8212- \u8220"this is the law of jealousy.\u8221" The {\i tana kama} reads {\i zot torat} as an expansive formulation: {\i melamed she-isha shota ve-shona} \u8212- a woman may be made to drink the waters twice. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: {\i zot} is a limiting word, implying {\i she\u8217'einah shota ve-shona} \u8212- she may not be made to drink a second time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} introduce a third position: {\i ein isha shota ve-shona bein be-ish echad bein bishnei anashim} \u8212- a woman never undergoes the ordeal a second time, whether the second instance involves the same husband or a different one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus the full spectrum: the {\i tana kama} permits a second ordeal even with the same husband; Rabbi Yehuda permits it with a different husband but not the same; the {\i Chachamim} prohibit a second ordeal altogether, regardless of the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nechunya Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda reports: {\i maaseh ve-heid lefaneinu Nechunya Chofer Shichin} \u8212- Nechunya the Digger of Wells testified before us that a woman may drink twice \u8212- but this was accepted only {\i bishnei anashim}, with two different husbands, not with the same husband. Tosafot cite a {\i Yerushalmi} in which a woman named Korkemit actually underwent the ordeal three times before Shemaya and Avtalyon \u8212- evidence that the ritual did occur historically, and that the debate about double jeopardy reflects a genuine and consequential disagreement. Whether Nechunya\u8217's testimony was to a received halakhic tradition or to something he personally witnessed is left open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's presentation of three positions \u8212- unlimited repetition, repetition with a different husband only, and no repetition at all \u8212- remains to be analyzed further in the discussion that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 19\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Sotah Tests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah vs.\u160 ?Zot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by revisiting a {\i braita} that was quoted at the close of 18b. The {\i braita} begins with the phrase {\i zot torat ha-kena\u8217'ot} \u8212- \u8220"this is the {\i torah} of jealousies\u8221" \u8212- and the interpretive tension is immediately apparent: the word {\i torah} is an expansive, inclusive term, while {\i zot} is a narrowing, limiting one. These two words pull in opposite directions, and it is precisely this tension that generates the three-way dispute among the {\i tanna\u8217'im}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} fastens onto the word {\i torah} to allow for multiple occurrences of the {\i sotah} procedure. That is, a woman who has been suspected, warned before witnesses, and made to drink the bitter waters could, if later suspected again under fresh circumstances, be subjected to the entire process a second time. {\i Torah} signals multiplicity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} counters by emphasizing the exclusive force of {\i zot}: {\i she-en isha shota ve-shona} \u8212- a woman cannot be made to undergo the procedure twice. There is no double jeopardy. He does, however, qualify this position based on testimony. The {\i braita} records that {\i Nechunya Chofre Shichin} \u8212- Nechunya the ditch-digger \u8212- testified before the sages that a woman could in fact be made to drink a second time, and this testimony was accepted, but only in a limited context: {\i bi-shne anashim} \u8212- if it involves two different husbands. {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's reasoning is that when the same husband suspects his wife a second time, the problem may lie more with the husband than with the woman. But if an entirely different husband brings the accusation, that represents a genuinely different set of circumstances, and the procedure may be repeated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chakhamim Rejection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chakhamim} \u8212- identified in the {\i braita} as {\i Rabbanan Batrai}, the later sages \u8212- take a more sweeping position: {\i en isha shota ve-shona ben be-ish echad ben bi-shne anashim} \u8212- a woman can never be made to drink again, whether the suspicion comes from the same husband or from two different ones. This represents a rejection even of the testimony of {\i Nechunya}, and applies across the board.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is not content to leave the dispute as a simple three-way disagreement, because the positions must account for both textual signals \u8212- the inclusive {\i torat} and the exclusive {\i zot}. The {\i gemara} therefore presses: does the {\i tanna kamma}, who emphasizes {\i torat}, have no use for {\i zot}? And conversely, does the {\i Rabbanan Batrai}, who effectively reads the law as prohibiting a second procedure in virtually every case, have no use for {\i torat}? These questions prompt {\i Rava} to offer a comprehensive analytical framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identical Circumstances\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} maps out the full logical space of possibilities. At one extreme: {\i be-ish echad u-vo\u8217'el echad} \u8212- the same husband, and the same individual suspected as the adulterer. Here everything is perfectly identical. The husband was unsatisfied with the first result and wishes to repeat the process against the same person. This is pure double jeopardy in the strictest sense, and {\i de-khule alma lo pligi} \u8212- all opinions agree that this is prohibited. The word {\i zot} performs its exclusionary function precisely here: this case of absolute identity is excluded by all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Different\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the other extreme: {\i bi-shne anashim u-shne bo\u8217'alim} \u8212- two different husbands, and a different suspected adulterer in each instance. Here every relevant circumstance has changed. The woman has never been tested with respect to this husband or this suspected paramour; nothing about the prior proceeding is being relitigated. Here too all opinions agree \u8212- but in the opposite direction: {\i de-isha shota ve-shona} \u8212- she may undergo the procedure again. The word {\i torat} achieves its inclusive purpose precisely in this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute among the three {\i tanna\u8217'im} therefore concerns only the two intermediate cases: one husband who suspects his wife of having conducted herself inappropriately with two different men ({\i ish echad u-shne bo\u8217'alim}), and two different husbands who each suspect their wife of involvement with the same individual ({\i shne anashim u-vo\u8217'el echad}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} adopts the most permissive position. He reads {\i torat} as broadly inclusive \u8212- {\i lerabbuye kulhu} \u8212- encompassing virtually all cases. The word {\i zot} serves only to exclude the one scenario on which everyone agrees: identical husband, identical suspected adulterer. Everything else \u8212- both middle cases included \u8212- is permitted. The {\i tanna kamma} is the maximalist here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Batrai Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan Batrai} adopt the inverse approach. {\i Zot limute kulhu} \u8212- the exclusive term excludes virtually everything \u8212- both middle cases are prohibited. The word {\i torat} serves only to include the one scenario on which all agree: different husband, different suspected adulterer. Everything short of total difference in circumstances is excluded. The {\i Rabbanan Batrai} are the minimalists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} stakes out the middle position with elegant symmetry: {\i zot limutei tartei, torah lirboyei tartei} \u8212- {\i zot} excludes two cases, and {\i torat} includes two cases. The two excluded cases share a single variable: the same husband. Whether that husband is suspicious of the same man or a different man, if it is the same husband a second time, the procedure cannot be repeated. The two included cases share the opposite variable: different husbands. Whether those two husbands suspect her of involvement with the same man or different men, the fact of a different husband constitutes a genuinely different set of circumstances, and the procedure may be repeated. This makes intuitive sense as well: since the {\i sotah} procedure is fundamentally about the wife\u8217's relationship with her husband, it follows that cases involving the same husband form a natural category of exclusion, while cases involving a different husband form a natural category of inclusion \u8212- even if the suspected adulterer happens to be the same person.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Menachah Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Takes It\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i perek} opens with the {\i mishnah} describing the ritual steps of the {\i sotah}\u8217's {\i mincha} offering. The {\i mishnah} states that \u8220"he\u8221" would take the {\i mincha} from a wicker basket ({\i kefifah mitzrit}) and place it into a {\i kli shareis} \u8212- a sanctified temple vessel \u8212- and then place it upon her hand. The identity of the \u8220"he\u8221" is disputed. {\i Rashi} understands this to refer to the husband, reasoning that until the offering is placed into a {\i kli shareis} it has not yet entered the domain of the {\i kohen}\u8217's responsibility. {\i Tosafot}, by contrast, argues that this must refer to the {\i kohen} himself, since the {\i pasuk} speaks of the {\i kohen} taking it from her hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the {\i mincha} is placed into the {\i kli shareis}, it becomes sanctified. It is then returned to the woman\u8217's hands, so that she holds the now-sanctified offering. At this point, the {\i kohen} places his hands underneath hers \u8212- {\i maniach yado tachat yadah} \u8212- preparing for the waving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Steps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} enumerates the steps of the {\i mincha} procedure. First, the {\i kohen} waves the offering ({\i tenufah}), with his hands underneath and her hands on top, as a single joint act. Second, he brings it close to the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} ({\i hagashah}). Third, he takes the {\i kometz} \u8212- a handful of the offering. Fourth, he burns the {\i kometz} on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} ({\i haktarah}). Fifth, the remainder ({\i shirayim}) is eaten by the {\i kohanim} in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. These five steps \u8212- {\i waving, touching the altar, taking the handful, burning, and the remainder being eaten} \u8212- constitute the full procedure for every {\i mincha}, and the {\i sotah}\u8217's offering is no exception.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Order Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having described the steps of the {\i mincha}, the {\i mishnah} now turns to the question of order: does the drinking precede the {\i mincha}, or does the {\i mincha} precede the drinking? The {\i tanna kamma} holds that first she drinks and only then is the {\i mincha} brought. The understanding implicit in this position is that once the {\i mincha} is subsequently brought, it triggers or activates the effect of the bitter waters that she has already consumed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon} reverses the order: {\i makriv et minchatah ve-achar kach mashkeh} \u8212- first the {\i mincha}, then the drinking. And {\i Rabbi Shimon} does not rest on inference alone; he cites an explicit {\i pasuk}: {\i ve-achar yashkeh et ha-ishah et ha-mayim} \u8212- \u8220"and afterwards he shall cause the woman to drink the waters.\u8221" The word {\i ve-achar}, \u8220"afterwards,\u8221" appears after the verses describing the {\i mincha} procedure, making the sequence clear \u8212- or so it would seem. The {\i pasuk} appears to place the drinking explicitly after the {\i mincha} offering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite his clear preference, {\i Rabbi Shimon} concedes that if the order was reversed \u8212- if she drank first and the {\i mincha} was brought afterward \u8212- the {\i mincha} is nonetheless {\i kesherah} ({\i be-di\u8217'avad}, after the fact). The procedure is valid retroactively. This concession, as we will see, becomes important when the {\i gemara} tries to understand what {\i Rabbi Shimon} does with the earlier {\i pasuk} that seems to place the drinking first.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Waving Process\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Owner Waving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the textual analysis of the order dispute, the {\i gemara} records a separate exchange. {\i Rabbi Elazar} said to {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} of his generation \u8212- notably a colleague rather than an elder, which is why the pointed manner of the remark was appropriate \u8212- that he should not sit down until he could explain: {\i minayin le-minchat sotah she-te\u8217'unah tenufah} \u8212- whence do we know that the {\i sotah}\u8217's {\i mincha} requires {\i tenufah}, waving? At first glance this question is surprising, since the {\i pasuk} explicitly states that the {\i kohen} shall wave it. {\i Rabbi Elazar} clarifies: that the {\i kohen} waves it is explicit. The question is, whence do we know that the {\i owner} \u8212- the woman herself \u8212- must also participate in the waving?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is derived through a {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- a verbal analogy between two passages. By the {\i sotah}\u8217's {\i mincha}, the verse states {\i ve-lakach ha-kohen mi-yad ha-isha} \u8212- the {\i kohen} shall take from the hand of the woman. By the {\i shelamim}, the peace-offering, the verse states {\i yadav tevi\u8217'enah} \u8212- his hands shall bring it \u8212- and this verse speaks about the owner\u8217's hands in the context of waving the breast and leg of the {\i shelamim}. The shared term \u8220"hand\u8221" links the two passages and allows us to apply the rules of each to the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joint Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gezeirah shavah} runs in both directions: just as by the {\i sotah} we have a {\i kohen} performing the waving, so too by the {\i shelamim}; and just as by the {\i shelamim} the owner participates in the waving, so too by the {\i sotah}. The practical upshot is that this is not accomplished in two separate acts. {\i Maniach yado tachat yad be\u8217'alim u-menif} \u8212- the {\i kohen} places his hands underneath the hands of the owner and they wave together as one. The owner is in direct contact with the offering because it flows from her and is connected to her; the {\i kohen}\u8217's hands underneath represent the transition from personal ownership toward the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the {\i mishnah} lists the steps \u8212- {\i henif, higish, kamatz, hiktir} \u8212- and then says \u8220"he would have her drink and then bring the {\i mincha},\u8221" a difficulty arises: the {\i mishnah} has already said {\i hiktir}, that he burned the {\i kometz}. How can it then say he would first have her drink and only then bring the {\i mincha}, if the {\i kometz} has already been described as burned?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by distinguishing between two separate discussions embedded in the {\i mishnah}. The first part of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i henif ve-higish kamatz ve-hiktir, ve-ha-sha\u8217'ar ne\u8217'echal la-kohanim} \u8212- is answering a structural question: {\i seder mincha keitzad?} What is the procedure of the {\i mincha}? What are its steps? The {\i mishnah} is describing the internal sequence of the {\i mincha} offering itself, not yet addressing when it occurs relative to the drinking. Only after completing this description does the {\i mishnah} turn to the separate question of the relationship between the drinking and the {\i mincha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequence Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that structural clarification established, the {\i gemara} proceeds to the substantive debate. {\i Rabbanan} hold: {\i mashkeh ve-achar kach makriv} \u8212- first she drinks, then the {\i mincha} is brought. {\i Rabbi Shimon} holds the opposite: {\i makriv et minchatah ve-achar kach mashkeh} \u8212- first the {\i mincha}, then she drinks \u8212- as the {\i pasuk} explicitly states with the word {\i ve-achar}. And {\i Rabbi Shimon} concedes {\i be-di\u8217'avad}: if she drank first and the {\i mincha} was brought afterward, it is {\i kesherah}. The challenge, then, is to explain how the {\i chakhamim} can argue against {\i Rabbi Shimon} given the apparent clarity of the {\i pasuk}. This requires a careful analysis of all three drinking references in the relevant verses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Pesukim Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First V\u8217'Hishkah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies three distinct references to drinking in the passage in {\i Bamidbar} 5. The first \u8212- {\i ve-hishkah} \u8212- appears immediately after the verse describing the scraping of the {\i megillah} into the water ({\i ve-chatav et ha-alot ha-eleh ha-kohen ba-sefer u-macha el mei ha-marim}), and states that he shall cause the woman to drink the bitter, cursing waters. This occurs before any mention of the {\i mincha} procedure in the {\i pesukim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 V\u8217'Achar Yashkeh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second reference \u8212- {\i ve-achar yashkeh et ha-ishah et ha-mayim} \u8212- appears explicitly after the {\i pesukim} describing the {\i kohen} taking the {\i mincha} from her hand, waving it, performing {\i hagashah}, taking the {\i kometz}, and burning it on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}. It then says \u8220"and afterwards he shall cause the woman to drink the waters.\u8221" {\i Rabbi Shimon} reads this as the operative instruction for timing: the word {\i ve-achar}, \u8220"afterwards,\u8221" places the drinking definitively after the {\i mincha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third V\u8217'Hishkah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third reference \u8212- {\i ve-hishkah et ha-mayim} \u8212- appears yet again following the second reference, in the context of what happens once she has drunk. This repetition creates the exegetical problem: if the second {\i ve-hishkah} already addressed the drinking after the {\i mincha}, what work does the third do? And if the first {\i ve-hishkah} already addressed the drinking before the {\i mincha}, how does {\i Rabbi Shimon}, who privileges the second reference, account for the first? These three {\i pesukim} become the terrain on which {\i Rabbanan} and {\i Rabbi Shimon} stake out their respective positions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Prerequisites\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} quotes a {\i braita} that draws on {\i Rabbi Shimon} and {\i Rabbi Akiva} jointly: {\i ve-achar yashkeh} \u8212- the word {\i ve-achar} teaches that the drinking comes {\i l\u8217'achar kol ha-ma\u8217'asim kulan ha-amurim le-ma\u8217'alah} \u8212- after everything enumerated above. And specifically, {\i maggid she-shloshah devarim me\u8217'akvin bah} \u8212- three things must precede the drinking for it to be valid: the {\i kometz} must have been offered, the {\i megillah} must have been fully erased, and the woman must have already accepted the {\i shvuah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prerequisites Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kometz Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first prerequisite \u8212- {\i ad she-lo karav ha-kometz} \u8212- is precisely the point at issue between {\i Rabbi Shimon} and the {\i rabbanan} in the {\i mishnah}. The {\i braita} records {\i Rabbi Shimon le-ta\u8217'ameih}: he is consistent with his own position \u8212- first the {\i mincha}, then the drinking, exactly as the {\i pasuk} indicates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Erasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second prerequisite \u8212- {\i ve-ad she-lo nimchakah megillah} \u8212- seems puzzling at first. Of course she cannot drink if the ink has not yet been scraped into the water; there would be nothing to drink. The {\i gemara} therefore asks: {\i ela mai mashkeh lah?} \u8212- What exactly would she be drinking if it hasn\u8217't been erased?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Clarify\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} resolves this: {\i lo nitzrecha ela le-shirumo nikkar} \u8212- the requirement is not simply that some erasure has occurred, but that the erasure must be total and complete. If words were scraped into the water but faint traces of the writing remained visible on the {\i megillah} \u8212- {\i rishumo nikkar} \u8212- the water cannot be drunk. The {\i pasuk}\u8217's requirement of erasure demands that the writing be entirely undetectable; a partially erased {\i megillah} renders the water invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third prerequisite \u8212- {\i ve-ad she-lo tekabbel aleha ha-shvuah} \u8212- teaches that the woman must have already accepted the oath before she can drink. The {\i gemara} raises a subtle point here: this sounds as though the restriction is only that she cannot drink before the oath, but that the {\i megillah} itself could have been written beforehand. Yet {\i Rava} elsewhere states clearly that a {\i megillat sotah} written before the woman accepted the {\i shvuah} is entirely invalid \u8212- {\i lo asah ve-lo klum}. The order of the {\i pesukim} must be followed: first the oath, then the writing of the {\i megillah}, then the erasing, and then the drinking. The {\i gemara} acknowledges that the inclusion of the oath requirement as a distinct point here was perhaps to round out the list to three, since the underlying rule \u8212- that the oath must precede the writing \u8212- is already established elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbanan Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Mention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's reading of {\i ve-achar yashkeh} as the primary timing instruction, the {\i gemara} now explains how {\i Rabbanan} \u8212- who hold that the drinking precedes the {\i mincha} \u8212- account for all three {\i pesukim}. Their logic rests on the principle that the first mention establishes the operative timing. {\i Rabbanan savri ve-hishkah kama legufo de-mashkeh ve-achar kach makriv} \u8212- the {\i Rabbanan} treat the first {\i ve-hishkah}, which appears before the {\i mincha} steps, as the authoritative statement of sequence: she drinks first, and only then is the {\i mincha} brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rishumo Nikkar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the first {\i ve-hishkah} establishes timing, then what does the second reference \u8212- {\i ve-achar yashkeh} \u8212- teach? {\i Rabbanan} say it is not about timing at all, but about a separate {\i halakhah}: {\i mi-ba\u8217'ei lei she-rishumo nikkar} \u8212- it teaches the {\i din} of {\i rishumo nikkar}. The word {\i ve-achar}, \u8220"afterwards,\u8221" is read not as \u8220"after the {\i mincha}\u8221" but as \u8220"after everything that preceded the drinking has been properly completed\u8221" \u8212- including, most critically, the requirement that the {\i megillah} be fully erased with no faint writing remaining. The verse is a parenthetical reminder: before the actual drinking takes place, ensure that all prior steps \u8212- particularly the erasure \u8212- were properly performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forcing Compliance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i ve-hishkah} \u8212- {\i ve-hishkah batra} \u8212- teaches the position of {\i Rabbi Akiva}: {\i she-im nimchakah megillah ve-amrah eini shotah, me\u8217'arin otah u-mashkin otah be-al karchah} \u8212- if after the {\i megillah} was erased the woman declares that she will not drink, we press her and cause her to drink against her will. The final mention of drinking is thus not about timing, but about the obligation to ensure compliance: once the {\i megillah} has been scraped into the water, the process must be seen through, even coercively. In this way, {\i Rabbanan} derive three distinct teachings from the three {\i pesukim}, while maintaining that the first {\i ve-hishkah} governs the actual sequence: drinking first, {\i mincha} second.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon Counter (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Afterwards Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon} takes the opposite approach. {\i Ve-achar yashkeh legufo} \u8212- the explicit {\i ve-achar yashkeh} is the primary timing instruction, and it places the drinking definitively after the {\i mincha}. This is not a parenthetical point about proper procedure; it is the statement of the correct sequence: first the {\i mincha}, then the drinking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How then does {\i Rabbi Shimon} handle the first {\i ve-hishkah}, which appears before the {\i mincha} steps? He reads it as establishing the {\i be-di\u8217'avad} ruling: {\i she-im hishkah ve-achar kach hikriv et minchatah, kesherah} \u8212- if the order was reversed and she drank before the {\i mincha} was brought, the procedure is nonetheless valid after the fact. The earlier {\i ve-hishkah} is not saying \u8220"now have her drink\u8221"; it is saying that even if you were to have her drink at this earlier point, you could still go forward with the {\i mincha} and the {\i sotah} procedure would remain intact. It establishes retroactive validity for an out-of-order sequence, while the true {\i lechatchila} sequence is governed by {\i ve-achar yashkeh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i ve-hishkah batra} \u8212- like {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's reading \u8212- teaches that if the {\i megillah} was erased and the woman refuses to drink, she is compelled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbanan}, for their part, refuse to accept that the {\i pasuk} would open with a {\i be-di\u8217'avad} scenario. Since the {\i pasuk} describes drinking before the {\i mincha}, that must be the {\i lechatchila} order, not a concession to a substandard procedure. This is the crux of why {\i Rabbanan} resist {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's reading: the {\i pshat} of the {\i pesukim} \u8212- however much {\i Rabbi Shimon}\u8217's overall reading may commend itself \u8212- militates against treating the earlier mention of drinking as a mere {\i be-di\u8217'avad} allowance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a challenge against the attribution of forced drinking to {\i Rabbi Akiva}. A separate {\i braita} records a dispute about what happens when the {\i megillah} has been erased and the woman refuses to drink. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} states that an iron jaw-spreader \u8212- {\i kalbos shel barzel} \u8212- would be placed into her mouth to force it open, and she would be made to drink against her will in any such case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} rejects this. The purpose of the entire procedure, he argues, is {\i le-vodkah} \u8212- to test her, to determine whether she committed adultery. The bitter waters are an instrument of investigation, not punishment in their own right. If the very prospect of drinking has caused her to admit guilt \u8212- {\i ve-halo bedukah ve-omedet}, she is already as good as proven \u8212- then the waters have achieved their purpose. The name of God has been erased and this has produced the desired result: a confession or its equivalent. There is no basis to compound this by forcing the water upon her as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet {\i Rabbi Akiva} is not categorically opposed to forced drinking. He draws a temporal line: {\i ad she-lo karav ha-kometz, yekholah la-chazor bah} \u8212- before the {\i kometz} has been offered, the woman can retract. {\i Mishe-karav ha-kometz, einah yekholah la-chazor bah} \u8212- once the {\i kometz} has been offered, she cannot retract, and she is made to drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction seems initially puzzling. If the entire basis for {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's leniency is that her refusal constitutes an implicit confession \u8212- rendering forced drinking unnecessary because the waters have already served their purpose \u8212- then why does it matter whether the {\i kometz} has been offered or not? An implicit confession is an implicit confession regardless of where in the procedure one stands. The {\i gemara} presses this internal tension within {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Trembling Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear vs.\u160 ?Guilt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction within {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's position by introducing a crucial distinction between two types of refusal. The fundamental question underlying {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's entire framework is: can we reliably read her refusal to drink as an implicit admission of adultery? The answer is: not always. There is a real concern that the ritual\u8217's terrifying nature \u8212- the solemn oath, the divine name being erased, the entire theatre of the {\i Beis Hamikdash} \u8212- may have so overwhelmed an innocent woman that she refuses out of sheer panic, not out of guilt. If that is the case, her refusal is not a confession at all; it is a symptom of fear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Calm Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's categorical position against forced drinking applies specifically to {\i be-riyuta} \u8212- a calm, collected demeanor. If a woman is composed and deliberately says she will not drink, that is unmistakably an implicit admission. She understands precisely what is at stake; she is not overwhelmed by terror; and she is choosing not to proceed. {\i Rabbi Akiva} therefore holds: {\i kol machamat briyuta klal klal lo shatya} \u8212- whenever the refusal comes from a calm, composed state, we never force her under any circumstances. That refusal is treated as a confession, and the waters have served their purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before Kometz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more complex case is {\i retita} \u8212- a woman who is trembling and visibly terrified. Here {\i Rabbi Akiva} cannot confidently read her refusal as a confession; the trembling suggests that fear, not guilt, may be driving her behavior. In this case, {\i Rabbi Akiva} draws his distinction based on when in the procedure the refusal occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the {\i kometz} is offered, {\i Rabbi Akiva} holds that she may retract \u8212- she cannot be forced to drink. {\i Rav Ashi} explains: either the {\i megillah} has not yet been erased, or \u8212- following {\i Rashi}\u8217's interpretation of {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's position \u8212- the {\i megillah} was erased, but the {\i kohanim} did so prematurely, before the {\i kometz} was brought. Since the proper order, according to {\i Rabbi Akiva}, should be {\i mincha} first and then erasure, a {\i kohen} who erased the {\i megillah} before the {\i kometz} was offered acted improperly \u8212- {\i she-lo ke-din avud kohanim de-machki}. In that case, the woman had more time rightfully available to her before being confronted with the erased water, and the situation was precipitated by the {\i kohanim}\u8217's jumping ahead. Given both the trembling and the procedural irregularity, {\i Rabbi Akiva} does not treat her refusal as a confession, and she may back out \u8212- {\i matzi hadra bah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Kometz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the {\i kometz} has been offered and the erasure was performed in the proper order \u8212- {\i ke-din avud kohanim de-machki} \u8212- the situation changes. Everything has been done correctly; all proper steps have been completed. Now, even though she is trembling and her refusal might stem from fear, {\i Rabbi Akiva} holds that we cannot grant her an exit. The trembling means we cannot confidently interpret her refusal as an admission of guilt, and therefore the logic that \u8220"the waters have already served their purpose\u8221" does not apply. Paradoxically, it is precisely because she may be innocent but simply terrified that {\i Rabbi Akiva} insists she must drink \u8212- {\i lo matzi hadra bah} \u8212- and if necessary, she is compelled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This elegant resolution reconciles the apparently contradictory positions. {\i Rabbi Akiva} never forces a calm woman, because her composure makes her refusal a clear implicit admission. He never forces before the {\i kometz} when a trembling woman refuses, because procedural considerations allow her to withdraw. But he does force after the {\i kometz} when a trembling woman refuses, precisely because her trembling renders the refusal ambiguous rather than confessional, and since the procedure has been properly completed, it must be seen through. Two {\i tanna\u8217'im} debate exactly where {\i Rabbi Akiva} placed this line \u8212- the earlier {\i braita} suggests the critical factor is the erasure of the {\i megillah}, while this {\i braita} places it at the offering of the {\i kometz}. The {\i gemara} resolves this as {\i trei tanna\u8217'ei ve-aliba de-Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- two {\i tanna\u8217'im} each transmitting a version of {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Retraction Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a further question. The case of the calm woman whose refusal is read as an implicit admission has been established. But what happens when that woman subsequently changes her mind? She said {\i eini shotah} \u8212- calmly and clearly, without trembling \u8212- and {\i Rabbi Akiva} has read this as a tacit confession of adultery. But then she reverses course and says {\i shotah ani} \u8212- \u8220"I will drink.\u8221" Does she get to retract?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Retract\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One view holds that she cannot retract. {\i Keivan de-amrah eini shotah, teme\u8217'ah ani ka-amrah} \u8212- since she said she would not drink, she implicitly declared herself to have committed adultery. And {\i keivan de-achzik nafshah be-tum\u8217'ah, lo matzi hadra bah} \u8212- once she established her status as one who committed adultery, she cannot undo it. The {\i gemara} notes the suggestive force of the word {\i de-achzik} \u8212- \u8220"established\u8221" \u8212- which invokes a quasi-judicial framework: she has fixed a {\i halakhic} status for herself, and one cannot simply reverse such an establishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear-Based Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposing view argues that we should not be so quick to finalize the admission. {\i Keivan de-amrah shotah ani, galya da\u8217'ata de-machamat biyututa hu de-amrah} \u8212- now that she is willing to drink, it becomes clear that even what appeared to be a calm, collected refusal may have been rooted in fear rather than guilt. The external appearance of composure may not perfectly track inner states. Since she never explicitly confessed \u8212- she only implicitly did so through refusal \u8212- and since she is now reversing that refusal, perhaps we should not press the implicit admission further and should allow her to drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiku\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} leaves this question unresolved: {\i teiku}. The tension between treating an implicit admission as irrevocable and allowing the woman\u8217's demonstrated willingness to drink to recast the prior refusal as fear-based cannot be adjudicated here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Water Bitterness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bitter Additive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf concludes with a ruling of {\i Avuyei di-Shmuel} \u8212- the father of {\i Shmuel}. He holds that the {\i sota}h\u8217's water must contain an actual bitter additive: {\i tzarich she-yitein mar le-tokh ha-mayim} \u8212- something physically bitter must be placed into the water, independent of any effect produced by the erasing of the curses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this requirement is the redundant language of the verse itself: {\i mei ha-marim ha-marim} \u8212- the bitter, bitter waters. The word for bitterness appears twice, and {\i Avuyei di-Shmuel} reads this as indicating two independent sources of bitterness. The {\i meararim} \u8212- the cursing quality \u8212- is produced by the erasing of God\u8217's name and the curses into the water. But the {\i marim} \u8212- the physical bitterness \u8212- must be present independently. {\i Marim ke-var} \u8212- the waters must already be bitter before the spiritual dimension is added. The physical bitterness of the water is thus a freestanding requirement, not a byproduct of the spiritual process.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 20\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Review & Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mincha & Megilla Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 20 continues the tractate\u8217's systematic walk through the {\i sotah} ceremony. The previous daf addressed the {\i mincha} \u8212- its composition and preparation \u8212- as well as the writing of the {\i megilla}: the ink, the parchment, and the requirement that it be written only after she took the {\i shvua}. Notably, the {\i mishna} itself omits any mention of the {\i shvua}, but the requirement emerges from other sources. The daf also treated the scraping of the {\i megilla} into the water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drinking Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A major debate occupied the previous daf as well: which comes first, the drinking of the water or the bringing of the {\i mincha}? The ambiguity arises directly from the {\i psukim}, which first describe the drinking and then the {\i mincha}, yet conclude with the phrase {\i v\u8217'achar yashke et ha\u8217'isha et hamayim} \u8212- \u8220"and afterward he shall make the woman drink the waters.\u8221" Whatever the precise {\i halachic} order, it is clear that the {\i mishna} deliberately reserves the drinking for last, since it is the climax of the entire procedure. The dominant position, in any event, appears to be that the drinking follows the {\i mincha}, consistent with {\i v\u8217'achar yashke et ha\u8217'isha et hamayim}. With that backdrop established, the {\i mishna} of daf 20 now turns to the drinking itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Refusal to Drink\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before Erasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens with the case in which the woman refuses to drink before the {\i megilla} has been erased. If she says {\i eini shota} \u8212- \u8220"I will not drink\u8221" \u8212- she does not thereby admit guilt, but the proceedings cannot be completed through her refusal. In that case, {\i megillata nignenzet} \u8212- the {\i megilla} is placed in {\i gniza}. Since it bears God\u8217's name, it cannot simply be destroyed, yet it cannot be reused, as the {\i megilla} must be written specifically for that particular {\i sotah}. It is therefore stored away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzir & Wearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that there was a small designated space near the walls of the {\i heichal} where it would be buried. Tosafot, however, cites a striking {\i Yerushalmi}: {\i megillata nignenzet tachat tziro shel heichal} \u8212- it would be placed beneath the {\i tzir} of the {\i heichal}, the hinge-socket or pivot of the door. The {\i Yerushalmi} adds that this was done {\i bishvil leshachaka} \u8212- in order to wear it down gradually. Small amounts of water would flow there and continue to dissolve the writing. This is a remarkable practice: rather than direct erasure, they arranged for the {\i megilla} to be worn away through {\i grama}, an indirect process. Why they would seek to cause it to be worn away rather than simply keep it buried is not entirely clear, but the {\i Yerushalmi}\u8217's account is what it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mincha & Single Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the {\i mincha} \u8212- once it had entered a {\i kli sharet} \u8212- could not be redeemed or removed from the {\i azara}. The {\i mishna} states {\i u\u8217'minchata mitpazeret al hadeshen}: it would be scattered on the ashes, meaning burned in the {\i Beit hadeshen}, the section of the {\i azara} designated for {\i korbanot} that had become {\i pasul}. The {\i mishna} also states explicitly: {\i v\u8217'ein megillata kesheira lehashkot ba sotah acheret} \u8212- a {\i megilla} written for one woman cannot be used for another. This is precisely why it must be placed in {\i gniza} rather than reused. If she refuses to drink, she would be divorced without her {\i ksuva} \u8212- not because she admitted guilt, but because she is the cause of the proceedings\u8217' failure to complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 After Erasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced to Drink\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then turns to the case in which the {\i megilla} has already been erased and she then says {\i eini shota}. If she were to admit guilt at this point, the procedure would still be halted; but without an admission, the situation is entirely different. Now that God\u8217's name has been erased into the water, the proceedings cannot simply be abandoned at her whim. The {\i mishna} rules: {\i m\u8217'ar\u8217'arin otah u\u8217'mashkin otah al karchah} \u8212- they confuse her, agitate her, and force her to drink against her will. Some form of physical compulsion, such as a device to keep her mouth open, is implied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 How Much Erased?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot cites an important {\i Yerushalmi} on this point: {\i kama yimachak?} \u8212- how much of the {\i megilla} must have been erased before we reach the threshold of compulsion? {\i Tani Rabbi Chanina: Beit Shammai omrim ot achat, Beit Hillel omrim shtei otiyot} \u8212- Beit Shammai holds that even one letter of erasure triggers the obligation, while Beit Hillel requires two letters. Rabbi Ila explains Beit Hillel\u8217's reasoning: the threshold is {\i kedei lichtov Yud Hei} \u8212- enough to have written two letters of God\u8217's name. This implies that the concern is specifically reaching God\u8217's name itself. It is worth noting that one could technically transgress the {\i issur mechikat hashem} even by erasing a single letter of the divine name \u8212- perhaps that is Beit Shammai\u8217's position. In any case, the {\i Yerushalmi} makes clear that they had to reach the point of erasing {\i shem Hashem} for the compulsion to apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Physical Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Manifestation Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once she drinks \u8212- whether willingly or by compulsion \u8212- the {\i mishna} describes the consequences in vivid terms: {\i eina maspeket lishtot ad shepaneiha morikot} \u8212- almost instantaneously, her face turns green or jaundiced; {\i v\u8217'eineiha boltot} \u8212- her eyes bulge out; {\i v\u8217'hi mitmaleit gidim} \u8212- and she is filled with protrusions. Rashi explains {\i hagidim shebilchayeiha} \u8212- the sinews or veins of her jaw \u8212- {\i noffin venirin kemalei gidin}: it looks as though her entire face is swollen with distended veins. {\i Vegam besarah naaseit shitah shitah shel nefichah} \u8212- her flesh becomes puffed up in rows. The {\i Sifri} describes it as though poles are thrust into her, so she has ridges protruding from her flesh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Removal & Impurity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes this passage with: {\i v\u8217'heim omrim hotziuha, shelo tetamei ha\u8217'azara} \u8212- the attendants say, take her out, so that she does not render the {\i azara} impure. Notably, the {\i mishna} does not state that she dies. Yet the urgency of removing her strongly implies she is about to die, and the {\i gemara} will qualify this point further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption Guilt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One striking feature of this {\i mishna} is that it does not say {\i im hayeta temei\u8217'ah} \u8212- \u8220"if she was impure\u8221" \u8212- or {\i keshehayetah temei\u8217'ah}. It simply describes these physical effects as the natural, expected outcome. The {\i mishna} thus speaks from a presumption of guilt, without conditional language. This is a significant editorial choice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zechus Toleh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One-Three Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i im yesh lah zechut hayetah tolah lah} \u8212- if she had merit, it would hold the punishment in abeyance. The {\i mishna} specifies that merit can suspend the punishment for one year, two years, or three years, depending on the degree of merit. The {\i gemara} understands this as {\i bedavka} \u8212- precise \u8212- and will discuss it accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concept of {\i zechut tolah} raises immediate difficulties. If women are seen going through the {\i sotah} procedure without apparent consequences, it undermines public confidence in the efficacy of the waters. This very concern, as we shall see, prompted one of the {\i tannaim} to object to the concept altogether. Nevertheless, this is the {\i mishna}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether {\i teshuva} could forestall the punishment entirely. This depends on how one understands the nature of the procedure. If the {\i sotah} process is viewed as a purely divine punishment \u8212- a matter between the woman and God \u8212- then {\i teshuva} might logically be effective. There is a well-known responsum, likely from the {\i Noda Biyehudah}, addressing a parallel case: a person who is {\i chayav misa} and performs sincere {\i teshuva} before execution. The ruling is that the court carries out the sentence nonetheless. Divine forgiveness is between the person and God; the court operates by different parameters. The same principle may apply here. Yet this very distinction underscores a tension in the {\i sotah} procedure: to whatever degree it functions as a court-supervised process \u8212- the couple appeared before the court, the court sent her to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, there are witnesses \u8212- it is not simply a private matter between the woman and God. As we will discuss at the end of the {\i daf}, this tension becomes increasingly significant as the {\i gemara} develops the concept of {\i zechut tolah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Relatedly, the concept of {\i zechut tolah} is very disruptive to the practical reality of the procedure. If the proceedings appear to work \u8212- husband and wife resume marital life \u8212- and then two or three years later it becomes apparent she was guilty, what does that do for the husband\u8217's understanding of what transpired? How can they resume normal life if the water has not definitively proven anything in the immediate aftermath? The {\i gemara} offers various explanations for when the water fails to take effect \u8212- if the husband is not {\i menukeh me\u8217'avon}, if she has {\i zechus}, and so on \u8212- but one wonders whether these explanations arose precisely in response to periods when the procedure did not appear to be functioning as intended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teaching Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} draws an explicit conclusion from the concept of {\i zechut tolah}: {\i mikan omer Ben Azzai: chayav adam lelamed et bito Torah}. From here, Ben Azzai derived that a person is obligated to teach his daughter {\i Torah}. The reasoning is direct: if a woman drinks the water and experiences no immediate effect, she might conclude that the entire system is a fraud. However, if she has been taught {\i Torah} and specifically this {\i halacha} \u8212- that {\i zechut} sometimes suspends the punishment \u8212- she will understand that the absence of immediate effect does not invalidate the system. Knowledge of the system preserves faith in it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai & R. Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer disputes this directly: {\i kol hamelamed et bito Torah ke\u8217'ilu melamdah tiflut} \u8212- whoever teaches his daughter {\i Torah} is as though he taught her {\i tiflus}, a term with clear sexual connotations, which Birnbaum translates as promiscuity. The context makes this meaning unmistakable. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's concern is precisely the inverse of Ben Azzai\u8217's optimism: yes, knowing how the system works produces trust in it \u8212- but it also reveals how to work around it. If she knows that {\i zechut} can suspend punishment, she might reason that by accumulating {\i zechus} in other areas of life, she can commit adultery and defer consequences indefinitely. A little knowledge, on this view, is a dangerous thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the broader question of why the debate is framed specifically around women. The same concern could theoretically apply to men: someone who has learned {\i Nezikin} thoroughly can find technically permissible ways to act improperly in business. There is even a tradition recorded in the {\i gemara} that one who learns {\i Torah shelo lishma} in the sense of manipulating the system would have been better off not being created. The reason the debate focuses on women is likely twofold. First, men are already obligated in {\i talmud Torah}, so whatever potential negatives exist, the {\i mitzva} is not up for debate. What is open for debate is whether women, who are not so obligated, should be actively educated \u8212- and there one can weigh costs and benefits. Second, there may be a dimension here of how Rabbi Eliezer views women specifically: the final line of the {\i mishna} will make this clearer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting that Ben Azzai\u8217's formulation is about preserving the woman\u8217's confidence in the system, not about enabling {\i zechut} to shield her. Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's concern is not that {\i talmud Torah} itself will give her the {\i zechut} to evade punishment \u8212- {\i zechut} only suspends, it does not fully exempt \u8212- but rather that knowing the mechanics of the system will teach her how to acquire {\i zechus} strategically in other ways. The distinction matters. One might also note an argument entirely absent from the {\i mishna}\u8217's debate: that teaching a woman {\i Torah} might elevate her character and make her less inclined toward wrongdoing in the first place. The {\i mishna} does not entertain this possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further point: the {\i Rambam}, when codifying this material, uses the language {\i aval amru chachamim: kol hamelamed et bito Torah ke\u8217'ilu melamdah tiflut} \u8212- he frames it as rabbinic guidance rather than an outright prohibition, with the formulation {\i amru chachamim} suggesting policy direction rather than strict {\i isur}. To what degree this entire debate is a {\i halachic} debate, as opposed to a matter of {\i da\u8217'at Torah}-style communal guidance, is worth considering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes this section with Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's famous statement: {\i rotzah isha b\u8217'kav v\u8217'tiflut mitishah kabim u\u8217'prishut} \u8212- a woman would rather have one measure of sustenance and be able to have her sexual pleasure than nine measures with the requirement of abstinence. On this view, women are more prepared to sacrifice material wellbeing in order to maintain access to physical intimacy, and therefore cannot be trusted with knowledge that might give them theological cover for transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then lists, in what appears to be Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's name, figures who destroy the world: {\i chasid shoteh} \u8212- a foolish pietist; {\i rasha arum} \u8212- a crafty wicked person; {\i isha prushah} \u8212- a woman who is abstinent; and {\i makkot perushim} \u8212- the lashes of the ascetics. Rashi explains {\i makkot perushim} in the {\i gemara} as someone who strikes himself against walls to appear humble and modest \u8212- a performative false righteousness that has lost sight of genuine {\i frumkeit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inclusion of {\i isha prushah} is striking. Rabbi Yehoshua has just argued that women inherently prefer physical pleasure over abstinence, and yet here he lists an abstinent woman as one who destroys the world. The implication appears to be that a woman who presents herself as {\i prushah} is dissembling \u8212- all women, on this view, are ultimately seeking physical intimacy, and the woman who performs abstinence is the most dangerous of all. This connects directly to the broader question that runs through the entire {\i masechet}: how one understands the cheating woman and where blame is located. Some threads in the {\i gemara} have refocused or at least included the husband in the picture; here, the lens is firmly on the woman as the locus of sexual transgression. The debate about teaching women {\i Torah} is not purely an educational policy discussion \u8212- it reflects fundamentally different views of what the {\i sotah} represents and who bears moral responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ink Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Accounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now records a tradition transmitted by Rabbi Yehuda in the name of Shmuel, in the name of Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Meir was a scribe ({\i lavlar}), and he reported that when he was learning {\i Torah} with Rabbi Akiva \u8212- {\i keshehaiti lomed Torah etzel Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- he would add {\i kankantom} to his ink, {\i v\u8217'lo amar li davar}: Rabbi Akiva said nothing to him. When he subsequently came to Rabbi Yishmael \u8212- {\i keshebati etzel Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael asked him about his trade, and upon learning he was a scribe, told him: {\i hevei zahir shemelachtacha melachat shamayim hi} \u8212- be very careful, for your work is the work of heaven. If you add or omit even one letter \u8212- {\i shema tachsir ot achat o tatir ot achat} \u8212- you could destroy the entire world: {\i nimtzata ata machriv et ha\u8217'olam kulo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi illustrates why this is no hyperbole in terms of theological consequence: if one erases the {\i aleph} from {\i emet}, or the {\i heh} from {\i Elohim}, or adds a letter to {\i Bereishit} such that it reads as though something created God \u8212- one letter transforms the meaning entirely. The analogy to \u8220"let\u8217's eat grandma\u8221" versus \u8220"let\u8217's eat, grandma\u8221" captures the principle: punctuation, or in this case a single letter, saves worlds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kankantom Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir responded to Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's warning by saying: I already know the precise spellings \u8212- {\i debaki ana b\u8217'chasirut veyitirut} \u8212- but there is an additional concern beyond correctness at the moment of writing. Even after writing, {\i dilma ati veyativ akarna de\u8217'dalet} \u8212- perhaps a fly will land on the protruding corner of a {\i dalet} and smudge it, {\i u\u8217'mashvei lei reish} \u8212- turning it into a {\i reish}. Therefore, {\i davar acher yesh li she\u8217'ani matil l\u8217'toch hadyo v\u8217'kankantom shemo} \u8212- I add {\i kankantom} to the ink to ensure indelibility, so that no smudging can corrupt the letters after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's response: {\i vechi matilim kankantom l\u8217'toch hadyo? Ha-Torah amrah u\u8217'macha} \u8212- the {\i Torah} requires that the {\i megillat sotah} be erasable, and therefore the writing must be of a kind that can be erased. Indelible ink violates this requirement, at least for the {\i megillat sotah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Source Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges the account: {\i v\u8217'hatanya} \u8212- we have a {\i braisa} that tells a different version. In that {\i braisa}, Rabbi Meir says it was when he was learning with Rabbi Yishmael that he used {\i kankantom} and Rabbi Yishmael said nothing \u8212- and it was when he came to Rabbi Akiva that Rabbi Akiva forbade it. So we have two contradictions: first, the order of apprenticeship (which teacher came first?), and second, who actually objected to the {\i kankantom} (Rabbi Yishmael or Rabbi Akiva?).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning Sequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} says: {\i bishlama shimush a-shimush lo kashya} \u8212- the apparent contradiction in order of apprenticeship can be resolved, because both sequences are true. {\i Me\u8217'ikara asa l\u8217'kamei d\u8217'Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- he first came to Rabbi Akiva. {\i Keivan d\u8217'lo matzi ka\u8217'ei alibeih} \u8212- but he could not follow what Rabbi Akiva was getting at. This is, incidentally, a remarkable statement about the future Rabbi Meir \u8212- the very sage whom no one else could fully understand \u8212- who in his youth could not keep up with Rabbi Akiva. He then went to Rabbi Yishmael and {\i gamar gemara} \u8212- he acquired the tradition, the foundational knowledge. Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's approach was more about transmitting tradition, while Rabbi Akiva\u8217's was more about analysis and creative reasoning. {\i Hadar asa l\u8217'kamei d\u8217'Rabbi Akiva savar svara} \u8212- he then returned to Rabbi Akiva and learned how to analyze and break down that foundation. So both sequences are accurate: it was Rabbi Akiva first and then Rabbi Yishmael, and also Rabbi Yishmael and then Rabbi Akiva.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Forbade?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the contradiction about who forbade the {\i kankantom} cannot be resolved similarly. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i ela asrah a-asrah kashya} \u8212- this remains a genuine difficulty, meaning the two {\i braisot} reflect a disagreement about what actually happened. The {\i gemara} leaves this as an unresolved contradiction between the sources.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Ink Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parshat Sotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} then records Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's account of Rabbi Meir\u8217's ultimate position: {\i l\u8217'chol matilin kankantom l\u8217'toch hadyo} \u8212- for all purposes one may add {\i kankantom} to the ink, {\i chutz mi-parshat sotah bilvad} \u8212- except for {\i parshat sotah} alone. Rabbi Meir was unwilling to abandon his practice of using {\i kankantom} for scribal work generally; he accepted the restriction only for the {\i sotah} passage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefer vs.\u160 ?Mikdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov then refines this further in Rabbi Meir\u8217's name: {\i chutz mi-parshat sotah she-ba-mikdash} \u8212- the restriction applies only to the {\i parshat sotah} that is actually written in the {\i Mikdash} for the procedure itself; the section as it appears in a {\i Sefer Torah} does not require erasable ink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai beinaihu} \u8212- what is the practical difference between these two formulations? Rabbi Yirmiyah answers: {\i limchok limdinata ika beinaihu} \u8212- the debate concerns whether one may use the {\i parshat sotah} as written in a {\i Sefer Torah} for the actual {\i sotah} procedure. The question is thus not merely academic: can the {\i kohen} open a {\i Sefer Torah} to the relevant passage and erase it into the water, or must a dedicated {\i megilla} be written separately for this purpose?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains the underlying logic: since the {\i Torah} states {\i u\u8217'macha} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall erase\u8221" \u8212- there is an understanding that any text functioning as the {\i parshat sotah} must be written in a form that is capable of being erased. If the passage in a {\i Sefer Torah} could in theory be used for the procedure, then the {\i Torah} itself would demand that this passage be written in erasable ink. Conversely, if one holds that the {\i Sefer Torah} passage can never serve as the {\i megillat sotah}, there is simply no requirement that it be written with erasable ink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This debate connects to a broader parallel about ink and {\i Sifrei Torah} generally. The ability to correct a {\i Sefer Torah} depends on the ink being erasable \u8212- it rides on the surface of the {\i klaf} and can be scraped away. Were the ink to be absorbed into the {\i klaf}, correction would be impossible. This capacity for erasure is almost a practical necessity, given that scribes do make mistakes. Similarly, a {\i Sefer Torah} need not be written {\i ksidran} \u8212- in order \u8212- which means corrections can be made without invalidating the entire scroll. The {\i Rambam} rules that writing with erasable ink is {\i mitzva min hamuvchar}, the preferred manner, but that {\i bedieved}, if one wrote with {\i kankantom} or similar indelible materials \u8212- {\i she-hu omed v\u8217'eino nimchak} \u8212- the scroll remains {\i kasher}. The practical {\i halacha} thus aligns with Rabbi Akiva\u8217's general principle, at least {\i lechatchila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Megilla Reuse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces an independent debate: {\i ein megilla kesheira lehashkot ba sotah acheret} \u8212- a {\i megilla} written for one woman cannot be used to administer the waters to another woman. This is the standard position. But Rabbi Akha berabbi Yoshiya holds the contrary: {\i megilla kesheira lehashkot ba sotah acheret} \u8212- the same {\i megilla} can indeed be reused for another woman. This is a position we have not encountered until now. (One must also contend with the earlier ruling of Rava that the {\i megilla} cannot be written before the woman took the oath \u8212- Tosafot notes that this {\i gemara} appears to ignore that requirement, and raises the question of how to harmonize the two. One possibility is that both women in this scenario had already taken their oaths before the writing occurred.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes that this debate about reuse is structurally parallel to the debate about whether the passage in a {\i Sefer Torah} can be used: if you cannot reuse a {\i megilla} written for one woman for another, perhaps the same logic prevents using the generic {\i Sefer Torah} text for any woman; and if you can reuse it, perhaps the generic text suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Papa then demonstrates \u8212- {\i dilma la hi} \u8212- that these debates need not align. The {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's reason for prohibiting reuse is not about {\i lishmah} per se but about {\i keivan de\u8217'itkazai leshum Rachel}: once the {\i megilla} has been assigned to Rachel, it cannot be reassigned to Leah. But a {\i Sefer Torah} is {\i stama kesiva} \u8212- written generically \u8212- and was never assigned to any particular woman, so perhaps it {\i can} be used. Conversely, Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak argues the reverse: Rabbi Akha berabbi Yoshiya permits reuse {\i keivan de\u8217'ichtiv leshum alos v\u8217'alah} \u8212- because the {\i megilla} was written for the purpose of functioning as a curse, regardless of which woman it serves. But a {\i Sefer Torah} written {\i lilmod kesiva} \u8212- to be a {\i Sefer Torah} for learning \u8212- was never written for the purpose of being a curse at all, and {\i hacha nami delo machakinan} \u8212- one cannot use it for the {\i sotah} procedure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus elegantly demonstrates that there are independent considerations in each case pointing in opposite directions, and one cannot simply map one debate onto the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A challenge is then raised from the laws of {\i gittin}: if a man wrote a {\i get} for his wife and changed his mind, and a townsman found him and said {\i shmi keshimcha v\u8217'shem ishti k\u8217'shem ishtecha} \u8212- my name is the same as yours and my wife\u8217's name is the same as your wife\u8217's \u8212- may he use that {\i get}? The answer is clearly no: {\i ha-hu pasul legarsheto bo}. A {\i get} written for one woman is invalid for another. This would seem to support the view that a {\i megilla} likewise cannot be reused. The {\i gemara} responds that {\i gittin} and {\i sotah} are not governed by the same principles: by a {\i get}, the {\i Torah} states {\i v\u8217'chatav lah} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall write for her\u8221" \u8212- making {\i lishmah} the essential act of writing. But for {\i sotah}, the operative term is {\i v\u8217'asah lah} \u8212- and what does \u8220"doing\u8221" mean in this context? {\i Mechikah} \u8212- the erasing. It is the act of erasing into the water that must be done for her; the writing itself may not require the same standard of {\i lishmah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fear & Niddah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sudden Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the question raised by the {\i mishna}\u8217's statement that they say {\i hotziuha} \u8212- take her out of the {\i azara}. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- why must she be removed? Is it because she will die, and they want to prevent a corpse from rendering the {\i azara} impure?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this reasoning: a corpse is in fact permitted in {\i machaneh Leviyah}, as derived from the verse that Moshe took the bones of Yosef {\i imo b\u8217'mchitzato} \u8212- with him in his camp, and Moshe was in {\i machaneh Leviyah}. Furthermore, the area where the {\i sotah} stands \u8212- {\i Sha\u8217'ar Nikanor}, at the boundary between {\i Ezrat Nashim} and {\i Ezrat Yisrael} \u8212- has only the sanctity of {\i Ezrat Nashim}, which is {\i machaneh Leviyah}, not {\i machaneh Shechinah}. Even if she were to die there, a corpse would technically be permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye therefore offers a different explanation: {\i shema tifros niddah} \u8212- the concern is not that she will die, but that the physical trauma and shock of the experience will cause her to emit blood and become a {\i niddah}. A {\i niddah} is forbidden in {\i machaneh Leviyah} because {\i tumah yotzei migufo} \u8212- impurity that issues from the body \u8212- renders one prohibited from entering that domain. This is also why, in contemporary times, those who ascend to Har HaBayit are advised regarding their status with respect to both {\i ba\u8217'al keri} and {\i niddah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the {\i gemara} to ask: {\i l\u8217'meimra d\u8217'be\u8217'isusa marafya} \u8212- does fear cause the body to loosen and bleed? Yes: {\i dechtiv vatischalchal hamalka} \u8212- Esther trembled greatly \u8212- and {\i amar Rav shepirsah niddah} \u8212- Rav says that she became a {\i niddah} from the trembling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a difficulty arises: we have a {\i mishna} that rules that a woman hiding in a cave, afraid of bandits, need not fear that she has begun her period, because fear suppresses bleeding. Which is it \u8212- does fear cause bleeding or prevent it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with a distinction: {\i pachda tzamit} \u8212- chronic anxiety, the kind that has been present for some time, causes the body to constrict and suppresses bleeding; {\i be\u8217'isusa marafya} \u8212- sudden shock causes the body to loosen and triggers bleeding. Rashi explains {\i pachda} as prolonged worry, as distinguished from the sudden seizure of fear. It is this sudden shock \u8212- {\i be\u8217'isuta} \u8212- that the {\i gemara} identifies as the concern when the woman drinks the {\i sotah} waters, and therefore the attendants must remove her from the {\i azara} before she becomes a {\i niddah} there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note a secondary implication: a woman who wished to delay or obstruct the {\i sotah} proceedings could theoretically do so by ensuring she became a {\i niddah}, since the procedure cannot be conducted while she is in that state. Whether this would be treated analogously to simply refusing to drink \u8212- resulting in divorce without {\i ksuva} \u8212- is an interesting question left open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Suspension Durations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i man masnisin?} \u8212- which {\i tanna} authored our {\i mishna}, which states that {\i zechut} can suspend punishment for one, two, or three years? It turns out that at least three other {\i tannaim} hold positions inconsistent with this formulation. The {\i braisa} records: if she has merit, it suspends punishment for three months \u8212- {\i kedei hakcarat ha\u8217'ubar} \u8212- just long enough for a pregnancy to become visible. This is the view of Abba Yossi ben Chanan. His reading of the verse {\i v\u8217'niktah v\u8217'nizra\u8217'ah zera} is that {\i v\u8217'niktah} refers not only to the {\i tehorah} woman but also to the meritoriously guilty one, and the suspension lasts until {\i v\u8217'nizra\u8217'ah zera} \u8212- when a pregnancy would be recognizable, not when it is conceived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Yitzchak ish Kfar Darom reads {\i v\u8217'nizra\u8217'ah zera} differently: {\i zera} refers to the time it takes for a child to be born. The suspension therefore lasts nine months. A secondary reading he offers draws from {\i zera ya\u8217'avdenu yesupar} \u8212- \u8220"the seed that serves him will be told\u8221" \u8212- i.e., {\i zera hara\u8217'uy lesaper}, seed mature enough to speak. This would extend the period further, but the primary position is nine months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twelve Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael holds: {\i shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- twelve months. Even though there is no direct {\i rava} for this, there is a {\i zecher ladavar} \u8212- a hint \u8212- from the story of Daniel and Nebuchadnezzar. Daniel advised Nebuchadnezzar: {\i lahen malka malki yishpar alach, v\u8217'chatayach b\u8217'tzedaka ferok, va\u8217'avayach bemichan inayin} \u8212- redeem your sins through righteousness, your iniquities through generosity to the poor \u8212- and Nebuchadnezzar\u8217's well-being would be extended. The verse then records: {\i u\u8217'liktzas yarchin terei asar} \u8212- after twelve months, all these things came upon Nebuchadnezzar. He had followed Daniel\u8217's advice, performed righteous acts, and thereby postponed the divine punishment by a full year. So merit forestalls punishment for twelve months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 None of these positions, however, reaches three years. The {\i gemara} resolves this by saying that the {\i mishna} indeed follows Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's twelve-month view, but adds a multiplier from the verse: {\i ko amar Hashem: al shelosha pish\u8217'ei Edom v\u8217'al arba\u8217'ah lo ashivenu} \u8212- \u8220"for three transgressions of Edom and for four I will not reverse it.\u8221" The verse implies that for three sins, God holds back punishment; the model of one year per instance is multiplied threefold, yielding three years. Even though this is only a {\i zecher ladavar} rather than a definitive proof, it accounts for the {\i mishna}\u8217's formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divine vs.\u160 ?Court\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Model Shift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final movement of the {\i daf}\u8217's discussion marks a significant conceptual shift. As the {\i gemara} elaborates on {\i zechus toleh}, it moves away from the specific dynamics of the {\i sotah} procedure \u8212- the {\i v\u8217'niktah v\u8217'nizra\u8217'ah zera} of the relevant {\i parshat sotah} \u8212- and toward a much more generic model: any person who deserves divine punishment may have that punishment forestalled by merit. The proof from Nebuchadnezzar\u8217's twelve-month reprieve is the clearest illustration: Nebuchadnezzar is not a {\i sotah}, not subject to any court proceeding, not even Jewish \u8212- yet the same principle of merit suspending divine punishment applies to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God vs.\u160 ?Court\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This shift has a positive dimension: it normalizes the {\i sotah} within a broader theology of divine justice and human merit. The guilty woman is not being treated as a unique category of sinner with her own special divine mechanism; she is simply a person whose deserved punishment can be forestalled, just like anyone else. This arguably {\i downplays} the hyperfocus on the adulterous woman as an exceptional figure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, this shift pulls in a direction that complicates the court-supervised nature of the entire proceeding. If {\i zechut tolah} is understood as simply a matter of divine punishment operating between the woman and God \u8212- forestalled by merit, regardless of {\i teshuva} or any court action \u8212- then the entire procedure begins to look less like a legal determination and more like an occasion for divine intervention. As was pointed out in the earlier discussion of {\i teshuva}: the more one sees {\i zechus toleh} through this lens of purely divine punishment, the more it becomes a matter between her and God alone, with the court serving only as the context in which God acts \u8212- not as the body that determines her fate. The question of how this interacts with the husband\u8217's ability to resume marital relations, and whether the procedure has genuinely \u8220"proven\u8221" anything if the water does not take immediate effect, remains a deep tension within the entire {\i masechet} \u8212- one that the subsequent {\i dafim} will continue to address.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 21\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Merit Forestall Puni (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Merit Three Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} established that {\i zechus} can forestall the punishment of the {\i sotah} for as long as three years. The {\i gemara} now asks: {\i zechus d\u8217'mai}? What type of merit possesses sufficient power to delay punishment for that length of time?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises two possibilities. The first is {\i zechus d\u8217'Torah} \u8212- perhaps it is the merit of learning Torah. Yet this is immediately challenged: {\i ha einah metzuva v\u8217'osa} \u8212- a woman is not commanded to learn Torah, and therefore, as an {\i eina metzuva v\u8217'osa}, the merit she accumulates through Torah study does not carry the full weight that {\i talmud Torah} ordinarily confers. The second possibility is {\i zechus d\u8217'mitzvah} \u8212- the merit of other {\i mitzvos} she has performed. But this too is called into question. {\i Zechus d\u8217'mitzvah mi magna kulei hai?} Since when does a {\i mitzvah} wield that kind of protective power?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ner Mitzvah Torah Or\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To press this challenge, the {\i gemara} cites a {\i braita} in the name of Rabbi Menachem bar Rabbi Yosi, a teaching entirely general in character, concerned with the relative protective power of {\i mitzvah} as against Torah. {\i Ki ner mitzvah v\u8217'Torah or} \u8212- for a {\i mitzvah} is a candle and the Torah is light. The verse, says the {\i braita}, deliberately compares the {\i mitzvah} to a {\i ner} and the Torah to {\i or}, and the lesson drawn from each comparison is distinct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ma ner einah megina ela lefi sha\u8217'ah} \u8212- just as a candle provides light or protection only for a limited time, so too {\i af mitzvah einah megina ela lefi sha\u8217'ah}, a {\i mitzvah} only protects for a brief period. {\i Torah}, by contrast, is compared to {\i or} in the broader sense of light itself \u8212- {\i ma or magin l\u8217'olam}, light illuminates perpetually \u8212- and so {\i af Torah megina l\u8217'olam}, Torah protects forever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites the verse from Mishlei: {\i b\u8217'hishalchecha tancha osach, b\u8217'shochbecha tishmor alecha, v\u8217'hakitzosa hi tisichecha}. The {\i braita} reads this verse as a sequence. {\i B\u8217'hishalchecha tancha osach} \u8212- when you walk, it will guide you \u8212- {\i zeh l\u8217'olam hazeh}, this refers to Torah\u8217's guidance in this world. {\i B\u8217'shochbecha tishmor alecha} \u8212- when you sleep, it will guard you \u8212- {\i zu misah}, Torah protects even in death. {\i V\u8217'hakitzosa hi tisichecha} \u8212- and when you awaken it will speak for you \u8212- {\i l\u8217'asid lavo}, in the future world, where the Torah one has learned will serve as an advocate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty this creates for the {\i gemara}\u8217's question is now clear. If a {\i mitzvah} protects only briefly, it would seem insufficient to forestall punishment for as long as three years. And if the relevant {\i zechus} is Torah, the power of that protection would seem unavailable to a woman at full strength, since she is an {\i eina metzuva v\u8217'osa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue that the protective power of Torah does not derive from the {\i kiyum hamitzvah} of {\i talmud Torah}, but from the intrinsic quality of Torah as {\i dvar Hashem} \u8212- from connecting with the word of God \u8212- and that this power operates regardless of whether one is commanded. That argument is available, and it is not without force. Yet the {\i stam} of the {\i gemara}, at least at this stage of the discussion, appears to proceed on the assumption that the protective quality of Torah flows through the value of fulfilling the {\i mitzvah} of {\i talmud Torah}, not from some independent sacramental power of Torah itself. This raises a further question: even if a woman\u8217's Torah study is understood merely as {\i hechsher mitzvah} \u8212- a means of learning the {\i halachos} of what she must do \u8212- how could that instrumental learning confer greater protection than the {\i mitzvos} it enables her to perform? If a {\i mitzvah} protects only briefly, the learning that enables it cannot protect longer. The {\i gemara} thus leaves both possibilities in difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before moving to the {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution, it is worth noting an apparent tension in the tradition\u8217's broader framework. The {\i Shulchan Aruch} rules that women do not recite a {\i bracha} over {\i mitzvas asei shehazman grama} if they choose to perform them, yet women do recite {\i birchas haTorah} in the morning. The Soloveitchik analysis holds that {\i birchas haTorah} is not a {\i birchas hamitzvah} in the ordinary sense but rather a {\i bracha} on the {\i cheftza} of Torah itself \u8212- as evidenced by the blessing\u8217's own language: {\i asher kidshanu b\u8217'mitzvosav v\u8217'tzivanu la\u8217'asok b\u8217'divrei Torah} points toward the {\i mitzvah} dimension, but {\i v\u8217'ha\u8217'arev na}, {\i melamed Torah l\u8217'amo Yisrael}, and {\i bocher asher bachar banu} address Torah\u8217's intrinsic character as {\i Nosen HaTorah}. On this view, the protective power of Torah could indeed be understood as independent of {\i kiyum hamitzvah}. Yet, as noted, this is not the path the {\i gemara} chooses to take.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then adds a second formulation: {\i aveira mechaba mitzvah, v\u8217'ein aveira mechaba Torah} \u8212- a sin extinguishes a {\i mitzvah}, but a sin cannot extinguish Torah. Rashi explains that \u8220"extinguish\u8221" here refers not to the protective power of the {\i mitzvah} but to its {\i s\u8217'char} \u8212- its reward. One who has performed {\i mitzvos} and also committed {\i aveiros} may find that {\i le\u8217'atid lavo} they cancel each other out. But {\i aveiros} will not cancel the reward of Torah. This is supported by the verse {\i mayim rabim lo yuchlu lechabot es ha\u8217'ahavah} \u8212- great waters cannot extinguish the love. The {\i Maharsha} observes that the imagery is striking: Torah is generally compared to water, yet here the {\i aveiros} are the water and Torah is the flame \u8212- the {\i ner}, the {\i or} \u8212- that cannot be quenched. So {\i mitzvos} afford brief, momentary protection that may ultimately be neutralized by {\i aveiros}, while Torah confers a lasting, encompassing protection that endures. Neither option, then, cleanly solves the {\i gemara}\u8217's problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parable Traveler\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate the difference between the protection of {\i mitzvah} and the protection of Torah, the {\i braita} of Rabbi Menachem bar Rabbi Yosi offers a {\i mashal}. A person is traveling in the darkness of night, {\i b\u8217'ishon layla va\u8217'afeila}, and he fears the thorns, the pits, the thistles, the wild animals, and the bandits. He also does not know which path to take.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then chances upon a torch \u8212- {\i avuka shel or}. The torch immediately delivers him from the thorns, the pits, and the thistles \u8212- he can now see what lies immediately before him. Yet he is still afraid of the wild animals and the bandits, and he still does not know which path to take. When dawn breaks \u8212- {\i keivan she\u8217'ala amud hashachar} \u8212- the daylight saves him from the animals and the bandits, for these are dangers of the night that the light of day dispels. Yet even now he still does not know which way to go. When he reaches a crossroads \u8212- {\i parshas drachim} \u8212- he is saved from everything and knows the right path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mashal} is clear in its first two stages. The torch is the {\i ner mitzvah}: it provides immediate protection from immediate dangers, but its reach is limited. The light of day is the {\i or Torah}: it is broader, more comprehensive, and protects from graver threats. But what is this {\i parshas drachim}, this crossroads where one finally knows the right direction? The {\i gemara} will address this \u8212- and the three opinions about its meaning constitute a striking discussion in their own right.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yosef Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef proposes a resolution that cuts through the apparent impasse. He distinguishes between two dimensions of protection \u8212- protection from punishment and protection from sin \u8212- and argues that {\i mitzvah} and Torah provide these in different combinations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mitzvah b\u8217'idna d\u8217'asik bah megana u\u8217'matzla} \u8212- a {\i mitzvah} while you are in the course of performing it both protects you from punishment and, as Rashi explains based on the continuation of the {\i gemara}, saves you from sin. {\i B\u8217'idna d\u8217'lo asik bah} \u8212- once you have finished, {\i aguni magnei} \u8212- it still forestalls punishment, but {\i atzulei lo matzla} \u8212- it no longer protects you from sinning. Torah, by contrast, {\i bein b\u8217'idna d\u8217'asik bah u\u8217'vein b\u8217'idna d\u8217'lo asik bah}, whether you are actively learning or not, {\i megana u\u8217'matzla} \u8212- it both forestalls punishment and protects from sin. The educative power of Torah shapes character in a lasting way, setting a person on a more elevated path even when he is not sitting in the {\i beit midrash}. On this account, a {\i mitzvah} does forestall punishment even after it is completed, and that is sufficient to explain how this {\i sotah}\u8217's merit delayed her punishment for up to three years. Torah\u8217's additional power is the ongoing protection from future sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Doeg Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges Rav Yosef\u8217's position. {\i Ela me\u8217'ata} \u8212- if Torah protects one from sinning even when not actively learning, how do we account for Doeg and Achitophel? These two figures, prominent in rabbinic literature as great {\i talmidei chachamim}, nonetheless sinned grievously, pursuing and attacking David. {\i Mi lo askei beTorah} \u8212- they certainly engaged in Torah study. {\i Amai lo higina aleihu} \u8212- why did their Torah learning not prevent them from sinning? The objection is fundamental: there are those who learn Torah and nonetheless go badly astray. Rav Yosef\u8217's formulation that Torah protects from sin while not learning cannot be sustained if it is falsified by such prominent counterexamples.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Revision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore revises the framework. Torah, {\i b\u8217'idna de\u8217'asik bah}, while one is actively learning, {\i magna u\u8217'matzla} \u8212- protects from punishment and saves from sin. {\i B\u8217'idna delo asik bah}, when not learning, {\i aguni magna} \u8212- it still forestalls punishment, but {\i atzulei lo matzla} \u8212- it does not necessarily prevent sin. A person can learn Torah and then leave the {\i beit midrash} and sin. {\i Mitzvah}, meanwhile \u8212- {\i bein b\u8217'idna de\u8217'asik bah bein b\u8217'idna delo asik bah} \u8212- whether performing or not, {\i aguni magna} \u8212- protects from punishment, but {\i atzulei lo matzla} \u8212- cannot protect from sin, even during the act of performance. (It is at least theoretically possible, as the {\i Mishnah Berurah} hints regarding {\i lashon hara} in a {\i sukkah}, to be engaged in a {\i mitzvah} while simultaneously sinning in another way.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This revised formulation serves the {\i gemara}\u8217's purpose. The advantage of Torah over {\i mitzvah} is now narrowly defined: Torah, uniquely, prevents sin {\i while one is actively learning}. Both Torah and {\i mitzvah} forestall punishment after their performance is complete. This explains how a {\i sotah}\u8217's merit of {\i mitzvah} could protect her for up to three years. It also accounts for Doeg and Achitophel \u8212- men who learned Torah but sinned when they left their learning. The difficulty with Rava\u8217's formulation is that it substantially attenuates the lovely imagery of the {\i braita}, which suggested Torah\u8217's protection was far more sweeping and enduring. But this is where the {\i gemara} arrives.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ravina Women Merit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina Merit Enable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina offers a different resolution entirely, one that returns to the original question about the {\i sotah} rather than reconstructing the topology of {\i mitzvah} and Torah protection. {\i Le\u8217'olam zechus Torah} \u8212- it is in fact the merit of Torah that protects this woman. And as for the objection that she is an {\i eina metzuva v\u8217'osa} and therefore her Torah learning lacks full merit \u8212- {\i nihi depikudei lo mifkada} \u8212- it is true she is not commanded, {\i be\u8217'agra demakrin u\u8217'matnyan bnaihu} \u8212- but she shares in the reward that accrues from causing her sons to read {\i pesukim} and study {\i mishnah}, and {\i u\u8217'natrin lehu le\u8217'gavreihu ad de\u8217'atu mi-bei midrasha} \u8212- from waiting patiently for her husband to return from the study hall, maintaining the home, and enabling him to learn. {\i Mi lo palga bahadayhu?} \u8212- does she not receive a portion of that reward? By enabling the Torah learning of the men in her life \u8212- her sons and her husband \u8212- she participates in their {\i mitzva ve\u8217'oseh}, and it is that participation which confers upon her the {\i zechus Torah} that can forestall punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Enablers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer is candid about its assumptions: a woman\u8217's own Torah learning, as {\i eina metzuva v\u8217'osa}, does not carry the full protective weight of a man\u8217's Torah learning. The merit she acquires comes not from her own direct engagement with Torah as a commanded act, but from her role as enabler. She sends her children to {\i cheder}; she waits for her husband to return from the {\i beit midrash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not a {\i gemara} that necessarily sits easily with contemporary sensibilities, and it is worth noting that the {\i gemara} did not have to construct the question this way. The {\i mishnah} earlier cited Nevuchadnezzar\u8217's capacity for {\i zechus} to forestall punishment for three years \u8212- and his merit was simply giving charity to the poor. Nothing required the {\i gemara} to introduce the {\i braita} of {\i ner mitzvah v\u8217'Torah or}, to reject the possibility that an ordinary {\i mitzvah} could carry this power, or to resist the idea that a woman\u8217's Torah study as {\i eina metzuva v\u8217'osa} might nonetheless provide protection. The {\i gemara} chose this path. And the path it chose has a pointed contextual relationship with what follows: the later {\i mishnah} contains both Ben Azzai\u8217's view that one is obligated to teach one\u8217's daughter Torah and Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's opposition. Ravina\u8217's answer, appearing immediately before that discussion, effectively frames women\u8217's relationship to Torah learning in instrumental rather than intrinsic terms \u8212- their real merit lies in enabling men\u8217's Torah, not in their own engagement with it as an independent value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with the {\i sotah} herself is also worth observing. The {\i sotah} was waiting \u8212- apparently \u8212- for her paramour. The righteous woman whom Ravina describes is waiting for her husband to return from learning Torah. The {\i gemara} appears to be drawing a deliberate contrast between two types of waiting.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Merit Forestall Puni (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crossroads Interpretatio\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the unresolved element of the parable \u8212- the {\i parshas drachim}, the crossroads where the traveler is finally saved from everything and knows which path to take. {\i Mai parshas drachim?} Three {\i amoraim} offer interpretations, and taken together they form a suggestive continuum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda says: {\i zu talmid chacham v\u8217'yom ha-misa} \u8212- this is a {\i talmid chacham} and the day of death. The Torah has guided one through life, protected from dangers both immediate and profound, and the {\i parshas drachim} is that final threshold \u8212- the moment of departing this world for the next. At that crossroads, one does not yet know whether the path leads to {\i olam ha-ba} or not \u8212- {\i al ta\u8217'amin b\u8217'atzmecha ad yom mosecha} \u8212- yet having arrived there through the guidance and protection of Torah, one now knows the Torah has led one through to the end, set one on the right path. The image is almost entirely sacramental: Torah, sustained throughout a lifetime of learning, carries a person through to the final reckoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak says: {\i zeh talmid chacham b\u8217'yirat chet} \u8212- this is a {\i talmid chacham} with fear of sin. For Rav Yitzchak, Torah illuminates the world and shows what is right, but Torah alone cannot guarantee that one will choose correctly. {\i Ra\u8217'eh nasati lefanecha hayom et ha-chayim v\u8217'et ha-tov v\u8217'et ha-mavet v\u8217'et ha-ra} \u8212- one now knows the two paths, but will one choose the right one? That depends on bringing to the {\i parshas drachim} a quality that Torah cannot by itself supply: {\i yirat chet}, the internalized fear of sin. Torah is necessary but not sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra says: {\i zeh talmid chacham d\u8217'sakla shma\u8217'ta aliba d\u8217'hilchasa} \u8212- this is a {\i talmid chacham} who successfully brings legal reasoning to its correct {\i halachic} conclusion. For Mar Zutra, even choosing to act rightly is not enough if one\u8217's Torah learning leads to incorrect {\i halachic} conclusions. Torah shows what is right in principle, but one must be able to translate that learning into sound {\i halacha l\u8217'maaseh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These three positions form a continuum from the mystical to the rational. For Rav Chisda, Torah has an almost intrinsic, sacramental protective power \u8212- sustain it till you die, and it will carry you through. For Rav Yitzchak, Torah is necessary but must be complemented by character, by {\i yirat chet}, the quality that ensures one actually chooses rightly when confronted with the fork in the road. For Mar Zutra, Torah must be disciplined into correct {\i halachic} reasoning \u8212- even character and good intent are insufficient if one\u8217's learning has led to wrong conclusions. Each of the latter two answers also implicitly pulls back from the claim that Torah does everything: one can be a {\i talmid chacham} without having the right character; one can learn Torah without arriving at the correct {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aveira Sin Extinguish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aveira Mechabah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the formulation encountered earlier: {\i aveira mechaba mitzvah, v\u8217'ein aveira mechaba Torah} \u8212- a sin extinguishes a {\i mitzvah}, but a sin cannot extinguish Torah. The verse from Shir HaShirim is adduced: {\i mayim rabim lo yuchlu lechabot es ha\u8217'ahavah} \u8212- great waters cannot extinguish the love, where \u8220"love\u8221" signifies Torah. The {\i Maharsha} notes the poignancy of the imagery: elsewhere Torah is compared to water, but here the {\i aveiros} are the water and Torah is the flame \u8212- the {\i ner}, the {\i or} \u8212- that cannot be drowned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doeg Achitophel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef introduces a {\i drasha} by Rabbi Menachem bar Rabbi Yosi on a verse that Doeg and Achitophel might have encountered \u8212- and which, had they properly understood it as Rabbi Menachem bar Rabbi Yosi did, {\i lo radfu batar Dovid}, they would not have pursued David.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Their reasoning, it appears, ran as follows. The verse {\i v\u8217'lo yeira\u8217'eh becha ervat davar v\u8217'shav me\u8217'acharecha} teaches that a sin of forbidden sexual relations causes God to withdraw. David had sinned in the matter of Bathsheba. Therefore, they concluded, God had abandoned David, and he was now susceptible to attack. {\i Leimor Elohim azavo} \u8212- God has forsaken him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What they failed to understand \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hein einam yodim} \u8212- is precisely the principle of {\i aveira mechaba mitzvah, v\u8217'ein aveira mechaba Torah}. It is true that a {\i sin} can extinguish the protection of a {\i mitzvah}. But it cannot extinguish the protection of Torah. David was not merely a performer of {\i mitzvos}; he was a {\i talmid chacham}, a man of Torah. And because Torah\u8217's protection cannot be extinguished by an {\i aveira}, God did not forsake David. Doeg and Achitophel, brilliant scholars though they were in their own right, misread the verse and drew the wrong conclusion \u8212- they did not reckon with the distinct and indelible protective power of Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the verse {\i im yitein ish es kol hon beiso b\u8217'ahavah boz yavozu lo} \u8212- if a man were to give all the wealth of his house for love, people would scorn him. The discussion of Hillel and his brother Shevna illuminates an ideal: Hillel devoted himself entirely to Torah without relying on material support from others, unwilling to trade any portion of his Torah\u8217's reward for financial ease. When Shevna proposed a {\i Yissachar-Zevulun} arrangement, a {\i bat kol} emerged and cited this very verse. Hillel would not commodify his Torah, would not give up so much as a fraction of his {\i talmud Torah} for worldly benefit. Ulla notes that this surpasses the model of those who learned Torah under the support of a brother or a {\i nasi} \u8212- worthy arrangements, but not the highest ideal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teach Daughters Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai Teach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the new {\i amud} the {\i gemara} turns to the next {\i mishnah}, which contains a debate about teaching one\u8217's daughter Torah. Ben Azzai states: {\i chayav adam l\u8217'lamed es bito Torah} \u8212- a person is required to teach his daughter Torah. The reason is connected to the {\i sotah} procedure: the {\i mishnah} establishes that {\i zechus} can suspend the effects of the water, and a woman who has learned Torah will understand this \u8212- she will know why the water may have failed to act immediately and not suppose the {\i sotah} ritual has been definitively disproven. There is at least a practical argument for teaching women the relevant Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the discussion that follows is inflected by the {\i gemara} we have just completed. If teaching one\u8217's daughter Torah confers upon her the {\i zechus} of Torah \u8212- or at least, as Ravina suggested, enables her to become an instrument of Torah\u8217's merit \u8212- one might think there is every reason to teach her. But the opposing view raises a different concern entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Tiflus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer states: {\i kol ha-melamed bito Torah k\u8217'ilu melamdah tiflus} \u8212- whoever teaches his daughter Torah is as though he has taught her {\i tiflus}. The term {\i tiflus}, which in context is clearly juxtaposed with {\i perishus} and tied to sexual transgression, most naturally denotes sexual licentiousness or conduct of a sexually improper character. Several sources confirm this reading: the {\i gemara} elsewhere connects {\i tiflus} to the disqualification of women and slaves from {\i zimmun} together, and the word appears in descriptions of the banquet of Achashverosh where the officers competed in boasting about women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why would teaching one\u8217's daughter Torah be equivalent to teaching her {\i tiflus}? Several possibilities present themselves. The most direct, and most relevant to the {\i sotah} context, is that Torah knowledge shows her the loopholes \u8212- she learns the {\i halachos} and learns how the system works, and that knowledge enables her to exploit it. A second possibility: Torah study develops nuance and analytical subtlety, and that capacity for fine distinctions can be turned to finding ways around prohibited conduct. A third, more structural concern is that Torah\u8217's {\i zechus} will protect her from punishment, thereby removing the deterrent effect of the {\i sotah} ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Effect Tiflus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's statement cannot be taken literally \u8212- Torah is obviously not itself {\i tiflus}. {\i Ela eima k\u8217'ilu limda tiflus} \u8212- it is as if he taught her {\i tiflus}, because the effect of her having learned Torah is equivalent in its practical consequences to having been taught licentiousness. It is not that Torah is shameful; it is that in this context, for this person, the knowledge functions as an opening toward transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wisdom Craftiness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu identifies the underlying principle of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position: {\i d\u8217'ksiv ani chochma shakanti arma} \u8212- I am wisdom, I have dwelt with craftiness. {\i Keivan she-nichnas chochma b\u8217'adam nichnesa imo armumis} \u8212- once wisdom enters a person, craftiness enters with it. Wisdom and cunning travel together; learning sharpens the mind, and a sharper mind finds more ways to achieve what it desires. The concern, then, is not that Torah is corrupting in any direct sense, but that it arms one with intellectual tools that can be turned in any direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious question arises: what of men? Men learn Torah and thereby also acquire {\i arma} \u8212- craftiness. Rabbi Abbahu\u8217's principle seems to apply universally. The {\i gemara} does not resolve this question explicitly here. One might say that men are obligated to learn regardless and cannot be exempt on account of this danger, or that the tradition places greater trust in men\u8217's use of those tools \u8212- though neither answer is articulated in so many words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Three Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbanan who disagree with Rabbi Eliezer ask: {\i hai ani chochma mai avdi lei?} What do they do with the verse {\i ani chochma shakanti arma}? They apply it differently. Rabbi Yosi bar Chanina reads {\i arma} not as craftiness but as nakedness \u8212- {\i ein divrei Torah miskaymin ela b\u8217'mi she-ma\u8217'amid atzmo arom aleihen} \u8212- Torah endures only in one who makes himself naked for it, strips away all other pursuits and devotes himself entirely. Rashi, however, reads it as a variant of the craftiness interpretation: one who schemes and maneuvers to find every possible opportunity to learn Torah, seizing moments wherever they appear. Rabbi Yochanan adds: {\i ein divrei Torah miskaymin ela b\u8217'mi she-mesim atzmo k\u8217'mi she-eino} \u8212- Torah endures only in one who makes himself as nothing, practicing a radical self-negation and self-sacrifice for the sake of learning. {\i V\u8217'hachochma me\u8217'ayin timatzei} \u8212- wisdom comes from nothingness; make yourself nothing, and Torah will find its home in you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These three readings of {\i arma} \u8212- the sacramental (nakedness, total devotion), the strategic (seizing every opportunity to learn), and the spiritual (self-negation) \u8212- stand in contrast to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's use of the same word. Rabbi Eliezer sees {\i arma} as the danger that Torah\u8217's wisdom produces; the Rabbanan see {\i arma} as precisely the quality one must bring {\i to} Torah in order to truly acquire it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi Matron\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot cites a crucial passage from the Yerushalmi that illuminates the full scope of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. A {\i matrona} \u8212- a Roman noblewoman \u8212- posed a genuine question to Rabbi Eliezer: why did the sin of the golden calf, which was one sin, result in three different forms of death? Rabbi Eliezer refused to answer her. {\i Ein isha chachma ela bapalach} \u8212- a woman\u8217's wisdom is only in her weaving. The school in Israel called {\i Pelech} derives its name from this phrase, chosen deliberately and ironically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When his son Horkenos reproached him \u8212- {\i bishvil shelo le\u8217'ashivah davar echad min haTorah}, you refused to answer a single question of Torah to this woman, and by insulting her you have cost me three hundred {\i kor} of {\i ma\u8217'aser} annually \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer replied: {\i yisrefu divrei Torah v\u8217'al yimsaru divrei Torah l\u8217'nashim} \u8212- better that the words of Torah be burnt than that they be transmitted to women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This was not merely the concern about {\i melamdah tiflus}. Rabbi Eliezer held a principled, sweeping opposition to women receiving Torah. The image is striking in context: in the {\i sotah} ritual, Torah is dissolved into water to be tested against the woman; here, Rabbi Eliezer would rather Torah burn than be given over to women. In both cases, Torah must be protected from a certain kind of contact with women \u8212- though obviously for entirely different reasons and with very different valences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Yerushalmi also provides important context for Ben Azzai\u8217's position. Ben Azzai is said to follow the view of Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah, who expounded the verse of {\i hakhel}: {\i ha\u8217'anashim, v\u8217'hanashim, v\u8217'hatef} \u8212- the men came to learn, {\i nashim ba\u8217'os lishmoa}, the women came to hear. As Tosafot explains, this means women\u8217's participation in {\i hakhel} is not for the sake of {\i zechus Torah} per se, but {\i kedei sheyeidu lekayeim mitzvah} \u8212- so that they know how to fulfill {\i mitzvos}. This establishes a middle position between the poles of the debate: it is not an intrinsic {\i Torah lishma} for women, but neither is it {\i melamdah tiflus}. There are thus three positions in the tradition: Torah study for women has intrinsic value; Torah study for women is valuable as instrumental knowledge enabling {\i kiyum mitzvos}; and Torah study for women is actively dangerous.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehoshua Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kav Tiflus Nine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's statement returns the discussion to the sexual dimension that runs throughout the tractate. {\i Rotze isha bekav ve\u8217'tiflut mitisha kabim u\u8217'frishus} \u8212- a woman prefers one measure of sustenance with {\i tiflus} over nine measures with {\i perishus}, with asceticism and separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Income Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two readings of this statement emerge from the {\i rishonim}. Rashi understands {\i tiflus} in the general sense of greater sexual freedom, and the contrast is between a modest life that affords greater sexual opportunity and an affluent one with more separation. Rabbeinu Chananel, cited by Tosafot, makes the {\i pshat} more specific and more domestic: a woman would rather her husband be a donkey driver who comes home regularly \u8212- once a week on {\i Shabbat}, providing {\i onah} \u8212- earning a modest income of one {\i kav}, than have him be a camel driver who travels on long routes, comes home only once every thirty days, and earns the much larger income of nine {\i kabim}. {\i Chavivah aleiha onah bechol Shabbat vekav echad mitisha kabim u\u8217'frishus sheloshim yom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might observe that this preference \u8212- for the husband\u8217's presence over material wealth \u8212- seems entirely natural and even admirable. Yet in context Rabbi Yehoshua presents it as a concern, because the same drive, misdirected, becomes the source of the {\i sotah}\u8217's downfall. And the teaching connects back to the question of Torah instruction: the danger of teaching a woman Torah, in Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view, is that Torah arms her with the tools to pursue what she desires. Rabbi Yehoshua simply asserts that what she desires, at bottom, is this.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Destroyers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chasid Shoteh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the same context, the {\i mishnah} records a teaching attributed to Rabbi Yehoshua: there are those who destroy the world, and among them is the {\i chasid shoteh}, the foolish pious person. {\i Heichy dami chasid shoteh?} \u8212- what does a {\i chasid shoteh} look like? {\i Kegon deka taveia itta benahra, ve\u8217'amar lav orach ara listakulei bah, ve\u8217'atzulah} \u8212- a woman is drowning in the river, and he says it is improper to gaze at a woman, and he fails to save her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot cites a parallel from the Yerushalmi: an infant is drowning, and the {\i chasid shoteh} says, let me first remove my {\i tefillin} and then I will save him \u8212- {\i ad shecholeitz tefillin yatzah nishmato}, and by the time he finishes removing his {\i tefillin}, the child has died. There is also the neat wordplay embedded in the Yerushalmi version: {\i echlotz} \u8212- I will remove \u8212- echoes the language of {\i tefillin} removal, {\i chalitzah}, while the word itself sounds like a cry of urgency. The point in both cases is the same: piety that is divorced from practical moral reasoning, that privileges the form of righteousness over its substance, destroys lives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rasha Arum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rasha arum}, the crafty wicked person, is the counterpart of the {\i chasid shoteh}. Where the {\i chasid shoteh} is conspicuously righteous while causing harm through his failure to engage with reality, the {\i rasha arum} conceals his wickedness behind a facade of ordinariness or even apparent virtue. What ties this figure to the others in the list is the same element \u8212- he is not what he appears. His {\i rishus} is executed {\i b\u8217'armimus}, with craftiness, such that it is not apparent to observers that anything wrongful has occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Early Case Judge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan identifies the {\i rasha arum} as {\i zeh hamati\u8217'im devarav le\u8217'dayan kodem sheba ba\u8217'al din chaveiro} \u8212- one who presents his version of the case to the judge before the opposing litigant arrives. Even if the judge intends to be impartial, once a case has been framed in a particular way it is very difficult to genuinely reorganize one\u8217's understanding around the opposing account. First impressions in litigation create a bias that is hard to dislodge. There is in fact a prohibition against hearing one {\i ba\u8217'al din} in the absence of the other, and Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's identification of this as {\i rasha arum} behavior underlines why: it is subtle, it does not appear to be a violation of anything, and yet it has a real and corrupting effect on the judicial process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poor Disqualify\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu identifies the {\i rasha arum} as one who gives a poor person just enough money to push him over the threshold of eligibility \u8212- {\i zeh ha-nosein dinar le\u8217'ani le\u8217'hashlim lo masayim zuz}. The rule, as the {\i mishnah} establishes, is that one who possesses two hundred {\i zuz} may not take {\i leket}, {\i shichecha}, {\i peah}, or {\i maaser ani} \u8212- he is not considered an {\i ani}. One who has one {\i dinar} less than two hundred {\i zuz} is an {\i ani} and may receive these gifts in full. By giving the poor person precisely the {\i dinar} that brings him to two hundred {\i zuz}, the donor appears to be performing an act of generosity \u8212- he gives the man something! \u8212- while in fact cutting him off from all future entitlement to {\i matnos aniyim}. The one-time gift costs the donor a {\i dinar}; the loss to the poor person is potentially enormous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estate Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi, citing Rabbi Yochanan, identifies a further instance of the {\i rasha arum}: {\i zeh ha-masi eitza limkor b\u8217'nechasim mu\u8217'atim} \u8212- one who counsels the sale of a small estate in circumstances where this advice will harm the orphaned daughters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The legal background, as Rabbi Yosi cites in the name of Rabbi Yochanan, is the rule that if male heirs from a small estate sell it prematurely, {\i mah she-machru machru} \u8212- what they have sold is sold. In a small estate, all the assets are in principle earmarked to support the daughters \u8212- {\i mezonos} for the unmarried sisters \u8212- until they marry. The sons technically have no share. Yet if the sons liquidate the estate, the daughters\u8217' claim, which lies against real property, cannot follow the proceeds; the sale stands and the daughters\u8217' support disappears.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rasha arum} here is not the son who sells \u8212- Tosafot notes that even the son\u8217's action may not be strictly forbidden. What is wrongful is the unsolicited outsider who, having no stake in the outcome, volunteers advice that serves one party at the direct expense of another and subverts the intent of the original estate arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye adds a variant: {\i zeh ha-masi eitza limkor b\u8217'nechasim k\u8217'Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel}. The relevant dispute between Rabbi and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel concerns a gift made with a subsequent gift-over: I give my property to Reuven, and after Reuven, to Shimon. If Reuven sells the property during his lifetime, Rabbi holds that Shimon can reclaim it from the purchasers; Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel holds that Shimon receives only what Reuven leaves over \u8212- if Reuven sold it, it is gone. The {\i rasha arum} advises Reuven to sell under the authority of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's position, thereby defeating Shimon\u8217's expectation under the original gift. Again, it is not the advice itself that is necessarily wrong if solicited by one\u8217's client, but the uninvited intervention to harm one party for the benefit of another that constitutes {\i rasha arum} conduct.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 22\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women\u8217's Torah Study\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Tiflus Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 22 in {\i Sotah}. We pick up at the very bottom of 21b, having just worked through a series of fascinating and contested reflections on the question of teaching one\u8217's daughter {\i Torah}. The Gemara raised the concern of {\i tiflus} \u8212- the idea expressed in the famous formulation {\i kol hamelamed bito Torah ke\u8217'ilu limdah tiflus}, that teaching a daughter {\i Torah} is as if one taught her foolishness or licentiousness. This issue was itself preceded by a broader Gemara discussion about the value of {\i Talmud Torah} for women, arriving at the question indirectly by asking what kind of {\i zechus} is {\i toleh} for women \u8212- and exploring women\u8217's role as {\i eina metzuvah ve\u8217'osah}. The Gemara then moved into Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's skepticism about women who present themselves as ascetic and uninterested in physical pleasure, and from there into the broader theme of people who do not present themselves as they truly are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before returning to the Gemara, a brief word on the {\i tiflus} debate is in order. Historically, women were often not educated simply because women were not educated in general society \u8212- not necessarily from any religious conviction. But in the modern era, when women\u8217's education became both possible and expected, the question of women learning {\i Torah} and specifically {\i Torah Sheba\u8217'al Peh} became, for some communities, a serious ideological and {\i halachic} position grounded in the concept of {\i tiflus}, and for others \u8212- including the {\i Rav} and the Lubavitcher Rebbe \u8212- something considered absolutely essential. The real battleground has far more to do with broad sociological and religious attitudes than with the precise interpretation of this single line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As we saw, {\i Tosfos} quotes the {\i Yerushalmi} where Rabbi Eliezer takes not merely a cautionary position about {\i tiflus} as a loophole in {\i sotah} proceedings, but an outright harsh stance on the value of women learning {\i Torah} altogether: {\i mutav yisrefu divrei Torah ve\u8217'al yimsru lenashim} \u8212- it is better that the words of {\i Torah} be burned than transmitted to women. Whatever one makes of the attitude toward women underlying that statement, it reflects a very strong position. And yet the {\i Gemara} does not come down definitively on one side of the Rabbi Eliezer versus Ben Azzai debate, leaving open the question of how to rule \u8212- or even whether this is a matter of {\i psak} at all, rather than competing positions in {\i aggadic} literature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\u8217's Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam}\u8217's treatment is instructive here. He rules that a woman {\i can} learn {\i Torah} and that she is like {\i eina metzuvah ve\u8217'osah} \u8212- she performs an act that is not obligatory upon her \u8212- but he affirms {\i yesh lah s\u8217'char}, she receives reward for it. Then, introduced with the formula {\i aval amru chachamim}, he cites {\i kol hamelamed bito Torah ke\u8217'ilu limdah tiflus}. The phrasing {\i aval amru chachamim} signals explicitly that the {\i Rambam} is not presenting this as {\i halachah}, but rather as a form of religious direction or advice. He is not {\i paskening} it as a binding ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context & Realities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several distinct considerations shape how one understands Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. First, there is the question of whether the Gemara establishes any definitive ruling at all. Second, even within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's own statement, how much is it {\i halachah} and how much shaped by the particular social realities of women in his era? Third \u8212- and this is the crucial contemporary dimension \u8212- how much do present-day realities demand a fundamentally different response, such that following Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's approach today would be not only counterproductive but genuinely destructive? The reason different communities take different positions on women\u8217's {\i Torah} education is not primarily because they interpret this one line differently; it is because they hold different broader views about women\u8217's roles, intellectual capacities, and relationship to society. The interpretation of the line follows from those views \u8212- not the other way around.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deceptive People\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theme Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the Gemara, we are in the midst of a discussion about people who do not present themselves as they truly are. The earlier material included Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's comment about the {\i isha perushah} \u8212- the woman who presents herself as ascetically pious but whose motives are suspect \u8212- and the {\i chassid shoteh}, someone who has absorbed the externals of {\i chassidus} without grasping its essence, drawn entirely toward outward form rather than inner reality. There was also the {\i rasha arum}, the deceptive wicked person who appears righteous while scheming in the background.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues at the bottom of 21b. {\i Amar Rav Sheshet}: the {\i rasha arum} is one who {\i machtiya acherim b\u8217'orchotav} \u8212- who tips other people toward his path. {\i Rashi} explains: {\i reu v\u8217'asu kamoni, l\u8217'chu b\u8217'drachai} \u8212- \u8220"Look at me, follow my example, walk in my ways.\u8221" The person presents himself as a model of righteousness, saying \u8220"imitate me,\u8221" while in truth this performance is designed to deflect suspicion from his real conduct. The outward {\i chassidus} is a cover for inner corruption: {\i lo nitkaven zeh l\u8217'harot atzmo k\u8217'chassid b\u8217'einei habriyot v\u8217'ein tocho k\u8217'baro}. Admittedly, this does not fit the Gemara\u8217's language perfectly. {\i Tosfos} offers what feels like a more compelling reading: this person gives advice to others that ultimately serves his own interests rather than theirs. Like an advisor who steers a client toward an investment because it benefits himself \u8212- the guidance appears altruistic, but is entirely self-serving. This relates to the prohibition of {\i lifnei iver}, though the Gemara\u8217's concern here is not with cataloguing specific {\i issurim} but with identifying character types: the sort of person who always presents as deeply generous and other-directed, while every move serves himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lenient to Self\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Zreika amar Rav Huna}: the {\i rasha arum} is {\i zeh hameikal le\u8217'atzmo u\u8217'machmir le\u8217'acheirim} \u8212- one who is lenient for himself and stringent toward others. This person projects an image of great {\i frumkeit}; whenever asked a {\i sha\u8217'ila}, he responds with the strictest ruling. But privately, when no one is watching, he applies the most lenient standard to himself. The rigorous public persona is inversely related to his private behavior.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ulla amar}: such a person is one who {\i kara ve\u8217'shana velo shimish talmidei chachamim} \u8212- who learned {\i Mikra} and {\i Mishna} but never genuinely attended upon Torah sages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimush Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the precise problem with {\i velo shimish}? {\i Rashi} explains: {\i lilmod sevarat haGemara b\u8217'ta\u8217'amei haMishna} \u8212- the person knows all the material but has never engaged in the kind of rabbinic discourse that reveals what it actually means. {\i She\u8217'ein Torato al boryah} \u8212- his {\i Torah} learning lacks depth \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'ein lilmod heimenno}, one cannot simply learn from {\i Mishna} alone, because {\i sheharei al yedei hata\u8217'amim yesh chilluk be\u8217'issur ve\u8217'heter, be\u8217'dinei mamonot leZakot uleChayev, uvetahara letamei uletaher} \u8212- only by understanding the reasoning behind rulings does one grasp the distinctions governing {\i issur ve\u8217'heter}, monetary law, and matters of {\i tahara}. The {\i Gemara} itself is always asking {\i mai ta\u8217'ama amar hachi} \u8212- what is the underlying reason \u8212- and tracing {\i ika beineihum} \u8212- the practical differences that flow from competing rationales. Without that conceptual scaffolding, a person cannot actually apply the {\i halachah} properly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why is this person an {\i arum}, a schemer? The Gemara explains: {\i vearum hu shemashmi\u8217'a kolo ve\u8217'shoneh mishnayotav}, {\i kasevar hu shevaki b\u8217'ta\u8217'ameihem}, {\i ve\u8217'nohegin bo kavod ke\u8217'talmidei chachamim} \u8212- he broadcasts his erudition, recites {\i mishnayos} with great confidence, and people assume he is a genuine {\i talmid chacham} and defer to him accordingly. We all know people of this type: very erudite, able to cite a thousand sources, impressive in their fluency \u8212- but with no real depth of understanding. The Gemara elsewhere captures this as {\i chamor nosei sefarim}, a donkey carrying books \u8212- all the information on its back, none of it genuinely internalized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also suggest that {\i shimush talmidei chachamim} points to something beyond intellectual comprehension. The purpose of attending upon Torah sages is not only to acquire the conceptual tools for {\i halachic} reasoning, but to observe how {\i Torah} actually transforms character \u8212- how it shapes the small, daily ways in which one interacts with other people. A person who has only pursued the knowledge, without that investment in personal development, remains in some sense a {\i rasha arum}: his unrefined character traits persist beneath a surface that looks entirely like that of a {\i talmid chacham}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Xerox & Education\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion connects in an interesting way back to the earlier conversation about women\u8217's {\i Torah} education. The Gemara\u8217's concern about {\i tiflus} was in some quarters associated specifically with {\i Torah Sheba\u8217'al Peh} as distinct from {\i Torah Shebichtav}. In certain {\i chareidi} schools, the approach has been to teach girls {\i Mikra} but not {\i Rashi}, on the grounds that {\i Torah Sheba\u8217'al Peh} is the dangerous knowledge. There was even a period when the \u8220"progressive\u8221" approach to women studying {\i Gemara} was to allow it only from a photocopied sheet \u8212- as if physical distance from the {\i Gemara} itself mitigated something. The point is that learning {\i Mikra} and {\i Mishna} without {\i Gemara} and without genuine depth is not only intellectually incomplete; it is, in the Gemara\u8217's framework, precisely the profile of someone who appears knowledgeable but cannot actually navigate {\i halachah}. And part of what makes {\i Torah Sheba\u8217'al Peh} feel threatening to some may be exactly that it is empowering \u8212- it is the knowledge that actually governs how we live and act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Am Ha\u8217'aretz Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having described the person who learned but did not attend upon sages, the Gemara now assigns him a category. {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer: harei zeh am ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- he is like a commoner. This is notable because the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} designation, while often carrying negative connotations in rabbinic literature, is actually a step back from the {\i rasha arum}. An {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} is not necessarily malicious \u8212- he is simply someone whose {\i halachic} practice cannot be relied upon and who is outside the rabbinic world. Perhaps he is a fine person who loves learning but lacks intellectual depth, or who has no bad intentions but simply cannot represent himself as more knowledgeable than he is. The {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} is better than the {\i rasha arum}, though one also cannot trust his {\i halachic} precision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bur Classification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachmani omer: harei zeh bur} \u8212- worse still, he is a complete ignoramus. This is surprising, and {\i Tosfos} himself flags it: {\i teima, keman sevira lei?} How can someone who has learned {\i Mikra} and {\i Mishna} be worse than an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}? The intuition may be that knowledge without understanding is, in a certain sense, more problematic than simple ignorance. The {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} at least knows what he does not know. The person who has memorized masses of material without grasping it occupies a more confused and dangerous epistemic position \u8212- though calling him a {\i bur}, a word associated with the most complete kind of ignorance, remains a harsh verdict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kuti Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yannai omer: harei zeh kuti} \u8212- a Samaritan. This is a striking escalation. {\i Rashi} explains that since this person has not attended upon sages, {\i eino makpid al divrei chachamim lada\u8217'atam} \u8212- he does not concern himself with rabbinic rulings because he does not even want to know what they are. {\i Velo shana ela leharot atzmo ketalmid chachamim} \u8212- and whatever he has learned was only to present himself as a {\i talmid chacham}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i Rashi}\u8217's comparison to the {\i Kutim} is worth examining more closely. The {\i Kutim} were not simply lax observers. They had a fundamentally different authority structure: a different {\i Sefer Torah}, a different understanding of {\i Torah}, and a complete non-recognition of rabbinic authority \u8212- not a failure to comply with it, but a wholesale rejection of it. So {\i Rashi}\u8217's point, reflected in the prohibition on eating their bread and wine, is not merely that they were punctilious in the wrong things, but that {\i keivan shelo shimish chachamim, eino makpid al divrei chachamim lada\u8217'atam} \u8212- because he has not attended upon sages, he does not recognize rabbinic authority as binding on him at all. Someone who says \u8220"I want to know the texts but I don\u8217't care what the rabbinic tradition has to say about them\u8221" \u8212- who effectively becomes an authority unto himself \u8212- is more threatening than someone who is simply ignorant. The ignorant person at least knows where the authority lies. This figure rejects the entire system, like a kind of intellectual priesthood-of-the-laity: \u8220"I\u8217'll learn these texts and I won\u8217't care what anyone else has determined.\u8221" {\i Eino makpid al divrei chachamim lada\u8217'atam} \u8212- he doesn\u8217't even want to know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Magus Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Acha bar Yaakov omer: harei zeh magus} \u8212- a mage. {\i Rashi} interprets this as {\i mechashef ve\u8217'ochez et ha\u8217'einayim ve\u8217'gonev et halevavot} \u8212- someone who casts illusions, bewitching people into believing he is brilliant when there is no substance behind it. This is in the spirit of the {\i rasha arum}: he casts his spell, and people fall under it. One might also note, as was raised in discussion, that in the world of the Gemara a {\i magus} referred to a Persian priest \u8212- a figure who represents an entirely different religious tradition and authority system, not merely a charlatan. On that reading, the {\i magus} represents yet a further step along the same axis as the {\i Kuti}: not just a rejection of rabbinic authority, but an allegiance to an altogether foreign system.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Magus & Tana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then clarifies the comparison. {\i Amar Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak: mistavra keRav Acha bar Yaakov}, because people say: {\i rati magusa} \u8212- the magus chants \u8212- {\i velo yada mai amar} \u8212- and he has no idea what he is saying. He recites his incantations as pure gibberish, with no comprehension of their content. And similarly, {\i tani tana velo yada mai amar} \u8212- the {\i tana}, the memorizer of {\i Tannaitic} material, recites what he has memorized without understanding a word of it. As Saul Lieberman once remarked, what the rabbis wanted in their {\i tanna\u8217'im} \u8212- the small-{\i tav tanna\u8217'im}, not the {\i tanna\u8217'im} of the {\i mishnaic} period \u8212- were people with excellent memories and very little creativity. You wanted them to transmit the text accurately; you did not want them to think about it, because then they would introduce their own ideas. The comparison to the {\i magus}, then, is ultimately about the complete absence of comprehension beneath an imposing performance of erudition: it looks like knowledge, but it is ignorance all the way down.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Am Ha\u8217'aretz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shema Criterion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks directly: {\i tnu rabanan, eizehu am ha\u8217'aretz?} \u8212- Who is an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}? The first answer: {\i kol she\u8217'eino korei kri\u8217'as shema shacharis ve\u8217'arvis} \u8212- whoever does not recite {\i Kri\u8217'as Shema} morning and evening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shema Significance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This criterion is tied to a teaching elsewhere in the Gemara \u8212- in {\i Menachos}, if I recall correctly \u8212- that someone who recites {\i Kri\u8217'as Shema} morning and evening has fulfilled the verse {\i lo yamush sefer hatorah hazeh mipicha vehagisa bo yomam valaylah}: if you merely frame your day with {\i Torah} at its beginning and end, it is as if {\i Torah} has not departed from your mouth the entire day. The {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} who does not even recite {\i shema shacharis ve\u8217'arvis} is, in the Gemara\u8217's view, someone entirely outside the world of {\i Torah}. One thinks perhaps of the figure of Tevye in Fiddler on the Roof, who has almost certainly never learned a word of {\i Gemara} in his life, but who understands viscerally that sitting in the {\i beis knesses} and learning the holy books is what he should aspire to. That self-understanding \u8212- that connection, however tenuous \u8212- is what distinguishes him from someone who has entirely vacated the world of {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Berakhot Significance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds {\i u\u8217'birchasa} \u8212- even the {\i berachos} of {\i Shema} matter here, and notes that these are {\i miderabbanan}. This is significant: the {\i berachos} surrounding {\i Kri\u8217'as Shema} double as {\i birchos haTorah} \u8212- someone who forgets {\i birchos haTorah} can rely on the {\i berachos} of {\i Kri\u8217'as Shema}, because those blessings are fundamentally about God\u8217's having chosen us and given us {\i Torah}. They frame the recitation of {\i Shema} itself as an act of {\i Talmud Torah}. Being outside even that minimal rabbinic framing places a person outside the entire enterprise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- this is Rabbi Meir\u8217's position: the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} is defined by failure to recite {\i Shema} morning and evening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin Criterion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vachachamim omrim: kol she\u8217'eino meni\u8217'ach tefillin} \u8212- the Sages define the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} as one who does not put on {\i tefillin}. This criterion shifts the focus from {\i Torah} knowledge to a fundamental daily practice: even if you are not deeply learned, at minimum the wearing of {\i tefillin} marks your participation in the world of {\i mitzvos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin History\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is actually quite striking historically. We tend to hold a romanticized view of how universally observant communities were in earlier centuries. Yet we have evidence from the period of the {\i Rishonim} \u8212- I believe it is the {\i Smag}, though I am not certain \u8212- that there were communities where {\i tefillin} wearing was seriously challenged, and this authority would travel from town to town urging people to begin putting on {\i tefillin} and affixing {\i mezuzos} to their doors. The assumption that these practices were universal even in ostensibly observant communities may not be warranted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Symbolic Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a symbolic dimension worth noting. The {\i Shema} itself contains {\i vehayu le\u8217'os al yadecha} \u8212- and they shall be a sign upon your arm \u8212- and {\i uletotafos bein einecha} \u8212- and frontlets between your eyes. {\i Tefillin} is, in a sense, the physical embodiment of the {\i Shema}\u8217's own words: even if one is not engaged in daily {\i Torah} study, wearing {\i tefillin} represents a binding of {\i Torah} to oneself, an external sign of internal connection. Someone who does not even perform that minimal act of symbolic identification has removed himself from the chain entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ben Azzai omer: kol she\u8217'ein lo tzitzis bevigdo} \u8212- whoever does not have {\i tzitzis} on his garment. This too is part of the {\i parshiyos} read in {\i Kri\u8217'as Shema}, and similarly functions as an outward symbol of Jewish identity and connection to {\i Torah}, even apart from the level of one\u8217's actual learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yonatan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yonasan ben Yosef omer: kol sheyesh lo banim ve\u8217'eino megadlam leTalmud Torah} \u8212- whoever has children and does not raise them toward {\i Talmud Torah}. We are back here to the {\i Torah} axis. Significantly, the phrasing is not simply {\i eino melamdam Torah} \u8212- he does not teach them {\i Torah} \u8212- but {\i eino megadlam leTalmud Torah}, he does not raise them {\i toward} it. The question is whether one sees {\i Torah} education as an aspiration worth striving for and investing in, even if one cannot provide it directly. Is it something you see your children as needing? Does it matter to you?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acheirim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Acheirim omrim: afilu korei ve\u8217'shoneh velo shimish talmidei chachamim} \u8212- even one who has learned {\i Mikra} and {\i Mishna} but has not attended upon Torah sages \u8212- {\i zehu am ha\u8217'aretz}. This brings us back precisely to where we began in the prior discussion. Knowledge without genuine engagement \u8212- without the character development and deep understanding that come from {\i shimush} \u8212- does not lift a person out of the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Severity Scale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then establishes an escalating scale of severity. {\i Kara velo shanah} \u8212- one who knows {\i Mikra} but not {\i Mishna} \u8212- {\i harei zeh bur}, is a complete ignoramus in all of rabbinic discourse. And {\i lo kara velo shanah} \u8212- one who has learned nothing at all \u8212- {\i alav hakasuv omer}: the verse says {\i vezarati es beis Yisrael ve\u8217'es beis Yehudah zera adam vezera beheimah} \u8212- I will plant the house of Israel and the house of Judah with the seed of man and the seed of animals. In context, that verse is a {\i beracha} about the repopulation of the land. Here, it is deployed with the implicit suggestion that someone entirely outside the world of {\i Torah} is, in a certain sense, like an animal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women & Talmud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where do women fit within all these criteria for {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}? This is an entirely fair and pointed question. The Gemara has just laid out a comprehensive taxonomy \u8212- reciting {\i Shema}, wearing {\i tefillin}, wearing {\i tzitzis}, raising children for {\i Talmud Torah}, pursuing {\i shimush talmidei chachamim} \u8212- and women are exempt from, or simply absent from, most of these categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us back directly to the opening discussion. Juxtapose {\i kol hamelamed bito Torah ke\u8217'ilu limdah tiflus} with the full weight of everything the Gemara has now said about what it means to be disconnected from {\i Torah} \u8212- the escalating scale from {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} to {\i bur} to {\i kuti} to {\i magus} \u8212- and the tension becomes very sharp indeed. Women are told, in one tradition, that too much {\i Torah} learning is a problem; and yet the framework the Gemara has built places the absence of {\i Torah} engagement among the most troubling characteristics a person can have.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Am Ha\u8217'aretz (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosfot Complexity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} raises a critical point that cannot be passed over: {\i teima lari de\u8217'amar perek Hanizakin} \u8212- there is a {\i baraita} in {\i Gittin} that defines {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} as whoever does not eat {\i chullin} in a state of {\i tahara}, {\i divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- and the criteria there are entirely different from what we have here. {\i Tosfos} resolves this by stating {\i upirush deharbei inyanei am ha\u8217'aretz hein} \u8212- {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} means different things in different contexts. Sometimes the term disqualifies a person from serving as a witness; sometimes it concerns trustworthiness regarding {\i tithes} and {\i purity}; sometimes it touches on {\i zun}, {\i shemitah}, or the acquisition of certain goods. The categories can be contradictory with one another precisely because they are not describing a single unified group.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point has broader historiographic significance. There has been considerable scholarly work \u8212- including a paper by Yair Furstenberg on the status of {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} in {\i Chazal} \u8212- arguing that we should not assume the Rabbis were always referring to a single identifiable sociological group. The term may function more like a concept \u8212- a flexible category that plays different roles in different {\i halachic} and social contexts. There was certainly a time when it referred to a very concrete population. But once it enters the fabric of rabbinic literature as a concept, it begins to operate in more abstract ways, and the contradictions between different {\i gemarot} reflect that flexibility rather than simple inconsistency. The parallel in our own world might be the whole spectrum of what it means to call someone \u8220"not {\i frum}\u8221" \u8212- a label that encompasses an enormous range of people and behaviors, none of which are really equivalent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites: {\i yera Hashem beni umelech, ve\u8217'im shonim al titarev} \u8212- fear God and the king, and do not mix with those who are {\i shonim}. {\i Amar Rabbi Yitzchak: elu sheshonim halachos} \u8212- these are people who merely recite {\i halachos} by rote. One might think {\i halachos} sounds more advanced than {\i Mishna} \u8212- you are actually studying law. But {\i halachos} here refers to apodictic legal statements memorized without any underlying discussion or reasoning, which is functionally equivalent to the problem already described. And of course, there is the famous teaching {\i kol hashoneh halachos bechol yom muvtach lo shehu ben olam haba} \u8212- one who reviews {\i halachos} every day is assured a share in the world to come. The Gemara addresses this: the problem is not the memorization itself but thinking one can {\i pasken} from it. {\i Mahu deteima} \u8212- one might have thought {\i shonim becheta} meant repeated sinners, as per Rav Huna\u8217's teaching {\i keivan she\u8217'avar adam aveirah veshana bah hutra lo} \u8212- once a person transgresses and repeats it, it becomes, as it were, permitted to him; or as Rav Yisrael Salanter reportedly added, if you do it a third time it becomes a {\i mitzvah}. But no \u8212- {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- the {\i shonim} here are those who merely mouth {\i mishnayos} without comprehension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaim & Memorization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Atanna\u8217'im meval\u8217'ei olam} \u8212- the {\i tanna\u8217'im}, the memorizers of {\i Mishna}, destroy the world. One can appreciate the irony: the {\i tanna} who had to memorize this {\i baraisa} was memorizing a statement about how memorizers destroy the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mavlei olam salka daitach?} \u8212- Do they really destroy the world? {\i Amar Ravina}: of course not. The problem is not memorization itself \u8212- we need those people to preserve the {\i Mishna} with accuracy. The problem is {\i she\u8217'morim halacha mitoch mishnasam} \u8212- that they believe they can {\i pasken halachah} directly from what they have memorized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanya nami hachi, amar Rabbi Yehoshua: vechi mavlei olam hein? Vahala miyashvei olam hein} \u8212- are they not in fact settlers of the world? \u8212- {\i shenemar halichos olam lo}. The entire Talmudic discussion is built upon the foundation of memorized {\i mishnayos}. {\i Kol hashoneh halachos bechol yom} is the indispensable substrate. Without accurate memorization, there is no tradition to discuss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela she\u8217'morim halacha mitoch mishnasam} \u8212- the problem is not memorizing, it is thinking one can issue rulings on its basis. The {\i tanna} who memorizes is {\i miyashvei olam}; the one who thinks his memorization qualifies him to {\i pasken} is {\i mevallei olam}. The distinction matters enormously, and the Gemara\u8217's care in making it reflects a deep awareness of how dangerous a little learning can be when misapplied.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mavlei Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isha Perushah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Isha perushah} \u8212- the woman who presents herself as separated from physical pleasures \u8212- is among the {\i mavlei olam}, the destroyers of the world. The skepticism, as established earlier, is that her presentation of austere piety conceals something else entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now adds additional categories: {\i tanu rabanan, besulah tzalya-nis} \u8212- a praying virgin \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'almanah shovevis} \u8212- a wandering widow \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'katan shelo kalu chodashav} \u8212- a minor whose months have not been completed \u8212- {\i harei elu mavlei olam}, these destroy the world. The {\i besulah tzalya-nis}, according to {\i Rashi}, is someone always engaged in prayer and devotion, projecting an image of extraordinary piety. The {\i almanah shovevis} is a widow constantly traveling through the community, visiting everyone. {\i Rashi} suggests she is a gossip \u8212- though as was noted, that does not perfectly fit the model of someone presenting themselves as other than they are; perhaps the better reading is that she visits presenting herself as motivated by care and concern when her real motivation is social engagement or information-gathering. The {\i katan shelo kalu chodashav} the Gemara will clarify momentarily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pious Virgin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'amar Rabbi Yochanan: lamadnu yiras cheit mibesulah} \u8212- we can learn fear of sin from the young woman, {\i ve\u8217'kibul sachar mei\u8217'almanah} \u8212- and the meaning of receiving reward from the widow. What is the story of the {\i besulah}? {\i Rabbi Yochanan shama lah le\u8217'hahi besulah de\u8217'nafla apei} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan heard this young woman fall on her face in prayer \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'amra: ribbono shel olam, baras Gan Eden u\u8217'varas Gehennom, baras tzaddikim baras reshaim} \u8212- You have created Paradise and {\i Gehennom}, righteous people and wicked people \u8212- {\i yehi ratzon milfanecha shelo yikashlu bi bnei adam} \u8212- may it be Your will that people not stumble through me, that men not sin on my account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problematic Aspects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is presented as a model of {\i yiras cheit} \u8212- and in one sense it is striking. True {\i yiras cheit} is not merely fear that {\i I} will sin, but fear that I might inadvertently cause {\i others} to sin. One is reminded of the prayer recited before entering the {\i beis hamidrash} \u8212- {\i shelo yikashlu}, that my colleagues not stumble through anything I say. To be so attuned to the possibility of causing harm, even unintentionally \u8212- that is a profound expression of moral seriousness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, there is something deeply troubling here. {\i Yiras cheit} properly begins with concern for one\u8217's own conduct. The framing of this prayer \u8212- in which a woman\u8217's central religious preoccupation is with male desire and male sin, and in which she understands her own body as the primary locus of danger \u8212- reflects and reinscribes a way of thinking about women that is, to put it plainly, very deeply problematic. It connects to the entire fabric of {\i Maseches Sotah} and its preoccupations. These difficulties do not disappear simply because the text presents it as admirable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous Widow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The story of the {\i almanah} who demonstrates {\i kibul sachar}: there was a certain widow in whose neighborhood there was a synagogue, yet every day she would come and pray in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's {\i beis hamidrash} rather than in the closer {\i beis haknesseth}. Rabbi Yochanan said to her: {\i biti, lo beis haknesseth be\u8217'shivvuseich?} \u8212- is there not a synagogue in your community? She replied: {\i rabbi, lo sechar psios yesh li} \u8212- do I not receive the reward for the extra walking? The lesson in {\i kibul sachar}: she extended herself beyond what was required, internalizing a disposition of exerting extra effort for the sake of {\i mitzvos} even when the effort was not strictly necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Raised\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet both examples are, on closer examination, somewhat awkward as ideals. The {\i yiras cheit} of the young woman is entangled with the male gaze and the internalization of responsibility for male desire. The {\i almanah}\u8217's extra walking, while representing admirable devotion, raises its own questions \u8212- {\i is} it better to pray in a {\i beis hamidrash} than a {\i beis haknesseth}? And can it be that this widow is also, perhaps, seeking connection to the world of {\i Torah}, which may be why she extends herself specifically to come to Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's {\i beis hamidrash} rather than the local synagogue? Perhaps that dimension is part of the point. But regardless, both examples are in some sense extreme, and one must ask whether extremity in this direction is genuinely praiseworthy or instead reflects an excess that has its own distortions. How can the Gemara claim these figures are {\i mavlei olam} when the examples are so clearly righteous?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension: {\i ki ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the original teaching does not refer to all widows and virgins, but to specific types. {\i Kegon Yochni bat Retivi} \u8212- like this woman Yochni bat Retivi. {\i Rashi} explains \u8212- drawing on what appears to be a {\i mesorah} not found elsewhere in rabbinic literature \u8212- that this woman was a witch who made her {\i parnassa} as a midwife: she would use incantations to prevent a woman from giving birth, then be called in to help, reverse the incantations, and earn a great reputation. The details of {\i Rashi}\u8217's source are unclear, and he sometimes provides {\i aggadic} backdrop that does not appear in other texts, but the point is clear: it is {\i that} kind of widow who is {\i mavlei olam}, not every widow who moves through her community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Young Scholar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Back to {\i katan shelo kalu chodashav}: {\i hacha targimu} \u8212- here in {\i Bavel} they explained it as {\i zeh talmid chacham hamiva\u8217'eit berabbotav} \u8212- a young Torah scholar who is dismissive and disrespectful of his teachers and of those with greater age and authority, overly impressed with his own attainments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gap Year Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} quotes a {\i Yerushalmi}: {\i Rabbi Chilkiya beshem Rabbi Simon: zeh shehu gadol baTorah shelo befirko, umevazeh et hagedolim mimenu} \u8212- someone who is genuinely beyond his years in {\i Torah} knowledge and therefore feels entitled to denigrate those who are chronologically or institutionally his seniors. This is, regrettably, a recognizable phenomenon: students who return from a year of intensive study abroad suddenly finding themselves, in their own estimation, the world\u8217's greatest {\i talmidei chachamim}, while their community rabbi appears to them as inadequate. The brilliance is real; the judgment is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Abba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Abba amar: zeh talmid shelo higia lehora\u8217'ah} \u8212- not the relationship to others but the effect on the community: a student who has not reached the level of {\i hora\u8217'ah} and yet issues rulings. This is what makes such a person {\i mavlei olam} in the deepest sense. And the failure here is not necessarily one of knowledge. A person might be genuinely brilliant and yet not ready to {\i pasken}, because {\i hora\u8217'ah} demands wisdom, understanding of people, judgment about circumstances \u8212- not merely {\i sefarim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Piskening Readiness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i D\u8217'amar Rabbi Abbahu amar Rav Huna amar Rav: mai d\u8217'chtiv, ki rabim chalalim hipila} \u8212- what is the meaning of the verse \u8220"many are those she has caused to fall\u8221"? (The verse, drawn from the context of a seductive woman, is now applied to an entirely different situation.) {\i Zeh talmid chacham shelo higia lehora\u8217'ah u\u8217'moreh} \u8212- this refers to a {\i talmid chacham} who has not yet reached the level of {\i hora\u8217'ah} and yet rules. {\i Rashi} notes a play on {\i hipila} and {\i nefel} \u8212- a premature birth \u8212- connecting back to {\i katan shelo kalu chodashav}: like an infant born before its time, this scholar\u8217's rulings are a kind of miscarriage of judgment. He is, in effect, murdering people through his premature {\i psak}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opposite Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'atzumim kol harugeha} \u8212- \u8220"and great are all those she has slaughtered.\u8221" {\i Zeh talmid chacham shehigia lehora\u8217'ah ve\u8217'eino moreh} \u8212- this refers to a {\i talmid chacham} who has reached the level of {\i hora\u8217'ah} and does not rule. If you are qualified to {\i pasken} and you withhold that service, you are equally culpable \u8212- perhaps more so. You leave the field open to those who are not qualified, you fail the people who need your guidance, and you close your eyes to your own responsibility. {\i Rashi} reads {\i atzumim} as a play on {\i otzem einav} \u8212- closing one\u8217's eyes. The {\i Maharsha} reads it differently: {\i atzum} means great, powerful \u8212- you are great, you are fit, and yet you do not act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Moshe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This tension is captured with great personal immediacy in Rav Moshe Feinstein\u8217's introduction to the {\i Igros Moshe}. He writes that he was deeply hesitant to {\i pasken} \u8212- who was he to rule, and what if he ruled incorrectly? The Gemara itself warns: {\i zeh talmid shelo higia lehora\u8217'ah u\u8217'moreh, rabim chalalim hipila}. Yet Rav Moshe could not simply decline to {\i pasken}, because the very next line says {\i ve\u8217'atzumim kol harugeha} \u8212- if you are {\i higia lehora\u8217'ah ve\u8217'eino moreh}, you are equally causing destruction. The obligation is real and cannot be evaded by excessive humility once one has genuinely reached the level of {\i hora\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 40 Years\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'ad kamah} \u8212- until what age must one wait? {\i Arbaim shanah} \u8212- forty years. {\i Aini?} \u8212- Is this really so? {\i Ve\u8217'ha Rabbah ori} \u8212- Rabbah ruled on {\i halachic} matters, and he lived only until forty! The Gemara answers: {\i b\u8217'shavi} \u8212- only if one is equivalent to the {\i poskim} of one\u8217's generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosfot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a notable dispute between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosfos} about how to read this. {\i Rashi} holds that if you are equivalent to the {\i poskim} of your generation, you may rule even before forty \u8212- you are only prohibited from ruling before forty if you are below their level. {\i Tosfos} reads it differently: equivalence alone is insufficient \u8212- only if you are better than everyone around you may you rule before forty; equivalence requires waiting. The distinction is between adequacy and superiority as the threshold for early {\i hora\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} also clarifies that forty years does not mean forty years of age but forty years of learning \u8212- which would imply an even later start for most people. Yet one can understand the number in terms of what it was trying to achieve: {\i psak} requires not just accumulated knowledge but life experience, judgment, and knowledge of people \u8212- not only of {\i sefarim}. And contemporary {\i poskim} note that much of the reason for that lengthy formation was the sheer volume of material that had to be internalized from memory. Now that all the material is available in written and printed form, some of those considerations change substantially.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seven Types of Prushim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machos Perushim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'makkos perushim} \u8212- the smitings of the ascetics \u8212- constitute the final category of those who destroy the world. The term {\i perushim} here carries a double valence: it means both Pharisees and people who separate themselves from ordinary life in the pursuit of piety. {\i Rashi}\u8217's understanding is that these are people who engage in self-flagellation and the externals of extreme piety while fundamentally missing what righteousness actually requires \u8212- and worse, presenting that performance to the world, perhaps even to themselves, as genuine holiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu Rabbanan: shiva perushim hein} \u8212- there are seven types of false pietists. {\i Parush Shichmi, Parush Nikpi, Parush Kizai, Parush Meduchya, Parush Machovasi}, {\i Parush Mei\u8217'ahava}, and {\i Parush Mi\u8217'yira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shichmi Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Parush Shichmi: zeh oseh ma\u8217'aseh Shechem} \u8212- one who performs the deeds of Shechem. {\i Rashi} explains: the people of Shechem underwent {\i bris milah} not out of genuine religious conviction but to serve ulterior interests. The act was performed but for entirely the wrong reason. The {\i Maharsha} adds a sharper dimension: the sequence matters. Shechem first committed a terrible act and then undertook {\i bris milah} \u8212- so the religious performance functions as a cover-up for prior wrongdoing. It is not even simply misguided motivation; it is a kind of religious whitewashing of genuine transgression. The principle {\i mitoch shelo lishmah ba lishmah} \u8212- that improper motivation can eventually lead to proper motivation \u8212- does not apply when the religious act is being used to conceal something already done wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nikpi Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Parush Nikpi: zeh hemenakeif et raglav} \u8212- who walks very daintily. {\i Rashi} says this person minces his steps to project an image of humility and piety. It is entirely performative \u8212- a presentation of holiness through gait, with nothing behind it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kizai Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Parush Kizai} \u8212- {\i kizai} meaning bloodletting. {\i Amar Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak: zeh hamakiz dam lektalin} \u8212- one who bangs into walls and causes himself to bleed. {\i Rashi}: this person is too scrupulously {\i frum} to look at a woman and therefore walks into walls. The absurdity is intentional \u8212- and it calls to mind the experience of excessive scrupulosity taken to a self-harmful extreme.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meduchya Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Parush Meduchya: demishapei k\u8217'meduchya} \u8212- bent over like a pestle. The pestle has a distinctive curved shape. This person walks hunched over to display humility. Again: purely external show, with no genuine substance behind it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machovasi Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Parush Machovasi}: this person says {\i mah chovati tu va\u8217'esenu} \u8212- tell me what my obligation is and I will fulfill it. At first the Gemara protests: {\i ma\u8217'alyusa hi} \u8212- that is admirable! Tell me what I need to do and I will do it. But no \u8212- the {\i Gemara} clarifies: the problem is the full phrasing: \u8220"I have done everything required of me; {\i if} there is anything more, tell me and I will do it.\u8221" The person presents themselves as having already discharged all responsibilities, seeking only to identify any remaining obligations. The posture of prior completion is the problem \u8212- the claim to have already done everything, combined with the performance of eagerness to do more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ahava & Yira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Parush Mei\u8217'ahava} and {\i Parush Mi\u8217'yira} \u8212- motivated by love of reward or fear of punishment. These sound problematic but are actually defended. {\i Amar lei Abaye ve\u8217'Rava letana: ha lo tisni} \u8212- do not include these in the problematic list. Even if the {\i ahava} is love of reward and the {\i yira} is fear of punishment \u8212- improper motivations \u8212- these are not categorically bad.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De\u8217'amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav: le\u8217'olam ya\u8217'asok adam beTorah u\u8217'vemitzvos af al pi shelo lishmah, shemitoch shelo lishmah ba lishmah} \u8212- a person should always engage in {\i Torah} and {\i mitzvos} even if not {\i lishmah}, because acting {\i shelo lishmah} will ultimately lead to {\i lishmah}. The problem with all the other false pietists was that they were presenting themselves as something other than what they were. Here the motivation is impure, but it is not a deception that harms others \u8212- and it carries within itself the seed of genuine transformation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hidden vs.\u160 ?Revealed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidden vs.\u160 ?Revealed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak: demitamera mitamera, demigalya migalya} \u8212- what is hidden stays hidden, what is revealed stays revealed. We have just spent considerable time cataloguing people who present as something they are not. The practical reality is: we know what we know. We cannot peer behind the facade. What is hidden from us remains hidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Justice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis dina rabba le\u8217'iparei mehani dechafu gundei} \u8212- but the ultimate High Court will ultimately bring justice to those who have wrapped themselves in a {\i tallis} and presented as pious when they were not. Human judgment is limited; divine judgment is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King Yannai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Yannai Malka l\u8217'devasei} \u8212- King Yannai, who had caused the deaths of many of the Sages, says to his wife on his deathbed: {\i al tishari min haPerushim} \u8212- do not fear from the Pharisees. And {\i velo mi\u8217'mi she\u8217'einam Perushim} \u8212- nor from those who are not Pharisees. The genuinely righteous will not harm you even after what was done to them; the genuinely wicked are no threat to you either, as they have no moral standing to act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzvu\u8217'im Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela min hatzvu\u8217'in} \u8212- fear only the {\i tzvu\u8217'im}, the chameleons \u8212- {\i she\u8217'domin laPerushim}, who appear to be ascetics and Pharisees, {\i shema\u8217'aseihem ma\u8217'aseh Zimri}, but whose deeds are the deeds of Zimri \u8212- who violate the most fundamental prohibitions \u8212- {\i umevakshim sachar k\u8217'Pinchas}, while demanding reward as if they were Pinchas.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Image\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the closing image of the {\i daf}, and it is a powerful one. The Gemara has traveled a long arc: from the question of women\u8217's {\i Torah} education and the concern about those who appear as what they are not, through the {\i rasha arum} and his many incarnations, through the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} and {\i bur} and {\i kuti} and {\i magus}, through the {\i tanna\u8217'im} who cannot {\i pasken} and the scholars who can but will not, through the seven false pietists \u8212- and arrives here at the {\i tzvu\u8217'im}: people who act like Zimri and claim the reward of Pinchas. They are, in the Gemara\u8217's reckoning, the most dangerous figures of all \u8212- not the openly wicked, not the genuinely righteous, but those who commit transgressions while wrapping themselves in the garments of holiness. Against them, human vigilance has limits. Only divine judgment, in the end, can see through.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 23\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zimri & Pinchas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zimri & Pinchas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to the close of the {\i aggadata} passage that concluded the previous amud. The Gemara had been exploring the phenomenon of women who appear one way and are in reality another \u8212- the {\i isha perusha} who presents outward piety while concealing inner corruption. But as the discussion developed, the Gemara broadened its scope considerably, recognizing this as a wider human phenomenon that applies to men no less than to women. This broadening itself is worth noting: even within a tractate devoted to the {\i sotah}, the Gemara is attuned to the question of whether we are examining something specific to women\u8217's sin or something universal in human nature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Broader Phenomenon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final line of the {\i aggadata} before the {\i mishna} is worth savoring for the precision with which it captures a particular personality type \u8212- one that has since become a stock figure in Rabbinic literature: {\i ma\u8217'aseihem kema\u8217'asei Zimri u\u8217'mevakshim sachar kePinchas} \u8212- they act like Zimri yet expect to be rewarded like Pinchas. The figure is not merely about duplicity before others, the hypocrite who performs righteousness publicly while sinning in private. At a deeper level, it describes people who have somehow convinced themselves of their own righteousness, who have internalized the self-image of Pinchas while their conduct mirrors Zimri.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zenut & Kina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pairing of Zimri and Pinchas is especially resonant in this context. Zimri sins through public sexual transgression with a Midianite woman, while Pinchas is the {\i kanai}, the zealot, whose defining act is {\i kina} \u8212- zealotry. The concept of {\i kina} runs through the Torah on multiple levels. On one plane it describes the jealous husband; on another it describes God\u8217's own demand for exclusive fidelity: {\i El kana hu}, God is a jealous God. The metaphor of Israel\u8217's worship of other gods is framed throughout the Torah as {\i zenut} \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'zanah acharei elohei nechar ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- a betrayal of the covenantal relationship with God, depicted as a kind of spiritual adultery. Pinchas acts {\i asher kina l\u8217'elohav}, as one who is jealous on God\u8217's behalf, because Zimri\u8217's act was simultaneously an act of physical {\i zenut} and a form of abandonment of God through entanglement with the deities of Midian. The intersection of the metaphorical {\i zenut} and the literal {\i zenut} is striking, and it also reinforces that this passage is not exclusively about women \u8212- it is about men, about the fundamental human capacity for self-deception in matters of fidelity and devotion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zechut Toleh Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon: No Zechut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now opens with a striking position: {\i Rabbi Shimon omer ein zechut tola b\u8217'mayim hamarim} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon flatly denies that merit ever caused the bitter waters to suspend their judgment. It is worth pausing on the epistemological strangeness of this debate. Generations after the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, the Tannaim are debating what actually occurred when a woman drank the {\i mayim hamarim}. No doubt all parties could observe that sometimes a woman drank the water and nothing happened. The question is how to interpret that fact: does it mean she was genuinely innocent, or that her {\i zechut} suspended the judgment? The observable facts may have been shared; the dispute is about what those facts mean.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Trust Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's argument against {\i zechut tola} is primarily a policy argument. {\i Ve\u8217'im atah omer hazechut tola} \u8212- if you were to say that merit can suspend the effects of the waters, then {\i madhe ata et hamayim bifnei kol hanashim} \u8212- you undermine the waters\u8217' credibility in the eyes of all women. The {\i Sifrei} formulates this as {\i makhshikh gavno}, you cause it to become blurry, to lose its potency in the public\u8217's perception. The image is almost that of muddying the water itself. Two distinct harms follow. First, people will see a woman emerge unaffected and say: I know this woman committed adultery, and yet nothing happened \u8212- the water does not work. Second, the innocent women suffer: {\i motzi shem ra al hatehorot}. Women who were genuinely innocent and whose innocence was confirmed by the waters will now be suspected retroactively \u8212- people will say, {\i tme\u8217'iot hayu}, they were really guilty, {\i ela shetaltah lahen hazchut}, it is just that their merit suspended the punishment. For Rabbi Shimon, this double injury to the institution\u8217's integrity proves it cannot be so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further note the practical problem raised in this connection: if {\i zechut tola} were operative, how would a husband ever know that he could resume marital relations with his wife? You might answer that he works with the {\i chazakah} \u8212- what has been observed provides a presumptive basis for conduct. But once you introduce the possibility of {\i zechut tola}, you have complicated that presumption considerably, and the question of when the husband and wife may resume their relationship becomes genuinely difficult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbi: Zechut Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi disagrees. {\i Hazechut tola bamayim hamererim} \u8212- merit does suspend the judgment of the bitter waters. But Rebbi\u8217's position is more nuanced than simply affirming {\i zechut tola} without qualification. He adds: {\i ve\u8217'einah yoledet ve\u8217'einah meshabbachat} \u8212- even when merit suspends the negative judgment, the positive effect of the waters is not realized either. The waters, had she been innocent, would have brought enhanced fertility \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'niketah ve\u8217'nizre\u8217'ah zara}. If she is guilty but her {\i zechut} intervenes, she escapes the physical destruction, but she does not receive the blessing. She does not bear children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Observable Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi\u8217's answer to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's concern is built on observability. If a woman drank the waters and afterward bore healthy children and her body flourished, people can see she was innocent. If a woman drank and received no blessing of children, people will say: the {\i pasuk} promises {\i ve\u8217'niketah ve\u8217'nizre\u8217'ah zara} \u8212- and she has not been {\i nizre\u8217'ah zara}. We can infer that her {\i zechut} must have been operative. Rebbi goes further still: {\i ela mitnavnevet ve\u8217'holekhet} \u8212- not only does she fail to bear children, but she actually deteriorates. And {\i sof hi meta be\u8217'otah mitah} \u8212- ultimately she dies from it, in the same recognizable way, only stretched over a longer period. So the public will see a clear spectrum: women who drank and flourished, whose innocence is confirmed, and women who drank and deteriorated, whose guilt is evident even if the timeline is extended. This, Rebbi argues, is a reasonable answer to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's concerns about the water\u8217's reputation. What is striking throughout this entire discussion is how the Tannaim construct the reality of what {\i must} have happened based on what the halacha and policy require \u8212- an argument from necessity rather than from direct observation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mincha Tamei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now transitions, somewhat abruptly, back to the {\i mincha} of the {\i sotah}. The assumed order of the {\i sotah} procedure is: the {\i mincha} is brought and offered, and then she drinks the water \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'achar yashkeh et hamayim}. The {\i mishna} doubles back, however, to address what happens if the {\i mincha} becomes {\i tamei} at different stages of the process. If the {\i mincha} became {\i tamei} before it was placed into the {\i kli sharet}, it is treated like any other {\i mincha} in that pre-sanctified state. At that stage it has only {\i kedushat peh} \u8212- a sanctity conferred through verbal designation \u8212- and such sanctity can be transferred: the {\i mincha} can be redeemed for money and a new {\i mincha} brought in its place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the {\i mincha} has been sanctified by the {\i kli sharet}, however, it acquires {\i kedushat haguf} \u8212- an intrinsic, constitutive sanctity that defines its very identity rather than being merely instilled upon it. Once something attains {\i kedushat haguf}, it cannot be redeemed and cannot be removed from the {\i azarah}. If it subsequently becomes {\i tamei}, it must be burned within the {\i azarah}. This is not a law specific to the {\i minchat sotah}; it is the universal rule for all {\i menachot}. The {\i mishna} establishes this baseline in order to set up the more specific cases that follow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mincha Burned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Mincha Burned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established when an ordinary {\i mincha} must be burned on account of {\i tum\u8217'ah}, the {\i mishna} now introduces a related but distinct category: {\i ve\u8217'elu sheminchoteihem nisrafot} \u8212- the following women are those whose {\i mincha} is burned even though it has not become {\i tamei}. The {\i minchat sotah} has a set of unique circumstances under which the offering is rendered without purpose, and in those cases, since the {\i mincha} has already been sanctified in the vessel and cannot be redeemed, it must be burned. The fire is not on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- it is on the ash pile within the {\i azarah}, where {\i pesulim} are burned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i ha\u8217'omeret tme\u8217'ah ani lach} \u8212- if the woman confesses her guilt. She is not subject to execution under these circumstances, since there were no witnesses and no formal warning, but her confession renders the entire {\i sotah} procedure moot. There is no longer any purpose for the {\i mincha}. Since it has been sanctified, it cannot be redeemed, so it is burned, and she is divorced without receiving her {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: witnesses come forward and testify to her guilt. Even without a formal confession, the existence of witness testimony eliminates the need for the ordeal. Again the {\i mincha} is burned, and she is divorced without her {\i kesubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refuses to Drink\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case: {\i ha\u8217'omeret eini shota} \u8212- she refuses to drink. This is a kind of {\i nolo contendere}: she neither admits guilt nor contests the charges, but she refuses to submit to the process. The result is that she is divorced without her {\i kesubah}, since she is the one obstructing the mechanism by which her innocence could be established. The husband remains in a state of prohibition relative to her, and since she is preventing resolution, she bears the consequence. The {\i mincha} is burned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth case is distinctive in that the fault lies with the husband: {\i ve-she-ba\u8217'alah ba aleha baderech} \u8212- the husband had sexual relations with her after she became a {\i sotah} and before the ordeal was completed. The Torah conditions the efficacy of the waters on the husband being {\i nikah me\u8217'avon} \u8212- free from sin in this matter. If he is not, the waters will not work. Once it is known that this condition has been violated, there is no point in proceeding with the {\i mincha} or the ordeal, and the {\i mincha} is not brought. In this case, since it is the husband\u8217's conduct that has nullified the process, she would presumably receive her {\i kesubah} \u8212- though the {\i mishna} notes this as a matter to be examined further.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife of Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shared Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now arrives at an entirely different category of {\i nisrafot}: {\i ve-chol hanesu\u8217'ot le-kohanim minchateihem nisrafot} \u8212- all women married to {\i kohanim} have their {\i mincha} burned. This burning is of a different character from the burning of the {\i pesul} cases above. The rule of {\i minchat kohen kalil tihyeh lo te\u8217'achel} \u8212- the {\i mincha} of a {\i kohen} must be entirely consumed, burned on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- applies here. The question is why the wife of a {\i kohen} should have her {\i mincha} treated as a {\i minchat kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that the husband is considered a part-owner of the {\i mincha}. Rashi explains this in relation to the broader rule that a husband is obligated to pay for his wife\u8217's obligatory {\i korbanot} \u8212- a principle derived from the Gemara in Nazir. The husband foots the bill; she is the {\i mitkapeir}, the one for whom the atonement is being made and on whose behalf the offering is brought. Tosafot raises the question of whether this applies equally to a {\i minchat nedava}, a voluntary offering, since the financial obligation of the husband is specifically tied to obligatory {\i korbanot}. There, perhaps, he does not bear the cost even if, technically, {\i kinyan isha kana ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- the husband\u8217's acquisition of his wife entails some rights in her property. But for the {\i minchat sotah} specifically, the case is especially strong: {\i ve-hevi ha\u8217'ish et ishto} \u8212- the Torah frames the entire process as the husband bringing his wife. It says {\i ve-hevi et korbanah aleha} \u8212- he is the one who brings the {\i korban} on her behalf. More than any other case where the husband merely writes the check, here the Torah explicitly casts him as the active agent bringing the offering for her. Consequently, it is both her {\i mincha} and his {\i mincha}, and since it is his {\i mincha}, the rule of {\i minchat kohen} applies and the remainder may not be eaten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Beis Hadeshen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi, however, reads the {\i mishna}\u8217's term {\i nisrafot} differently. In his commentary, Rashi states: {\i ve\u8217'afillu karav hakometz ke-hilchato ve-lo nitme\u8217'u nisrafim shirayim be-veit hadeshen} \u8212- even though the {\i kometz} was offered properly and nothing became {\i tamei}, the {\i shirayim} are burned in the {\i beis hadeshen}. On Rashi\u8217's reading, the burning of the remainder is not a burning on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} as a true {\i minchat kohen} would be, but rather a burning on the ash pile within the {\i azarah}, the same place where {\i pesulim} are burned. This anticipates the Gemara\u8217's discussion of two competing positions: is the remainder burned on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} as befits a genuine {\i minchat kohen}, or is it burned in the {\i beis hadeshen} because this {\i mincha} is a hybrid of two statuses? The {\i beis hadeshen}, it should be noted, is located within the {\i azarah} itself \u8212- it is not {\i chutz la-machaneh} \u8212- but it is distinct from the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Differences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohenet Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then pivots to a broader discussion, introduced by the contrast between the wife of a {\i kohen} and a {\i kohenet} \u8212- a woman who is herself a daughter of a {\i kohen} \u8212- who marries an Israelite. {\i Ve-hakohenet she-niset le-Yisrael minchatah ne\u8217'echelet} \u8212- her {\i mincha} is eaten in the normal fashion, because she carries no {\i kohen} status herself and her husband is not a {\i kohen}, so the {\i mincha} is treated as a standard {\i minchat Yisrael}. The {\i kometz} is burned on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} and the {\i shirayim} are eaten by the {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the terminology here. Throughout the Talmud, a woman who is a daughter of a {\i kohen} is almost always referred to as {\i bat kohen}. The usage of {\i kohenet} is relatively unusual. It is striking that a {\i mishna} which calls her {\i kohenet} proceeds to demonstrate that she has virtually no {\i kohen} status in practice. The identity of the tribe, particularly with respect to priestly lineage, flows through the male line. A woman\u8217's tribal identity is defined by the man she marries, not the family she was born into. This is reflected in law throughout: a {\i bat kohen} who marries an Israelite loses access to {\i terumah}; a woman who marries a {\i kohen} gains access to it. The discussion of the {\i mishna} is organized around a question: {\i mah bein kohen le-kohenet} \u8212- what is the difference between a {\i kohen} and a {\i kohenet}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i kohenet mitchalelet, ve-kohen eino mitchalel} \u8212- a {\i kohenet} can have her status compromised; a {\i kohen} cannot. The concept of {\i chalalah} refers to a woman whose priestly fitness has been violated, rendering her forbidden to a {\i kohen}. This status does not belong only to daughters of {\i kohanim} \u8212- any woman, whether {\i bat kohen} or {\i bat Yisrael}, who engages in certain categories of forbidden sexual acts becomes {\i pesulah le-kohen}. For example, an {\i almanah} who has relations with a {\i kohen gadol} becomes a {\i chalalah} and is forbidden even to a {\i kohen hedyot}. The act of sex is what determines her status; the precise scope of the husband\u8217's transgression \u8212- whether his violation requires {\i kiddushin} or obtains even without it \u8212- is a {\i machloket rishonim}, with the Rambam holding that the {\i kohen} only transgresses in the context of {\i kiddushin} while other {\i rishonim} disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is remarkable, and what the {\i mishna} draws attention to, is the asymmetry. A {\i kohen} who commits any sexual sin under the sun does not lose his {\i kehuna} status through his own action. His sanctity is not penetrable from without. He can be born a {\i chalal} \u8212- if he is the product of a forbidden union \u8212- but a born {\i kohen}\u8217's status cannot be forfeited through his own conduct. There are {\i halachot} of social censure \u8212- a {\i kohen} currently married to a woman he is forbidden to marry is not permitted to {\i duchan}, for example \u8212- but these are social mechanisms of pressure, not a change in fundamental status. The disparity here mirrors a broader asymmetry: for a married woman, sexual infidelity constitutes adultery and violates her personal status; for a married man, sexual relations with an unmarried woman are not technically adultery in the same sense, and his personal status is not affected. The woman can be rendered {\i tamei} and forbidden; the man\u8217's status persists unchanged. This is reflected as well in the {\i kehuna} context: the woman\u8217's priestly fitness can be violated; the man\u8217's cannot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Differences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i kohenet mitame\u8217'ah le-meitim, ve-ein kohen mitame\u8217'ah le-meitim} \u8212- a daughter of a {\i kohen} is permitted to become {\i tamei} through contact with the dead; a male {\i kohen} is not. The source for this distinction is {\i emor el ha-kohanim benei Aharon} \u8212- the prohibition of {\i tum\u8217'at meit} is addressed to {\i benei Aharon}, the sons of Aaron, {\i ve-lo benot Aharon} \u8212- not the daughters of Aaron. The {\i Tosefta} also notes the related case, conspicuous in its absence from the {\i mishna}, that a {\i kohen} serves on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- {\i kohen omed u-makriv al gabei ha-mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- which plainly does not apply to a {\i kohenet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating Offerings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes: {\i kohen ochel be-kodshei kodashim, ve-ein kohenet ochelet be-kodshei kodashim} \u8212- a {\i kohen} eats from the {\i kodshei kodashim} such as {\i chatat} and {\i asham} within the {\i azarah}, but a {\i kohenet} does not. The source is {\i kol zachar bi-vnei Aharon yochlenu} \u8212- all males among the sons of Aaron shall eat from it. The Torah is explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary: Kohenet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony of this entire {\i mishna} is profound. It designates the woman as {\i kohenet} \u8212- a term that implies an affirmative priestly identity \u8212- yet enumerates exclusively cases where she lacks any distinct status on account of her father being a {\i kohen}. Every item in the list demonstrates that she is, in terms of {\i halachic} identity, simply a woman, with no privileges and no restrictions that differ from those of an ordinary {\i Yisraelit}. Even the {\i chalalah} status, which might seem like a priestly restriction, applies to any Jewish woman. The {\i kohenet} of this {\i mishna} is, functionally, a {\i bat kohen} with no operative {\i kohen} status of her own.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hybrid Mincha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now examines the central legal puzzle embedded in the {\i mishna}: what exactly happens to the {\i minchat sotah} of a woman married to a {\i kohen}? The Gemara frames it as follows. {\i Kol hanesu\u8217'ot li-khehunah minchatam nisrafet} \u8212- all women married to {\i kohanim} have their {\i mincha} burned, regardless of whether the woman is a {\i kohenet}, a {\i leviyah}, or an {\i Israelite}. The reason is {\i mipnei she-yesh lo chelek bah} \u8212- because the husband has a share in it. Insofar as it is his {\i mincha}, {\i lo te\u8217'achel} \u8212- it may not be eaten. But then: {\i ve-ein olah kalil, mipnei she-yesh lah chelek bah} \u8212- you cannot simply offer the whole thing on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} as a single unit, because she has a share in it. A true {\i minchat kohen} is offered whole, without the separation of {\i kmitzah}; but here, because the woman is not a {\i kohen}, you must take a {\i kmitzah}, as would be done for any {\i minchat Yisrael}. So the {\i mishna} resolves it: {\i ela ha-kometz karev be-atzmo ve-ha-shirayim kreivim be-atzmam} \u8212- the {\i kometz} is offered by itself, and the remainder is offered by itself. You perform the {\i kmitzah} in deference to its identity as a {\i minchat Yisrael}, and you burn the remainder in deference to its identity as a {\i minchat kohen}. The difficulty, however, is immediately apparent: once part of the offering has been placed on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- namely the {\i kometz} \u8212- is it permitted to place the remainder on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} as well? The {\i pasuk} teaches: {\i ki kol se\u8217'or ve-chol devash lo taktiru mimenu isheh la-Hashem}, and the {\i drasha} from {\i mimenu} is that once part of a {\i korban} has been offered on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, the remainder is forbidden to be placed there. This is a general principle: even the uneaten meat of a {\i korban} that is not an {\i olah} \u8212- the innards are offered on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, but the remaining flesh that is designated for eating is forbidden to go up on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Eitzim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers in the name of Yehudah the son of Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi: {\i de-masik lehu le-shem eitzim} \u8212- you burn the remainder by designating it as firewood rather than as a {\i korban}. The {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} always requires fuel; you are not offering the remainder as a sacrifice but as combustible material to feed the fire. This is the position of Rabbi Eliezer, as found in a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Eliezer reads the phrase {\i lerei\u8217'ach nicho\u8217'ach} as a limiting qualifier. The prohibition is {\i lo ya\u8217'alu lerei\u8217'ach nicho\u8217'ach} \u8212- you may not offer it up as a pleasing fragrance, as a {\i korban}. But you may place it on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach le-shem eitzim} \u8212- as firewood, which is not the same act as bringing a {\i korban}. The context for this {\i sevara} is a dispute about what to do when pieces of {\i korbanot} have become mixed together \u8212- say, limbs from an {\i olah} and limbs from a {\i chatat}, and it is no longer possible to determine which is which. According to Rabbi Eliezer, you may put all of them on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} and designate the {\i chatat} limbs as firewood; according to the Rabbis, you may not do this \u8212- you must allow them to become {\i pasul} and then burn them, passively, without putting them on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi, in his commentary on this passage, speaks of a {\i tenai} \u8212- a conditional designation \u8212- that one makes when burning the remainder: if the {\i kometz} was not truly a proper {\i kmitzah} and the whole thing should be treated as a {\i minchat kohen}, then this burning is valid as an offering; and if it is a {\i minchat Yisrael} and the {\i shirayim} are truly {\i shirayim}, then these are to be regarded as firewood. Tosafot objects to this framing. A {\i tenai} presupposes a {\i safek} \u8212- an uncertainty as to which of two possibilities obtains. By the legs of the mixed lambs, you genuinely do not know whether a given limb came from the {\i olah} or the {\i chatat}, and so a conditional designation makes sense. But here there is no uncertainty. This {\i mincha} is simultaneously a {\i minchat kohen} and a {\i minchat Yisrael} \u8212- both statuses coexist in it at the same time. It is not a question of which it is; it is both. The situation is analogous to an {\i androgynos} who is both male and female, or to a {\i chatzi eved ve-chatzi ben chorin} \u8212- the half-slave, half-free individual, familiar from discussions in {\i Minchat Chinuch}. You cannot make a {\i tenai} between two simultaneous realities. Tosafot\u8217's resolution is that you simply accept the best possible compromise: the remainder is burned, it is not eaten, and it is not offered as a proper {\i korban} \u8212- it occupies an intermediate status that satisfies neither identity fully. As the {\i shiur} puts it, a compromise is not when everyone is happy; a compromise is when everyone is equally unhappy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further question arises from this discussion: when burning {\i le-shem eitzim}, does the act require the same standards of {\i haktarah} that a proper offering does? Does it need to occur by day? Must there be proper {\i kavana}? Tosafot, citing the {\i Yerushalmi}, explores whether the requirement of intent for {\i haktarah} applies when the material is being burned as firewood rather than as a {\i korban}. The implicit answer seems to be that if you are genuinely burning it {\i le-shem eitzim}, the technical requirements of {\i haktarah} do not apply \u8212- it is not being brought as a {\i korban} in any formal sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar b.R. Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara has proceeded on the assumption of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. The Rabbis who disagree with Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- who hold that you cannot designate the mixed limbs as firewood \u8212- would be unable to use that solution here. The Gemara therefore pivots to the ruling of Rabbi Elazar beRebbi Shimon, found in a {\i baraita}: {\i ha-kometz karev le-atzmo ve-ha-shirayim mitpazrin al beit ha-deshen} \u8212- the {\i kometz} is offered on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} on its own, and the remainder is scattered on the {\i beis hadeshen}. This is precisely the reading Rashi adopted for the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case Rabbi Elazar beRebbi Shimon was originally addressing is a third scenario: a {\i kohen} who brings a {\i minchat chotei} \u8212- the meal offering that serves as the poverty-tier substitute in an {\i oleh ve-yored} obligation. When the Torah describes this offering it says {\i ve-hayeta la-kohen ke-minchah} \u8212- it shall be for the {\i kohen} like a {\i mincha}. The question is: like which {\i mincha}? Rabbi Elazar beRebbi Shimon argues that the word {\i ke-minchah} \u8212- like a {\i mincha}, not like {\i his} {\i mincha} \u8212- means it is to be treated like a standard {\i minchat Yisrael} in one respect: a {\i kmitzah} is taken. The word is {\i ke-minchah}, not {\i ke-minchat kohen}. However, since it is still a {\i minchat kohen} in some sense, the {\i shirayim} cannot be eaten. So he reaches the same intermediate resolution: {\i kmitzah} is taken, the {\i kometz} is offered, and the remainder cannot be eaten and cannot be properly offered, so it is burned in the {\i beis hadeshen}. The Rabbis, by contrast, hold that {\i ke-minchah} means it is fully like a {\i minchat kohen} and the whole thing is burned on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}. Their disagreement with Rabbi Elazar beRebbi Shimon is specifically about this case, which they regard as a straightforward {\i minchat kohen}, not a hybrid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Agree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then makes a crucial concession: {\i ve-afillu Rabbanan lo paligi alei de-Rabbi Elazar beRebbi Shimon ella be-Minchat Chotei shel Kohanim de-vat akrava hi} \u8212- even the Rabbis only disagree with Rabbi Elazar beRebbi Shimon in the case of the {\i minchat chotei} of a {\i kohen}, which they regard as a genuine {\i minchat kohen} that should be fully offered. But in the case of the wife of a {\i kohen}, where the {\i mincha} is genuinely a hybrid \u8212- simultaneously carrying the status of a {\i minchat kohen} and a {\i minchat Yisrael} \u8212- {\i afillu Rabbanan modu} \u8212- even the Rabbis concede that you cannot eat the {\i shirayim} and cannot properly offer them on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}. You take the {\i kmitzah}, you offer it, and the remainder is burned in the {\i beis hadeshen}. This is the position that Rashi had already adopted in the {\i mishna}, and which now receives its Talmudic grounding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Differences (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General M/F Diffs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} transitions again, now moving from the specific {\i kohen}/{\i kohenet} dichotomy to a general enumeration of differences between men and women across a range of {\i halachic} contexts. The Gemara supplies the {\i pesukim} underlying each distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metzora Hair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i ha\u8217'ish poreia u-forem} \u8212- a man who is a {\i metzora} must let his hair grow wild and rend his garments. A woman does not. The Gemara derives this from the {\i pasuk}. The {\i Torah} begins the laws of {\i tzara\u8217'at} with {\i ish ish ki yihyeh be-or besaro} \u8212- it opens with {\i ish}, man. One might have thought that women are entirely exempt from {\i tzara\u8217'at} laws. The {\i pasuk} then says {\i ve-ha-tzarua asher bo ha-nega} \u8212- using a general term for the {\i metzora} without specifying gender \u8212- to teach that a woman too can be a {\i metzora} and is subject to {\i tum\u8217'at tzara\u8217'at}. The {\i hava amina} from {\i ish} is not that women are exempt from {\i tum\u8217'ah}, but that some of the active behavioral obligations incumbent upon a {\i metzora} \u8212- letting the hair grow wild, rending the garments \u8212- apply specifically to men by virtue of that initial {\i ish}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha\u8217'ish madir et beno be-Nazir} \u8212- a man can make his son a {\i Nazir}, even without the son\u8217's consent or explicit agreement, {\i ve-ein ha-ishah maderet et benah be-Nazir} \u8212- but a woman cannot impose a {\i nazirite} vow on her son. The Gemara cites Rabbi Yochanan: {\i halacha hi be-Nazir} \u8212- this is a {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} with respect to the laws of {\i nezirut}. Reish Lakish had offered a different rationale, rooting the distinction in the laws of {\i chinuch} \u8212- the obligation to educate a child in {\i mitzvot} \u8212- and holding that {\i chinuch} falls specifically on fathers with respect to sons but not on mothers with respect to daughters. But that was Reish Lakish\u8217's reading; the Gemara here follows Rabbi Yochanan and grounds the law in {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha\u8217'ish megalei\u8217'ach al nezirut aviv} \u8212- a man who himself became a {\i Nazir} after his father was a {\i Nazir} may, under certain circumstances, make use of his father\u8217's remaining {\i korbanot} for his own {\i nezirut}, {\i ve-ein ha-ishah megale\u8217'ichat al nezirut aviha} \u8212- but this does not apply to a woman. Again Rabbi Yochanan: {\i halacha hi be-Nazir} \u8212- this too is {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Marries Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha\u8217'ish mekadesh et bito} \u8212- a father can arrange the {\i kiddushin} of his daughter, giving her in marriage, {\i ve-ein ha-ishah mekadeshet et bitah} \u8212- a mother cannot. The source is the {\i pasuk}: {\i et bitti natati la\u8217'ish ha-zeh} \u8212- \u8220"I gave my daughter to this man\u8221" \u8212- the father speaking. This right belongs to the father {\i de-oraita}; {\i de-rabbanan} a mechanism was created for the mother as well, but the Biblical institution is paternal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sale to Slavery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha\u8217'ish mocher et bito} \u8212- a father can sell his daughter as a slave in certain circumstances of poverty; {\i ve-ein ha-ishah mocheret et bitah} \u8212- a mother cannot. The source is {\i ki yimkor ish et bito} \u8212- the Torah specifies {\i ish}, the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stoning Execution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha\u8217'ish niskal arom} \u8212- a man who is stoned is stoned while naked; {\i ve-ein ha-ishah niskelet arumah} \u8212- a woman is not. The Gemara derives this from {\i ve-ragmu oto kol ha-edah} \u8212- they shall stone him, and {\i oto} is taken to mean him in his own state, without his garments. The Gemara preempts the possible misreading: {\i oto} cannot mean to exclude women from capital punishment altogether, since the Torah elsewhere explicitly says {\i ve-hotzeita et ha-ish ha-hu o et ha-ishah ha-hi el she\u8217'arecha} \u8212- you shall bring out that man or that woman to your gates, and execute them. Rather, {\i oto} teaches {\i oso be-lo chesuto ve-lo osah be-lo chesusah} \u8212- him without his clothes, but not her without hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hanging\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha\u8217'ish nitlah} \u8212- after execution by stoning, a man\u8217's body is hung; {\i ve-ein ha-ishah nitlit} \u8212- not a woman. The source is {\i ve-talita oto al ha-eitz} \u8212- and you shall hang him, {\i oto ve-lo otah}. This raises the perennial question in the {\i Torah}\u8217's legislative language: since the default gender of Hebrew is masculine, when does the masculine pronoun limit the law to men, and when is it inclusive? The {\i gemara} consistently addresses this on a case-by-case basis, and the masculine genitive is not always read as exclusionary. In these cases, it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sale for Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha\u8217'ish nimkar bi-gnivato ve-ein ishah nimkeret bi-gneivatah} \u8212- a man who steals and cannot repay is sold into slavery by {\i beit din}, but a woman is not. The {\i pasuk} says {\i ve-nimkar bi-gnivato} \u8212- the word {\i bi-gnivato}, using the masculine possessive, is the basis: {\i bi-gnivato ve-lo bi-gneivatah} \u8212- for his theft, not for her theft. The {\i beit din} has no authority to sell a woman in satisfaction of her theft debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} in chapter 2, {\i halacha} 8, supplements this list considerably: a {\i kohen} serves on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, which a {\i kohenet} does not; the father retains rights to his minor daughter\u8217's found objects, her earnings, and the power to annul her vows; a man is obligated in {\i mitzvot asei she-ha-zman geramah} while a woman is not; a man transgresses {\i bal takif} and {\i bal tashchit} \u8212- shaving the sidelocks and beard \u8212- and the prohibition of {\i tum\u8217'at meitim} for a {\i kohen}; only a boy can be a {\i ben sorer u-moreh}; a man can be sold into slavery more than once, and a woman cannot be; a man undergoes {\i retziah} \u8212- ear-piercing to mark perpetual slavery \u8212- and a woman does not; and various other distinctions related to the laws of {\i eved ivri}. The {\i mishna}\u8217's list, while substantial, is thus only a selection from a fuller catalogue of male-female {\i halachic} differences.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 24\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arusa & Shomeret Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Perek Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i daf} 24, we arrive at a new {\i perek}. Having completed the step-by-step description of the {\i sotah} procedure in the previous chapters, the {\i Mishnah} now returns to the more characteristically Talmudic mode of analysis: identifying categories, determining who qualifies and who does not, and working through the criteria that govern the {\i sotah} process. This is the kind of sustained halakhic analysis more familiar from tractates like {\i Kodashim}, where the procedure has already been described and the focus shifts to the underlying rules.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding, it is worth noting a common misconception about the {\i sotah}: neither the {\i Torah}, the {\i Mishnah}, nor the {\i Gemara} actually states that she dies. The single dramatic line \u8212- \u8220"remove her lest she make the {\i Azarah tamei}\u8221" \u8212- is interpreted by the {\i Gemara} to mean that she may be about to become a {\i niddah}. The popular imagination has embellished this considerably beyond what the sources actually say.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusa Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} opens: {\i arusa v\u8217'shomeret yavam lo shotot v\u8217'lo notlot ktubatah} \u8212- a betrothed woman and a woman awaiting levirate marriage neither drink nor collect their {\i ktuba}. The {\i arusa} is a woman who has accepted {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the ring or its equivalent \u8212- from her husband, rendering her fully married in law. If she commits adultery, it is a genuine act of adultery: the children would be {\i mamzerim}, she would be forbidden to her husband, and all attendant legal consequences apply. The sole distinction between {\i eirusin} and {\i nisuin} is that the couple has not yet begun a shared life together. They are not living together, they are not intimate, and the reciprocal obligations of married life have not yet commenced. She remains, in that sense, a marriage on paper \u8212- complete in its legal weight, but not yet actualized in lived reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shomeret Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i shomeret yavam} is the woman whose husband has died childless, leaving her bound by the tie of {\i zika} \u8212- a quasi-marital connection \u8212- to her deceased husband\u8217's brother, the {\i yavam}. She awaits either {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah}. She is prohibited by an {\i issur lav} from marrying or sleeping with any other man. Although this bond is not a full marriage, it is understood as a continuation, in potential form, of the marriage with the deceased brother, now existing in a kind of anticipatory relationship with the living brother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Drink/Ktuba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In both cases, if {\i kinui} was issued and the woman subsequently entered seclusion, she cannot be compelled to drink, and she does not collect her {\i ktuba}. The logic runs as follows: she becomes forbidden to her husband as a {\i sotah} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} treats this as implicit \u8212- but since the process cannot be completed, the only resolution is divorce without {\i ktuba}. The general principle is that when the inability to complete the procedure is attributable to the wife, she forfeits her {\i ktuba}; when it is attributable to the husband, she collects it. In these cases, even though no one is formally at fault \u8212- she simply is an {\i arusa} or {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- she is nonetheless considered responsible, because she need not have entered seclusion with another man after her husband warned her. She made that choice, and the consequence follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question was raised about the language of the {\i Mishnah}: why does it say {\i lo notlot ktubatam} rather than the more familiar formula {\i v\u8217'teitzei blo ktuba} \u8212- \u8220"she goes out without a {\i ktuba}\u8221"? The answer lies in structure. The {\i Mishnah} is about to present cases where women {\i do} collect their {\i ktuba} despite not drinking \u8212- {\i lo shotot v\u8217'notlot}. The parallel formulation {\i lo shotot v\u8217'lo notlot} sets up that contrast neatly, and the stylistic symmetry justifies the phrasing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi-Tosafot Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of the {\i shomeret yavam} is particularly rich. {\i Rashi} explains that even if she were not halakhically forbidden to the {\i yavam} by virtue of her suspected conduct, the brother would still be entitled to declare that he refuses to marry such a woman. That refusal alone is sufficient to dissolve the {\i zika} without her collecting a {\i ktuba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} objects. The rule established earlier in the tractate is that if the act itself \u8212- had it actually occurred \u8212- would not have rendered her forbidden, then the suspicion of having done it cannot render her forbidden either. It would be strange if the {\i sotah} process made her status worse than actual proven misconduct would. {\i Tosafot} therefore resolves the matter by working from the premise that a {\i shomeret yavam} who sleeps with another man would in fact be forbidden to the {\i yavam}. On that premise, the {\i sotah} process is coherent: since proven infidelity would forbid her, suspected infidelity similarly triggers the {\i sotah} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question was posed: if there are multiple brothers, who among them is entitled to issue {\i kinui}? And if one brother does so and she becomes forbidden on account of his {\i kinui}, does she remain permissible to the other brothers? This is a genuinely difficult question that does not appear to have been resolved in the discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The textual basis for excluding the {\i arusa} and {\i shomeret yavam} from drinking comes from the phrase {\i asher tisteh ishah tachat ishah} \u8212- \u8220"that a woman strays from her husband\u8221" \u8212- where {\i ishah} denotes a complete husband, not merely an {\i arus} or a {\i yavam}. This excludes cases where the marriage is not yet full. The {\i Gemara} will explain how the beginning of the {\i parasha} \u8212- the phrase {\i daber el Bnei Yisrael v\u8217'amarta} \u8212- is interpreted to include the {\i arusa} and {\i shomeret yavam} within the ambit of {\i kinui} even while they are excluded from the drinking. The two phrases thus operate on two different parts of the process.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Marriages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow/Divorcee Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} turns to a different category: women in marriages that, while legally binding, are in violation of a negative prohibition. If a prohibition carries the weight of {\i issur kareit} \u8212- incest, for example \u8212- the marriage is not binding at all. But if the prohibition is merely an {\i issur lav}, the marriage takes legal effect even though it transgresses the law. The question is whether such a husband may charge his wife as a {\i sotah} and bring her through the procedure, when one might argue that the entire marriage ought to be dissolved regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The examples given are: a {\i kohen gadol} married to a widow ({\i almanah}); a divorced woman or a {\i chalutza} married to an ordinary {\i kohen} ({\i kohen hedyot}) \u8212- with the note that the {\i Bavli} tends to treat the prohibition of a {\i chalutza} marrying a {\i kohen} as rabbinic rather than biblical; a {\i mamzeret} or {\i netina} married to a regular {\i Yisrael}; and conversely, a regular Jewish woman married to a {\i mamzer} or {\i netin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer/Netin Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These cases all share the characteristic that the marriage involves a violation of a negative prohibition touching on personal status. The specific prohibition varies \u8212- in some cases it concerns priestly restrictions ({\i issurei kehunah}), in others it concerns lineage ({\i mamzer}, {\i netin}). In all of them, however, the marriage is technically valid even as it transgresses the law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Drink Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling in all such cases is {\i lo shotot v\u8217'lo notlot ktubatam}: they do not drink and they do not collect the {\i ktuba}. The reason they do not drink is that the marriage is not recognized as a legitimate {\i ishah} \u8212- not a marriage that should remain in place. Since the whole framework of the {\i sotah} process presupposes a valid marriage worth protecting, these marriages fall outside that framework. And since the women themselves are held responsible for having entered seclusion after being warned, they forfeit their {\i ktuba} as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Process Failures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confession/Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} now shifts to a different class of cases \u8212- not those where the nature of the marriage disqualifies the woman, but those where the {\i sotah} process breaks down for other reasons. The list opens: if a woman confesses her guilt, or if witnesses testify that she committed adultery, there is nothing left to establish through drinking. The process becomes superfluous. In such cases, she does not drink and does not collect her {\i ktuba} \u8212- the outcome is determined by evidence rather than by the ordeal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Refuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the woman simply refuses to drink, she is likewise forbidden without a {\i ktuba}. She is the obstacle. Even though she has not confessed and there are no witnesses, her refusal creates a situation that cannot be resolved in her favor, and since the inability to complete the process is attributable to her, she bears the consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Default\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cases shift when the fault lies with the husband. If the husband declares {\i eini mashkeh} \u8212- \u8220"I will not make her drink\u8221" \u8212- and withdraws from the process, or if the husband has relations with her after she has become a {\i sotah} and before the process was completed (which he is prohibited from doing, and which renders the {\i mei sotah} ineffective in any case), then the ruling changes: {\i notlot ktuba v\u8217'lo shosot}. She collects her {\i ktuba} even though she cannot drink. He is the responsible party. Notably, she remains forbidden to him in her status as {\i sotah}, since there is no mechanism to resolve the suspicion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Dies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most complex case in this section is where the husband dies before the process is completed \u8212- an act of God, without fault on either side. Here {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} disagree. {\i Beit Shammai} holds that she collects her {\i ktuba} and does not drink; {\i Beit Hillel} holds {\i o shotot o lo notlot ktubasan} \u8212- either drink or forfeit the {\i ktuba}, and since she cannot drink, she does not collect. The underlying question is how to allocate the consequences when no one is at fault. {\i Beit Shammai} appears willing to attribute the problem to the husband, since he is the one who died, even without moral fault; {\i Beit Hillel} takes the position that the inability to complete the process, absent a responsible party, falls on the wife.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Marriages (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nursing/Pregnant Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further category is introduced: {\i u\u8217'uveres chavairta u\u8217'meinekes chavairta} \u8212- a woman who is pregnant with another man\u8217's child, or nursing another man\u8217's child, and whose husband married her during that period. The prohibition here is not about personal status or a violation of Torah law; it is rabbinic in origin and rooted in a specific concern. If a man marries a woman who is nursing an infant from her deceased first husband, the concern is that the new husband \u8212- for whom the child is not his \u8212- will not weigh the infant\u8217's welfare appropriately. He may engage in marital relations, she may become pregnant, and the demands of a new pregnancy and new infant will compromise her ability to nurse and care for the existing child. The {\i Gemara} here reflects a recognition that having children in close succession can be harmful to an infant, particularly when the decision is made by a party without skin in the game.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This emerges into a law: a man should not marry a nursing or pregnant woman until the child has passed the two-year nursing period. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's acknowledgment of this concern, while framed as a prohibition rather than a permission to use contraception, resonates with the practice documented in the name of {\i Rav Yosef Eliyahu Henkin}, who would regularly grant {\i heterim} for women to use contraception for two or more years after giving birth, in order to allow focused care for the existing child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If such a man then issued {\i kinui} and seclusion followed, is this treated like the cases of prohibited marriages \u8212- where the couple should not remain married and therefore the full {\i sotah} process cannot proceed \u8212- or is it different?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir vs Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} applies the same principle as in the cases of {\i issurei kehunah}: since they should not be married right now, the {\i sotah} procedure cannot be pursued. She neither drinks nor collects her {\i ktuba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} disagree. They distinguish this case from the earlier prohibited marriages on a crucial ground: in this case, the man is not required to divorce his wife. He could live separately from her during the two-year period and then resume the marriage. Since we would not demand a divorce, the marriage retains its legitimacy, and therefore the {\i sotah} procedure may be carried out. {\i Rabbi Meir} applies the principle broadly \u8212- any reason they should not be married currently disqualifies the {\i sotah} process. The {\i Chachamim} apply it narrowly \u8212- only where the marriage is so defective that divorce is required does the process become unavailable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Infertile Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Infertile Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aylonit v\u8217'zkeinah u\u8217'she\u8217'eina re\u8217'uya l\u8217'yeled} \u8212- an {\i aylonit}, an elderly woman, and a woman who cannot bear children \u8212- these are the next cases considered. The {\i aylonit} is a woman with markedly masculine characteristics who is considered not only infertile but physically distinct in ways the {\i Gemara} associates with that condition. The {\i zkeinah} is simply an older woman past childbearing age. {\i She\u8217'eina re\u8217'uya l\u8217'yeled} \u8212- {\i Rashi} understands this as a woman who drank a sterilizing potion; {\i Tosafot} offers a different explanation, and also asks why the {\i Mishnah} does not simply use the standard term {\i akara} (barren woman), which the Torah uses for classic infertility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why does infertility raise a question about the {\i sotah} process at all? The issue is not the verse {\i v\u8217'nikta v\u8217'nizra zara} \u8212- that she will be purified and bear seed \u8212- though that might be a passing thought. The concern parallels the earlier cases: the man has a {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, an obligation to have children. If his wife cannot bear children, perhaps the marriage is one that should not continue, and therefore falls outside the framework of a legitimate {\i ishah} that the {\i sotah} process presupposes. This question arises even in a marriage of fifty years \u8212- he could theoretically always take an additional wife and thus fulfill his obligation, but the concern is whether this marriage, as it stands, is one the {\i Torah} views as a {\i sotah}-eligible union.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Drink/Ktuba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's ruling: {\i lo notlet ktuba v\u8217'lo shotah} \u8212- she does not collect her {\i ktuba} and does not drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} dissents: {\i yachol hu v\u8217'yissa isha acheret v\u8217'yifros v\u8217'ya\u8217'amod im zu} \u8212- he can simply marry another woman and thereby fulfill his obligation of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, while remaining married to his current wife. On this view, there is no reason to dissolve the marriage or to bar the {\i sotah} process, since the man has an available remedy that does not require divorcing his wife. Intuitively, {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's position has considerable force: there is nothing inherently wrong with this marriage, particularly in a halakhic framework that permits polygamy. If his {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} can be satisfied through a second marriage, the first marriage need not be undone, and the {\i sotah} process should apply normally.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixed Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayot Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al yedai kol ha\u8217'arayot mikanyin} \u8212- the {\i kinui} process applies even when the woman is suspected not merely of adultery but of another forbidden sexual relationship. One might have supposed that when the suspicion involves an entirely separate category of prohibition \u8212- sleeping with a close relative, for instance \u8212- the case should be analyzed through that other framework rather than through the {\i sotah} framework. The {\i chiddush} here is that the {\i sotah} process is not dislodged by the presence of a concurrent prohibition. The {\i kinui} and the {\i sotah} status remain operative, and if she undergoes the ordeal and is cleared, this clears up both the adultery question and the {\i arayot} question simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor/Non-Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exception: a woman cannot be made a {\i sotah} based on suspicion of having been with a minor ({\i katan}) or with someone who is not a man ({\i mi she\u8217'eino ish}). The phrase {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish} is deliberately opaque, and the {\i Gemara} will address it on a subsequent {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of the {\i katan} is not about physical capacity but about legal significance. The act, even if it occurred, would not carry sufficient legal weight to create {\i sotah} status. A question was raised about a non-Jew: would suspicion of sleeping with a non-Jew similarly disqualify the {\i sotah} process? The suggestion that {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish} might encompass a non-Jew was floated, though this was not resolved. Both the {\i katan} and the potential non-Jew share the characteristic of not being {\i tofes kiddushin} \u8212- not being able to effect a legally binding betrothal \u8212- and that may be the relevant common denominator, though this remains to be seen in the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Din Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cases for Intervention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'elu she\u8217'beis din mikanyin lahen} \u8212- the following women, whose husbands are not in a position to issue {\i kinui}, will have {\i beit din} step in on the husband\u8217's behalf. The cases are: the husband became a {\i cheresh} (deaf-mute, who lacks full legal competence); the husband became a {\i shoteh} (mentally ill, not in the sense of being demented but of having a mental condition that disrupts his ability to function legally); or the husband is {\i chavush b\u8217'veit ha\u8217'asurim} \u8212- imprisoned and unable to monitor or respond to his wife\u8217's conduct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important conceptual question. Earlier in the tractate, there was a debate about whether it is {\i assur l\u8217'kanos} \u8212- forbidden for a husband to issue {\i kinui} \u8212- or {\i mutar}, or even a {\i chiyuv}. If it is {\i assur l\u8217'kanos} when the husband acts out of his own jealousy and subjective passion, perhaps when a third party \u8212- {\i beit din}, an objective body with no personal stake \u8212- observes concerning behavior, they bear a responsibility to address it that the emotionally invested husband does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first opinion sharply limits what {\i beit din} can accomplish: {\i lo lehashkotah amru ela lefoslah miktuvatah} \u8212- the {\i Sages} did not say that {\i beit din} can represent the husband to the point of compelling her to drink. All they can do is disqualify her from collecting her {\i ktuba}. The immediate consequence of {\i beit din}\u8217's intervention, therefore, is that the situation moves toward divorce without {\i ktuba} \u8212- since there is no mechanism to complete the {\i sotah} process when the husband is incapacitated, and the {\i sotah} status cannot be resolved, the only exit is dissolution of the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a searching question about what has actually been achieved. If the woman knows that the only possible outcome is divorce without {\i ktuba}, has her behavior been deterred? It seems unlikely. Perhaps the purpose is not deterrence of her current conduct but protection of his interests \u8212- ensuring that when and if he returns to capacity, he will not be required to pay a {\i ktuba} to a wife who behaved this way in his absence. But even that framing is not fully satisfying, since his financial interests seem an insufficient reason for {\i beit din} to intervene as actively as it does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another possibility is that {\i beit din} is acting on halakhic grounds: they believe this marriage should end, not merely for his financial protection, but because they suspect adultery has occurred and they do not want the couple to remain married in such circumstances. On this reading, {\i beit din} is acting to achieve a halakhic result \u8212- dissolution of a marriage they believe should not continue \u8212- and the {\i ktuba} consequence is a tool toward that end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further wrinkle: in the cases of {\i cheresh} and {\i shoteh}, a {\i get} cannot be arranged even from a distance, since the husband lacks legal competence to issue one. Only in the case of the imprisoned husband could a {\i get} potentially be arranged from prison. So what {\i beit din} has accomplished in the {\i cheresh} and {\i shoteh} cases is, in effect, a kind of conditional verdict \u8212- setting the stage so that if the husband ever recovers capacity, divorce without {\i ktuba} is the pre-determined result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yosi} goes further: {\i af lehashkotah} \u8212- {\i beit din} is empowered to authorize her actually drinking, {\i liksheyeitzei ba\u8217'alah mibeit ha\u8217'asurim yashkenah} \u8212- when the husband is released from prison, he can make her drink on the strength of {\i beit din}\u8217's prior {\i kinui}. This is a significant expansion. On {\i Rabbi Yosi}\u8217's view, the {\i kinui} issued by {\i beit din} has full legal force, and the process is merely suspended \u8212- not foreclosed \u8212- until the husband regains the ability to bring it to completion. In the interim, the woman is in a kind of {\i agunah} state, unable to move forward, but the path to resolution remains open. The husband, upon release, could choose either to complete the {\i sotah} process or, if the {\i sotah} process vindicates her, to resume the marriage \u8212- he need not necessarily divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Philosophy/Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual architecture here is striking. The {\i Mishnah} has already shown, across several categories, that {\i sotah} status can be created even in cases where the process can never be completed \u8212- where from the very outset there is no possible {\i sotah} outcome. The {\i Torah} permits this intermediate status, even without any path to resolution, apparently because the suspicion and the conduct that created it are themselves legally significant, independent of whether the ordeal can be performed. {\i Beit din}\u8217's intervention extends this logic to cases of an absent or incapacitated husband, authorizing the creation of that status through a surrogate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whose interests are being protected is not straightforwardly answered. If it is about deterrence, it is unlikely to deter, because once the {\i sotah} status is established and no resolution is possible, the woman has nothing further to lose. If it is about his financial interests, the magnitude of {\i beit din}\u8217's involvement seems disproportionate to a monetary concern. The most coherent account may be halakhic: {\i beit din} acts because it views the continuation of this marriage under these circumstances as a problem the law demands they address, and stripping the {\i ktuba} is the mechanism available to them in his absence. And as a secondary consideration, there is also the deterrent function for future cases \u8212- if women know that their conduct during a husband\u8217's incapacity will be met with a consequence, even a delayed or partial one, that knowledge may shape behavior.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinui Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now takes up the question implicit in the {\i Mishnah}: since the {\i arusa} and {\i shomeret yavam} cannot drink, how can {\i kinui} apply to them at all? {\i Mishna hu d\u8217'lo shatya} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} presupposes that {\i kinui} can be issued but drinking is unavailable. {\i Ha\u8217'kinui mikanei lah} \u8212- {\i kinui} does apply. {\i Mna hani mili} \u8212- from where do we derive this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: the phrase {\i daber el Bnei Yisrael v\u8217'amarta} at the opening of the {\i parshat sotah} \u8212- the doubled language of \u8220"speak\u8221" and \u8220"say\u8221" \u8212- is interpreted as an expansion. It includes the {\i arusa} and {\i shomeret yavam} within the {\i kinui} stage of the {\i parasha}, even though they are excluded from the drinking stage by the later phrase {\i tachat ishah}. The opening of the {\i parasha} draws them in; the closing phrase cuts them off before the final step.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yoshiya vs Yonatan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records a debate between {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} and {\i Rabbi Yonatan} that cuts against the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's unanimous ruling. {\i Rabbi Yonatan} \u8212- whose position matches the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- excludes both {\i arusa} and {\i shomeret yavam} from drinking. He reads {\i tachat ishech} as excluding the {\i shomeret yavam} first (since that is the more obvious exclusion for him), and a second phrase, {\i asher tisteh isha tachat ishah}, as excluding the {\i arusa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} takes the opposing view: he uses the double language of {\i ish ish} to {\i include} the {\i shomeret yavam} in the full {\i sotah} process, up to and including drinking. For {\i Rabbi Yoshiya}, the {\i shomeret yavam} is sufficiently married \u8212- or nearly so \u8212- that she drinks just as a fully married woman would. It is only the {\i arusa} who is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceptual Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying conceptual disagreement is this: which of the two relationships \u8212- {\i eirusin} or {\i zika} \u8212- is more weighty, more fully an {\i ishut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} (and the position associated with him in the {\i Gemara}) holds that the {\i arusa} relationship is more of a marriage. The man actively chose it; he performed an act of {\i kiddushin}; she bears full capital liability for adultery. The relationship is enormously weighty, even if not yet a lived marriage. The {\i shomeret yavam}, by contrast, had this bond thrust upon her without choice, and her liability for sleeping with another man is more limited. So {\i eirusin} is closer to full {\i ishut} and harder to exclude; {\i zika} is more peripheral and more easily read out of the {\i parasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yonatan} takes the opposite view. The {\i shomeret yavam} is more obviously included \u8212- and thus more surprisingly excluded by the verse \u8212- because her path to full marriage requires no additional act. She does not need {\i kiddushin}, she does not need a formal wedding ceremony. Sexual relations alone, even unintentional ones, immediately actualize the {\i zika} into full marriage. The marriage is almost there; it could happen in an instant. The {\i arusa}, despite her weighty legal status, still requires the full process of {\i nisuin} to actualize. So the {\i shomeret yavam} is the one you would expect to be more included in {\i parshat sotah}, and therefore she is the first one the verse needs to exclude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conceptual debate is not merely academic \u8212- it maps onto a disagreement in the {\i Gemara} between {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} about the legal effect of sexual relations in the {\i yibbum} context, which the {\i Gemara} explores in the next section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Consummation Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Acha bar Chanina} came from the south and brought a {\i baraita} with him: {\i mishekadma shchivat ba\u8217'al l\u8217'vo\u8217'el} \u8212- the {\i sotah} process can only apply if the husband consummated the marriage before the suspected act of infidelity occurred. {\i V\u8217'lo shekadma shchivat bo\u8217'el l\u8217'ba\u8217'al} \u8212- if the suspected act came first and the marriage was never consummated, the husband cannot subject her to the {\i sotah} ordeal. This is derived from the phrase {\i miba\u8217'aladei ishech} \u8212- \u8220"other than your husband\u8221" \u8212- with emphasis on {\i u\u8217'nitein ish bach et shichvato}, implying that the husband\u8217's intimacy must precede the other man\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a rule not stated explicitly in the {\i Mishnah} itself, but one the {\i Gemara} adopts as authoritative. It raises the question of what, precisely, consummation adds to the legal picture. One possibility is that it is simply another dimension of {\i ishut} \u8212- the marriage must be fully realized, not only in its formal structure but in its physical consummation, before the {\i sotah} framework applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zera Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A deeper dimension of this requirement may be found in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's language. The {\i parasha} uses the phrase {\i shichvat zera} \u8212- \u8220"a lying of seed.\u8221" Halakhically, intercourse without ejaculation would carry the same prohibition, so the phrase seems to have no strictly legal content. The {\i Ramban} explains this as pointing to the real engine of the husband\u8217's jealousy: the concern about {\i zera} \u8212- whose children these are. If another man has lain with his wife, the fundamental fear is that the children he is raising may not be his. The end of the {\i parasha} echoes this \u8212- {\i v\u8217'nikta v\u8217'nizra zara}, she will be cleansed and bear seed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same concern animates the position of the {\i Tzitz Eliezer} in the modern debate about artificial insemination with donor sperm. He cited precisely this {\i Ramban} to argue that another man\u8217's {\i zera}, even without a sexual act, constitutes a form of quasi-adultery. {\i Rav Moshe Feinstein}, by contrast, dismissed this entirely: halakha deals only with acts, and without a sexual act there is no adultery. He regarded the {\i Tzitz Eliezer}\u8217's concern as imported from outside values rather than grounded in {\i Torah} \u8212- a remarkable response, given that it is {\i pshat} of the {\i psukim} and the {\i Ramban} says it explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied here: is it possible that the consummation requirement is connected to this concern about {\i zera}? If the husband has never been intimate with his wife, he has no reason to worry about the paternity of her children; any future children would obviously not be his. The concern about {\i zera} that drives the {\i sotah} process only becomes relevant once he has been intimate with her. One cannot say this with certainty \u8212- the {\i Gemara} does not state it this way \u8212- but the thematic resonance is present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusa Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a puzzle for the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's discussion of the {\i arusa}. If consummation is required for the {\i sotah} process, why does the {\i Mishnah} need a separate verse to exclude the {\i arusa} from drinking? She has not yet consummated the marriage; she is automatically excluded under the consummation requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this via {\i Rami bar Chama}: {\i mashkachat lah k\u8217'egon sheba al arusato b\u8217'veit aviha} \u8212- the exclusion is still needed for the case where the husband had relations with his {\i arusa} while she was still living in her father\u8217's house. She is still an {\i arusa} \u8212- they have not yet made a home together, she has not yet entered the {\i chuppah} \u8212- but the marriage has in fact been consummated. (The {\i Gemara} notes, citing what we will encounter in {\i Ketubot}, that in {\i Yehuda} it was the practice for a betrothed couple to spend private time together, and this sometimes led to sexual relations.) In that case, consummation alone does not transform her into a {\i nesua}. {\i Rashi} describes such relations as {\i b\u8217'znut} \u8212- not in the context of beginning a married life, and therefore not actuating {\i nisuin}. For that case, the verse {\i tachat ishah} is needed to exclude her from drinking.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yavam Marriage Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns the same question on the {\i shomeret yavam}: {\i kehavata gabei shomeres yavam} \u8212- how do you have a case of a {\i shomeret yavam} after sexual relations who is still a {\i shomeret yavam}? The challenge is sharp: the moment {\i yibbum} relations occur, she should automatically become his full wife \u8212- {\i ishto me\u8217'aleta hi}. Why would you ever need a verse to exclude a {\i shomeret yavam} if the moment she has relations with the {\i yavam} she is no longer a {\i shomeret yavam} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} holds {\i kanah l\u8217'chol davar} \u8212- that sexual relations between a {\i yavam} and his {\i yevama}, regardless of intent or context, create a complete and full marriage in every respect. On {\i Rav}\u8217's view, there is never a case of a {\i shomeret yavam} after relations \u8212- she is immediately and fully his wife. The {\i Gemara} notes that this means {\i Rav} must say like {\i Rabbi Yoshiya}, who holds that the {\i shomeret yavam} does drink. But the {\i Gemara} will complicate this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel} disagrees: {\i lo kanah ela l\u8217'devarim ha\u8217'amurim b\u8217'parsha} \u8212- relations in the context of {\i yibbum}, when not accompanied by the intent to begin a married life together, do not create full {\i nisuin}. She becomes legally his wife in certain respects specified in the {\i parasha} \u8212- adultery would be a capital crime, he inherits his brother\u8217's estate through her ({\i l\u8217'hakim shem achiv hamet}) \u8212- but she does not achieve the status of full {\i nisuin}. She remains, in some sense, more like an {\i arusa} than a {\i nesua}. On {\i Shmuel}\u8217's view, there can be a {\i shomeret yavam} after relations, and that is precisely the case the verse needs to address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} draws the natural inference: {\i Rav} must hold like {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} and {\i Shmuel} must hold like {\i Rabbi Yonatan}. But both {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} push back against this assignment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} says: I could actually say like {\i Rabbi Yonatan} \u8212- that the {\i shomeret yavam} does not drink even after relations. Here is why: {\i mideitztrich kra lemoutei, miklal d\u8217'ishto me\u8217'aleta hi} \u8212- the very fact that a verse was needed to exclude the {\i shomeret yavam} demonstrates, according to {\i Rav}, that without the verse she would be considered completely his wife. She is fully married after relations, as {\i Rav} maintains \u8212- and yet the verse nonetheless excludes her from the {\i sotah} process. The reason, on this reading, is not that the marriage is incomplete, but that a marriage entered through {\i yibbum} \u8212- through the continuation of the dead brother\u8217's bond rather than through an independent act of will \u8212- is categorically different from a marriage the man chose. Even though she is fully his wife, it is not the kind of marriage the {\i Torah} placed within the {\i sotah} framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel} responds in kind: I could actually say like {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} \u8212- that the {\i shomeret yavam} does drink. {\i Mideitztrich kra l\u8217'rabuyei, miklal d\u8217'lav ishto hi klal} \u8212- the verse needed to include the {\i shomeret yavam} in the {\i sotah} process, which shows that without it she would not be considered fully his wife at all. That matches {\i Shmuel}\u8217's view that relations without intent to begin a married life do not create full {\i nisuin}. Nevertheless, the verse includes her sufficiently in the {\i parasha} to make her drink \u8212- even this incomplete marriage is enough to trigger the {\i sotah} ordeal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final picture is thus: {\i Rav} says relations create full marriage, and yet a verse excludes the {\i yevama} from {\i sotah} because of the particular nature of {\i yibbum} marriages. {\i Shmuel} says relations without marital intent do not create full {\i nisuin}, and yet a verse includes the {\i yevama} in the {\i sotah} process because even that partial bond is sufficient for the {\i parasha}. Both amoraim can therefore align with either {\i tanna}, because each is reading the textual derivation through his own understanding of what kind of relationship {\i yibbum} creates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the {\i daf} concludes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 25\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Overes Al Das & Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition of Overes Al Das\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a fresh topic, framed as an {\i ibayahu} \u8212- a question raised by the rabbis. Before examining that question, however, it is worth establishing the meaning of the central term: {\i overes al das}, a woman who transgresses the norms of married life. This concept originates in a {\i mishnah} in {\i Kesubos} that distinguishes between {\i das Moshe} \u8212- biblical norms \u8212- and {\i das Yehudis} \u8212- the recognized practices of Jewish women. The category is not about a woman who sins against God; it is specifically about behaviors that affect the marriage itself. The Gemara\u8217's examples of {\i overes al das Moshe} include a woman who does not go to the {\i mikvah} or who feeds her husband non-kosher food. Notably, the Gemara does not include {\i chilul Shabbos} in this list, because a woman who violates Shabbos has sinned between herself and God \u8212- that transgression does not intrinsically damage the marital relationship. By contrast, {\i overes al das} describes conduct that compromises the intimacy of the marriage, or that, in the idiom of the Mishnah, attracts unwanted attention from other men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah enumerates behaviors such as going out with uncovered hair, nursing in public, or engaging in flirtatious interactions with men outside the household. Translated into contemporary terms, these are behaviors perceived as undermining the private sphere of the marriage \u8212- drawing the gaze, or worse, the involvement, of other men, whether practically or in the imagination. These behaviors resemble the {\i sotah} situation in spirit, though they fall short of it in substance: a woman who has not committed adultery, and has not even been subjected to {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah}, may nonetheless be acting in ways that garner her husband\u8217's jealousy and compromise the relationship. If the husband seeks to divorce her on account of such conduct, he may do so without paying her {\i kesuvah} \u8212- she is considered to be at fault, and it is a fault divorce. That is the legal framework of {\i overes al das}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Warning Requirement?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now poses its question: does a woman who is {\i overes al das} require warning \u8212- {\i hasra\u8217'ah} \u8212- in order to forfeit her {\i kesuvah}? The framing of the question is important. The Gemara is not asking whether {\i hasra\u8217'ah} is required in the same sense as in criminal law, where warning is necessary to establish {\i meizid} and enable corporal punishment. Rather, the Gemara is asking whether the husband \u8212- or {\i beis din} \u8212- must first inform the woman that her behavior is unacceptable and that continued conduct will lead to divorce without a {\i kesuvah}, before that consequence can take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara lays out two sides of the question. On one hand, {\i keivan d\u8217'overes al das hi lo ba\u8217'ei hasra\u8217'ah}: since she has already acted inappropriately in a way that damages the marriage, perhaps no warning is needed \u8212- she has forfeited the {\i kesuvah} through her own conduct. On the other hand, perhaps she does require warning, {\i d\u8217'hadra bah} \u8212- so that she has the opportunity to mend her ways. This second framing is revealing: the concern is not that her conduct was technically insufficient, but that a broader, more humane consideration applies. Breaking up a marriage is a serious matter, and perhaps the healthier approach \u8212- one that the rabbis who establish the rule would endorse \u8212- is to first attempt repair rather than rushing to the most severe consequence. Warning signals that the situation can still be corrected, and that divorce without a {\i kesuvah} should be a last resort rather than an automatic outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this same logic does not apply where adultery has actually occurred. In that case there is a {\i halachic} reality \u8212- the woman is forbidden to her husband \u8212- and no amount of willingness to repair the relationship can set that aside. But {\i overes al das} operates in a different register: there is no absolute {\i halachic} prohibition, the relationship is not destroyed in a formal sense, and so the possibility of repair is genuine. Additionally, even if a husband wishes to claim that his wife was {\i overes al das} and refuse to pay her {\i kesuvah}, he would need to bring objective evidence before a court \u8212- the {\i kesuvah} is an obligation, and he cannot simply declare himself exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also ask whether {\i shogeg} \u8212- the woman not realizing she was forbidden from acting in a certain way \u8212- bears on the question. The Gemara does not frame the issue in those terms. It does not say that {\i hasra\u8217'ah} is needed because she was acting unknowingly and therefore should not be penalized as harshly. Instead, the focus is on the broader consideration of preserving the marriage. It is nonetheless worth observing that the degree to which conduct damages a marriage may be affected by whether it was done knowingly or in ignorance \u8212- a woman who defies a known standard has communicated something different about her relationship than one who simply did not understand what was expected of her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusah/Shomeret Yavam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to resolve the question from a source. {\i Ta shema}: an {\i arusah} and a {\i shomeret yavam} do not drink and do not collect a {\i kesuvah}. What is accomplished by the husband performing {\i kinuy} in such cases? The Gemara initially suggests: {\i lav l\u8217'afsidei kesuvaseh} \u8212- it must be to cause her to forfeit her {\i kesuvah}. If so, this would prove that {\i kinuy} is required for that purpose, meaning that the mere fact of her going into seclusion with another man is not sufficient \u8212- you need the additional step of {\i kinuy}, which functions as a form of {\i hasra\u8217'ah}. Why else would the husband need to go through the process of warning her if she were going to lose her {\i kesuvah} simply by having gone into seclusion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye pushes back: {\i lo, le\u8217'osrah alav} \u8212- perhaps the purpose of the {\i kinuy} is not to cause her to lose her {\i kesuvah} at all. Rather, the husband performs {\i kinuy} because he wants to impose upon her the status of {\i sotah}, making her forbidden to him. This is a distinct legal goal \u8212- it is not about the money, but about creating a formal forbidden status. The {\i kinuy} can be used, in other words, not as a financial measure but as an act of imposing a religious-legal status that goes beyond the civil consequence of losing the {\i kesuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa offers another answer: {\i lehashkosah k\u8217'she\u8217'yinasu} \u8212- the husband performs {\i kinuy} now, while she is an {\i arusah}, intending that if she later goes into seclusion after {\i nisuin}, he will be able to make her drink on the basis of the earlier warning. There is a {\i baraisa} that makes this explicit: one cannot make an {\i arusah} drink while she is an {\i arusah}, but one can perform {\i kinuy} while she is an {\i arusah} so that if she goes into seclusion after {\i nisuin}, she becomes a {\i sotah} on the basis of that prior warning. This is a legitimate strategic use of {\i kinuy} \u8212- keeping one\u8217's options open \u8212- and therefore the {\i kinuy} in the case of an {\i arusah} is fully explained without needing to appeal to the {\i kesuvah} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then offers a much stronger proof. {\i Ta shema}: the {\i mishnah} enumerates cases of {\i issurei lavin} \u8212- women whose marriages, though formally binding, are biblically prohibited. These include, for example, a widow married to a {\i Kohen Gadol}, a divorc\u233 ?e or {\i chalutza} married to an ordinary {\i kohen}, a {\i mamzeres} or {\i nesinah} married to a Yisrael, or a daughter of Israel married to a {\i mamzer} or {\i nasin}. In all these cases, the {\i mishnah} rules that such women do not drink and do not collect their {\i kesuvah}. Yet the husband still goes through the process of {\i kinuy}. Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a brilliant point because it eliminates both of Abaye\u8217's and Rav Papa\u8217's answers simultaneously. The answer {\i le\u8217'osrah alav} \u8212- to make her forbidden to him \u8212- cannot apply here, because she is already forbidden by a biblical prohibition; {\i hai issura deka\u8217'i} \u8212- the prohibition already stands. And the answer {\i lehashkosah k\u8217'she\u8217'yinasu} \u8212- to make her drink after {\i nisuin} \u8212- also cannot apply, because they are already in a state of {\i nisuin}; there is no future {\i nisuin} to wait for. So why is {\i kinuy} necessary at all? {\i Ella lav l\u8217'afsidei kesuvaseh} \u8212- it must be solely to cause her to forfeit her {\i kesuvah}. And the implication is that the {\i stirah} alone \u8212- the mere fact of her going into seclusion \u8212- would not have been sufficient. Something more was needed, and that something more is the {\i kinuy}, which here functions as a warning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda of Diskarta then steps in to rescue the earlier approach: {\i le\u8217'osrah labo\u8217'el k\u8217'ba\u8217'al}. The purpose of {\i kinuy} in the cases of {\i issurei lavin} is to make her forbidden to the man she is suspected of having slept with, just as she is forbidden to her husband. A {\i sotah} \u8212- a woman suspected of adultery \u8212- becomes forbidden both to her husband and to the man she is suspected of. If the husband performs {\i kinuy}, establishes the {\i sotah} process, and then for whatever reason divorces her without her ever having a chance to drink \u8212- because he never brought her to the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, or the {\i Beis HaMikdash} was destroyed \u8212- she remains forbidden to that other man. This is a significant and independent legal consequence of {\i kinuy}, entirely separate from the {\i kesuvah}. Therefore, one still cannot prove that {\i hasra\u8217'ah} is required for the {\i kesuvah} from these cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Acts for Incapa\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pivots to a cleaner proof. {\i Ta shema}: the {\i mishnah} teaches that {\i beis din} steps in and performs {\i kinuy} on behalf of a husband in certain cases \u8212- when the husband became a deaf-mute, became insane or mentally incapacitated, or is imprisoned. And the {\i mishnah} specifies the purpose explicitly: {\i lifoslah miksuvashah} \u8212- to cause the wife to lose her {\i kesuvah}. This is not framed as being about making her forbidden to her husband or forbidden to the suspected paramour. It is stated plainly that {\i beis din} acts for this specific purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This seems decisive. If going into seclusion were sufficient on its own to cause her to forfeit the {\i kesuvah}, why would {\i beis din} need to perform {\i kinuy} at all? The witnesses to her {\i stirah} could simply testify, and she would lose the {\i kesuvah} without any further step. The fact that {\i beis din} found it necessary to go through the process of {\i kinuy} indicates that {\i stirah} alone is insufficient \u8212- some form of warning is required, and in the absence of a capable husband, {\i beis din} steps in to provide that warning through {\i kinuy}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion: Warning Need\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, entertains the possibility of distinguishing this case. Perhaps the reason {\i hasra\u8217'ah} is required for the {\i beis din} cases, even though in ordinary circumstances it would not be needed, is that the husband is absent. When the husband is not present \u8212- when he is deaf, insane, or imprisoned \u8212- the wife\u8217's behavior will naturally be less constrained, and her conduct therefore does not carry the same weight of defiance as it would if she were knowingly acting against her present husband\u8217's wishes. In that context, one might say that she cannot be penalized without warning, since her behavior is not as blameworthy. But when the husband is present and she still acts this way, it is worse \u8212- and perhaps there no warning would be needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this distinction. The conclusion stands: {\i ba\u8217'ei hasra\u8217'ah} \u8212- warning is required for a woman to lose her {\i kesuvah} on account of {\i overes al das}. This is a resolution that, remarkably, does not appear explicitly in {\i Kesubos}, where the entire topic of {\i overes al das} is discussed at length. The foundational question of what kind of evidence or warning is needed to forfeit the {\i kesuvah} is answered here, in {\i Sotah} \u8212- making this {\i sugyah} an essential clarification of material studied in a different tractate entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband\u8217's Right to Waive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If Husband Doesn\u8217't Mind?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a second {\i ibayahu}, which is in many ways the more fundamental question. The scenario: the wife has been {\i overes al das}, but the husband does not object \u8212- {\i lo kapid}. He is aware of her behavior and is not troubled by it, or at least does not wish to end the marriage on account of it. Can he simply continue the marriage as if nothing happened, or does the court step in and require that the marriage be dissolved?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kapida} \u8212- or {\i hakpada} \u8212- is perhaps best rendered as something one finds deeply objectionable, something that genuinely disturbs. The question is whether the husband\u8217's subjective disposition \u8212- his not finding the behavior objectionable \u8212- has legal force in this context. The Gemara frames this as: {\i mi amrinan b\u8217'kapida d\u8217'ba\u8217'al tala Rachmana} \u8212- do we say that the whole structure of {\i overes al das} is contingent on the husband\u8217's {\i kapida}? And if he is {\i lo kapid}, the whole framework simply does not apply?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Or do we say, on the other side, {\i keivan deka kapid kapid} \u8212- meaning, since this behavior is the sort of thing that {\i should} bother him, it matters even if he says it does not? Rashi explains this phrase as: since this is conduct that is objectively objectionable, his claimed indifference is beside the point. This can be understood in two ways. One is psychological: perhaps he says he does not mind, but we know that deep down he does, and the marriage is being damaged even if he is not willing to acknowledge it. The other is societal: even if he genuinely does not mind, there is a broader communal interest in not allowing such behavior to be implicitly sanctioned by the continuation of the marriage. The court\u8217's involvement in marking certain conduct as unacceptable is not merely for the benefit of this particular husband; it sends a message about the standards of marriage in the community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a striking position \u8212- the idea that a rabbinical court would compel the dissolution of a marriage that both parties wish to continue. But the comparison is apt: {\i halacha} already determines whether marriages can continue at all, and {\i beis din} is already empowered to intervene in ways that override the personal preferences of the individuals involved. The question is only how far that authority extends into the domain of conduct that, while objectionable, does not create an absolute {\i halachic} prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal vs.\u160 ?Objective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara seeks to resolve the question from the {\i mishnah} about {\i beis din} stepping in for a deaf-mute, insane, or imprisoned husband. If the law were entirely subjective \u8212- if the husband\u8217's {\i kapida} were the sole determining factor \u8212- then {\i beis din} would have no basis to act on his behalf, since the husband might recover or be released from prison and then express that he does not mind his wife\u8217's behavior. How can {\i beis din} presume to act against his potential interests?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds with {\i stama demilta}: generally speaking, {\i keivan d\u8217'avras adas}, since she has violated these norms, we assume that {\i nicha lei} \u8212- it is in his interest for {\i beis din} to act. The presumption is that a normal husband would want his wife\u8217's {\i kesuvah} forfeited under such circumstances, and {\i beis din} acts on that presumption. This answer does not resolve the underlying debate \u8212- it does not tell us definitively whether a fully capable husband who explicitly states he does not mind can preserve the marriage \u8212- but it demonstrates that the mere theoretical possibility of his indifference does not paralyze {\i beis din} from acting in his name. The question of {\i ratzah lekayem mekayem} remains formally open at this stage of the discussion, though the trajectory of the {\i sugyah} implicitly moves toward an answer.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Revoking Kinuy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retract Kinuy?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a third {\i ibayahu}, this one concerning the {\i kinuy} in the {\i sotah} context proper rather than the {\i overes al das} framework. The husband has issued a {\i kinuy} \u8212- he has formally warned his wife not to enter into private seclusion with a specific man \u8212- and then he changes his mind. He becomes {\i mochel}: he forgives, or withdraws, his warning. Can the {\i kinuy} be revoked?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara frames the question around the phrase {\i tala Rachmana}, which now applies literally, since we are dealing with the {\i sotah} case: {\i kinuy} is rooted in the husband\u8217's jealousy and {\i hakpada}. One side says: since it was all driven by his objection, and he has now stopped objecting, the {\i kinuy} dissolves \u8212- {\i kinuyo machul}. The other side says: once he has created this formal legal act, he cannot simply retract it \u8212- {\i lo matzi machil leh}. This second position may be grounded in the idea that the {\i kinuy} is not merely the expression of a subjective emotional state but the invocation of a formal legal process that, once set in motion, operates independently of whether he continues to feel aggrieved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More substantively, one can frame this as a question about what the {\i kinuy} fundamentally is. At the beginning of the {\i masechet}, this tension was already present: is {\i sotah} all about the husband\u8217's jealousy and {\i hakpada}, an outgrowth of the subjective dynamics of the marriage, or has the Gemara transformed it into an objective legal proceeding driven by {\i raglayim ladavar} and the involvement of {\i beis din}? If the former, then when he stops being jealous, the whole structure collapses. If the latter, then the process, once initiated, belongs to an objective domain and is no longer his to withdraw.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Revoke After Stira?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises the more challenging case: can the husband revoke his {\i kinuy} even after the wife has gone into seclusion \u8212- after {\i stirah} has occurred? At that point she has already become a {\i sotah}. He now says: I retroactively retract my {\i kinuy}. Can he do this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stakes of this question are high. If revocation is possible even after {\i stirah}, then the entire {\i sotah} process is ultimately dependent on the husband\u8217's ongoing willingness to pursue it, and the objective legal reality established by {\i stirah} counts for little. If revocation is not possible after {\i stirah}, then the moment she enters seclusion, the case passes from the subjective realm of his jealousy into the objective domain of {\i beis din}, and his feelings at that point become legally irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts several proofs. First it revisits the {\i mishnah} about {\i beis din} acting on behalf of a deaf-mute, insane, or imprisoned husband: if the husband could revoke his {\i kinuy} after the fact, then what {\i beis din} did \u8212- acting in his name \u8212- could be undone by him the moment he recovered. Would {\i beis din} really take an action that was so easily reversible? The Gemara answers: {\i stama demilta mistafi umaski keBeis Dina} \u8212- the presumption is that the husband will go along with what {\i beis din} has done. {\i Beis din} leads, and the husband follows. This is not because he is compelled to agree, but because the ordinary presumption is that he will follow the court\u8217's lead, and the rare case of someone refusing to do so does not prevent {\i beis din} from acting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof from Scholars\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites the {\i mishnah} about escorting the {\i sotah} to court with two Torah scholars, {\i shema yavo aleha baderech} \u8212- lest the husband succumb to temptation and have relations with her along the way, which would be a violation. But if the husband can simply revoke his {\i kinuy} and thereby permit himself to have relations with her \u8212- {\i achil lekinuyacha u\u8217'va\u8217'ol}, as the scholars could instruct him \u8212- then why is the concern about illicit relations relevant at all? He has a {\i heter} readily available!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by explaining precisely why {\i talmidei chachamim} are specifically designated for this escort duty: because they know the {\i halacha}. If it appears that the husband is inclined to have relations with his wife, the scholars can inform him of the proper way to accomplish this \u8212- by first revoking his {\i kinuy}. So the purpose of the scholars is not to prevent any possibility of relations at all costs, but to ensure that whatever course of action the husband takes is done in a legally proper manner. This source, then, does not prove that revocation is impossible; it merely establishes that revocation is a knowledgeable act requiring proper instruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ushaya\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara finds its resolution in a teaching of Rabbi Ushaya. He reports three things that were whispered to him by the young men of Jerusalem. First: {\i ba\u8217'al shemachal al kinuyo kinuyo machul} \u8212- a husband who revokes his {\i kinuy} has effectively revoked it. The {\i kinuy} is undone. Second: a {\i zaken mamre} \u8212- a sage who defies the ruling of the high court \u8212- if {\i beis din} wishes to be {\i mochel} and forgo the prosecution of his case, they may do so. Third: a {\i ben sorer u\u8217'moreh} \u8212- a wayward and rebellious son \u8212- if the parents wish to be {\i mochel}, they may do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These three cases share a common structure: in each, the authority of an institution is being challenged or undermined, and the question is whether the {\i personal} parties who were wronged can waive the institutional consequence. Rabbi Ushaya\u8217's position is that in all three cases, {\i mechilah} is effective. When Rabbi Ushaya brought these teachings to his colleagues in the south, they agreed with him on two of the three: the {\i sotah} case and the {\i ben sorer u\u8217'moreh} case. They disagreed on the {\i zaken mamre}: {\i shelo yarbu machloket beYisrael} \u8212- because the institution of the supreme court is not a private matter. There is one court for all of society, and its authority must be protected for the sake of social order. The defiance of the {\i zaken mamre} is not an offense against the personal dignity of the court\u8217's judges that they could choose to waive; it is a structural threat to the entire system of halachic authority. The parents in {\i ben sorer u\u8217'moreh} and the husband in {\i sotah}, by contrast, are each contending with their own private relationship, and they retain the authority to be {\i mochel} on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes the parallel to the Ramban\u8217's observation that four places in the Torah \u8212- {\i zaken mamre}, {\i ben sorer u\u8217'moreh}, {\i eidim zomemim}, and {\i navi sheker} \u8212- all invoke the phrase {\i vehanish\u8217'arim yishme\u8217'u veyira\u8217'u}, indicating that the punishment is not solely proportional to the gravity of the individual sin, but serves the broader purpose of protecting institutional authority in society. What is telling is that {\i sotah} is conspicuously absent from that list: {\i ki yikne ish et ishto} is explicitly rooted in the husband\u8217's jealousy, making {\i sotah} the paradigmatic case of a law that is anchored in the subjective realm of personal relationships, not in the protection of social institutions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shema minah}: the husband can revoke his {\i kinuy}. The Gemara accepts this conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before vs.\u160 ?After Stira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now that the principle of revocability is established, the Gemara returns to the harder question: can the husband revoke his {\i kinuy} after {\i stirah} has already occurred? Two Amoraim \u8212- Rav Acha and Ravina \u8212- debate this. One says: {\i kodem stirah machul, ve\u8217'achar stirah eino machul} \u8212- revocation is effective only before she goes into seclusion, not after. Once {\i stirah} has occurred, an objective {\i raglayim ladavar} has been created; the case enters the domain of {\i beis din}, and the husband\u8217's retroactive change of heart cannot undo it. The other says: {\i achar stirah nami machul} \u8212- even after {\i stirah} he can revoke, reflecting the view that the entire {\i sotah} structure remains fundamentally about his jealousy and subjective disposition, even after {\i stirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then resolves the debate. {\i Mistabra keman de\u8217'amar eino machul} \u8212- it is most reasonable to hold that after {\i stirah} he cannot revoke. The proof comes from the debate between Rabbi Yosi and the Sages recorded in a {\i baraisa}. Rabbi Yosi held that we need not send scholars to escort the {\i sotah} to court, because the husband can be trusted to control himself just as he controls himself when his wife is a {\i niddah}. The Sages responded: you cannot compare the cases. With {\i niddah}, there is relief in sight \u8212- she will eventually become permitted to him again \u8212- and so he will restrain himself. But with a {\i sotah}, she will never be permitted to him again \u8212- {\i ein lah heter} \u8212- and therefore he may not be able to restrain himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara seizes on this argument. If the husband can revoke his {\i kinuy} even after {\i stirah}, then there {\i is} a {\i heter} available to him \u8212- he can simply be {\i mochel} on the {\i kinuy} and resume relations whenever he wishes. In that case, the Sages\u8217' argument would collapse, because the husband would know he always has a ready remedy. The fact that the Sages argue, and that the Gemara treats their argument as compelling, implies that no such {\i heter} exists after {\i stirah}. {\i Ella lav shema mina achar stirah eino machul shema mina.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion is thus a clean and elegant one: before {\i stirah}, the {\i kinuy} is entirely in the domain of the husband\u8217's subjective jealousy, and he may revoke it at will. After {\i stirah} \u8212- once she has gone into seclusion and the objective {\i raglayim ladavar} has been created \u8212- the case passes into the domain of {\i beis din}, and the husband no longer has the power to halt it unilaterally.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Dies Before Drink\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns the page and introduces a new scenario: the husband died before the {\i sotah} was able to drink the water. The process was initiated, the {\i kinuy} was made, the {\i stirah} occurred, but the husband did not survive to see the {\i sotah} drink. Now the question of {\i kesuvah} arises: is the woman entitled to collect her {\i kesuvah} from the husband\u8217's estate, or does she forfeit it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai} and {\i Beis Hillel} dispute this. The underlying principle at issue is a general rule in monetary law: {\i Beis Shammai} holds {\i shtar ha\u8217'omed ligbos kegavui dami} \u8212- a document that is standing for collection is considered as if already collected. The {\i kesuvah} is such a document \u8212- it is an outstanding obligation to the woman, and since she holds it, she is in a position of possession. The burden of proof therefore rests on the estate to demonstrate that she is not entitled to it, i.e., that she actually committed adultery. Since that can no longer be proven \u8212- the {\i sotah} process was interrupted by the husband\u8217's death \u8212- she collects her {\i kesuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Hillel} disagrees: {\i shtar ha\u8217'omed ligbos lav kegavui dami}. The document standing for collection is not considered as if already collected. In this framework, the estate is still considered {\i muchzak} \u8212- in possession \u8212- and the burden of proof lies with the woman to establish her innocence. Since the {\i sotah} process was never completed and she cannot now prove her innocence, she does not collect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about this formulation is that the question is framed not around whether she is currently a {\i sotah} or currently forbidden to her husband, but around the deeper question of whether she committed adultery. The existence of the {\i sotah} process and its incompletion are almost beside the point; the {\i kesuvah} dispute ultimately comes down to who bears the burden of proof on the underlying factual question. The {\i tosafos} on this passage are extensive, attempting to reconcile this {\i machlokes} with other {\i sugyos} in which the same rule of {\i shtar ha\u8217'omed ligbos} appears to operate differently, noting that the surrounding circumstances make a significant difference in how the rule is applied.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Special Cases: Sotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Infertile Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final section of the daf addresses a {\i mishnah} whose analysis was begun earlier: can a husband make a woman a {\i sotah} when she is incapable of bearing children? This is the position of Rav Nachman citing Rabbah bar Avuha: {\i machlokes be\u8217'akarah u\u8217'zekeinah} \u8212- the dispute in the {\i mishnah} between the Sages and Rabbi Eliezer concerns specifically an {\i akarah}, an infertile woman, or a {\i zekeinah}, an elderly woman. The Sages held that since the husband has an obligation to have children, and this woman cannot provide that, the marriage is not a fully viable one, and therefore she may not drink. Rabbi Eliezer disagreed, arguing that the husband can always marry an additional wife to fulfill that obligation, so this marriage is not thereby rendered invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akarah vs.\u160 ?Aylonit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, all of this \u8212- the entire debate \u8212- applies only to an {\i akarah} or {\i zekeinah}. The case of an {\i aylonit} \u8212- a woman who from birth has been constitutionally incapable of bearing children \u8212- is in a different category entirely, and {\i kuli alma lo pligi}: everyone agrees that an {\i aylonit} does not drink. The reason is not the question of whether the husband must divorce her to fulfill his obligation, but a direct exclusion from the biblical text itself. The Torah uses the phrase {\i venizre\u8217'a zara} \u8212- \u8220"and she shall bear seed\u8221" \u8212- in describing the {\i sotah} ordeal. {\i Mi shedarka lehazri\u8217'a}: the {\i parasha} of {\i sotah} applies only to a woman who is in the category of one who can potentially bear seed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\i akarah} or {\i zekeinah} was once capable of bearing children, or at least was naturally in the category of those who might; their infertility is a medical condition, not an intrinsic feature of their identity. An {\i aylonit}, by contrast, is defined precisely by her congenital inability to bear children \u8212- that is the very essence of the category. She is therefore structurally excluded from {\i yatza zo she\u8217'ein darka lehazri\u8217'a} \u8212- she is outside the {\i parasha} of {\i sotah} entirely, regardless of any questions about the viability of the marriage or the husband\u8217's other obligations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akarah & Zkeinah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Unable to Conceive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 26, and we pick up on 25b with the topic of {\i muberet chaveiro}. The {\i mishnah} deals with women who cannot give birth \u8212- either because they are elderly ({\i zkeinah}) or infertile ({\i akarah}) \u8212- and their status with respect to the {\i sotah} procedure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Requirement Deba\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} ruled that such a woman cannot be made to drink the {\i sotah} water, because she falls into a category of women whom the husband is obligated to divorce. The reason is that he must fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu}, and remaining married to a woman incapable of bearing children prevents this. To this, {\i Rabbi Eliezer} cogently responds: there is no need to divorce her, since he could simply marry another woman and thereby fulfill {\i pru u\u8217'revu}. The marriage to the infertile or elderly woman thus remains valid, and consequently he may administer the {\i sotah} water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then records the view of Rav Nachman citing Rabbah bar Bar Chana: {\i machloket be\u8217'akarah u\u8217'zkeinah} \u8212- the debate between the {\i Chachamim} and {\i Rabbi Eliezer} concerns only the {\i akarah} and the {\i zkeinah}. But regarding the {\i ailonit}, all agree \u8212- {\i divrei hakol} \u8212- that she does not drink and does not collect her {\i ketuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit & Zera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ailonit} is a woman with pronounced male characteristics \u8212- she does not develop pubic hair or breasts, and it is physically impossible for her to conceive. The reason she is categorically excluded is not the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru u\u8217'revu} but rather a separate derivation from the verse itself: {\i veniktah venizra zara} \u8212- \u8220"and she shall be cleansed and bear seed.\u8221" The Gemara derives: {\i mi shedarkah lehazria} \u8212- the entire {\i sotah} category pertains only to a woman capable of bearing seed; {\i yatza zo she\u8217'ein darkah lehazria} \u8212- it excludes one for whom that is impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is this different from an {\i akarah}? Rashi explains that the {\i ailonit}\u8217's incapacity has existed from birth \u8212- it is a congenital condition, not a circumstantial one. The same logic would apply, presumably, to any woman born without the anatomical capacity to conceive. An {\i akarah}, by contrast, is someone who might have been expected to conceive; who knows, perhaps the waters themselves would effect a cure. The {\i ailonit}, however, is categorically outside the framework of {\i nizra zara} from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This emphasis on {\i zera} is worth pausing over. The {\i sotah} waters cause the guilty woman\u8217's body to deteriorate, or alternatively cause an innocent woman to conceive. The jealousy undergirding the entire {\i sotah} process is not merely about the act of intercourse itself but about the concern over whose child a woman might be carrying. The focus on {\i zera} throughout this {\i parsha} reflects that central preoccupation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arusah & Muberet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinuy Timing Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a challenge from a {\i beraita} that lists a series of cases. A man who performs {\i kinuy} \u8212- the formal warning of jealousy \u8212- directed at his {\i arusah} (betrothed woman) or his {\i shomeret yavam} (a widow awaiting {\i yibum}): if she was secluded with the suspected man ({\i stirah}) before the couple actually came together through {\i nisuim} or {\i yibum}, she cannot be made to drink and does not collect her {\i ketuvah}. This reflects the principle of {\i tachat ishah} \u8212- the {\i sotah} laws apply only once the couple is living a fully married life together. However, if the {\i stirah} occurred after {\i nisuim} or {\i yibum} were completed, then the earlier {\i kinuy} remains operative, as the {\i mishnah} has already taught.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Muberet & Meneket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another case where the {\i sotah} procedure does not apply: a man who married a woman still carrying ({\i muberet}) or nursing ({\i meneket}) a child from a previous relationship. This is the position of {\i Rabbi Meir}, who held that one may not marry a woman who is pregnant with or nursing another man\u8217's child \u8212- the concern being for the welfare of that child. The worry is that the new husband, whose investment in the child is limited, might be insufficiently careful in ways that could harm the child\u8217's health. {\i Rabbi Meir} maintained that if such a marriage was contracted in violation of this rule, the husband must divorce her ({\i yotzi}), and moreover, he may never remarry her ({\i ve\u8217'lo yachazir olamit}). Since in {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's view the marriage must be dissolved, the {\i sotah} procedure cannot be administered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage After Nursing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim}, however, take a more lenient position: {\i yotzi u\u8217'ksheyagia zemana liknos yichnos} \u8212- he separates from her, but once the pregnancy or nursing period has concluded, he may return to her. Since the separation is temporary and not a permanent dissolution of the marriage, this woman retains her status within the marital framework, and she can accordingly be made to drink the {\i sotah} water once the relevant period has passed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beraita Cases List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Young Man & Old Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now cites a comprehensive {\i beraita} that systematically lists cases in which the {\i sotah} procedure either does or does not apply. The first case involves a young man who married an {\i akarah} or {\i zkeinah} and has no children from a prior wife. In this situation, {\i lo shotah ve\u8217'lo notelet ketubatah} \u8212- she cannot be made to drink, and she does not collect her {\i ketuvah}. This aligns with the {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i mishnah}: since he is obligated to fulfill {\i pru u\u8217'revu} and has no other means of doing so, he must divorce this woman, and therefore the {\i sotah} framework is inapplicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} counters: {\i yachol lissa acheret velifrot heimena} \u8212- he can marry another woman and have children through her. There is therefore no requirement to divorce his current wife, and accordingly she can be made to drink the {\i sotah} water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinuy Post-Nisuim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita} continues: if a man performed {\i kinuy} toward his {\i arusah} or his {\i shomeret yavam} while she was still in that pre-marital status, but the {\i stirah} occurred only after they entered into full marriage ({\i nisuim} or {\i yibum}), then {\i o shotah o lo notelet ketubatah} \u8212- she either drinks, or she forfeits her {\i ketuvah} without drinking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pregnant/Nursing Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If his own wife is pregnant with or nursing his own child, {\i o shotah o lo notelet ketubatah} \u8212- she can be made to drink. This case is presented almost as an obvious contrast to the earlier case: he has no reason to divorce her, and we assume he will attend to the health of his own child with appropriate care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Young Man Has Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, if a young man married an {\i akarah} or {\i zkeinah} but already has children from another wife, {\i o shotah o notelet ketubatah} \u8212- there is no {\i pru u\u8217'revu} concern, the marriage stands, and she can be made to drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer & Others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita} then lists a series of valid, if unconventional, marriages: the wife of a {\i mamzer} married to a {\i mamzeret} or a {\i giyoret}; the wife of a {\i natin}; the wife of a {\i ger} or a freed slave ({\i eved meshuchrar}); and an {\i ailonit}. In all these cases, {\i o shotah o lo notelet ketubatah} \u8212- the woman can be made to drink. The {\i Gemara} will analyze each of these cases momentarily, but first it focuses on the inclusion of the {\i ailonit}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ailonit Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beraita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita} explicitly states that an {\i ailonit} can be made to drink \u8212- {\i katani miha ailonit}. This appears to be a direct contradiction to Rav Nachman, who held that all authorities agree an {\i ailonit} cannot drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman responds: {\i tanna\u8217'ei hi} \u8212- it is a dispute among {\i Tanna\u8217'im}. Rav Nachman aligns himself with one particular {\i tanna}: {\i ve\u8217'ana d\u8217'amri ki hai tanna} \u8212- I follow this {\i tanna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon ben Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} is then cited: {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} says an {\i ailonit} does not drink and does not collect her {\i ketuvah}, from the verse {\i veniktah venizra zara} \u8212- {\i mi shedarkah lehazria yatzah zo she\u8217'ein darkah lehazria}. This is precisely the reasoning articulated earlier by Rav Nachman: the {\i sotah} procedure is restricted to a woman for whom conception is a natural possibility, categorically excluding the {\i ailonit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Interpret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But how do the {\i Rabbanan} \u8212- who include the {\i ailonit} \u8212- read the same verse? They hold that {\i veniktah venizra zara} is required for a different teaching, recorded in another {\i beraita}: {\i Rabbi Akiva} understands the verse in its most literal sense \u8212- that if the woman was infertile ({\i akarah}) and is innocent, the waters will actually render her fertile. The verse, on his reading, promises a genuine blessing of childbirth where none existed before.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael} found this reading troubling. If it were true that the {\i sotah} waters cure infertility, one would create a perverse incentive: {\i im ken yistaru kol ha\u8217'akarot ve\u8217'yifkedu} \u8212- all infertile women would arrange for their husbands to perform {\i kinuy} and engineer a {\i stirah} so that they could drink and become fertile. Beyond the moral problem, {\i Rabbi Yishmael} notes the injustice: {\i ve\u8217'zo ho\u8217'il ve\u8217'lo nistarah hifsidah} \u8212- if an infertile woman who never went into seclusion with another man is thereby denied this miraculous cure, she has been treated unfairly for simply having remained faithful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Childbirth Benefits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, {\i Rabbi Yishmael} proposes that the verse promises more modest improvements: {\i she\u8217'im haytah yoledet b\u8217'tzaar teled b\u8217'revach} \u8212- if she previously gave birth with great pain, she will now give birth with ease. {\i Nekeivot teled zecharim} \u8212- if she previously bore daughters, she will now bear sons, since sons were socially preferred in that context. {\i Ketzarim teled aruchim} \u8212- if her children were short, they will now be tall. {\i Shechorim teled levanim} \u8212- if they were dark-skinned, they will now be lighter-skinned. These details offer a striking sociological window into the values and aesthetic preferences of the time. {\i Rabbi Yishmael} was unconcerned that women would engineer the {\i sotah} process merely for these secondary benefits; the incentive is simply not strong enough. The radical incentive \u8212- moving from infertility to fertility \u8212- was the problem, not these more modest enhancements.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Valid Marriages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to analyze the {\i beraita}\u8217's list of cases. If a {\i mamzer} is married to a {\i mamzeret} or a {\i giyoret}, {\i peshita} \u8212- obviously it is a valid marriage. Why would we think otherwise? The concern might be: {\i afushei psulim lo lifshash} \u8212- perhaps one should not encourage a {\i mamzer} and {\i mamzeret} to remain married, given that their children will also be {\i mamzerim}. The {\i Gemara} therefore teaches that we are not concerned with this; people have a right to marry, and the {\i sotah} procedure applies to this couple.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The wife of a {\i ger} or {\i eved meshuchrar} \u8212- again, {\i peshita}, obviously. The concern is the verse {\i dabber el bnei Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"speak to the children of Israel\u8221" \u8212- which might be read as excluding {\i gerim}. The {\i Gemara} responds that {\i ve\u8217'amarta aleihem} \u8212- \u8220"and you shall say to them\u8221" \u8212- serves as a {\i ribbuy}, an expansion, including {\i gerim} within the {\i parsha}. And an {\i eved meshuchrar} has the same status as a {\i ger}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This formulation is worth noting carefully. The {\i Gemara} implies that a {\i ger} is not encompassed within the literal meaning of {\i bnei Yisrael} and requires a separate {\i ribbuy} to be included. It may be that the issue is specifically with {\i bnei Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"children of Israel\u8221" \u8212- which focuses on patrilineal descent, rather than with {\i Yisrael} more broadly. Even so, the apparent need for an additional verse to include {\i gerim} is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen\u8217's Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the wife of a {\i kohen} returns us to the {\i mishnah}. {\i Peshita} \u8212- obviously she can drink. Why might one have thought otherwise?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape & Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse states {\i ve\u8217'hi lo nitpasa asura} \u8212- \u8220"and if she was not raped, she is forbidden\u8221" \u8212- implying that if she was raped, she is {\i permitted} to her husband. This {\i halakha} applies to ordinary women. But the wife of a {\i kohen} is subject to a more stringent rule: even if she was raped, she becomes forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband. Since the {\i pesukim} seem to presuppose that rape results in the woman remaining permitted, one might have thought the {\i parsha} does not apply to the {\i kohen}\u8217's wife at all, and that she therefore cannot be made to drink. The {\i Gemara} teaches that she is nonetheless included in the {\i sotah} procedure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen\u8217's Wife Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, once she drinks and the waters demonstrate her innocence, she is permitted to return to her {\i kohen} husband. This too seems obvious \u8212- {\i peshita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Illness Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa addresses a subtle case: a woman who is showing signs of physical deterioration \u8212- {\i misnavna}. We know that a guilty woman with great merit ({\i zechut}) may not suffer the full effects of the {\i sotah} waters immediately but will gradually decline in health over time. One might therefore think that a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife showing such symptoms should not be permitted to return to her husband, as the symptoms might indicate delayed guilt. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i b\u8217'misnavna derech evarim} \u8212- the ailments in question are not the characteristic symptoms associated with the {\i sotah}\u8217's punishment. She has a headache, or various other bodily discomforts \u8212- but not the specific deterioration of the thigh and stomach that the {\i Torah} describes. These general symptoms do not prove guilt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape Symptoms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if she is not showing those characteristic symptoms, what is the {\i chidush}? The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i ma d\u8217'teima hazinnai zannai} \u8212- one might have thought she actually did commit the act willingly, and the reason the waters are not producing their characteristic effect \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ha d\u8217'lo badku amaya} \u8212- is that the act was in fact performed under compulsion ({\i be-ones}). In the case of the {\i kohen}\u8217's wife, compulsion makes no moral difference \u8212- she is forbidden to him regardless, since any intercourse with another man, even against her will, disqualifies the wife of a {\i kohen}. One might therefore speculate that the unusual symptom pattern is signaling exactly that: she was compelled, she is therefore forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband, and yet the waters do not produce the full effect because she bears no moral culpability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rules that we do not construct such reasoning out of thin air \u8212- {\i kamashma lan} that she remains permitted. It is worth noting, however, how troubling it is that the {\i Gemara} employs the term {\i zannai} \u8212- \u8220"she fornicated\u8221" \u8212- in a context where the woman was raped. The {\i Gemara} appears to use it as a shorthand for \u8220"she had intercourse with another man,\u8221" but the terminology is jarring. A more neutral formulation \u8212- {\i de\u8217'ivla}, that she had intercourse \u8212- would have been more appropriate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Man\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife of Seris\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: the wife of a {\i seris} \u8212- a man who cannot produce children \u8212- can be made to drink the {\i sotah} water. {\i Peshita} \u8212- obviously. Why might one have thought otherwise? The verse says {\i mibila\u8217'adei ishecha} \u8212- \u8220"other than your husband\u8221" \u8212- and one might think the {\i seris} is not in the category of a husband contemplated by the verse, since the concern over {\i zera} does not arise with him. The {\i Gemara} rejects this: he is capable of intercourse and erection, even if he cannot father children. Rashi clarifies that this is not a case of {\i patzua dakah} \u8212- a specific halachic category of genital injury \u8212- but simply a man who is infertile by some other means, and who is fully capable of normal marital life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking here is that the verse does include the language of {\i shichvat zera}: {\i ve\u8217'shachav ish otah shichvat zera} \u8212- \u8220"a man lay with her in the lying of seed.\u8221" One might have thought this demands that the suspected paramour be capable of producing {\i zera}. The {\i Gemara} rules that this is not so \u8212- just as we concluded (against Rav Nachman\u8217's excluded position) that the {\i ailonit} is included even though she cannot bear {\i zera}, so too the {\i seris} is included even though he cannot produce it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seris Does Kinuy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, the {\i seris} himself can perform {\i kinuy}. And if a {\i seris} had intercourse with the daughter of a {\i kohen} ({\i bat kohen}), he can invalidate her \u8212- render her unable to eat {\i terumah} as a result of intercourse with a {\i pasul} individual, such as a {\i chalal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shachuf Kinuy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same rules apply to a {\i shachuf} \u8212- a man whose body has deteriorated in a way that impairs his sexual function, but who retains some capacity for intercourse. Shmuel teaches that a {\i shachuf} does {\i kinuy}, and that intercourse with a {\i bat kohen} can similarly render her {\i pesulah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zera Not Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} makes explicit: {\i peshita} \u8212- obviously he can do {\i kinuy}. The potential objection would have been {\i im niphat ve\u8217'shachav ish otah shichvat zera} \u8212- the verse\u8217's mention of {\i shichvat zera} might have implied that the suspected paramour must be capable of ejaculation. The {\i Gemara} rules that the {\i zera} requirement is not operative here; {\i kamashma lan} that it does not matter whether the man in question can produce {\i zera}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalidation Regardless\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the {\i shachuf}\u8217's ability to invalidate a {\i bat kohen} for {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i peshita}, obviously. The concern might have been: {\i lo yechalel zaro} \u8212- we learn that when a {\i chalal} has intercourse with a {\i bat kohen}, she and any offspring become {\i challal} and {\i chalalah} through the act. If the child \u8212- {\i zaro} \u8212- is the mechanism of {\i chillul}, perhaps a man who cannot produce {\i zera} cannot effect the {\i chillul}. The {\i Gemara} rules: {\i kamashma lan} that the disqualification applies regardless.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jew & Behemah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Kinuy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to analyze who is excluded by the phrase {\i she\u8217'eino ish} \u8212- a \u8220"non-man.\u8221" Having considered and dismissed various candidates, the discussion now turns to the {\i oved kochavim} (non-Jew). Rav Hamnuna teaches: a non-Jew can be the subject of a {\i kinuy} \u8212- a husband may warn his wife not to seclude herself with a particular non-Jew \u8212- and if she violated the warning, she can be made to undergo the {\i sotah} procedure. Furthermore, if a non-Jew had intercourse with a {\i bat kohen}, he renders her invalid for {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Already Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Peshita} \u8212- obviously a non-Jew can be the subject of {\i kinuy}. The potential objection is drawn from the {\i pasuk}\u8217's formulation about the woman becoming forbidden to the {\i ba\u8217'al} and to the {\i bo\u8217'el} simultaneously: this might suggest the entire {\i parsha} applies only when the act itself would create a new prohibition on the woman relative to the suspected man. But a Jewish woman is already forbidden to a non-Jew \u8212- {\i asura ve\u8217'kayma} \u8212- so perhaps the {\i parsha} does not apply?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Invalidates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rules that the {\i parsha} does apply. Regarding the invalidation for {\i terumah}: {\i peshita}, obviously. The concern here is the verse {\i u\u8217'vat kohen ki siheye le\u8217'ish zar} \u8212- when a {\i bat kohen} is \u8220"with a foreign man,\u8221" this effects her disqualification. One might have thought this refers only to a man she could theoretically be married to \u8212- someone with whom {\i havayah} (a binding marital relationship) is conceivable \u8212- and that a non-Jew, with whom marriage is categorically impossible, would fall outside this framework. {\i Kamashma lan} \u8212- we learn from Rav Hamnuna\u8217's ruling that she is nonetheless disqualified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this is the teaching of Rabbi Yochanan in the name of {\i Rabbi Yishmael}: how do we know that if a non-Jew or a slave had intercourse with a {\i kohenet}, a {\i leviyah}, or even a {\i bat Yisrael}, she is rendered {\i pesulah}? From the verse {\i u\u8217'vat kohen ki siheye almana u\u8217'grusha} \u8212- \u8220"and the daughter of a {\i kohen} who becomes a widow or a divorcee.\u8221" {\i Mi she\u8217'yesh lo bah almanus ve\u8217'gerushin} \u8212- only a man in whose context widowhood and divorce are applicable; {\i yatza oved kochavim ve\u8217'eved she\u8217'ein lo bah almanus ve\u8217'gerushin} \u8212- this excludes the non-Jew and slave, for whom these concepts have no application, meaning that intercourse with such an individual always creates disqualification, since there is no permissible context in which such intercourse could occur without consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Behemah Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa then provides the answer to what {\i she\u8217'eino ish} actually excludes: {\i behemah} \u8212- an animal. If a husband says \u8220"do not be secluded with this animal,\u8221" that does not constitute a valid {\i kinuy}, because {\i ein znus b\u8217'vehemah} \u8212- there is no concept of {\i znus} (adulterous violation) with an animal. The act is certainly forbidden, but it does not fall within the category of {\i znus} that the {\i sotah parsha} presupposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mi\u8217'Pashika asked Rav Ashi: what is the source for the principle that there is no concept of {\i znus} with a {\i behemah}? The answer comes from the verse {\i lo tavi esnan zona u\u8217'mechir kelev} \u8212- do not bring to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} an animal given as payment to a {\i zona} (prostitute) or acquired in exchange for a dog. A {\i beraita} derives: if the payment is an animal given in exchange for sleeping with a dog, or an animal given to purchase a {\i zona} (in Rashi\u8217's reading, to purchase a female slave), such an animal is in fact {\i permitted} as a {\i korban} \u8212- {\i shenayim ve\u8217'lo arba\u8217'ah}, only two [items are forbidden], not four. Since payment for intercourse with a dog is not considered {\i esnan} \u8212- the prohibited \u8220"harlot\u8217's fee\u8221" \u8212- this demonstrates that intercourse with a {\i behemah} is not conceptually {\i znus}, which is the principle applied here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shichvat Zera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anal Intercourse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: given that we have accounted for the exclusion of {\i behemah}, what work is the term {\i shichvat zera} doing in the verse? What does it come to exclude? A {\i beraita} answers: {\i shichvat zera perat l\u8217'davar acher} \u8212- it excludes \u8220"another matter.\u8221" What is this \u8220"another matter\u8221"? Rav Sheshes explains: it excludes a case where the husband\u8217's {\i kinuy} was formulated specifically with reference to anal intercourse ({\i she\u8217'lo k\u8217'darkah}). Since that mode of intercourse does not involve the natural context of conception, it is not included within {\i shichvat zera}, and such a {\i kinuy} would be invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects to this: {\i she\u8217'lo k\u8217'darkah mishkevei isha ksiv} \u8212- we know from the {\i Torah}\u8217's treatment of forbidden sexual acts that anal intercourse between a man and a woman is halachically considered as much an act of intercourse as vaginal intercourse. The term {\i mishkevei isha} encompasses both modes. One could have argued that this is precisely why {\i shichvat zera} is needed \u8212- to limit the {\i sotah parsha} to the standard mode of intercourse, given the emphasis on {\i zera}. But since we have already established that neither the woman nor the suspected man need be capable of producing {\i zera}, it would be inconsistent to then exclude a mode of intercourse that is otherwise fully recognized as intercourse under {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Intercourse Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore offers an alternative: {\i perat le\u8217'shikina lah derech evarim} \u8212- the verse excludes a case where the {\i kinuy} was directed at non-intercourse sexual acts: oral contact, or other forms of sexual behavior that do not constitute intercourse. Even though such acts are clearly a serious violation of the marital relationship, they would not trigger the {\i sotah} procedure and would not render the woman forbidden to her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye on Kissing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges even this: {\i peritzuta be\u8217'alma hi} \u8212- these acts are mere licentiousness. They are obviously not intercourse. Why would I need a verse to exclude them? {\i Uperitzuta mi assar Rachmana?} \u8212- these acts do not make the woman forbidden to her husband in any case. There is simply no need for a verse to teach this. Abaye therefore proposes a different reading: the exclusion is directed at a {\i kinuy} concerning {\i neshikah} \u8212- a term encompassing the touching of the genitals \u8212- what the {\i Gemara} calls {\i hachnasat atarah} \u8212- the initial physical contact that precedes full intercourse. Though this is a hair\u8217's breadth from intercourse, if one holds that {\i hachnasat atarah} is not yet technically {\i bi\u8217'ah}, then it makes sense for a verse to be needed to exclude it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinuy Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i Gemara} notes that this reasoning works only according to the opinion that {\i hara\u8217'ah} means {\i hachnasat atarah} \u8212- that the beginning of intercourse is defined by full penetration. According to the opinion that {\i hara\u8217'ah} means {\i neshikah} \u8212- that even initial external contact constitutes the commencement of intercourse \u8212- it would not make sense for the verse to exclude this, since it is effectively intercourse. Tosafot notes that this would be the point of the exclusion: it is an act of intercourse, but not one in the context of {\i zera}, and therefore outside the {\i parsha}. The {\i Gemara}, however, declines to go in that direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore returns to Rava\u8217's answer: {\i le\u8217'olam le\u8217'shikina lah derech evarim} \u8212- the verse indeed excludes non-intercourse sexual acts. And in response to Abaye\u8217's challenge that such a {\i kinuy} is obviously ineffective, the {\i Gemara} clarifies that the novelty lies elsewhere: one might have thought that even without intercourse, the {\i sotah} procedure should apply because the husband has expressed explicit jealousy \u8212- {\i bikfida deba\u8217'al talya milta} \u8212- and he is plainly aggrieved. {\i Kamashma lan} that this is not so: without actual intercourse or a genuine concern about intercourse, the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah} does not operate. The husband\u8217's jealousy alone, directed at conduct that falls short of intercourse, is insufficient to invoke the {\i sotah} framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 27\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zera & Bracketing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zera Theme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The central theme running through the opening discussion of Daf 27 is the role of {\i zera} \u8212- seed \u8212- in the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah}. The term appears repeatedly in the relevant {\i pesukim}: {\i v\u8217'nikesah v\u8217'nizre\u8217'ah zera}, \u8220"she will be cleansed and give forth seed,\u8221" and {\i v\u8217'shachav ish osah shichvat zera}, \u8220"a man slept with her, a lying of seed.\u8221" These formulations place {\i zera} at the heart of the {\i sotah} ritual both linguistically and experientially.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ramban\u8217's Zera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ramban observed that much of the jealousy underlying {\i sotah} is fueled by the husband\u8217's deep-seated anxiety \u8212- not merely that his wife has been with another man, but that she may have been impregnated by him. The fundamental concern is whether he will find himself raising another man\u8217's children. This is why the {\i pesukim} emphasize {\i zera} so insistently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ritual Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This emphasis on {\i zera} is not incidental but structural. The very effects of the {\i sotah} water mirror this concern. If she is innocent, the outcome is {\i v\u8217'nikesah v\u8217'nizre\u8217'ah zera} \u8212- she conceives and gives birth. If she is guilty, the water enters her and causes her belly to swell, producing a kind of perverse pregnancy. The water thus functions as a liquid that travels inward, toward the same place {\i zera} would travel, but in the opposite mode: rather than generating life, it generates a grotesque distension. The concern about whose {\i zera} is within her is literalized in the ritual\u8217's physiological effects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sterile Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet precisely here the {\i Gemara} begins a systematic process of bracketing the halachic significance of {\i zera}. Even a woman who is {\i akarah} or {\i zekeinah} \u8212- sterile or post-menopausal \u8212- remains within the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah}, despite the fact that pregnancy would be impossible. The only figure whose inclusion was genuinely debated was the {\i aylonit}, a categorically infertile woman, and even that was contested. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's willingness to include women for whom the {\i zera}-concern is biologically irrelevant already signals that the formal halachic category operates independently of the underlying anxiety.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Impotent Husbands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic applies on the husband\u8217's side. Even if the husband is unable to be {\i mazria} \u8212- unable to ejaculate \u8212- this does not remove his wife from the {\i parsha}. Whatever the concern about whose {\i zera} the child carries, the husband\u8217's own incapacity in that regard is halachically immaterial. Perhaps, one might say, it makes things worse \u8212- or perhaps, paradoxically, better, since he already knows any child cannot be his. Either way, the {\i Gemara} does not allow that calculus to enter the legal framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Impotent Suspected Men\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Continuing on the same trajectory, the {\i Gemara} establishes that even if the suspected adulterer \u8212- the {\i shachuf}, a man described as unable to be {\i mazria} \u8212- cannot ejaculate, the case still falls within the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah}. The {\i pesukim} themselves, read carefully, teach that this condition is irrelevant. In every direction \u8212- the woman, the husband, the suspected man \u8212- {\i halacha} brackets the significance of {\i zera} even as the {\i pesukim} make it central.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mi She\u8217'eino Ish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns to the phrase {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish} \u8212- \u8220"one who is not a man\u8221" \u8212- asking what category of person this expression is meant to exclude from the {\i sotah} framework. Several possibilities are considered. It cannot mean a man who cannot be {\i mazria}, since such a man is included. It cannot mean a non-Jew, since a woman suspected of relations with a non-Jew is also included in the {\i parsha}. The discussion thus moves toward more categorical exclusions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Between Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One possibility conspicuously absent from the list of {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish} is the case of another woman. The reason is structural rather than accidental: for halachic purposes, sexual contact between two women is not defined as an act of sex. In {\i halacha}, the act of sex is always defined in terms of penile penetration \u8212- vaginal or anal. This is why there can be a formal prohibition concerning sexual acts between two men, whose act is defined as sex, but no parallel prohibition structured as {\i bi\u8217'ah} between two women, whose contact does not meet that formal definition. The absence of this case from the discussion is not an oversight; it reflects a definitional boundary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bestiality Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish} ultimately excludes, according to the {\i Gemara}, is {\i behemah} \u8212- an animal. An act of bestiality is a gravely prohibited act, but it does not make a woman a {\i sotah} in the technical sense. The {\i Gemara} reasons that because this is {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish} \u8212- not a human male \u8212- the {\i sotah} framework does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shade Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An interesting marginal case was raised in the name of an {\i acharon} \u8212- cited by Rabbi Shmuel Herzfeld \u8212- who suggested that {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish} might also encompass a shade or spirit. A historical case is invoked in which a woman whose husband had been away returned pregnant and claimed to have been impregnated by a shade. The argument that this constituted {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish} could have allowed her to continue living with her husband \u8212- but only in a social context in which such a claim was considered plausible. That such a line of reasoning could be entertained at all reflects the degree to which these categories were understood to depend on the nature of the entity involved, not merely on the act itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shichvat Zera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Behemah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i behemah} falls under {\i mi she\u8217'eino ish}, the {\i Gemara} returns to the precise mechanism of exclusion. The {\i Gemara} asks: what does the {\i idach} \u8212- the alternative category \u8212- come to exclude? Rav Papa answers: {\i le\u8217'a\u8217'tuyei beheimah} \u8212- to exclude the case of bestiality. The reason, he says, is {\i de\u8217'ein zenut be-beheimah} \u8212- bestiality is not considered an act of {\i zenut}, fornication. This does not mean bestiality is not a prohibition; it is {\i chayav mitah}. But it is not categorized within the ordinary framework of sexual sin, of a woman betraying her husband. It is a perverse act, but not {\i zenut} in the technical sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava of Parzikaya then challenges: from where do we know that bestiality is not considered {\i zenut}? The principle that {\i ein zenut be-beheimah} needs a source, not merely an intuition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Etnan Zonah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source is derived from the verse {\i lo tavi etnan zonah u-mechir kelev beit Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- do not bring the payment of a prostitute into the Temple. The {\i etnan zonah} \u8212- the gift paid to a prostitute \u8212- cannot be used as a {\i korban}. This establishes the formal category of {\i zenut} as operative in the context of human prostitution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Etnan Kelev\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-mechir kelev} \u8212- the exchange price of a dog \u8212- similarly cannot be brought as a {\i korban}. The {\i peshat} understanding is that a sheep exchanged for a dog is excluded, though scholars note the phrase may euphemistically refer to payment to a male prostitute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then teaches: {\i etnan kelev u-mechir zonah} \u8212- however, if the payment was for bestiality with a dog, or a woman was sold for {\i zenut}, these are {\i mutarin}, permitted in the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. The source is the phrase {\i gam sheneihem} \u8212- \u8220"both of them\u8221" \u8212- which the {\i Gemara} reads as {\i shenayim v\u8217'lo arba}, meaning only the two originally mentioned categories are prohibited, not four.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Zenut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is clear: since there is no concept of {\i etnan kelev} \u8212- no formal category of \u8220"payment for sex with an animal\u8221" \u8212- bestiality does not fall within the legal structure of sexual sin. It is an obvious prohibition, gravely serious, but not categorized as {\i zenut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zenut Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a genuinely difficult question: what, precisely, is the halachic content of the statement that bestiality is {\i ein zenut}? What specific {\i halacha} turns on whether something is classified as {\i zenut}? The {\i Gemara} does not fully spell this out. One dimension relevant here is whether the act constitutes a betrayal of the husband in the relational sense that would bring a woman within the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah}. What \u8220"not {\i zenut}\u8221" means in terms of other categories \u8212- whether it affects {\i gilui arayot}, {\i yehareg ve\u8217'al ya\u8217'avor}, and so forth \u8212- remains debated and not resolved in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Shichvat Zera?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks a fundamental question: {\i ve-ela shichvat zera lama li} \u8212- given all we have said, what function does the phrase {\i shichvat zera} actually serve? It does not exclude the impotent husband, the impotent suspected man, or the sterile woman \u8212- all of whom remain within the {\i parsha}. The {\i Gemara} has systematically bracketed every concrete implication of {\i shichvat zera}. So what, then, is it doing in the {\i pesukim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anal vs Vaginal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers by citing a {\i baraita}: {\i shichvat zera, perat l\u8217'davar acher} \u8212- {\i shichvat zera} comes to exclude something else. What is this \u8220"something else\u8221"? Rav Sheshes explains: {\i perat she-kina lah she-lo ke-darkah} \u8212- it excludes the case where the husband\u8217's {\i kinui} was specifically directed at the suspicion of anal sex. In that case, even though the act is halachically defined as {\i bi\u8217'ah} in other contexts \u8212- it is an act of sex \u8212- it is excluded from {\i sotah} because it is not an act that could produce {\i zera} in any biological sense. {\i Shichvat zera} thus demands vaginal intercourse, a physical act that under other circumstances could lead to pregnancy, as opposed to anal intercourse, which could never do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking claim. The argument is that even though anal sex is defined as {\i bi\u8217'ah} throughout {\i halacha}, {\i shichvat zera} narrows the {\i sotah} case specifically to the vaginal act \u8212- the act that, categorically speaking, is associated with the possibility of pregnancy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rav Sheshes immediately. He points out that {\i she-lo ke-darkah} \u8212- anal sex \u8212- is indeed defined as sex by {\i halacha}. One cannot so easily exclude it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishkevei Isha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds by invoking the phrase {\i mishkevei ishah}, \u8220"the lyings of a woman,\u8221" which appears in a different context and teaches that there are {\i two} forms of {\i bi\u8217'ah} that are halachically recognized: vaginal and anal. Both are formally defined as acts of sex. This directly undermines Rav Sheshes\u8217' position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anal Sex Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} reinforces the point: even {\i mishkav zachar}, the prohibition of sex between men, is framed in terms of {\i mishkevei ishah}, demonstrating that anal sex is indeed categorically defined as sex. There is no room within {\i halacha} to treat it as something less.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limbs Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore revises the answer: {\i perat she-kina lah derech evarim} \u8212- {\i shichvat zera} excludes the case where the {\i kinui} was directed at sexual activity performed through limbs \u8212- oral or manual stimulation, not penetrative sex of any kind. That would be the excluded category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peritzuta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, challenges this as well. He argues: does one need a {\i pasuk} to exclude such cases? {\i Peritzuta be-alma hi} \u8212- that is simply licentiousness, not an act of adultery. And {\i peritzuta mi asar Rachmana} \u8212- the Torah does not define that as forbidden adultery. Abaye is insisting on a rigidly formal position: what falls outside the halachic definition of adultery requires no exclusion, because it was never included to begin with. The {\i Gemara} is being highly formalistic at this juncture \u8212- rather than allowing the husband\u8217's subjective jealousy to expand the scope of {\i sotah}, the {\i Gemara} insists on applying the same categories that operate throughout the rest of {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shichvat Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And yet, Abaye himself acknowledges that {\i shichvat zera} still performs a necessary function \u8212- the {\i pasuk} is not superfluous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neshika Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore offers yet another proposal. The case {\i shichvat zera} excludes is one where the {\i kinui} involved {\i neshika}. This term requires careful definition. {\i Neshika} here is not simply kissing; it is a technical euphemism for genital contact that stops short of full penetration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ha\u8217'arah Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative concept is {\i ha\u8217'arah}, derived from the phrase {\i mekorah he\u8217'erah}, \u8220"he uncovered her nakedness.\u8221" Two definitions of {\i ha\u8217'arah} appear in the literature: one holds it means {\i hachnasat atarah}, the actual entry of the crown of the penis into the vagina \u8212- and this is the dominant understanding by which {\i ha\u8217'arah} constitutes the beginning of a complete sexual act. The other holds that {\i ha\u8217'arah} means {\i neshika}, the \u8220"kissing\u8221" of the genitals, meaning the placement of the penis against the vaginal opening without actual entry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes a paradox. If {\i ha\u8217'arah} means {\i hachnasat atarah} \u8212- actual entry of the crown \u8212- then it is clearly an act of sex, and excluding it via {\i shichvat zera} makes sense: we understand why the {\i pasuk} needed to specify. But if {\i ha\u8217'arah} means {\i neshika} \u8212- mere contact without entry \u8212- then it is already excluded from the definition of sex without any {\i pasuk} being required. Why would the {\i pasuk} need to exclude what was never included?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that the expectation seems reversed: logically, one would think the {\i pasuk}\u8217's exclusion is necessary precisely when the thing excluded would otherwise have been included. If {\i neshika} is not sex, no exclusion is needed. If it {\i is} sex, the exclusion is meaningful. Yet the {\i Gemara} seems to be struggling in precisely the opposite direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jealousy vs Adultery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This paradox brings the discussion back to its deepest conceptual tension. The question underlying all of this is: is {\i sotah} fundamentally about the husband\u8217's jealousy, or is it about an objective halachic category of adultery? If {\i sotah} tracks the husband\u8217's subjective state \u8212- his anxiety, his sense of betrayal, his concern about {\i zera} \u8212- then arguably the {\i parsha} could expand or contract based on his particular {\i kinui}. He might be jealous of acts that are not technically adultery; he might not care about acts that technically are. From that perspective, the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah} would be shaped by the contours of his concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} throughout this entire discussion refuses that framework. It insists that {\i sotah} must be mapped precisely onto the objective halachic definition of adultery \u8212- no broader and no narrower \u8212- and that {\i shichvat zera} functions to enforce exactly that alignment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution reflects this principle directly. {\i L\u8217'olam v\u8217'shikina lah derech evarim} \u8212- we return to the case where the {\i kinui} was directed at sexual activity through limbs. And the reason {\i shichvat zera} is needed to exclude this: {\i u-mahu d\u8217'teima b\u8217'kpeida d\u8217'ba\u8217'al talya rachmana} \u8212- one might have thought that {\i sotah} is determined by the husband\u8217's anger and jealousy, and if he is angry about this form of sexual activity, the {\i Torah} would include it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jealousy Limited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- {\i shichvat zera} teaches that even where the husband is genuinely jealous, even where his {\i kinui} was directed at precisely this activity, if it does not constitute halachic adultery, the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah} does not apply. His jealousy cannot broaden the category beyond what the {\i Torah} objectively defines.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objective Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is clear: {\i sotah} applies only to what falls within the narrow, formal definition of adultery throughout the rest of the {\i Torah}. Not broader because of his subjectivity; not narrower because of biological considerations like the incapacity of {\i zera}. The same categories of act that constitute adultery everywhere in {\i halacha} constitute the {\i sotah} case \u8212- and only those categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Insemination Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion resonates directly with a major modern dispute. The debate between Rav Moshe Feinstein and the {\i Tzitz Eliezer} over the permissibility of artificial insemination using another man\u8217's semen turns precisely on the question of what role {\i zera} plays in the definition of adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Moshe Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Moshe\u8217's position was that without an act of sex, there is no adultery and no {\i mamzerus}. The legal category requires the act, not merely the biological material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzitz Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tzitz Eliezer} disagreed, arguing that the Ramban\u8217's analysis of {\i shichvat zera} shows that another man\u8217's {\i zera} is itself central to the concern of adultery. Even absent a physical act of sex, the introduction of another man\u8217's {\i zera} into the picture raises concerns analogous to adultery \u8212- and potentially touches on {\i mamzerus} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Moshe Dismisses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Moshe reportedly dismissed this argument sharply, characterizing the {\i Tzitz Eliezer}\u8217's framework as foreign to Torah values \u8212- a {\i traif} non-Jewish idea smuggled into {\i halacha}. He insisted that {\i pesak halacha} must be grounded in the categories of the {\i Torah} itself, not in philosophical ideas imported from outside. His implicit reading of {\i shichvat zera} was entirely consistent with what the {\i Gemara} on this {\i daf} has demonstrated: {\i halacha} systematically empties {\i shichvat zera} of its biological content and treats it as a formal legal marker \u8212- not a substantive concern about {\i zera} per se.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Brackets Zera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} on this {\i daf} has thus completely bracketed the meaning of {\i shichvat zera}. Despite its prominence in the {\i pesukim} and despite the Ramban\u8217's compelling reading of the ritual\u8217's underlying psychology, {\i halacha} refuses to assign {\i shichvat zera} any independent weight. What {\i shichvat zera} ultimately teaches is not that {\i zera} matters, but that {\i sotah} applies only where adultery would apply \u8212- and only where adultery would apply. Rav Moshe\u8217's position, far from being an innovation, reflects exactly the lesson this {\i daf} teaches.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} transitions now from the formal categories of {\i sotah} to a related but distinct concern \u8212- the practical questions that arise around women who are not technically {\i sotah} but about whom there are questions and suspicions. This is connected to earlier discussions about women who are {\i overet al das} and whether the husband may continue to live with such a woman, or is compelled to divorce her. The present discussion concerns women who are free \u8212- widowed or divorced \u8212- but whose reputation carries a cloud.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Shmuel: yisa adam dumah v\u8217'al yisa bat dumah} \u8212- a man should sooner marry a {\i dumah}, a woman about whom people are gossiping, than her daughter. The scenario is a married woman about whom there is communal gossip \u8212- {\i dumah} \u8212- suggesting acts of adultery. There is no concrete evidence, no formal prohibition, merely talk. Now she is free \u8212- divorced or widowed \u8212- and the question is: if a man must choose between marrying this woman and marrying her daughter, which is preferable?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dumah Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains {\i dumah} as {\i nishanes v\u8217'nidaberes b\u8217'fi kol al niyufeiha} \u8212- she has become spoken of and gossiped about throughout the community regarding acts of adultery. He derives the term from {\i dumah ircha alayich savach}, a phrase in which the same root connotes communal talk or chatter. Related words can denote either praise or criticism, excessive talk in general; here the context is clearly derogatory. Notably, the woman is not {\i assur} to remarry on any formal halachic ground \u8212- the gossip alone does not constitute evidence. Even if she did commit adultery, she is only forbidden to her husband and to the specific man she committed adultery with; she is not forbidden to this third party who now wishes to marry her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seed Quality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's reasoning is as follows: {\i she-zu ba mi-tipa ksheira} \u8212- this woman, the {\i dumah} herself, came from valid seed; her own status is halachically unimpeachable. {\i V\u8217'zu ba mi-tipa psula} \u8212- her daughter, by contrast, may have come from invalid seed, having potentially been conceived through an adulterous union. Shmuel phrases the concern in terms of {\i tipa psula} rather than simply {\i safeik mamzeret}, which is interesting. The language of {\i tipa psula} suggests something deeper than a formal halachic classification \u8212- it evokes the idea that a soul formed through such a union is itself marked, not merely categorized differently in {\i sefer yuchasin}. This parallels older discussions about whether ancestry concerns are fundamentally about observed character, halachic status, or something more metaphysical about the nature of a person formed through a {\i bi\u8217'ah psula}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's formulation adds a further dimension: {\i shema me-oved kochavim u-mamzer} \u8212- perhaps the man who impregnated her was a non-Jew or a {\i mamzer}, which would complicate the daughter\u8217's status further. The concern is not only that the daughter is a {\i safeik mamzeret} in the technical sense, but that her father is a figure of unknown and possibly problematic lineage entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disagrees and rules in the opposite direction: {\i yisa adam bat duma v\u8217'lo yisa duma} \u8212- better to marry the daughter. His reasoning: {\i she-zu omedet b\u8217'chezkat kashrut} \u8212- the daughter has a {\i chazakah} of {\i kashrut}, no suspicion has ever been raised about her personally. The mother, by contrast, {\i eina omedet b\u8217'chezkat kashrut} \u8212- she is under suspicion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: didn\u8217't the mother also begin with a {\i chazakat kashrut}? She too was once a young woman about whom no suspicion existed. Why does Rabbi Yochanan treat the daughter as having a {\i chazakah} that the mother lacks?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This apparent contradiction is noted by the {\i poskim}. The answer seems to lie in distinguishing between two different concerns. The {\i chazakah} of the daughter addresses the question of halachic {\i mamzerus}: she was born without any formal {\i pesul}, there is no reason to suppose she is a {\i mamzeret}, and one can apply the standard presumption of {\i kashrut}. The concern about the mother, by contrast, is not primarily halachic \u8212- she is not formally {\i assur} to remarry. The concern is one of character: communal observation has generated reasonable suspicion about her fidelity. These are different categories of concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Character vs Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position prioritizes the halachic dimension \u8212- {\i mamzerus} \u8212- and finds the daughter superior on that ground. Shmuel prioritizes the character dimension and finds the daughter inferior: no one has raised questions about the daughter\u8217's character, but the mother\u8217's character is in question. Both are legitimate concerns; the debate is over which concern is weightier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: {\i nosei adam duma} \u8212- a man may marry a {\i dumah}. This seems to support Shmuel\u8217's position, since Shmuel holds that the {\i dumah} herself is the preferable choice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava immediately qualifies: {\i v\u8217'tisbra? nosei l\u8217'chatchila?} \u8212- does that make sense? Is marrying a {\i dumah} really the recommended course of action, the top priority? Clearly not. What the {\i baraita} means is {\i im nasa} \u8212- if one has already married, it is acceptable, it is not grounds for annulment. The {\i baraita} is stating a {\i b\u8217'diavad} ruling, not a {\i l\u8217'chatchila} preference. And the same would apply to marrying the {\i bat duma} \u8212- {\i b\u8217'diavad}, it is acceptable as well. The {\i baraita} therefore does not resolve which is preferable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i hilchata} \u8212- the final ruling \u8212- sides with Rabbi Yochanan: {\i yisa adam bat duma v\u8217'al yisa duma} \u8212- better to marry the daughter. The dominant concern in the final halachic determination is the character concern: the daughter has no mark against her character, while the mother does. This represents a prioritization of observed behavior over speculative halachic concern about lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov Beilos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An additional principle is invoked by Rav Tachlifa bar Maarava, taught before Rabbi Abbahu: {\i isha m\u8217'zanah baneiha k\u8217'sheirim, rov beilos achar ha-ba\u8217'al} \u8212- a woman who commits adultery, her children are nonetheless presumed {\i k\u8217'sheirim}, because the majority of her sexual relations are with her husband. Even in cases where adultery is confirmed, the statistical presumption is that any given child was conceived through legitimate relations. This principle of {\i rov beilos achar ha-ba\u8217'al} is a powerful tool: even when infidelity is known, it does not automatically render the children suspect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halachic Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking ruling. Even a confirmed adulteress \u8212- a woman about whom it is established, not merely rumored, that she has been with another man \u8212- her children carry the presumption of being her husband\u8217's children, because statistically, even a woman engaged in an affair continues to have relations with her husband more frequently than with a lover. The halachic status of the children is thus insulated from the confirmed fact of the mother\u8217's conduct.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prutza Beyoter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama raises a sharper question: {\i haysa prutza b\u8217'yoter mahu} \u8212- what if the woman is extraordinarily licentious? Not merely a woman about whom there is gossip, nor even a confirmed occasional adulteress, but a woman of extreme and persistent licentiousness \u8212- someone seen constantly with strange men, leaving no doubt that she is engaged in habitual infidelity. Does the principle of {\i rov beilos achar ha-ba\u8217'al} still apply?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fertile Window Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} first considers a view that a woman only becomes pregnant at a specific point near her expected period. Under this view \u8212- which is biologically incorrect but was the operative assumption in this context \u8212- pregnancy can only occur during a narrow window.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Uncertainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this view is correct, then even in the case of a very licentious woman, the question depends on whether the husband knows precisely when his wife\u8217's fertile window occurs. Women do not always have perfectly regular cycles, and a husband may not know the exact date of her expected period. If he does not know when she is fertile, he cannot specifically guard her during that time \u8212- he cannot watch her closely enough during the window that matters \u8212- and therefore even his awareness of her licentiousness does not enable him to ensure that any child is his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikveh Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second view holds that a woman becomes pregnant specifically near the time of her immersion in the {\i mikveh}. This is, in fact, biologically accurate: a woman immerses approximately twelve days after the onset of her period, which places immersion very close to ovulation under Rabbinic {\i halacha}, and somewhat less precisely so under purely {\i Torah}-level reckoning. According to this view, the husband knows exactly when his wife\u8217's fertile period is, because it is tied directly to the {\i mikveh} calendar, which is structured and predictable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the husband knows precisely when the fertile window is, he can in principle watch his wife closely during that time. But, the {\i Gemara} asks, what if she is so extraordinarily licentious that even his heightened vigilance during the critical period is futile? She simply walks out on him. He knows, he is watching, but he cannot control her. Does {\i rov beilos achar ha-ba\u8217'al} still operate under those conditions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiku\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} does not resolve this question. {\i Teiku} \u8212- the question stands unanswered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evidence Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This unresolved question has practical significance. For a woman to be forbidden to her husband \u8212- whether as a {\i sotah}, as an {\i overet al das}, or on grounds of confirmed adultery \u8212- some evidence is required. The {\i Gemara} earlier discussed {\i edei kiyur}, witnesses of unseemly behavior: witnesses who saw the couple enter a hotel room, who observed conduct that strongly implies an adulterous relationship, even without witnessing the act itself sufficient to impose capital punishment. At what point does the accumulation of such evidence reach a threshold that makes it no longer a matter of the husband\u8217's discretion whether to stay married?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question has direct practical application in contemporary cases. A situation in which a husband suspects his wife of adultery, hires a private investigator, and obtains photographs and other evidence of the wife entering a hotel with another man presents precisely this problem. No witness observed the act directly in a manner sufficient for a formal {\i chiyuv misa}, yet the evidence of {\i kiyur} is overwhelming. If the couple wishes to reconcile, can they? And at what stage does {\i halacha} intervene to say they may not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects as well to the principle discussed in {\i Nedarim} at the end of the tractate: a woman who claims to have committed adultery is not believed \u8212- which has the practical consequence of protecting reconciliation efforts when the wife\u8217's own statement is the only evidence. The asymmetry between the treatment of a woman\u8217's self-incriminating claim and the treatment of external {\i edei kiyur} is a tension that emerges naturally from these discussions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditions & Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Eligibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now transitions to the next {\i perek}, which deals with the conditions under which the {\i sotah} process may be carried out and who qualifies as subject to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} addresses a series of physical conditions that exclude a woman from the {\i sotah} process: if she is blind, a {\i cheresh} (deaf-mute), lame, armless, or mute. Each exclusion is derived from specific {\i pesukim} in the {\i parsha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshes Blind\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshes derives the exclusion of a blind woman from the phrase {\i v\u8217'nistarah}, \u8220"it was hidden,\u8221" and the related phrase {\i u-minalam me\u8217'einei ishah}, \u8220"hidden from the eyes of her husband.\u8221" The verse implies that the husband can normally see \u8212- {\i v\u8217'nistarah} presupposes a seeing husband \u8212- and by analogy, a seeing woman is required. A blind woman is thus excluded because the verse presupposes normal vision on both sides of the relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beisi Lame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beisi derives additional exclusions from the procedural requirements of the ritual itself. The verse {\i v\u8217'he\u8217'emid ha-kohein et ha-ishah lifnei Hashem} \u8212- \u8220"the {\i kohen} shall make her stand before God\u8221" \u8212- requires that she be able to stand, which excludes a woman who is lame. The verse {\i v\u8217'natan al kapeha} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall place upon her hands\u8221" the {\i minchah} \u8212- requires that she have hands, which excludes a woman who is armless. By the principle of {\i lehakish ish le-ishah}, these requirements apply symmetrically to the husband: a lame or armless husband similarly cannot initiate the {\i sotah} process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar bar Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi adds the exclusion of a mute woman, derived from {\i v\u8217'amrah ha-ishah amein amein} \u8212- \u8220"and the woman shall say {\i amein amein}.\u8221" The ritual requires her verbal response. A mute woman cannot give that response and is therefore excluded. By the same principle of symmetry, a mute husband cannot bring his wife through the {\i sotah} process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the {\i Gemara} makes explicit through the verse {\i asher tisteh ishah tachat ishah} \u8212- {\i lehakish ish le-ishah v\u8217'ishah le-ish} \u8212- the man and the woman must be in equivalent categories. Every physical condition that disqualifies the wife from the {\i sotah} process disqualifies the husband from initiating it. The symmetry is structurally built into the {\i parsha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek End\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the {\i Gemara} concludes the {\i perek} of {\i HaMekane} \u8212- a remarkable chapter that has exhaustively examined which types of men, which types of women, and which types of marriages fall within the {\i parsha} of {\i sotah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Water Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The new {\i perek} opens with a striking {\i mishnah}: {\i k\u8217'sheim she-hamayim bodkin osah, kach hamayim bodkin oso} \u8212- the same way the water tests her, it tests him. The \u8220"him\u8221" here requires immediate clarification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uvau Uvau\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} derives this principle from the phrase {\i u-va\u8217'u u-va\u8217'u} \u8212- \u8220"the waters will come in and come in\u8221" \u8212- which appears twice in the {\i pesukim}, in different verses, indicating that the waters act upon both parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Is Him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have assumed that \u8220"him\u8221" refers to the husband \u8212- perhaps because he too must be {\i menukkeh me-avon}, free of sin, for the water to work, or perhaps because sleeping with a {\i sotah} after her status is established might incur its own punishment. But the {\i Gemara} clarifies: \u8220"him\u8221" is the {\i boel} \u8212- the suspected adulterer. When the waters act upon the woman and she suffers their consequences, the man who committed adultery with her, wherever he may be, suffers the same fate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i k\u8217'sheim she-asurah la-ba\u8217'al kach asurah la-boel} \u8212- the same way a {\i sotah} is forbidden to her husband, she is forbidden to the {\i boel}, the man with whom she committed adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has immediate practical implications. A woman who commits adultery and is subsequently divorced \u8212- or whose husband dies \u8212- cannot then marry the man she committed adultery with. Even if years have passed, even if the formal {\i sotah} process was never completed, the adultery itself forbids her to that specific man. The waters\u8217' failure to \u8220"test\u8221" her does not undo the underlying prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case becomes even sharper in a contemporary context. A woman whose husband has not given her a {\i get} \u8212- perhaps they have a civil divorce but she remains halachically married \u8212- who then lives with another man is, during that entire period, committing adultery in halachic terms. When she finally receives the {\i get}, she is formally free from her husband \u8212- but she may not marry the man she has been living with. That man falls within the category of {\i boel}, and the rule of {\i asurah la-boel} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Venitma\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The textual basis for this dual prohibition is the word {\i v\u8217'nitme\u8217'ah}, \u8220"and she is defiled,\u8221" which appears twice in the {\i parsha}. Each instance of {\i nitme\u8217'ah} is read as teaching a separate forbiddance \u8212- one to the husband, one to the {\i boel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva derives both prohibitions from the extra {\i vav} in {\i v\u8217'nitme\u8217'ah} \u8212- the prefix {\i vav} adds an additional instance of defilement, extending the prohibition to the second man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua, citing the teaching of Zecharya ben HaKatzav, holds that it is not the {\i vav} that is being {\i doreshed}, but rather the fact that the word {\i nitme\u8217'ah} appears twice in the {\i parsha} entirely. {\i Shnei pe\u8217'amim amur ba-parsha nitme\u8217'ah v\u8217'nitme\u8217'ah \u8212- echad l\u8217'ba\u8217'al v\u8217'echad l\u8217'boel}: one instance teaches defilement to the husband, and one teaches defilement to the {\i boel}. The two {\i amoraim} reach the same conclusion through different hermeneutical paths.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva Drashos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Medrash Opens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i drashot} that follow are introduced with the phrase {\i bo bayom} \u8212- on that day. Rashi explains, citing the {\i Gemara} in {\i Maseches Brachos}, that whenever the phrase {\i bo bayom} appears, it refers to the day that Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah was appointed {\i nasi} in place of Rabban Gamliel. On that day, the gates of the {\i Beit Medrash} were opened broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bo Bayom Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel had maintained a strict policy: {\i kol talmid chacham she-ein tocho k\u8217'baro al yikanes l\u8217'veit ha-midrash} \u8212- any Torah scholar whose inner character does not match his outer presentation may not enter the {\i Beit Medrash}. This was an elitist standard of admission. When Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah was elevated to the {\i nesiut} and relaxed this restriction, many additional students entered \u8212- {\i v\u8217'rabta Torah bo bayom}, and Torah was greatly expanded on that day, because the diversity of voices allowed many previously unresolved matters to be clarified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi further notes that the {\i drasha} immediately preceding \u8212- the teaching about {\i v\u8217'nitme\u8217'ah v\u8217'nitme\u8217'ah} \u8212- may itself have occurred on that same day, so all the teachings clustered under {\i bo bayom} belong together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yitma Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's first {\i drasha} on that day addresses a verse in {\i Vayikra} 11 about the {\i kli cheres}, a pottery vessel, and the {\i sheretz}, a dead rodent. The verse states: {\i u-chli cheres asher yipol mehem el tocho, kol asher b\u8217'tocho yitma} \u8212- any pottery vessel into which a {\i sheretz} falls, everything inside it becomes {\i tamei}. Rabbi Akiva focuses on the word {\i yitma}: the verse does not say {\i tamei} \u8212- it will be {\i tamei} \u8212- but {\i yitma}, \u8220"shall become {\i tamei},\u8221" which he reads as causative \u8212- it shall {\i make others tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessel Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure of the {\i tumah} transmission is as follows. The {\i sheretz} is an {\i av ha-tumah}. The {\i kli cheres}, even without direct contact \u8212- merely by virtue of being an open vessel in whose airspace the {\i sheretz} exists \u8212- becomes a {\i rishon l\u8217'tumah}. Any food inside the vessel contracts {\i tumah} from the {\i kli}, making the food a {\i sheni}. Now Rabbi Akiva derives from {\i yitma} that this {\i sheni} food can then render other food a {\i shlishi} \u8212- a third-degree {\i tumah}. This is the teaching: {\i limed al kikar sheni she-metamei et ha-shlishi} \u8212- a {\i kikar} (loaf) that is {\i sheni} can make something else {\i shlishi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's reaction is famous and deeply moving. He invokes the name of Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai: {\i mi gala afar me\u8217'einecha, Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai} \u8212- who will remove the dust from your eyes, Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai! The idiom suggests: if only you were alive to witness this. Rashi understands this as a near-literal image: the dust of the grave, removed from the eyes of the deceased, so that he might see what is happening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The context is this: Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai had taught that the {\i kashrut} of {\i kikar shlishi} \u8212- third-degree {\i tumah} in food \u8212- had no explicit {\i pasuk}, and he was troubled by this. He feared that a future generation might reject the ruling as lacking a {\i mikra min ha-Torah}. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i drasha} now provides precisely that explicit verse. Rabbi Yehoshua, on behalf of his departed master, celebrates: Rabbi Akiva \u8212- your student \u8212- has found the {\i pasuk} you sought. {\i Matza lo mikra min ha-Torah she-hu tamei}, as it says {\i kol asher b\u8217'tocho yitma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levite Cities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i drasha} of that day addresses a contradiction between two {\i pesukim} concerning the {\i arei Levi\u8217'im}, the cities of the {\i Levi\u8217'im}. One verse states {\i u-madotem mi-chutz la-ir pe\u8217'at kedma alpayim ba-amah} \u8212- you shall measure two thousand {\i amot} outside the city in each direction. Another verse states {\i mi-kir ha-ir v\u8217'chutzah elef amah saviv} \u8212- from the city wall and outward, one thousand {\i amot} around.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Conflict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two {\i pesukim} appear to contradict each other directly: one says two thousand {\i amot}, the other says one thousand. {\i Ee efshar lomar elef amah she-kvar ne\u8217'emar alpayim ba-amah, v\u8217'ee efshar lomar alpayim ba-amah she-kvar ne\u8217'emar elef amah} \u8212- it is impossible to say one thousand if it already says two thousand, and it is impossible to say two thousand if it already says one thousand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's resolution: {\i elef amah migrash} \u8212- the one thousand {\i amot} constitute the {\i migrash}, the open land, the equivalent of landscaped fields immediately surrounding the city. {\i V\u8217'alpayim ba-amah} \u8212- and the two thousand {\i amot} define the {\i techum Shabbat}, the outer boundary of the city for purposes of {\i Shabbat} travel. The two thousand {\i amot} include the one thousand of {\i migrash}; the {\i Levi\u8217'im} actually own real estate only out to one thousand {\i amot}, and the next thousand constitutes the formal city limit. This is the source for the {\i halacha} of {\i techum Shabbat} being two thousand {\i amot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar the son of Rabbi Yose HaGlili offers a different reading. He agrees that {\i elef amah migrash} \u8212- the first thousand is the open field surrounding the city \u8212- but holds that {\i alpayim ba-amah sadot u-chramim} \u8212- the second thousand is actual agricultural land, fields and vineyards owned by the {\i Levi\u8217'im}. According to this view, the {\i Levi\u8217'im} receive two thousand {\i amot} of real estate: one thousand of open {\i migrash} and one thousand of productive agricultural land. The two-thousand-{\i amah} figure is not a city limit for {\i Shabbat} but represents actual territorial holdings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Red Sea Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i drasha} of that day concerns the singing at the Red Sea. The verse states: {\i az yashir Moshe u-vnei Yisrael et ha-shirah ha-zot la-Hashem va-yomru leimor} \u8212- then Moshe and the children of Israel sang this song to God and they said, to say.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vayomru Leimor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i leimor} is seemingly redundant. The verse already says {\i va-yomru}, \u8220"they said\u8221"; why add {\i leimor}, \u8220"to say\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hallel Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva derives from this redundancy that the singing was {\i responsive} in structure: Moshe would sing a phrase and Israel would respond. He compares it to the way {\i Hallel} is read: the {\i chazan} recites and the congregation responds after each verse. {\i U-l\u8217'chach ne\u8217'emar leimor} \u8212- that is why the word {\i leimor} was needed, to indicate that Israel was saying back what Moshe said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nechemia View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nechemia disagrees. He holds that the singing was structured not like {\i Hallel} but like the reading of {\i Shema}: the leader begins and everyone reads together in unison, rather than responsively. The model is communal recitation, not responsive antiphony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Job Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Also on that day, Rabbi Yehoshua ben Horkenos taught: {\i lo avad Iyov et ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu ela me-ahavah} \u8212- Iyov served God not out of fear but out of love. This might seem counterintuitive: the text of {\i Sefer Iyov} explicitly states {\i haya yereh Elokim}, \u8220"he feared God.\u8221" Rabbi Yehoshua ben Horkenos argues that despite the language of fear, the deeper reality of Iyov\u8217's service was love.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Job Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The textual basis is subtle. The verse reads: {\i hen yikteleini lo ayachel} \u8212- \u8220"if He kills me, I will still hope.\u8221" But there is a {\i keri-ktiv} discrepancy. The {\i ktiv} \u8212- the written text \u8212- is {\i lo} with an {\i alef}, meaning \u8220"not\u8221": \u8220"if He kills me, I will no longer hope.\u8221" The {\i keri} \u8212- the oral reading \u8212- is {\i lo} with a {\i vav}, meaning \u8220"to Him\u8221": \u8220"if He kills me, I will hope toward Him.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} notes the ambiguity: {\i v\u8217'adayin ha-davar shakul} \u8212- the matter remains balanced, indeterminate, between the two readings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Love Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution comes from another verse: {\i ad egva lo asir tumati mimeni} \u8212- \u8220"until I die, I will not remove my righteousness from me.\u8221" This verse, read alongside the ambiguous {\i lo/lo} verse, demonstrates that Iyov\u8217's commitment was absolute \u8212- even unto death, he would not swerve. This is the language not of mere fear, which might bend under sufficient pressure, but of love, which remains steadfast regardless of consequence. {\i Melamed she-me-ahavah asa} \u8212- this teaches that Iyov acted out of love.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Student Vindication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua then mirrors the earlier reaction of his namesake to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i drasha}, offering the same exclamation to Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai: {\i mi yigaleh afar me-einecha, Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai} \u8212- who will remove the dust from your eyes! For Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai had spent his lifetime teaching that Iyov served God only out of fear, based on the verse {\i ish tam v\u8217'yashar, yereh Elokim v\u8217'sar me-ra}. And now Rabbi Yehoshua ben Horkenos \u8212- a student of your student, your halachic grandchild \u8212- has demonstrated that it was love all along. {\i V\u8217'halo Yehoshua talmid talmidecha limed she-me-ahavah asa} \u8212- look at what your student\u8217's student has taught. Even in disagreement with the master, the student vindicates the deepest aspiration: that Torah be clarified, and that its most profound truths come to light.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 28\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Identity of Him\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who is \u8220"Him\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} with which we conclude the fifth {\i perek} opens with two parallel principles: just as the waters check her, they check him, and just as she is forbidden to the husband, she is forbidden to the adulterer. Before proceeding further, the {\i Gemara} pauses to ask a basic question: when the {\i mishnah} says the waters check {\i otoh} \u8212- him \u8212- who exactly is \u8220"him\u8221"? There are two candidates: the husband ({\i ba\u8217'al}) or the adulterer ({\i bo\u8217'el}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waters Check Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises the possibility that the waters check the husband. But what would the husband have done wrong? The {\i Gemara} considers that perhaps the husband himself sinned \u8212- for example, by sleeping with his wife after she had already acquired the status of a {\i sotah}, thereby rendering that act of intercourse forbidden. The {\i Gemara} immediately rejects this: if the husband is not free of sexual sin, the waters do not merely fail to check him \u8212- they fail to check {\i her} as well. The {\i baraita} is explicit: {\i venikah ish me\u8217'avon vehishah hahi tisa et avonah} \u8212- the man will be clean of sin and the woman will bear her sin. {\i Bizman she\u8217'ish menukah me\u8217'avon}, when he is clean of that specific sin, the waters are effective. If he is not, the waters are effective for neither of them. So the waters cannot be \u8220"checking him\u8221" in any sense that\u8217's useful \u8212- if he has sinned, nothing gets checked at all. The {\i otoh} must therefore refer to the {\i bo\u8217'el}, the man she is suspected of having had relations with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a stylistic difficulty. If the {\i mishnah} intends the {\i bo\u8217'el}, why not name him as such? Later in the very same {\i mishnah}, when it says she is forbidden to the husband just as she is forbidden to the adulterer \u8212- {\i keshem she\u8217'asurah laba\u8217'al kakh asurah labo\u8217'el} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} uses the explicit term {\i bo\u8217'el}. So why say {\i otoh} in the first clause instead of {\i bo\u8217'el}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this elegantly: {\i reisha aidei detana otah tana otoh} \u8212- because the opening clause describes what happens to {\i her}, using the word {\i otah}, the natural parallel is {\i otoh}. The linguistic symmetry of {\i otah/otoh} dictates the phrasing. In the second clause, where the {\i mishnah} introduces the word {\i ba\u8217'al} for the husband, the natural parallel becomes {\i bo\u8217'el}. The parallel pairs are {\i otah/otoh} and {\i ba\u8217'al/bo\u8217'el} \u8212- and in both cases, the referent is the same man: the one she is suspected of having had relations with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi & Gematria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} on 27b quotes a {\i Yerushalmi} that develops this theme richly, and it is worth pausing over because the {\i Bavli} itself contains no parallel discussion. The {\i Yerushalmi} in the name of Rav Tanchuma observes that the {\i gematria} of {\i hamar\u8217'arim} \u8212- the cursed waters \u8212- equals 248 times two, corresponding to the 248 limbs in his body and the 248 limbs in her body: {\i minyan hamar\u8217'arim neged remach evarim sheyesh bo veneged remach evarim sheyesh bah}. The word {\i mar\u8217'arim} contains a {\i resh-mem-resh-mem} for 240, and the remaining letters bring the total to 480 \u8212- that is, 248 doubled. The waters thus symbolically destroy two complete bodies, his and hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} then adds a further and striking observation: the waters check her for every act of forbidden intercourse she receives from her husband after she has slept with the {\i bo\u8217'el} \u8212- {\i keshem shemayim bodkin otah al kol biah shehi mekabeles miba\u8217'alah le\u8217'achar habo\u8217'el kakh heim bodkin otoh}. This is surprising on multiple levels. First, it implies that the waters\u8217' effect is not a single event but cumulative, potentially multiplying across repeated violations. Second, it implies that the {\i bo\u8217'el} is held responsible not only for his own act but for the cascade of forbidden acts that follow from it \u8212- her subsequent, now-prohibited intercourse with her husband. He is punished, as it were, for her sins that occur after his sin, because he is the root cause of all that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also challenges a common assumption \u8212- that the {\i sotah} waters simply kill the guilty woman. The {\i Yerushalmi} pictures something more complex: an ongoing, iterative consequence that unfolds over time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} then addresses the legal logic of why her status works through her: {\i lo eshet amisecha lo sitein shichavsecha lezara litam\u8217'ah bah} \u8212- you make yourself {\i tamei} through {\i her}. Her status is the operative one. {\i Im haysa mezidah asurah, shogeges muteres} \u8212- if she was fully intentional, she is forbidden; if she was unwitting, she may be permitted to her husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Status Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} adds another striking consequence: {\i ukhshem she\u8217'asurah le\u8217'achiv shel ba\u8217'al kakh asurah le\u8217'achiv shel bo\u8217'el} \u8212- just as she is forbidden to the {\i ba\u8217'al\u8217's} brother (in the case of {\i yibum}), so too she is forbidden to the {\i bo\u8217'el\u8217's} brother. The case of the husband\u8217's brother is understandable: the marriage has been compromised, and therefore the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}, which depends on the legitimacy of that marriage, can no longer apply. But that she is forbidden to the {\i bo\u8217'el\u8217's} brother is far more difficult to understand. What is the mechanism? What logic connects her status to the family of the man she is suspected of having sinned with?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might suggest \u8212- and this is an appealing idea \u8212- that the concern is a practical one: if she were permitted to marry the {\i bo\u8217'el\u8217's} brother and then that brother died, the result would be a {\i yibum} obligation with the {\i bo\u8217'el} himself, to whom she is forbidden. We thus preemptively prohibit the marriage to the brother in order to foreclose that possibility. That would at least offer a functional rationale, even if the prohibition seems unusual at first glance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Works Through Her\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle that emerges from the {\i Yerushalmi} is that her status is entirely operative through her, not through the man. A man who commits adultery with a married woman does not change his own personal status. So if the suspected man actually committed adultery not with her but with some other woman, the waters will not affect him when she drinks, because it all passes through her. The principle is: {\i bah hadavar talui} \u8212- it is all dependent on her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asymmetrical Outcomes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Him Affected Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now considers an unusual scenario: what if she drinks the waters and he is affected but she is not? The immediate explanation offered is {\i zechus toleh} \u8212- merit. She must have done it, since otherwise the {\i bo\u8217'el} would not have been affected. But her merit delayed or concealed the impact on her. This explains the phenomenon, but only according to the view that merit prevents the waters from showing any visible effect. If she is definitely guilty but her merit shields her temporarily, yet the {\i bo\u8217'el} had no such merit, then he is affected and she appears unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intentional/Unwitting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this explanation fails according to the view that even when merit protects someone, the effect is nonetheless visible \u8212- the person begins to deteriorate, even if slowly. Under that view, if she showed no sign whatsoever, the {\i zechus} explanation is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then considers and rejects the possibility that he drank contaminated water ({\i mayim megulim}) independently \u8212- the effects of snake venom do not replicate the specific physiological signs of the {\i sotah} waters, namely the belly swelling and the thigh collapsing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} ultimately resolves the case: {\i she\u8217'hayah hu mazid vehee shoggeget} \u8212- he was intentional, she was unwitting. He knew he was sleeping with a married woman; she, through some unusual circumstance, did not \u8212- perhaps she mistook him for her husband. According to {\i Rashi}, the paradigmatic case of {\i shogeg} for the {\i sotah} is exactly this: both parties thought they were sleeping with their own spouses. Because she was {\i shogegget}, the waters do not affect her. Because he was {\i mazid}, he is affected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One must ask: what exactly constitutes {\i shogeg} in the context of adultery? The {\i Gemara} and {\i Rashi} define it narrowly \u8212- she must have genuinely believed the man was her husband. This is far more restrictive than simply believing the act was permitted. A famous responsum addresses a case where a woman was told by a man claiming to be Elijah the Prophet that sleeping with him would bring the {\i Mashiach}, and she believed him. The {\i posek} ruled she was not considered {\i shogegget}, because she knew she was transgressing against her husband \u8212- the only valid {\i shogeg} is genuinely mistaking the man for one\u8217's spouse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Her Affected Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then considers the reverse: she is affected by the waters but he is not. The immediate explanation is again {\i zechus} \u8212- his merit prevented the waters from affecting him. This works according to the view that merit shields someone entirely, but fails according to the view that merit still allows visible deterioration. Contaminated water is again raised as an alternative explanation and again rejected for the same reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The simplest explanation is that she committed adultery with a different man altogether \u8212- {\i ela ani omer im acher nistarah} \u8212- and the man we suspected is innocent. That accounts for why he was unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he was truly unaffected because he was not the adulterer, might she now be permitted to him if she is divorced? The {\i Gemara} says no: {\i tifater she\u8217'hayah hu shogeg vehee mezidah} \u8212- we must entertain the possibility that he was the adulterer but was {\i shogeg} while she was {\i mezidah}. Since he was {\i shogeg}, the waters did not affect him. But since she was {\i mezidah}, she remains forbidden to him. We cannot read his non-reaction as proof of innocence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Cases Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now systematically works through all four combinations of intentionality. If he was {\i mazid} and she was {\i shogegget}: the waters affect him, not her; she is permitted to continue living with her husband. If he was {\i shogeg} and she was {\i mezidah}: the waters affect her, not him; she is forbidden to her husband, and she cannot go and be with the {\i bo\u8217'el} \u8212- {\i efshar lomar yatza mitachas yado vehayeta le\u8217'ish acher}, could it be that she divorces from her husband on account of her adultery and then goes to live with the very man she committed adultery with? The answer is no. If he was {\i mazid} and she was {\i mezidah}: both are fully affected. If he was {\i shogeg} and she was {\i shogegget}: perhaps neither is affected, and theoretically those two might end up together \u8212- although this is a deeply unusual case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative principle in all of this: she cannot become permitted to the {\i bo\u8217'el} as long as one of them was {\i mazid}. The only scenario in which they could perhaps eventually be together is if neither had any intentional culpability.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Source from Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vav or Repetition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i Bavli} proper, the {\i Gemara} at the top of 28a takes up the question of the {\i drashot} underlying the {\i mishnah\u8217's} opening clause. The waters are described in the {\i Torah} text using the word {\i ba\u8217'u} \u8212- they shall enter \u8212- and this word appears three times in the relevant {\i parsha}. Moreover, in two of those appearances, the word is prefaced by a {\i vav}, giving {\i uva\u8217'u}. The question: is the {\i drasha} that the waters affect the {\i bo\u8217'el} derived from the extra {\i vav} (i.e., {\i uva\u8217'u} rather than {\i ba\u8217'u}), or from the repetition of the entire word {\i ba\u8217'u} itself?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to resolve this from the second clause of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i nitme\u8217'ah venitme\u8217'ah} \u8212- where similarly a word appears with and without a {\i vav}. But the {\i Gemara} notes that the same question applies there as well, so it cannot resolve our question from that source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution comes from the explicit statement of {\i Rebbi} later in the {\i mishnah}, who says {\i shenei pe\u8217'amim} \u8212- twice, i.e., the derivation is from the repetition of the word itself, not from the extra {\i vav}. From this we infer by contrast that {\i Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- the one from whose position {\i Rebbi} is departing \u8212- does indeed derive {\i drashot} from the extra {\i vav}. This is consistent with Rabbi Akiva\u8217's well-known method of deriving meaning from every additional letter in the text, a method he received from Nachum Ish Gamzu.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Instances Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Akiva, then, there are effectively six textual units requiring explanation: the three appearances of {\i ba\u8217'u} and the three accompanying {\i vav}s \u8212- {\i shita krai ketivei}. Each pair of units \u8212- one for her and one for him \u8212- teaches something distinct about the manner in which the waters operate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzava, Asiyah, Yedia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three categories are {\i tzava}, {\i asiyah}, and {\i yedia}. The {\i tzava} is where the {\i pasuk} issues a divine declaration, as it were, dictating what the waters will do \u8212- {\i uvau hamayim hamarerim litzvot beten} \u8212- this is the moment of commanding or establishing the reality of what the waters accomplish. The {\i asiyah} is where the {\i pasuk} describes what actually happens in practice \u8212- the water enters and her belly swells. The {\i yedia} is where the {\i kohen} informs her explicitly what she should expect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i yedia} carries a specific legal significance. There is an apparent contradiction in the {\i psukim} about the order in which the waters take effect. In one place, the {\i Torah} speaks of the thigh collapsing first, then the belly swelling \u8212- which corresponds to the anatomical sequence of the act of relations, progressing downward. But when the waters actually take effect, the belly is affected first, because that is the order in which the water physically enters the body. The {\i kohen} must inform her of this discrepancy explicitly, so that she does not think the waters failed to work because they did not proceed in the order she expected. The pair of {\i vav}s attached to each of these three categories indicates that the same framework \u8212- command, action, and informing \u8212- applies equally to the {\i bo\u8217'el}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Instances\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi} disagrees. He does not derive {\i drashot} from extra {\i vav}s \u8212- {\i tlata krai ketivei}, there are simply three instances of the word {\i ba\u8217'u}, one for each of the three categories: {\i tzava}, {\i asiyah}, and {\i yedia}. Under this approach, since every appearance of the word {\i ba\u8217'u} is already needed for those three contexts, how does {\i Rebbi} know that the waters affect the {\i bo\u8217'el} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: from the phrase {\i litzvot beten velanpil yarech} \u8212- to cause a swelling of the belly and a collapsing of the thigh \u8212- where the {\i Torah} does not use the feminine possessive. Elsewhere the {\i Torah} says {\i vetzavtah betnah venaphlah yerechah} \u8212- her belly and her thigh, with the female possessive. Since that verse already covers her belly and her thigh, the earlier, unpossessed phrase is redundant if applied to her. Its purpose must therefore be to indicate that his belly and his thigh will also collapse \u8212- {\i betno veyerecho shebo\u8217'el}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva can then respond: according to him, the unpossessed phrase serves a different purpose \u8212- it is the {\i yedia}, the {\i kohen}\u8217's informing her of the order of effects (belly first, then thigh). {\i Rebbi} counters: if that were all, the {\i Torah} would have simply written {\i betnah verechah}, with the female possessive. The deliberately generic phrasing signals that two things are being taught simultaneously: first, the order of effects for her, and second \u8212- by virtue of the absence of the feminine possessive \u8212- that the same effects apply to him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now cites a {\i baraita} addressing the word {\i nitme\u8217'ah}, which appears three times in the {\i parsha}. According to Rabbi Akiva, each appearance teaches a distinct consequence of her {\i tum\u8217'ah} status. The first: she is forbidden to her husband. The second: she is forbidden to the {\i bo\u8217'el}. The third: {\i echad litrumah} \u8212- she is disqualified from eating {\i terumah}. If she is the wife of a {\i kohen}, or even a {\i bat kohen}, her status has been so fundamentally compromised that she may no longer partake of {\i terumah}. {\i Divrei Rabbi Akiva.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael responds with a {\i kal vachomer} that derives a fourth consequence. There exist women who are forbidden to marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- such as a {\i grushah} \u8212- and yet who may still eat {\i terumah} as daughters of {\i kohanim}. These women have a compromised marital status relative to {\i kehunah} but retain their {\i terumah} rights through their lineage. Now: if this {\i sotah} woman cannot even eat {\i terumah} \u8212- as Rabbi Akiva derives \u8212- {\i eino din she\u8217'asurah likhehunah}? How much more so must she be forbidden to marry a {\i kohen} altogether? This is not a disagreement with Rabbi Akiva but an extension: Rabbi Yishmael adds a fourth {\i halacha} using the existing three as a springboard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical significance of this point is noteworthy. {\i Rashi} clarifies: {\i afilu hi bat kohen uba\u8217'alah kohen, nifselah min haterumah} \u8212- even if she is herself the daughter of a {\i kohen} and her husband is also a {\i kohen}, she is disqualified from {\i terumah}. This is not merely a consequence of the marital relationship being compromised; it affects her personal status as a {\i bat kohen}. So even if her husband dies and the marital basis for eating {\i terumah} is gone, she remains disqualified on the basis of her own defiled status. That is precisely what gives Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's {\i kal vachomer} its force: since even her personal status as a {\i bat kohen} has been overridden by her {\i sotah} status, certainly she cannot now marry a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Presumed Guilty Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Repetition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then poses a sharp question: given that the {\i Torah} itself repeatedly acknowledges uncertainty \u8212- describing both the case where she became {\i nitme\u8217'ah} and the case where she did not \u8212- why does it use the word {\i nitme\u8217'ah} repeatedly as though her guilt were established? {\i Im nitme\u8217'ah lamah shoseh? Ve\u8217'im lo nitme\u8217'ah lamah mashkeh?} If she is definitely guilty, why go through the process? And if she is innocent, on what basis does the husband have the right to subject her to it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definitely Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that the {\i Torah} is telling us something precise: {\i magid lecha hakatuv shehasafek asurah} \u8212- she is forbidden by virtue of the doubt alone. The repeated use of {\i nitme\u8217'ah} is not a declaration of certain guilt; it is a declaration that the {\i safek} itself confers a definite forbidden status. She is {\i vadai asurah} not because we know she sinned, but because the formal process of {\i kinui} and {\i stirah} \u8212- warning and seclusion \u8212- has created an objective status of {\i issur} that persists until resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable {\i chiddush}. The normal approach to a {\i safek} in {\i halacha} is either to be stringent as a precautionary measure or to permit on the basis of a {\i chazakah}. What is unusual about the {\i sotah} is that the {\i safek} itself \u8212- independent of any probability assessment \u8212- creates a {\i vadai} prohibition. She knows whether she sinned or not. She could be entirely innocent. And yet, the moment she formally becomes a {\i sotah} through the required legal process, she is objectively and certainly forbidden to sleep with her husband. This is not merely \u8220"we are being cautious because we don\u8217't know.\u8221" It is a real, positive {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Asei Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} develops this point carefully. The question is raised: why does the Torah need to establish this special status at all? A {\i safek} of adultery would presumably be {\i assur} anyway on general principles. The obvious answer is that she herself knows the truth \u8212- but {\i Tosafot} presses further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes that the {\i issur} here is an {\i issur asei} \u8212- a prohibition derived from a positive formulation. The {\i Torah} does not say \u8220"do not sleep with your husband\u8221"; it describes her as {\i tamei}. The operative prohibition flows from that status: {\i venitme\u8217'ah} \u8212- she is {\i tamei}, and therefore sleeping with her husband violates the status that has been declared. This is structurally similar to other cases of {\i issur asei}, such as the derivation that consuming human flesh is prohibited through the positive command {\i kol beheima tehorah tocheilu} \u8212- since human flesh is not a {\i beheima tehorah}, consuming it violates that positive imperative implicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence is significant: {\i afilu hi tehorah ne\u8217'eneshes b\u8217'veis din shel ma\u8217'alah im ba\u8217'alah k\u8217'chayvei asei} \u8212- even if she is entirely innocent of adultery, if she sleeps with her husband while she formally holds the status of a {\i sotah}, she has violated an {\i issur asei}, and she will be held accountable before the heavenly court together with her husband. The waters clearing her do not retroactively permit what was done while she held the {\i sotah} status. The prohibition is real and present from the moment the status attaches.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Tumah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Comparison to Sheretz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the principle that the {\i sotah\u8217's} doubtful status creates a definite {\i issur}, the {\i Gemara} extrapolates to general laws of {\i tum\u8217'ah}. {\i Mikhan attah dan lesheretz} \u8212- from here you can derive the rules governing {\i safek tum\u8217'ah} for ordinary {\i tumah} cases such as contact with a {\i sheretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} proceeds as follows. By a {\i sotah}, we do not treat {\i shogeg} as equivalent to {\i mazid}, and we do not treat {\i ones} as equivalent to {\i ratzon} \u8212- that is, involuntary exposure does not make her a {\i sotah}. Yet despite this, {\i asah bah safek kevadai} \u8212- the doubt is treated as certainty. By a {\i sheretz}, in contrast, the law makes no distinction at all between {\i shogeg} and {\i mazid} or between {\i ones} and {\i ratzon} \u8212- any contact creates {\i tum\u8217'ah} regardless of intent. Therefore, how much more so should a {\i safek} contact with a {\i sheretz} be treated as {\i vadai tum\u8217'ah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the source for the principle of {\i safek tum\u8217'ah bireshut hayachid safeko tamei} \u8212- a doubtful {\i tum\u8217'ah} in a private domain is treated as {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Private Domain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then applies the parameters of the {\i sotah} case to define when this rule operates. {\i Umah sotah reshut hayachid af sheretz reshut hayachid} \u8212- just as the {\i sotah} situation involves a {\i reshut hayachid}, the {\i safek tum\u8217'ah} rule applies only in a {\i reshut hayachid}, not in a {\i reshut harabbim}. One might immediately note that {\i seclusion} and {\i reshut hayachid} are not identical concepts. The definition of {\i reshut hayachid} for purposes of {\i safek tum\u8217'ah} is not the same as the Shabbat definition. It has more to do with the degree of privacy and the question of who was present and capable of observing what occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinui & Sitrah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a pointed question about the comparison. By a {\i sotah}, the reason we treat the doubt as a definite {\i issur} is that there are {\i raglayim ladavar} \u8212- circumstantial grounds for suspicion: there was a {\i kinui} (warning) and a {\i stirah} (seclusion in defiance of that warning). By a simple {\i safek tum\u8217'ah} case \u8212- did the {\i sheretz} touch the food or not? \u8212- there are no such grounds. How can the comparison hold?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} answers by reframing the role of {\i kinui} and {\i stirah} in the {\i sotah} case. The {\i kinui} and {\i stirah} do not establish that it is {\i likely} she committed adultery \u8212- they merely elevate the situation from irrelevant seclusion to a genuine {\i safek}. Without {\i kinui}, a woman going into seclusion with a man would not constitute a {\i safek} at all, because we operate with a {\i chezkas kashrus} \u8212- a presumption of propriety. We do not assume that every private encounter involves adultery. The {\i kinui} strips away that presumption and converts the situation into a genuine 50-50 {\i safek}. Once it is a genuine 50-50 {\i safek}, it is comparable to a simple {\i safek} of whether the {\i sheretz} touched the food. The {\i raglayim ladavar} do not tip the balance toward guilt; they merely create the {\i safek} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knowledge Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second parameter derived from {\i sotah}: {\i umah sotah davar sheyesh bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el} \u8212- the {\i sotah} situation is one in which someone could in principle be asked what happened, namely the woman herself. Therefore, {\i af sheretz davar sheyesh bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el} \u8212- the rule of {\i safek tum\u8217'ah bireshut hayachid} applies only where there is someone present who could have knowledge of what occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why should this matter? One approach is that the function of treating the {\i safek} as a {\i vadai issur} is to force the matter toward resolution. If someone is present who knows what happened, declaring the situation {\i tamei} creates pressure for that knowledge to surface. If no one could possibly know, the declaration of {\i tum\u8217'ah} serves no such clarifying function. This is also why {\i reshut harabbim} tends toward {\i tahor} \u8212- not simply because many people were present, but paradoxically because in a very public setting, no one specific individual can be said to have observed what occurred. It is the intermediate case \u8212- a few people present, one of whom could know \u8212- that triggers the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes an application of this principle to the case of a woman with a doubtful {\i niddah} status when others were present: even if people were nearby, they could not observe anything about her internal status. The \u8220"someone who could be asked\u8221" must be someone with actual epistemic access to the relevant facts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes with the fully articulated rule: {\i davar sheyesh bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el bireshut hayachid safeko tamei} \u8212- if someone could be asked and the event occurred in a private domain, the {\i safek} is treated as {\i tamei}. {\i Bireshut harabbim safeko tahor} \u8212- if it occurred in a public domain, the {\i safek} is treated as {\i tahor}. {\i Veshein bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el, bein bireshut hayachid uvein bireshut harabbim, safeko tahor} \u8212- and if there is no one who could be asked, whether in a private or public domain, the {\i safek} is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 29\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sotah & Safek Tumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Sotah to Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 29, and we pick up at the bottom of 28b. The sugya has now transitioned from {\i sotah} to {\i terumah} \u8212- which, somewhat surprisingly, will be the focus of this entire daf. The immediate reason for the shift is not primarily the connection between {\i sotah} and {\i terumah} as such, but rather that our {\i mishnah} of this {\i perek} recorded a series of {\i drashos} under the heading {\i bo bayom darash Rabbi Akiva}, several of which concern {\i tumah}, and it is those {\i drashos} that will occupy us.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The original connection was that from the case of {\i sotah} \u8212- a wife who secluded herself ({\i yichud}) with another man after her husband\u8217's formal warning \u8212- we derived the broader principle governing {\i safek tumah bereshut hayachid}. The {\i sotah\u8217's} status demonstrates that once the relevant circumstances are established, the presumption shifts: she is treated as definitively forbidden rather than merely doubtfully so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Status & Tosafos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} is that a {\i sotah} is {\i vadai assur} \u8212- definitively forbidden \u8212- on the basis of the {\i safek}. This status holds until it is clarified through the formal procedure of drinking the bitter water. {\i Tosafos} draws out an important implication: even if it later becomes clear that she had not in fact committed adultery, or even if she herself knows she has not, the violation of her current status \u8212- such as sleeping with her husband before the matter is resolved \u8212- constitutes a genuine transgression. Her situation is one of definitive prohibition, not merely doubtful prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Tumah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this, the Gemara derives the general principle of {\i safek tumah bereshut hayachid}. The language of {\i tumah} in the {\i sotah} context carries its own specific meaning, but the transfer to the general laws of {\i tumah} throughout the Torah is significant. The conditions are formal and precise: the doubt must arise in a {\i reshut hayachid}, a private domain, and there must be {\i yesh bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el} \u8212- someone present who is capable of being asked about the circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One way of understanding why the {\i safek} generates {\i vadai tumah} is that treating the matter as definitively impure, pending clarification, creates incentive for resolution: witnesses may come forward, participants may remember. This social function, however, only makes sense when there is actually someone present who might know something \u8212- hence the requirement of {\i yesh bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el}. If no one is present who could be questioned, {\i ein bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el}, then even in a private domain the doubt is resolved leniently as {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinuy & Stira Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises a pointed question about the analogy between {\i sotah} and {\i safek tumah}. In the case of ordinary {\i tumah}, the doubt is a straightforward fifty-fifty uncertainty. By contrast, the {\i sotah} case seems to involve {\i raglayim ladavar} \u8212- circumstantial indicators suggesting guilt \u8212- and not a mere equipoise. Where, then, is the fifty-fifty doubt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} introduces a crucial conceptual framework in response. The {\i kinuy} (the husband\u8217's formal warning) and {\i stira} (the subsequent seclusion) together produce a fifty-fifty doubt. Without them, the situation would not even qualify as a genuine {\i safek} in the first place. The {\i kinuy} and {\i stira} are thus not evidence of guilt; they are the precondition that elevates the situation to the status of a doubt worth taking seriously. This is an important lens through which to understand the {\i sotah} institution, and one that had not been fully foregrounded in earlier discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should also be noted that {\i reshut hayachid} in this context does not carry its Shabbos meaning. Here it refers more to a private location, and the determination is functional rather than spatial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To close the introductory survey, the Gemara recaps the three restrictions the {\i sotah} incurs from the word {\i venitma\u8217'ah}: she is forbidden to her {\i ba\u8217'al} (her husband), forbidden to the {\i bo\u8217'el} (the suspected adulterer), and forbidden to eat {\i terumah}. The last applies whether she is the wife of a {\i kohen} \u8212- in which case her marital status no longer entitles her to {\i terumah} \u8212- or a {\i bat kohen} herself, in which case this act is understood to invalidate her personal entitlement as well. It is worth noting, as was observed in discussion, that while her husband\u8217's financial obligations toward her presumably continue during the pendency of the process, the prohibition from {\i terumah} takes immediate effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Akiva vs.\u160 ?R. Yishmael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael\u8217's Kal VaChom\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael, responding to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's derivation of {\i terumah} from {\i venitma\u8217'ah}, proposes a {\i kal vachomer}: if there exist women who are forbidden to marry a {\i kohen} yet are still permitted to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- such as a {\i gerushah} who is a {\i bat kohen} \u8212- then a woman who cannot eat {\i terumah} at all should certainly be forbidden from marrying a {\i kohen}. The scenario being contemplated is where this woman\u8217's husband subsequently dies; her status as a presumed adulteress or as a {\i sotah} should, by this logic, render her permanently forbidden to a {\i kohen} just as a {\i zonah} is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position, as introduced in the {\i mishnah}, had been that she is forbidden to the {\i ba\u8217'al}, forbidden to the {\i bo\u8217'el}, and forbidden to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- three distinct restrictions, each derived from a separate word or letter. The Gemara now examines whether Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's {\i kal vachomer} was meant as an addition to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's teaching or as a disagreement with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a sharp challenge: according to Rabbi Akiva, where does the prohibition of {\i kehunah} come from? And if Rabbi Akiva would say he simply does not need a {\i pasuk} for {\i kehunah} \u8212- because a woman who commits adultery acquires the status of a {\i zonah}, and since we treat {\i safek} like {\i vadai} we would say {\i safek zonah ke-zonah}, rendering her forbidden to a {\i kohen} automatically \u8212- then the same argument should equally spare him the need for a {\i pasuk} regarding {\i terumah}. The prohibition of a {\i zonah} from {\i terumah} is already established by the verse {\i u\u8217'vat kohen ki tihyeh le-ish zar hi betrumas hakodashim lo tochel}, which Rabbi Akiva interprets to cover a woman who has relations with a man forbidden to her. So why would he need a separate {\i pasuk} to tell him she is forbidden to {\i terumah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a tight and well-constructed challenge: if the logic of {\i zonah} status can do the work for {\i kehunah}, it should equally do the work for {\i terumah}, leaving Rabbi Akiva\u8217's three {\i venitma\u8217'ah} derivations without adequate content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Four Pesukim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the difficulty by noting that according to Rabbi Akiva there are in fact four textual indicators, not three. The word {\i venitma\u8217'ah} appears three times in the {\i sotah} section, but one of the instances is preceded by a {\i vav} that Rabbi Akiva treats as generating an additional derivation. So we have three {\i venitma\u8217'ahs} plus one {\i vav} \u8212- four sources in total. One teaches that she is forbidden to her {\i ba\u8217'al}, one that she is forbidden to the {\i bo\u8217'el}, one that she is forbidden to {\i terumah}, and one \u8212- derived from the extra {\i vav} \u8212- that she is forbidden in {\i kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The need for the {\i kehunah} derivation from the {\i vav} is that we cannot simply assume her status as a {\i zonah} provides it. Evidently Rabbi Akiva holds that a woman in the uncertain status of {\i sotah} does not automatically become a {\i zonah} in a way that would independently establish all the attendant restrictions. Hence the {\i pasuk} must supply the {\i kehunah} prohibition explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael\u8217's Three + Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael, by contrast, does not derive from the extra {\i vav}. He therefore has only three {\i venitma\u8217'ahs}: one for {\i ba\u8217'al}, one for {\i bo\u8217'el}, and one for {\i terumah}. From {\i terumah} he then constructs a {\i kal vachomer} to {\i kehunah}: since she is forbidden from eating {\i terumah}, she should certainly be forbidden from marrying a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relevance Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses: how does Rabbi Yishmael know that the third {\i venitma\u8217'ah} teaches {\i terumah} rather than {\i kehunah}? One could argue the reverse \u8212- that the third {\i venitma\u8217'ah} establishes the prohibition of {\i kehunah}, and that {\i terumah} would then be permitted, since we know there are women forbidden to marry a {\i kohen} who nevertheless retain the right to eat {\i terumah} (a {\i gerushah bat kohen} being the paradigm case).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's response is elegant: the third {\i venitma\u8217'ah} should be read in parallel with the first two. What do the first two establish? Prohibitions that take effect {\i m\u8217'chayim} \u8212- while everyone is still alive: she is forbidden to her husband and to the suspected adulterer here and now. A prohibition of {\i terumah} similarly applies immediately: she is a {\i bat kohen}, say, and she loses her right to eat {\i terumah} at this moment. A prohibition of {\i kehunah}, by contrast, only becomes relevant if her husband dies \u8212- only then would the question of whether she may marry another {\i kohen} arise. Rabbi Yishmael therefore concludes that the third {\i venitma\u8217'ah} must be targeting the immediately applicable prohibition, which is {\i terumah}, and {\i kehunah} is then derived by {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Rejects Dumiya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva does not accept this framework of {\i dumya de-ba\u8217'al u-vo\u8217'el} \u8212- the comparison that presumes the third {\i venitma\u8217'ah} must, like the first two, address something immediately applicable. In his view, the third {\i venitma\u8217'ah} might well teach the lesser {\i chiddush}, namely the prohibition of {\i kehunah}, which applies only post-mortem, while {\i terumah} would remain unknown without an additional source. That is why he requires the extra {\i vav} \u8212- to capture {\i kehunah} \u8212- while dedicating the third {\i venitma\u8217'ah} to {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redundancy Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, the Gemara suggests, Rabbi Akiva might agree that in principle a {\i kal vachomer} could yield the {\i kehunah} prohibition, but he nonetheless uses the {\i vav} to state it explicitly. The principle invoked is {\i milsa d\u8217'asi b\u8217'kal vachomer tarach v\u8217'kasav lah kra}: something that could have been derived logically, the Torah nevertheless troubled itself to write explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle stands in interesting tension with the Gemara\u8217's occasional declaration elsewhere of {\i lama li kra, sevara hi} \u8212- why do I need a {\i pasuk}, I could figure it out myself? That formulation reflects the remarkable weight accorded to human reason and {\i sevara} in the tradition of {\i Torah she-be\u8217'al peh}, to the point that a verse can be set aside or interpreted non-literally when it conflicts with what logic demands. The flip side, captured by {\i milsa d\u8217'asi b\u8217'kal vachomer tarach v\u8217'kasav lah kra}, is that this same rational force does not make a scriptural reaffirmation unwelcome or superfluous. It is not a problem for the Torah to state explicitly what one could have derived \u8212- the two sources are not in conflict, they simply converge on the same conclusion from different directions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek Tumah from Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Tumah Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Gidal said in the name of Rav: the rule that {\i davar she-yesh bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el} in a {\i reshut hayachid} yields {\i safek tamei}, while {\i ein bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el} yields {\i safek tahor} regardless of domain \u8212- this rule is not derived primarily from {\i sotah}. It is derived from a different {\i pasuk} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Meat Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source is the verse {\i ve-habasar asher yiga b\u8217'chol tamei lo ye\u8217'achel} \u8212- meat of a {\i korban} that touches anything {\i tamei} may not be eaten. The Gemara reads this carefully: {\i vadai tamei hu de-lo ye\u8217'achel} \u8212- it is only if it is definitively known to be {\i tamei} that it may not be eaten; if there is doubt, {\i ha-safek tamei safek tahor}, it may be eaten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Tahor Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading, however, creates an apparent contradiction. The end of the verse states {\i kol tahor yochal basar} \u8212- whoever is {\i tahor} may eat the meat \u8212- from which one might derive that it is only when one is definitively {\i tahor} that eating is permitted, and that {\i safek tamei} is therefore forbidden. The beginning of the verse seems to teach {\i safek tahor} while the end teaches {\i safek tamei}. The Gemara resolves this by distinguishing between contexts: one clause addresses the case where no person is on the scene \u8212- only the object, {\i ein bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el} \u8212- and the doubt about the meat itself is resolved leniently; the other clause addresses the case where a person is present, {\i yesh bo da\u8217'at lisha\u8217'el}, and in that case doubt is resolved strictly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have proposed a different distinction \u8212- that the beginning of the verse speaks about doubt concerning the meat and the end about doubt concerning the person \u8212- but the Gemara does not adopt this reading. The determining factor is not which party is in doubt but whether anyone capable of being consulted is present in the situation at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Domain Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one had only this {\i pasuk} from {\i korban} meat, one might have concluded that the {\i yesh bo da\u8217'at} / {\i ein bo da\u8217'at} distinction applies equally regardless of whether the incident occurred in a public or private domain. It is the {\i sotah} case that teaches the additional variable of {\i reshut hayachid} versus {\i reshut harabbim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Sources Combined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if one had only the {\i sotah} model, one might have concluded that {\i safek tumah} in a {\i reshut hayachid} requires {\i da\u8217'at} on the part of both parties \u8212- the one who touches and the one who is touched \u8212- since in {\i sotah} both the man and the woman are present and aware of the circumstances. By {\i sotah} alone, one might limit the strict ruling to cases where both the {\i tamei} and {\i tahor} parties have {\i da\u8217'at}, making the rule inapplicable when, for instance, a rodent touches an object with no human witness. It is the {\i korban} meat {\i pasuk} that teaches the rule requires only that some person be on the scene who is capable of being asked \u8212- not that both parties to the contact must have {\i da\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both sources are therefore necessary: {\i sotah} supplies the {\i reshut hayachid} dimension, and the {\i korban} meat verse supplies the {\i da\u8217'at} principle in its correct formulation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlishi LeTerumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the {\i mishnah}, where Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i bo bayom} teachings include a derivation from the {\i pasuk} of {\i kli cheres}: {\i kol asher betocho yitma} \u8212- anything inside an earthenware vessel contaminated by a {\i sheretz} becomes {\i tamei}. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i drasha} of {\i yitma} \u8212- reading it as {\i yitamei}, meaning it has the capacity to further transmit {\i tumah} \u8212- establishes that food can become a {\i shlishi}, a third-degree derivative of {\i tumah}, with respect to {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's famous reaction was: {\i mi gala afar me\u8217'einecha, Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai?} \u8212- \u8220"Who removed the dust from your eyes, Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai?\u8221" The comment is pointed: Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai had taught that the concept of {\i shlishi biterumah} rests only on a {\i kal vachomer}, not on an explicit {\i pasuk}. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's derivation demonstrated that it has a scriptural basis after all, sparing it from challenge. The remark comes in appreciation \u8212- the earlier master had said we must acknowledge the weakness of the derivation, and the later master has now shored it up.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Akiva Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the Gemara to ask: if before Rabbi Akiva there was no {\i pasuk}, why did everyone nonetheless assume that food could become a {\i shlishi} with respect to {\i terumah}? Why was it treated as {\i tamei} without scriptural warrant?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: although there was no explicit {\i pasuk}, there is nevertheless a {\i din kal vachomer} \u8212- the concept of {\i shlishi} for {\i terumah} can be derived logically. The {\i halacha} was assumed based on this {\i kal vachomer} even before Rabbi Akiva supplied the {\i pasuk}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevul Yom Kal VaChomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} proceeds as follows: a {\i tevul yom} \u8212- a person who has immersed in a {\i mikvah} but for whom sunset has not yet arrived \u8212- is {\i mutar b\u8217'chullin}, permitted with respect to non-sanctified food, meaning he is not considered {\i tamei} in a way that would compromise ordinary {\i chullin}. Yet {\i poseil bitrumah} \u8212- he invalidates {\i terumah} that he touches. Now, a {\i kikar sheini} \u8212- a loaf of bread that is a second-degree derivative of {\i tumah} \u8212- is itself {\i pasul b\u8217'chullin}, meaning it already creates a problem even at the level of non-sanctified food. Surely, then \u8212- {\i eino din sheyaaseh shlishi biterumah} \u8212- this loaf, which is already {\i tamei} with respect to {\i chullin}, should certainly be able to render {\i terumah} it touches {\i pasul} as a {\i shlishi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structural logic of the {\i kal vachomer} calls for something that is {\i tahor} with respect to {\i chullin} yet invalidates {\i terumah}, from which we argue {\i a fortiori} that something {\i tamei} with respect to {\i chullin} should certainly invalidate {\i terumah}. The {\i tevul yom} approximates the first role \u8212- he does not disqualify {\i chullin} yet he cannot eat {\i terumah}. There is, however, an acknowledged imprecision in comparing a person to a loaf of bread, and one might equally observe that a {\i tevul yom} is not formally classified as a {\i sheini betu\u8217'mah}; his status is its own category of residual {\i tumah}. But that is the argument as it stands, and we proceed to examine where it can be challenged.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzad HaShaVeh Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avi HaTumah Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} is immediately open to challenge: {\i mah letevul yom she-kein avi hatumah}. The {\i tevul yom}\u8217's residual {\i tumah} may seem weak, but it originated from something weightier. The person in question began as an {\i av hatumah} or at least a {\i rishon} \u8212- perhaps he was a {\i baal keri} who himself had the status of an {\i av hatumah}, or at minimum he had directly touched a {\i sheretz} and began as a {\i rishon}. The residual {\i tumah} of a person who started at a higher level may be more potent than the initial {\i tumah} of a {\i kikar sheini}, which never started above {\i sheini}. One cannot therefore conclude from the {\i tevul yom}\u8217's power over {\i terumah} that a {\i sheini} of food equally has that power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Middle Ground\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to circumvent this objection by substituting a different {\i tevul yom}: {\i tevul yom de-sheretz}, a person who touched a {\i sheretz} and then immersed. Such a person was only a {\i rishon}, not an {\i av hatumah}, and his residual {\i tumah} after immersion started from a lower point. If even the residual {\i tumah} of someone who started only as a {\i rishon} is powerful enough to disqualify {\i terumah}, then the argument toward {\i sheini} of food should be stronger.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Persons vs.\u160 ?Foods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this middle ground still does not close the gap. {\i Mah letevul yom de-sheretz she-kein bemino avi hatumah} \u8212- even in the case of {\i tevul yom de-sheretz}, the {\i tumah} of persons belongs to a category that is intrinsically more potent, because a person is capable, at least in principle, of becoming an {\i av hatumah}. Food, by contrast, can never become an {\i av hatumah} no matter what it touches \u8212- even if food were to come into contact with a {\i meit}, it becomes a {\i rishon} at most, never an {\i av}. The {\i tumah} of persons may therefore be categorically weightier than the {\i tumah} of foods, and one cannot use residual personal {\i tumah} as a lever to prove anything about the initial {\i tumah} of a {\i sheini} in food. This distinction \u8212- that the category of personal {\i tumah} carries an intrinsic weight that food {\i tumah} does not, even before considering the actual degree \u8212- is striking and conceptually important.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combined Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the {\i kal vachomer} from {\i tevul yom} cannot stand alone, the Gemara introduces {\i kli cheres} as a second source and proposes learning from a {\i tzad hashaveh} \u8212- a common denominator between the two. The argument runs as follows: a {\i tevul yom} (a person with residual {\i tumah}) can invalidate {\i terumah}; a {\i kli cheres} that is a {\i rishon} (and which, like food, can never become an {\i av hatumah}) can invalidate {\i terumah}. Since we have two instances \u8212- one a person, one a vessel \u8212- we can extract what is common to both and argue that anything sharing that common feature should similarly invalidate {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kli cheres} case is useful because it removes the objection about personal {\i tumah} being categorically weightier: {\i kli cheres} is not a person, it cannot become an {\i av hatumah}, and yet it invalidates {\i terumah}. Against this, one might note that {\i kli cheres} is a {\i rishon}, not a {\i sheini}, and its case involves {\i tumah me\u8217'aviro} \u8212- it transmits {\i tumah} even without direct contact through the airspace of the vessel \u8212- which is a distinctive stringency not found in the {\i tevul yom}. Hence neither source is free of distinctive characteristics.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weakness Eliminated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely the {\i chozar ha-din} dynamic: each of the two sources has characteristics that might explain its power over {\i terumah} that the other does not share, and neither characteristic is present in the {\i kikar sheini}. The argument oscillates between the two \u8212- one deflects the objection raised against the other \u8212- until we are left not with a true {\i kal vachomer} but with a {\i tzad hashaveh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Denominator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tzad hashaveh}, as formulated by Rashi (who emends the text from {\i she-mutarin b\u8217'chullin} to {\i she-metammim}, since a {\i kli cheres} that is a {\i rishon} does in fact render {\i chullin} a {\i sheini}), is that both a {\i tevul yom} and a {\i kli cheres} are called {\i tamei} and both invalidate {\i terumah}. Anything that is called {\i tamei}, including a {\i kikar sheini}, should therefore invalidate {\i terumah}. This is the {\i tzad hashaveh}, and it is, as the Gemara notes, a weaker form of argument than the original {\i kal vachomer} \u8212- it is more analogical than deductive, resting on the extraction of a common property rather than on a strict hierarchy of stringency.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzad Chamur Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzad Chamur Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The vulnerability of any {\i tzad hashaveh} is well known: it is always possible to object that the distinctive characteristics of the two source cases \u8212- rather than their common denominator \u8212- are what explain the {\i halacha} in each instance. If that is so, then the common denominator cannot carry the {\i halacha} to the new case. This is the {\i tzad chamur} challenge. In our case, the distinctive characteristics are: the {\i kli cheres} transmits {\i tumah} through airspace ({\i me\u8217'aviro}), which is a unique stringency; and the {\i tevul yom} either originated from a higher level of {\i tumah} or belongs to the categorically weightier domain of personal {\i tumah}. A {\i kikar sheini} has neither of these features, and one cannot therefore conclude from the {\i tzad hashaveh} that it invalidates {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generational Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i tzad chamur} objection is what later generations raised against the derivation. The Gemara notes that Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai was apparently not troubled by this challenge \u8212- perhaps because raising a {\i tzad chamur} objection against every {\i tzad hashaveh} would effectively eliminate all such arguments from the tradition, which he was unwilling to do. But the later generation was not prepared to rest easy with a {\i tzad hashaveh} that could be challenged, and it was Rabbi Akiva\u8217's contribution that saved the {\i halacha} from ongoing uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Pesach Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's derivation from the {\i kli cheres pasuk} gave {\i shlishi biterumah} an explicit scriptural foundation. Once there is a {\i pasuk}, the {\i tzad hashaveh} and its attendant vulnerability to {\i tzad chamur} objection become moot. The {\i halacha} rests on the {\i derasha}, not on the logical argument, and the challenge of the later generation is answered. This is a concrete illustration of the principle noted earlier: a {\i pasuk} is more secure than a {\i sevara}, because {\i sevara} can always be countered by a counter-{\i sevara}, while an explicit scriptural derivation \u8212- or one firmly grounded in {\i drasha} \u8212- cannot be similarly dislodged.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Revi\u8217'i BeKodesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yosei\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues: given that we now know {\i shlishi biterumah}, how do we derive the parallel rule for {\i kodesh} \u8212- namely, {\i revi\u8217'i bekodesh}, that a fourth-degree derivative of {\i tumah} invalidates sanctified food? A {\i braita} records Rabbi Yosei\u8217's question: {\i minayin lerevi\u8217'i bekodesh she-pasul?} The answer, he says, is a {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechusar Kipurim Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} parallels the one used to derive {\i shlishi biterumah} from {\i tevul yom}, but operates one level higher. {\i Mechusar kipurim} refers to a person who has completed the immersion and waited for sunset but whose {\i tahara} process is not yet complete because he still must bring a {\i korban} \u8212- such as a {\i zav}, {\i zavah}, or {\i yoledet}. After sunset, such a person may eat {\i terumah}, but remains forbidden from eating {\i kodashim} until the {\i korban} is brought the next morning. This is an even more residual {\i tumah} than that of a {\i tevul yom}: by {\i mechusar kipurim} it is barely a {\i tumah} at all, yet it is enough to bar the person from {\i kodashim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} runs: if a {\i mechusar kipurim}, who is {\i mutar biterumah} \u8212- permitted to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- is nonetheless {\i pasul bekodesh} \u8212- barred from {\i kodashim} \u8212- then {\i shlishi she-pasul biterumah} \u8212- something that is a {\i shlishi} and has thereby become invalid for {\i terumah} \u8212- surely should be {\i pasul bekodesh} as well. The parallel to the original {\i tevul yom} argument is tight: just as the residual {\i tumah} of {\i tevul yom} proves that {\i sheini} of food creates a problem for {\i terumah}, so the residual {\i tumah} of {\i mechusar kipurim} proves that {\i shlishi} of food creates a problem for {\i kodesh}, yielding the status of {\i revi\u8217'i bekodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlishi LKodesh Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before arriving at {\i revi\u8217'i}, however, we must first establish {\i shlishi lekodesh} \u8212- that food which is a {\i shlishi} is invalid for {\i kodashim}. This is derived from the verse {\i ve-habasar asher yiga b\u8217'chol tamei lo ye\u8217'achel}, which concerns meat of a {\i korban}. The Torah called food that fell into a {\i kli cheres} contaminated by a {\i sheretz} \u8212- a {\i sheini} \u8212- by the term {\i tamei} (as established earlier in the {\i pasuk} of {\i kol asher betocho yitma}). Thus when this verse says that meat of a {\i korban} that touches anything {\i tamei} \u8212- including a {\i sheini} \u8212- may not be eaten, it establishes that if {\i korban} meat becomes a {\i shlishi} by touching a {\i sheini}, it is forbidden. {\i Shlishi lekodesh} is therefore scripturally grounded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Revi\u8217'i Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From {\i shlishi lekodesh} being established by the {\i pasuk}, we then derive {\i revi\u8217'i} by the {\i kal vachomer} from {\i mechusar kipurim} as described: if someone whose {\i tumah} is so residual that he may eat {\i terumah} yet cannot eat {\i kodashim}, then {\i shlishi} food \u8212- which genuinely cannot be eaten as {\i terumah} \u8212- should certainly be able to invalidate {\i kodashim} it touches, producing a {\i revi\u8217'i bekodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan\u8217's Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, however, objects sharply: {\i ta\u8217'am de-Rabbi Yosei ani yode\u8217'a, mahu? She-harei teshuvato betzido} \u8212- I understand what Rabbi Yosei is doing, but the refutation is built right into the argument. {\i Ochel ha-ba machmas tevul yom yochiach} \u8212- consider food that has been rendered {\i pasul} by contact with a {\i tevul yom}. Such food is {\i pasul biterumah} \u8212- it invalidates {\i terumah} \u8212- yet {\i eino oseh revi\u8217'i bekodesh} \u8212- it does not create a {\i revi\u8217'i} for {\i kodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking counter-example. The entire {\i kal vachomer} rested on analogizing the {\i shlishi} in food to the {\i mechusar kipurim} \u8212- if a person with only residual {\i tumah} can bar {\i kodashim}, then food at the {\i shlishi} level should certainly do so. But the case of food touched by a {\i tevul yom} reveals that the analogy between persons and foods in this chain does not hold cleanly: food touched by a {\i tevul yom} is {\i pasul biterumah} and yet does not produce a {\i revi\u8217'i bekodesh}. This breaks the very logical bridge on which the {\i kal vachomer} depends, and the full resolution of this challenge must await further analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sheini/Shelishi/Revii\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tvul Yom & Mechussar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion on this {\i daf} picks up from the bottom of 29b, deep in the analysis of the degrees of {\i tumah} and how they propagate across different categories of food. The foundational structure of the argument is this: we learn that there is a concept of {\i shlishi bi-terumah} \u8212- that {\i terumah} can become {\i tamei} at a third degree of remove \u8212- from the case of a {\i tvul yom}, a person who has immersed in a {\i mikvah} but whose {\i taharah} is not yet complete until nightfall. Such a person, though carrying only residual {\i tumah}, is forbidden to eat {\i terumah} but is permitted to eat {\i chulin}. The analogy then runs: if a person with residual {\i tumah} is forbidden to eat {\i terumah}, then food which is itself {\i tamei} \u8212- a {\i sheini} \u8212- should certainly be capable of rendering {\i terumah tamei}, producing a {\i shlishi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second step extends the logic further. A {\i mechussar kippurim} \u8212- one who has immersed and whose {\i taharah} is complete but who has not yet brought the required atonement offerings \u8212- is permitted to eat {\i terumah} but is forbidden to eat {\i kodashim}. By the same analogical structure, if a person with this residual status cannot partake of {\i kodashim}, then food which is itself {\i tamei} for {\i terumah} \u8212- a {\i shlishi} \u8212- should certainly be capable of rendering {\i kodashim tamei} at a fourth degree, producing a {\i revii}. These derivations proceed via {\i kal va-chomer} or {\i gezerah shavah}, not as {\i halachah le-Moshe mi-Sinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan objects to the entire structure of this {\i drashah} attributed to Rabbi Yose, declaring that he cannot understand it \u8212- {\i ta\u8217'am b\u8217'Rebbi eini yodea} \u8212- because the refutation lies right alongside the argument. He introduces a counterexample: {\i ochel ha-ba machmat tvul yom} \u8212- food that was touched by a {\i tvul yom}. Such food, namely {\i terumah} touched by a {\i tvul yom}, would effectively be a {\i shlishi}. According to the logic of the derivation, it should then be capable of rendering {\i kodashim} into a {\i revii}. Yet we know that this is not the case: such food is {\i pasul bi-terumah} \u8212- it invalidates the {\i terumah} \u8212- but {\i eino oseh revii ba-kodesh}, it does not go on to produce a {\i revii} in {\i kodashim}. The existence of this category \u8212- things that are {\i pasul} for {\i terumah} and yet do not transmit {\i tumah} further into {\i kodashim} \u8212- undermines the inference from {\i mechussar kippurim} to {\i shlishi bi-terumah} making {\i kodashim} into a {\i revii}. If the pattern were consistent, Rabbi Yochanan argues, why should we assume that a {\i shlishi} of {\i terumah} \u8212- which proceeds through food all the way down \u8212- passes the {\i tumah} into {\i kodashim} when the analogous case of food derived from a {\i tvul yom} does not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tvul Yom Food Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is how we know that food touched by a {\i tvul yom} does not make {\i kodashim} into a {\i revii}. The Gemara cites a {\i Braitah} in which a dispute among {\i Tannaim} is recorded. Abba Shaul holds a stringency {\i de-rabbanan}: a {\i tvul yom}, when it comes to matters of {\i kodashim}, is treated as a {\i rishon}, not merely as a {\i sheini}. The formulation is {\i metamei shnayim u-fosel echad} \u8212- he renders two levels {\i tamei} and invalidates one further level. In practical terms, for {\i kodshim} purposes, the {\i tvul yom} touching food makes the food a {\i sheini}, which touching further food makes it a {\i shlishi}, and that {\i shlishi} touching {\i kodesh} renders it {\i pasul} as a {\i revii}. So according to Abba Shaul, the {\i tumah} deriving from a {\i tvul yom} does in fact descend all the way to a {\i revii} in {\i kodashim}. Rabbi Meir holds that the {\i tvul yom} is treated as a {\i sheini} \u8212- {\i metamei echad u-fosel echad} \u8212- making {\i terumah} into a {\i shlishi}, which then renders {\i kodesh} into a {\i revii}. So according to both Abba Shaul and Rabbi Meir, the {\i tumah} of the {\i tvul yom} does proceed through to a {\i revii} in {\i kodashim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The position that creates a genuine counterexample is that of the {\i Chachamim}, who say: just as a {\i tvul yom} invalidates {\i terumah} food and liquid, so too he invalidates {\i kodashim} food and liquid \u8212- but it stops there. The food that he invalidated as a {\i shlishi} does not go on to render {\i kodashim} into a {\i revii}. If we rule like the {\i Chachamim}, then food touched by a {\i tvul yom} is indeed a counterexample to the proposed derivation from {\i mechussar kippurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa challenges this framing: {\i be-mai} \u8212- upon what basis does Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's objection rest? It rests on the assumption that the ruling follows the {\i Rabbanan}, who say the {\i tvul yom}\u8217's {\i tumah} stops at the {\i shlishi} and does not extend to a {\i revii} in {\i kodashim}. But perhaps the {\i drashah} of Rabbi Yose follows the position of Abba Shaul, who holds that the {\i tvul yom}\u8217's {\i tumah} does reach a {\i revii} in {\i kodashim}. According to Abba Shaul, even though this represents a rabbinic stringency in its characterization of the {\i tvul yom} as a {\i rishon}, the underlying principle is that {\i tumah} deriving from a {\i tvul yom} can descend two full levels to render {\i kodashim} into a {\i revii}. Under that view, there is no counterexample at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Countermove Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds that if we accept Abba Shaul\u8217's position \u8212- that the {\i tvul yom}\u8217's {\i tumah} does pass all the way through to render {\i kodashim} into a {\i revii} \u8212- then there is a different problem. If this is consistent rather than a counterexample, then there is no need to derive the rule about {\i revii} in {\i kodashim} from the case of {\i mechussar kippurim} at all. The Gemara proposes an alternative derivation: {\i u-mah ochel ha-ba machmat tvul yom} \u8212- if food that comes from a {\i tvul yom}, who himself is {\i mutar be-chulin}, whose {\i tumah} starts at a relatively weak point, nonetheless generates {\i tumah} that descends all the way to produce a {\i revii} in {\i kodashim}, then certainly {\i ochel shlishi ha-ba machmat sheni} \u8212- food that is a {\i shlishi} deriving from a {\i sheini} \u8212- where the {\i sheini} itself, unlike the {\i tvul yom}, is {\i assur be-chulin}, should certainly produce a {\i revii} in {\i kodashim} by {\i kal va-chomer}. The Gemara is asking: why did the {\i drashah} run to the case of {\i mechussar kippurim} when, if Abba Shaul is correct, there was a ready inference available from the case of food touched by a {\i tvul yom}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzad Hashaveh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then considers whether one might deflect this proposed inference with a {\i pircha}: perhaps the {\i tvul yom} case cannot serve as the basis for the derivation because {\i mah la-tvul yom she-ken av tumah} \u8212- the {\i tvul yom} could be an {\i av tumah}, a primary source of {\i tumah}, which is a stringency that food can never possess. Food can never be an {\i av tumah}, and so one cannot argue from the {\i tvul yom}\u8217's {\i tumah} extending to a {\i revii} in {\i kodashim} to the same being true for food that is itself {\i tamei}. The Gemara notes that this same {\i pircha} would apply equally to the case of the {\i mechussar kippurim} \u8212- he too is a person, who could potentially be an {\i av tumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Person-to-Object Challen\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge of learning from a person to food is a recurring structural problem in this entire {\i sugya}. The {\i tvul yom} and the {\i mechussar kippurim} are both persons, and persons have a dimension of {\i tumah} \u8212- their potential to be an {\i av tumah} \u8212- that food simply does not share. This asymmetry means that any inference from the person\u8217's {\i tumah} behavior to what food does with {\i tumah} is potentially flawed by this {\i pircha}. It is worth noting that this objection could have been raised much earlier in the entire deliberation; indeed, it was, and the Gemara dealt with it by invoking a {\i tzad ha-shaveh} involving {\i keli cheres}, drawing on the {\i Sotah} context in which the very problematic of persons being {\i tamei} generating rules about objects being {\i tamei} is central to the tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary & Closure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the core of this difficult passage: the Gemara seeks to establish, through derivation, that a {\i sheini} can make {\i terumah} into a {\i shlishi}, and that a {\i shlishi} of {\i terumah} can make {\i kodashim} into a {\i revii}. The first is derived by analogy to the {\i tvul yom} who is forbidden from {\i terumah} yet permitted with {\i chulin} \u8212- so {\i chulin} that is itself {\i tamei} as a {\i sheini} should make {\i terumah} into a {\i shlishi}. The second is derived by analogy to the {\i mechussar kippurim} who may eat {\i terumah} yet is forbidden from {\i kodashim} \u8212- so {\i terumah} that is itself {\i tamei} as a {\i shlishi} should render {\i kodashim} into a {\i revii}. The Gemara\u8217's challenge comes from the case of food touched by a {\i tvul yom}, which is {\i pasul} for {\i terumah} yet \u8212- according to the {\i Chachamim} \u8212- does not pass its {\i tumah} on to {\i kodashim}. This is a counterexample if we follow the {\i Chachamim}; but if we follow Abba Shaul and say it is not a counterexample, then why was it not itself used as the basis for the derivation? The Gemara leaves the tension unresolved in a fully satisfying way, and ends by noting that the challenge of learning from a person to food \u8212- the {\i pircha} that persons can be {\i avos tumah} while food cannot \u8212- remains a relevant critique of both potential sources of derivation, even if it was not formally pressed to its ultimate conclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shelishi Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a broad statement from Rav Assi in the name of Rav \u8212- with an alternative tradition attributing it to Rabba bar bar Assi in the name of Rav \u8212- establishing that the following {\i Tannaim} all share a single foundational position: Rabbi Meir, Rabbi Yose, Rabbi Yehoshua, Rabbi Eliezer, and Rabbi Elazar \u8212- all of them hold {\i de-ein sheni oseh shlishi be-chulin}, that a {\i sheini} does not make a {\i shlishi} in ordinary {\i chulin}. The category of {\i shlishi} is limited to {\i terumah}; ordinary {\i chulin} does not achieve third-degree {\i tumah} status. This point is worth pausing on, because the {\i limudim} of the preceding discussion \u8212- the {\i tvul yom} and {\i mechussar kippurim} derivations \u8212- were in each case explicitly about {\i terumah}. The {\i tvul yom} who may not eat {\i terumah}, the {\i mechussar kippurim} who may not eat {\i kodashim} \u8212- both derivations operated within the world of {\i terumah} and {\i kodashim}, never extending the rule of {\i shlishi} to {\i chulin}. Nevertheless, Rashi on the {\i Mishnah} had noted that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's derivation from the {\i pasuk} of {\i kol asher yipol me-hem el tocho yitma} \u8212- {\i yitma le-tamei acherim}, that the loaf in the {\i keli cheres} is {\i tamei} to make other things into a {\i shlishi} \u8212- contains nothing in its text specifically limiting it to {\i terumah}. It sounds as though Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i pasuk}-based derivation would extend {\i shlishi} even to {\i chulin}. The Gemara now reinforces that the consensus of {\i Tannaim} restricts {\i shlishi} to {\i terumah}, and proceeds to demonstrate this from each authority\u8217's rulings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir is sourced from a {\i Mishnah}: {\i kol ha-ta\u8217'un bi\u8217'at mayim mi-divrei soferim} \u8212- anything that requires immersion by rabbinic decree. The paradigmatic case of rabbinic {\i tumah} requiring immersion is human hands. Hands were assigned a default status of {\i sheini le-tumah} by rabbinic enactment, because their condition is uncertain \u8212- one does not know what they have touched. This is a {\i tumah} that exists only {\i de-rabbanan}; {\i de-oraita} it is impossible for a part of the body to be {\i tamei} while the rest is not, but rabbinically they assigned this status. The consequence is that one washes hands before eating {\i terumah} since hands in this {\i sheini} status cannot handle {\i terumah} \u8212- and this practice eventually extended to washing before eating bread even for non-{\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule the {\i Mishnah} articulates is: {\i kol ha-tamei mi-divrei soferim metamei et ha-kodesh u-fosel et ha-terumah}. Anyone who is {\i tamei} by rabbinic decree \u8212- thus effectively a {\i sheini} \u8212- {\i metamei et ha-kodesh}, renders {\i kodesh tamei}, and {\i fosel et ha-terumah}, invalidates {\i terumah}. The distinction between {\i metamei} and {\i fosel} is crucial: {\i metamei} means the {\i tumah} can be transmitted one further level, whereas {\i fosel} means it invalidates but does not transmit further. So if such a person touches {\i kodesh}, they make it a {\i shlishi}, and that {\i shlishi} can render other {\i kodesh} into a {\i revii} \u8212- since {\i kodesh} goes up to {\i revii}. But if they touch {\i terumah}, it is merely {\i pasul} without passing on further. And the continuation: {\i u-mutar be-chulin u-ve-ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- this person is permitted to handle {\i chulin} and {\i ma\u8217'aser}; {\i chulin} is entirely unaffected. This is explicit: a {\i sheini} does not produce a {\i shlishi} in {\i chulin}. These are the words of Rabbi Meir. The {\i Chachamim} add {\i ma\u8217'aser} to the list of things that are affected \u8212- the debate about {\i ma\u8217'aser} concerns whether {\i ma\u8217'aser} is treated for these purposes like {\i kodesh} \u8212- but both Rabbi Meir and the {\i Chachamim} agree unambiguously that {\i sheini} does not make a {\i shlishi} in {\i chulin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose\u8217's position is sourced from the very derivation discussed earlier in this {\i sugya} \u8212- {\i ha d\u8217'amaran}, the entire preceding analysis. The evidence that Rabbi Yose does not hold {\i sheini} makes {\i shlishi} in {\i chulin} comes not from what he said but from what he did not say. Had Rabbi Yose held that {\i chulin} could become a {\i shlishi}, his derivation would have needed to go further: {\i de-im ita, leisei le-revii bi-terumah va-chamishi be-kodesh}. If {\i chulin} itself could be a {\i shlishi}, then {\i terumah} should have been able to reach a {\i revii}, and {\i kodesh} should have been able to reach a {\i chamishi}, a fifth degree. The logic flows from the same {\i tvul yom} analogy: a {\i tvul yom} who is {\i mutar be-chulin} renders {\i terumah tamei}, so {\i chulin tamei} as a {\i sheini} should render {\i terumah} into a {\i shlishi}. But if {\i chulin} could be a {\i shlishi}, then by the same logic {\i chulin tamei} as a {\i shlishi} should render {\i terumah} into a {\i revii}, and then {\i terumah} as a {\i revii} should render {\i kodesh} into a {\i chamishi}. The fact that Rabbi Yose\u8217's derivation stopped at {\i revii} for {\i kodashim} demonstrates that he holds {\i chulin} reaches only to {\i sheini}, and it is {\i terumah} that reaches {\i shlishi} and {\i kodesh} that reaches {\i revii}. The hierarchy is clean: the greater the sanctity of the object, the more susceptible it is to progressively attenuated {\i tumah}. As one of the participants observed, this is structurally analogous to the principle that {\i tzaddikim ha-kadosh baruch hu medakdek aleihem ke-chut ha-sa\u8217'arah} \u8212- those who are holier attract even the most residual forces of {\i tumah}; or conversely, the forces of {\i tumah} can adhere even to the most attenuated {\i tumah} precisely when it encounters the most sacred objects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's position emerges from a {\i Mishnah}: Rabbi Eliezer says that if one eats food that is {\i rishon} one becomes a {\i rishon}; if one eats food that is {\i sheini} one becomes a {\i sheini}; if one eats food that is {\i shlishi} one becomes a {\i shlishi}. The principle is, in effect, that you are what you eat \u8212- eating {\i tamei} food assigns you the same {\i tumah} status as that food. Tosafot notes that this operates on two levels of rabbinic enactment: the first level holds that if you eat something like a {\i sheini}, you cannot then eat {\i terumah}, since people would perceive the {\i terumah} as going into a stomach that contains {\i sheini} and being rendered {\i tamei} there. The second level goes further \u8212- after eating it, you are treated as being at that level even for purposes of what you touch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua disagrees and says: one who eats {\i rishon} or {\i sheini} becomes a {\i sheini}; one who eats {\i shlishi} becomes a {\i sheini} only for {\i kodesh} \u8212- {\i sheni be-kodesh ve-ein sheni bi-terumah} \u8212- meaning it would affect {\i kodesh} but not {\i terumah}. What kind of food that was a {\i shlishi} could one have eaten? The {\i Mishnah} specifies: {\i be-chulin she-naasu al taharas ha-terumah} \u8212- {\i chulin} that was treated with the same care and stringency as {\i terumah}. This leads to the next subsection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chulin Al Taharas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a {\i kohen} has {\i terumah} in the house continuously and wishes to maintain a single uniform standard of purity, he may treat his {\i chulin} as if it were {\i terumah} \u8212- handling it with the same care and scrutiny. This practice is called {\i chulin she-naasu al taharas ha-terumah}. The halachic consequence is that the {\i chulin} that is handled with this {\i terumah}-level care acquires \u8212- by rabbinic enactment \u8212- the same {\i tumah} susceptibility as {\i terumah}. If a {\i sheini} touches standard {\i chulin}, nothing happens. But if a {\i sheini} touches {\i chulin} treated {\i al taharas ha-terumah}, that {\i chulin} becomes a {\i shlishi}. The Gemara draws out the implication: {\i al taharas ha-terumah in}, yes \u8212- only if it was treated with the care of {\i terumah} does it achieve {\i shlishi} status \u8212- {\i al taharas ha-kodesh lo}, not if it was merely treated with the care of {\i kodesh}. Rashi\u8217's text simply reads {\i chulin lo} \u8212- that standard {\i chulin} simply does not become a {\i shlishi}. The conclusion is the same either way: {\i alma kasavar ein sheni oseh shlishi be-chulin} \u8212- it is clear that Rabbi Yehoshua holds a {\i sheini} does not make a {\i shlishi} in standard {\i chulin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar\u8217's position comes from a {\i Braitah}: {\i shloshtan shavin} \u8212- the following three are equivalent in one respect. Whether one has a {\i rishon} in the world of {\i kodesh}, of {\i chulin}, or of {\i terumah}, it is {\i metamei shnayim u-fosel echad be-kodesh} \u8212- it transmits {\i tumah} two levels down and invalidates one more, meaning it reaches all the way to {\i revii} in {\i kodesh} and there stops. Then: {\i metamei echad u-fosel echad bi-terumah} \u8212- in the world of {\i terumah}, the {\i rishon} goes down two levels, making a {\i sheini} and then a {\i shlishi}, and stops. And {\i u-fosel echad ba-chulin} \u8212- in the world of {\i chulin}, the {\i rishon} goes down only one level to a {\i sheini} and stops. This is a beautifully clear formulation: {\i chulin} ends at {\i sheini}, {\i terumah} ends at {\i shlishi}, and {\i kodesh} ends at {\i revii}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shelishi Limits (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position is derived from a {\i Mishnah} concerning {\i challah}: {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer, challah nitelet min ha-tehorah al ha-teme\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i challah} may be taken from {\i tahor} dough on behalf of {\i tamei} dough. The background is as follows: if one\u8217's dough became {\i tamei}, the {\i challah} separated from it becomes {\i terumah teme\u8217'ah}, which the {\i kohen} cannot eat and can only burn. Rabbi Eliezer rules that if the dough was made {\i tamei} deliberately \u8212- {\i be-meizid} \u8212- one is required to separate {\i tahor challah} from {\i tahor} dough on behalf of the {\i tamei} dough as well, so that the {\i kohen} receives a {\i tahor challah}. The practical challenge is how to accomplish this while keeping the doughs connected for purposes of the separation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Challah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's solution is to interpose a small piece of {\i tahor} dough between the {\i tahor} and {\i tamei} portions. The quantity of this interposing piece is critical: it must be {\i pachos mi-kebeitzah}, less than a {\i kebeitzah} in volume. This is because food that is less than a {\i kebeitzah} can become {\i tamei} itself but does not transmit {\i tumah} further. The piece in the middle will be rendered {\i tamei} by contact with the {\i tamei} dough, but since it is less than a {\i kebeitzah}, it will not pass the {\i tumah} on to the {\i tahor} dough on the other side. The three portions \u8212- {\i tamei} dough, small interposing piece, {\i tahor} dough \u8212- are thereby treated as one connected mass, and {\i challah} can be taken from the {\i tahor} portion {\i mi-n ha-mukaf}, as if taken from a connected whole. The {\i Chachamim} forbid this arrangement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Challah Dispute (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kebeitzah Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i Braitah} reports that Rabbi Eliezer permitted this even with a {\i kebeitzah}-sized interposing piece, not only with less than a {\i kebeitzah}. This version of the ruling is more striking, because a {\i kebeitzah} of food does transmit {\i tumah}. How then could Rabbi Eliezer permit using a {\i kebeitzah} of {\i tahor} dough in the middle without concern that it will become a {\i shlishi} and transmit {\i tumah} to the {\i tahor} dough on the far side? The configuration would be: {\i tamei} dough \u8212- a {\i rishon} \u8212- touching the interposing piece making it a {\i sheini}, which then contacts the far {\i tahor} dough making it potentially a {\i shlishi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara initially assumes that the dispute between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i Chachamim} is precisely about whether {\i sheini} makes {\i shlishi} in {\i chulin}: {\i mai lav be-ha kamifligi} \u8212- is this not the crux of their disagreement? The {\i Tamei} dough is a {\i rishon}, the interposing dough becomes a {\i sheini}, and the question is whether the far {\i tahor} dough becomes a {\i shlishi}. Rabbi Eliezer holds {\i ein sheini oseh shlishi be-chulin} \u8212- no, it stops at {\i sheini} \u8212- so the scheme works. The {\i Chachamim} hold {\i sheini oseh shlishi be-chulin} \u8212- yes, the {\i tumah} would reach the far dough \u8212- so they forbid it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An additional assumption underlying this reading is {\i chulin ha-tevulim le-challah lo ke-challah damu} \u8212- dough from which {\i challah} has not yet been separated is treated as ordinary {\i chulin}, not as {\i challah}/{\i terumah}, even though it contains actualized potential {\i challah} within it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Mari Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mari son of Rav Kahana resolves the apparent debate differently. Since we have already shown from so many {\i Tannaim} that all agree {\i ein sheini oseh shlishi be-chulin}, it cannot be that this is the point of contention between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i Chachamim}. Instead, {\i ve-hacha be-chulin ha-tevulim le-challah kamifligi} \u8212- what they actually debate is whether {\i chulin} from which {\i challah} has not yet been separated is treated as {\i challah} ({\i terumah}) in its {\i tumah} susceptibility, or as plain {\i chulin}. The {\i Chachamim} hold {\i ke-challah damu} \u8212- it is like {\i challah}, and therefore the interposing dough can become a {\i shlishi}, making the scheme impermissible. Rabbi Eliezer holds {\i lo ke-challah damu} \u8212- it remains {\i chulin}, cannot become a {\i shlishi}, and the scheme is therefore permissible. Alternatively, the Gemara offers a second resolution: {\i de-chulei alma chulin ha-tevulim le-challah lo ke-challah damu ve-ein sheini oseh shlishi be-chulin} \u8212- everyone agrees on both of these points, and the dispute is actually about {\i ha-mutar ligrom tumah le-chulin she-be-chutz la-aretz} \u8212- whether one is permitted to cause {\i tumah} to spread to {\i chulin} outside of the Land of Israel. Rabbi Eliezer permits deliberately spreading {\i tumah} to the interposing {\i chulin} dough for the constructive purpose of enabling {\i challah tehorah} to be separated on behalf of everything combined. The {\i Chachamim} hold this is forbidden: {\i assur ligrom tumah le-chulin she-be-chutz la-aretz}. The upshot in any case is that virtually every {\i Tanna} agrees that {\i shlishi} is limited to {\i terumah}; the consensus is overwhelming, even if Rashi\u8217's observation about Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i pasuk}-based derivation raises a lingering question about whether the {\i Mishnah} itself implies a broader reach.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Techumin Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levitic Cities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions to a different {\i drashah} of Rabbi Akiva: {\i bo bayom darash Rabbi Akiva}. The {\i pesukim} about the {\i arei ha-Leviyim} \u8212- the Levitic cities \u8212- contain an apparent contradiction. One {\i pasuk} speaks of a thousand {\i amah} around the cities and another speaks of two thousand. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's resolution is that a thousand {\i amah} represents one measure \u8212- the immediate surrounds, fields or yards \u8212- while two thousand {\i amah} represents the city limit proper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The halachic significance of establishing a two-thousand {\i amah} city limit is its relevance to {\i Shabbat} boundaries. If Rabbi Akiva is deriving a two-thousand {\i amah} measure from these {\i pesukim}, it is presumably because this establishes {\i techumin} \u8212- the {\i Shabbat} boundary \u8212- as a {\i de-oraita} concept, derived from these very verses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara frames the underlying debate as follows: {\i mar savar techumin de-oraita u-mar savar de-rabbanan} \u8212- one authority holds that {\i techumin} is a biblical prohibition and the other holds it is rabbinic. If Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i drashah} establishes a two-thousand {\i amah} city limit for the {\i Leviyim}, the point of the derivation \u8212- what {\i nafka mina} does it serve \u8212- is presumably to establish that the prohibition of leaving the {\i Shabbat} boundary is {\i de-oraita}. The other {\i Tanna} who does not accept this reading of the {\i pesukim} would instead understand the two thousand {\i amah} as describing where the {\i Leviyim}\u8217's fields and vineyards lay, not as establishing a halachic limit for {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Nuance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a nuance: the dichotomy need not be absolute. Perhaps even one who reads the {\i pasuk} as describing the {\i Leviyim}\u8217's agricultural land could still derive {\i techumin de-oraita} from a different {\i pasuk}. Conversely, perhaps Rabbi Akiva could acknowledge that the two-thousand {\i amah} figure also describes the {\i Leviyim}\u8217's land without that undermining the {\i Shabbat} application. However, at minimum, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's derivation is most coherent if he is drawing a {\i de-oraita} norm about {\i Shabbat} boundaries, since for him the verse\u8217's import cannot simply be the agricultural distribution to the {\i Leviyim}. One might also note that there is another potential application of knowing the city limit of the {\i arei ha-Leviyim} \u8212- namely, establishing when one is considered to be within the city for purposes of {\i arei miklat}, cities of refuge, which function as Levitic cities. Yet the Gemara specifically frames the debate around {\i techumin de-oraita} versus {\i de-rabbanan}, and this is the most naturally compelling reason why Rabbi Akiva would need to derive a precise two-thousand {\i amah} measure from these {\i pesukim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Song at Sea\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Song Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i drashah} of Rabbi Akiva concerns the {\i Shirah} at the sea \u8212- {\i Az Yashir Moshe}. The {\i Braitah} introduces the scene: when Israel ascended from the sea, {\i natnu eineihem lomar shirah} \u8212- they set their eyes upon saying {\i shirah}. The question is: how did they say it? The word {\i lemor} in the verse \u8212- {\i vayomru lemor Ashira la-Shem} \u8212- suggests a responsive mode, but what exactly does this mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Responsive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is that {\i lemor} refers to responsive singing analogous to the way an adult {\i makri} reads {\i Hallel} for a congregation when that congregation is largely unlettered. The adult would chant each section, and the congregation \u8212- not knowing the full text \u8212- would respond with the opening words of the first verse: {\i Ashira la-Shem}. So Moshe would say {\i Ashira la-Shem ki ga\u8217'oh ga\u8217'ah}, and they would respond {\i Ashira la-Shem}; Moshe would continue, and they would again respond {\i Ashira la-Shem} \u8212- a recurring {\i refrain} that constituted their participation. This is like the practice of {\i Hodu la-Shem ki tov, Hodu la-Shem ki tov} \u8212- a responsive verse that allows full participation without requiring knowledge of the entire text. The {\i lemor} thus means: Moshe said it, and they responded with the opening line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer Repetition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer son of Rabbi Yose HaGelili holds differently. He compares it to the case where a {\i katan}, a minor, reads {\i Hallel} for the congregation. Since a minor cannot discharge the obligation of adults, the congregation cannot simply respond {\i Amen} and be {\i yotzei}; they must repeat every word themselves. So here too, {\i lemor} means that for every word Moshe said, all of Israel repeated that word \u8212- complete word-for-word repetition, which also explains how they knew the text: they simply repeated after Moshe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Unison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} interpret the verse differently. {\i Vayomru} \u8212- they all said it \u8212- indicates that everyone recited the entire {\i Shirah} simultaneously, in unison. {\i Lemor} means that Moshe opened first \u8212- {\i d\u8217'patach Moshe be-reisha} \u8212- and then everyone said it together with him. This is analogous to the way {\i Shema} was recited in the {\i beit ha-knesset}, where the {\i sofer} or {\i sheliach tzibbur} would open and then everyone would join in unison, rather than responding responsively or repeating word by word. But this position raises the obvious question: how did all of Israel know the words if Moshe composed the song on the spot?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yose HaGelili Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose HaGelili addresses this question directly in a {\i drashah}: {\i b\u8217'sha\u8217'ah she-alu Yisrael min ha-yam, natnu eineihem lomar shira} \u8212- when they came up from the sea, they wanted to say {\i shira}. How did they all know to say exactly what they said? His answer: {\i ol mutal al birkei imo} \u8212- a toddler sitting on his mother\u8217's lap \u8212- and {\i tinok yonek mi-shadei imo} \u8212- an infant nursing from its mother\u8217's breast. When the {\i Shechina} revealed itself at {\i Yam Suf}, the toddler lifted his neck and the infant released the breast from his mouth, and they said: {\i Zeh Eli ve-anvehu}. The miracle is that even infants were imbued with prophecy and song at that moment. The people did not need to learn the words from Moshe; they were supernaturally inspired to sing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Infants Midrash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} cited as the proof text is {\i mi-pi olelim ve-yonkim yisadta oz} \u8212- from the mouths of toddlers and infants You established strength \u8212- where {\i oz} echoes {\i Ozzi ve-zimrat Yah}, the opening of the {\i Shirah} itself, suggesting that the {\i oz}, the strength of song, came even from the youngest. The image is deliberately vivid and even graphic: the infant releases the nipple from his mouth to sing. The Gemara records that this text was once cited at a {\i brit}, which is perhaps not surprising.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Enhancement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir goes further still: {\i minayin she-afilu ubarim she-be-me\u8217'ei imman amru shira} \u8212- how do we know that even the fetuses in their mothers\u8217' wombs said {\i shira}? The proof text is {\i be-makhelot barechu Elohim, Hashem, mi-mekor Yisrael} \u8212- in the assemblies bless God, from the {\i mekor} of Israel \u8212- where {\i mekor} is the rabbinic anatomical term for the womb, the source that nurtures the child. The objection is immediately raised: {\i va-halo lo chazu} \u8212- the fetuses could not see anything. Rabbi Tanchum resolves this: {\i keres na\u8217'asa lahem ke-aspaklarya ha-me\u8217'irah} \u8212- the womb became for them like a luminous transparent glass, and they could see through it and behold the divine presence. What the infants perceived through sight, the fetuses perceived through a miraculous transparency of the womb itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Job Love vs Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} concludes with a discussion of {\i Iyov} and the question of whether his service of God was {\i me-ahavah} or {\i mi-yirah} \u8212- from love or from fear. The {\i Mishnah} had cited the verse {\i hen yikteleni, lo ayachel} \u8212- even if He kills me, I will still hope in Him \u8212- as evidence that Iyov served from love. However, as the {\i Mishnah} noted, {\i adayin ha-davar shakul} \u8212- the matter is still uncertain \u8212- because the word {\i lo} could be written with a {\i lammed alef} meaning \u8220"not\u8221" \u8212- I will not have faith \u8212- or with a {\i lammed vav} meaning \u8220"for Him\u8221" \u8212- I will maintain my faith in Him. The Gemara asks the obvious question: let us simply look at how the word is written. It is in fact written with a {\i lammed alef}. Does that not settle it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written Form Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges the reliability of reading the written form as definitive by bringing another {\i pasuk}: {\i be-chol tzaratam lo tzar} \u8212- in all their afflictions, He is not afflicted \u8212- where {\i lo} is also written with a {\i lammed alef}, yet the meaning, given the continuation of the verse ({\i u-malach panav hoshi\u8217'am}, and His angel saved them), must be {\i lo} with a {\i vav} \u8212- meaning God is afflicted by their suffering. So the written {\i lammed alef} cannot be read as determinative; it can carry either meaning. The Gemara notes that this is not a standard {\i keri u-chtiv} discrepancy; rather, a {\i lammed alef} itself can mean {\i lo} with a {\i vav}. This may reflect archaic orthographic ambiguity: just as the feminine pronoun {\i hi} was sometimes written with a {\i vav} rather than a {\i yod} in older Hebrew texts, the {\i alef} in {\i lo} may have functioned as a vowel placeholder rather than a semantic indicator of negation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir resolves the question differently, without relying on the written form of the word at all. He notes that {\i Iyov} is described as {\i yere Elokim}, fearing God \u8212- and so is {\i Avraham}, of whom the {\i pasuk} says {\i ki ata yadati ki yere Elokim atah} \u8212- now I know that you fear God \u8212- after the {\i Akeidah}. Since we know from another {\i pasuk} that Avraham is called {\i ohavi} \u8212- My beloved, the one who loves Me \u8212- the description of Avraham as {\i yere Elokim} did not preclude but rather included {\i ahavah}. By {\i gezeirah shavah} of the common phrase {\i yere Elokim}, the same is true of Iyov: {\i yirah} in Iyov\u8217's case also emerged from a foundation of {\i ahavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reward Differential\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar articulates the practical difference in reward between those who serve {\i mi-yirah} and those who serve {\i me-ahavah}. One who serves from fear \u8212- {\i ze taluy le-elef dor} \u8212- the reward extends for a thousand generations. One who serves from love \u8212- {\i le-alpayim dor} \u8212- two thousand generations. The Gemara supports this from two {\i pesukim}. In one, {\i le-alafim le-ohavai u-le-shomrei mitzvotai} \u8212- thousands of generations for those who love Me and keep My commandments \u8212- {\i alafim} (thousands) is juxtaposed to {\i ohavai} (those who love Me). In the other, {\i le-ohavav u-le-shomrei mitzvotav le-elef dor} \u8212- for those who love Him and keep His commandments, a thousand generations \u8212- {\i elef} (one thousand) is juxtaposed to {\i shomrei mitzvotav} (those who keep His commandments). The distinction turns on what each word is adjacent to: love is paired with thousands, observance without the same quality of love is paired with one thousand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Students\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} concludes with an aggadic coda. Two students sat before Rava. The first said: I was told in my dream \u8212- {\i ma rav tuvcha asher tzafanta li-rei\u8217'echa} \u8212- how great is Your good that you have stored away for those who fear You. The second said: I was told in my dream \u8212- {\i ve-yismechu vecha kol chosei vach, le-olam yeranenu ve-ya\u8217'altzu vach ohavei shemecha} \u8212- and all who take shelter in You will rejoice; forever those who love Your name will sing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Judgment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava said to both of them: {\i travaychu rabbanan tzadikei gemurei atun} \u8212- you are both complete {\i tzaddikim}. But: {\i mar me-ahavah u-mar mi-yirah} \u8212- one serves from love, and one serves from fear. The first student\u8217's dream cited a verse about {\i rei\u8217'echa}, those who fear; the second student\u8217's dream cited a verse about {\i ohavei shemecha}, those who love Your name. Each man\u8217's dream reflected his own spiritual mode of service \u8212- and Rava affirmed that both paths lead to the status of {\i tzaddik gamur}, a fully righteous person, even as they are not identical in their nature or their reward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach ha-Shole\u8217'ach.}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 31\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Bird Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins two-thirds of the way down the page, at the opening of a new {\i perek}. The {\i mishnah} returns to the very first {\i mishnah} of the {\i masechta}: the case of {\i mi she-kina le-ishto ve-nistara} \u8212- a man who warned his wife not to go into seclusion and she did so anyway. The question before us is: what quality of proof is required to establish that she went into seclusion, so as to permit or compel the continuation of the {\i sotah} process?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, already stated in the opening {\i mishnah}, is stated here in its most radical form: {\i afilu shama mi-of pore\u8217'ach} \u8212- even if one merely heard the claim of a passing bird. No matter how tenuous the assertion, any claim that she went into seclusion suffices as the basis for the {\i sotah} process. This is, by any measure, a strikingly unfair standard for the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence the {\i mishnah} states is {\i yotzi ve-yiten ketubah} \u8212- he divorces her and pays the {\i ketubah}. This phrasing is initially puzzling: one might have expected it to say that he must make her drink the {\i sotah} water, not that he must pay the {\i ketubah}. Rashi explains the logic: once the {\i sotah} process is triggered, she is forbidden to him. He has two options \u8212- either she undergoes the drinking, or, if neither party wishes to pursue that path, he divorces her and pays the {\i ketubah}. If she refuses to drink and the process is abandoned, she leaves without the {\i ketubah}. The {\i mishnah} states the case from the perspective of the husband who chooses not to proceed with the drinking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehoshua: Rumors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua requires something more substantial. His formulation is {\i ad she-ye-hayu nos\u8217'ot ve-notnot bah nashim ba-matyah ba-levanah} \u8212- until the women who sit and spin by moonlight begin to gossip about her. This somewhat colorful idiom conveys the requirement that there be actual social rumor circulating about her conduct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's standard, understood correctly, is that one needs two proper witnesses that she went into seclusion. The case of gossip here is not a {\i halacha} about rendering her forbidden to her husband as a {\i sotah}; there are no witnesses to seclusion at all. Rather, the issue is with the phrase {\i tehorah hi} \u8212- she is pure \u8212- which appears in connection with the {\i sotah} water. Once her reputation has been publicly tarnished, the waters will not be effective in any case. So the option of drinking is no longer on the table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, since there are no formal witnesses to seclusion, she has not been rendered {\i teme\u8217'ah} as a {\i sotah}. The husband could theoretically remain married to her \u8212- there is merely this cloud of rumor. But if he chooses to divorce her because of this reputation, he must pay the {\i ketubah}. The result is an asymmetry in how {\i yotzi ve-yiten ketubah} functions in each case: for Rabbi Eliezer, it implies she {\i is} a {\i sotah} and may be compelled to drink; for Rabbi Yehoshua, she is not a {\i sotah}, but the husband who wishes to leave must nonetheless pay. This is, as Rashi reads the {\i mishnah}, a difficult but defensible reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One Witness After\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave/Maidservant Testim\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Assuming the standard of Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- two witnesses to seclusion \u8212- the {\i mishnah} now addresses the consequences of testimony regarding the act of adultery itself. The {\i halacha} is that once the predicate of warning and seclusion has been established, a single witness who comes and says {\i ani ra\u8217'iti she-nitme\u8217'ah} \u8212- \u8220"I saw that she became {\i teme\u8217'ah},\u8221" that is, that she committed adultery within that seclusion \u8212- is believed. The consequence is not execution, for which two {\i kosher} witnesses would be required, but rather that she may not drink the {\i sotah} water; she is considered a proven adulteress, and upon divorce she forfeits her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} extends this further: {\i afilu eved afilu shifchah harei elu ne\u8217'emanim} \u8212- even a male or female slave would be believed for this purpose. The one witness who testifies to the act of adultery need not be a {\i kosher} witness in the formal sense. The consequence of such testimony is that she loses her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jealous Relatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then lists five categories of women who are presumed to harbor enmity toward the wife: the mother-in-law ({\i chamot}), the mother-in-law\u8217's daughter, the co-wife ({\i tzarat}), the widowed sister-in-law who stands to be subject to {\i yibum} with the husband, and the husband\u8217's daughter from another wife. These women all have identifiable personal interest in the wife\u8217's downfall and their credibility is therefore diminished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the {\i mishnah} rules {\i harei elu ne\u8217'emanos} \u8212- they are believed \u8212- but with a qualification: {\i ve-lo le-fosla mi-ketubatah ella shelo tishteh}. Their testimony is sufficient to stop the {\i sotah} drinking process \u8212- we treat their claim as sufficiently credible that we will not proceed with the water \u8212- but it is {\i not} sufficient to strip the wife of her {\i ketubah}. The husband must divorce her and pay the {\i ketubah}. So she loses the option of drinking and clearing her name, but she retains her financial protection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening Mishnah (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal vaComer Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then raises the question of how we know that the {\i eid echad} is believed for the testimony of adultery at all. One might have constructed a {\i kal va-chomer} that would have led to the opposite conclusion. The reasoning would proceed: the first testimony \u8212- that she went into seclusion \u8212- is a less severe form of evidence, since even after seclusion is established she still has the option of the {\i sotah} water to clear her name. Yet even for this less consequential testimony, the Torah requires two witnesses. All the more so, then, for the second testimony \u8212- the one that actually seals her fate and makes her permanently forbidden \u8212- should we not require two witnesses?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a compelling argument. The {\i mishnah} answers by citing the verse {\i ve-eid ein bah} \u8212- there is no witness against her, implying that even one witness suffices. The {\i mishnah} reads this as: if there {\i is} even one witness against her, there is a problem; she may not drink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then runs the {\i kal va-chomer} in the opposite direction. Now that we have established from this verse that one witness suffices to seal her fate in the more consequential second testimony, we might reverse the logic: {\i kal va-chomer}, the first testimony about seclusion, which is less consequential because the {\i sotah} water remains available, should also be achievable with one witness. That would unravel the entire structure, eliminating the requirement for two witnesses to seclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To block this reversal, the {\i mishnah} cites the verse {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} \u8212- when the husband finds \u8220"a matter of nakedness\u8221" regarding her, with the word {\i davar} ({\i matter}) linked by {\i gezeirah shavah} to the verse {\i al pi shenayim eidim yakum davar} \u8212- a matter is established by two witnesses. From this parallel, the {\i mishnah} derives that the seclusion testimony requires two witnesses: {\i mah lehavalahn al pi shenayim af kan al pi shenayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gezeirah shavah} is somewhat surprising in context, since the verse {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} appears in the laws of {\i get} and not in the {\i sotah} passage itself. The {\i mishnah} reads it as referring to the husband\u8217's suspicion, which is precisely the situation of seclusion. The {\i Gemara}, however, will read both the {\i derashot} and this {\i pasuk} somewhat differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} interjects: why do we need this {\i gezeirah shavah} from the word {\i davar}? There is a simpler derivation. The verse {\i ve-eid ein bah} that grants believability to the single witness uses the word {\i bah} \u8212- \u8220"regarding her.\u8221" This word {\i bah} is limiting: it is specifically in regard to the act of adultery itself that a single witness is believed. For {\i kinuy} and {\i stirah} \u8212- the warning and seclusion stages \u8212- the verse offers no such license, and those require two. The {\i Gemara} concludes {\i hachi nami ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- yes, that is indeed what the {\i mishnah} means to convey: {\i bah} \u8212- regarding the act itself one witness is believed, {\i ve-lo be-kinuy ve-lo be-stirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question arises: once we know that in the {\i sotah} context one witness is believed for the act of adultery when building on the seclusion, might one extend this to suggest that one witness would always be believed to accuse a woman of adultery even without the prior structure of warning and seclusion? The answer comes from the same {\i gezeirah shavah}: {\i ne\u8217'emar kan davar ve-ne\u8217'emar lehavalahn davar} \u8212- the word {\i davar} appears here and in the verse about two witnesses, establishing that outside the specific {\i sotah} framework, testimony of adultery requires two. The {\i sotah} context is the exception; the general rule remains two {\i kosher} witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Default Two Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Two Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the section of the {\i mishnah} dealing with one witness against one witness, where the {\i mishnah} appears to say they neutralize one another and the drinking proceeds. Before engaging with that debate, the {\i Gemara} establishes the foundational principle: {\i minani mili} \u8212- from where do we know that one witness is believed for the act of adultery in the {\i sotah} case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: {\i ve-eid ein bah bi-shnayim ha-katuv medaber} \u8212- when the verse says \u8220"there is no witness against her,\u8221" the Torah is speaking about two witnesses. That is, \u8220"no witness\u8221" means no {\i two} witnesses. The verse is understood as: there are no two witnesses against her, only one \u8212- and she was not raped \u8212- in which case she is forbidden and the drinking is halted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading may seem counterintuitive. The plain reading of the {\i mishnah} seemed to say that \u8220"no witness\u8221" permits drinking, and \u8220"one witness\u8221" prohibits it. The {\i Gemara} instead understands the verse as: no two witnesses \u8212- only one \u8212- and that one witness suffices to stop the process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eid = Two Default\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know that the unqualified word {\i eid} means two? The {\i Gemara} derives this from the verse {\i lo yakum eid echad be-ish le-chol avon} \u8212- one witness may not rise up against a person for any offense. The {\i Gemara} asks: since {\i eid} is already singular in form, why does the verse need to specify {\i echad} \u8212- one? It must be that the word {\i eid} by itself ordinarily denotes two witnesses, and the qualifier {\i echad} is needed to indicate that only one is meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields a general {\i klal}: {\i kol makom she-ne\u8217'emar eid harei kan shnayim ad she-yafrit lecha ha-katuv echad} \u8212- wherever the word {\i eid} appears, it means two witnesses, unless the text explicitly specifies {\i echad} to indicate a single one. This is a far-reaching principle that recurs throughout the {\i Gemara} in matters of testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this principle established, the {\i Gemara} can read {\i ve-eid ein bah} as referring to the absence of two witnesses: the Torah is saying there are no two witnesses against her, there is only one \u8212- and it is that one witness, acting in the context of established seclusion, who is believed to render her forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Competing Testimonies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla: Cannot Neutralize\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the foundational principle established, the {\i Gemara} turns to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement that one witness saying she became {\i teme\u8217'ah} against one witness saying she did not would neutralize one another, allowing the drinking to proceed. Rashi notes that the witness who says {\i lo nitme\u8217'ah} is not claiming to have observed the room and seen that nothing happened; rather, he is saying something more modest \u8212- \u8220"your observation is not reliable\u8221" or \u8220"I was with you and I know you could not have seen what you claim.\u8221" It is a contradiction of credibility, not an affirmative counterclaim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges the premise that such a contradiction could have any force at all. The first witness \u8212- the one who says she became {\i teme\u8217'ah} \u8212- is not an ordinary witness. He comes equipped with the prior testimony of warning and seclusion, which form a {\i raglayim la-davar}, a factual foundation for the suspicion. The Torah, given this foundation, has granted this witness extraordinary weight, treating his single testimony as authoritative. How, then, can a single witness offering a mere contradiction \u8212- without any comparable foundation \u8212- neutralize this specially-empowered testimony?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is Ulla\u8217's principle: {\i kol makom she-min ha-Torah eid echad harei kan shnayim} \u8212- wherever the Torah accords special believability to a single witness, that witness effectively counts as two. And {\i ein dvaro shel echad bi-makom shnayim} \u8212- the word of an ordinary single witness has no weight against two. If the specially-empowered witness counts as two, the ordinary contradiction counts as one, and one cannot override two. It follows that the contradiction is simply ineffective, and we would consider her {\i teme\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla therefore proposes a different {\i girsa} of the {\i mishnah}: where the {\i mishnah} reads {\i hayetah shotah} \u8212- she would drink \u8212- in the case of one against one, Ulla reads {\i lo hayetah shotah} \u8212- she would {\i not} drink. The one who says she became {\i teme\u8217'ah} wins, and the process is stopped. Rabbi Yitzchak adopts the same reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Chiya: Simultaneous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya, however, retains the original text of the {\i mishnah}: in the case of one against one, {\i hayetah shotah} \u8212- she would drink. The two testimonies neutralize one another, and the process continues. The {\i Gemara} notes that Ulla\u8217's argument \u8212- based on the specially-granted weight of the first witness \u8212- poses a genuine difficulty for Rabbi Chiya. It is not merely that Rabbi Chiya disagrees with Ulla\u8217's opinion; Ulla\u8217's position has compelling logical force, grounded in the structure of the Torah\u8217's grant of special believability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing: Simultaneous vs\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this tension by distinguishing between the timing of the testimonies. {\i Kan be-vat achas, kan be-zeh achar zeh} \u8212- in one case the witnesses arrive simultaneously, in the other they arrive sequentially. Rashi specifies that \u8220"simultaneously\u8221" means within {\i toch kedei dibbur} \u8212- the brief interval of a normal utterance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of this distinction is as follows. If both witnesses arrive at the same time, the court has not yet accorded full weight to the first witness\u8217's claim. Two people step forward together; one says she became {\i teme\u8217'ah} and the other immediately disputes this. Since the challenge is present from the outset, the testimony of the first has never been fully established, and the situation remains unresolved \u8212- she drinks. This is the case Rabbi Chiya is addressing, and it does not contradict Ulla\u8217's principle, because Ulla\u8217's principle applies once the testimony has been accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the first witness testified and the court recognized his testimony \u8212- if that testimony has been received and accorded its special weight \u8212- then a witness who arrives afterward cannot dislodge it. The first testimony, once accepted, is treated as two witnesses, and nothing that comes subsequently can override it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two-Against-One Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 1 vs 2: Neutralizes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's next case: one says she became {\i teme\u8217'ah} and two say she did not \u8212- {\i hayetah shotah}, she would drink. The two neutralize the one, and the process continues. This makes immediate sense in light of everything established: if even a single opposing witness can neutralize the one who says {\i teme\u8217'ah} (when they arrive simultaneously), two opposing witnesses can certainly do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is also why Rashi insists that {\i lo nitme\u8217'ah} does not mean the witnesses actively observed innocence; if two witnesses had positively testified that she did not commit adultery, the question would be entirely settled and she obviously would not drink. Their testimony is a contradiction of the first witness\u8217's claim, not an affirmative attestation of innocence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2 vs 1: Two Win\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final case in the {\i mishnah}: two say she became {\i teme\u8217'ah} and one says she did not \u8212- {\i lo hayetah shotah}, she would not drink. Two prevail over one and the process stops. This seems almost self-evident. One might wonder: if two witnesses testified that she committed adultery, why is she not executed? The {\i Gemara} implies they may not be {\i kosher} witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes an interesting structural point. The {\i mishnah} had just stated that one against one neutralizes (at least simultaneously). If so, the case of two against one \u8212- where the two win \u8212- does not seem to add much; by the same logic, one against one stopping the process ought to follow even more readily. And conversely, if the point of two against one stopping the process is to suggest that one against one would {\i not} stop it \u8212- that one against one would neutralize \u8212- then this aligns with Rabbi Chiya\u8217's reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presses: doesn\u8217't the case of two beating one ({\i lo hayetah shotah}) prove that one against one {\i would} let her drink \u8212- a problem for Ulla, who rules that even one against one stops the process? Rabbi Chiya responds: {\i al tameich, eima seifa} \u8212- look at the final clause of the {\i mishnah}. Two say {\i teme\u8217'ah}, one says not \u8212- she would not drink. Would you then say that one and one she {\i would} drink? That would also follow from the same logic. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's final clause cannot be read as implying that two and one is different from one and one in terms of which direction they cut. Something else must be the purpose of these final clauses, and the {\i Gemara} now explains what that is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Nechemya: Majority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Majority When One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes that the closing section of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the two-against-one cases in both directions \u8212- is not addressing the debate between Ulla and Rabbi Chiya at all. Rather, {\i kula be-pasulei edus ve-Rabbi Nechemya hi} \u8212- the entire passage concerns witnesses who are not {\i kosher} in the formal sense, and it follows the opinion of Rabbi Nechemya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records Rabbi Nechemya\u8217's principle: {\i kol makom she-he\u8217'emina Torah eid echad halach achar rov de\u8217'os} \u8212- wherever the Torah has granted believability to a single witness, you follow the majority of opinions. The reasoning is fundamental. The Torah\u8217's ordinary framework of testimony requires two {\i kosher} witnesses. The {\i sotah} case has already broken that framework by accepting a single witness, and by accepting witnesses who are not formally {\i kosher}. Once the normal rules are set aside, the remaining organizing principle is simply numerical: whichever side has more voices prevails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the normal law of testimony, two witnesses are formally equivalent to one hundred \u8212- quantity above the threshold of two is irrelevant. But when we have already departed from the formal framework, we revert to a more intuitive standard: more voices on one side carry greater weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Parity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nechemya extends this logic explicitly: {\i afilu shtei nashim be-ish echad ki-shnei anashim be-ish echad} \u8212- even two women against one man are equivalent to two men against one man. One might have thought that a single {\i kosher} male witness carries inherently greater weight than women, who are ordinarily {\i pasul} for testimony. Rabbi Nechemya rules otherwise. Since the entire framework of formal testimony has been suspended, there is no basis for preferring a {\i kosher} witness\u8217's testimony over that of a {\i pasul} witness. Two is simply more than one, and two women beat one man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable {\i chiddush}. The {\i mishnah} in its closing cases is teaching precisely this: two women can override one man, and the majority \u8212- in either direction \u8212- determines the outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male Witness Lock-In\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} introduces a qualification. The full reach of Rabbi Nechemya\u8217's principle \u8212- that women can override a man even when they arrive later \u8212- may be too broad. A secondary opinion holds: {\i kol heicha de-ata eid echad kosher me-ikara afilu me\u8217'ah nashim nami ke-eid echad damyan} \u8212- if a single {\i kosher} male witness testified first, even one hundred women who came afterward count as only a single witness. His testimony locks in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question concerns sequential testimony, and here the {\i Gemara} takes the discussion in a direction that goes beyond the earlier Ulla\u8211-Rabbi Chiya debate. Ulla and Rabbi Chiya agreed: once a witness testifies and is accepted, later testimony cannot undo it. Rabbi Nechemya\u8217's {\i rov de\u8217'os} principle seemed to challenge this: perhaps if enough people testify afterward \u8212- even women against a man \u8212- the later majority prevails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One position within the discussion of Rabbi Nechemya\u8217's view accepts this: even if the first witness was a man, the later majority can override. The second position, which the {\i Gemara} ultimately elaborates, resists this conclusion where a {\i kosher} witness testified first. In that case, his testimony acquires the status of formal {\i kosher edut}, equivalent to two proper witnesses, and that cannot be overridden by any subsequent accumulation of {\i pasul} witnesses. The {\i Gemara} therefore explains the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case: {\i ve-hacha be-mai askinan ke-gon de-asi ishah me-ikara} \u8212- the case in the {\i mishnah} where later testimony does override must be one where the first witness was herself a woman. When the first to testify is a woman, her testimony does not attain the status of formal {\i edut}, and a later majority of women can override it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strict vs Lenient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further question: why does the {\i mishnah} need to give {\i both} two-against-one cases \u8212- two saying {\i teme\u8217'ah} against one saying not, {\i and} one saying {\i teme\u8217'ah} against two saying not? If the principle is simply that the majority prevails, both cases follow automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i ma d\u8217'teima ki azlinan basar rov de\u8217'os le-chumra aval le-kula lo azlinan} \u8212- one might have thought that we follow the majority only when it produces the stringent outcome, but not the lenient one. That is, if the majority says {\i teme\u8217'ah}, we follow them because it is {\i le-chumra} to stop the process; but if the majority says she did not become {\i teme\u8217'ah}, perhaps we would not follow them {\i le-kula} to permit her to drink. The {\i mishnah} therefore needed both cases to teach that the majority principle operates symmetrically: we follow the greater number regardless of which direction the leniency or stringency cuts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's view of what constitutes {\i chumra} and {\i kula} here is interesting. He reads it as lenient to believe the witness who says she committed adultery, since the consequence \u8212- that she does not drink and goes out without her {\i ketubah} \u8212- spares her from the risk of the waters. Others might read the {\i kula} as permitting her to drink and clear her name. Either way, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's teaching is that the majority principle is not asymmetric: it applies in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the overall architecture of this {\i Gemara}: the one witness who testifies to adultery in the {\i sotah} context carries extraordinary weight, grounded in the prior testimony of warning and seclusion. Ulla holds that once accepted, this testimony is unassailable by a single contradicting voice \u8212- it is treated as two witnesses. Rabbi Chiya limits this to sequential testimony; simultaneous contradiction still neutralizes. Rabbi Nechemya introduces the further principle that when the Torah has suspended the formal rules of testimony by accepting a single witness, the remaining criterion is the majority of voices \u8212- and this majority operates across gender lines and, in some views, even overrides testimony already accepted, provided the first witness was not a {\i kosher} male witness whose testimony locks in as formal {\i edut}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 32\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Previous Perek Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the new {\i perek}, it is worth revisiting two points from the material just completed. The {\i Mishnah} presented a dispute between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua regarding the circumstances under which a husband would divorce his wife and provide a {\i kesuvah}. Rabbi Eliezer held that even a rumor as tenuous as what one might overhear from a passing bird would suffice. Rabbi Yehoshua required something more substantial \u8212- people actually witnessed the two together in circumstances suggesting impropriety.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi understood this dispute as relating to the earlier debate about what constitutes valid {\i stira} \u8212- the act of seclusion required to trigger the {\i sotah} process. That reading, however, sits awkwardly with the plain text of the {\i Mishnah}. Tosafos offers a considerably cleaner approach: the two Tannaim are actually debating something entirely different. In the cases they discuss, there is no {\i kinuy} and no {\i stira} at all \u8212- only rumors. The debate concerns what kind of rumors are sufficient to warrant advising the husband to divorce his wife. Since there is no proper halachic basis for the {\i sotah} procedure, the husband must in any event provide a {\i kesuvah}. Tosafos\u8217's reading is the more natural one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses & Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second point concerns the case of two witnesses who contradict one another \u8212- one testifying that the woman became {\i temei\u8217'ah} and the other that she did not. The question is whether they are neutralized by one another only when they testify simultaneously, or also when one comes after the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains \u8220"simultaneously\u8221" as meaning within {\i toch k\u8217'dei dibbur} \u8212- the brief interval considered legally contemporaneous with speech. Tosafos disagrees. According to Tosafos, once {\i beis din} has already heard and ruled on the first testimony, a subsequent contradicting witness carries no weight. But if both witnesses appear before {\i beis din} before any ruling is issued, they neutralize one another, regardless of whether one spoke a moment before the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elu Ne\u8217'emarim Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With those clarifications in place, we now open a new {\i perek} \u8212- {\i Elu Ne\u8217'emarim}. While it begins with a point about {\i sotah}, the {\i perek} is in fact broad and consequential, addressing the {\i halacha} of reciting texts: which texts must be recited specifically in {\i lashon hakodesh}, and which may be said in translation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bechol Lashon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah & Ma\u8217'aser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} opens: {\i elu ne\u8217'emarim bechol lashon} \u8212- the following may be recited in any language. The first two items are {\i Parshas Sotah} and {\i viduy ma\u8217'aser}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Parshas Sotah} refers to the verbal component of the {\i sotah} procedure \u8212- the priest\u8217's address to the woman, her response of {\i amen amen}, and so forth. This is striking: the text is written in Hebrew, and the document is scraped into the water in Hebrew, yet the recitation itself may be in any language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Viduy ma\u8217'aser} is the declaration made at the end of the third year, in which the person states that all accumulated {\i ma\u8217'aser} has been properly distributed and removed from his home. The {\i Torah} provides a specific text for this declaration. That both of these \u8212- cases where the {\i Torah} itself specifies a formal text \u8212- may nonetheless be recited in translation is a considerable {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shema & Tefillah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} continues its list: {\i Krias Shema}, {\i tefillah} (meaning {\i Shemoneh Esrei}), and {\i Birkas HaMazon} may all be recited in any language. One might pause and ask: for {\i tefillah} and {\i Birkas HaMazon}, the {\i Torah} provides no fixed text \u8212- it simply requires that one thank and bless God. Why would I have assumed these must be in Hebrew at all? Perhaps the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's point is that even where there is no prescribed text, one might have thought there exists an inherent sanctity to the Hebrew language that demands its use even in open-ended recitation. The {\i Mishnah} dispels this: in all these cases, any language is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvuas Ha\u8217'Eidus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} also lists {\i shvuas ha\u8217'eidus} and {\i shvuas hapikadon} as permissible in any language. {\i Shvuas ha\u8217'eidus} arises when someone adjures witnesses to testify on his behalf, and they falsely deny having seen anything relevant \u8212- they are liable to bring a {\i korban} for this oath. {\i Shvuas hapikadon} arises when someone denies having received an object for safekeeping, swears falsely to that effect, and later admits the truth \u8212- similarly requiring a {\i korban}. Both may be administered and taken in any language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Shomeia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos makes an important observation about the phrase {\i elu ne\u8217'emarim bilshonam} \u8212- they are recited in {\i their} language. He explains: {\i u\u8217'peirush kol adam bilshono she\u8217'hu shomeia} \u8212- the meaning is that each person recites in a language he actually understands. The example he gives is telling: one cannot fulfill the obligation by reciting in a foreign language he does not comprehend. Interestingly, however, Tosafos concedes that Hebrew \u8212- even if not understood \u8212- would still be valid, which opens a broader question about {\i kavana} and the degree of comprehension required for each individual area of {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos then raises a tension: the word {\i bechol lashon} in the {\i Mishnah} seems to imply any language whatsoever, without any requirement of understanding \u8212- {\i bein shomeia bein eino shomeia}. And yet, as the {\i Gemara} will show regarding {\i Shema}, understanding does seem to matter. Tosafos thus flags the question of whether one can recite a translated text in a language he does not understand and still fulfill his obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Other Items\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos raises a further question: the {\i Mishnah} lists certain items that may be said in any language, but omits a great many others \u8212- {\i Hallel}, {\i Shir HaShirim}, {\i Kiddush} of Shabbos, {\i brachos} before food, {\i birkas hamitzvos}, and so forth. Why are these not listed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might suggest the {\i Mishnah} limits itself to {\i de-Oraisa} obligations, but Tosafos dismisses this: {\i tefillah} is most likely {\i de-Rabbanan}, {\i Krias Shema} is {\i de-Rabbanan} according to some views, while {\i Kiddush} is {\i de-Oraisa}. The categories do not align neatly enough to support such a distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: No Shomeia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos resolves this by drawing a distinction between two types of items. The items the {\i Mishnah} lists \u8212- {\i Parshas Sotah}, {\i viduy ma\u8217'aser}, {\i Krias Shema} \u8212- may be said in any language, but only if the speaker understands what he is saying. The items Tosafos mentions as omitted \u8212- {\i brachos} before food, {\i birkas hamitzvos}, and the like \u8212- may be said in any language even without understanding. Because those items operate differently, they are not comparable to the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's list and were therefore not included. This distinction is admittedly difficult to explain on principled grounds, but Tosafos acknowledges it clearly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bilshon Hakodesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hebrew Texts List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then turns to texts that must be recited specifically in {\i lashon hakodesh}: {\i Mikra Bikkurim} (the declaration of {\i aram oveid avi} when bringing first fruits), {\i chalitza} (including the woman\u8217's declaration of {\i kacha ye\u8217'aseh la\u8217'ish} and the accompanying shoe removal and spitting), {\i brachos} and {\i klalos} on {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival}, {\i Birkas Kohanim}, the blessing of the {\i Kohen Gadol} on {\i Yom Kippur} when he reads from the {\i Sefer Torah}, {\i Parshas Hamelech} at {\i Hakhel}, and {\i Parshas Eglah Arufah} \u8212- the declaration of the elders: {\i yadeinu lo shafchu es hadam hazeh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One item that stands out as particularly surprising is the address of the {\i mashuach milchama} \u8212- the {\i kohen} anointed for war \u8212- when he speaks to the troops. He informs them: whoever has built a new house, planted a vineyard, or betrothed a wife may return home. This is not a ritual recitation in any conventional sense; it is a practical communication designed specifically to be understood. If there is any context in which we would expect translation to be permitted \u8212- indeed required \u8212- it would be this one. And yet this too must be in {\i lashon hakodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted: just because the recitation must be in Hebrew does not necessarily mean the {\i chalitza} itself is invalid if the text is not recited in Hebrew. That is a separate question treated elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for {\i brachos} and {\i klalos} on {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival}: this is not an ongoing {\i halachic} obligation but a historical event. The reason it is mentioned in the {\i Mishnah} is that it serves as the exegetical basis \u8212- the {\i gehezera shava} anchor \u8212- for the other items requiring {\i lashon hakodesh}, as the {\i Gemara} will now explain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikra Bikkurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know that {\i Mikra Bikkurim} must be in {\i lashon hakodesh}? The {\i Gemara} points to the juxtaposition of two phrases. By {\i bikkurim} the {\i Torah} says {\i ve\u8217'anita ve\u8217'amarta lifnei Hashem elokecha} \u8212- you shall respond and say before God. By the {\i brachos} and {\i klalos} on the mountains, it says {\i ve\u8217'anu haLevi\u8217'im ve\u8217'amru} \u8212- the Levites shall respond and say. The combination of the verbs \u8220"respond\u8221" and \u8220"say\u8221" appears in both contexts. Just as the {\i brachos} and {\i klalos} on {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival} were in {\i lashon hakodesh}, so too {\i Mikra Bikkurim} must be in {\i lashon hakodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same {\i gezera shava} applies to {\i chalitza}. By {\i chalitza}, the {\i Torah} writes {\i ve\u8217'aneta ve\u8217'amra} \u8212- she shall respond and say. By the Levites on the mountains, it says {\i ve\u8217'anu haLevi\u8217'im ve\u8217'amru}. The same dual-verb construction creates the link: just as the mountains required {\i lashon hakodesh}, so does {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees. He holds that by {\i chalitza} there is no need to derive the Hebrew requirement from the mountains via this {\i gezera shava}. The {\i Torah} itself says {\i ve\u8217'amra kacha} \u8212- she shall say \u8220"thus,\u8221" meaning she must say it in precisely this manner \u8212- {\i ad she\u8217'tomar balashon hazeh}, until she says it in this very language. The word {\i kacha} itself implies the exact wording, which is inherently the Hebrew text as given.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Har Gerizim & Eival\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geography\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the {\i brachos} and {\i klalos} on {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival} serve as the foundation for the other {\i lashon hakodesh} requirements, the {\i Mishnah} now describes that ceremony in detail. It begins with the geography.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the Jewish people crossed the Jordan, they came to {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival}, which are in {\i Shomron}, alongside {\i Shechem}, near {\i elonei Moreh}. The {\i Torah} provides these geographic markers, and the {\i Gemara} explains how we know that {\i elonei Moreh} refers to {\i Shechem}: the {\i Torah} elsewhere says that Avram passed through the land {\i ad mekom Shechem ad elon Moreh}. Just as {\i elon Moreh} in that earlier verse refers to {\i Shechem}, so the {\i elon Moreh} mentioned in connection with {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival} likewise refers to {\i Shechem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arrangement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Six tribes ascended {\i Har Gerizim} and six ascended {\i Har Eival} \u8212- those on {\i Har Gerizim} for the {\i bracha}, those on {\i Har Eival} for the {\i kelala}. It is worth noting that even today {\i Har Gerizim} is covered with vegetation while {\i Har Eival} is entirely barren \u8212- a striking natural correspondence to the roles each mountain was assigned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kohanim}, {\i Levi\u8217'im}, and the {\i aron} stood below in the middle, between the two mountains. The {\i kohanim} encircled the {\i aron} as an inner protective ring; the {\i Levi\u8217'im} encircled the {\i kohanim}. All the remaining Israelites stood on both sides \u8212- six tribes on each mountain. This is supported by the verse in {\i Yehoshua}: {\i vechol Yisrael uzekanav veshotrav veshofetav omdim mizeh umizeh la\u8217'aron}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brachos & Klalos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then describes the recitation. They turned toward {\i Har Gerizim} and opened with the {\i bracha}: {\i baruch haish asher lo yaaseh pesel umasecha} \u8212- blessed is the man who does not make a graven image. Both groups \u8212- those on {\i Har Gerizim} and those on {\i Har Eival} \u8212- responded {\i amen}. This is notable: despite the two mountains representing two opposing paths, the {\i amen} response came from all of Israel together. Then they turned toward {\i Har Eival} and recited the corresponding {\i klala}: {\i arur haish asher yaaseh pesel umasecha} \u8212- and again, both groups responded {\i amen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sequence alternated \u8212- {\i bracha}, {\i klala}, {\i bracha}, {\i klala} \u8212- until all the {\i brachos} and {\i klalos} were completed. This interleaving is natural and deliberate: the two choices are set before the people together, blessing and curse side by side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 70 Languages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After completing the {\i brachos} and {\i klalos}, they brought the stones and built the {\i mizbeiach} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} understands that the inscribed stones and the altar stones were one and the same \u8212- plastered them over with lime and wrote upon them {\i kol divrei haTorah beshivim lashon}, the entire {\i Torah} in 70 languages. The source for this is the {\i Torah}\u8217's instruction to write these words {\i be\u8217'er heitev} \u8212- very clearly and well explicated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this deeply significant in context is the juxtaposition. The {\i brachos} and {\i klalos} \u8212- which must be in {\i lashon hakodesh} \u8212- serve as the basis for other things that must be in {\i lashon hakodesh}. And yet standing right alongside them, at this very same moment of national commitment, is the writing of the {\i Torah} in all 70 languages. This will become directly relevant when the {\i Gemara} later discusses the principle of {\i kol haTorah kulah bechol lashon ne\u8217'emra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the ceremony, the stones were dismantled, and the people went and spent the night in their place \u8212- Rashi identifies this as {\i Gilgal}. There is some complexity here, as {\i Yehoshua} describes twelve stones taken from the feet of the {\i kohanim} at the crossing of the {\i Yarden}, which seem to be a distinct monument erected at {\i Gilgal} to commemorate the crossing. A degree of conflation between the two accounts exists, but the {\i Mishnah} does not address it directly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezera Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: how do we know that {\i Parshas Sotah} may be recited in any language? The source is the verse {\i ve\u8217'amar hakohen laisha} \u8212- the priest shall say to the woman. Rashi cites the parallel verse {\i ve\u8217'amar el haisha} \u8212- to the woman \u8212- which implies that the recitation is about reaching her, communicating to her in a way she takes to heart. The word {\i amar} is a generic verb of communication, as opposed to the double-verb construction ({\i ve\u8217'anu ve\u8217'amru}) that signals a specific formal text. The sense of {\i el ha\u8217'isha} is {\i devarim hanichnasim belibah} \u8212- words that enter her heart \u8212- meaning any language that she understands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Understanding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} makes this explicit: {\i mashmi\u8217'in osah bechol lashon shehi shoma\u8217'as} \u8212- she is informed in any language that she understands. This connects to Tosafos\u8217's earlier point about {\i shomeia}: in translation, the requirement of understanding the language is operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Topics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraisa} then specifies the four topics that must be communicated to her: {\i al ma hi shotah uvmah hi shotah, al mah nitmes uvmah hi nitmes} \u8212- why she is drinking and in what vessel, and for what she has become {\i tamei} and by what means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These four topics are not uniform in character. {\i Al ma hi shotah} \u8212- why she is drinking \u8212- refers to the formal halachic criteria: there was {\i kinuy} and {\i stira}; this is why the procedure is taking place. {\i Uvmah hi shotah} \u8212- in what she is drinking \u8212- refers to the earthenware vessel used, a detail chosen for its demeaning quality, signaling the diminished state she has brought upon herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al mah nitmes} \u8212- for what she became {\i tamei} \u8212- is not halachic criteria but {\i mussar}: {\i al iskei schok veyaldus}, it comes from frivolity and immaturity, from conduct that opens the door to such situations. {\i Uvmah hi nitmes} \u8212- by what means \u8212- returns to halachic territory: {\i beshogeg o bemeizid, be\u8217'ones o beratzon} \u8212- was it inadvertent or deliberate, coerced or willing? This matters greatly because only the willful and uncoerced transgression is tested by the waters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason all of this is spelled out to her \u8212- at least the last point \u8212- is {\i shelo lehotzi la\u8217'az al hamayim hamarim}: so she should not emerge and claim the waters have no power, having drunk them without effect. She must understand that certain circumstances \u8212- {\i shogeg}, {\i ones} \u8212- exempt from the trial, and her survival in those cases reflects nothing about the efficacy of the waters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Praise & Faults\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai teaches: a person should speak his own praises in a low voice, and his own faults loudly. How do we know praises should be in an undertone? From {\i viduy ma\u8217'aser}: the declaration is in essence a statement of everything one did correctly \u8212- {\i asisi kechol asher tzivisani, hashkifa mimon kodshecha} \u8212- yet the {\i Torah} does not say {\i ve\u8217'anu ve\u8217'amru}, the language of proclamation; it simply says {\i ve\u8217'amarta}, you shall say. Quietly, without fanfare.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And how do we know one\u8217's pain or difficult circumstances should be proclaimed? The {\i Gemara} says: from {\i Mikra Bikkurim}. There, {\i matchil bignus} \u8212- one begins with the difficult opening, {\i arami oveid avi}, evoking the suffering of our ancestors and the precarious origins of our people \u8212- and this is recited publicly and aloud.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Viduy as Praise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} observes that the parallel drawn here is somewhat imprecise. {\i Viduy ma\u8217'aser} is straightforwardly self-praise, but {\i Mikra Bikkurim} is not exactly {\i gnus} in the sense of personal fault. It is more in the category of {\i tza\u8217'ara} \u8212- difficulty, pain, a less than triumphant chapter of one\u8217's story. The teaching therefore is refined: one should not merely hide sinful conduct; rather, one should feel free to share one\u8217's suffering openly, because doing so invites others to pray on one\u8217's behalf. This is the deeper point: {\i kol mi she\u8217'ira bo davar tzarich lehodi\u8217'a lerabbim, verabbim mevakshim alav rachamim} \u8212- whoever experiences hardship should make it known, so that others may seek mercy on his behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is illustrated by {\i vetamei tamei yikra}: the {\i metzora} sent outside the camp is required to announce his condition \u8212- {\i tzarich lehodi\u8217'a tza\u8217'aro lerabbim verabbim mevakshim alav rachamim}. The pastoral point is powerful: people who are suffering often conceal it, either out of pride or a reluctance to burden others. The {\i Torah} and {\i Chazal} take a different view \u8212- sharing one\u8217's pain opens the door for communal support and prayer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillah Undertone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, citing Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, teaches: why was {\i tefillah} established as a silent, undertone recitation? We know the model from {\i Chana} \u8212- {\i vekolah lo yishama} \u8212- but why did {\i Chazal} adopt that model? {\i Kedei shelo levayesh es ovrei aveirah} \u8212- in order not to embarrass those who have sinned. When a person stands before God in {\i tefillah}, he should feel free to speak honestly about his failures and transgressions. If {\i tefillah} were recited aloud, people would be inhibited. The intimate, private character of silent {\i tefillah} allows for genuine, unguarded honesty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation also carries with it an implicit point: {\i tefillah} is not merely the recitation of a fixed text. One is expected to add personal words within {\i Shemoneh Esrei} \u8212- to speak to God about one\u8217's own life, needs, and failures. The structure exists to enable that conversation, not to replace it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas & Olah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle \u8212- protecting the sinner from public embarrassment \u8212- is illustrated from the laws of {\i korbanos}. A {\i chatas} and an {\i olah} are both slaughtered in the northern part of the {\i azarah}. Because they share the same location, a bystander watching someone bring an animal cannot tell from the place of {\i shechita} whether that person has sinned and is bringing a {\i chatas} or has done nothing wrong and is bringing an {\i olah}. The {\i halacha} itself is structured to protect a sinner\u8217's dignity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises a challenge: is the distinction truly concealed? The application of the blood differs \u8212- {\i dam chatas lemala}, the blood of a {\i chatas} is applied to the horns at the top of the {\i mizbeiach}, while {\i dam olah lemata}, the blood of an {\i olah} is cast on the lower walls. A careful observer could discern the difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i hasam kohen hu deyada} \u8212- only the {\i kohen} knows. By the time the blood is being applied, it is the {\i kohen} performing the service, and the layperson watching from the {\i azarah} may not readily follow the technical details of blood application. Tosafos, however, raises the obvious question: are we really to assume that observers are so inattentive? The {\i Gemara} accepts the answer but the difficulty is real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Concealment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a further challenge: a {\i chatas} must be a female animal and an {\i olah} must be male. If observers can see which sex of animal a person hands to the {\i kohen}, the distinction is transparent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i chasuy mechasya be-alyah} \u8212- the fat tail of a sheep covers and conceals the genitals, so passersby cannot readily determine the animal\u8217's sex. The {\i Gemara} thus assumes that for sheep specifically, the distinction is not obvious. Tosafos again notes the somewhat strained assumption that people are carefully observing these details.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avodah Zarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presses the point: what about a {\i chatas} brought specifically for {\i avodah zarah}? That {\i korban} may only be a female goat, and a goat has no concealing fat tail. The sex of the animal is visible, and therefore the nature of the sin is apparent to anyone watching. How does the principle of concealment apply here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i hasam nichsof ve-nechsi ki heichi de-niskapar lei} \u8212- in the case of {\i avodah zarah}, a measure of additional embarrassment is not only appropriate but perhaps necessary. The severity of the sin of {\i avodah zarah} demands a more complete process of atonement and shame, and the public visibility of one\u8217's {\i korban} contributes to that process. For ordinary sins, the {\i Torah} protects the sinner\u8217's dignity; for {\i avodah zarah}, it does not \u8212- and this is entirely fitting.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shema Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shema Any Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to {\i Krias Shema}. How do we know it may be recited in any language? From the word {\i shema} \u8212- {\i be-chol lashon she-ata shomea} \u8212- \u8220"hear\u8221" means: in any manner and language in which you can understand. The {\i Gemara} is treating the first word of {\i Krias Shema} as itself a legal instruction, indicating that understanding is central and therefore any language that conveys understanding is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that this {\i Gemara} treats {\i Krias Shema} as {\i de-Oraisa}. As Tosafos discusses at length, other {\i Gemoras} seem to suggest it may be {\i de-Rabbanan}, and no fully settled position emerges from the {\i stam} of the {\i Gemara}. But the present sugya proceeds on the assumption that at least the first {\i pasuk} or first paragraph carries biblical force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi vs.\u160 ?Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} records the dispute directly. Rabbi says: {\i Krias Shema ki-ksava} \u8212- it must be read exactly as it is written, meaning in Hebrew, and cannot be recited in translation. The Sages say: it may be recited in any language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Ve\u8217'hayu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi\u8217's reason: the verse says {\i ve\u8217'hayu hadevarim ha-eileh al levavecha} \u8212- \u8220"and these words shall be upon your heart.\u8221" The word {\i ve\u8217'hayu} \u8212- they shall be \u8212- implies that the words must remain exactly as they are, in the form in which they were given. This is analogous to the principle of {\i kacha} in {\i chalitza}: the formulation demands the precise original text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Shema\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages, by contrast, emphasize {\i shema} \u8212- hear, understand. The central obligation is comprehension, and comprehension is achievable in any language. As for {\i ve\u8217'hayu} \u8212- the Sages use it to derive a different {\i halacha} entirely: {\i shelo yikra lamafreia} \u8212- one may not recite {\i Krias Shema} out of order. The words, the sentences, the paragraphs must follow their proper sequence. {\i Ve\u8217'hayu} thus governs order rather than language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where does Rabbi derive the prohibition against reciting {\i Krias Shema} out of order, given that he uses {\i ve\u8217'hayu} for the language requirement? The {\i Gemara} answers: from the doubling of the expression \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'hayu hadevarim}, rather than simply {\i ve\u8217'hayu devarim}. The additional {\i heh} signals an additional requirement. The Sages do not accept this kind of inference from the extra {\i heh} and therefore have no need to overload {\i ve\u8217'hayu} with both requirements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does Rabbi do with the word {\i shema}? {\i Lehashmia oznecha mah she-ata motzi mi-picha} \u8212- you must let your ears hear what comes out of your mouth. This is the {\i halacha} of audibility: one must recite {\i Shema} at a volume that at minimum is audible to oneself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages, on the other hand, hold in accordance with the view that {\i hakore es Shema v\u8217'lo hishmia l\u8217'ozno yatza} \u8212- one fulfills the obligation even if one does not hear oneself. For the Sages, {\i shema} means language comprehension, not audibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is that Rabbi is strict on both dimensions: Hebrew is required, and audibility is required. The Sages are lenient on both: any language is valid, and audibility is not required. One might have expected the correlations to run differently \u8212- those who require the formal Hebrew text might care less about audibility (since the focus is on the text, not comprehension), while those who permit translation might specifically demand audibility (since comprehension is the goal). Tosafos notes and explores these possibilities, but the {\i Gemara} as it stands presents Rabbi and the Sages each internally consistent on their respective positions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Bechol Lashon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a broader question flowing from the dispute between Rabbi and the Sages. Can we infer from Rabbi\u8217's position \u8212- which requires {\i Krias Shema} in Hebrew and derives this from {\i ve\u8217'hayu} \u8212- that Rabbi holds {\i kol haTorah bechol lashon ne\u8217'emra}, that the general rule is that Torah texts may be recited in any language? For if Rabbi\u8217's default were that everything must be in Hebrew, why would he need {\i ve\u8217'hayu} to establish that {\i Krias Shema} must be in Hebrew? That would simply follow from the general rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects: the verse was still necessary because of the word {\i shema}, which could have implied that even Rabbi permits any language here. {\i Ve\u8217'hayu} is therefore needed to counter that inference and confirm that even in this case, the general requirement of Hebrew applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, one might infer from the Sages\u8217' position that they hold the default is Hebrew \u8212- otherwise, why would they need the word {\i shema} to permit any language? The {\i Gemara} deflects again: {\i shema} was still necessary because {\i ve\u8217'hayu} could have been read as an exception demanding Hebrew even for those who in principle permit any language. Both proofs are thus deflected, and neither Rabbi nor the Sages can be definitively assigned to one side of the broader principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Default Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i kol haTorah kulah bechol lashon ne\u8217'emra} \u8212- Torah was given in all languages \u8212- is best understood as a question about the default rule. When the {\i Torah} requires a text to be recited, is the baseline assumption that any language will serve, or is the baseline Hebrew? The position of {\i bechol lashon ne\u8217'emra} holds that all languages are inherently valid; items requiring specifically {\i lashon hakodesh} are exceptions to that default. The opposing view holds that {\i lashon hakodesh} is the default, and explicit permission is needed to allow translation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the Tosafot Shantz explains, the practical implication is this: when the {\i Torah} provides a text for recitation and says nothing explicit about language, does the text remain inherently open to translation, or does it presumptively require Hebrew?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 70 Languages Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This entire discussion connects powerfully to what the {\i Mishnah} described earlier. At the very moment the Jewish people crossed the {\i Yarden} and committed themselves anew to the {\i Torah}, the first thing they did was write the entire {\i Torah} in 70 languages \u8212- {\i beshivim lashon}, with the instruction {\i be\u8217'er heitev}, to write it clearly and accessibly. This is an astonishing counterpoint to the {\i brachos} and {\i klalos} that must be in {\i lashon hakodesh} and that serve as the basis for other requirements of Hebrew. Standing alongside those requirements is the vision of {\i Torah} written in all the languages of the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This suggests that {\i kol haTorah kulah bechol lashon ne\u8217'emra} is not merely a legal default rule but reflects something fundamental: the {\i Torah} is inherently accessible to all peoples in all languages. Where the {\i Torah} demands {\i lashon hakodesh} specifically \u8212- as it does in the cases derived in this {\i perek} \u8212- that is an exception, requiring its own source and justification. Where no such demand is made, the {\i Torah}\u8217's native posture is openness to every language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that Rashi and Tosafos raise the question of whether this principle relates to whether a {\i Sefer Torah} itself may be written in another language \u8212- a separate and well-known debate. But that does not appear to be what the {\i Gemara} here is discussing. The present discussion concerns something more fundamental: whether Torah recitation, as a category, was always understood to allow for translation \u8212- a position grounded in the very act with which Bnei Yisrael began their life in the land.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 33\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 All Languages: Sotah, Vi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah, Viduy, Shema\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a discussion about which ritual declarations may be recited in any language. Already mentioned are {\i sotah} \u8212- not the writing of the {\i parsha} but the reciting of it \u8212- along with {\i viduy maaser} and {\i shema}. All three derive from the {\i Torah}, and the Mishnah has established that each may be performed in any language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillah & Rachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tefillah} is now addressed. The Gemara states: {\i rachamei hi} \u8212- it is supplication to God, direct communication with God \u8212- {\i vechol heicha deba\u8217'i matzlei}, and so one may pray however one needs to speak. Because {\i tefillah} is at its core a matter of personal address to the Almighty, it is most appropriate that it be expressed in whatever language allows a person to be most articulate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avot vs.\u160 ?Korbanot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant point, because the word {\i rachamei} carries a precise conceptual weight in the Gemara. It stands in contrast to the classic debate in Berakhot over whether {\i tefillah} is {\i keneged avot} or {\i keneged korbanot} \u8212- whether it corresponds structurally to the {\i korbanot}, with all their prescribed forms and regulations, or whether it corresponds to the way Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov prayed, which involved no fixed text, no fixed time, and was very much of the heart. The Gemara\u8217's conclusion in Berakhot is that at its core {\i tefillah} is {\i keneged avot}, though the structure and timing of the {\i korbanot} have been layered upon it. When the Gemara wants to invoke that core dimension \u8212- that {\i tefillah} is fundamentally personal communication \u8212- it uses the phrase {\i rachamei ninhu}. And it is precisely because of that core that {\i tefillah} may, in principle, be offered in any language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda & Aramaic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this. {\i Vetefillah bechol lashon?} Is it truly the case that {\i Shemoneh Esrei} may be said in any language? Does not Rav Yehuda say {\i leolam al yishal adam tzarachav bilshon Aramit} \u8212- one should never make a personal request in Aramaic? And does not Rabbi Yochanan add that whoever makes their request in Aramaic, {\i ein malachei hasharet nizkakim lo} \u8212- the ministering angels will not attend to them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Angels & Aramaic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason given is {\i lefi she\u8217'ein malachei hasharet makirim bilshon Aramit} \u8212- they do not recognize Aramaic. This is a striking idea: that there is something a heavenly being would fail to recognize. Some {\i Rishonim} qualify this, suggesting it applies specifically to Aramaic rather than to all non-Hebrew languages, and that the issue is not literal non-recognition but a kind of disapproval \u8212- the angels regard Aramaic as a corruption of Hebrew, being itself a Semitic language. In any event, the principle is that one should not recite {\i tefillot} in Aramaic because the angels serve as the bearers of prayer, and they will not convey what is said in that language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Individual vs.\u160 ?Communal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension: {\i lo kashya \u8212- ha beyachid ha betzibur}. The question depends on whether one is praying privately or communally. When one prays as part of a community, God\u8217's presence is found directly among the congregation, and there is no need for angelic intermediaries. When one prays privately, the angels are needed to carry the prayer upward, and in that context the Aramaic restriction applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can also understand this on a more conceptual level that extends beyond the specific issue of Aramaic. When one recites the fixed Hebrew text, even if it somewhat constrains one\u8217's ability to express oneself with full personal feeling, what is achieved is connection \u8212- to the tradition, to the weight and depth of the words themselves, and to a sense of community through a shared text. When one is already praying {\i betzibur}, that sense of communal connection and {\i mesorah} is present regardless, and it therefore becomes permissible to pray in another language. But when one is praying alone, the Hebrew text may itself serve as the vehicle that connects the individual to something larger than the private moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women & Vernacular\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction generated discussion among the {\i Rishonim} because in practice, women \u8212- and others not fluent in Hebrew \u8212- would recite {\i Shemoneh Esrei} even when praying privately. This led some {\i Rishonim} to refine the distinction: the relevant factor is not whether one is literally praying among others, but whether one is reciting a communal prayer as opposed to a wholly personal, self-composed text. If it is a communal prayer like {\i Shemoneh Esrei}, it may be recited in translation even by someone praying alone \u8212- perhaps because God hears the communal prayer wherever it is said, or because one praying {\i Shemoneh Esrei} at home is, in some sense, joining the larger community offering that same prayer. It may also be that when one composes a personal prayer in one\u8217's own words, using one\u8217's own language is the most natural approach. In any case, the {\i Rishonim} ultimately tend to limit Rav Yehuda\u8217's restriction specifically to Aramaic, so that once Aramaic ceased to be the vernacular, the practical import of the restriction largely disappeared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Angels & Languages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the underlying premise and challenges it directly. {\i V\u8217'ei malachei hasharet makiri belashon Aramit} \u8212- is it truly the case that the angels do not know Aramaic? A {\i baraita} is cited: Yochanan the {\i Kohen Gadol} heard a {\i bas kol} from the {\i Kodesh Hakodashim} \u8212- Rashi explains this occurred while he was performing the {\i avodah} on Yom Kippur \u8212- and the voice proclaimed {\i nitzchu talyaya de\u8217'azlu le\u8217'agacha kerava le\u8217'Antyocha}, that the young men of the {\i Chashmonaim} who went to battle against the Seleucids were victorious. A second story follows: Shimon HaTzaddik heard a {\i bas kol} from the {\i Kodesh Hakodashim} declaring {\i batlis avidata de\u8217'amar sanay le\u8217'aysay al heichala} \u8212- the campaign that the enemy had planned against the {\i Beis Hamikdash} has been nullified \u8212- and that a certain Greek ruler had been killed and his decrees annulled. The time was recorded, and it was found to correspond exactly to the moment those events occurred. In both cases, {\i uvelashon Aramit hayah} \u8212- the {\i bas kol} was delivered in Aramaic. Since a {\i bas kol} presumably passes through an angel, this seems to demonstrate that the angels do know Aramaic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers two responses. First: {\i bas kol shani} \u8212- a {\i bas kol} is different, {\i de\u8217'le\u8217'ishtamuei avida}, because its very purpose is to be heard and understood by people, so perhaps a special angel delivers such proclamations specifically in the people\u8217's language. Second: {\i ve\u8217'i bayit eima Gavriel hava} \u8212- perhaps it was Gavriel who was speaking, and Gavriel is the one angel who knows all languages, {\i de\u8217'amar mar ba Gavriel velimdo shivim lashon} \u8212- as the master taught, Gavriel came and taught Yosef all seventy languages. These two possibilities resolve the challenge, and the restriction on Aramaic prayer stands as stated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birchat Hamazon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamazon Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah next establishes that {\i birchat hamazon} may also be recited in any language. This requires discussion because {\i birchat hamazon} is clearly grounded in a {\i pasuk} in the {\i Torah}: {\i ve\u8217'achalta vesavata uverachta et Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- you shall eat and be satisfied and bless the Lord your God. The fact that it derives from a {\i Torah} verse might lead one to think that Hebrew is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reads the Gemara as simply noting the {\i pasuk} to indicate that there is no fixed text \u8212- since the verse just says \u8220"bless,\u8221" without specifying any particular wording, there is no reason to require Hebrew. On Rashi\u8217's reading, the Gemara is not answering a question but simply observing that of course it can be in any language, since there is nothing to translate. Tosafot, however, records a different {\i girsa} of the Gemara \u8212- one that begins with the question {\i mena hanei milei}, from where do we know this \u8212- and argues that Rashi\u8217's text has been altered. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, the Gemara does need to derive that {\i birchat hamazon} may be said in any language, because the presence of the word {\i uverachta} might have led us to think \u8212- by comparison to other {\i brachot} such as {\i birchat kohanim} that must be said in Hebrew \u8212- that here too Hebrew is required. The {\i pasuk} must therefore be cited to show that it may be said in any language. This is a genuinely striking point: even when there is no fixed textual formula to translate, there is still reason to think that perhaps Hebrew specifically is required \u8212- and a {\i pasuk} is needed to dispel that assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamazon & God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the proof from the verse? Tosafot explains: {\i uverachta et Hashem} \u8212- since the {\i bracha} and the praise are directed {\i klapei haShechinah}, toward God directly, one may bless in any language one chooses. When the communication is purely between the person and God, with no ritual dimension addressed to others, the language barrier dissolves. This is, notably, quite parallel to the justification for {\i tefillah} as {\i rachamei} \u8212- direct address to God removes the requirement of a specific sacred tongue. Yet, as Tosafot makes clear, even in such a case the principle needed to be explicitly derived, since there remains an independent reason to require Hebrew even when no prescribed text exists.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holy Tongue Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oaths in Any Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah also includes {\i shevuat ha\u8217'eidut} \u8212- the oath administered to a witness who denies knowing relevant testimony \u8212- and derives its permissibility in any language from the verse {\i v\u8217'nefesh ki techeta v\u8217'shama kol alah}, since it says {\i bechol lashon she\u8217'hi shoma\u8217'at}, in whatever language she hears. The need for a {\i pasuk} here, too, indicates that even for something not purely ritualistic, there is reason to suppose Hebrew might be required. {\i Shevuat hapikadon} \u8212- the oath regarding a withheld object \u8212- is derived from {\i shevuat ha\u8217'eidut} through a {\i gezeirah shavah} on the word {\i techeta} appearing in both contexts, and similarly may be administered in any language. The Mishnah\u8217's list of things permitted in all languages is thus complete; the {\i Rishonim} note that many other {\i brachot} and recitations are of course also said in other languages, but these are the specific items singled out by the Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashon Hakodesh List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then turns to the contrasting category: {\i v\u8217'eilu ne\u8217'emarim belashon hakodesh} \u8212- the following may only be said in {\i lashon hakodesh}: {\i mikra bikkurim}, {\i chalitzah}, {\i brachot v\u8217'klalot}, and others enumerated in the Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikra Bikkurim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara addresses each in turn. For {\i mikra bikkurim}, the Mishnah derives the requirement from the verse {\i v\u8217'anita v\u8217'amarta lifnei Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- you shall respond and say before the Lord your God. The double verb {\i v\u8217'anita v\u8217'amarta} \u8212- respond and say \u8212- also appears in the passage of the Levites at {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival}, where it is written {\i v\u8217'anu halevi\u8217'im v\u8217'amru el kol ish Yisrael kol ram}. By means of {\i gezeirah shavah} on the term {\i aniyah}, we learn: just as there by the {\i brachot v\u8217'klalot} it is in {\i lashon hakodesh}, so too here by {\i mikra bikkurim} it must be in {\i lashon hakodesh}. This is a notable ruling, because one might have thought that {\i mikra bikkurim} \u8212- which is an expression of personal gratitude to God \u8212- should be in the language that most allows the individual to speak from the heart. Nevertheless, the {\i gezeirah shavah} establishes that it requires Hebrew. Perhaps because the declaration in the {\i Beis Hamikdash} is a public ritual, heard by all present, its formal and communal character calls for the sacred tongue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brachot v\u8217'Klalot Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: how do we know that the {\i brachot v\u8217'klalot} of the Levites were themselves in {\i lashon hakodesh}? The answer comes through another {\i gezeirah shavah}: the verse by the Levites uses the expression {\i kol ram} \u8212- a loud voice \u8212- and separately, in the passage of {\i matan Torah}, it says {\i Moshe yedaber vehaElokim ya\u8217'anenu b\u8217'kol} \u8212- Moshe would speak and God would answer him with a voice. Just as there, when God spoke, it was clearly in Hebrew, so too the {\i kol ram} of the Levites must be in {\i lashon hakodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah & Kacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i chalitzah}, the Mishnah presents two approaches. The {\i rabbanan} learn from the double verb {\i v\u8217'ansa v\u8217'amra} \u8212- she shall respond and say \u8212- the same pattern of {\i aniyah v\u8217'amirah} that signals {\i lashon hakodesh}. Rabbi Yehuda, however, does not require this derivation for {\i chalitzah} because he learns it from the word {\i kacha} in the verse {\i kacha ye\u8217'aseh} \u8212- so shall it be done. Rabbi Yehuda reads {\i kacha} rather than the simpler {\i ko} as indicating two things: the declaration must be in Hebrew, and furthermore, it is specifically the physical acts of {\i chalitzah} \u8212- not the verbal declaration \u8212- that are indispensable for the {\i chalitzah} to be valid. The {\i rabbanan}, who do not read the elongated {\i kacha} as teaching two separate points, use it only to establish that the acts are essential, and derive the requirement of Hebrew from {\i v\u8217'ansa v\u8217'amra} instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: if Rabbi Yehuda uses {\i kacha} for both points and does not use {\i v\u8217'ansa v\u8217'amra} by {\i chalitzah}, what does he do with that phrase? The answer is that he needs it to teach regarding the Levites that the {\i brachot v\u8217'klalot} are in {\i lashon hakodesh}. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's entire chain of derivation thus runs differently from that of the {\i rabbanan}: he learns that {\i chalitzah} must be in Hebrew from {\i kacha}, then uses {\i chalitzah} to teach that {\i brachot v\u8217'klalot} are in Hebrew, and from there applies the {\i gezeirah shavah} to {\i mikra bikkurim}. The Gemara asks why Rabbi Yehuda does not simply learn the Levites\u8217' {\i brachot v\u8217'klalot} from the {\i gezeirah shavah} on {\i kol} connecting to Moshe and God\u8217's voice, as the first approach had done. The answer is that the {\i gezeirah shavah} on {\i aniyah} is the tradition he received, while the {\i kol-kol} connection is not his tradition \u8212- at a certain point one cannot ask why one {\i gezeirah shavah} was chosen over another, as these are matters of received tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited in support of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view: {\i kol makom she\u8217'ne\u8217'emar ko, kacha, aniyah, v\u8217'amirah, einu ela lashon hakodesh} \u8212- wherever the {\i Torah} uses the expressions {\i ko}, {\i kacha}, {\i aniyah}, or {\i amirah}, the requirement is that it be in {\i lashon hakodesh}. The Baraita applies this to three instances: {\i ko tevarachu et bnei Yisrael} \u8212- referring to {\i birchat kohanim}; {\i kacha d\u8217'chalitzah} \u8212- the {\i kacha} of {\i chalitzah}; and {\i aniyah v\u8217'amirah d\u8217'Levi\u8217'im} \u8212- the {\i aniyah v\u8217'amirah} of the Levites by {\i brachot v\u8217'klalot}. From that last anchor point, the derivation extends further to {\i mikra bikkurim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Har Gerizim & Eival\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Location Description\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah speaks of the ceremony on {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival}, and the Gemara now examines a {\i baraita} addressing the precise location of those mountains as described in the verses. The {\i baraita} cites the verse {\i halo hemah b\u8217'ever haYarden}, behold they are across the Jordan. The phrase is then parsed: {\i me\u8217'ever l\u8217'Yarden u\u8217'l\u8217'halan divrei Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- on the other side of the Jordan, and further beyond, according to Rabbi Yehuda. On this reading, {\i ever haYarden} denotes the east bank, and the mountains are described as being at a substantial distance from it. {\i Acharei derech mevo hashemesh} \u8212- after the way of the coming of the sun \u8212- is read as {\i mekom she\u8217'chamah zorachat}, the place where the sun rises, i.e., from the east. This is admittedly not the simple {\i peshat} of the verse, but the intent is to emphasize that the mountains are located far from the eastern side of the Jordan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Continuing: {\i b\u8217'eretz ha\u8217'Kna\u8217'ani hayoshev ba\u8217'aravah} \u8212- in the land of the Canaanites who dwell in the valley. This, according to the {\i baraita}, refers to {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival}, where the {\i Kuti\u8217'im} (Samaritans) dwell in the current era. {\i Mul haGilgal} \u8212- opposite Gilgal, {\i samuch l\u8217'Gilgal} \u8212- near Gilgal, because these mountains are located well to the west of the Jordan, in the direction of Gilgal. {\i Etzel elonei Moreh} \u8212- near {\i elonei Moreh}, which is identified as {\i Shechem}. The {\i baraita} then cites the parallel verse from the story of Avraham \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ya\u8217'avor Avram ba\u8217'aretz ad mekom Shechem, Alon Moreh} \u8212- to establish by {\i gezeirah shavah} that {\i Alon Moreh} here too means {\i Shechem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraita} is cited, in which Rabbi Elazar b\u8217'Rabbi Yose recounts that with this very passage he demonstrated to the Samaritans that their {\i sifrei Torah} were falsified. The Samaritans, who rejected the oral tradition, claimed that {\i Elon Moreh} by {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival} referred to {\i Shechem} \u8212- the location they held sacred. Rabbi Elazar responded: {\i zieftem Toratechem v\u8217'lo alissem b\u8217'yedchem klum} \u8212- you have falsified your Torah and gained nothing thereby \u8212- for we too agree that {\i Elon Moreh} is {\i Shechem}. But we know this through a {\i gezeirah shavah} from the verse about Avraham. You, who reject the oral tradition, have no such derivation \u8212- where did you learn it from? The precise logic of this exchange requires closer examination; the Gemara\u8217's point seems to turn on something beyond the simple identification of the name, though exactly what that additional element is remains somewhat opaque in the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A different approach to the verse is now presented. Rabbi Elazar says: {\i halo hem b\u8217'ever haYarden \u8212- samoch l\u8217'Yarden}. The word {\i ever} does not mean a far distance but rather immediately near. The mountains are located just on the other side of the Jordan \u8212- immediately to the west, as soon as one crosses. This contrasts with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's reading of the verse as pointing to a location far from the eastern bank. Rabbi Elazar supports his reading: one cannot interpret the verse as pointing far away from the Jordan, because another verse says {\i v\u8217'haya b\u8217'ovrechem et haYarden} \u8212- it will be when you cross the Jordan \u8212- implying that the erection of the stones and the ceremony of {\i brachot v\u8217'klalot} follow immediately. The phrase {\i b\u8217'yom asher tavru} \u8212- on the day that you cross \u8212- reinforces that this was to take place immediately upon crossing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Rabbi Elazar\u8217's reading, {\i acharei derech mevo hashemesh} means toward the setting of the sun \u8212- i.e., to the west \u8212- which coheres naturally with a location just across the Jordan on the western bank.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problems & Responses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His reading, however, presents difficulties. The verse describes the location as {\i b\u8217'eretz ha\u8217'Kna\u8217'ani}, yet the territory immediately west of the Jordan at the crossing point is the land of the {\i Chivi}, not the {\i Kena\u8217'ani}. Furthermore, the verse says {\i hayoshev ba\u8217'aravah} \u8212- who dwells in the valley \u8212- but the {\i Chivi\u8217'im} dwell among mountains and hills, not in a valley. And {\i mul haGilgal} \u8212- opposite Gilgal \u8212- is problematic because Gilgal was itself some distance from the actual crossing point, so one could not truly say that the crossing spot faces Gilgal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Direction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These difficulties lead to the interpretation of Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov: {\i lo ba hakatuv ela l\u8217'harot lahem derech bishniya k\u8217'derech sheher\u8217'a lahem berishona} \u8212- the verse from {\i mul haGilgal} onward is not describing the location of {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival} at all. Rather, it is providing directional guidance for entering the land. When they left Egypt, the {\i Bnei Yisrael} were guided by the {\i ananei kavod}; now, entering the land a second time without those clouds, the Torah provides geographical markers to orient their movement. The mention of the valley, Gilgal, and the land of the {\i Kena\u8217'ani} are thus waypoints for the journey into the land \u8212- not descriptors of where the ceremony took place. Rashi adds that following this path through the valley, past Gilgal, and through the land of the {\i Kena\u8217'ani} ensures that they penetrate deep into the land rather than remaining near the Jordan crossing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Jordan Crossing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aron Crosses First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} describes how the {\i Bnei Yisrael} actually crossed the Jordan. On a normal day of travel through the wilderness, the order was: two of the tribal camps would march first, then the {\i aron} would follow behind the first two {\i degalim}. But on the day of the crossing, {\i nasa techila} \u8212- the {\i aron} went first. This was necessary because the {\i aron} carried by the Levites entering the water is what caused the waters to stop flowing. The verse is cited: {\i hinei aron habrit adon kol ha\u8217'aretz over lifneichem baYarden} \u8212- the {\i aron habrit} of the master of the whole world passes before you into the Jordan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Times\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ordinarily it was the {\i Levi\u8217'im} who carried the {\i aron}, but on the day of the crossing, {\i nas\u8217'u hakohanim} \u8212- it was the {\i kohanim} who carried it. The verse confirms: {\i vehaya kenach kapot raglei hakohanim nosei aron Hashem} \u8212- when the feet of the {\i kohanim} bearing the {\i aron of Hashem} came to rest in the Jordan. A {\i baraita} adds: Rabbi Yosei says there were three occasions on which the {\i kohanim} specifically carried the {\i aron} \u8212- when they crossed the Jordan, when they circled Yericho, and when the {\i aron} was returned to its permanent place when Shlomo brought it into the {\i Kodesh Hakodashim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waters Back Up\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As soon as the feet of the {\i kohanim} touched the water, {\i chazaru hamayim le\u8217'achoreihem} \u8212- the waters turned backward. Unlike the splitting of the {\i Yam Suf}, where a sea was divided, here the Jordan was a rushing river; the oncoming water met resistance and could no longer flow forward, while the water already below drained away, leaving dry ground. The verse is cited: {\i vaya\u8217'amdu hamayim hayordim milmala, kamu ned echad} \u8212- the waters descending from above stood, forming a single wall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 12 Mil Dimensions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How high did this wall of water rise? Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i shneim asar mil al shneim asar mil, keneged machaneh Yisrael} \u8212- 12 {\i mil} by 12 {\i mil}, corresponding to the size of the camp of Israel. The width of the Jordan itself was approximately 50 {\i amot} (which corresponds closely to the river\u8217's present width of about 60 feet). The dimensions of 12 {\i mil} by 12 {\i mil} therefore describe not the width of the wall across the river, but the extent to which the water backed up \u8212- 12 {\i mil} in depth along the river and 12 {\i mil} in height. A {\i mil} is 2,000 {\i amot}, approximately a kilometer, so 12 {\i mil} represents roughly 12 kilometers. The camp of Israel was understood to occupy a square of 12 {\i mil} by 12 {\i mil} when they traveled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water Speed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Shimon challenges Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's calculation. {\i Adam kal o mayim kalim} \u8212- which moves faster, a person or flowing water? {\i Hevei omer mayim kalim} \u8212- clearly the water is faster. If the wall was only 12 {\i mil} high, and water moves faster than people, then by the time the {\i Bnei Yisrael} finished crossing \u8212- which took as long as it would take to traverse 12 {\i mil} \u8212- the rushing water from upstream would have risen far higher than 12 {\i mil} and would have simply crashed over that wall and drowned them. {\i Im ken bayim mayim veshotef otam} \u8212- if so, the waters would have come and swept them away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 300 Mil Height\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i ela melamed shehayu mayim nikdashim ve\u8217'olim kippin al gabei kippin} \u8212- rather, the waters were sanctified and piled up in layers upon layers, {\i yoter mishlosh meot mil} \u8212- more than 300 {\i mil} in height. This reflects the assumption that water travels approximately 25 times faster than people walk, and so in the time it took the nation to cross, the backed-up water rose to more than 300 {\i mil} high.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kings\u8217' Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The miraculous scale of the event was such that {\i ad she\u8217'ra\u8217'u otam kol malchei mizrach uma\u8217'arav} \u8212- until all the kings of the East and the West saw it. The verse in Sefer Yehoshua is cited: when all the kings of the Amorites on the western side of the Jordan heard how God had dried up the waters of the Jordan before the {\i Bnei Yisrael} until they had passed through, {\i vayimas levavam} \u8212- their hearts melted \u8212- and {\i velo hayah bahem od ruach mipnei bnei Yisrael} \u8212- they no longer had any spirit to stand against the {\i Bnei Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds that even before the crossing of the Jordan, Rachav had told Yehoshua\u8217's messengers about the earlier miracle of the {\i Yam Suf}: {\i ki shamanu et asher hovish Hashem et mei Yam Suf} \u8212- we heard how God dried up the waters of the {\i Yam Suf} \u8212- and {\i venishma vayimas levavenu velo kamah od} \u8212- and we heard and our hearts melted and no spirit remained. The parallel phrasing between Rachav\u8217's account of the {\i Yam Suf} and the verse describing the reaction of the kings to the Jordan crossing underscores that the miracles at both bodies of water formed a unified arc of Divine intervention that broke the resolve of {\i Bnei Yisrael}\u8217's enemies. The discussion of the Jordan crossing will continue on the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 34\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Jordan Crossing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf 34 Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 34 picks up twenty lines down, at the words {\i odam ba-Yarden} \u8212- \u8220"while they were still in the Jordan\u8221" \u8212- in the midst of a {\i baraita} drawn from the {\i Tosefta}. The broader context is the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's discussion of what may be said in any language versus what must be recited specifically in {\i lashon ha-kodesh}. The {\i Mishnah} had ruled that the blessings and curses pronounced on Har Gerizim and Har Eival could only be said in Hebrew, and the {\i Gemara} has been unfolding a detailed account of everything that accompanied the crossing of the Jordan into Canaan: the erection of the stones, the building of the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, and the proclamation of the {\i brachos} and {\i klalos}. Daf 34 continues from within that narrative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joshua\u8217's Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While the {\i kohanim} were still standing in the Jordan \u8212- and indeed all of {\i Bnei Yisrael} were still in the river \u8212- Yehoshua addressed them: {\i de\u8217'u al mah atem overim et ha-Yarden}, \u8220"understand for what purpose you are crossing the Jordan.\u8221" He stated the condition explicitly: {\i al menat she-torishu et yoshvei ha-aretz mi-pneichem} \u8212- you are crossing on the condition that you commit yourselves to driving out the inhabitants of the land. The {\i baraita} cites the verse in Bamidbar, {\i ve-horashtem et kol yoshvei ha-aretz mi-pneichem}, and adds: if you fulfill this, well and good \u8212- but {\i im lav}, if you do not, {\i ba\u8217'u mayim u-shotefim etchem}, the waters will come and drown you. Whether this means that failure to agree at that moment would bring immediate catastrophe, or that failure to ultimately carry it out would lead to that consequence, the text leaves somewhat open; the latter reading seems more natural. The {\i Gemara} then notes the unusual phrasing {\i otichem} \u8212- \u8220"you\u8221" \u8212- and derives that Yehoshua was saying {\i oti ve-etchem}, the waters will come and destroy me together with you. He included himself in the threat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twelve Stones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again while standing in the Jordan, Yehoshua instructed them: {\i harimu lachem ish even achat al shichmo le-mispar shivtei Yisrael} \u8212- each man should take up one stone on his shoulder, corresponding to the twelve tribes, taken from the Jordan itself, and erect them after crossing over. The verse states: {\i le-ma\u8217'an tihyeh zot ot be-kirbechem} \u8212- this shall be a sign in your midst \u8212- so that when future generations ask, {\i mah ha-avanim ha-eleh lachem}, \u8220"what are these stones?\u8221", they will be told that the forefathers crossed the Jordan. This language resonates with the {\i pasuk} about the {\i Korban Pesach} and the exchange recalled in the {\i Seder}. The purpose of the stones, the {\i Gemara} says, is as a {\i siman la-vanim she-avru avot ha-Yarden} \u8212- a sign for children that the forefathers crossed the Jordan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further instruction followed: {\i se\u8217'u lachem mizeh mi-toch ha-Yarden mi-matzav raglei ha-kohanim} \u8212- take from where the {\i kohanim} are standing twelve stones, carry them with you, and place them {\i ba-malon asher talinu vo ha-laila}, in the place where you will sleep that night. One might have thought this meant they should carry the stones to every encampment \u8212- but the {\i Talmud} derives from the phrase {\i asher talinu vo ha-laila}, \u8220"where we will sleep this night,\u8221" that only the first encampment is meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's comment at the verse {\i seu mizeh mi-toch ha-Yarden mi-matzav} is worth noting: he explains that the people lifted up stones, the {\i kohanim} stood on them, and the stones were then taken from beneath the feet of the {\i kohanim}. Rashi further states that these were the very stones later erected on Har Eival, upon which the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} was built and the {\i Torah} inscribed {\i be\u8217'er heitev} \u8212- and that they were subsequently taken down and re-erected in Gilgal. The precise relationship among the three distinct elements \u8212- the altar, the writing of the {\i Torah} on the stones {\i be\u8217'er heitev}, and the twelve commemorative stones \u8212- is not straightforward, and working out their interconnection is no simple matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grape Cluster\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} records that Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- or according to another reading, Rabbi Yose \u8212- reported that Abba Chalafta, Rabbi Eliezer ben Matya, and Chananya ben Chakinai once stood on those very stones, and measured that each stone weighed the equivalent of forty {\i se\u8217'ah}, an enormous weight. A received tradition ({\i gemiri}) teaches that the load a person can lift and hoist onto his own shoulders is only one-third of what he can carry if another person loads him. Since Yehoshua instructed them to have the stones lifted onto their shoulders \u8212- implying they were loaded by others \u8212- this shows that each carrier could bear the equivalent of 120 {\i se\u8217'ah}. From this calculation, the {\i Gemara} derives: {\i mikan ata mitchashev la-eshkol} \u8212- from here you can reckon the size of the grape cluster.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse says the spies carried the grape cluster {\i bamot bishnayim}, with a pole between two. But if a pole already implies two carriers, why does the verse add the word {\i bishnayim}? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i bishnei motot} \u8212- there were two poles. Rabbi Yitzchak adds the term {\i turtani ve-turtani de-turtani} \u8212- poles upon poles \u8212- indicating not merely two poles but four. Rashi explains the arrangement: two poles running in parallel and two crossing beneath them in an X formation, providing additional support. Eight people in total carried it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question about the sheer weight: if a single person, when loaded by another, can carry 120 {\i se\u8217'ah}, and eight people are carrying together with four poles, the cluster must have been of staggering proportions. The {\i Gemara} elaborates: one person carried a pomegranate, one carried a fig, eight carried the single cluster of grapes \u8212- and Yehoshua and Kalev carried nothing at all. Two explanations are offered: either {\i mishum de-chashivei}, because they were too distinguished to carry anything, or {\i she-lo hayu be-oto eitzah} \u8212- because they were not part of the scheme. The bringing back of the fruit was, in their view, part of the strategy to frighten the people and was not a genuine celebration of the land\u8217's bounty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Formation Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a dispute between Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Yitzchak Napcha concerning how the Israelites crossed the Jordan. This connects to the earlier debate \u8212- recalled from the previous daf \u8212- about how high the waters piled up. Rabbi Yehuda had held that the waters rose twelve {\i mil}, because the camp was twelve {\i mil} across, and the time it took the camp to cross was the time during which the waters accumulated. Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'Rabbi Shimon disagreed sharply, saying the waters rose 300 {\i mil} \u8212- twenty-five times the camp\u8217's width \u8212- because water moves far faster than people walk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One opinion among Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Yitzchak Napcha holds: according to Rabbi Yehuda, {\i ke-chaniyatan avru} \u8212- they crossed just as they were encamped, in a twelve-by-twelve square formation \u8212- and that is why it took the length of time it did to traverse the Jordan, producing a twelve-{\i mil} accumulation of water. According to Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'Rabbi Shimon, {\i be-fispecha achat avru} \u8212- they crossed in a more elongated procession, something closer to a marching line rather than a square \u8212- and therefore it took far longer to pass through, causing the waters to pile much higher. This reading is somewhat surprising, since Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'Rabbi Shimon\u8217's stated position was simply that water travels faster than people, not that they marched in a different formation \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} suggests this may also be part of his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other opinion holds that {\i bein mar u-vein mar ke-chaniyatan avru} \u8212- both authorities agree that they crossed in the same square formation as they were encamped. The debate is purely about speed: {\i mar savar adam kal}, one holds that a person travels as fast as the water (an admittedly difficult image), while {\i mar savar mayim kalim}, the other holds that water moves far faster than people. This second reading appears to be the more straightforward account of what the earlier dispute actually was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot engages in an extended analysis arising from the fact that the Jordan was only fifty {\i amot} wide. If Yehoshua addressed all of {\i Bnei Yisrael} while they were standing in the river, and if the camp measured twelve {\i mil} \u8212- which equals 2,400 {\i amot} \u8212- then there would need to be 480 units of people in a fifty-{\i amot}-wide riverbed to accommodate the entire camp, meaning the people would have had to move steadily downstream as more entered behind them. Tosafot attempts to calculate whether the Jordan itself was even long enough to contain them all simultaneously, and the mathematics are far from trivial.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Spies Mission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlach vs.\u160 ?Devarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the discussion has been centered on Yehoshua, the crossing into the land, and the figures of Yehoshua and Kalev who brought back fruit from Canaan, the {\i Gemara} transitions naturally to the episode of the spies. The text now takes up the relationship between the two accounts of that mission: the version in {\i Shlach} and the retelling in Devarim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In {\i Shlach} the Torah reads {\i shlach lecha anashim} \u8212- send men. Reish Lakish explains: {\i shlach lecha mada\u8217'atcha} \u8212- \u8220"go ahead, do it from your own mind,\u8221" meaning God was granting permission, not issuing a command. This reconciles the two accounts: in Devarim, Moshe recalls that the people came to him requesting the mission; in {\i Shlach}, God responds by permitting it. The question is then implicit: would God have commanded something that ended so catastrophically? The answer is evidently no \u8212- {\i vachi adam zeh borer chelek ra le-atzmo}, would a person really choose a bad portion for himself? Clearly God would not have ordained this; it was only a concession to the people\u8217's request.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is also the meaning, Reish Lakish continues, of the verse {\i va-yitav be-einai ha-davar} \u8212- \u8220"and the matter was good in my eyes.\u8221" Reish Lakish reads it: {\i be-einai ve-lo be-einav shel makom} \u8212- it seemed good to me, but not in God\u8217's eyes. God gave allowance, but it was not God\u8217's own preference \u8212- as the catastrophic outcome made plain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then adds: {\i vayachperu lanu et ha-aretz} \u8212- \u8220"and let them search out the land for us.\u8221" Rabbi Chiya bar Abba comments: the spies intended from the outset nothing less than the shaming of Eretz Yisrael. He draws a verbal parallel: {\i vayachperu} here echoes the verse {\i ve-chafra ha-levanah u-vosha ha-chamah} \u8212- the moon shall be shamed and the sun shall be embarrassed \u8212- showing that {\i vayachpor} carries the connotation of shame. Their mission was aimed at disgracing the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spies\u8217' Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then lists the names of the spies as the Torah records them, beginning with {\i le-mateh Reuven Shamua ben Zakur}. Rabbi Yitzchak states: we have a received tradition going back through the generations \u8212- {\i masoret be-yadeinu me-avoteinu} \u8212- that the spies were called by the names they bore in the Torah because those names reflected their deeds, {\i meraglim al shem ma\u8217'aseihem nikre\u8217'u}. Whether this means their parents providentially gave them names that came to describe their actions, or whether the Torah assigns them names that function as epithets, the {\i Gemara} does not resolve definitively. But the principle stands: the names encode what they did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rabbi Yitzchak acknowledges, we have only managed to work this out for one of them: {\i Satur ben Michael}. {\i Satur} \u8212- from the root meaning to undermine or destroy \u8212- {\i satar ma\u8217'asav shel ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu}: he undermined God\u8217's design, which was to bring the people into the land. {\i Michael} \u8212- the name contains {\i El}, God \u8212- {\i she-asah atzmo mach}: he made God appear weak, as though God lacked the power to bring them in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan then takes up the challenge and proposes an additional example: {\i Nachbi ben Vafsi}. {\i Nachbi} \u8212- {\i she-hechbi devarav shel ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- he concealed God\u8217's words, hiding the truth about what God could and would do. {\i Vafsi} \u8212- {\i she-pisa al midotav shel ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- he strode over, as it were, leaped past God\u8217's attributes, failing to acknowledge divine power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caleb\u8217's Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse in the {\i parasha} reads {\i va-ya\u8217'alu ba-negev va-yavo ad Chevron} \u8212- they went up through the south and he came to Hebron. The singular {\i va-yavo} is striking; it should read {\i va-yavo\u8217'u}, in the plural. Rava derives from this: {\i melamed she-parash Kalev me-atzat ha-meraglim} \u8212- the singular teaches that Kalev broke away from the conspiracy of the spies. Not only did he refuse to participate in their scheme, but he physically separated himself from the group. {\i Va-halach ve-nishtate\u8217'ach al kivrei avot} \u8212- he went and prostrated himself on the graves of the forefathers \u8212- Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov \u8212- who are buried in Chevron, along with the {\i Imahos}. He prayed: {\i avosai bikshu alai rachamim} \u8212- my fathers, seek mercy for me, that I be saved from the conspiracy of the spies, for it was enormously difficult to stand up against that pressure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah\u8217's statement \u8212- {\i ve-avdi Kalev ekev hayta ruach acheret imo} \u8212- that Kalev possessed a different spirit \u8212- is thus explained by this act: he went to Chevron, he prayed, and that inner strength was what distinguished him. Of course there is a further point of connection: Kalev ultimately received Chevron as his portion when the land was divided. And Rashi notes on the verse {\i va-avi\u8217'osiv el ha-aretz asher ba shama} \u8212- \u8220"and I will bring him to the land he went to\u8221" \u8212- this language is purposefully specific. It is not a general promise of entry into Canaan; it is a promise of the very place, Chevron, that Kalev had visited to pray.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed question about this episode: the {\i Gemara} elsewhere teaches that the dead are unaware of what transpires in this world \u8212- {\i meisim lo yad\u8217'ei midi}. If so, what is the purpose of prostrating oneself and praying at the graves of the forefathers? Tosafot answers: when a person prays in this way, they inform the deceased in heaven that someone below is praying and seeking their intercession, and those holy souls can then themselves pray to God on that person\u8217's behalf. There is also support from the {\i baraita} that asks why people go out to cemeteries \u8212- the answer given is {\i k\u8217'dei she-yivakshu aleihem meisim rachamim}, so that the dead will pray for mercy on behalf of the living. The matter is theologically sensitive \u8212- rationalist authorities were wary of any formulation that might slide into the appearance of praying {\i to} the dead rather than asking them to pray \u8212- but the passage makes clear that there is a recognized tradition here, and both the metaphysical question (do the dead have awareness?) and the theological question (what is the proper role of an intermediary?) warrant careful handling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joshua Protected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yehoshua did not need to go pray at the graves of the forefathers because Moshe had already prayed on his behalf. The verse states: {\i va-yikra Moshe le-Hoshea bin Nun Yehoshua} \u8212- and Moshe called Hoshea the son of Nun \u8220"Yehoshua.\u8221" The name {\i Yehoshua} encodes the prayer: {\i Yah yoshia\u8217'acha me-atzat ha-meraglim} \u8212- may God save you from the plot of the spies. Moshe\u8217's {\i tefilla} for Yehoshua thus preceded and preempted the need Kalev felt to go seek intercession for himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then returns to the verse {\i ve-avdi Kalev ekev hayta ruach acheret imo} and notes that the subsequent promise \u8212- {\i va-avi\u8217'osiv el ha-aretz asher ba shama} \u8212- refers precisely to Chevron, the land to which he came to pray. As Rashi underscores, the Torah does not say he will enter Canaan in general, but specifically the land he went to, which is Chevron.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giants Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse records: {\i ve-sham Achiman Sheshai ve-Talmai yelidei ha-anak} \u8212- there were Achiman, Sheshai, and Talmai, the children of the giant. The {\i Gemara} interprets each name. {\i Achiman} \u8212- {\i meyuman she-ba-echav} \u8212- the most capable among his brothers, the strongest. {\i Sheshai} \u8212- {\i she-mesim et ha-aretz ke-shechitut} \u8212- he caused the earth to collapse beneath him as he walked, leaving deep craters, he was so massive. {\i Talmai} \u8212- {\i she-mesim et ha-aretz telamim telamim} \u8212- he plowed furrows in the earth with each step. A second interpretation, framed as {\i davar acher}, shifts from etymology to historical record: {\i Achiman bana Anat}, Achiman built the city of Anat; {\i Sheshai bana Alash}, Sheshai built Alash; {\i Talmai bana Talbush}, Talmai built Talbush. As for the phrase {\i yelidei ha-anak}, the {\i Gemara} notes that {\i anak} relates to the word for neck: Rashi explains that their necks appeared to pierce through the heavens \u8212- or through the sun itself \u8212- so towering was their stature.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hebron\u8217's Qualities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hebron vs.\u160 ?Zoan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse states: {\i ve-Chevron sheva shanim nivnesa lifnei Tzoan Mitzrayim} \u8212- Chevron was built seven years before Tzoan of Egypt. The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: {\i mai nivnesa} \u8212- what does \u8220"built\u8221" mean here? If taken literally, it would mean that Chevron as a city was constructed before Tzoan. But this is difficult: Canaan was the youngest son of Cham, while Mitzrayim \u8212- after whom Egypt is named \u8212- was an older son, as the verse enumerates: {\i bnei Cham Kush u-Mitzrayim u-Put ve-Chanaan}. Would a father really build a city for his youngest son before his older son? And Tzoan was Mitzrayim\u8217's city.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore reinterprets: {\i she-hayta mevunah echad mi-shiva be-Tzoan} \u8212- Chevron was seven times more {\i mevunah} \u8212- more built up, more developed \u8212- than Tzoan. The point is comparative quality, not chronological construction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where the statement becomes striking. The {\i Gemara} observes: {\i ein lecha tarshin be-chol Eretz Yisrael yoter mi-Chevron} \u8212- there is no place in all of Eretz Yisrael that is more rocky and barren than Chevron \u8212- {\i de-kivrei bei shichvei} \u8212- which is precisely why the dead are buried there, since the ground is not useful for agriculture. Yet {\i ein lecha me\u8217'ulah be-chol ha-aratzot yoter mi-Eretz Mitzrayim} \u8212- there is no land in the world more fertile than Egypt, compared to the Garden of God, as the verse says \u8212- and {\i ein lecha me\u8217'ulah be-chol Eretz Mitzrayim yoter mi-Tzoan} \u8212- and within all of Egypt, there is no more fertile and distinguished region than Tzoan, where the high dignitaries of Israel resided during their dealings with Egypt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And yet: {\i afilu hachi}, even so, Chevron is seven times more {\i mevunah} than Tzoan. The nature of that superiority \u8212- rocky Chevron surpassing the most fertile place in the most fertile land \u8212- suggests that Rashi\u8217's reading, {\i meyushevet be-peiros}, \u8220"settled with fruits,\u8221" may itself be pointing toward something more than agricultural output. It is perhaps more natural to understand this as a spiritual statement about the quality of the place rather than a claim about its physical productivity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rocky Land Grazing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might object: is it really true that Chevron is so rocky? The {\i Gemara} cites a verse \u8212- {\i va-yehi mi-ketz arba\u8217'im shanah va-yomer Avshalom el ha-melech elcha na} \u8212- and Avshalom, requesting leave to go to Chevron, said he wanted to bring a sacrifice. Rabbi Avyu, and some attribute this to Rabbah bar bar Chana, explains: {\i Chanan she-halach le-havi kevasim mi-Chevron} \u8212- he went to bring lambs from Chevron. And a {\i baraita} teaches: if you want fine rams, you get them from Moav; if you want the best lambs, you get them from Chevron. This appears to contradict the characterization of Chevron as barren and rocky: how can the best lambs come from such inhospitable terrain?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction directly: {\i aidi de-klisha ar\u8217'a avda ra\u8217'aya, ve-shamin kinyana} \u8212- it is precisely because the land is thin and poor that it produces excellent grazing, and so you get fat, prized flocks. Rocky, sparse land is ideal grazing country; the animals forage over wide, lean terrain and produce superior quality. The paradox resolves itself: Chevron\u8217's very poverty as agricultural land is what makes it outstanding for flocks. And with that observation, the {\i Gemara} continues into the next daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 35\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meraglim & Lashon Hara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kalev at Chevron\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i aggadeta} on this daf opens in the midst of a long narrative touching on the crossing of the {\i Yarden} at the time of Yehoshua, with digressions into related topics \u8212- most immediately, the story of the {\i meraglim}. The {\i gemara} had already noted that the verse describing the spies\u8217' arrival at Chevron employs the singular form \u8212- {\i vayavo ad Chevron} \u8212- and derives from this that only Kalev went to Chevron, where he prostrated himself at the graves of the {\i avot} and prayed: {\i bikshu alai rachamim}, \u8220"pray for mercy on my behalf.\u8221" Rashi observes that this detail provides a satisfying closure to a parallel found later in Yehoshua\u8217's narrative: it is Kalev who ultimately receives Chevron as his inheritance \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'aretz asher darach bah} \u8212- a fitting reward for the very place where he had gone to seek the protection of the ancestors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Dead & Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a pointed question raised by Tosafot on this passage. The {\i gemara} in the chapter {\i Mi She-meto} concludes that the dead are not aware of what transpires in this world \u8212- {\i lo yad\u8217'in midi} \u8212- and this applies even to the {\i avot ha-olam}, to Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov themselves. If so, what is the point of praying that the {\i avot} should intercede on one\u8217's behalf, as Kalev did?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot resolves this by suggesting that the dead do not directly hear prayers addressed to them, but they are informed \u8212- {\i modi\u8217'in lahem} \u8212- that someone below has prayed that they should in turn pray on his behalf. They receive, as it were, a summary of what has been requested of them. Tosafot adds corroborating sources: the practice cited in the second chapter of Ta\u8217'anis of going out to the cemeteries so that the {\i meitim} should pray on behalf of the living, and the episode in the chapter of the {\i chasidim} involving Rabbi Mana who prostrated himself at his father\u8217's grave. These precedents reflect a clearly encoded {\i minhag}, observed particularly during the {\i aseres yemei teshuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intermediaries & Worship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper concern with this practice, however, is not primarily whether the dead have awareness \u8212- Tosafot\u8217's answer addresses that \u8212- but whether the practice remains properly directed. The ideal, as understood by Tosafot and by the {\i gemara}, is that one requests of the deceased that they pray to God on one\u8217's behalf; they function as an intermediary in petition, not as the object of worship. The problem is that this line becomes quite blurred in practice. When people go to a {\i beit ha-kevarot}, it is very likely that they direct their prayers to the {\i meitim} themselves, not merely asking them to carry prayers forward to God. At that point, it becomes a {\i tefillah} addressed to a being other than God \u8212- something that would deeply disturb the Rambam, and which touches on a fundamental question about the nature of worship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concern is distinct from the concept of a {\i shaliach tzibbur}, who represents the congregation but remains a this-worldly representative praying together with the community, not an intermediary in any theologically charged sense. Similarly, the liturgical passage of {\i Machnisei Rachamim}, in which we ask angels to bring our prayers before God, occupies its own category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with certain Christian practices is instructive here. Many Christian communities pray directly to deceased saints \u8212- not \u8220"please bring my prayer to God,\u8221" but \u8220"Saint so-and-so, please help me\u8221" \u8212- where the saint is effectively the one prayed to, not merely the conduit. This is understandable in a theological framework where God has taken human form and where deceased holy individuals are correspondingly relatable objects of petition. But it is precisely this blurring \u8212- from intermediary to the thing prayed to \u8212- that raises serious concerns in the Jewish framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might raise the concept of {\i zechut avot} in this context, but that is fundamentally different. {\i Zechut avot} is not a matter of directing prayer to the ancestors; it is a theological accounting in which one stands before God and invokes the accumulated merit of prior generations. The two concepts are not merely adjacent on a continuum \u8212- one is a claim about how God reckons merit, the other is a question about the very object of worship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spies\u8217' Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After this digression, the {\i gemara} returns to the narrative of the spies, picking up from {\i daf} 34b. The verse reads: {\i vayashuvu mitur ha\u8217'aretz miketz arba\u8217'im yom} \u8212- and they returned from scouting the land. A subsequent verse says {\i vayelchu vayavo\u8217'u} \u8212- they went and they came. Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai derives from the juxtaposition of the going and the coming \u8212- {\i makish halicha livi\u8217'ah} \u8212- that just as their return was characterized by {\i etza ra\u8217'ah}, an evil plan or collusion, so too their original departure was already undertaken with that same evil design. They left with bad intentions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The spies\u8217' report itself begins with a remarkable blend of truth and slander. They affirm that the land is indeed flowing with milk and honey and offer its fruit as evidence \u8212- {\i vegam zavat chalav udevash hi} \u8212- but then immediately pivot: {\i efes ki az ha\u8217'am}, the people are too strong for us. Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Meir derives a principle from this structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashon Hara & Truth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle is striking: {\i kol lashon hara she\u8217'ein bo devar emet bit\u8217'chilato ein mitkayem besofo} \u8212- any {\i lashon hara} that does not contain some element of truth at the outset will not endure. A skilled purveyor of harmful speech knows that proximity to the truth lends credibility and staying power. By contrast, slander that contains no factual core fails to take hold. It is worth noting that the {\i Chafetz Chaim} draws a careful distinction between {\i lashon hara} \u8212- true but harmful speech \u8212- and {\i motzi shem ra} \u8212- false slander. The {\i gemara} here uses {\i lashon hara} in its more generic sense of harmful speech, without that technical distinction, but the practical observation is about slander: it is most destructive when anchored in verifiable truth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kalev\u8217's Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse then says {\i vayahas Kalev et ha\u8217'am el Moshe} \u8212- Caleb silenced the people before Moses. Rava interprets {\i vayahas} as {\i she\u8217'hisitam bidvarim} \u8212- he incited or seduced them with words, a way of drawing them in before pivoting. The question arises: why was it Kalev who spoke and not Yehoshua?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh Ketiah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that Yehoshua did try to speak, but the people dismissed him immediately: {\i dein rosh keti\u8217'ah yemallel} \u8212- should this \u8220"cut-off head\u8221" speak? Rashi explains that Yehoshua had no children, and therefore the people reasoned that he had no personal stake in the outcome \u8212- he would not inherit the land himself and thus had no basis to advocate for entering it. His words carried no weight in their eyes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Aruch} offers an alternative interpretation: {\i rosh keti\u8217'ah} alludes to the letter {\i yud} at the beginning of Yehoshua\u8217's name. Moshe had renamed him {\i Yehoshua} \u8212- adding a {\i yud} to {\i Hoshea ben Nun} \u8212- and a {\i yud} is a tiny, seemingly \u8220"cut-off\u8221" letter. The remark was simply a dismissive insult: this shrimp should speak? Either way, Kalev saw that whatever Yehoshua said, the people would find some way to be dismissive of him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kalev\u8217's Counterargument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So Kalev calculated differently. He reasoned: {\i im ishta\u8217'ina, amrei bi milta, vachasmi li} \u8212- if I try to speak directly against them, they\u8217'll find a way to silence me too. So he said to them: {\i vachi zo bilvad asa lanu Ben Amram} \u8212- is this the only thing the son of Amram did to us? Note that he uses the dismissive epithet {\i Ben Amram} \u8212- a degrading way of referring to Moshe \u8212- as a rhetorical device. The people thought he was about to say something against Moshe and fell silent to hear the complaint. He had them. And then he pivoted: {\i hotzianu miMitzrayim, uvaka lanu et hayam, vehe\u8217'echilanu et haman} \u8212- he took us out of Egypt, split the sea, fed us the manna. And even if he told us to make ladders and climb to heaven, would we not listen? The structure is a direct parallel \u8212- and almost the inverse \u8212- of what the {\i gemara} said about {\i lashon hara} that must begin with truth. Here, a false premise \u8212- the suggestion that Moshe had wronged them \u8212- was used to gain the audience\u8217's attention, and then the truth was deployed to make the real point. It is, as one student aptly noted, a case of coming to praise Caesar by first seeming to come to bury him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Consumes Inhabitants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the body of the spies\u8217' report: {\i eretz ochelet yoshveha hi} \u8212- it is a land that consumes its inhabitants. Rava expounds: God says, {\i ani chashavti letova} \u8212- I arranged this for their benefit. Whenever the spies passed through, a prominent person among the local inhabitants died \u8212- {\i meis chashiva didehu ki heichi denitardu} \u8212- so that the locals would be distracted by mourning and would not investigate who these strangers were. There is a second version: it was Iyov who died at that time, and all the world was occupied with his {\i hespeda}, which additionally removed the {\i zechut} that had protected the Canaanites \u8212- {\i sar tzilam me\u8217'aleihem} \u8212- since Iyov\u8217's merit had served as a kind of shield for them. But what the spies interpreted as ominous devastation was in fact divine protection in disguise. {\i Hem chashvu leraa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liars or Interpreters?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the {\i gemara} reaches the spies\u8217' claim: {\i vanehi be\u8217'eineinu kachagavim vechen hayinu be\u8217'eineihem} \u8212- we were in our own eyes like grasshoppers and so too in their eyes. Hamasharshiya declares bluntly: {\i meraglim shikrei havu} \u8212- the spies were liars. This is presented as something of a {\i chidush}, since until this point the spies had not said anything demonstrably false \u8212- {\i eretz ochelet yoshveha} is an interpretation of facts, not a fabrication. But now Hamasharshiya says outright they were lying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bishlama vanehi be\u8217'eineinu kachagavim lachayei} \u8212- granted, their self-assessment as grasshoppers is subjective and could be true. {\i Ela vechen hayinu be\u8217'eineihem mna yadei} \u8212- but how did they know how they appeared to the Canaanites?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back \u8212- {\i velo hi} \u8212- and offers a resolution. Since there were constant deaths among the inhabitants, the spies would observe them providing {\i havra\u8217'ah} \u8212- the meal of condolence \u8212- to mourners under cedar trees. Bnei Yisrael, needing to conceal themselves, climbed up into those very cedar trees. From there they overheard the locals saying: {\i kachazinan inshei dedamu lekamzei be\u8217'ilanei} \u8212- we see people up there that look just like grasshoppers in the trees. The cedar trees appear in the story for a simple reason: they had to be elevated to hear what was being said at the level of giants, and concealment in the trees is what allowed them to overhear the remark at all. So the {\i gemara} concludes: they were not liars \u8212- they had actual evidence for their claim.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Punishment & Plague\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crying & Tisha B\u8217'Av\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse states: {\i vatissa kol ha\u8217'eda vayitnu et kolam vayivku ha\u8217'am balayla hahu} \u8212- the entire congregation lifted their voice and the people wept on that night. Rabba in the name of Rabbi Yochanan identifies the timing: {\i oto halayla erev Tisha B\u8217'Av haya} \u8212- that night was the eve of the 9th of Av. God declared: {\i hem bachu bechiya shel chinam} \u8212- they wept for nothing. So God responded: {\i ani ekba lahem bechiya l\u8217'dorot} \u8212- I will establish for them a night of weeping for all generations. The report of the {\i meraglim} thus marks the very first of the catastrophes associated with Tisha B\u8217'Av.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stoning & Divine Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse continues: {\i vayomru kol ha\u8217'eda lirgom otam ba\u8217'avanim} \u8212- the congregation said to stone them with stones. Immediately the verse adds that {\i kevod Hashem} appeared in the {\i Ohel Mo\u8217'ed}. Rabbi Chiya bar Abba teaches: this teaches that they actually took stones and threw them upward \u8212- {\i klapei ma\u8217'ala} \u8212- toward God Himself. This reading connects to the earlier teaching about {\i mimenu} \u8212- greater than Him \u8212- in which the spies had implicitly disparaged even God\u8217's power. The throwing of stones at the divine presence is of a piece with that blasphemy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spies\u8217' Plague\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse then records: {\i vayamutu ha\u8217'anashim motzi\u8217'ei dibat ha\u8217'aretz ra\u8217'ah ba-mageifah} \u8212- the men who spread the evil report of the land died in the plague. The word {\i ra\u8217'ah} is read in conjunction with {\i mageifah} \u8212- it was a particularly terrible plague, a {\i mitah meshunah}, an unusual death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Worm Cycle Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish teaches, citing the {\i drasha} of Rav Shila of Kefar Tamarta: their tongues stretched downward and fell to their navels \u8212- {\i nishtarbev lishonam v\u8217'nafal al tiburam} \u8212- and worms emerged from their tongues and entered their navels, and then cycled back from their navels to their tongues. The punishment was calibrated to the sin: they had misused their speech through {\i lashon hara}, and so the tongue became the locus of their grotesque death. The {\i tibur} \u8212- the navel \u8212- is significant as well, since the {\i gemara} in Yoma discusses the navel as a marker of the seat of life; the worms thus invaded the very center of their being and cycled between the organ of their sin and the core of their existence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak offers an alternative: they died of {\i askara}, croup \u8212- again, a disease of the throat and tongue, fitting punishment for those who destroyed through speech.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Jordan Crossing & Ar (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waters Return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns now from the digression about the {\i meraglim} to the narrative of Yehoshua. {\i V\u8217'keivan she-alah ha-acharon she-b\u8217'Yisrael min ha-Yarden} \u8212- when the last Israelite left the Jordan, {\i chazru mayim limkomam} \u8212- the waters returned to their place. The verse quoted is {\i vayhi ba-alot ha-kohanim nosei aron brit Hashem mi-toch ha-Yarden}, when the {\i kohanim} emerged from the Jordan, at which point {\i nitku kapot raglei ha-kohanim el ha-charavah} \u8212- the soles of their feet stepped onto dry land \u8212- and {\i vayashuvu mei ha-Yarden limkomam}, the waters returned. They returned {\i al kol gedotav} \u8212- to their full banks \u8212- because, as the earlier {\i psukim} indicate, the Jordan was at peak strength following the snowmelt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ark Lifts Carriers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A problem arises when one reads the {\i psukim} carefully. The Jordan split as soon as the {\i kohanim} stepped in \u8212- {\i as soon as their feet touched the water, the waters stopped}. That means the {\i kohanim} themselves never crossed the Jordan; they stepped in from the east bank, the waters held, all of Israel passed through, and then the {\i kohanim} stepped back out \u8212- onto the east bank, not the west. The {\i gemara} states this explicitly: {\i nimtza aron v\u8217'nos\u8217'av v\u8217'kohanim mi-tzad echad} \u8212- the {\i aron} and its carriers and the {\i kohanim} ended up on the east side of the Jordan, while all of Israel was on the west side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i nassa aron et nosav} \u8212- the {\i aron} lifted up those who were carrying it and miraculously transported them across. The {\i aron} was {\i nosei et nosav} \u8212- it carried those who bore it. The proof-text is the verse {\i vayhi ka\u8217'asher tam kol ha\u8217'am la\u8217'avor vaya\u8217'avor aron Hashem v\u8217'ha-kohanim lifnei ha\u8217'am} \u8212- when all the people had finished crossing, the {\i aron} passed and the {\i kohanim} were before the people. The logical reading of that verse is that the {\i kohanim} caused the {\i aron} to pass; but the {\i gemara} inverts this: it was the {\i aron} that passed and, in doing so, carried the {\i kohanim} over.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Uzza\u8217's Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uzza\u8217's Error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It was precisely a failure to appreciate this power of the {\i aron} that brought about the punishment of Uzza. The verse recounts how, during King David\u8217's attempt to transport the {\i aron} to Jerusalem, they arrived at {\i goren Kidon}. {\i Vayishlach Uza yado le\u8217'echoz et ha\u8217'aron} \u8212- Uzza extended his hand to grab the {\i aron}, thinking the oxen had slipped and it was about to fall \u8212- {\i ki shamtu ha-bakar}. He was struck down on the spot.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Uzza\u8217's Punishment (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Rebuke\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames God\u8217's rebuke of Uzza as follows: {\i nosav nasa, atzmo lo kol sheken} \u8212- the {\i aron} lifted those who were carrying it; surely it can take care of itself without your assistance. {\i Vayichar af Hashem b\u8217'Uzza vayakehu sham al ha-shal} \u8212- God\u8217's anger burned against Uzza and He smote him there on the {\i shal}, the error. The {\i gemara} emphasizes the word {\i shal} \u8212- it was not {\i meizid}, a deliberate sin. Uzza was trying to do something that appeared good. Yet the consequence was fatal \u8212- a pattern recognizable from the story of Nadav and Avihu, and from the broader principle that God holds the righteous to a more exacting standard, {\i medakdek im ha-tzadikim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The power-plant analogy is apt here: the presence of God is like a power plant that gives light and energy to the entire world, but proximity without proper protection \u8212- approaching incorrectly, doing the wrong thing \u8212- carries severe consequences. This is not a negation of the source\u8217's beneficence; it is a function of its intensity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uzza\u8217's Merit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Elazar debate the nature of {\i al ha-shal}. One says it refers to the error itself \u8212- the act, though well-intentioned, constituted a transgression of protocol. The other says {\i shal} means he relieved himself in its presence \u8212- a reading that attempts to account for why the consequences were so severe by suggesting a secondary disrespect. Rashi reads {\i shal} through the lens of {\i nashal zeitecha}, something that falls away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, however, consistent with his framing of Uzza\u8217's act as an error rather than deliberate sin, rules that {\i Uza ba le\u8217'Olam Haba} \u8212- Uzza enters the world to come. The proof-text is the verse {\i vayamat sham im Aron Ha\u8217'elokim} \u8212- he died there {\i with} the {\i aron} of God. Just as the {\i aron} endures forever, so too Uzza will come into the world to come.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David\u8217's Zmirot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: why was David also deserving of punishment in the context of this episode? {\i Mipnei she-kara l\u8217'divrei Torah zmirot} \u8212- because he referred to the words of Torah as {\i zmirot}, as songs. The reference is to the verse in Tehillim: {\i zmirot hayu li chukecha b\u8217'veit megurai} \u8212- your statutes were to me like {\i zmirot} in the house of my dwelling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requires some unpacking. David\u8217's dancing before the {\i aron} is not the problem \u8212- that was an expression of joy and was regarded as proper honor. The concern is specifically that he characterized the {\i chukim} \u8212- the statutes of Torah \u8212- as {\i zmirot}. {\i Shira} is elevated poetic composition; {\i zemer} is a common tune. The distinction matters because one is expressing that Torah is a source of joy, which is legitimate, while the other is recasting the very genre of the words themselves \u8212- as if calling Torah a {\i zemer} places it in a different literary category, something lighter, something comparable to any pleasurable reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a contrast: if David had said Torah is a {\i sha\u8217'ashua}, a plaything, that remains clearly a metaphor for how one experiences it. But calling it {\i zmirot} is recasting the text itself as belonging to a different genre of writing, and that is what seems to trouble the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Treating Torah Lightly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God\u8217's response to David is: {\i divrei Torah she-katuv bahem hata\u8217'if einecha bo v\u8217'eineno} \u8212- the words of Torah are such that if you but flutter your eye away, they disappear. You called them {\i zmirot}? {\i Hareini machshilcha b\u8217'davar she-afilu tinokot shel beit raban yod\u8217'in oto} \u8212- I will cause you to stumble in a matter that even schoolchildren know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection between the {\i zmirot} rebuke and the {\i hata\u8217'if einecha} verse may initially seem opaque. But the logic runs as follows: if you treat Torah only as a source of pleasant entertainment \u8212- {\i zmirot} \u8212- and never as something requiring serious, careful attention, then you will not pay sufficient care to its words. That carelessness will cost you. And indeed: {\i u\u8217'vnei Kehos lo nasan ki avodat ha-kodesh aleihem b\u8217'katef yisa\u8217'u} \u8212- the Torah states explicitly that the {\i aron} was to be carried on the shoulder, not on a wagon. Every schoolchild knows this. Yet David had the {\i aron} transported on a wagon \u8212- {\i va\u8217'atun asinu ba\u8217'agalah} \u8212- an error that directly precipitated the tragedy of Uzza. The consequence of treating Torah lightly is that one commits errors that children know better than to make.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Jordan Crossing & Ar (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ark\u8217's Autonomy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader principle the {\i gemara} has been developing is that the {\i aron} is not merely a sacred object that requires protection \u8212- it is itself a source of divine power that protects those who carry it, indeed that carries those who carry it. This is the inverse of what Uzza assumed when he reached out to steady it. The {\i aron} is {\i nosei et nosav}: it bears its bearers. Like a power plant, it is the source of energy and protection for those who relate to it correctly; but approached incorrectly, it is lethal. This principle connects the miraculous transport of the {\i kohanim} across the Jordan, the death of Uzza, the smiting of the men of Beit Shemesh who looked at the {\i aron} dismissively while continuing to harvest \u8212- and, in a different register, the punishment of David for treating the Torah with insufficient gravity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} relates the episode of Beit Shemesh as well: when the {\i aron} was returned by the Philistines, the people of Beit Shemesh were smitten. One opinion holds that the problem was they kept on harvesting in the field while the {\i aron} passed through \u8212- continuing their mundane work without pausing to acknowledge the moment. A second opinion holds that they went further and spoke dismissively of it: {\i man amricha de\u8217'mrayat, u\u8217'man asach de\u8217'puyasat} \u8212- who got you so angry? And who appeased you? \u8212- addressing the {\i aron} in a personified but contemptuous fashion. Either way \u8212- passive impassivity or active dismissal \u8212- the {\i aron} is not an object that tolerates being taken lightly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Stone Sets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe\u8217's Stones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moving on, the {\i gemara} establishes that there were three distinct sets of stones erected in connection with the giving and writing of the Torah. The first was erected by Moshe in {\i Eretz Moav}, before the crossing of the Jordan. The connection is made through the parallel language of {\i be\u8217'er}: the opening of Devarim says {\i ho\u8217'il Moshe be\u8217'er et ha-Torah ha-zot} \u8212- Moshe began to explicate this Torah \u8212- and the commandment later uses {\i ba\u8217'er heiteiv}, explicate well. The {\i gemara} links the two instances of {\i be\u8217'er} to establish that Moshe himself performed a similar act of inscription.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua\u8217's Stones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second set was erected by Yehoshua within the Jordan itself. The verse states: {\i ushteim esreh avanim hekim Yehoshua besoch ha-Yarden} \u8212- Yehoshua erected twelve stones in the midst of the Jordan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gilgal Stones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third set was the re-erection of those same twelve stones at Gilgal after the crossing. The verse states that they took the stones from the Jordan and erected them at Gilgal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing Method Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} addresses the question of how exactly Israel wrote the Torah on the stones, given that the Torah also commands {\i vesadta osam ba-sid} \u8212- you shall plaster them with plaster. The intersection of carved inscription and plaster covering requires explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i al gabei avanim kesavuha} \u8212- they wrote it directly on the stones, as the verse states, {\i vechasavta al ha-avanim et kol divrei ha-Torah ha-zot}. And afterward, {\i sadu osan ba-sid} \u8212- they covered the stones with plaster. Presumably the inscription was carved into the stone, so it would remain legible beneath the plaster, though the readability would certainly be compromised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon challenges Rabbi Yehuda directly: {\i lidvarecha, heich lamdu umot she-be\u8217'oto ha-zman Torah} \u8212- according to your view, how did the nations of that time learn Torah? This is a striking assumption embedded in the question: the purpose of writing the Torah {\i ba\u8217'er heiteiv} in {\i shivim lashon} was not merely internal \u8212- not merely so that Israel would know its own covenant \u8212- but so that the nations would have access to it. The Torah of Israel was to serve as a model for the world. If the inscription was then covered with plaster, how could the nations have read it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plaster Printing Press\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda responds: {\i binah yeseirah nasan bahem ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- God granted the nations particular wisdom and understanding. They sent their scribes, {\i veshigru notirin shelahem}, and those scribes {\i vekilpu et ha-sid} \u8212- peeled off the plaster \u8212- and carried it away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, on reflection, a brilliant solution. When text is carved into stone and plaster is poured over it, the plaster hardens in relief, with the letters raised on its surface \u8212- which is precisely how printing type functions. The peeled plaster becomes a perfect printing surface, a mirror image that can be pressed onto other materials to produce copies. The nations sent their scribes, who removed the plaster and thereby obtained what amounts to a printing press for the Torah in seventy languages. The {\i ba\u8217'er heiteiv} \u8212- the clear, detailed exposition \u8212- was not destroyed when the plaster was applied; it was preserved in a form ideally suited for distribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing on Plaster\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's own alternative view is: {\i al gabei sid ktavuha} \u8212- they wrote directly on top of the plaster, not on the stones beneath. And underneath \u8212- {\i vechatav lahem lemata} \u8212- was written the verse {\i leman asher lo yilamdu etchem la\u8217'asot kechol ha-to\u8217'evot} \u8212- so that they should not teach you to do all of their abominations \u8212- a verse about why the seven nations must be destroyed. On Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view as well, the plaster was pulled off and sent to the nations, but it was written on top of the plaster rather than transferred from the stone beneath. Either way, both Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon are answering the same practical question: if the writing was meant for the {\i umot ha-olam}, how did they receive it when the stones stood in Eretz Yisrael? The answer is that the plaster itself \u8212- whether as printing press or as inscribed surface \u8212- was sent to them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nations & Repentance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nations Could Repent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava bar Shila asks: what is the reasoning behind Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view? The verse beneath the inscription \u8212- {\i leman asher lo yilamdu etchem} \u8212- contains within it an implication: {\i ha lamadeta she\u8217'im hayu chozrim bi-teshuva, hayu mekabelin otan} \u8212- this teaches that had the nations repented, they would have been accepted. The very fact that God provided the Torah to the nations \u8212- that it was published for them in seventy languages \u8212- meant that repentance and acceptance were genuinely available to them. Rabbi Shimon understands that even the seven nations, had they changed their ways, could have been received rather than destroyed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi extends this: not only would they not have been destroyed, they could even have converted. The publication of Torah in seventy languages was a genuine invitation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plaster Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse then cited is: {\i vehayu amim misrephot sid} \u8212- the nations will be like the burning of a plaster pit. Rava bar Shila reads this as {\i al iskei sid} \u8212- because of the plaster. The nations had the Torah given to them through the medium of the plaster in seventy languages, and they failed to learn from it. Therefore they will burn like plaster. The plaster that was meant to transmit Torah to them becomes the symbol of their judgment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Nations Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda reads the same imagery differently: {\i mah sid ein lo takana ela sreifa} \u8212- just as plaster has no remedy other than burning, {\i af otam ovdei kochavim ein lahem takana ela sreifa} \u8212- so too those nations have no remedy other than destruction. On this reading, the {\i gemara} is establishing a debate specifically about the {\i shiva amamin}, the seven Canaanite nations. Rabbi Shimon holds that even they, had they repented upon receiving the Torah through the plaster, would have been accepted. Rabbi Yehuda holds that {\i ein lahem takana} \u8212- they were destined for destruction regardless, with no possibility of reprieve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This debate is focused specifically on the seven nations and not on the {\i umot ha-olam} generally. Regarding the general nations, neither opinion could hold {\i ein lahem takana} \u8212- otherwise the entire exercise of publishing Torah in seventy languages would have been pointless. The debate is thus a narrow one about whether the Canaanite nations, whom Israel was commanded to destroy upon entering the land, had any theoretical avenue of escape through repentance \u8212- and on this, Rabbi Shimon and Rabbi Yehuda disagree.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 36\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stones & Torah Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesukim Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin at the bottom of 35b, in the midst of a debate concerning the details surrounding the erection of the stones and the writing of the Torah. Before proceeding, a correction is in order regarding what was said in the previous session. A more careful reading of the relevant {\i pesukim} clarifies considerably what the {\i gemara} is about to do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Stone Sets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies three distinct sets of stones. The first are those that Moshe erected on his side of the {\i Yarden} \u8212- a fact not stated explicitly in the {\i pesukim} but derived by the {\i gemara} through a {\i gezeira shava} on the word {\i be\u8217'er}, \u8220"well.\u8221" The second are the stones that were set in the {\i Yarden} itself: {\i vayusham sham ad hayom hazeh} \u8212- they were erected there and remain there to this day, hidden beneath the water, their erection symbolic, their presence known but unseen. These are distinct from the third set, the twelve stones taken {\i out} of the {\i Yarden} and subsequently erected in {\i Gilgal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deuteronomy 27 Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to examine the {\i pesukim} in Devarim 27 carefully, because they generate a significant textual ambiguity. The Torah commands: {\i ve-hayah bayom asher tavru et ha-Yarden\u8230 ? ve-hakemota lecha avanim gedolot ve-sata otam ba-sid} \u8212- on the day you cross the {\i Yarden}, you shall erect large stones and plaster them with lime. It then commands: {\i ve-katavta aleihem et kol divrei ha-Torah ha-zot} \u8212- and you shall write upon them the entire Torah. So far, it appears the Torah is to be written on the erected stones. The text then continues: {\i ve-hayah be-ovrechem et ha-Yarden takimu et ha-avanim ha-eleh\u8230 ? be-Har Eival ve-sata otam ba-sid} \u8212- you shall erect these stones on Har Eival and plaster them. Then follows the command to build a {\i mizbeiach} of whole, unhewn stones, offer {\i olot} and {\i shelamim}, eat, and rejoice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ambiguity is this: the Torah speaks of writing on the stones and then speaks of building a {\i mizbeiach} from {\i avanim shelemot}, and then concludes with the instruction to write {\i et divrei ha-Torah ha-zot ba\u8217'er hetev} \u8212- the words of this Torah, explained well. Are we returning to the erected stones, or is this writing upon the {\i avnei ha-mizbeiach}? The {\i pesukim} are genuinely ambiguous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Malon vs.\u160 ?Mizbe\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} on 35b, {\i keitzad katvu Yisrael et ha-Torah}, addresses this directly. A {\i Tosefta} records that Rabbi Yehudah holds {\i al gabi ha-mizbeiach katvuha} \u8212- it was written on the {\i mizbeiach}. But the {\i Yerushalmi} preserves a different tradition: {\i al avnei ha-malon nichtevu} \u8212- it was written on the stones of the lodging place, i.e., the erected stones. The practical difference is significant. According to the view that it was written on the {\i avnei ha-malon}, the nations of the world could send their scribes day after day to transcribe the Torah, which was written in seventy languages, from this enduring public monument. According to the view that it was written on the {\i avnei ha-mizbeiach} \u8212- {\i lo le-sha\u8217'ah hayu ve-nignazu}, they were not permanent and were eventually hidden away. The {\i gemara} there resolves that even so, God placed understanding in the heart of every nation and they somehow acquired the Torah that was written in seventy languages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} also raises the linguistic difficulty: for those who say it was written on the {\i avnei ha-mizbeiach}, what does {\i ve-sata otam ba-sid} \u8212- and you shall plaster them \u8212- refer to? The answer given is {\i bein kol even va-even} \u8212- the plaster between each stone, i.e., the mortar binding the {\i mizbeiach} together, not a surface coating for writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Separate Events\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a second layer of complexity that emerges from the narrative in {\i Yehoshua}, namely the apparent separation of the stone erection and the Har Gerizim/Har Eival ceremony into two distinct events. The Torah in Devarim seems to present everything \u8212- erecting the stones, writing the Torah, building the {\i mizbeiach}, and the {\i brachot} and {\i klalos} \u8212- as one unified event at Har Gerizim and Har Eival. But in {\i Yehoshua}, these come apart.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geographic Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A look at the geography and the narrative sequence in {\i Yehoshua} reveals the full extent of this problem. {\i Gilgal}, where the Israelites camped after crossing the {\i Yarden}, was right at the eastern edge of {\i Yericho}: {\i vayachanu ba-Gilgal biktzeh mizrach Yericho}. Har Gerizim and Har Eival are far to the interior \u8212- the {\i gemara} later says more than sixty {\i mil} away, a journey of multiple days. And the narrative in {\i Yehoshua} does not conflate these events at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Perek 4 of {\i Yehoshua} records that {\i Yehoshua} erected the twelve stones taken from the {\i Yarden} in {\i Gilgal}, with no mention of writing Torah and no mention of building a {\i mizbeiach}: {\i ve-et shteim esrei ha-avanim ha-eleh asher lakechu min ha-Yarden hekim Yehoshua ba-Gilgal}. The purpose stated for the stones is purely commemorative \u8212- so that future generations would know that Israel crossed the {\i Yarden} on dry land, {\i lema\u8217'an da\u8217'at kol amei ha-aretz ki yad Hashem ki chazaka hi}. Then the narrative proceeds to the wars of {\i Yericho} and {\i Ha\u8217'Ai}, and the episode of {\i Achan}, spanning multiple {\i perakim} of wars and battles. Only at the end of Perek 8 does the text say: {\i az yivneh Yehoshua mizbe\u8217'ach l\u8217'Hashem Elokei Yisrael be-Har Eival} \u8212- and there, in Perek 8, it describes the building of the {\i mizbeiach}, the writing: {\i vayichtav sham al ha-avanim mishne Torat Moshe}, and the performance of the {\i brachot} and {\i klalos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means that according to the plain reading of {\i Yehoshua}, the erection of the stones happened immediately after crossing the {\i Yarden}, and the {\i mizbeiach}, the writing of the Torah, and the {\i brachot} and {\i klalos} happened weeks or months later, well into the land, after several significant military campaigns. The Torah\u8217's account in Devarim, by contrast, presents it all as a single integrated sequence of events occurring upon entry. The {\i gemara} is faced with the task of reconciling these two very different presentations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Malbim\u8217's Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One approach to the ambiguity of the sequence of plastering and writing is that of the {\i Malbim}, who proposes that the writing of the Torah on the plaster occurred at multiple stages. His view is that first, while the plaster was just beginning to dry \u8212- a state when one can still inscribe into it \u8212- they wrote the Torah. This would correspond to what occurred at or near the {\i Yarden}. Subsequently, when they built the {\i mizbeiach} and the {\i olot} were offered, the stones were plastered again, the plaster dried somewhat, and they wrote the Torah yet again. This approach attempts to honor both the Devarim and the {\i Yehoshua} accounts by distributing the writing across different moments. However, this is a complex theory and we must set it aside for now to turn to what the {\i gemara} itself requires.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teshuvah & Seven Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava on Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the main {\i gemara}: the discussion concerns a debate between Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Shimon regarding the nations who witnessed the writing of the Torah on the stones. Six lines from the bottom of 35b, {\i Rava bar Shila} explains the reasoning behind Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position. The verse states: {\i ve-hayu amim misrofot sid} \u8212- the nations shall be as burning lime. Rabbi Shimon reads this {\i al iskei sid} \u8212- as referring to the matter of the lime, the plaster. Had the nations learned from what was written on the plaster, they would not have been destroyed. The implication of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view is that the option of {\i teshuvah} and acceptance was genuinely open to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah, by contrast, reads {\i kesid} \u8212- just as lime has no remedy other than burning, so too these nations have no remedy other than destruction. For Rabbi Yehudah, their fate is unconditional; {\i teshuvah} would not have altered it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yefat Toar & Canaanites\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited that connects to this debate: {\i ve-shavita shivyo} \u8212- the laws of {\i yefat toar} \u8212- is read as including {\i Kena\u8217'anim} who were outside the land of Israel, meaning that if they were to do {\i teshuvah} they would be accepted: {\i im chozrin be-teshuvah mekabelin otam}. The {\i gemara} concludes that this {\i baraita} follows the position of Rabbi Shimon, since it is Rabbi Shimon who holds that {\i teshuvah} would have been effective for the seven nations. Rabbi Yehudah, by contrast, maintains that the obligation to destroy the {\i shiva umot} applies regardless, even outside of Israel, even if they do {\i teshuvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Rachav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a sharp question: {\i teima heich kibelu la l\u8217'Rachav} \u8212- how was {\i Rachav} accepted, given that she was from {\i Kena\u8217'an} within the land, as is established in the {\i Sifri}? This is a serious difficulty for any reading that would accept {\i Kena\u8217'anim} under certain conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yefat Toar Override?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses both this question and a related difficulty. Regarding {\i Rachav}, {\i Tosafot} explains: {\i im hayu chozrin az be-teshuvah be-sha\u8217'ah she-kasav et ha-Torah, afilu Kena\u8217'anim she-be-tocha hayu mekabelin} \u8212- had the {\i Kena\u8217'anim} done {\i teshuvah} at the time the Torah was written on the stones, even those within the land would have been accepted, and this holds even according to Rabbi Yehudah. The critical distinction is the timing. The debate about whether {\i teshuvah} is effective pertains to the period after the war had already begun. {\i Rachav}\u8217's acceptance thus occurred within the pre-war window, before the conflict was joined in earnest. Once war commenced, that is where the dispute between Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Shimon becomes operative, and Rabbi Yehudah holds that subsequent {\i teshuvah} would not exempt them \u8212- whereas the {\i Kena\u8217'anim} outside the land remain under discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second difficulty {\i Tosafot} raises is whether {\i yefat toar} could itself constitute an exception to the obligation to destroy the {\i shiva umot}: {\i dilma be-yefat toar afilu Rabbi Yehudah modeh} \u8212- perhaps even Rabbi Yehudah would agree that the laws of {\i yefat toar} override the obligation to destroy them, given that the {\i gemara} holds {\i yefat toar} waives even the prohibitions of {\i kehunah}. {\i Tosafot} responds that the laws permitting sexual relations with a captive woman do not carry with them an exemption from the separate affirmative {\i mitzvah} to destroy the seven nations. The {\i mitzvah} to eliminate the {\i shiva umot} outside of Israel, according to Rabbi Yehudah, would not be overridden by the {\i heter} of {\i yefat toar}. These are, in sum, several intertwined questions: the degree to which there is an obligation to destroy the {\i shiva umot} outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, whether {\i teshuvah} would have constituted a remedy, how to handle the case of {\i Rachav}, and whether {\i yefat toar} could serve as a basis for exception.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One Day Narrative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sixty Mil Journey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the central narrative and its harmonization of the {\i Yehoshua} account with the Devarim commands. {\i Bo re\u8217'eh} \u8212- come and see \u8212- {\i kama nisim na\u8217'asu be-oto ha-yom}: how many miracles were performed on that very day they crossed the {\i Yarden}. The {\i gemara} states that {\i Bnei Yisrael} crossed the {\i Yarden} and then came to Har Gerizim and Har Eival \u8212- {\i yoter mi-shishim mil}, more than sixty {\i mil} away, which is approximately sixty kilometers. This is a remarkable claim. The point is precisely that the {\i gemara} is taking the events of Perek 8 of {\i Yehoshua} \u8212- the {\i mizbeiach}, the writing of the Torah, the {\i brachot} and {\i klalos} \u8212- and asserting they occurred on the same day as the crossing of the {\i Yarden}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bracketing Battles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requires bracketing the battles described in Perakim 6 and 7 of {\i Yehoshua} \u8212- the conquest of {\i Yericho} and the campaign against {\i Ha\u8217'Ai} \u8212- and treating them as narratively displaced from their chronological position. This is essentially the principle of {\i ein mukdam u-me\u8217'uchar ba-Torah} applied to the book of {\i Yehoshua}: the battles of {\i Yericho} and {\i Ha\u8217'Ai} did not occur on that first day but are recorded out of sequence. {\i V\u8217'ein kol beryah yechola la\u8217'amod bifneihem} \u8212- no creature was able to stand against {\i Bnei Yisrael}; {\i ve-chol ha-omed bifneihem haya nisraz} \u8212- anyone who tried was immediately, let us say, undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fear Before Them\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws upon a verse: {\i ve-es emasi ashalach lefanecha ve-hamoti et kol ha-am} \u8212- I will send My fear before you and I will disturb all the people. {\i Rashi} explains {\i ve-hamoti} as {\i lashon mehumah ve-irvuv ha-guf mah she-betocho} \u8212- a language of confusion and disordering of the inner body, i.e., causing the enemy to lose control of his bowels. And the verse continues: {\i tipol aleihem eimatah va-fachad} \u8212- fear and trembling will fall upon them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then applies a {\i derasha} to the verse {\i ad ya\u8217'avor amcha Hashem / ad ya\u8217'avor am zu kanita} \u8212- the two halves of the verse are read as referring to two distinct entries into the land. The first half, {\i ad ya\u8217'avor amcha Hashem}, refers to {\i bi\u8217'ah rishonah}, the first entry in the time of {\i Yehoshua}. The second half, {\i ad ya\u8217'avor am zu kanita}, this nation that You have acquired, refers to {\i bi\u8217'ah sheniyah}, the second entry in the time of {\i Ezra}. The {\i gemara} observes: {\i amru me\u8217'atah re\u8217'uyim hayu Yisrael la\u8217'asot lahem nes be-vi\u8217'ah sheniyah ke-vi\u8217'ah rishonah} \u8212- it would have been fitting for miracles to accompany the second entry as they had the first. {\i Ela she-garam ha-chet} \u8212- but sin caused otherwise. {\i Rashi} makes the important observation that it was not merely the absence of miracles that was at stake, but the fundamental nature of the entry itself: in the second entry, {\i Bnei Yisrael} entered as subjects of the Babylonian kingdom, {\i mishta\u8217'abdim le-malchut}, settling the land by permission rather than conquering it as a sovereign nation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stones Round Trip\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues: {\i ve-achar kach hevi\u8217'u et ha-avanim u-vanu et ha-mizbe\u8217'ach ve-sadu va-sid ve-chatvu aleihem et kol divrei ha-Torah be-shivim lashon}. This is the {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution of the ambiguity in the {\i pesukim}: the same stones that had been erected in {\i Gilgal} were transported to Har Gerizim and Har Eival, where they were used to build the {\i mizbeiach}, plastered, and the Torah was written upon them in seventy languages. The {\i olot} and {\i shelamim} were offered, they ate, drank, rejoiced, performed the {\i brachot} and the {\i klalos} \u8212- {\i ve-kiplu et ha-avanim} \u8212- and then folded up, so to speak, the stones. This phrase raises a question: if the stones were brought all the way to Har Gerizim and Har Eival, when did they get erected in {\i Gilgal}, as {\i Yehoshua} describes? The answer is that after all this was done, they brought the stones back. {\i Ve-ba\u8217'u va-lanu be-Gilgal} \u8212- and they came and lodged in {\i Gilgal}. As the verse says, {\i ve-ha\u8217'avartem otam imachem ve-hinachtem otam ba-malon} \u8212- you shall carry them with you and place them at the lodging place. One might ask: perhaps any lodging place would do? The {\i gemara} derives from the phrase {\i asher talinu bo ha-lailah} \u8212- where you sleep that night \u8212- that it refers to one specific place. And then the verse in {\i Yehoshua} records: {\i ve-et shteim esrei ha-avanim ha-eleh asher lakechu\u8230 ? hekim Yehoshua ba-Gilgal} \u8212- the twelve stones were erected in {\i Gilgal}. According to the {\i gemara}\u8217's reading, this erection in {\i Gilgal} happened after the round trip to Har Gerizim and Har Eival, not before.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Synthesis & Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to appreciate the full scope of what the {\i gemara} has accomplished here. There were two distinct problems in the {\i pesukim}. The first was the internal ambiguity in Devarim 27: was the Torah written on the erected stones or on the {\i avnei ha-mizbeiach}? The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer is that these were the same stones \u8212- the stones erected in {\i Gilgal} became the stones of the {\i mizbeiach} at Har Gerizim and Har Eival, and the Torah was written on them in that dual capacity. The second problem was the apparent disjunction between Devarim, which presents the erection of the stones, the building of the {\i mizbeiach}, the writing of the Torah, and the {\i brachot} and {\i klalos} as one integrated event, and {\i Yehoshua}, which places the erection of the stones in {\i Gilgal} immediately after the crossing and the {\i mizbeiach} and {\i brachot u-klalos} many chapters and months later in Perek 8. The {\i gemara} resolves this by collapsing both accounts into a single miraculous day: the stones were taken from the {\i Yarden}, transported more than sixty {\i mil} to Har Gerizim and Har Eival, used for the {\i mizbeiach} and the writing of the Torah, and then brought back to {\i Gilgal} \u8212- all on the same day \u8212- while the battles of {\i Yericho} and {\i Ha\u8217'Ai} are treated as occurring at a later time, bracketed out of chronological sequence. This is a {\i gemara} that is solving problems in the {\i pesukim} that one would not have recognized without reading the narrative of {\i Yehoshua} alongside Devarim with great care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzira Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i tanna}: {\i tzira lo avra imahen} \u8212- the {\i tzira}, the wasps, did not cross over with them into {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The verse had promised: {\i ve-shalachti et ha-tzira lefanecha} \u8212- I will send the wasps before you. So why does the {\i baraita} say they did not cross? Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish explains: {\i al sfat ha-Yarden amda} \u8212- they stood at the bank of the {\i Yarden}. They did not enter the land but remained hovering at its border, and from there they launched their attack. {\i Ve-zarka bahen mara} \u8212- they spat their venom across, blinding the enemy: {\i ve-simta eineihem mi-l\u8217'mala} \u8212- blinded their eyes above \u8212- {\i ve-sersatan mi-l\u8217'mata} \u8212- and castrated them below. This was, in effect, a long-range assault \u8212- a kind of guided weapon that operated from outside the border of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The verse in Amos confirms both aspects: {\i va-ashmid piryo mi-ma\u8217'al} \u8212- I destroyed the fruit from above \u8212- {\i ve-sharashav mi-tachat} \u8212- and the roots below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Tzira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa offers a different resolution: {\i shtei tzirot havai} \u8212- there were two distinct {\i tzirot}. One was associated with Moshe, and one with {\i Yehoshua}. The {\i tzira} of Moshe did not cross over into {\i Eretz Yisrael}, while the {\i tzira} of {\i Yehoshua} did cross and operated within the land, fulfilling the verse {\i ve-shalachti et ha-tzira lefanecha}. What problem is Rav Papa solving by positing two? It is difficult to say with certainty, and a closer examination of the {\i Maharsha} would be required. The {\i Maharsha} does observe that the verse referring to the {\i tzira} specifies only the {\i Kena\u8217'ani} and the {\i Chiti} and does not mention the other nations \u8212- which may be part of what the {\i gemara} is working through. The matter requires further study.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Division of Tribes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six on Each\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the actual events at Har Gerizim and Har Eival. Six tribes went up to the top of Har Gerizim and six to Har Eival. The verse in {\i Yehoshua} states: {\i chetzyo el mul Har Gerizim ve-ha-chetzyo el mul Har Eival} \u8212- half facing Har Gerizim and the [other] half facing Har Eival. What is the significance of the {\i ve-ha-chetzyo}, the particular phrasing of \u8220"the half\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ephod Stones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana answers: {\i ke-derech she-chalukim kan, kach chalukim be-avnei efod} \u8212- just as they are divided here on the mountains, so are they divided on the stones of the {\i efod}. The {\i ve-ha-chetzyo} alludes to a division recognized elsewhere in the Torah, namely on the two precious stones worn on the shoulders of the {\i Kohen Gadol}. The {\i gemara} challenges this with a {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ephod Letter Count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} teaches: {\i shtei avanim tovot hayu lo le-Kohen Gadol al ketfav} \u8212- the {\i Kohen Gadol} had two precious stones on his shoulders, one on each side. On them were written the names of the twelve tribes: six names on one stone and six on the other. The verse states: {\i shishah mi-shemotam al ha-even ha-achat ve-et shemot ha-shishah ha-no\u8217'tarim al ha-even ha-sheniyah ke-toldotam}. The {\i baraita} notes that {\i ke-toldotam} \u8212- by their birth order \u8212- applies to the second stone but not the first. Why? Because on the first stone, {\i Yehudah} is listed first even though he is the fourth-born, since he is the leader \u8212- {\i mipnei she-Yehudah mukdam}. The {\i baraita} further states: {\i va-chamishim otiyot hayu} \u8212- there were fifty letters in total \u8212- {\i esrim va-chamishah al even zo ve-esrim va-chamishah al even zo} \u8212- twenty-five on each stone, a perfectly equal division.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chanina View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel disagrees with the first opinion. He holds that the division on the {\i avnei efod} did not follow the order found in {\i Chumash ha-Pekudim}, i.e., {\i Bamidbar}, but rather the order found in {\i Chumash Sheni}, i.e., {\i Shemot}. The opening of {\i Shemot} lists: {\i Reuven, Shimon, Levi, Yehudah, Yissachar, Zevulun, u-Vinyamin} \u8212- and then: {\i Dan, Naftali, Gad, ve-Asher} \u8212- and the section concludes with {\i Yosef}. The {\i gemara} explains that in this listing, {\i benei Leah} appear in their birth order together, while {\i benei Rachel} appear one at the beginning ({\i Binyamin}) and one at the end ({\i Yosef}), with the children of the {\i shefachot} in the middle: {\i benei Rachel echad mi-kan ve-echad mi-kan, u-venei shefachot be-emtza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises a difficulty: the verse says the names were inscribed {\i ke-toldotam} \u8212- and the second stone in this arrangement is not in birth order at all. The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i ki-shmos she-kara lahem avihen, ve-lo ki-shmos she-kara lahem Moshe} \u8212- it means by the names their father gave them at birth, not the tribal appellations used by Moshe later. Thus {\i Reuven} and not {\i Re\u8217'uveni}, {\i Shimon} and not {\i Shimoni}, {\i Dan} and not {\i ha-Dani}, {\i Gad} and not {\i ha-Gadi}. The intent is not a sequential birth order but rather personal names given at birth. This is a subtle and meaningful distinction: the {\i avnei efod} bore not the names of the tribes as national entities \u8212- as they appear in the census of {\i Bamidbar} \u8212- but the names of the twelve sons of {\i Yaakov} as individual human beings, as they appear in the opening of {\i Shemot}. There is something deeply personal about this understanding: the {\i Kohen Gadol} carried these twelve men on his shoulders, not twelve tribes in the abstract.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then challenges Rav Kahana: the {\i ve-ha-chetzyo} of Har Gerizim and Har Eival corresponds to neither of these two divisions on the {\i avnei efod}. The arrangement on the mountains \u8212- {\i Shimon, Levi, Yehudah, Yissachar, Yosef, u-Vinyamin} on Har Gerizim, and {\i Reuven, Gad, Asher, Zevulun, Dan, ve-Naftali} on Har Eival \u8212- does not match either the {\i Bamidbar} order or the {\i Shemot} order on the stones of the {\i efod}. {\i Tiyuvta de-Rav Kahana tiyuvta}: Rav Kahana\u8217's position is refuted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ve\u8217'hachetzyo Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore returns to the question: if {\i ve-ha-chetzyo} does not refer to the division of the stones of the {\i efod}, what does it mean? The answer comes from a {\i tanna}: {\i hatziyah shel mul Har Gerizim merubah me-hatziyah shel Har Eival} \u8212- the half facing Har Gerizim was larger in number than the half on Har Eival. The {\i ve-ha-chetzyo} on Har Eival highlights that it was the lesser half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi Below Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for this disparity: {\i mipnei she-Levi lemata} \u8212- because {\i Levi} was below, not on top of Har Gerizim. Since the {\i Levi\u8217'im} and {\i Kohanim} stood below in the valley rather than ascending the mountain, the side of Har Gerizim was diminished by their absence. One might object: {\i adaraba}, since {\i Levi} was below, that should make the Har Gerizim side smaller \u8212- yet the {\i gemara} says it was larger. The {\i gemara} clarifies: even though {\i Levi} was not fully counted in the Har Gerizim side, nevertheless the Har Gerizim side was still numerically greater.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef\u8217's Population\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is {\i bidei Yosef imahem} \u8212- because {\i benei Yosef} were on Har Gerizim, and {\i Yosef}\u8217's population was enormous, more than enough to compensate for the absence of {\i Levi}. The proof is from {\i Yehoshua}: {\i vayidabru bnei Yosef et Yehoshua lemor, madua natata li nachalah goral echad ve-chevel echad va-ani am rav} \u8212- the children of {\i Yosef} complained to {\i Yehoshua} that he had given them only one portion even though they were so numerous. {\i Yehoshua} replied: {\i aleh lecha ha-ya\u8217'arah} \u8212- go up into the forest and take more land. The {\i gemara} interprets this exchange with a remarkable aside: {\i amar lahem lechu ve-hachvi\u8217'u atzmechem ba-ye\u8217'arim she-lo tishlot bachem ayin ha-ra} \u8212- {\i Yehoshua} was in effect telling them: you are so many, go hide yourselves in the forests so the {\i ayin ha-ra} will not afflict you. They responded: {\i zar\u8217'eih de-Yosef lo shaltah beih eina bisha} \u8212- the seed of {\i Yosef} is not subject to the evil eye. The verse itself confirms: {\i ben porat Yosef, ben porat alei ayin} \u8212- Rabbi Abbahu reads {\i alei ayin} not as \u8220"by the spring\u8221" but as {\i olei ayin}, rising above the evil eye. Rabbi Yosei bar Chanina derives the same from the {\i berachah} of {\i Yaakov}: {\i ve-yidgu la-rov be-kerev ha-aretz} \u8212- they shall multiply like fish within the land. Just as fish in the sea are covered by water and the eye has no power over them, so too the descendants of {\i Yosef} are not subject to the {\i ayin ha-ra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Census Math\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} on 36a, {\i mahi ve-ha-chetzyo}, takes the discussion further by actually performing a tabulation from the census at the end of {\i Bamidbar} \u8212- the census taken at the end of the forty years in the wilderness, after the generation of the desert had died, as is established by {\i Seder Olam}. {\i Tosafot} computes the actual numbers tribe by tribe and concludes: {\i timtza be-minyan shvatim she-amdu al Har Gerizim odef al otam she-amdu al Har Eival tish\u8217'at alafim chasar kuf-lamed} \u8212- the tribes on Har Gerizim exceeded those on Har Eival by nine thousand minus 130. So the Har Gerizim side was indeed larger, consistent with the {\i gemara}. Crucially, {\i Levi} numbered approximately eight thousand. If one removes {\i Levi} from the Har Gerizim side, the difference narrows to only about one thousand, rendering the two sides nearly equal. This is entirely consistent with the {\i gemara}\u8217's statement that it was {\i Levi}\u8217's absence below that nearly equalized the sides, and that {\i Yosef}\u8217's large numbers made the difference in keeping Har Gerizim the larger side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fifty Letters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises an arithmetical problem. The {\i baraita} had stated that there were fifty letters across the two stones of the {\i efod}, twenty-five on each. But when one actually counts the letters in the twelve tribal names, the total is forty-nine, not fifty \u8212- and the division is twenty-five and twenty-four, not twenty-five and twenty-five. {\i Hamishim ve-ki chada havei} \u8212- fifty? It is forty-nine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehosef Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak offers the first solution: {\i Yosef hosifu lo ot achat} \u8212- an additional letter was added to {\i Yosef}\u8217's name. The verse states: {\i edut be-Yehosef samo be-tzeto al eretz Mitzrayim} \u8212- there is a verse in {\i Tehillim} where {\i Yosef} appears with an extra {\i heh}, written {\i Yehosef}. It was this spelling that was inscribed on the {\i efod} stone, adding one letter to reach fifty total, with exactly twenty-five on each stone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Binyamin Shalem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak challenges this: the {\i baraita} says {\i ke-toldotam} \u8212- the inscription must reflect the names as given at birth. {\i Yehosef} was not a name by which he was known during his lifetime in that form; it appears only in a late verse in {\i Tehillim}. Therefore, the name {\i Yehosef} does not satisfy the requirement of {\i ketoldotam}. Instead, Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak proposes a different solution: throughout the entire Torah, {\i Binyamin} is written without a {\i yud} \u8212- {\i Binyamin chaser} \u8212- but when he was born and his father named him, the verse reads {\i va-aviv kara lo Binyamin} \u8212- and this spelling is with the full {\i yud}, {\i Binyamin shalem}. It is precisely this birth spelling that satisfies {\i ke-toldotam} \u8212- the name as called at birth \u8212- and that provides the fiftieth letter, yielding twenty-five letters on each stone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosef\u8217's Temptation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef Sanctifies Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since we have been speaking of {\i Yosef} and the extra letter added to his name, the {\i gemara} now tells us why he merited this distinction. Rav Chana bar Bizna in the name of Rabbi Shimon Chasida teaches: {\i Yosef she-kidesh Shem Shamayim be-seter, hosifu lo ot achat mi-shmo shel Hakadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- {\i Yosef}, who sanctified God\u8217's name in private, was rewarded with one letter from God\u8217's name: the {\i heh} of {\i Yehosef}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehudah Public Sanctific\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast is drawn immediately: {\i Yehudah she-kidesh Shem Shamayim be-farhesia, nikra kulo al shemo shel Hakadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- {\i Yehudah}, who sanctified God\u8217's name publicly, merited that his entire name reflects God\u8217's name. Indeed, {\i Yehudah} contains the letters {\i yud, heh, vav, heh} \u8212- the four letters of the divine name \u8212- with only an additional {\i dalet}. We will return tomorrow to the public sanctification of {\i Yehudah}, which the {\i gemara} will identify not with the {\i Yehudah} of this week\u8217's {\i parsha} but with the story of {\i Nachshon ben Aminadav} at the {\i Yam Suf}. For now, the focus is on {\i Yosef}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temptation Scene\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites the verse: {\i va-yehi ke-hayom ha-zeh va-yavo ha-bayta la\u8217'asot melachto} \u8212- it was on such a day and he came to the house to do his work. Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i melamed she-shneihem li-dvar aveirah nitkavnu} \u8212- both of them, {\i Yosef} and the wife of {\i Potiphar}, intended that day to sin. What pushes Rabbi Yochanan to this unflattering reading about {\i Yosef}? {\i Tosafot} explains that it is the juxtaposition of {\i va-yavo ha-bayta} \u8212- \u8220"and he came to the house\u8221" \u8212- a word {\i va-yavo} associated with {\i bi\u8217'ah}, with {\i la\u8217'asot melachto}, doing his work. On this reading, the text hints that his coming was charged with intent. {\i Rav u-Shmuel} debate this directly: one says {\i la\u8217'asot melachto mamash} \u8212- to do his actual work \u8212- and the other says {\i la\u8217'asot tzorachav nichnas} \u8212- he entered to fulfill his desires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-ein ish me-anshei ha-bayit sham ba-bayit} \u8212- none of the household staff was present. How could such a large household be completely empty? {\i Tana de-bei Rabbi Yishmael} explains: {\i oto ha-yom yom chagam haya} \u8212- it was a festival day of theirs, {\i ve-halchu kulam le-veit avodah zarah shelahem} \u8212- and all of them went to their house of idol worship. She told them she was ill and remained behind. {\i Amra}, she said to herself: {\i ein li yom she-nizkak li ke-hayom ha-zeh} \u8212- I will never have an opportunity like today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yaakov\u8217's Image\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that critical moment, {\i va-titpesehu be-vigdo lemor}, she grabbed him by his garment. It was at this moment that {\i ba\u8217'ah demut dyukno shel aviv} \u8212- the image of his father appeared to him \u8212- {\i ve-nirah lo ba-chalon}, in the window. And the image spoke. {\i Yaakov}\u8217's image said to him: {\i Yosef, atidim achecha she-yikkatvu al avnei efod} \u8212- your brothers are destined to be inscribed on the {\i avnei efod}, {\i ve-ata beneihem} \u8212- and you are among them. {\i Retzoncha she-yimacheh shimcha mi-beneihem} \u8212- do you want your name erased from among them? This warning carries a profound resonance, given the context in which the {\i gemara} has placed it. We have spent considerable time discussing the writing of names on the {\i avnei efod} and the erasure of names \u8212- and now the threat to {\i Yosef} is precisely the erasure of his name from among his brothers on those very stones. One might also detect here a deeper echo: the concept of {\i mechiyat ha-shem}, the erasure of the divine name, is particularly associated with grave sexual transgression. The warning is simultaneously about physical erasure from the {\i efod} and about the theological weight of such a sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-tikara ro\u8217'eh zonot} \u8212- and you will be called a shepherd of prostitutes. The verse in Mishlei says: {\i ro\u8217'eh zonot ye\u8217'abed hon} \u8212- one who shepherds prostitutes wastes great treasure. This was the warning. People dissipate immense reservoirs of dignity and honor by following their desires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Resistance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Miyad} \u8212- immediately \u8212- {\i va-teshev be-eitan kashto} \u8212- he placed his strength in his bow. The bow is transparently a phallic metaphor for the tension of sexual arousal. Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Meir reads: {\i she-shav kashto le-eitano} \u8212- his bow returned to its strength, or alternatively, his {\i keri} returned to its source. {\i Va-yaphozu zero\u8217'ei yadav} \u8212- and the sinews of his arms were scattered. He pressed his fingers into the ground to prevent ejaculation. {\i Va-yetze shichvat zera mi-bein tzippornei yadav} \u8212- the seminal emission came out through his fingernails. This graphic account is conveying something important about the nature of what {\i Yosef} was resisting: he had come to a point where resistance was physiologically nearly impossible, where the body\u8217's drive was so powerful that even in holding back, the seed had to discharge somehow. He redirected it at enormous personal cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shepherd Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Midei avir Yaakov} \u8212- from the power of {\i Yaakov}. Who caused it that his name was ultimately inscribed on the {\i avnei efod}? {\i Avir Yaakov} \u8212- the strength of {\i Yaakov}, whose image appeared and pulled him back. {\i Misham ro\u8217'eh} \u8212- the {\i gemara} now returns to the image of the shepherd. Because he resisted and became worthy of having his name inscribed on the {\i efod}, {\i misham zachah ve-na\u8217'aseh ro\u8217'eh} \u8212- from there he merited and became a shepherd. {\i She-ne\u8217'emar: ro\u8217'eh Yisrael ha\u8217'azinah, noheig ke-tzon Yosef} \u8212- the verse says, Shepherd of Israel, hear, who leads like the flock of {\i Yosef}. {\i Yosef}, who might have become a {\i ro\u8217'eh zonot}, a shepherd of prostitutes, instead became the shepherd of {\i Bnei Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 37\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Joseph\u8217's Restraint\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joseph Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens against the backdrop of a broader discussion about the division of tribes at {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival} and the figures of Yosef and Yehoshua. The Gemara had noted that two individuals are paradigmatic of {\i kiddush shem shamayim}: Yosef performed it {\i b\u8217'seter} \u8212- in secret \u8212- while Yehudah performed it {\i b\u8217'rabbim} \u8212- in public. The Gemara now turns to examine each case in depth, beginning with Yosef.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twelve Tribes from Joseph\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tanna} teaches: {\i haya ra\u8217'ui Yosef latzeit mimenu yud-bet shvatim} \u8212- Yosef should rightfully have fathered all twelve tribes, just as Yaakov his father did. The textual basis is the juxtaposition in {\i Bereishit}: {\i Eleh toldot Yaakov Yosef} \u8212- \u8220"These are the generations of Yaakov: Yosef.\u8221" The language suggests a direct parallel, as if Yosef were to be to his generation what Yaakov was to his. The {\i toldot} formula ordinarily introduces a genealogical listing, and the singular mention of Yosef implies he was meant to carry that generative role in full.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benjamin\u8217's Ten Sons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet this potential was forfeited at the decisive moment of temptation with the wife of Potifar: {\i ela sheyatza shichvat zaro mibein tzipornei yadav} \u8212- his seed went forth from between his fingernails. The act of restraint was heroic, yet it entailed a real loss. The Gemara notes, somewhat ruefully, that even though the occasion was a {\i kiddush shem shamayim}, the circumstances that led to it could perhaps have been avoided altogether. As the old distinction goes: a wise person never enters the situation a clever person needs to escape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, {\i ve\u8217'af al pi chen yatze\u8217'u miBinyamin achiv} \u8212- the ten tribes Yosef was meant to father emerged instead from his brother Binyamin. {\i Vekulan nikre\u8217'u al shemo} \u8212- all ten of Binyamin\u8217's sons were named in memory of Yosef and the separation between them. The names are parsed one by one: {\i Bela}, because Yosef was {\i nivla bein ha\u8217'umot} \u8212- swallowed among the nations; {\i Vacher}, because Yosef was at least the firstborn of their shared mother; {\i Ashbel}, because God had taken him captive ({\i shevao Kel}); {\i Gera}, because he dwelt in exile like a lodger ({\i ger be\u8217'achsanyot}); {\i Naaman}, because he was exceedingly pleasant; {\i Achi v\u8217'Rosh}, my brother who was also my head, my elder; {\i Mupim v\u8217'Chupim} \u8212- I did not see his wedding ({\i chupah}) and he did not see mine. Rashi adds that {\i Mupim} also alludes to {\i piv k\u8217'fi Yaakov Avinu}, the teaching he received from Shem and Ever; and {\i Ard} \u8212- who descended among the nations, or according to another opinion, whose face resembled a rose ({\i vered}). What is striking is the intimacy of the naming: Binyamin, who was perhaps ten years old when Yosef was sold, named every one of his children as an expression of that unrelenting grief and longing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Joseph in Egypt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pharaoh\u8217's Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Abba said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: when Pharaoh declared to Yosef, {\i uviladecha lo yarim ish et yado} \u8212- no man shall lift a hand without your authority \u8212- the astrologers of Pharaoh objected. {\i Eved shelekachato b\u8217'esrim kesef} \u8212- a slave purchased for twenty pieces of silver, you would set him over us? Pharaoh responded that he perceived in Yosef the {\i ginunei malchut} \u8212- the qualities of royalty \u8212- citing wisdom, strength, and beauty. The astrologers countered: if this is to be so, then let him demonstrate fitness for such office by knowing all seventy languages, as befits a man of truly royal upbringing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gabriel & Languages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ata Gavriel v\u8217'limdo shivim lashon} \u8212- the angel Gavriel came and taught Yosef the seventy languages. There is something deliberately inverted about this in relation to the story of Moshe, where Gavriel moved the infant\u8217's hand from gold to coals, resulting in Moshe\u8217's becoming {\i kvad lashon} \u8212- heavy of speech. Here the movement is in the opposite direction, opening Yosef\u8217's linguistic capacity rather than constraining it. Yet even with Gavriel\u8217's instruction, Yosef could not master the learning: {\i lo hava ka gamir}. At that point, {\i hosifu lo ot achat mishmo} \u8212- God added one letter of His own name to Yosef\u8217's name, transforming it into {\i Yehosef}, as the verse states: {\i eidut bi\u8217'Yehosef samo b\u8217'tzeito al eretz Mitzrayim}, with the completion of the verse {\i sfat lo yadati eshma} \u8212- a language I did not know I now hear. The letter unlocked what instruction alone could not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This detail connects back to the theme of {\i kiddush shem shamayim b\u8217'seter}: the reward for Yosef\u8217's hidden sanctification of God\u8217's name is precisely this hidden gift \u8212- {\i ot achat mishmo shel Hakadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- one letter from God\u8217's own name. And it is worth noting the contrast this creates with Pharaoh, who presented himself to the world as a divine figure: the one who truly carries that {\i element} of divinity is Yosef, through the letter of God\u8217's name embedded in his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hebrew Language Secret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next day, whatever language Pharaoh tried, Yosef responded in kind \u8212- the test was passed. Then Yosef spoke to Pharaoh in {\i lashon hakodesh}, in Hebrew, and Pharaoh did not understand. Pharaoh asked Yosef to teach him this language. Yosef tried, but Pharaoh could not learn it \u8212- Gavriel was not there for him. Pharaoh then asked Yosef to swear that he would never reveal to anyone that there existed one language Pharaoh did not know, since a king who was worshipped as a god could not be known to lack mastery of any tongue. {\i V\u8217'ishtaba lei} \u8212- and Yosef swore.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevance of this oath surfaces later, at the end of Yaakov\u8217's life, when Yosef tells Pharaoh: {\i avi hishpiani leimor} \u8212- my father made me swear to bury him in {\i Kena\u8217'an}. Pharaoh replied: {\i zil tishol ashvuatcha} \u8212- go and seek release from your oath. Yosef responded: {\i v\u8217'eishul nami ashvuatach} \u8212- if I seek release from my oath regarding my father, I will also seek release from the oath I made to you, the oath to keep your linguistic secret. And though Pharaoh was displeased, he had no choice: {\i alei ukvor et avicha ka\u8217'asher hishbi\u8217'echa} \u8212- go, bury your father as he made you swear. The Midrash reads {\i ka\u8217'asher hishbi\u8217'echa} as indicating that Pharaoh\u8217's acquiescence was entirely conditioned on the weight of the oath, and the passage subtly explains why Pharaoh honored it at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a sharp halachic question on the phrase {\i v\u8217'eishul nami ashvuatach}. One who swears an oath to another person cannot seek {\i hatarat nedarim} \u8212- annulment of that vow \u8212- without that person\u8217's knowledge, or at least without some form of acquiescence, analogous to the episode of Tzidkiyahu and Nevuchadnetzar. Pharaoh never agreed to Yosef\u8217's annulling the oath made to him; he only agreed to release Yosef from his father\u8217's oath. So how could Yosef even threaten to do so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot works through several possibilities before concluding: {\i veshema okminehu adigeizum} \u8212- perhaps Yosef was simply bluffing. He threatened something he in fact could not halachically have done, but Pharaoh did not know that. {\i Lo haya yachol lehishael alav velo nicha lei lePharaoh} \u8212- Yosef truly could not have obtained release from that oath, and Pharaoh was not happy about the situation, but Pharaoh did not know Yosef lacked the means to carry through. So Yosef\u8217's leverage was a bluff \u8212- and it worked.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Judah\u8217's Leadership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benjamin Jumps First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the tribe of Yehudah and its {\i kiddush shem shamayim b\u8217'rabbim} at {\i Kriat Yam Suf}. A {\i tanna} teaches in Rabbi Meir\u8217's name: when the Jewish people stood at the sea, the tribes competed with one another \u8212- {\i zeh omer ani yored techilah layam, vezeh omer ani yored techilah layam} \u8212- each declaring that it would descend first. While they argued, {\i kafatz shivto shel Binyamin veyarad layam techilah} \u8212- the tribe of Binyamin leaped and went down into the sea first. The proof text is {\i sham Binyamin tzair verodem}, which is read: {\i al tikri verodem} \u8212- do not read \u8220"rules them\u8221" \u8212- {\i ela rad yam} \u8212- but \u8220"went down into the sea.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response of Yehudah was not gracious: {\i vehayu sarei Yehudah rogmem otam} \u8212- the princes of Yehudah were stoning them, as the verse states {\i sarei Yehudah rigmatam}. The implication, though not spelled out explicitly in the Gemara, is that Yehudah felt this was their prerogative, stolen from them by Binyamin\u8217's initiative. {\i Lefichach} \u8212- therefore \u8212- {\i zacha Binyamin hatzaddik venaaseh ushpizin lagvura} \u8212- Binyamin merited to become the host of the Divine Presence, as the verse promises: {\i uvein ktefav shachan} \u8212- between his shoulders it dwelt. This connects to the well-known geographical-theological point that the {\i Kodesh Kodashim} fell within Binyamin\u8217's {\i chelek}, even as the broader {\i Har Habayit} fell within Yehudah\u8217's \u8212- two tribes of {\i Bnei Rachel} and {\i Bnei Leah} standing right at the threshold of the Divine dwelling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot cites a {\i mashal} from the {\i Tosefta} or {\i Mechilta} that frames this somewhat differently. A king had two sons; he told the younger to wake him at sunrise, and the older at the third hour. The younger came to wake the king at sunrise, but the older prevented him, insisting the time was wrong. While they argued, the king rose on his own and said: {\i mah banai l\u8217'kape\u8217'ach s\u8217'charchem} \u8212- I will not deprive you of your reward. Binyamin\u8217's reward for going first was {\i Shechinah b\u8217'chelko} \u8212- the Divine Presence resting in his portion. Yehudah\u8217's reward for its role \u8212- for the leadership it embodied even in the dispute \u8212- was {\i malchut}, as the verse implies: {\i ein rad ela malchut}. This version is notably more generous to Yehudah: both tribes were doing something they understood to be right, and each received a corresponding reward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the Gemara\u8217's version is harder on Yehudah. The stoning of Binyamin\u8217's tribe hardly sounds like an act of leadership. One response to this tension is that the {\i Tosefta} version reframes the dispute as a principled disagreement about {\i timing} \u8212- Yehudah believed the moment had not yet come \u8212- whereas the Gemara\u8217's account suggests a competition over privilege. The {\i Kotzker Rebbe} is said to have reconciled the two opinions: during the theoretical argument, everyone was talking; when the actual moment of action arrived, Binyamin moved \u8212- and {\i lo palgi ahadedi}, the two accounts do not truly contradict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachshon\u8217's Faith\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah offers a different version entirely: {\i lo kach hayah ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- that is not how it happened. In Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's account, {\i kafatz Nachshon ben Aminadav veyarad layam techilah} \u8212- Nachshon ben Aminadav leaped first into the sea. The verse {\i v\u8217'Yehudah od rad im El} is read as \u8220"Yehudah went down with God,\u8221" or on account of his faith in God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And the verse cited immediately afterwards \u8212- {\i hoshieini Elokim ki va\u8217'u mayim ad nafesh} \u8212- \u8220"save me, God, for the waters have reached my soul\u8221" \u8212- makes clear that the sea did not split immediately. Nachshon had gone in, and the waters were at his throat. {\i Tavati b\u8217'yevein metzulah v\u8217'ein ma\u8217'amad} \u8212- I have sunk into the deep mire and there is no footing. This is the Gemara\u8217's way of squaring the famous narrative question: who split the sea \u8212- Nachshon or Moshe? The answer given here is that both acts occurred, but sequentially, and with a frightening interval between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachshon Drowning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While Nachshon was drowning, {\i hayah Moshe maarich bitfilah} \u8212- Moshe was prolonging his prayer. God responded: {\i yedidai tov\u8217'im bayam} \u8212- My beloved ones are drowning in the sea \u8212- interestingly in the plural, perhaps echoing the famous Midrash that God\u8217's own handiwork was drowning: {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadai tov\u8217'im bayam v\u8217'atem omrim shirah}. God says to Moshe: {\i v\u8217'atah maarich bitfilah l\u8217'fanai?} \u8212- and you are prolonging your prayer before Me? Moshe replied: {\i Ribbono Shel Olam, u\u8217'mah b\u8217'yadi la\u8217'asot?} \u8212- Master of the Universe, what else should I do? And God\u8217's answer: {\i daber el Bnei Yisrael veyisau} \u8212- speak to the children of Israel and let them go forward \u8212- and then lift your staff.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an extraordinarily powerful moment that is not always appreciated in its full depth. Moshe was doing the entirely natural, even obligatory, thing: he was praying, awaiting divine instruction. And God essentially says: stop. There is a time for prayer, a time to wait for instruction \u8212- and a time to simply {\i know what you have to do and do it}. Nachshon embodied that third mode. He did not wait for the sea to split. He did not wait for instruction. He understood, at some deep level, that if this is the situation, this is what God wants \u8212- and he leaped.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also illuminates the famous verse after the crossing: {\i vaya\u8217'aminu b\u8217'Hashem uvMoshe avdo} \u8212- and they believed in God and in Moshe His servant. If they had seen all the plagues and wonders, what does it mean that only now did they believe? The answer is that {\i emunah} here is not abstract theological belief, not assent to God\u8217's existence. It is live, active, embodied faith \u8212- the kind of faith that acts before it sees, that jumps into the water before the water parts. Nachshon demonstrated that faith before anyone else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judah\u8217's Kingdom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vezachu Yehudah la\u8217'asot memshalah b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- Yehudah merited to exercise dominion in Israel. The verse: {\i haysah Yehudah l\u8217'kodsho, Yisrael mamshelosav} \u8212- Yehudah was for God\u8217's holy one, Israel for His dominion. And the reason: {\i mishum dehayam ra\u8217'ah vayanos} \u8212- because the sea saw and fled, because of the actions of Yehudah. It was the act of {\i Nachshon} \u8212- and the tribe that followed \u8212- which, together with Moshe\u8217's subsequent lifting of his staff, brought about the splitting of the sea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the contrast between Yosef and Yehudah that runs beneath the surface here. Yosef operates throughout in a mode of {\i histapkut} \u8212- trusting that God is working through events, saying to his brothers {\i al te\u8217'atzvu}, do not be distressed, it is all God\u8217's doing. This is admirable from a theological standpoint, but it is not the posture of a leader. Yehudah, by contrast, says {\i anochi e\u8217'ervenu} \u8212- I take personal responsibility. The reward for Binyamin\u8217's leap was {\i Shechinah} \u8212- God\u8217's Presence. The reward for Yehudah\u8217's leadership was {\i memshalah} \u8212- dominion, kingship. Faith and leadership are not identical, and both have their distinct expressions and rewards.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerizim & Eival\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov opens a new problem: {\i iy efshar lomar Levi lemattah} \u8212- it is impossible to say that Levi was below, at the foot of the mountains, because the Torah\u8217's own division places Levi on {\i Har Gerizim}. But neither can one say they were above on the mountains, because the account in {\i Yehoshua} places the {\i Kohanim} and {\i Levi\u8217'im} in the center, with the {\i Aron}, below. How to reconcile?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer: {\i ziknei kehunah v\u8217'leviya lemattah} \u8212- the elders of the {\i kohanim} and {\i Levi\u8217'im} were below with the {\i Aron}, while the remaining members of the tribe were above on the mountain, as the Torah prescribes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yoshiya offers an alternative: everyone fit for {\i avodah} \u8212- those between the ages of 30 and 50, the active years of Levitical service \u8212- was below, while the rest were above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Below Mountains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi disagrees with both: {\i elu v\u8217'elu lemattah} \u8212- both groups, all the tribes, were below. This resolves a different textual tension. The Torah says {\i eleh ya\u8217'amdu al Har Gerizim}, which sounds like they stood {\i on} the mountain. The account in {\i Yehoshua} says {\i mul Har Gerizim} \u8212- facing, or at the foot of, Har Gerizim. Rebbi reads {\i al} not as \u8220"upon\u8221" but as \u8220"near,\u8221" {\i besamuch}, and marshals a parallel: the verse {\i venasata al hamareches levonah zakah} \u8212- you shall place the frankincense {\i on} the arrangement of the {\i lechem hapanim} \u8212- cannot mean literally atop the bread, as the incense sat in the space between the two stacks. So too {\i al} in this context means adjacent, not literally upon. Just as Rebbi in a separate {\i braita} rules {\i al besamuch} regarding the frankincense of the {\i lechem hapanim} \u8212- deriving this from the verse that the {\i parochet} shades {\i over} the {\i Aron} without literally resting on it \u8212- so too here: everyone stood {\i near} the mountains, not on them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The image that results is of the entire people gathered in the valley between the two mountains, all below, all present, all participants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Facing Mountains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hafchu pneichem klapei Har Gerizim} \u8212- they all turned to face Har Gerizim \u8212- {\i upetchu b\u8217'bracha} \u8212- and they opened with the blessing. Then they turned toward {\i Har Eival} and opened with the {\i klala}. The choreography is total participation: no one stands apart while others recite; the entire people face the mountain of blessing and then face the mountain of curse. Every voice is included. The Torah\u8217's language confirms this: {\i v\u8217'anu kol ha-am v\u8217'amru amen} \u8212- all the people answered and said {\i amen}. One might feel that the original image \u8212- tribes positioned on visually distinct mountains, one lush and abundant, one barren \u8212- carries a certain dramatic power. But what this arrangement achieves is that every person owns every blessing and every curse, and no one is merely an observer.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Covenant Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Covenants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now counts: {\i baruch bikhlal baruch biprat, arur bikhlal arur biprat} \u8212- there is a general blessing and a specific blessing, a general curse and a specific curse. Beyond that, each {\i mitzvah} has four dimensions: {\i lilmod u\u8217'lelamed lishmor v\u8217'la\u8217'asot} \u8212- to learn, to teach, to observe, and to do. For each of those four dimensions there is a corresponding {\i baruch}, and for each a corresponding {\i arur}. That is four plus four equals eight; eight blessings and eight curses equals sixteen dimensions per {\i mitzvah} \u8212- sixteen {\i britot}. And this was enacted three times: at {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival}, at {\i Sinai}, and at {\i Arvot Moav}. Three times sixteen yields forty-eight {\i britot} \u8212- forty-eight covenantal dimensions \u8212- for every single {\i mitzvah} in the Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon, however, disputes whether {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival} constituted one of the three {\i brit}-moments at all. His alternative three loci are {\i Har Sinai}, {\i Ohel Moed}, and {\i Arvot Moav}. His reasoning reflects a conceptual reframing: we have shifted from counting {\i brachot} and {\i klalot} to speaking of {\i brit} \u8212- covenant. And when the question is where the covenant was established, {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eival} are a ceremony of {\i baruch} and {\i arur}, not the {\i ikar brit}. The covenant proper is sealed at {\i Sinai}, elaborated at {\i Ohel Moed}, and renewed at {\i Arvot Moav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generalities & Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dispute maps onto a deeper {\i tannaitic} disagreement. Rabbi Yishmael holds: {\i klalot ne\u8217'emru b\u8217'Sinai, pratot b\u8217'Ohel Moed} \u8212- the general principles were stated at Sinai, the specific details at the Tent of Meeting. Rashi illustrates: the verse {\i vezavachta alav et olotecha ve\u8217'et shelamecha} \u8212- you shall offer your burnt offerings and peace offerings upon it \u8212- was stated in the context of Sinai, but it gives no detail about {\i matan damim}, the application of the blood, or {\i haktarat emurin}, or {\i hafshat v\u8217'nituach}. All of those details appear only in {\i Sefer Vayikra}, which is the Torah of {\i Ohel Moed}. On this reading, everything in the Torah from {\i Vayikra} onward is the {\i prat} of what was sketched at {\i Sinai}. This also implies a natural reading of what {\i Sinai} encompasses: it need not be limited to the {\i Aseret HaDibrot} alone, but may extend through {\i parshat Mishpatim}, where Moshe descended, read the Torah to the people, and sealed the {\i Sefer HaBrit} \u8212- the covenant scroll \u8212- at the end of {\i Mishpatim}. That is the natural terminus of the {\i Sinai} revelation as a legal-covenantal unit, and including {\i Mishpatim} makes it considerably more plausible to view it as the repository of {\i klalot} from which all subsequent detail is drawn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva disagrees: {\i klalot u\u8217'pratot ne\u8217'emru b\u8217'Sinai} \u8212- both generalities and specifics were stated at Sinai. {\i V\u8217'nishnu b\u8217'Ohel Moed} \u8212- and they were repeated at the Tent of Meeting. {\i V\u8217'nishtalshu b\u8217'Arvot Moav} \u8212- and repeated a third time at Arvot Moav. On this view there are three fully parallel transmissions of the entire Torah, each in its own setting and context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a dispute between Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva that extends well beyond the immediate question of covenant-locations. Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's famous principle is {\i dibra Torah kilshon bnei adam} \u8212- the Torah speaks in human language, in accessible, contextually organic terms \u8212- and his approach to hermeneutics tends toward restraint, not pressing every detail to yield independent legal force. Rabbi Akiva, who {\i darshens} every letter, would naturally hold that even the details were already present at {\i Sinai} in their fullness \u8212- nothing was communicated in a lesser or more mediated mode. The question of how many times the Torah was transmitted, and whether details were already embedded in the original revelation or unfolded progressively, appears to track these broader commitments. This axis is, of course, a central concern of Heschel\u8217's {\i Torah Min Hashamayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 48 Covenants Math\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mathematical upshot: once we have 48 {\i britot} per {\i mitzvah} (16 \u215 ? 3), and this applies to every individual {\i mitzvah} in the Torah, we have 48 {\i britot} per {\i mitzvah} multiplied across 613 {\i mitzvot}. But Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda of Kfar Akko, citing Rabbi Shimon, goes further: {\i ein lecha mitzvah u\u8217'mitzvah she\u8217'ktuvah baTorah shelo nichratu aleha arbaim u\u8217'shmonah britot shel shesh me\u8217'ot elef u\u8217'shloshet alafim v\u8217'chamesh me\u8217'ot v\u8217'chamishim} \u8212- for every single {\i mitzvah}, the 48 {\i britot} are covenanted not just once but 603,550 times \u8212- the number of adult men counted in the census. For every {\i mitzvah}, each individual Israelite carries 48 covenantal obligations, and there were 603,550 individuals. The Gemara then notes: for every individual Israelite, the total number of covenantal obligations equals 603,550.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Guarantee\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharshaya explains the distinction between the two positions: {\i arva d\u8217'arva ika beinaihu} \u8212- the second position adds the dimension of {\i arvut} upon {\i arvut} \u8212- guarantee upon guarantee. On the first reading, one might simply be multiplying the total {\i britot} across the total population, an arithmetic aggregation. But Rav Mesharshaya\u8217's point is different: each individual does not merely have 48 {\i britot} for each {\i mitzvah}. Each individual is also an {\i arev} \u8212- a guarantor \u8212- for every other individual\u8217's 48 {\i britot}. So for every {\i mitzvah}, each person carries 48 obligations on their own account, plus responsibility for 603,549 others, each carrying their own 48. The numbers become astronomical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And one can extend the logic: I am not only responsible for your keeping your 48 {\i britot}, I am also responsible for your discharging your {\i arvut} obligations toward everyone else. That level of {\i arvut d\u8217'arvut} makes the total obligation truly exponential \u8212- and Rav Mesharshaya appears to intend exactly that, which is why he adds this observation rather than simply restating what came before. Tosafot works through some of the arithmetic, though by that point the numbers have grown well beyond the scope of a brief calculation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Curses & Adultery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adultery Theme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah ben Nechemia, the {\i meturgamana} \u8212- the public announcer \u8212- of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, offered a striking interpretation: {\i kol haparsha kula lo ne\u8217'emra ela b\u8217'mna\u8217'ef u\u8217'mna\u8217'efet} \u8212- the entire section of {\i brachot} and {\i klalos} was stated only with reference to an adulterer and adulteress. This is a striking return to the {\i Sotah} theme, and it is notable that the formulation includes the male sinner, not only the woman. The {\i parasha} is not, on this reading, a general moral catalogue \u8212- it is a unified statement about the consequences of sexual betrayal within marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Curses Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then proceeds to trace every {\i arur} back to the act of adultery, with varying degrees of directness. {\i Arur ha\u8217'ish asher ya\u8217'aseh pesel u\u8217'masecha} \u8212- the verse curses one who makes an idol. But, the Gemara explains, this means one who committed adultery, produced a {\i mamzer}, and that child \u8212- excluded from {\i Bnei Yisrael} \u8212- went among idol worshippers and worshipped {\i avoda zara}. The sin of adultery cascades into idolatry: {\i sheken garmu lo} \u8212- for they caused it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi extends the reading systematically: the adulterer fathers a {\i mamzer}; the child, shamed and excluded, turns to the nations and their worship. Similarly, {\i shochev im behemah} is read in the context of the {\i Sotah} ordeal: {\i eishes ish she\u8217'asa ma\u8217'aseh behemah she\u8217'mna\u8217'efet}. {\i Mikalel aviv v\u8217'imo} \u8212- one who curses his father and mother \u8212- is the child of an adulterous union, born into a household whose shame brought disgrace on the parents who raised such wrongdoing. {\i Mashgeh iver} \u8212- who misleads the blind \u8212- is the seducer of a married woman: {\i m\u8217'fateh eshet ish}, blinding her to the weight of the sin she is being drawn into, since {\i she\u8217'isuma badavar v\u8217'eino yodei\u8217'a onsho kamohu} \u8212- she is blinded in the matter and does not fully perceive the punishment as he does. {\i Masig gevul re\u8217'ehu} \u8212- who removes a neighbor\u8217's boundary \u8212- is one who takes another man\u8217's wife: {\i ba\u8217'al ishto, harei zeh hesig gevul}. {\i Makeh re\u8217'ehu baseter} \u8212- who strikes his neighbor in secret \u8212- is one who causes the bitter waters of the {\i Sotah} to work their effect through whispered manipulation. {\i Lokei\u8217'ach shochad l\u8217'hakot nefesh} \u8212- one who takes a bribe to cause death \u8212- is the one who accepts payment to aid the adulterer in seducing a married woman and thereby brings about her death through the {\i Sotah} ordeal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final verse \u8212- {\i mateh mishpat ger yatom} \u8212- the Gemara concedes: {\i eini yodei\u8217'a lidrosh} \u8212- I do not know how to interpret it in this framework. The intellectual honesty of that admission is, in its own way, as noteworthy as all the interpretations that preceded it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blessings & Curses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternating Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna states: {\i Tanu Rabanan}: the verse {\i venasata et habracha al Har Gerizim ve\u8217'et haklala al Har Eival} \u8212- why does the Torah need to state this, given that the allocation of tribes to each mountain has already been specified? The answer is: {\i ela l\u8217'hakdim bracha laklala} \u8212- to establish that the blessing is pronounced before the curse. And lest one think this means all the {\i brachot} are recited together before all the {\i klalos}, the singular language \u8212- {\i bracha v\u8217'klala} \u8212- indicates: one blessing, then one curse, then the next blessing, then the next curse. They alternate, one by one, individually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Parallels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishna then enumerates seven structural parallels between the {\i brachot} and the {\i klalos}. Each of the following features that characterizes the {\i klalot} applies equally to the {\i brachot}: they were pronounced by the {\i Levi\u8217'im} ({\i ma klalos b\u8217'Levi\u8217'im af brachot b\u8217'Levi\u8217'im}); they were said in a loud voice ({\i ma klala b\u8217'kol ram af bracha b\u8217'kol ram}), as the phrasing {\i v\u8217'anita v\u8217'amarta} implies; they were in {\i lashon hakodesh} ({\i ma klala b\u8217'lashon hakodesh af bracha b\u8217'lashon hakodesh}) \u8212- which connects this passage directly to the discussion that opened the tractate; they had both a general and specific form ({\i ma klala b\u8217'klal u\u8217'vfrat af bracha b\u8217'klal u\u8217'vfrat}); and finally, for both, {\i elu v\u8217'elu onim v\u8217'omrim amen} \u8212- both these and those, all the people together, answered and said {\i amen}. Not half the people for the blessings and the other half for the curses \u8212- everyone said {\i amen} to both. The structural symmetry is total: in form, in voice, in language, in scope, and in communal response, blessing and curse are mirror images of one another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 38\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro: Birkat Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf 38 Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 38 marks a transition away from the {\i agadata} and returns to the {\i mishnayot}, specifically to the category of things that must be said in any language \u8212- {\i ne\u8217'emarin bechol lashon} \u8212- as well as those requiring {\i lashon hakodesh}. The daf now turns its attention to {\i birkat kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why in Sotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note how much of the {\i halachot} of {\i birkat kohanim} come to be discussed precisely here, in Masechta Sotah. The reason is structural: {\i birkat kohanim} appears in the {\i mishna} as one of the items requiring {\i lashon hakodesh}, and so Sotah becomes the occasion for a full treatment of the subject. The same dynamic explains why the laws of {\i egla arufa} \u8212- which might more naturally have found a home in Kodshim \u8212- end up receiving an entire {\i perek} here: because {\i egla arufa} is mentioned in the {\i mishna}\u8217's discussion of {\i lashon hakodesh} versus {\i bechol lashon}, and one topic pulls the other in its wake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: Birkat Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} asks: {\i birkat kohanim keitzad} \u8212- how is {\i birkat kohanim} performed? The {\i keitzad} here is not asking how to recite the blessing in {\i lashon hakodesh}; that requirement is taken for granted. Rather, the {\i mishna} uses this as an opportunity to address the broader laws of how {\i birkat kohanim} is conducted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three issues are addressed. First, the number of blessings: {\i bamedina omer osa shalosh berachos} \u8212- outside the {\i mikdash}, the blessing is divided into three, meaning the congregation responds {\i amen} after each of the three verses ({\i Yevarechecha}, {\i Ya\u8217'er}, {\i Yisa}), treating each as a separate unit. {\i Ubamikdash bracha achat} \u8212- in the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, they do not interrupt with {\i amen} between the verses, and so the entire blessing constitutes one. In the {\i mikdash}, the response was {\i baruch shem} rather than {\i amen}, and since this does not interrupt the flow of the three verses, they are treated as a single unified blessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, the divine name: {\i bamikdash omer es Hashem kichsavov} \u8212- in the {\i mikdash}, God\u8217's name is pronounced as written, that is, the {\i shem hameforash}, the four-letter name that is not sounded outside the {\i mikdash}. {\i Ubamedina bekinuyov} \u8212- outside the {\i mikdash}, the name is pronounced in its customary substitute form. Rashi explains: {\i kichsavov be-Yud-Heh}, {\i bekinuyov be-Aleph-Dalet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third, the position of the hands: {\i bamedina kohanim nosim es yedeihen kneged kitfeihen} \u8212- outside the {\i mikdash}, the {\i kohanim} lift their hands to shoulder height. {\i Ubamikdash al gabei rasheihen} \u8212- in the {\i mikdash}, the hands are raised above the head. The reason given is that the {\i shechina} rests in the {\i mikdash}, and the hands should therefore be raised high enough that the divine presence is, in a sense, above them. An exception is made for the {\i kohen gadol}, {\i chutz mikohen gadol she\u8217'eino magbiya yadav lema\u8217'la min hatzitz} \u8212- his hands should not rise above the {\i tzitz} on his forehead. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} disagrees, holding that even the {\i kohen gadol} raises his hands above the {\i tzitz}, citing the verse {\i vayisa Aharon es yadav el ha\u8217'am vayivarchaim} \u8212- and since Aharon was a {\i kohen gadol}, the word {\i vayisa} implies a particularly high elevation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's organization is notable. The pattern alternates between {\i medina} and {\i mikdash}: the first ruling goes {\i medina-mikdash}, the second goes {\i mikdash-medina}, and the third returns to {\i medina-mikdash}. This may simply be a mnemonic device \u8212- each clause flows into the next through an ABBA-like alternation. There may also be a more deliberate reason: when it comes to the issue of pronouncing God\u8217's name as written, the {\i mishna} places the {\i mikdash} practice first, because pronouncing the {\i shem hameforash} is a matter of such gravity that it cannot be mentioned as an afterthought. The other issues are more historically remote and so their secondary mention is less problematic.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Language Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Koh Tevarchu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a {\i baraita}: {\i koh tevarchu} \u8212- the word {\i koh} indicates that the blessing must be given in {\i lashon hakodesh}. The {\i baraita} asks: perhaps it may be said in any language? Perhaps {\i koh} does not carry this implication?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezerah Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this through a {\i gezerah shava}. The word {\i koh} appears here in {\i koh tevarchu}, and the word {\i eileh} appears elsewhere: {\i eileh ya\u8217'amdu levarech et ha\u8217'am} \u8212- the blessing pronounced on Har Gerizim. Just as the blessing at Har Gerizim was given in {\i lashon hakodesh}, so too {\i birkat kohanim} must be in {\i lashon hakodesh}: {\i mah lehalan bi-lashon hakodesh af kan bi-lashon hakodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an incisive question: why derive the requirement from Har Gerizim? One could equally argue the opposite \u8212- learn from the verse {\i ve\u8217'achalta ve\u8217'savata u\u8217'verachta}, which governs {\i birkat hamazon}, and which we know may be said in any language. The {\i gezerah shava} could just as well have pointed toward {\i bechol lashon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Text vs.\u160 ?Non-Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot answers: it is more compelling to derive {\i birkat kohanim} from the blessings of Har Gerizim, because both are instances where the Torah provides an explicit text \u8212- {\i dil\u8217'shteihen meforash ba\u8217'Torah}. The text of the {\i kohanim}\u8217's blessing is fully spelled out in the Torah; similarly, the blessings and curses at Har Gerizim are spelled out. By contrast, the text of {\i birkat hamazon} is not specified in the Torah \u8212- the verse {\i ve\u8217'achalta ve\u8217'savata u\u8217'verachta} simply requires expressing gratitude, and any adequate formulation suffices. Since there is no prescribed text, the question of original versus translation does not arise, and of course it may be said in any language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This points to a deeper conceptual distinction. The requirement to speak in {\i lashon hakodesh} is, in many cases, really a requirement to recite the original text rather than a translation. When the Torah provides a specific text, the question becomes: must you say the original, or may you say it in translation? Things permitted in any language may simply be those for which no specific text is mandated \u8212- in which case the concept of \u8220"original versus translation\u8221" is inapplicable, and naturally any language will do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Categorization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examining the lists in the {\i mishna} bears this out. Those items requiring {\i lashon hakodesh} \u8212- {\i mikra bikkurim}, {\i chalitzah}, {\i berachot ukelalot}, {\i birkat kohanim}, {\i parshat hamelech}, {\i parshat egla arufa}, and {\i mashuach milchamah} \u8212- nearly all have a specific Torah text. Those permitted {\i bechol lashon} that have no formal text \u8212- like {\i tefilla} and {\i birkat hamazon} \u8212- are of course open to any language. And some things with a formal text \u8212- like {\i parshat sotah}, {\i vidui ma\u8217'aser}, and {\i kriat shema} \u8212- are permitted in any language, meaning translation is acceptable. The one exception in the {\i lashon hakodesh} list is {\i birkat kohen gadol} on Yom Kippur, which has no fixed text, making it the outlier. On this reading, the emphasis of {\i lashon hakodesh} is less about the Hebrew language per se and more about the obligation to use the original text rather than a translation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds that no derivation from Har Gerizim is necessary. {\i Koh} itself \u8212- \u8220"exactly like this\u8221" \u8212- is sufficient to teach that the blessing must be recited in precisely this form, in this language. No external source is needed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Standing Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Koh Tevarchu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i baraita} derives from {\i koh tevarchu} that the blessing must be given while standing. Perhaps, the {\i baraita} asks, the {\i kohanim} may be seated? The verse provides the initial opening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eileh Ya\u8217'amdu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i gezerah shava} resolves the question. {\i Eileh ya\u8217'amdu levarech} \u8212- \u8220"these shall stand to bless\u8221" \u8212- at Har Gerizim makes clear that standing is required: {\i mah lehalan be\u8217'amida af kan be\u8217'amida}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Standing Requirement (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lesharto Ulevarech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Natan} holds that the standing requirement need not be derived from Har Gerizim. The verse {\i lesharto ulevarech bishmo} \u8212- the {\i kohanim} are chosen to serve God and to bless in His name \u8212- is itself sufficient. This verse powerfully links the priestly role of service with the role of blessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avodah Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation proceeds as follows: just as priestly service ({\i sheirut}) must be performed while standing, so too the blessing must be given while standing \u8212- {\i mah mesharet be\u8217'amida af mevarech be\u8217'amida}. And how do we know that priestly service itself requires standing? From the verse {\i la\u8217'amod lesharet} \u8212- \u8220"to stand and to serve.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Approaches (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the discussion develops, a clear structural pattern emerges. One approach \u8212- that of {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and those who derive requirements from {\i koh tevarchu} combined with {\i eileh ya\u8217'amdu levarech} \u8212- situates {\i birkat kohanim} within the category of a {\i beracha}. The relevant constellation is the nature of blessing itself: {\i koh} points us to the blessing text, and Har Gerizim provides a parallel instance of blessing. The priestly identity is not the hermeneutic focus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Natan} adopts an entirely different orientation. He consistently derives the requirements of {\i birkat kohanim} from {\i lesharto ulevarech bishmo} \u8212- framing {\i birkat kohanim} as a form of {\i avodah}, priestly service. The model is not \u8220"how does one give a blessing\u8221" but \u8220"what do {\i kohanim} do in their role as servants of God.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceptual Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not merely a technical dispute about which verse to use. The two approaches reflect a deeper disagreement about what {\i birkat kohanim} fundamentally is. Is it a {\i beracha} \u8212- an act of blessing that happens to be performed by {\i kohanim} \u8212- or is it an {\i avodah} \u8212- a form of priestly service that happens to take the form of a blessing? Both approaches reach the same practical conclusions so far, but the underlying framework diverges significantly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Conclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stakes of this dispute become visible when one asks whether {\i birkat kohanim} is a {\i mitzvah de\u8217'oraita} outside the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. If one learns everything from how {\i berachot} work \u8212- and especially from Har Gerizim, which itself took place outside any {\i mikdash} \u8212- then there is no inherent reason to confine the {\i mitzvah} to the {\i mikdash}. The practice outside the {\i mikdash} is straightforwardly a {\i mitzvah de\u8217'oraita}. But if one learns everything from {\i sheirut} \u8212- from priestly service in the {\i mikdash} \u8212- then one might well conclude that {\i birkat kohanim} is fundamentally a {\i mikdash}-centered {\i avodah}, and whatever is practiced outside is something of a lesser status. This question will continue to resonate throughout the subsequent discussions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hand Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nesi\u8217'as Kapayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i baraita} derives from {\i koh tevarchu} that the blessing requires {\i nesi\u8217'as kapayim} \u8212- the lifting of the hands. The verse itself might not make this obvious, so the {\i baraita} turns to a {\i gezerah shava}: {\i ne\u8217'emar kan koh tevarchu} and {\i ne\u8217'emar lehallan vayisa Aharon es yadav el ha\u8217'am vayivarchaim} \u8212- Aharon lifted his hands toward the people and blessed them. Just as there the blessing was given with raised hands, so too here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yonatan Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yonatan} challenges this derivation. If we are going to learn from Aharon\u8217's blessing, we are obligated to learn all the contextual circumstances of that blessing: Aharon was the {\i kohen gadol}, the occasion was {\i Rosh Chodesh}, and it followed a series of {\i korbanos tzibbur} at the time of the {\i miluim}. If we learn from there, perhaps {\i nesi\u8217'as kapayim} is only required when all these circumstances converge \u8212- when it is a {\i kohen gadol}, on {\i Rosh Chodesh}, after public offerings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hu Uvanav Kol Hayamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Natan} responds: the derivation from Aharon\u8217's blessing is unnecessary. The verse {\i lesharto ulevarech bishmo hu uvanav kol hayamim} \u8212- \u8220"to serve and to bless in His name, he and his sons, all the days\u8221" \u8212- provides the needed answer. From {\i hu uvanav}, we learn that just as Aharon performed {\i nesi\u8217'as kapayim}, so too his sons do the same \u8212- eliminating the restriction to {\i kohen gadol}. And from {\i kol hayamim} \u8212- \u8220"all the days\u8221" \u8212- we learn that the practice is not limited to special occasions like {\i Rosh Chodesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliminating Circumstances\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This disposes of two of the three limiting circumstances: the {\i kohen gadol} restriction falls away through {\i hu uvanav}, and the {\i Rosh Chodesh} restriction falls away through {\i kol hayamim}. The question of the structural link is then addressed: the verse {\i hu uvanav kol hayamim} speaks of {\i la\u8217'amod lesharet} \u8212- priestly service \u8212- and we connect the {\i beracha} to the {\i sheirut} through the other verse, creating the {\i hekesh} between {\i beracha} and {\i sheirut} that makes the {\i avodah} comparison operative. The general principle is then stated: just as {\i avodah} is performed the same way by ordinary {\i kohanim} as by the {\i kohen gadol}, and under ordinary circumstances as under special ones, the same applies to {\i birkat kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Korban Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a further question. Even granting all of the above \u8212- even accepting that {\i nesi\u8217'as kapayim} is not limited to the {\i kohen gadol} and not limited to {\i Rosh Chodesh} \u8212- how do we know it applies in the absence of a {\i korban tzibbur}? In the case of Aharon, the setting was a {\i korban tzibbur}, and {\i kol hayamim} could itself be satisfied by the {\i korban tamid}, which is itself a {\i korban tzibbur} offered every single day. So perhaps {\i nesi\u8217'as kapayim} is required every day \u8212- but only because every day there is a {\i tamid}, a public offering. How do we know it would apply by a {\i korban yachid}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot resolves this by accepting the implicit premise: in fact, {\i birkat kohanim} in the {\i mikdash} was only performed in the context of {\i korban tzibbur}, not {\i korban yachid}. This is documented explicitly in the fifth {\i perek} of Masechta Tamid, which describes the {\i kohanim} descending after placing the limbs on the ramp, gathering in the {\i lishkat hagazit}, reciting the {\i aseret hadibrot}, {\i shema}, and other prayers, and then blessing the people \u8212- and the same practice applied at {\i musaf} and at the afternoon {\i tamid}. {\i Rabbi Yonatan}\u8217's challenge about {\i korban yachid} was therefore not truly a challenge at all: {\i nesi\u8217'as kapayim} was always tied to {\i korban tzibbur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this answer carries a consequence that Tosafot makes explicit: if {\i birkat kohanim} in its full {\i halachic} form was tied to {\i korban tzibbur}, then the question of whether the {\i mitzvah de\u8217'oraita} applies outside the {\i Beis HaMikdash} becomes acute. The {\i avodah}-centered approach has now established that the practice is inherently bound to the context of communal sacrifice in the {\i mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 God\u8217's Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vesamu Et Shemi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the question of the divine name. A {\i baraita} teaches: {\i koh tevarchu es benei Yisrael beshem hameforash} \u8212- since the verse says {\i koh}, and the word {\i koh} specifies the exact form, one reads the name as it is written, yielding the {\i shem hameforash}. But perhaps one may use only a {\i kinuy}, a substitute pronunciation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} resolves this from the verse {\i vesamu es shemi al bnei Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"and they shall place My name upon the children of Israel.\u8221" {\i Shemi hameyuchad li} \u8212- my distinctive, unique name. That name is the {\i shem hameforash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Outside Mikdash Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But one might ask: if that is the source, perhaps the {\i shem hameforash} is to be used even outside the {\i Beis HaMikdash}? After all, the verse {\i vesamu es shemi} does not itself specify a location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} answers through a verbal analogy: {\i ne\u8217'emar kan vesamu} and {\i ne\u8217'emar lehallan lasum et shemo sham} \u8212- \u8220"the place that God has chosen to place His name there.\u8221" The word {\i vesamu} here echoes {\i lasum et shemo sham} there. Just as {\i lasum et shemo sham} refers to the {\i Beis Habechira}, the chosen place, so too {\i vesamu es shemi} applies only in the {\i Beis Habechira}. Outside the {\i mikdash}, only the {\i kinuy} is used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechol Hamakom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} holds that no such derivation is necessary. He cites the verse {\i bechol hamakom asher azkir es shemi avo eilecha uverachticha} \u8212- \u8220"in every place where I will cause My name to be mentioned, I will come to you and bless you.\u8221" From this verse he derives that wherever God\u8217's name is mentioned \u8212- meaning wherever the {\i kohanim} invoke it \u8212- God will come and bless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Resistance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resists the straightforward reading of this verse. {\i Bechol makom salka deitach?} \u8212- does it truly mean everywhere? Rashi explains the difficulty on two levels. First, there is a circular problem: the verse {\i ze shemi leolam veze zichri} already teaches that God\u8217's written name is not pronounced as written \u8212- {\i lo keshe\u8217'ani nichtav ani nikra} \u8212- so the idea that the {\i shem hameforash} would be used everywhere already runs against an established principle. Second, and more substantively, Rashi notes: {\i vechi bechol makom shechina ba\u8217'ah vesharah} \u8212- does the {\i shechina} truly descend and rest in every place? The verse says {\i avo eilecha} \u8212- \u8220"I will come to you\u8221" \u8212- which implies divine presence descending. That is not a universal phenomenon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading Out of Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i ella mikra ze mesuras hu} \u8212- this verse must be read out of sequence. The correct reading is: {\i bechol hamakom asher avo eilecha uverachticha} \u8212- wherever I choose to come and bless you, wherever I cause My {\i shechina} to dwell \u8212- {\i sham azkir es shemi}, there I will cause My name to be mentioned. The location of {\i avo eilecha uverachticha} is the {\i Beis Habechira}, and it is there that {\i azkir es shemi} occurs. Rashi adds that {\i bechol hamakom} refers to the various sacred sites \u8212- such as {\i Shilo} \u8212- that served as the chosen location before the permanent {\i Beis HaMikdash} was established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theological Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Placing the {\i gemara}\u8217's rearrangement alongside the plain meaning of the verse reveals a profound theological tension. On one side stands {\i hamakom asher bachar Hashem Elokecha lasum es shemo sham} \u8212- one specific, chosen place where God places His name. On the other stands {\i bechol hamakom asher azkir es shemi} \u8212- which, on its face, sounds like God\u8217's presence can be invoked anywhere. These two verses pull in opposite directions: one speaks of radical particularity, the other of apparent universality. The {\i gemara} resolves this tension by rearranging the universal verse so that it, too, points back to the one chosen place. But the tension itself is theologically significant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a reading of the {\i psukim} that preserves both dimensions rather than collapsing the universal into the particular. {\i Lasum es shemo sham} \u8212- God places His name in the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. But the {\i kohanim} are {\i vesamu es shemi al bnei Yisrael} \u8212- they place God\u8217's name upon the people. They do not live only in the {\i mikdash}; their role, as stated in {\i koh tevarchu es benei Yisrael}, extends to all of Israel. When they perform {\i birkat kohanim} and invoke the divine name, they are causing God\u8217's name to be mentioned \u8212- {\i azkir es shemi} \u8212- and God responds: {\i avo eilecha uverachticha}, {\i va\u8217'ani avarchem}. On this reading, the verse is not rearranged but understood as a description of the full dynamic of {\i birkat kohanim}: God concentrates His presence in one place ({\i lasum es shemo sham}), but commissions the {\i kohanim} to carry that name outward ({\i vesamu es shemi}), and promises that wherever they do so, He will bless the people ({\i bechol hamakom asher azkir es shemi avo eilecha uverachticha}). The universal and the local are both real \u8212- the {\i kohanim} are the bridge between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the structural debate: it is noteworthy that the second approach \u8212- the {\i avodah}-centered one \u8212- is precisely the one that resists the universal reading and rearranges the verse. The first approach, less invested in tying {\i birkat kohanim} to {\i mikdash}-based service, is more compatible with the universalist implication of {\i bechol hamakom}. The {\i avodah} approach needs to pull the verse back toward the {\i Beis Habechira}; the {\i beracha} approach is less compelled to do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both approaches, it should be noted, seem to presuppose that {\i birkat kohanim} applies outside the {\i mikdash} as a {\i mitzvah de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- the {\i baraitot} in this section treat the distinction between {\i mikdash} and {\i medina} as a question of {\i how} the {\i mitzvah} is performed (with or without the {\i shem hameforash}), not {\i whether} it exists outside. Yet the internal logic of the {\i avodah} approach \u8212- with its emphasis on {\i sheirut}, {\i korban tzibbur}, and {\i mikdash} \u8212- creates real hermeneutical pressure toward the conclusion that the full {\i mitzvah de\u8217'oraita} belongs in the {\i mikdash} alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Additional Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Receives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i baraita}: {\i koh tevarchu es benei Yisrael} \u8212- one might think this limits the blessing to native-born Israelites. The verse {\i emor lahem} \u8212- \u8220"say to them\u8221" \u8212- extends it to all, including {\i geirim} and freed slaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Face to Face\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another {\i baraita}: {\i koh tevarchu} \u8212- the blessing is given {\i panim keneged panim}, face to face. Perhaps the {\i kohanim} could face away from the congregation? The verse {\i emor lahem} teaches otherwise: just as one speaks to a friend \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'adam she\u8217'omer lachaveiro} \u8212- so here, the {\i kohanim} must face those they bless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Audible Voice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another {\i baraita}: {\i koh tevarchu bekol ram} \u8212- the blessing must be given in a loud, fully audible voice. Perhaps a whispered recitation suffices? Again, {\i emor lahem} teaches: one speaks to a friend in an ordinary, audible manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Calling Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} teaches: {\i naktinan} \u8212- we hold the position that when there are two {\i kohanim}, the {\i chazzan} calls out {\i kohanim} to prompt them to begin. When there is only one {\i kohen}, the {\i chazzan} does not call out {\i kohen}; the single {\i kohen} begins on his own. The source is {\i emor lahem} \u8212- here read in the liturgical sense, as the caller addressing those who will bless. {\i Lahem} implies a plural audience; one person alone does not require the call.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} adds a further position: {\i naktinan}, we hold that a {\i kohen} may call out {\i kohanim} to prompt his fellow {\i kohanim}, but a {\i Yisrael} leading the service does not call out {\i kohanim}. His source: {\i amira mishelachem} \u8212- the calling must come from among their own. It may be that having a {\i Yisrael} instruct {\i kohanim} to perform their sacred role is considered inappropriate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} follows {\i Abaye} and not {\i Rav Chisda}. The {\i chazzan} calls {\i kohanim} when there are two, not when there is one \u8212- but the caller need not himself be a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that these discussions about the {\i chazzan} calling {\i kohanim} are evidently conducted in a contemporary {\i tefilla} context entirely outside the {\i mikdash}, which retrospectively confirms that the earlier statements about {\i oleh laduchan} and the obligations of {\i birkat kohanim} are also addressed to the {\i shul} reality \u8212- not exclusively to the {\i mikdash} setting.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehoshua ben L (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Desire\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} teaches: how do we know that God desires {\i birkat kohanim} to be performed? From the verse {\i vesamu es shemi al bnei Yisrael va\u8217'ani avarchem} \u8212- \u8220"they shall place My name upon the children of Israel, and I will bless them.\u8221" The phrasing suggests not merely that if the name is placed God will bless, but that God needs this to happen \u8212- that the {\i kohanim} placing His name enables God to bless Israel. God, so to speak, desires to bless His people and requires the {\i kohanim}\u8217' invocation as the vehicle for that blessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Blessed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} further teaches: any {\i kohen} who gives the blessing is himself blessed \u8212- {\i kol kohen hamivarech misbarech}. But a {\i kohen} who does not give the blessing is himself not blessed. The source: {\i va\u8217'avaracha mevarachecha} \u8212- \u8220"I will bless those who bless you.\u8221" The {\i kohen} who blesses the people places himself within the covenant blessing promised to Avraham, receiving blessing in return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} adds: any {\i kohen} who does not go up to the {\i duchan} transgresses three positive commandments \u8212- {\i koh tevarchu}, {\i emor lahem}, and {\i vesamu es shemi}. This is striking: alongside the encouragement that the blessing kohen is himself blessed, there is a hard obligation \u8212- failure to go up is an active transgression of three {\i aseis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Gerusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, a {\i kohen} who does not go up on every occasion does not necessarily arouse suspicion on each absence. The {\i gemara} distinguishes: if a {\i kohen} goes up {\i leprakim} \u8212- occasionally \u8212- then people understand he simply did not go up on this particular occasion, and no one suspects he is a {\i ben gerusha} or {\i ben chalutza} (a {\i kohen} rendered unfit by illegitimate lineage). But if a {\i kohen} never goes up at all, he arouses suspicion that his lineage disqualifies him. These are two separate concerns \u8212- the halachic transgression attaches each time he fails to go up; the social suspicion attaches only to one who never goes up. Rashi explains {\i leprakim} as referring to {\i chagim umoadim}, which may reflect the {\i Ashkenazi} practice of performing {\i birkat kohanim} only on {\i yom tov} \u8212- though the plain meaning of the {\i gemara} is simply \u8220"sometimes,\u8221" without specifying which occasions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehoshua ben L (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} further teaches: a {\i kohen} who does not go up {\i ba\u8217'avoda} \u8212- while the {\i shliach tzibur} is in the midst of the {\i retzei} blessing \u8212- may no longer go up afterward. {\i Shuv eino oleh}. The source: {\i vayisa Aharon es yadav el ha\u8217'am vayivarchaim vayered me\u8217'asos hachatat veha\u8217'olah vehashlamim} \u8212- Aharon blessed the people while still in the context of the {\i korbanot}, before descending from the {\i mizbeach}. The blessing must take place while the {\i avodah} is still in active progress. Translated into the {\i shul} setting: the {\i kohen} must begin his ascent to the {\i duchan} while the {\i retzei} is still being recited \u8212- not after the {\i beracha} is complete.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Timing in Tefilla\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aaron\u8217's Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle \u8212- that the blessing must occur within the context of the {\i avodah} \u8212- is itself a remarkable statement about the structure of {\i tefilla} as it corresponds to the {\i mikdash}. Even when {\i birkat kohanim} is performed entirely outside any {\i mikdash}, the {\i gemara} insists on situating it within the {\i avodah} context, represented in {\i tefilla} by the {\i retzei} blessing. The verse {\i vayered me\u8217'asos hachatat} makes clear that Aharon had not yet descended \u8212- he blessed the people while still above, in the very midst of the sacrificial service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beginning Movement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i Rav Ami} and {\i Rav Assi} \u8212- who were themselves {\i kohanim} \u8212- apparently went up to perform {\i birkat kohanim} after the {\i beracha} of {\i retzei} was complete. Does this not contradict the ruling just stated? The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i meikara havu akiri kora\u8217'aihu matu lo havu matu} \u8212- from the beginning they were lifting their legs, they had begun the physical movement, they simply had not yet arrived at the {\i duchan} by the time the {\i beracha} was completed. The process of going up was already underway during {\i retzei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leg Lifting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is given a more general formulation by {\i Rabbi Oshaya}: {\i lo shanu ela shelo akar raglav} \u8212- the rule that one may not go up after the {\i beracha} ends applies only when the {\i kohen} has not yet lifted his leg at all during {\i retzei}. But once he has lifted his leg \u8212- even slightly \u8212- he may go up even after the {\i beracha} has concluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shliach Tzibur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same principle resolves a related question: if the {\i shliach tzibur} is himself a {\i kohen}, may he interrupt his role to perform {\i birkat kohanim} and then return? The answer is: if he is certain he can do so without losing his place \u8212- {\i im havtachto she-nosei kapav ve-chozer litfilato} \u8212- he is permitted. But the difficulty is that as {\i shliach tzibur}, he was standing at the {\i amud} reciting {\i shemoneh esrei} during {\i retzei}, not moving toward the {\i duchan}. How can he be considered to have begun the movement?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Movement Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hacha nami de\u8217'akar porta} \u8212- here too, the slightest movement suffices. Even the faintest physical beginning \u8212- even a shift of weight, a barely perceptible forward motion \u8212- is enough to situate the {\i kohen} within the {\i avodah} context and permit the subsequent ascent. The principle is that the connection to {\i avodah} does not require completed movement; intention expressed in even the most minimal physical act is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Character & Generosity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tov Ayin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} teaches: {\i ein nosnim kos shel bracha levarech ela letov ayin} \u8212- the cup of blessing for {\i birkat hamazon} is given only to one who is {\i tov ayin}, of a generous and benevolent disposition. The reasoning: a {\i beracha} is not only words. The spirit in which a blessing is given matters. A person with a generous heart blesses from abundance; such a blessing carries weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generous Disposition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source verse: {\i tov ayin hu yevorach ki nasan milachmo ladal} \u8212- the one of good eye will be blessed, for he gave of his bread to the poor. The {\i gemara} reads this {\i midrashically}: do not read {\i yevorach} (he will be blessed) but {\i yevarech} (he will bless) \u8212- the same quality of generosity that leads one to give to the poor qualifies one to bestow blessings upon others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birds Know\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} further teaches: even birds recognize the stingy person \u8212- {\i afilu ofos makirim betzarei ayin}. A person\u8217's character is communicated not only through words but through every dimension of his presence. Someone who is stingy broadcasts that quality non-verbally, and even creatures who do not understand speech can sense it. The source: {\i ki chinam mezorah hareshes be\u8217'einei chol ba\u8217'al kanaf} \u8212- \u8220"for in vain is the net spread in the sight of every winged creature.\u8221" The birds see someone scattering seed and they know it is a stingy person who will take and not give; they do not come to him. The deeper point is about the {\i kohanim}: the {\i beracha} is not merely a verbal formula. A {\i kohen} of generous spirit communicates that generosity through the blessing itself, and the non-verbal dimension of the blessing is as real as the words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stingy Transgression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} concludes: {\i kol haneheneh mitzarei ayin over belav} \u8212- one who benefits from a stingy person transgresses a negative commandment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra Ayin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source: {\i al tilcham es lechem ra ayin} \u8212- do not eat the bread of one who has a bad eye. {\i Ki kemo sha\u8217'ar benafsho hu} \u8212- his soul is pained by it. He may say {\i echol ushesah} \u8212- \u8220"eat and drink\u8221" \u8212- but he does not mean it. His outward hospitality is empty; inwardly, he resents the giving. To accept from him is to take what was never truly offered, which is a form of wrongful acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Lavin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} adds: in fact, such a person transgresses not one but two negative commandments \u8212- {\i al tilcham} and {\i al sis\u8217'av}, the second derived from the continuation of the verse: \u8220"do not desire his delicacies.\u8221" Both the act of eating and the cultivating of desire for what a stingy person offers constitute separate transgressions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 39\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tov Ayin & Egla Arufa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Tov Ayin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of the {\i sugya} on {\i birkas kohanim}, picking up from the bottom of 38b. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi had offered a string of statements around {\i birkas kohanim}, including the principle that a {\i bracha} communicates not merely through words but through the inner disposition of the one who gives it. The ideal is {\i tov ayin} \u8212- a generous, well-wishing spirit. The {\i tzar ayin}, by contrast, communicates its stinginess even nonverbally, even to the birds, regardless of what is said aloud. This non-verbal dimension of blessing is the backdrop for what follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzar Ayin & Egla Arufa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi continues: {\i ein egla arufa ba ela bishvil tzaray ha\u8217'ayin} \u8212- the ritual of {\i egla arufa}, which addresses cases of unsolved murder, only becomes necessary on account of those who are stingy of eye. The scriptural basis is the declaration made by the elders: {\i yadeinu lo shafchu es hadam hazeh v\u8217'eineinu lo ra\u8217'u} \u8212- our hands did not spill this blood and our eyes did not see. The Gemara notes the obvious question: would anyone suspect the elders of the court of murder? The answer is that the declaration addresses a different kind of culpability. {\i Ela lo ba l\u8217'yadeinu ufataruhu belo mezonos} \u8212- he came to our hands and we dismissed him without food; {\i velo re\u8217'inuhu vehinachnuhu belo levayah} \u8212- and we did not see to it that he had accompaniment on his journey.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abandoned Traveler\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario implied is that a traveler passed through town without receiving food or escort, and as a consequence \u8212- Rashi suggests he may have attempted to rob someone for food and was killed in the encounter \u8212- something went wrong. The stinginess of the community, its failure to take responsibility for the vulnerable person passing through, lies at the root of the tragedy. It is therefore fitting that {\i egla arufa} is connected to {\i tzar ayin}: the communal failure flows from an absence of the generous, caring spirit that {\i birkas kohanim} is meant to embody. It is also worth noting that the very next chapter of the tractate deals with {\i egla arufa}, making this a natural thematic bridge.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 All Kohanim Congregation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kulo Kohanim Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a ruling of Rav Simlai: {\i beis hakeneses shekula kohanim, kulam olim laduchan} \u8212- in a synagogue where all the members are {\i kohanim}, they all ascend to the {\i duchan}. The Gemara immediately raises the obvious question: {\i lemi mevarchin} \u8212- to whom do they give the blessing? If everyone is on the {\i duchan}, who remains to receive it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Am Besadot & Absent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira answers: {\i l\u8217'acheihem shebasados} \u8212- to their brothers in the fields. The blessing goes out to those who are part of the community even if not physically present in the synagogue. The Gemara supports this with the verse {\i v\u8217'es asher einenu poh imanu hayom} \u8212- and those who are not here with us today \u8212- suggesting that people who belong to the community, even if absent, can receive the blessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones vs.\u160 ?Reshus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara raises a challenge from a {\i baraisa} of Abba the son of Rav Minyamin bar Chiyya: {\i am she\u8217'achorei kohanim einam bikhlal bracha} \u8212- those who stand behind the {\i kohanim} are not included in the blessing. The requirement is {\i panim k\u8217'neged panim}, face to face. If those who are merely behind the {\i kohanim} are excluded, certainly those outside the synagogue entirely should be excluded. The Gemara resolves this: {\i lo kashya, ha d\u8217'anisi, ha d\u8217'lo anisi} \u8212- it is not difficult. The distinction is between those whose absence is coerced ({\i ones}) and those whose absence is voluntary ({\i reshus}). Those who could have been present but chose not to be have effectively removed themselves from the community and do not receive the blessing. Those whose absence is beyond their control remain connected to the {\i tzibur} and receive the blessing. The people standing behind the {\i kohanim} are in a position they could have changed \u8212- they could have been in front \u8212- and so their being behind reflects a voluntary self-exclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Split Arrangement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} of Rabbi Simi from Bei Bersa addresses this practically: in a congregation of all {\i kohanim}, some go up to the {\i duchan} while others remain below to answer {\i amen}. The ideal arrangement, then, is to split the {\i kohanim} so that a receiving congregation remains below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minyan Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies the conditions: {\i ha d\u8217'ishtayer bei asara, ha d\u8217'lo ishtayer bei asara} \u8212- the question is whether ten remain below or not. If the congregation is exactly ten, there is no way to send some up while keeping a {\i kahal} below. But if there are more than ten, some may ascend while a {\i minyan} remains as the receiving {\i tzibur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kahal Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a broader principle: {\i vecho sevarchu es benei Yisrael} \u8212- the {\i bracha} is addressed to {\i benei Yisrael} as a collective. One does not perform {\i birkas kohanim} without a {\i tzibur} present to receive it. The {\i bracha} is inherently communal in character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a series of practical questions that flow naturally from the discussion. If {\i birkas kohanim} requires a {\i minyan}, what happens if the {\i minyan} dissipates after {\i shemoneh esrei} has begun? For {\i Sefardim} who perform {\i birkas kohanim} daily, this could arise in principle; for {\i Ashkenazim} it is largely a {\i Yom Tov} question. The relationship between {\i birkas kohanim} as a component of {\i chazaras hashatz} and as an independent act requiring its own {\i minyan} is genuinely complex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further nuance emerges: one needs a {\i minyan} present in the synagogue in order to perform {\i birkas kohanim} at all, but one does not require a {\i minyan} physically below to receive the blessing \u8212- the {\i am shebesados} fills that role. Ideally, however, if it can be arranged so that a {\i minyan} remains below, it should be. Crucially, the {\i kohanim} who ascend to the {\i duchan} do not count toward the {\i minyan} of those receiving the blessing, but they do count toward the overall {\i minyan} required to hold the service. The scenario of nine {\i Yisraelim} and an indefinite number of {\i am shebesados} filling out the quorum by themselves is plainly untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what constitutes the relevant community for {\i am shebesados} also bears consideration. In the classical case of a town where many people are out in the fields during {\i davening}, the connection to the local community is clear. But what of a tiny congregation where there are simply no others nearby \u8212- would the blessing extend to {\i Klal Yisrael} broadly, or must there be some communal association? The {\i sugya} seems to assume the latter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exclusion & Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Behind Kohanim Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established who is included in {\i birkas kohanim} through the lens of {\i ones} and {\i reshus}, the Gemara now takes up the formal {\i baraisa} of Abba the son of Rav Minyamin bar Chiyya directly: {\i am she\u8217'achorei kohanim einam bikhlal bracha} \u8212- those standing behind the {\i kohanim} are excluded from the blessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Hefsek: Height/Teivah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately asks: is it not obvious? It proceeds to clarify what does {\i not} constitute an interruption. {\i Aricha be\u8217'appei gutzi} \u8212- a tall person standing in front of a shorter person \u8212- {\i lo mafsik}, that does not interrupt. As Rashi notes, there would be no end to this if height were an issue; we cannot require stadium seating in the synagogue. Similarly, {\i teiva lo mafsik} \u8212- the {\i teiva}, the reading platform or {\i bimah}, does not constitute a {\i hefsek} even if it sits between the congregation and the {\i kohanim}. The Gemara then raises the harder case: {\i mechitzah mai} \u8212- what about someone standing behind a partition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos: Self-Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos reframes the question importantly. He argues that the case of the {\i mechitzah} must be referring to someone who is not {\i anus} \u8212- because if they were {\i anus}, it is obvious that a {\i mechitzah} is no worse than being out in the fields entirely, and such a person would clearly receive the blessing along with the {\i am shebesados}. But if they are not {\i anus}, Tosafos raises a pointed question: why should being behind a {\i mechitzah} but in front of the {\i kohanim} be better than being behind the {\i kohanim} without a {\i mechitzah}? The person without a {\i mechitzah} who stands behind the {\i kohanim} seems less physically blocked, and yet they are excluded. Tosafos resolves this by explaining that standing behind the {\i kohanim} \u8212- {\i achorei oref} \u8212- is a statement. {\i Mar\u8217'im d\u8217'atzman ke\u8217'ilu ein habracha chashuvah b\u8217'eineihem} \u8212- they demonstrate that the blessing is not important to them. The Torah uses the language of {\i panim k\u8217'neged panim}; to place yourself behind them is to opt out of the face-to-face relationship. By contrast, someone standing behind a {\i mechitzah} but facing the {\i kohanim} has not made that statement. They are oriented toward the blessing, even if physically obstructed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Iron Wall & Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites the well-known teaching of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i afilu mechitzah shel barzel einah mafsekes bein Yisrael l\u8217'avihem shebashamayim} \u8212- even an iron wall does not divide between Israel and their Father in heaven. This provides the conceptual foundation: physical barriers do not sever the spiritual relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechitzah & Core Minyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos addresses the apparent tension with other {\i sugyos} that seem to require people to be in the same physical space in order to be {\i mitztaref} for a {\i minyan}. The resolution that Tosafos offers, which has become broadly accepted, distinguishes between two questions. To constitute a {\i tzibur} in the first place \u8212- to be able to perform {\i chazaras hashatz}, {\i devarim shebikdusha}, and {\i birkas kohanim} \u8212- one needs a core {\i minyan} in the same location. Once that core {\i minyan} exists and is performing these acts, then others who are behind {\i mechitzos} or in adjoining rooms can connect to and be included in what that {\i tzibur} is doing. The mechanic has something of the quality of {\i hashakah} in a {\i mikvah}: the connected body must maintain its core integrity, and from there extension is possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Drush: God\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos closes with a passage of striking beauty. Citing the {\i gemara} in {\i Perek Ein Omdin} (32b) where Rabbi Elazar states that since the destruction of the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, an iron wall has separated Israel from their Father in heaven, Tosafos notes that these two teachings are not in contradiction. The {\i gemara} in {\i Berachos} is referring to the diminished reception of prayer in the absence of the {\i Beis Hamikdash} \u8212- {\i ein tefilasam miskabeles kemo shebizman Beis Hamikdash kayam}. But with respect to whether the {\i tzibur} is blessed through {\i birkas kohanim} or discharged through the {\i shliach tzibur} \u8212- {\i d\u8217'klapi avinu shebashamayim lo tali b\u8217'hefsek mechitzah} \u8212- from God\u8217's perspective, the walls between us simply do not register. There may be walls that affect how our prayers ascend, but there is no wall that prevents God\u8217's blessing from reaching Israel. This, Tosafos notes, lends itself to a broader {\i drush}: from God\u8217's vantage point, the barriers we perceive between ourselves and Him are not acknowledged on His side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standing to the Side\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a further question: {\i tzadadim mahu} \u8212- what about those who are not behind the {\i kohanim} and not directly in front, but standing alongside them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parah Adumah Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer comes from a {\i mishnah} about the sprinkling of {\i parah adumah} waters. The rule there is: if one intended to sprinkle in front and the water landed behind, or intended to sprinkle behind and it landed in front, the {\i hazayah} is invalid. But {\i lefanav vehizzah al tzadadim shebifanav} \u8212- if one intended to sprinkle in front and it landed on the sides, the {\i hazayah} is {\i kesherah}. Rashi clarifies that {\i tzadadim shebifanav} means things that are alongside, not directly in front and not behind \u8212- and yet they are treated as \u8220"in front.\u8221" The principle follows: being alongside the {\i kohanim} is included within the category of being in front of them. It would not make sense otherwise \u8212- in a large hall, no one could literally stand directly facing the {\i kohanim} at all times, and those alongside them are naturally part of the facing congregation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Washing Hands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Washing & Holiness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi teaches: {\i kol kohen she\u8217'lo natal yadav lo yisa kapav} \u8212- a {\i kohen} who has not washed his hands may not lift his hands to bless. The source is the verse {\i se\u8217'u yedeikhem kodesh u\u8217'varchu es Hashem} \u8212- lift up your hands in holiness and bless God. The word {\i kodesh} in the verse indicates that the hands must be sanctified \u8212- which is achieved through washing \u8212- before the blessing can be given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Immediate Before\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that this washing must take place {\i lifnei aloto laduchan} \u8212- immediately before ascending to the {\i duchan}. The requirement is not merely that the {\i kohen} washed at some point that day, but that the washing is proximate to the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos: When Washing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos engages critically with a version of Rashi\u8217's text that appears to say that morning washing suffices and that a {\i kohen} who washed his hands in the morning and was careful ({\i natar}) need not wash again before ascending. Tosafos questions whether this line is authentic to Rashi or was inserted by a student \u8212- {\i ke\u8217'imdumeh she\u8217'hu hageh mi\u8217'hagahos talmid} \u8212- because Rashi\u8217's own language in explaining the Gemara implies that the washing must be truly proximate, {\i mamash samuch}, to the ascent. The Gemara itself reinforces this by quoting the verse {\i se\u8217'u yedeikhem kodesh u\u8217'varchu} \u8212- suggesting that {\i netilat yadayim} and {\i birkas kohanim} are to follow immediately one upon the other. The practical implication is familiar: when the {\i shliach tzibur} is himself a {\i kohen}, he must wash before the service begins so that he can proceed without interruption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An underlying question, discussed before the {\i daf} began, concerns how one conceptualizes this washing \u8212- as an element of {\i avodah}, aligned with the {\i kohanim}\u8217's washing before Temple service, or as something unique to {\i birkas kohanim} reflected in this particular verse. The choice of verse is telling: the Gemara does not cite the general {\i pasuk} about washing before {\i avodah}, but this specific verse connecting holiness, hands, and blessing. One might argue this makes the {\i netilah} particular to {\i birkas kohanim}, though the word {\i kodesh} in Biblical Hebrew does not technically mean washing the way {\i kiddesh} does in Mishnaic usage \u8212- itself perhaps a deliberate play on the resonance between sanctification and cleansing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bracha Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to ask: what {\i bracha} does a {\i kohen} recite? Rabbi Elazar ben Shamua, in the context of explaining his longevity, mentions that he never performed {\i nesias kapayim} without first making a {\i bracha} \u8212- implying that this was not universal practice. The formula articulated by Rav Zeira citing Rav Chisda is: {\i asher kidshanu b\u8217'mitzvosav u\u8217'vikdushaso shel Aharon v\u8217'tzivanu levarech es amo Yisrael b\u8217'ahava}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aharon\u8217's Sanctity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i bracha} is structurally distinctive. The standard form of a {\i birkas hamitzvah} is {\i asher kidshanu b\u8217'mitzvosav v\u8217'tzivanu}, but here an additional phrase is inserted: {\i u\u8217'vikdushaso shel Aharon} \u8212- and through the sanctity of Aharon. This insertion serves a necessary clarifying function. The word {\i kidshanu} with its plural suffix raises a question: who is the \u8220"we\u8221"? {\i Birkas kohanim} is not a {\i mitzvah} incumbent upon all of Israel. So the {\i bracha} specifies: we \u8212- the {\i kohanim} \u8212- possess a {\i kedushah} rooted in Aharon, and it is by virtue of {\i kedushas Aharon} that we are commanded in this {\i mitzvah}. This is not a claim of superiority but a necessary statement of which {\i kedushah} grants the obligation. It would be worth examining whether a parallel {\i nusach} applies to other distinctly {\i kohen} obligations \u8212- for instance, the {\i bracha} before eating a {\i chatas} \u8212- whether there too the {\i sheiltas Aharon} language appears in the formula.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 B\u8217'ahava (With Love)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final word of the {\i bracha} \u8212- {\i b\u8217'ahava} \u8212- brings the discussion full circle. The verse {\i vecho sevarchu} does not itself contain the word {\i ahava}. Its inclusion in the {\i bracha} formula directly echoes the opening theme of the {\i sugya}: that {\i birkas kohanim} is not merely a ritual act but must be animated by genuine love and generosity of spirit \u8212- the {\i tov ayin} that Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi placed at the center of authentic blessing. The {\i nusach} of the {\i bracha} is thus a theological statement: this blessing works only when it flows from {\i ahava}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birkas Kohanim Choreogra\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehi Ratzon Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now details the choreography of {\i birkas kohanim} step by step. {\i Ki akar krei\u8217'ei mai amar} \u8212- as the {\i kohen} begins moving his legs toward the {\i duchan}, what does he say? He recites a {\i yehi ratzon}: that the blessing should contain no {\i michshol v\u8217'avon} \u8212- no stumbling and no sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific content of the {\i yehi ratzon} invites reflection on what {\i michshol v\u8217'avon} might mean in this context. One reading \u8212- and a compelling one \u8212- is that the {\i kohen} is praying that the very blessing he is about to convey should not itself become a source of stumbling for those who receive it. Wealth, success, and divine favor can become {\i michsholim} when they lead people to arrogance or sin. The {\i kohen} standing as a conduit of blessing is thus asking that what flows through him be genuinely good for those who receive it \u8212- a prayer for the blessing\u8217's positive effect, not merely its transmission. This reading resonates with a parallel seen in the {\i tefillah} recited upon leaving the {\i beis medrash}, where one prays that Torah study not become a source of harm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ribbono Shel Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the blessing is complete and the {\i kohanim} turn their faces back toward the {\i aron}, the Gemara asks what they say at that moment. Rav Chisda expounded before Rav Ukva: {\i Ribbono Shel Olam asinu mah she\u8217'gazarta aleinu} \u8212- Master of the Universe, we have done what You decreed upon us; {\i aseh immanu mah she\u8217'hivtachtanu} \u8212- now do for us what You have promised us. The prayer then quotes {\i Hashkifah mi\u8217'me\u8217'on kodshecha min hashamayim u\u8217'varech es amcha Yisrael} \u8212- look down from Your holy dwelling in the heavens and bless Your people Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about this formulation is the shift in pronouns. The {\i kohanim} say \u8220"do for {\i us}\u8221" \u8212- not \u8220"do for {\i them}.\u8221" Having performed their distinctive role as the conduit of blessing, they now re-enter the general community of Israel as recipients. The {\i hivtachtanu} alludes to {\i Va\u8217'ani Avarchem} \u8212- and I will bless them \u8212- God\u8217's own assurance in the {\i pasuk} that He will confer the blessing upon Israel when the {\i kohanim} pronounce His name. The {\i Ribbono Shel Olam} does not quote this verse explicitly, and perhaps wisely so \u8212- there is a certain {\i chutzpah} in saying \u8220"You promised, now deliver.\u8221" Instead, the {\i kohanim} invoke the closing verse of {\i Hashkifah mi\u8217'me\u8217'on kodshecha} as a request, while the implicit allusion to {\i Va\u8217'ani Avarchem} provides the theological grounding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fingers Straight Until\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda rules that the {\i kohanim} may not fold their fingers back until they have turned their faces away from the congregation. Even after the blessing is complete and {\i amen} has been said, the hands remain extended as long as the {\i kohanim} are still facing the {\i tzibur}. The reason is not stated explicitly, but one attractive reading is that folding the fingers might feel like a withdrawal \u8212- like taking back what has been given \u8212- and as long as one faces the congregation the gesture of blessing remains open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequencing Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira citing Rav Chisda now lays out a precise series of sequencing rules, each one requiring a stage to be fully completed before the next begins. The {\i korei} \u8212- the one who calls out \u8220"kohanim\u8221" \u8212- may not do so until the congregation has fully finished its {\i amen} to {\i Retzei}. The {\i kohanim} may not begin their {\i bracha} until the {\i korei} has finished calling. The congregation may not answer {\i amen} until the {\i kohanim} have completed their {\i bracha} \u8212- all the way to the final word. And the {\i kohanim} may not begin the next {\i bracha} until the congregation\u8217's {\i amen} to the previous one is fully concluded. Each act must be given its full space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remain Until Sim Shalom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The choreography extends further. The {\i kohanim} may not turn their faces away from the congregation \u8212- {\i l\u8217'hachzir p\u8217'neihem min hatzibur} \u8212- until the {\i shliach tzibur} has begun {\i Sim Shalom}. And they may not step down from the {\i duchan} \u8212- {\i la\u8217'akor ragleihem} \u8212- until the {\i shliach tzibur} has completed {\i Sim Shalom}. The sequencing is layered: turn away only when {\i Sim Shalom} begins; descend only when it concludes. The sense is one of sustained presence and dignified transition rather than a quick departure once the ritual words are done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos: Hearing Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos analyzes the reason behind these sequencing requirements by asking whether they are based on the principle of {\i trei kali lo mishtamei} \u8212- that two voices speaking simultaneously cannot both be heard. Tosafos notes a difficulty: the Gemara elsewhere implies that when something is {\i chaviv}, beloved and important, {\i trei kali} are indeed {\i mishtamei}. Moreover, the {\i kohanim} themselves constitute multiple voices, yet they speak simultaneously without concern. Tosafos resolves that the sequencing rules are not fundamentally about the general public failing to hear. They are about the individual worshiper who is still saying {\i amen}: if that person is in the middle of their own {\i amen} when the {\i kohanim} begin the next {\i bracha}, that specific person will not be able to hear the new {\i bracha} they are in the midst of responding to. The concern is thus for the experience of each individual \u8212- the {\i kohanim} must wait not because sound is technically inaudible but because, as a matter of consideration, one should not begin a new stage while any member of the {\i tzibur} is still meaningfully engaged in the previous one. The Gemara\u8217's framing, however, does not seem quite so focused on the hearing of specific individuals \u8212- it has more the quality of giving each act its proper, non-overlapping space.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah & Haftarah Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aliyah Before Haftarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tanchum citing Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i hamaftir banavi tzarich sheyikra baTorah techila} \u8212- one who reads the {\i haftarah} must first receive an {\i aliyah} in the Torah. One cannot leap directly to the {\i haftarah} without first being called to the Torah reading. The proper sequence of sacred acts requires traversing them in order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Roll Torah First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Continuing in the same sequence of teachings, Rabbi Tanchum citing Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi rules: {\i ein hamaftir rashai lehaftir banavi ad sheyigolel Sefer Torah} \u8212- the {\i maftir} may not begin the {\i haftarah} from the prophets until he has rolled up the {\i Sefer Torah} and completed that prior act. One finishes with the Torah before turning to the {\i haftarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kvod Hatzibur: Coverings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tanchum citing Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi adds: {\i ein shaliach tzibur rashai lehafshit es hateiva betzibur} \u8212- the {\i shliach tzibur} may not remove the decorative coverings from the {\i teiva} while the congregation is still present, {\i mipnei kvod hatzibur}. After {\i kriat haTorah}, the elaborate coverings and cloths adorning the reading platform would be put away. To do so while the congregation is still assembled is disrespectful \u8212- stripping the {\i teiva} bare in their presence demeans the setting in which they have gathered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Removal Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tanchum citing Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi rules further: {\i ein hatzibur rasha\u8217'in latzeit ad sheyinnatel Sefer Torah veyaniach bimkomo} \u8212- the congregation may not leave until the {\i Sefer Torah} has been taken and restored to its place. In the time of the Gemara, the {\i Sefer Torah} would sometimes be carried to a more protected private home for safekeeping. Shmuel, however, says: {\i ad sheyeitzei} \u8212- until it goes out, the congregation must wait, even before it has been fully restored to its destination. The Gemara resolves the apparent disagreement: {\i lo pligi} \u8212- they do not actually argue. {\i Ha d\u8217'ika pitcha achrina} \u8212- if there is another door in the synagogue, the congregation may leave by the other door while the {\i Sefer Torah} exits by the first, since they are not walking ahead of it. {\i Ha d\u8217'leika pitcha achrina} \u8212- if there is only one door, the {\i Sefer Torah} must go first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Goes First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concluding verse that grounds this entire sequence of rulings: {\i Acharei Hashem Elokeichem teleichu} \u8212- after God shall you walk. The {\i Sefer Torah}, as the tangible representation of {\i dvar Hashem}, goes first. The people follow. This principle of sequencing \u8212- giving precedence to what is sacred, waiting until the sacred act is complete before moving on \u8212- runs through the entire {\i sugya} and finds its most elemental expression here: you follow after God, not before.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 40\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Response to Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesukim During Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} begins at the bottom of 39b, in the midst of the discussion of {\i birchas kohanim} \u8212- one of the rituals that must be performed in Hebrew. The {\i gemara} poses the question: {\i bizman shehakohanim mevarchin et ha\u8217'am mah hem omrim} \u8212- when the {\i kohanim} bless the people, what do the people say?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nowadays, {\i siddurim} print {\i pesukim} for the congregation to recite silently \u8212- a verse corresponding to each word of the blessing \u8212- though many people simply scan the text with their eyes. The assumption in the {\i gemara}, however, is more active: the congregation would say something during the blessing itself, something that relates not merely to each individual word but to the very fact of receiving a divine blessing through the {\i kohanim}. This is distinct from the {\i amen} said at the conclusion; what is described here occurs during the blessing itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shacharis Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira said in the name of Rav Hisda: the congregation recites the closing verses of Psalm 103 \u8212- {\i barechu Hashem malachav gibborei ko\u8217'ach}, praise God, His angels of great strength; {\i barechu Hashem kol tzeva\u8217'av meshartav osei retzono}, all His hosts, those who serve God and do His will; {\i barechu Hashem kol ma\u8217'asav bechol mekomos memshalto barchi nafshi et Hashem}, all His creatures in all places of His dominion should praise Him \u8212- my soul shall bless God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inner logic here is striking: the {\i kohanim} declare that God will bless the people, and the people respond, as it were, in kind \u8212- praising and blessing God in return. God blesses you, and you, in gratitude, call upon all of creation to bless God back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Musaf Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i Shabbas Musaf} \u8212- an additional {\i Shemoneh Esrei} \u8212- Rabbi Assi states that the congregation recites: {\i Shir hama\u8217'alos hinei barechu et Hashem kol avdei Hashem} (Psalm 134:1), behold, bless God all you servants of God; {\i se\u8217'u yedekhem kodesh uvarechu et Hashem} (ibid. 2), lift up your hands in holiness and bless God; and {\i baruch Hashem mitziyon shokhen Yerushalayim halleluyah} (Psalm 135:21), blessed is God from Zion, who dwells in Jerusalem, praise God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes, however, that the third {\i pasuk} is taken from a different {\i perek} in {\i Tehillim}. Why not use {\i yevarekhekha Hashem mitziyon}, which appears in the same {\i perek} as the first two verses? Yehudah the son of Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi explains: since the theme of the congregational response is blessing God \u8212- mirroring the blessing the {\i kohanim} channel toward the people \u8212- one must end with a verse that blesses God. {\i Baruch Hashem mitziyon} accomplishes this. {\i Yevarekhekha Hashem mitziyon}, by contrast, speaks of God blessing you \u8212- precisely what the {\i kohanim} are already saying. The response must be a reciprocal blessing directed toward God, not a repetition of what is being received.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One observes, as well, that just as there are three {\i pesukim} of {\i birchas kohanim}, so too there are three {\i pesukim} of response \u8212- one corresponding to each.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taanis & Yom Kippur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i mincha} on a {\i taanis} \u8212- when {\i birchas kohanim} is performed because there is no concern that the {\i kohanim} have been drinking \u8212- Rav Acha bar Yaakov provides a markedly different set of verses: {\i im avonoseinu anu vanu Hashem aseh lema\u8217'an shemekha} (Jeremiah 14:7), though our sins testify against us, God act for the sake of Your name; {\i mikveh Yisrael moshio be\u8217'eis tzarah} (ibid. 8), O Hope of Israel, its savior in time of distress; {\i lamah tihyeh keger ba\u8217'aretz lamah tihyeh khe\u8217'ish nidham kegibor} (ibid. 9), why should You be like a stranger in the land, like a mighty man who cannot save?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is notably different in character from the other responses. Rather than blessing God in return for the blessing received, the congregation uses the occasion as an opening to pray for the very purpose of the {\i taanis} \u8212- to importune God to answer and to act. Receiving a blessing and standing in connection with God, they seize the moment to beseech Him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At {\i ne\u8217'ila} on {\i Yom Kippur}, Mar Zutra (and some say this appears in a {\i baraisa}) gives: {\i hinei ki chen yevorach gever yere Hashem} (Psalm 128:4), behold, so shall the man who fears God be blessed; {\i yevarekhekha Hashem miTziyon ure\u8217'eh betuv Yerushalayim kol yemei chayekha ure\u8217'eh vanim levanekha shalom al Yisrael} (ibid. 5\u8211-6), God should bless you from Zion and may you see Jerusalem\u8217's goodness all the days of your life, and see your children\u8217's children \u8212- peace upon Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here, at the close of {\i Yom Kippur}, the response is neither a return-blessing to God nor a petition for rain. Instead it expresses the aspiration of a {\i yerei Hashem}, a God-fearing person receiving blessing, looking forward to a year of life and continuity and peace. Of all the responses enumerated, only the standard {\i shacharis} version \u8212- where one blesses God in response to receiving blessing from God \u8212- follows the pure structure of reciprocal praise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing of Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the question of where exactly within {\i birchas kohanim} these {\i pesukim} would be recited. Rabbi Elazar says: between each {\i bracha} \u8212- that is, after each of the three {\i pesukim} of the blessing. Rav Sheshet says: at the mention of God\u8217's name \u8212- {\i b\u8217'hazkarat Hashem} \u8212- since hearing the divine name in the context of this blessing is itself what calls forth the response of praise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mar and Rav Zevid dispute the structure of the response. One holds that it was {\i pasuka likaval pasuka} \u8212- one response-{\i pasuk} corresponding to each {\i pasuk} of the blessing. The other holds that upon each {\i pasuk} of the {\i kohanim}, the congregation recited all three {\i pesukim} of the response.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Only in Mikdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya bar Abba View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that these {\i pesukim} were said, the {\i gemara} turns to whether they may be recited outside the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. Rabbi Chiya bar Abba rules: {\i kol ha\u8217'omram bigvulin eino ela to\u8217'eh} \u8212- whoever recites them outside the {\i Beis Hamikdash} is simply making a mistake. The rationale is that these verses were a response to the power of hearing the explicit divine name pronounced in the {\i Mikdash}. Whether one holds that the {\i pesukim} were said at {\i shem Hashem} or between the {\i brachot}, the sense of God\u8217's manifest presence bestowing blessing \u8212- which invokes this particular response \u8212- belongs specifically to the {\i Mikdash} context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanina bar Pappa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa goes further and disagrees more fundamentally. He says: {\i teda} \u8212- know \u8212- that not only are they inappropriate outside the {\i Mikdash}, they should not be said even in the {\i Mikdash}. His reasoning: {\i klum yesh lecha eved shemevarchin oto vehu machzir} \u8212- is there a slave who, when receiving a blessing, talks back? Even if his words are grateful and responsive, should one not simply listen attentively to the blessing being given? The argument implies that the {\i pesukim} are said during the blessing itself \u8212- during the words of the {\i kohanim} \u8212- which makes Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa\u8217's objection coherent. One is supposed to receive the blessing, not be murmuring verses simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing, however, sits in some tension with Rabbi Elazar\u8217's view that the {\i pesukim} are said between the {\i brachot}, not during them \u8212- and the {\i gemara} does not fully reconcile these positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yitzchak bar Chanina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak bar Chanina takes the opposite position: {\i teda} \u8212- know \u8212- that it is entirely appropriate to say them even outside the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. His counter-argument mirrors Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa\u8217's imagery but inverts it: {\i klum yesh eved shemevarchin oso ve\u8217'ein mekabbel panim} \u8212- is there a slave who, when blessed, shows no acknowledgment at all? If one receives a blessing, one ought to demonstrate gratitude and respond in kind. This reasoning aligns naturally with the view that the {\i pesukim} are said between the {\i brachot} rather than during them \u8212- a moment of response, not of distraction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abahu\u8217's Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abahu reports: {\i merish hava amina lehu} \u8212- I used to recite these {\i pesukim} outside the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. But once he observed Rabbi Abba of Akko not saying them, he too stopped. The specific weight Rabbi Abahu gave to this behavior will become clear momentarily, in light of his deep respect for Rabbi Abba as a man of exceptional humility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Humility Digression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abahu also relates: I used to think of myself as a humble person \u8212- {\i anvetana ana}. But once I observed Rabbi Abba of Akko\u8217's conduct, I realized that I was not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What was Rabbi Abba\u8217's conduct? When Rabbi Abba would convey a teaching to his {\i amora} \u8212- together with the reasoning behind it \u8212- the {\i amora} would then stand up and announce to the assembled crowd his own reason instead of, or in addition to, Rabbi Abba\u8217's. This requires some explanation of the role of the {\i amora}. In the academies, the term {\i tana} referred not only to the sages of the {\i Mishnah} and {\i baraisa} era but to a specific functionary: a person who had memorized all the {\i mishnayos} and {\i baraisos} and would recite them upon request \u8212- a living repository. Similarly, the {\i amora} (from {\i amira}, speech) was a kind of loudspeaker: the rabbi would say his {\i shiur} quietly, and the {\i amora} would repeat it loudly so the entire crowd could hear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What Rabbi Abba\u8217's {\i amora} would do was substitute \u8212- or supplement \u8212- Rabbi Abba\u8217's reasoning with his own. Perhaps he would say: \u8220"Rabbi Abba teaches this {\i halacha}, and here is my reason.\u8221" Rabbi Abba {\i lo kapid} \u8212- he did not mind at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most plausible reading, in order to reconcile this with the obligation to teach Torah accurately, is that the {\i amora} announced his own reasoning in his own name \u8212- explicitly \u8212- and Rabbi Abba allowed him to use the public platform to do so. The humility displayed was not just tolerance of being contradicted, but the willingness to let a subordinate elevate himself to a seemingly equal footing in public.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, Rabbi Abahu\u8217's own humility \u8212- which he now recognized as lesser \u8212- consisted of the following: the wife of Rabbi Abahu\u8217's {\i amora} told Rabbi Abahu\u8217's wife: \u8220"Ours doesn\u8217't need yours. My husband is capable enough to give his own {\i shiur} \u8212- he bends down to hear Rabbi Abahu and then stands up to announce it only as a gesture of respect, because Rabbi Abahu has connections to the government ({\i bei Keisar}). But he could manage entirely on his own.\u8221" Rabbi Abahu\u8217's wife went and told him this. His response: {\i mai nafka lach minha} \u8212- what does it matter to you? {\i Minei uminah yitkales ila\u8217'ah} \u8212- between me and him, God\u8217's name is elevated. He was not {\i makpid}. Let the man think he gives just as good a {\i shiur} \u8212- who is harmed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rabbi Abahu recognized that this was a private remark, made to a wife \u8212- not a public act of self-elevation like what Rabbi Abba\u8217's {\i amora} had done. The degree of humility demanded to truly not mind, as Rabbi Abba of Akko did not mind, was of a different order entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further story about Rabbi Abahu\u8217's regard for Rabbi Abba: when the rabbis considered appointing Rabbi Abahu as {\i Rosh Yeshiva} \u8212- a position that brought with it significant material support \u8212- Rabbi Abahu saw that Rabbi Abba of Akko was burdened with many creditors and owed considerable debts. He told the rabbis: there is a greater rabbi \u8212- appoint Rabbi Abba of Akko. This would allow Rabbi Abba to pay off his debts through the income that came with the position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewels & Knickknacks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now relates a famous episode directly involving Rabbi Abahu and Rabbi Chiya bar Abba. The two came together to a certain town. Rabbi Abahu delivered a {\i shiur} in {\i agadata} \u8212- a {\i midrash}, a sermon, a meaningful message. Rabbi Chiya bar Abba delivered a serious {\i halacha shiur} with rigorous sources and analysis. Everyone left Rabbi Chiya bar Abba\u8217's lecture and went to hear Rabbi Abahu. Rabbi Chiya bar Abba was despondent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abahu consoled him with a {\i mashal}: to what may this be compared? To two merchants who come to a village \u8212- one selling precious gems and pearls, the other selling small knickknacks, needles, and trinkets. {\i Al mi koftzin} \u8212- to whom does everyone rush? To the one selling the knickknacks, of course, because everyone can buy something small and useful. Nobody can afford the jewels. Your {\i shiur} is clearly at a far higher level, Rabbi Abahu was saying \u8212- your Torah is the jewels. But it is simply beyond these people. They come to me not because my Torah is greater, but because it is more accessible to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Every day, Rabbi Chiya bar Abba would escort Rabbi Abahu to his lodging \u8212- {\i mishum yekara d\u8217'vei Keisar} \u8212- as a gesture of honor, since Rabbi Abahu\u8217's proximity to the imperial government gave him special status. On that day, in recognition of Rabbi Chiya bar Abba\u8217's true greatness in Torah, Rabbi Abahu reversed the gesture and escorted Rabbi Chiya bar Abba to his lodging instead. And even so, {\i lo itosev da\u8217'tei minei} \u8212- it did not fully settle Rabbi Chiya bar Abba\u8217's spirit. Being told that one possesses extraordinary Torah is gratifying, but it does not entirely substitute for feeling that people want to hear from you.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modim Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modim Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions naturally: having discussed what the congregation says when receiving {\i birchas kohanim}, it asks what the congregation says when the {\i shaliach tzibbur} recites {\i modim} during {\i chazaras hashatz}. This is one of the two signature moments of the {\i chazara} \u8212- alongside {\i kedusha} \u8212- where communal participation is most essential. During {\i kedusha}, the congregation sanctifies God\u8217's name together; during {\i modim}, the congregation echoes the {\i shaliach tzibbur}\u8217's expression of communal gratitude, each person saying their own {\i modim} while he says his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So {\i mah hem omrim} \u8212- what does the congregation say? Rav says: {\i Modim anachnu lach Hashem Elokeinu al she\u8217'anu modim lach} \u8212- we thank You, God our Lord, for the fact that we thank You. This is a remarkable formulation. Rashi explains: we thank You for having given us the capacity and the closeness to You that enables us to thank You \u8212- gratitude for the very ability to be grateful. In one\u8217's own silent {\i Shemoneh Esrei}, one has already thanked God for all one has prayed for; now, in the repetition, the congregation says: we are so thankful to be a people who stand before You in prayer and are able to offer You thanks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modim Variations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel elaborates: {\i Elokei kol basar al she\u8217'anu modim lach} \u8212- God of all flesh, that we thank You. Rabbi Simai elaborates further: {\i Yotzreinu yotzer bereishis} \u8212- our Creator, Creator of the beginning \u8212- {\i al she\u8217'anu modim lach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sages of Nehardea add, in the name of Rabbi Simai, something more concrete: {\i berachos vehoda\u8217'os lishmecha hagadol al shehecheyisanu vekiyamsanu} \u8212- blessings and thanks to Your great name for having given us life and sustained us \u8212- {\i al she\u8217'anu modim lach}. Now the thanks are no longer purely reflexive; something specific is named: life itself. Rav Acha bar Yaakov then appends a {\i bakasha} to Rabbi Simai\u8217's formulation: {\i ken techayeinu usechaneinu} \u8212- so too may You give us life and be gracious to us \u8212- {\i vetikabetzeinu ve\u8217'esof galuyoseinu lechatzros kodshecha} \u8212- gather us to Your holy courtyards \u8212- {\i velishmor chukecha vela\u8217'asos retzonecha belevav shalem} \u8212- to observe Your laws and do Your will wholeheartedly \u8212- {\i al she\u8217'anachnu modim lach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the formulation has traveled quite far from its origin. The elegant core \u8212- {\i modim al she\u8217'anu modim}, thanksgiving for the capacity of thanksgiving \u8212- has been expanded into petition, and the {\i netziv} of {\i Modim Derabbanan} as commonly recited today includes this fuller material. Something of the purity of that original formulation is worth pausing over.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa concludes: since all of these formulations contain beautiful and fitting content, we say all of them together \u8212- which is precisely what became our practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Community Respect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak states: a leader should always have {\i eimas tzibbur} upon him \u8212- a sense of reverence for, and obligation toward, the community. The {\i gemara} derives this from {\i birchas kohanim}: when the {\i kohanim} bless the people, their faces are toward the people and their backs are, as it were, toward the {\i Shechina} \u8212- toward where the {\i Aron} stands, the front of the sanctuary. Such is the degree of respect shown to the congregation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman derives the same principle from a verse in Divrei Hayamim: {\i vayakom hamelech David al raglav vayomer shim\u8217'uni achai ve\u8217'ami} \u8212- King David rose to his feet and said, hear me, my brothers and my people. The {\i gemara} asks: if they are {\i achai}, my brothers \u8212- his equals \u8212- why call them {\i ami}, my people? And if they are {\i ami}, his subjects \u8212- why call them {\i achai}? Rabbi Elazar explains: David was saying, if you are prepared to listen to me willingly, then I will treat you as brothers, as equals. {\i Ve\u8217'im lav ami atem va\u8217'ani rodeh eschem bemakkel} \u8212- and if not, I will treat you as subjects and compel you. The honor and equality offered to the community is thus conditional on their responsiveness \u8212- but the baseline posture of a leader toward his community is one of respect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} then bring this discussion back to {\i birchas kohanim} with the teaching: {\i ein kohanim rashaim la\u8217'alos besandaleihem laduchan} \u8212- {\i kohanim} may not ascend the {\i duchan} wearing sandals. This is one of the nine {\i takanos} of Rabban Yochanan ben Zakai.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blessing Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim Sandals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what is the reason for this {\i takana}? The obvious suggestion is {\i kevod tzibbur} \u8212- when the {\i kohanim} lift their arms and the hem of their garments rises, muddy sandals are exposed, and this is unseemly before the congregation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi, however, disagrees. The real concern is different: {\i shema nifsika lei retzu\u8217'a besandalo} \u8212- perhaps the strap of a {\i kohen}\u8217's sandal breaks on the way up to the {\i duchan}, and he bends down to retie it. Everyone else continues up to perform {\i birchas kohanim}, and he is left crouching on the ground. People will say: he is not going up because he is a {\i ben grusha o ben chalutza} \u8212- the son of a divorced woman or a woman who performed {\i chalitza} \u8212- and is therefore disqualified from performing {\i birchas kohanim}. To avoid this source of suspicion, the {\i takana} requires all {\i kohanim} to remove their sandals before ascending.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i bamikdash bracha achas} \u8212- in the {\i Mikdash}, {\i birchas kohanim} was recited as a single uninterrupted blessing. The reason: {\i lefi she\u8217'ein onim amen bamikdash} \u8212- because {\i amen} is not said in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. Since there is no {\i amen} following each of the three {\i pesukim}, they are not treated as three separate {\i brachot} but as a continuous unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a notable tension with the earlier discussion in the {\i gemara}. The {\i gemara} above spoke of reciting {\i pesukim} in the {\i Mikdash} between or during the {\i brachot} \u8212- and that would seem to break them up. {\i Tosafos} resolves this by suggesting that {\i baruch shem kavod malchuto le\u8217'olam va\u8217'ed} \u8212- said at {\i shem Hashem}, in the middle of a {\i bracha} rather than at its conclusion \u8212- would not constitute a division between the three {\i pesukim}. This works well according to the opinion that the {\i pesukim} were recited at {\i shem Hashem}. According to the opinion that they were said between the {\i brachot}, the difficulty remains, and the {\i gemara} does not directly address it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Amen Mikdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: from where do we know that {\i amen} is not said in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}? The source is the verse in Ezra: {\i kumu barchu et Hashem Elokeichem min ha\u8217'olam ve\u8217'ad ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- rise and bless God from this world to the next world \u8212- which encapsulates the liturgical formula {\i baruch shem kavod malchuto le\u8217'olam va\u8217'ed} as the response in the {\i Mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And from where do we know that this response is given after each individual {\i bracha}? From the verse: {\i veyivarchu shem kevodecha umeromam al kol bracha usehilla} \u8212- and they shall bless Your glorious name, which is exalted above every {\i bracha} and praise \u8212- teaching that for every single {\i bracha}, one gives praise. That praise takes the form of {\i baruch shem kavod}, not {\i amen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is {\i amen} not said in the {\i Mikdash}? The {\i Maharsha} offers an explanation involving {\i gematria}. A more compelling approach may be conceptual: {\i amen} is fundamentally the response of a follower \u8212- one who hears another initiate a blessing and affirms it. There is power in this role; the {\i gemara} elsewhere speaks at length of the extraordinary spiritual weight of {\i amen}. But in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, where one stands in the direct presence of God, perhaps the expectation is different. If one is going to respond to a blessing of God at all, one must do so with one\u8217's own active blessing \u8212- {\i baruch shem kavod} \u8212- not merely with a secondary affirmation of someone else\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 41\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 High Priest\u8217's Blessi (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 High Priest Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} on 40b opens a new topic: having concluded the extended discussion of {\i birchas kohanim}, the {\i mishna} turns to other rituals that must be performed specifically in Hebrew. The first of these is {\i birchas kohen gadol} \u8212- and by introducing it this way, the {\i mishna} uses the occasion to explore how these ceremonies were actually conducted. Some of the rituals described in this section of {\i Sotah} are primarily historical in character, like the {\i brachos u\u8217'klallos}; others, like {\i birchas kohanim}, remain practically operative. {\i Birchas kohen gadol} falls somewhere in between \u8212- it was fully operative when the {\i beis hamikdash} stood, and the {\i mishna} treats it as an opportunity to reconstruct what it looked like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The context is {\i yom hakippurim}. After all the {\i korbanos} have been brought and the {\i sa\u8217'ir} has been sent {\i l\u8217'azazel}, the {\i kohen gadol} and the assembly conduct a {\i keriat hatorah} in the {\i azarah}. This occurs at the conclusion of the {\i avodas hayom}, when the bulk of the unique service has been completed. It is worth noting that the {\i mishna} in {\i Yoma} records an interesting variant: the reading could be performed either in the white {\i bigdei kehuna} of {\i yom kippur} or in a special cloak prepared for this service \u8212- a detail that raises its own questions about how fully this reading was incorporated into the structure of the {\i avoda} proper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Passing Ritual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} describes the ceremony of transferring the {\i sefer torah} in detail. The {\i chazzan haknesset} \u8212- the attendant of the synagogue who handles all the practical affairs of the {\i knesset}, whom {\i Rashi} describes as the {\i shammash} who bears responsibility for all the workings of the synagogue, bringing things in and out, preparing everything \u8212- takes the {\i sefer torah} and gives it to the {\i rosh haknesset}. The {\i rosh haknesset} is the one who makes the decisions: who reads the {\i haftarah}, who leads the prayers. {\i Rashi} captures this distinction sharply: {\i al piv nechtachin divrei haknesset} \u8212- on his word all matters of the {\i knesset} are decided. The roles correspond, roughly, to what we today combine in the {\i gabbai}. The {\i rosh haknesset} then passes the {\i sefer torah} to the {\i segan}, the deputy {\i kohen gadol}, who in turn gives it to the {\i kohen gadol} himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might pause here to ask: which {\i knesset} is this? Are we speaking of a synagogue on {\i har habayit}? Was there some formal communal institution in {\i yerushalayim} that the {\i chazzan haknesset} represented? This is genuinely unclear. What is evident is that the ritual is synagogue-like in character \u8212- a ceremonial reading of the {\i torah} \u8212- yet the precise identity of the {\i knesset} in question remains uncertain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading Sections\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the {\i sefer torah} reaches him, the {\i kohen gadol} stands and receives it, and reads from {\i acharei mos} \u8212- the {\i avodas yom hakippurim} \u8212- and from {\i ach ba\u8217'asor}, the section in {\i Emor} in {\i Vayikra} describing the {\i musafin} of {\i yom kippur}, drawn from the cycle of {\i musvei yom tov}. After reading these sections, he rolls the {\i sefer torah} closed, holds it against his chest, and declares: {\i yoser mi-mah she-karasi lifneichem kasuv kan} \u8212- more than what I have read before you is written here. He is signaling that there is an additional section he will recite, but which he will not read from the scroll. That additional section \u8212- {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'asur sheb\u8217'chumash hapekudim} \u8212- is the corresponding passage in {\i Pinchas} in {\i Bamidbar}, which also describes the {\i musaf hayom} of {\i yom kippur}. He recites this second passage by heart, {\i al peh}, rather than reading it inside the scroll.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason he does not simply scroll to {\i Pinchas} will be addressed by the {\i gemara}, but the statement itself requires explanation. Why does he feel compelled to announce that more is written here than he read? Because he then proceeds to recite something without reading from the {\i sefer torah}, one might suspect that the text he is reciting is simply not found in it. To dispel any suspicion that there is some deficiency in the {\i sefer torah}, he preemptively declares that everything is indeed written \u8212- he is simply not reading it from inside. This logic, as we will see, connects directly to the broader {\i halachic} principle of {\i tircha d\u8217'tzibura}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After completing both readings, the {\i kohen gadol} recites eight {\i brachos}. The {\i mishna} lists them: on the {\i torah}, on the {\i avoda}, on the {\i hoda\u8217'ah} (thanksgiving), on {\i mechilas avon} (forgiveness of sin), on the {\i mikdash}, on {\i yisrael}, on the {\i kohanim}, and on {\i yerushalayim} \u8212- with a final element described as {\i v\u8217'hashar tefillah}, a general supplication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about these {\i brachos} is that they are not merely {\i birchos hatorah} in the ordinary sense. Rather, the completion of the {\i keriat hatorah} is being used as a framework for reciting {\i brachos} over the entirety of the {\i avoda} that has just culminated. The {\i al ha\u8217'avoda} is, according to {\i Rashi}, the {\i retzei} \u8212- the prayer asking God to accept the service \u8212- though {\i Rashi} notes in {\i Yoma} that the concluding formula cannot be the {\i hamachazir shechinaso l\u8217'tziyon} of our {\i shemoneh esrei}, since that does not fit the context of an {\i avoda} just completed. Instead, it may correspond to the {\i retzei} found in the context of {\i birchas kohanim}: {\i she\u8217'otcha levadcha b\u8217'yirah na\u8217'avod}, which carries precisely the sense that the {\i avoda} is being performed right now. {\i Rashi} adds the principle: {\i achar zevichah hoda\u8217'ah} \u8212- after the sacrifice one gives thanks \u8212- which is why the {\i al ha\u8217'avoda} leads naturally into the {\i al ha\u8217'hoda\u8217'ah}, corresponding to {\i modim} of the {\i shemoneh esrei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure thus mirrors the {\i shemoneh esrei} in a loose way: {\i retzei}, {\i modim}, and then the hope that the {\i avoda} will effect {\i mechilas avon} \u8212- corresponding to the {\i atah bechartanu} type of blessing in which God is described as {\i melech mochel v\u8217'solei\u8217'ach la\u8217'avonot}. This is followed by {\i birchos} on the {\i mikdash}, on {\i yisrael}, and so on. {\i Rashi}\u8217's formulations for some of these are suggestive: {\i baruch asher bachar baMikdash}, {\i baruch habocher b\u8217'amo Yisrael}. The shape of the {\i davening} is recognizable, even though one is entering it, so to speak, from the middle. What is notable is that this is not fundamentally about the {\i keriat hatorah} at all \u8212- it is using the reading of the {\i torah} as the occasion to offer a comprehensive liturgical response to everything that has just happened on {\i yom kippur}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Honor Display\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws an inference from the chain of transmission: since the {\i sefer torah} passed from {\i chazzan} to {\i rosh haknesset} to {\i segan} before reaching the {\i kohen gadol}, one might conclude that we practice {\i cholkin kavod l\u8217'talmid bimkom harav} \u8212- we honor the lesser figure even in the presence of the greater one. But this reading, while initially plausible, does not survive scrutiny.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} sharpens the problem considerably. The obvious inference \u8212- that all these intermediate stages are simply meant to heighten the honor of the {\i kohen gadol} by approaching him through multiple steps \u8212- is exactly right. The question is more specific: granted that you want to demonstrate the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's elevated status by passing the {\i sefer torah} upward through the hierarchy, why was it necessary to include the {\i rosh haknesset} at all? Why not go from the {\i chazzan} directly to the {\i segan}? Skipping the {\i rosh haknesset} would still produce an ascending chain \u8212- {\i chazzan} to {\i segan} to {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- which would equally serve to highlight how high the {\i kohen gadol} stands. The {\i rosh haknesset} is, after all, considerably lower in the hierarchy than the {\i segan} or the {\i kohen gadol}. Who is slighted by bypassing him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} answers its own question: even the {\i segan} would be slightly dishonored if the {\i sefer torah} arrived in his hands having come directly from someone two levels below him rather than from the person immediately beneath him in the hierarchy. But {\i Tosafot} then presses further \u8212- and this is where the genuine insight lies \u8212- why should we care? If the entire enterprise is about the honor of the {\i kohen gadol}, let the {\i segan} be slighted. What is wrong with going from {\i chazzan} to {\i segan}, bypassing the {\i rosh haknesset}, and then from {\i segan} to {\i kohen gadol}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's answer is: {\i kulo mishum kevodo d\u8217'kohen gadol} \u8212- the entire protocol, including every intermediate stage, is entirely for the sake of the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's honor. This is not a concession to the lesser figures in the middle. Rather, having every step proceed in careful, orderly sequence \u8212- one rung at a time \u8212- itself amplifies the honor of the person at the top. To bypass any intermediate figure, even if it would only slightly reduce the honor of those bypassed, would diminish the cumulative effect of the ascent. Every stage of the ceremony increases the {\i kavod} of the {\i kohen gadol}, not merely the final transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading represents a significant refinement over the simpler understanding. {\i Rashi} might have read the chain as a natural courtesy to the intermediate figures. {\i Tosafot}, and through them Abaye\u8217's principle, establishes something more precise: the staged ascent is not a balance between honoring multiple parties but a single coherent act of honoring one supreme authority. Even a subtle disruption to the continuity of that ascent \u8212- even bypassing the {\i rosh haknesset} \u8212- would undermine the message that the entire ceremony is designed to convey.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah vs Haftarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jump Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After establishing that the {\i kohen gadol} reads from {\i acharei mos} and then from {\i ach ba\u8217'asor}, the {\i gemara} raises a contradiction. There is a principle that one may jump ({\i medalgin}) in the {\i navi} \u8212- that is, when reading the {\i haftarah} one may skip from one passage to another \u8212- but one may not jump in the {\i torah}. Yet here the {\i kohen gadol} is apparently jumping from one section of the {\i torah} to another. How is this permitted?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Translator Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a first resolution: {\i lo kashya} \u8212- there is no contradiction. The distinction depends on whether the skip can be completed before the {\i meturgeman} \u8212- the translator \u8212- finishes rendering the preceding passage. If the reader can reach the next {\i pasuk} before the translator concludes, there is no pause, no moment of waiting, and the jump is permissible. If not, there is a pause, and that is {\i tircha d\u8217'tzibura}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} then cites a {\i braita} that complicates this resolution. The {\i braita} teaches: one may jump in {\i navi}, one may not jump in {\i torah} \u8212- and then specifies that even in {\i navi}, jumping is permitted only up to the point where the translator has not yet finished. This implies that even in {\i navi}, you cannot create a pause. But the {\i braita} is also saying that in {\i torah}, even if you can reach the next {\i pasuk} before the translator finishes, you still cannot jump. Timing alone, then, cannot be the full criterion. So Abaye offers a second, more refined resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theme Continuity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo kashya} \u8212- there is no difficulty. The real distinction is between jumping within a single theme ({\i inyan echad}) and jumping between different themes ({\i shnei inyanim}). In the {\i torah}: you may jump if you are staying within the same subject matter and can complete the jump before the translator finishes. In the {\i navi}: you may jump even between different themes, but still only if you can do so before the translator finishes. And within the {\i navi}, you may not jump from one {\i sefer} of {\i navi} to another \u8212- with the exception of {\i trei asar}, the twelve minor prophets, which form a single {\i sefer}. Even there, however, one may not go backwards within the {\i sefer}, as that would create confusion for the listeners.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Listener Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} provides a beautifully precise explanation for why you cannot jump between themes in the {\i torah} even with adequate timing. When a person\u8217's mind is engaged with one subject, it does not immediately disengage and redirect itself toward a new theme. The listener cannot easily switch attention. In the context of reading the {\i torah} \u8212- where the purpose is not merely ritual but genuine instruction in the {\i mitzvos} \u8212- this matters enormously. {\i Rashi} puts it sharply: {\i she\u8217'ein halev shehu nimshach achar inyan echad mmaher latzet mimenu lishmoa lehavin divrei inyan echad} \u8212- a heart drawn along one line of thought does not quickly exit it to absorb a different subject.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And the stakes are high. The {\i torah} is the source of the {\i mitzvos} that people are obligated to perform. Reading the {\i torah} publicly is an opportunity for people to hear and internalize what is required of them. If a listener is shifting between topics and cannot follow properly, a {\i pasuk} goes ununderstood, it passes over him \u8212- {\i over alav v\u8217'eino mekayma} \u8212- and he will not fulfill it. In this light, it is better not to read the passage at all than to read it in a way that guarantees the listener will not absorb it. Switching themes mid-reading ensures exactly that failure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i navi}, by contrast, does not carry the same weight of obligatory practice. Missing a nuance or losing the thread in the {\i haftarah} does not produce the same halakhic consequences. Hence the asymmetry: in {\i torah}, thematic continuity is a precondition; in {\i navi}, it is not required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 High Priest\u8217's Blessi (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Scrolling Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the framework for jumping in the {\i torah}, the {\i gemara} returns to the earlier question: why does the {\i kohen gadol} recite the passage from {\i pinchas} by heart rather than reading it inside? The {\i gemara} phrases the question with apparent surprise \u8212- {\i velirkacha lesifra vlikrei}, why not just scroll and read? This is puzzling because the issue seems already to have been addressed. {\i Rashi} in fact explains that what was happening is that while the {\i kohen gadol} was scrolling from {\i acharei mos} toward the next section, the {\i meturgeman} was translating the last {\i pasuk} \u8212- they were working simultaneously so there would be no perceived pause. It is {\i pasuk} by {\i pasuk}, not section by section, that the translation proceeds, and the scrolling and translating overlap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a further question is articulated explicitly: {\i v\u8217'ha\u8217'amar Rav Huna bar Yehuda amar Rav Sheshet} \u8212- because {\i ein golin sefer torah b\u8217'tzibur}, one does not scroll the {\i sefer torah} before the public. The act of visibly scrolling while the congregation sits and waits is itself a form of {\i tircha d\u8217'tzibura}, not merely the passage of time but the spectacle of delay. Even if time is being used productively \u8212- even if the translator is speaking \u8212- the visual fact of a pause in the reading, with the scroll being moved, is unseemly. It is not the absolute quantity of time that constitutes {\i tircha} but rather the felt sense of waiting and hesitation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then ask: why not simply bring a second {\i sefer torah} opened to {\i pinchas}? {\i Rav Huna bar Yehuda} addresses this as well: {\i mishum pigmo shel rishon} \u8212- because it raises the appearance of a defect in the first {\i sefer torah}. If the {\i kohen gadol} sets aside one scroll and takes up another in the middle of his reading, the onlookers will wonder why. There must be something wrong with the first one. {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish} adds an additional reason: bringing a second {\i sefer torah} within the same {\i aliyah} would necessitate an additional and superfluous {\i bracha} \u8212- {\i bracha she\u8217'eina tzricha}. The {\i bracha} attaches to the {\i cheftza}, the {\i sefer torah} itself, and to bring a second one in the midst of the same reading implies an additional {\i bracha} over it, which is unnecessary and therefore improper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks whether this concern about {\i pigmo shel rishon} is really coherent, since we know from {\i Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha} that on a {\i shabbat} that coincides with {\i rosh chodesh} and {\i chanukah}, three {\i sifrei torah} are brought out simultaneously. How is that not {\i pigmo}? The answer: {\i tlat gavrei b\u8217'tlat sifei leika pgama} \u8212- when three different people read from three different scrolls, there is no implication that any one scroll is defective, since each person has his own scroll for his own {\i aliyah}. But {\i chad gavra b\u8217'trei sifei ika pgama} \u8212- when the same person, in the same {\i aliyah}, sets aside one scroll and picks up another, the contrast is inescapable. Why was the first one abandoned? That generates the suspicion of a defect. The solution, then, is to recite the passage from {\i pinchas} entirely from memory, with the statement {\i yoser mi-mah she-karasi kasuv kan} serving to assure the congregation that the omission of that passage from the reading was a matter of protocol, not of deficiency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eight Blessings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} lists eight {\i brachos} recited by the {\i kohen gadol} following the reading. A {\i braita} elaborates: the {\i bracha al hatorah} follows the form used in the synagogue \u8212- {\i asher natan lanu torat emet}. The {\i brachos al ha\u8217'avoda}, {\i al ha\u8217'hoda\u8217'ah}, and {\i al mechilas ha\u8217'avon} follow their established formulations familiar from the {\i shemoneh esrei}. Then come separate {\i brachos} for the {\i mikdash}, for {\i yisrael}, for the {\i kohanim}, and for {\i yerushalayim}, each as a discrete {\i bracha} unto itself. The {\i braita} concludes: {\i v\u8217'hashar tefillah} \u8212- a final petition, described as {\i techina}, {\i rina}, and {\i bakasha}, for whatever {\i yisrael} needs, concluding with the {\i chatimah} of {\i shomea tefillah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The count raises a question. If each of the seven named categories constitutes one {\i bracha}, and {\i v\u8217'hashar tefillah} is an eighth, the tally reaches eight. But the {\i braita}\u8217's description of that final element as a {\i bracha} containing {\i techina}, {\i rina}, and {\i bakasha} does not clearly resolve whether it is to be counted separately or understood as subsumed within one of the seven. {\i Rashi}\u8217's explanation here does not fully clarify the matter, and a parallel difficulty arises from noting that some textual traditions omit the {\i bracha} for {\i yerushalayim}, apparently following a {\i girsa} in {\i Yoma} and the {\i Yerushalmi} that does not list it, while {\i Rashi} does mention {\i yerushalayim} but omits the {\i kohanim} as a separate category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is clear in any case is that the {\i brachos} as a whole are not oriented toward the {\i torah} reading alone. They are a comprehensive liturgical response to the entire {\i yom kippur} experience: acknowledging the {\i avoda} that has just been performed, giving thanks for it, praying that it has effected atonement, and invoking God\u8217's connection to His {\i mikdash}, His people, His city. The {\i keriat hatorah} provides the occasion; the {\i brachos} are the substance of what the occasion demands. There is something deeply interesting about how this draws the whole congregation into participation in what was otherwise entirely the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's private service.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 King\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakhel Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} introduces {\i parshas hamelech} \u8212- the reading of {\i hakhel}. The {\i hakhel} ceremony, described in {\i devarim} as occurring at the conclusion of the {\i shmita} year, gathers all of {\i klal yisrael} for a public reading of the {\i torah}. The {\i torah} itself says {\i tikra es hatorah hazos} without specifying that it is the king who reads, yet the {\i mishna} takes for granted that the king performs the reading. The ceremony takes place {\i b\u8217'motzei yom tov harishon shel chag} \u8212- the {\i gemara} will shortly clarify that this means not the second day of {\i succos} but rather the eighth year, {\i bishminis}, following the {\i shmita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King\u8217's Platform\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A large wooden platform \u8212- {\i bima shel etz} \u8212- is constructed in the {\i azarah}. As with the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's reading, the {\i gemara} will confirm that {\i azarah} here means the {\i ezrat nashim}, which is the only space large enough to accommodate the full assembly of {\i klal yisrael}. The king sits upon this platform. The significance of this detail will become apparent shortly, since kings who are not of {\i malchei beit david} are not permitted to sit in the {\i azarah} proper; the {\i ezrat nashim} resolves this difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The chain of transmission of the {\i sefer torah} is identical to what was described for the {\i kohen gadol}: from {\i chazzan haknesset} to {\i rosh haknesset} to {\i segan} to {\i kohen gadol}, and then from the {\i kohen gadol} to the king. The king stands to receive the scroll and then sits to read it \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hamelech omed u\u8217'mekabel, v\u8217'korei yoshev} \u8212- which is the prerogative of {\i malchei beit david} in the {\i azarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agrippas\u8217's Stand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Agrippas the king \u8212- the last king of {\i yisrael} before the {\i churban}, a descendant of Herod \u8212- stood both to receive the {\i sefer torah} and to read from it, declining to exercise his privilege of sitting while reading. The sages praised him for this. He chose to show honor to the {\i torah} by standing, setting aside his royal prerogative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But when Agrippas reached the passage {\i lo suchal laseis alecha ish nochri} \u8212- you may not set a foreigner over you \u8212- his eyes filled with tears. He recognized that this {\i pasuk} disqualified him: he was descended from Herod, who was of servile and non-Jewish origin, and he therefore could not claim fully Jewish lineage on his father\u8217's side. The assembled people called out to him: {\i al tisra Agrippas, achinu atah, achinu atah} \u8212- do not fear, Agrippas, you are our brother. {\i Rashi} explains that they said this because his {\i mother} was Jewish \u8212- {\i imo mi\u8217'yisrael} \u8212- which they held sufficient to regard him as one of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King\u8217's Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sections read at {\i hakhel} are drawn from {\i sefer devarim}. The king begins from the opening of {\i devarim} \u8212- understood as {\i mishneh hatorah hazos} \u8212- reading through to {\i shema}. He then reads the {\i shema} itself, which {\i Rashi} identifies as the declaration of {\i kabalat malchus shamayim}. He continues with {\i v\u8217'haya im shamoa}, {\i Rashi} noting that this constitutes {\i kabalat ol mitzvos} \u8212- again, our familiar structure from {\i keriat shema}. He then reads {\i aser t\u8217'aser} and {\i ki tichaleh l\u8217'aser}, passages concerning {\i ma\u8217'aser} and the gifts to the {\i aniyim}, because this is the time of {\i zman ha\u8217'asif} \u8212- the harvest season of {\i succos} \u8212- and these obligations are particularly timely. {\i Rashi} explains that the passage {\i parshat hamelech} intervenes between {\i aser t\u8217'aser} and {\i ki tichaleh}, but it was read together with the surrounding material so as not to break up the {\i ma\u8217'aser} reading, with {\i parshat hamelech} read immediately afterward. Finally, he reads the {\i brachos u\u8217'klallos} from {\i ki tavo} and continues through to the end of the entire {\i parsha}. {\i Rashi} identifies the {\i birchos u\u8217'klallos} as the acceptance of the {\i bris} of the {\i torah}, the covenant \u8212- {\i kabalat britot shel Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the reading, the king recites the same eight {\i brachos} as the {\i kohen gadol}, with one modification: instead of {\i al mechilas ha\u8217'avon} \u8212- which is appropriate to {\i yom kippur} \u8212- he substitutes a {\i bracha} for the {\i regalim}, concluding with {\i mekadesh yisrael v\u8217'hazmanim}, which fits the {\i succos} setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The translator, {\i Rashi} notes, does not accompany the king\u8217's reading. There is no {\i meturgeman} when the king reads. This creates an apparent difficulty regarding the jumping between passages in the {\i torah} \u8212- if there is no translator, there is no framework within which to evaluate whether a skip is made before the translator finishes, since there is no translator at all. {\i Rashi} resolves this by saying that the absence of a translator means the constraint of {\i birkat she\u8217'ein tzricha} and {\i tircha} operate differently, though this explanation is acknowledged to be somewhat difficult to fully work out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakhel Day\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if the {\i mishna} says {\i motzei yom tov harishon shel chag} \u8212- after the first day of {\i yom tov} of {\i succos} \u8212- does this mean {\i hakhel} is done on the second day of {\i succos}? {\i Bishmini salka dach\u8217'tacha}? The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i ela eima bishminis} \u8212- no, it means in the eighth year, the year following {\i shmita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why all the different temporal designations in the {\i pasuk} are necessary \u8212- {\i miketz sheva shanim}, {\i hashmita}, {\i b\u8217'moed}, {\i b\u8217'chag hasuccos}, {\i b\u8217'vo kol yisrael}. It answers that each one is required to exclude a different mistaken inference. {\i Miketz sheva shanim} alone might have been taken to mean seven years from the time {\i moshe} gave the {\i torah}, not the end of the {\i shmita} cycle. {\i Hashmita} alone might have meant the very end of the {\i shmita} year, rather than the beginning of the following year\u8217's {\i yom tov}. {\i B\u8217'moed} is necessary to tell you it is a {\i yom tov}, excluding {\i rosh hashanah}. {\i B\u8217'chag hasuccos} specifies which {\i yom tov}. And {\i b\u8217'vo kol yisrael} tells you it is at the beginning of the {\i chag} when they are arriving, not the last day.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Honor Display (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King\u8217's Progression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} repeats its earlier observation in the context of the king\u8217's ceremony: the chain of transmission from {\i chazzan haknesset} to {\i rosh haknesset} to {\i segan} to {\i kohen gadol} to king again prompts the inference {\i shema mina cholkin kavod l\u8217'talmid b\u8217'makom harav} \u8212- and again Abaye supplies the same answer: {\i kula mishum kevod hamelech}. All the intermediate stages exist solely to magnify the honor of the king. The principle applies identically in both contexts: a staged, orderly ascent through every level of the hierarchy, without skipping, is itself the most powerful expression of honor for the person at the top.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kingly Honor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King\u8217's Honor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that Agrippas stood to read and was praised for it, the {\i gemara} raises a question: the sages praised him \u8212- {\i shivchuhu}, implying that it was well done \u8212- but how? A king is forbidden to waive his honor. The principle is stated explicitly: {\i melech she-machal al kevodo ein kevodo machul} \u8212- a king who relinquishes his honor has not succeeded in relinquishing it. The basis for this principle is the verse {\i som tasim alecha melech she-tehei imato alecha} \u8212- set a king over you, that his fear should be upon you. This is not merely an expectation or a convention; it is a legal requirement. The people need the institution of monarchy to carry its full weight of awe, and the king has no authority to unilaterally diminish that weight. By contrast, a {\i nasi} or any other officeholder who waives his honor \u8212- even the opinion that this is permitted applies to them, according to {\i amar Avatya}: {\i nasi she-machal al kevodo kevodo machul}. But the king is categorically different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then comes the answer: {\i mitzvah shani} \u8212- for the sake of a {\i mitzvah}, it is different. When a {\i mitzvah} is at stake, the king may set aside the protocol of his honor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvah Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a powerful objection. The principle {\i mitzvah shani} \u8212- that a {\i mitzvah} overrides the prohibition of a king compromising his honor \u8212- cannot be unlimited. There are cases in the {\i gemara} where a king is said to be {\i makhal al kevodo} in the context of a {\i mitzvah}, such as honoring guests ({\i hachnosas orchim}) or honoring a bride and groom ({\i chosson v\u8217'kallah}), and yet the ruling there appears to be that even so, {\i ein kevodo machul}. If {\i mitzvah shani} applied universally, how could that be?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} distinguishes between types of {\i mitzvos}. When the king stands to read the {\i torah} out of honor for the {\i torah} itself \u8212- which is honor for the {\i shechina} \u8212- he is, in effect, saying: my honor means nothing in the presence of God\u8217's honor. The {\i kavod} he is yielding is entirely subordinated to and absorbed by a higher {\i kavod}: {\i kavod shamayim}. Similarly, in cases such as whether a king may perform {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibbum}, the king does what he does in order to fulfill {\i mitzvas hamelech} \u8212- God\u8217's command \u8212- which is, in a direct sense, subjugating himself to God\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interpersonal Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when the {\i mitzvah} in question is {\i bein adam l\u8217'chaveiro} \u8212- honoring a {\i kallah} or rising before a {\i talmid chakham} \u8212- what is brought to the forefront is not God\u8217's honor but the other person\u8217's honor. Even though there is a {\i mitzvah} from God to perform these acts, the relational dynamic of the act itself is human-to-human. In that context, the king is effectively trading his honor for theirs. That is not {\i mitzvah shani} in the relevant sense; it more closely resembles the king simply being {\i makhal al kevodo} in favor of another human being.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} adds a further dimension: in such cases, the other party \u8212- the {\i kallah}, the {\i talmid chakham} \u8212- themselves bear an obligation to honor the king. There is therefore a countervailing {\i mitzvah} on the other side: the obligation {\i upon them} to give the king honor. The king cannot waive what is not solely his to waive; others are bound to honor him, and that obligation is not erased by the king\u8217's willingness to forgo it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agrippas & Flattery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Agrippas reached the passage {\i lo suchal laseis alecha ish nochri}, he wept. A {\i tanna} taught in the name of {\i Rabbi Natan}: on that day, the enemies of {\i yisrael} \u8212- the {\i gemara}\u8217's euphemism for {\i yisrael} itself \u8212- became deserving of destruction, because they flattered him: {\i she\u8217'hechnifu lo l\u8217'Agrippas}. They called out {\i achinu atah, achinu atah} \u8212- and by doing so, they assured him that he was fit, that there was no problem, when in fact there was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Genealogy Req.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question immediately arises: what exactly was wrong? If his mother was Jewish \u8212- {\i imo mi\u8217'yisrael} \u8212- is he not halakhically Jewish? And if he is Jewish, what precisely is the deficiency that the {\i pasuk} is pointing to? {\i Rashi} says that regardless of his mother\u8217's status, his descent from non-Jewish ancestry made him {\i unseemly} for kingship, and the sages were wrong to reassure him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} goes considerably further. Normally, we follow the rule that for various positions of authority that are restricted to those with proper Jewish lineage, {\i imo mi\u8217'yisrael} \u8212- a Jewish mother \u8212- is sufficient to satisfy the requirement. But {\i Tosafot} argues that the office of king is uniquely stringent. The verse itself uses the phrase {\i mi\u8217'kerev acheicha tasim alecha melech} \u8212- from {\i among your brothers} you shall set a king over you \u8212- and {\i Tosafot} reads this as requiring that the king be {\i mikerev}, fully from within, on {\i both} sides: {\i me\u8217'aviv u\u8217'me\u8217'imo mi\u8217'yisrael}. The {\i parsha} of the king thus sets a stricter standard than the general rule about {\i serarah} and Jewish lineage. Agrippas\u8217's father was not Jewish, and therefore, regardless of his mother, he did not meet the {\i torah}\u8217's requirement for kingship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flattery Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reading is correct, then Agrippas was not merely of questionable lineage \u8212- he was legally disqualified by the {\i torah} from holding the kingship. His kingship was {\i b\u8217'zroa she\u8217'lo k\u8217'din torah} \u8212- established by force and in violation of {\i din torah}. The people who called out {\i achinu atah} were not merely being tactful or kind; they were actively affirming that he was fulfilling a {\i mitzvah} when in fact he was violating a {\i toraitic} prohibition. {\i V\u8217'chizku b\u8217'kach} \u8212- they strengthened him in this course of action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the real nature of {\i chanufah} as the {\i gemara} will go on to discuss. {\i Chanufah} is not simply flattery in the ordinary sense of the word \u8212- empty praise or unwarranted compliments. It is the act of telling someone who is doing something in violation of the {\i torah} that what they are doing is perfectly fine and legitimate, and thereby reinforcing them in their transgression. The sages did not just say something kind to Agrippas; they {\i machzik}-ed him, they gave him the stamp of approval for conduct that the {\i torah} forbade. That distinction \u8212- between flattery as pleasant speech and {\i chanufah} as endorsement of wrongdoing \u8212- will carry the discussion forward into the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 42\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meshauach Milchama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hebrew Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The new {\i perek} opens with the institution of the {\i meshiach milchama} \u8212- the {\i kohen} specially anointed for war \u8212- and immediately establishes that his address to the people before battle must be delivered specifically in {\i lashon ha-kodesh}, Hebrew. This is somewhat counterintuitive: if the entire purpose of the address is to reach the hearts of the soldiers and steel their courage, one might expect that the most widely understood vernacular would be preferred. Yet the {\i mishnah} is clear \u8212- {\i b\u8217'lashon ha-kodesh hayah medaber}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation proceeds from the verse {\i v\u8217'hayah k\u8217'korovchem el hamilchama v\u8217'nigash ha-kohen v\u8217'diber el ha-am} \u8212- when you draw close to battle, the {\i kohen} shall draw near and speak to the people. The {\i Gemara} then links this {\i dibbur} to another instance of the same root elsewhere, in the context of the giving of the Torah: {\i Moshe yedaber veha-Elokim ya\u8217'anenu be-kol} \u8212- and just as that speech was in {\i lashon ha-kodesh}, so too this one. The {\i Gemara} acknowledges that there are many instances of {\i dibber} throughout the Torah, and {\i Tosafot} engages with this directly. The {\i Yerushalmi} formulation runs as follows: why does the word {\i v\u8217'dibber} alone establish Hebrew? After all, {\i keri\u8217'at shema} also uses the related form {\i ve-dibarta bam}, yet it may be recited in any language. The {\i Yerushalmi} answers that what is decisive is the combination of {\i dibber} and {\i amirah} together \u8212- {\i v\u8217'diber ha-kohen v\u8217'amar} \u8212- and then proceeds to distinguish the case of {\i viduy ma\u8217'aser}, which also contains a form of {\i amirah} yet may be recited in any language. Rabbi Chagai then offers the alternative derivation through a {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i hagashah}: {\i v\u8217'nigshu ha-kohanim} corresponds to another instance of {\i hagashah} spoken in {\i lashon ha-kodesh}, and therefore here too the language must be Hebrew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic may be that there is a spiritual power to the use of the sacred tongue, particularly when one is invoking divine protection. In any case, the requirement stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brothers vs Enemies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i meshiach milchama} begins his address with the words {\i Shema Yisrael \u8212- atem k\u8217'revim ha-yom el ha-milchama al oyveichem}: you are drawing close today against your enemies. The emphasis on {\i al oyveichem} \u8212- against your {\i enemies} \u8212- is deliberate. He continues: not against your brothers, not Yehudah against Shimon or Shimon against Binyamin. This is a distinction that carries real weight, because internecine conflict did occur during the pre-monarchic period and even afterward. The reasoning given is {\i she-im tiplu b\u8217'yadam yerachamu aleichem} \u8212- if you were to fall in the hands of your brothers, they would have compassion on you. By contrast, these are your enemies, and if you fall in their hands they will have no mercy. So the address begins by clarifying the stakes: this is not a civil war with its implicit safety net; this is war against those who will show no quarter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pekach & Oded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof-text for the brotherly compassion argument comes from the incident of {\i Pekach ben Remalyahu}, as summarized by {\i Rashi} at the end of the {\i perek}. {\i Pekach}, king of the northern kingdom of {\i Yisrael}, waged war against {\i Achaz} of {\i Yehudah} and took many captives from {\i Bnei Yehudah}. A prophet named {\i Oded} went out before the returning army of {\i Yisrael} and declared: God was angry at {\i Bnei Yehudah} and therefore delivered them into your hands \u8212- but you have smitten them without restraint, and now you intend to enslave them? {\i Hashivu et ha-shivya} \u8212- return the captives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} then relates: {\i va-yakumu ha-anashim asher nikre\u8217'u vi-shemot va-yachaziku ba-shivya} \u8212- those who were designated by name took hold of the captives, clothed the naked from the booty, shod them, fed them, gave them drink, anointed them, placed the ones who could not walk on donkeys, and brought them to {\i Yericho}, city of palm trees, to their brethren \u8212- and then returned to {\i Shomron}. This is what happens when you fall among brothers. The {\i meshiach milchama} invokes this episode to sharpen the contrast: your current enemy will not treat you this way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Courage Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having made the soldiers aware of exactly what they face, the {\i meshiach milchama} turns to fortifying their resolve. {\i Al yerach levavchem v\u8217'al tira\u8217'u v\u8217'al tachpezu v\u8217'al ta\u8217'artzu} \u8212- do not let your heart grow faint, do not fear, do not tremble, do not be terrified. He then enumerates the specific psychological weapons the enemy will deploy: the braying of the horses, the clashing of swords, the clamoring of shields \u8212- {\i Rashi} explains that armies would deliberately bang their shields together to spread fear among the opposing side \u8212- the hordes of enemy soldiers, and the blasting of horns. Each of these is a form of psychological warfare, and the address names them explicitly so the soldiers know what to expect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cure for all of it is theological: {\i ki Hashem Elokeichem holech imachem} \u8212- God is going with you. The address continues: {\i hem ba\u8217'im be-nitzchono shel basar va-dam} \u8212- they come with the strength of flesh and blood, {\i ve-atem ba\u8217'im be-nitzchono shel Makom} \u8212- and you come with the strength of God. Two examples are then marshaled. The Philistines came with the valor of Goliath \u8212- {\i mah hayah sofo?} \u8212- what was his end? {\i U-lesof nafal be-cherev} \u8212- he fell by the sword, and his people fell with him. The Ammonites came with the valor of {\i Shovach} \u8212- what was his end? He too fell by the sword, and his nation with him. But you are not like them, for God goes with you to fight for you, you come with the {\i aron}, and you will be victorious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about this formulation is that the address does not minimize the threat. The soldier is told precisely how terrible his enemy is, and precisely how little mercy he can expect. The courage comes not from illusion but from faith. The {\i meshiach milchama} strengthens hearts not by misrepresenting the danger, but by orienting the army toward the source of genuine power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i yachol kol kohen she-yirtzeh} \u8212- might any {\i kohen} who wishes step forward and deliver this address? After all, the verse does not specify a particular appointment. The {\i Gemara} answers from a {\i kal va-chomer} or analogy to the {\i shotrim}, the officers who speak afterward ({\i mi ha-ish ha-yarei ve-rach ha-levav \u8212- yeilech ve-yashuv le-veito} and so on): just as the {\i shotrim} hold formally appointed positions \u8212- presumably appointed by the {\i shofetim}, the judges they serve \u8212- so too the {\i kohen} who speaks must be someone specifically appointed to that role. {\i Mah shotrim bi-memunah \u8212- af kohen bi-memunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then suggest that it must be the {\i kohen gadol}. But this is rejected by analogy to the {\i shoter} as well: just as the {\i shoter} is appointed yet has someone above him in the hierarchy, so the {\i kohen} here must be someone appointed yet with a superior over him. The {\i kohen gadol} does have the king above him in certain respects \u8212- as we have seen, the king hands the {\i Sefer Torah} to the {\i kohen gadol} at {\i Hakhel} \u8212- but within the realm of {\i avodah}, of the Temple service itself, no one outranks the {\i kohen gadol}. The comparison to a {\i shoter} who has a {\i shofet} over him therefore does not fit the {\i kohen gadol} in that domain. The {\i meshiach milchama} must thus be someone appointed to this specific task yet subordinate to the {\i kohen gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sgan HaKohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The natural candidate would then seem to be the {\i sgan} \u8212- the deputy or vice {\i kohen gadol}. But the {\i Gemara} rejects this too: {\i sgan lav memunah hu} \u8212- the {\i sgan} does not hold the kind of appointment that qualifies here. The {\i Gemara} supports this from a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Chanina, who was himself the {\i sgan ha-kohanim}, states that the sole purpose of the {\i sgan}\u8217's appointment is {\i she-im yei\u8217'ara bo psul be-kohen gadol \u8212- nichnas u-meshamesh tachtav} \u8212- if the {\i kohen gadol} becomes disqualified or dies, the {\i sgan} enters and serves in his place. He is the vice president. He has no independent mandate beyond standing ready to replace the {\i kohen gadol} when necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises an interesting difficulty here. A {\i baraita} in {\i Sanhedrin} states that when the {\i kohen gadol} was receiving mourners, {\i ha-sgan u-meshiach milchama omed bi-ymino} \u8212- the {\i sgan} and the {\i meshiach milchama} would stand to his right. This implies that the {\i sgan} does play some active role alongside the {\i kohen gadol}, rather than simply waiting for him to die. {\i Tosafot} resolves this by distinguishing between rabbinic and biblical appointment. The {\i sgan} was certainly given certain tasks by his fellow {\i kohanim} \u8212- there is a {\i mishnah} that describes the {\i memuneh} giving instructions, and there are various roles assigned. But these were internal arrangements made by the {\i kohanim} among themselves, similar to how they might appoint a {\i markal} (overseer) or a {\i gizbar} (treasurer). What the {\i sgan} lacks is a {\i biblically recognized status} of appointment that would make him the {\i kohen} who speaks before battle. His only Torah-level appointment is to be ready to replace the {\i kohen gadol}; everything else is institutional arrangement, not a recognized {\i minui} from the Torah itself. The {\i meshiach milchama} must therefore be a distinct appointment \u8212- someone designated specifically for this function.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 David & Goliath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shema Yisrael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pauses on the opening words of the {\i meshiach milchama}\u8217's battlefield address \u8212- {\i Shema Yisrael} \u8212- and asks: {\i mai shna Shema Yisrael?} Why specifically this formulation, so resonant with the declaration of God\u8217's unity? Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai explains: God says to {\i Yisrael} \u8212- even if you have fulfilled nothing other than {\i keri\u8217'at shema} of the morning and evening, you will not be delivered into their hands. The {\i zechus} of reciting {\i Shema Yisrael} twice daily is sufficient protection. This is a notably accessible guarantee \u8212- not dependent on extraordinary merit, but on the most basic and universal Jewish practice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Addresses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing & Content\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now establishes that the {\i kohen} spoke to the people not once but twice. {\i Pa\u8217'amayim medaber imahem} \u8212- he would address them on two separate occasions. The first was {\i ba-sfar} \u8212- at the border city, before the army had actually left the land of {\i Yisrael} proper. The second was {\i ba-milchama} \u8212- at the battlefield itself, immediately before engagement. At the border, the content was essentially administrative: {\i shimu divrei ma\u8217'archei milchama ve-chazru} \u8212- hear what the officers are telling you about who is eligible to return, and return accordingly. That is, the {\i shotrim} would there enumerate the categories of those who should go home \u8212- one who has built a house and not dedicated it, betrothed a woman and not yet married her, planted a vineyard and not redeemed it, and the one who is fearful and soft-hearted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The address at the battlefield itself was the courage speech: {\i al yerach levavchem al tira\u8217'u v\u8217'al tachpezu v\u8217'al ta\u8217'artzu}. This sequence \u8212- first sending home those who should not be there, then inspiring those who remain \u8212- is logically the reverse of the order in which the verses appear in the Torah. The Torah presents the encouragement of the {\i kohen} before the {\i shotrim} announce the exemption categories. The {\i Gemara} does not comment directly on this discrepancy but it is evident that the practical sequence makes more sense: eliminate the fearful first, and deliver the courage speech to those who are actually going into battle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Tactics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The four expressions of fear \u8212- {\i al yerach}, {\i al tira\u8217'u}, {\i al tachpezu}, {\i al ta\u8217'artzu} \u8212- correspond to four specific things that enemy armies would do in order to break the morale of the opposing force. They would clang their shields ({\i magifim}); they would blast the horns ({\i meri\u8217'im}); they would let out war cries ({\i tzovchim}); and they would trample forward with their armies ({\i romsim}). Each phrase in the {\i kohen}\u8217's address is a counter to one of these tactics, reassuring the soldiers against each specific form of psychological warfare.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 David & Goliath (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goliath Brazenness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan offers a {\i drasha} on the name {\i Golyat} itself: {\i she-amad be-giluy panim} \u8212- he stood with brazenness before God. The source is his challenge: {\i baru lachem ish v\u8217'yered elai} \u8212- choose for yourselves a man and let him come down against me. The {\i Gemara} reads this as a direct affront to the divine, since {\i v\u8217'ein ish ela Ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- the word {\i ish} in certain contexts refers specifically to God, as in the verse {\i Hashem ish milchama}, God is a man of war. Goliath was therefore not merely challenging the army of Israel; he was effectively saying: send down your God and I will fight Him. God\u8217's response: {\i hareini mapilo al yedei ben ish} \u8212- I will bring him down through the hands of a {\i ben ish}, a son of a man, a mere youth \u8212- as in the verse {\i ve-David ben ish Ephrati}, David son of a man of Ephrat. The wordplay is pointed: Goliath invoked {\i ish} as a challenge to God, and it is through a {\i ben ish} that he is undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Trapping Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Meir identifies three specific moments where Goliath\u8217's own words sealed his fate. First, {\i baru lachem ish v\u8217'yered elai} \u8212- the word {\i v\u8217'yered}, let him come down, implies a descent upon Goliath, a conquest. Second, {\i im yuchal le-hilachem iti ve-hikani} \u8212- if he will be able to fight me and smite me; even phrased conditionally, Goliath articulated his own defeat. Third, his taunt to David \u8212- {\i ha-kelev anochi ki ata ba elai be-maklot} \u8212- am I a dog that you come to me with sticks? \u8212- ultimately described how he would be beaten, like a dog.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: David also makes a forceful declaration \u8212- {\i ata ba elai be-cherev u-ve-chanit u-ve-kidon} \u8212- you come to me with sword and spear and javelin \u8212- does this not similarly describe David being attacked? The {\i Gemara} answers no, because David immediately continues: {\i ve-anochi ba eleicha be-shem Hashem Tzeva\u8217'ot elokei ma\u8217'archot Yisrael} \u8212- but I am coming against you in the name of God. David\u8217's statement is not a prediction of his own defeat; it is precisely the declaration the {\i meshiach milchama} would make \u8212- we come in the name of God. The contrast between their respective sources of power is the entire point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to the earlier theme is explicit: Goliath would come out {\i ha-shakem ve-ha\u8217'arev} \u8212- early in the morning and in the evening \u8212- precisely at the times of {\i keri\u8217'at shema shacharit ve-arvit}, in order to prevent Israel from reciting {\i Shema}. This echoes Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's earlier teaching: the {\i zechus} of {\i Shema} is what protects in battle, and Goliath knew exactly what to undermine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beinayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} describes Goliath as {\i ish ha-beinayim} \u8212- a man of the {\i beinayim}. The {\i Gemara} explores several interpretations of this term. Rav says {\i she-mevuneh mi-kol mum} \u8212- he was perfectly constructed, without any blemish or defect. Shmuel says {\i beinoni she-b\u8217'achav} \u8212- he was merely the middling one among his brothers; if you think Goliath was large, you should see his older brothers. The school of Rabbi Shila says {\i she-hu asuy ke-vinyan} \u8212- he was built like a building, massive and solid. And Rabbi Yochanan says {\i bar me\u8217'ah papi ve-chada nani} \u8212- he had a hundred fathers and one mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} elaborates on this last reading: {\i ben ha-ta\u8217'arovet} \u8212- a son of mixture \u8212- because many men came upon his mother in a single night and she conceived from one of them. One is the biological father and all the others are adulterers. {\i Rashi} adds that {\i nani} is the word for father in Persian. The {\i drasha} on {\i beinayim} \u8212- from the root {\i bein}, between \u8212- thus suggests he emerged from {\i bein anashim harbei}, from among many men. {\i Rav Yosef} adds in the same vein: {\i she-ha-kol dashim et imo ke-gat} \u8212- everyone trampled his mother like a {\i gat}, a winepress. The word {\i me\u8217'aros} in the phrase {\i mime\u8217'aros Plishtim} is read {\i ma\u8217'archos}, and {\i she-ha-kol he\u8217'eru be-imo} \u8212- everyone performed the act of {\i giluy arayot} with his mother. The portrait being drawn is of a man of illegitimate and sordid origins, despite his fearsome appearance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harpa/Arpa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A later {\i pasuk} in {\i Sefer Shmuel} refers to four sons born {\i le-Harpa be-Gat} \u8212- to {\i Harpa} in {\i Gat}. These four, who fell in battle against David and his servants, appear to be Goliath\u8217's brothers, and {\i Harpa} is their mother. Yet elsewhere her name appears as {\i Arpa}. This {\i Arpa} is, of course, known from the story of {\i Rut} \u8212- she is the sister-in-law of {\i Rut} who kissed her mother-in-law {\i Na\u8217'omi} and turned back, rather than accompanying her to the land of {\i Yisrael}. The {\i Gemara} now draws the connection between the two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav u-Shmuel} debate the two names. One holds that her name was {\i Harpa}, and she was called {\i Arpa} because {\i she-ha-kol orfin otah me-achoreiha} \u8212- everyone would approach her from behind, the word {\i oref} denoting the back of the neck. The other holds that her name was {\i Arpa}, and she was called {\i Harpa} because {\i she-ha-kol dashim otah ka-arifot} \u8212- everyone trampled her like {\i harifot}, the crushed or milled grain. Two {\i pesukim} are adduced in support of the {\i harifot} reading: the woman who spread a cloth over the pit and scattered {\i arifot} upon it as a trap ({\i va-tishpach aleha ha-arifot}), and the verse {\i im tichtosh et ha-evil ba-machtesh be-toch ha-arifot} \u8212- if the foolish one is ground up in the mortar among the grain. Both images carry the sense of something being violently broken down. The point in all of these interpretations is consistent: the mother of these giants was a woman of profound immorality, and her sons\u8217' enormous physical power is in inverse proportion to the legitimacy of their birth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} identifies the four sons born to {\i Harpa} in {\i Gat}: {\i Saph u-Madon Golyat ve-Yishbi ve-Nov} \u8212- names that appear in the text of the {\i pesukim} themselves. Each fell {\i be-yad David u-ve-yad avadav} \u8212- at the hands of David and his servants in separate engagements. The connection to {\i Rut} is now drawn explicitly: {\i de-kasiv va-tishak Arpa la-chamotah ve-Rut davkah bah} \u8212- {\i Orpah} kissed her mother-in-law and {\i Rut} cleaved to her. Rabbi Yitzchak states in the name of God, as it were: {\i yavo benei ha-neshukah} \u8212- let the children of the one who gave the goodbye kiss come forth, {\i ve-yippelu be-yad benei ha-devukah} \u8212- and let them fall at the hands of the children of the one who cleaved. The symmetry is striking and purposeful: the choice {\i Orpah} made on the road to {\i Yisrael} \u8212- the kiss of farewell \u8212- determined the fate of her descendants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orpah\u8217's Reward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} adds a dimension of nuance to {\i Orpah}\u8217's story. {\i Be-schar arba d\u8217'maot she-horida Orpah al chamotah} \u8212- in the reward of the four tears that {\i Orpah} shed for her mother-in-law, {\i zachta ve-yatza mimenah arba\u8217'ah gibborim} \u8212- she merited that four mighty warriors emerged from her. The four tears are derived from the verse {\i va-tisenah kolam va-tivkena od} \u8212- {\i Rashi} understands this as weeping from both eyes, making two, doubled by the word {\i od} to four. {\i Orpah} was not simply a wicked person; she wept genuinely for {\i Na\u8217'omi} and accompanied her a significant distance before turning back. Her failing was not cruelty but a lesser degree of self-sacrifice than {\i Rut}. And so she received a reward \u8212- four sons of extraordinary physical power \u8212- even as those sons ultimately fell at the hands of David, the descendant of {\i Rut}, {\i Orpah}\u8217's counterpart.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Praising Wicked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes a textual detail about Goliath\u8217's description: {\i k\u8217'siv chetz chanito ve-karinan etz chanito} \u8212- the written form of the text says {\i chetz}, the arrow of his spear, while the read form ({\i keri}) says {\i etz}, the wood of his spear. Rabbi Elazar draws the following conclusion from the sparse description of Goliath\u8217's might: {\i adayin lo higanu le-chatzi shevacho shel oto rasha} \u8212- we have not even reached half of the praise due to that wicked one. The Torah\u8217's description, impressive as it is, captures less than half of his actual power and stature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: if that is so, why describe him at all? {\i She-asur le-saper be-shivchen shel resha\u8217'im} \u8212- it is forbidden to enumerate the praises of the wicked. The answer: {\i lo dumi shavkei d\u8217'dami} \u8212- you cannot omit it entirely, because you must describe Goliath\u8217's greatness in order to convey the magnitude of David\u8217's achievement. The praise of Goliath is not for Goliath\u8217's sake; it is for David\u8217's. Without knowing what David overcame, one cannot fully appreciate the victory.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shovach Army\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shovach Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the motif introduced earlier in the {\i meshiach milchama}\u8217's address \u8212- {\i Bnei Amon ba-u be-nitzchono shel Shovach} \u8212- the Ammonites came to battle relying on the valor of {\i Shovach}, their general, whose story is recounted in {\i Sefer Shmuel}. However, in {\i Divrei ha-Yamim} the same figure is called {\i Shofach}. The {\i Gemara} notes: {\i k\u8217'siv Shovach u-ch\u8217'siv Shofach} \u8212- both names appear in the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav u-Shmuel} debate his true name. One holds that his name was {\i Shofach}, and he was called {\i Shovach} because {\i she-asui ke-shovach} \u8212- he was built like a pigeon coop, extraordinarily tall and imposing. The other holds his name was {\i Shovach}, and he was called {\i Shofach} because {\i she-kol ha-ro\u8217'eh oto nishpach le-fanav ke-kiton} \u8212- everyone who saw him would spill out before him like a pitcher, perhaps losing control of their bowels or simply dissolving in fear before him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashpato Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns to a {\i pasuk} about the armies of {\i Nevuchadnezzar}: {\i ashpato ke-kever patuach kulam gibborim} \u8212- his quiver is like an open grave; they are all mighty men. The word {\i ashpato} can mean a quiver for arrows, but it can also mean a garbage heap or {\i ashpah}. {\i Rav u-Shmuel} \u8212- or in another version, {\i Rav Ami ve-Rav Asi} \u8212- debate the meaning. One holds that {\i ashpato} refers to the quiver of arrows: {\i b\u8217'sha\u8217'ah she-zorkim chetz osim ashpah ashpah shel chalalim} \u8212- when they shoot their arrows they make mound upon mound of corpses, each mound like an {\i ashpah}, a garbage heap. One might then think that this merely reflects their skill at archery rather than their personal might \u8212- therefore {\i Talmud lomar kulam gibborim}, the verse concludes that they are all personally mighty men as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Excrement View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other opinion reads {\i ashpato} differently. {\i B\u8217'sha\u8217'ah she-osim tzorcheihem osim ashpah ashpah shel zevel} \u8212- when they relieve themselves, they make mound upon mound of excrement, suggesting by their sheer physical size the enormous quantities they discharge. This too is a form of praising their might, if an unusual one. Here again the verse\u8217's conclusion is necessary: {\i kulam gibborim} \u8212- they are indeed mighty, not merely large in an unhealthy way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Mari} draws a practical medical inference from this discussion: {\i shema minah \u8212- hai man de-nafish zivlei cholei me\u8217'ayim hu} \u8212- one who produces excessive excrement is to be presumed to have intestinal illness. The {\i nafka mina} is {\i le-itpuchi be-nafshei} \u8212- the person should attend to his own welfare and seek medical attention. {\i Rashi} explains this as: {\i le-vakesh alav refu\u8217'ot} \u8212- to go seek healing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Worry & Health\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Managing Worry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the verse {\i da\u8217'agah be-lev ish yashchenah} \u8212- if there is worry in a man\u8217's heart, he should do something with it ({\i yashchenah} or {\i yassichennah}, depending on how one reads the vowels). {\i Rav Asi} introduces the discussion and notes two competing readings of the operative word, both using a {\i sin} \u8212- {\i yassichennah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Therapy Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two readings yield two opposing therapeutic strategies. One says {\i yassichennah mi-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- push it out of his mind, distract himself from the worry, deal with problems by redirecting his attention elsewhere. The other says {\i yassichennah la-acherim} \u8212- speak about it to others, share the burden of the concern. The {\i Gemara} here reflects a genuine debate about how human beings should manage anxiety: suppression and distraction on one side, disclosure and conversation on the other. The {\i Gemara} presents both as legitimate readings of the verse without definitively resolving between them \u8212- though the pairing with the discussion of intestinal health in the preceding lines creates a suggestive context, implying that unexpressed worry has a way of manifesting physically.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 43\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meshuch Milchama Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priest & Ark\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the very last line of 42b, continuing the discussion of the {\i meshuch milchama} \u8212- the anointed priest who addresses the troops before battle. The Torah declares: {\i Hashem elokeichem hu haholeich imachem} \u8212- \u8220"Hashem your God is the one who walks with you.\u8221" The {\i gemara} asks: {\i vechol kach lama} \u8212- what is the purpose of all of this? The obvious answer is that it gives the soldiers courage. But the question, as Rashi frames it, is more precise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Halicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that the question is not why this statement is made at all, but what is the emphasis of the word {\i haholeich} \u8212- \u8220"walking\u8221" or \u8220"going\u8221" \u8212- rather than simply \u8220"is with you.\u8221" The answer is: {\i mipnei sheHashem vechol kinuyav munachim ba\u8217'aron} \u8212- because God\u8217's name and all of the various divine appellations are actually present in the {\i aron}. It is therefore not merely an abstract declaration that God accompanies the army; it is a statement that the physical object representing God\u8217's presence \u8212- the {\i aron} \u8212- is literally going with them into battle. This raises the interesting question of whether the {\i aron} itself was taken out of the {\i Beit Hamikdash} or the {\i Mishkan} and brought to the battlefield, and there is a view that a separate {\i aron} was used for battle, distinct from the one that resided in the sanctuary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midian War Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then brings a proof from the war against Midian: {\i vayishlach otam Moshe elef lamateh latzava, otam ve\u8217'et Pinechas ben Elazar hakohein latzava uchelei hakodesh vechatzotzrot hateruah beyado}. The {\i gemara} identifies the components: {\i otam} refers to the Sanhedrin; {\i Pinechas ben Elazar} is the {\i meshuch milchama}; {\i uchelei hakodesh} \u8212- the holy vessels \u8212- refers to the {\i aron} and the {\i luchot}; and {\i vechatzotzrot hateruah} \u8212- the trumpets of the blast \u8212- is rendered as {\i shofrot}. This last identification is noteworthy: {\i chatzotzrot} are the metal trumpets, while {\i shofrot} are made from an animal\u8217's horn. The {\i gemara} elsewhere indicates that in the Amoraic period these two terms exchanged their meanings in vernacular usage, which may explain why the {\i gemara} here renders the {\i chatzotzrot} of the {\i pasuk} as {\i shofrot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pinchas Genealogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then adds: {\i lo chinam holeich Pinechas lamilchama} \u8212- it was not for nothing that Pinchas went to war against Midian. He went specifically to avenge the honor of his mother\u8217's father, as Scripture records: {\i vehamidanim machru oto el Mitzrayim} \u8212- the Midianites sold Yosef into Egypt. This implies that Pinchas descended from Yosef on his maternal side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: is Pinchas really descended from Yosef? The {\i pasuk} states that Elazar ben Aharon took a wife from the daughters of {\i Putiel}, and she bore him Pinchas. So who is this {\i Putiel}? The {\i gemara} presents the classic explanation that {\i Putiel} alludes to Yitro \u8212- {\i shepitem agalim l\u8217'avoda zara} \u8212- who fattened calves for idol worship, giving rise to the name {\i Puti El} in the sense of fattening for the gods. This would make Pinchas a descendant of Yitro on his mother\u8217's side, not of Yosef.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then invokes the well-known {\i aggadic} tradition that after Pinchas slew Zimri, the tribes mocked him: {\i re\u8217'item ben Puti zeh} \u8212- \u8220"have you seen this descendant of Puti?\u8221" \u8212- {\i shepitem avi imo agalim l\u8217'avoda zara} \u8212- \u8220"whose grandfather fattened calves for idol worship?\u8221" \u8212- {\i veharag nesi miYisrael} \u8212- \u8220"and yet he had the audacity to slay a leader of Israel?\u8221" This {\i aggadic} understanding runs deep enough that one cannot simply discard the derivation from Yitro. The {\i gemara} therefore resolves the tension: Pinchas was descended from both lineages on his mother\u8217's side. Either his maternal grandfather descended from Yosef and his maternal grandmother descended from Yitro, or the reverse. The {\i gemara} notes that the word {\i Putiel} is pointed in a way that implies two roots \u8212- {\i dayka nami dektiv mibnot Putiel}, {\i trei mashma}, \u8220"the word implies two.\u8221" He therefore avenged the honor of his ancestor Yosef by leading the war against the very people who sold him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Military Exemptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the next {\i mishnah}, which addresses the speech of the {\i shotrim} \u8212- the officers \u8212- to the assembled troops. The Torah instructs them to announce three categories of exemption: one who built a new house and did not yet inaugurate it; one who planted a vineyard and did not yet perform {\i chilul}; and one who betrothed a woman and did not yet marry her. Those who qualify return from the front. Alongside this, there is a category of soldiers who do not go back to the front line but remain in service providing support \u8212- supplying food, water, and maintaining the roads.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} broadens the house category significantly. It is not limited to a standard dwelling: {\i echad haboneh beit hateven beit habakar beit ha\u8217'etzim} \u8212- whether it is a barn for straw, a stable for cattle, a shed for wood, or any storage facility with a roof. What matters is that it is a structure of some substance. Nor does one need to have built it himself: whether one built it, purchased it, inherited it, or received it as a gift \u8212- all are included.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vineyard Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The vineyard exemption is likewise broad. The word {\i chilulo} in the {\i pasuk} \u8212- \u8220"and did not perform {\i chilul}\u8221" \u8212- is understood by the {\i gemara} to refer to the fourth year of {\i neta revai}, when the owner performs {\i chilul} by redeeming the fruit\u8217's sanctity onto money and spending that money in Jerusalem. This provides a window of four full years from the time of planting during which one is eligible for the exemption. (Some medieval commentators suggest that {\i chilul} may allude to a dedication ceremony, similar to {\i chanacho} by the house, but the {\i gemara} reads it as referring specifically to the fourth-year {\i kerem revai} process.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then expands the category of {\i kerem}: it includes not only a full vineyard but also five fruit-bearing trees \u8212- since the minimum {\i kerem} for purposes of other {\i halakhot} consists of five vines \u8212- even from five different species. And, parallel to the house, it includes not only planting but also {\i mavrich} (layering a branch underground to take root elsewhere), {\i markiv} (grafting), purchasing, inheriting, or receiving as a gift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betrothal Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third category \u8212- betrothal \u8212- is similarly expanded. Whether the woman is a {\i betulah} (never-married virgin), a widow ({\i almanah}), or even a {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibum} \u8212- the exemption applies. Remarkably, the {\i mishnah} extends this even to a case where a soldier heard during battle that his brother died in the war, leaving behind a widow: he returns as well, because he now has the obligation of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Support vs.\u160 ?Leave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Those in these categories do not simply go home. They return from the front line but remain in national service, as the {\i mishnah} specifies: {\i umsapkim mayim umazon umtaknim es hadrachim} \u8212- they provide water and food and repair the roads. They serve the army in a support capacity without engaging in combat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Exemption Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then identifies cases where one does not return even to support duty. If one built a {\i sha\u8217'ar}, {\i achsadra}, or {\i mirpeset} \u8212- a gatehouse, colonnade, or porch \u8212- that does not qualify, because these are not habitable structures. If one planted only four fruit trees, or planted five trees that are of a species that does not bear fruit, that does not qualify. If a man remarries his own divorced wife \u8212- {\i hamachazir gerushato} \u8212- that does not qualify, since she is not a new wife to him. And if the betrothal or marriage involves a forbidden union \u8212- an {\i almanah} to a {\i kohen gadol}, a {\i gerusha} or {\i chalutzah} to a regular {\i kohen}, a {\i mamzeret} to a {\i Yisrael}, or a {\i bat Yisrael} to a {\i mamzer} or {\i nasin} \u8212- none of these qualify. (The {\i gemara} will raise the question of why forbidden unions need to be excluded, and that discussion will be addressed below.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda adds that one who rebuilt his house on its original foundation \u8212- {\i al mechono} \u8212- does not return, because it is analogous to restoring a prior state rather than inaugurating something genuinely new, similar to the logic of {\i hamachazir gerushato}. Rabbi Eliezer adds that even one who builds a standard brick house in the Sharon Valley does not return, because the ground there is so unstable \u8212- as recalled in the Yom Kippur prayer for the inhabitants of the Sharon \u8212- that such houses require frequent rebuilding and are not reliably permanent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third group is those who do not even go to the front at all and are entirely exempt from any form of military service. These are: one who built a house and {\i chanacho} \u8212- actually inaugurated it; one who planted a vineyard and performed {\i chilul}; one who betrothed a woman and completed the marriage ({\i nisu\u8217'in}); and one who performed {\i yibum} with his {\i yevamah}. The {\i pasuk} specifying this final level of exemption \u8212- {\i naki yiheyeh l\u8217'veiso shanah achas vesimach es ishto asher lakach} \u8212- grants a full year at home to a man who has actually married, not merely betrothed. The {\i mishnah} applies this principle to all three categories: once the house, vineyard, or marriage has been fully actualized, one receives a complete year off from all military obligation, including support services.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, as a careful reading makes clear, somewhat counterintuitive. One might have expected that unfinished business \u8212- the un-inaugurated house, the unmarried bride \u8212- would produce the greater exemption, while completion would return one to duty. The {\i mishnah} teaches the opposite: partial actualization leads to support service; full actualization leads to complete exemption for a year. This is a {\i milchemet reshut}, an optional war, and the Torah\u8217's provisions apply accordingly. In a {\i milchemet mitzvah}, an obligatory war, all go to battle.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Officers\u8217' Communication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Speaker Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses the question of who speaks what. The {\i tanna} asks: when the Torah says {\i vedibru hashotrim} \u8212- and the officers shall speak \u8212- could this mean they speak entirely in their own words? Perhaps. But the Torah elsewhere says {\i veyasfu hashotrim} \u8212- and the officers shall add \u8212- which refers to the additional address about {\i hayarei verach halevav}, the fearful-hearted. That passage is clearly the officers\u8217' own speech. So what is the verse {\i vedibru hashotrim} referring to? The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i bedivrei meshuch milchama haksav medaber} \u8212- the Torah is speaking about the words of the {\i meshuch milchama}, which the officers communicate: {\i kohen medaber veshoter mashmia} \u8212- the {\i kohen} speaks and the officers amplify, projecting his words throughout the assembled ranks, precisely as a {\i tanna} (reciter) amplifies the words of an {\i amora} in the study hall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Part Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, there is a difficulty: three {\i beraitot} seem to contradict one another. One says {\i kohen medaber veshoter mashmia}; a second says {\i kohen medaber vekohen mashmia}; a third says {\i shoter medaber veshoter mashmia}. Abaye resolves this by dividing the speech into three sections. From the moment the {\i kohen} draws near until the verse {\i vedibru hashotrim} \u8212- the section about courage and God\u8217's presence \u8212- the {\i kohen} both speaks and amplifies: {\i kohen medaber vekohen mashmia}. The section from {\i vedibru hashotrim} through the categories of house, vineyard, and betrothal is {\i kohen medaber veshoter mashmia} \u8212- the {\i kohen} speaks and the officers amplify. From {\i veyasfu} onward \u8212- the address about the fearful-hearted \u8212- it is {\i shoter medaber veshoter mashmia}, entirely the officers\u8217' domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is coherent. The call for courage and divine presence appropriately comes entirely from the {\i kohen}, who embodies the sacred. The final section about the fearful soldier is entirely the officers\u8217', because it is the officers who will enforce order in the ranks \u8212- tradition records that deserters could be summarily punished \u8212- and it is they who must manage the question of who leaves and who stays. The middle section, dealing with the practical exemption categories, is a joint operation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 House Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hermeneutics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now probes the textual basis for each exemption category. A {\i baraita} addresses the house: {\i asher bana} \u8212- \u8220"who built\u8221" \u8212- I would know only about building. How do I know that purchasing, inheriting, or receiving as a gift are also included? {\i Talmud lomar: mi ha\u8217'ish asher bana} \u8212- the formulation {\i asher bana} is read as an expansion. Similarly: {\i bayit} \u8212- \u8220"house\u8221" \u8212- I would know only a house. How do I know that a barn ({\i beit hateven}), stable ({\i beit habakar}), woodshed ({\i beit ha\u8217'etzim}), or storehouse ({\i beit ha\u8217'otzarot}) are included? {\i Talmud lomar: asher bana mikol makom} \u8212- because the word {\i bana} precedes the word {\i bayit} in the verse, the act of building is mentioned before its object, allowing {\i bana} to encompass anything built that resembles a {\i bayit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Habitable Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, one might then go too far and include even a gatehouse, colonnade, or porch. The {\i gemara} limits this: {\i talmud lomar bayit} \u8212- the word {\i bayit} sets the threshold. {\i Mah bayit haraui ledirah} \u8212- just as a house is something fit for habitation, {\i af kol haraui ledirah} \u8212- so too, only structures fit for habitation are included. A gatehouse or porch fails this test, even though a barn technically could serve as sleeping quarters if necessary. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov dissents from the broader reading and insists that {\i bayit} means a {\i bayit} literally: only a proper dwelling qualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanuch & Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the requirement of {\i lo chanacho} \u8212- that he did not yet inaugurate it. Why does the verse use the personal pronoun {\i chanacho} \u8212- \u8220"inaugurate it\u8221" \u8212- rather than the simpler {\i chanach}? The answer: {\i prat legazlan} \u8212- to exclude a thief. The inauguration must be something the person was legitimately entitled to perform; if the property was stolen, he has no right to inaugurate it, and it does not qualify for the exemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks whether this exclusion of the {\i gazlan} conflicts with the position of Rabbi Yosi haGelili, who interprets {\i verach halevav} \u8212- the fearful-hearted \u8212- as referring to one who fears his own sins: {\i zu hamityare me\u8217'aveirot shebeyado}. If so, a thief would already be excluded on those grounds \u8212- he is fearful of punishment for theft. Why state it separately? The {\i gemara} answers: the case under discussion is one where the thief has already done {\i teshuvah} and paid restitution. Since he has resolved his sin, he no longer qualifies as {\i verach halevav} due to his {\i aveirot}. Yet, the {\i gemara} counters, if he paid for it, he is now a legitimate purchaser \u8212- {\i havei lei lokei\u8217'ach} \u8212- and should be entitled to the exemption as one who purchased a house. The {\i gemara} responds: since the property originally came to him through theft \u8212- {\i keivan demeyikara betorat gezeilah ata lidei} \u8212- it remains outside the scope of this passage, regardless of subsequent payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: should we not apply {\i takanat hashavim} \u8212- the rabbinic principle of encouraging penitents \u8212- and allow him back from the {\i milchamah}? The {\i gemara} acknowledges the principle but maintains the exclusion nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vineyard Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Trees Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} applies the same hermeneutical method to the vineyard. {\i Asher nata} \u8212- \u8220"who planted\u8221" \u8212- I would know only planting. How do I know purchasing, inheriting, and receiving as a gift? {\i Talmud lomar: umi ha\u8217'ish asher nata kerem} \u8212- the word {\i asher} is expansive. And {\i kerem} \u8212- I would know only a vineyard. How do I know that five fruit-bearing trees of any species are included? {\i Talmud lomar: asher nata} \u8212- again, {\i nata} precedes {\i kerem} in the verse, allowing {\i nata} to encompass anything planted that resembles a {\i kerem}. One might then include even four fruit trees or five non-fruit-bearing trees. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i talmud lomar kerem} \u8212- it must resemble a {\i kerem}, and neither four trees nor barren trees constitute a {\i kerem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chilul & Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov again defends the plain meaning: {\i kerem kemashma\u8217'o} \u8212- a {\i kerem} means what it sounds like, literally a vineyard, not five fruit trees of another species. The {\i gemara} also treats the phrase {\i lo chilalo} as containing a textual exclusion: {\i prat lemavrich umarkiv} \u8212- to exclude one who performed {\i mavrich} (layering) or {\i markiv} (grafting). This is puzzling, since the {\i mishnah} itself listed {\i mavrich} and {\i markiv} as modes that qualify for the exemption. The resolution will come in the following section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grafting Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tension is significant: the {\i mishnah} includes {\i mavrich} and {\i markiv} in the exemption, yet the {\i baraita} derived from {\i lo chilalo} seems to exclude them. Moreover, why would grafting be worse than simply purchasing a vineyard? The {\i gemara} addresses this directly in the next section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Grafting & Orlah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prohibited Grafting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda, as transmitted by Rabbi Zeira, resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya, kan beharkavas issur, kan beharkavas heter} \u8212- there is no difficulty. The exclusion of {\i mavrich} and {\i markiv} from the exemption refers specifically to a case where the grafting was performed through a forbidden act \u8212- namely, {\i kilayim} (prohibited crossbreeding of species). When the {\i baraita} excludes it, it is referring to {\i harkavat issur}. When the {\i mishnah} includes {\i mavrich} and {\i markiv}, it refers to {\i harkavat heter}, a permitted grafting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: what exactly is a case of {\i harkavat heter} that would entitle one to the exemption? The {\i gemara} first considers {\i yeladah be-yeladah} \u8212- grafting a young vine (within three years, i.e., still in {\i orlah} status) onto another young vine. But this is rejected: {\i teipuk lei d\u8217'bamehadar mishum yeladah rishonah} \u8212- the exemption would apply anyway on account of the base vine itself, which is already in {\i orlah} status. That case requires no separate derivation from the graft.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative is {\i yeladah be-zkeinah} \u8212- grafting a young shoot onto an older vine. Here Rabbi Abahu\u8217's ruling applies: {\i yeladah she-sibchah be-zkeinah batlah yeladah be-zkeinah} \u8212- a young vine that becomes incorporated into an old one takes on the status of the old vine and is no longer subject to {\i orlah}. If the exemption from war depends on having a vine in {\i orlah} status (as the {\i gemara} derives from {\i chililo}), then grafting a young vine onto an old one would not give the owner a new {\i orlah}-status vine; the graft loses its youth status by incorporation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Young on Young\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya therefore proposes: {\i le-olam yeladah be-yeladah} \u8212- the case remains young-on-young, but with a critical distinction. The base vine, though young, was planted not for fruit but {\i li-syag u-le-kora} \u8212- as a fence or for its timber. A vine planted for non-fruit purposes is exempt from {\i orlah}, as the {\i mishnah} in another tractate teaches: {\i hanotei\u8217'a li-syag u-le-kora patur min ha-orlah}. So the base has no {\i orlah} status, and the owner of the base would have no military exemption on its account. Now someone grafts onto it a young shoot intended for fruit. That shoot is {\i chayav} in {\i orlah}, and through it the grafter earns his exemption from the war.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beams Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case now presents a conceptual question: why does the grafted shoot retain its own {\i orlah} status rather than being absorbed into the base, which is {\i patur} from {\i orlah}? If \u8212- as Rabbi Abahu teaches for the opposite configuration \u8212- a young vine grafted onto an old vine loses its own status and takes on the status of the host, why doesn\u8217't the same logic apply here, so that the young shoot grafted onto the non-{\i orlah} base would become {\i patur} along with it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bas Mahdar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers with the principle of {\i bas mahdar} \u8212- the potential to cause a return from war. In the case of {\i yeladah be-zkeinah} (young onto old), if the owner were to reconsider and want the old vine to produce fruit, it would still not subject the old vine to {\i orlah} (it is too old), and so there is no scenario in which the old vine becomes {\i bas mahdar} \u8212- no scenario in which it could ever generate a reason to return from the {\i milchamah}. Therefore the young shoot is fully incorporated into it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here, by contrast \u8212- {\i yeladah be-yeladah}, with the base being {\i patur} because it was planted for timber \u8212- if the owner were to change his mind and decide to use the base vine for fruit, it {\i would} then become {\i chayav} in {\i orlah}, because it is still young. {\i Im mimlech aleih bas mahdar hi} \u8212- if he changed his mind about the base, it would be a basis for returning from battle. Since the base at least has the {\i potential} to become {\i chayav} in {\i orlah}, the grafted young shoot retains its own {\i orlah} status and is not absorbed. The {\i gemara} supports this with the ruling that fruit that grows spontaneously without any planting intention is still {\i chayav} in {\i orlah} \u8212- {\i olu me\u8217'eileihen chayavin b\u8217'orlah} \u8212- since the potential for {\i orlah} obligation inheres in youth regardless of the planter\u8217's purpose. The {\i gemara} acknowledges that this is something of a {\i chiddush}, since the {\i bas mahdar} reasoning would suggest that merely changing one\u8217's mind about the purpose of a fence-vine would be sufficient to both impose {\i orlah} and entitle one to return from war.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joint Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises an alternative reading of the case of {\i harkavat heter}: perhaps the exemption applies to a {\i kerem shel shenei shutfin} \u8212- a vineyard with two partners \u8212- where one partner owns the base vine and another owns the grafted shoot. In such a case, the owner of the shoot would have a young vine in {\i orlah} status but could not claim exemption on account of the base. Rav Pappa derives from this discussion: {\i zo omeret, kerem shel shenei shutfin ein hozrin aleiv mei-orkhei hamilchama} \u8212- a jointly-owned vineyard does not entitle anyone to return from battle. One must have sole ownership to qualify for the exemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this from a case discussed elsewhere: if five brothers go to war and one is killed, leaving a widow who requires {\i yibum}, all the remaining brothers return from the front, since any one of them might fulfill the obligation of {\i yibum}. Is this not analogous to the partnership case \u8212- multiple people sharing a kind of joint claim, none of whom has exclusive title? The {\i gemara} distinguishes: {\i hasam kochad vechad karinan bei ishto} \u8212- there, whichever brother ultimately performs {\i yibum}, that woman will be exclusively his wife. The claim is individual and exclusive in its realization, even if it is currently shared. {\i Hacha kochad vechad lo karinan bei karmo} \u8212- here, neither partner will ever be able to say \u8220"my vineyard\u8221" in an exclusive sense. The verse requires a sense of individual ownership, and joint partnership cannot supply that.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mavrich & Stunted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Branch on Veg\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak offers yet another case of {\i harkavat heter} that would qualify for the exemption: {\i bemavrich ilan beyerek} \u8212- grafting a tree branch onto a vegetable plant. In such a case, the base is a vegetable, not a tree, so the grafted branch is not absorbed into a tree-host and retains its own status as a young tree, remaining {\i chayav} in {\i orlah}. This would therefore constitute a permitted grafting that carries {\i orlah} status and thus entitles the owner to return from battle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vegetable Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, this case may itself be {\i harkavat issur} according to one opinion. A {\i baraita} records the dispute: Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel, in the name of Rabbi Yehuda ben Gamash of Kfar Akko, permits grafting a tree onto a vegetable; the Sages forbid it. According to the Sages, then, this would be a forbidden act, and the exemption derived from it would fall under {\i harkavat issur} rather than {\i harkavat heter}. According to Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel it is permitted, and in that case the branch retains its {\i orlah} status \u8212- since the vegetable host would never be subject to {\i orlah} \u8212- and the grafter would have a basis for returning from war.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stunted Trees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then shifts to a related discussion brought by Rav Dimi in the name of Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov. If a vine or tree is stunted, never growing more than a {\i tefach} in height, it is {\i chayav} in {\i orlah} for its entire life \u8212- {\i chayava b\u8217'orla kol shenoseha}. The reason is {\i d\u8217'michzi k\u8217'vas shata} \u8212- it always appears to be a one-year-old plant. Even if it is already five years old, observers will assume it is still within its first three years and therefore {\i orlah}, so it must always be treated as such to avoid causing others to sin. This ruling applies specifically when one has only a small stand of such stunted vines or trees \u8212- even a minimal {\i kerem} of five \u8212- because the configuration looks like a normal vineyard of young plants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orchard Known\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if one has an entire orchard of such stunted trees \u8212- {\i kula kerem} \u8212- the ruling changes. {\i Kala is lei} \u8212- the word gets out. Everyone in the area knows that this particular farmer has an unusual orchard of miniature trees. Since the reality is publicly known, there is no concern that observers will err, and the trees are not treated as perpetually in {\i orlah} status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Corpse Proximity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Amos Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with one final tradition transmitted by Rav Dimi in the name of Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov \u8212- collected here solely because it shares the same chain of transmission as the preceding rulings. {\i Meis tofes arba amos l\u8217'krias shema} \u8212- a corpse occupies four {\i amot} for the purpose of reciting {\i Shema}. One who is within four {\i amot} of a dead body may not recite {\i Shema}. The reason is the principle of {\i lo\u8217'eg l\u8217'rash chereif oseihu} \u8212- one who mocks the poor blasphemes his Maker \u8212- applied here to mean that reciting {\i Shema} in the presence of a {\i meis} who cannot himself fulfill the commandment is a form of mockery of the dead. This principle extends to other acts of religious observance as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 44\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meit Tofes Arba Amot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a statement transmitted by Rabbi Yitzchak in the name of Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Eleazar ben Yaakov: {\i meit tofes arba amot letumah} \u8212- that within four {\i amot} of a corpse, a person is considered {\i tamei}, this rule functioning as a mechanism to compel people to maintain distance from the dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatzar HaKever\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara marshals a corroborating teaching: {\i chatzar hakever} \u8212- the courtyard situated in front of a burial cave. If one stands in such a courtyard, {\i ha\u8217'omed betochah tahor} \u8212- he is {\i tahor}. The underlying rationale, as Rashi explains, is that the wall of the cave serves as a clear demarcation: a person standing in the courtyard can see exactly where the cave begins and therefore will not inadvertently enter the space of the dead. The qualifying condition is {\i vehu sheyiyu bah arba amot} \u8212- the courtyard must measure four {\i amot}. This minimum is not a distance requirement from the graves themselves but a size requirement for the courtyard as an independent space. If the courtyard is too small, it cannot be considered a genuinely separate domain, and one standing in it would effectively be considered co-located with the interior of the cave. This is the position of Beit Shammai.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai v. Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel disagrees, holding that {\i arbaah tefachim} suffice. Even a very small courtyard \u8212- provided it affords a person some place to stand \u8212- is adequate, since the wall itself provides the meaningful separation. The presence of the wall, on this view, does the primary halachic work, and the minimum size threshold can therefore be set much lower.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Access Orientation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then introduces a distinction based on how one enters the courtyard: {\i bameh devarim amurim shepitchah milamalah} \u8212- when does the four-{\i amah} requirement apply? When the entrance to the courtyard is from above, meaning one must climb or drop down into the space. {\i Aval pitchah min hatzad, divrei hakol arba amot} \u8212- but if the entrance is from the side, then all opinions agree the courtyard must be four {\i amot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately objects: {\i klapi laya} \u8212- this seems backwards. If entry is from the side \u8212- {\i madrik venafik} \u8212- one can navigate in and out smoothly without any part of the body passing over the cave. It is entry from above that is genuinely dangerous, since {\i iy efshar delo mayei} \u8212- it is virtually impossible to jump in and out without some part of one\u8217's body hovering over the burial space. The Gemara therefore reverses the ruling: the smaller size ({\i tefachim}) suffices when entry is from the side, because the risk of overhang is minimal; when entry is from above, the larger four-{\i amah} dimension is required so as to ensure the person can remain safely clear of the cave\u8217's interior space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wall Demarcation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws the larger point together: {\i chatzar hakever, demisayma mechitzta} \u8212- the courtyard of a burial cave works because the wall is clearly delineated. One can see exactly where the boundary lies and stay clear of it. {\i Aval meit dealma, tefis} \u8212- but in the case of an ordinary corpse lying in an open space, there is no such demarcation, and the four-{\i amah} {\i tumah} zone takes hold automatically, compelling people to keep their distance. The proof from {\i chatzar hakever} is admittedly indirect \u8212- one might question whether the existence of a wall truly maps onto the rule of {\i meit tofes arba amot} \u8212- but the Gemara presents it as consistent with the principle: the extension of {\i tumah} to four {\i amot} functions precisely as a distancing mechanism in the absence of physical demarcation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Expansion Hermeneutics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Betroths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the {\i meshuach milchama} and the exemptions from military service, the Gemara takes up the phrase {\i mi ha\u8217'ish asher eiras isha} \u8212- \u8220"who is the man who betroths a woman.\u8221" Just as the earlier sections expanded the categories of houses and vineyards beyond their most straightforward applications, so too the category of betrothal is subject to interpretive expansion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betrothal Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu rabbanan}: {\i asher eiras} \u8212- \u8220"who betroths\u8221" \u8212- includes not only one who betroths a virgin ({\i betulah}), but also one who betroths a widow or divorc\u233 ?e, and even one whose connection to the woman is not through a formal act of {\i erusin} at all but through the bond of {\i yibum} \u8212- where a brother died and left a widow, making the surviving brother a {\i yavam} with an obligation to that woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even more expansively: if five brothers fought together and one died in battle, having left a childless widow, all the remaining brothers return home \u8212- even though only one of them will ultimately perform {\i yibum}. Since at the time of departure any one of them might be the one to take her, all of them share the exemption. This holding is notable: the exemption attaches not to the certainty of the obligation but to the possibility of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Marriages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i velo lekacha} \u8212- \u8220"and did not take her\u8221" \u8212- functions as an exclusion. Just as the analogous phrases {\i velo chanacho} and {\i velo chilalo} excluded cases of {\i gezelah} or {\i issur} in earlier applications, so here the Torah excludes marriages that are halachically forbidden even while technically binding. The list includes: a widow ({\i almanah}) married to a {\i kohen gadol}, a divorced woman or {\i chalutzah} married to a regular {\i kohen}, a {\i mamzeret} or {\i netinah} married to an Israelite, and a daughter of Israel married to a {\i mamzer} or {\i natin}. In all these cases \u8212- unions that constitute a {\i lav} but where {\i kiddushin} nevertheless takes effect \u8212- the betrothed man does not receive the military exemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Transgression Ru\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This exclusion generates a {\i kushya}: given that Rabbi Yose HaGlili will say later that the {\i yarei verach halevav} \u8212- the fearful-hearted one \u8212- refers to someone who fears on account of {\i aveirot she\u8217'beyado}, sins presently in his possession, why do we need a special {\i pasuk} to exclude the man in a forbidden marriage? He has an {\i aveirah she\u8217'beyado} already! In the case of {\i gezelah} discussed earlier, the Gemara could say the person did {\i teshuvah} and repaid the theft, thereby clearing his slate. But here the man is still in the relationship \u8212- he cannot have rectified it without having divorced her. The {\i aveirah} should still be {\i beyado}. And yet the Gemara answers: {\i kiderabba} \u8212- this follows the view of Rabba.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba holds: {\i le\u8217'olam eino chayav ad she\u8217'yiv\u8217'ol} \u8212- the man does not transgress at all until he actually consummates the union through {\i biah}. Although the {\i pesukim} governing {\i issurei kehunah} speak in the language of {\i kicha} \u8212- \u8220"taking,\u8221" which sounds like {\i kiddushin} \u8212- Rabba maintains that the actual prohibition is only violated through the act of intercourse. His proof: the {\i pasuk} links {\i lo yikach} to {\i lo yechallel zaro be\u8217'amav} \u8212- the prohibition against \u8220"taking\u8221" is connected to the concern about desecrating his seed and altering her status. Neither of those consequences is possible without {\i biah}. Therefore: {\i mishum hachi eino lokeh ad she\u8217'yivol} \u8212- he is not flogged until {\i biah} has occurred. Prior to that act, whatever groundwork is being laid, no formal {\i aveirah} has yet been committed. This is why the {\i pasuk} of {\i velo lekacha} must explicitly exclude these cases \u8212- without it, since no {\i aveirah she\u8217'beyado} exists yet under Rabba\u8217's framework, they might otherwise qualify for the exemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi notes that this point applies with even greater force to the case of {\i mamzer}, since the {\i pasuk lo yavo mamzer bikhal hashem} does not even employ the language of {\i kicha}. All of the {\i arayot} \u8212- the most severe forbidden relations, those carrying {\i karet} \u8212- are formulated exclusively in terms of {\i biah}, not {\i kiddushin}. It is specifically the {\i issurei kehunah} where the {\i pasuk} unusually employs the language of marriage-taking, and it is for that reason that Rabba\u8217's ruling is most needed there: even with language that sounds like {\i kiddushin}, the transgression only attaches at the point of {\i biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further point of interest arises in this context: there is a {\i machloket rishonim} about the converse scenario \u8212- a {\i kohen} who cohabits with a {\i gerushah} outside the context of {\i kiddushin} entirely. According to the Rambam, such a {\i kohen} is not {\i over} on {\i lo yikach}, since the prohibition technically requires both {\i kiddushin} and {\i biah} to be fully constituted. One might ask, finally, why a {\i kohen} is fighting in the army at all \u8212- a genuinely interesting question that warrants further analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Order of Life\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah\u8217's Derech Eretz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i halachot} of all three exemptions having been treated, the Gemara turns to a thematic reflection on the sequence in which the Torah presents them: {\i asher bana, asher nata, asher eiras} \u8212- who built, who planted, who betrothed. {\i Limdah Torah derech eretz} \u8212- the Torah is teaching the proper order of human life: first build a house, then plant a vineyard, then marry. The principle is that one should first establish the material capacity to support a family before undertaking the responsibility of one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solomon\u8217's Wisdom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shlomo expressed the same idea in his wisdom (Proverbs 24): {\i hachein bachutz melachtecha ve\u8217'atadah basadeh lach, achar u\u8217'vanita beisecha} \u8212- \u8220"Prepare your labor outside, ready it in the field, and afterward build your house.\u8221" The peshat appears to counsel: first secure your livelihood and prepare your field, then establish your home. The Gemara proceeds to offer two {\i derashot} on this verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first interpretation: {\i hachein bachutz melachtecha} \u8212- prepare your labor outside \u8212- {\i zeh bayis}, this refers to building a house. {\i Ve\u8217'atadah basadeh lach} \u8212- prepare it in the field for yourself \u8212- {\i zeh karem}, this is the vineyard, one\u8217's source of livelihood. {\i Achar u\u8217'vanita beisecha} \u8212- afterward build your house \u8212- {\i zu isha}, this signifies marriage, since the Gemara equates {\i bayit} with {\i ishah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second interpretation \u8212- {\i davar acher} \u8212- the verse is read as a curriculum of Torah study: {\i hachein bachutz melachtecha} \u8212- {\i zeh mikra}, this is Scripture; {\i ve\u8217'atadah basadeh lach} \u8212- {\i zeh mishnah}, this is Mishnah; {\i achar u\u8217'vanita beisecha} \u8212- {\i zeh Gemara}, this is the study of Gemara. The point, made explicit by the Maharsha but already implicit in the Gemara, is that learning {\i mikra} and {\i mishnah} is preparatory groundwork \u8212- necessary stages, but ultimately in service of the genuine intellectual construction that is {\i talmud Gemara}. Just as one must first secure a livelihood and a house before starting a family, so one must traverse the prior stages of learning before engaging in the ultimate building, which is the {\i beit ne\u8217'eman} of Gemara study.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third reading \u8212- another {\i davar acher} \u8212- reframes the structure: {\i hachein bachutz melachtecha} \u8212- {\i zeh mikra u\u8217'mishnah}, Scripture and Mishnah together constitute stage one; {\i ve\u8217'atadah basadeh lach} \u8212- {\i zeh Gemara}, the study of Gemara is stage two; {\i achar u\u8217'vanita beisecha} \u8212- {\i eilu ma\u8217'asim tovim}, this is good deeds. On this reading, the ultimate goal beyond even the study of Gemara is the {\i ma\u8217'asim tovim} to which it should give rise \u8212- the classic question of whether the telos of Torah learning is the learning itself or the action it generates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer, son of Rabbi Yose HaGlili, offers yet a fourth reading. For him, all of {\i mikra}, {\i mishnah}, and {\i Gemara} together constitute stage one \u8212- {\i hachein bachutz melachtecha}. Stage two \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'atadah basadeh lach} \u8212- is {\i ma\u8217'asim tovim}. And the culminating stage three \u8212- {\i achar u\u8217'vanita beisecha} \u8212- is {\i derosh u\u8217'kabel sachar}: theoretical analysis, for which one receives reward. This phrase, {\i derosh u\u8217'kabel sachar}, is typically invoked in contexts where a {\i pasuk} is being explained as existing purely for theoretical derivation without practical application \u8212- such as the case of {\i ben sorer u\u8217'moreh}. What does it mean here as the highest stage? The Maharsha understands it as {\i chochmah iyunit} \u8212- speculative philosophical wisdom, as distinct from the {\i chochmah} of Gemara study per se. The Rambam\u8217's {\i Moreh Nevuchim} offers the clearest framework: the entire structure of {\i mitzvot}, learning, and {\i ma\u8217'asim tovim} exists to cultivate sound character, a stable society, and disciplined intellect \u8212- all so that one may ultimately dedicate oneself to pure theoretical knowledge of God. That is the {\i derosh} \u8212- the pure contemplation \u8212- that constitutes the final, highest {\i bayit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the three things invoked in these interpretations \u8212- Torah, {\i chuppah}, and {\i ma\u8217'asim tovim} \u8212- are the very same trio mentioned at a {\i brit milah}: {\i keshem she\u8217'nichnas la\u8217'brit kein yikanes le\u8217'Torah u\u8217'le\u8217'chupah u\u8217'le\u8217'ma\u8217'asim tovim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Returners\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gatehouse Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah turns to those who do {\i not} return from the front lines: among them is one who built only a {\i beit sha\u8217'ar} \u8212- a gatehouse \u8212- which does not constitute a true dwelling. The {\i Tanna} adds: if a man tore down his house and rebuilt it according to its original specifications, he does not return. However, {\i im hosif bo dimus echad, chozer} \u8212- if he added even a single row of bricks, departing even slightly from the original plan, he does return. Rabbi Eliezer adds: even one who built a {\i beit levenim} \u8212- a house of bricks \u8212- in the Sharon region would not return, because {\i mipnei shemechadeshin oto paamayim beshavua} \u8212- such structures require rebuilding twice every seven years, rendering the house insufficiently permanent to qualify as a stable dwelling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Completion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A separate and stronger category is {\i ve\u8217'elu she\u8217'einam zazim mimkomam} \u8212- those who do not even move from their position to provide support services. This category is derived from the {\i pasuk} concerning the newly married man: {\i lo yeitzei batzava velo yaavor alav lechol davar} \u8212- he shall not go out to the army and shall not be tasked with any matter. The expansion of this complete exemption to the cases of the house and vineyard requires that those cases have reached culmination: the house must have been dedicated ({\i chancho}), the vineyard must have reached its fourth year ({\i chillelo}), and the wife must have been fully married ({\i lekachah}).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Complete Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Wife Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu rabbanan}: the {\i pasuk} states {\i ki yikach ish ishah chadashah} \u8212- when a man takes a new wife \u8212- {\i lo yeitzei batzava velo yaavor alav lechol davar, naki yihyeh leveiso shanah achat veshimach et ishto asher lakach} \u8212- he shall be free for his home for one year and shall gladden the wife he has taken. The Gemara asks: {\i ein li ela ishah chadashah} \u8212- I only know this applies to a new wife. What of a widow or divorc\u233 ?e who has been married before? {\i Talmud lomar ishah, mikol makom} \u8212- the broader word {\i ishah} includes all women. Then: {\i im kein mah talmud lomar ishah chadashah} \u8212- if so, what is the point of the word {\i chadashah}? The answer: {\i ishah chadashah lo} \u8212- new to {\i him}. This serves to exclude {\i perat lemachazir gerushato} \u8212- a man who remarries his own divorc\u233 ?e; since she is not new to him, the full exemption does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Support Services\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo yaavor alav lechol davar} \u8212- he shall not be put to any service whatsoever. One might have thought the exemption is only from frontline combat while logistical support duties \u8212- supplying water, food, and maintaining roads \u8212- remain obligatory. The {\i pasuk} therefore adds {\i lechol davar} to indicate that not even auxiliary military service may be demanded of him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Culmination Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, one might think the complete exemption \u8212- release from all army-related duty \u8212- should extend also to the person who built a house but did not yet dedicate it, or who planted a vineyard but did not yet reach the fourth year, or who betrothed a woman but did not yet marry her. The {\i pasuk} responds: {\i alav} \u8212- {\i velo yaavor alav lechol davar} \u8212- {\i alav iy atah maavir}, only upon {\i him}, upon the one who has reached full culmination, do you impose no obligation whatsoever. {\i Aval atah maavir al acherim} \u8212- but those in the preliminary stages are subject to support duties, even though they are exempt from frontline combat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Lavin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does the {\i pasuk} state both {\i lo yeitzei batzava} and {\i velo yaavor alav lechol davar}, given that once any obligation to support is removed, the prohibition against frontline service should be self-evident? The answer: {\i laavor alav bishnei lavin} \u8212- to teach that if such a man does go out to fight, he has transgressed two separate {\i lavin}. Whether this prohibition runs against the army authorities for sending him or against the man himself for going is an interesting question \u8212- though the language of {\i lo yeitzei} and {\i lo yaavor} does suggest personal prohibition, not merely entitlement. The Rambam\u8217's formulation in {\i Hilchot Melachim} (7:11) confirms: if the man provides water, food, or road service, or stands guard at the wall, or contributes in any capacity to the army\u8217's function throughout that year, {\i lo ya\u8217'avor alav shum davar ba\u8217'olam} \u8212- he violates {\i lo yeitzei} and {\i lo yaavor}, two {\i lavin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two additional questions merit further attention: whether the man in the preliminary categories ({\i lo chancho}, {\i lo chillelo}, {\i lo lekachah}) can waive his exemption from frontline duty, and the underlying question of why a {\i kohen} would be fighting in the army at all \u8212- a point flagged earlier that remains unresolved and worth examining in the {\i Sefer Minchat Chinuch}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hayrei Verach Halevav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Simple\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah commands: {\i veyasfu hashotrim ledaber el ha\u8217'am} \u8212- and the officers shall continue to speak to the people \u8212- and then introduces the case of {\i ha\u8217'yarei verach halevav}, the fearful and tender-hearted. Rabbi Akiva reads this straightforwardly, {\i kemashmao} \u8212- as it sounds: a person who is genuinely afraid, {\i she\u8217'eino yachol la\u8217'amod bekishrei milchama} \u8212- who cannot hold his place in the battle formation when soldiers bind together into a fighting unit \u8212- {\i velirot cherev shlufa} \u8212- and who cannot bear to face a drawn sword. According to Rashi, {\i kishrei milchama} refers to the moment when soldiers lock together to form a human wall or phalanx.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose HaGlili Sin-Based\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose HaGlili disagrees, interpreting {\i ha\u8217'yarei verach halevav} as {\i ze hamityare me\u8217'aveirot she\u8217'beyado} \u8212- the one who fears on account of the sins presently in his possession. It is not physical fear of battle that the Torah addresses here but a consciousness of moral vulnerability: the awareness that one\u8217's sins have diminished one\u8217's merit and left one exposed on the battlefield.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Cover\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ulfichach taltah lo hatorah et kol elu sheyachazor biglalan} \u8212- it is for this reason that the Torah provided all these other exemptions \u8212- the house, the vineyard, the wife \u8212- so that the person who must return on account of his {\i aveirot she\u8217'beyado} could do so without publicly disclosing the real reason. He can go back in the company of those who have built houses or betrothed wives, and no one need know that his departure is motivated by sin-fear rather than any qualifying material circumstance. This raises a striking implication embedded in the word {\i lefichach} \u8212- as if to say the Torah\u8217's entire framework of practical exemptions exists, at least in part, to provide cover for the {\i aveira}-bearing soldier. On this reading, there is no misrepresentation involved in availing oneself of a legitimate exemption even when one\u8217's actual motivation is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva, notably, is the one advancing the {\i pshat} reading here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose Addition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose adds: {\i almanah lekohen gadol, gerushah vechalutzah lekohen hedyot, mamzeret unetinah leyisrael, bat yisrael lemamzer ulenatin} \u8212- all the cases of forbidden marriage discussed above \u8212- {\i harei hu ha\u8217'yarei verach halevav}. These men, too, fall under the category of the fearful-hearted, since their sin-laden situation makes them spiritually vulnerable. The Gemara had noted earlier that these cases seemingly present no {\i aveira she\u8217'beyado} under Rabba\u8217's framework (since the transgression only attaches at {\i biah}); Rabbi Yose is apparently disagreeing and treating the forbidden betrothal itself as an ongoing {\i aveirah she\u8217'beyado}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of how Rabbi Yose differs from Rabbi Yose HaGlili in this regard is taken up directly: {\i ika beiniyahu aveira de-rabbanan} \u8212- the distinction lies in whether Rabbinic violations also qualify. Rabbi Yose HaGlili, who spoke in general terms of any {\i aveirot she\u8217'beyado}, would include even Rabbinic transgressions. Rabbi Yose gave specific examples that are all Biblical prohibitions, which suggests he would not extend the category to {\i aveirot de-rabbanan}. The Gemara confirms this by asking {\i keman azla} \u8212- according to whom is the {\i baraita} that teaches: one who speaks between the {\i tefillin shel yad} and the {\i tefillin shel rosh} has an {\i aveirah beyado} and returns from the battle array? The answer: this goes according to Rabbi Yose HaGlili, who extends the principle even to Rabbinic violations. Similarly, the Gemara asks the same question regarding the {\i baraita} that teaches: one who heard the sound of trumpets and trembled, heard the clashing of shields and trembled, heard the clashing of swords, and lost control of his bladder \u8212- {\i chozer} \u8212- he too must return. {\i Leima Rabbi Akiva hi} \u8212- perhaps this goes only according to Rabbi Akiva, who makes the category about simple physical fear? The Gemara answers: no, even Rabbi Yose HaGlili would agree in that extreme case, {\i mishum de\u8217'ksiv velo yimas levav achiv k\u8217'levavo} \u8212- because the Torah explicitly prohibits the melting of one\u8217's fellow soldier\u8217's heart; at that degree of visible fear, even Rabbi Yose HaGlili concedes the man must depart lest he infect the rest of the army.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Officers & Deterrence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Officer Placement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vahaya kechalot hashotrim ledaber el ha\u8217'am} \u8212- after the officers finish speaking \u8212- {\i upakdu sarei tzevaot berosh ha\u8217'am} \u8212- officers are appointed at the head of the force. The Gemara adds {\i ube\u8217'akeivo} \u8212- and also at the rear: {\i ma\u8217'amidin zekifim lifnehem ve\u8217'acherim me\u8217'achareihem} \u8212- they station strong officers both in front of the troops and behind them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Iron Staffs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vekashilin shel barzel beyadeihem} \u8212- and in their hands are iron implements \u8212- whether staffs, blades, or some form of weapon. The purpose: {\i vechol hamevakesh lachazor, hareshut beyado lekapeach et shokav} \u8212- anyone who seeks to retreat, it is within the officers\u8217' authority to cut him down at the knees. The rationale is the principle: {\i techilat nifila nisa} \u8212- the beginning of falling in battle is flight. The first instinct under fire is to turn and run; armies throughout history have recognized that the most dangerous moment is when soldiers begin to flee. The fear of the consequences of desertion must outweigh the fear of advancing into battle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Philistine Wars\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scriptural proof comes from two {\i pesukim} juxtaposed in the Gemara: {\i nass Yisrael lifnei Plishtim vegam mageifa gedolah haytah ba\u8217'am} \u8212- Israel fled before the Philistines and there was a great blow among the people; and {\i vayanusu anshei Yisrael mipnei Plishtim vayiplu chalalim} \u8212- they fled before the Philistines and fell as corpses. The pairing demonstrates the causal chain: flight {\i is} the beginning of slaughter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 War Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Discretionary v. Obligat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bameh devarim amurim} \u8212- when do all these exemptions apply? According to Rabbi Yose HaGlili: in a {\i milchemet reshut}, a discretionary war. {\i Aval bemilchemet mitzvah, hakol yotzim} \u8212- but in an obligatory {\i mitzvah} war, all go out, without exemption. The paradigmatic example Rashi gives of a {\i milchemet mitzvah} is {\i kibush Eretz Yisrael bimei Yehoshua} \u8212- the conquest of the Land in the days of Joshua. The Rambam includes also {\i milchemet Amalek} and defensive wars \u8212- {\i lehoshia et Yisrael miyad tzar} \u8212- wars to rescue Israel from an attacking enemy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Y. HaGlili Discretion\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In a {\i milchemet reshut}, the exemptions operate fully, and those with unfinished houses, undedicated vineyards, and recent betrothals return home.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Obligatory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda frames the same dichotomy with different terminology: {\i bameh devarim amurim bemilchemet mitzvah} \u8212- when do the exemptions apply? In a {\i mitzvah} war. {\i Aval bemilchemet chovah, hakol yotzim afilu chatan mechedro vechallah mechupatah} \u8212- but in a {\i chovah} war, all go out, even the groom from his chamber and the bride from her canopy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terminology Equivalence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies: are these two {\i tannaim} actually debating, or merely using different vocabulary for the same categories? Rabbi Yochanan states: {\i reshut de\u8217'rabbanan zu hi mitzvah de\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- what the {\i Rabbanan} call {\i reshut} is what Rabbi Yehuda calls {\i mitzvah}. {\i Mitzvah de\u8217'rabbanan zu hi chovah de\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- what the {\i Rabbanan} call {\i mitzvah} is what Rabbi Yehuda calls {\i chovah}. On this reading, the categories are substantively identical; the dispute is purely terminological.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three War Levels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava sharpens the analysis by introducing a tripartite framework. {\i Milchemet Yehoshua lichbosh \u8212- divrei hakol chovah}: the war of Joshua to conquer the Land is universally placed in the highest category. {\i Milchemet beit David l\u8217'rivachah \u8212- divrei hakol reshut}: expansionary wars fought by David and his successors for enlargement of territory are universally placed in the lowest category. The contested middle ground is preemptive war \u8212- {\i lema\u8217'utei ovdei kochavim delo leisu aleihu} \u8212- striking against enemy nations before they launch an attack. For such a war, {\i mar kari lei mitzvah umar kari lei reshut}: one authority calls it {\i mitzvah}, the other calls it {\i reshut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking Gemara. In terms of halachic outcome \u8212- whether the exemptions apply \u8212- both opinions agree: a preemptive war is not in the highest ({\i chovah}) category, so the exemptions for the newly built house, the vineyard, and the betrothed woman are all operative. The argument between them is not about the exemptions at all. Both the {\i Tanna Kamma} calling it {\i reshut} and Rabbi Yehuda calling it {\i mitzvah} agree that it is not {\i chovah} and thus the exemptions stand. This can be laid out concisely:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna Kamma} operates with two levels \u8212- {\i reshut} and {\i mitzvah}. Rabbi Yehuda operates with two levels \u8212- {\i mitzvah} and {\i chovah}. Rabbi Yochanan equates these: the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's {\i reshut} = Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's {\i mitzvah}; the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's {\i mitzvah} = Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's {\i chovah}. In both systems, the lower level carries the exemptions; the upper level sends everyone to battle. Everyone agrees that Joshua\u8217's conquest belongs in the upper level and expansionary wars belong in the lower level. The preemptive war falls in the lower level for both \u8212- they simply name it differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nifka Mina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication of calling a preemptive war a {\i mitzvah} rather than a {\i reshut} is not about the exemptions but about an entirely different halachic principle: {\i osek bemitzvah patur min hamitzvah} \u8212- one engaged in a {\i mitzvah} is exempt from other {\i mitzvot}. If a preemptive war is classified as a {\i mitzvah}, then a soldier engaged in it need not interrupt his duties to perform other obligations \u8212- he would not be required to don {\i tefillin}, take a {\i lulav}, or fulfill other {\i mitzvot} during the campaign. If it is merely {\i reshut}, no such exemption applies. The {\i nafka mina} between the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Yehuda on the question of preemptive war thus surfaces here: not in who fights, but in what obligations are suspended for those who do.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eglah Arufah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the conclusion of the discussion of {\i milchama}, the Gemara opens the final {\i perek} of the tractate with the {\i mitzvah} of {\i eglah arufah} \u8212- the decapitated heifer performed in response to an unsolved murder. When a corpse is discovered and the killer is unknown, the Torah prescribes a ritual in which the {\i zekanim} come forth and make the declaration: {\i yadeinu lo shafchu et hadam hazeh} \u8212- our hands did not shed this blood. This declaration is in {\i lashon hakodesh}, as the Gemara will address. The governing {\i pasuk} is {\i ki yimatze challal ba\u8217'adamah\u8230 ? veyatzu zekenecha veshoftecha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three v. Five\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Sheloshah mibeit din hagadol shebiyerushalayim hayu yotzin} \u8212- three representatives from the Great Sanhedrin of Jerusalem would go out. The {\i zekanim} referred to in the {\i pasuk} are not merely the elders of the local city but the elders of the entire nation \u8212- representatives of the central court. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: {\i chamishah} \u8212- five are required. His derivation: {\i zekenecha} \u8212- your elders (plural, implying at least two) \u8212- {\i veshoftecha} \u8212- your judges (plural, implying at least two more) \u8212- totals four. {\i Ve\u8217'ein beit din shakul} \u8212- and since a {\i beit din} must have an odd number to avoid a deadlock, {\i mosifin aleihem od echad} \u8212- one more is added, yielding five.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disqualifying Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Certain circumstances disqualify the {\i eglah arufah} ritual altogether. {\i Nimtza tamun begal} \u8212- if the corpse was found buried under a heap of stones; {\i o talui be\u8217'ilan} \u8212- or hanging from a tree; {\i o tzaf al penei hamayim} \u8212- or floating on the surface of water \u8212- {\i lo hayu orfin}, the ritual is not performed. The {\i pasuk} requires {\i challal ba\u8217'adamah} \u8212- fallen on the ground: exposed on the ground ({\i lo tamun begal}), fallen rather than suspended ({\i lo talui be\u8217'ilan}), and on the ground rather than floating ({\i lo tzaf al penei hamayim}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proximity & Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additionally, if the corpse was found near a border city ({\i samuch le\u8217'sfar}), or near a city whose majority population is non-Jewish ({\i rubah ovdei kochavim}), or near a city that lacks a functioning {\i beit din} ({\i she\u8217'ein bah beit din}) \u8212- {\i lo hayu orfin}, the ritual is not performed. The measurement to determine which city is closest is conducted only with respect to cities that have a {\i beit din}: {\i ein modedin ela le\u8217'ir sheyesh bah beit din}. The significance of the local {\i beit din} here \u8212- distinct from the Great Sanhedrin representatives who come from Jerusalem \u8212- reflects the role of {\i ziknei ha\u8217'ir hahi}, the elders of that particular city, as participants in the ritual alongside the central court emissaries. The Gemara will elaborate on all of these requirements, and the discussion of {\i eglah arufah} will continue in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 45\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Egla Arufah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i perek} of {\i Sotah} turns to {\i egla arufa}, the ritual of the beheaded calf performed when a murder victim is found and the killer cannot be identified. Like the {\i sota} procedure, {\i egla arufa} involves an unresolved situation \u8212- in the case of {\i sota}, it is uncertainty about whether a woman committed adultery; here, the act of murder is known but the perpetrator is not. The {\i mishna} establishes that the declaration made by the elders over the calf must be recited in {\i lashon hakodesh}, and it cites the verse {\i ki yimatzei chalal} as its source. The {\i gemara}, however, immediately asks: {\i mai ka\u8217'amar}? What is the {\i mishna} actually saying? The verse {\i ki yimatzei chalal} does not appear to contain any indication that the declaration is made in Hebrew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbahu\u8217's V\u8217'anu V\u8217'amru\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu resolves the difficulty. The {\i mishna}\u8217's citation of {\i ki yimatzei chalal} is not the source for the Hebrew-language requirement \u8212- that source is derived elsewhere, through a {\i gezerah shavah} based on the phrase {\i v\u8217'anu v\u8217'amru}. The verse in the {\i egla arufa} passage reads {\i v\u8217'anu v\u8217'amru: yadeinu lo shafchu es hadam hazeh} \u8212- \u8220"they shall respond and say: our hands did not spill this blood.\u8221" The same linguistic formula, {\i v\u8217'anu v\u8217'amru}, appears in the context of the blessings and curses on {\i Har Gerizim} and {\i Har Eval}, where the {\i Levi\u8217'im} respond and say \u8212- a context already established as requiring {\i lashon hakodesh}. The parallel phrasing creates the equation: just as the response there is in Hebrew, so too here. The {\i mishna}\u8217's citation of {\i ki yimatzei chalal} is therefore not addressing the language requirement at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Procedure & Measurement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, the {\i mishna} is introducing the procedure of {\i egla arufa} \u8212- {\i seder egla arufa keitzad}. The verse {\i ki yimatzei chalal ba\u8217'adamah v\u8217'yatzu zkeinecha v\u8217'shoftecha u\u8217'madadu svivos hachalal} describes how the elders and judges go out and measure around the corpse to determine which city is nearest. The {\i mishna} teaches that {\i shlosha mibeis din hagadol shebiYerushalayim hayu yotzin} \u8212- three representatives from the central {\i beis din} in Jerusalem would go out to perform this measurement. It is these central figures who are identified by the words {\i zkeinecha v\u8217'shoftecha}, not the {\i ziknei ha\u8217'ir}, the elders of the nearby city, who come later to perform the actual ritual once the measurement has established which city is closest.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three vs.\u160 ?Five Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states that three representatives go out, but Rabbi Yehuda says five. A {\i braita} sets out the reasoning behind each position. The word {\i zkeinecha}, \u8220"your elders,\u8221" is in the plural, indicating a minimum of two. The word {\i v\u8217'shoftecha}, \u8220"and your judges,\u8221" likewise indicates two. But a {\i beis din} cannot have an even number \u8212- it requires an odd number to allow for a deciding vote and to structurally represent judicial authority. Therefore, one more is added: {\i masifin aleihem od echad harei kan chamisha} \u8212- that gives five, in the view of Rabbi Yehuda. Rabbi Shimon, identified as the {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i mishna}, holds three: he counts only from {\i zkeinecha}, yielding two, and adds one to make an odd number.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zkeinecha Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses Rabbi Shimon: the verse explicitly says {\i v\u8217'shoftecha} \u8212- what does he do with that word? The answer is that Rabbi Shimon reads {\i v\u8217'shoftecha} not as adding to the count but as identifying the character of those going out \u8212- {\i l\u8217'meyuchadim shebishoftecha}, the distinguished ones among your judges, meaning the central court, the {\i beis din hagadol}. The word tells you {\i who}, not {\i how many}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, draws the identification of the {\i beis din hagadol} from a different source \u8212- the possessive form {\i zkeinecha} itself, which he parses in two stages. Had the Torah written simply {\i ziknei}, one might have thought this meant any elderly men found in the marketplace; the possessive \u8220"yours\u8221" signals that these are men of judicial standing. But even knowing they are judges, one might have assumed a local {\i beis din} \u8212- a {\i sanhedrei ktana} of twenty-three \u8212- suffices. The additional word {\i v\u8217'shoftecha} then clarifies that it must be the {\i hamiyuchadin shebishoftecha}, the most distinguished among your judges, meaning the {\i beis din hagadol}. Since Rabbi Yehuda uses {\i v\u8217'shoftecha} for this qualitative identification, he needs another mechanism to arrive at the count of five.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} considers whether Rabbi Yehuda could learn both the identity of the {\i beis din hagadol} and the number five from {\i ziknei ha\u8217'eidah}, the verse in {\i Vayikra} concerning the {\i sanhedrin} that rules erroneously and must bring a special {\i korban}. From that context Rabbi Yehuda derives that only five members of the {\i beis din hagadol} need to be present \u8212- not the entire court. The {\i gemara} suggests that if Rabbi Yehuda learns both identity and number from that source, he would have no need to use {\i v\u8217'shoftecha} for numerical purposes at all. This challenge is acknowledged and the reasoning is refined accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vav Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the matter by focusing on the conjunction {\i vav} at the beginning of {\i v\u8217'shoftecha}. Had the verse read {\i zkeinecha shoftecha} \u8212- without the connecting {\i vav} \u8212- it would read naturally as an appositive: \u8220"your elders who are your judges,\u8221" a single group described in two ways, yielding no additional count. The extra {\i vav} signals that {\i v\u8217'shoftecha} is to be counted as a distinct unit, adding two more to the two of {\i zkeinecha}, and requiring one more for an odd number \u8212- hence five. {\i V\u8217'shoftecha l\u8217'minyana}. Rabbi Shimon, by contrast, does not derive significant weight from the additional {\i vav}, and therefore holds three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also considers whether the plural verbs {\i v\u8217'yatzu} (\u8220"and they shall go out\u8221") and {\i u\u8217'madadu} (\u8220"and they shall measure\u8221") should independently add to the count \u8212- potentially yielding nine for Rabbi Yehuda or seven for Rabbi Shimon. The answer is that those verbs are needed for their own teaching, as derived from a {\i braita}: {\i hen v\u8217'lo shlucheihen} \u8212- they themselves must go out, not send proxies, and {\i she\u8217'afillu nimtza ba\u8217'alil la\u8217'ir} \u8212- even if it is obvious which city is nearest, they are still required to measure, because {\i shemitzvah la\u8217'asok bemedidah}, the act of measurement is itself part of the {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer\u8217's Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the {\i mishna} is not in accordance with Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, as a {\i braita} teaches. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov reads the verse entirely differently: {\i zkeinecha zo sanhedrin} \u8212- \u8220"your elders\u8221" refers to the full {\i sanhedrin}; {\i shoftecha ze melech v\u8217'kohen gadol} \u8212- \u8220"and your judges\u8221" refers to the king and the {\i kohen gadol}. The textual support for each: the king is called {\i shofet} from the verse {\i melech bemishpat ya\u8217'amid aretz}, the king upholds the land through justice; the {\i kohen gadol} is likewise included under {\i shoftecha} from the verse {\i u\u8217'vata el hakohanim haleviyim v\u8217'el hashofet asher yihyeh bayamim hahem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 National Responsibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interpretation carries profound weight. It is already striking that when a body is found in some remote location, representatives of the highest court in the land must travel out to measure and identify the nearest city. But Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov takes this further: not only the {\i sanhedrin}, but the political leader and the religious leader of the entire nation must become involved. The reason lies in the nature of the ritual itself. A murder has occurred and gone unresolved \u8212- the killer has not been identified, justice has not been served, and the verse speaks of {\i v\u8217'nitchaper lahem hadam}, an expiation for the blood. The principle that {\i la\u8217'aretz lo yechuppar badam asher shupach bah ki im bedam shofcho} \u8212- the land cannot be fully atoned for without justice being done \u8212- means that this unresolved guilt operates at a national level. There is a murderer somewhere at large; responsibility for that has not been discharged. The entire national leadership \u8212- the courts, the king representing civil order, the {\i kohen gadol} representing divine law \u8212- must together take ownership of that failure. It is not simply that a crime occurred, which would be handled locally; it is that a crime occurred and was never resolved, and that demands a national response.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Entire Sanhedrin Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the further question: when Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov says {\i zkeinecha zo sanhedrin}, does he mean only that {\i sanhedrin} representatives \u8212- three or five, as debated by Rabbi Shimon and Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- must participate, with his dispute being only about the addition of the king and {\i kohen gadol}? Or does he mean the entire {\i sanhedrin} must go out? The phrasing {\i zkeinecha zo sanhedrin} seems to suggest the whole body, which would be consistent with the overarching theme of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's position \u8212- that the entire national leadership must assume communal ownership of this unresolved murder. The question is left open for the {\i gemara} to resolve.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zaken Mamre Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zaken Mamre Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef introduces a proof from the laws of {\i zaken mamrei}, the rebellious elder. If an elder of sufficient standing disputed a ruling of the central court, received their ruling against him, returned to his community, and continued to teach and rule in accordance with his own position, he is liable as a {\i zaken mamrei}. The case the {\i gemara} presents is: {\i matza zakein mamrei abei pagi v\u8217'himrah aleihem} \u8212- he encountered the {\i sanhedrin} not in the {\i lishkas ha-gazis}, their official chamber in the {\i beis hamikdash}, but in the {\i abei pagi}, the environs or surroundings of Jerusalem outside that chamber, and he voiced his dissenting position there. The question: {\i yachol tehei hamraso hamraah} \u8212- could that constitute actionable rebellion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is no, because the verse introducing {\i zaken mamrei} reads {\i v\u8217'kamta v\u8217'alisa el ha-makom asher yivchar}, you shall arise and ascend to the place that God has chosen \u8212- {\i melamed she-ha-makom goreim}, the place determines the law. Only when the court is sitting in their designated location does the confrontation carry the full weight of divine authority necessary to make defiance of their ruling a capital offense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why They Left\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a critical question: for the {\i sanhedrin} to have been found outside the {\i lishkas ha-gazis} \u8212- which this scenario assumes \u8212- why were they there? The {\i gemara} cites the verse {\i sarech agan ha-sohar al yechsar ha-mazeg} from {\i Shir HaShirim}, understood as a metaphor for the {\i sanhedrin}. Rashi explains that the image of a round goblet corresponds to the {\i sanhedrin}\u8217's semicircular seating arrangement. From the phrase {\i al yechsar ha-mazeg} \u8212- \u8220"the mixed wine shall not be lacking\u8221" \u8212- the {\i gemara} derives a rule: if any member of the {\i sanhedrin} needs to leave, it is permitted only if twenty-three members remain, corresponding to a full {\i sanhedrei ktana} capable of adjudicating capital cases. The proportion of twenty-three out of seventy-one approximates a one-to-three ratio, paralleling the one-to-three wine-to-water dilution of mixed wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, could the {\i sanhedrin} have left their chamber for a {\i dvar reshus}, an ordinary non-{\i mitzvah} purpose? The {\i gemara} concludes they could not have \u8212- at minimum, the scenario of the {\i zaken mamrei} encountering the entire court outside implies that the whole {\i sanhedrin} had left, and the entire {\i sanhedrin} cannot leave for a {\i dvar reshus}. They must have had a {\i mitzvah} purpose. What {\i mitzvah} purpose would bring the entire {\i sanhedrin} outside? {\i Lav lemedidat egla} \u8212- is it not for the measurement of the {\i egla arufa}? This, the {\i gemara} suggests, must be the position of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov: that the entire {\i sanhedrin} goes out for the measurement, consistent with his view that {\i zkeinecha zo sanhedrin} means the full court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges this inference: there is another {\i mitzvah} purpose for which the entire {\i sanhedrin} is required outside the chamber \u8212- the expansion of the sanctified boundaries of Jerusalem or the {\i azarot} of the {\i beis hamikdash}, as the {\i mishna} teaches, {\i ein mosifin al ha-ir v\u8217'al ha-azarot ela be-veis din shel shivim v\u8217'echad} \u8212- such expansion requires all seventy-one members. So the {\i zaken mamrei} scenario does not uniquely point to {\i egla arufa}; the {\i sanhedrin} might have left for boundary expansion. The question of whether Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov requires the entire {\i sanhedrin} for {\i egla arufa} therefore remains unresolved from this reasoning alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Egla Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is then cited in support of Rav Yosef\u8217's original position: {\i tanya kevateih d\u8217'Rav Yosef}. The {\i braita} itself states \u8212- {\i matzan beis pagi v\u8217'himrah aleihem} \u8212- that if the {\i zaken} found the {\i sanhedrin} outside the {\i beis hamikdash} and rebelled against them, {\i kegon she-yatzu li-medidat egla o le-hosif al ha-ir v\u8217'al ha-azarot} \u8212- as for example when they went out for the measurement of the {\i egla} or to add to the city or the {\i azarot} \u8212- he is nevertheless not a {\i zaken mamrei}, since the verse requires that the confrontation occur in their designated place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The significance of this {\i braita} is that it explicitly lists the measurement of {\i egla arufa} as a legitimate reason for the {\i sanhedrin} to be found outside \u8212- not as the only reason, but as a named scenario. And this scenario is coherent only on the view of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, who holds that the entire {\i sanhedrin} participates in the {\i egla arufa} measurement. On the views of Rabbi Shimon or Rabbi Yehuda, where only three or five representatives go out, the remaining {\i sanhedrin} members would have no reason to leave the {\i lishkas ha-gazis}. The {\i braita} thus provides strong support that Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov requires the full court, consistent with his broader vision of total national leadership assuming responsibility for unresolved murder.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Body Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} and accompanying {\i baraita} enumerate the conditions under which a body qualifies for {\i egla arufa}: it must be a {\i chalal}, understood as one killed by a sword ({\i be-chalal cherev}) and not merely strangled ({\i v\u8217'lo chanuk}); it must be fully still and not still convulsing on the ground ({\i v\u8217'lo mefarper ba-adamah}); it must not be buried under a pile ({\i v\u8217'lo tamun be-gal}); and it must be lying fallen on the ground ({\i nofel ba-adamah}) rather than suspended from a tree ({\i v\u8217'lo talui be-ilan}). The body must also be found in a field ({\i ba-sadeh}) and not floating on water ({\i v\u8217'lo tzaf al penei ha-mayim}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamun & Tzaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether the {\i mishna}\u8217's requirement that the body be exposed \u8212- not buried and not suspended \u8212- reflects the position of Rabbi Yehuda specifically, as against the {\i rabbanan}. The comparison is to the laws of {\i shichcha}, the forgotten sheaf: the verse {\i ki tikzor ketzircha besadecha v\u8217'shachachta omer basadeh} \u8212- when you harvest your field and forget a sheaf in the field \u8212- is the basis for {\i shichcha}. There, a {\i braita} records that the word {\i basadeh} is interpreted by Rabbi Yehuda as {\i perat le-tamun} \u8212- excluding a sheaf that is buried; whereas the {\i rabbanan} say {\i basadeh le-rabot et ha-tamun} \u8212- including even a buried sheaf within the laws of {\i shichcha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, the {\i mishna}\u8217's apparent exclusion of a buried body from {\i egla arufa} sounds like Rabbi Yehuda. Rav responds: {\i afilu teima Rabbanan} \u8212- even the {\i rabbanan} could agree that the body must be exposed, because the derivation in each case depends on its own contextual logic. By {\i egla arufa}, the verse reads {\i ki yimatzei chalal ba\u8217'adamah} \u8212- \u8220"if a corpse is found on the ground.\u8221" Before reaching the word {\i ba\u8217'adamah}, one would have assumed inclusively that the corpse could be found anywhere. The word {\i ba\u8217'adamah} therefore comes to narrow \u8212- {\i perat le-tamun}, excluding what is buried. The direction of the derivation is from broad to narrow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By {\i shichcha}, the context is different. The verse reads {\i ki tikzor ketzircha} \u8212- your harvest \u8212- and then {\i v\u8217'shachachta}. The act of forgetting is juxtaposed to the act of cutting, and just as cutting involves grain that is exposed and visible, so too forgetting in that context initially suggests an exposed sheaf \u8212- narrow from the outset. The word {\i basadeh} then comes to broaden \u8212- {\i le-rabot et ha-tamun} \u8212- including even a buried sheaf. Each derivation responds to its own starting assumption, and the parallel positions by {\i shichcha} do not necessarily map onto {\i egla arufa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheaves Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then develops the {\i shichcha} analysis further. If the juxtaposition of forgetting to cutting already suggests that only an exposed sheaf qualifies \u8212- which is what Rabbi Yehuda holds \u8212- then why does he need the word {\i basadeh} to tell him so? The {\i gemara} answers: Rabbi Yehuda uses {\i basadeh} for a different purpose \u8212- {\i le-rabot shikchat kama}, to include the case of standing grain ({\i kama}) that has not yet been cut. If someone forgets unharvested grain still standing in the field, that too is {\i shichcha}. The word {\i besadecha} \u8212- \u8220"in your field\u8221" \u8212- teaches that even grain still {\i in} the field, not yet harvested, is subject to {\i shichcha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan}, who use {\i basadeh} to include buried sheaves, derive the inclusion of {\i shikchat kama} differently \u8212- from the juxtaposition {\i besadecha v\u8217'shachachta}, the very collocation of \u8220"your field\u8221" and \u8220"you forgot\u8221" suggesting that forgetting pertains to whatever remains {\i in} your field, including standing grain. Conversely, the {\i rabbanan} derive the exclusion of sheaves that flew into a neighbor\u8217's field \u8212- {\i perat le-shetzafu omarim le-toch sadeh chaveiro} \u8212- from the possessive {\i besadecha}, your field, not your neighbor\u8217's. Rabbi Yehuda, who does not place the same weight on the possessive, gets this exclusion from the juxtaposition rather than the possessive form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamun Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through those exegetical debates, the {\i gemara} circles back to the question of {\i min b\u8217'mino} \u8212- whether being buried or suspended under an object of the same type affects the determination of {\i tamun} or {\i tzaf}. A {\i baraita} discusses the case of a sheaf that was picked up to be carried to the city, placed on top of a fellow sheaf, and then forgotten. The {\i tanna kamma} rules: {\i hatachton shichcha v\u8217'ha-elyon eino shichcha} \u8212- the bottom sheaf is {\i shichcha}, the top is not. Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda in the name of Rabbi Shimon says both are exempt: {\i ha-tachton mipnei she-hu tamun}, the bottom because it is buried, {\i v\u8217'ha-elyon mipnei she-hu tzaf}, the top because it is floating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially assumes that both {\i tannaim} agree with Rabbi Yehuda that {\i tamun} creates an exemption for {\i shichcha}, and that their debate is specifically about {\i min b\u8217'mino} \u8212- whether being buried under a similar object (another sheaf) counts as {\i tamun}. On this reading, Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda holds that {\i min b\u8217'mino havei tamun}, while the {\i tanna kamma} holds it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this reading. If both agreed that {\i tamun} is an exemption, everyone would agree that being buried under another sheaf constitutes {\i tamun}, since that is clearly a form of burial. The more defensible reading is that they are not debating the definition of {\i tamun} at all \u8212- they are debating whether {\i tamun} creates any exemption from {\i shichcha} in the first place. The {\i tanna kamma} holds like the {\i rabbanan} \u8212- buried sheaves are still subject to {\i shichcha} \u8212- while Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda holds like his father Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- buried sheaves are exempt. The scenario involving one sheaf on top of another is chosen not to illustrate the minimum threshold of what counts as {\i tamun}, but to highlight the full scope of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position: {\i odi\u8217'acha kocho d\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}, to show that even burial under a similar object \u8212- {\i min b\u8217'mino} \u8212- constitutes {\i tamun} in his view.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exposure & Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nofel Ba\u8217'adamah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As enumerated in the {\i baraita}, the body qualifying for {\i egla arufa} must be {\i nofel ba\u8217'adamah} \u8212- fallen and lying on the ground. If it is buried under a pile ({\i tamun be-gal}) or suspended from a tree ({\i taluy be-ilan}), the obligation does not apply. These requirements parallel, in their structure, the {\i tamun} and {\i tzaf} conditions explored in the {\i shichcha} discussion, though as demonstrated above, the derivations for {\i egla arufa} and {\i shichcha} proceed from different contextual starting points and cannot simply be read across from one to the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stacked Corpses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problem Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, in a moment of confident self-assessment \u8212- declaring {\i hareini k\u8217'Ben Azai b\u8217'shukei Tveria}, I am ready to take on any question as Ben Azai was in the marketplaces of Tiberias \u8212- is challenged by Rabbah bar Abba with the following problem: {\i shenei chalalim zeh al gabei zeh} \u8212- two corpses, one lying on top of the other. {\i Meihechan hu moded} \u8212- from which one does one measure for the purposes of {\i egla arufa}, and does either, or both, or neither qualify for the ritual?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Min B\u8217'Mino Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question unfolds into multiple possibilities. Does one say {\i min b\u8217'mino havei tamun} \u8212- that being under another corpse counts as buried \u8212- such that the bottom corpse is excluded, while {\i min b\u8217'mino lavei tzaf} \u8212- being on top of another corpse does not count as suspended \u8212- leaving the top corpse as the qualified one, and one measures from it? Or conversely, {\i min b\u8217'mino havei tzaf} \u8212- the top one is considered suspended \u8212- but {\i min b\u8217'mino lavei tamun} \u8212- the bottom one is not considered buried \u8212- leaving the bottom as the qualified one? Or {\i min b\u8217'mino havei tamun u\u8217'min b\u8217'mino havei tzaf} \u8212- both determinations apply \u8212- leaving neither corpse qualifying, and no {\i egla arufa} is performed at all? Rashi adds the implicit fourth possibility: that {\i min b\u8217'mino} does not count as either buried or suspended, and both corpses qualify independently, potentially each triggering an obligation tied to a different nearby city.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds by analogy to the sheaf {\i baraita}: the case of the sheaf placed on top of another sheaf and forgotten, where the bottom is {\i shichcha} and the top is not. At minimum, the {\i tanna kamma} of that {\i baraita} holds that the top sheaf is exempt while the bottom qualifies. This, Abaye suggests, reflects the debate about {\i min b\u8217'mino} that we explored in the {\i shichcha} context. However, the {\i gemara} has already established that the top sheaf\u8217's exemption in that case derives not from its being suspended but from the fact that the carrier had taken physical possession of it \u8212- {\i keivan de\u8217'achzik bah zachah bah} \u8212- and taken possession removes the category of {\i shichcha} entirely, regardless of where the sheaf comes to rest. The question of whether {\i min b\u8217'mino} suspension exempts for {\i egla arufa} therefore remains formally unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exposure & Location (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'Sadeh Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} further specifies {\i ba-sadeh} \u8212- the body must be in a field. The verse {\i ki yimatzei chalal ba\u8217'adamah} is read to exclude a body floating on the surface of water: {\i v\u8217'lo tzaf al penei ha-mayim}. Being on water is not {\i ba-sadeh} and not {\i ba\u8217'adamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar dissents from the collection of technical exclusions. He holds: {\i be-chulan im hayu chalal orfin} \u8212- in all of these scenarios, as long as the body is a {\i chalal} \u8212- killed by a sword \u8212- the {\i egla arufa} obligation applies. He does not accept the technical exemptions based on burial, suspension, or location on water. The {\i gemara} challenges him: surely he agrees that a strangled victim lying on a garbage heap would not trigger {\i egla arufa}, since the requirement of {\i chalal v\u8217'lo chanuk} \u8212- a sword-slain victim, not a strangled one \u8212- is derived from the repeated use of the word {\i chalal} throughout the {\i parasha}, and he accepts that {\i derasha}. If so, he should similarly accept the {\i drashot} derived from {\i ba\u8217'adamah v\u8217'lo tamun be-gal}, {\i nofel v\u8217'lo taluy be-ilan}, and {\i ba-sadeh v\u8217'lo tzaf al gabei mayim}. Rabbi Elazar replies: {\i chalal yeteira k\u8217'tiv} \u8212- the word {\i chalal} is written multiple times in the {\i parasha}, and he therefore gives weight to the {\i derasha} of {\i chalal v\u8217'lo chanuk}. The other words \u8212- {\i nofel}, {\i ba\u8217'adamah}, {\i ba-sadeh} \u8212- he does not accord the same exegetical significance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Border Town Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the body is found near a border town ({\i samuch le-sefer}) or near a city whose majority population is non-Jewish, {\i egla arufa} is not performed. The source is {\i d\u8217'chtiv ki yimatzei} \u8212- \u8220"if it is found\u8221" \u8212- {\i perat le-matzui}, excluding what is commonly found. Along a border with a hostile neighboring territory, corpses are a frequent occurrence from border conflicts; in a majority non-Jewish city under Roman rule, the unfortunate reality is that murders were not uncommon. In either case, the finding of a body is not a rare event that demands the extraordinary national response of {\i egla arufa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Din Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, if the body is found near a city that has no {\i beis din}, the ritual is not performed \u8212- {\i d\u8217'ba\u8217'ina ziknei ha\u8217'ir v\u8217'leika}, because the verse requires the {\i ziknei ha\u8217'ir} to participate, and without a {\i beis din} there are no {\i zekeinim} of that city in the relevant sense. This leads to the final ruling: {\i ein modedin ela le-ir she-yesh bah beis din} \u8212- one measures only to a city that has a {\i beis din}. The {\i gemara} notes that this might appear obvious given the preceding rule, but it carries an independent teaching: if the closest city lacks a {\i beis din}, one does not simply abandon the entire ritual \u8212- one passes over that city and measures to the next-closest city that does have a {\i beis din}. The {\i talmud lomar} derives this from {\i v\u8217'lakchu ziknei ha-ir ha-hi mi-kol makom} \u8212- the {\i zekeinim} of {\i that} city, wherever it may be relative to the body, take responsibility. The point is not merely topographical proximity but judicial accountability: responsibility must remain anchored to a body capable of bearing it, even if that body is not the geographically nearest settlement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 46\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Equidistant Towns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Body Equidistant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} before us concerns the ritual of {\i egla arufa}, the broken-necked calf performed when an unidentified murder victim is found in an open field. The procedure requires the elders of the {\i beit din hagadol} in Yerushalayim to come out and measure which nearby town \u8212- specifically, which town possessing its own {\i beit din} \u8212- is closest to the corpse, and it is that town\u8217's elders who must carry out the ritual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} addresses a striking edge case: {\i nimtza mechuvon bein shtei ayarot} \u8212- what if the body is found precisely equidistant between two towns?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer: Two Calves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shteihen mevi\u8217'ot shtei agalot divrei Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer rules that each town brings its own calf. The phrasing makes clear that it is not both towns jointly bringing two calves, but each town independently bringing one. It is worth noting that the {\i mishnah} here cites this as {\i divrei Rabbi Eliezer} without recording a dissenting view \u8212- a formulation that clearly implies the ruling was contested, even if the opposing opinion is not spelled out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling might seem strange. After all, there is only one body and, one might think, only one responsible town. One could have suggested that the two {\i batei din} cooperate in bringing a single calf with a {\i tenai} \u8212- a stipulation that if the body is closer to one town, the calf counts as that town\u8217's {\i egla}, and if closer to the other, it counts as the other\u8217's. Alternatively, one might have argued for complete exemption: since the verse specifies {\i ha\u8217'ir hakerova}, the nearby city (in the singular), perhaps when no single city qualifies as definitively nearest, the entire obligation lapses. Rabbi Eliezer rejects both options and requires two calves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precise Measurement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains the reasoning: {\i kasavar efshar l\u8217'tzamtzem} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer holds that precise equidistance is a real and legally cognizable possibility. The position of {\i i\u8217'efshar l\u8217'tzamtzem} would hold that in principle one town must always be fractionally closer, and our inability to measure with sufficient precision merely creates a {\i safek}. On that view, you would indeed bring one calf with a {\i tenai}. But Rabbi Eliezer maintains that {\i efshar l\u8217'tzamtzem} \u8212- genuine equidistance can exist, and when it does, each town bears its own independent obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the concern that the singular language of {\i ha\u8217'ir hakerova} might exempt the case of two towns entirely, the {\i gemara} dismisses this: {\i v\u8217'afillu krovot} \u8212- the word {\i kerova} is not understood as excluding the scenario of multiple equidistant towns. There is no textual basis for such an exclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jerusalem Exempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also states that Yerushalayim does not bring an {\i egla arufa}, even if it happens to be the nearest city. The {\i gemara} explains: {\i de\u8217'amar kra lerishtah} \u8212- the verse ties the obligation to a city given as an inheritance to one of the tribes, and Yerushalayim was never apportioned to any individual tribe. It remained, in a sense, an inter-tribal city, and therefore does not fall within the category of {\i lerishtah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Separated Head\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head vs.\u160 ?Body\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with another unusual case: {\i nimtza rosho b\u8217'makom echad v\u8217'gufo b\u8217'makom acher} \u8212- the head of the corpse is found in one location and the body in another. Rabbi Eliezer rules {\i molichin et harosh etzel haguf} \u8212- you bring the head to the body. Rabbi Akiva rules the opposite: {\i haguf etzel harosh} \u8212- you bring the body to the head.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At first glance this dispute seems to replicate the later dispute at the end of the {\i mishnah} about where one measures from. Rabbi Eliezer, who focuses on the body, says one measures from the belly button; Rabbi Akiva, who focuses on the head, says one measures from the nostrils. The positions appear perfectly parallel. However, the fact that the {\i mishnah} presents them as two separate disputes suggests that the earlier dispute \u8212- about which part of the body you bring to which \u8212- is dealing with a distinct question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Met Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent duplication. Rabbi Yitzchak explains that the first dispute is actually about burial: {\i be\u8217'met mitzvah kanah mekomo} \u8212- a {\i met mitzvah}, a corpse with no one to bury it, acquires its place of rest where it lies. The question when the head and body are separated is therefore: to which location does the body \u8220"belong\u8221" for purposes of burial? Rabbi Eliezer says you bring the head to the body, since the body is buried where it is found. Rabbi Akiva says you bring the body to the head.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head or Body Moved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: what is the underlying disagreement that drives these two rulings? {\i Mar savar gufa b\u8217'duchtei nafal reisha denada venafal} \u8212- one opinion holds that the body fell in place at the spot of the killing, and it was the head that flew off. Therefore the location of the body is the true location of the death, and the head must be brought there. {\i V\u8217'mar savar reisha heicha denafal nafal gufa hu derahit v\u8217'azal} \u8212- the other opinion holds that the head fell right where it was severed, while the body kept moving forward \u8212- as a body sometimes does when suddenly decapitated \u8212- before finally falling at a different spot. On this view, the head\u8217's location is the true locus of death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Measurement Points\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to the second dispute, the {\i mishnah} presents three views on which part of the body to measure from when determining which town is nearest. Rabbi Eliezer says {\i mitiburo} \u8212- from the belly button. Rabbi Akiva says {\i mechotamo} \u8212- from the nostrils. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov says {\i mimakom shena\u8217'asah chalal} \u8212- from the place that made the person a {\i chalal}, a slain corpse \u8212- which he identifies as {\i mitzavaro}, the neck.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Life Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies the underlying question between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Akiva: {\i mar savar ikar chayuta b\u8217'apei} \u8212- one holds that the essential life force of a person resides in the nostrils; {\i v\u8217'mar savar ikar chayuta b\u8217'tibura} \u8212- the other holds it resides in the belly button. This debate also arises in the context of {\i pikuach nefesh} in Yoma, specifically regarding how to determine whether a person trapped under rubble is still alive. Some wish to read this as a dispute about whether life is defined by respiration or by circulation \u8212- though the belly button\u8217's connection to circulation is unclear. It seems more likely that both positions are describing breath, with the belly button understood as relating to the diaphragm and the rhythmic rise and fall of breathing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Development & Life\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} entertains the possibility that this dispute parallels a separate {\i tannaitic} debate: {\i me\u8217'heichan ha\u8217'valad notzar} \u8212- from where does a fetus develop in the womb? One {\i tanna} says from the head; {\i Abba Shaul} says from the belly button, since the belly button is at the center, and development radiates outward in all directions. Each cites the verse {\i mi\u8217'mei immi atah gozi} in support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes, however, that even {\i Abba Shaul} would agree that the locus of {\i chiyuta} \u8212- life force \u8212- is the nostrils. {\i Ad kan lo ka\u8217'amar Abba Shaul ela le\u8217'inyan yetzirah} \u8212- his position concerns {\i yetzirah}, fetal development, not the seat of life itself. {\i Aval le\u8217'inyan chiyuta dechulei alma b\u8217'apeih hu} \u8212- when it comes to the life force, everyone agrees it is in the nostrils, as the verse states: {\i kol asher nishmat ruach chayyim b\u8217'appav} \u8212- all in whose nostrils is the breath of life. The {\i gemara} thus consistently comes down on the side of breath in the nostrils as the defining measure of life \u8212- a point of considerable significance for contemporary halachic discussions surrounding the definition of death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar: Neck\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's position \u8212- that one measures from the neck \u8212- derives from the verse {\i latet otcha al tzavverei challalei resha\u8217'im}, which locates the image of a {\i chalal}, a sword-slain corpse, at the neck. Since the typical {\i chalal} involves decapitation, the neck is the defining anatomical locus of that category of death.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ritual Procedure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elders Depart\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the nearest city has been identified through measurement, {\i niftaru ziknei Yerushalayim v\u8217'halchu lahem} \u8212- the elders of Yerushalayim depart. The ritual itself is now the responsibility of the local elders of the identified town.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Calf Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ziknei otah ha\u8217'ir mevi\u8217'in eglat bakar} \u8212- the elders of that town bring a calf. The calf must be {\i asher lo mashcha b\u8217'ol} \u8212- one that has not drawn a yoke, meaning it must never have been used for labor. Notably, however, {\i v\u8217'ein ha\u8217'mum posel bah} \u8212- a physical blemish does not invalidate it. The presence or absence of a {\i mum} is irrelevant; only prior {\i avoda}, labor, disqualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wadi Eitan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'moridin otah l\u8217'nachal eitan} \u8212- they bring it down to a {\i nachal eitan}, a hard wadi. {\i Eitan k\u8217'mashma\u8217'o kasheh} \u8212- the word {\i eitan} in its plain sense means hard, referring to bedrock. Nevertheless, {\i af al pi she\u8217'eino eitan kasher} \u8212- even if the wadi is not actually of hard bedrock, it remains valid after the fact; the requirement of hard rock is only {\i lechatchilah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Breaking the Neck\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'orfin otah b\u8217'kofitz me\u8217'achoreha} \u8212- they break its neck from behind using a hatchet. This is done from behind the neck, not the front.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ground Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'mekomo assur milizro\u8217'a umila\u8217'avod le\u8217'olam} \u8212- the location where the ritual was performed is permanently forbidden for planting and agricultural work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Uses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'mutar lisrok sham pishtan ul\u8217'naker sham avanim} \u8212- it is, however, permitted to comb flax there or to clear stones from the area, since these activities do not involve working the soil itself. The prohibition falls specifically on uses that engage the ground; standing or sitting on the location, or conducting activities that merely take place upon it without making use of the earth, remain permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Declaration & Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand Washing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ziknei oto ir rochtzim et yedeihem b\u8217'mayim b\u8217'makom arifah} \u8212- the elders of the town wash their hands with water at the place where the neck was broken, over the calf. There is something visually striking about this: on the one hand, the washing of hands in a ritual context conveys assumption of responsibility, a cleansing of moral contamination; on the other hand, it calls to mind the idiom of \u8220"washing one\u8217's hands of\u8221" a matter, of disavowal. Perhaps both ideas are present \u8212- that there was a kind of defilement, a residual moral burden, that must be actively addressed and cleansed through the ritual act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Declarations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following the hand washing, the elders declare: {\i yadeinu lo shafchu et hadam hazeh v\u8217'eineinu lo ra\u8217'u} \u8212- our hands did not shed this blood and our eyes did not see.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: surely no one suspected the {\i beit din} of murder \u8212- why must they declare that their hands did not spill the blood? The answer: {\i ela shelo ba leyadenu upatarnuhu belo mezonot velo ra\u8217'inuhu v\u8217'hinachnuhu belo levayah} \u8212- the declaration is not a denial of having committed murder, but an assertion of having fulfilled their communal obligations. They are affirming that this traveler did not come to them seeking help and get turned away without food; that they did not see him passing through and fail to accompany him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable and demanding standard. The declaration does not merely address the question of direct culpability; it addresses the question of whether the community exercised sufficient care for the stranger in their midst. If a man had passed through town asking for shelter or assistance, and the {\i beit din} was aware of him and did nothing, they would be unable to make this declaration. The ritual thus encodes a positive communal responsibility for the welfare of travelers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priestly Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'hakohanim omrim kaper l\u8217'amcha Yisrael asher padita Hashem v\u8217'al titein dam naki b\u8217'kerev amcha Yisrael} \u8212- the {\i kohanim} then recite the prayer: atone for Your people Israel whom You have redeemed, God, and do not place the guilt of innocent blood in the midst of Your people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Atonement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} notes that the final clause of the {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'nichpar lahem hadam}, and the blood shall be atoned for them \u8212- is not part of the {\i kohanim}\u8217's declaration. Rather, {\i ela ruach hakodesh mevasertam} \u8212- the divine spirit announces it to them as a promise: {\i eimatai sheta\u8217'asu kachah hadam yitkaper lahem} \u8212- when you perform this ritual, atonement for the blood will follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a striking question about the theological structure of the {\i egla arufa} ritual. The {\i mishnah} earlier noted that the calf is killed by breaking its neck from behind \u8212- not by {\i shechitah} from the front, as in a {\i korban}. Why? The deliberate departure from the form of sacrifice signals that this is not a {\i korban} in the conventional sense. Yet the ritual does involve the shedding of blood in a context of atonement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One way to understand this is that the {\i egla arufa} functions not as a sacrifice but as a substitute execution. The verse states {\i ki la\u8217'aretz lo yechupar ladam asher shupach bah ki im bedam shofcho} \u8212- the only true atonement for innocent blood is the execution of the murderer. Since the murderer is unknown and cannot be executed, the calf is slain in his stead. The blood being atoned for is the victim\u8217's innocent blood; the calf\u8217's blood is not what atones \u8212- rather, it is the act of execution, performed upon an innocent creature in place of the guilty one, that achieves atonement. This may be precisely why {\i avoda}, prior labor, disqualifies the calf: not only must it be young and never have borne offspring, it must also be wholly untarnished by human use \u8212- innocent, in the fullest sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mum & Avoda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blemish Permitted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that a {\i mum} does not disqualify the {\i egla} invites scrutiny. One might have expected the opposite conclusion based on a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} from {\i parah adumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal VeChomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} constructs the argument: {\i u\u8217'mah parah she\u8217'ein ha\u8217'shanim posel bah mum posel bah} \u8212- by a {\i parah adumah} the age of the animal is irrelevant, and yet a {\i mum} disqualifies it. {\i Eglah she\u8217'shanim posel bah} \u8212- an {\i eglah} must be young (the word {\i eglah} itself implies a young animal), so age is a disqualifying factor. If even {\i parah adumah}, where age is not an issue, requires the absence of a {\i mum}, surely an {\i eglah}, which is already stricter with respect to age, should require the absence of a {\i mum} as well. The {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} seems compelling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mum vs.\u160 ?Avoda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects it: {\i shani hatam de\u8217'amar kra asher ein bah mum} \u8212- by {\i parah adumah}, the Torah explicitly specifies {\i asher ein bah mum}, that there be no blemish in it. The word {\i bah} \u8212- \u8220"in it\u8221" \u8212- limits the requirement to {\i parah adumah} specifically. The absence of a {\i mum} is a requirement {\i bah}, in the {\i parah}, and need not be extended elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this immediately generates a counter-argument: if the word {\i bah} by {\i parah adumah} restricts the {\i mum} requirement to {\i parah} alone and prevents us from extending it to {\i eglah}, then by the same logic the word {\i bah} by {\i eglah} \u8212- which says {\i asher lo uvad bah}, that it was not worked \u8212- should restrict the {\i avoda} disqualification to {\i eglah} alone and prevent us from extending it to {\i parah adumah}. Yet we clearly do apply the {\i avoda} requirement to {\i parah adumah} (as seen from the ruling of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav discussed below). How can we apply {\i avoda} from {\i eglah} to {\i parah} while simultaneously refusing to apply {\i mum} from {\i parah} to {\i eglah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avoda & Kodshim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is elegant and surprising. The {\i gemara} states: {\i hahi miba\u8217'ei lei l\u8217'mi\u8217'utei kodshim d\u8217'lo pasla behu avoda} \u8212- the word {\i bah} by {\i eglah} is not being used to exclude {\i parah adumah} from the {\i avoda} requirement. Rather, it is being used to exclude {\i kodshim} \u8212- sacrificial animals \u8212- from the {\i avoda} requirement. Remarkably, prior labor does not disqualify a {\i korban}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is counterintuitive. One would naturally assume that {\i kodshim} are subject to at least as stringent requirements as {\i eglah} and {\i parah adumah}. But the {\i gemara} holds the opposite: the disqualification of {\i avoda} is specific to these two non-{\i korban} contexts and does not apply to consecrated offerings. An animal that has been used for agricultural labor can still be brought as a {\i korban}, as long as it otherwise meets the required criteria.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i bah} by {\i parah adumah} therefore functions as follows: it restricts the {\i mum} requirement to {\i parah adumah}, excluding {\i eglah} (which indeed has no {\i mum} requirement). The {\i bah} by {\i eglah} restricts the {\i avoda} requirement to {\i eglah}, excluding not {\i parah} but {\i kodshim}. The two {\i bah}s are doing different limiting work and are not in tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: is this really where we learn that {\i avoda} does not disqualify {\i kodshim}? Isn\u8217't that derived from the verse {\i iver o shavur o charutz o yavelet o garav o yalefet lo takrivu eleh la-Hashem} \u8212- a list of blemishes that disqualify offerings? {\i Eleh atah makriv} \u8212- only these disqualify, but an animal that was used for labor you may bring. So we already know {\i avoda} is not a problem for {\i kodshim} from this verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds that this verse alone would not have been sufficient. Without the {\i bah} by {\i eglah}, one might have thought the {\i eleh} verse only exempts {\i avodat heter} \u8212- labor performed when it was still ordinary, unconsecrated property \u8212- but {\i avodat issur}, labor performed in a prohibited manner (such as being yoked in a forbidden mixture), might still create a metaphysical defect. The {\i bah} teaches that even {\i avodat issur} does not disqualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second verse \u8212- {\i u\u8217'mi\u8217'yad ben nechar lo takrivu et lechem Eloheikhem mi\u8217'kol eleh} \u8212- also teaches the exemption. But even two verses would not have been enough: one might have thought the exemption applies only when the labor was performed while the animal was still {\i chullin}, unconsecrated property, but not when the labor was performed after the animal was already consecrated as {\i kadosh}. The third source \u8212- the {\i bah} by {\i eglah} \u8212- teaches that even labor performed on an animal after it was already consecrated as a {\i korban} does not disqualify it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable ruling. Even if one violated the sanctity of the {\i korban} by using it for labor after it had been designated, the offering remains valid. It may well be that the person incurred {\i me\u8217'ilah} \u8212- misappropriation of consecrated property \u8212- but the {\i korban} itself retains its halachic status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall picture is therefore illuminating: {\i eglah} has no {\i mum} requirement but does have an {\i avoda} requirement. {\i Korbanot} have a {\i mum} requirement but no {\i avoda} requirement. {\i Parah adumah} has both \u8212- and in this sense stands in the middle, sharing characteristics with both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bundle of Sacks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to examine the statement of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav: {\i ha\u8217'maniach chavilah shel sakin al ha\u8217'parah} \u8212- if one placed a bundle of sacks upon the {\i parah adumah}, it is invalid. This is striking because a bundle of sacks is not an {\i ol}, a yoke \u8212- yet it disqualifies. Meanwhile, the rule by {\i eglah} is {\i u\u8217've\u8217'eglah ad she\u8217'timshoch} \u8212- by an {\i eglah} the animal must actually draw the load, actually walk with it, for the {\i avoda} to be operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why the asymmetry? Why is {\i parah adumah} disqualified merely by having a burden placed upon it, while {\i eglah} requires the animal to actually move?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in the different legal sources for the two rulings. By {\i eglah}, the verse reads {\i asher lo mashcha b\u8217'ol} \u8212- which did not draw with a yoke. The term {\i mashcha}, drawing, connotes actual movement. And by {\i parah adumah}, the verse reads {\i asher lo alah aleha ol} \u8212- upon which no yoke has gone up. If we were to learn {\i parah} from {\i eglah} via a {\i gezeirah shavah} on the shared word {\i ol} \u8212- {\i ne\u8217'emar kan ol v\u8217'ne\u8217'emar lehalan ol} \u8212- then we would expect {\i parah} to also require actual movement, {\i meshichah}, just as {\i eglah} does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confirms this logic: {\i mah lehalan ad she\u8217'timshoch af kan ad she\u8217'timshoch} \u8212- if the source is {\i eglah}, the threshold should be the same. Rav Yehuda\u8217's ruling that even placing a bundle of sacks without movement disqualifies the {\i parah} must therefore reflect an alternative source that does not derive from {\i eglah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is indeed a {\i tannaitic} dispute: some derive the {\i avoda} disqualification for {\i parah} from {\i eglah} via the {\i gezeirah shavah}, in which case the definition is parallel \u8212- movement is required. Others derive it from the internal language of the {\i parah adumah} verse itself, in which case the conceptual framework is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ol vs.\u160 ?Avoda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the view that derives the rule from the {\i parah} verse itself, the operative phrase is not {\i avoda} at all. The disqualification comes from the words {\i asher lo alah aleha} \u8212- upon which nothing has gone up. The word {\i ol}, yoke, at the end of the phrase then has a specific additional function: {\i ol posel bein be\u8217'sha\u8217'at avoda bein shelo be\u8217'sha\u8217'at avoda} \u8212- a yoke disqualifies whether placed for the purpose of work or not. The mere presence of a yoke upon the {\i parah} is disqualifying, regardless of intent or use. For all other burdens, however, they disqualify only {\i be\u8217'sha\u8217'at avoda} \u8212- in the context of actual labor use. Rashi explains this to mean not that the animal must have walked, but that the burden must have been placed upon it for the purpose of work, not merely set down in passing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the conceptual framework for {\i parah adumah} is not fundamentally about {\i avoda} at all \u8212- it is about {\i alah aleha}, having anything placed upon it. The significance of the word {\i ol} in the verse is specifically to inform us that a yoke, even placed without any intent to use it for work, is immediately disqualifying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefach Movement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, for {\i eglah}, where the standard is {\i meshichah} \u8212- actual drawing \u8212- the {\i gemara} records a tradition from Rabbi Yochanan as transmitted by Rabbi Yaakov: {\i meshichat ol l\u8217'rochbo tefach} \u8212- the drawing of the yoke requires the animal to have moved the width of a yoke, which is a {\i tefach}. Rabbi Abbahu had posed the question of whether the relevant dimension was the length or the width of the yoke; Rabbi Yaakov transmitted that Rabbi Yochanan had specified the width, which is a {\i tefach}. This formulation also conveys a practical corollary for commercial purposes: a yoke that is less than a {\i tefach} in width does not qualify as a yoke at all and could be rejected in a sale transaction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Theology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Never Produced\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Shaul offers a theological reading of why the Torah requires both a calf and a wadi: {\i mipnei mah amra Torah hevi eglah b\u8217'nachal} \u8212- why did the Torah say to bring a calf down to the wadi? {\i Amar ha\u8217'Kadosh Baruch Hu yavo davar shelo asah peirot} \u8212- the Holy One said: let come something that has never produced fruit. The calf, young and never used for labor, is an animal that has produced nothing; the wadi, hard and barren, is a place that produces nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wasted Potential\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ye\u8217'aref bimkom she\u8217'ein oseh peirot vichaper al mi shelo hinicho la\u8217'asot peirot} \u8212- and let it be killed in a place that does not produce anything, to atone for one who was not permitted to produce fruit. The victim \u8212- murdered, perhaps in youth \u8212- was a person whose potential was cut off before it could be realized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit = Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai peiros} \u8212- what do we mean by \u8220"fruit\u8221" in this context? If it means {\i priyah v\u8217'rivyah}, children, then one would not perform {\i egla arufa} for an elderly man or a sterile person who could not have had children regardless \u8212- which is surely not the case. Rather, {\i ela mitzvot} \u8212- \u8220"fruit\u8221" means deeds, the good acts and {\i mitzvot} one might have performed in a lifetime. Every murder is an assault not merely on a life but on all the potential {\i mitzvot} that life would have generated. The calf and the wadi together form a symbolic tableau of unrealized potential, offering atonement for a world diminished by the act of violence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ancient Ground\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also explores the meaning of {\i nachal eitan}. One opinion derives from verses that {\i eitan} denotes hardness \u8212- a wadi of hard, rocky ground. Another opinion, {\i acherim omrim}, holds that {\i eitan} means ancient: {\i nachal eitan} is a wadi whose soil has never been disturbed, ground that goes back to primordial times, ground that carries with it no history of human cultivation. The two readings are not necessarily in conflict; Rashi reads {\i eitan} as ground that has never been dug up, which combines both the sense of hardness and antiquity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ground Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 From Behind\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'orfin otah b\u8217'kofitz me\u8217'achoreha} \u8212- the neck is broken from behind with a hatchet. The {\i gemara} derives this through {\i gezeirah shavah}: {\i gamar arifa arifa me\u8217'chatat ha\u8217'of} \u8212- the word {\i arifa} here is linked to the word {\i arifa} used in the context of {\i chatat ha\u8217'of}, the bird sin-offering, where the priest pinches the neck from behind. Just as there the action is performed from the nape of the neck, so here too the breaking of the neck is from behind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Past vs.\u160 ?Future\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse states {\i asher lo ye\u8217'aved bah v\u8217'lo yizare\u8217'a} regarding the site of the {\i egla arufa}. A {\i tannaitic} dispute addresses whether this language refers to the past or the future. Rabbi Yoshiya reads it as describing the past: the wadi must be one that has not previously been worked or planted. Rabbi Yonatan reads it as prescribing the future: henceforth, this ground may not be worked or planted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava clarifies: {\i lehaba d\u8217'chulei alma lo peligi} \u8212- on the future prohibition, there is no dispute. {\i V\u8217'lo yizare\u8217'a} means \u8220"shall not be seeded\u8221" \u8212- this is universally accepted as a prospective prohibition. The debate is only about {\i leshe\u8217'avar}, whether the verse additionally specifies that the ground must have been previously unworked. Rabbi Yoshiya argues that {\i asher} introduces a description of a past state; Rabbi Yonatan argues that had the Torah meant the past it would have said {\i ne\u8217'evad}, and the form used implies future obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Soil Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} extends the scope of the future prohibition. The verse says {\i asher lo ye\u8217'aved bo} \u8212- it shall not be worked \u8212- and {\i lo yizare\u8217'a} \u8212- and it shall not be planted. From the combination, the {\i braita} derives: {\i ein li ela zri\u8217'ah, she\u8217'ar avodot minayin} \u8212- I know only that planting is forbidden; how do I know all other forms of soil work are also forbidden? {\i Talmud lomar asher lo ye\u8217'aved bo mikol makom} \u8212- the phrase \u8220"shall not be worked\u8221" encompasses all soil use. The specific mention of planting then teaches the principle: {\i mah zri\u8217'ah meyuchedet she\u8217'hi b\u8217'gufah shel karka, af kol she\u8217'hi b\u8217'gufah shel karka assur} \u8212- just as planting is distinctive in that it engages the body of the earth itself, so anything that engages the body of the earth is forbidden. {\i Yatza srikat pishtan v\u8217'nikur avanim} \u8212- excluded are combing flax and clearing stones, which take place upon the ground without working the earth itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Accompaniment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Declaration Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the elders\u8217' declaration: {\i lo ba leyadenu upatarnuhu belo mezonot v\u8217'lo ra\u8217'inuhu v\u8217'hinachnuhu belo levayah} \u8212- the stranger did not come before us such that we dismissed him without food, and we did not see him pass through and leave him without accompaniment. The declaration contains within it two distinct communal obligations: the provision of sustenance ({\i mezonot}) and {\i levayah}, accompaniment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir: Enforcement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hachi darish Rabbi Meir omer kofin l\u8217'levayah} \u8212- Rabbi Meir taught: we coerce accompaniment. The obligation of {\i levayah} is not merely a matter of good character; it is of such fundamental importance that it may be enforced. {\i She\u8217'sechar ha\u8217'levayah ein lo shiur} \u8212- the reward for accompanying one\u8217's fellow has no measure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 City of Luz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir derives this from the story in {\i Shoftim} involving the city of Luz. When the Israelites were conquering the land, watchmen saw a man leaving a city. {\i Vayomru lo hareinu na et mevo ha\u8217'ir} \u8212- they asked him to show them the entrance to the city. {\i Ve\u8217'asinu imach chesed} \u8212- and we will deal kindly with you. He showed them, and while the entire city was put to the sword, {\i v\u8217'oto ha\u8217'ish umishpachto shilechu} \u8212- that man and his family were sent free. He went to the land of the Hittites, built a new city, and named it Luz \u8212- and the {\i gemara} identifies this as the Luz of later tradition, a city that dyed {\i techelet}, survived the campaigns of Sancheriv and Nevuchadnetzar undisturbed, and into which the Angel of Death has no entry. When its elders finally tire of life, they must leave the city walls in order to die.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an astonishing reward. The man had arguably betrayed his own people \u8212- he was a collaborator with the conquering force. Yet the {\i gemara} reads his act as one of {\i levayah}: he accompanied them toward their destination, showing them the way. The theological message is that the act of accompanying someone on their way carries merit of an almost unbounded kind, even when the context is fraught.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Canaanite Pointer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then intensifies the lesson through a {\i kal v\u8217'chomer}: {\i uma kena\u8217'ani ze shelo diber befiv v\u8217'lo halach beraglav} \u8212- this Canaanite neither spoke aloud nor walked alongside them; he merely indicated the way \u8212- whether with his lips or with his finger, the {\i amora\u8217'im} dispute \u8212- {\i garam hatzalah lo ul\u8217'zar\u8217'o ad sof kol hadoros}. He brought salvation to himself and his descendants until the end of all generations. {\i Mi she\u8217'oseh levayah beraglav} \u8212- one who actually accompanies another on foot \u8212- {\i al achat kama v\u8217'chama} \u8212- how much more so will such a person merit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Accompaniment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi addresses the case of one who travels alone with no one to accompany him: {\i yiasok b\u8217'Torah} \u8212- he should occupy himself with Torah. The verse says {\i ki livyat chen hem l\u8217'roshecha} \u8212- for they are an ornament of grace for your head \u8212- a play on the word {\i levayah}, accompaniment. Torah accompanies the solitary traveler and affords the protection that human accompaniment would otherwise provide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pharaoh & Abraham\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi further teaches: {\i bishvil arba pesi\u8217'ot sheleivah Paroh l\u8217'Avraham} \u8212- because Pharaoh walked four steps to accompany Avraham, as the verse states {\i vayetzav alav Paroh anashim vayishalchu oto v\u8217'et ishto v\u8217'et kol asher lo} \u8212- he commanded men to escort Avraham and his wife and all his possessions \u8212- {\i nishtabedu b\u8217'vanav arba meot shanah} \u8212- Avraham\u8217's descendants were enslaved for four hundred years, as the verse states {\i va\u8217'avaddum v\u8217'inu otam arba meot shanah}. Four steps of accompaniment yielded four hundred years of dominion. This is a striking and paradoxical formulation: the reward for Pharaoh\u8217's {\i levayah} was the power to subjugate the very family he had honored. The arithmetic \u8212- four steps, four hundred years \u8212- makes the principle vivid and arresting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Amot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav teaches: {\i ha\u8217'malveh et chavero arba amot b\u8217'ir eino nizok} \u8212- one who accompanies a neighbor even four {\i amot} within a city will not be harmed. The protective benefit of {\i levayah} accrues to the one who accompanies. Beyond the formal spiritual merit, there is a practical logic: a traveler accompanied even briefly signals to bystanders that he has a protector and is not unguarded. Walking someone even a short distance out of one\u8217's home communicates care and connection, discouraging those who might otherwise take advantage of a stranger traveling alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina & Rescue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes this section with an illustrative incident: {\i Ravina alveiya l\u8217'Rava bar Yitzchak arba amot b\u8217'ir} \u8212- Ravina accompanied Rava bar Yitzchak four {\i amot} in the city. {\i Mata lideih hezika v\u8217'ishtaziv} \u8212- he subsequently encountered something that would have caused him injury, and he was saved from it. The protective promise of {\i levayah} materialized in a concrete and immediate way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 47\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Levayah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 HaRav LeTalmid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins on 46b, approximately twenty lines from the bottom, with a {\i baraita}: {\i tanu rabanan}, {\i harav letalmid} \u8212- even a rabbi who is hosting a student bears an obligation to accompany that student out of the city upon his departure. This is the {\i mitzvah} of {\i levayah}, rooted in the declaration of the {\i sanhedrin} in the {\i eglah arufah} ceremony: \u8220"our hands did not spill this blood\u8221" \u8212- meaning, we were not negligent in accompanying him out of the city. The {\i gemara} has been discussing this in the context of what declarations must be made in Hebrew, and now turns to the practical parameters of {\i levayah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distances: Ibburah, Tech\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How far must one accompany? The {\i baraita} specifies: {\i harav letalmid}, a rabbi accompanying a student, must go {\i ad ibburah shel ir} \u8212- until the outer houses of the city, the so-called \u8220"pregnancy\u8221" of the city, which corresponds to the measurement used for {\i techum shabbos}. {\i Chaver lechaver}, between equals, the obligation extends {\i ad techum shabbos} \u8212- two thousand {\i amot} beyond the city\u8217's edge, approximately one kilometer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmid LeRav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the direction is reversed \u8212- a {\i talmid} accompanying his {\i rebbe} \u8212- the rule changes significantly. The {\i gemara} states {\i ein lo shi\u8217'ur}, there is no fixed limit. Yet immediately after asserting this, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i ein lo shi\u8217'ur vekama} \u8212- so how much really? Rav Sheshet answers: {\i ad parsah}, until a {\i parsah}, which equals four {\i mil}, roughly four kilometers \u8212- three times the distance of {\i techum shabbos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe vs.\u160 ?Rabbo Muvhak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This limit of a {\i parsah} applies, however, only with respect to a {\i rebbe} who is not one\u8217's primary teacher. {\i Aval rabbo muvhak} \u8212- but for one\u8217's primary and most formative teacher \u8212- the obligation extends to {\i shalosh parsaot}, three {\i parsaot}, or twelve kilometers, approximately seven miles. This is a substantial distance, reflecting the depth of the relationship between a student and the teacher who shaped him most profoundly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Accompaniment Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kahana & Tinisa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} illustrates these principles with two brief accounts of {\i talmidim} who accompanied their {\i rebbes}. Rav Kahana would accompany Rav Shimi bar Ashi from Pum Nahara all the way to Bei Tinisa of Bavel \u8212- a location known for its distinctive groves of palm trees.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adam HaRishon Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Upon arriving at Bei Tinisa, Rav Shimi bar Ashi remarked that people say these palm trees of Bavel go back to the days of {\i Adam HaRishon}. Rav Kahana responded: {\i idkeran milta} \u8212- you have reminded me of a Torah teaching. He then cited Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chanina on the verse, {\i be\u8217'eretz lo avar bah ish velo yashav adam sham} \u8212- in a land that no person passed through and no {\i adam} ever settled. The question is a classic one: if no one passed through, it is obvious that no one settled \u8212- why repeat the point? The answer reads {\i adam} literally: only the land that {\i Adam HaRishon} decreed for human settlement was ever settled. The regions he did not designate remained uninhabited throughout history. Thus the two terms are not redundant; one speaks of ordinary travelers and the other of the primordial decree of Adam. The story illustrates how even the act of accompanying a {\i rebbe} \u8212- what might seem like mere travel \u8212- becomes an occasion for {\i divrei Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Mordechai Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, Rav Mordechai would accompany Rav Ashi from Hagronya to Bei Kifei \u8212- and some say all the way to Bei Dura.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shofech Damim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan said in the name of Rabbi Meir: {\i kol she-eino melaveh u-mitlaveh ke\u8217'ilu shofech damim} \u8212- anyone who neither accompanies nor is accompanied is as though he spilled blood. The inclusion of {\i mitlaveh} \u8212- the one being accompanied \u8212- is noteworthy: it implies that even the person refusing accompaniment bears responsibility if harm results. The connection to {\i eglah arufah} is direct: the failure to accompany leaves a person vulnerable, exposed to attack, and the community becomes implicated in whatever violence follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elisha & Bears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now invokes a biblical narrative to drive this point home. {\i She\u8217'ilmalei livuhu anshei Yericho l\u8217'Elisha lo girah dubim batinokot} \u8212- had only the people of Yericho accompanied Elisha, he would not have sent the bears upon the young men. The verse (II Kings 2) recounts that Elisha traveled alone from Yericho to Beit El, at which point a group of young men emerged from the city, mocked him, and called out: {\i alei kerei\u8217'ach alei kerei\u8217'ach} \u8212- \u8220"get out of here, baldy.\u8221" The {\i gemara} explains the word {\i kerei\u8217'ach} \u8212- baldy \u8212- as referring to: {\i shekeirachta lanu et hamakom}, you have stripped this place. These young men had been selling good water to the inhabitants; when Elisha performed a miracle sweetening Yericho\u8217's waters, he destroyed their livelihood. They were, in effect, accusing him of having impoverished them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korach: Baldy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does the word {\i kerei\u8217'ach} \u8212- \u8220"baldy\u8221" \u8212- signify beyond the surface insult? The {\i gemara} connects it to {\i shekeirachta lanu et hamakom}, as just noted. The taunts were not random cruelty but a grievance rooted in their economic loss from Elisha\u8217's miracle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ne\u8217'arim & Ketanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A deeper question surrounds the phrase {\i ne\u8217'arim ketanim} \u8212- literally \u8220"young lads who are small.\u8221" Rabbi Eliezer explains: they are called {\i ne\u8217'arim} because they were {\i menue\u8217'arim min hamitzvot}, emptied of {\i mitzvot} \u8212- people with no serious religious commitment, which is why they could so callously insult the {\i navi}. They are called {\i ketanim} \u8212- small \u8212- because they were {\i m\u8217'katnei emunah}, of diminished faith, for rather than trusting that God would provide for them in other ways after the waters were sweetened, they attacked the man responsible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenged this reading. Perhaps, he suggested, {\i ne\u8217'arim} simply designates their place of origin \u8212- there was a town called {\i Ne\u8217'urin}, and {\i ne\u8217'arim} would mean \u8220"people from Na\u8217'ar,\u8221" not young men at all. He cites support from another verse (II Kings 5) describing a {\i na\u8217'ara ketana} \u8212- a young girl \u8212- captured from the land of Israel by Aram; perhaps there too, {\i na\u8217'ara} means she was from a place called {\i Ne\u8217'urin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: in the verse about the captive girl, her place of origin is not specified, so attributing {\i na\u8217'ara} to a geographical name is plausible. Here, however, the text explicitly states that these young men came from the city \u8212- from Yericho \u8212- {\i hacha mefareish mekumo}. The place is given; {\i ne\u8217'arim} therefore cannot be a geographical designation, and the original reading stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elisha\u8217's Curse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elisha\u8217's Ra\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse continues: {\i vayifen acharav vayar\u8217'em vayakalem beshem Hashem} \u8212- he turned, he saw them, and he cursed them in God\u8217's name. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai ra\u8217'ah} \u8212- what is meant by \u8220"he saw\u8221"? Was it simply that he looked at them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav says: {\i ra\u8217'ah mamash} \u8212- he literally just saw them. Yet even this bare seeing is consequential, as we learn from a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel teaches that whenever the Sages directed their gaze against someone \u8212- {\i kol makom shenasnu chachamim eineihem} \u8212- that person either dies or becomes impoverished. A look from a {\i navi} of this stature carries devastating force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitigating Factors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel offers a different reading: he saw that all of them had been conceived on {\i Yom Kippur} \u8212- their mothers had violated the sanctity of that day. This is the {\i gemara}\u8217's attempt, however tentatively, to explain why such an extreme punishment befell them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha adds another dimension: he saw that they wore their hair in a style called {\i blurei}, associated with the Amorites, suggesting involvement in idolatrous practices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan goes furthest in the prosecutorial direction: he saw that {\i lo hayta bahen lachluchit shel mitzvah} \u8212- there was not even a trace of moisture of {\i mitzvah} in them; they were entirely bereft of religious life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet even this does not fully satisfy. One might still argue: {\i vedilma bezar\u8217'aihu nihavi} \u8212- perhaps from their descendants something good might have come? Rabbi Eliezer answers: he saw that neither from them nor from any of their descendants through all generations would any good emerge \u8212- {\i lo bam velo bezaram ad sof kol hadorot}. The {\i gemara} is not celebrating what Elisha did; it is straining to make some peace with a troubling text by marshaling every possible mitigating consideration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bears & Casualties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vatetzeina shtayim dubim min haya\u8217'ar} \u8212- two bears emerged from the forest and mauled forty-two of the youths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ness v. Ness B\u8217'Toch Ness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A dispute between Rav and Shmuel: one said it was a single miracle; the other said it was a miracle within a miracle. According to the first view, there was a forest but no bears in it \u8212- the bears themselves were miraculous. According to the second, there was neither a forest nor bears \u8212- both were created for the occasion, making it a miracle layered upon a miracle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question: if both forest and bears had to be created miraculously, why create the forest at all? Why not simply produce the bears? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i de\u8217'vi\u8217'isi} \u8212- because the bears would have been afraid to attack without a place of refuge. Even within a miracle, the bears behave like natural bears; they will only charge if they have somewhere to retreat. The forest was necessary to make the miracle function through natural-seeming animal psychology.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arba\u8217'im u\u8217'Shnayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina notes that forty-two children perished \u8212- and this corresponds precisely to the forty-two sacrifices that Balak king of Moab offered in his effort to bring a curse upon Israel through Bilaam. {\i Bishvil arbaim ushnayim korbanot shehikriv Balak melech Moav hufka miYisrael arbaim ushnayim yeladim}. Even Balak\u8217's evil sacrifices had some effect \u8212- not the effect he intended, but they registered sufficiently to ultimately account for these forty-two deaths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shelo Lishma to Lishma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelo Lishma Ba Lishma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a teaching of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav: one should always engage in Torah and {\i mitzvot} even {\i shelo lishma} \u8212- not for the right reason \u8212- because from {\i shelo lishma} one comes eventually to {\i lishma}, acting for the proper motive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balak & Rut Legacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof: {\i bishvil arbaim ushnayim korbanot shehikriv Balak melech Moav zacha veyatzta mimenu Rut} \u8212- in the merit of those forty-two sacrifices that Balak offered, he merited that Rut descended from him. Rav Yosef ben Choni specifies the lineage: Rut was the daughter of Eglon, who was the son of Balak. And from Rut came David and Shlomo \u8212- {\i elef olot ya\u8217'aleh Shlomo}, who brought a thousand burnt offerings to God for the purest of motives. Thus a chain beginning with Balak\u8217's deeply corrupted sacrifices ends with Shlomo\u8217's thousand offerings brought in perfect {\i lishma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ta\u8217'avato La\u8217'Kella\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might object: surely the parallel is strained. Normally we say {\i shelo lishma} means a self-interested but not malicious motive. Balak\u8217's intent was thoroughly evil. Can we say even evil sacrifices lead to good? Moreover, did those evil intentions have no effect at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ta\u8217'avaso miya lekilela hava} \u8212- his desire {\i was} for curse, and that too had its impact. The forty-two deaths attributed to those sacrifices are the evil effect that his evil intention generated. The good that ultimately flowed from it \u8212- Rut, David, Shlomo \u8212- was the {\i shelo lishma} becoming {\i lishma} across generations, but his wicked intention was not without consequence either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yericho & Chen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshav HaIr Tov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now circles back to the story of Elisha\u8217's arrival in Yericho. The people of the city addressed him: {\i hinei na moshav ha\u8217'ir tov ka\u8217'asher adoni ro\u8217'eh} \u8212- behold, the dwelling of the city is pleasant as my master can see. And yet the verse concludes that the waters are bad and the land causes miscarriage. The {\i gemara} presses: if the water is bitter and the land causes {\i shichlul}, what exactly is pleasant about the city? Why would they call it a good dwelling?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chen Makom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanin answers: {\i chen makom al yoshvav} \u8212- a place has a certain charm upon those who dwell there. People who live somewhere develop a fondness for it that is not fully explicable from the outside. The inhabitants of Yericho found their city beautiful despite its obvious deficiencies, because familiarity and belonging produce an attachment that transcends objective assessment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlosha Chinot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan extends this into a general principle: {\i shlosha chinot hein} \u8212- there are three types of {\i chen}, charm, that operate similarly. {\i Chen makom al yoshvav} \u8212- the charm of a place upon its inhabitants. {\i Chen isha al ba\u8217'ala} \u8212- the charm of a woman upon her husband; what an outsider might not appreciate, a husband finds profoundly attractive in his wife, because {\i chen} is relational, not objective. And {\i chen mekach al mikcho} \u8212- the charm of a purchase upon its buyer; a person who has acquired something finds it wonderful in a way that someone who hasn\u8217't bought it may not see. All three share the same logic: intimacy and commitment generate a subjective beauty that is real even if not universally perceptible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elisha\u8217's Illnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlosha Cholaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} teaches: {\i shlosha cholaim chala Elisha} \u8212- Elisha suffered three serious illnesses. The first was punishment for having sent the bears against the children. The second was for having pushed Geichazi away {\i bishtei yadayim} \u8212- with both hands \u8212- giving him only harsh rebuke without subsequently drawing him back. The third was simply the illness from which he ultimately died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does the {\i gemara} derive that there were three separate illnesses? From the verse: {\i ve\u8217'Elisha chala et chalyo asher yamut bo}. The {\i gemara} reads {\i chala}, {\i chalyo}, and {\i asher yamut bo} as three distinct expressions of illness \u8212- three episodes, three occasions of serious sickness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smol Docha Yamin Mekarev\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From Elisha\u8217's error with Geichazi, the {\i gemara} derives a broader principle. {\i Le\u8217'olam tehei smol dochah ve\u8217'yamin mekarevet} \u8212- always let the left hand push away and the right hand draw close. When a person must rebuke and distance someone, the pulling close must ultimately be stronger than the pushing away. The {\i gemara} states explicitly: {\i lo ke\u8217'Elisha shedachafoto l\u8217'Geichazi bishtei yadav} \u8212- not like Elisha, who pushed Geichazi away with both hands. And the printed text says {\i velo ki-Yehoshua ben Perachiah shedachafoto le\u8217'echad mitalmidav bishtei yadav} \u8212- and not like Yehoshua ben Perachiah, who pushed away one of his students with both hands. The original text, which was censored to protect the Talmud from church persecution, names that student as Yeshu HaNotzri. The historical difficulty \u8212- Yehoshua ben Perachiah lived roughly a century before the common era, perhaps seventy years before Jesus \u8212- does not necessarily mean the {\i gemara} did not identify them; it may simply mean the {\i gemara}\u8217's historical chronology does not align with ours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geichazi Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geichazi\u8217's Greed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What precisely was Geichazi\u8217's sin? The {\i gemara} recounts: after Elisha had healed Naaman the Syrian general of his leprosy and explicitly refused any reward \u8212- establishing the principle that one does not take payment for such a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- Geichazi ran after Naaman in secret and took silver and garments in Elisha\u8217's name. When Geichazi returned, Elisha confronted him: {\i lo libi halach ka\u8217'asher hafach ish me\u8217'al merkavto likrasecha} \u8212- was my heart not with you when that man turned from his chariot to greet you? {\i Ha\u8217'eit lakachas et hakesef} \u8212- is now really the moment to accept such gifts?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmonah Sheratzim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the verse mentions not only silver and garments but a long catalogue of goods: olives, vineyards, sheep, cattle, male and female slaves. But in fact Geichazi only took silver and garments \u8212- why does the verse enumerate so much more? Rabbi Yitzchak explains: {\i be\u8217'osa sha\u8217'ah hayah Elisha osek b\u8217'shmonah sheratzim} \u8212- at that very time, Elisha had been studying the laws of the eight {\i sheratzim}, the eight species of rodents that convey {\i tuma}. The eight items mentioned in the verse correspond to those eight categories. Elisha said to Geichazi: you have taken the reward for the {\i Torah} you studied \u8212- the reward you might have received in the World to Come for learning those laws you have now forfeited and will receive in this world in the form of material goods.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzaraas Geichazi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse continues: {\i ve\u8217'tzaraas Naaman tidbak becha u-vezarcha le\u8217'olam} \u8212- the {\i tzaraas} of Naaman shall cleave to you and to your children forever. The {\i gemara} connects this to a later verse: {\i ve\u8217'arba\u8217'ah anashim hayu metzora\u8217'im petach hasha\u8217'ar} \u8212- four men afflicted with {\i tzaraas} sat at the gate. Rabbi Yochanan identifies them as Geichazi and his three sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elisha Seeks Teshuvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A later verse records: {\i vayelech Elisha Damesek} \u8212- Elisha went to Damascus. Why did he go? Rabbi Yochanan answers: {\i shehalach lehachziro l\u8217'Geichazi b\u8217'teshuva} \u8212- he went to bring Geichazi back to {\i teshuvah}. Elisha had apparently come to feel some remorse about how he had handled his student and sought to repair it. He said to Geichazi: {\i chazor becha} \u8212- return, repent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Maspikin B\u8217'Yado\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Geichazi refused. He said: {\i kach mikublani mimcha kol mi shechata vehichti et harabbim ein maspikin b\u8217'yado la\u8217'asot teshuvah} \u8212- this is what I have received as tradition from you: anyone who sinned and caused others to sin does not receive the wherewithal to do {\i teshuvah}. It is not that {\i teshuvah} is metaphysically impossible, but that it becomes practically unattainable \u8212- the door feels permanently shut. One wonders, and the {\i gemara} implicitly raises this, whether teaching this principle so categorically to students is itself a form of pushing away with both hands. Geichazi took the teaching as license to give up entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hichti Es HaRabim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In what way had Geichazi caused others to sin? The {\i gemara} records several traditions. One says he suspended the golden calves of Yerovam ben Nevat with lodestones \u8212- magnets arranged so the calves floated in midair \u8212- creating the illusion that they were genuine deities. Another says he engraved God\u8217's name on the mouth of the golden calves, so that they would supernaturally proclaim {\i Anochi Hashem Elokecha} and {\i Lo yiheyeh lecha}, mimicking the divine voice. Each of these represents an act of profound mass corruption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third tradition points in a slightly different direction: {\i rabbanan dacha mikamei} \u8212- he pushed away scholars from Elisha\u8217's presence, seeking to monopolize access to his master. How is this inferred? After Geichazi was sent away, the {\i bnei nevi\u8217'im} said to Elisha: {\i hinei na hamakom asher anachnu yoshvim sham lefanecha tzar mimenu} \u8212- the place where we sit before you has become too crowded. {\i Michlal de\u8217'ad idna lo hava dachik} \u8212- this implies that until then it had not been crowded, because Geichazi had been keeping people away. His removal opened the doors, and suddenly the place was full. His restricting access to Elisha constituted a form of {\i hichti et harabbim} \u8212- causing many to be deprived of Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader lesson here is sobering. A small wrongdoing, handled with a heavy and unrelenting hand, can drive a person from occasional dishonesty into wholesale heresy and mass spiritual corruption. The consequences of pushing away entirely, without the right hand drawing close, are incalculable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehoshua ben Perachiah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yannai\u8217's Persecution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now tells the parallel story. During the period when King Yannai was killing the Sages and Pharisees, Shimon ben Shetach was protected because his sister had married Yannai and hid him. Yehoshua ben Perachiah, who was the {\i nasi}, had no such protection and fled \u8212- {\i azal arek l\u8217'Alexandria shel Mitzrayim} \u8212- he ran away to Alexandria in Egypt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Return & Inn\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the persecutions subsided, Shimon ben Shetach sent a message to his colleague, composed as a poetic epistle: from me, Jerusalem the Holy City, to you, Alexandria of Egypt \u8212- my sister \u8212- my husband dwells within you and I sit desolate. The message was at once poetic, coded, and emotionally resonant. Yehoshua ben Perachiah understood: {\i shema minah hava lei shlama} \u8212- it must now be peaceful. He set out to return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the journey back, he stopped at an inn. The innkeeper treated him with exceptional honor \u8212- {\i shapir avid lei yekara tuva}, with great and fitting respect. Yehoshua ben Perachiah sat and began praising the situation: {\i kama na\u8217'eh achsanya zo} \u8212- how beautiful is this inn. He meant, evidently, how beautiful is her manner of hosting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Student Rebuked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of his students \u8212- the text preserves this as Yeshu HaNotzri in the original {\i girsa} \u8212- heard this and replied: {\i Rabbi, eineiha trutot} \u8212- her eyes are round, they are not beautiful. The student had interpreted the remark as a comment on the innkeeper\u8217's physical appearance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yehoshua ben Perachiah reacted immediately and harshly: {\i rasha, bekach ata osek} \u8212- wicked one, is this what occupies your mind? He was incensed that his student\u8217's thoughts had gone in that direction. He then took out four hundred {\i shofarot} and placed the student under {\i cherem}, the ban of excommunication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repeated Rejection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Day after day the student returned, seeking to be readmitted. {\i Kol yoma ata lekamei velo kiblei} \u8212- every day he came and was not accepted. Then one day, as Yehoshua ben Perachiah was reciting {\i kri\u8217'at shema}, the student arrived once again. That very day, Yehoshua ben Perachiah had actually reached a change of heart \u8212- {\i hava be-da\u8217'ato lekablei} \u8212- he was prepared to accept him back. But as he was in the middle of {\i kri\u8217'at shema} he could not speak. He gestured with his hand \u8212- {\i achvei lei be-yadei}. The student interpreted the gesture as another rejection: {\i savar midche dachei lei} \u8212- he assumed he was being pushed away yet again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stone Worship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Azal zakfei livneita palcha} \u8212- the student went and erected a stone idol and worshipped it. He had crossed the line from estrangement to {\i avodah zarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Maspikin (Repeated)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Yehoshua ben Perachiah finally came to him and said {\i chazor becha} \u8212- return, repent \u8212- the student gave the same answer Geichazi had given: {\i kach mekublani mimcha, kol hachotei umachti et harabbim ein maspikin b\u8217'yado la\u8217'asot teshuvah} \u8212- this is what I have received as tradition from you: one who sins and causes others to sin is not given the opportunity to repent. The student invoked his teacher\u8217's own principle against his teacher\u8217's attempt at reconciliation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the {\i gemara} confirms: {\i kishef vehisit vehidiach vehichti et Yisrael} \u8212- he practiced sorcery, he seduced, he led astray, and he caused Israel to sin. This is how he had caused others to sin, making {\i teshuvah} practically foreclosed. And it confirms what the {\i gemara} already signaled: the dates in the story do not align historically with what we know of Jesus, yet the {\i gemara} clearly identifies the student as Yeshu HaNotzri and attributes to him the founding of a movement that led vast numbers away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Smol Docha Yamin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeitzer, Tinok, Ishah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the principle of {\i smol dochah ve\u8217'yamin mekarevet}, the {\i gemara} now extends it to other domains of personal life. Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar teaches: {\i yeitzer, tinok, ve\u8217'ishah \u8212- tehei smol dochah ve\u8217'yamin mekarevet} \u8212- with regard to one\u8217's {\i yeitzer} (sexual desire), one\u8217's children, and one\u8217's wife, always let the left hand push away and the right hand draw close.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a distinctively Jewish teaching. The {\i yeitzer} is not to be annihilated \u8212- Judaism does not embrace asceticism \u8212- but moderated. Yet the moderation must always be in the service of embrace rather than rejection. The right hand, which draws close, must be stronger. A man\u8217's relationship with his children \u8212- even when a father must set limits \u8212- should ultimately be defined by closeness and warmth. The same applies to the relationship with one\u8217's wife. The principle is the same throughout: never push away so hard that the right hand cannot draw close again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eglah Arufah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Horeg Ad Lo Neerfah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to the {\i mishnah}. {\i Nimtza ha-horeig ad shelo neerfah ha-eglah} \u8212- if the murderer was discovered before the heifer\u8217's neck was broken, {\i teitzei vetireh ba-eider} \u8212- the heifer returns to the flock and may be used. It does not acquire the status of {\i eglah arufah} and is not forbidden. Rashi notes that there is an opinion in the {\i gemara} that once the heifer descends into the valley even before the neck is broken, it becomes forbidden \u8212- but the ruling here follows the view that it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acharei Neerfah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the neck has already been broken \u8212- {\i misheneerfah ha-eglah tikaver bimkomah} \u8212- it is buried in place and remains permanently forbidden in benefit, even after the murderer is subsequently found. The reason is given in the next subsection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiphrah B\u8217'Safeik\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i She\u8217'al safeik ba\u8217'ah mitchilatah} \u8212- it came originally on account of doubt. The entire purpose of the {\i eglah arufah} ritual is to achieve atonement for the community and the land during the period when no murderer is known and justice cannot be done. {\i Mitchilah kiphrah sefeika vehalchah lah} \u8212- it achieved its intended atonement during that period of uncertainty, and therefore it is not retroactively invalidated by the later discovery of the murderer. The ritual served its function; it may not be undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehareg\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Neerfah ha-eglah ve-achar kach nimtza ha-horeig \u8212- harei zeh yeihareig} \u8212- if the neck was already broken and then the murderer is found, he is nevertheless executed. The {\i eglah arufah} provided communal atonement for the land; it did not substitute for the murderer\u8217's punishment. The verse is explicit: {\i ula-aretz lo yechupar la-dam asher shupach bah ki im be-dam shofcho} \u8212- the land cannot be atoned for spilled blood except through the blood of the one who spilled it. Performing the ritual does not spare the murderer; it only addresses the communal dimension of the crime.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Edim v. Eglah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ed Echad Ra\u8217'iti\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i eid echad omer ra\u8217'iti et ha-horeig} \u8212- if one witness says \u8220"I saw the killer\u8221" \u8212- even though a single witness cannot convict in {\i beit din} \u8212- the {\i eglah arufah} is suspended. This parallels what we saw with {\i sotah}: a single witness testifying to the adultery suspends the {\i sotah} ordeal. The Torah grants {\i ed echad} a form of credibility that prevents proceeding with these rituals even without the formal requirement of two witnesses for conviction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neutralization Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents a series of cases involving competing testimony. One witness says he saw and another says he did not: they neutralize each other and the neck is broken. A woman says she saw and another woman says she did not: the same, they neutralize. One witness says he saw and two say he did not: the two prevail, the neck is broken. Two witnesses say they saw and one says they did not: the two prevail, the neck is not broken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishe-Rabu Rotzchanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishe-rabu ha-rotzchanim batla eglah arufah} \u8212- when murders became rampant in Israel, the {\i eglah arufah} ritual was discontinued. The ritual presupposes a society where murder is an aberration that shocks the community into communal soul-searching. When murder had become routine \u8212- particularly associated with the period of the {\i sicarri} and the final decades of the Second Temple \u8212- the ritual could no longer carry its intended meaning. The {\i mishnah} mentions that this shift is associated with the rise of {\i Elazar ben Dinai}, who was originally known as {\i Tchina ben Perisha} and was later renamed {\i ben ha-ratzchan} \u8212- \u8220"son of the murderer.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Batlu HaMitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishe-Rabu Menafim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishe-rabu ha-menafim pasku ha-mayim ha-marim} \u8212- when adulterers became numerous, the bitter waters of {\i sotah} ceased to function. The {\i sotah} waters test a wife whose husband suspects her. But the waters only worked when the husband himself was innocent of adultery. Once men were routinely committing adultery, the waters would no longer have the power to check their wives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan ben Zakkai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai hifsikan} \u8212- it was Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai who formally discontinued the {\i sotah} waters, citing the verse: {\i lo efkod al benoteichem ki tizneina ve\u8217'al kaloteichem ki tinafna} \u8212- I will not punish your daughters when they fornicate, nor your daughters-in-law when they commit adultery. When the men of the generation are themselves guilty, the divine mechanism of the {\i sotah} waters is suspended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishe-Meit Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishe-meit Yosi ben Yoezer ish Tsereidah ve-Yosi ben Yehudah ish Yerushalayim batlu ha-eshkolot} \u8212- when Yosi ben Yoezer and Yosi ben Yehudah died \u8212- the earliest {\i zugot} recorded in {\i Pirkei Avot} \u8212- the {\i eshkolot} ceased. {\i Eshkol} means a cluster of grapes; the {\i gemara} uses it to describe Torah scholars who were complete, containing within themselves the totality of the tradition. With their deaths, gaps began to appear in the transmission. Debates multiplied because the tradition was no longer perfectly intact from teacher to student, and subsequent generations were simply not at the same level of comprehensive mastery. The verse cited is {\i ein eshkol le-echol bikurah ivta nafshi} \u8212- there is no longer a cluster to eat, no one of that completeness to be found.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Kohen Gadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yochanan Kohen Gadol instituted several reforms. He discontinued the {\i viduy ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- the declaration that the Torah requires be made every third year confirming that one has properly distributed all {\i ma\u8217'aser} from one\u8217's home. He also abolished the {\i me\u8217'orerim} and the {\i nokfim} \u8212- practices associated with the Temple which the {\i gemara} will explain further. He stopped the practice of metalworkers hammering and smelting in Jerusalem during {\i Chol HaMoed}, as the constant noise of work undermined the spirit of the festival and created a false impression for the populace that labor during {\i Chol HaMoed} was entirely permitted. And in his time, {\i ein tzarich lish\u8217'ol al demai} \u8212- one no longer had to inquire about {\i demai} produce, because he instituted the systematic taking of {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot} from doubtfully-tithed produce, rendering the question practically resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Yichupuhr\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now analyzes the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rulings systematically. First, on the question of how we know that finding the murderer after the {\i eglah arufah} does not exempt him: {\i talmud lomar ula-aretz lo yechupar la-dam asher shupach bah ki im be-dam shofcho} \u8212- the land requires the blood of the murderer for atonement; the {\i eglah arufah} atones for the communal dimension but cannot replace the execution of the murderer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ed Echad Ne\u8217'eman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the rule that a single witness saying \u8220"I saw\u8221" suspends the {\i eglah arufah} ritual: {\i ta\u8217'ama demachish leh ha lo machish leh ed echad ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- the reason that when one contradicts him they neutralize is that when the first witness stands uncontradicted, one witness is believed. {\i Minani mili} \u8212- from where do we derive this? From the verse {\i lo noda mi hikahu} \u8212- the ritual is only done when it is not known who the killer was. {\i Ha noda afilu echad besof ha\u8217'olam lo hayu orfin} \u8212- if it is known at all, even through a single witness anywhere, the neck is not broken. This is the basis for {\i ed echad} being believed in this context. The {\i gemara} also cites Rabbi Akiva: from the elders\u8217' declaration {\i ve\u8217'eineinu lo rau} \u8212- our eyes did not see it \u8212- we derive that if the {\i sanhedrin} itself had witnessed the murder without identifying the killer, they too would not perform the {\i eglah arufah}, even without a named individual witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kashya on Neutralization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a difficulty. If one witness is believed \u8212- and Ulla\u8217's principle is that {\i kol makom she-heimina Torah ed echad harei kan shnayim} (wherever the Torah believes one witness, that witness counts as two) \u8212- then how can a subsequent single witness come and neutralize him? The one who said he saw has the weight of two; the one who says he did not see has only the weight of one. They should not cancel each other out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Girsa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla therefore emends the {\i mishnah}: {\i teni lo hayu orfin} \u8212- read the {\i mishnah} as \u8220"they would not break the neck\u8221" even in the case of one against one. The first witness, having been believed and having the weight of two witnesses, cannot be neutralized by a second who merely contradicts him. Rabbi Yitzchak agrees and makes the same emendation. The {\i mishnah} should be read: one against one \u8212- they do not break the neck, the first witness\u8217's testimony stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Garsot HaMishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya, however, maintains the standard reading of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i hayu orfin}, one against one they do neutralize and the neck is broken. How does he resolve Ulla\u8217's principle?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Achat v. Zeh Achar Z\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i lo kashya, kan be-vat achat, kan be-zeh achar zeh} \u8212- there is no contradiction. It depends on timing. When both witnesses testify simultaneously \u8212- or at least before the first has been formally accepted \u8212- they neutralize each other, and the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling of {\i hayu orfin} applies. When the first witness testified and was already accepted and treated as credible, only then does Ulla\u8217's principle apply: the second witness cannot retroactively neutralize the first. Rashi understands simultaneity as {\i toch kidei dibbur}; Tosafot understand it more broadly as any testimony that occurred before the {\i beit din} had formally acted on the first witness\u8217's account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Complications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents cases in both directions: one witness against two, and two witnesses against one \u8212- with different outcomes depending on which side has the majority. But the {\i mishnah} never explicitly states the baseline case of one against one. This creates an inferential problem, since each of the two cases involving two against one seems to imply something contradictory about what happens in the intermediate case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psulei Edut Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction by observing: {\i ella matnitin kula bi-psulei edut} \u8212- the {\i mishnah}\u8217's two-against-one cases are addressing witnesses who are {\i psulei edut}, disqualified witnesses. This connects to the principle of Rabbi Nechemya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Nechemya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nechemya teaches: {\i kol makom she-heimina Torah ed echad halech achar rov deot} \u8212- wherever the Torah believed one witness with special standing, when we move outside the realm of that specially-believed witness and deal instead with {\i psulei edut}, we follow a simple numbers game \u8212- majority of opinions. {\i Ve\u8217'asu shtei nashim be-ish echad ki-shnei anashim be-ish echad} \u8212- two women count against one man as two men count against one man. Within the domain of {\i psulei edut}, the arithmetic applies straightforwardly: two beats one regardless of gender.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shnei Nashim v. Ish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative reading, however, maintains: {\i kol heicha de-ata ed echad me-ikara afilu meah nashim ke-chad dami} \u8212- if a {\i kasher} male witness testified first, even a hundred women subsequently cannot neutralize him, because his testimony has the full weight of two {\i kasher} witnesses. In that reading, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's cases of two against one must involve a {\i pasul} as the initial witness, so that the two {\i psulot} can effectively neutralize.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mention Zwei?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the principle is simply that two {\i psulei edut} neutralize one, why does the {\i mishnah} need to state both directions \u8212- two saying they saw against one saying they didn\u8217't, and two saying they didn\u8217't against one saying they did? {\i Mahu de-teima ki azlinan batar rov deot le-chumra aval le-kula lo} \u8212- one might have thought that the majority rule among {\i psulei edut} is applied only stringently \u8212- that is, only in the direction that prevents breaking the neck \u8212- but not leniently, to permit breaking the neck when two {\i psulei edut} say the murderer was not seen. Therefore the {\i mishnah} states both cases explicitly to teach that the numbers game applies in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's ultimate position can be summarized as follows. When a single {\i kasher} witness testifies that he saw the killer, he is believed to the extent that the {\i eglah arufah} ritual is suspended; this parallels the {\i ed echad} who is believed regarding {\i sotah}. If that witness testifies first and is accepted, a subsequent single witness cannot neutralize him. If they testify simultaneously, or before formal acceptance of the first, they neutralize. Two {\i kasher} witnesses subsequently can neutralize one {\i kasher} witness. When the witnesses involved are {\i psulei edut}, the rule follows simple numerical majority in either direction. Whether two {\i psulei edut} can neutralize a single {\i kasher} male witness who testified first depends on the dispute between the readings of Rabbi Nechemya \u8212- some holding that two women cannot overcome the specially-believed man, others holding that they can, provided the first witness was not of that specially-elevated status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 48\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Egla Arufa & Sota\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egla Arufa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a {\i mishnah} establishing that when murderers increased, the practice of {\i egla arufa} \u8212- the beheaded heifer brought in cases of an unsolved murder \u8212- was discontinued. The {\i Tanna} explains the reasoning directly: {\i egla arufa} was instituted only for cases of doubt, where the killer was unknown. Once murderers were acting openly and brazenly, killing with impunity in full public view, the entire institutional logic of the ritual collapsed. Even if a particular case remained technically unsolved, a society in which murder was rampant and unpunished could no longer sustain the moral outrage and communal responsibility that {\i egla arufa} was designed to express.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sota Discontinued\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the {\i mishnah} records that when adulterers multiplied, the {\i sota} water ordeal was discontinued \u8212- tying the discussion back to the central theme of the tractate. A {\i beraita} is cited: {\i venizka ha\u8217'isha me\u8217'avon bizman she\u8217'ish menuke me\u8217'avon} \u8212- the waters test the wife only when the husband himself is clean from sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Purity Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking here is the scope of \u8220"clean from sin.\u8221" When this principle was discussed earlier in the tractate, the focus was specifically on the husband having violated the laws of {\i sota} \u8212- for example, by continuing to live with his wife after she had been warned. Here, however, the {\i Gemara} broadens the requirement to encompass any type of sin. The {\i beraita} cites the verse: {\i lo efkod al benoseichem ki tiznena} \u8212- \u8220"I will not punish your daughters when they fornicate\u8221" \u8212- with the continuation of the verse making clear that the reason God withholds punishment is that the men themselves are running after prostitutes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sexual Sin Affects Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then anticipates a series of potential limitations. One might argue that only the husband\u8217's own sexual transgression disqualifies the water test, but not the sins of his sons or daughters. The verse, however, speaks of both daughters and daughters-in-law \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'al kaloseichem ki tena\u8217'afena} \u8212- extending the husband\u8217's responsibility to encompass the sexual conduct of his household members. A further objection suggests that perhaps only adultery ({\i avon eshet ish}) disqualifies the test, not general sexual immorality with an unmarried woman. The verse again responds: {\i ki heim im hazonot yepareidu} \u8212- \u8220"for they go off with prostitutes\u8221" \u8212- which is ordinary sexual violation rather than technical adultery, yet it is precisely this that renders the {\i mayim ha-marim} inoperative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then cites Rabbi Elazar\u8217's reading of the prophet\u8217's message to Israel: if you are careful about your own conduct, the waters will test your wives; if not, they will not. It is worth noting that {\i Rashi} reads {\i yepareidu} as deriving from {\i pered}, a mule \u8212- signifying habitual, animal-like fornication.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Justice Perverted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pleasure & Perversion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} shifts to a broader catalogue of societal decline and its institutional consequences. When {\i ba\u8217'alei hana\u8217'ah} increased \u8212- people who sought ease and pleasure, who preferred comfort to rigorous effort \u8212- justice became perverted. {\i Rashi} clarifies that this does not refer to extreme hedonism, but simply to people who preferred to take life a little too easy, who were unwilling to do the hard work that genuine adjudication demands. From this complacency, {\i nit\u8217'avu hadinim} \u8212- judgments were distorted \u8212- and {\i venitkalkelu hama\u8217'asim} \u8212- actions in general became corrupted \u8212- since people sensed they could act with impunity. The result: {\i ve\u8217'ein no\u8217'ach ba\u8217'olam} \u8212- there is no pleasantness in the world, which {\i Rashi} interprets as God no longer taking pleasure in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about this entire catalogue is that it focuses overwhelmingly on the corruption of justice and the courts, not on ritual violations or even general moral failures. The point seems to be that once the justice system is compromised \u8212- once judges can be influenced, flattered, or financially entangled \u8212- the entire fabric of society unravels. The {\i dayan} and the {\i beit din} send the decisive signal to society both practically and symbolically, and their failure reverberates through every domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partiality in Court\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i roei panim} increased in judgment \u8212- judges who showed favor to litigants, not necessarily through outright bribery but through personal recognition and partiality \u8212- the prohibition {\i lo taguru mipnei ish} ceased to operate, and the injunction {\i lo takiru panim} was abandoned. Judges became responsive to who stood before them rather than to the demands of {\i din emet}. The consequence: they cast off the yoke of heaven and took upon themselves the yoke of {\i basar vadam} \u8212- flesh and blood \u8212- since the faces of the litigants now exerted more influence on their rulings than the awareness that they were conducting {\i din shamayim} and would answer to God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lawyers\u8217' Influence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i lochashei lechishot b\u8217'din} increased \u8212- those who whispered in court \u8212- divine anger intensified among Israel and the {\i Shechinah} withdrew. {\i Rashi} here clarifies that these are not practitioners of incantation but rather the legal advocates, {\i orchei dayanim}, who would whisper into the ears of the judges, attempting to find them an angle of acquittal for their client. The verse is invoked: {\i b\u8217'kerev Elohim yishpot} \u8212- judgment is conducted in the presence of God \u8212- and when it is subverted through this kind of behind-the-scenes manipulation, God is, as it were, removed from the proceeding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Profit Over Truth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When people\u8217's hearts followed after gain \u8212- {\i acharei bitzam libam holech} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} records that {\i rabu ha\u8217'omrim lara tov velatov ra}: people began calling bad good and good bad. The dynamic is recognizable: when profit is the dominant motivation, truth becomes a casualty, flattery replaces honest speech, and people say whatever is needed to get ahead. The {\i pasuk} in the {\i navi} continues with {\i hoy hoy ba\u8217'olam} \u8212- woe, woe to the world \u8212- and this, the {\i mishnah} observes, became the prevailing condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haughtiness & Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i moshchei harok} increased \u8212- those who draw out their speech in an act of self-important haughtiness \u8212- {\i rabu hayehirim}, the arrogant multiplied, and {\i venitma\u8217'atu hatalmidim}, Torah scholars decreased. {\i Rashi} invokes the principle that haughtiness is a sign of Torah poverty: {\i siman legasut haruach aniyut shel Torah}. Perhaps the connection is that when people are focused on external performance and on projecting status, the genuine internal investment that Torah learning demands is neglected. Consequently, {\i vehaTorah chozeret al lomdeha} \u8212- the Torah goes searching for those who will truly devote themselves to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The social consequence follows directly: when the haughty multiplied, {\i hitchilu bnot Yisrael lehinase layehirim} \u8212- women began marrying such men \u8212- because {\i she\u8217'ein dorenu ro\u8217'eh ela lifnim}, the generation sees only the external presentation and cannot perceive whether the status on display has been genuinely earned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haughtiness Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises an obvious objection. We have a principle: {\i hai man demayahir afilu inish beiteih lo mikabli leih} \u8212- someone who is haughty is not even tolerated by the members of his own household. The verse is cited: {\i gever yahir velo yanveh} \u8212- the haughty man cannot even dwell in his own home, because his household sees through him and will not abide him. How then can it be said that women are attracted to such men?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the tension: {\i me\u8217'ikara kaftza leih} \u8212- initially, before the man is known, he appears distinguished and is eagerly sought in marriage. {\i Ulevasof mitzla leih} \u8212- but once he is married and his wife comes to know him for what he truly is, he becomes lowly in her eyes. The attraction and the disillusionment are sequential, not contradictory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Judges\u8217' Investments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i matilei melai al ba\u8217'alei batim} increased \u8212- when judges began placing their money with businessmen to invest on their behalf \u8212- bribery increased and justice was perverted. The problem, it should be noted, is not anonymized investment in a general fund; it is the specific entanglement between a judge and particular businessmen who offer to handle his money as a personal favor. The judge who benefits from such an arrangement cannot adjudicate impartially in any dispute touching those businessmen or their interests. The result: {\i raba hashochar}, bribery increased, {\i vehatayas mishpat}, justice was tilted, {\i vepaska tova}, and all good ceased.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acceptance of Favors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When people multiplied who would say \u8212- in a context of judges and community members \u8212- \u8220"thank you for the favor you did for me,\u8221" acknowledging and accepting kindnesses from those who might come before them in court, the consequence was {\i ish hayashar be\u8217'einav ya\u8217'aseh} \u8212- everyone acted as they saw fit, secure in the knowledge that the judges were indebted to them. {\i Shfalim hugbehu vehagvohim hushpelu} \u8212- the low were raised up and the high brought low \u8212- and {\i malchusa azla venavla}, governance collapsed entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stinginess & Robbery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the stingy ({\i tsarei ayin}) and those who seize what belongs to others ({\i torfei teref} \u8212- {\i Rashi} reads this simply as {\i gazlanim}, robbers) increased, {\i rabu ma\u8217'amitsei halev} \u8212- those of hardened heart multiplied \u8212- and {\i vekofsei hayadayim}, those who close their hands against lending, increased. The {\i Gemara} connects this explicitly to the Torah\u8217's warning: {\i hishamer lecha pen yihyeh im levavcha} \u8212- be careful that your heart does not harbor the miserly thought that withholds a loan, especially as {\i shemita} approaches. Whether stinginess produces robbery or the two are manifestations of the same underlying character, the {\i Gemara} groups them together as forces that produce a society of closed hearts and closed hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seductive Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i netuyot garon umeshakrot einayim} increased \u8212- women of outstretched necks and beckoning eyes, acting in a manner calculated to attract male attention \u8212- {\i rabu mayim hamarim} should, in theory, have increased as well, since the prevalence of such behavior would presumably generate more cases of suspected adultery warranting the {\i sota} ordeal. Instead, {\i pasku} \u8212- the ordeal ceased \u8212- because by that point the practice had already been suspended: the husbands themselves were not clean from sin, rendering the bitter water inoperative in any case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift-Taking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When those who accept gifts ({\i mekablei matanos}) increased, {\i nitma\u8217'atu hayamim veniktseru hashanim} \u8212- days diminished and years shortened. People lived shorter lives. The proof-text is direct: {\i veson\u8217'eh matanos yichyeh} \u8212- only one who hates gifts will live. It is notable that this consequence \u8212- shortened lifespan \u8212- is among the more overtly divine punishments in this catalogue, most of which operate through more recognizable social and political mechanisms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arrogance & Disputes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the arrogant of heart ({\i zechuchei halev}) increased, {\i rabu machlokes b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- disputes among Israel multiplied \u8212- because students no longer devoted their full energies to attending upon their teachers and mastering what they were being taught. The {\i mishnah} then makes this explicit with the famous statement: when the disciples of Shammai and Hillel who had not adequately served their teachers ({\i shelo shimshu kol tzorkan}) increased, debates multiplied and {\i na\u8217'asu Torah kishetei Toros} \u8212- the Torah became as though it were two Torahs. Until that moment, there had been virtually nothing debated in {\i Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh}; after it, the great controversies that characterize Talmudic literature took hold.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosi ben Yoezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eshkol Ceased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Yosi ben Yoezer died, {\i batlu ha-eshkolos} \u8212- the {\i eshkolot} ceased. The {\i Gemara} asks: what is an {\i eshkol}? Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel explains: {\i ish she-hakol bo} \u8212- a person in whom everything is contained, a completely rounded {\i talmid chacham}. This connects directly to what was just said: the era of undisputed transmission ended, the unbroken {\i Mesorah} was disrupted \u8212- or even if not every {\i halacha} had been transmitted explicitly by {\i Mesorah}, there had been sufficient unanimity and consensus that any given question could be settled. After Yosi ben Yoezer\u8217's death, the great debates began.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Abolishes Viduy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} turns to the reforms of Yochanan the High Priest. He abolished the {\i viduy ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- the declaration made when presenting the tithe distribution after the three-year cycle, in which one attests to having distributed correctly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser to Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: what was his reason? One might have assumed the answer fits naturally into the theme of societal decline \u8212- perhaps people were no longer distributing correctly and the declaration had become a fiction. But Rabbi Yosi bar Chanina gives a different reason entirely: the problem was not individual dishonesty but a systemic departure from the Torah\u8217's prescription. The Torah requires {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} to be given to {\i Levi\u8217'im} \u8212- {\i de-rachmana amar di-yehavi le-Levi\u8217'im} \u8212- but the practice had become to give it to {\i kohanim} instead. This shift had come about as a {\i kenas}, a penalty, against the {\i Levi\u8217'im} who had not returned to the land in the time of Ezra. Since {\i kohanim} are themselves {\i Levi\u8217'im}, the technical obligation was technically satisfied \u8212- but the declaration {\i vegam netativ la-Levi} could no longer be recited honestly, because the {\i ma\u8217'aser} was manifestly not going to the {\i Levi\u8217'im} as the Torah had specified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is remarkable about this juxtaposition is the way it complicates the preceding narrative of decline. Throughout that narrative, societal corruption produces institutional failure. Here, however, it is a {\i takkanah} of {\i Chazal} that caused the divergence from the Torah\u8217's ideal \u8212- and the reason for that {\i takkanah} was itself rooted in an earlier failure, the {\i Levi\u8217'im}\u8217's failure to return with Ezra. Even five hundred years before the events under discussion, cracks were already appearing in the edifice of ideal Torah observance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a further question: if the {\i viduy} was blocked only by the first clause \u8212- {\i vegam netativ la-Levi} \u8212- why not recite the rest of the {\i viduy} formula while omitting that clause? Reish Lakish explains: a {\i viduy} that cannot begin with {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} cannot continue with the rest of the {\i ma\u8217'asrot}. Abaye identifies the reason: since the {\i katuv} opens with {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}, a declaration that cannot include it cannot proceed at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demai Ordinance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adds that Yochanan also instituted the law of {\i demai} \u8212- the requirement that produce purchased from an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} be treated as doubtful with respect to tithe separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Am Ha\u8217'aretz Practices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason was empirical. Yochanan sent agents throughout all the borders of Israel and observed that {\i ammei ha\u8217'aretz} were routinely separating only {\i terumah gedolah}, while {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} and {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} were observed inconsistently \u8212- some separating them, some not. They assumed that {\i terumah gedolah} required attention, since eating it untithed is punishable by death at divine hands, but regarded the other obligations as less urgent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heavenly Warnings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yochanan gathered the people and delivered a striking teaching: just as {\i terumah gedolah} carries the sin of death, so too does {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser} and {\i tevel} \u8212- grain from which {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser} has not been taken. The underlying logic is precise: if one does not separate {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon}, the {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser} that the {\i Levi} would have been obligated to separate from that {\i ma\u8217'aser} remains embedded within the produce \u8212- and that {\i terumat ma\u8217'aser} renders the entire quantity {\i tevel} subject to the {\i chiyyuv mitah}. So the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} who neglects {\i ma\u8217'aser rishon} is not merely failing to give the {\i Levi} his due; he is leaving the buyer\u8217's produce in a state of genuine {\i tevel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then arises: to whom exactly was Yochanan delivering this message? A close reading of {\i Rashi} suggests it was addressed not to the {\i ammei ha\u8217'aretz} themselves but to the {\i chaverim} \u8212- the observant scholars who were purchasing from them. They were the ones who needed to understand that when they bought produce from an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}, they could not assume proper tithe separation had occurred; the obligation to complete the separation rested with them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then harmonizes the apparently contradictory accounts \u8212- one suggesting that {\i ammei ha\u8217'aretz} were in fact separating all required {\i ma\u8217'asrot}, another suggesting they were not \u8212- by concluding that Yochanan established two separate measures: first, he abolished the {\i viduy} even for {\i chaverim} separating from their own grain, because the first clause could not honestly be recited; second, he imposed the {\i demai} ordinance on produce purchased from {\i ammei ha\u8217'aretz}, requiring buyers to complete whatever tithe separations might have been omitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yochanan\u8217's Reforms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abolished Wake-Upers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yochanan the High Priest also abolished the practice of the {\i me\u8217'orerim} \u8212- the \u8220"wake-upers.\u8221" Every day the {\i Levi\u8217'im} would ascend the {\i duchan} and, before their singing, would call out: {\i urah lamah tishan Hashem} \u8212- \u8220"Awake, why do You sleep, O God?\u8221" Yochanan put an end to this. He argued: {\i vechi yesh sheinah lifnei hamakom}? Is there sleep before God? Has it not already been stated: {\i hinei lo yanum velo yishan shomer Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"Behold, the Guardian of Israel neither slumbers nor sleeps\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His response was not to deny that the verse had a legitimate metaphorical meaning \u8212- the {\i Gemara} acknowledges that {\i urah lamah tishan} is appropriate language when Israel is suffering and the nations are at ease, as a way of saying, \u8220"How can You allow this to continue?\u8221" But that is not appropriate as a daily declaration in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, and the fact that Yochanan needed to explain it as metaphor implies that the {\i Levi\u8217'im} had been reciting it in something closer to its literal sense. How this practice ever began in the middle of the Second Temple period, when the situation was not necessarily one of acute national tragedy, is a striking and somewhat disturbing question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abolished Scratching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yochanan also abolished the {\i nokfim} \u8212- the \u8220"bangers.\u8221" Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel explains: they would scratch the animal between its eyes so that blood would run into them, confusing the animal and making it easier to slaughter without it thrashing. Yochanan abolished this because {\i demechazi kib\u8217'al mum} \u8212- it resembles a {\i mum}, a disqualifying blemish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} teaches a related practice: some would strike the animal with sticks \u8212- in the manner performed before pagan worship \u8212- to stun it before slaughter. Yochanan objected to this as well, decrying it with the words: \u8220"How long will you feed {\i neveilot} to the altar?\u8221" The {\i Gemara} objects: a properly slaughtered animal is not a {\i neveilah}. The {\i Gemara} corrects: not {\i neveilah} but {\i tereifot} \u8212- by striking the animal violently you may have perforated the membrane of the brain ({\i nikev krum shel moach}), rendering it a {\i tereifah}. It is worth noting that the {\i beraita} describes the striking as resembling pagan practice, connecting this concern back to the earlier abolition of the {\i me\u8217'orerim} \u8212- both practices had acquired an uncomfortably pagan appearance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rings for Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In place of the striking with sticks, Yochanan instituted {\i tabaot bakarka} \u8212- rings fixed in the ground through which the animals\u8217' heads could be placed, holding them steady during {\i shechita} without any risk of injury.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blacksmith Hammer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He also stopped the sound of the blacksmith\u8217's hammer in Jerusalem on {\i Chol HaMoed}. Until his time, {\i devar ha\u8217'avad} \u8212- the principle that work which would cause irretrievable loss may be performed on {\i Chol HaMoed} \u8212- had been applied liberally enough that the hammer of the smithy was audible throughout the city. Yochanan found this too public and too permissive a signal, and he put an end to it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sanhedrin & Song\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanhedrin Gone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} declares: {\i mishebatlah Sanhedrin batel hashir mibeit hamishtaot} \u8212- when the {\i Sanhedrin} ceased, song ceased from the drinking houses. The proof-text: {\i bashir lo yishtu yayin} \u8212- \u8220"they shall not drink wine with song.\u8221" This is a striking formulation. The cessation of song is usually associated in the tradition with the {\i Churban Beit HaMikdash}, but here it is connected specifically to the {\i Sanhedrin}. The connection is not arbitrary: the entire arc of this section of the tractate has been about the corrupted justice system and the failure of the courts and their authority. The {\i Sanhedrin} is the apex of that system, and its disappearance extinguishes the institutional structure that had kept society intact \u8212- and with it, the capacity for genuine joy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises the question of whether this statement is purely descriptive \u8212- recording that joy ceased when the {\i Sanhedrin} fell \u8212- or also prescriptive, directing that song should not be practiced today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse establishing the connection between the {\i Sanhedrin} and song is supplied by Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua: {\i zkeinim misha\u8217'ar shavatu bachurim miginatam} \u8212- \u8220"the elders have ceased from the gate, and the young men from their music.\u8221" The elders in the gate are the {\i beit din}, and their departure takes the young men\u8217's music with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Song Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav then rules prescriptively: {\i ozna d\u8217'shama zimra te\u8217'aker} \u8212- an ear that hears song nowadays should be uprooted. One should not be listening to music at all, as an expression of mourning and loss. Whether this applies to all song or specifically to instrumental music is not spelled out here, though the term {\i zemer} could carry connotations of instrumental performance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Song Brings Destruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds: {\i zimra beveita churba beseifa} \u8212- song in the household brings destruction at its threshold. The proof is from the verse: {\i kol yeshorer bachalon chorev basaf} \u8212- the sound of singing at the window, destruction at the threshold. Rabbi Yitzchak draws out the full force of this: even a house surrounded by cedars \u8212- seemingly impregnable and protected \u8212- will not be spared. {\i Mitro\u8217'eia}, it will be shattered. Rav Ashi adds: from this verse we also learn that destruction, when it comes, begins at the threshold \u8212- {\i ki matchali churba beseifa matchali}. An additional verse supports this: {\i vesha\u8217'iyah yukat sha\u8217'ar} \u8212- its desolation is smitten at the gate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Song Ban Failed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Attempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna attempted to move from the theoretical to the practical and actually annul song within his community. The result was economic devastation. A hundred geese could be had for a {\i zuz} and a hundred {\i sa\u8217'im} of wheat for a {\i zuz} \u8212- and yet no one wanted to buy. Prices collapsed not because supply had increased but because the prohibition on song had drained all joy from life, leaving people without the will to engage in normal commerce and consumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Reverses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda came and overturned the prohibition \u8212- disparaging it, or perhaps himself demonstrating that it was not to be enforced. The economic reversal was immediate: a single goose now cost a {\i zuz} and could not be found. Joy returned, demand surged, and prosperity was restored. The {\i Gemara} does not explicitly endorse either Rav Huna\u8217's stringency or Rav Chisda\u8217's reversal, but the narrative arc makes the practical lesson clear: however principled the mourning behind the song prohibition may be, its full enforcement produces consequences that are themselves destructive. Society cannot function in a state of enforced joylessness.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Song Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted vs.\u160 ?Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna himself, even while seeking to restrict song, draws a distinction: {\i zimra denagdei udevakrei shari} \u8212- song used to lead cattle is permitted, because it serves a functional purpose in the work. {\i Degarda\u8217'ei asir} \u8212- but song sung by weavers for their own entertainment is forbidden, because it is simply {\i stam} pleasure with no connection to any necessary task. This distinction also supports the observation that the prohibition is not limited specifically to musical instruments \u8212- the issue seems to be the nature and purpose of the singing rather than its medium.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed-Gender Singing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef introduces a distinction that is widely cited in {\i halachic} literature in discussions of {\i kol isha} and related matters. If men are singing and women respond to them responsively \u8212- {\i zimrei gavrei ve\u8217'anei nashei} \u8212- this is {\i pritzuta}, general sexual looseness. But if women are singing and men are responding \u8212- {\i zimrei nashei ve\u8217'anei gavrei} \u8212- the situation is far more serious: {\i ke\u8217'esh bene\u8217'oret}, like fire in flax. The men, waiting for their cue, focus intensely on the women\u8217's singing; the potential for genuine transgression is far greater.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priority Elimination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: what is the practical difference between the two levels? They are both problematic. The answer: {\i levatulei hai mikamei hai} \u8212- to tell us which to eliminate first if one cannot eliminate both. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's realism here is notable. Not every wrong in society can be stamped out simultaneously; sometimes one must prioritize. If forced to choose, eliminate the women-led responsive singing, since it is the more combustible of the two. This principle \u8212- that practical {\i halacha} must account for what can and cannot be achieved \u8212- resonates directly with the earlier episode of Rav Huna and Rav Chisda.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five Punishments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Music & Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan teaches: {\i kol hashoteh be\u8217'arba\u8217'ah minei zemer meivi chamesh puraniyot la\u8217'olam} \u8212- whoever drinks with four types of musical instruments brings five punishments upon the world. The verse is from the {\i navi}: {\i vehaya kinor vanevel tof vachalil veyayin mishteihem} \u8212- it was the {\i kinor} (harp), {\i nevel} (lyre), {\i tof} (cymbal), {\i chalil} (flute), and wine their drink \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'et po\u8217'al Hashem lo yabitu}, and the acts of God they do not see. It should be noted that the people described in these verses are not simply listening to music at a religious celebration; they are rising early in the morning to run after {\i shechar} and staying late into the night inflamed by wine. The music is a feature of a thoroughgoing lifestyle of excess and indifference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exile & Famine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What follows from this? {\i Mah ketiv achrav} \u8212- what does the text say afterward? {\i Lachen gala ami mibli da\u8217'at} \u8212- therefore My people will go into exile for lack of knowledge: {\i she-gormim galut la\u8217'olam}, this behavior causes exile. {\i Ukhevodo metei ra\u8217'av} \u8212- and their honored ones will die of hunger: {\i she-mevi\u8217'im ra\u8217'av la\u8217'olam}, it brings famine. {\i Vehamono tzecheih tzamah} \u8212- and its multitude is parched with thirst \u8212- read metaphorically: {\i she-gormim la-Torah she-tishtakach mi-lomdeiha}, it causes Torah to be forgotten by those who learn it, since Torah is likened to water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous Diminished\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vayishach adam vayishpel ish} \u8212- a person is humbled and a man is brought low: {\i she-gormim shiflut le-son\u8217'o shel HaKadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- it even brings humiliation to the One Who is called the enemy of sin, which is to say God Himself, since {\i ein ish ela HaKadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- the term {\i ish} alludes to God, as the verse says {\i Hashem ish milchama}. And {\i veinei gevohim tishpalna} \u8212- the eyes of those held high will be brought low: {\i she-gormim shiflut shel Yisrael}, it diminishes Israel. The final consequence: {\i hirchiva she\u8217'ol nafsha ufa\u8217'ara piha livli chok} \u8212- the depths have opened their mouth without limit, consuming all glory.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Urim & Tumim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Prophets Died\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} records: {\i mishemetu nevi\u8217'im rishonim batlu urim vetumim} \u8212- when the first prophets died, the {\i urim vetumim} ceased to function. The phrasing is precise: it was not that the physical {\i urim vetumim} disappeared, but that they ceased to deliver answers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Prophets Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna identifies the \u8220"first prophets\u8221" as David, Solomon, and Samuel \u8212- an early and distinguished group. According to this view, the {\i urim vetumim} lost its efficacy already in that era. Rav Nachman qualifies: in the time of David it was not entirely defunct \u8212- {\i zimen solek vezimen lo solek}, sometimes an inquiry would receive a response and sometimes not. When Tzadok the {\i kohen} consulted it, he received an answer; when Avyatar did, he did not \u8212- as the verse implies when it records that Avyatar was removed from his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder whether David himself qualifies as a {\i navi}, since the {\i mishnah} uses the term {\i nevi\u8217'im rishonim}. It is a legitimate question; while David is known for his close association with prophets, he is not typically listed among them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Destruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} tests the claim that the {\i urim vetumim} ceased so early against a verse: {\i vayehi lidrosh Elokim kol yemei Zecharya hameivin bire\u8217'ot Elokim} \u8212- it was there to inquire of God all the days of Zecharya who understood the visions of God. This Zecharya \u8212- {\i Rashi} identifies him as the son of Yehoyada \u8212- lived very late in the period of the kings, well after David and Solomon. Does this not show the {\i urim vetumim} was operative until much later? The {\i Gemara} deflects: {\i lo, bineviim} \u8212- no, they would inquire of God through prophets, not through {\i urim vetumim}. The verse is about prophetic consultation, not the priestly oracle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i tanna\u8217'itic} source proves more decisive: {\i mishecharav Beit HaMikdash rishon batlu arei migrash, upasku urim vetumim, upasak melech mibeit David} \u8212- from the time the First Temple was destroyed, the cities of the {\i Levi\u8217'im} ceased, the {\i urim vetumim} ceased, and kingship from the house of David ended. This connects the {\i urim vetumim} definitively to the destruction of the First Temple, not to the earlier era of David and Solomon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final text from the Second Temple period \u8212- the {\i tirshata\u8217's} declaration that certain {\i kohanim} of uncertain lineage must refrain from eating sacred food {\i ad amod kohen le-urim vetumim}, until a {\i kohen} arises who can consult the {\i urim vetumim} \u8212- might seem to suggest that restoration was imminent. The {\i Gemara} firmly rejects this reading: that phrase means nothing of the sort. It is like one person saying to another: \u8220"until the dead are revived and {\i Mashiach ben David} comes.\u8221" It points to the eschaton, not to the near term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In view of all this, Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak redefines \u8220"first prophets\u8221" in the {\i mishnah}: the phrase means to the exclusion of the {\i last} prophets \u8212- Chaggai, Zechariah, and Malachi. These three prophesied at the very beginning of the Second Temple period, and the {\i urim vetumim} ceased no later than their time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Last Prophets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} confirms: when Chaggai, Zechariah, and Malachi died, {\i nistalka ruach hakodesh miYisrael} \u8212- divine inspiration departed from Israel. These were the last of the {\i nevi\u8217'im}. After them, there was no more {\i nevuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bat Kol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And yet \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'af al pi chen hayu mishtamshim bebat kol} \u8212- even after prophecy ceased, they would make use of a {\i bat kol}, a heavenly voice. This was not {\i ruach hakodesh} proper; it was a more attenuated form of divine communication, and its precise phenomenological character invites reflection. It is worth noting that while in the {\i Bavli} a {\i bat kol} is consistently presented as a miraculous heavenly voice, Saul Lieberman has demonstrated that in the {\i Yerushalmi} the term more often means something like a widely circulating report \u8212- a {\i shemuah} that spreads and acquires the status of near-certain information. The degree to which the {\i Bavli}\u8217's {\i bat kol} was experienced as a literally miraculous auditory phenomenon or as a form of divinely inspired collective intuition is itself a question the text leaves open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jericho Bat Kol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once, the Sages were gathered at a meal in the upper room of the house of Gurya in Jericho, when a {\i bat kol} issued from heaven declaring: {\i yesh bachem adam echad sherauy shetishre Shechinah alav} \u8212- there is among you one person upon whom the divine presence could rest. The declaration itself is poignant in its implications: there is someone here worthy of {\i nevuah}, but the {\i bat kol} goes on to add \u8212- {\i ela she\u8217'ein doro rauy lekach} \u8212- the generation is not fit for it. The very vehicle of the announcement, the {\i bat kol}, thus serves simultaneously to identify prophetic potential and to explain why prophecy cannot be restored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Identified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All eyes turned to Hillel the Elder \u8212- the assembled Sages recognized immediately who was being described. When Hillel died, the eulogy over him invoked the language of loss: {\i hei chasid, hei anav, talmido shel Ezra} \u8212- woe to the pious man, woe to the humble man, disciple of Ezra. The designation {\i talmido shel Ezra} is not biographical but typological: as Ezra had embodied the transmission of {\i Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh} in his generation, so Hillel embodied it in his \u8212- the continuation of an unbroken spiritual lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samuel the Small\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i bat kol} came, this time at a gathering in Yavneh, with the identical declaration: one person among them is worthy of the {\i Shechinah}\u8217's resting upon him, but the generation is not fit. This time all eyes turned to {\i Shmuel haKatan} \u8212- Samuel the Small \u8212- a student of Hillel in the same typological sense, spiritually his disciple even though Hillel had lived roughly a hundred and fifty years earlier. When Shmuel haKatan died, his eulogy echoed Hillel\u8217's: {\i hei anav, hei chasid, talmido shel Hillel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is particularly remarkable is that at the moment of his death, Shmuel haKatan uttered a prophecy: {\i Shimon veYishmael lecharba, vachavro leketala, usha\u8217'ar ama leviza, ve\u8217'akan sagi\u8217'in atidin lemeti al ama} \u8212- Shimon and Yishmael are destined for the sword, their colleagues for slaughter, the rest of the nation for despoilment, and great travails will come upon the people. This refers to Rabban Shimon haNasi and Rabbi Yishmael ben Elisha, among the {\i asara harugei malchut} \u8212- the ten martyrs of the Hadrianic persecutions, placing Shmuel haKatan at or near the time of the {\i Churban}. The {\i bat kol} had identified him as worthy of {\i nevuah}; this dying prophecy represents, it seems, at least a partial fulfillment of that verdict \u8212- something approaching, if not fully achieving, the {\i hashraat haShechina} that was declared his due.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda ben Bava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A similar {\i hesped} was sought for Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava \u8212- they wished to say the same {\i hei chasid, hei anav} over him \u8212- but {\i nitrefa hasha\u8217'ah}, the opportunity was lost. Since he was among the {\i harugei malchut}, the martyrs executed by the Roman government, they could not deliver a public eulogy over him without incurring dangerous attention from the authorities.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shamir & Miracles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shamir Worm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the destruction of the First Temple, the {\i shamir} was lost. A {\i beraita} records: {\i shamir shebo bana Shlomo et Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- the {\i shamir} was the means by which Solomon built the Temple.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metal Vessels Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for this claim is the verse: {\i vehabayit behibanoto even shlemah masa nivnah} \u8212- the Temple was built with complete stones brought from the quarry. Rabbi Yehuda reads this literally: no metal tools were used at all; the stones were hewn from the quarry in their final form and brought as they were. Rabbi Nechemya objects: can this really be maintained? Another verse states clearly that the stones were {\i meGuzorot baMegeirah} \u8212- hewn with a metal saw. What then is the meaning of the prohibition on metal in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}? Rabbi Nechemya explains: {\i shehaya matkin mibachutz umachnis mibifnim} \u8212- the metal work was done off-site, and only the finished stones were brought to the Temple Mount, where no sound of metal was heard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav attempts a synthesis: Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position applies to the stones of the {\i Mikdash} proper \u8212- no metal was used on them at any stage \u8212- while Rabbi Nechemya\u8217's position, permitting off-site metal work, applies to the stones of Solomon\u8217's palace. On this reading, Rabbi Nechemya has no need of the {\i shamir} for the Temple stones at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shamir & Breastplate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But according to Rabbi Nechemya, who permits metal work off-site, why was the {\i shamir} needed at all? The answer comes from a {\i beraita}: the stones of the {\i efod} \u8212- the breastplate of the High Priest, upon which the twelve names of the tribes were engraved \u8212- presented a unique set of constraints. They could not be inscribed with ink, because the verse says {\i pituchei chotam}, carved engraving. But they also could not be cut with a metal awl, because the verse says {\i bimlu\u8217'otam}, they must be complete \u8212- nothing may be removed. The solution: the name was written in ink on the surface of the stone, the stone was then shown to the {\i shamir} \u8212- {\i umar\u8217'eh lahem shamir mibachutz} \u8212- and the {\i shamir}, following the path of the ink, caused the stone to split open precisely along those lines. {\i Vehen nivka\u8217'ot me\u8217'aleihen} \u8212- they would crack of their own accord, like a fig splitting in summer heat or a crevice opening in the earth during the rainy season, so that nothing was removed and the stone remained {\i malu\u8217'ot}, complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shamir Properties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i shamir} was created at twilight on the eve of the first Sabbath \u8212- among the items listed in {\i Pirkei Avot} as created at {\i bein hashemashot}. {\i Ve\u8217'ein kol davar kashe yakhol la\u8217'amod befanav} \u8212- nothing hard can withstand it. This raises the obvious question: what vessel can contain it? If it eats through everything, what does one keep it in? {\i Korkhin oto bisfogin shel tzemer, uminichin oto be\u8217'itanei shel avar, mele\u8217'ah suvei se\u8217'orin} \u8212- it is wrapped in wool and placed in a lead container filled with barley bran. The logic is the same as \u8212- and apparently analogous to \u8212- the use of soft or pliable materials to contain highly corrosive or penetrating substances: the very softness of the material is what neutralizes the {\i shamir}\u8217's destructive capacity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lost Blessings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silk & Glass Lost\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ami teaches: {\i mishecharav mikdash rishon batla shira paranda uzkhukhit levana} \u8212- from the destruction of the First Temple, {\i shira paranda} (a type of fine silk) and clear white glass were lost. A {\i beraita} confirms this and adds further losses: {\i rekhev barzel}, iron chariots, were also lost. Some say even {\i yayin karush haba mi-Shanir} \u8212- a congealed wine from Mount Senir that resembled pressed fig rounds \u8212- was lost. It could be used a little at a time and reconstituted with water, making it a kind of ancient concentrated wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nofet Tzufim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Also lost: {\i nofet tzufim}. Rav explains: {\i solet shetzafa al gabei nafa} \u8212- a finest flour so rich that it would adhere to the top of the sieve rather than passing through, and it resembled dough kneaded with honey and oil.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Levi offers a different interpretation: {\i shtei kikkarot hanidbakot batanur} \u8212- two loaves placed at opposite walls of the oven that would expand and rise until they touched each other in the middle. This recalls the discussion of the original blessing of dough, and the implication is clear: once the blessing departed from bread, loaves no longer expanded with such abundance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wildflower Honey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi identifies {\i nofet tzufim} as honey coming from {\i tzufim} \u8212- the highlands, the mountains. The word {\i nofet} suggests dripping, and {\i tzufim} here is read as the high places \u8212- hence wildflower honey gathered by bees from the mountain grasses. Rav Sheshet\u8217's {\i targum} of a verse about bees \u8212- {\i kema denitzan devyata veshaitan beruma alma, umaskan duvsha me\u8217'ishbei tura} \u8212- supports this: the finest honey comes from bees that soar to the heights and bring down honey from the mountain grasses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then connects this to a {\i mishnah} elsewhere: {\i kol hanitzok tahor} \u8212- anything poured from one vessel to another does not create a link ({\i tziruf}) that would transmit impurity back to the upper vessel. The exception is honey of {\i Zifim} and {\i tzapichin} \u8212- pressed honey cakes so thick and congealed they form a solid mass, so that if the lower portion becomes {\i tamei}, the upper portion is {\i tamei} as well. What is {\i dvash Zifim}? Rabbi Yochanan says it is honey so potent that one can adulterate other honey with it without the blend being detected \u8212- it can be used for \u8220"cutting\u8221" lesser honey. Reish Lakish maintains that {\i Zifim} is simply a place name, as the verse {\i Zif veTelem uVe\u8217'alot} confirms \u8212- a town in the territory of Yehuda, and the same place mentioned in the incident when the people of Zif informed Saul of David\u8217's whereabouts. Rabbi Yochanan, separately, interprets {\i anshei haZifim} in that narrative as people who falsify their words \u8212- {\i mezayefim divreihem} \u8212- while Rabbi Elazar maintains it is simply the place name.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 People of Faith\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Faith Ceased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Upasku anshei amanah miYisrael} \u8212- the people of faith ceased from Israel. Rabbi Yitzchak explains: these are people who genuinely, fully believe in God \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aminim b\u8217'HaKadosh Baruch Hu} in the deepest sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 High Faith Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What level of faith did they possess? Rabbi Eliezer the Great articulates the standard: {\i kol mi she-yesh lo pat besalo ve\u8217'omer mah ochal lemachaR} \u8212- whoever has bread in his pantry and says \u8220"what will I eat tomorrow?\u8221" \u8212- that person is among the {\i katnei emunah}, those of small faith. The bar is extraordinarily high: if you have food today and are already worrying about tomorrow, you lack complete trust in divine providence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous Lack Faith\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence reaches even the {\i tzadikim} of later generations. Rabbi Elazar expounds the verse {\i ki mi vaz l\u8217'yom katanot} \u8212- who has despised the day of smallness? \u8212- as a question: what caused the righteous to have their table diminished in the world to come, not receiving the full reward they deserve? {\i Katanut she-hayah bahen} \u8212- the smallness that was in them; even the righteous of our time lack complete {\i emunah}, worrying about their material provision in this world. {\i She-lo he\u8217'eminu b\u8217'HaKadosh Baruch Hu} \u8212- they did not fully trust that God would provide. Measure for measure, their heavenly table in the world to come will not be complete either.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Sotah 49\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Emuna & Yeridas Hadoros\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer: Bread & Faith\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final daf of Masechet Sotah picks up at the very bottom of 48b, and the overarching theme \u8212- that since the destruction of the {\i beit hamikdash} everything has gone to pot \u8212- frames the entire discussion. The Gemara opens with a statement attributed to Rabbi Yitzchak: {\i upasku anshei emuna} \u8212- the people of faith have ceased. Who are these people of faith? They are those who truly believe in God with complete trust. A {\i baraita} is cited: Rabbi Eliezer HaGadol says that anyone who has bread in his basket and worries about what he will eat tomorrow is among those of little faith \u8212- {\i mikatnei emuna}. God will provide for tomorrow; anxiety about tomorrow\u8217's sustenance reflects an incomplete trust in divine providence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer further interprets the verse {\i ki mi baz leyom katnut} \u8212- who has despised a day of smallness? \u8212- as referring to the righteous whose tables in the {\i olam haba} will not be fully laden. Why? {\i Katnut shehayah bahem} \u8212- because of the smallness that was in them, meaning that even they did not maintain complete faith in God. This is a striking formulation: even the righteous, if they harbored some anxiety about their livelihood, are measured accordingly, a case of {\i mida kneged mida}, in which their table in the future world is diminished because they did not fully trust that God would fill it in this world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Young Wicked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a sharp reinterpretation of the very same verse. He says: {\i elu katnei bnei reshei Yisrael} \u8212- these are the young children of wicked Jews. These are children who died young, presumably on account of punishment visited upon their wicked fathers. The phrase {\i ki mi baz leyom katnut} is read by Rava as a reference to those who diminish the judgment deserved by the fathers in the world to come \u8212- {\i shemevazin dina deavihem leatid lavo}. These children argue before God: {\i Ribono shel olam}, if You were going to exact full punishment from our fathers in the future world, why did You blunt their teeth already \u8212- why did You cause us to die young? Our early death should count as partial atonement for our fathers\u8217' sins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is quite an interesting twist on the theme, and it does not obviously connect to the surrounding discussion of {\i emuna}. Normally one might be satisfied to see the wicked punished, yet here there is a note of compassion: even the wicked suffer when they lose young children, and at least that suffering ought to count for something as partial atonement {\i leatid lavo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David\u8217's Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ilai bar Yevarchia offers a statement: were it not for the prayer of David, all of Israel would have been reduced to selling {\i rivav} \u8212- some lowly commodity that only the poor are reduced to peddling. The verse cited is {\i shisa Hashem mora lahem} \u8212- God, place upon them a teacher, or give them mastery. Rashi explains this by tracing the context: the preceding verse reads {\i ki lo lanetzach yishachach evyon} \u8212- God will not eternally forget the poor \u8212- {\i tikvat aniyim tovad la\u8217'ad} \u8212- the hope of the lowly shall not be lost forever. Rashi reads {\i mora} as {\i marut} \u8212- mastery or status \u8212- and understands the verse as David\u8217's prayer on behalf of the poor, asking God to give them some standing in this world, so that the nations who look upon them with a degree of honor should know that they are merely human and not divine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The precise connection of this statement to the theme of {\i emuna} is not entirely transparent. Perhaps the link is that David\u8217's prayer relates to anxiety about financial well-being, or to the general suffering of poverty discussed in the surrounding passages. Rabbi Ilai bar Yevarchia is mentioned again immediately for additional teachings, and the repeated citation of the same {\i amora} may itself be the editorial thread connecting these passages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chabakuk Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ilai bar Yevarchia further states: what was the function of the prayer of Chabakuk? {\i Habat talmidei chachamim shemiskasim betalis achas} \u8212- that Torah scholars so impoverished that they would share a single garment between them, and yet continue to learn Torah together, should be sustained. The image captures both their devotion and their destitution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse cited is from Chabakuk: {\i Hashem shamati shimacha yarasi} \u8212- I have heard Your report and I was awed \u8212- {\i bekerev shanim chayeihu} \u8212- in the midst of years give it life. The Gemara reads {\i bekerev shanim} not as \u8220"in the midst of years\u8221" but as {\i bekiruv shnayim} \u8212- in the proximity of two people \u8212- {\i chayeihu} \u8212- give them life, give them {\i parnasa}. Who are those who hear God\u8217's message and witness God\u8217's deeds? Those who study Torah. So the prayer of Chabakuk on behalf of those two Torah scholars sharing a single garment is that God sustain them with a livelihood. This, too, connects to the broader theme of faith and sustenance: even in the most desperate circumstances, one who learns Torah out of poverty can trust that his prayer \u8212- or the prayer of the great \u8212- will be heard on his behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Torah Discourse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ilai bar Yevarchia continues: two Torah scholars who travel together on the road and do not speak words of Torah between them are deserving of being consumed by fire \u8212- {\i re\u8217'uyim lehisharef ba\u8217'esh}. The proof is from the episode of Elisha and Eliyahu: {\i vayehi heima holchim haloch vedaber} \u8212- they were walking and speaking \u8212- {\i vehinei rechev esh} \u8212- and behold a chariot of fire. The Gemara infers: the reason the fire did not harm them is because they were speaking Torah. {\i Ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'ika dibur ha leika dibur re\u8217'uyim lehisharef} \u8212- had there been no Torah discourse, they would have been deserving of being burned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Sages Tallit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The image of two Torah scholars sharing a single {\i talis} is recalled precisely here in contrast to the next teaching: whereas the shared {\i talis} reflects an intimacy and mutual dedication in the pursuit of Torah, the following passage depicts the opposite dynamic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scholars Without Respect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ilai bar Yevarchia adds: two Torah scholars who live in the same city and are not gracious and pleasant to one another in matters of {\i halacha} \u8212- {\i ein nochim zeh lazeh bahalacha} \u8212- such that there is no reciprocal respect, no willingness to say \u8220"you might also ask the other rabbi,\u8221" no collegial discourse at all \u8212- will come to a bad end: {\i echad meis ve\u8217'echad goleh}, one will die and the other will go into exile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse cited is {\i lanus shama rotze\u8217'ach asher yirtzach es re\u8217'eihu bevli da\u8217'as} \u8212- the city of refuge is for one who kills his friend {\i bevli da\u8217'as}, without knowledge. And {\i da\u8217'as} means Torah, as the verse says {\i nidmu ami mibli hada\u8217'as} \u8212- my people are struck down for lack of knowledge. So if there is no Torah shared between two scholars in the same city, it is as if one has killed the other through that absence, and the punishment of exile follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Study in Hardship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah bar breih de-Rabbi Chiya teaches: any Torah scholar who learns Torah out of {\i dachak} \u8212- out of pressing straits, in privation \u8212- {\i tefilato nishma\u8217'at}, his prayer will be heard. This builds on the foregoing: David and Chabakuk had to pray on behalf of the poor scholars; here the scholar himself, by virtue of learning in hardship, earns that his own prayers are answered directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof is from the verse {\i ki am be-Tziyon yeshev bi-Yerushalayim bacho lo tivke chanon yachnecha le-kol za\u8217'akecha ke-shom\u8217'ato anacha} \u8212- to the voice of your crying out, when God hears, He will answer. The next verse \u8212- {\i ve-natan lachem Hashem lechem tzar u-mayim lachatz} \u8212- God will give you bread of privation and water of oppression \u8212- is read as the situational context: these are people who have very little bread and very little water, yet learn Torah nonetheless. It is precisely because of that privation that God answers their cry immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu adds a further dimension. The question hovering over this entire passage is that {\i emuna} is all well and good, but the reality is that many Torah scholars do suffer, and the promises of answered prayer do not always seem to manifest materially. Rabbi Abbahu addresses this: {\i masbi\u8217'in oto mi-ziv Shechina} \u8212- such a person is sustained from the radiance of the Divine Presence itself. The verse cited is {\i ve-hayu einecha ro\u8217'ot et morecha} \u8212- your eyes will see your teacher. The experience of God\u8217's presence is itself a form of sustenance, even when it does not translate into food on the table. The comparison that naturally arises \u8212- Moshe Rabbeinu, who was literally in God\u8217's presence and did not eat \u8212- suggests that there are indeed souls capable of being sustained on something far beyond physical nourishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Curtain Not Sealed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan adds: {\i af ein ha-pargod nin\u8217'al be-fanav} \u8212- the heavenly curtain is not sealed before such a person, meaning his prayers have direct access. The verse cited is {\i ve-lo yikanef od morecha} \u8212- your teacher will no longer be hidden beneath a garment, covered from you. God will no longer be concealed. The spiritual access, even when material provision remains elusive, represents a real and sustaining form of divine response.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daily Curse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua: from the day the {\i beit hamikdash} was destroyed, there is no day that does not carry within it some element of curse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Increasing Curse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava intensifies this: {\i be-chol yom va-yom meruba klalato mi-shel chaveiro} \u8212- not only is every day cursed since the destruction, but every single day the curse is greater than the day before. Every passing day is worse than the previous one. The verse cited is {\i ba-boker tomar mi yiten erev u-va-erev tomar mi yiten boker} \u8212- in the morning you will say, who will give evening, and in the evening you will say, who will give morning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Morning/Evening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: when it is evening and you long for morning, which morning are you longing for? Perhaps the morning yet to come? But the Gemara dismisses this: maybe tomorrow morning will be even worse \u8212- {\i mi yada mai havi}. Rather, {\i ella dechalif} \u8212- you are longing for the morning that has already passed. When it is night, you wish it were yesterday morning, because yesterday morning things were not yet as bad as they are tonight. Every passing moment, a person wishes it were the moment before. Things are continuously getting worse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedusha & Kaddish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'ella alma amai mikayeim} \u8212- if the world is getting progressively worse, what sustains it? The Gemara answers: {\i a\u8217'Kedusha DeSidra ve\u8217'Yehei Shmei Rabba de\u8217'Agadata} \u8212- it is the {\i kedusha} of the {\i seder} recited during {\i U\u8217'va LeTziyon}, and the {\i Yehei Shmei Rabba} recited after a public teaching of {\i agadata}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking Gemara, one of the very few places where these prayers are explicitly identified as sustaining the world. The {\i Kedusha DeSidra} is the {\i Kedushas Hashem} recited in {\i U\u8217'va LeTziyon} after {\i Shacharis}, where {\i kadosh kadosh} is recited along with its Aramaic translation. The {\i Yehei Shmei Rabba} refers to {\i Kaddish} recited specifically after public {\i agadata} teachings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Universal Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's commentary here is essential. He explains: {\i seder kedusha shelo tiknuha ela sheyehu kol Yisrael oskin baTorah bechol yom davar mu\u8217'at} \u8212- this order of {\i kedusha} was established precisely so that all Jews would engage in Torah study each day, even if only a little. The key feature of {\i Kedusha DeSidra} is that the {\i pesukim} are recited together with their Aramaic translation, {\i targumo}. Aramaic was the vernacular, which meant that everyone \u8212- scholars and {\i amei ha\u8217'aretz} alike \u8212- actually understood what was being said. They were genuinely learning Torah, not merely reciting words. It created no hierarchy; it was accessible to all. And it combined two elements simultaneously: {\i Kedushas Hashem} and {\i Talmud Torah}. Rashi calls this {\i chaviva} \u8212- precious, beloved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the {\i Yehei Shmei Rabba} recited after {\i agadata} teachings has the same character: {\i agadata} was the form of Torah accessible to everyone, the public sermon of the {\i darshan} that would draw the whole community together on {\i Shabbos}. The people would gather, everyone would hear the Torah teaching, and everyone would respond with {\i Yehei Shmei Rabba}. Again: {\i Kedushas Hashem} combined with {\i Talmud Torah}, universal and egalitarian. It is this minimum \u8212- not some extraordinary spiritual achievement, but a daily communal practice accessible to all \u8212- that keeps the world going even as deeds continue to deteriorate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites the verse: {\i eretz afaisa kmo ofel tzalmaves velo sedarim} \u8212- a land shrouded in darkness and the shadow of death, without order. {\i Hayesh sedarim tofei\u8217'ah me\u8217'ofel} \u8212- if these orders of {\i kedusha} and {\i kaddish} are in place, then light breaks through the darkness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taharah Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues: {\i v\u8217'lo yardah l\u8217'vrachah v\u8217'nitma\u8217'et ta\u8217'am peirot} \u8212- the rains have not descended for blessing since the destruction, and the taste of fruit has diminished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar teaches: {\i taharah batlah ta\u8217'am v\u8217'rei\u8217'ach} \u8212- the loss of {\i taharah} caused the loss of taste and smell in fruit. Rashi explains beautifully: because people were {\i tehorim} and conducted themselves in {\i taharah}, God purified their fruit of bad smell and bad taste \u8212- the metaphysical purity of the people generated a corresponding purity and sweetness in the produce of the land. When {\i taharah} was lost, that spiritual refinement of the fruit was lost as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds: {\i ma\u8217'aser bitlu shuman dagan} \u8212- the failure to give {\i ma\u8217'aser} caused the fatness of the grain to cease. The {\i ma\u8217'aser} is described in the {\i Torah} as {\i kol chelev dagan} \u8212- the fatness of the grain \u8212- because one gives from the best to God. If one withholds this, the fatness disappears. And indeed, {\i mida kneged mida}: the very fatness one tried to keep for oneself by not giving it is the fatness that is lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna\u8217's Date\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna once found a particularly sweet and rich date \u8212- {\i tamarta d\u8217'chinunita}, a species known for its exceptional sweetness and juiciness. He took it, wrapped it in his handkerchief, and was going to give it to his son. His son Rabbah arrived and said: {\i murchina reicha d\u8217'chinunita} \u8212- I smell the fragrance of this amazing date. Rav Huna responded: {\i bni, taharah yesh becha} \u8212- my son, you must possess a special purity, because as we have seen, {\i taharah} enables one to perceive taste and smell. He gave it to him. Then Rabbah\u8217's own son Abba arrived, and Rabbah took the date and gave it to his son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna said to Rabbah: {\i bni, simachtani libi} \u8212- you made my heart rejoice that you could perceive the fragrance of the date, which speaks to your purity of soul. {\i Aval hikhita et shinai} \u8212- but you blunted my teeth, meaning you hurt me: rather than keeping what I gave you, you immediately passed it on to your own son. That is what people say: {\i rachamei d\u8217'abba a-vnei} \u8212- a father\u8217's love flows toward his children. {\i Aval rachamei d\u8217'vnei a-vnei d\u8217'havu lei} \u8212- but the love of children flows not back to the parent but downward to their own children. Parents give more and feel more for their children than children feel for parents \u8212- not that this is always so, and certainly there are exceptions, but it reflects a natural asymmetry in the direction of parental love.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grandson Dishonor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This theme finds a darker echo immediately after. Rav Aha bar Yaakov raised his daughter\u8217's son, Rav Yaakov bar bartei, as his own. When the boy grew up, Rav Aha said to him: {\i ashkin maya} \u8212- could you please pour me some water? The grandson replied: {\i lav avuna at} \u8212- you are not my father, and I owe you no honor. The Gemara comments: {\i vehainu d\u8217'amri inshi} \u8212- that is what people say: {\i rabi rabi verabi lei} \u8212- raise children, raise them up \u8212- {\i ubesof amar bar bartekh ana} \u8212- and in the end he says, I am your daughter\u8217's son, not your son, and therefore I owe you nothing. A dark coda to the theme of how things have declined.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 War Decrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vespasian Decrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The last {\i mishna} of Masechet Sotah turns to a related but somewhat different note: not merely the world going to pot of its own deterioration, but the communal response to the {\i churban} \u8212- the {\i gezeirot} enacted to limit expressions of joy as a {\i zecher l\u8217'churban}. It is worth noting, as Rav Soloveitchik emphasized, that there is a distinction between {\i zecher l\u8217'mikdash} \u8212- commemorating what the {\i beit hamikdash} was like in its glory \u8212- and {\i zecher l\u8217'churban} \u8212- commemorating the fact of its destruction. These decrees fall into the latter category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bepulmus shel Vespasian} \u8212- in the wars of Vespasian \u8212- they decreed against {\i atarot hatanim}, the crowns worn by grooms, and against {\i hairus} (a kind of drum instrument, which we will return to).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Titus Decrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bepulmus shel Titus} \u8212- in the wars of Titus, who succeeded Vespasian \u8212- they decreed against {\i atarot kallot}, the crowns worn by brides, and {\i shelo yilamed adam et beno yevanit}, that a person should not teach his son Greek wisdom. The reasoning behind this latter decree will be explained in the {\i gemara} below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Carriage Decree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bepulmus ha\u8217'acharon} \u8212- in the last war, which may refer either to the destruction itself or, according to some opinions, to the great revolt concluded around the time of Bar Kochba \u8212- they decreed {\i shelo tetze kallah ba\u8217'apiryon betokh ha\u8217'ir}, that a bride should not go out in a carriage or litter through the city during her wedding procession. The {\i mishna} notes that subsequently {\i veraboteinu hitiru} \u8212- the Rabbis permitted it again. Indeed, in Masechet Ketubot, the public procession of the bride in such a litter was a marker of a first marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deaths of Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Parables\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues with a litany of losses, enumerating what the world lost upon the death of each great sage. {\i Mishemmet Rabbi Meir batlu moshlei meshalim} \u8212- when Rabbi Meir died, those who could craft parables were gone, for he was known for his mastery of the {\i mashal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Azzai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Ben Azzai batlu hashakdanim} \u8212- when Ben Azzai died, those characterized by unceasing, tireless diligence in Torah study were gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Zoma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Ben Zoma batlu hadarshanim} \u8212- when Ben Zoma died, those who could derive teachings from {\i pesukim} were gone. Rashi explains that this refers not to {\i agadic} homiletics but to the kind of textual derivation associated with Ben Zoma himself, as in the famous teaching {\i ad she-darsha Ben Zoma}, where he derived the requirement to remember the Exodus at night from a careful reading of the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Rabbi Akiva batel kvod haTorah} \u8212- when Rabbi Akiva died, the honor of Torah departed \u8212- {\i v\u8217'nistatmu ma\u8217'ayanot hachachmah}, the wellsprings of wisdom were stopped up. This is because Rabbi Akiva was the supreme {\i mechadesh}, the one who drew new Torah from every extra letter and from every {\i kots shel yud}, and with his death that power of creative {\i chiddush} was lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chanina Miracles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa batlu anshei ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- when Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa died, those who performed wondrous deeds were gone. The stories surrounding Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa are replete with accounts of miraculous occurrences in his presence, and with his death that dimension of the world\u8217's experience was closed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pious End\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Abba Yosi ben Katnuta batlu chasidim} \u8212- when Abba Yosi ben Katnuta died, the truly pious were gone. And why was he called {\i Katnuta} \u8212- the small one? {\i She-hayah mikatnei chasidim} \u8212- because he was even one of the most minor of the pious. The very fact that the smallest of the pious was still around had sustained the world; once even he was gone, the category of {\i chasid} was extinguished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Wisdom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai batal ziv hachochmah} \u8212- when Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai died, the luminous radiance of wisdom departed. Rashi acknowledges with characteristic honesty: {\i lo yadana mai hi} \u8212- I do not know precisely what the connection is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel Health\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Rabban Gamliel HaZaken batel kvod haTorah} \u8212- when Rabban Gamliel HaZaken died, the honor of Torah was lost, and {\i umesah taharah u\u8217'ferishut} \u8212- purity and asceticism ended. Rashi points to the Gemara in Megillah which records that after his death, people became far less healthy, and when people are not well, their Torah study necessarily diminishes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael Glory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Rabbi Yishmael ben Pabi batlah ziv hakehunah} \u8212- when Rabbi Yishmael ben Pabi died, the glory of the priesthood departed. He was a wealthy man who supported the {\i kohanim}, and with him the {\i kavod} of the {\i kehunah} was extinguished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe Humility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mishemmet Rebbe batlah anavah v\u8217'yirat chet} \u8212- when Rebbe died \u8212- that is, Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi, the final editor of the {\i Mishna} \u8212- both humility and true fear of sin departed from the world. This is a somewhat sobering note on which to end the {\i mishna}. Most final {\i mishnayot} of a {\i masechet} conclude on an uplifting note, and some {\i rishonim} indeed have a different {\i girsa} that includes additional lines here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Path to Mashiach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pinchas Ladder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some versions of the {\i mishna} \u8212- though not in the standard printed {\i gemara} text \u8212- include the following statement from Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair, which in many {\i mishnayot} prints appears as the conclusion of the {\i masechet}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Zerizut mevia lidei nekiut, nekiut mevia lidei taharah, taharah mevia lidei perishut, perishut mevia lidei kedushah, kedushah mevia lidei anavah, anavah mevia lidei yirat chet, yirat chet mevia lidei chasidut, chasidut mevia lidei ruach hakodesh, ruach hakodesh mevia lidei techiyat hametim, u\u8217'techiyat hametim ba al yedei Eliyahu zachur latov.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Diligence leads to cleanliness, cleanliness to {\i taharah}, {\i taharah} to {\i perishut}, {\i perishut} to {\i kedushah}, {\i kedushah} to {\i anavah}, {\i anavah} to fear of sin, fear of sin to {\i chasidut}, {\i chasidut} to {\i ruach hakodesh}, {\i ruach hakodesh} to the resurrection of the dead, and the resurrection will come through Eliyahu, may his memory be for blessing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a profoundly different note on which to end the {\i masechet}. Everything may indeed be going to pot \u8212- but here is a ladder. Small, incremental steps of spiritual discipline, each one drawing the next, can reverse the entire process and lead ultimately to the coming of {\i Mashiach} and the resurrection of the dead. That this statement serves as the structural framework for the Ramchal\u8217's {\i Mesillat Yesharim} is well known. It is genuinely sad that it is not in our standard {\i girsa}, because it is such a powerful and necessary counterweight to everything that preceded it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-Mashiach Chaos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chutzpah & Heresy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues \u8212- whether as {\i baraita} or otherwise \u8212- with the famous passage about {\i ikveta d\u8217'Meshicha}, the footsteps of {\i Mashiach}, the period immediately preceding the redemption. {\i Be\u8217'ikvata d\u8217'Meshicha chutzpa yasga} \u8212- in the footsteps of {\i Mashiach}, insolence will proliferate. {\i Veyoker yaamir} \u8212- honor will be perverted. {\i Hagefen titein pirya vehayayin beyoker} \u8212- even though the vine will give its fruit, wine will be expensive, because people will be running after hedonistic pleasures. {\i U\u8217'malchut tahafech leminut} \u8212- the kingdom will turn to {\i minut}, heresy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last phrase requires explanation. Why should we care whether a non-Jewish government becomes heretical? Rashi \u8212- in the passage that appears in the outer margin of some editions, in text that was edited out of the standard Rashi \u8212- explains: the kingdom that rules over most of the world will turn toward {\i minut}, following the error of a particular figure and his disciples, who were called {\i minim}. Rashi is referring to the Roman Empire becoming the Christian Holy Roman Empire. The phrase {\i tahafech leminut} thus means the Christianization of Rome.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Path to Mashiach (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ikvata Meshicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The placement of these passages \u8212- everything getting worse right before {\i Mashiach} \u8212- is itself understood as a positive spin on the preceding litany of decline. The worse things get, the more they constitute the {\i ikveta d\u8217'Meshicha}, the footsteps, the foreshadowing of the redemption. The darkness is itself the sign that dawn approaches.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-Mashiach Chaos (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Rebuke\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'ein tochecha} \u8212- there will be no one to remonstrate, because everyone is equally sinning and no one can rebuke another. {\i Beit va\u8217'ad yihiyeh liznut} \u8212- the house of assembly, formerly the place of Torah, will become a place of prostitution. {\i Vehagalil yecherav vehagavlan yaishom} \u8212- the Galil will be destroyed and this place called {\i Gavlan} will be desolate \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'anshei hagvul yesovevu me\u8217'ir le\u8217'ir velo yechonenu} \u8212- and the people of the border will wander from city to city and find no rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wisdom Lost\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'chochmat sofrim tisrach} \u8212- the wisdom of the scribes will become corrupted and putrid. {\i Veyirei chet yimasu} \u8212- those who fear sin will be despised. {\i Ve\u8217'ha\u8217'emet tehei ne\u8217'ederet} \u8212- truth will be absent, will be a rarity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Youth Shame Elders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ne\u8217'arim pnei zekenim yalbinu} \u8212- youth will shame and humiliate elders. {\i Zekenim yaamdu mipnei ktanim} \u8212- elders will be expected to stand before the young. {\i Ben menavel av bat kama be\u8217'ima kala bachamota} \u8212- a son will disgrace his father, a daughter will rise against her mother, a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law. {\i Oivei ish anshei beito} \u8212- a person\u8217's enemies will be the members of his own household \u8212- an image that conjures the world of informers and denunciations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generation Face\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Pnei hador k\u8217'pnei hakelev} \u8212- the face of the generation will be like the face of dogs. Whether this means cruelty, indifference, or simply shamelessness is left somewhat open, though the following phrase clarifies the register.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son Shame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Habein eino mitbayes me\u8217'aviv} \u8212- a son will not be embarrassed before his father. The breakdown of shame, of basic filial inhibition, is the culminating sign.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Heaven\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'al mah yesh lanu lehisha\u8217'en? Al avinu shebashamayim} \u8212- upon whom do we have to rely? Only upon our Father in heaven. This phrase serves as the conclusion of the {\i mishna} in some versions, and it is a genuine note of faith: despite the complete collapse of social and moral order, the only stable anchor that remains is God Himself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 War Decrees (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Groom Crowns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to elaborate on the details of the {\i mishna}\u8217's decrees. Rav says: the prohibition on grooms\u8217' crowns applies only to those made of salt and sulfur \u8212- some type of mineral formation used for such adornments. But crowns made of myrtle or rose \u8212- {\i shel hadas veshel vered} \u8212- are permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crown Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees: even a crown of myrtle and rose is forbidden. However, one made of reeds or some type of seaweed \u8212- {\i shel kanim veshel chilat} \u8212- is permitted. Levi goes further still: even reeds and seaweed are forbidden, and this is what he also taught in his {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Irus Drum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What exactly is the {\i irus} mentioned in the {\i mishna}? Rav Elazar says: {\i tavla dechad puma} \u8212- a tablet with one mouth, which Rashi understands as some type of clapper or gong. The discussion then turns to drums. Rabbah bar Rav Huna made a {\i tanbura} \u8212- a type of drum \u8212- for his son. His father came and broke it. He said: if you make that kind of drum, it will be confused with a {\i tavla dechad puma}, which is forbidden. If you want to make a drum for your son, make one on the mouth of a clay pot \u8212- {\i apuma dechatztzava} \u8212- or on the opening of some other household container, so that it will not be confused with the instruments that are actually prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bride Crowns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What were the {\i atarot kallot} \u8212- the brides\u8217' crowns \u8212- that were prohibited during Titus\u8217's wars? Rabbah bar bar Chana says in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ir shel zahav} \u8212- a golden city, some type of tiara made to look like the city of Jerusalem worked in gold. A {\i baraita} confirms this, and adds that what one may do instead is make {\i kippah shel milat} \u8212- some type of head covering of silk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wedding Canopy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also records that a {\i tanna} taught that the prohibition extended to {\i chuppat chatanim} \u8212- a particular type of wedding canopy. What was this {\i chuppah}? {\i Zehorit mezuhevet} \u8212- a crimson fabric adorned with gold, a {\i tallit} dyed crimson with golden ornaments woven through it, which would be fashioned into a tent-like canopy. Rashi describes this in some detail. What remains permitted is a {\i papirit} \u8212- Rashi describes this as a frame of wooden arches upon which one can hang various ornaments and decorations as one wishes, which is the permissible alternative canopy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Alternatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus establishes that while the most ornate and elaborate forms of wedding adornment were prohibited in the wake of the various wars, the Rabbis permitted alternative forms that preserved the dignity and joy of the occasion without the extravagance associated with the pre-destruction era.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pig & Greek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A famous {\i baraita} is now cited to explain the prohibition on teaching Greek wisdom. When the Hasmonean kings were at war with one another \u8212- Hyrcanus on the outside of Jerusalem with Roman troops, and Aristobulus on the inside \u8212- the besieged would daily lower {\i dinarim} in a basket, and those outside would send up animals for the {\i temidim}, the daily sacrifices, because everyone still cared about the {\i avodah} in the {\i beit hamikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There was an elderly man on the inside of the walls who knew {\i chochmah Yevanis} \u8212- Greek wisdom, apparently a method of covert signaling. He communicated to those outside: as long as those inside continue performing the {\i avodah}, they will not be surrendered into your hands. The next day, when the besiegers lowered the {\i dinarim}, instead of sending up a lamb, they sent up a pig. When the pig reached halfway up the wall, it dug its claws into the wall, and the entire land of Israel shook for a distance of 400 {\i parsah}. At that moment they declared: {\i arur adam she-yigadel chazirim} \u8212- cursed is the person who raises pigs \u8212- {\i ve-arur adam she-yelamed livno chochmah Yevanis} \u8212- and cursed is the person who teaches his son Greek wisdom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds that in that same year, {\i mavi\u8217'in ha-omer mi-gagots tzerifin} \u8212- they had to bring the {\i omer} from grain stored on rooftops or from distant places like {\i Bik\u8217'at Ein Socher}, since the besieging troops had burned all the fields around Jerusalem, and normal supplies were unavailable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Greek Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Is the prohibition really absolute? The Gemara raises the question, for Rav teaches: Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi said that in Eretz Yisrael, one should not speak {\i Sursi} \u8212- Aramaic, a vernacular considered coarse \u8212- but rather either {\i leshon hakodesh} or Greek. If one is not going to speak the holy tongue, at least speak a refined and culturally elevated language. And Rav Yosef similarly teaches that in Bavel, one should not speak Aramaic but rather either {\i leshon hakodesh} or Persian \u8212- {\i leshon Parsi} \u8212- meaning Farsi, which was considered the elevated vernacular of that region.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i leshon Yevanis lachud, chochmah Yevanis lachud} \u8212- the Greek {\i language} is one matter, Greek {\i wisdom} is quite another. One may speak Greek; what is forbidden is the study of {\i chochmah Yevanis} in its technical sense \u8212- the esoteric or philosophical wisdom that was used deceptively in the incident above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel Family\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehudah in the name of Shmuel cites a statement of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel in explanation of the verse {\i eini olelah le-nafshi mi-kol benos iri} \u8212- my eye brings grief to my soul more than all the daughters of my city. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel said: my father\u8217's household had a thousand children. Five hundred learned Torah and five hundred learned {\i chochmah Yevanis}. And of all of them, only I remain, and the son of my father\u8217's brother who is in Asia. Clearly, then, the Nasi\u8217's household itself had half its children studying Greek wisdom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Close to Govt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains: {\i shani shel beis Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel de-krovim le-malchus havu} \u8212- the household of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel was different, because they were close to the Roman government and had to maintain those relations. A {\i baraita} confirms this principle: Avtolmos ben Reuven was permitted to wear his hair in the {\i komi} style \u8212- a particular Roman hairstyle that would normally constitute {\i darchei ha-Emori}, pagan practices \u8212- because he was close to the government and had to appear a certain way. Similarly, the household of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel was permitted to learn {\i chochmah Yevanis} because of their proximity to the Roman government. The category of {\i karov le-malchus} \u8212- proximity to the government \u8212- thus functions as a recognized exception to otherwise forbidden practices.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halachic Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crown Materials\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibition on grooms\u8217' crowns enacted during Vespasian\u8217's wars, the {\i gemara} clarifies, applies specifically to crowns made of salt and sulfur. This is the baseline position \u8212- not all materials are equally forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crown Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate among Rav, Shmuel, and Levi, as detailed above, represents a spectrum of increasing stringency: Rav permits myrtle and rose; Shmuel forbids those but permits reeds and seaweed; Levi forbids even reeds and seaweed. Levi\u8217's position is supported by his own {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drum Making\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar Rav Huna\u8217's attempt to make a {\i tanbur}-style drum for his son, and his father\u8217's intervention, illustrates the concern about confusion with genuinely forbidden instruments. The practical ruling is to construct a simple drum from a household pot or similar vessel \u8212- {\i apuma dechatztzava} or {\i apuma dekafiza} \u8212- precisely so that no one mistakes it for a {\i tavla dechad puma}, which falls within the scope of the decree.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Hope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosef Humility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes, following the {\i mishna}\u8217's statement that when Rebbe died {\i batlah anavah} \u8212- humility departed \u8212- with a wonderful moment of humor. Rav Yosef said to the {\i tanna} reciting the {\i baraita}: do not recite that {\i anavah} is gone \u8212- {\i d\u8217'ika ana}, I am still here! This is the Gemara\u8217's way of not ending on pure despair. Humility does not mean denying one\u8217's own greatness; as the Gemara implies by bringing Rav Yosef\u8217's protest here, true {\i anavah} is about how one carries oneself, not false modesty. Moshe Rabbeinu was {\i anav mikol adam} and yet he knew his own stature. Rav Yosef\u8217's claim is itself a statement about what {\i anavah} means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, Rav Nachman said to the {\i tanna}: do not say that {\i yirat chet} has disappeared \u8212- {\i d\u8217'ika ana}, I am still around. Again, a note of hope: however grim the picture painted by the {\i mishna} and {\i baraita}, there is still someone left. Things are not completely lost. The Gemara ends with a gentle insistence that we are not yet beyond redemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hadran\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach Masechet Sotah.}\par}
